if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-28054"]="Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov was the third son of Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, a land owner well known in our district in his own day, and still remembered among us owing to his gloomy and tragic death, which happened thirteen years ago, and which I shall describe in its proper place. For the present I will only say that this “landowner”—for so we used to call him, although he hardly spent a day of his life on his own estate—was a strange type, yet one pretty frequently to be met with, a type abject and vicious and at the same time senseless. But he was one of those senseless persons who are very well capable of looking after their worldly affairs, and, apparently, after nothing else. Fyodor Pavlovitch, for instance, began with next to nothing; his estate was of the smallest; he ran to dine at other men’s tables, and fastened on them as a toady, yet at his death it appeared that he had a hundred thousand roubles in hard cash. At the same time, he was all his life one of the most senseless, fantastical fellows in the whole district. I repeat, it was not stupidity—the majority of these fantastical fellows are shrewd and intelligent enough—but just senselessness, and a peculiar national form of it.\n\nHe was married twice, and had three sons, the eldest, Dmitri, by his first wife, and two, Ivan and Alexey, by his second. Fyodor Pavlovitch’s first wife, Adelaïda Ivanovna, belonged to a fairly rich and distinguished noble family, also landowners in our district, the Miüsovs. How it came to pass that an heiress, who was also a beauty, and moreover one of those vigorous, intelligent girls, so common in this generation, but sometimes also to be found in the last, could have married such a worthless, puny weakling, as we all called him, I won’t attempt to explain. I knew a young lady of the last “romantic” generation who after some years of an enigmatic passion for a gentleman, whom she might quite easily have married at any moment, invented insuperable obstacles to their union, and ended by throwing herself one stormy night into a rather deep and rapid river from a high bank, almost a precipice, and so perished, entirely to satisfy her own caprice, and to be like Shakespeare’s Ophelia. Indeed, if this precipice, a chosen and favorite spot of hers, had been less picturesque, if there had been a prosaic flat bank in its place, most likely the suicide would never have taken place. This is a fact, and probably there have been not a few similar instances in the last two or three generations. Adelaïda Ivanovna Miüsov’s action was similarly, no doubt, an echo of other people’s ideas, and was due to the irritation caused by lack of mental freedom. She wanted, perhaps, to show her feminine independence, to override class distinctions and the despotism of her family. And a pliable imagination persuaded her, we must suppose, for a brief moment, that Fyodor Pavlovitch, in spite of his parasitic position, was one of the bold and ironical spirits of that progressive epoch, though he was, in fact, an ill-natured buffoon and nothing more. What gave the marriage piquancy was that it was preceded by an elopement, and this greatly captivated Adelaïda Ivanovna’s fancy. Fyodor Pavlovitch’s position at the time made him specially eager for any such enterprise, for he was passionately anxious to make a career in one way or another. To attach himself to a good family and obtain a dowry was an alluring prospect. As for mutual love it did not exist apparently, either in the bride or in him, in spite of Adelaïda Ivanovna’s beauty. This was, perhaps, a unique case of the kind in the life of Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was always of a voluptuous temper, and ready to run after any petticoat on the slightest encouragement. She seems to have been the only woman who made no particular appeal to his senses.\n\nImmediately after the elopement Adelaïda Ivanovna discerned in a flash that she had no feeling for her husband but contempt. The marriage accordingly showed itself in its true colors with extraordinary rapidity. Although the family accepted the event pretty quickly and apportioned the runaway bride her dowry, the husband and wife began to lead a most disorderly life, and there were everlasting scenes between them. It was said that the young wife showed incomparably more generosity and dignity than Fyodor Pavlovitch, who, as is now known, got hold of all her money up to twenty-five thousand roubles as soon as she received it, so that those thousands were lost to her for ever. The little village and the rather fine town house which formed part of her dowry he did his utmost for a long time to transfer to his name, by means of some deed of conveyance. He would probably have succeeded, merely from her moral fatigue and desire to get rid of him, and from the contempt and loathing he aroused by his persistent and shameless importunity. But, fortunately, Adelaïda Ivanovna’s family intervened and circumvented his greediness. It is known for a fact that frequent fights took place between the husband and wife, but rumor had it that Fyodor Pavlovitch did not beat his wife but was beaten by her, for she was a hot-tempered, bold, dark-browed, impatient woman, possessed of remarkable physical strength. Finally, she left the house and ran away from Fyodor Pavlovitch with a destitute divinity student, leaving Mitya, a child of three years old, in her husband’s hands. Immediately Fyodor Pavlovitch introduced a regular harem into the house, and abandoned himself to orgies of drunkenness. In the intervals he used to drive all over the province, complaining tearfully to each and all of Adelaïda Ivanovna’s having left him, going into details too disgraceful for a husband to mention in regard to his own married life. What seemed to gratify him and flatter his self-love most was to play the ridiculous part of the injured husband, and to parade his woes with embellishments.\n\n“One would think that you’d got a promotion, Fyodor Pavlovitch, you seem so pleased in spite of your sorrow,” scoffers said to him. Many even added that he was glad of a new comic part in which to play the buffoon, and that it was simply to make it funnier that he pretended to be unaware of his ludicrous position. But, who knows, it may have been simplicity. At last he succeeded in getting on the track of his runaway wife. The poor woman turned out to be in Petersburg, where she had gone with her divinity student, and where she had thrown herself into a life of complete emancipation. Fyodor Pavlovitch at once began bustling about, making preparations to go to Petersburg, with what object he could not himself have said. He would perhaps have really gone; but having determined to do so he felt at once entitled to fortify himself for the journey by another bout of reckless drinking. And just at that time his wife’s family received the news of her death in Petersburg. She had died quite suddenly in a garret, according to one story, of typhus, or as another version had it, of starvation. Fyodor Pavlovitch was drunk when he heard of his wife’s death, and the story is that he ran out into the street and began shouting with joy, raising his hands to Heaven: “Lord, now lettest Thou Thy servant depart in peace,” but others say he wept without restraint like a little child, so much so that people were sorry for him, in spite of the repulsion he inspired. It is quite possible that both versions were true, that he rejoiced at his release, and at the same time wept for her who released him. As a general rule, people, even the wicked, are much more naïve and simple-hearted than we suppose. And we ourselves are, too.\n\n# Chapter II. He Gets Rid Of His Eldest Son\n\nYou can easily imagine what a father such a man could be and how he would bring up his children. His behavior as a father was exactly what might be expected. He completely abandoned the child of his marriage with Adelaïda Ivanovna, not from malice, nor because of his matrimonial grievances, but simply because he forgot him. While he was wearying every one with his tears and complaints, and turning his house into a sink of debauchery, a faithful servant of the family, Grigory, took the three-year-old Mitya into his care. If he hadn’t looked after him there would have been no one even to change the baby’s little shirt.\n\nIt happened moreover that the child’s relations on his mother’s side forgot him too at first. His grandfather was no longer living, his widow, Mitya’s grandmother, had moved to Moscow, and was seriously ill, while his daughters were married, so that Mitya remained for almost a whole year in old Grigory’s charge and lived with him in the servant’s cottage. But if his father had remembered him (he could not, indeed, have been altogether unaware of his existence) he would have sent him back to the cottage, as the child would only have been in the way of his debaucheries. But a cousin of Mitya’s mother, Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, happened to return from Paris. He lived for many years afterwards abroad, but was at that time quite a young man, and distinguished among the Miüsovs as a man of enlightened ideas and of European culture, who had been in the capitals and abroad. Towards the end of his life he became a Liberal of the type common in the forties and fifties. In the course of his career he had come into contact with many of the most Liberal men of his epoch, both in Russia and abroad. He had known Proudhon and Bakunin personally, and in his declining years was very fond of describing the three days of the Paris Revolution of February 1848, hinting that he himself had almost taken part in the fighting on the barricades. This was one of the most grateful recollections of his youth. He had an independent property of about a thousand souls, to reckon in the old style. His splendid estate lay on the outskirts of our little town and bordered on the lands of our famous monastery, with which Pyotr Alexandrovitch began an endless lawsuit, almost as soon as he came into the estate, concerning the rights of fishing in the river or wood-cutting in the forest, I don’t know exactly which. He regarded it as his duty as a citizen and a man of culture to open an attack upon the “clericals.” Hearing all about Adelaïda Ivanovna, whom he, of course, remembered, and in whom he had at one time been interested, and learning of the existence of Mitya, he intervened, in spite of all his youthful indignation and contempt for Fyodor Pavlovitch. He made the latter’s acquaintance for the first time, and told him directly that he wished to undertake the child’s education. He used long afterwards to tell as a characteristic touch, that when he began to speak of Mitya, Fyodor Pavlovitch looked for some time as though he did not understand what child he was talking about, and even as though he was surprised to hear that he had a little son in the house. The story may have been exaggerated, yet it must have been something like the truth.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch was all his life fond of acting, of suddenly playing an unexpected part, sometimes without any motive for doing so, and even to his own direct disadvantage, as, for instance, in the present case. This habit, however, is characteristic of a very great number of people, some of them very clever ones, not like Fyodor Pavlovitch. Pyotr Alexandrovitch carried the business through vigorously, and was appointed, with Fyodor Pavlovitch, joint guardian of the child, who had a small property, a house and land, left him by his mother. Mitya did, in fact, pass into this cousin’s keeping, but as the latter had no family of his own, and after securing the revenues of his estates was in haste to return at once to Paris, he left the boy in charge of one of his cousins, a lady living in Moscow. It came to pass that, settling permanently in Paris he, too, forgot the child, especially when the Revolution of February broke out, making an impression on his mind that he remembered all the rest of his life. The Moscow lady died, and Mitya passed into the care of one of her married daughters. I believe he changed his home a fourth time later on. I won’t enlarge upon that now, as I shall have much to tell later of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s firstborn, and must confine myself now to the most essential facts about him, without which I could not begin my story.\n\nIn the first place, this Mitya, or rather Dmitri Fyodorovitch, was the only one of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s three sons who grew up in the belief that he had property, and that he would be independent on coming of age. He spent an irregular boyhood and youth. He did not finish his studies at the gymnasium, he got into a military school, then went to the Caucasus, was promoted, fought a duel, and was degraded to the ranks, earned promotion again, led a wild life, and spent a good deal of money. He did not begin to receive any income from Fyodor Pavlovitch until he came of age, and until then got into debt. He saw and knew his father, Fyodor Pavlovitch, for the first time on coming of age, when he visited our neighborhood on purpose to settle with him about his property. He seems not to have liked his father. He did not stay long with him, and made haste to get away, having only succeeded in obtaining a sum of money, and entering into an agreement for future payments from the estate, of the revenues and value of which he was unable (a fact worthy of note), upon this occasion, to get a statement from his father. Fyodor Pavlovitch remarked for the first time then (this, too, should be noted) that Mitya had a vague and exaggerated idea of his property. Fyodor Pavlovitch was very well satisfied with this, as it fell in with his own designs. He gathered only that the young man was frivolous, unruly, of violent passions, impatient, and dissipated, and that if he could only obtain ready money he would be satisfied, although only, of course, for a short time. So Fyodor Pavlovitch began to take advantage of this fact, sending him from time to time small doles, installments. In the end, when four years later, Mitya, losing patience, came a second time to our little town to settle up once for all with his father, it turned out to his amazement that he had nothing, that it was difficult to get an account even, that he had received the whole value of his property in sums of money from Fyodor Pavlovitch, and was perhaps even in debt to him, that by various agreements into which he had, of his own desire, entered at various previous dates, he had no right to expect anything more, and so on, and so on. The young man was overwhelmed, suspected deceit and cheating, and was almost beside himself. And, indeed, this circumstance led to the catastrophe, the account of which forms the subject of my first introductory story, or rather the external side of it. But before I pass to that story I must say a little of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s other two sons, and of their origin.\n\n# Chapter III. The Second Marriage And The Second Family\n\nVery shortly after getting his four-year-old Mitya off his hands Fyodor Pavlovitch married a second time. His second marriage lasted eight years. He took this second wife, Sofya Ivanovna, also a very young girl, from another province, where he had gone upon some small piece of business in company with a Jew. Though Fyodor Pavlovitch was a drunkard and a vicious debauchee he never neglected investing his capital, and managed his business affairs very successfully, though, no doubt, not over-scrupulously. Sofya Ivanovna was the daughter of an obscure deacon, and was left from childhood an orphan without relations. She grew up in the house of a general’s widow, a wealthy old lady of good position, who was at once her benefactress and tormentor. I do not know the details, but I have only heard that the orphan girl, a meek and gentle creature, was once cut down from a halter in which she was hanging from a nail in the loft, so terrible were her sufferings from the caprice and everlasting nagging of this old woman, who was apparently not bad-hearted but had become an insufferable tyrant through idleness.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch made her an offer; inquiries were made about him and he was refused. But again, as in his first marriage, he proposed an elopement to the orphan girl. There is very little doubt that she would not on any account have married him if she had known a little more about him in time. But she lived in another province; besides, what could a little girl of sixteen know about it, except that she would be better at the bottom of the river than remaining with her benefactress. So the poor child exchanged a benefactress for a benefactor. Fyodor Pavlovitch did not get a penny this time, for the general’s widow was furious. She gave them nothing and cursed them both. But he had not reckoned on a dowry; what allured him was the remarkable beauty of the innocent girl, above all her innocent appearance, which had a peculiar attraction for a vicious profligate, who had hitherto admired only the coarser types of feminine beauty.\n\n“Those innocent eyes slit my soul up like a razor,” he used to say afterwards, with his loathsome snigger. In a man so depraved this might, of course, mean no more than sensual attraction. As he had received no dowry with his wife, and had, so to speak, taken her “from the halter,” he did not stand on ceremony with her. Making her feel that she had “wronged” him, he took advantage of her phenomenal meekness and submissiveness to trample on the elementary decencies of marriage. He gathered loose women into his house, and carried on orgies of debauchery in his wife’s presence. To show what a pass things had come to, I may mention that Grigory, the gloomy, stupid, obstinate, argumentative servant, who had always hated his first mistress, Adelaïda Ivanovna, took the side of his new mistress. He championed her cause, abusing Fyodor Pavlovitch in a manner little befitting a servant, and on one occasion broke up the revels and drove all the disorderly women out of the house. In the end this unhappy young woman, kept in terror from her childhood, fell into that kind of nervous disease which is most frequently found in peasant women who are said to be “possessed by devils.” At times after terrible fits of hysterics she even lost her reason. Yet she bore Fyodor Pavlovitch two sons, Ivan and Alexey, the eldest in the first year of marriage and the second three years later. When she died, little Alexey was in his fourth year, and, strange as it seems, I know that he remembered his mother all his life, like a dream, of course. At her death almost exactly the same thing happened to the two little boys as to their elder brother, Mitya. They were completely forgotten and abandoned by their father. They were looked after by the same Grigory and lived in his cottage, where they were found by the tyrannical old lady who had brought up their mother. She was still alive, and had not, all those eight years, forgotten the insult done her. All that time she was obtaining exact information as to her Sofya’s manner of life, and hearing of her illness and hideous surroundings she declared aloud two or three times to her retainers:\n\n“It serves her right. God has punished her for her ingratitude.”\n\nExactly three months after Sofya Ivanovna’s death the general’s widow suddenly appeared in our town, and went straight to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house. She spent only half an hour in the town but she did a great deal. It was evening. Fyodor Pavlovitch, whom she had not seen for those eight years, came in to her drunk. The story is that instantly upon seeing him, without any sort of explanation, she gave him two good, resounding slaps on the face, seized him by a tuft of hair, and shook him three times up and down. Then, without a word, she went straight to the cottage to the two boys. Seeing, at the first glance, that they were unwashed and in dirty linen, she promptly gave Grigory, too, a box on the ear, and announcing that she would carry off both the children she wrapped them just as they were in a rug, put them in the carriage, and drove off to her own town. Grigory accepted the blow like a devoted slave, without a word, and when he escorted the old lady to her carriage he made her a low bow and pronounced impressively that, “God would repay her for the orphans.” “You are a blockhead all the same,” the old lady shouted to him as she drove away.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch, thinking it over, decided that it was a good thing, and did not refuse the general’s widow his formal consent to any proposition in regard to his children’s education. As for the slaps she had given him, he drove all over the town telling the story.\n\nIt happened that the old lady died soon after this, but she left the boys in her will a thousand roubles each “for their instruction, and so that all be spent on them exclusively, with the condition that it be so portioned out as to last till they are twenty-one, for it is more than adequate provision for such children. If other people think fit to throw away their money, let them.” I have not read the will myself, but I heard there was something queer of the sort, very whimsically expressed. The principal heir, Yefim Petrovitch Polenov, the Marshal of Nobility of the province, turned out, however, to be an honest man. Writing to Fyodor Pavlovitch, and discerning at once that he could extract nothing from him for his children’s education (though the latter never directly refused but only procrastinated as he always did in such cases, and was, indeed, at times effusively sentimental), Yefim Petrovitch took a personal interest in the orphans. He became especially fond of the younger, Alexey, who lived for a long while as one of his family. I beg the reader to note this from the beginning. And to Yefim Petrovitch, a man of a generosity and humanity rarely to be met with, the young people were more indebted for their education and bringing up than to any one. He kept the two thousand roubles left to them by the general’s widow intact, so that by the time they came of age their portions had been doubled by the accumulation of interest. He educated them both at his own expense, and certainly spent far more than a thousand roubles upon each of them. I won’t enter into a detailed account of their boyhood and youth, but will only mention a few of the most important events. Of the elder, Ivan, I will only say that he grew into a somewhat morose and reserved, though far from timid boy. At ten years old he had realized that they were living not in their own home but on other people’s charity, and that their father was a man of whom it was disgraceful to speak. This boy began very early, almost in his infancy (so they say at least), to show a brilliant and unusual aptitude for learning. I don’t know precisely why, but he left the family of Yefim Petrovitch when he was hardly thirteen, entering a Moscow gymnasium, and boarding with an experienced and celebrated teacher, an old friend of Yefim Petrovitch. Ivan used to declare afterwards that this was all due to the “ardor for good works” of Yefim Petrovitch, who was captivated by the idea that the boy’s genius should be trained by a teacher of genius. But neither Yefim Petrovitch nor this teacher was living when the young man finished at the gymnasium and entered the university. As Yefim Petrovitch had made no provision for the payment of the tyrannical old lady’s legacy, which had grown from one thousand to two, it was delayed, owing to formalities inevitable in Russia, and the young man was in great straits for the first two years at the university, as he was forced to keep himself all the time he was studying. It must be noted that he did not even attempt to communicate with his father, perhaps from pride, from contempt for him, or perhaps from his cool common sense, which told him that from such a father he would get no real assistance. However that may have been, the young man was by no means despondent and succeeded in getting work, at first giving sixpenny lessons and afterwards getting paragraphs on street incidents into the newspapers under the signature of “Eye-Witness.” These paragraphs, it was said, were so interesting and piquant that they were soon taken. This alone showed the young man’s practical and intellectual superiority over the masses of needy and unfortunate students of both sexes who hang about the offices of the newspapers and journals, unable to think of anything better than everlasting entreaties for copying and translations from the French. Having once got into touch with the editors Ivan Fyodorovitch always kept up his connection with them, and in his latter years at the university he published brilliant reviews of books upon various special subjects, so that he became well known in literary circles. But only in his last year he suddenly succeeded in attracting the attention of a far wider circle of readers, so that a great many people noticed and remembered him. It was rather a curious incident. When he had just left the university and was preparing to go abroad upon his two thousand roubles, Ivan Fyodorovitch published in one of the more important journals a strange article, which attracted general notice, on a subject of which he might have been supposed to know nothing, as he was a student of natural science. The article dealt with a subject which was being debated everywhere at the time—the position of the ecclesiastical courts. After discussing several opinions on the subject he went on to explain his own view. What was most striking about the article was its tone, and its unexpected conclusion. Many of the Church party regarded him unquestioningly as on their side. And yet not only the secularists but even atheists joined them in their applause. Finally some sagacious persons opined that the article was nothing but an impudent satirical burlesque. I mention this incident particularly because this article penetrated into the famous monastery in our neighborhood, where the inmates, being particularly interested in the question of the ecclesiastical courts, were completely bewildered by it. Learning the author’s name, they were interested in his being a native of the town and the son of “that Fyodor Pavlovitch.” And just then it was that the author himself made his appearance among us.\n\nWhy Ivan Fyodorovitch had come amongst us I remember asking myself at the time with a certain uneasiness. This fateful visit, which was the first step leading to so many consequences, I never fully explained to myself. It seemed strange on the face of it that a young man so learned, so proud, and apparently so cautious, should suddenly visit such an infamous house and a father who had ignored him all his life, hardly knew him, never thought of him, and would not under any circumstances have given him money, though he was always afraid that his sons Ivan and Alexey would also come to ask him for it. And here the young man was staying in the house of such a father, had been living with him for two months, and they were on the best possible terms. This last fact was a special cause of wonder to many others as well as to me. Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, of whom we have spoken already, the cousin of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s first wife, happened to be in the neighborhood again on a visit to his estate. He had come from Paris, which was his permanent home. I remember that he was more surprised than any one when he made the acquaintance of the young man, who interested him extremely, and with whom he sometimes argued and not without an inner pang compared himself in acquirements.\n\n“He is proud,” he used to say, “he will never be in want of pence; he has got money enough to go abroad now. What does he want here? Every one can see that he hasn’t come for money, for his father would never give him any. He has no taste for drink and dissipation, and yet his father can’t do without him. They get on so well together!”\n\nThat was the truth; the young man had an unmistakable influence over his father, who positively appeared to be behaving more decently and even seemed at times ready to obey his son, though often extremely and even spitefully perverse.\n\nIt was only later that we learned that Ivan had come partly at the request of, and in the interests of, his elder brother, Dmitri, whom he saw for the first time on this very visit, though he had before leaving Moscow been in correspondence with him about an important matter of more concern to Dmitri than himself. What that business was the reader will learn fully in due time. Yet even when I did know of this special circumstance I still felt Ivan Fyodorovitch to be an enigmatic figure, and thought his visit rather mysterious.\n\nI may add that Ivan appeared at the time in the light of a mediator between his father and his elder brother Dmitri, who was in open quarrel with his father and even planning to bring an action against him.\n\nThe family, I repeat, was now united for the first time, and some of its members met for the first time in their lives. The younger brother, Alexey, had been a year already among us, having been the first of the three to arrive. It is of that brother Alexey I find it most difficult to speak in this introduction. Yet I must give some preliminary account of him, if only to explain one queer fact, which is that I have to introduce my hero to the reader wearing the cassock of a novice. Yes, he had been for the last year in our monastery, and seemed willing to be cloistered there for the rest of his life.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Third Son, Alyosha\n\nHe was only twenty, his brother Ivan was in his twenty-fourth year at the time, while their elder brother Dmitri was twenty-seven. First of all, I must explain that this young man, Alyosha, was not a fanatic, and, in my opinion at least, was not even a mystic. I may as well give my full opinion from the beginning. He was simply an early lover of humanity, and that he adopted the monastic life was simply because at that time it struck him, so to say, as the ideal escape for his soul struggling from the darkness of worldly wickedness to the light of love. And the reason this life struck him in this way was that he found in it at that time, as he thought, an extraordinary being, our celebrated elder, Zossima, to whom he became attached with all the warm first love of his ardent heart. But I do not dispute that he was very strange even at that time, and had been so indeed from his cradle. I have mentioned already, by the way, that though he lost his mother in his fourth year he remembered her all his life—her face, her caresses, “as though she stood living before me.” Such memories may persist, as every one knows, from an even earlier age, even from two years old, but scarcely standing out through a whole lifetime like spots of light out of darkness, like a corner torn out of a huge picture, which has all faded and disappeared except that fragment. That is how it was with him. He remembered one still summer evening, an open window, the slanting rays of the setting sun (that he recalled most vividly of all); in a corner of the room the holy image, before it a lighted lamp, and on her knees before the image his mother, sobbing hysterically with cries and moans, snatching him up in both arms, squeezing him close till it hurt, and praying for him to the Mother of God, holding him out in both arms to the image as though to put him under the Mother’s protection ... and suddenly a nurse runs in and snatches him from her in terror. That was the picture! And Alyosha remembered his mother’s face at that minute. He used to say that it was frenzied but beautiful as he remembered. But he rarely cared to speak of this memory to any one. In his childhood and youth he was by no means expansive, and talked little indeed, but not from shyness or a sullen unsociability; quite the contrary, from something different, from a sort of inner preoccupation entirely personal and unconcerned with other people, but so important to him that he seemed, as it were, to forget others on account of it. But he was fond of people: he seemed throughout his life to put implicit trust in people: yet no one ever looked on him as a simpleton or naïve person. There was something about him which made one feel at once (and it was so all his life afterwards) that he did not care to be a judge of others—that he would never take it upon himself to criticize and would never condemn any one for anything. He seemed, indeed, to accept everything without the least condemnation though often grieving bitterly: and this was so much so that no one could surprise or frighten him even in his earliest youth. Coming at twenty to his father’s house, which was a very sink of filthy debauchery, he, chaste and pure as he was, simply withdrew in silence when to look on was unbearable, but without the slightest sign of contempt or condemnation. His father, who had once been in a dependent position, and so was sensitive and ready to take offense, met him at first with distrust and sullenness. “He does not say much,” he used to say, “and thinks the more.” But soon, within a fortnight indeed, he took to embracing him and kissing him terribly often, with drunken tears, with sottish sentimentality, yet he evidently felt a real and deep affection for him, such as he had never been capable of feeling for any one before.\n\nEvery one, indeed, loved this young man wherever he went, and it was so from his earliest childhood. When he entered the household of his patron and benefactor, Yefim Petrovitch Polenov, he gained the hearts of all the family, so that they looked on him quite as their own child. Yet he entered the house at such a tender age that he could not have acted from design nor artfulness in winning affection. So that the gift of making himself loved directly and unconsciously was inherent in him, in his very nature, so to speak. It was the same at school, though he seemed to be just one of those children who are distrusted, sometimes ridiculed, and even disliked by their schoolfellows. He was dreamy, for instance, and rather solitary. From his earliest childhood he was fond of creeping into a corner to read, and yet he was a general favorite all the while he was at school. He was rarely playful or merry, but any one could see at the first glance that this was not from any sullenness. On the contrary he was bright and good-tempered. He never tried to show off among his schoolfellows. Perhaps because of this, he was never afraid of any one, yet the boys immediately understood that he was not proud of his fearlessness and seemed to be unaware that he was bold and courageous. He never resented an insult. It would happen that an hour after the offense he would address the offender or answer some question with as trustful and candid an expression as though nothing had happened between them. And it was not that he seemed to have forgotten or intentionally forgiven the affront, but simply that he did not regard it as an affront, and this completely conquered and captivated the boys. He had one characteristic which made all his schoolfellows from the bottom class to the top want to mock at him, not from malice but because it amused them. This characteristic was a wild fanatical modesty and chastity. He could not bear to hear certain words and certain conversations about women. There are “certain” words and conversations unhappily impossible to eradicate in schools. Boys pure in mind and heart, almost children, are fond of talking in school among themselves, and even aloud, of things, pictures, and images of which even soldiers would sometimes hesitate to speak. More than that, much that soldiers have no knowledge or conception of is familiar to quite young children of our intellectual and higher classes. There is no moral depravity, no real corrupt inner cynicism in it, but there is the appearance of it, and it is often looked upon among them as something refined, subtle, daring, and worthy of imitation. Seeing that Alyosha Karamazov put his fingers in his ears when they talked of “that,” they used sometimes to crowd round him, pull his hands away, and shout nastiness into both ears, while he struggled, slipped to the floor, tried to hide himself without uttering one word of abuse, enduring their insults in silence. But at last they left him alone and gave up taunting him with being a “regular girl,” and what’s more they looked upon it with compassion as a weakness. He was always one of the best in the class but was never first.\n\nAt the time of Yefim Petrovitch’s death Alyosha had two more years to complete at the provincial gymnasium. The inconsolable widow went almost immediately after his death for a long visit to Italy with her whole family, which consisted only of women and girls. Alyosha went to live in the house of two distant relations of Yefim Petrovitch, ladies whom he had never seen before. On what terms he lived with them he did not know himself. It was very characteristic of him, indeed, that he never cared at whose expense he was living. In that respect he was a striking contrast to his elder brother Ivan, who struggled with poverty for his first two years in the university, maintained himself by his own efforts, and had from childhood been bitterly conscious of living at the expense of his benefactor. But this strange trait in Alyosha’s character must not, I think, be criticized too severely, for at the slightest acquaintance with him any one would have perceived that Alyosha was one of those youths, almost of the type of religious enthusiast, who, if they were suddenly to come into possession of a large fortune, would not hesitate to give it away for the asking, either for good works or perhaps to a clever rogue. In general he seemed scarcely to know the value of money, not, of course, in a literal sense. When he was given pocket-money, which he never asked for, he was either terribly careless of it so that it was gone in a moment, or he kept it for weeks together, not knowing what to do with it.\n\nIn later years Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, a man very sensitive on the score of money and bourgeois honesty, pronounced the following judgment, after getting to know Alyosha:\n\n“Here is perhaps the one man in the world whom you might leave alone without a penny, in the center of an unknown town of a million inhabitants, and he would not come to harm, he would not die of cold and hunger, for he would be fed and sheltered at once; and if he were not, he would find a shelter for himself, and it would cost him no effort or humiliation. And to shelter him would be no burden, but, on the contrary, would probably be looked on as a pleasure.”\n\nHe did not finish his studies at the gymnasium. A year before the end of the course he suddenly announced to the ladies that he was going to see his father about a plan which had occurred to him. They were sorry and unwilling to let him go. The journey was not an expensive one, and the ladies would not let him pawn his watch, a parting present from his benefactor’s family. They provided him liberally with money and even fitted him out with new clothes and linen. But he returned half the money they gave him, saying that he intended to go third class. On his arrival in the town he made no answer to his father’s first inquiry why he had come before completing his studies, and seemed, so they say, unusually thoughtful. It soon became apparent that he was looking for his mother’s tomb. He practically acknowledged at the time that that was the only object of his visit. But it can hardly have been the whole reason of it. It is more probable that he himself did not understand and could not explain what had suddenly arisen in his soul, and drawn him irresistibly into a new, unknown, but inevitable path. Fyodor Pavlovitch could not show him where his second wife was buried, for he had never visited her grave since he had thrown earth upon her coffin, and in the course of years had entirely forgotten where she was buried.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch, by the way, had for some time previously not been living in our town. Three or four years after his wife’s death he had gone to the south of Russia and finally turned up in Odessa, where he spent several years. He made the acquaintance at first, in his own words, “of a lot of low Jews, Jewesses, and Jewkins,” and ended by being received by “Jews high and low alike.” It may be presumed that at this period he developed a peculiar faculty for making and hoarding money. He finally returned to our town only three years before Alyosha’s arrival. His former acquaintances found him looking terribly aged, although he was by no means an old man. He behaved not exactly with more dignity but with more effrontery. The former buffoon showed an insolent propensity for making buffoons of others. His depravity with women was not simply what it used to be, but even more revolting. In a short time he opened a great number of new taverns in the district. It was evident that he had perhaps a hundred thousand roubles or not much less. Many of the inhabitants of the town and district were soon in his debt, and, of course, had given good security. Of late, too, he looked somehow bloated and seemed more irresponsible, more uneven, had sunk into a sort of incoherence, used to begin one thing and go on with another, as though he were letting himself go altogether. He was more and more frequently drunk. And, if it had not been for the same servant Grigory, who by that time had aged considerably too, and used to look after him sometimes almost like a tutor, Fyodor Pavlovitch might have got into terrible scrapes. Alyosha’s arrival seemed to affect even his moral side, as though something had awakened in this prematurely old man which had long been dead in his soul.\n\n“Do you know,” he used often to say, looking at Alyosha, “that you are like her, ‘the crazy woman’”—that was what he used to call his dead wife, Alyosha’s mother. Grigory it was who pointed out the “crazy woman’s” grave to Alyosha. He took him to our town cemetery and showed him in a remote corner a cast-iron tombstone, cheap but decently kept, on which were inscribed the name and age of the deceased and the date of her death, and below a four-lined verse, such as are commonly used on old-fashioned middle-class tombs. To Alyosha’s amazement this tomb turned out to be Grigory’s doing. He had put it up on the poor “crazy woman’s” grave at his own expense, after Fyodor Pavlovitch, whom he had often pestered about the grave, had gone to Odessa, abandoning the grave and all his memories. Alyosha showed no particular emotion at the sight of his mother’s grave. He only listened to Grigory’s minute and solemn account of the erection of the tomb; he stood with bowed head and walked away without uttering a word. It was perhaps a year before he visited the cemetery again. But this little episode was not without an influence upon Fyodor Pavlovitch—and a very original one. He suddenly took a thousand roubles to our monastery to pay for requiems for the soul of his wife; but not for the second, Alyosha’s mother, the “crazy woman,” but for the first, Adelaïda Ivanovna, who used to thrash him. In the evening of the same day he got drunk and abused the monks to Alyosha. He himself was far from being religious; he had probably never put a penny candle before the image of a saint. Strange impulses of sudden feeling and sudden thought are common in such types.\n\nI have mentioned already that he looked bloated. His countenance at this time bore traces of something that testified unmistakably to the life he had led. Besides the long fleshy bags under his little, always insolent, suspicious, and ironical eyes; besides the multitude of deep wrinkles in his little fat face, the Adam’s apple hung below his sharp chin like a great, fleshy goiter, which gave him a peculiar, repulsive, sensual appearance; add to that a long rapacious mouth with full lips, between which could be seen little stumps of black decayed teeth. He slobbered every time he began to speak. He was fond indeed of making fun of his own face, though, I believe, he was well satisfied with it. He used particularly to point to his nose, which was not very large, but very delicate and conspicuously aquiline. “A regular Roman nose,” he used to say, “with my goiter I’ve quite the countenance of an ancient Roman patrician of the decadent period.” He seemed proud of it.\n\nNot long after visiting his mother’s grave Alyosha suddenly announced that he wanted to enter the monastery, and that the monks were willing to receive him as a novice. He explained that this was his strong desire, and that he was solemnly asking his consent as his father. The old man knew that the elder Zossima, who was living in the monastery hermitage, had made a special impression upon his “gentle boy.”\n\n“That is the most honest monk among them, of course,” he observed, after listening in thoughtful silence to Alyosha, and seeming scarcely surprised at his request. “H’m!... So that’s where you want to be, my gentle boy?”\n\nHe was half drunk, and suddenly he grinned his slow half-drunken grin, which was not without a certain cunning and tipsy slyness. “H’m!... I had a presentiment that you would end in something like this. Would you believe it? You were making straight for it. Well, to be sure you have your own two thousand. That’s a dowry for you. And I’ll never desert you, my angel. And I’ll pay what’s wanted for you there, if they ask for it. But, of course, if they don’t ask, why should we worry them? What do you say? You know, you spend money like a canary, two grains a week. H’m!... Do you know that near one monastery there’s a place outside the town where every baby knows there are none but ‘the monks’ wives’ living, as they are called. Thirty women, I believe. I have been there myself. You know, it’s interesting in its own way, of course, as a variety. The worst of it is it’s awfully Russian. There are no French women there. Of course they could get them fast enough, they have plenty of money. If they get to hear of it they’ll come along. Well, there’s nothing of that sort here, no ‘monks’ wives,’ and two hundred monks. They’re honest. They keep the fasts. I admit it.... H’m.... So you want to be a monk? And do you know I’m sorry to lose you, Alyosha; would you believe it, I’ve really grown fond of you? Well, it’s a good opportunity. You’ll pray for us sinners; we have sinned too much here. I’ve always been thinking who would pray for me, and whether there’s any one in the world to do it. My dear boy, I’m awfully stupid about that. You wouldn’t believe it. Awfully. You see, however stupid I am about it, I keep thinking, I keep thinking—from time to time, of course, not all the while. It’s impossible, I think, for the devils to forget to drag me down to hell with their hooks when I die. Then I wonder—hooks? Where would they get them? What of? Iron hooks? Where do they forge them? Have they a foundry there of some sort? The monks in the monastery probably believe that there’s a ceiling in hell, for instance. Now I’m ready to believe in hell, but without a ceiling. It makes it more refined, more enlightened, more Lutheran that is. And, after all, what does it matter whether it has a ceiling or hasn’t? But, do you know, there’s a damnable question involved in it? If there’s no ceiling there can be no hooks, and if there are no hooks it all breaks down, which is unlikely again, for then there would be none to drag me down to hell, and if they don’t drag me down what justice is there in the world? Il faudrait les inventer, those hooks, on purpose for me alone, for, if you only knew, Alyosha, what a blackguard I am.”\n\n“But there are no hooks there,” said Alyosha, looking gently and seriously at his father.\n\n“Yes, yes, only the shadows of hooks, I know, I know. That’s how a Frenchman described hell: ‘J’ai vu l’ombre d’un cocher qui avec l’ombre d’une brosse frottait l’ombre d’une carrosse.’ How do you know there are no hooks, darling? When you’ve lived with the monks you’ll sing a different tune. But go and get at the truth there, and then come and tell me. Anyway it’s easier going to the other world if one knows what there is there. Besides, it will be more seemly for you with the monks than here with me, with a drunken old man and young harlots ... though you’re like an angel, nothing touches you. And I dare say nothing will touch you there. That’s why I let you go, because I hope for that. You’ve got all your wits about you. You will burn and you will burn out; you will be healed and come back again. And I will wait for you. I feel that you’re the only creature in the world who has not condemned me. My dear boy, I feel it, you know. I can’t help feeling it.”\n\nAnd he even began blubbering. He was sentimental. He was wicked and sentimental.\n\n# Chapter V. Elders\n\nSome of my readers may imagine that my young man was a sickly, ecstatic, poorly developed creature, a pale, consumptive dreamer. On the contrary, Alyosha was at this time a well-grown, red-cheeked, clear-eyed lad of nineteen, radiant with health. He was very handsome, too, graceful, moderately tall, with hair of a dark brown, with a regular, rather long, oval-shaped face, and wide-set dark gray, shining eyes; he was very thoughtful, and apparently very serene. I shall be told, perhaps, that red cheeks are not incompatible with fanaticism and mysticism; but I fancy that Alyosha was more of a realist than any one. Oh! no doubt, in the monastery he fully believed in miracles, but, to my thinking, miracles are never a stumbling-block to the realist. It is not miracles that dispose realists to belief. The genuine realist, if he is an unbeliever, will always find strength and ability to disbelieve in the miraculous, and if he is confronted with a miracle as an irrefutable fact he would rather disbelieve his own senses than admit the fact. Even if he admits it, he admits it as a fact of nature till then unrecognized by him. Faith does not, in the realist, spring from the miracle but the miracle from faith. If the realist once believes, then he is bound by his very realism to admit the miraculous also. The Apostle Thomas said that he would not believe till he saw, but when he did see he said, “My Lord and my God!” Was it the miracle forced him to believe? Most likely not, but he believed solely because he desired to believe and possibly he fully believed in his secret heart even when he said, “I do not believe till I see.”\n\nI shall be told, perhaps, that Alyosha was stupid, undeveloped, had not finished his studies, and so on. That he did not finish his studies is true, but to say that he was stupid or dull would be a great injustice. I’ll simply repeat what I have said above. He entered upon this path only because, at that time, it alone struck his imagination and presented itself to him as offering an ideal means of escape for his soul from darkness to light. Add to that that he was to some extent a youth of our last epoch—that is, honest in nature, desiring the truth, seeking for it and believing in it, and seeking to serve it at once with all the strength of his soul, seeking for immediate action, and ready to sacrifice everything, life itself, for it. Though these young men unhappily fail to understand that the sacrifice of life is, in many cases, the easiest of all sacrifices, and that to sacrifice, for instance, five or six years of their seething youth to hard and tedious study, if only to multiply tenfold their powers of serving the truth and the cause they have set before them as their goal—such a sacrifice is utterly beyond the strength of many of them. The path Alyosha chose was a path going in the opposite direction, but he chose it with the same thirst for swift achievement. As soon as he reflected seriously he was convinced of the existence of God and immortality, and at once he instinctively said to himself: “I want to live for immortality, and I will accept no compromise.” In the same way, if he had decided that God and immortality did not exist, he would at once have become an atheist and a socialist. For socialism is not merely the labor question, it is before all things the atheistic question, the question of the form taken by atheism to-day, the question of the tower of Babel built without God, not to mount to heaven from earth but to set up heaven on earth. Alyosha would have found it strange and impossible to go on living as before. It is written: “Give all that thou hast to the poor and follow Me, if thou wouldst be perfect.”\n\nAlyosha said to himself: “I can’t give two roubles instead of ‘all,’ and only go to mass instead of ‘following Him.’” Perhaps his memories of childhood brought back our monastery, to which his mother may have taken him to mass. Perhaps the slanting sunlight and the holy image to which his poor “crazy” mother had held him up still acted upon his imagination. Brooding on these things he may have come to us perhaps only to see whether here he could sacrifice all or only “two roubles,” and in the monastery he met this elder. I must digress to explain what an “elder” is in Russian monasteries, and I am sorry that I do not feel very competent to do so. I will try, however, to give a superficial account of it in a few words. Authorities on the subject assert that the institution of “elders” is of recent date, not more than a hundred years old in our monasteries, though in the orthodox East, especially in Sinai and Athos, it has existed over a thousand years. It is maintained that it existed in ancient times in Russia also, but through the calamities which overtook Russia—the Tartars, civil war, the interruption of relations with the East after the destruction of Constantinople—this institution fell into oblivion. It was revived among us towards the end of last century by one of the great “ascetics,” as they called him, Païssy Velitchkovsky, and his disciples. But to this day it exists in few monasteries only, and has sometimes been almost persecuted as an innovation in Russia. It flourished especially in the celebrated Kozelski Optin Monastery. When and how it was introduced into our monastery I cannot say. There had already been three such elders and Zossima was the last of them. But he was almost dying of weakness and disease, and they had no one to take his place. The question for our monastery was an important one, for it had not been distinguished by anything in particular till then: they had neither relics of saints, nor wonder-working ikons, nor glorious traditions, nor historical exploits. It had flourished and been glorious all over Russia through its elders, to see and hear whom pilgrims had flocked for thousands of miles from all parts.\n\nWhat was such an elder? An elder was one who took your soul, your will, into his soul and his will. When you choose an elder, you renounce your own will and yield it to him in complete submission, complete self-abnegation. This novitiate, this terrible school of abnegation, is undertaken voluntarily, in the hope of self-conquest, of self-mastery, in order, after a life of obedience, to attain perfect freedom, that is, from self; to escape the lot of those who have lived their whole life without finding their true selves in themselves. This institution of elders is not founded on theory, but was established in the East from the practice of a thousand years. The obligations due to an elder are not the ordinary “obedience” which has always existed in our Russian monasteries. The obligation involves confession to the elder by all who have submitted themselves to him, and to the indissoluble bond between him and them.\n\nThe story is told, for instance, that in the early days of Christianity one such novice, failing to fulfill some command laid upon him by his elder, left his monastery in Syria and went to Egypt. There, after great exploits, he was found worthy at last to suffer torture and a martyr’s death for the faith. When the Church, regarding him as a saint, was burying him, suddenly, at the deacon’s exhortation, “Depart all ye unbaptized,” the coffin containing the martyr’s body left its place and was cast forth from the church, and this took place three times. And only at last they learnt that this holy man had broken his vow of obedience and left his elder, and, therefore, could not be forgiven without the elder’s absolution in spite of his great deeds. Only after this could the funeral take place. This, of course, is only an old legend. But here is a recent instance.\n\nA monk was suddenly commanded by his elder to quit Athos, which he loved as a sacred place and a haven of refuge, and to go first to Jerusalem to do homage to the Holy Places and then to go to the north to Siberia: “There is the place for thee and not here.” The monk, overwhelmed with sorrow, went to the Œcumenical Patriarch at Constantinople and besought him to release him from his obedience. But the Patriarch replied that not only was he unable to release him, but there was not and could not be on earth a power which could release him except the elder who had himself laid that duty upon him. In this way the elders are endowed in certain cases with unbounded and inexplicable authority. That is why in many of our monasteries the institution was at first resisted almost to persecution. Meantime the elders immediately began to be highly esteemed among the people. Masses of the ignorant people as well as men of distinction flocked, for instance, to the elders of our monastery to confess their doubts, their sins, and their sufferings, and ask for counsel and admonition. Seeing this, the opponents of the elders declared that the sacrament of confession was being arbitrarily and frivolously degraded, though the continual opening of the heart to the elder by the monk or the layman had nothing of the character of the sacrament. In the end, however, the institution of elders has been retained and is becoming established in Russian monasteries. It is true, perhaps, that this instrument which had stood the test of a thousand years for the moral regeneration of a man from slavery to freedom and to moral perfectibility may be a two-edged weapon and it may lead some not to humility and complete self-control but to the most Satanic pride, that is, to bondage and not to freedom.\n\nThe elder Zossima was sixty-five. He came of a family of landowners, had been in the army in early youth, and served in the Caucasus as an officer. He had, no doubt, impressed Alyosha by some peculiar quality of his soul. Alyosha lived in the cell of the elder, who was very fond of him and let him wait upon him. It must be noted that Alyosha was bound by no obligation and could go where he pleased and be absent for whole days. Though he wore the monastic dress it was voluntarily, not to be different from others. No doubt he liked to do so. Possibly his youthful imagination was deeply stirred by the power and fame of his elder. It was said that so many people had for years past come to confess their sins to Father Zossima and to entreat him for words of advice and healing, that he had acquired the keenest intuition and could tell from an unknown face what a new-comer wanted, and what was the suffering on his conscience. He sometimes astounded and almost alarmed his visitors by his knowledge of their secrets before they had spoken a word.\n\nAlyosha noticed that many, almost all, went in to the elder for the first time with apprehension and uneasiness, but came out with bright and happy faces. Alyosha was particularly struck by the fact that Father Zossima was not at all stern. On the contrary, he was always almost gay. The monks used to say that he was more drawn to those who were more sinful, and the greater the sinner the more he loved him. There were, no doubt, up to the end of his life, among the monks some who hated and envied him, but they were few in number and they were silent, though among them were some of great dignity in the monastery, one, for instance, of the older monks distinguished for his strict keeping of fasts and vows of silence. But the majority were on Father Zossima’s side and very many of them loved him with all their hearts, warmly and sincerely. Some were almost fanatically devoted to him, and declared, though not quite aloud, that he was a saint, that there could be no doubt of it, and, seeing that his end was near, they anticipated miracles and great glory to the monastery in the immediate future from his relics. Alyosha had unquestioning faith in the miraculous power of the elder, just as he had unquestioning faith in the story of the coffin that flew out of the church. He saw many who came with sick children or relatives and besought the elder to lay hands on them and to pray over them, return shortly after—some the next day—and, falling in tears at the elder’s feet, thank him for healing their sick.\n\nWhether they had really been healed or were simply better in the natural course of the disease was a question which did not exist for Alyosha, for he fully believed in the spiritual power of his teacher and rejoiced in his fame, in his glory, as though it were his own triumph. His heart throbbed, and he beamed, as it were, all over when the elder came out to the gates of the hermitage into the waiting crowd of pilgrims of the humbler class who had flocked from all parts of Russia on purpose to see the elder and obtain his blessing. They fell down before him, wept, kissed his feet, kissed the earth on which he stood, and wailed, while the women held up their children to him and brought him the sick “possessed with devils.” The elder spoke to them, read a brief prayer over them, blessed them, and dismissed them. Of late he had become so weak through attacks of illness that he was sometimes unable to leave his cell, and the pilgrims waited for him to come out for several days. Alyosha did not wonder why they loved him so, why they fell down before him and wept with emotion merely at seeing his face. Oh! he understood that for the humble soul of the Russian peasant, worn out by grief and toil, and still more by the everlasting injustice and everlasting sin, his own and the world’s, it was the greatest need and comfort to find some one or something holy to fall down before and worship.\n\n“Among us there is sin, injustice, and temptation, but yet, somewhere on earth there is some one holy and exalted. He has the truth; he knows the truth; so it is not dead upon the earth; so it will come one day to us, too, and rule over all the earth according to the promise.”\n\nAlyosha knew that this was just how the people felt and even reasoned. He understood it, but that the elder Zossima was this saint and custodian of God’s truth—of that he had no more doubt than the weeping peasants and the sick women who held out their children to the elder. The conviction that after his death the elder would bring extraordinary glory to the monastery was even stronger in Alyosha than in any one there, and, of late, a kind of deep flame of inner ecstasy burnt more and more strongly in his heart. He was not at all troubled at this elder’s standing as a solitary example before him.\n\n“No matter. He is holy. He carries in his heart the secret of renewal for all: that power which will, at last, establish truth on the earth, and all men will be holy and love one another, and there will be no more rich nor poor, no exalted nor humbled, but all will be as the children of God, and the true Kingdom of Christ will come.” That was the dream in Alyosha’s heart.\n\nThe arrival of his two brothers, whom he had not known till then, seemed to make a great impression on Alyosha. He more quickly made friends with his half-brother Dmitri (though he arrived later) than with his own brother Ivan. He was extremely interested in his brother Ivan, but when the latter had been two months in the town, though they had met fairly often, they were still not intimate. Alyosha was naturally silent, and he seemed to be expecting something, ashamed about something, while his brother Ivan, though Alyosha noticed at first that he looked long and curiously at him, seemed soon to have left off thinking of him. Alyosha noticed it with some embarrassment. He ascribed his brother’s indifference at first to the disparity of their age and education. But he also wondered whether the absence of curiosity and sympathy in Ivan might be due to some other cause entirely unknown to him. He kept fancying that Ivan was absorbed in something—something inward and important—that he was striving towards some goal, perhaps very hard to attain, and that that was why he had no thought for him. Alyosha wondered, too, whether there was not some contempt on the part of the learned atheist for him—a foolish novice. He knew for certain that his brother was an atheist. He could not take offense at this contempt, if it existed; yet, with an uneasy embarrassment which he did not himself understand, he waited for his brother to come nearer to him. Dmitri used to speak of Ivan with the deepest respect and with a peculiar earnestness. From him Alyosha learnt all the details of the important affair which had of late formed such a close and remarkable bond between the two elder brothers. Dmitri’s enthusiastic references to Ivan were the more striking in Alyosha’s eyes since Dmitri was, compared with Ivan, almost uneducated, and the two brothers were such a contrast in personality and character that it would be difficult to find two men more unlike.\n\nIt was at this time that the meeting, or, rather gathering of the members of this inharmonious family took place in the cell of the elder who had such an extraordinary influence on Alyosha. The pretext for this gathering was a false one. It was at this time that the discord between Dmitri and his father seemed at its acutest stage and their relations had become insufferably strained. Fyodor Pavlovitch seems to have been the first to suggest, apparently in joke, that they should all meet in Father Zossima’s cell, and that, without appealing to his direct intervention, they might more decently come to an understanding under the conciliating influence of the elder’s presence. Dmitri, who had never seen the elder, naturally supposed that his father was trying to intimidate him, but, as he secretly blamed himself for his outbursts of temper with his father on several recent occasions, he accepted the challenge. It must be noted that he was not, like Ivan, staying with his father, but living apart at the other end of the town. It happened that Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, who was staying in the district at the time, caught eagerly at the idea. A Liberal of the forties and fifties, a freethinker and atheist, he may have been led on by boredom or the hope of frivolous diversion. He was suddenly seized with the desire to see the monastery and the holy man. As his lawsuit with the monastery still dragged on, he made it the pretext for seeing the Superior, in order to attempt to settle it amicably. A visitor coming with such laudable intentions might be received with more attention and consideration than if he came from simple curiosity. Influences from within the monastery were brought to bear on the elder, who of late had scarcely left his cell, and had been forced by illness to deny even his ordinary visitors. In the end he consented to see them, and the day was fixed.\n\n“Who has made me a judge over them?” was all he said, smilingly, to Alyosha.\n\nAlyosha was much perturbed when he heard of the proposed visit. Of all the wrangling, quarrelsome party, Dmitri was the only one who could regard the interview seriously. All the others would come from frivolous motives, perhaps insulting to the elder. Alyosha was well aware of that. Ivan and Miüsov would come from curiosity, perhaps of the coarsest kind, while his father might be contemplating some piece of buffoonery. Though he said nothing, Alyosha thoroughly understood his father. The boy, I repeat, was far from being so simple as every one thought him. He awaited the day with a heavy heart. No doubt he was always pondering in his mind how the family discord could be ended. But his chief anxiety concerned the elder. He trembled for him, for his glory, and dreaded any affront to him, especially the refined, courteous irony of Miüsov and the supercilious half-utterances of the highly educated Ivan. He even wanted to venture on warning the elder, telling him something about them, but, on second thoughts, said nothing. He only sent word the day before, through a friend, to his brother Dmitri, that he loved him and expected him to keep his promise. Dmitri wondered, for he could not remember what he had promised, but he answered by letter that he would do his utmost not to let himself be provoked “by vileness,” but that, although he had a deep respect for the elder and for his brother Ivan, he was convinced that the meeting was either a trap for him or an unworthy farce.\n\n“Nevertheless I would rather bite out my tongue than be lacking in respect to the sainted man whom you reverence so highly,” he wrote in conclusion. Alyosha was not greatly cheered by the letter.\n\n# Book II. An Unfortunate Gathering Chapter I. They Arrive At The Monastery\n\nIt was a warm, bright day at the end of August. The interview with the elder had been fixed for half-past eleven, immediately after late mass. Our visitors did not take part in the service, but arrived just as it was over. First an elegant open carriage, drawn by two valuable horses, drove up with Miüsov and a distant relative of his, a young man of twenty, called Pyotr Fomitch Kalganov. This young man was preparing to enter the university. Miüsov, with whom he was staying for the time, was trying to persuade him to go abroad to the university of Zurich or Jena. The young man was still undecided. He was thoughtful and absent-minded. He was nice-looking, strongly built, and rather tall. There was a strange fixity in his gaze at times. Like all very absent-minded people he would sometimes stare at a person without seeing him. He was silent and rather awkward, but sometimes, when he was alone with any one, he became talkative and effusive, and would laugh at anything or nothing. But his animation vanished as quickly as it appeared. He was always well and even elaborately dressed; he had already some independent fortune and expectations of much more. He was a friend of Alyosha’s.\n\nIn an ancient, jolting, but roomy, hired carriage, with a pair of old pinkish-gray horses, a long way behind Miüsov’s carriage, came Fyodor Pavlovitch, with his son Ivan. Dmitri was late, though he had been informed of the time the evening before. The visitors left their carriage at the hotel, outside the precincts, and went to the gates of the monastery on foot. Except Fyodor Pavlovitch, none of the party had ever seen the monastery, and Miüsov had probably not even been to church for thirty years. He looked about him with curiosity, together with assumed ease. But, except the church and the domestic buildings, though these too were ordinary enough, he found nothing of interest in the interior of the monastery. The last of the worshippers were coming out of the church, bareheaded and crossing themselves. Among the humbler people were a few of higher rank—two or three ladies and a very old general. They were all staying at the hotel. Our visitors were at once surrounded by beggars, but none of them gave them anything, except young Kalganov, who took a ten-copeck piece out of his purse, and, nervous and embarrassed—God knows why!—hurriedly gave it to an old woman, saying: “Divide it equally.” None of his companions made any remark upon it, so that he had no reason to be embarrassed; but, perceiving this, he was even more overcome.\n\nIt was strange that their arrival did not seem expected, and that they were not received with special honor, though one of them had recently made a donation of a thousand roubles, while another was a very wealthy and highly cultured landowner, upon whom all in the monastery were in a sense dependent, as a decision of the lawsuit might at any moment put their fishing rights in his hands. Yet no official personage met them.\n\nMiüsov looked absent-mindedly at the tombstones round the church, and was on the point of saying that the dead buried here must have paid a pretty penny for the right of lying in this “holy place,” but refrained. His liberal irony was rapidly changing almost into anger.\n\n“Who the devil is there to ask in this imbecile place? We must find out, for time is passing,” he observed suddenly, as though speaking to himself.\n\nAll at once there came up a bald-headed, elderly man with ingratiating little eyes, wearing a full, summer overcoat. Lifting his hat, he introduced himself with a honeyed lisp as Maximov, a landowner of Tula. He at once entered into our visitors’ difficulty.\n\n“Father Zossima lives in the hermitage, apart, four hundred paces from the monastery, the other side of the copse.”\n\n“I know it’s the other side of the copse,” observed Fyodor Pavlovitch, “but we don’t remember the way. It is a long time since we’ve been here.”\n\n“This way, by this gate, and straight across the copse ... the copse. Come with me, won’t you? I’ll show you. I have to go.... I am going myself. This way, this way.”\n\nThey came out of the gate and turned towards the copse. Maximov, a man of sixty, ran rather than walked, turning sideways to stare at them all, with an incredible degree of nervous curiosity. His eyes looked starting out of his head.\n\n“You see, we have come to the elder upon business of our own,” observed Miüsov severely. “That personage has granted us an audience, so to speak, and so, though we thank you for showing us the way, we cannot ask you to accompany us.”\n\n“I’ve been there. I’ve been already; un chevalier parfait,” and Maximov snapped his fingers in the air.\n\n“Who is a chevalier?” asked Miüsov.\n\n“The elder, the splendid elder, the elder! The honor and glory of the monastery, Zossima. Such an elder!”\n\nBut his incoherent talk was cut short by a very pale, wan-looking monk of medium height, wearing a monk’s cap, who overtook them. Fyodor Pavlovitch and Miüsov stopped.\n\nThe monk, with an extremely courteous, profound bow, announced:\n\n“The Father Superior invites all of you gentlemen to dine with him after your visit to the hermitage. At one o’clock, not later. And you also,” he added, addressing Maximov.\n\n“That I certainly will, without fail,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, hugely delighted at the invitation. “And, believe me, we’ve all given our word to behave properly here.... And you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, will you go, too?”\n\n“Yes, of course. What have I come for but to study all the customs here? The only obstacle to me is your company....”\n\n“Yes, Dmitri Fyodorovitch is non-existent as yet.”\n\n“It would be a capital thing if he didn’t turn up. Do you suppose I like all this business, and in your company, too? So we will come to dinner. Thank the Father Superior,” he said to the monk.\n\n“No, it is my duty now to conduct you to the elder,” answered the monk.\n\n“If so I’ll go straight to the Father Superior—to the Father Superior,” babbled Maximov.\n\n“The Father Superior is engaged just now. But as you please—” the monk hesitated.\n\n“Impertinent old man!” Miüsov observed aloud, while Maximov ran back to the monastery.\n\n“He’s like von Sohn,” Fyodor Pavlovitch said suddenly.\n\n“Is that all you can think of?... In what way is he like von Sohn? Have you ever seen von Sohn?”\n\n“I’ve seen his portrait. It’s not the features, but something indefinable. He’s a second von Sohn. I can always tell from the physiognomy.”\n\n“Ah, I dare say you are a connoisseur in that. But, look here, Fyodor Pavlovitch, you said just now that we had given our word to behave properly. Remember it. I advise you to control yourself. But, if you begin to play the fool I don’t intend to be associated with you here.... You see what a man he is”—he turned to the monk—“I’m afraid to go among decent people with him.” A fine smile, not without a certain slyness, came on to the pale, bloodless lips of the monk, but he made no reply, and was evidently silent from a sense of his own dignity. Miüsov frowned more than ever.\n\n“Oh, devil take them all! An outer show elaborated through centuries, and nothing but charlatanism and nonsense underneath,” flashed through Miüsov’s mind.\n\n“Here’s the hermitage. We’ve arrived,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch. “The gates are shut.”\n\nAnd he repeatedly made the sign of the cross to the saints painted above and on the sides of the gates.\n\n“When you go to Rome you must do as the Romans do. Here in this hermitage there are twenty-five saints being saved. They look at one another, and eat cabbages. And not one woman goes in at this gate. That’s what is remarkable. And that really is so. But I did hear that the elder receives ladies,” he remarked suddenly to the monk.\n\n“Women of the people are here too now, lying in the portico there waiting. But for ladies of higher rank two rooms have been built adjoining the portico, but outside the precincts—you can see the windows—and the elder goes out to them by an inner passage when he is well enough. They are always outside the precincts. There is a Harkov lady, Madame Hohlakov, waiting there now with her sick daughter. Probably he has promised to come out to her, though of late he has been so weak that he has hardly shown himself even to the people.”\n\n“So then there are loopholes, after all, to creep out of the hermitage to the ladies. Don’t suppose, holy father, that I mean any harm. But do you know that at Athos not only the visits of women are not allowed, but no creature of the female sex—no hens, nor turkey-hens, nor cows.”\n\n“Fyodor Pavlovitch, I warn you I shall go back and leave you here. They’ll turn you out when I’m gone.”\n\n“But I’m not interfering with you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch. Look,” he cried suddenly, stepping within the precincts, “what a vale of roses they live in!”\n\nThough there were no roses now, there were numbers of rare and beautiful autumn flowers growing wherever there was space for them, and evidently tended by a skillful hand; there were flower-beds round the church, and between the tombs; and the one-storied wooden house where the elder lived was also surrounded with flowers.\n\n“And was it like this in the time of the last elder, Varsonofy? He didn’t care for such elegance. They say he used to jump up and thrash even ladies with a stick,” observed Fyodor Pavlovitch, as he went up the steps.\n\n“The elder Varsonofy did sometimes seem rather strange, but a great deal that’s told is foolishness. He never thrashed any one,” answered the monk. “Now, gentlemen, if you will wait a minute I will announce you.”\n\n“Fyodor Pavlovitch, for the last time, your compact, do you hear? Behave properly or I will pay you out!” Miüsov had time to mutter again.\n\n“I can’t think why you are so agitated,” Fyodor Pavlovitch observed sarcastically. “Are you uneasy about your sins? They say he can tell by one’s eyes what one has come about. And what a lot you think of their opinion! you, a Parisian, and so advanced. I’m surprised at you.”\n\nBut Miüsov had no time to reply to this sarcasm. They were asked to come in. He walked in, somewhat irritated.\n\n“Now, I know myself, I am annoyed, I shall lose my temper and begin to quarrel—and lower myself and my ideas,” he reflected.\n\n# Chapter II. The Old Buffoon\n\nThey entered the room almost at the same moment that the elder came in from his bedroom. There were already in the cell, awaiting the elder, two monks of the hermitage, one the Father Librarian, and the other Father Païssy, a very learned man, so they said, in delicate health, though not old. There was also a tall young man, who looked about two and twenty, standing in the corner throughout the interview. He had a broad, fresh face, and clever, observant, narrow brown eyes, and was wearing ordinary dress. He was a divinity student, living under the protection of the monastery. His expression was one of unquestioning, but self-respecting, reverence. Being in a subordinate and dependent position, and so not on an equality with the guests, he did not greet them with a bow.\n\nFather Zossima was accompanied by a novice, and by Alyosha. The two monks rose and greeted him with a very deep bow, touching the ground with their fingers; then kissed his hand. Blessing them, the elder replied with as deep a reverence to them, and asked their blessing. The whole ceremony was performed very seriously and with an appearance of feeling, not like an everyday rite. But Miüsov fancied that it was all done with intentional impressiveness. He stood in front of the other visitors. He ought—he had reflected upon it the evening before—from simple politeness, since it was the custom here, to have gone up to receive the elder’s blessing, even if he did not kiss his hand. But when he saw all this bowing and kissing on the part of the monks he instantly changed his mind. With dignified gravity he made a rather deep, conventional bow, and moved away to a chair. Fyodor Pavlovitch did the same, mimicking Miüsov like an ape. Ivan bowed with great dignity and courtesy, but he too kept his hands at his sides, while Kalganov was so confused that he did not bow at all. The elder let fall the hand raised to bless them, and bowing to them again, asked them all to sit down. The blood rushed to Alyosha’s cheeks. He was ashamed. His forebodings were coming true.\n\nFather Zossima sat down on a very old-fashioned mahogany sofa, covered with leather, and made his visitors sit down in a row along the opposite wall on four mahogany chairs, covered with shabby black leather. The monks sat, one at the door and the other at the window. The divinity student, the novice, and Alyosha remained standing. The cell was not very large and had a faded look. It contained nothing but the most necessary furniture, of coarse and poor quality. There were two pots of flowers in the window, and a number of holy pictures in the corner. Before one huge ancient ikon of the Virgin a lamp was burning. Near it were two other holy pictures in shining settings, and, next them, carved cherubims, china eggs, a Catholic cross of ivory, with a Mater Dolorosa embracing it, and several foreign engravings from the great Italian artists of past centuries. Next to these costly and artistic engravings were several of the roughest Russian prints of saints and martyrs, such as are sold for a few farthings at all the fairs. On the other walls were portraits of Russian bishops, past and present.\n\nMiüsov took a cursory glance at all these “conventional” surroundings and bent an intent look upon the elder. He had a high opinion of his own insight, a weakness excusable in him as he was fifty, an age at which a clever man of the world of established position can hardly help taking himself rather seriously. At the first moment he did not like Zossima. There was, indeed, something in the elder’s face which many people besides Miüsov might not have liked. He was a short, bent, little man, with very weak legs, and though he was only sixty-five, he looked at least ten years older. His face was very thin and covered with a network of fine wrinkles, particularly numerous about his eyes, which were small, light-colored, quick, and shining like two bright points. He had a sprinkling of gray hair about his temples. His pointed beard was small and scanty, and his lips, which smiled frequently, were as thin as two threads. His nose was not long, but sharp, like a bird’s beak.\n\n“To all appearances a malicious soul, full of petty pride,” thought Miüsov. He felt altogether dissatisfied with his position.\n\nA cheap little clock on the wall struck twelve hurriedly, and served to begin the conversation.\n\n“Precisely to our time,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, “but no sign of my son, Dmitri. I apologize for him, sacred elder!” (Alyosha shuddered all over at “sacred elder.”) “I am always punctual myself, minute for minute, remembering that punctuality is the courtesy of kings....”\n\n“But you are not a king, anyway,” Miüsov muttered, losing his self-restraint at once.\n\n“Yes; that’s true. I’m not a king, and, would you believe it, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, I was aware of that myself. But, there! I always say the wrong thing. Your reverence,” he cried, with sudden pathos, “you behold before you a buffoon in earnest! I introduce myself as such. It’s an old habit, alas! And if I sometimes talk nonsense out of place it’s with an object, with the object of amusing people and making myself agreeable. One must be agreeable, mustn’t one? I was seven years ago in a little town where I had business, and I made friends with some merchants there. We went to the captain of police because we had to see him about something, and to ask him to dine with us. He was a tall, fat, fair, sulky man, the most dangerous type in such cases. It’s their liver. I went straight up to him, and with the ease of a man of the world, you know, ‘Mr. Ispravnik,’ said I, ‘be our Napravnik.’ ‘What do you mean by Napravnik?’ said he. I saw, at the first half-second, that it had missed fire. He stood there so glum. ‘I wanted to make a joke,’ said I, ‘for the general diversion, as Mr. Napravnik is our well-known Russian orchestra conductor and what we need for the harmony of our undertaking is some one of that sort.’ And I explained my comparison very reasonably, didn’t I? ‘Excuse me,’ said he, ‘I am an Ispravnik, and I do not allow puns to be made on my calling.’ He turned and walked away. I followed him, shouting, ‘Yes, yes, you are an Ispravnik, not a Napravnik.’ ‘No,’ he said, ‘since you called me a Napravnik I am one.’ And would you believe it, it ruined our business! And I’m always like that, always like that. Always injuring myself with my politeness. Once, many years ago, I said to an influential person: ‘Your wife is a ticklish lady,’ in an honorable sense, of the moral qualities, so to speak. But he asked me, ‘Why, have you tickled her?’ I thought I’d be polite, so I couldn’t help saying, ‘Yes,’ and he gave me a fine tickling on the spot. Only that happened long ago, so I’m not ashamed to tell the story. I’m always injuring myself like that.”\n\n“You’re doing it now,” muttered Miüsov, with disgust.\n\nFather Zossima scrutinized them both in silence.\n\n“Am I? Would you believe it, I was aware of that, too, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, and let me tell you, indeed, I foresaw I should as soon as I began to speak. And do you know I foresaw, too, that you’d be the first to remark on it. The minute I see my joke isn’t coming off, your reverence, both my cheeks feel as though they were drawn down to the lower jaw and there is almost a spasm in them. That’s been so since I was young, when I had to make jokes for my living in noblemen’s families. I am an inveterate buffoon, and have been from birth up, your reverence, it’s as though it were a craze in me. I dare say it’s a devil within me. But only a little one. A more serious one would have chosen another lodging. But not your soul, Pyotr Alexandrovitch; you’re not a lodging worth having either. But I do believe—I believe in God, though I have had doubts of late. But now I sit and await words of wisdom. I’m like the philosopher, Diderot, your reverence. Did you ever hear, most Holy Father, how Diderot went to see the Metropolitan Platon, in the time of the Empress Catherine? He went in and said straight out, ‘There is no God.’ To which the great bishop lifted up his finger and answered, ‘The fool hath said in his heart there is no God.’ And he fell down at his feet on the spot. ‘I believe,’ he cried, ‘and will be christened.’ And so he was. Princess Dashkov was his godmother, and Potyomkin his godfather.”\n\n“Fyodor Pavlovitch, this is unbearable! You know you’re telling lies and that that stupid anecdote isn’t true. Why are you playing the fool?” cried Miüsov in a shaking voice.\n\n“I suspected all my life that it wasn’t true,” Fyodor Pavlovitch cried with conviction. “But I’ll tell you the whole truth, gentlemen. Great elder! Forgive me, the last thing about Diderot’s christening I made up just now. I never thought of it before. I made it up to add piquancy. I play the fool, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, to make myself agreeable. Though I really don’t know myself, sometimes, what I do it for. And as for Diderot, I heard as far as ‘the fool hath said in his heart’ twenty times from the gentry about here when I was young. I heard your aunt, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, tell the story. They all believe to this day that the infidel Diderot came to dispute about God with the Metropolitan Platon....”\n\nMiüsov got up, forgetting himself in his impatience. He was furious, and conscious of being ridiculous.\n\nWhat was taking place in the cell was really incredible. For forty or fifty years past, from the times of former elders, no visitors had entered that cell without feelings of the profoundest veneration. Almost every one admitted to the cell felt that a great favor was being shown him. Many remained kneeling during the whole visit. Of those visitors, many had been men of high rank and learning, some even freethinkers, attracted by curiosity, but all without exception had shown the profoundest reverence and delicacy, for here there was no question of money, but only, on the one side love and kindness, and on the other penitence and eager desire to decide some spiritual problem or crisis. So that such buffoonery amazed and bewildered the spectators, or at least some of them. The monks, with unchanged countenances, waited, with earnest attention, to hear what the elder would say, but seemed on the point of standing up, like Miüsov. Alyosha stood, with hanging head, on the verge of tears. What seemed to him strangest of all was that his brother Ivan, on whom alone he had rested his hopes, and who alone had such influence on his father that he could have stopped him, sat now quite unmoved, with downcast eyes, apparently waiting with interest to see how it would end, as though he had nothing to do with it. Alyosha did not dare to look at Rakitin, the divinity student, whom he knew almost intimately. He alone in the monastery knew Rakitin’s thoughts.\n\n“Forgive me,” began Miüsov, addressing Father Zossima, “for perhaps I seem to be taking part in this shameful foolery. I made a mistake in believing that even a man like Fyodor Pavlovitch would understand what was due on a visit to so honored a personage. I did not suppose I should have to apologize simply for having come with him....”\n\nPyotr Alexandrovitch could say no more, and was about to leave the room, overwhelmed with confusion.\n\n“Don’t distress yourself, I beg.” The elder got on to his feeble legs, and taking Pyotr Alexandrovitch by both hands, made him sit down again. “I beg you not to disturb yourself. I particularly beg you to be my guest.” And with a bow he went back and sat down again on his little sofa.\n\n“Great elder, speak! Do I annoy you by my vivacity?” Fyodor Pavlovitch cried suddenly, clutching the arms of his chair in both hands, as though ready to leap up from it if the answer were unfavorable.\n\n“I earnestly beg you, too, not to disturb yourself, and not to be uneasy,” the elder said impressively. “Do not trouble. Make yourself quite at home. And, above all, do not be so ashamed of yourself, for that is at the root of it all.”\n\n“Quite at home? To be my natural self? Oh, that is much too much, but I accept it with grateful joy. Do you know, blessed Father, you’d better not invite me to be my natural self. Don’t risk it.... I will not go so far as that myself. I warn you for your own sake. Well, the rest is still plunged in the mists of uncertainty, though there are people who’d be pleased to describe me for you. I mean that for you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch. But as for you, holy being, let me tell you, I am brimming over with ecstasy.”\n\nHe got up, and throwing up his hands, declaimed, “Blessed be the womb that bare thee, and the paps that gave thee suck—the paps especially. When you said just now, ‘Don’t be so ashamed of yourself, for that is at the root of it all,’ you pierced right through me by that remark, and read me to the core. Indeed, I always feel when I meet people that I am lower than all, and that they all take me for a buffoon. So I say, ‘Let me really play the buffoon. I am not afraid of your opinion, for you are every one of you worse than I am.’ That is why I am a buffoon. It is from shame, great elder, from shame; it’s simply over-sensitiveness that makes me rowdy. If I had only been sure that every one would accept me as the kindest and wisest of men, oh, Lord, what a good man I should have been then! Teacher!” he fell suddenly on his knees, “what must I do to gain eternal life?”\n\nIt was difficult even now to decide whether he was joking or really moved.\n\nFather Zossima, lifting his eyes, looked at him, and said with a smile:\n\n“You have known for a long time what you must do. You have sense enough: don’t give way to drunkenness and incontinence of speech; don’t give way to sensual lust; and, above all, to the love of money. And close your taverns. If you can’t close all, at least two or three. And, above all—don’t lie.”\n\n“You mean about Diderot?”\n\n“No, not about Diderot. Above all, don’t lie to yourself. The man who lies to himself and listens to his own lie comes to such a pass that he cannot distinguish the truth within him, or around him, and so loses all respect for himself and for others. And having no respect he ceases to love, and in order to occupy and distract himself without love he gives way to passions and coarse pleasures, and sinks to bestiality in his vices, all from continual lying to other men and to himself. The man who lies to himself can be more easily offended than any one. You know it is sometimes very pleasant to take offense, isn’t it? A man may know that nobody has insulted him, but that he has invented the insult for himself, has lied and exaggerated to make it picturesque, has caught at a word and made a mountain out of a molehill—he knows that himself, yet he will be the first to take offense, and will revel in his resentment till he feels great pleasure in it, and so pass to genuine vindictiveness. But get up, sit down, I beg you. All this, too, is deceitful posturing....”\n\n“Blessed man! Give me your hand to kiss.”\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch skipped up, and imprinted a rapid kiss on the elder’s thin hand. “It is, it is pleasant to take offense. You said that so well, as I never heard it before. Yes, I have been all my life taking offense, to please myself, taking offense on esthetic grounds, for it is not so much pleasant as distinguished sometimes to be insulted—that you had forgotten, great elder, it is distinguished! I shall make a note of that. But I have been lying, lying positively my whole life long, every day and hour of it. Of a truth, I am a lie, and the father of lies. Though I believe I am not the father of lies. I am getting mixed in my texts. Say, the son of lies, and that will be enough. Only ... my angel ... I may sometimes talk about Diderot! Diderot will do no harm, though sometimes a word will do harm. Great elder, by the way, I was forgetting, though I had been meaning for the last two years to come here on purpose to ask and to find out something. Only do tell Pyotr Alexandrovitch not to interrupt me. Here is my question: Is it true, great Father, that the story is told somewhere in the Lives of the Saints of a holy saint martyred for his faith who, when his head was cut off at last, stood up, picked up his head, and, ‘courteously kissing it,’ walked a long way, carrying it in his hands. Is that true or not, honored Father?”\n\n“No, it is untrue,” said the elder.\n\n“There is nothing of the kind in all the lives of the saints. What saint do you say the story is told of?” asked the Father Librarian.\n\n“I do not know what saint. I do not know, and can’t tell. I was deceived. I was told the story. I had heard it, and do you know who told it? Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov here, who was so angry just now about Diderot. He it was who told the story.”\n\n“I have never told it you, I never speak to you at all.”\n\n“It is true you did not tell me, but you told it when I was present. It was three years ago. I mentioned it because by that ridiculous story you shook my faith, Pyotr Alexandrovitch. You knew nothing of it, but I went home with my faith shaken, and I have been getting more and more shaken ever since. Yes, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, you were the cause of a great fall. That was not a Diderot!”\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch got excited and pathetic, though it was perfectly clear to every one by now that he was playing a part again. Yet Miüsov was stung by his words.\n\n“What nonsense, and it is all nonsense,” he muttered. “I may really have told it, some time or other ... but not to you. I was told it myself. I heard it in Paris from a Frenchman. He told me it was read at our mass from the Lives of the Saints ... he was a very learned man who had made a special study of Russian statistics and had lived a long time in Russia.... I have not read the Lives of the Saints myself, and I am not going to read them ... all sorts of things are said at dinner—we were dining then.”\n\n“Yes, you were dining then, and so I lost my faith!” said Fyodor Pavlovitch, mimicking him.\n\n“What do I care for your faith?” Miüsov was on the point of shouting, but he suddenly checked himself, and said with contempt, “You defile everything you touch.”\n\nThe elder suddenly rose from his seat. “Excuse me, gentlemen, for leaving you a few minutes,” he said, addressing all his guests. “I have visitors awaiting me who arrived before you. But don’t you tell lies all the same,” he added, turning to Fyodor Pavlovitch with a good-humored face. He went out of the cell. Alyosha and the novice flew to escort him down the steps. Alyosha was breathless: he was glad to get away, but he was glad, too, that the elder was good-humored and not offended. Father Zossima was going towards the portico to bless the people waiting for him there. But Fyodor Pavlovitch persisted in stopping him at the door of the cell.\n\n“Blessed man!” he cried, with feeling. “Allow me to kiss your hand once more. Yes, with you I could still talk, I could still get on. Do you think I always lie and play the fool like this? Believe me, I have been acting like this all the time on purpose to try you. I have been testing you all the time to see whether I could get on with you. Is there room for my humility beside your pride? I am ready to give you a testimonial that one can get on with you! But now, I’ll be quiet; I will keep quiet all the time. I’ll sit in a chair and hold my tongue. Now it is for you to speak, Pyotr Alexandrovitch. You are the principal person left now—for ten minutes.”\n\n# Chapter III. Peasant Women Who Have Faith\n\nNear the wooden portico below, built on to the outer wall of the precinct, there was a crowd of about twenty peasant women. They had been told that the elder was at last coming out, and they had gathered together in anticipation. Two ladies, Madame Hohlakov and her daughter, had also come out into the portico to wait for the elder, but in a separate part of it set aside for women of rank.\n\nMadame Hohlakov was a wealthy lady, still young and attractive, and always dressed with taste. She was rather pale, and had lively black eyes. She was not more than thirty-three, and had been five years a widow. Her daughter, a girl of fourteen, was partially paralyzed. The poor child had not been able to walk for the last six months, and was wheeled about in a long reclining chair. She had a charming little face, rather thin from illness, but full of gayety. There was a gleam of mischief in her big dark eyes with their long lashes. Her mother had been intending to take her abroad ever since the spring, but they had been detained all the summer by business connected with their estate. They had been staying a week in our town, where they had come more for purposes of business than devotion, but had visited Father Zossima once already, three days before. Though they knew that the elder scarcely saw any one, they had now suddenly turned up again, and urgently entreated “the happiness of looking once again on the great healer.”\n\nThe mother was sitting on a chair by the side of her daughter’s invalid carriage, and two paces from her stood an old monk, not one of our monastery, but a visitor from an obscure religious house in the far north. He too sought the elder’s blessing.\n\nBut Father Zossima, on entering the portico, went first straight to the peasants who were crowded at the foot of the three steps that led up into the portico. Father Zossima stood on the top step, put on his stole, and began blessing the women who thronged about him. One crazy woman was led up to him. As soon as she caught sight of the elder she began shrieking and writhing as though in the pains of childbirth. Laying the stole on her forehead, he read a short prayer over her, and she was at once soothed and quieted.\n\nI do not know how it may be now, but in my childhood I often happened to see and hear these “possessed” women in the villages and monasteries. They used to be brought to mass; they would squeal and bark like a dog so that they were heard all over the church. But when the sacrament was carried in and they were led up to it, at once the “possession” ceased, and the sick women were always soothed for a time. I was greatly impressed and amazed at this as a child; but then I heard from country neighbors and from my town teachers that the whole illness was simulated to avoid work, and that it could always be cured by suitable severity; various anecdotes were told to confirm this. But later on I learnt with astonishment from medical specialists that there is no pretense about it, that it is a terrible illness to which women are subject, specially prevalent among us in Russia, and that it is due to the hard lot of the peasant women. It is a disease, I was told, arising from exhausting toil too soon after hard, abnormal and unassisted labor in childbirth, and from the hopeless misery, from beatings, and so on, which some women were not able to endure like others. The strange and instant healing of the frantic and struggling woman as soon as she was led up to the holy sacrament, which had been explained to me as due to malingering and the trickery of the “clericals,” arose probably in the most natural manner. Both the women who supported her and the invalid herself fully believed as a truth beyond question that the evil spirit in possession of her could not hold out if the sick woman were brought to the sacrament and made to bow down before it. And so, with a nervous and psychically deranged woman, a sort of convulsion of the whole organism always took place, and was bound to take place, at the moment of bowing down to the sacrament, aroused by the expectation of the miracle of healing and the implicit belief that it would come to pass; and it did come to pass, though only for a moment. It was exactly the same now as soon as the elder touched the sick woman with the stole.\n\nMany of the women in the crowd were moved to tears of ecstasy by the effect of the moment: some strove to kiss the hem of his garment, others cried out in sing-song voices.\n\nHe blessed them all and talked with some of them. The “possessed” woman he knew already. She came from a village only six versts from the monastery, and had been brought to him before.\n\n“But here is one from afar.” He pointed to a woman by no means old but very thin and wasted, with a face not merely sunburnt but almost blackened by exposure. She was kneeling and gazing with a fixed stare at the elder; there was something almost frenzied in her eyes.\n\n“From afar off, Father, from afar off! From two hundred miles from here. From afar off, Father, from afar off!” the woman began in a sing-song voice as though she were chanting a dirge, swaying her head from side to side with her cheek resting in her hand.\n\nThere is silent and long-suffering sorrow to be met with among the peasantry. It withdraws into itself and is still. But there is a grief that breaks out, and from that minute it bursts into tears and finds vent in wailing. This is particularly common with women. But it is no lighter a grief than the silent. Lamentations comfort only by lacerating the heart still more. Such grief does not desire consolation. It feeds on the sense of its hopelessness. Lamentations spring only from the constant craving to reopen the wound.\n\n“You are of the tradesman class?” said Father Zossima, looking curiously at her.\n\n“Townfolk we are, Father, townfolk. Yet we are peasants though we live in the town. I have come to see you, O Father! We heard of you, Father, we heard of you. I have buried my little son, and I have come on a pilgrimage. I have been in three monasteries, but they told me, ‘Go, Nastasya, go to them’—that is to you. I have come; I was yesterday at the service, and to-day I have come to you.”\n\n“What are you weeping for?”\n\n“It’s my little son I’m grieving for, Father. He was three years old—three years all but three months. For my little boy, Father, I’m in anguish, for my little boy. He was the last one left. We had four, my Nikita and I, and now we’ve no children, our dear ones have all gone. I buried the first three without grieving overmuch, and now I have buried the last I can’t forget him. He seems always standing before me. He never leaves me. He has withered my heart. I look at his little clothes, his little shirt, his little boots, and I wail. I lay out all that is left of him, all his little things. I look at them and wail. I say to Nikita, my husband, ‘Let me go on a pilgrimage, master.’ He is a driver. We’re not poor people, Father, not poor; he drives our own horse. It’s all our own, the horse and the carriage. And what good is it all to us now? My Nikita has begun drinking while I am away. He’s sure to. It used to be so before. As soon as I turn my back he gives way to it. But now I don’t think about him. It’s three months since I left home. I’ve forgotten him. I’ve forgotten everything. I don’t want to remember. And what would our life be now together? I’ve done with him, I’ve done. I’ve done with them all. I don’t care to look upon my house and my goods. I don’t care to see anything at all!”\n\n“Listen, mother,” said the elder. “Once in olden times a holy saint saw in the Temple a mother like you weeping for her little one, her only one, whom God had taken. ‘Knowest thou not,’ said the saint to her, ‘how bold these little ones are before the throne of God? Verily there are none bolder than they in the Kingdom of Heaven. “Thou didst give us life, O Lord,” they say, “and scarcely had we looked upon it when Thou didst take it back again.” And so boldly they ask and ask again that God gives them at once the rank of angels. Therefore,’ said the saint, ‘thou, too, O mother, rejoice and weep not, for thy little son is with the Lord in the fellowship of the angels.’ That’s what the saint said to the weeping mother of old. He was a great saint and he could not have spoken falsely. Therefore you too, mother, know that your little one is surely before the throne of God, is rejoicing and happy, and praying to God for you, and therefore weep not, but rejoice.”\n\nThe woman listened to him, looking down with her cheek in her hand. She sighed deeply.\n\n“My Nikita tried to comfort me with the same words as you. ‘Foolish one,’ he said, ‘why weep? Our son is no doubt singing with the angels before God.’ He says that to me, but he weeps himself. I see that he cries like me. ‘I know, Nikita,’ said I. ‘Where could he be if not with the Lord God? Only, here with us now he is not as he used to sit beside us before.’ And if only I could look upon him one little time, if only I could peep at him one little time, without going up to him, without speaking, if I could be hidden in a corner and only see him for one little minute, hear him playing in the yard, calling in his little voice, ‘Mammy, where are you?’ If only I could hear him pattering with his little feet about the room just once, only once; for so often, so often I remember how he used to run to me and shout and laugh, if only I could hear his little feet I should know him! But he’s gone, Father, he’s gone, and I shall never hear him again. Here’s his little sash, but him I shall never see or hear now.”\n\nShe drew out of her bosom her boy’s little embroidered sash, and as soon as she looked at it she began shaking with sobs, hiding her eyes with her fingers through which the tears flowed in a sudden stream.\n\n“It is Rachel of old,” said the elder, “weeping for her children, and will not be comforted because they are not. Such is the lot set on earth for you mothers. Be not comforted. Consolation is not what you need. Weep and be not consoled, but weep. Only every time that you weep be sure to remember that your little son is one of the angels of God, that he looks down from there at you and sees you, and rejoices at your tears, and points at them to the Lord God; and a long while yet will you keep that great mother’s grief. But it will turn in the end into quiet joy, and your bitter tears will be only tears of tender sorrow that purifies the heart and delivers it from sin. And I shall pray for the peace of your child’s soul. What was his name?”\n\n“Alexey, Father.”\n\n“A sweet name. After Alexey, the man of God?”\n\n“Yes, Father.”\n\n“What a saint he was! I will remember him, mother, and your grief in my prayers, and I will pray for your husband’s health. It is a sin for you to leave him. Your little one will see from heaven that you have forsaken his father, and will weep over you. Why do you trouble his happiness? He is living, for the soul lives for ever, and though he is not in the house he is near you, unseen. How can he go into the house when you say that the house is hateful to you? To whom is he to go if he find you not together, his father and mother? He comes to you in dreams now, and you grieve. But then he will send you gentle dreams. Go to your husband, mother; go this very day.”\n\n“I will go, Father, at your word. I will go. You’ve gone straight to my heart. My Nikita, my Nikita, you are waiting for me,” the woman began in a sing-song voice; but the elder had already turned away to a very old woman, dressed like a dweller in the town, not like a pilgrim. Her eyes showed that she had come with an object, and in order to say something. She said she was the widow of a non-commissioned officer, and lived close by in the town. Her son Vasenka was in the commissariat service, and had gone to Irkutsk in Siberia. He had written twice from there, but now a year had passed since he had written. She did inquire about him, but she did not know the proper place to inquire.\n\n“Only the other day Stepanida Ilyinishna—she’s a rich merchant’s wife—said to me, ‘You go, Prohorovna, and put your son’s name down for prayer in the church, and pray for the peace of his soul as though he were dead. His soul will be troubled,’ she said, ‘and he will write you a letter.’ And Stepanida Ilyinishna told me it was a certain thing which had been many times tried. Only I am in doubt.... Oh, you light of ours! is it true or false, and would it be right?”\n\n“Don’t think of it. It’s shameful to ask the question. How is it possible to pray for the peace of a living soul? And his own mother too! It’s a great sin, akin to sorcery. Only for your ignorance it is forgiven you. Better pray to the Queen of Heaven, our swift defense and help, for his good health, and that she may forgive you for your error. And another thing I will tell you, Prohorovna. Either he will soon come back to you, your son, or he will be sure to send a letter. Go, and henceforward be in peace. Your son is alive, I tell you.”\n\n“Dear Father, God reward you, our benefactor, who prays for all of us and for our sins!”\n\nBut the elder had already noticed in the crowd two glowing eyes fixed upon him. An exhausted, consumptive-looking, though young peasant woman was gazing at him in silence. Her eyes besought him, but she seemed afraid to approach.\n\n“What is it, my child?”\n\n“Absolve my soul, Father,” she articulated softly, and slowly sank on her knees and bowed down at his feet. “I have sinned, Father. I am afraid of my sin.”\n\nThe elder sat down on the lower step. The woman crept closer to him, still on her knees.\n\n“I am a widow these three years,” she began in a half-whisper, with a sort of shudder. “I had a hard life with my husband. He was an old man. He used to beat me cruelly. He lay ill; I thought looking at him, if he were to get well, if he were to get up again, what then? And then the thought came to me—”\n\n“Stay!” said the elder, and he put his ear close to her lips.\n\nThe woman went on in a low whisper, so that it was almost impossible to catch anything. She had soon done.\n\n“Three years ago?” asked the elder.\n\n“Three years. At first I didn’t think about it, but now I’ve begun to be ill, and the thought never leaves me.”\n\n“Have you come from far?”\n\n“Over three hundred miles away.”\n\n“Have you told it in confession?”\n\n“I have confessed it. Twice I have confessed it.”\n\n“Have you been admitted to Communion?”\n\n“Yes. I am afraid. I am afraid to die.”\n\n“Fear nothing and never be afraid; and don’t fret. If only your penitence fail not, God will forgive all. There is no sin, and there can be no sin on all the earth, which the Lord will not forgive to the truly repentant! Man cannot commit a sin so great as to exhaust the infinite love of God. Can there be a sin which could exceed the love of God? Think only of repentance, continual repentance, but dismiss fear altogether. Believe that God loves you as you cannot conceive; that He loves you with your sin, in your sin. It has been said of old that over one repentant sinner there is more joy in heaven than over ten righteous men. Go, and fear not. Be not bitter against men. Be not angry if you are wronged. Forgive the dead man in your heart what wrong he did you. Be reconciled with him in truth. If you are penitent, you love. And if you love you are of God. All things are atoned for, all things are saved by love. If I, a sinner, even as you are, am tender with you and have pity on you, how much more will God. Love is such a priceless treasure that you can redeem the whole world by it, and expiate not only your own sins but the sins of others.”\n\nHe signed her three times with the cross, took from his own neck a little ikon and put it upon her. She bowed down to the earth without speaking.\n\nHe got up and looked cheerfully at a healthy peasant woman with a tiny baby in her arms.\n\n“From Vyshegorye, dear Father.”\n\n“Five miles you have dragged yourself with the baby. What do you want?”\n\n“I’ve come to look at you. I have been to you before—or have you forgotten? You’ve no great memory if you’ve forgotten me. They told us you were ill. Thinks I, I’ll go and see him for myself. Now I see you, and you’re not ill! You’ll live another twenty years. God bless you! There are plenty to pray for you; how should you be ill?”\n\n“I thank you for all, daughter.”\n\n“By the way, I have a thing to ask, not a great one. Here are sixty copecks. Give them, dear Father, to some one poorer than me. I thought as I came along, better give through him. He’ll know whom to give to.”\n\n“Thanks, my dear, thanks! You are a good woman. I love you. I will do so certainly. Is that your little girl?”\n\n“My little girl, Father, Lizaveta.”\n\n“May the Lord bless you both, you and your babe Lizaveta! You have gladdened my heart, mother. Farewell, dear children, farewell, dear ones.”\n\nHe blessed them all and bowed low to them.\n\n# Chapter IV. A Lady Of Little Faith\n\nA visitor looking on the scene of his conversation with the peasants and his blessing them shed silent tears and wiped them away with her handkerchief. She was a sentimental society lady of genuinely good disposition in many respects. When the elder went up to her at last she met him enthusiastically.\n\n“Ah, what I have been feeling, looking on at this touching scene!...” She could not go on for emotion. “Oh, I understand the people’s love for you. I love the people myself. I want to love them. And who could help loving them, our splendid Russian people, so simple in their greatness!”\n\n“How is your daughter’s health? You wanted to talk to me again?”\n\n“Oh, I have been urgently begging for it, I have prayed for it! I was ready to fall on my knees and kneel for three days at your windows until you let me in. We have come, great healer, to express our ardent gratitude. You have healed my Lise, healed her completely, merely by praying over her last Thursday and laying your hands upon her. We have hastened here to kiss those hands, to pour out our feelings and our homage.”\n\n“What do you mean by healed? But she is still lying down in her chair.”\n\n“But her night fevers have entirely ceased ever since Thursday,” said the lady with nervous haste. “And that’s not all. Her legs are stronger. This morning she got up well; she had slept all night. Look at her rosy cheeks, her bright eyes! She used to be always crying, but now she laughs and is gay and happy. This morning she insisted on my letting her stand up, and she stood up for a whole minute without any support. She wagers that in a fortnight she’ll be dancing a quadrille. I’ve called in Doctor Herzenstube. He shrugged his shoulders and said, ‘I am amazed; I can make nothing of it.’ And would you have us not come here to disturb you, not fly here to thank you? Lise, thank him—thank him!”\n\nLise’s pretty little laughing face became suddenly serious. She rose in her chair as far as she could and, looking at the elder, clasped her hands before him, but could not restrain herself and broke into laughter.\n\n“It’s at him,” she said, pointing to Alyosha, with childish vexation at herself for not being able to repress her mirth.\n\nIf any one had looked at Alyosha standing a step behind the elder, he would have caught a quick flush crimsoning his cheeks in an instant. His eyes shone and he looked down.\n\n“She has a message for you, Alexey Fyodorovitch. How are you?” the mother went on, holding out her exquisitely gloved hand to Alyosha.\n\nThe elder turned round and all at once looked attentively at Alyosha. The latter went nearer to Lise and, smiling in a strangely awkward way, held out his hand to her too. Lise assumed an important air.\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna has sent you this through me.” She handed him a little note. “She particularly begs you to go and see her as soon as possible; that you will not fail her, but will be sure to come.”\n\n“She asks me to go and see her? Me? What for?” Alyosha muttered in great astonishment. His face at once looked anxious. “Oh, it’s all to do with Dmitri Fyodorovitch and—what has happened lately,” the mother explained hurriedly. “Katerina Ivanovna has made up her mind, but she must see you about it.... Why, of course, I can’t say. But she wants to see you at once. And you will go to her, of course. It is a Christian duty.”\n\n“I have only seen her once,” Alyosha protested with the same perplexity.\n\n“Oh, she is such a lofty, incomparable creature! If only for her suffering.... Think what she has gone through, what she is enduring now! Think what awaits her! It’s all terrible, terrible!”\n\n“Very well, I will come,” Alyosha decided, after rapidly scanning the brief, enigmatic note, which consisted of an urgent entreaty that he would come, without any sort of explanation.\n\n“Oh, how sweet and generous that would be of you!” cried Lise with sudden animation. “I told mamma you’d be sure not to go. I said you were saving your soul. How splendid you are! I’ve always thought you were splendid. How glad I am to tell you so!”\n\n“Lise!” said her mother impressively, though she smiled after she had said it.\n\n“You have quite forgotten us, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” she said; “you never come to see us. Yet Lise has told me twice that she is never happy except with you.”\n\nAlyosha raised his downcast eyes and again flushed, and again smiled without knowing why. But the elder was no longer watching him. He had begun talking to a monk who, as mentioned before, had been awaiting his entrance by Lise’s chair. He was evidently a monk of the humblest, that is of the peasant, class, of a narrow outlook, but a true believer, and, in his own way, a stubborn one. He announced that he had come from the far north, from Obdorsk, from Saint Sylvester, and was a member of a poor monastery, consisting of only ten monks. The elder gave him his blessing and invited him to come to his cell whenever he liked.\n\n“How can you presume to do such deeds?” the monk asked suddenly, pointing solemnly and significantly at Lise. He was referring to her “healing.”\n\n“It’s too early, of course, to speak of that. Relief is not complete cure, and may proceed from different causes. But if there has been any healing, it is by no power but God’s will. It’s all from God. Visit me, Father,” he added to the monk. “It’s not often I can see visitors. I am ill, and I know that my days are numbered.”\n\n“Oh, no, no! God will not take you from us. You will live a long, long time yet,” cried the lady. “And in what way are you ill? You look so well, so gay and happy.”\n\n“I am extraordinarily better to-day. But I know that it’s only for a moment. I understand my disease now thoroughly. If I seem so happy to you, you could never say anything that would please me so much. For men are made for happiness, and any one who is completely happy has a right to say to himself, ‘I am doing God’s will on earth.’ All the righteous, all the saints, all the holy martyrs were happy.”\n\n“Oh, how you speak! What bold and lofty words!” cried the lady. “You seem to pierce with your words. And yet—happiness, happiness—where is it? Who can say of himself that he is happy? Oh, since you have been so good as to let us see you once more to-day, let me tell you what I could not utter last time, what I dared not say, all I am suffering and have been for so long! I am suffering! Forgive me! I am suffering!”\n\nAnd in a rush of fervent feeling she clasped her hands before him.\n\n“From what specially?”\n\n“I suffer ... from lack of faith.”\n\n“Lack of faith in God?”\n\n“Oh, no, no! I dare not even think of that. But the future life—it is such an enigma! And no one, no one can solve it. Listen! You are a healer, you are deeply versed in the human soul, and of course I dare not expect you to believe me entirely, but I assure you on my word of honor that I am not speaking lightly now. The thought of the life beyond the grave distracts me to anguish, to terror. And I don’t know to whom to appeal, and have not dared to all my life. And now I am so bold as to ask you. Oh, God! What will you think of me now?”\n\nShe clasped her hands.\n\n“Don’t distress yourself about my opinion of you,” said the elder. “I quite believe in the sincerity of your suffering.”\n\n“Oh, how thankful I am to you! You see, I shut my eyes and ask myself if every one has faith, where did it come from? And then they do say that it all comes from terror at the menacing phenomena of nature, and that none of it’s real. And I say to myself, ‘What if I’ve been believing all my life, and when I come to die there’s nothing but the burdocks growing on my grave?’ as I read in some author. It’s awful! How—how can I get back my faith? But I only believed when I was a little child, mechanically, without thinking of anything. How, how is one to prove it? I have come now to lay my soul before you and to ask you about it. If I let this chance slip, no one all my life will answer me. How can I prove it? How can I convince myself? Oh, how unhappy I am! I stand and look about me and see that scarcely any one else cares; no one troubles his head about it, and I’m the only one who can’t stand it. It’s deadly—deadly!”\n\n“No doubt. But there’s no proving it, though you can be convinced of it.”\n“How?”\n\n“By the experience of active love. Strive to love your neighbor actively and indefatigably. In as far as you advance in love you will grow surer of the reality of God and of the immortality of your soul. If you attain to perfect self-forgetfulness in the love of your neighbor, then you will believe without doubt, and no doubt can possibly enter your soul. This has been tried. This is certain.”\n\n“In active love? There’s another question—and such a question! You see, I so love humanity that—would you believe it?—I often dream of forsaking all that I have, leaving Lise, and becoming a sister of mercy. I close my eyes and think and dream, and at that moment I feel full of strength to overcome all obstacles. No wounds, no festering sores could at that moment frighten me. I would bind them up and wash them with my own hands. I would nurse the afflicted. I would be ready to kiss such wounds.”\n\n“It is much, and well that your mind is full of such dreams and not others. Sometime, unawares, you may do a good deed in reality.”\n\n“Yes. But could I endure such a life for long?” the lady went on fervently, almost frantically. “That’s the chief question—that’s my most agonizing question. I shut my eyes and ask myself, ‘Would you persevere long on that path? And if the patient whose wounds you are washing did not meet you with gratitude, but worried you with his whims, without valuing or remarking your charitable services, began abusing you and rudely commanding you, and complaining to the superior authorities of you (which often happens when people are in great suffering)—what then? Would you persevere in your love, or not?’ And do you know, I came with horror to the conclusion that, if anything could dissipate my love to humanity, it would be ingratitude. In short, I am a hired servant, I expect my payment at once—that is, praise, and the repayment of love with love. Otherwise I am incapable of loving any one.”\n\nShe was in a very paroxysm of self-castigation, and, concluding, she looked with defiant resolution at the elder.\n\n“It’s just the same story as a doctor once told me,” observed the elder. “He was a man getting on in years, and undoubtedly clever. He spoke as frankly as you, though in jest, in bitter jest. ‘I love humanity,’ he said, ‘but I wonder at myself. The more I love humanity in general, the less I love man in particular. In my dreams,’ he said, ‘I have often come to making enthusiastic schemes for the service of humanity, and perhaps I might actually have faced crucifixion if it had been suddenly necessary; and yet I am incapable of living in the same room with any one for two days together, as I know by experience. As soon as any one is near me, his personality disturbs my self-complacency and restricts my freedom. In twenty-four hours I begin to hate the best of men: one because he’s too long over his dinner; another because he has a cold and keeps on blowing his nose. I become hostile to people the moment they come close to me. But it has always happened that the more I detest men individually the more ardent becomes my love for humanity.’”\n\n“But what’s to be done? What can one do in such a case? Must one despair?”\n\n“No. It is enough that you are distressed at it. Do what you can, and it will be reckoned unto you. Much is done already in you since you can so deeply and sincerely know yourself. If you have been talking to me so sincerely, simply to gain approbation for your frankness, as you did from me just now, then of course you will not attain to anything in the achievement of real love; it will all get no further than dreams, and your whole life will slip away like a phantom. In that case you will naturally cease to think of the future life too, and will of yourself grow calmer after a fashion in the end.”\n\n“You have crushed me! Only now, as you speak, I understand that I was really only seeking your approbation for my sincerity when I told you I could not endure ingratitude. You have revealed me to myself. You have seen through me and explained me to myself!”\n\n“Are you speaking the truth? Well, now, after such a confession, I believe that you are sincere and good at heart. If you do not attain happiness, always remember that you are on the right road, and try not to leave it. Above all, avoid falsehood, every kind of falsehood, especially falseness to yourself. Watch over your own deceitfulness and look into it every hour, every minute. Avoid being scornful, both to others and to yourself. What seems to you bad within you will grow purer from the very fact of your observing it in yourself. Avoid fear, too, though fear is only the consequence of every sort of falsehood. Never be frightened at your own faint-heartedness in attaining love. Don’t be frightened overmuch even at your evil actions. I am sorry I can say nothing more consoling to you, for love in action is a harsh and dreadful thing compared with love in dreams. Love in dreams is greedy for immediate action, rapidly performed and in the sight of all. Men will even give their lives if only the ordeal does not last long but is soon over, with all looking on and applauding as though on the stage. But active love is labor and fortitude, and for some people too, perhaps, a complete science. But I predict that just when you see with horror that in spite of all your efforts you are getting farther from your goal instead of nearer to it—at that very moment I predict that you will reach it and behold clearly the miraculous power of the Lord who has been all the time loving and mysteriously guiding you. Forgive me for not being able to stay longer with you. They are waiting for me. Good-by.”\n\nThe lady was weeping.\n\n“Lise, Lise! Bless her—bless her!” she cried, starting up suddenly.\n\n“She does not deserve to be loved. I have seen her naughtiness all along,” the elder said jestingly. “Why have you been laughing at Alexey?”\n\nLise had in fact been occupied in mocking at him all the time. She had noticed before that Alyosha was shy and tried not to look at her, and she found this extremely amusing. She waited intently to catch his eye. Alyosha, unable to endure her persistent stare, was irresistibly and suddenly drawn to glance at her, and at once she smiled triumphantly in his face. Alyosha was even more disconcerted and vexed. At last he turned away from her altogether and hid behind the elder’s back. After a few minutes, drawn by the same irresistible force, he turned again to see whether he was being looked at or not, and found Lise almost hanging out of her chair to peep sideways at him, eagerly waiting for him to look. Catching his eye, she laughed so that the elder could not help saying, “Why do you make fun of him like that, naughty girl?”\n\nLise suddenly and quite unexpectedly blushed. Her eyes flashed and her face became quite serious. She began speaking quickly and nervously in a warm and resentful voice:\n\n“Why has he forgotten everything, then? He used to carry me about when I was little. We used to play together. He used to come to teach me to read, do you know. Two years ago, when he went away, he said that he would never forget me, that we were friends for ever, for ever, for ever! And now he’s afraid of me all at once. Am I going to eat him? Why doesn’t he want to come near me? Why doesn’t he talk? Why won’t he come and see us? It’s not that you won’t let him. We know that he goes everywhere. It’s not good manners for me to invite him. He ought to have thought of it first, if he hasn’t forgotten me. No, now he’s saving his soul! Why have you put that long gown on him? If he runs he’ll fall.”\n\nAnd suddenly she hid her face in her hand and went off into irresistible, prolonged, nervous, inaudible laughter. The elder listened to her with a smile, and blessed her tenderly. As she kissed his hand she suddenly pressed it to her eyes and began crying.\n\n“Don’t be angry with me. I’m silly and good for nothing ... and perhaps Alyosha’s right, quite right, in not wanting to come and see such a ridiculous girl.”\n\n“I will certainly send him,” said the elder.\n\n# Chapter V. So Be It! So Be It!\n\nThe elder’s absence from his cell had lasted for about twenty-five minutes. It was more than half-past twelve, but Dmitri, on whose account they had all met there, had still not appeared. But he seemed almost to be forgotten, and when the elder entered the cell again, he found his guests engaged in eager conversation. Ivan and the two monks took the leading share in it. Miüsov, too, was trying to take a part, and apparently very eagerly, in the conversation. But he was unsuccessful in this also. He was evidently in the background, and his remarks were treated with neglect, which increased his irritability. He had had intellectual encounters with Ivan before and he could not endure a certain carelessness Ivan showed him.\n\n“Hitherto at least I have stood in the front ranks of all that is progressive in Europe, and here the new generation positively ignores us,” he thought.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch, who had given his word to sit still and be quiet, had actually been quiet for some time, but he watched his neighbor Miüsov with an ironical little smile, obviously enjoying his discomfiture. He had been waiting for some time to pay off old scores, and now he could not let the opportunity slip. Bending over his shoulder he began teasing him again in a whisper.\n\n“Why didn’t you go away just now, after the ‘courteously kissing’? Why did you consent to remain in such unseemly company? It was because you felt insulted and aggrieved, and you remained to vindicate yourself by showing off your intelligence. Now you won’t go till you’ve displayed your intellect to them.”\n\n“You again?... On the contrary, I’m just going.”\n\n“You’ll be the last, the last of all to go!” Fyodor Pavlovitch delivered him another thrust, almost at the moment of Father Zossima’s return.\n\nThe discussion died down for a moment, but the elder, seating himself in his former place, looked at them all as though cordially inviting them to go on. Alyosha, who knew every expression of his face, saw that he was fearfully exhausted and making a great effort. Of late he had been liable to fainting fits from exhaustion. His face had the pallor that was common before such attacks, and his lips were white. But he evidently did not want to break up the party. He seemed to have some special object of his own in keeping them. What object? Alyosha watched him intently.\n\n“We are discussing this gentleman’s most interesting article,” said Father Iosif, the librarian, addressing the elder, and indicating Ivan. “He brings forward much that is new, but I think the argument cuts both ways. It is an article written in answer to a book by an ecclesiastical authority on the question of the ecclesiastical court, and the scope of its jurisdiction.”\n\n“I’m sorry I have not read your article, but I’ve heard of it,” said the elder, looking keenly and intently at Ivan.\n\n“He takes up a most interesting position,” continued the Father Librarian. “As far as Church jurisdiction is concerned he is apparently quite opposed to the separation of Church from State.”\n\n“That’s interesting. But in what sense?” Father Zossima asked Ivan.\n\nThe latter, at last, answered him, not condescendingly, as Alyosha had feared, but with modesty and reserve, with evident goodwill and apparently without the slightest arrière-pensée.\n\n“I start from the position that this confusion of elements, that is, of the essential principles of Church and State, will, of course, go on for ever, in spite of the fact that it is impossible for them to mingle, and that the confusion of these elements cannot lead to any consistent or even normal results, for there is falsity at the very foundation of it. Compromise between the Church and State in such questions as, for instance, jurisdiction, is, to my thinking, impossible in any real sense. My clerical opponent maintains that the Church holds a precise and defined position in the State. I maintain, on the contrary, that the Church ought to include the whole State, and not simply to occupy a corner in it, and, if this is, for some reason, impossible at present, then it ought, in reality, to be set up as the direct and chief aim of the future development of Christian society!”\n\n“Perfectly true,” Father Païssy, the silent and learned monk, assented with fervor and decision.\n\n“The purest Ultramontanism!” cried Miüsov impatiently, crossing and recrossing his legs.\n\n“Oh, well, we have no mountains,” cried Father Iosif, and turning to the elder he continued: “Observe the answer he makes to the following ‘fundamental and essential’ propositions of his opponent, who is, you must note, an ecclesiastic. First, that ‘no social organization can or ought to arrogate to itself power to dispose of the civic and political rights of its members.’ Secondly, that ‘criminal and civil jurisdiction ought not to belong to the Church, and is inconsistent with its nature, both as a divine institution and as an organization of men for religious objects,’ and, finally, in the third place, ‘the Church is a kingdom not of this world.’”\n\n“A most unworthy play upon words for an ecclesiastic!” Father Païssy could not refrain from breaking in again. “I have read the book which you have answered,” he added, addressing Ivan, “and was astounded at the words ‘the Church is a kingdom not of this world.’ If it is not of this world, then it cannot exist on earth at all. In the Gospel, the words ‘not of this world’ are not used in that sense. To play with such words is indefensible. Our Lord Jesus Christ came to set up the Church upon earth. The Kingdom of Heaven, of course, is not of this world, but in Heaven; but it is only entered through the Church which has been founded and established upon earth. And so a frivolous play upon words in such a connection is unpardonable and improper. The Church is, in truth, a kingdom and ordained to rule, and in the end must undoubtedly become the kingdom ruling over all the earth. For that we have the divine promise.”\n\nHe ceased speaking suddenly, as though checking himself. After listening attentively and respectfully Ivan went on, addressing the elder with perfect composure and as before with ready cordiality:\n\n“The whole point of my article lies in the fact that during the first three centuries Christianity only existed on earth in the Church and was nothing but the Church. When the pagan Roman Empire desired to become Christian, it inevitably happened that, by becoming Christian, it included the Church but remained a pagan State in very many of its departments. In reality this was bound to happen. But Rome as a State retained too much of the pagan civilization and culture, as, for example, in the very objects and fundamental principles of the State. The Christian Church entering into the State could, of course, surrender no part of its fundamental principles—the rock on which it stands—and could pursue no other aims than those which have been ordained and revealed by God Himself, and among them that of drawing the whole world, and therefore the ancient pagan State itself, into the Church. In that way (that is, with a view to the future) it is not the Church that should seek a definite position in the State, like ‘every social organization,’ or as ‘an organization of men for religious purposes’ (as my opponent calls the Church), but, on the contrary, every earthly State should be, in the end, completely transformed into the Church and should become nothing else but a Church, rejecting every purpose incongruous with the aims of the Church. All this will not degrade it in any way or take from its honor and glory as a great State, nor from the glory of its rulers, but only turns it from a false, still pagan, and mistaken path to the true and rightful path, which alone leads to the eternal goal. This is why the author of the book On the Foundations of Church Jurisdiction would have judged correctly if, in seeking and laying down those foundations, he had looked upon them as a temporary compromise inevitable in our sinful and imperfect days. But as soon as the author ventures to declare that the foundations which he predicates now, part of which Father Iosif just enumerated, are the permanent, essential, and eternal foundations, he is going directly against the Church and its sacred and eternal vocation. That is the gist of my article.”\n\n“That is, in brief,” Father Païssy began again, laying stress on each word, “according to certain theories only too clearly formulated in the nineteenth century, the Church ought to be transformed into the State, as though this would be an advance from a lower to a higher form, so as to disappear into it, making way for science, for the spirit of the age, and civilization. And if the Church resists and is unwilling, some corner will be set apart for her in the State, and even that under control—and this will be so everywhere in all modern European countries. But Russian hopes and conceptions demand not that the Church should pass as from a lower into a higher type into the State, but, on the contrary, that the State should end by being worthy to become only the Church and nothing else. So be it! So be it!”\n\n“Well, I confess you’ve reassured me somewhat,” Miüsov said smiling, again crossing his legs. “So far as I understand, then, the realization of such an ideal is infinitely remote, at the second coming of Christ. That’s as you please. It’s a beautiful Utopian dream of the abolition of war, diplomacy, banks, and so on—something after the fashion of socialism, indeed. But I imagined that it was all meant seriously, and that the Church might be now going to try criminals, and sentence them to beating, prison, and even death.”\n\n“But if there were none but the ecclesiastical court, the Church would not even now sentence a criminal to prison or to death. Crime and the way of regarding it would inevitably change, not all at once of course, but fairly soon,” Ivan replied calmly, without flinching.\n\n“Are you serious?” Miüsov glanced keenly at him.\n\n“If everything became the Church, the Church would exclude all the criminal and disobedient, and would not cut off their heads,” Ivan went on. “I ask you, what would become of the excluded? He would be cut off then not only from men, as now, but from Christ. By his crime he would have transgressed not only against men but against the Church of Christ. This is so even now, of course, strictly speaking, but it is not clearly enunciated, and very, very often the criminal of to-day compromises with his conscience: ‘I steal,’ he says, ‘but I don’t go against the Church. I’m not an enemy of Christ.’ That’s what the criminal of to-day is continually saying to himself, but when the Church takes the place of the State it will be difficult for him, in opposition to the Church all over the world, to say: ‘All men are mistaken, all in error, all mankind are the false Church. I, a thief and murderer, am the only true Christian Church.’ It will be very difficult to say this to himself; it requires a rare combination of unusual circumstances. Now, on the other side, take the Church’s own view of crime: is it not bound to renounce the present almost pagan attitude, and to change from a mechanical cutting off of its tainted member for the preservation of society, as at present, into completely and honestly adopting the idea of the regeneration of the man, of his reformation and salvation?”\n\n“What do you mean? I fail to understand again,” Miüsov interrupted. “Some sort of dream again. Something shapeless and even incomprehensible. What is excommunication? What sort of exclusion? I suspect you are simply amusing yourself, Ivan Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“Yes, but you know, in reality it is so now,” said the elder suddenly, and all turned to him at once. “If it were not for the Church of Christ there would be nothing to restrain the criminal from evil-doing, no real chastisement for it afterwards; none, that is, but the mechanical punishment spoken of just now, which in the majority of cases only embitters the heart; and not the real punishment, the only effectual one, the only deterrent and softening one, which lies in the recognition of sin by conscience.”\n\n“How is that, may one inquire?” asked Miüsov, with lively curiosity.\n\n“Why,” began the elder, “all these sentences to exile with hard labor, and formerly with flogging also, reform no one, and what’s more, deter hardly a single criminal, and the number of crimes does not diminish but is continually on the increase. You must admit that. Consequently the security of society is not preserved, for, although the obnoxious member is mechanically cut off and sent far away out of sight, another criminal always comes to take his place at once, and often two of them. If anything does preserve society, even in our time, and does regenerate and transform the criminal, it is only the law of Christ speaking in his conscience. It is only by recognizing his wrong-doing as a son of a Christian society—that is, of the Church—that he recognizes his sin against society—that is, against the Church. So that it is only against the Church, and not against the State, that the criminal of to-day can recognize that he has sinned. If society, as a Church, had jurisdiction, then it would know when to bring back from exclusion and to reunite to itself. Now the Church having no real jurisdiction, but only the power of moral condemnation, withdraws of her own accord from punishing the criminal actively. She does not excommunicate him but simply persists in motherly exhortation of him. What is more, the Church even tries to preserve all Christian communion with the criminal. She admits him to church services, to the holy sacrament, gives him alms, and treats him more as a captive than as a convict. And what would become of the criminal, O Lord, if even the Christian society—that is, the Church—were to reject him even as the civil law rejects him and cuts him off? What would become of him if the Church punished him with her excommunication as the direct consequence of the secular law? There could be no more terrible despair, at least for a Russian criminal, for Russian criminals still have faith. Though, who knows, perhaps then a fearful thing would happen, perhaps the despairing heart of the criminal would lose its faith and then what would become of him? But the Church, like a tender, loving mother, holds aloof from active punishment herself, as the sinner is too severely punished already by the civil law, and there must be at least some one to have pity on him. The Church holds aloof, above all, because its judgment is the only one that contains the truth, and therefore cannot practically and morally be united to any other judgment even as a temporary compromise. She can enter into no compact about that. The foreign criminal, they say, rarely repents, for the very doctrines of to-day confirm him in the idea that his crime is not a crime, but only a reaction against an unjustly oppressive force. Society cuts him off completely by a force that triumphs over him mechanically and (so at least they say of themselves in Europe) accompanies this exclusion with hatred, forgetfulness, and the most profound indifference as to the ultimate fate of the erring brother. In this way, it all takes place without the compassionate intervention of the Church, for in many cases there are no churches there at all, for though ecclesiastics and splendid church buildings remain, the churches themselves have long ago striven to pass from Church into State and to disappear in it completely. So it seems at least in Lutheran countries. As for Rome, it was proclaimed a State instead of a Church a thousand years ago. And so the criminal is no longer conscious of being a member of the Church and sinks into despair. If he returns to society, often it is with such hatred that society itself instinctively cuts him off. You can judge for yourself how it must end. In many cases it would seem to be the same with us, but the difference is that besides the established law courts we have the Church too, which always keeps up relations with the criminal as a dear and still precious son. And besides that, there is still preserved, though only in thought, the judgment of the Church, which though no longer existing in practice is still living as a dream for the future, and is, no doubt, instinctively recognized by the criminal in his soul. What was said here just now is true too, that is, that if the jurisdiction of the Church were introduced in practice in its full force, that is, if the whole of the society were changed into the Church, not only the judgment of the Church would have influence on the reformation of the criminal such as it never has now, but possibly also the crimes themselves would be incredibly diminished. And there can be no doubt that the Church would look upon the criminal and the crime of the future in many cases quite differently and would succeed in restoring the excluded, in restraining those who plan evil, and in regenerating the fallen. It is true,” said Father Zossima, with a smile, “the Christian society now is not ready and is only resting on some seven righteous men, but as they are never lacking, it will continue still unshaken in expectation of its complete transformation from a society almost heathen in character into a single universal and all-powerful Church. So be it, so be it! Even though at the end of the ages, for it is ordained to come to pass! And there is no need to be troubled about times and seasons, for the secret of the times and seasons is in the wisdom of God, in His foresight, and His love. And what in human reckoning seems still afar off, may by the Divine ordinance be close at hand, on the eve of its appearance. And so be it, so be it!”\n\n“So be it, so be it!” Father Païssy repeated austerely and reverently.\n\n“Strange, extremely strange!” Miüsov pronounced, not so much with heat as with latent indignation.\n\n“What strikes you as so strange?” Father Iosif inquired cautiously.\n\n“Why, it’s beyond anything!” cried Miüsov, suddenly breaking out; “the State is eliminated and the Church is raised to the position of the State. It’s not simply Ultramontanism, it’s arch-Ultramontanism! It’s beyond the dreams of Pope Gregory the Seventh!”\n\n“You are completely misunderstanding it,” said Father Païssy sternly. “Understand, the Church is not to be transformed into the State. That is Rome and its dream. That is the third temptation of the devil. On the contrary, the State is transformed into the Church, will ascend and become a Church over the whole world—which is the complete opposite of Ultramontanism and Rome, and your interpretation, and is only the glorious destiny ordained for the Orthodox Church. This star will arise in the east!”\n\nMiüsov was significantly silent. His whole figure expressed extraordinary personal dignity. A supercilious and condescending smile played on his lips. Alyosha watched it all with a throbbing heart. The whole conversation stirred him profoundly. He glanced casually at Rakitin, who was standing immovable in his place by the door listening and watching intently though with downcast eyes. But from the color in his cheeks Alyosha guessed that Rakitin was probably no less excited, and he knew what caused his excitement.\n\n“Allow me to tell you one little anecdote, gentlemen,” Miüsov said impressively, with a peculiarly majestic air. “Some years ago, soon after the coup d’état of December, I happened to be calling in Paris on an extremely influential personage in the Government, and I met a very interesting man in his house. This individual was not precisely a detective but was a sort of superintendent of a whole regiment of political detectives—a rather powerful position in its own way. I was prompted by curiosity to seize the opportunity of conversation with him. And as he had not come as a visitor but as a subordinate official bringing a special report, and as he saw the reception given me by his chief, he deigned to speak with some openness, to a certain extent only, of course. He was rather courteous than open, as Frenchmen know how to be courteous, especially to a foreigner. But I thoroughly understood him. The subject was the socialist revolutionaries who were at that time persecuted. I will quote only one most curious remark dropped by this person. ‘We are not particularly afraid,’ said he, ‘of all these socialists, anarchists, infidels, and revolutionists; we keep watch on them and know all their goings on. But there are a few peculiar men among them who believe in God and are Christians, but at the same time are socialists. These are the people we are most afraid of. They are dreadful people! The socialist who is a Christian is more to be dreaded than a socialist who is an atheist.’ The words struck me at the time, and now they have suddenly come back to me here, gentlemen.”\n\n“You apply them to us, and look upon us as socialists?” Father Païssy asked directly, without beating about the bush.\n\nBut before Pyotr Alexandrovitch could think what to answer, the door opened, and the guest so long expected, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, came in. They had, in fact, given up expecting him, and his sudden appearance caused some surprise for a moment.\n\n# Chapter VI. Why Is Such A Man Alive?\n\nDmitri Fyodorovitch, a young man of eight and twenty, of medium height and agreeable countenance, looked older than his years. He was muscular, and showed signs of considerable physical strength. Yet there was something not healthy in his face. It was rather thin, his cheeks were hollow, and there was an unhealthy sallowness in their color. His rather large, prominent, dark eyes had an expression of firm determination, and yet there was a vague look in them, too. Even when he was excited and talking irritably, his eyes somehow did not follow his mood, but betrayed something else, sometimes quite incongruous with what was passing. “It’s hard to tell what he’s thinking,” those who talked to him sometimes declared. People who saw something pensive and sullen in his eyes were startled by his sudden laugh, which bore witness to mirthful and light-hearted thoughts at the very time when his eyes were so gloomy. A certain strained look in his face was easy to understand at this moment. Every one knew, or had heard of, the extremely restless and dissipated life which he had been leading of late, as well as of the violent anger to which he had been roused in his quarrels with his father. There were several stories current in the town about it. It is true that he was irascible by nature, “of an unstable and unbalanced mind,” as our justice of the peace, Katchalnikov, happily described him.\n\nHe was stylishly and irreproachably dressed in a carefully buttoned frock-coat. He wore black gloves and carried a top-hat. Having only lately left the army, he still had mustaches and no beard. His dark brown hair was cropped short, and combed forward on his temples. He had the long, determined stride of a military man. He stood still for a moment on the threshold, and glancing at the whole party went straight up to the elder, guessing him to be their host. He made him a low bow, and asked his blessing. Father Zossima, rising in his chair, blessed him. Dmitri kissed his hand respectfully, and with intense feeling, almost anger, he said:\n\n“Be so generous as to forgive me for having kept you waiting so long, but Smerdyakov, the valet sent me by my father, in reply to my inquiries, told me twice over that the appointment was for one. Now I suddenly learn—”\n\n“Don’t disturb yourself,” interposed the elder. “No matter. You are a little late. It’s of no consequence....”\n\n“I’m extremely obliged to you, and expected no less from your goodness.”\n\nSaying this, Dmitri bowed once more. Then, turning suddenly towards his father, made him, too, a similarly low and respectful bow. He had evidently considered it beforehand, and made this bow in all seriousness, thinking it his duty to show his respect and good intentions.\n\nAlthough Fyodor Pavlovitch was taken unawares, he was equal to the occasion. In response to Dmitri’s bow he jumped up from his chair and made his son a bow as low in return. His face was suddenly solemn and impressive, which gave him a positively malignant look. Dmitri bowed generally to all present, and without a word walked to the window with his long, resolute stride, sat down on the only empty chair, near Father Païssy, and, bending forward, prepared to listen to the conversation he had interrupted.\n\nDmitri’s entrance had taken no more than two minutes, and the conversation was resumed. But this time Miüsov thought it unnecessary to reply to Father Païssy’s persistent and almost irritable question.\n\n“Allow me to withdraw from this discussion,” he observed with a certain well-bred nonchalance. “It’s a subtle question, too. Here Ivan Fyodorovitch is smiling at us. He must have something interesting to say about that also. Ask him.”\n\n“Nothing special, except one little remark,” Ivan replied at once. “European Liberals in general, and even our liberal dilettanti, often mix up the final results of socialism with those of Christianity. This wild notion is, of course, a characteristic feature. But it’s not only Liberals and dilettanti who mix up socialism and Christianity, but, in many cases, it appears, the police—the foreign police, of course—do the same. Your Paris anecdote is rather to the point, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.”\n\n“I ask your permission to drop this subject altogether,” Miüsov repeated. “I will tell you instead, gentlemen, another interesting and rather characteristic anecdote of Ivan Fyodorovitch himself. Only five days ago, in a gathering here, principally of ladies, he solemnly declared in argument that there was nothing in the whole world to make men love their neighbors. That there was no law of nature that man should love mankind, and that, if there had been any love on earth hitherto, it was not owing to a natural law, but simply because men have believed in immortality. Ivan Fyodorovitch added in parenthesis that the whole natural law lies in that faith, and that if you were to destroy in mankind the belief in immortality, not only love but every living force maintaining the life of the world would at once be dried up. Moreover, nothing then would be immoral, everything would be lawful, even cannibalism. That’s not all. He ended by asserting that for every individual, like ourselves, who does not believe in God or immortality, the moral law of nature must immediately be changed into the exact contrary of the former religious law, and that egoism, even to crime, must become not only lawful but even recognized as the inevitable, the most rational, even honorable outcome of his position. From this paradox, gentlemen, you can judge of the rest of our eccentric and paradoxical friend Ivan Fyodorovitch’s theories.”\n\n“Excuse me,” Dmitri cried suddenly; “if I’ve heard aright, crime must not only be permitted but even recognized as the inevitable and the most rational outcome of his position for every infidel! Is that so or not?”\n\n“Quite so,” said Father Païssy.\n\n“I’ll remember it.”\n\nHaving uttered these words Dmitri ceased speaking as suddenly as he had begun. Every one looked at him with curiosity.\n\n“Is that really your conviction as to the consequences of the disappearance of the faith in immortality?” the elder asked Ivan suddenly.\n\n“Yes. That was my contention. There is no virtue if there is no immortality.”\n\n“You are blessed in believing that, or else most unhappy.”\n\n“Why unhappy?” Ivan asked smiling.\n\n“Because, in all probability you don’t believe yourself in the immortality of your soul, nor in what you have written yourself in your article on Church jurisdiction.”\n\n“Perhaps you are right! ... But I wasn’t altogether joking,” Ivan suddenly and strangely confessed, flushing quickly.\n\n“You were not altogether joking. That’s true. The question is still fretting your heart, and not answered. But the martyr likes sometimes to divert himself with his despair, as it were driven to it by despair itself. Meanwhile, in your despair, you, too, divert yourself with magazine articles, and discussions in society, though you don’t believe your own arguments, and with an aching heart mock at them inwardly.... That question you have not answered, and it is your great grief, for it clamors for an answer.”\n\n“But can it be answered by me? Answered in the affirmative?” Ivan went on asking strangely, still looking at the elder with the same inexplicable smile.\n\n“If it can’t be decided in the affirmative, it will never be decided in the negative. You know that that is the peculiarity of your heart, and all its suffering is due to it. But thank the Creator who has given you a lofty heart capable of such suffering; of thinking and seeking higher things, for our dwelling is in the heavens. God grant that your heart will attain the answer on earth, and may God bless your path.”\n\nThe elder raised his hand and would have made the sign of the cross over Ivan from where he stood. But the latter rose from his seat, went up to him, received his blessing, and kissing his hand went back to his place in silence. His face looked firm and earnest. This action and all the preceding conversation, which was so surprising from Ivan, impressed every one by its strangeness and a certain solemnity, so that all were silent for a moment, and there was a look almost of apprehension in Alyosha’s face. But Miüsov suddenly shrugged his shoulders. And at the same moment Fyodor Pavlovitch jumped up from his seat.\n\n“Most pious and holy elder,” he cried, pointing to Ivan, “that is my son, flesh of my flesh, the dearest of my flesh! He is my most dutiful Karl Moor, so to speak, while this son who has just come in, Dmitri, against whom I am seeking justice from you, is the undutiful Franz Moor—they are both out of Schiller’s Robbers, and so I am the reigning Count von Moor! Judge and save us! We need not only your prayers but your prophecies!”\n\n“Speak without buffoonery, and don’t begin by insulting the members of your family,” answered the elder, in a faint, exhausted voice. He was obviously getting more and more fatigued, and his strength was failing.\n\n“An unseemly farce which I foresaw when I came here!” cried Dmitri indignantly. He too leapt up. “Forgive it, reverend Father,” he added, addressing the elder. “I am not a cultivated man, and I don’t even know how to address you properly, but you have been deceived and you have been too good-natured in letting us meet here. All my father wants is a scandal. Why he wants it only he can tell. He always has some motive. But I believe I know why—”\n\n“They all blame me, all of them!” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch in his turn. “Pyotr Alexandrovitch here blames me too. You have been blaming me, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, you have!” he turned suddenly to Miüsov, although the latter was not dreaming of interrupting him. “They all accuse me of having hidden the children’s money in my boots, and cheated them, but isn’t there a court of law? There they will reckon out for you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, from your notes, your letters, and your agreements, how much money you had, how much you have spent, and how much you have left. Why does Pyotr Alexandrovitch refuse to pass judgment? Dmitri is not a stranger to him. Because they are all against me, while Dmitri Fyodorovitch is in debt to me, and not a little, but some thousands of which I have documentary proof. The whole town is echoing with his debaucheries. And where he was stationed before, he several times spent a thousand or two for the seduction of some respectable girl; we know all about that, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, in its most secret details. I’ll prove it.... Would you believe it, holy Father, he has captivated the heart of the most honorable of young ladies of good family and fortune, daughter of a gallant colonel, formerly his superior officer, who had received many honors and had the Anna Order on his breast. He compromised the girl by his promise of marriage, now she is an orphan and here; she is betrothed to him, yet before her very eyes he is dancing attendance on a certain enchantress. And although this enchantress has lived in, so to speak, civil marriage with a respectable man, yet she is of an independent character, an unapproachable fortress for everybody, just like a legal wife—for she is virtuous, yes, holy Fathers, she is virtuous. Dmitri Fyodorovitch wants to open this fortress with a golden key, and that’s why he is insolent to me now, trying to get money from me, though he has wasted thousands on this enchantress already. He’s continually borrowing money for the purpose. From whom do you think? Shall I say, Mitya?”\n\n“Be silent!” cried Dmitri, “wait till I’m gone. Don’t dare in my presence to asperse the good name of an honorable girl! That you should utter a word about her is an outrage, and I won’t permit it!”\n\nHe was breathless.\n\n“Mitya! Mitya!” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch hysterically, squeezing out a tear. “And is your father’s blessing nothing to you? If I curse you, what then?”\n\n“Shameless hypocrite!” exclaimed Dmitri furiously.\n\n“He says that to his father! his father! What would he be with others? Gentlemen, only fancy; there’s a poor but honorable man living here, burdened with a numerous family, a captain who got into trouble and was discharged from the army, but not publicly, not by court-martial, with no slur on his honor. And three weeks ago, Dmitri seized him by the beard in a tavern, dragged him out into the street and beat him publicly, and all because he is an agent in a little business of mine.”\n\n“It’s all a lie! Outwardly it’s the truth, but inwardly a lie!” Dmitri was trembling with rage. “Father, I don’t justify my action. Yes, I confess it publicly, I behaved like a brute to that captain, and I regret it now, and I’m disgusted with myself for my brutal rage. But this captain, this agent of yours, went to that lady whom you call an enchantress, and suggested to her from you, that she should take I.O.U.’s of mine which were in your possession, and should sue me for the money so as to get me into prison by means of them, if I persisted in claiming an account from you of my property. Now you reproach me for having a weakness for that lady when you yourself incited her to captivate me! She told me so to my face.... She told me the story and laughed at you.... You wanted to put me in prison because you are jealous of me with her, because you’d begun to force your attentions upon her; and I know all about that, too; she laughed at you for that as well—you hear—she laughed at you as she described it. So here you have this man, this father who reproaches his profligate son! Gentlemen, forgive my anger, but I foresaw that this crafty old man would only bring you together to create a scandal. I had come to forgive him if he held out his hand; to forgive him, and ask forgiveness! But as he has just this minute insulted not only me, but an honorable young lady, for whom I feel such reverence that I dare not take her name in vain, I have made up my mind to show up his game, though he is my father....”\n\nHe could not go on. His eyes were glittering and he breathed with difficulty. But every one in the cell was stirred. All except Father Zossima got up from their seats uneasily. The monks looked austere but waited for guidance from the elder. He sat still, pale, not from excitement but from the weakness of disease. An imploring smile lighted up his face; from time to time he raised his hand, as though to check the storm, and, of course, a gesture from him would have been enough to end the scene; but he seemed to be waiting for something and watched them intently as though trying to make out something which was not perfectly clear to him. At last Miüsov felt completely humiliated and disgraced.\n\n“We are all to blame for this scandalous scene,” he said hotly. “But I did not foresee it when I came, though I knew with whom I had to deal. This must be stopped at once! Believe me, your reverence, I had no precise knowledge of the details that have just come to light, I was unwilling to believe them, and I learn for the first time.... A father is jealous of his son’s relations with a woman of loose behavior and intrigues with the creature to get his son into prison! This is the company in which I have been forced to be present! I was deceived. I declare to you all that I was as much deceived as any one.”\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” yelled Fyodor Pavlovitch suddenly, in an unnatural voice, “if you were not my son I would challenge you this instant to a duel ... with pistols, at three paces ... across a handkerchief,” he ended, stamping with both feet.\n\nWith old liars who have been acting all their lives there are moments when they enter so completely into their part that they tremble or shed tears of emotion in earnest, although at that very moment, or a second later, they are able to whisper to themselves, “You know you are lying, you shameless old sinner! You’re acting now, in spite of your ‘holy’ wrath.”\n\nDmitri frowned painfully, and looked with unutterable contempt at his father.\n\n“I thought ... I thought,” he said, in a soft and, as it were, controlled voice, “that I was coming to my native place with the angel of my heart, my betrothed, to cherish his old age, and I find nothing but a depraved profligate, a despicable clown!”\n\n“A duel!” yelled the old wretch again, breathless and spluttering at each syllable. “And you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, let me tell you that there has never been in all your family a loftier, and more honest—you hear—more honest woman than this ‘creature,’ as you have dared to call her! And you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, have abandoned your betrothed for that ‘creature,’ so you must yourself have thought that your betrothed couldn’t hold a candle to her. That’s the woman called a ‘creature’!”\n\n“Shameful!” broke from Father Iosif.\n\n“Shameful and disgraceful!” Kalganov, flushing crimson, cried in a boyish voice, trembling with emotion. He had been silent till that moment.\n\n“Why is such a man alive?” Dmitri, beside himself with rage, growled in a hollow voice, hunching up his shoulders till he looked almost deformed. “Tell me, can he be allowed to go on defiling the earth?” He looked round at every one and pointed at the old man. He spoke evenly and deliberately.\n\n“Listen, listen, monks, to the parricide!” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, rushing up to Father Iosif. “That’s the answer to your ‘shameful!’ What is shameful? That ‘creature,’ that ‘woman of loose behavior’ is perhaps holier than you are yourselves, you monks who are seeking salvation! She fell perhaps in her youth, ruined by her environment. But she loved much, and Christ himself forgave the woman ‘who loved much.’”\n\n“It was not for such love Christ forgave her,” broke impatiently from the gentle Father Iosif.\n\n“Yes, it was for such, monks, it was! You save your souls here, eating cabbage, and think you are the righteous. You eat a gudgeon a day, and you think you bribe God with gudgeon.”\n\n“This is unendurable!” was heard on all sides in the cell.\n\nBut this unseemly scene was cut short in a most unexpected way. Father Zossima rose suddenly from his seat. Almost distracted with anxiety for the elder and every one else, Alyosha succeeded, however, in supporting him by the arm. Father Zossima moved towards Dmitri and reaching him sank on his knees before him. Alyosha thought that he had fallen from weakness, but this was not so. The elder distinctly and deliberately bowed down at Dmitri’s feet till his forehead touched the floor. Alyosha was so astounded that he failed to assist him when he got up again. There was a faint smile on his lips.\n\n“Good-by! Forgive me, all of you!” he said, bowing on all sides to his guests.\n\nDmitri stood for a few moments in amazement. Bowing down to him—what did it mean? Suddenly he cried aloud, “Oh, God!” hid his face in his hands, and rushed out of the room. All the guests flocked out after him, in their confusion not saying good-by, or bowing to their host. Only the monks went up to him again for a blessing.\n\n“What did it mean, falling at his feet like that? Was it symbolic or what?” said Fyodor Pavlovitch, suddenly quieted and trying to reopen conversation without venturing to address anybody in particular. They were all passing out of the precincts of the hermitage at the moment.\n\n“I can’t answer for a madhouse and for madmen,” Miüsov answered at once ill-humoredly, “but I will spare myself your company, Fyodor Pavlovitch, and, trust me, for ever. Where’s that monk?”\n\n“That monk,” that is, the monk who had invited them to dine with the Superior, did not keep them waiting. He met them as soon as they came down the steps from the elder’s cell, as though he had been waiting for them all the time.\n\n“Reverend Father, kindly do me a favor. Convey my deepest respect to the Father Superior, apologize for me, personally, Miüsov, to his reverence, telling him that I deeply regret that owing to unforeseen circumstances I am unable to have the honor of being present at his table, greatly as I should desire to do so,” Miüsov said irritably to the monk.\n\n“And that unforeseen circumstance, of course, is myself,” Fyodor Pavlovitch cut in immediately. “Do you hear, Father; this gentleman doesn’t want to remain in my company or else he’d come at once. And you shall go, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, pray go to the Father Superior and good appetite to you. I will decline, and not you. Home, home, I’ll eat at home, I don’t feel equal to it here, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, my amiable relative.”\n\n“I am not your relative and never have been, you contemptible man!”\n\n“I said it on purpose to madden you, because you always disclaim the relationship, though you really are a relation in spite of your shuffling. I’ll prove it by the church calendar. As for you, Ivan, stay if you like. I’ll send the horses for you later. Propriety requires you to go to the Father Superior, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, to apologize for the disturbance we’ve been making....”\n\n“Is it true that you are going home? Aren’t you lying?”\n\n“Pyotr Alexandrovitch! How could I dare after what’s happened! Forgive me, gentlemen, I was carried away! And upset besides! And, indeed, I am ashamed. Gentlemen, one man has the heart of Alexander of Macedon and another the heart of the little dog Fido. Mine is that of the little dog Fido. I am ashamed! After such an escapade how can I go to dinner, to gobble up the monastery’s sauces? I am ashamed, I can’t. You must excuse me!”\n\n“The devil only knows, what if he deceives us?” thought Miüsov, still hesitating, and watching the retreating buffoon with distrustful eyes. The latter turned round, and noticing that Miüsov was watching him, waved him a kiss.\n\n“Well, are you coming to the Superior?” Miüsov asked Ivan abruptly.\n\n“Why not? I was especially invited yesterday.”\n\n“Unfortunately I feel myself compelled to go to this confounded dinner,” said Miüsov with the same irritability, regardless of the fact that the monk was listening. “We ought, at least, to apologize for the disturbance, and explain that it was not our doing. What do you think?”\n\n“Yes, we must explain that it wasn’t our doing. Besides, father won’t be there,” observed Ivan.\n\n“Well, I should hope not! Confound this dinner!”\n\nThey all walked on, however. The monk listened in silence. On the road through the copse he made one observation however—that the Father Superior had been waiting a long time, and that they were more than half an hour late. He received no answer. Miüsov looked with hatred at Ivan.\n\n“Here he is, going to the dinner as though nothing had happened,” he thought. “A brazen face, and the conscience of a Karamazov!”\n\n# Chapter VII. A Young Man Bent On A Career\n\nAlyosha helped Father Zossima to his bedroom and seated him on his bed. It was a little room furnished with the bare necessities. There was a narrow iron bedstead, with a strip of felt for a mattress. In the corner, under the ikons, was a reading-desk with a cross and the Gospel lying on it. The elder sank exhausted on the bed. His eyes glittered and he breathed hard. He looked intently at Alyosha, as though considering something.\n\n“Go, my dear boy, go. Porfiry is enough for me. Make haste, you are needed there, go and wait at the Father Superior’s table.”\n\n“Let me stay here,” Alyosha entreated.\n\n“You are more needed there. There is no peace there. You will wait, and be of service. If evil spirits rise up, repeat a prayer. And remember, my son”—the elder liked to call him that—“this is not the place for you in the future. When it is God’s will to call me, leave the monastery. Go away for good.”\n\nAlyosha started.\n\n“What is it? This is not your place for the time. I bless you for great service in the world. Yours will be a long pilgrimage. And you will have to take a wife, too. You will have to bear all before you come back. There will be much to do. But I don’t doubt of you, and so I send you forth. Christ is with you. Do not abandon Him and He will not abandon you. You will see great sorrow, and in that sorrow you will be happy. This is my last message to you: in sorrow seek happiness. Work, work unceasingly. Remember my words, for although I shall talk with you again, not only my days but my hours are numbered.”\n\nAlyosha’s face again betrayed strong emotion. The corners of his mouth quivered.\n\n“What is it again?” Father Zossima asked, smiling gently. “The worldly may follow the dead with tears, but here we rejoice over the father who is departing. We rejoice and pray for him. Leave me, I must pray. Go, and make haste. Be near your brothers. And not near one only, but near both.”\n\nFather Zossima raised his hand to bless him. Alyosha could make no protest, though he had a great longing to remain. He longed, moreover, to ask the significance of his bowing to Dmitri, the question was on the tip of his tongue, but he dared not ask it. He knew that the elder would have explained it unasked if he had thought fit. But evidently it was not his will. That action had made a terrible impression on Alyosha; he believed blindly in its mysterious significance. Mysterious, and perhaps awful.\n\nAs he hastened out of the hermitage precincts to reach the monastery in time to serve at the Father Superior’s dinner, he felt a sudden pang at his heart, and stopped short. He seemed to hear again Father Zossima’s words, foretelling his approaching end. What he had foretold so exactly must infallibly come to pass. Alyosha believed that implicitly. But how could he be left without him? How could he live without seeing and hearing him? Where should he go? He had told him not to weep, and to leave the monastery. Good God! It was long since Alyosha had known such anguish. He hurried through the copse that divided the monastery from the hermitage, and unable to bear the burden of his thoughts, he gazed at the ancient pines beside the path. He had not far to go—about five hundred paces. He expected to meet no one at that hour, but at the first turn of the path he noticed Rakitin. He was waiting for some one.\n\n“Are you waiting for me?” asked Alyosha, overtaking him.\n\n“Yes,” grinned Rakitin. “You are hurrying to the Father Superior, I know; he has a banquet. There’s not been such a banquet since the Superior entertained the Bishop and General Pahatov, do you remember? I shan’t be there, but you go and hand the sauces. Tell me one thing, Alexey, what does that vision mean? That’s what I want to ask you.”\n\n“What vision?”\n\n“That bowing to your brother, Dmitri. And didn’t he tap the ground with his forehead, too!”\n\n“You speak of Father Zossima?”\n\n“Yes, of Father Zossima.”\n\n“Tapped the ground?”\n\n“Ah, an irreverent expression! Well, what of it? Anyway, what does that vision mean?”\n\n“I don’t know what it means, Misha.”\n\n“I knew he wouldn’t explain it to you! There’s nothing wonderful about it, of course, only the usual holy mummery. But there was an object in the performance. All the pious people in the town will talk about it and spread the story through the province, wondering what it meant. To my thinking the old man really has a keen nose; he sniffed a crime. Your house stinks of it.”\n\n“What crime?”\n\nRakitin evidently had something he was eager to speak of.\n\n“It’ll be in your family, this crime. Between your brothers and your rich old father. So Father Zossima flopped down to be ready for what may turn up. If something happens later on, it’ll be: ‘Ah, the holy man foresaw it, prophesied it!’ though it’s a poor sort of prophecy, flopping like that. ‘Ah, but it was symbolic,’ they’ll say, ‘an allegory,’ and the devil knows what all! It’ll be remembered to his glory: ‘He predicted the crime and marked the criminal!’ That’s always the way with these crazy fanatics; they cross themselves at the tavern and throw stones at the temple. Like your elder, he takes a stick to a just man and falls at the feet of a murderer.”\n\n“What crime? What murderer? What do you mean?”\n\nAlyosha stopped dead. Rakitin stopped, too.\n\n“What murderer? As though you didn’t know! I’ll bet you’ve thought of it before. That’s interesting, too, by the way. Listen, Alyosha, you always speak the truth, though you’re always between two stools. Have you thought of it or not? Answer.”\n\n“I have,” answered Alyosha in a low voice. Even Rakitin was taken aback.\n\n“What? Have you really?” he cried.\n\n“I ... I’ve not exactly thought it,” muttered Alyosha, “but directly you began speaking so strangely, I fancied I had thought of it myself.”\n\n“You see? (And how well you expressed it!) Looking at your father and your brother Mitya to-day you thought of a crime. Then I’m not mistaken?”\n\n“But wait, wait a minute,” Alyosha broke in uneasily. “What has led you to see all this? Why does it interest you? That’s the first question.”\n\n“Two questions, disconnected, but natural. I’ll deal with them separately. What led me to see it? I shouldn’t have seen it, if I hadn’t suddenly understood your brother Dmitri, seen right into the very heart of him all at once. I caught the whole man from one trait. These very honest but passionate people have a line which mustn’t be crossed. If it were, he’d run at your father with a knife. But your father’s a drunken and abandoned old sinner, who can never draw the line—if they both let themselves go, they’ll both come to grief.”\n\n“No, Misha, no. If that’s all, you’ve reassured me. It won’t come to that.”\n\n“But why are you trembling? Let me tell you; he may be honest, our Mitya (he is stupid, but honest), but he’s—a sensualist. That’s the very definition and inner essence of him. It’s your father has handed him on his low sensuality. Do you know, I simply wonder at you, Alyosha, how you can have kept your purity. You’re a Karamazov too, you know! In your family sensuality is carried to a disease. But now, these three sensualists are watching one another, with their knives in their belts. The three of them are knocking their heads together, and you may be the fourth.”\n\n“You are mistaken about that woman. Dmitri—despises her,” said Alyosha, with a sort of shudder.\n\n“Grushenka? No, brother, he doesn’t despise her. Since he has openly abandoned his betrothed for her, he doesn’t despise her. There’s something here, my dear boy, that you don’t understand yet. A man will fall in love with some beauty, with a woman’s body, or even with a part of a woman’s body (a sensualist can understand that), and he’ll abandon his own children for her, sell his father and mother, and his country, Russia, too. If he’s honest, he’ll steal; if he’s humane, he’ll murder; if he’s faithful, he’ll deceive. Pushkin, the poet of women’s feet, sung of their feet in his verse. Others don’t sing their praises, but they can’t look at their feet without a thrill—and it’s not only their feet. Contempt’s no help here, brother, even if he did despise Grushenka. He does, but he can’t tear himself away.”\n\n“I understand that,” Alyosha jerked out suddenly.\n\n“Really? Well, I dare say you do understand, since you blurt it out at the first word,” said Rakitin, malignantly. “That escaped you unawares, and the confession’s the more precious. So it’s a familiar subject; you’ve thought about it already, about sensuality, I mean! Oh, you virgin soul! You’re a quiet one, Alyosha, you’re a saint, I know, but the devil only knows what you’ve thought about, and what you know already! You are pure, but you’ve been down into the depths.... I’ve been watching you a long time. You’re a Karamazov yourself; you’re a thorough Karamazov—no doubt birth and selection have something to answer for. You’re a sensualist from your father, a crazy saint from your mother. Why do you tremble? Is it true, then? Do you know, Grushenka has been begging me to bring you along. ‘I’ll pull off his cassock,’ she says. You can’t think how she keeps begging me to bring you. I wondered why she took such an interest in you. Do you know, she’s an extraordinary woman, too!”\n\n“Thank her and say I’m not coming,” said Alyosha, with a strained smile. “Finish what you were saying, Misha. I’ll tell you my idea after.”\n\n“There’s nothing to finish. It’s all clear. It’s the same old tune, brother. If even you are a sensualist at heart, what of your brother, Ivan? He’s a Karamazov, too. What is at the root of all you Karamazovs is that you’re all sensual, grasping and crazy! Your brother Ivan writes theological articles in joke, for some idiotic, unknown motive of his own, though he’s an atheist, and he admits it’s a fraud himself—that’s your brother Ivan. He’s trying to get Mitya’s betrothed for himself, and I fancy he’ll succeed, too. And what’s more, it’s with Mitya’s consent. For Mitya will surrender his betrothed to him to be rid of her, and escape to Grushenka. And he’s ready to do that in spite of all his nobility and disinterestedness. Observe that. Those are the most fatal people! Who the devil can make you out? He recognizes his vileness and goes on with it! Let me tell you, too, the old man, your father, is standing in Mitya’s way now. He has suddenly gone crazy over Grushenka. His mouth waters at the sight of her. It’s simply on her account he made that scene in the cell just now, simply because Miüsov called her an ‘abandoned creature.’ He’s worse than a tom-cat in love. At first she was only employed by him in connection with his taverns and in some other shady business, but now he has suddenly realized all she is and has gone wild about her. He keeps pestering her with his offers, not honorable ones, of course. And they’ll come into collision, the precious father and son, on that path! But Grushenka favors neither of them, she’s still playing with them, and teasing them both, considering which she can get most out of. For though she could filch a lot of money from the papa he wouldn’t marry her, and maybe he’ll turn stingy in the end, and keep his purse shut. That’s where Mitya’s value comes in; he has no money, but he’s ready to marry her. Yes, ready to marry her! to abandon his betrothed, a rare beauty, Katerina Ivanovna, who’s rich, and the daughter of a colonel, and to marry Grushenka, who has been the mistress of a dissolute old merchant, Samsonov, a coarse, uneducated, provincial mayor. Some murderous conflict may well come to pass from all this, and that’s what your brother Ivan is waiting for. It would suit him down to the ground. He’ll carry off Katerina Ivanovna, for whom he is languishing, and pocket her dowry of sixty thousand. That’s very alluring to start with, for a man of no consequence and a beggar. And, take note, he won’t be wronging Mitya, but doing him the greatest service. For I know as a fact that Mitya only last week, when he was with some gypsy girls drunk in a tavern, cried out aloud that he was unworthy of his betrothed, Katya, but that his brother Ivan, he was the man who deserved her. And Katerina Ivanovna will not in the end refuse such a fascinating man as Ivan. She’s hesitating between the two of them already. And how has that Ivan won you all, so that you all worship him? He is laughing at you, and enjoying himself at your expense.”\n\n“How do you know? How can you speak so confidently?” Alyosha asked sharply, frowning.\n\n“Why do you ask, and are frightened at my answer? It shows that you know I’m speaking the truth.”\n\n“You don’t like Ivan. Ivan wouldn’t be tempted by money.”\n\n“Really? And the beauty of Katerina Ivanovna? It’s not only the money, though a fortune of sixty thousand is an attraction.”\n\n“Ivan is above that. He wouldn’t make up to any one for thousands. It is not money, it’s not comfort Ivan is seeking. Perhaps it’s suffering he is seeking.”\n\n“What wild dream now? Oh, you—aristocrats!”\n\n“Ah, Misha, he has a stormy spirit. His mind is in bondage. He is haunted by a great, unsolved doubt. He is one of those who don’t want millions, but an answer to their questions.”\n\n“That’s plagiarism, Alyosha. You’re quoting your elder’s phrases. Ah, Ivan has set you a problem!” cried Rakitin, with undisguised malice. His face changed, and his lips twitched. “And the problem’s a stupid one. It is no good guessing it. Rack your brains—you’ll understand it. His article is absurd and ridiculous. And did you hear his stupid theory just now: if there’s no immortality of the soul, then there’s no virtue, and everything is lawful. (And by the way, do you remember how your brother Mitya cried out: ‘I will remember!’) An attractive theory for scoundrels!—(I’m being abusive, that’s stupid.) Not for scoundrels, but for pedantic poseurs, ‘haunted by profound, unsolved doubts.’ He’s showing off, and what it all comes to is, ‘on the one hand we cannot but admit’ and ‘on the other it must be confessed!’ His whole theory is a fraud! Humanity will find in itself the power to live for virtue even without believing in immortality. It will find it in love for freedom, for equality, for fraternity.”\n\nRakitin could hardly restrain himself in his heat, but, suddenly, as though remembering something, he stopped short.\n\n“Well, that’s enough,” he said, with a still more crooked smile. “Why are you laughing? Do you think I’m a vulgar fool?”\n\n“No, I never dreamed of thinking you a vulgar fool. You are clever but ... never mind, I was silly to smile. I understand your getting hot about it, Misha. I guess from your warmth that you are not indifferent to Katerina Ivanovna yourself; I’ve suspected that for a long time, brother, that’s why you don’t like my brother Ivan. Are you jealous of him?”\n\n“And jealous of her money, too? Won’t you add that?”\n\n“I’ll say nothing about money. I am not going to insult you.”\n\n“I believe it, since you say so, but confound you, and your brother Ivan with you. Don’t you understand that one might very well dislike him, apart from Katerina Ivanovna. And why the devil should I like him? He condescends to abuse me, you know. Why haven’t I a right to abuse him?”\n\n“I never heard of his saying anything about you, good or bad. He doesn’t speak of you at all.”\n\n“But I heard that the day before yesterday at Katerina Ivanovna’s he was abusing me for all he was worth—you see what an interest he takes in your humble servant. And which is the jealous one after that, brother, I can’t say. He was so good as to express the opinion that, if I don’t go in for the career of an archimandrite in the immediate future and don’t become a monk, I shall be sure to go to Petersburg and get on to some solid magazine as a reviewer, that I shall write for the next ten years, and in the end become the owner of the magazine, and bring it out on the liberal and atheistic side, with a socialistic tinge, with a tiny gloss of socialism, but keeping a sharp look out all the time, that is, keeping in with both sides and hoodwinking the fools. According to your brother’s account, the tinge of socialism won’t hinder me from laying by the proceeds and investing them under the guidance of some Jew, till at the end of my career I build a great house in Petersburg and move my publishing offices to it, and let out the upper stories to lodgers. He has even chosen the place for it, near the new stone bridge across the Neva, which they say is to be built in Petersburg.”\n\n“Ah, Misha, that’s just what will really happen, every word of it,” cried Alyosha, unable to restrain a good-humored smile.\n\n“You are pleased to be sarcastic, too, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“No, no, I’m joking, forgive me. I’ve something quite different in my mind. But, excuse me, who can have told you all this? You can’t have been at Katerina Ivanovna’s yourself when he was talking about you?”\n\n“I wasn’t there, but Dmitri Fyodorovitch was; and I heard him tell it with my own ears; if you want to know, he didn’t tell me, but I overheard him, unintentionally, of course, for I was sitting in Grushenka’s bedroom and I couldn’t go away because Dmitri Fyodorovitch was in the next room.”\n\n“Oh, yes, I’d forgotten she was a relation of yours.”\n\n“A relation! That Grushenka a relation of mine!” cried Rakitin, turning crimson. “Are you mad? You’re out of your mind!”\n\n“Why, isn’t she a relation of yours? I heard so.”\n\n“Where can you have heard it? You Karamazovs brag of being an ancient, noble family, though your father used to run about playing the buffoon at other men’s tables, and was only admitted to the kitchen as a favor. I may be only a priest’s son, and dirt in the eyes of noblemen like you, but don’t insult me so lightly and wantonly. I have a sense of honor, too, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I couldn’t be a relation of Grushenka, a common harlot. I beg you to understand that!”\n\nRakitin was intensely irritated.\n\n“Forgive me, for goodness’ sake, I had no idea ... besides ... how can you call her a harlot? Is she ... that sort of woman?” Alyosha flushed suddenly. “I tell you again, I heard that she was a relation of yours. You often go to see her, and you told me yourself you’re not her lover. I never dreamed that you of all people had such contempt for her! Does she really deserve it?”\n\n“I may have reasons of my own for visiting her. That’s not your business. But as for relationship, your brother, or even your father, is more likely to make her yours than mine. Well, here we are. You’d better go to the kitchen. Hullo! what’s wrong, what is it? Are we late? They can’t have finished dinner so soon! Have the Karamazovs been making trouble again? No doubt they have. Here’s your father and your brother Ivan after him. They’ve broken out from the Father Superior’s. And look, Father Isidor’s shouting out something after them from the steps. And your father’s shouting and waving his arms. I expect he’s swearing. Bah, and there goes Miüsov driving away in his carriage. You see, he’s going. And there’s old Maximov running!—there must have been a row. There can’t have been any dinner. Surely they’ve not been beating the Father Superior! Or have they, perhaps, been beaten? It would serve them right!”\n\nThere was reason for Rakitin’s exclamations. There had been a scandalous, an unprecedented scene. It had all come from the impulse of a moment.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Scandalous Scene\n\nMiüsov, as a man of breeding and delicacy, could not but feel some inward qualms, when he reached the Father Superior’s with Ivan: he felt ashamed of having lost his temper. He felt that he ought to have disdained that despicable wretch, Fyodor Pavlovitch, too much to have been upset by him in Father Zossima’s cell, and so to have forgotten himself. “The monks were not to blame, in any case,” he reflected, on the steps. “And if they’re decent people here (and the Father Superior, I understand, is a nobleman) why not be friendly and courteous with them? I won’t argue, I’ll fall in with everything, I’ll win them by politeness, and ... and ... show them that I’ve nothing to do with that Æsop, that buffoon, that Pierrot, and have merely been taken in over this affair, just as they have.”\n\nHe determined to drop his litigation with the monastery, and relinquish his claims to the wood-cutting and fishery rights at once. He was the more ready to do this because the rights had become much less valuable, and he had indeed the vaguest idea where the wood and river in question were.\n\nThese excellent intentions were strengthened when he entered the Father Superior’s dining-room, though, strictly speaking, it was not a dining-room, for the Father Superior had only two rooms altogether; they were, however, much larger and more comfortable than Father Zossima’s. But there was no great luxury about the furnishing of these rooms either. The furniture was of mahogany, covered with leather, in the old-fashioned style of 1820; the floor was not even stained, but everything was shining with cleanliness, and there were many choice flowers in the windows; the most sumptuous thing in the room at the moment was, of course, the beautifully decorated table. The cloth was clean, the service shone; there were three kinds of well-baked bread, two bottles of wine, two of excellent mead, and a large glass jug of kvas—both the latter made in the monastery, and famous in the neighborhood. There was no vodka. Rakitin related afterwards that there were five dishes: fish-soup made of sterlets, served with little fish patties; then boiled fish served in a special way; then salmon cutlets, ice pudding and compote, and finally, blanc-mange. Rakitin found out about all these good things, for he could not resist peeping into the kitchen, where he already had a footing. He had a footing everywhere, and got information about everything. He was of an uneasy and envious temper. He was well aware of his own considerable abilities, and nervously exaggerated them in his self-conceit. He knew he would play a prominent part of some sort, but Alyosha, who was attached to him, was distressed to see that his friend Rakitin was dishonorable, and quite unconscious of being so himself, considering, on the contrary, that because he would not steal money left on the table he was a man of the highest integrity. Neither Alyosha nor any one else could have influenced him in that.\n\nRakitin, of course, was a person of too little consequence to be invited to the dinner, to which Father Iosif, Father Païssy, and one other monk were the only inmates of the monastery invited. They were already waiting when Miüsov, Kalganov, and Ivan arrived. The other guest, Maximov, stood a little aside, waiting also. The Father Superior stepped into the middle of the room to receive his guests. He was a tall, thin, but still vigorous old man, with black hair streaked with gray, and a long, grave, ascetic face. He bowed to his guests in silence. But this time they approached to receive his blessing. Miüsov even tried to kiss his hand, but the Father Superior drew it back in time to avoid the salute. But Ivan and Kalganov went through the ceremony in the most simple-hearted and complete manner, kissing his hand as peasants do.\n\n“We must apologize most humbly, your reverence,” began Miüsov, simpering affably, and speaking in a dignified and respectful tone. “Pardon us for having come alone without the gentleman you invited, Fyodor Pavlovitch. He felt obliged to decline the honor of your hospitality, and not without reason. In the reverend Father Zossima’s cell he was carried away by the unhappy dissension with his son, and let fall words which were quite out of keeping ... in fact, quite unseemly ... as”—he glanced at the monks—“your reverence is, no doubt, already aware. And therefore, recognizing that he had been to blame, he felt sincere regret and shame, and begged me, and his son Ivan Fyodorovitch, to convey to you his apologies and regrets. In brief, he hopes and desires to make amends later. He asks your blessing, and begs you to forget what has taken place.”\n\nAs he uttered the last word of his tirade, Miüsov completely recovered his self-complacency, and all traces of his former irritation disappeared. He fully and sincerely loved humanity again.\n\nThe Father Superior listened to him with dignity, and, with a slight bend of the head, replied:\n\n“I sincerely deplore his absence. Perhaps at our table he might have learnt to like us, and we him. Pray be seated, gentlemen.”\n\nHe stood before the holy image, and began to say grace, aloud. All bent their heads reverently, and Maximov clasped his hands before him, with peculiar fervor.\n\nIt was at this moment that Fyodor Pavlovitch played his last prank. It must be noted that he really had meant to go home, and really had felt the impossibility of going to dine with the Father Superior as though nothing had happened, after his disgraceful behavior in the elder’s cell. Not that he was so very much ashamed of himself—quite the contrary perhaps. But still he felt it would be unseemly to go to dinner. Yet his creaking carriage had hardly been brought to the steps of the hotel, and he had hardly got into it, when he suddenly stopped short. He remembered his own words at the elder’s: “I always feel when I meet people that I am lower than all, and that they all take me for a buffoon; so I say let me play the buffoon, for you are, every one of you, stupider and lower than I.” He longed to revenge himself on every one for his own unseemliness. He suddenly recalled how he had once in the past been asked, “Why do you hate so and so, so much?” And he had answered them, with his shameless impudence, “I’ll tell you. He has done me no harm. But I played him a dirty trick, and ever since I have hated him.”\n\nRemembering that now, he smiled quietly and malignantly, hesitating for a moment. His eyes gleamed, and his lips positively quivered. “Well, since I have begun, I may as well go on,” he decided. His predominant sensation at that moment might be expressed in the following words, “Well, there is no rehabilitating myself now. So let me shame them for all I am worth. I will show them I don’t care what they think—that’s all!”\n\nHe told the coachman to wait, while with rapid steps he returned to the monastery and straight to the Father Superior’s. He had no clear idea what he would do, but he knew that he could not control himself, and that a touch might drive him to the utmost limits of obscenity, but only to obscenity, to nothing criminal, nothing for which he could be legally punished. In the last resort, he could always restrain himself, and had marveled indeed at himself, on that score, sometimes. He appeared in the Father Superior’s dining-room, at the moment when the prayer was over, and all were moving to the table. Standing in the doorway, he scanned the company, and laughing his prolonged, impudent, malicious chuckle, looked them all boldly in the face. “They thought I had gone, and here I am again,” he cried to the whole room.\n\nFor one moment every one stared at him without a word; and at once every one felt that something revolting, grotesque, positively scandalous, was about to happen. Miüsov passed immediately from the most benevolent frame of mind to the most savage. All the feelings that had subsided and died down in his heart revived instantly.\n\n“No! this I cannot endure!” he cried. “I absolutely cannot! and ... I certainly cannot!”\n\nThe blood rushed to his head. He positively stammered; but he was beyond thinking of style, and he seized his hat.\n\n“What is it he cannot?” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, “that he absolutely cannot and certainly cannot? Your reverence, am I to come in or not? Will you receive me as your guest?”\n\n“You are welcome with all my heart,” answered the Superior. “Gentlemen!” he added, “I venture to beg you most earnestly to lay aside your dissensions, and to be united in love and family harmony—with prayer to the Lord at our humble table.”\n\n“No, no, it is impossible!” cried Miüsov, beside himself.\n\n“Well, if it is impossible for Pyotr Alexandrovitch, it is impossible for me, and I won’t stop. That is why I came. I will keep with Pyotr Alexandrovitch everywhere now. If you will go away, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, I will go away too, if you remain, I will remain. You stung him by what you said about family harmony, Father Superior, he does not admit he is my relation. That’s right, isn’t it, von Sohn? Here’s von Sohn. How are you, von Sohn?”\n\n“Do you mean me?” muttered Maximov, puzzled.\n\n“Of course I mean you,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch. “Who else? The Father Superior could not be von Sohn.”\n\n“But I am not von Sohn either. I am Maximov.”\n\n“No, you are von Sohn. Your reverence, do you know who von Sohn was? It was a famous murder case. He was killed in a house of harlotry—I believe that is what such places are called among you—he was killed and robbed, and in spite of his venerable age, he was nailed up in a box and sent from Petersburg to Moscow in the luggage van, and while they were nailing him up, the harlots sang songs and played the harp, that is to say, the piano. So this is that very von Sohn. He has risen from the dead, hasn’t he, von Sohn?”\n\n“What is happening? What’s this?” voices were heard in the group of monks.\n\n“Let us go,” cried Miüsov, addressing Kalganov.\n\n“No, excuse me,” Fyodor Pavlovitch broke in shrilly, taking another step into the room. “Allow me to finish. There in the cell you blamed me for behaving disrespectfully just because I spoke of eating gudgeon, Pyotr Alexandrovitch. Miüsov, my relation, prefers to have plus de noblesse que de sincérité in his words, but I prefer in mine plus de sincérité que de noblesse, and—damn the noblesse! That’s right, isn’t it, von Sohn? Allow me, Father Superior, though I am a buffoon and play the buffoon, yet I am the soul of honor, and I want to speak my mind. Yes, I am the soul of honor, while in Pyotr Alexandrovitch there is wounded vanity and nothing else. I came here perhaps to have a look and speak my mind. My son, Alexey, is here, being saved. I am his father; I care for his welfare, and it is my duty to care. While I’ve been playing the fool, I have been listening and having a look on the sly; and now I want to give you the last act of the performance. You know how things are with us? As a thing falls, so it lies. As a thing once has fallen, so it must lie for ever. Not a bit of it! I want to get up again. Holy Father, I am indignant with you. Confession is a great sacrament, before which I am ready to bow down reverently; but there in the cell, they all kneel down and confess aloud. Can it be right to confess aloud? It was ordained by the holy Fathers to confess in secret: then only your confession will be a mystery, and so it was of old. But how can I explain to him before every one that I did this and that ... well, you understand what—sometimes it would not be proper to talk about it—so it is really a scandal! No, Fathers, one might be carried along with you to the Flagellants, I dare say ... at the first opportunity I shall write to the Synod, and I shall take my son, Alexey, home.”\n\nWe must note here that Fyodor Pavlovitch knew where to look for the weak spot. There had been at one time malicious rumors which had even reached the Archbishop (not only regarding our monastery, but in others where the institution of elders existed) that too much respect was paid to the elders, even to the detriment of the authority of the Superior, that the elders abused the sacrament of confession and so on and so on—absurd charges which had died away of themselves everywhere. But the spirit of folly, which had caught up Fyodor Pavlovitch, and was bearing him on the current of his own nerves into lower and lower depths of ignominy, prompted him with this old slander. Fyodor Pavlovitch did not understand a word of it, and he could not even put it sensibly, for on this occasion no one had been kneeling and confessing aloud in the elder’s cell, so that he could not have seen anything of the kind. He was only speaking from confused memory of old slanders. But as soon as he had uttered his foolish tirade, he felt he had been talking absurd nonsense, and at once longed to prove to his audience, and above all to himself, that he had not been talking nonsense. And, though he knew perfectly well that with each word he would be adding more and more absurdity, he could not restrain himself, and plunged forward blindly.\n\n“How disgraceful!” cried Pyotr Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Pardon me!” said the Father Superior. “It was said of old, ‘Many have begun to speak against me and have uttered evil sayings about me. And hearing it I have said to myself: it is the correction of the Lord and He has sent it to heal my vain soul.’ And so we humbly thank you, honored guest!” and he made Fyodor Pavlovitch a low bow.\n\n“Tut—tut—tut—sanctimoniousness and stock phrases! Old phrases and old gestures. The old lies and formal prostrations. We know all about them. A kiss on the lips and a dagger in the heart, as in Schiller’s Robbers. I don’t like falsehood, Fathers, I want the truth. But the truth is not to be found in eating gudgeon and that I proclaim aloud! Father monks, why do you fast? Why do you expect reward in heaven for that? Why, for reward like that I will come and fast too! No, saintly monk, you try being virtuous in the world, do good to society, without shutting yourself up in a monastery at other people’s expense, and without expecting a reward up aloft for it—you’ll find that a bit harder. I can talk sense, too, Father Superior. What have they got here?” He went up to the table. “Old port wine, mead brewed by the Eliseyev Brothers. Fie, fie, fathers! That is something beyond gudgeon. Look at the bottles the fathers have brought out, he he he! And who has provided it all? The Russian peasant, the laborer, brings here the farthing earned by his horny hand, wringing it from his family and the tax-gatherer! You bleed the people, you know, holy fathers.”\n\n“This is too disgraceful!” said Father Iosif.\n\nFather Païssy kept obstinately silent. Miüsov rushed from the room, and Kalganov after him.\n\n“Well, Father, I will follow Pyotr Alexandrovitch! I am not coming to see you again. You may beg me on your knees, I shan’t come. I sent you a thousand roubles, so you have begun to keep your eye on me. He he he! No, I’ll say no more. I am taking my revenge for my youth, for all the humiliation I endured.” He thumped the table with his fist in a paroxysm of simulated feeling. “This monastery has played a great part in my life! It has cost me many bitter tears. You used to set my wife, the crazy one, against me. You cursed me with bell and book, you spread stories about me all over the place. Enough, fathers! This is the age of Liberalism, the age of steamers and railways. Neither a thousand, nor a hundred roubles, no, nor a hundred farthings will you get out of me!”\n\nIt must be noted again that our monastery never had played any great part in his life, and he never had shed a bitter tear owing to it. But he was so carried away by his simulated emotion, that he was for one moment almost believing it himself. He was so touched he was almost weeping. But at that very instant, he felt that it was time to draw back.\n\nThe Father Superior bowed his head at his malicious lie, and again spoke impressively:\n\n“It is written again, ‘Bear circumspectly and gladly dishonor that cometh upon thee by no act of thine own, be not confounded and hate not him who hath dishonored thee.’ And so will we.”\n\n“Tut, tut, tut! Bethinking thyself and the rest of the rigmarole. Bethink yourselves, Fathers, I will go. But I will take my son, Alexey, away from here for ever, on my parental authority. Ivan Fyodorovitch, my most dutiful son, permit me to order you to follow me. Von Sohn, what have you to stay for? Come and see me now in the town. It is fun there. It is only one short verst; instead of lenten oil, I will give you sucking-pig and kasha. We will have dinner with some brandy and liqueur to it.... I’ve cloudberry wine. Hey, von Sohn, don’t lose your chance.” He went out, shouting and gesticulating.\n\nIt was at that moment Rakitin saw him and pointed him out to Alyosha.\n\n“Alexey!” his father shouted, from far off, catching sight of him. “You come home to me to-day, for good, and bring your pillow and mattress, and leave no trace behind.”\n\nAlyosha stood rooted to the spot, watching the scene in silence. Meanwhile, Fyodor Pavlovitch had got into the carriage, and Ivan was about to follow him in grim silence without even turning to say good-by to Alyosha. But at this point another almost incredible scene of grotesque buffoonery gave the finishing touch to the episode. Maximov suddenly appeared by the side of the carriage. He ran up, panting, afraid of being too late. Rakitin and Alyosha saw him running. He was in such a hurry that in his impatience he put his foot on the step on which Ivan’s left foot was still resting, and clutching the carriage he kept trying to jump in. “I am going with you!” he kept shouting, laughing a thin mirthful laugh with a look of reckless glee in his face. “Take me, too.”\n\n“There!” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, delighted. “Did I not say he was von Sohn. It is von Sohn himself, risen from the dead. Why, how did you tear yourself away? What did you vonsohn there? And how could you get away from the dinner? You must be a brazen-faced fellow! I am that myself, but I am surprised at you, brother! Jump in, jump in! Let him pass, Ivan. It will be fun. He can lie somewhere at our feet. Will you lie at our feet, von Sohn? Or perch on the box with the coachman. Skip on to the box, von Sohn!”\n\nBut Ivan, who had by now taken his seat, without a word gave Maximov a violent punch in the breast and sent him flying. It was quite by chance he did not fall.\n\n“Drive on!” Ivan shouted angrily to the coachman.\n\n“Why, what are you doing, what are you about? Why did you do that?” Fyodor Pavlovitch protested.\n\nBut the carriage had already driven away. Ivan made no reply.\n\n“Well, you are a fellow,” Fyodor Pavlovitch said again.\n\nAfter a pause of two minutes, looking askance at his son, “Why, it was you got up all this monastery business. You urged it, you approved of it. Why are you angry now?”\n\n“You’ve talked rot enough. You might rest a bit now,” Ivan snapped sullenly.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch was silent again for two minutes.\n\n“A drop of brandy would be nice now,” he observed sententiously, but Ivan made no response.\n\n“You shall have some, too, when we get home.”\n\nIvan was still silent.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch waited another two minutes.\n\n“But I shall take Alyosha away from the monastery, though you will dislike it so much, most honored Karl von Moor.”\n\nIvan shrugged his shoulders contemptuously, and turning away stared at the road. And they did not speak again all the way home.\n\n# Book III. The Sensualists Chapter I. In The Servants’ Quarters\n\nThe Karamazovs’ house was far from being in the center of the town, but it was not quite outside it. It was a pleasant-looking old house of two stories, painted gray, with a red iron roof. It was roomy and snug, and might still last many years. There were all sorts of unexpected little cupboards and closets and staircases. There were rats in it, but Fyodor Pavlovitch did not altogether dislike them. “One doesn’t feel so solitary when one’s left alone in the evening,” he used to say. It was his habit to send the servants away to the lodge for the night and to lock himself up alone. The lodge was a roomy and solid building in the yard. Fyodor Pavlovitch used to have the cooking done there, although there was a kitchen in the house; he did not like the smell of cooking, and, winter and summer alike, the dishes were carried in across the courtyard. The house was built for a large family; there was room for five times as many, with their servants. But at the time of our story there was no one living in the house but Fyodor Pavlovitch and his son Ivan. And in the lodge there were only three servants: old Grigory, and his old wife Marfa, and a young man called Smerdyakov. Of these three we must say a few words. Of old Grigory we have said something already. He was firm and determined and went blindly and obstinately for his object, if once he had been brought by any reasons (and they were often very illogical ones) to believe that it was immutably right. He was honest and incorruptible. His wife, Marfa Ignatyevna, had obeyed her husband’s will implicitly all her life, yet she had pestered him terribly after the emancipation of the serfs. She was set on leaving Fyodor Pavlovitch and opening a little shop in Moscow with their small savings. But Grigory decided then, once for all, that “the woman’s talking nonsense, for every woman is dishonest,” and that they ought not to leave their old master, whatever he might be, for “that was now their duty.”\n\n“Do you understand what duty is?” he asked Marfa Ignatyevna.\n\n“I understand what duty means, Grigory Vassilyevitch, but why it’s our duty to stay here I never shall understand,” Marfa answered firmly.\n\n“Well, don’t understand then. But so it shall be. And you hold your tongue.”\n\nAnd so it was. They did not go away, and Fyodor Pavlovitch promised them a small sum for wages, and paid it regularly. Grigory knew, too, that he had an indisputable influence over his master. It was true, and he was aware of it. Fyodor Pavlovitch was an obstinate and cunning buffoon, yet, though his will was strong enough “in some of the affairs of life,” as he expressed it, he found himself, to his surprise, extremely feeble in facing certain other emergencies. He knew his weaknesses and was afraid of them. There are positions in which one has to keep a sharp look out. And that’s not easy without a trustworthy man, and Grigory was a most trustworthy man. Many times in the course of his life Fyodor Pavlovitch had only just escaped a sound thrashing through Grigory’s intervention, and on each occasion the old servant gave him a good lecture. But it wasn’t only thrashings that Fyodor Pavlovitch was afraid of. There were graver occasions, and very subtle and complicated ones, when Fyodor Pavlovitch could not have explained the extraordinary craving for some one faithful and devoted, which sometimes unaccountably came upon him all in a moment. It was almost a morbid condition. Corrupt and often cruel in his lust, like some noxious insect, Fyodor Pavlovitch was sometimes, in moments of drunkenness, overcome by superstitious terror and a moral convulsion which took an almost physical form. “My soul’s simply quaking in my throat at those times,” he used to say. At such moments he liked to feel that there was near at hand, in the lodge if not in the room, a strong, faithful man, virtuous and unlike himself, who had seen all his debauchery and knew all his secrets, but was ready in his devotion to overlook all that, not to oppose him, above all, not to reproach him or threaten him with anything, either in this world or in the next, and, in case of need, to defend him—from whom? From somebody unknown, but terrible and dangerous. What he needed was to feel that there was another man, an old and tried friend, that he might call him in his sick moments merely to look at his face, or, perhaps, exchange some quite irrelevant words with him. And if the old servant were not angry, he felt comforted, and if he were angry, he was more dejected. It happened even (very rarely however) that Fyodor Pavlovitch went at night to the lodge to wake Grigory and fetch him for a moment. When the old man came, Fyodor Pavlovitch would begin talking about the most trivial matters, and would soon let him go again, sometimes even with a jest. And after he had gone, Fyodor Pavlovitch would get into bed with a curse and sleep the sleep of the just. Something of the same sort had happened to Fyodor Pavlovitch on Alyosha’s arrival. Alyosha “pierced his heart” by “living with him, seeing everything and blaming nothing.” Moreover, Alyosha brought with him something his father had never known before: a complete absence of contempt for him and an invariable kindness, a perfectly natural unaffected devotion to the old man who deserved it so little. All this was a complete surprise to the old profligate, who had dropped all family ties. It was a new and surprising experience for him, who had till then loved nothing but “evil.” When Alyosha had left him, he confessed to himself that he had learnt something he had not till then been willing to learn.\n\nI have mentioned already that Grigory had detested Adelaïda Ivanovna, the first wife of Fyodor Pavlovitch and the mother of Dmitri, and that he had, on the contrary, protected Sofya Ivanovna, the poor “crazy woman,” against his master and any one who chanced to speak ill or lightly of her. His sympathy for the unhappy wife had become something sacred to him, so that even now, twenty years after, he could not bear a slighting allusion to her from any one, and would at once check the offender. Externally, Grigory was cold, dignified and taciturn, and spoke, weighing his words, without frivolity. It was impossible to tell at first sight whether he loved his meek, obedient wife; but he really did love her, and she knew it.\n\nMarfa Ignatyevna was by no means foolish; she was probably, indeed, cleverer than her husband, or, at least, more prudent than he in worldly affairs, and yet she had given in to him in everything without question or complaint ever since her marriage, and respected him for his spiritual superiority. It was remarkable how little they spoke to one another in the course of their lives, and only of the most necessary daily affairs. The grave and dignified Grigory thought over all his cares and duties alone, so that Marfa Ignatyevna had long grown used to knowing that he did not need her advice. She felt that her husband respected her silence, and took it as a sign of her good sense. He had never beaten her but once, and then only slightly. Once during the year after Fyodor Pavlovitch’s marriage with Adelaïda Ivanovna, the village girls and women—at that time serfs—were called together before the house to sing and dance. They were beginning “In the Green Meadows,” when Marfa, at that time a young woman, skipped forward and danced “the Russian Dance,” not in the village fashion, but as she had danced it when she was a servant in the service of the rich Miüsov family, in their private theater, where the actors were taught to dance by a dancing master from Moscow. Grigory saw how his wife danced, and, an hour later, at home in their cottage he gave her a lesson, pulling her hair a little. But there it ended: the beating was never repeated, and Marfa Ignatyevna gave up dancing.\n\nGod had not blessed them with children. One child was born but it died. Grigory was fond of children, and was not ashamed of showing it. When Adelaïda Ivanovna had run away, Grigory took Dmitri, then a child of three years old, combed his hair and washed him in a tub with his own hands, and looked after him for almost a year. Afterwards he had looked after Ivan and Alyosha, for which the general’s widow had rewarded him with a slap in the face; but I have already related all that. The only happiness his own child had brought him had been in the anticipation of its birth. When it was born, he was overwhelmed with grief and horror. The baby had six fingers. Grigory was so crushed by this, that he was not only silent till the day of the christening, but kept away in the garden. It was spring, and he spent three days digging the kitchen garden. The third day was fixed for christening the baby: mean-time Grigory had reached a conclusion. Going into the cottage where the clergy were assembled and the visitors had arrived, including Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was to stand god-father, he suddenly announced that the baby “ought not to be christened at all.” He announced this quietly, briefly, forcing out his words, and gazing with dull intentness at the priest.\n\n“Why not?” asked the priest with good-humored surprise.\n\n“Because it’s a dragon,” muttered Grigory.\n\n“A dragon? What dragon?”\n\nGrigory did not speak for some time. “It’s a confusion of nature,” he muttered vaguely, but firmly, and obviously unwilling to say more.\n\nThey laughed, and of course christened the poor baby. Grigory prayed earnestly at the font, but his opinion of the new-born child remained unchanged. Yet he did not interfere in any way. As long as the sickly infant lived he scarcely looked at it, tried indeed not to notice it, and for the most part kept out of the cottage. But when, at the end of a fortnight, the baby died of thrush, he himself laid the child in its little coffin, looked at it in profound grief, and when they were filling up the shallow little grave he fell on his knees and bowed down to the earth. He did not for years afterwards mention his child, nor did Marfa speak of the baby before him, and, even if Grigory were not present, she never spoke of it above a whisper. Marfa observed that, from the day of the burial, he devoted himself to “religion,” and took to reading the Lives of the Saints, for the most part sitting alone and in silence, and always putting on his big, round, silver-rimmed spectacles. He rarely read aloud, only perhaps in Lent. He was fond of the Book of Job, and had somehow got hold of a copy of the sayings and sermons of “the God-fearing Father Isaac the Syrian,” which he read persistently for years together, understanding very little of it, but perhaps prizing and loving it the more for that. Of late he had begun to listen to the doctrines of the sect of Flagellants settled in the neighborhood. He was evidently shaken by them, but judged it unfitting to go over to the new faith. His habit of theological reading gave him an expression of still greater gravity.\n\nHe was perhaps predisposed to mysticism. And the birth of his deformed child, and its death, had, as though by special design, been accompanied by another strange and marvelous event, which, as he said later, had left a “stamp” upon his soul. It happened that, on the very night after the burial of his child, Marfa was awakened by the wail of a new-born baby. She was frightened and waked her husband. He listened and said he thought it was more like some one groaning, “it might be a woman.” He got up and dressed. It was a rather warm night in May. As he went down the steps, he distinctly heard groans coming from the garden. But the gate from the yard into the garden was locked at night, and there was no other way of entering it, for it was enclosed all round by a strong, high fence. Going back into the house, Grigory lighted a lantern, took the garden key, and taking no notice of the hysterical fears of his wife, who was still persuaded that she heard a child crying, and that it was her own baby crying and calling for her, went into the garden in silence. There he heard at once that the groans came from the bath-house that stood near the garden gate, and that they were the groans of a woman. Opening the door of the bath-house, he saw a sight which petrified him. An idiot girl, who wandered about the streets and was known to the whole town by the nickname of Lizaveta Smerdyastchaya (Stinking Lizaveta), had got into the bath-house and had just given birth to a child. She lay dying with the baby beside her. She said nothing, for she had never been able to speak. But her story needs a chapter to itself.\n\n# Chapter II. Lizaveta\n\nThere was one circumstance which struck Grigory particularly, and confirmed a very unpleasant and revolting suspicion. This Lizaveta was a dwarfish creature, “not five foot within a wee bit,” as many of the pious old women said pathetically about her, after her death. Her broad, healthy, red face had a look of blank idiocy and the fixed stare in her eyes was unpleasant, in spite of their meek expression. She wandered about, summer and winter alike, barefooted, wearing nothing but a hempen smock. Her coarse, almost black hair curled like lamb’s wool, and formed a sort of huge cap on her head. It was always crusted with mud, and had leaves, bits of stick, and shavings clinging to it, as she always slept on the ground and in the dirt. Her father, a homeless, sickly drunkard, called Ilya, had lost everything and lived many years as a workman with some well-to-do tradespeople. Her mother had long been dead. Spiteful and diseased, Ilya used to beat Lizaveta inhumanly whenever she returned to him. But she rarely did so, for every one in the town was ready to look after her as being an idiot, and so specially dear to God. Ilya’s employers, and many others in the town, especially of the tradespeople, tried to clothe her better, and always rigged her out with high boots and sheepskin coat for the winter. But, although she allowed them to dress her up without resisting, she usually went away, preferably to the cathedral porch, and taking off all that had been given her—kerchief, sheepskin, skirt or boots—she left them there and walked away barefoot in her smock as before. It happened on one occasion that a new governor of the province, making a tour of inspection in our town, saw Lizaveta, and was wounded in his tenderest susceptibilities. And though he was told she was an idiot, he pronounced that for a young woman of twenty to wander about in nothing but a smock was a breach of the proprieties, and must not occur again. But the governor went his way, and Lizaveta was left as she was. At last her father died, which made her even more acceptable in the eyes of the religious persons of the town, as an orphan. In fact, every one seemed to like her; even the boys did not tease her, and the boys of our town, especially the schoolboys, are a mischievous set. She would walk into strange houses, and no one drove her away. Every one was kind to her and gave her something. If she were given a copper, she would take it, and at once drop it in the alms-jug of the church or prison. If she were given a roll or bun in the market, she would hand it to the first child she met. Sometimes she would stop one of the richest ladies in the town and give it to her, and the lady would be pleased to take it. She herself never tasted anything but black bread and water. If she went into an expensive shop, where there were costly goods or money lying about, no one kept watch on her, for they knew that if she saw thousands of roubles overlooked by them, she would not have touched a farthing. She scarcely ever went to church. She slept either in the church porch or climbed over a hurdle (there are many hurdles instead of fences to this day in our town) into a kitchen garden. She used at least once a week to turn up “at home,” that is at the house of her father’s former employers, and in the winter went there every night, and slept either in the passage or the cowhouse. People were amazed that she could stand such a life, but she was accustomed to it, and, although she was so tiny, she was of a robust constitution. Some of the townspeople declared that she did all this only from pride, but that is hardly credible. She could hardly speak, and only from time to time uttered an inarticulate grunt. How could she have been proud?\n\nIt happened one clear, warm, moonlight night in September (many years ago) five or six drunken revelers were returning from the club at a very late hour, according to our provincial notions. They passed through the “back-way,” which led between the back gardens of the houses, with hurdles on either side. This way leads out on to the bridge over the long, stinking pool which we were accustomed to call a river. Among the nettles and burdocks under the hurdle our revelers saw Lizaveta asleep. They stopped to look at her, laughing, and began jesting with unbridled licentiousness. It occurred to one young gentleman to make the whimsical inquiry whether any one could possibly look upon such an animal as a woman, and so forth.... They all pronounced with lofty repugnance that it was impossible. But Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was among them, sprang forward and declared that it was by no means impossible, and that, indeed, there was a certain piquancy about it, and so on.... It is true that at that time he was overdoing his part as a buffoon. He liked to put himself forward and entertain the company, ostensibly on equal terms, of course, though in reality he was on a servile footing with them. It was just at the time when he had received the news of his first wife’s death in Petersburg, and, with crape upon his hat, was drinking and behaving so shamelessly that even the most reckless among us were shocked at the sight of him. The revelers, of course, laughed at this unexpected opinion; and one of them even began challenging him to act upon it. The others repelled the idea even more emphatically, although still with the utmost hilarity, and at last they went on their way. Later on, Fyodor Pavlovitch swore that he had gone with them, and perhaps it was so, no one knows for certain, and no one ever knew. But five or six months later, all the town was talking, with intense and sincere indignation, of Lizaveta’s condition, and trying to find out who was the miscreant who had wronged her. Then suddenly a terrible rumor was all over the town that this miscreant was no other than Fyodor Pavlovitch. Who set the rumor going? Of that drunken band five had left the town and the only one still among us was an elderly and much respected civil councilor, the father of grown-up daughters, who could hardly have spread the tale, even if there had been any foundation for it. But rumor pointed straight at Fyodor Pavlovitch, and persisted in pointing at him. Of course this was no great grievance to him: he would not have troubled to contradict a set of tradespeople. In those days he was proud, and did not condescend to talk except in his own circle of the officials and nobles, whom he entertained so well.\n\nAt the time, Grigory stood up for his master vigorously. He provoked quarrels and altercations in defense of him and succeeded in bringing some people round to his side. “It’s the wench’s own fault,” he asserted, and the culprit was Karp, a dangerous convict, who had escaped from prison and whose name was well known to us, as he had hidden in our town. This conjecture sounded plausible, for it was remembered that Karp had been in the neighborhood just at that time in the autumn, and had robbed three people. But this affair and all the talk about it did not estrange popular sympathy from the poor idiot. She was better looked after than ever. A well-to-do merchant’s widow named Kondratyev arranged to take her into her house at the end of April, meaning not to let her go out until after the confinement. They kept a constant watch over her, but in spite of their vigilance she escaped on the very last day, and made her way into Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden. How, in her condition, she managed to climb over the high, strong fence remained a mystery. Some maintained that she must have been lifted over by somebody; others hinted at something more uncanny. The most likely explanation is that it happened naturally—that Lizaveta, accustomed to clambering over hurdles to sleep in gardens, had somehow managed to climb this fence, in spite of her condition, and had leapt down, injuring herself.\n\nGrigory rushed to Marfa and sent her to Lizaveta, while he ran to fetch an old midwife who lived close by. They saved the baby, but Lizaveta died at dawn. Grigory took the baby, brought it home, and making his wife sit down, put it on her lap. “A child of God—an orphan is akin to all,” he said, “and to us above others. Our little lost one has sent us this, who has come from the devil’s son and a holy innocent. Nurse him and weep no more.”\n\nSo Marfa brought up the child. He was christened Pavel, to which people were not slow in adding Fyodorovitch (son of Fyodor). Fyodor Pavlovitch did not object to any of this, and thought it amusing, though he persisted vigorously in denying his responsibility. The townspeople were pleased at his adopting the foundling. Later on, Fyodor Pavlovitch invented a surname for the child, calling him Smerdyakov, after his mother’s nickname.\n\nSo this Smerdyakov became Fyodor Pavlovitch’s second servant, and was living in the lodge with Grigory and Marfa at the time our story begins. He was employed as cook. I ought to say something of this Smerdyakov, but I am ashamed of keeping my readers’ attention so long occupied with these common menials, and I will go back to my story, hoping to say more of Smerdyakov in the course of it.\n\n# Chapter III. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Verse\n\nAlyosha remained for some time irresolute after hearing the command his father shouted to him from the carriage. But in spite of his uneasiness he did not stand still. That was not his way. He went at once to the kitchen to find out what his father had been doing above. Then he set off, trusting that on the way he would find some answer to the doubt tormenting him. I hasten to add that his father’s shouts, commanding him to return home “with his mattress and pillow” did not frighten him in the least. He understood perfectly that those peremptory shouts were merely “a flourish” to produce an effect. In the same way a tradesman in our town who was celebrating his name-day with a party of friends, getting angry at being refused more vodka, smashed up his own crockery and furniture and tore his own and his wife’s clothes, and finally broke his windows, all for the sake of effect. Next day, of course, when he was sober, he regretted the broken cups and saucers. Alyosha knew that his father would let him go back to the monastery next day, possibly even that evening. Moreover, he was fully persuaded that his father might hurt any one else, but would not hurt him. Alyosha was certain that no one in the whole world ever would want to hurt him, and, what is more, he knew that no one could hurt him. This was for him an axiom, assumed once for all without question, and he went his way without hesitation, relying on it.\n\nBut at that moment an anxiety of a different sort disturbed him, and worried him the more because he could not formulate it. It was the fear of a woman, of Katerina Ivanovna, who had so urgently entreated him in the note handed to him by Madame Hohlakov to come and see her about something. This request and the necessity of going had at once aroused an uneasy feeling in his heart, and this feeling had grown more and more painful all the morning in spite of the scenes at the hermitage and at the Father Superior’s. He was not uneasy because he did not know what she would speak of and what he must answer. And he was not afraid of her simply as a woman. Though he knew little of women, he had spent his life, from early childhood till he entered the monastery, entirely with women. He was afraid of that woman, Katerina Ivanovna. He had been afraid of her from the first time he saw her. He had only seen her two or three times, and had only chanced to say a few words to her. He thought of her as a beautiful, proud, imperious girl. It was not her beauty which troubled him, but something else. And the vagueness of his apprehension increased the apprehension itself. The girl’s aims were of the noblest, he knew that. She was trying to save his brother Dmitri simply through generosity, though he had already behaved badly to her. Yet, although Alyosha recognized and did justice to all these fine and generous sentiments, a shiver began to run down his back as soon as he drew near her house.\n\nHe reflected that he would not find Ivan, who was so intimate a friend, with her, for Ivan was certainly now with his father. Dmitri he was even more certain not to find there, and he had a foreboding of the reason. And so his conversation would be with her alone. He had a great longing to run and see his brother Dmitri before that fateful interview. Without showing him the letter, he could talk to him about it. But Dmitri lived a long way off, and he was sure to be away from home too. Standing still for a minute, he reached a final decision. Crossing himself with a rapid and accustomed gesture, and at once smiling, he turned resolutely in the direction of his terrible lady.\n\nHe knew her house. If he went by the High Street and then across the market-place, it was a long way round. Though our town is small, it is scattered, and the houses are far apart. And meanwhile his father was expecting him, and perhaps had not yet forgotten his command. He might be unreasonable, and so he had to make haste to get there and back. So he decided to take a short cut by the back-way, for he knew every inch of the ground. This meant skirting fences, climbing over hurdles, and crossing other people’s back-yards, where every one he met knew him and greeted him. In this way he could reach the High Street in half the time.\n\nHe had to pass the garden adjoining his father’s, and belonging to a little tumbledown house with four windows. The owner of this house, as Alyosha knew, was a bedridden old woman, living with her daughter, who had been a genteel maid-servant in generals’ families in Petersburg. Now she had been at home a year, looking after her sick mother. She always dressed up in fine clothes, though her old mother and she had sunk into such poverty that they went every day to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s kitchen for soup and bread, which Marfa gave readily. Yet, though the young woman came up for soup, she had never sold any of her dresses, and one of these even had a long train—a fact which Alyosha had learned from Rakitin, who always knew everything that was going on in the town. He had forgotten it as soon as he heard it, but now, on reaching the garden, he remembered the dress with the train, raised his head, which had been bowed in thought, and came upon something quite unexpected.\n\nOver the hurdle in the garden, Dmitri, mounted on something, was leaning forward, gesticulating violently, beckoning to him, obviously afraid to utter a word for fear of being overheard. Alyosha ran up to the hurdle.\n\n“It’s a good thing you looked up. I was nearly shouting to you,” Mitya said in a joyful, hurried whisper. “Climb in here quickly! How splendid that you’ve come! I was just thinking of you!”\n\nAlyosha was delighted too, but he did not know how to get over the hurdle. Mitya put his powerful hand under his elbow to help him jump. Tucking up his cassock, Alyosha leapt over the hurdle with the agility of a bare-legged street urchin.\n\n“Well done! Now come along,” said Mitya in an enthusiastic whisper.\n\n“Where?” whispered Alyosha, looking about him and finding himself in a deserted garden with no one near but themselves. The garden was small, but the house was at least fifty paces away.\n\n“There’s no one here. Why do you whisper?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Why do I whisper? Deuce take it!” cried Dmitri at the top of his voice. “You see what silly tricks nature plays one. I am here in secret, and on the watch. I’ll explain later on, but, knowing it’s a secret, I began whispering like a fool, when there’s no need. Let us go. Over there. Till then be quiet. I want to kiss you.\n\nGlory to God in the world, Glory to God in me ...\n\nI was just repeating that, sitting here, before you came.”\n\nThe garden was about three acres in extent, and planted with trees only along the fence at the four sides. There were apple-trees, maples, limes and birch-trees. The middle of the garden was an empty grass space, from which several hundredweight of hay was carried in the summer. The garden was let out for a few roubles for the summer. There were also plantations of raspberries and currants and gooseberries laid out along the sides; a kitchen garden had been planted lately near the house.\n\nDmitri led his brother to the most secluded corner of the garden. There, in a thicket of lime-trees and old bushes of black currant, elder, snowball-tree, and lilac, there stood a tumble-down green summer-house, blackened with age. Its walls were of lattice-work, but there was still a roof which could give shelter. God knows when this summer-house was built. There was a tradition that it had been put up some fifty years before by a retired colonel called von Schmidt, who owned the house at that time. It was all in decay, the floor was rotting, the planks were loose, the woodwork smelled musty. In the summer-house there was a green wooden table fixed in the ground, and round it were some green benches upon which it was still possible to sit. Alyosha had at once observed his brother’s exhilarated condition, and on entering the arbor he saw half a bottle of brandy and a wineglass on the table.\n\n“That’s brandy,” Mitya laughed. “I see your look: ‘He’s drinking again!’ Distrust the apparition.\n\nDistrust the worthless, lying crowd, And lay aside thy doubts.\n\nI’m not drinking, I’m only ‘indulging,’ as that pig, your Rakitin, says. He’ll be a civil councilor one day, but he’ll always talk about ‘indulging.’ Sit down. I could take you in my arms, Alyosha, and press you to my bosom till I crush you, for in the whole world—in reality—in re-al-i-ty—(can you take it in?) I love no one but you!”\n\nHe uttered the last words in a sort of exaltation.\n\n“No one but you and one ‘jade’ I have fallen in love with, to my ruin. But being in love doesn’t mean loving. You may be in love with a woman and yet hate her. Remember that! I can talk about it gayly still. Sit down here by the table and I’ll sit beside you and look at you, and go on talking. You shall keep quiet and I’ll go on talking, for the time has come. But on reflection, you know, I’d better speak quietly, for here—here—you can never tell what ears are listening. I will explain everything; as they say, ‘the story will be continued.’ Why have I been longing for you? Why have I been thirsting for you all these days, and just now? (It’s five days since I’ve cast anchor here.) Because it’s only to you I can tell everything; because I must, because I need you, because to-morrow I shall fly from the clouds, because to-morrow life is ending and beginning. Have you ever felt, have you ever dreamt of falling down a precipice into a pit? That’s just how I’m falling, but not in a dream. And I’m not afraid, and don’t you be afraid. At least, I am afraid, but I enjoy it. It’s not enjoyment though, but ecstasy. Damn it all, whatever it is! A strong spirit, a weak spirit, a womanish spirit—whatever it is! Let us praise nature: you see what sunshine, how clear the sky is, the leaves are all green, it’s still summer; four o’clock in the afternoon and the stillness! Where were you going?”\n\n“I was going to father’s, but I meant to go to Katerina Ivanovna’s first.”\n\n“To her, and to father! Oo! what a coincidence! Why was I waiting for you? Hungering and thirsting for you in every cranny of my soul and even in my ribs? Why, to send you to father and to her, Katerina Ivanovna, so as to have done with her and with father. To send an angel. I might have sent any one, but I wanted to send an angel. And here you are on your way to see father and her.”\n\n“Did you really mean to send me?” cried Alyosha with a distressed expression.\n\n“Stay! You knew it! And I see you understand it all at once. But be quiet, be quiet for a time. Don’t be sorry, and don’t cry.”\n\nDmitri stood up, thought a moment, and put his finger to his forehead.\n\n“She’s asked you, written to you a letter or something, that’s why you’re going to her? You wouldn’t be going except for that?”\n\n“Here is her note.” Alyosha took it out of his pocket. Mitya looked through it quickly.\n\n“And you were going the back-way! Oh, gods, I thank you for sending him by the back-way, and he came to me like the golden fish to the silly old fishermen in the fable! Listen, Alyosha, listen, brother! Now I mean to tell you everything, for I must tell some one. An angel in heaven I’ve told already; but I want to tell an angel on earth. You are an angel on earth. You will hear and judge and forgive. And that’s what I need, that some one above me should forgive. Listen! If two people break away from everything on earth and fly off into the unknown, or at least one of them, and before flying off or going to ruin he comes to some one else and says, ‘Do this for me’—some favor never asked before that could only be asked on one’s deathbed—would that other refuse, if he were a friend or a brother?”\n\n“I will do it, but tell me what it is, and make haste,” said Alyosha.\n\n“Make haste! H’m!... Don’t be in a hurry, Alyosha, you hurry and worry yourself. There’s no need to hurry now. Now the world has taken a new turning. Ah, Alyosha, what a pity you can’t understand ecstasy. But what am I saying to him? As though you didn’t understand it. What an ass I am! What am I saying? ‘Be noble, O man!’—who says that?”\n\nAlyosha made up his mind to wait. He felt that, perhaps, indeed, his work lay here. Mitya sank into thought for a moment, with his elbow on the table and his head in his hand. Both were silent.\n\n“Alyosha,” said Mitya, “you’re the only one who won’t laugh. I should like to begin—my confession—with Schiller’s Hymn to Joy, An die Freude! I don’t know German, I only know it’s called that. Don’t think I’m talking nonsense because I’m drunk. I’m not a bit drunk. Brandy’s all very well, but I need two bottles to make me drunk:\n\nSilenus with his rosy phiz Upon his stumbling ass.\n\nBut I’ve not drunk a quarter of a bottle, and I’m not Silenus. I’m not Silenus, though I am strong,[1] for I’ve made a decision once for all. Forgive me the pun; you’ll have to forgive me a lot more than puns to-day. Don’t be uneasy. I’m not spinning it out. I’m talking sense, and I’ll come to the point in a minute. I won’t keep you in suspense. Stay, how does it go?”\n\nHe raised his head, thought a minute, and began with enthusiasm:\n\n“Wild and fearful in his cavern Hid the naked troglodyte, And the homeless nomad wandered Laying waste the fertile plain. Menacing with spear and arrow In the woods the hunter strayed.... Woe to all poor wretches stranded On those cruel and hostile shores!\n\n“From the peak of high Olympus Came the mother Ceres down, Seeking in those savage regions Her lost daughter Proserpine. But the Goddess found no refuge, Found no kindly welcome there, And no temple bearing witness To the worship of the gods.\n\n“From the fields and from the vineyards Came no fruits to deck the feasts, Only flesh of bloodstained victims Smoldered on the altar-fires, And where’er the grieving goddess Turns her melancholy gaze, Sunk in vilest degradation Man his loathsomeness displays.”\n\nMitya broke into sobs and seized Alyosha’s hand.\n\n“My dear, my dear, in degradation, in degradation now, too. There’s a terrible amount of suffering for man on earth, a terrible lot of trouble. Don’t think I’m only a brute in an officer’s uniform, wallowing in dirt and drink. I hardly think of anything but of that degraded man—if only I’m not lying. I pray God I’m not lying and showing off. I think about that man because I am that man myself.\n\nWould he purge his soul from vileness And attain to light and worth, He must turn and cling for ever To his ancient Mother Earth.\n\nBut the difficulty is how am I to cling for ever to Mother Earth. I don’t kiss her. I don’t cleave to her bosom. Am I to become a peasant or a shepherd? I go on and I don’t know whether I’m going to shame or to light and joy. That’s the trouble, for everything in the world is a riddle! And whenever I’ve happened to sink into the vilest degradation (and it’s always been happening) I always read that poem about Ceres and man. Has it reformed me? Never! For I’m a Karamazov. For when I do leap into the pit, I go headlong with my heels up, and am pleased to be falling in that degrading attitude, and pride myself upon it. And in the very depths of that degradation I begin a hymn of praise. Let me be accursed. Let me be vile and base, only let me kiss the hem of the veil in which my God is shrouded. Though I may be following the devil, I am Thy son, O Lord, and I love Thee, and I feel the joy without which the world cannot stand.\n\nJoy everlasting fostereth The soul of all creation, It is her secret ferment fires The cup of life with flame. ’Tis at her beck the grass hath turned Each blade towards the light And solar systems have evolved From chaos and dark night, Filling the realms of boundless space Beyond the sage’s sight. At bounteous Nature’s kindly breast, All things that breathe drink Joy, And birds and beasts and creeping things All follow where She leads. Her gifts to man are friends in need, The wreath, the foaming must, To angels—vision of God’s throne, To insects—sensual lust.\n\nBut enough poetry! I am in tears; let me cry. It may be foolishness that every one would laugh at. But you won’t laugh. Your eyes are shining, too. Enough poetry. I want to tell you now about the insects to whom God gave “sensual lust.”\n\nTo insects—sensual lust.\n\nI am that insect, brother, and it is said of me specially. All we Karamazovs are such insects, and, angel as you are, that insect lives in you, too, and will stir up a tempest in your blood. Tempests, because sensual lust is a tempest—worse than a tempest! Beauty is a terrible and awful thing! It is terrible because it has not been fathomed and never can be fathomed, for God sets us nothing but riddles. Here the boundaries meet and all contradictions exist side by side. I am not a cultivated man, brother, but I’ve thought a lot about this. It’s terrible what mysteries there are! Too many riddles weigh men down on earth. We must solve them as we can, and try to keep a dry skin in the water. Beauty! I can’t endure the thought that a man of lofty mind and heart begins with the ideal of the Madonna and ends with the ideal of Sodom. What’s still more awful is that a man with the ideal of Sodom in his soul does not renounce the ideal of the Madonna, and his heart may be on fire with that ideal, genuinely on fire, just as in his days of youth and innocence. Yes, man is broad, too broad, indeed. I’d have him narrower. The devil only knows what to make of it! What to the mind is shameful is beauty and nothing else to the heart. Is there beauty in Sodom? Believe me, that for the immense mass of mankind beauty is found in Sodom. Did you know that secret? The awful thing is that beauty is mysterious as well as terrible. God and the devil are fighting there and the battlefield is the heart of man. But a man always talks of his own ache. Listen, now to come to facts.”\n\n# Chapter IV. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Anecdote\n\n“I was leading a wild life then. Father said just now that I spent several thousand roubles in seducing young girls. That’s a swinish invention, and there was nothing of the sort. And if there was, I didn’t need money simply for that. With me money is an accessory, the overflow of my heart, the framework. To-day she would be my lady, to-morrow a wench out of the streets in her place. I entertained them both. I threw away money by the handful on music, rioting, and gypsies. Sometimes I gave it to the ladies, too, for they’ll take it greedily, that must be admitted, and be pleased and thankful for it. Ladies used to be fond of me: not all of them, but it happened, it happened. But I always liked side-paths, little dark back-alleys behind the main road—there one finds adventures and surprises, and precious metal in the dirt. I am speaking figuratively, brother. In the town I was in, there were no such back-alleys in the literal sense, but morally there were. If you were like me, you’d know what that means. I loved vice, I loved the ignominy of vice. I loved cruelty; am I not a bug, am I not a noxious insect? In fact a Karamazov! Once we went, a whole lot of us, for a picnic, in seven sledges. It was dark, it was winter, and I began squeezing a girl’s hand, and forced her to kiss me. She was the daughter of an official, a sweet, gentle, submissive creature. She allowed me, she allowed me much in the dark. She thought, poor thing, that I should come next day to make her an offer (I was looked upon as a good match, too). But I didn’t say a word to her for five months. I used to see her in a corner at dances (we were always having dances), her eyes watching me. I saw how they glowed with fire—a fire of gentle indignation. This game only tickled that insect lust I cherished in my soul. Five months later she married an official and left the town, still angry, and still, perhaps, in love with me. Now they live happily. Observe that I told no one. I didn’t boast of it. Though I’m full of low desires, and love what’s low, I’m not dishonorable. You’re blushing; your eyes flashed. Enough of this filth with you. And all this was nothing much—wayside blossoms à la Paul de Kock—though the cruel insect had already grown strong in my soul. I’ve a perfect album of reminiscences, brother. God bless them, the darlings. I tried to break it off without quarreling. And I never gave them away. I never bragged of one of them. But that’s enough. You can’t suppose I brought you here simply to talk of such nonsense. No, I’m going to tell you something more curious; and don’t be surprised that I’m glad to tell you, instead of being ashamed.”\n\n“You say that because I blushed,” Alyosha said suddenly. “I wasn’t blushing at what you were saying or at what you’ve done. I blushed because I am the same as you are.”\n\n“You? Come, that’s going a little too far!”\n\n“No, it’s not too far,” said Alyosha warmly (obviously the idea was not a new one). “The ladder’s the same. I’m at the bottom step, and you’re above, somewhere about the thirteenth. That’s how I see it. But it’s all the same. Absolutely the same in kind. Any one on the bottom step is bound to go up to the top one.”\n\n“Then one ought not to step on at all.”\n\n“Any one who can help it had better not.”\n\n“But can you?”\n“I think not.”\n\n“Hush, Alyosha, hush, darling! I could kiss your hand, you touch me so. That rogue Grushenka has an eye for men. She told me once that she’d devour you one day. There, there, I won’t! From this field of corruption fouled by flies, let’s pass to my tragedy, also befouled by flies, that is by every sort of vileness. Although the old man told lies about my seducing innocence, there really was something of the sort in my tragedy, though it was only once, and then it did not come off. The old man who has reproached me with what never happened does not even know of this fact; I never told any one about it. You’re the first, except Ivan, of course—Ivan knows everything. He knew about it long before you. But Ivan’s a tomb.”\n\n“Ivan’s a tomb?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nAlyosha listened with great attention.\n\n“I was lieutenant in a line regiment, but still I was under supervision, like a kind of convict. Yet I was awfully well received in the little town. I spent money right and left. I was thought to be rich; I thought so myself. But I must have pleased them in other ways as well. Although they shook their heads over me, they liked me. My colonel, who was an old man, took a sudden dislike to me. He was always down upon me, but I had powerful friends, and, moreover, all the town was on my side, so he couldn’t do me much harm. I was in fault myself for refusing to treat him with proper respect. I was proud. This obstinate old fellow, who was really a very good sort, kind-hearted and hospitable, had had two wives, both dead. His first wife, who was of a humble family, left a daughter as unpretentious as herself. She was a young woman of four and twenty when I was there, and was living with her father and an aunt, her mother’s sister. The aunt was simple and illiterate; the niece was simple but lively. I like to say nice things about people. I never knew a woman of more charming character than Agafya—fancy, her name was Agafya Ivanovna! And she wasn’t bad-looking either, in the Russian style: tall, stout, with a full figure, and beautiful eyes, though a rather coarse face. She had not married, although she had had two suitors. She refused them, but was as cheerful as ever. I was intimate with her, not in ‘that’ way, it was pure friendship. I have often been friendly with women quite innocently. I used to talk to her with shocking frankness, and she only laughed. Many women like such freedom, and she was a girl too, which made it very amusing. Another thing, one could never think of her as a young lady. She and her aunt lived in her father’s house with a sort of voluntary humility, not putting themselves on an equality with other people. She was a general favorite, and of use to every one, for she was a clever dressmaker. She had a talent for it. She gave her services freely without asking for payment, but if any one offered her payment, she didn’t refuse. The colonel, of course, was a very different matter. He was one of the chief personages in the district. He kept open house, entertained the whole town, gave suppers and dances. At the time I arrived and joined the battalion, all the town was talking of the expected return of the colonel’s second daughter, a great beauty, who had just left a fashionable school in the capital. This second daughter is Katerina Ivanovna, and she was the child of the second wife, who belonged to a distinguished general’s family; although, as I learnt on good authority, she too brought the colonel no money. She had connections, and that was all. There may have been expectations, but they had come to nothing.\n\n“Yet, when the young lady came from boarding-school on a visit, the whole town revived. Our most distinguished ladies—two ‘Excellencies’ and a colonel’s wife—and all the rest following their lead, at once took her up and gave entertainments in her honor. She was the belle of the balls and picnics, and they got up tableaux vivants in aid of distressed governesses. I took no notice, I went on as wildly as before, and one of my exploits at the time set all the town talking. I saw her eyes taking my measure one evening at the battery commander’s, but I didn’t go up to her, as though I disdained her acquaintance. I did go up and speak to her at an evening party not long after. She scarcely looked at me, and compressed her lips scornfully. ‘Wait a bit. I’ll have my revenge,’ thought I. I behaved like an awful fool on many occasions at that time, and I was conscious of it myself. What made it worse was that I felt that ‘Katenka’ was not an innocent boarding-school miss, but a person of character, proud and really high-principled; above all, she had education and intellect, and I had neither. You think I meant to make her an offer? No, I simply wanted to revenge myself, because I was such a hero and she didn’t seem to feel it.\n\n“Meanwhile, I spent my time in drink and riot, till the lieutenant-colonel put me under arrest for three days. Just at that time father sent me six thousand roubles in return for my sending him a deed giving up all claims upon him—settling our accounts, so to speak, and saying that I wouldn’t expect anything more. I didn’t understand a word of it at the time. Until I came here, Alyosha, till the last few days, indeed, perhaps even now, I haven’t been able to make head or tail of my money affairs with father. But never mind that, we’ll talk of it later.\n\n“Just as I received the money, I got a letter from a friend telling me something that interested me immensely. The authorities, I learnt, were dissatisfied with our lieutenant-colonel. He was suspected of irregularities; in fact, his enemies were preparing a surprise for him. And then the commander of the division arrived, and kicked up the devil of a shindy. Shortly afterwards he was ordered to retire. I won’t tell you how it all happened. He had enemies certainly. Suddenly there was a marked coolness in the town towards him and all his family. His friends all turned their backs on him. Then I took my first step. I met Agafya Ivanovna, with whom I’d always kept up a friendship, and said, ‘Do you know there’s a deficit of 4,500 roubles of government money in your father’s accounts?’\n\n“‘What do you mean? What makes you say so? The general was here not long ago, and everything was all right.’\n\n“‘Then it was, but now it isn’t.’\n\n“She was terribly scared.\n\n“‘Don’t frighten me!’ she said. ‘Who told you so?’\n\n“‘Don’t be uneasy,’ I said, ‘I won’t tell any one. You know I’m as silent as the tomb. I only wanted, in view of “possibilities,” to add, that when they demand that 4,500 roubles from your father, and he can’t produce it, he’ll be tried, and made to serve as a common soldier in his old age, unless you like to send me your young lady secretly. I’ve just had money paid me. I’ll give her four thousand, if you like, and keep the secret religiously.’\n\n“‘Ah, you scoundrel!’—that’s what she said. ‘You wicked scoundrel! How dare you!’\n\n“She went away furiously indignant, while I shouted after her once more that the secret should be kept sacred. Those two simple creatures, Agafya and her aunt, I may as well say at once, behaved like perfect angels all through this business. They genuinely adored their ‘Katya,’ thought her far above them, and waited on her, hand and foot. But Agafya told her of our conversation. I found that out afterwards. She didn’t keep it back, and of course that was all I wanted.\n\n“Suddenly the new major arrived to take command of the battalion. The old lieutenant-colonel was taken ill at once, couldn’t leave his room for two days, and didn’t hand over the government money. Dr. Kravchenko declared that he really was ill. But I knew for a fact, and had known for a long time, that for the last four years the money had never been in his hands except when the Commander made his visits of inspection. He used to lend it to a trustworthy person, a merchant of our town called Trifonov, an old widower, with a big beard and gold-rimmed spectacles. He used to go to the fair, do a profitable business with the money, and return the whole sum to the colonel, bringing with it a present from the fair, as well as interest on the loan. But this time (I heard all about it quite by chance from Trifonov’s son and heir, a driveling youth and one of the most vicious in the world)—this time, I say, Trifonov brought nothing back from the fair. The lieutenant-colonel flew to him. ‘I’ve never received any money from you, and couldn’t possibly have received any.’ That was all the answer he got. So now our lieutenant-colonel is confined to the house, with a towel round his head, while they’re all three busy putting ice on it. All at once an orderly arrives on the scene with the book and the order to ‘hand over the battalion money immediately, within two hours.’ He signed the book (I saw the signature in the book afterwards), stood up, saying he would put on his uniform, ran to his bedroom, loaded his double-barreled gun with a service bullet, took the boot off his right foot, fixed the gun against his chest, and began feeling for the trigger with his foot. But Agafya, remembering what I had told her, had her suspicions. She stole up and peeped into the room just in time. She rushed in, flung herself upon him from behind, threw her arms round him, and the gun went off, hit the ceiling, but hurt no one. The others ran in, took away the gun, and held him by the arms. I heard all about this afterwards. I was at home, it was getting dusk, and I was just preparing to go out. I had dressed, brushed my hair, scented my handkerchief, and taken up my cap, when suddenly the door opened, and facing me in the room stood Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“It’s strange how things happen sometimes. No one had seen her in the street, so that no one knew of it in the town. I lodged with two decrepit old ladies, who looked after me. They were most obliging old things, ready to do anything for me, and at my request were as silent afterwards as two cast-iron posts. Of course I grasped the position at once. She walked in and looked straight at me, her dark eyes determined, even defiant, but on her lips and round her mouth I saw uncertainty.\n\n“‘My sister told me,’ she began, ‘that you would give me 4,500 roubles if I came to you for it—myself. I have come ... give me the money!’\n\n“She couldn’t keep it up. She was breathless, frightened, her voice failed her, and the corners of her mouth and the lines round it quivered. Alyosha, are you listening, or are you asleep?”\n\n“Mitya, I know you will tell the whole truth,” said Alyosha in agitation.\n\n“I am telling it. If I tell the whole truth just as it happened I shan’t spare myself. My first idea was a—Karamazov one. Once I was bitten by a centipede, brother, and laid up a fortnight with fever from it. Well, I felt a centipede biting at my heart then—a noxious insect, you understand? I looked her up and down. You’ve seen her? She’s a beauty. But she was beautiful in another way then. At that moment she was beautiful because she was noble, and I was a scoundrel; she in all the grandeur of her generosity and sacrifice for her father, and I—a bug! And, scoundrel as I was, she was altogether at my mercy, body and soul. She was hemmed in. I tell you frankly, that thought, that venomous thought, so possessed my heart that it almost swooned with suspense. It seemed as if there could be no resisting it; as though I should act like a bug, like a venomous spider, without a spark of pity. I could scarcely breathe. Understand, I should have gone next day to ask for her hand, so that it might end honorably, so to speak, and that nobody would or could know. For though I’m a man of base desires, I’m honest. And at that very second some voice seemed to whisper in my ear, ‘But when you come to-morrow to make your proposal, that girl won’t even see you; she’ll order her coachman to kick you out of the yard. “Publish it through all the town,” she would say, “I’m not afraid of you.”’ I looked at the young lady, my voice had not deceived me. That is how it would be, not a doubt of it. I could see from her face now that I should be turned out of the house. My spite was roused. I longed to play her the nastiest swinish cad’s trick: to look at her with a sneer, and on the spot where she stood before me to stun her with a tone of voice that only a shopman could use.\n\n“‘Four thousand! What do you mean? I was joking. You’ve been counting your chickens too easily, madam. Two hundred, if you like, with all my heart. But four thousand is not a sum to throw away on such frivolity. You’ve put yourself out to no purpose.’\n\n“I should have lost the game, of course. She’d have run away. But it would have been an infernal revenge. It would have been worth it all. I’d have howled with regret all the rest of my life, only to have played that trick. Would you believe it, it has never happened to me with any other woman, not one, to look at her at such a moment with hatred. But, on my oath, I looked at her for three seconds, or five perhaps, with fearful hatred—that hate which is only a hair’s-breadth from love, from the maddest love!\n\n“I went to the window, put my forehead against the frozen pane, and I remember the ice burnt my forehead like fire. I did not keep her long, don’t be afraid. I turned round, went up to the table, opened the drawer and took out a banknote for five thousand roubles (it was lying in a French dictionary). Then I showed it her in silence, folded it, handed it to her, opened the door into the passage, and, stepping back, made her a deep bow, a most respectful, a most impressive bow, believe me! She shuddered all over, gazed at me for a second, turned horribly pale—white as a sheet, in fact—and all at once, not impetuously but softly, gently, bowed down to my feet—not a boarding-school curtsey, but a Russian bow, with her forehead to the floor. She jumped up and ran away. I was wearing my sword. I drew it and nearly stabbed myself with it on the spot; why, I don’t know. It would have been frightfully stupid, of course. I suppose it was from delight. Can you understand that one might kill oneself from delight? But I didn’t stab myself. I only kissed my sword and put it back in the scabbard—which there was no need to have told you, by the way. And I fancy that in telling you about my inner conflict I have laid it on rather thick to glorify myself. But let it pass, and to hell with all who pry into the human heart! Well, so much for that ‘adventure’ with Katerina Ivanovna. So now Ivan knows of it, and you—no one else.”\n\nDmitri got up, took a step or two in his excitement, pulled out his handkerchief and mopped his forehead, then sat down again, not in the same place as before, but on the opposite side, so that Alyosha had to turn quite round to face him.\n\n“Now,” said Alyosha, “I understand the first half.”\n\n“You understand the first half. That half is a drama, and it was played out there. The second half is a tragedy, and it is being acted here.”\n\n“And I understand nothing of that second half so far,” said Alyosha.\n\n“And I? Do you suppose I understand it?”\n\n“Stop, Dmitri. There’s one important question. Tell me, you were betrothed, you are betrothed still?”\n\n“We weren’t betrothed at once, not for three months after that adventure. The next day I told myself that the incident was closed, concluded, that there would be no sequel. It seemed to me caddish to make her an offer. On her side she gave no sign of life for the six weeks that she remained in the town; except, indeed, for one action. The day after her visit the maid-servant slipped round with an envelope addressed to me. I tore it open: it contained the change out of the banknote. Only four thousand five hundred roubles was needed, but there was a discount of about two hundred on changing it. She only sent me about two hundred and sixty. I don’t remember exactly, but not a note, not a word of explanation. I searched the packet for a pencil mark—n-nothing! Well, I spent the rest of the money on such an orgy that the new major was obliged to reprimand me.\n\n“Well, the lieutenant-colonel produced the battalion money, to the astonishment of every one, for nobody believed that he had the money untouched. He’d no sooner paid it than he fell ill, took to his bed, and, three weeks later, softening of the brain set in, and he died five days afterwards. He was buried with military honors, for he had not had time to receive his discharge. Ten days after his funeral, Katerina Ivanovna, with her aunt and sister, went to Moscow. And, behold, on the very day they went away (I hadn’t seen them, didn’t see them off or take leave) I received a tiny note, a sheet of thin blue paper, and on it only one line in pencil: ‘I will write to you. Wait. K.’ And that was all.\n\n“I’ll explain the rest now, in two words. In Moscow their fortunes changed with the swiftness of lightning and the unexpectedness of an Arabian fairy-tale. That general’s widow, their nearest relation, suddenly lost the two nieces who were her heiresses and next-of-kin—both died in the same week of small-pox. The old lady, prostrated with grief, welcomed Katya as a daughter, as her one hope, clutched at her, altered her will in Katya’s favor. But that concerned the future. Meanwhile she gave her, for present use, eighty thousand roubles, as a marriage portion, to do what she liked with. She was an hysterical woman. I saw something of her in Moscow, later.\n\n“Well, suddenly I received by post four thousand five hundred roubles. I was speechless with surprise, as you may suppose. Three days later came the promised letter. I have it with me now. You must read it. She offers to be my wife, offers herself to me. ‘I love you madly,’ she says, ‘even if you don’t love me, never mind. Be my husband. Don’t be afraid. I won’t hamper you in any way. I will be your chattel. I will be the carpet under your feet. I want to love you for ever. I want to save you from yourself.’ Alyosha, I am not worthy to repeat those lines in my vulgar words and in my vulgar tone, my everlastingly vulgar tone, that I can never cure myself of. That letter stabs me even now. Do you think I don’t mind—that I don’t mind still? I wrote her an answer at once, as it was impossible for me to go to Moscow. I wrote to her with tears. One thing I shall be ashamed of for ever. I referred to her being rich and having a dowry while I was only a stuck-up beggar! I mentioned money! I ought to have borne it in silence, but it slipped from my pen. Then I wrote at once to Ivan, and told him all I could about it in a letter of six pages, and sent him to her. Why do you look like that? Why are you staring at me? Yes, Ivan fell in love with her; he’s in love with her still. I know that. I did a stupid thing, in the world’s opinion; but perhaps that one stupid thing may be the saving of us all now. Oo! Don’t you see what a lot she thinks of Ivan, how she respects him? When she compares us, do you suppose she can love a man like me, especially after all that has happened here?”\n\n“But I am convinced that she does love a man like you, and not a man like him.”\n\n“She loves her own virtue, not me.” The words broke involuntarily, and almost malignantly, from Dmitri. He laughed, but a minute later his eyes gleamed, he flushed crimson and struck the table violently with his fist.\n\n“I swear, Alyosha,” he cried, with intense and genuine anger at himself; “you may not believe me, but as God is holy, and as Christ is God, I swear that though I smiled at her lofty sentiments just now, I know that I am a million times baser in soul than she, and that these lofty sentiments of hers are as sincere as a heavenly angel’s. That’s the tragedy of it—that I know that for certain. What if any one does show off a bit? Don’t I do it myself? And yet I’m sincere, I’m sincere. As for Ivan, I can understand how he must be cursing nature now—with his intellect, too! To see the preference given—to whom, to what? To a monster who, though he is betrothed and all eyes are fixed on him, can’t restrain his debaucheries—and before the very eyes of his betrothed! And a man like me is preferred, while he is rejected. And why? Because a girl wants to sacrifice her life and destiny out of gratitude. It’s ridiculous! I’ve never said a word of this to Ivan, and Ivan of course has never dropped a hint of the sort to me. But destiny will be accomplished, and the best man will hold his ground while the undeserving one will vanish into his back-alley for ever—his filthy back-alley, his beloved back-alley, where he is at home and where he will sink in filth and stench at his own free will and with enjoyment. I’ve been talking foolishly. I’ve no words left. I use them at random, but it will be as I have said. I shall drown in the back-alley, and she will marry Ivan.”\n\n“Stop, Dmitri,” Alyosha interrupted again with great anxiety. “There’s one thing you haven’t made clear yet: you are still betrothed all the same, aren’t you? How can you break off the engagement if she, your betrothed, doesn’t want to?”\n\n“Yes, formally and solemnly betrothed. It was all done on my arrival in Moscow, with great ceremony, with ikons, all in fine style. The general’s wife blessed us, and—would you believe it?—congratulated Katya. ‘You’ve made a good choice,’ she said, ‘I see right through him.’ And—would you believe it?—she didn’t like Ivan, and hardly greeted him. I had a lot of talk with Katya in Moscow. I told her about myself—sincerely, honorably. She listened to everything.\n\nThere was sweet confusion, There were tender words.\n\nThough there were proud words, too. She wrung out of me a mighty promise to reform. I gave my promise, and here—”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Why, I called to you and brought you out here to-day, this very day—remember it—to send you—this very day again—to Katerina Ivanovna, and—”\n\n“What?”\n\n“To tell her that I shall never come to see her again. Say, ‘He sends you his compliments.’”\n\n“But is that possible?”\n\n“That’s just the reason I’m sending you, in my place, because it’s impossible. And, how could I tell her myself?”\n\n“And where are you going?”\n\n“To the back-alley.”\n\n“To Grushenka, then!” Alyosha exclaimed mournfully, clasping his hands. “Can Rakitin really have told the truth? I thought that you had just visited her, and that was all.”\n\n“Can a betrothed man pay such visits? Is such a thing possible and with such a betrothed, and before the eyes of all the world? Confound it, I have some honor! As soon as I began visiting Grushenka, I ceased to be betrothed, and to be an honest man. I understand that. Why do you look at me? You see, I went in the first place to beat her. I had heard, and I know for a fact now, that that captain, father’s agent, had given Grushenka an I.O.U. of mine for her to sue me for payment, so as to put an end to me. They wanted to scare me. I went to beat her. I had had a glimpse of her before. She doesn’t strike one at first sight. I knew about her old merchant, who’s lying ill now, paralyzed; but he’s leaving her a decent little sum. I knew, too, that she was fond of money, that she hoarded it, and lent it at a wicked rate of interest, that she’s a merciless cheat and swindler. I went to beat her, and I stayed. The storm broke—it struck me down like the plague. I’m plague-stricken still, and I know that everything is over, that there will never be anything more for me. The cycle of the ages is accomplished. That’s my position. And though I’m a beggar, as fate would have it, I had three thousand just then in my pocket. I drove with Grushenka to Mokroe, a place twenty-five versts from here. I got gypsies there and champagne and made all the peasants there drunk on it, and all the women and girls. I sent the thousands flying. In three days’ time I was stripped bare, but a hero. Do you suppose the hero had gained his end? Not a sign of it from her. I tell you that rogue, Grushenka, has a supple curve all over her body. You can see it in her little foot, even in her little toe. I saw it, and kissed it, but that was all, I swear! ‘I’ll marry you if you like,’ she said, ‘you’re a beggar, you know. Say that you won’t beat me, and will let me do anything I choose, and perhaps I will marry you.’ She laughed, and she’s laughing still!”\n\nDmitri leapt up with a sort of fury. He seemed all at once as though he were drunk. His eyes became suddenly bloodshot.\n\n“And do you really mean to marry her?”\n\n“At once, if she will. And if she won’t, I shall stay all the same. I’ll be the porter at her gate. Alyosha!” he cried. He stopped short before him, and taking him by the shoulders began shaking him violently. “Do you know, you innocent boy, that this is all delirium, senseless delirium, for there’s a tragedy here. Let me tell you, Alexey, that I may be a low man, with low and degraded passions, but a thief and a pickpocket Dmitri Karamazov never can be. Well, then; let me tell you that I am a thief and a pickpocket. That very morning, just before I went to beat Grushenka, Katerina Ivanovna sent for me, and in strict secrecy (why I don’t know, I suppose she had some reason) asked me to go to the chief town of the province and to post three thousand roubles to Agafya Ivanovna in Moscow, so that nothing should be known of it in the town here. So I had that three thousand roubles in my pocket when I went to see Grushenka, and it was that money we spent at Mokroe. Afterwards I pretended I had been to the town, but did not show her the post office receipt. I said I had sent the money and would bring the receipt, and so far I haven’t brought it. I’ve forgotten it. Now what do you think you’re going to her to-day to say? ‘He sends his compliments,’ and she’ll ask you, ‘What about the money?’ You might still have said to her, ‘He’s a degraded sensualist, and a low creature, with uncontrolled passions. He didn’t send your money then, but wasted it, because, like a low brute, he couldn’t control himself.’ But still you might have added, ‘He isn’t a thief though. Here is your three thousand; he sends it back. Send it yourself to Agafya Ivanovna. But he told me to say “he sends his compliments.”’ But, as it is, she will ask, ‘But where is the money?’”\n\n“Mitya, you are unhappy, yes! But not as unhappy as you think. Don’t worry yourself to death with despair.”\n\n“What, do you suppose I’d shoot myself because I can’t get three thousand to pay back? That’s just it. I shan’t shoot myself. I haven’t the strength now. Afterwards, perhaps. But now I’m going to Grushenka. I don’t care what happens.”\n\n“And what then?”\n\n“I’ll be her husband if she deigns to have me, and when lovers come, I’ll go into the next room. I’ll clean her friends’ goloshes, blow up their samovar, run their errands.”\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna will understand it all,” Alyosha said solemnly. “She’ll understand how great this trouble is and will forgive. She has a lofty mind, and no one could be more unhappy than you. She’ll see that for herself.”\n\n“She won’t forgive everything,” said Dmitri, with a grin. “There’s something in it, brother, that no woman could forgive. Do you know what would be the best thing to do?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Pay back the three thousand.”\n\n“Where can we get it from? I say, I have two thousand. Ivan will give you another thousand—that makes three. Take it and pay it back.”\n\n“And when would you get it, your three thousand? You’re not of age, besides, and you must—you absolutely must—take my farewell to her to-day, with the money or without it, for I can’t drag on any longer, things have come to such a pass. To-morrow is too late. I shall send you to father.”\n\n“To father?”\n\n“Yes, to father first. Ask him for three thousand.”\n\n“But, Mitya, he won’t give it.”\n\n“As though he would! I know he won’t. Do you know the meaning of despair, Alexey?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Listen. Legally he owes me nothing. I’ve had it all from him, I know that. But morally he owes me something, doesn’t he? You know he started with twenty-eight thousand of my mother’s money and made a hundred thousand with it. Let him give me back only three out of the twenty-eight thousand, and he’ll draw my soul out of hell, and it will atone for many of his sins. For that three thousand—I give you my solemn word—I’ll make an end of everything, and he shall hear nothing more of me. For the last time I give him the chance to be a father. Tell him God Himself sends him this chance.”\n\n“Mitya, he won’t give it for anything.”\n\n“I know he won’t. I know it perfectly well. Now, especially. That’s not all. I know something more. Now, only a few days ago, perhaps only yesterday he found out for the first time in earnest (underline in earnest) that Grushenka is really perhaps not joking, and really means to marry me. He knows her nature; he knows the cat. And do you suppose he’s going to give me money to help to bring that about when he’s crazy about her himself? And that’s not all, either. I can tell you more than that. I know that for the last five days he has had three thousand drawn out of the bank, changed into notes of a hundred roubles, packed into a large envelope, sealed with five seals, and tied across with red tape. You see how well I know all about it! On the envelope is written: ‘To my angel, Grushenka, when she will come to me.’ He scrawled it himself in silence and in secret, and no one knows that the money’s there except the valet, Smerdyakov, whom he trusts like himself. So now he has been expecting Grushenka for the last three or four days; he hopes she’ll come for the money. He has sent her word of it, and she has sent him word that perhaps she’ll come. And if she does go to the old man, can I marry her after that? You understand now why I’m here in secret and what I’m on the watch for.”\n\n“For her?”\n\n“Yes, for her. Foma has a room in the house of these sluts here. Foma comes from our parts; he was a soldier in our regiment. He does jobs for them. He’s watchman at night and goes grouse-shooting in the day-time; and that’s how he lives. I’ve established myself in his room. Neither he nor the women of the house know the secret—that is, that I am on the watch here.”\n\n“No one but Smerdyakov knows, then?”\n\n“No one else. He will let me know if she goes to the old man.”\n\n“It was he told you about the money, then?”\n\n“Yes. It’s a dead secret. Even Ivan doesn’t know about the money, or anything. The old man is sending Ivan to Tchermashnya on a two or three days’ journey. A purchaser has turned up for the copse: he’ll give eight thousand for the timber. So the old man keeps asking Ivan to help him by going to arrange it. It will take him two or three days. That’s what the old man wants, so that Grushenka can come while he’s away.”\n\n“Then he’s expecting Grushenka to-day?”\n\n“No, she won’t come to-day; there are signs. She’s certain not to come,” cried Mitya suddenly. “Smerdyakov thinks so, too. Father’s drinking now. He’s sitting at table with Ivan. Go to him, Alyosha, and ask for the three thousand.”\n\n“Mitya, dear, what’s the matter with you?” cried Alyosha, jumping up from his place, and looking keenly at his brother’s frenzied face. For one moment the thought struck him that Dmitri was mad.\n\n“What is it? I’m not insane,” said Dmitri, looking intently and earnestly at him. “No fear. I am sending you to father, and I know what I’m saying. I believe in miracles.”\n\n“In miracles?”\n\n“In a miracle of Divine Providence. God knows my heart. He sees my despair. He sees the whole picture. Surely He won’t let something awful happen. Alyosha, I believe in miracles. Go!”\n\n“I am going. Tell me, will you wait for me here?”\n\n“Yes. I know it will take some time. You can’t go at him point blank. He’s drunk now. I’ll wait three hours—four, five, six, seven. Only remember you must go to Katerina Ivanovna to-day, if it has to be at midnight, with the money or without the money, and say, ‘He sends his compliments to you.’ I want you to say that verse to her: ‘He sends his compliments to you.’”\n\n“Mitya! And what if Grushenka comes to-day—if not to-day, to-morrow, or the next day?”\n\n“Grushenka? I shall see her. I shall rush out and prevent it.”\n\n“And if—”\n\n“If there’s an if, it will be murder. I couldn’t endure it.”\n\n“Who will be murdered?”\n\n“The old man. I shan’t kill her.”\n\n“Brother, what are you saying?”\n\n“Oh, I don’t know.... I don’t know. Perhaps I shan’t kill, and perhaps I shall. I’m afraid that he will suddenly become so loathsome to me with his face at that moment. I hate his ugly throat, his nose, his eyes, his shameless snigger. I feel a physical repulsion. That’s what I’m afraid of. That’s what may be too much for me.”\n\n“I’ll go, Mitya. I believe that God will order things for the best, that nothing awful may happen.”\n\n“And I will sit and wait for the miracle. And if it doesn’t come to pass—”\n\nAlyosha went thoughtfully towards his father’s house.\n\n# Chapter VI. Smerdyakov\n\nHe did in fact find his father still at table. Though there was a dining-room in the house, the table was laid as usual in the drawing-room, which was the largest room, and furnished with old-fashioned ostentation. The furniture was white and very old, upholstered in old, red, silky material. In the spaces between the windows there were mirrors in elaborate white and gilt frames, of old-fashioned carving. On the walls, covered with white paper, which was torn in many places, there hung two large portraits—one of some prince who had been governor of the district thirty years before, and the other of some bishop, also long since dead. In the corner opposite the door there were several ikons, before which a lamp was lighted at nightfall ... not so much for devotional purposes as to light the room. Fyodor Pavlovitch used to go to bed very late, at three or four o’clock in the morning, and would wander about the room at night or sit in an arm-chair, thinking. This had become a habit with him. He often slept quite alone in the house, sending his servants to the lodge; but usually Smerdyakov remained, sleeping on a bench in the hall.\n\nWhen Alyosha came in, dinner was over, but coffee and preserves had been served. Fyodor Pavlovitch liked sweet things with brandy after dinner. Ivan was also at table, sipping coffee. The servants, Grigory and Smerdyakov, were standing by. Both the gentlemen and the servants seemed in singularly good spirits. Fyodor Pavlovitch was roaring with laughter. Before he entered the room, Alyosha heard the shrill laugh he knew so well, and could tell from the sound of it that his father had only reached the good-humored stage, and was far from being completely drunk.\n\n“Here he is! Here he is!” yelled Fyodor Pavlovitch, highly delighted at seeing Alyosha. “Join us. Sit down. Coffee is a lenten dish, but it’s hot and good. I don’t offer you brandy, you’re keeping the fast. But would you like some? No; I’d better give you some of our famous liqueur. Smerdyakov, go to the cupboard, the second shelf on the right. Here are the keys. Look sharp!”\n\nAlyosha began refusing the liqueur.\n\n“Never mind. If you won’t have it, we will,” said Fyodor Pavlovitch, beaming. “But stay—have you dined?”\n\n“Yes,” answered Alyosha, who had in truth only eaten a piece of bread and drunk a glass of kvas in the Father Superior’s kitchen. “Though I should be pleased to have some hot coffee.”\n\n“Bravo, my darling! He’ll have some coffee. Does it want warming? No, it’s boiling. It’s capital coffee: Smerdyakov’s making. My Smerdyakov’s an artist at coffee and at fish patties, and at fish soup, too. You must come one day and have some fish soup. Let me know beforehand.... But, stay; didn’t I tell you this morning to come home with your mattress and pillow and all? Have you brought your mattress? He he he!”\n\n“No, I haven’t,” said Alyosha, smiling, too.\n\n“Ah, but you were frightened, you were frightened this morning, weren’t you? There, my darling, I couldn’t do anything to vex you. Do you know, Ivan, I can’t resist the way he looks one straight in the face and laughs? It makes me laugh all over. I’m so fond of him. Alyosha, let me give you my blessing—a father’s blessing.”\n\nAlyosha rose, but Fyodor Pavlovitch had already changed his mind.\n\n“No, no,” he said. “I’ll just make the sign of the cross over you, for now. Sit still. Now we’ve a treat for you, in your own line, too. It’ll make you laugh. Balaam’s ass has begun talking to us here—and how he talks! How he talks!”\n\nBalaam’s ass, it appeared, was the valet, Smerdyakov. He was a young man of about four and twenty, remarkably unsociable and taciturn. Not that he was shy or bashful. On the contrary, he was conceited and seemed to despise everybody.\n\nBut we must pause to say a few words about him now. He was brought up by Grigory and Marfa, but the boy grew up “with no sense of gratitude,” as Grigory expressed it; he was an unfriendly boy, and seemed to look at the world mistrustfully. In his childhood he was very fond of hanging cats, and burying them with great ceremony. He used to dress up in a sheet as though it were a surplice, and sang, and waved some object over the dead cat as though it were a censer. All this he did on the sly, with the greatest secrecy. Grigory caught him once at this diversion and gave him a sound beating. He shrank into a corner and sulked there for a week. “He doesn’t care for you or me, the monster,” Grigory used to say to Marfa, “and he doesn’t care for any one. Are you a human being?” he said, addressing the boy directly. “You’re not a human being. You grew from the mildew in the bath-house.[2] That’s what you are.” Smerdyakov, it appeared afterwards, could never forgive him those words. Grigory taught him to read and write, and when he was twelve years old, began teaching him the Scriptures. But this teaching came to nothing. At the second or third lesson the boy suddenly grinned.\n\n“What’s that for?” asked Grigory, looking at him threateningly from under his spectacles.\n\n“Oh, nothing. God created light on the first day, and the sun, moon, and stars on the fourth day. Where did the light come from on the first day?”\n\nGrigory was thunderstruck. The boy looked sarcastically at his teacher. There was something positively condescending in his expression. Grigory could not restrain himself. “I’ll show you where!” he cried, and gave the boy a violent slap on the cheek. The boy took the slap without a word, but withdrew into his corner again for some days. A week later he had his first attack of the disease to which he was subject all the rest of his life—epilepsy. When Fyodor Pavlovitch heard of it, his attitude to the boy seemed changed at once. Till then he had taken no notice of him, though he never scolded him, and always gave him a copeck when he met him. Sometimes, when he was in good humor, he would send the boy something sweet from his table. But as soon as he heard of his illness, he showed an active interest in him, sent for a doctor, and tried remedies, but the disease turned out to be incurable. The fits occurred, on an average, once a month, but at various intervals. The fits varied too, in violence: some were light and some were very severe. Fyodor Pavlovitch strictly forbade Grigory to use corporal punishment to the boy, and began allowing him to come upstairs to him. He forbade him to be taught anything whatever for a time, too. One day when the boy was about fifteen, Fyodor Pavlovitch noticed him lingering by the bookcase, and reading the titles through the glass. Fyodor Pavlovitch had a fair number of books—over a hundred—but no one ever saw him reading. He at once gave Smerdyakov the key of the bookcase. “Come, read. You shall be my librarian. You’ll be better sitting reading than hanging about the courtyard. Come, read this,” and Fyodor Pavlovitch gave him Evenings in a Cottage near Dikanka.\n\nHe read a little but didn’t like it. He did not once smile, and ended by frowning.\n\n“Why? Isn’t it funny?” asked Fyodor Pavlovitch.\n\nSmerdyakov did not speak.\n\n“Answer, stupid!”\n\n“It’s all untrue,” mumbled the boy, with a grin.\n\n“Then go to the devil! You have the soul of a lackey. Stay, here’s Smaragdov’s Universal History. That’s all true. Read that.”\n\nBut Smerdyakov did not get through ten pages of Smaragdov. He thought it dull. So the bookcase was closed again.\n\nShortly afterwards Marfa and Grigory reported to Fyodor Pavlovitch that Smerdyakov was gradually beginning to show an extraordinary fastidiousness. He would sit before his soup, take up his spoon and look into the soup, bend over it, examine it, take a spoonful and hold it to the light.\n\n“What is it? A beetle?” Grigory would ask.\n\n“A fly, perhaps,” observed Marfa.\n\nThe squeamish youth never answered, but he did the same with his bread, his meat, and everything he ate. He would hold a piece on his fork to the light, scrutinize it microscopically, and only after long deliberation decide to put it in his mouth.\n\n“Ach! What fine gentlemen’s airs!” Grigory muttered, looking at him.\n\nWhen Fyodor Pavlovitch heard of this development in Smerdyakov he determined to make him his cook, and sent him to Moscow to be trained. He spent some years there and came back remarkably changed in appearance. He looked extraordinarily old for his age. His face had grown wrinkled, yellow, and strangely emasculate. In character he seemed almost exactly the same as before he went away. He was just as unsociable, and showed not the slightest inclination for any companionship. In Moscow, too, as we heard afterwards, he had always been silent. Moscow itself had little interest for him; he saw very little there, and took scarcely any notice of anything. He went once to the theater, but returned silent and displeased with it. On the other hand, he came back to us from Moscow well dressed, in a clean coat and clean linen. He brushed his clothes most scrupulously twice a day invariably, and was very fond of cleaning his smart calf boots with a special English polish, so that they shone like mirrors. He turned out a first-rate cook. Fyodor Pavlovitch paid him a salary, almost the whole of which Smerdyakov spent on clothes, pomade, perfumes, and such things. But he seemed to have as much contempt for the female sex as for men; he was discreet, almost unapproachable, with them. Fyodor Pavlovitch began to regard him rather differently. His fits were becoming more frequent, and on the days he was ill Marfa cooked, which did not suit Fyodor Pavlovitch at all.\n\n“Why are your fits getting worse?” asked Fyodor Pavlovitch, looking askance at his new cook. “Would you like to get married? Shall I find you a wife?”\n\nBut Smerdyakov turned pale with anger, and made no reply. Fyodor Pavlovitch left him with an impatient gesture. The great thing was that he had absolute confidence in his honesty. It happened once, when Fyodor Pavlovitch was drunk, that he dropped in the muddy courtyard three hundred-rouble notes which he had only just received. He only missed them next day, and was just hastening to search his pockets when he saw the notes lying on the table. Where had they come from? Smerdyakov had picked them up and brought them in the day before.\n\n“Well, my lad, I’ve never met any one like you,” Fyodor Pavlovitch said shortly, and gave him ten roubles. We may add that he not only believed in his honesty, but had, for some reason, a liking for him, although the young man looked as morosely at him as at every one and was always silent. He rarely spoke. If it had occurred to any one to wonder at the time what the young man was interested in, and what was in his mind, it would have been impossible to tell by looking at him. Yet he used sometimes to stop suddenly in the house, or even in the yard or street, and would stand still for ten minutes, lost in thought. A physiognomist studying his face would have said that there was no thought in it, no reflection, but only a sort of contemplation. There is a remarkable picture by the painter Kramskoy, called “Contemplation.” There is a forest in winter, and on a roadway through the forest, in absolute solitude, stands a peasant in a torn kaftan and bark shoes. He stands, as it were, lost in thought. Yet he is not thinking; he is “contemplating.” If any one touched him he would start and look at one as though awakening and bewildered. It’s true he would come to himself immediately; but if he were asked what he had been thinking about, he would remember nothing. Yet probably he has, hidden within himself, the impression which had dominated him during the period of contemplation. Those impressions are dear to him and no doubt he hoards them imperceptibly, and even unconsciously. How and why, of course, he does not know either. He may suddenly, after hoarding impressions for many years, abandon everything and go off to Jerusalem on a pilgrimage for his soul’s salvation, or perhaps he will suddenly set fire to his native village, and perhaps do both. There are a good many “contemplatives” among the peasantry. Well, Smerdyakov was probably one of them, and he probably was greedily hoarding up his impressions, hardly knowing why.\n\n# Chapter VII. The Controversy\n\nBut Balaam’s ass had suddenly spoken. The subject was a strange one. Grigory had gone in the morning to make purchases, and had heard from the shopkeeper Lukyanov the story of a Russian soldier which had appeared in the newspaper of that day. This soldier had been taken prisoner in some remote part of Asia, and was threatened with an immediate agonizing death if he did not renounce Christianity and follow Islam. He refused to deny his faith, and was tortured, flayed alive, and died, praising and glorifying Christ. Grigory had related the story at table. Fyodor Pavlovitch always liked, over the dessert after dinner, to laugh and talk, if only with Grigory. This afternoon he was in a particularly good-humored and expansive mood. Sipping his brandy and listening to the story, he observed that they ought to make a saint of a soldier like that, and to take his skin to some monastery. “That would make the people flock, and bring the money in.”\n\nGrigory frowned, seeing that Fyodor Pavlovitch was by no means touched, but, as usual, was beginning to scoff. At that moment Smerdyakov, who was standing by the door, smiled. Smerdyakov often waited at table towards the end of dinner, and since Ivan’s arrival in our town he had done so every day.\n\n“What are you grinning at?” asked Fyodor Pavlovitch, catching the smile instantly, and knowing that it referred to Grigory.\n\n“Well, my opinion is,” Smerdyakov began suddenly and unexpectedly in a loud voice, “that if that laudable soldier’s exploit was so very great there would have been, to my thinking, no sin in it if he had on such an emergency renounced, so to speak, the name of Christ and his own christening, to save by that same his life, for good deeds, by which, in the course of years to expiate his cowardice.”\n\n“How could it not be a sin? You’re talking nonsense. For that you’ll go straight to hell and be roasted there like mutton,” put in Fyodor Pavlovitch.\n\nIt was at this point that Alyosha came in, and Fyodor Pavlovitch, as we have seen, was highly delighted at his appearance.\n\n“We’re on your subject, your subject,” he chuckled gleefully, making Alyosha sit down to listen.\n\n“As for mutton, that’s not so, and there’ll be nothing there for this, and there shouldn’t be either, if it’s according to justice,” Smerdyakov maintained stoutly.\n\n“How do you mean ‘according to justice’?” Fyodor Pavlovitch cried still more gayly, nudging Alyosha with his knee.\n\n“He’s a rascal, that’s what he is!” burst from Grigory. He looked Smerdyakov wrathfully in the face.\n\n“As for being a rascal, wait a little, Grigory Vassilyevitch,” answered Smerdyakov with perfect composure. “You’d better consider yourself that, once I am taken prisoner by the enemies of the Christian race, and they demand from me to curse the name of God and to renounce my holy christening, I am fully entitled to act by my own reason, since there would be no sin in it.”\n\n“But you’ve said that before. Don’t waste words. Prove it,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch.\n\n“Soup-maker!” muttered Grigory contemptuously.\n\n“As for being a soup-maker, wait a bit, too, and consider for yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch, without abusing me. For as soon as I say to those enemies, ‘No, I’m not a Christian, and I curse my true God,’ then at once, by God’s high judgment, I become immediately and specially anathema accursed, and am cut off from the Holy Church, exactly as though I were a heathen, so that at that very instant, not only when I say it aloud, but when I think of saying it, before a quarter of a second has passed, I am cut off. Is that so or not, Grigory Vassilyevitch?”\n\nHe addressed Grigory with obvious satisfaction, though he was really answering Fyodor Pavlovitch’s questions, and was well aware of it, and intentionally pretending that Grigory had asked the questions.\n\n“Ivan,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch suddenly, “stoop down for me to whisper. He’s got this all up for your benefit. He wants you to praise him. Praise him.”\n\nIvan listened with perfect seriousness to his father’s excited whisper.\n\n“Stay, Smerdyakov, be quiet a minute,” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch once more. “Ivan, your ear again.”\n\nIvan bent down again with a perfectly grave face.\n\n“I love you as I do Alyosha. Don’t think I don’t love you. Some brandy?”\n\n“Yes.—But you’re rather drunk yourself,” thought Ivan, looking steadily at his father.\n\nHe was watching Smerdyakov with great curiosity.\n\n“You’re anathema accursed, as it is,” Grigory suddenly burst out, “and how dare you argue, you rascal, after that, if—”\n\n“Don’t scold him, Grigory, don’t scold him,” Fyodor Pavlovitch cut him short.\n\n“You should wait, Grigory Vassilyevitch, if only a short time, and listen, for I haven’t finished all I had to say. For at the very moment I become accursed, at that same highest moment, I become exactly like a heathen, and my christening is taken off me and becomes of no avail. Isn’t that so?”\n\n“Make haste and finish, my boy,” Fyodor Pavlovitch urged him, sipping from his wine-glass with relish.\n\n“And if I’ve ceased to be a Christian, then I told no lie to the enemy when they asked whether I was a Christian or not a Christian, seeing I had already been relieved by God Himself of my Christianity by reason of the thought alone, before I had time to utter a word to the enemy. And if I have already been discharged, in what manner and with what sort of justice can I be held responsible as a Christian in the other world for having denied Christ, when, through the very thought alone, before denying Him I had been relieved from my christening? If I’m no longer a Christian, then I can’t renounce Christ, for I’ve nothing then to renounce. Who will hold an unclean Tatar responsible, Grigory Vassilyevitch, even in heaven, for not having been born a Christian? And who would punish him for that, considering that you can’t take two skins off one ox? For God Almighty Himself, even if He did make the Tatar responsible, when he dies would give him the smallest possible punishment, I imagine (since he must be punished), judging that he is not to blame if he has come into the world an unclean heathen, from heathen parents. The Lord God can’t surely take a Tatar and say he was a Christian? That would mean that the Almighty would tell a real untruth. And can the Lord of Heaven and earth tell a lie, even in one word?”\n\nGrigory was thunderstruck and looked at the orator, his eyes nearly starting out of his head. Though he did not clearly understand what was said, he had caught something in this rigmarole, and stood, looking like a man who has just hit his head against a wall. Fyodor Pavlovitch emptied his glass and went off into his shrill laugh.\n\n“Alyosha! Alyosha! What do you say to that! Ah, you casuist! He must have been with the Jesuits, somewhere, Ivan. Oh, you stinking Jesuit, who taught you? But you’re talking nonsense, you casuist, nonsense, nonsense, nonsense. Don’t cry, Grigory, we’ll reduce him to smoke and ashes in a moment. Tell me this, O ass; you may be right before your enemies, but you have renounced your faith all the same in your own heart, and you say yourself that in that very hour you became anathema accursed. And if once you’re anathema they won’t pat you on the head for it in hell. What do you say to that, my fine Jesuit?”\n\n“There is no doubt that I have renounced it in my own heart, but there was no special sin in that. Or if there was sin, it was the most ordinary.”\n\n“How’s that the most ordinary?”\n\n“You lie, accursed one!” hissed Grigory.\n\n“Consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch,” Smerdyakov went on, staid and unruffled, conscious of his triumph, but, as it were, generous to the vanquished foe. “Consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch; it is said in the Scripture that if you have faith, even as a mustard seed, and bid a mountain move into the sea, it will move without the least delay at your bidding. Well, Grigory Vassilyevitch, if I’m without faith and you have so great a faith that you are continually swearing at me, you try yourself telling this mountain, not to move into the sea for that’s a long way off, but even to our stinking little river which runs at the bottom of the garden. You’ll see for yourself that it won’t budge, but will remain just where it is however much you shout at it, and that shows, Grigory Vassilyevitch, that you haven’t faith in the proper manner, and only abuse others about it. Again, taking into consideration that no one in our day, not only you, but actually no one, from the highest person to the lowest peasant, can shove mountains into the sea—except perhaps some one man in the world, or, at most, two, and they most likely are saving their souls in secret somewhere in the Egyptian desert, so you wouldn’t find them—if so it be, if all the rest have no faith, will God curse all the rest? that is, the population of the whole earth, except about two hermits in the desert, and in His well-known mercy will He not forgive one of them? And so I’m persuaded that though I may once have doubted I shall be forgiven if I shed tears of repentance.”\n\n“Stay!” cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, in a transport of delight. “So you do suppose there are two who can move mountains? Ivan, make a note of it, write it down. There you have the Russian all over!”\n\n“You’re quite right in saying it’s characteristic of the people’s faith,” Ivan assented, with an approving smile.\n\n“You agree. Then it must be so, if you agree. It’s true, isn’t it, Alyosha? That’s the Russian faith all over, isn’t it?”\n\n“No, Smerdyakov has not the Russian faith at all,” said Alyosha firmly and gravely.\n\n“I’m not talking about his faith. I mean those two in the desert, only that idea. Surely that’s Russian, isn’t it?”\n\n“Yes, that’s purely Russian,” said Alyosha smiling.\n\n“Your words are worth a gold piece, O ass, and I’ll give it to you to-day. But as to the rest you talk nonsense, nonsense, nonsense. Let me tell you, stupid, that we here are all of little faith, only from carelessness, because we haven’t time; things are too much for us, and, in the second place, the Lord God has given us so little time, only twenty-four hours in the day, so that one hasn’t even time to get sleep enough, much less to repent of one’s sins. While you have denied your faith to your enemies when you’d nothing else to think about but to show your faith! So I consider, brother, that it constitutes a sin.”\n\n“Constitute a sin it may, but consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch, that it only extenuates it, if it does constitute. If I had believed then in very truth, as I ought to have believed, then it really would have been sinful if I had not faced tortures for my faith, and had gone over to the pagan Mohammedan faith. But, of course, it wouldn’t have come to torture then, because I should only have had to say at that instant to the mountain, ‘Move and crush the tormentor,’ and it would have moved and at the very instant have crushed him like a black-beetle, and I should have walked away as though nothing had happened, praising and glorifying God. But, suppose at that very moment I had tried all that, and cried to that mountain, ‘Crush these tormentors,’ and it hadn’t crushed them, how could I have helped doubting, pray, at such a time, and at such a dread hour of mortal terror? And apart from that, I should know already that I could not attain to the fullness of the Kingdom of Heaven (for since the mountain had not moved at my word, they could not think very much of my faith up aloft, and there could be no very great reward awaiting me in the world to come). So why should I let them flay the skin off me as well, and to no good purpose? For, even though they had flayed my skin half off my back, even then the mountain would not have moved at my word or at my cry. And at such a moment not only doubt might come over one but one might lose one’s reason from fear, so that one would not be able to think at all. And, therefore, how should I be particularly to blame if not seeing my advantage or reward there or here, I should, at least, save my skin. And so trusting fully in the grace of the Lord I should cherish the hope that I might be altogether forgiven.”\n\n# Chapter VIII. Over The Brandy\n\nThe controversy was over. But, strange to say, Fyodor Pavlovitch, who had been so gay, suddenly began frowning. He frowned and gulped brandy, and it was already a glass too much.\n\n“Get along with you, Jesuits!” he cried to the servants. “Go away, Smerdyakov. I’ll send you the gold piece I promised you to-day, but be off! Don’t cry, Grigory. Go to Marfa. She’ll comfort you and put you to bed. The rascals won’t let us sit in peace after dinner,” he snapped peevishly, as the servants promptly withdrew at his word.\n\n“Smerdyakov always pokes himself in now, after dinner. It’s you he’s so interested in. What have you done to fascinate him?” he added to Ivan.\n\n“Nothing whatever,” answered Ivan. “He’s pleased to have a high opinion of me; he’s a lackey and a mean soul. Raw material for revolution, however, when the time comes.”\n\n“For revolution?”\n\n“There will be others and better ones. But there will be some like him as well. His kind will come first, and better ones after.”\n\n“And when will the time come?”\n\n“The rocket will go off and fizzle out, perhaps. The peasants are not very fond of listening to these soup-makers, so far.”\n\n“Ah, brother, but a Balaam’s ass like that thinks and thinks, and the devil knows where he gets to.”\n\n“He’s storing up ideas,” said Ivan, smiling.\n\n“You see, I know he can’t bear me, nor any one else, even you, though you fancy that he has a high opinion of you. Worse still with Alyosha, he despises Alyosha. But he doesn’t steal, that’s one thing, and he’s not a gossip, he holds his tongue, and doesn’t wash our dirty linen in public. He makes capital fish pasties too. But, damn him, is he worth talking about so much?”\n\n“Of course he isn’t.”\n\n“And as for the ideas he may be hatching, the Russian peasant, generally speaking, needs thrashing. That I’ve always maintained. Our peasants are swindlers, and don’t deserve to be pitied, and it’s a good thing they’re still flogged sometimes. Russia is rich in birches. If they destroyed the forests, it would be the ruin of Russia. I stand up for the clever people. We’ve left off thrashing the peasants, we’ve grown so clever, but they go on thrashing themselves. And a good thing too. ‘For with what measure ye mete it shall be measured to you again,’ or how does it go? Anyhow, it will be measured. But Russia’s all swinishness. My dear, if you only knew how I hate Russia.... That is, not Russia, but all this vice! But maybe I mean Russia. Tout cela c’est de la cochonnerie.... Do you know what I like? I like wit.”\n\n“You’ve had another glass. That’s enough.”\n\n“Wait a bit. I’ll have one more, and then another, and then I’ll stop. No, stay, you interrupted me. At Mokroe I was talking to an old man, and he told me: ‘There’s nothing we like so much as sentencing girls to be thrashed, and we always give the lads the job of thrashing them. And the girl he has thrashed to-day, the young man will ask in marriage to-morrow. So it quite suits the girls, too,’ he said. There’s a set of de Sades for you! But it’s clever, anyway. Shall we go over and have a look at it, eh? Alyosha, are you blushing? Don’t be bashful, child. I’m sorry I didn’t stay to dinner at the Superior’s and tell the monks about the girls at Mokroe. Alyosha, don’t be angry that I offended your Superior this morning. I lost my temper. If there is a God, if He exists, then, of course, I’m to blame, and I shall have to answer for it. But if there isn’t a God at all, what do they deserve, your fathers? It’s not enough to cut their heads off, for they keep back progress. Would you believe it, Ivan, that that lacerates my sentiments? No, you don’t believe it as I see from your eyes. You believe what people say, that I’m nothing but a buffoon. Alyosha, do you believe that I’m nothing but a buffoon?”\n\n“No, I don’t believe it.”\n\n“And I believe you don’t, and that you speak the truth. You look sincere and you speak sincerely. But not Ivan. Ivan’s supercilious.... I’d make an end of your monks, though, all the same. I’d take all that mystic stuff and suppress it, once for all, all over Russia, so as to bring all the fools to reason. And the gold and the silver that would flow into the mint!”\n\n“But why suppress it?” asked Ivan.\n\n“That Truth may prevail. That’s why.”\n\n“Well, if Truth were to prevail, you know, you’d be the first to be robbed and suppressed.”\n\n“Ah! I dare say you’re right. Ah, I’m an ass!” burst out Fyodor Pavlovitch, striking himself lightly on the forehead. “Well, your monastery may stand then, Alyosha, if that’s how it is. And we clever people will sit snug and enjoy our brandy. You know, Ivan, it must have been so ordained by the Almighty Himself. Ivan, speak, is there a God or not? Stay, speak the truth, speak seriously. Why are you laughing again?”\n\n“I’m laughing that you should have made a clever remark just now about Smerdyakov’s belief in the existence of two saints who could move mountains.”\n\n“Why, am I like him now, then?”\n\n“Very much.”\n\n“Well, that shows I’m a Russian, too, and I have a Russian characteristic. And you may be caught in the same way, though you are a philosopher. Shall I catch you? What do you bet that I’ll catch you to-morrow. Speak, all the same, is there a God, or not? Only, be serious. I want you to be serious now.”\n\n“No, there is no God.”\n\n“Alyosha, is there a God?”\n\n“There is.”\n\n“Ivan, and is there immortality of some sort, just a little, just a tiny bit?”\n\n“There is no immortality either.”\n\n“None at all?”\n\n“None at all.”\n\n“There’s absolute nothingness then. Perhaps there is just something? Anything is better than nothing!”\n\n“Absolute nothingness.”\n\n“Alyosha, is there immortality?”\n\n“There is.”\n\n“God and immortality?”\n\n“God and immortality. In God is immortality.”\n\n“H’m! It’s more likely Ivan’s right. Good Lord! to think what faith, what force of all kinds, man has lavished for nothing, on that dream, and for how many thousand years. Who is it laughing at man? Ivan! For the last time, once for all, is there a God or not? I ask for the last time!”\n\n“And for the last time there is not.”\n\n“Who is laughing at mankind, Ivan?”\n\n“It must be the devil,” said Ivan, smiling.\n\n“And the devil? Does he exist?”\n\n“No, there’s no devil either.”\n\n“It’s a pity. Damn it all, what wouldn’t I do to the man who first invented God! Hanging on a bitter aspen tree would be too good for him.”\n\n“There would have been no civilization if they hadn’t invented God.”\n\n“Wouldn’t there have been? Without God?”\n\n“No. And there would have been no brandy either. But I must take your brandy away from you, anyway.”\n\n“Stop, stop, stop, dear boy, one more little glass. I’ve hurt Alyosha’s feelings. You’re not angry with me, Alyosha? My dear little Alexey!”\n\n“No, I am not angry. I know your thoughts. Your heart is better than your head.”\n\n“My heart better than my head, is it? Oh, Lord! And that from you. Ivan, do you love Alyosha?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You must love him” (Fyodor Pavlovitch was by this time very drunk). “Listen, Alyosha, I was rude to your elder this morning. But I was excited. But there’s wit in that elder, don’t you think, Ivan?”\n\n“Very likely.”\n\n“There is, there is. Il y a du Piron là-dedans. He’s a Jesuit, a Russian one, that is. As he’s an honorable person there’s a hidden indignation boiling within him at having to pretend and affect holiness.”\n\n“But, of course, he believes in God.”\n\n“Not a bit of it. Didn’t you know? Why, he tells every one so, himself. That is, not every one, but all the clever people who come to him. He said straight out to Governor Schultz not long ago: ‘Credo, but I don’t know in what.’”\n\n“Really?”\n\n“He really did. But I respect him. There’s something of Mephistopheles about him, or rather of ‘The hero of our time’ ... Arbenin, or what’s his name?... You see, he’s a sensualist. He’s such a sensualist that I should be afraid for my daughter or my wife if she went to confess to him. You know, when he begins telling stories.... The year before last he invited us to tea, tea with liqueur (the ladies send him liqueur), and began telling us about old times till we nearly split our sides.... Especially how he once cured a paralyzed woman. ‘If my legs were not bad I know a dance I could dance you,’ he said. What do you say to that? ‘I’ve plenty of tricks in my time,’ said he. He did Dernidov, the merchant, out of sixty thousand.”\n\n“What, he stole it?”\n\n“He brought him the money as a man he could trust, saying, ‘Take care of it for me, friend, there’ll be a police search at my place to-morrow.’ And he kept it. ‘You have given it to the Church,’ he declared. I said to him: ‘You’re a scoundrel,’ I said. ‘No,’ said he, ‘I’m not a scoundrel, but I’m broad-minded.’ But that wasn’t he, that was some one else. I’ve muddled him with some one else ... without noticing it. Come, another glass and that’s enough. Take away the bottle, Ivan. I’ve been telling lies. Why didn’t you stop me, Ivan, and tell me I was lying?”\n\n“I knew you’d stop of yourself.”\n\n“That’s a lie. You did it from spite, from simple spite against me. You despise me. You have come to me and despised me in my own house.”\n\n“Well, I’m going away. You’ve had too much brandy.”\n\n“I’ve begged you for Christ’s sake to go to Tchermashnya for a day or two, and you don’t go.”\n\n“I’ll go to-morrow if you’re so set upon it.”\n\n“You won’t go. You want to keep an eye on me. That’s what you want, spiteful fellow. That’s why you won’t go.”\n\nThe old man persisted. He had reached that state of drunkenness when the drunkard who has till then been inoffensive tries to pick a quarrel and to assert himself.\n\n“Why are you looking at me? Why do you look like that? Your eyes look at me and say, ‘You ugly drunkard!’ Your eyes are mistrustful. They’re contemptuous.... You’ve come here with some design. Alyosha, here, looks at me and his eyes shine. Alyosha doesn’t despise me. Alexey, you mustn’t love Ivan.”\n\n“Don’t be ill-tempered with my brother. Leave off attacking him,” Alyosha said emphatically.\n\n“Oh, all right. Ugh, my head aches. Take away the brandy, Ivan. It’s the third time I’ve told you.”\n\nHe mused, and suddenly a slow, cunning grin spread over his face.\n\n“Don’t be angry with a feeble old man, Ivan. I know you don’t love me, but don’t be angry all the same. You’ve nothing to love me for. You go to Tchermashnya. I’ll come to you myself and bring you a present. I’ll show you a little wench there. I’ve had my eye on her a long time. She’s still running about bare-foot. Don’t be afraid of bare-footed wenches—don’t despise them—they’re pearls!”\n\nAnd he kissed his hand with a smack.\n\n“To my thinking,” he revived at once, seeming to grow sober the instant he touched on his favorite topic. “To my thinking ... Ah, you boys! You children, little sucking-pigs, to my thinking ... I never thought a woman ugly in my life—that’s been my rule! Can you understand that? How could you understand it? You’ve milk in your veins, not blood. You’re not out of your shells yet. My rule has been that you can always find something devilishly interesting in every woman that you wouldn’t find in any other. Only, one must know how to find it, that’s the point! That’s a talent! To my mind there are no ugly women. The very fact that she is a woman is half the battle ... but how could you understand that? Even in vieilles filles, even in them you may discover something that makes you simply wonder that men have been such fools as to let them grow old without noticing them. Bare-footed girls or unattractive ones, you must take by surprise. Didn’t you know that? You must astound them till they’re fascinated, upset, ashamed that such a gentleman should fall in love with such a little slut. It’s a jolly good thing that there always are and will be masters and slaves in the world, so there always will be a little maid-of-all-work and her master, and you know, that’s all that’s needed for happiness. Stay ... listen, Alyosha, I always used to surprise your mother, but in a different way. I paid no attention to her at all, but all at once, when the minute came, I’d be all devotion to her, crawl on my knees, kiss her feet, and I always, always—I remember it as though it were to-day—reduced her to that tinkling, quiet, nervous, queer little laugh. It was peculiar to her. I knew her attacks always used to begin like that. The next day she would begin shrieking hysterically, and this little laugh was not a sign of delight, though it made a very good counterfeit. That’s the great thing, to know how to take every one. Once Belyavsky—he was a handsome fellow, and rich—used to like to come here and hang about her—suddenly gave me a slap in the face in her presence. And she—such a mild sheep—why, I thought she would have knocked me down for that blow. How she set on me! ‘You’re beaten, beaten now,’ she said. ‘You’ve taken a blow from him. You have been trying to sell me to him,’ she said.... ‘And how dared he strike you in my presence! Don’t dare come near me again, never, never! Run at once, challenge him to a duel!’... I took her to the monastery then to bring her to her senses. The holy Fathers prayed her back to reason. But I swear, by God, Alyosha, I never insulted the poor crazy girl! Only once, perhaps, in the first year; then she was very fond of praying. She used to keep the feasts of Our Lady particularly and used to turn me out of her room then. I’ll knock that mysticism out of her, thought I! ‘Here,’ said I, ‘you see your holy image. Here it is. Here I take it down. You believe it’s miraculous, but here, I’ll spit on it directly and nothing will happen to me for it!’... When she saw it, good Lord! I thought she would kill me. But she only jumped up, wrung her hands, then suddenly hid her face in them, began trembling all over and fell on the floor ... fell all of a heap. Alyosha, Alyosha, what’s the matter?”\n\nThe old man jumped up in alarm. From the time he had begun speaking about his mother, a change had gradually come over Alyosha’s face. He flushed crimson, his eyes glowed, his lips quivered. The old sot had gone spluttering on, noticing nothing, till the moment when something very strange happened to Alyosha. Precisely what he was describing in the crazy woman was suddenly repeated with Alyosha. He jumped up from his seat exactly as his mother was said to have done, wrung his hands, hid his face in them, and fell back in his chair, shaking all over in an hysterical paroxysm of sudden violent, silent weeping. His extraordinary resemblance to his mother particularly impressed the old man.\n\n“Ivan, Ivan! Water, quickly! It’s like her, exactly as she used to be then, his mother. Spurt some water on him from your mouth, that’s what I used to do to her. He’s upset about his mother, his mother,” he muttered to Ivan.\n\n“But she was my mother, too, I believe, his mother. Was she not?” said Ivan, with uncontrolled anger and contempt. The old man shrank before his flashing eyes. But something very strange had happened, though only for a second; it seemed really to have escaped the old man’s mind that Alyosha’s mother actually was the mother of Ivan too.\n\n“Your mother?” he muttered, not understanding. “What do you mean? What mother are you talking about? Was she?... Why, damn it! of course she was yours too! Damn it! My mind has never been so darkened before. Excuse me, why, I was thinking, Ivan.... He he he!” He stopped. A broad, drunken, half-senseless grin overspread his face.\n\nAt that moment a fearful noise and clamor was heard in the hall, there were violent shouts, the door was flung open, and Dmitri burst into the room. The old man rushed to Ivan in terror.\n\n“He’ll kill me! He’ll kill me! Don’t let him get at me!” he screamed, clinging to the skirt of Ivan’s coat.\n\n# Chapter IX. The Sensualists\n\nGrigory and Smerdyakov ran into the room after Dmitri. They had been struggling with him in the passage, refusing to admit him, acting on instructions given them by Fyodor Pavlovitch some days before. Taking advantage of the fact that Dmitri stopped a moment on entering the room to look about him, Grigory ran round the table, closed the double doors on the opposite side of the room leading to the inner apartments, and stood before the closed doors, stretching wide his arms, prepared to defend the entrance, so to speak, with the last drop of his blood. Seeing this, Dmitri uttered a scream rather than a shout and rushed at Grigory.\n\n“Then she’s there! She’s hidden there! Out of the way, scoundrel!”\n\nHe tried to pull Grigory away, but the old servant pushed him back. Beside himself with fury, Dmitri struck out, and hit Grigory with all his might. The old man fell like a log, and Dmitri, leaping over him, broke in the door. Smerdyakov remained pale and trembling at the other end of the room, huddling close to Fyodor Pavlovitch.\n\n“She’s here!” shouted Dmitri. “I saw her turn towards the house just now, but I couldn’t catch her. Where is she? Where is she?”\n\nThat shout, “She’s here!” produced an indescribable effect on Fyodor Pavlovitch. All his terror left him.\n\n“Hold him! Hold him!” he cried, and dashed after Dmitri. Meanwhile Grigory had got up from the floor, but still seemed stunned. Ivan and Alyosha ran after their father. In the third room something was heard to fall on the floor with a ringing crash: it was a large glass vase—not an expensive one—on a marble pedestal which Dmitri had upset as he ran past it.\n\n“At him!” shouted the old man. “Help!”\n\nIvan and Alyosha caught the old man and were forcibly bringing him back.\n\n“Why do you run after him? He’ll murder you outright,” Ivan cried wrathfully at his father.\n\n“Ivan! Alyosha! She must be here. Grushenka’s here. He said he saw her himself, running.”\n\nHe was choking. He was not expecting Grushenka at the time, and the sudden news that she was here made him beside himself. He was trembling all over. He seemed frantic.\n\n“But you’ve seen for yourself that she hasn’t come,” cried Ivan.\n\n“But she may have come by that other entrance.”\n\n“You know that entrance is locked, and you have the key.”\n\nDmitri suddenly reappeared in the drawing-room. He had, of course, found the other entrance locked, and the key actually was in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s pocket. The windows of all the rooms were also closed, so Grushenka could not have come in anywhere nor have run out anywhere.\n\n“Hold him!” shrieked Fyodor Pavlovitch, as soon as he saw him again. “He’s been stealing money in my bedroom.” And tearing himself from Ivan he rushed again at Dmitri. But Dmitri threw up both hands and suddenly clutched the old man by the two tufts of hair that remained on his temples, tugged at them, and flung him with a crash on the floor. He kicked him two or three times with his heel in the face. The old man moaned shrilly. Ivan, though not so strong as Dmitri, threw his arms round him, and with all his might pulled him away. Alyosha helped him with his slender strength, holding Dmitri in front.\n\n“Madman! You’ve killed him!” cried Ivan.\n\n“Serve him right!” shouted Dmitri breathlessly. “If I haven’t killed him, I’ll come again and kill him. You can’t protect him!”\n\n“Dmitri! Go away at once!” cried Alyosha commandingly.\n\n“Alexey! You tell me. It’s only you I can believe; was she here just now, or not? I saw her myself creeping this way by the fence from the lane. I shouted, she ran away.”\n\n“I swear she’s not been here, and no one expected her.”\n\n“But I saw her.... So she must ... I’ll find out at once where she is.... Good-by, Alexey! Not a word to Æsop about the money now. But go to Katerina Ivanovna at once and be sure to say, ‘He sends his compliments to you!’ Compliments, his compliments! Just compliments and farewell! Describe the scene to her.”\n\nMeanwhile Ivan and Grigory had raised the old man and seated him in an arm-chair. His face was covered with blood, but he was conscious and listened greedily to Dmitri’s cries. He was still fancying that Grushenka really was somewhere in the house. Dmitri looked at him with hatred as he went out.\n\n“I don’t repent shedding your blood!” he cried. “Beware, old man, beware of your dream, for I have my dream, too. I curse you, and disown you altogether.”\n\nHe ran out of the room.\n\n“She’s here. She must be here. Smerdyakov! Smerdyakov!” the old man wheezed, scarcely audibly, beckoning to him with his finger.\n\n“No, she’s not here, you old lunatic!” Ivan shouted at him angrily. “Here, he’s fainting! Water! A towel! Make haste, Smerdyakov!”\n\nSmerdyakov ran for water. At last they got the old man undressed, and put him to bed. They wrapped a wet towel round his head. Exhausted by the brandy, by his violent emotion, and the blows he had received, he shut his eyes and fell asleep as soon as his head touched the pillow. Ivan and Alyosha went back to the drawing-room. Smerdyakov removed the fragments of the broken vase, while Grigory stood by the table looking gloomily at the floor.\n\n“Shouldn’t you put a wet bandage on your head and go to bed, too?” Alyosha said to him. “We’ll look after him. My brother gave you a terrible blow—on the head.”\n\n“He’s insulted me!” Grigory articulated gloomily and distinctly.\n\n“He’s ‘insulted’ his father, not only you,” observed Ivan with a forced smile.\n\n“I used to wash him in his tub. He’s insulted me,” repeated Grigory.\n\n“Damn it all, if I hadn’t pulled him away perhaps he’d have murdered him. It wouldn’t take much to do for Æsop, would it?” whispered Ivan to Alyosha.\n\n“God forbid!” cried Alyosha.\n\n“Why should He forbid?” Ivan went on in the same whisper, with a malignant grimace. “One reptile will devour the other. And serve them both right, too.”\n\nAlyosha shuddered.\n\n“Of course I won’t let him be murdered as I didn’t just now. Stay here, Alyosha, I’ll go for a turn in the yard. My head’s begun to ache.”\n\nAlyosha went to his father’s bedroom and sat by his bedside behind the screen for about an hour. The old man suddenly opened his eyes and gazed for a long while at Alyosha, evidently remembering and meditating. All at once his face betrayed extraordinary excitement.\n\n“Alyosha,” he whispered apprehensively, “where’s Ivan?”\n\n“In the yard. He’s got a headache. He’s on the watch.”\n\n“Give me that looking-glass. It stands over there. Give it me.”\n\nAlyosha gave him a little round folding looking-glass which stood on the chest of drawers. The old man looked at himself in it; his nose was considerably swollen, and on the left side of his forehead there was a rather large crimson bruise.\n\n“What does Ivan say? Alyosha, my dear, my only son, I’m afraid of Ivan. I’m more afraid of Ivan than the other. You’re the only one I’m not afraid of....”\n\n“Don’t be afraid of Ivan either. He is angry, but he’ll defend you.”\n\n“Alyosha, and what of the other? He’s run to Grushenka. My angel, tell me the truth, was she here just now or not?”\n\n“No one has seen her. It was a mistake. She has not been here.”\n\n“You know Mitya wants to marry her, to marry her.”\n\n“She won’t marry him.”\n\n“She won’t. She won’t. She won’t. She won’t on any account!”\n\nThe old man fairly fluttered with joy, as though nothing more comforting could have been said to him. In his delight he seized Alyosha’s hand and pressed it warmly to his heart. Tears positively glittered in his eyes.\n\n“That image of the Mother of God of which I was telling you just now,” he said. “Take it home and keep it for yourself. And I’ll let you go back to the monastery.... I was joking this morning, don’t be angry with me. My head aches, Alyosha.... Alyosha, comfort my heart. Be an angel and tell me the truth!”\n\n“You’re still asking whether she has been here or not?” Alyosha said sorrowfully.\n\n“No, no, no. I believe you. I’ll tell you what it is: you go to Grushenka yourself, or see her somehow; make haste and ask her; see for yourself, which she means to choose, him or me. Eh? What? Can you?”\n\n“If I see her I’ll ask her,” Alyosha muttered, embarrassed.\n\n“No, she won’t tell you,” the old man interrupted, “she’s a rogue. She’ll begin kissing you and say that it’s you she wants. She’s a deceitful, shameless hussy. You mustn’t go to her, you mustn’t!”\n\n“No, father, and it wouldn’t be suitable, it wouldn’t be right at all.”\n\n“Where was he sending you just now? He shouted ‘Go’ as he ran away.”\n\n“To Katerina Ivanovna.”\n\n“For money? To ask her for money?”\n\n“No. Not for money.”\n\n“He’s no money; not a farthing. I’ll settle down for the night, and think things over, and you can go. Perhaps you’ll meet her.... Only be sure to come to me to-morrow in the morning. Be sure to. I have a word to say to you to-morrow. Will you come?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“When you come, pretend you’ve come of your own accord to ask after me. Don’t tell any one I told you to. Don’t say a word to Ivan.”\n\n“Very well.”\n\n“Good-by, my angel. You stood up for me, just now. I shall never forget it. I’ve a word to say to you to-morrow—but I must think about it.”\n\n“And how do you feel now?”\n\n“I shall get up to-morrow and go out, perfectly well, perfectly well!”\n\nCrossing the yard Alyosha found Ivan sitting on the bench at the gateway. He was sitting writing something in pencil in his note-book. Alyosha told Ivan that their father had waked up, was conscious, and had let him go back to sleep at the monastery.\n\n“Alyosha, I should be very glad to meet you to-morrow morning,” said Ivan cordially, standing up. His cordiality was a complete surprise to Alyosha.\n\n“I shall be at the Hohlakovs’ to-morrow,” answered Alyosha, “I may be at Katerina Ivanovna’s, too, if I don’t find her now.”\n\n“But you’re going to her now, anyway? For that ‘compliments and farewell,’” said Ivan smiling. Alyosha was disconcerted.\n\n“I think I quite understand his exclamations just now, and part of what went before. Dmitri has asked you to go to her and say that he—well, in fact—takes his leave of her?”\n\n“Brother, how will all this horror end between father and Dmitri?” exclaimed Alyosha.\n\n“One can’t tell for certain. Perhaps in nothing: it may all fizzle out. That woman is a beast. In any case we must keep the old man indoors and not let Dmitri in the house.”\n\n“Brother, let me ask one thing more: has any man a right to look at other men and decide which is worthy to live?”\n\n“Why bring in the question of worth? The matter is most often decided in men’s hearts on other grounds much more natural. And as for rights—who has not the right to wish?”\n\n“Not for another man’s death?”\n\n“What even if for another man’s death? Why lie to oneself since all men live so and perhaps cannot help living so. Are you referring to what I said just now—that one reptile will devour the other? In that case let me ask you, do you think me like Dmitri capable of shedding Æsop’s blood, murdering him, eh?”\n\n“What are you saying, Ivan? Such an idea never crossed my mind. I don’t think Dmitri is capable of it, either.”\n\n“Thanks, if only for that,” smiled Ivan. “Be sure, I should always defend him. But in my wishes I reserve myself full latitude in this case. Good-by till to-morrow. Don’t condemn me, and don’t look on me as a villain,” he added with a smile.\n\nThey shook hands warmly as they had never done before. Alyosha felt that his brother had taken the first step towards him, and that he had certainly done this with some definite motive.\n\n# Chapter X. Both Together\n\nAlyosha left his father’s house feeling even more exhausted and dejected in spirit than when he had entered it. His mind too seemed shattered and unhinged, while he felt that he was afraid to put together the disjointed fragments and form a general idea from all the agonizing and conflicting experiences of the day. He felt something bordering upon despair, which he had never known till then. Towering like a mountain above all the rest stood the fatal, insoluble question: How would things end between his father and his brother Dmitri with this terrible woman? Now he had himself been a witness of it, he had been present and seen them face to face. Yet only his brother Dmitri could be made unhappy, terribly, completely unhappy: there was trouble awaiting him. It appeared too that there were other people concerned, far more so than Alyosha could have supposed before. There was something positively mysterious in it, too. Ivan had made a step towards him, which was what Alyosha had been long desiring. Yet now he felt for some reason that he was frightened at it. And these women? Strange to say, that morning he had set out for Katerina Ivanovna’s in the greatest embarrassment; now he felt nothing of the kind. On the contrary, he was hastening there as though expecting to find guidance from her. Yet to give her this message was obviously more difficult than before. The matter of the three thousand was decided irrevocably, and Dmitri, feeling himself dishonored and losing his last hope, might sink to any depth. He had, moreover, told him to describe to Katerina Ivanovna the scene which had just taken place with his father.\n\nIt was by now seven o’clock, and it was getting dark as Alyosha entered the very spacious and convenient house in the High Street occupied by Katerina Ivanovna. Alyosha knew that she lived with two aunts. One of them, a woman of little education, was that aunt of her half-sister Agafya Ivanovna who had looked after her in her father’s house when she came from boarding-school. The other aunt was a Moscow lady of style and consequence, though in straitened circumstances. It was said that they both gave way in everything to Katerina Ivanovna, and that she only kept them with her as chaperons. Katerina Ivanovna herself gave way to no one but her benefactress, the general’s widow, who had been kept by illness in Moscow, and to whom she was obliged to write twice a week a full account of all her doings.\n\nWhen Alyosha entered the hall and asked the maid who opened the door to him to take his name up, it was evident that they were already aware of his arrival. Possibly he had been noticed from the window. At least, Alyosha heard a noise, caught the sound of flying footsteps and rustling skirts. Two or three women, perhaps, had run out of the room.\n\nAlyosha thought it strange that his arrival should cause such excitement. He was conducted however to the drawing-room at once. It was a large room, elegantly and amply furnished, not at all in provincial style. There were many sofas, lounges, settees, big and little tables. There were pictures on the walls, vases and lamps on the tables, masses of flowers, and even an aquarium in the window. It was twilight and rather dark. Alyosha made out a silk mantle thrown down on the sofa, where people had evidently just been sitting; and on a table in front of the sofa were two unfinished cups of chocolate, cakes, a glass saucer with blue raisins, and another with sweetmeats. Alyosha saw that he had interrupted visitors, and frowned. But at that instant the portière was raised, and with rapid, hurrying footsteps Katerina Ivanovna came in, holding out both hands to Alyosha with a radiant smile of delight. At the same instant a servant brought in two lighted candles and set them on the table.\n\n“Thank God! At last you have come too! I’ve been simply praying for you all day! Sit down.”\n\nAlyosha had been struck by Katerina Ivanovna’s beauty when, three weeks before, Dmitri had first brought him, at Katerina Ivanovna’s special request, to be introduced to her. There had been no conversation between them at that interview, however. Supposing Alyosha to be very shy, Katerina Ivanovna had talked all the time to Dmitri to spare him. Alyosha had been silent, but he had seen a great deal very clearly. He was struck by the imperiousness, proud ease, and self-confidence of the haughty girl. And all that was certain, Alyosha felt that he was not exaggerating it. He thought her great glowing black eyes were very fine, especially with her pale, even rather sallow, longish face. But in those eyes and in the lines of her exquisite lips there was something with which his brother might well be passionately in love, but which perhaps could not be loved for long. He expressed this thought almost plainly to Dmitri when, after the visit, his brother besought and insisted that he should not conceal his impressions on seeing his betrothed.\n\n“You’ll be happy with her, but perhaps—not tranquilly happy.”\n\n“Quite so, brother. Such people remain always the same. They don’t yield to fate. So you think I shan’t love her for ever.”\n\n“No; perhaps you will love her for ever. But perhaps you won’t always be happy with her.”\n\nAlyosha had given his opinion at the time, blushing, and angry with himself for having yielded to his brother’s entreaties and put such “foolish” ideas into words. For his opinion had struck him as awfully foolish immediately after he had uttered it. He felt ashamed too of having given so confident an opinion about a woman. It was with the more amazement that he felt now, at the first glance at Katerina Ivanovna as she ran in to him, that he had perhaps been utterly mistaken. This time her face was beaming with spontaneous good-natured kindliness, and direct warm-hearted sincerity. The “pride and haughtiness,” which had struck Alyosha so much before, was only betrayed now in a frank, generous energy and a sort of bright, strong faith in herself. Alyosha realized at the first glance, at the first word, that all the tragedy of her position in relation to the man she loved so dearly was no secret to her; that she perhaps already knew everything, positively everything. And yet, in spite of that, there was such brightness in her face, such faith in the future. Alyosha felt at once that he had gravely wronged her in his thoughts. He was conquered and captivated immediately. Besides all this, he noticed at her first words that she was in great excitement, an excitement perhaps quite exceptional and almost approaching ecstasy.\n\n“I was so eager to see you, because I can learn from you the whole truth—from you and no one else.”\n\n“I have come,” muttered Alyosha confusedly, “I—he sent me.”\n\n“Ah, he sent you! I foresaw that. Now I know everything—everything!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, her eyes flashing. “Wait a moment, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I’ll tell you why I’ve been so longing to see you. You see, I know perhaps far more than you do yourself, and there’s no need for you to tell me anything. I’ll tell you what I want from you. I want to know your own last impression of him. I want you to tell me most directly, plainly, coarsely even (oh, as coarsely as you like!), what you thought of him just now and of his position after your meeting with him to-day. That will perhaps be better than if I had a personal explanation with him, as he does not want to come to me. Do you understand what I want from you? Now, tell me simply, tell me every word of the message he sent you with (I knew he would send you).”\n\n“He told me to give you his compliments—and to say that he would never come again—but to give you his compliments.”\n\n“His compliments? Was that what he said—his own expression?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Accidentally perhaps he made a mistake in the word, perhaps he did not use the right word?”\n\n“No; he told me precisely to repeat that word. He begged me two or three times not to forget to say so.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna flushed hotly.\n\n“Help me now, Alexey Fyodorovitch. Now I really need your help. I’ll tell you what I think, and you must simply say whether it’s right or not. Listen! If he had sent me his compliments in passing, without insisting on your repeating the words, without emphasizing them, that would be the end of everything! But if he particularly insisted on those words, if he particularly told you not to forget to repeat them to me, then perhaps he was in excitement, beside himself. He had made his decision and was frightened at it. He wasn’t walking away from me with a resolute step, but leaping headlong. The emphasis on that phrase may have been simply bravado.”\n\n“Yes, yes!” cried Alyosha warmly. “I believe that is it.”\n\n“And, if so, he’s not altogether lost. I can still save him. Stay! Did he not tell you anything about money—about three thousand roubles?”\n\n“He did speak about it, and it’s that more than anything that’s crushing him. He said he had lost his honor and that nothing matters now,” Alyosha answered warmly, feeling a rush of hope in his heart and believing that there really might be a way of escape and salvation for his brother. “But do you know about the money?” he added, and suddenly broke off.\n\n“I’ve known of it a long time; I telegraphed to Moscow to inquire, and heard long ago that the money had not arrived. He hadn’t sent the money, but I said nothing. Last week I learnt that he was still in need of money. My only object in all this was that he should know to whom to turn, and who was his true friend. No, he won’t recognize that I am his truest friend; he won’t know me, and looks on me merely as a woman. I’ve been tormented all the week, trying to think how to prevent him from being ashamed to face me because he spent that three thousand. Let him feel ashamed of himself, let him be ashamed of other people’s knowing, but not of my knowing. He can tell God everything without shame. Why is it he still does not understand how much I am ready to bear for his sake? Why, why doesn’t he know me? How dare he not know me after all that has happened? I want to save him for ever. Let him forget me as his betrothed. And here he fears that he is dishonored in my eyes. Why, he wasn’t afraid to be open with you, Alexey Fyodorovitch. How is it that I don’t deserve the same?”\n\nThe last words she uttered in tears. Tears gushed from her eyes.\n\n“I must tell you,” Alyosha began, his voice trembling too, “what happened just now between him and my father.”\n\nAnd he described the whole scene, how Dmitri had sent him to get the money, how he had broken in, knocked his father down, and after that had again specially and emphatically begged him to take his compliments and farewell. “He went to that woman,” Alyosha added softly.\n\n“And do you suppose that I can’t put up with that woman? Does he think I can’t? But he won’t marry her,” she suddenly laughed nervously. “Could such a passion last for ever in a Karamazov? It’s passion, not love. He won’t marry her because she won’t marry him.” Again Katerina Ivanovna laughed strangely.\n\n“He may marry her,” said Alyosha mournfully, looking down.\n\n“He won’t marry her, I tell you. That girl is an angel. Do you know that? Do you know that?” Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed suddenly with extraordinary warmth. “She is one of the most fantastic of fantastic creatures. I know how bewitching she is, but I know too that she is kind, firm and noble. Why do you look at me like that, Alexey Fyodorovitch? Perhaps you are wondering at my words, perhaps you don’t believe me? Agrafena Alexandrovna, my angel!” she cried suddenly to some one, peeping into the next room, “come in to us. This is a friend. This is Alyosha. He knows all about our affairs. Show yourself to him.”\n\n“I’ve only been waiting behind the curtain for you to call me,” said a soft, one might even say sugary, feminine voice.\n\nThe portière was raised and Grushenka herself, smiling and beaming, came up to the table. A violent revulsion passed over Alyosha. He fixed his eyes on her and could not take them off. Here she was, that awful woman, the “beast,” as Ivan had called her half an hour before. And yet one would have thought the creature standing before him most simple and ordinary, a good-natured, kind woman, handsome certainly, but so like other handsome ordinary women! It is true she was very, very good-looking with that Russian beauty so passionately loved by many men. She was a rather tall woman, though a little shorter than Katerina Ivanovna, who was exceptionally tall. She had a full figure, with soft, as it were, noiseless, movements, softened to a peculiar over-sweetness, like her voice. She moved, not like Katerina Ivanovna, with a vigorous, bold step, but noiselessly. Her feet made absolutely no sound on the floor. She sank softly into a low chair, softly rustling her sumptuous black silk dress, and delicately nestling her milk-white neck and broad shoulders in a costly cashmere shawl. She was twenty-two years old, and her face looked exactly that age. She was very white in the face, with a pale pink tint on her cheeks. The modeling of her face might be said to be too broad, and the lower jaw was set a trifle forward. Her upper lip was thin, but the slightly prominent lower lip was at least twice as full, and looked pouting. But her magnificent, abundant dark brown hair, her sable-colored eyebrows and charming gray-blue eyes with their long lashes would have made the most indifferent person, meeting her casually in a crowd in the street, stop at the sight of her face and remember it long after. What struck Alyosha most in that face was its expression of childlike good nature. There was a childlike look in her eyes, a look of childish delight. She came up to the table, beaming with delight and seeming to expect something with childish, impatient, and confiding curiosity. The light in her eyes gladdened the soul—Alyosha felt that. There was something else in her which he could not understand, or would not have been able to define, and which yet perhaps unconsciously affected him. It was that softness, that voluptuousness of her bodily movements, that catlike noiselessness. Yet it was a vigorous, ample body. Under the shawl could be seen full broad shoulders, a high, still quite girlish bosom. Her figure suggested the lines of the Venus of Milo, though already in somewhat exaggerated proportions. That could be divined. Connoisseurs of Russian beauty could have foretold with certainty that this fresh, still youthful beauty would lose its harmony by the age of thirty, would “spread”; that the face would become puffy, and that wrinkles would very soon appear upon her forehead and round the eyes; the complexion would grow coarse and red perhaps—in fact, that it was the beauty of the moment, the fleeting beauty which is so often met with in Russian women. Alyosha, of course, did not think of this; but though he was fascinated, yet he wondered with an unpleasant sensation, and as it were regretfully, why she drawled in that way and could not speak naturally. She did so evidently feeling there was a charm in the exaggerated, honeyed modulation of the syllables. It was, of course, only a bad, underbred habit that showed bad education and a false idea of good manners. And yet this intonation and manner of speaking impressed Alyosha as almost incredibly incongruous with the childishly simple and happy expression of her face, the soft, babyish joy in her eyes. Katerina Ivanovna at once made her sit down in an arm-chair facing Alyosha, and ecstatically kissed her several times on her smiling lips. She seemed quite in love with her.\n\n“This is the first time we’ve met, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” she said rapturously. “I wanted to know her, to see her. I wanted to go to her, but I’d no sooner expressed the wish than she came to me. I knew we should settle everything together—everything. My heart told me so—I was begged not to take the step, but I foresaw it would be a way out of the difficulty, and I was not mistaken. Grushenka has explained everything to me, told me all she means to do. She flew here like an angel of goodness and brought us peace and joy.”\n\n“You did not disdain me, sweet, excellent young lady,” drawled Grushenka in her sing-song voice, still with the same charming smile of delight.\n\n“Don’t dare to speak to me like that, you sorceress, you witch! Disdain you! Here, I must kiss your lower lip once more. It looks as though it were swollen, and now it will be more so, and more and more. Look how she laughs, Alexey Fyodorovitch! It does one’s heart good to see the angel.”\n\nAlyosha flushed, and faint, imperceptible shivers kept running down him.\n\n“You make so much of me, dear young lady, and perhaps I am not at all worthy of your kindness.”\n\n“Not worthy! She’s not worthy of it!” Katerina Ivanovna cried again with the same warmth. “You know, Alexey Fyodorovitch, we’re fanciful, we’re self-willed, but proudest of the proud in our little heart. We’re noble, we’re generous, Alexey Fyodorovitch, let me tell you. We have only been unfortunate. We were too ready to make every sacrifice for an unworthy, perhaps, or fickle man. There was one man—one, an officer too, we loved him, we sacrificed everything to him. That was long ago, five years ago, and he has forgotten us, he has married. Now he is a widower, he has written, he is coming here, and, do you know, we’ve loved him, none but him, all this time, and we’ve loved him all our life! He will come, and Grushenka will be happy again. For the last five years she’s been wretched. But who can reproach her, who can boast of her favor? Only that bedridden old merchant, but he is more like her father, her friend, her protector. He found her then in despair, in agony, deserted by the man she loved. She was ready to drown herself then, but the old merchant saved her—saved her!”\n\n“You defend me very kindly, dear young lady. You are in a great hurry about everything,” Grushenka drawled again.\n\n“Defend you! Is it for me to defend you? Should I dare to defend you? Grushenka, angel, give me your hand. Look at that charming soft little hand, Alexey Fyodorovitch! Look at it! It has brought me happiness and has lifted me up, and I’m going to kiss it, outside and inside, here, here, here!”\n\nAnd three times she kissed the certainly charming, though rather fat, hand of Grushenka in a sort of rapture. She held out her hand with a charming musical, nervous little laugh, watched the “sweet young lady,” and obviously liked having her hand kissed.\n\n“Perhaps there’s rather too much rapture,” thought Alyosha. He blushed. He felt a peculiar uneasiness at heart the whole time.\n\n“You won’t make me blush, dear young lady, kissing my hand like this before Alexey Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“Do you think I meant to make you blush?” said Katerina Ivanovna, somewhat surprised. “Ah, my dear, how little you understand me!”\n\n“Yes, and you too perhaps quite misunderstand me, dear young lady. Maybe I’m not so good as I seem to you. I’ve a bad heart; I will have my own way. I fascinated poor Dmitri Fyodorovitch that day simply for fun.”\n\n“But now you’ll save him. You’ve given me your word. You’ll explain it all to him. You’ll break to him that you have long loved another man, who is now offering you his hand.”\n\n“Oh, no! I didn’t give you my word to do that. It was you kept talking about that. I didn’t give you my word.”\n\n“Then I didn’t quite understand you,” said Katerina Ivanovna slowly, turning a little pale. “You promised—”\n\n“Oh, no, angel lady, I’ve promised nothing,” Grushenka interrupted softly and evenly, still with the same gay and simple expression. “You see at once, dear young lady, what a willful wretch I am compared with you. If I want to do a thing I do it. I may have made you some promise just now. But now again I’m thinking: I may take to Mitya again. I liked him very much once—liked him for almost a whole hour. Now maybe I shall go and tell him to stay with me from this day forward. You see, I’m so changeable.”\n\n“Just now you said—something quite different,” Katerina Ivanovna whispered faintly.\n\n“Ah, just now! But, you know. I’m such a soft-hearted, silly creature. Only think what he’s gone through on my account! What if when I go home I feel sorry for him? What then?”\n\n“I never expected—”\n\n“Ah, young lady, how good and generous you are compared with me! Now perhaps you won’t care for a silly creature like me, now you know my character. Give me your sweet little hand, angelic lady,” she said tenderly, and with a sort of reverence took Katerina Ivanovna’s hand.\n\n“Here, dear young lady, I’ll take your hand and kiss it as you did mine. You kissed mine three times, but I ought to kiss yours three hundred times to be even with you. Well, but let that pass. And then it shall be as God wills. Perhaps I shall be your slave entirely and want to do your bidding like a slave. Let it be as God wills, without any agreements and promises. What a sweet hand—what a sweet hand you have! You sweet young lady, you incredible beauty!”\n\nShe slowly raised the hands to her lips, with the strange object indeed of “being even” with her in kisses.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna did not take her hand away. She listened with timid hope to the last words, though Grushenka’s promise to do her bidding like a slave was very strangely expressed. She looked intently into her eyes; she still saw in those eyes the same simple-hearted, confiding expression, the same bright gayety.\n\n“She’s perhaps too naïve,” thought Katerina Ivanovna, with a gleam of hope.\n\nGrushenka meanwhile seemed enthusiastic over the “sweet hand.” She raised it deliberately to her lips. But she held it for two or three minutes near her lips, as though reconsidering something.\n\n“Do you know, angel lady,” she suddenly drawled in an even more soft and sugary voice, “do you know, after all, I think I won’t kiss your hand?” And she laughed a little merry laugh.\n\n“As you please. What’s the matter with you?” said Katerina Ivanovna, starting suddenly.\n\n“So that you may be left to remember that you kissed my hand, but I didn’t kiss yours.”\n\nThere was a sudden gleam in her eyes. She looked with awful intentness at Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“Insolent creature!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, as though suddenly grasping something. She flushed all over and leapt up from her seat.\n\nGrushenka too got up, but without haste.\n\n“So I shall tell Mitya how you kissed my hand, but I didn’t kiss yours at all. And how he will laugh!”\n\n“Vile slut! Go away!”\n\n“Ah, for shame, young lady! Ah, for shame! That’s unbecoming for you, dear young lady, a word like that.”\n\n“Go away! You’re a creature for sale!” screamed Katerina Ivanovna. Every feature was working in her utterly distorted face.\n\n“For sale indeed! You used to visit gentlemen in the dusk for money once; you brought your beauty for sale. You see, I know.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna shrieked, and would have rushed at her, but Alyosha held her with all his strength.\n\n“Not a step, not a word! Don’t speak, don’t answer her. She’ll go away—she’ll go at once.”\n\nAt that instant Katerina Ivanovna’s two aunts ran in at her cry, and with them a maid-servant. All hurried to her.\n\n“I will go away,” said Grushenka, taking up her mantle from the sofa. “Alyosha, darling, see me home!”\n\n“Go away—go away, make haste!” cried Alyosha, clasping his hands imploringly.\n\n“Dear little Alyosha, see me home! I’ve got a pretty little story to tell you on the way. I got up this scene for your benefit, Alyosha. See me home, dear, you’ll be glad of it afterwards.”\n\nAlyosha turned away, wringing his hands. Grushenka ran out of the house, laughing musically.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna went into a fit of hysterics. She sobbed, and was shaken with convulsions. Every one fussed round her.\n\n“I warned you,” said the elder of her aunts. “I tried to prevent your doing this. You’re too impulsive. How could you do such a thing? You don’t know these creatures, and they say she’s worse than any of them. You are too self-willed.”\n\n“She’s a tigress!” yelled Katerina Ivanovna. “Why did you hold me, Alexey Fyodorovitch? I’d have beaten her—beaten her!”\n\nShe could not control herself before Alyosha; perhaps she did not care to, indeed.\n\n“She ought to be flogged in public on a scaffold!”\n\nAlyosha withdrew towards the door.\n\n“But, my God!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, clasping her hands. “He! He! He could be so dishonorable, so inhuman! Why, he told that creature what happened on that fatal, accursed day! ‘You brought your beauty for sale, dear young lady.’ She knows it! Your brother’s a scoundrel, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”\n\nAlyosha wanted to say something, but he couldn’t find a word. His heart ached.\n\n“Go away, Alexey Fyodorovitch! It’s shameful, it’s awful for me! To-morrow, I beg you on my knees, come to-morrow. Don’t condemn me. Forgive me. I don’t know what I shall do with myself now!”\n\nAlyosha walked out into the street reeling. He could have wept as she did. Suddenly he was overtaken by the maid.\n\n“The young lady forgot to give you this letter from Madame Hohlakov; it’s been left with us since dinner-time.”\n\nAlyosha took the little pink envelope mechanically and put it, almost unconsciously, into his pocket.\n\n# Chapter XI. Another Reputation Ruined\n\nIt was not much more than three-quarters of a mile from the town to the monastery. Alyosha walked quickly along the road, at that hour deserted. It was almost night, and too dark to see anything clearly at thirty paces ahead. There were cross-roads half-way. A figure came into sight under a solitary willow at the cross-roads. As soon as Alyosha reached the cross-roads the figure moved out and rushed at him, shouting savagely:\n\n“Your money or your life!”\n\n“So it’s you, Mitya,” cried Alyosha, in surprise, violently startled however.\n\n“Ha ha ha! You didn’t expect me? I wondered where to wait for you. By her house? There are three ways from it, and I might have missed you. At last I thought of waiting here, for you had to pass here, there’s no other way to the monastery. Come, tell me the truth. Crush me like a beetle. But what’s the matter?”\n\n“Nothing, brother—it’s the fright you gave me. Oh, Dmitri! Father’s blood just now.” (Alyosha began to cry, he had been on the verge of tears for a long time, and now something seemed to snap in his soul.) “You almost killed him—cursed him—and now—here—you’re making jokes—‘Your money or your life!’”\n\n“Well, what of that? It’s not seemly—is that it? Not suitable in my position?”\n\n“No—I only—”\n\n“Stay. Look at the night. You see what a dark night, what clouds, what a wind has risen. I hid here under the willow waiting for you. And as God’s above, I suddenly thought, why go on in misery any longer, what is there to wait for? Here I have a willow, a handkerchief, a shirt, I can twist them into a rope in a minute, and braces besides, and why go on burdening the earth, dishonoring it with my vile presence? And then I heard you coming—Heavens, it was as though something flew down to me suddenly. So there is a man, then, whom I love. Here he is, that man, my dear little brother, whom I love more than any one in the world, the only one I love in the world. And I loved you so much, so much at that moment that I thought, ‘I’ll fall on his neck at once.’ Then a stupid idea struck me, to have a joke with you and scare you. I shouted, like a fool, ‘Your money!’ Forgive my foolery—it was only nonsense, and there’s nothing unseemly in my soul.... Damn it all, tell me what’s happened. What did she say? Strike me, crush me, don’t spare me! Was she furious?”\n\n“No, not that.... There was nothing like that, Mitya. There—I found them both there.”\n\n“Both? Whom?”\n\n“Grushenka at Katerina Ivanovna’s.”\n\nDmitri was struck dumb.\n\n“Impossible!” he cried. “You’re raving! Grushenka with her?”\n\nAlyosha described all that had happened from the moment he went in to Katerina Ivanovna’s. He was ten minutes telling his story. He can’t be said to have told it fluently and consecutively, but he seemed to make it clear, not omitting any word or action of significance, and vividly describing, often in one word, his own sensations. Dmitri listened in silence, gazing at him with a terrible fixed stare, but it was clear to Alyosha that he understood it all, and had grasped every point. But as the story went on, his face became not merely gloomy, but menacing. He scowled, he clenched his teeth, and his fixed stare became still more rigid, more concentrated, more terrible, when suddenly, with incredible rapidity, his wrathful, savage face changed, his tightly compressed lips parted, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch broke into uncontrolled, spontaneous laughter. He literally shook with laughter. For a long time he could not speak.\n\n“So she wouldn’t kiss her hand! So she didn’t kiss it; so she ran away!” he kept exclaiming with hysterical delight; insolent delight it might have been called, if it had not been so spontaneous. “So the other one called her tigress! And a tigress she is! So she ought to be flogged on a scaffold? Yes, yes, so she ought. That’s just what I think; she ought to have been long ago. It’s like this, brother, let her be punished, but I must get better first. I understand the queen of impudence. That’s her all over! You saw her all over in that hand-kissing, the she-devil! She’s magnificent in her own line! So she ran home? I’ll go—ah—I’ll run to her! Alyosha, don’t blame me, I agree that hanging is too good for her.”\n\n“But Katerina Ivanovna!” exclaimed Alyosha sorrowfully.\n\n“I see her, too! I see right through her, as I’ve never done before! It’s a regular discovery of the four continents of the world, that is, of the five! What a thing to do! That’s just like Katya, who was not afraid to face a coarse, unmannerly officer and risk a deadly insult on a generous impulse to save her father! But the pride, the recklessness, the defiance of fate, the unbounded defiance! You say that aunt tried to stop her? That aunt, you know, is overbearing, herself. She’s the sister of the general’s widow in Moscow, and even more stuck-up than she. But her husband was caught stealing government money. He lost everything, his estate and all, and the proud wife had to lower her colors, and hasn’t raised them since. So she tried to prevent Katya, but she wouldn’t listen to her! She thinks she can overcome everything, that everything will give way to her. She thought she could bewitch Grushenka if she liked, and she believed it herself: she plays a part to herself, and whose fault is it? Do you think she kissed Grushenka’s hand first, on purpose, with a motive? No, she really was fascinated by Grushenka, that’s to say, not by Grushenka, but by her own dream, her own delusion—because it was her dream, her delusion! Alyosha, darling, how did you escape from them, those women? Did you pick up your cassock and run? Ha ha ha!”\n\n“Brother, you don’t seem to have noticed how you’ve insulted Katerina Ivanovna by telling Grushenka about that day. And she flung it in her face just now that she had gone to gentlemen in secret to sell her beauty! Brother, what could be worse than that insult?”\n\nWhat worried Alyosha more than anything was that, incredible as it seemed, his brother appeared pleased at Katerina Ivanovna’s humiliation.\n\n“Bah!” Dmitri frowned fiercely, and struck his forehead with his hand. He only now realized it, though Alyosha had just told him of the insult, and Katerina Ivanovna’s cry: “Your brother is a scoundrel!”\n\n“Yes, perhaps, I really did tell Grushenka about that ‘fatal day,’ as Katya calls it. Yes, I did tell her, I remember! It was that time at Mokroe. I was drunk, the gypsies were singing.... But I was sobbing. I was sobbing then, kneeling and praying to Katya’s image, and Grushenka understood it. She understood it all then. I remember, she cried herself.... Damn it all! But it’s bound to be so now.... Then she cried, but now ‘the dagger in the heart’! That’s how women are.”\n\nHe looked down and sank into thought.\n\n“Yes, I am a scoundrel, a thorough scoundrel!” he said suddenly, in a gloomy voice. “It doesn’t matter whether I cried or not, I’m a scoundrel! Tell her I accept the name, if that’s any comfort. Come, that’s enough. Good-by. It’s no use talking! It’s not amusing. You go your way and I mine. And I don’t want to see you again except as a last resource. Good-by, Alexey!”\n\nHe warmly pressed Alyosha’s hand, and still looking down, without raising his head, as though tearing himself away, turned rapidly towards the town.\n\nAlyosha looked after him, unable to believe he would go away so abruptly.\n\n“Stay, Alexey, one more confession to you alone!” cried Dmitri, suddenly turning back. “Look at me. Look at me well. You see here, here—there’s terrible disgrace in store for me.” (As he said “here,” Dmitri struck his chest with his fist with a strange air, as though the dishonor lay precisely on his chest, in some spot, in a pocket, perhaps, or hanging round his neck.) “You know me now, a scoundrel, an avowed scoundrel, but let me tell you that I’ve never done anything before and never shall again, anything that can compare in baseness with the dishonor which I bear now at this very minute on my breast, here, here, which will come to pass, though I’m perfectly free to stop it. I can stop it or carry it through, note that. Well, let me tell you, I shall carry it through. I shan’t stop it. I told you everything just now, but I didn’t tell you this, because even I had not brass enough for it. I can still pull up; if I do, I can give back the full half of my lost honor to-morrow. But I shan’t pull up. I shall carry out my base plan, and you can bear witness that I told you so beforehand. Darkness and destruction! No need to explain. You’ll find out in due time. The filthy back-alley and the she-devil. Good-by. Don’t pray for me, I’m not worth it. And there’s no need, no need at all.... I don’t need it! Away!”\n\nAnd he suddenly retreated, this time finally. Alyosha went towards the monastery.\n\n“What? I shall never see him again! What is he saying?” he wondered wildly. “Why, I shall certainly see him to-morrow. I shall look him up. I shall make a point of it. What does he mean?”\n\nHe went round the monastery, and crossed the pine-wood to the hermitage. The door was opened to him, though no one was admitted at that hour. There was a tremor in his heart as he went into Father Zossima’s cell.\n\n“Why, why, had he gone forth? Why had he sent him into the world? Here was peace. Here was holiness. But there was confusion, there was darkness in which one lost one’s way and went astray at once....”\n\nIn the cell he found the novice Porfiry and Father Païssy, who came every hour to inquire after Father Zossima. Alyosha learnt with alarm that he was getting worse and worse. Even his usual discourse with the brothers could not take place that day. As a rule every evening after service the monks flocked into Father Zossima’s cell, and all confessed aloud their sins of the day, their sinful thoughts and temptations; even their disputes, if there had been any. Some confessed kneeling. The elder absolved, reconciled, exhorted, imposed penance, blessed, and dismissed them. It was against this general “confession” that the opponents of “elders” protested, maintaining that it was a profanation of the sacrament of confession, almost a sacrilege, though this was quite a different thing. They even represented to the diocesan authorities that such confessions attained no good object, but actually to a large extent led to sin and temptation. Many of the brothers disliked going to the elder, and went against their own will because every one went, and for fear they should be accused of pride and rebellious ideas. People said that some of the monks agreed beforehand, saying, “I’ll confess I lost my temper with you this morning, and you confirm it,” simply in order to have something to say. Alyosha knew that this actually happened sometimes. He knew, too, that there were among the monks some who deeply resented the fact that letters from relations were habitually taken to the elder, to be opened and read by him before those to whom they were addressed.\n\nIt was assumed, of course, that all this was done freely, and in good faith, by way of voluntary submission and salutary guidance. But, in fact, there was sometimes no little insincerity, and much that was false and strained in this practice. Yet the older and more experienced of the monks adhered to their opinion, arguing that “for those who have come within these walls sincerely seeking salvation, such obedience and sacrifice will certainly be salutary and of great benefit; those, on the other hand, who find it irksome, and repine, are no true monks, and have made a mistake in entering the monastery—their proper place is in the world. Even in the temple one cannot be safe from sin and the devil. So it was no good taking it too much into account.”\n\n“He is weaker, a drowsiness has come over him,” Father Païssy whispered to Alyosha, as he blessed him. “It’s difficult to rouse him. And he must not be roused. He waked up for five minutes, sent his blessing to the brothers, and begged their prayers for him at night. He intends to take the sacrament again in the morning. He remembered you, Alexey. He asked whether you had gone away, and was told that you were in the town. ‘I blessed him for that work,’ he said, ‘his place is there, not here, for awhile.’ Those were his words about you. He remembered you lovingly, with anxiety; do you understand how he honored you? But how is it that he has decided that you shall spend some time in the world? He must have foreseen something in your destiny! Understand, Alexey, that if you return to the world, it must be to do the duty laid upon you by your elder, and not for frivolous vanity and worldly pleasures.”\n\nFather Païssy went out. Alyosha had no doubt that Father Zossima was dying, though he might live another day or two. Alyosha firmly and ardently resolved that in spite of his promises to his father, the Hohlakovs, and Katerina Ivanovna, he would not leave the monastery next day, but would remain with his elder to the end. His heart glowed with love, and he reproached himself bitterly for having been able for one instant to forget him whom he had left in the monastery on his deathbed, and whom he honored above every one in the world. He went into Father Zossima’s bedroom, knelt down, and bowed to the ground before the elder, who slept quietly without stirring, with regular, hardly audible breathing and a peaceful face.\n\nAlyosha returned to the other room, where Father Zossima had received his guests in the morning. Taking off his boots, he lay down on the hard, narrow, leathern sofa, which he had long used as a bed, bringing nothing but a pillow. The mattress, about which his father had shouted to him that morning, he had long forgotten to lie on. He took off his cassock, which he used as a covering. But before going to bed, he fell on his knees and prayed a long time. In his fervent prayer he did not beseech God to lighten his darkness but only thirsted for the joyous emotion, which always visited his soul after the praise and adoration, of which his evening prayer usually consisted. That joy always brought him light untroubled sleep. As he was praying, he suddenly felt in his pocket the little pink note the servant had handed him as he left Katerina Ivanovna’s. He was disturbed, but finished his prayer. Then, after some hesitation, he opened the envelope. In it was a letter to him, signed by Lise, the young daughter of Madame Hohlakov, who had laughed at him before the elder in the morning.\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch,” she wrote, “I am writing to you without any one’s knowledge, even mamma’s, and I know how wrong it is. But I cannot live without telling you the feeling that has sprung up in my heart, and this no one but us two must know for a time. But how am I to say what I want so much to tell you? Paper, they say, does not blush, but I assure you it’s not true and that it’s blushing just as I am now, all over. Dear Alyosha, I love you, I’ve loved you from my childhood, since our Moscow days, when you were very different from what you are now, and I shall love you all my life. My heart has chosen you, to unite our lives, and pass them together till our old age. Of course, on condition that you will leave the monastery. As for our age we will wait for the time fixed by the law. By that time I shall certainly be quite strong, I shall be walking and dancing. There can be no doubt of that.\n\n“You see how I’ve thought of everything. There’s only one thing I can’t imagine: what you’ll think of me when you read this. I’m always laughing and being naughty. I made you angry this morning, but I assure you before I took up my pen, I prayed before the Image of the Mother of God, and now I’m praying, and almost crying.\n\n“My secret is in your hands. When you come to-morrow, I don’t know how I shall look at you. Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, what if I can’t restrain myself like a silly and laugh when I look at you as I did to-day. You’ll think I’m a nasty girl making fun of you, and you won’t believe my letter. And so I beg you, dear one, if you’ve any pity for me, when you come to-morrow, don’t look me straight in the face, for if I meet your eyes, it will be sure to make me laugh, especially as you’ll be in that long gown. I feel cold all over when I think of it, so when you come, don’t look at me at all for a time, look at mamma or at the window....\n\n“Here I’ve written you a love-letter. Oh, dear, what have I done? Alyosha, don’t despise me, and if I’ve done something very horrid and wounded you, forgive me. Now the secret of my reputation, ruined perhaps for ever, is in your hands.\n\n“I shall certainly cry to-day. Good-by till our meeting, our awful meeting.—LISE.\n\n“P.S.—Alyosha! You must, must, must come!—LISE.”\n\nAlyosha read the note in amazement, read it through twice, thought a little, and suddenly laughed a soft, sweet laugh. He started. That laugh seemed to him sinful. But a minute later he laughed again just as softly and happily. He slowly replaced the note in the envelope, crossed himself and lay down. The agitation in his heart passed at once. “God, have mercy upon all of them, have all these unhappy and turbulent souls in Thy keeping, and set them in the right path. All ways are Thine. Save them according to Thy wisdom. Thou art love. Thou wilt send joy to all!” Alyosha murmured, crossing himself, and falling into peaceful sleep.\n\n# PART II Book IV. Lacerations Chapter I. Father Ferapont\n\nAlyosha was roused early, before daybreak. Father Zossima woke up feeling very weak, though he wanted to get out of bed and sit up in a chair. His mind was quite clear; his face looked very tired, yet bright and almost joyful. It wore an expression of gayety, kindness and cordiality. “Maybe I shall not live through the coming day,” he said to Alyosha. Then he desired to confess and take the sacrament at once. He always confessed to Father Païssy. After taking the communion, the service of extreme unction followed. The monks assembled and the cell was gradually filled up by the inmates of the hermitage. Meantime it was daylight. People began coming from the monastery. After the service was over the elder desired to kiss and take leave of every one. As the cell was so small the earlier visitors withdrew to make room for others. Alyosha stood beside the elder, who was seated again in his arm-chair. He talked as much as he could. Though his voice was weak, it was fairly steady.\n\n“I’ve been teaching you so many years, and therefore I’ve been talking aloud so many years, that I’ve got into the habit of talking, and so much so that it’s almost more difficult for me to hold my tongue than to talk, even now, in spite of my weakness, dear Fathers and brothers,” he jested, looking with emotion at the group round him.\n\nAlyosha remembered afterwards something of what he said to them. But though he spoke out distinctly and his voice was fairly steady, his speech was somewhat disconnected. He spoke of many things, he seemed anxious before the moment of death to say everything he had not said in his life, and not simply for the sake of instructing them, but as though thirsting to share with all men and all creation his joy and ecstasy, and once more in his life to open his whole heart.\n\n“Love one another, Fathers,” said Father Zossima, as far as Alyosha could remember afterwards. “Love God’s people. Because we have come here and shut ourselves within these walls, we are no holier than those that are outside, but on the contrary, from the very fact of coming here, each of us has confessed to himself that he is worse than others, than all men on earth.... And the longer the monk lives in his seclusion, the more keenly he must recognize that. Else he would have had no reason to come here. When he realizes that he is not only worse than others, but that he is responsible to all men for all and everything, for all human sins, national and individual, only then the aim of our seclusion is attained. For know, dear ones, that every one of us is undoubtedly responsible for all men and everything on earth, not merely through the general sinfulness of creation, but each one personally for all mankind and every individual man. This knowledge is the crown of life for the monk and for every man. For monks are not a special sort of men, but only what all men ought to be. Only through that knowledge, our heart grows soft with infinite, universal, inexhaustible love. Then every one of you will have the power to win over the whole world by love and to wash away the sins of the world with your tears.... Each of you keep watch over your heart and confess your sins to yourself unceasingly. Be not afraid of your sins, even when perceiving them, if only there be penitence, but make no conditions with God. Again I say, Be not proud. Be proud neither to the little nor to the great. Hate not those who reject you, who insult you, who abuse and slander you. Hate not the atheists, the teachers of evil, the materialists—and I mean not only the good ones—for there are many good ones among them, especially in our day—hate not even the wicked ones. Remember them in your prayers thus: Save, O Lord, all those who have none to pray for them, save too all those who will not pray. And add: it is not in pride that I make this prayer, O Lord, for I am lower than all men.... Love God’s people, let not strangers draw away the flock, for if you slumber in your slothfulness and disdainful pride, or worse still, in covetousness, they will come from all sides and draw away your flock. Expound the Gospel to the people unceasingly ... be not extortionate.... Do not love gold and silver, do not hoard them.... Have faith. Cling to the banner and raise it on high.”\n\nBut the elder spoke more disconnectedly than Alyosha reported his words afterwards. Sometimes he broke off altogether, as though to take breath, and recover his strength, but he was in a sort of ecstasy. They heard him with emotion, though many wondered at his words and found them obscure.... Afterwards all remembered those words.\n\nWhen Alyosha happened for a moment to leave the cell, he was struck by the general excitement and suspense in the monks who were crowding about it. This anticipation showed itself in some by anxiety, in others by devout solemnity. All were expecting that some marvel would happen immediately after the elder’s death. Their suspense was, from one point of view, almost frivolous, but even the most austere of the monks were affected by it. Father Païssy’s face looked the gravest of all.\n\nAlyosha was mysteriously summoned by a monk to see Rakitin, who had arrived from town with a singular letter for him from Madame Hohlakov. In it she informed Alyosha of a strange and very opportune incident. It appeared that among the women who had come on the previous day to receive Father Zossima’s blessing, there had been an old woman from the town, a sergeant’s widow, called Prohorovna. She had inquired whether she might pray for the rest of the soul of her son, Vassenka, who had gone to Irkutsk, and had sent her no news for over a year. To which Father Zossima had answered sternly, forbidding her to do so, and saying that to pray for the living as though they were dead was a kind of sorcery. He afterwards forgave her on account of her ignorance, and added, “as though reading the book of the future” (this was Madame Hohlakov’s expression), words of comfort: “that her son Vassya was certainly alive and he would either come himself very shortly or send a letter, and that she was to go home and expect him.” And “Would you believe it?” exclaimed Madame Hohlakov enthusiastically, “the prophecy has been fulfilled literally indeed, and more than that.” Scarcely had the old woman reached home when they gave her a letter from Siberia which had been awaiting her. But that was not all; in the letter written on the road from Ekaterinenburg, Vassya informed his mother that he was returning to Russia with an official, and that three weeks after her receiving the letter he hoped “to embrace his mother.”\n\nMadame Hohlakov warmly entreated Alyosha to report this new “miracle of prediction” to the Superior and all the brotherhood. “All, all, ought to know of it!” she concluded. The letter had been written in haste, the excitement of the writer was apparent in every line of it. But Alyosha had no need to tell the monks, for all knew of it already. Rakitin had commissioned the monk who brought his message “to inform most respectfully his reverence Father Païssy, that he, Rakitin, has a matter to speak of with him, of such gravity that he dare not defer it for a moment, and humbly begs forgiveness for his presumption.” As the monk had given the message to Father Païssy before that to Alyosha, the latter found after reading the letter, there was nothing left for him to do but to hand it to Father Païssy in confirmation of the story.\n\nAnd even that austere and cautious man, though he frowned as he read the news of the “miracle,” could not completely restrain some inner emotion. His eyes gleamed, and a grave and solemn smile came into his lips.\n\n“We shall see greater things!” broke from him.\n\n“We shall see greater things, greater things yet!” the monks around repeated.\n\nBut Father Païssy, frowning again, begged all of them, at least for a time, not to speak of the matter “till it be more fully confirmed, seeing there is so much credulity among those of this world, and indeed this might well have chanced naturally,” he added, prudently, as it were to satisfy his conscience, though scarcely believing his own disavowal, a fact his listeners very clearly perceived.\n\nWithin the hour the “miracle” was of course known to the whole monastery, and many visitors who had come for the mass. No one seemed more impressed by it than the monk who had come the day before from St. Sylvester, from the little monastery of Obdorsk in the far North. It was he who had been standing near Madame Hohlakov the previous day and had asked Father Zossima earnestly, referring to the “healing” of the lady’s daughter, “How can you presume to do such things?”\n\nHe was now somewhat puzzled and did not know whom to believe. The evening before he had visited Father Ferapont in his cell apart, behind the apiary, and had been greatly impressed and overawed by the visit. This Father Ferapont was that aged monk so devout in fasting and observing silence who has been mentioned already, as antagonistic to Father Zossima and the whole institution of “elders,” which he regarded as a pernicious and frivolous innovation. He was a very formidable opponent, although from his practice of silence he scarcely spoke a word to any one. What made him formidable was that a number of monks fully shared his feeling, and many of the visitors looked upon him as a great saint and ascetic, although they had no doubt that he was crazy. But it was just his craziness attracted them.\n\nFather Ferapont never went to see the elder. Though he lived in the hermitage they did not worry him to keep its regulations, and this too because he behaved as though he were crazy. He was seventy-five or more, and he lived in a corner beyond the apiary in an old decaying wooden cell which had been built long ago for another great ascetic, Father Iona, who had lived to be a hundred and five, and of whose saintly doings many curious stories were still extant in the monastery and the neighborhood.\n\nFather Ferapont had succeeded in getting himself installed in this same solitary cell seven years previously. It was simply a peasant’s hut, though it looked like a chapel, for it contained an extraordinary number of ikons with lamps perpetually burning before them—which men brought to the monastery as offerings to God. Father Ferapont had been appointed to look after them and keep the lamps burning. It was said (and indeed it was true) that he ate only two pounds of bread in three days. The beekeeper, who lived close by the apiary, used to bring him the bread every three days, and even to this man who waited upon him, Father Ferapont rarely uttered a word. The four pounds of bread, together with the sacrament bread, regularly sent him on Sundays after the late mass by the Father Superior, made up his weekly rations. The water in his jug was changed every day. He rarely appeared at mass. Visitors who came to do him homage saw him sometimes kneeling all day long at prayer without looking round. If he addressed them, he was brief, abrupt, strange, and almost always rude. On very rare occasions, however, he would talk to visitors, but for the most part he would utter some one strange saying which was a complete riddle, and no entreaties would induce him to pronounce a word in explanation. He was not a priest, but a simple monk. There was a strange belief, chiefly however among the most ignorant, that Father Ferapont had communication with heavenly spirits and would only converse with them, and so was silent with men.\n\nThe monk from Obdorsk, having been directed to the apiary by the beekeeper, who was also a very silent and surly monk, went to the corner where Father Ferapont’s cell stood. “Maybe he will speak as you are a stranger and maybe you’ll get nothing out of him,” the beekeeper had warned him. The monk, as he related afterwards, approached in the utmost apprehension. It was rather late in the evening. Father Ferapont was sitting at the door of his cell on a low bench. A huge old elm was lightly rustling overhead. There was an evening freshness in the air. The monk from Obdorsk bowed down before the saint and asked his blessing.\n\n“Do you want me to bow down to you, monk?” said Father Ferapont. “Get up!”\n\nThe monk got up.\n\n“Blessing, be blessed! Sit beside me. Where have you come from?”\n\nWhat most struck the poor monk was the fact that in spite of his strict fasting and great age, Father Ferapont still looked a vigorous old man. He was tall, held himself erect, and had a thin, but fresh and healthy face. There was no doubt he still had considerable strength. He was of athletic build. In spite of his great age he was not even quite gray, and still had very thick hair and a full beard, both of which had once been black. His eyes were gray, large and luminous, but strikingly prominent. He spoke with a broad accent. He was dressed in a peasant’s long reddish coat of coarse convict cloth (as it used to be called) and had a stout rope round his waist. His throat and chest were bare. Beneath his coat, his shirt of the coarsest linen showed almost black with dirt, not having been changed for months. They said that he wore irons weighing thirty pounds under his coat. His stockingless feet were thrust in old slippers almost dropping to pieces.\n\n“From the little Obdorsk monastery, from St. Sylvester,” the monk answered humbly, whilst his keen and inquisitive, but rather frightened little eyes kept watch on the hermit.\n\n“I have been at your Sylvester’s. I used to stay there. Is Sylvester well?”\n\nThe monk hesitated.\n\n“You are a senseless lot! How do you keep the fasts?”\n\n“Our dietary is according to the ancient conventual rules. During Lent there are no meals provided for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday. For Tuesday and Thursday we have white bread, stewed fruit with honey, wild berries, or salt cabbage and wholemeal stirabout. On Saturday white cabbage soup, noodles with peas, kasha, all with hemp oil. On weekdays we have dried fish and kasha with the cabbage soup. From Monday till Saturday evening, six whole days in Holy Week, nothing is cooked, and we have only bread and water, and that sparingly; if possible not taking food every day, just the same as is ordered for first week in Lent. On Good Friday nothing is eaten. In the same way on the Saturday we have to fast till three o’clock, and then take a little bread and water and drink a single cup of wine. On Holy Thursday we drink wine and have something cooked without oil or not cooked at all, inasmuch as the Laodicean council lays down for Holy Thursday: ‘It is unseemly by remitting the fast on the Holy Thursday to dishonor the whole of Lent!’ This is how we keep the fast. But what is that compared with you, holy Father,” added the monk, growing more confident, “for all the year round, even at Easter, you take nothing but bread and water, and what we should eat in two days lasts you full seven. It’s truly marvelous—your great abstinence.”\n\n“And mushrooms?” asked Father Ferapont, suddenly.\n\n“Mushrooms?” repeated the surprised monk.\n\n“Yes. I can give up their bread, not needing it at all, and go away into the forest and live there on the mushrooms or the berries, but they can’t give up their bread here, wherefore they are in bondage to the devil. Nowadays the unclean deny that there is need of such fasting. Haughty and unclean is their judgment.”\n\n“Och, true,” sighed the monk.\n\n“And have you seen devils among them?” asked Ferapont.\n\n“Among them? Among whom?” asked the monk, timidly.\n\n“I went to the Father Superior on Trinity Sunday last year, I haven’t been since. I saw a devil sitting on one man’s chest hiding under his cassock, only his horns poked out; another had one peeping out of his pocket with such sharp eyes, he was afraid of me; another settled in the unclean belly of one, another was hanging round a man’s neck, and so he was carrying him about without seeing him.”\n\n“You—can see spirits?” the monk inquired.\n\n“I tell you I can see, I can see through them. When I was coming out from the Superior’s I saw one hiding from me behind the door, and a big one, a yard and a half or more high, with a thick long gray tail, and the tip of his tail was in the crack of the door and I was quick and slammed the door, pinching his tail in it. He squealed and began to struggle, and I made the sign of the cross over him three times. And he died on the spot like a crushed spider. He must have rotted there in the corner and be stinking, but they don’t see, they don’t smell it. It’s a year since I have been there. I reveal it to you, as you are a stranger.”\n\n“Your words are terrible! But, holy and blessed Father,” said the monk, growing bolder and bolder, “is it true, as they noise abroad even to distant lands about you, that you are in continual communication with the Holy Ghost?”\n\n“He does fly down at times.”\n\n“How does he fly down? In what form?”\n\n“As a bird.”\n\n“The Holy Ghost in the form of a dove?”\n\n“There’s the Holy Ghost and there’s the Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit can appear as other birds—sometimes as a swallow, sometimes a goldfinch and sometimes as a blue-tit.”\n\n“How do you know him from an ordinary tit?”\n\n“He speaks.”\n\n“How does he speak, in what language?”\n\n“Human language.”\n\n“And what does he tell you?”\n\n“Why, to-day he told me that a fool would visit me and would ask me unseemly questions. You want to know too much, monk.”\n\n“Terrible are your words, most holy and blessed Father,” the monk shook his head. But there was a doubtful look in his frightened little eyes.\n\n“Do you see this tree?” asked Father Ferapont, after a pause.\n\n“I do, blessed Father.”\n\n“You think it’s an elm, but for me it has another shape.”\n\n“What sort of shape?” inquired the monk, after a pause of vain expectation.\n\n“It happens at night. You see those two branches? In the night it is Christ holding out His arms to me and seeking me with those arms, I see it clearly and tremble. It’s terrible, terrible!”\n\n“What is there terrible if it’s Christ Himself?”\n\n“Why, He’ll snatch me up and carry me away.”\n\n“Alive?”\n\n“In the spirit and glory of Elijah, haven’t you heard? He will take me in His arms and bear me away.”\n\nThough the monk returned to the cell he was sharing with one of the brothers, in considerable perplexity of mind, he still cherished at heart a greater reverence for Father Ferapont than for Father Zossima. He was strongly in favor of fasting, and it was not strange that one who kept so rigid a fast as Father Ferapont should “see marvels.” His words seemed certainly queer, but God only could tell what was hidden in those words, and were not worse words and acts commonly seen in those who have sacrificed their intellects for the glory of God? The pinching of the devil’s tail he was ready and eager to believe, and not only in the figurative sense. Besides he had, before visiting the monastery, a strong prejudice against the institution of “elders,” which he only knew of by hearsay and believed to be a pernicious innovation. Before he had been long at the monastery, he had detected the secret murmurings of some shallow brothers who disliked the institution. He was, besides, a meddlesome, inquisitive man, who poked his nose into everything. This was why the news of the fresh “miracle” performed by Father Zossima reduced him to extreme perplexity. Alyosha remembered afterwards how their inquisitive guest from Obdorsk had been continually flitting to and fro from one group to another, listening and asking questions among the monks that were crowding within and without the elder’s cell. But he did not pay much attention to him at the time, and only recollected it afterwards.\n\nHe had no thought to spare for it indeed, for when Father Zossima, feeling tired again, had gone back to bed, he thought of Alyosha as he was closing his eyes, and sent for him. Alyosha ran at once. There was no one else in the cell but Father Païssy, Father Iosif, and the novice Porfiry. The elder, opening his weary eyes and looking intently at Alyosha, asked him suddenly:\n\n“Are your people expecting you, my son?”\n\nAlyosha hesitated.\n\n“Haven’t they need of you? Didn’t you promise some one yesterday to see them to-day?”\n\n“I did promise—to my father—my brothers—others too.”\n\n“You see, you must go. Don’t grieve. Be sure I shall not die without your being by to hear my last word. To you I will say that word, my son, it will be my last gift to you. To you, dear son, because you love me. But now go to keep your promise.”\n\nAlyosha immediately obeyed, though it was hard to go. But the promise that he should hear his last word on earth, that it should be the last gift to him, Alyosha, sent a thrill of rapture through his soul. He made haste that he might finish what he had to do in the town and return quickly. Father Païssy, too, uttered some words of exhortation which moved and surprised him greatly. He spoke as they left the cell together.\n\n“Remember, young man, unceasingly,” Father Païssy began, without preface, “that the science of this world, which has become a great power, has, especially in the last century, analyzed everything divine handed down to us in the holy books. After this cruel analysis the learned of this world have nothing left of all that was sacred of old. But they have only analyzed the parts and overlooked the whole, and indeed their blindness is marvelous. Yet the whole still stands steadfast before their eyes, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. Has it not lasted nineteen centuries, is it not still a living, a moving power in the individual soul and in the masses of people? It is still as strong and living even in the souls of atheists, who have destroyed everything! For even those who have renounced Christianity and attack it, in their inmost being still follow the Christian ideal, for hitherto neither their subtlety nor the ardor of their hearts has been able to create a higher ideal of man and of virtue than the ideal given by Christ of old. When it has been attempted, the result has been only grotesque. Remember this especially, young man, since you are being sent into the world by your departing elder. Maybe, remembering this great day, you will not forget my words, uttered from the heart for your guidance, seeing you are young, and the temptations of the world are great and beyond your strength to endure. Well, now go, my orphan.”\n\nWith these words Father Païssy blessed him. As Alyosha left the monastery and thought them over, he suddenly realized that he had met a new and unexpected friend, a warmly loving teacher, in this austere monk who had hitherto treated him sternly. It was as though Father Zossima had bequeathed him to him at his death, and “perhaps that’s just what had passed between them,” Alyosha thought suddenly. The philosophic reflections he had just heard so unexpectedly testified to the warmth of Father Païssy’s heart. He was in haste to arm the boy’s mind for conflict with temptation and to guard the young soul left in his charge with the strongest defense he could imagine.\n\n# Chapter II. At His Father’s\n\nFirst of all, Alyosha went to his father. On the way he remembered that his father had insisted the day before that he should come without his brother Ivan seeing him. “Why so?” Alyosha wondered suddenly. “Even if my father has something to say to me alone, why should I go in unseen? Most likely in his excitement yesterday he meant to say something different,” he decided. Yet he was very glad when Marfa Ignatyevna, who opened the garden gate to him (Grigory, it appeared, was ill in bed in the lodge), told him in answer to his question that Ivan Fyodorovitch had gone out two hours ago.\n\n“And my father?”\n\n“He is up, taking his coffee,” Marfa answered somewhat dryly.\n\nAlyosha went in. The old man was sitting alone at the table wearing slippers and a little old overcoat. He was amusing himself by looking through some accounts, rather inattentively however. He was quite alone in the house, for Smerdyakov too had gone out marketing. Though he had got up early and was trying to put a bold face on it, he looked tired and weak. His forehead, upon which huge purple bruises had come out during the night, was bandaged with a red handkerchief; his nose too had swollen terribly in the night, and some smaller bruises covered it in patches, giving his whole face a peculiarly spiteful and irritable look. The old man was aware of this, and turned a hostile glance on Alyosha as he came in.\n\n“The coffee is cold,” he cried harshly; “I won’t offer you any. I’ve ordered nothing but a Lenten fish soup to-day, and I don’t invite any one to share it. Why have you come?”\n\n“To find out how you are,” said Alyosha.\n\n“Yes. Besides, I told you to come yesterday. It’s all of no consequence. You need not have troubled. But I knew you’d come poking in directly.”\n\nHe said this with almost hostile feeling. At the same time he got up and looked anxiously in the looking-glass (perhaps for the fortieth time that morning) at his nose. He began, too, binding his red handkerchief more becomingly on his forehead.\n\n“Red’s better. It’s just like the hospital in a white one,” he observed sententiously. “Well, how are things over there? How is your elder?”\n\n“He is very bad; he may die to-day,” answered Alyosha. But his father had not listened, and had forgotten his own question at once.\n\n“Ivan’s gone out,” he said suddenly. “He is doing his utmost to carry off Mitya’s betrothed. That’s what he is staying here for,” he added maliciously, and, twisting his mouth, looked at Alyosha.\n\n“Surely he did not tell you so?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, he did, long ago. Would you believe it, he told me three weeks ago? You don’t suppose he too came to murder me, do you? He must have had some object in coming.”\n\n“What do you mean? Why do you say such things?” said Alyosha, troubled.\n\n“He doesn’t ask for money, it’s true, but yet he won’t get a farthing from me. I intend living as long as possible, you may as well know, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, and so I need every farthing, and the longer I live, the more I shall need it,” he continued, pacing from one corner of the room to the other, keeping his hands in the pockets of his loose greasy overcoat made of yellow cotton material. “I can still pass for a man at five and fifty, but I want to pass for one for another twenty years. As I get older, you know, I shan’t be a pretty object. The wenches won’t come to me of their own accord, so I shall want my money. So I am saving up more and more, simply for myself, my dear son Alexey Fyodorovitch. You may as well know. For I mean to go on in my sins to the end, let me tell you. For sin is sweet; all abuse it, but all men live in it, only others do it on the sly, and I openly. And so all the other sinners fall upon me for being so simple. And your paradise, Alexey Fyodorovitch, is not to my taste, let me tell you that; and it’s not the proper place for a gentleman, your paradise, even if it exists. I believe that I fall asleep and don’t wake up again, and that’s all. You can pray for my soul if you like. And if you don’t want to, don’t, damn you! That’s my philosophy. Ivan talked well here yesterday, though we were all drunk. Ivan is a conceited coxcomb, but he has no particular learning ... nor education either. He sits silent and smiles at one without speaking—that’s what pulls him through.”\n\nAlyosha listened to him in silence.\n\n“Why won’t he talk to me? If he does speak, he gives himself airs. Your Ivan is a scoundrel! And I’ll marry Grushenka in a minute if I want to. For if you’ve money, Alexey Fyodorovitch, you have only to want a thing and you can have it. That’s what Ivan is afraid of, he is on the watch to prevent me getting married and that’s why he is egging on Mitya to marry Grushenka himself. He hopes to keep me from Grushenka by that (as though I should leave him my money if I don’t marry her!). Besides if Mitya marries Grushenka, Ivan will carry off his rich betrothed, that’s what he’s reckoning on! He is a scoundrel, your Ivan!”\n\n“How cross you are! It’s because of yesterday; you had better lie down,” said Alyosha.\n\n“There! you say that,” the old man observed suddenly, as though it had struck him for the first time, “and I am not angry with you. But if Ivan said it, I should be angry with him. It is only with you I have good moments, else you know I am an ill-natured man.”\n\n“You are not ill-natured, but distorted,” said Alyosha with a smile.\n\n“Listen. I meant this morning to get that ruffian Mitya locked up and I don’t know now what I shall decide about it. Of course in these fashionable days fathers and mothers are looked upon as a prejudice, but even now the law does not allow you to drag your old father about by the hair, to kick him in the face in his own house, and brag of murdering him outright—all in the presence of witnesses. If I liked, I could crush him and could have him locked up at once for what he did yesterday.”\n\n“Then you don’t mean to take proceedings?”\n\n“Ivan has dissuaded me. I shouldn’t care about Ivan, but there’s another thing.”\n\nAnd bending down to Alyosha, he went on in a confidential half-whisper.\n\n“If I send the ruffian to prison, she’ll hear of it and run to see him at once. But if she hears that he has beaten me, a weak old man, within an inch of my life, she may give him up and come to me.... For that’s her way, everything by contraries. I know her through and through! Won’t you have a drop of brandy? Take some cold coffee and I’ll pour a quarter of a glass of brandy into it, it’s delicious, my boy.”\n\n“No, thank you. I’ll take that roll with me if I may,” said Alyosha, and taking a halfpenny French roll he put it in the pocket of his cassock. “And you’d better not have brandy, either,” he suggested apprehensively, looking into the old man’s face.\n\n“You are quite right, it irritates my nerves instead of soothing them. Only one little glass. I’ll get it out of the cupboard.”\n\nHe unlocked the cupboard, poured out a glass, drank it, then locked the cupboard and put the key back in his pocket.\n\n“That’s enough. One glass won’t kill me.”\n\n“You see you are in a better humor now,” said Alyosha, smiling.\n\n“Um! I love you even without the brandy, but with scoundrels I am a scoundrel. Ivan is not going to Tchermashnya—why is that? He wants to spy how much I give Grushenka if she comes. They are all scoundrels! But I don’t recognize Ivan, I don’t know him at all. Where does he come from? He is not one of us in soul. As though I’d leave him anything! I shan’t leave a will at all, you may as well know. And I’ll crush Mitya like a beetle. I squash black-beetles at night with my slipper; they squelch when you tread on them. And your Mitya will squelch too. Your Mitya, for you love him. Yes, you love him and I am not afraid of your loving him. But if Ivan loved him I should be afraid for myself at his loving him. But Ivan loves nobody. Ivan is not one of us. People like Ivan are not our sort, my boy. They are like a cloud of dust. When the wind blows, the dust will be gone.... I had a silly idea in my head when I told you to come to-day; I wanted to find out from you about Mitya. If I were to hand him over a thousand or maybe two now, would the beggarly wretch agree to take himself off altogether for five years or, better still, thirty-five, and without Grushenka, and give her up once for all, eh?”\n\n“I—I’ll ask him,” muttered Alyosha. “If you would give him three thousand, perhaps he—”\n\n“That’s nonsense! You needn’t ask him now, no need! I’ve changed my mind. It was a nonsensical idea of mine. I won’t give him anything, not a penny, I want my money myself,” cried the old man, waving his hand. “I’ll crush him like a beetle without it. Don’t say anything to him or else he will begin hoping. There’s nothing for you to do here, you needn’t stay. Is that betrothed of his, Katerina Ivanovna, whom he has kept so carefully hidden from me all this time, going to marry him or not? You went to see her yesterday, I believe?”\n\n“Nothing will induce her to abandon him.”\n\n“There you see how dearly these fine young ladies love a rake and a scoundrel. They are poor creatures I tell you, those pale young ladies, very different from—Ah, if I had his youth and the looks I had then (for I was better-looking than he at eight and twenty) I’d have been a conquering hero just as he is. He is a low cad! But he shan’t have Grushenka, anyway, he shan’t! I’ll crush him!”\n\nHis anger had returned with the last words.\n\n“You can go. There’s nothing for you to do here to-day,” he snapped harshly.\n\nAlyosha went up to say good-by to him, and kissed him on the shoulder.\n\n“What’s that for?” The old man was a little surprised. “We shall see each other again, or do you think we shan’t?”\n\n“Not at all, I didn’t mean anything.”\n\n“Nor did I, I did not mean anything,” said the old man, looking at him. “Listen, listen,” he shouted after him, “make haste and come again and I’ll have a fish soup for you, a fine one, not like to-day. Be sure to come! Come to-morrow, do you hear, to-morrow!”\n\nAnd as soon as Alyosha had gone out of the door, he went to the cupboard again and poured out another half-glass.\n\n“I won’t have more!” he muttered, clearing his throat, and again he locked the cupboard and put the key in his pocket. Then he went into his bedroom, lay down on the bed, exhausted, and in one minute he was asleep.\n\n# Chapter III. A Meeting With The Schoolboys\n\n“Thank goodness he did not ask me about Grushenka,” thought Alyosha, as he left his father’s house and turned towards Madame Hohlakov’s, “or I might have to tell him of my meeting with Grushenka yesterday.”\n\nAlyosha felt painfully that since yesterday both combatants had renewed their energies, and that their hearts had grown hard again. “Father is spiteful and angry, he’s made some plan and will stick to it. And what of Dmitri? He too will be harder than yesterday, he too must be spiteful and angry, and he too, no doubt, has made some plan. Oh, I must succeed in finding him to-day, whatever happens.”\n\nBut Alyosha had not long to meditate. An incident occurred on the road, which, though apparently of little consequence, made a great impression on him. Just after he had crossed the square and turned the corner coming out into Mihailovsky Street, which is divided by a small ditch from the High Street (our whole town is intersected by ditches), he saw a group of schoolboys between the ages of nine and twelve, at the bridge. They were going home from school, some with their bags on their shoulders, others with leather satchels slung across them, some in short jackets, others in little overcoats. Some even had those high boots with creases round the ankles, such as little boys spoilt by rich fathers love to wear. The whole group was talking eagerly about something, apparently holding a council. Alyosha had never from his Moscow days been able to pass children without taking notice of them, and although he was particularly fond of children of three or thereabout, he liked schoolboys of ten and eleven too. And so, anxious as he was to-day, he wanted at once to turn aside to talk to them. He looked into their excited rosy faces, and noticed at once that all the boys had stones in their hands. Behind the ditch some thirty paces away, there was another schoolboy standing by a fence. He too had a satchel at his side. He was about ten years old, pale, delicate-looking and with sparkling black eyes. He kept an attentive and anxious watch on the other six, obviously his schoolfellows with whom he had just come out of school, but with whom he had evidently had a feud.\n\nAlyosha went up and, addressing a fair, curly-headed, rosy boy in a black jacket, observed:\n\n“When I used to wear a satchel like yours, I always used to carry it on my left side, so as to have my right hand free, but you’ve got yours on your right side. So it will be awkward for you to get at it.”\n\nAlyosha had no art or premeditation in beginning with this practical remark. But it is the only way for a grown-up person to get at once into confidential relations with a child, or still more with a group of children. One must begin in a serious, businesslike way so as to be on a perfectly equal footing. Alyosha understood it by instinct.\n\n“But he is left-handed,” another, a fine healthy-looking boy of eleven, answered promptly. All the others stared at Alyosha.\n\n“He even throws stones with his left hand,” observed a third.\n\nAt that instant a stone flew into the group, but only just grazed the left-handed boy, though it was well and vigorously thrown by the boy standing the other side of the ditch.\n\n“Give it him, hit him back, Smurov,” they all shouted. But Smurov, the left-handed boy, needed no telling, and at once revenged himself; he threw a stone, but it missed the boy and hit the ground. The boy the other side of the ditch, the pocket of whose coat was visibly bulging with stones, flung another stone at the group; this time it flew straight at Alyosha and hit him painfully on the shoulder.\n\n“He aimed it at you, he meant it for you. You are Karamazov, Karamazov!” the boys shouted, laughing. “Come, all throw at him at once!” and six stones flew at the boy. One struck the boy on the head and he fell down, but at once leapt up and began ferociously returning their fire. Both sides threw stones incessantly. Many of the group had their pockets full too.\n\n“What are you about! Aren’t you ashamed? Six against one! Why, you’ll kill him,” cried Alyosha.\n\nHe ran forward and met the flying stones to screen the solitary boy. Three or four ceased throwing for a minute.\n\n“He began first!” cried a boy in a red shirt in an angry childish voice. “He is a beast, he stabbed Krassotkin in class the other day with a penknife. It bled. Krassotkin wouldn’t tell tales, but he must be thrashed.”\n\n“But what for? I suppose you tease him.”\n\n“There, he sent a stone in your back again, he knows you,” cried the children. “It’s you he is throwing at now, not us. Come, all of you, at him again, don’t miss, Smurov!” and again a fire of stones, and a very vicious one, began. The boy the other side of the ditch was hit in the chest; he screamed, began to cry and ran away uphill towards Mihailovsky Street. They all shouted: “Aha, he is funking, he is running away. Wisp of tow!”\n\n“You don’t know what a beast he is, Karamazov, killing is too good for him,” said the boy in the jacket, with flashing eyes. He seemed to be the eldest.\n\n“What’s wrong with him?” asked Alyosha, “is he a tell-tale or what?”\n\nThe boys looked at one another as though derisively.\n\n“Are you going that way, to Mihailovsky?” the same boy went on. “Catch him up.... You see he’s stopped again, he is waiting and looking at you.”\n\n“He is looking at you,” the other boys chimed in.\n\n“You ask him, does he like a disheveled wisp of tow. Do you hear, ask him that!”\n\nThere was a general burst of laughter. Alyosha looked at them, and they at him.\n\n“Don’t go near him, he’ll hurt you,” cried Smurov in a warning voice.\n\n“I shan’t ask him about the wisp of tow, for I expect you tease him with that question somehow. But I’ll find out from him why you hate him so.”\n\n“Find out then, find out,” cried the boys, laughing.\n\nAlyosha crossed the bridge and walked uphill by the fence, straight towards the boy.\n\n“You’d better look out,” the boys called after him; “he won’t be afraid of you. He will stab you in a minute, on the sly, as he did Krassotkin.”\n\nThe boy waited for him without budging. Coming up to him, Alyosha saw facing him a child of about nine years old. He was an undersized weakly boy with a thin pale face, with large dark eyes that gazed at him vindictively. He was dressed in a rather shabby old overcoat, which he had monstrously outgrown. His bare arms stuck out beyond his sleeves. There was a large patch on the right knee of his trousers, and in his right boot just at the toe there was a big hole in the leather, carefully blackened with ink. Both the pockets of his great-coat were weighed down with stones. Alyosha stopped two steps in front of him, looking inquiringly at him. The boy, seeing at once from Alyosha’s eyes that he wouldn’t beat him, became less defiant, and addressed him first.\n\n“I am alone, and there are six of them. I’ll beat them all, alone!” he said suddenly, with flashing eyes.\n\n“I think one of the stones must have hurt you badly,” observed Alyosha.\n\n“But I hit Smurov on the head!” cried the boy.\n\n“They told me that you know me, and that you threw a stone at me on purpose,” said Alyosha.\n\nThe boy looked darkly at him.\n\n“I don’t know you. Do you know me?” Alyosha continued.\n\n“Let me alone!” the boy cried irritably; but he did not move, as though he were expecting something, and again there was a vindictive light in his eyes.\n\n“Very well, I am going,” said Alyosha; “only I don’t know you and I don’t tease you. They told me how they tease you, but I don’t want to tease you. Good-by!”\n\n“Monk in silk trousers!” cried the boy, following Alyosha with the same vindictive and defiant expression, and he threw himself into an attitude of defense, feeling sure that now Alyosha would fall upon him; but Alyosha turned, looked at him, and walked away. He had not gone three steps before the biggest stone the boy had in his pocket hit him a painful blow in the back.\n\n“So you’ll hit a man from behind! They tell the truth, then, when they say that you attack on the sly,” said Alyosha, turning round again. This time the boy threw a stone savagely right into Alyosha’s face; but Alyosha just had time to guard himself, and the stone struck him on the elbow.\n\n“Aren’t you ashamed? What have I done to you?” he cried.\n\nThe boy waited in silent defiance, certain that now Alyosha would attack him. Seeing that even now he would not, his rage was like a little wild beast’s; he flew at Alyosha himself, and before Alyosha had time to move, the spiteful child had seized his left hand with both of his and bit his middle finger. He fixed his teeth in it and it was ten seconds before he let go. Alyosha cried out with pain and pulled his finger away with all his might. The child let go at last and retreated to his former distance. Alyosha’s finger had been badly bitten to the bone, close to the nail; it began to bleed. Alyosha took out his handkerchief and bound it tightly round his injured hand. He was a full minute bandaging it. The boy stood waiting all the time. At last Alyosha raised his gentle eyes and looked at him.\n\n“Very well,” he said, “you see how badly you’ve bitten me. That’s enough, isn’t it? Now tell me, what have I done to you?”\n\nThe boy stared in amazement.\n\n“Though I don’t know you and it’s the first time I’ve seen you,” Alyosha went on with the same serenity, “yet I must have done something to you—you wouldn’t have hurt me like this for nothing. So what have I done? How have I wronged you, tell me?”\n\nInstead of answering, the boy broke into a loud tearful wail and ran away. Alyosha walked slowly after him towards Mihailovsky Street, and for a long time he saw the child running in the distance as fast as ever, not turning his head, and no doubt still keeping up his tearful wail. He made up his mind to find him out as soon as he had time, and to solve this mystery. Just now he had not the time.\n\n# Chapter IV. At The Hohlakovs’\n\nAlyosha soon reached Madame Hohlakov’s house, a handsome stone house of two stories, one of the finest in our town. Though Madame Hohlakov spent most of her time in another province where she had an estate, or in Moscow, where she had a house of her own, yet she had a house in our town too, inherited from her forefathers. The estate in our district was the largest of her three estates, yet she had been very little in our province before this time. She ran out to Alyosha in the hall.\n\n“Did you get my letter about the new miracle?” She spoke rapidly and nervously.\n\n“Yes.”\n“Did you show it to every one? He restored the son to his mother!”\n\n“He is dying to-day,” said Alyosha.\n\n“I have heard, I know, oh, how I long to talk to you, to you or some one, about all this. No, to you, to you! And how sorry I am I can’t see him! The whole town is in excitement, they are all suspense. But now—do you know Katerina Ivanovna is here now?”\n\n“Ah, that’s lucky,” cried Alyosha. “Then I shall see her here. She told me yesterday to be sure to come and see her to-day.”\n\n“I know, I know all. I’ve heard exactly what happened yesterday—and the atrocious behavior of that—creature. C’est tragique, and if I’d been in her place I don’t know what I should have done. And your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch, what do you think of him?—my goodness! Alexey Fyodorovitch, I am forgetting, only fancy; your brother is in there with her, not that dreadful brother who was so shocking yesterday, but the other, Ivan Fyodorovitch, he is sitting with her talking; they are having a serious conversation. If you could only imagine what’s passing between them now—it’s awful, I tell you it’s lacerating, it’s like some incredible tale of horror. They are ruining their lives for no reason any one can see. They both recognize it and revel in it. I’ve been watching for you! I’ve been thirsting for you! It’s too much for me, that’s the worst of it. I’ll tell you all about it presently, but now I must speak of something else, the most important thing—I had quite forgotten what’s most important. Tell me, why has Lise been in hysterics? As soon as she heard you were here, she began to be hysterical!”\n\n“Maman, it’s you who are hysterical now, not I,” Lise’s voice caroled through a tiny crack of the door at the side. Her voice sounded as though she wanted to laugh, but was doing her utmost to control it. Alyosha at once noticed the crack, and no doubt Lise was peeping through it, but that he could not see.\n\n“And no wonder, Lise, no wonder ... your caprices will make me hysterical too. But she is so ill, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she has been so ill all night, feverish and moaning! I could hardly wait for the morning and for Herzenstube to come. He says that he can make nothing of it, that we must wait. Herzenstube always comes and says that he can make nothing of it. As soon as you approached the house, she screamed, fell into hysterics, and insisted on being wheeled back into this room here.”\n\n“Mamma, I didn’t know he had come. It wasn’t on his account I wanted to be wheeled into this room.”\n\n“That’s not true, Lise, Yulia ran to tell you that Alexey Fyodorovitch was coming. She was on the look-out for you.”\n\n“My darling mamma, it’s not at all clever of you. But if you want to make up for it and say something very clever, dear mamma, you’d better tell our honored visitor, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that he has shown his want of wit by venturing to us after what happened yesterday and although every one is laughing at him.”\n\n“Lise, you go too far. I declare I shall have to be severe. Who laughs at him? I am so glad he has come, I need him, I can’t do without him. Oh, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I am exceedingly unhappy!”\n\n“But what’s the matter with you, mamma, darling?”\n\n“Ah, your caprices, Lise, your fidgetiness, your illness, that awful night of fever, that awful everlasting Herzenstube, everlasting, everlasting, that’s the worst of it! Everything, in fact, everything.... Even that miracle, too! Oh, how it has upset me, how it has shattered me, that miracle, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch! And that tragedy in the drawing-room, it’s more than I can bear, I warn you. I can’t bear it. A comedy, perhaps, not a tragedy. Tell me, will Father Zossima live till to-morrow, will he? Oh, my God! What is happening to me? Every minute I close my eyes and see that it’s all nonsense, all nonsense.”\n\n“I should be very grateful,” Alyosha interrupted suddenly, “if you could give me a clean rag to bind up my finger with. I have hurt it, and it’s very painful.”\n\nAlyosha unbound his bitten finger. The handkerchief was soaked with blood. Madame Hohlakov screamed and shut her eyes.\n\n“Good heavens, what a wound, how awful!”\n\nBut as soon as Lise saw Alyosha’s finger through the crack, she flung the door wide open.\n\n“Come, come here,” she cried, imperiously. “No nonsense now! Good heavens, why did you stand there saying nothing about it all this time? He might have bled to death, mamma! How did you do it? Water, water! You must wash it first of all, simply hold it in cold water to stop the pain, and keep it there, keep it there.... Make haste, mamma, some water in a slop-basin. But do make haste,” she finished nervously. She was quite frightened at the sight of Alyosha’s wound.\n\n“Shouldn’t we send for Herzenstube?” cried Madame Hohlakov.\n\n“Mamma, you’ll be the death of me. Your Herzenstube will come and say that he can make nothing of it! Water, water! Mamma, for goodness’ sake go yourself and hurry Yulia, she is such a slowcoach and never can come quickly! Make haste, mamma, or I shall die.”\n\n“Why, it’s nothing much,” cried Alyosha, frightened at this alarm.\n\nYulia ran in with water and Alyosha put his finger in it.\n\n“Some lint, mamma, for mercy’s sake, bring some lint and that muddy caustic lotion for wounds, what’s it called? We’ve got some. You know where the bottle is, mamma; it’s in your bedroom in the right-hand cupboard, there’s a big bottle of it there with the lint.”\n\n“I’ll bring everything in a minute, Lise, only don’t scream and don’t fuss. You see how bravely Alexey Fyodorovitch bears it. Where did you get such a dreadful wound, Alexey Fyodorovitch?”\n\nMadame Hohlakov hastened away. This was all Lise was waiting for.\n\n“First of all, answer the question, where did you get hurt like this?” she asked Alyosha, quickly. “And then I’ll talk to you about something quite different. Well?”\n\nInstinctively feeling that the time of her mother’s absence was precious for her, Alyosha hastened to tell her of his enigmatic meeting with the schoolboys in the fewest words possible. Lise clasped her hands at his story.\n\n“How can you, and in that dress too, associate with schoolboys?” she cried angrily, as though she had a right to control him. “You are nothing but a boy yourself if you can do that, a perfect boy! But you must find out for me about that horrid boy and tell me all about it, for there’s some mystery in it. Now for the second thing, but first a question: does the pain prevent you talking about utterly unimportant things, but talking sensibly?”\n\n“Of course not, and I don’t feel much pain now.”\n\n“That’s because your finger is in the water. It must be changed directly, for it will get warm in a minute. Yulia, bring some ice from the cellar and another basin of water. Now she is gone, I can speak; will you give me the letter I sent you yesterday, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch—be quick, for mamma will be back in a minute and I don’t want—”\n\n“I haven’t got the letter.”\n\n“That’s not true, you have. I knew you would say that. You’ve got it in that pocket. I’ve been regretting that joke all night. Give me back the letter at once, give it me.”\n\n“I’ve left it at home.”\n\n“But you can’t consider me as a child, a little girl, after that silly joke! I beg your pardon for that silliness, but you must bring me the letter, if you really haven’t got it—bring it to-day, you must, you must.”\n\n“To-day I can’t possibly, for I am going back to the monastery and I shan’t come and see you for the next two days—three or four perhaps—for Father Zossima—”\n\n“Four days, what nonsense! Listen. Did you laugh at me very much?”\n\n“I didn’t laugh at all.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because I believed all you said.”\n\n“You are insulting me!”\n\n“Not at all. As soon as I read it, I thought that all that would come to pass, for as soon as Father Zossima dies, I am to leave the monastery. Then I shall go back and finish my studies, and when you reach the legal age we will be married. I shall love you. Though I haven’t had time to think about it, I believe I couldn’t find a better wife than you, and Father Zossima tells me I must marry.”\n\n“But I am a cripple, wheeled about in a chair,” laughed Lise, flushing crimson.\n\n“I’ll wheel you about myself, but I’m sure you’ll get well by then.”\n\n“But you are mad,” said Lise, nervously, “to make all this nonsense out of a joke! Here’s mamma, very à propos, perhaps. Mamma, how slow you always are, how can you be so long! And here’s Yulia with the ice!”\n\n“Oh, Lise, don’t scream, above all things don’t scream. That scream drives me ... How can I help it when you put the lint in another place? I’ve been hunting and hunting—I do believe you did it on purpose.”\n\n“But I couldn’t tell that he would come with a bad finger, or else perhaps I might have done it on purpose. My darling mamma, you begin to say really witty things.”\n\n“Never mind my being witty, but I must say you show nice feeling for Alexey Fyodorovitch’s sufferings! Oh, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, what’s killing me is no one thing in particular, not Herzenstube, but everything together, that’s what is too much for me.”\n\n“That’s enough, mamma, enough about Herzenstube,” Lise laughed gayly. “Make haste with the lint and the lotion, mamma. That’s simply Goulard’s water, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I remember the name now, but it’s a splendid lotion. Would you believe it, mamma, on the way here he had a fight with the boys in the street, and it was a boy bit his finger, isn’t he a child, a child himself? Is he fit to be married after that? For only fancy, he wants to be married, mamma. Just think of him married, wouldn’t it be funny, wouldn’t it be awful?”\n\nAnd Lise kept laughing her thin hysterical giggle, looking slyly at Alyosha.\n\n“But why married, Lise? What makes you talk of such a thing? It’s quite out of place—and perhaps the boy was rabid.”\n\n“Why, mamma! As though there were rabid boys!”\n\n“Why not, Lise, as though I had said something stupid! Your boy might have been bitten by a mad dog and he would become mad and bite any one near him. How well she has bandaged it, Alexey Fyodorovitch! I couldn’t have done it. Do you still feel the pain?”\n\n“It’s nothing much now.”\n\n“You don’t feel afraid of water?” asked Lise.\n\n“Come, that’s enough, Lise, perhaps I really was rather too quick talking of the boy being rabid, and you pounced upon it at once Katerina Ivanovna has only just heard that you are here, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she simply rushed at me, she’s dying to see you, dying!”\n\n“Ach, mamma, go to them yourself. He can’t go just now, he is in too much pain.”\n\n“Not at all, I can go quite well,” said Alyosha.\n\n“What! You are going away? Is that what you say?”\n\n“Well, when I’ve seen them, I’ll come back here and we can talk as much as you like. But I should like to see Katerina Ivanovna at once, for I am very anxious to be back at the monastery as soon as I can.”\n\n“Mamma, take him away quickly. Alexey Fyodorovitch, don’t trouble to come and see me afterwards, but go straight back to your monastery and a good riddance. I want to sleep, I didn’t sleep all night.”\n\n“Ah, Lise, you are only making fun, but how I wish you would sleep!” cried Madame Hohlakov.\n\n“I don’t know what I’ve done.... I’ll stay another three minutes, five if you like,” muttered Alyosha.\n\n“Even five! Do take him away quickly, mamma, he is a monster.”\n\n“Lise, you are crazy. Let us go, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she is too capricious to-day. I am afraid to cross her. Oh, the trouble one has with nervous girls! Perhaps she really will be able to sleep after seeing you. How quickly you have made her sleepy, and how fortunate it is!”\n\n“Ah, mamma, how sweetly you talk! I must kiss you for it, mamma.”\n\n“And I kiss you too, Lise. Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” Madame Hohlakov began mysteriously and importantly, speaking in a rapid whisper. “I don’t want to suggest anything, I don’t want to lift the veil, you will see for yourself what’s going on. It’s appalling. It’s the most fantastic farce. She loves your brother, Ivan, and she is doing her utmost to persuade herself she loves your brother, Dmitri. It’s appalling! I’ll go in with you, and if they don’t turn me out, I’ll stay to the end.”\n\n# Chapter V. A Laceration In The Drawing-Room\n\nBut in the drawing-room the conversation was already over. Katerina Ivanovna was greatly excited, though she looked resolute. At the moment Alyosha and Madame Hohlakov entered, Ivan Fyodorovitch stood up to take leave. His face was rather pale, and Alyosha looked at him anxiously. For this moment was to solve a doubt, a harassing enigma which had for some time haunted Alyosha. During the preceding month it had been several times suggested to him that his brother Ivan was in love with Katerina Ivanovna, and, what was more, that he meant “to carry her off” from Dmitri. Until quite lately the idea seemed to Alyosha monstrous, though it worried him extremely. He loved both his brothers, and dreaded such rivalry between them. Meantime, Dmitri had said outright on the previous day that he was glad that Ivan was his rival, and that it was a great assistance to him, Dmitri. In what way did it assist him? To marry Grushenka? But that Alyosha considered the worst thing possible. Besides all this, Alyosha had till the evening before implicitly believed that Katerina Ivanovna had a steadfast and passionate love for Dmitri; but he had only believed it till the evening before. He had fancied, too, that she was incapable of loving a man like Ivan, and that she did love Dmitri, and loved him just as he was, in spite of all the strangeness of such a passion.\n\nBut during yesterday’s scene with Grushenka another idea had struck him. The word “lacerating,” which Madame Hohlakov had just uttered, almost made him start, because half waking up towards daybreak that night he had cried out “Laceration, laceration,” probably applying it to his dream. He had been dreaming all night of the previous day’s scene at Katerina Ivanovna’s. Now Alyosha was impressed by Madame Hohlakov’s blunt and persistent assertion that Katerina Ivanovna was in love with Ivan, and only deceived herself through some sort of pose, from “self-laceration,” and tortured herself by her pretended love for Dmitri from some fancied duty of gratitude. “Yes,” he thought, “perhaps the whole truth lies in those words.” But in that case what was Ivan’s position? Alyosha felt instinctively that a character like Katerina Ivanovna’s must dominate, and she could only dominate some one like Dmitri, and never a man like Ivan. For Dmitri might at last submit to her domination “to his own happiness” (which was what Alyosha would have desired), but Ivan—no, Ivan could not submit to her, and such submission would not give him happiness. Alyosha could not help believing that of Ivan. And now all these doubts and reflections flitted through his mind as he entered the drawing-room. Another idea, too, forced itself upon him: “What if she loved neither of them—neither Ivan nor Dmitri?”\n\nIt must be noted that Alyosha felt as it were ashamed of his own thoughts and blamed himself when they kept recurring to him during the last month. “What do I know about love and women and how can I decide such questions?” he thought reproachfully, after such doubts and surmises. And yet it was impossible not to think about it. He felt instinctively that this rivalry was of immense importance in his brothers’ lives and that a great deal depended upon it.\n\n“One reptile will devour the other,” Ivan had pronounced the day before, speaking in anger of his father and Dmitri. So Ivan looked upon Dmitri as a reptile, and perhaps had long done so. Was it perhaps since he had known Katerina Ivanovna? That phrase had, of course, escaped Ivan unawares yesterday, but that only made it more important. If he felt like that, what chance was there of peace? Were there not, on the contrary, new grounds for hatred and hostility in their family? And with which of them was Alyosha to sympathize? And what was he to wish for each of them? He loved them both, but what could he desire for each in the midst of these conflicting interests? He might go quite astray in this maze, and Alyosha’s heart could not endure uncertainty, because his love was always of an active character. He was incapable of passive love. If he loved any one, he set to work at once to help him. And to do so he must know what he was aiming at; he must know for certain what was best for each, and having ascertained this it was natural for him to help them both. But instead of a definite aim, he found nothing but uncertainty and perplexity on all sides. “It was lacerating,” as was said just now. But what could he understand even in this “laceration”? He did not understand the first word in this perplexing maze.\n\nSeeing Alyosha, Katerina Ivanovna said quickly and joyfully to Ivan, who had already got up to go, “A minute! Stay another minute! I want to hear the opinion of this person here whom I trust absolutely. Don’t go away,” she added, addressing Madame Hohlakov. She made Alyosha sit down beside her, and Madame Hohlakov sat opposite, by Ivan.\n\n“You are all my friends here, all I have in the world, my dear friends,” she began warmly, in a voice which quivered with genuine tears of suffering, and Alyosha’s heart warmed to her at once. “You, Alexey Fyodorovitch, were witness yesterday of that abominable scene, and saw what I did. You did not see it, Ivan Fyodorovitch, he did. What he thought of me yesterday I don’t know. I only know one thing, that if it were repeated to-day, this minute, I should express the same feelings again as yesterday—the same feelings, the same words, the same actions. You remember my actions, Alexey Fyodorovitch; you checked me in one of them” ... (as she said that, she flushed and her eyes shone). “I must tell you that I can’t get over it. Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch. I don’t even know whether I still love him. I feel pity for him, and that is a poor sign of love. If I loved him, if I still loved him, perhaps I shouldn’t be sorry for him now, but should hate him.”\n\nHer voice quivered, and tears glittered on her eyelashes. Alyosha shuddered inwardly. “That girl is truthful and sincere,” he thought, “and she does not love Dmitri any more.”\n\n“That’s true, that’s true,” cried Madame Hohlakov.\n\n“Wait, dear. I haven’t told you the chief, the final decision I came to during the night. I feel that perhaps my decision is a terrible one—for me, but I foresee that nothing will induce me to change it—nothing. It will be so all my life. My dear, kind, ever-faithful and generous adviser, the one friend I have in the world, Ivan Fyodorovitch, with his deep insight into the heart, approves and commends my decision. He knows it.”\n\n“Yes, I approve of it,” Ivan assented, in a subdued but firm voice.\n\n“But I should like Alyosha, too (Ah! Alexey Fyodorovitch, forgive my calling you simply Alyosha), I should like Alexey Fyodorovitch, too, to tell me before my two friends whether I am right. I feel instinctively that you, Alyosha, my dear brother (for you are a dear brother to me),” she said again ecstatically, taking his cold hand in her hot one, “I foresee that your decision, your approval, will bring me peace, in spite of all my sufferings, for, after your words, I shall be calm and submit—I feel that.”\n\n“I don’t know what you are asking me,” said Alyosha, flushing. “I only know that I love you and at this moment wish for your happiness more than my own!... But I know nothing about such affairs,” something impelled him to add hurriedly.\n\n“In such affairs, Alexey Fyodorovitch, in such affairs, the chief thing is honor and duty and something higher—I don’t know what—but higher perhaps even than duty. I am conscious of this irresistible feeling in my heart, and it compels me irresistibly. But it may all be put in two words. I’ve already decided, even if he marries that—creature,” she began solemnly, “whom I never, never can forgive, even then I will not abandon him. Henceforward I will never, never abandon him!” she cried, breaking into a sort of pale, hysterical ecstasy. “Not that I would run after him continually, get in his way and worry him. Oh, no! I will go away to another town—where you like—but I will watch over him all my life—I will watch over him all my life unceasingly. When he becomes unhappy with that woman, and that is bound to happen quite soon, let him come to me and he will find a friend, a sister.... Only a sister, of course, and so for ever; but he will learn at least that that sister is really his sister, who loves him and has sacrificed all her life to him. I will gain my point. I will insist on his knowing me and confiding entirely in me, without reserve,” she cried, in a sort of frenzy. “I will be a god to whom he can pray—and that, at least, he owes me for his treachery and for what I suffered yesterday through him. And let him see that all my life I will be true to him and the promise I gave him, in spite of his being untrue and betraying me. I will—I will become nothing but a means for his happiness, or—how shall I say?—an instrument, a machine for his happiness, and that for my whole life, my whole life, and that he may see that all his life! That’s my decision. Ivan Fyodorovitch fully approves me.”\n\nShe was breathless. She had perhaps intended to express her idea with more dignity, art and naturalness, but her speech was too hurried and crude. It was full of youthful impulsiveness, it betrayed that she was still smarting from yesterday’s insult, and that her pride craved satisfaction. She felt this herself. Her face suddenly darkened, an unpleasant look came into her eyes. Alyosha at once saw it and felt a pang of sympathy. His brother Ivan made it worse by adding:\n\n“I’ve only expressed my own view,” he said. “From any one else, this would have been affected and overstrained, but from you—no. Any other woman would have been wrong, but you are right. I don’t know how to explain it, but I see that you are absolutely genuine and, therefore, you are right.”\n\n“But that’s only for the moment. And what does this moment stand for? Nothing but yesterday’s insult.” Madame Hohlakov obviously had not intended to interfere, but she could not refrain from this very just comment.\n\n“Quite so, quite so,” cried Ivan, with peculiar eagerness, obviously annoyed at being interrupted, “in any one else this moment would be only due to yesterday’s impression and would be only a moment. But with Katerina Ivanovna’s character, that moment will last all her life. What for any one else would be only a promise is for her an everlasting burdensome, grim perhaps, but unflagging duty. And she will be sustained by the feeling of this duty being fulfilled. Your life, Katerina Ivanovna, will henceforth be spent in painful brooding over your own feelings, your own heroism, and your own suffering; but in the end that suffering will be softened and will pass into sweet contemplation of the fulfillment of a bold and proud design. Yes, proud it certainly is, and desperate in any case, but a triumph for you. And the consciousness of it will at last be a source of complete satisfaction and will make you resigned to everything else.”\n\nThis was unmistakably said with some malice and obviously with intention; even perhaps with no desire to conceal that he spoke ironically and with intention.\n\n“Oh, dear, how mistaken it all is!” Madame Hohlakov cried again.\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch, you speak. I want dreadfully to know what you will say!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, and burst into tears. Alyosha got up from the sofa.\n\n“It’s nothing, nothing!” she went on through her tears. “I’m upset, I didn’t sleep last night. But by the side of two such friends as you and your brother I still feel strong—for I know—you two will never desert me.”\n\n“Unluckily I am obliged to return to Moscow—perhaps to-morrow—and to leave you for a long time—And, unluckily, it’s unavoidable,” Ivan said suddenly.\n\n“To-morrow—to Moscow!” her face was suddenly contorted; “but—but, dear me, how fortunate!” she cried in a voice suddenly changed. In one instant there was no trace left of her tears. She underwent an instantaneous transformation, which amazed Alyosha. Instead of a poor, insulted girl, weeping in a sort of “laceration,” he saw a woman completely self-possessed and even exceedingly pleased, as though something agreeable had just happened.\n\n“Oh, not fortunate that I am losing you, of course not,” she corrected herself suddenly, with a charming society smile. “Such a friend as you are could not suppose that. I am only too unhappy at losing you.” She rushed impulsively at Ivan, and seizing both his hands, pressed them warmly. “But what is fortunate is that you will be able in Moscow to see auntie and Agafya and to tell them all the horror of my present position. You can speak with complete openness to Agafya, but spare dear auntie. You will know how to do that. You can’t think how wretched I was yesterday and this morning, wondering how I could write them that dreadful letter—for one can never tell such things in a letter.... Now it will be easy for me to write, for you will see them and explain everything. Oh, how glad I am! But I am only glad of that, believe me. Of course, no one can take your place.... I will run at once to write the letter,” she finished suddenly, and took a step as though to go out of the room.\n\n“And what about Alyosha and his opinion, which you were so desperately anxious to hear?” cried Madame Hohlakov. There was a sarcastic, angry note in her voice.\n\n“I had not forgotten that,” cried Katerina Ivanovna, coming to a sudden standstill, “and why are you so antagonistic at such a moment?” she added, with warm and bitter reproachfulness. “What I said, I repeat. I must have his opinion. More than that, I must have his decision! As he says, so it shall be. You see how anxious I am for your words, Alexey Fyodorovitch.... But what’s the matter?”\n\n“I couldn’t have believed it. I can’t understand it!” Alyosha cried suddenly in distress.\n\n“What? What?”\n\n“He is going to Moscow, and you cry out that you are glad. You said that on purpose! And you begin explaining that you are not glad of that but sorry to be—losing a friend. But that was acting, too—you were playing a part—as in a theater!”\n\n“In a theater? What? What do you mean?” exclaimed Katerina Ivanovna, profoundly astonished, flushing crimson, and frowning.\n\n“Though you assure him you are sorry to lose a friend in him, you persist in telling him to his face that it’s fortunate he is going,” said Alyosha breathlessly. He was standing at the table and did not sit down.\n\n“What are you talking about? I don’t understand.”\n\n“I don’t understand myself.... I seemed to see in a flash ... I know I am not saying it properly, but I’ll say it all the same,” Alyosha went on in the same shaking and broken voice. “What I see is that perhaps you don’t love Dmitri at all ... and never have, from the beginning.... And Dmitri, too, has never loved you ... and only esteems you.... I really don’t know how I dare to say all this, but somebody must tell the truth ... for nobody here will tell the truth.”\n\n“What truth?” cried Katerina Ivanovna, and there was an hysterical ring in her voice.\n\n“I’ll tell you,” Alyosha went on with desperate haste, as though he were jumping from the top of a house. “Call Dmitri; I will fetch him—and let him come here and take your hand and take Ivan’s and join your hands. For you’re torturing Ivan, simply because you love him—and torturing him, because you love Dmitri through ‘self-laceration’—with an unreal love—because you’ve persuaded yourself.”\n\nAlyosha broke off and was silent.\n\n“You ... you ... you are a little religious idiot—that’s what you are!” Katerina Ivanovna snapped. Her face was white and her lips were moving with anger.\n\nIvan suddenly laughed and got up. His hat was in his hand.\n\n“You are mistaken, my good Alyosha,” he said, with an expression Alyosha had never seen in his face before—an expression of youthful sincerity and strong, irresistibly frank feeling. “Katerina Ivanovna has never cared for me! She has known all the time that I cared for her—though I never said a word of my love to her—she knew, but she didn’t care for me. I have never been her friend either, not for one moment; she is too proud to need my friendship. She kept me at her side as a means of revenge. She revenged with me and on me all the insults which she has been continually receiving from Dmitri ever since their first meeting. For even that first meeting has rankled in her heart as an insult—that’s what her heart is like! She has talked to me of nothing but her love for him. I am going now; but, believe me, Katerina Ivanovna, you really love him. And the more he insults you, the more you love him—that’s your ‘laceration.’ You love him just as he is; you love him for insulting you. If he reformed, you’d give him up at once and cease to love him. But you need him so as to contemplate continually your heroic fidelity and to reproach him for infidelity. And it all comes from your pride. Oh, there’s a great deal of humiliation and self-abasement about it, but it all comes from pride.... I am too young and I’ve loved you too much. I know that I ought not to say this, that it would be more dignified on my part simply to leave you, and it would be less offensive for you. But I am going far away, and shall never come back.... It is for ever. I don’t want to sit beside a ‘laceration.’... But I don’t know how to speak now. I’ve said everything.... Good-by, Katerina Ivanovna; you can’t be angry with me, for I am a hundred times more severely punished than you, if only by the fact that I shall never see you again. Good-by! I don’t want your hand. You have tortured me too deliberately for me to be able to forgive you at this moment. I shall forgive you later, but now I don’t want your hand. ‘Den Dank, Dame, begehr ich nicht,’” he added, with a forced smile, showing, however, that he could read Schiller, and read him till he knew him by heart—which Alyosha would never have believed. He went out of the room without saying good-by even to his hostess, Madame Hohlakov. Alyosha clasped his hands.\n\n“Ivan!” he cried desperately after him. “Come back, Ivan! No, nothing will induce him to come back now!” he cried again, regretfully realizing it; “but it’s my fault, my fault. I began it! Ivan spoke angrily, wrongly. Unjustly and angrily. He must come back here, come back,” Alyosha kept exclaiming frantically.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna went suddenly into the next room.\n\n“You have done no harm. You behaved beautifully, like an angel,” Madame Hohlakov whispered rapidly and ecstatically to Alyosha. “I will do my utmost to prevent Ivan Fyodorovitch from going.”\n\nHer face beamed with delight, to the great distress of Alyosha, but Katerina Ivanovna suddenly returned. She had two hundred-rouble notes in her hand.\n\n“I have a great favor to ask of you, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” she began, addressing Alyosha with an apparently calm and even voice, as though nothing had happened. “A week—yes, I think it was a week ago—Dmitri Fyodorovitch was guilty of a hasty and unjust action—a very ugly action. There is a low tavern here, and in it he met that discharged officer, that captain, whom your father used to employ in some business. Dmitri Fyodorovitch somehow lost his temper with this captain, seized him by the beard and dragged him out into the street and for some distance along it, in that insulting fashion. And I am told that his son, a boy, quite a child, who is at the school here, saw it and ran beside them crying and begging for his father, appealing to every one to defend him, while every one laughed. You must forgive me, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I cannot think without indignation of that disgraceful action of his ... one of those actions of which only Dmitri Fyodorovitch would be capable in his anger ... and in his passions! I can’t describe it even.... I can’t find my words. I’ve made inquiries about his victim, and find he is quite a poor man. His name is Snegiryov. He did something wrong in the army and was discharged. I can’t tell you what. And now he has sunk into terrible destitution, with his family—an unhappy family of sick children, and, I believe, an insane wife. He has been living here a long time; he used to work as a copying clerk, but now he is getting nothing. I thought if you ... that is I thought ... I don’t know. I am so confused. You see, I wanted to ask you, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, to go to him, to find some excuse to go to them—I mean to that captain—oh, goodness, how badly I explain it!—and delicately, carefully, as only you know how to” (Alyosha blushed), “manage to give him this assistance, these two hundred roubles. He will be sure to take it.... I mean, persuade him to take it.... Or, rather, what do I mean? You see it’s not by way of compensation to prevent him from taking proceedings (for I believe he meant to), but simply a token of sympathy, of a desire to assist him from me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s betrothed, not from himself.... But you know.... I would go myself, but you’ll know how to do it ever so much better. He lives in Lake Street, in the house of a woman called Kalmikov.... For God’s sake, Alexey Fyodorovitch, do it for me, and now ... now I am rather ... tired. Good-by!”\n\nShe turned and disappeared behind the portière so quickly that Alyosha had not time to utter a word, though he wanted to speak. He longed to beg her pardon, to blame himself, to say something, for his heart was full and he could not bear to go out of the room without it. But Madame Hohlakov took him by the hand and drew him along with her. In the hall she stopped him again as before.\n\n“She is proud, she is struggling with herself; but kind, charming, generous,” she exclaimed, in a half-whisper. “Oh, how I love her, especially sometimes, and how glad I am again of everything! Dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, you didn’t know, but I must tell you, that we all, all—both her aunts, I and all of us, Lise, even—have been hoping and praying for nothing for the last month but that she may give up your favorite Dmitri, who takes no notice of her and does not care for her, and may marry Ivan Fyodorovitch—such an excellent and cultivated young man, who loves her more than anything in the world. We are in a regular plot to bring it about, and I am even staying on here perhaps on that account.”\n\n“But she has been crying—she has been wounded again,” cried Alyosha.\n\n“Never trust a woman’s tears, Alexey Fyodorovitch. I am never for the women in such cases. I am always on the side of the men.”\n\n“Mamma, you are spoiling him,” Lise’s little voice cried from behind the door.\n\n“No, it was all my fault. I am horribly to blame,” Alyosha repeated unconsoled, hiding his face in his hands in an agony of remorse for his indiscretion.\n\n“Quite the contrary; you behaved like an angel, like an angel. I am ready to say so a thousand times over.”\n\n“Mamma, how has he behaved like an angel?” Lise’s voice was heard again.\n\n“I somehow fancied all at once,” Alyosha went on as though he had not heard Lise, “that she loved Ivan, and so I said that stupid thing.... What will happen now?”\n\n“To whom, to whom?” cried Lise. “Mamma, you really want to be the death of me. I ask you and you don’t answer.”\n\nAt the moment the maid ran in.\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna is ill.... She is crying, struggling ... hysterics.”\n\n“What is the matter?” cried Lise, in a tone of real anxiety. “Mamma, I shall be having hysterics, and not she!”\n\n“Lise, for mercy’s sake, don’t scream, don’t persecute me. At your age one can’t know everything that grown-up people know. I’ll come and tell you everything you ought to know. Oh, mercy on us! I am coming, I am coming.... Hysterics is a good sign, Alexey Fyodorovitch; it’s an excellent thing that she is hysterical. That’s just as it ought to be. In such cases I am always against the woman, against all these feminine tears and hysterics. Run and say, Yulia, that I’ll fly to her. As for Ivan Fyodorovitch’s going away like that, it’s her own fault. But he won’t go away. Lise, for mercy’s sake, don’t scream! Oh, yes; you are not screaming. It’s I am screaming. Forgive your mamma; but I am delighted, delighted, delighted! Did you notice, Alexey Fyodorovitch, how young, how young Ivan Fyodorovitch was just now when he went out, when he said all that and went out? I thought he was so learned, such a savant, and all of a sudden he behaved so warmly, openly, and youthfully, with such youthful inexperience, and it was all so fine, like you.... And the way he repeated that German verse, it was just like you! But I must fly, I must fly! Alexey Fyodorovitch, make haste to carry out her commission, and then make haste back. Lise, do you want anything now? For mercy’s sake, don’t keep Alexey Fyodorovitch a minute. He will come back to you at once.”\n\nMadame Hohlakov at last ran off. Before leaving, Alyosha would have opened the door to see Lise.\n\n“On no account,” cried Lise. “On no account now. Speak through the door. How have you come to be an angel? That’s the only thing I want to know.”\n\n“For an awful piece of stupidity, Lise! Good-by!”\n\n“Don’t dare to go away like that!” Lise was beginning.\n\n“Lise, I have a real sorrow! I’ll be back directly, but I have a great, great sorrow!”\n\nAnd he ran out of the room.\n\n# Chapter VI. A Laceration In The Cottage\n\nHe certainly was really grieved in a way he had seldom been before. He had rushed in like a fool, and meddled in what? In a love-affair. “But what do I know about it? What can I tell about such things?” he repeated to himself for the hundredth time, flushing crimson. “Oh, being ashamed would be nothing; shame is only the punishment I deserve. The trouble is I shall certainly have caused more unhappiness.... And Father Zossima sent me to reconcile and bring them together. Is this the way to bring them together?” Then he suddenly remembered how he had tried to join their hands, and he felt fearfully ashamed again. “Though I acted quite sincerely, I must be more sensible in the future,” he concluded suddenly, and did not even smile at his conclusion.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna’s commission took him to Lake Street, and his brother Dmitri lived close by, in a turning out of Lake Street. Alyosha decided to go to him in any case before going to the captain, though he had a presentiment that he would not find his brother. He suspected that he would intentionally keep out of his way now, but he must find him anyhow. Time was passing: the thought of his dying elder had not left Alyosha for one minute from the time he set off from the monastery.\n\nThere was one point which interested him particularly about Katerina Ivanovna’s commission; when she had mentioned the captain’s son, the little schoolboy who had run beside his father crying, the idea had at once struck Alyosha that this must be the schoolboy who had bitten his finger when he, Alyosha, asked him what he had done to hurt him. Now Alyosha felt practically certain of this, though he could not have said why. Thinking of another subject was a relief, and he resolved to think no more about the “mischief” he had done, and not to torture himself with remorse, but to do what he had to do, let come what would. At that thought he was completely comforted. Turning to the street where Dmitri lodged, he felt hungry, and taking out of his pocket the roll he had brought from his father’s, he ate it. It made him feel stronger.\n\nDmitri was not at home. The people of the house, an old cabinet-maker, his son, and his old wife, looked with positive suspicion at Alyosha. “He hasn’t slept here for the last three nights. Maybe he has gone away,” the old man said in answer to Alyosha’s persistent inquiries. Alyosha saw that he was answering in accordance with instructions. When he asked whether he were not at Grushenka’s or in hiding at Foma’s (Alyosha spoke so freely on purpose), all three looked at him in alarm. “They are fond of him, they are doing their best for him,” thought Alyosha. “That’s good.”\n\nAt last he found the house in Lake Street. It was a decrepit little house, sunk on one side, with three windows looking into the street, and with a muddy yard, in the middle of which stood a solitary cow. He crossed the yard and found the door opening into the passage. On the left of the passage lived the old woman of the house with her old daughter. Both seemed to be deaf. In answer to his repeated inquiry for the captain, one of them at last understood that he was asking for their lodgers, and pointed to a door across the passage. The captain’s lodging turned out to be a simple cottage room. Alyosha had his hand on the iron latch to open the door, when he was struck by the strange hush within. Yet he knew from Katerina Ivanovna’s words that the man had a family. “Either they are all asleep or perhaps they have heard me coming and are waiting for me to open the door. I’d better knock first,” and he knocked. An answer came, but not at once, after an interval of perhaps ten seconds.\n\n“Who’s there?” shouted some one in a loud and very angry voice.\n\nThen Alyosha opened the door and crossed the threshold. He found himself in a regular peasant’s room. Though it was large, it was cumbered up with domestic belongings of all sorts, and there were several people in it. On the left was a large Russian stove. From the stove to the window on the left was a string running across the room, and on it there were rags hanging. There was a bedstead against the wall on each side, right and left, covered with knitted quilts. On the one on the left was a pyramid of four print-covered pillows, each smaller than the one beneath. On the other there was only one very small pillow. The opposite corner was screened off by a curtain or a sheet hung on a string. Behind this curtain could be seen a bed made up on a bench and a chair. The rough square table of plain wood had been moved into the middle window. The three windows, which consisted each of four tiny greenish mildewy panes, gave little light, and were close shut, so that the room was not very light and rather stuffy. On the table was a frying-pan with the remains of some fried eggs, a half-eaten piece of bread, and a small bottle with a few drops of vodka.\n\nA woman of genteel appearance, wearing a cotton gown, was sitting on a chair by the bed on the left. Her face was thin and yellow, and her sunken cheeks betrayed at the first glance that she was ill. But what struck Alyosha most was the expression in the poor woman’s eyes—a look of surprised inquiry and yet of haughty pride. And while he was talking to her husband, her big brown eyes moved from one speaker to the other with the same haughty and questioning expression. Beside her at the window stood a young girl, rather plain, with scanty reddish hair, poorly but very neatly dressed. She looked disdainfully at Alyosha as he came in. Beside the other bed was sitting another female figure. She was a very sad sight, a young girl of about twenty, but hunchback and crippled “with withered legs,” as Alyosha was told afterwards. Her crutches stood in the corner close by. The strikingly beautiful and gentle eyes of this poor girl looked with mild serenity at Alyosha. A man of forty-five was sitting at the table, finishing the fried eggs. He was spare, small and weakly built. He had reddish hair and a scanty light-colored beard, very much like a wisp of tow (this comparison and the phrase “a wisp of tow” flashed at once into Alyosha’s mind for some reason, he remembered it afterwards). It was obviously this gentleman who had shouted to him, as there was no other man in the room. But when Alyosha went in, he leapt up from the bench on which he was sitting, and, hastily wiping his mouth with a ragged napkin, darted up to Alyosha.\n\n“It’s a monk come to beg for the monastery. A nice place to come to!” the girl standing in the left corner said aloud. The man spun round instantly towards her and answered her in an excited and breaking voice:\n\n“No, Varvara, you are wrong. Allow me to ask,” he turned again to Alyosha, “what has brought you to—our retreat?”\n\nAlyosha looked attentively at him. It was the first time he had seen him. There was something angular, flurried and irritable about him. Though he had obviously just been drinking, he was not drunk. There was extraordinary impudence in his expression, and yet, strange to say, at the same time there was fear. He looked like a man who had long been kept in subjection and had submitted to it, and now had suddenly turned and was trying to assert himself. Or, better still, like a man who wants dreadfully to hit you but is horribly afraid you will hit him. In his words and in the intonation of his shrill voice there was a sort of crazy humor, at times spiteful and at times cringing, and continually shifting from one tone to another. The question about “our retreat” he had asked as it were quivering all over, rolling his eyes, and skipping up so close to Alyosha that he instinctively drew back a step. He was dressed in a very shabby dark cotton coat, patched and spotted. He wore checked trousers of an extremely light color, long out of fashion, and of very thin material. They were so crumpled and so short that he looked as though he had grown out of them like a boy.\n\n“I am Alexey Karamazov,” Alyosha began in reply.\n\n“I quite understand that, sir,” the gentleman snapped out at once to assure him that he knew who he was already. “I am Captain Snegiryov, sir, but I am still desirous to know precisely what has led you—”\n\n“Oh, I’ve come for nothing special. I wanted to have a word with you—if only you allow me.”\n\n“In that case, here is a chair, sir; kindly be seated. That’s what they used to say in the old comedies, ‘kindly be seated,’” and with a rapid gesture he seized an empty chair (it was a rough wooden chair, not upholstered) and set it for him almost in the middle of the room; then, taking another similar chair for himself, he sat down facing Alyosha, so close to him that their knees almost touched.\n\n“Nikolay Ilyitch Snegiryov, sir, formerly a captain in the Russian infantry, put to shame for his vices, but still a captain. Though I might not be one now for the way I talk; for the last half of my life I’ve learnt to say ‘sir.’ It’s a word you use when you’ve come down in the world.”\n\n“That’s very true,” smiled Alyosha. “But is it used involuntarily or on purpose?”\n\n“As God’s above, it’s involuntary, and I usen’t to use it! I didn’t use the word ‘sir’ all my life, but as soon as I sank into low water I began to say ‘sir.’ It’s the work of a higher power. I see you are interested in contemporary questions, but how can I have excited your curiosity, living as I do in surroundings impossible for the exercise of hospitality?”\n\n“I’ve come—about that business.”\n\n“About what business?” the captain interrupted impatiently.\n\n“About your meeting with my brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” Alyosha blurted out awkwardly.\n\n“What meeting, sir? You don’t mean that meeting? About my ‘wisp of tow,’ then?” He moved closer so that his knees positively knocked against Alyosha. His lips were strangely compressed like a thread.\n\n“What wisp of tow?” muttered Alyosha.\n\n“He is come to complain of me, father!” cried a voice familiar to Alyosha—the voice of the schoolboy—from behind the curtain. “I bit his finger just now.” The curtain was pulled, and Alyosha saw his assailant lying on a little bed made up on the bench and the chair in the corner under the ikons. The boy lay covered by his coat and an old wadded quilt. He was evidently unwell, and, judging by his glittering eyes, he was in a fever. He looked at Alyosha without fear, as though he felt he was at home and could not be touched.\n\n“What! Did he bite your finger?” The captain jumped up from his chair. “Was it your finger he bit?”\n\n“Yes. He was throwing stones with other schoolboys. There were six of them against him alone. I went up to him, and he threw a stone at me and then another at my head. I asked him what I had done to him. And then he rushed at me and bit my finger badly, I don’t know why.”\n\n“I’ll thrash him, sir, at once—this minute!” The captain jumped up from his seat.\n\n“But I am not complaining at all, I am simply telling you ... I don’t want him to be thrashed. Besides, he seems to be ill.”\n\n“And do you suppose I’d thrash him? That I’d take my Ilusha and thrash him before you for your satisfaction? Would you like it done at once, sir?” said the captain, suddenly turning to Alyosha, as though he were going to attack him. “I am sorry about your finger, sir; but instead of thrashing Ilusha, would you like me to chop off my four fingers with this knife here before your eyes to satisfy your just wrath? I should think four fingers would be enough to satisfy your thirst for vengeance. You won’t ask for the fifth one too?” He stopped short with a catch in his throat. Every feature in his face was twitching and working; he looked extremely defiant. He was in a sort of frenzy.\n\n“I think I understand it all now,” said Alyosha gently and sorrowfully, still keeping his seat. “So your boy is a good boy, he loves his father, and he attacked me as the brother of your assailant.... Now I understand it,” he repeated thoughtfully. “But my brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch regrets his action, I know that, and if only it is possible for him to come to you, or better still, to meet you in that same place, he will ask your forgiveness before every one—if you wish it.”\n\n“After pulling out my beard, you mean, he will ask my forgiveness? And he thinks that will be a satisfactory finish, doesn’t he?”\n\n“Oh, no! On the contrary, he will do anything you like and in any way you like.”\n\n“So if I were to ask his highness to go down on his knees before me in that very tavern—‘The Metropolis’ it’s called—or in the market-place, he would do it?”\n\n“Yes, he would even go down on his knees.”\n\n“You’ve pierced me to the heart, sir. Touched me to tears and pierced me to the heart! I am only too sensible of your brother’s generosity. Allow me to introduce my family, my two daughters and my son—my litter. If I die, who will care for them, and while I live who but they will care for a wretch like me? That’s a great thing the Lord has ordained for every man of my sort, sir. For there must be some one able to love even a man like me.”\n\n“Ah, that’s perfectly true!” exclaimed Alyosha.\n\n“Oh, do leave off playing the fool! Some idiot comes in, and you put us to shame!” cried the girl by the window, suddenly turning to her father with a disdainful and contemptuous air.\n\n“Wait a little, Varvara!” cried her father, speaking peremptorily but looking at her quite approvingly. “That’s her character,” he said, addressing Alyosha again.\n\n“And in all nature there was naught That could find favor in his eyes—\n\nor rather in the feminine: that could find favor in her eyes. But now let me present you to my wife, Arina Petrovna. She is crippled, she is forty-three; she can move, but very little. She is of humble origin. Arina Petrovna, compose your countenance. This is Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov. Get up, Alexey Fyodorovitch.” He took him by the hand and with unexpected force pulled him up. “You must stand up to be introduced to a lady. It’s not the Karamazov, mamma, who ... h’m ... etcetera, but his brother, radiant with modest virtues. Come, Arina Petrovna, come, mamma, first your hand to be kissed.”\n\nAnd he kissed his wife’s hand respectfully and even tenderly. The girl at the window turned her back indignantly on the scene; an expression of extraordinary cordiality came over the haughtily inquiring face of the woman.\n\n“Good morning! Sit down, Mr. Tchernomazov,” she said.\n\n“Karamazov, mamma, Karamazov. We are of humble origin,” he whispered again.\n\n“Well, Karamazov, or whatever it is, but I always think of Tchernomazov.... Sit down. Why has he pulled you up? He calls me crippled, but I am not, only my legs are swollen like barrels, and I am shriveled up myself. Once I used to be so fat, but now it’s as though I had swallowed a needle.”\n\n“We are of humble origin,” the captain muttered again.\n\n“Oh, father, father!” the hunchback girl, who had till then been silent on her chair, said suddenly, and she hid her eyes in her handkerchief.\n\n“Buffoon!” blurted out the girl at the window.\n\n“Have you heard our news?” said the mother, pointing at her daughters. “It’s like clouds coming over; the clouds pass and we have music again. When we were with the army, we used to have many such guests. I don’t mean to make any comparisons; every one to their taste. The deacon’s wife used to come then and say, ‘Alexandr Alexandrovitch is a man of the noblest heart, but Nastasya Petrovna,’ she would say, ‘is of the brood of hell.’ ‘Well,’ I said, ‘that’s a matter of taste; but you are a little spitfire.’ ‘And you want keeping in your place,’ says she. ‘You black sword,’ said I, ‘who asked you to teach me?’ ‘But my breath,’ says she, ‘is clean, and yours is unclean.’ ‘You ask all the officers whether my breath is unclean.’ And ever since then I had it in my mind. Not long ago I was sitting here as I am now, when I saw that very general come in who came here for Easter, and I asked him: ‘Your Excellency,’ said I, ‘can a lady’s breath be unpleasant?’ ‘Yes,’ he answered; ‘you ought to open a window-pane or open the door, for the air is not fresh here.’ And they all go on like that! And what is my breath to them? The dead smell worse still! ‘I won’t spoil the air,’ said I, ‘I’ll order some slippers and go away.’ My darlings, don’t blame your own mother! Nikolay Ilyitch, how is it I can’t please you? There’s only Ilusha who comes home from school and loves me. Yesterday he brought me an apple. Forgive your own mother—forgive a poor lonely creature! Why has my breath become unpleasant to you?”\n\nAnd the poor mad woman broke into sobs, and tears streamed down her cheeks. The captain rushed up to her.\n\n“Mamma, mamma, my dear, give over! You are not lonely. Every one loves you, every one adores you.” He began kissing both her hands again and tenderly stroking her face; taking the dinner-napkin, he began wiping away her tears. Alyosha fancied that he too had tears in his eyes. “There, you see, you hear?” he turned with a sort of fury to Alyosha, pointing to the poor imbecile.\n\n“I see and hear,” muttered Alyosha.\n\n“Father, father, how can you—with him! Let him alone!” cried the boy, sitting up in his bed and gazing at his father with glowing eyes.\n\n“Do give over fooling, showing off your silly antics which never lead to anything!” shouted Varvara, stamping her foot with passion.\n\n“Your anger is quite just this time, Varvara, and I’ll make haste to satisfy you. Come, put on your cap, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and I’ll put on mine. We will go out. I have a word to say to you in earnest, but not within these walls. This girl sitting here is my daughter Nina; I forgot to introduce her to you. She is a heavenly angel incarnate ... who has flown down to us mortals,... if you can understand.”\n\n“There he is shaking all over, as though he is in convulsions!” Varvara went on indignantly.\n\n“And she there stamping her foot at me and calling me a fool just now, she is a heavenly angel incarnate too, and she has good reason to call me so. Come along, Alexey Fyodorovitch, we must make an end.”\n\nAnd, snatching Alyosha’s hand, he drew him out of the room into the street.\n\n# Chapter VII. And In The Open Air\n\n“The air is fresh, but in my apartment it is not so in any sense of the word. Let us walk slowly, sir. I should be glad of your kind interest.”\n\n“I too have something important to say to you,” observed Alyosha, “only I don’t know how to begin.”\n\n“To be sure you must have business with me. You would never have looked in upon me without some object. Unless you come simply to complain of the boy, and that’s hardly likely. And, by the way, about the boy: I could not explain to you in there, but here I will describe that scene to you. My tow was thicker a week ago—I mean my beard. That’s the nickname they give to my beard, the schoolboys most of all. Well, your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch was pulling me by my beard, I’d done nothing, he was in a towering rage and happened to come upon me. He dragged me out of the tavern into the market-place; at that moment the boys were coming out of school, and with them Ilusha. As soon as he saw me in such a state he rushed up to me. ‘Father,’ he cried, ‘father!’ He caught hold of me, hugged me, tried to pull me away, crying to my assailant, ‘Let go, let go, it’s my father, forgive him!’—yes, he actually cried ‘forgive him.’ He clutched at that hand, that very hand, in his little hands and kissed it.... I remember his little face at that moment, I haven’t forgotten it and I never shall!”\n\n“I swear,” cried Alyosha, “that my brother will express his most deep and sincere regret, even if he has to go down on his knees in that same market-place.... I’ll make him or he is no brother of mine!”\n\n“Aha, then it’s only a suggestion! And it does not come from him but simply from the generosity of your own warm heart. You should have said so. No, in that case allow me to tell you of your brother’s highly chivalrous soldierly generosity, for he did give expression to it at the time. He left off dragging me by my beard and released me: ‘You are an officer,’ he said, ‘and I am an officer, if you can find a decent man to be your second send me your challenge. I will give satisfaction, though you are a scoundrel.’ That’s what he said. A chivalrous spirit indeed! I retired with Ilusha, and that scene is a family record imprinted for ever on Ilusha’s soul. No, it’s not for us to claim the privileges of noblemen. Judge for yourself. You’ve just been in our mansion, what did you see there? Three ladies, one a cripple and weak-minded, another a cripple and hunchback and the third not crippled but far too clever. She is a student, dying to get back to Petersburg, to work for the emancipation of the Russian woman on the banks of the Neva. I won’t speak of Ilusha, he is only nine. I am alone in the world, and if I die, what will become of all of them? I simply ask you that. And if I challenge him and he kills me on the spot, what then? What will become of them? And worse still, if he doesn’t kill me but only cripples me: I couldn’t work, but I should still be a mouth to feed. Who would feed it and who would feed them all? Must I take Ilusha from school and send him to beg in the streets? That’s what it means for me to challenge him to a duel. It’s silly talk and nothing else.”\n\n“He will beg your forgiveness, he will bow down at your feet in the middle of the market-place,” cried Alyosha again, with glowing eyes.\n\n“I did think of prosecuting him,” the captain went on, “but look in our code, could I get much compensation for a personal injury? And then Agrafena Alexandrovna[3] sent for me and shouted at me: ‘Don’t dare to dream of it! If you proceed against him, I’ll publish it to all the world that he beat you for your dishonesty, and then you will be prosecuted.’ I call God to witness whose was the dishonesty and by whose commands I acted, wasn’t it by her own and Fyodor Pavlovitch’s? ‘And what’s more,’ she went on, ‘I’ll dismiss you for good and you’ll never earn another penny from me. I’ll speak to my merchant too’ (that’s what she calls her old man) ‘and he will dismiss you!’ And if he dismisses me, what can I earn then from any one? Those two are all I have to look to, for your Fyodor Pavlovitch has not only given over employing me, for another reason, but he means to make use of papers I’ve signed to go to law against me. And so I kept quiet, and you have seen our retreat. But now let me ask you: did Ilusha hurt your finger much? I didn’t like to go into it in our mansion before him.”\n\n“Yes, very much, and he was in a great fury. He was avenging you on me as a Karamazov, I see that now. But if only you had seen how he was throwing stones at his school-fellows! It’s very dangerous. They might kill him. They are children and stupid. A stone may be thrown and break somebody’s head.”\n\n“That’s just what has happened. He has been bruised by a stone to-day. Not on the head but on the chest, just above the heart. He came home crying and groaning and now he is ill.”\n\n“And you know he attacks them first. He is bitter against them on your account. They say he stabbed a boy called Krassotkin with a pen-knife not long ago.”\n\n“I’ve heard about that too, it’s dangerous. Krassotkin is an official here, we may hear more about it.”\n\n“I would advise you,” Alyosha went on warmly, “not to send him to school at all for a time till he is calmer ... and his anger is passed.”\n\n“Anger!” the captain repeated, “that’s just what it is. He is a little creature, but it’s a mighty anger. You don’t know all, sir. Let me tell you more. Since that incident all the boys have been teasing him about the ‘wisp of tow.’ Schoolboys are a merciless race, individually they are angels, but together, especially in schools, they are often merciless. Their teasing has stirred up a gallant spirit in Ilusha. An ordinary boy, a weak son, would have submitted, have felt ashamed of his father, sir, but he stood up for his father against them all. For his father and for truth and justice. For what he suffered when he kissed your brother’s hand and cried to him ‘Forgive father, forgive him,’—that only God knows—and I, his father. For our children—not your children, but ours—the children of the poor gentlemen looked down upon by every one—know what justice means, sir, even at nine years old. How should the rich know? They don’t explore such depths once in their lives. But at that moment in the square when he kissed his hand, at that moment my Ilusha had grasped all that justice means. That truth entered into him and crushed him for ever, sir,” the captain said hotly again with a sort of frenzy, and he struck his right fist against his left palm as though he wanted to show how “the truth” crushed Ilusha. “That very day, sir, he fell ill with fever and was delirious all night. All that day he hardly said a word to me, but I noticed he kept watching me from the corner, though he turned to the window and pretended to be learning his lessons. But I could see his mind was not on his lessons. Next day I got drunk to forget my troubles, sinful man as I am, and I don’t remember much. Mamma began crying, too—I am very fond of mamma—well, I spent my last penny drowning my troubles. Don’t despise me for that, sir, in Russia men who drink are the best. The best men amongst us are the greatest drunkards. I lay down and I don’t remember about Ilusha, though all that day the boys had been jeering at him at school. ‘Wisp of tow,’ they shouted, ‘your father was pulled out of the tavern by his wisp of tow, you ran by and begged forgiveness.’”\n\n“On the third day when he came back from school, I saw he looked pale and wretched. ‘What is it?’ I asked. He wouldn’t answer. Well, there’s no talking in our mansion without mamma and the girls taking part in it. What’s more, the girls had heard about it the very first day. Varvara had begun snarling. ‘You fools and buffoons, can you ever do anything rational?’ ‘Quite so,’ I said, ‘can we ever do anything rational?’ For the time I turned it off like that. So in the evening I took the boy out for a walk, for you must know we go for a walk every evening, always the same way, along which we are going now—from our gate to that great stone which lies alone in the road under the hurdle, which marks the beginning of the town pasture. A beautiful and lonely spot, sir. Ilusha and I walked along hand in hand as usual. He has a little hand, his fingers are thin and cold—he suffers with his chest, you know. ‘Father,’ said he, ‘father!’ ‘Well?’ said I. I saw his eyes flashing. ‘Father, how he treated you then!’ ‘It can’t be helped, Ilusha,’ I said. ‘Don’t forgive him, father, don’t forgive him! At school they say that he has paid you ten roubles for it.’ ‘No, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘I would not take money from him for anything.’ Then he began trembling all over, took my hand in both his and kissed it again. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘father, challenge him to a duel, at school they say you are a coward and won’t challenge him, and that you’ll accept ten roubles from him.’ ‘I can’t challenge him to a duel, Ilusha,’ I answered. And I told briefly what I’ve just told you. He listened. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘anyway don’t forgive it. When I grow up I’ll call him out myself and kill him.’ His eyes shone and glowed. And of course I am his father, and I had to put in a word: ‘It’s a sin to kill,’ I said, ‘even in a duel.’ ‘Father,’ he said, ‘when I grow up, I’ll knock him down, knock the sword out of his hand, I’ll fall on him, wave my sword over him and say: “I could kill you, but I forgive you, so there!”’ You see what the workings of his little mind have been during these two days; he must have been planning that vengeance all day, and raving about it at night.\n\n“But he began to come home from school badly beaten, I found out about it the day before yesterday, and you are right, I won’t send him to that school any more. I heard that he was standing up against all the class alone and defying them all, that his heart was full of resentment, of bitterness—I was alarmed about him. We went for another walk. ‘Father,’ he asked, ‘are the rich people stronger than any one else on earth?’ ‘Yes, Ilusha,’ I said, ‘there are no people on earth stronger than the rich.’ ‘Father,’ he said, ‘I will get rich, I will become an officer and conquer everybody. The Tsar will reward me, I will come back here and then no one will dare—’ Then he was silent and his lips still kept trembling. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘what a horrid town this is.’ ‘Yes, Ilusha,’ I said, ‘it isn’t a very nice town.’ ‘Father, let us move into another town, a nice one,’ he said, ‘where people don’t know about us.’ ‘We will move, we will, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘only I must save up for it.’ I was glad to be able to turn his mind from painful thoughts, and we began to dream of how we would move to another town, how we would buy a horse and cart. ‘We will put mamma and your sisters inside, we will cover them up and we’ll walk, you shall have a lift now and then, and I’ll walk beside, for we must take care of our horse, we can’t all ride. That’s how we’ll go.’ He was enchanted at that, most of all at the thought of having a horse and driving him. For of course a Russian boy is born among horses. We chattered a long while. Thank God, I thought, I have diverted his mind and comforted him.\n\n“That was the day before yesterday, in the evening, but last night everything was changed. He had gone to school in the morning, he came back depressed, terribly depressed. In the evening I took him by the hand and we went for a walk; he would not talk. There was a wind blowing and no sun, and a feeling of autumn; twilight was coming on. We walked along, both of us depressed. ‘Well, my boy,’ said I, ‘how about our setting off on our travels?’ I thought I might bring him back to our talk of the day before. He didn’t answer, but I felt his fingers trembling in my hand. Ah, I thought, it’s a bad job; there’s something fresh. We had reached the stone where we are now. I sat down on the stone. And in the air there were lots of kites flapping and whirling. There were as many as thirty in sight. Of course, it’s just the season for the kites. ‘Look, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘it’s time we got out our last year’s kite again. I’ll mend it, where have you put it away?’ My boy made no answer. He looked away and turned sideways to me. And then a gust of wind blew up the sand. He suddenly fell on me, threw both his little arms round my neck and held me tight. You know, when children are silent and proud, and try to keep back their tears when they are in great trouble and suddenly break down, their tears fall in streams. With those warm streams of tears, he suddenly wetted my face. He sobbed and shook as though he were in convulsions, and squeezed up against me as I sat on the stone. ‘Father,’ he kept crying, ‘dear father, how he insulted you!’ And I sobbed too. We sat shaking in each other’s arms. ‘Ilusha,’ I said to him, ‘Ilusha darling.’ No one saw us then. God alone saw us, I hope He will record it to my credit. You must thank your brother, Alexey Fyodorovitch. No, sir, I won’t thrash my boy for your satisfaction.”\n\nHe had gone back to his original tone of resentful buffoonery. Alyosha felt though that he trusted him, and that if there had been some one else in his, Alyosha’s place, the man would not have spoken so openly and would not have told what he had just told. This encouraged Alyosha, whose heart was trembling on the verge of tears.\n\n“Ah, how I would like to make friends with your boy!” he cried. “If you could arrange it—”\n\n“Certainly, sir,” muttered the captain.\n\n“But now listen to something quite different!” Alyosha went on. “I have a message for you. That same brother of mine, Dmitri, has insulted his betrothed, too, a noble-hearted girl of whom you have probably heard. I have a right to tell you of her wrong; I ought to do so, in fact, for hearing of the insult done to you and learning all about your unfortunate position, she commissioned me at once—just now—to bring you this help from her—but only from her alone, not from Dmitri, who has abandoned her. Nor from me, his brother, nor from any one else, but from her, only from her! She entreats you to accept her help.... You have both been insulted by the same man. She thought of you only when she had just received a similar insult from him—similar in its cruelty, I mean. She comes like a sister to help a brother in misfortune.... She told me to persuade you to take these two hundred roubles from her, as from a sister, knowing that you are in such need. No one will know of it, it can give rise to no unjust slander. There are the two hundred roubles, and I swear you must take them unless—unless all men are to be enemies on earth! But there are brothers even on earth.... You have a generous heart ... you must see that, you must,” and Alyosha held out two new rainbow-colored hundred-rouble notes.\n\nThey were both standing at the time by the great stone close to the fence, and there was no one near. The notes seemed to produce a tremendous impression on the captain. He started, but at first only from astonishment. Such an outcome of their conversation was the last thing he expected. Nothing could have been farther from his dreams than help from any one—and such a sum!\n\nHe took the notes, and for a minute he was almost unable to answer, quite a new expression came into his face.\n\n“That for me? So much money—two hundred roubles! Good heavens! Why, I haven’t seen so much money for the last four years! Mercy on us! And she says she is a sister.... And is that the truth?”\n\n“I swear that all I told you is the truth,” cried Alyosha.\n\nThe captain flushed red.\n\n“Listen, my dear, listen. If I take it, I shan’t be behaving like a scoundrel? In your eyes, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I shan’t be a scoundrel? No, Alexey Fyodorovitch, listen, listen,” he hurried, touching Alyosha with both his hands. “You are persuading me to take it, saying that it’s a sister sends it, but inwardly, in your heart won’t you feel contempt for me if I take it, eh?”\n\n“No, no, on my salvation I swear I shan’t! And no one will ever know but me—I, you and she, and one other lady, her great friend.”\n\n“Never mind the lady! Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch, at a moment like this you must listen, for you can’t understand what these two hundred roubles mean to me now.” The poor fellow went on rising gradually into a sort of incoherent, almost wild enthusiasm. He was thrown off his balance and talked extremely fast, as though afraid he would not be allowed to say all he had to say.\n\n“Besides its being honestly acquired from a ‘sister,’ so highly respected and revered, do you know that now I can look after mamma and Nina, my hunchback angel daughter? Doctor Herzenstube came to me in the kindness of his heart and was examining them both for a whole hour. ‘I can make nothing of it,’ said he, but he prescribed a mineral water which is kept at a chemist’s here. He said it would be sure to do her good, and he ordered baths, too, with some medicine in them. The mineral water costs thirty copecks, and she’d need to drink forty bottles perhaps; so I took the prescription and laid it on the shelf under the ikons, and there it lies. And he ordered hot baths for Nina with something dissolved in them, morning and evening. But how can we carry out such a cure in our mansion, without servants, without help, without a bath, and without water? Nina is rheumatic all over, I don’t think I told you that. All her right side aches at night, she is in agony, and, would you believe it, the angel bears it without groaning for fear of waking us. We eat what we can get, and she’ll only take the leavings, what you’d scarcely give to a dog. ‘I am not worth it, I am taking it from you, I am a burden on you,’ that’s what her angel eyes try to express. We wait on her, but she doesn’t like it. ‘I am a useless cripple, no good to any one.’ As though she were not worth it, when she is the saving of all of us with her angelic sweetness. Without her, without her gentle word it would be hell among us! She softens even Varvara. And don’t judge Varvara harshly either, she is an angel too, she, too, has suffered wrong. She came to us for the summer, and she brought sixteen roubles she had earned by lessons and saved up, to go back with to Petersburg in September, that is now. But we took her money and lived on it, so now she has nothing to go back with. Though indeed she couldn’t go back, for she has to work for us like a slave. She is like an overdriven horse with all of us on her back. She waits on us all, mends and washes, sweeps the floor, puts mamma to bed. And mamma is capricious and tearful and insane! And now I can get a servant with this money, you understand, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I can get medicines for the dear creatures, I can send my student to Petersburg, I can buy beef, I can feed them properly. Good Lord, but it’s a dream!”\n\nAlyosha was delighted that he had brought him such happiness and that the poor fellow had consented to be made happy.\n\n“Stay, Alexey Fyodorovitch, stay,” the captain began to talk with frenzied rapidity, carried away by a new day-dream. “Do you know that Ilusha and I will perhaps really carry out our dream. We will buy a horse and cart, a black horse, he insists on its being black, and we will set off as we pretended the other day. I have an old friend, a lawyer in K. province, and I heard through a trustworthy man that if I were to go he’d give me a place as clerk in his office, so, who knows, maybe he would. So I’d just put mamma and Nina in the cart, and Ilusha could drive, and I’d walk, I’d walk.... Why, if I only succeed in getting one debt paid that’s owing me, I should have perhaps enough for that too!”\n\n“There would be enough!” cried Alyosha. “Katerina Ivanovna will send you as much more as you need, and you know, I have money too, take what you want, as you would from a brother, from a friend, you can give it back later.... (You’ll get rich, you’ll get rich!) And you know you couldn’t have a better idea than to move to another province! It would be the saving of you, especially of your boy—and you ought to go quickly, before the winter, before the cold. You must write to us when you are there, and we will always be brothers.... No, it’s not a dream!”\n\nAlyosha could have hugged him, he was so pleased. But glancing at him he stopped short. The man was standing with his neck outstretched and his lips protruding, with a pale and frenzied face. His lips were moving as though trying to articulate something; no sound came, but still his lips moved. It was uncanny.\n\n“What is it?” asked Alyosha, startled.\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch ... I ... you,” muttered the captain, faltering, looking at him with a strange, wild, fixed stare, and an air of desperate resolution. At the same time there was a sort of grin on his lips. “I ... you, sir ... wouldn’t you like me to show you a little trick I know?” he murmured, suddenly, in a firm rapid whisper, his voice no longer faltering.\n\n“What trick?”\n\n“A pretty trick,” whispered the captain. His mouth was twisted on the left side, his left eye was screwed up. He still stared at Alyosha.\n\n“What is the matter? What trick?” Alyosha cried, now thoroughly alarmed.\n\n“Why, look,” squealed the captain suddenly, and showing him the two notes which he had been holding by one corner between his thumb and forefinger during the conversation, he crumpled them up savagely and squeezed them tight in his right hand. “Do you see, do you see?” he shrieked, pale and infuriated. And suddenly flinging up his hand, he threw the crumpled notes on the sand. “Do you see?” he shrieked again, pointing to them. “Look there!”\n\nAnd with wild fury he began trampling them under his heel, gasping and exclaiming as he did so:\n\n“So much for your money! So much for your money! So much for your money! So much for your money!”\n\nSuddenly he darted back and drew himself up before Alyosha, and his whole figure expressed unutterable pride.\n\n“Tell those who sent you that the wisp of tow does not sell his honor,” he cried, raising his arm in the air. Then he turned quickly and began to run; but he had not run five steps before he turned completely round and kissed his hand to Alyosha. He ran another five paces and then turned round for the last time. This time his face was not contorted with laughter, but quivering all over with tears. In a tearful, faltering, sobbing voice he cried:\n\n“What should I say to my boy if I took money from you for our shame?”\n\nAnd then he ran on without turning. Alyosha looked after him, inexpressibly grieved. Oh, he saw that till the very last moment the man had not known he would crumple up and fling away the notes. He did not turn back. Alyosha knew he would not. He would not follow him and call him back, he knew why. When he was out of sight, Alyosha picked up the two notes. They were very much crushed and crumpled, and had been pressed into the sand, but were uninjured and even rustled like new ones when Alyosha unfolded them and smoothed them out. After smoothing them out, he folded them up, put them in his pocket and went to Katerina Ivanovna to report on the success of her commission.\n\n# Book V. Pro And Contra Chapter I. The Engagement\n\nMadame Hohlakov was again the first to meet Alyosha. She was flustered; something important had happened. Katerina Ivanovna’s hysterics had ended in a fainting fit, and then “a terrible, awful weakness had followed, she lay with her eyes turned up and was delirious. Now she was in a fever. They had sent for Herzenstube; they had sent for the aunts. The aunts were already here, but Herzenstube had not yet come. They were all sitting in her room, waiting. She was unconscious now, and what if it turned to brain fever!”\n\nMadame Hohlakov looked gravely alarmed. “This is serious, serious,” she added at every word, as though nothing that had happened to her before had been serious. Alyosha listened with distress, and was beginning to describe his adventures, but she interrupted him at the first words. She had not time to listen. She begged him to sit with Lise and wait for her there.\n\n“Lise,” she whispered almost in his ear, “Lise has greatly surprised me just now, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch. She touched me, too, and so my heart forgives her everything. Only fancy, as soon as you had gone, she began to be truly remorseful for having laughed at you to-day and yesterday, though she was not laughing at you, but only joking. But she was seriously sorry for it, almost ready to cry, so that I was quite surprised. She has never been really sorry for laughing at me, but has only made a joke of it. And you know she is laughing at me every minute. But this time she was in earnest. She thinks a great deal of your opinion, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and don’t take offense or be wounded by her if you can help it. I am never hard upon her, for she’s such a clever little thing. Would you believe it? She said just now that you were a friend of her childhood, ‘the greatest friend of her childhood’—just think of that—‘greatest friend’—and what about me? She has very strong feelings and memories, and, what’s more, she uses these phrases, most unexpected words, which come out all of a sudden when you least expect them. She spoke lately about a pine-tree, for instance: there used to be a pine-tree standing in our garden in her early childhood. Very likely it’s standing there still; so there’s no need to speak in the past tense. Pine-trees are not like people, Alexey Fyodorovitch, they don’t change quickly. ‘Mamma,’ she said, ‘I remember this pine-tree as in a dream,’ only she said something so original about it that I can’t repeat it. Besides, I’ve forgotten it. Well, good-by! I am so worried I feel I shall go out of my mind. Ah! Alexey Fyodorovitch, I’ve been out of my mind twice in my life. Go to Lise, cheer her up, as you always can so charmingly. Lise,” she cried, going to her door, “here I’ve brought you Alexey Fyodorovitch, whom you insulted so. He is not at all angry, I assure you; on the contrary, he is surprised that you could suppose so.”\n\n“Merci, maman. Come in, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”\n\nAlyosha went in. Lise looked rather embarrassed, and at once flushed crimson. She was evidently ashamed of something, and, as people always do in such cases, she began immediately talking of other things, as though they were of absorbing interest to her at the moment.\n\n“Mamma has just told me all about the two hundred roubles, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and your taking them to that poor officer ... and she told me all the awful story of how he had been insulted ... and you know, although mamma muddles things ... she always rushes from one thing to another ... I cried when I heard. Well, did you give him the money and how is that poor man getting on?”\n\n“The fact is I didn’t give it to him, and it’s a long story,” answered Alyosha, as though he, too, could think of nothing but his regret at having failed, yet Lise saw perfectly well that he, too, looked away, and that he, too, was trying to talk of other things.\n\nAlyosha sat down to the table and began to tell his story, but at the first words he lost his embarrassment and gained the whole of Lise’s attention as well. He spoke with deep feeling, under the influence of the strong impression he had just received, and he succeeded in telling his story well and circumstantially. In old days in Moscow he had been fond of coming to Lise and describing to her what had just happened to him, what he had read, or what he remembered of his childhood. Sometimes they had made day-dreams and woven whole romances together—generally cheerful and amusing ones. Now they both felt suddenly transported to the old days in Moscow, two years before. Lise was extremely touched by his story. Alyosha described Ilusha with warm feeling. When he finished describing how the luckless man trampled on the money, Lise could not help clasping her hands and crying out:\n\n“So you didn’t give him the money! So you let him run away! Oh, dear, you ought to have run after him!”\n\n“No, Lise; it’s better I didn’t run after him,” said Alyosha, getting up from his chair and walking thoughtfully across the room.\n\n“How so? How is it better? Now they are without food and their case is hopeless?”\n\n“Not hopeless, for the two hundred roubles will still come to them. He’ll take the money to-morrow. To-morrow he will be sure to take it,” said Alyosha, pacing up and down, pondering. “You see, Lise,” he went on, stopping suddenly before her, “I made one blunder, but that, even that, is all for the best.”\n\n“What blunder, and why is it for the best?”\n\n“I’ll tell you. He is a man of weak and timorous character; he has suffered so much and is very good-natured. I keep wondering why he took offense so suddenly, for I assure you, up to the last minute, he did not know that he was going to trample on the notes. And I think now that there was a great deal to offend him ... and it could not have been otherwise in his position.... To begin with, he was sore at having been so glad of the money in my presence and not having concealed it from me. If he had been pleased, but not so much; if he had not shown it; if he had begun affecting scruples and difficulties, as other people do when they take money, he might still endure to take it. But he was too genuinely delighted, and that was mortifying. Ah, Lise, he is a good and truthful man—that’s the worst of the whole business. All the while he talked, his voice was so weak, so broken, he talked so fast, so fast, he kept laughing such a laugh, or perhaps he was crying—yes, I am sure he was crying, he was so delighted—and he talked about his daughters—and about the situation he could get in another town.... And when he had poured out his heart, he felt ashamed at having shown me his inmost soul like that. So he began to hate me at once. He is one of those awfully sensitive poor people. What had made him feel most ashamed was that he had given in too soon and accepted me as a friend, you see. At first he almost flew at me and tried to intimidate me, but as soon as he saw the money he had begun embracing me; he kept touching me with his hands. This must have been how he came to feel it all so humiliating, and then I made that blunder, a very important one. I suddenly said to him that if he had not money enough to move to another town, we would give it to him, and, indeed, I myself would give him as much as he wanted out of my own money. That struck him all at once. Why, he thought, did I put myself forward to help him? You know, Lise, it’s awfully hard for a man who has been injured, when other people look at him as though they were his benefactors.... I’ve heard that; Father Zossima told me so. I don’t know how to put it, but I have often seen it myself. And I feel like that myself, too. And the worst of it was that though he did not know, up to the very last minute, that he would trample on the notes, he had a kind of presentiment of it, I am sure of that. That’s just what made him so ecstatic, that he had that presentiment.... And though it’s so dreadful, it’s all for the best. In fact, I believe nothing better could have happened.”\n\n“Why, why could nothing better have happened?” cried Lise, looking with great surprise at Alyosha.\n\n“Because if he had taken the money, in an hour after getting home, he would be crying with mortification, that’s just what would have happened. And most likely he would have come to me early to-morrow, and perhaps have flung the notes at me and trampled upon them as he did just now. But now he has gone home awfully proud and triumphant, though he knows he has ‘ruined himself.’ So now nothing could be easier than to make him accept the two hundred roubles by to-morrow, for he has already vindicated his honor, tossed away the money, and trampled it under foot.... He couldn’t know when he did it that I should bring it to him again to-morrow, and yet he is in terrible need of that money. Though he is proud of himself now, yet even to-day he’ll be thinking what a help he has lost. He will think of it more than ever at night, will dream of it, and by to-morrow morning he may be ready to run to me to ask forgiveness. It’s just then that I’ll appear. ‘Here, you are a proud man,’ I shall say: ‘you have shown it; but now take the money and forgive us!’ And then he will take it!”\n\nAlyosha was carried away with joy as he uttered his last words, “And then he will take it!” Lise clapped her hands.\n\n“Ah, that’s true! I understand that perfectly now. Ah, Alyosha, how do you know all this? So young and yet he knows what’s in the heart.... I should never have worked it out.”\n\n“The great thing now is to persuade him that he is on an equal footing with us, in spite of his taking money from us,” Alyosha went on in his excitement, “and not only on an equal, but even on a higher footing.”\n\n“‘On a higher footing’ is charming, Alexey Fyodorovitch; but go on, go on!”\n\n“You mean there isn’t such an expression as ‘on a higher footing’; but that doesn’t matter because—”\n\n“Oh, no, of course it doesn’t matter. Forgive me, Alyosha, dear.... You know, I scarcely respected you till now—that is I respected you but on an equal footing; but now I shall begin to respect you on a higher footing. Don’t be angry, dear, at my joking,” she put in at once, with strong feeling. “I am absurd and small, but you, you! Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch. Isn’t there in all our analysis—I mean your analysis ... no, better call it ours—aren’t we showing contempt for him, for that poor man—in analyzing his soul like this, as it were, from above, eh? In deciding so certainly that he will take the money?”\n\n“No, Lise, it’s not contempt,” Alyosha answered, as though he had prepared himself for the question. “I was thinking of that on the way here. How can it be contempt when we are all like him, when we are all just the same as he is? For you know we are just the same, no better. If we are better, we should have been just the same in his place.... I don’t know about you, Lise, but I consider that I have a sordid soul in many ways, and his soul is not sordid; on the contrary, full of fine feeling.... No, Lise, I have no contempt for him. Do you know, Lise, my elder told me once to care for most people exactly as one would for children, and for some of them as one would for the sick in hospitals.”\n\n“Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, dear, let us care for people as we would for the sick!”\n\n“Let us, Lise; I am ready. Though I am not altogether ready in myself. I am sometimes very impatient and at other times I don’t see things. It’s different with you.”\n\n“Ah, I don’t believe it! Alexey Fyodorovitch, how happy I am!”\n\n“I am so glad you say so, Lise.”\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch, you are wonderfully good, but you are sometimes sort of formal.... And yet you are not a bit formal really. Go to the door, open it gently, and see whether mamma is listening,” said Lise, in a nervous, hurried whisper.\n\nAlyosha went, opened the door, and reported that no one was listening.\n\n“Come here, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” Lise went on, flushing redder and redder. “Give me your hand—that’s right. I have to make a great confession, I didn’t write to you yesterday in joke, but in earnest,” and she hid her eyes with her hand. It was evident that she was greatly ashamed of the confession.\n\nSuddenly she snatched his hand and impulsively kissed it three times.\n\n“Ah, Lise, what a good thing!” cried Alyosha joyfully. “You know, I was perfectly sure you were in earnest.”\n\n“Sure? Upon my word!” She put aside his hand, but did not leave go of it, blushing hotly, and laughing a little happy laugh. “I kiss his hand and he says, ‘What a good thing!’”\n\nBut her reproach was undeserved. Alyosha, too, was greatly overcome.\n\n“I should like to please you always, Lise, but I don’t know how to do it,” he muttered, blushing too.\n\n“Alyosha, dear, you are cold and rude. Do you see? He has chosen me as his wife and is quite settled about it. He is sure I was in earnest. What a thing to say! Why, that’s impertinence—that’s what it is.”\n\n“Why, was it wrong of me to feel sure?” Alyosha asked, laughing suddenly.\n\n“Ah, Alyosha, on the contrary, it was delightfully right,” cried Lise, looking tenderly and happily at him.\n\nAlyosha stood still, holding her hand in his. Suddenly he stooped down and kissed her on her lips.\n\n“Oh, what are you doing?” cried Lise. Alyosha was terribly abashed.\n\n“Oh, forgive me if I shouldn’t.... Perhaps I’m awfully stupid.... You said I was cold, so I kissed you.... But I see it was stupid.”\n\nLise laughed, and hid her face in her hands. “And in that dress!” she ejaculated in the midst of her mirth. But she suddenly ceased laughing and became serious, almost stern.\n\n“Alyosha, we must put off kissing. We are not ready for that yet, and we shall have a long time to wait,” she ended suddenly. “Tell me rather why you who are so clever, so intellectual, so observant, choose a little idiot, an invalid like me? Ah, Alyosha, I am awfully happy, for I don’t deserve you a bit.”\n\n“You do, Lise. I shall be leaving the monastery altogether in a few days. If I go into the world, I must marry. I know that. He told me to marry, too. Whom could I marry better than you—and who would have me except you? I have been thinking it over. In the first place, you’ve known me from a child and you’ve a great many qualities I haven’t. You are more light-hearted than I am; above all, you are more innocent than I am. I have been brought into contact with many, many things already.... Ah, you don’t know, but I, too, am a Karamazov. What does it matter if you do laugh and make jokes, and at me, too? Go on laughing. I am so glad you do. You laugh like a little child, but you think like a martyr.”\n\n“Like a martyr? How?”\n\n“Yes, Lise, your question just now: whether we weren’t showing contempt for that poor man by dissecting his soul—that was the question of a sufferer.... You see, I don’t know how to express it, but any one who thinks of such questions is capable of suffering. Sitting in your invalid chair you must have thought over many things already.”\n\n“Alyosha, give me your hand. Why are you taking it away?” murmured Lise in a failing voice, weak with happiness. “Listen, Alyosha. What will you wear when you come out of the monastery? What sort of suit? Don’t laugh, don’t be angry, it’s very, very important to me.”\n\n“I haven’t thought about the suit, Lise; but I’ll wear whatever you like.”\n\n“I should like you to have a dark blue velvet coat, a white piqué waistcoat, and a soft gray felt hat.... Tell me, did you believe that I didn’t care for you when I said I didn’t mean what I wrote?”\n\n“No, I didn’t believe it.”\n\n“Oh, you insupportable person, you are incorrigible.”\n\n“You see, I knew that you—seemed to care for me, but I pretended to believe that you didn’t care for me to make it—easier for you.”\n\n“That makes it worse! Worse and better than all! Alyosha, I am awfully fond of you. Just before you came this morning, I tried my fortune. I decided I would ask you for my letter, and if you brought it out calmly and gave it to me (as might have been expected from you) it would mean that you did not love me at all, that you felt nothing, and were simply a stupid boy, good for nothing, and that I am ruined. But you left the letter at home and that cheered me. You left it behind on purpose, so as not to give it back, because you knew I would ask for it? That was it, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Ah, Lise, it was not so a bit. The letter is with me now, and it was this morning, in this pocket. Here it is.”\n\nAlyosha pulled the letter out laughing, and showed it her at a distance.\n\n“But I am not going to give it to you. Look at it from here.”\n\n“Why, then you told a lie? You, a monk, told a lie!”\n\n“I told a lie if you like,” Alyosha laughed, too. “I told a lie so as not to give you back the letter. It’s very precious to me,” he added suddenly, with strong feeling, and again he flushed. “It always will be, and I won’t give it up to any one!”\n\nLise looked at him joyfully. “Alyosha,” she murmured again, “look at the door. Isn’t mamma listening?”\n\n“Very well, Lise, I’ll look; but wouldn’t it be better not to look? Why suspect your mother of such meanness?”\n\n“What meanness? As for her spying on her daughter, it’s her right, it’s not meanness!” cried Lise, firing up. “You may be sure, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that when I am a mother, if I have a daughter like myself I shall certainly spy on her!”\n\n“Really, Lise? That’s not right.”\n\n“Oh, my goodness! What has meanness to do with it? If she were listening to some ordinary worldly conversation, it would be meanness, but when her own daughter is shut up with a young man.... Listen, Alyosha, do you know I shall spy upon you as soon as we are married, and let me tell you I shall open all your letters and read them, so you may as well be prepared.”\n\n“Yes, of course, if so—” muttered Alyosha, “only it’s not right.”\n\n“Ah, how contemptuous! Alyosha, dear, we won’t quarrel the very first day. I’d better tell you the whole truth. Of course, it’s very wrong to spy on people, and, of course, I am not right and you are, only I shall spy on you all the same.”\n\n“Do, then; you won’t find out anything,” laughed Alyosha.\n\n“And, Alyosha, will you give in to me? We must decide that too.”\n\n“I shall be delighted to, Lise, and certain to, only not in the most important things. Even if you don’t agree with me, I shall do my duty in the most important things.”\n\n“That’s right; but let me tell you I am ready to give in to you not only in the most important matters, but in everything. And I am ready to vow to do so now—in everything, and for all my life!” cried Lise fervently, “and I’ll do it gladly, gladly! What’s more, I’ll swear never to spy on you, never once, never to read one of your letters. For you are right and I am not. And though I shall be awfully tempted to spy, I know that I won’t do it since you consider it dishonorable. You are my conscience now.... Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch, why have you been so sad lately—both yesterday and to-day? I know you have a lot of anxiety and trouble, but I see you have some special grief besides, some secret one, perhaps?”\n\n“Yes, Lise, I have a secret one, too,” answered Alyosha mournfully. “I see you love me, since you guessed that.”\n\n“What grief? What about? Can you tell me?” asked Lise with timid entreaty.\n\n“I’ll tell you later, Lise—afterwards,” said Alyosha, confused. “Now you wouldn’t understand it perhaps—and perhaps I couldn’t explain it.”\n\n“I know your brothers and your father are worrying you, too.”\n\n“Yes, my brothers too,” murmured Alyosha, pondering.\n\n“I don’t like your brother Ivan, Alyosha,” said Lise suddenly.\n\nHe noticed this remark with some surprise, but did not answer it.\n\n“My brothers are destroying themselves,” he went on, “my father, too. And they are destroying others with them. It’s ‘the primitive force of the Karamazovs,’ as Father Païssy said the other day, a crude, unbridled, earthly force. Does the spirit of God move above that force? Even that I don’t know. I only know that I, too, am a Karamazov.... Me a monk, a monk! Am I a monk, Lise? You said just now that I was.”\n\n“Yes, I did.”\n\n“And perhaps I don’t even believe in God.”\n\n“You don’t believe? What is the matter?” said Lise quietly and gently. But Alyosha did not answer. There was something too mysterious, too subjective in these last words of his, perhaps obscure to himself, but yet torturing him.\n\n“And now on the top of it all, my friend, the best man in the world, is going, is leaving the earth! If you knew, Lise, how bound up in soul I am with him! And then I shall be left alone.... I shall come to you, Lise.... For the future we will be together.”\n\n“Yes, together, together! Henceforward we shall be always together, all our lives! Listen, kiss me, I allow you.”\n\nAlyosha kissed her.\n\n“Come, now go. Christ be with you!” and she made the sign of the cross over him. “Make haste back to him while he is alive. I see I’ve kept you cruelly. I’ll pray to-day for him and you. Alyosha, we shall be happy! Shall we be happy, shall we?”\n\n“I believe we shall, Lise.”\n\nAlyosha thought it better not to go in to Madame Hohlakov and was going out of the house without saying good-by to her. But no sooner had he opened the door than he found Madame Hohlakov standing before him. From the first word Alyosha guessed that she had been waiting on purpose to meet him.\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch, this is awful. This is all childish nonsense and ridiculous. I trust you won’t dream—It’s foolishness, nothing but foolishness!” she said, attacking him at once.\n\n“Only don’t tell her that,” said Alyosha, “or she will be upset, and that’s bad for her now.”\n\n“Sensible advice from a sensible young man. Am I to understand that you only agreed with her from compassion for her invalid state, because you didn’t want to irritate her by contradiction?”\n\n“Oh, no, not at all. I was quite serious in what I said,” Alyosha declared stoutly.\n\n“To be serious about it is impossible, unthinkable, and in the first place I shall never be at home to you again, and I shall take her away, you may be sure of that.”\n\n“But why?” asked Alyosha. “It’s all so far off. We may have to wait another year and a half.”\n\n“Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that’s true, of course, and you’ll have time to quarrel and separate a thousand times in a year and a half. But I am so unhappy! Though it’s such nonsense, it’s a great blow to me. I feel like Famusov in the last scene of Sorrow from Wit. You are Tchatsky and she is Sofya, and, only fancy, I’ve run down to meet you on the stairs, and in the play the fatal scene takes place on the staircase. I heard it all; I almost dropped. So this is the explanation of her dreadful night and her hysterics of late! It means love to the daughter but death to the mother. I might as well be in my grave at once. And a more serious matter still, what is this letter she has written? Show it me at once, at once!”\n\n“No, there’s no need. Tell me, how is Katerina Ivanovna now? I must know.”\n\n“She still lies in delirium; she has not regained consciousness. Her aunts are here; but they do nothing but sigh and give themselves airs. Herzenstube came, and he was so alarmed that I didn’t know what to do for him. I nearly sent for a doctor to look after him. He was driven home in my carriage. And on the top of it all, you and this letter! It’s true nothing can happen for a year and a half. In the name of all that’s holy, in the name of your dying elder, show me that letter, Alexey Fyodorovitch. I’m her mother. Hold it in your hand, if you like, and I will read it so.”\n\n“No, I won’t show it to you. Even if she sanctioned it, I wouldn’t. I am coming to-morrow, and if you like, we can talk over many things, but now good-by!”\n\nAnd Alyosha ran downstairs and into the street.\n\n# Chapter II. Smerdyakov With A Guitar\n\nHe had no time to lose indeed. Even while he was saying good-by to Lise, the thought had struck him that he must attempt some stratagem to find his brother Dmitri, who was evidently keeping out of his way. It was getting late, nearly three o’clock. Alyosha’s whole soul turned to the monastery, to his dying saint, but the necessity of seeing Dmitri outweighed everything. The conviction that a great inevitable catastrophe was about to happen grew stronger in Alyosha’s mind with every hour. What that catastrophe was, and what he would say at that moment to his brother, he could perhaps not have said definitely. “Even if my benefactor must die without me, anyway I won’t have to reproach myself all my life with the thought that I might have saved something and did not, but passed by and hastened home. If I do as I intend, I shall be following his great precept.”\n\nHis plan was to catch his brother Dmitri unawares, to climb over the fence, as he had the day before, get into the garden and sit in the summer-house. If Dmitri were not there, thought Alyosha, he would not announce himself to Foma or the women of the house, but would remain hidden in the summer-house, even if he had to wait there till evening. If, as before, Dmitri were lying in wait for Grushenka to come, he would be very likely to come to the summer-house. Alyosha did not, however, give much thought to the details of his plan, but resolved to act upon it, even if it meant not getting back to the monastery that day.\n\nEverything happened without hindrance, he climbed over the hurdle almost in the same spot as the day before, and stole into the summer-house unseen. He did not want to be noticed. The woman of the house and Foma too, if he were here, might be loyal to his brother and obey his instructions, and so refuse to let Alyosha come into the garden, or might warn Dmitri that he was being sought and inquired for.\n\nThere was no one in the summer-house. Alyosha sat down and began to wait. He looked round the summer-house, which somehow struck him as a great deal more ancient than before. Though the day was just as fine as yesterday, it seemed a wretched little place this time. There was a circle on the table, left no doubt from the glass of brandy having been spilt the day before. Foolish and irrelevant ideas strayed about his mind, as they always do in a time of tedious waiting. He wondered, for instance, why he had sat down precisely in the same place as before, why not in the other seat. At last he felt very depressed—depressed by suspense and uncertainty. But he had not sat there more than a quarter of an hour, when he suddenly heard the thrum of a guitar somewhere quite close. People were sitting, or had only just sat down, somewhere in the bushes not more than twenty paces away. Alyosha suddenly recollected that on coming out of the summer-house the day before, he had caught a glimpse of an old green low garden-seat among the bushes on the left, by the fence. The people must be sitting on it now. Who were they?\n\nA man’s voice suddenly began singing in a sugary falsetto, accompanying himself on the guitar:\n\nWith invincible force I am bound to my dear. O Lord, have mercy On her and on me! On her and on me! On her and on me!\n\nThe voice ceased. It was a lackey’s tenor and a lackey’s song. Another voice, a woman’s, suddenly asked insinuatingly and bashfully, though with mincing affectation:\n\n“Why haven’t you been to see us for so long, Pavel Fyodorovitch? Why do you always look down upon us?”\n\n“Not at all,” answered a man’s voice politely, but with emphatic dignity. It was clear that the man had the best of the position, and that the woman was making advances. “I believe the man must be Smerdyakov,” thought Alyosha, “from his voice. And the lady must be the daughter of the house here, who has come from Moscow, the one who wears the dress with a tail and goes to Marfa for soup.”\n\n“I am awfully fond of verses of all kinds, if they rhyme,” the woman’s voice continued. “Why don’t you go on?”\n\nThe man sang again:\n\nWhat do I care for royal wealth If but my dear one be in health? Lord have mercy On her and on me! On her and on me! On her and on me!\n\n“It was even better last time,” observed the woman’s voice. “You sang ‘If my darling be in health’; it sounded more tender. I suppose you’ve forgotten to-day.”\n\n“Poetry is rubbish!” said Smerdyakov curtly.\n\n“Oh, no! I am very fond of poetry.”\n\n“So far as it’s poetry, it’s essential rubbish. Consider yourself, who ever talks in rhyme? And if we were all to talk in rhyme, even though it were decreed by government, we shouldn’t say much, should we? Poetry is no good, Marya Kondratyevna.”\n\n“How clever you are! How is it you’ve gone so deep into everything?” The woman’s voice was more and more insinuating.\n\n“I could have done better than that. I could have known more than that, if it had not been for my destiny from my childhood up. I would have shot a man in a duel if he called me names because I am descended from a filthy beggar and have no father. And they used to throw it in my teeth in Moscow. It had reached them from here, thanks to Grigory Vassilyevitch. Grigory Vassilyevitch blames me for rebelling against my birth, but I would have sanctioned their killing me before I was born that I might not have come into the world at all. They used to say in the market, and your mamma too, with great lack of delicacy, set off telling me that her hair was like a mat on her head, and that she was short of five foot by a wee bit. Why talk of a wee bit while she might have said ‘a little bit,’ like every one else? She wanted to make it touching, a regular peasant’s feeling. Can a Russian peasant be said to feel, in comparison with an educated man? He can’t be said to have feeling at all, in his ignorance. From my childhood up when I hear ‘a wee bit,’ I am ready to burst with rage. I hate all Russia, Marya Kondratyevna.”\n\n“If you’d been a cadet in the army, or a young hussar, you wouldn’t have talked like that, but would have drawn your saber to defend all Russia.”\n\n“I don’t want to be a hussar, Marya Kondratyevna, and, what’s more, I should like to abolish all soldiers.”\n\n“And when an enemy comes, who is going to defend us?”\n\n“There’s no need of defense. In 1812 there was a great invasion of Russia by Napoleon, first Emperor of the French, father of the present one, and it would have been a good thing if they had conquered us. A clever nation would have conquered a very stupid one and annexed it. We should have had quite different institutions.”\n\n“Are they so much better in their own country than we are? I wouldn’t change a dandy I know of for three young Englishmen,” observed Marya Kondratyevna tenderly, doubtless accompanying her words with a most languishing glance.\n\n“That’s as one prefers.”\n\n“But you are just like a foreigner—just like a most gentlemanly foreigner. I tell you that, though it makes me bashful.”\n\n“If you care to know, the folks there and ours here are just alike in their vice. They are swindlers, only there the scoundrel wears polished boots and here he grovels in filth and sees no harm in it. The Russian people want thrashing, as Fyodor Pavlovitch said very truly yesterday, though he is mad, and all his children.”\n\n“You said yourself you had such a respect for Ivan Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“But he said I was a stinking lackey. He thinks that I might be unruly. He is mistaken there. If I had a certain sum in my pocket, I would have left here long ago. Dmitri Fyodorovitch is lower than any lackey in his behavior, in his mind, and in his poverty. He doesn’t know how to do anything, and yet he is respected by every one. I may be only a soup-maker, but with luck I could open a café restaurant in Petrovka, in Moscow, for my cookery is something special, and there’s no one in Moscow, except the foreigners, whose cookery is anything special. Dmitri Fyodorovitch is a beggar, but if he were to challenge the son of the first count in the country, he’d fight him. Though in what way is he better than I am? For he is ever so much stupider than I am. Look at the money he has wasted without any need!”\n\n“It must be lovely, a duel,” Marya Kondratyevna observed suddenly.\n\n“How so?”\n\n“It must be so dreadful and so brave, especially when young officers with pistols in their hands pop at one another for the sake of some lady. A perfect picture! Ah, if only girls were allowed to look on, I’d give anything to see one!”\n\n“It’s all very well when you are firing at some one, but when he is firing straight in your mug, you must feel pretty silly. You’d be glad to run away, Marya Kondratyevna.”\n\n“You don’t mean you would run away?” But Smerdyakov did not deign to reply. After a moment’s silence the guitar tinkled again, and he sang again in the same falsetto:\n\nWhatever you may say, I shall go far away. Life will be bright and gay In the city far away. I shall not grieve, I shall not grieve at all, I don’t intend to grieve at all.\n\nThen something unexpected happened. Alyosha suddenly sneezed. They were silent. Alyosha got up and walked towards them. He found Smerdyakov dressed up and wearing polished boots, his hair pomaded, and perhaps curled. The guitar lay on the garden-seat. His companion was the daughter of the house, wearing a light-blue dress with a train two yards long. She was young and would not have been bad-looking, but that her face was so round and terribly freckled.\n\n“Will my brother Dmitri soon be back?” asked Alyosha with as much composure as he could.\n\nSmerdyakov got up slowly; Marya Kondratyevna rose too.\n“How am I to know about Dmitri Fyodorovitch? It’s not as if I were his keeper,” answered Smerdyakov quietly, distinctly, and superciliously.\n\n“But I simply asked whether you do know?” Alyosha explained.\n\n“I know nothing of his whereabouts and don’t want to.”\n\n“But my brother told me that you let him know all that goes on in the house, and promised to let him know when Agrafena Alexandrovna comes.”\n\nSmerdyakov turned a deliberate, unmoved glance upon him.\n\n“And how did you get in this time, since the gate was bolted an hour ago?” he asked, looking at Alyosha.\n\n“I came in from the back-alley, over the fence, and went straight to the summer-house. I hope you’ll forgive me,” he added, addressing Marya Kondratyevna. “I was in a hurry to find my brother.”\n\n“Ach, as though we could take it amiss in you!” drawled Marya Kondratyevna, flattered by Alyosha’s apology. “For Dmitri Fyodorovitch often goes to the summer-house in that way. We don’t know he is here and he is sitting in the summer-house.”\n\n“I am very anxious to find him, or to learn from you where he is now. Believe me, it’s on business of great importance to him.”\n\n“He never tells us,” lisped Marya Kondratyevna.\n\n“Though I used to come here as a friend,” Smerdyakov began again, “Dmitri Fyodorovitch has pestered me in a merciless way even here by his incessant questions about the master. ‘What news?’ he’ll ask. ‘What’s going on in there now? Who’s coming and going?’ and can’t I tell him something more. Twice already he’s threatened me with death.”\n\n“With death?” Alyosha exclaimed in surprise.\n\n“Do you suppose he’d think much of that, with his temper, which you had a chance of observing yourself yesterday? He says if I let Agrafena Alexandrovna in and she passes the night there, I’ll be the first to suffer for it. I am terribly afraid of him, and if I were not even more afraid of doing so, I ought to let the police know. God only knows what he might not do!”\n\n“His honor said to him the other day, ‘I’ll pound you in a mortar!’” added Marya Kondratyevna.\n\n“Oh, if it’s pounding in a mortar, it may be only talk,” observed Alyosha. “If I could meet him, I might speak to him about that too.”\n\n“Well, the only thing I can tell you is this,” said Smerdyakov, as though thinking better of it; “I am here as an old friend and neighbor, and it would be odd if I didn’t come. On the other hand, Ivan Fyodorovitch sent me first thing this morning to your brother’s lodging in Lake Street, without a letter, but with a message to Dmitri Fyodorovitch to go to dine with him at the restaurant here, in the market-place. I went, but didn’t find Dmitri Fyodorovitch at home, though it was eight o’clock. ‘He’s been here, but he is quite gone,’ those were the very words of his landlady. It’s as though there was an understanding between them. Perhaps at this moment he is in the restaurant with Ivan Fyodorovitch, for Ivan Fyodorovitch has not been home to dinner and Fyodor Pavlovitch dined alone an hour ago, and is gone to lie down. But I beg you most particularly not to speak of me and of what I have told you, for he’d kill me for nothing at all.”\n\n“Brother Ivan invited Dmitri to the restaurant to-day?” repeated Alyosha quickly.\n\n“That’s so.”\n\n“The Metropolis tavern in the market-place?”\n\n“The very same.”\n\n“That’s quite likely,” cried Alyosha, much excited. “Thank you, Smerdyakov; that’s important. I’ll go there at once.”\n\n“Don’t betray me,” Smerdyakov called after him.\n\n“Oh, no, I’ll go to the tavern as though by chance. Don’t be anxious.”\n\n“But wait a minute, I’ll open the gate to you,” cried Marya Kondratyevna.\n\n“No; it’s a short cut, I’ll get over the fence again.”\n\nWhat he had heard threw Alyosha into great agitation. He ran to the tavern. It was impossible for him to go into the tavern in his monastic dress, but he could inquire at the entrance for his brothers and call them down. But just as he reached the tavern, a window was flung open, and his brother Ivan called down to him from it.\n\n“Alyosha, can’t you come up here to me? I shall be awfully grateful.”\n\n“To be sure I can, only I don’t quite know whether in this dress—”\n\n“But I am in a room apart. Come up the steps; I’ll run down to meet you.”\n\nA minute later Alyosha was sitting beside his brother. Ivan was alone dining.\n\n# Chapter III. The Brothers Make Friends\n\nIvan was not, however, in a separate room, but only in a place shut off by a screen, so that it was unseen by other people in the room. It was the first room from the entrance with a buffet along the wall. Waiters were continually darting to and fro in it. The only customer in the room was an old retired military man drinking tea in a corner. But there was the usual bustle going on in the other rooms of the tavern; there were shouts for the waiters, the sound of popping corks, the click of billiard balls, the drone of the organ. Alyosha knew that Ivan did not usually visit this tavern and disliked taverns in general. So he must have come here, he reflected, simply to meet Dmitri by arrangement. Yet Dmitri was not there.\n\n“Shall I order you fish, soup or anything. You don’t live on tea alone, I suppose,” cried Ivan, apparently delighted at having got hold of Alyosha. He had finished dinner and was drinking tea.\n\n“Let me have soup, and tea afterwards, I am hungry,” said Alyosha gayly.\n\n“And cherry jam? They have it here. You remember how you used to love cherry jam when you were little?”\n\n“You remember that? Let me have jam too, I like it still.”\n\nIvan rang for the waiter and ordered soup, jam and tea.\n\n“I remember everything, Alyosha, I remember you till you were eleven, I was nearly fifteen. There’s such a difference between fifteen and eleven that brothers are never companions at those ages. I don’t know whether I was fond of you even. When I went away to Moscow for the first few years I never thought of you at all. Then, when you came to Moscow yourself, we only met once somewhere, I believe. And now I’ve been here more than three months, and so far we have scarcely said a word to each other. To-morrow I am going away, and I was just thinking as I sat here how I could see you to say good-by and just then you passed.”\n\n“Were you very anxious to see me, then?”\n\n“Very. I want to get to know you once for all, and I want you to know me. And then to say good-by. I believe it’s always best to get to know people just before leaving them. I’ve noticed how you’ve been looking at me these three months. There has been a continual look of expectation in your eyes, and I can’t endure that. That’s how it is I’ve kept away from you. But in the end I have learned to respect you. The little man stands firm, I thought. Though I am laughing, I am serious. You do stand firm, don’t you? I like people who are firm like that whatever it is they stand by, even if they are such little fellows as you. Your expectant eyes ceased to annoy me, I grew fond of them in the end, those expectant eyes. You seem to love me for some reason, Alyosha?”\n\n“I do love you, Ivan. Dmitri says of you—Ivan is a tomb! I say of you, Ivan is a riddle. You are a riddle to me even now. But I understand something in you, and I did not understand it till this morning.”\n\n“What’s that?” laughed Ivan.\n\n“You won’t be angry?” Alyosha laughed too.\n\n“Well?”\n\n“That you are just as young as other young men of three and twenty, that you are just a young and fresh and nice boy, green in fact! Now, have I insulted you dreadfully?”\n\n“On the contrary, I am struck by a coincidence,” cried Ivan, warmly and good-humoredly. “Would you believe it that ever since that scene with her, I have thought of nothing else but my youthful greenness, and just as though you guessed that, you begin about it. Do you know I’ve been sitting here thinking to myself: that if I didn’t believe in life, if I lost faith in the woman I love, lost faith in the order of things, were convinced in fact that everything is a disorderly, damnable, and perhaps devil-ridden chaos, if I were struck by every horror of man’s disillusionment—still I should want to live and, having once tasted of the cup, I would not turn away from it till I had drained it! At thirty, though, I shall be sure to leave the cup, even if I’ve not emptied it, and turn away—where I don’t know. But till I am thirty, I know that my youth will triumph over everything—every disillusionment, every disgust with life. I’ve asked myself many times whether there is in the world any despair that would overcome this frantic and perhaps unseemly thirst for life in me, and I’ve come to the conclusion that there isn’t, that is till I am thirty, and then I shall lose it of myself, I fancy. Some driveling consumptive moralists—and poets especially—often call that thirst for life base. It’s a feature of the Karamazovs, it’s true, that thirst for life regardless of everything; you have it no doubt too, but why is it base? The centripetal force on our planet is still fearfully strong, Alyosha. I have a longing for life, and I go on living in spite of logic. Though I may not believe in the order of the universe, yet I love the sticky little leaves as they open in spring. I love the blue sky, I love some people, whom one loves you know sometimes without knowing why. I love some great deeds done by men, though I’ve long ceased perhaps to have faith in them, yet from old habit one’s heart prizes them. Here they have brought the soup for you, eat it, it will do you good. It’s first-rate soup, they know how to make it here. I want to travel in Europe, Alyosha, I shall set off from here. And yet I know that I am only going to a graveyard, but it’s a most precious graveyard, that’s what it is! Precious are the dead that lie there, every stone over them speaks of such burning life in the past, of such passionate faith in their work, their truth, their struggle and their science, that I know I shall fall on the ground and kiss those stones and weep over them; though I’m convinced in my heart that it’s long been nothing but a graveyard. And I shall not weep from despair, but simply because I shall be happy in my tears, I shall steep my soul in my emotion. I love the sticky leaves in spring, the blue sky—that’s all it is. It’s not a matter of intellect or logic, it’s loving with one’s inside, with one’s stomach. One loves the first strength of one’s youth. Do you understand anything of my tirade, Alyosha?” Ivan laughed suddenly.\n\n“I understand too well, Ivan. One longs to love with one’s inside, with one’s stomach. You said that so well and I am awfully glad that you have such a longing for life,” cried Alyosha. “I think every one should love life above everything in the world.”\n\n“Love life more than the meaning of it?”\n\n“Certainly, love it, regardless of logic as you say, it must be regardless of logic, and it’s only then one will understand the meaning of it. I have thought so a long time. Half your work is done, Ivan, you love life, now you’ve only to try to do the second half and you are saved.”\n\n“You are trying to save me, but perhaps I am not lost! And what does your second half mean?”\n\n“Why, one has to raise up your dead, who perhaps have not died after all. Come, let me have tea. I am so glad of our talk, Ivan.”\n\n“I see you are feeling inspired. I am awfully fond of such professions de foi from such—novices. You are a steadfast person, Alexey. Is it true that you mean to leave the monastery?”\n\n“Yes, my elder sends me out into the world.”\n\n“We shall see each other then in the world. We shall meet before I am thirty, when I shall begin to turn aside from the cup. Father doesn’t want to turn aside from his cup till he is seventy, he dreams of hanging on to eighty in fact, so he says. He means it only too seriously, though he is a buffoon. He stands on a firm rock, too, he stands on his sensuality—though after we are thirty, indeed, there may be nothing else to stand on.... But to hang on to seventy is nasty, better only to thirty; one might retain ‘a shadow of nobility’ by deceiving oneself. Have you seen Dmitri to-day?”\n\n“No, but I saw Smerdyakov,” and Alyosha rapidly, though minutely, described his meeting with Smerdyakov. Ivan began listening anxiously and questioned him.\n\n“But he begged me not to tell Dmitri that he had told me about him,” added Alyosha. Ivan frowned and pondered.\n\n“Are you frowning on Smerdyakov’s account?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, on his account. Damn him, I certainly did want to see Dmitri, but now there’s no need,” said Ivan reluctantly.\n\n“But are you really going so soon, brother?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What of Dmitri and father? how will it end?” asked Alyosha anxiously.\n\n“You are always harping upon it! What have I to do with it? Am I my brother Dmitri’s keeper?” Ivan snapped irritably, but then he suddenly smiled bitterly. “Cain’s answer about his murdered brother, wasn’t it? Perhaps that’s what you’re thinking at this moment? Well, damn it all, I can’t stay here to be their keeper, can I? I’ve finished what I had to do, and I am going. Do you imagine I am jealous of Dmitri, that I’ve been trying to steal his beautiful Katerina Ivanovna for the last three months? Nonsense, I had business of my own. I finished it. I am going. I finished it just now, you were witness.”\n\n“At Katerina Ivanovna’s?”\n\n“Yes, and I’ve released myself once for all. And after all, what have I to do with Dmitri? Dmitri doesn’t come in. I had my own business to settle with Katerina Ivanovna. You know, on the contrary, that Dmitri behaved as though there was an understanding between us. I didn’t ask him to do it, but he solemnly handed her over to me and gave us his blessing. It’s all too funny. Ah, Alyosha, if you only knew how light my heart is now! Would you believe, it, I sat here eating my dinner and was nearly ordering champagne to celebrate my first hour of freedom. Tfoo! It’s been going on nearly six months, and all at once I’ve thrown it off. I could never have guessed even yesterday, how easy it would be to put an end to it if I wanted.”\n\n“You are speaking of your love, Ivan?”\n\n“Of my love, if you like. I fell in love with the young lady, I worried myself over her and she worried me. I sat watching over her ... and all at once it’s collapsed! I spoke this morning with inspiration, but I went away and roared with laughter. Would you believe it? Yes, it’s the literal truth.”\n\n“You seem very merry about it now,” observed Alyosha, looking into his face, which had suddenly grown brighter.\n\n“But how could I tell that I didn’t care for her a bit! Ha ha! It appears after all I didn’t. And yet how she attracted me! How attractive she was just now when I made my speech! And do you know she attracts me awfully even now, yet how easy it is to leave her. Do you think I am boasting?”\n\n“No, only perhaps it wasn’t love.”\n\n“Alyosha,” laughed Ivan, “don’t make reflections about love, it’s unseemly for you. How you rushed into the discussion this morning! I’ve forgotten to kiss you for it.... But how she tormented me! It certainly was sitting by a ‘laceration.’ Ah, she knew how I loved her! She loved me and not Dmitri,” Ivan insisted gayly. “Her feeling for Dmitri was simply a self-laceration. All I told her just now was perfectly true, but the worst of it is, it may take her fifteen or twenty years to find out that she doesn’t care for Dmitri, and loves me whom she torments, and perhaps she may never find it out at all, in spite of her lesson to-day. Well, it’s better so; I can simply go away for good. By the way, how is she now? What happened after I departed?”\n\nAlyosha told him she had been hysterical, and that she was now, he heard, unconscious and delirious.\n\n“Isn’t Madame Hohlakov laying it on?”\n\n“I think not.”\n\n“I must find out. Nobody dies of hysterics, though. They don’t matter. God gave woman hysterics as a relief. I won’t go to her at all. Why push myself forward again?”\n\n“But you told her that she had never cared for you.”\n\n“I did that on purpose. Alyosha, shall I call for some champagne? Let us drink to my freedom. Ah, if only you knew how glad I am!”\n\n“No, brother, we had better not drink,” said Alyosha suddenly. “Besides I feel somehow depressed.”\n\n“Yes, you’ve been depressed a long time, I’ve noticed it.”\n\n“Have you settled to go to-morrow morning, then?”\n\n“Morning? I didn’t say I should go in the morning.... But perhaps it may be the morning. Would you believe it, I dined here to-day only to avoid dining with the old man, I loathe him so. I should have left long ago, so far as he is concerned. But why are you so worried about my going away? We’ve plenty of time before I go, an eternity!”\n\n“If you are going away to-morrow, what do you mean by an eternity?”\n\n“But what does it matter to us?” laughed Ivan. “We’ve time enough for our talk, for what brought us here. Why do you look so surprised? Answer: why have we met here? To talk of my love for Katerina Ivanovna, of the old man and Dmitri? of foreign travel? of the fatal position of Russia? Of the Emperor Napoleon? Is that it?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then you know what for. It’s different for other people; but we in our green youth have to settle the eternal questions first of all. That’s what we care about. Young Russia is talking about nothing but the eternal questions now. Just when the old folks are all taken up with practical questions. Why have you been looking at me in expectation for the last three months? To ask me, ‘What do you believe, or don’t you believe at all?’ That’s what your eyes have been meaning for these three months, haven’t they?”\n\n“Perhaps so,” smiled Alyosha. “You are not laughing at me, now, Ivan?”\n\n“Me laughing! I don’t want to wound my little brother who has been watching me with such expectation for three months. Alyosha, look straight at me! Of course I am just such a little boy as you are, only not a novice. And what have Russian boys been doing up till now, some of them, I mean? In this stinking tavern, for instance, here, they meet and sit down in a corner. They’ve never met in their lives before and, when they go out of the tavern, they won’t meet again for forty years. And what do they talk about in that momentary halt in the tavern? Of the eternal questions, of the existence of God and immortality. And those who do not believe in God talk of socialism or anarchism, of the transformation of all humanity on a new pattern, so that it all comes to the same, they’re the same questions turned inside out. And masses, masses of the most original Russian boys do nothing but talk of the eternal questions! Isn’t it so?”\n\n“Yes, for real Russians the questions of God’s existence and of immortality, or, as you say, the same questions turned inside out, come first and foremost, of course, and so they should,” said Alyosha, still watching his brother with the same gentle and inquiring smile.\n\n“Well, Alyosha, it’s sometimes very unwise to be a Russian at all, but anything stupider than the way Russian boys spend their time one can hardly imagine. But there’s one Russian boy called Alyosha I am awfully fond of.”\n\n“How nicely you put that in!” Alyosha laughed suddenly.\n\n“Well, tell me where to begin, give your orders. The existence of God, eh?”\n\n“Begin where you like. You declared yesterday at father’s that there was no God.” Alyosha looked searchingly at his brother.\n\n“I said that yesterday at dinner on purpose to tease you and I saw your eyes glow. But now I’ve no objection to discussing with you, and I say so very seriously. I want to be friends with you, Alyosha, for I have no friends and want to try it. Well, only fancy, perhaps I too accept God,” laughed Ivan; “that’s a surprise for you, isn’t it?”\n\n“Yes, of course, if you are not joking now.”\n\n“Joking? I was told at the elder’s yesterday that I was joking. You know, dear boy, there was an old sinner in the eighteenth century who declared that, if there were no God, he would have to be invented. S’il n’existait pas Dieu, il faudrait l’inventer. And man has actually invented God. And what’s strange, what would be marvelous, is not that God should really exist; the marvel is that such an idea, the idea of the necessity of God, could enter the head of such a savage, vicious beast as man. So holy it is, so touching, so wise and so great a credit it does to man. As for me, I’ve long resolved not to think whether man created God or God man. And I won’t go through all the axioms laid down by Russian boys on that subject, all derived from European hypotheses; for what’s a hypothesis there, is an axiom with the Russian boy, and not only with the boys but with their teachers too, for our Russian professors are often just the same boys themselves. And so I omit all the hypotheses. For what are we aiming at now? I am trying to explain as quickly as possible my essential nature, that is what manner of man I am, what I believe in, and for what I hope, that’s it, isn’t it? And therefore I tell you that I accept God simply. But you must note this: if God exists and if He really did create the world, then, as we all know, He created it according to the geometry of Euclid and the human mind with the conception of only three dimensions in space. Yet there have been and still are geometricians and philosophers, and even some of the most distinguished, who doubt whether the whole universe, or to speak more widely the whole of being, was only created in Euclid’s geometry; they even dare to dream that two parallel lines, which according to Euclid can never meet on earth, may meet somewhere in infinity. I have come to the conclusion that, since I can’t understand even that, I can’t expect to understand about God. I acknowledge humbly that I have no faculty for settling such questions, I have a Euclidian earthly mind, and how could I solve problems that are not of this world? And I advise you never to think about it either, my dear Alyosha, especially about God, whether He exists or not. All such questions are utterly inappropriate for a mind created with an idea of only three dimensions. And so I accept God and am glad to, and what’s more, I accept His wisdom, His purpose—which are utterly beyond our ken; I believe in the underlying order and the meaning of life; I believe in the eternal harmony in which they say we shall one day be blended. I believe in the Word to Which the universe is striving, and Which Itself was ‘with God,’ and Which Itself is God and so on, and so on, to infinity. There are all sorts of phrases for it. I seem to be on the right path, don’t I? Yet would you believe it, in the final result I don’t accept this world of God’s, and, although I know it exists, I don’t accept it at all. It’s not that I don’t accept God, you must understand, it’s the world created by Him I don’t and cannot accept. Let me make it plain. I believe like a child that suffering will be healed and made up for, that all the humiliating absurdity of human contradictions will vanish like a pitiful mirage, like the despicable fabrication of the impotent and infinitely small Euclidian mind of man, that in the world’s finale, at the moment of eternal harmony, something so precious will come to pass that it will suffice for all hearts, for the comforting of all resentments, for the atonement of all the crimes of humanity, of all the blood they’ve shed; that it will make it not only possible to forgive but to justify all that has happened with men—but though all that may come to pass, I don’t accept it. I won’t accept it. Even if parallel lines do meet and I see it myself, I shall see it and say that they’ve met, but still I won’t accept it. That’s what’s at the root of me, Alyosha; that’s my creed. I am in earnest in what I say. I began our talk as stupidly as I could on purpose, but I’ve led up to my confession, for that’s all you want. You didn’t want to hear about God, but only to know what the brother you love lives by. And so I’ve told you.”\n\nIvan concluded his long tirade with marked and unexpected feeling.\n\n“And why did you begin ‘as stupidly as you could’?” asked Alyosha, looking dreamily at him.\n\n“To begin with, for the sake of being Russian. Russian conversations on such subjects are always carried on inconceivably stupidly. And secondly, the stupider one is, the closer one is to reality. The stupider one is, the clearer one is. Stupidity is brief and artless, while intelligence wriggles and hides itself. Intelligence is a knave, but stupidity is honest and straightforward. I’ve led the conversation to my despair, and the more stupidly I have presented it, the better for me.”\n\n“You will explain why you don’t accept the world?” said Alyosha.\n\n“To be sure I will, it’s not a secret, that’s what I’ve been leading up to. Dear little brother, I don’t want to corrupt you or to turn you from your stronghold, perhaps I want to be healed by you.” Ivan smiled suddenly quite like a little gentle child. Alyosha had never seen such a smile on his face before.\n\n# Chapter IV. Rebellion\n\n“I must make you one confession,” Ivan began. “I could never understand how one can love one’s neighbors. It’s just one’s neighbors, to my mind, that one can’t love, though one might love those at a distance. I once read somewhere of John the Merciful, a saint, that when a hungry, frozen beggar came to him, he took him into his bed, held him in his arms, and began breathing into his mouth, which was putrid and loathsome from some awful disease. I am convinced that he did that from ‘self-laceration,’ from the self-laceration of falsity, for the sake of the charity imposed by duty, as a penance laid on him. For any one to love a man, he must be hidden, for as soon as he shows his face, love is gone.”\n\n“Father Zossima has talked of that more than once,” observed Alyosha; “he, too, said that the face of a man often hinders many people not practiced in love, from loving him. But yet there’s a great deal of love in mankind, and almost Christ-like love. I know that myself, Ivan.”\n\n“Well, I know nothing of it so far, and can’t understand it, and the innumerable mass of mankind are with me there. The question is, whether that’s due to men’s bad qualities or whether it’s inherent in their nature. To my thinking, Christ-like love for men is a miracle impossible on earth. He was God. But we are not gods. Suppose I, for instance, suffer intensely. Another can never know how much I suffer, because he is another and not I. And what’s more, a man is rarely ready to admit another’s suffering (as though it were a distinction). Why won’t he admit it, do you think? Because I smell unpleasant, because I have a stupid face, because I once trod on his foot. Besides, there is suffering and suffering; degrading, humiliating suffering such as humbles me—hunger, for instance—my benefactor will perhaps allow me; but when you come to higher suffering—for an idea, for instance—he will very rarely admit that, perhaps because my face strikes him as not at all what he fancies a man should have who suffers for an idea. And so he deprives me instantly of his favor, and not at all from badness of heart. Beggars, especially genteel beggars, ought never to show themselves, but to ask for charity through the newspapers. One can love one’s neighbors in the abstract, or even at a distance, but at close quarters it’s almost impossible. If it were as on the stage, in the ballet, where if beggars come in, they wear silken rags and tattered lace and beg for alms dancing gracefully, then one might like looking at them. But even then we should not love them. But enough of that. I simply wanted to show you my point of view. I meant to speak of the suffering of mankind generally, but we had better confine ourselves to the sufferings of the children. That reduces the scope of my argument to a tenth of what it would be. Still we’d better keep to the children, though it does weaken my case. But, in the first place, children can be loved even at close quarters, even when they are dirty, even when they are ugly (I fancy, though, children never are ugly). The second reason why I won’t speak of grown-up people is that, besides being disgusting and unworthy of love, they have a compensation—they’ve eaten the apple and know good and evil, and they have become ‘like gods.’ They go on eating it still. But the children haven’t eaten anything, and are so far innocent. Are you fond of children, Alyosha? I know you are, and you will understand why I prefer to speak of them. If they, too, suffer horribly on earth, they must suffer for their fathers’ sins, they must be punished for their fathers, who have eaten the apple; but that reasoning is of the other world and is incomprehensible for the heart of man here on earth. The innocent must not suffer for another’s sins, and especially such innocents! You may be surprised at me, Alyosha, but I am awfully fond of children, too. And observe, cruel people, the violent, the rapacious, the Karamazovs are sometimes very fond of children. Children while they are quite little—up to seven, for instance—are so remote from grown-up people; they are different creatures, as it were, of a different species. I knew a criminal in prison who had, in the course of his career as a burglar, murdered whole families, including several children. But when he was in prison, he had a strange affection for them. He spent all his time at his window, watching the children playing in the prison yard. He trained one little boy to come up to his window and made great friends with him.... You don’t know why I am telling you all this, Alyosha? My head aches and I am sad.”\n\n“You speak with a strange air,” observed Alyosha uneasily, “as though you were not quite yourself.”\n\n“By the way, a Bulgarian I met lately in Moscow,” Ivan went on, seeming not to hear his brother’s words, “told me about the crimes committed by Turks and Circassians in all parts of Bulgaria through fear of a general rising of the Slavs. They burn villages, murder, outrage women and children, they nail their prisoners by the ears to the fences, leave them so till morning, and in the morning they hang them—all sorts of things you can’t imagine. People talk sometimes of bestial cruelty, but that’s a great injustice and insult to the beasts; a beast can never be so cruel as a man, so artistically cruel. The tiger only tears and gnaws, that’s all he can do. He would never think of nailing people by the ears, even if he were able to do it. These Turks took a pleasure in torturing children, too; cutting the unborn child from the mother’s womb, and tossing babies up in the air and catching them on the points of their bayonets before their mothers’ eyes. Doing it before the mothers’ eyes was what gave zest to the amusement. Here is another scene that I thought very interesting. Imagine a trembling mother with her baby in her arms, a circle of invading Turks around her. They’ve planned a diversion: they pet the baby, laugh to make it laugh. They succeed, the baby laughs. At that moment a Turk points a pistol four inches from the baby’s face. The baby laughs with glee, holds out its little hands to the pistol, and he pulls the trigger in the baby’s face and blows out its brains. Artistic, wasn’t it? By the way, Turks are particularly fond of sweet things, they say.”\n\n“Brother, what are you driving at?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“I think if the devil doesn’t exist, but man has created him, he has created him in his own image and likeness.”\n\n“Just as he did God, then?” observed Alyosha.\n\n“‘It’s wonderful how you can turn words,’ as Polonius says in Hamlet,” laughed Ivan. “You turn my words against me. Well, I am glad. Yours must be a fine God, if man created Him in his image and likeness. You asked just now what I was driving at. You see, I am fond of collecting certain facts, and, would you believe, I even copy anecdotes of a certain sort from newspapers and books, and I’ve already got a fine collection. The Turks, of course, have gone into it, but they are foreigners. I have specimens from home that are even better than the Turks. You know we prefer beating—rods and scourges—that’s our national institution. Nailing ears is unthinkable for us, for we are, after all, Europeans. But the rod and the scourge we have always with us and they cannot be taken from us. Abroad now they scarcely do any beating. Manners are more humane, or laws have been passed, so that they don’t dare to flog men now. But they make up for it in another way just as national as ours. And so national that it would be practically impossible among us, though I believe we are being inoculated with it, since the religious movement began in our aristocracy. I have a charming pamphlet, translated from the French, describing how, quite recently, five years ago, a murderer, Richard, was executed—a young man, I believe, of three and twenty, who repented and was converted to the Christian faith at the very scaffold. This Richard was an illegitimate child who was given as a child of six by his parents to some shepherds on the Swiss mountains. They brought him up to work for them. He grew up like a little wild beast among them. The shepherds taught him nothing, and scarcely fed or clothed him, but sent him out at seven to herd the flock in cold and wet, and no one hesitated or scrupled to treat him so. Quite the contrary, they thought they had every right, for Richard had been given to them as a chattel, and they did not even see the necessity of feeding him. Richard himself describes how in those years, like the Prodigal Son in the Gospel, he longed to eat of the mash given to the pigs, which were fattened for sale. But they wouldn’t even give him that, and beat him when he stole from the pigs. And that was how he spent all his childhood and his youth, till he grew up and was strong enough to go away and be a thief. The savage began to earn his living as a day laborer in Geneva. He drank what he earned, he lived like a brute, and finished by killing and robbing an old man. He was caught, tried, and condemned to death. They are not sentimentalists there. And in prison he was immediately surrounded by pastors, members of Christian brotherhoods, philanthropic ladies, and the like. They taught him to read and write in prison, and expounded the Gospel to him. They exhorted him, worked upon him, drummed at him incessantly, till at last he solemnly confessed his crime. He was converted. He wrote to the court himself that he was a monster, but that in the end God had vouchsafed him light and shown grace. All Geneva was in excitement about him—all philanthropic and religious Geneva. All the aristocratic and well-bred society of the town rushed to the prison, kissed Richard and embraced him; ‘You are our brother, you have found grace.’ And Richard does nothing but weep with emotion, ‘Yes, I’ve found grace! All my youth and childhood I was glad of pigs’ food, but now even I have found grace. I am dying in the Lord.’ ‘Yes, Richard, die in the Lord; you have shed blood and must die. Though it’s not your fault that you knew not the Lord, when you coveted the pigs’ food and were beaten for stealing it (which was very wrong of you, for stealing is forbidden); but you’ve shed blood and you must die.’ And on the last day, Richard, perfectly limp, did nothing but cry and repeat every minute: ‘This is my happiest day. I am going to the Lord.’ ‘Yes,’ cry the pastors and the judges and philanthropic ladies. ‘This is the happiest day of your life, for you are going to the Lord!’ They all walk or drive to the scaffold in procession behind the prison van. At the scaffold they call to Richard: ‘Die, brother, die in the Lord, for even thou hast found grace!’ And so, covered with his brothers’ kisses, Richard is dragged on to the scaffold, and led to the guillotine. And they chopped off his head in brotherly fashion, because he had found grace. Yes, that’s characteristic. That pamphlet is translated into Russian by some Russian philanthropists of aristocratic rank and evangelical aspirations, and has been distributed gratis for the enlightenment of the people. The case of Richard is interesting because it’s national. Though to us it’s absurd to cut off a man’s head, because he has become our brother and has found grace, yet we have our own speciality, which is all but worse. Our historical pastime is the direct satisfaction of inflicting pain. There are lines in Nekrassov describing how a peasant lashes a horse on the eyes, ‘on its meek eyes,’ every one must have seen it. It’s peculiarly Russian. He describes how a feeble little nag has foundered under too heavy a load and cannot move. The peasant beats it, beats it savagely, beats it at last not knowing what he is doing in the intoxication of cruelty, thrashes it mercilessly over and over again. ‘However weak you are, you must pull, if you die for it.’ The nag strains, and then he begins lashing the poor defenseless creature on its weeping, on its ‘meek eyes.’ The frantic beast tugs and draws the load, trembling all over, gasping for breath, moving sideways, with a sort of unnatural spasmodic action—it’s awful in Nekrassov. But that’s only a horse, and God has given horses to be beaten. So the Tatars have taught us, and they left us the knout as a remembrance of it. But men, too, can be beaten. A well-educated, cultured gentleman and his wife beat their own child with a birch-rod, a girl of seven. I have an exact account of it. The papa was glad that the birch was covered with twigs. ‘It stings more,’ said he, and so he began stinging his daughter. I know for a fact there are people who at every blow are worked up to sensuality, to literal sensuality, which increases progressively at every blow they inflict. They beat for a minute, for five minutes, for ten minutes, more often and more savagely. The child screams. At last the child cannot scream, it gasps, ‘Daddy! daddy!’ By some diabolical unseemly chance the case was brought into court. A counsel is engaged. The Russian people have long called a barrister ‘a conscience for hire.’ The counsel protests in his client’s defense. ‘It’s such a simple thing,’ he says, ‘an everyday domestic event. A father corrects his child. To our shame be it said, it is brought into court.’ The jury, convinced by him, give a favorable verdict. The public roars with delight that the torturer is acquitted. Ah, pity I wasn’t there! I would have proposed to raise a subscription in his honor! Charming pictures.\n\n“But I’ve still better things about children. I’ve collected a great, great deal about Russian children, Alyosha. There was a little girl of five who was hated by her father and mother, ‘most worthy and respectable people, of good education and breeding.’ You see, I must repeat again, it is a peculiar characteristic of many people, this love of torturing children, and children only. To all other types of humanity these torturers behave mildly and benevolently, like cultivated and humane Europeans; but they are very fond of tormenting children, even fond of children themselves in that sense. It’s just their defenselessness that tempts the tormentor, just the angelic confidence of the child who has no refuge and no appeal, that sets his vile blood on fire. In every man, of course, a demon lies hidden—the demon of rage, the demon of lustful heat at the screams of the tortured victim, the demon of lawlessness let off the chain, the demon of diseases that follow on vice, gout, kidney disease, and so on.\n\n“This poor child of five was subjected to every possible torture by those cultivated parents. They beat her, thrashed her, kicked her for no reason till her body was one bruise. Then, they went to greater refinements of cruelty—shut her up all night in the cold and frost in a privy, and because she didn’t ask to be taken up at night (as though a child of five sleeping its angelic, sound sleep could be trained to wake and ask), they smeared her face and filled her mouth with excrement, and it was her mother, her mother did this. And that mother could sleep, hearing the poor child’s groans! Can you understand why a little creature, who can’t even understand what’s done to her, should beat her little aching heart with her tiny fist in the dark and the cold, and weep her meek unresentful tears to dear, kind God to protect her? Do you understand that, friend and brother, you pious and humble novice? Do you understand why this infamy must be and is permitted? Without it, I am told, man could not have existed on earth, for he could not have known good and evil. Why should he know that diabolical good and evil when it costs so much? Why, the whole world of knowledge is not worth that child’s prayer to ‘dear, kind God’! I say nothing of the sufferings of grown-up people, they have eaten the apple, damn them, and the devil take them all! But these little ones! I am making you suffer, Alyosha, you are not yourself. I’ll leave off if you like.”\n\n“Never mind. I want to suffer too,” muttered Alyosha.\n\n“One picture, only one more, because it’s so curious, so characteristic, and I have only just read it in some collection of Russian antiquities. I’ve forgotten the name. I must look it up. It was in the darkest days of serfdom at the beginning of the century, and long live the Liberator of the People! There was in those days a general of aristocratic connections, the owner of great estates, one of those men—somewhat exceptional, I believe, even then—who, retiring from the service into a life of leisure, are convinced that they’ve earned absolute power over the lives of their subjects. There were such men then. So our general, settled on his property of two thousand souls, lives in pomp, and domineers over his poor neighbors as though they were dependents and buffoons. He has kennels of hundreds of hounds and nearly a hundred dog-boys—all mounted, and in uniform. One day a serf-boy, a little child of eight, threw a stone in play and hurt the paw of the general’s favorite hound. ‘Why is my favorite dog lame?’ He is told that the boy threw a stone that hurt the dog’s paw. ‘So you did it.’ The general looked the child up and down. ‘Take him.’ He was taken—taken from his mother and kept shut up all night. Early that morning the general comes out on horseback, with the hounds, his dependents, dog-boys, and huntsmen, all mounted around him in full hunting parade. The servants are summoned for their edification, and in front of them all stands the mother of the child. The child is brought from the lock-up. It’s a gloomy, cold, foggy autumn day, a capital day for hunting. The general orders the child to be undressed; the child is stripped naked. He shivers, numb with terror, not daring to cry.... ‘Make him run,’ commands the general. ‘Run! run!’ shout the dog-boys. The boy runs.... ‘At him!’ yells the general, and he sets the whole pack of hounds on the child. The hounds catch him, and tear him to pieces before his mother’s eyes!... I believe the general was afterwards declared incapable of administering his estates. Well—what did he deserve? To be shot? To be shot for the satisfaction of our moral feelings? Speak, Alyosha!”\n\n“To be shot,” murmured Alyosha, lifting his eyes to Ivan with a pale, twisted smile.\n\n“Bravo!” cried Ivan, delighted. “If even you say so.... You’re a pretty monk! So there is a little devil sitting in your heart, Alyosha Karamazov!”\n\n“What I said was absurd, but—”\n\n“That’s just the point, that ‘but’!” cried Ivan. “Let me tell you, novice, that the absurd is only too necessary on earth. The world stands on absurdities, and perhaps nothing would have come to pass in it without them. We know what we know!”\n\n“What do you know?”\n\n“I understand nothing,” Ivan went on, as though in delirium. “I don’t want to understand anything now. I want to stick to the fact. I made up my mind long ago not to understand. If I try to understand anything, I shall be false to the fact, and I have determined to stick to the fact.”\n\n“Why are you trying me?” Alyosha cried, with sudden distress. “Will you say what you mean at last?”\n\n“Of course, I will; that’s what I’ve been leading up to. You are dear to me, I don’t want to let you go, and I won’t give you up to your Zossima.”\n\nIvan for a minute was silent, his face became all at once very sad.\n\n“Listen! I took the case of children only to make my case clearer. Of the other tears of humanity with which the earth is soaked from its crust to its center, I will say nothing. I have narrowed my subject on purpose. I am a bug, and I recognize in all humility that I cannot understand why the world is arranged as it is. Men are themselves to blame, I suppose; they were given paradise, they wanted freedom, and stole fire from heaven, though they knew they would become unhappy, so there is no need to pity them. With my pitiful, earthly, Euclidian understanding, all I know is that there is suffering and that there are none guilty; that cause follows effect, simply and directly; that everything flows and finds its level—but that’s only Euclidian nonsense, I know that, and I can’t consent to live by it! What comfort is it to me that there are none guilty and that cause follows effect simply and directly, and that I know it?—I must have justice, or I will destroy myself. And not justice in some remote infinite time and space, but here on earth, and that I could see myself. I have believed in it. I want to see it, and if I am dead by then, let me rise again, for if it all happens without me, it will be too unfair. Surely I haven’t suffered, simply that I, my crimes and my sufferings, may manure the soil of the future harmony for somebody else. I want to see with my own eyes the hind lie down with the lion and the victim rise up and embrace his murderer. I want to be there when every one suddenly understands what it has all been for. All the religions of the world are built on this longing, and I am a believer. But then there are the children, and what am I to do about them? That’s a question I can’t answer. For the hundredth time I repeat, there are numbers of questions, but I’ve only taken the children, because in their case what I mean is so unanswerably clear. Listen! If all must suffer to pay for the eternal harmony, what have children to do with it, tell me, please? It’s beyond all comprehension why they should suffer, and why they should pay for the harmony. Why should they, too, furnish material to enrich the soil for the harmony of the future? I understand solidarity in sin among men. I understand solidarity in retribution, too; but there can be no such solidarity with children. And if it is really true that they must share responsibility for all their fathers’ crimes, such a truth is not of this world and is beyond my comprehension. Some jester will say, perhaps, that the child would have grown up and have sinned, but you see he didn’t grow up, he was torn to pieces by the dogs, at eight years old. Oh, Alyosha, I am not blaspheming! I understand, of course, what an upheaval of the universe it will be, when everything in heaven and earth blends in one hymn of praise and everything that lives and has lived cries aloud: ‘Thou art just, O Lord, for Thy ways are revealed.’ When the mother embraces the fiend who threw her child to the dogs, and all three cry aloud with tears, ‘Thou art just, O Lord!’ then, of course, the crown of knowledge will be reached and all will be made clear. But what pulls me up here is that I can’t accept that harmony. And while I am on earth, I make haste to take my own measures. You see, Alyosha, perhaps it really may happen that if I live to that moment, or rise again to see it, I, too, perhaps, may cry aloud with the rest, looking at the mother embracing the child’s torturer, ‘Thou art just, O Lord!’ but I don’t want to cry aloud then. While there is still time, I hasten to protect myself, and so I renounce the higher harmony altogether. It’s not worth the tears of that one tortured child who beat itself on the breast with its little fist and prayed in its stinking outhouse, with its unexpiated tears to ‘dear, kind God’! It’s not worth it, because those tears are unatoned for. They must be atoned for, or there can be no harmony. But how? How are you going to atone for them? Is it possible? By their being avenged? But what do I care for avenging them? What do I care for a hell for oppressors? What good can hell do, since those children have already been tortured? And what becomes of harmony, if there is hell? I want to forgive. I want to embrace. I don’t want more suffering. And if the sufferings of children go to swell the sum of sufferings which was necessary to pay for truth, then I protest that the truth is not worth such a price. I don’t want the mother to embrace the oppressor who threw her son to the dogs! She dare not forgive him! Let her forgive him for herself, if she will, let her forgive the torturer for the immeasurable suffering of her mother’s heart. But the sufferings of her tortured child she has no right to forgive; she dare not forgive the torturer, even if the child were to forgive him! And if that is so, if they dare not forgive, what becomes of harmony? Is there in the whole world a being who would have the right to forgive and could forgive? I don’t want harmony. From love for humanity I don’t want it. I would rather be left with the unavenged suffering. I would rather remain with my unavenged suffering and unsatisfied indignation, even if I were wrong. Besides, too high a price is asked for harmony; it’s beyond our means to pay so much to enter on it. And so I hasten to give back my entrance ticket, and if I am an honest man I am bound to give it back as soon as possible. And that I am doing. It’s not God that I don’t accept, Alyosha, only I most respectfully return Him the ticket.”\n\n“That’s rebellion,” murmured Alyosha, looking down.\n\n“Rebellion? I am sorry you call it that,” said Ivan earnestly. “One can hardly live in rebellion, and I want to live. Tell me yourself, I challenge you—answer. Imagine that you are creating a fabric of human destiny with the object of making men happy in the end, giving them peace and rest at last, but that it was essential and inevitable to torture to death only one tiny creature—that baby beating its breast with its fist, for instance—and to found that edifice on its unavenged tears, would you consent to be the architect on those conditions? Tell me, and tell the truth.”\n\n“No, I wouldn’t consent,” said Alyosha softly.\n\n“And can you admit the idea that men for whom you are building it would agree to accept their happiness on the foundation of the unexpiated blood of a little victim? And accepting it would remain happy for ever?”\n\n“No, I can’t admit it. Brother,” said Alyosha suddenly, with flashing eyes, “you said just now, is there a being in the whole world who would have the right to forgive and could forgive? But there is a Being and He can forgive everything, all and for all, because He gave His innocent blood for all and everything. You have forgotten Him, and on Him is built the edifice, and it is to Him they cry aloud, ‘Thou art just, O Lord, for Thy ways are revealed!’”\n\n“Ah! the One without sin and His blood! No, I have not forgotten Him; on the contrary I’ve been wondering all the time how it was you did not bring Him in before, for usually all arguments on your side put Him in the foreground. Do you know, Alyosha—don’t laugh! I made a poem about a year ago. If you can waste another ten minutes on me, I’ll tell it to you.”\n\n“You wrote a poem?”\n\n“Oh, no, I didn’t write it,” laughed Ivan, “and I’ve never written two lines of poetry in my life. But I made up this poem in prose and I remembered it. I was carried away when I made it up. You will be my first reader—that is listener. Why should an author forego even one listener?” smiled Ivan. “Shall I tell it to you?”\n\n“I am all attention,” said Alyosha.\n\n“My poem is called ‘The Grand Inquisitor’; it’s a ridiculous thing, but I want to tell it to you.”\n\n# Chapter V. The Grand Inquisitor\n\n“Even this must have a preface—that is, a literary preface,” laughed Ivan, “and I am a poor hand at making one. You see, my action takes place in the sixteenth century, and at that time, as you probably learnt at school, it was customary in poetry to bring down heavenly powers on earth. Not to speak of Dante, in France, clerks, as well as the monks in the monasteries, used to give regular performances in which the Madonna, the saints, the angels, Christ, and God himself were brought on the stage. In those days it was done in all simplicity. In Victor Hugo’s Notre Dame de Paris an edifying and gratuitous spectacle was provided for the people in the Hôtel de Ville of Paris in the reign of Louis XI. in honor of the birth of the dauphin. It was called Le bon jugement de la très sainte et gracieuse Vierge Marie, and she appears herself on the stage and pronounces her bon jugement. Similar plays, chiefly from the Old Testament, were occasionally performed in Moscow too, up to the times of Peter the Great. But besides plays there were all sorts of legends and ballads scattered about the world, in which the saints and angels and all the powers of Heaven took part when required. In our monasteries the monks busied themselves in translating, copying, and even composing such poems—and even under the Tatars. There is, for instance, one such poem (of course, from the Greek), The Wanderings of Our Lady through Hell, with descriptions as bold as Dante’s. Our Lady visits hell, and the Archangel Michael leads her through the torments. She sees the sinners and their punishment. There she sees among others one noteworthy set of sinners in a burning lake; some of them sink to the bottom of the lake so that they can’t swim out, and ‘these God forgets’—an expression of extraordinary depth and force. And so Our Lady, shocked and weeping, falls before the throne of God and begs for mercy for all in hell—for all she has seen there, indiscriminately. Her conversation with God is immensely interesting. She beseeches Him, she will not desist, and when God points to the hands and feet of her Son, nailed to the Cross, and asks, ‘How can I forgive His tormentors?’ she bids all the saints, all the martyrs, all the angels and archangels to fall down with her and pray for mercy on all without distinction. It ends by her winning from God a respite of suffering every year from Good Friday till Trinity Day, and the sinners at once raise a cry of thankfulness from hell, chanting, ‘Thou art just, O Lord, in this judgment.’ Well, my poem would have been of that kind if it had appeared at that time. He comes on the scene in my poem, but He says nothing, only appears and passes on. Fifteen centuries have passed since He promised to come in His glory, fifteen centuries since His prophet wrote, ‘Behold, I come quickly’; ‘Of that day and that hour knoweth no man, neither the Son, but the Father,’ as He Himself predicted on earth. But humanity awaits him with the same faith and with the same love. Oh, with greater faith, for it is fifteen centuries since man has ceased to see signs from heaven.\n\nNo signs from heaven come to-day To add to what the heart doth say.\n\nThere was nothing left but faith in what the heart doth say. It is true there were many miracles in those days. There were saints who performed miraculous cures; some holy people, according to their biographies, were visited by the Queen of Heaven herself. But the devil did not slumber, and doubts were already arising among men of the truth of these miracles. And just then there appeared in the north of Germany a terrible new heresy. “A huge star like to a torch” (that is, to a church) “fell on the sources of the waters and they became bitter.” These heretics began blasphemously denying miracles. But those who remained faithful were all the more ardent in their faith. The tears of humanity rose up to Him as before, awaited His coming, loved Him, hoped for Him, yearned to suffer and die for Him as before. And so many ages mankind had prayed with faith and fervor, “O Lord our God, hasten Thy coming,” so many ages called upon Him, that in His infinite mercy He deigned to come down to His servants. Before that day He had come down, He had visited some holy men, martyrs and hermits, as is written in their lives. Among us, Tyutchev, with absolute faith in the truth of his words, bore witness that\n\nBearing the Cross, in slavish dress, Weary and worn, the Heavenly King Our mother, Russia, came to bless, And through our land went wandering.\n\nAnd that certainly was so, I assure you.\n\n“And behold, He deigned to appear for a moment to the people, to the tortured, suffering people, sunk in iniquity, but loving Him like children. My story is laid in Spain, in Seville, in the most terrible time of the Inquisition, when fires were lighted every day to the glory of God, and ‘in the splendid auto da fé the wicked heretics were burnt.’ Oh, of course, this was not the coming in which He will appear according to His promise at the end of time in all His heavenly glory, and which will be sudden ‘as lightning flashing from east to west.’ No, He visited His children only for a moment, and there where the flames were crackling round the heretics. In His infinite mercy He came once more among men in that human shape in which He walked among men for three years fifteen centuries ago. He came down to the ‘hot pavements’ of the southern town in which on the day before almost a hundred heretics had, ad majorem gloriam Dei, been burnt by the cardinal, the Grand Inquisitor, in a magnificent auto da fé, in the presence of the king, the court, the knights, the cardinals, the most charming ladies of the court, and the whole population of Seville.\n\n“He came softly, unobserved, and yet, strange to say, every one recognized Him. That might be one of the best passages in the poem. I mean, why they recognized Him. The people are irresistibly drawn to Him, they surround Him, they flock about Him, follow Him. He moves silently in their midst with a gentle smile of infinite compassion. The sun of love burns in His heart, light and power shine from His eyes, and their radiance, shed on the people, stirs their hearts with responsive love. He holds out His hands to them, blesses them, and a healing virtue comes from contact with Him, even with His garments. An old man in the crowd, blind from childhood, cries out, ‘O Lord, heal me and I shall see Thee!’ and, as it were, scales fall from his eyes and the blind man sees Him. The crowd weeps and kisses the earth under His feet. Children throw flowers before Him, sing, and cry hosannah. ‘It is He—it is He!’ all repeat. ‘It must be He, it can be no one but Him!’ He stops at the steps of the Seville cathedral at the moment when the weeping mourners are bringing in a little open white coffin. In it lies a child of seven, the only daughter of a prominent citizen. The dead child lies hidden in flowers. ‘He will raise your child,’ the crowd shouts to the weeping mother. The priest, coming to meet the coffin, looks perplexed, and frowns, but the mother of the dead child throws herself at His feet with a wail. ‘If it is Thou, raise my child!’ she cries, holding out her hands to Him. The procession halts, the coffin is laid on the steps at His feet. He looks with compassion, and His lips once more softly pronounce, ‘Maiden, arise!’ and the maiden arises. The little girl sits up in the coffin and looks round, smiling with wide-open wondering eyes, holding a bunch of white roses they had put in her hand.\n\n“There are cries, sobs, confusion among the people, and at that moment the cardinal himself, the Grand Inquisitor, passes by the cathedral. He is an old man, almost ninety, tall and erect, with a withered face and sunken eyes, in which there is still a gleam of light. He is not dressed in his gorgeous cardinal’s robes, as he was the day before, when he was burning the enemies of the Roman Church—at this moment he is wearing his coarse, old, monk’s cassock. At a distance behind him come his gloomy assistants and slaves and the ‘holy guard.’ He stops at the sight of the crowd and watches it from a distance. He sees everything; he sees them set the coffin down at His feet, sees the child rise up, and his face darkens. He knits his thick gray brows and his eyes gleam with a sinister fire. He holds out his finger and bids the guards take Him. And such is his power, so completely are the people cowed into submission and trembling obedience to him, that the crowd immediately makes way for the guards, and in the midst of deathlike silence they lay hands on Him and lead Him away. The crowd instantly bows down to the earth, like one man, before the old Inquisitor. He blesses the people in silence and passes on. The guards lead their prisoner to the close, gloomy vaulted prison in the ancient palace of the Holy Inquisition and shut Him in it. The day passes and is followed by the dark, burning, ‘breathless’ night of Seville. The air is ‘fragrant with laurel and lemon.’ In the pitch darkness the iron door of the prison is suddenly opened and the Grand Inquisitor himself comes in with a light in his hand. He is alone; the door is closed at once behind him. He stands in the doorway and for a minute or two gazes into His face. At last he goes up slowly, sets the light on the table and speaks.\n\n“‘Is it Thou? Thou?’ but receiving no answer, he adds at once, ‘Don’t answer, be silent. What canst Thou say, indeed? I know too well what Thou wouldst say. And Thou hast no right to add anything to what Thou hadst said of old. Why, then, art Thou come to hinder us? For Thou hast come to hinder us, and Thou knowest that. But dost Thou know what will be to-morrow? I know not who Thou art and care not to know whether it is Thou or only a semblance of Him, but to-morrow I shall condemn Thee and burn Thee at the stake as the worst of heretics. And the very people who have to-day kissed Thy feet, to-morrow at the faintest sign from me will rush to heap up the embers of Thy fire. Knowest Thou that? Yes, maybe Thou knowest it,’ he added with thoughtful penetration, never for a moment taking his eyes off the Prisoner.”\n\n“I don’t quite understand, Ivan. What does it mean?” Alyosha, who had been listening in silence, said with a smile. “Is it simply a wild fantasy, or a mistake on the part of the old man—some impossible quiproquo?”\n\n“Take it as the last,” said Ivan, laughing, “if you are so corrupted by modern realism and can’t stand anything fantastic. If you like it to be a case of mistaken identity, let it be so. It is true,” he went on, laughing, “the old man was ninety, and he might well be crazy over his set idea. He might have been struck by the appearance of the Prisoner. It might, in fact, be simply his ravings, the delusion of an old man of ninety, over-excited by the auto da fé of a hundred heretics the day before. But does it matter to us after all whether it was a mistake of identity or a wild fantasy? All that matters is that the old man should speak out, should speak openly of what he has thought in silence for ninety years.”\n\n“And the Prisoner too is silent? Does He look at him and not say a word?”\n\n“That’s inevitable in any case,” Ivan laughed again. “The old man has told Him He hasn’t the right to add anything to what He has said of old. One may say it is the most fundamental feature of Roman Catholicism, in my opinion at least. ‘All has been given by Thee to the Pope,’ they say, ‘and all, therefore, is still in the Pope’s hands, and there is no need for Thee to come now at all. Thou must not meddle for the time, at least.’ That’s how they speak and write too—the Jesuits, at any rate. I have read it myself in the works of their theologians. ‘Hast Thou the right to reveal to us one of the mysteries of that world from which Thou hast come?’ my old man asks Him, and answers the question for Him. ‘No, Thou hast not; that Thou mayest not add to what has been said of old, and mayest not take from men the freedom which Thou didst exalt when Thou wast on earth. Whatsoever Thou revealest anew will encroach on men’s freedom of faith; for it will be manifest as a miracle, and the freedom of their faith was dearer to Thee than anything in those days fifteen hundred years ago. Didst Thou not often say then, “I will make you free”? But now Thou hast seen these “free” men,’ the old man adds suddenly, with a pensive smile. ‘Yes, we’ve paid dearly for it,’ he goes on, looking sternly at Him, ‘but at last we have completed that work in Thy name. For fifteen centuries we have been wrestling with Thy freedom, but now it is ended and over for good. Dost Thou not believe that it’s over for good? Thou lookest meekly at me and deignest not even to be wroth with me. But let me tell Thee that now, to-day, people are more persuaded than ever that they have perfect freedom, yet they have brought their freedom to us and laid it humbly at our feet. But that has been our doing. Was this what Thou didst? Was this Thy freedom?’”\n\n“I don’t understand again,” Alyosha broke in. “Is he ironical, is he jesting?”\n\n“Not a bit of it! He claims it as a merit for himself and his Church that at last they have vanquished freedom and have done so to make men happy. ‘For now’ (he is speaking of the Inquisition, of course) ‘for the first time it has become possible to think of the happiness of men. Man was created a rebel; and how can rebels be happy? Thou wast warned,’ he says to Him. ‘Thou hast had no lack of admonitions and warnings, but Thou didst not listen to those warnings; Thou didst reject the only way by which men might be made happy. But, fortunately, departing Thou didst hand on the work to us. Thou hast promised, Thou hast established by Thy word, Thou hast given to us the right to bind and to unbind, and now, of course, Thou canst not think of taking it away. Why, then, hast Thou come to hinder us?’”\n\n“And what’s the meaning of ‘no lack of admonitions and warnings’?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Why, that’s the chief part of what the old man must say.\n\n“‘The wise and dread spirit, the spirit of self-destruction and non- existence,’ the old man goes on, ‘the great spirit talked with Thee in the wilderness, and we are told in the books that he “tempted” Thee. Is that so? And could anything truer be said than what he revealed to Thee in three questions and what Thou didst reject, and what in the books is called “the temptation”? And yet if there has ever been on earth a real stupendous miracle, it took place on that day, on the day of the three temptations. The statement of those three questions was itself the miracle. If it were possible to imagine simply for the sake of argument that those three questions of the dread spirit had perished utterly from the books, and that we had to restore them and to invent them anew, and to do so had gathered together all the wise men of the earth—rulers, chief priests, learned men, philosophers, poets—and had set them the task to invent three questions, such as would not only fit the occasion, but express in three words, three human phrases, the whole future history of the world and of humanity—dost Thou believe that all the wisdom of the earth united could have invented anything in depth and force equal to the three questions which were actually put to Thee then by the wise and mighty spirit in the wilderness? From those questions alone, from the miracle of their statement, we can see that we have here to do not with the fleeting human intelligence, but with the absolute and eternal. For in those three questions the whole subsequent history of mankind is, as it were, brought together into one whole, and foretold, and in them are united all the unsolved historical contradictions of human nature. At the time it could not be so clear, since the future was unknown; but now that fifteen hundred years have passed, we see that everything in those three questions was so justly divined and foretold, and has been so truly fulfilled, that nothing can be added to them or taken from them.\n\n“‘Judge Thyself who was right—Thou or he who questioned Thee then? Remember the first question; its meaning, in other words, was this: “Thou wouldst go into the world, and art going with empty hands, with some promise of freedom which men in their simplicity and their natural unruliness cannot even understand, which they fear and dread—for nothing has ever been more insupportable for a man and a human society than freedom. But seest Thou these stones in this parched and barren wilderness? Turn them into bread, and mankind will run after Thee like a flock of sheep, grateful and obedient, though for ever trembling, lest Thou withdraw Thy hand and deny them Thy bread.” But Thou wouldst not deprive man of freedom and didst reject the offer, thinking, what is that freedom worth, if obedience is bought with bread? Thou didst reply that man lives not by bread alone. But dost Thou know that for the sake of that earthly bread the spirit of the earth will rise up against Thee and will strive with Thee and overcome Thee, and all will follow him, crying, “Who can compare with this beast? He has given us fire from heaven!” Dost Thou know that the ages will pass, and humanity will proclaim by the lips of their sages that there is no crime, and therefore no sin; there is only hunger? “Feed men, and then ask of them virtue!” that’s what they’ll write on the banner, which they will raise against Thee, and with which they will destroy Thy temple. Where Thy temple stood will rise a new building; the terrible tower of Babel will be built again, and though, like the one of old, it will not be finished, yet Thou mightest have prevented that new tower and have cut short the sufferings of men for a thousand years; for they will come back to us after a thousand years of agony with their tower. They will seek us again, hidden underground in the catacombs, for we shall be again persecuted and tortured. They will find us and cry to us, “Feed us, for those who have promised us fire from heaven haven’t given it!” And then we shall finish building their tower, for he finishes the building who feeds them. And we alone shall feed them in Thy name, declaring falsely that it is in Thy name. Oh, never, never can they feed themselves without us! No science will give them bread so long as they remain free. In the end they will lay their freedom at our feet, and say to us, “Make us your slaves, but feed us.” They will understand themselves, at last, that freedom and bread enough for all are inconceivable together, for never, never will they be able to share between them! They will be convinced, too, that they can never be free, for they are weak, vicious, worthless and rebellious. Thou didst promise them the bread of Heaven, but, I repeat again, can it compare with earthly bread in the eyes of the weak, ever sinful and ignoble race of man? And if for the sake of the bread of Heaven thousands shall follow Thee, what is to become of the millions and tens of thousands of millions of creatures who will not have the strength to forego the earthly bread for the sake of the heavenly? Or dost Thou care only for the tens of thousands of the great and strong, while the millions, numerous as the sands of the sea, who are weak but love Thee, must exist only for the sake of the great and strong? No, we care for the weak too. They are sinful and rebellious, but in the end they too will become obedient. They will marvel at us and look on us as gods, because we are ready to endure the freedom which they have found so dreadful and to rule over them—so awful it will seem to them to be free. But we shall tell them that we are Thy servants and rule them in Thy name. We shall deceive them again, for we will not let Thee come to us again. That deception will be our suffering, for we shall be forced to lie.\n\n“‘This is the significance of the first question in the wilderness, and this is what Thou hast rejected for the sake of that freedom which Thou hast exalted above everything. Yet in this question lies hid the great secret of this world. Choosing “bread,” Thou wouldst have satisfied the universal and everlasting craving of humanity—to find some one to worship. So long as man remains free he strives for nothing so incessantly and so painfully as to find some one to worship. But man seeks to worship what is established beyond dispute, so that all men would agree at once to worship it. For these pitiful creatures are concerned not only to find what one or the other can worship, but to find something that all would believe in and worship; what is essential is that all may be together in it. This craving for community of worship is the chief misery of every man individually and of all humanity from the beginning of time. For the sake of common worship they’ve slain each other with the sword. They have set up gods and challenged one another, “Put away your gods and come and worship ours, or we will kill you and your gods!” And so it will be to the end of the world, even when gods disappear from the earth; they will fall down before idols just the same. Thou didst know, Thou couldst not but have known, this fundamental secret of human nature, but Thou didst reject the one infallible banner which was offered Thee to make all men bow down to Thee alone—the banner of earthly bread; and Thou hast rejected it for the sake of freedom and the bread of Heaven. Behold what Thou didst further. And all again in the name of freedom! I tell Thee that man is tormented by no greater anxiety than to find some one quickly to whom he can hand over that gift of freedom with which the ill-fated creature is born. But only one who can appease their conscience can take over their freedom. In bread there was offered Thee an invincible banner; give bread, and man will worship thee, for nothing is more certain than bread. But if some one else gains possession of his conscience—oh! then he will cast away Thy bread and follow after him who has ensnared his conscience. In that Thou wast right. For the secret of man’s being is not only to live but to have something to live for. Without a stable conception of the object of life, man would not consent to go on living, and would rather destroy himself than remain on earth, though he had bread in abundance. That is true. But what happened? Instead of taking men’s freedom from them, Thou didst make it greater than ever! Didst Thou forget that man prefers peace, and even death, to freedom of choice in the knowledge of good and evil? Nothing is more seductive for man than his freedom of conscience, but nothing is a greater cause of suffering. And behold, instead of giving a firm foundation for setting the conscience of man at rest for ever, Thou didst choose all that is exceptional, vague and enigmatic; Thou didst choose what was utterly beyond the strength of men, acting as though Thou didst not love them at all—Thou who didst come to give Thy life for them! Instead of taking possession of men’s freedom, Thou didst increase it, and burdened the spiritual kingdom of mankind with its sufferings for ever. Thou didst desire man’s free love, that he should follow Thee freely, enticed and taken captive by Thee. In place of the rigid ancient law, man must hereafter with free heart decide for himself what is good and what is evil, having only Thy image before him as his guide. But didst Thou not know that he would at last reject even Thy image and Thy truth, if he is weighed down with the fearful burden of free choice? They will cry aloud at last that the truth is not in Thee, for they could not have been left in greater confusion and suffering than Thou hast caused, laying upon them so many cares and unanswerable problems.\n\n“‘So that, in truth, Thou didst Thyself lay the foundation for the destruction of Thy kingdom, and no one is more to blame for it. Yet what was offered Thee? There are three powers, three powers alone, able to conquer and to hold captive for ever the conscience of these impotent rebels for their happiness—those forces are miracle, mystery and authority. Thou hast rejected all three and hast set the example for doing so. When the wise and dread spirit set Thee on the pinnacle of the temple and said to Thee, “If Thou wouldst know whether Thou art the Son of God then cast Thyself down, for it is written: the angels shall hold him up lest he fall and bruise himself, and Thou shalt know then whether Thou art the Son of God and shalt prove then how great is Thy faith in Thy Father.” But Thou didst refuse and wouldst not cast Thyself down. Oh, of course, Thou didst proudly and well, like God; but the weak, unruly race of men, are they gods? Oh, Thou didst know then that in taking one step, in making one movement to cast Thyself down, Thou wouldst be tempting God and have lost all Thy faith in Him, and wouldst have been dashed to pieces against that earth which Thou didst come to save. And the wise spirit that tempted Thee would have rejoiced. But I ask again, are there many like Thee? And couldst Thou believe for one moment that men, too, could face such a temptation? Is the nature of men such, that they can reject miracle, and at the great moments of their life, the moments of their deepest, most agonizing spiritual difficulties, cling only to the free verdict of the heart? Oh, Thou didst know that Thy deed would be recorded in books, would be handed down to remote times and the utmost ends of the earth, and Thou didst hope that man, following Thee, would cling to God and not ask for a miracle. But Thou didst not know that when man rejects miracle he rejects God too; for man seeks not so much God as the miraculous. And as man cannot bear to be without the miraculous, he will create new miracles of his own for himself, and will worship deeds of sorcery and witchcraft, though he might be a hundred times over a rebel, heretic and infidel. Thou didst not come down from the Cross when they shouted to Thee, mocking and reviling Thee, “Come down from the cross and we will believe that Thou art He.” Thou didst not come down, for again Thou wouldst not enslave man by a miracle, and didst crave faith given freely, not based on miracle. Thou didst crave for free love and not the base raptures of the slave before the might that has overawed him for ever. But Thou didst think too highly of men therein, for they are slaves, of course, though rebellious by nature. Look round and judge; fifteen centuries have passed, look upon them. Whom hast Thou raised up to Thyself? I swear, man is weaker and baser by nature than Thou hast believed him! Can he, can he do what Thou didst? By showing him so much respect, Thou didst, as it were, cease to feel for him, for Thou didst ask far too much from him—Thou who hast loved him more than Thyself! Respecting him less, Thou wouldst have asked less of him. That would have been more like love, for his burden would have been lighter. He is weak and vile. What though he is everywhere now rebelling against our power, and proud of his rebellion? It is the pride of a child and a schoolboy. They are little children rioting and barring out the teacher at school. But their childish delight will end; it will cost them dear. They will cast down temples and drench the earth with blood. But they will see at last, the foolish children, that, though they are rebels, they are impotent rebels, unable to keep up their own rebellion. Bathed in their foolish tears, they will recognize at last that He who created them rebels must have meant to mock at them. They will say this in despair, and their utterance will be a blasphemy which will make them more unhappy still, for man’s nature cannot bear blasphemy, and in the end always avenges it on itself. And so unrest, confusion and unhappiness—that is the present lot of man after Thou didst bear so much for their freedom! The great prophet tells in vision and in image, that he saw all those who took part in the first resurrection and that there were of each tribe twelve thousand. But if there were so many of them, they must have been not men but gods. They had borne Thy cross, they had endured scores of years in the barren, hungry wilderness, living upon locusts and roots—and Thou mayest indeed point with pride at those children of freedom, of free love, of free and splendid sacrifice for Thy name. But remember that they were only some thousands; and what of the rest? And how are the other weak ones to blame, because they could not endure what the strong have endured? How is the weak soul to blame that it is unable to receive such terrible gifts? Canst Thou have simply come to the elect and for the elect? But if so, it is a mystery and we cannot understand it. And if it is a mystery, we too have a right to preach a mystery, and to teach them that it’s not the free judgment of their hearts, not love that matters, but a mystery which they must follow blindly, even against their conscience. So we have done. We have corrected Thy work and have founded it upon miracle, mystery and authority. And men rejoiced that they were again led like sheep, and that the terrible gift that had brought them such suffering was, at last, lifted from their hearts. Were we right teaching them this? Speak! Did we not love mankind, so meekly acknowledging their feebleness, lovingly lightening their burden, and permitting their weak nature even sin with our sanction? Why hast Thou come now to hinder us? And why dost Thou look silently and searchingly at me with Thy mild eyes? Be angry. I don’t want Thy love, for I love Thee not. And what use is it for me to hide anything from Thee? Don’t I know to Whom I am speaking? All that I can say is known to Thee already. And is it for me to conceal from Thee our mystery? Perhaps it is Thy will to hear it from my lips. Listen, then. We are not working with Thee, but with him—that is our mystery. It’s long—eight centuries—since we have been on his side and not on Thine. Just eight centuries ago, we took from him what Thou didst reject with scorn, that last gift he offered Thee, showing Thee all the kingdoms of the earth. We took from him Rome and the sword of Cæsar, and proclaimed ourselves sole rulers of the earth, though hitherto we have not been able to complete our work. But whose fault is that? Oh, the work is only beginning, but it has begun. It has long to await completion and the earth has yet much to suffer, but we shall triumph and shall be Cæsars, and then we shall plan the universal happiness of man. But Thou mightest have taken even then the sword of Cæsar. Why didst Thou reject that last gift? Hadst Thou accepted that last counsel of the mighty spirit, Thou wouldst have accomplished all that man seeks on earth—that is, some one to worship, some one to keep his conscience, and some means of uniting all in one unanimous and harmonious ant-heap, for the craving for universal unity is the third and last anguish of men. Mankind as a whole has always striven to organize a universal state. There have been many great nations with great histories, but the more highly they were developed the more unhappy they were, for they felt more acutely than other people the craving for world-wide union. The great conquerors, Timours and Ghenghis-Khans, whirled like hurricanes over the face of the earth striving to subdue its people, and they too were but the unconscious expression of the same craving for universal unity. Hadst Thou taken the world and Cæsar’s purple, Thou wouldst have founded the universal state and have given universal peace. For who can rule men if not he who holds their conscience and their bread in his hands? We have taken the sword of Cæsar, and in taking it, of course, have rejected Thee and followed him. Oh, ages are yet to come of the confusion of free thought, of their science and cannibalism. For having begun to build their tower of Babel without us, they will end, of course, with cannibalism. But then the beast will crawl to us and lick our feet and spatter them with tears of blood. And we shall sit upon the beast and raise the cup, and on it will be written, “Mystery.” But then, and only then, the reign of peace and happiness will come for men. Thou art proud of Thine elect, but Thou hast only the elect, while we give rest to all. And besides, how many of those elect, those mighty ones who could become elect, have grown weary waiting for Thee, and have transferred and will transfer the powers of their spirit and the warmth of their heart to the other camp, and end by raising their free banner against Thee. Thou didst Thyself lift up that banner. But with us all will be happy and will no more rebel nor destroy one another as under Thy freedom. Oh, we shall persuade them that they will only become free when they renounce their freedom to us and submit to us. And shall we be right or shall we be lying? They will be convinced that we are right, for they will remember the horrors of slavery and confusion to which Thy freedom brought them. Freedom, free thought and science, will lead them into such straits and will bring them face to face with such marvels and insoluble mysteries, that some of them, the fierce and rebellious, will destroy themselves, others, rebellious but weak, will destroy one another, while the rest, weak and unhappy, will crawl fawning to our feet and whine to us: “Yes, you were right, you alone possess His mystery, and we come back to you, save us from ourselves!”\n\n“‘Receiving bread from us, they will see clearly that we take the bread made by their hands from them, to give it to them, without any miracle. They will see that we do not change the stones to bread, but in truth they will be more thankful for taking it from our hands than for the bread itself! For they will remember only too well that in old days, without our help, even the bread they made turned to stones in their hands, while since they have come back to us, the very stones have turned to bread in their hands. Too, too well will they know the value of complete submission! And until men know that, they will be unhappy. Who is most to blame for their not knowing it?—speak! Who scattered the flock and sent it astray on unknown paths? But the flock will come together again and will submit once more, and then it will be once for all. Then we shall give them the quiet humble happiness of weak creatures such as they are by nature. Oh, we shall persuade them at last not to be proud, for Thou didst lift them up and thereby taught them to be proud. We shall show them that they are weak, that they are only pitiful children, but that childlike happiness is the sweetest of all. They will become timid and will look to us and huddle close to us in fear, as chicks to the hen. They will marvel at us and will be awe-stricken before us, and will be proud at our being so powerful and clever, that we have been able to subdue such a turbulent flock of thousands of millions. They will tremble impotently before our wrath, their minds will grow fearful, they will be quick to shed tears like women and children, but they will be just as ready at a sign from us to pass to laughter and rejoicing, to happy mirth and childish song. Yes, we shall set them to work, but in their leisure hours we shall make their life like a child’s game, with children’s songs and innocent dance. Oh, we shall allow them even sin, they are weak and helpless, and they will love us like children because we allow them to sin. We shall tell them that every sin will be expiated, if it is done with our permission, that we allow them to sin because we love them, and the punishment for these sins we take upon ourselves. And we shall take it upon ourselves, and they will adore us as their saviors who have taken on themselves their sins before God. And they will have no secrets from us. We shall allow or forbid them to live with their wives and mistresses, to have or not to have children—according to whether they have been obedient or disobedient—and they will submit to us gladly and cheerfully. The most painful secrets of their conscience, all, all they will bring to us, and we shall have an answer for all. And they will be glad to believe our answer, for it will save them from the great anxiety and terrible agony they endure at present in making a free decision for themselves. And all will be happy, all the millions of creatures except the hundred thousand who rule over them. For only we, we who guard the mystery, shall be unhappy. There will be thousands of millions of happy babes, and a hundred thousand sufferers who have taken upon themselves the curse of the knowledge of good and evil. Peacefully they will die, peacefully they will expire in Thy name, and beyond the grave they will find nothing but death. But we shall keep the secret, and for their happiness we shall allure them with the reward of heaven and eternity. Though if there were anything in the other world, it certainly would not be for such as they. It is prophesied that Thou wilt come again in victory, Thou wilt come with Thy chosen, the proud and strong, but we will say that they have only saved themselves, but we have saved all. We are told that the harlot who sits upon the beast, and holds in her hands the mystery, shall be put to shame, that the weak will rise up again, and will rend her royal purple and will strip naked her loathsome body. But then I will stand up and point out to Thee the thousand millions of happy children who have known no sin. And we who have taken their sins upon us for their happiness will stand up before Thee and say: “Judge us if Thou canst and darest.” Know that I fear Thee not. Know that I too have been in the wilderness, I too have lived on roots and locusts, I too prized the freedom with which Thou hast blessed men, and I too was striving to stand among Thy elect, among the strong and powerful, thirsting “to make up the number.” But I awakened and would not serve madness. I turned back and joined the ranks of those who have corrected Thy work. I left the proud and went back to the humble, for the happiness of the humble. What I say to Thee will come to pass, and our dominion will be built up. I repeat, to-morrow Thou shalt see that obedient flock who at a sign from me will hasten to heap up the hot cinders about the pile on which I shall burn Thee for coming to hinder us. For if any one has ever deserved our fires, it is Thou. To-morrow I shall burn Thee. Dixi.’”\n\nIvan stopped. He was carried away as he talked, and spoke with excitement; when he had finished, he suddenly smiled.\n\nAlyosha had listened in silence; towards the end he was greatly moved and seemed several times on the point of interrupting, but restrained himself. Now his words came with a rush.\n\n“But ... that’s absurd!” he cried, flushing. “Your poem is in praise of Jesus, not in blame of Him—as you meant it to be. And who will believe you about freedom? Is that the way to understand it? That’s not the idea of it in the Orthodox Church.... That’s Rome, and not even the whole of Rome, it’s false—those are the worst of the Catholics, the Inquisitors, the Jesuits!... And there could not be such a fantastic creature as your Inquisitor. What are these sins of mankind they take on themselves? Who are these keepers of the mystery who have taken some curse upon themselves for the happiness of mankind? When have they been seen? We know the Jesuits, they are spoken ill of, but surely they are not what you describe? They are not that at all, not at all.... They are simply the Romish army for the earthly sovereignty of the world in the future, with the Pontiff of Rome for Emperor ... that’s their ideal, but there’s no sort of mystery or lofty melancholy about it.... It’s simple lust of power, of filthy earthly gain, of domination—something like a universal serfdom with them as masters—that’s all they stand for. They don’t even believe in God perhaps. Your suffering Inquisitor is a mere fantasy.”\n\n“Stay, stay,” laughed Ivan, “how hot you are! A fantasy you say, let it be so! Of course it’s a fantasy. But allow me to say: do you really think that the Roman Catholic movement of the last centuries is actually nothing but the lust of power, of filthy earthly gain? Is that Father Païssy’s teaching?”\n\n“No, no, on the contrary, Father Païssy did once say something rather the same as you ... but of course it’s not the same, not a bit the same,” Alyosha hastily corrected himself.\n\n“A precious admission, in spite of your ‘not a bit the same.’ I ask you why your Jesuits and Inquisitors have united simply for vile material gain? Why can there not be among them one martyr oppressed by great sorrow and loving humanity? You see, only suppose that there was one such man among all those who desire nothing but filthy material gain—if there’s only one like my old Inquisitor, who had himself eaten roots in the desert and made frenzied efforts to subdue his flesh to make himself free and perfect. But yet all his life he loved humanity, and suddenly his eyes were opened, and he saw that it is no great moral blessedness to attain perfection and freedom, if at the same time one gains the conviction that millions of God’s creatures have been created as a mockery, that they will never be capable of using their freedom, that these poor rebels can never turn into giants to complete the tower, that it was not for such geese that the great idealist dreamt his dream of harmony. Seeing all that he turned back and joined—the clever people. Surely that could have happened?”\n\n“Joined whom, what clever people?” cried Alyosha, completely carried away. “They have no such great cleverness and no mysteries and secrets.... Perhaps nothing but Atheism, that’s all their secret. Your Inquisitor does not believe in God, that’s his secret!”\n\n“What if it is so! At last you have guessed it. It’s perfectly true, it’s true that that’s the whole secret, but isn’t that suffering, at least for a man like that, who has wasted his whole life in the desert and yet could not shake off his incurable love of humanity? In his old age he reached the clear conviction that nothing but the advice of the great dread spirit could build up any tolerable sort of life for the feeble, unruly, ‘incomplete, empirical creatures created in jest.’ And so, convinced of this, he sees that he must follow the counsel of the wise spirit, the dread spirit of death and destruction, and therefore accept lying and deception, and lead men consciously to death and destruction, and yet deceive them all the way so that they may not notice where they are being led, that the poor blind creatures may at least on the way think themselves happy. And note, the deception is in the name of Him in Whose ideal the old man had so fervently believed all his life long. Is not that tragic? And if only one such stood at the head of the whole army ‘filled with the lust of power only for the sake of filthy gain’—would not one such be enough to make a tragedy? More than that, one such standing at the head is enough to create the actual leading idea of the Roman Church with all its armies and Jesuits, its highest idea. I tell you frankly that I firmly believe that there has always been such a man among those who stood at the head of the movement. Who knows, there may have been some such even among the Roman Popes. Who knows, perhaps the spirit of that accursed old man who loves mankind so obstinately in his own way, is to be found even now in a whole multitude of such old men, existing not by chance but by agreement, as a secret league formed long ago for the guarding of the mystery, to guard it from the weak and the unhappy, so as to make them happy. No doubt it is so, and so it must be indeed. I fancy that even among the Masons there’s something of the same mystery at the bottom, and that that’s why the Catholics so detest the Masons as their rivals breaking up the unity of the idea, while it is so essential that there should be one flock and one shepherd.... But from the way I defend my idea I might be an author impatient of your criticism. Enough of it.”\n\n“You are perhaps a Mason yourself!” broke suddenly from Alyosha. “You don’t believe in God,” he added, speaking this time very sorrowfully. He fancied besides that his brother was looking at him ironically. “How does your poem end?” he asked, suddenly looking down. “Or was it the end?”\n\n“I meant to end it like this. When the Inquisitor ceased speaking he waited some time for his Prisoner to answer him. His silence weighed down upon him. He saw that the Prisoner had listened intently all the time, looking gently in his face and evidently not wishing to reply. The old man longed for Him to say something, however bitter and terrible. But He suddenly approached the old man in silence and softly kissed him on his bloodless aged lips. That was all His answer. The old man shuddered. His lips moved. He went to the door, opened it, and said to Him: ‘Go, and come no more ... come not at all, never, never!’ And he let Him out into the dark alleys of the town. The Prisoner went away.”\n\n“And the old man?”\n\n“The kiss glows in his heart, but the old man adheres to his idea.”\n\n“And you with him, you too?” cried Alyosha, mournfully.\n\nIvan laughed.\n\n“Why, it’s all nonsense, Alyosha. It’s only a senseless poem of a senseless student, who could never write two lines of verse. Why do you take it so seriously? Surely you don’t suppose I am going straight off to the Jesuits, to join the men who are correcting His work? Good Lord, it’s no business of mine. I told you, all I want is to live on to thirty, and then ... dash the cup to the ground!”\n\n“But the little sticky leaves, and the precious tombs, and the blue sky, and the woman you love! How will you live, how will you love them?” Alyosha cried sorrowfully. “With such a hell in your heart and your head, how can you? No, that’s just what you are going away for, to join them ... if not, you will kill yourself, you can’t endure it!”\n\n“There is a strength to endure everything,” Ivan said with a cold smile.\n\n“What strength?”\n\n“The strength of the Karamazovs—the strength of the Karamazov baseness.”\n\n“To sink into debauchery, to stifle your soul with corruption, yes?”\n\n“Possibly even that ... only perhaps till I am thirty I shall escape it, and then—”\n\n“How will you escape it? By what will you escape it? That’s impossible with your ideas.”\n\n“In the Karamazov way, again.”\n\n“‘Everything is lawful,’ you mean? Everything is lawful, is that it?”\n\nIvan scowled, and all at once turned strangely pale.\n\n“Ah, you’ve caught up yesterday’s phrase, which so offended Miüsov—and which Dmitri pounced upon so naïvely, and paraphrased!” he smiled queerly. “Yes, if you like, ‘everything is lawful’ since the word has been said. I won’t deny it. And Mitya’s version isn’t bad.”\n\nAlyosha looked at him in silence.\n\n“I thought that going away from here I have you at least,” Ivan said suddenly, with unexpected feeling; “but now I see that there is no place for me even in your heart, my dear hermit. The formula, ‘all is lawful,’ I won’t renounce—will you renounce me for that, yes?”\n\nAlyosha got up, went to him and softly kissed him on the lips.\n\n“That’s plagiarism,” cried Ivan, highly delighted. “You stole that from my poem. Thank you though. Get up, Alyosha, it’s time we were going, both of us.”\n\nThey went out, but stopped when they reached the entrance of the restaurant.\n\n“Listen, Alyosha,” Ivan began in a resolute voice, “if I am really able to care for the sticky little leaves I shall only love them, remembering you. It’s enough for me that you are somewhere here, and I shan’t lose my desire for life yet. Is that enough for you? Take it as a declaration of love if you like. And now you go to the right and I to the left. And it’s enough, do you hear, enough. I mean even if I don’t go away to-morrow (I think I certainly shall go) and we meet again, don’t say a word more on these subjects. I beg that particularly. And about Dmitri too, I ask you specially, never speak to me again,” he added, with sudden irritation; “it’s all exhausted, it has all been said over and over again, hasn’t it? And I’ll make you one promise in return for it. When at thirty, I want to ‘dash the cup to the ground,’ wherever I may be I’ll come to have one more talk with you, even though it were from America, you may be sure of that. I’ll come on purpose. It will be very interesting to have a look at you, to see what you’ll be by that time. It’s rather a solemn promise, you see. And we really may be parting for seven years or ten. Come, go now to your Pater Seraphicus, he is dying. If he dies without you, you will be angry with me for having kept you. Good-by, kiss me once more; that’s right, now go.”\n\nIvan turned suddenly and went his way without looking back. It was just as Dmitri had left Alyosha the day before, though the parting had been very different. The strange resemblance flashed like an arrow through Alyosha’s mind in the distress and dejection of that moment. He waited a little, looking after his brother. He suddenly noticed that Ivan swayed as he walked and that his right shoulder looked lower than his left. He had never noticed it before. But all at once he turned too, and almost ran to the monastery. It was nearly dark, and he felt almost frightened; something new was growing up in him for which he could not account. The wind had risen again as on the previous evening, and the ancient pines murmured gloomily about him when he entered the hermitage copse. He almost ran. “Pater Seraphicus—he got that name from somewhere—where from?” Alyosha wondered. “Ivan, poor Ivan, and when shall I see you again?... Here is the hermitage. Yes, yes, that he is, Pater Seraphicus, he will save me—from him and for ever!”\n\nSeveral times afterwards he wondered how he could on leaving Ivan so completely forget his brother Dmitri, though he had that morning, only a few hours before, so firmly resolved to find him and not to give up doing so, even should he be unable to return to the monastery that night.\n\n# Chapter VI. For Awhile A Very Obscure One\n\nAnd Ivan, on parting from Alyosha, went home to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house. But, strange to say, he was overcome by insufferable depression, which grew greater at every step he took towards the house. There was nothing strange in his being depressed; what was strange was that Ivan could not have said what was the cause of it. He had often been depressed before, and there was nothing surprising at his feeling so at such a moment, when he had broken off with everything that had brought him here, and was preparing that day to make a new start and enter upon a new, unknown future. He would again be as solitary as ever, and though he had great hopes, and great—too great—expectations from life, he could not have given any definite account of his hopes, his expectations, or even his desires.\n\nYet at that moment, though the apprehension of the new and unknown certainly found place in his heart, what was worrying him was something quite different. “Is it loathing for my father’s house?” he wondered. “Quite likely; I am so sick of it; and though it’s the last time I shall cross its hateful threshold, still I loathe it.... No, it’s not that either. Is it the parting with Alyosha and the conversation I had with him? For so many years I’ve been silent with the whole world and not deigned to speak, and all of a sudden I reel off a rigmarole like that.” It certainly might have been the youthful vexation of youthful inexperience and vanity—vexation at having failed to express himself, especially with such a being as Alyosha, on whom his heart had certainly been reckoning. No doubt that came in, that vexation, it must have done indeed; but yet that was not it, that was not it either. “I feel sick with depression and yet I can’t tell what I want. Better not think, perhaps.”\n\nIvan tried “not to think,” but that, too, was no use. What made his depression so vexatious and irritating was that it had a kind of casual, external character—he felt that. Some person or thing seemed to be standing out somewhere, just as something will sometimes obtrude itself upon the eye, and though one may be so busy with work or conversation that for a long time one does not notice it, yet it irritates and almost torments one till at last one realizes, and removes the offending object, often quite a trifling and ridiculous one—some article left about in the wrong place, a handkerchief on the floor, a book not replaced on the shelf, and so on.\n\nAt last, feeling very cross and ill-humored, Ivan arrived home, and suddenly, about fifteen paces from the garden gate, he guessed what was fretting and worrying him.\n\nOn a bench in the gateway the valet Smerdyakov was sitting enjoying the coolness of the evening, and at the first glance at him Ivan knew that the valet Smerdyakov was on his mind, and that it was this man that his soul loathed. It all dawned upon him suddenly and became clear. Just before, when Alyosha had been telling him of his meeting with Smerdyakov, he had felt a sudden twinge of gloom and loathing, which had immediately stirred responsive anger in his heart. Afterwards, as he talked, Smerdyakov had been forgotten for the time; but still he had been in his mind, and as soon as Ivan parted with Alyosha and was walking home, the forgotten sensation began to obtrude itself again. “Is it possible that a miserable, contemptible creature like that can worry me so much?” he wondered, with insufferable irritation.\n\nIt was true that Ivan had come of late to feel an intense dislike for the man, especially during the last few days. He had even begun to notice in himself a growing feeling that was almost of hatred for the creature. Perhaps this hatred was accentuated by the fact that when Ivan first came to the neighborhood he had felt quite differently. Then he had taken a marked interest in Smerdyakov, and had even thought him very original. He had encouraged him to talk to him, although he had always wondered at a certain incoherence, or rather restlessness, in his mind, and could not understand what it was that so continually and insistently worked upon the brain of “the contemplative.” They discussed philosophical questions and even how there could have been light on the first day when the sun, moon, and stars were only created on the fourth day, and how that was to be understood. But Ivan soon saw that, though the sun, moon, and stars might be an interesting subject, yet that it was quite secondary to Smerdyakov, and that he was looking for something altogether different. In one way and another, he began to betray a boundless vanity, and a wounded vanity, too, and that Ivan disliked. It had first given rise to his aversion. Later on, there had been trouble in the house. Grushenka had come on the scene, and there had been the scandals with his brother Dmitri—they discussed that, too. But though Smerdyakov always talked of that with great excitement, it was impossible to discover what he desired to come of it. There was, in fact, something surprising in the illogicality and incoherence of some of his desires, accidentally betrayed and always vaguely expressed. Smerdyakov was always inquiring, putting certain indirect but obviously premeditated questions, but what his object was he did not explain, and usually at the most important moment he would break off and relapse into silence or pass to another subject. But what finally irritated Ivan most and confirmed his dislike for him was the peculiar, revolting familiarity which Smerdyakov began to show more and more markedly. Not that he forgot himself and was rude; on the contrary, he always spoke very respectfully, yet he had obviously begun to consider—goodness knows why!—that there was some sort of understanding between him and Ivan Fyodorovitch. He always spoke in a tone that suggested that those two had some kind of compact, some secret between them, that had at some time been expressed on both sides, only known to them and beyond the comprehension of those around them. But for a long while Ivan did not recognize the real cause of his growing dislike and he had only lately realized what was at the root of it.\n\nWith a feeling of disgust and irritation he tried to pass in at the gate without speaking or looking at Smerdyakov. But Smerdyakov rose from the bench, and from that action alone, Ivan knew instantly that he wanted particularly to talk to him. Ivan looked at him and stopped, and the fact that he did stop, instead of passing by, as he meant to the minute before, drove him to fury. With anger and repulsion he looked at Smerdyakov’s emasculate, sickly face, with the little curls combed forward on his forehead. His left eye winked and he grinned as if to say, “Where are you going? You won’t pass by; you see that we two clever people have something to say to each other.”\n\nIvan shook. “Get away, miserable idiot. What have I to do with you?” was on the tip of his tongue, but to his profound astonishment he heard himself say, “Is my father still asleep, or has he waked?”\n\nHe asked the question softly and meekly, to his own surprise, and at once, again to his own surprise, sat down on the bench. For an instant he felt almost frightened; he remembered it afterwards. Smerdyakov stood facing him, his hands behind his back, looking at him with assurance and almost severity.\n\n“His honor is still asleep,” he articulated deliberately (“You were the first to speak, not I,” he seemed to say). “I am surprised at you, sir,” he added, after a pause, dropping his eyes affectedly, setting his right foot forward, and playing with the tip of his polished boot.\n\n“Why are you surprised at me?” Ivan asked abruptly and sullenly, doing his utmost to restrain himself, and suddenly realizing, with disgust, that he was feeling intense curiosity and would not, on any account, have gone away without satisfying it.\n\n“Why don’t you go to Tchermashnya, sir?” Smerdyakov suddenly raised his eyes and smiled familiarly. “Why I smile you must understand of yourself, if you are a clever man,” his screwed-up left eye seemed to say.\n\n“Why should I go to Tchermashnya?” Ivan asked in surprise.\n\nSmerdyakov was silent again.\n\n“Fyodor Pavlovitch himself has so begged you to,” he said at last, slowly and apparently attaching no significance to his answer. “I put you off with a secondary reason,” he seemed to suggest, “simply to say something.”\n\n“Damn you! Speak out what you want!” Ivan cried angrily at last, passing from meekness to violence.\n\nSmerdyakov drew his right foot up to his left, pulled himself up, but still looked at him with the same serenity and the same little smile.\n\n“Substantially nothing—but just by way of conversation.”\n\nAnother silence followed. They did not speak for nearly a minute. Ivan knew that he ought to get up and show anger, and Smerdyakov stood before him and seemed to be waiting as though to see whether he would be angry or not. So at least it seemed to Ivan. At last he moved to get up. Smerdyakov seemed to seize the moment.\n\n“I’m in an awful position, Ivan Fyodorovitch. I don’t know how to help myself,” he said resolutely and distinctly, and at his last word he sighed. Ivan Fyodorovitch sat down again.\n\n“They are both utterly crazy, they are no better than little children,” Smerdyakov went on. “I am speaking of your parent and your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch. Here Fyodor Pavlovitch will get up directly and begin worrying me every minute, ‘Has she come? Why hasn’t she come?’ and so on up till midnight and even after midnight. And if Agrafena Alexandrovna doesn’t come (for very likely she does not mean to come at all) then he will be at me again to-morrow morning, ‘Why hasn’t she come? When will she come?’—as though I were to blame for it. On the other side it’s no better. As soon as it gets dark, or even before, your brother will appear with his gun in his hands: ‘Look out, you rogue, you soup-maker. If you miss her and don’t let me know she’s been—I’ll kill you before any one.’ When the night’s over, in the morning, he, too, like Fyodor Pavlovitch, begins worrying me to death. ‘Why hasn’t she come? Will she come soon?’ And he, too, thinks me to blame because his lady hasn’t come. And every day and every hour they get angrier and angrier, so that I sometimes think I shall kill myself in a fright. I can’t depend upon them, sir.”\n\n“And why have you meddled? Why did you begin to spy for Dmitri Fyodorovitch?” said Ivan irritably.\n\n“How could I help meddling? Though, indeed, I haven’t meddled at all, if you want to know the truth of the matter. I kept quiet from the very beginning, not daring to answer; but he pitched on me to be his servant. He has had only one thing to say since: ‘I’ll kill you, you scoundrel, if you miss her,’ I feel certain, sir, that I shall have a long fit to-morrow.”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘a long fit’?”\n\n“A long fit, lasting a long time—several hours, or perhaps a day or two. Once it went on for three days. I fell from the garret that time. The struggling ceased and then began again, and for three days I couldn’t come back to my senses. Fyodor Pavlovitch sent for Herzenstube, the doctor here, and he put ice on my head and tried another remedy, too.... I might have died.”\n\n“But they say one can’t tell with epilepsy when a fit is coming. What makes you say you will have one to-morrow?” Ivan inquired, with a peculiar, irritable curiosity.\n\n“That’s just so. You can’t tell beforehand.”\n\n“Besides, you fell from the garret then.”\n\n“I climb up to the garret every day. I might fall from the garret again to-morrow. And, if not, I might fall down the cellar steps. I have to go into the cellar every day, too.”\n\nIvan took a long look at him.\n\n“You are talking nonsense, I see, and I don’t quite understand you,” he said softly, but with a sort of menace. “Do you mean to pretend to be ill to-morrow for three days, eh?”\n\nSmerdyakov, who was looking at the ground again, and playing with the toe of his right foot, set the foot down, moved the left one forward, and, grinning, articulated:\n\n“If I were able to play such a trick, that is, pretend to have a fit—and it would not be difficult for a man accustomed to them—I should have a perfect right to use such a means to save myself from death. For even if Agrafena Alexandrovna comes to see his father while I am ill, his honor can’t blame a sick man for not telling him. He’d be ashamed to.”\n\n“Hang it all!” Ivan cried, his face working with anger, “why are you always in such a funk for your life? All my brother Dmitri’s threats are only hasty words and mean nothing. He won’t kill you; it’s not you he’ll kill!”\n\n“He’d kill me first of all, like a fly. But even more than that, I am afraid I shall be taken for an accomplice of his when he does something crazy to his father.”\n\n“Why should you be taken for an accomplice?”\n\n“They’ll think I am an accomplice, because I let him know the signals as a great secret.”\n\n“What signals? Whom did you tell? Confound you, speak more plainly.”\n\n“I’m bound to admit the fact,” Smerdyakov drawled with pedantic composure, “that I have a secret with Fyodor Pavlovitch in this business. As you know yourself (if only you do know it) he has for several days past locked himself in as soon as night or even evening comes on. Of late you’ve been going upstairs to your room early every evening, and yesterday you did not come down at all, and so perhaps you don’t know how carefully he has begun to lock himself in at night, and even if Grigory Vassilyevitch comes to the door he won’t open to him till he hears his voice. But Grigory Vassilyevitch does not come, because I wait upon him alone in his room now. That’s the arrangement he made himself ever since this to-do with Agrafena Alexandrovna began. But at night, by his orders, I go away to the lodge so that I don’t get to sleep till midnight, but am on the watch, getting up and walking about the yard, waiting for Agrafena Alexandrovna to come. For the last few days he’s been perfectly frantic expecting her. What he argues is, she is afraid of him, Dmitri Fyodorovitch (Mitya, as he calls him), ‘and so,’ says he, ‘she’ll come the back-way, late at night, to me. You look out for her,’ says he, ‘till midnight and later; and if she does come, you run up and knock at my door or at the window from the garden. Knock at first twice, rather gently, and then three times more quickly, then,’ says he, ‘I shall understand at once that she has come, and will open the door to you quietly.’ Another signal he gave me in case anything unexpected happens. At first, two knocks, and then, after an interval, another much louder. Then he will understand that something has happened suddenly and that I must see him, and he will open to me so that I can go and speak to him. That’s all in case Agrafena Alexandrovna can’t come herself, but sends a message. Besides, Dmitri Fyodorovitch might come, too, so I must let him know he is near. His honor is awfully afraid of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, so that even if Agrafena Alexandrovna had come and were locked in with him, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch were to turn up anywhere near at the time, I should be bound to let him know at once, knocking three times. So that the first signal of five knocks means Agrafena Alexandrovna has come, while the second signal of three knocks means ‘something important to tell you.’ His honor has shown me them several times and explained them. And as in the whole universe no one knows of these signals but myself and his honor, so he’d open the door without the slightest hesitation and without calling out (he is awfully afraid of calling out aloud). Well, those signals are known to Dmitri Fyodorovitch too, now.”\n\n“How are they known? Did you tell him? How dared you tell him?”\n\n“It was through fright I did it. How could I dare to keep it back from him? Dmitri Fyodorovitch kept persisting every day, ‘You are deceiving me, you are hiding something from me! I’ll break both your legs for you.’ So I told him those secret signals that he might see my slavish devotion, and might be satisfied that I was not deceiving him, but was telling him all I could.”\n\n“If you think that he’ll make use of those signals and try to get in, don’t let him in.”\n\n“But if I should be laid up with a fit, how can I prevent him coming in then, even if I dared prevent him, knowing how desperate he is?”\n\n“Hang it! How can you be so sure you are going to have a fit, confound you? Are you laughing at me?”\n\n“How could I dare laugh at you? I am in no laughing humor with this fear on me. I feel I am going to have a fit. I have a presentiment. Fright alone will bring it on.”\n\n“Confound it! If you are laid up, Grigory will be on the watch. Let Grigory know beforehand; he will be sure not to let him in.”\n\n“I should never dare to tell Grigory Vassilyevitch about the signals without orders from my master. And as for Grigory Vassilyevitch hearing him and not admitting him, he has been ill ever since yesterday, and Marfa Ignatyevna intends to give him medicine to-morrow. They’ve just arranged it. It’s a very strange remedy of hers. Marfa Ignatyevna knows of a preparation and always keeps it. It’s a strong thing made from some herb. She has the secret of it, and she always gives it to Grigory Vassilyevitch three times a year when his lumbago’s so bad he is almost paralyzed by it. Then she takes a towel, wets it with the stuff, and rubs his whole back for half an hour till it’s quite red and swollen, and what’s left in the bottle she gives him to drink with a special prayer; but not quite all, for on such occasions she leaves some for herself, and drinks it herself. And as they never take strong drink, I assure you they both drop asleep at once and sleep sound a very long time. And when Grigory Vassilyevitch wakes up he is perfectly well after it, but Marfa Ignatyevna always has a headache from it. So, if Marfa Ignatyevna carries out her intention to-morrow, they won’t hear anything and hinder Dmitri Fyodorovitch. They’ll be asleep.”\n\n“What a rigmarole! And it all seems to happen at once, as though it were planned. You’ll have a fit and they’ll both be unconscious,” cried Ivan. “But aren’t you trying to arrange it so?” broke from him suddenly, and he frowned threateningly.\n\n“How could I?... And why should I, when it all depends on Dmitri Fyodorovitch and his plans?... If he means to do anything, he’ll do it; but if not, I shan’t be thrusting him upon his father.”\n\n“And why should he go to father, especially on the sly, if, as you say yourself, Agrafena Alexandrovna won’t come at all?” Ivan went on, turning white with anger. “You say that yourself, and all the while I’ve been here, I’ve felt sure it was all the old man’s fancy, and the creature won’t come to him. Why should Dmitri break in on him if she doesn’t come? Speak, I want to know what you are thinking!”\n\n“You know yourself why he’ll come. What’s the use of what I think? His honor will come simply because he is in a rage or suspicious on account of my illness perhaps, and he’ll dash in, as he did yesterday through impatience to search the rooms, to see whether she hasn’t escaped him on the sly. He is perfectly well aware, too, that Fyodor Pavlovitch has a big envelope with three thousand roubles in it, tied up with ribbon and sealed with three seals. On it is written in his own hand, ‘To my angel Grushenka, if she will come,’ to which he added three days later, ‘for my little chicken.’ There’s no knowing what that might do.”\n\n“Nonsense!” cried Ivan, almost beside himself. “Dmitri won’t come to steal money and kill my father to do it. He might have killed him yesterday on account of Grushenka, like the frantic, savage fool he is, but he won’t steal.”\n\n“He is in very great need of money now—the greatest need, Ivan Fyodorovitch. You don’t know in what need he is,” Smerdyakov explained, with perfect composure and remarkable distinctness. “He looks on that three thousand as his own, too. He said so to me himself. ‘My father still owes me just three thousand,’ he said. And besides that, consider, Ivan Fyodorovitch, there is something else perfectly true. It’s as good as certain, so to say, that Agrafena Alexandrovna will force him, if only she cares to, to marry her—the master himself, I mean, Fyodor Pavlovitch—if only she cares to, and of course she may care to. All I’ve said is that she won’t come, but maybe she’s looking for more than that—I mean to be mistress here. I know myself that Samsonov, her merchant, was laughing with her about it, telling her quite openly that it would not be at all a stupid thing to do. And she’s got plenty of sense. She wouldn’t marry a beggar like Dmitri Fyodorovitch. So, taking that into consideration, Ivan Fyodorovitch, reflect that then neither Dmitri Fyodorovitch nor yourself and your brother, Alexey Fyodorovitch, would have anything after the master’s death, not a rouble, for Agrafena Alexandrovna would marry him simply to get hold of the whole, all the money there is. But if your father were to die now, there’d be some forty thousand for sure, even for Dmitri Fyodorovitch whom he hates so, for he’s made no will.... Dmitri Fyodorovitch knows all that very well.”\n\nA sort of shudder passed over Ivan’s face. He suddenly flushed.\n\n“Then why on earth,” he suddenly interrupted Smerdyakov, “do you advise me to go to Tchermashnya? What did you mean by that? If I go away, you see what will happen here.” Ivan drew his breath with difficulty.\n\n“Precisely so,” said Smerdyakov, softly and reasonably, watching Ivan intently, however.\n\n“What do you mean by ‘precisely so’?” Ivan questioned him, with a menacing light in his eyes, restraining himself with difficulty.\n\n“I spoke because I felt sorry for you. If I were in your place I should simply throw it all up ... rather than stay on in such a position,” answered Smerdyakov, with the most candid air looking at Ivan’s flashing eyes. They were both silent.\n\n“You seem to be a perfect idiot, and what’s more ... an awful scoundrel, too.” Ivan rose suddenly from the bench. He was about to pass straight through the gate, but he stopped short and turned to Smerdyakov. Something strange followed. Ivan, in a sudden paroxysm, bit his lip, clenched his fists, and, in another minute, would have flung himself on Smerdyakov. The latter, anyway, noticed it at the same moment, started, and shrank back. But the moment passed without mischief to Smerdyakov, and Ivan turned in silence, as it seemed in perplexity, to the gate.\n\n“I am going away to Moscow to-morrow, if you care to know—early to-morrow morning. That’s all!” he suddenly said aloud angrily, and wondered himself afterwards what need there was to say this then to Smerdyakov.\n\n“That’s the best thing you can do,” he responded, as though he had expected to hear it; “except that you can always be telegraphed for from Moscow, if anything should happen here.”\n\nIvan stopped again, and again turned quickly to Smerdyakov. But a change had passed over him, too. All his familiarity and carelessness had completely disappeared. His face expressed attention and expectation, intent but timid and cringing.\n\n“Haven’t you something more to say—something to add?” could be read in the intent gaze he fixed on Ivan.\n\n“And couldn’t I be sent for from Tchermashnya, too—in case anything happened?” Ivan shouted suddenly, for some unknown reason raising his voice.\n\n“From Tchermashnya, too ... you could be sent for,” Smerdyakov muttered, almost in a whisper, looking disconcerted, but gazing intently into Ivan’s eyes.\n\n“Only Moscow is farther and Tchermashnya is nearer. Is it to save my spending money on the fare, or to save my going so far out of my way, that you insist on Tchermashnya?”\n\n“Precisely so ...” muttered Smerdyakov, with a breaking voice. He looked at Ivan with a revolting smile, and again made ready to draw back. But to his astonishment Ivan broke into a laugh, and went through the gate still laughing. Any one who had seen his face at that moment would have known that he was not laughing from lightness of heart, and he could not have explained himself what he was feeling at that instant. He moved and walked as though in a nervous frenzy.\n\n# Chapter VII. “It’s Always Worth While Speaking To A Clever Man”\n\nAnd in the same nervous frenzy, too, he spoke. Meeting Fyodor Pavlovitch in the drawing-room directly he went in, he shouted to him, waving his hands, “I am going upstairs to my room, not in to you. Good-by!” and passed by, trying not even to look at his father. Very possibly the old man was too hateful to him at that moment; but such an unceremonious display of hostility was a surprise even to Fyodor Pavlovitch. And the old man evidently wanted to tell him something at once and had come to meet him in the drawing-room on purpose. Receiving this amiable greeting, he stood still in silence and with an ironical air watched his son going upstairs, till he passed out of sight.\n\n“What’s the matter with him?” he promptly asked Smerdyakov, who had followed Ivan.\n\n“Angry about something. Who can tell?” the valet muttered evasively.\n\n“Confound him! Let him be angry then. Bring in the samovar, and get along with you. Look sharp! No news?”\n\nThen followed a series of questions such as Smerdyakov had just complained of to Ivan, all relating to his expected visitor, and these questions we will omit. Half an hour later the house was locked, and the crazy old man was wandering along through the rooms in excited expectation of hearing every minute the five knocks agreed upon. Now and then he peered out into the darkness, seeing nothing.\n\nIt was very late, but Ivan was still awake and reflecting. He sat up late that night, till two o’clock. But we will not give an account of his thoughts, and this is not the place to look into that soul—its turn will come. And even if one tried, it would be very hard to give an account of them, for there were no thoughts in his brain, but something very vague, and, above all, intense excitement. He felt himself that he had lost his bearings. He was fretted, too, by all sorts of strange and almost surprising desires; for instance, after midnight he suddenly had an intense irresistible inclination to go down, open the door, go to the lodge and beat Smerdyakov. But if he had been asked why, he could not have given any exact reason, except perhaps that he loathed the valet as one who had insulted him more gravely than any one in the world. On the other hand, he was more than once that night overcome by a sort of inexplicable humiliating terror, which he felt positively paralyzed his physical powers. His head ached and he was giddy. A feeling of hatred was rankling in his heart, as though he meant to avenge himself on some one. He even hated Alyosha, recalling the conversation he had just had with him. At moments he hated himself intensely. Of Katerina Ivanovna he almost forgot to think, and wondered greatly at this afterwards, especially as he remembered perfectly that when he had protested so valiantly to Katerina Ivanovna that he would go away next day to Moscow, something had whispered in his heart, “That’s nonsense, you are not going, and it won’t be so easy to tear yourself away as you are boasting now.”\n\nRemembering that night long afterwards, Ivan recalled with peculiar repulsion how he had suddenly got up from the sofa and had stealthily, as though he were afraid of being watched, opened the door, gone out on the staircase and listened to Fyodor Pavlovitch stirring down below, had listened a long while—some five minutes—with a sort of strange curiosity, holding his breath while his heart throbbed. And why he had done all this, why he was listening, he could not have said. That “action” all his life afterwards he called “infamous,” and at the bottom of his heart, he thought of it as the basest action of his life. For Fyodor Pavlovitch himself he felt no hatred at that moment, but was simply intensely curious to know how he was walking down there below and what he must be doing now. He wondered and imagined how he must be peeping out of the dark windows and stopping in the middle of the room, listening, listening—for some one to knock. Ivan went out on to the stairs twice to listen like this.\n\nAbout two o’clock when everything was quiet, and even Fyodor Pavlovitch had gone to bed, Ivan had got into bed, firmly resolved to fall asleep at once, as he felt fearfully exhausted. And he did fall asleep at once, and slept soundly without dreams, but waked early, at seven o’clock, when it was broad daylight. Opening his eyes, he was surprised to feel himself extraordinarily vigorous. He jumped up at once and dressed quickly; then dragged out his trunk and began packing immediately. His linen had come back from the laundress the previous morning. Ivan positively smiled at the thought that everything was helping his sudden departure. And his departure certainly was sudden. Though Ivan had said the day before (to Katerina Ivanovna, Alyosha, and Smerdyakov) that he was leaving next day, yet he remembered that he had no thought of departure when he went to bed, or, at least, had not dreamed that his first act in the morning would be to pack his trunk. At last his trunk and bag were ready. It was about nine o’clock when Marfa Ignatyevna came in with her usual inquiry, “Where will your honor take your tea, in your own room or downstairs?” He looked almost cheerful, but there was about him, about his words and gestures, something hurried and scattered. Greeting his father affably, and even inquiring specially after his health, though he did not wait to hear his answer to the end, he announced that he was starting off in an hour to return to Moscow for good, and begged him to send for the horses. His father heard this announcement with no sign of surprise, and forgot in an unmannerly way to show regret at losing him. Instead of doing so, he flew into a great flutter at the recollection of some important business of his own.\n\n“What a fellow you are! Not to tell me yesterday! Never mind; we’ll manage it all the same. Do me a great service, my dear boy. Go to Tchermashnya on the way. It’s only to turn to the left from the station at Volovya, only another twelve versts and you come to Tchermashnya.”\n\n“I’m sorry, I can’t. It’s eighty versts to the railway and the train starts for Moscow at seven o’clock to-night. I can only just catch it.”\n\n“You’ll catch it to-morrow or the day after, but to-day turn off to Tchermashnya. It won’t put you out much to humor your father! If I hadn’t had something to keep me here, I would have run over myself long ago, for I’ve some business there in a hurry. But here I ... it’s not the time for me to go now.... You see, I’ve two pieces of copse land there. The Maslovs, an old merchant and his son, will give eight thousand for the timber. But last year I just missed a purchaser who would have given twelve. There’s no getting any one about here to buy it. The Maslovs have it all their own way. One has to take what they’ll give, for no one here dare bid against them. The priest at Ilyinskoe wrote to me last Thursday that a merchant called Gorstkin, a man I know, had turned up. What makes him valuable is that he is not from these parts, so he is not afraid of the Maslovs. He says he will give me eleven thousand for the copse. Do you hear? But he’ll only be here, the priest writes, for a week altogether, so you must go at once and make a bargain with him.”\n\n“Well, you write to the priest; he’ll make the bargain.”\n\n“He can’t do it. He has no eye for business. He is a perfect treasure, I’d give him twenty thousand to take care of for me without a receipt; but he has no eye for business, he is a perfect child, a crow could deceive him. And yet he is a learned man, would you believe it? This Gorstkin looks like a peasant, he wears a blue kaftan, but he is a regular rogue. That’s the common complaint. He is a liar. Sometimes he tells such lies that you wonder why he is doing it. He told me the year before last that his wife was dead and that he had married another, and would you believe it, there was not a word of truth in it? His wife has never died at all, she is alive to this day and gives him a beating twice a week. So what you have to find out is whether he is lying or speaking the truth, when he says he wants to buy it and would give eleven thousand.”\n\n“I shall be no use in such a business. I have no eye either.”\n\n“Stay, wait a bit! You will be of use, for I will tell you the signs by which you can judge about Gorstkin. I’ve done business with him a long time. You see, you must watch his beard; he has a nasty, thin, red beard. If his beard shakes when he talks and he gets cross, it’s all right, he is saying what he means, he wants to do business. But if he strokes his beard with his left hand and grins—he is trying to cheat you. Don’t watch his eyes, you won’t find out anything from his eyes, he is a deep one, a rogue—but watch his beard! I’ll give you a note and you show it to him. He’s called Gorstkin, though his real name is Lyagavy;[4] but don’t call him so, he will be offended. If you come to an understanding with him, and see it’s all right, write here at once. You need only write: ‘He’s not lying.’ Stand out for eleven thousand; one thousand you can knock off, but not more. Just think! there’s a difference between eight thousand and eleven thousand. It’s as good as picking up three thousand; it’s not so easy to find a purchaser, and I’m in desperate need of money. Only let me know it’s serious, and I’ll run over and fix it up. I’ll snatch the time somehow. But what’s the good of my galloping over, if it’s all a notion of the priest’s? Come, will you go?”\n\n“Oh, I can’t spare the time. You must excuse me.”\n\n“Come, you might oblige your father. I shan’t forget it. You’ve no heart, any of you—that’s what it is? What’s a day or two to you? Where are you going now—to Venice? Your Venice will keep another two days. I would have sent Alyosha, but what use is Alyosha in a thing like that? I send you just because you are a clever fellow. Do you suppose I don’t see that? You know nothing about timber, but you’ve got an eye. All that is wanted is to see whether the man is in earnest. I tell you, watch his beard—if his beard shakes you know he is in earnest.”\n\n“You force me to go to that damned Tchermashnya yourself, then?” cried Ivan, with a malignant smile.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch did not catch, or would not catch, the malignancy, but he caught the smile.\n\n“Then you’ll go, you’ll go? I’ll scribble the note for you at once.”\n\n“I don’t know whether I shall go. I don’t know. I’ll decide on the way.”\n\n“Nonsense! Decide at once. My dear fellow, decide! If you settle the matter, write me a line; give it to the priest and he’ll send it on to me at once. And I won’t delay you more than that. You can go to Venice. The priest will give you horses back to Volovya station.”\n\nThe old man was quite delighted. He wrote the note, and sent for the horses. A light lunch was brought in, with brandy. When Fyodor Pavlovitch was pleased, he usually became expansive, but to-day he seemed to restrain himself. Of Dmitri, for instance, he did not say a word. He was quite unmoved by the parting, and seemed, in fact, at a loss for something to say. Ivan noticed this particularly. “He must be bored with me,” he thought. Only when accompanying his son out on to the steps, the old man began to fuss about. He would have kissed him, but Ivan made haste to hold out his hand, obviously avoiding the kiss. His father saw it at once, and instantly pulled himself up.\n\n“Well, good luck to you, good luck to you!” he repeated from the steps. “You’ll come again some time or other? Mind you do come. I shall always be glad to see you. Well, Christ be with you!”\n\nIvan got into the carriage.\n\n“Good-by, Ivan! Don’t be too hard on me!” the father called for the last time.\n\nThe whole household came out to take leave—Smerdyakov, Marfa and Grigory. Ivan gave them ten roubles each. When he had seated himself in the carriage, Smerdyakov jumped up to arrange the rug.\n\n“You see ... I am going to Tchermashnya,” broke suddenly from Ivan. Again, as the day before, the words seemed to drop of themselves, and he laughed, too, a peculiar, nervous laugh. He remembered it long after.\n\n“It’s a true saying then, that ‘it’s always worth while speaking to a clever man,’” answered Smerdyakov firmly, looking significantly at Ivan.\n\nThe carriage rolled away. Nothing was clear in Ivan’s soul, but he looked eagerly around him at the fields, at the hills, at the trees, at a flock of geese flying high overhead in the bright sky. And all of a sudden he felt very happy. He tried to talk to the driver, and he felt intensely interested in an answer the peasant made him; but a minute later he realized that he was not catching anything, and that he had not really even taken in the peasant’s answer. He was silent, and it was pleasant even so. The air was fresh, pure and cool, the sky bright. The images of Alyosha and Katerina Ivanovna floated into his mind. But he softly smiled, blew softly on the friendly phantoms, and they flew away. “There’s plenty of time for them,” he thought. They reached the station quickly, changed horses, and galloped to Volovya. “Why is it worth while speaking to a clever man? What did he mean by that?” The thought seemed suddenly to clutch at his breathing. “And why did I tell him I was going to Tchermashnya?” They reached Volovya station. Ivan got out of the carriage, and the drivers stood round him bargaining over the journey of twelve versts to Tchermashnya. He told them to harness the horses. He went into the station house, looked round, glanced at the overseer’s wife, and suddenly went back to the entrance.\n\n“I won’t go to Tchermashnya. Am I too late to reach the railway by seven, brothers?”\n\n“We shall just do it. Shall we get the carriage out?”\n\n“At once. Will any one of you be going to the town to-morrow?”\n\n“To be sure. Mitri here will.”\n\n“Can you do me a service, Mitri? Go to my father’s, to Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, and tell him I haven’t gone to Tchermashnya. Can you?”\n\n“Of course I can. I’ve known Fyodor Pavlovitch a long time.”\n\n“And here’s something for you, for I dare say he won’t give you anything,” said Ivan, laughing gayly.\n\n“You may depend on it he won’t.” Mitya laughed too. “Thank you, sir. I’ll be sure to do it.”\n\nAt seven o’clock Ivan got into the train and set off to Moscow “Away with the past. I’ve done with the old world for ever, and may I have no news, no echo, from it. To a new life, new places and no looking back!” But instead of delight his soul was filled with such gloom, and his heart ached with such anguish, as he had never known in his life before. He was thinking all the night. The train flew on, and only at daybreak, when he was approaching Moscow, he suddenly roused himself from his meditation.\n\n“I am a scoundrel,” he whispered to himself.\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch remained well satisfied at having seen his son off. For two hours afterwards he felt almost happy, and sat drinking brandy. But suddenly something happened which was very annoying and unpleasant for every one in the house, and completely upset Fyodor Pavlovitch’s equanimity at once. Smerdyakov went to the cellar for something and fell down from the top of the steps. Fortunately, Marfa Ignatyevna was in the yard and heard him in time. She did not see the fall, but heard his scream—the strange, peculiar scream, long familiar to her—the scream of the epileptic falling in a fit. They could not tell whether the fit had come on him at the moment he was descending the steps, so that he must have fallen unconscious, or whether it was the fall and the shock that had caused the fit in Smerdyakov, who was known to be liable to them. They found him at the bottom of the cellar steps, writhing in convulsions and foaming at the mouth. It was thought at first that he must have broken something—an arm or a leg—and hurt himself, but “God had preserved him,” as Marfa Ignatyevna expressed it—nothing of the kind had happened. But it was difficult to get him out of the cellar. They asked the neighbors to help and managed it somehow. Fyodor Pavlovitch himself was present at the whole ceremony. He helped, evidently alarmed and upset. The sick man did not regain consciousness; the convulsions ceased for a time, but then began again, and every one concluded that the same thing would happen, as had happened a year before, when he accidentally fell from the garret. They remembered that ice had been put on his head then. There was still ice in the cellar, and Marfa Ignatyevna had some brought up. In the evening, Fyodor Pavlovitch sent for Doctor Herzenstube, who arrived at once. He was a most estimable old man, and the most careful and conscientious doctor in the province. After careful examination, he concluded that the fit was a very violent one and might have serious consequences; that meanwhile he, Herzenstube, did not fully understand it, but that by to-morrow morning, if the present remedies were unavailing, he would venture to try something else. The invalid was taken to the lodge, to a room next to Grigory’s and Marfa Ignatyevna’s.\n\nThen Fyodor Pavlovitch had one misfortune after another to put up with that day. Marfa Ignatyevna cooked the dinner, and the soup, compared with Smerdyakov’s, was “no better than dish-water,” and the fowl was so dried up that it was impossible to masticate it. To her master’s bitter, though deserved, reproaches, Marfa Ignatyevna replied that the fowl was a very old one to begin with, and that she had never been trained as a cook. In the evening there was another trouble in store for Fyodor Pavlovitch; he was informed that Grigory, who had not been well for the last three days, was completely laid up by his lumbago. Fyodor Pavlovitch finished his tea as early as possible and locked himself up alone in the house. He was in terrible excitement and suspense. That evening he reckoned on Grushenka’s coming almost as a certainty. He had received from Smerdyakov that morning an assurance “that she had promised to come without fail.” The incorrigible old man’s heart throbbed with excitement; he paced up and down his empty rooms listening. He had to be on the alert. Dmitri might be on the watch for her somewhere, and when she knocked on the window (Smerdyakov had informed him two days before that he had told her where and how to knock) the door must be opened at once. She must not be a second in the passage, for fear—which God forbid!—that she should be frightened and run away. Fyodor Pavlovitch had much to think of, but never had his heart been steeped in such voluptuous hopes. This time he could say almost certainly that she would come!\n\n# Book VI. The Russian Monk Chapter I. Father Zossima And His Visitors\n\nWhen with an anxious and aching heart Alyosha went into his elder’s cell, he stood still almost astonished. Instead of a sick man at his last gasp, perhaps unconscious, as he had feared to find him, he saw him sitting up in his chair and, though weak and exhausted, his face was bright and cheerful, he was surrounded by visitors and engaged in a quiet and joyful conversation. But he had only got up from his bed a quarter of an hour before Alyosha’s arrival; his visitors had gathered together in his cell earlier, waiting for him to wake, having received a most confident assurance from Father Païssy that “the teacher would get up, and as he had himself promised in the morning, converse once more with those dear to his heart.” This promise and indeed every word of the dying elder Father Païssy put implicit trust in. If he had seen him unconscious, if he had seen him breathe his last, and yet had his promise that he would rise up and say good-by to him, he would not have believed perhaps even in death, but would still have expected the dead man to recover and fulfill his promise. In the morning as he lay down to sleep, Father Zossima had told him positively: “I shall not die without the delight of another conversation with you, beloved of my heart. I shall look once more on your dear face and pour out my heart to you once again.” The monks, who had gathered for this probably last conversation with Father Zossima, had all been his devoted friends for many years. There were four of them: Father Iosif and Father Païssy, Father Mihaïl, the warden of the hermitage, a man not very old and far from being learned. He was of humble origin, of strong will and steadfast faith, of austere appearance, but of deep tenderness, though he obviously concealed it as though he were almost ashamed of it. The fourth, Father Anfim, was a very old and humble little monk of the poorest peasant class. He was almost illiterate, and very quiet, scarcely speaking to any one. He was the humblest of the humble, and looked as though he had been frightened by something great and awful beyond the scope of his intelligence. Father Zossima had a great affection for this timorous man, and always treated him with marked respect, though perhaps there was no one he had known to whom he had said less, in spite of the fact that he had spent years wandering about holy Russia with him. That was very long ago, forty years before, when Father Zossima first began his life as a monk in a poor and little monastery at Kostroma, and when, shortly after, he had accompanied Father Anfim on his pilgrimage to collect alms for their poor monastery.\n\nThe whole party were in the bedroom which, as we mentioned before, was very small, so that there was scarcely room for the four of them (in addition to Porfiry, the novice, who stood) to sit round Father Zossima on chairs brought from the sitting-room. It was already beginning to get dark, the room was lighted up by the lamps and the candles before the ikons.\n\nSeeing Alyosha standing embarrassed in the doorway, Father Zossima smiled at him joyfully and held out his hand.\n\n“Welcome, my quiet one, welcome, my dear, here you are too. I knew you would come.”\n\nAlyosha went up to him, bowed down before him to the ground and wept. Something surged up from his heart, his soul was quivering, he wanted to sob.\n\n“Come, don’t weep over me yet,” Father Zossima smiled, laying his right hand on his head. “You see I am sitting up talking; maybe I shall live another twenty years yet, as that dear good woman from Vishegorye, with her little Lizaveta in her arms, wished me yesterday. God bless the mother and the little girl Lizaveta,” he crossed himself. “Porfiry, did you take her offering where I told you?”\n\nHe meant the sixty copecks brought him the day before by the good-humored woman to be given “to some one poorer than me.” Such offerings, always of money gained by personal toil, are made by way of penance voluntarily undertaken. The elder had sent Porfiry the evening before to a widow, whose house had been burnt down lately, and who after the fire had gone with her children begging alms. Porfiry hastened to reply that he had given the money, as he had been instructed, “from an unknown benefactress.”\n\n“Get up, my dear boy,” the elder went on to Alyosha. “Let me look at you. Have you been home and seen your brother?” It seemed strange to Alyosha that he asked so confidently and precisely, about one of his brothers only—but which one? Then perhaps he had sent him out both yesterday and to-day for the sake of that brother.\n\n“I have seen one of my brothers,” answered Alyosha.\n\n“I mean the elder one, to whom I bowed down.”\n\n“I only saw him yesterday and could not find him to-day,” said Alyosha.\n\n“Make haste to find him, go again to-morrow and make haste, leave everything and make haste. Perhaps you may still have time to prevent something terrible. I bowed down yesterday to the great suffering in store for him.”\n\nHe was suddenly silent and seemed to be pondering. The words were strange. Father Iosif, who had witnessed the scene yesterday, exchanged glances with Father Païssy. Alyosha could not resist asking:\n\n“Father and teacher,” he began with extreme emotion, “your words are too obscure.... What is this suffering in store for him?”\n\n“Don’t inquire. I seemed to see something terrible yesterday ... as though his whole future were expressed in his eyes. A look came into his eyes—so that I was instantly horror-stricken at what that man is preparing for himself. Once or twice in my life I’ve seen such a look in a man’s face ... reflecting as it were his future fate, and that fate, alas, came to pass. I sent you to him, Alexey, for I thought your brotherly face would help him. But everything and all our fates are from the Lord. ‘Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone; but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit.’ Remember that. You, Alexey, I’ve many times silently blessed for your face, know that,” added the elder with a gentle smile. “This is what I think of you, you will go forth from these walls, but will live like a monk in the world. You will have many enemies, but even your foes will love you. Life will bring you many misfortunes, but you will find your happiness in them, and will bless life and will make others bless it—which is what matters most. Well, that is your character. Fathers and teachers,” he addressed his friends with a tender smile, “I have never till to-day told even him why the face of this youth is so dear to me. Now I will tell you. His face has been as it were a remembrance and a prophecy for me. At the dawn of my life when I was a child I had an elder brother who died before my eyes at seventeen. And later on in the course of my life I gradually became convinced that that brother had been for a guidance and a sign from on high for me. For had he not come into my life, I should never perhaps, so I fancy at least, have become a monk and entered on this precious path. He appeared first to me in my childhood, and here, at the end of my pilgrimage, he seems to have come to me over again. It is marvelous, fathers and teachers, that Alexey, who has some, though not a great, resemblance in face, seems to me so like him spiritually, that many times I have taken him for that young man, my brother, mysteriously come back to me at the end of my pilgrimage, as a reminder and an inspiration. So that I positively wondered at so strange a dream in myself. Do you hear this, Porfiry?” he turned to the novice who waited on him. “Many times I’ve seen in your face as it were a look of mortification that I love Alexey more than you. Now you know why that was so, but I love you too, know that, and many times I grieved at your mortification. I should like to tell you, dear friends, of that youth, my brother, for there has been no presence in my life more precious, more significant and touching. My heart is full of tenderness, and I look at my whole life at this moment as though living through it again.”\n\nHere I must observe that this last conversation of Father Zossima with the friends who visited him on the last day of his life has been partly preserved in writing. Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov wrote it down from memory, some time after his elder’s death. But whether this was only the conversation that took place then, or whether he added to it his notes of parts of former conversations with his teacher, I cannot determine. In his account, Father Zossima’s talk goes on without interruption, as though he told his life to his friends in the form of a story, though there is no doubt, from other accounts of it, that the conversation that evening was general. Though the guests did not interrupt Father Zossima much, yet they too talked, perhaps even told something themselves. Besides, Father Zossima could not have carried on an uninterrupted narrative, for he was sometimes gasping for breath, his voice failed him, and he even lay down to rest on his bed, though he did not fall asleep and his visitors did not leave their seats. Once or twice the conversation was interrupted by Father Païssy’s reading the Gospel. It is worthy of note, too, that no one of them supposed that he would die that night, for on that evening of his life after his deep sleep in the day he seemed suddenly to have found new strength, which kept him up through this long conversation. It was like a last effort of love which gave him marvelous energy; only for a little time, however, for his life was cut short immediately.... But of that later. I will only add now that I have preferred to confine myself to the account given by Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov. It will be shorter and not so fatiguing, though of course, as I must repeat, Alyosha took a great deal from previous conversations and added them to it.\n\nNotes of the Life of the deceased Priest and Monk, the Elder Zossima, taken from his own words by Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov.\n\n(a) Father Zossima’s Brother\n\nBeloved fathers and teachers, I was born in a distant province in the north, in the town of V. My father was a gentleman by birth, but of no great consequence or position. He died when I was only two years old, and I don’t remember him at all. He left my mother a small house built of wood, and a fortune, not large, but sufficient to keep her and her children in comfort. There were two of us, my elder brother Markel and I. He was eight years older than I was, of hasty irritable temperament, but kind-hearted and never ironical. He was remarkably silent, especially at home with me, his mother, and the servants. He did well at school, but did not get on with his schoolfellows, though he never quarreled, at least so my mother has told me. Six months before his death, when he was seventeen, he made friends with a political exile who had been banished from Moscow to our town for freethinking, and led a solitary existence there. He was a good scholar who had gained distinction in philosophy in the university. Something made him take a fancy to Markel, and he used to ask him to see him. The young man would spend whole evenings with him during that winter, till the exile was summoned to Petersburg to take up his post again at his own request, as he had powerful friends.\n\nIt was the beginning of Lent, and Markel would not fast, he was rude and laughed at it. “That’s all silly twaddle, and there is no God,” he said, horrifying my mother, the servants, and me too. For though I was only nine, I too was aghast at hearing such words. We had four servants, all serfs. I remember my mother selling one of the four, the cook Afimya, who was lame and elderly, for sixty paper roubles, and hiring a free servant to take her place.\n\nIn the sixth week in Lent, my brother, who was never strong and had a tendency to consumption, was taken ill. He was tall but thin and delicate-looking, and of very pleasing countenance. I suppose he caught cold, anyway the doctor, who came, soon whispered to my mother that it was galloping consumption, that he would not live through the spring. My mother began weeping, and, careful not to alarm my brother, she entreated him to go to church, to confess and take the sacrament, as he was still able to move about. This made him angry, and he said something profane about the church. He grew thoughtful, however; he guessed at once that he was seriously ill, and that that was why his mother was begging him to confess and take the sacrament. He had been aware, indeed, for a long time past, that he was far from well, and had a year before coolly observed at dinner to our mother and me, “My life won’t be long among you, I may not live another year,” which seemed now like a prophecy.\n\nThree days passed and Holy Week had come. And on Tuesday morning my brother began going to church. “I am doing this simply for your sake, mother, to please and comfort you,” he said. My mother wept with joy and grief. “His end must be near,” she thought, “if there’s such a change in him.” But he was not able to go to church long, he took to his bed, so he had to confess and take the sacrament at home.\n\nIt was a late Easter, and the days were bright, fine, and full of fragrance. I remember he used to cough all night and sleep badly, but in the morning he dressed and tried to sit up in an arm-chair. That’s how I remember him sitting, sweet and gentle, smiling, his face bright and joyous, in spite of his illness. A marvelous change passed over him, his spirit seemed transformed. The old nurse would come in and say, “Let me light the lamp before the holy image, my dear.” And once he would not have allowed it and would have blown it out.\n\n“Light it, light it, dear, I was a wretch to have prevented you doing it. You are praying when you light the lamp, and I am praying when I rejoice seeing you. So we are praying to the same God.”\n\nThose words seemed strange to us, and mother would go to her room and weep, but when she went in to him she wiped her eyes and looked cheerful. “Mother, don’t weep, darling,” he would say, “I’ve long to live yet, long to rejoice with you, and life is glad and joyful.”\n\n“Ah, dear boy, how can you talk of joy when you lie feverish at night, coughing as though you would tear yourself to pieces.”\n\n“Don’t cry, mother,” he would answer, “life is paradise, and we are all in paradise, but we won’t see it, if we would, we should have heaven on earth the next day.”\n\nEvery one wondered at his words, he spoke so strangely and positively; we were all touched and wept. Friends came to see us. “Dear ones,” he would say to them, “what have I done that you should love me so, how can you love any one like me, and how was it I did not know, I did not appreciate it before?”\n\nWhen the servants came in to him he would say continually, “Dear, kind people, why are you doing so much for me, do I deserve to be waited on? If it were God’s will for me to live, I would wait on you, for all men should wait on one another.”\n\nMother shook her head as she listened. “My darling, it’s your illness makes you talk like that.”\n\n“Mother, darling,” he would say, “there must be servants and masters, but if so I will be the servant of my servants, the same as they are to me. And another thing, mother, every one of us has sinned against all men, and I more than any.”\n\nMother positively smiled at that, smiled through her tears. “Why, how could you have sinned against all men, more than all? Robbers and murderers have done that, but what sin have you committed yet, that you hold yourself more guilty than all?”\n\n“Mother, little heart of mine,” he said (he had begun using such strange caressing words at that time), “little heart of mine, my joy, believe me, every one is really responsible to all men for all men and for everything. I don’t know how to explain it to you, but I feel it is so, painfully even. And how is it we went on then living, getting angry and not knowing?”\n\nSo he would get up every day, more and more sweet and joyous and full of love. When the doctor, an old German called Eisenschmidt, came:\n\n“Well, doctor, have I another day in this world?” he would ask, joking.\n\n“You’ll live many days yet,” the doctor would answer, “and months and years too.”\n\n“Months and years!” he would exclaim. “Why reckon the days? One day is enough for a man to know all happiness. My dear ones, why do we quarrel, try to outshine each other and keep grudges against each other? Let’s go straight into the garden, walk and play there, love, appreciate, and kiss each other, and glorify life.”\n\n“Your son cannot last long,” the doctor told my mother, as she accompanied him to the door. “The disease is affecting his brain.”\n\nThe windows of his room looked out into the garden, and our garden was a shady one, with old trees in it which were coming into bud. The first birds of spring were flitting in the branches, chirruping and singing at the windows. And looking at them and admiring them, he began suddenly begging their forgiveness too: “Birds of heaven, happy birds, forgive me, for I have sinned against you too.” None of us could understand that at the time, but he shed tears of joy. “Yes,” he said, “there was such a glory of God all about me: birds, trees, meadows, sky; only I lived in shame and dishonored it all and did not notice the beauty and glory.”\n\n“You take too many sins on yourself,” mother used to say, weeping.\n\n“Mother, darling, it’s for joy, not for grief I am crying. Though I can’t explain it to you, I like to humble myself before them, for I don’t know how to love them enough. If I have sinned against every one, yet all forgive me, too, and that’s heaven. Am I not in heaven now?”\n\nAnd there was a great deal more I don’t remember. I remember I went once into his room when there was no one else there. It was a bright evening, the sun was setting, and the whole room was lighted up. He beckoned me, and I went up to him. He put his hands on my shoulders and looked into my face tenderly, lovingly; he said nothing for a minute, only looked at me like that.\n\n“Well,” he said, “run and play now, enjoy life for me too.”\n\nI went out then and ran to play. And many times in my life afterwards I remembered even with tears how he told me to enjoy life for him too. There were many other marvelous and beautiful sayings of his, though we did not understand them at the time. He died the third week after Easter. He was fully conscious though he could not talk; up to his last hour he did not change. He looked happy, his eyes beamed and sought us, he smiled at us, beckoned us. There was a great deal of talk even in the town about his death. I was impressed by all this at the time, but not too much so, though I cried a good deal at his funeral. I was young then, a child, but a lasting impression, a hidden feeling of it all, remained in my heart, ready to rise up and respond when the time came. So indeed it happened.\n\n(b) Of the Holy Scriptures in the Life of Father Zossima\n\nI was left alone with my mother. Her friends began advising her to send me to Petersburg as other parents did. “You have only one son now,” they said, “and have a fair income, and you will be depriving him perhaps of a brilliant career if you keep him here.” They suggested I should be sent to Petersburg to the Cadet Corps, that I might afterwards enter the Imperial Guard. My mother hesitated for a long time, it was awful to part with her only child, but she made up her mind to it at last, though not without many tears, believing she was acting for my happiness. She brought me to Petersburg and put me into the Cadet Corps, and I never saw her again. For she too died three years afterwards. She spent those three years mourning and grieving for both of us.\n\nFrom the house of my childhood I have brought nothing but precious memories, for there are no memories more precious than those of early childhood in one’s first home. And that is almost always so if there is any love and harmony in the family at all. Indeed, precious memories may remain even of a bad home, if only the heart knows how to find what is precious. With my memories of home I count, too, my memories of the Bible, which, child as I was, I was very eager to read at home. I had a book of Scripture history then with excellent pictures, called A Hundred and Four Stories from the Old and New Testament, and I learned to read from it. I have it lying on my shelf now, I keep it as a precious relic of the past. But even before I learned to read, I remember first being moved to devotional feeling at eight years old. My mother took me alone to mass (I don’t remember where my brother was at the time) on the Monday before Easter. It was a fine day, and I remember to-day, as though I saw it now, how the incense rose from the censer and softly floated upwards and, overhead in the cupola, mingled in rising waves with the sunlight that streamed in at the little window. I was stirred by the sight, and for the first time in my life I consciously received the seed of God’s word in my heart. A youth came out into the middle of the church carrying a big book, so large that at the time I fancied he could scarcely carry it. He laid it on the reading desk, opened it, and began reading, and suddenly for the first time I understood something read in the church of God. In the land of Uz, there lived a man, righteous and God-fearing, and he had great wealth, so many camels, so many sheep and asses, and his children feasted, and he loved them very much and prayed for them. “It may be that my sons have sinned in their feasting.” Now the devil came before the Lord together with the sons of God, and said to the Lord that he had gone up and down the earth and under the earth. “And hast thou considered my servant Job?” God asked of him. And God boasted to the devil, pointing to his great and holy servant. And the devil laughed at God’s words. “Give him over to me and Thou wilt see that Thy servant will murmur against Thee and curse Thy name.” And God gave up the just man He loved so, to the devil. And the devil smote his children and his cattle and scattered his wealth, all of a sudden like a thunderbolt from heaven. And Job rent his mantle and fell down upon the ground and cried aloud, “Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return into the earth; the Lord gave and the Lord has taken away. Blessed be the name of the Lord for ever and ever.”\n\nFathers and teachers, forgive my tears now, for all my childhood rises up again before me, and I breathe now as I breathed then, with the breast of a little child of eight, and I feel as I did then, awe and wonder and gladness. The camels at that time caught my imagination, and Satan, who talked like that with God, and God who gave His servant up to destruction, and His servant crying out: “Blessed be Thy name although Thou dost punish me,” and then the soft and sweet singing in the church: “Let my prayer rise up before Thee,” and again incense from the priest’s censer and the kneeling and the prayer. Ever since then—only yesterday I took it up—I’ve never been able to read that sacred tale without tears. And how much that is great, mysterious and unfathomable there is in it! Afterwards I heard the words of mockery and blame, proud words, “How could God give up the most loved of His saints for the diversion of the devil, take from him his children, smite him with sore boils so that he cleansed the corruption from his sores with a pot-sherd—and for no object except to boast to the devil! ‘See what My saint can suffer for My sake.’” But the greatness of it lies just in the fact that it is a mystery—that the passing earthly show and the eternal verity are brought together in it. In the face of the earthly truth, the eternal truth is accomplished. The Creator, just as on the first days of creation He ended each day with praise: “That is good that I have created,” looks upon Job and again praises His creation. And Job, praising the Lord, serves not only Him but all His creation for generations and generations, and for ever and ever, since for that he was ordained. Good heavens, what a book it is, and what lessons there are in it! What a book the Bible is, what a miracle, what strength is given with it to man! It is like a mold cast of the world and man and human nature, everything is there, and a law for everything for all the ages. And what mysteries are solved and revealed! God raises Job again, gives him wealth again. Many years pass by, and he has other children and loves them. But how could he love those new ones when those first children are no more, when he has lost them? Remembering them, how could he be fully happy with those new ones, however dear the new ones might be? But he could, he could. It’s the great mystery of human life that old grief passes gradually into quiet, tender joy. The mild serenity of age takes the place of the riotous blood of youth. I bless the rising sun each day, and, as before, my hearts sings to meet it, but now I love even more its setting, its long slanting rays and the soft, tender, gentle memories that come with them, the dear images from the whole of my long, happy life—and over all the Divine Truth, softening, reconciling, forgiving! My life is ending, I know that well, but every day that is left me I feel how my earthly life is in touch with a new infinite, unknown, that approaching life, the nearness of which sets my soul quivering with rapture, my mind glowing and my heart weeping with joy.\n\nFriends and teachers, I have heard more than once, and of late one may hear it more often, that the priests, and above all the village priests, are complaining on all sides of their miserable income and their humiliating lot. They plainly state, even in print—I’ve read it myself—that they are unable to teach the Scriptures to the people because of the smallness of their means, and if Lutherans and heretics come and lead the flock astray, they let them lead them astray because they have so little to live upon. May the Lord increase the sustenance that is so precious to them, for their complaint is just, too. But of a truth I say, if any one is to blame in the matter, half the fault is ours. For he may be short of time, he may say truly that he is overwhelmed all the while with work and services, but still it’s not all the time, even he has an hour a week to remember God. And he does not work the whole year round. Let him gather round him once a week, some hour in the evening, if only the children at first—the fathers will hear of it and they too will begin to come. There’s no need to build halls for this, let him take them into his own cottage. They won’t spoil his cottage, they would only be there one hour. Let him open that book and begin reading it without grand words or superciliousness, without condescension to them, but gently and kindly, being glad that he is reading to them and that they are listening with attention, loving the words himself, only stopping from time to time to explain words that are not understood by the peasants. Don’t be anxious, they will understand everything, the orthodox heart will understand all! Let him read them about Abraham and Sarah, about Isaac and Rebecca, of how Jacob went to Laban and wrestled with the Lord in his dream and said, “This place is holy”—and he will impress the devout mind of the peasant. Let him read, especially to the children, how the brothers sold Joseph, the tender boy, the dreamer and prophet, into bondage, and told their father that a wild beast had devoured him, and showed him his blood-stained clothes. Let him read them how the brothers afterwards journeyed into Egypt for corn, and Joseph, already a great ruler, unrecognized by them, tormented them, accused them, kept his brother Benjamin, and all through love: “I love you, and loving you I torment you.” For he remembered all his life how they had sold him to the merchants in the burning desert by the well, and how, wringing his hands, he had wept and besought his brothers not to sell him as a slave in a strange land. And how, seeing them again after many years, he loved them beyond measure, but he harassed and tormented them in love. He left them at last not able to bear the suffering of his heart, flung himself on his bed and wept. Then, wiping his tears away, he went out to them joyful and told them, “Brothers, I am your brother Joseph!” Let him read them further how happy old Jacob was on learning that his darling boy was still alive, and how he went to Egypt leaving his own country, and died in a foreign land, bequeathing his great prophecy that had lain mysteriously hidden in his meek and timid heart all his life, that from his offspring, from Judah, will come the great hope of the world, the Messiah and Saviour.\n\nFathers and teachers, forgive me and don’t be angry, that like a little child I’ve been babbling of what you know long ago, and can teach me a hundred times more skillfully. I only speak from rapture, and forgive my tears, for I love the Bible. Let him too weep, the priest of God, and be sure that the hearts of his listeners will throb in response. Only a little tiny seed is needed—drop it into the heart of the peasant and it won’t die, it will live in his soul all his life, it will be hidden in the midst of his darkness and sin, like a bright spot, like a great reminder. And there’s no need of much teaching or explanation, he will understand it all simply. Do you suppose that the peasants don’t understand? Try reading them the touching story of the fair Esther and the haughty Vashti; or the miraculous story of Jonah in the whale. Don’t forget either the parables of Our Lord, choose especially from the Gospel of St. Luke (that is what I did), and then from the Acts of the Apostles the conversion of St. Paul (that you mustn’t leave out on any account), and from the Lives of the Saints, for instance, the life of Alexey, the man of God and, greatest of all, the happy martyr and the seer of God, Mary of Egypt—and you will penetrate their hearts with these simple tales. Give one hour a week to it in spite of your poverty, only one little hour. And you will see for yourselves that our people is gracious and grateful, and will repay you a hundred-fold. Mindful of the kindness of their priest and the moving words they have heard from him, they will of their own accord help him in his fields and in his house, and will treat him with more respect than before—so that it will even increase his worldly well-being too. The thing is so simple that sometimes one is even afraid to put it into words, for fear of being laughed at, and yet how true it is! One who does not believe in God will not believe in God’s people. He who believes in God’s people will see His Holiness too, even though he had not believed in it till then. Only the people and their future spiritual power will convert our atheists, who have torn themselves away from their native soil.\n\nAnd what is the use of Christ’s words, unless we set an example? The people is lost without the Word of God, for its soul is athirst for the Word and for all that is good.\n\nIn my youth, long ago, nearly forty years ago, I traveled all over Russia with Father Anfim, collecting funds for our monastery, and we stayed one night on the bank of a great navigable river with some fishermen. A good-looking peasant lad, about eighteen, joined us; he had to hurry back next morning to pull a merchant’s barge along the bank. I noticed him looking straight before him with clear and tender eyes. It was a bright, warm, still, July night, a cool mist rose from the broad river, we could hear the plash of a fish, the birds were still, all was hushed and beautiful, everything praying to God. Only we two were not sleeping, the lad and I, and we talked of the beauty of this world of God’s and of the great mystery of it. Every blade of grass, every insect, ant, and golden bee, all so marvelously know their path, though they have not intelligence, they bear witness to the mystery of God and continually accomplish it themselves. I saw the dear lad’s heart was moved. He told me that he loved the forest and the forest birds. He was a bird-catcher, knew the note of each of them, could call each bird. “I know nothing better than to be in the forest,” said he, “though all things are good.”\n\n“Truly,” I answered him, “all things are good and fair, because all is truth. Look,” said I, “at the horse, that great beast that is so near to man; or the lowly, pensive ox, which feeds him and works for him; look at their faces, what meekness, what devotion to man, who often beats them mercilessly. What gentleness, what confidence and what beauty! It’s touching to know that there’s no sin in them, for all, all except man, is sinless, and Christ has been with them before us.”\n\n“Why,” asked the boy, “is Christ with them too?”\n\n“It cannot but be so,” said I, “since the Word is for all. All creation and all creatures, every leaf is striving to the Word, singing glory to God, weeping to Christ, unconsciously accomplishing this by the mystery of their sinless life. Yonder,” said I, “in the forest wanders the dreadful bear, fierce and menacing, and yet innocent in it.” And I told him how once a bear came to a great saint who had taken refuge in a tiny cell in the wood. And the great saint pitied him, went up to him without fear and gave him a piece of bread. “Go along,” said he, “Christ be with you,” and the savage beast walked away meekly and obediently, doing no harm. And the lad was delighted that the bear had walked away without hurting the saint, and that Christ was with him too. “Ah,” said he, “how good that is, how good and beautiful is all God’s work!” He sat musing softly and sweetly. I saw he understood. And he slept beside me a light and sinless sleep. May God bless youth! And I prayed for him as I went to sleep. Lord, send peace and light to Thy people!\n\n# Chapter II. The Duel\n\n(c) Recollections of Father Zossima’s Youth before he became a Monk. The Duel\n\nI spent a long time, almost eight years, in the military cadet school at Petersburg, and in the novelty of my surroundings there, many of my childish impressions grew dimmer, though I forgot nothing. I picked up so many new habits and opinions that I was transformed into a cruel, absurd, almost savage creature. A surface polish of courtesy and society manners I did acquire together with the French language.\n\nBut we all, myself included, looked upon the soldiers in our service as cattle. I was perhaps worse than the rest in that respect, for I was so much more impressionable than my companions. By the time we left the school as officers, we were ready to lay down our lives for the honor of the regiment, but no one of us had any knowledge of the real meaning of honor, and if any one had known it, he would have been the first to ridicule it. Drunkenness, debauchery and devilry were what we almost prided ourselves on. I don’t say that we were bad by nature, all these young men were good fellows, but they behaved badly, and I worst of all. What made it worse for me was that I had come into my own money, and so I flung myself into a life of pleasure, and plunged headlong into all the recklessness of youth.\n\nI was fond of reading, yet strange to say, the Bible was the one book I never opened at that time, though I always carried it about with me, and I was never separated from it; in very truth I was keeping that book “for the day and the hour, for the month and the year,” though I knew it not.\n\nAfter four years of this life, I chanced to be in the town of K. where our regiment was stationed at the time. We found the people of the town hospitable, rich and fond of entertainments. I met with a cordial reception everywhere, as I was of a lively temperament and was known to be well off, which always goes a long way in the world. And then a circumstance happened which was the beginning of it all.\n\nI formed an attachment to a beautiful and intelligent young girl of noble and lofty character, the daughter of people much respected. They were well-to-do people of influence and position. They always gave me a cordial and friendly reception. I fancied that the young lady looked on me with favor and my heart was aflame at such an idea. Later on I saw and fully realized that I perhaps was not so passionately in love with her at all, but only recognized the elevation of her mind and character, which I could not indeed have helped doing. I was prevented, however, from making her an offer at the time by my selfishness, I was loath to part with the allurements of my free and licentious bachelor life in the heyday of my youth, and with my pockets full of money. I did drop some hint as to my feelings however, though I put off taking any decisive step for a time. Then, all of a sudden, we were ordered off for two months to another district.\n\nOn my return two months later, I found the young lady already married to a rich neighboring landowner, a very amiable man, still young though older than I was, connected with the best Petersburg society, which I was not, and of excellent education, which I also was not. I was so overwhelmed at this unexpected circumstance that my mind was positively clouded. The worst of it all was that, as I learned then, the young landowner had been a long while betrothed to her, and I had met him indeed many times in her house, but blinded by my conceit I had noticed nothing. And this particularly mortified me; almost everybody had known all about it, while I knew nothing. I was filled with sudden irrepressible fury. With flushed face I began recalling how often I had been on the point of declaring my love to her, and as she had not attempted to stop me or to warn me, she must, I concluded, have been laughing at me all the time. Later on, of course, I reflected and remembered that she had been very far from laughing at me; on the contrary, she used to turn off any love-making on my part with a jest and begin talking of other subjects; but at that moment I was incapable of reflecting and was all eagerness for revenge. I am surprised to remember that my wrath and revengeful feelings were extremely repugnant to my own nature, for being of an easy temper, I found it difficult to be angry with any one for long, and so I had to work myself up artificially and became at last revolting and absurd.\n\nI waited for an opportunity and succeeded in insulting my “rival” in the presence of a large company. I insulted him on a perfectly extraneous pretext, jeering at his opinion upon an important public event—it was in the year 1826[5]—and my jeer was, so people said, clever and effective. Then I forced him to ask for an explanation, and behaved so rudely that he accepted my challenge in spite of the vast inequality between us, as I was younger, a person of no consequence, and of inferior rank. I learned afterwards for a fact that it was from a jealous feeling on his side also that my challenge was accepted; he had been rather jealous of me on his wife’s account before their marriage; he fancied now that if he submitted to be insulted by me and refused to accept my challenge, and if she heard of it, she might begin to despise him and waver in her love for him. I soon found a second in a comrade, an ensign of our regiment. In those days though duels were severely punished, yet dueling was a kind of fashion among the officers—so strong and deeply rooted will a brutal prejudice sometimes be.\n\nIt was the end of June, and our meeting was to take place at seven o’clock the next day on the outskirts of the town—and then something happened that in very truth was the turning-point of my life. In the evening, returning home in a savage and brutal humor, I flew into a rage with my orderly Afanasy, and gave him two blows in the face with all my might, so that it was covered with blood. He had not long been in my service and I had struck him before, but never with such ferocious cruelty. And, believe me, though it’s forty years ago, I recall it now with shame and pain. I went to bed and slept for about three hours; when I waked up the day was breaking. I got up—I did not want to sleep any more—I went to the window—opened it, it looked out upon the garden; I saw the sun rising; it was warm and beautiful, the birds were singing.\n\n“What’s the meaning of it?” I thought. “I feel in my heart as it were something vile and shameful. Is it because I am going to shed blood? No,” I thought, “I feel it’s not that. Can it be that I am afraid of death, afraid of being killed? No, that’s not it, that’s not it at all.”... And all at once I knew what it was: it was because I had beaten Afanasy the evening before! It all rose before my mind, it all was as it were repeated over again; he stood before me and I was beating him straight on the face and he was holding his arms stiffly down, his head erect, his eyes fixed upon me as though on parade. He staggered at every blow and did not even dare to raise his hands to protect himself. That is what a man has been brought to, and that was a man beating a fellow creature! What a crime! It was as though a sharp dagger had pierced me right through. I stood as if I were struck dumb, while the sun was shining, the leaves were rejoicing and the birds were trilling the praise of God.... I hid my face in my hands, fell on my bed and broke into a storm of tears. And then I remembered my brother Markel and what he said on his death-bed to his servants: “My dear ones, why do you wait on me, why do you love me, am I worth your waiting on me?”\n\n“Yes, am I worth it?” flashed through my mind. “After all what am I worth, that another man, a fellow creature, made in the likeness and image of God, should serve me?” For the first time in my life this question forced itself upon me. He had said, “Mother, my little heart, in truth we are each responsible to all for all, it’s only that men don’t know this. If they knew it, the world would be a paradise at once.”\n\n“God, can that too be false?” I thought as I wept. “In truth, perhaps, I am more than all others responsible for all, a greater sinner than all men in the world.” And all at once the whole truth in its full light appeared to me; what was I going to do? I was going to kill a good, clever, noble man, who had done me no wrong, and by depriving his wife of happiness for the rest of her life, I should be torturing and killing her too. I lay thus in my bed with my face in the pillow, heedless how the time was passing. Suddenly my second, the ensign, came in with the pistols to fetch me.\n\n“Ah,” said he, “it’s a good thing you are up already, it’s time we were off, come along!”\n\nI did not know what to do and hurried to and fro undecided; we went out to the carriage, however.\n\n“Wait here a minute,” I said to him. “I’ll be back directly, I have forgotten my purse.”\n\nAnd I ran back alone, to Afanasy’s little room.\n\n“Afanasy,” I said, “I gave you two blows on the face yesterday, forgive me,” I said.\n\nHe started as though he were frightened, and looked at me; and I saw that it was not enough, and on the spot, in my full officer’s uniform, I dropped at his feet and bowed my head to the ground.\n\n“Forgive me,” I said.\n\nThen he was completely aghast.\n\n“Your honor ... sir, what are you doing? Am I worth it?”\n\nAnd he burst out crying as I had done before, hid this face in his hands, turned to the window and shook all over with his sobs. I flew out to my comrade and jumped into the carriage.\n\n“Ready,” I cried. “Have you ever seen a conqueror?” I asked him. “Here is one before you.”\n\nI was in ecstasy, laughing and talking all the way, I don’t remember what about.\n\nHe looked at me. “Well, brother, you are a plucky fellow, you’ll keep up the honor of the uniform, I can see.”\n\nSo we reached the place and found them there, waiting us. We were placed twelve paces apart; he had the first shot. I stood gayly, looking him full in the face; I did not twitch an eyelash, I looked lovingly at him, for I knew what I would do. His shot just grazed my cheek and ear.\n\n“Thank God,” I cried, “no man has been killed,” and I seized my pistol, turned back and flung it far away into the wood. “That’s the place for you,” I cried.\n\nI turned to my adversary.\n\n“Forgive me, young fool that I am, sir,” I said, “for my unprovoked insult to you and for forcing you to fire at me. I am ten times worse than you and more, maybe. Tell that to the person whom you hold dearest in the world.”\n\nI had no sooner said this than they all three shouted at me.\n\n“Upon my word,” cried my adversary, annoyed, “if you did not want to fight, why did not you let me alone?”\n\n“Yesterday I was a fool, to-day I know better,” I answered him gayly.\n\n“As to yesterday, I believe you, but as for to-day, it is difficult to agree with your opinion,” said he.\n\n“Bravo,” I cried, clapping my hands. “I agree with you there too. I have deserved it!”\n\n“Will you shoot, sir, or not?”\n\n“No, I won’t,” I said; “if you like, fire at me again, but it would be better for you not to fire.”\n\nThe seconds, especially mine, were shouting too: “Can you disgrace the regiment like this, facing your antagonist and begging his forgiveness! If I’d only known this!”\n\nI stood facing them all, not laughing now.\n\n“Gentlemen,” I said, “is it really so wonderful in these days to find a man who can repent of his stupidity and publicly confess his wrongdoing?”\n\n“But not in a duel,” cried my second again.\n\n“That’s what’s so strange,” I said. “For I ought to have owned my fault as soon as I got here, before he had fired a shot, before leading him into a great and deadly sin; but we have made our life so grotesque, that to act in that way would have been almost impossible, for only after I have faced his shot at the distance of twelve paces could my words have any significance for him, and if I had spoken before, he would have said, ‘He is a coward, the sight of the pistols has frightened him, no use to listen to him.’ Gentlemen,” I cried suddenly, speaking straight from my heart, “look around you at the gifts of God, the clear sky, the pure air, the tender grass, the birds; nature is beautiful and sinless, and we, only we, are sinful and foolish, and we don’t understand that life is heaven, for we have only to understand that and it will at once be fulfilled in all its beauty, we shall embrace each other and weep.”\n\nI would have said more but I could not; my voice broke with the sweetness and youthful gladness of it, and there was such bliss in my heart as I had never known before in my life.\n\n“All this as rational and edifying,” said my antagonist, “and in any case you are an original person.”\n\n“You may laugh,” I said to him, laughing too, “but afterwards you will approve of me.”\n\n“Oh, I am ready to approve of you now,” said he; “will you shake hands? for I believe you are genuinely sincere.”\n\n“No,” I said, “not now, later on when I have grown worthier and deserve your esteem, then shake hands and you will do well.”\n\nWe went home, my second upbraiding me all the way, while I kissed him. All my comrades heard of the affair at once and gathered together to pass judgment on me the same day.\n\n“He has disgraced the uniform,” they said; “let him resign his commission.”\n\nSome stood up for me: “He faced the shot,” they said.\n\n“Yes, but he was afraid of his other shot and begged for forgiveness.”\n\n“If he had been afraid of being shot, he would have shot his own pistol first before asking forgiveness, while he flung it loaded into the forest. No, there’s something else in this, something original.”\n\nI enjoyed listening and looking at them. “My dear friends and comrades,” said I, “don’t worry about my resigning my commission, for I have done so already. I have sent in my papers this morning and as soon as I get my discharge I shall go into a monastery—it’s with that object I am leaving the regiment.”\n\nWhen I had said this every one of them burst out laughing.\n\n“You should have told us of that first, that explains everything, we can’t judge a monk.”\n\nThey laughed and could not stop themselves, and not scornfully, but kindly and merrily. They all felt friendly to me at once, even those who had been sternest in their censure, and all the following month, before my discharge came, they could not make enough of me. “Ah, you monk,” they would say. And every one said something kind to me, they began trying to dissuade me, even to pity me: “What are you doing to yourself?”\n\n“No,” they would say, “he is a brave fellow, he faced fire and could have fired his own pistol too, but he had a dream the night before that he should become a monk, that’s why he did it.”\n\nIt was the same thing with the society of the town. Till then I had been kindly received, but had not been the object of special attention, and now all came to know me at once and invited me; they laughed at me, but they loved me. I may mention that although everybody talked openly of our duel, the authorities took no notice of it, because my antagonist was a near relation of our general, and as there had been no bloodshed and no serious consequences, and as I resigned my commission, they took it as a joke. And I began then to speak aloud and fearlessly, regardless of their laughter, for it was always kindly and not spiteful laughter. These conversations mostly took place in the evenings, in the company of ladies; women particularly liked listening to me then and they made the men listen.\n\n“But how can I possibly be responsible for all?” every one would laugh in my face. “Can I, for instance, be responsible for you?”\n\n“You may well not know it,” I would answer, “since the whole world has long been going on a different line, since we consider the veriest lies as truth and demand the same lies from others. Here I have for once in my life acted sincerely and, well, you all look upon me as a madman. Though you are friendly to me, yet, you see, you all laugh at me.”\n\n“But how can we help being friendly to you?” said my hostess, laughing. The room was full of people. All of a sudden the young lady rose, on whose account the duel had been fought and whom only lately I had intended to be my future wife. I had not noticed her coming into the room. She got up, came to me and held out her hand.\n\n“Let me tell you,” she said, “that I am the first not to laugh at you, but on the contrary I thank you with tears and express my respect for you for your action then.”\n\nHer husband, too, came up and then they all approached me and almost kissed me. My heart was filled with joy, but my attention was especially caught by a middle-aged man who came up to me with the others. I knew him by name already, but had never made his acquaintance nor exchanged a word with him till that evening.\n\n(d) The Mysterious Visitor\n\nHe had long been an official in the town; he was in a prominent position, respected by all, rich and had a reputation for benevolence. He subscribed considerable sums to the almshouse and the orphan asylum; he was very charitable, too, in secret, a fact which only became known after his death. He was a man of about fifty, almost stern in appearance and not much given to conversation. He had been married about ten years and his wife, who was still young, had borne him three children. Well, I was sitting alone in my room the following evening, when my door suddenly opened and this gentleman walked in.\n\nI must mention, by the way, that I was no longer living in my former quarters. As soon as I resigned my commission, I took rooms with an old lady, the widow of a government clerk. My landlady’s servant waited upon me, for I had moved into her rooms simply because on my return from the duel I had sent Afanasy back to the regiment, as I felt ashamed to look him in the face after my last interview with him. So prone is the man of the world to be ashamed of any righteous action.\n\n“I have,” said my visitor, “with great interest listened to you speaking in different houses the last few days and I wanted at last to make your personal acquaintance, so as to talk to you more intimately. Can you, dear sir, grant me this favor?”\n\n“I can, with the greatest pleasure, and I shall look upon it as an honor.” I said this, though I felt almost dismayed, so greatly was I impressed from the first moment by the appearance of this man. For though other people had listened to me with interest and attention, no one had come to me before with such a serious, stern and concentrated expression. And now he had come to see me in my own rooms. He sat down.\n\n“You are, I see, a man of great strength of character,” he said; “as you have dared to serve the truth, even when by doing so you risked incurring the contempt of all.”\n\n“Your praise is, perhaps, excessive,” I replied.\n\n“No, it’s not excessive,” he answered; “believe me, such a course of action is far more difficult than you think. It is that which has impressed me, and it is only on that account that I have come to you,” he continued. “Tell me, please, that is if you are not annoyed by my perhaps unseemly curiosity, what were your exact sensations, if you can recall them, at the moment when you made up your mind to ask forgiveness at the duel. Do not think my question frivolous; on the contrary, I have in asking the question a secret motive of my own, which I will perhaps explain to you later on, if it is God’s will that we should become more intimately acquainted.”\n\nAll the while he was speaking, I was looking at him straight into the face and I felt all at once a complete trust in him and great curiosity on my side also, for I felt that there was some strange secret in his soul.\n\n“You ask what were my exact sensations at the moment when I asked my opponent’s forgiveness,” I answered; “but I had better tell you from the beginning what I have not yet told any one else.” And I described all that had passed between Afanasy and me, and how I had bowed down to the ground at his feet. “From that you can see for yourself,” I concluded, “that at the time of the duel it was easier for me, for I had made a beginning already at home, and when once I had started on that road, to go farther along it was far from being difficult, but became a source of joy and happiness.”\n\nI liked the way he looked at me as he listened. “All that,” he said, “is exceedingly interesting. I will come to see you again and again.”\n\nAnd from that time forth he came to see me nearly every evening. And we should have become greater friends, if only he had ever talked of himself. But about himself he scarcely ever said a word, yet continually asked me about myself. In spite of that I became very fond of him and spoke with perfect frankness to him about all my feelings; “for,” thought I, “what need have I to know his secrets, since I can see without that that he is a good man? Moreover, though he is such a serious man and my senior, he comes to see a youngster like me and treats me as his equal.” And I learned a great deal that was profitable from him, for he was a man of lofty mind.\n\n“That life is heaven,” he said to me suddenly, “that I have long been thinking about”; and all at once he added, “I think of nothing else indeed.” He looked at me and smiled. “I am more convinced of it than you are, I will tell you later why.”\n\nI listened to him and thought that he evidently wanted to tell me something.\n\n“Heaven,” he went on, “lies hidden within all of us—here it lies hidden in me now, and if I will it, it will be revealed to me to-morrow and for all time.”\n\nI looked at him; he was speaking with great emotion and gazing mysteriously at me, as if he were questioning me.\n\n“And that we are all responsible to all for all, apart from our own sins, you were quite right in thinking that, and it is wonderful how you could comprehend it in all its significance at once. And in very truth, so soon as men understand that, the Kingdom of Heaven will be for them not a dream, but a living reality.”\n\n“And when,” I cried out to him bitterly, “when will that come to pass? and will it ever come to pass? Is not it simply a dream of ours?”\n\n“What then, you don’t believe it,” he said. “You preach it and don’t believe it yourself. Believe me, this dream, as you call it, will come to pass without doubt; it will come, but not now, for every process has its law. It’s a spiritual, psychological process. To transform the world, to recreate it afresh, men must turn into another path psychologically. Until you have become really, in actual fact, a brother to every one, brotherhood will not come to pass. No sort of scientific teaching, no kind of common interest, will ever teach men to share property and privileges with equal consideration for all. Every one will think his share too small and they will be always envying, complaining and attacking one another. You ask when it will come to pass; it will come to pass, but first we have to go through the period of isolation.”\n\n“What do you mean by isolation?” I asked him.\n\n“Why, the isolation that prevails everywhere, above all in our age—it has not fully developed, it has not reached its limit yet. For every one strives to keep his individuality as apart as possible, wishes to secure the greatest possible fullness of life for himself; but meantime all his efforts result not in attaining fullness of life but self-destruction, for instead of self-realization he ends by arriving at complete solitude. All mankind in our age have split up into units, they all keep apart, each in his own groove; each one holds aloof, hides himself and hides what he has, from the rest, and he ends by being repelled by others and repelling them. He heaps up riches by himself and thinks, ‘How strong I am now and how secure,’ and in his madness he does not understand that the more he heaps up, the more he sinks into self-destructive impotence. For he is accustomed to rely upon himself alone and to cut himself off from the whole; he has trained himself not to believe in the help of others, in men and in humanity, and only trembles for fear he should lose his money and the privileges that he has won for himself. Everywhere in these days men have, in their mockery, ceased to understand that the true security is to be found in social solidarity rather than in isolated individual effort. But this terrible individualism must inevitably have an end, and all will suddenly understand how unnaturally they are separated from one another. It will be the spirit of the time, and people will marvel that they have sat so long in darkness without seeing the light. And then the sign of the Son of Man will be seen in the heavens.... But, until then, we must keep the banner flying. Sometimes even if he has to do it alone, and his conduct seems to be crazy, a man must set an example, and so draw men’s souls out of their solitude, and spur them to some act of brotherly love, that the great idea may not die.”\n\nOur evenings, one after another, were spent in such stirring and fervent talk. I gave up society and visited my neighbors much less frequently. Besides, my vogue was somewhat over. I say this, not as blame, for they still loved me and treated me good-humoredly, but there’s no denying that fashion is a great power in society. I began to regard my mysterious visitor with admiration, for besides enjoying his intelligence, I began to perceive that he was brooding over some plan in his heart, and was preparing himself perhaps for a great deed. Perhaps he liked my not showing curiosity about his secret, not seeking to discover it by direct question nor by insinuation. But I noticed at last, that he seemed to show signs of wanting to tell me something. This had become quite evident, indeed, about a month after he first began to visit me.\n\n“Do you know,” he said to me once, “that people are very inquisitive about us in the town and wonder why I come to see you so often. But let them wonder, for soon all will be explained.”\n\nSometimes an extraordinary agitation would come over him, and almost always on such occasions he would get up and go away. Sometimes he would fix a long piercing look upon me, and I thought, “He will say something directly now.” But he would suddenly begin talking of something ordinary and familiar. He often complained of headache too.\n\nOne day, quite unexpectedly indeed, after he had been talking with great fervor a long time, I saw him suddenly turn pale, and his face worked convulsively, while he stared persistently at me.\n\n“What’s the matter?” I said; “do you feel ill?”—he had just been complaining of headache.\n\n“I ... do you know ... I murdered some one.”\n\nHe said this and smiled with a face as white as chalk. “Why is it he is smiling?” The thought flashed through my mind before I realized anything else. I too turned pale.\n\n“What are you saying?” I cried.\n\n“You see,” he said, with a pale smile, “how much it has cost me to say the first word. Now I have said it, I feel I’ve taken the first step and shall go on.”\n\nFor a long while I could not believe him, and I did not believe him at that time, but only after he had been to see me three days running and told me all about it. I thought he was mad, but ended by being convinced, to my great grief and amazement. His crime was a great and terrible one.\n\nFourteen years before, he had murdered the widow of a landowner, a wealthy and handsome young woman who had a house in our town. He fell passionately in love with her, declared his feeling and tried to persuade her to marry him. But she had already given her heart to another man, an officer of noble birth and high rank in the service, who was at that time away at the front, though she was expecting him soon to return. She refused his offer and begged him not to come and see her. After he had ceased to visit her, he took advantage of his knowledge of the house to enter at night through the garden by the roof, at great risk of discovery. But, as often happens, a crime committed with extraordinary audacity is more successful than others.\n\nEntering the garret through the skylight, he went down the ladder, knowing that the door at the bottom of it was sometimes, through the negligence of the servants, left unlocked. He hoped to find it so, and so it was. He made his way in the dark to her bedroom, where a light was burning. As though on purpose, both her maids had gone off to a birthday-party in the same street, without asking leave. The other servants slept in the servants’ quarters or in the kitchen on the ground-floor. His passion flamed up at the sight of her asleep, and then vindictive, jealous anger took possession of his heart, and like a drunken man, beside himself, he thrust a knife into her heart, so that she did not even cry out. Then with devilish and criminal cunning he contrived that suspicion should fall on the servants. He was so base as to take her purse, to open her chest with keys from under her pillow, and to take some things from it, doing it all as it might have been done by an ignorant servant, leaving valuable papers and taking only money. He took some of the larger gold things, but left smaller articles that were ten times as valuable. He took with him, too, some things for himself as remembrances, but of that later. Having done this awful deed, he returned by the way he had come.\n\nNeither the next day, when the alarm was raised, nor at any time after in his life, did any one dream of suspecting that he was the criminal. No one indeed knew of his love for her, for he was always reserved and silent and had no friend to whom he would have opened his heart. He was looked upon simply as an acquaintance, and not a very intimate one, of the murdered woman, as for the previous fortnight he had not even visited her. A serf of hers called Pyotr was at once suspected, and every circumstance confirmed the suspicion. The man knew—indeed his mistress did not conceal the fact—that having to send one of her serfs as a recruit she had decided to send him, as he had no relations and his conduct was unsatisfactory. People had heard him angrily threatening to murder her when he was drunk in a tavern. Two days before her death, he had run away, staying no one knew where in the town. The day after the murder, he was found on the road leading out of the town, dead drunk, with a knife in his pocket, and his right hand happened to be stained with blood. He declared that his nose had been bleeding, but no one believed him. The maids confessed that they had gone to a party and that the street-door had been left open till they returned. And a number of similar details came to light, throwing suspicion on the innocent servant.\n\nThey arrested him, and he was tried for the murder; but a week after the arrest, the prisoner fell sick of a fever and died unconscious in the hospital. There the matter ended and the judges and the authorities and every one in the town remained convinced that the crime had been committed by no one but the servant who had died in the hospital. And after that the punishment began.\n\nMy mysterious visitor, now my friend, told me that at first he was not in the least troubled by pangs of conscience. He was miserable a long time, but not for that reason; only from regret that he had killed the woman he loved, that she was no more, that in killing her he had killed his love, while the fire of passion was still in his veins. But of the innocent blood he had shed, of the murder of a fellow creature, he scarcely thought. The thought that his victim might have become the wife of another man was insupportable to him, and so, for a long time, he was convinced in his conscience that he could not have acted otherwise.\n\nAt first he was worried at the arrest of the servant, but his illness and death soon set his mind at rest, for the man’s death was apparently (so he reflected at the time) not owing to his arrest or his fright, but a chill he had taken on the day he ran away, when he had lain all night dead drunk on the damp ground. The theft of the money and other things troubled him little, for he argued that the theft had not been committed for gain but to avert suspicion. The sum stolen was small, and he shortly afterwards subscribed the whole of it, and much more, towards the funds for maintaining an almshouse in the town. He did this on purpose to set his conscience at rest about the theft, and it’s a remarkable fact that for a long time he really was at peace—he told me this himself. He entered then upon a career of great activity in the service, volunteered for a difficult and laborious duty, which occupied him two years, and being a man of strong will almost forgot the past. Whenever he recalled it, he tried not to think of it at all. He became active in philanthropy too, founded and helped to maintain many institutions in the town, did a good deal in the two capitals, and in both Moscow and Petersburg was elected a member of philanthropic societies.\n\nAt last, however, he began brooding over the past, and the strain of it was too much for him. Then he was attracted by a fine and intelligent girl and soon after married her, hoping that marriage would dispel his lonely depression, and that by entering on a new life and scrupulously doing his duty to his wife and children, he would escape from old memories altogether. But the very opposite of what he expected happened. He began, even in the first month of his marriage, to be continually fretted by the thought, “My wife loves me—but what if she knew?” When she first told him that she would soon bear him a child, he was troubled. “I am giving life, but I have taken life.” Children came. “How dare I love them, teach and educate them, how can I talk to them of virtue? I have shed blood.” They were splendid children, he longed to caress them; “and I can’t look at their innocent candid faces, I am unworthy.”\n\nAt last he began to be bitterly and ominously haunted by the blood of his murdered victim, by the young life he had destroyed, by the blood that cried out for vengeance. He had begun to have awful dreams. But, being a man of fortitude, he bore his suffering a long time, thinking: “I shall expiate everything by this secret agony.” But that hope, too, was vain; the longer it went on, the more intense was his suffering.\n\nHe was respected in society for his active benevolence, though every one was overawed by his stern and gloomy character. But the more he was respected, the more intolerable it was for him. He confessed to me that he had thoughts of killing himself. But he began to be haunted by another idea—an idea which he had at first regarded as impossible and unthinkable, though at last it got such a hold on his heart that he could not shake it off. He dreamed of rising up, going out and confessing in the face of all men that he had committed murder. For three years this dream had pursued him, haunting him in different forms. At last he believed with his whole heart that if he confessed his crime, he would heal his soul and would be at peace for ever. But this belief filled his heart with terror, for how could he carry it out? And then came what happened at my duel.\n\n“Looking at you, I have made up my mind.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“Is it possible,” I cried, clasping my hands, “that such a trivial incident could give rise to such a resolution in you?”\n\n“My resolution has been growing for the last three years,” he answered, “and your story only gave the last touch to it. Looking at you, I reproached myself and envied you.” He said this to me almost sullenly.\n\n“But you won’t be believed,” I observed; “it’s fourteen years ago.”\n\n“I have proofs, great proofs. I shall show them.”\n\nThen I cried and kissed him.\n\n“Tell me one thing, one thing,” he said (as though it all depended upon me), “my wife, my children! My wife may die of grief, and though my children won’t lose their rank and property, they’ll be a convict’s children and for ever! And what a memory, what a memory of me I shall leave in their hearts!”\n\nI said nothing.\n\n“And to part from them, to leave them for ever? It’s for ever, you know, for ever!”\n\nI sat still and repeated a silent prayer. I got up at last, I felt afraid.\n\n“Well?” He looked at me.\n\n“Go!” said I, “confess. Everything passes, only the truth remains. Your children will understand, when they grow up, the nobility of your resolution.”\n\nHe left me that time as though he had made up his mind. Yet for more than a fortnight afterwards, he came to me every evening, still preparing himself, still unable to bring himself to the point. He made my heart ache. One day he would come determined and say fervently:\n\n“I know it will be heaven for me, heaven, the moment I confess. Fourteen years I’ve been in hell. I want to suffer. I will take my punishment and begin to live. You can pass through the world doing wrong, but there’s no turning back. Now I dare not love my neighbor nor even my own children. Good God, my children will understand, perhaps, what my punishment has cost me and will not condemn me! God is not in strength but in truth.”\n\n“All will understand your sacrifice,” I said to him, “if not at once, they will understand later; for you have served truth, the higher truth, not of the earth.”\n\nAnd he would go away seeming comforted, but next day he would come again, bitter, pale, sarcastic.\n\n“Every time I come to you, you look at me so inquisitively as though to say, ‘He has still not confessed!’ Wait a bit, don’t despise me too much. It’s not such an easy thing to do, as you would think. Perhaps I shall not do it at all. You won’t go and inform against me then, will you?”\n\nAnd far from looking at him with indiscreet curiosity, I was afraid to look at him at all. I was quite ill from anxiety, and my heart was full of tears. I could not sleep at night.\n\n“I have just come from my wife,” he went on. “Do you understand what the word ‘wife’ means? When I went out, the children called to me, ‘Good-by, father, make haste back to read The Children’s Magazine with us.’ No, you don’t understand that! No one is wise from another man’s woe.”\n\nHis eyes were glittering, his lips were twitching. Suddenly he struck the table with his fist so that everything on it danced—it was the first time he had done such a thing, he was such a mild man.\n\n“But need I?” he exclaimed, “must I? No one has been condemned, no one has been sent to Siberia in my place, the man died of fever. And I’ve been punished by my sufferings for the blood I shed. And I shan’t be believed, they won’t believe my proofs. Need I confess, need I? I am ready to go on suffering all my life for the blood I have shed, if only my wife and children may be spared. Will it be just to ruin them with me? Aren’t we making a mistake? What is right in this case? And will people recognize it, will they appreciate it, will they respect it?”\n\n“Good Lord!” I thought to myself, “he is thinking of other people’s respect at such a moment!” And I felt so sorry for him then, that I believe I would have shared his fate if it could have comforted him. I saw he was beside himself. I was aghast, realizing with my heart as well as my mind what such a resolution meant.\n\n“Decide my fate!” he exclaimed again.\n\n“Go and confess,” I whispered to him. My voice failed me, but I whispered it firmly. I took up the New Testament from the table, the Russian translation, and showed him the Gospel of St. John, chapter xii. verse 24:\n\n“Verily, verily, I say unto you, except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone: but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit.”\n\nI had just been reading that verse when he came in. He read it.\n\n“That’s true,” he said, but he smiled bitterly. “It’s terrible the things you find in those books,” he said, after a pause. “It’s easy enough to thrust them upon one. And who wrote them? Can they have been written by men?”\n\n“The Holy Spirit wrote them,” said I.\n\n“It’s easy for you to prate,” he smiled again, this time almost with hatred.\n\nI took the book again, opened it in another place and showed him the Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter x. verse 31. He read:\n\n“It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.”\n\nHe read it and simply flung down the book. He was trembling all over.\n\n“An awful text,” he said. “There’s no denying you’ve picked out fitting ones.” He rose from the chair. “Well!” he said, “good-by, perhaps I shan’t come again ... we shall meet in heaven. So I have been for fourteen years ‘in the hands of the living God,’ that’s how one must think of those fourteen years. To-morrow I will beseech those hands to let me go.”\n\nI wanted to take him in my arms and kiss him, but I did not dare—his face was contorted and somber. He went away.\n\n“Good God,” I thought, “what has he gone to face!” I fell on my knees before the ikon and wept for him before the Holy Mother of God, our swift defender and helper. I was half an hour praying in tears, and it was late, about midnight. Suddenly I saw the door open and he came in again. I was surprised.\n\n“Where have you been?” I asked him.\n\n“I think,” he said, “I’ve forgotten something ... my handkerchief, I think.... Well, even if I’ve not forgotten anything, let me stay a little.”\n\nHe sat down. I stood over him.\n\n“You sit down, too,” said he.\n\nI sat down. We sat still for two minutes; he looked intently at me and suddenly smiled—I remembered that—then he got up, embraced me warmly and kissed me.\n\n“Remember,” he said, “how I came to you a second time. Do you hear, remember it!”\nAnd he went out.\n\n“To-morrow,” I thought.\n\nAnd so it was. I did not know that evening that the next day was his birthday. I had not been out for the last few days, so I had no chance of hearing it from any one. On that day he always had a great gathering, every one in the town went to it. It was the same this time. After dinner he walked into the middle of the room, with a paper in his hand—a formal declaration to the chief of his department who was present. This declaration he read aloud to the whole assembly. It contained a full account of the crime, in every detail.\n\n“I cut myself off from men as a monster. God has visited me,” he said in conclusion. “I want to suffer for my sin!”\n\nThen he brought out and laid on the table all the things he had been keeping for fourteen years, that he thought would prove his crime, the jewels belonging to the murdered woman which he had stolen to divert suspicion, a cross and a locket taken from her neck with a portrait of her betrothed in the locket, her notebook and two letters; one from her betrothed, telling her that he would soon be with her, and her unfinished answer left on the table to be sent off next day. He carried off these two letters—what for? Why had he kept them for fourteen years afterwards instead of destroying them as evidence against him?\n\nAnd this is what happened: every one was amazed and horrified, every one refused to believe it and thought that he was deranged, though all listened with intense curiosity. A few days later it was fully decided and agreed in every house that the unhappy man was mad. The legal authorities could not refuse to take the case up, but they too dropped it. Though the trinkets and letters made them ponder, they decided that even if they did turn out to be authentic, no charge could be based on those alone. Besides, she might have given him those things as a friend, or asked him to take care of them for her. I heard afterwards, however, that the genuineness of the things was proved by the friends and relations of the murdered woman, and that there was no doubt about them. Yet nothing was destined to come of it, after all.\n\nFive days later, all had heard that he was ill and that his life was in danger. The nature of his illness I can’t explain, they said it was an affection of the heart. But it became known that the doctors had been induced by his wife to investigate his mental condition also, and had come to the conclusion that it was a case of insanity. I betrayed nothing, though people ran to question me. But when I wanted to visit him, I was for a long while forbidden to do so, above all by his wife.\n\n“It’s you who have caused his illness,” she said to me; “he was always gloomy, but for the last year people noticed that he was peculiarly excited and did strange things, and now you have been the ruin of him. Your preaching has brought him to this; for the last month he was always with you.”\n\nIndeed, not only his wife but the whole town were down upon me and blamed me. “It’s all your doing,” they said. I was silent and indeed rejoiced at heart, for I saw plainly God’s mercy to the man who had turned against himself and punished himself. I could not believe in his insanity.\n\nThey let me see him at last, he insisted upon saying good-by to me. I went in to him and saw at once, that not only his days, but his hours were numbered. He was weak, yellow, his hands trembled, he gasped for breath, but his face was full of tender and happy feeling.\n\n“It is done!” he said. “I’ve long been yearning to see you, why didn’t you come?”\n\nI did not tell him that they would not let me see him.\n\n“God has had pity on me and is calling me to Himself. I know I am dying, but I feel joy and peace for the first time after so many years. There was heaven in my heart from the moment I had done what I had to do. Now I dare to love my children and to kiss them. Neither my wife nor the judges, nor any one has believed it. My children will never believe it either. I see in that God’s mercy to them. I shall die, and my name will be without a stain for them. And now I feel God near, my heart rejoices as in Heaven ... I have done my duty.”\n\nHe could not speak, he gasped for breath, he pressed my hand warmly, looking fervently at me. We did not talk for long, his wife kept peeping in at us. But he had time to whisper to me:\n\n“Do you remember how I came back to you that second time, at midnight? I told you to remember it. You know what I came back for? I came to kill you!”\n\nI started.\n\n“I went out from you then into the darkness, I wandered about the streets, struggling with myself. And suddenly I hated you so that I could hardly bear it. Now, I thought, he is all that binds me, and he is my judge. I can’t refuse to face my punishment to-morrow, for he knows all. It was not that I was afraid you would betray me (I never even thought of that), but I thought, ‘How can I look him in the face if I don’t confess?’ And if you had been at the other end of the earth, but alive, it would have been all the same, the thought was unendurable that you were alive knowing everything and condemning me. I hated you as though you were the cause, as though you were to blame for everything. I came back to you then, remembering that you had a dagger lying on your table. I sat down and asked you to sit down, and for a whole minute I pondered. If I had killed you, I should have been ruined by that murder even if I had not confessed the other. But I didn’t think about that at all, and I didn’t want to think of it at that moment. I only hated you and longed to revenge myself on you for everything. The Lord vanquished the devil in my heart. But let me tell you, you were never nearer death.”\n\nA week later he died. The whole town followed him to the grave. The chief priest made a speech full of feeling. All lamented the terrible illness that had cut short his days. But all the town was up in arms against me after the funeral, and people even refused to see me. Some, at first a few and afterwards more, began indeed to believe in the truth of his story, and they visited me and questioned me with great interest and eagerness, for man loves to see the downfall and disgrace of the righteous. But I held my tongue, and very shortly after, I left the town, and five months later by God’s grace I entered upon the safe and blessed path, praising the unseen finger which had guided me so clearly to it. But I remember in my prayer to this day, the servant of God, Mihail, who suffered so greatly.\n\n(e) The Russian Monk and his possible Significance\nFathers and teachers, what is the monk? In the cultivated world the word is nowadays pronounced by some people with a jeer, and by others it is used as a term of abuse, and this contempt for the monk is growing. It is true, alas, it is true, that there are many sluggards, gluttons, profligates and insolent beggars among monks. Educated people point to these: “You are idlers, useless members of society, you live on the labor of others, you are shameless beggars.” And yet how many meek and humble monks there are, yearning for solitude and fervent prayer in peace! These are less noticed, or passed over in silence. And how surprised men would be if I were to say that from these meek monks, who yearn for solitary prayer, the salvation of Russia will come perhaps once more! For they are in truth made ready in peace and quiet “for the day and the hour, the month and the year.” Meanwhile, in their solitude, they keep the image of Christ fair and undefiled, in the purity of God’s truth, from the times of the Fathers of old, the Apostles and the martyrs. And when the time comes they will show it to the tottering creeds of the world. That is a great thought. That star will rise out of the East.\n\nThat is my view of the monk, and is it false? is it too proud? Look at the worldly and all who set themselves up above the people of God, has not God’s image and His truth been distorted in them? They have science; but in science there is nothing but what is the object of sense. The spiritual world, the higher part of man’s being is rejected altogether, dismissed with a sort of triumph, even with hatred. The world has proclaimed the reign of freedom, especially of late, but what do we see in this freedom of theirs? Nothing but slavery and self-destruction! For the world says:\n\n“You have desires and so satisfy them, for you have the same rights as the most rich and powerful. Don’t be afraid of satisfying them and even multiply your desires.” That is the modern doctrine of the world. In that they see freedom. And what follows from this right of multiplication of desires? In the rich, isolation and spiritual suicide; in the poor, envy and murder; for they have been given rights, but have not been shown the means of satisfying their wants. They maintain that the world is getting more and more united, more and more bound together in brotherly community, as it overcomes distance and sets thoughts flying through the air.\n\nAlas, put no faith in such a bond of union. Interpreting freedom as the multiplication and rapid satisfaction of desires, men distort their own nature, for many senseless and foolish desires and habits and ridiculous fancies are fostered in them. They live only for mutual envy, for luxury and ostentation. To have dinners, visits, carriages, rank and slaves to wait on one is looked upon as a necessity, for which life, honor and human feeling are sacrificed, and men even commit suicide if they are unable to satisfy it. We see the same thing among those who are not rich, while the poor drown their unsatisfied need and their envy in drunkenness. But soon they will drink blood instead of wine, they are being led on to it. I ask you is such a man free? I knew one “champion of freedom” who told me himself that, when he was deprived of tobacco in prison, he was so wretched at the privation that he almost went and betrayed his cause for the sake of getting tobacco again! And such a man says, “I am fighting for the cause of humanity.”\n\nHow can such a one fight? what is he fit for? He is capable perhaps of some action quickly over, but he cannot hold out long. And it’s no wonder that instead of gaining freedom they have sunk into slavery, and instead of serving the cause of brotherly love and the union of humanity have fallen, on the contrary, into dissension and isolation, as my mysterious visitor and teacher said to me in my youth. And therefore the idea of the service of humanity, of brotherly love and the solidarity of mankind, is more and more dying out in the world, and indeed this idea is sometimes treated with derision. For how can a man shake off his habits? What can become of him if he is in such bondage to the habit of satisfying the innumerable desires he has created for himself? He is isolated, and what concern has he with the rest of humanity? They have succeeded in accumulating a greater mass of objects, but the joy in the world has grown less.\n\nThe monastic way is very different. Obedience, fasting and prayer are laughed at, yet only through them lies the way to real, true freedom. I cut off my superfluous and unnecessary desires, I subdue my proud and wanton will and chastise it with obedience, and with God’s help I attain freedom of spirit and with it spiritual joy. Which is most capable of conceiving a great idea and serving it—the rich man in his isolation or the man who has freed himself from the tyranny of material things and habits? The monk is reproached for his solitude, “You have secluded yourself within the walls of the monastery for your own salvation, and have forgotten the brotherly service of humanity!” But we shall see which will be most zealous in the cause of brotherly love. For it is not we, but they, who are in isolation, though they don’t see that. Of old, leaders of the people came from among us, and why should they not again? The same meek and humble ascetics will rise up and go out to work for the great cause. The salvation of Russia comes from the people. And the Russian monk has always been on the side of the people. We are isolated only if the people are isolated. The people believe as we do, and an unbelieving reformer will never do anything in Russia, even if he is sincere in heart and a genius. Remember that! The people will meet the atheist and overcome him, and Russia will be one and orthodox. Take care of the peasant and guard his heart. Go on educating him quietly. That’s your duty as monks, for the peasant has God in his heart.\n(f) Of Masters and Servants, and of whether it is possible for them to be Brothers in the Spirit\n\nOf course, I don’t deny that there is sin in the peasants too. And the fire of corruption is spreading visibly, hourly, working from above downwards. The spirit of isolation is coming upon the people too. Money-lenders and devourers of the commune are rising up. Already the merchant grows more and more eager for rank, and strives to show himself cultured though he has not a trace of culture, and to this end meanly despises his old traditions, and is even ashamed of the faith of his fathers. He visits princes, though he is only a peasant corrupted. The peasants are rotting in drunkenness and cannot shake off the habit. And what cruelty to their wives, to their children even! All from drunkenness! I’ve seen in the factories children of nine years old, frail, rickety, bent and already depraved. The stuffy workshop, the din of machinery, work all day long, the vile language and the drink, the drink—is that what a little child’s heart needs? He needs sunshine, childish play, good examples all about him, and at least a little love. There must be no more of this, monks, no more torturing of children, rise up and preach that, make haste, make haste!\n\nBut God will save Russia, for though the peasants are corrupted and cannot renounce their filthy sin, yet they know it is cursed by God and that they do wrong in sinning. So that our people still believe in righteousness, have faith in God and weep tears of devotion.\n\nIt is different with the upper classes. They, following science, want to base justice on reason alone, but not with Christ, as before, and they have already proclaimed that there is no crime, that there is no sin. And that’s consistent, for if you have no God what is the meaning of crime? In Europe the people are already rising up against the rich with violence, and the leaders of the people are everywhere leading them to bloodshed, and teaching them that their wrath is righteous. But their “wrath is accursed, for it is cruel.” But God will save Russia as He has saved her many times. Salvation will come from the people, from their faith and their meekness.\n\nFathers and teachers, watch over the people’s faith and this will not be a dream. I’ve been struck all my life in our great people by their dignity, their true and seemly dignity. I’ve seen it myself, I can testify to it, I’ve seen it and marveled at it, I’ve seen it in spite of the degraded sins and poverty-stricken appearance of our peasantry. They are not servile, and even after two centuries of serfdom they are free in manner and bearing, yet without insolence, and not revengeful and not envious. “You are rich and noble, you are clever and talented, well, be so, God bless you. I respect you, but I know that I too am a man. By the very fact that I respect you without envy I prove my dignity as a man.”\n\nIn truth if they don’t say this (for they don’t know how to say this yet), that is how they act. I have seen it myself, I have known it myself, and, would you believe it, the poorer our Russian peasant is, the more noticeable is that serene goodness, for the rich among them are for the most part corrupted already, and much of that is due to our carelessness and indifference. But God will save His people, for Russia is great in her humility. I dream of seeing, and seem to see clearly already, our future. It will come to pass, that even the most corrupt of our rich will end by being ashamed of his riches before the poor, and the poor, seeing his humility, will understand and give way before him, will respond joyfully and kindly to his honorable shame. Believe me that it will end in that; things are moving to that. Equality is to be found only in the spiritual dignity of man, and that will only be understood among us. If we were brothers, there would be fraternity, but before that, they will never agree about the division of wealth. We preserve the image of Christ, and it will shine forth like a precious diamond to the whole world. So may it be, so may it be!\n\nFathers and teachers, a touching incident befell me once. In my wanderings I met in the town of K. my old orderly, Afanasy. It was eight years since I had parted from him. He chanced to see me in the market-place, recognized me, ran up to me, and how delighted he was! He simply pounced on me: “Master dear, is it you? Is it really you I see?” He took me home with him.\n\nHe was no longer in the army, he was married and already had two little children. He and his wife earned their living as costermongers in the market-place. His room was poor, but bright and clean. He made me sit down, set the samovar, sent for his wife, as though my appearance were a festival for them. He brought me his children: “Bless them, Father.”\n\n“Is it for me to bless them? I am only a humble monk. I will pray for them. And for you, Afanasy Pavlovitch, I have prayed every day since that day, for it all came from you,” said I. And I explained that to him as well as I could. And what do you think? The man kept gazing at me and could not believe that I, his former master, an officer, was now before him in such a guise and position; it made him shed tears.\n\n“Why are you weeping?” said I, “better rejoice over me, dear friend, whom I can never forget, for my path is a glad and joyful one.”\n\nHe did not say much, but kept sighing and shaking his head over me tenderly.\n\n“What has became of your fortune?” he asked.\n\n“I gave it to the monastery,” I answered; “we live in common.”\n\nAfter tea I began saying good-by, and suddenly he brought out half a rouble as an offering to the monastery, and another half-rouble I saw him thrusting hurriedly into my hand: “That’s for you in your wanderings, it may be of use to you, Father.”\n\nI took his half-rouble, bowed to him and his wife, and went out rejoicing. And on my way I thought: “Here we are both now, he at home and I on the road, sighing and shaking our heads, no doubt, and yet smiling joyfully in the gladness of our hearts, remembering how God brought about our meeting.”\n\nI have never seen him again since then. I had been his master and he my servant, but now when we exchanged a loving kiss with softened hearts, there was a great human bond between us. I have thought a great deal about that, and now what I think is this: Is it so inconceivable that that grand and simple-hearted unity might in due time become universal among the Russian people? I believe that it will come to pass and that the time is at hand.\n\nAnd of servants I will add this: In old days when I was young I was often angry with servants; “the cook had served something too hot, the orderly had not brushed my clothes.” But what taught me better then was a thought of my dear brother’s, which I had heard from him in childhood: “Am I worth it, that another should serve me and be ordered about by me in his poverty and ignorance?” And I wondered at the time that such simple and self-evident ideas should be so slow to occur to our minds.\n\nIt is impossible that there should be no servants in the world, but act so that your servant may be freer in spirit than if he were not a servant. And why cannot I be a servant to my servant and even let him see it, and that without any pride on my part or any mistrust on his? Why should not my servant be like my own kindred, so that I may take him into my family and rejoice in doing so? Even now this can be done, but it will lead to the grand unity of men in the future, when a man will not seek servants for himself, or desire to turn his fellow creatures into servants as he does now, but on the contrary, will long with his whole heart to be the servant of all, as the Gospel teaches.\n\nAnd can it be a dream, that in the end man will find his joy only in deeds of light and mercy, and not in cruel pleasures as now, in gluttony, fornication, ostentation, boasting and envious rivalry of one with the other? I firmly believe that it is not and that the time is at hand. People laugh and ask: “When will that time come and does it look like coming?” I believe that with Christ’s help we shall accomplish this great thing. And how many ideas there have been on earth in the history of man which were unthinkable ten years before they appeared! Yet when their destined hour had come, they came forth and spread over the whole earth. So it will be with us, and our people will shine forth in the world, and all men will say: “The stone which the builders rejected has become the corner-stone of the building.”\n\nAnd we may ask the scornful themselves: If our hope is a dream, when will you build up your edifice and order things justly by your intellect alone, without Christ? If they declare that it is they who are advancing towards unity, only the most simple-hearted among them believe it, so that one may positively marvel at such simplicity. Of a truth, they have more fantastic dreams than we. They aim at justice, but, denying Christ, they will end by flooding the earth with blood, for blood cries out for blood, and he that taketh up the sword shall perish by the sword. And if it were not for Christ’s covenant, they would slaughter one another down to the last two men on earth. And those two last men would not be able to restrain each other in their pride, and the one would slay the other and then himself. And that would come to pass, were it not for the promise of Christ that for the sake of the humble and meek the days shall be shortened.\n\nWhile I was still wearing an officer’s uniform after my duel, I talked about servants in general society, and I remember every one was amazed at me. “What!” they asked, “are we to make our servants sit down on the sofa and offer them tea?” And I answered them: “Why not, sometimes at least?” Every one laughed. Their question was frivolous and my answer was not clear; but the thought in it was to some extent right.\n\n(g) Of Prayer, of Love, and of Contact with other Worlds\nYoung man, be not forgetful of prayer. Every time you pray, if your prayer is sincere, there will be new feeling and new meaning in it, which will give you fresh courage, and you will understand that prayer is an education. Remember, too, every day, and whenever you can, repeat to yourself, “Lord, have mercy on all who appear before Thee to-day.” For every hour and every moment thousands of men leave life on this earth, and their souls appear before God. And how many of them depart in solitude, unknown, sad, dejected that no one mourns for them or even knows whether they have lived or not! And behold, from the other end of the earth perhaps, your prayer for their rest will rise up to God though you knew them not nor they you. How touching it must be to a soul standing in dread before the Lord to feel at that instant that, for him too, there is one to pray, that there is a fellow creature left on earth to love him too! And God will look on you both more graciously, for if you have had so much pity on him, how much will He have pity Who is infinitely more loving and merciful than you! And He will forgive him for your sake.\n\nBrothers, have no fear of men’s sin. Love a man even in his sin, for that is the semblance of Divine Love and is the highest love on earth. Love all God’s creation, the whole and every grain of sand in it. Love every leaf, every ray of God’s light. Love the animals, love the plants, love everything. If you love everything, you will perceive the divine mystery in things. Once you perceive it, you will begin to comprehend it better every day. And you will come at last to love the whole world with an all-embracing love. Love the animals: God has given them the rudiments of thought and joy untroubled. Do not trouble it, don’t harass them, don’t deprive them of their happiness, don’t work against God’s intent. Man, do not pride yourself on superiority to the animals; they are without sin, and you, with your greatness, defile the earth by your appearance on it, and leave the traces of your foulness after you—alas, it is true of almost every one of us! Love children especially, for they too are sinless like the angels; they live to soften and purify our hearts and as it were to guide us. Woe to him who offends a child! Father Anfim taught me to love children. The kind, silent man used often on our wanderings to spend the farthings given us on sweets and cakes for the children. He could not pass by a child without emotion. That’s the nature of the man.\n\nAt some thoughts one stands perplexed, especially at the sight of men’s sin, and wonders whether one should use force or humble love. Always decide to use humble love. If you resolve on that once for all, you may subdue the whole world. Loving humility is marvelously strong, the strongest of all things, and there is nothing else like it.\n\nEvery day and every hour, every minute, walk round yourself and watch yourself, and see that your image is a seemly one. You pass by a little child, you pass by, spiteful, with ugly words, with wrathful heart; you may not have noticed the child, but he has seen you, and your image, unseemly and ignoble, may remain in his defenseless heart. You don’t know it, but you may have sown an evil seed in him and it may grow, and all because you were not careful before the child, because you did not foster in yourself a careful, actively benevolent love. Brothers, love is a teacher; but one must know how to acquire it, for it is hard to acquire, it is dearly bought, it is won slowly by long labor. For we must love not only occasionally, for a moment, but for ever. Every one can love occasionally, even the wicked can.\n\nMy brother asked the birds to forgive him; that sounds senseless, but it is right; for all is like an ocean, all is flowing and blending; a touch in one place sets up movement at the other end of the earth. It may be senseless to beg forgiveness of the birds, but birds would be happier at your side—a little happier, anyway—and children and all animals, if you were nobler than you are now. It’s all like an ocean, I tell you. Then you would pray to the birds too, consumed by an all-embracing love, in a sort of transport, and pray that they too will forgive you your sin. Treasure this ecstasy, however senseless it may seem to men.\n\nMy friends, pray to God for gladness. Be glad as children, as the birds of heaven. And let not the sin of men confound you in your doings. Fear not that it will wear away your work and hinder its being accomplished. Do not say, “Sin is mighty, wickedness is mighty, evil environment is mighty, and we are lonely and helpless, and evil environment is wearing us away and hindering our good work from being done.” Fly from that dejection, children! There is only one means of salvation, then take yourself and make yourself responsible for all men’s sins, that is the truth, you know, friends, for as soon as you sincerely make yourself responsible for everything and for all men, you will see at once that it is really so, and that you are to blame for every one and for all things. But throwing your own indolence and impotence on others you will end by sharing the pride of Satan and murmuring against God.\n\nOf the pride of Satan what I think is this: it is hard for us on earth to comprehend it, and therefore it is so easy to fall into error and to share it, even imagining that we are doing something grand and fine. Indeed, many of the strongest feelings and movements of our nature we cannot comprehend on earth. Let not that be a stumbling-block, and think not that it may serve as a justification to you for anything. For the Eternal Judge asks of you what you can comprehend and not what you cannot. You will know that yourself hereafter, for you will behold all things truly then and will not dispute them. On earth, indeed, we are as it were astray, and if it were not for the precious image of Christ before us, we should be undone and altogether lost, as was the human race before the flood. Much on earth is hidden from us, but to make up for that we have been given a precious mystic sense of our living bond with the other world, with the higher heavenly world, and the roots of our thoughts and feelings are not here but in other worlds. That is why the philosophers say that we cannot apprehend the reality of things on earth.\n\nGod took seeds from different worlds and sowed them on this earth, and His garden grew up and everything came up that could come up, but what grows lives and is alive only through the feeling of its contact with other mysterious worlds. If that feeling grows weak or is destroyed in you, the heavenly growth will die away in you. Then you will be indifferent to life and even grow to hate it. That’s what I think.\n\n(h) Can a Man judge his Fellow Creatures? Faith to the End\nRemember particularly that you cannot be a judge of any one. For no one can judge a criminal, until he recognizes that he is just such a criminal as the man standing before him, and that he perhaps is more than all men to blame for that crime. When he understands that, he will be able to be a judge. Though that sounds absurd, it is true. If I had been righteous myself, perhaps there would have been no criminal standing before me. If you can take upon yourself the crime of the criminal your heart is judging, take it at once, suffer for him yourself, and let him go without reproach. And even if the law itself makes you his judge, act in the same spirit so far as possible, for he will go away and condemn himself more bitterly than you have done. If, after your kiss, he goes away untouched, mocking at you, do not let that be a stumbling-block to you. It shows his time has not yet come, but it will come in due course. And if it come not, no matter; if not he, then another in his place will understand and suffer, and judge and condemn himself, and the truth will be fulfilled. Believe that, believe it without doubt; for in that lies all the hope and faith of the saints.\n\nWork without ceasing. If you remember in the night as you go to sleep, “I have not done what I ought to have done,” rise up at once and do it. If the people around you are spiteful and callous and will not hear you, fall down before them and beg their forgiveness; for in truth you are to blame for their not wanting to hear you. And if you cannot speak to them in their bitterness, serve them in silence and in humility, never losing hope. If all men abandon you and even drive you away by force, then when you are left alone fall on the earth and kiss it, water it with your tears and it will bring forth fruit even though no one has seen or heard you in your solitude. Believe to the end, even if all men went astray and you were left the only one faithful; bring your offering even then and praise God in your loneliness. And if two of you are gathered together—then there is a whole world, a world of living love. Embrace each other tenderly and praise God, for if only in you two His truth has been fulfilled.\n\nIf you sin yourself and grieve even unto death for your sins or for your sudden sin, then rejoice for others, rejoice for the righteous man, rejoice that if you have sinned, he is righteous and has not sinned.\n\nIf the evil-doing of men moves you to indignation and overwhelming distress, even to a desire for vengeance on the evil-doers, shun above all things that feeling. Go at once and seek suffering for yourself, as though you were yourself guilty of that wrong. Accept that suffering and bear it and your heart will find comfort, and you will understand that you too are guilty, for you might have been a light to the evil-doers, even as the one man sinless, and you were not a light to them. If you had been a light, you would have lightened the path for others too, and the evil-doer might perhaps have been saved by your light from his sin. And even though your light was shining, yet you see men were not saved by it, hold firm and doubt not the power of the heavenly light. Believe that if they were not saved, they will be saved hereafter. And if they are not saved hereafter, then their sons will be saved, for your light will not die even when you are dead. The righteous man departs, but his light remains. Men are always saved after the death of the deliverer. Men reject their prophets and slay them, but they love their martyrs and honor those whom they have slain. You are working for the whole, you are acting for the future. Seek no reward, for great is your reward on this earth: the spiritual joy which is only vouchsafed to the righteous man. Fear not the great nor the mighty, but be wise and ever serene. Know the measure, know the times, study that. When you are left alone, pray. Love to throw yourself on the earth and kiss it. Kiss the earth and love it with an unceasing, consuming love. Love all men, love everything. Seek that rapture and ecstasy. Water the earth with the tears of your joy and love those tears. Don’t be ashamed of that ecstasy, prize it, for it is a gift of God and a great one; it is not given to many but only to the elect.\n\n(i) Of Hell and Hell Fire, a Mystic Reflection\nFathers and teachers, I ponder, “What is hell?” I maintain that it is the suffering of being unable to love. Once in infinite existence, immeasurable in time and space, a spiritual creature was given on his coming to earth, the power of saying, “I am and I love.” Once, only once, there was given him a moment of active living love, and for that was earthly life given him, and with it times and seasons. And that happy creature rejected the priceless gift, prized it and loved it not, scorned it and remained callous. Such a one, having left the earth, sees Abraham’s bosom and talks with Abraham as we are told in the parable of the rich man and Lazarus, and beholds heaven and can go up to the Lord. But that is just his torment, to rise up to the Lord without ever having loved, to be brought close to those who have loved when he has despised their love. For he sees clearly and says to himself, “Now I have understanding, and though I now thirst to love, there will be nothing great, no sacrifice in my love, for my earthly life is over, and Abraham will not come even with a drop of living water (that is the gift of earthly active life) to cool the fiery thirst of spiritual love which burns in me now, though I despised it on earth; there is no more life for me and will be no more time! Even though I would gladly give my life for others, it can never be, for that life is passed which can be sacrificed for love, and now there is a gulf fixed between that life and this existence.”\n\nThey talk of hell fire in the material sense. I don’t go into that mystery and I shun it. But I think if there were fire in material sense, they would be glad of it, for I imagine that in material agony, their still greater spiritual agony would be forgotten for a moment. Moreover, that spiritual agony cannot be taken from them, for that suffering is not external but within them. And if it could be taken from them, I think it would be bitterer still for the unhappy creatures. For even if the righteous in Paradise forgave them, beholding their torments, and called them up to heaven in their infinite love, they would only multiply their torments, for they would arouse in them still more keenly a flaming thirst for responsive, active and grateful love which is now impossible. In the timidity of my heart I imagine, however, that the very recognition of this impossibility would serve at last to console them. For accepting the love of the righteous together with the impossibility of repaying it, by this submissiveness and the effect of this humility, they will attain at last, as it were, to a certain semblance of that active love which they scorned in life, to something like its outward expression.... I am sorry, friends and brothers, that I cannot express this clearly. But woe to those who have slain themselves on earth, woe to the suicides! I believe that there can be none more miserable than they. They tell us that it is a sin to pray for them and outwardly the Church, as it were, renounces them, but in my secret heart I believe that we may pray even for them. Love can never be an offense to Christ. For such as those I have prayed inwardly all my life, I confess it, fathers and teachers, and even now I pray for them every day.\n\nOh, there are some who remain proud and fierce even in hell, in spite of their certain knowledge and contemplation of the absolute truth; there are some fearful ones who have given themselves over to Satan and his proud spirit entirely. For such, hell is voluntary and ever consuming; they are tortured by their own choice. For they have cursed themselves, cursing God and life. They live upon their vindictive pride like a starving man in the desert sucking blood out of his own body. But they are never satisfied, and they refuse forgiveness, they curse God Who calls them. They cannot behold the living God without hatred, and they cry out that the God of life should be annihilated, that God should destroy Himself and His own creation. And they will burn in the fire of their own wrath for ever and yearn for death and annihilation. But they will not attain to death....\n\nHere Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov’s manuscript ends. I repeat, it is incomplete and fragmentary. Biographical details, for instance, cover only Father Zossima’s earliest youth. Of his teaching and opinions we find brought together sayings evidently uttered on very different occasions. His utterances during the last few hours have not been kept separate from the rest, but their general character can be gathered from what we have in Alexey Fyodorovitch’s manuscript.\n\nThe elder’s death came in the end quite unexpectedly. For although those who were gathered about him that last evening realized that his death was approaching, yet it was difficult to imagine that it would come so suddenly. On the contrary, his friends, as I observed already, seeing him that night apparently so cheerful and talkative, were convinced that there was at least a temporary change for the better in his condition. Even five minutes before his death, they said afterwards wonderingly, it was impossible to foresee it. He seemed suddenly to feel an acute pain in his chest, he turned pale and pressed his hands to his heart. All rose from their seats and hastened to him. But though suffering, he still looked at them with a smile, sank slowly from his chair on to his knees, then bowed his face to the ground, stretched out his arms and as though in joyful ecstasy, praying and kissing the ground, quietly and joyfully gave up his soul to God.\n\nThe news of his death spread at once through the hermitage and reached the monastery. The nearest friends of the deceased and those whose duty it was from their position began to lay out the corpse according to the ancient ritual, and all the monks gathered together in the church. And before dawn the news of the death reached the town. By the morning all the town was talking of the event, and crowds were flocking from the town to the monastery. But this subject will be treated in the next book; I will only add here that before a day had passed something happened so unexpected, so strange, upsetting, and bewildering in its effect on the monks and the townspeople, that after all these years, that day of general suspense is still vividly remembered in the town.\n\n# PART III Book VII. Alyosha Chapter I. The Breath Of Corruption\n\nThe body of Father Zossima was prepared for burial according to the established ritual. As is well known, the bodies of dead monks and hermits are not washed. In the words of the Church Ritual: “If any one of the monks depart in the Lord, the monk designated (that is, whose office it is) shall wipe the body with warm water, making first the sign of the cross with a sponge on the forehead of the deceased, on the breast, on the hands and feet and on the knees, and that is enough.” All this was done by Father Païssy, who then clothed the deceased in his monastic garb and wrapped him in his cloak, which was, according to custom, somewhat slit to allow of its being folded about him in the form of a cross. On his head he put a hood with an eight-cornered cross. The hood was left open and the dead man’s face was covered with black gauze. In his hands was put an ikon of the Saviour. Towards morning he was put in the coffin which had been made ready long before. It was decided to leave the coffin all day in the cell, in the larger room in which the elder used to receive his visitors and fellow monks. As the deceased was a priest and monk of the strictest rule, the Gospel, not the Psalter, had to be read over his body by monks in holy orders. The reading was begun by Father Iosif immediately after the requiem service. Father Païssy desired later on to read the Gospel all day and night over his dead friend, but for the present he, as well as the Father Superintendent of the Hermitage, was very busy and occupied, for something extraordinary, an unheard-of, even “unseemly” excitement and impatient expectation began to be apparent in the monks, and the visitors from the monastery hostels, and the crowds of people flocking from the town. And as time went on, this grew more and more marked. Both the Superintendent and Father Païssy did their utmost to calm the general bustle and agitation.\n\nWhen it was fully daylight, some people began bringing their sick, in most cases children, with them from the town—as though they had been waiting expressly for this moment to do so, evidently persuaded that the dead elder’s remains had a power of healing, which would be immediately made manifest in accordance with their faith. It was only then apparent how unquestionably every one in our town had accepted Father Zossima during his lifetime as a great saint. And those who came were far from being all of the humbler classes.\n\nThis intense expectation on the part of believers displayed with such haste, such openness, even with impatience and almost insistence, impressed Father Païssy as unseemly. Though he had long foreseen something of the sort, the actual manifestation of the feeling was beyond anything he had looked for. When he came across any of the monks who displayed this excitement, Father Païssy began to reprove them. “Such immediate expectation of something extraordinary,” he said, “shows a levity, possible to worldly people but unseemly in us.”\n\nBut little attention was paid him and Father Païssy noticed it uneasily. Yet he himself (if the whole truth must be told), secretly at the bottom of his heart, cherished almost the same hopes and could not but be aware of it, though he was indignant at the too impatient expectation around him, and saw in it light-mindedness and vanity. Nevertheless, it was particularly unpleasant to him to meet certain persons, whose presence aroused in him great misgivings. In the crowd in the dead man’s cell he noticed with inward aversion (for which he immediately reproached himself) the presence of Rakitin and of the monk from Obdorsk, who was still staying in the monastery. Of both of them Father Païssy felt for some reason suddenly suspicious—though, indeed, he might well have felt the same about others.\n\nThe monk from Obdorsk was conspicuous as the most fussy in the excited crowd. He was to be seen everywhere; everywhere he was asking questions, everywhere he was listening, on all sides he was whispering with a peculiar, mysterious air. His expression showed the greatest impatience and even a sort of irritation.\n\nAs for Rakitin, he, as appeared later, had come so early to the hermitage at the special request of Madame Hohlakov. As soon as that good-hearted but weak-minded woman, who could not herself have been admitted to the hermitage, waked and heard of the death of Father Zossima, she was overtaken with such intense curiosity that she promptly dispatched Rakitin to the hermitage, to keep a careful look out and report to her by letter every half-hour or so “everything that takes place.” She regarded Rakitin as a most religious and devout young man. He was particularly clever in getting round people and assuming whatever part he thought most to their taste, if he detected the slightest advantage to himself from doing so.\n\nIt was a bright, clear day, and many of the visitors were thronging about the tombs, which were particularly numerous round the church and scattered here and there about the hermitage. As he walked round the hermitage, Father Païssy remembered Alyosha and that he had not seen him for some time, not since the night. And he had no sooner thought of him than he at once noticed him in the farthest corner of the hermitage garden, sitting on the tombstone of a monk who had been famous long ago for his saintliness. He sat with his back to the hermitage and his face to the wall, and seemed to be hiding behind the tombstone. Going up to him, Father Païssy saw that he was weeping quietly but bitterly, with his face hidden in his hands, and that his whole frame was shaking with sobs. Father Païssy stood over him for a little.\n\n“Enough, dear son, enough, dear,” he pronounced with feeling at last. “Why do you weep? Rejoice and weep not. Don’t you know that this is the greatest of his days? Think only where he is now, at this moment!”\n\nAlyosha glanced at him, uncovering his face, which was swollen with crying like a child’s, but turned away at once without uttering a word and hid his face in his hands again.\n\n“Maybe it is well,” said Father Païssy thoughtfully; “weep if you must, Christ has sent you those tears.”\n\n“Your touching tears are but a relief to your spirit and will serve to gladden your dear heart,” he added to himself, walking away from Alyosha, and thinking lovingly of him. He moved away quickly, however, for he felt that he too might weep looking at him.\n\nMeanwhile the time was passing; the monastery services and the requiems for the dead followed in their due course. Father Païssy again took Father Iosif’s place by the coffin and began reading the Gospel. But before three o’clock in the afternoon that something took place to which I alluded at the end of the last book, something so unexpected by all of us and so contrary to the general hope, that, I repeat, this trivial incident has been minutely remembered to this day in our town and all the surrounding neighborhood. I may add here, for myself personally, that I feel it almost repulsive to recall that event which caused such frivolous agitation and was such a stumbling-block to many, though in reality it was the most natural and trivial matter. I should, of course, have omitted all mention of it in my story, if it had not exerted a very strong influence on the heart and soul of the chief, though future, hero of my story, Alyosha, forming a crisis and turning-point in his spiritual development, giving a shock to his intellect, which finally strengthened it for the rest of his life and gave it a definite aim.\n\nAnd so, to return to our story. When before dawn they laid Father Zossima’s body in the coffin and brought it into the front room, the question of opening the windows was raised among those who were around the coffin. But this suggestion made casually by some one was unanswered and almost unnoticed. Some of those present may perhaps have inwardly noticed it, only to reflect that the anticipation of decay and corruption from the body of such a saint was an actual absurdity, calling for compassion (if not a smile) for the lack of faith and the frivolity it implied. For they expected something quite different.\n\nAnd, behold, soon after midday there were signs of something, at first only observed in silence by those who came in and out and were evidently each afraid to communicate the thought in his mind. But by three o’clock those signs had become so clear and unmistakable, that the news swiftly reached all the monks and visitors in the hermitage, promptly penetrated to the monastery, throwing all the monks into amazement, and finally, in the shortest possible time, spread to the town, exciting every one in it, believers and unbelievers alike. The unbelievers rejoiced, and as for the believers some of them rejoiced even more than the unbelievers, for “men love the downfall and disgrace of the righteous,” as the deceased elder had said in one of his exhortations.\n\nThe fact is that a smell of decomposition began to come from the coffin, growing gradually more marked, and by three o’clock it was quite unmistakable. In all the past history of our monastery, no such scandal could be recalled, and in no other circumstances could such a scandal have been possible, as showed itself in unseemly disorder immediately after this discovery among the very monks themselves. Afterwards, even many years afterwards, some sensible monks were amazed and horrified, when they recalled that day, that the scandal could have reached such proportions. For in the past, monks of very holy life had died, God-fearing old men, whose saintliness was acknowledged by all, yet from their humble coffins, too, the breath of corruption had come, naturally, as from all dead bodies, but that had caused no scandal nor even the slightest excitement. Of course there had been, in former times, saints in the monastery whose memory was carefully preserved and whose relics, according to tradition, showed no signs of corruption. This fact was regarded by the monks as touching and mysterious, and the tradition of it was cherished as something blessed and miraculous, and as a promise, by God’s grace, of still greater glory from their tombs in the future.\n\nOne such, whose memory was particularly cherished, was an old monk, Job, who had died seventy years before at the age of a hundred and five. He had been a celebrated ascetic, rigid in fasting and silence, and his tomb was pointed out to all visitors on their arrival with peculiar respect and mysterious hints of great hopes connected with it. (That was the very tomb on which Father Païssy had found Alyosha sitting in the morning.) Another memory cherished in the monastery was that of the famous Father Varsonofy, who was only recently dead and had preceded Father Zossima in the eldership. He was reverenced during his lifetime as a crazy saint by all the pilgrims to the monastery. There was a tradition that both of these had lain in their coffins as though alive, that they had shown no signs of decomposition when they were buried and that there had been a holy light in their faces. And some people even insisted that a sweet fragrance came from their bodies.\n\nYet, in spite of these edifying memories, it would be difficult to explain the frivolity, absurdity and malice that were manifested beside the coffin of Father Zossima. It is my private opinion that several different causes were simultaneously at work, one of which was the deeply-rooted hostility to the institution of elders as a pernicious innovation, an antipathy hidden deep in the hearts of many of the monks. Even more powerful was jealousy of the dead man’s saintliness, so firmly established during his lifetime that it was almost a forbidden thing to question it. For though the late elder had won over many hearts, more by love than by miracles, and had gathered round him a mass of loving adherents, none the less, in fact, rather the more on that account he had awakened jealousy and so had come to have bitter enemies, secret and open, not only in the monastery but in the world outside it. He did no one any harm, but “Why do they think him so saintly?” And that question alone, gradually repeated, gave rise at last to an intense, insatiable hatred of him. That, I believe, was why many people were extremely delighted at the smell of decomposition which came so quickly, for not a day had passed since his death. At the same time there were some among those who had been hitherto reverently devoted to the elder, who were almost mortified and personally affronted by this incident. This was how the thing happened.\n\nAs soon as signs of decomposition had begun to appear, the whole aspect of the monks betrayed their secret motives in entering the cell. They went in, stayed a little while and hastened out to confirm the news to the crowd of other monks waiting outside. Some of the latter shook their heads mournfully, but others did not even care to conceal the delight which gleamed unmistakably in their malignant eyes. And now no one reproached them for it, no one raised his voice in protest, which was strange, for the majority of the monks had been devoted to the dead elder. But it seemed as though God had in this case let the minority get the upper hand for a time.\n\nVisitors from outside, particularly of the educated class, soon went into the cell, too, with the same spying intent. Of the peasantry few went into the cell, though there were crowds of them at the gates of the hermitage. After three o’clock the rush of worldly visitors was greatly increased and this was no doubt owing to the shocking news. People were attracted who would not otherwise have come on that day and had not intended to come, and among them were some personages of high standing. But external decorum was still preserved and Father Païssy, with a stern face, continued firmly and distinctly reading aloud the Gospel, apparently not noticing what was taking place around him, though he had, in fact, observed something unusual long before. But at last the murmurs, first subdued but gradually louder and more confident, reached even him. “It shows God’s judgment is not as man’s,” Father Païssy heard suddenly. The first to give utterance to this sentiment was a layman, an elderly official from the town, known to be a man of great piety. But he only repeated aloud what the monks had long been whispering. They had long before formulated this damning conclusion, and the worst of it was that a sort of triumphant satisfaction at that conclusion became more and more apparent every moment. Soon they began to lay aside even external decorum and almost seemed to feel they had a sort of right to discard it.\n\n“And for what reason can this have happened,” some of the monks said, at first with a show of regret; “he had a small frame and his flesh was dried up on his bones, what was there to decay?”\n\n“It must be a sign from heaven,” others hastened to add, and their opinion was adopted at once without protest. For it was pointed out, too, that if the decomposition had been natural, as in the case of every dead sinner, it would have been apparent later, after a lapse of at least twenty-four hours, but this premature corruption “was in excess of nature,” and so the finger of God was evident. It was meant for a sign. This conclusion seemed irresistible.\n\nGentle Father Iosif, the librarian, a great favorite of the dead man’s, tried to reply to some of the evil speakers that “this is not held everywhere alike,” and that the incorruptibility of the bodies of the just was not a dogma of the Orthodox Church, but only an opinion, and that even in the most Orthodox regions, at Athos for instance, they were not greatly confounded by the smell of corruption, and there the chief sign of the glorification of the saved was not bodily incorruptibility, but the color of the bones when the bodies have lain many years in the earth and have decayed in it. “And if the bones are yellow as wax, that is the great sign that the Lord has glorified the dead saint, if they are not yellow but black, it shows that God has not deemed him worthy of such glory—that is the belief in Athos, a great place, where the Orthodox doctrine has been preserved from of old, unbroken and in its greatest purity,” said Father Iosif in conclusion.\n\nBut the meek Father’s words had little effect and even provoked a mocking retort. “That’s all pedantry and innovation, no use listening to it,” the monks decided. “We stick to the old doctrine, there are all sorts of innovations nowadays, are we to follow them all?” added others.\n\n“We have had as many holy fathers as they had. There they are among the Turks, they have forgotten everything. Their doctrine has long been impure and they have no bells even,” the most sneering added.\n\nFather Iosif walked away, grieving the more since he had put forward his own opinion with little confidence as though scarcely believing in it himself. He foresaw with distress that something very unseemly was beginning and that there were positive signs of disobedience. Little by little, all the sensible monks were reduced to silence like Father Iosif. And so it came to pass that all who loved the elder and had accepted with devout obedience the institution of the eldership were all at once terribly cast down and glanced timidly in one another’s faces, when they met. Those who were hostile to the institution of elders, as a novelty, held up their heads proudly. “There was no smell of corruption from the late elder Varsonofy, but a sweet fragrance,” they recalled malignantly. “But he gained that glory not because he was an elder, but because he was a holy man.”\n\nAnd this was followed by a shower of criticism and even blame of Father Zossima. “His teaching was false; he taught that life is a great joy and not a vale of tears,” said some of the more unreasonable. “He followed the fashionable belief, he did not recognize material fire in hell,” others, still more unreasonable, added. “He was not strict in fasting, allowed himself sweet things, ate cherry jam with his tea, ladies used to send it to him. Is it for a monk of strict rule to drink tea?” could be heard among some of the envious. “He sat in pride,” the most malignant declared vindictively; “he considered himself a saint and he took it as his due when people knelt before him.” “He abused the sacrament of confession,” the fiercest opponents of the institution of elders added in a malicious whisper. And among these were some of the oldest monks, strictest in their devotion, genuine ascetics, who had kept silent during the life of the deceased elder, but now suddenly unsealed their lips. And this was terrible, for their words had great influence on young monks who were not yet firm in their convictions. The monk from Obdorsk heard all this attentively, heaving deep sighs and nodding his head. “Yes, clearly Father Ferapont was right in his judgment yesterday,” and at that moment Father Ferapont himself made his appearance, as though on purpose to increase the confusion.\n\nI have mentioned already that he rarely left his wooden cell by the apiary. He was seldom even seen at church and they overlooked this neglect on the ground of his craziness, and did not keep him to the rules binding on all the rest. But if the whole truth is to be told, they hardly had a choice about it. For it would have been discreditable to insist on burdening with the common regulations so great an ascetic, who prayed day and night (he even dropped asleep on his knees). If they had insisted, the monks would have said, “He is holier than all of us and he follows a rule harder than ours. And if he does not go to church, it’s because he knows when he ought to; he has his own rule.” It was to avoid the chance of these sinful murmurs that Father Ferapont was left in peace.\n\nAs every one was aware, Father Ferapont particularly disliked Father Zossima. And now the news had reached him in his hut that “God’s judgment is not the same as man’s,” and that something had happened which was “in excess of nature.” It may well be supposed that among the first to run to him with the news was the monk from Obdorsk, who had visited him the evening before and left his cell terror-stricken.\n\nI have mentioned above, that though Father Païssy, standing firm and immovable reading the Gospel over the coffin, could not hear nor see what was passing outside the cell, he gauged most of it correctly in his heart, for he knew the men surrounding him, well. He was not shaken by it, but awaited what would come next without fear, watching with penetration and insight for the outcome of the general excitement.\n\nSuddenly an extraordinary uproar in the passage in open defiance of decorum burst on his ears. The door was flung open and Father Ferapont appeared in the doorway. Behind him there could be seen accompanying him a crowd of monks, together with many people from the town. They did not, however, enter the cell, but stood at the bottom of the steps, waiting to see what Father Ferapont would say or do. For they felt with a certain awe, in spite of their audacity, that he had not come for nothing. Standing in the doorway, Father Ferapont raised his arms, and under his right arm the keen inquisitive little eyes of the monk from Obdorsk peeped in. He alone, in his intense curiosity, could not resist running up the steps after Father Ferapont. The others, on the contrary, pressed farther back in sudden alarm when the door was noisily flung open. Holding his hands aloft, Father Ferapont suddenly roared:\n\n“Casting out I cast out!” and, turning in all directions, he began at once making the sign of the cross at each of the four walls and four corners of the cell in succession. All who accompanied Father Ferapont immediately understood his action. For they knew he always did this wherever he went, and that he would not sit down or say a word, till he had driven out the evil spirits.\n\n“Satan, go hence! Satan, go hence!” he repeated at each sign of the cross. “Casting out I cast out,” he roared again.\n\nHe was wearing his coarse gown girt with a rope. His bare chest, covered with gray hair, could be seen under his hempen shirt. His feet were bare. As soon as he began waving his arms, the cruel irons he wore under his gown could be heard clanking.\n\nFather Païssy paused in his reading, stepped forward and stood before him waiting.\n\n“What have you come for, worthy Father? Why do you offend against good order? Why do you disturb the peace of the flock?” he said at last, looking sternly at him.\n\n“What have I come for? You ask why? What is your faith?” shouted Father Ferapont crazily. “I’ve come here to drive out your visitors, the unclean devils. I’ve come to see how many have gathered here while I have been away. I want to sweep them out with a birch broom.”\n\n“You cast out the evil spirit, but perhaps you are serving him yourself,” Father Païssy went on fearlessly. “And who can say of himself ‘I am holy’? Can you, Father?”\n\n“I am unclean, not holy. I would not sit in an arm-chair and would not have them bow down to me as an idol,” thundered Father Ferapont. “Nowadays folk destroy the true faith. The dead man, your saint,” he turned to the crowd, pointing with his finger to the coffin, “did not believe in devils. He gave medicine to keep off the devils. And so they have become as common as spiders in the corners. And now he has begun to stink himself. In that we see a great sign from God.”\n\nThe incident he referred to was this. One of the monks was haunted in his dreams and, later on, in waking moments, by visions of evil spirits. When in the utmost terror he confided this to Father Zossima, the elder had advised continual prayer and rigid fasting. But when that was of no use, he advised him, while persisting in prayer and fasting, to take a special medicine. Many persons were shocked at the time and wagged their heads as they talked over it—and most of all Father Ferapont, to whom some of the censorious had hastened to report this “extraordinary” counsel on the part of the elder.\n\n“Go away, Father!” said Father Païssy, in a commanding voice, “it’s not for man to judge but for God. Perhaps we see here a ‘sign’ which neither you, nor I, nor any one of us is able to comprehend. Go, Father, and do not trouble the flock!” he repeated impressively.\n\n“He did not keep the fasts according to the rule and therefore the sign has come. That is clear and it’s a sin to hide it,” the fanatic, carried away by a zeal that outstripped his reason, would not be quieted. “He was seduced by sweetmeats, ladies brought them to him in their pockets, he sipped tea, he worshiped his belly, filling it with sweet things and his mind with haughty thoughts.... And for this he is put to shame....”\n\n“You speak lightly, Father.” Father Païssy, too, raised his voice. “I admire your fasting and severities, but you speak lightly like some frivolous youth, fickle and childish. Go away, Father, I command you!” Father Païssy thundered in conclusion.\n\n“I will go,” said Ferapont, seeming somewhat taken aback, but still as bitter. “You learned men! You are so clever you look down upon my humbleness. I came hither with little learning and here I have forgotten what I did know, God Himself has preserved me in my weakness from your subtlety.”\n\nFather Païssy stood over him, waiting resolutely. Father Ferapont paused and, suddenly leaning his cheek on his hand despondently, pronounced in a sing-song voice, looking at the coffin of the dead elder:\n\n“To-morrow they will sing over him ‘Our Helper and Defender’—a splendid anthem—and over me when I die all they’ll sing will be ‘What earthly joy’—a little canticle,”[6] he added with tearful regret. “You are proud and puffed up, this is a vain place!” he shouted suddenly like a madman, and with a wave of his hand he turned quickly and quickly descended the steps. The crowd awaiting him below wavered; some followed him at once and some lingered, for the cell was still open, and Father Païssy, following Father Ferapont on to the steps, stood watching him. But the excited old fanatic was not completely silenced. Walking twenty steps away, he suddenly turned towards the setting sun, raised both his arms and, as though some one had cut him down, fell to the ground with a loud scream.\n\n“My God has conquered! Christ has conquered the setting sun!” he shouted frantically, stretching up his hands to the sun, and falling face downwards on the ground, he sobbed like a little child, shaken by his tears and spreading out his arms on the ground. Then all rushed up to him; there were exclamations and sympathetic sobs ... a kind of frenzy seemed to take possession of them all.\n\n“This is the one who is a saint! This is the one who is a holy man!” some cried aloud, losing their fear. “This is he who should be an elder,” others added malignantly.\n\n“He wouldn’t be an elder ... he would refuse ... he wouldn’t serve a cursed innovation ... he wouldn’t imitate their foolery,” other voices chimed in at once. And it is hard to say how far they might have gone, but at that moment the bell rang summoning them to service. All began crossing themselves at once. Father Ferapont, too, got up and crossing himself went back to his cell without looking round, still uttering exclamations which were utterly incoherent. A few followed him, but the greater number dispersed, hastening to service. Father Païssy let Father Iosif read in his place and went down. The frantic outcries of bigots could not shake him, but his heart was suddenly filled with melancholy for some special reason and he felt that. He stood still and suddenly wondered, “Why am I sad even to dejection?” and immediately grasped with surprise that his sudden sadness was due to a very small and special cause. In the crowd thronging at the entrance to the cell, he had noticed Alyosha and he remembered that he had felt at once a pang at heart on seeing him. “Can that boy mean so much to my heart now?” he asked himself, wondering.\n\nAt that moment Alyosha passed him, hurrying away, but not in the direction of the church. Their eyes met. Alyosha quickly turned away his eyes and dropped them to the ground, and from the boy’s look alone, Father Païssy guessed what a great change was taking place in him at that moment.\n\n“Have you, too, fallen into temptation?” cried Father Païssy. “Can you be with those of little faith?” he added mournfully.\n\nAlyosha stood still and gazed vaguely at Father Païssy, but quickly turned his eyes away again and again looked on the ground. He stood sideways and did not turn his face to Father Païssy, who watched him attentively.\n\n“Where are you hastening? The bell calls to service,” he asked again, but again Alyosha gave no answer.\n\n“Are you leaving the hermitage? What, without asking leave, without asking a blessing?”\n\nAlyosha suddenly gave a wry smile, cast a strange, very strange, look at the Father to whom his former guide, the former sovereign of his heart and mind, his beloved elder, had confided him as he lay dying. And suddenly, still without speaking, waved his hand, as though not caring even to be respectful, and with rapid steps walked towards the gates away from the hermitage.\n\n“You will come back again!” murmured Father Païssy, looking after him with sorrowful surprise.\n\n# Chapter II. A Critical Moment\n\nFather Païssy, of course, was not wrong when he decided that his “dear boy” would come back again. Perhaps indeed, to some extent, he penetrated with insight into the true meaning of Alyosha’s spiritual condition. Yet I must frankly own that it would be very difficult for me to give a clear account of that strange, vague moment in the life of the young hero I love so much. To Father Païssy’s sorrowful question, “Are you too with those of little faith?” I could of course confidently answer for Alyosha, “No, he is not with those of little faith. Quite the contrary.” Indeed, all his trouble came from the fact that he was of great faith. But still the trouble was there and was so agonizing that even long afterwards Alyosha thought of that sorrowful day as one of the bitterest and most fatal days of his life. If the question is asked: “Could all his grief and disturbance have been only due to the fact that his elder’s body had shown signs of premature decomposition instead of at once performing miracles?” I must answer without beating about the bush, “Yes, it certainly was.” I would only beg the reader not to be in too great a hurry to laugh at my young hero’s pure heart. I am far from intending to apologize for him or to justify his innocent faith on the ground of his youth, or the little progress he had made in his studies, or any such reason. I must declare, on the contrary, that I have genuine respect for the qualities of his heart. No doubt a youth who received impressions cautiously, whose love was lukewarm, and whose mind was too prudent for his age and so of little value, such a young man might, I admit, have avoided what happened to my hero. But in some cases it is really more creditable to be carried away by an emotion, however unreasonable, which springs from a great love, than to be unmoved. And this is even truer in youth, for a young man who is always sensible is to be suspected and is of little worth—that’s my opinion!\n\n“But,” reasonable people will exclaim perhaps, “every young man cannot believe in such a superstition and your hero is no model for others.”\n\nTo this I reply again, “Yes! my hero had faith, a faith holy and steadfast, but still I am not going to apologize for him.”\n\nThough I declared above, and perhaps too hastily, that I should not explain or justify my hero, I see that some explanation is necessary for the understanding of the rest of my story. Let me say then, it was not a question of miracles. There was no frivolous and impatient expectation of miracles in his mind. And Alyosha needed no miracles at the time, for the triumph of some preconceived idea—oh, no, not at all—what he saw before all was one figure—the figure of his beloved elder, the figure of that holy man whom he revered with such adoration. The fact is that all the love that lay concealed in his pure young heart for every one and everything had, for the past year, been concentrated—and perhaps wrongly so—on one being, his beloved elder. It is true that being had for so long been accepted by him as his ideal, that all his young strength and energy could not but turn towards that ideal, even to the forgetting at the moment “of every one and everything.” He remembered afterwards how, on that terrible day, he had entirely forgotten his brother Dmitri, about whom he had been so anxious and troubled the day before; he had forgotten, too, to take the two hundred roubles to Ilusha’s father, though he had so warmly intended to do so the preceding evening. But again it was not miracles he needed but only “the higher justice” which had been in his belief outraged by the blow that had so suddenly and cruelly wounded his heart. And what does it signify that this “justice” looked for by Alyosha inevitably took the shape of miracles to be wrought immediately by the ashes of his adored teacher? Why, every one in the monastery cherished the same thought and the same hope, even those whose intellects Alyosha revered, Father Païssy himself, for instance. And so Alyosha, untroubled by doubts, clothed his dreams too in the same form as all the rest. And a whole year of life in the monastery had formed the habit of this expectation in his heart. But it was justice, justice, he thirsted for, not simply miracles.\n\nAnd now the man who should, he believed, have been exalted above every one in the whole world, that man, instead of receiving the glory that was his due, was suddenly degraded and dishonored! What for? Who had judged him? Who could have decreed this? Those were the questions that wrung his inexperienced and virginal heart. He could not endure without mortification, without resentment even, that the holiest of holy men should have been exposed to the jeering and spiteful mockery of the frivolous crowd so inferior to him. Even had there been no miracles, had there been nothing marvelous to justify his hopes, why this indignity, why this humiliation, why this premature decay, “in excess of nature,” as the spiteful monks said? Why this “sign from heaven,” which they so triumphantly acclaimed in company with Father Ferapont, and why did they believe they had gained the right to acclaim it? Where is the finger of Providence? Why did Providence hide its face “at the most critical moment” (so Alyosha thought it), as though voluntarily submitting to the blind, dumb, pitiless laws of nature?\n\nThat was why Alyosha’s heart was bleeding, and, of course, as I have said already, the sting of it all was that the man he loved above everything on earth should be put to shame and humiliated! This murmuring may have been shallow and unreasonable in my hero, but I repeat again for the third time—and am prepared to admit that it might be difficult to defend my feeling—I am glad that my hero showed himself not too reasonable at that moment, for any man of sense will always come back to reason in time, but, if love does not gain the upper hand in a boy’s heart at such an exceptional moment, when will it? I will not, however, omit to mention something strange, which came for a time to the surface of Alyosha’s mind at this fatal and obscure moment. This new something was the harassing impression left by the conversation with Ivan, which now persistently haunted Alyosha’s mind. At this moment it haunted him. Oh, it was not that something of the fundamental, elemental, so to speak, faith of his soul had been shaken. He loved his God and believed in Him steadfastly, though he was suddenly murmuring against Him. Yet a vague but tormenting and evil impression left by his conversation with Ivan the day before, suddenly revived again now in his soul and seemed forcing its way to the surface of his consciousness.\n\nIt had begun to get dusk when Rakitin, crossing the pine copse from the hermitage to the monastery, suddenly noticed Alyosha, lying face downwards on the ground under a tree, not moving and apparently asleep. He went up and called him by his name.\n\n“You here, Alexey? Can you have—” he began wondering but broke off. He had meant to say, “Can you have come to this?”\n\nAlyosha did not look at him, but from a slight movement Rakitin at once saw that he heard and understood him.\n\n“What’s the matter?” he went on; but the surprise in his face gradually passed into a smile that became more and more ironical.\n\n“I say, I’ve been looking for you for the last two hours. You suddenly disappeared. What are you about? What foolery is this? You might just look at me...”\n\nAlyosha raised his head, sat up and leaned his back against the tree. He was not crying, but there was a look of suffering and irritability in his face. He did not look at Rakitin, however, but looked away to one side of him.\n\n“Do you know your face is quite changed? There’s none of your famous mildness to be seen in it. Are you angry with some one? Have they been ill-treating you?”\n\n“Let me alone,” said Alyosha suddenly, with a weary gesture of his hand, still looking away from him.\n\n“Oho! So that’s how we are feeling! So you can shout at people like other mortals. That is a come-down from the angels. I say, Alyosha, you have surprised me, do you hear? I mean it. It’s long since I’ve been surprised at anything here. I always took you for an educated man....”\n\nAlyosha at last looked at him, but vaguely, as though scarcely understanding what he said.\n\n“Can you really be so upset simply because your old man has begun to stink? You don’t mean to say you seriously believed that he was going to work miracles?” exclaimed Rakitin, genuinely surprised again.\n\n“I believed, I believe, I want to believe, and I will believe, what more do you want?” cried Alyosha irritably.\n\n“Nothing at all, my boy. Damn it all! why, no schoolboy of thirteen believes in that now. But there.... So now you are in a temper with your God, you are rebelling against Him; He hasn’t given promotion, He hasn’t bestowed the order of merit! Eh, you are a set!”\n\nAlyosha gazed a long while with his eyes half closed at Rakitin, and there was a sudden gleam in his eyes ... but not of anger with Rakitin.\n\n“I am not rebelling against my God; I simply ‘don’t accept His world.’” Alyosha suddenly smiled a forced smile.\n\n“How do you mean, you don’t accept the world?” Rakitin thought a moment over his answer. “What idiocy is this?”\n\nAlyosha did not answer.\n\n“Come, enough nonsense, now to business. Have you had anything to eat to-day?”\n\n“I don’t remember.... I think I have.”\n\n“You need keeping up, to judge by your face. It makes one sorry to look at you. You didn’t sleep all night either, I hear, you had a meeting in there. And then all this bobbery afterwards. Most likely you’ve had nothing to eat but a mouthful of holy bread. I’ve got some sausage in my pocket; I’ve brought it from the town in case of need, only you won’t eat sausage....”\n\n“Give me some.”\n\n“I say! You are going it! Why, it’s a regular mutiny, with barricades! Well, my boy, we must make the most of it. Come to my place.... I shouldn’t mind a drop of vodka myself, I am tired to death. Vodka is going too far for you, I suppose ... or would you like some?”\n\n“Give me some vodka too.”\n\n“Hullo! You surprise me, brother!” Rakitin looked at him in amazement. “Well, one way or another, vodka or sausage, this is a jolly fine chance and mustn’t be missed. Come along.”\n\nAlyosha got up in silence and followed Rakitin.\n\n“If your little brother Ivan could see this—wouldn’t he be surprised! By the way, your brother Ivan set off to Moscow this morning, did you know?”\n\n“Yes,” answered Alyosha listlessly, and suddenly the image of his brother Dmitri rose before his mind. But only for a minute, and though it reminded him of something that must not be put off for a moment, some duty, some terrible obligation, even that reminder made no impression on him, did not reach his heart and instantly faded out of his mind and was forgotten. But, a long while afterwards, Alyosha remembered this.\n\n“Your brother Ivan declared once that I was a ‘liberal booby with no talents whatsoever.’ Once you, too, could not resist letting me know I was ‘dishonorable.’ Well! I should like to see what your talents and sense of honor will do for you now.” This phrase Rakitin finished to himself in a whisper.\n\n“Listen!” he said aloud, “let’s go by the path beyond the monastery straight to the town. Hm! I ought to go to Madame Hohlakov’s by the way. Only fancy, I’ve written to tell her everything that happened, and would you believe it, she answered me instantly in pencil (the lady has a passion for writing notes) that ‘she would never have expected such conduct from a man of such a reverend character as Father Zossima.’ That was her very word: ‘conduct.’ She is angry too. Eh, you are a set! Stay!” he cried suddenly again. He suddenly stopped and taking Alyosha by the shoulder made him stop too.\n\n“Do you know, Alyosha,” he peeped inquisitively into his eyes, absorbed in a sudden new thought which had dawned on him, and though he was laughing outwardly he was evidently afraid to utter that new idea aloud, so difficult he still found it to believe in the strange and unexpected mood in which he now saw Alyosha. “Alyosha, do you know where we had better go?” he brought out at last timidly, and insinuatingly.\n\n“I don’t care ... where you like.”\n\n“Let’s go to Grushenka, eh? Will you come?” pronounced Rakitin at last, trembling with timid suspense.\n\n“Let’s go to Grushenka,” Alyosha answered calmly, at once, and this prompt and calm agreement was such a surprise to Rakitin that he almost started back.\n\n“Well! I say!” he cried in amazement, but seizing Alyosha firmly by the arm he led him along the path, still dreading that he would change his mind.\n\nThey walked along in silence, Rakitin was positively afraid to talk.\n\n“And how glad she will be, how delighted!” he muttered, but lapsed into silence again. And indeed it was not to please Grushenka he was taking Alyosha to her. He was a practical person and never undertook anything without a prospect of gain for himself. His object in this case was twofold, first a revengeful desire to see “the downfall of the righteous,” and Alyosha’s fall “from the saints to the sinners,” over which he was already gloating in his imagination, and in the second place he had in view a certain material gain for himself, of which more will be said later.\n\n“So the critical moment has come,” he thought to himself with spiteful glee, “and we shall catch it on the hop, for it’s just what we want.”\n\n# Chapter III. An Onion\n\nGrushenka lived in the busiest part of the town, near the cathedral square, in a small wooden lodge in the courtyard belonging to the house of the widow Morozov. The house was a large stone building of two stories, old and very ugly. The widow led a secluded life with her two unmarried nieces, who were also elderly women. She had no need to let her lodge, but every one knew that she had taken in Grushenka as a lodger, four years before, solely to please her kinsman, the merchant Samsonov, who was known to be the girl’s protector. It was said that the jealous old man’s object in placing his “favorite” with the widow Morozov was that the old woman should keep a sharp eye on her new lodger’s conduct. But this sharp eye soon proved to be unnecessary, and in the end the widow Morozov seldom met Grushenka and did not worry her by looking after her in any way. It is true that four years had passed since the old man had brought the slim, delicate, shy, timid, dreamy, and sad girl of eighteen from the chief town of the province, and much had happened since then. Little was known of the girl’s history in the town and that little was vague. Nothing more had been learnt during the last four years, even after many persons had become interested in the beautiful young woman into whom Agrafena Alexandrovna had meanwhile developed. There were rumors that she had been at seventeen betrayed by some one, some sort of officer, and immediately afterwards abandoned by him. The officer had gone away and afterwards married, while Grushenka had been left in poverty and disgrace. It was said, however, that though Grushenka had been raised from destitution by the old man, Samsonov, she came of a respectable family belonging to the clerical class, that she was the daughter of a deacon or something of the sort.\n\nAnd now after four years the sensitive, injured and pathetic little orphan had become a plump, rosy beauty of the Russian type, a woman of bold and determined character, proud and insolent. She had a good head for business, was acquisitive, saving and careful, and by fair means or foul had succeeded, it was said, in amassing a little fortune. There was only one point on which all were agreed. Grushenka was not easily to be approached and except her aged protector there had not been one man who could boast of her favors during those four years. It was a positive fact, for there had been a good many, especially during the last two years, who had attempted to obtain those favors. But all their efforts had been in vain and some of these suitors had been forced to beat an undignified and even comic retreat, owing to the firm and ironical resistance they met from the strong-willed young person. It was known, too, that the young person had, especially of late, been given to what is called “speculation,” and that she had shown marked abilities in that direction, so that many people began to say that she was no better than a Jew. It was not that she lent money on interest, but it was known, for instance, that she had for some time past, in partnership with old Karamazov, actually invested in the purchase of bad debts for a trifle, a tenth of their nominal value, and afterwards had made out of them ten times their value.\n\nThe old widower Samsonov, a man of large fortune, was stingy and merciless. He tyrannized over his grown-up sons, but, for the last year during which he had been ill and lost the use of his swollen legs, he had fallen greatly under the influence of his protégée, whom he had at first kept strictly and in humble surroundings, “on Lenten fare,” as the wits said at the time. But Grushenka had succeeded in emancipating herself, while she established in him a boundless belief in her fidelity. The old man, now long since dead, had had a large business in his day and was also a noteworthy character, miserly and hard as flint. Though Grushenka’s hold upon him was so strong that he could not live without her (it had been so especially for the last two years), he did not settle any considerable fortune on her and would not have been moved to do so, if she had threatened to leave him. But he had presented her with a small sum, and even that was a surprise to every one when it became known.\n\n“You are a wench with brains,” he said to her, when he gave her eight thousand roubles, “and you must look after yourself, but let me tell you that except your yearly allowance as before, you’ll get nothing more from me to the day of my death, and I’ll leave you nothing in my will either.”\n\nAnd he kept his word; he died and left everything to his sons, whom, with their wives and children, he had treated all his life as servants. Grushenka was not even mentioned in his will. All this became known afterwards. He helped Grushenka with his advice to increase her capital and put business in her way.\n\nWhen Fyodor Pavlovitch, who first came into contact with Grushenka over a piece of speculation, ended to his own surprise by falling madly in love with her, old Samsonov, gravely ill as he was, was immensely amused. It is remarkable that throughout their whole acquaintance Grushenka was absolutely and spontaneously open with the old man, and he seems to have been the only person in the world with whom she was so. Of late, when Dmitri too had come on the scene with his love, the old man left off laughing. On the contrary, he once gave Grushenka a stern and earnest piece of advice.\n\n“If you have to choose between the two, father or son, you’d better choose the old man, if only you make sure the old scoundrel will marry you and settle some fortune on you beforehand. But don’t keep on with the captain, you’ll get no good out of that.”\n\nThese were the very words of the old profligate, who felt already that his death was not far off and who actually died five months later.\n\nI will note, too, in passing, that although many in our town knew of the grotesque and monstrous rivalry of the Karamazovs, father and son, the object of which was Grushenka, scarcely any one understood what really underlay her attitude to both of them. Even Grushenka’s two servants (after the catastrophe of which we will speak later) testified in court that she received Dmitri Fyodorovitch simply from fear because “he threatened to murder her.” These servants were an old cook, invalidish and almost deaf, who came from Grushenka’s old home, and her granddaughter, a smart young girl of twenty, who performed the duties of a maid. Grushenka lived very economically and her surroundings were anything but luxurious. Her lodge consisted of three rooms furnished with mahogany furniture in the fashion of 1820, belonging to her landlady.\n\nIt was quite dark when Rakitin and Alyosha entered her rooms, yet they were not lighted up. Grushenka was lying down in her drawing-room on the big, hard, clumsy sofa, with a mahogany back. The sofa was covered with shabby and ragged leather. Under her head she had two white down pillows taken from her bed. She was lying stretched out motionless on her back with her hands behind her head. She was dressed as though expecting some one, in a black silk dress, with a dainty lace fichu on her head, which was very becoming. Over her shoulders was thrown a lace shawl pinned with a massive gold brooch. She certainly was expecting some one. She lay as though impatient and weary, her face rather pale and her lips and eyes hot, restlessly tapping the arm of the sofa with the tip of her right foot. The appearance of Rakitin and Alyosha caused a slight excitement. From the hall they could hear Grushenka leap up from the sofa and cry out in a frightened voice, “Who’s there?” But the maid met the visitors and at once called back to her mistress.\n\n“It’s not he, it’s nothing, only other visitors.”\n\n“What can be the matter?” muttered Rakitin, leading Alyosha into the drawing-room.\n\nGrushenka was standing by the sofa as though still alarmed. A thick coil of her dark brown hair escaped from its lace covering and fell on her right shoulder, but she did not notice it and did not put it back till she had gazed at her visitors and recognized them.\n\n“Ah, it’s you, Rakitin? You quite frightened me. Whom have you brought? Who is this with you? Good heavens, you have brought him!” she exclaimed, recognizing Alyosha.\n\n“Do send for candles!” said Rakitin, with the free-and-easy air of a most intimate friend, who is privileged to give orders in the house.\n\n“Candles ... of course, candles.... Fenya, fetch him a candle.... Well, you have chosen a moment to bring him!” she exclaimed again, nodding towards Alyosha, and turning to the looking-glass she began quickly fastening up her hair with both hands. She seemed displeased.\n\n“Haven’t I managed to please you?” asked Rakitin, instantly almost offended.\n\n“You frightened me, Rakitin, that’s what it is.” Grushenka turned with a smile to Alyosha. “Don’t be afraid of me, my dear Alyosha, you cannot think how glad I am to see you, my unexpected visitor. But you frightened me, Rakitin, I thought it was Mitya breaking in. You see, I deceived him just now, I made him promise to believe me and I told him a lie. I told him that I was going to spend the evening with my old man, Kuzma Kuzmitch, and should be there till late counting up his money. I always spend one whole evening a week with him making up his accounts. We lock ourselves in and he counts on the reckoning beads while I sit and put things down in the book. I am the only person he trusts. Mitya believes that I am there, but I came back and have been sitting locked in here, expecting some news. How was it Fenya let you in? Fenya, Fenya, run out to the gate, open it and look about whether the captain is to be seen! Perhaps he is hiding and spying, I am dreadfully frightened.”\n\n“There’s no one there, Agrafena Alexandrovna, I’ve just looked out, I keep running to peep through the crack, I am in fear and trembling myself.”\n\n“Are the shutters fastened, Fenya? And we must draw the curtains—that’s better!” She drew the heavy curtains herself. “He’d rush in at once if he saw a light. I am afraid of your brother Mitya to-day, Alyosha.”\n\nGrushenka spoke aloud, and, though she was alarmed, she seemed very happy about something.\n\n“Why are you so afraid of Mitya to-day?” inquired Rakitin. “I should have thought you were not timid with him, you’d twist him round your little finger.”\n\n“I tell you, I am expecting news, priceless news, so I don’t want Mitya at all. And he didn’t believe, I feel he didn’t, that I should stay at Kuzma Kuzmitch’s. He must be in his ambush now, behind Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, in the garden, watching for me. And if he’s there, he won’t come here, so much the better! But I really have been to Kuzma Kuzmitch’s, Mitya escorted me there. I told him I should stay there till midnight, and I asked him to be sure to come at midnight to fetch me home. He went away and I sat ten minutes with Kuzma Kuzmitch and came back here again. Ugh, I was afraid, I ran for fear of meeting him.”\n\n“And why are you so dressed up? What a curious cap you’ve got on!”\n\n“How curious you are yourself, Rakitin! I tell you, I am expecting a message. If the message comes, I shall fly, I shall gallop away and you will see no more of me. That’s why I am dressed up, so as to be ready.”\n\n“And where are you flying to?”\n\n“If you know too much, you’ll get old too soon.”\n\n“Upon my word! You are highly delighted ... I’ve never seen you like this before. You are dressed up as if you were going to a ball.” Rakitin looked her up and down.\n\n“Much you know about balls.”\n\n“And do you know much about them?”\n\n“I have seen a ball. The year before last, Kuzma Kuzmitch’s son was married and I looked on from the gallery. Do you suppose I want to be talking to you, Rakitin, while a prince like this is standing here. Such a visitor! Alyosha, my dear boy, I gaze at you and can’t believe my eyes. Good heavens, can you have come here to see me! To tell you the truth, I never had a thought of seeing you and I didn’t think that you would ever come and see me. Though this is not the moment now, I am awfully glad to see you. Sit down on the sofa, here, that’s right, my bright young moon. I really can’t take it in even now.... Eh, Rakitin, if only you had brought him yesterday or the day before! But I am glad as it is! Perhaps it’s better he has come now, at such a moment, and not the day before yesterday.”\n\nShe gayly sat down beside Alyosha on the sofa, looking at him with positive delight. And she really was glad, she was not lying when she said so. Her eyes glowed, her lips laughed, but it was a good-hearted merry laugh. Alyosha had not expected to see such a kind expression in her face.... He had hardly met her till the day before, he had formed an alarming idea of her, and had been horribly distressed the day before by the spiteful and treacherous trick she had played on Katerina Ivanovna. He was greatly surprised to find her now altogether different from what he had expected. And, crushed as he was by his own sorrow, his eyes involuntarily rested on her with attention. Her whole manner seemed changed for the better since yesterday, there was scarcely any trace of that mawkish sweetness in her speech, of that voluptuous softness in her movements. Everything was simple and good-natured, her gestures were rapid, direct, confiding, but she was greatly excited.\n\n“Dear me, how everything comes together to-day!” she chattered on again. “And why I am so glad to see you, Alyosha, I couldn’t say myself! If you ask me, I couldn’t tell you.”\n\n“Come, don’t you know why you’re glad?” said Rakitin, grinning. “You used to be always pestering me to bring him, you’d some object, I suppose.”\n\n“I had a different object once, but now that’s over, this is not the moment. I say, I want you to have something nice. I am so good-natured now. You sit down, too, Rakitin; why are you standing? You’ve sat down already? There’s no fear of Rakitin’s forgetting to look after himself. Look, Alyosha, he’s sitting there opposite us, so offended that I didn’t ask him to sit down before you. Ugh, Rakitin is such a one to take offense!” laughed Grushenka. “Don’t be angry, Rakitin, I’m kind to-day. Why are you so depressed, Alyosha? Are you afraid of me?” She peeped into his eyes with merry mockery.\n\n“He’s sad. The promotion has not been given,” boomed Rakitin.\n\n“What promotion?”\n\n“His elder stinks.”\n\n“What? You are talking some nonsense, you want to say something nasty. Be quiet, you stupid! Let me sit on your knee, Alyosha, like this.” She suddenly skipped forward and jumped, laughing, on his knee, like a nestling kitten, with her right arm about his neck. “I’ll cheer you up, my pious boy. Yes, really, will you let me sit on your knee? You won’t be angry? If you tell me, I’ll get off?”\n\nAlyosha did not speak. He sat afraid to move, he heard her words, “If you tell me, I’ll get off,” but he did not answer. But there was nothing in his heart such as Rakitin, for instance, watching him malignantly from his corner, might have expected or fancied. The great grief in his heart swallowed up every sensation that might have been aroused, and, if only he could have thought clearly at that moment, he would have realized that he had now the strongest armor to protect him from every lust and temptation. Yet in spite of the vague irresponsiveness of his spiritual condition and the sorrow that overwhelmed him, he could not help wondering at a new and strange sensation in his heart. This woman, this “dreadful” woman, had no terror for him now, none of that terror that had stirred in his soul at any passing thought of woman. On the contrary, this woman, dreaded above all women, sitting now on his knee, holding him in her arms, aroused in him now a quite different, unexpected, peculiar feeling, a feeling of the intensest and purest interest without a trace of fear, of his former terror. That was what instinctively surprised him.\n\n“You’ve talked nonsense enough,” cried Rakitin, “you’d much better give us some champagne. You owe it me, you know you do!”\n\n“Yes, I really do. Do you know, Alyosha, I promised him champagne on the top of everything, if he’d bring you? I’ll have some too! Fenya, Fenya, bring us the bottle Mitya left! Look sharp! Though I am so stingy, I’ll stand a bottle, not for you, Rakitin, you’re a toadstool, but he is a falcon! And though my heart is full of something very different, so be it, I’ll drink with you. I long for some dissipation.”\n\n“But what is the matter with you? And what is this message, may I ask, or is it a secret?” Rakitin put in inquisitively, doing his best to pretend not to notice the snubs that were being continually aimed at him.\n\n“Ech, it’s not a secret, and you know it, too,” Grushenka said, in a voice suddenly anxious, turning her head towards Rakitin, and drawing a little away from Alyosha, though she still sat on his knee with her arm round his neck. “My officer is coming, Rakitin, my officer is coming.”\n\n“I heard he was coming, but is he so near?”\n\n“He is at Mokroe now; he’ll send a messenger from there, so he wrote; I got a letter from him to-day. I am expecting the messenger every minute.”\n\n“You don’t say so! Why at Mokroe?”\n\n“That’s a long story, I’ve told you enough.”\n\n“Mitya’ll be up to something now—I say! Does he know or doesn’t he?”\n\n“He know! Of course he doesn’t. If he knew, there would be murder. But I am not afraid of that now, I am not afraid of his knife. Be quiet, Rakitin, don’t remind me of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, he has bruised my heart. And I don’t want to think of that at this moment. I can think of Alyosha here, I can look at Alyosha ... smile at me, dear, cheer up, smile at my foolishness, at my pleasure.... Ah, he’s smiling, he’s smiling! How kindly he looks at me! And you know, Alyosha, I’ve been thinking all this time you were angry with me, because of the day before yesterday, because of that young lady. I was a cur, that’s the truth.... But it’s a good thing it happened so. It was a horrid thing, but a good thing too.” Grushenka smiled dreamily and a little cruel line showed in her smile. “Mitya told me that she screamed out that I ‘ought to be flogged.’ I did insult her dreadfully. She sent for me, she wanted to make a conquest of me, to win me over with her chocolate.... No, it’s a good thing it did end like that.” She smiled again. “But I am still afraid of your being angry.”\n\n“Yes, that’s really true,” Rakitin put in suddenly with genuine surprise. “Alyosha, she is really afraid of a chicken like you.”\n\n“He is a chicken to you, Rakitin ... because you’ve no conscience, that’s what it is! You see, I love him with all my soul, that’s how it is! Alyosha, do you believe I love you with all my soul?”\n\n“Ah, you shameless woman! She is making you a declaration, Alexey!”\n\n“Well, what of it, I love him!”\n\n“And what about your officer? And the priceless message from Mokroe?”\n\n“That is quite different.”\n\n“That’s a woman’s way of looking at it!”\n\n“Don’t you make me angry, Rakitin.” Grushenka caught him up hotly. “This is quite different. I love Alyosha in a different way. It’s true, Alyosha, I had sly designs on you before. For I am a horrid, violent creature. But at other times I’ve looked upon you, Alyosha, as my conscience. I’ve kept thinking ‘how any one like that must despise a nasty thing like me.’ I thought that the day before yesterday, as I ran home from the young lady’s. I have thought of you a long time in that way, Alyosha, and Mitya knows, I’ve talked to him about it. Mitya understands. Would you believe it, I sometimes look at you and feel ashamed, utterly ashamed of myself.... And how, and since when, I began to think about you like that, I can’t say, I don’t remember....”\n\nFenya came in and put a tray with an uncorked bottle and three glasses of champagne on the table.\n\n“Here’s the champagne!” cried Rakitin. “You’re excited, Agrafena Alexandrovna, and not yourself. When you’ve had a glass of champagne, you’ll be ready to dance. Eh, they can’t even do that properly,” he added, looking at the bottle. “The old woman’s poured it out in the kitchen and the bottle’s been brought in warm and without a cork. Well, let me have some, anyway.”\n\nHe went up to the table, took a glass, emptied it at one gulp and poured himself out another.\n\n“One doesn’t often stumble upon champagne,” he said, licking his lips. “Now, Alyosha, take a glass, show what you can do! What shall we drink to? The gates of paradise? Take a glass, Grushenka, you drink to the gates of paradise, too.”\n\n“What gates of paradise?”\n\nShe took a glass, Alyosha took his, tasted it and put it back.\n\n“No, I’d better not,” he smiled gently.\n\n“And you bragged!” cried Rakitin.\n\n“Well, if so, I won’t either,” chimed in Grushenka, “I really don’t want any. You can drink the whole bottle alone, Rakitin. If Alyosha has some, I will.”\n\n“What touching sentimentality!” said Rakitin tauntingly; “and she’s sitting on his knee, too! He’s got something to grieve over, but what’s the matter with you? He is rebelling against his God and ready to eat sausage....”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“His elder died to-day, Father Zossima, the saint.”\n\n“So Father Zossima is dead,” cried Grushenka. “Good God, I did not know!” She crossed herself devoutly. “Goodness, what have I been doing, sitting on his knee like this at such a moment!” She started up as though in dismay, instantly slipped off his knee and sat down on the sofa.\n\nAlyosha bent a long wondering look upon her and a light seemed to dawn in his face.\n\n“Rakitin,” he said suddenly, in a firm and loud voice; “don’t taunt me with having rebelled against God. I don’t want to feel angry with you, so you must be kinder, too, I’ve lost a treasure such as you have never had, and you cannot judge me now. You had much better look at her—do you see how she has pity on me? I came here to find a wicked soul—I felt drawn to evil because I was base and evil myself, and I’ve found a true sister, I have found a treasure—a loving heart. She had pity on me just now.... Agrafena Alexandrovna, I am speaking of you. You’ve raised my soul from the depths.”\n\nAlyosha’s lips were quivering and he caught his breath.\n\n“She has saved you, it seems,” laughed Rakitin spitefully. “And she meant to get you in her clutches, do you realize that?”\n\n“Stay, Rakitin.” Grushenka jumped up. “Hush, both of you. Now I’ll tell you all about it. Hush, Alyosha, your words make me ashamed, for I am bad and not good—that’s what I am. And you hush, Rakitin, because you are telling lies. I had the low idea of trying to get him in my clutches, but now you are lying, now it’s all different. And don’t let me hear anything more from you, Rakitin.”\n\nAll this Grushenka said with extreme emotion.\n\n“They are both crazy,” said Rakitin, looking at them with amazement. “I feel as though I were in a madhouse. They’re both getting so feeble they’ll begin crying in a minute.”\n\n“I shall begin to cry, I shall,” repeated Grushenka. “He called me his sister and I shall never forget that. Only let me tell you, Rakitin, though I am bad, I did give away an onion.”\n\n“An onion? Hang it all, you really are crazy.”\n\nRakitin wondered at their enthusiasm. He was aggrieved and annoyed, though he might have reflected that each of them was just passing through a spiritual crisis such as does not come often in a lifetime. But though Rakitin was very sensitive about everything that concerned himself, he was very obtuse as regards the feelings and sensations of others—partly from his youth and inexperience, partly from his intense egoism.\n\n“You see, Alyosha,” Grushenka turned to him with a nervous laugh. “I was boasting when I told Rakitin I had given away an onion, but it’s not to boast I tell you about it. It’s only a story, but it’s a nice story. I used to hear it when I was a child from Matryona, my cook, who is still with me. It’s like this. Once upon a time there was a peasant woman and a very wicked woman she was. And she died and did not leave a single good deed behind. The devils caught her and plunged her into the lake of fire. So her guardian angel stood and wondered what good deed of hers he could remember to tell to God; ‘She once pulled up an onion in her garden,’ said he, ‘and gave it to a beggar woman.’ And God answered: ‘You take that onion then, hold it out to her in the lake, and let her take hold and be pulled out. And if you can pull her out of the lake, let her come to Paradise, but if the onion breaks, then the woman must stay where she is.’ The angel ran to the woman and held out the onion to her. ‘Come,’ said he, ‘catch hold and I’ll pull you out.’ And he began cautiously pulling her out. He had just pulled her right out, when the other sinners in the lake, seeing how she was being drawn out, began catching hold of her so as to be pulled out with her. But she was a very wicked woman and she began kicking them. ‘I’m to be pulled out, not you. It’s my onion, not yours.’ As soon as she said that, the onion broke. And the woman fell into the lake and she is burning there to this day. So the angel wept and went away. So that’s the story, Alyosha; I know it by heart, for I am that wicked woman myself. I boasted to Rakitin that I had given away an onion, but to you I’ll say: ‘I’ve done nothing but give away one onion all my life, that’s the only good deed I’ve done.’ So don’t praise me, Alyosha, don’t think me good, I am bad, I am a wicked woman and you make me ashamed if you praise me. Eh, I must confess everything. Listen, Alyosha. I was so anxious to get hold of you that I promised Rakitin twenty-five roubles if he would bring you to me. Stay, Rakitin, wait!”\n\nShe went with rapid steps to the table, opened a drawer, pulled out a purse and took from it a twenty-five rouble note.\n\n“What nonsense! What nonsense!” cried Rakitin, disconcerted.\n\n“Take it. Rakitin, I owe it you, there’s no fear of your refusing it, you asked for it yourself.” And she threw the note to him.\n\n“Likely I should refuse it,” boomed Rakitin, obviously abashed, but carrying off his confusion with a swagger. “That will come in very handy; fools are made for wise men’s profit.”\n\n“And now hold your tongue, Rakitin, what I am going to say now is not for your ears. Sit down in that corner and keep quiet. You don’t like us, so hold your tongue.”\n\n“What should I like you for?” Rakitin snarled, not concealing his ill-humor. He put the twenty-five rouble note in his pocket and he felt ashamed at Alyosha’s seeing it. He had reckoned on receiving his payment later, without Alyosha’s knowing of it, and now, feeling ashamed, he lost his temper. Till that moment he had thought it discreet not to contradict Grushenka too flatly in spite of her snubbing, since he had something to get out of her. But now he, too, was angry:\n\n“One loves people for some reason, but what have either of you done for me?”\n\n“You should love people without a reason, as Alyosha does.”\n\n“How does he love you? How has he shown it, that you make such a fuss about it?”\n\nGrushenka was standing in the middle of the room; she spoke with heat and there were hysterical notes in her voice.\n\n“Hush, Rakitin, you know nothing about us! And don’t dare to speak to me like that again. How dare you be so familiar! Sit in that corner and be quiet, as though you were my footman! And now, Alyosha, I’ll tell you the whole truth, that you may see what a wretch I am! I am not talking to Rakitin, but to you. I wanted to ruin you, Alyosha, that’s the holy truth; I quite meant to. I wanted to so much, that I bribed Rakitin to bring you. And why did I want to do such a thing? You knew nothing about it, Alyosha, you turned away from me; if you passed me, you dropped your eyes. And I’ve looked at you a hundred times before to-day; I began asking every one about you. Your face haunted my heart. ‘He despises me,’ I thought; ‘he won’t even look at me.’ And I felt it so much at last that I wondered at myself for being so frightened of a boy. I’ll get him in my clutches and laugh at him. I was full of spite and anger. Would you believe it, nobody here dares talk or think of coming to Agrafena Alexandrovna with any evil purpose. Old Kuzma is the only man I have anything to do with here; I was bound and sold to him; Satan brought us together, but there has been no one else. But looking at you, I thought, I’ll get him in my clutches and laugh at him. You see what a spiteful cur I am, and you called me your sister! And now that man who wronged me has come; I sit here waiting for a message from him. And do you know what that man has been to me? Five years ago, when Kuzma brought me here, I used to shut myself up, that no one might have sight or sound of me. I was a silly slip of a girl; I used to sit here sobbing; I used to lie awake all night, thinking: ‘Where is he now, the man who wronged me? He is laughing at me with another woman, most likely. If only I could see him, if I could meet him again, I’d pay him out, I’d pay him out!’ At night I used to lie sobbing into my pillow in the dark, and I used to brood over it; I used to tear my heart on purpose and gloat over my anger. ‘I’ll pay him out, I’ll pay him out!’ That’s what I used to cry out in the dark. And when I suddenly thought that I should really do nothing to him, and that he was laughing at me then, or perhaps had utterly forgotten me, I would fling myself on the floor, melt into helpless tears, and lie there shaking till dawn. In the morning I would get up more spiteful than a dog, ready to tear the whole world to pieces. And then what do you think? I began saving money, I became hard-hearted, grew stout—grew wiser, would you say? No, no one in the whole world sees it, no one knows it, but when night comes on, I sometimes lie as I did five years ago, when I was a silly girl, clenching my teeth and crying all night, thinking, ‘I’ll pay him out, I’ll pay him out!’ Do you hear? Well then, now you understand me. A month ago a letter came to me—he was coming, he was a widower, he wanted to see me. It took my breath away; then I suddenly thought: ‘If he comes and whistles to call me, I shall creep back to him like a beaten dog.’ I couldn’t believe myself. Am I so abject? Shall I run to him or not? And I’ve been in such a rage with myself all this month that I am worse than I was five years ago. Do you see now, Alyosha, what a violent, vindictive creature I am? I have shown you the whole truth! I played with Mitya to keep me from running to that other. Hush, Rakitin, it’s not for you to judge me, I am not speaking to you. Before you came in, I was lying here waiting, brooding, deciding my whole future life, and you can never know what was in my heart. Yes, Alyosha, tell your young lady not to be angry with me for what happened the day before yesterday.... Nobody in the whole world knows what I am going through now, and no one ever can know.... For perhaps I shall take a knife with me to-day, I can’t make up my mind ...”\n\nAnd at this “tragic” phrase Grushenka broke down, hid her face in her hands, flung herself on the sofa pillows, and sobbed like a little child.\n\nAlyosha got up and went to Rakitin.\n\n“Misha,” he said, “don’t be angry. She wounded you, but don’t be angry. You heard what she said just now? You mustn’t ask too much of human endurance, one must be merciful.”\n\nAlyosha said this at the instinctive prompting of his heart. He felt obliged to speak and he turned to Rakitin. If Rakitin had not been there, he would have spoken to the air. But Rakitin looked at him ironically and Alyosha stopped short.\n\n“You were so primed up with your elder’s teaching last night that now you have to let it off on me, Alexey, man of God!” said Rakitin, with a smile of hatred.\n\n“Don’t laugh, Rakitin, don’t smile, don’t talk of the dead—he was better than any one in the world!” cried Alyosha, with tears in his voice. “I didn’t speak to you as a judge but as the lowest of the judged. What am I beside her? I came here seeking my ruin, and said to myself, ‘What does it matter?’ in my cowardliness, but she, after five years in torment, as soon as any one says a word from the heart to her—it makes her forget everything, forgive everything, in her tears! The man who has wronged her has come back, he sends for her and she forgives him everything, and hastens joyfully to meet him and she won’t take a knife with her. She won’t! No, I am not like that. I don’t know whether you are, Misha, but I am not like that. It’s a lesson to me.... She is more loving than we.... Have you heard her speak before of what she has just told us? No, you haven’t; if you had, you’d have understood her long ago ... and the person insulted the day before yesterday must forgive her, too! She will, when she knows ... and she shall know.... This soul is not yet at peace with itself, one must be tender with it ... there may be a treasure in that soul....”\n\nAlyosha stopped, because he caught his breath. In spite of his ill-humor Rakitin looked at him with astonishment. He had never expected such a tirade from the gentle Alyosha.\n\n“She’s found some one to plead her cause! Why, are you in love with her? Agrafena Alexandrovna, our monk’s really in love with you, you’ve made a conquest!” he cried, with a coarse laugh.\n\nGrushenka lifted her head from the pillow and looked at Alyosha with a tender smile shining on her tear-stained face.\n\n“Let him alone, Alyosha, my cherub; you see what he is, he is not a person for you to speak to. Mihail Osipovitch,” she turned to Rakitin, “I meant to beg your pardon for being rude to you, but now I don’t want to. Alyosha, come to me, sit down here.” She beckoned to him with a happy smile. “That’s right, sit here. Tell me,” she shook him by the hand and peeped into his face, smiling, “tell me, do I love that man or not? the man who wronged me, do I love him or not? Before you came, I lay here in the dark, asking my heart whether I loved him. Decide for me, Alyosha, the time has come, it shall be as you say. Am I to forgive him or not?”\n\n“But you have forgiven him already,” said Alyosha, smiling.\n\n“Yes, I really have forgiven him,” Grushenka murmured thoughtfully. “What an abject heart! To my abject heart!” She snatched up a glass from the table, emptied it at a gulp, lifted it in the air and flung it on the floor. The glass broke with a crash. A little cruel line came into her smile.\n\n“Perhaps I haven’t forgiven him, though,” she said, with a sort of menace in her voice, and she dropped her eyes to the ground as though she were talking to herself. “Perhaps my heart is only getting ready to forgive. I shall struggle with my heart. You see, Alyosha, I’ve grown to love my tears in these five years.... Perhaps I only love my resentment, not him ...”\n\n“Well, I shouldn’t care to be in his shoes,” hissed Rakitin.\n\n“Well, you won’t be, Rakitin, you’ll never be in his shoes. You shall black my shoes, Rakitin, that’s the place you are fit for. You’ll never get a woman like me ... and he won’t either, perhaps ...”\n\n“Won’t he? Then why are you dressed up like that?” said Rakitin, with a venomous sneer.\n\n“Don’t taunt me with dressing up, Rakitin, you don’t know all that is in my heart! If I choose to tear off my finery, I’ll tear it off at once, this minute,” she cried in a resonant voice. “You don’t know what that finery is for, Rakitin! Perhaps I shall see him and say: ‘Have you ever seen me look like this before?’ He left me a thin, consumptive cry-baby of seventeen. I’ll sit by him, fascinate him and work him up. ‘Do you see what I am like now?’ I’ll say to him; ‘well, and that’s enough for you, my dear sir, there’s many a slip twixt the cup and the lip!’ That may be what the finery is for, Rakitin.” Grushenka finished with a malicious laugh. “I’m violent and resentful, Alyosha, I’ll tear off my finery, I’ll destroy my beauty, I’ll scorch my face, slash it with a knife, and turn beggar. If I choose, I won’t go anywhere now to see any one. If I choose, I’ll send Kuzma back all he has ever given me, to-morrow, and all his money and I’ll go out charing for the rest of my life. You think I wouldn’t do it, Rakitin, that I would not dare to do it? I would, I would, I could do it directly, only don’t exasperate me ... and I’ll send him about his business, I’ll snap my fingers in his face, he shall never see me again!”\n\nShe uttered the last words in an hysterical scream, but broke down again, hid her face in her hands, buried it in the pillow and shook with sobs.\n\nRakitin got up.\n\n“It’s time we were off,” he said, “it’s late, we shall be shut out of the monastery.”\n\nGrushenka leapt up from her place.\n\n“Surely you don’t want to go, Alyosha!” she cried, in mournful surprise. “What are you doing to me? You’ve stirred up my feeling, tortured me, and now you’ll leave me to face this night alone!”\n\n“He can hardly spend the night with you! Though if he wants to, let him! I’ll go alone,” Rakitin scoffed jeeringly.\n\n“Hush, evil tongue!” Grushenka cried angrily at him; “you never said such words to me as he has come to say.”\n\n“What has he said to you so special?” asked Rakitin irritably.\n\n“I can’t say, I don’t know. I don’t know what he said to me, it went straight to my heart; he has wrung my heart.... He is the first, the only one who has pitied me, that’s what it is. Why did you not come before, you angel?” She fell on her knees before him as though in a sudden frenzy. “I’ve been waiting all my life for some one like you, I knew that some one like you would come and forgive me. I believed that, nasty as I am, some one would really love me, not only with a shameful love!”\n\n“What have I done to you?” answered Alyosha, bending over her with a tender smile, and gently taking her by the hands; “I only gave you an onion, nothing but a tiny little onion, that was all!”\n\nHe was moved to tears himself as he said it. At that moment there was a sudden noise in the passage, some one came into the hall. Grushenka jumped up, seeming greatly alarmed. Fenya ran noisily into the room, crying out:\n\n“Mistress, mistress darling, a messenger has galloped up,” she cried, breathless and joyful. “A carriage from Mokroe for you, Timofey the driver, with three horses, they are just putting in fresh horses.... A letter, here’s the letter, mistress.”\n\nA letter was in her hand and she waved it in the air all the while she talked. Grushenka snatched the letter from her and carried it to the candle. It was only a note, a few lines. She read it in one instant.\n\n“He has sent for me,” she cried, her face white and distorted, with a wan smile; “he whistles! Crawl back, little dog!”\n\nBut only for one instant she stood as though hesitating; suddenly the blood rushed to her head and sent a glow to her cheeks.\n\n“I will go,” she cried; “five years of my life! Good-by! Good-by, Alyosha, my fate is sealed. Go, go, leave me all of you, don’t let me see you again! Grushenka is flying to a new life.... Don’t you remember evil against me either, Rakitin. I may be going to my death! Ugh! I feel as though I were drunk!”\n\nShe suddenly left them and ran into her bedroom.\n\n“Well, she has no thoughts for us now!” grumbled Rakitin. “Let’s go, or we may hear that feminine shriek again. I am sick of all these tears and cries.”\n\nAlyosha mechanically let himself be led out. In the yard stood a covered cart. Horses were being taken out of the shafts, men were running to and fro with a lantern. Three fresh horses were being led in at the open gate. But when Alyosha and Rakitin reached the bottom of the steps, Grushenka’s bedroom window was suddenly opened and she called in a ringing voice after Alyosha:\n\n“Alyosha, give my greetings to your brother Mitya and tell him not to remember evil against me, though I have brought him misery. And tell him, too, in my words: ‘Grushenka has fallen to a scoundrel, and not to you, noble heart.’ And add, too, that Grushenka loved him only one hour, only one short hour she loved him—so let him remember that hour all his life—say, ‘Grushenka tells you to!’”\n\nShe ended in a voice full of sobs. The window was shut with a slam.\n\n“H’m, h’m!” growled Rakitin, laughing, “she murders your brother Mitya and then tells him to remember it all his life! What ferocity!”\n\nAlyosha made no reply, he seemed not to have heard. He walked fast beside Rakitin as though in a terrible hurry. He was lost in thought and moved mechanically. Rakitin felt a sudden twinge as though he had been touched on an open wound. He had expected something quite different by bringing Grushenka and Alyosha together. Something very different from what he had hoped for had happened.\n\n“He is a Pole, that officer of hers,” he began again, restraining himself; “and indeed he is not an officer at all now. He served in the customs in Siberia, somewhere on the Chinese frontier, some puny little beggar of a Pole, I expect. Lost his job, they say. He’s heard now that Grushenka’s saved a little money, so he’s turned up again—that’s the explanation of the mystery.”\n\nAgain Alyosha seemed not to hear. Rakitin could not control himself.\n\n“Well, so you’ve saved the sinner?” he laughed spitefully. “Have you turned the Magdalene into the true path? Driven out the seven devils, eh? So you see the miracles you were looking out for just now have come to pass!”\n\n“Hush, Rakitin,” Alyosha answered with an aching heart.\n\n“So you despise me now for those twenty-five roubles? I’ve sold my friend, you think. But you are not Christ, you know, and I am not Judas.”\n\n“Oh, Rakitin, I assure you I’d forgotten about it,” cried Alyosha, “you remind me of it yourself....”\n\nBut this was the last straw for Rakitin.\n\n“Damnation take you all and each of you!” he cried suddenly, “why the devil did I take you up? I don’t want to know you from this time forward. Go alone, there’s your road!”\n\nAnd he turned abruptly into another street, leaving Alyosha alone in the dark. Alyosha came out of the town and walked across the fields to the monastery.\n\n# Chapter IV. Cana Of Galilee\n\nIt was very late, according to the monastery ideas, when Alyosha returned to the hermitage; the door-keeper let him in by a special entrance. It had struck nine o’clock—the hour of rest and repose after a day of such agitation for all. Alyosha timidly opened the door and went into the elder’s cell where his coffin was now standing. There was no one in the cell but Father Païssy, reading the Gospel in solitude over the coffin, and the young novice Porfiry, who, exhausted by the previous night’s conversation and the disturbing incidents of the day, was sleeping the deep sound sleep of youth on the floor of the other room. Though Father Païssy heard Alyosha come in, he did not even look in his direction. Alyosha turned to the right from the door to the corner, fell on his knees and began to pray.\n\nHis soul was overflowing but with mingled feelings; no single sensation stood out distinctly; on the contrary, one drove out another in a slow, continual rotation. But there was a sweetness in his heart and, strange to say, Alyosha was not surprised at it. Again he saw that coffin before him, the hidden dead figure so precious to him, but the weeping and poignant grief of the morning was no longer aching in his soul. As soon as he came in, he fell down before the coffin as before a holy shrine, but joy, joy was glowing in his mind and in his heart. The one window of the cell was open, the air was fresh and cool. “So the smell must have become stronger, if they opened the window,” thought Alyosha. But even this thought of the smell of corruption, which had seemed to him so awful and humiliating a few hours before, no longer made him feel miserable or indignant. He began quietly praying, but he soon felt that he was praying almost mechanically. Fragments of thought floated through his soul, flashed like stars and went out again at once, to be succeeded by others. But yet there was reigning in his soul a sense of the wholeness of things—something steadfast and comforting—and he was aware of it himself. Sometimes he began praying ardently, he longed to pour out his thankfulness and love....\n\nBut when he had begun to pray, he passed suddenly to something else, and sank into thought, forgetting both the prayer and what had interrupted it. He began listening to what Father Païssy was reading, but worn out with exhaustion he gradually began to doze.\n\n“And the third day there was a marriage in Cana of Galilee;” read Father Païssy. “And the mother of Jesus was there; And both Jesus was called, and his disciples, to the marriage.”\n\n“Marriage? What’s that?... A marriage!” floated whirling through Alyosha’s mind. “There is happiness for her, too.... She has gone to the feast.... No, she has not taken the knife.... That was only a tragic phrase.... Well ... tragic phrases should be forgiven, they must be. Tragic phrases comfort the heart.... Without them, sorrow would be too heavy for men to bear. Rakitin has gone off to the back alley. As long as Rakitin broods over his wrongs, he will always go off to the back alley.... But the high road ... The road is wide and straight and bright as crystal, and the sun is at the end of it.... Ah!... What’s being read?”...\n\n“And when they wanted wine, the mother of Jesus saith unto him, They have no wine” ... Alyosha heard.\n\n“Ah, yes, I was missing that, and I didn’t want to miss it, I love that passage: it’s Cana of Galilee, the first miracle.... Ah, that miracle! Ah, that sweet miracle! It was not men’s grief, but their joy Christ visited, He worked His first miracle to help men’s gladness.... ‘He who loves men loves their gladness, too’ ... He was always repeating that, it was one of his leading ideas.... ‘There’s no living without joy,’ Mitya says.... Yes, Mitya.... ‘Everything that is true and good is always full of forgiveness,’ he used to say that, too” ...\n\n“Jesus saith unto her, Woman, what has it to do with thee or me? Mine hour is not yet come.\n\n“His mother saith unto the servants, Whatsoever he saith unto you, do it” ...\n\n“Do it.... Gladness, the gladness of some poor, very poor, people.... Of course they were poor, since they hadn’t wine enough even at a wedding.... The historians write that, in those days, the people living about the Lake of Gennesaret were the poorest that can possibly be imagined ... and another great heart, that other great being, His Mother, knew that He had come not only to make His great terrible sacrifice. She knew that His heart was open even to the simple, artless merrymaking of some obscure and unlearned people, who had warmly bidden Him to their poor wedding. ‘Mine hour is not yet come,’ He said, with a soft smile (He must have smiled gently to her). And, indeed, was it to make wine abundant at poor weddings He had come down to earth? And yet He went and did as she asked Him.... Ah, he is reading again”....\n\n“Jesus saith unto them, Fill the waterpots with water. And they filled them up to the brim.\n\n“And he saith unto them, Draw out now and bear unto the governor of the feast. And they bare it.\n\n“When the ruler of the feast had tasted the water that was made wine, and knew not whence it was; (but the servants which drew the water knew;) the governor of the feast called the bridegroom,\n\n“And saith unto him, Every man at the beginning doth set forth good wine; and when men have well drunk, that which is worse; but thou hast kept the good wine until now.”\n\n“But what’s this, what’s this? Why is the room growing wider?... Ah, yes ... It’s the marriage, the wedding ... yes, of course. Here are the guests, here are the young couple sitting, and the merry crowd and ... Where is the wise governor of the feast? But who is this? Who? Again the walls are receding.... Who is getting up there from the great table? What!... He here, too? But he’s in the coffin ... but he’s here, too. He has stood up, he sees me, he is coming here.... God!”...\n\nYes, he came up to him, to him, he, the little, thin old man, with tiny wrinkles on his face, joyful and laughing softly. There was no coffin now, and he was in the same dress as he had worn yesterday sitting with them, when the visitors had gathered about him. His face was uncovered, his eyes were shining. How was this, then? He, too, had been called to the feast. He, too, at the marriage of Cana in Galilee....\n\n“Yes, my dear, I am called, too, called and bidden,” he heard a soft voice saying over him. “Why have you hidden yourself here, out of sight? You come and join us too.”\n\nIt was his voice, the voice of Father Zossima. And it must be he, since he called him!\n\nThe elder raised Alyosha by the hand and he rose from his knees.\n\n“We are rejoicing,” the little, thin old man went on. “We are drinking the new wine, the wine of new, great gladness; do you see how many guests? Here are the bride and bridegroom, here is the wise governor of the feast, he is tasting the new wine. Why do you wonder at me? I gave an onion to a beggar, so I, too, am here. And many here have given only an onion each—only one little onion.... What are all our deeds? And you, my gentle one, you, my kind boy, you too have known how to give a famished woman an onion to-day. Begin your work, dear one, begin it, gentle one!... Do you see our Sun, do you see Him?”\n\n“I am afraid ... I dare not look,” whispered Alyosha.\n\n“Do not fear Him. He is terrible in His greatness, awful in His sublimity, but infinitely merciful. He has made Himself like unto us from love and rejoices with us. He is changing the water into wine that the gladness of the guests may not be cut short. He is expecting new guests, He is calling new ones unceasingly for ever and ever.... There they are bringing new wine. Do you see they are bringing the vessels....”\n\nSomething glowed in Alyosha’s heart, something filled it till it ached, tears of rapture rose from his soul.... He stretched out his hands, uttered a cry and waked up.\n\nAgain the coffin, the open window, and the soft, solemn, distinct reading of the Gospel. But Alyosha did not listen to the reading. It was strange, he had fallen asleep on his knees, but now he was on his feet, and suddenly, as though thrown forward, with three firm rapid steps he went right up to the coffin. His shoulder brushed against Father Païssy without his noticing it. Father Païssy raised his eyes for an instant from his book, but looked away again at once, seeing that something strange was happening to the boy. Alyosha gazed for half a minute at the coffin, at the covered, motionless dead man that lay in the coffin, with the ikon on his breast and the peaked cap with the octangular cross, on his head. He had only just been hearing his voice, and that voice was still ringing in his ears. He was listening, still expecting other words, but suddenly he turned sharply and went out of the cell.\n\nHe did not stop on the steps either, but went quickly down; his soul, overflowing with rapture, yearned for freedom, space, openness. The vault of heaven, full of soft, shining stars, stretched vast and fathomless above him. The Milky Way ran in two pale streams from the zenith to the horizon. The fresh, motionless, still night enfolded the earth. The white towers and golden domes of the cathedral gleamed out against the sapphire sky. The gorgeous autumn flowers, in the beds round the house, were slumbering till morning. The silence of earth seemed to melt into the silence of the heavens. The mystery of earth was one with the mystery of the stars....\n\nAlyosha stood, gazed, and suddenly threw himself down on the earth. He did not know why he embraced it. He could not have told why he longed so irresistibly to kiss it, to kiss it all. But he kissed it weeping, sobbing and watering it with his tears, and vowed passionately to love it, to love it for ever and ever. “Water the earth with the tears of your joy and love those tears,” echoed in his soul.\n\nWhat was he weeping over?\n\nOh! in his rapture he was weeping even over those stars, which were shining to him from the abyss of space, and “he was not ashamed of that ecstasy.” There seemed to be threads from all those innumerable worlds of God, linking his soul to them, and it was trembling all over “in contact with other worlds.” He longed to forgive every one and for everything, and to beg forgiveness. Oh, not for himself, but for all men, for all and for everything. “And others are praying for me too,” echoed again in his soul. But with every instant he felt clearly and, as it were, tangibly, that something firm and unshakable as that vault of heaven had entered into his soul. It was as though some idea had seized the sovereignty of his mind—and it was for all his life and for ever and ever. He had fallen on the earth a weak boy, but he rose up a resolute champion, and he knew and felt it suddenly at the very moment of his ecstasy. And never, never, all his life long, could Alyosha forget that minute.\n\n“Some one visited my soul in that hour,” he used to say afterwards, with implicit faith in his words.\n\nWithin three days he left the monastery in accordance with the words of his elder, who had bidden him “sojourn in the world.”\n\n# Book VIII. Mitya Chapter I. Kuzma Samsonov\n\nBut Dmitri, to whom Grushenka, flying away to a new life, had left her last greetings, bidding him remember the hour of her love for ever, knew nothing of what had happened to her, and was at that moment in a condition of feverish agitation and activity. For the last two days he had been in such an inconceivable state of mind that he might easily have fallen ill with brain fever, as he said himself afterwards. Alyosha had not been able to find him the morning before, and Ivan had not succeeded in meeting him at the tavern on the same day. The people at his lodgings, by his orders, concealed his movements.\n\nHe had spent those two days literally rushing in all directions, “struggling with his destiny and trying to save himself,” as he expressed it himself afterwards, and for some hours he even made a dash out of the town on urgent business, terrible as it was to him to lose sight of Grushenka for a moment. All this was explained afterwards in detail, and confirmed by documentary evidence; but for the present we will only note the most essential incidents of those two terrible days immediately preceding the awful catastrophe, that broke so suddenly upon him.\n\nThough Grushenka had, it is true, loved him for an hour, genuinely and sincerely, yet she tortured him sometimes cruelly and mercilessly. The worst of it was that he could never tell what she meant to do. To prevail upon her by force or kindness was also impossible: she would yield to nothing. She would only have become angry and turned away from him altogether, he knew that well already. He suspected, quite correctly, that she, too, was passing through an inward struggle, and was in a state of extraordinary indecision, that she was making up her mind to something, and unable to determine upon it. And so, not without good reason, he divined, with a sinking heart, that at moments she must simply hate him and his passion. And so, perhaps, it was, but what was distressing Grushenka he did not understand. For him the whole tormenting question lay between him and Fyodor Pavlovitch.\n\nHere, we must note, by the way, one certain fact: he was firmly persuaded that Fyodor Pavlovitch would offer, or perhaps had offered, Grushenka lawful wedlock, and did not for a moment believe that the old voluptuary hoped to gain his object for three thousand roubles. Mitya had reached this conclusion from his knowledge of Grushenka and her character. That was how it was that he could believe at times that all Grushenka’s uneasiness rose from not knowing which of them to choose, which was most to her advantage.\n\nStrange to say, during those days it never occurred to him to think of the approaching return of the “officer,” that is, of the man who had been such a fatal influence in Grushenka’s life, and whose arrival she was expecting with such emotion and dread. It is true that of late Grushenka had been very silent about it. Yet he was perfectly aware of a letter she had received a month ago from her seducer, and had heard of it from her own lips. He partly knew, too, what the letter contained. In a moment of spite Grushenka had shown him that letter, but to her astonishment he attached hardly any consequence to it. It would be hard to say why this was. Perhaps, weighed down by all the hideous horror of his struggle with his own father for this woman, he was incapable of imagining any danger more terrible, at any rate for the time. He simply did not believe in a suitor who suddenly turned up again after five years’ disappearance, still less in his speedy arrival. Moreover, in the “officer’s” first letter which had been shown to Mitya, the possibility of his new rival’s visit was very vaguely suggested. The letter was very indefinite, high-flown, and full of sentimentality. It must be noted that Grushenka had concealed from him the last lines of the letter, in which his return was alluded to more definitely. He had, besides, noticed at that moment, he remembered afterwards, a certain involuntary proud contempt for this missive from Siberia on Grushenka’s face. Grushenka told him nothing of what had passed later between her and this rival; so that by degrees he had completely forgotten the officer’s existence.\n\nHe felt that whatever might come later, whatever turn things might take, his final conflict with Fyodor Pavlovitch was close upon him, and must be decided before anything else. With a sinking heart he was expecting every moment Grushenka’s decision, always believing that it would come suddenly, on the impulse of the moment. All of a sudden she would say to him: “Take me, I’m yours for ever,” and it would all be over. He would seize her and bear her away at once to the ends of the earth. Oh, then he would bear her away at once, as far, far away as possible; to the farthest end of Russia, if not of the earth, then he would marry her, and settle down with her incognito, so that no one would know anything about them, there, here, or anywhere. Then, oh, then, a new life would begin at once!\n\nOf this different, reformed and “virtuous” life (“it must, it must be virtuous”) he dreamed feverishly at every moment. He thirsted for that reformation and renewal. The filthy morass, in which he had sunk of his own free will, was too revolting to him, and, like very many men in such cases, he put faith above all in change of place. If only it were not for these people, if only it were not for these circumstances, if only he could fly away from this accursed place—he would be altogether regenerated, would enter on a new path. That was what he believed in, and what he was yearning for.\n\nBut all this could only be on condition of the first, the happy solution of the question. There was another possibility, a different and awful ending. Suddenly she might say to him: “Go away. I have just come to terms with Fyodor Pavlovitch. I am going to marry him and don’t want you”—and then ... but then.... But Mitya did not know what would happen then. Up to the last hour he didn’t know. That must be said to his credit. He had no definite intentions, had planned no crime. He was simply watching and spying in agony, while he prepared himself for the first, happy solution of his destiny. He drove away any other idea, in fact. But for that ending a quite different anxiety arose, a new, incidental, but yet fatal and insoluble difficulty presented itself.\n\nIf she were to say to him: “I’m yours; take me away,” how could he take her away? Where had he the means, the money to do it? It was just at this time that all sources of revenue from Fyodor Pavlovitch, doles which had gone on without interruption for so many years, ceased. Grushenka had money, of course, but with regard to this Mitya suddenly evinced extraordinary pride; he wanted to carry her away and begin the new life with her himself, at his own expense, not at hers. He could not conceive of taking her money, and the very idea caused him a pang of intense repulsion. I won’t enlarge on this fact or analyze it here, but confine myself to remarking that this was his attitude at the moment. All this may have arisen indirectly and unconsciously from the secret stings of his conscience for the money of Katerina Ivanovna that he had dishonestly appropriated. “I’ve been a scoundrel to one of them, and I shall be a scoundrel again to the other directly,” was his feeling then, as he explained after: “and when Grushenka knows, she won’t care for such a scoundrel.”\n\nWhere then was he to get the means, where was he to get the fateful money? Without it, all would be lost and nothing could be done, “and only because I hadn’t the money. Oh, the shame of it!”\n\nTo anticipate things: he did, perhaps, know where to get the money, knew, perhaps, where it lay at that moment. I will say no more of this here, as it will all be clear later. But his chief trouble, I must explain however obscurely, lay in the fact that to have that sum he knew of, to have the right to take it, he must first restore Katerina Ivanovna’s three thousand—if not, “I’m a common pickpocket, I’m a scoundrel, and I don’t want to begin a new life as a scoundrel,” Mitya decided. And so he made up his mind to move heaven and earth to return Katerina Ivanovna that three thousand, and that first of all. The final stage of this decision, so to say, had been reached only during the last hours, that is, after his last interview with Alyosha, two days before, on the high-road, on the evening when Grushenka had insulted Katerina Ivanovna, and Mitya, after hearing Alyosha’s account of it, had admitted that he was a scoundrel, and told him to tell Katerina Ivanovna so, if it could be any comfort to her. After parting from his brother on that night, he had felt in his frenzy that it would be better “to murder and rob some one than fail to pay my debt to Katya. I’d rather every one thought me a robber and a murderer, I’d rather go to Siberia than that Katya should have the right to say that I deceived her and stole her money, and used her money to run away with Grushenka and begin a new life! That I can’t do!” So Mitya decided, grinding his teeth, and he might well fancy at times that his brain would give way. But meanwhile he went on struggling....\n\nStrange to say, though one would have supposed there was nothing left for him but despair—for what chance had he, with nothing in the world, to raise such a sum?—yet to the very end he persisted in hoping that he would get that three thousand, that the money would somehow come to him of itself, as though it might drop from heaven. That is just how it is with people who, like Dmitri, have never had anything to do with money, except to squander what has come to them by inheritance without any effort of their own, and have no notion how money is obtained. A whirl of the most fantastic notions took possession of his brain immediately after he had parted with Alyosha two days before, and threw his thoughts into a tangle of confusion. This is how it was he pitched first on a perfectly wild enterprise. And perhaps to men of that kind in such circumstances the most impossible, fantastic schemes occur first, and seem most practical.\n\nHe suddenly determined to go to Samsonov, the merchant who was Grushenka’s protector, and to propose a “scheme” to him, and by means of it to obtain from him at once the whole of the sum required. Of the commercial value of his scheme he had no doubt, not the slightest, and was only uncertain how Samsonov would look upon his freak, supposing he were to consider it from any but the commercial point of view. Though Mitya knew the merchant by sight, he was not acquainted with him and had never spoken a word to him. But for some unknown reason he had long entertained the conviction that the old reprobate, who was lying at death’s door, would perhaps not at all object now to Grushenka’s securing a respectable position, and marrying a man “to be depended upon.” And he believed not only that he would not object, but that this was what he desired, and, if opportunity arose, that he would be ready to help. From some rumor, or perhaps from some stray word of Grushenka’s, he had gathered further that the old man would perhaps prefer him to Fyodor Pavlovitch for Grushenka.\n\nPossibly many of the readers of my novel will feel that in reckoning on such assistance, and being ready to take his bride, so to speak, from the hands of her protector, Dmitri showed great coarseness and want of delicacy. I will only observe that Mitya looked upon Grushenka’s past as something completely over. He looked on that past with infinite pity and resolved with all the fervor of his passion that when once Grushenka told him she loved him and would marry him, it would mean the beginning of a new Grushenka and a new Dmitri, free from every vice. They would forgive one another and would begin their lives afresh. As for Kuzma Samsonov, Dmitri looked upon him as a man who had exercised a fateful influence in that remote past of Grushenka’s, though she had never loved him, and who was now himself a thing of the past, completely done with, and, so to say, non-existent. Besides, Mitya hardly looked upon him as a man at all, for it was known to every one in the town that he was only a shattered wreck, whose relations with Grushenka had changed their character and were now simply paternal, and that this had been so for a long time.\n\nIn any case there was much simplicity on Mitya’s part in all this, for in spite of all his vices, he was a very simple-hearted man. It was an instance of this simplicity that Mitya was seriously persuaded that, being on the eve of his departure for the next world, old Kuzma must sincerely repent of his past relations with Grushenka, and that she had no more devoted friend and protector in the world than this now harmless old man.\n\nAfter his conversation with Alyosha, at the cross-roads, he hardly slept all night, and at ten o’clock next morning, he was at the house of Samsonov and telling the servant to announce him. It was a very large and gloomy old house of two stories, with a lodge and outhouses. In the lower story lived Samsonov’s two married sons with their families, his old sister, and his unmarried daughter. In the lodge lived two of his clerks, one of whom also had a large family. Both the lodge and the lower story were overcrowded, but the old man kept the upper floor to himself, and would not even let the daughter live there with him, though she waited upon him, and in spite of her asthma was obliged at certain fixed hours, and at any time he might call her, to run upstairs to him from below.\n\nThis upper floor contained a number of large rooms kept purely for show, furnished in the old-fashioned merchant style, with long monotonous rows of clumsy mahogany chairs along the walls, with glass chandeliers under shades, and gloomy mirrors on the walls. All these rooms were entirely empty and unused, for the old man kept to one room, a small, remote bedroom, where he was waited upon by an old servant with a kerchief on her head, and by a lad, who used to sit on the locker in the passage. Owing to his swollen legs, the old man could hardly walk at all, and was only rarely lifted from his leather arm-chair, when the old woman supporting him led him up and down the room once or twice. He was morose and taciturn even with this old woman.\n\nWhen he was informed of the arrival of the “captain,” he at once refused to see him. But Mitya persisted and sent his name up again. Samsonov questioned the lad minutely: What he looked like? Whether he was drunk? Was he going to make a row? The answer he received was: that he was sober, but wouldn’t go away. The old man again refused to see him. Then Mitya, who had foreseen this, and purposely brought pencil and paper with him, wrote clearly on the piece of paper the words: “On most important business closely concerning Agrafena Alexandrovna,” and sent it up to the old man.\n\nAfter thinking a little Samsonov told the lad to take the visitor to the drawing-room, and sent the old woman downstairs with a summons to his younger son to come upstairs to him at once. This younger son, a man over six foot and of exceptional physical strength, who was closely-shaven and dressed in the European style, though his father still wore a kaftan and a beard, came at once without a comment. All the family trembled before the father. The old man had sent for this giant, not because he was afraid of the “captain” (he was by no means of a timorous temper), but in order to have a witness in case of any emergency. Supported by his son and the servant-lad, he waddled at last into the drawing-room. It may be assumed that he felt considerable curiosity. The drawing-room in which Mitya was awaiting him was a vast, dreary room that laid a weight of depression on the heart. It had a double row of windows, a gallery, marbled walls, and three immense chandeliers with glass lusters covered with shades.\n\nMitya was sitting on a little chair at the entrance, awaiting his fate with nervous impatience. When the old man appeared at the opposite door, seventy feet away, Mitya jumped up at once, and with his long, military stride walked to meet him. Mitya was well dressed, in a frock-coat, buttoned up, with a round hat and black gloves in his hands, just as he had been three days before at the elder’s, at the family meeting with his father and brothers. The old man waited for him, standing dignified and unbending, and Mitya felt at once that he had looked him through and through as he advanced. Mitya was greatly impressed, too, with Samsonov’s immensely swollen face. His lower lip, which had always been thick, hung down now, looking like a bun. He bowed to his guest in dignified silence, motioned him to a low chair by the sofa, and, leaning on his son’s arm he began lowering himself on to the sofa opposite, groaning painfully, so that Mitya, seeing his painful exertions, immediately felt remorseful and sensitively conscious of his insignificance in the presence of the dignified person he had ventured to disturb.\n\n“What is it you want of me, sir?” said the old man, deliberately, distinctly, severely, but courteously, when he was at last seated.\n\nMitya started, leapt up, but sat down again. Then he began at once speaking with loud, nervous haste, gesticulating, and in a positive frenzy. He was unmistakably a man driven into a corner, on the brink of ruin, catching at the last straw, ready to sink if he failed. Old Samsonov probably grasped all this in an instant, though his face remained cold and immovable as a statue’s.\n\n“Most honored sir, Kuzma Kuzmitch, you have no doubt heard more than once of my disputes with my father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, who robbed me of my inheritance from my mother ... seeing the whole town is gossiping about it ... for here every one’s gossiping of what they shouldn’t ... and besides, it might have reached you through Grushenka ... I beg your pardon, through Agrafena Alexandrovna ... Agrafena Alexandrovna, the lady for whom I have the highest respect and esteem ...”\n\nSo Mitya began, and broke down at the first sentence. We will not reproduce his speech word for word, but will only summarize the gist of it. Three months ago, he said, he had of express intention (Mitya purposely used these words instead of “intentionally”) consulted a lawyer in the chief town of the province, “a distinguished lawyer, Kuzma Kuzmitch, Pavel Pavlovitch Korneplodov. You have perhaps heard of him? A man of vast intellect, the mind of a statesman ... he knows you, too ... spoke of you in the highest terms ...” Mitya broke down again. But these breaks did not deter him. He leapt instantly over the gaps, and struggled on and on.\n\nThis Korneplodov, after questioning him minutely, and inspecting the documents he was able to bring him (Mitya alluded somewhat vaguely to these documents, and slurred over the subject with special haste), reported that they certainly might take proceedings concerning the village of Tchermashnya, which ought, he said, to have come to him, Mitya, from his mother, and so checkmate the old villain, his father ... “because every door was not closed and justice might still find a loophole.” In fact, he might reckon on an additional sum of six or even seven thousand roubles from Fyodor Pavlovitch, as Tchermashnya was worth, at least, twenty-five thousand, he might say twenty-eight thousand, in fact, “thirty, thirty, Kuzma Kuzmitch, and would you believe it, I didn’t get seventeen from that heartless man!” So he, Mitya, had thrown the business up, for the time, knowing nothing about the law, but on coming here was struck dumb by a cross-claim made upon him (here Mitya went adrift again and again took a flying leap forward), “so will not you, excellent and honored Kuzma Kuzmitch, be willing to take up all my claims against that unnatural monster, and pay me a sum down of only three thousand?... You see, you cannot, in any case, lose over it. On my honor, my honor, I swear that. Quite the contrary, you may make six or seven thousand instead of three.” Above all, he wanted this concluded that very day.\n\n“I’ll do the business with you at a notary’s, or whatever it is ... in fact, I’m ready to do anything.... I’ll hand over all the deeds ... whatever you want, sign anything ... and we could draw up the agreement at once ... and if it were possible, if it were only possible, that very morning.... You could pay me that three thousand, for there isn’t a capitalist in this town to compare with you, and so would save me from ... would save me, in fact ... for a good, I might say an honorable action.... For I cherish the most honorable feelings for a certain person, whom you know well, and care for as a father. I would not have come, indeed, if it had not been as a father. And, indeed, it’s a struggle of three in this business, for it’s fate—that’s a fearful thing, Kuzma Kuzmitch! A tragedy, Kuzma Kuzmitch, a tragedy! And as you’ve dropped out long ago, it’s a tug-of-war between two. I’m expressing it awkwardly, perhaps, but I’m not a literary man. You see, I’m on the one side, and that monster on the other. So you must choose. It’s either I or the monster. It all lies in your hands—the fate of three lives, and the happiness of two.... Excuse me, I’m making a mess of it, but you understand ... I see from your venerable eyes that you understand ... and if you don’t understand, I’m done for ... so you see!”\n\nMitya broke off his clumsy speech with that, “so you see!” and jumping up from his seat, awaited the answer to his foolish proposal. At the last phrase he had suddenly become hopelessly aware that it had all fallen flat, above all, that he had been talking utter nonsense.\n\n“How strange it is! On the way here it seemed all right, and now it’s nothing but nonsense.” The idea suddenly dawned on his despairing mind. All the while he had been talking, the old man sat motionless, watching him with an icy expression in his eyes. After keeping him for a moment in suspense, Kuzma Kuzmitch pronounced at last in the most positive and chilling tone:\n\n“Excuse me, we don’t undertake such business.”\n\nMitya suddenly felt his legs growing weak under him.\n\n“What am I to do now, Kuzma Kuzmitch?” he muttered, with a pale smile. “I suppose it’s all up with me—what do you think?”\n\n“Excuse me....”\n\nMitya remained standing, staring motionless. He suddenly noticed a movement in the old man’s face. He started.\n\n“You see, sir, business of that sort’s not in our line,” said the old man slowly. “There’s the court, and the lawyers—it’s a perfect misery. But if you like, there is a man here you might apply to.”\n\n“Good heavens! Who is it? You’re my salvation, Kuzma Kuzmitch,” faltered Mitya.\n\n“He doesn’t live here, and he’s not here just now. He is a peasant, he does business in timber. His name is Lyagavy. He’s been haggling with Fyodor Pavlovitch for the last year, over your copse at Tchermashnya. They can’t agree on the price, maybe you’ve heard? Now he’s come back again and is staying with the priest at Ilyinskoe, about twelve versts from the Volovya station. He wrote to me, too, about the business of the copse, asking my advice. Fyodor Pavlovitch means to go and see him himself. So if you were to be beforehand with Fyodor Pavlovitch and to make Lyagavy the offer you’ve made me, he might possibly—”\n\n“A brilliant idea!” Mitya interrupted ecstatically. “He’s the very man, it would just suit him. He’s haggling with him for it, being asked too much, and here he would have all the documents entitling him to the property itself. Ha ha ha!”\n\nAnd Mitya suddenly went off into his short, wooden laugh, startling Samsonov.\n\n“How can I thank you, Kuzma Kuzmitch?” cried Mitya effusively.\n\n“Don’t mention it,” said Samsonov, inclining his head.\n\n“But you don’t know, you’ve saved me. Oh, it was a true presentiment brought me to you.... So now to this priest!”\n\n“No need of thanks.”\n\n“I’ll make haste and fly there. I’m afraid I’ve overtaxed your strength. I shall never forget it. It’s a Russian says that, Kuzma Kuzmitch, a R-r-russian!”\n\n“To be sure!”\n\nMitya seized his hand to press it, but there was a malignant gleam in the old man’s eye. Mitya drew back his hand, but at once blamed himself for his mistrustfulness.\n\n“It’s because he’s tired,” he thought.\n\n“For her sake! For her sake, Kuzma Kuzmitch! You understand that it’s for her,” he cried, his voice ringing through the room. He bowed, turned sharply round, and with the same long stride walked to the door without looking back. He was trembling with delight.\n\n“Everything was on the verge of ruin and my guardian angel saved me,” was the thought in his mind. And if such a business man as Samsonov (a most worthy old man, and what dignity!) had suggested this course, then ... then success was assured. He would fly off immediately. “I will be back before night, I shall be back at night and the thing is done. Could the old man have been laughing at me?” exclaimed Mitya, as he strode towards his lodging. He could, of course, imagine nothing, but that the advice was practical “from such a business man” with an understanding of the business, with an understanding of this Lyagavy (curious surname!). Or—the old man was laughing at him.\n\nAlas! The second alternative was the correct one. Long afterwards, when the catastrophe had happened, old Samsonov himself confessed, laughing, that he had made a fool of the “captain.” He was a cold, spiteful and sarcastic man, liable to violent antipathies. Whether it was the “captain’s” excited face, or the foolish conviction of the “rake and spendthrift,” that he, Samsonov, could be taken in by such a cock-and-bull story as his scheme, or his jealousy of Grushenka, in whose name this “scapegrace” had rushed in on him with such a tale to get money which worked on the old man, I can’t tell. But at the instant when Mitya stood before him, feeling his legs grow weak under him, and frantically exclaiming that he was ruined, at that moment the old man looked at him with intense spite, and resolved to make a laughing-stock of him. When Mitya had gone, Kuzma Kuzmitch, white with rage, turned to his son and bade him see to it that that beggar be never seen again, and never admitted even into the yard, or else he’d—\n\nHe did not utter his threat. But even his son, who often saw him enraged, trembled with fear. For a whole hour afterwards, the old man was shaking with anger, and by evening he was worse, and sent for the doctor.\n\n# Chapter II. Lyagavy\n\nSo he must drive at full speed, and he had not the money for horses. He had forty kopecks, and that was all, all that was left after so many years of prosperity! But he had at home an old silver watch which had long ceased to go. He snatched it up and carried it to a Jewish watchmaker who had a shop in the market-place. The Jew gave him six roubles for it.\n\n“And I didn’t expect that,” cried Mitya, ecstatically. (He was still in a state of ecstasy.) He seized his six roubles and ran home. At home he borrowed three roubles from the people of the house, who loved him so much that they were pleased to give it him, though it was all they had. Mitya in his excitement told them on the spot that his fate would be decided that day, and he described, in desperate haste, the whole scheme he had put before Samsonov, the latter’s decision, his own hopes for the future, and so on. These people had been told many of their lodger’s secrets before, and so looked upon him as a gentleman who was not at all proud, and almost one of themselves. Having thus collected nine roubles Mitya sent for posting-horses to take him to the Volovya station. This was how the fact came to be remembered and established that “at midday, on the day before the event, Mitya had not a farthing, and that he had sold his watch to get money and had borrowed three roubles from his landlord, all in the presence of witnesses.”\n\nI note this fact, later on it will be apparent why I do so.\n\nThough he was radiant with the joyful anticipation that he would at last solve all his difficulties, yet, as he drew near Volovya station, he trembled at the thought of what Grushenka might be doing in his absence. What if she made up her mind to-day to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch? This was why he had gone off without telling her and why he left orders with his landlady not to let out where he had gone, if any one came to inquire for him.\n\n“I must, I must get back to-night,” he repeated, as he was jolted along in the cart, “and I dare say I shall have to bring this Lyagavy back here ... to draw up the deed.” So mused Mitya, with a throbbing heart, but alas! his dreams were not fated to be carried out.\n\nTo begin with, he was late, taking a short cut from Volovya station which turned out to be eighteen versts instead of twelve. Secondly, he did not find the priest at home at Ilyinskoe; he had gone off to a neighboring village. While Mitya, setting off there with the same exhausted horses, was looking for him, it was almost dark.\n\nThe priest, a shy and amiable looking little man, informed him at once that though Lyagavy had been staying with him at first, he was now at Suhoy Possyolok, that he was staying the night in the forester’s cottage, as he was buying timber there too. At Mitya’s urgent request that he would take him to Lyagavy at once, and by so doing “save him, so to speak,” the priest agreed, after some demur, to conduct him to Suhoy Possyolok; his curiosity was obviously aroused. But, unluckily, he advised their going on foot, as it would not be “much over” a verst. Mitya, of course, agreed, and marched off with his yard-long strides, so that the poor priest almost ran after him. He was a very cautious man, though not old.\n\nMitya at once began talking to him, too, of his plans, nervously and excitedly asking advice in regard to Lyagavy, and talking all the way. The priest listened attentively, but gave little advice. He turned off Mitya’s questions with: “I don’t know. Ah, I can’t say. How can I tell?” and so on. When Mitya began to speak of his quarrel with his father over his inheritance, the priest was positively alarmed, as he was in some way dependent on Fyodor Pavlovitch. He inquired, however, with surprise, why he called the peasant-trader Gorstkin, Lyagavy, and obligingly explained to Mitya that, though the man’s name really was Lyagavy, he was never called so, as he would be grievously offended at the name, and that he must be sure to call him Gorstkin, “or you’ll do nothing with him; he won’t even listen to you,” said the priest in conclusion.\n\nMitya was somewhat surprised for a moment, and explained that that was what Samsonov had called him. On hearing this fact, the priest dropped the subject, though he would have done well to put into words his doubt whether, if Samsonov had sent him to that peasant, calling him Lyagavy, there was not something wrong about it and he was turning him into ridicule. But Mitya had no time to pause over such trifles. He hurried, striding along, and only when he reached Suhoy Possyolok did he realize that they had come not one verst, nor one and a half, but at least three. This annoyed him, but he controlled himself.\n\nThey went into the hut. The forester lived in one half of the hut, and Gorstkin was lodging in the other, the better room the other side of the passage. They went into that room and lighted a tallow candle. The hut was extremely overheated. On the table there was a samovar that had gone out, a tray with cups, an empty rum bottle, a bottle of vodka partly full, and some half-eaten crusts of wheaten bread. The visitor himself lay stretched at full length on the bench, with his coat crushed up under his head for a pillow, snoring heavily. Mitya stood in perplexity.\n\n“Of course I must wake him. My business is too important. I’ve come in such haste. I’m in a hurry to get back to-day,” he said in great agitation. But the priest and the forester stood in silence, not giving their opinion. Mitya went up and began trying to wake him himself; he tried vigorously, but the sleeper did not wake.\n\n“He’s drunk,” Mitya decided. “Good Lord! What am I to do? What am I to do?” And, terribly impatient, he began pulling him by the arms, by the legs, shaking his head, lifting him up and making him sit on the bench. Yet, after prolonged exertions, he could only succeed in getting the drunken man to utter absurd grunts, and violent, but inarticulate oaths.\n\n“No, you’d better wait a little,” the priest pronounced at last, “for he’s obviously not in a fit state.”\n\n“He’s been drinking the whole day,” the forester chimed in.\n\n“Good heavens!” cried Mitya. “If only you knew how important it is to me and how desperate I am!”\n\n“No, you’d better wait till morning,” the priest repeated.\n\n“Till morning? Mercy! that’s impossible!”\n\nAnd in his despair he was on the point of attacking the sleeping man again, but stopped short at once, realizing the uselessness of his efforts. The priest said nothing, the sleepy forester looked gloomy.\n\n“What terrible tragedies real life contrives for people,” said Mitya, in complete despair. The perspiration was streaming down his face. The priest seized the moment to put before him, very reasonably, that, even if he succeeded in wakening the man, he would still be drunk and incapable of conversation. “And your business is important,” he said, “so you’d certainly better put it off till morning.” With a gesture of despair Mitya agreed.\n\n“Father, I will stay here with a light, and seize the favorable moment. As soon as he wakes I’ll begin. I’ll pay you for the light,” he said to the forester, “for the night’s lodging, too; you’ll remember Dmitri Karamazov. Only, Father, I don’t know what we’re to do with you. Where will you sleep?”\n\n“No, I’m going home. I’ll take his horse and get home,” he said, indicating the forester. “And now I’ll say good-by. I wish you all success.”\n\nSo it was settled. The priest rode off on the forester’s horse, delighted to escape, though he shook his head uneasily, wondering whether he ought not next day to inform his benefactor Fyodor Pavlovitch of this curious incident, “or he may in an unlucky hour hear of it, be angry, and withdraw his favor.”\n\nThe forester, scratching himself, went back to his room without a word, and Mitya sat on the bench to “catch the favorable moment,” as he expressed it. Profound dejection clung about his soul like a heavy mist. A profound, intense dejection! He sat thinking, but could reach no conclusion. The candle burnt dimly, a cricket chirped; it became insufferably close in the overheated room. He suddenly pictured the garden, the path behind the garden, the door of his father’s house mysteriously opening and Grushenka running in. He leapt up from the bench.\n\n“It’s a tragedy!” he said, grinding his teeth. Mechanically he went up to the sleeping man and looked in his face. He was a lean, middle-aged peasant, with a very long face, flaxen curls, and a long, thin, reddish beard, wearing a blue cotton shirt and a black waistcoat, from the pocket of which peeped the chain of a silver watch. Mitya looked at his face with intense hatred, and for some unknown reason his curly hair particularly irritated him.\n\nWhat was insufferably humiliating was, that after leaving things of such importance and making such sacrifices, he, Mitya, utterly worn out, should with business of such urgency be standing over this dolt on whom his whole fate depended, while he snored as though there were nothing the matter, as though he’d dropped from another planet.\n\n“Oh, the irony of fate!” cried Mitya, and, quite losing his head, he fell again to rousing the tipsy peasant. He roused him with a sort of ferocity, pulled at him, pushed him, even beat him; but after five minutes of vain exertions, he returned to his bench in helpless despair, and sat down.\n\n“Stupid! Stupid!” cried Mitya. “And how dishonorable it all is!” something made him add. His head began to ache horribly. “Should he fling it up and go away altogether?” he wondered. “No, wait till to-morrow now. I’ll stay on purpose. What else did I come for? Besides, I’ve no means of going. How am I to get away from here now? Oh, the idiocy of it!”\n\nBut his head ached more and more. He sat without moving, and unconsciously dozed off and fell asleep as he sat. He seemed to have slept for two hours or more. He was waked up by his head aching so unbearably that he could have screamed. There was a hammering in his temples, and the top of his head ached. It was a long time before he could wake up fully and understand what had happened to him.\n\nAt last he realized that the room was full of charcoal fumes from the stove, and that he might die of suffocation. And the drunken peasant still lay snoring. The candle guttered and was about to go out. Mitya cried out, and ran staggering across the passage into the forester’s room. The forester waked up at once, but hearing that the other room was full of fumes, to Mitya’s surprise and annoyance, accepted the fact with strange unconcern, though he did go to see to it.\n\n“But he’s dead, he’s dead! and ... what am I to do then?” cried Mitya frantically.\n\nThey threw open the doors, opened a window and the chimney. Mitya brought a pail of water from the passage. First he wetted his own head, then, finding a rag of some sort, dipped it into the water, and put it on Lyagavy’s head. The forester still treated the matter contemptuously, and when he opened the window said grumpily:\n\n“It’ll be all right, now.”\n\nHe went back to sleep, leaving Mitya a lighted lantern. Mitya fussed about the drunken peasant for half an hour, wetting his head, and gravely resolved not to sleep all night. But he was so worn out that when he sat down for a moment to take breath, he closed his eyes, unconsciously stretched himself full length on the bench and slept like the dead.\n\nIt was dreadfully late when he waked. It was somewhere about nine o’clock. The sun was shining brightly in the two little windows of the hut. The curly-headed peasant was sitting on the bench and had his coat on. He had another samovar and another bottle in front of him. Yesterday’s bottle had already been finished, and the new one was more than half empty. Mitya jumped up and saw at once that the cursed peasant was drunk again, hopelessly and incurably. He stared at him for a moment with wide opened eyes. The peasant was silently and slyly watching him, with insulting composure, and even a sort of contemptuous condescension, so Mitya fancied. He rushed up to him.\n\n“Excuse me, you see ... I ... you’ve most likely heard from the forester here in the hut. I’m Lieutenant Dmitri Karamazov, the son of the old Karamazov whose copse you are buying.”\n\n“That’s a lie!” said the peasant, calmly and confidently.\n\n“A lie? You know Fyodor Pavlovitch?”\n\n“I don’t know any of your Fyodor Pavlovitches,” said the peasant, speaking thickly.\n\n“You’re bargaining with him for the copse, for the copse. Do wake up, and collect yourself. Father Pavel of Ilyinskoe brought me here. You wrote to Samsonov, and he has sent me to you,” Mitya gasped breathlessly.\n\n“You’re l-lying!” Lyagavy blurted out again. Mitya’s legs went cold.\n\n“For mercy’s sake! It isn’t a joke! You’re drunk, perhaps. Yet you can speak and understand ... or else ... I understand nothing!”\n\n“You’re a painter!”\n\n“For mercy’s sake! I’m Karamazov, Dmitri Karamazov. I have an offer to make you, an advantageous offer ... very advantageous offer, concerning the copse!”\n\nThe peasant stroked his beard importantly.\n\n“No, you’ve contracted for the job and turned out a scamp. You’re a scoundrel!”\n\n“I assure you you’re mistaken,” cried Mitya, wringing his hands in despair. The peasant still stroked his beard, and suddenly screwed up his eyes cunningly.\n\n“No, you show me this: you tell me the law that allows roguery. D’you hear? You’re a scoundrel! Do you understand that?”\n\nMitya stepped back gloomily, and suddenly “something seemed to hit him on the head,” as he said afterwards. In an instant a light seemed to dawn in his mind, “a light was kindled and I grasped it all.” He stood, stupefied, wondering how he, after all a man of intelligence, could have yielded to such folly, have been led into such an adventure, and have kept it up for almost twenty-four hours, fussing round this Lyagavy, wetting his head.\n\n“Why, the man’s drunk, dead drunk, and he’ll go on drinking now for a week; what’s the use of waiting here? And what if Samsonov sent me here on purpose? What if she—? Oh, God, what have I done?”\n\nThe peasant sat watching him and grinning. Another time Mitya might have killed the fool in a fury, but now he felt as weak as a child. He went quietly to the bench, took up his overcoat, put it on without a word, and went out of the hut. He did not find the forester in the next room; there was no one there. He took fifty kopecks in small change out of his pocket and put them on the table for his night’s lodging, the candle, and the trouble he had given. Coming out of the hut he saw nothing but forest all round. He walked at hazard, not knowing which way to turn out of the hut, to the right or to the left. Hurrying there the evening before with the priest, he had not noticed the road. He had no revengeful feeling for anybody, even for Samsonov, in his heart. He strode along a narrow forest path, aimless, dazed, without heeding where he was going. A child could have knocked him down, so weak was he in body and soul. He got out of the forest somehow, however, and a vista of fields, bare after the harvest, stretched as far as the eye could see.\n\n“What despair! What death all round!” he repeated, striding on and on.\n\nHe was saved by meeting an old merchant who was being driven across country in a hired trap. When he overtook him, Mitya asked the way, and it turned out that the old merchant, too, was going to Volovya. After some discussion Mitya got into the trap. Three hours later they arrived. At Volovya, Mitya at once ordered posting-horses to drive to the town, and suddenly realized that he was appallingly hungry. While the horses were being harnessed, an omelette was prepared for him. He ate it all in an instant, ate a huge hunk of bread, ate a sausage, and swallowed three glasses of vodka. After eating, his spirits and his heart grew lighter. He flew towards the town, urged on the driver, and suddenly made a new and “unalterable” plan to procure that “accursed money” before evening. “And to think, only to think that a man’s life should be ruined for the sake of that paltry three thousand!” he cried, contemptuously. “I’ll settle it to-day.” And if it had not been for the thought of Grushenka and of what might have happened to her, which never left him, he would perhaps have become quite cheerful again.... But the thought of her was stabbing him to the heart every moment, like a sharp knife.\n\nAt last they arrived, and Mitya at once ran to Grushenka.\n\n# Chapter III. Gold-Mines\n\nThis was the visit of Mitya of which Grushenka had spoken to Rakitin with such horror. She was just then expecting the “message,” and was much relieved that Mitya had not been to see her that day or the day before. She hoped that “please God he won’t come till I’m gone away,” and he suddenly burst in on her. The rest we know already. To get him off her hands she suggested at once that he should walk with her to Samsonov’s, where she said she absolutely must go “to settle his accounts,” and when Mitya accompanied her at once, she said good-by to him at the gate, making him promise to come at twelve o’clock to take her home again. Mitya, too, was delighted at this arrangement. If she was sitting at Samsonov’s she could not be going to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, “if only she’s not lying,” he added at once. But he thought she was not lying from what he saw.\n\nHe was that sort of jealous man who, in the absence of the beloved woman, at once invents all sorts of awful fancies of what may be happening to her, and how she may be betraying him, but, when shaken, heartbroken, convinced of her faithlessness, he runs back to her; at the first glance at her face, her gay, laughing, affectionate face, he revives at once, lays aside all suspicion and with joyful shame abuses himself for his jealousy.\n\nAfter leaving Grushenka at the gate he rushed home. Oh, he had so much still to do that day! But a load had been lifted from his heart, anyway.\n\n“Now I must only make haste and find out from Smerdyakov whether anything happened there last night, whether, by any chance, she went to Fyodor Pavlovitch; ough!” floated through his mind.\n\nBefore he had time to reach his lodging, jealousy had surged up again in his restless heart.\n\nJealousy! “Othello was not jealous, he was trustful,” observed Pushkin. And that remark alone is enough to show the deep insight of our great poet. Othello’s soul was shattered and his whole outlook clouded simply because his ideal was destroyed. But Othello did not begin hiding, spying, peeping. He was trustful, on the contrary. He had to be led up, pushed on, excited with great difficulty before he could entertain the idea of deceit. The truly jealous man is not like that. It is impossible to picture to oneself the shame and moral degradation to which the jealous man can descend without a qualm of conscience. And yet it’s not as though the jealous were all vulgar and base souls. On the contrary, a man of lofty feelings, whose love is pure and full of self-sacrifice, may yet hide under tables, bribe the vilest people, and be familiar with the lowest ignominy of spying and eavesdropping.\n\nOthello was incapable of making up his mind to faithlessness—not incapable of forgiving it, but of making up his mind to it—though his soul was as innocent and free from malice as a babe’s. It is not so with the really jealous man. It is hard to imagine what some jealous men can make up their mind to and overlook, and what they can forgive! The jealous are the readiest of all to forgive, and all women know it. The jealous man can forgive extraordinarily quickly (though, of course, after a violent scene), and he is able to forgive infidelity almost conclusively proved, the very kisses and embraces he has seen, if only he can somehow be convinced that it has all been “for the last time,” and that his rival will vanish from that day forward, will depart to the ends of the earth, or that he himself will carry her away somewhere, where that dreaded rival will not get near her. Of course the reconciliation is only for an hour. For, even if the rival did disappear next day, he would invent another one and would be jealous of him. And one might wonder what there was in a love that had to be so watched over, what a love could be worth that needed such strenuous guarding. But that the jealous will never understand. And yet among them are men of noble hearts. It is remarkable, too, that those very men of noble hearts, standing hidden in some cupboard, listening and spying, never feel the stings of conscience at that moment, anyway, though they understand clearly enough with their “noble hearts” the shameful depths to which they have voluntarily sunk.\n\nAt the sight of Grushenka, Mitya’s jealousy vanished, and, for an instant he became trustful and generous, and positively despised himself for his evil feelings. But it only proved that, in his love for the woman, there was an element of something far higher than he himself imagined, that it was not only a sensual passion, not only the “curve of her body,” of which he had talked to Alyosha. But, as soon as Grushenka had gone, Mitya began to suspect her of all the low cunning of faithlessness, and he felt no sting of conscience at it.\n\nAnd so jealousy surged up in him again. He had, in any case, to make haste. The first thing to be done was to get hold of at least a small, temporary loan of money. The nine roubles had almost all gone on his expedition. And, as we all know, one can’t take a step without money. But he had thought over in the cart where he could get a loan. He had a brace of fine dueling pistols in a case, which he had not pawned till then because he prized them above all his possessions.\n\nIn the “Metropolis” tavern he had some time since made acquaintance with a young official and had learnt that this very opulent bachelor was passionately fond of weapons. He used to buy pistols, revolvers, daggers, hang them on his wall and show them to acquaintances. He prided himself on them, and was quite a specialist on the mechanism of the revolver. Mitya, without stopping to think, went straight to him, and offered to pawn his pistols to him for ten roubles. The official, delighted, began trying to persuade him to sell them outright. But Mitya would not consent, so the young man gave him ten roubles, protesting that nothing would induce him to take interest. They parted friends.\n\nMitya was in haste; he rushed towards Fyodor Pavlovitch’s by the back way, to his arbor, to get hold of Smerdyakov as soon as possible. In this way the fact was established that three or four hours before a certain event, of which I shall speak later on, Mitya had not a farthing, and pawned for ten roubles a possession he valued, though, three hours later, he was in possession of thousands.... But I am anticipating. From Marya Kondratyevna (the woman living near Fyodor Pavlovitch’s) he learned the very disturbing fact of Smerdyakov’s illness. He heard the story of his fall in the cellar, his fit, the doctor’s visit, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s anxiety; he heard with interest, too, that his brother Ivan had set off that morning for Moscow.\n\n“Then he must have driven through Volovya before me,” thought Dmitri, but he was terribly distressed about Smerdyakov. “What will happen now? Who’ll keep watch for me? Who’ll bring me word?” he thought. He began greedily questioning the women whether they had seen anything the evening before. They quite understood what he was trying to find out, and completely reassured him. No one had been there. Ivan Fyodorovitch had been there the night; everything had been perfectly as usual. Mitya grew thoughtful. He would certainly have to keep watch to-day, but where? Here or at Samsonov’s gate? He decided that he must be on the look out both here and there, and meanwhile ... meanwhile.... The difficulty was that he had to carry out the new plan that he had made on the journey back. He was sure of its success, but he must not delay acting upon it. Mitya resolved to sacrifice an hour to it: “In an hour I shall know everything, I shall settle everything, and then, then, first of all to Samsonov’s. I’ll inquire whether Grushenka’s there and instantly be back here again, stay till eleven, and then to Samsonov’s again to bring her home.” This was what he decided.\n\nHe flew home, washed, combed his hair, brushed his clothes, dressed, and went to Madame Hohlakov’s. Alas! he had built his hopes on her. He had resolved to borrow three thousand from that lady. And what was more, he felt suddenly convinced that she would not refuse to lend it to him. It may be wondered why, if he felt so certain, he had not gone to her at first, one of his own sort, so to speak, instead of to Samsonov, a man he did not know, who was not of his own class, and to whom he hardly knew how to speak.\n\nBut the fact was that he had never known Madame Hohlakov well, and had seen nothing of her for the last month, and that he knew she could not endure him. She had detested him from the first because he was engaged to Katerina Ivanovna, while she had, for some reason, suddenly conceived the desire that Katerina Ivanovna should throw him over, and marry the “charming, chivalrously refined Ivan, who had such excellent manners.” Mitya’s manners she detested. Mitya positively laughed at her, and had once said about her that she was just as lively and at her ease as she was uncultivated. But that morning in the cart a brilliant idea had struck him: “If she is so anxious I should not marry Katerina Ivanovna” (and he knew she was positively hysterical upon the subject) “why should she refuse me now that three thousand, just to enable me to leave Katya and get away from her for ever. These spoilt fine ladies, if they set their hearts on anything, will spare no expense to satisfy their caprice. Besides, she’s so rich,” Mitya argued.\n\nAs for his “plan” it was just the same as before; it consisted of the offer of his rights to Tchermashnya—but not with a commercial object, as it had been with Samsonov, not trying to allure the lady with the possibility of making a profit of six or seven thousand—but simply as a security for the debt. As he worked out this new idea, Mitya was enchanted with it, but so it always was with him in all his undertakings, in all his sudden decisions. He gave himself up to every new idea with passionate enthusiasm. Yet, when he mounted the steps of Madame Hohlakov’s house he felt a shiver of fear run down his spine. At that moment he saw fully, as a mathematical certainty, that this was his last hope, that if this broke down, nothing else was left him in the world, but to “rob and murder some one for the three thousand.” It was half-past seven when he rang at the bell.\n\nAt first fortune seemed to smile upon him. As soon as he was announced he was received with extraordinary rapidity. “As though she were waiting for me,” thought Mitya, and as soon as he had been led to the drawing-room, the lady of the house herself ran in, and declared at once that she was expecting him.\n\n“I was expecting you! I was expecting you! Though I’d no reason to suppose you would come to see me, as you will admit yourself. Yet, I did expect you. You may marvel at my instinct, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but I was convinced all the morning that you would come.”\n\n“That is certainly wonderful, madam,” observed Mitya, sitting down limply, “but I have come to you on a matter of great importance.... On a matter of supreme importance for me, that is, madam ... for me alone ... and I hasten—”\n\n“I know you’ve come on most important business, Dmitri Fyodorovitch; it’s not a case of presentiment, no reactionary harking back to the miraculous (have you heard about Father Zossima?). This is a case of mathematics: you couldn’t help coming, after all that has passed with Katerina Ivanovna; you couldn’t, you couldn’t, that’s a mathematical certainty.”\n\n“The realism of actual life, madam, that’s what it is. But allow me to explain—”\n\n“Realism indeed, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. I’m all for realism now. I’ve seen too much of miracles. You’ve heard that Father Zossima is dead?”\n\n“No, madam, it’s the first time I’ve heard of it.” Mitya was a little surprised. The image of Alyosha rose to his mind.\n\n“Last night, and only imagine—”\n\n“Madam,” said Mitya, “I can imagine nothing except that I’m in a desperate position, and that if you don’t help me, everything will come to grief, and I first of all. Excuse me for the triviality of the expression, but I’m in a fever—”\n\n“I know, I know that you’re in a fever. You could hardly fail to be, and whatever you may say to me, I know beforehand. I have long been thinking over your destiny, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I am watching over it and studying it.... Oh, believe me, I’m an experienced doctor of the soul, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“Madam, if you are an experienced doctor, I’m certainly an experienced patient,” said Mitya, with an effort to be polite, “and I feel that if you are watching over my destiny in this way, you will come to my help in my ruin, and so allow me, at least to explain to you the plan with which I have ventured to come to you ... and what I am hoping of you.... I have come, madam—”\n\n“Don’t explain it. It’s of secondary importance. But as for help, you’re not the first I have helped, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. You have most likely heard of my cousin, Madame Belmesov. Her husband was ruined, ‘had come to grief,’ as you characteristically express it, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. I recommended him to take to horse-breeding, and now he’s doing well. Have you any idea of horse-breeding, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”\n\n“Not the faintest, madam; ah, madam, not the faintest!” cried Mitya, in nervous impatience, positively starting from his seat. “I simply implore you, madam, to listen to me. Only give me two minutes of free speech that I may just explain to you everything, the whole plan with which I have come. Besides, I am short of time. I’m in a fearful hurry,” Mitya cried hysterically, feeling that she was just going to begin talking again, and hoping to cut her short. “I have come in despair ... in the last gasp of despair, to beg you to lend me the sum of three thousand, a loan, but on safe, most safe security, madam, with the most trustworthy guarantees! Only let me explain—”\n\n“You must tell me all that afterwards, afterwards!” Madame Hohlakov with a gesture demanded silence in her turn, “and whatever you may tell me, I know it all beforehand; I’ve told you so already. You ask for a certain sum, for three thousand, but I can give you more, immeasurably more, I will save you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but you must listen to me.”\n\nMitya started from his seat again.\n\n“Madam, will you really be so good!” he cried, with strong feeling. “Good God, you’ve saved me! You have saved a man from a violent death, from a bullet.... My eternal gratitude—”\n\n“I will give you more, infinitely more than three thousand!” cried Madame Hohlakov, looking with a radiant smile at Mitya’s ecstasy.\n\n“Infinitely? But I don’t need so much. I only need that fatal three thousand, and on my part I can give security for that sum with infinite gratitude, and I propose a plan which—”\n\n“Enough, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, it’s said and done.” Madame Hohlakov cut him short, with the modest triumph of beneficence: “I have promised to save you, and I will save you. I will save you as I did Belmesov. What do you think of the gold-mines, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”\n\n“Of the gold-mines, madam? I have never thought anything about them.”\n\n“But I have thought of them for you. Thought of them over and over again. I have been watching you for the last month. I’ve watched you a hundred times as you’ve walked past, saying to myself: that’s a man of energy who ought to be at the gold-mines. I’ve studied your gait and come to the conclusion: that’s a man who would find gold.”\n\n“From my gait, madam?” said Mitya, smiling.\n\n“Yes, from your gait. You surely don’t deny that character can be told from the gait, Dmitri Fyodorovitch? Science supports the idea. I’m all for science and realism now. After all this business with Father Zossima, which has so upset me, from this very day I’m a realist and I want to devote myself to practical usefulness. I’m cured. ‘Enough!’ as Turgenev says.”\n\n“But, madam, the three thousand you so generously promised to lend me—”\n\n“It is yours, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” Madame Hohlakov cut in at once. “The money is as good as in your pocket, not three thousand, but three million, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, in less than no time. I’ll make you a present of the idea: you shall find gold-mines, make millions, return and become a leading man, and wake us up and lead us to better things. Are we to leave it all to the Jews? You will found institutions and enterprises of all sorts. You will help the poor, and they will bless you. This is the age of railways, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. You’ll become famous and indispensable to the Department of Finance, which is so badly off at present. The depreciation of the rouble keeps me awake at night, Dmitri Fyodorovitch; people don’t know that side of me—”\n\n“Madam, madam!” Dmitri interrupted with an uneasy presentiment. “I shall indeed, perhaps, follow your advice, your wise advice, madam.... I shall perhaps set off ... to the gold-mines.... I’ll come and see you again about it ... many times, indeed ... but now, that three thousand you so generously ... oh, that would set me free, and if you could to-day ... you see, I haven’t a minute, a minute to lose to-day—”\n\n“Enough, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, enough!” Madame Hohlakov interrupted emphatically. “The question is, will you go to the gold-mines or not; have you quite made up your mind? Answer yes or no.”\n\n“I will go, madam, afterwards.... I’ll go where you like ... but now—”\n\n“Wait!” cried Madame Hohlakov. And jumping up and running to a handsome bureau with numerous little drawers, she began pulling out one drawer after another, looking for something with desperate haste.\n\n“The three thousand,” thought Mitya, his heart almost stopping, “and at the instant ... without any papers or formalities ... that’s doing things in gentlemanly style! She’s a splendid woman, if only she didn’t talk so much!”\n\n“Here!” cried Madame Hohlakov, running back joyfully to Mitya, “here is what I was looking for!”\n\nIt was a tiny silver ikon on a cord, such as is sometimes worn next the skin with a cross.\n\n“This is from Kiev, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” she went on reverently, “from the relics of the Holy Martyr, Varvara. Let me put it on your neck myself, and with it dedicate you to a new life, to a new career.”\n\nAnd she actually put the cord round his neck, and began arranging it. In extreme embarrassment, Mitya bent down and helped her, and at last he got it under his neck-tie and collar through his shirt to his chest.\n\n“Now you can set off,” Madame Hohlakov pronounced, sitting down triumphantly in her place again.\n\n“Madam, I am so touched. I don’t know how to thank you, indeed ... for such kindness, but ... If only you knew how precious time is to me.... That sum of money, for which I shall be indebted to your generosity.... Oh, madam, since you are so kind, so touchingly generous to me,” Mitya exclaimed impulsively, “then let me reveal to you ... though, of course, you’ve known it a long time ... that I love somebody here.... I have been false to Katya ... Katerina Ivanovna I should say.... Oh, I’ve behaved inhumanly, dishonorably to her, but I fell in love here with another woman ... a woman whom you, madam, perhaps, despise, for you know everything already, but whom I cannot leave on any account, and therefore that three thousand now—”\n\n“Leave everything, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” Madame Hohlakov interrupted in the most decisive tone. “Leave everything, especially women. Gold-mines are your goal, and there’s no place for women there. Afterwards, when you come back rich and famous, you will find the girl of your heart in the highest society. That will be a modern girl, a girl of education and advanced ideas. By that time the dawning woman question will have gained ground, and the new woman will have appeared.”\n\n“Madam, that’s not the point, not at all....” Mitya clasped his hands in entreaty.\n\n“Yes, it is, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, just what you need; the very thing you’re yearning for, though you don’t realize it yourself. I am not at all opposed to the present woman movement, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. The development of woman, and even the political emancipation of woman in the near future—that’s my ideal. I’ve a daughter myself, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, people don’t know that side of me. I wrote a letter to the author, Shtchedrin, on that subject. He has taught me so much, so much about the vocation of woman. So last year I sent him an anonymous letter of two lines: ‘I kiss and embrace you, my teacher, for the modern woman. Persevere.’ And I signed myself, ‘A Mother.’ I thought of signing myself ‘A contemporary Mother,’ and hesitated, but I stuck to the simple ‘Mother’; there’s more moral beauty in that, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. And the word ‘contemporary’ might have reminded him of ‘The Contemporary’—a painful recollection owing to the censorship.... Good Heavens, what is the matter!”\n\n“Madam!” cried Mitya, jumping up at last, clasping his hands before her in helpless entreaty. “You will make me weep if you delay what you have so generously—”\n\n“Oh, do weep, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, do weep! That’s a noble feeling ... such a path lies open before you! Tears will ease your heart, and later on you will return rejoicing. You will hasten to me from Siberia on purpose to share your joy with me—”\n\n“But allow me, too!” Mitya cried suddenly. “For the last time I entreat you, tell me, can I have the sum you promised me to-day, if not, when may I come for it?”\n\n“What sum, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”\n\n“The three thousand you promised me ... that you so generously—”\n\n“Three thousand? Roubles? Oh, no, I haven’t got three thousand,” Madame Hohlakov announced with serene amazement. Mitya was stupefied.\n\n“Why, you said just now ... you said ... you said it was as good as in my hands—”\n\n“Oh, no, you misunderstood me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. In that case you misunderstood me. I was talking of the gold-mines. It’s true I promised you more, infinitely more than three thousand, I remember it all now, but I was referring to the gold-mines.”\n\n“But the money? The three thousand?” Mitya exclaimed, awkwardly.\n\n“Oh, if you meant money, I haven’t any. I haven’t a penny, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. I’m quarreling with my steward about it, and I’ve just borrowed five hundred roubles from Miüsov, myself. No, no, I’ve no money. And, do you know, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, if I had, I wouldn’t give it to you. In the first place I never lend money. Lending money means losing friends. And I wouldn’t give it to you particularly. I wouldn’t give it you, because I like you and want to save you, for all you need is the gold-mines, the gold-mines, the gold-mines!”\n\n“Oh, the devil!” roared Mitya, and with all his might brought his fist down on the table.\n\n“Aie! Aie!” cried Madame Hohlakov, alarmed, and she flew to the other end of the drawing-room.\n\nMitya spat on the ground, and strode rapidly out of the room, out of the house, into the street, into the darkness! He walked like one possessed, and beating himself on the breast, on the spot where he had struck himself two days previously, before Alyosha, the last time he saw him in the dark, on the road. What those blows upon his breast signified, on that spot, and what he meant by it—that was, for the time, a secret which was known to no one in the world, and had not been told even to Alyosha. But that secret meant for him more than disgrace; it meant ruin, suicide. So he had determined, if he did not get hold of the three thousand that would pay his debt to Katerina Ivanovna, and so remove from his breast, from that spot on his breast, the shame he carried upon it, that weighed on his conscience. All this will be fully explained to the reader later on, but now that his last hope had vanished, this man, so strong in appearance, burst out crying like a little child a few steps from the Hohlakovs’ house. He walked on, and not knowing what he was doing, wiped away his tears with his fist. In this way he reached the square, and suddenly became aware that he had stumbled against something. He heard a piercing wail from an old woman whom he had almost knocked down.\n\n“Good Lord, you’ve nearly killed me! Why don’t you look where you’re going, scapegrace?”\n\n“Why, it’s you!” cried Mitya, recognizing the old woman in the dark. It was the old servant who waited on Samsonov, whom Mitya had particularly noticed the day before.\n\n“And who are you, my good sir?” said the old woman, in quite a different voice. “I don’t know you in the dark.”\n\n“You live at Kuzma Kuzmitch’s. You’re the servant there?”\n\n“Just so, sir, I was only running out to Prohoritch’s.... But I don’t know you now.”\n\n“Tell me, my good woman, is Agrafena Alexandrovna there now?” said Mitya, beside himself with suspense. “I saw her to the house some time ago.”\n\n“She has been there, sir. She stayed a little while, and went off again.”\n\n“What? Went away?” cried Mitya. “When did she go?”\n\n“Why, as soon as she came. She only stayed a minute. She only told Kuzma Kuzmitch a tale that made him laugh, and then she ran away.”\n\n“You’re lying, damn you!” roared Mitya.\n\n“Aie! Aie!” shrieked the old woman, but Mitya had vanished.\n\nHe ran with all his might to the house where Grushenka lived. At the moment he reached it, Grushenka was on her way to Mokroe. It was not more than a quarter of an hour after her departure.\n\nFenya was sitting with her grandmother, the old cook, Matryona, in the kitchen when “the captain” ran in. Fenya uttered a piercing shriek on seeing him.\n\n“You scream?” roared Mitya, “where is she?”\n\nBut without giving the terror-stricken Fenya time to utter a word, he fell all of a heap at her feet.\n\n“Fenya, for Christ’s sake, tell me, where is she?”\n\n“I don’t know. Dmitri Fyodorovitch, my dear, I don’t know. You may kill me but I can’t tell you.” Fenya swore and protested. “You went out with her yourself not long ago—”\n\n“She came back!”\n\n“Indeed she didn’t. By God I swear she didn’t come back.”\n\n“You’re lying!” shouted Mitya. “From your terror I know where she is.”\n\nHe rushed away. Fenya in her fright was glad she had got off so easily. But she knew very well that it was only that he was in such haste, or she might not have fared so well. But as he ran, he surprised both Fenya and old Matryona by an unexpected action. On the table stood a brass mortar, with a pestle in it, a small brass pestle, not much more than six inches long. Mitya already had opened the door with one hand when, with the other, he snatched up the pestle, and thrust it in his side-pocket.\n\n“Oh, Lord! He’s going to murder some one!” cried Fenya, flinging up her hands.\n\n# Chapter IV. In The Dark\n\nWhere was he running? “Where could she be except at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s? She must have run straight to him from Samsonov’s, that was clear now. The whole intrigue, the whole deceit was evident.” ... It all rushed whirling through his mind. He did not run to Marya Kondratyevna’s. “There was no need to go there ... not the slightest need ... he must raise no alarm ... they would run and tell directly.... Marya Kondratyevna was clearly in the plot, Smerdyakov too, he too, all had been bought over!”\n\nHe formed another plan of action: he ran a long way round Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house, crossing the lane, running down Dmitrovsky Street, then over the little bridge, and so came straight to the deserted alley at the back, which was empty and uninhabited, with, on one side the hurdle fence of a neighbor’s kitchen-garden, on the other the strong high fence, that ran all round Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden. Here he chose a spot, apparently the very place, where according to the tradition, he knew Lizaveta had once climbed over it: “If she could climb over it,” the thought, God knows why, occurred to him, “surely I can.” He did in fact jump up, and instantly contrived to catch hold of the top of the fence. Then he vigorously pulled himself up and sat astride on it. Close by, in the garden stood the bath-house, but from the fence he could see the lighted windows of the house too.\n\n“Yes, the old man’s bedroom is lighted up. She’s there!” and he leapt from the fence into the garden. Though he knew Grigory was ill and very likely Smerdyakov, too, and that there was no one to hear him, he instinctively hid himself, stood still, and began to listen. But there was dead silence on all sides and, as though of design, complete stillness, not the slightest breath of wind.\n\n“And naught but the whispering silence,” the line for some reason rose to his mind. “If only no one heard me jump over the fence! I think not.” Standing still for a minute, he walked softly over the grass in the garden, avoiding the trees and shrubs. He walked slowly, creeping stealthily at every step, listening to his own footsteps. It took him five minutes to reach the lighted window. He remembered that just under the window there were several thick and high bushes of elder and whitebeam. The door from the house into the garden on the left-hand side, was shut; he had carefully looked on purpose to see, in passing. At last he reached the bushes and hid behind them. He held his breath. “I must wait now,” he thought, “to reassure them, in case they heard my footsteps and are listening ... if only I don’t cough or sneeze.”\n\nHe waited two minutes. His heart was beating violently, and, at moments, he could scarcely breathe. “No, this throbbing at my heart won’t stop,” he thought. “I can’t wait any longer.” He was standing behind a bush in the shadow. The light of the window fell on the front part of the bush.\n\n“How red the whitebeam berries are!” he murmured, not knowing why. Softly and noiselessly, step by step, he approached the window, and raised himself on tiptoe. All Fyodor Pavlovitch’s bedroom lay open before him. It was not a large room, and was divided in two parts by a red screen, “Chinese,” as Fyodor Pavlovitch used to call it. The word “Chinese” flashed into Mitya’s mind, “and behind the screen, is Grushenka,” thought Mitya. He began watching Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was wearing his new striped-silk dressing-gown, which Mitya had never seen, and a silk cord with tassels round the waist. A clean, dandified shirt of fine linen with gold studs peeped out under the collar of the dressing-gown. On his head Fyodor Pavlovitch had the same red bandage which Alyosha had seen.\n\n“He has got himself up,” thought Mitya.\n\nHis father was standing near the window, apparently lost in thought. Suddenly he jerked up his head, listened a moment, and hearing nothing went up to the table, poured out half a glass of brandy from a decanter and drank it off. Then he uttered a deep sigh, again stood still a moment, walked carelessly up to the looking-glass on the wall, with his right hand raised the red bandage on his forehead a little, and began examining his bruises and scars, which had not yet disappeared.\n\n“He’s alone,” thought Mitya, “in all probability he’s alone.”\n\nFyodor Pavlovitch moved away from the looking-glass, turned suddenly to the window and looked out. Mitya instantly slipped away into the shadow.\n\n“She may be there behind the screen. Perhaps she’s asleep by now,” he thought, with a pang at his heart. Fyodor Pavlovitch moved away from the window. “He’s looking for her out of the window, so she’s not there. Why should he stare out into the dark? He’s wild with impatience.” ... Mitya slipped back at once, and fell to gazing in at the window again. The old man was sitting down at the table, apparently disappointed. At last he put his elbow on the table, and laid his right cheek against his hand. Mitya watched him eagerly.\n\n“He’s alone, he’s alone!” he repeated again. “If she were here, his face would be different.”\n\nStrange to say, a queer, irrational vexation rose up in his heart that she was not here. “It’s not that she’s not here,” he explained to himself, immediately, “but that I can’t tell for certain whether she is or not.” Mitya remembered afterwards that his mind was at that moment exceptionally clear, that he took in everything to the slightest detail, and missed no point. But a feeling of misery, the misery of uncertainty and indecision, was growing in his heart with every instant. “Is she here or not?” The angry doubt filled his heart, and suddenly, making up his mind, he put out his hand and softly knocked on the window frame. He knocked the signal the old man had agreed upon with Smerdyakov, twice slowly and then three times more quickly, the signal that meant “Grushenka is here!”\n\nThe old man started, jerked up his head, and, jumping up quickly, ran to the window. Mitya slipped away into the shadow. Fyodor Pavlovitch opened the window and thrust his whole head out.\n\n“Grushenka, is it you? Is it you?” he said, in a sort of trembling half-whisper. “Where are you, my angel, where are you?” He was fearfully agitated and breathless.\n\n“He’s alone.” Mitya decided.\n\n“Where are you?” cried the old man again; and he thrust his head out farther, thrust it out to the shoulders, gazing in all directions, right and left. “Come here, I’ve a little present for you. Come, I’ll show you....”\n\n“He means the three thousand,” thought Mitya.\n\n“But where are you? Are you at the door? I’ll open it directly.”\n\nAnd the old man almost climbed out of the window, peering out to the right, where there was a door into the garden, trying to see into the darkness. In another second he would certainly have run out to open the door without waiting for Grushenka’s answer.\n\nMitya looked at him from the side without stirring. The old man’s profile that he loathed so, his pendent Adam’s apple, his hooked nose, his lips that smiled in greedy expectation, were all brightly lighted up by the slanting lamplight falling on the left from the room. A horrible fury of hatred suddenly surged up in Mitya’s heart: “There he was, his rival, the man who had tormented him, had ruined his life!” It was a rush of that sudden, furious, revengeful anger of which he had spoken, as though foreseeing it, to Alyosha, four days ago in the arbor, when, in answer to Alyosha’s question, “How can you say you’ll kill our father?” “I don’t know, I don’t know,” he had said then. “Perhaps I shall not kill him, perhaps I shall. I’m afraid he’ll suddenly be so loathsome to me at that moment. I hate his double chin, his nose, his eyes, his shameless grin. I feel a personal repulsion. That’s what I’m afraid of, that’s what may be too much for me.” ... This personal repulsion was growing unendurable. Mitya was beside himself, he suddenly pulled the brass pestle out of his pocket.\n\n“God was watching over me then,” Mitya himself said afterwards. At that very moment Grigory waked up on his bed of sickness. Earlier in the evening he had undergone the treatment which Smerdyakov had described to Ivan. He had rubbed himself all over with vodka mixed with a secret, very strong decoction, had drunk what was left of the mixture while his wife repeated a “certain prayer” over him, after which he had gone to bed. Marfa Ignatyevna had tasted the stuff, too, and, being unused to strong drink, slept like the dead beside her husband.\n\nBut Grigory waked up in the night, quite suddenly, and, after a moment’s reflection, though he immediately felt a sharp pain in his back, he sat up in bed. Then he deliberated again, got up and dressed hurriedly. Perhaps his conscience was uneasy at the thought of sleeping while the house was unguarded “in such perilous times.” Smerdyakov, exhausted by his fit, lay motionless in the next room. Marfa Ignatyevna did not stir. “The stuff’s been too much for the woman,” Grigory thought, glancing at her, and groaning, he went out on the steps. No doubt he only intended to look out from the steps, for he was hardly able to walk, the pain in his back and his right leg was intolerable. But he suddenly remembered that he had not locked the little gate into the garden that evening. He was the most punctual and precise of men, a man who adhered to an unchangeable routine, and habits that lasted for years. Limping and writhing with pain he went down the steps and towards the garden. Yes, the gate stood wide open. Mechanically he stepped into the garden. Perhaps he fancied something, perhaps caught some sound, and, glancing to the left he saw his master’s window open. No one was looking out of it then.\n\n“What’s it open for? It’s not summer now,” thought Grigory, and suddenly, at that very instant he caught a glimpse of something extraordinary before him in the garden. Forty paces in front of him a man seemed to be running in the dark, a sort of shadow was moving very fast.\n\n“Good Lord!” cried Grigory beside himself, and forgetting the pain in his back, he hurried to intercept the running figure. He took a short cut, evidently he knew the garden better; the flying figure went towards the bath-house, ran behind it and rushed to the garden fence. Grigory followed, not losing sight of him, and ran, forgetting everything. He reached the fence at the very moment the man was climbing over it. Grigory cried out, beside himself, pounced on him, and clutched his leg in his two hands.\n\nYes, his foreboding had not deceived him. He recognized him, it was he, the “monster,” the “parricide.”\n\n“Parricide!” the old man shouted so that the whole neighborhood could hear, but he had not time to shout more, he fell at once, as though struck by lightning.\n\nMitya jumped back into the garden and bent over the fallen man. In Mitya’s hands was a brass pestle, and he flung it mechanically in the grass. The pestle fell two paces from Grigory, not in the grass but on the path, in a most conspicuous place. For some seconds he examined the prostrate figure before him. The old man’s head was covered with blood. Mitya put out his hand and began feeling it. He remembered afterwards clearly, that he had been awfully anxious to make sure whether he had broken the old man’s skull, or simply stunned him with the pestle. But the blood was flowing horribly; and in a moment Mitya’s fingers were drenched with the hot stream. He remembered taking out of his pocket the clean white handkerchief with which he had provided himself for his visit to Madame Hohlakov, and putting it to the old man’s head, senselessly trying to wipe the blood from his face and temples. But the handkerchief was instantly soaked with blood.\n\n“Good heavens! what am I doing it for?” thought Mitya, suddenly pulling himself together. “If I have broken his skull, how can I find out now? And what difference does it make now?” he added, hopelessly. “If I’ve killed him, I’ve killed him.... You’ve come to grief, old man, so there you must lie!” he said aloud. And suddenly turning to the fence, he vaulted over it into the lane and fell to running—the handkerchief soaked with blood he held, crushed up in his right fist, and as he ran he thrust it into the back pocket of his coat. He ran headlong, and the few passers-by who met him in the dark, in the streets, remembered afterwards that they had met a man running that night. He flew back again to the widow Morozov’s house.\n\nImmediately after he had left it that evening, Fenya had rushed to the chief porter, Nazar Ivanovitch, and besought him, for Christ’s sake, “not to let the captain in again to-day or to-morrow.” Nazar Ivanovitch promised, but went upstairs to his mistress who had suddenly sent for him, and meeting his nephew, a boy of twenty, who had recently come from the country, on the way up told him to take his place, but forgot to mention “the captain.” Mitya, running up to the gate, knocked. The lad instantly recognized him, for Mitya had more than once tipped him. Opening the gate at once, he let him in, and hastened to inform him with a good-humored smile that “Agrafena Alexandrovna is not at home now, you know.”\n\n“Where is she then, Prohor?” asked Mitya, stopping short.\n\n“She set off this evening, some two hours ago, with Timofey, to Mokroe.”\n\n“What for?” cried Mitya.\n\n“That I can’t say. To see some officer. Some one invited her and horses were sent to fetch her.”\n\nMitya left him, and ran like a madman to Fenya.\n\n# Chapter V. A Sudden Resolution\n\nShe was sitting in the kitchen with her grandmother; they were both just going to bed. Relying on Nazar Ivanovitch, they had not locked themselves in. Mitya ran in, pounced on Fenya and seized her by the throat.\n\n“Speak at once! Where is she? With whom is she now, at Mokroe?” he roared furiously.\n\nBoth the women squealed.\n\n“Aie! I’ll tell you. Aie! Dmitri Fyodorovitch, darling, I’ll tell you everything directly, I won’t hide anything,” gabbled Fenya, frightened to death; “she’s gone to Mokroe, to her officer.”\n\n“What officer?” roared Mitya.\n\n“To her officer, the same one she used to know, the one who threw her over five years ago,” cackled Fenya, as fast as she could speak.\n\nMitya withdrew the hands with which he was squeezing her throat. He stood facing her, pale as death, unable to utter a word, but his eyes showed that he realized it all, all, from the first word, and guessed the whole position. Poor Fenya was not in a condition at that moment to observe whether he understood or not. She remained sitting on the trunk as she had been when he ran into the room, trembling all over, holding her hands out before her as though trying to defend herself. She seemed to have grown rigid in that position. Her wide-opened, scared eyes were fixed immovably upon him. And to make matters worse, both his hands were smeared with blood. On the way, as he ran, he must have touched his forehead with them, wiping off the perspiration, so that on his forehead and his right cheek were blood-stained patches. Fenya was on the verge of hysterics. The old cook had jumped up and was staring at him like a mad woman, almost unconscious with terror.\n\nMitya stood for a moment, then mechanically sank on to a chair next to Fenya. He sat, not reflecting but, as it were, terror-stricken, benumbed. Yet everything was clear as day: that officer, he knew about him, he knew everything perfectly, he had known it from Grushenka herself, had known that a letter had come from him a month before. So that for a month, for a whole month, this had been going on, a secret from him, till the very arrival of this new man, and he had never thought of him! But how could he, how could he not have thought of him? Why was it he had forgotten this officer, like that, forgotten him as soon as he heard of him? That was the question that faced him like some monstrous thing. And he looked at this monstrous thing with horror, growing cold with horror.\n\nBut suddenly, as gently and mildly as a gentle and affectionate child, he began speaking to Fenya as though he had utterly forgotten how he had scared and hurt her just now. He fell to questioning Fenya with an extreme preciseness, astonishing in his position, and though the girl looked wildly at his blood-stained hands, she, too, with wonderful readiness and rapidity, answered every question as though eager to put the whole truth and nothing but the truth before him. Little by little, even with a sort of enjoyment, she began explaining every detail, not wanting to torment him, but, as it were, eager to be of the utmost service to him. She described the whole of that day, in great detail, the visit of Rakitin and Alyosha, how she, Fenya, had stood on the watch, how the mistress had set off, and how she had called out of the window to Alyosha to give him, Mitya, her greetings, and to tell him “to remember for ever how she had loved him for an hour.”\n\nHearing of the message, Mitya suddenly smiled, and there was a flush of color on his pale cheeks. At the same moment Fenya said to him, not a bit afraid now to be inquisitive:\n\n“Look at your hands, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. They’re all over blood!”\n\n“Yes,” answered Mitya mechanically. He looked carelessly at his hands and at once forgot them and Fenya’s question.\n\nHe sank into silence again. Twenty minutes had passed since he had run in. His first horror was over, but evidently some new fixed determination had taken possession of him. He suddenly stood up, smiling dreamily.\n\n“What has happened to you, sir?” said Fenya, pointing to his hands again. She spoke compassionately, as though she felt very near to him now in his grief. Mitya looked at his hands again.\n\n“That’s blood, Fenya,” he said, looking at her with a strange expression. “That’s human blood, and my God! why was it shed? But ... Fenya ... there’s a fence here” (he looked at her as though setting her a riddle), “a high fence, and terrible to look at. But at dawn to-morrow, when the sun rises, Mitya will leap over that fence.... You don’t understand what fence, Fenya, and, never mind.... You’ll hear to-morrow and understand ... and now, good-by. I won’t stand in her way. I’ll step aside, I know how to step aside. Live, my joy.... You loved me for an hour, remember Mityenka Karamazov so for ever.... She always used to call me Mityenka, do you remember?”\n\nAnd with those words he went suddenly out of the kitchen. Fenya was almost more frightened at this sudden departure than she had been when he ran in and attacked her.\n\nJust ten minutes later Dmitri went in to Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, the young official with whom he had pawned his pistols. It was by now half-past eight, and Pyotr Ilyitch had finished his evening tea, and had just put his coat on again to go to the “Metropolis” to play billiards. Mitya caught him coming out.\n\nSeeing him with his face all smeared with blood, the young man uttered a cry of surprise.\n\n“Good heavens! What is the matter?”\n\n“I’ve come for my pistols,” said Mitya, “and brought you the money. And thanks very much. I’m in a hurry, Pyotr Ilyitch, please make haste.”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch grew more and more surprised; he suddenly caught sight of a bundle of bank-notes in Mitya’s hand, and what was more, he had walked in holding the notes as no one walks in and no one carries money: he had them in his right hand, and held them outstretched as if to show them. Perhotin’s servant-boy, who met Mitya in the passage, said afterwards that he walked into the passage in the same way, with the money outstretched in his hand, so he must have been carrying them like that even in the streets. They were all rainbow-colored hundred-rouble notes, and the fingers holding them were covered with blood.\n\nWhen Pyotr Ilyitch was questioned later on as to the sum of money, he said that it was difficult to judge at a glance, but that it might have been two thousand, or perhaps three, but it was a big, “fat” bundle. “Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” so he testified afterwards, “seemed unlike himself, too; not drunk, but, as it were, exalted, lost to everything, but at the same time, as it were, absorbed, as though pondering and searching for something and unable to come to a decision. He was in great haste, answered abruptly and very strangely, and at moments seemed not at all dejected but quite cheerful.”\n\n“But what is the matter with you? What’s wrong?” cried Pyotr Ilyitch, looking wildly at his guest. “How is it that you’re all covered with blood? Have you had a fall? Look at yourself!”\n\nHe took him by the elbow and led him to the glass.\n\nSeeing his blood-stained face, Mitya started and scowled wrathfully.\n\n“Damnation! That’s the last straw,” he muttered angrily, hurriedly changing the notes from his right hand to the left, and impulsively jerked the handkerchief out of his pocket. But the handkerchief turned out to be soaked with blood, too (it was the handkerchief he had used to wipe Grigory’s face). There was scarcely a white spot on it, and it had not merely begun to dry, but had stiffened into a crumpled ball and could not be pulled apart. Mitya threw it angrily on the floor.\n\n“Oh, damn it!” he said. “Haven’t you a rag of some sort ... to wipe my face?”\n\n“So you’re only stained, not wounded? You’d better wash,” said Pyotr Ilyitch. “Here’s a wash-stand. I’ll pour you out some water.”\n\n“A wash-stand? That’s all right ... but where am I to put this?”\n\nWith the strangest perplexity he indicated his bundle of hundred-rouble notes, looking inquiringly at Pyotr Ilyitch as though it were for him to decide what he, Mitya, was to do with his own money.\n\n“In your pocket, or on the table here. They won’t be lost.”\n\n“In my pocket? Yes, in my pocket. All right.... But, I say, that’s all nonsense,” he cried, as though suddenly coming out of his absorption. “Look here, let’s first settle that business of the pistols. Give them back to me. Here’s your money ... because I am in great need of them ... and I haven’t a minute, a minute to spare.”\n\nAnd taking the topmost note from the bundle he held it out to Pyotr Ilyitch.\n\n“But I shan’t have change enough. Haven’t you less?”\n\n“No,” said Mitya, looking again at the bundle, and as though not trusting his own words he turned over two or three of the topmost ones.\n\n“No, they’re all alike,” he added, and again he looked inquiringly at Pyotr Ilyitch.\n\n“How have you grown so rich?” the latter asked. “Wait, I’ll send my boy to Plotnikov’s, they close late—to see if they won’t change it. Here, Misha!” he called into the passage.\n\n“To Plotnikov’s shop—first-rate!” cried Mitya, as though struck by an idea. “Misha,” he turned to the boy as he came in, “look here, run to Plotnikov’s and tell them that Dmitri Fyodorovitch sends his greetings, and will be there directly.... But listen, listen, tell them to have champagne, three dozen bottles, ready before I come, and packed as it was to take to Mokroe. I took four dozen with me then,” he added (suddenly addressing Pyotr Ilyitch); “they know all about it, don’t you trouble, Misha,” he turned again to the boy. “Stay, listen; tell them to put in cheese, Strasburg pies, smoked fish, ham, caviare, and everything, everything they’ve got, up to a hundred roubles, or a hundred and twenty as before.... But wait: don’t let them forget dessert, sweets, pears, water-melons, two or three or four—no, one melon’s enough, and chocolate, candy, toffee, fondants; in fact, everything I took to Mokroe before, three hundred roubles’ worth with the champagne ... let it be just the same again. And remember, Misha, if you are called Misha—His name is Misha, isn’t it?” He turned to Pyotr Ilyitch again.\n\n“Wait a minute,” Protr Ilyitch intervened, listening and watching him uneasily, “you’d better go yourself and tell them. He’ll muddle it.”\n\n“He will, I see he will! Eh, Misha! Why, I was going to kiss you for the commission.... If you don’t make a mistake, there’s ten roubles for you, run along, make haste.... Champagne’s the chief thing, let them bring up champagne. And brandy, too, and red and white wine, and all I had then.... They know what I had then.”\n\n“But listen!” Pyotr Ilyitch interrupted with some impatience. “I say, let him simply run and change the money and tell them not to close, and you go and tell them.... Give him your note. Be off, Misha! Put your best leg forward!”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch seemed to hurry Misha off on purpose, because the boy remained standing with his mouth and eyes wide open, apparently understanding little of Mitya’s orders, gazing up with amazement and terror at his blood-stained face and the trembling bloodstained fingers that held the notes.\n\n“Well, now come and wash,” said Pyotr Ilyitch sternly. “Put the money on the table or else in your pocket.... That’s right, come along. But take off your coat.”\n\nAnd beginning to help him off with his coat, he cried out again:\n\n“Look, your coat’s covered with blood, too!”\n\n“That ... it’s not the coat. It’s only a little here on the sleeve.... And that’s only here where the handkerchief lay. It must have soaked through. I must have sat on the handkerchief at Fenya’s, and the blood’s come through,” Mitya explained at once with a childlike unconsciousness that was astounding. Pyotr Ilyitch listened, frowning.\n\n“Well, you must have been up to something; you must have been fighting with some one,” he muttered.\n\nThey began to wash. Pyotr Ilyitch held the jug and poured out the water. Mitya, in desperate haste, scarcely soaped his hands (they were trembling, and Pyotr Ilyitch remembered it afterwards). But the young official insisted on his soaping them thoroughly and rubbing them more. He seemed to exercise more and more sway over Mitya, as time went on. It may be noted in passing that he was a young man of sturdy character.\n\n“Look, you haven’t got your nails clean. Now rub your face; here, on your temples, by your ear.... Will you go in that shirt? Where are you going? Look, all the cuff of your right sleeve is covered with blood.”\n\n“Yes, it’s all bloody,” observed Mitya, looking at the cuff of his shirt.\n\n“Then change your shirt.”\n\n“I haven’t time. You see I’ll ...” Mitya went on with the same confiding ingenuousness, drying his face and hands on the towel, and putting on his coat. “I’ll turn it up at the wrist. It won’t be seen under the coat.... You see!”\n\n“Tell me now, what game have you been up to? Have you been fighting with some one? In the tavern again, as before? Have you been beating that captain again?” Pyotr Ilyitch asked him reproachfully. “Whom have you been beating now ... or killing, perhaps?”\n\n“Nonsense!” said Mitya.\n\n“Why ‘nonsense’?”\n\n“Don’t worry,” said Mitya, and he suddenly laughed. “I smashed an old woman in the market-place just now.”\n\n“Smashed? An old woman?”\n\n“An old man!” cried Mitya, looking Pyotr Ilyitch straight in the face, laughing, and shouting at him as though he were deaf.\n\n“Confound it! An old woman, an old man.... Have you killed some one?”\n\n“We made it up. We had a row—and made it up. In a place I know of. We parted friends. A fool.... He’s forgiven me.... He’s sure to have forgiven me by now ... if he had got up, he wouldn’t have forgiven me”—Mitya suddenly winked—“only damn him, you know, I say, Pyotr Ilyitch, damn him! Don’t worry about him! I don’t want to just now!” Mitya snapped out, resolutely.\n\n“Whatever do you want to go picking quarrels with every one for? ... Just as you did with that captain over some nonsense.... You’ve been fighting and now you’re rushing off on the spree—that’s you all over! Three dozen champagne—what do you want all that for?”\n\n“Bravo! Now give me the pistols. Upon my honor I’ve no time now. I should like to have a chat with you, my dear boy, but I haven’t the time. And there’s no need, it’s too late for talking. Where’s my money? Where have I put it?” he cried, thrusting his hands into his pockets.\n\n“You put it on the table ... yourself.... Here it is. Had you forgotten? Money’s like dirt or water to you, it seems. Here are your pistols. It’s an odd thing, at six o’clock you pledged them for ten roubles, and now you’ve got thousands. Two or three I should say.”\n\n“Three, you bet,” laughed Mitya, stuffing the notes into the side-pocket of his trousers.\n\n“You’ll lose it like that. Have you found a gold-mine?”\n\n“The mines? The gold-mines?” Mitya shouted at the top of his voice and went off into a roar of laughter. “Would you like to go to the mines, Perhotin? There’s a lady here who’ll stump up three thousand for you, if only you’ll go. She did it for me, she’s so awfully fond of gold-mines. Do you know Madame Hohlakov?”\n\n“I don’t know her, but I’ve heard of her and seen her. Did she really give you three thousand? Did she really?” said Pyotr Ilyitch, eyeing him dubiously.\n\n“As soon as the sun rises to-morrow, as soon as Phœbus, ever young, flies upwards, praising and glorifying God, you go to her, this Madame Hohlakov, and ask her whether she did stump up that three thousand or not. Try and find out.”\n\n“I don’t know on what terms you are ... since you say it so positively, I suppose she did give it to you. You’ve got the money in your hand, but instead of going to Siberia you’re spending it all.... Where are you really off to now, eh?”\n\n“To Mokroe.”\n\n“To Mokroe? But it’s night!”\n\n“Once the lad had all, now the lad has naught,” cried Mitya suddenly.\n\n“How ‘naught’? You say that with all those thousands!”\n\n“I’m not talking about thousands. Damn thousands! I’m talking of the female character.\n\nFickle is the heart of woman Treacherous and full of vice;\n\nI agree with Ulysses. That’s what he says.”\n\n“I don’t understand you!”\n\n“Am I drunk?”\n\n“Not drunk, but worse.”\n\n“I’m drunk in spirit, Pyotr Ilyitch, drunk in spirit! But that’s enough!”\n\n“What are you doing, loading the pistol?”\n\n“I’m loading the pistol.”\n\nUnfastening the pistol-case, Mitya actually opened the powder horn, and carefully sprinkled and rammed in the charge. Then he took the bullet and, before inserting it, held it in two fingers in front of the candle.\n\n“Why are you looking at the bullet?” asked Pyotr Ilyitch, watching him with uneasy curiosity.\n\n“Oh, a fancy. Why, if you meant to put that bullet in your brain, would you look at it or not?”\n\n“Why look at it?”\n\n“It’s going into my brain, so it’s interesting to look and see what it’s like. But that’s foolishness, a moment’s foolishness. Now that’s done,” he added, putting in the bullet and driving it home with the ramrod. “Pyotr Ilyitch, my dear fellow, that’s nonsense, all nonsense, and if only you knew what nonsense! Give me a little piece of paper now.”\n\n“Here’s some paper.”\n\n“No, a clean new piece, writing-paper. That’s right.”\n\nAnd taking a pen from the table, Mitya rapidly wrote two lines, folded the paper in four, and thrust it in his waistcoat pocket. He put the pistols in the case, locked it up, and kept it in his hand. Then he looked at Pyotr Ilyitch with a slow, thoughtful smile.\n\n“Now, let’s go.”\n\n“Where are we going? No, wait a minute.... Are you thinking of putting that bullet in your brain, perhaps?” Pyotr Ilyitch asked uneasily.\n\n“I was fooling about the bullet! I want to live. I love life! You may be sure of that. I love golden-haired Phœbus and his warm light.... Dear Pyotr Ilyitch, do you know how to step aside?”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘stepping aside’?”\n\n“Making way. Making way for a dear creature, and for one I hate. And to let the one I hate become dear—that’s what making way means! And to say to them: God bless you, go your way, pass on, while I—”\n\n“While you—?”\n\n“That’s enough, let’s go.”\n\n“Upon my word. I’ll tell some one to prevent your going there,” said Pyotr Ilyitch, looking at him. “What are you going to Mokroe for, now?”\n\n“There’s a woman there, a woman. That’s enough for you. You shut up.”\n\n“Listen, though you’re such a savage I’ve always liked you.... I feel anxious.”\n\n“Thanks, old fellow. I’m a savage you say. Savages, savages! That’s what I am always saying. Savages! Why, here’s Misha! I was forgetting him.”\n\nMisha ran in, post-haste, with a handful of notes in change, and reported that every one was in a bustle at the Plotnikovs’; “They’re carrying down the bottles, and the fish, and the tea; it will all be ready directly.” Mitya seized ten roubles and handed it to Pyotr Ilyitch, then tossed another ten-rouble note to Misha.\n\n“Don’t dare to do such a thing!” cried Pyotr Ilyitch. “I won’t have it in my house, it’s a bad, demoralizing habit. Put your money away. Here, put it here, why waste it? It would come in handy to-morrow, and I dare say you’ll be coming to me to borrow ten roubles again. Why do you keep putting the notes in your side-pocket? Ah, you’ll lose them!”\n\n“I say, my dear fellow, let’s go to Mokroe together.”\n\n“What should I go for?”\n\n“I say, let’s open a bottle at once, and drink to life! I want to drink, and especially to drink with you. I’ve never drunk with you, have I?”\n\n“Very well, we can go to the ‘Metropolis.’ I was just going there.”\n\n“I haven’t time for that. Let’s drink at the Plotnikovs’, in the back room. Shall I ask you a riddle?”\n\n“Ask away.”\n\nMitya took the piece of paper out of his waistcoat pocket, unfolded it and showed it. In a large, distinct hand was written: “I punish myself for my whole life, my whole life I punish!”\n\n“I will certainly speak to some one, I’ll go at once,” said Pyotr Ilyitch, after reading the paper.\n\n“You won’t have time, dear boy, come and have a drink. March!”\n\nPlotnikov’s shop was at the corner of the street, next door but one to Pyotr Ilyitch’s. It was the largest grocery shop in our town, and by no means a bad one, belonging to some rich merchants. They kept everything that could be got in a Petersburg shop, grocery of all sort, wines “bottled by the brothers Eliseyev,” fruits, cigars, tea, coffee, sugar, and so on. There were three shop-assistants and two errand boys always employed. Though our part of the country had grown poorer, the landowners had gone away, and trade had got worse, yet the grocery stores flourished as before, every year with increasing prosperity; there were plenty of purchasers for their goods.\n\nThey were awaiting Mitya with impatience in the shop. They had vivid recollections of how he had bought, three or four weeks ago, wine and goods of all sorts to the value of several hundred roubles, paid for in cash (they would never have let him have anything on credit, of course). They remembered that then, as now, he had had a bundle of hundred-rouble notes in his hand, and had scattered them at random, without bargaining, without reflecting, or caring to reflect what use so much wine and provisions would be to him. The story was told all over the town that, driving off then with Grushenka to Mokroe, he had “spent three thousand in one night and the following day, and had come back from the spree without a penny.” He had picked up a whole troop of gypsies (encamped in our neighborhood at the time), who for two days got money without stint out of him while he was drunk, and drank expensive wine without stint. People used to tell, laughing at Mitya, how he had given champagne to grimy-handed peasants, and feasted the village women and girls on sweets and Strasburg pies. Though to laugh at Mitya to his face was rather a risky proceeding, there was much laughter behind his back, especially in the tavern, at his own ingenuous public avowal that all he had got out of Grushenka by this “escapade” was “permission to kiss her foot, and that was the utmost she had allowed him.”\n\nBy the time Mitya and Pyotr Ilyitch reached the shop, they found a cart with three horses harnessed abreast with bells, and with Andrey, the driver, ready waiting for Mitya at the entrance. In the shop they had almost entirely finished packing one box of provisions, and were only waiting for Mitya’s arrival to nail it down and put it in the cart. Pyotr Ilyitch was astounded.\n\n“Where did this cart come from in such a hurry?” he asked Mitya.\n\n“I met Andrey as I ran to you, and told him to drive straight here to the shop. There’s no time to lose. Last time I drove with Timofey, but Timofey now has gone on before me with the witch. Shall we be very late, Andrey?”\n\n“They’ll only get there an hour at most before us, not even that maybe. I got Timofey ready to start. I know how he’ll go. Their pace won’t be ours, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. How could it be? They won’t get there an hour earlier!” Andrey, a lanky, red-haired, middle-aged driver, wearing a full-skirted coat, and with a kaftan on his arm, replied warmly.\n\n“Fifty roubles for vodka if we’re only an hour behind them.”\n\n“I warrant the time, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. Ech, they won’t be half an hour before us, let alone an hour.”\n\nThough Mitya bustled about seeing after things, he gave his orders strangely, as it were disconnectedly, and inconsecutively. He began a sentence and forgot the end of it. Pyotr Ilyitch found himself obliged to come to the rescue.\n\n“Four hundred roubles’ worth, not less than four hundred roubles’ worth, just as it was then,” commanded Mitya. “Four dozen champagne, not a bottle less.”\n\n“What do you want with so much? What’s it for? Stay!” cried Pyotr Ilyitch. “What’s this box? What’s in it? Surely there isn’t four hundred roubles’ worth here?”\n\nThe officious shopmen began explaining with oily politeness that the first box contained only half a dozen bottles of champagne, and only “the most indispensable articles,” such as savories, sweets, toffee, etc. But the main part of the goods ordered would be packed and sent off, as on the previous occasion, in a special cart also with three horses traveling at full speed, so that it would arrive not more than an hour later than Dmitri Fyodorovitch himself.\n\n“Not more than an hour! Not more than an hour! And put in more toffee and fondants. The girls there are so fond of it,” Mitya insisted hotly.\n\n“The fondants are all right. But what do you want with four dozen of champagne? One would be enough,” said Pyotr Ilyitch, almost angry. He began bargaining, asking for a bill of the goods, and refused to be satisfied. But he only succeeded in saving a hundred roubles. In the end it was agreed that only three hundred roubles’ worth should be sent.\n\n“Well, you may go to the devil!” cried Pyotr Ilyitch, on second thoughts. “What’s it to do with me? Throw away your money, since it’s cost you nothing.”\n\n“This way, my economist, this way, don’t be angry.” Mitya drew him into a room at the back of the shop. “They’ll give us a bottle here directly. We’ll taste it. Ech, Pyotr Ilyitch, come along with me, for you’re a nice fellow, the sort I like.”\n\nMitya sat down on a wicker chair, before a little table, covered with a dirty dinner-napkin. Pyotr Ilyitch sat down opposite, and the champagne soon appeared, and oysters were suggested to the gentlemen. “First-class oysters, the last lot in.”\n\n“Hang the oysters. I don’t eat them. And we don’t need anything,” cried Pyotr Ilyitch, almost angrily.\n\n“There’s no time for oysters,” said Mitya. “And I’m not hungry. Do you know, friend,” he said suddenly, with feeling, “I never have liked all this disorder.”\n\n“Who does like it? Three dozen of champagne for peasants, upon my word, that’s enough to make any one angry!”\n\n“That’s not what I mean. I’m talking of a higher order. There’s no order in me, no higher order. But ... that’s all over. There’s no need to grieve about it. It’s too late, damn it! My whole life has been disorder, and one must set it in order. Is that a pun, eh?”\n\n“You’re raving, not making puns!”\n\n“Glory be to God in Heaven, Glory be to God in me....\n\n“That verse came from my heart once, it’s not a verse, but a tear.... I made it myself ... not while I was pulling the captain’s beard, though....”\n\n“Why do you bring him in all of a sudden?”\n\n“Why do I bring him in? Foolery! All things come to an end; all things are made equal. That’s the long and short of it.”\n\n“You know, I keep thinking of your pistols.”\n\n“That’s all foolery, too! Drink, and don’t be fanciful. I love life. I’ve loved life too much, shamefully much. Enough! Let’s drink to life, dear boy, I propose the toast. Why am I pleased with myself? I’m a scoundrel, but I’m satisfied with myself. And yet I’m tortured by the thought that I’m a scoundrel, but satisfied with myself. I bless the creation. I’m ready to bless God and His creation directly, but ... I must kill one noxious insect for fear it should crawl and spoil life for others.... Let us drink to life, dear brother. What can be more precious than life? Nothing! To life, and to one queen of queens!”\n\n“Let’s drink to life and to your queen, too, if you like.”\n\nThey drank a glass each. Although Mitya was excited and expansive, yet he was melancholy, too. It was as though some heavy, overwhelming anxiety were weighing upon him.\n\n“Misha ... here’s your Misha come! Misha, come here, my boy, drink this glass to Phœbus, the golden-haired, of to-morrow morn....”\n\n“What are you giving it him for?” cried Pyotr Ilyitch, irritably.\n\n“Yes, yes, yes, let me! I want to!”\n\n“E—ech!”\n\nMisha emptied the glass, bowed, and ran out.\n\n“He’ll remember it afterwards,” Mitya remarked. “Woman, I love woman! What is woman? The queen of creation! My heart is sad, my heart is sad, Pyotr Ilyitch. Do you remember Hamlet? ‘I am very sorry, good Horatio! Alas, poor Yorick!’ Perhaps that’s me, Yorick? Yes, I’m Yorick now, and a skull afterwards.”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch listened in silence. Mitya, too, was silent for a while.\n\n“What dog’s that you’ve got here?” he asked the shopman, casually, noticing a pretty little lap-dog with dark eyes, sitting in the corner.\n\n“It belongs to Varvara Alexyevna, the mistress,” answered the clerk. “She brought it and forgot it here. It must be taken back to her.”\n\n“I saw one like it ... in the regiment ...” murmured Mitya dreamily, “only that one had its hind leg broken.... By the way, Pyotr Ilyitch, I wanted to ask you: have you ever stolen anything in your life?”\n\n“What a question!”\n\n“Oh, I didn’t mean anything. From somebody’s pocket, you know. I don’t mean government money, every one steals that, and no doubt you do, too....”\n\n“You go to the devil.”\n\n“I’m talking of other people’s money. Stealing straight out of a pocket? Out of a purse, eh?”\n\n“I stole twenty copecks from my mother when I was nine years old. I took it off the table on the sly, and held it tight in my hand.”\n\n“Well, and what happened?”\n\n“Oh, nothing. I kept it three days, then I felt ashamed, confessed, and gave it back.”\n\n“And what then?”\n\n“Naturally I was whipped. But why do you ask? Have you stolen something?”\n\n“I have,” said Mitya, winking slyly.\n\n“What have you stolen?” inquired Pyotr Ilyitch curiously.\n\n“I stole twenty copecks from my mother when I was nine years old, and gave it back three days after.”\n\nAs he said this, Mitya suddenly got up.\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, won’t you come now?” called Andrey from the door of the shop.\n\n“Are you ready? We’ll come!” Mitya started. “A few more last words and—Andrey, a glass of vodka at starting. Give him some brandy as well! That box” (the one with the pistols) “put under my seat. Good-by, Pyotr Ilyitch, don’t remember evil against me.”\n\n“But you’re coming back to-morrow?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Will you settle the little bill now?” cried the clerk, springing forward.\n\n“Oh, yes, the bill. Of course.”\n\nHe pulled the bundle of notes out of his pocket again, picked out three hundred roubles, threw them on the counter, and ran hurriedly out of the shop. Every one followed him out, bowing and wishing him good luck. Andrey, coughing from the brandy he had just swallowed, jumped up on the box. But Mitya was only just taking his seat when suddenly to his surprise he saw Fenya before him. She ran up panting, clasped her hands before him with a cry, and plumped down at his feet.\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear good Dmitri Fyodorovitch, don’t harm my mistress. And it was I told you all about it.... And don’t murder him, he came first, he’s hers! He’ll marry Agrafena Alexandrovna now. That’s why he’s come back from Siberia. Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear, don’t take a fellow creature’s life!”\n\n“Tut—tut—tut! That’s it, is it? So you’re off there to make trouble!” muttered Pyotr Ilyitch. “Now, it’s all clear, as clear as daylight. Dmitri Fyodorovitch, give me your pistols at once if you mean to behave like a man,” he shouted aloud to Mitya. “Do you hear, Dmitri?”\n\n“The pistols? Wait a bit, brother, I’ll throw them into the pool on the road,” answered Mitya. “Fenya, get up, don’t kneel to me. Mitya won’t hurt any one, the silly fool won’t hurt any one again. But I say, Fenya,” he shouted, after having taken his seat. “I hurt you just now, so forgive me and have pity on me, forgive a scoundrel.... But it doesn’t matter if you don’t. It’s all the same now. Now then, Andrey, look alive, fly along full speed!”\n\nAndrey whipped up the horses, and the bells began ringing.\n\n“Good-by, Pyotr Ilyitch! My last tear is for you!...”\n\n“He’s not drunk, but he keeps babbling like a lunatic,” Pyotr Ilyitch thought as he watched him go. He had half a mind to stay and see the cart packed with the remaining wines and provisions, knowing that they would deceive and defraud Mitya. But, suddenly feeling vexed with himself, he turned away with a curse and went to the tavern to play billiards.\n\n“He’s a fool, though he’s a good fellow,” he muttered as he went. “I’ve heard of that officer, Grushenka’s former flame. Well, if he has turned up.... Ech, those pistols! Damn it all! I’m not his nurse! Let them do what they like! Besides, it’ll all come to nothing. They’re a set of brawlers, that’s all. They’ll drink and fight, fight and make friends again. They are not men who do anything real. What does he mean by ‘I’m stepping aside, I’m punishing myself?’ It’ll come to nothing! He’s shouted such phrases a thousand times, drunk, in the taverns. But now he’s not drunk. ‘Drunk in spirit’—they’re fond of fine phrases, the villains. Am I his nurse? He must have been fighting, his face was all over blood. With whom? I shall find out at the ‘Metropolis.’ And his handkerchief was soaked in blood.... It’s still lying on my floor.... Hang it!”\n\nHe reached the tavern in a bad humor and at once made up a game. The game cheered him. He played a second game, and suddenly began telling one of his partners that Dmitri Karamazov had come in for some cash again—something like three thousand roubles, and had gone to Mokroe again to spend it with Grushenka.... This news roused singular interest in his listeners. They all spoke of it, not laughing, but with a strange gravity. They left off playing.\n\n“Three thousand? But where can he have got three thousand?”\n\nQuestions were asked. The story of Madame Hohlakov’s present was received with skepticism.\n\n“Hasn’t he robbed his old father?—that’s the question.”\n\n“Three thousand! There’s something odd about it.”\n\n“He boasted aloud that he would kill his father; we all heard him, here. And it was three thousand he talked about ...”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch listened. All at once he became short and dry in his answers. He said not a word about the blood on Mitya’s face and hands, though he had meant to speak of it at first.\n\nThey began a third game, and by degrees the talk about Mitya died away. But by the end of the third game, Pyotr Ilyitch felt no more desire for billiards; he laid down the cue, and without having supper as he had intended, he walked out of the tavern. When he reached the market-place he stood still in perplexity, wondering at himself. He realized that what he wanted was to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s and find out if anything had happened there. “On account of some stupid nonsense—as it’s sure to turn out—am I going to wake up the household and make a scandal? Fooh! damn it, is it my business to look after them?”\n\nIn a very bad humor he went straight home, and suddenly remembered Fenya. “Damn it all! I ought to have questioned her just now,” he thought with vexation, “I should have heard everything.” And the desire to speak to her, and so find out, became so pressing and importunate that when he was half-way home he turned abruptly and went towards the house where Grushenka lodged. Going up to the gate he knocked. The sound of the knock in the silence of the night sobered him and made him feel annoyed. And no one answered him; every one in the house was asleep.\n\n“And I shall be making a fuss!” he thought, with a feeling of positive discomfort. But instead of going away altogether, he fell to knocking again with all his might, filling the street with clamor.\n\n“Not coming? Well, I will knock them up, I will!” he muttered at each knock, fuming at himself, but at the same time he redoubled his knocks on the gate.\n\n# Chapter VI. “I Am Coming, Too!”\n\nBut Dmitri Fyodorovitch was speeding along the road. It was a little more than twenty versts to Mokroe, but Andrey’s three horses galloped at such a pace that the distance might be covered in an hour and a quarter. The swift motion revived Mitya. The air was fresh and cool, there were big stars shining in the sky. It was the very night, and perhaps the very hour, in which Alyosha fell on the earth, and rapturously swore to love it for ever and ever.\n\nAll was confusion, confusion, in Mitya’s soul, but although many things were goading his heart, at that moment his whole being was yearning for her, his queen, to whom he was flying to look on her for the last time. One thing I can say for certain; his heart did not waver for one instant. I shall perhaps not be believed when I say that this jealous lover felt not the slightest jealousy of this new rival, who seemed to have sprung out of the earth. If any other had appeared on the scene, he would have been jealous at once, and would perhaps have stained his fierce hands with blood again. But as he flew through the night, he felt no envy, no hostility even, for the man who had been her first lover.... It is true he had not yet seen him.\n\n“Here there was no room for dispute: it was her right and his; this was her first love which, after five years, she had not forgotten; so she had loved him only for those five years, and I, how do I come in? What right have I? Step aside, Mitya, and make way! What am I now? Now everything is over apart from the officer—even if he had not appeared, everything would be over ...”\n\nThese words would roughly have expressed his feelings, if he had been capable of reasoning. But he could not reason at that moment. His present plan of action had arisen without reasoning. At Fenya’s first words, it had sprung from feeling, and been adopted in a flash, with all its consequences. And yet, in spite of his resolution, there was confusion in his soul, an agonizing confusion: his resolution did not give him peace. There was so much behind that tortured him. And it seemed strange to him, at moments, to think that he had written his own sentence of death with pen and paper: “I punish myself,” and the paper was lying there in his pocket, ready; the pistol was loaded; he had already resolved how, next morning, he would meet the first warm ray of “golden-haired Phœbus.”\n\nAnd yet he could not be quit of the past, of all that he had left behind and that tortured him. He felt that miserably, and the thought of it sank into his heart with despair. There was one moment when he felt an impulse to stop Andrey, to jump out of the cart, to pull out his loaded pistol, and to make an end of everything without waiting for the dawn. But that moment flew by like a spark. The horses galloped on, “devouring space,” and as he drew near his goal, again the thought of her, of her alone, took more and more complete possession of his soul, chasing away the fearful images that had been haunting it. Oh, how he longed to look upon her, if only for a moment, if only from a distance!\n\n“She’s now with him,” he thought, “now I shall see what she looks like with him, her first love, and that’s all I want.” Never had this woman, who was such a fateful influence in his life, aroused such love in his breast, such new and unknown feeling, surprising even to himself, a feeling tender to devoutness, to self-effacement before her! “I will efface myself!” he said, in a rush of almost hysterical ecstasy.\n\nThey had been galloping nearly an hour. Mitya was silent, and though Andrey was, as a rule, a talkative peasant, he did not utter a word, either. He seemed afraid to talk, he only whipped up smartly his three lean, but mettlesome, bay horses. Suddenly Mitya cried out in horrible anxiety:\n\n“Andrey! What if they’re asleep?”\n\nThis thought fell upon him like a blow. It had not occurred to him before.\n\n“It may well be that they’re gone to bed, by now, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\nMitya frowned as though in pain. Yes, indeed ... he was rushing there ... with such feelings ... while they were asleep ... she was asleep, perhaps, there too.... An angry feeling surged up in his heart.\n\n“Drive on, Andrey! Whip them up! Look alive!” he cried, beside himself.\n\n“But maybe they’re not in bed!” Andrey went on after a pause. “Timofey said they were a lot of them there—”\n\n“At the station?”\n\n“Not at the posting-station, but at Plastunov’s, at the inn, where they let out horses, too.”\n\n“I know. So you say there are a lot of them? How’s that? Who are they?” cried Mitya, greatly dismayed at this unexpected news.\n\n“Well, Timofey was saying they’re all gentlefolk. Two from our town—who they are I can’t say—and there are two others, strangers, maybe more besides. I didn’t ask particularly. They’ve set to playing cards, so Timofey said.”\n\n“Cards?”\n\n“So, maybe they’re not in bed if they’re at cards. It’s most likely not more than eleven.”\n\n“Quicker, Andrey! Quicker!” Mitya cried again, nervously.\n\n“May I ask you something, sir?” said Andrey, after a pause. “Only I’m afraid of angering you, sir.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Why, Fenya threw herself at your feet just now, and begged you not to harm her mistress, and some one else, too ... so you see, sir— It’s I am taking you there ... forgive me, sir, it’s my conscience ... maybe it’s stupid of me to speak of it—”\n\nMitya suddenly seized him by the shoulders from behind.\n\n“Are you a driver?” he asked frantically.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Then you know that one has to make way. What would you say to a driver who wouldn’t make way for any one, but would just drive on and crush people? No, a driver mustn’t run over people. One can’t run over a man. One can’t spoil people’s lives. And if you have spoilt a life—punish yourself.... If only you’ve spoilt, if only you’ve ruined any one’s life—punish yourself and go away.”\n\nThese phrases burst from Mitya almost hysterically. Though Andrey was surprised at him, he kept up the conversation.\n\n“That’s right, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, you’re quite right, one mustn’t crush or torment a man, or any kind of creature, for every creature is created by God. Take a horse, for instance, for some folks, even among us drivers, drive anyhow. Nothing will restrain them, they just force it along.”\n\n“To hell?” Mitya interrupted, and went off into his abrupt, short laugh. “Andrey, simple soul,” he seized him by the shoulders again, “tell me, will Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov go to hell, or not, what do you think?”\n\n“I don’t know, darling, it depends on you, for you are ... you see, sir, when the Son of God was nailed on the Cross and died, He went straight down to hell from the Cross, and set free all sinners that were in agony. And the devil groaned, because he thought that he would get no more sinners in hell. And God said to him, then, ‘Don’t groan, for you shall have all the mighty of the earth, the rulers, the chief judges, and the rich men, and shall be filled up as you have been in all the ages till I come again.’ Those were His very words ...”\n\n“A peasant legend! Capital! Whip up the left, Andrey!”\n\n“So you see, sir, who it is hell’s for,” said Andrey, whipping up the left horse, “but you’re like a little child ... that’s how we look on you ... and though you’re hasty-tempered, sir, yet God will forgive you for your kind heart.”\n\n“And you, do you forgive me, Andrey?”\n\n“What should I forgive you for, sir? You’ve never done me any harm.”\n\n“No, for every one, for every one, you here alone, on the road, will you forgive me for every one? Speak, simple peasant heart!”\n\n“Oh, sir! I feel afraid of driving you, your talk is so strange.”\n\nBut Mitya did not hear. He was frantically praying and muttering to himself.\n\n“Lord, receive me, with all my lawlessness, and do not condemn me. Let me pass by Thy judgment ... do not condemn me, for I have condemned myself, do not condemn me, for I love Thee, O Lord. I am a wretch, but I love Thee. If Thou sendest me to hell, I shall love Thee there, and from there I shall cry out that I love Thee for ever and ever.... But let me love to the end.... Here and now for just five hours ... till the first light of Thy day ... for I love the queen of my soul ... I love her and I cannot help loving her. Thou seest my whole heart.... I shall gallop up, I shall fall before her and say, ‘You are right to pass on and leave me. Farewell and forget your victim ... never fret yourself about me!’”\n\n“Mokroe!” cried Andrey, pointing ahead with his whip.\n\nThrough the pale darkness of the night loomed a solid black mass of buildings, flung down, as it were, in the vast plain. The village of Mokroe numbered two thousand inhabitants, but at that hour all were asleep, and only here and there a few lights still twinkled.\n\n“Drive on, Andrey, I come!” Mitya exclaimed, feverishly.\n\n“They’re not asleep,” said Andrey again, pointing with his whip to the Plastunovs’ inn, which was at the entrance to the village. The six windows, looking on the street, were all brightly lighted up.\n\n“They’re not asleep,” Mitya repeated joyously. “Quicker, Andrey! Gallop! Drive up with a dash! Set the bells ringing! Let all know that I have come. I’m coming! I’m coming, too!”\n\nAndrey lashed his exhausted team into a gallop, drove with a dash and pulled up his steaming, panting horses at the high flight of steps.\n\nMitya jumped out of the cart just as the innkeeper, on his way to bed, peeped out from the steps curious to see who had arrived.\n\n“Trifon Borissovitch, is that you?”\n\nThe innkeeper bent down, looked intently, ran down the steps, and rushed up to the guest with obsequious delight.\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, your honor! Do I see you again?”\n\nTrifon Borissovitch was a thick-set, healthy peasant, of middle height, with a rather fat face. His expression was severe and uncompromising, especially with the peasants of Mokroe, but he had the power of assuming the most obsequious countenance, when he had an inkling that it was to his interest. He dressed in Russian style, with a shirt buttoning down on one side, and a full-skirted coat. He had saved a good sum of money, but was for ever dreaming of improving his position. More than half the peasants were in his clutches, every one in the neighborhood was in debt to him. From the neighboring landowners he bought and rented lands which were worked by the peasants, in payment of debts which they could never shake off. He was a widower, with four grown-up daughters. One of them was already a widow and lived in the inn with her two children, his grandchildren, and worked for him like a charwoman. Another of his daughters was married to a petty official, and in one of the rooms of the inn, on the wall could be seen, among the family photographs, a miniature photograph of this official in uniform and official epaulettes. The two younger daughters used to wear fashionable blue or green dresses, fitting tight at the back, and with trains a yard long, on Church holidays or when they went to pay visits. But next morning they would get up at dawn, as usual, sweep out the rooms with a birch-broom, empty the slops, and clean up after lodgers.\n\nIn spite of the thousands of roubles he had saved, Trifon Borissovitch was very fond of emptying the pockets of a drunken guest, and remembering that not a month ago he had, in twenty-four hours, made two if not three hundred roubles out of Dmitri, when he had come on his escapade with Grushenka, he met him now with eager welcome, scenting his prey the moment Mitya drove up to the steps.\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear sir, we see you once more!”\n\n“Stay, Trifon Borissovitch,” began Mitya, “first and foremost, where is she?”\n\n“Agrafena Alexandrovna?” The inn-keeper understood at once, looking sharply into Mitya’s face. “She’s here, too ...”\n\n“With whom? With whom?”\n\n“Some strangers. One is an official gentleman, a Pole, to judge from his speech. He sent the horses for her from here; and there’s another with him, a friend of his, or a fellow traveler, there’s no telling. They’re dressed like civilians.”\n\n“Well, are they feasting? Have they money?”\n\n“Poor sort of a feast! Nothing to boast of, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“Nothing to boast of? And who are the others?”\n\n“They’re two gentlemen from the town.... They’ve come back from Tcherny, and are putting up here. One’s quite a young gentleman, a relative of Mr. Miüsov, he must be, but I’ve forgotten his name ... and I expect you know the other, too, a gentleman called Maximov. He’s been on a pilgrimage, so he says, to the monastery in the town. He’s traveling with this young relation of Mr. Miüsov.”\n\n“Is that all?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Stay, listen, Trifon Borissovitch. Tell me the chief thing: What of her? How is she?”\n\n“Oh, she’s only just come. She’s sitting with them.”\n\n“Is she cheerful? Is she laughing?”\n\n“No, I think she’s not laughing much. She’s sitting quite dull. She’s combing the young gentleman’s hair.”\n\n“The Pole—the officer?”\n\n“He’s not young, and he’s not an officer, either. Not him, sir. It’s the young gentleman that’s Mr. Miüsov’s relation ... I’ve forgotten his name.”\n\n“Kalganov.”\n\n“That’s it, Kalganov!”\n\n“All right. I’ll see for myself. Are they playing cards?”\n\n“They have been playing, but they’ve left off. They’ve been drinking tea, the official gentleman asked for liqueurs.”\n\n“Stay, Trifon Borissovitch, stay, my good soul, I’ll see for myself. Now answer one more question: are the gypsies here?”\n\n“You can’t have the gypsies now, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. The authorities have sent them away. But we’ve Jews that play the cymbals and the fiddle in the village, so one might send for them. They’d come.”\n\n“Send for them. Certainly send for them!” cried Mitya. “And you can get the girls together as you did then, Marya especially, Stepanida, too, and Arina. Two hundred roubles for a chorus!”\n\n“Oh, for a sum like that I can get all the village together, though by now they’re asleep. Are the peasants here worth such kindness, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, or the girls either? To spend a sum like that on such coarseness and rudeness! What’s the good of giving a peasant a cigar to smoke, the stinking ruffian! And the girls are all lousy. Besides, I’ll get my daughters up for nothing, let alone a sum like that. They’ve only just gone to bed, I’ll give them a kick and set them singing for you. You gave the peasants champagne to drink the other day, e—ech!”\n\nFor all his pretended compassion for Mitya, Trifon Borissovitch had hidden half a dozen bottles of champagne on that last occasion, and had picked up a hundred-rouble note under the table, and it had remained in his clutches.\n\n“Trifon Borissovitch, I sent more than one thousand flying last time I was here. Do you remember?”\n\n“You did send it flying. I may well remember. You must have left three thousand behind you.”\n\n“Well, I’ve come to do the same again, do you see?”\n\nAnd he pulled out his roll of notes, and held them up before the innkeeper’s nose.\n\n“Now, listen and remember. In an hour’s time the wine will arrive, savories, pies, and sweets—bring them all up at once. That box Andrey has got is to be brought up at once, too. Open it, and hand champagne immediately. And the girls, we must have the girls, Marya especially.”\n\nHe turned to the cart and pulled out the box of pistols.\n\n“Here, Andrey, let’s settle. Here’s fifteen roubles for the drive, and fifty for vodka ... for your readiness, for your love.... Remember Karamazov!”\n\n“I’m afraid, sir,” faltered Andrey. “Give me five roubles extra, but more I won’t take. Trifon Borissovitch, bear witness. Forgive my foolish words ...”\n\n“What are you afraid of?” asked Mitya, scanning him. “Well, go to the devil, if that’s it!” he cried, flinging him five roubles. “Now, Trifon Borissovitch, take me up quietly and let me first get a look at them, so that they don’t see me. Where are they? In the blue room?”\n\nTrifon Borissovitch looked apprehensively at Mitya, but at once obediently did his bidding. Leading him into the passage, he went himself into the first large room, adjoining that in which the visitors were sitting, and took the light away. Then he stealthily led Mitya in, and put him in a corner in the dark, whence he could freely watch the company without being seen. But Mitya did not look long, and, indeed, he could not see them, he saw her, his heart throbbed violently, and all was dark before his eyes.\n\nShe was sitting sideways to the table in a low chair, and beside her, on the sofa, was the pretty youth, Kalganov. She was holding his hand and seemed to be laughing, while he, seeming vexed and not looking at her, was saying something in a loud voice to Maximov, who sat the other side of the table, facing Grushenka. Maximov was laughing violently at something. On the sofa sat he, and on a chair by the sofa there was another stranger. The one on the sofa was lolling backwards, smoking a pipe, and Mitya had an impression of a stoutish, broad-faced, short little man, who was apparently angry about something. His friend, the other stranger, struck Mitya as extraordinarily tall, but he could make out nothing more. He caught his breath. He could not bear it for a minute, he put the pistol-case on a chest, and with a throbbing heart he walked, feeling cold all over, straight into the blue room to face the company.\n\n“Aie!” shrieked Grushenka, the first to notice him.\n\n# Chapter VII. The First And Rightful Lover\n\nWith his long, rapid strides, Mitya walked straight up to the table.\n\n“Gentlemen,” he said in a loud voice, almost shouting, yet stammering at every word, “I ... I’m all right! Don’t be afraid!” he exclaimed, “I—there’s nothing the matter,” he turned suddenly to Grushenka, who had shrunk back in her chair towards Kalganov, and clasped his hand tightly. “I ... I’m coming, too. I’m here till morning. Gentlemen, may I stay with you till morning? Only till morning, for the last time, in this same room?”\n\nSo he finished, turning to the fat little man, with the pipe, sitting on the sofa. The latter removed his pipe from his lips with dignity and observed severely:\n\n“Panie, we’re here in private. There are other rooms.”\n\n“Why, it’s you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch! What do you mean?” answered Kalganov suddenly. “Sit down with us. How are you?”\n\n“Delighted to see you, dear ... and precious fellow, I always thought a lot of you.” Mitya responded, joyfully and eagerly, at once holding out his hand across the table.\n\n“Aie! How tight you squeeze! You’ve quite broken my fingers,” laughed Kalganov.\n\n“He always squeezes like that, always,” Grushenka put in gayly, with a timid smile, seeming suddenly convinced from Mitya’s face that he was not going to make a scene. She was watching him with intense curiosity and still some uneasiness. She was impressed by something about him, and indeed the last thing she expected of him was that he would come in and speak like this at such a moment.\n\n“Good evening,” Maximov ventured blandly on the left. Mitya rushed up to him, too.\n\n“Good evening. You’re here, too! How glad I am to find you here, too! Gentlemen, gentlemen, I—” (He addressed the Polish gentleman with the pipe again, evidently taking him for the most important person present.) “I flew here.... I wanted to spend my last day, my last hour in this room, in this very room ... where I, too, adored ... my queen.... Forgive me, panie,” he cried wildly, “I flew here and vowed— Oh, don’t be afraid, it’s my last night! Let’s drink to our good understanding. They’ll bring the wine at once.... I brought this with me.” (Something made him pull out his bundle of notes.) “Allow me, panie! I want to have music, singing, a revel, as we had before. But the worm, the unnecessary worm, will crawl away, and there’ll be no more of him. I will commemorate my day of joy on my last night.”\n\nHe was almost choking. There was so much, so much he wanted to say, but strange exclamations were all that came from his lips. The Pole gazed fixedly at him, at the bundle of notes in his hand; looked at Grushenka, and was in evident perplexity.\n\n“If my suverin lady is permitting—” he was beginning.\n\n“What does ‘suverin’ mean? ‘Sovereign,’ I suppose?” interrupted Grushenka. “I can’t help laughing at you, the way you talk. Sit down, Mitya, what are you talking about? Don’t frighten us, please. You won’t frighten us, will you? If you won’t, I am glad to see you ...”\n\n“Me, me frighten you?” cried Mitya, flinging up his hands. “Oh, pass me by, go your way, I won’t hinder you!...”\n\nAnd suddenly he surprised them all, and no doubt himself as well, by flinging himself on a chair, and bursting into tears, turning his head away to the opposite wall, while his arms clasped the back of the chair tight, as though embracing it.\n\n“Come, come, what a fellow you are!” cried Grushenka reproachfully. “That’s just how he comes to see me—he begins talking, and I can’t make out what he means. He cried like that once before, and now he’s crying again! It’s shameful! Why are you crying? As though you had anything to cry for!” she added enigmatically, emphasizing each word with some irritability.\n\n“I ... I’m not crying.... Well, good evening!” He instantly turned round in his chair, and suddenly laughed, not his abrupt wooden laugh, but a long, quivering, inaudible nervous laugh.\n\n“Well, there you are again.... Come, cheer up, cheer up!” Grushenka said to him persuasively. “I’m very glad you’ve come, very glad, Mitya, do you hear, I’m very glad! I want him to stay here with us,” she said peremptorily, addressing the whole company, though her words were obviously meant for the man sitting on the sofa. “I wish it, I wish it! And if he goes away I shall go, too!” she added with flashing eyes.\n\n“What my queen commands is law!” pronounced the Pole, gallantly kissing Grushenka’s hand. “I beg you, panie, to join our company,” he added politely, addressing Mitya.\n\nMitya was jumping up with the obvious intention of delivering another tirade, but the words did not come.\n\n“Let’s drink, panie,” he blurted out instead of making a speech. Every one laughed.\n\n“Good heavens! I thought he was going to begin again!” Grushenka exclaimed nervously. “Do you hear, Mitya,” she went on insistently, “don’t prance about, but it’s nice you’ve brought the champagne. I want some myself, and I can’t bear liqueurs. And best of all, you’ve come yourself. We were fearfully dull here.... You’ve come for a spree again, I suppose? But put your money in your pocket. Where did you get such a lot?”\n\nMitya had been, all this time, holding in his hand the crumpled bundle of notes on which the eyes of all, especially of the Poles, were fixed. In confusion he thrust them hurriedly into his pocket. He flushed. At that moment the innkeeper brought in an uncorked bottle of champagne, and glasses on a tray. Mitya snatched up the bottle, but he was so bewildered that he did not know what to do with it. Kalganov took it from him and poured out the champagne.\n\n“Another! Another bottle!” Mitya cried to the innkeeper, and, forgetting to clink glasses with the Pole whom he had so solemnly invited to drink to their good understanding, he drank off his glass without waiting for any one else. His whole countenance suddenly changed. The solemn and tragic expression with which he had entered vanished completely, and a look of something childlike came into his face. He seemed to have become suddenly gentle and subdued. He looked shyly and happily at every one, with a continual nervous little laugh, and the blissful expression of a dog who has done wrong, been punished, and forgiven. He seemed to have forgotten everything, and was looking round at every one with a childlike smile of delight. He looked at Grushenka, laughing continually, and bringing his chair close up to her. By degrees he had gained some idea of the two Poles, though he had formed no definite conception of them yet.\n\nThe Pole on the sofa struck him by his dignified demeanor and his Polish accent; and, above all, by his pipe. “Well, what of it? It’s a good thing he’s smoking a pipe,” he reflected. The Pole’s puffy, middle-aged face, with its tiny nose and two very thin, pointed, dyed and impudent-looking mustaches, had not so far roused the faintest doubts in Mitya. He was not even particularly struck by the Pole’s absurd wig made in Siberia, with love-locks foolishly combed forward over the temples. “I suppose it’s all right since he wears a wig,” he went on, musing blissfully. The other, younger Pole, who was staring insolently and defiantly at the company and listening to the conversation with silent contempt, still only impressed Mitya by his great height, which was in striking contrast to the Pole on the sofa. “If he stood up he’d be six foot three.” The thought flitted through Mitya’s mind. It occurred to him, too, that this Pole must be the friend of the other, as it were, a “bodyguard,” and no doubt the big Pole was at the disposal of the little Pole with the pipe. But this all seemed to Mitya perfectly right and not to be questioned. In his mood of doglike submissiveness all feeling of rivalry had died away.\n\nGrushenka’s mood and the enigmatic tone of some of her words he completely failed to grasp. All he understood, with thrilling heart, was that she was kind to him, that she had forgiven him, and made him sit by her. He was beside himself with delight, watching her sip her glass of champagne. The silence of the company seemed somehow to strike him, however, and he looked round at every one with expectant eyes.\n\n“Why are we sitting here though, gentlemen? Why don’t you begin doing something?” his smiling eyes seemed to ask.\n\n“He keeps talking nonsense, and we were all laughing,” Kalganov began suddenly, as though divining his thought, and pointing to Maximov.\n\nMitya immediately stared at Kalganov and then at Maximov.\n\n“He’s talking nonsense?” he laughed, his short, wooden laugh, seeming suddenly delighted at something—“ha ha!”\n\n“Yes. Would you believe it, he will have it that all our cavalry officers in the twenties married Polish women. That’s awful rot, isn’t it?”\n\n“Polish women?” repeated Mitya, perfectly ecstatic.\n\nKalganov was well aware of Mitya’s attitude to Grushenka, and he guessed about the Pole, too, but that did not so much interest him, perhaps did not interest him at all; what he was interested in was Maximov. He had come here with Maximov by chance, and he met the Poles here at the inn for the first time in his life. Grushenka he knew before, and had once been with some one to see her; but she had not taken to him. But here she looked at him very affectionately: before Mitya’s arrival, she had been making much of him, but he seemed somehow to be unmoved by it. He was a boy, not over twenty, dressed like a dandy, with a very charming fair-skinned face, and splendid thick, fair hair. From his fair face looked out beautiful pale blue eyes, with an intelligent and sometimes even deep expression, beyond his age indeed, although the young man sometimes looked and talked quite like a child, and was not at all ashamed of it, even when he was aware of it himself. As a rule he was very willful, even capricious, though always friendly. Sometimes there was something fixed and obstinate in his expression. He would look at you and listen, seeming all the while to be persistently dreaming over something else. Often he was listless and lazy, at other times he would grow excited, sometimes, apparently, over the most trivial matters.\n\n“Only imagine, I’ve been taking him about with me for the last four days,” he went on, indolently drawling his words, quite naturally though, without the slightest affectation. “Ever since your brother, do you remember, shoved him off the carriage and sent him flying. That made me take an interest in him at the time, and I took him into the country, but he keeps talking such rot I’m ashamed to be with him. I’m taking him back.”\n\n“The gentleman has not seen Polish ladies, and says what is impossible,” the Pole with the pipe observed to Maximov.\n\nHe spoke Russian fairly well, much better, anyway, than he pretended. If he used Russian words, he always distorted them into a Polish form.\n\n“But I was married to a Polish lady myself,” tittered Maximov.\n\n“But did you serve in the cavalry? You were talking about the cavalry. Were you a cavalry officer?” put in Kalganov at once.\n\n“Was he a cavalry officer indeed? Ha ha!” cried Mitya, listening eagerly, and turning his inquiring eyes to each as he spoke, as though there were no knowing what he might hear from each.\n\n“No, you see,” Maximov turned to him. “What I mean is that those pretty Polish ladies ... when they danced the mazurka with our Uhlans ... when one of them dances a mazurka with a Uhlan she jumps on his knee like a kitten ... a little white one ... and the pan-father and pan-mother look on and allow it.... They allow it ... and next day the Uhlan comes and offers her his hand.... That’s how it is ... offers her his hand, he he!” Maximov ended, tittering.\n\n“The pan is a lajdak!” the tall Pole on the chair growled suddenly and crossed one leg over the other. Mitya’s eye was caught by his huge greased boot, with its thick, dirty sole. The dress of both the Poles looked rather greasy.\n\n“Well, now it’s lajdak! What’s he scolding about?” said Grushenka, suddenly vexed.\n\n“Pani Agrippina, what the gentleman saw in Poland were servant girls, and not ladies of good birth,” the Pole with the pipe observed to Grushenka.\n\n“You can reckon on that,” the tall Pole snapped contemptuously.\n\n“What next! Let him talk! People talk, why hinder them? It makes it cheerful,” Grushenka said crossly.\n\n“I’m not hindering them, pani,” said the Pole in the wig, with a long look at Grushenka, and relapsing into dignified silence he sucked his pipe again.\n\n“No, no. The Polish gentleman spoke the truth.” Kalganov got excited again, as though it were a question of vast import. “He’s never been in Poland, so how can he talk about it? I suppose you weren’t married in Poland, were you?”\n\n“No, in the Province of Smolensk. Only, a Uhlan had brought her to Russia before that, my future wife, with her mamma and her aunt, and another female relation with a grown-up son. He brought her straight from Poland and gave her up to me. He was a lieutenant in our regiment, a very nice young man. At first he meant to marry her himself. But he didn’t marry her, because she turned out to be lame.”\n\n“So you married a lame woman?” cried Kalganov.\n\n“Yes. They both deceived me a little bit at the time, and concealed it. I thought she was hopping; she kept hopping.... I thought it was for fun.”\n\n“So pleased she was going to marry you!” yelled Kalganov, in a ringing, childish voice.\n\n“Yes, so pleased. But it turned out to be quite a different cause. Afterwards, when we were married, after the wedding, that very evening, she confessed, and very touchingly asked forgiveness. ‘I once jumped over a puddle when I was a child,’ she said, ‘and injured my leg.’ He he!”\n\nKalganov went off into the most childish laughter, almost falling on the sofa. Grushenka, too, laughed. Mitya was at the pinnacle of happiness.\n\n“Do you know, that’s the truth, he’s not lying now,” exclaimed Kalganov, turning to Mitya; “and do you know, he’s been married twice; it’s his first wife he’s talking about. But his second wife, do you know, ran away, and is alive now.”\n\n“Is it possible?” said Mitya, turning quickly to Maximov with an expression of the utmost astonishment.\n\n“Yes. She did run away. I’ve had that unpleasant experience,” Maximov modestly assented, “with a monsieur. And what was worse, she’d had all my little property transferred to her beforehand. ‘You’re an educated man,’ she said to me. ‘You can always get your living.’ She settled my business with that. A venerable bishop once said to me: ‘One of your wives was lame, but the other was too light-footed.’ He he!”\n\n“Listen, listen!” cried Kalganov, bubbling over, “if he’s telling lies—and he often is—he’s only doing it to amuse us all. There’s no harm in that, is there? You know, I sometimes like him. He’s awfully low, but it’s natural to him, eh? Don’t you think so? Some people are low from self-interest, but he’s simply so, from nature. Only fancy, he claims (he was arguing about it all the way yesterday) that Gogol wrote Dead Souls about him. Do you remember, there’s a landowner called Maximov in it, whom Nozdryov thrashed. He was charged, do you remember, ‘for inflicting bodily injury with rods on the landowner Maximov in a drunken condition.’ Would you believe it, he claims that he was that Maximov and that he was beaten! Now can it be so? Tchitchikov made his journey, at the very latest, at the beginning of the twenties, so that the dates don’t fit. He couldn’t have been thrashed then, he couldn’t, could he?”\n\nIt was difficult to imagine what Kalganov was excited about, but his excitement was genuine. Mitya followed his lead without protest.\n\n“Well, but if they did thrash him!” he cried, laughing.\n\n“It’s not that they thrashed me exactly, but what I mean is—” put in Maximov.\n\n“What do you mean? Either they thrashed you or they didn’t.”\n\n“What o’clock is it, panie?” the Pole, with the pipe, asked his tall friend, with a bored expression. The other shrugged his shoulders in reply. Neither of them had a watch.\n\n“Why not talk? Let other people talk. Mustn’t other people talk because you’re bored?” Grushenka flew at him with evident intention of finding fault. Something seemed for the first time to flash upon Mitya’s mind. This time the Pole answered with unmistakable irritability.\n\n“Pani, I didn’t oppose it. I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“All right then. Come, tell us your story,” Grushenka cried to Maximov. “Why are you all silent?”\n\n“There’s nothing to tell, it’s all so foolish,” answered Maximov at once, with evident satisfaction, mincing a little. “Besides, all that’s by way of allegory in Gogol, for he’s made all the names have a meaning. Nozdryov was really called Nosov, and Kuvshinikov had quite a different name, he was called Shkvornev. Fenardi really was called Fenardi, only he wasn’t an Italian but a Russian, and Mamsel Fenardi was a pretty girl with her pretty little legs in tights, and she had a little short skirt with spangles, and she kept turning round and round, only not for four hours but for four minutes only, and she bewitched every one...”\n\n“But what were you beaten for?” cried Kalganov.\n\n“For Piron!” answered Maximov.\n\n“What Piron?” cried Mitya.\n\n“The famous French writer, Piron. We were all drinking then, a big party of us, in a tavern at that very fair. They’d invited me, and first of all I began quoting epigrams. ‘Is that you, Boileau? What a funny get-up!’ and Boileau answers that he’s going to a masquerade, that is to the baths, he he! And they took it to themselves, so I made haste to repeat another, very sarcastic, well known to all educated people:\n\nYes, Sappho and Phaon are we! But one grief is weighing on me. You don’t know your way to the sea!\n\nThey were still more offended and began abusing me in the most unseemly way for it. And as ill-luck would have it, to set things right, I began telling a very cultivated anecdote about Piron, how he was not accepted into the French Academy, and to revenge himself wrote his own epitaph:\n\nCi-gît Piron qui ne fut rien, Pas même académicien.\n\nThey seized me and thrashed me.”\n\n“But what for? What for?”\n“For my education. People can thrash a man for anything,” Maximov concluded, briefly and sententiously.\n\n“Eh, that’s enough! That’s all stupid, I don’t want to listen. I thought it would be amusing,” Grushenka cut them short, suddenly.\n\nMitya started, and at once left off laughing. The tall Pole rose upon his feet, and with the haughty air of a man, bored and out of his element, began pacing from corner to corner of the room, his hands behind his back.\n\n“Ah, he can’t sit still,” said Grushenka, looking at him contemptuously. Mitya began to feel anxious. He noticed besides, that the Pole on the sofa was looking at him with an irritable expression.\n\n“Panie!” cried Mitya, “let’s drink! and the other pan, too! Let us drink.”\n\nIn a flash he had pulled three glasses towards him, and filled them with champagne.\n\n“To Poland, panovie, I drink to your Poland!” cried Mitya.\n\n“I shall be delighted, panie,” said the Pole on the sofa, with dignity and affable condescension, and he took his glass.\n\n“And the other pan, what’s his name? Drink, most illustrious, take your glass!” Mitya urged.\n\n“Pan Vrublevsky,” put in the Pole on the sofa.\n\nPan Vrublevsky came up to the table, swaying as he walked.\n\n“To Poland, panovie!” cried Mitya, raising his glass. “Hurrah!”\n\nAll three drank. Mitya seized the bottle and again poured out three glasses.\n\n“Now to Russia, panovie, and let us be brothers!”\n\n“Pour out some for us,” said Grushenka; “I’ll drink to Russia, too!”\n\n“So will I,” said Kalganov.\n\n“And I would, too ... to Russia, the old grandmother!” tittered Maximov.\n\n“All! All!” cried Mitya. “Trifon Borissovitch, some more bottles!”\n\nThe other three bottles Mitya had brought with him were put on the table. Mitya filled the glasses.\n\n“To Russia! Hurrah!” he shouted again. All drank the toast except the Poles, and Grushenka tossed off her whole glass at once. The Poles did not touch theirs.\n\n“How’s this, panovie?” cried Mitya, “won’t you drink it?”\n\nPan Vrublevsky took the glass, raised it and said with a resonant voice:\n\n“To Russia as she was before 1772.”\n\n“Come, that’s better!” cried the other Pole, and they both emptied their glasses at once.\n\n“You’re fools, you panovie,” broke suddenly from Mitya.\n\n“Panie!” shouted both the Poles, menacingly, setting on Mitya like a couple of cocks. Pan Vrublevsky was specially furious.\n\n“Can one help loving one’s own country?” he shouted.\n\n“Be silent! Don’t quarrel! I won’t have any quarreling!” cried Grushenka imperiously, and she stamped her foot on the floor. Her face glowed, her eyes were shining. The effects of the glass she had just drunk were apparent. Mitya was terribly alarmed.\n\n“Panovie, forgive me! It was my fault, I’m sorry. Vrublevsky, panie Vrublevsky, I’m sorry.”\n\n“Hold your tongue, you, anyway! Sit down, you stupid!” Grushenka scolded with angry annoyance.\n\nEvery one sat down, all were silent, looking at one another.\n\n“Gentlemen, I was the cause of it all,” Mitya began again, unable to make anything of Grushenka’s words. “Come, why are we sitting here? What shall we do ... to amuse ourselves again?”\n\n“Ach, it’s certainly anything but amusing!” Kalganov mumbled lazily.\n\n“Let’s play faro again, as we did just now,” Maximov tittered suddenly.\n\n“Faro? Splendid!” cried Mitya. “If only the panovie—”\n\n“It’s lite, panovie,” the Pole on the sofa responded, as it were unwillingly.\n\n“That’s true,” assented Pan Vrublevsky.\n\n“Lite? What do you mean by ‘lite’?” asked Grushenka.\n\n“Late, pani! ‘a late hour’ I mean,” the Pole on the sofa explained.\n\n“It’s always late with them. They can never do anything!” Grushenka almost shrieked in her anger. “They’re dull themselves, so they want others to be dull. Before you came, Mitya, they were just as silent and kept turning up their noses at me.”\n\n“My goddess!” cried the Pole on the sofa, “I see you’re not well-disposed to me, that’s why I’m gloomy. I’m ready, panie,” added he, addressing Mitya.\n\n“Begin, panie,” Mitya assented, pulling his notes out of his pocket, and laying two hundred-rouble notes on the table. “I want to lose a lot to you. Take your cards. Make the bank.”\n\n“We’ll have cards from the landlord, panie,” said the little Pole, gravely and emphatically.\n\n“That’s much the best way,” chimed in Pan Vrublevsky.\n\n“From the landlord? Very good, I understand, let’s get them from him. Cards!” Mitya shouted to the landlord.\n\nThe landlord brought in a new, unopened pack, and informed Mitya that the girls were getting ready, and that the Jews with the cymbals would most likely be here soon; but the cart with the provisions had not yet arrived. Mitya jumped up from the table and ran into the next room to give orders, but only three girls had arrived, and Marya was not there yet. And he did not know himself what orders to give and why he had run out. He only told them to take out of the box the presents for the girls, the sweets, the toffee and the fondants. “And vodka for Andrey, vodka for Andrey!” he cried in haste. “I was rude to Andrey!”\n\nSuddenly Maximov, who had followed him out, touched him on the shoulder.\n\n“Give me five roubles,” he whispered to Mitya. “I’ll stake something at faro, too, he he!”\n\n“Capital! Splendid! Take ten, here!”\n\nAgain he took all the notes out of his pocket and picked out one for ten roubles. “And if you lose that, come again, come again.”\n\n“Very good,” Maximov whispered joyfully, and he ran back again. Mitya, too, returned, apologizing for having kept them waiting. The Poles had already sat down, and opened the pack. They looked much more amiable, almost cordial. The Pole on the sofa had lighted another pipe and was preparing to throw. He wore an air of solemnity.\n\n“To your places, gentlemen,” cried Pan Vrublevsky.\n\n“No, I’m not going to play any more,” observed Kalganov, “I’ve lost fifty roubles to them just now.”\n\n“The pan had no luck, perhaps he’ll be lucky this time,” the Pole on the sofa observed in his direction.\n\n“How much in the bank? To correspond?” asked Mitya.\n\n“That’s according, panie, maybe a hundred, maybe two hundred, as much as you will stake.”\n\n“A million!” laughed Mitya.\n\n“The Pan Captain has heard of Pan Podvysotsky, perhaps?”\n\n“What Podvysotsky?”\n\n“In Warsaw there was a bank and any one comes and stakes against it. Podvysotsky comes, sees a thousand gold pieces, stakes against the bank. The banker says, ‘Panie Podvysotsky, are you laying down the gold, or must we trust to your honor?’ ‘To my honor, panie,’ says Podvysotsky. ‘So much the better.’ The banker throws the dice. Podvysotsky wins. ‘Take it, panie,’ says the banker, and pulling out the drawer he gives him a million. ‘Take it, panie, this is your gain.’ There was a million in the bank. ‘I didn’t know that,’ says Podvysotsky. ‘Panie Podvysotsky,’ said the banker, ‘you pledged your honor and we pledged ours.’ Podvysotsky took the million.”\n\n“That’s not true,” said Kalganov.\n\n“Panie Kalganov, in gentlemanly society one doesn’t say such things.”\n\n“As if a Polish gambler would give away a million!” cried Mitya, but checked himself at once. “Forgive me, panie, it’s my fault again, he would, he would give away a million, for honor, for Polish honor. You see how I talk Polish, ha ha! Here, I stake ten roubles, the knave leads.”\n\n“And I put a rouble on the queen, the queen of hearts, the pretty little panienotchka, he he!” laughed Maximov, pulling out his queen, and, as though trying to conceal it from every one, he moved right up and crossed himself hurriedly under the table. Mitya won. The rouble won, too.\n\n“A corner!” cried Mitya.\n\n“I’ll bet another rouble, a ‘single’ stake,” Maximov muttered gleefully, hugely delighted at having won a rouble.\n\n“Lost!” shouted Mitya. “A ‘double’ on the seven!”\n\nThe seven too was trumped.\n\n“Stop!” cried Kalganov suddenly.\n\n“Double! Double!” Mitya doubled his stakes, and each time he doubled the stake, the card he doubled was trumped by the Poles. The rouble stakes kept winning.\n\n“On the double!” shouted Mitya furiously.\n\n“You’ve lost two hundred, panie. Will you stake another hundred?” the Pole on the sofa inquired.\n\n“What? Lost two hundred already? Then another two hundred! All doubles!”\n\nAnd pulling his money out of his pocket, Mitya was about to fling two hundred roubles on the queen, but Kalganov covered it with his hand.\n\n“That’s enough!” he shouted in his ringing voice.\n\n“What’s the matter?” Mitya stared at him.\n\n“That’s enough! I don’t want you to play any more. Don’t!”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I don’t. Hang it, come away. That’s why. I won’t let you go on playing.”\n\nMitya gazed at him in astonishment.\n\n“Give it up, Mitya. He may be right. You’ve lost a lot as it is,” said Grushenka, with a curious note in her voice. Both the Poles rose from their seats with a deeply offended air.\n\n“Are you joking, panie?” said the short man, looking severely at Kalganov.\n\n“How dare you!” Pan Vrublevsky, too, growled at Kalganov.\n\n“Don’t dare to shout like that,” cried Grushenka. “Ah, you turkey-cocks!”\n\nMitya looked at each of them in turn. But something in Grushenka’s face suddenly struck him, and at the same instant something new flashed into his mind—a strange new thought!\n\n“Pani Agrippina,” the little Pole was beginning, crimson with anger, when Mitya suddenly went up to him and slapped him on the shoulder.\n\n“Most illustrious, two words with you.”\n\n“What do you want?”\n\n“In the next room, I’ve two words to say to you, something pleasant, very pleasant. You’ll be glad to hear it.”\n\nThe little pan was taken aback and looked apprehensively at Mitya. He agreed at once, however, on condition that Pan Vrublevsky went with them.\n\n“The bodyguard? Let him come, and I want him, too. I must have him!” cried Mitya. “March, panovie!”\n\n“Where are you going?” asked Grushenka, anxiously.\n\n“We’ll be back in one moment,” answered Mitya.\n\nThere was a sort of boldness, a sudden confidence shining in his eyes. His face had looked very different when he entered the room an hour before.\n\nHe led the Poles, not into the large room where the chorus of girls was assembling and the table was being laid, but into the bedroom on the right, where the trunks and packages were kept, and there were two large beds, with pyramids of cotton pillows on each. There was a lighted candle on a small deal table in the corner. The small man and Mitya sat down to this table, facing each other, while the huge Vrublevsky stood beside them, his hands behind his back. The Poles looked severe but were evidently inquisitive.\n\n“What can I do for you, panie?” lisped the little Pole.\n\n“Well, look here, panie, I won’t keep you long. There’s money for you,” he pulled out his notes. “Would you like three thousand? Take it and go your way.”\n\nThe Pole gazed open-eyed at Mitya, with a searching look.\n\n“Three thousand, panie?” He exchanged glances with Vrublevsky.\n\n“Three, panovie, three! Listen, panie, I see you’re a sensible man. Take three thousand and go to the devil, and Vrublevsky with you—d’you hear? But, at once, this very minute, and for ever. You understand that, panie, for ever. Here’s the door, you go out of it. What have you got there, a great-coat, a fur coat? I’ll bring it out to you. They’ll get the horses out directly, and then—good-by, panie!”\n\nMitya awaited an answer with assurance. He had no doubts. An expression of extraordinary resolution passed over the Pole’s face.\n\n“And the money, panie?”\n\n“The money, panie? Five hundred roubles I’ll give you this moment for the journey, and as a first installment, and two thousand five hundred to-morrow, in the town—I swear on my honor, I’ll get it, I’ll get it at any cost!” cried Mitya.\n\nThe Poles exchanged glances again. The short man’s face looked more forbidding.\n\n“Seven hundred, seven hundred, not five hundred, at once, this minute, cash down!” Mitya added, feeling something wrong. “What’s the matter, panie? Don’t you trust me? I can’t give you the whole three thousand straight off. If I give it, you may come back to her to-morrow.... Besides, I haven’t the three thousand with me. I’ve got it at home in the town,” faltered Mitya, his spirit sinking at every word he uttered. “Upon my word, the money’s there, hidden.”\n\nIn an instant an extraordinary sense of personal dignity showed itself in the little man’s face.\n\n“What next?” he asked ironically. “For shame!” and he spat on the floor. Pan Vrublevsky spat too.\n\n“You do that, panie,” said Mitya, recognizing with despair that all was over, “because you hope to make more out of Grushenka? You’re a couple of capons, that’s what you are!”\n\n“This is a mortal insult!” The little Pole turned as red as a crab, and he went out of the room, briskly, as though unwilling to hear another word. Vrublevsky swung out after him, and Mitya followed, confused and crestfallen. He was afraid of Grushenka, afraid that the pan would at once raise an outcry. And so indeed he did. The Pole walked into the room and threw himself in a theatrical attitude before Grushenka.\n\n“Pani Agrippina, I have received a mortal insult!” he exclaimed. But Grushenka suddenly lost all patience, as though they had wounded her in the tenderest spot.\n\n“Speak Russian! Speak Russian!” she cried, “not another word of Polish! You used to talk Russian. You can’t have forgotten it in five years.”\n\nShe was red with passion.\n\n“Pani Agrippina—”\n\n“My name’s Agrafena, Grushenka, speak Russian or I won’t listen!”\n\nThe Pole gasped with offended dignity, and quickly and pompously delivered himself in broken Russian:\n\n“Pani Agrafena, I came here to forget the past and forgive it, to forget all that has happened till to-day—”\n\n“Forgive? Came here to forgive me?” Grushenka cut him short, jumping up from her seat.\n\n“Just so, pani, I’m not pusillanimous, I’m magnanimous. But I was astounded when I saw your lovers. Pan Mitya offered me three thousand, in the other room to depart. I spat in the pan’s face.”\n\n“What? He offered you money for me?” cried Grushenka, hysterically. “Is it true, Mitya? How dare you? Am I for sale?”\n\n“Panie, panie!” yelled Mitya, “she’s pure and shining, and I have never been her lover! That’s a lie....”\n\n“How dare you defend me to him?” shrieked Grushenka. “It wasn’t virtue kept me pure, and it wasn’t that I was afraid of Kuzma, but that I might hold up my head when I met him, and tell him he’s a scoundrel. And he did actually refuse the money?”\n\n“He took it! He took it!” cried Mitya; “only he wanted to get the whole three thousand at once, and I could only give him seven hundred straight off.”\n\n“I see: he heard I had money, and came here to marry me!”\n\n“Pani Agrippina!” cried the little Pole. “I’m—a knight, I’m—a nobleman, and not a lajdak. I came here to make you my wife and I find you a different woman, perverse and shameless.”\n\n“Oh, go back where you came from! I’ll tell them to turn you out and you’ll be turned out,” cried Grushenka, furious. “I’ve been a fool, a fool, to have been miserable these five years! And it wasn’t for his sake, it was my anger made me miserable. And this isn’t he at all! Was he like this? It might be his father! Where did you get your wig from? He was a falcon, but this is a gander. He used to laugh and sing to me.... And I’ve been crying for five years, damned fool, abject, shameless I was!”\n\nShe sank back in her low chair and hid her face in her hands. At that instant the chorus of Mokroe began singing in the room on the left—a rollicking dance song.\n\n“A regular Sodom!” Vrublevsky roared suddenly. “Landlord, send the shameless hussies away!”\n\nThe landlord, who had been for some time past inquisitively peeping in at the door, hearing shouts and guessing that his guests were quarreling, at once entered the room.\n\n“What are you shouting for? D’you want to split your throat?” he said, addressing Vrublevsky, with surprising rudeness.\n\n“Animal!” bellowed Pan Vrublevsky.\n\n“Animal? And what sort of cards were you playing with just now? I gave you a pack and you hid it. You played with marked cards! I could send you to Siberia for playing with false cards, d’you know that, for it’s just the same as false banknotes....”\n\nAnd going up to the sofa he thrust his fingers between the sofa back and the cushion, and pulled out an unopened pack of cards.\n\n“Here’s my pack unopened!”\n\nHe held it up and showed it to all in the room. “From where I stood I saw him slip my pack away, and put his in place of it—you’re a cheat and not a gentleman!”\n\n“And I twice saw the pan change a card!” cried Kalganov.\n\n“How shameful! How shameful!” exclaimed Grushenka, clasping her hands, and blushing for genuine shame. “Good Lord, he’s come to that!”\n\n“I thought so, too!” said Mitya. But before he had uttered the words, Vrublevsky, with a confused and infuriated face, shook his fist at Grushenka, shouting:\n\n“You low harlot!”\n\nMitya flew at him at once, clutched him in both hands, lifted him in the air, and in one instant had carried him into the room on the right, from which they had just come.\n\n“I’ve laid him on the floor, there,” he announced, returning at once, gasping with excitement. “He’s struggling, the scoundrel! But he won’t come back, no fear of that!...”\n\nHe closed one half of the folding doors, and holding the other ajar called out to the little Pole:\n\n“Most illustrious, will you be pleased to retire as well?”\n\n“My dear Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” said Trifon Borissovitch, “make them give you back the money you lost. It’s as good as stolen from you.”\n\n“I don’t want my fifty roubles back,” Kalganov declared suddenly.\n\n“I don’t want my two hundred, either,” cried Mitya, “I wouldn’t take it for anything! Let him keep it as a consolation.”\n\n“Bravo, Mitya! You’re a trump, Mitya!” cried Grushenka, and there was a note of fierce anger in the exclamation.\n\nThe little pan, crimson with fury but still mindful of his dignity, was making for the door, but he stopped short and said suddenly, addressing Grushenka:\n\n“Pani, if you want to come with me, come. If not, good-by.”\n\nAnd swelling with indignation and importance he went to the door. This was a man of character: he had so good an opinion of himself that after all that had passed, he still expected that she would marry him. Mitya slammed the door after him.\n\n“Lock it,” said Kalganov. But the key clicked on the other side, they had locked it from within.\n\n“That’s capital!” exclaimed Grushenka relentlessly. “Serve them right!”\n\n# Chapter VIII. Delirium\n\nWhat followed was almost an orgy, a feast to which all were welcome. Grushenka was the first to call for wine.\n\n“I want to drink. I want to be quite drunk, as we were before. Do you remember, Mitya, do you remember how we made friends here last time!”\n\nMitya himself was almost delirious, feeling that his happiness was at hand. But Grushenka was continually sending him away from her.\n\n“Go and enjoy yourself. Tell them to dance, to make merry, ‘let the stove and cottage dance’; as we had it last time,” she kept exclaiming. She was tremendously excited. And Mitya hastened to obey her. The chorus were in the next room. The room in which they had been sitting till that moment was too small, and was divided in two by cotton curtains, behind which was a huge bed with a puffy feather mattress and a pyramid of cotton pillows. In the four rooms for visitors there were beds. Grushenka settled herself just at the door. Mitya set an easy chair for her. She had sat in the same place to watch the dancing and singing “the time before,” when they had made merry there. All the girls who had come had been there then; the Jewish band with fiddles and zithers had come, too, and at last the long expected cart had arrived with the wines and provisions.\n\nMitya bustled about. All sorts of people began coming into the room to look on, peasants and their women, who had been roused from sleep and attracted by the hopes of another marvelous entertainment such as they had enjoyed a month before. Mitya remembered their faces, greeting and embracing every one he knew. He uncorked bottles and poured out wine for every one who presented himself. Only the girls were very eager for the champagne. The men preferred rum, brandy, and, above all, hot punch. Mitya had chocolate made for all the girls, and ordered that three samovars should be kept boiling all night to provide tea and punch for everyone to help himself.\n\nAn absurd chaotic confusion followed, but Mitya was in his natural element, and the more foolish it became, the more his spirits rose. If the peasants had asked him for money at that moment, he would have pulled out his notes and given them away right and left. This was probably why the landlord, Trifon Borissovitch, kept hovering about Mitya to protect him. He seemed to have given up all idea of going to bed that night; but he drank little, only one glass of punch, and kept a sharp look-out on Mitya’s interests after his own fashion. He intervened in the nick of time, civilly and obsequiously persuading Mitya not to give away “cigars and Rhine wine,” and, above all, money to the peasants as he had done before. He was very indignant, too, at the peasant girls drinking liqueur, and eating sweets.\n\n“They’re a lousy lot, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” he said. “I’d give them a kick, every one of them, and they’d take it as an honor—that’s all they’re worth!”\n\nMitya remembered Andrey again, and ordered punch to be sent out to him. “I was rude to him just now,” he repeated with a sinking, softened voice. Kalganov did not want to drink, and at first did not care for the girls’ singing; but after he had drunk a couple of glasses of champagne he became extraordinarily lively, strolling about the room, laughing and praising the music and the songs, admiring every one and everything. Maximov, blissfully drunk, never left his side. Grushenka, too, was beginning to get drunk. Pointing to Kalganov, she said to Mitya:\n\n“What a dear, charming boy he is!”\n\nAnd Mitya, delighted, ran to kiss Kalganov and Maximov. Oh, great were his hopes! She had said nothing yet, and seemed, indeed, purposely to refrain from speaking. But she looked at him from time to time with caressing and passionate eyes. At last she suddenly gripped his hand and drew him vigorously to her. She was sitting at the moment in the low chair by the door.\n\n“How was it you came just now, eh? Have you walked in!... I was frightened. So you wanted to give me up to him, did you? Did you really want to?”\n\n“I didn’t want to spoil your happiness!” Mitya faltered blissfully. But she did not need his answer.\n\n“Well, go and enjoy yourself ...” she sent him away once more. “Don’t cry, I’ll call you back again.”\n\nHe would run away, and she listened to the singing and looked at the dancing, though her eyes followed him wherever he went. But in another quarter of an hour she would call him once more and again he would run back to her.\n\n“Come, sit beside me, tell me, how did you hear about me, and my coming here yesterday? From whom did you first hear it?”\n\nAnd Mitya began telling her all about it, disconnectedly, incoherently, feverishly. He spoke strangely, often frowning, and stopping abruptly.\n\n“What are you frowning at?” she asked.\n\n“Nothing.... I left a man ill there. I’d give ten years of my life for him to get well, to know he was all right!”\n\n“Well, never mind him, if he’s ill. So you meant to shoot yourself to-morrow! What a silly boy! What for? I like such reckless fellows as you,” she lisped, with a rather halting tongue. “So you would go any length for me, eh? Did you really mean to shoot yourself to-morrow, you stupid? No, wait a little. To-morrow I may have something to say to you.... I won’t say it to-day, but to-morrow. You’d like it to be to-day? No, I don’t want to to-day. Come, go along now, go and amuse yourself.”\n\nOnce, however, she called him, as it were, puzzled and uneasy.\n\n“Why are you sad? I see you’re sad.... Yes, I see it,” she added, looking intently into his eyes. “Though you keep kissing the peasants and shouting, I see something. No, be merry. I’m merry; you be merry, too.... I love somebody here. Guess who it is. Ah, look, my boy has fallen asleep, poor dear, he’s drunk.”\n\nShe meant Kalganov. He was, in fact, drunk, and had dropped asleep for a moment, sitting on the sofa. But he was not merely drowsy from drink; he felt suddenly dejected, or, as he said, “bored.” He was intensely depressed by the girls’ songs, which, as the drinking went on, gradually became coarse and more reckless. And the dances were as bad. Two girls dressed up as bears, and a lively girl, called Stepanida, with a stick in her hand, acted the part of keeper, and began to “show them.”\n\n“Look alive, Marya, or you’ll get the stick!”\n\nThe bears rolled on the ground at last in the most unseemly fashion, amid roars of laughter from the closely-packed crowd of men and women.\n\n“Well, let them! Let them!” said Grushenka sententiously, with an ecstatic expression on her face. “When they do get a day to enjoy themselves, why shouldn’t folks be happy?”\n\nKalganov looked as though he had been besmirched with dirt.\n\n“It’s swinish, all this peasant foolery,” he murmured, moving away; “it’s the game they play when it’s light all night in summer.”\n\nHe particularly disliked one “new” song to a jaunty dance-tune. It described how a gentleman came and tried his luck with the girls, to see whether they would love him:\n\nThe master came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?\n\nBut the girls could not love the master:\n\nHe would beat me cruelly And such love won’t do for me.\n\nThen a gypsy comes along and he, too, tries:\n\nThe gypsy came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?\n\nBut they couldn’t love the gypsy either:\n\nHe would be a thief, I fear, And would cause me many a tear.\n\nAnd many more men come to try their luck, among them a soldier:\n\nThe soldier came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?\n\nBut the soldier is rejected with contempt, in two indecent lines, sung with absolute frankness and producing a furore in the audience. The song ends with a merchant:\n\nThe merchant came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?\n\nAnd it appears that he wins their love because:\n\nThe merchant will make gold for me And his queen I’ll gladly be.\n\nKalganov was positively indignant.\n\n“That’s just a song of yesterday,” he said aloud. “Who writes such things for them? They might just as well have had a railwayman or a Jew come to try his luck with the girls; they’d have carried all before them.”\n\nAnd, almost as though it were a personal affront, he declared, on the spot, that he was bored, sat down on the sofa and immediately fell asleep. His pretty little face looked rather pale, as it fell back on the sofa cushion.\n\n“Look how pretty he is,” said Grushenka, taking Mitya up to him. “I was combing his hair just now; his hair’s like flax, and so thick....”\n\nAnd, bending over him tenderly, she kissed his forehead. Kalganov instantly opened his eyes, looked at her, stood up, and with the most anxious air inquired where was Maximov?\n\n“So that’s who it is you want.” Grushenka laughed. “Stay with me a minute. Mitya, run and find his Maximov.”\n\nMaximov, it appeared, could not tear himself away from the girls, only running away from time to time to pour himself out a glass of liqueur. He had drunk two cups of chocolate. His face was red, and his nose was crimson; his eyes were moist and mawkishly sweet. He ran up and announced that he was going to dance the “sabotière.”\n\n“They taught me all those well-bred, aristocratic dances when I was little....”\n\n“Go, go with him, Mitya, and I’ll watch from here how he dances,” said Grushenka.\n\n“No, no, I’m coming to look on, too,” exclaimed Kalganov, brushing aside in the most naïve way Grushenka’s offer to sit with him. They all went to look on. Maximov danced his dance. But it roused no great admiration in any one but Mitya. It consisted of nothing but skipping and hopping, kicking up the feet, and at every skip Maximov slapped the upturned sole of his foot. Kalganov did not like it at all, but Mitya kissed the dancer.\n\n“Thanks. You’re tired perhaps? What are you looking for here? Would you like some sweets? A cigar, perhaps?”\n\n“A cigarette.”\n\n“Don’t you want a drink?”\n\n“I’ll just have a liqueur.... Have you any chocolates?”\n\n“Yes, there’s a heap of them on the table there. Choose one, my dear soul!”\n\n“I like one with vanilla ... for old people. He he!”\n\n“No, brother, we’ve none of that special sort.”\n\n“I say,” the old man bent down to whisper in Mitya’s ear. “That girl there, little Marya, he he! How would it be if you were to help me make friends with her?”\n\n“So that’s what you’re after! No, brother, that won’t do!”\n\n“I’d do no harm to any one,” Maximov muttered disconsolately.\n\n“Oh, all right, all right. They only come here to dance and sing, you know, brother. But damn it all, wait a bit!... Eat and drink and be merry, meanwhile. Don’t you want money?”\n\n“Later on, perhaps,” smiled Maximov.\n\n“All right, all right....”\n\nMitya’s head was burning. He went outside to the wooden balcony which ran round the whole building on the inner side, overlooking the courtyard. The fresh air revived him. He stood alone in a dark corner, and suddenly clutched his head in both hands. His scattered thoughts came together; his sensations blended into a whole and threw a sudden light into his mind. A fearful and terrible light! “If I’m to shoot myself, why not now?” passed through his mind. “Why not go for the pistols, bring them here, and here, in this dark dirty corner, make an end?” Almost a minute he stood, undecided. A few hours earlier, when he had been dashing here, he was pursued by disgrace, by the theft he had committed, and that blood, that blood!... But yet it was easier for him then. Then everything was over: he had lost her, given her up. She was gone, for him—oh, then his death sentence had been easier for him; at least it had seemed necessary, inevitable, for what had he to stay on earth for?\n\nBut now? Was it the same as then? Now one phantom, one terror at least was at an end: that first, rightful lover, that fateful figure had vanished, leaving no trace. The terrible phantom had turned into something so small, so comic; it had been carried into the bedroom and locked in. It would never return. She was ashamed, and from her eyes he could see now whom she loved. Now he had everything to make life happy ... but he could not go on living, he could not; oh, damnation! “O God! restore to life the man I knocked down at the fence! Let this fearful cup pass from me! Lord, thou hast wrought miracles for such sinners as me! But what, what if the old man’s alive? Oh, then the shame of the other disgrace I would wipe away. I would restore the stolen money. I’d give it back; I’d get it somehow.... No trace of that shame will remain except in my heart for ever! But no, no; oh, impossible cowardly dreams! Oh, damnation!”\n\nYet there was a ray of light and hope in his darkness. He jumped up and ran back to the room—to her, to her, his queen for ever! Was not one moment of her love worth all the rest of life, even in the agonies of disgrace? This wild question clutched at his heart. “To her, to her alone, to see her, to hear her, to think of nothing, to forget everything, if only for that night, for an hour, for a moment!” Just as he turned from the balcony into the passage, he came upon the landlord, Trifon Borissovitch. He thought he looked gloomy and worried, and fancied he had come to find him.\n\n“What is it, Trifon Borissovitch? are you looking for me?”\n\n“No, sir.” The landlord seemed disconcerted. “Why should I be looking for you? Where have you been?”\n\n“Why do you look so glum? You’re not angry, are you? Wait a bit, you shall soon get to bed.... What’s the time?”\n\n“It’ll be three o’clock. Past three, it must be.”\n\n“We’ll leave off soon. We’ll leave off.”\n\n“Don’t mention it; it doesn’t matter. Keep it up as long as you like....”\n\n“What’s the matter with him?” Mitya wondered for an instant, and he ran back to the room where the girls were dancing. But she was not there. She was not in the blue room either; there was no one but Kalganov asleep on the sofa. Mitya peeped behind the curtain—she was there. She was sitting in the corner, on a trunk. Bent forward, with her head and arms on the bed close by, she was crying bitterly, doing her utmost to stifle her sobs that she might not be heard. Seeing Mitya, she beckoned him to her, and when he ran to her, she grasped his hand tightly.\n\n“Mitya, Mitya, I loved him, you know. How I have loved him these five years, all that time! Did I love him or only my own anger? No, him, him! It’s a lie that it was my anger I loved and not him. Mitya, I was only seventeen then; he was so kind to me, so merry; he used to sing to me.... Or so it seemed to a silly girl like me.... And now, O Lord, it’s not the same man. Even his face is not the same; he’s different altogether. I shouldn’t have known him. I drove here with Timofey, and all the way I was thinking how I should meet him, what I should say to him, how we should look at one another. My soul was faint, and all of a sudden it was just as though he had emptied a pail of dirty water over me. He talked to me like a schoolmaster, all so grave and learned; he met me so solemnly that I was struck dumb. I couldn’t get a word in. At first I thought he was ashamed to talk before his great big Pole. I sat staring at him and wondering why I couldn’t say a word to him now. It must have been his wife that ruined him; you know he threw me up to get married. She must have changed him like that. Mitya, how shameful it is! Oh, Mitya, I’m ashamed, I’m ashamed for all my life. Curse it, curse it, curse those five years!”\n\nAnd again she burst into tears, but clung tight to Mitya’s hand and did not let it go.\n\n“Mitya, darling, stay, don’t go away. I want to say one word to you,” she whispered, and suddenly raised her face to him. “Listen, tell me who it is I love? I love one man here. Who is that man? That’s what you must tell me.”\n\nA smile lighted up her face that was swollen with weeping, and her eyes shone in the half darkness.\n\n“A falcon flew in, and my heart sank. ‘Fool! that’s the man you love!’ That was what my heart whispered to me at once. You came in and all grew bright. What’s he afraid of? I wondered. For you were frightened; you couldn’t speak. It’s not them he’s afraid of—could you be frightened of any one? It’s me he’s afraid of, I thought, only me. So Fenya told you, you little stupid, how I called to Alyosha out of the window that I’d loved Mityenka for one hour, and that I was going now to love ... another. Mitya, Mitya, how could I be such a fool as to think I could love any one after you? Do you forgive me, Mitya? Do you forgive me or not? Do you love me? Do you love me?” She jumped up and held him with both hands on his shoulders. Mitya, dumb with rapture, gazed into her eyes, at her face, at her smile, and suddenly clasped her tightly in his arms and kissed her passionately.\n\n“You will forgive me for having tormented you? It was through spite I tormented you all. It was for spite I drove the old man out of his mind.... Do you remember how you drank at my house one day and broke the wine-glass? I remembered that and I broke a glass to-day and drank ‘to my vile heart.’ Mitya, my falcon, why don’t you kiss me? He kissed me once, and now he draws back and looks and listens. Why listen to me? Kiss me, kiss me hard, that’s right. If you love, well, then, love! I’ll be your slave now, your slave for the rest of my life. It’s sweet to be a slave. Kiss me! Beat me, ill-treat me, do what you will with me.... And I do deserve to suffer. Stay, wait, afterwards, I won’t have that....” she suddenly thrust him away. “Go along, Mitya, I’ll come and have some wine, I want to be drunk, I’m going to get drunk and dance; I must, I must!” She tore herself away from him and disappeared behind the curtain. Mitya followed like a drunken man.\n\n“Yes, come what may—whatever may happen now, for one minute I’d give the whole world,” he thought. Grushenka did, in fact, toss off a whole glass of champagne at one gulp, and became at once very tipsy. She sat down in the same chair as before, with a blissful smile on her face. Her cheeks were glowing, her lips were burning, her flashing eyes were moist; there was passionate appeal in her eyes. Even Kalganov felt a stir at the heart and went up to her.\n\n“Did you feel how I kissed you when you were asleep just now?” she said thickly. “I’m drunk now, that’s what it is.... And aren’t you drunk? And why isn’t Mitya drinking? Why don’t you drink, Mitya? I’m drunk, and you don’t drink....”\n\n“I am drunk! I’m drunk as it is ... drunk with you ... and now I’ll be drunk with wine, too.”\n\nHe drank off another glass, and—he thought it strange himself—that glass made him completely drunk. He was suddenly drunk, although till that moment he had been quite sober, he remembered that. From that moment everything whirled about him, as though he were delirious. He walked, laughed, talked to everybody, without knowing what he was doing. Only one persistent burning sensation made itself felt continually, “like a red-hot coal in his heart,” he said afterwards. He went up to her, sat beside her, gazed at her, listened to her.... She became very talkative, kept calling every one to her, and beckoned to different girls out of the chorus. When the girl came up, she either kissed her, or made the sign of the cross over her. In another minute she might have cried. She was greatly amused by the “little old man,” as she called Maximov. He ran up every minute to kiss her hands, “each little finger,” and finally he danced another dance to an old song, which he sang himself. He danced with special vigor to the refrain:\n\nThe little pig says—umph! umph! umph! The little calf says—moo, moo, moo, The little duck says—quack, quack, quack, The little goose says—ga, ga, ga. The hen goes strutting through the porch; Troo-roo-roo-roo-roo, she’ll say, Troo-roo-roo-roo-roo, she’ll say!\n\n“Give him something, Mitya,” said Grushenka. “Give him a present, he’s poor, you know. Ah, the poor, the insulted!... Do you know, Mitya, I shall go into a nunnery. No, I really shall one day, Alyosha said something to me to-day that I shall remember all my life.... Yes.... But to-day let us dance. To-morrow to the nunnery, but to-day we’ll dance. I want to play to-day, good people, and what of it? God will forgive us. If I were God, I’d forgive every one: ‘My dear sinners, from this day forth I forgive you.’ I’m going to beg forgiveness: ‘Forgive me, good people, a silly wench.’ I’m a beast, that’s what I am. But I want to pray. I gave a little onion. Wicked as I’ve been, I want to pray. Mitya, let them dance, don’t stop them. Every one in the world is good. Every one—even the worst of them. The world’s a nice place. Though we’re bad the world’s all right. We’re good and bad, good and bad.... Come, tell me, I’ve something to ask you: come here every one, and I’ll ask you: Why am I so good? You know I am good. I’m very good.... Come, why am I so good?”\n\nSo Grushenka babbled on, getting more and more drunk. At last she announced that she was going to dance, too. She got up from her chair, staggering. “Mitya, don’t give me any more wine—if I ask you, don’t give it to me. Wine doesn’t give peace. Everything’s going round, the stove, and everything. I want to dance. Let every one see how I dance ... let them see how beautifully I dance....”\n\nShe really meant it. She pulled a white cambric handkerchief out of her pocket, and took it by one corner in her right hand, to wave it in the dance. Mitya ran to and fro, the girls were quiet, and got ready to break into a dancing song at the first signal. Maximov, hearing that Grushenka wanted to dance, squealed with delight, and ran skipping about in front of her, humming:\n\nWith legs so slim and sides so trim And its little tail curled tight.\n\nBut Grushenka waved her handkerchief at him and drove him away.\n\n“Sh-h! Mitya, why don’t they come? Let every one come ... to look on. Call them in, too, that were locked in.... Why did you lock them in? Tell them I’m going to dance. Let them look on, too....”\n\nMitya walked with a drunken swagger to the locked door, and began knocking to the Poles with his fist.\n\n“Hi, you ... Podvysotskys! Come, she’s going to dance. She calls you.”\n\n“Lajdak!” one of the Poles shouted in reply.\n\n“You’re a lajdak yourself! You’re a little scoundrel, that’s what you are.”\n\n“Leave off laughing at Poland,” said Kalganov sententiously. He too was drunk.\n\n“Be quiet, boy! If I call him a scoundrel, it doesn’t mean that I called all Poland so. One lajdak doesn’t make a Poland. Be quiet, my pretty boy, eat a sweetmeat.”\n\n“Ach, what fellows! As though they were not men. Why won’t they make friends?” said Grushenka, and went forward to dance. The chorus broke into “Ah, my porch, my new porch!” Grushenka flung back her head, half opened her lips, smiled, waved her handkerchief, and suddenly, with a violent lurch, stood still in the middle of the room, looking bewildered.\n\n“I’m weak....” she said in an exhausted voice. “Forgive me.... I’m weak, I can’t.... I’m sorry.”\n\nShe bowed to the chorus, and then began bowing in all directions.\n\n“I’m sorry.... Forgive me....”\n\n“The lady’s been drinking. The pretty lady has been drinking,” voices were heard saying.\n\n“The lady’s drunk too much,” Maximov explained to the girls, giggling.\n\n“Mitya, lead me away ... take me,” said Grushenka helplessly. Mitya pounced on her, snatched her up in his arms, and carried the precious burden through the curtains.\n\n“Well, now I’ll go,” thought Kalganov, and walking out of the blue room, he closed the two halves of the door after him. But the orgy in the larger room went on and grew louder and louder. Mitya laid Grushenka on the bed and kissed her on the lips.\n\n“Don’t touch me....” she faltered, in an imploring voice. “Don’t touch me, till I’m yours.... I’ve told you I’m yours, but don’t touch me ... spare me.... With them here, with them close, you mustn’t. He’s here. It’s nasty here....”\n\n“I’ll obey you! I won’t think of it ... I worship you!” muttered Mitya. “Yes, it’s nasty here, it’s abominable.”\n\nAnd still holding her in his arms, he sank on his knees by the bedside.\n\n“I know, though you’re a brute, you’re generous,” Grushenka articulated with difficulty. “It must be honorable ... it shall be honorable for the future ... and let us be honest, let us be good, not brutes, but good ... take me away, take me far away, do you hear? I don’t want it to be here, but far, far away....”\n\n“Oh, yes, yes, it must be!” said Mitya, pressing her in his arms. “I’ll take you and we’ll fly away.... Oh, I’d give my whole life for one year only to know about that blood!”\n\n“What blood?” asked Grushenka, bewildered.\n\n“Nothing,” muttered Mitya, through his teeth. “Grusha, you wanted to be honest, but I’m a thief. But I’ve stolen money from Katya.... Disgrace, a disgrace!”\n\n“From Katya, from that young lady? No, you didn’t steal it. Give it her back, take it from me.... Why make a fuss? Now everything of mine is yours. What does money matter? We shall waste it anyway.... Folks like us are bound to waste money. But we’d better go and work the land. I want to dig the earth with my own hands. We must work, do you hear? Alyosha said so. I won’t be your mistress, I’ll be faithful to you, I’ll be your slave, I’ll work for you. We’ll go to the young lady and bow down to her together, so that she may forgive us, and then we’ll go away. And if she won’t forgive us, we’ll go, anyway. Take her her money and love me.... Don’t love her.... Don’t love her any more. If you love her, I shall strangle her.... I’ll put out both her eyes with a needle....”\n\n“I love you. I love only you. I’ll love you in Siberia....”\n\n“Why Siberia? Never mind, Siberia, if you like. I don’t care ... we’ll work ... there’s snow in Siberia.... I love driving in the snow ... and must have bells.... Do you hear, there’s a bell ringing? Where is that bell ringing? There are people coming.... Now it’s stopped.”\n\nShe closed her eyes, exhausted, and suddenly fell asleep for an instant. There had certainly been the sound of a bell in the distance, but the ringing had ceased. Mitya let his head sink on her breast. He did not notice that the bell had ceased ringing, nor did he notice that the songs had ceased, and that instead of singing and drunken clamor there was absolute stillness in the house. Grushenka opened her eyes.\n\n“What’s the matter? Was I asleep? Yes ... a bell ... I’ve been asleep and dreamt I was driving over the snow with bells, and I dozed. I was with some one I loved, with you. And far, far away. I was holding you and kissing you, nestling close to you. I was cold, and the snow glistened.... You know how the snow glistens at night when the moon shines. It was as though I was not on earth. I woke up, and my dear one is close to me. How sweet that is!...”\n\n“Close to you,” murmured Mitya, kissing her dress, her bosom, her hands. And suddenly he had a strange fancy: it seemed to him that she was looking straight before her, not at him, not into his face, but over his head, with an intent, almost uncanny fixity. An expression of wonder, almost of alarm, came suddenly into her face.\n\n“Mitya, who is that looking at us?” she whispered.\n\nMitya turned, and saw that some one had, in fact, parted the curtains and seemed to be watching them. And not one person alone, it seemed.\n\nHe jumped up and walked quickly to the intruder.\n\n“Here, come to us, come here,” said a voice, speaking not loudly, but firmly and peremptorily.\n\nMitya passed to the other side of the curtain and stood stock still. The room was filled with people, but not those who had been there before. An instantaneous shiver ran down his back, and he shuddered. He recognized all those people instantly. That tall, stout old man in the overcoat and forage-cap with a cockade—was the police captain, Mihail Makarovitch. And that “consumptive-looking” trim dandy, “who always has such polished boots”—that was the deputy prosecutor. “He has a chronometer worth four hundred roubles; he showed it to me.” And that small young man in spectacles.... Mitya forgot his surname though he knew him, had seen him: he was the “investigating lawyer,” from the “school of jurisprudence,” who had only lately come to the town. And this man—the inspector of police, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, a man he knew well. And those fellows with the brass plates on, why are they here? And those other two ... peasants.... And there at the door Kalganov with Trifon Borissovitch....\n\n“Gentlemen! What’s this for, gentlemen?” began Mitya, but suddenly, as though beside himself, not knowing what he was doing, he cried aloud, at the top of his voice:\n\n“I un—der—stand!”\n\nThe young man in spectacles moved forward suddenly, and stepping up to Mitya, began with dignity, though hurriedly:\n\n“We have to make ... in brief, I beg you to come this way, this way to the sofa.... It is absolutely imperative that you should give an explanation.”\n\n“The old man!” cried Mitya frantically. “The old man and his blood!... I understand.”\n\nAnd he sank, almost fell, on a chair close by, as though he had been mown down by a scythe.\n\n“You understand? He understands it! Monster and parricide! Your father’s blood cries out against you!” the old captain of police roared suddenly, stepping up to Mitya.\n\nHe was beside himself, crimson in the face and quivering all over.\n\n“This is impossible!” cried the small young man. “Mihail Makarovitch, Mihail Makarovitch, this won’t do!... I beg you’ll allow me to speak. I should never have expected such behavior from you....”\n\n“This is delirium, gentlemen, raving delirium,” cried the captain of police; “look at him: drunk, at this time of night, in the company of a disreputable woman, with the blood of his father on his hands.... It’s delirium!...”\n\n“I beg you most earnestly, dear Mihail Makarovitch, to restrain your feelings,” the prosecutor said in a rapid whisper to the old police captain, “or I shall be forced to resort to—”\n\nBut the little lawyer did not allow him to finish. He turned to Mitya, and delivered himself in a loud, firm, dignified voice:\n\n“Ex-Lieutenant Karamazov, it is my duty to inform you that you are charged with the murder of your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, perpetrated this night....”\n\nHe said something more, and the prosecutor, too, put in something, but though Mitya heard them he did not understand them. He stared at them all with wild eyes.\n\nBook IX. The Preliminary Investigation Chapter I. The Beginning Of Perhotin’s Official Career\nPyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, whom we left knocking at the strong locked gates of the widow Morozov’s house, ended, of course, by making himself heard. Fenya, who was still excited by the fright she had had two hours before, and too much “upset” to go to bed, was almost frightened into hysterics on hearing the furious knocking at the gate. Though she had herself seen him drive away, she fancied that it must be Dmitri Fyodorovitch knocking again, no one else could knock so savagely. She ran to the house-porter, who had already waked up and gone out to the gate, and began imploring him not to open it. But having questioned Pyotr Ilyitch, and learned that he wanted to see Fenya on very “important business,” the man made up his mind at last to open. Pyotr Ilyitch was admitted into Fenya’s kitchen, but the girl begged him to allow the house-porter to be present, “because of her misgivings.” He began questioning her and at once learnt the most vital fact, that is, that when Dmitri Fyodorovitch had run out to look for Grushenka, he had snatched up a pestle from the mortar, and that when he returned, the pestle was not with him and his hands were smeared with blood.\n\n“And the blood was simply flowing, dripping from him, dripping!” Fenya kept exclaiming. This horrible detail was simply the product of her disordered imagination. But although not “dripping,” Pyotr Ilyitch had himself seen those hands stained with blood, and had helped to wash them. Moreover, the question he had to decide was not how soon the blood had dried, but where Dmitri Fyodorovitch had run with the pestle, or rather, whether it really was to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, and how he could satisfactorily ascertain. Pyotr Ilyitch persisted in returning to this point, and though he found out nothing conclusive, yet he carried away a conviction that Dmitri Fyodorovitch could have gone nowhere but to his father’s house, and that therefore something must have happened there.\n\n“And when he came back,” Fenya added with excitement, “I told him the whole story, and then I began asking him, ‘Why have you got blood on your hands, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?’ and he answered that that was human blood, and that he had just killed some one. He confessed it all to me, and suddenly ran off like a madman. I sat down and began thinking, where’s he run off to now like a madman? He’ll go to Mokroe, I thought, and kill my mistress there. I ran out to beg him not to kill her. I was running to his lodgings, but I looked at Plotnikov’s shop, and saw him just setting off, and there was no blood on his hands then.” (Fenya had noticed this and remembered it.) Fenya’s old grandmother confirmed her evidence as far as she was capable. After asking some further questions, Pyotr Ilyitch left the house, even more upset and uneasy than he had been when he entered it.\n\nThe most direct and the easiest thing for him to do would have been to go straight to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, to find out whether anything had happened there, and if so, what; and only to go to the police captain, as Pyotr Ilyitch firmly intended doing, when he had satisfied himself of the fact. But the night was dark, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s gates were strong, and he would have to knock again. His acquaintance with Fyodor Pavlovitch was of the slightest, and what if, after he had been knocking, they opened to him, and nothing had happened? Then Fyodor Pavlovitch in his jeering way would go telling the story all over the town, how a stranger, called Perhotin, had broken in upon him at midnight to ask if any one had killed him. It would make a scandal. And scandal was what Pyotr Ilyitch dreaded more than anything in the world.\n\nYet the feeling that possessed him was so strong, that though he stamped his foot angrily and swore at himself, he set off again, not to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s but to Madame Hohlakov’s. He decided that if she denied having just given Dmitri Fyodorovitch three thousand roubles, he would go straight to the police captain, but if she admitted having given him the money, he would go home and let the matter rest till next morning.\n\nIt is, of course, perfectly evident that there was even more likelihood of causing scandal by going at eleven o’clock at night to a fashionable lady, a complete stranger, and perhaps rousing her from her bed to ask her an amazing question, than by going to Fyodor Pavlovitch. But that is just how it is, sometimes, especially in cases like the present one, with the decisions of the most precise and phlegmatic people. Pyotr Ilyitch was by no means phlegmatic at that moment. He remembered all his life how a haunting uneasiness gradually gained possession of him, growing more and more painful and driving him on, against his will. Yet he kept cursing himself, of course, all the way for going to this lady, but “I will get to the bottom of it, I will!” he repeated for the tenth time, grinding his teeth, and he carried out his intention.\n\nIt was exactly eleven o’clock when he entered Madame Hohlakov’s house. He was admitted into the yard pretty quickly, but, in response to his inquiry whether the lady was still up, the porter could give no answer, except that she was usually in bed by that time.\n\n“Ask at the top of the stairs. If the lady wants to receive you, she’ll receive you. If she won’t, she won’t.”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch went up, but did not find things so easy here. The footman was unwilling to take in his name, but finally called a maid. Pyotr Ilyitch politely but insistently begged her to inform her lady that an official, living in the town, called Perhotin, had called on particular business, and that if it were not of the greatest importance he would not have ventured to come. “Tell her in those words, in those words exactly,” he asked the girl.\n\nShe went away. He remained waiting in the entry. Madame Hohlakov herself was already in her bedroom, though not yet asleep. She had felt upset ever since Mitya’s visit, and had a presentiment that she would not get through the night without the sick headache which always, with her, followed such excitement. She was surprised on hearing the announcement from the maid. She irritably declined to see him, however, though the unexpected visit at such an hour, of an “official living in the town,” who was a total stranger, roused her feminine curiosity intensely. But this time Pyotr Ilyitch was as obstinate as a mule. He begged the maid most earnestly to take another message in these very words:\n\n“That he had come on business of the greatest importance, and that Madame Hohlakov might have cause to regret it later, if she refused to see him now.”\n\n“I plunged headlong,” he described it afterwards.\n\nThe maid, gazing at him in amazement, went to take his message again. Madame Hohlakov was impressed. She thought a little, asked what he looked like, and learned that he was “very well dressed, young and so polite.” We may note, parenthetically, that Pyotr Ilyitch was a rather good-looking young man, and well aware of the fact. Madame Hohlakov made up her mind to see him. She was in her dressing-gown and slippers, but she flung a black shawl over her shoulders. “The official” was asked to walk into the drawing-room, the very room in which Mitya had been received shortly before. The lady came to meet her visitor, with a sternly inquiring countenance, and, without asking him to sit down, began at once with the question:\n\n“What do you want?”\n\n“I have ventured to disturb you, madam, on a matter concerning our common acquaintance, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov,” Perhotin began.\n\nBut he had hardly uttered the name, when the lady’s face showed signs of acute irritation. She almost shrieked, and interrupted him in a fury:\n\n“How much longer am I to be worried by that awful man?” she cried hysterically. “How dare you, sir, how could you venture to disturb a lady who is a stranger to you, in her own house at such an hour!... And to force yourself upon her to talk of a man who came here, to this very drawing-room, only three hours ago, to murder me, and went stamping out of the room, as no one would go out of a decent house. Let me tell you, sir, that I shall lodge a complaint against you, that I will not let it pass. Kindly leave me at once.... I am a mother.... I ... I—”\n\n“Murder! then he tried to murder you, too?”\n\n“Why, has he killed somebody else?” Madame Hohlakov asked impulsively.\n\n“If you would kindly listen, madam, for half a moment, I’ll explain it all in a couple of words,” answered Perhotin, firmly. “At five o’clock this afternoon Dmitri Fyodorovitch borrowed ten roubles from me, and I know for a fact he had no money. Yet at nine o’clock, he came to see me with a bundle of hundred-rouble notes in his hand, about two or three thousand roubles. His hands and face were all covered with blood, and he looked like a madman. When I asked him where he had got so much money, he answered that he had just received it from you, that you had given him a sum of three thousand to go to the gold-mines....”\n\nMadame Hohlakov’s face assumed an expression of intense and painful excitement.\n\n“Good God! He must have killed his old father!” she cried, clasping her hands. “I have never given him money, never! Oh, run, run!... Don’t say another word! Save the old man ... run to his father ... run!”\n\n“Excuse me, madam, then you did not give him money? You remember for a fact that you did not give him any money?”\n\n“No, I didn’t, I didn’t! I refused to give it him, for he could not appreciate it. He ran out in a fury, stamping. He rushed at me, but I slipped away.... And let me tell you, as I wish to hide nothing from you now, that he positively spat at me. Can you fancy that! But why are we standing? Ah, sit down.”\n\n“Excuse me, I....”\n\n“Or better run, run, you must run and save the poor old man from an awful death!”\n\n“But if he has killed him already?”\n\n“Ah, good heavens, yes! Then what are we to do now? What do you think we must do now?”\n\nMeantime she had made Pyotr Ilyitch sit down and sat down herself, facing him. Briefly, but fairly clearly, Pyotr Ilyitch told her the history of the affair, that part of it at least which he had himself witnessed. He described, too, his visit to Fenya, and told her about the pestle. All these details produced an overwhelming effect on the distracted lady, who kept uttering shrieks, and covering her face with her hands....\n\n“Would you believe it, I foresaw all this! I have that special faculty, whatever I imagine comes to pass. And how often I’ve looked at that awful man and always thought, that man will end by murdering me. And now it’s happened ... that is, if he hasn’t murdered me, but only his own father, it’s only because the finger of God preserved me, and what’s more, he was ashamed to murder me because, in this very place, I put the holy ikon from the relics of the holy martyr, Saint Varvara, on his neck.... And to think how near I was to death at that minute, I went close up to him and he stretched out his neck to me!... Do you know, Pyotr Ilyitch (I think you said your name was Pyotr Ilyitch), I don’t believe in miracles, but that ikon and this unmistakable miracle with me now—that shakes me, and I’m ready to believe in anything you like. Have you heard about Father Zossima?... But I don’t know what I’m saying ... and only fancy, with the ikon on his neck he spat at me.... He only spat, it’s true, he didn’t murder me and ... he dashed away! But what shall we do, what must we do now? What do you think?”\n\nPyotr Ilyitch got up, and announced that he was going straight to the police captain, to tell him all about it, and leave him to do what he thought fit.\n\n“Oh, he’s an excellent man, excellent! Mihail Makarovitch, I know him. Of course, he’s the person to go to. How practical you are, Pyotr Ilyitch! How well you’ve thought of everything! I should never have thought of it in your place!”\n\n“Especially as I know the police captain very well, too,” observed Pyotr Ilyitch, who still continued to stand, and was obviously anxious to escape as quickly as possible from the impulsive lady, who would not let him say good-by and go away.\n\n“And be sure, be sure,” she prattled on, “to come back and tell me what you see there, and what you find out ... what comes to light ... how they’ll try him ... and what he’s condemned to.... Tell me, we have no capital punishment, have we? But be sure to come, even if it’s at three o’clock at night, at four, at half-past four.... Tell them to wake me, to wake me, to shake me, if I don’t get up.... But, good heavens, I shan’t sleep! But wait, hadn’t I better come with you?”\n\n“N—no. But if you would write three lines with your own hand, stating that you did not give Dmitri Fyodorovitch money, it might, perhaps, be of use ... in case it’s needed....”\n\n“To be sure!” Madame Hohlakov skipped, delighted, to her bureau. “And you know I’m simply struck, amazed at your resourcefulness, your good sense in such affairs. Are you in the service here? I’m delighted to think that you’re in the service here!”\n\nAnd still speaking, she scribbled on half a sheet of notepaper the following lines:\n\nI’ve never in my life lent to that unhappy man, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov (for, in spite of all, he is unhappy), three thousand roubles to-day. I’ve never given him money, never: That I swear by all that’s holy!\n\n# K. HOHLAKOV.\n\n“Here’s the note!” she turned quickly to Pyotr Ilyitch. “Go, save him. It’s a noble deed on your part!”\n\nAnd she made the sign of the cross three times over him. She ran out to accompany him to the passage.\n\n“How grateful I am to you! You can’t think how grateful I am to you for having come to me, first. How is it I haven’t met you before? I shall feel flattered at seeing you at my house in the future. How delightful it is that you are living here!... Such precision! Such practical ability!... They must appreciate you, they must understand you. If there’s anything I can do, believe me ... oh, I love young people! I’m in love with young people! The younger generation are the one prop of our suffering country. Her one hope.... Oh, go, go!...”\n\nBut Pyotr Ilyitch had already run away or she would not have let him go so soon. Yet Madame Hohlakov had made a rather agreeable impression on him, which had somewhat softened his anxiety at being drawn into such an unpleasant affair. Tastes differ, as we all know. “She’s by no means so elderly,” he thought, feeling pleased, “on the contrary I should have taken her for her daughter.”\n\nAs for Madame Hohlakov, she was simply enchanted by the young man. “Such sense! such exactness! in so young a man! in our day! and all that with such manners and appearance! People say the young people of to-day are no good for anything, but here’s an example!” etc. So she simply forgot this “dreadful affair,” and it was only as she was getting into bed, that, suddenly recalling “how near death she had been,” she exclaimed: “Ah, it is awful, awful!”\nBut she fell at once into a sound, sweet sleep.\n\nI would not, however, have dwelt on such trivial and irrelevant details, if this eccentric meeting of the young official with the by no means elderly widow had not subsequently turned out to be the foundation of the whole career of that practical and precise young man. His story is remembered to this day with amazement in our town, and I shall perhaps have something to say about it, when I have finished my long history of the Brothers Karamazov.\n\n# Chapter II. The Alarm\n\nOur police captain, Mihail Makarovitch Makarov, a retired lieutenant-colonel, was a widower and an excellent man. He had only come to us three years previously, but had won general esteem, chiefly because he “knew how to keep society together.” He was never without visitors, and could not have got on without them. Some one or other was always dining with him; he never sat down to table without guests. He gave regular dinners, too, on all sorts of occasions, sometimes most surprising ones. Though the fare was not recherché, it was abundant. The fish-pies were excellent, and the wine made up in quantity for what it lacked in quality.\n\nThe first room his guests entered was a well-fitted billiard-room, with pictures of English race-horses, in black frames on the walls, an essential decoration, as we all know, for a bachelor’s billiard-room. There was card-playing every evening at his house, if only at one table. But at frequent intervals, all the society of our town, with the mammas and young ladies, assembled at his house to dance. Though Mihail Makarovitch was a widower, he did not live alone. His widowed daughter lived with him, with her two unmarried daughters, grown-up girls, who had finished their education. They were of agreeable appearance and lively character, and though every one knew they would have no dowry, they attracted all the young men of fashion to their grandfather’s house.\n\nMihail Makarovitch was by no means very efficient in his work, though he performed his duties no worse than many others. To speak plainly, he was a man of rather narrow education. His understanding of the limits of his administrative power could not always be relied upon. It was not so much that he failed to grasp certain reforms enacted during the present reign, as that he made conspicuous blunders in his interpretation of them. This was not from any special lack of intelligence, but from carelessness, for he was always in too great a hurry to go into the subject.\n\n“I have the heart of a soldier rather than of a civilian,” he used to say of himself. He had not even formed a definite idea of the fundamental principles of the reforms connected with the emancipation of the serfs, and only picked it up, so to speak, from year to year, involuntarily increasing his knowledge by practice. And yet he was himself a landowner. Pyotr Ilyitch knew for certain that he would meet some of Mihail Makarovitch’s visitors there that evening, but he didn’t know which. As it happened, at that moment the prosecutor, and Varvinsky, our district doctor, a young man, who had only just come to us from Petersburg after taking a brilliant degree at the Academy of Medicine, were playing whist at the police captain’s. Ippolit Kirillovitch, the prosecutor (he was really the deputy prosecutor, but we always called him the prosecutor), was rather a peculiar man, of about five and thirty, inclined to be consumptive, and married to a fat and childless woman. He was vain and irritable, though he had a good intellect, and even a kind heart. It seemed that all that was wrong with him was that he had a better opinion of himself than his ability warranted. And that made him seem constantly uneasy. He had, moreover, certain higher, even artistic, leanings, towards psychology, for instance, a special study of the human heart, a special knowledge of the criminal and his crime. He cherished a grievance on this ground, considering that he had been passed over in the service, and being firmly persuaded that in higher spheres he had not been properly appreciated, and had enemies. In gloomy moments he even threatened to give up his post, and practice as a barrister in criminal cases. The unexpected Karamazov case agitated him profoundly: “It was a case that might well be talked about all over Russia.” But I am anticipating.\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch Nelyudov, the young investigating lawyer, who had only come from Petersburg two months before, was sitting in the next room with the young ladies. People talked about it afterwards and wondered that all the gentlemen should, as though intentionally, on the evening of “the crime” have been gathered together at the house of the executive authority. Yet it was perfectly simple and happened quite naturally.\n\nIppolit Kirillovitch’s wife had had toothache for the last two days, and he was obliged to go out to escape from her groans. The doctor, from the very nature of his being, could not spend an evening except at cards. Nikolay Parfenovitch Nelyudov had been intending for three days past to drop in that evening at Mihail Makarovitch’s, so to speak casually, so as slyly to startle the eldest granddaughter, Olga Mihailovna, by showing that he knew her secret, that he knew it was her birthday, and that she was trying to conceal it on purpose, so as not to be obliged to give a dance. He anticipated a great deal of merriment, many playful jests about her age, and her being afraid to reveal it, about his knowing her secret and telling everybody, and so on. The charming young man was a great adept at such teasing; the ladies had christened him “the naughty man,” and he seemed to be delighted at the name. He was extremely well-bred, however, of good family, education and feelings, and, though leading a life of pleasure, his sallies were always innocent and in good taste. He was short, and delicate-looking. On his white, slender, little fingers he always wore a number of big, glittering rings. When he was engaged in his official duties, he always became extraordinarily grave, as though realizing his position and the sanctity of the obligations laid upon him. He had a special gift for mystifying murderers and other criminals of the peasant class during interrogation, and if he did not win their respect, he certainly succeeded in arousing their wonder.\n\nPyotr Ilyitch was simply dumbfounded when he went into the police captain’s. He saw instantly that every one knew. They had positively thrown down their cards, all were standing up and talking. Even Nikolay Parfenovitch had left the young ladies and run in, looking strenuous and ready for action. Pyotr Ilyitch was met with the astounding news that old Fyodor Pavlovitch really had been murdered that evening in his own house, murdered and robbed. The news had only just reached them in the following manner.\n\nMarfa Ignatyevna, the wife of old Grigory, who had been knocked senseless near the fence, was sleeping soundly in her bed and might well have slept till morning after the draught she had taken. But, all of a sudden she waked up, no doubt roused by a fearful epileptic scream from Smerdyakov, who was lying in the next room unconscious. That scream always preceded his fits, and always terrified and upset Marfa Ignatyevna. She could never get accustomed to it. She jumped up and ran half-awake to Smerdyakov’s room. But it was dark there, and she could only hear the invalid beginning to gasp and struggle. Then Marfa Ignatyevna herself screamed out and was going to call her husband, but suddenly realized that when she had got up, he was not beside her in bed. She ran back to the bedstead and began groping with her hands, but the bed was really empty. Then he must have gone out—where? She ran to the steps and timidly called him. She got no answer, of course, but she caught the sound of groans far away in the garden in the darkness. She listened. The groans were repeated, and it was evident they came from the garden.\n\n“Good Lord! Just as it was with Lizaveta Smerdyastchaya!” she thought distractedly. She went timidly down the steps and saw that the gate into the garden was open.\n\n“He must be out there, poor dear,” she thought. She went up to the gate and all at once she distinctly heard Grigory calling her by name, “Marfa! Marfa!” in a weak, moaning, dreadful voice.\n\n“Lord, preserve us from harm!” Marfa Ignatyevna murmured, and ran towards the voice, and that was how she found Grigory. But she found him not by the fence where he had been knocked down, but about twenty paces off. It appeared later, that he had crawled away on coming to himself, and probably had been a long time getting so far, losing consciousness several times. She noticed at once that he was covered with blood, and screamed at the top of her voice. Grigory was muttering incoherently:\n\n“He has murdered ... his father murdered.... Why scream, silly ... run ... fetch some one....”\n\nBut Marfa continued screaming, and seeing that her master’s window was open and that there was a candle alight in the window, she ran there and began calling Fyodor Pavlovitch. But peeping in at the window, she saw a fearful sight. Her master was lying on his back, motionless, on the floor. His light-colored dressing-gown and white shirt were soaked with blood. The candle on the table brightly lighted up the blood and the motionless dead face of Fyodor Pavlovitch. Terror-stricken, Marfa rushed away from the window, ran out of the garden, drew the bolt of the big gate and ran headlong by the back way to the neighbor, Marya Kondratyevna. Both mother and daughter were asleep, but they waked up at Marfa’s desperate and persistent screaming and knocking at the shutter. Marfa, shrieking and screaming incoherently, managed to tell them the main fact, and to beg for assistance. It happened that Foma had come back from his wanderings and was staying the night with them. They got him up immediately and all three ran to the scene of the crime. On the way, Marya Kondratyevna remembered that at about eight o’clock she heard a dreadful scream from their garden, and this was no doubt Grigory’s scream, “Parricide!” uttered when he caught hold of Mitya’s leg.\n\n“Some one person screamed out and then was silent,” Marya Kondratyevna explained as she ran. Running to the place where Grigory lay, the two women with the help of Foma carried him to the lodge. They lighted a candle and saw that Smerdyakov was no better, that he was writhing in convulsions, his eyes fixed in a squint, and that foam was flowing from his lips. They moistened Grigory’s forehead with water mixed with vinegar, and the water revived him at once. He asked immediately:\n\n“Is the master murdered?”\n\nThen Foma and both the women ran to the house and saw this time that not only the window, but also the door into the garden was wide open, though Fyodor Pavlovitch had for the last week locked himself in every night and did not allow even Grigory to come in on any pretext. Seeing that door open, they were afraid to go in to Fyodor Pavlovitch “for fear anything should happen afterwards.” And when they returned to Grigory, the old man told them to go straight to the police captain. Marya Kondratyevna ran there and gave the alarm to the whole party at the police captain’s. She arrived only five minutes before Pyotr Ilyitch, so that his story came, not as his own surmise and theory, but as the direct confirmation, by a witness, of the theory held by all, as to the identity of the criminal (a theory he had in the bottom of his heart refused to believe till that moment).\n\nIt was resolved to act with energy. The deputy police inspector of the town was commissioned to take four witnesses, to enter Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house and there to open an inquiry on the spot, according to the regular forms, which I will not go into here. The district doctor, a zealous man, new to his work, almost insisted on accompanying the police captain, the prosecutor, and the investigating lawyer.\n\nI will note briefly that Fyodor Pavlovitch was found to be quite dead, with his skull battered in. But with what? Most likely with the same weapon with which Grigory had been attacked. And immediately that weapon was found, Grigory, to whom all possible medical assistance was at once given, described in a weak and breaking voice how he had been knocked down. They began looking with a lantern by the fence and found the brass pestle dropped in a most conspicuous place on the garden path. There were no signs of disturbance in the room where Fyodor Pavlovitch was lying. But by the bed, behind the screen, they picked up from the floor a big and thick envelope with the inscription: “A present of three thousand roubles for my angel Grushenka, if she is willing to come.” And below had been added by Fyodor Pavlovitch, “For my little chicken.” There were three seals of red sealing-wax on the envelope, but it had been torn open and was empty: the money had been removed. They found also on the floor a piece of narrow pink ribbon, with which the envelope had been tied up.\n\nOne piece of Pyotr Ilyitch’s evidence made a great impression on the prosecutor and the investigating magistrate, namely, his idea that Dmitri Fyodorovitch would shoot himself before daybreak, that he had resolved to do so, had spoken of it to Ilyitch, had taken the pistols, loaded them before him, written a letter, put it in his pocket, etc. When Pyotr Ilyitch, though still unwilling to believe in it, threatened to tell some one so as to prevent the suicide, Mitya had answered grinning: “You’ll be too late.” So they must make haste to Mokroe to find the criminal, before he really did shoot himself.\n\n“That’s clear, that’s clear!” repeated the prosecutor in great excitement. “That’s just the way with mad fellows like that: ‘I shall kill myself to-morrow, so I’ll make merry till I die!’”\n\nThe story of how he had bought the wine and provisions excited the prosecutor more than ever.\n\n“Do you remember the fellow that murdered a merchant called Olsufyev, gentlemen? He stole fifteen hundred, went at once to have his hair curled, and then, without even hiding the money, carrying it almost in his hand in the same way, he went off to the girls.”\n\nAll were delayed, however, by the inquiry, the search, and the formalities, etc., in the house of Fyodor Pavlovitch. It all took time and so, two hours before starting, they sent on ahead to Mokroe the officer of the rural police, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch Schmertsov, who had arrived in the town the morning before to get his pay. He was instructed to avoid raising the alarm when he reached Mokroe, but to keep constant watch over the “criminal” till the arrival of the proper authorities, to procure also witnesses for the arrest, police constables, and so on. Mavriky Mavrikyevitch did as he was told, preserving his incognito, and giving no one but his old acquaintance, Trifon Borissovitch, the slightest hint of his secret business. He had spoken to him just before Mitya met the landlord in the balcony, looking for him in the dark, and noticed at once a change in Trifon Borissovitch’s face and voice. So neither Mitya nor any one else knew that he was being watched. The box with the pistols had been carried off by Trifon Borissovitch and put in a suitable place. Only after four o’clock, almost at sunrise, all the officials, the police captain, the prosecutor, the investigating lawyer, drove up in two carriages, each drawn by three horses. The doctor remained at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s to make a post-mortem next day on the body. But he was particularly interested in the condition of the servant, Smerdyakov.\n\n“Such violent and protracted epileptic fits, recurring continually for twenty-four hours, are rarely to be met with, and are of interest to science,” he declared enthusiastically to his companions, and as they left they laughingly congratulated him on his find. The prosecutor and the investigating lawyer distinctly remembered the doctor’s saying that Smerdyakov could not outlive the night.\n\nAfter these long, but I think necessary explanations, we will return to that moment of our tale at which we broke off.\n\n# Chapter III. The Sufferings Of A Soul, The First Ordeal\n\nAnd so Mitya sat looking wildly at the people round him, not understanding what was said to him. Suddenly he got up, flung up his hands, and shouted aloud:\n\n“I’m not guilty! I’m not guilty of that blood! I’m not guilty of my father’s blood.... I meant to kill him. But I’m not guilty. Not I.”\n\nBut he had hardly said this, before Grushenka rushed from behind the curtain and flung herself at the police captain’s feet.\n\n“It was my fault! Mine! My wickedness!” she cried, in a heartrending voice, bathed in tears, stretching out her clasped hands towards them. “He did it through me. I tortured him and drove him to it. I tortured that poor old man that’s dead, too, in my wickedness, and brought him to this! It’s my fault, mine first, mine most, my fault!”\n\n“Yes, it’s your fault! You’re the chief criminal! You fury! You harlot! You’re the most to blame!” shouted the police captain, threatening her with his hand. But he was quickly and resolutely suppressed. The prosecutor positively seized hold of him.\n\n“This is absolutely irregular, Mihail Makarovitch!” he cried. “You are positively hindering the inquiry.... You’re ruining the case....” he almost gasped.\n\n“Follow the regular course! Follow the regular course!” cried Nikolay Parfenovitch, fearfully excited too, “otherwise it’s absolutely impossible!...”\n\n“Judge us together!” Grushenka cried frantically, still kneeling. “Punish us together. I will go with him now, if it’s to death!”\n\n“Grusha, my life, my blood, my holy one!” Mitya fell on his knees beside her and held her tight in his arms. “Don’t believe her,” he cried, “she’s not guilty of anything, of any blood, of anything!”\n\nHe remembered afterwards that he was forcibly dragged away from her by several men, and that she was led out, and that when he recovered himself he was sitting at the table. Beside him and behind him stood the men with metal plates. Facing him on the other side of the table sat Nikolay Parfenovitch, the investigating lawyer. He kept persuading him to drink a little water out of a glass that stood on the table.\n\n“That will refresh you, that will calm you. Be calm, don’t be frightened,” he added, extremely politely. Mitya (he remembered it afterwards) became suddenly intensely interested in his big rings, one with an amethyst, and another with a transparent bright yellow stone, of great brilliance. And long afterwards he remembered with wonder how those rings had riveted his attention through all those terrible hours of interrogation, so that he was utterly unable to tear himself away from them and dismiss them, as things that had nothing to do with his position. On Mitya’s left side, in the place where Maximov had been sitting at the beginning of the evening, the prosecutor was now seated, and on Mitya’s right hand, where Grushenka had been, was a rosy-cheeked young man in a sort of shabby hunting-jacket, with ink and paper before him. This was the secretary of the investigating lawyer, who had brought him with him. The police captain was now standing by the window at the other end of the room, beside Kalganov, who was sitting there.\n\n“Drink some water,” said the investigating lawyer softly, for the tenth time.\n\n“I have drunk it, gentlemen, I have ... but ... come, gentlemen, crush me, punish me, decide my fate!” cried Mitya, staring with terribly fixed wide-open eyes at the investigating lawyer.\n\n“So you positively declare that you are not guilty of the death of your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch?” asked the investigating lawyer, softly but insistently.\n\n“I am not guilty. I am guilty of the blood of another old man but not of my father’s. And I weep for it! I killed, I killed the old man and knocked him down.... But it’s hard to have to answer for that murder with another, a terrible murder of which I am not guilty.... It’s a terrible accusation, gentlemen, a knock-down blow. But who has killed my father, who has killed him? Who can have killed him if I didn’t? It’s marvelous, extraordinary, impossible.”\n\n“Yes, who can have killed him?” the investigating lawyer was beginning, but Ippolit Kirillovitch, the prosecutor, glancing at him, addressed Mitya.\n\n“You need not worry yourself about the old servant, Grigory Vassilyevitch. He is alive, he has recovered, and in spite of the terrible blows inflicted, according to his own and your evidence, by you, there seems no doubt that he will live, so the doctor says, at least.”\n\n“Alive? He’s alive?” cried Mitya, flinging up his hands. His face beamed. “Lord, I thank Thee for the miracle Thou has wrought for me, a sinner and evildoer. That’s an answer to my prayer. I’ve been praying all night.” And he crossed himself three times. He was almost breathless.\n\n“So from this Grigory we have received such important evidence concerning you, that—” The prosecutor would have continued, but Mitya suddenly jumped up from his chair.\n\n“One minute, gentlemen, for God’s sake, one minute; I will run to her—”\n\n“Excuse me, at this moment it’s quite impossible,” Nikolay Parfenovitch almost shrieked. He, too, leapt to his feet. Mitya was seized by the men with the metal plates, but he sat down of his own accord....\n\n“Gentlemen, what a pity! I wanted to see her for one minute only; I wanted to tell her that it has been washed away, it has gone, that blood that was weighing on my heart all night, and that I am not a murderer now! Gentlemen, she is my betrothed!” he said ecstatically and reverently, looking round at them all. “Oh, thank you, gentlemen! Oh, in one minute you have given me new life, new heart!... That old man used to carry me in his arms, gentlemen. He used to wash me in the tub when I was a baby three years old, abandoned by every one, he was like a father to me!...”\n\n“And so you—” the investigating lawyer began.\n\n“Allow me, gentlemen, allow me one minute more,” interposed Mitya, putting his elbows on the table and covering his face with his hands. “Let me have a moment to think, let me breathe, gentlemen. All this is horribly upsetting, horribly. A man is not a drum, gentlemen!”\n\n“Drink a little more water,” murmured Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\nMitya took his hands from his face and laughed. His eyes were confident. He seemed completely transformed in a moment. His whole bearing was changed; he was once more the equal of these men, with all of whom he was acquainted, as though they had all met the day before, when nothing had happened, at some social gathering. We may note in passing that, on his first arrival, Mitya had been made very welcome at the police captain’s, but later, during the last month especially, Mitya had hardly called at all, and when the police captain met him, in the street, for instance, Mitya noticed that he frowned and only bowed out of politeness. His acquaintance with the prosecutor was less intimate, though he sometimes paid his wife, a nervous and fanciful lady, visits of politeness, without quite knowing why, and she always received him graciously and had, for some reason, taken an interest in him up to the last. He had not had time to get to know the investigating lawyer, though he had met him and talked to him twice, each time about the fair sex.\n\n“You’re a most skillful lawyer, I see, Nikolay Parfenovitch,” cried Mitya, laughing gayly, “but I can help you now. Oh, gentlemen, I feel like a new man, and don’t be offended at my addressing you so simply and directly. I’m rather drunk, too, I’ll tell you that frankly. I believe I’ve had the honor and pleasure of meeting you, Nikolay Parfenovitch, at my kinsman Miüsov’s. Gentlemen, gentlemen, I don’t pretend to be on equal terms with you. I understand, of course, in what character I am sitting before you. Oh, of course, there’s a horrible suspicion ... hanging over me ... if Grigory has given evidence.... A horrible suspicion! It’s awful, awful, I understand that! But to business, gentlemen, I am ready, and we will make an end of it in one moment; for, listen, listen, gentlemen! Since I know I’m innocent, we can put an end to it in a minute. Can’t we? Can’t we?”\n\nMitya spoke much and quickly, nervously and effusively, as though he positively took his listeners to be his best friends.\n\n“So, for the present, we will write that you absolutely deny the charge brought against you,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, impressively, and bending down to the secretary he dictated to him in an undertone what to write.\n\n“Write it down? You want to write that down? Well, write it; I consent, I give my full consent, gentlemen, only ... do you see?... Stay, stay, write this. Of disorderly conduct I am guilty, of violence on a poor old man I am guilty. And there is something else at the bottom of my heart, of which I am guilty, too—but that you need not write down” (he turned suddenly to the secretary); “that’s my personal life, gentlemen, that doesn’t concern you, the bottom of my heart, that’s to say.... But of the murder of my old father I’m not guilty. That’s a wild idea. It’s quite a wild idea!... I will prove you that and you’ll be convinced directly.... You will laugh, gentlemen. You’ll laugh yourselves at your suspicion!...”\n\n“Be calm, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” said the investigating lawyer evidently trying to allay Mitya’s excitement by his own composure. “Before we go on with our inquiry, I should like, if you will consent to answer, to hear you confirm the statement that you disliked your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch, that you were involved in continual disputes with him. Here at least, a quarter of an hour ago, you exclaimed that you wanted to kill him: ‘I didn’t kill him,’ you said, ‘but I wanted to kill him.’”\n\n“Did I exclaim that? Ach, that may be so, gentlemen! Yes, unhappily, I did want to kill him ... many times I wanted to ... unhappily, unhappily!”\n\n“You wanted to. Would you consent to explain what motives precisely led you to such a sentiment of hatred for your parent?”\n\n“What is there to explain, gentlemen?” Mitya shrugged his shoulders sullenly, looking down. “I have never concealed my feelings. All the town knows about it—every one knows in the tavern. Only lately I declared them in Father Zossima’s cell.... And the very same day, in the evening I beat my father. I nearly killed him, and I swore I’d come again and kill him, before witnesses.... Oh, a thousand witnesses! I’ve been shouting it aloud for the last month, any one can tell you that!... The fact stares you in the face, it speaks for itself, it cries aloud, but feelings, gentlemen, feelings are another matter. You see, gentlemen”—Mitya frowned—“it seems to me that about feelings you’ve no right to question me. I know that you are bound by your office, I quite understand that, but that’s my affair, my private, intimate affair, yet ... since I haven’t concealed my feelings in the past ... in the tavern, for instance, I’ve talked to every one, so ... so I won’t make a secret of it now. You see, I understand, gentlemen, that there are terrible facts against me in this business. I told every one that I’d kill him, and now, all of a sudden, he’s been killed. So it must have been me! Ha ha! I can make allowances for you, gentlemen, I can quite make allowances. I’m struck all of a heap myself, for who can have murdered him, if not I? That’s what it comes to, isn’t it? If not I, who can it be, who? Gentlemen, I want to know, I insist on knowing!” he exclaimed suddenly. “Where was he murdered? How was he murdered? How, and with what? Tell me,” he asked quickly, looking at the two lawyers.\n\n“We found him in his study, lying on his back on the floor, with his head battered in,” said the prosecutor.\n\n“That’s horrible!” Mitya shuddered and, putting his elbows on the table, hid his face in his right hand.\n\n“We will continue,” interposed Nikolay Parfenovitch. “So what was it that impelled you to this sentiment of hatred? You have asserted in public, I believe, that it was based upon jealousy?”\n\n“Well, yes, jealousy. And not only jealousy.”\n\n“Disputes about money?”\n\n“Yes, about money, too.”\n\n“There was a dispute about three thousand roubles, I think, which you claimed as part of your inheritance?”\n\n“Three thousand! More, more,” cried Mitya hotly; “more than six thousand, more than ten, perhaps. I told every one so, shouted it at them. But I made up my mind to let it go at three thousand. I was desperately in need of that three thousand ... so the bundle of notes for three thousand that I knew he kept under his pillow, ready for Grushenka, I considered as simply stolen from me. Yes, gentlemen, I looked upon it as mine, as my own property....”\n\nThe prosecutor looked significantly at the investigating lawyer, and had time to wink at him on the sly.\n\n“We will return to that subject later,” said the lawyer promptly. “You will allow us to note that point and write it down; that you looked upon that money as your own property?”\n\n“Write it down, by all means. I know that’s another fact that tells against me, but I’m not afraid of facts and I tell them against myself. Do you hear? Do you know, gentlemen, you take me for a different sort of man from what I am,” he added, suddenly gloomy and dejected. “You have to deal with a man of honor, a man of the highest honor; above all—don’t lose sight of it—a man who’s done a lot of nasty things, but has always been, and still is, honorable at bottom, in his inner being. I don’t know how to express it. That’s just what’s made me wretched all my life, that I yearned to be honorable, that I was, so to say, a martyr to a sense of honor, seeking for it with a lantern, with the lantern of Diogenes, and yet all my life I’ve been doing filthy things like all of us, gentlemen ... that is like me alone. That was a mistake, like me alone, me alone!... Gentlemen, my head aches ...” His brows contracted with pain. “You see, gentlemen, I couldn’t bear the look of him, there was something in him ignoble, impudent, trampling on everything sacred, something sneering and irreverent, loathsome, loathsome. But now that he’s dead, I feel differently.”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“I don’t feel differently, but I wish I hadn’t hated him so.”\n\n“You feel penitent?”\n\n“No, not penitent, don’t write that. I’m not much good myself, I’m not very beautiful, so I had no right to consider him repulsive. That’s what I mean. Write that down, if you like.”\n\nSaying this Mitya became very mournful. He had grown more and more gloomy as the inquiry continued.\n\nAt that moment another unexpected scene followed. Though Grushenka had been removed, she had not been taken far away, only into the room next but one from the blue room, in which the examination was proceeding. It was a little room with one window, next beyond the large room in which they had danced and feasted so lavishly. She was sitting there with no one by her but Maximov, who was terribly depressed, terribly scared, and clung to her side, as though for security. At their door stood one of the peasants with a metal plate on his breast. Grushenka was crying, and suddenly her grief was too much for her, she jumped up, flung up her arms and, with a loud wail of sorrow, rushed out of the room to him, to her Mitya, and so unexpectedly that they had not time to stop her. Mitya, hearing her cry, trembled, jumped up, and with a yell rushed impetuously to meet her, not knowing what he was doing. But they were not allowed to come together, though they saw one another. He was seized by the arms. He struggled, and tried to tear himself away. It took three or four men to hold him. She was seized too, and he saw her stretching out her arms to him, crying aloud as they carried her away. When the scene was over, he came to himself again, sitting in the same place as before, opposite the investigating lawyer, and crying out to them:\n\n“What do you want with her? Why do you torment her? She’s done nothing, nothing!...”\n\nThe lawyers tried to soothe him. About ten minutes passed like this. At last Mihail Makarovitch, who had been absent, came hurriedly into the room, and said in a loud and excited voice to the prosecutor:\n\n“She’s been removed, she’s downstairs. Will you allow me to say one word to this unhappy man, gentlemen? In your presence, gentlemen, in your presence.”\n\n“By all means, Mihail Makarovitch,” answered the investigating lawyer. “In the present case we have nothing against it.”\n\n“Listen, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, my dear fellow,” began the police captain, and there was a look of warm, almost fatherly, feeling for the luckless prisoner on his excited face. “I took your Agrafena Alexandrovna downstairs myself, and confided her to the care of the landlord’s daughters, and that old fellow Maximov is with her all the time. And I soothed her, do you hear? I soothed and calmed her. I impressed on her that you have to clear yourself, so she mustn’t hinder you, must not depress you, or you may lose your head and say the wrong thing in your evidence. In fact, I talked to her and she understood. She’s a sensible girl, my boy, a good-hearted girl, she would have kissed my old hands, begging help for you. She sent me herself, to tell you not to worry about her. And I must go, my dear fellow, I must go and tell her that you are calm and comforted about her. And so you must be calm, do you understand? I was unfair to her; she is a Christian soul, gentlemen, yes, I tell you, she’s a gentle soul, and not to blame for anything. So what am I to tell her, Dmitri Fyodorovitch? Will you sit quiet or not?”\n\nThe good-natured police captain said a great deal that was irregular, but Grushenka’s suffering, a fellow creature’s suffering, touched his good-natured heart, and tears stood in his eyes. Mitya jumped up and rushed towards him.\n\n“Forgive me, gentlemen, oh, allow me, allow me!” he cried. “You’ve the heart of an angel, an angel, Mihail Makarovitch, I thank you for her. I will, I will be calm, cheerful, in fact. Tell her, in the kindness of your heart, that I am cheerful, quite cheerful, that I shall be laughing in a minute, knowing that she has a guardian angel like you. I shall have done with all this directly, and as soon as I’m free, I’ll be with her, she’ll see, let her wait. Gentlemen,” he said, turning to the two lawyers, “now I’ll open my whole soul to you; I’ll pour out everything. We’ll finish this off directly, finish it off gayly. We shall laugh at it in the end, shan’t we? But, gentlemen, that woman is the queen of my heart. Oh, let me tell you that. That one thing I’ll tell you now.... I see I’m with honorable men. She is my light, she is my holy one, and if only you knew! Did you hear her cry, ‘I’ll go to death with you’? And what have I, a penniless beggar, done for her? Why such love for me? How can a clumsy, ugly brute like me, with my ugly face, deserve such love, that she is ready to go to exile with me? And how she fell down at your feet for my sake, just now!... and yet she’s proud and has done nothing! How can I help adoring her, how can I help crying out and rushing to her as I did just now? Gentlemen, forgive me! But now, now I am comforted.”\n\nAnd he sank back in his chair and, covering his face with his hands, burst into tears. But they were happy tears. He recovered himself instantly. The old police captain seemed much pleased, and the lawyers also. They felt that the examination was passing into a new phase. When the police captain went out, Mitya was positively gay.\n\n“Now, gentlemen, I am at your disposal, entirely at your disposal. And if it were not for all these trivial details, we should understand one another in a minute. I’m at those details again. I’m at your disposal, gentlemen, but I declare that we must have mutual confidence, you in me and I in you, or there’ll be no end to it. I speak in your interests. To business, gentlemen, to business, and don’t rummage in my soul; don’t tease me with trifles, but only ask me about facts and what matters, and I will satisfy you at once. And damn the details!”\n\nSo spoke Mitya. The interrogation began again.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Second Ordeal\n\n“You don’t know how you encourage us, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, by your readiness to answer,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, with an animated air, and obvious satisfaction beaming in his very prominent, short-sighted, light gray eyes, from which he had removed his spectacles a moment before. “And you have made a very just remark about the mutual confidence, without which it is sometimes positively impossible to get on in cases of such importance, if the suspected party really hopes and desires to defend himself and is in a position to do so. We, on our side, will do everything in our power, and you can see for yourself how we are conducting the case. You approve, Ippolit Kirillovitch?” He turned to the prosecutor.\n\n“Oh, undoubtedly,” replied the prosecutor. His tone was somewhat cold, compared with Nikolay Parfenovitch’s impulsiveness.\n\nI will note once for all that Nikolay Parfenovitch, who had but lately arrived among us, had from the first felt marked respect for Ippolit Kirillovitch, our prosecutor, and had become almost his bosom friend. He was almost the only person who put implicit faith in Ippolit Kirillovitch’s extraordinary talents as a psychologist and orator and in the justice of his grievance. He had heard of him in Petersburg. On the other hand, young Nikolay Parfenovitch was the only person in the whole world whom our “unappreciated” prosecutor genuinely liked. On their way to Mokroe they had time to come to an understanding about the present case. And now as they sat at the table, the sharp-witted junior caught and interpreted every indication on his senior colleague’s face—half a word, a glance, or a wink.\n\n“Gentlemen, only let me tell my own story and don’t interrupt me with trivial questions and I’ll tell you everything in a moment,” said Mitya excitedly.\n\n“Excellent! Thank you. But before we proceed to listen to your communication, will you allow me to inquire as to another little fact of great interest to us? I mean the ten roubles you borrowed yesterday at about five o’clock on the security of your pistols, from your friend, Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin.”\n\n“I pledged them, gentlemen. I pledged them for ten roubles. What more? That’s all about it. As soon as I got back to town I pledged them.”\n\n“You got back to town? Then you had been out of town?”\n\n“Yes, I went a journey of forty versts into the country. Didn’t you know?”\n\nThe prosecutor and Nikolay Parfenovitch exchanged glances.\n\n“Well, how would it be if you began your story with a systematic description of all you did yesterday, from the morning onwards? Allow us, for instance, to inquire why you were absent from the town, and just when you left and when you came back—all those facts.”\n\n“You should have asked me like that from the beginning,” cried Mitya, laughing aloud, “and, if you like, we won’t begin from yesterday, but from the morning of the day before; then you’ll understand how, why, and where I went. I went the day before yesterday, gentlemen, to a merchant of the town, called Samsonov, to borrow three thousand roubles from him on safe security. It was a pressing matter, gentlemen, it was a sudden necessity.”\n\n“Allow me to interrupt you,” the prosecutor put in politely. “Why were you in such pressing need for just that sum, three thousand?”\n\n“Oh, gentlemen, you needn’t go into details, how, when and why, and why just so much money, and not so much, and all that rigmarole. Why, it’ll run to three volumes, and then you’ll want an epilogue!”\n\nMitya said all this with the good-natured but impatient familiarity of a man who is anxious to tell the whole truth and is full of the best intentions.\n\n“Gentlemen!”—he corrected himself hurriedly—“don’t be vexed with me for my restiveness, I beg you again. Believe me once more, I feel the greatest respect for you and understand the true position of affairs. Don’t think I’m drunk. I’m quite sober now. And, besides, being drunk would be no hindrance. It’s with me, you know, like the saying: ‘When he is sober, he is a fool; when he is drunk, he is a wise man.’ Ha ha! But I see, gentlemen, it’s not the proper thing to make jokes to you, till we’ve had our explanation, I mean. And I’ve my own dignity to keep up, too. I quite understand the difference for the moment. I am, after all, in the position of a criminal, and so, far from being on equal terms with you. And it’s your business to watch me. I can’t expect you to pat me on the head for what I did to Grigory, for one can’t break old men’s heads with impunity. I suppose you’ll put me away for him for six months, or a year perhaps, in a house of correction. I don’t know what the punishment is—but it will be without loss of the rights of my rank, without loss of my rank, won’t it? So you see, gentlemen, I understand the distinction between us.... But you must see that you could puzzle God Himself with such questions. ‘How did you step? Where did you step? When did you step? And on what did you step?’ I shall get mixed up, if you go on like this, and you will put it all down against me. And what will that lead to? To nothing! And even if it’s nonsense I’m talking now, let me finish, and you, gentlemen, being men of honor and refinement, will forgive me! I’ll finish by asking you, gentlemen, to drop that conventional method of questioning. I mean, beginning from some miserable trifle, how I got up, what I had for breakfast, how I spat, and where I spat, and so distracting the attention of the criminal, suddenly stun him with an overwhelming question, ‘Whom did you murder? Whom did you rob?’ Ha ha! That’s your regulation method, that’s where all your cunning comes in. You can put peasants off their guard like that, but not me. I know the tricks. I’ve been in the service, too. Ha ha ha! You’re not angry, gentlemen? You forgive my impertinence?” he cried, looking at them with a good-nature that was almost surprising. “It’s only Mitya Karamazov, you know, so you can overlook it. It would be inexcusable in a sensible man; but you can forgive it in Mitya. Ha ha!”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch listened, and laughed too. Though the prosecutor did not laugh, he kept his eyes fixed keenly on Mitya, as though anxious not to miss the least syllable, the slightest movement, the smallest twitch of any feature of his face.\n\n“That’s how we have treated you from the beginning,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, still laughing. “We haven’t tried to put you out by asking how you got up in the morning and what you had for breakfast. We began, indeed, with questions of the greatest importance.”\n\n“I understand. I saw it and appreciated it, and I appreciate still more your present kindness to me, an unprecedented kindness, worthy of your noble hearts. We three here are gentlemen, and let everything be on the footing of mutual confidence between educated, well-bred people, who have the common bond of noble birth and honor. In any case, allow me to look upon you as my best friends at this moment of my life, at this moment when my honor is assailed. That’s no offense to you, gentlemen, is it?”\n\n“On the contrary. You’ve expressed all that so well, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” Nikolay Parfenovitch answered with dignified approbation.\n\n“And enough of those trivial questions, gentlemen, all those tricky questions!” cried Mitya enthusiastically. “Or there’s simply no knowing where we shall get to! Is there?”\n\n“I will follow your sensible advice entirely,” the prosecutor interposed, addressing Mitya. “I don’t withdraw my question, however. It is now vitally important for us to know exactly why you needed that sum, I mean precisely three thousand.”\n\n“Why I needed it?... Oh, for one thing and another.... Well, it was to pay a debt.”\n\n“A debt to whom?”\n\n“That I absolutely refuse to answer, gentlemen. Not because I couldn’t, or because I shouldn’t dare, or because it would be damaging, for it’s all a paltry matter and absolutely trifling, but—I won’t, because it’s a matter of principle: that’s my private life, and I won’t allow any intrusion into my private life. That’s my principle. Your question has no bearing on the case, and whatever has nothing to do with the case is my private affair. I wanted to pay a debt. I wanted to pay a debt of honor but to whom I won’t say.”\n\n“Allow me to make a note of that,” said the prosecutor.\n\n“By all means. Write down that I won’t say, that I won’t. Write that I should think it dishonorable to say. Ech! you can write it; you’ve nothing else to do with your time.”\n\n“Allow me to caution you, sir, and to remind you once more, if you are unaware of it,” the prosecutor began, with a peculiar and stern impressiveness, “that you have a perfect right not to answer the questions put to you now, and we on our side have no right to extort an answer from you, if you decline to give it for one reason or another. That is entirely a matter for your personal decision. But it is our duty, on the other hand, in such cases as the present, to explain and set before you the degree of injury you will be doing yourself by refusing to give this or that piece of evidence. After which I will beg you to continue.”\n\n“Gentlemen, I’m not angry ... I ...” Mitya muttered in a rather disconcerted tone. “Well, gentlemen, you see, that Samsonov to whom I went then ...”\n\nWe will, of course, not reproduce his account of what is known to the reader already. Mitya was impatiently anxious not to omit the slightest detail. At the same time he was in a hurry to get it over. But as he gave his evidence it was written down, and therefore they had continually to pull him up. Mitya disliked this, but submitted; got angry, though still good-humoredly. He did, it is true, exclaim, from time to time, “Gentlemen, that’s enough to make an angel out of patience!” Or, “Gentlemen, it’s no good your irritating me.”\n\nBut even though he exclaimed he still preserved for a time his genially expansive mood. So he told them how Samsonov had made a fool of him two days before. (He had completely realized by now that he had been fooled.) The sale of his watch for six roubles to obtain money for the journey was something new to the lawyers. They were at once greatly interested, and even, to Mitya’s intense indignation, thought it necessary to write the fact down as a secondary confirmation of the circumstance that he had hardly a farthing in his pocket at the time. Little by little Mitya began to grow surly. Then, after describing his journey to see Lyagavy, the night spent in the stifling hut, and so on, he came to his return to the town. Here he began, without being particularly urged, to give a minute account of the agonies of jealousy he endured on Grushenka’s account.\n\nHe was heard with silent attention. They inquired particularly into the circumstance of his having a place of ambush in Marya Kondratyevna’s house at the back of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden to keep watch on Grushenka, and of Smerdyakov’s bringing him information. They laid particular stress on this, and noted it down. Of his jealousy he spoke warmly and at length, and though inwardly ashamed at exposing his most intimate feelings to “public ignominy,” so to speak, he evidently overcame his shame in order to tell the truth. The frigid severity, with which the investigating lawyer, and still more the prosecutor, stared intently at him as he told his story, disconcerted him at last considerably.\n\n“That boy, Nikolay Parfenovitch, to whom I was talking nonsense about women only a few days ago, and that sickly prosecutor are not worth my telling this to,” he reflected mournfully. “It’s ignominious. ‘Be patient, humble, hold thy peace.’” He wound up his reflections with that line. But he pulled himself together to go on again. When he came to telling of his visit to Madame Hohlakov, he regained his spirits and even wished to tell a little anecdote of that lady which had nothing to do with the case. But the investigating lawyer stopped him, and civilly suggested that he should pass on to “more essential matters.” At last, when he described his despair and told them how, when he left Madame Hohlakov’s, he thought that he’d “get three thousand if he had to murder some one to do it,” they stopped him again and noted down that he had “meant to murder some one.” Mitya let them write it without protest. At last he reached the point in his story when he learned that Grushenka had deceived him and had returned from Samsonov’s as soon as he left her there, though she had said that she would stay there till midnight.\n\n“If I didn’t kill Fenya then, gentlemen, it was only because I hadn’t time,” broke from him suddenly at that point in his story. That, too, was carefully written down. Mitya waited gloomily, and was beginning to tell how he ran into his father’s garden when the investigating lawyer suddenly stopped him, and opening the big portfolio that lay on the sofa beside him he brought out the brass pestle.\n\n“Do you recognize this object?” he asked, showing it to Mitya.\n\n“Oh, yes,” he laughed gloomily. “Of course I recognize it. Let me have a look at it.... Damn it, never mind!”\n\n“You have forgotten to mention it,” observed the investigating lawyer.\n\n“Hang it all, I shouldn’t have concealed it from you. Do you suppose I could have managed without it? It simply escaped my memory.”\n\n“Be so good as to tell us precisely how you came to arm yourself with it.”\n\n“Certainly I will be so good, gentlemen.”\n\nAnd Mitya described how he took the pestle and ran.\n\n“But what object had you in view in arming yourself with such a weapon?”\n\n“What object? No object. I just picked it up and ran off.”\n\n“What for, if you had no object?”\n\nMitya’s wrath flared up. He looked intently at “the boy” and smiled gloomily and malignantly. He was feeling more and more ashamed at having told “such people” the story of his jealousy so sincerely and spontaneously.\n\n“Bother the pestle!” broke from him suddenly.\n\n“But still—”\n\n“Oh, to keep off dogs.... Oh, because it was dark.... In case anything turned up.”\n\n“But have you ever on previous occasions taken a weapon with you when you went out, since you’re afraid of the dark?”\n\n“Ugh! damn it all, gentlemen! There’s positively no talking to you!” cried Mitya, exasperated beyond endurance, and turning to the secretary, crimson with anger, he said quickly, with a note of fury in his voice:\n\n“Write down at once ... at once ... ‘that I snatched up the pestle to go and kill my father ... Fyodor Pavlovitch ... by hitting him on the head with it!’ Well, now are you satisfied, gentlemen? Are your minds relieved?” he said, glaring defiantly at the lawyers.\n\n“We quite understand that you made that statement just now through exasperation with us and the questions we put to you, which you consider trivial, though they are, in fact, essential,” the prosecutor remarked dryly in reply.\n\n“Well, upon my word, gentlemen! Yes, I took the pestle.... What does one pick things up for at such moments? I don’t know what for. I snatched it up and ran—that’s all. For to me, gentlemen, passons, or I declare I won’t tell you any more.”\n\nHe sat with his elbows on the table and his head in his hand. He sat sideways to them and gazed at the wall, struggling against a feeling of nausea. He had, in fact, an awful inclination to get up and declare that he wouldn’t say another word, “not if you hang me for it.”\n\n“You see, gentlemen,” he said at last, with difficulty controlling himself, “you see. I listen to you and am haunted by a dream.... It’s a dream I have sometimes, you know.... I often dream it—it’s always the same ... that some one is hunting me, some one I’m awfully afraid of ... that he’s hunting me in the dark, in the night ... tracking me, and I hide somewhere from him, behind a door or cupboard, hide in a degrading way, and the worst of it is, he always knows where I am, but he pretends not to know where I am on purpose, to prolong my agony, to enjoy my terror.... That’s just what you’re doing now. It’s just like that!”\n\n“Is that the sort of thing you dream about?” inquired the prosecutor.\n\n“Yes, it is. Don’t you want to write it down?” said Mitya, with a distorted smile.\n\n“No; no need to write it down. But still you do have curious dreams.”\n\n“It’s not a question of dreams now, gentlemen—this is realism, this is real life! I’m a wolf and you’re the hunters. Well, hunt him down!”\n\n“You are wrong to make such comparisons ...” began Nikolay Parfenovitch, with extraordinary softness.\n\n“No, I’m not wrong, not at all!” Mitya flared up again, though his outburst of wrath had obviously relieved his heart. He grew more good-humored at every word. “You may not trust a criminal or a man on trial tortured by your questions, but an honorable man, the honorable impulses of the heart (I say that boldly!)—no! That you must believe you have no right indeed ... but—\n\nBe silent, heart, Be patient, humble, hold thy peace.\n\nWell, shall I go on?” he broke off gloomily.\n\n“If you’ll be so kind,” answered Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n# Chapter V. The Third Ordeal\n\nThough Mitya spoke sullenly, it was evident that he was trying more than ever not to forget or miss a single detail of his story. He told them how he had leapt over the fence into his father’s garden; how he had gone up to the window; told them all that had passed under the window. Clearly, precisely, distinctly, he described the feelings that troubled him during those moments in the garden when he longed so terribly to know whether Grushenka was with his father or not. But, strange to say, both the lawyers listened now with a sort of awful reserve, looked coldly at him, asked few questions. Mitya could gather nothing from their faces.\n\n“They’re angry and offended,” he thought. “Well, bother them!”\n\nWhen he described how he made up his mind at last to make the “signal” to his father that Grushenka had come, so that he should open the window, the lawyers paid no attention to the word “signal,” as though they entirely failed to grasp the meaning of the word in this connection: so much so, that Mitya noticed it. Coming at last to the moment when, seeing his father peering out of the window, his hatred flared up and he pulled the pestle out of his pocket, he suddenly, as though of design, stopped short. He sat gazing at the wall and was aware that their eyes were fixed upon him.\n\n“Well?” said the investigating lawyer. “You pulled out the weapon and ... and what happened then?”\n\n“Then? Why, then I murdered him ... hit him on the head and cracked his skull.... I suppose that’s your story. That’s it!”\n\nHis eyes suddenly flashed. All his smothered wrath suddenly flamed up with extraordinary violence in his soul.\n\n“Our story?” repeated Nikolay Parfenovitch. “Well—and yours?”\n\nMitya dropped his eyes and was a long time silent.\n\n“My story, gentlemen? Well, it was like this,” he began softly. “Whether it was some one’s tears, or my mother prayed to God, or a good angel kissed me at that instant, I don’t know. But the devil was conquered. I rushed from the window and ran to the fence. My father was alarmed and, for the first time, he saw me then, cried out, and sprang back from the window. I remember that very well. I ran across the garden to the fence ... and there Grigory caught me, when I was sitting on the fence.”\n\nAt that point he raised his eyes at last and looked at his listeners. They seemed to be staring at him with perfectly unruffled attention. A sort of paroxysm of indignation seized on Mitya’s soul.\n\n“Why, you’re laughing at me at this moment, gentlemen!” he broke off suddenly.\n\n“What makes you think that?” observed Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“You don’t believe one word—that’s why! I understand, of course, that I have come to the vital point. The old man’s lying there now with his skull broken, while I—after dramatically describing how I wanted to kill him, and how I snatched up the pestle—I suddenly run away from the window. A romance! Poetry! As though one could believe a fellow on his word. Ha ha! You are scoffers, gentlemen!”\n\nAnd he swung round on his chair so that it creaked.\n\n“And did you notice,” asked the prosecutor suddenly, as though not observing Mitya’s excitement, “did you notice when you ran away from the window, whether the door into the garden was open?”\n\n“No, it was not open.”\n\n“It was not?”\n“It was shut. And who could open it? Bah! the door. Wait a bit!” he seemed suddenly to bethink himself, and almost with a start:\n\n“Why, did you find the door open?”\n\n“Yes, it was open.”\n\n“Why, who could have opened it if you did not open it yourselves?” cried Mitya, greatly astonished.\n\n“The door stood open, and your father’s murderer undoubtedly went in at that door, and, having accomplished the crime, went out again by the same door,” the prosecutor pronounced deliberately, as though chiseling out each word separately. “That is perfectly clear. The murder was committed in the room and not through the window; that is absolutely certain from the examination that has been made, from the position of the body and everything. There can be no doubt of that circumstance.”\n\nMitya was absolutely dumbfounded.\n\n“But that’s utterly impossible!” he cried, completely at a loss. “I ... I didn’t go in.... I tell you positively, definitely, the door was shut the whole time I was in the garden, and when I ran out of the garden. I only stood at the window and saw him through the window. That’s all, that’s all.... I remember to the last minute. And if I didn’t remember, it would be just the same. I know it, for no one knew the signals except Smerdyakov, and me, and the dead man. And he wouldn’t have opened the door to any one in the world without the signals.”\n\n“Signals? What signals?” asked the prosecutor, with greedy, almost hysterical, curiosity. He instantly lost all trace of his reserve and dignity. He asked the question with a sort of cringing timidity. He scented an important fact of which he had known nothing, and was already filled with dread that Mitya might be unwilling to disclose it.\n\n“So you didn’t know!” Mitya winked at him with a malicious and mocking smile. “What if I won’t tell you? From whom could you find out? No one knew about the signals except my father, Smerdyakov, and me: that was all. Heaven knew, too, but it won’t tell you. But it’s an interesting fact. There’s no knowing what you might build on it. Ha ha! Take comfort, gentlemen, I’ll reveal it. You’ve some foolish idea in your hearts. You don’t know the man you have to deal with! You have to do with a prisoner who gives evidence against himself, to his own damage! Yes, for I’m a man of honor and you—are not.”\n\nThe prosecutor swallowed this without a murmur. He was trembling with impatience to hear the new fact. Minutely and diffusely Mitya told them everything about the signals invented by Fyodor Pavlovitch for Smerdyakov. He told them exactly what every tap on the window meant, tapped the signals on the table, and when Nikolay Parfenovitch said that he supposed he, Mitya, had tapped the signal “Grushenka has come,” when he tapped to his father, he answered precisely that he had tapped that signal, that “Grushenka had come.”\n\n“So now you can build up your tower,” Mitya broke off, and again turned away from them contemptuously.\n\n“So no one knew of the signals but your dead father, you, and the valet Smerdyakov? And no one else?” Nikolay Parfenovitch inquired once more.\n\n“Yes. The valet Smerdyakov, and Heaven. Write down about Heaven. That may be of use. Besides, you will need God yourselves.”\n\nAnd they had already, of course, begun writing it down. But while they wrote, the prosecutor said suddenly, as though pitching on a new idea:\n\n“But if Smerdyakov also knew of these signals and you absolutely deny all responsibility for the death of your father, was it not he, perhaps, who knocked the signal agreed upon, induced your father to open to him, and then ... committed the crime?”\n\nMitya turned upon him a look of profound irony and intense hatred. His silent stare lasted so long that it made the prosecutor blink.\n\n“You’ve caught the fox again,” commented Mitya at last; “you’ve got the beast by the tail. Ha ha! I see through you, Mr. Prosecutor. You thought, of course, that I should jump at that, catch at your prompting, and shout with all my might, ‘Aie! it’s Smerdyakov; he’s the murderer.’ Confess that’s what you thought. Confess, and I’ll go on.”\n\nBut the prosecutor did not confess. He held his tongue and waited.\n\n“You’re mistaken. I’m not going to shout ‘It’s Smerdyakov,’” said Mitya.\n\n“And you don’t even suspect him?”\n\n“Why, do you suspect him?”\n\n“He is suspected, too.”\n\nMitya fixed his eyes on the floor.\n\n“Joking apart,” he brought out gloomily. “Listen. From the very beginning, almost from the moment when I ran out to you from behind the curtain, I’ve had the thought of Smerdyakov in my mind. I’ve been sitting here, shouting that I’m innocent and thinking all the time ‘Smerdyakov!’ I can’t get Smerdyakov out of my head. In fact, I, too, thought of Smerdyakov just now; but only for a second. Almost at once I thought, ‘No, it’s not Smerdyakov.’ It’s not his doing, gentlemen.”\n\n“In that case is there anybody else you suspect?” Nikolay Parfenovitch inquired cautiously.\n\n“I don’t know any one it could be, whether it’s the hand of Heaven or Satan, but ... not Smerdyakov,” Mitya jerked out with decision.\n\n“But what makes you affirm so confidently and emphatically that it’s not he?”\n\n“From my conviction—my impression. Because Smerdyakov is a man of the most abject character and a coward. He’s not a coward, he’s the epitome of all the cowardice in the world walking on two legs. He has the heart of a chicken. When he talked to me, he was always trembling for fear I should kill him, though I never raised my hand against him. He fell at my feet and blubbered; he has kissed these very boots, literally, beseeching me ‘not to frighten him.’ Do you hear? ‘Not to frighten him.’ What a thing to say! Why, I offered him money. He’s a puling chicken—sickly, epileptic, weak-minded—a child of eight could thrash him. He has no character worth talking about. It’s not Smerdyakov, gentlemen. He doesn’t care for money; he wouldn’t take my presents. Besides, what motive had he for murdering the old man? Why, he’s very likely his son, you know—his natural son. Do you know that?”\n\n“We have heard that legend. But you are your father’s son, too, you know; yet you yourself told every one you meant to murder him.”\n\n“That’s a thrust! And a nasty, mean one, too! I’m not afraid! Oh, gentlemen, isn’t it too base of you to say that to my face? It’s base, because I told you that myself. I not only wanted to murder him, but I might have done it. And, what’s more, I went out of my way to tell you of my own accord that I nearly murdered him. But, you see, I didn’t murder him; you see, my guardian angel saved me—that’s what you’ve not taken into account. And that’s why it’s so base of you. For I didn’t kill him, I didn’t kill him! Do you hear, I did not kill him.”\n\nHe was almost choking. He had not been so moved before during the whole interrogation.\n\n“And what has he told you, gentlemen—Smerdyakov, I mean?” he added suddenly, after a pause. “May I ask that question?”\n\n“You may ask any question,” the prosecutor replied with frigid severity, “any question relating to the facts of the case, and we are, I repeat, bound to answer every inquiry you make. We found the servant Smerdyakov, concerning whom you inquire, lying unconscious in his bed, in an epileptic fit of extreme severity, that had recurred, possibly, ten times. The doctor who was with us told us, after seeing him, that he may possibly not outlive the night.”\n\n“Well, if that’s so, the devil must have killed him,” broke suddenly from Mitya, as though until that moment he had been asking himself: “Was it Smerdyakov or not?”\n\n“We will come back to this later,” Nikolay Parfenovitch decided. “Now, wouldn’t you like to continue your statement?”\n\nMitya asked for a rest. His request was courteously granted. After resting, he went on with his story. But he was evidently depressed. He was exhausted, mortified and morally shaken. To make things worse the prosecutor exasperated him, as though intentionally, by vexatious interruptions about “trifling points.” Scarcely had Mitya described how, sitting on the wall, he had struck Grigory on the head with the pestle, while the old man had hold of his left leg, and how he had then jumped down to look at him, when the prosecutor stopped him to ask him to describe exactly how he was sitting on the wall. Mitya was surprised.\n\n“Oh, I was sitting like this, astride, one leg on one side of the wall and one on the other.”\n\n“And the pestle?”\n\n“The pestle was in my hand.”\n\n“Not in your pocket? Do you remember that precisely? Was it a violent blow you gave him?”\n\n“It must have been a violent one. But why do you ask?”\n\n“Would you mind sitting on the chair just as you sat on the wall then and showing us just how you moved your arm, and in what direction?”\n\n“You’re making fun of me, aren’t you?” asked Mitya, looking haughtily at the speaker; but the latter did not flinch.\n\nMitya turned abruptly, sat astride on his chair, and swung his arm.\n\n“This was how I struck him! That’s how I knocked him down! What more do you want?”\n\n“Thank you. May I trouble you now to explain why you jumped down, with what object, and what you had in view?”\n\n“Oh, hang it!... I jumped down to look at the man I’d hurt ... I don’t know what for!”\n\n“Though you were so excited and were running away?”\n\n“Yes, though I was excited and running away.”\n\n“You wanted to help him?”\n\n“Help!... Yes, perhaps I did want to help him.... I don’t remember.”\n\n“You don’t remember? Then you didn’t quite know what you were doing?”\n\n“Not at all. I remember everything—every detail. I jumped down to look at him, and wiped his face with my handkerchief.”\n\n“We have seen your handkerchief. Did you hope to restore him to consciousness?”\n\n“I don’t know whether I hoped it. I simply wanted to make sure whether he was alive or not.”\n\n“Ah! You wanted to be sure? Well, what then?”\n\n“I’m not a doctor. I couldn’t decide. I ran away thinking I’d killed him. And now he’s recovered.”\n\n“Excellent,” commented the prosecutor. “Thank you. That’s all I wanted. Kindly proceed.”\n\nAlas! it never entered Mitya’s head to tell them, though he remembered it, that he had jumped back from pity, and standing over the prostrate figure had even uttered some words of regret: “You’ve come to grief, old man—there’s no help for it. Well, there you must lie.”\n\nThe prosecutor could only draw one conclusion: that the man had jumped back “at such a moment and in such excitement simply with the object of ascertaining whether the only witness of his crime were dead; that he must therefore have been a man of great strength, coolness, decision and foresight even at such a moment,” ... and so on. The prosecutor was satisfied: “I’ve provoked the nervous fellow by ‘trifles’ and he has said more than he meant to.”\n\nWith painful effort Mitya went on. But this time he was pulled up immediately by Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“How came you to run to the servant, Fedosya Markovna, with your hands so covered with blood, and, as it appears, your face, too?”\n\n“Why, I didn’t notice the blood at all at the time,” answered Mitya.\n\n“That’s quite likely. It does happen sometimes.” The prosecutor exchanged glances with Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“I simply didn’t notice. You’re quite right there, prosecutor,” Mitya assented suddenly.\n\nNext came the account of Mitya’s sudden determination to “step aside” and make way for their happiness. But he could not make up his mind to open his heart to them as before, and tell them about “the queen of his soul.” He disliked speaking of her before these chilly persons “who were fastening on him like bugs.” And so in response to their reiterated questions he answered briefly and abruptly:\n\n“Well, I made up my mind to kill myself. What had I left to live for? That question stared me in the face. Her first rightful lover had come back, the man who wronged her but who’d hurried back to offer his love, after five years, and atone for the wrong with marriage.... So I knew it was all over for me.... And behind me disgrace, and that blood—Grigory’s.... What had I to live for? So I went to redeem the pistols I had pledged, to load them and put a bullet in my brain to-morrow.”\n\n“And a grand feast the night before?”\n\n“Yes, a grand feast the night before. Damn it all, gentlemen! Do make haste and finish it. I meant to shoot myself not far from here, beyond the village, and I’d planned to do it at five o’clock in the morning. And I had a note in my pocket already. I wrote it at Perhotin’s when I loaded my pistols. Here’s the letter. Read it! It’s not for you I tell it,” he added contemptuously. He took it from his waistcoat pocket and flung it on the table. The lawyers read it with curiosity, and, as is usual, added it to the papers connected with the case.\n\n“And you didn’t even think of washing your hands at Perhotin’s? You were not afraid then of arousing suspicion?”\n\n“What suspicion? Suspicion or not, I should have galloped here just the same, and shot myself at five o’clock, and you wouldn’t have been in time to do anything. If it hadn’t been for what’s happened to my father, you would have known nothing about it, and wouldn’t have come here. Oh, it’s the devil’s doing. It was the devil murdered father, it was through the devil that you found it out so soon. How did you manage to get here so quick? It’s marvelous, a dream!”\n\n“Mr. Perhotin informed us that when you came to him, you held in your hands ... your blood-stained hands ... your money ... a lot of money ... a bundle of hundred-rouble notes, and that his servant-boy saw it too.”\n\n“That’s true, gentlemen. I remember it was so.”\n\n“Now, there’s one little point presents itself. Can you inform us,” Nikolay Parfenovitch began, with extreme gentleness, “where did you get so much money all of a sudden, when it appears from the facts, from the reckoning of time, that you had not been home?”\n\nThe prosecutor’s brows contracted at the question being asked so plainly, but he did not interrupt Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“No, I didn’t go home,” answered Mitya, apparently perfectly composed, but looking at the floor.\n\n“Allow me then to repeat my question,” Nikolay Parfenovitch went on as though creeping up to the subject. “Where were you able to procure such a sum all at once, when by your own confession, at five o’clock the same day you—”\n\n“I was in want of ten roubles and pledged my pistols with Perhotin, and then went to Madame Hohlakov to borrow three thousand which she wouldn’t give me, and so on, and all the rest of it,” Mitya interrupted sharply. “Yes, gentlemen, I was in want of it, and suddenly thousands turned up, eh? Do you know, gentlemen, you’re both afraid now ‘what if he won’t tell us where he got it?’ That’s just how it is. I’m not going to tell you, gentlemen. You’ve guessed right. You’ll never know,” said Mitya, chipping out each word with extraordinary determination. The lawyers were silent for a moment.\n\n“You must understand, Mr. Karamazov, that it is of vital importance for us to know,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, softly and suavely.\n\n“I understand; but still I won’t tell you.”\n\nThe prosecutor, too, intervened, and again reminded the prisoner that he was at liberty to refuse to answer questions, if he thought it to his interest, and so on. But in view of the damage he might do himself by his silence, especially in a case of such importance as—\n\n“And so on, gentlemen, and so on. Enough! I’ve heard that rigmarole before,” Mitya interrupted again. “I can see for myself how important it is, and that this is the vital point, and still I won’t say.”\n\n“What is it to us? It’s not our business, but yours. You are doing yourself harm,” observed Nikolay Parfenovitch nervously.\n\n“You see, gentlemen, joking apart”—Mitya lifted his eyes and looked firmly at them both—“I had an inkling from the first that we should come to loggerheads at this point. But at first when I began to give my evidence, it was all still far away and misty; it was all floating, and I was so simple that I began with the supposition of mutual confidence existing between us. Now I can see for myself that such confidence is out of the question, for in any case we were bound to come to this cursed stumbling-block. And now we’ve come to it! It’s impossible and there’s an end of it! But I don’t blame you. You can’t believe it all simply on my word. I understand that, of course.”\n\nHe relapsed into gloomy silence.\n\n“Couldn’t you, without abandoning your resolution to be silent about the chief point, could you not, at the same time, give us some slight hint as to the nature of the motives which are strong enough to induce you to refuse to answer, at a crisis so full of danger to you?”\n\nMitya smiled mournfully, almost dreamily.\n\n“I’m much more good-natured than you think, gentlemen. I’ll tell you the reason why and give you that hint, though you don’t deserve it. I won’t speak of that, gentlemen, because it would be a stain on my honor. The answer to the question where I got the money would expose me to far greater disgrace than the murder and robbing of my father, if I had murdered and robbed him. That’s why I can’t tell you. I can’t for fear of disgrace. What, gentlemen, are you going to write that down?”\n\n“Yes, we’ll write it down,” lisped Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“You ought not to write that down about ‘disgrace.’ I only told you that in the goodness of my heart. I needn’t have told you. I made you a present of it, so to speak, and you pounce upon it at once. Oh, well, write—write what you like,” he concluded, with scornful disgust. “I’m not afraid of you and I can still hold up my head before you.”\n\n“And can’t you tell us the nature of that disgrace?” Nikolay Parfenovitch hazarded.\n\nThe prosecutor frowned darkly.\n\n“No, no, c’est fini, don’t trouble yourselves. It’s not worth while soiling one’s hands. I have soiled myself enough through you as it is. You’re not worth it—no one is ... Enough, gentlemen. I’m not going on.”\n\nThis was said too peremptorily. Nikolay Parfenovitch did not insist further, but from Ippolit Kirillovitch’s eyes he saw that he had not given up hope.\n\n“Can you not, at least, tell us what sum you had in your hands when you went into Mr. Perhotin’s—how many roubles exactly?”\n\n“I can’t tell you that.”\n\n“You spoke to Mr. Perhotin, I believe, of having received three thousand from Madame Hohlakov.”\n\n“Perhaps I did. Enough, gentlemen. I won’t say how much I had.”\n\n“Will you be so good then as to tell us how you came here and what you have done since you arrived?”\n\n“Oh! you might ask the people here about that. But I’ll tell you if you like.”\n\nHe proceeded to do so, but we won’t repeat his story. He told it dryly and curtly. Of the raptures of his love he said nothing, but told them that he abandoned his determination to shoot himself, owing to “new factors in the case.” He told the story without going into motives or details. And this time the lawyers did not worry him much. It was obvious that there was no essential point of interest to them here.\n\n“We shall verify all that. We will come back to it during the examination of the witnesses, which will, of course, take place in your presence,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch in conclusion. “And now allow me to request you to lay on the table everything in your possession, especially all the money you still have about you.”\n\n“My money, gentlemen? Certainly. I understand that that is necessary. I’m surprised, indeed, that you haven’t inquired about it before. It’s true I couldn’t get away anywhere. I’m sitting here where I can be seen. But here’s my money—count it—take it. That’s all, I think.”\n\nHe turned it all out of his pockets; even the small change—two pieces of twenty copecks—he pulled out of his waistcoat pocket. They counted the money, which amounted to eight hundred and thirty-six roubles, and forty copecks.\n\n“And is that all?” asked the investigating lawyer.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You stated just now in your evidence that you spent three hundred roubles at Plotnikovs’. You gave Perhotin ten, your driver twenty, here you lost two hundred, then....”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch reckoned it all up. Mitya helped him readily. They recollected every farthing and included it in the reckoning. Nikolay Parfenovitch hurriedly added up the total.\n\n“With this eight hundred you must have had about fifteen hundred at first?”\n\n“I suppose so,” snapped Mitya.\n\n“How is it they all assert there was much more?”\n\n“Let them assert it.”\n\n“But you asserted it yourself.”\n\n“Yes, I did, too.”\n\n“We will compare all this with the evidence of other persons not yet examined. Don’t be anxious about your money. It will be properly taken care of and be at your disposal at the conclusion of ... what is beginning ... if it appears, or, so to speak, is proved that you have undisputed right to it. Well, and now....”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch suddenly got up, and informed Mitya firmly that it was his duty and obligation to conduct a minute and thorough search “of your clothes and everything else....”\n\n“By all means, gentlemen. I’ll turn out all my pockets, if you like.”\n\nAnd he did, in fact, begin turning out his pockets.\n\n“It will be necessary to take off your clothes, too.”\n\n“What! Undress? Ugh! Damn it! Won’t you search me as I am! Can’t you?”\n\n“It’s utterly impossible, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. You must take off your clothes.”\n\n“As you like,” Mitya submitted gloomily; “only, please, not here, but behind the curtains. Who will search them?”\n\n“Behind the curtains, of course.”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch bent his head in assent. His small face wore an expression of peculiar solemnity.\n\n# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor Catches Mitya\n\nSomething utterly unexpected and amazing to Mitya followed. He could never, even a minute before, have conceived that any one could behave like that to him, Mitya Karamazov. What was worst of all, there was something humiliating in it, and on their side something “supercilious and scornful.” It was nothing to take off his coat, but he was asked to undress further, or rather not asked but “commanded,” he quite understood that. From pride and contempt he submitted without a word. Several peasants accompanied the lawyers and remained on the same side of the curtain. “To be ready if force is required,” thought Mitya, “and perhaps for some other reason, too.”\n\n“Well, must I take off my shirt, too?” he asked sharply, but Nikolay Parfenovitch did not answer. He was busily engaged with the prosecutor in examining the coat, the trousers, the waistcoat and the cap; and it was evident that they were both much interested in the scrutiny. “They make no bones about it,” thought Mitya, “they don’t keep up the most elementary politeness.”\n\n“I ask you for the second time—need I take off my shirt or not?” he said, still more sharply and irritably.\n\n“Don’t trouble yourself. We will tell you what to do,” Nikolay Parfenovitch said, and his voice was positively peremptory, or so it seemed to Mitya.\n\nMeantime a consultation was going on in undertones between the lawyers. There turned out to be on the coat, especially on the left side at the back, a huge patch of blood, dry, and still stiff. There were bloodstains on the trousers, too. Nikolay Parfenovitch, moreover, in the presence of the peasant witnesses, passed his fingers along the collar, the cuffs, and all the seams of the coat and trousers, obviously looking for something—money, of course. He didn’t even hide from Mitya his suspicion that he was capable of sewing money up in his clothes.\n\n“He treats me not as an officer but as a thief,” Mitya muttered to himself. They communicated their ideas to one another with amazing frankness. The secretary, for instance, who was also behind the curtain, fussing about and listening, called Nikolay Parfenovitch’s attention to the cap, which they were also fingering.\n\n“You remember Gridyenko, the copying-clerk,” observed the secretary. “Last summer he received the wages of the whole office, and pretended to have lost the money when he was drunk. And where was it found? Why, in just such pipings in his cap. The hundred-rouble notes were screwed up in little rolls and sewed in the piping.”\n\nBoth the lawyers remembered Gridyenko’s case perfectly, and so laid aside Mitya’s cap, and decided that all his clothes must be more thoroughly examined later.\n\n“Excuse me,” cried Nikolay Parfenovitch, suddenly, noticing that the right cuff of Mitya’s shirt was turned in, and covered with blood, “excuse me, what’s that, blood?”\n\n“Yes,” Mitya jerked out.\n\n“That is, what blood? ... and why is the cuff turned in?”\n\nMitya told him how he had got the sleeve stained with blood looking after Grigory, and had turned it inside when he was washing his hands at Perhotin’s.\n\n“You must take off your shirt, too. That’s very important as material evidence.”\n\nMitya flushed red and flew into a rage.\n\n“What, am I to stay naked?” he shouted.\n\n“Don’t disturb yourself. We will arrange something. And meanwhile take off your socks.”\n\n“You’re not joking? Is that really necessary?” Mitya’s eyes flashed.\n\n“We are in no mood for joking,” answered Nikolay Parfenovitch sternly.\n\n“Well, if I must—” muttered Mitya, and sitting down on the bed, he took off his socks. He felt unbearably awkward. All were clothed, while he was naked, and strange to say, when he was undressed he felt somehow guilty in their presence, and was almost ready to believe himself that he was inferior to them, and that now they had a perfect right to despise him.\n\n“When all are undressed, one is somehow not ashamed, but when one’s the only one undressed and everybody is looking, it’s degrading,” he kept repeating to himself, again and again. “It’s like a dream, I’ve sometimes dreamed of being in such degrading positions.” It was a misery to him to take off his socks. They were very dirty, and so were his underclothes, and now every one could see it. And what was worse, he disliked his feet. All his life he had thought both his big toes hideous. He particularly loathed the coarse, flat, crooked nail on the right one, and now they would all see it. Feeling intolerably ashamed made him, at once and intentionally, rougher. He pulled off his shirt, himself.\n\n“Would you like to look anywhere else if you’re not ashamed to?”\n\n“No, there’s no need to, at present.”\n\n“Well, am I to stay naked like this?” he added savagely.\n\n“Yes, that can’t be helped for the time.... Kindly sit down here for a while. You can wrap yourself in a quilt from the bed, and I ... I’ll see to all this.”\n\nAll the things were shown to the witnesses. The report of the search was drawn up, and at last Nikolay Parfenovitch went out, and the clothes were carried out after him. Ippolit Kirillovitch went out, too. Mitya was left alone with the peasants, who stood in silence, never taking their eyes off him. Mitya wrapped himself up in the quilt. He felt cold. His bare feet stuck out, and he couldn’t pull the quilt over so as to cover them. Nikolay Parfenovitch seemed to be gone a long time, “an insufferable time.” “He thinks of me as a puppy,” thought Mitya, gnashing his teeth. “That rotten prosecutor has gone, too, contemptuous no doubt, it disgusts him to see me naked!”\n\nMitya imagined, however, that his clothes would be examined and returned to him. But what was his indignation when Nikolay Parfenovitch came back with quite different clothes, brought in behind him by a peasant.\n\n“Here are clothes for you,” he observed airily, seeming well satisfied with the success of his mission. “Mr. Kalganov has kindly provided these for this unusual emergency, as well as a clean shirt. Luckily he had them all in his trunk. You can keep your own socks and underclothes.”\n\nMitya flew into a passion.\n\n“I won’t have other people’s clothes!” he shouted menacingly, “give me my own!”\n\n“It’s impossible!”\n\n“Give me my own. Damn Kalganov and his clothes, too!”\n\nIt was a long time before they could persuade him. But they succeeded somehow in quieting him down. They impressed upon him that his clothes, being stained with blood, must be “included with the other material evidence,” and that they “had not even the right to let him have them now ... taking into consideration the possible outcome of the case.” Mitya at last understood this. He subsided into gloomy silence and hurriedly dressed himself. He merely observed, as he put them on, that the clothes were much better than his old ones, and that he disliked “gaining by the change.” The coat was, besides, “ridiculously tight. Am I to be dressed up like a fool ... for your amusement?”\n\nThey urged upon him again that he was exaggerating, that Kalganov was only a little taller, so that only the trousers might be a little too long. But the coat turned out to be really tight in the shoulders.\n\n“Damn it all! I can hardly button it,” Mitya grumbled. “Be so good as to tell Mr. Kalganov from me that I didn’t ask for his clothes, and it’s not my doing that they’ve dressed me up like a clown.”\n\n“He understands that, and is sorry ... I mean, not sorry to lend you his clothes, but sorry about all this business,” mumbled Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“Confound his sorrow! Well, where now? Am I to go on sitting here?”\n\nHe was asked to go back to the “other room.” Mitya went in, scowling with anger, and trying to avoid looking at any one. Dressed in another man’s clothes he felt himself disgraced, even in the eyes of the peasants, and of Trifon Borissovitch, whose face appeared, for some reason, in the doorway, and vanished immediately. “He’s come to look at me dressed up,” thought Mitya. He sat down on the same chair as before. He had an absurd nightmarish feeling, as though he were out of his mind.\n\n“Well, what now? Are you going to flog me? That’s all that’s left for you,” he said, clenching his teeth and addressing the prosecutor. He would not turn to Nikolay Parfenovitch, as though he disdained to speak to him.\n\n“He looked too closely at my socks, and turned them inside out on purpose to show every one how dirty they were—the scoundrel!”\n\n“Well, now we must proceed to the examination of witnesses,” observed Nikolay Parfenovitch, as though in reply to Mitya’s question.\n\n“Yes,” said the prosecutor thoughtfully, as though reflecting on something.\n\n“We’ve done what we could in your interest, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” Nikolay Parfenovitch went on, “but having received from you such an uncompromising refusal to explain to us the source from which you obtained the money found upon you, we are, at the present moment—”\n\n“What is the stone in your ring?” Mitya interrupted suddenly, as though awakening from a reverie. He pointed to one of the three large rings adorning Nikolay Parfenovitch’s right hand.\n\n“Ring?” repeated Nikolay Parfenovitch with surprise.\n\n“Yes, that one ... on your middle finger, with the little veins in it, what stone is that?” Mitya persisted, like a peevish child.\n\n“That’s a smoky topaz,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, smiling. “Would you like to look at it? I’ll take it off ...”\n\n“No, don’t take it off,” cried Mitya furiously, suddenly waking up, and angry with himself. “Don’t take it off ... there’s no need.... Damn it!... Gentlemen, you’ve sullied my heart! Can you suppose that I would conceal it from you, if I had really killed my father, that I would shuffle, lie, and hide myself? No, that’s not like Dmitri Karamazov, that he couldn’t do, and if I were guilty, I swear I shouldn’t have waited for your coming, or for the sunrise as I meant at first, but should have killed myself before this, without waiting for the dawn! I know that about myself now. I couldn’t have learnt so much in twenty years as I’ve found out in this accursed night!... And should I have been like this on this night, and at this moment, sitting with you, could I have talked like this, could I have moved like this, could I have looked at you and at the world like this, if I had really been the murderer of my father, when the very thought of having accidentally killed Grigory gave me no peace all night—not from fear—oh, not simply from fear of your punishment! The disgrace of it! And you expect me to be open with such scoffers as you, who see nothing and believe in nothing, blind moles and scoffers, and to tell you another nasty thing I’ve done, another disgrace, even if that would save me from your accusation! No, better Siberia! The man who opened the door to my father and went in at that door, he killed him, he robbed him. Who was he? I’m racking my brains and can’t think who. But I can tell you it was not Dmitri Karamazov, and that’s all I can tell you, and that’s enough, enough, leave me alone.... Exile me, punish me, but don’t bother me any more. I’ll say no more. Call your witnesses!”\n\nMitya uttered his sudden monologue as though he were determined to be absolutely silent for the future. The prosecutor watched him the whole time and only when he had ceased speaking, observed, as though it were the most ordinary thing, with the most frigid and composed air:\n\n“Oh, about the open door of which you spoke just now, we may as well inform you, by the way, now, of a very interesting piece of evidence of the greatest importance both to you and to us, that has been given us by Grigory, the old man you wounded. On his recovery, he clearly and emphatically stated, in reply to our questions, that when, on coming out to the steps, and hearing a noise in the garden, he made up his mind to go into it through the little gate which stood open, before he noticed you running, as you have told us already, in the dark from the open window where you saw your father, he, Grigory, glanced to the left, and, while noticing the open window, observed at the same time, much nearer to him, the door, standing wide open—that door which you have stated to have been shut the whole time you were in the garden. I will not conceal from you that Grigory himself confidently affirms and bears witness that you must have run from that door, though, of course, he did not see you do so with his own eyes, since he only noticed you first some distance away in the garden, running towards the fence.”\n\nMitya had leapt up from his chair half-way through this speech.\n\n“Nonsense!” he yelled, in a sudden frenzy, “it’s a barefaced lie. He couldn’t have seen the door open because it was shut. He’s lying!”\n\n“I consider it my duty to repeat that he is firm in his statement. He does not waver. He adheres to it. We’ve cross-examined him several times.”\n\n“Precisely. I have cross-examined him several times,” Nikolay Parfenovitch confirmed warmly.\n\n“It’s false, false! It’s either an attempt to slander me, or the hallucination of a madman,” Mitya still shouted. “He’s simply raving, from loss of blood, from the wound. He must have fancied it when he came to.... He’s raving.”\n\n“Yes, but he noticed the open door, not when he came to after his injuries, but before that, as soon as he went into the garden from the lodge.”\n\n“But it’s false, it’s false! It can’t be so! He’s slandering me from spite.... He couldn’t have seen it ... I didn’t come from the door,” gasped Mitya.\n\nThe prosecutor turned to Nikolay Parfenovitch and said to him impressively:\n\n“Confront him with it.”\n\n“Do you recognize this object?”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch laid upon the table a large and thick official envelope, on which three seals still remained intact. The envelope was empty, and slit open at one end. Mitya stared at it with open eyes.\n\n“It ... it must be that envelope of my father’s, the envelope that contained the three thousand roubles ... and if there’s inscribed on it, allow me, ‘For my little chicken’ ... yes—three thousand!” he shouted, “do you see, three thousand, do you see?”\n\n“Of course, we see. But we didn’t find the money in it. It was empty, and lying on the floor by the bed, behind the screen.”\n\nFor some seconds Mitya stood as though thunderstruck.\n\n“Gentlemen, it’s Smerdyakov!” he shouted suddenly, at the top of his voice. “It’s he who’s murdered him! He’s robbed him! No one else knew where the old man hid the envelope. It’s Smerdyakov, that’s clear, now!”\n\n“But you, too, knew of the envelope and that it was under the pillow.”\n\n“I never knew it. I’ve never seen it. This is the first time I’ve looked at it. I’d only heard of it from Smerdyakov.... He was the only one who knew where the old man kept it hidden, I didn’t know ...” Mitya was completely breathless.\n\n“But you told us yourself that the envelope was under your deceased father’s pillow. You especially stated that it was under the pillow, so you must have known it.”\n\n“We’ve got it written down,” confirmed Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“Nonsense! It’s absurd! I’d no idea it was under the pillow. And perhaps it wasn’t under the pillow at all.... It was just a chance guess that it was under the pillow. What does Smerdyakov say? Have you asked him where it was? What does Smerdyakov say? that’s the chief point.... And I went out of my way to tell lies against myself.... I told you without thinking that it was under the pillow, and now you— Oh, you know how one says the wrong thing, without meaning it. No one knew but Smerdyakov, only Smerdyakov, and no one else.... He didn’t even tell me where it was! But it’s his doing, his doing; there’s no doubt about it, he murdered him, that’s as clear as daylight now,” Mitya exclaimed more and more frantically, repeating himself incoherently, and growing more and more exasperated and excited. “You must understand that, and arrest him at once.... He must have killed him while I was running away and while Grigory was unconscious, that’s clear now.... He gave the signal and father opened to him ... for no one but he knew the signal, and without the signal father would never have opened the door....”\n\n“But you’re again forgetting the circumstance,” the prosecutor observed, still speaking with the same restraint, though with a note of triumph, “that there was no need to give the signal if the door already stood open when you were there, while you were in the garden....”\n\n“The door, the door,” muttered Mitya, and he stared speechless at the prosecutor. He sank back helpless in his chair. All were silent.\n\n“Yes, the door!... It’s a nightmare! God is against me!” he exclaimed, staring before him in complete stupefaction.\n\n“Come, you see,” the prosecutor went on with dignity, “and you can judge for yourself, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. On the one hand we have the evidence of the open door from which you ran out, a fact which overwhelms you and us. On the other side your incomprehensible, persistent, and, so to speak, obdurate silence with regard to the source from which you obtained the money which was so suddenly seen in your hands, when only three hours earlier, on your own showing, you pledged your pistols for the sake of ten roubles! In view of all these facts, judge for yourself. What are we to believe, and what can we depend upon? And don’t accuse us of being ‘frigid, cynical, scoffing people,’ who are incapable of believing in the generous impulses of your heart.... Try to enter into our position ...”\n\nMitya was indescribably agitated. He turned pale.\n\n“Very well!” he exclaimed suddenly. “I will tell you my secret. I’ll tell you where I got the money!... I’ll reveal my shame, that I may not have to blame myself or you hereafter.”\n\n“And believe me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” put in Nikolay Parfenovitch, in a voice of almost pathetic delight, “that every sincere and complete confession on your part at this moment may, later on, have an immense influence in your favor, and may, indeed, moreover—”\n\nBut the prosecutor gave him a slight shove under the table, and he checked himself in time. Mitya, it is true, had not heard him.\n\n# Chapter VII. Mitya’s Great Secret. Received With Hisses\n\n“Gentlemen,” he began, still in the same agitation, “I want to make a full confession: that money was my own.” The lawyers’ faces lengthened. That was not at all what they expected.\n\n“How do you mean?” faltered Nikolay Parfenovitch, “when at five o’clock on the same day, from your own confession—”\n\n“Damn five o’clock on the same day and my own confession! That’s nothing to do with it now! That money was my own, my own, that is, stolen by me ... not mine, I mean, but stolen by me, and it was fifteen hundred roubles, and I had it on me all the time, all the time ...”\n\n“But where did you get it?”\n\n“I took it off my neck, gentlemen, off this very neck ... it was here, round my neck, sewn up in a rag, and I’d had it round my neck a long time, it’s a month since I put it round my neck ... to my shame and disgrace!”\n\n“And from whom did you ... appropriate it?”\n\n“You mean, ‘steal it’? Speak out plainly now. Yes, I consider that I practically stole it, but, if you prefer, I ‘appropriated it.’ I consider I stole it. And last night I stole it finally.”\n\n“Last night? But you said that it’s a month since you ... obtained it?...”\n\n“Yes. But not from my father. Not from my father, don’t be uneasy. I didn’t steal it from my father, but from her. Let me tell you without interrupting. It’s hard to do, you know. You see, a month ago, I was sent for by Katerina Ivanovna, formerly my betrothed. Do you know her?”\n\n“Yes, of course.”\n\n“I know you know her. She’s a noble creature, noblest of the noble. But she has hated me ever so long, oh, ever so long ... and hated me with good reason, good reason!”\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna!” Nikolay Parfenovitch exclaimed with wonder. The prosecutor, too, stared.\n\n“Oh, don’t take her name in vain! I’m a scoundrel to bring her into it. Yes, I’ve seen that she hated me ... a long while.... From the very first, even that evening at my lodging ... but enough, enough. You’re unworthy even to know of that. No need of that at all.... I need only tell you that she sent for me a month ago, gave me three thousand roubles to send off to her sister and another relation in Moscow (as though she couldn’t have sent it off herself!) and I ... it was just at that fatal moment in my life when I ... well, in fact, when I’d just come to love another, her, she’s sitting down below now, Grushenka. I carried her off here to Mokroe then, and wasted here in two days half that damned three thousand, but the other half I kept on me. Well, I’ve kept that other half, that fifteen hundred, like a locket round my neck, but yesterday I undid it, and spent it. What’s left of it, eight hundred roubles, is in your hands now, Nikolay Parfenovitch. That’s the change out of the fifteen hundred I had yesterday.”\n\n“Excuse me. How’s that? Why, when you were here a month ago you spent three thousand, not fifteen hundred, everybody knows that.”\n\n“Who knows it? Who counted the money? Did I let any one count it?”\n\n“Why, you told every one yourself that you’d spent exactly three thousand.”\n\n“It’s true, I did. I told the whole town so, and the whole town said so. And here, at Mokroe, too, every one reckoned it was three thousand. Yet I didn’t spend three thousand, but fifteen hundred. And the other fifteen hundred I sewed into a little bag. That’s how it was, gentlemen. That’s where I got that money yesterday....”\n\n“This is almost miraculous,” murmured Nikolay Parfenovitch.\n\n“Allow me to inquire,” observed the prosecutor at last, “have you informed any one whatever of this circumstance before, I mean that you had fifteen hundred left about you a month ago?”\n\n“I told no one.”\n“That’s strange. Do you mean absolutely no one?”\n\n“Absolutely no one. No one and nobody.”\n\n“What was your reason for this reticence? What was your motive for making such a secret of it? To be more precise: You have told us at last your secret, in your words, so ‘disgraceful,’ though in reality—that is, of course, comparatively speaking—this action, that is, the appropriation of three thousand roubles belonging to some one else, and, of course, only for a time is, in my view at least, only an act of the greatest recklessness and not so disgraceful, when one takes into consideration your character.... Even admitting that it was an action in the highest degree discreditable, still, discreditable is not ‘disgraceful.’... Many people have already guessed, during this last month, about the three thousand of Katerina Ivanovna’s, that you have spent, and I heard the legend myself, apart from your confession.... Mihail Makarovitch, for instance, had heard it, too, so that indeed, it was scarcely a legend, but the gossip of the whole town. There are indications, too, if I am not mistaken, that you confessed this yourself to some one, I mean that the money was Katerina Ivanovna’s, and so, it’s extremely surprising to me that hitherto, that is, up to the present moment, you have made such an extraordinary secret of the fifteen hundred you say you put by, apparently connecting a feeling of positive horror with that secret.... It’s not easy to believe that it could cost you such distress to confess such a secret.... You cried out, just now, that Siberia would be better than confessing it ...”\n\nThe prosecutor ceased speaking. He was provoked. He did not conceal his vexation, which was almost anger, and gave vent to all his accumulated spleen, disconnectedly and incoherently, without choosing words.\n\n“It’s not the fifteen hundred that’s the disgrace, but that I put it apart from the rest of the three thousand,” said Mitya firmly.\n\n“Why?” smiled the prosecutor irritably. “What is there disgraceful, to your thinking, in your having set aside half of the three thousand you had discreditably, if you prefer, ‘disgracefully,’ appropriated? Your taking the three thousand is more important than what you did with it. And by the way, why did you do that—why did you set apart that half, for what purpose, for what object did you do it? Can you explain that to us?”\n\n“Oh, gentlemen, the purpose is the whole point!” cried Mitya. “I put it aside because I was vile, that is, because I was calculating, and to be calculating in such a case is vile ... and that vileness has been going on a whole month.”\n\n“It’s incomprehensible.”\n\n“I wonder at you. But I’ll make it clearer. Perhaps it really is incomprehensible. You see, attend to what I say. I appropriate three thousand entrusted to my honor, I spend it on a spree, say I spend it all, and next morning I go to her and say, ‘Katya, I’ve done wrong, I’ve squandered your three thousand,’ well, is that right? No, it’s not right—it’s dishonest and cowardly, I’m a beast, with no more self-control than a beast, that’s so, isn’t it? But still I’m not a thief? Not a downright thief, you’ll admit! I squandered it, but I didn’t steal it. Now a second, rather more favorable alternative: follow me carefully, or I may get confused again—my head’s going round—and so, for the second alternative: I spend here only fifteen hundred out of the three thousand, that is, only half. Next day I go and take that half to her: ‘Katya, take this fifteen hundred from me, I’m a low beast, and an untrustworthy scoundrel, for I’ve wasted half the money, and I shall waste this, too, so keep me from temptation!’ Well, what of that alternative? I should be a beast and a scoundrel, and whatever you like; but not a thief, not altogether a thief, or I should not have brought back what was left, but have kept that, too. She would see at once that since I brought back half, I should pay back what I’d spent, that I should never give up trying to, that I should work to get it and pay it back. So in that case I should be a scoundrel, but not a thief, you may say what you like, not a thief!”\n\n“I admit that there is a certain distinction,” said the prosecutor, with a cold smile. “But it’s strange that you see such a vital difference.”\n\n“Yes, I see a vital difference! Every man may be a scoundrel, and perhaps every man is a scoundrel, but not every one can be a thief, it takes an arch-scoundrel to be that. Oh, of course, I don’t know how to make these fine distinctions ... but a thief is lower than a scoundrel, that’s my conviction. Listen, I carry the money about me a whole month, I may make up my mind to give it back to-morrow, and I’m a scoundrel no longer, but I cannot make up my mind, you see, though I’m making up my mind every day, and every day spurring myself on to do it, and yet for a whole month I can’t bring myself to it, you see. Is that right to your thinking, is that right?”\n\n“Certainly, that’s not right, that I can quite understand, and that I don’t dispute,” answered the prosecutor with reserve. “And let us give up all discussion of these subtleties and distinctions, and, if you will be so kind, get back to the point. And the point is, that you have still not told us, altogether we’ve asked you, why, in the first place, you halved the money, squandering one half and hiding the other? For what purpose exactly did you hide it, what did you mean to do with that fifteen hundred? I insist upon that question, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“Yes, of course!” cried Mitya, striking himself on the forehead; “forgive me, I’m worrying you, and am not explaining the chief point, or you’d understand in a minute, for it’s just the motive of it that’s the disgrace! You see, it was all to do with the old man, my dead father. He was always pestering Agrafena Alexandrovna, and I was jealous; I thought then that she was hesitating between me and him. So I kept thinking every day, suppose she were to make up her mind all of a sudden, suppose she were to leave off tormenting me, and were suddenly to say to me, ‘I love you, not him; take me to the other end of the world.’ And I’d only forty copecks; how could I take her away, what could I do? Why, I’d be lost. You see, I didn’t know her then, I didn’t understand her, I thought she wanted money, and that she wouldn’t forgive my poverty. And so I fiendishly counted out the half of that three thousand, sewed it up, calculating on it, sewed it up before I was drunk, and after I had sewn it up, I went off to get drunk on the rest. Yes, that was base. Do you understand now?”\n\nBoth the lawyers laughed aloud.\n\n“I should have called it sensible and moral on your part not to have squandered it all,” chuckled Nikolay Parfenovitch, “for after all what does it amount to?”\n\n“Why, that I stole it, that’s what it amounts to! Oh, God, you horrify me by not understanding! Every day that I had that fifteen hundred sewn up round my neck, every day and every hour I said to myself, ‘You’re a thief! you’re a thief!’ Yes, that’s why I’ve been so savage all this month, that’s why I fought in the tavern, that’s why I attacked my father, it was because I felt I was a thief. I couldn’t make up my mind, I didn’t dare even to tell Alyosha, my brother, about that fifteen hundred: I felt I was such a scoundrel and such a pickpocket. But, do you know, while I carried it I said to myself at the same time every hour: ‘No, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, you may yet not be a thief.’ Why? Because I might go next day and pay back that fifteen hundred to Katya. And only yesterday I made up my mind to tear my amulet off my neck, on my way from Fenya’s to Perhotin. I hadn’t been able till that moment to bring myself to it. And it was only when I tore it off that I became a downright thief, a thief and a dishonest man for the rest of my life. Why? Because, with that I destroyed, too, my dream of going to Katya and saying, ‘I’m a scoundrel, but not a thief!’ Do you understand now? Do you understand?”\n\n“What was it made you decide to do it yesterday?” Nikolay Parfenovitch interrupted.\n\n“Why? It’s absurd to ask. Because I had condemned myself to die at five o’clock this morning, here, at dawn. I thought it made no difference whether I died a thief or a man of honor. But I see it’s not so, it turns out that it does make a difference. Believe me, gentlemen, what has tortured me most during this night has not been the thought that I’d killed the old servant, and that I was in danger of Siberia just when my love was being rewarded, and Heaven was open to me again. Oh, that did torture me, but not in the same way: not so much as the damned consciousness that I had torn that damned money off my breast at last and spent it, and had become a downright thief! Oh, gentlemen, I tell you again, with a bleeding heart, I have learnt a great deal this night. I have learnt that it’s not only impossible to live a scoundrel, but impossible to die a scoundrel.... No, gentlemen, one must die honest....”\n\nMitya was pale. His face had a haggard and exhausted look, in spite of his being intensely excited.\n\n“I am beginning to understand you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” the prosecutor said slowly, in a soft and almost compassionate tone. “But all this, if you’ll excuse my saying so, is a matter of nerves, in my opinion ... your overwrought nerves, that’s what it is. And why, for instance, should you not have saved yourself such misery for almost a month, by going and returning that fifteen hundred to the lady who had entrusted it to you? And why could you not have explained things to her, and in view of your position, which you describe as being so awful, why could you not have had recourse to the plan which would so naturally have occurred to one’s mind, that is, after honorably confessing your errors to her, why could you not have asked her to lend you the sum needed for your expenses, which, with her generous heart, she would certainly not have refused you in your distress, especially if it had been with some guarantee, or even on the security you offered to the merchant Samsonov, and to Madame Hohlakov? I suppose you still regard that security as of value?”\n\nMitya suddenly crimsoned.\n\n“Surely you don’t think me such an out and out scoundrel as that? You can’t be speaking in earnest?” he said, with indignation, looking the prosecutor straight in the face, and seeming unable to believe his ears.\n\n“I assure you I’m in earnest.... Why do you imagine I’m not serious?” It was the prosecutor’s turn to be surprised.\n\n“Oh, how base that would have been! Gentlemen, do you know, you are torturing me! Let me tell you everything, so be it. I’ll confess all my infernal wickedness, but to put you to shame, and you’ll be surprised yourselves at the depth of ignominy to which a medley of human passions can sink. You must know that I already had that plan myself, that plan you spoke of, just now, prosecutor! Yes, gentlemen, I, too, have had that thought in my mind all this current month, so that I was on the point of deciding to go to Katya—I was mean enough for that. But to go to her, to tell her of my treachery, and for that very treachery, to carry it out, for the expenses of that treachery, to beg for money from her, Katya (to beg, do you hear, to beg), and go straight from her to run away with the other, the rival, who hated and insulted her—to think of it! You must be mad, prosecutor!”\n\n“Mad I am not, but I did speak in haste, without thinking ... of that feminine jealousy ... if there could be jealousy in this case, as you assert ... yes, perhaps there is something of the kind,” said the prosecutor, smiling.\n\n“But that would have been so infamous!” Mitya brought his fist down on the table fiercely. “That would have been filthy beyond everything! Yes, do you know that she might have given me that money, yes, and she would have given it, too; she’d have been certain to give it, to be revenged on me, she’d have given it to satisfy her vengeance, to show her contempt for me, for hers is an infernal nature, too, and she’s a woman of great wrath. I’d have taken the money, too, oh, I should have taken it; I should have taken it, and then, for the rest of my life ... oh, God! Forgive me, gentlemen, I’m making such an outcry because I’ve had that thought in my mind so lately, only the day before yesterday, that night when I was having all that bother with Lyagavy, and afterwards yesterday, all day yesterday, I remember, till that happened ...”\n\n“Till what happened?” put in Nikolay Parfenovitch inquisitively, but Mitya did not hear it.\n\n“I have made you an awful confession,” Mitya said gloomily in conclusion. “You must appreciate it, and what’s more, you must respect it, for if not, if that leaves your souls untouched, then you’ve simply no respect for me, gentlemen, I tell you that, and I shall die of shame at having confessed it to men like you! Oh, I shall shoot myself! Yes, I see, I see already that you don’t believe me. What, you want to write that down, too?” he cried in dismay.\n\n“Yes, what you said just now,” said Nikolay Parfenovitch, looking at him in surprise, “that is, that up to the last hour you were still contemplating going to Katerina Ivanovna to beg that sum from her.... I assure you, that’s a very important piece of evidence for us, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I mean for the whole case ... and particularly for you, particularly important for you.”\n\n“Have mercy, gentlemen!” Mitya flung up his hands. “Don’t write that, anyway; have some shame. Here I’ve torn my heart asunder before you, and you seize the opportunity and are fingering the wounds in both halves.... Oh, my God!”\n\nIn despair he hid his face in his hands.\n\n“Don’t worry yourself so, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” observed the prosecutor, “everything that is written down will be read over to you afterwards, and what you don’t agree to we’ll alter as you like. But now I’ll ask you one little question for the second time. Has no one, absolutely no one, heard from you of that money you sewed up? That, I must tell you, is almost impossible to believe.”\n\n“No one, no one, I told you so before, or you’ve not understood anything! Let me alone!”\n\n“Very well, this matter is bound to be explained, and there’s plenty of time for it, but meantime, consider; we have perhaps a dozen witnesses that you yourself spread it abroad, and even shouted almost everywhere about the three thousand you’d spent here; three thousand, not fifteen hundred. And now, too, when you got hold of the money you had yesterday, you gave many people to understand that you had brought three thousand with you.”\n\n“You’ve got not dozens, but hundreds of witnesses, two hundred witnesses, two hundred have heard it, thousands have heard it!” cried Mitya.\n\n“Well, you see, all bear witness to it. And the word all means something.”\n\n“It means nothing. I talked rot, and every one began repeating it.”\n\n“But what need had you to ‘talk rot,’ as you call it?”\n\n“The devil knows. From bravado perhaps ... at having wasted so much money.... To try and forget that money I had sewn up, perhaps ... yes, that was why ... damn it ... how often will you ask me that question? Well, I told a fib, and that was the end of it, once I’d said it, I didn’t care to correct it. What does a man tell lies for sometimes?”\n\n“That’s very difficult to decide, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, what makes a man tell lies,” observed the prosecutor impressively. “Tell me, though, was that ‘amulet,’ as you call it, on your neck, a big thing?”\n\n“No, not big.”\n\n“How big, for instance?”\n\n“If you fold a hundred-rouble note in half, that would be the size.”\n\n“You’d better show us the remains of it. You must have them somewhere.”\n\n“Damnation, what nonsense! I don’t know where they are.”\n\n“But excuse me: where and when did you take it off your neck? According to your own evidence you didn’t go home.”\n\n“When I was going from Fenya’s to Perhotin’s, on the way I tore it off my neck and took out the money.”\n\n“In the dark?”\n\n“What should I want a light for? I did it with my fingers in one minute.”\n\n“Without scissors, in the street?”\n\n“In the market-place I think it was. Why scissors? It was an old rag. It was torn in a minute.”\n\n“Where did you put it afterwards?”\n\n“I dropped it there.”\n\n“Where was it, exactly?”\n\n“In the market-place, in the market-place! The devil knows whereabouts. What do you want to know for?”\n\n“That’s extremely important, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. It would be material evidence in your favor. How is it you don’t understand that? Who helped you to sew it up a month ago?”\n\n“No one helped me. I did it myself.”\n\n“Can you sew?”\n\n“A soldier has to know how to sew. No knowledge was needed to do that.”\n\n“Where did you get the material, that is, the rag in which you sewed the money?”\n\n“Are you laughing at me?”\n\n“Not at all. And we are in no mood for laughing, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“I don’t know where I got the rag from—somewhere, I suppose.”\n\n“I should have thought you couldn’t have forgotten it?”\n\n“Upon my word, I don’t remember. I might have torn a bit off my linen.”\n\n“That’s very interesting. We might find in your lodgings to-morrow the shirt or whatever it is from which you tore the rag. What sort of rag was it, cloth or linen?”\n\n“Goodness only knows what it was. Wait a bit.... I believe I didn’t tear it off anything. It was a bit of calico.... I believe I sewed it up in a cap of my landlady’s.”\n\n“In your landlady’s cap?”\n\n“Yes. I took it from her.”\n\n“How did you get it?”\n\n“You see, I remember once taking a cap for a rag, perhaps to wipe my pen on. I took it without asking, because it was a worthless rag. I tore it up, and I took the notes and sewed them up in it. I believe it was in that very rag I sewed them. An old piece of calico, washed a thousand times.”\n\n“And you remember that for certain now?”\n\n“I don’t know whether for certain. I think it was in the cap. But, hang it, what does it matter?”\n\n“In that case your landlady will remember that the thing was lost?”\n\n“No, she won’t, she didn’t miss it. It was an old rag, I tell you, an old rag not worth a farthing.”\n\n“And where did you get the needle and thread?”\n\n“I’ll stop now. I won’t say any more. Enough of it!” said Mitya, losing his temper at last.\n\n“It’s strange that you should have so completely forgotten where you threw the pieces in the market-place.”\n\n“Give orders for the market-place to be swept to-morrow, and perhaps you’ll find it,” said Mitya, sneering. “Enough, gentlemen, enough!” he decided, in an exhausted voice. “I see you don’t believe me! Not for a moment! It’s my fault, not yours. I ought not to have been so ready. Why, why did I degrade myself by confessing my secret to you? It’s a joke to you. I see that from your eyes. You led me on to it, prosecutor? Sing a hymn of triumph if you can.... Damn you, you torturers!”\n\nHe bent his head, and hid his face in his hands. The lawyers were silent. A minute later he raised his head and looked at them almost vacantly. His face now expressed complete, hopeless despair, and he sat mute and passive as though hardly conscious of what was happening. In the meantime they had to finish what they were about. They had immediately to begin examining the witnesses. It was by now eight o’clock in the morning. The lights had been extinguished long ago. Mihail Makarovitch and Kalganov, who had been continually in and out of the room all the while the interrogation had been going on, had now both gone out again. The lawyers, too, looked very tired. It was a wretched morning, the whole sky was overcast, and the rain streamed down in bucketfuls. Mitya gazed blankly out of the window.\n\n“May I look out of the window?” he asked Nikolay Parfenovitch, suddenly.\n\n“Oh, as much as you like,” the latter replied.\n\nMitya got up and went to the window.... The rain lashed against its little greenish panes. He could see the muddy road just below the house, and farther away, in the rain and mist, a row of poor, black, dismal huts, looking even blacker and poorer in the rain. Mitya thought of “Phœbus the golden-haired,” and how he had meant to shoot himself at his first ray. “Perhaps it would be even better on a morning like this,” he thought with a smile, and suddenly, flinging his hand downwards, he turned to his “torturers.”\n\n“Gentlemen,” he cried, “I see that I am lost! But she? Tell me about her, I beseech you. Surely she need not be ruined with me? She’s innocent, you know, she was out of her mind when she cried last night ‘It’s all my fault!’ She’s done nothing, nothing! I’ve been grieving over her all night as I sat with you.... Can’t you, won’t you tell me what you are going to do with her now?”\n\n“You can set your mind quite at rest on that score, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” the prosecutor answered at once, with evident alacrity. “We have, so far, no grounds for interfering with the lady in whom you are so interested. I trust that it may be the same in the later development of the case.... On the contrary, we’ll do everything that lies in our power in that matter. Set your mind completely at rest.”\n\n“Gentlemen, I thank you. I knew that you were honest, straight-forward people in spite of everything. You’ve taken a load off my heart.... Well, what are we to do now? I’m ready.”\n\n“Well, we ought to make haste. We must pass to examining the witnesses without delay. That must be done in your presence and therefore—”\n\n“Shouldn’t we have some tea first?” interposed Nikolay Parfenovitch, “I think we’ve deserved it!”\n\nThey decided that if tea were ready downstairs (Mihail Makarovitch had, no doubt, gone down to get some) they would have a glass and then “go on and on,” putting off their proper breakfast until a more favorable opportunity. Tea really was ready below, and was soon brought up. Mitya at first refused the glass that Nikolay Parfenovitch politely offered him, but afterwards he asked for it himself and drank it greedily. He looked surprisingly exhausted. It might have been supposed from his Herculean strength that one night of carousing, even accompanied by the most violent emotions, could have had little effect on him. But he felt that he could hardly hold his head up, and from time to time all the objects about him seemed heaving and dancing before his eyes. “A little more and I shall begin raving,” he said to himself.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Evidence Of The Witnesses. The Babe\n\nThe examination of the witnesses began. But we will not continue our story in such detail as before. And so we will not dwell on how Nikolay Parfenovitch impressed on every witness called that he must give his evidence in accordance with truth and conscience, and that he would afterwards have to repeat his evidence on oath, how every witness was called upon to sign the protocol of his evidence, and so on. We will only note that the point principally insisted upon in the examination was the question of the three thousand roubles, that is, was the sum spent here, at Mokroe, by Mitya on the first occasion, a month before, three thousand or fifteen hundred? And again had he spent three thousand or fifteen hundred yesterday? Alas, all the evidence given by every one turned out to be against Mitya. There was not one in his favor, and some witnesses introduced new, almost crushing facts, in contradiction of his, Mitya’s, story.\n\nThe first witness examined was Trifon Borissovitch. He was not in the least abashed as he stood before the lawyers. He had, on the contrary, an air of stern and severe indignation with the accused, which gave him an appearance of truthfulness and personal dignity. He spoke little, and with reserve, waited to be questioned, answered precisely and deliberately. Firmly and unhesitatingly he bore witness that the sum spent a month before could not have been less than three thousand, that all the peasants about here would testify that they had heard the sum of three thousand mentioned by Dmitri Fyodorovitch himself. “What a lot of money he flung away on the gypsy girls alone! He wasted a thousand, I daresay, on them alone.”\n\n“I don’t believe I gave them five hundred,” was Mitya’s gloomy comment on this. “It’s a pity I didn’t count the money at the time, but I was drunk....”\n\nMitya was sitting sideways with his back to the curtains. He listened gloomily, with a melancholy and exhausted air, as though he would say:\n\n“Oh, say what you like. It makes no difference now.”\n\n“More than a thousand went on them, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,” retorted Trifon Borissovitch firmly. “You flung it about at random and they picked it up. They were a rascally, thievish lot, horse-stealers, they’ve been driven away from here, or maybe they’d bear witness themselves how much they got from you. I saw the sum in your hands, myself—count it I didn’t, you didn’t let me, that’s true enough—but by the look of it I should say it was far more than fifteen hundred ... fifteen hundred, indeed! We’ve seen money too. We can judge of amounts....”\n\nAs for the sum spent yesterday he asserted that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had told him, as soon as he arrived, that he had brought three thousand with him.\n\n“Come now, is that so, Trifon Borissovitch?” replied Mitya. “Surely I didn’t declare so positively that I’d brought three thousand?”\n\n“You did say so, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. You said it before Andrey. Andrey himself is still here. Send for him. And in the hall, when you were treating the chorus, you shouted straight out that you would leave your sixth thousand here—that is with what you spent before, we must understand. Stepan and Semyon heard it, and Pyotr Fomitch Kalganov, too, was standing beside you at the time. Maybe he’d remember it....”\n\nThe evidence as to the “sixth” thousand made an extraordinary impression on the two lawyers. They were delighted with this new mode of reckoning; three and three made six, three thousand then and three now made six, that was clear.\n\nThey questioned all the peasants suggested by Trifon Borissovitch, Stepan and Semyon, the driver Andrey, and Kalganov. The peasants and the driver unhesitatingly confirmed Trifon Borissovitch’s evidence. They noted down, with particular care, Andrey’s account of the conversation he had had with Mitya on the road: “‘Where,’ says he, ‘am I, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, going, to heaven or to hell, and shall I be forgiven in the next world or not?’”\n\nThe psychological Ippolit Kirillovitch heard this with a subtle smile, and ended by recommending that these remarks as to where Dmitri Fyodorovitch would go should be “included in the case.”\n\nKalganov, when called, came in reluctantly, frowning and ill-humored, and he spoke to the lawyers as though he had never met them before in his life, though they were acquaintances whom he had been meeting every day for a long time past. He began by saying that “he knew nothing about it and didn’t want to.” But it appeared that he had heard of the “sixth” thousand, and he admitted that he had been standing close by at the moment. As far as he could see he “didn’t know” how much money Mitya had in his hands. He affirmed that the Poles had cheated at cards. In reply to reiterated questions he stated that, after the Poles had been turned out, Mitya’s position with Agrafena Alexandrovna had certainly improved, and that she had said that she loved him. He spoke of Agrafena Alexandrovna with reserve and respect, as though she had been a lady of the best society, and did not once allow himself to call her Grushenka. In spite of the young man’s obvious repugnance at giving evidence, Ippolit Kirillovitch examined him at great length, and only from him learnt all the details of what made up Mitya’s “romance,” so to say, on that night. Mitya did not once pull Kalganov up. At last they let the young man go, and he left the room with unconcealed indignation.\n\nThe Poles, too, were examined. Though they had gone to bed in their room, they had not slept all night, and on the arrival of the police officers they hastily dressed and got ready, realizing that they would certainly be sent for. They gave their evidence with dignity, though not without some uneasiness. The little Pole turned out to be a retired official of the twelfth class, who had served in Siberia as a veterinary surgeon. His name was Mussyalovitch. Pan Vrublevsky turned out to be an uncertificated dentist. Although Nikolay Parfenovitch asked them questions on entering the room they both addressed their answers to Mihail Makarovitch, who was standing on one side, taking him in their ignorance for the most important person and in command, and addressed him at every word as “Pan Colonel.” Only after several reproofs from Mihail Makarovitch himself, they grasped that they had to address their answers to Nikolay Parfenovitch only. It turned out that they could speak Russian quite correctly except for their accent in some words. Of his relations with Grushenka, past and present, Pan Mussyalovitch spoke proudly and warmly, so that Mitya was roused at once and declared that he would not allow the “scoundrel” to speak like that in his presence! Pan Mussyalovitch at once called attention to the word “scoundrel” and begged that it should be put down in the protocol. Mitya fumed with rage.\n\n“He’s a scoundrel! A scoundrel! You can put that down. And put down, too, that, in spite of the protocol I still declare that he’s a scoundrel!” he cried.\n\nThough Nikolay Parfenovitch did insert this in the protocol, he showed the most praiseworthy tact and management. After sternly reprimanding Mitya, he cut short all further inquiry into the romantic aspect of the case, and hastened to pass to what was essential. One piece of evidence given by the Poles roused special interest in the lawyers: that was how, in that very room, Mitya had tried to buy off Pan Mussyalovitch, and had offered him three thousand roubles to resign his claims, seven hundred roubles down, and the remaining two thousand three hundred “to be paid next day in the town.” He had sworn at the time that he had not the whole sum with him at Mokroe, but that his money was in the town. Mitya observed hotly that he had not said that he would be sure to pay him the remainder next day in the town. But Pan Vrublevsky confirmed the statement, and Mitya, after thinking for a moment admitted, frowning, that it must have been as the Poles stated, that he had been excited at the time, and might indeed have said so.\n\nThe prosecutor positively pounced on this piece of evidence. It seemed to establish for the prosecution (and they did, in fact, base this deduction on it) that half, or a part of, the three thousand that had come into Mitya’s hands might really have been left somewhere hidden in the town, or even, perhaps, somewhere here, in Mokroe. This would explain the circumstance, so baffling for the prosecution, that only eight hundred roubles were to be found in Mitya’s hands. This circumstance had been the one piece of evidence which, insignificant as it was, had hitherto told, to some extent, in Mitya’s favor. Now this one piece of evidence in his favor had broken down. In answer to the prosecutor’s inquiry, where he would have got the remaining two thousand three hundred roubles, since he himself had denied having more than fifteen hundred, Mitya confidently replied that he had meant to offer the “little chap,” not money, but a formal deed of conveyance of his rights to the village of Tchermashnya, those rights which he had already offered to Samsonov and Madame Hohlakov. The prosecutor positively smiled at the “innocence of this subterfuge.”\n\n“And you imagine he would have accepted such a deed as a substitute for two thousand three hundred roubles in cash?”\n\n“He certainly would have accepted it,” Mitya declared warmly. “Why, look here, he might have grabbed not two thousand, but four or six, for it. He would have put his lawyers, Poles and Jews, on to the job, and might have got, not three thousand, but the whole property out of the old man.”\n\nThe evidence of Pan Mussyalovitch was, of course, entered in the protocol in the fullest detail. Then they let the Poles go. The incident of the cheating at cards was hardly touched upon. Nikolay Parfenovitch was too well pleased with them, as it was, and did not want to worry them with trifles, moreover, it was nothing but a foolish, drunken quarrel over cards. There had been drinking and disorder enough, that night.... So the two hundred roubles remained in the pockets of the Poles.\n\nThen old Maximov was summoned. He came in timidly, approached with little steps, looking very disheveled and depressed. He had, all this time, taken refuge below with Grushenka, sitting dumbly beside her, and “now and then he’d begin blubbering over her and wiping his eyes with a blue check handkerchief,” as Mihail Makarovitch described afterwards. So that she herself began trying to pacify and comfort him. The old man at once confessed that he had done wrong, that he had borrowed “ten roubles in my poverty,” from Dmitri Fyodorovitch, and that he was ready to pay it back. To Nikolay Parfenovitch’s direct question, had he noticed how much money Dmitri Fyodorovitch held in his hand, as he must have been able to see the sum better than any one when he took the note from him, Maximov, in the most positive manner, declared that there was twenty thousand.\n\n“Have you ever seen so much as twenty thousand before, then?” inquired Nikolay Parfenovitch, with a smile.\n\n“To be sure I have, not twenty, but seven, when my wife mortgaged my little property. She’d only let me look at it from a distance, boasting of it to me. It was a very thick bundle, all rainbow-colored notes. And Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s were all rainbow-colored....”\n\nHe was not kept long. At last it was Grushenka’s turn. Nikolay Parfenovitch was obviously apprehensive of the effect her appearance might have on Mitya, and he muttered a few words of admonition to him, but Mitya bowed his head in silence, giving him to understand “that he would not make a scene.” Mihail Makarovitch himself led Grushenka in. She entered with a stern and gloomy face, that looked almost composed and sat down quietly on the chair offered her by Nikolay Parfenovitch. She was very pale, she seemed to be cold, and wrapped herself closely in her magnificent black shawl. She was suffering from a slight feverish chill—the first symptom of the long illness which followed that night. Her grave air, her direct earnest look and quiet manner made a very favorable impression on every one. Nikolay Parfenovitch was even a little bit “fascinated.” He admitted himself, when talking about it afterwards, that only then had he seen “how handsome the woman was,” for, though he had seen her several times before, he had always looked upon her as something of a “provincial hetaira.” “She has the manners of the best society,” he said enthusiastically, gossiping about her in a circle of ladies. But this was received with positive indignation by the ladies, who immediately called him a “naughty man,” to his great satisfaction.\n\nAs she entered the room, Grushenka only glanced for an instant at Mitya, who looked at her uneasily. But her face reassured him at once. After the first inevitable inquiries and warnings, Nikolay Parfenovitch asked her, hesitating a little, but preserving the most courteous manner, on what terms she was with the retired lieutenant, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov. To this Grushenka firmly and quietly replied:\n\n“He was an acquaintance. He came to see me as an acquaintance during the last month.” To further inquisitive questions she answered plainly and with complete frankness, that, though “at times” she had thought him attractive, she had not loved him, but had won his heart as well as his old father’s “in my nasty spite,” that she had seen that Mitya was very jealous of Fyodor Pavlovitch and every one else; but that had only amused her. She had never meant to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch, she had simply been laughing at him. “I had no thoughts for either of them all this last month. I was expecting another man who had wronged me. But I think,” she said in conclusion, “that there’s no need for you to inquire about that, nor for me to answer you, for that’s my own affair.”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch immediately acted upon this hint. He again dismissed the “romantic” aspect of the case and passed to the serious one, that is, to the question of most importance, concerning the three thousand roubles. Grushenka confirmed the statement that three thousand roubles had certainly been spent on the first carousal at Mokroe, and, though she had not counted the money herself, she had heard that it was three thousand from Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s own lips.\n\n“Did he tell you that alone, or before some one else, or did you only hear him speak of it to others in your presence?” the prosecutor inquired immediately.\n\nTo which Grushenka replied that she had heard him say so before other people, and had heard him say so when they were alone.\n\n“Did he say it to you alone once, or several times?” inquired the prosecutor, and learned that he had told Grushenka so several times.\n\nIppolit Kirillovitch was very well satisfied with this piece of evidence. Further examination elicited that Grushenka knew, too, where that money had come from, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had got it from Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“And did you never, once, hear that the money spent a month ago was not three thousand, but less, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had saved half that sum for his own use?”\n\n“No, I never heard that,” answered Grushenka.\n\nIt was explained further that Mitya had, on the contrary, often told her that he hadn’t a farthing.\n\n“He was always expecting to get some from his father,” said Grushenka in conclusion.\n\n“Did he never say before you ... casually, or in a moment of irritation,” Nikolay Parfenovitch put in suddenly, “that he intended to make an attempt on his father’s life?”\n\n“Ach, he did say so,” sighed Grushenka.\n\n“Once or several times?”\n\n“He mentioned it several times, always in anger.”\n\n“And did you believe he would do it?”\n\n“No, I never believed it,” she answered firmly. “I had faith in his noble heart.”\n\n“Gentlemen, allow me,” cried Mitya suddenly, “allow me to say one word to Agrafena Alexandrovna, in your presence.”\n\n“You can speak,” Nikolay Parfenovitch assented.\n\n“Agrafena Alexandrovna!” Mitya got up from his chair, “have faith in God and in me. I am not guilty of my father’s murder!”\n\nHaving uttered these words Mitya sat down again on his chair. Grushenka stood up and crossed herself devoutly before the ikon. “Thanks be to Thee, O Lord,” she said, in a voice thrilled with emotion, and still standing, she turned to Nikolay Parfenovitch and added:\n\n“As he has spoken now, believe it! I know him. He’ll say anything as a joke or from obstinacy, but he’ll never deceive you against his conscience. He’s telling the whole truth, you may believe it.”\n\n“Thanks, Agrafena Alexandrovna, you’ve given me fresh courage,” Mitya responded in a quivering voice.\n\nAs to the money spent the previous day, she declared that she did not know what sum it was, but had heard him tell several people that he had three thousand with him. And to the question where he got the money, she said that he had told her that he had “stolen” it from Katerina Ivanovna, and that she had replied to that that he hadn’t stolen it, and that he must pay the money back next day. On the prosecutor’s asking her emphatically whether the money he said he had stolen from Katerina Ivanovna was what he had spent yesterday, or what he had squandered here a month ago, she declared that he meant the money spent a month ago, and that that was how she understood him.\n\nGrushenka was at last released, and Nikolay Parfenovitch informed her impulsively that she might at once return to the town and that if he could be of any assistance to her, with horses for example, or if she would care for an escort, he ... would be—\n\n“I thank you sincerely,” said Grushenka, bowing to him, “I’m going with this old gentleman, I am driving him back to town with me, and meanwhile, if you’ll allow me, I’ll wait below to hear what you decide about Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\nShe went out. Mitya was calm, and even looked more cheerful, but only for a moment. He felt more and more oppressed by a strange physical weakness. His eyes were closing with fatigue. The examination of the witnesses was, at last, over. They proceeded to a final revision of the protocol. Mitya got up, moved from his chair to the corner by the curtain, lay down on a large chest covered with a rug, and instantly fell asleep.\n\nHe had a strange dream, utterly out of keeping with the place and the time.\n\nHe was driving somewhere in the steppes, where he had been stationed long ago, and a peasant was driving him in a cart with a pair of horses, through snow and sleet. He was cold, it was early in November, and the snow was falling in big wet flakes, melting as soon as it touched the earth. And the peasant drove him smartly, he had a fair, long beard. He was not an old man, somewhere about fifty, and he had on a gray peasant’s smock. Not far off was a village, he could see the black huts, and half the huts were burnt down, there were only the charred beams sticking up. And as they drove in, there were peasant women drawn up along the road, a lot of women, a whole row, all thin and wan, with their faces a sort of brownish color, especially one at the edge, a tall, bony woman, who looked forty, but might have been only twenty, with a long thin face. And in her arms was a little baby crying. And her breasts seemed so dried up that there was not a drop of milk in them. And the child cried and cried, and held out its little bare arms, with its little fists blue from cold.\n\n“Why are they crying? Why are they crying?” Mitya asked, as they dashed gayly by.\n\n“It’s the babe,” answered the driver, “the babe weeping.”\n\nAnd Mitya was struck by his saying, in his peasant way, “the babe,” and he liked the peasant’s calling it a “babe.” There seemed more pity in it.\n\n“But why is it weeping?” Mitya persisted stupidly, “why are its little arms bare? Why don’t they wrap it up?”\n\n“The babe’s cold, its little clothes are frozen and don’t warm it.”\n\n“But why is it? Why?” foolish Mitya still persisted.\n\n“Why, they’re poor people, burnt out. They’ve no bread. They’re begging because they’ve been burnt out.”\n\n“No, no,” Mitya, as it were, still did not understand. “Tell me why it is those poor mothers stand there? Why are people poor? Why is the babe poor? Why is the steppe barren? Why don’t they hug each other and kiss? Why don’t they sing songs of joy? Why are they so dark from black misery? Why don’t they feed the babe?”\n\nAnd he felt that, though his questions were unreasonable and senseless, yet he wanted to ask just that, and he had to ask it just in that way. And he felt that a passion of pity, such as he had never known before, was rising in his heart, that he wanted to cry, that he wanted to do something for them all, so that the babe should weep no more, so that the dark-faced, dried-up mother should not weep, that no one should shed tears again from that moment, and he wanted to do it at once, at once, regardless of all obstacles, with all the recklessness of the Karamazovs.\n\n“And I’m coming with you. I won’t leave you now for the rest of my life, I’m coming with you,” he heard close beside him Grushenka’s tender voice, thrilling with emotion. And his heart glowed, and he struggled forward towards the light, and he longed to live, to live, to go on and on, towards the new, beckoning light, and to hasten, hasten, now, at once!\n\n“What! Where?” he exclaimed opening his eyes, and sitting up on the chest, as though he had revived from a swoon, smiling brightly. Nikolay Parfenovitch was standing over him, suggesting that he should hear the protocol read aloud and sign it. Mitya guessed that he had been asleep an hour or more, but he did not hear Nikolay Parfenovitch. He was suddenly struck by the fact that there was a pillow under his head, which hadn’t been there when he had leant back, exhausted, on the chest.\n\n“Who put that pillow under my head? Who was so kind?” he cried, with a sort of ecstatic gratitude, and tears in his voice, as though some great kindness had been shown him.\n\nHe never found out who this kind man was; perhaps one of the peasant witnesses, or Nikolay Parfenovitch’s little secretary, had compassionately thought to put a pillow under his head; but his whole soul was quivering with tears. He went to the table and said that he would sign whatever they liked.\n\n“I’ve had a good dream, gentlemen,” he said in a strange voice, with a new light, as of joy, in his face.\n\n# Chapter IX. They Carry Mitya Away\n\nWhen the protocol had been signed, Nikolay Parfenovitch turned solemnly to the prisoner and read him the “Committal,” setting forth, that in such a year, on such a day, in such a place, the investigating lawyer of such-and-such a district court, having examined so-and-so (to wit, Mitya) accused of this and of that (all the charges were carefully written out) and having considered that the accused, not pleading guilty to the charges made against him, had brought forward nothing in his defense, while the witnesses, so-and-so, and so-and-so, and the circumstances such-and-such testify against him, acting in accordance with such-and-such articles of the Statute Book, and so on, has ruled, that, in order to preclude so-and-so (Mitya) from all means of evading pursuit and judgment he be detained in such-and-such a prison, which he hereby notifies to the accused and communicates a copy of this same “Committal” to the deputy prosecutor, and so on, and so on.\n\nIn brief, Mitya was informed that he was, from that moment, a prisoner, and that he would be driven at once to the town, and there shut up in a very unpleasant place. Mitya listened attentively, and only shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“Well, gentlemen, I don’t blame you. I’m ready.... I understand that there’s nothing else for you to do.”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch informed him gently that he would be escorted at once by the rural police officer, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, who happened to be on the spot....\n\n“Stay,” Mitya interrupted, suddenly, and impelled by uncontrollable feeling he pronounced, addressing all in the room:\n\n“Gentlemen, we’re all cruel, we’re all monsters, we all make men weep, and mothers, and babes at the breast, but of all, let it be settled here, now, of all I am the lowest reptile! I’ve sworn to amend, and every day I’ve done the same filthy things. I understand now that such men as I need a blow, a blow of destiny to catch them as with a noose, and bind them by a force from without. Never, never should I have risen of myself! But the thunderbolt has fallen. I accept the torture of accusation, and my public shame, I want to suffer and by suffering I shall be purified. Perhaps I shall be purified, gentlemen? But listen, for the last time, I am not guilty of my father’s blood. I accept my punishment, not because I killed him, but because I meant to kill him, and perhaps I really might have killed him. Still I mean to fight it out with you. I warn you of that. I’ll fight it out with you to the end, and then God will decide. Good-by, gentlemen, don’t be vexed with me for having shouted at you during the examination. Oh, I was still such a fool then.... In another minute I shall be a prisoner, but now, for the last time, as a free man, Dmitri Karamazov offers you his hand. Saying good-by to you, I say it to all men.”\n\nHis voice quivered and he stretched out his hand, but Nikolay Parfenovitch, who happened to stand nearest to him, with a sudden, almost nervous movement, hid his hands behind his back. Mitya instantly noticed this, and started. He let his outstretched hand fall at once.\n\n“The preliminary inquiry is not yet over,” Nikolay Parfenovitch faltered, somewhat embarrassed. “We will continue it in the town, and I, for my part, of course, am ready to wish you all success ... in your defense.... As a matter of fact, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I’ve always been disposed to regard you as, so to speak, more unfortunate than guilty. All of us here, if I may make bold to speak for all, we are all ready to recognize that you are, at bottom, a young man of honor, but, alas, one who has been carried away by certain passions to a somewhat excessive degree....”\n\nNikolay Parfenovitch’s little figure was positively majestic by the time he had finished speaking. It struck Mitya that in another minute this “boy” would take his arm, lead him to another corner, and renew their conversation about “girls.” But many quite irrelevant and inappropriate thoughts sometimes occur even to a prisoner when he is being led out to execution.\n\n“Gentlemen, you are good, you are humane, may I see her to say ‘good-by’ for the last time?” asked Mitya.\n\n“Certainly, but considering ... in fact, now it’s impossible except in the presence of—”\n\n“Oh, well, if it must be so, it must!”\n\nGrushenka was brought in, but the farewell was brief, and of few words, and did not at all satisfy Nikolay Parfenovitch. Grushenka made a deep bow to Mitya.\n\n“I have told you I am yours, and I will be yours. I will follow you for ever, wherever they may send you. Farewell; you are guiltless, though you’ve been your own undoing.”\n\nHer lips quivered, tears flowed from her eyes.\n\n“Forgive me, Grusha, for my love, for ruining you, too, with my love.”\n\nMitya would have said something more, but he broke off and went out. He was at once surrounded by men who kept a constant watch on him. At the bottom of the steps to which he had driven up with such a dash the day before with Andrey’s three horses, two carts stood in readiness. Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, a sturdy, thick-set man with a wrinkled face, was annoyed about something, some sudden irregularity. He was shouting angrily. He asked Mitya to get into the cart with somewhat excessive surliness.\n\n“When I stood him drinks in the tavern, the man had quite a different face,” thought Mitya, as he got in. At the gates there was a crowd of people, peasants, women and drivers. Trifon Borissovitch came down the steps too. All stared at Mitya.\n\n“Forgive me at parting, good people!” Mitya shouted suddenly from the cart.\n\n“Forgive us too!” he heard two or three voices.\n\n“Good-by to you, too, Trifon Borissovitch!”\n\nBut Trifon Borissovitch did not even turn round. He was, perhaps, too busy. He, too, was shouting and fussing about something. It appeared that everything was not yet ready in the second cart, in which two constables were to accompany Mavriky Mavrikyevitch. The peasant who had been ordered to drive the second cart was pulling on his smock, stoutly maintaining that it was not his turn to go, but Akim’s. But Akim was not to be seen. They ran to look for him. The peasant persisted and besought them to wait.\n\n“You see what our peasants are, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch. They’ve no shame!” exclaimed Trifon Borissovitch. “Akim gave you twenty-five copecks the day before yesterday. You’ve drunk it all and now you cry out. I’m simply surprised at your good-nature, with our low peasants, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, that’s all I can say.”\n\n“But what do we want a second cart for?” Mitya put in. “Let’s start with the one, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch. I won’t be unruly, I won’t run away from you, old fellow. What do we want an escort for?”\n\n“I’ll trouble you, sir, to learn how to speak to me if you’ve never been taught. I’m not ‘old fellow’ to you, and you can keep your advice for another time!” Mavriky Mavrikyevitch snapped out savagely, as though glad to vent his wrath.\n\nMitya was reduced to silence. He flushed all over. A moment later he felt suddenly very cold. The rain had ceased, but the dull sky was still overcast with clouds, and a keen wind was blowing straight in his face.\n\n“I’ve taken a chill,” thought Mitya, twitching his shoulders.\n\nAt last Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, too, got into the cart, sat down heavily, and, as though without noticing it, squeezed Mitya into the corner. It is true that he was out of humor and greatly disliked the task that had been laid upon him.\n\n“Good-by, Trifon Borissovitch!” Mitya shouted again, and felt himself, that he had not called out this time from good-nature, but involuntarily, from resentment.\n\nBut Trifon Borissovitch stood proudly, with both hands behind his back, and staring straight at Mitya with a stern and angry face, he made no reply.\n\n“Good-by, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, good-by!” he heard all at once the voice of Kalganov, who had suddenly darted out. Running up to the cart he held out his hand to Mitya. He had no cap on.\n\nMitya had time to seize and press his hand.\n\n“Good-by, dear fellow! I shan’t forget your generosity,” he cried warmly.\n\nBut the cart moved and their hands parted. The bell began ringing and Mitya was driven off.\n\nKalganov ran back, sat down in a corner, bent his head, hid his face in his hands, and burst out crying. For a long while he sat like that, crying as though he were a little boy instead of a young man of twenty. Oh, he believed almost without doubt in Mitya’s guilt.\n\n“What are these people? What can men be after this?” he exclaimed incoherently, in bitter despondency, almost despair. At that moment he had no desire to live.\n\n“Is it worth it? Is it worth it?” exclaimed the boy in his grief.\n\n# PART IV Book X. The Boys Chapter I. Kolya Krassotkin\n\nIt was the beginning of November. There had been a hard frost, eleven degrees Réaumur, without snow, but a little dry snow had fallen on the frozen ground during the night, and a keen dry wind was lifting and blowing it along the dreary streets of our town, especially about the market-place. It was a dull morning, but the snow had ceased.\n\nNot far from the market-place, close to Plotnikov’s shop, there stood a small house, very clean both without and within. It belonged to Madame Krassotkin, the widow of a former provincial secretary, who had been dead for fourteen years. His widow, still a nice-looking woman of thirty-two, was living in her neat little house on her private means. She lived in respectable seclusion; she was of a soft but fairly cheerful disposition. She was about eighteen at the time of her husband’s death; she had been married only a year and had just borne him a son. From the day of his death she had devoted herself heart and soul to the bringing up of her precious treasure, her boy Kolya. Though she had loved him passionately those fourteen years, he had caused her far more suffering than happiness. She had been trembling and fainting with terror almost every day, afraid he would fall ill, would catch cold, do something naughty, climb on a chair and fall off it, and so on and so on. When Kolya began going to school, the mother devoted herself to studying all the sciences with him so as to help him, and go through his lessons with him. She hastened to make the acquaintance of the teachers and their wives, even made up to Kolya’s schoolfellows, and fawned upon them in the hope of thus saving Kolya from being teased, laughed at, or beaten by them. She went so far that the boys actually began to mock at him on her account and taunt him with being a “mother’s darling.”\n\nBut the boy could take his own part. He was a resolute boy, “tremendously strong,” as was rumored in his class, and soon proved to be the fact; he was agile, strong-willed, and of an audacious and enterprising temper. He was good at lessons, and there was a rumor in the school that he could beat the teacher, Dardanelov, at arithmetic and universal history. Though he looked down upon every one, he was a good comrade and not supercilious. He accepted his schoolfellows’ respect as his due, but was friendly with them. Above all, he knew where to draw the line. He could restrain himself on occasion, and in his relations with the teachers he never overstepped that last mystic limit beyond which a prank becomes an unpardonable breach of discipline. But he was as fond of mischief on every possible occasion as the smallest boy in the school, and not so much for the sake of mischief as for creating a sensation, inventing something, something effective and conspicuous. He was extremely vain. He knew how to make even his mother give way to him; he was almost despotic in his control of her. She gave way to him, oh, she had given way to him for years. The one thought unendurable to her was that her boy had no great love for her. She was always fancying that Kolya was “unfeeling” to her, and at times, dissolving into hysterical tears, she used to reproach him with his coldness. The boy disliked this, and the more demonstrations of feeling were demanded of him the more he seemed intentionally to avoid them. Yet it was not intentional on his part but instinctive—it was his character. His mother was mistaken; he was very fond of her. He only disliked “sheepish sentimentality,” as he expressed it in his schoolboy language.\n\nThere was a bookcase in the house containing a few books that had been his father’s. Kolya was fond of reading, and had read several of them by himself. His mother did not mind that and only wondered sometimes at seeing the boy stand for hours by the bookcase poring over a book instead of going to play. And in that way Kolya read some things unsuitable for his age.\n\nThough the boy, as a rule, knew where to draw the line in his mischief, he had of late begun to play pranks that caused his mother serious alarm. It is true there was nothing vicious in what he did, but a wild mad recklessness.\n\nIt happened that July, during the summer holidays, that the mother and son went to another district, forty-five miles away, to spend a week with a distant relation, whose husband was an official at the railway station (the very station, the nearest one to our town, from which a month later Ivan Fyodorovitch Karamazov set off for Moscow). There Kolya began by carefully investigating every detail connected with the railways, knowing that he could impress his schoolfellows when he got home with his newly acquired knowledge. But there happened to be some other boys in the place with whom he soon made friends. Some of them were living at the station, others in the neighborhood; there were six or seven of them, all between twelve and fifteen, and two of them came from our town. The boys played together, and on the fourth or fifth day of Kolya’s stay at the station, a mad bet was made by the foolish boys. Kolya, who was almost the youngest of the party and rather looked down upon by the others in consequence, was moved by vanity or by reckless bravado to bet them two roubles that he would lie down between the rails at night when the eleven o’clock train was due, and would lie there without moving while the train rolled over him at full speed. It is true they made a preliminary investigation, from which it appeared that it was possible to lie so flat between the rails that the train could pass over without touching, but to lie there was no joke! Kolya maintained stoutly that he would. At first they laughed at him, called him a little liar, a braggart, but that only egged him on. What piqued him most was that these boys of fifteen turned up their noses at him too superciliously, and were at first disposed to treat him as “a small boy,” not fit to associate with them, and that was an unendurable insult.\n\nAnd so it was resolved to go in the evening, half a mile from the station, so that the train might have time to get up full speed after leaving the station. The boys assembled. It was a pitch-dark night without a moon. At the time fixed, Kolya lay down between the rails. The five others who had taken the bet waited among the bushes below the embankment, their hearts beating with suspense, which was followed by alarm and remorse. At last they heard in the distance the rumble of the train leaving the station. Two red lights gleamed out of the darkness; the monster roared as it approached.\n\n“Run, run away from the rails,” the boys cried to Kolya from the bushes, breathless with terror. But it was too late: the train darted up and flew past. The boys rushed to Kolya. He lay without moving. They began pulling at him, lifting him up. He suddenly got up and walked away without a word. Then he explained that he had lain there as though he were insensible to frighten them, but the fact was that he really had lost consciousness, as he confessed long after to his mother. In this way his reputation as “a desperate character,” was established for ever. He returned home to the station as white as a sheet. Next day he had a slight attack of nervous fever, but he was in high spirits and well pleased with himself. The incident did not become known at once, but when they came back to the town it penetrated to the school and even reached the ears of the masters. But then Kolya’s mother hastened to entreat the masters on her boy’s behalf, and in the end Dardanelov, a respected and influential teacher, exerted himself in his favor, and the affair was ignored.\n\nDardanelov was a middle-aged bachelor, who had been passionately in love with Madame Krassotkin for many years past, and had once already, about a year previously, ventured, trembling with fear and the delicacy of his sentiments, to offer her most respectfully his hand in marriage. But she refused him resolutely, feeling that to accept him would be an act of treachery to her son, though Dardanelov had, to judge from certain mysterious symptoms, reason for believing that he was not an object of aversion to the charming but too chaste and tender-hearted widow. Kolya’s mad prank seemed to have broken the ice, and Dardanelov was rewarded for his intercession by a suggestion of hope. The suggestion, it is true, was a faint one, but then Dardanelov was such a paragon of purity and delicacy that it was enough for the time being to make him perfectly happy. He was fond of the boy, though he would have felt it beneath him to try and win him over, and was severe and strict with him in class. Kolya, too, kept him at a respectful distance. He learned his lessons perfectly; he was second in his class, was reserved with Dardanelov, and the whole class firmly believed that Kolya was so good at universal history that he could “beat” even Dardanelov. Kolya did indeed ask him the question, “Who founded Troy?” to which Dardanelov had made a very vague reply, referring to the movements and migrations of races, to the remoteness of the period, to the mythical legends. But the question, “Who had founded Troy?” that is, what individuals, he could not answer, and even for some reason regarded the question as idle and frivolous. But the boys remained convinced that Dardanelov did not know who founded Troy. Kolya had read of the founders of Troy in Smaragdov, whose history was among the books in his father’s bookcase. In the end all the boys became interested in the question, who it was that had founded Troy, but Krassotkin would not tell his secret, and his reputation for knowledge remained unshaken.\n\nAfter the incident on the railway a certain change came over Kolya’s attitude to his mother. When Anna Fyodorovna (Madame Krassotkin) heard of her son’s exploit, she almost went out of her mind with horror. She had such terrible attacks of hysterics, lasting with intervals for several days, that Kolya, seriously alarmed at last, promised on his honor that such pranks should never be repeated. He swore on his knees before the holy image, and swore by the memory of his father, at Madame Krassotkin’s instance, and the “manly” Kolya burst into tears like a boy of six. And all that day the mother and son were constantly rushing into each other’s arms sobbing. Next day Kolya woke up as “unfeeling” as before, but he had become more silent, more modest, sterner, and more thoughtful.\n\nSix weeks later, it is true, he got into another scrape, which even brought his name to the ears of our Justice of the Peace, but it was a scrape of quite another kind, amusing, foolish, and he did not, as it turned out, take the leading part in it, but was only implicated in it. But of this later. His mother still fretted and trembled, but the more uneasy she became, the greater were the hopes of Dardanelov. It must be noted that Kolya understood and divined what was in Dardanelov’s heart and, of course, despised him profoundly for his “feelings”; he had in the past been so tactless as to show this contempt before his mother, hinting vaguely that he knew what Dardanelov was after. But from the time of the railway incident his behavior in this respect also was changed; he did not allow himself the remotest allusion to the subject and began to speak more respectfully of Dardanelov before his mother, which the sensitive woman at once appreciated with boundless gratitude. But at the slightest mention of Dardanelov by a visitor in Kolya’s presence, she would flush as pink as a rose. At such moments Kolya would either stare out of the window scowling, or would investigate the state of his boots, or would shout angrily for “Perezvon,” the big, shaggy, mangy dog, which he had picked up a month before, brought home, and kept for some reason secretly indoors, not showing him to any of his schoolfellows. He bullied him frightfully, teaching him all sorts of tricks, so that the poor dog howled for him whenever he was absent at school, and when he came in, whined with delight, rushed about as if he were crazy, begged, lay down on the ground pretending to be dead, and so on; in fact, showed all the tricks he had taught him, not at the word of command, but simply from the zeal of his excited and grateful heart.\n\nI have forgotten, by the way, to mention that Kolya Krassotkin was the boy stabbed with a penknife by the boy already known to the reader as the son of Captain Snegiryov. Ilusha had been defending his father when the schoolboys jeered at him, shouting the nickname “wisp of tow.”\n\n# Chapter II. Children\n\nAnd so on that frosty, snowy, and windy day in November, Kolya Krassotkin was sitting at home. It was Sunday and there was no school. It had just struck eleven, and he particularly wanted to go out “on very urgent business,” but he was left alone in charge of the house, for it so happened that all its elder inmates were absent owing to a sudden and singular event. Madame Krassotkin had let two little rooms, separated from the rest of the house by a passage, to a doctor’s wife with her two small children. This lady was the same age as Anna Fyodorovna, and a great friend of hers. Her husband, the doctor, had taken his departure twelve months before, going first to Orenburg and then to Tashkend, and for the last six months she had not heard a word from him. Had it not been for her friendship with Madame Krassotkin, which was some consolation to the forsaken lady, she would certainly have completely dissolved away in tears. And now, to add to her misfortunes, Katerina, her only servant, was suddenly moved the evening before to announce, to her mistress’s amazement, that she proposed to bring a child into the world before morning. It seemed almost miraculous to every one that no one had noticed the probability of it before. The astounded doctor’s wife decided to move Katerina while there was still time to an establishment in the town kept by a midwife for such emergencies. As she set great store by her servant, she promptly carried out this plan and remained there looking after her. By the morning all Madame Krassotkin’s friendly sympathy and energy were called upon to render assistance and appeal to some one for help in the case.\n\nSo both the ladies were absent from home, the Krassotkins’ servant, Agafya, had gone out to the market, and Kolya was thus left for a time to protect and look after “the kids,” that is, the son and daughter of the doctor’s wife, who were left alone. Kolya was not afraid of taking care of the house, besides he had Perezvon, who had been told to lie flat, without moving, under the bench in the hall. Every time Kolya, walking to and fro through the rooms, came into the hall, the dog shook his head and gave two loud and insinuating taps on the floor with his tail, but alas! the whistle did not sound to release him. Kolya looked sternly at the luckless dog, who relapsed again into obedient rigidity. The one thing that troubled Kolya was “the kids.” He looked, of course, with the utmost scorn on Katerina’s unexpected adventure, but he was very fond of the bereaved “kiddies,” and had already taken them a picture-book. Nastya, the elder, a girl of eight, could read, and Kostya, the boy, aged seven, was very fond of being read to by her. Krassotkin could, of course, have provided more diverting entertainment for them. He could have made them stand side by side and played soldiers with them, or sent them hiding all over the house. He had done so more than once before and was not above doing it, so much so that a report once spread at school that Krassotkin played horses with the little lodgers at home, prancing with his head on one side like a trace-horse. But Krassotkin haughtily parried this thrust, pointing out that to play horses with boys of one’s own age, boys of thirteen, would certainly be disgraceful “at this date,” but that he did it for the sake of “the kids” because he liked them, and no one had a right to call him to account for his feelings. The two “kids” adored him.\n\nBut on this occasion he was in no mood for games. He had very important business of his own before him, something almost mysterious. Meanwhile time was passing and Agafya, with whom he could have left the children, would not come back from market. He had several times already crossed the passage, opened the door of the lodgers’ room and looked anxiously at “the kids” who were sitting over the book, as he had bidden them. Every time he opened the door they grinned at him, hoping he would come in and would do something delightful and amusing. But Kolya was bothered and did not go in.\n\nAt last it struck eleven and he made up his mind, once for all, that if that “damned” Agafya did not come back within ten minutes he should go out without waiting for her, making “the kids” promise, of course, to be brave when he was away, not to be naughty, not to cry from fright. With this idea he put on his wadded winter overcoat with its catskin fur collar, slung his satchel round his shoulder, and, regardless of his mother’s constantly reiterated entreaties that he would always put on goloshes in such cold weather, he looked at them contemptuously as he crossed the hall and went out with only his boots on. Perezvon, seeing him in his outdoor clothes, began tapping nervously, yet vigorously, on the floor with his tail. Twitching all over, he even uttered a plaintive whine. But Kolya, seeing his dog’s passionate excitement, decided that it was a breach of discipline, kept him for another minute under the bench, and only when he had opened the door into the passage, whistled for him. The dog leapt up like a mad creature and rushed bounding before him rapturously.\n\nKolya opened the door to peep at “the kids.” They were both sitting as before at the table, not reading but warmly disputing about something. The children often argued together about various exciting problems of life, and Nastya, being the elder, always got the best of it. If Kostya did not agree with her, he almost always appealed to Kolya Krassotkin, and his verdict was regarded as infallible by both of them. This time the “kids’” discussion rather interested Krassotkin, and he stood still in the passage to listen. The children saw he was listening and that made them dispute with even greater energy.\n\n“I shall never, never believe,” Nastya prattled, “that the old women find babies among the cabbages in the kitchen-garden. It’s winter now and there are no cabbages, and so the old woman couldn’t have taken Katerina a daughter.”\n\nKolya whistled to himself.\n\n“Or perhaps they do bring babies from somewhere, but only to those who are married.”\n\nKostya stared at Nastya and listened, pondering profoundly.\n\n“Nastya, how silly you are!” he said at last, firmly and calmly. “How can Katerina have a baby when she isn’t married?”\n\nNastya was exasperated.\n\n“You know nothing about it,” she snapped irritably. “Perhaps she has a husband, only he is in prison, so now she’s got a baby.”\n\n“But is her husband in prison?” the matter-of-fact Kostya inquired gravely.\n\n“Or, I tell you what,” Nastya interrupted impulsively, completely rejecting and forgetting her first hypothesis. “She hasn’t a husband, you are right there, but she wants to be married, and so she’s been thinking of getting married, and thinking and thinking of it till now she’s got it, that is, not a husband but a baby.”\n\n“Well, perhaps so,” Kostya agreed, entirely vanquished. “But you didn’t say so before. So how could I tell?”\n\n“Come, kiddies,” said Kolya, stepping into the room. “You’re terrible people, I see.”\n\n“And Perezvon with you!” grinned Kostya, and began snapping his fingers and calling Perezvon.\n\n“I am in a difficulty, kids,” Krassotkin began solemnly, “and you must help me. Agafya must have broken her leg, since she has not turned up till now, that’s certain. I must go out. Will you let me go?”\n\nThe children looked anxiously at one another. Their smiling faces showed signs of uneasiness, but they did not yet fully grasp what was expected of them.\n\n“You won’t be naughty while I am gone? You won’t climb on the cupboard and break your legs? You won’t be frightened alone and cry?”\n\nA look of profound despondency came into the children’s faces.\n\n“And I could show you something as a reward, a little copper cannon which can be fired with real gunpowder.”\n\nThe children’s faces instantly brightened. “Show us the cannon,” said Kostya, beaming all over.\n\nKrassotkin put his hand in his satchel, and pulling out a little bronze cannon stood it on the table.\n\n“Ah, you are bound to ask that! Look, it’s on wheels.” He rolled the toy on along the table. “And it can be fired off, too. It can be loaded with shot and fired off.”\n\n“And it could kill any one?”\n\n“It can kill any one; you’ve only got to aim at anybody,” and Krassotkin explained where the powder had to be put, where the shot should be rolled in, showing a tiny hole like a touch-hole, and told them that it kicked when it was fired.\n\nThe children listened with intense interest. What particularly struck their imagination was that the cannon kicked.\n\n“And have you got any powder?” Nastya inquired.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Show us the powder, too,” she drawled with a smile of entreaty.\n\nKrassotkin dived again into his satchel and pulled out a small flask containing a little real gunpowder. He had some shot, too, in a screw of paper. He even uncorked the flask and shook a little powder into the palm of his hand.\n\n“One has to be careful there’s no fire about, or it would blow up and kill us all,” Krassotkin warned them sensationally.\n\nThe children gazed at the powder with an awe-stricken alarm that only intensified their enjoyment. But Kostya liked the shot better.\n\n“And does the shot burn?” he inquired.\n\n“No, it doesn’t.”\n\n“Give me a little shot,” he asked in an imploring voice.\n\n“I’ll give you a little shot; here, take it, but don’t show it to your mother till I come back, or she’ll be sure to think it’s gunpowder, and will die of fright and give you a thrashing.”\n\n“Mother never does whip us,” Nastya observed at once.\n\n“I know, I only said it to finish the sentence. And don’t you ever deceive your mother except just this once, until I come back. And so, kiddies, can I go out? You won’t be frightened and cry when I’m gone?”\n\n“We sha—all cry,” drawled Kostya, on the verge of tears already.\n\n“We shall cry, we shall be sure to cry,” Nastya chimed in with timid haste.\n\n“Oh, children, children, how fraught with peril are your years! There’s no help for it, chickens, I shall have to stay with you I don’t know how long. And time is passing, time is passing, oogh!”\n\n“Tell Perezvon to pretend to be dead!” Kostya begged.\n\n“There’s no help for it, we must have recourse to Perezvon. Ici, Perezvon.” And Kolya began giving orders to the dog, who performed all his tricks.\n\nHe was a rough-haired dog, of medium size, with a coat of a sort of lilac-gray color. He was blind in his right eye, and his left ear was torn. He whined and jumped, stood and walked on his hind legs, lay on his back with his paws in the air, rigid as though he were dead. While this last performance was going on, the door opened and Agafya, Madame Krassotkin’s servant, a stout woman of forty, marked with small-pox, appeared in the doorway. She had come back from market and had a bag full of provisions in her hand. Holding up the bag of provisions in her left hand she stood still to watch the dog. Though Kolya had been so anxious for her return, he did not cut short the performance, and after keeping Perezvon dead for the usual time, at last he whistled to him. The dog jumped up and began bounding about in his joy at having done his duty.\n\n“Only think, a dog!” Agafya observed sententiously.\n\n“Why are you late, female?” asked Krassotkin sternly.\n\n“Female, indeed! Go on with you, you brat.”\n“Brat?”\n\n“Yes, a brat. What is it to you if I’m late; if I’m late, you may be sure I have good reason,” muttered Agafya, busying herself about the stove, without a trace of anger or displeasure in her voice. She seemed quite pleased, in fact, to enjoy a skirmish with her merry young master.\n\n“Listen, you frivolous young woman,” Krassotkin began, getting up from the sofa, “can you swear by all you hold sacred in the world and something else besides, that you will watch vigilantly over the kids in my absence? I am going out.”\n\n“And what am I going to swear for?” laughed Agafya. “I shall look after them without that.”\n\n“No, you must swear on your eternal salvation. Else I shan’t go.”\n\n“Well, don’t then. What does it matter to me? It’s cold out; stay at home.”\n\n“Kids,” Kolya turned to the children, “this woman will stay with you till I come back or till your mother comes, for she ought to have been back long ago. She will give you some lunch, too. You’ll give them something, Agafya, won’t you?”\n\n“That I can do.”\n\n“Good-by, chickens, I go with my heart at rest. And you, granny,” he added gravely, in an undertone, as he passed Agafya, “I hope you’ll spare their tender years and not tell them any of your old woman’s nonsense about Katerina. Ici, Perezvon!”\n\n“Get along with you!” retorted Agafya, really angry this time. “Ridiculous boy! You want a whipping for saying such things, that’s what you want!”\n\n# Chapter III. The Schoolboy\n\nBut Kolya did not hear her. At last he could go out. As he went out at the gate he looked round him, shrugged up his shoulders, and saying “It is freezing,” went straight along the street and turned off to the right towards the market-place. When he reached the last house but one before the market-place he stopped at the gate, pulled a whistle out of his pocket, and whistled with all his might as though giving a signal. He had not to wait more than a minute before a rosy-cheeked boy of about eleven, wearing a warm, neat and even stylish coat, darted out to meet him. This was Smurov, a boy in the preparatory class (two classes below Kolya Krassotkin), son of a well-to-do official. Apparently he was forbidden by his parents to associate with Krassotkin, who was well known to be a desperately naughty boy, so Smurov was obviously slipping out on the sly. He was—if the reader has not forgotten—one of the group of boys who two months before had thrown stones at Ilusha. He was the one who told Alyosha Karamazov about Ilusha.\n\n“I’ve been waiting for you for the last hour, Krassotkin,” said Smurov stolidly, and the boys strode towards the market-place.\n\n“I am late,” answered Krassotkin. “I was detained by circumstances. You won’t be thrashed for coming with me?”\n\n“Come, I say, I’m never thrashed! And you’ve got Perezvon with you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You’re taking him, too?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Ah! if it were only Zhutchka!”\n\n“That’s impossible. Zhutchka’s non-existent. Zhutchka is lost in the mists of obscurity.”\n\n“Ah! couldn’t we do this?” Smurov suddenly stood still. “You see Ilusha says that Zhutchka was a shaggy, grayish, smoky-looking dog like Perezvon. Couldn’t you tell him this is Zhutchka, and he might believe you?”\n\n“Boy, shun a lie, that’s one thing; even with a good object—that’s another. Above all, I hope you’ve not told them anything about my coming.”\n\n“Heaven forbid! I know what I am about. But you won’t comfort him with Perezvon,” said Smurov, with a sigh. “You know his father, the captain, ‘the wisp of tow,’ told us that he was going to bring him a real mastiff pup, with a black nose, to-day. He thinks that would comfort Ilusha; but I doubt it.”\n\n“And how is Ilusha?”\n\n“Ah, he is bad, very bad! I believe he’s in consumption: he is quite conscious, but his breathing! His breathing’s gone wrong. The other day he asked to have his boots on to be led round the room. He tried to walk, but he couldn’t stand. ‘Ah, I told you before, father,’ he said, ‘that those boots were no good. I could never walk properly in them.’ He fancied it was his boots that made him stagger, but it was simply weakness, really. He won’t live another week. Herzenstube is looking after him. Now they are rich again—they’ve got heaps of money.”\n\n“They are rogues.”\n\n“Who are rogues?”\n\n“Doctors and the whole crew of quacks collectively, and also, of course, individually. I don’t believe in medicine. It’s a useless institution. I mean to go into all that. But what’s that sentimentality you’ve got up there? The whole class seems to be there every day.”\n\n“Not the whole class: it’s only ten of our fellows who go to see him every day. There’s nothing in that.”\n\n“What I don’t understand in all this is the part that Alexey Karamazov is taking in it. His brother’s going to be tried to-morrow or next day for such a crime, and yet he has so much time to spend on sentimentality with boys.”\n\n“There’s no sentimentality about it. You are going yourself now to make it up with Ilusha.”\n\n“Make it up with him? What an absurd expression! But I allow no one to analyze my actions.”\n\n“And how pleased Ilusha will be to see you! He has no idea that you are coming. Why was it, why was it you wouldn’t come all this time?” Smurov cried with sudden warmth.\n\n“My dear boy, that’s my business, not yours. I am going of myself because I choose to, but you’ve all been hauled there by Alexey Karamazov—there’s a difference, you know. And how do you know? I may not be going to make it up at all. It’s a stupid expression.”\n\n“It’s not Karamazov at all; it’s not his doing. Our fellows began going there of themselves. Of course, they went with Karamazov at first. And there’s been nothing of that sort—no silliness. First one went, and then another. His father was awfully pleased to see us. You know he will simply go out of his mind if Ilusha dies. He sees that Ilusha’s dying. And he seems so glad we’ve made it up with Ilusha. Ilusha asked after you, that was all. He just asks and says no more. His father will go out of his mind or hang himself. He behaved like a madman before. You know he is a very decent man. We made a mistake then. It’s all the fault of that murderer who beat him then.”\n\n“Karamazov’s a riddle to me all the same. I might have made his acquaintance long ago, but I like to have a proper pride in some cases. Besides, I have a theory about him which I must work out and verify.”\n\nKolya subsided into dignified silence. Smurov, too, was silent. Smurov, of course, worshiped Krassotkin and never dreamed of putting himself on a level with him. Now he was tremendously interested at Kolya’s saying that he was “going of himself” to see Ilusha. He felt that there must be some mystery in Kolya’s suddenly taking it into his head to go to him that day. They crossed the market-place, in which at that hour were many loaded wagons from the country and a great number of live fowls. The market women were selling rolls, cottons and threads, etc., in their booths. These Sunday markets were naïvely called “fairs” in the town, and there were many such fairs in the year.\n\nPerezvon ran about in the wildest spirits, sniffing about first one side, then the other. When he met other dogs they zealously smelt each other over according to the rules of canine etiquette.\n\n“I like to watch such realistic scenes, Smurov,” said Kolya suddenly. “Have you noticed how dogs sniff at one another when they meet? It seems to be a law of their nature.”\n\n“Yes; it’s a funny habit.”\n\n“No, it’s not funny; you are wrong there. There’s nothing funny in nature, however funny it may seem to man with his prejudices. If dogs could reason and criticize us they’d be sure to find just as much that would be funny to them, if not far more, in the social relations of men, their masters—far more, indeed. I repeat that, because I am convinced that there is far more foolishness among us. That’s Rakitin’s idea—a remarkable idea. I am a Socialist, Smurov.”\n\n“And what is a Socialist?” asked Smurov.\n\n“That’s when all are equal and all have property in common, there are no marriages, and every one has any religion and laws he likes best, and all the rest of it. You are not old enough to understand that yet. It’s cold, though.”\n\n“Yes, twelve degrees of frost. Father looked at the thermometer just now.”\n\n“Have you noticed, Smurov, that in the middle of winter we don’t feel so cold even when there are fifteen or eighteen degrees of frost as we do now, in the beginning of winter, when there is a sudden frost of twelve degrees, especially when there is not much snow. It’s because people are not used to it. Everything is habit with men, everything even in their social and political relations. Habit is the great motive-power. What a funny-looking peasant!”\n\nKolya pointed to a tall peasant, with a good-natured countenance in a long sheepskin coat, who was standing by his wagon, clapping together his hands, in their shapeless leather gloves, to warm them. His long fair beard was all white with frost.\n\n“That peasant’s beard’s frozen,” Kolya cried in a loud provocative voice as he passed him.\n\n“Lots of people’s beards are frozen,” the peasant replied, calmly and sententiously.\n\n“Don’t provoke him,” observed Smurov.\n\n“It’s all right; he won’t be cross; he’s a nice fellow. Good-by, Matvey.”\n\n“Good-by.”\n\n“Is your name Matvey?”\n\n“Yes. Didn’t you know?”\n\n“No, I didn’t. It was a guess.”\n\n“You don’t say so! You are a schoolboy, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You get whipped, I expect?”\n\n“Nothing to speak of—sometimes.”\n\n“Does it hurt?”\n\n“Well, yes, it does.”\n\n“Ech, what a life!” The peasant heaved a sigh from the bottom of his heart.\n\n“Good-by, Matvey.”\n\n“Good-by. You are a nice chap, that you are.”\n\nThe boys went on.\n\n“That was a nice peasant,” Kolya observed to Smurov. “I like talking to the peasants, and am always glad to do them justice.”\n\n“Why did you tell a lie, pretending we are thrashed?” asked Smurov.\n\n“I had to say that to please him.”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“You know, Smurov, I don’t like being asked the same thing twice. I like people to understand at the first word. Some things can’t be explained. According to a peasant’s notions, schoolboys are whipped, and must be whipped. What would a schoolboy be if he were not whipped? And if I were to tell him we are not, he’d be disappointed. But you don’t understand that. One has to know how to talk to the peasants.”\n\n“Only don’t tease them, please, or you’ll get into another scrape as you did about that goose.”\n\n“So you’re afraid?”\n\n“Don’t laugh, Kolya. Of course I’m afraid. My father would be awfully cross. I am strictly forbidden to go out with you.”\n\n“Don’t be uneasy, nothing will happen this time. Hallo, Natasha!” he shouted to a market woman in one of the booths.\n\n“Call me Natasha! What next! My name is Marya,” the middle-aged market woman shouted at him.\n\n“I am so glad it’s Marya. Good-by!”\n\n“Ah, you young rascal! A brat like you to carry on so!”\n\n“I’m in a hurry. I can’t stay now. You shall tell me next Sunday.” Kolya waved his hand at her, as though she had attacked him and not he her.\n\n“I’ve nothing to tell you next Sunday. You set upon me, you impudent young monkey. I didn’t say anything,” bawled Marya. “You want a whipping, that’s what you want, you saucy jackanapes!”\n\nThere was a roar of laughter among the other market women round her. Suddenly a man in a violent rage darted out from the arcade of shops close by. He was a young man, not a native of the town, with dark, curly hair and a long, pale face, marked with smallpox. He wore a long blue coat and a peaked cap, and looked like a merchant’s clerk. He was in a state of stupid excitement and brandished his fist at Kolya.\n\n“I know you!” he cried angrily, “I know you!”\n\nKolya stared at him. He could not recall when he could have had a row with the man. But he had been in so many rows in the street that he could hardly remember them all.\n\n“Do you?” he asked sarcastically.\n\n“I know you! I know you!” the man repeated idiotically.\n\n“So much the better for you. Well, it’s time I was going. Good-by!”\n\n“You are at your saucy pranks again?” cried the man. “You are at your saucy pranks again? I know, you are at it again!”\n\n“It’s not your business, brother, if I am at my saucy pranks again,” said Kolya, standing still and scanning him.\n\n“Not my business?”\n\n“No; it’s not your business.”\n\n“Whose then? Whose then? Whose then?”\n\n“It’s Trifon Nikititch’s business, not yours.”\n\n“What Trifon Nikititch?” asked the youth, staring with loutish amazement at Kolya, but still as angry as ever.\n\nKolya scanned him gravely.\n\n“Have you been to the Church of the Ascension?” he suddenly asked him, with stern emphasis.\n\n“What Church of Ascension? What for? No, I haven’t,” said the young man, somewhat taken aback.\n\n“Do you know Sabaneyev?” Kolya went on even more emphatically and even more severely.\n\n“What Sabaneyev? No, I don’t know him.”\n\n“Well then you can go to the devil,” said Kolya, cutting short the conversation; and turning sharply to the right he strode quickly on his way as though he disdained further conversation with a dolt who did not even know Sabaneyev.\n\n“Stop, heigh! What Sabaneyev?” the young man recovered from his momentary stupefaction and was as excited as before. “What did he say?” He turned to the market women with a silly stare.\n\nThe women laughed.\n\n“You can never tell what he’s after,” said one of them.\n\n“What Sabaneyev is it he’s talking about?” the young man repeated, still furious and brandishing his right arm.\n\n“It must be a Sabaneyev who worked for the Kuzmitchovs, that’s who it must be,” one of the women suggested.\n\nThe young man stared at her wildly.\n\n“For the Kuzmitchovs?” repeated another woman. “But his name wasn’t Trifon. His name’s Kuzma, not Trifon; but the boy said Trifon Nikititch, so it can’t be the same.”\n\n“His name is not Trifon and not Sabaneyev, it’s Tchizhov,” put in suddenly a third woman, who had hitherto been silent, listening gravely. “Alexey Ivanitch is his name. Tchizhov, Alexey Ivanitch.”\n\n“Not a doubt about it, it’s Tchizhov,” a fourth woman emphatically confirmed the statement.\n\nThe bewildered youth gazed from one to another.\n\n“But what did he ask for, what did he ask for, good people?” he cried almost in desperation. “‘Do you know Sabaneyev?’ says he. And who the devil’s to know who is Sabaneyev?”\n\n“You’re a senseless fellow. I tell you it’s not Sabaneyev, but Tchizhov, Alexey Ivanitch Tchizhov, that’s who it is!” one of the women shouted at him impressively.\n\n“What Tchizhov? Who is he? Tell me, if you know.”\n\n“That tall, sniveling fellow who used to sit in the market in the summer.”\n\n“And what’s your Tchizhov to do with me, good people, eh?”\n\n“How can I tell what he’s to do with you?” put in another. “You ought to know yourself what you want with him, if you make such a clamor about him. He spoke to you, he did not speak to us, you stupid. Don’t you really know him?”\n\n“Know whom?”\n\n“Tchizhov.”\n\n“The devil take Tchizhov and you with him. I’ll give him a hiding, that I will. He was laughing at me!”\n\n“Will give Tchizhov a hiding! More likely he will give you one. You are a fool, that’s what you are!”\n\n“Not Tchizhov, not Tchizhov, you spiteful, mischievous woman. I’ll give the boy a hiding. Catch him, catch him, he was laughing at me!”\n\nThe woman guffawed. But Kolya was by now a long way off, marching along with a triumphant air. Smurov walked beside him, looking round at the shouting group far behind. He too was in high spirits, though he was still afraid of getting into some scrape in Kolya’s company.\n\n“What Sabaneyev did you mean?” he asked Kolya, foreseeing what his answer would be.\n\n“How do I know? Now there’ll be a hubbub among them all day. I like to stir up fools in every class of society. There’s another blockhead, that peasant there. You know, they say ‘there’s no one stupider than a stupid Frenchman,’ but a stupid Russian shows it in his face just as much. Can’t you see it all over his face that he is a fool, that peasant, eh?”\n\n“Let him alone, Kolya. Let’s go on.”\n\n“Nothing could stop me, now I am once off. Hey, good morning, peasant!”\n\nA sturdy-looking peasant, with a round, simple face and grizzled beard, who was walking by, raised his head and looked at the boy. He seemed not quite sober.\n\n“Good morning, if you are not laughing at me,” he said deliberately in reply.\n\n“And if I am?” laughed Kolya.\n\n“Well, a joke’s a joke. Laugh away. I don’t mind. There’s no harm in a joke.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, brother, it was a joke.”\n\n“Well, God forgive you!”\n\n“Do you forgive me, too?”\n\n“I quite forgive you. Go along.”\n\n“I say, you seem a clever peasant.”\n\n“Cleverer than you,” the peasant answered unexpectedly, with the same gravity.\n\n“I doubt it,” said Kolya, somewhat taken aback.\n\n“It’s true, though.”\n\n“Perhaps it is.”\n\n“It is, brother.”\n\n“Good-by, peasant!”\n\n“Good-by!”\n\n“There are all sorts of peasants,” Kolya observed to Smurov after a brief silence. “How could I tell I had hit on a clever one? I am always ready to recognize intelligence in the peasantry.”\n\nIn the distance the cathedral clock struck half-past eleven. The boys made haste and they walked as far as Captain Snegiryov’s lodging, a considerable distance, quickly and almost in silence. Twenty paces from the house Kolya stopped and told Smurov to go on ahead and ask Karamazov to come out to him.\n\n“One must sniff round a bit first,” he observed to Smurov.\n\n“Why ask him to come out?” Smurov protested. “You go in; they will be awfully glad to see you. What’s the sense of making friends in the frost out here?”\n\n“I know why I want to see him out here in the frost,” Kolya cut him short in the despotic tone he was fond of adopting with “small boys,” and Smurov ran to do his bidding.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Lost Dog\n\nKolya leaned against the fence with an air of dignity, waiting for Alyosha to appear. Yes, he had long wanted to meet him. He had heard a great deal about him from the boys, but hitherto he had always maintained an appearance of disdainful indifference when he was mentioned, and he had even “criticized” what he heard about Alyosha. But secretly he had a great longing to make his acquaintance; there was something sympathetic and attractive in all he was told about Alyosha. So the present moment was important: to begin with, he had to show himself at his best, to show his independence, “Or he’ll think of me as thirteen and take me for a boy, like the rest of them. And what are these boys to him? I shall ask him when I get to know him. It’s a pity I am so short, though. Tuzikov is younger than I am, yet he is half a head taller. But I have a clever face. I am not good-looking. I know I’m hideous, but I’ve a clever face. I mustn’t talk too freely; if I fall into his arms all at once, he may think—Tfoo! how horrible if he should think—!”\n\nSuch were the thoughts that excited Kolya while he was doing his utmost to assume the most independent air. What distressed him most was his being so short; he did not mind so much his “hideous” face, as being so short. On the wall in a corner at home he had the year before made a pencil-mark to show his height, and every two months since he anxiously measured himself against it to see how much he had gained. But alas! he grew very slowly, and this sometimes reduced him almost to despair. His face was in reality by no means “hideous”; on the contrary, it was rather attractive, with a fair, pale skin, freckled. His small, lively gray eyes had a fearless look, and often glowed with feeling. He had rather high cheekbones; small, very red, but not very thick, lips; his nose was small and unmistakably turned up. “I’ve a regular pug nose, a regular pug nose,” Kolya used to mutter to himself when he looked in the looking-glass, and he always left it with indignation. “But perhaps I haven’t got a clever face?” he sometimes thought, doubtful even of that. But it must not be supposed that his mind was preoccupied with his face and his height. On the contrary, however bitter the moments before the looking-glass were to him, he quickly forgot them, and forgot them for a long time, “abandoning himself entirely to ideas and to real life,” as he formulated it to himself.\n\nAlyosha came out quickly and hastened up to Kolya. Before he reached him, Kolya could see that he looked delighted. “Can he be so glad to see me?” Kolya wondered, feeling pleased. We may note here, in passing, that Alyosha’s appearance had undergone a complete change since we saw him last. He had abandoned his cassock and was wearing now a well-cut coat, a soft, round hat, and his hair had been cropped short. All this was very becoming to him, and he looked quite handsome. His charming face always had a good-humored expression; but there was a gentleness and serenity in his good-humor. To Kolya’s surprise, Alyosha came out to him just as he was, without an overcoat. He had evidently come in haste. He held out his hand to Kolya at once.\n\n“Here you are at last! How anxious we’ve been to see you!”\n\n“There were reasons which you shall know directly. Anyway, I am glad to make your acquaintance. I’ve long been hoping for an opportunity, and have heard a great deal about you,” Kolya muttered, a little breathless.\n\n“We should have met anyway. I’ve heard a great deal about you, too; but you’ve been a long time coming here.”\n\n“Tell me, how are things going?”\n\n“Ilusha is very ill. He is certainly dying.”\n\n“How awful! You must admit that medicine is a fraud, Karamazov,” cried Kolya warmly.\n\n“Ilusha has mentioned you often, very often, even in his sleep, in delirium, you know. One can see that you used to be very, very dear to him ... before the incident ... with the knife.... Then there’s another reason.... Tell me, is that your dog?”\n\n“Yes, Perezvon.”\n\n“Not Zhutchka?” Alyosha looked at Kolya with eyes full of pity. “Is she lost for ever?”\n\n“I know you would all like it to be Zhutchka. I’ve heard all about it.” Kolya smiled mysteriously. “Listen, Karamazov, I’ll tell you all about it. That’s what I came for; that’s what I asked you to come out here for, to explain the whole episode to you before we go in,” he began with animation. “You see, Karamazov, Ilusha came into the preparatory class last spring. Well, you know what our preparatory class is—a lot of small boys. They began teasing Ilusha at once. I am two classes higher up, and, of course, I only look on at them from a distance. I saw the boy was weak and small, but he wouldn’t give in to them; he fought with them. I saw he was proud, and his eyes were full of fire. I like children like that. And they teased him all the more. The worst of it was he was horribly dressed at the time, his breeches were too small for him, and there were holes in his boots. They worried him about it; they jeered at him. That I can’t stand. I stood up for him at once, and gave it to them hot. I beat them, but they adore me, do you know, Karamazov?” Kolya boasted impulsively; “but I am always fond of children. I’ve two chickens in my hands at home now—that’s what detained me to-day. So they left off beating Ilusha and I took him under my protection. I saw the boy was proud. I tell you that, the boy was proud; but in the end he became slavishly devoted to me: he did my slightest bidding, obeyed me as though I were God, tried to copy me. In the intervals between the classes he used to run to me at once, and I’d go about with him. On Sundays, too. They always laugh when an older boy makes friends with a younger one like that; but that’s a prejudice. If it’s my fancy, that’s enough. I am teaching him, developing him. Why shouldn’t I develop him if I like him? Here you, Karamazov, have taken up with all these nestlings. I see you want to influence the younger generation—to develop them, to be of use to them, and I assure you this trait in your character, which I knew by hearsay, attracted me more than anything. Let us get to the point, though. I noticed that there was a sort of softness and sentimentality coming over the boy, and you know I have a positive hatred of this sheepish sentimentality, and I have had it from a baby. There were contradictions in him, too: he was proud, but he was slavishly devoted to me, and yet all at once his eyes would flash and he’d refuse to agree with me; he’d argue, fly into a rage. I used sometimes to propound certain ideas; I could see that it was not so much that he disagreed with the ideas, but that he was simply rebelling against me, because I was cool in responding to his endearments. And so, in order to train him properly, the tenderer he was, the colder I became. I did it on purpose: that was my idea. My object was to form his character, to lick him into shape, to make a man of him ... and besides ... no doubt, you understand me at a word. Suddenly I noticed for three days in succession he was downcast and dejected, not because of my coldness, but for something else, something more important. I wondered what the tragedy was. I have pumped him and found out that he had somehow got to know Smerdyakov, who was footman to your late father—it was before his death, of course—and he taught the little fool a silly trick—that is, a brutal, nasty trick. He told him to take a piece of bread, to stick a pin in it, and throw it to one of those hungry dogs who snap up anything without biting it, and then to watch and see what would happen. So they prepared a piece of bread like that and threw it to Zhutchka, that shaggy dog there’s been such a fuss about. The people of the house it belonged to never fed it at all, though it barked all day. (Do you like that stupid barking, Karamazov? I can’t stand it.) So it rushed at the bread, swallowed it, and began to squeal; it turned round and round and ran away, squealing as it ran out of sight. That was Ilusha’s own account of it. He confessed it to me, and cried bitterly. He hugged me, shaking all over. He kept on repeating ‘He ran away squealing’: the sight of that haunted him. He was tormented by remorse, I could see that. I took it seriously. I determined to give him a lesson for other things as well. So I must confess I wasn’t quite straightforward, and pretended to be more indignant perhaps than I was. ‘You’ve done a nasty thing,’ I said, ‘you are a scoundrel. I won’t tell of it, of course, but I shall have nothing more to do with you for a time. I’ll think it over and let you know through Smurov’—that’s the boy who’s just come with me; he’s always ready to do anything for me—‘whether I will have anything to do with you in the future or whether I give you up for good as a scoundrel.’ He was tremendously upset. I must own I felt I’d gone too far as I spoke, but there was no help for it. I did what I thought best at the time. A day or two after, I sent Smurov to tell him that I would not speak to him again. That’s what we call it when two schoolfellows refuse to have anything more to do with one another. Secretly I only meant to send him to Coventry for a few days and then, if I saw signs of repentance, to hold out my hand to him again. That was my intention. But what do you think happened? He heard Smurov’s message, his eyes flashed. ‘Tell Krassotkin from me,’ he cried, ‘that I will throw bread with pins to all the dogs—all—all of them!’ ‘So he’s going in for a little temper. We must smoke it out of him.’ And I began to treat him with contempt; whenever I met him I turned away or smiled sarcastically. And just then that affair with his father happened. You remember? You must realize that he was fearfully worked up by what had happened already. The boys, seeing I’d given him up, set on him and taunted him, shouting, ‘Wisp of tow, wisp of tow!’ And he had soon regular skirmishes with them, which I am very sorry for. They seem to have given him one very bad beating. One day he flew at them all as they were coming out of school. I stood a few yards off, looking on. And, I swear, I don’t remember that I laughed; it was quite the other way, I felt awfully sorry for him, in another minute I would have run up to take his part. But he suddenly met my eyes. I don’t know what he fancied; but he pulled out a penknife, rushed at me, and struck at my thigh, here in my right leg. I didn’t move. I don’t mind owning I am plucky sometimes, Karamazov. I simply looked at him contemptuously, as though to say, ‘This is how you repay all my kindness! Do it again, if you like, I’m at your service.’ But he didn’t stab me again; he broke down, he was frightened at what he had done, he threw away the knife, burst out crying, and ran away. I did not sneak on him, of course, and I made them all keep quiet, so it shouldn’t come to the ears of the masters. I didn’t even tell my mother till it had healed up. And the wound was a mere scratch. And then I heard that the same day he’d been throwing stones and had bitten your finger—but you understand now what a state he was in! Well, it can’t be helped: it was stupid of me not to come and forgive him—that is, to make it up with him—when he was taken ill. I am sorry for it now. But I had a special reason. So now I’ve told you all about it ... but I’m afraid it was stupid of me.”\n\n“Oh, what a pity,” exclaimed Alyosha, with feeling, “that I didn’t know before what terms you were on with him, or I’d have come to you long ago to beg you to go to him with me. Would you believe it, when he was feverish he talked about you in delirium. I didn’t know how much you were to him! And you’ve really not succeeded in finding that dog? His father and the boys have been hunting all over the town for it. Would you believe it, since he’s been ill, I’ve three times heard him repeat with tears, ‘It’s because I killed Zhutchka, father, that I am ill now. God is punishing me for it.’ He can’t get that idea out of his head. And if the dog were found and proved to be alive, one might almost fancy the joy would cure him. We have all rested our hopes on you.”\n\n“Tell me, what made you hope that I should be the one to find him?” Kolya asked, with great curiosity. “Why did you reckon on me rather than any one else?”\n\n“There was a report that you were looking for the dog, and that you would bring it when you’d found it. Smurov said something of the sort. We’ve all been trying to persuade Ilusha that the dog is alive, that it’s been seen. The boys brought him a live hare; he just looked at it, with a faint smile, and asked them to set it free in the fields. And so we did. His father has just this moment come back, bringing him a mastiff pup, hoping to comfort him with that; but I think it only makes it worse.”\n\n“Tell me, Karamazov, what sort of man is the father? I know him, but what do you make of him—a mountebank, a buffoon?”\n\n“Oh, no; there are people of deep feeling who have been somehow crushed. Buffoonery in them is a form of resentful irony against those to whom they daren’t speak the truth, from having been for years humiliated and intimidated by them. Believe me, Krassotkin, that sort of buffoonery is sometimes tragic in the extreme. His whole life now is centered in Ilusha, and if Ilusha dies, he will either go mad with grief or kill himself. I feel almost certain of that when I look at him now.”\n\n“I understand you, Karamazov. I see you understand human nature,” Kolya added, with feeling.\n\n“And as soon as I saw you with a dog, I thought it was Zhutchka you were bringing.”\n\n“Wait a bit, Karamazov, perhaps we shall find it yet; but this is Perezvon. I’ll let him go in now and perhaps it will amuse Ilusha more than the mastiff pup. Wait a bit, Karamazov, you will know something in a minute. But, I say, I am keeping you here!” Kolya cried suddenly. “You’ve no overcoat on in this bitter cold. You see what an egoist I am. Oh, we are all egoists, Karamazov!”\n\n“Don’t trouble; it is cold, but I don’t often catch cold. Let us go in, though, and, by the way, what is your name? I know you are called Kolya, but what else?”\n\n“Nikolay—Nikolay Ivanovitch Krassotkin, or, as they say in official documents, ‘Krassotkin son.’” Kolya laughed for some reason, but added suddenly, “Of course I hate my name Nikolay.”\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“It’s so trivial, so ordinary.”\n\n“You are thirteen?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“No, fourteen—that is, I shall be fourteen very soon, in a fortnight. I’ll confess one weakness of mine, Karamazov, just to you, since it’s our first meeting, so that you may understand my character at once. I hate being asked my age, more than that ... and in fact ... there’s a libelous story going about me, that last week I played robbers with the preparatory boys. It’s a fact that I did play with them, but it’s a perfect libel to say I did it for my own amusement. I have reasons for believing that you’ve heard the story; but I wasn’t playing for my own amusement, it was for the sake of the children, because they couldn’t think of anything to do by themselves. But they’ve always got some silly tale. This is an awful town for gossip, I can tell you.”\n\n“But what if you had been playing for your own amusement, what’s the harm?”\n\n“Come, I say, for my own amusement! You don’t play horses, do you?”\n\n“But you must look at it like this,” said Alyosha, smiling. “Grown-up people go to the theater and there the adventures of all sorts of heroes are represented—sometimes there are robbers and battles, too—and isn’t that just the same thing, in a different form, of course? And young people’s games of soldiers or robbers in their playtime are also art in its first stage. You know, they spring from the growing artistic instincts of the young. And sometimes these games are much better than performances in the theater, the only difference is that people go there to look at the actors, while in these games the young people are the actors themselves. But that’s only natural.”\n\n“You think so? Is that your idea?” Kolya looked at him intently. “Oh, you know, that’s rather an interesting view. When I go home, I’ll think it over. I’ll admit I thought I might learn something from you. I’ve come to learn of you, Karamazov,” Kolya concluded, in a voice full of spontaneous feeling.\n\n“And I of you,” said Alyosha, smiling and pressing his hand.\n\nKolya was much pleased with Alyosha. What struck him most was that he treated him exactly like an equal and that he talked to him just as if he were “quite grown up.”\n\n“I’ll show you something directly, Karamazov; it’s a theatrical performance, too,” he said, laughing nervously. “That’s why I’ve come.”\n\n“Let us go first to the people of the house, on the left. All the boys leave their coats in there, because the room is small and hot.”\n\n“Oh, I’m only coming in for a minute. I’ll keep on my overcoat. Perezvon will stay here in the passage and be dead. Ici, Perezvon, lie down and be dead! You see how he’s dead. I’ll go in first and explore, then I’ll whistle to him when I think fit, and you’ll see, he’ll dash in like mad. Only Smurov must not forget to open the door at the moment. I’ll arrange it all and you’ll see something.”\n\n# Chapter V. By Ilusha’s Bedside\n\nThe room inhabited by the family of the retired captain Snegiryov is already familiar to the reader. It was close and crowded at that moment with a number of visitors. Several boys were sitting with Ilusha, and though all of them, like Smurov, were prepared to deny that it was Alyosha who had brought them and reconciled them with Ilusha, it was really the fact. All the art he had used had been to take them, one by one, to Ilusha, without “sheepish sentimentality,” appearing to do so casually and without design. It was a great consolation to Ilusha in his suffering. He was greatly touched by seeing the almost tender affection and sympathy shown him by these boys, who had been his enemies. Krassotkin was the only one missing and his absence was a heavy load on Ilusha’s heart. Perhaps the bitterest of all his bitter memories was his stabbing Krassotkin, who had been his one friend and protector. Clever little Smurov, who was the first to make it up with Ilusha, thought it was so. But when Smurov hinted to Krassotkin that Alyosha wanted to come and see him about something, the latter cut him short, bidding Smurov tell “Karamazov” at once that he knew best what to do, that he wanted no one’s advice, and that, if he went to see Ilusha, he would choose his own time for he had “his own reasons.”\n\nThat was a fortnight before this Sunday. That was why Alyosha had not been to see him, as he had meant to. But though he waited, he sent Smurov to him twice again. Both times Krassotkin met him with a curt, impatient refusal, sending Alyosha a message not to bother him any more, that if he came himself, he, Krassotkin, would not go to Ilusha at all. Up to the very last day, Smurov did not know that Kolya meant to go to Ilusha that morning, and only the evening before, as he parted from Smurov, Kolya abruptly told him to wait at home for him next morning, for he would go with him to the Snegiryovs’, but warned him on no account to say he was coming, as he wanted to drop in casually. Smurov obeyed. Smurov’s fancy that Kolya would bring back the lost dog was based on the words Kolya had dropped that “they must be asses not to find the dog, if it was alive.” When Smurov, waiting for an opportunity, timidly hinted at his guess about the dog, Krassotkin flew into a violent rage. “I’m not such an ass as to go hunting about the town for other people’s dogs when I’ve got a dog of my own! And how can you imagine a dog could be alive after swallowing a pin? Sheepish sentimentality, that’s what it is!”\n\nFor the last fortnight Ilusha had not left his little bed under the ikons in the corner. He had not been to school since the day he met Alyosha and bit his finger. He was taken ill the same day, though for a month afterwards he was sometimes able to get up and walk about the room and passage. But latterly he had become so weak that he could not move without help from his father. His father was terribly concerned about him. He even gave up drinking and was almost crazy with terror that his boy would die. And often, especially after leading him round the room on his arm and putting him back to bed, he would run to a dark corner in the passage and, leaning his head against the wall, he would break into paroxysms of violent weeping, stifling his sobs that they might not be heard by Ilusha.\n\nReturning to the room, he would usually begin doing something to amuse and comfort his precious boy; he would tell him stories, funny anecdotes, or would mimic comic people he had happened to meet, even imitate the howls and cries of animals. But Ilusha could not bear to see his father fooling and playing the buffoon. Though the boy tried not to show how he disliked it, he saw with an aching heart that his father was an object of contempt, and he was continually haunted by the memory of the “wisp of tow” and that “terrible day.”\n\nNina, Ilusha’s gentle, crippled sister, did not like her father’s buffoonery either (Varvara had been gone for some time past to Petersburg to study at the university). But the half-imbecile mother was greatly diverted and laughed heartily when her husband began capering about or performing something. It was the only way she could be amused; all the rest of the time she was grumbling and complaining that now every one had forgotten her, that no one treated her with respect, that she was slighted, and so on. But during the last few days she had completely changed. She began looking constantly at Ilusha’s bed in the corner and seemed lost in thought. She was more silent, quieter, and, if she cried, she cried quietly so as not to be heard. The captain noticed the change in her with mournful perplexity. The boys’ visits at first only angered her, but later on their merry shouts and stories began to divert her, and at last she liked them so much that, if the boys had given up coming, she would have felt dreary without them. When the children told some story or played a game, she laughed and clapped her hands. She called some of them to her and kissed them. She was particularly fond of Smurov.\n\nAs for the captain, the presence in his room of the children, who came to cheer up Ilusha, filled his heart from the first with ecstatic joy. He even hoped that Ilusha would now get over his depression, and that that would hasten his recovery. In spite of his alarm about Ilusha, he had not, till lately, felt one minute’s doubt of his boy’s ultimate recovery.\n\nHe met his little visitors with homage, waited upon them hand and foot; he was ready to be their horse and even began letting them ride on his back, but Ilusha did not like the game and it was given up. He began buying little things for them, gingerbread and nuts, gave them tea and cut them sandwiches. It must be noted that all this time he had plenty of money. He had taken the two hundred roubles from Katerina Ivanovna just as Alyosha had predicted he would. And afterwards Katerina Ivanovna, learning more about their circumstances and Ilusha’s illness, visited them herself, made the acquaintance of the family, and succeeded in fascinating the half-imbecile mother. Since then she had been lavish in helping them, and the captain, terror-stricken at the thought that his boy might be dying, forgot his pride and humbly accepted her assistance.\n\nAll this time Doctor Herzenstube, who was called in by Katerina Ivanovna, came punctually every other day, but little was gained by his visits and he dosed the invalid mercilessly. But on that Sunday morning a new doctor was expected, who had come from Moscow, where he had a great reputation. Katerina Ivanovna had sent for him from Moscow at great expense, not expressly for Ilusha, but for another object of which more will be said in its place hereafter. But, as he had come, she had asked him to see Ilusha as well, and the captain had been told to expect him. He hadn’t the slightest idea that Kolya Krassotkin was coming, though he had long wished for a visit from the boy for whom Ilusha was fretting.\n\nAt the moment when Krassotkin opened the door and came into the room, the captain and all the boys were round Ilusha’s bed, looking at a tiny mastiff pup, which had only been born the day before, though the captain had bespoken it a week ago to comfort and amuse Ilusha, who was still fretting over the lost and probably dead Zhutchka. Ilusha, who had heard three days before that he was to be presented with a puppy, not an ordinary puppy, but a pedigree mastiff (a very important point, of course), tried from delicacy of feeling to pretend that he was pleased. But his father and the boys could not help seeing that the puppy only served to recall to his little heart the thought of the unhappy dog he had killed. The puppy lay beside him feebly moving and he, smiling sadly, stroked it with his thin, pale, wasted hand. Clearly he liked the puppy, but ... it wasn’t Zhutchka; if he could have had Zhutchka and the puppy, too, then he would have been completely happy.\n\n“Krassotkin!” cried one of the boys suddenly. He was the first to see him come in.\n\nKrassotkin’s entrance made a general sensation; the boys moved away and stood on each side of the bed, so that he could get a full view of Ilusha. The captain ran eagerly to meet Kolya.\n\n“Please come in ... you are welcome!” he said hurriedly. “Ilusha, Mr. Krassotkin has come to see you!”\n\nBut Krassotkin, shaking hands with him hurriedly, instantly showed his complete knowledge of the manners of good society. He turned first to the captain’s wife sitting in her arm-chair, who was very ill-humored at the moment, and was grumbling that the boys stood between her and Ilusha’s bed and did not let her see the new puppy. With the greatest courtesy he made her a bow, scraping his foot, and turning to Nina, he made her, as the only other lady present, a similar bow. This polite behavior made an extremely favorable impression on the deranged lady.\n\n“There, you can see at once he is a young man that has been well brought up,” she commented aloud, throwing up her hands; “but as for our other visitors they come in one on the top of another.”\n\n“How do you mean, mamma, one on the top of another, how is that?” muttered the captain affectionately, though a little anxious on her account.\n\n“That’s how they ride in. They get on each other’s shoulders in the passage and prance in like that on a respectable family. Strange sort of visitors!”\n\n“But who’s come in like that, mamma?”\n\n“Why, that boy came in riding on that one’s back and this one on that one’s.”\n\nKolya was already by Ilusha’s bedside. The sick boy turned visibly paler. He raised himself in the bed and looked intently at Kolya. Kolya had not seen his little friend for two months, and he was overwhelmed at the sight of him. He had never imagined that he would see such a wasted, yellow face, such enormous, feverishly glowing eyes and such thin little hands. He saw, with grieved surprise, Ilusha’s rapid, hard breathing and dry lips. He stepped close to him, held out his hand, and almost overwhelmed, he said:\n\n“Well, old man ... how are you?” But his voice failed him, he couldn’t achieve an appearance of ease; his face suddenly twitched and the corners of his mouth quivered. Ilusha smiled a pitiful little smile, still unable to utter a word. Something moved Kolya to raise his hand and pass it over Ilusha’s hair.\n\n“Never mind!” he murmured softly to him to cheer him up, or perhaps not knowing why he said it. For a minute they were silent again.\n\n“Hallo, so you’ve got a new puppy?” Kolya said suddenly, in a most callous voice.\n\n“Ye—es,” answered Ilusha in a long whisper, gasping for breath.\n\n“A black nose, that means he’ll be fierce, a good house-dog,” Kolya observed gravely and stolidly, as if the only thing he cared about was the puppy and its black nose. But in reality he still had to do his utmost to control his feelings not to burst out crying like a child, and do what he would he could not control it. “When it grows up, you’ll have to keep it on the chain, I’m sure.”\n\n“He’ll be a huge dog!” cried one of the boys.\n\n“Of course he will,” “a mastiff,” “large,” “like this,” “as big as a calf,” shouted several voices.\n\n“As big as a calf, as a real calf,” chimed in the captain. “I got one like that on purpose, one of the fiercest breed, and his parents are huge and very fierce, they stand as high as this from the floor.... Sit down here, on Ilusha’s bed, or here on the bench. You are welcome, we’ve been hoping to see you a long time.... You were so kind as to come with Alexey Fyodorovitch?”\n\nKrassotkin sat on the edge of the bed, at Ilusha’s feet. Though he had perhaps prepared a free-and-easy opening for the conversation on his way, now he completely lost the thread of it.\n\n“No ... I came with Perezvon. I’ve got a dog now, called Perezvon. A Slavonic name. He’s out there ... if I whistle, he’ll run in. I’ve brought a dog, too,” he said, addressing Ilusha all at once. “Do you remember Zhutchka, old man?” he suddenly fired the question at him.\n\nIlusha’s little face quivered. He looked with an agonized expression at Kolya. Alyosha, standing at the door, frowned and signed to Kolya not to speak of Zhutchka, but he did not or would not notice.\n\n“Where ... is Zhutchka?” Ilusha asked in a broken voice.\n\n“Oh, well, my boy, your Zhutchka’s lost and done for!”\n\nIlusha did not speak, but he fixed an intent gaze once more on Kolya. Alyosha, catching Kolya’s eye, signed to him vigorously again, but he turned away his eyes pretending not to have noticed.\n\n“It must have run away and died somewhere. It must have died after a meal like that,” Kolya pronounced pitilessly, though he seemed a little breathless. “But I’ve got a dog, Perezvon ... A Slavonic name.... I’ve brought him to show you.”\n\n“I don’t want him!” said Ilusha suddenly.\n\n“No, no, you really must see him ... it will amuse you. I brought him on purpose.... He’s the same sort of shaggy dog.... You allow me to call in my dog, madam?” He suddenly addressed Madame Snegiryov, with inexplicable excitement in his manner.\n\n“I don’t want him, I don’t want him!” cried Ilusha, with a mournful break in his voice. There was a reproachful light in his eyes.\n\n“You’d better,” the captain started up from the chest by the wall on which he had just sat down, “you’d better ... another time,” he muttered, but Kolya could not be restrained. He hurriedly shouted to Smurov, “Open the door,” and as soon as it was open, he blew his whistle. Perezvon dashed headlong into the room.\n\n“Jump, Perezvon, beg! Beg!” shouted Kolya, jumping up, and the dog stood erect on its hind-legs by Ilusha’s bedside. What followed was a surprise to every one: Ilusha started, lurched violently forward, bent over Perezvon and gazed at him, faint with suspense.\n\n“It’s ... Zhutchka!” he cried suddenly, in a voice breaking with joy and suffering.\n\n“And who did you think it was?” Krassotkin shouted with all his might, in a ringing, happy voice, and bending down he seized the dog and lifted him up to Ilusha.\n\n“Look, old man, you see, blind of one eye and the left ear is torn, just the marks you described to me. It was by that I found him. I found him directly. He did not belong to any one!” he explained, turning quickly to the captain, to his wife, to Alyosha and then again to Ilusha. “He used to live in the Fedotovs’ back-yard. Though he made his home there, they did not feed him. He was a stray dog that had run away from the village ... I found him.... You see, old man, he couldn’t have swallowed what you gave him. If he had, he must have died, he must have! So he must have spat it out, since he is alive. You did not see him do it. But the pin pricked his tongue, that is why he squealed. He ran away squealing and you thought he’d swallowed it. He might well squeal, because the skin of dogs’ mouths is so tender ... tenderer than in men, much tenderer!” Kolya cried impetuously, his face glowing and radiant with delight. Ilusha could not speak. White as a sheet, he gazed open-mouthed at Kolya, with his great eyes almost starting out of his head. And if Krassotkin, who had no suspicion of it, had known what a disastrous and fatal effect such a moment might have on the sick child’s health, nothing would have induced him to play such a trick on him. But Alyosha was perhaps the only person in the room who realized it. As for the captain he behaved like a small child.\n\n“Zhutchka! It’s Zhutchka!” he cried in a blissful voice, “Ilusha, this is Zhutchka, your Zhutchka! Mamma, this is Zhutchka!” He was almost weeping.\n\n“And I never guessed!” cried Smurov regretfully. “Bravo, Krassotkin! I said he’d find the dog and here he’s found him.”\n\n“Here he’s found him!” another boy repeated gleefully.\n\n“Krassotkin’s a brick!” cried a third voice.\n\n“He’s a brick, he’s a brick!” cried the other boys, and they began clapping.\n\n“Wait, wait,” Krassotkin did his utmost to shout above them all. “I’ll tell you how it happened, that’s the whole point. I found him, I took him home and hid him at once. I kept him locked up at home and did not show him to any one till to-day. Only Smurov has known for the last fortnight, but I assured him this dog was called Perezvon and he did not guess. And meanwhile I taught the dog all sorts of tricks. You should only see all the things he can do! I trained him so as to bring you a well-trained dog, in good condition, old man, so as to be able to say to you, ‘See, old man, what a fine dog your Zhutchka is now!’ Haven’t you a bit of meat? He’ll show you a trick that will make you die with laughing. A piece of meat, haven’t you got any?”\n\nThe captain ran across the passage to the landlady, where their cooking was done. Not to lose precious time, Kolya, in desperate haste, shouted to Perezvon, “Dead!” And the dog immediately turned round and lay on his back with its four paws in the air. The boys laughed. Ilusha looked on with the same suffering smile, but the person most delighted with the dog’s performance was “mamma.” She laughed at the dog and began snapping her fingers and calling it, “Perezvon, Perezvon!”\n\n“Nothing will make him get up, nothing!” Kolya cried triumphantly, proud of his success. “He won’t move for all the shouting in the world, but if I call to him, he’ll jump up in a minute. Ici, Perezvon!” The dog leapt up and bounded about, whining with delight. The captain ran back with a piece of cooked beef.\n\n“Is it hot?” Kolya inquired hurriedly, with a business-like air, taking the meat. “Dogs don’t like hot things. No, it’s all right. Look, everybody, look, Ilusha, look, old man; why aren’t you looking? He does not look at him, now I’ve brought him.”\n\nThe new trick consisted in making the dog stand motionless with his nose out and putting a tempting morsel of meat just on his nose. The luckless dog had to stand without moving, with the meat on his nose, as long as his master chose to keep him, without a movement, perhaps for half an hour. But he kept Perezvon only for a brief moment.\n\n“Paid for!” cried Kolya, and the meat passed in a flash from the dog’s nose to his mouth. The audience, of course, expressed enthusiasm and surprise.\n\n“Can you really have put off coming all this time simply to train the dog?” exclaimed Alyosha, with an involuntary note of reproach in his voice.\n\n“Simply for that!” answered Kolya, with perfect simplicity. “I wanted to show him in all his glory.”\n\n“Perezvon! Perezvon,” called Ilusha suddenly, snapping his thin fingers and beckoning to the dog.\n\n“What is it? Let him jump up on the bed! Ici, Perezvon!” Kolya slapped the bed and Perezvon darted up by Ilusha. The boy threw both arms round his head and Perezvon instantly licked his cheek. Ilusha crept close to him, stretched himself out in bed and hid his face in the dog’s shaggy coat.\n\n“Dear, dear!” kept exclaiming the captain. Kolya sat down again on the edge of the bed.\n\n“Ilusha, I can show you another trick. I’ve brought you a little cannon. You remember, I told you about it before and you said how much you’d like to see it. Well, here, I’ve brought it to you.”\n\nAnd Kolya hurriedly pulled out of his satchel the little bronze cannon. He hurried, because he was happy himself. Another time he would have waited till the sensation made by Perezvon had passed off, now he hurried on regardless of all consideration. “You are all happy now,” he felt, “so here’s something to make you happier!” He was perfectly enchanted himself.\n\n“I’ve been coveting this thing for a long while; it’s for you, old man, it’s for you. It belonged to Morozov, it was no use to him, he had it from his brother. I swopped a book from father’s book-case for it, A Kinsman of Mahomet or Salutary Folly, a scandalous book published in Moscow a hundred years ago, before they had any censorship. And Morozov has a taste for such things. He was grateful to me, too....”\n\nKolya held the cannon in his hand so that all could see and admire it. Ilusha raised himself, and, with his right arm still round the dog, he gazed enchanted at the toy. The sensation was even greater when Kolya announced that he had gunpowder too, and that it could be fired off at once “if it won’t alarm the ladies.” “Mamma” immediately asked to look at the toy closer and her request was granted. She was much pleased with the little bronze cannon on wheels and began rolling it to and fro on her lap. She readily gave permission for the cannon to be fired, without any idea of what she had been asked. Kolya showed the powder and the shot. The captain, as a military man, undertook to load it, putting in a minute quantity of powder. He asked that the shot might be put off till another time. The cannon was put on the floor, aiming towards an empty part of the room, three grains of powder were thrust into the touch-hole and a match was put to it. A magnificent explosion followed. Mamma was startled, but at once laughed with delight. The boys gazed in speechless triumph. But the captain, looking at Ilusha, was more enchanted than any of them. Kolya picked up the cannon and immediately presented it to Ilusha, together with the powder and the shot.\n\n“I got it for you, for you! I’ve been keeping it for you a long time,” he repeated once more in his delight.\n\n“Oh, give it to me! No, give me the cannon!” mamma began begging like a little child. Her face showed a piteous fear that she would not get it. Kolya was disconcerted. The captain fidgeted uneasily.\n\n“Mamma, mamma,” he ran to her, “the cannon’s yours, of course, but let Ilusha have it, because it’s a present to him, but it’s just as good as yours. Ilusha will always let you play with it; it shall belong to both of you, both of you.”\n\n“No, I don’t want it to belong to both of us, I want it to be mine altogether, not Ilusha’s,” persisted mamma, on the point of tears.\n\n“Take it, mother, here, keep it!” Ilusha cried. “Krassotkin, may I give it to my mother?” he turned to Krassotkin with an imploring face, as though he were afraid he might be offended at his giving his present to some one else.\n\n“Of course you may,” Krassotkin assented heartily, and, taking the cannon from Ilusha, he handed it himself to mamma with a polite bow. She was so touched that she cried.\n\n“Ilusha, darling, he’s the one who loves his mamma!” she said tenderly, and at once began wheeling the cannon to and fro on her lap again.\n\n“Mamma, let me kiss your hand.” The captain darted up to her at once and did so.\n\n“And I never saw such a charming fellow as this nice boy,” said the grateful lady, pointing to Krassotkin.\n\n“And I’ll bring you as much powder as you like, Ilusha. We make the powder ourselves now. Borovikov found out how it’s made—twenty-four parts of saltpeter, ten of sulphur and six of birchwood charcoal. It’s all pounded together, mixed into a paste with water and rubbed through a tammy sieve—that’s how it’s done.”\n\n“Smurov told me about your powder, only father says it’s not real gunpowder,” responded Ilusha.\n\n“Not real?” Kolya flushed. “It burns. I don’t know, of course.”\n\n“No, I didn’t mean that,” put in the captain with a guilty face. “I only said that real powder is not made like that, but that’s nothing, it can be made so.”\n\n“I don’t know, you know best. We lighted some in a pomatum pot, it burned splendidly, it all burnt away leaving only a tiny ash. But that was only the paste, and if you rub it through ... but of course you know best, I don’t know.... And Bulkin’s father thrashed him on account of our powder, did you hear?” he turned to Ilusha.\n\n“Yes,” answered Ilusha. He listened to Kolya with immense interest and enjoyment.\n\n“We had prepared a whole bottle of it and he used to keep it under his bed. His father saw it. He said it might explode, and thrashed him on the spot. He was going to make a complaint against me to the masters. He is not allowed to go about with me now, no one is allowed to go about with me now. Smurov is not allowed to either, I’ve got a bad name with every one. They say I’m a ‘desperate character,’” Kolya smiled scornfully. “It all began from what happened on the railway.”\n\n“Ah, we’ve heard of that exploit of yours, too,” cried the captain. “How could you lie still on the line? Is it possible you weren’t the least afraid, lying there under the train? Weren’t you frightened?”\n\nThe captain was abject in his flattery of Kolya.\n\n“N—not particularly,” answered Kolya carelessly. “What’s blasted my reputation more than anything here was that cursed goose,” he said, turning again to Ilusha. But though he assumed an unconcerned air as he talked, he still could not control himself and was continually missing the note he tried to keep up.\n\n“Ah! I heard about the goose!” Ilusha laughed, beaming all over. “They told me, but I didn’t understand. Did they really take you to the court?”\n\n“The most stupid, trivial affair, they made a mountain of a molehill as they always do,” Kolya began carelessly. “I was walking through the market-place here one day, just when they’d driven in the geese. I stopped and looked at them. All at once a fellow, who is an errand-boy at Plotnikov’s now, looked at me and said, ‘What are you looking at the geese for?’ I looked at him; he was a stupid, moon-faced fellow of twenty. I am always on the side of the peasantry, you know. I like talking to the peasants.... We’ve dropped behind the peasants—that’s an axiom. I believe you are laughing, Karamazov?”\n\n“No, Heaven forbid, I am listening,” said Alyosha with a most good-natured air, and the sensitive Kolya was immediately reassured.\n\n“My theory, Karamazov, is clear and simple,” he hurried on again, looking pleased. “I believe in the people and am always glad to give them their due, but I am not for spoiling them, that is a sine qua non ... But I was telling you about the goose. So I turned to the fool and answered, ‘I am wondering what the goose thinks about.’ He looked at me quite stupidly, ‘And what does the goose think about?’ he asked. ‘Do you see that cart full of oats?’ I said. ‘The oats are dropping out of the sack, and the goose has put its neck right under the wheel to gobble them up—do you see?’ ‘I see that quite well,’ he said. ‘Well,’ said I, ‘if that cart were to move on a little, would it break the goose’s neck or not?’ ‘It’d be sure to break it,’ and he grinned all over his face, highly delighted. ‘Come on, then,’ said I, ‘let’s try.’ ‘Let’s,’ he said. And it did not take us long to arrange: he stood at the bridle without being noticed, and I stood on one side to direct the goose. And the owner wasn’t looking, he was talking to some one, so I had nothing to do, the goose thrust its head in after the oats of itself, under the cart, just under the wheel. I winked at the lad, he tugged at the bridle, and crack. The goose’s neck was broken in half. And, as luck would have it, all the peasants saw us at that moment and they kicked up a shindy at once. ‘You did that on purpose!’ ‘No, not on purpose.’ ‘Yes, you did, on purpose!’ Well, they shouted, ‘Take him to the justice of the peace!’ They took me, too. ‘You were there, too,’ they said, ‘you helped, you’re known all over the market!’ And, for some reason, I really am known all over the market,” Kolya added conceitedly. “We all went off to the justice’s, they brought the goose, too. The fellow was crying in a great funk, simply blubbering like a woman. And the farmer kept shouting that you could kill any number of geese like that. Well, of course, there were witnesses. The justice of the peace settled it in a minute, that the farmer was to be paid a rouble for the goose, and the fellow to have the goose. And he was warned not to play such pranks again. And the fellow kept blubbering like a woman. ‘It wasn’t me,’ he said, ‘it was he egged me on,’ and he pointed to me. I answered with the utmost composure that I hadn’t egged him on, that I simply stated the general proposition, had spoken hypothetically. The justice of the peace smiled and was vexed with himself at once for having smiled. ‘I’ll complain to your masters of you, so that for the future you mayn’t waste your time on such general propositions, instead of sitting at your books and learning your lessons.’ He didn’t complain to the masters, that was a joke, but the matter was noised abroad and came to the ears of the masters. Their ears are long, you know! The classical master, Kolbasnikov, was particularly shocked about it, but Dardanelov got me off again. But Kolbasnikov is savage with every one now like a green ass. Did you know, Ilusha, he is just married, got a dowry of a thousand roubles, and his bride’s a regular fright of the first rank and the last degree. The third-class fellows wrote an epigram on it:\n\nAstounding news has reached the class, Kolbasnikov has been an ass.\n\nAnd so on, awfully funny, I’ll bring it to you later on. I say nothing against Dardanelov, he is a learned man, there’s no doubt about it. I respect men like that and it’s not because he stood up for me.”\n\n“But you took him down about the founders of Troy!” Smurov put in suddenly, unmistakably proud of Krassotkin at such a moment. He was particularly pleased with the story of the goose.\n\n“Did you really take him down?” the captain inquired, in a flattering way. “On the question who founded Troy? We heard of it, Ilusha told me about it at the time.”\n\n“He knows everything, father, he knows more than any of us!” put in Ilusha; “he only pretends to be like that, but really he is top in every subject....”\n\nIlusha looked at Kolya with infinite happiness.\n\n“Oh, that’s all nonsense about Troy, a trivial matter. I consider this an unimportant question,” said Kolya with haughty humility. He had by now completely recovered his dignity, though he was still a little uneasy. He felt that he was greatly excited and that he had talked about the goose, for instance, with too little reserve, while Alyosha had looked serious and had not said a word all the time. And the vain boy began by degrees to have a rankling fear that Alyosha was silent because he despised him, and thought he was showing off before him. If he dared to think anything like that Kolya would—\n\n“I regard the question as quite a trivial one,” he rapped out again, proudly.\n\n“And I know who founded Troy,” a boy, who had not spoken before, said suddenly, to the surprise of every one. He was silent and seemed to be shy. He was a pretty boy of about eleven, called Kartashov. He was sitting near the door. Kolya looked at him with dignified amazement.\n\nThe fact was that the identity of the founders of Troy had become a secret for the whole school, a secret which could only be discovered by reading Smaragdov, and no one had Smaragdov but Kolya. One day, when Kolya’s back was turned, Kartashov hastily opened Smaragdov, which lay among Kolya’s books, and immediately lighted on the passage relating to the foundation of Troy. This was a good time ago, but he felt uneasy and could not bring himself to announce publicly that he too knew who had founded Troy, afraid of what might happen and of Krassotkin’s somehow putting him to shame over it. But now he couldn’t resist saying it. For weeks he had been longing to.\n\n“Well, who did found it?” asked Kolya, turning to him with haughty superciliousness. He saw from his face that he really did know and at once made up his mind how to take it. There was, so to speak, a discordant note in the general harmony.\n\n“Troy was founded by Teucer, Dardanus, Ilius and Tros,” the boy rapped out at once, and in the same instant he blushed, blushed so, that it was painful to look at him. But the boys stared at him, stared at him for a whole minute, and then all the staring eyes turned at once and were fastened upon Kolya, who was still scanning the audacious boy with disdainful composure.\n\n“In what sense did they found it?” he deigned to comment at last. “And what is meant by founding a city or a state? What do they do? Did they go and each lay a brick, do you suppose?”\n\nThere was laughter. The offending boy turned from pink to crimson. He was silent and on the point of tears. Kolya held him so for a minute.\n\n“Before you talk of a historical event like the foundation of a nationality, you must first understand what you mean by it,” he admonished him in stern, incisive tones. “But I attach no consequence to these old wives’ tales and I don’t think much of universal history in general,” he added carelessly, addressing the company generally.\n\n“Universal history?” the captain inquired, looking almost scared.\n\n“Yes, universal history! It’s the study of the successive follies of mankind and nothing more. The only subjects I respect are mathematics and natural science,” said Kolya. He was showing off and he stole a glance at Alyosha; his was the only opinion he was afraid of there. But Alyosha was still silent and still serious as before. If Alyosha had said a word it would have stopped him, but Alyosha was silent and “it might be the silence of contempt,” and that finally irritated Kolya.\n\n“The classical languages, too ... they are simply madness, nothing more. You seem to disagree with me again, Karamazov?”\n\n“I don’t agree,” said Alyosha, with a faint smile.\n\n“The study of the classics, if you ask my opinion, is simply a police measure, that’s simply why it has been introduced into our schools.” By degrees Kolya began to get breathless again. “Latin and Greek were introduced because they are a bore and because they stupefy the intellect. It was dull before, so what could they do to make things duller? It was senseless enough before, so what could they do to make it more senseless? So they thought of Greek and Latin. That’s my opinion, I hope I shall never change it,” Kolya finished abruptly. His cheeks were flushed.\n\n“That’s true,” assented Smurov suddenly, in a ringing tone of conviction. He had listened attentively.\n\n“And yet he is first in Latin himself,” cried one of the group of boys suddenly.\n\n“Yes, father, he says that and yet he is first in Latin,” echoed Ilusha.\n\n“What of it?” Kolya thought fit to defend himself, though the praise was very sweet to him. “I am fagging away at Latin because I have to, because I promised my mother to pass my examination, and I think that whatever you do, it’s worth doing it well. But in my soul I have a profound contempt for the classics and all that fraud.... You don’t agree, Karamazov?”\n\n“Why ‘fraud’?” Alyosha smiled again.\n\n“Well, all the classical authors have been translated into all languages, so it was not for the sake of studying the classics they introduced Latin, but solely as a police measure, to stupefy the intelligence. So what can one call it but a fraud?”\n\n“Why, who taught you all this?” cried Alyosha, surprised at last.\n\n“In the first place I am capable of thinking for myself without being taught. Besides, what I said just now about the classics being translated our teacher Kolbasnikov has said to the whole of the third class.”\n\n“The doctor has come!” cried Nina, who had been silent till then.\n\nA carriage belonging to Madame Hohlakov drove up to the gate. The captain, who had been expecting the doctor all the morning, rushed headlong out to meet him. “Mamma” pulled herself together and assumed a dignified air. Alyosha went up to Ilusha and began setting his pillows straight. Nina, from her invalid chair, anxiously watched him putting the bed tidy. The boys hurriedly took leave. Some of them promised to come again in the evening. Kolya called Perezvon and the dog jumped off the bed.\n\n“I won’t go away, I won’t go away,” Kolya said hastily to Ilusha. “I’ll wait in the passage and come back when the doctor’s gone, I’ll come back with Perezvon.”\n\nBut by now the doctor had entered, an important-looking person with long, dark whiskers and a shiny, shaven chin, wearing a bearskin coat. As he crossed the threshold he stopped, taken aback; he probably fancied he had come to the wrong place. “How is this? Where am I?” he muttered, not removing his coat nor his peaked sealskin cap. The crowd, the poverty of the room, the washing hanging on a line in the corner, puzzled him. The captain, bent double, was bowing low before him.\n\n“It’s here, sir, here, sir,” he muttered cringingly; “it’s here, you’ve come right, you were coming to us...”\n\n“Sne-gi-ryov?” the doctor said loudly and pompously. “Mr. Snegiryov—is that you?”\n\n“That’s me, sir!”\n\n“Ah!”\n\nThe doctor looked round the room with a squeamish air once more and threw off his coat, displaying to all eyes the grand decoration at his neck. The captain caught the fur coat in the air, and the doctor took off his cap.\n\n“Where is the patient?” he asked emphatically.\n\n# Chapter VI. Precocity\n\n“What do you think the doctor will say to him?” Kolya asked quickly. “What a repulsive mug, though, hasn’t he? I can’t endure medicine!”\n\n“Ilusha is dying. I think that’s certain,” answered Alyosha, mournfully.\n\n“They are rogues! Medicine’s a fraud! I am glad to have made your acquaintance, though, Karamazov. I wanted to know you for a long time. I am only sorry we meet in such sad circumstances.”\n\nKolya had a great inclination to say something even warmer and more demonstrative, but he felt ill at ease. Alyosha noticed this, smiled, and pressed his hand.\n\n“I’ve long learned to respect you as a rare person,” Kolya muttered again, faltering and uncertain. “I have heard you are a mystic and have been in the monastery. I know you are a mystic, but ... that hasn’t put me off. Contact with real life will cure you.... It’s always so with characters like yours.”\n\n“What do you mean by mystic? Cure me of what?” Alyosha was rather astonished.\n\n“Oh, God and all the rest of it.”\n\n“What, don’t you believe in God?”\n\n“Oh, I’ve nothing against God. Of course, God is only a hypothesis, but ... I admit that He is needed ... for the order of the universe and all that ... and that if there were no God He would have to be invented,” added Kolya, beginning to blush. He suddenly fancied that Alyosha might think he was trying to show off his knowledge and to prove that he was “grown up.” “I haven’t the slightest desire to show off my knowledge to him,” Kolya thought indignantly. And all of a sudden he felt horribly annoyed.\n\n“I must confess I can’t endure entering on such discussions,” he said with a final air. “It’s possible for one who doesn’t believe in God to love mankind, don’t you think so? Voltaire didn’t believe in God and loved mankind?” (“I am at it again,” he thought to himself.)\n\n“Voltaire believed in God, though not very much, I think, and I don’t think he loved mankind very much either,” said Alyosha quietly, gently, and quite naturally, as though he were talking to some one of his own age, or even older. Kolya was particularly struck by Alyosha’s apparent diffidence about his opinion of Voltaire. He seemed to be leaving the question for him, little Kolya, to settle.\n\n“Have you read Voltaire?” Alyosha finished.\n\n“No, not to say read.... But I’ve read Candide in the Russian translation ... in an absurd, grotesque, old translation ... (At it again! again!)”\n\n“And did you understand it?”\n\n“Oh, yes, everything.... That is ... Why do you suppose I shouldn’t understand it? There’s a lot of nastiness in it, of course.... Of course I can understand that it’s a philosophical novel and written to advocate an idea....” Kolya was getting mixed by now. “I am a Socialist, Karamazov, I am an incurable Socialist,” he announced suddenly, apropos of nothing.\n\n“A Socialist?” laughed Alyosha. “But when have you had time to become one? Why, I thought you were only thirteen?”\n\nKolya winced.\n\n“In the first place I am not thirteen, but fourteen, fourteen in a fortnight,” he flushed angrily, “and in the second place I am at a complete loss to understand what my age has to do with it? The question is what are my convictions, not what is my age, isn’t it?”\n\n“When you are older, you’ll understand for yourself the influence of age on convictions. I fancied, too, that you were not expressing your own ideas,” Alyosha answered serenely and modestly, but Kolya interrupted him hotly:\n\n“Come, you want obedience and mysticism. You must admit that the Christian religion, for instance, has only been of use to the rich and the powerful to keep the lower classes in slavery. That’s so, isn’t it?”\n\n“Ah, I know where you read that, and I am sure some one told you so!” cried Alyosha.\n\n“I say, what makes you think I read it? And certainly no one told me so. I can think for myself.... I am not opposed to Christ, if you like. He was a most humane person, and if He were alive to-day, He would be found in the ranks of the revolutionists, and would perhaps play a conspicuous part.... There’s no doubt about that.”\n\n“Oh, where, where did you get that from? What fool have you made friends with?” exclaimed Alyosha.\n\n“Come, the truth will out! It has so chanced that I have often talked to Mr. Rakitin, of course, but ... old Byelinsky said that, too, so they say.”\n\n“Byelinsky? I don’t remember. He hasn’t written that anywhere.”\n\n“If he didn’t write it, they say he said it. I heard that from a ... but never mind.”\n\n“And have you read Byelinsky?”\n\n“Well, no ... I haven’t read all of him, but ... I read the passage about Tatyana, why she didn’t go off with Onyegin.”\n\n“Didn’t go off with Onyegin? Surely you don’t ... understand that already?”\n\n“Why, you seem to take me for little Smurov,” said Kolya, with a grin of irritation. “But please don’t suppose I am such a revolutionist. I often disagree with Mr. Rakitin. Though I mention Tatyana, I am not at all for the emancipation of women. I acknowledge that women are a subject race and must obey. Les femmes tricottent, as Napoleon said.” Kolya, for some reason, smiled, “And on that question at least I am quite of one mind with that pseudo-great man. I think, too, that to leave one’s own country and fly to America is mean, worse than mean—silly. Why go to America when one may be of great service to humanity here? Now especially. There’s a perfect mass of fruitful activity open to us. That’s what I answered.”\n\n“What do you mean? Answered whom? Has some one suggested your going to America already?”\n\n“I must own, they’ve been at me to go, but I declined. That’s between ourselves, of course, Karamazov; do you hear, not a word to any one. I say this only to you. I am not at all anxious to fall into the clutches of the secret police and take lessons at the Chain bridge.\n\nLong will you remember The house at the Chain bridge.\n\nDo you remember? It’s splendid. Why are you laughing? You don’t suppose I am fibbing, do you?” (“What if he should find out that I’ve only that one number of The Bell in father’s bookcase, and haven’t read any more of it?” Kolya thought with a shudder.)\n\n“Oh, no, I am not laughing and don’t suppose for a moment that you are lying. No, indeed, I can’t suppose so, for all this, alas! is perfectly true. But tell me, have you read Pushkin—Onyegin, for instance?... You spoke just now of Tatyana.”\n\n“No, I haven’t read it yet, but I want to read it. I have no prejudices, Karamazov; I want to hear both sides. What makes you ask?”\n\n“Oh, nothing.”\n\n“Tell me, Karamazov, have you an awful contempt for me?” Kolya rapped out suddenly and drew himself up before Alyosha, as though he were on drill. “Be so kind as to tell me, without beating about the bush.”\n\n“I have a contempt for you?” Alyosha looked at him wondering. “What for? I am only sad that a charming nature such as yours should be perverted by all this crude nonsense before you have begun life.”\n\n“Don’t be anxious about my nature,” Kolya interrupted, not without complacency. “But it’s true that I am stupidly sensitive, crudely sensitive. You smiled just now, and I fancied you seemed to—”\n\n“Oh, my smile meant something quite different. I’ll tell you why I smiled. Not long ago I read the criticism made by a German who had lived in Russia, on our students and schoolboys of to-day. ‘Show a Russian schoolboy,’ he writes, ‘a map of the stars, which he knows nothing about, and he will give you back the map next day with corrections on it.’ No knowledge and unbounded conceit—that’s what the German meant to say about the Russian schoolboy.”\n\n“Yes, that’s perfectly right,” Kolya laughed suddenly, “exactly so! Bravo the German! But he did not see the good side, what do you think? Conceit may be, that comes from youth, that will be corrected if need be, but, on the other hand, there is an independent spirit almost from childhood, boldness of thought and conviction, and not the spirit of these sausage makers, groveling before authority.... But the German was right all the same. Bravo the German! But Germans want strangling all the same. Though they are so good at science and learning they must be strangled.”\n\n“Strangled, what for?” smiled Alyosha.\n\n“Well, perhaps I am talking nonsense, I agree. I am awfully childish sometimes, and when I am pleased about anything I can’t restrain myself and am ready to talk any stuff. But, I say, we are chattering away here about nothing, and that doctor has been a long time in there. But perhaps he’s examining the mamma and that poor crippled Nina. I liked that Nina, you know. She whispered to me suddenly as I was coming away, ‘Why didn’t you come before?’ And in such a voice, so reproachfully! I think she is awfully nice and pathetic.”\n\n“Yes, yes! Well, you’ll be coming often, you will see what she is like. It would do you a great deal of good to know people like that, to learn to value a great deal which you will find out from knowing these people,” Alyosha observed warmly. “That would have more effect on you than anything.”\n\n“Oh, how I regret and blame myself for not having come sooner!” Kolya exclaimed, with bitter feeling.\n\n“Yes, it’s a great pity. You saw for yourself how delighted the poor child was to see you. And how he fretted for you to come!”\n\n“Don’t tell me! You make it worse! But it serves me right. What kept me from coming was my conceit, my egoistic vanity, and the beastly wilfullness, which I never can get rid of, though I’ve been struggling with it all my life. I see that now. I am a beast in lots of ways, Karamazov!”\n\n“No, you have a charming nature, though it’s been distorted, and I quite understand why you have had such an influence on this generous, morbidly sensitive boy,” Alyosha answered warmly.\n\n“And you say that to me!” cried Kolya; “and would you believe it, I thought—I’ve thought several times since I’ve been here—that you despised me! If only you knew how I prize your opinion!”\n\n“But are you really so sensitive? At your age! Would you believe it, just now, when you were telling your story, I thought, as I watched you, that you must be very sensitive!”\n\n“You thought so? What an eye you’ve got, I say! I bet that was when I was talking about the goose. That was just when I was fancying you had a great contempt for me for being in such a hurry to show off, and for a moment I quite hated you for it, and began talking like a fool. Then I fancied—just now, here—when I said that if there were no God He would have to be invented, that I was in too great a hurry to display my knowledge, especially as I got that phrase out of a book. But I swear I wasn’t showing off out of vanity, though I really don’t know why. Because I was so pleased? Yes, I believe it was because I was so pleased ... though it’s perfectly disgraceful for any one to be gushing directly they are pleased, I know that. But I am convinced now that you don’t despise me; it was all my imagination. Oh, Karamazov, I am profoundly unhappy. I sometimes fancy all sorts of things, that every one is laughing at me, the whole world, and then I feel ready to overturn the whole order of things.”\n\n“And you worry every one about you,” smiled Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, I worry every one about me, especially my mother. Karamazov, tell me, am I very ridiculous now?”\n\n“Don’t think about that, don’t think of it at all!” cried Alyosha. “And what does ridiculous mean? Isn’t every one constantly being or seeming ridiculous? Besides, nearly all clever people now are fearfully afraid of being ridiculous, and that makes them unhappy. All I am surprised at is that you should be feeling that so early, though I’ve observed it for some time past, and not only in you. Nowadays the very children have begun to suffer from it. It’s almost a sort of insanity. The devil has taken the form of that vanity and entered into the whole generation; it’s simply the devil,” added Alyosha, without a trace of the smile that Kolya, staring at him, expected to see. “You are like every one else,” said Alyosha, in conclusion, “that is, like very many others. Only you must not be like everybody else, that’s all.”\n\n“Even if every one is like that?”\n\n“Yes, even if every one is like that. You be the only one not like it. You really are not like every one else, here you are not ashamed to confess to something bad and even ridiculous. And who will admit so much in these days? No one. And people have even ceased to feel the impulse to self-criticism. Don’t be like every one else, even if you are the only one.”\n\n“Splendid! I was not mistaken in you. You know how to console one. Oh, how I have longed to know you, Karamazov! I’ve long been eager for this meeting. Can you really have thought about me, too? You said just now that you thought of me, too?”\n\n“Yes, I’d heard of you and had thought of you, too ... and if it’s partly vanity that makes you ask, it doesn’t matter.”\n\n“Do you know, Karamazov, our talk has been like a declaration of love,” said Kolya, in a bashful and melting voice. “That’s not ridiculous, is it?”\n\n“Not at all ridiculous, and if it were, it wouldn’t matter, because it’s been a good thing.” Alyosha smiled brightly.\n\n“But do you know, Karamazov, you must admit that you are a little ashamed yourself, now.... I see it by your eyes.” Kolya smiled with a sort of sly happiness.\n\n“Why ashamed?”\n\n“Well, why are you blushing?”\n\n“It was you made me blush,” laughed Alyosha, and he really did blush. “Oh, well, I am a little, goodness knows why, I don’t know...” he muttered, almost embarrassed.\n\n“Oh, how I love you and admire you at this moment just because you are rather ashamed! Because you are just like me,” cried Kolya, in positive ecstasy. His cheeks glowed, his eyes beamed.\n\n“You know, Kolya, you will be very unhappy in your life,” something made Alyosha say suddenly.\n\n“I know, I know. How you know it all beforehand!” Kolya agreed at once.\n\n“But you will bless life on the whole, all the same.”\n\n“Just so, hurrah! You are a prophet. Oh, we shall get on together, Karamazov! Do you know, what delights me most, is that you treat me quite like an equal. But we are not equals, no, we are not, you are better! But we shall get on. Do you know, all this last month, I’ve been saying to myself, ‘Either we shall be friends at once, for ever, or we shall part enemies to the grave!’”\n\n“And saying that, of course, you loved me,” Alyosha laughed gayly.\n\n“I did. I loved you awfully. I’ve been loving and dreaming of you. And how do you know it all beforehand? Ah, here’s the doctor. Goodness! What will he tell us? Look at his face!”\n\n# Chapter VII. Ilusha\n\nThe doctor came out of the room again, muffled in his fur coat and with his cap on his head. His face looked almost angry and disgusted, as though he were afraid of getting dirty. He cast a cursory glance round the passage, looking sternly at Alyosha and Kolya as he did so. Alyosha waved from the door to the coachman, and the carriage that had brought the doctor drove up. The captain darted out after the doctor, and, bowing apologetically, stopped him to get the last word. The poor fellow looked utterly crushed; there was a scared look in his eyes.\n\n“Your Excellency, your Excellency ... is it possible?” he began, but could not go on and clasped his hands in despair. Yet he still gazed imploringly at the doctor, as though a word from him might still change the poor boy’s fate.\n\n“I can’t help it, I am not God!” the doctor answered offhand, though with the customary impressiveness.\n\n“Doctor ... your Excellency ... and will it be soon, soon?”\n\n“You must be prepared for anything,” said the doctor in emphatic and incisive tones, and dropping his eyes, he was about to step out to the coach.\n\n“Your Excellency, for Christ’s sake!” the terror-stricken captain stopped him again. “Your Excellency! but can nothing, absolutely nothing save him now?”\n\n“It’s not in my hands now,” said the doctor impatiently, “but h’m!...” he stopped suddenly. “If you could, for instance ... send ... your patient ... at once, without delay” (the words “at once, without delay,” the doctor uttered with an almost wrathful sternness that made the captain start) “to Syracuse, the change to the new be-ne-ficial climatic conditions might possibly effect—”\n\n“To Syracuse!” cried the captain, unable to grasp what was said.\n\n“Syracuse is in Sicily,” Kolya jerked out suddenly in explanation. The doctor looked at him.\n\n“Sicily! your Excellency,” faltered the captain, “but you’ve seen”—he spread out his hands, indicating his surroundings—“mamma and my family?”\n\n“N—no, Sicily is not the place for the family, the family should go to Caucasus in the early spring ... your daughter must go to the Caucasus, and your wife ... after a course of the waters in the Caucasus for her rheumatism ... must be sent straight to Paris to the mental specialist Lepelletier; I could give you a note to him, and then ... there might be a change—”\n\n“Doctor, doctor! But you see!” The captain flung wide his hands again despairingly, indicating the bare wooden walls of the passage.\n\n“Well, that’s not my business,” grinned the doctor. “I have only told you the answer of medical science to your question as to possible treatment. As for the rest, to my regret—”\n\n“Don’t be afraid, apothecary, my dog won’t bite you,” Kolya rapped out loudly, noticing the doctor’s rather uneasy glance at Perezvon, who was standing in the doorway. There was a wrathful note in Kolya’s voice. He used the word apothecary instead of doctor on purpose, and, as he explained afterwards, used it “to insult him.”\n\n“What’s that?” The doctor flung up his head, staring with surprise at Kolya. “Who’s this?” he addressed Alyosha, as though asking him to explain.\n\n“It’s Perezvon’s master, don’t worry about me,” Kolya said incisively again.\n\n“Perezvon?”[7] repeated the doctor, perplexed.\n\n“He hears the bell, but where it is he cannot tell. Good-by, we shall meet in Syracuse.”\n\n“Who’s this? Who’s this?” The doctor flew into a terrible rage.\n\n“He is a schoolboy, doctor, he is a mischievous boy; take no notice of him,” said Alyosha, frowning and speaking quickly. “Kolya, hold your tongue!” he cried to Krassotkin. “Take no notice of him, doctor,” he repeated, rather impatiently.\n\n“He wants a thrashing, a good thrashing!” The doctor stamped in a perfect fury.\n\n“And you know, apothecary, my Perezvon might bite!” said Kolya, turning pale, with quivering voice and flashing eyes. “Ici, Perezvon!”\n\n“Kolya, if you say another word, I’ll have nothing more to do with you,” Alyosha cried peremptorily.\n\n“There is only one man in the world who can command Nikolay Krassotkin—this is the man”; Kolya pointed to Alyosha. “I obey him, good-by!”\n\nHe stepped forward, opened the door, and quickly went into the inner room. Perezvon flew after him. The doctor stood still for five seconds in amazement, looking at Alyosha; then, with a curse, he went out quickly to the carriage, repeating aloud, “This is ... this is ... I don’t know what it is!” The captain darted forward to help him into the carriage. Alyosha followed Kolya into the room. He was already by Ilusha’s bedside. The sick boy was holding his hand and calling for his father. A minute later the captain, too, came back.\n\n“Father, father, come ... we ...” Ilusha faltered in violent excitement, but apparently unable to go on, he flung his wasted arms round his father and Kolya, uniting them in one embrace, and hugging them as tightly as he could. The captain suddenly began to shake with dumb sobs, and Kolya’s lips and chin twitched.\n\n“Father, father! How sorry I am for you!” Ilusha moaned bitterly.\n\n“Ilusha ... darling ... the doctor said ... you would be all right ... we shall be happy ... the doctor ...” the captain began.\n\n“Ah, father! I know what the new doctor said to you about me.... I saw!” cried Ilusha, and again he hugged them both with all his strength, hiding his face on his father’s shoulder.\n\n“Father, don’t cry, and when I die get a good boy, another one ... choose one of them all, a good one, call him Ilusha and love him instead of me....”\n\n“Hush, old man, you’ll get well,” Krassotkin cried suddenly, in a voice that sounded angry.\n\n“But don’t ever forget me, father,” Ilusha went on, “come to my grave ... and, father, bury me by our big stone, where we used to go for our walk, and come to me there with Krassotkin in the evening ... and Perezvon ... I shall expect you.... Father, father!”\n\nHis voice broke. They were all three silent, still embracing. Nina was crying quietly in her chair, and at last seeing them all crying, “mamma,” too, burst into tears.\n\n“Ilusha! Ilusha!” she exclaimed.\n\nKrassotkin suddenly released himself from Ilusha’s embrace.\n\n“Good-by, old man, mother expects me back to dinner,” he said quickly. “What a pity I did not tell her! She will be dreadfully anxious.... But after dinner I’ll come back to you for the whole day, for the whole evening, and I’ll tell you all sorts of things, all sorts of things. And I’ll bring Perezvon, but now I will take him with me, because he will begin to howl when I am away and bother you. Good-by!”\n\nAnd he ran out into the passage. He didn’t want to cry, but in the passage he burst into tears. Alyosha found him crying.\n\n“Kolya, you must be sure to keep your word and come, or he will be terribly disappointed,” Alyosha said emphatically.\n\n“I will! Oh, how I curse myself for not having come before!” muttered Kolya, crying, and no longer ashamed of it.\n\nAt that moment the captain flew out of the room, and at once closed the door behind him. His face looked frenzied, his lips were trembling. He stood before the two and flung up his arms.\n\n“I don’t want a good boy! I don’t want another boy!” he muttered in a wild whisper, clenching his teeth. “If I forget thee, Jerusalem, may my tongue—” He broke off with a sob and sank on his knees before the wooden bench. Pressing his fists against his head, he began sobbing with absurd whimpering cries, doing his utmost that his cries should not be heard in the room.\n\nKolya ran out into the street.\n\n“Good-by, Karamazov? Will you come yourself?” he cried sharply and angrily to Alyosha.\n\n“I will certainly come in the evening.”\n\n“What was that he said about Jerusalem?... What did he mean by that?”\n\n“It’s from the Bible. ‘If I forget thee, Jerusalem,’ that is, if I forget all that is most precious to me, if I let anything take its place, then may—”\n\n“I understand, that’s enough! Mind you come! Ici, Perezvon!” he cried with positive ferocity to the dog, and with rapid strides he went home.\n\n# Book XI. Ivan Chapter I. At Grushenka’s\n\nAlyosha went towards the cathedral square to the widow Morozov’s house to see Grushenka, who had sent Fenya to him early in the morning with an urgent message begging him to come. Questioning Fenya, Alyosha learned that her mistress had been particularly distressed since the previous day. During the two months that had passed since Mitya’s arrest, Alyosha had called frequently at the widow Morozov’s house, both from his own inclination and to take messages for Mitya. Three days after Mitya’s arrest, Grushenka was taken very ill and was ill for nearly five weeks. For one whole week she was unconscious. She was very much changed—thinner and a little sallow, though she had for the past fortnight been well enough to go out. But to Alyosha her face was even more attractive than before, and he liked to meet her eyes when he went in to her. A look of firmness and intelligent purpose had developed in her face. There were signs of a spiritual transformation in her, and a steadfast, fine and humble determination that nothing could shake could be discerned in her. There was a small vertical line between her brows which gave her charming face a look of concentrated thought, almost austere at the first glance. There was scarcely a trace of her former frivolity.\n\nIt seemed strange to Alyosha, too, that in spite of the calamity that had overtaken the poor girl, betrothed to a man who had been arrested for a terrible crime, almost at the instant of their betrothal, in spite of her illness and the almost inevitable sentence hanging over Mitya, Grushenka had not yet lost her youthful cheerfulness. There was a soft light in the once proud eyes, though at times they gleamed with the old vindictive fire when she was visited by one disturbing thought stronger than ever in her heart. The object of that uneasiness was the same as ever—Katerina Ivanovna, of whom Grushenka had even raved when she lay in delirium. Alyosha knew that she was fearfully jealous of her. Yet Katerina Ivanovna had not once visited Mitya in his prison, though she might have done it whenever she liked. All this made a difficult problem for Alyosha, for he was the only person to whom Grushenka opened her heart and from whom she was continually asking advice. Sometimes he was unable to say anything.\n\nFull of anxiety he entered her lodging. She was at home. She had returned from seeing Mitya half an hour before, and from the rapid movement with which she leapt up from her chair to meet him he saw that she had been expecting him with great impatience. A pack of cards dealt for a game of “fools” lay on the table. A bed had been made up on the leather sofa on the other side and Maximov lay, half-reclining, on it. He wore a dressing-gown and a cotton nightcap, and was evidently ill and weak, though he was smiling blissfully. When the homeless old man returned with Grushenka from Mokroe two months before, he had simply stayed on and was still staying with her. He arrived with her in rain and sleet, sat down on the sofa, drenched and scared, and gazed mutely at her with a timid, appealing smile. Grushenka, who was in terrible grief and in the first stage of fever, almost forgot his existence in all she had to do the first half-hour after her arrival. Suddenly she chanced to look at him intently: he laughed a pitiful, helpless little laugh. She called Fenya and told her to give him something to eat. All that day he sat in the same place, almost without stirring. When it got dark and the shutters were closed, Fenya asked her mistress:\n\n“Is the gentleman going to stay the night, mistress?”\n\n“Yes; make him a bed on the sofa,” answered Grushenka.\n\nQuestioning him more in detail, Grushenka learned from him that he had literally nowhere to go, and that “Mr. Kalganov, my benefactor, told me straight that he wouldn’t receive me again and gave me five roubles.”\n\n“Well, God bless you, you’d better stay, then,” Grushenka decided in her grief, smiling compassionately at him. Her smile wrung the old man’s heart and his lips twitched with grateful tears. And so the destitute wanderer had stayed with her ever since. He did not leave the house even when she was ill. Fenya and her grandmother, the cook, did not turn him out, but went on serving him meals and making up his bed on the sofa. Grushenka had grown used to him, and coming back from seeing Mitya (whom she had begun to visit in prison before she was really well) she would sit down and begin talking to “Maximushka” about trifling matters, to keep her from thinking of her sorrow. The old man turned out to be a good story-teller on occasions, so that at last he became necessary to her. Grushenka saw scarcely any one else beside Alyosha, who did not come every day and never stayed long. Her old merchant lay seriously ill at this time, “at his last gasp” as they said in the town, and he did, in fact, die a week after Mitya’s trial. Three weeks before his death, feeling the end approaching, he made his sons, their wives and children, come upstairs to him at last and bade them not leave him again. From that moment he gave strict orders to his servants not to admit Grushenka and to tell her if she came, “The master wishes you long life and happiness and tells you to forget him.” But Grushenka sent almost every day to inquire after him.\n\n“You’ve come at last!” she cried, flinging down the cards and joyfully greeting Alyosha, “and Maximushka’s been scaring me that perhaps you wouldn’t come. Ah, how I need you! Sit down to the table. What will you have—coffee?”\n\n“Yes, please,” said Alyosha, sitting down at the table. “I am very hungry.”\n\n“That’s right. Fenya, Fenya, coffee,” cried Grushenka. “It’s been made a long time ready for you. And bring some little pies, and mind they are hot. Do you know, we’ve had a storm over those pies to-day. I took them to the prison for him, and would you believe it, he threw them back to me: he would not eat them. He flung one of them on the floor and stamped on it. So I said to him: ‘I shall leave them with the warder; if you don’t eat them before evening, it will be that your venomous spite is enough for you!’ With that I went away. We quarreled again, would you believe it? Whenever I go we quarrel.”\n\nGrushenka said all this in one breath in her agitation. Maximov, feeling nervous, at once smiled and looked on the floor.\n\n“What did you quarrel about this time?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“I didn’t expect it in the least. Only fancy, he is jealous of the Pole. ‘Why are you keeping him?’ he said. ‘So you’ve begun keeping him.’ He is jealous, jealous of me all the time, jealous eating and sleeping! He even took it into his head to be jealous of Kuzma last week.”\n\n“But he knew about the Pole before?”\n\n“Yes, but there it is. He has known about him from the very beginning, but to-day he suddenly got up and began scolding about him. I am ashamed to repeat what he said. Silly fellow! Rakitin went in as I came out. Perhaps Rakitin is egging him on. What do you think?” she added carelessly.\n\n“He loves you, that’s what it is: he loves you so much. And now he is particularly worried.”\n\n“I should think he might be, with the trial to-morrow. And I went to him to say something about to-morrow, for I dread to think what’s going to happen then. You say that he is worried, but how worried I am! And he talks about the Pole! He’s too silly! He is not jealous of Maximushka yet, anyway.”\n\n“My wife was dreadfully jealous over me, too,” Maximov put in his word.\n\n“Jealous of you?” Grushenka laughed in spite of herself. “Of whom could she have been jealous?”\n\n“Of the servant girls.”\n\n“Hold your tongue, Maximushka, I am in no laughing mood now; I feel angry. Don’t ogle the pies. I shan’t give you any; they are not good for you, and I won’t give you any vodka either. I have to look after him, too, just as though I kept an almshouse,” she laughed.\n\n“I don’t deserve your kindness. I am a worthless creature,” said Maximov, with tears in his voice. “You would do better to spend your kindness on people of more use than me.”\n\n“Ech, every one is of use, Maximushka, and how can we tell who’s of most use? If only that Pole didn’t exist, Alyosha. He’s taken it into his head to fall ill, too, to-day. I’ve been to see him also. And I shall send him some pies, too, on purpose. I hadn’t sent him any, but Mitya accused me of it, so now I shall send some! Ah, here’s Fenya with a letter! Yes, it’s from the Poles—begging again!”\n\nPan Mussyalovitch had indeed sent an extremely long and characteristically eloquent letter in which he begged her to lend him three roubles. In the letter was enclosed a receipt for the sum, with a promise to repay it within three months, signed by Pan Vrublevsky as well. Grushenka had received many such letters, accompanied by such receipts, from her former lover during the fortnight of her convalescence. But she knew that the two Poles had been to ask after her health during her illness. The first letter Grushenka got from them was a long one, written on large notepaper and with a big family crest on the seal. It was so obscure and rhetorical that Grushenka put it down before she had read half, unable to make head or tail of it. She could not attend to letters then. The first letter was followed next day by another in which Pan Mussyalovitch begged her for a loan of two thousand roubles for a very short period. Grushenka left that letter, too, unanswered. A whole series of letters had followed—one every day—all as pompous and rhetorical, but the loan asked for, gradually diminishing, dropped to a hundred roubles, then to twenty-five, to ten, and finally Grushenka received a letter in which both the Poles begged her for only one rouble and included a receipt signed by both.\n\nThen Grushenka suddenly felt sorry for them, and at dusk she went round herself to their lodging. She found the two Poles in great poverty, almost destitution, without food or fuel, without cigarettes, in debt to their landlady. The two hundred roubles they had carried off from Mitya at Mokroe had soon disappeared. But Grushenka was surprised at their meeting her with arrogant dignity and self-assertion, with the greatest punctilio and pompous speeches. Grushenka simply laughed, and gave her former admirer ten roubles. Then, laughing, she told Mitya of it and he was not in the least jealous. But ever since, the Poles had attached themselves to Grushenka and bombarded her daily with requests for money and she had always sent them small sums. And now that day Mitya had taken it into his head to be fearfully jealous.\n\n“Like a fool, I went round to him just for a minute, on the way to see Mitya, for he is ill, too, my Pole,” Grushenka began again with nervous haste. “I was laughing, telling Mitya about it. ‘Fancy,’ I said, ‘my Pole had the happy thought to sing his old songs to me to the guitar. He thought I would be touched and marry him!’ Mitya leapt up swearing.... So, there, I’ll send them the pies! Fenya, is it that little girl they’ve sent? Here, give her three roubles and pack a dozen pies up in a paper and tell her to take them. And you, Alyosha, be sure to tell Mitya that I did send them the pies.”\n\n“I wouldn’t tell him for anything,” said Alyosha, smiling.\n\n“Ech! You think he is unhappy about it. Why, he’s jealous on purpose. He doesn’t care,” said Grushenka bitterly.\n\n“On purpose?” queried Alyosha.\n\n“I tell you you are silly, Alyosha. You know nothing about it, with all your cleverness. I am not offended that he is jealous of a girl like me. I would be offended if he were not jealous. I am like that. I am not offended at jealousy. I have a fierce heart, too. I can be jealous myself. Only what offends me is that he doesn’t love me at all. I tell you he is jealous now on purpose. Am I blind? Don’t I see? He began talking to me just now of that woman, of Katerina, saying she was this and that, how she had ordered a doctor from Moscow for him, to try and save him; how she had ordered the best counsel, the most learned one, too. So he loves her, if he’ll praise her to my face, more shame to him! He’s treated me badly himself, so he attacked me, to make out I am in fault first and to throw it all on me. ‘You were with your Pole before me, so I can’t be blamed for Katerina,’ that’s what it amounts to. He wants to throw the whole blame on me. He attacked me on purpose, on purpose, I tell you, but I’ll—”\n\nGrushenka could not finish saying what she would do. She hid her eyes in her handkerchief and sobbed violently.\n\n“He doesn’t love Katerina Ivanovna,” said Alyosha firmly.\n\n“Well, whether he loves her or not, I’ll soon find out for myself,” said Grushenka, with a menacing note in her voice, taking the handkerchief from her eyes. Her face was distorted. Alyosha saw sorrowfully that from being mild and serene, it had become sullen and spiteful.\n\n“Enough of this foolishness,” she said suddenly; “it’s not for that I sent for you. Alyosha, darling, to-morrow—what will happen to-morrow? That’s what worries me! And it’s only me it worries! I look at every one and no one is thinking of it. No one cares about it. Are you thinking about it even? To-morrow he’ll be tried, you know. Tell me, how will he be tried? You know it’s the valet, the valet killed him! Good heavens! Can they condemn him in place of the valet and will no one stand up for him? They haven’t troubled the valet at all, have they?”\n\n“He’s been severely cross-examined,” observed Alyosha thoughtfully; “but every one came to the conclusion it was not he. Now he is lying very ill. He has been ill ever since that attack. Really ill,” added Alyosha.\n\n“Oh, dear! couldn’t you go to that counsel yourself and tell him the whole thing by yourself? He’s been brought from Petersburg for three thousand roubles, they say.”\n\n“We gave these three thousand together—Ivan, Katerina Ivanovna and I—but she paid two thousand for the doctor from Moscow herself. The counsel Fetyukovitch would have charged more, but the case has become known all over Russia; it’s talked of in all the papers and journals. Fetyukovitch agreed to come more for the glory of the thing, because the case has become so notorious. I saw him yesterday.”\n\n“Well? Did you talk to him?” Grushenka put in eagerly.\n\n“He listened and said nothing. He told me that he had already formed his opinion. But he promised to give my words consideration.”\n\n“Consideration! Ah, they are swindlers! They’ll ruin him. And why did she send for the doctor?”\n\n“As an expert. They want to prove that Mitya’s mad and committed the murder when he didn’t know what he was doing”; Alyosha smiled gently; “but Mitya won’t agree to that.”\n\n“Yes; but that would be the truth if he had killed him!” cried Grushenka. “He was mad then, perfectly mad, and that was my fault, wretch that I am! But, of course, he didn’t do it, he didn’t do it! And they are all against him, the whole town. Even Fenya’s evidence went to prove he had done it. And the people at the shop, and that official, and at the tavern, too, before, people had heard him say so! They are all, all against him, all crying out against him.”\n\n“Yes, there’s a fearful accumulation of evidence,” Alyosha observed grimly.\n\n“And Grigory—Grigory Vassilyevitch—sticks to his story that the door was open, persists that he saw it—there’s no shaking him. I went and talked to him myself. He’s rude about it, too.”\n\n“Yes, that’s perhaps the strongest evidence against him,” said Alyosha.\n\n“And as for Mitya’s being mad, he certainly seems like it now,” Grushenka began with a peculiarly anxious and mysterious air. “Do you know, Alyosha, I’ve been wanting to talk to you about it for a long time. I go to him every day and simply wonder at him. Tell me, now, what do you suppose he’s always talking about? He talks and talks and I can make nothing of it. I fancied he was talking of something intellectual that I couldn’t understand in my foolishness. Only he suddenly began talking to me about a babe—that is, about some child. ‘Why is the babe poor?’ he said. ‘It’s for that babe I am going to Siberia now. I am not a murderer, but I must go to Siberia!’ What that meant, what babe, I couldn’t tell for the life of me. Only I cried when he said it, because he said it so nicely. He cried himself, and I cried, too. He suddenly kissed me and made the sign of the cross over me. What did it mean, Alyosha, tell me? What is this babe?”\n\n“It must be Rakitin, who’s been going to see him lately,” smiled Alyosha, “though ... that’s not Rakitin’s doing. I didn’t see Mitya yesterday. I’ll see him to-day.”\n\n“No, it’s not Rakitin; it’s his brother Ivan Fyodorovitch upsetting him. It’s his going to see him, that’s what it is,” Grushenka began, and suddenly broke off. Alyosha gazed at her in amazement.\n\n“Ivan’s going? Has he been to see him? Mitya told me himself that Ivan hasn’t been once.”\n\n“There ... there! What a girl I am! Blurting things out!” exclaimed Grushenka, confused and suddenly blushing. “Stay, Alyosha, hush! Since I’ve said so much I’ll tell the whole truth—he’s been to see him twice, the first directly he arrived. He galloped here from Moscow at once, of course, before I was taken ill; and the second time was a week ago. He told Mitya not to tell you about it, under any circumstances; and not to tell any one, in fact. He came secretly.”\n\nAlyosha sat plunged in thought, considering something. The news evidently impressed him.\n\n“Ivan doesn’t talk to me of Mitya’s case,” he said slowly. “He’s said very little to me these last two months. And whenever I go to see him, he seems vexed at my coming, so I’ve not been to him for the last three weeks. H’m!... if he was there a week ago ... there certainly has been a change in Mitya this week.”\n\n“There has been a change,” Grushenka assented quickly. “They have a secret, they have a secret! Mitya told me himself there was a secret, and such a secret that Mitya can’t rest. Before then, he was cheerful—and, indeed, he is cheerful now—but when he shakes his head like that, you know, and strides about the room and keeps pulling at the hair on his right temple with his right hand, I know there is something on his mind worrying him.... I know! He was cheerful before, though, indeed, he is cheerful to-day.”\n\n“But you said he was worried.”\n\n“Yes, he is worried and yet cheerful. He keeps on being irritable for a minute and then cheerful and then irritable again. And you know, Alyosha, I am constantly wondering at him—with this awful thing hanging over him, he sometimes laughs at such trifles as though he were a baby himself.”\n\n“And did he really tell you not to tell me about Ivan? Did he say, ‘Don’t tell him’?”\n\n“Yes, he told me, ‘Don’t tell him.’ It’s you that Mitya’s most afraid of. Because it’s a secret: he said himself it was a secret. Alyosha, darling, go to him and find out what their secret is and come and tell me,” Grushenka besought him with sudden eagerness. “Set my mind at rest that I may know the worst that’s in store for me. That’s why I sent for you.”\n\n“You think it’s something to do with you? If it were, he wouldn’t have told you there was a secret.”\n\n“I don’t know. Perhaps he wants to tell me, but doesn’t dare to. He warns me. There is a secret, he tells me, but he won’t tell me what it is.”\n\n“What do you think yourself?”\n\n“What do I think? It’s the end for me, that’s what I think. They all three have been plotting my end, for Katerina’s in it. It’s all Katerina, it all comes from her. She is this and that, and that means that I am not. He tells me that beforehand—warns me. He is planning to throw me over, that’s the whole secret. They’ve planned it together, the three of them—Mitya, Katerina, and Ivan Fyodorovitch. Alyosha, I’ve been wanting to ask you a long time. A week ago he suddenly told me that Ivan was in love with Katerina, because he often goes to see her. Did he tell me the truth or not? Tell me, on your conscience, tell me the worst.”\n\n“I won’t tell you a lie. Ivan is not in love with Katerina Ivanovna, I think.”\n\n“Oh, that’s what I thought! He is lying to me, shameless deceiver, that’s what it is! And he was jealous of me just now, so as to put the blame on me afterwards. He is stupid, he can’t disguise what he is doing; he is so open, you know.... But I’ll give it to him, I’ll give it to him! ‘You believe I did it,’ he said. He said that to me, to me. He reproached me with that! God forgive him! You wait, I’ll make it hot for Katerina at the trial! I’ll just say a word then ... I’ll tell everything then!”\n\nAnd again she cried bitterly.\n\n“This I can tell you for certain, Grushenka,” Alyosha said, getting up. “First, that he loves you, loves you more than any one in the world, and you only, believe me. I know. I do know. The second thing is that I don’t want to worm his secret out of him, but if he’ll tell me of himself to-day, I shall tell him straight out that I have promised to tell you. Then I’ll come to you to-day, and tell you. Only ... I fancy ... Katerina Ivanovna has nothing to do with it, and that the secret is about something else. That’s certain. It isn’t likely it’s about Katerina Ivanovna, it seems to me. Good-by for now.”\n\nAlyosha shook hands with her. Grushenka was still crying. He saw that she put little faith in his consolation, but she was better for having had her sorrow out, for having spoken of it. He was sorry to leave her in such a state of mind, but he was in haste. He had a great many things to do still.\n\n# Chapter II. The Injured Foot\n\nThe first of these things was at the house of Madame Hohlakov, and he hurried there to get it over as quickly as possible and not be too late for Mitya. Madame Hohlakov had been slightly ailing for the last three weeks: her foot had for some reason swollen up, and though she was not in bed, she lay all day half-reclining on the couch in her boudoir, in a fascinating but decorous déshabillé. Alyosha had once noted with innocent amusement that, in spite of her illness, Madame Hohlakov had begun to be rather dressy—top-knots, ribbons, loose wrappers, had made their appearance, and he had an inkling of the reason, though he dismissed such ideas from his mind as frivolous. During the last two months the young official, Perhotin, had become a regular visitor at the house.\n\nAlyosha had not called for four days and he was in haste to go straight to Lise, as it was with her he had to speak, for Lise had sent a maid to him the previous day, specially asking him to come to her “about something very important,” a request which, for certain reasons, had interest for Alyosha. But while the maid went to take his name in to Lise, Madame Hohlakov heard of his arrival from some one, and immediately sent to beg him to come to her “just for one minute.” Alyosha reflected that it was better to accede to the mamma’s request, or else she would be sending down to Lise’s room every minute that he was there. Madame Hohlakov was lying on a couch. She was particularly smartly dressed and was evidently in a state of extreme nervous excitement. She greeted Alyosha with cries of rapture.\n\n“It’s ages, ages, perfect ages since I’ve seen you! It’s a whole week—only think of it! Ah, but you were here only four days ago, on Wednesday. You have come to see Lise. I’m sure you meant to slip into her room on tiptoe, without my hearing you. My dear, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, if you only knew how worried I am about her! But of that later, though that’s the most important thing, of that later. Dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, I trust you implicitly with my Lise. Since the death of Father Zossima—God rest his soul!” (she crossed herself)—“I look upon you as a monk, though you look charming in your new suit. Where did you find such a tailor in these parts? No, no, that’s not the chief thing—of that later. Forgive me for sometimes calling you Alyosha; an old woman like me may take liberties,” she smiled coquettishly; “but that will do later, too. The important thing is that I shouldn’t forget what is important. Please remind me of it yourself. As soon as my tongue runs away with me, you just say ‘the important thing?’ Ach! how do I know now what is of most importance? Ever since Lise took back her promise—her childish promise, Alexey Fyodorovitch—to marry you, you’ve realized, of course, that it was only the playful fancy of a sick child who had been so long confined to her chair—thank God, she can walk now!... that new doctor Katya sent for from Moscow for your unhappy brother, who will to-morrow—But why speak of to-morrow? I am ready to die at the very thought of to-morrow. Ready to die of curiosity.... That doctor was with us yesterday and saw Lise.... I paid him fifty roubles for the visit. But that’s not the point, that’s not the point again. You see, I’m mixing everything up. I am in such a hurry. Why am I in a hurry? I don’t understand. It’s awful how I seem growing unable to understand anything. Everything seems mixed up in a sort of tangle. I am afraid you are so bored you will jump up and run away, and that will be all I shall see of you. Goodness! Why are we sitting here and no coffee? Yulia, Glafira, coffee!”\n\nAlyosha made haste to thank her, and said that he had only just had coffee.\n\n“Where?”\n\n“At Agrafena Alexandrovna’s.”\n\n“At ... at that woman’s? Ah, it’s she has brought ruin on every one. I know nothing about it though. They say she has become a saint, though it’s rather late in the day. She had better have done it before. What use is it now? Hush, hush, Alexey Fyodorovitch, for I have so much to say to you that I am afraid I shall tell you nothing. This awful trial ... I shall certainly go, I am making arrangements. I shall be carried there in my chair; besides I can sit up. I shall have people with me. And, you know, I am a witness. How shall I speak, how shall I speak? I don’t know what I shall say. One has to take an oath, hasn’t one?”\n\n“Yes; but I don’t think you will be able to go.”\n\n“I can sit up. Ah, you put me out! Ah! this trial, this savage act, and then they are all going to Siberia, some are getting married, and all this so quickly, so quickly, everything’s changing, and at last—nothing. All grow old and have death to look forward to. Well, so be it! I am weary. This Katya, cette charmante personne, has disappointed all my hopes. Now she is going to follow one of your brothers to Siberia, and your other brother is going to follow her, and will live in the nearest town, and they will all torment one another. It drives me out of my mind. Worst of all—the publicity. The story has been told a million times over in all the papers in Moscow and Petersburg. Ah! yes, would you believe it, there’s a paragraph that I was ‘a dear friend’ of your brother’s ——, I can’t repeat the horrid word. Just fancy, just fancy!”\n\n“Impossible! Where was the paragraph? What did it say?”\n\n“I’ll show you directly. I got the paper and read it yesterday. Here, in the Petersburg paper Gossip. The paper began coming out this year. I am awfully fond of gossip, and I take it in, and now it pays me out—this is what gossip comes to! Here it is, here, this passage. Read it.”\n\nAnd she handed Alyosha a sheet of newspaper which had been under her pillow.\n\nIt was not exactly that she was upset, she seemed overwhelmed and perhaps everything really was mixed up in a tangle in her head. The paragraph was very typical, and must have been a great shock to her, but, fortunately perhaps, she was unable to keep her mind fixed on any one subject at that moment, and so might race off in a minute to something else and quite forget the newspaper.\n\nAlyosha was well aware that the story of the terrible case had spread all over Russia. And, good heavens! what wild rumors about his brother, about the Karamazovs, and about himself he had read in the course of those two months, among other equally credible items! One paper had even stated that he had gone into a monastery and become a monk, in horror at his brother’s crime. Another contradicted this, and stated that he and his elder, Father Zossima, had broken into the monastery chest and “made tracks from the monastery.” The present paragraph in the paper Gossip was under the heading, “The Karamazov Case at Skotoprigonyevsk.” (That, alas! was the name of our little town. I had hitherto kept it concealed.) It was brief, and Madame Hohlakov was not directly mentioned in it. No names appeared, in fact. It was merely stated that the criminal, whose approaching trial was making such a sensation—retired army captain, an idle swaggerer, and reactionary bully—was continually involved in amorous intrigues, and particularly popular with certain ladies “who were pining in solitude.” One such lady, a pining widow, who tried to seem young though she had a grown-up daughter, was so fascinated by him that only two hours before the crime she offered him three thousand roubles, on condition that he would elope with her to the gold mines. But the criminal, counting on escaping punishment, had preferred to murder his father to get the three thousand rather than go off to Siberia with the middle-aged charms of his pining lady. This playful paragraph finished, of course, with an outburst of generous indignation at the wickedness of parricide and at the lately abolished institution of serfdom. Reading it with curiosity, Alyosha folded up the paper and handed it back to Madame Hohlakov.\n\n“Well, that must be me,” she hurried on again. “Of course I am meant. Scarcely more than an hour before, I suggested gold mines to him, and here they talk of ‘middle-aged charms’ as though that were my motive! He writes that out of spite! God Almighty forgive him for the middle-aged charms, as I forgive him! You know it’s— Do you know who it is? It’s your friend Rakitin.”\n\n“Perhaps,” said Alyosha, “though I’ve heard nothing about it.”\n\n“It’s he, it’s he! No ‘perhaps’ about it. You know I turned him out of the house.... You know all that story, don’t you?”\n\n“I know that you asked him not to visit you for the future, but why it was, I haven’t heard ... from you, at least.”\n\n“Ah, then you’ve heard it from him! He abuses me, I suppose, abuses me dreadfully?”\n\n“Yes, he does; but then he abuses every one. But why you’ve given him up I haven’t heard from him either. I meet him very seldom now, indeed. We are not friends.”\n\n“Well, then, I’ll tell you all about it. There’s no help for it, I’ll confess, for there is one point in which I was perhaps to blame. Only a little, little point, so little that perhaps it doesn’t count. You see, my dear boy”—Madame Hohlakov suddenly looked arch and a charming, though enigmatic, smile played about her lips—“you see, I suspect ... You must forgive me, Alyosha. I am like a mother to you.... No, no; quite the contrary. I speak to you now as though you were my father—mother’s quite out of place. Well, it’s as though I were confessing to Father Zossima, that’s just it. I called you a monk just now. Well, that poor young man, your friend, Rakitin (Mercy on us! I can’t be angry with him. I feel cross, but not very), that frivolous young man, would you believe it, seems to have taken it into his head to fall in love with me. I only noticed it later. At first—a month ago—he only began to come oftener to see me, almost every day; though, of course, we were acquainted before. I knew nothing about it ... and suddenly it dawned upon me, and I began to notice things with surprise. You know, two months ago, that modest, charming, excellent young man, Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, who’s in the service here, began to be a regular visitor at the house. You met him here ever so many times yourself. And he is an excellent, earnest young man, isn’t he? He comes once every three days, not every day (though I should be glad to see him every day), and always so well dressed. Altogether, I love young people, Alyosha, talented, modest, like you, and he has almost the mind of a statesman, he talks so charmingly, and I shall certainly, certainly try and get promotion for him. He is a future diplomat. On that awful day he almost saved me from death by coming in the night. And your friend Rakitin comes in such boots, and always stretches them out on the carpet.... He began hinting at his feelings, in fact, and one day, as he was going, he squeezed my hand terribly hard. My foot began to swell directly after he pressed my hand like that. He had met Pyotr Ilyitch here before, and would you believe it, he is always gibing at him, growling at him, for some reason. I simply looked at the way they went on together and laughed inwardly. So I was sitting here alone—no, I was laid up then. Well, I was lying here alone and suddenly Rakitin comes in, and only fancy! brought me some verses of his own composition—a short poem, on my bad foot: that is, he described my foot in a poem. Wait a minute—how did it go?\n\nA captivating little foot.\n\nIt began somehow like that. I can never remember poetry. I’ve got it here. I’ll show it to you later. But it’s a charming thing—charming; and, you know, it’s not only about the foot, it had a good moral, too, a charming idea, only I’ve forgotten it; in fact, it was just the thing for an album. So, of course, I thanked him, and he was evidently flattered. I’d hardly had time to thank him when in comes Pyotr Ilyitch, and Rakitin suddenly looked as black as night. I could see that Pyotr Ilyitch was in the way, for Rakitin certainly wanted to say something after giving me the verses. I had a presentiment of it; but Pyotr Ilyitch came in. I showed Pyotr Ilyitch the verses and didn’t say who was the author. But I am convinced that he guessed, though he won’t own it to this day, and declares he had no idea. But he says that on purpose. Pyotr Ilyitch began to laugh at once, and fell to criticizing it. ‘Wretched doggerel,’ he said they were, ‘some divinity student must have written them,’ and with such vehemence, such vehemence! Then, instead of laughing, your friend flew into a rage. ‘Good gracious!’ I thought, ‘they’ll fly at each other.’ ‘It was I who wrote them,’ said he. ‘I wrote them as a joke,’ he said, ‘for I think it degrading to write verses.... But they are good poetry. They want to put a monument to your Pushkin for writing about women’s feet, while I wrote with a moral purpose, and you,’ said he, ‘are an advocate of serfdom. You’ve no humane ideas,’ said he. ‘You have no modern enlightened feelings, you are uninfluenced by progress, you are a mere official,’ he said, ‘and you take bribes.’ Then I began screaming and imploring them. And, you know, Pyotr Ilyitch is anything but a coward. He at once took up the most gentlemanly tone, looked at him sarcastically, listened, and apologized. ‘I’d no idea,’ said he. ‘I shouldn’t have said it, if I had known. I should have praised it. Poets are all so irritable,’ he said. In short, he laughed at him under cover of the most gentlemanly tone. He explained to me afterwards that it was all sarcastic. I thought he was in earnest. Only as I lay there, just as before you now, I thought, ‘Would it, or would it not, be the proper thing for me to turn Rakitin out for shouting so rudely at a visitor in my house?’ And, would you believe it, I lay here, shut my eyes, and wondered, would it be the proper thing or not. I kept worrying and worrying, and my heart began to beat, and I couldn’t make up my mind whether to make an outcry or not. One voice seemed to be telling me, ‘Speak,’ and the other ‘No, don’t speak.’ And no sooner had the second voice said that than I cried out, and fainted. Of course, there was a fuss. I got up suddenly and said to Rakitin, ‘It’s painful for me to say it, but I don’t wish to see you in my house again.’ So I turned him out. Ah! Alexey Fyodorovitch, I know myself I did wrong. I was putting it on. I wasn’t angry with him at all, really; but I suddenly fancied—that was what did it—that it would be such a fine scene.... And yet, believe me, it was quite natural, for I really shed tears and cried for several days afterwards, and then suddenly, one afternoon, I forgot all about it. So it’s a fortnight since he’s been here, and I kept wondering whether he would come again. I wondered even yesterday, then suddenly last night came this Gossip. I read it and gasped. Who could have written it? He must have written it. He went home, sat down, wrote it on the spot, sent it, and they put it in. It was a fortnight ago, you see. But, Alyosha, it’s awful how I keep talking and don’t say what I want to say. Ah! the words come of themselves!”\n\n“It’s very important for me to be in time to see my brother to-day,” Alyosha faltered.\n\n“To be sure, to be sure! You bring it all back to me. Listen, what is an aberration?”\n\n“What aberration?” asked Alyosha, wondering.\n\n“In the legal sense. An aberration in which everything is pardonable. Whatever you do, you will be acquitted at once.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“I’ll tell you. This Katya ... Ah! she is a charming, charming creature, only I never can make out who it is she is in love with. She was with me some time ago and I couldn’t get anything out of her. Especially as she won’t talk to me except on the surface now. She is always talking about my health and nothing else, and she takes up such a tone with me, too. I simply said to myself, ‘Well, so be it. I don’t care’... Oh, yes. I was talking of aberration. This doctor has come. You know a doctor has come? Of course, you know it—the one who discovers madmen. You wrote for him. No, it wasn’t you, but Katya. It’s all Katya’s doing. Well, you see, a man may be sitting perfectly sane and suddenly have an aberration. He may be conscious and know what he is doing and yet be in a state of aberration. And there’s no doubt that Dmitri Fyodorovitch was suffering from aberration. They found out about aberration as soon as the law courts were reformed. It’s all the good effect of the reformed law courts. The doctor has been here and questioned me about that evening, about the gold mines. ‘How did he seem then?’ he asked me. He must have been in a state of aberration. He came in shouting, ‘Money, money, three thousand! Give me three thousand!’ and then went away and immediately did the murder. ‘I don’t want to murder him,’ he said, and he suddenly went and murdered him. That’s why they’ll acquit him, because he struggled against it and yet he murdered him.”\n\n“But he didn’t murder him,” Alyosha interrupted rather sharply. He felt more and more sick with anxiety and impatience.\n\n“Yes, I know it was that old man Grigory murdered him.”\n\n“Grigory?” cried Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, yes; it was Grigory. He lay as Dmitri Fyodorovitch struck him down, and then got up, saw the door open, went in and killed Fyodor Pavlovitch.”\n\n“But why, why?”\n\n“Suffering from aberration. When he recovered from the blow Dmitri Fyodorovitch gave him on the head, he was suffering from aberration; he went and committed the murder. As for his saying he didn’t, he very likely doesn’t remember. Only, you know, it’ll be better, ever so much better, if Dmitri Fyodorovitch murdered him. And that’s how it must have been, though I say it was Grigory. It certainly was Dmitri Fyodorovitch, and that’s better, ever so much better! Oh! not better that a son should have killed his father, I don’t defend that. Children ought to honor their parents, and yet it would be better if it were he, as you’d have nothing to cry over then, for he did it when he was unconscious or rather when he was conscious, but did not know what he was doing. Let them acquit him—that’s so humane, and would show what a blessing reformed law courts are. I knew nothing about it, but they say they have been so a long time. And when I heard it yesterday, I was so struck by it that I wanted to send for you at once. And if he is acquitted, make him come straight from the law courts to dinner with me, and I’ll have a party of friends, and we’ll drink to the reformed law courts. I don’t believe he’d be dangerous; besides, I’ll invite a great many friends, so that he could always be led out if he did anything. And then he might be made a justice of the peace or something in another town, for those who have been in trouble themselves make the best judges. And, besides, who isn’t suffering from aberration nowadays?—you, I, all of us are in a state of aberration, and there are ever so many examples of it: a man sits singing a song, suddenly something annoys him, he takes a pistol and shoots the first person he comes across, and no one blames him for it. I read that lately, and all the doctors confirm it. The doctors are always confirming; they confirm anything. Why, my Lise is in a state of aberration. She made me cry again yesterday, and the day before, too, and to-day I suddenly realized that it’s all due to aberration. Oh, Lise grieves me so! I believe she’s quite mad. Why did she send for you? Did she send for you or did you come of yourself?”\n\n“Yes, she sent for me, and I am just going to her.” Alyosha got up resolutely.\n\n“Oh, my dear, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, perhaps that’s what’s most important,” Madame Hohlakov cried, suddenly bursting into tears. “God knows I trust Lise to you with all my heart, and it’s no matter her sending for you on the sly, without telling her mother. But forgive me, I can’t trust my daughter so easily to your brother Ivan Fyodorovitch, though I still consider him the most chivalrous young man. But only fancy, he’s been to see Lise and I knew nothing about it!”\n\n“How? What? When?” Alyosha was exceedingly surprised. He had not sat down again and listened standing.\n\n“I will tell you; that’s perhaps why I asked you to come, for I don’t know now why I did ask you to come. Well, Ivan Fyodorovitch has been to see me twice, since he came back from Moscow. First time he came as a friend to call on me, and the second time Katya was here and he came because he heard she was here. I didn’t, of course, expect him to come often, knowing what a lot he has to do as it is, vous comprenez, cette affaire et la mort terrible de votre papa. But I suddenly heard he’d been here again, not to see me but to see Lise. That’s six days ago now. He came, stayed five minutes, and went away. And I didn’t hear of it till three days afterwards, from Glafira, so it was a great shock to me. I sent for Lise directly. She laughed. ‘He thought you were asleep,’ she said, ‘and came in to me to ask after your health.’ Of course, that’s how it happened. But Lise, Lise, mercy on us, how she distresses me! Would you believe it, one night, four days ago, just after you saw her last time, and had gone away, she suddenly had a fit, screaming, shrieking, hysterics! Why is it I never have hysterics? Then, next day another fit, and the same thing on the third, and yesterday too, and then yesterday that aberration. She suddenly screamed out, ‘I hate Ivan Fyodorovitch. I insist on your never letting him come to the house again.’ I was struck dumb at these amazing words, and answered, ‘On what grounds could I refuse to see such an excellent young man, a young man of such learning too, and so unfortunate?’—for all this business is a misfortune, isn’t it? She suddenly burst out laughing at my words, and so rudely, you know. Well, I was pleased; I thought I had amused her and the fits would pass off, especially as I wanted to refuse to see Ivan Fyodorovitch anyway on account of his strange visits without my knowledge, and meant to ask him for an explanation. But early this morning Lise waked up and flew into a passion with Yulia and, would you believe it, slapped her in the face. That’s monstrous; I am always polite to my servants. And an hour later she was hugging Yulia’s feet and kissing them. She sent a message to me that she wasn’t coming to me at all, and would never come and see me again, and when I dragged myself down to her, she rushed to kiss me, crying, and as she kissed me, she pushed me out of the room without saying a word, so I couldn’t find out what was the matter. Now, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, I rest all my hopes on you, and, of course, my whole life is in your hands. I simply beg you to go to Lise and find out everything from her, as you alone can, and come back and tell me—me, her mother, for you understand it will be the death of me, simply the death of me, if this goes on, or else I shall run away. I can stand no more. I have patience; but I may lose patience, and then ... then something awful will happen. Ah, dear me! At last, Pyotr Ilyitch!” cried Madame Hohlakov, beaming all over as she saw Perhotin enter the room. “You are late, you are late! Well, sit down, speak, put us out of suspense. What does the counsel say. Where are you off to, Alexey Fyodorovitch?”\n\n“To Lise.”\n\n“Oh, yes. You won’t forget, you won’t forget what I asked you? It’s a question of life and death!”\n\n“Of course, I won’t forget, if I can ... but I am so late,” muttered Alyosha, beating a hasty retreat.\n\n“No, be sure, be sure to come in; don’t say ‘If you can.’ I shall die if you don’t,” Madame Hohlakov called after him, but Alyosha had already left the room.\n\n# Chapter III. A Little Demon\n\nGoing in to Lise, he found her half reclining in the invalid-chair, in which she had been wheeled when she was unable to walk. She did not move to meet him, but her sharp, keen eyes were simply riveted on his face. There was a feverish look in her eyes, her face was pale and yellow. Alyosha was amazed at the change that had taken place in her in three days. She was positively thinner. She did not hold out her hand to him. He touched the thin, long fingers which lay motionless on her dress, then he sat down facing her, without a word.\n\n“I know you are in a hurry to get to the prison,” Lise said curtly, “and mamma’s kept you there for hours; she’s just been telling you about me and Yulia.”\n\n“How do you know?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“I’ve been listening. Why do you stare at me? I want to listen and I do listen, there’s no harm in that. I don’t apologize.”\n\n“You are upset about something?”\n\n“On the contrary, I am very happy. I’ve only just been reflecting for the thirtieth time what a good thing it is I refused you and shall not be your wife. You are not fit to be a husband. If I were to marry you and give you a note to take to the man I loved after you, you’d take it and be sure to give it to him and bring an answer back, too. If you were forty, you would still go on taking my love-letters for me.”\n\nShe suddenly laughed.\n\n“There is something spiteful and yet open-hearted about you,” Alyosha smiled to her.\n\n“The open-heartedness consists in my not being ashamed of myself with you. What’s more, I don’t want to feel ashamed with you, just with you. Alyosha, why is it I don’t respect you? I am very fond of you, but I don’t respect you. If I respected you, I shouldn’t talk to you without shame, should I?”\n\n“No.”\n“But do you believe that I am not ashamed with you?”\n\n“No, I don’t believe it.”\n\nLise laughed nervously again; she spoke rapidly.\n\n“I sent your brother, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, some sweets in prison. Alyosha, you know, you are quite pretty! I shall love you awfully for having so quickly allowed me not to love you.”\n\n“Why did you send for me to-day, Lise?”\n\n“I wanted to tell you of a longing I have. I should like some one to torture me, marry me and then torture me, deceive me and go away. I don’t want to be happy.”\n\n“You are in love with disorder?”\n\n“Yes, I want disorder. I keep wanting to set fire to the house. I keep imagining how I’ll creep up and set fire to the house on the sly; it must be on the sly. They’ll try to put it out, but it’ll go on burning. And I shall know and say nothing. Ah, what silliness! And how bored I am!”\n\nShe waved her hand with a look of repulsion.\n\n“It’s your luxurious life,” said Alyosha, softly.\n\n“Is it better, then, to be poor?”\n\n“Yes, it is better.”\n\n“That’s what your monk taught you. That’s not true. Let me be rich and all the rest poor, I’ll eat sweets and drink cream and not give any to any one else. Ach, don’t speak, don’t say anything,” she shook her hand at him, though Alyosha had not opened his mouth. “You’ve told me all that before, I know it all by heart. It bores me. If I am ever poor, I shall murder somebody, and even if I am rich, I may murder some one, perhaps—why do nothing! But do you know, I should like to reap, cut the rye? I’ll marry you, and you shall become a peasant, a real peasant; we’ll keep a colt, shall we? Do you know Kalganov?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“He is always wandering about, dreaming. He says, ‘Why live in real life? It’s better to dream. One can dream the most delightful things, but real life is a bore.’ But he’ll be married soon for all that; he’s been making love to me already. Can you spin tops?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, he’s just like a top: he wants to be wound up and set spinning and then to be lashed, lashed, lashed with a whip. If I marry him, I’ll keep him spinning all his life. You are not ashamed to be with me?”\n\n“No.”\n“You are awfully cross, because I don’t talk about holy things. I don’t want to be holy. What will they do to one in the next world for the greatest sin? You must know all about that.”\n\n“God will censure you.” Alyosha was watching her steadily.\n\n“That’s just what I should like. I would go up and they would censure me, and I would burst out laughing in their faces. I should dreadfully like to set fire to the house, Alyosha, to our house; you still don’t believe me?”\n\n“Why? There are children of twelve years old, who have a longing to set fire to something and they do set things on fire, too. It’s a sort of disease.”\n\n“That’s not true, that’s not true; there may be children, but that’s not what I mean.”\n\n“You take evil for good; it’s a passing crisis, it’s the result of your illness, perhaps.”\n\n“You do despise me, though! It’s simply that I don’t want to do good, I want to do evil, and it has nothing to do with illness.”\n\n“Why do evil?”\n\n“So that everything might be destroyed. Ah, how nice it would be if everything were destroyed! You know, Alyosha, I sometimes think of doing a fearful lot of harm and everything bad, and I should do it for a long while on the sly and suddenly every one would find it out. Every one will stand round and point their fingers at me and I would look at them all. That would be awfully nice. Why would it be so nice, Alyosha?”\n\n“I don’t know. It’s a craving to destroy something good or, as you say, to set fire to something. It happens sometimes.”\n\n“I not only say it, I shall do it.”\n“I believe you.”\n\n“Ah, how I love you for saying you believe me. And you are not lying one little bit. But perhaps you think that I am saying all this on purpose to annoy you?”\n\n“No, I don’t think that ... though perhaps there is a little desire to do that in it, too.”\n\n“There is a little. I never can tell lies to you,” she declared, with a strange fire in her eyes.\n\nWhat struck Alyosha above everything was her earnestness. There was not a trace of humor or jesting in her face now, though, in old days, fun and gayety never deserted her even at her most “earnest” moments.\n\n“There are moments when people love crime,” said Alyosha thoughtfully.\n\n“Yes, yes! You have uttered my thought; they love crime, every one loves crime, they love it always, not at some ‘moments.’ You know, it’s as though people have made an agreement to lie about it and have lied about it ever since. They all declare that they hate evil, but secretly they all love it.”\n\n“And are you still reading nasty books?”\n\n“Yes, I am. Mamma reads them and hides them under her pillow and I steal them.”\n\n“Aren’t you ashamed to destroy yourself?”\n\n“I want to destroy myself. There’s a boy here, who lay down between the railway lines when the train was passing. Lucky fellow! Listen, your brother is being tried now for murdering his father and every one loves his having killed his father.”\n\n“Loves his having killed his father?”\n\n“Yes, loves it; every one loves it! Everybody says it’s so awful, but secretly they simply love it. I for one love it.”\n\n“There is some truth in what you say about every one,” said Alyosha softly.\n\n“Oh, what ideas you have!” Lise shrieked in delight. “And you a monk, too! You wouldn’t believe how I respect you, Alyosha, for never telling lies. Oh, I must tell you a funny dream of mine. I sometimes dream of devils. It’s night; I am in my room with a candle and suddenly there are devils all over the place, in all the corners, under the table, and they open the doors; there’s a crowd of them behind the doors and they want to come and seize me. And they are just coming, just seizing me. But I suddenly cross myself and they all draw back, though they don’t go away altogether, they stand at the doors and in the corners, waiting. And suddenly I have a frightful longing to revile God aloud, and so I begin, and then they come crowding back to me, delighted, and seize me again and I cross myself again and they all draw back. It’s awful fun. it takes one’s breath away.”\n\n“I’ve had the same dream, too,” said Alyosha suddenly.\n\n“Really?” cried Lise, surprised. “I say, Alyosha, don’t laugh, that’s awfully important. Could two different people have the same dream?”\n\n“It seems they can.”\n\n“Alyosha, I tell you, it’s awfully important,” Lise went on, with really excessive amazement. “It’s not the dream that’s important, but your having the same dream as me. You never lie to me, don’t lie now: is it true? You are not laughing?”\n\n“It’s true.”\n\nLise seemed extraordinarily impressed and for half a minute she was silent.\n\n“Alyosha, come and see me, come and see me more often,” she said suddenly, in a supplicating voice.\n\n“I’ll always come to see you, all my life,” answered Alyosha firmly.\n\n“You are the only person I can talk to, you know,” Lise began again. “I talk to no one but myself and you. Only you in the whole world. And to you more readily than to myself. And I am not a bit ashamed with you, not a bit. Alyosha, why am I not ashamed with you, not a bit? Alyosha, is it true that at Easter the Jews steal a child and kill it?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“There’s a book here in which I read about the trial of a Jew, who took a child of four years old and cut off the fingers from both hands, and then crucified him on the wall, hammered nails into him and crucified him, and afterwards, when he was tried, he said that the child died soon, within four hours. That was ‘soon’! He said the child moaned, kept on moaning and he stood admiring it. That’s nice!”\n\n“Nice?”\n“Nice; I sometimes imagine that it was I who crucified him. He would hang there moaning and I would sit opposite him eating pineapple compote. I am awfully fond of pineapple compote. Do you like it?”\n\nAlyosha looked at her in silence. Her pale, sallow face was suddenly contorted, her eyes burned.\n\n“You know, when I read about that Jew I shook with sobs all night. I kept fancying how the little thing cried and moaned (a child of four years old understands, you know), and all the while the thought of pineapple compote haunted me. In the morning I wrote a letter to a certain person, begging him particularly to come and see me. He came and I suddenly told him all about the child and the pineapple compote. All about it, all, and said that it was nice. He laughed and said it really was nice. Then he got up and went away. He was only here five minutes. Did he despise me? Did he despise me? Tell me, tell me, Alyosha, did he despise me or not?” She sat up on the couch, with flashing eyes.\n\n“Tell me,” Alyosha asked anxiously, “did you send for that person?”\n\n“Yes, I did.”\n\n“Did you send him a letter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Simply to ask about that, about that child?”\n\n“No, not about that at all. But when he came, I asked him about that at once. He answered, laughed, got up and went away.”\n\n“That person behaved honorably,” Alyosha murmured.\n\n“And did he despise me? Did he laugh at me?”\n\n“No, for perhaps he believes in the pineapple compote himself. He is very ill now, too, Lise.”\n\n“Yes, he does believe in it,” said Lise, with flashing eyes.\n\n“He doesn’t despise any one,” Alyosha went on. “Only he does not believe any one. If he doesn’t believe in people, of course, he does despise them.”\n\n“Then he despises me, me?”\n\n“You, too.”\n\n“Good,” Lise seemed to grind her teeth. “When he went out laughing, I felt that it was nice to be despised. The child with fingers cut off is nice, and to be despised is nice....”\n\nAnd she laughed in Alyosha’s face, a feverish malicious laugh.\n\n“Do you know, Alyosha, do you know, I should like—Alyosha, save me!” She suddenly jumped from the couch, rushed to him and seized him with both hands. “Save me!” she almost groaned. “Is there any one in the world I could tell what I’ve told you? I’ve told you the truth, the truth. I shall kill myself, because I loathe everything! I don’t want to live, because I loathe everything! I loathe everything, everything. Alyosha, why don’t you love me in the least?” she finished in a frenzy.\n\n“But I do love you!” answered Alyosha warmly.\n\n“And will you weep over me, will you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Not because I won’t be your wife, but simply weep for me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Thank you! It’s only your tears I want. Every one else may punish me and trample me under foot, every one, every one, not excepting any one. For I don’t love any one. Do you hear, not any one! On the contrary, I hate him! Go, Alyosha; it’s time you went to your brother”; she tore herself away from him suddenly.\n\n“How can I leave you like this?” said Alyosha, almost in alarm.\n\n“Go to your brother, the prison will be shut; go, here’s your hat. Give my love to Mitya, go, go!”\n\nAnd she almost forcibly pushed Alyosha out of the door. He looked at her with pained surprise, when he was suddenly aware of a letter in his right hand, a tiny letter folded up tight and sealed. He glanced at it and instantly read the address, “To Ivan Fyodorovitch Karamazov.” He looked quickly at Lise. Her face had become almost menacing.\n\n“Give it to him, you must give it to him!” she ordered him, trembling and beside herself. “To-day, at once, or I’ll poison myself! That’s why I sent for you.”\n\nAnd she slammed the door quickly. The bolt clicked. Alyosha put the note in his pocket and went straight downstairs, without going back to Madame Hohlakov; forgetting her, in fact. As soon as Alyosha had gone, Lise unbolted the door, opened it a little, put her finger in the crack and slammed the door with all her might, pinching her finger. Ten seconds after, releasing her finger, she walked softly, slowly to her chair, sat up straight in it and looked intently at her blackened finger and at the blood that oozed from under the nail. Her lips were quivering and she kept whispering rapidly to herself:\n\n“I am a wretch, wretch, wretch, wretch!”\n\n# Chapter IV. A Hymn And A Secret\n\nIt was quite late (days are short in November) when Alyosha rang at the prison gate. It was beginning to get dusk. But Alyosha knew that he would be admitted without difficulty. Things were managed in our little town, as everywhere else. At first, of course, on the conclusion of the preliminary inquiry, relations and a few other persons could only obtain interviews with Mitya by going through certain inevitable formalities. But later, though the formalities were not relaxed, exceptions were made for some, at least, of Mitya’s visitors. So much so, that sometimes the interviews with the prisoner in the room set aside for the purpose were practically tête-à-tête.\n\nThese exceptions, however, were few in number; only Grushenka, Alyosha and Rakitin were treated like this. But the captain of the police, Mihail Mihailovitch, was very favorably disposed to Grushenka. His abuse of her at Mokroe weighed on the old man’s conscience, and when he learned the whole story, he completely changed his view of her. And strange to say, though he was firmly persuaded of his guilt, yet after Mitya was once in prison, the old man came to take a more and more lenient view of him. “He was a man of good heart, perhaps,” he thought, “who had come to grief from drinking and dissipation.” His first horror had been succeeded by pity. As for Alyosha, the police captain was very fond of him and had known him for a long time. Rakitin, who had of late taken to coming very often to see the prisoner, was one of the most intimate acquaintances of the “police captain’s young ladies,” as he called them, and was always hanging about their house. He gave lessons in the house of the prison superintendent, too, who, though scrupulous in the performance of his duties, was a kind-hearted old man. Alyosha, again, had an intimate acquaintance of long standing with the superintendent, who was fond of talking to him, generally on sacred subjects. He respected Ivan Fyodorovitch, and stood in awe of his opinion, though he was a great philosopher himself; “self-taught,” of course. But Alyosha had an irresistible attraction for him. During the last year the old man had taken to studying the Apocryphal Gospels, and constantly talked over his impressions with his young friend. He used to come and see him in the monastery and discussed for hours together with him and with the monks. So even if Alyosha were late at the prison, he had only to go to the superintendent and everything was made easy. Besides, every one in the prison, down to the humblest warder, had grown used to Alyosha. The sentry, of course, did not trouble him so long as the authorities were satisfied.\n\nWhen Mitya was summoned from his cell, he always went downstairs, to the place set aside for interviews. As Alyosha entered the room he came upon Rakitin, who was just taking leave of Mitya. They were both talking loudly. Mitya was laughing heartily as he saw him out, while Rakitin seemed grumbling. Rakitin did not like meeting Alyosha, especially of late. He scarcely spoke to him, and bowed to him stiffly. Seeing Alyosha enter now, he frowned and looked away, as though he were entirely absorbed in buttoning his big, warm, fur-trimmed overcoat. Then he began looking at once for his umbrella.\n\n“I must mind not to forget my belongings,” he muttered, simply to say something.\n\n“Mind you don’t forget other people’s belongings,” said Mitya, as a joke, and laughed at once at his own wit. Rakitin fired up instantly.\n\n“You’d better give that advice to your own family, who’ve always been a slave-driving lot, and not to Rakitin,” he cried, suddenly trembling with anger.\n\n“What’s the matter? I was joking,” cried Mitya. “Damn it all! They are all like that,” he turned to Alyosha, nodding towards Rakitin’s hurriedly retreating figure. “He was sitting here, laughing and cheerful, and all at once he boils up like that. He didn’t even nod to you. Have you broken with him completely? Why are you so late? I’ve not been simply waiting, but thirsting for you the whole morning. But never mind. We’ll make up for it now.”\n\n“Why does he come here so often? Surely you are not such great friends?” asked Alyosha. He, too, nodded at the door through which Rakitin had disappeared.\n\n“Great friends with Rakitin? No, not as much as that. Is it likely—a pig like that? He considers I am ... a blackguard. They can’t understand a joke either, that’s the worst of such people. They never understand a joke, and their souls are dry, dry and flat; they remind me of prison walls when I was first brought here. But he is a clever fellow, very clever. Well, Alexey, it’s all over with me now.”\n\nHe sat down on the bench and made Alyosha sit down beside him.\n\n“Yes, the trial’s to-morrow. Are you so hopeless, brother?” Alyosha said, with an apprehensive feeling.\n\n“What are you talking about?” said Mitya, looking at him rather uncertainly. “Oh, you mean the trial! Damn it all! Till now we’ve been talking of things that don’t matter, about this trial, but I haven’t said a word to you about the chief thing. Yes, the trial is to-morrow; but it wasn’t the trial I meant, when I said it was all over with me. Why do you look at me so critically?”\n\n“What do you mean, Mitya?”\n\n“Ideas, ideas, that’s all! Ethics! What is ethics?”\n\n“Ethics?” asked Alyosha, wondering.\n\n“Yes; is it a science?”\n\n“Yes, there is such a science ... but ... I confess I can’t explain to you what sort of science it is.”\n\n“Rakitin knows. Rakitin knows a lot, damn him! He’s not going to be a monk. He means to go to Petersburg. There he’ll go in for criticism of an elevating tendency. Who knows, he may be of use and make his own career, too. Ough! they are first-rate, these people, at making a career! Damn ethics, I am done for, Alexey, I am, you man of God! I love you more than any one. It makes my heart yearn to look at you. Who was Karl Bernard?”\n\n“Karl Bernard?” Alyosha was surprised again.\n\n“No, not Karl. Stay, I made a mistake. Claude Bernard. What was he? Chemist or what?”\n\n“He must be a savant,” answered Alyosha; “but I confess I can’t tell you much about him, either. I’ve heard of him as a savant, but what sort I don’t know.”\n\n“Well, damn him, then! I don’t know either,” swore Mitya. “A scoundrel of some sort, most likely. They are all scoundrels. And Rakitin will make his way. Rakitin will get on anywhere; he is another Bernard. Ugh, these Bernards! They are all over the place.”\n\n“But what is the matter?” Alyosha asked insistently.\n\n“He wants to write an article about me, about my case, and so begin his literary career. That’s what he comes for; he said so himself. He wants to prove some theory. He wants to say ‘he couldn’t help murdering his father, he was corrupted by his environment,’ and so on. He explained it all to me. He is going to put in a tinge of Socialism, he says. But there, damn the fellow, he can put in a tinge if he likes, I don’t care. He can’t bear Ivan, he hates him. He’s not fond of you, either. But I don’t turn him out, for he is a clever fellow. Awfully conceited, though. I said to him just now, ‘The Karamazovs are not blackguards, but philosophers; for all true Russians are philosophers, and though you’ve studied, you are not a philosopher—you are a low fellow.’ He laughed, so maliciously. And I said to him, ‘De ideabus non est disputandum.’ Isn’t that rather good? I can set up for being a classic, you see!” Mitya laughed suddenly.\n\n“Why is it all over with you? You said so just now,” Alyosha interposed.\n\n“Why is it all over with me? H’m!... The fact of it is ... if you take it as a whole, I am sorry to lose God—that’s why it is.”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘sorry to lose God’?”\n\n“Imagine: inside, in the nerves, in the head—that is, these nerves are there in the brain ... (damn them!) there are sort of little tails, the little tails of those nerves, and as soon as they begin quivering ... that is, you see, I look at something with my eyes and then they begin quivering, those little tails ... and when they quiver, then an image appears ... it doesn’t appear at once, but an instant, a second, passes ... and then something like a moment appears; that is, not a moment—devil take the moment!—but an image; that is, an object, or an action, damn it! That’s why I see and then think, because of those tails, not at all because I’ve got a soul, and that I am some sort of image and likeness. All that is nonsense! Rakitin explained it all to me yesterday, brother, and it simply bowled me over. It’s magnificent, Alyosha, this science! A new man’s arising—that I understand.... And yet I am sorry to lose God!”\n\n“Well, that’s a good thing, anyway,” said Alyosha.\n\n“That I am sorry to lose God? It’s chemistry, brother, chemistry! There’s no help for it, your reverence, you must make way for chemistry. And Rakitin does dislike God. Ough! doesn’t he dislike Him! That’s the sore point with all of them. But they conceal it. They tell lies. They pretend. ‘Will you preach this in your reviews?’ I asked him. ‘Oh, well, if I did it openly, they won’t let it through,’ he said. He laughed. ‘But what will become of men then?’ I asked him, ‘without God and immortal life? All things are lawful then, they can do what they like?’ ‘Didn’t you know?’ he said laughing, ‘a clever man can do what he likes,’ he said. ‘A clever man knows his way about, but you’ve put your foot in it, committing a murder, and now you are rotting in prison.’ He says that to my face! A regular pig! I used to kick such people out, but now I listen to them. He talks a lot of sense, too. Writes well. He began reading me an article last week. I copied out three lines of it. Wait a minute. Here it is.”\n\nMitya hurriedly pulled out a piece of paper from his pocket and read:\n\n“‘In order to determine this question, it is above all essential to put one’s personality in contradiction to one’s reality.’ Do you understand that?”\n\n“No, I don’t,” said Alyosha. He looked at Mitya and listened to him with curiosity.\n\n“I don’t understand either. It’s dark and obscure, but intellectual. ‘Every one writes like that now,’ he says, ‘it’s the effect of their environment.’ They are afraid of the environment. He writes poetry, too, the rascal. He’s written in honor of Madame Hohlakov’s foot. Ha ha ha!”\n\n“I’ve heard about it,” said Alyosha.\n\n“Have you? And have you heard the poem?”\n\n“No.”\n“I’ve got it. Here it is. I’ll read it to you. You don’t know—I haven’t told you—there’s quite a story about it. He’s a rascal! Three weeks ago he began to tease me. ‘You’ve got yourself into a mess, like a fool, for the sake of three thousand, but I’m going to collar a hundred and fifty thousand. I am going to marry a widow and buy a house in Petersburg.’ And he told me he was courting Madame Hohlakov. She hadn’t much brains in her youth, and now at forty she has lost what she had. ‘But she’s awfully sentimental,’ he says; ‘that’s how I shall get hold of her. When I marry her, I shall take her to Petersburg and there I shall start a newspaper.’ And his mouth was simply watering, the beast, not for the widow, but for the hundred and fifty thousand. And he made me believe it. He came to see me every day. ‘She is coming round,’ he declared. He was beaming with delight. And then, all of a sudden, he was turned out of the house. Perhotin’s carrying everything before him, bravo! I could kiss the silly old noodle for turning him out of the house. And he had written this doggerel. ‘It’s the first time I’ve soiled my hands with writing poetry,’ he said. ‘It’s to win her heart, so it’s in a good cause. When I get hold of the silly woman’s fortune, I can be of great social utility.’ They have this social justification for every nasty thing they do! ‘Anyway it’s better than your Pushkin’s poetry,’ he said, ‘for I’ve managed to advocate enlightenment even in that.’ I understand what he means about Pushkin, I quite see that, if he really was a man of talent and only wrote about women’s feet. But wasn’t Rakitin stuck up about his doggerel! The vanity of these fellows! ‘On the convalescence of the swollen foot of the object of my affections’—he thought of that for a title. He’s a waggish fellow.\n\nA captivating little foot, Though swollen and red and tender! The doctors come and plasters put, But still they cannot mend her.\n\nYet, ’tis not for her foot I dread— A theme for Pushkin’s muse more fit— It’s not her foot, it is her head: I tremble for her loss of wit!\n\nFor as her foot swells, strange to say, Her intellect is on the wane— Oh, for some remedy I pray That may restore both foot and brain!\n\nHe is a pig, a regular pig, but he’s very arch, the rascal! And he really has put in a progressive idea. And wasn’t he angry when she kicked him out! He was gnashing his teeth!”\n\n“He’s taken his revenge already,” said Alyosha. “He’s written a paragraph about Madame Hohlakov.”\n\nAnd Alyosha told him briefly about the paragraph in Gossip.\n\n“That’s his doing, that’s his doing!” Mitya assented, frowning. “That’s him! These paragraphs ... I know ... the insulting things that have been written about Grushenka, for instance.... And about Katya, too.... H’m!”\n\nHe walked across the room with a harassed air.\n\n“Brother, I cannot stay long,” Alyosha said, after a pause. “To-morrow will be a great and awful day for you, the judgment of God will be accomplished ... I am amazed at you, you walk about here, talking of I don’t know what ...”\n\n“No, don’t be amazed at me,” Mitya broke in warmly. “Am I to talk of that stinking dog? Of the murderer? We’ve talked enough of him. I don’t want to say more of the stinking son of Stinking Lizaveta! God will kill him, you will see. Hush!”\n\nHe went up to Alyosha excitedly and kissed him. His eyes glowed.\n\n“Rakitin wouldn’t understand it,” he began in a sort of exaltation; “but you, you’ll understand it all. That’s why I was thirsting for you. You see, there’s so much I’ve been wanting to tell you for ever so long, here, within these peeling walls, but I haven’t said a word about what matters most; the moment never seems to have come. Now I can wait no longer. I must pour out my heart to you. Brother, these last two months I’ve found in myself a new man. A new man has risen up in me. He was hidden in me, but would never have come to the surface, if it hadn’t been for this blow from heaven. I am afraid! And what do I care if I spend twenty years in the mines, breaking ore with a hammer? I am not a bit afraid of that—it’s something else I am afraid of now: that that new man may leave me. Even there, in the mines, under-ground, I may find a human heart in another convict and murderer by my side, and I may make friends with him, for even there one may live and love and suffer. One may thaw and revive a frozen heart in that convict, one may wait upon him for years, and at last bring up from the dark depths a lofty soul, a feeling, suffering creature; one may bring forth an angel, create a hero! There are so many of them, hundreds of them, and we are all to blame for them. Why was it I dreamed of that ‘babe’ at such a moment? ‘Why is the babe so poor?’ That was a sign to me at that moment. It’s for the babe I’m going. Because we are all responsible for all. For all the ‘babes,’ for there are big children as well as little children. All are ‘babes.’ I go for all, because some one must go for all. I didn’t kill father, but I’ve got to go. I accept it. It’s all come to me here, here, within these peeling walls. There are numbers of them there, hundreds of them underground, with hammers in their hands. Oh, yes, we shall be in chains and there will be no freedom, but then, in our great sorrow, we shall rise again to joy, without which man cannot live nor God exist, for God gives joy: it’s His privilege—a grand one. Ah, man should be dissolved in prayer! What should I be underground there without God? Rakitin’s laughing! If they drive God from the earth, we shall shelter Him underground. One cannot exist in prison without God; it’s even more impossible than out of prison. And then we men underground will sing from the bowels of the earth a glorious hymn to God, with Whom is joy. Hail to God and His joy! I love Him!”\n\nMitya was almost gasping for breath as he uttered his wild speech. He turned pale, his lips quivered, and tears rolled down his cheeks.\n\n“Yes, life is full, there is life even underground,” he began again. “You wouldn’t believe, Alexey, how I want to live now, what a thirst for existence and consciousness has sprung up in me within these peeling walls. Rakitin doesn’t understand that; all he cares about is building a house and letting flats. But I’ve been longing for you. And what is suffering? I am not afraid of it, even if it were beyond reckoning. I am not afraid of it now. I was afraid of it before. Do you know, perhaps I won’t answer at the trial at all.... And I seem to have such strength in me now, that I think I could stand anything, any suffering, only to be able to say and to repeat to myself every moment, ‘I exist.’ In thousands of agonies—I exist. I’m tormented on the rack—but I exist! Though I sit alone on a pillar—I exist! I see the sun, and if I don’t see the sun, I know it’s there. And there’s a whole life in that, in knowing that the sun is there. Alyosha, my angel, all these philosophies are the death of me. Damn them! Brother Ivan—”\n\n“What of brother Ivan?” interrupted Alyosha, but Mitya did not hear.\n\n“You see, I never had any of these doubts before, but it was all hidden away in me. It was perhaps just because ideas I did not understand were surging up in me, that I used to drink and fight and rage. It was to stifle them in myself, to still them, to smother them. Ivan is not Rakitin, there is an idea in him. Ivan is a sphinx and is silent; he is always silent. It’s God that’s worrying me. That’s the only thing that’s worrying me. What if He doesn’t exist? What if Rakitin’s right—that it’s an idea made up by men? Then if He doesn’t exist, man is the chief of the earth, of the universe. Magnificent! Only how is he going to be good without God? That’s the question. I always come back to that. For whom is man going to love then? To whom will he be thankful? To whom will he sing the hymn? Rakitin laughs. Rakitin says that one can love humanity without God. Well, only a sniveling idiot can maintain that. I can’t understand it. Life’s easy for Rakitin. ‘You’d better think about the extension of civic rights, or even of keeping down the price of meat. You will show your love for humanity more simply and directly by that, than by philosophy.’ I answered him, ‘Well, but you, without a God, are more likely to raise the price of meat, if it suits you, and make a rouble on every copeck.’ He lost his temper. But after all, what is goodness? Answer me that, Alexey. Goodness is one thing with me and another with a Chinaman, so it’s a relative thing. Or isn’t it? Is it not relative? A treacherous question! You won’t laugh if I tell you it’s kept me awake two nights. I only wonder now how people can live and think nothing about it. Vanity! Ivan has no God. He has an idea. It’s beyond me. But he is silent. I believe he is a free-mason. I asked him, but he is silent. I wanted to drink from the springs of his soul—he was silent. But once he did drop a word.”\n\n“What did he say?” Alyosha took it up quickly.\n\n“I said to him, ‘Then everything is lawful, if it is so?’ He frowned. ‘Fyodor Pavlovitch, our papa,’ he said, ‘was a pig, but his ideas were right enough.’ That was what he dropped. That was all he said. That was going one better than Rakitin.”\n\n“Yes,” Alyosha assented bitterly. “When was he with you?”\n\n“Of that later; now I must speak of something else. I have said nothing about Ivan to you before. I put it off to the last. When my business here is over and the verdict has been given, then I’ll tell you something. I’ll tell you everything. We’ve something tremendous on hand.... And you shall be my judge in it. But don’t begin about that now; be silent. You talk of to-morrow, of the trial; but, would you believe it, I know nothing about it.”\n\n“Have you talked to the counsel?”\n\n“What’s the use of the counsel? I told him all about it. He’s a soft, city-bred rogue—a Bernard! But he doesn’t believe me—not a bit of it. Only imagine, he believes I did it. I see it. ‘In that case,’ I asked him, ‘why have you come to defend me?’ Hang them all! They’ve got a doctor down, too, want to prove I’m mad. I won’t have that! Katerina Ivanovna wants to do her ‘duty’ to the end, whatever the strain!” Mitya smiled bitterly. “The cat! Hard-hearted creature! She knows that I said of her at Mokroe that she was a woman of ‘great wrath.’ They repeated it. Yes, the facts against me have grown numerous as the sands of the sea. Grigory sticks to his point. Grigory’s honest, but a fool. Many people are honest because they are fools: that’s Rakitin’s idea. Grigory’s my enemy. And there are some people who are better as foes than friends. I mean Katerina Ivanovna. I am afraid, oh, I am afraid she will tell how she bowed to the ground after that four thousand. She’ll pay it back to the last farthing. I don’t want her sacrifice; they’ll put me to shame at the trial. I wonder how I can stand it. Go to her, Alyosha, ask her not to speak of that in the court, can’t you? But damn it all, it doesn’t matter! I shall get through somehow. I don’t pity her. It’s her own doing. She deserves what she gets. I shall have my own story to tell, Alexey.” He smiled bitterly again. “Only ... only Grusha, Grusha! Good Lord! Why should she have such suffering to bear?” he exclaimed suddenly, with tears. “Grusha’s killing me; the thought of her’s killing me, killing me. She was with me just now....”\n\n“She told me she was very much grieved by you to-day.”\n\n“I know. Confound my temper! It was jealousy. I was sorry, I kissed her as she was going. I didn’t ask her forgiveness.”\n\n“Why didn’t you?” exclaimed Alyosha.\n\nSuddenly Mitya laughed almost mirthfully.\n\n“God preserve you, my dear boy, from ever asking forgiveness for a fault from a woman you love. From one you love especially, however greatly you may have been in fault. For a woman—devil only knows what to make of a woman! I know something about them, anyway. But try acknowledging you are in fault to a woman. Say, ‘I am sorry, forgive me,’ and a shower of reproaches will follow! Nothing will make her forgive you simply and directly, she’ll humble you to the dust, bring forward things that have never happened, recall everything, forget nothing, add something of her own, and only then forgive you. And even the best, the best of them do it. She’ll scrape up all the scrapings and load them on your head. They are ready to flay you alive, I tell you, every one of them, all these angels without whom we cannot live! I tell you plainly and openly, dear boy, every decent man ought to be under some woman’s thumb. That’s my conviction—not conviction, but feeling. A man ought to be magnanimous, and it’s no disgrace to a man! No disgrace to a hero, not even a Cæsar! But don’t ever beg her pardon all the same for anything. Remember that rule given you by your brother Mitya, who’s come to ruin through women. No, I’d better make it up to Grusha somehow, without begging pardon. I worship her, Alexey, worship her. Only she doesn’t see it. No, she still thinks I don’t love her enough. And she tortures me, tortures me with her love. The past was nothing! In the past it was only those infernal curves of hers that tortured me, but now I’ve taken all her soul into my soul and through her I’ve become a man myself. Will they marry us? If they don’t, I shall die of jealousy. I imagine something every day.... What did she say to you about me?”\n\nAlyosha repeated all Grushenka had said to him that day. Mitya listened, made him repeat things, and seemed pleased.\n\n“Then she is not angry at my being jealous?” he exclaimed. “She is a regular woman! ‘I’ve a fierce heart myself!’ Ah, I love such fierce hearts, though I can’t bear any one’s being jealous of me. I can’t endure it. We shall fight. But I shall love her, I shall love her infinitely. Will they marry us? Do they let convicts marry? That’s the question. And without her I can’t exist....”\n\nMitya walked frowning across the room. It was almost dark. He suddenly seemed terribly worried.\n\n“So there’s a secret, she says, a secret? We have got up a plot against her, and Katya is mixed up in it, she thinks. No, my good Grushenka, that’s not it. You are very wide of the mark, in your foolish feminine way. Alyosha, darling, well, here goes! I’ll tell you our secret!”\n\nHe looked round, went close up quickly to Alyosha, who was standing before him, and whispered to him with an air of mystery, though in reality no one could hear them: the old warder was dozing in the corner, and not a word could reach the ears of the soldiers on guard.\n\n“I will tell you all our secret,” Mitya whispered hurriedly. “I meant to tell you later, for how could I decide on anything without you? You are everything to me. Though I say that Ivan is superior to us, you are my angel. It’s your decision will decide it. Perhaps it’s you that is superior and not Ivan. You see, it’s a question of conscience, question of the higher conscience—the secret is so important that I can’t settle it myself, and I’ve put it off till I could speak to you. But anyway it’s too early to decide now, for we must wait for the verdict. As soon as the verdict is given, you shall decide my fate. Don’t decide it now. I’ll tell you now. You listen, but don’t decide. Stand and keep quiet. I won’t tell you everything. I’ll only tell you the idea, without details, and you keep quiet. Not a question, not a movement. You agree? But, goodness, what shall I do with your eyes? I’m afraid your eyes will tell me your decision, even if you don’t speak. Oo! I’m afraid! Alyosha, listen! Ivan suggests my escaping. I won’t tell you the details: it’s all been thought out: it can all be arranged. Hush, don’t decide. I should go to America with Grusha. You know I can’t live without Grusha! What if they won’t let her follow me to Siberia? Do they let convicts get married? Ivan thinks not. And without Grusha what should I do there underground with a hammer? I should only smash my skull with the hammer! But, on the other hand, my conscience? I should have run away from suffering. A sign has come, I reject the sign. I have a way of salvation and I turn my back on it. Ivan says that in America, ‘with the good-will,’ I can be of more use than underground. But what becomes of our hymn from underground? What’s America? America is vanity again! And there’s a lot of swindling in America, too, I expect. I should have run away from crucifixion! I tell you, you know, Alexey, because you are the only person who can understand this. There’s no one else. It’s folly, madness to others, all I’ve told you of the hymn. They’ll say I’m out of my mind or a fool. I am not out of my mind and I am not a fool. Ivan understands about the hymn, too. He understands, only he doesn’t answer—he doesn’t speak. He doesn’t believe in the hymn. Don’t speak, don’t speak. I see how you look! You have already decided. Don’t decide, spare me! I can’t live without Grusha. Wait till after the trial!”\n\nMitya ended beside himself. He held Alyosha with both hands on his shoulders, and his yearning, feverish eyes were fixed on his brother’s.\n\n“They don’t let convicts marry, do they?” he repeated for the third time in a supplicating voice.\n\nAlyosha listened with extreme surprise and was deeply moved.\n\n“Tell me one thing,” he said. “Is Ivan very keen on it, and whose idea was it?”\n\n“His, his, and he is very keen on it. He didn’t come to see me at first, then he suddenly came a week ago and he began about it straight away. He is awfully keen on it. He doesn’t ask me, but orders me to escape. He doesn’t doubt of my obeying him, though I showed him all my heart as I have to you, and told him about the hymn, too. He told me he’d arrange it; he’s found out about everything. But of that later. He’s simply set on it. It’s all a matter of money: he’ll pay ten thousand for escape and give me twenty thousand for America. And he says we can arrange a magnificent escape for ten thousand.”\n\n“And he told you on no account to tell me?” Alyosha asked again.\n\n“To tell no one, and especially not you; on no account to tell you. He is afraid, no doubt, that you’ll stand before me as my conscience. Don’t tell him I told you. Don’t tell him, for anything.”\n\n“You are right,” Alyosha pronounced; “it’s impossible to decide anything before the trial is over. After the trial you’ll decide of yourself. Then you’ll find that new man in yourself and he will decide.”\n\n“A new man, or a Bernard who’ll decide à la Bernard, for I believe I’m a contemptible Bernard myself,” said Mitya, with a bitter grin.\n\n“But, brother, have you no hope then of being acquitted?”\n\nMitya shrugged his shoulders nervously and shook his head. “Alyosha, darling, it’s time you were going,” he said, with a sudden haste. “There’s the superintendent shouting in the yard. He’ll be here directly. We are late; it’s irregular. Embrace me quickly. Kiss me! Sign me with the cross, darling, for the cross I have to bear to-morrow.”\n\nThey embraced and kissed.\n\n“Ivan,” said Mitya suddenly, “suggests my escaping; but, of course, he believes I did it.”\n\nA mournful smile came on to his lips.\n\n“Have you asked him whether he believes it?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“No, I haven’t. I wanted to, but I couldn’t. I hadn’t the courage. But I saw it from his eyes. Well, good-by!”\n\nOnce more they kissed hurriedly, and Alyosha was just going out, when Mitya suddenly called him back.\n\n“Stand facing me! That’s right!” And again he seized Alyosha, putting both hands on his shoulders. His face became suddenly quite pale, so that it was dreadfully apparent, even through the gathering darkness. His lips twitched, his eyes fastened upon Alyosha.\n\n“Alyosha, tell me the whole truth, as you would before God. Do you believe I did it? Do you, do you in yourself, believe it? The whole truth, don’t lie!” he cried desperately.\n\nEverything seemed heaving before Alyosha, and he felt something like a stab at his heart.\n\n“Hush! What do you mean?” he faltered helplessly.\n\n“The whole truth, the whole, don’t lie!” repeated Mitya.\n\n“I’ve never for one instant believed that you were the murderer!” broke in a shaking voice from Alyosha’s breast, and he raised his right hand in the air, as though calling God to witness his words.\n\nMitya’s whole face was lighted up with bliss.\n\n“Thank you!” he articulated slowly, as though letting a sigh escape him after fainting. “Now you have given me new life. Would you believe it, till this moment I’ve been afraid to ask you, you, even you. Well, go! You’ve given me strength for to-morrow. God bless you! Come, go along! Love Ivan!” was Mitya’s last word.\n\nAlyosha went out in tears. Such distrustfulness in Mitya, such lack of confidence even to him, to Alyosha—all this suddenly opened before Alyosha an unsuspected depth of hopeless grief and despair in the soul of his unhappy brother. Intense, infinite compassion overwhelmed him instantly. There was a poignant ache in his torn heart. “Love Ivan!”—he suddenly recalled Mitya’s words. And he was going to Ivan. He badly wanted to see Ivan all day. He was as much worried about Ivan as about Mitya, and more than ever now.\n\n# Chapter V. Not You, Not You!\n\nOn the way to Ivan he had to pass the house where Katerina Ivanovna was living. There was light in the windows. He suddenly stopped and resolved to go in. He had not seen Katerina Ivanovna for more than a week. But now it struck him that Ivan might be with her, especially on the eve of the terrible day. Ringing, and mounting the staircase, which was dimly lighted by a Chinese lantern, he saw a man coming down, and as they met, he recognized him as his brother. So he was just coming from Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“Ah, it’s only you,” said Ivan dryly. “Well, good-by! You are going to her?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I don’t advise you to; she’s upset and you’ll upset her more.”\n\nA door was instantly flung open above, and a voice cried suddenly:\n\n“No, no! Alexey Fyodorovitch, have you come from him?”\n\n“Yes, I have been with him.”\n\n“Has he sent me any message? Come up, Alyosha, and you, Ivan Fyodorovitch, you must come back, you must. Do you hear?”\n\nThere was such a peremptory note in Katya’s voice that Ivan, after a moment’s hesitation, made up his mind to go back with Alyosha.\n\n“She was listening,” he murmured angrily to himself, but Alyosha heard it.\n\n“Excuse my keeping my greatcoat on,” said Ivan, going into the drawing-room. “I won’t sit down. I won’t stay more than a minute.”\n\n“Sit down, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” said Katerina Ivanovna, though she remained standing. She had changed very little during this time, but there was an ominous gleam in her dark eyes. Alyosha remembered afterwards that she had struck him as particularly handsome at that moment.\n\n“What did he ask you to tell me?”\n\n“Only one thing,” said Alyosha, looking her straight in the face, “that you would spare yourself and say nothing at the trial of what” (he was a little confused) “... passed between you ... at the time of your first acquaintance ... in that town.”\n\n“Ah! that I bowed down to the ground for that money!” She broke into a bitter laugh. “Why, is he afraid for me or for himself? He asks me to spare—whom? Him or myself? Tell me, Alexey Fyodorovitch!”\n\nAlyosha watched her intently, trying to understand her.\n\n“Both yourself and him,” he answered softly.\n\n“I am glad to hear it,” she snapped out maliciously, and she suddenly blushed.\n\n“You don’t know me yet, Alexey Fyodorovitch,” she said menacingly. “And I don’t know myself yet. Perhaps you’ll want to trample me under foot after my examination to-morrow.”\n\n“You will give your evidence honorably,” said Alyosha; “that’s all that’s wanted.”\n\n“Women are often dishonorable,” she snarled. “Only an hour ago I was thinking I felt afraid to touch that monster ... as though he were a reptile ... but no, he is still a human being to me! But did he do it? Is he the murderer?” she cried, all of a sudden, hysterically, turning quickly to Ivan. Alyosha saw at once that she had asked Ivan that question before, perhaps only a moment before he came in, and not for the first time, but for the hundredth, and that they had ended by quarreling.\n\n“I’ve been to see Smerdyakov.... It was you, you who persuaded me that he murdered his father. It’s only you I believed!” she continued, still addressing Ivan. He gave her a sort of strained smile. Alyosha started at her tone. He had not suspected such familiar intimacy between them.\n\n“Well, that’s enough, anyway,” Ivan cut short the conversation. “I am going. I’ll come to-morrow.” And turning at once, he walked out of the room and went straight downstairs.\n\nWith an imperious gesture, Katerina Ivanovna seized Alyosha by both hands.\n\n“Follow him! Overtake him! Don’t leave him alone for a minute!” she said, in a hurried whisper. “He’s mad! Don’t you know that he’s mad? He is in a fever, nervous fever. The doctor told me so. Go, run after him....”\n\nAlyosha jumped up and ran after Ivan, who was not fifty paces ahead of him.\n\n“What do you want?” He turned quickly on Alyosha, seeing that he was running after him. “She told you to catch me up, because I’m mad. I know it all by heart,” he added irritably.\n\n“She is mistaken, of course; but she is right that you are ill,” said Alyosha. “I was looking at your face just now. You look very ill, Ivan.”\n\nIvan walked on without stopping. Alyosha followed him.\n\n“And do you know, Alexey Fyodorovitch, how people do go out of their mind?” Ivan asked in a voice suddenly quiet, without a trace of irritation, with a note of the simplest curiosity.\n\n“No, I don’t. I suppose there are all kinds of insanity.”\n\n“And can one observe that one’s going mad oneself?”\n\n“I imagine one can’t see oneself clearly in such circumstances,” Alyosha answered with surprise.\n\nIvan paused for half a minute.\n\n“If you want to talk to me, please change the subject,” he said suddenly.\n\n“Oh, while I think of it, I have a letter for you,” said Alyosha timidly, and he took Lise’s note from his pocket and held it out to Ivan. They were just under a lamp-post. Ivan recognized the handwriting at once.\n\n“Ah, from that little demon!” he laughed maliciously, and, without opening the envelope, he tore it into bits and threw it in the air. The bits were scattered by the wind.\n\n“She’s not sixteen yet, I believe, and already offering herself,” he said contemptuously, striding along the street again.\n\n“How do you mean, offering herself?” exclaimed Alyosha.\n\n“As wanton women offer themselves, to be sure.”\n\n“How can you, Ivan, how can you?” Alyosha cried warmly, in a grieved voice. “She is a child; you are insulting a child! She is ill; she is very ill, too. She is on the verge of insanity, too, perhaps.... I had hoped to hear something from you ... that would save her.”\n\n“You’ll hear nothing from me. If she is a child I am not her nurse. Be quiet, Alexey. Don’t go on about her. I am not even thinking about it.”\n\nThey were silent again for a moment.\n\n“She will be praying all night now to the Mother of God to show her how to act to-morrow at the trial,” he said sharply and angrily again.\n\n“You ... you mean Katerina Ivanovna?”\n\n“Yes. Whether she’s to save Mitya or ruin him. She’ll pray for light from above. She can’t make up her mind for herself, you see. She has not had time to decide yet. She takes me for her nurse, too. She wants me to sing lullabies to her.”\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna loves you, brother,” said Alyosha sadly.\n\n“Perhaps; but I am not very keen on her.”\n\n“She is suffering. Why do you ... sometimes say things to her that give her hope?” Alyosha went on, with timid reproach. “I know that you’ve given her hope. Forgive me for speaking to you like this,” he added.\n\n“I can’t behave to her as I ought—break off altogether and tell her so straight out,” said Ivan, irritably. “I must wait till sentence is passed on the murderer. If I break off with her now, she will avenge herself on me by ruining that scoundrel to-morrow at the trial, for she hates him and knows she hates him. It’s all a lie—lie upon lie! As long as I don’t break off with her, she goes on hoping, and she won’t ruin that monster, knowing how I want to get him out of trouble. If only that damned verdict would come!”\n\nThe words “murderer” and “monster” echoed painfully in Alyosha’s heart.\n\n“But how can she ruin Mitya?” he asked, pondering on Ivan’s words. “What evidence can she give that would ruin Mitya?”\n\n“You don’t know that yet. She’s got a document in her hands, in Mitya’s own writing, that proves conclusively that he did murder Fyodor Pavlovitch.”\n\n“That’s impossible!” cried Alyosha.\n\n“Why is it impossible? I’ve read it myself.”\n\n“There can’t be such a document!” Alyosha repeated warmly. “There can’t be, because he’s not the murderer. It’s not he murdered father, not he!”\n\nIvan suddenly stopped.\n\n“Who is the murderer then, according to you?” he asked, with apparent coldness. There was even a supercilious note in his voice.\n\n“You know who,” Alyosha pronounced in a low, penetrating voice.\n\n“Who? You mean the myth about that crazy idiot, the epileptic, Smerdyakov?”\n\nAlyosha suddenly felt himself trembling all over.\n\n“You know who,” broke helplessly from him. He could scarcely breathe.\n\n“Who? Who?” Ivan cried almost fiercely. All his restraint suddenly vanished.\n\n“I only know one thing,” Alyosha went on, still almost in a whisper, “it wasn’t you killed father.”\n\n“‘Not you’! What do you mean by ‘not you’?” Ivan was thunderstruck.\n\n“It was not you killed father, not you!” Alyosha repeated firmly.\n\nThe silence lasted for half a minute.\n\n“I know I didn’t. Are you raving?” said Ivan, with a pale, distorted smile. His eyes were riveted on Alyosha. They were standing again under a lamp-post.\n\n“No, Ivan. You’ve told yourself several times that you are the murderer.”\n\n“When did I say so? I was in Moscow.... When have I said so?” Ivan faltered helplessly.\n\n“You’ve said so to yourself many times, when you’ve been alone during these two dreadful months,” Alyosha went on softly and distinctly as before. Yet he was speaking now, as it were, not of himself, not of his own will, but obeying some irresistible command. “You have accused yourself and have confessed to yourself that you are the murderer and no one else. But you didn’t do it: you are mistaken: you are not the murderer. Do you hear? It was not you! God has sent me to tell you so.”\n\nThey were both silent. The silence lasted a whole long minute. They were both standing still, gazing into each other’s eyes. They were both pale. Suddenly Ivan began trembling all over, and clutched Alyosha’s shoulder.\n\n“You’ve been in my room!” he whispered hoarsely. “You’ve been there at night, when he came.... Confess ... have you seen him, have you seen him?”\n\n“Whom do you mean—Mitya?” Alyosha asked, bewildered.\n\n“Not him, damn the monster!” Ivan shouted, in a frenzy. “Do you know that he visits me? How did you find out? Speak!”\n\n“Who is he! I don’t know whom you are talking about,” Alyosha faltered, beginning to be alarmed.\n\n“Yes, you do know ... or how could you—? It’s impossible that you don’t know.”\n\nSuddenly he seemed to check himself. He stood still and seemed to reflect. A strange grin contorted his lips.\n\n“Brother,” Alyosha began again, in a shaking voice, “I have said this to you, because you’ll believe my word, I know that. I tell you once and for all, it’s not you. You hear, once for all! God has put it into my heart to say this to you, even though it may make you hate me from this hour.”\n\nBut by now Ivan had apparently regained his self-control.\n\n“Alexey Fyodorovitch,” he said, with a cold smile, “I can’t endure prophets and epileptics—messengers from God especially—and you know that only too well. I break off all relations with you from this moment and probably for ever. I beg you to leave me at this turning. It’s the way to your lodgings, too. You’d better be particularly careful not to come to me to-day! Do you hear?”\n\nHe turned and walked on with a firm step, not looking back.\n\n“Brother,” Alyosha called after him, “if anything happens to you to-day, turn to me before any one!”\n\nBut Ivan made no reply. Alyosha stood under the lamp-post at the cross roads, till Ivan had vanished into the darkness. Then he turned and walked slowly homewards. Both Alyosha and Ivan were living in lodgings; neither of them was willing to live in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s empty house. Alyosha had a furnished room in the house of some working people. Ivan lived some distance from him. He had taken a roomy and fairly comfortable lodge attached to a fine house that belonged to a well-to-do lady, the widow of an official. But his only attendant was a deaf and rheumatic old crone who went to bed at six o’clock every evening and got up at six in the morning. Ivan had become remarkably indifferent to his comforts of late, and very fond of being alone. He did everything for himself in the one room he lived in, and rarely entered any of the other rooms in his abode.\n\nHe reached the gate of the house and had his hand on the bell, when he suddenly stopped. He felt that he was trembling all over with anger. Suddenly he let go of the bell, turned back with a curse, and walked with rapid steps in the opposite direction. He walked a mile and a half to a tiny, slanting, wooden house, almost a hut, where Marya Kondratyevna, the neighbor who used to come to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s kitchen for soup and to whom Smerdyakov had once sung his songs and played on the guitar, was now lodging. She had sold their little house, and was now living here with her mother. Smerdyakov, who was ill—almost dying—had been with them ever since Fyodor Pavlovitch’s death. It was to him Ivan was going now, drawn by a sudden and irresistible prompting.\n\n# Chapter VI. The First Interview With Smerdyakov\n\nThis was the third time that Ivan had been to see Smerdyakov since his return from Moscow. The first time he had seen him and talked to him was on the first day of his arrival, then he had visited him once more, a fortnight later. But his visits had ended with that second one, so that it was now over a month since he had seen him. And he had scarcely heard anything of him.\n\nIvan had only returned five days after his father’s death, so that he was not present at the funeral, which took place the day before he came back. The cause of his delay was that Alyosha, not knowing his Moscow address, had to apply to Katerina Ivanovna to telegraph to him, and she, not knowing his address either, telegraphed to her sister and aunt, reckoning on Ivan’s going to see them as soon as he arrived in Moscow. But he did not go to them till four days after his arrival. When he got the telegram, he had, of course, set off post-haste to our town. The first to meet him was Alyosha, and Ivan was greatly surprised to find that, in opposition to the general opinion of the town, he refused to entertain a suspicion against Mitya, and spoke openly of Smerdyakov as the murderer. Later on, after seeing the police captain and the prosecutor, and hearing the details of the charge and the arrest, he was still more surprised at Alyosha, and ascribed his opinion only to his exaggerated brotherly feeling and sympathy with Mitya, of whom Alyosha, as Ivan knew, was very fond.\n\nBy the way, let us say a word or two of Ivan’s feeling to his brother Dmitri. He positively disliked him; at most, felt sometimes a compassion for him, and even that was mixed with great contempt, almost repugnance. Mitya’s whole personality, even his appearance, was extremely unattractive to him. Ivan looked with indignation on Katerina Ivanovna’s love for his brother. Yet he went to see Mitya on the first day of his arrival, and that interview, far from shaking Ivan’s belief in his guilt, positively strengthened it. He found his brother agitated, nervously excited. Mitya had been talkative, but very absent-minded and incoherent. He used violent language, accused Smerdyakov, and was fearfully muddled. He talked principally about the three thousand roubles, which he said had been “stolen” from him by his father.\n\n“The money was mine, it was my money,” Mitya kept repeating. “Even if I had stolen it, I should have had the right.”\n\nHe hardly contested the evidence against him, and if he tried to turn a fact to his advantage, it was in an absurd and incoherent way. He hardly seemed to wish to defend himself to Ivan or any one else. Quite the contrary, he was angry and proudly scornful of the charges against him; he was continually firing up and abusing every one. He only laughed contemptuously at Grigory’s evidence about the open door, and declared that it was “the devil that opened it.” But he could not bring forward any coherent explanation of the fact. He even succeeded in insulting Ivan during their first interview, telling him sharply that it was not for people who declared that “everything was lawful,” to suspect and question him. Altogether he was anything but friendly with Ivan on that occasion. Immediately after that interview with Mitya, Ivan went for the first time to see Smerdyakov.\n\nIn the railway train on his way from Moscow, he kept thinking of Smerdyakov and of his last conversation with him on the evening before he went away. Many things seemed to him puzzling and suspicious. But when he gave his evidence to the investigating lawyer Ivan said nothing, for the time, of that conversation. He put that off till he had seen Smerdyakov, who was at that time in the hospital.\n\nDoctor Herzenstube and Varvinsky, the doctor he met in the hospital, confidently asserted in reply to Ivan’s persistent questions, that Smerdyakov’s epileptic attack was unmistakably genuine, and were surprised indeed at Ivan asking whether he might not have been shamming on the day of the catastrophe. They gave him to understand that the attack was an exceptional one, the fits persisting and recurring several times, so that the patient’s life was positively in danger, and it was only now, after they had applied remedies, that they could assert with confidence that the patient would survive. “Though it might well be,” added Doctor Herzenstube, “that his reason would be impaired for a considerable period, if not permanently.” On Ivan’s asking impatiently whether that meant that he was now mad, they told him that this was not yet the case, in the full sense of the word, but that certain abnormalities were perceptible. Ivan decided to find out for himself what those abnormalities were.\n\nAt the hospital he was at once allowed to see the patient. Smerdyakov was lying on a truckle-bed in a separate ward. There was only one other bed in the room, and in it lay a tradesman of the town, swollen with dropsy, who was obviously almost dying; he could be no hindrance to their conversation. Smerdyakov grinned uncertainly on seeing Ivan, and for the first instant seemed nervous. So at least Ivan fancied. But that was only momentary. For the rest of the time he was struck, on the contrary, by Smerdyakov’s composure. From the first glance Ivan had no doubt that he was very ill. He was very weak; he spoke slowly, seeming to move his tongue with difficulty; he was much thinner and sallower. Throughout the interview, which lasted twenty minutes, he kept complaining of headache and of pain in all his limbs. His thin emasculate face seemed to have become so tiny; his hair was ruffled, and his crest of curls in front stood up in a thin tuft. But in the left eye, which was screwed up and seemed to be insinuating something, Smerdyakov showed himself unchanged. “It’s always worth while speaking to a clever man.” Ivan was reminded of that at once. He sat down on the stool at his feet. Smerdyakov, with painful effort, shifted his position in bed, but he was not the first to speak. He remained dumb, and did not even look much interested.\n\n“Can you talk to me?” asked Ivan. “I won’t tire you much.”\n\n“Certainly I can,” mumbled Smerdyakov, in a faint voice. “Has your honor been back long?” he added patronizingly, as though encouraging a nervous visitor.\n\n“I only arrived to-day.... To see the mess you are in here.”\n\nSmerdyakov sighed.\n\n“Why do you sigh? You knew of it all along,” Ivan blurted out.\n\nSmerdyakov was stolidly silent for a while.\n\n“How could I help knowing? It was clear beforehand. But how could I tell it would turn out like that?”\n\n“What would turn out? Don’t prevaricate! You’ve foretold you’d have a fit; on the way down to the cellar, you know. You mentioned the very spot.”\n\n“Have you said so at the examination yet?” Smerdyakov queried with composure.\n\nIvan felt suddenly angry.\n\n“No, I haven’t yet, but I certainly shall. You must explain a great deal to me, my man; and let me tell you, I am not going to let you play with me!”\n\n“Why should I play with you, when I put my whole trust in you, as in God Almighty?” said Smerdyakov, with the same composure, only for a moment closing his eyes.\n\n“In the first place,” began Ivan, “I know that epileptic fits can’t be told beforehand. I’ve inquired; don’t try and take me in. You can’t foretell the day and the hour. How was it you told me the day and the hour beforehand, and about the cellar, too? How could you tell that you would fall down the cellar stairs in a fit, if you didn’t sham a fit on purpose?”\n\n“I had to go to the cellar anyway, several times a day, indeed,” Smerdyakov drawled deliberately. “I fell from the garret just in the same way a year ago. It’s quite true you can’t tell the day and hour of a fit beforehand, but you can always have a presentiment of it.”\n\n“But you did foretell the day and the hour!”\n\n“In regard to my epilepsy, sir, you had much better inquire of the doctors here. You can ask them whether it was a real fit or a sham; it’s no use my saying any more about it.”\n\n“And the cellar? How could you know beforehand of the cellar?”\n\n“You don’t seem able to get over that cellar! As I was going down to the cellar, I was in terrible dread and doubt. What frightened me most was losing you and being left without defense in all the world. So I went down into the cellar thinking, ‘Here, it’ll come on directly, it’ll strike me down directly, shall I fall?’ And it was through this fear that I suddenly felt the spasm that always comes ... and so I went flying. All that and all my previous conversation with you at the gate the evening before, when I told you how frightened I was and spoke of the cellar, I told all that to Doctor Herzenstube and Nikolay Parfenovitch, the investigating lawyer, and it’s all been written down in the protocol. And the doctor here, Mr. Varvinsky, maintained to all of them that it was just the thought of it brought it on, the apprehension that I might fall. It was just then that the fit seized me. And so they’ve written it down, that it’s just how it must have happened, simply from my fear.”\n\nAs he finished, Smerdyakov drew a deep breath, as though exhausted.\n\n“Then you have said all that in your evidence?” said Ivan, somewhat taken aback. He had meant to frighten him with the threat of repeating their conversation, and it appeared that Smerdyakov had already reported it all himself.\n\n“What have I to be afraid of? Let them write down the whole truth,” Smerdyakov pronounced firmly.\n\n“And have you told them every word of our conversation at the gate?”\n\n“No, not to say every word.”\n\n“And did you tell them that you can sham fits, as you boasted then?”\n\n“No, I didn’t tell them that either.”\n\n“Tell me now, why did you send me then to Tchermashnya?”\n\n“I was afraid you’d go away to Moscow; Tchermashnya is nearer, anyway.”\n\n“You are lying; you suggested my going away yourself; you told me to get out of the way of trouble.”\n\n“That was simply out of affection and my sincere devotion to you, foreseeing trouble in the house, to spare you. Only I wanted to spare myself even more. That’s why I told you to get out of harm’s way, that you might understand that there would be trouble in the house, and would remain at home to protect your father.”\n\n“You might have said it more directly, you blockhead!” Ivan suddenly fired up.\n\n“How could I have said it more directly then? It was simply my fear that made me speak, and you might have been angry, too. I might well have been apprehensive that Dmitri Fyodorovitch would make a scene and carry away that money, for he considered it as good as his own; but who could tell that it would end in a murder like this? I thought that he would only carry off the three thousand that lay under the master’s mattress in the envelope, and you see, he’s murdered him. How could you guess it either, sir?”\n\n“But if you say yourself that it couldn’t be guessed, how could I have guessed and stayed at home? You contradict yourself!” said Ivan, pondering.\n\n“You might have guessed from my sending you to Tchermashnya and not to Moscow.”\n\n“How could I guess it from that?”\n\nSmerdyakov seemed much exhausted, and again he was silent for a minute.\n\n“You might have guessed from the fact of my asking you not to go to Moscow, but to Tchermashnya, that I wanted to have you nearer, for Moscow’s a long way off, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch, knowing you are not far off, would not be so bold. And if anything had happened, you might have come to protect me, too, for I warned you of Grigory Vassilyevitch’s illness, and that I was afraid of having a fit. And when I explained those knocks to you, by means of which one could go in to the deceased, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch knew them all through me, I thought that you would guess yourself that he would be sure to do something, and so wouldn’t go to Tchermashnya even, but would stay.”\n\n“He talks very coherently,” thought Ivan, “though he does mumble; what’s the derangement of his faculties that Herzenstube talked of?”\n\n“You are cunning with me, damn you!” he exclaimed, getting angry.\n\n“But I thought at the time that you quite guessed,” Smerdyakov parried with the simplest air.\n\n“If I’d guessed, I should have stayed,” cried Ivan.\n\n“Why, I thought that it was because you guessed, that you went away in such a hurry, only to get out of trouble, only to run away and save yourself in your fright.”\n\n“You think that every one is as great a coward as yourself?”\n\n“Forgive me, I thought you were like me.”\n\n“Of course, I ought to have guessed,” Ivan said in agitation; “and I did guess there was some mischief brewing on your part ... only you are lying, you are lying again,” he cried, suddenly recollecting. “Do you remember how you went up to the carriage and said to me, ‘It’s always worth while speaking to a clever man’? So you were glad I went away, since you praised me?”\n\nSmerdyakov sighed again and again. A trace of color came into his face.\n\n“If I was pleased,” he articulated rather breathlessly, “it was simply because you agreed not to go to Moscow, but to Tchermashnya. For it was nearer, anyway. Only when I said these words to you, it was not by way of praise, but of reproach. You didn’t understand it.”\n\n“What reproach?”\n\n“Why, that foreseeing such a calamity you deserted your own father, and would not protect us, for I might have been taken up any time for stealing that three thousand.”\n\n“Damn you!” Ivan swore again. “Stay, did you tell the prosecutor and the investigating lawyer about those knocks?”\n\n“I told them everything just as it was.”\n\nIvan wondered inwardly again.\n\n“If I thought of anything then,” he began again, “it was solely of some wickedness on your part. Dmitri might kill him, but that he would steal—I did not believe that then.... But I was prepared for any wickedness from you. You told me yourself you could sham a fit. What did you say that for?”\n\n“It was just through my simplicity, and I never have shammed a fit on purpose in my life. And I only said so then to boast to you. It was just foolishness. I liked you so much then, and was open-hearted with you.”\n\n“My brother directly accuses you of the murder and theft.”\n\n“What else is left for him to do?” said Smerdyakov, with a bitter grin. “And who will believe him with all the proofs against him? Grigory Vassilyevitch saw the door open. What can he say after that? But never mind him! He is trembling to save himself.”\n\nHe slowly ceased speaking; then suddenly, as though on reflection, added:\n\n“And look here again. He wants to throw it on me and make out that it is the work of my hands—I’ve heard that already. But as to my being clever at shamming a fit: should I have told you beforehand that I could sham one, if I really had had such a design against your father? If I had been planning such a murder could I have been such a fool as to give such evidence against myself beforehand? And to his son, too! Upon my word! Is that likely? As if that could be, such a thing has never happened. No one hears this talk of ours now, except Providence itself, and if you were to tell of it to the prosecutor and Nikolay Parfenovitch you might defend me completely by doing so, for who would be likely to be such a criminal, if he is so open-hearted beforehand? Any one can see that.”\n\n“Well,” and Ivan got up to cut short the conversation, struck by Smerdyakov’s last argument. “I don’t suspect you at all, and I think it’s absurd, indeed, to suspect you. On the contrary, I am grateful to you for setting my mind at rest. Now I am going, but I’ll come again. Meanwhile, good-by. Get well. Is there anything you want?”\n\n“I am very thankful for everything. Marfa Ignatyevna does not forget me, and provides me anything I want, according to her kindness. Good people visit me every day.”\n\n“Good-by. But I shan’t say anything of your being able to sham a fit, and I don’t advise you to, either,” something made Ivan say suddenly.\n\n“I quite understand. And if you don’t speak of that, I shall say nothing of that conversation of ours at the gate.”\n\nThen it happened that Ivan went out, and only when he had gone a dozen steps along the corridor, he suddenly felt that there was an insulting significance in Smerdyakov’s last words. He was almost on the point of turning back, but it was only a passing impulse, and muttering, “Nonsense!” he went out of the hospital.\n\nHis chief feeling was one of relief at the fact that it was not Smerdyakov, but Mitya, who had committed the murder, though he might have been expected to feel the opposite. He did not want to analyze the reason for this feeling, and even felt a positive repugnance at prying into his sensations. He felt as though he wanted to make haste to forget something. In the following days he became convinced of Mitya’s guilt, as he got to know all the weight of evidence against him. There was evidence of people of no importance, Fenya and her mother, for instance, but the effect of it was almost overpowering. As to Perhotin, the people at the tavern, and at Plotnikov’s shop, as well as the witnesses at Mokroe, their evidence seemed conclusive. It was the details that were so damning. The secret of the knocks impressed the lawyers almost as much as Grigory’s evidence as to the open door. Grigory’s wife, Marfa, in answer to Ivan’s questions, declared that Smerdyakov had been lying all night the other side of the partition wall. “He was not three paces from our bed,” and that although she was a sound sleeper she waked several times and heard him moaning, “He was moaning the whole time, moaning continually.”\n\nTalking to Herzenstube, and giving it as his opinion that Smerdyakov was not mad, but only rather weak, Ivan only evoked from the old man a subtle smile.\n\n“Do you know how he spends his time now?” he asked; “learning lists of French words by heart. He has an exercise-book under his pillow with the French words written out in Russian letters for him by some one, he he he!”\n\nIvan ended by dismissing all doubts. He could not think of Dmitri without repulsion. Only one thing was strange, however. Alyosha persisted that Dmitri was not the murderer, and that “in all probability” Smerdyakov was. Ivan always felt that Alyosha’s opinion meant a great deal to him, and so he was astonished at it now. Another thing that was strange was that Alyosha did not make any attempt to talk about Mitya with Ivan, that he never began on the subject and only answered his questions. This, too, struck Ivan particularly.\n\nBut he was very much preoccupied at that time with something quite apart from that. On his return from Moscow, he abandoned himself hopelessly to his mad and consuming passion for Katerina Ivanovna. This is not the time to begin to speak of this new passion of Ivan’s, which left its mark on all the rest of his life: this would furnish the subject for another novel, which I may perhaps never write. But I cannot omit to mention here that when Ivan, on leaving Katerina Ivanovna with Alyosha, as I’ve related already, told him, “I am not keen on her,” it was an absolute lie: he loved her madly, though at times he hated her so that he might have murdered her. Many causes helped to bring about this feeling. Shattered by what had happened with Mitya, she rushed on Ivan’s return to meet him as her one salvation. She was hurt, insulted and humiliated in her feelings. And here the man had come back to her, who had loved her so ardently before (oh! she knew that very well), and whose heart and intellect she considered so superior to her own. But the sternly virtuous girl did not abandon herself altogether to the man she loved, in spite of the Karamazov violence of his passions and the great fascination he had for her. She was continually tormented at the same time by remorse for having deserted Mitya, and in moments of discord and violent anger (and they were numerous) she told Ivan so plainly. This was what he had called to Alyosha “lies upon lies.” There was, of course, much that was false in it, and that angered Ivan more than anything.... But of all this later.\n\nHe did, in fact, for a time almost forget Smerdyakov’s existence, and yet, a fortnight after his first visit to him, he began to be haunted by the same strange thoughts as before. It’s enough to say that he was continually asking himself, why was it that on that last night in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house he had crept out on to the stairs like a thief and listened to hear what his father was doing below? Why had he recalled that afterwards with repulsion? Why next morning, had he been suddenly so depressed on the journey? Why, as he reached Moscow, had he said to himself, “I am a scoundrel”? And now he almost fancied that these tormenting thoughts would make him even forget Katerina Ivanovna, so completely did they take possession of him again. It was just after fancying this, that he met Alyosha in the street. He stopped him at once, and put a question to him:\n\n“Do you remember when Dmitri burst in after dinner and beat father, and afterwards I told you in the yard that I reserved ‘the right to desire’?... Tell me, did you think then that I desired father’s death or not?”\n\n“I did think so,” answered Alyosha, softly.\n\n“It was so, too; it was not a matter of guessing. But didn’t you fancy then that what I wished was just that ‘one reptile should devour another’; that is, just that Dmitri should kill father, and as soon as possible ... and that I myself was even prepared to help to bring that about?”\n\nAlyosha turned rather pale, and looked silently into his brother’s face.\n\n“Speak!” cried Ivan, “I want above everything to know what you thought then. I want the truth, the truth!”\n\nHe drew a deep breath, looking angrily at Alyosha before his answer came.\n\n“Forgive me, I did think that, too, at the time,” whispered Alyosha, and he did not add one softening phrase.\n\n“Thanks,” snapped Ivan, and, leaving Alyosha, he went quickly on his way. From that time Alyosha noticed that Ivan began obviously to avoid him and seemed even to have taken a dislike to him, so much so that Alyosha gave up going to see him. Immediately after that meeting with him, Ivan had not gone home, but went straight to Smerdyakov again.\n\n# Chapter VII. The Second Visit To Smerdyakov\n\nBy that time Smerdyakov had been discharged from the hospital. Ivan knew his new lodging, the dilapidated little wooden house, divided in two by a passage on one side of which lived Marya Kondratyevna and her mother, and on the other, Smerdyakov. No one knew on what terms he lived with them, whether as a friend or as a lodger. It was supposed afterwards that he had come to stay with them as Marya Kondratyevna’s betrothed, and was living there for a time without paying for board or lodging. Both mother and daughter had the greatest respect for him and looked upon him as greatly superior to themselves.\n\nIvan knocked, and, on the door being opened, went straight into the passage. By Marya Kondratyevna’s directions he went straight to the better room on the left, occupied by Smerdyakov. There was a tiled stove in the room and it was extremely hot. The walls were gay with blue paper, which was a good deal used however, and in the cracks under it cockroaches swarmed in amazing numbers, so that there was a continual rustling from them. The furniture was very scanty: two benches against each wall and two chairs by the table. The table of plain wood was covered with a cloth with pink patterns on it. There was a pot of geranium on each of the two little windows. In the corner there was a case of ikons. On the table stood a little copper samovar with many dents in it, and a tray with two cups. But Smerdyakov had finished tea and the samovar was out. He was sitting at the table on a bench. He was looking at an exercise-book and slowly writing with a pen. There was a bottle of ink by him and a flat iron candlestick, but with a composite candle. Ivan saw at once from Smerdyakov’s face that he had completely recovered from his illness. His face was fresher, fuller, his hair stood up jauntily in front, and was plastered down at the sides. He was sitting in a parti-colored, wadded dressing-gown, rather dirty and frayed, however. He had spectacles on his nose, which Ivan had never seen him wearing before. This trifling circumstance suddenly redoubled Ivan’s anger: “A creature like that and wearing spectacles!”\n\nSmerdyakov slowly raised his head and looked intently at his visitor through his spectacles; then he slowly took them off and rose from the bench, but by no means respectfully, almost lazily, doing the least possible required by common civility. All this struck Ivan instantly; he took it all in and noted it at once—most of all the look in Smerdyakov’s eyes, positively malicious, churlish and haughty. “What do you want to intrude for?” it seemed to say; “we settled everything then; why have you come again?” Ivan could scarcely control himself.\n\n“It’s hot here,” he said, still standing, and unbuttoned his overcoat.\n\n“Take off your coat,” Smerdyakov conceded.\n\nIvan took off his coat and threw it on a bench with trembling hands. He took a chair, moved it quickly to the table and sat down. Smerdyakov managed to sit down on his bench before him.\n\n“To begin with, are we alone?” Ivan asked sternly and impulsively. “Can they overhear us in there?”\n\n“No one can hear anything. You’ve seen for yourself: there’s a passage.”\n\n“Listen, my good fellow; what was that you babbled, as I was leaving the hospital, that if I said nothing about your faculty of shamming fits, you wouldn’t tell the investigating lawyer all our conversation at the gate? What do you mean by all? What could you mean by it? Were you threatening me? Have I entered into some sort of compact with you? Do you suppose I am afraid of you?”\n\nIvan said this in a perfect fury, giving him to understand with obvious intention that he scorned any subterfuge or indirectness and meant to show his cards. Smerdyakov’s eyes gleamed resentfully, his left eye winked, and he at once gave his answer, with his habitual composure and deliberation. “You want to have everything above-board; very well, you shall have it,” he seemed to say.\n\n“This is what I meant then, and this is why I said that, that you, knowing beforehand of this murder of your own parent, left him to his fate, and that people mightn’t after that conclude any evil about your feelings and perhaps of something else, too—that’s what I promised not to tell the authorities.”\n\nThough Smerdyakov spoke without haste and obviously controlling himself, yet there was something in his voice, determined and emphatic, resentful and insolently defiant. He stared impudently at Ivan. A mist passed before Ivan’s eyes for the first moment.\n\n“How? What? Are you out of your mind?”\n\n“I’m perfectly in possession of all my faculties.”\n\n“Do you suppose I knew of the murder?” Ivan cried at last, and he brought his fist violently on the table. “What do you mean by ‘something else, too’? Speak, scoundrel!”\n\nSmerdyakov was silent and still scanned Ivan with the same insolent stare.\n\n“Speak, you stinking rogue, what is that ‘something else, too’?”\n\n“The ‘something else’ I meant was that you probably, too, were very desirous of your parent’s death.”\n\nIvan jumped up and struck him with all his might on the shoulder, so that he fell back against the wall. In an instant his face was bathed in tears. Saying, “It’s a shame, sir, to strike a sick man,” he dried his eyes with a very dirty blue check handkerchief and sank into quiet weeping. A minute passed.\n\n“That’s enough! Leave off,” Ivan said peremptorily, sitting down again. “Don’t put me out of all patience.”\n\nSmerdyakov took the rag from his eyes. Every line of his puckered face reflected the insult he had just received.\n\n“So you thought then, you scoundrel, that together with Dmitri I meant to kill my father?”\n\n“I didn’t know what thoughts were in your mind then,” said Smerdyakov resentfully; “and so I stopped you then at the gate to sound you on that very point.”\n\n“To sound what, what?”\n\n“Why, that very circumstance, whether you wanted your father to be murdered or not.”\n\nWhat infuriated Ivan more than anything was the aggressive, insolent tone to which Smerdyakov persistently adhered.\n\n“It was you murdered him?” he cried suddenly.\n\nSmerdyakov smiled contemptuously.\n\n“You know of yourself, for a fact, that it wasn’t I murdered him. And I should have thought that there was no need for a sensible man to speak of it again.”\n\n“But why, why had you such a suspicion about me at the time?”\n\n“As you know already, it was simply from fear. For I was in such a position, shaking with fear, that I suspected every one. I resolved to sound you, too, for I thought if you wanted the same as your brother, then the business was as good as settled and I should be crushed like a fly, too.”\n\n“Look here, you didn’t say that a fortnight ago.”\n\n“I meant the same when I talked to you in the hospital, only I thought you’d understand without wasting words, and that being such a sensible man you wouldn’t care to talk of it openly.”\n\n“What next! Come answer, answer, I insist: what was it ... what could I have done to put such a degrading suspicion into your mean soul?”\n\n“As for the murder, you couldn’t have done that and didn’t want to, but as for wanting some one else to do it, that was just what you did want.”\n\n“And how coolly, how coolly he speaks! But why should I have wanted it; what grounds had I for wanting it?”\n\n“What grounds had you? What about the inheritance?” said Smerdyakov sarcastically, and, as it were, vindictively. “Why, after your parent’s death there was at least forty thousand to come to each of you, and very likely more, but if Fyodor Pavlovitch got married then to that lady, Agrafena Alexandrovna, she would have had all his capital made over to her directly after the wedding, for she’s plenty of sense, so that your parent would not have left you two roubles between the three of you. And were they far from a wedding, either? Not a hair’s-breadth: that lady had only to lift her little finger and he would have run after her to church, with his tongue out.”\n\nIvan restrained himself with painful effort.\n\n“Very good,” he commented at last. “You see, I haven’t jumped up, I haven’t knocked you down, I haven’t killed you. Speak on. So, according to you, I had fixed on Dmitri to do it; I was reckoning on him?”\n\n“How could you help reckoning on him? If he killed him, then he would lose all the rights of a nobleman, his rank and property, and would go off to exile; so his share of the inheritance would come to you and your brother Alexey Fyodorovitch in equal parts; so you’d each have not forty, but sixty thousand each. There’s not a doubt you did reckon on Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”\n\n“What I put up with from you! Listen, scoundrel, if I had reckoned on any one then, it would have been on you, not on Dmitri, and I swear I did expect some wickedness from you ... at the time.... I remember my impression!”\n\n“I thought, too, for a minute, at the time, that you were reckoning on me as well,” said Smerdyakov, with a sarcastic grin. “So that it was just by that more than anything you showed me what was in your mind. For if you had a foreboding about me and yet went away, you as good as said to me, ‘You can murder my parent, I won’t hinder you!’”\n\n“You scoundrel! So that’s how you understood it!”\n\n“It was all that going to Tchermashnya. Why! You were meaning to go to Moscow and refused all your father’s entreaties to go to Tchermashnya—and simply at a foolish word from me you consented at once! What reason had you to consent to Tchermashnya? Since you went to Tchermashnya with no reason, simply at my word, it shows that you must have expected something from me.”\n\n“No, I swear I didn’t!” shouted Ivan, grinding his teeth.\n\n“You didn’t? Then you ought, as your father’s son, to have had me taken to the lock-up and thrashed at once for my words then ... or at least, to have given me a punch in the face on the spot, but you were not a bit angry, if you please, and at once in a friendly way acted on my foolish word and went away, which was utterly absurd, for you ought to have stayed to save your parent’s life. How could I help drawing my conclusions?”\n\nIvan sat scowling, both his fists convulsively pressed on his knees.\n\n“Yes, I am sorry I didn’t punch you in the face,” he said with a bitter smile. “I couldn’t have taken you to the lock-up just then. Who would have believed me and what charge could I bring against you? But the punch in the face ... oh, I’m sorry I didn’t think of it. Though blows are forbidden, I should have pounded your ugly face to a jelly.”\n\nSmerdyakov looked at him almost with relish.\n\n“In the ordinary occasions of life,” he said in the same complacent and sententious tone in which he had taunted Grigory and argued with him about religion at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s table, “in the ordinary occasions of life, blows on the face are forbidden nowadays by law, and people have given them up, but in exceptional occasions of life people still fly to blows, not only among us but all over the world, be it even the fullest Republic of France, just as in the time of Adam and Eve, and they never will leave off, but you, even in an exceptional case, did not dare.”\n\n“What are you learning French words for?” Ivan nodded towards the exercise-book lying on the table.\n\n“Why shouldn’t I learn them so as to improve my education, supposing that I may myself chance to go some day to those happy parts of Europe?”\n\n“Listen, monster.” Ivan’s eyes flashed and he trembled all over. “I am not afraid of your accusations; you can say what you like about me, and if I don’t beat you to death, it’s simply because I suspect you of that crime and I’ll drag you to justice. I’ll unmask you.”\n\n“To my thinking, you’d better keep quiet, for what can you accuse me of, considering my absolute innocence? and who would believe you? Only if you begin, I shall tell everything, too, for I must defend myself.”\n\n“Do you think I am afraid of you now?”\n\n“If the court doesn’t believe all I’ve said to you just now, the public will, and you will be ashamed.”\n\n“That’s as much as to say, ‘It’s always worth while speaking to a sensible man,’ eh?” snarled Ivan.\n\n“You hit the mark, indeed. And you’d better be sensible.”\n\nIvan got up, shaking all over with indignation, put on his coat, and without replying further to Smerdyakov, without even looking at him, walked quickly out of the cottage. The cool evening air refreshed him. There was a bright moon in the sky. A nightmare of ideas and sensations filled his soul. “Shall I go at once and give information against Smerdyakov? But what information can I give? He is not guilty, anyway. On the contrary, he’ll accuse me. And in fact, why did I set off for Tchermashnya then? What for? What for?” Ivan asked himself. “Yes, of course, I was expecting something and he is right....” And he remembered for the hundredth time how, on the last night in his father’s house, he had listened on the stairs. But he remembered it now with such anguish that he stood still on the spot as though he had been stabbed. “Yes, I expected it then, that’s true! I wanted the murder, I did want the murder! Did I want the murder? Did I want it? I must kill Smerdyakov! If I don’t dare kill Smerdyakov now, life is not worth living!”\n\nIvan did not go home, but went straight to Katerina Ivanovna and alarmed her by his appearance. He was like a madman. He repeated all his conversation with Smerdyakov, every syllable of it. He couldn’t be calmed, however much she tried to soothe him: he kept walking about the room, speaking strangely, disconnectedly. At last he sat down, put his elbows on the table, leaned his head on his hands and pronounced this strange sentence: “If it’s not Dmitri, but Smerdyakov who’s the murderer, I share his guilt, for I put him up to it. Whether I did, I don’t know yet. But if he is the murderer, and not Dmitri, then, of course, I am the murderer, too.”\n\nWhen Katerina Ivanovna heard that, she got up from her seat without a word, went to her writing-table, opened a box standing on it, took out a sheet of paper and laid it before Ivan. This was the document of which Ivan spoke to Alyosha later on as a “conclusive proof” that Dmitri had killed his father. It was the letter written by Mitya to Katerina Ivanovna when he was drunk, on the very evening he met Alyosha at the crossroads on the way to the monastery, after the scene at Katerina Ivanovna’s, when Grushenka had insulted her. Then, parting from Alyosha, Mitya had rushed to Grushenka. I don’t know whether he saw her, but in the evening he was at the “Metropolis,” where he got thoroughly drunk. Then he asked for pen and paper and wrote a document of weighty consequences to himself. It was a wordy, disconnected, frantic letter, a drunken letter in fact. It was like the talk of a drunken man, who, on his return home, begins with extraordinary heat telling his wife or one of his household how he has just been insulted, what a rascal had just insulted him, what a fine fellow he is on the other hand, and how he will pay that scoundrel out; and all that at great length, with great excitement and incoherence, with drunken tears and blows on the table. The letter was written on a dirty piece of ordinary paper of the cheapest kind. It had been provided by the tavern and there were figures scrawled on the back of it. There was evidently not space enough for his drunken verbosity and Mitya not only filled the margins but had written the last line right across the rest. The letter ran as follows:\n\nFATAL KATYA: To-morrow I will get the money and repay your three thousand and farewell, woman of great wrath, but farewell, too, my love! Let us make an end! To-morrow I shall try and get it from every one, and if I can’t borrow it, I give you my word of honor I shall go to my father and break his skull and take the money from under the pillow, if only Ivan has gone. If I have to go to Siberia for it, I’ll give you back your three thousand. And farewell. I bow down to the ground before you, for I’ve been a scoundrel to you. Forgive me! No, better not forgive me, you’ll be happier and so shall I! Better Siberia than your love, for I love another woman and you got to know her too well to-day, so how can you forgive? I will murder the man who’s robbed me! I’ll leave you all and go to the East so as to see no one again. Not her either, for you are not my only tormentress; she is too. Farewell! P.S.—I write my curse, but I adore you! I hear it in my heart. One string is left, and it vibrates. Better tear my heart in two! I shall kill myself, but first of all that cur. I shall tear three thousand from him and fling it to you. Though I’ve been a scoundrel to you, I am not a thief! You can expect three thousand. The cur keeps it under his mattress, in pink ribbon. I am not a thief, but I’ll murder my thief. Katya, don’t look disdainful. Dmitri is not a thief! but a murderer! He has murdered his father and ruined himself to hold his ground, rather than endure your pride. And he doesn’t love you. P.P.S.—I kiss your feet, farewell! P.P.P.S.—Katya, pray to God that some one’ll give me the money. Then I shall not be steeped in gore, and if no one does—I shall! Kill me!\n\nYour slave and enemy, D. KARAMAZOV.\n\nWhen Ivan read this “document” he was convinced. So then it was his brother, not Smerdyakov. And if not Smerdyakov, then not he, Ivan. This letter at once assumed in his eyes the aspect of a logical proof. There could be no longer the slightest doubt of Mitya’s guilt. The suspicion never occurred to Ivan, by the way, that Mitya might have committed the murder in conjunction with Smerdyakov, and, indeed, such a theory did not fit in with the facts. Ivan was completely reassured. The next morning he only thought of Smerdyakov and his gibes with contempt. A few days later he positively wondered how he could have been so horribly distressed at his suspicions. He resolved to dismiss him with contempt and forget him. So passed a month. He made no further inquiry about Smerdyakov, but twice he happened to hear that he was very ill and out of his mind.\n\n“He’ll end in madness,” the young doctor Varvinsky observed about him, and Ivan remembered this. During the last week of that month Ivan himself began to feel very ill. He went to consult the Moscow doctor who had been sent for by Katerina Ivanovna just before the trial. And just at that time his relations with Katerina Ivanovna became acutely strained. They were like two enemies in love with one another. Katerina Ivanovna’s “returns” to Mitya, that is, her brief but violent revulsions of feeling in his favor, drove Ivan to perfect frenzy. Strange to say, until that last scene described above, when Alyosha came from Mitya to Katerina Ivanovna, Ivan had never once, during that month, heard her express a doubt of Mitya’s guilt, in spite of those “returns” that were so hateful to him. It is remarkable, too, that while he felt that he hated Mitya more and more every day, he realized that it was not on account of Katya’s “returns” that he hated him, but just because he was the murderer of his father. He was conscious of this and fully recognized it to himself.\n\nNevertheless, he went to see Mitya ten days before the trial and proposed to him a plan of escape—a plan he had obviously thought over a long time. He was partly impelled to do this by a sore place still left in his heart from a phrase of Smerdyakov’s, that it was to his, Ivan’s, advantage that his brother should be convicted, as that would increase his inheritance and Alyosha’s from forty to sixty thousand roubles. He determined to sacrifice thirty thousand on arranging Mitya’s escape. On his return from seeing him, he was very mournful and dispirited; he suddenly began to feel that he was anxious for Mitya’s escape, not only to heal that sore place by sacrificing thirty thousand, but for another reason. “Is it because I am as much a murderer at heart?” he asked himself. Something very deep down seemed burning and rankling in his soul. His pride above all suffered cruelly all that month. But of that later....\n\nWhen, after his conversation with Alyosha, Ivan suddenly decided with his hand on the bell of his lodging to go to Smerdyakov, he obeyed a sudden and peculiar impulse of indignation. He suddenly remembered how Katerina Ivanovna had only just cried out to him in Alyosha’s presence: “It was you, you, persuaded me of his” (that is, Mitya’s) “guilt!” Ivan was thunderstruck when he recalled it. He had never once tried to persuade her that Mitya was the murderer; on the contrary, he had suspected himself in her presence, that time when he came back from Smerdyakov. It was she, she, who had produced that “document” and proved his brother’s guilt. And now she suddenly exclaimed: “I’ve been at Smerdyakov’s myself!” When had she been there? Ivan had known nothing of it. So she was not at all so sure of Mitya’s guilt! And what could Smerdyakov have told her? What, what, had he said to her? His heart burned with violent anger. He could not understand how he could, half an hour before, have let those words pass and not have cried out at the moment. He let go of the bell and rushed off to Smerdyakov. “I shall kill him, perhaps, this time,” he thought on the way.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Third And Last Interview With Smerdyakov\n\nWhen he was half-way there, the keen dry wind that had been blowing early that morning rose again, and a fine dry snow began falling thickly. It did not lie on the ground, but was whirled about by the wind, and soon there was a regular snowstorm. There were scarcely any lamp-posts in the part of the town where Smerdyakov lived. Ivan strode alone in the darkness, unconscious of the storm, instinctively picking out his way. His head ached and there was a painful throbbing in his temples. He felt that his hands were twitching convulsively. Not far from Marya Kondratyevna’s cottage, Ivan suddenly came upon a solitary drunken little peasant. He was wearing a coarse and patched coat, and was walking in zigzags, grumbling and swearing to himself. Then suddenly he would begin singing in a husky drunken voice:\n\n“Ach, Vanka’s gone to Petersburg; I won’t wait till he comes back.”\n\nBut he broke off every time at the second line and began swearing again; then he would begin the same song again. Ivan felt an intense hatred for him before he had thought about him at all. Suddenly he realized his presence and felt an irresistible impulse to knock him down. At that moment they met, and the peasant with a violent lurch fell full tilt against Ivan, who pushed him back furiously. The peasant went flying backwards and fell like a log on the frozen ground. He uttered one plaintive “O—oh!” and then was silent. Ivan stepped up to him. He was lying on his back, without movement or consciousness. “He will be frozen,” thought Ivan, and he went on his way to Smerdyakov’s.\n\nIn the passage, Marya Kondratyevna, who ran out to open the door with a candle in her hand, whispered that Smerdyakov was very ill, “It’s not that he’s laid up, but he seems not himself, and he even told us to take the tea away; he wouldn’t have any.”\n\n“Why, does he make a row?” asked Ivan coarsely.\n\n“Oh, dear, no, quite the contrary, he’s very quiet. Only please don’t talk to him too long,” Marya Kondratyevna begged him. Ivan opened the door and stepped into the room.\n\nIt was over-heated as before, but there were changes in the room. One of the benches at the side had been removed, and in its place had been put a large old mahogany leather sofa, on which a bed had been made up, with fairly clean white pillows. Smerdyakov was sitting on the sofa, wearing the same dressing-gown. The table had been brought out in front of the sofa, so that there was hardly room to move. On the table lay a thick book in yellow cover, but Smerdyakov was not reading it. He seemed to be sitting doing nothing. He met Ivan with a slow silent gaze, and was apparently not at all surprised at his coming. There was a great change in his face; he was much thinner and sallower. His eyes were sunken and there were blue marks under them.\n\n“Why, you really are ill?” Ivan stopped short. “I won’t keep you long, I won’t even take off my coat. Where can one sit down?”\n\nHe went to the other end of the table, moved up a chair and sat down on it.\n\n“Why do you look at me without speaking? I’ve only come with one question, and I swear I won’t go without an answer. Has the young lady, Katerina Ivanovna, been with you?”\n\nSmerdyakov still remained silent, looking quietly at Ivan as before. Suddenly, with a motion of his hand, he turned his face away.\n\n“What’s the matter with you?” cried Ivan.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘nothing’?”\n\n“Yes, she has. It’s no matter to you. Let me alone.”\n\n“No, I won’t let you alone. Tell me, when was she here?”\n\n“Why, I’d quite forgotten about her,” said Smerdyakov, with a scornful smile, and turning his face to Ivan again, he stared at him with a look of frenzied hatred, the same look that he had fixed on him at their last interview, a month before.\n\n“You seem very ill yourself, your face is sunken; you don’t look like yourself,” he said to Ivan.\n\n“Never mind my health, tell me what I ask you.”\n\n“But why are your eyes so yellow? The whites are quite yellow. Are you so worried?” He smiled contemptuously and suddenly laughed outright.\n\n“Listen; I’ve told you I won’t go away without an answer!” Ivan cried, intensely irritated.\n\n“Why do you keep pestering me? Why do you torment me?” said Smerdyakov, with a look of suffering.\n\n“Damn it! I’ve nothing to do with you. Just answer my question and I’ll go away.”\n\n“I’ve no answer to give you,” said Smerdyakov, looking down again.\n\n“You may be sure I’ll make you answer!”\n\n“Why are you so uneasy?” Smerdyakov stared at him, not simply with contempt, but almost with repulsion. “Is this because the trial begins to-morrow? Nothing will happen to you; can’t you believe that at last? Go home, go to bed and sleep in peace, don’t be afraid of anything.”\n\n“I don’t understand you.... What have I to be afraid of to-morrow?” Ivan articulated in astonishment, and suddenly a chill breath of fear did in fact pass over his soul. Smerdyakov measured him with his eyes.\n\n“You don’t understand?” he drawled reproachfully. “It’s a strange thing a sensible man should care to play such a farce!”\n\nIvan looked at him speechless. The startling, incredibly supercilious tone of this man who had once been his valet, was extraordinary in itself. He had not taken such a tone even at their last interview.\n\n“I tell you, you’ve nothing to be afraid of. I won’t say anything about you; there’s no proof against you. I say, how your hands are trembling! Why are your fingers moving like that? Go home, you did not murder him.”\n\nIvan started. He remembered Alyosha.\n\n“I know it was not I,” he faltered.\n\n“Do you?” Smerdyakov caught him up again.\n\nIvan jumped up and seized him by the shoulder.\n\n“Tell me everything, you viper! Tell me everything!”\n\nSmerdyakov was not in the least scared. He only riveted his eyes on Ivan with insane hatred.\n\n“Well, it was you who murdered him, if that’s it,” he whispered furiously.\n\nIvan sank back on his chair, as though pondering something. He laughed malignantly.\n\n“You mean my going away. What you talked about last time?”\n\n“You stood before me last time and understood it all, and you understand it now.”\n\n“All I understand is that you are mad.”\n\n“Aren’t you tired of it? Here we are face to face; what’s the use of going on keeping up a farce to each other? Are you still trying to throw it all on me, to my face? You murdered him; you are the real murderer, I was only your instrument, your faithful servant, and it was following your words I did it.”\n\n“Did it? Why, did you murder him?” Ivan turned cold.\n\nSomething seemed to give way in his brain, and he shuddered all over with a cold shiver. Then Smerdyakov himself looked at him wonderingly; probably the genuineness of Ivan’s horror struck him.\n\n“You don’t mean to say you really did not know?” he faltered mistrustfully, looking with a forced smile into his eyes. Ivan still gazed at him, and seemed unable to speak.\n\nAch, Vanka’s gone to Petersburg; I won’t wait till he comes back,\n\nsuddenly echoed in his head.\n\n“Do you know, I am afraid that you are a dream, a phantom sitting before me,” he muttered.\n\n“There’s no phantom here, but only us two and one other. No doubt he is here, that third, between us.”\n\n“Who is he? Who is here? What third person?” Ivan cried in alarm, looking about him, his eyes hastily searching in every corner.\n\n“That third is God Himself—Providence. He is the third beside us now. Only don’t look for Him, you won’t find Him.”\n\n“It’s a lie that you killed him!” Ivan cried madly. “You are mad, or teasing me again!”\n\nSmerdyakov, as before, watched him curiously, with no sign of fear. He could still scarcely get over his incredulity; he still fancied that Ivan knew everything and was trying to “throw it all on him to his face.”\n\n“Wait a minute,” he said at last in a weak voice, and suddenly bringing up his left leg from under the table, he began turning up his trouser leg. He was wearing long white stockings and slippers. Slowly he took off his garter and fumbled to the bottom of his stocking. Ivan gazed at him, and suddenly shuddered in a paroxysm of terror.\n\n“He’s mad!” he cried, and rapidly jumping up, he drew back, so that he knocked his back against the wall and stood up against it, stiff and straight. He looked with insane terror at Smerdyakov, who, entirely unaffected by his terror, continued fumbling in his stocking, as though he were making an effort to get hold of something with his fingers and pull it out. At last he got hold of it and began pulling it out. Ivan saw that it was a piece of paper, or perhaps a roll of papers. Smerdyakov pulled it out and laid it on the table.\n\n“Here,” he said quietly.\n\n“What is it?” asked Ivan, trembling.\n\n“Kindly look at it,” Smerdyakov answered, still in the same low tone.\n\nIvan stepped up to the table, took up the roll of paper and began unfolding it, but suddenly he drew back his fingers, as though from contact with a loathsome reptile.\n\n“Your hands keep twitching,” observed Smerdyakov, and he deliberately unfolded the bundle himself. Under the wrapper were three packets of hundred-rouble notes.\n\n“They are all here, all the three thousand roubles; you need not count them. Take them,” Smerdyakov suggested to Ivan, nodding at the notes. Ivan sank back in his chair. He was as white as a handkerchief.\n\n“You frightened me ... with your stocking,” he said, with a strange grin.\n\n“Can you really not have known till now?” Smerdyakov asked once more.\n\n“No, I did not know. I kept thinking of Dmitri. Brother, brother! Ach!” He suddenly clutched his head in both hands.\n\n“Listen. Did you kill him alone? With my brother’s help or without?”\n\n“It was only with you, with your help, I killed him, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch is quite innocent.”\n\n“All right, all right. Talk about me later. Why do I keep on trembling? I can’t speak properly.”\n\n“You were bold enough then. You said ‘everything was lawful,’ and how frightened you are now,” Smerdyakov muttered in surprise. “Won’t you have some lemonade? I’ll ask for some at once. It’s very refreshing. Only I must hide this first.”\n\nAnd again he motioned at the notes. He was just going to get up and call at the door to Marya Kondratyevna to make some lemonade and bring it them, but, looking for something to cover up the notes that she might not see them, he first took out his handkerchief, and as it turned out to be very dirty, took up the big yellow book that Ivan had noticed at first lying on the table, and put it over the notes. The book was The Sayings of the Holy Father Isaac the Syrian. Ivan read it mechanically.\n\n“I won’t have any lemonade,” he said. “Talk of me later. Sit down and tell me how you did it. Tell me all about it.”\n\n“You’d better take off your greatcoat, or you’ll be too hot.” Ivan, as though he’d only just thought of it, took off his coat, and, without getting up from his chair, threw it on the bench.\n\n“Speak, please, speak.”\n\nHe seemed calmer. He waited, feeling sure that Smerdyakov would tell him all about it.\n\n“How it was done?” sighed Smerdyakov. “It was done in a most natural way, following your very words.”\n\n“Of my words later,” Ivan broke in again, apparently with complete self-possession, firmly uttering his words, and not shouting as before. “Only tell me in detail how you did it. Everything, as it happened. Don’t forget anything. The details, above everything, the details, I beg you.”\n\n“You’d gone away, then I fell into the cellar.”\n\n“In a fit or in a sham one?”\n\n“A sham one, naturally. I shammed it all. I went quietly down the steps to the very bottom and lay down quietly, and as I lay down I gave a scream, and struggled, till they carried me out.”\n\n“Stay! And were you shamming all along, afterwards, and in the hospital?”\n\n“No, not at all. Next day, in the morning, before they took me to the hospital, I had a real attack and a more violent one than I’ve had for years. For two days I was quite unconscious.”\n\n“All right, all right. Go on.”\n\n“They laid me on the bed. I knew I’d be the other side of the partition, for whenever I was ill, Marfa Ignatyevna used to put me there, near them. She’s always been very kind to me, from my birth up. At night I moaned, but quietly. I kept expecting Dmitri Fyodorovitch to come.”\n\n“Expecting him? To come to you?”\n\n“Not to me. I expected him to come into the house, for I’d no doubt that he’d come that night, for being without me and getting no news, he’d be sure to come and climb over the fence, as he used to, and do something.”\n\n“And if he hadn’t come?”\n\n“Then nothing would have happened. I should never have brought myself to it without him.”\n\n“All right, all right ... speak more intelligibly, don’t hurry; above all, don’t leave anything out!”\n\n“I expected him to kill Fyodor Pavlovitch. I thought that was certain, for I had prepared him for it ... during the last few days.... He knew about the knocks, that was the chief thing. With his suspiciousness and the fury which had been growing in him all those days, he was bound to get into the house by means of those taps. That was inevitable, so I was expecting him.”\n\n“Stay,” Ivan interrupted; “if he had killed him, he would have taken the money and carried it away; you must have considered that. What would you have got by it afterwards? I don’t see.”\n\n“But he would never have found the money. That was only what I told him, that the money was under the mattress. But that wasn’t true. It had been lying in a box. And afterwards I suggested to Fyodor Pavlovitch, as I was the only person he trusted, to hide the envelope with the notes in the corner behind the ikons, for no one would have guessed that place, especially if they came in a hurry. So that’s where the envelope lay, in the corner behind the ikons. It would have been absurd to keep it under the mattress; the box, anyway, could be locked. But all believe it was under the mattress. A stupid thing to believe. So if Dmitri Fyodorovitch had committed the murder, finding nothing, he would either have run away in a hurry, afraid of every sound, as always happens with murderers, or he would have been arrested. So I could always have clambered up to the ikons and have taken away the money next morning or even that night, and it would have all been put down to Dmitri Fyodorovitch. I could reckon upon that.”\n\n“But what if he did not kill him, but only knocked him down?”\n\n“If he did not kill him, of course, I would not have ventured to take the money, and nothing would have happened. But I calculated that he would beat him senseless, and I should have time to take it then, and then I’d make out to Fyodor Pavlovitch that it was no one but Dmitri Fyodorovitch who had taken the money after beating him.”\n\n“Stop ... I am getting mixed. Then it was Dmitri after all who killed him; you only took the money?”\n\n“No, he didn’t kill him. Well, I might as well have told you now that he was the murderer.... But I don’t want to lie to you now, because ... because if you really haven’t understood till now, as I see for myself, and are not pretending, so as to throw your guilt on me to my very face, you are still responsible for it all, since you knew of the murder and charged me to do it, and went away knowing all about it. And so I want to prove to your face this evening that you are the only real murderer in the whole affair, and I am not the real murderer, though I did kill him. You are the rightful murderer.”\n\n“Why, why, am I a murderer? Oh, God!” Ivan cried, unable to restrain himself at last, and forgetting that he had put off discussing himself till the end of the conversation. “You still mean that Tchermashnya? Stay, tell me, why did you want my consent, if you really took Tchermashnya for consent? How will you explain that now?”\n\n“Assured of your consent, I should have known that you wouldn’t have made an outcry over those three thousand being lost, even if I’d been suspected, instead of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, or as his accomplice; on the contrary, you would have protected me from others.... And when you got your inheritance you would have rewarded me when you were able, all the rest of your life. For you’d have received your inheritance through me, seeing that if he had married Agrafena Alexandrovna, you wouldn’t have had a farthing.”\n\n“Ah! Then you intended to worry me all my life afterwards,” snarled Ivan. “And what if I hadn’t gone away then, but had informed against you?”\n\n“What could you have informed? That I persuaded you to go to Tchermashnya? That’s all nonsense. Besides, after our conversation you would either have gone away or have stayed. If you had stayed, nothing would have happened. I should have known that you didn’t want it done, and should have attempted nothing. As you went away, it meant you assured me that you wouldn’t dare to inform against me at the trial, and that you’d overlook my having the three thousand. And, indeed, you couldn’t have prosecuted me afterwards, because then I should have told it all in the court; that is, not that I had stolen the money or killed him—I shouldn’t have said that—but that you’d put me up to the theft and the murder, though I didn’t consent to it. That’s why I needed your consent, so that you couldn’t have cornered me afterwards, for what proof could you have had? I could always have cornered you, revealing your eagerness for your father’s death, and I tell you the public would have believed it all, and you would have been ashamed for the rest of your life.”\n\n“Was I then so eager, was I?” Ivan snarled again.\n\n“To be sure you were, and by your consent you silently sanctioned my doing it.” Smerdyakov looked resolutely at Ivan. He was very weak and spoke slowly and wearily, but some hidden inner force urged him on. He evidently had some design. Ivan felt that.\n\n“Go on,” he said. “Tell me what happened that night.”\n\n“What more is there to tell! I lay there and I thought I heard the master shout. And before that Grigory Vassilyevitch had suddenly got up and came out, and he suddenly gave a scream, and then all was silence and darkness. I lay there waiting, my heart beating; I couldn’t bear it. I got up at last, went out. I saw the window open on the left into the garden, and I stepped to the left to listen whether he was sitting there alive, and I heard the master moving about, sighing, so I knew he was alive. ‘Ech!’ I thought. I went to the window and shouted to the master, ‘It’s I.’ And he shouted to me, ‘He’s been, he’s been; he’s run away.’ He meant Dmitri Fyodorovitch had been. ‘He’s killed Grigory!’ ‘Where?’ I whispered. ‘There, in the corner,’ he pointed. He was whispering, too. ‘Wait a bit,’ I said. I went to the corner of the garden to look, and there I came upon Grigory Vassilyevitch lying by the wall, covered with blood, senseless. So it’s true that Dmitri Fyodorovitch has been here, was the thought that came into my head, and I determined on the spot to make an end of it, as Grigory Vassilyevitch, even if he were alive, would see nothing of it, as he lay there senseless. The only risk was that Marfa Ignatyevna might wake up. I felt that at the moment, but the longing to get it done came over me, till I could scarcely breathe. I went back to the window to the master and said, ‘She’s here, she’s come; Agrafena Alexandrovna has come, wants to be let in.’ And he started like a baby. ‘Where is she?’ he fairly gasped, but couldn’t believe it. ‘She’s standing there,’ said I. ‘Open.’ He looked out of the window at me, half believing and half distrustful, but afraid to open. ‘Why, he is afraid of me now,’ I thought. And it was funny. I bethought me to knock on the window-frame those taps we’d agreed upon as a signal that Grushenka had come, in his presence, before his eyes. He didn’t seem to believe my word, but as soon as he heard the taps, he ran at once to open the door. He opened it. I would have gone in, but he stood in the way to prevent me passing. ‘Where is she? Where is she?’ He looked at me, all of a tremble. ‘Well,’ thought I, ‘if he’s so frightened of me as all that, it’s a bad look out!’ And my legs went weak with fright that he wouldn’t let me in or would call out, or Marfa Ignatyevna would run up, or something else might happen. I don’t remember now, but I must have stood pale, facing him. I whispered to him, ‘Why, she’s there, there, under the window; how is it you don’t see her?’ I said. ‘Bring her then, bring her.’ ‘She’s afraid,’ said I; ‘she was frightened at the noise, she’s hidden in the bushes; go and call to her yourself from the study.’ He ran to the window, put the candle in the window. ‘Grushenka,’ he cried, ‘Grushenka, are you here?’ Though he cried that, he didn’t want to lean out of the window, he didn’t want to move away from me, for he was panic-stricken; he was so frightened he didn’t dare to turn his back on me. ‘Why, here she is,’ said I. I went up to the window and leaned right out of it. ‘Here she is; she’s in the bush, laughing at you, don’t you see her?’ He suddenly believed it; he was all of a shake—he was awfully crazy about her—and he leaned right out of the window. I snatched up that iron paper-weight from his table; do you remember, weighing about three pounds? I swung it and hit him on the top of the skull with the corner of it. He didn’t even cry out. He only sank down suddenly, and I hit him again and a third time. And the third time I knew I’d broken his skull. He suddenly rolled on his back, face upwards, covered with blood. I looked round. There was no blood on me, not a spot. I wiped the paper-weight, put it back, went up to the ikons, took the money out of the envelope, and flung the envelope on the floor and the pink ribbon beside it. I went out into the garden all of a tremble, straight to the apple-tree with a hollow in it—you know that hollow. I’d marked it long before and put a rag and a piece of paper ready in it. I wrapped all the notes in the rag and stuffed it deep down in the hole. And there it stayed for over a fortnight. I took it out later, when I came out of the hospital. I went back to my bed, lay down and thought, ‘If Grigory Vassilyevitch has been killed outright it may be a bad job for me, but if he is not killed and recovers, it will be first-rate, for then he’ll bear witness that Dmitri Fyodorovitch has been here, and so he must have killed him and taken the money.’ Then I began groaning with suspense and impatience, so as to wake Marfa Ignatyevna as soon as possible. At last she got up and she rushed to me, but when she saw Grigory Vassilyevitch was not there, she ran out, and I heard her scream in the garden. And that set it all going and set my mind at rest.”\n\nHe stopped. Ivan had listened all the time in dead silence without stirring or taking his eyes off him. As he told his story Smerdyakov glanced at him from time to time, but for the most part kept his eyes averted. When he had finished he was evidently agitated and was breathing hard. The perspiration stood out on his face. But it was impossible to tell whether it was remorse he was feeling, or what.\n\n“Stay,” cried Ivan, pondering. “What about the door? If he only opened the door to you, how could Grigory have seen it open before? For Grigory saw it before you went.”\n\nIt was remarkable that Ivan spoke quite amicably, in a different tone, not angry as before, so if any one had opened the door at that moment and peeped in at them, he would certainly have concluded that they were talking peaceably about some ordinary, though interesting, subject.\n\n“As for that door and Grigory Vassilyevitch’s having seen it open, that’s only his fancy,” said Smerdyakov, with a wry smile. “He is not a man, I assure you, but an obstinate mule. He didn’t see it, but fancied he had seen it, and there’s no shaking him. It’s just our luck he took that notion into his head, for they can’t fail to convict Dmitri Fyodorovitch after that.”\n\n“Listen ...” said Ivan, beginning to seem bewildered again and making an effort to grasp something. “Listen. There are a lot of questions I want to ask you, but I forget them ... I keep forgetting and getting mixed up. Yes. Tell me this at least, why did you open the envelope and leave it there on the floor? Why didn’t you simply carry off the envelope?... When you were telling me, I thought you spoke about it as though it were the right thing to do ... but why, I can’t understand....”\n\n“I did that for a good reason. For if a man had known all about it, as I did for instance, if he’d seen those notes before, and perhaps had put them in that envelope himself, and had seen the envelope sealed up and addressed, with his own eyes, if such a man had done the murder, what should have made him tear open the envelope afterwards, especially in such desperate haste, since he’d know for certain the notes must be in the envelope? No, if the robber had been some one like me, he’d simply have put the envelope straight in his pocket and got away with it as fast as he could. But it’d be quite different with Dmitri Fyodorovitch. He only knew about the envelope by hearsay; he had never seen it, and if he’d found it, for instance, under the mattress, he’d have torn it open as quickly as possible to make sure the notes were in it. And he’d have thrown the envelope down, without having time to think that it would be evidence against him. Because he was not an habitual thief and had never directly stolen anything before, for he is a gentleman born, and if he did bring himself to steal, it would not be regular stealing, but simply taking what was his own, for he’d told the whole town he meant to before, and had even bragged aloud before every one that he’d go and take his property from Fyodor Pavlovitch. I didn’t say that openly to the prosecutor when I was being examined, but quite the contrary, I brought him to it by a hint, as though I didn’t see it myself, and as though he’d thought of it himself and I hadn’t prompted him; so that Mr. Prosecutor’s mouth positively watered at my suggestion.”\n\n“But can you possibly have thought of all that on the spot?” cried Ivan, overcome with astonishment. He looked at Smerdyakov again with alarm.\n\n“Mercy on us! Could any one think of it all in such a desperate hurry? It was all thought out beforehand.”\n\n“Well ... well, it was the devil helped you!” Ivan cried again. “No, you are not a fool, you are far cleverer than I thought....”\n\nHe got up, obviously intending to walk across the room. He was in terrible distress. But as the table blocked his way, and there was hardly room to pass between the table and the wall, he only turned round where he stood and sat down again. Perhaps the impossibility of moving irritated him, as he suddenly cried out almost as furiously as before.\n\n“Listen, you miserable, contemptible creature! Don’t you understand that if I haven’t killed you, it’s simply because I am keeping you to answer to-morrow at the trial. God sees,” Ivan raised his hand, “perhaps I, too, was guilty; perhaps I really had a secret desire for my father’s ... death, but I swear I was not as guilty as you think, and perhaps I didn’t urge you on at all. No, no, I didn’t urge you on! But no matter, I will give evidence against myself to-morrow at the trial. I’m determined to! I shall tell everything, everything. But we’ll make our appearance together. And whatever you may say against me at the trial, whatever evidence you give, I’ll face it; I am not afraid of you. I’ll confirm it all myself! But you must confess, too! You must, you must; we’ll go together. That’s how it shall be!”\n\nIvan said this solemnly and resolutely and from his flashing eyes alone it could be seen that it would be so.\n\n“You are ill, I see; you are quite ill. Your eyes are yellow,” Smerdyakov commented, without the least irony, with apparent sympathy in fact.\n\n“We’ll go together,” Ivan repeated. “And if you won’t go, no matter, I’ll go alone.”\n\nSmerdyakov paused as though pondering.\n\n“There’ll be nothing of the sort, and you won’t go,” he concluded at last positively.\n\n“You don’t understand me,” Ivan exclaimed reproachfully.\n\n“You’ll be too much ashamed, if you confess it all. And, what’s more, it will be no use at all, for I shall say straight out that I never said anything of the sort to you, and that you are either ill (and it looks like it, too), or that you’re so sorry for your brother that you are sacrificing yourself to save him and have invented it all against me, for you’ve always thought no more of me than if I’d been a fly. And who will believe you, and what single proof have you got?”\n\n“Listen, you showed me those notes just now to convince me.”\n\nSmerdyakov lifted the book off the notes and laid it on one side.\n\n“Take that money away with you,” Smerdyakov sighed.\n\n“Of course, I shall take it. But why do you give it to me, if you committed the murder for the sake of it?” Ivan looked at him with great surprise.\n\n“I don’t want it,” Smerdyakov articulated in a shaking voice, with a gesture of refusal. “I did have an idea of beginning a new life with that money in Moscow or, better still, abroad. I did dream of it, chiefly because ‘all things are lawful.’ That was quite right what you taught me, for you talked a lot to me about that. For if there’s no everlasting God, there’s no such thing as virtue, and there’s no need of it. You were right there. So that’s how I looked at it.”\n\n“Did you come to that of yourself?” asked Ivan, with a wry smile.\n\n“With your guidance.”\n\n“And now, I suppose, you believe in God, since you are giving back the money?”\n\n“No, I don’t believe,” whispered Smerdyakov.\n\n“Then why are you giving it back?”\n\n“Leave off ... that’s enough!” Smerdyakov waved his hand again. “You used to say yourself that everything was lawful, so now why are you so upset, too? You even want to go and give evidence against yourself.... Only there’ll be nothing of the sort! You won’t go to give evidence,” Smerdyakov decided with conviction.\n\n“You’ll see,” said Ivan.\n\n“It isn’t possible. You are very clever. You are fond of money, I know that. You like to be respected, too, for you’re very proud; you are far too fond of female charms, too, and you mind most of all about living in undisturbed comfort, without having to depend on any one—that’s what you care most about. You won’t want to spoil your life for ever by taking such a disgrace on yourself. You are like Fyodor Pavlovitch, you are more like him than any of his children; you’ve the same soul as he had.”\n\n“You are not a fool,” said Ivan, seeming struck. The blood rushed to his face. “You are serious now!” he observed, looking suddenly at Smerdyakov with a different expression.\n\n“It was your pride made you think I was a fool. Take the money.”\n\nIvan took the three rolls of notes and put them in his pocket without wrapping them in anything.\n\n“I shall show them at the court to-morrow,” he said.\n\n“Nobody will believe you, as you’ve plenty of money of your own; you may simply have taken it out of your cash-box and brought it to the court.”\n\nIvan rose from his seat.\n\n“I repeat,” he said, “the only reason I haven’t killed you is that I need you for to-morrow, remember that, don’t forget it!”\n\n“Well, kill me. Kill me now,” Smerdyakov said, all at once looking strangely at Ivan. “You won’t dare do that even!” he added, with a bitter smile. “You won’t dare to do anything, you, who used to be so bold!”\n\n“Till to-morrow,” cried Ivan, and moved to go out.\n\n“Stay a moment.... Show me those notes again.”\n\nIvan took out the notes and showed them to him. Smerdyakov looked at them for ten seconds.\n\n“Well, you can go,” he said, with a wave of his hand. “Ivan Fyodorovitch!” he called after him again.\n\n“What do you want?” Ivan turned without stopping.\n“Good-by!”\n\n“Till to-morrow!” Ivan cried again, and he walked out of the cottage.\n\nThe snowstorm was still raging. He walked the first few steps boldly, but suddenly began staggering. “It’s something physical,” he thought with a grin. Something like joy was springing up in his heart. He was conscious of unbounded resolution; he would make an end of the wavering that had so tortured him of late. His determination was taken, “and now it will not be changed,” he thought with relief. At that moment he stumbled against something and almost fell down. Stopping short, he made out at his feet the peasant he had knocked down, still lying senseless and motionless. The snow had almost covered his face. Ivan seized him and lifted him in his arms. Seeing a light in the little house to the right he went up, knocked at the shutters, and asked the man to whom the house belonged to help him carry the peasant to the police-station, promising him three roubles. The man got ready and came out. I won’t describe in detail how Ivan succeeded in his object, bringing the peasant to the police-station and arranging for a doctor to see him at once, providing with a liberal hand for the expenses. I will only say that this business took a whole hour, but Ivan was well content with it. His mind wandered and worked incessantly.\n\n“If I had not taken my decision so firmly for to-morrow,” he reflected with satisfaction, “I should not have stayed a whole hour to look after the peasant, but should have passed by, without caring about his being frozen. I am quite capable of watching myself, by the way,” he thought at the same instant, with still greater satisfaction, “although they have decided that I am going out of my mind!”\n\nJust as he reached his own house he stopped short, asking himself suddenly hadn’t he better go at once to the prosecutor and tell him everything. He decided the question by turning back to the house. “Everything together to-morrow!” he whispered to himself, and, strange to say, almost all his gladness and self-satisfaction passed in one instant.\n\nAs he entered his own room he felt something like a touch of ice on his heart, like a recollection or, more exactly, a reminder, of something agonizing and revolting that was in that room now, at that moment, and had been there before. He sank wearily on his sofa. The old woman brought him a samovar; he made tea, but did not touch it. He sat on the sofa and felt giddy. He felt that he was ill and helpless. He was beginning to drop asleep, but got up uneasily and walked across the room to shake off his drowsiness. At moments he fancied he was delirious, but it was not illness that he thought of most. Sitting down again, he began looking round, as though searching for something. This happened several times. At last his eyes were fastened intently on one point. Ivan smiled, but an angry flush suffused his face. He sat a long time in his place, his head propped on both arms, though he looked sideways at the same point, at the sofa that stood against the opposite wall. There was evidently something, some object, that irritated him there, worried him and tormented him.\n\n# Chapter IX. The Devil. Ivan’s Nightmare\n\nI am not a doctor, but yet I feel that the moment has come when I must inevitably give the reader some account of the nature of Ivan’s illness. Anticipating events I can say at least one thing: he was at that moment on the very eve of an attack of brain fever. Though his health had long been affected, it had offered a stubborn resistance to the fever which in the end gained complete mastery over it. Though I know nothing of medicine, I venture to hazard the suggestion that he really had perhaps, by a terrible effort of will, succeeded in delaying the attack for a time, hoping, of course, to check it completely. He knew that he was unwell, but he loathed the thought of being ill at that fatal time, at the approaching crisis in his life, when he needed to have all his wits about him, to say what he had to say boldly and resolutely and “to justify himself to himself.”\n\nHe had, however, consulted the new doctor, who had been brought from Moscow by a fantastic notion of Katerina Ivanovna’s to which I have referred already. After listening to him and examining him the doctor came to the conclusion that he was actually suffering from some disorder of the brain, and was not at all surprised by an admission which Ivan had reluctantly made him. “Hallucinations are quite likely in your condition,” the doctor opined, “though it would be better to verify them ... you must take steps at once, without a moment’s delay, or things will go badly with you.” But Ivan did not follow this judicious advice and did not take to his bed to be nursed. “I am walking about, so I am strong enough, if I drop, it’ll be different then, any one may nurse me who likes,” he decided, dismissing the subject.\n\nAnd so he was sitting almost conscious himself of his delirium and, as I have said already, looking persistently at some object on the sofa against the opposite wall. Some one appeared to be sitting there, though goodness knows how he had come in, for he had not been in the room when Ivan came into it, on his return from Smerdyakov. This was a person or, more accurately speaking, a Russian gentleman of a particular kind, no longer young, qui faisait la cinquantaine, as the French say, with rather long, still thick, dark hair, slightly streaked with gray and a small pointed beard. He was wearing a brownish reefer jacket, rather shabby, evidently made by a good tailor though, and of a fashion at least three years old, that had been discarded by smart and well-to-do people for the last two years. His linen and his long scarf-like neck-tie were all such as are worn by people who aim at being stylish, but on closer inspection his linen was not over-clean and his wide scarf was very threadbare. The visitor’s check trousers were of excellent cut, but were too light in color and too tight for the present fashion. His soft fluffy white hat was out of keeping with the season.\n\nIn brief there was every appearance of gentility on straitened means. It looked as though the gentleman belonged to that class of idle landowners who used to flourish in the times of serfdom. He had unmistakably been, at some time, in good and fashionable society, had once had good connections, had possibly preserved them indeed, but, after a gay youth, becoming gradually impoverished on the abolition of serfdom, he had sunk into the position of a poor relation of the best class, wandering from one good old friend to another and received by them for his companionable and accommodating disposition and as being, after all, a gentleman who could be asked to sit down with any one, though, of course, not in a place of honor. Such gentlemen of accommodating temper and dependent position, who can tell a story, take a hand at cards, and who have a distinct aversion for any duties that may be forced upon them, are usually solitary creatures, either bachelors or widowers. Sometimes they have children, but if so, the children are always being brought up at a distance, at some aunt’s, to whom these gentlemen never allude in good society, seeming ashamed of the relationship. They gradually lose sight of their children altogether, though at intervals they receive a birthday or Christmas letter from them and sometimes even answer it.\n\nThe countenance of the unexpected visitor was not so much good-natured, as accommodating and ready to assume any amiable expression as occasion might arise. He had no watch, but he had a tortoise-shell lorgnette on a black ribbon. On the middle finger of his right hand was a massive gold ring with a cheap opal stone in it.\n\nIvan was angrily silent and would not begin the conversation. The visitor waited and sat exactly like a poor relation who had come down from his room to keep his host company at tea, and was discreetly silent, seeing that his host was frowning and preoccupied. But he was ready for any affable conversation as soon as his host should begin it. All at once his face expressed a sudden solicitude.\n\n“I say,” he began to Ivan, “excuse me, I only mention it to remind you. You went to Smerdyakov’s to find out about Katerina Ivanovna, but you came away without finding out anything about her, you probably forgot—”\n\n“Ah, yes,” broke from Ivan and his face grew gloomy with uneasiness. “Yes, I’d forgotten ... but it doesn’t matter now, never mind, till to-morrow,” he muttered to himself, “and you,” he added, addressing his visitor, “I should have remembered that myself in a minute, for that was just what was tormenting me! Why do you interfere, as if I should believe that you prompted me, and that I didn’t remember it of myself?”\n\n“Don’t believe it then,” said the gentleman, smiling amicably, “what’s the good of believing against your will? Besides, proofs are no help to believing, especially material proofs. Thomas believed, not because he saw Christ risen, but because he wanted to believe, before he saw. Look at the spiritualists, for instance.... I am very fond of them ... only fancy, they imagine that they are serving the cause of religion, because the devils show them their horns from the other world. That, they say, is a material proof, so to speak, of the existence of another world. The other world and material proofs, what next! And if you come to that, does proving there’s a devil prove that there’s a God? I want to join an idealist society, I’ll lead the opposition in it, I’ll say I am a realist, but not a materialist, he he!”\n\n“Listen,” Ivan suddenly got up from the table. “I seem to be delirious.... I am delirious, in fact, talk any nonsense you like, I don’t care! You won’t drive me to fury, as you did last time. But I feel somehow ashamed.... I want to walk about the room.... I sometimes don’t see you and don’t even hear your voice as I did last time, but I always guess what you are prating, for it’s I, I myself speaking, not you. Only I don’t know whether I was dreaming last time or whether I really saw you. I’ll wet a towel and put it on my head and perhaps you’ll vanish into air.”\n\nIvan went into the corner, took a towel, and did as he said, and with a wet towel on his head began walking up and down the room.\n\n“I am so glad you treat me so familiarly,” the visitor began.\n\n“Fool,” laughed Ivan, “do you suppose I should stand on ceremony with you? I am in good spirits now, though I’ve a pain in my forehead ... and in the top of my head ... only please don’t talk philosophy, as you did last time. If you can’t take yourself off, talk of something amusing. Talk gossip, you are a poor relation, you ought to talk gossip. What a nightmare to have! But I am not afraid of you. I’ll get the better of you. I won’t be taken to a mad-house!”\n\n“C’est charmant, poor relation. Yes, I am in my natural shape. For what am I on earth but a poor relation? By the way, I am listening to you and am rather surprised to find you are actually beginning to take me for something real, not simply your fancy, as you persisted in declaring last time—”\n\n“Never for one minute have I taken you for reality,” Ivan cried with a sort of fury. “You are a lie, you are my illness, you are a phantom. It’s only that I don’t know how to destroy you and I see I must suffer for a time. You are my hallucination. You are the incarnation of myself, but only of one side of me ... of my thoughts and feelings, but only the nastiest and stupidest of them. From that point of view you might be of interest to me, if only I had time to waste on you—”\n\n“Excuse me, excuse me, I’ll catch you. When you flew out at Alyosha under the lamp-post this evening and shouted to him, ‘You learnt it from him! How do you know that he visits me?’ you were thinking of me then. So for one brief moment you did believe that I really exist,” the gentleman laughed blandly.\n\n“Yes, that was a moment of weakness ... but I couldn’t believe in you. I don’t know whether I was asleep or awake last time. Perhaps I was only dreaming then and didn’t see you really at all—”\n\n“And why were you so surly with Alyosha just now? He is a dear; I’ve treated him badly over Father Zossima.”\n\n“Don’t talk of Alyosha! How dare you, you flunkey!” Ivan laughed again.\n\n“You scold me, but you laugh—that’s a good sign. But you are ever so much more polite than you were last time and I know why: that great resolution of yours—”\n\n“Don’t speak of my resolution,” cried Ivan, savagely.\n\n“I understand, I understand, c’est noble, c’est charmant, you are going to defend your brother and to sacrifice yourself ... C’est chevaleresque.”\n\n“Hold your tongue, I’ll kick you!”\n\n“I shan’t be altogether sorry, for then my object will be attained. If you kick me, you must believe in my reality, for people don’t kick ghosts. Joking apart, it doesn’t matter to me, scold if you like, though it’s better to be a trifle more polite even to me. ‘Fool, flunkey!’ what words!”\n\n“Scolding you, I scold myself,” Ivan laughed again, “you are myself, myself, only with a different face. You just say what I am thinking ... and are incapable of saying anything new!”\n\n“If I am like you in my way of thinking, it’s all to my credit,” the gentleman declared, with delicacy and dignity.\n\n“You choose out only my worst thoughts, and what’s more, the stupid ones. You are stupid and vulgar. You are awfully stupid. No, I can’t put up with you! What am I to do, what am I to do?” Ivan said through his clenched teeth.\n\n“My dear friend, above all things I want to behave like a gentleman and to be recognized as such,” the visitor began in an excess of deprecating and simple-hearted pride, typical of a poor relation. “I am poor, but ... I won’t say very honest, but ... it’s an axiom generally accepted in society that I am a fallen angel. I certainly can’t conceive how I can ever have been an angel. If I ever was, it must have been so long ago that there’s no harm in forgetting it. Now I only prize the reputation of being a gentlemanly person and live as I can, trying to make myself agreeable. I love men genuinely, I’ve been greatly calumniated! Here when I stay with you from time to time, my life gains a kind of reality and that’s what I like most of all. You see, like you, I suffer from the fantastic and so I love the realism of earth. Here, with you, everything is circumscribed, here all is formulated and geometrical, while we have nothing but indeterminate equations! I wander about here dreaming. I like dreaming. Besides, on earth I become superstitious. Please don’t laugh, that’s just what I like, to become superstitious. I adopt all your habits here: I’ve grown fond of going to the public baths, would you believe it? and I go and steam myself with merchants and priests. What I dream of is becoming incarnate once for all and irrevocably in the form of some merchant’s wife weighing eighteen stone, and of believing all she believes. My ideal is to go to church and offer a candle in simple-hearted faith, upon my word it is. Then there would be an end to my sufferings. I like being doctored too; in the spring there was an outbreak of smallpox and I went and was vaccinated in a foundling hospital—if only you knew how I enjoyed myself that day. I subscribed ten roubles in the cause of the Slavs!... But you are not listening. Do you know, you are not at all well this evening? I know you went yesterday to that doctor ... well, what about your health? What did the doctor say?”\n\n“Fool!” Ivan snapped out.\n\n“But you are clever, anyway. You are scolding again? I didn’t ask out of sympathy. You needn’t answer. Now rheumatism has come in again—”\n\n“Fool!” repeated Ivan.\n\n“You keep saying the same thing; but I had such an attack of rheumatism last year that I remember it to this day.”\n\n“The devil have rheumatism!”\n\n“Why not, if I sometimes put on fleshly form? I put on fleshly form and I take the consequences. Satan sum et nihil humanum a me alienum puto.”\n\n“What, what, Satan sum et nihil humanum ... that’s not bad for the devil!”\n\n“I am glad I’ve pleased you at last.”\n\n“But you didn’t get that from me.” Ivan stopped suddenly, seeming struck. “That never entered my head, that’s strange.”\n\n“C’est du nouveau, n’est-ce pas? This time I’ll act honestly and explain to you. Listen, in dreams and especially in nightmares, from indigestion or anything, a man sees sometimes such artistic visions, such complex and real actuality, such events, even a whole world of events, woven into such a plot, with such unexpected details from the most exalted matters to the last button on a cuff, as I swear Leo Tolstoy has never invented. Yet such dreams are sometimes seen not by writers, but by the most ordinary people, officials, journalists, priests.... The subject is a complete enigma. A statesman confessed to me, indeed, that all his best ideas came to him when he was asleep. Well, that’s how it is now, though I am your hallucination, yet just as in a nightmare, I say original things which had not entered your head before. So I don’t repeat your ideas, yet I am only your nightmare, nothing more.”\n\n“You are lying, your aim is to convince me you exist apart and are not my nightmare, and now you are asserting you are a dream.”\n\n“My dear fellow, I’ve adopted a special method to-day, I’ll explain it to you afterwards. Stay, where did I break off? Oh, yes! I caught cold then, only not here but yonder.”\n\n“Where is yonder? Tell me, will you be here long? Can’t you go away?” Ivan exclaimed almost in despair. He ceased walking to and fro, sat down on the sofa, leaned his elbows on the table again and held his head tight in both hands. He pulled the wet towel off and flung it away in vexation. It was evidently of no use.\n\n“Your nerves are out of order,” observed the gentleman, with a carelessly easy, though perfectly polite, air. “You are angry with me even for being able to catch cold, though it happened in a most natural way. I was hurrying then to a diplomatic soirée at the house of a lady of high rank in Petersburg, who was aiming at influence in the Ministry. Well, an evening suit, white tie, gloves, though I was God knows where and had to fly through space to reach your earth.... Of course, it took only an instant, but you know a ray of light from the sun takes full eight minutes, and fancy in an evening suit and open waistcoat. Spirits don’t freeze, but when one’s in fleshly form, well ... in brief, I didn’t think, and set off, and you know in those ethereal spaces, in the water that is above the firmament, there’s such a frost ... at least one can’t call it frost, you can fancy, 150 degrees below zero! You know the game the village girls play—they invite the unwary to lick an ax in thirty degrees of frost, the tongue instantly freezes to it and the dupe tears the skin off, so it bleeds. But that’s only in 30 degrees, in 150 degrees I imagine it would be enough to put your finger on the ax and it would be the end of it ... if only there could be an ax there.”\n\n“And can there be an ax there?” Ivan interrupted, carelessly and disdainfully. He was exerting himself to the utmost not to believe in the delusion and not to sink into complete insanity.\n\n“An ax?” the guest interrupted in surprise.\n\n“Yes, what would become of an ax there?” Ivan cried suddenly, with a sort of savage and insistent obstinacy.\n\n“What would become of an ax in space? Quelle idée! If it were to fall to any distance, it would begin, I think, flying round the earth without knowing why, like a satellite. The astronomers would calculate the rising and the setting of the ax, Gatzuk would put it in his calendar, that’s all.”\n\n“You are stupid, awfully stupid,” said Ivan peevishly. “Fib more cleverly or I won’t listen. You want to get the better of me by realism, to convince me that you exist, but I don’t want to believe you exist! I won’t believe it!”\n\n“But I am not fibbing, it’s all the truth; the truth is unhappily hardly ever amusing. I see you persist in expecting something big of me, and perhaps something fine. That’s a great pity, for I only give what I can—”\n\n“Don’t talk philosophy, you ass!”\n\n“Philosophy, indeed, when all my right side is numb and I am moaning and groaning. I’ve tried all the medical faculty: they can diagnose beautifully, they have the whole of your disease at their finger-tips, but they’ve no idea how to cure you. There was an enthusiastic little student here, ‘You may die,’ said he, ‘but you’ll know perfectly what disease you are dying of!’ And then what a way they have sending people to specialists! ‘We only diagnose,’ they say, ‘but go to such-and-such a specialist, he’ll cure you.’ The old doctor who used to cure all sorts of disease has completely disappeared, I assure you, now there are only specialists and they all advertise in the newspapers. If anything is wrong with your nose, they send you to Paris: there, they say, is a European specialist who cures noses. If you go to Paris, he’ll look at your nose; I can only cure your right nostril, he’ll tell you, for I don’t cure the left nostril, that’s not my speciality, but go to Vienna, there there’s a specialist who will cure your left nostril. What are you to do? I fell back on popular remedies, a German doctor advised me to rub myself with honey and salt in the bath-house. Solely to get an extra bath I went, smeared myself all over and it did me no good at all. In despair I wrote to Count Mattei in Milan. He sent me a book and some drops, bless him, and, only fancy, Hoff’s malt extract cured me! I bought it by accident, drank a bottle and a half of it, and I was ready to dance, it took it away completely. I made up my mind to write to the papers to thank him, I was prompted by a feeling of gratitude, and only fancy, it led to no end of a bother: not a single paper would take my letter. ‘It would be very reactionary,’ they said, ‘no one will believe it. Le diable n’existe point. You’d better remain anonymous,’ they advised me. What use is a letter of thanks if it’s anonymous? I laughed with the men at the newspaper office; ‘It’s reactionary to believe in God in our days,’ I said, ‘but I am the devil, so I may be believed in.’ ‘We quite understand that,’ they said. ‘Who doesn’t believe in the devil? Yet it won’t do, it might injure our reputation. As a joke, if you like.’ But I thought as a joke it wouldn’t be very witty. So it wasn’t printed. And do you know, I have felt sore about it to this day. My best feelings, gratitude, for instance, are literally denied me simply from my social position.”\n\n“Philosophical reflections again?” Ivan snarled malignantly.\n\n“God preserve me from it, but one can’t help complaining sometimes. I am a slandered man. You upbraid me every moment with being stupid. One can see you are young. My dear fellow, intelligence isn’t the only thing! I have naturally a kind and merry heart. ‘I also write vaudevilles of all sorts.’ You seem to take me for Hlestakov grown old, but my fate is a far more serious one. Before time was, by some decree which I could never make out, I was pre-destined ‘to deny’ and yet I am genuinely good-hearted and not at all inclined to negation. ‘No, you must go and deny, without denial there’s no criticism and what would a journal be without a column of criticism?’ Without criticism it would be nothing but one ‘hosannah.’ But nothing but hosannah is not enough for life, the hosannah must be tried in the crucible of doubt and so on, in the same style. But I don’t meddle in that, I didn’t create it, I am not answerable for it. Well, they’ve chosen their scapegoat, they’ve made me write the column of criticism and so life was made possible. We understand that comedy; I, for instance, simply ask for annihilation. No, live, I am told, for there’d be nothing without you. If everything in the universe were sensible, nothing would happen. There would be no events without you, and there must be events. So against the grain I serve to produce events and do what’s irrational because I am commanded to. For all their indisputable intelligence, men take this farce as something serious, and that is their tragedy. They suffer, of course ... but then they live, they live a real life, not a fantastic one, for suffering is life. Without suffering what would be the pleasure of it? It would be transformed into an endless church service; it would be holy, but tedious. But what about me? I suffer, but still, I don’t live. I am x in an indeterminate equation. I am a sort of phantom in life who has lost all beginning and end, and who has even forgotten his own name. You are laughing— no, you are not laughing, you are angry again. You are for ever angry, all you care about is intelligence, but I repeat again that I would give away all this super-stellar life, all the ranks and honors, simply to be transformed into the soul of a merchant’s wife weighing eighteen stone and set candles at God’s shrine.”\n\n“Then even you don’t believe in God?” said Ivan, with a smile of hatred.\n\n“What can I say?—that is, if you are in earnest—”\n\n“Is there a God or not?” Ivan cried with the same savage intensity.\n\n“Ah, then you are in earnest! My dear fellow, upon my word I don’t know. There! I’ve said it now!”\n\n“You don’t know, but you see God? No, you are not some one apart, you are myself, you are I and nothing more! You are rubbish, you are my fancy!”\n\n“Well, if you like, I have the same philosophy as you, that would be true. Je pense, donc je suis, I know that for a fact; all the rest, all these worlds, God and even Satan—all that is not proved, to my mind. Does all that exist of itself, or is it only an emanation of myself, a logical development of my ego which alone has existed for ever: but I make haste to stop, for I believe you will be jumping up to beat me directly.”\n\n“You’d better tell me some anecdote!” said Ivan miserably.\n\n“There is an anecdote precisely on our subject, or rather a legend, not an anecdote. You reproach me with unbelief, you see, you say, yet you don’t believe. But, my dear fellow, I am not the only one like that. We are all in a muddle over there now and all through your science. Once there used to be atoms, five senses, four elements, and then everything hung together somehow. There were atoms in the ancient world even, but since we’ve learned that you’ve discovered the chemical molecule and protoplasm and the devil knows what, we had to lower our crest. There’s a regular muddle, and, above all, superstition, scandal; there’s as much scandal among us as among you, you know; a little more in fact, and spying, indeed, for we have our secret police department where private information is received. Well, this wild legend belongs to our middle ages—not yours, but ours—and no one believes it even among us, except the old ladies of eighteen stone, not your old ladies I mean, but ours. We’ve everything you have, I am revealing one of our secrets out of friendship for you; though it’s forbidden. This legend is about Paradise. There was, they say, here on earth a thinker and philosopher. He rejected everything, ‘laws, conscience, faith,’ and, above all, the future life. He died; he expected to go straight to darkness and death and he found a future life before him. He was astounded and indignant. ‘This is against my principles!’ he said. And he was punished for that ... that is, you must excuse me, I am just repeating what I heard myself, it’s only a legend ... he was sentenced to walk a quadrillion kilometers in the dark (we’ve adopted the metric system, you know) and when he has finished that quadrillion, the gates of heaven would be opened to him and he’ll be forgiven—”\n\n“And what tortures have you in the other world besides the quadrillion kilometers?” asked Ivan, with a strange eagerness.\n\n“What tortures? Ah, don’t ask. In old days we had all sorts, but now they have taken chiefly to moral punishments—‘the stings of conscience’ and all that nonsense. We got that, too, from you, from the softening of your manners. And who’s the better for it? Only those who have got no conscience, for how can they be tortured by conscience when they have none? But decent people who have conscience and a sense of honor suffer for it. Reforms, when the ground has not been prepared for them, especially if they are institutions copied from abroad, do nothing but mischief! The ancient fire was better. Well, this man, who was condemned to the quadrillion kilometers, stood still, looked round and lay down across the road. ‘I won’t go, I refuse on principle!’ Take the soul of an enlightened Russian atheist and mix it with the soul of the prophet Jonah, who sulked for three days and nights in the belly of the whale, and you get the character of that thinker who lay across the road.”\n\n“What did he lie on there?”\n\n“Well, I suppose there was something to lie on. You are not laughing?”\n\n“Bravo!” cried Ivan, still with the same strange eagerness. Now he was listening with an unexpected curiosity. “Well, is he lying there now?”\n\n“That’s the point, that he isn’t. He lay there almost a thousand years and then he got up and went on.”\n\n“What an ass!” cried Ivan, laughing nervously and still seeming to be pondering something intently. “Does it make any difference whether he lies there for ever or walks the quadrillion kilometers? It would take a billion years to walk it?”\n\n“Much more than that. I haven’t got a pencil and paper or I could work it out. But he got there long ago, and that’s where the story begins.”\n\n“What, he got there? But how did he get the billion years to do it?”\n\n“Why, you keep thinking of our present earth! But our present earth may have been repeated a billion times. Why, it’s become extinct, been frozen; cracked, broken to bits, disintegrated into its elements, again ‘the water above the firmament,’ then again a comet, again a sun, again from the sun it becomes earth—and the same sequence may have been repeated endlessly and exactly the same to every detail, most unseemly and insufferably tedious—”\n\n“Well, well, what happened when he arrived?”\n\n“Why, the moment the gates of Paradise were open and he walked in, before he had been there two seconds, by his watch (though to my thinking his watch must have long dissolved into its elements on the way), he cried out that those two seconds were worth walking not a quadrillion kilometers but a quadrillion of quadrillions, raised to the quadrillionth power! In fact, he sang ‘hosannah’ and overdid it so, that some persons there of lofty ideas wouldn’t shake hands with him at first—he’d become too rapidly reactionary, they said. The Russian temperament. I repeat, it’s a legend. I give it for what it’s worth. So that’s the sort of ideas we have on such subjects even now.”\n\n“I’ve caught you!” Ivan cried, with an almost childish delight, as though he had succeeded in remembering something at last. “That anecdote about the quadrillion years, I made up myself! I was seventeen then, I was at the high school. I made up that anecdote and told it to a schoolfellow called Korovkin, it was at Moscow.... The anecdote is so characteristic that I couldn’t have taken it from anywhere. I thought I’d forgotten it ... but I’ve unconsciously recalled it—I recalled it myself—it was not you telling it! Thousands of things are unconsciously remembered like that even when people are being taken to execution ... it’s come back to me in a dream. You are that dream! You are a dream, not a living creature!”\n\n“From the vehemence with which you deny my existence,” laughed the gentleman, “I am convinced that you believe in me.”\n\n“Not in the slightest! I haven’t a hundredth part of a grain of faith in you!”\n\n“But you have the thousandth of a grain. Homeopathic doses perhaps are the strongest. Confess that you have faith even to the ten-thousandth of a grain.”\n\n“Not for one minute,” cried Ivan furiously. “But I should like to believe in you,” he added strangely.\n\n“Aha! There’s an admission! But I am good-natured. I’ll come to your assistance again. Listen, it was I caught you, not you me. I told you your anecdote you’d forgotten, on purpose, so as to destroy your faith in me completely.”\n\n“You are lying. The object of your visit is to convince me of your existence!”\n\n“Just so. But hesitation, suspense, conflict between belief and disbelief—is sometimes such torture to a conscientious man, such as you are, that it’s better to hang oneself at once. Knowing that you are inclined to believe in me, I administered some disbelief by telling you that anecdote. I lead you to belief and disbelief by turns, and I have my motive in it. It’s the new method. As soon as you disbelieve in me completely, you’ll begin assuring me to my face that I am not a dream but a reality. I know you. Then I shall have attained my object, which is an honorable one. I shall sow in you only a tiny grain of faith and it will grow into an oak-tree—and such an oak-tree that, sitting on it, you will long to enter the ranks of ‘the hermits in the wilderness and the saintly women,’ for that is what you are secretly longing for. You’ll dine on locusts, you’ll wander into the wilderness to save your soul!”\n\n“Then it’s for the salvation of my soul you are working, is it, you scoundrel?”\n\n“One must do a good work sometimes. How ill-humored you are!”\n\n“Fool! did you ever tempt those holy men who ate locusts and prayed seventeen years in the wilderness till they were overgrown with moss?”\n\n“My dear fellow, I’ve done nothing else. One forgets the whole world and all the worlds, and sticks to one such saint, because he is a very precious diamond. One such soul, you know, is sometimes worth a whole constellation. We have our system of reckoning, you know. The conquest is priceless! And some of them, on my word, are not inferior to you in culture, though you won’t believe it. They can contemplate such depths of belief and disbelief at the same moment that sometimes it really seems that they are within a hair’s-breadth of being ‘turned upside down,’ as the actor Gorbunov says.”\n\n“Well, did you get your nose pulled?”[8]\n\n“My dear fellow,” observed the visitor sententiously, “it’s better to get off with your nose pulled than without a nose at all. As an afflicted marquis observed not long ago (he must have been treated by a specialist) in confession to his spiritual father—a Jesuit. I was present, it was simply charming. ‘Give me back my nose!’ he said, and he beat his breast. ‘My son,’ said the priest evasively, ‘all things are accomplished in accordance with the inscrutable decrees of Providence, and what seems a misfortune sometimes leads to extraordinary, though unapparent, benefits. If stern destiny has deprived you of your nose, it’s to your advantage that no one can ever pull you by your nose.’ ‘Holy father, that’s no comfort,’ cried the despairing marquis. ‘I’d be delighted to have my nose pulled every day of my life, if it were only in its proper place.’ ‘My son,’ sighs the priest, ‘you can’t expect every blessing at once. This is murmuring against Providence, who even in this has not forgotten you, for if you repine as you repined just now, declaring you’d be glad to have your nose pulled for the rest of your life, your desire has already been fulfilled indirectly, for when you lost your nose, you were led by the nose.’”\n\n“Fool, how stupid!” cried Ivan.\n\n“My dear friend, I only wanted to amuse you. But I swear that’s the genuine Jesuit casuistry and I swear that it all happened word for word as I’ve told you. It happened lately and gave me a great deal of trouble. The unhappy young man shot himself that very night when he got home. I was by his side till the very last moment. Those Jesuit confessionals are really my most delightful diversion at melancholy moments. Here’s another incident that happened only the other day. A little blonde Norman girl of twenty—a buxom, unsophisticated beauty that would make your mouth water—comes to an old priest. She bends down and whispers her sin into the grating. ‘Why, my daughter, have you fallen again already?’ cries the priest. ‘O Sancta Maria, what do I hear! Not the same man this time, how long is this going on? Aren’t you ashamed!’ ‘Ah, mon père,’ answers the sinner with tears of penitence, ‘ça lui fait tant de plaisir, et à moi si peu de peine!’ Fancy, such an answer! I drew back. It was the cry of nature, better than innocence itself, if you like. I absolved her sin on the spot and was turning to go, but I was forced to turn back. I heard the priest at the grating making an appointment with her for the evening—though he was an old man hard as flint, he fell in an instant! It was nature, the truth of nature asserted its rights! What, you are turning up your nose again? Angry again? I don’t know how to please you—”\n\n“Leave me alone, you are beating on my brain like a haunting nightmare,” Ivan moaned miserably, helpless before his apparition. “I am bored with you, agonizingly and insufferably. I would give anything to be able to shake you off!”\n\n“I repeat, moderate your expectations, don’t demand of me ‘everything great and noble’ and you’ll see how well we shall get on,” said the gentleman impressively. “You are really angry with me for not having appeared to you in a red glow, with thunder and lightning, with scorched wings, but have shown myself in such a modest form. You are wounded, in the first place, in your esthetic feelings, and, secondly, in your pride. How could such a vulgar devil visit such a great man as you! Yes, there is that romantic strain in you, that was so derided by Byelinsky. I can’t help it, young man, as I got ready to come to you I did think as a joke of appearing in the figure of a retired general who had served in the Caucasus, with a star of the Lion and the Sun on my coat. But I was positively afraid of doing it, for you’d have thrashed me for daring to pin the Lion and the Sun on my coat, instead of, at least, the Polar Star or the Sirius. And you keep on saying I am stupid, but, mercy on us! I make no claim to be equal to you in intelligence. Mephistopheles declared to Faust that he desired evil, but did only good. Well, he can say what he likes, it’s quite the opposite with me. I am perhaps the one man in all creation who loves the truth and genuinely desires good. I was there when the Word, Who died on the Cross, rose up into heaven bearing on His bosom the soul of the penitent thief. I heard the glad shrieks of the cherubim singing and shouting hosannah and the thunderous rapture of the seraphim which shook heaven and all creation, and I swear to you by all that’s sacred, I longed to join the choir and shout hosannah with them all. The word had almost escaped me, had almost broken from my lips ... you know how susceptible and esthetically impressionable I am. But common sense—oh, a most unhappy trait in my character—kept me in due bounds and I let the moment pass! For what would have happened, I reflected, what would have happened after my hosannah? Everything on earth would have been extinguished at once and no events could have occurred. And so, solely from a sense of duty and my social position, I was forced to suppress the good moment and to stick to my nasty task. Somebody takes all the credit of what’s good for Himself, and nothing but nastiness is left for me. But I don’t envy the honor of a life of idle imposture, I am not ambitious. Why am I, of all creatures in the world, doomed to be cursed by all decent people and even to be kicked, for if I put on mortal form I am bound to take such consequences sometimes? I know, of course, there’s a secret in it, but they won’t tell me the secret for anything, for then perhaps, seeing the meaning of it, I might bawl hosannah, and the indispensable minus would disappear at once, and good sense would reign supreme throughout the whole world. And that, of course, would mean the end of everything, even of magazines and newspapers, for who would take them in? I know that at the end of all things I shall be reconciled. I, too, shall walk my quadrillion and learn the secret. But till that happens I am sulking and fulfill my destiny though it’s against the grain—that is, to ruin thousands for the sake of saving one. How many souls have had to be ruined and how many honorable reputations destroyed for the sake of that one righteous man, Job, over whom they made such a fool of me in old days! Yes, till the secret is revealed, there are two sorts of truths for me—one, their truth, yonder, which I know nothing about so far, and the other my own. And there’s no knowing which will turn out the better.... Are you asleep?”\n\n“I might well be,” Ivan groaned angrily. “All my stupid ideas—outgrown, thrashed out long ago, and flung aside like a dead carcass—you present to me as something new!”\n\n“There’s no pleasing you! And I thought I should fascinate you by my literary style. That hosannah in the skies really wasn’t bad, was it? And then that ironical tone à la Heine, eh?”\n\n“No, I was never such a flunkey! How then could my soul beget a flunkey like you?”\n\n“My dear fellow, I know a most charming and attractive young Russian gentleman, a young thinker and a great lover of literature and art, the author of a promising poem entitled The Grand Inquisitor. I was only thinking of him!”\n\n“I forbid you to speak of The Grand Inquisitor,” cried Ivan, crimson with shame.\n\n“And the Geological Cataclysm. Do you remember? That was a poem, now!”\n\n“Hold your tongue, or I’ll kill you!”\n\n“You’ll kill me? No, excuse me, I will speak. I came to treat myself to that pleasure. Oh, I love the dreams of my ardent young friends, quivering with eagerness for life! ‘There are new men,’ you decided last spring, when you were meaning to come here, ‘they propose to destroy everything and begin with cannibalism. Stupid fellows! they didn’t ask my advice! I maintain that nothing need be destroyed, that we only need to destroy the idea of God in man, that’s how we have to set to work. It’s that, that we must begin with. Oh, blind race of men who have no understanding! As soon as men have all of them denied God—and I believe that period, analogous with geological periods, will come to pass—the old conception of the universe will fall of itself without cannibalism, and, what’s more, the old morality, and everything will begin anew. Men will unite to take from life all it can give, but only for joy and happiness in the present world. Man will be lifted up with a spirit of divine Titanic pride and the man-god will appear. From hour to hour extending his conquest of nature infinitely by his will and his science, man will feel such lofty joy from hour to hour in doing it that it will make up for all his old dreams of the joys of heaven. Every one will know that he is mortal and will accept death proudly and serenely like a god. His pride will teach him that it’s useless for him to repine at life’s being a moment, and he will love his brother without need of reward. Love will be sufficient only for a moment of life, but the very consciousness of its momentariness will intensify its fire, which now is dissipated in dreams of eternal love beyond the grave’... and so on and so on in the same style. Charming!”\n\nIvan sat with his eyes on the floor, and his hands pressed to his ears, but he began trembling all over. The voice continued.\n\n“The question now is, my young thinker reflected, is it possible that such a period will ever come? If it does, everything is determined and humanity is settled for ever. But as, owing to man’s inveterate stupidity, this cannot come about for at least a thousand years, every one who recognizes the truth even now may legitimately order his life as he pleases, on the new principles. In that sense, ‘all things are lawful’ for him. What’s more, even if this period never comes to pass, since there is anyway no God and no immortality, the new man may well become the man-god, even if he is the only one in the whole world, and promoted to his new position, he may lightheartedly overstep all the barriers of the old morality of the old slave-man, if necessary. There is no law for God. Where God stands, the place is holy. Where I stand will be at once the foremost place ... ‘all things are lawful’ and that’s the end of it! That’s all very charming; but if you want to swindle why do you want a moral sanction for doing it? But that’s our modern Russian all over. He can’t bring himself to swindle without a moral sanction. He is so in love with truth—”\n\nThe visitor talked, obviously carried away by his own eloquence, speaking louder and louder and looking ironically at his host. But he did not succeed in finishing; Ivan suddenly snatched a glass from the table and flung it at the orator.\n\n“Ah, mais c’est bête enfin,” cried the latter, jumping up from the sofa and shaking the drops of tea off himself. “He remembers Luther’s inkstand! He takes me for a dream and throws glasses at a dream! It’s like a woman! I suspected you were only pretending to stop up your ears.”\n\nA loud, persistent knocking was suddenly heard at the window. Ivan jumped up from the sofa.\n\n“Do you hear? You’d better open,” cried the visitor; “it’s your brother Alyosha with the most interesting and surprising news, I’ll be bound!”\n\n“Be silent, deceiver, I knew it was Alyosha, I felt he was coming, and of course he has not come for nothing; of course he brings ‘news,’” Ivan exclaimed frantically.\n\n“Open, open to him. There’s a snowstorm and he is your brother. Monsieur sait-il le temps qu’il fait? C’est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors.”\n\nThe knocking continued. Ivan wanted to rush to the window, but something seemed to fetter his arms and legs. He strained every effort to break his chains, but in vain. The knocking at the window grew louder and louder. At last the chains were broken and Ivan leapt up from the sofa. He looked round him wildly. Both candles had almost burnt out, the glass he had just thrown at his visitor stood before him on the table, and there was no one on the sofa opposite. The knocking on the window frame went on persistently, but it was by no means so loud as it had seemed in his dream; on the contrary, it was quite subdued.\n\n“It was not a dream! No, I swear it was not a dream, it all happened just now!” cried Ivan. He rushed to the window and opened the movable pane.\n\n“Alyosha, I told you not to come,” he cried fiercely to his brother. “In two words, what do you want? In two words, do you hear?”\n\n“An hour ago Smerdyakov hanged himself,” Alyosha answered from the yard.\n\n“Come round to the steps, I’ll open at once,” said Ivan, going to open the door to Alyosha.\n\n# Chapter X. “It Was He Who Said That”\n\nAlyosha coming in told Ivan that a little over an hour ago Marya Kondratyevna had run to his rooms and informed him Smerdyakov had taken his own life. “I went in to clear away the samovar and he was hanging on a nail in the wall.” On Alyosha’s inquiring whether she had informed the police, she answered that she had told no one, “but I flew straight to you, I’ve run all the way.” She seemed perfectly crazy, Alyosha reported, and was shaking like a leaf. When Alyosha ran with her to the cottage, he found Smerdyakov still hanging. On the table lay a note: “I destroy my life of my own will and desire, so as to throw no blame on any one.” Alyosha left the note on the table and went straight to the police captain and told him all about it. “And from him I’ve come straight to you,” said Alyosha, in conclusion, looking intently into Ivan’s face. He had not taken his eyes off him while he told his story, as though struck by something in his expression.\n\n“Brother,” he cried suddenly, “you must be terribly ill. You look and don’t seem to understand what I tell you.”\n\n“It’s a good thing you came,” said Ivan, as though brooding, and not hearing Alyosha’s exclamation. “I knew he had hanged himself.”\n\n“From whom?”\n\n“I don’t know. But I knew. Did I know? Yes, he told me. He told me so just now.”\n\nIvan stood in the middle of the room, and still spoke in the same brooding tone, looking at the ground.\n\n“Who is he?” asked Alyosha, involuntarily looking round.\n\n“He’s slipped away.”\n\nIvan raised his head and smiled softly.\n\n“He was afraid of you, of a dove like you. You are a ‘pure cherub.’ Dmitri calls you a cherub. Cherub!... the thunderous rapture of the seraphim. What are seraphim? Perhaps a whole constellation. But perhaps that constellation is only a chemical molecule. There’s a constellation of the Lion and the Sun. Don’t you know it?”\n\n“Brother, sit down,” said Alyosha in alarm. “For goodness’ sake, sit down on the sofa! You are delirious; put your head on the pillow, that’s right. Would you like a wet towel on your head? Perhaps it will do you good.”\n\n“Give me the towel: it’s here on the chair. I just threw it down there.”\n\n“It’s not here. Don’t worry yourself. I know where it is—here,” said Alyosha, finding a clean towel, folded up and unused, by Ivan’s dressing-table in the other corner of the room. Ivan looked strangely at the towel: recollection seemed to come back to him for an instant.\n\n“Stay”—he got up from the sofa—“an hour ago I took that new towel from there and wetted it. I wrapped it round my head and threw it down here ... How is it it’s dry? There was no other.”\n\n“You put that towel on your head?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, and walked up and down the room an hour ago ... Why have the candles burnt down so? What’s the time?”\n\n“Nearly twelve.”\n\n“No, no, no!” Ivan cried suddenly. “It was not a dream. He was here; he was sitting here, on that sofa. When you knocked at the window, I threw a glass at him ... this one. Wait a minute. I was asleep last time, but this dream was not a dream. It has happened before. I have dreams now, Alyosha ... yet they are not dreams, but reality. I walk about, talk and see ... though I am asleep. But he was sitting here, on that sofa there.... He is frightfully stupid, Alyosha, frightfully stupid.” Ivan laughed suddenly and began pacing about the room.\n\n“Who is stupid? Of whom are you talking, brother?” Alyosha asked anxiously again.\n\n“The devil! He’s taken to visiting me. He’s been here twice, almost three times. He taunted me with being angry at his being a simple devil and not Satan, with scorched wings, in thunder and lightning. But he is not Satan: that’s a lie. He is an impostor. He is simply a devil—a paltry, trivial devil. He goes to the baths. If you undressed him, you’d be sure to find he had a tail, long and smooth like a Danish dog’s, a yard long, dun color.... Alyosha, you are cold. You’ve been in the snow. Would you like some tea? What? Is it cold? Shall I tell her to bring some? C’est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors....”\n\nAlyosha ran to the washing-stand, wetted the towel, persuaded Ivan to sit down again, and put the wet towel round his head. He sat down beside him.\n\n“What were you telling me just now about Lise?” Ivan began again. (He was becoming very talkative.) “I like Lise. I said something nasty about her. It was a lie. I like her ... I am afraid for Katya to-morrow. I am more afraid of her than of anything. On account of the future. She will cast me off to-morrow and trample me under foot. She thinks that I am ruining Mitya from jealousy on her account! Yes, she thinks that! But it’s not so. To-morrow the cross, but not the gallows. No, I shan’t hang myself. Do you know, I can never commit suicide, Alyosha. Is it because I am base? I am not a coward. Is it from love of life? How did I know that Smerdyakov had hanged himself? Yes, it was he told me so.”\n\n“And you are quite convinced that there has been some one here?” asked Alyosha.\n\n“Yes, on that sofa in the corner. You would have driven him away. You did drive him away: he disappeared when you arrived. I love your face, Alyosha. Did you know that I loved your face? And he is myself, Alyosha. All that’s base in me, all that’s mean and contemptible. Yes, I am a romantic. He guessed it ... though it’s a libel. He is frightfully stupid; but it’s to his advantage. He has cunning, animal cunning—he knew how to infuriate me. He kept taunting me with believing in him, and that was how he made me listen to him. He fooled me like a boy. He told me a great deal that was true about myself, though. I should never have owned it to myself. Do you know, Alyosha,” Ivan added in an intensely earnest and confidential tone, “I should be awfully glad to think that it was he and not I.”\n\n“He has worn you out,” said Alyosha, looking compassionately at his brother.\n\n“He’s been teasing me. And you know he does it so cleverly, so cleverly. ‘Conscience! What is conscience? I make it up for myself. Why am I tormented by it? From habit. From the universal habit of mankind for the seven thousand years. So let us give it up, and we shall be gods.’ It was he said that, it was he said that!”\n\n“And not you, not you?” Alyosha could not help crying, looking frankly at his brother. “Never mind him, anyway; have done with him and forget him. And let him take with him all that you curse now, and never come back!”\n\n“Yes, but he is spiteful. He laughed at me. He was impudent, Alyosha,” Ivan said, with a shudder of offense. “But he was unfair to me, unfair to me about lots of things. He told lies about me to my face. ‘Oh, you are going to perform an act of heroic virtue: to confess you murdered your father, that the valet murdered him at your instigation.’”\n\n“Brother,” Alyosha interposed, “restrain yourself. It was not you murdered him. It’s not true!”\n\n“That’s what he says, he, and he knows it. ‘You are going to perform an act of heroic virtue, and you don’t believe in virtue; that’s what tortures you and makes you angry, that’s why you are so vindictive.’ He said that to me about me and he knows what he says.”\n\n“It’s you say that, not he,” exclaimed Alyosha mournfully, “and you say it because you are ill and delirious, tormenting yourself.”\n\n“No, he knows what he says. ‘You are going from pride,’ he says. ‘You’ll stand up and say it was I killed him, and why do you writhe with horror? You are lying! I despise your opinion, I despise your horror!’ He said that about me. ‘And do you know you are longing for their praise—“he is a criminal, a murderer, but what a generous soul; he wanted to save his brother and he confessed.”’ That’s a lie, Alyosha!” Ivan cried suddenly, with flashing eyes. “I don’t want the low rabble to praise me, I swear I don’t! That’s a lie! That’s why I threw the glass at him and it broke against his ugly face.”\n\n“Brother, calm yourself, stop!” Alyosha entreated him.\n\n“Yes, he knows how to torment one. He’s cruel,” Ivan went on, unheeding. “I had an inkling from the first what he came for. ‘Granting that you go through pride, still you had a hope that Smerdyakov might be convicted and sent to Siberia, and Mitya would be acquitted, while you would only be punished with moral condemnation’ (‘Do you hear?’ he laughed then)—‘and some people will praise you. But now Smerdyakov’s dead, he has hanged himself, and who’ll believe you alone? But yet you are going, you are going, you’ll go all the same, you’ve decided to go. What are you going for now?’ That’s awful, Alyosha. I can’t endure such questions. Who dare ask me such questions?”\n\n“Brother,” interposed Alyosha—his heart sank with terror, but he still seemed to hope to bring Ivan to reason—“how could he have told you of Smerdyakov’s death before I came, when no one knew of it and there was no time for any one to know of it?”\n\n“He told me,” said Ivan firmly, refusing to admit a doubt. “It was all he did talk about, if you come to that. ‘And it would be all right if you believed in virtue,’ he said. ‘No matter if they disbelieve you, you are going for the sake of principle. But you are a little pig like Fyodor Pavlovitch, and what do you want with virtue? Why do you want to go meddling if your sacrifice is of no use to any one? Because you don’t know yourself why you go! Oh, you’d give a great deal to know yourself why you go! And can you have made up your mind? You’ve not made up your mind. You’ll sit all night deliberating whether to go or not. But you will go; you know you’ll go. You know that whichever way you decide, the decision does not depend on you. You’ll go because you won’t dare not to go. Why won’t you dare? You must guess that for yourself. That’s a riddle for you!’ He got up and went away. You came and he went. He called me a coward, Alyosha! Le mot de l’énigme is that I am a coward. ‘It is not for such eagles to soar above the earth.’ It was he added that—he! And Smerdyakov said the same. He must be killed! Katya despises me. I’ve seen that for a month past. Even Lise will begin to despise me! ‘You are going in order to be praised.’ That’s a brutal lie! And you despise me too, Alyosha. Now I am going to hate you again! And I hate the monster, too! I hate the monster! I don’t want to save the monster. Let him rot in Siberia! He’s begun singing a hymn! Oh, to-morrow I’ll go, stand before them, and spit in their faces!”\n\nHe jumped up in a frenzy, flung off the towel, and fell to pacing up and down the room again. Alyosha recalled what he had just said. “I seem to be sleeping awake.... I walk, I speak, I see, but I am asleep.” It seemed to be just like that now. Alyosha did not leave him. The thought passed through his mind to run for a doctor, but he was afraid to leave his brother alone: there was no one to whom he could leave him. By degrees Ivan lost consciousness completely at last. He still went on talking, talking incessantly, but quite incoherently, and even articulated his words with difficulty. Suddenly he staggered violently; but Alyosha was in time to support him. Ivan let him lead him to his bed. Alyosha undressed him somehow and put him to bed. He sat watching over him for another two hours. The sick man slept soundly, without stirring, breathing softly and evenly. Alyosha took a pillow and lay down on the sofa, without undressing.\n\nAs he fell asleep he prayed for Mitya and Ivan. He began to understand Ivan’s illness. “The anguish of a proud determination. An earnest conscience!” God, in Whom he disbelieved, and His truth were gaining mastery over his heart, which still refused to submit. “Yes,” the thought floated through Alyosha’s head as it lay on the pillow, “yes, if Smerdyakov is dead, no one will believe Ivan’s evidence; but he will go and give it.” Alyosha smiled softly. “God will conquer!” he thought. “He will either rise up in the light of truth, or ... he’ll perish in hate, revenging on himself and on every one his having served the cause he does not believe in,” Alyosha added bitterly, and again he prayed for Ivan.\n\n# Book XII. A Judicial Error Chapter I. The Fatal Day\n\nAt ten o’clock in the morning of the day following the events I have described, the trial of Dmitri Karamazov began in our district court.\n\nI hasten to emphasize the fact that I am far from esteeming myself capable of reporting all that took place at the trial in full detail, or even in the actual order of events. I imagine that to mention everything with full explanation would fill a volume, even a very large one. And so I trust I may not be reproached, for confining myself to what struck me. I may have selected as of most interest what was of secondary importance, and may have omitted the most prominent and essential details. But I see I shall do better not to apologize. I will do my best and the reader will see for himself that I have done all I can.\n\nAnd, to begin with, before entering the court, I will mention what surprised me most on that day. Indeed, as it appeared later, every one was surprised at it, too. We all knew that the affair had aroused great interest, that every one was burning with impatience for the trial to begin, that it had been a subject of talk, conjecture, exclamation and surmise for the last two months in local society. Every one knew, too, that the case had become known throughout Russia, but yet we had not imagined that it had aroused such burning, such intense, interest in every one, not only among ourselves, but all over Russia. This became evident at the trial this day.\n\nVisitors had arrived not only from the chief town of our province, but from several other Russian towns, as well as from Moscow and Petersburg. Among them were lawyers, ladies, and even several distinguished personages. Every ticket of admission had been snatched up. A special place behind the table at which the three judges sat was set apart for the most distinguished and important of the men visitors; a row of arm-chairs had been placed there—something exceptional, which had never been allowed before. A large proportion—not less than half of the public—were ladies. There was such a large number of lawyers from all parts that they did not know where to seat them, for every ticket had long since been eagerly sought for and distributed. I saw at the end of the room, behind the platform, a special partition hurriedly put up, behind which all these lawyers were admitted, and they thought themselves lucky to have standing room there, for all chairs had been removed for the sake of space, and the crowd behind the partition stood throughout the case closely packed, shoulder to shoulder.\n\nSome of the ladies, especially those who came from a distance, made their appearance in the gallery very smartly dressed, but the majority of the ladies were oblivious even of dress. Their faces betrayed hysterical, intense, almost morbid, curiosity. A peculiar fact—established afterwards by many observations—was that almost all the ladies, or, at least the vast majority of them, were on Mitya’s side and in favor of his being acquitted. This was perhaps chiefly owing to his reputation as a conqueror of female hearts. It was known that two women rivals were to appear in the case. One of them—Katerina Ivanovna—was an object of general interest. All sorts of extraordinary tales were told about her, amazing anecdotes of her passion for Mitya, in spite of his crime. Her pride and “aristocratic connections” were particularly insisted upon (she had called upon scarcely any one in the town). People said she intended to petition the Government for leave to accompany the criminal to Siberia and to be married to him somewhere in the mines. The appearance of Grushenka in court was awaited with no less impatience. The public was looking forward with anxious curiosity to the meeting of the two rivals—the proud aristocratic girl and “the hetaira.” But Grushenka was a more familiar figure to the ladies of the district than Katerina Ivanovna. They had already seen “the woman who had ruined Fyodor Pavlovitch and his unhappy son,” and all, almost without exception, wondered how father and son could be so in love with “such a very common, ordinary Russian girl, who was not even pretty.”\n\nIn brief, there was a great deal of talk. I know for a fact that there were several serious family quarrels on Mitya’s account in our town. Many ladies quarreled violently with their husbands over differences of opinion about the dreadful case, and it was only natural that the husbands of these ladies, far from being favorably disposed to the prisoner, should enter the court bitterly prejudiced against him. In fact, one may say pretty certainly that the masculine, as distinguished from the feminine, part of the audience were biased against the prisoner. There were numbers of severe, frowning, even vindictive faces. Mitya, indeed, had managed to offend many people during his stay in the town. Some of the visitors were, of course, in excellent spirits and quite unconcerned as to the fate of Mitya personally. But all were interested in the trial, and the majority of the men were certainly hoping for the conviction of the criminal, except perhaps the lawyers, who were more interested in the legal than in the moral aspect of the case.\n\nEverybody was excited at the presence of the celebrated lawyer, Fetyukovitch. His talent was well known, and this was not the first time he had defended notorious criminal cases in the provinces. And if he defended them, such cases became celebrated and long remembered all over Russia. There were stories, too, about our prosecutor and about the President of the Court. It was said that Ippolit Kirillovitch was in a tremor at meeting Fetyukovitch, and that they had been enemies from the beginning of their careers in Petersburg, that though our sensitive prosecutor, who always considered that he had been aggrieved by some one in Petersburg because his talents had not been properly appreciated, was keenly excited over the Karamazov case, and was even dreaming of rebuilding his flagging fortunes by means of it, Fetyukovitch, they said, was his one anxiety. But these rumors were not quite just. Our prosecutor was not one of those men who lose heart in face of danger. On the contrary, his self-confidence increased with the increase of danger. It must be noted that our prosecutor was in general too hasty and morbidly impressionable. He would put his whole soul into some case and work at it as though his whole fate and his whole fortune depended on its result. This was the subject of some ridicule in the legal world, for just by this characteristic our prosecutor had gained a wider notoriety than could have been expected from his modest position. People laughed particularly at his passion for psychology. In my opinion, they were wrong, and our prosecutor was, I believe, a character of greater depth than was generally supposed. But with his delicate health he had failed to make his mark at the outset of his career and had never made up for it later.\n\nAs for the President of our Court, I can only say that he was a humane and cultured man, who had a practical knowledge of his work and progressive views. He was rather ambitious, but did not concern himself greatly about his future career. The great aim of his life was to be a man of advanced ideas. He was, too, a man of connections and property. He felt, as we learnt afterwards, rather strongly about the Karamazov case, but from a social, not from a personal standpoint. He was interested in it as a social phenomenon, in its classification and its character as a product of our social conditions, as typical of the national character, and so on, and so on. His attitude to the personal aspect of the case, to its tragic significance and the persons involved in it, including the prisoner, was rather indifferent and abstract, as was perhaps fitting, indeed.\n\nThe court was packed and overflowing long before the judges made their appearance. Our court is the best hall in the town—spacious, lofty, and good for sound. On the right of the judges, who were on a raised platform, a table and two rows of chairs had been put ready for the jury. On the left was the place for the prisoner and the counsel for the defense. In the middle of the court, near the judges, was a table with the “material proofs.” On it lay Fyodor Pavlovitch’s white silk dressing-gown, stained with blood; the fatal brass pestle with which the supposed murder had been committed; Mitya’s shirt, with a blood-stained sleeve; his coat, stained with blood in patches over the pocket in which he had put his handkerchief; the handkerchief itself, stiff with blood and by now quite yellow; the pistol loaded by Mitya at Perhotin’s with a view to suicide, and taken from him on the sly at Mokroe by Trifon Borissovitch; the envelope in which the three thousand roubles had been put ready for Grushenka, the narrow pink ribbon with which it had been tied, and many other articles I don’t remember. In the body of the hall, at some distance, came the seats for the public. But in front of the balustrade a few chairs had been placed for witnesses who remained in the court after giving their evidence.\n\nAt ten o’clock the three judges arrived—the President, one honorary justice of the peace, and one other. The prosecutor, of course, entered immediately after. The President was a short, stout, thick-set man of fifty, with a dyspeptic complexion, dark hair turning gray and cut short, and a red ribbon, of what Order I don’t remember. The prosecutor struck me and the others, too, as looking particularly pale, almost green. His face seemed to have grown suddenly thinner, perhaps in a single night, for I had seen him looking as usual only two days before. The President began with asking the court whether all the jury were present.\n\nBut I see I can’t go on like this, partly because some things I did not hear, others I did not notice, and others I have forgotten, but most of all because, as I have said before, I have literally no time or space to mention everything that was said and done. I only know that neither side objected to very many of the jurymen. I remember the twelve jurymen—four were petty officials of the town, two were merchants, and six peasants and artisans of the town. I remember, long before the trial, questions were continually asked with some surprise, especially by ladies: “Can such a delicate, complex and psychological case be submitted for decision to petty officials and even peasants?” and “What can an official, still more a peasant, understand in such an affair?” All the four officials in the jury were, in fact, men of no consequence and of low rank. Except one who was rather younger, they were gray-headed men, little known in society, who had vegetated on a pitiful salary, and who probably had elderly, unpresentable wives and crowds of children, perhaps even without shoes and stockings. At most, they spent their leisure over cards and, of course, had never read a single book. The two merchants looked respectable, but were strangely silent and stolid. One of them was close-shaven, and was dressed in European style; the other had a small, gray beard, and wore a red ribbon with some sort of a medal upon it on his neck. There is no need to speak of the artisans and the peasants. The artisans of Skotoprigonyevsk are almost peasants, and even work on the land. Two of them also wore European dress, and, perhaps for that reason, were dirtier and more uninviting-looking than the others. So that one might well wonder, as I did as soon as I had looked at them, “what men like that could possibly make of such a case?” Yet their faces made a strangely imposing, almost menacing, impression; they were stern and frowning.\n\nAt last the President opened the case of the murder of Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov. I don’t quite remember how he described him. The court usher was told to bring in the prisoner, and Mitya made his appearance. There was a hush through the court. One could have heard a fly. I don’t know how it was with others, but Mitya made a most unfavorable impression on me. He looked an awful dandy in a brand-new frock-coat. I heard afterwards that he had ordered it in Moscow expressly for the occasion from his own tailor, who had his measure. He wore immaculate black kid gloves and exquisite linen. He walked in with his yard-long strides, looking stiffly straight in front of him, and sat down in his place with a most unperturbed air.\n\nAt the same moment the counsel for defense, the celebrated Fetyukovitch, entered, and a sort of subdued hum passed through the court. He was a tall, spare man, with long thin legs, with extremely long, thin, pale fingers, clean-shaven face, demurely brushed, rather short hair, and thin lips that were at times curved into something between a sneer and a smile. He looked about forty. His face would have been pleasant, if it had not been for his eyes, which, in themselves small and inexpressive, were set remarkably close together, with only the thin, long nose as a dividing line between them. In fact, there was something strikingly birdlike about his face. He was in evening dress and white tie.\n\nI remember the President’s first questions to Mitya, about his name, his calling, and so on. Mitya answered sharply, and his voice was so unexpectedly loud that it made the President start and look at the prisoner with surprise. Then followed a list of persons who were to take part in the proceedings—that is, of the witnesses and experts. It was a long list. Four of the witnesses were not present—Miüsov, who had given evidence at the preliminary inquiry, but was now in Paris; Madame Hohlakov and Maximov, who were absent through illness; and Smerdyakov, through his sudden death, of which an official statement from the police was presented. The news of Smerdyakov’s death produced a sudden stir and whisper in the court. Many of the audience, of course, had not heard of the sudden suicide. What struck people most was Mitya’s sudden outburst. As soon as the statement of Smerdyakov’s death was made, he cried out aloud from his place:\n\n“He was a dog and died like a dog!”\n\nI remember how his counsel rushed to him, and how the President addressed him, threatening to take stern measures, if such an irregularity were repeated. Mitya nodded and in a subdued voice repeated several times abruptly to his counsel, with no show of regret:\n\n“I won’t again, I won’t. It escaped me. I won’t do it again.”\n\nAnd, of course, this brief episode did him no good with the jury or the public. His character was displayed, and it spoke for itself. It was under the influence of this incident that the opening statement was read. It was rather short, but circumstantial. It only stated the chief reasons why he had been arrested, why he must be tried, and so on. Yet it made a great impression on me. The clerk read it loudly and distinctly. The whole tragedy was suddenly unfolded before us, concentrated, in bold relief, in a fatal and pitiless light. I remember how, immediately after it had been read, the President asked Mitya in a loud impressive voice:\n\n“Prisoner, do you plead guilty?”\n\nMitya suddenly rose from his seat.\n\n“I plead guilty to drunkenness and dissipation,” he exclaimed, again in a startling, almost frenzied, voice, “to idleness and debauchery. I meant to become an honest man for good, just at the moment when I was struck down by fate. But I am not guilty of the death of that old man, my enemy and my father. No, no, I am not guilty of robbing him! I could not be. Dmitri Karamazov is a scoundrel, but not a thief.”\n\nHe sat down again, visibly trembling all over. The President again briefly, but impressively, admonished him to answer only what was asked, and not to go off into irrelevant exclamations. Then he ordered the case to proceed. All the witnesses were led up to take the oath. Then I saw them all together. The brothers of the prisoner were, however, allowed to give evidence without taking the oath. After an exhortation from the priest and the President, the witnesses were led away and were made to sit as far as possible apart from one another. Then they began calling them up one by one.\n\n# Chapter II. Dangerous Witnesses\n\nI do not know whether the witnesses for the defense and for the prosecution were separated into groups by the President, and whether it was arranged to call them in a certain order. But no doubt it was so. I only know that the witnesses for the prosecution were called first. I repeat I don’t intend to describe all the questions step by step. Besides, my account would be to some extent superfluous, because in the speeches for the prosecution and for the defense the whole course of the evidence was brought together and set in a strong and significant light, and I took down parts of those two remarkable speeches in full, and will quote them in due course, together with one extraordinary and quite unexpected episode, which occurred before the final speeches, and undoubtedly influenced the sinister and fatal outcome of the trial.\n\nI will only observe that from the first moments of the trial one peculiar characteristic of the case was conspicuous and observed by all, that is, the overwhelming strength of the prosecution as compared with the arguments the defense had to rely upon. Every one realized it from the first moment that the facts began to group themselves round a single point, and the whole horrible and bloody crime was gradually revealed. Every one, perhaps, felt from the first that the case was beyond dispute, that there was no doubt about it, that there could be really no discussion, and that the defense was only a matter of form, and that the prisoner was guilty, obviously and conclusively guilty. I imagine that even the ladies, who were so impatiently longing for the acquittal of the interesting prisoner, were at the same time, without exception, convinced of his guilt. What’s more, I believe they would have been mortified if his guilt had not been so firmly established, as that would have lessened the effect of the closing scene of the criminal’s acquittal. That he would be acquitted, all the ladies, strange to say, were firmly persuaded up to the very last moment. “He is guilty, but he will be acquitted, from motives of humanity, in accordance with the new ideas, the new sentiments that had come into fashion,” and so on, and so on. And that was why they had crowded into the court so impatiently. The men were more interested in the contest between the prosecutor and the famous Fetyukovitch. All were wondering and asking themselves what could even a talent like Fetyukovitch’s make of such a desperate case; and so they followed his achievements, step by step, with concentrated attention.\n\nBut Fetyukovitch remained an enigma to all up to the very end, up to his speech. Persons of experience suspected that he had some design, that he was working towards some object, but it was almost impossible to guess what it was. His confidence and self-reliance were unmistakable, however. Every one noticed with pleasure, moreover, that he, after so short a stay, not more than three days, perhaps, among us, had so wonderfully succeeded in mastering the case and “had studied it to a nicety.” People described with relish, afterwards, how cleverly he had “taken down” all the witnesses for the prosecution, and as far as possible perplexed them and, what’s more, had aspersed their reputation and so depreciated the value of their evidence. But it was supposed that he did this rather by way of sport, so to speak, for professional glory, to show nothing had been omitted of the accepted methods, for all were convinced that he could do no real good by such disparagement of the witnesses, and probably was more aware of this than any one, having some idea of his own in the background, some concealed weapon of defense, which he would suddenly reveal when the time came. But meanwhile, conscious of his strength, he seemed to be diverting himself.\n\nSo, for instance, when Grigory, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s old servant, who had given the most damning piece of evidence about the open door, was examined, the counsel for the defense positively fastened upon him when his turn came to question him. It must be noted that Grigory entered the hall with a composed and almost stately air, not the least disconcerted by the majesty of the court or the vast audience listening to him. He gave evidence with as much confidence as though he had been talking with his Marfa, only perhaps more respectfully. It was impossible to make him contradict himself. The prosecutor questioned him first in detail about the family life of the Karamazovs. The family picture stood out in lurid colors. It was plain to ear and eye that the witness was guileless and impartial. In spite of his profound reverence for the memory of his deceased master, he yet bore witness that he had been unjust to Mitya and “hadn’t brought up his children as he should. He’d have been devoured by lice when he was little, if it hadn’t been for me,” he added, describing Mitya’s early childhood. “It wasn’t fair either of the father to wrong his son over his mother’s property, which was by right his.”\n\nIn reply to the prosecutor’s question what grounds he had for asserting that Fyodor Pavlovitch had wronged his son in their money relations, Grigory, to the surprise of every one, had no proof at all to bring forward, but he still persisted that the arrangement with the son was “unfair,” and that he ought “to have paid him several thousand roubles more.” I must note, by the way, that the prosecutor asked this question whether Fyodor Pavlovitch had really kept back part of Mitya’s inheritance with marked persistence of all the witnesses who could be asked it, not excepting Alyosha and Ivan, but he obtained no exact information from any one; all alleged that it was so, but were unable to bring forward any distinct proof. Grigory’s description of the scene at the dinner-table, when Dmitri had burst in and beaten his father, threatening to come back to kill him, made a sinister impression on the court, especially as the old servant’s composure in telling it, his parsimony of words and peculiar phraseology, were as effective as eloquence. He observed that he was not angry with Mitya for having knocked him down and struck him on the face; he had forgiven him long ago, he said. Of the deceased Smerdyakov he observed, crossing himself, that he was a lad of ability, but stupid and afflicted, and, worse still, an infidel, and that it was Fyodor Pavlovitch and his elder son who had taught him to be so. But he defended Smerdyakov’s honesty almost with warmth, and related how Smerdyakov had once found the master’s money in the yard, and, instead of concealing it, had taken it to his master, who had rewarded him with a “gold piece” for it, and trusted him implicitly from that time forward. He maintained obstinately that the door into the garden had been open. But he was asked so many questions that I can’t recall them all.\n\nAt last the counsel for the defense began to cross-examine him, and the first question he asked was about the envelope in which Fyodor Pavlovitch was supposed to have put three thousand roubles for “a certain person.” “Have you ever seen it, you, who were for so many years in close attendance on your master?” Grigory answered that he had not seen it and had never heard of the money from any one “till everybody was talking about it.” This question about the envelope Fetyukovitch put to every one who could conceivably have known of it, as persistently as the prosecutor asked his question about Dmitri’s inheritance, and got the same answer from all, that no one had seen the envelope, though many had heard of it. From the beginning every one noticed Fetyukovitch’s persistence on this subject.\n\n“Now, with your permission I’ll ask you a question,” Fetyukovitch said, suddenly and unexpectedly. “Of what was that balsam, or, rather, decoction, made, which, as we learn from the preliminary inquiry, you used on that evening to rub your lumbago, in the hope of curing it?”\n\nGrigory looked blankly at the questioner, and after a brief silence muttered, “There was saffron in it.”\n\n“Nothing but saffron? Don’t you remember any other ingredient?”\n\n“There was milfoil in it, too.”\n\n“And pepper perhaps?” Fetyukovitch queried.\n\n“Yes, there was pepper, too.”\n\n“Etcetera. And all dissolved in vodka?”\n“In spirit.”\n\nThere was a faint sound of laughter in the court.\n\n“You see, in spirit. After rubbing your back, I believe, you drank what was left in the bottle with a certain pious prayer, only known to your wife?”\n\n“I did.”\n\n“Did you drink much? Roughly speaking, a wine-glass or two?”\n\n“It might have been a tumbler-full.”\n\n“A tumbler-full, even. Perhaps a tumbler and a half?”\n\nGrigory did not answer. He seemed to see what was meant.\n\n“A glass and a half of neat spirit—is not at all bad, don’t you think? You might see the gates of heaven open, not only the door into the garden?”\n\nGrigory remained silent. There was another laugh in the court. The President made a movement.\n\n“Do you know for a fact,” Fetyukovitch persisted, “whether you were awake or not when you saw the open door?”\n\n“I was on my legs.”\n\n“That’s not a proof that you were awake.” (There was again laughter in the court.) “Could you have answered at that moment, if any one had asked you a question—for instance, what year it is?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“And what year is it, Anno Domini, do you know?”\n\nGrigory stood with a perplexed face, looking straight at his tormentor. Strange to say, it appeared he really did not know what year it was.\n\n“But perhaps you can tell me how many fingers you have on your hands?”\n\n“I am a servant,” Grigory said suddenly, in a loud and distinct voice. “If my betters think fit to make game of me, it is my duty to suffer it.”\n\nFetyukovitch was a little taken aback, and the President intervened, reminding him that he must ask more relevant questions. Fetyukovitch bowed with dignity and said that he had no more questions to ask of the witness. The public and the jury, of course, were left with a grain of doubt in their minds as to the evidence of a man who might, while undergoing a certain cure, have seen “the gates of heaven,” and who did not even know what year he was living in. But before Grigory left the box another episode occurred. The President, turning to the prisoner, asked him whether he had any comment to make on the evidence of the last witness.\n\n“Except about the door, all he has said is true,” cried Mitya, in a loud voice. “For combing the lice off me, I thank him; for forgiving my blows, I thank him. The old man has been honest all his life and as faithful to my father as seven hundred poodles.”\n\n“Prisoner, be careful in your language,” the President admonished him.\n\n“I am not a poodle,” Grigory muttered.\n\n“All right, it’s I am a poodle myself,” cried Mitya. “If it’s an insult, I take it to myself and I beg his pardon. I was a beast and cruel to him. I was cruel to Æsop too.”\n\n“What Æsop?” the President asked sternly again.\n\n“Oh, Pierrot ... my father, Fyodor Pavlovitch.”\n\nThe President again and again warned Mitya impressively and very sternly to be more careful in his language.\n\n“You are injuring yourself in the opinion of your judges.”\n\nThe counsel for the defense was equally clever in dealing with the evidence of Rakitin. I may remark that Rakitin was one of the leading witnesses and one to whom the prosecutor attached great significance. It appeared that he knew everything; his knowledge was amazing, he had been everywhere, seen everything, talked to everybody, knew every detail of the biography of Fyodor Pavlovitch and all the Karamazovs. Of the envelope, it is true, he had only heard from Mitya himself. But he described minutely Mitya’s exploits in the “Metropolis,” all his compromising doings and sayings, and told the story of Captain Snegiryov’s “wisp of tow.” But even Rakitin could say nothing positive about Mitya’s inheritance, and confined himself to contemptuous generalities.\n\n“Who could tell which of them was to blame, and which was in debt to the other, with their crazy Karamazov way of muddling things so that no one could make head or tail of it?” He attributed the tragic crime to the habits that had become ingrained by ages of serfdom and the distressed condition of Russia, due to the lack of appropriate institutions. He was, in fact, allowed some latitude of speech. This was the first occasion on which Rakitin showed what he could do, and attracted notice. The prosecutor knew that the witness was preparing a magazine article on the case, and afterwards in his speech, as we shall see later, quoted some ideas from the article, showing that he had seen it already. The picture drawn by the witness was a gloomy and sinister one, and greatly strengthened the case for the prosecution. Altogether, Rakitin’s discourse fascinated the public by its independence and the extraordinary nobility of its ideas. There were even two or three outbreaks of applause when he spoke of serfdom and the distressed condition of Russia.\n\nBut Rakitin, in his youthful ardor, made a slight blunder, of which the counsel for the defense at once adroitly took advantage. Answering certain questions about Grushenka, and carried away by the loftiness of his own sentiments and his success, of which he was, of course, conscious, he went so far as to speak somewhat contemptuously of Agrafena Alexandrovna as “the kept mistress of Samsonov.” He would have given a good deal to take back his words afterwards, for Fetyukovitch caught him out over it at once. And it was all because Rakitin had not reckoned on the lawyer having been able to become so intimately acquainted with every detail in so short a time.\n\n“Allow me to ask,” began the counsel for the defense, with the most affable and even respectful smile, “you are, of course, the same Mr. Rakitin whose pamphlet, The Life of the Deceased Elder, Father Zossima, published by the diocesan authorities, full of profound and religious reflections and preceded by an excellent and devout dedication to the bishop, I have just read with such pleasure?”\n\n“I did not write it for publication ... it was published afterwards,” muttered Rakitin, for some reason fearfully disconcerted and almost ashamed.\n\n“Oh, that’s excellent! A thinker like you can, and indeed ought to, take the widest view of every social question. Your most instructive pamphlet has been widely circulated through the patronage of the bishop, and has been of appreciable service.... But this is the chief thing I should like to learn from you. You stated just now that you were very intimately acquainted with Madame Svyetlov.” (It must be noted that Grushenka’s surname was Svyetlov. I heard it for the first time that day, during the case.)\n\n“I cannot answer for all my acquaintances.... I am a young man ... and who can be responsible for every one he meets?” cried Rakitin, flushing all over.\n\n“I understand, I quite understand,” cried Fetyukovitch, as though he, too, were embarrassed and in haste to excuse himself. “You, like any other, might well be interested in an acquaintance with a young and beautiful woman who would readily entertain the élite of the youth of the neighborhood, but ... I only wanted to know ... It has come to my knowledge that Madame Svyetlov was particularly anxious a couple of months ago to make the acquaintance of the younger Karamazov, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and promised you twenty-five roubles, if you would bring him to her in his monastic dress. And that actually took place on the evening of the day on which the terrible crime, which is the subject of the present investigation, was committed. You brought Alexey Karamazov to Madame Svyetlov, and did you receive the twenty-five roubles from Madame Svyetlov as a reward, that’s what I wanted to hear from you?”\n\n“It was a joke.... I don’t see of what interest that can be to you.... I took it for a joke ... meaning to give it back later....”\n\n“Then you did take— But you have not given it back yet ... or have you?”\n\n“That’s of no consequence,” muttered Rakitin, “I refuse to answer such questions.... Of course I shall give it back.”\n\nThe President intervened, but Fetyukovitch declared he had no more questions to ask of the witness. Mr. Rakitin left the witness-box not absolutely without a stain upon his character. The effect left by the lofty idealism of his speech was somewhat marred, and Fetyukovitch’s expression, as he watched him walk away, seemed to suggest to the public “this is a specimen of the lofty-minded persons who accuse him.” I remember that this incident, too, did not pass off without an outbreak from Mitya. Enraged by the tone in which Rakitin had referred to Grushenka, he suddenly shouted “Bernard!” When, after Rakitin’s cross-examination, the President asked the prisoner if he had anything to say, Mitya cried loudly:\n\n“Since I’ve been arrested, he has borrowed money from me! He is a contemptible Bernard and opportunist, and he doesn’t believe in God; he took the bishop in!”\n\nMitya, of course, was pulled up again for the intemperance of his language, but Rakitin was done for. Captain Snegiryov’s evidence was a failure, too, but from quite a different reason. He appeared in ragged and dirty clothes, muddy boots, and in spite of the vigilance and expert observation of the police officers, he turned out to be hopelessly drunk. On being asked about Mitya’s attack upon him, he refused to answer.\n\n“God bless him. Ilusha told me not to. God will make it up to me yonder.”\n\n“Who told you not to tell? Of whom are you talking?”\n\n“Ilusha, my little son. ‘Father, father, how he insulted you!’ He said that at the stone. Now he is dying....”\n\nThe captain suddenly began sobbing, and plumped down on his knees before the President. He was hurriedly led away amidst the laughter of the public. The effect prepared by the prosecutor did not come off at all.\n\nFetyukovitch went on making the most of every opportunity, and amazed people more and more by his minute knowledge of the case. Thus, for example, Trifon Borissovitch made a great impression, of course, very prejudicial to Mitya. He calculated almost on his fingers that on his first visit to Mokroe, Mitya must have spent three thousand roubles, “or very little less. Just think what he squandered on those gypsy girls alone! And as for our lousy peasants, it wasn’t a case of flinging half a rouble in the street, he made them presents of twenty-five roubles each, at least, he didn’t give them less. And what a lot of money was simply stolen from him! And if any one did steal, he did not leave a receipt. How could one catch the thief when he was flinging his money away all the time? Our peasants are robbers, you know; they have no care for their souls. And the way he went on with the girls, our village girls! They’re completely set up since then, I tell you, they used to be poor.” He recalled, in fact, every item of expense and added it all up. So the theory that only fifteen hundred had been spent and the rest had been put aside in a little bag seemed inconceivable.\n\n“I saw three thousand as clear as a penny in his hands, I saw it with my own eyes; I should think I ought to know how to reckon money,” cried Trifon Borissovitch, doing his best to satisfy “his betters.”\n\nWhen Fetyukovitch had to cross-examine him, he scarcely tried to refute his evidence, but began asking him about an incident at the first carousal at Mokroe, a month before the arrest, when Timofey and another peasant called Akim had picked up on the floor in the passage a hundred roubles dropped by Mitya when he was drunk, and had given them to Trifon Borissovitch and received a rouble each from him for doing so. “Well,” asked the lawyer, “did you give that hundred roubles back to Mr. Karamazov?” Trifon Borissovitch shuffled in vain.... He was obliged, after the peasants had been examined, to admit the finding of the hundred roubles, only adding that he had religiously returned it all to Dmitri Fyodorovitch “in perfect honesty, and it’s only because his honor was in liquor at the time, he wouldn’t remember it.” But, as he had denied the incident of the hundred roubles till the peasants had been called to prove it, his evidence as to returning the money to Mitya was naturally regarded with great suspicion. So one of the most dangerous witnesses brought forward by the prosecution was again discredited.\n\nThe same thing happened with the Poles. They took up an attitude of pride and independence; they vociferated loudly that they had both been in the service of the Crown, and that “Pan Mitya” had offered them three thousand “to buy their honor,” and that they had seen a large sum of money in his hands. Pan Mussyalovitch introduced a terrible number of Polish words into his sentences, and seeing that this only increased his consequence in the eyes of the President and the prosecutor, grew more and more pompous, and ended by talking in Polish altogether. But Fetyukovitch caught them, too, in his snares. Trifon Borissovitch, recalled, was forced, in spite of his evasions, to admit that Pan Vrublevsky had substituted another pack of cards for the one he had provided, and that Pan Mussyalovitch had cheated during the game. Kalganov confirmed this, and both the Poles left the witness-box with damaged reputations, amidst laughter from the public.\n\nThen exactly the same thing happened with almost all the most dangerous witnesses. Fetyukovitch succeeded in casting a slur on all of them, and dismissing them with a certain derision. The lawyers and experts were lost in admiration, and were only at a loss to understand what good purpose could be served by it, for all, I repeat, felt that the case for the prosecution could not be refuted, but was growing more and more tragically overwhelming. But from the confidence of the “great magician” they saw that he was serene, and they waited, feeling that “such a man” had not come from Petersburg for nothing, and that he was not a man to return unsuccessful.\n\n# Chapter III. The Medical Experts And A Pound Of Nuts\n\nThe evidence of the medical experts, too, was of little use to the prisoner. And it appeared later that Fetyukovitch had not reckoned much upon it. The medical line of defense had only been taken up through the insistence of Katerina Ivanovna, who had sent for a celebrated doctor from Moscow on purpose. The case for the defense could, of course, lose nothing by it and might, with luck, gain something from it. There was, however, an element of comedy about it, through the difference of opinion of the doctors. The medical experts were the famous doctor from Moscow, our doctor, Herzenstube, and the young doctor, Varvinsky. The two latter appeared also as witnesses for the prosecution.\n\nThe first to be called in the capacity of expert was Doctor Herzenstube. He was a gray and bald old man of seventy, of middle height and sturdy build. He was much esteemed and respected by every one in the town. He was a conscientious doctor and an excellent and pious man, a Hernguter or Moravian brother, I am not quite sure which. He had been living amongst us for many years and behaved with wonderful dignity. He was a kind-hearted and humane man. He treated the sick poor and peasants for nothing, visited them in their slums and huts, and left money for medicine, but he was as obstinate as a mule. If once he had taken an idea into his head, there was no shaking it. Almost every one in the town was aware, by the way, that the famous doctor had, within the first two or three days of his presence among us, uttered some extremely offensive allusions to Doctor Herzenstube’s qualifications. Though the Moscow doctor asked twenty-five roubles for a visit, several people in the town were glad to take advantage of his arrival, and rushed to consult him regardless of expense. All these had, of course, been previously patients of Doctor Herzenstube, and the celebrated doctor had criticized his treatment with extreme harshness. Finally, he had asked the patients as soon as he saw them, “Well, who has been cramming you with nostrums? Herzenstube? He, he!” Doctor Herzenstube, of course, heard all this, and now all the three doctors made their appearance, one after another, to be examined.\n\nDoctor Herzenstube roundly declared that the abnormality of the prisoner’s mental faculties was self-evident. Then giving his grounds for this opinion, which I omit here, he added that the abnormality was not only evident in many of the prisoner’s actions in the past, but was apparent even now at this very moment. When he was asked to explain how it was apparent now at this moment, the old doctor, with simple-hearted directness, pointed out that the prisoner on entering the court had “an extraordinary air, remarkable in the circumstances”; that he had “marched in like a soldier, looking straight before him, though it would have been more natural for him to look to the left where, among the public, the ladies were sitting, seeing that he was a great admirer of the fair sex and must be thinking much of what the ladies are saying of him now,” the old man concluded in his peculiar language.\n\nI must add that he spoke Russian readily, but every phrase was formed in German style, which did not, however, trouble him, for it had always been a weakness of his to believe that he spoke Russian perfectly, better indeed than Russians. And he was very fond of using Russian proverbs, always declaring that the Russian proverbs were the best and most expressive sayings in the whole world. I may remark, too, that in conversation, through absent-mindedness he often forgot the most ordinary words, which sometimes went out of his head, though he knew them perfectly. The same thing happened, though, when he spoke German, and at such times he always waved his hand before his face as though trying to catch the lost word, and no one could induce him to go on speaking till he had found the missing word. His remark that the prisoner ought to have looked at the ladies on entering roused a whisper of amusement in the audience. All our ladies were very fond of our old doctor; they knew, too, that having been all his life a bachelor and a religious man of exemplary conduct, he looked upon women as lofty creatures. And so his unexpected observation struck every one as very queer.\n\nThe Moscow doctor, being questioned in his turn, definitely and emphatically repeated that he considered the prisoner’s mental condition abnormal in the highest degree. He talked at length and with erudition of “aberration” and “mania,” and argued that, from all the facts collected, the prisoner had undoubtedly been in a condition of aberration for several days before his arrest, and, if the crime had been committed by him, it must, even if he were conscious of it, have been almost involuntary, as he had not the power to control the morbid impulse that possessed him.\n\nBut apart from temporary aberration, the doctor diagnosed mania, which premised, in his words, to lead to complete insanity in the future. (It must be noted that I report this in my own words, the doctor made use of very learned and professional language.) “All his actions are in contravention of common sense and logic,” he continued. “Not to refer to what I have not seen, that is, the crime itself and the whole catastrophe, the day before yesterday, while he was talking to me, he had an unaccountably fixed look in his eye. He laughed unexpectedly when there was nothing to laugh at. He showed continual and inexplicable irritability, using strange words, ‘Bernard!’ ‘Ethics!’ and others equally inappropriate.” But the doctor detected mania, above all, in the fact that the prisoner could not even speak of the three thousand roubles, of which he considered himself to have been cheated, without extraordinary irritation, though he could speak comparatively lightly of other misfortunes and grievances. According to all accounts, he had even in the past, whenever the subject of the three thousand roubles was touched on, flown into a perfect frenzy, and yet he was reported to be a disinterested and not grasping man.\n\n“As to the opinion of my learned colleague,” the Moscow doctor added ironically in conclusion, “that the prisoner would, on entering the court, have naturally looked at the ladies and not straight before him, I will only say that, apart from the playfulness of this theory, it is radically unsound. For though I fully agree that the prisoner, on entering the court where his fate will be decided, would not naturally look straight before him in that fixed way, and that that may really be a sign of his abnormal mental condition, at the same time I maintain that he would naturally not look to the left at the ladies, but, on the contrary, to the right to find his legal adviser, on whose help all his hopes rest and on whose defense all his future depends.” The doctor expressed his opinion positively and emphatically.\n\nBut the unexpected pronouncement of Doctor Varvinsky gave the last touch of comedy to the difference of opinion between the experts. In his opinion the prisoner was now, and had been all along, in a perfectly normal condition, and, although he certainly must have been in a nervous and exceedingly excited state before his arrest, this might have been due to several perfectly obvious causes, jealousy, anger, continual drunkenness, and so on. But this nervous condition would not involve the mental aberration of which mention had just been made. As to the question whether the prisoner should have looked to the left or to the right on entering the court, “in his modest opinion,” the prisoner would naturally look straight before him on entering the court, as he had in fact done, as that was where the judges, on whom his fate depended, were sitting. So that it was just by looking straight before him that he showed his perfectly normal state of mind at the present. The young doctor concluded his “modest” testimony with some heat.\n\n“Bravo, doctor!” cried Mitya, from his seat, “just so!”\n\nMitya, of course, was checked, but the young doctor’s opinion had a decisive influence on the judges and on the public, and, as appeared afterwards, every one agreed with him. But Doctor Herzenstube, when called as a witness, was quite unexpectedly of use to Mitya. As an old resident in the town who had known the Karamazov family for years, he furnished some facts of great value for the prosecution, and suddenly, as though recalling something, he added:\n\n“But the poor young man might have had a very different life, for he had a good heart both in childhood and after childhood, that I know. But the Russian proverb says, ‘If a man has one head, it’s good, but if another clever man comes to visit him, it would be better still, for then there will be two heads and not only one.’”\n\n“One head is good, but two are better,” the prosecutor put in impatiently. He knew the old man’s habit of talking slowly and deliberately, regardless of the impression he was making and of the delay he was causing, and highly prizing his flat, dull and always gleefully complacent German wit. The old man was fond of making jokes.\n\n“Oh, yes, that’s what I say,” he went on stubbornly. “One head is good, but two are much better, but he did not meet another head with wits, and his wits went. Where did they go? I’ve forgotten the word.” He went on, passing his hand before his eyes, “Oh, yes, spazieren.”\n“Wandering?”\n\n“Oh, yes, wandering, that’s what I say. Well, his wits went wandering and fell in such a deep hole that he lost himself. And yet he was a grateful and sensitive boy. Oh, I remember him very well, a little chap so high, left neglected by his father in the back yard, when he ran about without boots on his feet, and his little breeches hanging by one button.”\n\nA note of feeling and tenderness suddenly came into the honest old man’s voice. Fetyukovitch positively started, as though scenting something, and caught at it instantly.\n\n“Oh, yes, I was a young man then.... I was ... well, I was forty-five then, and had only just come here. And I was so sorry for the boy then; I asked myself why shouldn’t I buy him a pound of ... a pound of what? I’ve forgotten what it’s called. A pound of what children are very fond of, what is it, what is it?” The doctor began waving his hands again. “It grows on a tree and is gathered and given to every one....”\n\n“Apples?”\n\n“Oh, no, no. You have a dozen of apples, not a pound.... No, there are a lot of them, and all little. You put them in the mouth and crack.”\n“Nuts?”\n\n“Quite so, nuts, I say so.” The doctor repeated in the calmest way as though he had been at no loss for a word. “And I bought him a pound of nuts, for no one had ever bought the boy a pound of nuts before. And I lifted my finger and said to him, ‘Boy, Gott der Vater.’ He laughed and said, ‘Gott der Vater.’... ‘Gott der Sohn.’ He laughed again and lisped, ‘Gott der Sohn.’ ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ Then he laughed and said as best he could, ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ I went away, and two days after I happened to be passing, and he shouted to me of himself, ‘Uncle, Gott der Vater, Gott der Sohn,’ and he had only forgotten ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ But I reminded him of it and I felt very sorry for him again. But he was taken away, and I did not see him again. Twenty-three years passed. I am sitting one morning in my study, a white-haired old man, when there walks into the room a blooming young man, whom I should never have recognized, but he held up his finger and said, laughing, ‘Gott der Vater, Gott der Sohn, and Gott der heilige Geist. I have just arrived and have come to thank you for that pound of nuts, for no one else ever bought me a pound of nuts; you are the only one that ever did.’ And then I remembered my happy youth and the poor child in the yard, without boots on his feet, and my heart was touched and I said, ‘You are a grateful young man, for you have remembered all your life the pound of nuts I bought you in your childhood.’ And I embraced him and blessed him. And I shed tears. He laughed, but he shed tears, too ... for the Russian often laughs when he ought to be weeping. But he did weep; I saw it. And now, alas!...”\n\n“And I am weeping now, German, I am weeping now, too, you saintly man,” Mitya cried suddenly.\n\nIn any case the anecdote made a certain favorable impression on the public. But the chief sensation in Mitya’s favor was created by the evidence of Katerina Ivanovna, which I will describe directly. Indeed, when the witnesses à décharge, that is, called by the defense, began giving evidence, fortune seemed all at once markedly more favorable to Mitya, and what was particularly striking, this was a surprise even to the counsel for the defense. But before Katerina Ivanovna was called, Alyosha was examined, and he recalled a fact which seemed to furnish positive evidence against one important point made by the prosecution.\n\n# Chapter IV. Fortune Smiles On Mitya\n\nIt came quite as a surprise even to Alyosha himself. He was not required to take the oath, and I remember that both sides addressed him very gently and sympathetically. It was evident that his reputation for goodness had preceded him. Alyosha gave his evidence modestly and with restraint, but his warm sympathy for his unhappy brother was unmistakable. In answer to one question, he sketched his brother’s character as that of a man, violent-tempered perhaps and carried away by his passions, but at the same time honorable, proud and generous, capable of self-sacrifice, if necessary. He admitted, however, that, through his passion for Grushenka and his rivalry with his father, his brother had been of late in an intolerable position. But he repelled with indignation the suggestion that his brother might have committed a murder for the sake of gain, though he recognized that the three thousand roubles had become almost an obsession with Mitya; that he looked upon them as part of the inheritance he had been cheated of by his father, and that, indifferent as he was to money as a rule, he could not even speak of that three thousand without fury. As for the rivalry of the two “ladies,” as the prosecutor expressed it—that is, of Grushenka and Katya—he answered evasively and was even unwilling to answer one or two questions altogether.\n\n“Did your brother tell you, anyway, that he intended to kill your father?” asked the prosecutor. “You can refuse to answer if you think necessary,” he added.\n\n“He did not tell me so directly,” answered Alyosha.\n\n“How so? Did he indirectly?”\n\n“He spoke to me once of his hatred for our father and his fear that at an extreme moment ... at a moment of fury, he might perhaps murder him.”\n\n“And you believed him?”\n\n“I am afraid to say that I did. But I never doubted that some higher feeling would always save him at the fatal moment, as it has indeed saved him, for it was not he killed my father,” Alyosha said firmly, in a loud voice that was heard throughout the court.\n\nThe prosecutor started like a war-horse at the sound of a trumpet.\n\n“Let me assure you that I fully believe in the complete sincerity of your conviction and do not explain it by or identify it with your affection for your unhappy brother. Your peculiar view of the whole tragic episode is known to us already from the preliminary investigation. I won’t attempt to conceal from you that it is highly individual and contradicts all the other evidence collected by the prosecution. And so I think it essential to press you to tell me what facts have led you to this conviction of your brother’s innocence and of the guilt of another person against whom you gave evidence at the preliminary inquiry?”\n\n“I only answered the questions asked me at the preliminary inquiry,” replied Alyosha, slowly and calmly. “I made no accusation against Smerdyakov of myself.”\n\n“Yet you gave evidence against him?”\n\n“I was led to do so by my brother Dmitri’s words. I was told what took place at his arrest and how he had pointed to Smerdyakov before I was examined. I believe absolutely that my brother is innocent, and if he didn’t commit the murder, then—”\n\n“Then Smerdyakov? Why Smerdyakov? And why are you so completely persuaded of your brother’s innocence?”\n\n“I cannot help believing my brother. I know he wouldn’t lie to me. I saw from his face he wasn’t lying.”\n\n“Only from his face? Is that all the proof you have?”\n\n“I have no other proof.”\n\n“And of Smerdyakov’s guilt you have no proof whatever but your brother’s word and the expression of his face?”\n\n“No, I have no other proof.”\n\nThe prosecutor dropped the examination at this point. The impression left by Alyosha’s evidence on the public was most disappointing. There had been talk about Smerdyakov before the trial; some one had heard something, some one had pointed out something else, it was said that Alyosha had gathered together some extraordinary proofs of his brother’s innocence and Smerdyakov’s guilt, and after all there was nothing, no evidence except certain moral convictions so natural in a brother.\n\nBut Fetyukovitch began his cross-examination. On his asking Alyosha when it was that the prisoner had told him of his hatred for his father and that he might kill him, and whether he had heard it, for instance, at their last meeting before the catastrophe, Alyosha started as he answered, as though only just recollecting and understanding something.\n\n“I remember one circumstance now which I’d quite forgotten myself. It wasn’t clear to me at the time, but now—”\n\nAnd, obviously only now for the first time struck by an idea, he recounted eagerly how, at his last interview with Mitya that evening under the tree, on the road to the monastery, Mitya had struck himself on the breast, “the upper part of the breast,” and had repeated several times that he had a means of regaining his honor, that that means was here, here on his breast. “I thought, when he struck himself on the breast, he meant that it was in his heart,” Alyosha continued, “that he might find in his heart strength to save himself from some awful disgrace which was awaiting him and which he did not dare confess even to me. I must confess I did think at the time that he was speaking of our father, and that the disgrace he was shuddering at was the thought of going to our father and doing some violence to him. Yet it was just then that he pointed to something on his breast, so that I remember the idea struck me at the time that the heart is not on that part of the breast, but below, and that he struck himself much too high, just below the neck, and kept pointing to that place. My idea seemed silly to me at the time, but he was perhaps pointing then to that little bag in which he had fifteen hundred roubles!”\n\n“Just so,” Mitya cried from his place. “That’s right, Alyosha, it was the little bag I struck with my fist.”\n\nFetyukovitch flew to him in hot haste entreating him to keep quiet, and at the same instant pounced on Alyosha. Alyosha, carried away himself by his recollection, warmly expressed his theory that this disgrace was probably just that fifteen hundred roubles on him, which he might have returned to Katerina Ivanovna as half of what he owed her, but which he had yet determined not to repay her and to use for another purpose—namely, to enable him to elope with Grushenka, if she consented.\n\n“It is so, it must be so,” exclaimed Alyosha, in sudden excitement. “My brother cried several times that half of the disgrace, half of it (he said half several times) he could free himself from at once, but that he was so unhappy in his weakness of will that he wouldn’t do it ... that he knew beforehand he was incapable of doing it!”\n\n“And you clearly, confidently remember that he struck himself just on this part of the breast?” Fetyukovitch asked eagerly.\n\n“Clearly and confidently, for I thought at the time, ‘Why does he strike himself up there when the heart is lower down?’ and the thought seemed stupid to me at the time ... I remember its seeming stupid ... it flashed through my mind. That’s what brought it back to me just now. How could I have forgotten it till now? It was that little bag he meant when he said he had the means but wouldn’t give back that fifteen hundred. And when he was arrested at Mokroe he cried out—I know, I was told it—that he considered it the most disgraceful act of his life that when he had the means of repaying Katerina Ivanovna half (half, note!) what he owed her, he yet could not bring himself to repay the money and preferred to remain a thief in her eyes rather than part with it. And what torture, what torture that debt has been to him!” Alyosha exclaimed in conclusion.\n\nThe prosecutor, of course, intervened. He asked Alyosha to describe once more how it had all happened, and several times insisted on the question, “Had the prisoner seemed to point to anything? Perhaps he had simply struck himself with his fist on the breast?”\n\n“But it was not with his fist,” cried Alyosha; “he pointed with his fingers and pointed here, very high up.... How could I have so completely forgotten it till this moment?”\n\nThe President asked Mitya what he had to say to the last witness’s evidence. Mitya confirmed it, saying that he had been pointing to the fifteen hundred roubles which were on his breast, just below the neck, and that that was, of course, the disgrace, “A disgrace I cannot deny, the most shameful act of my whole life,” cried Mitya. “I might have repaid it and didn’t repay it. I preferred to remain a thief in her eyes rather than give it back. And the most shameful part of it was that I knew beforehand I shouldn’t give it back! You are right, Alyosha! Thanks, Alyosha!”\n\nSo Alyosha’s cross-examination ended. What was important and striking about it was that one fact at least had been found, and even though this were only one tiny bit of evidence, a mere hint at evidence, it did go some little way towards proving that the bag had existed and had contained fifteen hundred roubles and that the prisoner had not been lying at the preliminary inquiry when he alleged at Mokroe that those fifteen hundred roubles were “his own.” Alyosha was glad. With a flushed face he moved away to the seat assigned to him. He kept repeating to himself: “How was it I forgot? How could I have forgotten it? And what made it come back to me now?”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna was called to the witness-box. As she entered something extraordinary happened in the court. The ladies clutched their lorgnettes and opera-glasses. There was a stir among the men: some stood up to get a better view. Everybody alleged afterwards that Mitya had turned “white as a sheet” on her entrance. All in black, she advanced modestly, almost timidly. It was impossible to tell from her face that she was agitated; but there was a resolute gleam in her dark and gloomy eyes. I may remark that many people mentioned that she looked particularly handsome at that moment. She spoke softly but clearly, so that she was heard all over the court. She expressed herself with composure, or at least tried to appear composed. The President began his examination discreetly and very respectfully, as though afraid to touch on “certain chords,” and showing consideration for her great unhappiness. But in answer to one of the first questions Katerina Ivanovna replied firmly that she had been formerly betrothed to the prisoner, “until he left me of his own accord...” she added quietly. When they asked her about the three thousand she had entrusted to Mitya to post to her relations, she said firmly, “I didn’t give him the money simply to send it off. I felt at the time that he was in great need of money.... I gave him the three thousand on the understanding that he should post it within the month if he cared to. There was no need for him to worry himself about that debt afterwards.”\n\nI will not repeat all the questions asked her and all her answers in detail. I will only give the substance of her evidence.\n\n“I was firmly convinced that he would send off that sum as soon as he got money from his father,” she went on. “I have never doubted his disinterestedness and his honesty ... his scrupulous honesty ... in money matters. He felt quite certain that he would receive the money from his father, and spoke to me several times about it. I knew he had a feud with his father and have always believed that he had been unfairly treated by his father. I don’t remember any threat uttered by him against his father. He certainly never uttered any such threat before me. If he had come to me at that time, I should have at once relieved his anxiety about that unlucky three thousand roubles, but he had given up coming to see me ... and I myself was put in such a position ... that I could not invite him.... And I had no right, indeed, to be exacting as to that money,” she added suddenly, and there was a ring of resolution in her voice. “I was once indebted to him for assistance in money for more than three thousand, and I took it, although I could not at that time foresee that I should ever be in a position to repay my debt.”\n\nThere was a note of defiance in her voice. It was then Fetyukovitch began his cross-examination.\n\n“Did that take place not here, but at the beginning of your acquaintance?” Fetyukovitch suggested cautiously, feeling his way, instantly scenting something favorable. I must mention in parenthesis that, though Fetyukovitch had been brought from Petersburg partly at the instance of Katerina Ivanovna herself, he knew nothing about the episode of the four thousand roubles given her by Mitya, and of her “bowing to the ground to him.” She concealed this from him and said nothing about it, and that was strange. It may be pretty certainly assumed that she herself did not know till the very last minute whether she would speak of that episode in the court, and waited for the inspiration of the moment.\n\nNo, I can never forget those moments. She began telling her story. She told everything, the whole episode that Mitya had told Alyosha, and her bowing to the ground, and her reason. She told about her father and her going to Mitya, and did not in one word, in a single hint, suggest that Mitya had himself, through her sister, proposed they should “send him Katerina Ivanovna” to fetch the money. She generously concealed that and was not ashamed to make it appear as though she had of her own impulse run to the young officer, relying on something ... to beg him for the money. It was something tremendous! I turned cold and trembled as I listened. The court was hushed, trying to catch each word. It was something unexampled. Even from such a self-willed and contemptuously proud girl as she was, such an extremely frank avowal, such sacrifice, such self-immolation, seemed incredible. And for what, for whom? To save the man who had deceived and insulted her and to help, in however small a degree, in saving him, by creating a strong impression in his favor. And, indeed, the figure of the young officer who, with a respectful bow to the innocent girl, handed her his last four thousand roubles—all he had in the world—was thrown into a very sympathetic and attractive light, but ... I had a painful misgiving at heart! I felt that calumny might come of it later (and it did, in fact, it did). It was repeated all over the town afterwards with spiteful laughter that the story was perhaps not quite complete—that is, in the statement that the officer had let the young lady depart “with nothing but a respectful bow.” It was hinted that something was here omitted.\n\n“And even if nothing had been omitted, if this were the whole story,” the most highly respected of our ladies maintained, “even then it’s very doubtful whether it was creditable for a young girl to behave in that way, even for the sake of saving her father.”\n\nAnd can Katerina Ivanovna, with her intelligence, her morbid sensitiveness, have failed to understand that people would talk like that? She must have understood it, yet she made up her mind to tell everything. Of course, all these nasty little suspicions as to the truth of her story only arose afterwards and at the first moment all were deeply impressed by it. As for the judges and the lawyers, they listened in reverent, almost shame-faced silence to Katerina Ivanovna. The prosecutor did not venture upon even one question on the subject. Fetyukovitch made a low bow to her. Oh, he was almost triumphant! Much ground had been gained. For a man to give his last four thousand on a generous impulse and then for the same man to murder his father for the sake of robbing him of three thousand—the idea seemed too incongruous. Fetyukovitch felt that now the charge of theft, at least, was as good as disproved. “The case” was thrown into quite a different light. There was a wave of sympathy for Mitya. As for him.... I was told that once or twice, while Katerina Ivanovna was giving her evidence, he jumped up from his seat, sank back again, and hid his face in his hands. But when she had finished, he suddenly cried in a sobbing voice:\n\n“Katya, why have you ruined me?” and his sobs were audible all over the court. But he instantly restrained himself, and cried again:\n\n“Now I am condemned!”\n\nThen he sat rigid in his place, with his teeth clenched and his arms across his chest. Katerina Ivanovna remained in the court and sat down in her place. She was pale and sat with her eyes cast down. Those who were sitting near her declared that for a long time she shivered all over as though in a fever. Grushenka was called.\n\nI am approaching the sudden catastrophe which was perhaps the final cause of Mitya’s ruin. For I am convinced, so is every one—all the lawyers said the same afterwards—that if the episode had not occurred, the prisoner would at least have been recommended to mercy. But of that later. A few words first about Grushenka.\n\nShe, too, was dressed entirely in black, with her magnificent black shawl on her shoulders. She walked to the witness-box with her smooth, noiseless tread, with the slightly swaying gait common in women of full figure. She looked steadily at the President, turning her eyes neither to the right nor to the left. To my thinking she looked very handsome at that moment, and not at all pale, as the ladies alleged afterwards. They declared, too, that she had a concentrated and spiteful expression. I believe that she was simply irritated and painfully conscious of the contemptuous and inquisitive eyes of our scandal-loving public. She was proud and could not stand contempt. She was one of those people who flare up, angry and eager to retaliate, at the mere suggestion of contempt. There was an element of timidity, too, of course, and inward shame at her own timidity, so it was not strange that her tone kept changing. At one moment it was angry, contemptuous and rough, and at another there was a sincere note of self-condemnation. Sometimes she spoke as though she were taking a desperate plunge; as though she felt, “I don’t care what happens, I’ll say it....” Apropos of her acquaintance with Fyodor Pavlovitch, she remarked curtly, “That’s all nonsense, and was it my fault that he would pester me?” But a minute later she added, “It was all my fault. I was laughing at them both—at the old man and at him, too—and I brought both of them to this. It was all on account of me it happened.”\n\nSamsonov’s name came up somehow. “That’s nobody’s business,” she snapped at once, with a sort of insolent defiance. “He was my benefactor; he took me when I hadn’t a shoe to my foot, when my family had turned me out.” The President reminded her, though very politely, that she must answer the questions directly, without going off into irrelevant details. Grushenka crimsoned and her eyes flashed.\n\nThe envelope with the notes in it she had not seen, but had only heard from “that wicked wretch” that Fyodor Pavlovitch had an envelope with notes for three thousand in it. “But that was all foolishness. I was only laughing. I wouldn’t have gone to him for anything.”\n\n“To whom are you referring as ‘that wicked wretch’?” inquired the prosecutor.\n\n“The lackey, Smerdyakov, who murdered his master and hanged himself last night.”\n\nShe was, of course, at once asked what ground she had for such a definite accusation; but it appeared that she, too, had no grounds for it.\n\n“Dmitri Fyodorovitch told me so himself; you can believe him. The woman who came between us has ruined him; she is the cause of it all, let me tell you,” Grushenka added. She seemed to be quivering with hatred, and there was a vindictive note in her voice.\n\nShe was again asked to whom she was referring.\n\n“The young lady, Katerina Ivanovna there. She sent for me, offered me chocolate, tried to fascinate me. There’s not much true shame about her, I can tell you that....”\n\nAt this point the President checked her sternly, begging her to moderate her language. But the jealous woman’s heart was burning, and she did not care what she did.\n\n“When the prisoner was arrested at Mokroe,” the prosecutor asked, “every one saw and heard you run out of the next room and cry out: ‘It’s all my fault. We’ll go to Siberia together!’ So you already believed him to have murdered his father?”\n\n“I don’t remember what I felt at the time,” answered Grushenka. “Every one was crying out that he had killed his father, and I felt that it was my fault, that it was on my account he had murdered him. But when he said he wasn’t guilty, I believed him at once, and I believe him now and always shall believe him. He is not the man to tell a lie.”\n\nFetyukovitch began his cross-examination. I remember that among other things he asked about Rakitin and the twenty-five roubles “you paid him for bringing Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov to see you.”\n\n“There was nothing strange about his taking the money,” sneered Grushenka, with angry contempt. “He was always coming to me for money: he used to get thirty roubles a month at least out of me, chiefly for luxuries: he had enough to keep him without my help.”\n\n“What led you to be so liberal to Mr. Rakitin?” Fetyukovitch asked, in spite of an uneasy movement on the part of the President.\n\n“Why, he is my cousin. His mother was my mother’s sister. But he’s always besought me not to tell any one here of it, he is so dreadfully ashamed of me.”\n\nThis fact was a complete surprise to every one; no one in the town nor in the monastery, not even Mitya, knew of it. I was told that Rakitin turned purple with shame where he sat. Grushenka had somehow heard before she came into the court that he had given evidence against Mitya, and so she was angry. The whole effect on the public, of Rakitin’s speech, of his noble sentiments, of his attacks upon serfdom and the political disorder of Russia, was this time finally ruined. Fetyukovitch was satisfied: it was another godsend. Grushenka’s cross-examination did not last long and, of course, there could be nothing particularly new in her evidence. She left a very disagreeable impression on the public; hundreds of contemptuous eyes were fixed upon her, as she finished giving her evidence and sat down again in the court, at a good distance from Katerina Ivanovna. Mitya was silent throughout her evidence. He sat as though turned to stone, with his eyes fixed on the ground.\n\nIvan was called to give evidence.\n\n# Chapter V. A Sudden Catastrophe\n\nI may note that he had been called before Alyosha. But the usher of the court announced to the President that, owing to an attack of illness or some sort of fit, the witness could not appear at the moment, but was ready to give his evidence as soon as he recovered. But no one seemed to have heard it and it only came out later.\n\nHis entrance was for the first moment almost unnoticed. The principal witnesses, especially the two rival ladies, had already been questioned. Curiosity was satisfied for the time; the public was feeling almost fatigued. Several more witnesses were still to be heard, who probably had little information to give after all that had been given. Time was passing. Ivan walked up with extraordinary slowness, looking at no one, and with his head bowed, as though plunged in gloomy thought. He was irreproachably dressed, but his face made a painful impression, on me at least: there was an earthy look in it, a look like a dying man’s. His eyes were lusterless; he raised them and looked slowly round the court. Alyosha jumped up from his seat and moaned “Ah!” I remember that, but it was hardly noticed.\n\nThe President began by informing him that he was a witness not on oath, that he might answer or refuse to answer, but that, of course, he must bear witness according to his conscience, and so on, and so on. Ivan listened and looked at him blankly, but his face gradually relaxed into a smile, and as soon as the President, looking at him in astonishment, finished, he laughed outright.\n\n“Well, and what else?” he asked in a loud voice.\n\nThere was a hush in the court; there was a feeling of something strange. The President showed signs of uneasiness.\n\n“You ... are perhaps still unwell?” he began, looking everywhere for the usher.\n\n“Don’t trouble yourself, your excellency, I am well enough and can tell you something interesting,” Ivan answered with sudden calmness and respectfulness.\n\n“You have some special communication to make?” the President went on, still mistrustfully.\n\nIvan looked down, waited a few seconds and, raising his head, answered, almost stammering:\n\n“No ... I haven’t. I have nothing particular.”\n\nThey began asking him questions. He answered, as it were, reluctantly, with extreme brevity, with a sort of disgust which grew more and more marked, though he answered rationally. To many questions he answered that he did not know. He knew nothing of his father’s money relations with Dmitri. “I wasn’t interested in the subject,” he added. Threats to murder his father he had heard from the prisoner. Of the money in the envelope he had heard from Smerdyakov.\n\n“The same thing over and over again,” he interrupted suddenly, with a look of weariness. “I have nothing particular to tell the court.”\n\n“I see you are unwell and understand your feelings,” the President began.\n\nHe turned to the prosecutor and the counsel for the defense to invite them to examine the witness, if necessary, when Ivan suddenly asked in an exhausted voice:\n\n“Let me go, your excellency, I feel very ill.”\n\nAnd with these words, without waiting for permission, he turned to walk out of the court. But after taking four steps he stood still, as though he had reached a decision, smiled slowly, and went back.\n\n“I am like the peasant girl, your excellency ... you know. How does it go? ‘I’ll stand up if I like, and I won’t if I don’t.’ They were trying to put on her sarafan to take her to church to be married, and she said, ‘I’ll stand up if I like, and I won’t if I don’t.’... It’s in some book about the peasantry.”\n\n“What do you mean by that?” the President asked severely.\n\n“Why, this,” Ivan suddenly pulled out a roll of notes. “Here’s the money ... the notes that lay in that envelope” (he nodded towards the table on which lay the material evidence), “for the sake of which our father was murdered. Where shall I put them? Mr. Superintendent, take them.”\n\nThe usher of the court took the whole roll and handed it to the President.\n\n“How could this money have come into your possession if it is the same money?” the President asked wonderingly.\n\n“I got them from Smerdyakov, from the murderer, yesterday.... I was with him just before he hanged himself. It was he, not my brother, killed our father. He murdered him and I incited him to do it ... Who doesn’t desire his father’s death?”\n\n“Are you in your right mind?” broke involuntarily from the President.\n\n“I should think I am in my right mind ... in the same nasty mind as all of you ... as all these ... ugly faces.” He turned suddenly to the audience. “My father has been murdered and they pretend they are horrified,” he snarled, with furious contempt. “They keep up the sham with one another. Liars! They all desire the death of their fathers. One reptile devours another.... If there hadn’t been a murder, they’d have been angry and gone home ill-humored. It’s a spectacle they want! Panem et circenses. Though I am one to talk! Have you any water? Give me a drink for Christ’s sake!” He suddenly clutched his head.\n\nThe usher at once approached him. Alyosha jumped up and cried, “He is ill. Don’t believe him: he has brain fever.” Katerina Ivanovna rose impulsively from her seat and, rigid with horror, gazed at Ivan. Mitya stood up and greedily looked at his brother and listened to him with a wild, strange smile.\n\n“Don’t disturb yourselves. I am not mad, I am only a murderer,” Ivan began again. “You can’t expect eloquence from a murderer,” he added suddenly for some reason and laughed a queer laugh.\n\nThe prosecutor bent over to the President in obvious dismay. The two other judges communicated in agitated whispers. Fetyukovitch pricked up his ears as he listened: the hall was hushed in expectation. The President seemed suddenly to recollect himself.\n\n“Witness, your words are incomprehensible and impossible here. Calm yourself, if you can, and tell your story ... if you really have something to tell. How can you confirm your statement ... if indeed you are not delirious?”\n\n“That’s just it. I have no proof. That cur Smerdyakov won’t send you proofs from the other world ... in an envelope. You think of nothing but envelopes—one is enough. I’ve no witnesses ... except one, perhaps,” he smiled thoughtfully.\n\n“Who is your witness?”\n\n“He has a tail, your excellency, and that would be irregular! Le diable n’existe point! Don’t pay attention: he is a paltry, pitiful devil,” he added suddenly. He ceased laughing and spoke as it were, confidentially. “He is here somewhere, no doubt—under that table with the material evidence on it, perhaps. Where should he sit if not there? You see, listen to me. I told him I don’t want to keep quiet, and he talked about the geological cataclysm ... idiocy! Come, release the monster ... he’s been singing a hymn. That’s because his heart is light! It’s like a drunken man in the street bawling how ‘Vanka went to Petersburg,’ and I would give a quadrillion quadrillions for two seconds of joy. You don’t know me! Oh, how stupid all this business is! Come, take me instead of him! I didn’t come for nothing.... Why, why is everything so stupid?...”\n\nAnd he began slowly, and as it were reflectively, looking round him again. But the court was all excitement by now. Alyosha rushed towards him, but the court usher had already seized Ivan by the arm.\n\n“What are you about?” he cried, staring into the man’s face, and suddenly seizing him by the shoulders, he flung him violently to the floor. But the police were on the spot and he was seized. He screamed furiously. And all the time he was being removed, he yelled and screamed something incoherent.\n\nThe whole court was thrown into confusion. I don’t remember everything as it happened. I was excited myself and could not follow. I only know that afterwards, when everything was quiet again and every one understood what had happened, the court usher came in for a reprimand, though he very reasonably explained that the witness had been quite well, that the doctor had seen him an hour ago, when he had a slight attack of giddiness, but that, until he had come into the court, he had talked quite consecutively, so that nothing could have been foreseen—that he had, in fact, insisted on giving evidence. But before every one had completely regained their composure and recovered from this scene, it was followed by another. Katerina Ivanovna had an attack of hysterics. She sobbed, shrieking loudly, but refused to leave the court, struggled, and besought them not to remove her. Suddenly she cried to the President:\n\n“There is more evidence I must give at once ... at once! Here is a document, a letter ... take it, read it quickly, quickly! It’s a letter from that monster ... that man there, there!” she pointed to Mitya. “It was he killed his father, you will see that directly. He wrote to me how he would kill his father! But the other one is ill, he is ill, he is delirious!” she kept crying out, beside herself.\n\nThe court usher took the document she held out to the President, and she, dropping into her chair, hiding her face in her hands, began convulsively and noiselessly sobbing, shaking all over, and stifling every sound for fear she should be ejected from the court. The document she had handed up was that letter Mitya had written at the “Metropolis” tavern, which Ivan had spoken of as a “mathematical proof.” Alas! its mathematical conclusiveness was recognized, and had it not been for that letter, Mitya might have escaped his doom or, at least, that doom would have been less terrible. It was, I repeat, difficult to notice every detail. What followed is still confused to my mind. The President must, I suppose, have at once passed on the document to the judges, the jury, and the lawyers on both sides. I only remember how they began examining the witness. On being gently asked by the President whether she had recovered sufficiently, Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed impetuously:\n\n“I am ready, I am ready! I am quite equal to answering you,” she added, evidently still afraid that she would somehow be prevented from giving evidence. She was asked to explain in detail what this letter was and under what circumstances she received it.\n\n“I received it the day before the crime was committed, but he wrote it the day before that, at the tavern—that is, two days before he committed the crime. Look, it is written on some sort of bill!” she cried breathlessly. “He hated me at that time, because he had behaved contemptibly and was running after that creature ... and because he owed me that three thousand.... Oh! he was humiliated by that three thousand on account of his own meanness! This is how it happened about that three thousand. I beg you, I beseech you, to hear me. Three weeks before he murdered his father, he came to me one morning. I knew he was in want of money, and what he wanted it for. Yes, yes—to win that creature and carry her off. I knew then that he had been false to me and meant to abandon me, and it was I, I, who gave him that money, who offered it to him on the pretext of his sending it to my sister in Moscow. And as I gave it him, I looked him in the face and said that he could send it when he liked, ‘in a month’s time would do.’ How, how could he have failed to understand that I was practically telling him to his face, ‘You want money to be false to me with your creature, so here’s the money for you. I give it to you myself. Take it, if you have so little honor as to take it!’ I wanted to prove what he was, and what happened? He took it, he took it, and squandered it with that creature in one night.... But he knew, he knew that I knew all about it. I assure you he understood, too, that I gave him that money to test him, to see whether he was so lost to all sense of honor as to take it from me. I looked into his eyes and he looked into mine, and he understood it all and he took it—he carried off my money!”\n\n“That’s true, Katya,” Mitya roared suddenly, “I looked into your eyes and I knew that you were dishonoring me, and yet I took your money. Despise me as a scoundrel, despise me, all of you! I’ve deserved it!”\n\n“Prisoner,” cried the President, “another word and I will order you to be removed.”\n\n“That money was a torment to him,” Katya went on with impulsive haste. “He wanted to repay it me. He wanted to, that’s true; but he needed money for that creature, too. So he murdered his father, but he didn’t repay me, and went off with her to that village where he was arrested. There, again, he squandered the money he had stolen after the murder of his father. And a day before the murder he wrote me this letter. He was drunk when he wrote it. I saw it at once, at the time. He wrote it from spite, and feeling certain, positively certain, that I should never show it to any one, even if he did kill him, or else he wouldn’t have written it. For he knew I shouldn’t want to revenge myself and ruin him! But read it, read it attentively—more attentively, please—and you will see that he had described it all in his letter, all beforehand, how he would kill his father and where his money was kept. Look, please, don’t overlook that, there’s one phrase there, ‘I shall kill him as soon as Ivan has gone away.’ So he thought it all out beforehand how he would kill him,” Katerina Ivanovna pointed out to the court with venomous and malignant triumph. Oh! it was clear she had studied every line of that letter and detected every meaning underlining it. “If he hadn’t been drunk, he wouldn’t have written to me; but, look, everything is written there beforehand, just as he committed the murder after. A complete program of it!” she exclaimed frantically.\n\nShe was reckless now of all consequences to herself, though, no doubt, she had foreseen them even a month ago, for even then, perhaps, shaking with anger, she had pondered whether to show it at the trial or not. Now she had taken the fatal plunge. I remember that the letter was read aloud by the clerk, directly afterwards, I believe. It made an overwhelming impression. They asked Mitya whether he admitted having written the letter.\n\n“It’s mine, mine!” cried Mitya. “I shouldn’t have written it, if I hadn’t been drunk!... We’ve hated each other for many things, Katya, but I swear, I swear I loved you even while I hated you, and you didn’t love me!”\n\nHe sank back on his seat, wringing his hands in despair. The prosecutor and counsel for the defense began cross-examining her, chiefly to ascertain what had induced her to conceal such a document and to give her evidence in quite a different tone and spirit just before.\n\n“Yes, yes. I was telling lies just now. I was lying against my honor and my conscience, but I wanted to save him, for he has hated and despised me so!” Katya cried madly. “Oh, he has despised me horribly, he has always despised me, and do you know, he has despised me from the very moment that I bowed down to him for that money. I saw that.... I felt it at once at the time, but for a long time I wouldn’t believe it. How often I have read it in his eyes, ‘You came of yourself, though.’ Oh, he didn’t understand, he had no idea why I ran to him, he can suspect nothing but baseness, he judged me by himself, he thought every one was like himself!” Katya hissed furiously, in a perfect frenzy. “And he only wanted to marry me, because I’d inherited a fortune, because of that, because of that! I always suspected it was because of that! Oh, he is a brute! He was always convinced that I should be trembling with shame all my life before him, because I went to him then, and that he had a right to despise me for ever for it, and so to be superior to me—that’s why he wanted to marry me! That’s so, that’s all so! I tried to conquer him by my love—a love that knew no bounds. I even tried to forgive his faithlessness; but he understood nothing, nothing! How could he understand indeed? He is a monster! I only received that letter the next evening: it was brought me from the tavern—and only that morning, only that morning I wanted to forgive him everything, everything—even his treachery!”\n\nThe President and the prosecutor, of course, tried to calm her. I can’t help thinking that they felt ashamed of taking advantage of her hysteria and of listening to such avowals. I remember hearing them say to her, “We understand how hard it is for you; be sure we are able to feel for you,” and so on, and so on. And yet they dragged the evidence out of the raving, hysterical woman. She described at last with extraordinary clearness, which is so often seen, though only for a moment, in such over-wrought states, how Ivan had been nearly driven out of his mind during the last two months trying to save “the monster and murderer,” his brother.\n\n“He tortured himself,” she exclaimed, “he was always trying to minimize his brother’s guilt and confessing to me that he, too, had never loved his father, and perhaps desired his death himself. Oh, he has a tender, over-tender conscience! He tormented himself with his conscience! He told me everything, everything! He came every day and talked to me as his only friend. I have the honor to be his only friend!” she cried suddenly with a sort of defiance, and her eyes flashed. “He had been twice to see Smerdyakov. One day he came to me and said, ‘If it was not my brother, but Smerdyakov committed the murder’ (for the legend was circulating everywhere that Smerdyakov had done it), ‘perhaps I too am guilty, for Smerdyakov knew I didn’t like my father and perhaps believed that I desired my father’s death.’ Then I brought out that letter and showed it him. He was entirely convinced that his brother had done it, and he was overwhelmed by it. He couldn’t endure the thought that his own brother was a parricide! Only a week ago I saw that it was making him ill. During the last few days he has talked incoherently in my presence. I saw his mind was giving way. He walked about, raving; he was seen muttering in the streets. The doctor from Moscow, at my request, examined him the day before yesterday and told me that he was on the eve of brain fever—and all on his account, on account of this monster! And last night he learnt that Smerdyakov was dead! It was such a shock that it drove him out of his mind ... and all through this monster, all for the sake of saving the monster!”\n\nOh, of course, such an outpouring, such an avowal is only possible once in a lifetime—at the hour of death, for instance, on the way to the scaffold! But it was in Katya’s character, and it was such a moment in her life. It was the same impetuous Katya who had thrown herself on the mercy of a young profligate to save her father; the same Katya who had just before, in her pride and chastity, sacrificed herself and her maidenly modesty before all these people, telling of Mitya’s generous conduct, in the hope of softening his fate a little. And now, again, she sacrificed herself; but this time it was for another, and perhaps only now—perhaps only at this moment—she felt and knew how dear that other was to her! She had sacrificed herself in terror for him, conceiving all of a sudden that he had ruined himself by his confession that it was he who had committed the murder, not his brother, she had sacrificed herself to save him, to save his good name, his reputation!\n\nAnd yet one terrible doubt occurred to one—was she lying in her description of her former relations with Mitya?—that was the question. No, she had not intentionally slandered him when she cried that Mitya despised her for her bowing down to him! She believed it herself. She had been firmly convinced, perhaps ever since that bow, that the simple-hearted Mitya, who even then adored her, was laughing at her and despising her. She had loved him with an hysterical, “lacerated” love only from pride, from wounded pride, and that love was not like love, but more like revenge. Oh! perhaps that lacerated love would have grown into real love, perhaps Katya longed for nothing more than that, but Mitya’s faithlessness had wounded her to the bottom of her heart, and her heart could not forgive him. The moment of revenge had come upon her suddenly, and all that had been accumulating so long and so painfully in the offended woman’s breast burst out all at once and unexpectedly. She betrayed Mitya, but she betrayed herself, too. And no sooner had she given full expression to her feelings than the tension of course was over and she was overwhelmed with shame. Hysterics began again: she fell on the floor, sobbing and screaming. She was carried out. At that moment Grushenka, with a wail, rushed towards Mitya before they had time to prevent her.\n\n“Mitya,” she wailed, “your serpent has destroyed you! There, she has shown you what she is!” she shouted to the judges, shaking with anger. At a signal from the President they seized her and tried to remove her from the court. She wouldn’t allow it. She fought and struggled to get back to Mitya. Mitya uttered a cry and struggled to get to her. He was overpowered.\n\nYes, I think the ladies who came to see the spectacle must have been satisfied—the show had been a varied one. Then I remember the Moscow doctor appeared on the scene. I believe the President had previously sent the court usher to arrange for medical aid for Ivan. The doctor announced to the court that the sick man was suffering from a dangerous attack of brain fever, and that he must be at once removed. In answer to questions from the prosecutor and the counsel for the defense he said that the patient had come to him of his own accord the day before yesterday and that he had warned him that he had such an attack coming on, but he had not consented to be looked after. “He was certainly not in a normal state of mind: he told me himself that he saw visions when he was awake, that he met several persons in the street, who were dead, and that Satan visited him every evening,” said the doctor, in conclusion. Having given his evidence, the celebrated doctor withdrew. The letter produced by Katerina Ivanovna was added to the material proofs. After some deliberation, the judges decided to proceed with the trial and to enter both the unexpected pieces of evidence (given by Ivan and Katerina Ivanovna) on the protocol.\n\nBut I will not detail the evidence of the other witnesses, who only repeated and confirmed what had been said before, though all with their characteristic peculiarities. I repeat, all was brought together in the prosecutor’s speech, which I shall quote immediately. Every one was excited, every one was electrified by the late catastrophe, and all were awaiting the speeches for the prosecution and the defense with intense impatience. Fetyukovitch was obviously shaken by Katerina Ivanovna’s evidence. But the prosecutor was triumphant. When all the evidence had been taken, the court was adjourned for almost an hour. I believe it was just eight o’clock when the President returned to his seat and our prosecutor, Ippolit Kirillovitch, began his speech.\n\n# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor’s Speech. Sketches Of Character\n\nIppolit Kirillovitch began his speech, trembling with nervousness, with cold sweat on his forehead, feeling hot and cold all over by turns. He described this himself afterwards. He regarded this speech as his chef-d’œuvre, the chef-d’œuvre of his whole life, as his swan-song. He died, it is true, nine months later of rapid consumption, so that he had the right, as it turned out, to compare himself to a swan singing his last song. He had put his whole heart and all the brain he had into that speech. And poor Ippolit Kirillovitch unexpectedly revealed that at least some feeling for the public welfare and “the eternal question” lay concealed in him. Where his speech really excelled was in its sincerity. He genuinely believed in the prisoner’s guilt; he was accusing him not as an official duty only, and in calling for vengeance he quivered with a genuine passion “for the security of society.” Even the ladies in the audience, though they remained hostile to Ippolit Kirillovitch, admitted that he made an extraordinary impression on them. He began in a breaking voice, but it soon gained strength and filled the court to the end of his speech. But as soon as he had finished, he almost fainted.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury,” began the prosecutor, “this case has made a stir throughout Russia. But what is there to wonder at, what is there so peculiarly horrifying in it for us? We are so accustomed to such crimes! That’s what’s so horrible, that such dark deeds have ceased to horrify us. What ought to horrify us is that we are so accustomed to it, and not this or that isolated crime. What are the causes of our indifference, our lukewarm attitude to such deeds, to such signs of the times, ominous of an unenviable future? Is it our cynicism, is it the premature exhaustion of intellect and imagination in a society that is sinking into decay, in spite of its youth? Is it that our moral principles are shattered to their foundations, or is it, perhaps, a complete lack of such principles among us? I cannot answer such questions; nevertheless they are disturbing, and every citizen not only must, but ought to be harassed by them. Our newborn and still timid press has done good service to the public already, for without it we should never have heard of the horrors of unbridled violence and moral degradation which are continually made known by the press, not merely to those who attend the new jury courts established in the present reign, but to every one. And what do we read almost daily? Of things beside which the present case grows pale, and seems almost commonplace. But what is most important is that the majority of our national crimes of violence bear witness to a widespread evil, now so general among us that it is difficult to contend against it.\n\n“One day we see a brilliant young officer of high society, at the very outset of his career, in a cowardly underhand way, without a pang of conscience, murdering an official who had once been his benefactor, and the servant girl, to steal his own I.O.U. and what ready money he could find on him; ‘it will come in handy for my pleasures in the fashionable world and for my career in the future.’ After murdering them, he puts pillows under the head of each of his victims; he goes away. Next, a young hero ‘decorated for bravery’ kills the mother of his chief and benefactor, like a highwayman, and to urge his companions to join him he asserts that ‘she loves him like a son, and so will follow all his directions and take no precautions.’ Granted that he is a monster, yet I dare not say in these days that he is unique. Another man will not commit the murder, but will feel and think like him, and is as dishonorable in soul. In silence, alone with his conscience, he asks himself perhaps, ‘What is honor, and isn’t the condemnation of bloodshed a prejudice?’\n\n“Perhaps people will cry out against me that I am morbid, hysterical, that it is a monstrous slander, that I am exaggerating. Let them say so—and heavens! I should be the first to rejoice if it were so! Oh, don’t believe me, think of me as morbid, but remember my words; if only a tenth, if only a twentieth part of what I say is true—even so it’s awful! Look how our young people commit suicide, without asking themselves Hamlet’s question what there is beyond, without a sign of such a question, as though all that relates to the soul and to what awaits us beyond the grave had long been erased in their minds and buried under the sands. Look at our vice, at our profligates. Fyodor Pavlovitch, the luckless victim in the present case, was almost an innocent babe compared with many of them. And yet we all knew him, ‘he lived among us!’...\n\n“Yes, one day perhaps the leading intellects of Russia and of Europe will study the psychology of Russian crime, for the subject is worth it. But this study will come later, at leisure, when all the tragic topsy-turvydom of to-day is farther behind us, so that it’s possible to examine it with more insight and more impartiality than I can do. Now we are either horrified or pretend to be horrified, though we really gloat over the spectacle, and love strong and eccentric sensations which tickle our cynical, pampered idleness. Or, like little children, we brush the dreadful ghosts away and hide our heads in the pillow so as to return to our sports and merriment as soon as they have vanished. But we must one day begin life in sober earnest, we must look at ourselves as a society; it’s time we tried to grasp something of our social position, or at least to make a beginning in that direction.\n\n“A great writer[9] of the last epoch, comparing Russia to a swift troika galloping to an unknown goal, exclaims, ‘Oh, troika, birdlike troika, who invented thee!’ and adds, in proud ecstasy, that all the peoples of the world stand aside respectfully to make way for the recklessly galloping troika to pass. That may be, they may stand aside, respectfully or no, but in my poor opinion the great writer ended his book in this way either in an access of childish and naïve optimism, or simply in fear of the censorship of the day. For if the troika were drawn by his heroes, Sobakevitch, Nozdryov, Tchitchikov, it could reach no rational goal, whoever might be driving it. And those were the heroes of an older generation, ours are worse specimens still....”\n\nAt this point Ippolit Kirillovitch’s speech was interrupted by applause. The liberal significance of this simile was appreciated. The applause was, it’s true, of brief duration, so that the President did not think it necessary to caution the public, and only looked severely in the direction of the offenders. But Ippolit Kirillovitch was encouraged; he had never been applauded before! He had been all his life unable to get a hearing, and now he suddenly had an opportunity of securing the ear of all Russia.\n\n“What, after all, is this Karamazov family, which has gained such an unenviable notoriety throughout Russia?” he continued. “Perhaps I am exaggerating, but it seems to me that certain fundamental features of the educated class of to-day are reflected in this family picture—only, of course, in miniature, ‘like the sun in a drop of water.’ Think of that unhappy, vicious, unbridled old man, who has met with such a melancholy end, the head of a family! Beginning life of noble birth, but in a poor dependent position, through an unexpected marriage he came into a small fortune. A petty knave, a toady and buffoon, of fairly good, though undeveloped, intelligence, he was, above all, a moneylender, who grew bolder with growing prosperity. His abject and servile characteristics disappeared, his malicious and sarcastic cynicism was all that remained. On the spiritual side he was undeveloped, while his vitality was excessive. He saw nothing in life but sensual pleasure, and he brought his children up to be the same. He had no feelings for his duties as a father. He ridiculed those duties. He left his little children to the servants, and was glad to be rid of them, forgot about them completely. The old man’s maxim was Après moi le déluge. He was an example of everything that is opposed to civic duty, of the most complete and malignant individualism. ‘The world may burn for aught I care, so long as I am all right,’ and he was all right; he was content, he was eager to go on living in the same way for another twenty or thirty years. He swindled his own son and spent his money, his maternal inheritance, on trying to get his mistress from him. No, I don’t intend to leave the prisoner’s defense altogether to my talented colleague from Petersburg. I will speak the truth myself, I can well understand what resentment he had heaped up in his son’s heart against him.\n\n“But enough, enough of that unhappy old man; he has paid the penalty. Let us remember, however, that he was a father, and one of the typical fathers of to-day. Am I unjust, indeed, in saying that he is typical of many modern fathers? Alas! many of them only differ in not openly professing such cynicism, for they are better educated, more cultured, but their philosophy is essentially the same as his. Perhaps I am a pessimist, but you have agreed to forgive me. Let us agree beforehand, you need not believe me, but let me speak. Let me say what I have to say, and remember something of my words.\n\n“Now for the children of this father, this head of a family. One of them is the prisoner before us, all the rest of my speech will deal with him. Of the other two I will speak only cursorily.\n\n“The elder is one of those modern young men of brilliant education and vigorous intellect, who has lost all faith in everything. He has denied and rejected much already, like his father. We have all heard him, he was a welcome guest in local society. He never concealed his opinions, quite the contrary in fact, which justifies me in speaking rather openly of him now, of course, not as an individual, but as a member of the Karamazov family. Another personage closely connected with the case died here by his own hand last night. I mean an afflicted idiot, formerly the servant, and possibly the illegitimate son, of Fyodor Pavlovitch, Smerdyakov. At the preliminary inquiry, he told me with hysterical tears how the young Ivan Karamazov had horrified him by his spiritual audacity. ‘Everything in the world is lawful according to him, and nothing must be forbidden in the future—that is what he always taught me.’ I believe that idiot was driven out of his mind by this theory, though, of course, the epileptic attacks from which he suffered, and this terrible catastrophe, have helped to unhinge his faculties. But he dropped one very interesting observation, which would have done credit to a more intelligent observer, and that is, indeed, why I’ve mentioned it: ‘If there is one of the sons that is like Fyodor Pavlovitch in character, it is Ivan Fyodorovitch.’\n\n“With that remark I conclude my sketch of his character, feeling it indelicate to continue further. Oh, I don’t want to draw any further conclusions and croak like a raven over the young man’s future. We’ve seen to-day in this court that there are still good impulses in his young heart, that family feeling has not been destroyed in him by lack of faith and cynicism, which have come to him rather by inheritance than by the exercise of independent thought.\n\n“Then the third son. Oh, he is a devout and modest youth, who does not share his elder brother’s gloomy and destructive theory of life. He has sought to cling to the ‘ideas of the people,’ or to what goes by that name in some circles of our intellectual classes. He clung to the monastery, and was within an ace of becoming a monk. He seems to me to have betrayed unconsciously, and so early, that timid despair which leads so many in our unhappy society, who dread cynicism and its corrupting influences, and mistakenly attribute all the mischief to European enlightenment, to return to their ‘native soil,’ as they say, to the bosom, so to speak, of their mother earth, like frightened children, yearning to fall asleep on the withered bosom of their decrepit mother, and to sleep there for ever, only to escape the horrors that terrify them.\n\n“For my part I wish the excellent and gifted young man every success; I trust that his youthful idealism and impulse towards the ideas of the people may never degenerate, as often happens, on the moral side into gloomy mysticism, and on the political into blind chauvinism—two elements which are even a greater menace to Russia than the premature decay, due to misunderstanding and gratuitous adoption of European ideas, from which his elder brother is suffering.”\n\nTwo or three people clapped their hands at the mention of chauvinism and mysticism. Ippolit Kirillovitch had been, indeed, carried away by his own eloquence. All this had little to do with the case in hand, to say nothing of the fact of its being somewhat vague, but the sickly and consumptive man was overcome by the desire to express himself once in his life. People said afterwards that he was actuated by unworthy motives in his criticism of Ivan, because the latter had on one or two occasions got the better of him in argument, and Ippolit Kirillovitch, remembering it, tried now to take his revenge. But I don’t know whether it was true. All this was only introductory, however, and the speech passed to more direct consideration of the case.\n\n“But to return to the eldest son,” Ippolit Kirillovitch went on. “He is the prisoner before us. We have his life and his actions, too, before us; the fatal day has come and all has been brought to the surface. While his brothers seem to stand for ‘Europeanism’ and ‘the principles of the people,’ he seems to represent Russia as she is. Oh, not all Russia, not all! God preserve us, if it were! Yet, here we have her, our mother Russia, the very scent and sound of her. Oh, he is spontaneous, he is a marvelous mingling of good and evil, he is a lover of culture and Schiller, yet he brawls in taverns and plucks out the beards of his boon companions. Oh, he, too, can be good and noble, but only when all goes well with him. What is more, he can be carried off his feet, positively carried off his feet by noble ideals, but only if they come of themselves, if they fall from heaven for him, if they need not be paid for. He dislikes paying for anything, but is very fond of receiving, and that’s so with him in everything. Oh, give him every possible good in life (he couldn’t be content with less), and put no obstacle in his way, and he will show that he, too, can be noble. He is not greedy, no, but he must have money, a great deal of money, and you will see how generously, with what scorn of filthy lucre, he will fling it all away in the reckless dissipation of one night. But if he has not money, he will show what he is ready to do to get it when he is in great need of it. But all this later, let us take events in their chronological order.\n\n“First, we have before us a poor abandoned child, running about the back-yard ‘without boots on his feet,’ as our worthy and esteemed fellow citizen, of foreign origin, alas! expressed it just now. I repeat it again, I yield to no one the defense of the criminal. I am here to accuse him, but to defend him also. Yes, I, too, am human; I, too, can weigh the influence of home and childhood on the character. But the boy grows up and becomes an officer; for a duel and other reckless conduct he is exiled to one of the remote frontier towns of Russia. There he led a wild life as an officer. And, of course, he needed money, money before all things, and so after prolonged disputes he came to a settlement with his father, and the last six thousand was sent him. A letter is in existence in which he practically gives up his claim to the rest and settles his conflict with his father over the inheritance on the payment of this six thousand.\n\n“Then came his meeting with a young girl of lofty character and brilliant education. Oh, I do not venture to repeat the details; you have only just heard them. Honor, self-sacrifice were shown there, and I will be silent. The figure of the young officer, frivolous and profligate, doing homage to true nobility and a lofty ideal, was shown in a very sympathetic light before us. But the other side of the medal was unexpectedly turned to us immediately after in this very court. Again I will not venture to conjecture why it happened so, but there were causes. The same lady, bathed in tears of long-concealed indignation, alleged that he, he of all men, had despised her for her action, which, though incautious, reckless perhaps, was still dictated by lofty and generous motives. He, he, the girl’s betrothed, looked at her with that smile of mockery, which was more insufferable from him than from any one. And knowing that he had already deceived her (he had deceived her, believing that she was bound to endure everything from him, even treachery), she intentionally offered him three thousand roubles, and clearly, too clearly, let him understand that she was offering him money to deceive her. ‘Well, will you take it or not, are you so lost to shame?’ was the dumb question in her scrutinizing eyes. He looked at her, saw clearly what was in her mind (he’s admitted here before you that he understood it all), appropriated that three thousand unconditionally, and squandered it in two days with the new object of his affections.\n\n“What are we to believe then? The first legend of the young officer sacrificing his last farthing in a noble impulse of generosity and doing reverence to virtue, or this other revolting picture? As a rule, between two extremes one has to find the mean, but in the present case this is not true. The probability is that in the first case he was genuinely noble, and in the second as genuinely base. And why? Because he was of the broad Karamazov character—that’s just what I am leading up to—capable of combining the most incongruous contradictions, and capable of the greatest heights and of the greatest depths. Remember the brilliant remark made by a young observer who has seen the Karamazov family at close quarters—Mr. Rakitin: ‘The sense of their own degradation is as essential to those reckless, unbridled natures as the sense of their lofty generosity.’ And that’s true, they need continually this unnatural mixture. Two extremes at the same moment, or they are miserable and dissatisfied and their existence is incomplete. They are wide, wide as mother Russia; they include everything and put up with everything.\n\n“By the way, gentlemen of the jury, we’ve just touched upon that three thousand roubles, and I will venture to anticipate things a little. Can you conceive that a man like that, on receiving that sum and in such a way, at the price of such shame, such disgrace, such utter degradation, could have been capable that very day of setting apart half that sum, that very day, and sewing it up in a little bag, and would have had the firmness of character to carry it about with him for a whole month afterwards, in spite of every temptation and his extreme need of it! Neither in drunken debauchery in taverns, nor when he was flying into the country, trying to get from God knows whom, the money so essential to him to remove the object of his affections from being tempted by his father, did he bring himself to touch that little bag! Why, if only to avoid abandoning his mistress to the rival of whom he was so jealous, he would have been certain to have opened that bag and to have stayed at home to keep watch over her, and to await the moment when she would say to him at last ‘I am yours,’ and to fly with her far from their fatal surroundings.\n\n“But no, he did not touch his talisman, and what is the reason he gives for it? The chief reason, as I have just said, was that when she would say, ‘I am yours, take me where you will,’ he might have the wherewithal to take her. But that first reason, in the prisoner’s own words, was of little weight beside the second. While I have that money on me, he said, I am a scoundrel, not a thief, for I can always go to my insulted betrothed, and, laying down half the sum I have fraudulently appropriated, I can always say to her, ‘You see, I’ve squandered half your money, and shown I am a weak and immoral man, and, if you like, a scoundrel’ (I use the prisoner’s own expressions), ‘but though I am a scoundrel, I am not a thief, for if I had been a thief, I shouldn’t have brought you back this half of the money, but should have taken it as I did the other half!’ A marvelous explanation! This frantic, but weak man, who could not resist the temptation of accepting the three thousand roubles at the price of such disgrace, this very man suddenly develops the most stoical firmness, and carries about a thousand roubles without daring to touch it. Does that fit in at all with the character we have analyzed? No, and I venture to tell you how the real Dmitri Karamazov would have behaved in such circumstances, if he really had brought himself to put away the money.\n\n“At the first temptation—for instance, to entertain the woman with whom he had already squandered half the money—he would have unpicked his little bag and have taken out some hundred roubles, for why should he have taken back precisely half the money, that is, fifteen hundred roubles? why not fourteen hundred? He could just as well have said then that he was not a thief, because he brought back fourteen hundred roubles. Then another time he would have unpicked it again and taken out another hundred, and then a third, and then a fourth, and before the end of the month he would have taken the last note but one, feeling that if he took back only a hundred it would answer the purpose, for a thief would have stolen it all. And then he would have looked at this last note, and have said to himself, ‘It’s really not worth while to give back one hundred; let’s spend that, too!’ That’s how the real Dmitri Karamazov, as we know him, would have behaved. One cannot imagine anything more incongruous with the actual fact than this legend of the little bag. Nothing could be more inconceivable. But we shall return to that later.”\n\nAfter touching upon what had come out in the proceedings concerning the financial relations of father and son, and arguing again and again that it was utterly impossible, from the facts known, to determine which was in the wrong, Ippolit Kirillovitch passed to the evidence of the medical experts in reference to Mitya’s fixed idea about the three thousand owing him.\n\n# Chapter VII. An Historical Survey\n\n“The medical experts have striven to convince us that the prisoner is out of his mind and, in fact, a maniac. I maintain that he is in his right mind, and that if he had not been, he would have behaved more cleverly. As for his being a maniac, that I would agree with, but only in one point, that is, his fixed idea about the three thousand. Yet I think one might find a much simpler cause than his tendency to insanity. For my part I agree thoroughly with the young doctor who maintained that the prisoner’s mental faculties have always been normal, and that he has only been irritable and exasperated. The object of the prisoner’s continual and violent anger was not the sum itself; there was a special motive at the bottom of it. That motive is jealousy!”\n\nHere Ippolit Kirillovitch described at length the prisoner’s fatal passion for Grushenka. He began from the moment when the prisoner went to the “young person’s” lodgings “to beat her”—“I use his own expression,” the prosecutor explained—“but instead of beating her, he remained there, at her feet. That was the beginning of the passion. At the same time the prisoner’s father was captivated by the same young person—a strange and fatal coincidence, for they both lost their hearts to her simultaneously, though both had known her before. And she inspired in both of them the most violent, characteristically Karamazov passion. We have her own confession: ‘I was laughing at both of them.’ Yes, the sudden desire to make a jest of them came over her, and she conquered both of them at once. The old man, who worshiped money, at once set aside three thousand roubles as a reward for one visit from her, but soon after that, he would have been happy to lay his property and his name at her feet, if only she would become his lawful wife. We have good evidence of this. As for the prisoner, the tragedy of his fate is evident; it is before us. But such was the young person’s ‘game.’ The enchantress gave the unhappy young man no hope until the last moment, when he knelt before her, stretching out hands that were already stained with the blood of his father and rival. It was in that position that he was arrested. ‘Send me to Siberia with him, I have brought him to this, I am most to blame,’ the woman herself cried, in genuine remorse at the moment of his arrest.\n\n“The talented young man, to whom I have referred already, Mr. Rakitin, characterized this heroine in brief and impressive terms: ‘She was disillusioned early in life, deceived and ruined by a betrothed, who seduced and abandoned her. She was left in poverty, cursed by her respectable family, and taken under the protection of a wealthy old man, whom she still, however, considers as her benefactor. There was perhaps much that was good in her young heart, but it was embittered too early. She became prudent and saved money. She grew sarcastic and resentful against society.’ After this sketch of her character it may well be understood that she might laugh at both of them simply from mischief, from malice.\n\n“After a month of hopeless love and moral degradation, during which he betrayed his betrothed and appropriated money entrusted to his honor, the prisoner was driven almost to frenzy, almost to madness by continual jealousy—and of whom? His father! And the worst of it was that the crazy old man was alluring and enticing the object of his affection by means of that very three thousand roubles, which the son looked upon as his own property, part of his inheritance from his mother, of which his father was cheating him. Yes, I admit it was hard to bear! It might well drive a man to madness. It was not the money, but the fact that this money was used with such revolting cynicism to ruin his happiness!”\n\nThen the prosecutor went on to describe how the idea of murdering his father had entered the prisoner’s head, and illustrated his theory with facts.\n\n“At first he only talked about it in taverns—he was talking about it all that month. Ah, he likes being always surrounded with company, and he likes to tell his companions everything, even his most diabolical and dangerous ideas; he likes to share every thought with others, and expects, for some reason, that those he confides in will meet him with perfect sympathy, enter into all his troubles and anxieties, take his part and not oppose him in anything. If not, he flies into a rage and smashes up everything in the tavern. [Then followed the anecdote about Captain Snegiryov.] Those who heard the prisoner began to think at last that he might mean more than threats, and that such a frenzy might turn threats into actions.”\n\nHere the prosecutor described the meeting of the family at the monastery, the conversations with Alyosha, and the horrible scene of violence when the prisoner had rushed into his father’s house just after dinner.\n\n“I cannot positively assert,” the prosecutor continued, “that the prisoner fully intended to murder his father before that incident. Yet the idea had several times presented itself to him, and he had deliberated on it—for that we have facts, witnesses, and his own words. I confess, gentlemen of the jury,” he added, “that till to-day I have been uncertain whether to attribute to the prisoner conscious premeditation. I was firmly convinced that he had pictured the fatal moment beforehand, but had only pictured it, contemplating it as a possibility. He had not definitely considered when and how he might commit the crime.\n\n“But I was only uncertain till to-day, till that fatal document was presented to the court just now. You yourselves heard that young lady’s exclamation, ‘It is the plan, the program of the murder!’ That is how she defined that miserable, drunken letter of the unhappy prisoner. And, in fact, from that letter we see that the whole fact of the murder was premeditated. It was written two days before, and so we know now for a fact that, forty-eight hours before the perpetration of his terrible design, the prisoner swore that, if he could not get money next day, he would murder his father in order to take the envelope with the notes from under his pillow, as soon as Ivan had left. ‘As soon as Ivan had gone away’—you hear that; so he had thought everything out, weighing every circumstance, and he carried it all out just as he had written it. The proof of premeditation is conclusive; the crime must have been committed for the sake of the money, that is stated clearly, that is written and signed. The prisoner does not deny his signature.\n\n“I shall be told he was drunk when he wrote it. But that does not diminish the value of the letter, quite the contrary; he wrote when drunk what he had planned when sober. Had he not planned it when sober, he would not have written it when drunk. I shall be asked: Then why did he talk about it in taverns? A man who premeditates such a crime is silent and keeps it to himself. Yes, but he talked about it before he had formed a plan, when he had only the desire, only the impulse to it. Afterwards he talked less about it. On the evening he wrote that letter at the ‘Metropolis’ tavern, contrary to his custom he was silent, though he had been drinking. He did not play billiards, he sat in a corner, talked to no one. He did indeed turn a shopman out of his seat, but that was done almost unconsciously, because he could never enter a tavern without making a disturbance. It is true that after he had taken the final decision, he must have felt apprehensive that he had talked too much about his design beforehand, and that this might lead to his arrest and prosecution afterwards. But there was nothing for it; he could not take his words back, but his luck had served him before, it would serve him again. He believed in his star, you know! I must confess, too, that he did a great deal to avoid the fatal catastrophe. ‘To-morrow I shall try and borrow the money from every one,’ as he writes in his peculiar language, ‘and if they won’t give it to me, there will be bloodshed.’”\n\nHere Ippolit Kirillovitch passed to a detailed description of all Mitya’s efforts to borrow the money. He described his visit to Samsonov, his journey to Lyagavy. “Harassed, jeered at, hungry, after selling his watch to pay for the journey (though he tells us he had fifteen hundred roubles on him—a likely story), tortured by jealousy at having left the object of his affections in the town, suspecting that she would go to Fyodor Pavlovitch in his absence, he returned at last to the town, to find, to his joy, that she had not been near his father. He accompanied her himself to her protector. (Strange to say, he doesn’t seem to have been jealous of Samsonov, which is psychologically interesting.) Then he hastens back to his ambush in the back gardens, and there learns that Smerdyakov is in a fit, that the other servant is ill—the coast is clear and he knows the ‘signals’—what a temptation! Still he resists it; he goes off to a lady who has for some time been residing in the town, and who is highly esteemed among us, Madame Hohlakov. That lady, who had long watched his career with compassion, gave him the most judicious advice, to give up his dissipated life, his unseemly love-affair, the waste of his youth and vigor in pot-house debauchery, and to set off to Siberia to the gold-mines: ‘that would be an outlet for your turbulent energies, your romantic character, your thirst for adventure.’”\n\nAfter describing the result of this conversation and the moment when the prisoner learnt that Grushenka had not remained at Samsonov’s, the sudden frenzy of the luckless man worn out with jealousy and nervous exhaustion, at the thought that she had deceived him and was now with his father, Ippolit Kirillovitch concluded by dwelling upon the fatal influence of chance. “Had the maid told him that her mistress was at Mokroe with her former lover, nothing would have happened. But she lost her head, she could only swear and protest her ignorance, and if the prisoner did not kill her on the spot, it was only because he flew in pursuit of his false mistress.\n\n“But note, frantic as he was, he took with him a brass pestle. Why that? Why not some other weapon? But since he had been contemplating his plan and preparing himself for it for a whole month, he would snatch up anything like a weapon that caught his eye. He had realized for a month past that any object of the kind would serve as a weapon, so he instantly, without hesitation, recognized that it would serve his purpose. So it was by no means unconsciously, by no means involuntarily, that he snatched up that fatal pestle. And then we find him in his father’s garden—the coast is clear, there are no witnesses, darkness and jealousy. The suspicion that she was there, with him, with his rival, in his arms, and perhaps laughing at him at that moment—took his breath away. And it was not mere suspicion, the deception was open, obvious. She must be there, in that lighted room, she must be behind the screen; and the unhappy man would have us believe that he stole up to the window, peeped respectfully in, and discreetly withdrew, for fear something terrible and immoral should happen. And he tries to persuade us of that, us, who understand his character, who know his state of mind at the moment, and that he knew the signals by which he could at once enter the house.” At this point Ippolit Kirillovitch broke off to discuss exhaustively the suspected connection of Smerdyakov with the murder. He did this very circumstantially, and every one realized that, although he professed to despise that suspicion, he thought the subject of great importance.\n\n# Chapter VIII. A Treatise On Smerdyakov\n\n“To begin with, what was the source of this suspicion?” (Ippolit Kirillovitch began.) “The first person who cried out that Smerdyakov had committed the murder was the prisoner himself at the moment of his arrest, yet from that time to this he had not brought forward a single fact to confirm the charge, nor the faintest suggestion of a fact. The charge is confirmed by three persons only—the two brothers of the prisoner and Madame Svyetlov. The elder of these brothers expressed his suspicions only to-day, when he was undoubtedly suffering from brain fever. But we know that for the last two months he has completely shared our conviction of his brother’s guilt and did not attempt to combat that idea. But of that later. The younger brother has admitted that he has not the slightest fact to support his notion of Smerdyakov’s guilt, and has only been led to that conclusion from the prisoner’s own words and the expression of his face. Yes, that astounding piece of evidence has been brought forward twice to-day by him. Madame Svyetlov was even more astounding. ‘What the prisoner tells you, you must believe; he is not a man to tell a lie.’ That is all the evidence against Smerdyakov produced by these three persons, who are all deeply concerned in the prisoner’s fate. And yet the theory of Smerdyakov’s guilt has been noised about, has been and is still maintained. Is it credible? Is it conceivable?”\n\nHere Ippolit Kirillovitch thought it necessary to describe the personality of Smerdyakov, “who had cut short his life in a fit of insanity.” He depicted him as a man of weak intellect, with a smattering of education, who had been thrown off his balance by philosophical ideas above his level and certain modern theories of duty, which he learnt in practice from the reckless life of his master, who was also perhaps his father—Fyodor Pavlovitch; and, theoretically, from various strange philosophical conversations with his master’s elder son, Ivan Fyodorovitch, who readily indulged in this diversion, probably feeling dull or wishing to amuse himself at the valet’s expense. “He spoke to me himself of his spiritual condition during the last few days at his father’s house,” Ippolit Kirillovitch explained; “but others too have borne witness to it—the prisoner himself, his brother, and the servant Grigory—that is, all who knew him well.\n\n“Moreover, Smerdyakov, whose health was shaken by his attacks of epilepsy, had not the courage of a chicken. ‘He fell at my feet and kissed them,’ the prisoner himself has told us, before he realized how damaging such a statement was to himself. ‘He is an epileptic chicken,’ he declared about him in his characteristic language. And the prisoner chose him for his confidant (we have his own word for it) and he frightened him into consenting at last to act as a spy for him. In that capacity he deceived his master, revealing to the prisoner the existence of the envelope with the notes in it and the signals by means of which he could get into the house. How could he help telling him, indeed? ‘He would have killed me, I could see that he would have killed me,’ he said at the inquiry, trembling and shaking even before us, though his tormentor was by that time arrested and could do him no harm. ‘He suspected me at every instant. In fear and trembling I hastened to tell him every secret to pacify him, that he might see that I had not deceived him and let me off alive.’ Those are his own words. I wrote them down and I remember them. ‘When he began shouting at me, I would fall on my knees.’\n\n“He was naturally very honest and enjoyed the complete confidence of his master, ever since he had restored him some money he had lost. So it may be supposed that the poor fellow suffered pangs of remorse at having deceived his master, whom he loved as his benefactor. Persons severely afflicted with epilepsy are, so the most skillful doctors tell us, always prone to continual and morbid self-reproach. They worry over their ‘wickedness,’ they are tormented by pangs of conscience, often entirely without cause; they exaggerate and often invent all sorts of faults and crimes. And here we have a man of that type who had really been driven to wrong-doing by terror and intimidation.\n\n“He had, besides, a strong presentiment that something terrible would be the outcome of the situation that was developing before his eyes. When Ivan Fyodorovitch was leaving for Moscow, just before the catastrophe, Smerdyakov besought him to remain, though he was too timid to tell him plainly what he feared. He confined himself to hints, but his hints were not understood.\n\n“It must be observed that he looked on Ivan Fyodorovitch as a protector, whose presence in the house was a guarantee that no harm would come to pass. Remember the phrase in Dmitri Karamazov’s drunken letter, ‘I shall kill the old man, if only Ivan goes away.’ So Ivan Fyodorovitch’s presence seemed to every one a guarantee of peace and order in the house.\n\n“But he went away, and within an hour of his young master’s departure Smerdyakov was taken with an epileptic fit. But that’s perfectly intelligible. Here I must mention that Smerdyakov, oppressed by terror and despair of a sort, had felt during those last few days that one of the fits from which he had suffered before at moments of strain, might be coming upon him again. The day and hour of such an attack cannot, of course, be foreseen, but every epileptic can feel beforehand that he is likely to have one. So the doctors tell us. And so, as soon as Ivan Fyodorovitch had driven out of the yard, Smerdyakov, depressed by his lonely and unprotected position, went to the cellar. He went down the stairs wondering if he would have a fit or not, and what if it were to come upon him at once. And that very apprehension, that very wonder, brought on the spasm in his throat that always precedes such attacks, and he fell unconscious into the cellar. And in this perfectly natural occurrence people try to detect a suspicion, a hint that he was shamming an attack on purpose. But, if it were on purpose, the question arises at once, what was his motive? What was he reckoning on? What was he aiming at? I say nothing about medicine: science, I am told, may go astray: the doctors were not able to discriminate between the counterfeit and the real. That may be so, but answer me one question: what motive had he for such a counterfeit? Could he, had he been plotting the murder, have desired to attract the attention of the household by having a fit just before?\n\n“You see, gentlemen of the jury, on the night of the murder, there were five persons in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s—Fyodor Pavlovitch himself (but he did not kill himself, that’s evident); then his servant, Grigory, but he was almost killed himself; the third person was Grigory’s wife, Marfa Ignatyevna, but it would be simply shameful to imagine her murdering her master. Two persons are left—the prisoner and Smerdyakov. But, if we are to believe the prisoner’s statement that he is not the murderer, then Smerdyakov must have been, for there is no other alternative, no one else can be found. That is what accounts for the artful, astounding accusation against the unhappy idiot who committed suicide yesterday. Had a shadow of suspicion rested on any one else, had there been any sixth person, I am persuaded that even the prisoner would have been ashamed to accuse Smerdyakov, and would have accused that sixth person, for to charge Smerdyakov with that murder is perfectly absurd.\n\n“Gentlemen, let us lay aside psychology, let us lay aside medicine, let us even lay aside logic, let us turn only to the facts and see what the facts tell us. If Smerdyakov killed him, how did he do it? Alone or with the assistance of the prisoner? Let us consider the first alternative—that he did it alone. If he had killed him it must have been with some object, for some advantage to himself. But not having a shadow of the motive that the prisoner had for the murder—hatred, jealousy, and so on—Smerdyakov could only have murdered him for the sake of gain, in order to appropriate the three thousand roubles he had seen his master put in the envelope. And yet he tells another person—and a person most closely interested, that is, the prisoner—everything about the money and the signals, where the envelope lay, what was written on it, what it was tied up with, and, above all, told him of those signals by which he could enter the house. Did he do this simply to betray himself, or to invite to the same enterprise one who would be anxious to get that envelope for himself? ‘Yes,’ I shall be told, ‘but he betrayed it from fear.’ But how do you explain this? A man who could conceive such an audacious, savage act, and carry it out, tells facts which are known to no one else in the world, and which, if he held his tongue, no one would ever have guessed!\n\n“No, however cowardly he might be, if he had plotted such a crime, nothing would have induced him to tell any one about the envelope and the signals, for that was as good as betraying himself beforehand. He would have invented something, he would have told some lie if he had been forced to give information, but he would have been silent about that. For, on the other hand, if he had said nothing about the money, but had committed the murder and stolen the money, no one in the world could have charged him with murder for the sake of robbery, since no one but he had seen the money, no one but he knew of its existence in the house. Even if he had been accused of the murder, it could only have been thought that he had committed it from some other motive. But since no one had observed any such motive in him beforehand, and every one saw, on the contrary, that his master was fond of him and honored him with his confidence, he would, of course, have been the last to be suspected. People would have suspected first the man who had a motive, a man who had himself declared he had such motives, who had made no secret of it; they would, in fact, have suspected the son of the murdered man, Dmitri Fyodorovitch. Had Smerdyakov killed and robbed him, and the son been accused of it, that would, of course, have suited Smerdyakov. Yet are we to believe that, though plotting the murder, he told that son, Dmitri, about the money, the envelope, and the signals? Is that logical? Is that clear?\n\n“When the day of the murder planned by Smerdyakov came, we have him falling downstairs in a feigned fit—with what object? In the first place that Grigory, who had been intending to take his medicine, might put it off and remain on guard, seeing there was no one to look after the house, and, in the second place, I suppose, that his master seeing that there was no one to guard him, and in terror of a visit from his son, might redouble his vigilance and precaution. And, most of all, I suppose that he, Smerdyakov, disabled by the fit, might be carried from the kitchen, where he always slept, apart from all the rest, and where he could go in and out as he liked, to Grigory’s room at the other end of the lodge, where he was always put, shut off by a screen three paces from their own bed. This was the immemorial custom established by his master and the kind-hearted Marfa Ignatyevna, whenever he had a fit. There, lying behind the screen, he would most likely, to keep up the sham, have begun groaning, and so keeping them awake all night (as Grigory and his wife testified). And all this, we are to believe, that he might more conveniently get up and murder his master!\n\n“But I shall be told that he shammed illness on purpose that he might not be suspected and that he told the prisoner of the money and the signals to tempt him to commit the murder, and when he had murdered him and had gone away with the money, making a noise, most likely, and waking people, Smerdyakov got up, am I to believe, and went in—what for? To murder his master a second time and carry off the money that had already been stolen? Gentlemen, are you laughing? I am ashamed to put forward such suggestions, but, incredible as it seems, that’s just what the prisoner alleges. When he had left the house, had knocked Grigory down and raised an alarm, he tells us Smerdyakov got up, went in and murdered his master and stole the money! I won’t press the point that Smerdyakov could hardly have reckoned on this beforehand, and have foreseen that the furious and exasperated son would simply come to peep in respectfully, though he knew the signals, and beat a retreat, leaving Smerdyakov his booty. Gentlemen of the jury, I put this question to you in earnest; when was the moment when Smerdyakov could have committed his crime? Name that moment, or you can’t accuse him.\n\n“But, perhaps, the fit was a real one, the sick man suddenly recovered, heard a shout, and went out. Well—what then? He looked about him and said, ‘Why not go and kill the master?’ And how did he know what had happened, since he had been lying unconscious till that moment? But there’s a limit to these flights of fancy.\n\n“‘Quite so,’ some astute people will tell me, ‘but what if they were in agreement? What if they murdered him together and shared the money—what then?’ A weighty question, truly! And the facts to confirm it are astounding. One commits the murder and takes all the trouble while his accomplice lies on one side shamming a fit, apparently to arouse suspicion in every one, alarm in his master and alarm in Grigory. It would be interesting to know what motives could have induced the two accomplices to form such an insane plan.\n\n“But perhaps it was not a case of active complicity on Smerdyakov’s part, but only of passive acquiescence; perhaps Smerdyakov was intimidated and agreed not to prevent the murder, and foreseeing that he would be blamed for letting his master be murdered, without screaming for help or resisting, he may have obtained permission from Dmitri Karamazov to get out of the way by shamming a fit—‘you may murder him as you like; it’s nothing to me.’ But as this attack of Smerdyakov’s was bound to throw the household into confusion, Dmitri Karamazov could never have agreed to such a plan. I will waive that point however. Supposing that he did agree, it would still follow that Dmitri Karamazov is the murderer and the instigator, and Smerdyakov is only a passive accomplice, and not even an accomplice, but merely acquiesced against his will through terror.\n\n“But what do we see? As soon as he is arrested the prisoner instantly throws all the blame on Smerdyakov, not accusing him of being his accomplice, but of being himself the murderer. ‘He did it alone,’ he says. ‘He murdered and robbed him. It was the work of his hands.’ Strange sort of accomplices who begin to accuse one another at once! And think of the risk for Karamazov. After committing the murder while his accomplice lay in bed, he throws the blame on the invalid, who might well have resented it and in self-preservation might well have confessed the truth. For he might well have seen that the court would at once judge how far he was responsible, and so he might well have reckoned that if he were punished, it would be far less severely than the real murderer. But in that case he would have been certain to make a confession, yet he has not done so. Smerdyakov never hinted at their complicity, though the actual murderer persisted in accusing him and declaring that he had committed the crime alone.\n\n“What’s more, Smerdyakov at the inquiry volunteered the statement that it was he who had told the prisoner of the envelope of notes and of the signals, and that, but for him, he would have known nothing about them. If he had really been a guilty accomplice, would he so readily have made this statement at the inquiry? On the contrary, he would have tried to conceal it, to distort the facts or minimize them. But he was far from distorting or minimizing them. No one but an innocent man, who had no fear of being charged with complicity, could have acted as he did. And in a fit of melancholy arising from his disease and this catastrophe he hanged himself yesterday. He left a note written in his peculiar language, ‘I destroy myself of my own will and inclination so as to throw no blame on any one.’ What would it have cost him to add: ‘I am the murderer, not Karamazov’? But that he did not add. Did his conscience lead him to suicide and not to avowing his guilt?\n\n“And what followed? Notes for three thousand roubles were brought into the court just now, and we were told that they were the same that lay in the envelope now on the table before us, and that the witness had received them from Smerdyakov the day before. But I need not recall the painful scene, though I will make one or two comments, selecting such trivial ones as might not be obvious at first sight to every one, and so may be overlooked. In the first place, Smerdyakov must have given back the money and hanged himself yesterday from remorse. And only yesterday he confessed his guilt to Ivan Karamazov, as the latter informs us. If it were not so, indeed, why should Ivan Fyodorovitch have kept silence till now? And so, if he has confessed, then why, I ask again, did he not avow the whole truth in the last letter he left behind, knowing that the innocent prisoner had to face this terrible ordeal the next day?\n\n“The money alone is no proof. A week ago, quite by chance, the fact came to the knowledge of myself and two other persons in this court that Ivan Fyodorovitch had sent two five per cent. coupons of five thousand each—that is, ten thousand in all—to the chief town of the province to be changed. I only mention this to point out that any one may have money, and that it can’t be proved that these notes are the same as were in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s envelope.\n\n“Ivan Karamazov, after receiving yesterday a communication of such importance from the real murderer, did not stir. Why didn’t he report it at once? Why did he put it all off till morning? I think I have a right to conjecture why. His health had been giving way for a week past: he had admitted to a doctor and to his most intimate friends that he was suffering from hallucinations and seeing phantoms of the dead: he was on the eve of the attack of brain fever by which he has been stricken down to-day. In this condition he suddenly heard of Smerdyakov’s death, and at once reflected, ‘The man is dead, I can throw the blame on him and save my brother. I have money. I will take a roll of notes and say that Smerdyakov gave them me before his death.’ You will say that was dishonorable: it’s dishonorable to slander even the dead, and even to save a brother. True, but what if he slandered him unconsciously? What if, finally unhinged by the sudden news of the valet’s death, he imagined it really was so? You saw the recent scene: you have seen the witness’s condition. He was standing up and was speaking, but where was his mind?\n\n“Then followed the document, the prisoner’s letter written two days before the crime, and containing a complete program of the murder. Why, then, are we looking for any other program? The crime was committed precisely according to this program, and by no other than the writer of it. Yes, gentlemen of the jury, it went off without a hitch! He did not run respectfully and timidly away from his father’s window, though he was firmly convinced that the object of his affections was with him. No, that is absurd and unlikely! He went in and murdered him. Most likely he killed him in anger, burning with resentment, as soon as he looked on his hated rival. But having killed him, probably with one blow of the brass pestle, and having convinced himself, after careful search, that she was not there, he did not, however, forget to put his hand under the pillow and take out the envelope, the torn cover of which lies now on the table before us.\n\n“I mention this fact that you may note one, to my thinking, very characteristic circumstance. Had he been an experienced murderer and had he committed the murder for the sake of gain only, would he have left the torn envelope on the floor as it was found, beside the corpse? Had it been Smerdyakov, for instance, murdering his master to rob him, he would have simply carried away the envelope with him, without troubling himself to open it over his victim’s corpse, for he would have known for certain that the notes were in the envelope—they had been put in and sealed up in his presence—and had he taken the envelope with him, no one would ever have known of the robbery. I ask you, gentlemen, would Smerdyakov have behaved in that way? Would he have left the envelope on the floor?\n\n“No, this was the action of a frantic murderer, a murderer who was not a thief and had never stolen before that day, who snatched the notes from under the pillow, not like a thief stealing them, but as though seizing his own property from the thief who had stolen it. For that was the idea which had become almost an insane obsession in Dmitri Karamazov in regard to that money. And pouncing upon the envelope, which he had never seen before, he tore it open to make sure whether the money was in it, and ran away with the money in his pocket, even forgetting to consider that he had left an astounding piece of evidence against himself in that torn envelope on the floor. All because it was Karamazov, not Smerdyakov, he didn’t think, he didn’t reflect, and how should he? He ran away; he heard behind him the servant cry out; the old man caught him, stopped him and was felled to the ground by the brass pestle.\n\n“The prisoner, moved by pity, leapt down to look at him. Would you believe it, he tells us that he leapt down out of pity, out of compassion, to see whether he could do anything for him. Was that a moment to show compassion? No; he jumped down simply to make certain whether the only witness of his crime were dead or alive. Any other feeling, any other motive would be unnatural. Note that he took trouble over Grigory, wiped his head with his handkerchief and, convincing himself he was dead, he ran to the house of his mistress, dazed and covered with blood. How was it he never thought that he was covered with blood and would be at once detected? But the prisoner himself assures us that he did not even notice that he was covered with blood. That may be believed, that is very possible, that always happens at such moments with criminals. On one point they will show diabolical cunning, while another will escape them altogether. But he was thinking at that moment of one thing only—where was she? He wanted to find out at once where she was, so he ran to her lodging and learnt an unexpected and astounding piece of news—she had gone off to Mokroe to meet her first lover.”\n\n# Chapter IX. The Galloping Troika. The End Of The Prosecutor’s Speech.\n\nIppolit Kirillovitch had chosen the historical method of exposition, beloved by all nervous orators, who find in its limitation a check on their own eager rhetoric. At this moment in his speech he went off into a dissertation on Grushenka’s “first lover,” and brought forward several interesting thoughts on this theme.\n\n“Karamazov, who had been frantically jealous of every one, collapsed, so to speak, and effaced himself at once before this first lover. What makes it all the more strange is that he seems to have hardly thought of this formidable rival. But he had looked upon him as a remote danger, and Karamazov always lives in the present. Possibly he regarded him as a fiction. But his wounded heart grasped instantly that the woman had been concealing this new rival and deceiving him, because he was anything but a fiction to her, because he was the one hope of her life. Grasping this instantly, he resigned himself.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, I cannot help dwelling on this unexpected trait in the prisoner’s character. He suddenly evinces an irresistible desire for justice, a respect for woman and a recognition of her right to love. And all this at the very moment when he had stained his hands with his father’s blood for her sake! It is true that the blood he had shed was already crying out for vengeance, for, after having ruined his soul and his life in this world, he was forced to ask himself at that same instant what he was and what he could be now to her, to that being, dearer to him than his own soul, in comparison with that former lover who had returned penitent, with new love, to the woman he had once betrayed, with honorable offers, with the promise of a reformed and happy life. And he, luckless man, what could he give her now, what could he offer her?\n\n“Karamazov felt all this, knew that all ways were barred to him by his crime and that he was a criminal under sentence, and not a man with life before him! This thought crushed him. And so he instantly flew to one frantic plan, which, to a man of Karamazov’s character, must have appeared the one inevitable way out of his terrible position. That way out was suicide. He ran for the pistols he had left in pledge with his friend Perhotin and on the way, as he ran, he pulled out of his pocket the money, for the sake of which he had stained his hands with his father’s gore. Oh, now he needed money more than ever. Karamazov would die, Karamazov would shoot himself and it should be remembered! To be sure, he was a poet and had burnt the candle at both ends all his life. ‘To her, to her! and there, oh, there I will give a feast to the whole world, such as never was before, that will be remembered and talked of long after! In the midst of shouts of wild merriment, reckless gypsy songs and dances I shall raise the glass and drink to the woman I adore and her new-found happiness! And then, on the spot, at her feet, I shall dash out my brains before her and punish myself! She will remember Mitya Karamazov sometimes, she will see how Mitya loved her, she will feel for Mitya!’\n\n“Here we see in excess a love of effect, a romantic despair and sentimentality, and the wild recklessness of the Karamazovs. Yes, but there is something else, gentlemen of the jury, something that cries out in the soul, throbs incessantly in the mind, and poisons the heart unto death—that something is conscience, gentlemen of the jury, its judgment, its terrible torments! The pistol will settle everything, the pistol is the only way out! But beyond—I don’t know whether Karamazov wondered at that moment ‘What lies beyond,’ and whether Karamazov could, like Hamlet, wonder ‘What lies beyond.’ No, gentlemen of the jury, they have their Hamlets, but we still have our Karamazovs!”\n\nHere Ippolit Kirillovitch drew a minute picture of Mitya’s preparations, the scene at Perhotin’s, at the shop, with the drivers. He quoted numerous words and actions, confirmed by witnesses, and the picture made a terrible impression on the audience. The guilt of this harassed and desperate man stood out clear and convincing, when the facts were brought together.\n\n“What need had he of precaution? Two or three times he almost confessed, hinted at it, all but spoke out.” (Then followed the evidence given by witnesses.) “He even cried out to the peasant who drove him, ‘Do you know, you are driving a murderer!’ But it was impossible for him to speak out, he had to get to Mokroe and there to finish his romance. But what was awaiting the luckless man? Almost from the first minute at Mokroe he saw that his invincible rival was perhaps by no means so invincible, that the toast to their new-found happiness was not desired and would not be acceptable. But you know the facts, gentlemen of the jury, from the preliminary inquiry. Karamazov’s triumph over his rival was complete and his soul passed into quite a new phase, perhaps the most terrible phase through which his soul has passed or will pass.\n\n“One may say with certainty, gentlemen of the jury,” the prosecutor continued, “that outraged nature and the criminal heart bring their own vengeance more completely than any earthly justice. What’s more, justice and punishment on earth positively alleviate the punishment of nature and are, indeed, essential to the soul of the criminal at such moments, as its salvation from despair. For I cannot imagine the horror and moral suffering of Karamazov when he learnt that she loved him, that for his sake she had rejected her first lover, that she was summoning him, Mitya, to a new life, that she was promising him happiness—and when? When everything was over for him and nothing was possible!\n\n“By the way, I will note in parenthesis a point of importance for the light it throws on the prisoner’s position at the moment. This woman, this love of his, had been till the last moment, till the very instant of his arrest, a being unattainable, passionately desired by him but unattainable. Yet why did he not shoot himself then, why did he relinquish his design and even forget where his pistol was? It was just that passionate desire for love and the hope of satisfying it that restrained him. Throughout their revels he kept close to his adored mistress, who was at the banquet with him and was more charming and fascinating to him than ever—he did not leave her side, abasing himself in his homage before her.\n\n“His passion might well, for a moment, stifle not only the fear of arrest, but even the torments of conscience. For a moment, oh, only for a moment! I can picture the state of mind of the criminal hopelessly enslaved by these influences—first, the influence of drink, of noise and excitement, of the thud of the dance and the scream of the song, and of her, flushed with wine, singing and dancing and laughing to him! Secondly, the hope in the background that the fatal end might still be far off, that not till next morning, at least, they would come and take him. So he had a few hours and that’s much, very much! In a few hours one can think of many things. I imagine that he felt something like what criminals feel when they are being taken to the scaffold. They have another long, long street to pass down and at walking pace, past thousands of people. Then there will be a turning into another street and only at the end of that street the dread place of execution! I fancy that at the beginning of the journey the condemned man, sitting on his shameful cart, must feel that he has infinite life still before him. The houses recede, the cart moves on—oh, that’s nothing, it’s still far to the turning into the second street and he still looks boldly to right and to left at those thousands of callously curious people with their eyes fixed on him, and he still fancies that he is just such a man as they. But now the turning comes to the next street. Oh, that’s nothing, nothing, there’s still a whole street before him, and however many houses have been passed, he will still think there are many left. And so to the very end, to the very scaffold.\n\n“This I imagine is how it was with Karamazov then. ‘They’ve not had time yet,’ he must have thought, ‘I may still find some way out, oh, there’s still time to make some plan of defense, and now, now—she is so fascinating!’\n\n“His soul was full of confusion and dread, but he managed, however, to put aside half his money and hide it somewhere—I cannot otherwise explain the disappearance of quite half of the three thousand he had just taken from his father’s pillow. He had been in Mokroe more than once before, he had caroused there for two days together already, he knew the old big house with all its passages and outbuildings. I imagine that part of the money was hidden in that house, not long before the arrest, in some crevice, under some floor, in some corner, under the roof. With what object? I shall be asked. Why, the catastrophe may take place at once, of course; he hadn’t yet considered how to meet it, he hadn’t the time, his head was throbbing and his heart was with her, but money—money was indispensable in any case! With money a man is always a man. Perhaps such foresight at such a moment may strike you as unnatural? But he assures us himself that a month before, at a critical and exciting moment, he had halved his money and sewn it up in a little bag. And though that was not true, as we shall prove directly, it shows the idea was a familiar one to Karamazov, he had contemplated it. What’s more, when he declared at the inquiry that he had put fifteen hundred roubles in a bag (which never existed) he may have invented that little bag on the inspiration of the moment, because he had two hours before divided his money and hidden half of it at Mokroe till morning, in case of emergency, simply not to have it on himself. Two extremes, gentlemen of the jury, remember that Karamazov can contemplate two extremes and both at once.\n\n“We have looked in the house, but we haven’t found the money. It may still be there or it may have disappeared next day and be in the prisoner’s hands now. In any case he was at her side, on his knees before her, she was lying on the bed, he had his hands stretched out to her and he had so entirely forgotten everything that he did not even hear the men coming to arrest him. He hadn’t time to prepare any line of defense in his mind. He was caught unawares and confronted with his judges, the arbiters of his destiny.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, there are moments in the execution of our duties when it is terrible for us to face a man, terrible on his account, too! The moments of contemplating that animal fear, when the criminal sees that all is lost, but still struggles, still means to struggle, the moments when every instinct of self-preservation rises up in him at once and he looks at you with questioning and suffering eyes, studies you, your face, your thoughts, uncertain on which side you will strike, and his distracted mind frames thousands of plans in an instant, but he is still afraid to speak, afraid of giving himself away! This purgatory of the spirit, this animal thirst for self-preservation, these humiliating moments of the human soul, are awful, and sometimes arouse horror and compassion for the criminal even in the lawyer. And this was what we all witnessed then.\n\n“At first he was thunderstruck and in his terror dropped some very compromising phrases. ‘Blood! I’ve deserved it!’ But he quickly restrained himself. He had not prepared what he was to say, what answer he was to make, he had nothing but a bare denial ready. ‘I am not guilty of my father’s death.’ That was his fence for the moment and behind it he hoped to throw up a barricade of some sort. His first compromising exclamations he hastened to explain by declaring that he was responsible for the death of the servant Grigory only. ‘Of that bloodshed I am guilty, but who has killed my father, gentlemen, who has killed him? Who can have killed him, if not I?’ Do you hear, he asked us that, us, who had come to ask him that question! Do you hear that phrase uttered with such premature haste—‘if not I’—the animal cunning, the naïveté, the Karamazov impatience of it? ‘I didn’t kill him and you mustn’t think I did! I wanted to kill him, gentlemen, I wanted to kill him,’ he hastens to admit (he was in a hurry, in a terrible hurry), ‘but still I am not guilty, it is not I murdered him.’ He concedes to us that he wanted to murder him, as though to say, you can see for yourselves how truthful I am, so you’ll believe all the sooner that I didn’t murder him. Oh, in such cases the criminal is often amazingly shallow and credulous.\n\n“At that point one of the lawyers asked him, as it were incidentally, the most simple question, ‘Wasn’t it Smerdyakov killed him?’ Then, as we expected, he was horribly angry at our having anticipated him and caught him unawares, before he had time to pave the way to choose and snatch the moment when it would be most natural to bring in Smerdyakov’s name. He rushed at once to the other extreme, as he always does, and began to assure us that Smerdyakov could not have killed him, was not capable of it. But don’t believe him, that was only his cunning; he didn’t really give up the idea of Smerdyakov; on the contrary, he meant to bring him forward again; for, indeed, he had no one else to bring forward, but he would do that later, because for the moment that line was spoiled for him. He would bring him forward perhaps next day, or even a few days later, choosing an opportunity to cry out to us, ‘You know I was more skeptical about Smerdyakov than you, you remember that yourselves, but now I am convinced. He killed him, he must have done!’ And for the present he falls back upon a gloomy and irritable denial. Impatience and anger prompted him, however, to the most inept and incredible explanation of how he looked into his father’s window and how he respectfully withdrew. The worst of it was that he was unaware of the position of affairs, of the evidence given by Grigory.\n\n“We proceeded to search him. The search angered, but encouraged him, the whole three thousand had not been found on him, only half of it. And no doubt only at that moment of angry silence, the fiction of the little bag first occurred to him. No doubt he was conscious himself of the improbability of the story and strove painfully to make it sound more likely, to weave it into a romance that would sound plausible. In such cases the first duty, the chief task of the investigating lawyers, is to prevent the criminal being prepared, to pounce upon him unexpectedly so that he may blurt out his cherished ideas in all their simplicity, improbability and inconsistency. The criminal can only be made to speak by the sudden and apparently incidental communication of some new fact, of some circumstance of great importance in the case, of which he had no previous idea and could not have foreseen. We had such a fact in readiness—that was Grigory’s evidence about the open door through which the prisoner had run out. He had completely forgotten about that door and had not even suspected that Grigory could have seen it.\n\n“The effect of it was amazing. He leapt up and shouted to us, ‘Then Smerdyakov murdered him, it was Smerdyakov!’ and so betrayed the basis of the defense he was keeping back, and betrayed it in its most improbable shape, for Smerdyakov could only have committed the murder after he had knocked Grigory down and run away. When we told him that Grigory saw the door was open before he fell down, and had heard Smerdyakov behind the screen as he came out of his bedroom—Karamazov was positively crushed. My esteemed and witty colleague, Nikolay Parfenovitch, told me afterwards that he was almost moved to tears at the sight of him. And to improve matters, the prisoner hastened to tell us about the much-talked-of little bag—so be it, you shall hear this romance!\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, I have told you already why I consider this romance not only an absurdity, but the most improbable invention that could have been brought forward in the circumstances. If one tried for a bet to invent the most unlikely story, one could hardly find anything more incredible. The worst of such stories is that the triumphant romancers can always be put to confusion and crushed by the very details in which real life is so rich and which these unhappy and involuntary story-tellers neglect as insignificant trifles. Oh, they have no thought to spare for such details, their minds are concentrated on their grand invention as a whole, and fancy any one daring to pull them up for a trifle! But that’s how they are caught. The prisoner was asked the question, ‘Where did you get the stuff for your little bag and who made it for you?’ ‘I made it myself.’ ‘And where did you get the linen?’ The prisoner was positively offended, he thought it almost insulting to ask him such a trivial question, and would you believe it, his resentment was genuine! But they are all like that. ‘I tore it off my shirt.’ ‘Then we shall find that shirt among your linen to-morrow, with a piece torn off.’ And only fancy, gentlemen of the jury, if we really had found that torn shirt (and how could we have failed to find it in his chest of drawers or trunk?) that would have been a fact, a material fact in support of his statement! But he was incapable of that reflection. ‘I don’t remember, it may not have been off my shirt, I sewed it up in one of my landlady’s caps.’ ‘What sort of a cap?’ ‘It was an old cotton rag of hers lying about.’ ‘And do you remember that clearly?’ ‘No, I don’t.’ And he was angry, very angry, and yet imagine not remembering it! At the most terrible moments of man’s life, for instance when he is being led to execution, he remembers just such trifles. He will forget anything but some green roof that has flashed past him on the road, or a jackdaw on a cross—that he will remember. He concealed the making of that little bag from his household, he must have remembered his humiliating fear that some one might come in and find him needle in hand, how at the slightest sound he slipped behind the screen (there is a screen in his lodgings).\n\n“But, gentlemen of the jury, why do I tell you all this, all these details, trifles?” cried Ippolit Kirillovitch suddenly. “Just because the prisoner still persists in these absurdities to this moment. He has not explained anything since that fatal night two months ago, he has not added one actual illuminating fact to his former fantastic statements; all those are trivialities. ‘You must believe it on my honor.’ Oh, we are glad to believe it, we are eager to believe it, even if only on his word of honor! Are we jackals thirsting for human blood? Show us a single fact in the prisoner’s favor and we shall rejoice; but let it be a substantial, real fact, and not a conclusion drawn from the prisoner’s expression by his own brother, or that when he beat himself on the breast he must have meant to point to the little bag, in the darkness, too. We shall rejoice at the new fact, we shall be the first to repudiate our charge, we shall hasten to repudiate it. But now justice cries out and we persist, we cannot repudiate anything.”\n\nIppolit Kirillovitch passed to his final peroration. He looked as though he was in a fever, he spoke of the blood that cried for vengeance, the blood of the father murdered by his son, with the base motive of robbery! He pointed to the tragic and glaring consistency of the facts.\n\n“And whatever you may hear from the talented and celebrated counsel for the defense,” Ippolit Kirillovitch could not resist adding, “whatever eloquent and touching appeals may be made to your sensibilities, remember that at this moment you are in a temple of justice. Remember that you are the champions of our justice, the champions of our holy Russia, of her principles, her family, everything that she holds sacred! Yes, you represent Russia here at this moment, and your verdict will be heard not in this hall only but will reëcho throughout the whole of Russia, and all Russia will hear you, as her champions and her judges, and she will be encouraged or disheartened by your verdict. Do not disappoint Russia and her expectations. Our fatal troika dashes on in her headlong flight perhaps to destruction and in all Russia for long past men have stretched out imploring hands and called a halt to its furious reckless course. And if other nations stand aside from that troika that may be, not from respect, as the poet would fain believe, but simply from horror. From horror, perhaps from disgust. And well it is that they stand aside, but maybe they will cease one day to do so and will form a firm wall confronting the hurrying apparition and will check the frenzied rush of our lawlessness, for the sake of their own safety, enlightenment and civilization. Already we have heard voices of alarm from Europe, they already begin to sound. Do not tempt them! Do not heap up their growing hatred by a sentence justifying the murder of a father by his son!”\n\nThough Ippolit Kirillovitch was genuinely moved, he wound up his speech with this rhetorical appeal—and the effect produced by him was extraordinary. When he had finished his speech, he went out hurriedly and, as I have mentioned before, almost fainted in the adjoining room. There was no applause in the court, but serious persons were pleased. The ladies were not so well satisfied, though even they were pleased with his eloquence, especially as they had no apprehensions as to the upshot of the trial and had full trust in Fetyukovitch. “He will speak at last and of course carry all before him.”\n\nEvery one looked at Mitya; he sat silent through the whole of the prosecutor’s speech, clenching his teeth, with his hands clasped, and his head bowed. Only from time to time he raised his head and listened, especially when Grushenka was spoken of. When the prosecutor mentioned Rakitin’s opinion of her, a smile of contempt and anger passed over his face and he murmured rather audibly, “The Bernards!” When Ippolit Kirillovitch described how he had questioned and tortured him at Mokroe, Mitya raised his head and listened with intense curiosity. At one point he seemed about to jump up and cry out, but controlled himself and only shrugged his shoulders disdainfully. People talked afterwards of the end of the speech, of the prosecutor’s feat in examining the prisoner at Mokroe, and jeered at Ippolit Kirillovitch. “The man could not resist boasting of his cleverness,” they said.\n\nThe court was adjourned, but only for a short interval, a quarter of an hour or twenty minutes at most. There was a hum of conversation and exclamations in the audience. I remember some of them.\n\n“A weighty speech,” a gentleman in one group observed gravely.\n\n“He brought in too much psychology,” said another voice.\n\n“But it was all true, the absolute truth!”\n\n“Yes, he is first rate at it.”\n\n“He summed it all up.”\n\n“Yes, he summed us up, too,” chimed in another voice. “Do you remember, at the beginning of his speech, making out we were all like Fyodor Pavlovitch?”\n\n“And at the end, too. But that was all rot.”\n\n“And obscure too.”\n\n“He was a little too much carried away.”\n\n“It’s unjust, it’s unjust.”\n\n“No, it was smartly done, anyway. He’s had long to wait, but he’s had his say, ha ha!”\n\n“What will the counsel for the defense say?”\n\nIn another group I heard:\n\n“He had no business to make a thrust at the Petersburg man like that; ‘appealing to your sensibilities’—do you remember?”\n\n“Yes, that was awkward of him.”\n\n“He was in too great a hurry.”\n\n“He is a nervous man.”\n\n“We laugh, but what must the prisoner be feeling?”\n\n“Yes, what must it be for Mitya?”\n\nIn a third group:\n\n“What lady is that, the fat one, with the lorgnette, sitting at the end?”\n\n“She is a general’s wife, divorced, I know her.”\n\n“That’s why she has the lorgnette.”\n\n“She is not good for much.”\n\n“Oh, no, she is a piquante little woman.”\n\n“Two places beyond her there is a little fair woman, she is prettier.”\n\n“They caught him smartly at Mokroe, didn’t they, eh?”\n\n“Oh, it was smart enough. We’ve heard it before, how often he has told the story at people’s houses!”\n\n“And he couldn’t resist doing it now. That’s vanity.”\n\n“He is a man with a grievance, he he!”\n\n“Yes, and quick to take offense. And there was too much rhetoric, such long sentences.”\n\n“Yes, he tries to alarm us, he kept trying to alarm us. Do you remember about the troika? Something about ‘They have Hamlets, but we have, so far, only Karamazovs!’ That was cleverly said!”\n\n“That was to propitiate the liberals. He is afraid of them.”\n\n“Yes, and he is afraid of the lawyer, too.”\n\n“Yes, what will Fetyukovitch say?”\n\n“Whatever he says, he won’t get round our peasants.”\n\n“Don’t you think so?”\n\nA fourth group:\n\n“What he said about the troika was good, that piece about the other nations.”\n\n“And that was true what he said about other nations not standing it.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Why, in the English Parliament a Member got up last week and speaking about the Nihilists asked the Ministry whether it was not high time to intervene, to educate this barbarous people. Ippolit was thinking of him, I know he was. He was talking about that last week.”\n\n“Not an easy job.”\n\n“Not an easy job? Why not?”\n\n“Why, we’d shut up Kronstadt and not let them have any corn. Where would they get it?”\n\n“In America. They get it from America now.”\n\n“Nonsense!”\n\nBut the bell rang, all rushed to their places. Fetyukovitch mounted the tribune.\n\n# Chapter X. The Speech For The Defense. An Argument That Cuts Both Ways\n\nAll was hushed as the first words of the famous orator rang out. The eyes of the audience were fastened upon him. He began very simply and directly, with an air of conviction, but not the slightest trace of conceit. He made no attempt at eloquence, at pathos, or emotional phrases. He was like a man speaking in a circle of intimate and sympathetic friends. His voice was a fine one, sonorous and sympathetic, and there was something genuine and simple in the very sound of it. But every one realized at once that the speaker might suddenly rise to genuine pathos and “pierce the heart with untold power.” His language was perhaps more irregular than Ippolit Kirillovitch’s, but he spoke without long phrases, and indeed, with more precision. One thing did not please the ladies: he kept bending forward, especially at the beginning of his speech, not exactly bowing, but as though he were about to dart at his listeners, bending his long spine in half, as though there were a spring in the middle that enabled him to bend almost at right angles.\n\nAt the beginning of his speech he spoke rather disconnectedly, without system, one may say, dealing with facts separately, though, at the end, these facts formed a whole. His speech might be divided into two parts, the first consisting of criticism in refutation of the charge, sometimes malicious and sarcastic. But in the second half he suddenly changed his tone, and even his manner, and at once rose to pathos. The audience seemed on the look-out for it, and quivered with enthusiasm.\n\nHe went straight to the point, and began by saying that although he practiced in Petersburg, he had more than once visited provincial towns to defend prisoners, of whose innocence he had a conviction or at least a preconceived idea. “That is what has happened to me in the present case,” he explained. “From the very first accounts in the newspapers I was struck by something which strongly prepossessed me in the prisoner’s favor. What interested me most was a fact which often occurs in legal practice, but rarely, I think, in such an extreme and peculiar form as in the present case. I ought to formulate that peculiarity only at the end of my speech, but I will do so at the very beginning, for it is my weakness to go to work directly, not keeping my effects in reserve and economizing my material. That may be imprudent on my part, but at least it’s sincere. What I have in my mind is this: there is an overwhelming chain of evidence against the prisoner, and at the same time not one fact that will stand criticism, if it is examined separately. As I followed the case more closely in the papers my idea was more and more confirmed, and I suddenly received from the prisoner’s relatives a request to undertake his defense. I at once hurried here, and here I became completely convinced. It was to break down this terrible chain of facts, and to show that each piece of evidence taken separately was unproved and fantastic, that I undertook the case.”\n\nSo Fetyukovitch began.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury,” he suddenly protested, “I am new to this district. I have no preconceived ideas. The prisoner, a man of turbulent and unbridled temper, has not insulted me. But he has insulted perhaps hundreds of persons in this town, and so prejudiced many people against him beforehand. Of course I recognize that the moral sentiment of local society is justly excited against him. The prisoner is of turbulent and violent temper. Yet he was received in society here; he was even welcome in the family of my talented friend, the prosecutor.”\n\n(N.B. At these words there were two or three laughs in the audience, quickly suppressed, but noticed by all. All of us knew that the prosecutor received Mitya against his will, solely because he had somehow interested his wife—a lady of the highest virtue and moral worth, but fanciful, capricious, and fond of opposing her husband, especially in trifles. Mitya’s visits, however, had not been frequent.)\n\n“Nevertheless I venture to suggest,” Fetyukovitch continued, “that in spite of his independent mind and just character, my opponent may have formed a mistaken prejudice against my unfortunate client. Oh, that is so natural; the unfortunate man has only too well deserved such prejudice. Outraged morality, and still more outraged taste, is often relentless. We have, in the talented prosecutor’s speech, heard a stern analysis of the prisoner’s character and conduct, and his severe critical attitude to the case was evident. And, what’s more, he went into psychological subtleties into which he could not have entered, if he had the least conscious and malicious prejudice against the prisoner. But there are things which are even worse, even more fatal in such cases, than the most malicious and consciously unfair attitude. It is worse if we are carried away by the artistic instinct, by the desire to create, so to speak, a romance, especially if God has endowed us with psychological insight. Before I started on my way here, I was warned in Petersburg, and was myself aware, that I should find here a talented opponent whose psychological insight and subtlety had gained him peculiar renown in legal circles of recent years. But profound as psychology is, it’s a knife that cuts both ways.” (Laughter among the public.) “You will, of course, forgive me my comparison; I can’t boast of eloquence. But I will take as an example any point in the prosecutor’s speech.\n\n“The prisoner, running away in the garden in the dark, climbed over the fence, was seized by the servant, and knocked him down with a brass pestle. Then he jumped back into the garden and spent five minutes over the man, trying to discover whether he had killed him or not. And the prosecutor refuses to believe the prisoner’s statement that he ran to old Grigory out of pity. ‘No,’ he says, ‘such sensibility is impossible at such a moment, that’s unnatural; he ran to find out whether the only witness of his crime was dead or alive, and so showed that he had committed the murder, since he would not have run back for any other reason.’\n\n“Here you have psychology; but let us take the same method and apply it to the case the other way round, and our result will be no less probable. The murderer, we are told, leapt down to find out, as a precaution, whether the witness was alive or not, yet he had left in his murdered father’s study, as the prosecutor himself argues, an amazing piece of evidence in the shape of a torn envelope, with an inscription that there had been three thousand roubles in it. ‘If he had carried that envelope away with him, no one in the world would have known of that envelope and of the notes in it, and that the money had been stolen by the prisoner.’ Those are the prosecutor’s own words. So on one side you see a complete absence of precaution, a man who has lost his head and run away in a fright, leaving that clew on the floor, and two minutes later, when he has killed another man, we are entitled to assume the most heartless and calculating foresight in him. But even admitting this was so, it is psychological subtlety, I suppose, that discerns that under certain circumstances I become as bloodthirsty and keen-sighted as a Caucasian eagle, while at the next I am as timid and blind as a mole. But if I am so bloodthirsty and cruelly calculating that when I kill a man I only run back to find out whether he is alive to witness against me, why should I spend five minutes looking after my victim at the risk of encountering other witnesses? Why soak my handkerchief, wiping the blood off his head so that it may be evidence against me later? If he were so cold-hearted and calculating, why not hit the servant on the head again and again with the same pestle so as to kill him outright and relieve himself of all anxiety about the witness?\n\n“Again, though he ran to see whether the witness was alive, he left another witness on the path, that brass pestle which he had taken from the two women, and which they could always recognize afterwards as theirs, and prove that he had taken it from them. And it is not as though he had forgotten it on the path, dropped it through carelessness or haste, no, he had flung away his weapon, for it was found fifteen paces from where Grigory lay. Why did he do so? Just because he was grieved at having killed a man, an old servant; and he flung away the pestle with a curse, as a murderous weapon. That’s how it must have been, what other reason could he have had for throwing it so far? And if he was capable of feeling grief and pity at having killed a man, it shows that he was innocent of his father’s murder. Had he murdered him, he would never have run to another victim out of pity; then he would have felt differently; his thoughts would have been centered on self-preservation. He would have had none to spare for pity, that is beyond doubt. On the contrary, he would have broken his skull instead of spending five minutes looking after him. There was room for pity and good-feeling just because his conscience had been clear till then. Here we have a different psychology. I have purposely resorted to this method, gentlemen of the jury, to show that you can prove anything by it. It all depends on who makes use of it. Psychology lures even most serious people into romancing, and quite unconsciously. I am speaking of the abuse of psychology, gentlemen.”\n\nSounds of approval and laughter, at the expense of the prosecutor, were again audible in the court. I will not repeat the speech in detail; I will only quote some passages from it, some leading points.\n\n# Chapter XI. There Was No Money. There Was No Robbery\n\nThere was one point that struck every one in Fetyukovitch’s speech. He flatly denied the existence of the fatal three thousand roubles, and consequently, the possibility of their having been stolen.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury,” he began. “Every new and unprejudiced observer must be struck by a characteristic peculiarity in the present case, namely, the charge of robbery, and the complete impossibility of proving that there was anything to be stolen. We are told that money was stolen—three thousand roubles—but whether those roubles ever existed, nobody knows. Consider, how have we heard of that sum, and who has seen the notes? The only person who saw them, and stated that they had been put in the envelope, was the servant, Smerdyakov. He had spoken of it to the prisoner and his brother, Ivan Fyodorovitch, before the catastrophe. Madame Svyetlov, too, had been told of it. But not one of these three persons had actually seen the notes, no one but Smerdyakov had seen them.\n\n“Here the question arises, if it’s true that they did exist, and that Smerdyakov had seen them, when did he see them for the last time? What if his master had taken the notes from under his bed and put them back in his cash-box without telling him? Note, that according to Smerdyakov’s story the notes were kept under the mattress; the prisoner must have pulled them out, and yet the bed was absolutely unrumpled; that is carefully recorded in the protocol. How could the prisoner have found the notes without disturbing the bed? How could he have helped soiling with his blood-stained hands the fine and spotless linen with which the bed had been purposely made?\n\n“But I shall be asked: What about the envelope on the floor? Yes, it’s worth saying a word or two about that envelope. I was somewhat surprised just now to hear the highly talented prosecutor declare of himself—of himself, observe—that but for that envelope, but for its being left on the floor, no one in the world would have known of the existence of that envelope and the notes in it, and therefore of the prisoner’s having stolen it. And so that torn scrap of paper is, by the prosecutor’s own admission, the sole proof on which the charge of robbery rests, ‘otherwise no one would have known of the robbery, nor perhaps even of the money.’ But is the mere fact that that scrap of paper was lying on the floor a proof that there was money in it, and that that money had been stolen? Yet, it will be objected, Smerdyakov had seen the money in the envelope. But when, when had he seen it for the last time, I ask you that? I talked to Smerdyakov, and he told me that he had seen the notes two days before the catastrophe. Then why not imagine that old Fyodor Pavlovitch, locked up alone in impatient and hysterical expectation of the object of his adoration, may have whiled away the time by breaking open the envelope and taking out the notes. ‘What’s the use of the envelope?’ he may have asked himself. ‘She won’t believe the notes are there, but when I show her the thirty rainbow-colored notes in one roll, it will make more impression, you may be sure, it will make her mouth water.’ And so he tears open the envelope, takes out the money, and flings the envelope on the floor, conscious of being the owner and untroubled by any fears of leaving evidence.\n\n“Listen, gentlemen, could anything be more likely than this theory and such an action? Why is it out of the question? But if anything of the sort could have taken place, the charge of robbery falls to the ground; if there was no money, there was no theft of it. If the envelope on the floor may be taken as evidence that there had been money in it, why may I not maintain the opposite, that the envelope was on the floor because the money had been taken from it by its owner?\n\n“But I shall be asked what became of the money if Fyodor Pavlovitch took it out of the envelope since it was not found when the police searched the house? In the first place, part of the money was found in the cash-box, and secondly, he might have taken it out that morning or the evening before to make some other use of it, to give or send it away; he may have changed his idea, his plan of action completely, without thinking it necessary to announce the fact to Smerdyakov beforehand. And if there is the barest possibility of such an explanation, how can the prisoner be so positively accused of having committed murder for the sake of robbery, and of having actually carried out that robbery? This is encroaching on the domain of romance. If it is maintained that something has been stolen, the thing must be produced, or at least its existence must be proved beyond doubt. Yet no one had ever seen these notes.\n\n“Not long ago in Petersburg a young man of eighteen, hardly more than a boy, who carried on a small business as a costermonger, went in broad daylight into a moneychanger’s shop with an ax, and with extraordinary, typical audacity killed the master of the shop and carried off fifteen hundred roubles. Five hours later he was arrested, and, except fifteen roubles he had already managed to spend, the whole sum was found on him. Moreover, the shopman, on his return to the shop after the murder, informed the police not only of the exact sum stolen, but even of the notes and gold coins of which that sum was made up, and those very notes and coins were found on the criminal. This was followed by a full and genuine confession on the part of the murderer. That’s what I call evidence, gentlemen of the jury! In that case I know, I see, I touch the money, and cannot deny its existence. Is it the same in the present case? And yet it is a question of life and death.\n\n“Yes, I shall be told, but he was carousing that night, squandering money; he was shown to have had fifteen hundred roubles—where did he get the money? But the very fact that only fifteen hundred could be found, and the other half of the sum could nowhere be discovered, shows that that money was not the same, and had never been in any envelope. By strict calculation of time it was proved at the preliminary inquiry that the prisoner ran straight from those women servants to Perhotin’s without going home, and that he had been nowhere. So he had been all the time in company and therefore could not have divided the three thousand in half and hidden half in the town. It’s just this consideration that has led the prosecutor to assume that the money is hidden in some crevice at Mokroe. Why not in the dungeons of the castle of Udolpho, gentlemen? Isn’t this supposition really too fantastic and too romantic? And observe, if that supposition breaks down, the whole charge of robbery is scattered to the winds, for in that case what could have become of the other fifteen hundred roubles? By what miracle could they have disappeared, since it’s proved the prisoner went nowhere else? And we are ready to ruin a man’s life with such tales!\n\n“I shall be told that he could not explain where he got the fifteen hundred that he had, and every one knew that he was without money before that night. Who knew it, pray? The prisoner has made a clear and unflinching statement of the source of that money, and if you will have it so, gentlemen of the jury, nothing can be more probable than that statement, and more consistent with the temper and spirit of the prisoner. The prosecutor is charmed with his own romance. A man of weak will, who had brought himself to take the three thousand so insultingly offered by his betrothed, could not, we are told, have set aside half and sewn it up, but would, even if he had done so, have unpicked it every two days and taken out a hundred, and so would have spent it all in a month. All this, you will remember, was put forward in a tone that brooked no contradiction. But what if the thing happened quite differently? What if you’ve been weaving a romance, and about quite a different kind of man? That’s just it, you have invented quite a different man!\n\n“I shall be told, perhaps, there are witnesses that he spent on one day all that three thousand given him by his betrothed a month before the catastrophe, so he could not have divided the sum in half. But who are these witnesses? The value of their evidence has been shown in court already. Besides, in another man’s hand a crust always seems larger, and no one of these witnesses counted that money; they all judged simply at sight. And the witness Maximov has testified that the prisoner had twenty thousand in his hand. You see, gentlemen of the jury, psychology is a two-edged weapon. Let me turn the other edge now and see what comes of it.\n\n“A month before the catastrophe the prisoner was entrusted by Katerina Ivanovna with three thousand roubles to send off by post. But the question is: is it true that they were entrusted to him in such an insulting and degrading way as was proclaimed just now? The first statement made by the young lady on the subject was different, perfectly different. In the second statement we heard only cries of resentment and revenge, cries of long-concealed hatred. And the very fact that the witness gave her first evidence incorrectly, gives us a right to conclude that her second piece of evidence may have been incorrect also. The prosecutor will not, dare not (his own words) touch on that story. So be it. I will not touch on it either, but will only venture to observe that if a lofty and high-principled person, such as that highly respected young lady unquestionably is, if such a person, I say, allows herself suddenly in court to contradict her first statement, with the obvious motive of ruining the prisoner, it is clear that this evidence has been given not impartially, not coolly. Have not we the right to assume that a revengeful woman might have exaggerated much? Yes, she may well have exaggerated, in particular, the insult and humiliation of her offering him the money. No, it was offered in such a way that it was possible to take it, especially for a man so easy-going as the prisoner, above all, as he expected to receive shortly from his father the three thousand roubles that he reckoned was owing to him. It was unreflecting of him, but it was just his irresponsible want of reflection that made him so confident that his father would give him the money, that he would get it, and so could always dispatch the money entrusted to him and repay the debt.\n\n“But the prosecutor refuses to allow that he could the same day have set aside half the money and sewn it up in a little bag. That’s not his character, he tells us, he couldn’t have had such feelings. But yet he talked himself of the broad Karamazov nature; he cried out about the two extremes which a Karamazov can contemplate at once. Karamazov is just such a two-sided nature, fluctuating between two extremes, that even when moved by the most violent craving for riotous gayety, he can pull himself up, if something strikes him on the other side. And on the other side is love—that new love which had flamed up in his heart, and for that love he needed money; oh, far more than for carousing with his mistress. If she were to say to him, ‘I am yours, I won’t have Fyodor Pavlovitch,’ then he must have money to take her away. That was more important than carousing. Could a Karamazov fail to understand it? That anxiety was just what he was suffering from—what is there improbable in his laying aside that money and concealing it in case of emergency?\n\n“But time passed, and Fyodor Pavlovitch did not give the prisoner the expected three thousand; on the contrary, the latter heard that he meant to use this sum to seduce the woman he, the prisoner, loved. ‘If Fyodor Pavlovitch doesn’t give the money,’ he thought, ‘I shall be put in the position of a thief before Katerina Ivanovna.’ And then the idea presented itself to him that he would go to Katerina Ivanovna, lay before her the fifteen hundred roubles he still carried round his neck, and say, ‘I am a scoundrel, but not a thief.’ So here we have already a twofold reason why he should guard that sum of money as the apple of his eye, why he shouldn’t unpick the little bag, and spend it a hundred at a time. Why should you deny the prisoner a sense of honor? Yes, he has a sense of honor, granted that it’s misplaced, granted it’s often mistaken, yet it exists and amounts to a passion, and he has proved that.\n\n“But now the affair becomes even more complex; his jealous torments reach a climax, and those same two questions torture his fevered brain more and more: ‘If I repay Katerina Ivanovna, where can I find the means to go off with Grushenka?’ If he behaved wildly, drank, and made disturbances in the taverns in the course of that month, it was perhaps because he was wretched and strained beyond his powers of endurance. These two questions became so acute that they drove him at last to despair. He sent his younger brother to beg for the last time for the three thousand roubles, but without waiting for a reply, burst in himself and ended by beating the old man in the presence of witnesses. After that he had no prospect of getting it from any one; his father would not give it him after that beating.\n\n“The same evening he struck himself on the breast, just on the upper part of the breast where the little bag was, and swore to his brother that he had the means of not being a scoundrel, but that still he would remain a scoundrel, for he foresaw that he would not use that means, that he wouldn’t have the character, that he wouldn’t have the will-power to do it. Why, why does the prosecutor refuse to believe the evidence of Alexey Karamazov, given so genuinely and sincerely, so spontaneously and convincingly? And why, on the contrary, does he force me to believe in money hidden in a crevice, in the dungeons of the castle of Udolpho?\n\n“The same evening, after his talk with his brother, the prisoner wrote that fatal letter, and that letter is the chief, the most stupendous proof of the prisoner having committed robbery! ‘I shall beg from every one, and if I don’t get it I shall murder my father and shall take the envelope with the pink ribbon on it from under his mattress as soon as Ivan has gone.’ A full program of the murder, we are told, so it must have been he. ‘It has all been done as he wrote,’ cries the prosecutor.\n\n“But in the first place, it’s the letter of a drunken man and written in great irritation; secondly, he writes of the envelope from what he has heard from Smerdyakov again, for he has not seen the envelope himself; and thirdly, he wrote it indeed, but how can you prove that he did it? Did the prisoner take the envelope from under the pillow, did he find the money, did that money exist indeed? And was it to get money that the prisoner ran off, if you remember? He ran off post-haste not to steal, but to find out where she was, the woman who had crushed him. He was not running to carry out a program, to carry out what he had written, that is, not for an act of premeditated robbery, but he ran suddenly, spontaneously, in a jealous fury. Yes! I shall be told, but when he got there and murdered him he seized the money, too. But did he murder him after all? The charge of robbery I repudiate with indignation. A man cannot be accused of robbery, if it’s impossible to state accurately what he has stolen; that’s an axiom. But did he murder him without robbery, did he murder him at all? Is that proved? Isn’t that, too, a romance?”\n\n# Chapter XII. And There Was No Murder Either\n\n“Allow me, gentlemen of the jury, to remind you that a man’s life is at stake and that you must be careful. We have heard the prosecutor himself admit that until to-day he hesitated to accuse the prisoner of a full and conscious premeditation of the crime; he hesitated till he saw that fatal drunken letter which was produced in court to-day. ‘All was done as written.’ But, I repeat again, he was running to her, to seek her, solely to find out where she was. That’s a fact that can’t be disputed. Had she been at home, he would not have run away, but would have remained at her side, and so would not have done what he promised in the letter. He ran unexpectedly and accidentally, and by that time very likely he did not even remember his drunken letter. ‘He snatched up the pestle,’ they say, and you will remember how a whole edifice of psychology was built on that pestle—why he was bound to look at that pestle as a weapon, to snatch it up, and so on, and so on. A very commonplace idea occurs to me at this point: What if that pestle had not been in sight, had not been lying on the shelf from which it was snatched by the prisoner, but had been put away in a cupboard? It would not have caught the prisoner’s eye, and he would have run away without a weapon, with empty hands, and then he would certainly not have killed any one. How then can I look upon the pestle as a proof of premeditation?\n\n“Yes, but he talked in the taverns of murdering his father, and two days before, on the evening when he wrote his drunken letter, he was quiet and only quarreled with a shopman in the tavern, because a Karamazov could not help quarreling, forsooth! But my answer to that is, that, if he was planning such a murder in accordance with his letter, he certainly would not have quarreled even with a shopman, and probably would not have gone into the tavern at all, because a person plotting such a crime seeks quiet and retirement, seeks to efface himself, to avoid being seen and heard, and that not from calculation, but from instinct. Gentlemen of the jury, the psychological method is a two-edged weapon, and we, too, can use it. As for all this shouting in taverns throughout the month, don’t we often hear children, or drunkards coming out of taverns shout, ‘I’ll kill you’? but they don’t murder any one. And that fatal letter—isn’t that simply drunken irritability, too? Isn’t that simply the shout of the brawler outside the tavern, ‘I’ll kill you! I’ll kill the lot of you!’ Why not, why could it not be that? What reason have we to call that letter ‘fatal’ rather than absurd? Because his father has been found murdered, because a witness saw the prisoner running out of the garden with a weapon in his hand, and was knocked down by him: therefore, we are told, everything was done as he had planned in writing, and the letter was not ‘absurd,’ but ‘fatal.’\n\n“Now, thank God! we’ve come to the real point: ‘since he was in the garden, he must have murdered him.’ In those few words: ‘since he was, then he must’ lies the whole case for the prosecution. He was there, so he must have. And what if there is no must about it, even if he was there? Oh, I admit that the chain of evidence—the coincidences—are really suggestive. But examine all these facts separately, regardless of their connection. Why, for instance, does the prosecution refuse to admit the truth of the prisoner’s statement that he ran away from his father’s window? Remember the sarcasms in which the prosecutor indulged at the expense of the respectful and ‘pious’ sentiments which suddenly came over the murderer. But what if there were something of the sort, a feeling of religious awe, if not of filial respect? ‘My mother must have been praying for me at that moment,’ were the prisoner’s words at the preliminary inquiry, and so he ran away as soon as he convinced himself that Madame Svyetlov was not in his father’s house. ‘But he could not convince himself by looking through the window,’ the prosecutor objects. But why couldn’t he? Why? The window opened at the signals given by the prisoner. Some word might have been uttered by Fyodor Pavlovitch, some exclamation which showed the prisoner that she was not there. Why should we assume everything as we imagine it, as we make up our minds to imagine it? A thousand things may happen in reality which elude the subtlest imagination.\n\n“‘Yes, but Grigory saw the door open and so the prisoner certainly was in the house, therefore he killed him.’ Now about that door, gentlemen of the jury.... Observe that we have only the statement of one witness as to that door, and he was at the time in such a condition, that— But supposing the door was open; supposing the prisoner has lied in denying it, from an instinct of self-defense, natural in his position; supposing he did go into the house—well, what then? How does it follow that because he was there he committed the murder? He might have dashed in, run through the rooms; might have pushed his father away; might have struck him; but as soon as he had made sure Madame Svyetlov was not there, he may have run away rejoicing that she was not there and that he had not killed his father. And it was perhaps just because he had escaped from the temptation to kill his father, because he had a clear conscience and was rejoicing at not having killed him, that he was capable of a pure feeling, the feeling of pity and compassion, and leapt off the fence a minute later to the assistance of Grigory after he had, in his excitement, knocked him down.\n\n“With terrible eloquence the prosecutor has described to us the dreadful state of the prisoner’s mind at Mokroe when love again lay before him calling him to new life, while love was impossible for him because he had his father’s bloodstained corpse behind him and beyond that corpse—retribution. And yet the prosecutor allowed him love, which he explained, according to his method, talking about his drunken condition, about a criminal being taken to execution, about it being still far off, and so on and so on. But again I ask, Mr. Prosecutor, have you not invented a new personality? Is the prisoner so coarse and heartless as to be able to think at that moment of love and of dodges to escape punishment, if his hands were really stained with his father’s blood? No, no, no! As soon as it was made plain to him that she loved him and called him to her side, promising him new happiness, oh! then, I protest he must have felt the impulse to suicide doubled, trebled, and must have killed himself, if he had his father’s murder on his conscience. Oh, no! he would not have forgotten where his pistols lay! I know the prisoner: the savage, stony heartlessness ascribed to him by the prosecutor is inconsistent with his character. He would have killed himself, that’s certain. He did not kill himself just because ‘his mother’s prayers had saved him,’ and he was innocent of his father’s blood. He was troubled, he was grieving that night at Mokroe only about old Grigory and praying to God that the old man would recover, that his blow had not been fatal, and that he would not have to suffer for it. Why not accept such an interpretation of the facts? What trustworthy proof have we that the prisoner is lying?\n\n“But we shall be told at once again, ‘There is his father’s corpse! If he ran away without murdering him, who did murder him?’ Here, I repeat, you have the whole logic of the prosecution. Who murdered him, if not he? There’s no one to put in his place.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, is that really so? Is it positively, actually true that there is no one else at all? We’ve heard the prosecutor count on his fingers all the persons who were in that house that night. They were five in number; three of them, I agree, could not have been responsible—the murdered man himself, old Grigory, and his wife. There are left then the prisoner and Smerdyakov, and the prosecutor dramatically exclaims that the prisoner pointed to Smerdyakov because he had no one else to fix on, that had there been a sixth person, even a phantom of a sixth person, he would have abandoned the charge against Smerdyakov at once in shame and have accused that other. But, gentlemen of the jury, why may I not draw the very opposite conclusion? There are two persons—the prisoner and Smerdyakov. Why can I not say that you accuse my client, simply because you have no one else to accuse? And you have no one else only because you have determined to exclude Smerdyakov from all suspicion.\n\n“It’s true, indeed, Smerdyakov is accused only by the prisoner, his two brothers, and Madame Svyetlov. But there are others who accuse him: there are vague rumors of a question, of a suspicion, an obscure report, a feeling of expectation. Finally, we have the evidence of a combination of facts very suggestive, though, I admit, inconclusive. In the first place we have precisely on the day of the catastrophe that fit, for the genuineness of which the prosecutor, for some reason, has felt obliged to make a careful defense. Then Smerdyakov’s sudden suicide on the eve of the trial. Then the equally startling evidence given in court to-day by the elder of the prisoner’s brothers, who had believed in his guilt, but has to-day produced a bundle of notes and proclaimed Smerdyakov as the murderer. Oh, I fully share the court’s and the prosecutor’s conviction that Ivan Karamazov is suffering from brain fever, that his statement may really be a desperate effort, planned in delirium, to save his brother by throwing the guilt on the dead man. But again Smerdyakov’s name is pronounced, again there is a suggestion of mystery. There is something unexplained, incomplete. And perhaps it may one day be explained. But we won’t go into that now. Of that later.\n\n“The court has resolved to go on with the trial, but, meantime, I might make a few remarks about the character-sketch of Smerdyakov drawn with subtlety and talent by the prosecutor. But while I admire his talent I cannot agree with him. I have visited Smerdyakov, I have seen him and talked to him, and he made a very different impression on me. He was weak in health, it is true; but in character, in spirit, he was by no means the weak man the prosecutor has made him out to be. I found in him no trace of the timidity on which the prosecutor so insisted. There was no simplicity about him, either. I found in him, on the contrary, an extreme mistrustfulness concealed under a mask of naïveté, and an intelligence of considerable range. The prosecutor was too simple in taking him for weak-minded. He made a very definite impression on me: I left him with the conviction that he was a distinctly spiteful creature, excessively ambitious, vindictive, and intensely envious. I made some inquiries: he resented his parentage, was ashamed of it, and would clench his teeth when he remembered that he was the son of ‘stinking Lizaveta.’ He was disrespectful to the servant Grigory and his wife, who had cared for him in his childhood. He cursed and jeered at Russia. He dreamed of going to France and becoming a Frenchman. He used often to say that he hadn’t the means to do so. I fancy he loved no one but himself and had a strangely high opinion of himself. His conception of culture was limited to good clothes, clean shirt-fronts and polished boots. Believing himself to be the illegitimate son of Fyodor Pavlovitch (there is evidence of this), he might well have resented his position, compared with that of his master’s legitimate sons. They had everything, he nothing. They had all the rights, they had the inheritance, while he was only the cook. He told me himself that he had helped Fyodor Pavlovitch to put the notes in the envelope. The destination of that sum—a sum which would have made his career—must have been hateful to him. Moreover, he saw three thousand roubles in new rainbow-colored notes. (I asked him about that on purpose.) Oh, beware of showing an ambitious and envious man a large sum of money at once! And it was the first time he had seen so much money in the hands of one man. The sight of the rainbow-colored notes may have made a morbid impression on his imagination, but with no immediate results.\n\n“The talented prosecutor, with extraordinary subtlety, sketched for us all the arguments for and against the hypothesis of Smerdyakov’s guilt, and asked us in particular what motive he had in feigning a fit. But he may not have been feigning at all, the fit may have happened quite naturally, but it may have passed off quite naturally, and the sick man may have recovered, not completely perhaps, but still regaining consciousness, as happens with epileptics.\n\n“The prosecutor asks at what moment could Smerdyakov have committed the murder. But it is very easy to point out that moment. He might have waked up from deep sleep (for he was only asleep—an epileptic fit is always followed by a deep sleep) at that moment when the old Grigory shouted at the top of his voice ‘Parricide!’ That shout in the dark and stillness may have waked Smerdyakov whose sleep may have been less sound at the moment: he might naturally have waked up an hour before.\n\n“Getting out of bed, he goes almost unconsciously and with no definite motive towards the sound to see what’s the matter. His head is still clouded with his attack, his faculties are half asleep; but, once in the garden, he walks to the lighted windows and he hears terrible news from his master, who would be, of course, glad to see him. His mind sets to work at once. He hears all the details from his frightened master, and gradually in his disordered brain there shapes itself an idea—terrible, but seductive and irresistibly logical. To kill the old man, take the three thousand, and throw all the blame on to his young master. A terrible lust of money, of booty, might seize upon him as he realized his security from detection. Oh! these sudden and irresistible impulses come so often when there is a favorable opportunity, and especially with murderers who have had no idea of committing a murder beforehand. And Smerdyakov may have gone in and carried out his plan. With what weapon? Why, with any stone picked up in the garden. But what for, with what object? Why, the three thousand which means a career for him. Oh, I am not contradicting myself—the money may have existed. And perhaps Smerdyakov alone knew where to find it, where his master kept it. And the covering of the money—the torn envelope on the floor?\n\n“Just now, when the prosecutor was explaining his subtle theory that only an inexperienced thief like Karamazov would have left the envelope on the floor, and not one like Smerdyakov, who would have avoided leaving a piece of evidence against himself, I thought as I listened that I was hearing something very familiar, and, would you believe it, I have heard that very argument, that very conjecture, of how Karamazov would have behaved, precisely two days before, from Smerdyakov himself. What’s more, it struck me at the time. I fancied that there was an artificial simplicity about him; that he was in a hurry to suggest this idea to me that I might fancy it was my own. He insinuated it, as it were. Did he not insinuate the same idea at the inquiry and suggest it to the talented prosecutor?\n\n“I shall be asked, ‘What about the old woman, Grigory’s wife? She heard the sick man moaning close by, all night.’ Yes, she heard it, but that evidence is extremely unreliable. I knew a lady who complained bitterly that she had been kept awake all night by a dog in the yard. Yet the poor beast, it appeared, had only yelped once or twice in the night. And that’s natural. If any one is asleep and hears a groan he wakes up, annoyed at being waked, but instantly falls asleep again. Two hours later, again a groan, he wakes up and falls asleep again; and the same thing again two hours later—three times altogether in the night. Next morning the sleeper wakes up and complains that some one has been groaning all night and keeping him awake. And it is bound to seem so to him: the intervals of two hours of sleep he does not remember, he only remembers the moments of waking, so he feels he has been waked up all night.\n\n“But why, why, asks the prosecutor, did not Smerdyakov confess in his last letter? Why did his conscience prompt him to one step and not to both? But, excuse me, conscience implies penitence, and the suicide may not have felt penitence, but only despair. Despair and penitence are two very different things. Despair may be vindictive and irreconcilable, and the suicide, laying his hands on himself, may well have felt redoubled hatred for those whom he had envied all his life.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, beware of a miscarriage of justice! What is there unlikely in all I have put before you just now? Find the error in my reasoning; find the impossibility, the absurdity. And if there is but a shade of possibility, but a shade of probability in my propositions, do not condemn him. And is there only a shade? I swear by all that is sacred, I fully believe in the explanation of the murder I have just put forward. What troubles me and makes me indignant is that of all the mass of facts heaped up by the prosecution against the prisoner, there is not a single one certain and irrefutable. And yet the unhappy man is to be ruined by the accumulation of these facts. Yes, the accumulated effect is awful: the blood, the blood dripping from his fingers, the bloodstained shirt, the dark night resounding with the shout ‘Parricide!’ and the old man falling with a broken head. And then the mass of phrases, statements, gestures, shouts! Oh! this has so much influence, it can so bias the mind; but, gentlemen of the jury, can it bias your minds? Remember, you have been given absolute power to bind and to loose, but the greater the power, the more terrible its responsibility.\n\n“I do not draw back one iota from what I have said just now, but suppose for one moment I agreed with the prosecution that my luckless client had stained his hands with his father’s blood. This is only hypothesis, I repeat; I never for one instant doubt of his innocence. But, so be it, I assume that my client is guilty of parricide. Even so, hear what I have to say. I have it in my heart to say something more to you, for I feel that there must be a great conflict in your hearts and minds.... Forgive my referring to your hearts and minds, gentlemen of the jury, but I want to be truthful and sincere to the end. Let us all be sincere!”\n\nAt this point the speech was interrupted by rather loud applause. The last words, indeed, were pronounced with a note of such sincerity that every one felt that he really might have something to say, and that what he was about to say would be of the greatest consequence. But the President, hearing the applause, in a loud voice threatened to clear the court if such an incident were repeated. Every sound was hushed and Fetyukovitch began in a voice full of feeling quite unlike the tone he had used hitherto.\n\n# Chapter XIII. A Corrupter Of Thought\n\n“It’s not only the accumulation of facts that threatens my client with ruin, gentlemen of the jury,” he began, “what is really damning for my client is one fact—the dead body of his father. Had it been an ordinary case of murder you would have rejected the charge in view of the triviality, the incompleteness, and the fantastic character of the evidence, if you examine each part of it separately; or, at least, you would have hesitated to ruin a man’s life simply from the prejudice against him which he has, alas! only too well deserved. But it’s not an ordinary case of murder, it’s a case of parricide. That impresses men’s minds, and to such a degree that the very triviality and incompleteness of the evidence becomes less trivial and less incomplete even to an unprejudiced mind. How can such a prisoner be acquitted? What if he committed the murder and gets off unpunished? That is what every one, almost involuntarily, instinctively, feels at heart.\n\n“Yes, it’s a fearful thing to shed a father’s blood—the father who has begotten me, loved me, not spared his life for me, grieved over my illnesses from childhood up, troubled all his life for my happiness, and has lived in my joys, in my successes. To murder such a father—that’s inconceivable. Gentlemen of the jury, what is a father—a real father? What is the meaning of that great word? What is the great idea in that name? We have just indicated in part what a true father is and what he ought to be. In the case in which we are now so deeply occupied and over which our hearts are aching—in the present case, the father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, did not correspond to that conception of a father to which we have just referred. That’s the misfortune. And indeed some fathers are a misfortune. Let us examine this misfortune rather more closely: we must shrink from nothing, gentlemen of the jury, considering the importance of the decision you have to make. It’s our particular duty not to shrink from any idea, like children or frightened women, as the talented prosecutor happily expresses it.\n\n“But in the course of his heated speech my esteemed opponent (and he was my opponent before I opened my lips) exclaimed several times, ‘Oh, I will not yield the defense of the prisoner to the lawyer who has come down from Petersburg. I accuse, but I defend also!’ He exclaimed that several times, but forgot to mention that if this terrible prisoner was for twenty-three years so grateful for a mere pound of nuts given him by the only man who had been kind to him, as a child in his father’s house, might not such a man well have remembered for twenty-three years how he ran in his father’s back-yard, ‘without boots on his feet and with his little trousers hanging by one button’—to use the expression of the kind-hearted doctor, Herzenstube?\n\n“Oh, gentlemen of the jury, why need we look more closely at this misfortune, why repeat what we all know already? What did my client meet with when he arrived here, at his father’s house, and why depict my client as a heartless egoist and monster? He is uncontrolled, he is wild and unruly—we are trying him now for that—but who is responsible for his life? Who is responsible for his having received such an unseemly bringing up, in spite of his excellent disposition and his grateful and sensitive heart? Did any one train him to be reasonable? Was he enlightened by study? Did any one love him ever so little in his childhood? My client was left to the care of Providence like a beast of the field. He thirsted perhaps to see his father after long years of separation. A thousand times perhaps he may, recalling his childhood, have driven away the loathsome phantoms that haunted his childish dreams and with all his heart he may have longed to embrace and to forgive his father! And what awaited him? He was met by cynical taunts, suspicions and wrangling about money. He heard nothing but revolting talk and vicious precepts uttered daily over the brandy, and at last he saw his father seducing his mistress from him with his own money. Oh, gentlemen of the jury, that was cruel and revolting! And that old man was always complaining of the disrespect and cruelty of his son. He slandered him in society, injured him, calumniated him, bought up his unpaid debts to get him thrown into prison.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, people like my client, who are fierce, unruly, and uncontrolled on the surface, are sometimes, most frequently indeed, exceedingly tender-hearted, only they don’t express it. Don’t laugh, don’t laugh at my idea! The talented prosecutor laughed mercilessly just now at my client for loving Schiller—loving the sublime and beautiful! I should not have laughed at that in his place. Yes, such natures—oh, let me speak in defense of such natures, so often and so cruelly misunderstood—these natures often thirst for tenderness, goodness, and justice, as it were, in contrast to themselves, their unruliness, their ferocity—they thirst for it unconsciously. Passionate and fierce on the surface, they are painfully capable of loving woman, for instance, and with a spiritual and elevated love. Again do not laugh at me, this is very often the case in such natures. But they cannot hide their passions—sometimes very coarse—and that is conspicuous and is noticed, but the inner man is unseen. Their passions are quickly exhausted; but, by the side of a noble and lofty creature that seemingly coarse and rough man seeks a new life, seeks to correct himself, to be better, to become noble and honorable, ‘sublime and beautiful,’ however much the expression has been ridiculed.\n\n“I said just now that I would not venture to touch upon my client’s engagement. But I may say half a word. What we heard just now was not evidence, but only the scream of a frenzied and revengeful woman, and it was not for her—oh, not for her!—to reproach him with treachery, for she has betrayed him! If she had had but a little time for reflection she would not have given such evidence. Oh, do not believe her! No, my client is not a monster, as she called him!\n\n“The Lover of Mankind on the eve of His Crucifixion said: ‘I am the Good Shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep, so that not one of them might be lost.’ Let not a man’s soul be lost through us!\n\n“I asked just now what does ‘father’ mean, and exclaimed that it was a great word, a precious name. But one must use words honestly, gentlemen, and I venture to call things by their right names: such a father as old Karamazov cannot be called a father and does not deserve to be. Filial love for an unworthy father is an absurdity, an impossibility. Love cannot be created from nothing: only God can create something from nothing.\n\n“‘Fathers, provoke not your children to wrath,’ the apostle writes, from a heart glowing with love. It’s not for the sake of my client that I quote these sacred words, I mention them for all fathers. Who has authorized me to preach to fathers? No one. But as a man and a citizen I make my appeal—vivos voco! We are not long on earth, we do many evil deeds and say many evil words. So let us all catch a favorable moment when we are all together to say a good word to each other. That’s what I am doing: while I am in this place I take advantage of my opportunity. Not for nothing is this tribune given us by the highest authority—all Russia hears us! I am not speaking only for the fathers here present, I cry aloud to all fathers: ‘Fathers, provoke not your children to wrath.’ Yes, let us first fulfill Christ’s injunction ourselves and only then venture to expect it of our children. Otherwise we are not fathers, but enemies of our children, and they are not our children, but our enemies, and we have made them our enemies ourselves. ‘What measure ye mete it shall be measured unto you again’—it’s not I who say that, it’s the Gospel precept, measure to others according as they measure to you. How can we blame children if they measure us according to our measure?\n\n“Not long ago a servant girl in Finland was suspected of having secretly given birth to a child. She was watched, and a box of which no one knew anything was found in the corner of the loft, behind some bricks. It was opened and inside was found the body of a new-born child which she had killed. In the same box were found the skeletons of two other babies which, according to her own confession, she had killed at the moment of their birth.\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, was she a mother to her children? She gave birth to them, indeed; but was she a mother to them? Would any one venture to give her the sacred name of mother? Let us be bold, gentlemen, let us be audacious even: it’s our duty to be so at this moment and not to be afraid of certain words and ideas like the Moscow women in Ostrovsky’s play, who are scared at the sound of certain words. No, let us prove that the progress of the last few years has touched even us, and let us say plainly, the father is not merely he who begets the child, but he who begets it and does his duty by it.\n\n“Oh, of course, there is the other meaning, there is the other interpretation of the word ‘father,’ which insists that any father, even though he be a monster, even though he be the enemy of his children, still remains my father simply because he begot me. But this is, so to say, the mystical meaning which I cannot comprehend with my intellect, but can only accept by faith, or, better to say, on faith, like many other things which I do not understand, but which religion bids me believe. But in that case let it be kept outside the sphere of actual life. In the sphere of actual life, which has, indeed, its own rights, but also lays upon us great duties and obligations, in that sphere, if we want to be humane—Christian, in fact—we must, or ought to, act only upon convictions justified by reason and experience, which have been passed through the crucible of analysis; in a word, we must act rationally, and not as though in dream and delirium, that we may not do harm, that we may not ill-treat and ruin a man. Then it will be real Christian work, not only mystic, but rational and philanthropic....”\n\nThere was violent applause at this passage from many parts of the court, but Fetyukovitch waved his hands as though imploring them to let him finish without interruption. The court relapsed into silence at once. The orator went on.\n\n“Do you suppose, gentlemen, that our children as they grow up and begin to reason can avoid such questions? No, they cannot, and we will not impose on them an impossible restriction. The sight of an unworthy father involuntarily suggests tormenting questions to a young creature, especially when he compares him with the excellent fathers of his companions. The conventional answer to this question is: ‘He begot you, and you are his flesh and blood, and therefore you are bound to love him.’ The youth involuntarily reflects: ‘But did he love me when he begot me?’ he asks, wondering more and more. ‘Was it for my sake he begot me? He did not know me, not even my sex, at that moment, at the moment of passion, perhaps, inflamed by wine, and he has only transmitted to me a propensity to drunkenness—that’s all he’s done for me.... Why am I bound to love him simply for begetting me when he has cared nothing for me all my life after?’\n\n“Oh, perhaps those questions strike you as coarse and cruel, but do not expect an impossible restraint from a young mind. ‘Drive nature out of the door and it will fly in at the window,’ and, above all, let us not be afraid of words, but decide the question according to the dictates of reason and humanity and not of mystic ideas. How shall it be decided? Why, like this. Let the son stand before his father and ask him, ‘Father, tell me, why must I love you? Father, show me that I must love you,’ and if that father is able to answer him and show him good reason, we have a real, normal, parental relation, not resting on mystical prejudice, but on a rational, responsible and strictly humanitarian basis. But if he does not, there’s an end to the family tie. He is not a father to him, and the son has a right to look upon him as a stranger, and even an enemy. Our tribune, gentlemen of the jury, ought to be a school of true and sound ideas.”\n\n(Here the orator was interrupted by irrepressible and almost frantic applause. Of course, it was not the whole audience, but a good half of it applauded. The fathers and mothers present applauded. Shrieks and exclamations were heard from the gallery, where the ladies were sitting. Handkerchiefs were waved. The President began ringing his bell with all his might. He was obviously irritated by the behavior of the audience, but did not venture to clear the court as he had threatened. Even persons of high position, old men with stars on their breasts, sitting on specially reserved seats behind the judges, applauded the orator and waved their handkerchiefs. So that when the noise died down, the President confined himself to repeating his stern threat to clear the court, and Fetyukovitch, excited and triumphant, continued his speech.)\n\n“Gentlemen of the jury, you remember that awful night of which so much has been said to-day, when the son got over the fence and stood face to face with the enemy and persecutor who had begotten him. I insist most emphatically it was not for money he ran to his father’s house: the charge of robbery is an absurdity, as I proved before. And it was not to murder him he broke into the house, oh, no! If he had had that design he would, at least, have taken the precaution of arming himself beforehand. The brass pestle he caught up instinctively without knowing why he did it. Granted that he deceived his father by tapping at the window, granted that he made his way in—I’ve said already that I do not for a moment believe that legend, but let it be so, let us suppose it for a moment. Gentlemen, I swear to you by all that’s holy, if it had not been his father, but an ordinary enemy, he would, after running through the rooms and satisfying himself that the woman was not there, have made off, post-haste, without doing any harm to his rival. He would have struck him, pushed him away perhaps, nothing more, for he had no thought and no time to spare for that. What he wanted to know was where she was. But his father, his father! The mere sight of the father who had hated him from his childhood, had been his enemy, his persecutor, and now his unnatural rival, was enough! A feeling of hatred came over him involuntarily, irresistibly, clouding his reason. It all surged up in one moment! It was an impulse of madness and insanity, but also an impulse of nature, irresistibly and unconsciously (like everything in nature) avenging the violation of its eternal laws.\n\n“But the prisoner even then did not murder him—I maintain that, I cry that aloud!—no, he only brandished the pestle in a burst of indignant disgust, not meaning to kill him, not knowing that he would kill him. Had he not had this fatal pestle in his hand, he would have only knocked his father down perhaps, but would not have killed him. As he ran away, he did not know whether he had killed the old man. Such a murder is not a murder. Such a murder is not a parricide. No, the murder of such a father cannot be called parricide. Such a murder can only be reckoned parricide by prejudice.\n\n“But I appeal to you again and again from the depths of my soul; did this murder actually take place? Gentlemen of the jury, if we convict and punish him, he will say to himself: ‘These people have done nothing for my bringing up, for my education, nothing to improve my lot, nothing to make me better, nothing to make me a man. These people have not given me to eat and to drink, have not visited me in prison and nakedness, and here they have sent me to penal servitude. I am quits, I owe them nothing now, and owe no one anything for ever. They are wicked and I will be wicked. They are cruel and I will be cruel.’ That is what he will say, gentlemen of the jury. And I swear, by finding him guilty you will only make it easier for him: you will ease his conscience, he will curse the blood he has shed and will not regret it. At the same time you will destroy in him the possibility of becoming a new man, for he will remain in his wickedness and blindness all his life.\n\n“But do you want to punish him fearfully, terribly, with the most awful punishment that could be imagined, and at the same time to save him and regenerate his soul? If so, overwhelm him with your mercy! You will see, you will hear how he will tremble and be horror-struck. ‘How can I endure this mercy? How can I endure so much love? Am I worthy of it?’ That’s what he will exclaim.\n\n“Oh, I know, I know that heart, that wild but grateful heart, gentlemen of the jury! It will bow before your mercy; it thirsts for a great and loving action, it will melt and mount upwards. There are souls which, in their limitation, blame the whole world. But subdue such a soul with mercy, show it love, and it will curse its past, for there are many good impulses in it. Such a heart will expand and see that God is merciful and that men are good and just. He will be horror-stricken; he will be crushed by remorse and the vast obligation laid upon him henceforth. And he will not say then, ‘I am quits,’ but will say, ‘I am guilty in the sight of all men and am more unworthy than all.’ With tears of penitence and poignant, tender anguish, he will exclaim: ‘Others are better than I, they wanted to save me, not to ruin me!’ Oh, this act of mercy is so easy for you, for in the absence of anything like real evidence it will be too awful for you to pronounce: ‘Yes, he is guilty.’\n\n“Better acquit ten guilty men than punish one innocent man! Do you hear, do you hear that majestic voice from the past century of our glorious history? It is not for an insignificant person like me to remind you that the Russian court does not exist for the punishment only, but also for the salvation of the criminal! Let other nations think of retribution and the letter of the law, we will cling to the spirit and the meaning—the salvation and the reformation of the lost. If this is true, if Russia and her justice are such, she may go forward with good cheer! Do not try to scare us with your frenzied troikas from which all the nations stand aside in disgust. Not a runaway troika, but the stately chariot of Russia will move calmly and majestically to its goal. In your hands is the fate of my client, in your hands is the fate of Russian justice. You will defend it, you will save it, you will prove that there are men to watch over it, that it is in good hands!”\n\n# Chapter XIV. The Peasants Stand Firm\n\nThis was how Fetyukovitch concluded his speech, and the enthusiasm of the audience burst like an irresistible storm. It was out of the question to stop it: the women wept, many of the men wept too, even two important personages shed tears. The President submitted, and even postponed ringing his bell. The suppression of such an enthusiasm would be the suppression of something sacred, as the ladies cried afterwards. The orator himself was genuinely touched.\n\nAnd it was at this moment that Ippolit Kirillovitch got up to make certain objections. People looked at him with hatred. “What? What’s the meaning of it? He positively dares to make objections,” the ladies babbled. But if the whole world of ladies, including his wife, had protested he could not have been stopped at that moment. He was pale, he was shaking with emotion, his first phrases were even unintelligible, he gasped for breath, could hardly speak clearly, lost the thread. But he soon recovered himself. Of this new speech of his I will quote only a few sentences.\n\n“... I am reproached with having woven a romance. But what is this defense if not one romance on the top of another? All that was lacking was poetry. Fyodor Pavlovitch, while waiting for his mistress, tears open the envelope and throws it on the floor. We are even told what he said while engaged in this strange act. Is not this a flight of fancy? And what proof have we that he had taken out the money? Who heard what he said? The weak-minded idiot, Smerdyakov, transformed into a Byronic hero, avenging society for his illegitimate birth—isn’t this a romance in the Byronic style? And the son who breaks into his father’s house and murders him without murdering him is not even a romance—this is a sphinx setting us a riddle which he cannot solve himself. If he murdered him, he murdered him, and what’s the meaning of his murdering him without having murdered him—who can make head or tail of this?\n\n“Then we are admonished that our tribune is a tribune of true and sound ideas and from this tribune of ‘sound ideas’ is heard a solemn declaration that to call the murder of a father ‘parricide’ is nothing but a prejudice! But if parricide is a prejudice, and if every child is to ask his father why he is to love him, what will become of us? What will become of the foundations of society? What will become of the family? Parricide, it appears, is only a bogy of Moscow merchants’ wives. The most precious, the most sacred guarantees for the destiny and future of Russian justice are presented to us in a perverted and frivolous form, simply to attain an object—to obtain the justification of something which cannot be justified. ‘Oh, crush him by mercy,’ cries the counsel for the defense; but that’s all the criminal wants, and to-morrow it will be seen how much he is crushed. And is not the counsel for the defense too modest in asking only for the acquittal of the prisoner? Why not found a charity in the honor of the parricide to commemorate his exploit among future generations? Religion and the Gospel are corrected—that’s all mysticism, we are told, and ours is the only true Christianity which has been subjected to the analysis of reason and common sense. And so they set up before us a false semblance of Christ! ‘What measure ye mete so it shall be meted unto you again,’ cried the counsel for the defense, and instantly deduces that Christ teaches us to measure as it is measured to us—and this from the tribune of truth and sound sense! We peep into the Gospel only on the eve of making speeches, in order to dazzle the audience by our acquaintance with what is, anyway, a rather original composition, which may be of use to produce a certain effect—all to serve the purpose! But what Christ commands us is something very different: He bids us beware of doing this, because the wicked world does this, but we ought to forgive and to turn the other cheek, and not to measure to our persecutors as they measure to us. This is what our God has taught us and not that to forbid children to murder their fathers is a prejudice. And we will not from the tribune of truth and good sense correct the Gospel of our Lord, Whom the counsel for the defense deigns to call only ‘the crucified lover of humanity,’ in opposition to all orthodox Russia, which calls to Him, ‘For Thou art our God!’”\n\nAt this the President intervened and checked the over-zealous speaker, begging him not to exaggerate, not to overstep the bounds, and so on, as presidents always do in such cases. The audience, too, was uneasy. The public was restless: there were even exclamations of indignation. Fetyukovitch did not so much as reply; he only mounted the tribune to lay his hand on his heart and, with an offended voice, utter a few words full of dignity. He only touched again, lightly and ironically, on “romancing” and “psychology,” and in an appropriate place quoted, “Jupiter, you are angry, therefore you are wrong,” which provoked a burst of approving laughter in the audience, for Ippolit Kirillovitch was by no means like Jupiter. Then, à propos of the accusation that he was teaching the young generation to murder their fathers, Fetyukovitch observed, with great dignity, that he would not even answer. As for the prosecutor’s charge of uttering unorthodox opinions, Fetyukovitch hinted that it was a personal insinuation and that he had expected in this court to be secure from accusations “damaging to my reputation as a citizen and a loyal subject.” But at these words the President pulled him up, too, and Fetyukovitch concluded his speech with a bow, amid a hum of approbation in the court. And Ippolit Kirillovitch was, in the opinion of our ladies, “crushed for good.”\n\nThen the prisoner was allowed to speak. Mitya stood up, but said very little. He was fearfully exhausted, physically and mentally. The look of strength and independence with which he had entered in the morning had almost disappeared. He seemed as though he had passed through an experience that day, which had taught him for the rest of his life something very important he had not understood till then. His voice was weak, he did not shout as before. In his words there was a new note of humility, defeat and submission.\n\n“What am I to say, gentlemen of the jury? The hour of judgment has come for me, I feel the hand of God upon me! The end has come to an erring man! But, before God, I repeat to you, I am innocent of my father’s blood! For the last time I repeat, it wasn’t I killed him! I was erring, but I loved what is good. Every instant I strove to reform, but I lived like a wild beast. I thank the prosecutor, he told me many things about myself that I did not know; but it’s not true that I killed my father, the prosecutor is mistaken. I thank my counsel, too. I cried listening to him; but it’s not true that I killed my father, and he needn’t have supposed it. And don’t believe the doctors. I am perfectly sane, only my heart is heavy. If you spare me, if you let me go, I will pray for you. I will be a better man. I give you my word before God I will! And if you will condemn me, I’ll break my sword over my head myself and kiss the pieces. But spare me, do not rob me of my God! I know myself, I shall rebel! My heart is heavy, gentlemen ... spare me!”\n\nHe almost fell back in his place: his voice broke: he could hardly articulate the last phrase. Then the judges proceeded to put the questions and began to ask both sides to formulate their conclusions.\n\nBut I will not describe the details. At last the jury rose to retire for consultation. The President was very tired, and so his last charge to the jury was rather feeble. “Be impartial, don’t be influenced by the eloquence of the defense, but yet weigh the arguments. Remember that there is a great responsibility laid upon you,” and so on and so on.\n\nThe jury withdrew and the court adjourned. People could get up, move about, exchange their accumulated impressions, refresh themselves at the buffet. It was very late, almost one o’clock in the night, but nobody went away: the strain was so great that no one could think of repose. All waited with sinking hearts; though that is, perhaps, too much to say, for the ladies were only in a state of hysterical impatience and their hearts were untroubled. An acquittal, they thought, was inevitable. They all prepared themselves for a dramatic moment of general enthusiasm. I must own there were many among the men, too, who were convinced that an acquittal was inevitable. Some were pleased, others frowned, while some were simply dejected, not wanting him to be acquitted. Fetyukovitch himself was confident of his success. He was surrounded by people congratulating him and fawning upon him.\n\n“There are,” he said to one group, as I was told afterwards, “there are invisible threads binding the counsel for the defense with the jury. One feels during one’s speech if they are being formed. I was aware of them. They exist. Our cause is won. Set your mind at rest.”\n\n“What will our peasants say now?” said one stout, cross-looking, pock-marked gentleman, a landowner of the neighborhood, approaching a group of gentlemen engaged in conversation.\n\n“But they are not all peasants. There are four government clerks among them.”\n\n“Yes, there are clerks,” said a member of the district council, joining the group.\n\n“And do you know that Nazaryev, the merchant with the medal, a juryman?”\n\n“What of him?”\n\n“He is a man with brains.”\n\n“But he never speaks.”\n\n“He is no great talker, but so much the better. There’s no need for the Petersburg man to teach him: he could teach all Petersburg himself. He’s the father of twelve children. Think of that!”\n\n“Upon my word, you don’t suppose they won’t acquit him?” one of our young officials exclaimed in another group.\n\n“They’ll acquit him for certain,” said a resolute voice.\n\n“It would be shameful, disgraceful, not to acquit him!” cried the official. “Suppose he did murder him—there are fathers and fathers! And, besides, he was in such a frenzy.... He really may have done nothing but swing the pestle in the air, and so knocked the old man down. But it was a pity they dragged the valet in. That was simply an absurd theory! If I’d been in Fetyukovitch’s place, I should simply have said straight out: ‘He murdered him; but he is not guilty, hang it all!’”\n\n“That’s what he did, only without saying, ‘Hang it all!’”\n\n“No, Mihail Semyonovitch, he almost said that, too,” put in a third voice.\n\n“Why, gentlemen, in Lent an actress was acquitted in our town who had cut the throat of her lover’s lawful wife.”\n\n“Oh, but she did not finish cutting it.”\n\n“That makes no difference. She began cutting it.”\n\n“What did you think of what he said about children? Splendid, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Splendid!”\n\n“And about mysticism, too!”\n\n“Oh, drop mysticism, do!” cried some one else; “think of Ippolit and his fate from this day forth. His wife will scratch his eyes out to-morrow for Mitya’s sake.”\n“Is she here?”\n\n“What an idea! If she’d been here she’d have scratched them out in court. She is at home with toothache. He he he!”\n\n“He he he!”\n\nIn a third group:\n\n“I dare say they will acquit Mitenka, after all.”\n\n“I should not be surprised if he turns the ‘Metropolis’ upside down to-morrow. He will be drinking for ten days!”\n\n“Oh, the devil!”\n\n“The devil’s bound to have a hand in it. Where should he be if not here?”\n\n“Well, gentlemen, I admit it was eloquent. But still it’s not the thing to break your father’s head with a pestle! Or what are we coming to?”\n\n“The chariot! Do you remember the chariot?”\n\n“Yes; he turned a cart into a chariot!”\n\n“And to-morrow he will turn a chariot into a cart, just to suit his purpose.”\n\n“What cunning chaps there are nowadays! Is there any justice to be had in Russia?”\n\nBut the bell rang. The jury deliberated for exactly an hour, neither more nor less. A profound silence reigned in the court as soon as the public had taken their seats. I remember how the jurymen walked into the court. At last! I won’t repeat the questions in order, and, indeed, I have forgotten them. I remember only the answer to the President’s first and chief question: “Did the prisoner commit the murder for the sake of robbery and with premeditation?” (I don’t remember the exact words.) There was a complete hush. The foreman of the jury, the youngest of the clerks, pronounced, in a clear, loud voice, amidst the deathlike stillness of the court:\n\n“Yes, guilty!”\n\nAnd the same answer was repeated to every question: “Yes, guilty!” and without the slightest extenuating comment. This no one had expected; almost every one had reckoned upon a recommendation to mercy, at least. The deathlike silence in the court was not broken—all seemed petrified: those who desired his conviction as well as those who had been eager for his acquittal. But that was only for the first instant, and it was followed by a fearful hubbub. Many of the men in the audience were pleased. Some were rubbing their hands with no attempt to conceal their joy. Those who disagreed with the verdict seemed crushed, shrugged their shoulders, whispered, but still seemed unable to realize this. But how shall I describe the state the ladies were in? I thought they would create a riot. At first they could scarcely believe their ears. Then suddenly the whole court rang with exclamations: “What’s the meaning of it? What next?” They leapt up from their places. They seemed to fancy that it might be at once reconsidered and reversed. At that instant Mitya suddenly stood up and cried in a heartrending voice, stretching his hands out before him:\n\n“I swear by God and the dreadful Day of Judgment I am not guilty of my father’s blood! Katya, I forgive you! Brothers, friends, have pity on the other woman!”\n\nHe could not go on, and broke into a terrible sobbing wail that was heard all over the court in a strange, unnatural voice unlike his own. From the farthest corner at the back of the gallery came a piercing shriek—it was Grushenka. She had succeeded in begging admittance to the court again before the beginning of the lawyers’ speeches. Mitya was taken away. The passing of the sentence was deferred till next day. The whole court was in a hubbub but I did not wait to hear. I only remember a few exclamations I heard on the steps as I went out.\n\n“He’ll have a twenty years’ trip to the mines!”\n“Not less.”\n\n“Well, our peasants have stood firm.”\n\n“And have done for our Mitya.”\n\n# Chapter I. Plans For Mitya’s Escape\n\nVery early, at nine o’clock in the morning, five days after the trial, Alyosha went to Katerina Ivanovna’s to talk over a matter of great importance to both of them, and to give her a message. She sat and talked to him in the very room in which she had once received Grushenka. In the next room Ivan Fyodorovitch lay unconscious in a high fever. Katerina Ivanovna had immediately after the scene at the trial ordered the sick and unconscious man to be carried to her house, disregarding the inevitable gossip and general disapproval of the public. One of the two relations who lived with her had departed to Moscow immediately after the scene in court, the other remained. But if both had gone away, Katerina Ivanovna would have adhered to her resolution, and would have gone on nursing the sick man and sitting by him day and night. Varvinsky and Herzenstube were attending him. The famous doctor had gone back to Moscow, refusing to give an opinion as to the probable end of the illness. Though the doctors encouraged Katerina Ivanovna and Alyosha, it was evident that they could not yet give them positive hopes of recovery.\n\nAlyosha came to see his sick brother twice a day. But this time he had specially urgent business, and he foresaw how difficult it would be to approach the subject, yet he was in great haste. He had another engagement that could not be put off for that same morning, and there was need of haste.\n\nThey had been talking for a quarter of an hour. Katerina Ivanovna was pale and terribly fatigued, yet at the same time in a state of hysterical excitement. She had a presentiment of the reason why Alyosha had come to her.\n\n“Don’t worry about his decision,” she said, with confident emphasis to Alyosha. “One way or another he is bound to come to it. He must escape. That unhappy man, that hero of honor and principle—not he, not Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but the man lying the other side of that door, who has sacrificed himself for his brother,” Katya added, with flashing eyes—“told me the whole plan of escape long ago. You know he has already entered into negotiations.... I’ve told you something already.... You see, it will probably come off at the third étape from here, when the party of prisoners is being taken to Siberia. Oh, it’s a long way off yet. Ivan Fyodorovitch has already visited the superintendent of the third étape. But we don’t know yet who will be in charge of the party, and it’s impossible to find that out so long beforehand. To-morrow perhaps I will show you in detail the whole plan which Ivan Fyodorovitch left me on the eve of the trial in case of need.... That was when—do you remember?—you found us quarreling. He had just gone down-stairs, but seeing you I made him come back; do you remember? Do you know what we were quarreling about then?”\n\n“No, I don’t,” said Alyosha.\n\n“Of course he did not tell you. It was about that plan of escape. He had told me the main idea three days before, and we began quarreling about it at once and quarreled for three days. We quarreled because, when he told me that if Dmitri Fyodorovitch were convicted he would escape abroad with that creature, I felt furious at once—I can’t tell you why, I don’t know myself why.... Oh, of course, I was furious then about that creature, and that she, too, should go abroad with Dmitri!” Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed suddenly, her lips quivering with anger. “As soon as Ivan Fyodorovitch saw that I was furious about that woman, he instantly imagined I was jealous of Dmitri and that I still loved Dmitri. That is how our first quarrel began. I would not give an explanation, I could not ask forgiveness. I could not bear to think that such a man could suspect me of still loving that ... and when I myself had told him long before that I did not love Dmitri, that I loved no one but him! It was only resentment against that creature that made me angry with him. Three days later, on the evening you came, he brought me a sealed envelope, which I was to open at once, if anything happened to him. Oh, he foresaw his illness! He told me that the envelope contained the details of the escape, and that if he died or was taken dangerously ill, I was to save Mitya alone. Then he left me money, nearly ten thousand—those notes to which the prosecutor referred in his speech, having learnt from some one that he had sent them to be changed. I was tremendously impressed to find that Ivan Fyodorovitch had not given up his idea of saving his brother, and was confiding this plan of escape to me, though he was still jealous of me and still convinced that I loved Mitya. Oh, that was a sacrifice! No, you cannot understand the greatness of such self-sacrifice, Alexey Fyodorovitch. I wanted to fall at his feet in reverence, but I thought at once that he would take it only for my joy at the thought of Mitya’s being saved (and he certainly would have imagined that!), and I was so exasperated at the mere possibility of such an unjust thought on his part that I lost my temper again, and instead of kissing his feet, flew into a fury again! Oh, I am unhappy! It’s my character, my awful, unhappy character! Oh, you will see, I shall end by driving him, too, to abandon me for another with whom he can get on better, like Dmitri. But ... no, I could not bear it, I should kill myself. And when you came in then, and when I called to you and told him to come back, I was so enraged by the look of contempt and hatred he turned on me that—do you remember?—I cried out to you that it was he, he alone who had persuaded me that his brother Dmitri was a murderer! I said that malicious thing on purpose to wound him again. He had never, never persuaded me that his brother was a murderer. On the contrary, it was I who persuaded him! Oh, my vile temper was the cause of everything! I paved the way to that hideous scene at the trial. He wanted to show me that he was an honorable man, and that, even if I loved his brother, he would not ruin him for revenge or jealousy. So he came to the court ... I am the cause of it all, I alone am to blame!”\n\nKatya never had made such confessions to Alyosha before, and he felt that she was now at that stage of unbearable suffering when even the proudest heart painfully crushes its pride and falls vanquished by grief. Oh, Alyosha knew another terrible reason of her present misery, though she had carefully concealed it from him during those days since the trial; but it would have been for some reason too painful to him if she had been brought so low as to speak to him now about that. She was suffering for her “treachery” at the trial, and Alyosha felt that her conscience was impelling her to confess it to him, to him, Alyosha, with tears and cries and hysterical writhings on the floor. But he dreaded that moment and longed to spare her. It made the commission on which he had come even more difficult. He spoke of Mitya again.\n\n“It’s all right, it’s all right, don’t be anxious about him!” she began again, sharply and stubbornly. “All that is only momentary, I know him, I know his heart only too well. You may be sure he will consent to escape. It’s not as though it would be immediately; he will have time to make up his mind to it. Ivan Fyodorovitch will be well by that time and will manage it all himself, so that I shall have nothing to do with it. Don’t be anxious; he will consent to run away. He has agreed already: do you suppose he would give up that creature? And they won’t let her go to him, so he is bound to escape. It’s you he’s most afraid of, he is afraid you won’t approve of his escape on moral grounds. But you must generously allow it, if your sanction is so necessary,” Katya added viciously. She paused and smiled.\n\n“He talks about some hymn,” she went on again, “some cross he has to bear, some duty; I remember Ivan Fyodorovitch told me a great deal about it, and if you knew how he talked!” Katya cried suddenly, with feeling she could not repress, “if you knew how he loved that wretched man at the moment he told me, and how he hated him, perhaps, at the same moment. And I heard his story and his tears with sneering disdain. Brute! Yes, I am a brute. I am responsible for his fever. But that man in prison is incapable of suffering,” Katya concluded irritably. “Can such a man suffer? Men like him never suffer!”\n\nThere was a note of hatred and contemptuous repulsion in her words. And yet it was she who had betrayed him. “Perhaps because she feels how she’s wronged him she hates him at moments,” Alyosha thought to himself. He hoped that it was only “at moments.” In Katya’s last words he detected a challenging note, but he did not take it up.\n\n“I sent for you this morning to make you promise to persuade him yourself. Or do you, too, consider that to escape would be dishonorable, cowardly, or something ... unchristian, perhaps?” Katya added, even more defiantly.\n\n“Oh, no. I’ll tell him everything,” muttered Alyosha. “He asks you to come and see him to-day,” he blurted out suddenly, looking her steadily in the face. She started, and drew back a little from him on the sofa.\n\n“Me? Can that be?” she faltered, turning pale.\n\n“It can and ought to be!” Alyosha began emphatically, growing more animated. “He needs you particularly just now. I would not have opened the subject and worried you, if it were not necessary. He is ill, he is beside himself, he keeps asking for you. It is not to be reconciled with you that he wants you, but only that you would go and show yourself at his door. So much has happened to him since that day. He realizes that he has injured you beyond all reckoning. He does not ask your forgiveness—‘It’s impossible to forgive me,’ he says himself—but only that you would show yourself in his doorway.”\n\n“It’s so sudden....” faltered Katya. “I’ve had a presentiment all these days that you would come with that message. I knew he would ask me to come. It’s impossible!”\n\n“Let it be impossible, but do it. Only think, he realizes for the first time how he has wounded you, the first time in his life; he had never grasped it before so fully. He said, ‘If she refuses to come I shall be unhappy all my life.’ Do you hear? though he is condemned to penal servitude for twenty years, he is still planning to be happy—is not that piteous? Think—you must visit him; though he is ruined, he is innocent,” broke like a challenge from Alyosha. “His hands are clean, there is no blood on them! For the sake of his infinite sufferings in the future visit him now. Go, greet him on his way into the darkness—stand at his door, that is all.... You ought to do it, you ought to!” Alyosha concluded, laying immense stress on the word “ought.”\n\n“I ought to ... but I cannot....” Katya moaned. “He will look at me.... I can’t.”\n\n“Your eyes ought to meet. How will you live all your life, if you don’t make up your mind to do it now?”\n\n“Better suffer all my life.”\n\n“You ought to go, you ought to go,” Alyosha repeated with merciless emphasis.\n\n“But why to-day, why at once?... I can’t leave our patient—”\n\n“You can for a moment. It will only be a moment. If you don’t come, he will be in delirium by to-night. I would not tell you a lie; have pity on him!”\n\n“Have pity on me!” Katya said, with bitter reproach, and she burst into tears.\n\n“Then you will come,” said Alyosha firmly, seeing her tears. “I’ll go and tell him you will come directly.”\n\n“No, don’t tell him so on any account,” cried Katya in alarm. “I will come, but don’t tell him beforehand, for perhaps I may go, but not go in.... I don’t know yet—”\n\nHer voice failed her. She gasped for breath. Alyosha got up to go.\n\n“And what if I meet any one?” she said suddenly, in a low voice, turning white again.\n\n“That’s just why you must go now, to avoid meeting any one. There will be no one there, I can tell you that for certain. We will expect you,” he concluded emphatically, and went out of the room.\n\n# Chapter II. For A Moment The Lie Becomes Truth\n\nHe hurried to the hospital where Mitya was lying now. The day after his fate was determined, Mitya had fallen ill with nervous fever, and was sent to the prison division of the town hospital. But at the request of several persons (Alyosha, Madame Hohlakov, Lise, etc.), Doctor Varvinsky had put Mitya not with other prisoners, but in a separate little room, the one where Smerdyakov had been. It is true that there was a sentinel at the other end of the corridor, and there was a grating over the window, so that Varvinsky could be at ease about the indulgence he had shown, which was not quite legal, indeed; but he was a kind-hearted and compassionate young man. He knew how hard it would be for a man like Mitya to pass at once so suddenly into the society of robbers and murderers, and that he must get used to it by degrees. The visits of relations and friends were informally sanctioned by the doctor and overseer, and even by the police captain. But only Alyosha and Grushenka had visited Mitya. Rakitin had tried to force his way in twice, but Mitya persistently begged Varvinsky not to admit him.\n\nAlyosha found him sitting on his bed in a hospital dressing-gown, rather feverish, with a towel, soaked in vinegar and water, on his head. He looked at Alyosha as he came in with an undefined expression, but there was a shade of something like dread discernible in it. He had become terribly preoccupied since the trial; sometimes he would be silent for half an hour together, and seemed to be pondering something heavily and painfully, oblivious of everything about him. If he roused himself from his brooding and began to talk, he always spoke with a kind of abruptness and never of what he really wanted to say. He looked sometimes with a face of suffering at his brother. He seemed to be more at ease with Grushenka than with Alyosha. It is true, he scarcely spoke to her at all, but as soon as she came in, his whole face lighted up with joy.\n\nAlyosha sat down beside him on the bed in silence. This time Mitya was waiting for Alyosha in suspense, but he did not dare ask him a question. He felt it almost unthinkable that Katya would consent to come, and at the same time he felt that if she did not come, something inconceivable would happen. Alyosha understood his feelings.\n\n“Trifon Borissovitch,” Mitya began nervously, “has pulled his whole inn to pieces, I am told. He’s taken up the flooring, pulled apart the planks, split up all the gallery, I am told. He is seeking treasure all the time—the fifteen hundred roubles which the prosecutor said I’d hidden there. He began playing these tricks, they say, as soon as he got home. Serve him right, the swindler! The guard here told me yesterday; he comes from there.”\n\n“Listen,” began Alyosha. “She will come, but I don’t know when. Perhaps to-day, perhaps in a few days, that I can’t tell. But she will come, she will, that’s certain.”\n\nMitya started, would have said something, but was silent. The news had a tremendous effect on him. It was evident that he would have liked terribly to know what had been said, but he was again afraid to ask. Something cruel and contemptuous from Katya would have cut him like a knife at that moment.\n\n“This was what she said among other things; that I must be sure to set your conscience at rest about escaping. If Ivan is not well by then she will see to it all herself.”\n\n“You’ve spoken of that already,” Mitya observed musingly.\n\n“And you have repeated it to Grusha,” observed Alyosha.\n\n“Yes,” Mitya admitted. “She won’t come this morning.” He looked timidly at his brother. “She won’t come till the evening. When I told her yesterday that Katya was taking measures, she was silent, but she set her mouth. She only whispered, ‘Let her!’ She understood that it was important. I did not dare to try her further. She understands now, I think, that Katya no longer cares for me, but loves Ivan.”\n\n“Does she?” broke from Alyosha.\n\n“Perhaps she does not. Only she is not coming this morning,” Mitya hastened to explain again; “I asked her to do something for me. You know, Ivan is superior to all of us. He ought to live, not us. He will recover.”\n\n“Would you believe it, though Katya is alarmed about him, she scarcely doubts of his recovery,” said Alyosha.\n\n“That means that she is convinced he will die. It’s because she is frightened she’s so sure he will get well.”\n\n“Ivan has a strong constitution, and I, too, believe there’s every hope that he will get well,” Alyosha observed anxiously.\n\n“Yes, he will get well. But she is convinced that he will die. She has a great deal of sorrow to bear...” A silence followed. A grave anxiety was fretting Mitya.\n\n“Alyosha, I love Grusha terribly,” he said suddenly in a shaking voice, full of tears.\n\n“They won’t let her go out there to you,” Alyosha put in at once.\n\n“And there is something else I wanted to tell you,” Mitya went on, with a sudden ring in his voice. “If they beat me on the way or out there, I won’t submit to it. I shall kill some one, and shall be shot for it. And this will be going on for twenty years! They speak to me rudely as it is. I’ve been lying here all night, passing judgment on myself. I am not ready! I am not able to resign myself. I wanted to sing a ‘hymn’; but if a guard speaks rudely to me, I have not the strength to bear it. For Grusha I would bear anything ... anything except blows.... But she won’t be allowed to come there.”\n\nAlyosha smiled gently.\n\n“Listen, brother, once for all,” he said. “This is what I think about it. And you know that I would not tell you a lie. Listen: you are not ready, and such a cross is not for you. What’s more, you don’t need such a martyr’s cross when you are not ready for it. If you had murdered our father, it would grieve me that you should reject your punishment. But you are innocent, and such a cross is too much for you. You wanted to make yourself another man by suffering. I say, only remember that other man always, all your life and wherever you go; and that will be enough for you. Your refusal of that great cross will only serve to make you feel all your life an even greater duty, and that constant feeling will do more to make you a new man, perhaps, than if you went there. For there you would not endure it and would repine, and perhaps at last would say: ‘I am quits.’ The lawyer was right about that. Such heavy burdens are not for all men. For some they are impossible. These are my thoughts about it, if you want them so much. If other men would have to answer for your escape, officers or soldiers, then I would not have ‘allowed’ you,” smiled Alyosha. “But they declare—the superintendent of that étape told Ivan himself—that if it’s well managed there will be no great inquiry, and that they can get off easily. Of course, bribing is dishonest even in such a case, but I can’t undertake to judge about it, because if Ivan and Katya commissioned me to act for you, I know I should go and give bribes. I must tell you the truth. And so I can’t judge of your own action. But let me assure you that I shall never condemn you. And it would be a strange thing if I could judge you in this. Now I think I’ve gone into everything.”\n\n“But I do condemn myself!” cried Mitya. “I shall escape, that was settled apart from you; could Mitya Karamazov do anything but run away? But I shall condemn myself, and I will pray for my sin for ever. That’s how the Jesuits talk, isn’t it? Just as we are doing?”\n\n“Yes.” Alyosha smiled gently.\n\n“I love you for always telling the whole truth and never hiding anything,” cried Mitya, with a joyful laugh. “So I’ve caught my Alyosha being Jesuitical. I must kiss you for that. Now listen to the rest; I’ll open the other side of my heart to you. This is what I planned and decided. If I run away, even with money and a passport, and even to America, I should be cheered up by the thought that I am not running away for pleasure, not for happiness, but to another exile as bad, perhaps, as Siberia. It is as bad, Alyosha, it is! I hate that America, damn it, already. Even though Grusha will be with me. Just look at her; is she an American? She is Russian, Russian to the marrow of her bones; she will be homesick for the mother country, and I shall see every hour that she is suffering for my sake, that she has taken up that cross for me. And what harm has she done? And how shall I, too, put up with the rabble out there, though they may be better than I, every one of them? I hate that America already! And though they may be wonderful at machinery, every one of them, damn them, they are not of my soul. I love Russia, Alyosha, I love the Russian God, though I am a scoundrel myself. I shall choke there!” he exclaimed, his eyes suddenly flashing. His voice was trembling with tears. “So this is what I’ve decided, Alyosha, listen,” he began again, mastering his emotion. “As soon as I arrive there with Grusha, we will set to work at once on the land, in solitude, somewhere very remote, with wild bears. There must be some remote parts even there. I am told there are still Redskins there, somewhere, on the edge of the horizon. So to the country of the Last of the Mohicans, and there we’ll tackle the grammar at once, Grusha and I. Work and grammar—that’s how we’ll spend three years. And by that time we shall speak English like any Englishman. And as soon as we’ve learnt it—good-by to America! We’ll run here to Russia as American citizens. Don’t be uneasy—we would not come to this little town. We’d hide somewhere, a long way off, in the north or in the south. I shall be changed by that time, and she will, too, in America. The doctors shall make me some sort of wart on my face—what’s the use of their being so mechanical!—or else I’ll put out one eye, let my beard grow a yard, and I shall turn gray, fretting for Russia. I dare say they won’t recognize us. And if they do, let them send us to Siberia. I don’t care. It will show it’s our fate. We’ll work on the land here, too, somewhere in the wilds, and I’ll make up as an American all my life. But we shall die on our own soil. That’s my plan, and it shan’t be altered. Do you approve?”\n\n“Yes,” said Alyosha, not wanting to contradict him. Mitya paused for a minute and said suddenly:\n\n“And how they worked it up at the trial! Didn’t they work it up!”\n\n“If they had not, you would have been convicted just the same,” said Alyosha, with a sigh.\n\n“Yes, people are sick of me here! God bless them, but it’s hard,” Mitya moaned miserably. Again there was silence for a minute.\n\n“Alyosha, put me out of my misery at once!” he exclaimed suddenly. “Tell me, is she coming now, or not? Tell me? What did she say? How did she say it?”\n\n“She said she would come, but I don’t know whether she will come to-day. It’s hard for her, you know,” Alyosha looked timidly at his brother.\n\n“I should think it is hard for her! Alyosha, it will drive me out of my mind. Grusha keeps looking at me. She understands. My God, calm my heart: what is it I want? I want Katya! Do I understand what I want? It’s the headstrong, evil Karamazov spirit! No, I am not fit for suffering. I am a scoundrel, that’s all one can say.”\n\n“Here she is!” cried Alyosha.\n\nAt that instant Katya appeared in the doorway. For a moment she stood still, gazing at Mitya with a dazed expression. He leapt impulsively to his feet, and a scared look came into his face. He turned pale, but a timid, pleading smile appeared on his lips at once, and with an irresistible impulse he held out both hands to Katya. Seeing it, she flew impetuously to him. She seized him by the hands, and almost by force made him sit down on the bed. She sat down beside him, and still keeping his hands pressed them violently. Several times they both strove to speak, but stopped short and again gazed speechless with a strange smile, their eyes fastened on one another. So passed two minutes.\n\n“Have you forgiven me?” Mitya faltered at last, and at the same moment turning to Alyosha, his face working with joy, he cried, “Do you hear what I am asking, do you hear?”\n\n“That’s what I loved you for, that you are generous at heart!” broke from Katya. “My forgiveness is no good to you, nor yours to me; whether you forgive me or not, you will always be a sore place in my heart, and I in yours—so it must be....” She stopped to take breath. “What have I come for?” she began again with nervous haste: “to embrace your feet, to press your hands like this, till it hurts—you remember how in Moscow I used to squeeze them—to tell you again that you are my god, my joy, to tell you that I love you madly,” she moaned in anguish, and suddenly pressed his hand greedily to her lips. Tears streamed from her eyes. Alyosha stood speechless and confounded; he had never expected what he was seeing.\n\n“Love is over, Mitya!” Katya began again, “but the past is painfully dear to me. Know that you will always be so. But now let what might have been come true for one minute,” she faltered, with a drawn smile, looking into his face joyfully again. “You love another woman, and I love another man, and yet I shall love you for ever, and you will love me; do you know that? Do you hear? Love me, love me all your life!” she cried, with a quiver almost of menace in her voice.\n\n“I shall love you, and ... do you know, Katya,” Mitya began, drawing a deep breath at each word, “do you know, five days ago, that same evening, I loved you.... When you fell down and were carried out ... All my life! So it will be, so it will always be—”\n\nSo they murmured to one another frantic words, almost meaningless, perhaps not even true, but at that moment it was all true, and they both believed what they said implicitly.\n\n“Katya,” cried Mitya suddenly, “do you believe I murdered him? I know you don’t believe it now, but then ... when you gave evidence.... Surely, surely you did not believe it!”\n\n“I did not believe it even then. I’ve never believed it. I hated you, and for a moment I persuaded myself. While I was giving evidence I persuaded myself and believed it, but when I’d finished speaking I left off believing it at once. Don’t doubt that! I have forgotten that I came here to punish myself,” she said, with a new expression in her voice, quite unlike the loving tones of a moment before.\n\n“Woman, yours is a heavy burden,” broke, as it were, involuntarily from Mitya.\n\n“Let me go,” she whispered. “I’ll come again. It’s more than I can bear now.”\n\nShe was getting up from her place, but suddenly uttered a loud scream and staggered back. Grushenka walked suddenly and noiselessly into the room. No one had expected her. Katya moved swiftly to the door, but when she reached Grushenka, she stopped suddenly, turned as white as chalk and moaned softly, almost in a whisper:\n\n“Forgive me!”\n\nGrushenka stared at her and, pausing for an instant, in a vindictive, venomous voice, answered:\n\n“We are full of hatred, my girl, you and I! We are both full of hatred! As though we could forgive one another! Save him, and I’ll worship you all my life.”\n\n“You won’t forgive her!” cried Mitya, with frantic reproach.\n\n“Don’t be anxious, I’ll save him for you!” Katya whispered rapidly, and she ran out of the room.\n\n“And you could refuse to forgive her when she begged your forgiveness herself?” Mitya exclaimed bitterly again.\n\n“Mitya, don’t dare to blame her; you have no right to!” Alyosha cried hotly.\n\n“Her proud lips spoke, not her heart,” Grushenka brought out in a tone of disgust. “If she saves you I’ll forgive her everything—”\n\nShe stopped speaking, as though suppressing something. She could not yet recover herself. She had come in, as appeared afterwards, accidentally, with no suspicion of what she would meet.\n\n“Alyosha, run after her!” Mitya cried to his brother; “tell her ... I don’t know ... don’t let her go away like this!”\n\n“I’ll come to you again at nightfall,” said Alyosha, and he ran after Katya. He overtook her outside the hospital grounds. She was walking fast, but as soon as Alyosha caught her up she said quickly:\n\n“No, before that woman I can’t punish myself! I asked her forgiveness because I wanted to punish myself to the bitter end. She would not forgive me.... I like her for that!” she added, in an unnatural voice, and her eyes flashed with fierce resentment.\n\n“My brother did not expect this in the least,” muttered Alyosha. “He was sure she would not come—”\n\n“No doubt. Let us leave that,” she snapped. “Listen: I can’t go with you to the funeral now. I’ve sent them flowers. I think they still have money. If necessary, tell them I’ll never abandon them.... Now leave me, leave me, please. You are late as it is—the bells are ringing for the service.... Leave me, please!”\n\n# Chapter III. Ilusha’s Funeral. The Speech At The Stone\n\nHe really was late. They had waited for him and had already decided to bear the pretty flower-decked little coffin to the church without him. It was the coffin of poor little Ilusha. He had died two days after Mitya was sentenced. At the gate of the house Alyosha was met by the shouts of the boys, Ilusha’s schoolfellows. They had all been impatiently expecting him and were glad that he had come at last. There were about twelve of them, they all had their school-bags or satchels on their shoulders. “Father will cry, be with father,” Ilusha had told them as he lay dying, and the boys remembered it. Kolya Krassotkin was the foremost of them.\n\n“How glad I am you’ve come, Karamazov!” he cried, holding out his hand to Alyosha. “It’s awful here. It’s really horrible to see it. Snegiryov is not drunk, we know for a fact he’s had nothing to drink to-day, but he seems as if he were drunk ... I am always manly, but this is awful. Karamazov, if I am not keeping you, one question before you go in?”\n\n“What is it, Kolya?” said Alyosha.\n\n“Is your brother innocent or guilty? Was it he killed your father or was it the valet? As you say, so it will be. I haven’t slept for the last four nights for thinking of it.”\n\n“The valet killed him, my brother is innocent,” answered Alyosha.\n\n“That’s what I said,” cried Smurov.\n\n“So he will perish an innocent victim!” exclaimed Kolya; “though he is ruined he is happy! I could envy him!”\n\n“What do you mean? How can you? Why?” cried Alyosha surprised.\n\n“Oh, if I, too, could sacrifice myself some day for truth!” said Kolya with enthusiasm.\n\n“But not in such a cause, not with such disgrace and such horror!” said Alyosha.\n\n“Of course ... I should like to die for all humanity, and as for disgrace, I don’t care about that—our names may perish. I respect your brother!”\n\n“And so do I!” the boy, who had once declared that he knew who had founded Troy, cried suddenly and unexpectedly, and he blushed up to his ears like a peony as he had done on that occasion.\n\nAlyosha went into the room. Ilusha lay with his hands folded and his eyes closed in a blue coffin with a white frill round it. His thin face was hardly changed at all, and strange to say there was no smell of decay from the corpse. The expression of his face was serious and, as it were, thoughtful. His hands, crossed over his breast, looked particularly beautiful, as though chiseled in marble. There were flowers in his hands and the coffin, inside and out, was decked with flowers, which had been sent early in the morning by Lise Hohlakov. But there were flowers too from Katerina Ivanovna, and when Alyosha opened the door, the captain had a bunch in his trembling hands and was strewing them again over his dear boy. He scarcely glanced at Alyosha when he came in, and he would not look at any one, even at his crazy weeping wife, “mamma,” who kept trying to stand on her crippled legs to get a nearer look at her dead boy. Nina had been pushed in her chair by the boys close up to the coffin. She sat with her head pressed to it and she too was no doubt quietly weeping. Snegiryov’s face looked eager, yet bewildered and exasperated. There was something crazy about his gestures and the words that broke from him. “Old man, dear old man!” he exclaimed every minute, gazing at Ilusha. It was his habit to call Ilusha “old man,” as a term of affection when he was alive.\n\n“Father, give me a flower, too; take that white one out of his hand and give it me,” the crazy mother begged, whimpering. Either because the little white rose in Ilusha’s hand had caught her fancy or that she wanted one from his hand to keep in memory of him, she moved restlessly, stretching out her hands for the flower.\n\n“I won’t give it to any one, I won’t give you anything,” Snegiryov cried callously. “They are his flowers, not yours! Everything is his, nothing is yours!”\n\n“Father, give mother a flower!” said Nina, lifting her face wet with tears.\n\n“I won’t give away anything and to her less than any one! She didn’t love Ilusha. She took away his little cannon and he gave it to her,” the captain broke into loud sobs at the thought of how Ilusha had given up his cannon to his mother. The poor, crazy creature was bathed in noiseless tears, hiding her face in her hands.\n\nThe boys, seeing that the father would not leave the coffin and that it was time to carry it out, stood round it in a close circle and began to lift it up.\n\n“I don’t want him to be buried in the churchyard,” Snegiryov wailed suddenly; “I’ll bury him by the stone, by our stone! Ilusha told me to. I won’t let him be carried out!”\n\nHe had been saying for the last three days that he would bury him by the stone, but Alyosha, Krassotkin, the landlady, her sister and all the boys interfered.\n\n“What an idea, bury him by an unholy stone, as though he had hanged himself!” the old landlady said sternly. “There in the churchyard the ground has been crossed. He’ll be prayed for there. One can hear the singing in church and the deacon reads so plainly and verbally that it will reach him every time just as though it were read over his grave.”\n\nAt last the captain made a gesture of despair as though to say, “Take him where you will.” The boys raised the coffin, but as they passed the mother, they stopped for a moment and lowered it that she might say good-by to Ilusha. But on seeing that precious little face, which for the last three days she had only looked at from a distance, she trembled all over and her gray head began twitching spasmodically over the coffin.\n\n“Mother, make the sign of the cross over him, give him your blessing, kiss him,” Nina cried to her. But her head still twitched like an automaton and with a face contorted with bitter grief she began, without a word, beating her breast with her fist. They carried the coffin past her. Nina pressed her lips to her brother’s for the last time as they bore the coffin by her. As Alyosha went out of the house he begged the landlady to look after those who were left behind, but she interrupted him before he had finished.\n\n“To be sure, I’ll stay with them, we are Christians, too.” The old woman wept as she said it.\n\nThey had not far to carry the coffin to the church, not more than three hundred paces. It was a still, clear day, with a slight frost. The church bells were still ringing. Snegiryov ran fussing and distracted after the coffin, in his short old summer overcoat, with his head bare and his soft, old, wide-brimmed hat in his hand. He seemed in a state of bewildered anxiety. At one minute he stretched out his hand to support the head of the coffin and only hindered the bearers, at another he ran alongside and tried to find a place for himself there. A flower fell on the snow and he rushed to pick it up as though everything in the world depended on the loss of that flower.\n\n“And the crust of bread, we’ve forgotten the crust!” he cried suddenly in dismay. But the boys reminded him at once that he had taken the crust of bread already and that it was in his pocket. He instantly pulled it out and was reassured.\n\n“Ilusha told me to, Ilusha,” he explained at once to Alyosha. “I was sitting by him one night and he suddenly told me: ‘Father, when my grave is filled up crumble a piece of bread on it so that the sparrows may fly down, I shall hear and it will cheer me up not to be lying alone.’”\n\n“That’s a good thing,” said Alyosha, “we must often take some.”\n\n“Every day, every day!” said the captain quickly, seeming cheered at the thought.\n\nThey reached the church at last and set the coffin in the middle of it. The boys surrounded it and remained reverently standing so, all through the service. It was an old and rather poor church; many of the ikons were without settings; but such churches are the best for praying in. During the mass Snegiryov became somewhat calmer, though at times he had outbursts of the same unconscious and, as it were, incoherent anxiety. At one moment he went up to the coffin to set straight the cover or the wreath, when a candle fell out of the candlestick he rushed to replace it and was a fearful time fumbling over it, then he subsided and stood quietly by the coffin with a look of blank uneasiness and perplexity. After the Epistle he suddenly whispered to Alyosha, who was standing beside him, that the Epistle had not been read properly but did not explain what he meant. During the prayer, “Like the Cherubim,” he joined in the singing but did not go on to the end. Falling on his knees, he pressed his forehead to the stone floor and lay so for a long while.\n\nAt last came the funeral service itself and candles were distributed. The distracted father began fussing about again, but the touching and impressive funeral prayers moved and roused his soul. He seemed suddenly to shrink together and broke into rapid, short sobs, which he tried at first to smother, but at last he sobbed aloud. When they began taking leave of the dead and closing the coffin, he flung his arms about, as though he would not allow them to cover Ilusha, and began greedily and persistently kissing his dead boy on the lips. At last they succeeded in persuading him to come away from the step, but suddenly he impulsively stretched out his hand and snatched a few flowers from the coffin. He looked at them and a new idea seemed to dawn upon him, so that he apparently forgot his grief for a minute. Gradually he seemed to sink into brooding and did not resist when the coffin was lifted up and carried to the grave. It was an expensive one in the churchyard close to the church, Katerina Ivanovna had paid for it. After the customary rites the grave-diggers lowered the coffin. Snegiryov with his flowers in his hands bent down so low over the open grave that the boys caught hold of his coat in alarm and pulled him back. He did not seem to understand fully what was happening. When they began filling up the grave, he suddenly pointed anxiously at the falling earth and began trying to say something, but no one could make out what he meant, and he stopped suddenly. Then he was reminded that he must crumble the bread and he was awfully excited, snatched up the bread and began pulling it to pieces and flinging the morsels on the grave.\n\n“Come, fly down, birds, fly down, sparrows!” he muttered anxiously.\n\nOne of the boys observed that it was awkward for him to crumble the bread with the flowers in his hands and suggested he should give them to some one to hold for a time. But he would not do this and seemed indeed suddenly alarmed for his flowers, as though they wanted to take them from him altogether. And after looking at the grave, and as it were, satisfying himself that everything had been done and the bread had been crumbled, he suddenly, to the surprise of every one, turned, quite composedly even, and made his way homewards. But his steps became more and more hurried, he almost ran. The boys and Alyosha kept up with him.\n\n“The flowers are for mamma, the flowers are for mamma! I was unkind to mamma,” he began exclaiming suddenly.\n\nSome one called to him to put on his hat as it was cold. But he flung the hat in the snow as though he were angry and kept repeating, “I won’t have the hat, I won’t have the hat.” Smurov picked it up and carried it after him. All the boys were crying, and Kolya and the boy who discovered about Troy most of all. Though Smurov, with the captain’s hat in his hand, was crying bitterly too, he managed, as he ran, to snatch up a piece of red brick that lay on the snow of the path, to fling it at the flock of sparrows that was flying by. He missed them, of course, and went on crying as he ran. Half-way, Snegiryov suddenly stopped, stood still for half a minute, as though struck by something, and suddenly turning back to the church, ran towards the deserted grave. But the boys instantly overtook him and caught hold of him on all sides. Then he fell helpless on the snow as though he had been knocked down, and struggling, sobbing, and wailing, he began crying out, “Ilusha, old man, dear old man!” Alyosha and Kolya tried to make him get up, soothing and persuading him.\n\n“Captain, give over, a brave man must show fortitude,” muttered Kolya.\n\n“You’ll spoil the flowers,” said Alyosha, “and mamma is expecting them, she is sitting crying because you would not give her any before. Ilusha’s little bed is still there—”\n\n“Yes, yes, mamma!” Snegiryov suddenly recollected, “they’ll take away the bed, they’ll take it away,” he added as though alarmed that they really would. He jumped up and ran homewards again. But it was not far off and they all arrived together. Snegiryov opened the door hurriedly and called to his wife with whom he had so cruelly quarreled just before:\n\n“Mamma, poor crippled darling, Ilusha has sent you these flowers,” he cried, holding out to her a little bunch of flowers that had been frozen and broken while he was struggling in the snow. But at that instant he saw in the corner, by the little bed, Ilusha’s little boots, which the landlady had put tidily side by side. Seeing the old, patched, rusty-looking, stiff boots he flung up his hands and rushed to them, fell on his knees, snatched up one boot and, pressing his lips to it, began kissing it greedily, crying, “Ilusha, old man, dear old man, where are your little feet?”\n\n“Where have you taken him away? Where have you taken him?” the lunatic cried in a heartrending voice. Nina, too, broke into sobs. Kolya ran out of the room, the boys followed him. At last Alyosha too went out.\n\n“Let them weep,” he said to Kolya, “it’s no use trying to comfort them just now. Let us wait a minute and then go back.”\n\n“No, it’s no use, it’s awful,” Kolya assented. “Do you know, Karamazov,” he dropped his voice so that no one could hear them, “I feel dreadfully sad, and if it were only possible to bring him back, I’d give anything in the world to do it.”\n\n“Ah, so would I,” said Alyosha.\n\n“What do you think, Karamazov? Had we better come back here to-night? He’ll be drunk, you know.”\n\n“Perhaps he will. Let us come together, you and I, that will be enough, to spend an hour with them, with the mother and Nina. If we all come together we shall remind them of everything again,” Alyosha suggested.\n\n“The landlady is laying the table for them now—there’ll be a funeral dinner or something, the priest is coming; shall we go back to it, Karamazov?”\n\n“Of course,” said Alyosha.\n\n“It’s all so strange, Karamazov, such sorrow and then pancakes after it, it all seems so unnatural in our religion.”\n\n“They are going to have salmon, too,” the boy who had discovered about Troy observed in a loud voice.\n\n“I beg you most earnestly, Kartashov, not to interrupt again with your idiotic remarks, especially when one is not talking to you and doesn’t care to know whether you exist or not!” Kolya snapped out irritably. The boy flushed crimson but did not dare to reply.\n\nMeantime they were strolling slowly along the path and suddenly Smurov exclaimed:\n\n“There’s Ilusha’s stone, under which they wanted to bury him.”\n\nThey all stood still by the big stone. Alyosha looked and the whole picture of what Snegiryov had described to him that day, how Ilusha, weeping and hugging his father, had cried, “Father, father, how he insulted you,” rose at once before his imagination.\n\nA sudden impulse seemed to come into his soul. With a serious and earnest expression he looked from one to another of the bright, pleasant faces of Ilusha’s schoolfellows, and suddenly said to them:\n\n“Boys, I should like to say one word to you, here at this place.”\n\nThe boys stood round him and at once bent attentive and expectant eyes upon him.\n\n“Boys, we shall soon part. I shall be for some time with my two brothers, of whom one is going to Siberia and the other is lying at death’s door. But soon I shall leave this town, perhaps for a long time, so we shall part. Let us make a compact here, at Ilusha’s stone, that we will never forget Ilusha and one another. And whatever happens to us later in life, if we don’t meet for twenty years afterwards, let us always remember how we buried the poor boy at whom we once threw stones, do you remember, by the bridge? and afterwards we all grew so fond of him. He was a fine boy, a kind-hearted, brave boy, he felt for his father’s honor and resented the cruel insult to him and stood up for him. And so in the first place, we will remember him, boys, all our lives. And even if we are occupied with most important things, if we attain to honor or fall into great misfortune—still let us remember how good it was once here, when we were all together, united by a good and kind feeling which made us, for the time we were loving that poor boy, better perhaps than we are. My little doves—let me call you so, for you are very like them, those pretty blue birds, at this minute as I look at your good dear faces. My dear children, perhaps you won’t understand what I am saying to you, because I often speak very unintelligibly, but you’ll remember it all the same and will agree with my words some time. You must know that there is nothing higher and stronger and more wholesome and good for life in the future than some good memory, especially a memory of childhood, of home. People talk to you a great deal about your education, but some good, sacred memory, preserved from childhood, is perhaps the best education. If a man carries many such memories with him into life, he is safe to the end of his days, and if one has only one good memory left in one’s heart, even that may sometime be the means of saving us. Perhaps we may even grow wicked later on, may be unable to refrain from a bad action, may laugh at men’s tears and at those people who say as Kolya did just now, ‘I want to suffer for all men,’ and may even jeer spitefully at such people. But however bad we may become—which God forbid—yet, when we recall how we buried Ilusha, how we loved him in his last days, and how we have been talking like friends all together, at this stone, the cruelest and most mocking of us—if we do become so—will not dare to laugh inwardly at having been kind and good at this moment! What’s more, perhaps, that one memory may keep him from great evil and he will reflect and say, ‘Yes, I was good and brave and honest then!’ Let him laugh to himself, that’s no matter, a man often laughs at what’s good and kind. That’s only from thoughtlessness. But I assure you, boys, that as he laughs he will say at once in his heart, ‘No, I do wrong to laugh, for that’s not a thing to laugh at.’”\n\n“That will be so, I understand you, Karamazov!” cried Kolya, with flashing eyes.\n\nThe boys were excited and they, too, wanted to say something, but they restrained themselves, looking with intentness and emotion at the speaker.\n\n“I say this in case we become bad,” Alyosha went on, “but there’s no reason why we should become bad, is there, boys? Let us be, first and above all, kind, then honest and then let us never forget each other! I say that again. I give you my word for my part that I’ll never forget one of you. Every face looking at me now I shall remember even for thirty years. Just now Kolya said to Kartashov that we did not care to know whether he exists or not. But I cannot forget that Kartashov exists and that he is not blushing now as he did when he discovered the founders of Troy, but is looking at me with his jolly, kind, dear little eyes. Boys, my dear boys, let us all be generous and brave like Ilusha, clever, brave and generous like Kolya (though he will be ever so much cleverer when he is grown up), and let us all be as modest, as clever and sweet as Kartashov. But why am I talking about those two? You are all dear to me, boys, from this day forth, I have a place in my heart for you all, and I beg you to keep a place in your hearts for me! Well, and who has united us in this kind, good feeling which we shall remember and intend to remember all our lives? Who, if not Ilusha, the good boy, the dear boy, precious to us for ever! Let us never forget him. May his memory live for ever in our hearts from this time forth!”\n\n“Yes, yes, for ever, for ever!” the boys cried in their ringing voices, with softened faces.\n\n“Let us remember his face and his clothes and his poor little boots, his coffin and his unhappy, sinful father, and how boldly he stood up for him alone against the whole school.”\n\n“We will remember, we will remember,” cried the boys. “He was brave, he was good!”\n\n“Ah, how I loved him!” exclaimed Kolya.\n\n“Ah, children, ah, dear friends, don’t be afraid of life! How good life is when one does something good and just!”\n\n“Yes, yes,” the boys repeated enthusiastically.\n\n“Karamazov, we love you!” a voice, probably Kartashov’s, cried impulsively.\n\n“We love you, we love you!” they all caught it up. There were tears in the eyes of many of them.\n\n“Hurrah for Karamazov!” Kolya shouted ecstatically.\n\n“And may the dead boy’s memory live for ever!” Alyosha added again with feeling.\n\n“For ever!” the boys chimed in again.\n\n“Karamazov,” cried Kolya, “can it be true what’s taught us in religion, that we shall all rise again from the dead and shall live and see each other again, all, Ilusha too?”\n\n“Certainly we shall all rise again, certainly we shall see each other and shall tell each other with joy and gladness all that has happened!” Alyosha answered, half laughing, half enthusiastic.\n\n“Ah, how splendid it will be!” broke from Kolya.\n\n“Well, now we will finish talking and go to his funeral dinner. Don’t be put out at our eating pancakes—it’s a very old custom and there’s something nice in that!” laughed Alyosha. “Well, let us go! And now we go hand in hand.”\n\n“And always so, all our lives hand in hand! Hurrah for Karamazov!” Kolya cried once more rapturously, and once more the boys took up his exclamation: “Hurrah for Karamazov!”\n[1] In Russian, “silen.”\n\n[5] Probably the public event was the Decabrist plot against the Tsar, of December 1825, in which the most distinguished men in Russia were concerned.—TRANSLATOR’S NOTE.\n[6] When a monk’s body is carried out from the cell to the church and from the church to the graveyard, the canticle “What earthly joy...” is sung. If the deceased was a priest as well as a monk the canticle “Our Helper and Defender” is sung instead.\n\n[8] Literally: “Did you get off with a long nose made at you?”—a proverbial expression in Russia for failure.\n[9] Gogol is meant.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-28054"]="알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프는 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프의 셋째 아들이었는데, 그 아버지는 한 시절 우리 고장에서 꽤 이름이 알려졌던 지주로, 십삼 년 전에 벌어진 음울하고 비극적인 죽음 때문에 지금도 우리 사이에서 회자되는 인물이거니와, 그 죽음에 관해서는 적절한 대목에서 따로 이야기하기로 하겠다. 지금으로서는 다만 이렇게만 말해 두겠다. 이 \"지주\"는—우리는 그를 그렇게 부르곤 했으나, 실은 평생토록 자기 영지에서 보낸 날이 거의 하루도 없었거니와—기이한 부류의 인간이었으되, 그래도 제법 흔히 마주칠 수 있는 부류로서, 비루하고 사악하면서 동시에 분별이 없는 자였다. 그러나 그는 그런 분별없는 인간들 가운데서도 자기 세속의 잇속만큼은 아주 잘 챙길 줄 아는, 보아하니 그 외에는 아무것도 못 하는 그런 부류였다. 가령 표도르 파블로비치는 거의 무일푼으로 시작했고, 영지라야 보잘것없이 작았으며, 남의 식탁에 끼어 밥을 얻어먹으려 들고 식객 노릇으로 빌붙어 살았으나, 정작 죽고 나니 현금으로만 십만 루블이 있었던 것이 드러났다. 그러면서도 그는 평생토록 온 고장에서 가장 분별없고 종잡을 수 없는 사내 가운데 하나였다. 거듭 말하지만 그것은 어리석음이 아니었으니—이런 종잡을 수 없는 사내들의 대다수는 제법 영민하고 똑똑하다—오로지 분별없음이었고, 그것도 우리 민족 특유의 한 형태였다.\n\n그는 두 번 결혼했고 세 아들을 두었는데, 맏이 드미트리는 첫 아내의 소생이었고, 이반과 알렉세이 두 사람은 둘째 아내의 소생이었다. 표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내 아델라이다 이바노브나는 제법 부유하고 명망 있는 귀족 가문, 역시 우리 고장의 지주인 미우소프 집안의 사람이었다. 어쩌다가 상속녀이자 미인인 데다, 게다가 이 세대에 흔하고 더러는 앞 세대에서도 볼 수 있던 그런 활달하고 똑똑한 처녀가, 우리가 모두 일컫듯이 그렇게 변변찮고 비실비실한 약골과 결혼하게 되었는지는 굳이 설명하려 들지 않겠다. 나는 지난 \"낭만적\" 세대에 속한 어느 젊은 아가씨를 알고 있었는데, 그녀는 마음만 먹으면 언제든 손쉽게 결혼할 수 있었던 한 신사를 향해 몇 해 동안이나 수수께끼 같은 열정을 품은 끝에, 두 사람의 결합에 넘을 수 없는 장애물을 스스로 지어내더니, 마침내 어느 폭풍우 치는 밤에 거의 벼랑이나 다름없는 높은 강기슭에서 제법 깊고 물살 빠른 강물 속으로 몸을 던져 스스로 목숨을 끊고 말았으니, 이는 오로지 제 변덕을 채우기 위해서이자 셰익스피어의 오필리아를 닮고자 함이었다. 실로 그녀가 골라 두고 즐겨 찾던 이 벼랑이 그토록 그림 같지 않았더라면, 그 자리에 무미건조하고 평평한 강기슭이 있었더라면, 십중팔구 그 자살은 결코 일어나지 않았을 것이다. 이것은 엄연한 사실이며, 아마 지난 두세 세대 동안 이와 비슷한 사례가 적지 않았을 것이다. 아델라이다 이바노브나 미우소바의 행동 역시 의심할 바 없이 다른 사람들의 관념의 메아리였고, 정신의 자유가 결핍된 데서 비롯된 짜증의 산물이었다. 그녀는 어쩌면 자신의 여성적 독립을 보이고, 신분의 차별과 가족의 전횡을 짓밟아 버리고 싶었던 것이리라. 그리고 짐작건대 잘 휘둘리는 상상력이 잠깐 동안 그녀를 설득했을 것이니, 표도르 파블로비치가 그 기생충 같은 처지에도 불구하고 그 진보적인 시대의 대담하고 냉소적인 정신의 소유자 가운데 하나라고 믿게 한 것이리라. 사실인즉 그는 심술궂은 어릿광대에 지나지 않았지만 말이다. 이 결혼에 묘미를 더한 것은 그것이 사랑의 도피로 시작되었다는 점이었고, 이는 아델라이다 이바노브나의 공상을 크게 사로잡았다. 당시 표도르 파블로비치의 처지는 그로 하여금 이런 종류의 모험이라면 무엇이든 유난히 열을 올리게 만들었으니, 그는 무슨 수로든 출세하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었기 때문이다. 좋은 가문에 빌붙어 지참금을 얻는다는 것은 솔깃한 전망이었다. 서로 간의 사랑으로 말하자면, 아델라이다 이바노브나의 미모에도 불구하고 신부에게나 그에게나 보아하니 그런 것은 존재하지 않았다. 이는 어쩌면 표도르 파블로비치의 생애에서 그런 종류로는 유일무이한 경우였을 것이니, 그는 언제나 호색적인 기질을 타고나 조금만 부추기면 어떤 치마폭이든 쫓아다닐 채비가 되어 있는 사내였다. 그녀만은 유독 그의 감각에 별다른 호소력을 발휘하지 못한 단 한 명의 여자였던 모양이다.\n\n사랑의 도피가 끝나자마자 아델라이다 이바노브나는 자신이 남편에게 품은 감정이라곤 경멸밖에 없음을 단숨에 알아차렸다. 그리하여 이 결혼은 놀라우리만치 빠르게 그 본색을 드러내었다. 가족이 제법 빨리 이 사태를 받아들이고 달아난 신부에게 지참금을 떼어 주기는 했으나, 부부는 더없이 난잡한 생활을 시작했고 두 사람 사이에는 끊일 줄 모르는 소동이 이어졌다. 젊은 아내가 표도르 파블로비치보다 비할 데 없이 더 너그럽고 기품이 있었다는 말이 있었으니, 지금은 다 알려진 대로 그는 그녀가 받은 돈을 이만오천 루블에 이르기까지 받자마자 죄다 가로채어, 그 수천 루블은 그녀에게서 영영 사라지고 말았다. 그녀의 지참금 일부였던 자그마한 마을과 제법 번듯한 읍내 저택을, 그는 오랫동안 무슨 양도 증서 따위를 동원해 제 명의로 옮기려고 갖은 애를 다 썼다. 아마 그는 성공했을 것이니, 그녀가 정신적으로 지쳐 그에게서 벗어나고 싶어 했고, 또 그가 집요하고 뻔뻔스럽게 들러붙어 자아낸 경멸과 혐오 때문에라도 그러했을 것이다. 그러나 다행히도 아델라이다 이바노브나의 가족이 끼어들어 그의 탐욕을 가로막았다. 부부 사이에 잦은 싸움이 벌어졌다는 것은 엄연한 사실로 알려져 있으나, 소문에 따르면 표도르 파블로비치가 아내를 때린 것이 아니라 오히려 아내에게 두들겨 맞았다고 하니, 그녀는 성미가 불같고 대담하며 눈썹이 짙고 참을성이 없는 여자로서 놀라운 완력을 지니고 있었기 때문이다. 마침내 그녀는 집을 나와, 세 살배기 어린 미탸를 남편의 손에 남겨 둔 채 한 가난뱅이 신학생과 함께 표도르 파블로비치에게서 달아나 버렸다. 그러자 표도르 파블로비치는 곧장 집 안에 버젓이 하렘을 들여놓고 곤드레만드레 술판에 빠져 지냈다. 그러다 짬이 나면 온 고을을 마차로 휘젓고 다니며 만나는 사람마다 붙들고 눈물을 짜내며 아델라이다 이바노브나가 자기를 버렸다고 하소연했는데, 남편이 자기 부부 생활을 두고 입에 담기에는 너무도 수치스러운 시시콜콜한 사연까지 늘어놓았다. 그가 가장 흐뭇해하고 그의 자존심을 가장 우쭐하게 만든 것은, 상처 입은 남편이라는 우스꽝스러운 배역을 연기하면서 자신의 비애를 갖은 윤색을 곁들여 떠벌리는 일이었던 듯하다.\n\n\"표도르 파블로비치, 자네는 슬픔에 잠겼다면서도 어찌나 기뻐 보이는지, 누가 보면 승진이라도 한 줄 알겠네그려.\" 조롱꾼들이 그에게 이렇게 빈정거렸다. 심지어 적잖은 이들은, 그가 어릿광대 노릇을 할 새로운 희극 배역이 생겨 기뻐하는 것이며, 더 우스꽝스럽게 만들려고 일부러 제 가소로운 처지를 모르는 체하는 것이라고 덧붙이기까지 했다. 그러나 누가 알겠는가, 어쩌면 그것은 순박함이었을지도 모른다. 마침내 그는 달아난 아내의 행방을 알아내는 데 성공했다. 가엾은 여인은 페테르부르크에 있는 것으로 드러났는데, 그곳으로 신학생과 함께 가서는 완전한 해방의 삶에 몸을 던지고 있었던 것이다. 표도르 파블로비치는 당장 부산을 떨며 페테르부르크로 갈 채비를 차렸으나, 무슨 목적으로 가려는 것인지는 자기 자신도 말할 수 없었으리라. 어쩌면 그는 정말로 갔을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇게 마음먹고 나자 그는 곧 그 여행에 대비해 기운을 돋운답시고 또 한바탕 무절제한 술판을 벌일 자격이 있다고 느꼈다. 그리고 바로 그 무렵 아내의 가족은 그녀가 페테르부르크에서 죽었다는 소식을 받았다. 한 이야기에 따르면 그녀는 어느 다락방에서 발진티푸스로, 또 다른 설에 따르면 굶주림으로 아주 갑작스레 죽었다고 했다. 표도르 파블로비치는 아내의 죽음을 전해 들었을 때 술에 취해 있었는데, 전하는 이야기로는 그가 거리로 뛰쳐나가 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고 기뻐 소리쳤다고 하니, \"주여, 이제는 당신의 종을 평안히 놓아 보내시나이다\"라고 외쳤다는 것이다. 그러나 어떤 이들은 그가 어린아이처럼 거침없이 울었다고 하는데, 어찌나 서럽게 울던지 그가 자아내는 혐오감에도 불구하고 사람들이 그를 가엾게 여길 정도였다고 한다. 두 가지 설이 다 사실이었을 가능성도 충분하니, 곧 그는 풀려난 것을 기뻐하면서 동시에 자기를 풀어 준 여자를 위해 울었을 수도 있는 것이다. 대체로 사람이란, 비록 사악한 자라 할지라도, 우리가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 순진하고 천진한 법이다. 그리고 우리 자신 또한 그러하다.\n\n# Chapter II. He Gets Rid Of His Eldest Son\n\n그런 사내가 어떤 아버지일 수 있었으며, 제 자식들을 어떻게 길렀을지는 쉬이 짐작할 수 있을 것이다. 아버지로서의 그의 행실은 능히 예상할 만한 그대로였다. 그는 아델라이다 이바노브나와의 결혼에서 얻은 자식을 완전히 내팽개쳤으니, 악의에서도 아니요 부부간의 원한 때문도 아니라, 그저 그 아이를 잊어버렸기 때문이었다. 그가 눈물과 하소연으로 모든 사람을 진절머리 나게 하고 제 집을 난봉의 소굴로 만들어 가던 동안, 이 집안에 충직하게 봉직하던 하인 그리고리가 세 살배기 미탸를 거두어 보살폈다. 만일 그가 돌보지 않았더라면 그 어린것의 작은 속옷 하나 갈아입혀 줄 사람조차 없었을 것이다.\n\n게다가 어머니 쪽 친척들조차 처음에는 그 아이를 잊고 말았다. 외할아버지는 이미 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 미망인이 된 미탸의 외할머니는 모스크바로 옮겨 가 중병을 앓고 있었으며, 딸들은 모두 시집을 가 버린 터라, 미탸는 거의 꼬박 한 해 동안을 늙은 그리고리의 손에 맡겨진 채 하인 오두막에서 그와 함께 지냈다. 그러나 설령 아버지가 그를 기억해 냈더라도(사실 그가 아이의 존재를 아주 모를 리는 없었으니), 아이는 제 난봉질에 거치적거리기만 할 터였으므로 그는 다시 오두막으로 돌려보냈을 것이다. 그런데 마침 미탸의 어머니 쪽 사촌인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 파리에서 돌아왔다. 그는 그 뒤로도 오랜 세월을 외국에서 보냈으나 당시에는 아직 제법 젊은 사내였고, 두 수도와 외국을 두루 다녀온, 계몽된 사상과 유럽적 교양을 갖춘 인물로서 미우소프 집안에서도 단연 두드러졌다. 만년에 이르러 그는 사십 년대와 오십 년대에 흔하던 부류의 자유주의자가 되었다. 그는 생애를 거치는 동안 러시아 안팎에서 당대의 가장 진보적인 인사들과 두루 교분을 맺었다. 프루동과 바쿠닌을 몸소 알고 지냈으며, 노년에 접어들어서는 1848년 2월 파리 혁명의 사흘간을 즐겨 늘어놓으면서, 자기도 바리케이드 전투에 거의 한몫 끼었던 듯이 넌지시 비추기를 좋아했다. 이것은 그의 젊은 시절을 통틀어 가장 흐뭇하게 떠올리는 추억 가운데 하나였다. 그는 옛 방식으로 헤아려 약 천 명의 농노를 거느린 독립된 재산을 지니고 있었다. 그의 훌륭한 영지는 우리 작은 읍내의 변두리에 자리 잡고 있었으며 우리 고장의 이름난 수도원 영지와 맞닿아 있었는데, 표트르 알렉산드로비치는 영지를 물려받자마자 거의 곧장 그 수도원을 상대로 끝 모를 소송을 시작했으니, 강에서의 어업권을 두고서였는지 숲에서의 벌목권을 두고서였는지 나로서는 정확히 알 수 없다. 그는 \"성직자들\"을 향해 공세를 펼치는 것을 시민이자 교양인으로서의 제 의무로 여겼다. 그는 물론 기억하고 있었고 한때 마음을 두기도 했던 아델라이다 이바노브나에 관한 자초지종을 전해 듣고, 또 미탸가 존재한다는 사실을 알게 되자, 표도르 파블로비치를 향한 온갖 젊은이다운 분노와 경멸에도 불구하고 끼어들었다. 그는 그제야 처음으로 표도르 파블로비치와 면식을 트고는, 그 아이의 교육을 자기가 맡고 싶다고 단도직입으로 말했다. 그는 그 사람됨을 보여 주는 일화로서 훗날 오래도록 이렇게 이야기하곤 했으니, 자기가 미탸 이야기를 꺼내기 시작하자 표도르 파블로비치는 한동안 도대체 무슨 아이를 두고 하는 말인지 모르겠다는 표정을 짓더니, 심지어 제 집에 어린 아들이 있다는 말에 놀라기까지 하더라는 것이었다. 이 이야기는 다소 과장되었을지도 모르나, 그래도 어지간히는 사실에 가까웠을 것이다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 평생토록 연기하기를 좋아하여, 때로는 아무런 동기도 없이, 심지어 지금 이 경우처럼 제게 곧장 손해가 되는데도 느닷없이 뜻밖의 배역을 연기하곤 했다. 그런데 이런 습성은 실로 수많은 사람들에게서, 그것도 더러는 표도르 파블로비치 따위와는 다른 아주 영민한 이들에게서도 흔히 볼 수 있는 특성이다. 표트르 알렉산드로비치는 그 일을 정력적으로 밀어붙여, 표도르 파블로비치와 더불어 아이의 공동 후견인으로 지명되었으니, 아이는 어머니가 남긴 자그마한 재산, 곧 집 한 채와 약간의 토지를 가지고 있었던 것이다. 미탸는 과연 이 사촌의 손으로 넘어갔으나, 그에게는 제 가정이 없었던 데다 영지의 수입을 확보하자마자 서둘러 곧장 파리로 돌아가고자 한 까닭에, 그는 아이를 자기 사촌 가운데 한 사람, 모스크바에 사는 어느 부인에게 맡겨 버렸다. 그런데 파리에 영영 눌러앉게 되면서 그 또한 아이를 잊고 말았으니, 특히 2월 혁명이 터져 그의 마음에 평생토록 기억할 인상을 남긴 뒤로는 더욱 그러했다. 모스크바의 부인은 세상을 떠났고, 미탸는 그 부인의 출가한 딸들 가운데 한 사람의 손으로 넘어갔다. 그 뒤로 그는 네 번째로 또 거처를 옮겼던 것 같다. 지금 그 일을 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 맏아들에 관해서는 나중에 들려줄 이야기가 많은 데다, 지금으로서는 이 이야기를 시작하기 위해 빼놓을 수 없는 가장 요긴한 사실들만 추려 두어야 하겠기 때문이다.\n\n우선 이 미탸, 아니 정확히 말해 드미트리 표도로비치는 표도르 파블로비치의 세 아들 가운데 유일하게 자신에게 재산이 있어 성년이 되면 자립할 수 있으리라 믿으며 자라난 인물이었다. 그는 들쭉날쭉하고 종잡을 수 없는 소년기와 청년기를 보냈다. 김나지움 학업을 마치지 못한 채 사관학교에 들어갔다가, 그다음에는 카프카스로 가서 진급했고, 결투를 벌여 사병으로 강등되었다가, 다시 진급했으며, 방탕한 생활을 일삼고 적지 않은 돈을 탕진했다. 그는 성년이 되기 전까지는 표도르 파블로비치에게서 한 푼의 수입도 받지 못했고, 그때까지 빚더미에 올라앉아 있었다. 그가 아버지 표도르 파블로비치를 처음으로 보고 알게 된 것은 성년이 되었을 때로, 제 재산 문제를 아버지와 매듭짓고자 일부러 우리 고장을 찾아왔을 무렵이었다. 그는 아버지가 그다지 마음에 들지 않았던 모양이다. 그는 아버지 곁에 오래 머물지 않고 서둘러 떠나 버렸는데, 다만 약간의 돈을 손에 넣는 데 성공하고, 또 영지로부터 앞으로 지급받기로 하는 약정을 맺었을 따름이었으니, 정작 그 영지의 수입과 가치가 어느 정도인지에 대해서는(주목할 만한 사실인바) 이번 기회에도 아버지에게서 명세를 받아 내지 못했다. 표도르 파블로비치는 그제야 처음으로(이 또한 새겨 둘 만하다) 미탸가 제 재산에 대해 막연하고 부풀려진 관념을 품고 있음을 알아차렸다. 표도르 파블로비치는 이를 두고 매우 흡족해했으니, 그것이 제 속셈과 딱 맞아떨어졌기 때문이다. 그는 다만 그 젊은이가 경박하고 다루기 힘들며 격정에 사로잡히기 쉽고 참을성이 없는 방탕한 자라는 것, 그리고 당장 손에 쥘 돈만 얻을 수 있다면, 물론 잠시뿐일망정, 그것으로 만족하리라는 것을 짐작했다. 그리하여 표도르 파블로비치는 이 점을 이용하기 시작하여, 이따금 푼돈을 분할로 조금씩 던져 주었다. 결국 사 년이 지나 미탸가 참다못해 두 번째로 우리 작은 읍내를 찾아와 아버지와 단번에 깨끗이 결판을 지으려 했을 때, 놀랍게도 자기에게는 아무것도 남은 것이 없으며, 셈을 따지기조차 어렵고, 자기는 제 재산의 값어치를 전부 표도르 파블로비치에게서 돈으로 받아 챙긴 데다 어쩌면 오히려 그에게 빚을 진 처지이며, 자신이 이전 여러 시기에 스스로 원하여 맺은 갖가지 약정에 따라 더 이상 아무것도 기대할 권리가 없다는 것 따위가 드러났다. 젊은이는 망연자실하여 속임수와 사기를 의심하고는 거의 제정신이 아니었다. 그리고 실로 이 사정이야말로 그 파국으로 이어졌으니, 그 전말이 바로 내 첫 도입 이야기의 주제, 아니 정확히는 그 외면을 이루는 것이다. 그러나 그 이야기로 넘어가기에 앞서, 나는 표도르 파블로비치의 다른 두 아들과 그들의 출생에 관해 잠깐 이야기해 두어야 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter III. The Second Marriage And The Second Family\n\n네 살배기 미탸를 자기 손에서 떼어 놓고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 표도르 파블로비치는 두 번째로 결혼했다. 이 두 번째 결혼은 팔 년 동안 이어졌다. 그가 둘째 아내로 맞은 소피야 이바노브나 역시 아주 젊은 처녀로, 어떤 유대인과 함께 자그마한 사업 거리를 좇아 다른 도(道)로 갔다가 거기서 데려온 여자였다. 표도르 파블로비치는 술꾼이며 사악한 난봉꾼이었으나 자기 자본을 굴리는 일만큼은 결코 게을리하지 않았고, 비록 의심할 바 없이 그리 양심적이지는 못했으되 자기 사업을 매우 능란하게 꾸려 나갔다. 소피야 이바노브나는 이름 없는 부제(副祭)의 딸로, 어려서부터 일가붙이 하나 없는 고아로 남겨졌다. 그녀는 어느 장군의 미망인 집에서 자랐는데, 그 노부인은 신분 높고 부유한 늙은 귀부인으로서, 그녀에게는 은인이자 동시에 학대자였다. 자세한 내막은 알지 못하나, 다만 이런 이야기를 들었을 뿐이다. 곧 이 고아 처녀는 온순하고 다정한 사람이었는데, 한번은 다락방 못에 목을 매단 올가미에서 끌어내려진 적이 있었다는 것이니, 그것은 분명 심성이 모질지는 않으되 무위(無爲) 탓에 견딜 수 없는 폭군이 되어 버린 이 노파의 변덕과 끝없는 잔소리에 시달리던 그녀의 고통이 그토록 끔찍했기 때문이었다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 그녀에게 청혼했다. 그를 두고 사람들이 알아보았고, 그는 거절당했다. 그러나 첫 번째 결혼 때와 마찬가지로 그는 이번에도 이 고아 처녀에게 사랑의 도피를 제안했다. 만약 그녀가 제때에 그에 관해 조금만 더 알았더라면 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 그와 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다. 그러나 그녀는 다른 도에 살고 있었고, 더구나 열여섯 살 소녀가 그런 일에 대해 무엇을 알 수 있었겠는가, 은인 곁에 남아 있느니 차라리 강바닥에 가라앉는 편이 낫다는 것 외에는. 그리하여 이 가엾은 아이는 은인을 은인과 맞바꾼 셈이었다. 이번에 표도르 파블로비치는 한 푼도 받지 못했으니, 장군의 미망인이 노발대발했기 때문이다. 그녀는 두 사람에게 아무것도 주지 않았고 둘 다 저주했다. 그러나 그는 애초에 지참금을 셈에 넣지 않았다. 그를 사로잡은 것은 이 순결한 처녀의 빼어난 미모, 무엇보다도 그 순결한 자태였으니, 그것은 지금껏 거칠고 천박한 여성의 미만을 탐해 온 사악한 난봉꾼에게 각별한 매력을 발휘했다.\n\n\"그 순결한 눈동자가 면도날처럼 내 영혼을 베어 가르더란 말이지.\" 훗날 그는 그 역겨운 낄낄거림과 함께 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 이토록 타락한 사내에게 이 말은 물론 육욕적 끌림 이상의 것을 뜻하지는 않았으리라. 그는 아내에게서 지참금을 한 푼도 받지 못했고, 말하자면 그녀를 \"올가미에서\" 데려온 셈이었으므로, 그녀에게 격식을 차리려 들지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 자기에게 \"잘못을 저질렀다\"고 느끼게 만들면서, 그녀의 놀라운 온순함과 순종을 이용하여 결혼 생활의 기본적인 예절마저 짓밟았다. 그는 헤픈 여자들을 집 안으로 끌어들였고, 아내가 보는 앞에서 난잡한 술판을 벌였다. 사태가 어느 지경에까지 이르렀는지 보이기 위해 이런 일을 언급해 두겠다. 곧 음울하고 우둔하며 고집스럽고 따지기 좋아하는 하인 그리고리는, 첫 번째 여주인 아델라이다 이바노브나를 늘 미워했으면서도 이번에는 새 여주인 편을 들었다. 그는 그녀를 두둔하며 하인답지 않은 태도로 표도르 파블로비치에게 욕설을 퍼부었고, 한번은 그 술판을 깨부수고 음탕한 여자들을 모조리 집 밖으로 쫓아내기까지 했다. 결국 어려서부터 공포 속에서 자란 이 불행한 젊은 여인은, 흔히 \"악령에 씌었다\"고들 하는 농촌 아낙들에게 가장 자주 나타나는 그런 종류의 신경병에 걸리고 말았다. 때로는 끔찍한 히스테리 발작 끝에 정신마저 잃곤 했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 표도르 파블로비치에게 이반과 알렉세이 두 아들을 낳아 주었으니, 맏이는 결혼한 첫해에, 둘째는 그로부터 삼 년 뒤에 태어났다. 그녀가 죽었을 때 어린 알렉세이는 네 살이었는데, 이상하게 들리겠지만 나는 그가 평생토록 어머니를 기억했음을 알고 있으니, 물론 꿈결 같은 기억이기는 했다. 그녀가 죽자 두 어린 사내아이에게는 그들의 형 미탸에게 일어났던 것과 거의 똑같은 일이 일어났다. 두 아이는 아버지에게서 완전히 잊히고 버림받았다. 그들은 같은 그리고리의 손에 맡겨져 그의 오두막에서 살았는데, 그들의 어머니를 키운 그 폭군 같은 노부인이 거기서 그들을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 아직 살아 있었고, 그 팔 년 내내 자기가 당한 모욕을 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다. 그 세월 동안 그녀는 자기 소피야가 어떻게 사는지 정확한 소식을 줄곧 캐내고 있었고, 그녀의 병과 끔찍한 처지를 전해 듣고는 자기 하인들 앞에서 두세 번 큰 소리로 단언했다.\n\n\"그래도 싸지. 하느님께서 그 배은망덕을 벌하신 게야.\"\n\n소피야 이바노브나가 죽은 지 꼭 석 달 만에 장군의 미망인이 느닷없이 우리 고을에 나타나 곧장 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 갔다. 그녀는 고을에서 반 시간밖에 머물지 않았으나 적지 않은 일을 해냈다. 때는 저녁이었다. 그 팔 년 동안 그녀가 보지 못했던 표도르 파블로비치가 술에 취해 그녀 앞에 나왔다. 전하는 이야기로는, 그녀가 그를 보자마자 아무 설명도 없이 그의 뺨을 두 차례 철썩철썩 후려갈기고, 한 줌의 머리채를 움켜쥐고 위아래로 세 번 흔들어 댔다는 것이다. 그러고는 한마디 말도 없이 곧장 두 사내아이가 있는 오두막으로 갔다. 한눈에 그 아이들이 씻지도 못하고 더러운 속옷을 걸치고 있는 것을 본 그녀는 즉시 그리고리에게도 따귀를 한 대 올려붙이고, 두 아이를 모두 데려가겠노라 선언하더니, 입은 그대로 아이들을 양탄자에 둘둘 말아 마차에 싣고 자기 고을로 떠나 버렸다. 그리고리는 충직한 종답게 한마디 없이 그 매를 받아들였고, 노부인을 마차까지 배웅하면서 깊숙이 절을 올리고 \"하느님께서 고아들을 위해 마님께 갚아 주실 겁니다\"라고 묵직하게 아뢰었다. \"그래도 너는 멍청이로구나.\" 노부인은 마차를 몰아 떠나면서 그에게 이렇게 소리쳤다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 잘된 일이라 판단했고, 제 아이들의 교육에 관한 어떤 제안에도 장군의 미망인에게 정식 동의를 거절하지 않았다. 그녀에게 얻어맞은 따귀로 말하자면, 그는 온 고을을 휘젓고 다니며 그 이야기를 떠벌렸다.\n\n마침 노부인은 이 일이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 죽었으나, 유언으로 두 사내아이에게 각각 천 루블씩을 남겼는데, \"그들의 교육을 위함이며, 오로지 그들에게만 전부 쓰일 것이되, 다만 스물한 살이 될 때까지 가도록 분배할 것을 조건으로 하노니, 이만하면 그런 아이들에게는 차고 넘치는 몫이니라. 다른 사람들이 제 돈을 함부로 내던지고 싶다면 그러라지.\" 나는 그 유언장을 직접 읽어 보지는 못했으나, 그런 종류의 무언가 이상하고도 몹시 변덕스럽게 표현된 대목이 있었다고 들었다. 그러나 주된 상속자인 그 도의 귀족 회장(會長) 예핌 페트로비치 폴레노프는 정직한 사람으로 드러났다. 그는 표도르 파블로비치에게 편지를 써 보냈으나, 그에게서 아이들 교육을 위해 한 푼도 끌어낼 수 없으리라는 것을 단번에 알아차렸으니(다만 그자는 결코 대놓고 거절하지는 않고 늘 그런 경우에 하듯 차일피일 미루기만 했고, 실은 때때로 주체할 수 없이 감상에 젖기도 했다), 예핌 페트로비치는 그 고아들에게 몸소 관심을 기울였다. 그는 특히 어린 쪽인 알렉세이를 아끼게 되었고, 그 아이는 오랫동안 그의 가족 가운데 한 사람처럼 그 집에서 살았다. 부디 독자들께서는 이 점을 처음부터 유념해 주시기 바란다. 그리고 좀처럼 찾아보기 힘든 너그러움과 인정을 지닌 이 예핌 페트로비치에게, 이 젊은이들은 그 누구보다도 더 많은 교육과 양육의 빚을 졌다. 그는 장군의 미망인이 그들에게 남긴 이천 루블을 한 푼도 손대지 않고 고스란히 간직했으므로, 그들이 성년에 이르렀을 무렵에는 이자가 불어 그 몫이 두 배로 늘어나 있었다. 그는 두 아이를 모두 자기 돈으로 교육시켰고, 분명 각자에게 천 루블을 훌쩍 넘는 돈을 들였다. 그들의 소년 시절과 청년 시절을 시시콜콜 늘어놓지는 않겠고, 다만 가장 중요한 사건 몇 가지만 언급하겠다. 맏이 이반에 관해서는 이것만 말해 두겠으니, 그는 다소 침울하고 속을 드러내지 않는, 그러나 결코 소심하지는 않은 소년으로 자라났다. 열 살 무렵 그는 자기들이 제 집이 아니라 남의 자선에 기대어 살고 있으며, 자기들 아버지가 입에 올리기조차 부끄러운 인간임을 깨달았다. 이 소년은 아주 일찍부터, 거의 갓난아이 적부터(적어도 사람들 말로는 그렇다고 한다) 학업에 비범하고 남다른 재능을 드러내기 시작했다. 정확히 무슨 까닭인지는 모르나, 그는 열세 살이 채 못 되어 예핌 페트로비치의 가족을 떠나 모스크바의 한 김나지움에 들어갔고, 예핌 페트로비치의 오랜 벗인 노련하고 이름난 교사의 집에서 하숙하게 되었다. 이반은 훗날 이것이 모두 예핌 페트로비치의 \"선행을 향한 열정\" 덕분이었다고 단언하곤 했으니, 그는 그 소년의 천재적 재능을 천재적인 교사가 길러야 한다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그 젊은이가 김나지움을 마치고 대학에 들어갔을 때는 예핌 페트로비치도 그 교사도 이미 세상을 떠나고 없었다. 예핌 페트로비치가 폭군 같은 노부인의 유산—천 루블에서 이천 루블로 불어난—을 지급할 채비를 미처 해 두지 못한 탓에, 러시아에서는 피할 수 없는 갖가지 형식 절차로 인해 그 지급이 지연되었고, 이 젊은이는 대학에서의 처음 두 해 동안 몹시 궁핍한 처지에 놓였으니, 공부하는 내내 스스로 생계를 꾸려 가지 않으면 안 되었기 때문이다. 여기서 짚어 둘 것은, 그가 아버지와 연락을 취하려는 시도조차 하지 않았다는 점인데, 이는 아마도 자존심에서, 아버지에 대한 경멸에서, 혹은 그런 아버지에게서는 진정한 도움을 얻지 못하리라 일러 주는 그의 냉정한 분별에서 비롯된 것이리라. 어찌 됐든 이 젊은이는 결코 낙담하지 않았고 일자리를 구하는 데 성공했으니, 처음에는 한 시간에 몇 푼 받는 과외를 했고 나중에는 \"목격자\"라는 서명을 달고 거리에서 일어난 사건들을 다룬 단신 기사를 신문에 실었다. 사람들 말로는, 이 단신들이 어찌나 흥미롭고 톡톡 쏘는 맛이 있던지 곧 받아들여졌다고 한다. 이것만으로도 이 젊은이가, 신문사와 잡지사 사무실 언저리를 어슬렁거리며 프랑스어 번역이나 필사 일거리를 끝없이 애걸하는 것 외에는 더 나은 궁리라곤 못 하는, 가난하고 불운한 남녀 학생 무리보다 실무적으로나 지적으로나 더 뛰어남을 보여 주었다. 일단 편집자들과 인연을 맺은 이반 표도로비치는 줄곧 그들과의 관계를 유지했고, 대학 말년에는 여러 전문 분야의 서적에 대해 빼어난 서평을 발표하여 문단에서 제법 이름이 알려지게 되었다. 그러나 그가 훨씬 더 폭넓은 독자층의 관심을 느닷없이 끌어모으는 데 성공한 것은 마지막 학년에 이르러서였으니, 그리하여 수많은 사람이 그를 주목하고 기억하게 되었다. 그것은 다소 기이한 사건이었다. 막 대학을 졸업하고 이천 루블을 가지고 외국으로 떠날 채비를 하던 무렵, 이반 표도로비치는 더 비중 있는 잡지 가운데 하나에 어떤 기묘한 논문을 발표했는데, 자연과학을 전공한 학생이었던 그가 아무것도 모르리라 여겨질 만한 주제를 다룬 것이어서 세간의 이목을 끌었다. 그 논문은 당시 도처에서 논쟁이 되던 주제—교회 재판소의 위상—를 다루었다. 그는 그 문제에 관한 여러 견해를 논한 뒤, 이어서 자신의 견해를 펼쳐 보였다. 그 논문에서 가장 두드러진 것은 그 어조와 뜻밖의 결론이었다. 교회파에 속한 많은 이들이 그를 의심 없이 자기네 편으로 여겼다. 그러면서도 세속주의자들뿐 아니라 무신론자들까지 그들과 함께 그에게 갈채를 보냈다. 마침내 몇몇 명민한 사람들은 그 논문이 뻔뻔스러운 풍자적 익살에 지나지 않는다는 견해를 내놓았다. 내가 이 사건을 특별히 언급하는 까닭은, 이 논문이 우리 이웃에 있는 그 유명한 수도원에까지 파고들었기 때문인데, 교회 재판소 문제에 각별한 관심을 두고 있던 그곳 수도사들은 그 논문에 완전히 어리둥절해졌다. 글쓴이의 이름을 알게 된 그들은, 그가 이 고을 태생이며 \"바로 그 표도르 파블로비치\"의 아들이라는 데 흥미를 느꼈다. 그리고 바로 그 무렵 글쓴이 본인이 우리 사이에 모습을 나타냈던 것이다.\n\n이반 표도로비치가 무슨 까닭으로 우리 사이에 찾아왔는지, 나는 그때 어떤 불안감을 품고 스스로 물어보았던 것을 기억한다. 그토록 많은 결과로 이어진 첫걸음이었던 이 운명적인 방문을, 나는 끝내 나 자신에게 온전히 설명해 내지 못했다. 겉으로 보기에 그것은 이상한 일이었으니, 그토록 학식 있고 그토록 거만하며 보아하니 그토록 신중한 젊은이가, 그처럼 추잡한 집과, 평생 그를 거들떠보지도 않고 거의 알지도 못하며 한 번도 마음에 두지 않았고 어떤 경우에도 그에게 돈을 주려 하지 않았을—그러면서도 늘 두 아들 이반과 알렉세이가 자기에게 돈을 요구하러 올까 봐 전전긍긍하던—그런 아버지를 느닷없이 찾아왔으니 말이다. 그런데 이 젊은이는 그런 아버지의 집에 머물며 두 달째 함께 살고 있었고, 두 사람은 더없이 좋은 사이였다. 이 마지막 사실은 나뿐 아니라 다른 많은 이들에게도 각별한 놀라움의 까닭이 되었다. 앞서 이미 이야기한 바 있는, 표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내의 사촌인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 마침 자기 영지를 둘러보러 다시 이 근방에 와 있었다. 그는 자신의 영구한 거처인 파리에서 왔다. 그가 그 젊은이와 알게 되었을 때 누구보다도 더 놀라워했던 것을 나는 기억하니, 그 젊은이는 그의 흥미를 몹시 자아냈고, 그는 때때로 젊은이와 논쟁을 벌이며 내심 적잖은 아픔과 더불어 자신의 학식을 그와 견주어 보기도 했다.\n\n\"저 친구는 자존심이 강해.\" 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"저 친구는 결코 푼돈이 아쉬울 일이 없을 거야. 지금 외국으로 떠날 만한 돈도 충분히 갖고 있고. 그런데 여기서 도대체 무얼 바라는 거지? 돈 때문에 온 게 아니라는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하지, 제 아비가 한 푼도 줄 리 없으니 말이야. 술이나 방탕에 취미가 있는 것도 아닌데, 그런데도 제 아비가 그 친구 없이는 못 견디거든. 둘이 어찌나 죽이 잘 맞는지!\"\n\n그것은 사실이었다. 이 젊은이는 자기 아버지에게 틀림없는 영향력을 미치고 있었으니, 아버지는 실제로 더 점잖게 처신하는 듯 보였고 때로는 아들의 말에 따를 채비가 된 것처럼 보이기까지 했는데, 그러면서도 종종 극도로, 심지어 심술궂게 비뚤어지게 굴곤 했다.\n\n이반이 부분적으로는 형 드미트리의 부탁으로, 또 그 형의 이익을 위해 찾아왔다는 사실을 우리가 알게 된 것은 나중의 일이었으니, 그는 바로 이번 방문에서 그 형을 난생처음 만났으나, 모스크바를 떠나기 전에 이미 자기보다는 드미트리에게 더 중대한 어떤 문제를 두고 그와 편지를 주고받은 터였다. 그 일이 무엇이었는지는 독자들께서 때가 되면 소상히 알게 될 것이다. 그러나 내가 이 특별한 사정을 알게 된 뒤에도, 나는 여전히 이반 표도로비치를 수수께끼 같은 인물로 느꼈고, 그의 방문이 어쩐지 의문스럽다고 여겼다.\n\n덧붙이자면, 이반은 당시 자기 아버지와, 아버지와 공공연히 다투며 심지어 소송까지 걸려고 별렀던 형 드미트리 사이를 중재하는 사람으로 비쳤다.\n\n거듭 말하거니와, 이 가족은 이제 처음으로 한자리에 모인 것이었고, 그 구성원 가운데 몇몇은 난생처음 서로 얼굴을 마주했다. 막내 알렉세이는 세 형제 가운데 가장 먼저 도착하여 이미 한 해 전부터 우리 사이에 와 있었다. 이 머리말에서 가장 말하기 어려운 것이 바로 그 동생 알렉세이에 관해서다. 그러나 한 가지 기이한 사실, 곧 내가 나의 주인공을 수련 수사의 법의를 입은 모습으로 독자들께 소개해야만 한다는 점을 설명하기 위해서라도, 나는 그에 관해 미리 얼마간 이야기해 두지 않으면 안 된다. 그렇다, 그는 지난 한 해 동안 우리 수도원에 머물러 왔고, 남은 평생을 거기서 은둔하며 보낼 마음인 듯했다.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Third Son, Alyosha\n\n알료샤는 그때 겨우 스무 살이었고, 그의 형 이반은 스물네 살이었으며, 그들의 맏형 드미트리는 스물일곱이었다. 우선 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이 젊은이 알료샤가 광신자가 아니었으며, 적어도 내 견해로는 신비주의자조차 아니었다는 점이다. 차라리 내 견해를 처음부터 온전히 털어놓는 편이 낫겠다. 그는 그저 일찍부터 인류를 사랑하는 사람이었을 뿐이며, 그가 수도 생활을 택한 것도 단지 그 무렵 그것이, 이를테면 세속적 사악함의 어둠 속에서 사랑의 빛을 향해 발버둥치는 자신의 영혼을 위한 이상적인 도피처로 그에게 비쳤기 때문이었다. 그리고 이 생활이 그에게 그토록 비친 까닭은, 그가 그때 그곳에서, 그가 생각하기에, 한 비범한 존재를—우리 고장의 이름난 조시마 장로를—발견했기 때문이었으니, 그는 자신의 뜨거운 마음의 온정 어린 첫사랑을 죄다 바쳐 그 장로에게 매달렸다. 그러나 그가 그 시절에도 매우 기이했으며, 사실 요람에 있을 때부터 그러했다는 점만은 나도 부인하지 않겠다. 그건 그렇고, 나는 이미 말한 바 있거니와, 그는 네 살 때 어머니를 잃었음에도 평생토록 어머니를, 곧 그 얼굴과 그 어루만짐을 \"마치 살아 계신 채로 내 앞에 서 계신 듯이\" 기억했다. 누구나 알다시피 이런 기억은 더 이른 나이에서, 심지어 두 살 무렵에서도 남아 있을 수 있으나, 어둠 속의 빛 점처럼, 온통 빛이 바래 사라져 버린 거대한 그림에서 그 조각 하나만 찢겨 나온 한 귀퉁이처럼, 한평생을 꿰뚫고 또렷이 떠오르는 일은 좀처럼 없다. 그런데 그의 경우가 바로 그러했다. 그는 어느 고요한 여름 저녁을, 열린 창문을, 지는 해의 비스듬한 햇살을(무엇보다도 이것을 가장 생생하게 떠올렸다) 기억했으니, 방 한 귀퉁이에는 성상이 있었고 그 앞에는 등불이 켜져 있었으며, 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 어머니가 외침과 신음으로 발작하듯 흐느끼면서, 그를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안아 아프도록 꼭 죄어 안고는 그를 위해 성모님께 기도하며, 마치 그를 성모님의 보호 아래 맡기려는 듯 그를 두 팔로 성상을 향해 내밀고 있었다…… 그러다 갑자기 유모가 달려 들어와 질겁하며 그를 어머니의 품에서 낚아챘다. 바로 그것이 그 그림이었다! 그리고 알료샤는 그 순간의 어머니 얼굴을 기억했다. 그는 기억 속의 그 얼굴이 광기에 차 있었으나 아름다웠다고 말하곤 했다. 그러나 그는 이 기억을 누군가에게 이야기하고 싶어 하는 일이 좀처럼 없었다. 어린 시절과 소년 시절에 그는 결코 속내를 잘 드러내는 편이 아니었고 실로 말수가 적었으나, 그것은 수줍음이나 음울한 비사교성 때문이 아니라, 오히려 정반대로, 무언가 다른 것에서, 곧 전적으로 개인적이며 다른 사람들과는 무관하지만 그에게는 너무도 중대한 어떤 내면의 골몰에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그는 그것 때문에 이를테면 남들을 잊어버리는 듯이 보일 지경이었다. 그러나 그는 사람들을 좋아했다. 그는 평생토록 사람들을 무턱대고 믿는 듯이 보였으나, 누구도 그를 바보나 순진한 사람으로 여기지 않았다. 그에게는 무언가 사람으로 하여금 단번에 느끼게 하는 것이 있었으니(그리고 그것은 그 후로도 평생토록 그러했다), 곧 그가 남을 심판하는 자가 되기를 바라지 않으며—결코 남을 비판하는 일을 자청하지 않고 무슨 일로든 누구도 단죄하지 않으리라는 것이었다. 실로 그는 더러 쓰라리게 슬퍼하면서도 모든 것을 조금의 단죄도 없이 받아들이는 듯이 보였으니, 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지 가장 어린 소년 시절에도 누구 하나 그를 놀라게 하거나 겁먹게 할 수 없었다. 스무 살에 더러운 방탕의 소굴이나 다름없는 아버지의 집에 와서도, 정결하고 순결한 그는 차마 두고 볼 수 없을 때면 그저 잠자코 물러났을 뿐, 멸시나 단죄의 기색이라고는 손톱만큼도 내비치지 않았다. 한때 남에게 빌붙어 살던 처지였던 까닭에 예민하고 걸핏하면 발끈하던 그의 아버지는, 처음에는 미심쩍어하며 음울하게 그를 맞았다. \"이 녀석은 말은 별로 없으면서,\" 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"속으로는 더 많이 생각한단 말이야.\" 그러나 곧, 실로 보름도 채 못 되어, 그는 술 취한 눈물을 흘리며 술주정뱅이의 감상에 젖어 무섭도록 자주 그를 끌어안고 입을 맞추기 시작했는데, 그것은 분명 일찍이 어느 누구에게도 품어 본 적 없는 진실하고 깊은 애정이었다.\n\n실로 어디를 가든 누구나 이 젊은이를 사랑했으니, 그것은 그의 가장 어린 시절부터 그러했다. 그가 자신의 후원자이자 은인인 예핌 페트로비치 폴레노프의 집안에 들어갔을 때, 그는 온 가족의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그리하여 그들은 그를 아주 제 자식처럼 여기게 되었다. 그러나 그가 그 집에 들어간 것은 너무도 어린 나이였으므로, 애정을 얻으려는 술수나 잔꾀를 부렸을 리는 없었다. 그러니 곧장 무의식적으로 사랑받게 되는 재주가 그에게는, 이를테면 그 본성에 타고난 것이었다. 학교에서도 마찬가지였으니, 그는 동무들에게 미덥지 못한 아이, 때로는 놀림감이 되고 심지어 미움까지 받는 그런 부류의 아이일 법도 했다. 가령 그는 몽상적이었고 다소 외톨이였다. 가장 어린 시절부터 그는 한 귀퉁이에 틀어박혀 책 읽기를 좋아했으나, 그러면서도 학교에 다니는 내내 모두의 귀염둥이였다. 그는 좀처럼 장난치거나 들떠 떠드는 일이 없었으나, 누구든 첫눈에 그것이 음울함에서 비롯된 것이 아님을 알아볼 수 있었다. 오히려 그는 밝고 성격이 좋았다. 그는 동무들 사이에서 결코 잘난 체하려 들지 않았다. 어쩌면 그래서인지 그는 누구도 두려워하지 않았으나, 소년들은 그가 자기의 그 대담함을 자랑스러워하지 않으며 자신이 담대하고 용감하다는 것조차 모르는 듯하다는 점을 곧 알아차렸다. 그는 결코 모욕에 앙심을 품지 않았다. 모욕을 당한 지 한 시간이 지나면, 그는 두 사람 사이에 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 미덥고 솔직한 표정으로 그 모욕한 자에게 말을 걸거나 무슨 물음에 대답하는 일이 더러 있었다. 그것은 그가 그 모욕을 잊어버린 듯이 보이거나 일부러 용서한 듯이 보여서가 아니라, 단지 그가 그것을 모욕으로 여기지 않았기 때문이었으니, 이것이 소년들을 완전히 정복하고 사로잡았다. 그에게는 한 가지 특성이 있어서, 가장 낮은 학년부터 가장 높은 학년에 이르기까지 모든 동무가 그를 놀려 주고 싶게 만들었는데, 그것은 악의에서가 아니라 그것이 그들을 즐겁게 했기 때문이었다. 이 특성이란 곧 정도가 지나친 광적인 수줍음과 순결함이었다. 그는 여자에 관한 어떤 말과 어떤 대화를 차마 들을 수가 없었다. 학교에는 불행히도 뿌리 뽑을 수 없는 \"어떤\" 말과 대화가 있는 법이다. 마음과 정신이 순결하며 거의 어린애나 다름없는 소년들이, 학교에서 자기네끼리, 심지어 소리 높여, 군인들조차 더러 입에 담기를 망설일 만한 것들과 그림들과 형상들에 관해 이야기하기를 즐긴다. 그뿐 아니라 군인들이 알지도 못하고 짐작조차 못 하는 많은 것을 우리네 지식 계층과 상류 계층의 제법 어린 아이들이 익히 알고 있다. 거기에 도덕적 타락이나 진정으로 썩어 빠진 내면의 냉소가 있는 것은 아니지만, 그런 겉모양은 있으며, 그것은 그들 사이에서 흔히 세련되고 미묘하며 대담하고 본받을 만한 무엇으로 여겨지곤 한다. 알료샤 카라마조프가 그들이 \"그것\"에 관해 이야기할 때 손가락으로 귀를 틀어막는 것을 보고, 그들은 더러 그를 빙 둘러싸고 그의 두 손을 잡아떼어 양쪽 귀에다 추잡한 소리를 외쳐 대곤 했는데, 그동안 그는 발버둥치다 바닥으로 미끄러지면서도 욕설 한마디 내뱉지 않고 몸을 숨기려 애쓰며 그 모욕을 묵묵히 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 마침내 그들은 그를 내버려 두었고, 그를 \"영락없는 계집애\"라고 놀려 대기를 그만두었으며, 더구나 그것을 일종의 약점으로 여겨 가엾게 보아 주기까지 했다. 그는 늘 반에서 우등생 가운데 하나였으나 결코 일등을 한 적은 없었다.\n\n예핌 페트로비치가 죽었을 때 알료샤는 지방 김나지움에서 아직 이태를 더 마쳐야 했다. 슬픔을 가눌 길 없던 미망인은 그가 죽은 직후 거의 곧바로 온 가족을—오직 여자들과 처녀들뿐인 가족을—데리고 이탈리아로 오랜 여행을 떠났다. 알료샤는 일찍이 한 번도 본 적 없는 예핌 페트로비치의 먼 친척인 두 부인의 집으로 가서 살게 되었다. 자신이 그들과 어떤 조건으로 사는지 그 자신도 알지 못했다. 실로 그가 누구의 신세를 지며 사는가에 도무지 마음을 쓰지 않았다는 것은 그의 매우 두드러진 특징이었다. 이 점에서 그는 자신의 형 이반과 뚜렷이 대조를 이루었으니, 이반은 대학에서의 첫 두 해 동안 가난과 씨름하며 제 힘으로 생계를 꾸렸고, 어릴 적부터 은인의 신세를 지며 산다는 것을 쓰라리게 의식해 왔던 것이다. 그러나 알료샤 성격의 이 기이한 특질을 너무 가혹하게 나무라서는 안 되리라고 나는 생각하는바, 그를 조금이라도 사귀어 본 사람이라면 누구든 알료샤가 그런 부류의 젊은이, 곧 거의 종교적 열광가에 가까운 부류로서, 만일 갑자기 큰 재산이 손에 들어온다면 누가 청하기만 해도 선행을 위해서든 혹 어쩌면 약삭빠른 사기꾼에게든 그것을 서슴없이 내주어 버릴 그런 젊은이임을 알아보았을 것이기 때문이다. 대체로 그는 돈의 가치를 거의 모르는 듯이 보였는데, 물론 글자 그대로의 뜻에서는 아니다. 그는 결코 달라고 조른 적이 없는 용돈을 받으면, 무섭도록 함부로 다루어 순식간에 다 써 버리거나, 아니면 그것을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 몇 주일씩 그대로 지니고 있었다.\n\n후년에 돈과 부르주아적 정직함의 문제에 매우 예민한 사람인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프는 알료샤를 알게 된 뒤에 다음과 같은 평을 내렸다.\n\n\"여기 어쩌면 세상에서 단 하나뿐인 사람이 있으니, 그를 백만 인구의 낯선 도시 한복판에 한 푼도 없이 홀로 내버려 두더라도 그는 해를 입지 않을 것이며, 추위와 굶주림으로 죽지도 않을 것이오. 누군가가 당장 그를 먹이고 재워 줄 테니 말이오. 설령 그렇지 않더라도 그는 제 힘으로 거처를 찾아낼 테고, 그것은 그에게 아무런 수고도 굴욕도 들지 않을 것이오. 그리고 그를 재워 주는 것이 짐이 되기는커녕 오히려 즐거움으로 여겨질 게 분명하오.\"\n\n그는 김나지움 학업을 마치지 못했다. 과정이 끝나기 한 해 전에 그는 두 부인에게 문득 자기에게 떠오른 어떤 계획에 관해 아버지를 만나러 가겠다고 알렸다. 그들은 안타까워하며 그를 보내기를 꺼렸다. 여비는 비싸지 않았고, 부인들은 그가 은인의 가족에게서 작별 선물로 받은 시계를 전당 잡히도록 내버려 두지 않았다. 그들은 그에게 넉넉히 돈을 마련해 주었고 심지어 새 옷과 속옷까지 갖추어 주었다. 그러나 그는 받은 돈의 절반을 돌려주며 삼등 칸으로 가겠다고 말했다. 그 고을에 도착한 뒤, 그는 어찌하여 학업도 마치기 전에 왔느냐는 아버지의 첫 물음에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 사람들의 말로는 유난히 생각에 잠긴 듯이 보였다고 한다. 곧 그가 어머니의 무덤을 찾고 있음이 드러났다. 그는 그때 실제로 그것이 자기가 찾아온 유일한 목적임을 인정했다. 그러나 그것이 그 전부의 이유였을 리는 없다. 차라리 그 자신도 자기 영혼 속에 무엇이 갑자기 일어나, 자신을 거역할 수 없이 새롭고 알 수 없으나 피할 수 없는 길로 끌어당겼는지 이해하지도 못하고 설명할 수도 없었다고 보는 편이 옳을 것이다. 표도르 파블로비치는 자신의 둘째 아내가 어디에 묻혔는지 알료샤에게 알려 줄 수 없었으니, 그는 아내의 관 위에 흙을 뿌린 이래 한 번도 그 무덤을 찾은 적이 없었고, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그녀가 어디에 묻혔는지 까맣게 잊어버렸기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 표도르 파블로비치는 얼마 전부터 우리 고을에 살고 있지 않았다. 아내가 죽고 서너 해가 지난 뒤 그는 러시아 남부로 떠났다가 결국 오데사에 모습을 드러내었고, 그곳에서 몇 해를 보냈다. 그는 처음에 제 말마따나 \"비천한 유대인 놈들과 유대인 여편네들과 유대인 새끼들을 잔뜩\" 알게 되었고, 마침내 \"지체 높은 유대인이든 낮은 유대인이든 가리지 않고\" 그들에게 받아들여지는 데까지 이르렀다. 짐작건대 이 시기에 그는 돈을 벌어 그러모으는 독특한 재주를 키운 모양이다. 그가 마침내 우리 고을로 돌아온 것은 알료샤가 도착하기 겨우 세 해 전이었다. 그의 옛 지인들은 그가 결코 노인이라 할 나이가 아닌데도 무섭도록 늙어 보인다는 것을 알아보았다. 그의 처신은 위엄이 더해졌다기보다는 차라리 뻔뻔함이 더해진 것이었다. 한때의 어릿광대는 이제 남을 어릿광대로 만들려는 오만한 버릇을 드러내었다. 여자에 대한 그의 방탕은 그저 예전 그대로가 아니라 더욱 역겨워졌다. 얼마 안 가서 그는 그 일대에 새 술집을 여럿 열었다. 그가 십만 루블, 혹은 그에 그리 못지않은 돈을 지녔으리라는 것이 분명했다. 고을과 일대의 많은 주민이 곧 그에게 빚을 졌고, 물론 든든한 담보를 잡혔다. 또 근래에 그는 어쩐지 부어오른 듯이 보였고 한층 무책임하고 더 변덕스러워졌으며, 일종의 종잡을 수 없는 상태에 빠져, 한 가지 일을 시작했다가 딴 일로 넘어가곤 하는 것이 마치 자신을 송두리째 내버려 두는 듯했다. 그는 점점 더 자주 술에 취했다. 그리고 만일 그 무렵 역시 상당히 늙어 버려 때로는 거의 가정교사처럼 그를 돌봐 주던 바로 그 하인 그리고리가 없었더라면, 표도르 파블로비치는 끔찍한 곤경에 빠졌을지도 모른다. 알료샤가 온 것은 그의 도덕적인 면까지도 건드린 듯했으니, 마치 이 일찍 늙어 버린 사내의 영혼 속에 오래도록 죽어 있던 무언가가 깨어난 것만 같았다.\n\n\"이거 아느냐,\" 그는 알료샤를 바라보며 자주 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"너는 그 여자를, '그 미친 여자'를 꼭 닮았다.\" 그것은 그가 죽은 아내, 곧 알료샤의 어머니를 부르던 말이었다. \"그 미친 여자\"의 무덤을 알료샤에게 가리켜 준 것은 그리고리였다. 그는 알료샤를 우리 고을 묘지로 데려가, 외진 한 귀퉁이에서 값은 싸지만 제법 단정하게 손질된 무쇠 묘비 하나를 보여 주었는데, 거기에는 고인의 이름과 나이와 사망 날짜가 새겨져 있었고 그 아래에는 옛날 중류층 무덤에 흔히 쓰이는 그런 넉 줄짜리 시구가 적혀 있었다. 알료샤가 놀랍게도 이 무덤은 그리고리가 손수 마련한 것으로 드러났다. 표도르 파블로비치가, 그토록 무덤 일로 자주 졸라 댔건만, 무덤도 모든 추억도 다 내버린 채 오데사로 떠나 버린 뒤, 그가 제 돈을 들여 가엾은 \"미친 여자\"의 무덤 위에 그것을 세워 놓았던 것이다. 알료샤는 어머니의 무덤을 보고도 별다른 감정을 드러내지 않았다. 그는 다만 무덤을 세운 경위에 관한 그리고리의 자상하고 엄숙한 이야기에 귀를 기울였을 뿐, 고개를 숙인 채 서 있다가 한마디 말도 없이 돌아서 갔다. 그가 다시 묘지를 찾기까지는 아마 한 해쯤 걸렸을 것이다. 그러나 이 자그마한 일화도 표도르 파블로비치에게 영향을 미치지 않은 것은 아니었으니—그것도 아주 별난 영향이었다. 그는 문득 우리 수도원에 천 루블을 가져가 죽은 아내의 영혼을 위한 진혼 미사를 부탁했는데, 그것은 둘째 아내인 알료샤의 어머니, 곧 \"미친 여자\"를 위한 것이 아니라, 그를 두들겨 패곤 하던 첫 아내 아델라이다 이바노브나를 위한 것이었다. 같은 날 저녁 그는 술에 취해 알료샤 앞에서 수도사들을 욕해 댔다. 그 자신은 신앙심과는 거리가 멀었으니, 아마 성인의 성상 앞에 동전 한 푼짜리 양초조차 바쳐 본 적이 없었을 것이다. 갑작스러운 감정과 갑작스러운 생각의 기이한 충동은 그런 부류에게 흔한 일이다.\n\n나는 이미 그가 부어오른 듯이 보였다고 말한 바 있다. 이 무렵 그의 얼굴에는 그가 살아온 삶을 틀림없이 증언하는 무언가의 흔적이 새겨져 있었다. 늘 오만하고 의심스럽고 비웃는 듯한 그 작은 두 눈 밑에 늘어진 길쭉한 살주머니 말고도, 그 작고 살진 얼굴에 깊이 팬 무수한 주름살 말고도, 뾰족한 턱 아래에는 커다란 살덩이 갑상선종처럼 울대뼈가 달랑거려 그에게 유난히 역겨운 호색의 인상을 풍겼으니, 거기에 두툼한 입술을 가진 길쭉하고 탐욕스러운 입까지 보태면, 그 입술 사이로는 까맣게 썩은 이의 자그마한 그루터기들이 들여다보였다. 그는 말을 시작할 때마다 침을 질질 흘렸다. 그는 실로 자기 얼굴을 두고 농지거리하기를 좋아했으나, 내 생각에 그는 자기 얼굴에 흡족해하고 있었다. 그는 특히 자기 코를 가리켜 보이곤 했는데, 그리 크지는 않으나 매우 섬세하고 두드러지게 매부리 모양이었다. \"영락없는 로마식 코지,\" 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"이 갑상선종까지 곁들이면 나는 영판 쇠퇴기 고대 로마 귀족의 상판이라니까.\" 그는 그것을 자랑스러워하는 듯했다.\n\n어머니의 무덤을 찾은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 알료샤는 문득 자기가 수도원에 들어가고 싶으며, 수도사들도 자기를 수련 수사로 받아들일 뜻이 있다고 알렸다. 그는 이것이 자신의 간절한 소망이며, 아버지로서 그의 허락을 정중히 구한다고 설명했다. 노인은 수도원 암자에 살고 있는 조시마 장로가 자신의 \"온순한 아들\"에게 특별한 감화를 주었음을 알고 있었다.\n\n\"하기야 그 사람이야 거기서 가장 정직한 수도사지,\" 그는 생각에 잠긴 채 잠자코 알료샤의 말에 귀를 기울이고 나서, 그 청에 별로 놀라지도 않은 듯이 이렇게 말했다. \"흠!…… 그래, 거기가 네가 가고 싶은 데란 말이지, 내 온순한 아들아?\"\n\n그는 반쯤 취해 있었는데, 갑자기 그 느릿한 반취반성의 히죽거림을 지었으니, 거기에는 어딘가 교활하고 술기운 어린 능청스러움이 없지 않았다. \"흠!…… 나는 네가 결국 이런 식으로 끝장날 거라는 예감이 들었더랬다. 믿어지느냐? 너는 곧장 그리로 향하고 있었어. 그래, 하긴 너에게는 네 몫의 이천 루블이 있지. 그게 네 지참금인 셈이야. 그리고 나는 결코 너를 저버리지 않으마, 내 천사야. 그리고 거기서 너를 위해 무엇이든 필요한 게 있으면, 저들이 청하기만 하면 내가 치러 주마. 하지만 물론 저들이 청하지 않으면야, 우리가 무엇하러 들볶겠느냐? 어떠냐? 너야 알다시피 카나리아처럼 한 주에 좁쌀 두 알밖에 안 쓰는 녀석이니 말이다. 흠!…… 너 아느냐, 어느 수도원 가까이, 그 고을 밖에 '수도사의 마누라들'이라 불리는 여편네들만 산다는 걸 갓난애도 다 아는 그런 데가 있다는 걸 말이다. 내 알기로 서른 명쯤 돼. 나도 거기 가 봤다. 너 알다시피, 그것도 그것대로 흥미롭거든, 별미라면 별미니까. 무엇보다 고약한 건 그게 지독하게 러시아적이라는 거다. 거기엔 프랑스 여자가 없어. 물론 마음만 먹으면야 금세 데려올 수 있지, 돈이야 넘쳐 나니까. 소문만 들으면 당장 몰려올걸. 자, 여기는 그런 게 전혀 없어, '수도사의 마누라들' 같은 건 없고 수도사가 이백 명이지. 그자들은 정직해. 단식도 지키고. 인정한다……흠……그래, 너는 수도사가 되겠단 말이지? 그런데 너 아느냐, 나는 너를 떠나보내는 게 섭섭하구나, 알료샤. 믿어지느냐, 내가 정말로 너를 좋아하게 됐단 말이다? 뭐, 그래도 좋은 기회지. 너는 우리 죄인들을 위해 기도해 주겠지. 우리는 여기서 너무 많은 죄를 지었으니까. 나는 늘 누가 나를 위해 기도해 줄까, 세상에 그래 줄 사람이 하나라도 있을까 생각해 왔다. 사랑하는 아들아, 나는 그 일에 대해선 지독하게 멍청하구나. 믿어지지 않겠지. 지독하게 말이다. 너도 알다시피, 내가 그 일에 아무리 멍청해도 자꾸 생각하게 되는 거야, 자꾸 생각하게 돼—물론 늘 그러는 건 아니고 이따금이다만. 내 생각엔 말이다, 내가 죽을 때 악마들이 그 갈고리로 나를 지옥에 끌어내리는 걸 잊어버릴 리가 없을 것 같단 말이야. 그러면 또 이런 의문이 들거든—갈고리라고? 그걸 어디서 구한담? 무얼로 만들었을까? 쇠 갈고리? 그걸 어디서 벼려 낸담? 거기 무슨 주물 공장이라도 있는 걸까? 수도원 수도사들이야 아마 가령 지옥에 천장이 있다고 믿겠지. 한데 나는 지옥은 믿을 채비가 돼 있다만, 천장 없는 지옥이라야 해. 그래야 한결 세련되고 한결 계몽되고 한결 루터교다워지거든. 그리고 따지고 보면 천장이 있건 없건 그게 무슨 대수냐? 한데 너 아느냐, 거기엔 망할 놈의 문제가 하나 얽혀 있단 말이다. 천장이 없으면 갈고리도 있을 수 없고, 갈고리가 없으면 죄다 무너지는 거야. 한데 그것도 영 그럴듯하지가 않은 게, 그러면 나를 지옥으로 끌어내릴 자가 아무도 없을 거고, 나를 끌어내리지 않는다면 세상에 무슨 정의가 있겠느냐? 그 갈고리들을 나 하나만을 위해 일부러라도 만들어 내야 할 판이지(Il faudrait les inventer), 왜냐하면 알료샤야, 네가 알기만 한다면 말이다, 내가 얼마나 못돼 먹은 놈인지 말이다.\"\n\n\"하지만 거기엔 갈고리 같은 건 없어요.\" 알료샤가 아버지를 부드럽고 진지하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 갈고리의 그림자뿐이겠지, 알아, 알아. 어느 프랑스 사람이 지옥을 그렇게 묘사했더랬다. '나는 한 마부의 그림자가 솔 한 자루의 그림자로 마차 한 대의 그림자를 닦고 있는 것을 보았노라(J'ai vu l'ombre d'un cocher qui avec l'ombre d'une brosse frottait l'ombre d'une carrosse).' 한데 거기 갈고리가 없다는 걸 네가 어찌 아느냐, 얘야? 수도사들하고 좀 살아 보면 너도 딴소리를 하게 될걸. 하지만 거기 가서 진리를 찾아내거든, 그러거든 와서 나한테 말해 다오. 어쨌든 저세상에 무엇이 있는지 알고 가는 편이 한결 수월할 테니까. 게다가 술 취한 늙은이하고 젊은 갈보들하고 여기 나하고 있는 것보다는 수도사들하고 있는 게 너한테 더 어울리기도 하고…… 하긴 너야 천사 같으니, 무엇 하나 너를 더럽히지 못하지. 그리고 거기서도 아마 무엇 하나 너를 더럽히지 못할 거다. 내가 너를 보내 주는 것도 그래서야, 그걸 바라기 때문이지. 너는 정신이 멀쩡하니까. 너는 타오를 테고 또 다 타 버릴 테지. 그러면 치유되어 다시 돌아올 거야. 그러면 나는 너를 기다리마. 나는 느낀단다, 네가 나를 단죄하지 않은 세상의 단 하나뿐인 존재라는 걸 말이다. 사랑하는 아들아, 나는 그걸 느껴, 알다시피. 느끼지 않을 수가 없구나.\"\n\n그러고는 그는 엉엉 울기까지 했다. 그는 감상적이었다. 그는 사악하면서도 감상적이었다.\n\n# Chapter V. Elders\n\n내 독자들 가운데 더러는 이 젊은이가 병약하고 황홀경에 잠긴, 제대로 자라지 못한 위인, 곧 창백하고 폐병이나 앓을 법한 몽상가라고 상상할지도 모른다. 그러나 그와는 반대로, 알료샤는 이 무렵 건강이 넘쳐흐르는 열아홉 살의 잘 자란 청년으로, 뺨이 붉고 눈이 맑았다. 그는 또한 무척 잘생겼고, 몸매가 곱고, 키는 알맞게 컸으며, 머리칼은 짙은 갈색이었고, 얼굴은 단정하고 다소 갸름한 타원형이었으며, 좌우로 멀찍이 자리한 짙은 잿빛 눈은 반짝였다. 그는 무척 생각이 깊었고, 보아하니 무척 평온한 사람이었다. 어쩌면 누군가는 붉은 뺨이 광신이나 신비주의와 양립하지 못할 까닭은 없다고 내게 말할지도 모른다. 그러나 내가 보기에 알료샤는 누구보다도 더한 현실주의자였다. 오, 물론 수도원에서 그는 기적을 전적으로 믿었다. 그러나 내 생각으로는 기적이란 현실주의자에게 결코 걸림돌이 되지 못한다. 현실주의자를 믿음으로 이끄는 것은 기적이 아니다. 진정한 현실주의자란, 만일 그가 믿지 않는 자라면 언제나 기적적인 것을 믿지 않을 힘과 능력을 찾아낼 것이며, 또 기적이 반박할 수 없는 사실로 그 앞에 들이닥친다 해도 그는 그 사실을 인정하느니 차라리 제 감각을 믿지 않으려 들 것이다. 설령 그가 그것을 인정한다 해도, 그것을 그때껏 자신이 알지 못했던 자연의 한 사실로서 인정하는 것이다. 현실주의자에게 믿음은 기적에서 솟아나는 것이 아니라, 도리어 기적이 믿음에서 솟아난다. 현실주의자가 일단 믿게 되면, 그는 바로 그 현실주의 때문에 기적 또한 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 사도 토마스는 보기 전에는 믿지 않겠노라고 했으나, 막상 보고 나서는 \"나의 주, 나의 하느님!\" 하고 외쳤다. 그를 믿게 만든 것이 기적이었던가? 십중팔구 아니었으니, 그는 다만 믿고자 갈망했기에 믿었던 것이며, 어쩌면 \"보기 전에는 믿지 않겠다\"고 말하던 바로 그 순간에도 마음 깊은 곳에서는 이미 온전히 믿고 있었을 것이다.\n\n어쩌면 누군가는 내게 알료샤가 어리석고 미숙하며 학업도 마치지 못했다는 따위의 말을 할지도 모른다. 그가 학업을 마치지 못한 것은 사실이지만, 그를 어리석다거나 둔하다고 한다면 그것은 큰 부당함이 될 것이다. 나는 그저 위에서 한 말을 다시 되풀이하겠다. 그가 이 길로 들어선 것은, 오로지 그 무렵에 그것만이 그의 상상을 사로잡았고, 어둠에서 빛으로 그의 영혼이 벗어날 이상적인 탈출구로 그에게 비쳤기 때문이다. 거기에 덧붙이자면 그는 어느 정도 우리 지난 시대의 청년이었으니—곧 본성이 정직하고, 진리를 갈망하며, 그것을 찾고 또 믿으며, 자기 영혼의 온 힘을 다해 당장 그것에 헌신하고자 하고, 즉각적인 행동을 추구하며, 그것을 위해서라면 무엇이든, 목숨 그 자체까지도 바칠 채비가 된 그런 청년이었다. 비록 이런 젊은이들은 안타깝게도, 목숨을 바치는 일이 많은 경우 모든 희생 가운데 가장 손쉬운 것임을, 그리고 이를테면 자기들이 목표로 세운 진리와 대의를 섬길 힘을 열 배로 키우기 위해서라도 끓어오르는 청춘의 다섯 해나 여섯 해를 고되고 따분한 공부에 바치는 일—그런 희생이야말로 그들 가운데 많은 이의 힘으로는 도저히 감당할 수 없는 것임을 깨닫지 못하지만 말이다. 알료샤가 택한 길은 그와는 정반대 방향으로 난 길이었으되, 그는 그 길 또한 빠른 성취를 향한 똑같은 갈망으로 택했다. 진지하게 사색하기 시작하자마자 그는 신과 불멸의 존재를 확신했고, 곧장 본능적으로 자신에게 일렀다. \"나는 불멸을 위해 살고 싶다, 그리고 어떤 타협도 받아들이지 않겠다.\" 마찬가지로, 만일 그가 신과 불멸이 존재하지 않는다고 결론지었다면, 그는 곧장 무신론자가 되고 사회주의자가 되었을 것이다. 사회주의란 한낱 노동 문제가 아니라, 무엇보다도 먼저 무신론의 문제, 곧 오늘날 무신론이 띠게 된 형태의 문제이며, 신 없이 세워진 바벨탑의 문제, 곧 땅에서 하늘로 오르기 위함이 아니라 땅 위에 하늘을 세우려는 문제인 것이다. 알료샤로서는 예전처럼 살아간다는 것이 기이하고 불가능한 일로 여겨졌으리라. 이렇게 기록되어 있지 않은가. \"네가 온전하고자 하거든, 네게 있는 모든 것을 가난한 자들에게 주고 나를 따르라.\"\n\n알료샤는 자신에게 일렀다. \"나는 '모든 것' 대신 두 루블만 줄 수도, '그분을 따르는' 대신 미사에만 다닐 수도 없다.\" 어쩌면 어린 시절의 기억이 우리 수도원을 떠올리게 했는지도 모르니, 그의 어머니가 그를 그곳 미사에 데려갔을 수도 있는 것이다. 어쩌면 비스듬히 비쳐 들던 햇살과, 가엾고 \"정신 나간\" 어머니가 그를 높이 들어 올려 보이던 성상이 여전히 그의 상상에 작용하고 있었는지도 모른다. 이런 것들을 깊이 곱씹다가 그는 어쩌면 이곳에서 자신이 모든 것을 바칠 수 있을지, 아니면 그저 \"두 루블\"만 바칠 수 있을지 알아보려는 마음만으로 우리에게 왔는지도 모르며, 그리하여 수도원에서 이 장로를 만난 것이다. 여기서 나는 러시아 수도원에서 \"장로\"가 무엇인지 설명하기 위해 곁길로 빠지지 않을 수 없는데, 그 일에 그리 능하지 못함이 유감스럽다. 그래도 몇 마디로 겉핥기식 설명이나마 해 보고자 한다. 이 문제의 권위자들은 \"장로\" 제도가 근래에 생긴 것으로, 우리 수도원에서는 백 년이 채 안 되었다고 단언하나, 정교회의 동방, 특히 시나이와 아토스에서는 천 년이 넘도록 존속해 왔다고 한다. 그것이 러시아에서도 옛날에 존재했었다는 주장이 있으나, 러시아를 덮친 재앙들—타타르의 침략, 내전, 콘스탄티노플 함락 이후 동방과의 관계가 끊긴 일—로 말미암아 이 제도는 망각 속에 묻혀 버렸다고 한다. 그것이 우리 사이에서 되살아난 것은 지난 세기말, 사람들이 일컫던 대로 위대한 \"고행자\" 가운데 한 사람인 파이시 벨리치콥스키와 그 제자들에 의해서였다. 그러나 그것은 오늘날까지도 몇몇 수도원에만 존재하며, 러시아에서는 이따금 새로운 폐단이라 하여 거의 박해받다시피 한 일도 있었다. 그것은 특히 저 유명한 코젤스카야 옵틴 수도원에서 융성했다. 그것이 언제 어떻게 우리 수도원에 도입되었는지는 나도 알 수 없다. 이미 그런 장로가 셋이나 있었고, 조시마는 그 마지막 사람이었다. 그러나 그는 쇠약과 병으로 거의 죽어 가고 있었고, 그의 자리를 대신할 사람이 아무도 없었다. 이는 우리 수도원으로서는 중대한 문제였으니, 그때껏 이렇다 할 무엇으로도 두드러진 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그곳에는 성인의 유물도, 기적을 일으키는 성상도, 영광스러운 전통도, 역사적 위업도 없었다. 그곳이 온 러시아에 두루 융성하고 영광을 누린 것은 오로지 그 장로들 덕분이었으니, 그들을 보고 그 말씀을 듣고자 사방 천 리 길에서 순례자들이 떼 지어 몰려들었던 것이다.\n\n그런 장로란 무엇이었던가? 장로란 그대의 영혼과 그대의 의지를 자기 영혼과 자기 의지 속으로 받아들이는 사람이었다. 그대가 한 장로를 택할 때, 그대는 자신의 의지를 버리고 완전한 복종, 완전한 자기 부정으로써 그것을 그에게 내맡기는 것이다. 이 수련, 이 무서운 자기 부정의 학교는 자발적으로 받아들이는 것으로, 자기 정복과 자기 통제의 희망 속에서, 복종의 생애를 거친 뒤에 완전한 자유, 곧 자기 자신으로부터의 자유에 이르기 위함이며, 평생을 살면서도 끝내 자기 안에서 참된 자아를 찾아내지 못한 자들의 운명을 면하기 위함이다. 이 장로 제도는 이론에 근거를 둔 것이 아니라, 동방에서 천 년에 걸친 실천으로부터 확립된 것이다. 장로에 대한 의무는 우리 러시아 수도원에 늘 있어 온 보통의 \"복종\"과는 다르다. 그 의무에는 그에게 자신을 내맡긴 모든 이가 장로에게 고해를 바치는 일과, 그와 그들 사이의 끊을 수 없는 유대가 포함된다.\n\n이를테면 이런 이야기가 전해진다. 그리스도교 초창기에 한 수련 수사가 자기 장로가 내린 어떤 명령을 이행하지 않고서 시리아의 수도원을 떠나 이집트로 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 큰 위업을 쌓은 끝에 마침내 신앙을 위해 고문과 순교의 죽음을 당할 만한 자로 인정받았다. 교회가 그를 성인으로 여겨 장사 지내려 할 때, 부제가 \"세례받지 않은 자들은 모두 물러가라\" 하고 권고하는 순간 별안간 순교자의 시신이 든 관이 제자리를 떠나 교회 밖으로 내던져졌는데, 이런 일이 세 번이나 거듭되었다. 그리고 마침내 사람들은 이 거룩한 사람이 복종의 서약을 깨고 자기 장로를 떠났던 까닭에, 그 위대한 행적에도 불구하고 장로의 사면 없이는 용서받을 수 없음을 알게 되었다. 오직 그러고 난 뒤에야 장례를 치를 수 있었다. 물론 이것은 한낱 옛 전설에 지나지 않는다. 그러나 여기 근래의 한 실례가 있다.\n\n한 수사가 어느 날 갑자기 자기 장로로부터, 거룩한 곳이요 피난처로 사랑해 마지않던 아토스를 떠나라는 명령을 받았으니, 먼저 예루살렘으로 가서 성지에 경배하고, 그다음에는 북녘 시베리아로 가라는 것이었다. \"거기가 네가 있을 곳이지 이곳이 아니다.\" 슬픔에 짓눌린 그 수사는 콘스탄티노플로 세계 총대주교를 찾아가 복종의 의무에서 풀어 달라고 간청했다. 그러나 총대주교는 자기로서는 그를 풀어 줄 수 없을 뿐 아니라, 그 의무를 친히 그에게 지운 장로 외에는 그를 풀어 줄 수 있는 권능이 이 땅 위에 있지도 않거니와 있을 수도 없다고 대답했다. 이렇듯 장로들은 어떤 경우에는 한없고 헤아릴 수 없는 권위를 부여받고 있다. 바로 그런 까닭에 우리네 수많은 수도원에서 이 제도는 처음에 박해에 가까울 만큼 저항을 받았던 것이다. 그러는 사이 장로들은 곧 백성들 사이에서 크게 존경받기 시작했다. 이를테면 무지한 백성의 무리는 물론 명망 높은 인사들까지도 우리 수도원의 장로들에게로 떼 지어 몰려와 자기들의 의혹과 죄와 고뇌를 고백하고 충고와 훈계를 청했다. 이를 본 장로 제도의 반대자들은, 비록 수사든 속인이든 장로에게 끊임없이 마음을 여는 일이 성사(聖事)의 성격이라고는 조금도 없는 것이었음에도, 고해성사가 함부로, 또 경솔하게 격하되고 있다고 떠들어 댔다. 그러나 결국 장로 제도는 유지되었고 러시아 수도원들에서 차츰 자리를 잡아 가고 있다. 노예 상태에서 자유로, 그리고 도덕적 완성으로 사람을 도덕적으로 거듭나게 하는 데 천 년의 시련을 견뎌 낸 이 방편이 어쩌면 양날의 무기일 수 있고, 또 어떤 이들을 겸손과 완전한 자기 통제로가 아니라 가장 악마적인 교만으로, 곧 자유가 아니라 속박으로 이끌 수도 있다는 것은, 어쩌면 사실일 것이다.\n\n조시마 장로는 예순다섯이었다. 그는 지주 집안 출신으로, 젊은 시절에는 군에 몸담아 캅카스에서 장교로 복무했다. 그는 분명 어떤 독특한 영혼의 자질로써 알료샤에게 깊은 인상을 남겼을 것이다. 알료샤는 장로의 암자에서 지냈는데, 장로는 그를 무척 아껴 곁에서 시중들게 했다. 짚어 둘 것은, 알료샤가 어떤 의무에도 매여 있지 않아 마음 내키는 대로 어디든 갈 수 있었고 며칠씩 자리를 비울 수도 있었다는 점이다. 그가 수도복을 입긴 했으나 그것은 자발적인 것이었지, 남들과 달라 보이려는 것이 아니었다. 분명 그는 그렇게 하는 것을 좋아했다. 어쩌면 그의 젊은 상상력이 장로의 권능과 명성에 깊이 흔들렸는지도 모른다. 듣자 하니 지난 여러 해 동안 그토록 많은 사람이 조시마 신부에게 죄를 고백하고 조언과 치유의 말씀을 간구하러 왔던 까닭에, 그는 더없이 예리한 직관을 얻어, 낯선 얼굴만 보고도 새로 온 이가 무엇을 원하는지, 그 양심을 짓누르는 고뇌가 무엇인지를 알아맞힐 수 있었다고 한다. 그는 이따금 찾아온 이들이 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그들의 비밀을 알아챔으로써 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 하고 거의 두렵게까지 만들었다.\n\n알료샤가 보니 많은 사람이, 거의 모든 사람이 처음에 장로에게 들어갈 때는 두려움과 불안을 품고 들어갔으나, 나올 때는 밝고 행복한 얼굴이 되어 나왔다. 알료샤가 특히 놀란 것은 조시마 신부가 조금도 엄격하지 않다는 사실이었다. 그와는 반대로 그는 늘 거의 쾌활하다시피 했다. 수사들이 하는 말로는, 그는 죄가 더 많은 자들에게 더 마음이 끌렸고, 죄인일수록 더욱 사랑했다고 한다. 물론 그의 생애가 끝날 때까지도 수사들 가운데는 그를 미워하고 시기하는 이들이 더러 있었으나, 그 수는 적었고 또 그들은 침묵을 지켰는데, 그중에는 수도원에서 큰 위엄을 지닌 이들도 있었으니, 이를테면 단식과 침묵의 서약을 엄히 지키기로 이름난 한 늙은 수사가 그러했다. 그러나 대다수는 조시마 신부 편이었고, 그들 가운데 아주 많은 이가 따뜻하고 진실하게 온 마음으로 그를 사랑했다. 어떤 이들은 거의 광신에 가깝도록 그에게 헌신하여, 비록 큰 소리로는 아니었으나, 그가 성인이며 거기에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 단언했고, 그의 임종이 가까워 옴을 보고는 머지않은 장래에 그의 유물에서 기적이 일어나고 수도원에 큰 영광이 임하리라 미리 기대했다. 알료샤는 교회 밖으로 날아가 버린 관 이야기를 의심 없이 믿었던 것과 똑같이, 장로의 기적적인 권능을 의심 없이 믿었다. 그는 병든 아이나 친지를 데리고 와서 장로에게 그들 위에 손을 얹고 기도해 달라고 간청한 많은 이가, 얼마 뒤에—어떤 이들은 이튿날—다시 와서 눈물을 흘리며 장로의 발치에 엎드려 병자를 낫게 해 준 것에 감사하는 것을 보았다.\n\n그들이 정말로 나은 것인지, 아니면 그저 병이 자연히 차도를 보인 것인지 하는 물음은 알료샤에게는 아예 존재하지 않았으니, 그는 스승의 영적 권능을 온전히 믿었고 마치 제 자신의 승리인 양 스승의 명성과 영광을 기뻐했기 때문이다. 장로를 보고 그 축복을 받고자 일부러 러시아 방방곡곡에서 몰려든 비천한 신분의 순례자들의 무리 속으로 장로가 암자 문께로 나설 때면, 그의 심장은 두근거렸고 온몸이 환히 빛나는 듯했다. 그들은 그의 앞에 엎드려 울며 그의 발에 입 맞추고, 그가 서 있는 땅에 입 맞추며 통곡했고, 여인들은 아이들을 그에게 들어 올려 보이고 \"마귀 들린\" 병자들을 데려왔다. 장로는 그들에게 말을 건네고, 그들 위에 짧은 기도를 외워 주고, 축복한 뒤 돌려보냈다. 근래 들어 그는 병의 발작으로 어찌나 쇠약해졌던지 이따금 암자를 나서지조차 못했고, 순례자들은 그가 나오기를 며칠씩 기다리곤 했다. 알료샤는 그들이 어찌하여 그토록 그를 사랑하는지, 어찌하여 그저 그 얼굴을 보는 것만으로 그 앞에 엎드려 감격의 눈물을 흘리는지 의아해하지 않았다. 오! 그는 알고 있었으니, 슬픔과 노역에 지치고, 더욱이 자기 자신과 세상의 영원한 부정(不正)과 영원한 죄에 지친 러시아 농민의 겸손한 영혼에게는, 그 앞에 엎드려 경배할 거룩한 누군가 혹은 무엇인가를 찾는 것이야말로 가장 큰 갈망이요 위안이라는 것을.\n\n\"우리 가운데는 죄와 불의와 유혹이 있으나, 그래도 이 땅 어딘가에는 거룩하고 고결한 누군가가 있다. 그분께는 진리가 있고, 그분은 진리를 아시니, 그러므로 진리는 이 땅에서 죽지 않았으며, 그러므로 그것은 언젠가 우리에게도 임하여 약속하신 대로 온 땅을 다스리게 되리라.\"\n\n알료샤는 백성이 바로 이렇게 느끼고 또 그렇게 헤아리기까지 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 그것을 이해했으나, 조시마 장로야말로 바로 이 성인이요 하느님의 진리를 지키는 수호자라는 것—그 점에 대해서만큼은 울며 매달리는 농민들이나 아이를 장로에게 내미는 병든 여인들에 못지않게 그도 추호의 의심이 없었다. 장로가 죽은 뒤에 수도원에 비범한 영광을 가져오리라는 확신은 그곳의 누구보다도 알료샤에게서 더욱 강했으며, 근래 들어 일종의 깊은 내면적 황홀의 불길이 그의 가슴속에서 점점 더 세차게 타올랐다. 이 장로가 자기 앞에 홀로 우뚝 선 본보기로 서 있다는 사실은 그를 조금도 괴롭히지 않았다.\n\n\"상관없다. 그분은 거룩하시다. 그분은 만인을 위한 갱생의 비밀을 가슴에 품고 계시니, 곧 마침내 이 땅 위에 진리를 세울 그 권능이며, 그리하여 모든 사람이 거룩해지고 서로 사랑하며, 더는 부자도 가난한 자도 없고, 높은 자도 낮은 자도 없이, 모두가 하느님의 자녀처럼 되어 참된 그리스도의 나라가 임하리라.\" 그것이 알료샤의 가슴속 꿈이었다.\n\n그때껏 알지 못했던 두 형의 도착은 알료샤에게 큰 인상을 남긴 듯했다. 그는 친형 이반보다 이복형 드미트리와 (드미트리가 더 늦게 왔는데도) 더 빨리 가까워졌다. 그는 형 이반에게 지극한 관심을 품었으나, 이반이 읍에 온 지 두 달이 지나 제법 자주 만났는데도 두 사람은 여전히 가까워지지 못했다. 알료샤는 본디 말수가 적었고, 무언가를 기다리는 듯, 무언가를 부끄러워하는 듯 보였으며, 한편 형 이반은—알료샤가 처음에는 그가 자기를 오래도록 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보는 것을 알아챘으나—이내 그에 대한 생각을 거둬 버린 듯했다. 알료샤는 다소 당혹스러운 마음으로 그것을 알아챘다. 그는 처음에 형의 무관심을 두 사람의 나이와 교육의 차이 탓으로 돌렸다. 그러나 그는 또한 이반에게 호기심도 공감도 없는 것이 자신은 전혀 알지 못하는 어떤 다른 까닭에서 비롯된 것은 아닐까 의아해하기도 했다. 그는 이반이 무언가에—내면적이고 중대한 무언가에—골몰해 있고, 어쩌면 도달하기 몹시 어려운 어떤 목표를 향해 애쓰고 있어서, 바로 그 때문에 자기에게 마음을 둘 겨를이 없는 것이리라는 생각이 자꾸만 들었다. 알료샤는 또한 학식 있는 무신론자인 형이 자기 같은—어리석은 수련 수사를—얼마간 경멸하고 있는 것은 아닐까 의아해하기도 했다. 그는 형이 무신론자임을 확실히 알고 있었다. 그런 경멸이 설령 있다 한들 그는 그것에 기분 상할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 자기 자신도 이해하지 못하는 불안한 당혹감을 품은 채, 형이 자기에게 더 가까이 다가와 주기를 기다렸다. 드미트리는 이반을 입에 올릴 때면 더없이 깊은 존경과 유별난 진지함을 담곤 했다. 알료샤는 그에게서, 근래 두 형 사이에 그토록 긴밀하고 두드러진 유대를 맺어 준 중대한 사건의 자초지종을 낱낱이 들었다. 드미트리가 이반을 두고 열을 올려 가며 늘어놓는 말들은 알료샤의 눈에 더욱 인상적이었으니, 드미트리는 이반에 비하면 거의 못 배운 사람이나 다름없었고, 두 형은 인품과 성격에서 어찌나 대조적이었던지 이보다 더 닮지 않은 두 사람을 찾아내기란 어려울 정도였기 때문이다.\n\n알료샤에게 그토록 비상한 영향을 끼친 장로의 암자에서, 이 화목하지 못한 집안 식구들의 회동, 아니 차라리 집결이라 할 만한 모임이 이루어진 것은 바로 이 무렵이었다. 이 집결의 구실은 거짓된 것이었다. 드미트리와 그 아버지 사이의 불화가 가장 극심한 단계에 이른 듯했고 두 사람의 관계가 견디기 힘들 만큼 팽팽해진 것도 바로 이 무렵이었다. 다 함께 조시마 신부의 암자에 모이자고, 또 장로의 직접적인 중재를 청하지 않더라도 장로가 그 자리에 있다는 그 화해의 영향력 아래 좀 더 점잖게 서로 합의에 이를 수 있으리라고 처음 제안한 사람은—겉보기에는 농담조였으나—표도르 파블로비치였던 듯하다. 장로를 한 번도 본 적이 없던 드미트리는 당연히 아버지가 자기를 위협하려 드는 것이라고 여겼으나, 근래 여러 차례 아버지 앞에서 울컥 성을 낸 일을 두고 속으로 자신을 책망하고 있던 터라 그 도전을 받아들였다. 짚어 둘 것은, 그가 이반과는 달리 아버지와 함께 지내고 있던 것이 아니라 읍의 반대편 끝에 따로 떨어져 살고 있었다는 점이다. 마침 그 무렵 이 고장에 머물고 있던 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 그 생각에 덥석 달려들었다. 사오십 년대의 자유주의자요, 자유사상가이자 무신론자였던 그는, 권태에 이끌렸거나 아니면 시시한 심심풀이에 대한 기대에 이끌렸는지도 모른다. 그는 별안간 수도원과 그 거룩한 사람을 보고 싶은 욕구에 사로잡혔다. 수도원과의 소송이 여전히 질질 끌고 있었으므로, 그는 그것을 원만히 매듭짓고자 시도한다는 명분으로 원장을 만나려는 구실로 삼았다. 그처럼 가상한 의도를 품고 찾아온 방문객이라면 단순한 호기심으로 온 경우보다 더한 관심과 배려로 맞아질 만했다. 근래 들어 거의 암자를 떠나지 못하고 병 때문에 평소의 방문객들조차 사절해야 했던 장로에게, 수도원 안쪽에서 손길이 미쳐 들어왔다. 결국 그는 그들을 만나기로 응낙했고, 날짜가 정해졌다.\n\n\"누가 나를 그들의 재판관으로 세웠던가?\" 그는 알료샤에게 빙그레 웃으며 그렇게만 말했다.\n\n알료샤는 그 방문 제안에 대해 듣고서 몹시 심란해졌다. 이 옥신각신하는 다툼꾼 일행 가운데 그 만남을 진지하게 여길 수 있는 사람은 드미트리 하나뿐이었다. 나머지는 모두 시시한 동기에서, 어쩌면 장로에게 모욕이 될 만한 동기에서 올 터였다. 알료샤는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다. 이반과 미우소프는 호기심에서, 어쩌면 가장 천박한 종류의 호기심에서 올 것이고, 한편 그의 아버지는 어떤 익살극이라도 꾸미고 있을지 몰랐다. 비록 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나, 알료샤는 자기 아버지를 속속들이 꿰뚫고 있었다. 거듭 말하거니와, 이 소년은 모든 이가 여기던 것처럼 그렇게 단순한 사람이 결코 아니었다. 그는 무거운 마음으로 그날을 기다렸다. 분명 그는 어떻게 하면 이 집안의 불화를 끝낼 수 있을지 마음속으로 늘 골똘히 궁리하고 있었다. 그러나 그의 가장 큰 근심은 장로에 관한 것이었다. 그는 장로를 위해, 또 그 영광을 위해 마음을 졸였고, 그에게 어떤 모욕이 가해질까 두려워했으니, 특히 미우소프의 세련되고 정중한 비꼼과, 학식 깊은 이반의 거만한 어중간한 말투가 그러했다. 그는 장로에게 미리 경고를 해 그들에 관해 무언가 일러 두는 모험까지 해 볼까도 했으나, 다시 생각한 끝에 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 다만 전날 한 친구를 통해 형 드미트리에게, 자기는 형을 사랑하며 형이 약속을 지켜 주기를 기대한다는 전갈만을 보냈다. 드미트리는 자기가 무엇을 약속했는지 도무지 기억나지 않아 의아해했으나, 답장으로 자기는 \"비열한 짓\"에 도발당하지 않도록 온 힘을 다하겠노라고, 다만 장로와 형 이반을 깊이 존경하긴 하지만 이 모임이 자기에게는 함정이거나 아니면 천박한 익살극이라고 확신한다고 적어 보냈다.\n\n\"그렇지만 자네가 그토록 떠받드는 그 거룩한 분께 결례를 범하느니 차라리 내 혀를 깨물어 버리겠네.\" 그는 끝맺음으로 그렇게 적었다. 알료샤는 그 편지에 그리 크게 기운이 나지는 않았다.\n\n# Book II. An Unfortunate Gathering Chapter I. They Arrive At The Monastery\n\n따뜻하고 화창한 8월 말의 하루였다. 장로와의 면담은 늦은 미사가 끝난 직후인 열한 시 반으로 정해져 있었다. 우리의 방문객들은 예배에는 참례하지 않고 마침 그것이 끝날 무렵에 도착했다. 먼저 값비싼 말 두 필이 끄는 우아한 사륜 포장마차가 들이닥쳤는데, 거기엔 미우소프와 그의 먼 친척으로 스무 살 난 표트르 포미치 칼가노프라는 청년이 타고 있었다. 이 청년은 대학에 들어갈 준비를 하고 있었다. 그가 당분간 함께 지내고 있던 미우소프는 그에게 외국으로 나가 취리히나 예나 대학에 진학하라고 설득하는 중이었다. 청년은 아직 마음을 정하지 못한 채였다. 그는 생각이 깊으면서도 멍한 데가 있었다. 인물이 곱고 다부지게 생겼으며 키도 제법 컸다. 이따금 그 눈길에는 야릇한 응시가 어렸다. 몹시 멍한 사람들이 다 그렇듯 그는 때로 어떤 사람을 보고 있으면서도 실은 보지 못하고 있을 때가 있었다. 그는 말수가 적고 다소 어색했으나, 더러 누군가와 단둘이 있을 때면 수다스럽고 격정적으로 변해서, 별것 아닌 일에도, 아무 까닭 없이도 웃음을 터뜨리곤 했다. 그러나 그의 그런 활기는 나타날 때와 마찬가지로 순식간에 사라져 버렸다. 그는 늘 잘, 심지어 공들여 차려입었다. 이미 어느 정도 독립된 재산을 가지고 있었고, 앞으로 훨씬 더 많은 유산을 받을 전망이었다. 그는 알료샤의 친구였다.\n\n낡고 덜컹거리지만 널찍한 삯마차에, 늙고 불그스름한 잿빛 말 두 필을 매단 채, 미우소프의 마차보다 한참 뒤처져서 표도르 파블로비치가 아들 이반과 함께 왔다. 드미트리는 전날 저녁에 시각을 통보받았는데도 늦었다. 방문객들은 경내 바깥, 여관 앞에 마차를 두고 걸어서 수도원 정문으로 향했다. 표도르 파블로비치를 빼고는 일행 가운데 누구도 이 수도원을 본 적이 없었고, 미우소프로 말하자면 삼십 년 동안 교회 근처에도 가 본 일이 없을 듯했다. 그는 짐짓 태연한 척하면서도 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 교회와 부속 건물들을 빼면—그것들조차 변변찮을 만큼 평범했지만—수도원 내부에서 그의 흥미를 끄는 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다. 마지막 예배자들이 모자를 벗고 성호를 그으며 교회에서 나오고 있었다. 신분이 낮은 사람들 사이에 지체 높은 이들도 몇 끼어 있었으니—두세 명의 부인과 매우 늙은 장군이 그러했다. 그들은 모두 여관에 묵고 있었다. 우리의 방문객들은 곧 거지들에게 둘러싸였으나, 누구 하나 적선하는 이가 없었고 다만 젊은 칼가노프만이 지갑에서 십 코페이카짜리 한 닢을 꺼내, 까닭 모를 초조함과 당혹감에 휩싸여—신만이 그 이유를 아시리라!—한 노파에게 황급히 건네며 \"고루 나누세요\" 하고 말했다. 일행 가운데 누구도 그 일을 두고 한마디 하지 않았으니 그가 무안해할 까닭은 없었으나, 정작 그는 그 점을 알아차리고 더욱 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다.\n\n이상하게도 그들의 방문은 미리 알려져 기다려진 것 같지 않았고, 특별한 예우로 맞아들여지지도 않았다. 일행 가운데 하나는 최근에 천 루블을 헌금했으며, 또 한 사람은 매우 부유하고 교양 높은 지주로서, 송사의 판결이 어느 순간에든 어업권을 그의 손에 넘겨줄 수도 있는 까닭에 수도원 전체가 어떤 의미로는 그에게 매여 있는 처지였는데도 그러했다. 그런데도 직책 있는 인물 하나 그들을 맞으러 나오지 않았다.\n\n미우소프는 교회 둘레의 묘비들을 멍하니 바라보다가, 여기 묻힌 망자들이 이 \"거룩한 곳\"에 누울 권리를 얻으려 꽤나 비싼 값을 치렀을 거라고 한마디 하려다가 참았다. 그의 자유주의적 빈정거림은 어느새 분노에 가까운 것으로 빠르게 바뀌고 있었다.\n\n\"이 멍청한 곳에서 도대체 누구한테 물어봐야 한단 말인가? 알아내야지, 시간이 자꾸 가니까.\" 그가 혼잣말하듯 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n그때 별안간 대머리에 알랑거리는 작은 눈을 가진 중년 사내가 다가왔는데, 헐렁한 여름 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 그는 모자를 들어 올리며, 꿀처럼 끈적이는 혀짤배기소리로 자신을 툴라의 지주 막시모프라고 소개했다. 그는 곧장 우리 방문객들의 곤란한 처지에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"조시마 신부님은 수도원에서 떨어져, 사백 보쯤 가서 잡목 숲 너머 은수처에 계십니다.\"\n\n\"잡목 숲 너머라는 건 알아요.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 말했다. \"한데 길을 통 기억할 수가 없구려. 여기 와 본 지가 하도 오래라서.\"\n\n\"이쪽으로, 이 문으로, 잡목 숲을 곧장 가로질러서…… 잡목 숲을요. 저랑 같이 가시지요, 예? 제가 안내해 드리겠습니다. 저도 가야 하니까요…… 저도 가는 길이에요. 이쪽, 이쪽입니다.\"\n\n그들은 문을 나서 잡목 숲 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 예순 살의 막시모프는 걷는다기보다 차라리 뛰다시피 하며, 도무지 믿기 어려울 만큼 신경질적인 호기심으로 몸을 옆으로 돌려 그들 모두를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯했다.\n\n\"보시오, 우리는 우리 나름의 용무가 있어 장로를 뵈러 온 거요.\" 미우소프가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다. \"그분이 말하자면 우리에게 면담을 허락해 주신 터라, 길을 안내해 주신 데 대해서는 고맙긴 합니다만, 동행을 청할 수는 없는 노릇이오.\"\n\n\"저도 가 봤습니다. 벌써 다녀왔어요. 운 슈발리에 파르페(완벽한 기사)지요.\" 그러더니 막시모프가 허공에서 손가락을 딱 튕겼다.\n\n\"누가 기사라는 거요?\" 미우소프가 물었다.\n\n\"장로님이죠, 그 훌륭한 장로님, 그 장로님! 수도원의 명예이자 영광인 조시마 신부님 말입니다. 그런 장로님이 또 어디 있겠어요!\"\n\n그러나 그의 두서없는 수다는, 키가 중간쯤 되는 몹시 창백하고 수척한 수사가 수사 모자를 쓰고 그들을 따라잡으며 끼어드는 바람에 뚝 끊겼다. 표도르 파블로비치와 미우소프는 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n수사는 더없이 정중하게 허리를 깊이 숙여 절하며 알렸다.\n\n\"원장 신부님께서 여러분 모두를 은수처 방문이 끝난 뒤 함께 식사하자고 청하십니다. 한 시에, 늦지 않게요. 그리고 당신도요.\" 그가 막시모프를 향해 덧붙였다.\n\n\"암, 그러고말고요, 꼭 가지요.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 그 초대에 몹시 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"그리고 정말이지, 우리는 다들 여기서 예의 바르게 처신하기로 단단히 약속했답니다…… 그래, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 자네도 가겠지?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 물론이죠. 내가 여기 온 게 다 여기 풍습을 두루 살펴보려는 것 아니겠습니까? 단 하나 걸리는 것이라곤 당신과 동석하는 일이지만……\"\n\n\"그래, 드미트리 표도로비치는 아직 코빼기도 안 보이는군.\"\n\n\"그 친구가 안 나타나면 더할 나위 없이 좋겠소만. 내가 이 모든 일을, 그것도 당신네와 함께하는 걸 즐기는 줄 아시오? 아무튼 식사하러 가겠소. 원장 신부님께 고맙다고 전하시오.\" 그가 수사에게 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 이제는 제가 여러분을 장로님께로 모시는 게 제 소임입니다.\" 수사가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 저는 곧장 원장 신부님께로—원장 신부님께로 가겠습니다.\" 막시모프가 더듬더듬 지껄였다.\n\n\"원장 신부님은 지금 일이 있으십니다. 하지만 정 원하신다면야—\" 수사가 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"건방진 영감 같으니!\" 막시모프가 수도원 쪽으로 도로 뛰어가자 미우소프가 들으라는 듯 내뱉었다.\n\n\"폰 존을 빼닮았군.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"고작 생각해 낸 게 그거요?…… 대체 어디가 폰 존을 닮았다는 거요? 폰 존을 본 적이나 있소?\"\n\n\"초상화를 봤지. 이목구비가 아니라, 뭐랄까 딱 꼬집을 수 없는 무언가요. 저자는 제2의 폰 존이오. 나는 늘 인상만 보면 알아맞힌다니까.\"\n\n\"흥, 과연 그 방면엔 일가견이 있으시구려. 한데 이보시오, 표도르 파블로비치, 방금 우리가 예의 바르게 처신하기로 약속했다고 했잖소. 그 말 잊지 마시오. 당신더러 자제하라고 권하는 거요. 하지만 만일 광대짓을 시작하면 나는 여기서 당신과 한패로 엮이고 싶지 않소…… 보십시오, 이 사람이 어떤 위인인지를\"—그가 수사 쪽으로 돌아섰다—\"이런 자와 함께 점잖은 분들 사이에 끼는 게 영 두렵단 말입니다.\" 창백하고 핏기 없는 수사의 입술에 어떤 교활함이 어린 옅은 미소가 떠올랐으나, 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으니, 분명 제 위엄을 지키려는 마음에서 입을 다문 것이었다. 미우소프는 한층 더 이맛살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"오, 빌어먹을, 죄다 악마에게나 가 버려라! 몇 세기에 걸쳐 공들여 다듬은 겉치레일 뿐, 그 속이라곤 야바위와 허튼소리밖에 없잖은가.\" 이런 생각이 미우소프의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"여기가 은수처군. 도착했어.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다. \"문이 닫혀 있군.\"\n\n그러더니 그는 문 위와 양옆에 그려진 성인들을 향해 거듭 성호를 그었다.\n\n\"로마에 가면 로마법을 따라야지. 이 은수처에는 스물다섯 명의 성인이 구원받겠다고 들어앉아 있다지. 서로 얼굴만 마주 보면서 양배추나 먹는다는군. 그리고 이 문으로는 여자라곤 한 사람도 못 들어가지. 그게 바로 진기한 점이라네. 그리고 그게 정말 그렇다는군. 그런데 듣자 하니 장로는 부인네들을 맞아들인다던데?\" 그가 불쑥 수사에게 물었다.\n\n\"평민 여인들도 지금 여기 있습니다. 저기 회랑에 누워 기다리고 있지요. 한데 지체 높은 부인들을 위해서는 회랑에 잇닿아, 다만 경내 바깥에 방 두 칸을 지어 두었는데—창문이 저기 보이실 겁니다—장로님께서 몸이 괜찮으실 때면 안쪽 통로를 통해 그분들께 나가십니다. 그 방들은 늘 경내 바깥에 있지요. 지금도 하리코프에서 온 호흘라코바 부인이 병든 따님을 데리고 거기서 기다리고 있습니다. 아마 그분은 나가 보겠다고 약속하셨을 텐데, 요즘은 워낙 기력이 쇠하셔서 평민들에게도 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않으십니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 결국 은수처에서 슬그머니 빠져나가 부인네들에게 갈 구멍이 있긴 있군그래. 거룩한 신부님, 내가 무슨 나쁜 뜻으로 하는 말이라곤 생각지 마시오. 한데 혹시 아시오, 아토스에서는 여자의 방문이 금지된 것은 물론이거니와, 암컷이라곤 그 어떤 짐승도—암탉이건 암칠면조건 암소건—발 못 들이게 한다는 걸?\"\n\n\"표도르 파블로비치, 경고하는데 나는 돌아가서 당신을 여기 내버려 두겠소. 내가 가고 나면 사람들이 당신을 쫓아낼 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 당신을 방해하는 게 아니잖소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 보시오.\" 그가 별안간 경내로 발을 들여놓으며 외쳤다. \"이 사람들이 얼마나 장미 골짜기 같은 데서 사는지를!\"\n\n지금은 장미가 없었으나, 자리만 나면 어디고 진귀하고 아름다운 가을 꽃들이 자라고 있었고, 분명 솜씨 좋은 손길이 보살핀 흔적이 역력했다. 교회 둘레와 무덤들 사이에 화단이 꾸며져 있었고, 장로가 사는 단층 목조 가옥 또한 꽃들에 에워싸여 있었다.\n\n\"전 장로 바르소노피 시절에도 이랬소? 그분은 이런 우아한 것 따위엔 통 관심이 없었다던데. 듣자 하니 그분은 벌떡 일어나 부인네들까지도 지팡이로 두들겨 패곤 했다더군.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 계단을 올라가며 말했다.\n\n\"바르소노피 장로님께서 더러 좀 별나 보이실 때가 있긴 했지요. 하지만 떠도는 얘기 가운데는 어리석은 것이 태반입니다. 그분은 누구도 매질하신 적이 없습니다.\" 수사가 대답했다. \"자, 여러분, 잠시 기다려 주시면 제가 알려 드리고 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"표도르 파블로비치, 마지막으로 한 번 더, 그 약속 말이오, 알아들었소? 점잖게 처신하시오, 안 그러면 가만 안 둘 테니!\" 미우소프가 다시 한번 중얼거릴 짬이 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 왜 이렇게 안절부절못하는지 도무지 모르겠구려.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 빈정거리듯 말했다. \"당신 죄가 켕기는 거요? 사람들 말이, 그 양반은 눈만 보고도 무슨 일로 왔는지 알아맞힌다는데. 한데 당신은 그들의 견해를 어찌 그리 대단하게 여기시오! 파리 물 먹고 그토록 앞서간다는 당신이 말이오. 새삼 놀랍구려.\"\n\n그러나 미우소프는 이 빈정거림에 대꾸할 겨를이 없었다. 그들에게 들어오라는 청이 떨어졌던 것이다. 그는 다소 약이 오른 채로 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"이거 봐, 나는 내 속을 잘 알지, 지금 짜증이 나 있고, 분통을 터뜨려 말다툼을 벌이고는—나 자신과 내 신념까지 깎아내리고 말 거야.\" 그가 속으로 생각했다.\n\n# Chapter II. The Old Buffoon\n\n그들은 거의 장로가 침실에서 나오는 바로 그 순간에 방으로 들어섰다. 암자에는 이미 장로를 기다리는 두 수도사가 있었으니, 한 사람은 도서 담당 신부였고, 다른 한 사람은 파이시 신부였는데, 소문에 따르면 대단히 학식이 깊은 인물로서 늙지는 않았으나 몸이 허약하다고 했다. 그 밖에도 스물두 살쯤 되어 보이는 키 큰 젊은이가 있어, 면담이 진행되는 내내 한구석에 서 있었다. 그는 넓적하고 혈색 좋은 얼굴에, 영리하고 관찰력이 날카로운 가느다란 갈색 눈을 지녔으며, 평상복을 입고 있었다. 그는 수도원의 보호 아래 살아가는 신학생이었다. 그의 표정에는 의심을 모르되 자존심을 잃지 않은 경건함이 어려 있었다. 종속적이고 의탁하는 처지였던 까닭에, 손님들과 동등한 위치가 아니었으므로 그는 절을 하여 그들을 맞이하지 않았다.\n\n조시마 장로는 한 수련 수사와 알료샤를 데리고 들어왔다. 두 수도사는 일어서서 손가락으로 땅을 짚으며 매우 깊이 절을 올려 그를 맞이한 뒤, 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다. 장로는 그들을 축복하면서 그들에게도 그만큼 깊이 머리를 숙여 답례하고, 그들의 축복을 청했다. 이 모든 의식은 매우 엄숙하게, 그리고 진심 어린 모습으로 행해졌으니, 여느 날의 일상적인 예식 같지 않았다. 그러나 미우소프는 이 모든 것이 일부러 인상을 남기려는 연출이라고 여겼다. 그는 다른 방문객들 앞에 서 있었다. 그는—전날 저녁에 이미 곰곰이 생각해 둔 바였으나—이곳의 관습이 그러하니 단순한 예의로라도 다가가 장로의 축복을 받았어야 했을 것이니, 비록 그 손에 입을 맞추지는 않더라도 말이다. 그러나 수도사들이 절하고 입 맞추는 그 모든 광경을 보자 그는 곧장 마음을 바꾸었다. 위엄 있고 진지한 태도로 그는 제법 깊숙이, 형식적인 절을 한 다음 의자 쪽으로 물러났다. 표도르 파블로비치도 똑같이 했는데, 마치 원숭이처럼 미우소프를 흉내 냈다. 이반은 대단히 위엄 있고 정중하게 절을 했으나, 그 역시 두 손을 옆구리에 붙인 채였고, 칼가노프는 너무도 당황한 나머지 아예 절을 하지 못했다. 장로는 축복하려고 들어 올렸던 손을 떨구고는 다시 한번 그들에게 절을 한 뒤 모두에게 앉기를 청했다. 알료샤의 두 뺨으로 피가 솟구쳤다. 그는 수치스러웠다. 그의 불길한 예감이 그대로 들어맞고 있었다.\n\n조시마 장로는 가죽을 씌운 아주 고풍스러운 마호가니 소파에 앉아, 손님들에게는 맞은편 벽을 따라 한 줄로 놓인 마호가니 의자 넷에 나란히 앉도록 했는데, 그 의자에는 낡고 검은 가죽이 씌워져 있었다. 수도사들은 하나는 문가에, 다른 하나는 창가에 자리를 잡았다. 신학생과 수련 수사, 그리고 알료샤는 선 채로 있었다. 암자는 그다지 넓지 않았고 빛바랜 모습이었다. 그 안에는 거칠고 조악한 품질의, 가장 긴요한 가구 외에는 아무것도 없었다. 창가에는 화분이 둘 놓여 있었고, 한구석에는 성화가 여럿 걸려 있었다. 거대하고 오래된 성모상 하나 앞에는 등불이 타오르고 있었다. 그 곁에는 번쩍이는 틀에 끼운 다른 성화가 둘 있었고, 그 옆으로는 조각된 천사상들과 도자기 달걀, 상아로 만든 가톨릭 십자가가 있었는데 그 십자가에는 슬픔에 잠긴 성모가 끌어안고 있었으며, 또 지난 세기 위대한 이탈리아 화가들의 외국 판화 몇 점이 있었다. 이 값비싸고 예술적인 판화들 곁에는 모든 장터에서 몇 푼이면 살 수 있는, 성인과 순교자를 새긴 가장 조잡한 러시아 판화 몇 점이 나란히 걸려 있었다. 다른 벽에는 과거와 현재의 러시아 주교들 초상이 걸려 있었다.\n\n미우소프는 이 모든 \"판에 박힌\" 주변을 스치듯 한번 둘러본 뒤, 장로에게 골똘한 눈길을 던졌다. 그는 자신의 통찰력을 매우 높이 평가하고 있었는데, 그것은 그에게서는 용서받을 만한 약점이었으니, 그도 이미 쉰의 나이로, 세상 물정에 밝고 지위가 확고한 영민한 사내가 자기 자신을 제법 진지하게 여기지 않기란 그 나이에 이르러서는 좀처럼 어려운 일이기 때문이다. 첫 순간부터 그는 조시마가 마음에 들지 않았다. 사실 장로의 얼굴에는 미우소프 말고도 많은 이들이 마음에 들어 하지 않을 만한 무언가가 있었다. 그는 키가 작고 등이 굽은 자그마한 사람으로, 다리가 몹시 약했으며, 비록 예순다섯밖에 되지 않았으나 적어도 열 살은 더 늙어 보였다. 그의 얼굴은 매우 야위었고 가느다란 주름이 그물처럼 뒤덮고 있었는데, 특히 눈가에 주름이 유난히 많았으니, 그 눈은 작고 빛깔이 옅으며 재빠르고, 두 개의 반짝이는 점처럼 빛나고 있었다. 그의 관자놀이 언저리에는 흰머리가 듬성듬성 나 있었다. 뾰족한 턱수염은 작고 성겼으며, 자주 미소 짓는 그의 입술은 두 가닥 실처럼 얇았다. 그의 코는 길지는 않았으나 새의 부리처럼 날카로웠다.\n\n\"누가 봐도 악의에 찬 영혼, 자잘한 자만심으로 가득한 자로군.\" 미우소프는 생각했다. 그는 자신의 처지가 도무지 못마땅하게 느껴졌다.\n\n벽에 걸린 값싼 작은 시계가 서둘러 열두 시를 알렸고, 그것이 대화를 시작하는 계기가 되었다.\n\n\"정확히 시간 맞춰 왔습니다그려.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다. \"한데 제 아들 드미트리는 코빼기도 안 보이는군요. 그 녀석을 대신해 사죄드립니다, 거룩하신 장로님!\" (알료샤는 \"거룩하신 장로님\"이라는 말에 온몸이 부르르 떨렸다.) \"저로 말할 것 같으면 늘 시간을 정확히, 일 분도 어김없이 지키지요. 시간 엄수란 제왕의 예의라는 말을 명심하고 있기에…….\"\n\n\"하지만 댁은 어쨌든 제왕이 아니지 않소.\" 미우소프가 곧장 자제심을 잃고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"옳소, 그건 맞는 말입니다. 나는 제왕이 아니지요. 한데 믿어 주시겠소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 나도 그쯤은 스스로 알고 있었답니다. 한데 이것 보시오! 나는 늘 엉뚱한 소리만 한단 말입니다. 신부님,\" 그는 별안간 비장한 어조로 외쳤다. \"당신 앞에 진짜배기 어릿광대가 서 있는 걸 보고 계십니다! 나 스스로 그렇게 소개하는 바입니다. 오래된 버릇이지요, 아아! 그리고 때때로 자리에 어울리지 않게 허튼소리를 늘어놓는 것도 다 속셈이 있어서랍니다. 사람들을 즐겁게 하고 나 자신을 호감 가게 만들려는 속셈이지요. 사람이란 호감을 사야 하지 않겠소, 안 그렇습니까? 칠 년 전에 볼일이 있어 어느 작은 읍내에 갔다가 그곳 상인들 몇몇과 친해졌지요. 우리가 무슨 일로 경찰서장을 만날 일이 생겨서, 함께 식사나 하자고 청하러 그를 찾아갔습니다. 그는 키 크고 뚱뚱하며 머리가 희끄무레하고 시무룩한 사내였는데, 이런 경우에는 가장 위험한 부류지요. 다 간 때문이랍니다. 나는 곧장 그에게 다가가, 세상 물정에 밝은 사내답게 여유롭게, 아시겠소, 이렇게 말했지요. '이스프라브니크* 나리, 우리의 나프라브니크가 되어 주시구려.' 그러자 그가 묻습디다. '나프라브니크라니 그게 무슨 소리요?' 나는 첫 반 초 만에 농담이 빗나갔다는 걸 알아챘지요. 그가 그렇게 시무룩하게 서 있는 걸 보고 말입니다. 나는 말했지요. '농담을 좀 해 보려던 거올시다. 다 함께 흥겨우라고요. 나프라브니크 씨로 말할 것 같으면 우리 러시아의 유명한 오케스트라 지휘자이고, 우리 일이 화합을 이루려면 바로 그런 인물 같은 사람이 필요하지 않겠소.' 이만하면 제법 그럴듯하게 비유를 설명한 거 아닙니까? 한데 그가 이러더군요. '실례지만, 나는 이스프라브니크요. 내 직무를 두고 말장난하는 건 용납하지 못하겠소.' 그러고는 돌아서 가 버렸습니다. 나는 뒤따라가며 외쳤지요. '옳소, 옳소, 당신은 이스프라브니크요, 나프라브니크가 아니라.' 그러자 그가 이러더군요. '아니, 당신이 나를 나프라브니크라고 불렀으니 나는 나프라브니크요.' 그래, 믿어지십니까, 그 일로 우리 볼일이 다 망쳐졌단 말입니다! 나는 늘 이 모양이지요, 늘 이 모양이라니까요. 언제나 내 예의 때문에 스스로를 다치게 한단 말입니다. 한번은, 여러 해 전 일입니다만, 어느 세도가에게 이렇게 말했지요. '댁의 부인은 간지럼을 잘 타는 분이시군요.' 점잖은 뜻으로, 말하자면 도덕적 품성을 두고 한 말이었지요. 한데 그가 내게 묻더군요. '아니, 그럼 당신이 내 아내를 간질여 봤단 말이오?' 나는 예의를 차리겠답시고 '예' 하고 대답하지 않을 수 없었는데, 그가 그 자리에서 나를 톡톡히 간질여 주더이다. 다만 그건 오래전 일이니 부끄러움 없이 이야기할 수 있군요. 나는 늘 이런 식으로 스스로를 다치게 한단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"지금도 그러고 있지 않소.\" 미우소프가 역겹다는 듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n조시마 장로는 말없이 두 사람을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"내가요? 믿어지십니까, 그것도 나는 알고 있었답니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 게다가 말이지요, 사실 나는 입을 떼는 순간 이미 이렇게 되리란 걸 내다보고 있었지요. 그리고 당신이 제일 먼저 그 점을 지적하리란 것도 내다봤단 걸 아시오? 신부님, 내 농담이 먹히지 않는다 싶은 순간이면 양쪽 뺨이 마치 아래턱까지 끌려 내려가는 것 같고 거의 경련이 일 지경이지요. 젊을 적부터 그랬답니다, 귀족 집안에서 농담을 해 가며 밥벌이를 해야 했던 그 시절부터요. 나는 뼛속까지 어릿광대이고, 태어날 때부터 그랬답니다, 신부님. 마치 광기처럼 내 안에 박혀 있는 것 같지요. 아마 내 안에 마귀가 들어 있는 게지요. 다만 아주 자그마한 놈이올시다. 좀 더 거물급 마귀라면 다른 거처를 골랐을 테니까요. 하지만 당신 영혼은 아니올시다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 당신 역시 들어앉을 만한 거처는 못 되거든요. 그래도 나는 믿습니다—나는 하느님을 믿어요, 요즘 들어 의심이 들기는 했습니다만. 한데 이제 나는 여기 앉아 지혜로운 말씀을 기다리고 있지요. 나는 철학자 디드로 같은 사람이올시다, 신부님. 거룩하신 신부님, 혹시 들어 보신 적 있으신지요, 디드로가 예카테리나 여제 시절에 대주교 플라톤을 찾아간 이야기 말입니다. 그가 들어서서 대뜸 이렇게 말했답니다. '하느님은 없소.' 그러자 그 위대한 주교가 손가락을 치켜들며 답했지요. '어리석은 자는 그 마음에 하느님이 없다 하였도다.' 그러자 그가 그 자리에서 주교의 발치에 엎드렸답니다. '나는 믿습니다,' 그가 외쳤지요. '세례를 받겠습니다.' 그래서 그는 세례를 받았답니다. 다시코바 공작부인이 그의 대모였고, 포툠킨이 대부였지요.\"\n\n\"표도르 파블로비치, 이건 정말 못 참겠소! 댁이 거짓말을 하고 있고 그 어이없는 일화가 사실이 아니란 걸 댁도 알지 않소. 어째서 그렇게 광대 짓을 하는 거요?\" 미우소프가 떨리는 목소리로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"나는 평생 그게 사실이 아닐 거라고 의심해 왔습니다.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 확신에 찬 어조로 외쳤다. \"한데 여러분, 솔직히 다 털어놓겠습니다. 위대한 장로님! 용서해 주십시오, 디드로의 세례 운운한 마지막 대목은 방금 막 지어낸 겁니다. 전에는 생각해 본 적도 없는 얘기지요. 좀 더 톡 쏘는 맛을 더하려고 꾸며 댄 거올시다. 나는 호감을 사려고 광대 짓을 하는 거랍니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 그래도 솔직히 가끔은 나 스스로도 왜 그러는지 모르겠어요. 디드로로 말하자면, '어리석은 자는 그 마음에' 하는 대목까지는 젊을 적 이 고장 양반들에게서 스무 번도 더 들었지요. 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신 고모님이 그 이야기를 하는 것도 들었답니다. 그들은 다들 지금까지도 무신론자 디드로가 하느님을 두고 따지러 대주교 플라톤을 찾아갔다고 믿고 있지요…….\"\n\n미우소프는 조바심에 자제심을 잃고 벌떡 일어섰다. 그는 격분해 있었고, 자기가 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되었다는 것도 의식하고 있었다.\n\n암자 안에서 벌어지고 있는 일은 실로 믿기 어려운 광경이었다. 지난 사오십 년 동안, 이전 장로들의 시대로부터 이 암자에 들어선 방문객 가운데 가장 깊은 경외심을 품지 않은 자는 없었다. 이 암자에 들어오도록 허락받은 사람치고 자기가 큰 은혜를 입고 있다고 느끼지 않은 이가 거의 없었다. 적잖은 이들이 방문하는 내내 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 그 방문객들 가운데는 높은 지위와 학식을 갖춘 인사들도 많았고, 더러는 호기심에 끌려 찾아온 자유사상가도 있었으나, 하나같이 예외 없이 더없이 깊은 경외심과 섬세한 예의를 보였으니, 이곳에서는 돈 문제 따위는 있을 수 없었고 오직 한편에는 사랑과 친절이, 다른 한편에는 참회와, 어떤 영혼의 문제나 위기를 풀고자 하는 간절한 열망이 있을 뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러니 이런 광대 짓은 구경꾼들을, 적어도 그들 가운데 일부를 깜짝 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 만들었다. 수도사들은 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않은 채 진지하게 귀를 기울이며 장로가 무어라 말할지 기다리고 있었으나, 미우소프처럼 막 일어서려는 듯 보였다. 알료샤는 고개를 떨군 채 눈물을 쏟기 직전이었다. 그에게 무엇보다 이상하게 여겨진 것은, 그가 유일하게 희망을 걸었던 형 이반이, 그리고 아버지에게 그를 막을 만한 영향력을 지닌 유일한 사람이었던 이반이, 지금은 눈을 내리깐 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아, 마치 자기와는 아무 상관도 없다는 듯 이 일이 어떻게 끝날지 흥미롭게 지켜보며 기다리고 있는 것 같았다는 점이었다. 알료샤는 신학생 라키틴 쪽으로는 차마 눈길을 돌리지 못했는데, 그와는 거의 막역한 사이였다. 수도원에서 라키틴의 속생각을 아는 자는 그뿐이었다.\n\n\"용서하십시오.\" 미우소프가 조시마 장로를 향해 입을 열었다. \"어쩌면 제가 이 수치스러운 어릿광대 짓에 한몫 끼는 것처럼 보일지도 모르겠습니다만. 표도르 파블로비치 같은 사람이라도 이토록 존경받는 어른을 찾아뵐 때 갖추어야 할 도리쯤은 알 줄로 믿었던 것이 제 잘못이었습니다. 그저 이 사람과 함께 왔다는 것만으로 사죄를 드려야 할 줄은 미처 생각지 못했습니다…….\"\n\n표트르 알렉산드로비치는 더는 말을 잇지 못한 채, 당혹감에 짓눌려 방을 나서려 했다.\n\n\"부디 괴로워 마십시오.\" 장로가 후들거리는 다리로 일어서더니, 표트르 알렉산드로비치의 두 손을 잡아 다시 앉혔다. \"제발 마음을 어지럽히지 마십시오. 특히 당신께 부디 제 손님으로 남아 주시기를 청합니다.\" 그러고는 절을 한 뒤 돌아가 다시 자기의 작은 소파에 앉았다.\n\n\"위대한 장로님, 말씀해 주십시오! 제 활달함이 장로님을 성가시게 합니까?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 소리치며, 답이 신통치 않으면 당장이라도 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어오를 듯 두 손으로 의자 팔걸이를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"당신께도 간곡히 청하건대, 부디 마음을 어지럽히지 말고 불안해하지 마십시오.\" 장로가 무게 있게 말했다. \"괴로워 마십시오. 편안히 계십시오. 그리고 무엇보다도 스스로를 그토록 부끄러워하지 마십시오. 모든 일의 뿌리가 바로 거기에 있으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"편안히 있으라고요? 본디의 나 자신이 되라고요? 오, 그건 너무도 과분한 말씀입니다만, 고마운 기쁨으로 받아들이겠습니다. 한데 아시겠습니까, 복되신 신부님, 저더러 본디의 나 자신이 되라고는 권하지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 그건 무릅쓰지 마십시오……. 저 스스로도 거기까지는 가지 않을 작정이니까요. 당신을 위해서 미리 경고해 두는 겁니다. 자, 나머지 일은 아직도 불확실의 안개 속에 잠겨 있습니다만, 저에 관해 기꺼이 묘사해 드리려는 사람들도 있긴 하지요. 그건 당신더러 들으라고 하는 말입니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 한데 거룩하신 분이여, 당신께는 말씀드리지요, 저는 환희로 넘쳐흐르고 있습니다.\"\n\n그는 일어서서 두 손을 치켜들고 낭송하듯 외쳤다. \"당신을 밴 태와 당신을 먹인 젖가슴이 복되도다—특히 그 젖가슴이 말입니다. 방금 전 당신이 '스스로를 그토록 부끄러워하지 마라, 모든 일의 뿌리가 거기에 있으니' 하고 말씀하셨을 때, 그 한마디가 저를 꿰뚫고 들어와 제 속을 속속들이 읽어 내셨습니다. 정말이지 저는 사람들을 만날 때마다 제가 누구보다 못난 자라고, 그리고 그들 모두가 저를 어릿광대로 여긴다고 느끼지요. 그래서 저는 이렇게 말합니다. '그래, 진짜 광대 노릇을 해 주겠다. 너희들 평판 따위 두렵지 않다. 너희들 하나하나가 다 나보다 더 못된 자들이니까.' 그래서 제가 어릿광대인 겁니다. 다 수치심에서 비롯된 거지요, 위대한 장로님, 수치심에서요. 그저 지나친 예민함 탓에 제가 이렇게 소란을 떠는 거랍니다. 모두가 저를 가장 친절하고 가장 지혜로운 사람으로 받아들여 주리란 확신만 있었더라면, 오, 주님, 그때 저는 얼마나 훌륭한 사람이 되었겠습니까! 스승님!\" 그는 별안간 무릎을 꿇었다. \"영생을 얻으려면 저는 무엇을 해야 합니까?\"\n\n지금도 그가 농담을 하는 것인지 정말로 감동한 것인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다.\n\n조시마 장로는 눈을 들어 그를 바라보더니, 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"무엇을 해야 할지는 당신도 이미 오래전부터 알고 있습니다. 당신에게는 분별력이 충분히 있으니까요. 폭음에 빠지지 말고, 함부로 입을 놀리지 마십시오. 육체의 정욕에 빠지지 말고, 무엇보다도 돈을 사랑하는 데 빠지지 마십시오. 그리고 당신의 술집들을 닫으십시오. 다 닫을 수 없다면 적어도 두셋이라도 닫으십시오. 그리고 무엇보다도—거짓말을 하지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"디드로 얘기 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 디드로 얘기가 아닙니다. 무엇보다도 자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하지 마십시오. 자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하고 제가 한 거짓말에 귀 기울이는 자는, 마침내 제 안에서도 제 주위에서도 진실을 분간하지 못하는 지경에 이르고, 그리하여 자기 자신과 남들에 대한 존경을 모두 잃고 맙니다. 그리고 존경할 줄 모르게 되면 사랑하기를 그치고, 사랑 없이 제 마음을 채우고 달래려고 온갖 정념과 거친 쾌락에 몸을 맡기다가, 끝내 그 악덕 속에서 짐승의 수준으로까지 가라앉고 마는데, 이 모두가 남들에게도 자기 자신에게도 끊임없이 거짓말을 한 데서 비롯되는 겁니다. 자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하는 자는 누구보다도 쉽게 모욕을 느낄 수 있지요. 더러는 모욕을 당하는 일이 참으로 기분 좋은 법이라는 걸 아시지요, 안 그렇습니까? 어떤 사람은 아무도 자기를 모욕하지 않았다는 걸, 자기가 그 모욕을 스스로 지어냈다는 걸, 그럴듯하게 보이려고 거짓말로 부풀렸다는 걸, 말 한마디를 붙잡아 도토리만 한 일을 태산처럼 키웠다는 걸—그 자신도 다 알면서, 그러면서도 누구보다 먼저 모욕을 느끼고, 제 원한 속에서 마냥 흐뭇해하다가 마침내 거기서 큰 쾌감을 느끼는 지경에 이르러, 그렇게 진짜 앙심으로 옮겨 가는 겁니다. 한데 일어나십시오, 앉으세요, 제발. 이것도 다 거짓된 몸짓이올시다…….\"\n\n\"복되신 분이여! 입 맞추도록 손을 내어 주십시오.\"\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 펄쩍 일어나, 장로의 가느다란 손에 잽싸게 입을 맞추었다. \"그렇습니다, 모욕을 당하는 일은 기분 좋은 거지요. 그 말씀을 어찌나 잘하시는지, 그런 말은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없습니다. 그렇습니다, 저는 평생토록 모욕을 당해 왔지요, 제 즐거움을 위해, 심미적인 이유로 모욕을 당해 왔습니다. 모욕을 당하는 일이란 기분 좋다기보다 때로는 품위 있는 일이거든요—그건 잊으셨군요, 위대한 장로님, 품위 있는 일이란 말입니다! 그건 메모해 두겠습니다. 한데 저는 거짓말을 해 왔습니다, 평생토록 줄곧 거짓말을 해 왔지요, 하루도, 한 시간도 빠짐없이요. 진실로 저는 거짓말 그 자체요, 거짓의 아비올시다. 하기야 제가 거짓의 아비는 아닌 것 같습니다만. 제가 성경 구절을 자꾸 헷갈리는군요. 거짓의 아들이라고 해 두지요, 그러면 충분할 겁니다. 다만…… 나의 천사여…… 저는 가끔 디드로 얘기쯤은 할 수도 있는 거지요! 디드로는 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 겁니다, 가끔은 말 한마디가 해를 끼치기도 합니다만. 위대한 장로님, 그건 그렇고, 깜빡 잊을 뻔했군요. 지난 이 년 동안 일부러 여기 찾아와 묻고 알아보려고 작정했던 일이 있습니다. 다만 표트르 알렉산드로비치더러 제 말을 막지 말라고 일러 주십시오. 제 질문은 이렇습니다. 위대한 신부님, 정말로 어딘가 성인전에 이런 이야기가 전해진다는 게 사실입니까. 신앙 때문에 순교한 어느 거룩한 성인이 마침내 목이 잘리자 일어서서 제 머리를 주워 들고, '정중히 입을 맞추며' 그것을 두 손에 받쳐 든 채 먼 길을 걸어갔다는 이야기 말입니다. 그게 사실입니까, 아닙니까, 존경하는 신부님?\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 사실이 아니오.\" 장로가 말했다.\n\n\"성인전 어디에도 그런 이야기는 없소. 그 이야기가 어느 성인에 관한 것이라고 하셨소?\" 도서 담당 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 성인인지는 모르오. 모릅니다, 말씀드릴 수가 없군요. 제가 속은 겁니다. 누가 그 이야기를 해 주더이다. 그걸 들었지요, 한데 누가 해 주었는지 아십니까? 바로 여기 계신 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프올시다, 방금 디드로 얘기로 그토록 화를 내시던 그분 말입니다. 그 이야기를 해 준 사람이 바로 저분이었지요.\"\n\n\"나는 당신에게 그런 이야기를 한 적이 없소, 당신과는 도통 말을 섞지도 않으니까.\"\n\n\"당신이 내게 직접 말한 건 아니지요, 하지만 내가 그 자리에 있을 때 그 이야기를 했잖소. 삼 년 전 일이올시다. 내가 이 얘길 꺼낸 건, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 그 어이없는 이야기로 당신이 내 신앙을 뒤흔들어 놓았기 때문이오. 당신은 까맣게 몰랐겠지만, 나는 신앙이 흔들린 채로 집에 돌아갔고, 그 뒤로 점점 더 흔들려 왔단 말입니다. 그렇소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신이 바로 큰 타락의 원인이었소. 그건 디드로 따위와는 차원이 다른 일이었단 말이오!\"\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 흥분하여 비장한 어조가 되었으나, 이제는 누구의 눈에도 그가 또다시 연극을 하고 있다는 것이 빤히 드러나 있었다. 그런데도 미우소프는 그의 말에 따끔하게 찔렸다.\n\n\"무슨 헛소리요, 이건 죄다 헛소리란 말이오.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"내가 정말로 언젠가 그 얘기를 했을 수는 있지요…… 하지만 당신에게 한 건 아니오. 나도 누군가에게서 들은 얘기란 말입니다. 파리에서 어느 프랑스인에게 들었지요. 그자는 그게 우리 미사 때 성인전에서 낭독되는 이야기라고 하더군요…… 그는 러시아 통계를 특별히 연구한 매우 학식 깊은 사람으로, 러시아에서 오래 살았던 인물이었소…… 나 자신은 성인전을 읽어 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 읽을 생각도 없소…… 식사 자리에서야 온갖 얘기가 다 나오는 법 아니오—그때 우리는 식사를 하던 중이었으니까.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 당신은 그때 식사를 하고 있었고, 그 바람에 나는 신앙을 잃었지요!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 그를 흉내 내며 말했다.\n\n\"당신 신앙 따위가 나와 무슨 상관이란 말이오?\" 미우소프는 막 소리를 지르려다가 별안간 자제하고는, 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"당신은 손대는 것마다 죄다 더럽혀 놓는군.\"\n\n장로가 별안간 자리에서 일어섰다. \"여러분, 몇 분간 자리를 비우는 것을 양해해 주십시오.\" 그가 모든 손님을 향해 말했다. \"여러분보다 먼저 도착해 기다리고 있는 방문객들이 있습니다. 그래도 당신은 거짓말을 하지 마십시오.\" 그가 너그러운 낯빛으로 표도르 파블로비치를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. 그는 암자를 나섰다. 알료샤와 수련 수사가 그를 모시고 계단을 내려가려 날듯이 따라나섰다. 알료샤는 숨이 가빴으니, 그 자리에서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤고, 또 장로가 너그러운 기분으로 노여워하지 않은 것도 기뻤다. 조시마 장로는 거기서 자기를 기다리는 사람들을 축복하려고 현관 쪽으로 가고 있었다. 그러나 표도르 파블로비치는 기어이 암자 문 앞에서 그를 붙들어 세웠다.\n\n\"복되신 분이여!\" 그가 감격에 차서 외쳤다. \"한 번만 더 손에 입 맞추게 해 주십시오. 그렇습니다, 당신과는 아직도 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠습니다, 아직도 잘 지낼 수 있겠어요. 제가 늘 이렇게 거짓말이나 하고 광대 짓이나 하는 줄 아십니까? 믿어 주십시오, 줄곧 일부러 이렇게 굴어 본 건 당신을 시험해 보려는 거였습니다. 당신과 잘 지낼 수 있을지 알아보려고 줄곧 떠본 거지요. 당신의 그 자긍심 곁에 제 겸손이 들어설 자리가 있을까요? 당신은 더불어 지낼 만한 분이라고 기꺼이 보증서라도 써 드리지요! 한데 이제는 잠자코 있겠습니다. 줄곧 입을 다물고 있겠어요. 의자에 앉아 혀를 묶어 두지요. 이제 말씀하실 차례는 당신이올시다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 이제 여기 남은 주인공은 당신이니까요—앞으로 십 분 동안 말입니다.\"\n* 이스프라브니크(исправник): 제정 러시아의 군(郡) 경찰서장.\n\n# Chapter III. Peasant Women Who Have Faith\n\n아래쪽, 경내의 바깥담에 잇대어 세운 나무 현관 곁에는 농사꾼 아낙네가 스무 명쯤 무리지어 모여 있었다. 장로가 마침내 나오신다는 말을 전해 듣고 다들 그 기대 속에 한데 모여든 것이었다. 호흘라코바 부인과 그 딸, 두 귀부인도 장로를 기다리려고 현관으로 나와 있었으나, 신분 있는 여인들을 위해 따로 마련된 자리에 떨어져 있었다.\n\n호흘라코바 부인은 부유한 부인으로, 아직 젊고 매력이 있었으며, 늘 취향 있게 차려입었다. 얼굴빛은 다소 창백했고, 검고 생기 넘치는 눈을 지녔다. 나이는 서른셋이 넘지 않았고, 과부가 된 지는 다섯 해가 되었다. 그 딸은 열네 살 난 소녀로, 몸 한쪽이 마비되어 있었다. 가엾은 아이는 지난 반년 동안 걷지 못해, 길게 누울 수 있는 의자에 실려 이리저리 옮겨 다녔다. 병으로 다소 야위기는 했으나 그 얼굴은 사랑스럽고 명랑함이 가득했다. 긴 속눈썹이 드리운 크고 검은 눈에는 장난기가 어른거렸다. 어머니는 봄부터 줄곧 딸을 데리고 외국으로 가려고 마음먹고 있었으나, 영지와 관련된 일 때문에 여름 내내 발이 묶여 있었다. 두 사람은 우리 고을에 일주일째 머무는 중이었는데, 신앙보다는 사무를 보러 온 것이었으나 사흘 전에 이미 한 차례 조시마 신부를 찾아뵌 바 있었다. 장로가 거의 아무도 만나지 않는다는 것을 알면서도, 두 사람은 이제 다시 불쑥 나타나 \"그 위대한 치유자를 다시 한번 뵙는 기쁨\"을 간절히 청했던 것이다.\n\n어머니는 딸의 병자용 수레 곁에 놓인 의자에 앉아 있었고, 그녀로부터 두어 걸음 떨어진 곳에 한 늙은 수도사가 서 있었는데, 우리 수도원의 사람이 아니라 머나먼 북방의 어느 이름 없는 수도원에서 온 객승이었다. 그 역시 장로의 축복을 구하러 온 것이었다.\n\n그러나 조시마 신부는 현관에 들어서자 먼저 곧장, 현관으로 오르는 세 계단 발치에 빽빽이 모여든 농사꾼들에게로 다가갔다. 조시마 신부는 맨 윗계단에 서서 영대를 두르고는, 자기 둘레로 몰려든 아낙네들을 축복하기 시작했다. 미친 여자 하나가 그의 앞으로 인도되어 왔다. 장로를 보자마자 여자는 마치 산고에 시달리기라도 하는 듯 비명을 지르며 몸을 비틀어 댔다. 그가 영대를 그 이마에 얹고 짧은 기도를 읽어 주자, 여자는 이내 가라앉아 잠잠해졌다.\n\n지금은 어떤지 모르겠으나, 내 어린 시절에는 시골 마을과 수도원에서 이런 \"들린\" 여자들을 보고 듣는 일이 흔했다. 그들은 미사에 끌려 나오곤 했는데, 교회 안이 떠나가도록 개처럼 깩깩거리고 짖어 댔다. 그러나 성체가 옮겨져 들어와 그들이 그 앞으로 인도되면, 그 \"들림\"은 즉시 멎었고, 병든 여자들은 언제나 한동안 가라앉았다. 어린 나는 이 광경에 크게 놀라고 감명을 받았다. 그러나 그 뒤로 시골 이웃들과 고을의 선생들에게서, 그 병은 모두 일을 피하려고 꾸며 낸 것이며 적당히 호되게 다루면 언제든 고칠 수 있다는 말을 들었고, 이를 뒷받침하는 갖가지 일화도 들려주었다. 그러나 훗날 나는 의학 전문가들에게서 놀랍게도 거기엔 거짓이라곤 없으며, 그것은 여자들이 걸리는 무서운 병으로 특히 우리 러시아에서 유난히 흔하며, 농사꾼 아낙네들의 고달픈 처지에서 비롯된다는 것을 배웠다. 그것은 산고라는, 힘겹고 비정상적이며 아무 도움도 받지 못한 출산을 치른 뒤 너무 일찍 기진맥진하도록 노역에 시달린 데서, 또 가망 없는 비참함에서, 매질 따위에서 생겨나는 병으로, 어떤 여자들은 다른 이들처럼 그것을 견뎌 내지 못한다는 것이었다. 발광하며 몸부림치던 여자가 거룩한 성체 앞으로 인도되자마자 기이하게도 단번에 낫는 그 현상을, 나는 꾀병이며 \"성직자들\"의 속임수 탓이라고 들어 왔으나, 실은 십중팔구 지극히 자연스러운 방식으로 일어난 것이었다. 그녀를 부축한 여자들이나 병든 여자 자신이나, 병든 여자를 성체 앞으로 데려가 그 앞에 엎드리게 하면 그녀에게 들린 악령이 버티지 못한다는 것을 의심할 여지 없는 진리로 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 신경이 곤두서고 정신이 어지러운 여자에게는, 성체 앞에 엎드리는 순간 온몸에 일종의 경련이 언제나 일어났고 또 일어날 수밖에 없었으니, 그것은 치유의 기적에 대한 기대와 그것이 반드시 이루어지리라는 무조건적인 믿음에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그리고 그것은 실제로 이루어졌다, 비록 한순간뿐일지언정. 지금도 장로가 영대로 병든 여자를 건드리자마자 똑같은 일이 벌어진 것이었다.\n\n무리 가운데 많은 여자들이 그 순간의 효험에 황홀한 눈물을 쏟았다. 어떤 이는 그의 옷자락에 입을 맞추려 애썼고, 어떤 이는 가락을 넣어 울부짖었다.\n\n그는 그들 모두를 축복하고 몇몇과는 이야기를 나누었다. 그 \"들린\" 여자는 이미 그가 아는 사람이었다. 수도원에서 겨우 육 베르스타 떨어진 마을에서 온 여자로, 전에도 그에게 인도되어 온 적이 있었다.\n\n\"그러나 여기 멀리서 온 사람이 있구나.\" 그가 결코 늙지는 않았으나 몹시 야위고 수척한 한 여자를 가리켰는데, 그녀의 얼굴은 햇볕에 그을린 정도가 아니라 풍상에 거의 새카맣게 탄 빛이었다. 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 장로를 뚫어지게 응시하고 있었으니, 그 눈에는 거의 광기 어린 무언가가 서려 있었다.\n\n\"멀리서, 신부님, 멀리서요! 여기서 이백 베르스타나 되는 데서요. 멀리서, 신부님, 멀리서 왔어요!\" 여자는 마치 만가라도 읊조리듯 가락을 넣어 운을 떼며, 한쪽 뺨을 손에 괸 채 머리를 좌우로 흔들었다.\n\n농사꾼들 사이에서는 말없이 오래 견디는 슬픔을 마주칠 수 있다. 그것은 제 안으로 움츠러들어 잠잠하다. 그러나 터져 나오는 비탄도 있어, 그 순간부터 울음이 복받쳐 통곡으로 발산된다. 이는 특히 여자들에게 흔한 일이다. 그러나 그것이 말없는 슬픔보다 가벼운 것은 결코 아니다. 통곡은 마음을 더욱 갈가리 찢음으로써만 위안을 준다. 그런 슬픔은 위안을 바라지 않는다. 그것은 제 가망 없음을 양식으로 삼아 자란다. 통곡은 오로지 그 상처를 다시 헤집고픈 끊임없는 갈망에서 솟아나는 것이다.\n\n\"그대는 상인 계층 사람인가?\" 조시마 신부가 그녀를 신기한 듯 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"읍내 사람입니다, 신부님, 읍내 사람이지요. 그래도 읍에 살지만 농사꾼이랍니다. 저는 신부님을 뵈러 왔어요, 오, 신부님! 신부님에 대한 소문을 들었어요, 신부님, 들었지요. 어린 자식을 묻고서 순례 길에 나섰답니다. 수도원을 세 군데나 다녀 봤는데, 그분들이 그러시더군요, '가라, 나스타시야, 그분들께로 가라'고요—바로 신부님께로요. 그래서 왔지요. 어제는 예배에 참석했고, 오늘은 신부님을 뵈러 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 울고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"어린 자식 때문에 슬퍼하는 거예요, 신부님. 세 살이었답니다—석 달만 모자란 세 살이었지요. 제 어린 사내아이 때문에, 신부님, 가슴이 미어집니다, 제 어린 사내아이 때문에요. 마지막 남은 아이였어요. 저와 제 남편 니키타 사이에 넷이 있었는데, 이제 자식이라곤 하나도 없답니다, 우리 귀한 것들이 모두 가 버렸어요. 처음 셋은 그리 깊이 슬퍼하지 않고 묻었는데, 이제 마지막 아이를 묻고 나니 그 애를 잊을 수가 없어요. 늘 제 앞에 서 있는 것만 같아요. 한시도 제게서 떠나질 않아요. 그 애가 제 가슴을 다 말려 버렸어요. 그 애의 자그마한 옷가지를, 자그마한 셔츠를, 자그마한 신발을 들여다보면 통곡이 나옵니다. 그 애가 남긴 것을, 그 애의 자그마한 물건들을 죄다 펼쳐 놓아요. 그것들을 들여다보면 통곡이 나오지요. 제 남편 니키타한테 말했어요, '여보, 순례 길을 떠나게 해 줘요'라고요. 그이는 마부랍니다. 저희는 가난한 사람들이 아니에요, 신부님, 가난하지 않아요. 그이는 우리 말을 부린답니다. 다 우리 것이에요, 말도 마차도요. 그런데 이제 와서 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요? 제가 없는 동안 우리 니키타는 술을 마시기 시작했답니다. 틀림없이 그럴 거예요. 전에도 그랬으니까요. 제가 등을 돌리기만 하면 그이는 술에 빠지지요. 하지만 이제는 그이 생각을 안 해요. 집을 떠난 지 석 달이 됐어요. 그이를 잊었답니다. 모든 걸 잊었어요. 기억하고 싶지 않아요. 이제 와서 우리가 함께 산들 그게 무슨 삶이겠어요? 그이와는 끝났어요, 끝났지요. 다 끝났어요. 우리 집도 우리 살림도 보고 싶지 않아요. 아무것도 보고 싶지 않아요!\"\n\n\"들어 보거라, 어미여.\" 장로가 말했다. \"옛적 한 거룩한 성인이 성전에서, 그대처럼 하느님이 데려가신 자기 어린것, 하나뿐인 자식을 위해 우는 어미를 보았느니라. 성인이 그 어미에게 말했지. '그대는 모르는가, 이 어린것들이 하느님의 보좌 앞에서 얼마나 담대한지를? 진실로 천국에서 그들보다 더 담대한 자는 없느니라. 그들은 말한다네, \"주여, 당신께서 우리에게 생명을 주셨으나, 우리가 미처 그것을 바라보기도 전에 도로 거두어 가셨나이다\"라고. 그리하여 그들은 어찌나 담대하게 묻고 또 묻는지, 하느님은 그들에게 곧바로 천사의 지위를 내려 주시느니라. 그러니' 성인이 말했지, '그대 또한, 오 어미여, 기뻐하고 울지 말라, 그대의 어린 자식은 천사들의 무리 가운데서 주님과 함께 있느니라.' 옛적에 그 성인이 우는 어미에게 한 말이 이러하였느니라. 그는 위대한 성인이었으니 거짓을 말했을 리 없다. 그러니 그대 또한, 어미여, 그대의 어린것이 분명히 하느님의 보좌 앞에서 기뻐하며 행복하게 그대를 위해 하느님께 기도하고 있음을 알지니, 그러므로 울지 말고 기뻐하라.\"\n\n여자는 한쪽 뺨을 손에 괸 채 눈을 내리뜨고 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 그녀는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"우리 니키타도 신부님과 똑같은 말로 저를 달래려 했어요. '어리석은 사람아,' 그이가 그러더군요. '왜 우는 거야? 우리 아들은 틀림없이 하느님 앞에서 천사들과 함께 노래하고 있을 거야.' 그이가 제게 그렇게 말하면서도 정작 자기도 우는 거예요. 그이도 저처럼 우는 게 보였어요. '알아요, 니키타,' 제가 그랬지요. '하느님 곁이 아니면 그 애가 어디 있겠어요? 다만 지금 여기 우리 곁에, 전처럼 우리 옆에 앉아 있지 못할 뿐이지요.' 단 한 번만이라도 그 애를 바라볼 수 있다면, 단 한 번만이라도 그 애를 살짝 엿볼 수 있다면, 다가가지 않고, 말도 걸지 않고, 구석에 숨어서 단 일 분만이라도 그 애를 볼 수 있다면, 그 애가 마당에서 노는 소리를, 그 자그마한 목소리로 '엄마, 어디 있어?' 하고 부르는 소리를 들을 수 있다면. 그 애가 자그마한 발로 방 안을 종종거리며 돌아다니는 소리를 단 한 번만, 딱 한 번만 들을 수 있다면. 그 애가 어찌나 자주, 어찌나 자주 제게 달려와 소리치며 웃어 댔는지 떠오르거든요. 그 자그마한 발소리만 들을 수 있다면 그게 그 앤 줄 알 텐데요! 하지만 그 애는 가 버렸어요, 신부님, 가 버렸어요, 다시는 그 애 소리를 듣지 못할 거예요. 여기 그 애의 자그마한 띠가 있는데, 그 애만은 이제 다시는 보지도 듣지도 못할 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 품에서 사내아이의 자그마한 수놓은 띠를 꺼냈는데, 그것을 보자마자 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨기 시작하더니 손가락으로 눈을 가렸고, 그 손가락 사이로 눈물이 갑작스레 줄줄 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"옛적의 라헬이로구나.\" 장로가 말했다. \"자식들 때문에 울며, 그들이 없으므로 위로받기를 마다하는도다. 그대들 어미에게 이 땅 위에 정해진 몫이 이러하니라. 위로받으려 하지 말라. 위안은 그대에게 필요한 것이 아니다. 울되 위로받지 말고, 다만 울라. 다만 울 때마다 그대의 어린 자식이 하느님의 천사들 가운데 하나임을, 그가 저 위에서 그대를 내려다보며 그대를 지켜보고, 그대의 눈물을 보고 기뻐하며 그것을 주 하느님께 가리켜 보이고 있음을 반드시 기억하라. 그리고 그대는 아직 오랫동안 그 큰 어미의 슬픔을 간직하리라. 그러나 그것은 끝내 고요한 기쁨으로 바뀔 것이며, 그대의 쓰라린 눈물은 다만 마음을 정화하여 죄에서 건져 주는 애틋한 슬픔의 눈물이 되리라. 나도 그대 아이의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도하겠노라. 그 애의 이름이 무엇이었느냐?\"\n\n\"알렉세이였어요, 신부님.\"\n\n\"고운 이름이로구나. 하느님의 사람 알렉세이를 따른 것이냐?\"\n\n\"네, 신부님.\"\n\n\"그분은 얼마나 거룩한 성인이셨던가! 기도 가운데 그 애를, 그리고 그대의 슬픔을 기억하겠노라, 어미여. 또한 그대 남편의 건강을 위해 기도하겠다. 그를 버리고 떠나는 것은 그대에게 죄가 된다. 그대의 어린것이 하늘에서 그대가 제 아비를 저버린 것을 보고 그대를 위해 울 것이다. 어찌하여 그 애의 행복을 어지럽히려 하느냐? 그 애는 살아 있으니, 영혼은 영원히 사는 까닭이라. 비록 그가 집 안에 있지 않을지라도 보이지 않게 그대 곁에 있느니라. 그대가 그 집이 밉다고 말하는데, 어찌 그 애가 집으로 들어가겠느냐? 제 아비와 어미가 함께 있지 않은 것을 보면, 그 애가 누구에게로 가겠느냐? 지금은 그 애가 그대의 꿈에 찾아오니 그대가 슬퍼하는 것이라. 그러나 그때가 되면 그 애가 그대에게 부드러운 꿈을 보내 주리라. 남편에게로 가거라, 어미여. 바로 오늘 가거라.\"\n\n\"가겠어요, 신부님, 신부님 말씀대로요. 가겠어요. 신부님 말씀이 곧장 제 가슴에 사무쳤어요. 우리 니키타, 우리 니키타, 당신이 나를 기다리고 있군요.\" 여자가 가락을 넣어 읊조리기 시작했으나, 장로는 이미 몸을 돌려 아주 늙은 한 여인에게로 향했는데, 그 여인은 순례자가 아니라 읍내에 사는 사람처럼 차려입고 있었다. 그 눈을 보니 무슨 목적이 있어, 또 무언가 할 말이 있어 온 것임을 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 자기가 하사관의 과부이며 이 읍내 가까이에 산다고 말했다. 아들 바센카는 군수품 부대에서 복무하다가 시베리아의 이르쿠츠크로 갔다고 했다. 거기서 두 번 편지를 보내왔으나, 이제 마지막 편지를 보낸 지 한 해가 지났다는 것이었다. 그녀는 아들의 소식을 알아보려 했으나, 어디다 알아봐야 마땅한지를 알지 못했다.\n\n\"엊그제 스테파니다 일리이니시나가—그이는 부유한 상인의 아내랍니다—제게 그러더군요. '프로호로브나, 가서 교회에 아들 이름을 적어 기도를 올리고, 마치 그 애가 죽은 사람인 양 그 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도하시구려. 그러면 그 애 영혼이 심란해져서,' 그러는 거예요. '당신한테 편지를 보낼 거요'라고요. 그리고 스테파니다 일리이니시나가 그러는데, 그건 여러 번 시험해 본 확실한 방법이라더군요. 다만 저는 미심쩍어요…… 오, 우리의 빛이신 분이여! 그게 참말인가요 거짓인가요, 또 그래도 괜찮은 일일까요?\"\n\n\"그런 생각일랑 말거라. 그런 것을 묻다니 부끄러운 일이로다. 어찌 산 사람의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도할 수 있단 말이냐? 더구나 친어미가 말이다! 그것은 요술과 다름없는 큰 죄로다. 다만 그대가 무지한 까닭에 용서받는 것이니라. 차라리 우리의 날랜 방벽이요 도움이신 하늘의 여왕께 그 애의 건강을 위해, 또 그분이 그대의 잘못을 용서해 주시기를 기도하라. 그리고 한 가지 더 일러 두겠다, 프로호로브나. 그대의 아들은 머잖아 그대에게 돌아오거나, 아니면 틀림없이 편지를 보내올 것이다. 가거라, 그리고 이제부터 마음을 편히 가지거라. 그대의 아들은 살아 있느니라, 내 그대에게 이르노라.\"\n\n\"고마우신 신부님, 하느님께서 갚아 주시기를, 우리 모두를 위해, 우리의 죄를 위해 기도해 주시는 우리의 은인이시여!\"\n\n그러나 장로는 이미 무리 가운데서 자기를 향해 못박힌 듯 타오르는 두 눈을 알아차렸다. 기진맥진하고 폐병이라도 든 듯한, 그러나 아직 젊은 농사꾼 아낙네 하나가 말없이 그를 응시하고 있었다. 그 눈은 그에게 애원하고 있었으나, 여자는 다가오기를 두려워하는 듯했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐, 내 딸아?\"\n\n\"제 영혼을 사하여 주세요, 신부님.\" 여자가 나직이 또박또박 말하고는, 천천히 무릎을 꿇고 그의 발치에 엎드렸다. \"저는 죄를 지었어요, 신부님. 제 죄가 두렵습니다.\"\n\n장로는 아래쪽 계단에 앉았다. 여자는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 그에게로 좀 더 바싹 기어왔다.\n\n\"과부가 된 지 세 해째예요.\" 여자가 일종의 전율과 함께 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 운을 뗐다. \"남편과 사는 동안 고된 삶을 살았어요. 늙은이였답니다. 저를 모질게 때리곤 했지요. 그이가 앓아누웠어요. 그이를 들여다보면서 생각했지요, 만약 저이가 나아 버리면, 만약 저이가 다시 일어나면, 그러면 어쩌나? 그러다 문득 이런 생각이 들었어요—\"\n\n\"멈추거라!\" 장로가 말하며, 그녀의 입술에 귀를 바싹 갖다 댔다.\n\n여자는 나직이 속삭이며 말을 이었는데, 무슨 말인지 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다. 이내 말을 마쳤다.\n\n\"세 해 전이라고?\" 장로가 물었다.\n\n\"세 해예요. 처음엔 그 일을 생각하지 않았는데, 이제 몸이 아프기 시작하면서 그 생각이 한시도 떠나질 않아요.\"\n\n\"먼 데서 왔느냐?\"\n\n\"삼백 베르스타도 넘는 데서요.\"\n\n\"고해성사로 그것을 고백했느냐?\"\n\n\"고백했어요. 두 번이나 고백했지요.\"\n\n\"영성체는 받았느냐?\"\n\n\"네. 두려워요. 죽는 게 두려워요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 두려워하지 말고 결코 겁내지 말라. 그리고 애태우지 말라. 그대의 뉘우침이 거짓되지만 않다면, 하느님께서 모든 것을 용서하시리라. 온 땅에 진실로 뉘우치는 자에게 주께서 용서하지 않으실 죄란 없으며, 있을 수도 없느니라! 사람은 하느님의 무한한 사랑을 다 길어 낼 만큼 큰 죄를 지을 수 없느니라. 하느님의 사랑을 넘어설 죄가 어디 있겠느냐? 오로지 뉘우침만을, 끊임없는 뉘우침만을 생각하고, 두려움일랑 송두리째 떨쳐 버려라. 하느님께서 그대가 헤아릴 수도 없을 만큼 그대를 사랑하심을, 그대의 죄째로, 그대의 죄 속에 있는 그대를 사랑하심을 믿으라. 옛적에 이르기를, 의인 열 명보다 회개하는 죄인 하나를 두고 하늘에서 더 큰 기쁨이 있다 하였느니라. 가거라, 그리고 두려워 말라. 사람들에게 모질게 굴지 말라. 그대가 부당한 일을 당하더라도 노여워하지 말라. 죽은 그 사람이 그대에게 끼친 잘못을 마음속으로 용서하라. 그와 진실로 화해하라. 그대가 뉘우친다면 그대는 사랑하는 것이라. 그리고 그대가 사랑한다면 그대는 하느님께 속한 것이라. 만물이 사랑으로 속죄되며, 만물이 사랑으로 구원받느니라. 그대와 똑같이 죄인인 나조차 그대에게 다정하며 그대를 가엾이 여기거늘, 하물며 하느님이야 얼마나 더하시겠느냐. 사랑은 그것 하나로 온 세상을 구할 수 있고, 그대 자신의 죄뿐 아니라 남들의 죄까지 갚을 수 있는, 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 보배니라.\"\n\n그는 그녀에게 세 번 십자가를 그어 주고, 자기 목에서 작은 성상을 벗어 그녀에게 걸어 주었다. 그녀는 아무 말 없이 땅에 엎드려 절했다.\n\n그는 일어나, 자그마한 갓난아기를 품에 안은 건강한 농사꾼 아낙네를 흐뭇하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"비셰고리예에서 왔어요, 고마우신 신부님.\"\n\n\"갓난것을 데리고 오 베르스타나 걸어왔구나. 무엇을 바라느냐?\"\n\n\"신부님을 뵈러 왔어요. 전에도 신부님을 찾아뵌 적이 있는데—혹 잊으셨나요? 절 잊으셨다면 신부님 기억력이 신통찮으신 거예요. 신부님이 편찮으시다고들 하더군요. 그래서 제가 생각했지요, 내가 직접 가서 뵈어야겠다고요. 이제 뵈니 편찮으신 게 아니네요! 앞으로 스무 해는 더 사시겠어요. 하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를! 신부님을 위해 기도하는 사람이 이렇게 많은데, 어찌 편찮으실 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"모든 것에 고맙구나, 딸아.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 청이 하나 있는데, 대단한 건 아니에요. 여기 육십 코페이카가 있어요. 고마우신 신부님, 이걸 저보다 더 가난한 누군가에게 주세요. 오는 길에 생각했는데, 신부님을 통해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어요. 신부님은 누구한테 줘야 할지 아실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 내 딸아, 고마워! 그대는 착한 여인이로다. 그대를 사랑하노라. 꼭 그리하겠다. 저 아이가 그대의 딸이냐?\"\n\n\"제 딸이에요, 신부님, 리자베타랍니다.\"\n\n\"주께서 그대 둘 다, 그대와 그대의 갓난것 리자베타를 축복하시기를! 그대가 내 마음을 기쁘게 하였구나, 어미여. 잘 가거라, 사랑스러운 아이들아, 잘 가거라, 귀한 사람들아.\"\n\n그는 그들 모두를 축복하고 그들에게 깊이 허리 굽혀 절했다.\n\n# Chapter IV. A Lady Of Little Faith\n\n그가 농부들과 대화를 나누고 그들을 축복하는 광경을 지켜보던 한 방문객은 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 손수건으로 그것을 닦아 냈다. 그녀는 여러 면에서 진정으로 선량한 천성을 지닌, 감상적인 사교계 부인이었다. 마침내 장로가 그녀에게 다가가자 그녀는 열렬하게 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"아아, 이 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 지켜보면서 제가 얼마나 마음이 벅찼는지요!……\" 그녀는 감격에 겨워 말을 잇지 못했다. \"오, 저는 백성들이 당신을 사랑하는 마음을 이해합니다. 저 자신도 백성을 사랑하지요. 저는 그들을 사랑하고 싶어요. 그리고 누군들 그들을 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어요, 그토록 위대하면서도 그토록 소박한 우리의 훌륭한 러시아 백성을!\"\n\n\"따님의 건강은 어떻습니까? 다시 저와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하셨다지요?\"\n\n\"오, 저는 그것을 간절히 청해 왔고, 그것을 위해 기도했답니다! 당신이 저를 들여보내 주실 때까지 무릎을 꿇고 사흘이라도 당신의 창 밑에 엎드릴 각오였어요. 위대한 치유자시여, 저희는 뜨거운 감사의 마음을 표하고자 찾아왔습니다. 당신은 제 리즈를, 완전히 치유해 주셨어요, 지난 목요일에 그 아이를 위해 기도해 주시고 그 아이에게 손을 얹어 주신 것만으로 말이지요. 저희는 그 손에 입 맞추고, 저희의 감정과 경의를 쏟아 내고자 부랴부랴 달려왔답니다.\"\n\n\"치유라니, 무슨 말씀이신지요? 하지만 따님은 아직도 의자에 누워 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 목요일 이후로 그 아이의 밤 열이 완전히 가셨답니다.\" 부인이 들뜬 듯 다급하게 말했다. \"그것뿐이 아니에요. 그 아이의 다리에 힘이 붙었답니다. 오늘 아침엔 멀쩡하게 일어났고, 밤새도록 잘 잤지요. 저 발그레한 뺨을, 저 반짝이는 눈을 보세요! 예전엔 노상 울기만 하더니, 이젠 웃고 명랑하고 행복하답니다. 오늘 아침엔 자기를 일으켜 세워 달라고 우기더니, 아무 데도 기대지 않고 꼬박 일 분 동안이나 서 있었어요. 그 아이는 보름 안에 카드리유를 추겠다고 장담한답니다. 헤르첸슈투베 박사를 불렀더니, 그는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말하더군요, '놀랍습니다. 도무지 영문을 모르겠어요.' 이러한데 저희더러 당신을 성가시게 하러 이곳에 오지 말라고, 당신께 감사드리러 날아오지 말라고 하실 수 있겠어요? 리즈, 이분께 감사드리렴ㅡ감사드려!\"\n\n리즈의 예쁘장한 작은 웃음 띤 얼굴이 별안간 진지해졌다. 그녀는 의자에서 일어설 수 있는 데까지 몸을 일으키더니, 장로를 바라보며 그 앞에서 두 손을 모았으나, 자신을 억누르지 못하고 그만 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n\"저분 때문이에요.\" 그녀가 알료샤를 가리키며 말했는데, 자신의 웃음을 참지 못하는 스스로에게 어린아이처럼 약이 오른 투였다.\n\n만약 누군가 장로 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서 있던 알료샤를 바라보았다면, 순식간에 그의 뺨이 새빨갛게 달아오르는 것을 보았으리라. 그의 눈이 빛났고, 그는 시선을 내리깔았다.\n\n\"이 아이가 당신께 전할 말이 있답니다, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 그동안 안녕하셨어요?\" 어머니가 우아하게 장갑을 낀 손을 알료샤에게 내밀며 말을 이었다.\n\n장로가 몸을 돌리더니 별안간 알료샤를 유심히 바라보았다. 알료샤는 리즈에게 가까이 다가가, 묘하게 어색한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게도 손을 내밀었다. 리즈는 짐짓 거드름을 피우는 태를 지었다.\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나가 저를 통해 이것을 당신께 보냈어요.\" 그녀가 그에게 쪽지를 건넸다. \"그분이 특별히 부탁하시기를, 되도록 빨리 자기를 찾아와 달라고, 그분을 저버리지 말고 꼭 와 달라고 하셨답니다.\"\n\n\"그분이 저더러 찾아와 달라고요? 저를요? 무슨 일로요?\" 알료샤가 몹시 놀라 중얼거렸다. 그의 얼굴이 단번에 불안한 빛을 띠었다. \"오, 그건 모두 드미트리 표도로비치와ㅡ요즘 벌어진 일과 관련된 거예요.\" 어머니가 황급히 설명했다. \"카테리나 이바노브나가 마음을 굳혔는데, 그 일에 관해 당신을 꼭 만나야 한답니다…… 무슨 일인지는, 글쎄요, 저로선 말할 수 없네요. 하지만 그분은 당장 당신을 보고 싶어 해요. 그러니 당신도 물론 그분께 가시겠지요. 그건 그리스도교인의 도리니까요.\"\n\n\"저는 그분을 단 한 번 뵈었을 뿐인데요.\" 알료샤가 여전히 어리둥절해하며 항변했다.\n\n\"오, 그분은 어쩌면 그렇게도 고결하고 비길 데 없는 분인지요! 그 고통만으로도 충분하답니다…… 그분이 무엇을 겪어 왔는지, 지금 무엇을 견디고 있는지 생각해 보세요! 그분 앞에 무엇이 기다리고 있는지 생각해 보세요! 모든 게 끔찍하고, 또 끔찍하답니다!\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 가지요.\" 알료샤는 간결하면서도 알 수 없는 그 쪽지를 재빨리 훑어본 뒤 그렇게 결정했는데, 거기에는 아무런 설명도 없이 그저 와 달라는 간절한 애원만이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"오, 그렇게 해 주시면 얼마나 다정하고 너그러운 일인지요!\" 리즈가 별안간 활기를 띠며 외쳤다. \"엄마한테는 분명 안 가실 거라고 했어요. 당신은 영혼을 구하느라 바쁘다고 했지요. 당신은 얼마나 훌륭한지! 저는 늘 당신이 훌륭하다고 생각했답니다. 그걸 당신께 말씀드릴 수 있어서 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"리즈!\" 그녀의 어머니가 위엄을 담아 말했으나, 말을 마친 뒤에는 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"당신은 저희를 까맣게 잊으셨군요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"한 번도 저희를 보러 오지 않으시니. 그런데 리즈는 두 번이나 제게 말했답니다, 당신과 함께 있을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없다고요.\"\n\n알료샤는 내리깔았던 눈을 들었다가 또다시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 또다시 까닭도 모르게 미소를 지었다. 그러나 장로는 더 이상 그를 바라보고 있지 않았다. 그는 앞서 말했듯 리즈의 의자 곁에서 자신이 나오기를 기다리고 있던 한 수도사와 이야기를 나누기 시작했던 것이다. 그는 분명 가장 비천한, 다시 말해 농민 계층 출신의 수도사로서, 견식은 좁으나 진실한 신자였고, 나름대로 고집스러운 사람이었다. 그는 자신이 머나먼 북방, 오브도르스크의 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 왔으며, 겨우 열 명의 수도사로 이루어진 가난한 수도원의 일원이라고 밝혔다. 장로는 그에게 축복을 내리고 언제든 마음 내킬 때 자신의 암자로 찾아오라고 청했다.\n\n\"어찌 감히 그런 일을 행하실 수 있습니까?\" 수도사가 별안간, 엄숙하고 의미심장하게 리즈를 가리키며 물었다. 그는 그녀의 \"치유\"를 두고 하는 말이었다.\n\n\"물론 그것을 논하기엔 아직 이릅니다. 차도는 완전한 치유가 아니며, 여러 다른 원인에서 비롯될 수도 있지요. 그러나 만약 어떤 치유가 일어났다면, 그것은 다른 어떤 힘이 아니라 오직 하느님의 뜻에 의한 것입니다. 모든 게 하느님으로부터 온 것이지요. 저를 찾아오십시오, 신부님.\" 그가 수도사에게 덧붙였다. \"제가 손님을 맞을 수 있는 날이 그리 많지 않답니다. 저는 병들었고, 제 날이 얼마 남지 않았음을 알고 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요! 하느님께서 당신을 저희에게서 데려가시지 않을 거예요. 당신은 아직 오래오래 사실 거예요.\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"한데 어디가 그리 편찮으신가요? 이토록 정정하시고, 이토록 명랑하고 행복해 보이시는데요.\"\n\n\"오늘은 유난히 좋아진 것이지요. 하지만 그것이 한순간에 지나지 않음을 저는 압니다. 이제 저는 제 병을 속속들이 알고 있어요. 제가 당신께 그토록 행복해 보인다면, 당신은 제게 그보다 더 기쁜 말을 결코 해 줄 수 없을 겁니다. 인간은 행복을 위해 지어졌고, 온전히 행복한 사람은 누구나 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 권리가 있으니까요, '나는 이 땅에서 하느님의 뜻을 행하고 있다'고요. 모든 의인이, 모든 성인이, 모든 거룩한 순교자가 행복했답니다.\"\n\n\"오, 어찌 그리 말씀하시는지요! 얼마나 대담하고 고결한 말씀인지!\" 부인이 외쳤다. \"당신의 말씀은 마음을 꿰뚫는 듯합니다. 그런데도ㅡ행복이, 행복이ㅡ대체 어디에 있을까요? 누가 스스로 행복하다고 말할 수 있을까요? 오, 당신께서 오늘 한 번 더 저희를 만나 주시는 은혜를 베푸셨으니, 지난번에는 차마 입에 담지 못했던 것, 감히 말하지 못했던 것, 제가 그토록 오랫동안 겪어 온 모든 괴로움을 말씀드리게 해 주세요! 저는 괴로워하고 있어요! 용서하세요! 저는 괴로워하고 있답니다!\"\n\n그러고는 북받쳐 오르는 열렬한 감정에 휩싸여 그녀는 그 앞에서 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"특히 무엇 때문에 그러십니까?\"\n\n\"저는 괴로워하고 있어요…… 믿음이 부족해서요.\"\n\n\"하느님에 대한 믿음이 부족하다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요! 저는 감히 그런 건 생각조차 할 수 없어요. 하지만 내세ㅡ그건 너무도 알 수 없는 수수께끼랍니다! 그리고 아무도, 아무도 그것을 풀 수 없어요. 들어 보세요! 당신은 치유자시고, 인간의 영혼에 깊이 통달하신 분이니, 물론 제가 당신께 저를 온전히 믿어 달라고 감히 바랄 수는 없지만, 제 명예를 걸고 단언컨대 저는 지금 결코 가벼이 말하고 있는 게 아니랍니다. 무덤 너머의 삶에 대한 생각이 저를 번민으로, 공포로 몰아넣어요. 그런데 저는 누구에게 호소해야 할지 모르겠고, 평생토록 감히 그러지도 못했어요. 그리고 이제 이렇게 대담하게 당신께 여쭙고 있는 거예요. 오, 하느님! 당신은 이제 저를 어떻게 생각하실까요?\"\n\n그녀가 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"제가 당신을 어떻게 생각할지로 괴로워하지 마십시오.\" 장로가 말했다. \"저는 당신이 겪는 고통의 진실함을 충분히 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 당신께 얼마나 감사한지 몰라요! 보세요, 저는 눈을 감고 스스로에게 물어본답니다, 만약 모두가 믿음을 지니고 있다면 그건 대체 어디서 온 것일까 하고요. 그런데 사람들이 말하기를, 그건 모두 자연의 위협적인 현상에 대한 공포에서 비롯된 것이며, 그 어느 것도 실재하지 않는다고 하지요. 그래서 저는 스스로에게 말한답니다, '내가 평생토록 믿어 왔는데, 막상 죽고 나면 내 무덤 위에 우엉이나 자라날 뿐이라면 어쩌지?' 하고요, 어느 작가의 글에서 읽은 대로 말이에요. 끔찍한 일이지요! 어떻게ㅡ어떻게 하면 제 믿음을 되찾을 수 있을까요? 하지만 저는 어린아이였을 적에만, 아무것도 생각하지 않고 그저 기계적으로 믿었을 뿐이에요. 어떻게, 어떻게 그것을 증명할 수 있을까요? 저는 이제 제 영혼을 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 그것에 관해 여쭙고자 찾아온 거예요. 만약 이 기회를 놓친다면, 평생토록 아무도 제게 답해 주지 않을 거예요. 어떻게 그것을 증명할 수 있을까요? 어떻게 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있을까요? 오, 저는 얼마나 불행한지! 저는 서서 주위를 둘러보며, 다른 사람은 거의 아무도 그것에 신경 쓰지 않는다는 걸 깨닫는답니다. 아무도 그 일로 골머리를 앓지 않는데, 그것을 견딜 수 없는 건 오직 저 하나뿐이에요. 죽을 것만 같아요ㅡ죽을 것만 같다고요!\"\n\n\"의심할 바 없는 일입니다. 그러나 그것은 증명할 수는 없으되, 확신할 수는 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\"\n\"능동적인 사랑의 경험을 통해서지요. 그대의 이웃을 능동적으로, 지칠 줄 모르고 사랑하려 애쓰십시오. 사랑 안에서 나아가는 만큼, 그대는 하느님의 실재와 그대 영혼의 불멸에 대한 확신이 더욱 굳어질 것입니다. 만약 그대가 이웃에 대한 사랑 속에서 완전한 자기 망각에 이른다면, 그때 그대는 의심 없이 믿게 될 것이며, 어떤 의심도 그대의 영혼에 결코 비집고 들어올 수 없을 것입니다. 이는 이미 시험된 것이며, 분명한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"능동적인 사랑 안에서요? 거기에 또 다른 문제가 있어요ㅡ그것도 이런 문제가요! 보세요, 저는 인류를 너무도 사랑한 나머지ㅡ믿으시겠어요?ㅡ제가 가진 모든 것을 버리고, 리즈도 떠나, 자선 수녀가 되는 꿈을 자주 꾼답니다. 저는 눈을 감고 생각하고 꿈꾸는데, 그 순간엔 모든 장애를 이겨 낼 힘이 가득 찬 것만 같아요. 그 어떤 상처도, 그 어떤 곪은 종기도 그 순간만큼은 저를 두렵게 하지 못해요. 저는 제 손으로 그것을 싸매고 씻어 줄 거예요. 저는 병든 이들을 간호할 거예요. 저는 그런 상처에 기꺼이 입 맞출 각오가 되어 있답니다.\"\n\n\"대단한 일이며, 그대의 마음이 다른 것이 아닌 그러한 꿈으로 가득 차 있다는 것은 좋은 일입니다. 언젠가 그대도 모르는 사이에, 그대는 현실에서 선한 일을 행하게 될지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"네. 하지만 제가 그런 삶을 오래 견뎌 낼 수 있을까요?\" 부인이 거의 미친 듯이, 열렬하게 말을 이었다. \"그게 가장 큰 문제예요ㅡ그게 저를 가장 번민케 하는 문제랍니다. 저는 눈을 감고 스스로에게 물어요, '너는 그 길을 오래 꿋꿋이 갈 수 있겠느냐? 그리고 만약 네가 그 상처를 씻어 주는 환자가 너를 감사로 맞기는커녕, 변덕으로 너를 괴롭히고, 네 자비로운 봉사를 가치 있게 여기지도 알아주지도 않으면서, 너를 욕하고 무례하게 부려 먹고, 윗사람에게 너를 일러바치기 시작한다면(사람이 큰 고통에 처했을 때 흔히 일어나는 일이지요)ㅡ그땐 어쩔 테냐? 너는 그 사랑을 꿋꿋이 이어 가겠느냐, 그러지 못하겠느냐?' 하고요. 그런데 아세요, 저는 소름 끼치는 결론에 이르렀답니다, 만약 인류를 향한 제 사랑을 흩어 버릴 수 있는 것이 있다면, 그건 바로 배은망덕이라는 결론에요. 한마디로 저는 삯을 받는 종이라, 제 보수를 당장 받기를 바라는 거예요ㅡ그러니까 칭찬과, 사랑에 대한 사랑의 보답을 말이지요. 그렇지 않으면 저는 누구도 사랑할 수 없답니다.\"\n\n그녀는 그야말로 자기 질책의 발작에 빠져 있었으며, 말을 맺으면서 도전적인 결의를 담아 장로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그것은 언젠가 한 의사가 제게 들려준 이야기와 꼭 같습니다.\" 장로가 말했다. \"나이가 지긋한 사람이었는데, 의심할 바 없이 영리한 자였지요. 그는 그대처럼 솔직하게 말했습니다, 비록 농담조였으나, 그것도 쓰디쓴 농담조였지만요. '나는 인류를 사랑하오.' 그가 말했지요. '하지만 나 자신이 의아할 따름이오. 인류 전체를 사랑하면 할수록, 개개의 인간은 그만큼 덜 사랑하게 되니 말이오. 내 꿈속에서는,' 그가 말했습니다, '나는 인류에 봉사하기 위한 열렬한 계획을 세우는 데까지 곧잘 이르렀고, 만약 갑자기 그래야만 한다면 어쩌면 실제로 십자가에 못 박히는 일도 마다하지 않았을 거요. 그런데도 나는 누구와도 단 이틀을 한방에서 함께 지낼 수가 없으니, 이는 내가 경험으로 아는 바요. 누군가 내 곁에 다가오기만 하면, 그의 인격이 내 자기만족을 어지럽히고 내 자유를 옭아매오. 스물네 시간 안에 나는 아무리 훌륭한 사람이라도 미워하기 시작하오. 어떤 자는 저녁 식사를 너무 오래 끈다고, 또 어떤 자는 감기에 걸려 줄곧 코를 푼다고 말이오. 사람들이 내게 가까이 다가오는 순간, 나는 그들에게 적의를 품게 되오. 그러나 늘 그러했듯, 내가 개개의 인간을 혐오하면 할수록, 인류를 향한 내 사랑은 그만큼 더 뜨거워지는 거요.'\"\n\n\"하지만 어찌해야 하나요? 그런 경우엔 어떻게 해야 하지요? 절망해야만 하나요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 그대가 그 일로 괴로워한다는 것만으로도 충분합니다. 할 수 있는 일을 하십시오, 그러면 그것이 그대에게 헤아려질 것입니다. 그대가 그토록 깊고 진실하게 자신을 알 수 있으니, 이미 그대 안에서 많은 일이 이루어진 셈입니다. 만약 그대가 제게 그토록 진실하게 이야기한 것이, 단지 방금 제게서 그러했듯 자신의 솔직함에 대한 칭찬을 얻기 위함이었다면, 물론 그대는 참된 사랑을 이루는 일에 아무것도 도달하지 못할 것입니다. 그 모든 것이 꿈에 머물고 말 것이며, 그대의 온 생애가 환영처럼 스러져 갈 것입니다. 그런 경우 그대는 자연히 내세에 대해서도 더는 생각하지 않게 될 것이며, 끝내는 어떤 식으로든 스스로 더 평온해질 것입니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 저를 무너뜨리셨어요! 이제야, 당신이 말씀하시는 이 순간에야, 제가 배은망덕을 견딜 수 없다고 당신께 말씀드렸을 때 정말이지 제 진실함에 대한 당신의 칭찬을 구하고 있었을 뿐임을 깨닫는답니다. 당신은 저를 제 자신에게 드러내 보이셨어요. 당신은 저를 꿰뚫어 보시고, 저를 제 자신에게 설명해 주셨어요!\"\n\n\"진실을 말씀하시는 겁니까? 자, 그럼, 그런 고백을 하셨으니, 저는 그대가 진실하고 마음이 선한 사람임을 믿습니다. 비록 그대가 행복에 이르지 못한다 하더라도, 그대가 옳은 길 위에 있음을 늘 기억하고, 그 길에서 벗어나지 않도록 애쓰십시오. 무엇보다 거짓을 피하십시오, 온갖 종류의 거짓을, 특히 자기 자신에 대한 거짓을요. 그대 자신의 기만을 살피고, 매시간, 매분 그것을 들여다보십시오. 남에게든 자신에게든 경멸하는 태도를 피하십시오. 그대 안에서 나쁘게 보이는 것은, 그대가 그것을 자신 안에서 관찰한다는 바로 그 사실로 인해 더욱 정결해질 것입니다. 두려움도 피하십시오, 비록 두려움이 온갖 거짓의 결과에 지나지 않는다 해도요. 사랑에 이르는 데 있어 자신의 나약함을 결코 두려워하지 마십시오. 자신의 악행조차도 지나치게 두려워하지 마십시오. 더 위안이 될 말을 해 드릴 수 없어 미안합니다만, 행동하는 사랑은 꿈속의 사랑에 비하면 가혹하고 무서운 것이니까요. 꿈속의 사랑은 즉각적인 행동을, 모두가 지켜보는 가운데 신속히 이루어지는 행동을 갈망합니다. 사람들은 시련이 오래 끌지 않고 곧 끝나기만 한다면, 마치 무대 위에서처럼 모두가 지켜보며 갈채를 보내는 가운데서라면, 심지어 제 목숨까지도 내놓을 것입니다. 그러나 능동적인 사랑은 노고이자 인내이며, 어떤 이들에게는 어쩌면 하나의 완전한 학문이기까지 합니다. 그러나 제가 예언하건대, 바로 그대가 온갖 노력에도 불구하고 목표에 가까워지기는커녕 오히려 멀어지고 있음을 소름 끼치도록 깨닫게 되는 그 순간ㅡ제가 예언하건대 바로 그 순간에 그대는 목표에 도달할 것이며, 그 모든 시간 동안 그대를 사랑하고 신비로이 인도해 오신 주님의 기적 같은 권능을 똑똑히 보게 될 것입니다. 더 오래 그대와 함께 있어 드리지 못하는 것을 용서하십시오. 사람들이 저를 기다리고 있군요. 그럼 안녕히.\"\n\n부인은 울고 있었다.\n\n\"리즈, 리즈! 이 아이를 축복해 주세요ㅡ축복해 주세요!\" 그녀가 별안간 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 아이는 사랑받을 자격이 없습니다. 저는 줄곧 이 아이의 짓궂음을 보아 왔으니까요.\" 장로가 농담조로 말했다. \"어찌하여 알렉세이를 놀려 댔느냐?\"\n\n리즈는 사실 내내 그를 조롱하는 데 골몰해 있었다. 그녀는 알료샤가 수줍어하며 자기를 보지 않으려 애쓰는 것을 진작에 알아챘고, 그것이 더없이 재미있었던 것이다. 그녀는 그의 눈길을 붙잡으려고 골똘히 기다렸다. 알료샤는 그녀의 집요한 응시를 견디지 못하고, 거역할 수 없이 별안간 그녀를 흘끗 보고 싶은 마음에 이끌렸는데, 그러자 그녀는 곧장 그의 얼굴에 대고 의기양양하게 미소를 지었다. 알료샤는 더욱 당황하고 약이 올랐다. 마침내 그는 그녀에게서 완전히 몸을 돌려 장로의 등 뒤로 숨었다. 몇 분 뒤, 똑같이 거역할 수 없는 힘에 이끌려, 그는 자기가 응시당하고 있는지 아닌지 보려고 다시 몸을 돌렸는데, 리즈가 그를 보려고 의자에서 거의 몸을 내밀다시피 곁눈질하며, 그가 자기를 봐 주기를 애타게 기다리고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그의 눈길을 붙잡자 그녀가 어찌나 웃어 대던지, 장로도 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"어찌하여 그렇게 저 아이를 놀려 대느냐, 짓궂은 아가씨야?\"\n\n리즈가 별안간, 전혀 뜻밖에도 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그녀의 눈이 번뜩였고 얼굴이 자못 진지해졌다. 그녀는 따뜻하면서도 원망스러운 목소리로 빠르고 격하게 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그럼 저 사람은 어째서 모든 걸 잊어버린 거예요? 제가 어렸을 적엔 저를 안고 다녔어요. 저희는 함께 놀곤 했지요. 저 사람은 제게 글 읽기를 가르쳐 주러 오곤 했답니다, 아세요. 이 년 전, 떠나갈 적에 저 사람은 저를 결코 잊지 않겠다고, 저희가 영원히, 영원히, 영원히 친구라고 말했어요! 그런데 이제 와선 별안간 저를 두려워하잖아요. 제가 저 사람을 잡아먹기라도 한대요? 어째서 제 곁에 오려 하지 않는 거죠? 어째서 말을 안 하는 거예요? 어째서 저희를 보러 오지 않는 거죠? 당신이 못 오게 하시는 것도 아닌데. 저 사람이 어딜 가든 다 돌아다닌다는 걸 저희도 알아요. 제가 저 사람을 초대하는 건 예의에 어긋나는 일이지요. 저를 잊지 않았다면 저 사람이 먼저 그 생각을 했어야죠. 아뇨, 이제 저 사람은 영혼을 구하느라 바쁘대요! 어째서 저 사람한테 저런 긴 가운을 입히신 거예요? 뛰다간 넘어지고 말 텐데.\"\n\n그러더니 그녀는 별안간 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 거역할 수 없이, 오래도록, 격하게, 소리 없는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 장로는 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 말을 듣고 있다가, 다정하게 그녀를 축복했다. 그녀는 그의 손에 입 맞추다가 별안간 그것을 자기 두 눈에 갖다 대고는 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"저한테 화내지 마세요. 저는 어리석고 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 아이예요…… 그리고 어쩌면 알료샤가 옳은지도, 아주 옳은지도 몰라요, 이렇게 우스꽝스러운 여자애를 보러 오고 싶어 하지 않는 게요.\"\n\n\"내가 반드시 저 아이를 보내마.\" 장로가 말했다.\n\n# Chapter V. So Be It! So Be It!\n\n장로가 암자를 비운 시간은 이십오 분쯤이었다. 벌써 열두 시 반이 넘었으나, 그들 모두가 그 때문에 이 자리에 모였던 드미트리는 아직 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그는 거의 잊힌 듯했으니, 장로가 다시 암자로 들어섰을 때 손님들은 열띤 대화에 빠져 있었다. 이반과 두 수도사가 그 대화를 주도하고 있었다. 미우소프 또한 끼어들려고, 그것도 자못 열을 올리며 애를 쓰고 있었다. 그러나 이 일에서도 그는 뜻을 이루지 못했다. 그는 분명히 뒷전으로 밀려나 있었고, 그의 말은 무시당하기 일쑤여서 그 짜증은 점점 더 부풀었다. 그는 전에도 이반과 지적인 충돌을 겪은 적이 있었고, 이반이 자기에게 내보이는 어떤 무심함을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n\"적어도 지금까지 나는 유럽의 모든 진보적인 것들 가운데 맨 앞줄에 서 있었거늘, 여기 새 세대는 우리를 아예 거들떠보지도 않는구나.\" 그는 이렇게 생각했다.\n\n가만히 앉아 입을 다물고 있겠노라 약속했던 표도르 파블로비치는 정말 한동안 잠잠히 있었으나, 옆자리의 미우소프를 조롱하는 듯한 엷은 웃음을 띠고 지켜보면서, 그의 낭패를 분명히 즐기고 있었다. 그는 묵은 빚을 갚을 기회를 한참 기다려 왔던 터라, 이제 그 기회를 놓칠 수가 없었다. 그의 어깨 너머로 몸을 숙이며 그는 다시 속삭임으로 그를 놀려 대기 시작했다.\n\n\"아까 '공손히 입을 맞춘' 다음에 왜 떠나지 않았소? 어쩌자고 이런 꼴사나운 패거리 속에 남아 있기로 했소? 그건 당신이 모욕을 당하고 속이 상해서, 자기 똑똑함을 뽐내어 명예를 회복해 보려고 남았기 때문이지. 이제 당신은 저들에게 자기 지성을 펼쳐 보이기 전에는 떠나지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"또 당신이군요?…… 천만에, 나는 지금 막 떠나려던 참이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 마지막에, 누구보다도 맨 나중에 떠나게 될 거요!\" 표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 또 한 번 찌르듯 말을 던졌으니, 그것은 거의 조시마 장로가 돌아오는 바로 그 순간이었다.\n\n논의는 잠시 사그라들었으나, 장로는 본래 자리에 앉으며 좌중을 두루 둘러보았는데, 마치 그들에게 어서 이야기를 이어 가라고 다정하게 권하는 듯했다. 그의 얼굴 표정 하나하나를 다 알고 있던 알료샤는, 그가 끔찍이도 지쳐 있으면서 안간힘을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 근래에 그는 탈진으로 인한 졸도 발작을 일으키기 쉬운 상태였다. 그의 얼굴에는 그런 발작이 닥치기 전에 흔히 나타나는 창백함이 어려 있었고, 입술은 하얗게 질려 있었다. 그러나 그는 분명 이 모임을 끝맺으려 하지 않았다. 그들을 붙들어 두는 데에 무슨 자기 나름의 특별한 목적이 있는 듯했다. 무슨 목적일까? 알료샤는 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보았다.\n\n\"저희는 이 신사분의 무척 흥미로운 논문을 두고 이야기하던 참이었습니다.\" 사서를 맡은 이오시프 신부가 장로에게 말하며 이반을 가리켰다. \"여러 가지 새로운 점을 내놓고 계시지만, 제가 보기에는 그 논리가 양날의 칼인 듯합니다. 이것은 교회 재판소의 문제와 그 관할 범위에 관해 어느 교회 권위자가 쓴 책에 대한 반론으로 쓰인 논문입니다.\"\n\n\"안타깝게도 그대의 논문을 읽지는 못했으나, 소문은 들었소.\" 장로가 이반을 날카롭고 골똘하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"이분은 무척 흥미로운 입장을 취하고 계십니다.\" 사서 신부가 말을 이었다. \"교회의 관할권에 관한 한, 이분은 보아하니 교회와 국가의 분리에 전적으로 반대하시는 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 흥미롭구려. 하지만 어떤 의미에서 그렇소?\" 조시마 장로가 이반에게 물었다.\n\n이반은 마침내 그에게 대답했는데, 알료샤가 우려했던 것처럼 거드름을 피우는 투가 아니라, 겸손하고 절제된 태도로, 분명한 호의를 담아, 보아하니 한 점의 속셈도 없이 답했다.\n\n\"저는 이러한 요소들의 혼동, 곧 교회와 국가의 본질적 원리들의 혼동이, 양자가 결코 뒤섞일 수 없음에도 불구하고 당연히 영원히 계속되리라는 입장에서 출발합니다. 그리고 이 요소들의 혼동은 일관되거나 정상적인 결과조차 결코 낳을 수 없으니, 그것은 바로 그 근저에 거짓이 깔려 있기 때문입니다. 가령 관할권 같은 문제에서 교회와 국가 사이의 타협이란, 제 생각으로는 어떤 진정한 의미로도 불가능합니다. 저와 맞서는 성직자 측은 교회가 국가 안에서 정확하고 한정된 지위를 차지한다고 주장합니다. 저는 그와 반대로, 교회가 단지 국가 안의 한 귀퉁이를 차지할 것이 아니라 국가 전체를 포괄해야 한다고 주장합니다. 그리고 만일 이것이 무슨 이유로 지금 당장은 불가능하다면, 그것은 마땅히 그리스도교 사회의 미래 발전이 지향할 직접적이고 으뜸가는 목표로서 세워져야 마땅합니다!\"\n\n\"지극히 옳은 말씀이오.\" 말수가 적고 학식이 깊은 수도사 파이시 신부가 열의와 단호함을 담아 동의했다.\n\n\"순전한 교황지상주의로군!\" 미우소프가 참다못해 외치며, 다리를 꼬았다 다시 풀었다.\n\n\"아니, 우리에게는 산이라는 게 없는걸요.\" 이오시프 신부가 외치더니, 장로를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. \"이분이 상대편의 다음과 같은 '근본적이고 본질적인' 명제들에, 유념하셔야 할 것은 그 상대편이 성직자라는 점입니다만, 어떤 답을 내놓는지 보십시오. 첫째, '어떠한 사회 조직도 그 구성원의 시민적·정치적 권리를 좌우할 권력을 스스로에게 참칭할 수 없으며 또 그래서도 안 된다'는 것. 둘째, '형사 및 민사 관할권은 교회에 속해서는 안 되며, 신적 제도로서나 종교적 목적을 위한 인간들의 조직으로서나 교회의 본성과 양립할 수 없다'는 것. 그리고 마지막으로 셋째, '교회는 이 세상에 속한 나라가 아니다'라는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"성직자로서는 더없이 못난 말장난이오!\" 파이시 신부가 또다시 끼어들지 않을 수 없었다. \"나는 그대가 반박한 그 책을 읽었소만,\" 그는 이반을 향해 덧붙였다. \"'교회는 이 세상에 속한 나라가 아니다'라는 그 말에 경악을 금치 못했소. 만일 그것이 이 세상에 속한 것이 아니라면, 그렇다면 그것은 이 땅에 도무지 존재할 수가 없는 것이오. 복음서에서 '이 세상에 속하지 않은'이라는 말은 그런 뜻으로 쓰이지 않았소. 그런 말을 가지고 농을 하는 것은 변명의 여지가 없는 일이오. 우리 주 예수 그리스도께서는 이 땅에 교회를 세우러 오셨소. 천국은 물론 이 세상에 속한 것이 아니라 하늘에 있는 것이나, 그곳에는 오로지 이 땅에 세워지고 견고히 다져진 교회를 통해서만 들어갈 수 있는 것이오. 그러니 그런 문제를 두고 경박하게 말장난을 하는 것은 용서할 수 없고 온당치 못한 짓이오. 교회는 진실로 하나의 나라이며 다스리도록 정해진 것이고, 마침내는 틀림없이 온 땅을 다스리는 나라가 되어야 하오. 그것에 대해서는 우리에게 신의 약속이 있소.\"\n\n그는 마치 스스로를 다잡듯 갑자기 말을 멈추었다. 이반은 주의 깊고 공손하게 귀 기울인 뒤, 더없이 침착하게, 그리고 앞서와 마찬가지로 선뜻한 다정함을 담아 장로를 향해 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제 논문의 요점은 그리스도교가 처음 삼백 년 동안 이 땅에서 오로지 교회 안에서만 존재했고, 교회 그 자체에 지나지 않았다는 사실에 있습니다. 이교도였던 로마 제국이 그리스도교 국가가 되고자 했을 때, 필연적으로 일어난 일은, 그것이 그리스도교 국가가 됨으로써 교회를 그 안에 포함하면서도 매우 많은 부문에서 여전히 이교 국가로 남았다는 것입니다. 사실 이것은 일어날 수밖에 없는 일이었습니다. 그러나 국가로서의 로마는 가령 국가의 바로 그 목적과 근본 원리에 이르기까지 이교 문명과 문화를 너무도 많이 간직하고 있었습니다. 그 국가 안으로 들어간 그리스도교 교회는, 물론 자신이 딛고 선 반석인 근본 원리의 어떤 부분도 내어줄 수 없었고, 신께서 친히 정하시고 계시하신 목표들 외에 다른 어떤 목표도 추구할 수 없었으니, 그 가운데 하나가 바로 온 세계를, 따라서 저 오랜 이교 국가 자체를 교회 안으로 끌어들이는 것이었습니다. 그런 까닭에 (다시 말해 미래를 내다볼 때) 교회가 '모든 사회 조직'처럼, 혹은 (제 상대편이 교회를 일컫는 대로) '종교적 목적을 위한 인간들의 조직'처럼 국가 안에서 어떤 한정된 지위를 구해야 하는 것이 아니라, 오히려 그 반대로 이 땅의 모든 국가가 마침내 온전히 교회로 변모하여 교회 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어야 하며, 교회의 목적과 어긋나는 온갖 목적을 버려야 하는 것입니다. 이 모든 일은 결코 국가의 품격을 떨어뜨리거나 위대한 국가로서의 명예와 영광을, 혹은 그 통치자들의 영광을 앗아 가는 것이 아니라, 다만 거짓되고 여전히 이교적이며 그릇된 길에서 참되고 마땅한 길로, 오직 영원한 목표로만 이끄는 그 길로 돌려세우는 것일 뿐입니다. 바로 이런 까닭에 『교회 관할권의 토대에 관하여』라는 책의 저자가, 만일 그 토대를 찾고 정립함에 있어 그것을 우리의 죄 많고 불완전한 시대에 불가피한 일시적 타협으로 여겼더라면, 옳게 판단한 것이 되었을 것입니다. 그러나 그 저자가 감히, 방금 이오시프 신부께서 일부 열거하신, 그가 지금 단언하는 그 토대들을 영구하고 본질적이며 영원한 토대라고 선언하는 순간, 그는 곧장 교회와 교회의 거룩하고 영원한 사명을 거스르는 셈이 됩니다. 이것이 제 논문의 골자입니다.\"\n\n\"다시 말하면, 요컨대,\" 파이시 신부가 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주며 다시 입을 열었다. \"십구 세기에 너무도 분명하게 정식화된 어떤 이론들에 따르면, 교회가 마치 낮은 형태에서 높은 형태로 나아가듯 국가로 변모하여 그 안으로 사라져 버려야 하며, 과학과 시대정신과 문명에 자리를 내주어야 한다는 것이오. 그리고 만일 교회가 저항하며 응하지 않으면, 국가 안에 무슨 한 귀퉁이가 떼어져 그녀에게 주어지되, 그것조차 통제 아래 놓이게 되는 것이오. 그리고 이것이 오늘날 모든 유럽 국가에서 도처에 그러할 것이오. 그러나 러시아의 희망과 관념은, 교회가 낮은 유형에서 높은 유형으로 옮아가듯 국가로 넘어가야 한다는 것이 아니라, 그와 반대로 국가가 마침내 오로지 교회가 될 자격을 갖추고 그 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어야 한다고 요구하는 것이오. 그렇게 될지어다! 그렇게 될지어다!\"\n\n\"음, 솔직히 말해 당신은 내 마음을 어느 정도 놓이게 해 주었소.\" 미우소프가 다시 다리를 꼬며 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"그러니까 내가 이해한 바로는, 그러한 이상의 실현이란 한없이 먼 일이로군, 그리스도의 재림 때나 가능한 일 말이오. 그것이야 당신들 좋을 대로 하시오. 전쟁과 외교와 은행 따위를 폐지하자는, 아름다운 유토피아의 꿈이로군요, 정말이지 사회주의 비슷한 어떤 것 말이오. 그러나 나는 그것이 죄다 진지하게 한 말인 줄로, 그래서 교회가 지금 당장이라도 죄인들을 재판하여 태형이나 투옥, 심지어 사형까지 선고하려는 줄로 알았소그려.\"\n\n\"그러나 만일 교회 재판소만이 있다면, 교회는 지금이라도 죄인에게 투옥이나 사형을 선고하지 않을 것입니다. 범죄와 그것을 보는 방식이 필연적으로 달라질 테니까요, 물론 한꺼번에는 아니겠으나 제법 머지않아 그리될 것입니다.\" 이반이 조금도 움츠러들지 않고 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"진심으로 하는 말이오?\" 미우소프가 그를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"만일 모든 것이 교회가 된다면, 교회는 모든 범죄자와 불순종하는 자를 배제하되 그들의 목을 베지는 않을 것입니다.\" 이반이 말을 이었다. \"그렇다면 묻겠습니다만, 배제된 자는 어찌 되겠습니까? 그때 그는 지금처럼 인간들로부터만이 아니라 그리스도로부터도 끊겨 나가게 되는 것입니다. 그는 자기 범죄로 인하여 인간들에게만이 아니라 그리스도의 교회에게도 죄를 범한 셈이 되니까요. 물론 엄밀히 말하면 지금도 그러하나, 그것이 분명하게 천명되어 있지 않아서, 오늘날의 범죄자는 아주아주 자주 제 양심과 타협하고 맙니다. '나는 도둑질을 한다,' 그는 이렇게 말하지요. '그러나 나는 교회를 거스르지는 않는다. 나는 그리스도의 적이 아니다.' 오늘날의 범죄자는 끊임없이 자기 자신에게 이렇게 되뇌는 것입니다. 그러나 교회가 국가를 대신하게 되면, 그가 온 세계의 교회에 맞서서 '모든 인간이 잘못 알고 있고, 모두가 오류에 빠져 있으며, 온 인류가 거짓 교회다. 도둑이요 살인자인 나야말로 유일한 참된 그리스도의 교회다'라고 말하기란 어려울 것입니다. 자기 자신에게 그렇게 말하기란 무척 어려운 일이니, 거기에는 보기 드물게 기이한 상황들이 한데 겹쳐야 합니다. 자, 이번에는 다른 쪽에서, 교회 자신이 범죄를 보는 관점을 들어 봅시다. 교회는 마땅히 지금의 거의 이교적인 태도를 버리고, 현재처럼 사회를 지키기 위해 오염된 지체를 기계적으로 잘라 내는 방식에서 벗어나, 인간의 갱생, 그의 교화와 구원이라는 사상을 온전하고 정직하게 받아들이는 쪽으로 변해야 하지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이오? 또 못 알아듣겠소그려.\" 미우소프가 가로막았다. \"또 무슨 꿈 같은 소리로군. 형체도 없고 도무지 알 수도 없는 어떤 것 말이오. 파문이란 게 대체 뭐요? 어떤 식의 배제란 말이오? 이반 표도로비치, 내 보기에 당신은 그저 혼자 재미 삼아 즐기고 있는 게 아닌가 싶소.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 하지만 실은 지금도 그러하다오.\" 장로가 불쑥 말하자 모두가 일제히 그에게로 돌아섰다. \"만일 그리스도의 교회가 없다면, 죄인이 악행을 저지르지 못하도록 막아 줄 것이 아무것도 없을 테고, 그 뒤에 그것에 대한 참된 징벌도 없을 것이오. 다시 말해 방금 이야기된 저 기계적인 형벌, 대다수의 경우 마음만 더 모질게 만들 뿐인 그 형벌 말고는 없을 것이며, 참된 형벌, 곧 유일하게 효력 있고 유일하게 억제하며 누그러뜨려 주는 그 형벌, 양심에 의한 죄의 자각 속에 깃든 그 형벌은 없을 것이오.\"\n\n\"어찌하여 그렇습니까, 여쭈어도 되겠는지요?\" 미우소프가 생생한 호기심을 띠며 물었다.\n\n\"이를테면,\" 장로가 말을 시작했다. \"이 모든 중노동 유형의 선고, 예전에는 거기에 태형까지 곁들였던 그 선고들이 아무도 교화하지 못하고, 더구나 죄인 한 사람조차 좀처럼 막지 못하며, 범죄의 수는 줄어들기는커녕 끊임없이 늘어만 가고 있소. 그대도 이 점은 인정해야 할 것이오. 따라서 사회의 안전은 지켜지지 않으니, 비록 그 해로운 지체가 기계적으로 잘려 나가 멀리 눈에 안 띄는 곳으로 보내진다 해도, 언제나 또 다른 죄인이 곧장 그 자리를 메우러 오고, 더러는 둘씩이나 오기 때문이오. 만일 무엇인가가, 우리 시대에라도, 사회를 지키고 또 죄인을 갱생시키고 변모시킨다면, 그것은 오로지 그의 양심 속에서 말하는 그리스도의 법뿐이오. 그가 그리스도교 사회의, 곧 교회의 아들로서 자기 죄과를 자각함으로써만, 그는 사회에 대한, 곧 교회에 대한 자기 죄를 자각하는 것이오. 그러므로 오늘날의 죄인이 자기가 죄를 지었음을 자각할 수 있는 것은 오로지 교회에 대해서이지 국가에 대해서가 아니오. 만일 사회가 교회로서 관할권을 지닌다면, 언제 배제로부터 다시 데려와 자신에게 합칠지를 알 것이오. 지금은 교회가 실질적인 관할권은 없고 다만 도덕적 단죄의 힘만을 지닌 채, 죄인을 능동적으로 벌하는 일에서 스스로 물러나 있소. 교회는 그를 파문하는 것이 아니라, 그저 어머니의 마음으로 그를 타이르기를 그치지 않을 따름이오. 더구나 교회는 죄인과의 온갖 그리스도교적 교류마저 지키려 애쓰오. 교회는 그를 예배에 받아들이고, 거룩한 성찬에 받아들이며, 그에게 자선을 베풀고, 그를 죄수라기보다 포로처럼 대하오. 오 주여, 만일 그리스도교 사회마저, 곧 교회마저 세속의 법이 그러하듯 그를 내치고 잘라 낸다면, 죄인은 대체 어찌 되겠소? 만일 교회가 세속법의 직접적 귀결로서 그를 파문으로 벌한다면, 그는 대체 어찌 되겠소? 적어도 러시아의 죄인에게는 그보다 더 끔찍한 절망이 없을 것이니, 러시아의 죄인들은 아직 믿음을 지니고 있기 때문이오. 하기야 누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 그때 끔찍한 일이 벌어져, 죄인의 절망에 빠진 마음이 그 믿음마저 잃어버릴는지도, 그러면 그는 또 어찌 되겠소? 그러나 교회는 자애롭고 사랑이 깊은 어머니처럼 능동적인 형벌에서 스스로 비켜서 있으니, 그 죄인은 이미 세속의 법으로 너무도 혹독하게 벌받고 있는 데다, 적어도 누군가 한 사람은 그를 가엾이 여겨야 하기 때문이오. 무엇보다도 교회가 비켜서 있는 까닭은, 교회의 판단만이 진리를 담고 있어, 따라서 실제로도 도덕적으로도 다른 어떤 판단과, 일시적 타협으로조차 결합될 수 없기 때문이오. 교회는 그것에 관하여 어떠한 협정도 맺을 수 없소. 외국의 죄인은 좀처럼 뉘우치지 않는다고들 하는데, 오늘날의 바로 그 교리들이 그에게, 제 범죄는 범죄가 아니라 부당하게 억압하는 힘에 대한 반작용일 뿐이라는 생각을 굳혀 주기 때문이오. 사회는 기계적으로 그를 짓이기는 힘으로써 그를 완전히 잘라 내고, (적어도 유럽에서는 자기들 스스로 그렇게 말하거니와) 이 배제에 증오와 망각을, 그리고 길 잃은 형제의 궁극적 운명에 대한 더없이 깊은 무관심을 곁들이오. 이런 식으로 모든 일이 교회의 자비로운 개입 없이 일어나니, 거기에는 많은 경우 교회라는 것이 아예 없기 때문이오. 비록 성직자들과 으리으리한 교회 건물은 남아 있을지언정, 교회 자체는 이미 오래전에 교회에서 국가로 옮아가 그 안으로 완전히 사라지려 애써 왔기 때문이오. 적어도 루터교 나라들에서는 그러해 보이오. 로마로 말하자면, 그것은 천 년 전에 이미 교회 대신 국가로 선포되었소. 그리하여 죄인은 더 이상 자기가 교회의 일원임을 의식하지 못하고 절망에 빠지오. 만일 그가 사회로 돌아가면, 흔히 그 증오가 어찌나 깊은지 사회 자체가 본능적으로 그를 잘라 내고 마오. 그것이 어떻게 끝날지는 그대 스스로 판단할 수 있을 것이오. 많은 경우 우리에게도 사정은 마찬가지인 듯 보이나, 다른 점이 있다면, 우리에게는 확립된 법정 외에 교회 또한 있어서, 그것이 죄인을 늘 소중하고 여전히 귀한 아들로서 관계를 이어 간다는 것이오. 그뿐 아니라, 비록 생각 속에서만일지언정 교회의 판단이 아직 보존되어 있으니, 그것이 실제로는 더 이상 존재하지 않더라도 미래를 향한 꿈으로서 여전히 살아 있고, 죄인 또한 그 영혼 속에서 본능적으로 그것을 알아본다는 데 의심의 여지가 없소. 방금 여기서 한 말 또한 진실이오, 다시 말해 만일 교회의 관할권이 그 온전한 힘으로 실제로 도입된다면, 곧 사회 전체가 교회로 바뀐다면, 교회의 판단이 지금으로서는 결코 미치지 못하는 식으로 죄인의 교화에 영향을 미칠 뿐 아니라, 어쩌면 범죄 자체도 믿기 어려울 만큼 줄어들 것이오. 그리고 교회가 미래의 죄인과 범죄를 많은 경우 사뭇 다르게 바라보고, 배제된 자를 다시 받아들이고, 악을 꾀하는 자를 억제하며, 타락한 자를 갱생시키는 데 성공하리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없소. 사실,\" 조시마 장로가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"지금의 그리스도교 사회는 아직 준비가 되어 있지 않고, 다만 일곱 명쯤의 의인들에게 의지하여 버티고 있을 따름이나, 그들이 결코 끊이지 않는 만큼, 거의 이교적 성격을 띤 사회에서 하나의 보편적이고 전능한 교회로 온전히 변모하기를 기다리며 여전히 흔들림 없이 이어질 것이오. 그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다! 비록 세상 끝에 가서라도 말이오, 그것은 반드시 이루어지도록 정해진 일이니! 그리고 그 시기와 때를 두고 마음 졸일 필요는 없으니, 시기와 때의 비밀은 신의 지혜 안에, 그 예지 안에, 그 사랑 안에 있기 때문이오. 인간의 셈으로는 아직 까마득해 보이는 것이, 신의 섭리로는 바로 코앞에, 그 출현의 전야에 와 있을 수도 있는 것이오. 그러니 그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다!\"\n\n\"그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다!\" 파이시 신부가 엄숙하고 경건하게 되뇌었다.\n\n\"이상하군요, 참으로 이상하오!\" 미우소프가, 격분이라기보다 억눌린 분노를 담아 내뱉었다.\n\n\"무엇이 그리도 이상하게 여겨지십니까?\" 이오시프 신부가 조심스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 이건 도무지 말도 안 되는 소리요!\" 미우소프가 불쑥 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"국가가 없어지고 교회가 국가의 자리로 올라선다니. 이건 단순한 교황지상주의가 아니라, 극도의 교황지상주의요! 교황 그레고리우스 칠세의 꿈마저 넘어서는 것이오!\"\n\n\"당신은 그것을 완전히 잘못 이해하고 있소.\" 파이시 신부가 준엄하게 말했다. \"잘 들으시오, 교회가 국가로 변모하는 것이 아니오. 그것은 로마이며 로마의 꿈이오. 그것은 악마의 세 번째 유혹이오. 그와 반대로, 국가가 교회로 변모하여, 위로 올라 온 세계를 아우르는 교회가 되는 것이니, 이는 교황지상주의나 로마와는, 또 당신의 해석과는 정반대되는 것이며, 오직 정교회를 위해 정해진 영광스러운 운명일 따름이오. 이 별은 동방에서 떠오를 것이오!\"\n\n미우소프는 의미심장하게 침묵했다. 그의 온 자태가 비범한 자존감을 드러냈다. 그의 입가에는 거만하고 내려다보는 듯한 미소가 감돌았다. 알료샤는 가슴을 두근거리며 이 모든 것을 지켜보았다. 그 대화 전체가 그의 마음을 깊이 뒤흔들었다. 그는 무심코 라키틴 쪽을 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 문 옆 제자리에 꼼짝도 않고 선 채, 눈은 내리뜨고 있었으나 골똘히 귀 기울이며 지켜보고 있었다. 그러나 그 두 뺨의 홍조로 미루어, 알료샤는 라키틴 역시 못지않게 흥분해 있으리라 짐작했고, 그 흥분의 까닭이 무엇인지도 알고 있었다.\n\n\"여러분, 한 가지 자그마한 일화를 들려드리는 것을 허락해 주시지요.\" 미우소프가 유난히 위엄 있는 태도로, 무게를 잡으며 말했다. \"몇 해 전, 십이월의 그 쿠데타가 있은 직후에, 나는 마침 파리에서 정부의 지극히 영향력 있는 한 인사를 방문하던 차에, 그의 집에서 무척 흥미로운 사람을 만난 적이 있소. 이 사람은 딱히 형사라기보다는, 정치 담당 형사들로 이루어진 한 부대 전체를 감독하는 일종의 책임자였는데, 그 나름으로 제법 막강한 자리였소. 호기심에 끌린 나는 그와 대화할 기회를 놓치지 않으려 했소. 그가 손님으로서가 아니라 특별 보고서를 가지고 온 하급 관리로서 와 있었던 데다, 그 상관이 나를 어떻게 맞이하는지를 보았던 까닭에, 그는 어느 정도 마음을 터놓고 이야기해 주는 영광을 베풀었소, 물론 어느 선까지만 말이오. 그는 솔직하다기보다는 정중한 편이었으니, 프랑스인들이, 특히 외국인에게 어떻게 정중할 줄 아는지는 다들 아는 바요. 하지만 나는 그를 속속들이 이해했소. 화제는 그 무렵 박해받고 있던 사회주의 혁명가들이었소. 이 사람이 무심코 흘린 더없이 기묘한 말 한 마디만 인용하겠소. '우리는 이 모든 사회주의자, 무정부주의자, 무신론자, 혁명가들을 그리 두려워하지 않습니다.' 그가 말했소. '우리는 그들을 감시하고 그들의 동향을 죄다 알고 있으니까요. 그러나 그들 중에는 신을 믿고 그리스도교도이면서 동시에 사회주의자인 몇몇 기이한 자들이 있습니다. 바로 이런 자들을 우리는 가장 두려워합니다. 그들은 무서운 사람들이지요! 그리스도교도인 사회주의자는 무신론자인 사회주의자보다 더 두려운 법입니다.' 그 말은 당시 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼는데, 여러분, 지금 여기서 그 말이 불현듯 다시 떠오르는구려.\"\n\n\"그 말을 우리에게 빗대시는군요, 그래서 우리를 사회주의자로 보시는 게요?\" 파이시 신부가 에둘러 가지 않고 곧장 물었다.\n\n그러나 표트르 알렉산드로비치가 무어라 답할지 미처 생각하기도 전에, 문이 열리며 그토록 오래 기다리던 손님 드미트리 표도로비치가 들어섰다. 사실 그들은 그가 오기를 단념하고 있던 터라, 그 갑작스러운 등장은 한순간 적잖은 놀라움을 자아냈다.\n\n# Chapter VI. Why Is Such A Man Alive?\n\n드미트리 표도로비치는 스물여덟 살의 청년으로, 중키에 호감 가는 용모를 지녔으나 나이보다 늙어 보였다. 근육질의 체격에 상당한 완력을 지닌 듯한 흔적이 엿보였다. 그런데도 그의 얼굴에는 어딘가 건강하지 못한 구석이 있었다. 얼굴은 다소 야위었고, 두 뺨은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 거기에는 건강하지 못한 누르께한 빛이 감돌았다. 다소 크고 불거진 검은 눈동자에는 굳은 결의의 표정이 서려 있으면서도, 동시에 어딘가 막연한 기색도 깃들어 있었다. 그가 흥분해서 짜증스럽게 떠들 때조차도 그의 두 눈은 어쩐 일인지 그의 기분을 그대로 따라가지 않고 무언가 다른 것을, 때로는 그 순간에 벌어지는 일과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 무언가를 드러내곤 했다. \"저 사람이 무슨 생각을 하는지 도무지 종잡을 수가 없어.\" 그와 이야기를 나눠 본 사람들이 더러 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그의 눈에서 무언가 우수에 잠긴, 침울한 기색을 읽어 낸 사람들은 그의 느닷없는 웃음에 흠칫 놀라곤 했는데, 눈은 그토록 음울한 바로 그 순간에 그 웃음은 즐겁고 경쾌한 생각이 일고 있음을 증언했기 때문이다. 이 순간 그의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 긴장된 빛은 이해하기 어렵지 않았다. 그가 근래 보내 온 더없이 들떠 있고 방탕한 생활에 대해서는, 또 아버지와의 다툼에서 그가 일으킨 격렬한 분노에 대해서는 누구나 알고 있거나 들어 본 적이 있었다. 그 일을 두고 고을에는 여러 가지 이야기가 떠돌았다. 사실 그는 천성이 성마른 사람으로, 우리 고을의 치안판사 카찰니코프가 그를 두고 적절하게 표현했듯이 \"불안정하고 균형이 잡히지 않은 정신\"의 소유자였다.\n\n그는 정성껏 단추를 채운 프록코트 차림에 멋스럽고 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입었다. 검은 장갑을 끼고 실크해트를 손에 들고 있었다. 군대를 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그는 아직 콧수염만 기르고 턱수염은 없었다. 짙은 갈색 머리는 짧게 깎아 관자놀이 쪽으로 앞으로 빗어 넘겼다. 그는 군인 특유의 길고 단호한 보폭으로 걸었다. 그는 문턱에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 좌중을 한번 훑어보더니 곧장 장로에게로 다가갔는데, 그를 이 자리의 주인이라고 짐작했던 것이다. 그는 장로에게 깊이 절하고 축복을 청했다. 조시마 장로가 의자에서 일어나 그를 축복했다. 드미트리는 공손하게 그의 손에 입을 맞추고, 격한 감정으로, 거의 노여움에 가까운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이토록 오래 기다리시게 한 저를 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 그러나 아버지께서 제게 보내신 하인 스메르자코프가 제 물음에 거듭 약속 시간이 한 시라고 일러 주었습니다. 그런데 이제 와서 갑자기 알고 보니—\"\n\n\"마음 쓰지 마시오.\" 장로가 말을 가로막았다. \"괜찮소. 조금 늦은 것뿐이오. 대수로운 일이 아니오…….\"\n\n\"참으로 감사합니다. 어른의 너그러우심에 그 이상은 바라지도 않았습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 드미트리는 한 번 더 절했다. 그러더니 갑자기 아버지 쪽으로 돌아서서 아버지에게도 똑같이 깊고 공손한 절을 올렸다. 그는 분명 미리 마음먹어 둔 듯, 자신의 존경과 선의를 보이는 것이 도리라 여기며 이 절을 자못 진지하게 올렸다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 불시에 허를 찔렸으나 그 자리에서 능히 대처했다. 드미트리의 절에 화답하여 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 아들에게 그에 못지않게 깊은 절을 돌려주었다. 그의 얼굴은 갑자기 엄숙하고 그럴듯해졌는데, 그 때문에 도리어 음흉하기 짝이 없는 표정이 되고 말았다. 드미트리는 자리에 있는 모든 사람에게 두루 인사한 뒤 한마디 말도 없이 그 길고 단호한 보폭으로 창가로 걸어가, 파이시 신부 곁의 단 하나뿐인 빈 의자에 앉더니, 몸을 앞으로 숙여 자기가 끊어 놓은 대화를 들을 채비를 했다.\n\n드미트리가 들어선 일은 채 이 분도 걸리지 않았고, 대화는 다시 이어졌다. 그러나 이번에는 미우소프가 파이시 신부의 집요하고도 거의 짜증스럽기까지 한 물음에 굳이 대답할 필요를 느끼지 못했다.\n\n\"이 논의에서는 저를 좀 빼 주십시오.\" 그가 점잖은 무관심을 곁들여 말했다. \"게다가 까다로운 문제이기도 하고요. 보아하니 이반 표도로비치가 우리를 보고 미소를 짓고 있군요. 저 사람도 거기에 대해 무언가 흥미로운 할 말이 있을 겁니다. 저 사람에게 물어보시지요.\"\n\n\"별다른 건 없습니다. 한 가지 자그마한 말씀만 드리지요.\" 이반이 즉시 대답했다. \"유럽의 자유주의자들 전반은, 심지어 우리네 자유주의 애호가들조차도, 사회주의의 궁극적인 결과를 그리스도교의 결과와 곧잘 뒤섞곤 합니다. 이런 터무니없는 생각은 물론 그 무리의 특징적인 면모지요. 하지만 사회주의와 그리스도교를 뒤섞는 것은 자유주의자나 애호가들뿐만이 아니라, 많은 경우 경찰까지도—물론 외국 경찰 말입니다—똑같이 그러는 모양입니다. 당신의 파리 일화는 제법 들어맞는 이야기로군요, 표트르 알렉산드로비치.\"\n\n\"이 주제는 아예 접도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.\" 미우소프가 거듭 말했다. \"그 대신, 여러분, 이반 표도로비치 본인에 관한 또 다른 흥미롭고 자못 특징적인 일화를 들려드리지요. 닷새 전, 여기서 주로 부인네들이 모인 한 자리에서, 이 사람은 논쟁 중에 이 세상 어디에도 사람으로 하여금 이웃을 사랑하게 만들 만한 것이 없다고 자못 엄숙하게 선언했습니다. 사람이 인류를 사랑해야 한다는 자연의 법칙 따위는 없으며, 만일 지금까지 이 땅에 사랑이라는 것이 있었다면 그것은 자연의 법칙 때문이 아니라 단지 사람들이 영혼 불멸을 믿어 왔기 때문이라는 거지요. 이반 표도로비치는 덧붙여 모든 자연의 법칙이 바로 그 믿음 안에 있으며, 만일 인류에게서 불멸의 믿음을 없애 버린다면 사랑뿐만 아니라 세계의 생명을 지탱하는 온갖 살아 있는 힘이 단숨에 말라 버릴 것이라고 했습니다. 그뿐만이 아닙니다. 그렇게 되면 그 무엇도 부도덕할 것이 없으며, 모든 것이, 식인 행위마저도 허용될 것이라고 했습니다. 그게 다가 아닙니다. 그는 마지막에 가서, 신도 불멸도 믿지 않는 우리 같은 사람 하나하나에게는 도덕적 자연 법칙이 곧장 종전의 종교적 법칙과 정반대의 것으로 바뀔 수밖에 없으며, 이기주의는, 심지어 범죄에 이르는 이기주의마저도, 그저 허용되는 것에 그치지 않고 오히려 그의 처지에서 비롯되는 불가피하고 가장 합리적이며 심지어 떳떳하기까지 한 귀결로 인정될 수밖에 없다고 단언했습니다. 이 역설만 보아도, 여러분, 우리의 그 별나고 역설적인 친구 이반 표도로비치의 나머지 이론들도 능히 짐작하실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 드미트리가 갑자기 소리쳤다. \"제가 제대로 들은 것이라면, 범죄는 비단 허용될 뿐만 아니라 신을 믿지 않는 모든 자에게 그 처지에서 비롯되는 불가피하고 가장 합리적인 귀결로 인정되어야 한다는 거로군요! 그렇습니까, 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 파이시 신부가 말했다.\n\n\"명심해 두지요.\"\n\n이 말을 내뱉고 나서 드미트리는 시작할 때처럼 갑작스레 입을 다물었다. 모든 사람이 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"불멸의 믿음이 사라진 결과에 대한 그것이 정말로 그대의 확신이오?\" 장로가 갑자기 이반에게 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그것이 제 주장이었습니다. 불멸이 없다면 미덕도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 믿는다면 그대는 복된 사람이거나, 아니면 더없이 불행한 사람이오.\"\n\n\"어째서 불행하다는 겁니까?\" 이반이 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 십중팔구 그대는 자기 영혼의 불멸도, 교회 재판권에 관해 그대 스스로 쓴 논문의 내용조차도 정작 믿지 않고 있기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 옳은 말씀일지도 모르지요! …… 하지만 제가 아주 농담만 한 것은 아니었습니다.\" 이반이 갑자기 묘한 어조로 고백하며 금세 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"아주 농담만 한 것은 아니었지. 그건 사실이오. 그 문제는 아직도 그대의 마음을 들쑤시고 있으며, 답을 얻지 못한 채로 있소. 그러나 순교자란 때때로 제 절망을 가지고 스스로를 달래기를 좋아하는 법이니, 마치 절망 그 자체에 떠밀려 그러는 것과도 같지. 그러는 사이 그대 역시 절망 속에서 잡지 기고문이며 사교계의 논쟁을 가지고 스스로를 달래고 있는데, 정작 자기 자신의 논리는 믿지 않으면서, 쓰라린 마음으로 속으로 그것을 비웃고 있소……. 그 문제에 그대는 답하지 못했고, 그것이 그대의 크나큰 슬픔이오. 그 문제가 답을 외쳐 부르고 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그러나 그것에 제가 답할 수 있겠습니까? 긍정으로 답할 수 있겠습니까?\" 이반은 여전히 그 알 수 없는 미소를 띤 채 장로를 바라보며 묘한 어조로 계속 물었다.\n\n\"그것이 긍정으로 결판날 수 없다면, 부정으로도 결코 결판나지 않을 것이오. 그대는 그것이 그대 마음의 특이한 점이며 그 마음의 온갖 고뇌가 거기서 비롯됨을 알고 있을 거요. 그러나 그토록 고뇌할 수 있는, 더 높은 것을 사색하고 추구할 수 있는 드높은 마음을 그대에게 주신 창조주께 감사드리시오. 우리의 거처는 하늘에 있으니 말이오. 부디 그대의 마음이 이 땅에서 답에 다다르기를, 또 신께서 그대의 길을 축복하시기를 비오.\"\n\n장로는 손을 들어 서 있던 자리에서 이반에게 십자가 성호를 그어 주려 했다. 그러나 이반은 자리에서 일어나 그에게 다가가 축복을 받고, 그 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 말없이 제자리로 돌아갔다. 그의 얼굴은 굳고 진지해 보였다. 이반답지 않은 이 행동과 그에 앞선 온갖 대화가 그 기이함과 어떤 엄숙함으로 모든 사람에게 깊은 인상을 남겨, 한순간 다들 입을 다물었고, 알료샤의 얼굴에는 거의 근심에 찬 빛이 떠올랐다. 그러나 미우소프는 느닷없이 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그리고 바로 그 순간 표도르 파블로비치가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"더없이 경건하고 거룩하신 장로님.\" 그가 이반을 가리키며 소리쳤다. \"저것이 제 아들, 제 살 중의 살이요, 제 살붙이 중에서도 가장 사랑스러운 놈입니다! 말하자면 저놈은 제게 더없이 효성스러운 카를 모어요, 방금 들어온 저 아들 드미트리, 제가 장로님께 정의를 구하고자 하는 저 불효한 놈은 프란츠 모어인 셈이지요—둘 다 실러의 「군도」에 나오는 인물들이니, 그렇다면 저는 군림하는 모어 백작이 되는 셈입니다! 저희를 심판하시고 구원해 주십시오! 저희에게는 장로님의 기도뿐만 아니라 예언도 필요합니다!\"\n\n\"광대짓은 그만두시오. 또한 댁의 가족부터 모욕하며 시작하지 마시오.\" 장로가 가냘프고 지친 목소리로 대답했다. 그는 보아하니 점점 더 피로해지고 있었고, 기력이 다해 가고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 여기 올 때 이미 내다보았던 꼴사나운 광대극이로구나!\" 드미트리가 분개해서 소리쳤다. 그 역시 벌떡 일어났다. \"용서하십시오, 존경하는 신부님.\" 그가 장로를 향해 말을 이었다. \"저는 교양 있는 사람이 못 되어 어른을 어떻게 불러야 옳은지조차 모릅니다. 그러나 어른은 속으셨고, 또 마음씨가 지나치게 좋으신 탓에 저희가 여기서 만나도록 허락하셨습니다. 제 아버지가 바라는 것이라곤 오직 한바탕 추문뿐입니다. 어째서 그것을 바라는지는 본인만이 말할 수 있겠지요. 저 사람에게는 늘 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있으니까요. 하지만 저는 그 까닭을 알 것 같습니다—\"\n\n\"다들 나를 탓하는구나, 죄다 나를 탓해!\" 이번에는 표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다. \"여기 이 표트르 알렉산드로비치도 나를 탓하고 있어. 자네는 나를 탓해 왔지, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 자네 말이야!\" 그가 갑자기 미우소프 쪽으로 돌아섰는데, 정작 미우소프는 그의 말을 가로막을 생각조차 없었다. \"다들 나더러 애들 몫의 돈을 장화 속에 숨기고 애들을 속여먹었다고 몰아붙이지. 하지만 법정이라는 것이 있지 않은가? 거기서 드미트리 표도로비치, 자네의 차용증서며 편지며 계약서를 가지고 따져 줄 걸세, 자네가 돈을 얼마나 가졌었고, 얼마나 썼으며, 얼마나 남았는지를 말이야. 어째서 표트르 알렉산드로비치가 판단하기를 마다하는 걸까? 드미트리가 그에게 남남도 아닌데 말이야. 그건 다들 나한테 등을 돌리고 있는데, 정작 드미트리 표도로비치는 나한테 빚을 졌기 때문이지. 그것도 적지 않은, 수천 루블이나 되는 빚을, 게다가 내겐 그걸 증명할 서류까지 있어. 저놈의 방탕질로 온 고을이 떠들썩하다네. 게다가 전에 주둔하던 곳에서는 변변한 처녀 하나를 유혹하려고 천 루블이고 이천 루블이고 여러 번 뿌려 댔지. 우리는 그 일을 죄다 알고 있어, 드미트리 표도로비치, 가장 은밀한 속속들이까지 말이야. 내가 증명해 보이지……. 거룩하신 신부님, 믿어지십니까, 저놈이 좋은 가문에 재산까지 갖춘, 더없이 명예로운 한 처녀의 마음을 사로잡았답니다. 그 애비는 용맹한 대령이요 한때 저놈의 상관이었던 사람으로, 숱한 훈장을 받고 가슴에 안나 훈장까지 단 분이지요. 저놈은 결혼하겠다는 약속으로 그 처녀의 처지를 곤란하게 만들어 놓았는데, 이제 그 애는 고아가 되어 여기 와 있고, 저놈과 약혼한 사이입니다. 그런데도 저놈은 그 애가 빤히 보는 앞에서 어떤 요부의 비위를 맞추며 알랑거리고 있단 말입니다. 이 요부가 말하자면 어떤 점잖은 사내와 내연 관계로 살아오기는 했어도, 어쨌거나 독립적인 성품의 여자라 누구도 범접 못 할 요새 같은 것이, 꼭 법적인 아내나 다름없지요—그 여자는 정숙하니까요, 그렇습니다, 거룩하신 신부님들, 그 여자는 정숙합니다. 드미트리 표도로비치는 그 요새를 황금 열쇠로 열어젖히려 하는 거고, 그래서 지금 나한테 이리 무례하게 굴며 돈을 우려내려 드는 겁니다. 그 요부한테 이미 수천 루블을 탕진해 놓고도 말이지요. 저놈은 그 목적으로 끊임없이 돈을 빌려 대고 있어요. 그게 누구한테서겠습니까? 말해 줄까, 미탸?\"\n\n\"입 다무세요!\" 드미트리가 소리쳤다. \"제가 나갈 때까지 기다리세요. 제 앞에서 감히 명예로운 처녀의 좋은 이름을 더럽히지 마세요! 그 사람에 관해 단 한마디라도 입에 올리는 것은 모욕이고, 저는 그것을 용납하지 않겠습니다!\"\n\n그는 숨이 막혀 했다.\n\n\"미탸! 미탸!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 신경질적으로 소리치며 눈물을 한 방울 쥐어짰다. \"그래, 네게는 애비의 축복이 아무것도 아니란 말이냐? 내가 너를 저주한다면, 그땐 어쩔 테냐?\"\n\n\"뻔뻔스러운 위선자!\" 드미트리가 격분하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"제 애비한테 저런 소릴 하다니! 애비한테 말이오! 그러니 남들한테야 오죽하겠습니까? 여러분, 한번 상상해들 보십시오. 여기 가난하지만 명예로운 사내가 살고 있는데, 식솔이 줄줄이 딸린 데다, 한때 곤경에 처해 군에서 면직당한 대위입니다. 그래도 공개적으로, 군법 회의를 거쳐, 명예에 흠집이 남은 채로 쫓겨난 건 아니지요. 그런데 삼 주 전에 드미트리가 술집에서 그자의 수염을 움켜잡고 거리로 끌어내 사람들이 보는 앞에서 두들겨 팼습니다. 그것도 그자가 내 자그마한 일거리에 심부름꾼 노릇을 했다는 이유 하나 때문에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 죄다 거짓말입니다! 겉으로는 사실이지만 속을 들여다보면 거짓말이에요!\" 드미트리는 분노로 부들부들 떨고 있었다. \"아버지, 저는 제 행동을 정당화하지 않겠습니다. 그래요, 사람들 앞에서 인정하지요. 저는 그 대위에게 짐승처럼 굴었고, 지금은 그것을 후회하며, 제 짐승 같은 분노에 스스로 진저리가 납니다. 하지만 그 대위는, 아버지의 그 심부름꾼은, 아버지가 요부라고 부르는 그 부인에게 찾아가, 아버지의 뜻을 받들어 이렇게 부추겼습니다. 아버지 수중에 있는 제 차용증서를 받아 두었다가, 만일 제가 아버지께 제 재산에 대한 셈을 끈질기게 요구하거든, 그것을 이용해 저를 상대로 소송을 걸어 감옥에 처넣으라고 말입니다. 그래 놓고 이제 와서 아버지가 그 부인을 향한 제 마음의 약점을 두고 저를 나무라는데, 정작 아버지 자신이 그 부인더러 저를 사로잡으라고 부추겼잖습니까! 그 사람이 제 얼굴에 대고 그렇게 말해 줬습니다……. 제게 그 이야기를 다 들려주며 아버지를 비웃었어요……. 아버지는 저를 감옥에 처넣으려 했는데, 그건 그 사람을 두고 저를 질투했기 때문이고, 아버지 자신이 그 사람에게 치근덕대기 시작했기 때문이지요. 저는 그것까지도 죄다 알고 있습니다. 그 사람은 그 일을 두고서도 아버지를 비웃었어요—들으셨습니까—그 일을 이야기하며 아버지를 비웃었다고요. 자, 그러니 여기 계신 것이 바로 이런 사내, 방탕한 아들을 나무라는 이런 애비입니다! 여러분, 제 노여움을 용서하십시오. 하지만 저는 이 교활한 늙은이가 그저 한바탕 추문을 일으키려고 여러분을 한자리에 불러 모았을 뿐이라는 것을 미리 내다보았습니다. 저는 저 사람이 손을 내민다면 용서하려고 왔던 겁니다. 용서하고, 또 용서를 구하려고요! 하지만 저 사람이 방금 이 순간 저뿐만 아니라, 제가 너무도 깊이 우러러 감히 그 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지도 못하는 한 명예로운 처녀까지 모욕했으니, 비록 제 애비라 할지라도 저 사람의 수작을 까발리기로 마음먹었습니다…….\"\n\n그는 더 말을 잇지 못했다. 두 눈은 번득였고 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다. 그러나 암자 안의 모든 사람이 술렁였다. 조시마 신부를 제외하고는 다들 불안한 기색으로 자리에서 일어났다. 수도사들은 근엄한 표정이었으나 장로의 지시를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 창백한 그 안색은 흥분 때문이 아니라 병으로 인한 쇠약 때문이었다. 그의 얼굴에는 애원하는 듯한 미소가 떠올랐고, 그는 이따금 마치 폭풍을 잠재우려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸으니, 물론 그의 손짓 하나면 이 소동을 끝내기에 충분했을 테지만, 그는 무언가를 기다리고 있는 듯, 자신에게 아직 온전히 또렷하지 않은 무언가를 헤아려 보려는 듯 그들을 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었다. 마침내 미우소프는 자신이 완전히 모욕당하고 망신을 당했다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"이 추잡한 꼴은 우리 모두의 탓입니다.\" 그가 격하게 말했다. \"그러나 저는 여기 올 때 이 사태를 미리 내다보지는 못했습니다, 제가 누구를 상대해야 하는지는 알고 있었지만 말입니다. 이건 당장 멈춰야 합니다! 믿어 주십시오, 신부님, 저는 방금 드러난 시시콜콜한 사정을 정확히 알지 못했고, 그것을 믿고 싶지도 않았으며, 지금 처음으로 알게 된 겁니다……. 애비가 행실이 헤픈 여자와 얽힌 제 아들의 관계를 질투하여, 그 여자와 작당해 자기 아들을 감옥에 처넣으려 하다니요! 이런 무리 속에 제가 어쩔 수 없이 끼게 된 겁니다! 저는 속았습니다. 여러분 모두에게 분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 그 누구 못지않게 속았던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 갑자기 부자연스러운 목소리로 악을 썼다. \"네가 내 아들만 아니라면 이 자리에서 당장 결투를 신청할 텐데…… 권총으로, 세 걸음 거리에서…… 손수건을 사이에 두고 말이야.\" 그가 두 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 말을 맺었다.\n\n평생토록 연기를 해 온 늙은 거짓말쟁이들에게는, 제 배역에 어찌나 완전히 몰입하는지 정말로 부들부들 떨거나 감정에 겨워 눈물까지 흘리는 순간이 있는 법인데, 정작 바로 그 순간이나 그 일 초 뒤에는 스스로에게 이렇게 속삭일 수 있는 것이다. \"너도 알다시피 넌 거짓말을 하고 있는 거야, 이 뻔뻔스러운 늙은 죄인아! 넌 지금 그 '거룩한' 분노에도 불구하고 연기를 하고 있는 거라고.\"\n\n드미트리는 고통스럽게 얼굴을 찡그리며 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸의 눈초리로 아버지를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저는 생각했습니다…… 저는 생각했어요.\" 그가 부드럽고, 말하자면 억누른 목소리로 말했다. \"제 마음의 천사, 제 약혼녀와 함께 고향으로 돌아와 늙은 아버지의 노년을 보살펴 드리려는 줄로요. 그런데 와서 보니 타락한 방탕아, 비열한 어릿광대밖에 없군요!\"\n\n\"결투다!\" 그 늙은 비렁뱅이가 또다시 악을 썼는데, 음절마다 숨이 차서 침을 튀겨 가며 외쳤다. \"그리고 자네, 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프, 내 말해 두지만, 자네 온 집안을 통틀어도 자네가 감히 그렇게 불러 댄 그 '계집'보다 더 드높고 더 정직한—들었나—더 정직한 여자는 단 한 명도 없었어! 또 드미트리 표도로비치 너는 그 '계집'을 위해 약혼녀를 내팽개쳤으니, 결국 네 약혼녀가 그 여자한테 발끝에도 못 미친다고 너 스스로 생각했다는 거 아니냐. 바로 그런 여자가 '계집'이라 불린 거란 말이다!\"\n\n\"수치스럽습니다!\" 이오시프 신부의 입에서 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"수치스럽고 망측합니다!\" 칼가노프가 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 소년 같은 목소리로, 감정에 겨워 떨면서 외쳤다. 그는 그 순간까지 줄곧 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n\"어째서 저런 인간이 살아 있는 겁니까?\" 드미트리가 분노로 제정신을 잃은 채 공허한 목소리로 으르렁대며, 어깨를 잔뜩 웅크려 거의 기형처럼 보일 지경이었다. \"말씀해 주십시오, 저자가 계속 이 땅을 더럽히도록 내버려 두어도 되는 겁니까?\" 그는 모든 사람을 둘러보며 늙은이를 손가락질했다. 그는 차분하고 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"들어들 보시오, 들어 보시오, 수도사 여러분, 애비를 죽이겠다는 소리를!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 이오시프 신부에게 달려들며 소리쳤다. \"그게 바로 당신의 그 '수치스럽다!'는 말에 대한 대답이오! 무엇이 수치스럽단 말이오? 그 '계집', 그 '행실 헤픈 여자'가 어쩌면 구원을 찾는다는 당신네 수도사들보다 더 거룩할지도 모르지요! 그 여자는 어쩌면 젊은 시절에 타락했을지도 모르오, 제 환경에 망가져서 말이오. 하지만 그 여자는 많이 사랑했고, 그리스도께서도 친히 '많이 사랑한' 그 여인을 용서하셨지 않소.\"\n\n\"그리스도께서 그 여인을 용서하신 것은 그런 사랑 때문이 아니었습니다.\" 온화한 이오시프 신부의 입에서 참다못해 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"아니오, 바로 그런 사랑 때문이었소, 수도사 여러분, 그랬다니까요! 당신들은 여기서 양배추나 뜯어 먹으며 영혼을 구한답시고, 스스로 의롭다고 여기고 있소. 하루에 모샘치 한 마리를 먹으면서 그 모샘치로 신을 매수한다고 생각하는 거요.\"\n\n\"이건 참을 수 없습니다!\" 암자 사방에서 이런 소리가 터져 나왔다.\n\n그러나 이 꼴사나운 소동은 더없이 뜻밖의 방식으로 끝이 났다. 조시마 신부가 갑자기 자리에서 일어났다. 장로와 다른 모든 사람을 향한 염려로 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었던 알료샤가 그래도 가까스로 장로의 팔을 부축했다. 조시마 신부는 드미트리 쪽으로 다가가, 그에게 이르러 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. 알료샤는 그가 쇠약해서 쓰러진 줄 알았으나 그렇지 않았다. 장로는 또렷하고도 의연하게 드미트리의 발치에 몸을 굽혀 이마가 바닥에 닿도록 절했다. 알료샤는 너무도 놀란 나머지 그가 다시 일어설 때 부축하는 것조차 잊었다. 장로의 입가에는 희미한 미소가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"잘들 계시오! 모두들 나를 용서하시오!\" 그가 손님들에게 사방으로 절하며 말했다.\n\n드미트리는 한동안 어안이 벙벙해서 서 있었다. 자기에게 절을 하다니—그게 대체 무슨 뜻인가? 그는 갑자기 \"오, 하느님!\" 하고 큰 소리로 외치며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 다른 손님들도 모두 그를 뒤따라 우르르 밖으로 나갔는데, 당황한 나머지 작별 인사도, 주인에 대한 절도 하지 못했다. 다만 수도사들만이 다시 그에게 다가가 축복을 청했다.\n\n\"저렇게 발치에 엎드린 게 대체 무슨 뜻일까? 무슨 상징인가, 뭔가?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 갑자기 잠잠해져서, 누구를 콕 집어 말할 엄두는 내지 못한 채 대화를 다시 트려고 애쓰며 말했다. 그들은 마침 암자 경내를 빠져나오고 있는 참이었다.\n\n\"나는 미친 집이며 미친 자들에 대해서는 책임질 수 없소.\" 미우소프가 즉시 언짢은 기색으로 대답했다. \"하지만 표도르 파블로비치, 당신과의 동행만은 면하도록 하겠소, 그것도 영영, 내 말을 믿으시오. 그 수도사는 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"그 수도사\", 곧 그들을 원장과의 만찬에 초대했던 그 수도사는 그들을 기다리게 하지 않았다. 그들이 장로의 암자 계단을 내려서기가 무섭게, 마치 내내 그들을 기다리고 있었다는 듯 그가 그들을 맞이했다.\n\n\"존경하는 신부님, 부디 제 청을 하나 들어주십시오. 원장 신부님께 저의 깊은 경의를 전해 주시고, 미우소프 본인이 직접 사죄드린다고, 뜻하지 않은 사정으로 그분의 식탁에 함께하는 영광을 누리지 못하게 되어 진심으로 유감스러우며, 그러기를 그토록 간절히 바라면서도 어쩔 수 없다고 신부님께 말씀드려 주십시오.\" 미우소프가 짜증스레 수도사에게 말했다.\n\n\"그 뜻하지 않은 사정이란 게 물론 나를 두고 하는 말이지요.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 즉시 끼어들었다. \"들으셨소, 신부님? 이 양반은 나하고 같이 있기 싫은 거요, 그게 아니면 당장 가셨겠지요. 그러니 가시오, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 어서 원장님께 가시구려, 그리고 맛있게 드시오. 사양하는 건 당신이 아니라 나일 테니. 집으로, 집으로 가겠소, 나는 집에서 먹지. 여기서는 도무지 그럴 마음이 안 나는구려, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 내 다정한 친척 양반.\"\n\n\"나는 당신 친척이 아니고, 여태껏 친척이었던 적도 없소, 이 비열한 인간!\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 약 올리려고 일부러 그렇게 말한 거요. 당신이야 늘 그 인척 관계를 부인하지만, 아무리 발뺌해도 당신은 실은 내 친척이거든. 교회 달력으로 증명해 보이지. 이반, 너는 남고 싶으면 남거라. 나중에 말을 보내 주마. 격식상 원장님께 가야 하는 거요, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 우리가 일으킨 소란을 사죄하러 말이오…….\"\n\n\"정말로 집으로 가는 거요? 거짓말은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"표트르 알렉산드로비치! 이런 일이 벌어진 마당에 내가 어찌 감히 가겠소! 용서들 하시오, 여러분, 내가 그만 흥분했던 거요! 게다가 속까지 뒤집어졌고! 그리고 솔직히 부끄럽기도 하오. 여러분, 어떤 사람은 마케도니아의 알렉산드로스 같은 심장을 지녔고, 또 어떤 사람은 강아지 피도 같은 심장을 지녔지요. 내 것은 그 강아지 피도의 심장이오. 부끄럽소이다! 그런 추태를 부려 놓고 무슨 낯으로 만찬에 가서 수도원의 소스를 게걸스레 먹어 치우겠소? 부끄러워서, 도저히 못 하겠소. 부디 양해해 주시구려!\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 만일 저자가 우리를 속이는 거라면 어쩐다?\" 미우소프는 여전히 망설이며, 멀어져 가는 그 어릿광대를 미심쩍은 눈으로 지켜보면서 생각했다. 어릿광대는 뒤를 돌아보더니, 미우소프가 자기를 지켜보고 있는 것을 알아채고는 그에게 손으로 입맞춤을 날려 보냈다.\n\n\"그래, 자네는 원장께 가겠나?\" 미우소프가 이반에게 퉁명스레 물었다.\n\n\"안 갈 까닭이 있나요? 어제 특별히 초대받았는걸요.\"\n\n\"유감스럽게도 나는 이 빌어먹을 만찬에 가야만 할 것 같군.\" 미우소프가 수도사가 듣고 있다는 사실도 아랑곳없이 똑같은 짜증스러운 어조로 말했다. \"우리는 적어도 소란을 일으킨 데 대해 사죄하고, 그게 우리 탓이 아니었음을 해명해야 하니까. 자네 생각은 어떤가?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그게 우리 탓이 아니었음을 해명해야지요. 게다가 아버지도 거기 안 계실 테고요.\" 이반이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지, 정말 그래야 할 거야! 이 빌어먹을 만찬 같으니!\"\n\n그래도 그들은 모두 걸음을 옮겼다. 수도사는 잠자코 듣고 있었다. 잡목 숲을 가로지르는 길에서 그가 한 가지만은 일러 주었으니—원장 신부님이 오래도록 기다리고 계시며, 그들이 삼십 분도 넘게 늦었다는 것이었다. 그는 아무 대답도 듣지 못했다. 미우소프는 증오에 찬 눈으로 이반을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저자는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 만찬에 가는군.\" 그는 생각했다. \"뻔뻔스러운 낯짝에다, 카라마조프의 양심을 지닌 놈이야!\"\n\n# Chapter VII. A Young Man Bent On A Career\n\n알료샤는 조시마 장로를 부축해 침실로 모시고 침대에 앉혀 드렸다.\n방은 꼭 필요한 세간만 갖추어진 자그마한 곳이었다. 좁은 철제 침대가 놓여 있었고, 요라고는 펠트 한 조각이 깔려 있을 뿐이었다. 한쪽 구석, 이콘 아래에는 십자가와 복음서가 얹힌 독서대가 있었다. 장로는 기진맥진하여 침대에 주저앉았다. 그의 두 눈은 번득였고 숨결은 거칠었다. 그는 마치 무언가를 곰곰 헤아리는 듯, 알료샤를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"가거라, 얘야, 가거라. 내게는 포르피리만 있으면 충분하다. 어서 가거라, 거기서 네가 필요하니, 가서 원장 신부님 식탁에서 시중을 들어야 한다.\"\n\n\"여기 남게 해 주십시오.\" 알료샤가 애원했다.\n\n\"네가 더 필요한 곳은 거기다. 거기엔 평안이 없으니. 너는 시중을 들며 도움이 되어야 한다. 악령이 일어나거든 기도문을 외워라. 그리고 잊지 말아라, 얘야\"—장로는 그를 그렇게 부르기를 좋아했다—\"이곳은 앞으로 네가 머물 자리가 아니다. 하느님께서 나를 부르실 뜻을 세우시거든, 수도원을 떠나거라. 영영 떠나거라.\"\n\n알료샤가 흠칫했다.\n\n\"왜 그러느냐? 지금으로서는 이곳이 네 자리가 아니다. 나는 너에게 세상에서 큰 봉사를 하도록 축복을 내린다. 너의 앞에는 기나긴 순례가 있을 것이다. 너는 또한 아내를 맞아야 할 것이다. 너는 돌아오기 전에 모든 것을 견뎌 내야 한다. 할 일이 많을 것이다. 그러나 나는 너를 의심하지 않으니, 그래서 너를 내보내는 것이다. 그리스도께서 너와 함께하신다. 그분을 저버리지 말아라, 그러면 그분도 너를 저버리지 않으실 것이다. 너는 큰 슬픔을 보게 될 것이며, 그 슬픔 속에서 너는 행복할 것이다. 이것이 너에게 남기는 나의 마지막 당부다. 슬픔 속에서 행복을 찾아라. 일하라, 쉬지 말고 일하라. 나의 말을 기억하여라, 비록 너와 다시 이야기를 나누게 되겠지만, 나의 날뿐만 아니라 나의 시간까지도 이미 다 헤아려졌으니.\"\n\n알료샤의 얼굴에 또다시 격한 감정이 드러났다. 입꼬리가 떨렸다.\n\n\"또 왜 그러느냐?\" 조시마 장로가 부드럽게 미소 지으며 물었다. \"세속 사람들은 죽은 자를 눈물로 배웅하지만, 이곳에서는 떠나가는 아버지를 두고 기뻐한다. 우리는 기뻐하며 그를 위해 기도한다. 나를 두고 가거라, 나는 기도를 드려야 한다. 가거라, 어서 서둘러라. 네 형들 곁에 있어라. 한 사람 곁이 아니라, 두 사람 모두의 곁에 있어라.\"\n\n조시마 장로가 그를 축복하려고 손을 들어 올렸다. 알료샤는 그토록 남아 있고 싶었으나 거역할 수 없었다. 게다가 그는 드미트리에게 절을 한 것이 무슨 뜻인지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수 없었으니, 그 물음이 혀끝까지 차올랐으나 감히 입 밖에 내지 못했다. 그는 장로가 적절하다고 여겼다면 묻지 않아도 설명해 주었으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 그것은 분명 장로의 뜻이 아니었다. 그 행동은 알료샤에게 무서운 인상을 남겼으니, 그는 거기에 깃든 신비로운 의미를 맹목적으로 믿었다. 신비롭고, 어쩌면 끔찍한 의미를.\n\n원장 신부의 만찬에서 시중을 들기 위해 제때 수도원에 닿으려고 암자 경내를 서둘러 빠져나오던 그는, 문득 가슴이 미어지는 듯한 통증을 느끼며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그는 다가올 자신의 임종을 예언하던 조시마 장로의 말이 다시금 들려오는 것만 같았다. 그토록 정확히 예언하신 것은 틀림없이 그대로 이루어지리라. 알료샤는 그것을 추호도 의심 없이 믿었다. 그러나 그를 잃고서 어찌 살아간단 말인가? 그를 보지도 못하고 그의 말을 듣지도 못한 채 어찌 살아간단 말인가? 어디로 가야 한단 말인가? 그는 울지 말라고, 수도원을 떠나라고 했다. 오, 하느님! 알료샤가 이런 비통함을 맛본 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다. 그는 수도원과 암자를 가르는 작은 숲을 가로질러 황급히 걸으면서, 제 생각의 무게를 견딜 수 없어 길가의 늙은 소나무들을 물끄러미 바라보았다. 갈 길은 멀지 않았으니—오백 걸음쯤이었다. 그는 그 시각에 아무도 마주치지 않으리라 여겼으나, 길의 첫 모퉁이에서 라키틴을 발견했다. 그는 누군가를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"나를 기다리는 건가?\" 알료샤가 그를 따라잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 라키틴이 히죽 웃었다. \"원장 신부님께 서둘러 가는 길이지, 나도 알아. 거기 잔치가 벌어졌으니까. 원장이 주교님과 파하토프 장군을 대접하던 그때 이후로 이런 잔치는 처음이지, 기억나나? 나야 거기 가지 않겠지만, 자넨 가서 소스나 받쳐 들고 다니게. 한 가지만 묻세, 알렉세이, 그 광경은 대체 무슨 뜻인가? 그걸 묻고 싶어서 그래.\"\n\n\"무슨 광경 말인가?\"\n\n\"자네 형 드미트리에게 절을 한 거 말일세. 게다가 이마로 땅을 톡톡 두드리기까지 했잖나!\"\n\n\"조시마 장로님 이야기인가?\"\n\n\"그래, 조시마 장로 이야기일세.\"\n\n\"땅을 두드렸다고?\"\n\n\"아, 불경한 표현이로군! 그래, 그게 뭐 어때서? 어쨌든 그 광경이 무슨 뜻이냔 말일세.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인지 나도 모르네, 미샤.\"\n\n\"자네한테 설명해 주지 않을 줄 알았어! 물론 거기에 신통할 건 하나도 없지, 흔해 빠진 성스러운 광대놀음일 뿐이니까. 하지만 그 연극에는 목적이 있었다네. 고을의 신심 깊은 사람들이 죄다 그 이야기를 떠들어 대며 무슨 뜻이었을까 궁금해하면서 온 고장에 퍼뜨릴 거야. 내 생각에 그 늙은이는 정말이지 코가 예민해. 범죄 냄새를 맡은 거지. 자네 집안에서 그 냄새가 풍기거든.\"\n\n\"무슨 범죄 말인가?\"\n\n라키틴은 분명 무언가 이야기하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다.\n\n\"자네 집안에서 벌어질 범죄 말이야, 이 범죄가. 자네 형들과 그 돈 많은 늙은 아버지 사이에서 말일세. 그래서 조시마가 무슨 일이 터질지 대비하려고 넙죽 엎드린 거야. 나중에 무슨 일이 벌어지면 이렇게들 말하겠지. '아, 그 성자께서 미리 내다보셨다, 예언하셨다!' 하지만 그렇게 넙죽 엎드리는 것쯤이야 시시한 예언이지. '아, 하지만 그건 상징이었어,' 사람들은 이렇게 말할 거야. '하나의 알레고리였지,' 그러고는 또 무슨 소릴 늘어놓을지 귀신이나 알겠지! 그것이 그의 영광으로 길이 기억될 거야. '그분은 범죄를 예언하셨고 범인을 지목하셨다!' 하고 말이지. 이런 미치광이 광신도들은 늘 그런 식이야. 술집에서 성호를 긋고 성전에 돌을 던진다니까. 자네네 장로처럼 말이지, 의인에게는 몽둥이를 들이대고 살인자의 발치엔 엎드리거든.\"\n\n\"무슨 범죄 말인가? 무슨 살인자 말이야? 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 건가?\"\n\n알료샤가 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 라키틴도 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"무슨 살인자라니? 자넨 모르는 척하는군! 자네도 진작에 그 생각을 했을 거라고 장담하네. 게다가 그것 또한 흥미롭단 말이지. 들어 보게, 알료샤, 자넨 늘 진실을 말하면서도 늘 이쪽도 저쪽도 아닌 어정쩡한 처지에 있잖아. 그 생각을 했나, 안 했나? 대답해 보게.\"\n\n\"했네.\" 알료샤가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다. 라키틴마저 흠칫했다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 정말로?\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"나는… 정확히 그 생각을 한 건 아니야.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 자네가 그렇게 이상한 말을 꺼내자마자, 나도 그 생각을 했었구나 싶더군.\"\n\n\"봤지? (게다가 표현도 어찌나 잘했는지!) 오늘 자네 아버지와 형 미탸를 보면서 자넨 범죄를 떠올린 거야. 그럼 내가 틀린 게 아니지?\"\n\n\"하지만 잠깐, 잠깐만 기다려.\" 알료샤가 불안한 듯 말을 막았다. \"자네는 어떻게 이 모든 걸 알아챈 거지? 왜 그게 자네 관심사인가? 그게 첫 번째 물음일세.\"\n\n\"두 가지 물음이로군, 서로 동떨어졌지만 자연스러운 물음이야. 따로따로 답해 주지. 내가 어떻게 알아챘느냐고? 자네 형 드미트리를 별안간 이해하고, 단번에 그 속마음 깊은 곳까지 꿰뚫어 보지 못했더라면 나도 알아채지 못했을 거야. 나는 단 하나의 특징에서 그 사람 전부를 잡아냈거든. 이렇게 아주 정직하지만 격정적인 사람들에게는 결코 넘어서는 안 될 선이 하나 있단 말이지. 만약 그 선을 넘으면, 그는 칼을 들고 자네 아버지에게 달려들 거야. 그런데 자네 아버지로 말하자면 술에 절어 막돼먹은 늙은 죄인이라 도무지 선을 그을 줄 모르거든—두 사람이 다 제멋대로 굴면, 둘 다 파멸하고 마는 거지.\"\n\n\"아니야, 미샤, 아니야. 그게 전부라면, 자넨 나를 안심시킨 거네. 그런 일은 일어나지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 떨고 있나? 내 말해 주지. 우리 미탸가 정직할 순 있어 (그는 어리석지만 정직하지), 하지만 그는—호색한이야. 그게 바로 그의 정의이자 본질적인 속성이지. 자네 아버지가 그 천박한 호색을 그에게 물려준 거야. 정말이지 난 자네가 신기하단 말이야, 알료샤, 어떻게 그 순결함을 지킬 수 있었는지. 자네도 카라마조프잖나! 자네 집안에선 호색이 병적인 지경까지 치닫는단 말이지. 그런데 지금, 이 세 호색한이 허리춤에 칼을 차고 서로를 노려보고 있어. 셋이서 머리를 맞부딪치고 있는데, 자넨 어쩌면 그 넷째가 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"자넨 그 여자를 잘못 알고 있어. 드미트리는—그 여자를 경멸한다네.\" 알료샤가 일종의 몸서리를 치며 말했다.\n\n\"그루셴카를? 아니야, 형제, 그는 그 여자를 경멸하지 않아. 그 여자 때문에 약혼녀를 버린 마당에, 그 여자를 경멸할 리가 없지. 여기엔 말일세, 친구, 자네가 아직 이해하지 못하는 무언가가 있어. 사내란 어떤 미녀에게, 여자의 몸에, 심지어 여자 몸의 어느 한 부분에까지 반할 수 있는 법이거든 (호색한이라면 이걸 이해하지), 그러면 그 여자를 위해 제 자식들도 버리고, 제 아비 어미도 팔아넘기고, 제 조국 러시아까지도 팔아넘기는 거야. 정직한 자라면 도둑질을 할 거고, 인정 많은 자라면 살인을 할 거고, 신의 있는 자라면 배신을 할 거야. 여인의 발을 노래한 시인 푸시킨은 시구에서 그 발을 찬미했지. 다른 자들은 발을 찬미하진 않아도, 그 발을 보면 전율하지 않을 수 없거든—그리고 그게 발만은 아니지. 여기서는 경멸도 소용이 없어, 형제, 설령 그가 그루셴카를 경멸한다 해도 말이지. 정말 경멸하긴 해, 하지만 그 여자에게서 헤어나질 못한단 말이야.\"\n\n\"나는 그걸 이해하네.\" 알료샤가 별안간 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"정말로? 흠, 첫마디에 그렇게 불쑥 내뱉는 걸 보니 정말 이해하는 모양이로군.\" 라키틴이 심술궂게 말했다. \"그건 자네도 모르게 새어 나온 거니, 그 고백이 더더욱 값진 거지. 그러니 익숙한 주제로군. 자넨 이미 그 생각을 했었던 거야, 호색에 관해서 말이지! 오, 이 동정의 영혼아! 자넨 얌전한 친구야, 알료샤, 자넨 성자지, 나도 알아, 하지만 자네가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 이미 무엇을 알고 있는지는 귀신이나 알겠지! 자넨 순결하지만, 심연의 밑바닥까지 내려갔다 왔어… 난 오래전부터 자넬 지켜봐 왔다네. 자네 자신도 카라마조프야. 자넨 철두철미한 카라마조프거든—혈통과 선택이라는 게 분명 책임져야 할 몫이 있는 거지. 자넨 아버지에게서 호색을, 어머니에게서 미치광이 같은 성자의 기질을 물려받은 거야. 왜 떠는 건가? 그럼 정말인가? 자네 알아? 그루셴카가 나더러 자넬 데려오라고 졸라 댔다네. '내가 그자의 사제복을 벗겨 줄게,' 이러더군. 그 여자가 자넬 데려오라고 얼마나 졸라 대는지 자넨 짐작도 못 할 거야. 난 그 여자가 왜 자네한테 그렇게 관심을 두는지 의아했지. 자네 알아, 그 여자도 보통 여자가 아니거든!\"\n\n\"고맙다고 전하고 난 가지 않겠다고 하게.\" 알료샤가 억지 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"하던 이야기를 마저 해 보게, 미샤. 내 생각은 그다음에 말해 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"마저 할 것도 없네. 다 뻔하니까. 늘 똑같은 가락이지, 형제. 자네마저도 속으로는 호색한이라면, 자네 형 이반은 또 어떻겠나? 그자도 카라마조프잖아. 자네들 카라마조프 일족의 뿌리에 박힌 게 바로 그거란 말이야, 자네들은 죄다 음탕하고 탐욕스럽고 미쳐 있다는 거! 자네 형 이반은 무신론자면서도, 무슨 알 수 없는 멍청한 동기로 신학 논문을 장난삼아 쓰고, 게다가 자기 입으로 그게 사기라고 인정하지—그게 자네 형 이반이야. 그자는 미탸의 약혼녀를 제 것으로 차지하려 하고 있고, 내 보기엔 성공할 것도 같아. 더구나 그게 미탸의 동의를 얻어서란 말이지. 미탸야 그 여자를 떨쳐 버리고 그루셴카에게 도망치려고 약혼녀를 그자에게 넘겨줄 테니까. 그리고 그자는 그 온갖 고결함과 무사무욕에도 불구하고 기꺼이 그럴 채비가 돼 있단 말이야. 잘 보게. 그런 자들이야말로 가장 끔찍한 인간들이지! 자넨 도대체 그자를 알아볼 수가 없어! 그자는 제 비열함을 인정하면서도 계속 그걸 밀고 나간단 말이야! 한 가지 더 말해 주지, 자네 아버지, 그 늙은이가 지금 미탸의 앞길을 가로막고 있다네. 그 늙은이가 그루셴카에게 갑자기 미쳐 버렸거든. 그 여자만 보면 입에 군침을 흘린다니까. 아까 방에서 그런 소동을 벌인 것도 순전히 그 여자 때문이었어, 단지 미우소프가 그 여자를 '막돼먹은 계집'이라고 불렀다는 이유로 말이지. 그자는 사랑에 빠진 수고양이보다도 못한 작자야. 처음에 그 여자는 그저 그자의 술집 일이나 다른 구린 사업에 부려졌을 뿐인데, 이제 그자는 그 여자가 어떤 존재인지 별안간 깨닫고 그 여자에게 미쳐 날뛰는 거지. 그자는 그 여자에게 끊임없이 제안을 들이밀며 귀찮게 굴어, 물론 떳떳한 제안은 아니고 말이지. 그러니 이 잘난 부자가 그 길 위에서 정면으로 충돌하고 말 거란 말일세! 하지만 그루셴카는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 편들지 않아, 여전히 둘을 가지고 놀면서 어느 쪽에게서 더 많이 우려낼 수 있을까 따져 보며 둘 다 애를 태우고 있지. 아비한테서야 큰돈을 우려낼 수 있겠지만 그자가 그 여자와 결혼하진 않을 테고, 어쩌면 결국 인색하게 굴며 지갑을 꽁꽁 닫아 버릴지도 모르거든. 바로 거기서 미탸의 값어치가 빛을 발하는 거야. 그자는 돈은 없어도 그 여자와 결혼할 채비가 돼 있으니까. 그래, 결혼할 채비가 돼 있단 말이지! 부유하고 대령의 딸인, 보기 드문 미녀 카테리나 이바노브나라는 약혼녀를 버리고, 방탕한 늙은 상인 삼소노프—거칠고 무식한 시골 시장 나부랭이의 정부 노릇을 하던 그루셴카와 결혼하겠다는 거야. 이 모든 일에서 살인의 충돌이 벌어질 만도 하고, 바로 그것이 자네 형 이반이 기다리는 바라네. 그자에게는 더없이 안성맞춤일 테니까. 그자는 애타게 그리는 카테리나 이바노브나를 채어 가고, 그 여자의 육만 루블 지참금을 챙길 거야. 그것만으로도 보잘것없고 거지 신세인 사내에겐 무척 솔깃한 일이지. 게다가 잘 보게, 그자는 미탸에게 해를 끼치는 게 아니라 도리어 더없이 큰 봉사를 베푸는 셈이 되거든. 사실인즉 바로 지난주에 미탸가 술에 취해 어느 술집에서 집시 계집들과 어울리다가 큰 소리로 외쳤다는 걸 내가 똑똑히 아는데, 자기는 약혼녀 카탸에게 어울리지 않는 자이고 형 이반이야말로 그 여자를 차지할 만한 사내라고 했다는 거야. 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나도 결국에는 이반처럼 매혹적인 사내를 거절하진 못할 거란 말이지. 그 여자는 이미 둘 사이에서 망설이고 있거든. 그런데 그 이반이란 자가 어떻게 자네들 모두를 사로잡아서, 자네들이 다 그자를 떠받들게 만든 걸까? 그자는 자네들을 비웃으며 자네들을 발판 삼아 즐기고 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"자넨 그걸 어떻게 아나? 어떻게 그렇게 장담할 수 있지?\" 알료샤가 미간을 찌푸리며 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"왜 묻는 건가, 게다가 내 대답을 두려워하면서 말이지? 그건 내가 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 자네가 안다는 증거야.\"\n\n\"자넨 이반을 좋아하지 않아. 이반은 돈에 넘어갈 사람이 아니야.\"\n\n\"정말로? 그럼 카테리나 이바노브나의 미모는? 육만이라는 재산이 솔깃한 거야 사실이지만, 비단 돈만은 아니란 말이지.\"\n\n\"이반은 그런 것을 초월한 사람이야. 그는 몇 천 루블 때문에 누구한테 아부할 사람이 아니야. 이반이 좇는 건 돈도 아니고 안락함도 아니야. 어쩌면 그가 좇는 건 고통일지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 허황된 꿈인가? 오, 자네들—귀족 나리들이란!\"\n\n\"아, 미샤, 그는 폭풍 같은 영혼을 지녔어. 그의 정신은 속박돼 있지. 그는 거대한, 풀리지 않는 의문에 시달리고 있어. 그는 수백만 루블을 바라는 게 아니라 제 물음에 대한 답을 바라는 그런 부류의 사람이라네.\"\n\n\"그건 표절이야, 알료샤. 자넨 자네네 장로의 말투를 그대로 인용하고 있잖아. 아하, 이반이 자네에게 문제를 하나 던졌구먼!\" 라키틴이 노골적인 악의를 드러내며 소리쳤다. 그의 얼굴빛이 변하고 입술이 씰룩거렸다. \"게다가 그 문제라는 게 멍청하기 짝이 없어. 알아맞히려고 애쓸 것도 없지. 골머리를 쥐어짜 보게나—이해하게 될 테니. 그자의 논문은 터무니없고 우스꽝스러워. 자네도 아까 그자의 멍청한 이론을 들었잖나. 영혼의 불멸이 없다면 미덕도 없고, 모든 것이 허용된다는 거 말이야. (그건 그렇고, 자네 형 미탸가 '나는 기억하겠다!'고 외쳤던 걸 기억하나?) 악당들에게 솔깃한 이론이지!—(내가 욕을 하는군, 멍청한 짓이야.) 악당들이 아니라, '심오하고 풀리지 않는 의문에 시달리는' 현학적인 허세꾼들에게나 솔깃한 거지. 그자는 잘난 척을 하는 거고, 결국 그게 다 무슨 소리냐 하면, '한편으로는 인정하지 않을 수 없으나' '다른 한편으로는 고백해야만 한다!' 따위 헛소리란 말일세. 그자의 이론은 통째로 사기야! 인류는 불멸을 믿지 않고도 미덕을 위해 살아갈 힘을 제 안에서 찾아낼 거야. 자유와 평등과 형제애에 대한 사랑 속에서 그걸 찾아낼 거란 말이지.\"\n\n라키틴은 그 격정에 못 이겨 좀처럼 자신을 억누르지 못하다가, 별안간 무언가 생각난 듯 뚝 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"자, 이쯤 하면 됐네.\" 그가 한층 더 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"왜 웃나? 내가 천박한 바보로 보이나?\"\n\n\"아니, 자넬 천박한 바보라고는 꿈에도 생각지 않았네. 자넨 영리해, 하지만… 아무튼 신경 쓰지 말게, 웃은 건 내가 어리석었어. 자네가 그 일에 흥분하는 걸 이해하네, 미샤. 그 열을 보니 자네 자신도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 무관심하지 않은 모양이군. 진작부터 그런 의심을 했었네, 형제, 그래서 자네가 내 형 이반을 좋아하지 않는 거지. 자넨 그를 질투하는 건가?\"\n\n\"게다가 그 여자의 돈까지 질투한다고? 그 말은 안 덧붙이려나?\"\n\n\"돈에 대해선 아무 말도 않겠네. 자넬 모욕하려는 게 아니니까.\"\n\n\"자네가 그렇게 말하니 믿어 주지, 하지만 자네도 자네 형 이반도 다 빌어먹을 인간들이야. 카테리나 이바노브나와는 별개로 그자를 충분히 싫어할 수 있다는 걸 모르겠나? 대체 내가 무슨 까닭으로 그자를 좋아해야 한단 말인가? 그자는 황송하게도 나를 깎아내리시거든. 그렇담 나라고 그자를 깎아내릴 권리가 없으란 법이 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"난 그가 자네에 대해 좋게든 나쁘게든 무슨 말을 했단 걸 들어 본 적이 없네. 그는 자네 얘기를 아예 입에 올리질 않아.\"\n\n\"하지만 그저께 카테리나 이바노브나 댁에서 그자가 나를 있는 대로 욕해 댔다는 걸 들었네—자, 그자가 이 미천한 종놈한테 얼마나 관심이 많은지 보게나. 그러니 둘 중 누가 질투하는 건지, 형제, 난 모르겠네. 그자는 황송하게도 이런 의견을 늘어놓으셨다더군. 내가 가까운 장래에 수도원장 자리를 노리는 길에 들어서서 수도사가 되지 않는다면, 분명 페테르부르크로 가서 어느 탄탄한 잡지에 평론가로 들어앉을 거고, 앞으로 십 년 동안 글을 쓰다가 끝내는 그 잡지의 주인이 되어, 사회주의의 옅은 빛깔을 입힌, 아주 자그마한 사회주의의 광택을 곁들인 자유주의적이고 무신론적인 노선으로 그 잡지를 펴내되, 내내 양쪽을 다 살피며—그러니까 양쪽 진영에 발을 걸쳐 두고 멍청이들을 속여 가며—날카롭게 눈치를 살필 거라더군. 자네 형 말로는, 그 옅은 사회주의 빛깔도 내가 수익을 챙겨 어느 유대인의 지도 아래 투자하는 데에는 아무 방해도 되지 않을 거고, 그렇게 해서 내 경력의 끝에 가서는 페테르부르크에 으리으리한 저택을 짓고 거기로 출판사를 옮긴 다음, 위층은 세놓아 먹게 될 거라는 거지. 그자는 그 저택 자리까지 골라 놓았다더군, 페테르부르크에 새로 놓는다는 네바강의 새 돌다리 근처로 말이야.\"\n\n\"아, 미샤, 그건 정말로 그대로 일어날 일이야, 한마디 한마디 죄다.\" 알료샤가 흐뭇한 미소를 참지 못하고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"자네도 빈정거리시는군그래, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야, 농담이야, 용서하게. 내 머릿속엔 전혀 다른 생각이 있었어. 그런데 말이지, 미안하지만, 누가 그 얘기를 다 자네한테 해 줄 수 있었던 거지? 그자가 자네 얘기를 할 때 설마 자네가 직접 카테리나 이바노브나 댁에 있었던 건 아닐 테고?\"\n\n\"난 거기 없었지만, 드미트리 표도로비치가 있었네. 그리고 난 내 두 귀로 그가 그 얘기를 하는 걸 들었지. 정 알고 싶다면 말해 주는데, 그가 내게 해 준 게 아니라 내가 우연히 엿들은 거야, 물론 일부러는 아니고. 내가 그루셴카의 침실에 앉아 있었는데 드미트리 표도로비치가 옆방에 있는 바람에 자리를 뜰 수가 없었거든.\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 그 여자가 자네 친척이라는 걸 깜빡했군.\"\n\n\"친척이라니! 그 그루셴카가 내 친척이라고!\" 라키틴이 새빨개지며 소리쳤다. \"자네 미쳤나? 정신이 나갔군!\"\n\n\"아니, 그 여자가 자네 친척 아닌가? 난 그렇게 들었는데.\"\n\n\"대체 어디서 그런 소릴 들었나? 자네네 카라마조프 집안은 유서 깊은 귀족 가문이라고 으스대지만, 자네 아버지로 말하자면 남의 집 식탁이나 전전하며 광대 노릇을 하고 다녔고, 그것도 선심 써서 부엌에나 들여보내 줬던 작자란 말이지. 나야 한낱 사제의 아들로, 자네 같은 귀족 나리들 눈엔 흙먼지에 지나지 않을지 모르지만, 그렇게 함부로 까닭 없이 나를 모욕하진 말게. 나에게도 명예심이라는 게 있어, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 천한 갈보 그루셴카 따위의 친척일 수가 없네. 그 점을 좀 알아 두게나!\"\n\n라키틴은 몹시 발끈해 있었다.\n\n\"제발 용서하게, 난 전혀 몰랐어… 게다가… 어떻게 자네는 그 여자를 갈보라고 부를 수가 있나? 그 여자가… 그런 부류의 여자인가?\" 알료샤가 별안간 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"다시 말하지만, 난 그 여자가 자네 친척이라고 들었어. 자넨 그 여자를 자주 찾아가고, 게다가 자네 입으로 그 여자의 정부는 아니라고 했잖아. 다른 누구도 아닌 자네가 그 여자를 그렇게 경멸할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐네! 정말 그 여자가 그런 경멸을 받아 마땅한가?\"\n\n\"내가 그 여자를 찾아가는 데엔 나름의 이유가 있을 수도 있지. 그건 자네가 상관할 바 아니야. 하지만 친척으로 말하자면, 나보다는 자네 형이, 아니 자네 아버지가 그 여자를 자네 친척으로 만들 가능성이 더 크지. 자, 다 왔군. 자넨 어서 부엌으로 가는 게 좋겠어. 어라! 무슨 일이지, 이게 뭐야? 우리가 늦은 건가? 만찬이 이렇게 빨리 끝났을 리가 없는데! 카라마조프 집안에서 또 한바탕 소동을 벌인 건가? 틀림없이 그랬겠지. 저기 자네 아버지가 있고 그 뒤로 자네 형 이반이 따라 나오는군. 둘이 원장 신부님 처소에서 뛰쳐나왔어. 그리고 저것 봐, 이시도르 신부님이 계단에서 그들 뒤에다 대고 뭐라고 소리치고 있잖아. 자네 아버지도 소리치며 팔을 휘저어 대는군. 욕을 퍼붓고 있는 모양이야. 어허, 저기 미우소프가 마차를 타고 떠나는군. 봐, 가 버리잖아. 그리고 저기 늙은 막시모프가 달려가네!—틀림없이 한바탕 난리가 났던 거야. 만찬이 제대로 됐을 리가 없지. 설마 원장 신부님을 두들겨 패기야 했겠나! 아니면 혹시, 그들이 도리어 얻어맞은 건가? 그래도 싸지!\"\n\n라키틴의 이런 탄성에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있었다. 추문이라 할 만한, 전례 없는 소동이 벌어졌던 것이다. 그것은 모두 한순간의 충동에서 비롯된 일이었다.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Scandalous Scene\n\n미우소프는 교양 있고 섬세한 사람인지라, 이반과 함께 수도원장의 처소에 이르렀을 때 내심 적잖은 가책을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으니, 그는 자제력을 잃은 것이 부끄러웠다. 그 비열한 작자 표도르 파블로비치 따위는 멸시하고 무시했어야 마땅했거늘, 조시마 장로의 암자에서 그자에게 휘둘려 자기 자신을 잊어버린 것이 부끄러웠다. \"어쨌든 수도사들에게야 무슨 잘못이 있겠는가.\" 그는 층계를 오르며 곰곰이 생각했다. \"여기 사람들이 점잖은 이들이라면—듣자 하니 수도원장도 귀족 출신이라고 하지 않던가—어찌 그들에게 친절하고 정중하게 대하지 않을 까닭이 있겠는가? 따지지 않으리라, 무엇이든 맞춰 주리라, 예의로써 그들의 호의를 사리라, 그리고… 그리고… 저 이솝 같은 작자, 저 어릿광대, 저 피에로와 나는 아무 상관이 없으며, 나 역시 저들과 똑같이 이 일에 그저 끌려들었을 뿐임을 보여 주리라.\"\n\n그는 수도원과의 소송을 그만두고 벌목권과 어업권에 대한 자기 청구를 당장 포기하기로 마음먹었다. 그가 이리하기로 한 데에는 그 권리들이 이제는 훨씬 값어치가 떨어진 데다, 정작 문제가 되는 숲과 강이 어디쯤에 있는지조차 자기로서는 도무지 가늠이 가지 않는다는 사정도 한몫했다.\n\n이 갸륵한 결심은 수도원장의 식당으로 들어서자 한층 굳어졌다. 하기야 엄밀히 말하면 그것은 식당이라 할 수 없었으니, 수도원장의 거처라야 도합 방이 둘뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 방들은 조시마 장로의 암자보다 훨씬 넓고 안락했다. 그렇다 해도 이 방들의 세간이 그리 호사스러운 것은 아니었다. 가구는 가죽을 씌운 마호가니로, 1820년대의 고풍스러운 양식이었고, 마룻바닥에는 칠조차 되어 있지 않았으나, 모든 것이 청결하게 빛나고 있었으며, 창가에는 진귀한 꽃들이 즐비했다. 지금 이 방에서 가장 호화로운 것은 물론 아름답게 차려진 식탁이었다. 식탁보는 정갈했고 식기는 반짝였으며, 잘 구운 빵이 세 종류, 포도주가 두 병, 빼어난 봉밀주가 두 병, 그리고 크바스가 큼직한 유리 단지로 하나 놓여 있었는데—이 뒤의 두 가지는 수도원에서 직접 빚은 것으로 인근에서 이름이 자자한 것이었다. 보드카는 없었다. 라키틴이 나중에 전한 바로는 요리가 다섯 가지였다고 한다. 쇠철갑상어로 끓인 생선 수프에 자그마한 생선 파이를 곁들인 것, 그다음에는 특별한 방식으로 삶아 낸 생선, 그다음에는 연어 커틀릿, 얼음 푸딩과 콤포트, 그리고 마지막으로 블랑망제였다. 라키틴이 이 온갖 좋은 음식들을 죄다 알아낸 것은, 자기가 이미 발을 들여놓은 부엌을 기어이 들여다보지 않고는 못 배기는 위인이었기 때문이다. 그는 어디에나 발을 들여놓았고 무엇이든 정보를 캐냈다. 그는 불안하고 시기심 많은 성미였다. 그는 자기에게 제법 대단한 재능이 있음을 잘 알았고, 자만심에서 그것을 신경질적으로 부풀려 생각했다. 그는 자기가 무언가 두드러진 역할을 하게 되리라는 것을 알았으나, 그에게 정을 붙이고 있던 알료샤로서는 자기 친구 라키틴이 비루한 인간이면서도 정작 자신은 그런 줄을 전혀 깨닫지 못한 채, 도리어 식탁에 놓인 돈을 훔치지 않는다는 이유만으로 자기를 더없이 청렴한 사람이라 여기는 것을 보며 마음 아파했다. 그 점에서는 알료샤도, 그 누구도 그를 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다.\n\n물론 라키틴은 만찬에 초대받기에는 너무도 보잘것없는 인물이었으니, 수도원의 식구들 가운데 초대받은 이는 이오시프 신부와 파이시 신부, 그리고 또 다른 수도사 한 사람뿐이었다. 미우소프와 칼가노프와 이반이 당도했을 때 그들은 이미 기다리고 있었다. 또 한 사람의 손님인 막시모프는 한쪽에 비켜선 채 역시 기다리고 있었다. 수도원장은 손님들을 맞이하려고 방 한가운데로 걸어 나왔다. 그는 키가 크고 야위었으나 아직 정정한 노인으로, 검은 머리에 희끗희끗 흰 것이 섞여 있었고, 길쭉하고 근엄한 고행자의 얼굴을 지니고 있었다. 그는 말없이 손님들에게 머리를 숙였다. 그러나 이번에는 손님들 쪽에서 그의 축복을 받으려고 다가갔다. 미우소프는 그의 손에 입을 맞추려고까지 했으나, 수도원장이 제때 손을 거두어 그 인사를 피했다. 그러나 이반과 칼가노프는 더없이 순박하고 빈틈없는 태도로 그 의식을 치러, 농부들이 하듯 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"저희가 황공하옵게도 머리 숙여 사죄드려야겠습니다, 신부님.\" 미우소프가 싹싹하게 미소 지으며 위엄 있고 공손한 어조로 입을 열었다. \"초대받으신 분, 표도르 파블로비치를 모시지 못하고 저희만 온 것을 용서해 주십시오. 그분은 환대의 영광을 사양해야겠다고 느끼셨는데, 그럴 만한 까닭이 없지 않았습니다. 존경하옵는 조시마 장로님의 암자에서 그분은 아들과의 불행한 불화에 휩쓸려, 도무지 어울리지 않는… 사실인즉 매우 점잖지 못한 말씀을… 하셨으니\"—그는 수도사들을 흘끗 보았다—\"신부님께서도 이미 알고 계실 줄 압니다. 그리하여 자기가 잘못했음을 깨닫고 진심으로 후회하며 부끄러워하시며, 저와 그 아드님 이반 표도로비치에게 부디 사죄와 유감의 뜻을 신부님께 전해 달라고 간청하셨습니다. 한마디로 말씀드리면, 그분은 훗날 그 허물을 보상하기를 바라고 또 그러기를 원하십니다. 신부님의 축복을 청하며, 일어난 일은 부디 잊어 주십사 빌고 계십니다.\"\n\n이 일장 연설의 마지막 말을 내뱉을 즈음 미우소프는 완전히 자기만족을 되찾았고, 조금 전의 노여움은 자취도 없이 사라졌다. 그는 다시금 온전하고 진심으로 인류를 사랑하게 되었다.\n\n수도원장은 위엄 있게 그의 말을 듣고 나서, 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다.\n\n\"그분이 오시지 못한 것을 진심으로 애석하게 여깁니다. 어쩌면 우리 식탁에서 그분이 우리를 좋아하게 되고, 우리 또한 그분을 좋아하게 되었을지도 모르는 일이지요. 자, 앉으시지요, 여러분.\"\n\n그는 성상 앞에 서서 소리 내어 식전 기도를 올리기 시작했다. 모두가 경건히 머리를 숙였고, 막시모프는 유난스러운 열성으로 두 손을 가슴 앞에 모았다.\n\n바로 이 순간 표도르 파블로비치가 마지막 장난을 벌였다. 한 가지 짚어 둘 것은, 그가 정말로 집에 돌아가려 했었고, 장로의 암자에서 그 망신스러운 추태를 부린 뒤에 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 수도원장과 마주 앉아 만찬을 든다는 것은 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라고 진정으로 느꼈었다는 점이다. 자기 자신이 그토록 부끄러웠던 것은 아니었으니—어쩌면 도리어 그 반대였으리라. 그래도 만찬에 가는 것은 점잖지 못하다고 느끼기는 했다. 그러나 삐걱거리는 그의 마차가 여관 층계 앞에 닿기가 무섭게, 그가 그 안에 올라타기가 무섭게, 그는 문득 동작을 딱 멈추었다. 그는 장로의 암자에서 자기가 했던 말을 떠올렸다. \"나는 사람들을 만날 때면 늘 내가 누구보다도 못난 놈이고, 다들 나를 어릿광대로 여긴다고 느낀단 말이오. 그러니 어디 한번 어릿광대 노릇을 해 보자, 너희야말로 하나같이 나보다 더 어리석고 더 못난 놈들이니까, 하고 마음먹는단 말이오.\" 그는 자기 자신의 추태에 대한 앙갚음을 모든 사람에게 하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그는 문득 언젠가 누군가가 자기에게 \"당신은 어째서 아무개를 그토록 미워하시오?\" 하고 물었던 일을 떠올렸다. 그러자 그는 뻔뻔스럽고 염치없이 이렇게 대답했었다. \"말해 주리다. 그자가 나에게 해코지를 한 적은 없소. 다만 내가 그자에게 더러운 짓을 한 번 했더니, 그때부터 줄곧 그자가 미워지더란 말이오.\"\n\n이제 그것을 떠올리며 그는 조용히, 그리고 음흉하게 미소 지으며 잠시 망설였다. 그의 두 눈이 번득였고, 입술은 영락없이 부르르 떨렸다. \"그래, 이왕 시작한 김에 끝까지 가 보는 게 낫지.\" 그는 그렇게 작정했다. 그 순간 그를 사로잡은 지배적인 감정은 다음과 같은 말로 표현할 수 있을 것이다. \"이제 와서 내 명예를 회복하기는 글렀어. 그러니 있는 힘껏 저들에게 망신을 주자. 저들이 나를 어떻게 생각하든 나는 아랑곳하지 않는다는 걸 보여 주는 거야—그뿐이다!\"\n\n그는 마부에게 기다리라고 일러 둔 채, 빠른 걸음으로 수도원으로, 곧장 수도원장의 처소로 되돌아갔다. 무엇을 어찌하겠다는 분명한 작정이 있는 것은 아니었으나, 자기로서는 도무지 자제할 수 없으며, 한 번 건드려지기만 하면 외설의 극한까지 치달을 수 있다는 것은 알고 있었다—그러나 어디까지나 외설일 뿐, 범죄에 이르는 짓이나 법으로 처벌받을 만한 짓은 결코 아니었다. 막판에 가서는 그도 늘 자제할 수 있었고, 그 점에 있어서는 때때로 스스로도 자기 자신에게 감탄하곤 했었다. 그는 기도가 막 끝나고 모두가 식탁으로 옮겨 가려던 바로 그 순간에 수도원장의 식당에 나타났다. 문간에 선 채 그는 좌중을 죽 훑어보더니, 길게 늘어지는 뻔뻔스럽고 음흉한 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 모두의 얼굴을 대담하게 마주 보았다. \"다들 내가 가 버린 줄 알았겠지, 그런데 여기 다시 왔소이다.\" 그는 방 안 전체를 향해 소리쳤다.\n\n한순간 모두가 말없이 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 곧 모두가, 무언가 역겹고 괴이하며 영락없이 망측한 일이 벌어지려 한다는 것을 직감했다. 미우소프는 더없이 자비로운 심경에서 단숨에 더없이 사나운 심경으로 돌변했다. 그의 가슴속에서 가라앉아 사그라들었던 온갖 감정이 일시에 되살아났다.\n\n\"안 돼! 나는 이것만은 참을 수 없소!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"도무지 참을 수 없단 말이오! 그리고… 정말이지 참을 수 없소!\"\n\n피가 그의 머리로 솟구쳤다. 그는 영락없이 말을 더듬었으나, 이미 말투를 따질 처지가 아니었으므로 모자를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"저자가 참을 수 없다는 게 대체 무엇이오?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다. \"도무지 참을 수 없고 정말이지 참을 수 없다는 게 뭐란 말이오? 신부님, 제가 들어가도 됩니까, 안 됩니까? 저를 손님으로 받아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"진심으로 환영합니다.\" 수도원장이 대답했다. \"여러분!\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"간곡히 청하건대, 부디 다툼을 내려놓으시고, 사랑과 가족의 화목 속에서 하나가 되시기를—주님께 기도드리며, 우리의 변변찮은 식탁에 함께하시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오!\" 미우소프가 제정신이 아닌 채 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 표트르 알렉산드로비치에게 있을 수 없는 일이라면 나에게도 있을 수 없는 일이오, 나도 머물지 않겠소. 바로 그러려고 내가 온 거니까. 이제 어디를 가든 표트르 알렉산드로비치와 함께 붙어 다니겠소. 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신이 가면 나도 가고, 당신이 머물면 나도 머물겠소. 수도원장님, 당신이 가족의 화목 어쩌고 한 그 말로 이 사람을 콕 찔러 놓으셨소이다. 이 사람은 자기가 나의 친척이라는 걸 인정하지 않거든요. 그렇지 않소, 폰 존? 자, 여기 폰 존이 계시오. 안녕하시오, 폰 존?\"\n\n\"나 말씀이오?\" 막시모프가 어리둥절하여 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"물론 당신 말이지.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다. \"누구겠소? 수도원장님이 폰 존일 리는 없잖소.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 폰 존이 아니외다. 나는 막시모프요.\"\n\n\"아니, 당신은 폰 존이오. 신부님, 폰 존이 누구였는지 아십니까? 그것은 유명한 살인 사건이었지요. 그자는 어떤 색주가에서—여러분들 사이에서는 그런 데를 그렇게 부르는 줄로 압니다만—살해당했지요, 살해당하고 강탈당한 데다, 그 점잖으신 나이에도 불구하고 궤짝에 못 박혀 넣어진 채로 페테르부르크에서 모스크바로 화물칸에 실려 갔답니다. 게다가 그자를 궤짝에 못 박는 동안, 갈보들이 노래를 부르고 하프를, 그러니까 피아노를 쳤다는 거요. 자, 이분이 바로 그 폰 존이외다. 죽었다가 다시 살아나신 거지요, 안 그렇소, 폰 존?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오? 이게 다 뭐란 말이오?\" 수도사들 무리에서 웅성거리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"갑시다.\" 미우소프가 칼가노프를 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"안 되오, 잠깐만.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 방 안으로 또 한 걸음 들어서며 날카로운 목소리로 가로막았다. \"내 말을 끝내게 해 주시오. 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신은 저기 암자에서 내가 모샘치를 먹는 얘기를 했다는 것만으로 나더러 불경스럽게 군다고 나무랐소. 나의 친척 미우소프는 자기 말에 진실보다 고상함을 더 담으려는 사람이지만(plus de noblesse que de sincérité), 나는 내 말에 고상함보다 진실을 더 담는 걸 좋아하오(plus de sincérité que de noblesse), 그러니—고상함 따위는 빌어먹으라지! 그렇지 않소, 폰 존? 수도원장님, 잠깐만 허락하시오, 내가 비록 어릿광대이고 어릿광대 노릇을 한다지만, 그래도 나는 명예의 화신이외다, 그러니 내 속마음을 털어놓고 싶소. 그렇소, 나는 명예의 화신인데, 표트르 알렉산드로비치한테는 상한 허영심밖에는 아무것도 없소. 내가 여기 온 것은 어쩌면 한번 둘러보고 내 속마음을 털어놓으려고 그런 것인지도 모르겠소. 내 아들 알렉세이가 여기 있소, 구원을 받고 있다지요. 나는 그 아이의 아비요. 나는 그 아이의 안녕을 염려하고, 또 염려하는 것이 내 도리요. 내가 광대짓을 하는 동안에도 나는 가만히 귀 기울여 듣고 슬그머니 살펴보고 있었소이다. 그러니 이제 그 공연의 마지막 막을 여러분께 보여 드리겠소. 우리네 형편이 어떤지 아시오? 무언가 한번 쓰러지면, 그대로 누워 있는 거요. 한번 쓰러진 것은, 영영 그대로 누워 있어야만 하는 거요. 천만에! 나는 다시 일어나고 싶소. 거룩하신 신부님, 나는 당신들에게 분개하고 있소이다. 고해는 위대한 성사라, 그 앞에서라면 나도 기꺼이 경건하게 머리를 조아릴 준비가 되어 있소. 그런데 저기 암자에서는 다들 무릎을 꿇고 큰 소리로 고해를 하더이다. 큰 소리로 고해를 한다는 게 과연 옳은 일이오? 거룩한 교부들께서는 은밀히 고해하도록 정하셨소. 그래야만 비로소 당신의 고해가 비밀이 되는 거고, 예로부터 그러했단 말이오. 그런데 모든 사람 앞에서 어떻게 내가 이런 짓 저런 짓을 했노라고… 그러니까, 무슨 말인지 아시잖소—더러는 입에 담기조차 마땅찮은 일을 어떻게 털어놓겠소—그러니 이건 정말 망측한 일이오! 안 되오, 신부님들, 이러다간 자칫 여러분 따라 채찍 고행파한테까지 끌려갈 판이외다… 기회가 닿는 대로 나는 종무원에 진정서를 써 보내고, 내 아들 알렉세이를 집으로 데려가겠소.\"\n\n여기서 짚어 둘 것은, 표도르 파블로비치가 약점이 어디 있는지 정확히 알고 있었다는 점이다. 한때 악의에 찬 소문이 돌아 대주교의 귀에까지 들어간 적이 있었으니(비단 우리 수도원뿐 아니라 장로 제도가 있는 다른 수도원들에 대해서도), 장로들을 지나치게 떠받들어 수도원장의 권위마저 손상될 지경이라느니, 장로들이 고해 성사를 악용한다느니 어쩌니 하는 따위의 터무니없는 비난이었는데, 이런 것들은 어디에서나 저절로 사그라들고 말았다. 그러나 표도르 파블로비치를 사로잡아 제 신경의 물살에 실어 점점 더 깊은 치욕의 나락으로 떠밀고 있던 그 어리석음의 망령이, 이 케케묵은 비방을 그에게 속삭여 준 것이었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 그 내용을 한마디도 제대로 이해하지 못했고, 조리 있게 늘어놓지도 못했으니, 이번 경우에는 장로의 암자에서 누구도 무릎을 꿇고 큰 소리로 고해한 일이 없었던지라, 그가 그런 따위의 광경을 목격했을 리가 없었기 때문이다. 그는 그저 케케묵은 비방의 어렴풋한 기억을 더듬어 지껄여 댄 것이었다. 그러나 그 어리석은 일장 연설을 내뱉기가 무섭게 자기가 터무니없는 헛소리를 지껄였음을 깨닫고, 곧장 청중에게, 무엇보다도 자기 자신에게, 자기가 헛소리를 한 게 아니라는 것을 증명하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그리고 한 마디 한 마디 내뱉을수록 점점 더 터무니없는 소리만 보탤 뿐임을 빤히 알면서도, 그는 자제하지 못하고 무작정 앞으로 곤두박질쳤다.\n\n\"이 무슨 망신스러운 짓이오!\" 표트르 알렉산드로비치가 외쳤다.\n\n\"용서하시오!\" 수도원장이 말했다. \"예로부터 이런 말씀이 있었지요. '많은 이들이 나를 거슬러 말하기 시작하였고 나에 대하여 악한 말을 내뱉었도다. 그것을 듣고 나는 스스로에게 일렀노라, 이는 주님의 징계이시며 나의 헛된 영혼을 고치시려고 주님께서 보내신 것이로다.' 그러므로 우리는 겸손히 당신께 감사드립니다, 귀하신 손님이시여!\" 그러고는 표도르 파블로비치에게 깊이 머리를 숙였다.\n\n\"쳇, 쳇, 쳇—위선이요, 판에 박은 문구로구먼! 케케묵은 문구에 케케묵은 몸짓이오. 묵은 거짓말에 형식뿐인 큰절이로다. 그런 건 우리도 다 아오. 실러의 '군도'에 나오듯, 입술에는 입맞춤을, 가슴에는 비수를 꽂는 격이외다. 나는 거짓을 좋아하지 않소, 신부님들, 나는 진실을 원하오. 그런데 진실은 모샘치를 먹는 데 있는 것이 아니라는 걸, 나는 큰 소리로 외치는 바이오! 수도사 신부님들, 당신들은 어찌하여 금식을 하시오? 어찌하여 그 대가로 천국에서 상을 바라시오? 아니, 그런 상이라면야 나도 와서 금식을 하겠소이다! 안 되오, 거룩하신 수도사 양반, 당신들 한번 세상 속에서 덕을 베풀어 보시오, 남의 돈으로 수도원에 틀어박히지 않고서, 또 그 대가로 저 위에서 상을 바라지도 않고서, 사회를 위해 좋은 일을 해 보란 말이오—그게 좀 더 힘들다는 걸 알게 될 거요. 나도 사리에 맞는 소리쯤은 할 줄 안단 말이오, 수도원장님. 여기 차려 놓은 게 다 뭐요?\" 그는 식탁으로 다가갔다. \"묵은 포트와인에, 옐리세예프 형제 상회에서 빚은 봉밀주라. 저런, 저런, 신부님들! 이건 모샘치 따위와는 차원이 다르구먼. 신부님들이 내놓은 이 술병들 좀 보시오, 헤헤헤! 그런데 이걸 다 누가 댄 거요? 러시아의 농부, 노동자가, 거친 손으로 벌어들인 한 푼을, 제 식구와 세금쟁이한테서 쥐어짜 가며 여기다 갖다 바친 거란 말이오! 당신들은 백성의 피를 빨고 있는 거요, 알겠소, 거룩하신 신부님들.\"\n\n\"이건 너무도 망측하오!\" 이오시프 신부가 말했다.\n\n파이시 신부는 완강하게 침묵을 지켰다. 미우소프는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 칼가노프가 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"좋소, 신부님, 나도 표트르 알렉산드로비치를 따라가겠소! 다시는 당신을 보러 오지 않겠소이다. 무릎 꿇고 빌어도 오지 않을 거요. 내가 당신네한테 천 루블을 보냈더니, 그때부터 나를 눈여겨보기 시작했겠다. 헤헤헤! 아니, 더는 말하지 않겠소. 나는 내 젊은 시절에 대한, 내가 당한 그 온갖 모욕에 대한 앙갚음을 하고 있는 거요.\" 그는 꾸며 낸 격정에 사로잡혀 주먹으로 식탁을 쾅 내리쳤다. \"이 수도원은 내 인생에서 큰 노릇을 했단 말이오! 이 때문에 나는 쓰디쓴 눈물을 얼마나 흘렸는지 모르오. 당신들은 내 마누라를, 그 미친 여편네를 부추겨 나한테 등을 돌리게 만들었소. 종을 울리고 성서를 펴 들고 나를 저주했으며, 사방에 나에 관한 소문을 퍼뜨렸소. 그만하면 됐소, 신부님들! 지금은 자유주의의 시대요, 증기선과 철도의 시대란 말이오. 천 루블은커녕, 백 루블도, 아니, 백 푼도 나한테서는 한 푼도 받아 내지 못할 거요!\"\n\n여기서 다시 짚어 둘 것은, 우리 수도원이 그의 인생에서 무슨 큰 노릇을 한 적이라곤 결코 없었으며, 그가 그 때문에 쓰디쓴 눈물을 흘린 적도 결코 없었다는 점이다. 그러나 그는 자기가 꾸며 낸 감정에 어찌나 휩쓸렸던지, 한순간 거의 자기 자신마저 그것을 믿을 지경이었다. 그는 어찌나 감격했던지 거의 울 뻔했다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 이제 물러설 때라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n수도원장은 그의 악의에 찬 거짓말에 고개를 숙이고, 다시금 묵직하게 말했다.\n\n\"또한 이렇게 기록되어 있습니다. '네 행위로 말미암지 아니한 치욕이 네게 닥치거든 신중하고 기쁘게 견디라, 당황하지 말며 너를 욕되게 한 자를 미워하지 말라.' 그러므로 우리도 그리하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇, 쳇! 자기 자신을 돌이켜보라느니 어쩌니 하는 그 나머지 잠꼬대까지 다 늘어놓는구먼. 당신네나 정신 차리시오, 신부님들, 나는 가겠소. 다만 내 아들 알렉세이만은, 부모 된 권리로 영영 이곳에서 데려가겠소. 이반 표도로비치, 더없이 효성스러운 내 아들아, 나를 따라오라고 명하는 것을 허락해 다오. 폰 존, 당신은 여기 남아 봤자 뭘 하겠소? 지금 나를 따라 읍내로 갑시다. 거기가 재미있단 말이오. 겨우 한 베르스타밖에 안 되오. 정진하는 기름 대신 새끼돼지 구이에 카샤를 대접하리다. 브랜디에 리큐어까지 곁들여 한 상 차려 먹읍시다… 나한테는 진들딸기주도 있소. 자, 폰 존, 이 기회를 놓치지 마시오.\" 그는 고래고래 소리치고 손을 휘저으며 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n바로 그 순간 라키틴이 그를 보고 알료샤에게 손가락질해 보였다.\n\n\"알렉세이!\" 그의 아비가 멀찍이서 그를 알아보고 외쳤다. \"오늘 나한테 집으로 돌아오너라, 아주 영영, 그러고 베개와 요도 챙겨 오고, 아무 흔적도 남기지 말거라.\"\n\n알료샤는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서서 말없이 그 광경을 지켜보았다. 그사이 표도르 파블로비치는 마차에 올라탔고, 이반도 알료샤에게 작별 인사조차 건네려 돌아보지 않은 채 침울한 침묵 속에 그 뒤를 따라 오르려던 참이었다. 그런데 이때 또 한 차례 거의 믿기지 않을 만큼 괴이한 광대극이 벌어져 이 사건에 마지막 마무리를 지었다. 막시모프가 느닷없이 마차 옆에 나타난 것이다. 그는 너무 늦을세라 헐떡이며 달려왔다. 라키틴과 알료샤는 그가 달려오는 것을 보았다. 그는 어찌나 다급했던지, 그 조바심에 그만 이반의 왼발이 아직 얹혀 있는 발판에 제 발을 올려놓고는, 마차를 부여잡은 채 자꾸만 뛰어오르려 했다. \"나도 함께 가겠소이다!\" 그는 얼굴에 분별없는 환희를 띤 채 가느다랗고 신명 난 웃음을 흘리며 연신 외쳤다. \"나도 데려가 주시오.\"\n\n\"바로 그거요!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 기뻐서 외쳤다. \"내가 이 사람이 폰 존이라고 하지 않았소. 죽었다가 다시 살아난 바로 그 폰 존이외다. 아니, 어떻게 빠져나왔소? 거기서 무슨 폰존질을 하셨소? 게다가 만찬에서는 어떻게 빠져나온 거요? 당신도 어지간히 뻔뻔스러운 위인이로구먼! 나도 그렇긴 하다만, 당신한테는 정말 놀랐소이다, 이 양반아! 어서 올라타시오, 어서! 이반, 이 사람을 들이게. 이거 재미있겠는걸. 우리 발치 아무 데나 누워 가면 되지. 우리 발치에 누워 가겠소, 폰 존? 아니면 마부 옆 마부석에 올라앉든가. 마부석으로 폴짝 뛰어오르시오, 폰 존!\"\n\n그러나 이미 자리에 앉아 있던 이반은 말 한마디 없이 막시모프의 가슴팍을 우악스레 한 대 쥐어박아 그를 나가떨어지게 했다. 그가 넘어지지 않은 것은 순전히 운이었다.\n\n\"가자!\" 이반이 마부에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 자네 무슨 짓인가, 대체 뭘 하는 거야? 어쩌자고 그런 짓을 한 게야?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 항의했다.\n\n그러나 마차는 이미 달려 나간 뒤였다. 이반은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"허, 자네도 어지간한 사람일세그려.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 다시 말했다.\n\n이 분쯤 잠자코 있다가, 아들을 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"아니, 이 수도원 일을 죄다 꾸민 게 바로 자네 아닌가. 자네가 부추겼고, 자네가 찬성했잖나. 그런데 이제 와서 왜 화를 내는 게야?\"\n\n\"아버지는 헛소리를 충분히 하셨습니다. 이제 좀 쉬셔도 되겠지요.\" 이반이 시무룩하게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 다시 이 분쯤 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"브랜디나 한 모금 했으면 좋겠구먼.\" 그가 잠언이라도 읊듯 한마디 했으나, 이반은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"집에 가면 자네도 한잔하게나.\"\n\n이반은 여전히 잠자코 있었다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 또 이 분쯤 기다렸다.\n\n\"그래도 나는 알료샤를 수도원에서 데려올 걸세, 자네가 아무리 싫어한다 해도 말이야, 더없이 존경스러운 카를 폰 모어 나리.\"\n\n이반은 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 고개를 돌려 길을 멀거니 바라보았다. 그리고 그들은 집에 이를 때까지 다시는 한마디도 나누지 않았다.\n\n# Book III. The Sensualists Chapter I. In The Servants’ Quarters\n\n카라마조프 집안의 저택은 읍내 한복판이라고 하기에는 거리가 멀었으나, 그렇다고 완전히 변두리에 떨어진 것도 아니었다. 회색으로 칠하고 붉은 철판 지붕을 얹은, 보기에 정겨운 이층짜리 낡은 집이었다. 넓고 아늑하여 앞으로도 여러 해는 너끈히 버틸 만했다. 온갖 예기치 못한 자그마한 벽장이며 골방, 계단들이 곳곳에 숨어 있었다. 쥐도 들끓었으나 표도르 파블로비치는 그것을 그리 싫어하지는 않았다. \"저녁에 홀로 남겨졌을 때 그래도 덜 적적한 법이지.\" 그는 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그는 밤이면 하인들을 별채로 내보내고 혼자 문을 잠가 걸어 두는 버릇이 있었다. 별채는 마당에 세워진 넓고 튼튼한 건물이었다. 집 안에 부엌이 따로 있었음에도 표도르 파블로비치는 음식을 별채에서 짓게 했으니, 그는 음식 냄새를 싫어하여 겨울이든 여름이든 한결같이 음식을 마당 너머로 날라 오게 했다. 이 집은 대가족을 위해 지어졌으므로, 하인들까지 합쳐 지금의 다섯 배에 달하는 식구가 들어와 살 만한 공간이 있었다. 그러나 우리 이야기가 펼쳐지는 무렵에는 이 집에 표도르 파블로비치와 그의 아들 이반 말고는 아무도 살지 않았다. 그리고 별채에는 하인이 셋뿐이었으니, 늙은 그리고리와 그의 늙은 아내 마르파, 그리고 스메르댜코프라 불리는 젊은이였다. 이 세 사람에 관해서는 몇 마디 해 두어야겠다. 늙은 그리고리에 대해서는 이미 얼마간 이야기한 바 있다. 그는 굳세고 단호한 인물로서, 일단 어떤 이유로(그것은 흔히 매우 비논리적인 이유였다) 그 일이 영원토록 옳다고 믿게 되면, 앞뒤 가리지 않고 고집스럽게 그 목적을 향해 밀고 나가는 사내였다. 그는 정직하고 매수당할 줄 모르는 자였다. 그의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나는 평생토록 남편의 뜻에 고분고분 따랐으나, 농노 해방이 있은 뒤로는 그를 지독하게 들볶았다. 그녀는 표도르 파블로비치를 떠나 모아 둔 푼돈으로 모스크바에 자그마한 가게라도 열고 싶어 했던 것이다. 그러나 그리고리는 그때 단번에 못 박아 결정하기를, \"여편네가 헛소리를 하는 게야, 계집이란 죄다 못 미더운 법이니\" 하면서, 주인이 어떤 인간이든 간에 늙은 주인을 떠나서는 안 된다고 했으니, \"이제 그것이 우리의 도리이기 때문\"이었다.\n\n\"자네는 도리가 뭔지 알기나 해?\" 그가 마르파 이그나티예브나에게 물었다.\n\n\"도리가 무슨 뜻인지야 알지요, 그리고리 바실리예비치. 하지만 어째서 여기 머무는 게 우리 도리인지는 죽어도 모르겠어요.\" 마르파가 단호하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼 모르고 살아. 어쨌든 그렇게 될 테니. 그러니 입 다물고 있어.\"\n\n그리하여 그대로 되었다. 두 사람은 떠나지 않았고, 표도르 파블로비치는 그들에게 적은 액수나마 품삯을 약속하고 꼬박꼬박 지불했다. 그리고리는 또한 자신이 주인에게 거역할 수 없는 영향력을 지니고 있음을 알고 있었다. 그것은 사실이었고, 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 고집 세고 교활한 어릿광대였으나, 그 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 \"인생의 어떤 일들\"에서는 의지가 제법 강했음에도 불구하고, 어떤 다른 위급한 상황 앞에서는 스스로도 놀라울 만큼 지극히 무력해지는 것을 깨닫곤 했다. 그는 제 약점을 알았고 그것을 두려워했다. 사람이 눈을 부릅뜨고 단단히 경계해야 하는 처지가 있는 법이다. 그런데 그것은 믿을 만한 사람 없이는 쉬운 노릇이 아니었고, 그리고리야말로 더없이 믿음직한 사람이었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 평생토록 여러 차례 그리고리가 끼어든 덕분에 가까스로 흠씬 두들겨 맞는 봉변을 면했는데, 그럴 때마다 늙은 하인은 그에게 따끔하게 설교를 늘어놓았다. 그러나 표도르 파블로비치가 두려워한 것은 매질만이 아니었다. 더 심각한, 그리고 매우 미묘하고 복잡한 경우들이 있었으니, 표도르 파블로비치는 때때로 까닭 모르게 한순간에 자기를 사로잡는, 충직하고 헌신적인 누군가를 향한 그 유별난 갈망을 스스로도 설명할 수 없었다. 그것은 거의 병적인 상태였다. 정욕에 있어 타락하고 흔히 잔인하기가 마치 해로운 벌레와도 같던 표도르 파블로비치는, 술에 취한 순간이면 이따금 미신적인 공포와 도덕적 발작에 사로잡혔는데, 그것은 거의 육체적인 형태를 띠었다. \"그런 때면 내 영혼이 목구멍에서 부들부들 떨리는 것만 같아.\" 그는 곧잘 이렇게 말했다. 그런 순간이면 그는 가까운 곳에, 방 안은 아니어도 별채에라도, 강인하고 충직한, 덕성스럽고 자기와는 딴판인 사내가 있음을 느끼고 싶어 했으니, 그자는 그의 온갖 방탕을 다 보았고 그의 모든 비밀을 알면서도 충성된 마음으로 그 모두를 눈감아 줄 채비가 되어 있고, 그를 거스르지 않으며, 무엇보다도 이 세상에서든 저 세상에서든 그를 책망하거나 무엇으로 위협하지 않으며, 필요할 때면 그를 지켜 줄—누구로부터? 알 수 없으나 무섭고 위험한 누군가로부터—그런 사내였다. 그에게 필요했던 것은, 또 다른 한 사람이, 오래되고 시험을 거친 벗이 곁에 있어, 병적인 순간이면 그저 그 얼굴이나 들여다보려고, 혹은 아무 상관도 없는 몇 마디나 주고받으려고 그를 불러들일 수 있다고 느끼는 것이었다. 그리고 그 늙은 하인이 성을 내지 않으면 그는 위로를 받았고, 성을 내면 더욱 풀이 죽었다. 더러는(그러나 아주 드물게) 표도르 파블로비치가 밤중에 별채로 가서 그리고리를 깨워 잠시 데려오는 일도 있었다. 늙은이가 오면 표도르 파블로비치는 더없이 사소한 일들을 두고 이야기를 늘어놓다가 이내 그를 다시 돌려보냈는데, 때로는 농담까지 곁들였다. 그러고는 늙은이가 가고 나면 표도르 파블로비치는 욕설을 내뱉으며 잠자리에 들어 의인의 잠을 잤다. 알료샤가 찾아왔을 때에도 표도르 파블로비치에게 이와 비슷한 무언가가 일어났다. 알료샤는 \"그와 함께 살며, 모든 것을 보면서도 아무것도 탓하지 않음\"으로써 \"그의 마음을 꿰뚫었다.\" 게다가 알료샤는 아버지가 그때까지 한 번도 알지 못했던 무언가를 가져다주었으니, 그것은 곧 그를 향한 경멸의 완전한 부재였고, 변함없는 다정함이었으며, 그토록 그것을 받을 자격이 없는 늙은이를 향한 더없이 자연스럽고 꾸밈없는 헌신이었다. 이 모든 것은 온갖 가족의 인연을 죄다 끊어 버렸던 늙은 난봉꾼에게는 완전히 뜻밖의 일이었다. 그때까지 \"악\" 말고는 아무것도 사랑해 본 적 없던 그에게는 새롭고 놀라운 경험이었다. 알료샤가 그를 떠났을 때, 그는 그때까지 결코 배우려 들지 않았던 무언가를 배웠노라고 스스로 고백했다.\n\n앞서 이미 말한 바 있거니와, 그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내이자 드미트리의 어머니인 아델라이다 이바노브나를 몹시 싫어했고, 반대로 가엾은 \"정신 나간 여자\" 소피야 이바노브나는 주인을 비롯해 그녀를 함부로 헐뜯거나 가볍게 입에 올리는 자가 있으면 누구든 막아서며 감싸 주었다. 이 불행한 아내를 향한 그의 연민은 그에게 거의 신성한 무언가가 되어 버려서, 이십 년이 지난 지금까지도 누구든 그녀를 두고 업신여기는 말을 흘리는 것을 견디지 못하고 당장 그 무례한 자를 제지하곤 했다. 겉으로 보면 그리고리는 차갑고 위엄 있고 말수가 적었으며, 말을 할 때면 경솔함 없이 한 마디 한 마디를 저울질하듯 신중하게 했다. 첫눈에는 그가 그 온순하고 고분고분한 아내를 사랑하는지 어떤지 도무지 알 수 없었으나, 그는 진정으로 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀 또한 그것을 알고 있었다.\n\n마르파 이그나티예브나는 결코 어리석은 여자가 아니었으니, 어쩌면 남편보다 영리했을지도 모르고, 적어도 세상사에 있어서만큼은 그보다 더 신중했다. 그럼에도 그녀는 시집온 이래로 의문이나 불평 한마디 없이 모든 일에서 남편에게 양보해 왔으며, 그의 정신적 우월함을 존경했다. 두 사람이 평생을 살아오며 서로 주고받은 말이라고는 놀라우리만치 적었고, 그것도 오직 일상의 가장 긴요한 일들에 관한 것뿐이었으니, 참으로 눈여겨볼 만한 노릇이었다. 근엄하고 위엄 있는 그리고리는 자신의 온갖 근심과 책무를 홀로 곱씹었으므로, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 그가 자기 조언 따위를 필요로 하지 않는다는 것을 진작부터 알아 익숙해져 있었다. 그녀는 남편이 자신의 침묵을 존중하고 그것을 그녀의 분별 있음의 표시로 받아들인다고 느꼈다. 그는 단 한 번을 빼고는 그녀를 때린 적이 없었고, 그 한 번도 가볍게 손을 댄 것에 지나지 않았다. 표도르 파블로비치가 아델라이다 이바노브나와 결혼하고 난 그 이듬해 어느 날, 마을의 처녀들과 아낙네들이—그때는 다들 농노였다—노래하고 춤추라는 부름을 받고 저택 앞으로 불려 나온 일이 있었다. 한창 \"푸른 풀밭에서\"를 부르기 시작할 무렵, 그때만 해도 젊은 여자였던 마르파가 앞으로 깡충 뛰어나와 \"러시아 춤\"을 추었는데, 그것이 마을식이 아니라 그녀가 부유한 미우소프 집안의 하녀로 일하던 시절에, 모스크바에서 온 무용 선생이 배우들에게 춤을 가르치던 그 집안 전용 극장에서 추던 식이었다. 그리고리는 제 아내가 춤추는 꼴을 보았고, 한 시간 뒤 집 안 오두막에서 그녀의 머리채를 살짝 잡아끌며 따끔하게 본때를 보여 주었다. 그러나 그것으로 끝이었다. 매질은 두 번 다시 되풀이되지 않았고, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 춤을 끊었다.\n\n하느님은 그들에게 자식을 점지해 주지 않았다. 아이 하나가 태어났으나 곧 죽고 말았다. 그리고리는 아이들을 좋아했고, 그것을 드러내는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다. 아델라이다 이바노브나가 달아났을 때, 그리고리는 그때 세 살배기 어린아이였던 드미트리를 맡아, 제 손으로 머리를 빗기고 함지박에 넣어 씻기며 거의 일 년 동안이나 돌보았다. 그 뒤에는 이반과 알료샤를 돌보았는데, 그 일로 장군 부인은 그의 따귀를 갈겨 보답했으나, 이에 관해서는 이미 다 이야기한 바 있다. 그가 제 자식에게서 얻은 유일한 행복이라고는 그 아이의 탄생을 기다리던 그 기대뿐이었다. 막상 아이가 태어났을 때 그는 슬픔과 공포에 짓눌렸다. 갓난아이에게는 손가락이 여섯이었던 것이다. 그리고리는 이 일에 어찌나 짓눌렸던지 세례식 날까지 입을 다물었을 뿐 아니라 정원에 틀어박혀 지냈다. 때는 봄이었고, 그는 사흘 동안 채마밭을 파헤쳤다. 셋째 날이 아이의 세례식으로 정해져 있었는데, 그동안 그리고리는 하나의 결론에 다다라 있었다. 성직자들이 모여 있고 대부 노릇을 맡기로 한 표도르 파블로비치를 비롯한 손님들이 와 있던 오두막으로 들어선 그는, 느닷없이 이 아이를 \"도무지 세례받게 해서는 안 된다\"고 선언했다. 그는 조용히, 간결하게, 한 마디 한 마디를 억지로 짜내듯 내뱉으며 무딘 눈초리로 사제를 뚫어지게 응시했다.\n\n\"어째서 안 된단 말이오?\" 사제가 사람 좋게 놀라며 물었다.\n\n\"이놈은 용이니까요.\" 그리고리가 웅얼거렸다.\n\n\"용이라니? 무슨 용 말이오?\"\n\n그리고리는 한동안 말이 없었다. \"자연의 어그러짐이지요.\" 그는 막연하게, 그러나 단호하게, 그리고 더는 말하기를 명백히 꺼리는 기색으로 웅얼거렸다.\n\n사람들은 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 물론 그 가엾은 아이에게 세례를 베풀었다. 그리고리는 세례반 앞에서 간절히 기도했으나, 그 갓난아이에 대한 그의 생각은 변함이 없었다. 그러면서도 그는 어떤 식으로도 끼어들지 않았다. 병약한 아이가 살아 있는 동안 그는 좀처럼 그 아이를 들여다보지 않았고, 아예 눈길조차 주지 않으려 했으며, 대개는 오두막을 멀리한 채 지냈다. 그러나 보름 만에 아이가 아구창으로 죽고 말았을 때, 그는 손수 아이를 그 작은 관에 뉘었고, 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 아이를 바라보았으며, 사람들이 그 얕은 작은 무덤을 메우고 있을 때 무릎을 꿇고 땅에 엎드려 절했다. 그 뒤로 그는 여러 해 동안 제 자식을 입에 올리지 않았고, 마르파 또한 그 앞에서 아이 이야기를 꺼내지 않았으며, 설령 그리고리가 없는 자리라 해도 결코 속삭이는 소리보다 크게는 그 이야기를 하지 않았다. 마르파의 눈에 띈 것은, 그가 그 매장의 날부터 \"신앙\"에 몰두하여 성인전을 읽기 시작했다는 것이었으니, 그는 대개 홀로 말없이 앉아, 늘 크고 둥근 은테 안경을 쓰고 그것을 읽었다. 소리 내어 읽는 일은 드물었고, 그래봤자 사순절에나 그러했다. 그는 욥기를 즐겨 읽었고, 또 어쩌다가 \"하느님을 경외하는 시리아 사람 이사크 신부\"의 어록과 설교집을 한 권 손에 넣어 여러 해를 두고 끈질기게 읽었는데, 그 내용을 거의 이해하지 못했으나 어쩌면 바로 그 까닭에 그것을 더욱 귀히 여기고 사랑했는지도 모른다. 근래에 그는 인근에 자리 잡은 채찍고행파 교단의 교리에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 그는 분명 그것에 마음이 흔들렸으나, 새 신앙으로 넘어가는 것은 합당치 않다고 판단했다. 신학서적을 읽는 그 버릇은 그에게 한층 더 근엄한 표정을 안겨 주었다.\n\n그는 어쩌면 신비주의에 끌리기 쉬운 천성이었는지도 모른다. 그리고 그의 기형아의 탄생과 그 죽음에는, 마치 특별한 섭리에 의한 것인 양, 또 하나의 기이하고 놀라운 사건이 따라붙었으니, 그것은 훗날 그 자신의 말마따나 그의 영혼에 \"도장\"을 찍어 놓았다. 자식을 묻은 바로 그날 밤, 마르파는 갓난아이의 울음소리에 잠을 깼다. 그녀는 겁에 질려 남편을 깨웠다. 그는 귀를 기울이더니 그것이 아이 울음이라기보다는 누군가 신음하는 소리에 더 가까운 것 같다고, \"여자일지도 모르겠다\"고 말했다. 그는 일어나 옷을 입었다. 제법 따스한 오월의 밤이었다. 그가 계단을 내려가는데, 정원 쪽에서 신음 소리가 또렷이 들려왔다. 그러나 마당에서 정원으로 통하는 문은 밤이면 잠가 두었고, 정원은 사방이 튼튼하고 높은 울타리로 둘러쳐져 있어 그 밖에는 달리 들어갈 길이 없었다. 그리고리는 집으로 되돌아가 등불을 켜고 정원 열쇠를 챙겨 들었는데, 여전히 아이 울음소리를 들었다고, 그것이 제 갓난아이가 울며 자기를 부르는 소리라고 믿고 있는 아내의 발작적인 두려움 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말없이 정원으로 들어섰다. 그곳에서 그는 그 신음 소리가 정원 문 가까이에 서 있는 목욕탕에서 나오는 것이며, 그것이 한 여자의 신음이라는 것을 단박에 알아챘다. 목욕탕 문을 열어젖힌 그는 그 자리에서 굳어 버릴 광경을 마주했다. 거리를 떠돌아다니며 온 읍내에 '냄새나는 리자베타'(리자베타 스메르댜시차야)라는 별명으로 알려진 백치 처녀가 목욕탕 안으로 기어들어 와 막 아이를 낳은 것이었다. 그녀는 아이를 곁에 둔 채 죽어 가며 누워 있었다. 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 평생 말이라고는 할 줄 몰랐기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀의 사연은 따로 한 장을 필요로 한다.\n\n# Chapter II. Lizaveta\n\n그리고리를 유난히 충격에 빠뜨리고, 매우 불쾌하고 역겨운 의심을 굳혀 준 한 가지 정황이 있었다. 이 리자베타는 난쟁이 같은 존재로, 그녀가 죽은 뒤 경건한 노파들이 가엾이 여기며 말하곤 했듯이 \"키가 다섯 자에서 손가락 한 마디쯤 모자랐다.\" 넓적하고 건강하며 불그레한 얼굴에는 텅 빈 백치의 표정이 어려 있었고, 두 눈에 박힌 멈춘 듯한 시선은 온순한 빛을 띠었음에도 불쾌하기 짝이 없었다. 그녀는 여름이건 겨울이건 가리지 않고 맨발로, 삼베 적삼 한 벌만 걸친 채 떠돌아다녔다. 거칠고 거의 검다시피 한 머리카락은 양털처럼 곱슬거리며 머리 위에 일종의 커다란 모자 같은 형상을 이루었다. 그것은 늘 진흙으로 딱딱하게 엉겨 있었고, 나뭇잎과 잔가지 조각, 대팻밥 따위가 들러붙어 있었으니, 그녀가 언제나 맨땅 위, 흙먼지 속에서 잠들었기 때문이다. 그녀의 아버지는 집도 없이 병들어 술에 절어 사는 사내로 일리야라는 이름이었는데, 가진 것을 모조리 잃고 여러 해 동안 어느 살림 넉넉한 상인 집에서 막일꾼으로 지냈다. 어머니는 이미 오래전에 죽고 없었다. 심술궂고 병든 일리야는 리자베타가 제게 돌아올 때마다 사람답지 않게 두들겨 패곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 그에게 돌아오는 일은 드물었으니, 온 고을 사람이 그녀를 백치라 하여, 그래서 하느님께 특별히 사랑받는 자라 하여 기꺼이 거두어 보살피려 했기 때문이다. 일리야의 고용주들과 고을의 많은 사람, 특히 상인들은 그녀에게 옷이라도 변변히 입혀 주려 애썼고, 겨울이면 으레 높은 장화와 양가죽 외투로 그녀를 그럴듯하게 차려입혔다. 그러나 그녀는 사람들이 입혀 주는 대로 저항 없이 받아 입기는 했으나, 으레 어디론가 가 버렸는데, 주로 대성당 현관 쪽으로 가서는 받은 것을 모두—머릿수건이며 양가죽 외투며 치마며 장화며—벗어 그 자리에 남겨 두고, 전처럼 적삼 차림으로 맨발이 되어 걸어가 버리곤 했다. 한번은 이런 일이 있었으니, 새로 부임한 도지사가 우리 고을을 순시하다가 리자베타를 보고는 가장 여린 감수성에 상처를 입었다. 그녀가 백치라는 말을 들었음에도 그는, 스무 살 된 젊은 여자가 적삼 한 벌만 걸치고 떠돌아다니는 것은 풍기를 어지럽히는 일이니 다시는 이런 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 선언했다. 그러나 도지사는 제 갈 길로 가 버렸고, 리자베타는 여전히 그대로 남았다. 마침내 그녀의 아버지가 죽자, 그녀는 고아가 되어 고을의 신앙심 깊은 사람들 눈에 한층 더 가엾고 받아들일 만한 존재가 되었다. 사실 모두가 그녀를 좋아하는 듯했고, 사내아이들조차 그녀를 놀리지 않았는데, 우리 고을의 사내아이들, 특히 학생들이 짓궂기로는 이만저만이 아니었음을 생각하면 놀라운 일이었다. 그녀는 낯선 집에 거리낌 없이 들어가곤 했으나 아무도 그녀를 내쫓지 않았다. 모두가 그녀에게 친절했고 무언가를 쥐여 주었다. 동전 한 닢을 받으면 그녀는 그것을 받아 들고는 곧장 교회나 감옥의 헌금 단지에 넣어 버렸다. 장터에서 둥근 빵이나 호빵을 받으면 그녀는 맨 처음 마주친 아이에게 건네주었다. 더러는 고을에서 가장 부유한 부인들 중 하나를 붙들어 세워 그것을 주기도 했는데, 그 부인은 기꺼이 그것을 받았다. 정작 그녀 자신은 검은 빵과 물 외에는 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다. 값비싼 물건이나 돈이 아무렇게나 놓여 있는 비싼 가게에 들어가더라도 아무도 그녀를 감시하지 않았으니, 그녀라면 사람들이 무심코 놓아둔 수천 루블을 보더라도 단돈 한 푼 손대지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 교회에 거의 나가지 않았다. 그녀는 교회 현관에서 잠을 자거나, 울짱(우리 고을에는 지금도 울타리 대신 울짱이 많다)을 넘어 채마밭으로 기어들어 가 잠을 잤다. 그녀는 적어도 일주일에 한 번은 \"집에\", 그러니까 아버지의 옛 고용주 집에 모습을 드러내곤 했고, 겨울이면 밤마다 그곳으로 가서 행랑이나 외양간에서 잠을 잤다. 사람들은 그녀가 이런 생활을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 놀라워했으나, 그녀는 그것에 익숙해져 있었고, 비록 그토록 작은 몸집이었으나 체질만은 튼튼했다. 고을 사람 중에는 그녀가 이 모든 일을 오로지 자존심 때문에 한다고 단언하는 이도 있었으나, 그것은 도무지 믿기 어려운 말이었다. 그녀는 말이라고는 거의 못 했고, 다만 이따금 알아들을 수 없는 끙끙 소리를 낼 따름이었다. 그런 그녀에게 자존심이라니 가당키나 한 말인가?\n\n(여러 해 전) 구월의 어느 맑고 따뜻한 달밤에 이런 일이 있었다. 술에 취한 대여섯 명의 한량들이 우리 지방의 관념으로 보아 몹시 늦은 시각에 클럽에서 돌아오는 길이었다. 그들은 집들의 뒤뜰 사이로 양쪽에 울짱이 늘어선 \"뒷길\"을 지났다. 이 길은 우리가 강이라 부르곤 하던 길고 악취 풍기는 웅덩이 위에 놓인 다리로 통했다. 한량들은 울짱 아래 쐐기풀과 우엉 더미 사이에서 리자베타가 잠들어 있는 것을 보았다. 그들은 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 들여다보며 웃어 댔고, 거리낌 없는 음탕한 농지거리를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그 가운데 한 젊은 신사가 짓궂은 호기심을 발동해, 과연 누가 이런 짐승 같은 것을 여자로 볼 수 있겠느냐는 둥 시답잖은 물음을 던졌다…… 모두가 고고한 혐오감을 드러내며 그것은 불가능한 일이라고 단언했다. 그러나 그 무리에 끼어 있던 표도르 파블로비치가 불쑥 앞으로 나서더니, 결코 불가능하지 않을뿐더러 오히려 거기엔 묘한 짜릿함이 있다는 둥 떠들어 댔다…… 사실 그 무렵 그는 어릿광대 노릇을 도가 지나치게 하고 있었다. 그는 앞에 나서서 좌중을 즐겁게 하는 것을 좋아했는데, 겉으로는 물론 대등한 처지인 척했으나 실상은 그들 앞에서 비굴한 신세였다. 마침 그것은 그가 페테르부르크에서 첫 아내가 죽었다는 소식을 받은 직후였고, 모자에 상장을 두른 채 술을 마시며 어찌나 파렴치하게 굴던지 우리 가운데 가장 막돼먹은 자들조차 그 꼴을 보고 충격을 받을 지경이었다. 한량들은 물론 이 뜻밖의 의견에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그 가운데 하나는 심지어 그더러 어디 그 말대로 해 보라고 부추기기까지 했다. 다른 자들은 여전히 더없이 흥겨운 가운데서도 그 생각을 한층 더 단호하게 물리쳤고, 마침내 그들은 제 갈 길로 떠났다. 훗날 표도르 파블로비치는 자기도 그들과 함께 갔노라고 맹세했는데, 어쩌면 정말 그랬을지도 모르나, 아무도 확실히 알지 못했고, 또 끝내 아무도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 대여섯 달이 지나자 온 고을이 격렬하고도 진실한 분노에 차서 리자베타의 처지를 입에 올리며, 그녀를 욕보인 그 파렴치한 놈이 대체 누구인지 알아내려 들었다. 그러다 별안간 온 고을에 무서운 소문이 퍼졌으니, 그 파렴치한 놈이 다름 아닌 표도르 파블로비치라는 것이었다. 누가 이 소문을 퍼뜨렸는가? 그 술 취한 무리 가운데 다섯은 고을을 떠났고, 우리 가운데 아직 남아 있는 자라곤 다 큰 딸들을 둔 나이 지긋하고 매우 존경받는 문관 참사관 하나뿐이었는데, 설령 무슨 근거가 있었다 한들 그가 이런 이야기를 퍼뜨렸을 리는 없었다. 그러나 소문은 곧장 표도르 파블로비치를 가리켰고, 끈질기게 그를 가리키기를 멈추지 않았다. 물론 이것은 그에게 그리 대수로운 일도 아니었으니, 그는 한낱 상인 무리를 상대로 굳이 반박하느라 수고할 위인이 아니었다. 그 시절 그는 거만하여, 자기가 그토록 융숭히 대접하던 관리와 귀족들로 이루어진 자기 모임 밖에서는 입을 여는 것조차 탐탁잖게 여겼다.\n\n당시 그리고리는 자기 주인을 위해 힘껏 두둔하고 나섰다. 그는 주인을 변호하느라 싸움과 언쟁을 마다하지 않았고, 마침내 몇몇 사람을 제 편으로 끌어들이는 데 성공했다. \"그건 그 계집년 잘못이오.\" 그는 이렇게 단언했고, 죄를 지은 자는 카르프라는, 감옥에서 탈출하여 우리 고을에 숨어 지냈던 까닭에 그 이름이 우리에게 익히 알려진 위험한 죄수라는 것이었다. 이 추측은 그럴듯하게 들렸으니, 카르프가 마침 그해 가을 그 무렵에 인근에 있었고 세 사람의 재물을 털었다는 것이 사람들의 기억에 남아 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 사건과 그것을 둘러싼 온갖 입방아도 가엾은 백치 여자를 향한 사람들의 동정을 거두어 가지는 못했다. 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 더 극진히 보살핌을 받았다. 콘드라티예바라는 살림 넉넉한 상인의 미망인이 사월 말께 그녀를 자기 집에 들이기로 하고, 해산할 때까지는 밖으로 내보내지 않으려 했다. 사람들은 그녀를 줄곧 지켜보았으나, 그 경계에도 불구하고 그녀는 바로 마지막 날 빠져나가, 표도르 파블로비치의 정원으로 들어갔다. 그 몸으로 어떻게 높고 견고한 울타리를 넘었는지는 끝내 수수께끼로 남았다. 누군가가 그녀를 들어 올려 넘겨 주었을 것이라고 주장하는 이들도 있었고, 그보다 더 으스스한 무엇을 넌지시 암시하는 이들도 있었다. 가장 그럴듯한 설명은 그것이 자연스레 일어난 일이라는 것이니—정원에서 잠을 자려 울짱을 넘어 다니는 데 익숙했던 리자베타가 그 몸으로도 어찌어찌 이 울타리를 기어올라, 뛰어내리다가 몸을 다쳤으리라는 것이다.\n\n그리고리는 마르파에게 달려가 그녀를 리자베타에게 보내고, 자기는 바로 가까이에 사는 늙은 산파를 데리러 달려갔다. 그들은 아기는 살려 냈으나, 리자베타는 새벽녘에 숨을 거두었다. 그리고리는 아기를 받아 안고 집으로 데려와, 아내를 자리에 앉히고는 그 무릎 위에 아기를 올려놓았다. \"하느님의 아이요—고아는 모두의 핏줄이거니와,\" 그가 말했다, \"우리에겐 그 누구보다도 그러하지. 우리의 잃어버린 어린것이 이 아이를 우리에게 보내 준 것이니, 악마의 아들과 거룩한 백치 사이에서 나온 아이라네. 이 아이를 거두어 키우고, 더는 울지 말게나.\"\n\n그리하여 마르파가 아이를 길렀다. 아이는 파벨이라는 세례명을 받았는데, 사람들은 곧 거기에 표도로비치(표도르의 아들)를 갖다 붙이기를 주저하지 않았다. 표도르 파블로비치는 이 모든 일에 아무 이의도 달지 않았고, 오히려 재미있어했으나, 자기 책임만은 여전히 한사코 부인했다. 고을 사람들은 그가 그 업둥이를 거두어들인 것을 흐뭇해했다. 훗날 표도르 파블로비치는 그 아이에게 성(姓)을 하나 지어 붙였으니, 어미의 별명을 따서 스메르댜코프라 불렀다.\n\n이렇게 하여 이 스메르댜코프는 표도르 파블로비치의 두 번째 하인이 되었고, 우리 이야기가 시작되는 무렵에는 그리고리와 마르파와 함께 별채에서 살고 있었다. 그는 요리사로 부려지고 있었다. 마땅히 이 스메르댜코프에 관해서도 무언가 이야기해야 하겠으나, 독자의 주의를 이런 천한 하인들에게 이토록 오래 붙들어 두는 것이 면구스러우니, 이야기를 풀어 가는 가운데 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 더 이야기할 수 있기를 바라며 본래의 이야기로 돌아가겠다.\n\n# Chapter III. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Verse\n\n알료샤는 아버지가 마차에서 그에게 소리쳐 던진 명령을 듣고 한동안 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그러나 마음이 편치 않았음에도 그는 가만히 서 있지는 않았다. 그것은 그의 방식이 아니었다. 그는 아버지가 위에서 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 알아보려고 곧장 부엌으로 갔다. 그러고는 길을 가는 동안 자기를 괴롭히는 의혹에 대한 답을 찾을 수 있으리라 믿으면서 발걸음을 옮겼다. 미리 덧붙여 말해 두자면, \"요와 베개를 가지고\" 집으로 돌아오라는 아버지의 고함은 조금도 그를 겁먹게 하지 못했다. 그는 그 위압적인 고함이 효과를 노린 \"허세\"에 지나지 않음을 완벽하게 이해하고 있었다. 마찬가지로 우리 고장의 한 장사꾼은 친구들을 불러 자기 명명일을 축하하다가 보드카를 더 달라는 청을 거절당하자 화가 치밀어 자기 그릇과 가구를 박살 내고 제 옷과 아내의 옷을 찢고 끝내는 제 집 창문까지 깨뜨렸는데, 이 모든 것이 오로지 효과를 노린 짓이었다. 물론 이튿날 술이 깨자 그는 깨진 잔과 받침을 두고 후회했다. 알료샤는 아버지가 이튿날, 어쩌면 그날 저녁에라도 자기를 수도원으로 돌려보내 주리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 더구나 그는 아버지가 다른 누구에게는 상처를 줄지언정 자기에게만은 그러지 않으리라고 확신하고 있었다. 알료샤는 온 세상 누구도 결코 자기에게 상처를 주려 하지 않으리라고, 게다가 그 누구도 자기에게 상처를 줄 수 없으리라고 확신하고 있었다. 이것은 그에게 하나의 공리로서, 한번 받아들인 뒤로는 두 번 다시 의심하지 않는 진리였으며, 그는 그것에 의지해 망설임 없이 길을 갔다.\n\n그러나 바로 그때 그는 또 다른 종류의 불안에 시달리고 있었는데, 그것을 또렷이 표현할 수 없었던 까닭에 더욱더 마음이 쓰였다. 그것은 한 여자에 대한, 곧 카테리나 이바노브나에 대한 두려움이었으니, 그녀는 호흘라코바 부인이 전해 준 쪽지를 통해 무슨 일로든 자기를 찾아와 달라고 그토록 절박하게 간청했던 것이다. 이 청과, 또 가야만 한다는 사실은 곧장 그의 마음에 불안한 감정을 불러일으켰고, 이 감정은 암자에서와 수도원장 처소에서 벌어진 소동에도 불구하고 오전 내내 점점 더 고통스럽게 자라났다. 그가 불안했던 것은 그녀가 무슨 이야기를 할지, 자기가 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 알지 못해서가 아니었다. 또한 그가 그녀를 그저 여자라는 이유로 두려워한 것도 아니었다. 비록 그가 여자에 대해 아는 것은 적었으나, 그는 어린 시절부터 수도원에 들어가기까지 평생을 오직 여자들 틈에서 보냈다. 그가 두려워한 것은 바로 그 여자, 카테리나 이바노브나였다. 그는 그녀를 처음 본 순간부터 두려워했다. 그는 그녀를 두세 번 보았을 뿐이고, 어쩌다 몇 마디 말을 건넸을 뿐이었다. 그는 그녀를 아름답고 오만하며 위압적인 처녀로 여기고 있었다. 그를 괴롭힌 것은 그녀의 아름다움이 아니라 무언가 다른 것이었다. 그리고 그 두려움의 막연함이 두려움 자체를 더 키웠다. 그 처녀의 목적이 더없이 고결하다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그녀는 형 드미트리가 이미 자기에게 못되게 굴었음에도 오로지 너그러운 마음으로 그를 구하려 애쓰고 있었다. 그러나 알료샤가 이 모든 곱고 너그러운 마음을 알아보고 정당하게 평가하면서도, 그녀의 집에 가까이 다가가자마자 등줄기에 오싹한 한기가 흐르기 시작했다.\n\n그는 그토록 가까운 벗인 이반이 그녀와 함께 있지는 않으리라고 생각했으니, 이반은 분명 지금 아버지와 함께 있을 것이기 때문이었다. 드미트리는 더더욱 거기 없으리라고 확신했으며, 그 까닭을 어렴풋이 예감하고 있었다. 그리하여 대화는 그녀와 단둘이서만 나누게 될 터였다. 그는 그 운명적인 만남에 앞서 형 드미트리에게 달려가 만나 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했다. 편지를 보여 주지는 않더라도 그것에 관해 형과 이야기를 나눌 수는 있을 터였다. 그러나 드미트리는 멀리 떨어진 곳에 살았고, 게다가 분명 집에 없을 것이 뻔했다. 잠시 멈춰 선 끝에 그는 마침내 결심을 굳혔다. 빠르고 익숙한 동작으로 성호를 긋고는 곧 미소를 지으며, 그는 단호하게 그 무서운 부인이 있는 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n그는 그녀의 집을 알고 있었다. 큰길을 지나 시장 광장을 가로질러 가면 한참을 돌아가야 했다. 우리 고장은 작기는 해도 흩어져 있어 집들이 서로 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그러는 동안 아버지가 그를 기다리고 있을 테고, 어쩌면 아직 그 명령을 잊지 않았을지도 몰랐다. 아버지가 억지를 부릴 수도 있으니, 그는 서둘러 다녀와야만 했다. 그래서 그는 뒷길로 질러가기로 했으니, 그 일대의 땅이라면 한 치도 빠짐없이 훤히 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러자면 울타리를 따라 돌고, 낮은 담을 넘고, 남의 집 뒤뜰을 가로질러야 했는데, 거기서 마주치는 사람마다 그를 알아보고 인사를 건넸다. 이렇게 가면 절반의 시간으로 큰길에 닿을 수 있었다.\n\n그는 아버지네 정원에 잇닿아 있는, 창문이 네 개 달린 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 집의 정원을 지나야 했다. 알료샤가 아는 바로, 이 집의 주인은 자리보전을 한 늙은 여자로, 페테르부르크의 장군 댁들에서 양갓집 하녀로 일하던 딸과 함께 살고 있었다. 이제 그 딸은 병든 어머니를 돌보느라 일 년째 집에 머물고 있었다. 그녀는 늘 고운 옷으로 차려입었는데, 늙은 어머니와 그녀가 어찌나 가난에 빠져 있던지 날마다 표도르 파블로비치네 부엌으로 수프와 빵을 얻으러 갔고, 마르파가 선뜻 내주었다. 그러면서도 이 젊은 여자는 수프를 얻으러 다니면서도 옷가지는 단 한 벌도 팔지 않았으니, 그중에는 긴 자락이 달린 옷까지 있었다는 것이다. 이 사실을 알료샤는 라키틴에게서 들었는데, 그는 고을 안에서 벌어지는 일이라면 무엇이든 늘 꿰고 있는 사내였다. 그는 듣자마자 그 이야기를 잊어버렸으나, 이제 정원에 다다르자 그 자락 달린 옷이 떠올라, 생각에 잠겨 숙이고 있던 고개를 들었고, 그러다 전혀 뜻밖의 광경과 마주쳤다.\n\n정원의 낮은 담 너머에서 드미트리가 무언가 위에 올라선 채 몸을 앞으로 기울이고 격하게 손짓하며 그를 향해 손짓해 부르고 있었는데, 누가 엿들을까 두려워 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하는 기색이 역력했다. 알료샤는 담 쪽으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"고개를 들어 주길 잘했다. 하마터면 너한테 소리를 지를 뻔했지 뭐냐.\" 미탸가 기쁘고 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"어서 이리로 넘어와! 네가 와 줘서 정말 잘됐다! 마침 네 생각을 하고 있던 참이거든!\"\n\n알료샤도 반가웠으나, 어떻게 담을 넘어야 할지 알 수 없었다. 미탸가 그 억센 손으로 그의 팔꿈치 밑을 받쳐 뛰어오르는 것을 도와주었다. 알료샤는 사제복 자락을 걷어 올리고는 맨다리로 거리를 누비는 부랑아 같은 날렵함으로 담을 뛰어넘었다.\n\n\"잘했다! 자, 이리 와.\" 미탸가 열에 들뜬 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"어디로요?\" 알료샤가 주위를 둘러보며 속삭였는데, 그러고 보니 자기들 말고는 아무도 없는 한적한 정원 안에 있었다. 정원은 작았지만 집은 적어도 쉰 걸음은 떨어져 있었다.\n\n\"여긴 아무도 없잖아요. 왜 속삭이세요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"내가 왜 속삭이냐고? 빌어먹을!\" 드미트리가 목청껏 소리쳤다. \"이것 봐라, 자연이 사람한테 얼마나 어리석은 장난을 치는지. 난 지금 여기 몰래 숨어서 망을 보고 있다. 나중에 설명해 주마. 그런데 비밀이라는 걸 알다 보니 그럴 필요도 없는데 바보처럼 속삭이기 시작한 거야. 가자. 저쪽으로. 그때까진 조용히 있어. 너한테 입 맞추고 싶구나.\n\n세상 가운데 계신 하느님께 영광을, 내 안에 계신 하느님께 영광을……\n\n네가 오기 전에 여기 앉아서 바로 그 구절을 되뇌고 있었단다.\"\n\n정원은 넓이가 삼 에이커쯤 되었고, 나무는 네 변의 울타리를 따라서만 심겨 있었다. 사과나무와 단풍나무, 보리수와 자작나무가 있었다. 정원 한가운데는 텅 빈 풀밭으로, 여름이면 거기서 건초가 수백 푸드씩 실려 나갔다. 정원은 여름 한철 몇 루블에 세를 놓았다. 또 가장자리를 따라 산딸기와 까치밥나무, 구스베리가 심긴 밭들이 펼쳐져 있었고, 집 가까이에는 얼마 전에 채소밭이 들어서 있었다.\n\n드미트리는 동생을 정원에서 가장 외진 구석으로 데려갔다. 그곳에는 보리수 덤불과 묵은 검은까치밥나무, 딱총나무, 불두화나무, 라일락 떨기 속에 세월에 검게 그을린, 다 쓰러져 가는 초록빛 정자가 한 채 서 있었다. 벽은 격자세공으로 되어 있었으나 비를 가릴 만한 지붕만은 아직 남아 있었다. 이 정자가 언제 세워졌는지는 아무도 모른다. 한 오십 년 전쯤 당시 그 집을 소유했던 폰 슈미트라는 퇴역 대령이 지었다는 이야기가 전해 내려왔다. 모든 것이 허물어져 가고 있었으니, 바닥은 썩어 가고, 널빤지는 헐거워졌으며, 목재에서는 곰팡내가 풍겼다. 정자 안에는 땅에 붙박인 초록빛 나무 탁자가 있었고 그 둘레로 아직은 앉을 만한 초록 의자 몇 개가 놓여 있었다. 알료샤는 형이 들뜬 상태에 있음을 곧바로 알아챘는데, 정자에 들어서면서 탁자 위에 반쯤 남은 브랜디 병과 포도주잔이 놓여 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"이건 브랜디다.\" 미탸가 웃었다. \"네 눈빛이 보이는구나. '형이 또 마시는구나!' 하는. 환영을 믿지 마라.\n\n쓸모없고 거짓된 무리를 믿지 말고 네 의혹일랑 내려놓아라.\n\n난 마시는 게 아니라, 그저 네 그 돼지 같은 라키틴 말마따나 '한잔 즐기는' 것뿐이다. 그자는 언젠가 칠등관쯤은 될 텐데도 늘 '한잔 즐긴다'는 소리나 늘어놓을 거야. 앉아라. 알료샤, 난 너를 두 팔로 끌어안고 으스러지도록 가슴에 꼭 껴안고 싶구나. 온 세상에서—정말로—저-엉-말로—(알아듣겠냐?) 내가 사랑하는 건 너밖에 없으니까!\"\n\n그는 마지막 말을 일종의 황홀경에 잠겨 내뱉었다.\n\n\"너 말고는, 내 신세를 망쳐 놓은 한 '계집'밖에 없지. 사랑에 빠지는 것과 사랑하는 것은 별개의 일이야. 한 여자에게 사랑에 빠지면서도 그 여자를 미워할 수 있는 법이거든. 그걸 명심해라! 아직은 명랑하게 이야기할 수 있구나. 여기 탁자 옆에 앉아라. 난 네 곁에 앉아서 너를 바라보며 계속 이야기하마. 너는 잠자코 있어. 내가 계속 이야기할 테니까, 이제 그럴 때가 됐거든. 그런데 생각해 보니, 알겠냐, 조용히 말하는 게 낫겠다. 여기서는—여기서는—어떤 귀가 엿듣고 있을지 도무지 알 수가 없으니까. 다 설명해 주마. 흔히들 말하듯이, '이야기는 다음으로 이어진다'고 말이야. 내가 왜 너를 그토록 그리워했겠냐? 왜 요 며칠 내내, 또 바로 지금 너를 그토록 목말라했겠냐? (내가 여기 닻을 내린 지 닷새째다.) 너한테만은 모든 걸 털어놓을 수 있으니까, 그래야만 하니까, 네가 필요하니까, 내일이면 내가 구름에서 떨어질 테니까, 내일이면 인생이 끝나면서 또 시작될 테니까. 너는 한 번이라도 절벽에서 구덩이로 곤두박질치는 걸 느껴 본 적 있냐, 꿈에서라도 꾸어 본 적 있냐? 내가 바로 그렇게 떨어지고 있는 거다, 다만 꿈속에서가 아니지. 그런데 난 두렵지 않아. 너도 두려워하지 마라. 사실 두렵긴 하지만, 그래도 난 그게 즐겁구나. 아니, 즐거운 게 아니라 황홀한 거야. 빌어먹을, 그게 뭐가 됐든! 강한 영혼이든, 약한 영혼이든, 계집 같은 영혼이든—그게 뭐가 됐든! 자연을 찬미하자. 보아라, 저 햇살을, 저 맑은 하늘을, 이파리들은 죄다 푸르고, 아직 여름이로구나. 오후 네 시에 이 고요함이라니! 너 어디 가는 길이었냐?\"\n\n\"아버지께 가는 길이었는데, 먼저 카테리나 이바노브나한테 들렀다 가려고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그 여자한테, 그리고 아버지한테! 오오! 이 무슨 우연이람! 내가 왜 너를 기다렸겠냐? 영혼의 구석구석, 심지어 내 갈빗대 속까지 너를 굶주리고 목말라하며 기다린 까닭이? 그야 너를 아버지한테, 또 그 여자, 카테리나 이바노브나한테 보내서, 그 여자와도 아버지와도 끝장을 내기 위해서지. 천사를 보내려는 거다. 아무나 보낼 수도 있었지만, 난 천사를 보내고 싶었어. 그런데 마침 네가 아버지와 그 여자를 만나러 가는 길이라니.\"\n\n\"형은 정말로 저를 보내려고 하셨어요?\" 알료샤가 괴로운 표정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"가만! 너도 알고 있었구나! 게다가 보아하니 단박에 다 알아챘구나. 하지만 조용히, 잠깐만 조용히 있어. 슬퍼하지도 말고, 울지도 마라.\"\n\n드미트리는 일어서서 잠시 생각하더니 손가락을 이마에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"그 여자가 너한테 청했지, 편지인지 뭔지를 써 보냈지, 그래서 그 여자한테 가는 거지? 그게 아니면 갈 일이 없었을 테니까?\"\n\n\"여기 그분 쪽지가 있어요.\" 알료샤가 호주머니에서 그것을 꺼냈다. 미탸는 그것을 재빨리 훑어보았다.\n\n\"게다가 너는 뒷길로 오는 길이었구나! 오, 신들이시여, 그를 뒷길로 보내 주셔서 감사합니다. 마치 우화에 나오는 어리석은 늙은 어부에게 황금 물고기가 찾아오듯이 그가 나한테 와 주었으니! 들어 봐라, 알료샤, 들어 봐라, 동생아! 이제 너한테 모든 걸 이야기하마. 누군가에게는 털어놓아야만 하니까. 하늘에 있는 천사한테는 이미 이야기했지만, 땅 위의 천사한테도 이야기하고 싶구나. 네가 바로 땅 위의 천사다. 너는 듣고 판단하고 용서해 줄 테지. 내가 필요한 게 바로 그거야, 누군가 나보다 위에 있는 이가 나를 용서해 주는 것. 들어 봐라! 만약 두 사람이 이 땅의 모든 것을 떨치고 미지의 세계로 날아가 버린다면, 적어도 둘 중 하나가 그렇게 떠나거나 파멸하기 전에 다른 누군가에게 찾아가 '날 위해 이걸 해 다오'라고 청한다면—임종의 자리에서나 청할 법한, 여태껏 한 번도 청해 본 적 없는 그런 부탁을—그 다른 사람이, 만약 그가 벗이거나 형제라면, 그걸 거절하겠냐?\"\n\n\"제가 하겠어요. 그러니 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요, 어서요.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"어서라! 흠!…… 서두르지 마라, 알료샤, 너는 서두르고 안달복달하는구나. 지금은 서두를 필요가 없다. 이제 세상이 새로운 국면으로 접어들었거든. 아, 알료샤, 네가 황홀경을 이해하지 못한다니 참 안타깝구나. 그런데 내가 너한테 무슨 소리를 하고 있는 거냐? 마치 네가 그걸 모르기라도 하는 것처럼. 내가 얼마나 어리석은지! 내가 무슨 소리를 하는 거지? '인간이여, 고결하라!'—이건 누가 한 말이더라?\"\n\n알료샤는 기다리기로 마음먹었다. 어쩌면 정말로 자기가 할 일이 여기 있을지도 모른다고 느꼈다. 미탸는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 받친 채 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 둘 다 말이 없었다.\n\n\"알료샤,\" 미탸가 말했다. \"비웃지 않을 사람은 너 하나뿐이야. 내 고백을 실러의 『환희의 송가』, 『안 디 프로이데』로 시작하고 싶구나! 난 독일어는 모른다. 그저 제목이 그렇다는 것만 알 뿐이야. 내가 취해서 헛소리를 하는 거라고 생각하지 마라. 난 조금도 취하지 않았다. 브랜디야 다 좋지만, 날 취하게 하려면 두 병은 있어야 하거든.\n\n비틀거리는 나귀 위에 올라탄, 발그레한 낯짝의 실레노스.\n\n하지만 난 사분의 일 병도 마시지 않았으니, 난 실레노스가 아니다. 난 실레노스가 아니야, 비록 강한[1] 사람이긴 해도. 한번 결심을 굳혔으니까. 말장난은 용서해 다오. 오늘은 말장난보다 훨씬 더 많은 걸 용서해 줘야 할 테니까. 불안해하지 마라. 시간을 질질 끄는 게 아니다. 난 말이 되는 소리를 하는 거고, 곧 본론으로 들어가마. 너를 마냥 애태우게 하진 않을 거야. 가만, 그게 어떻게 시작하더라?\"\n\n그는 고개를 들어 잠시 생각하더니 열정에 차서 읊기 시작했다.\n\n\"사납고 두려움에 떨며 동굴 속에 벌거벗은 혈거인이 몸을 숨기고, 집 없는 유랑민이 떠돌아다니며 기름진 들판을 황폐하게 만들었네. 창과 화살로 위협하며 숲속을 사냥꾼이 헤매었으니…… 저 잔혹하고 적대적인 기슭에 표류한 가엾은 자들에게 화 있을진저!\n\n높은 올림포스 봉우리에서 어머니 케레스가 내려왔으니, 저 야만의 땅을 헤매며 잃어버린 딸 프로세르피네를 찾았네. 허나 여신은 안식처를 찾지 못하고, 따뜻한 환대도 찾지 못했으며, 신들을 향한 경배를 증언하는 신전 하나 없었네.\n\n들판에서도, 포도밭에서도 잔치를 꾸밀 과실은 오지 않고, 오직 피로 물든 제물의 살점만이 제단의 불 위에서 타올랐으며, 비탄에 잠긴 여신이 우울한 눈길을 돌리는 곳마다, 더없이 천한 타락에 빠진 인간이 그 추악함을 드러냈네.\"\n\n미탸는 흐느껴 울기 시작하더니 알료샤의 손을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"얘야, 얘야, 타락 속에 있다, 지금 이 순간도 타락 속에 있어. 이 땅에서 인간이 겪어야 할 고통이 어마어마하구나, 견뎌야 할 시련이 산더미야. 내가 그저 진창과 술독에 뒹구는 장교복 입은 짐승일 뿐이라고는 생각하지 마라. 난 거의 오로지 그 타락한 인간만을 생각한다—내가 거짓말을 하는 게 아니라면 말이야. 부디 내가 거짓말로 잘난 척하는 게 아니기를 하느님께 빈다. 내가 그 인간을 두고 생각하는 건, 내가 바로 그 인간이기 때문이야.\n\n추악함에서 영혼을 씻어 내고 빛과 가치에 이르고자 한다면, 인간은 돌이켜 영원토록 옛 어머니 대지에 매달려야 하리.\n\n그런데 문제는, 내가 어떻게 영원토록 어머니 대지에 매달릴 수 있느냐는 거다. 난 그 대지에 입 맞추지 못하고, 그 품에 안기지도 못해. 내가 농부나 양치기라도 되어야 한단 말이냐? 난 계속 나아가지만, 내가 수치를 향해 가는지 빛과 기쁨을 향해 가는지 알지 못한다. 그게 바로 문제야, 세상 모든 것이 수수께끼니까! 그리고 내가 더없이 천한 타락에 빠질 때마다(그건 늘 일어나는 일이었지) 난 언제나 그 케레스와 인간에 관한 시를 읽곤 했다. 그게 날 바로잡아 주었느냐? 천만에! 난 카라마조프니까. 난 구덩이에 뛰어들 때도 머리부터 곤두박질치며 발꿈치를 위로 쳐들고, 그렇게 치욕스러운 꼴로 떨어지는 걸 즐기고, 게다가 그걸 자랑스러워하지. 그리고 바로 그 타락의 밑바닥에서 찬미의 송가를 부르기 시작한다. 내가 저주받아도 좋다. 내가 천하고 비루해도 좋아, 다만 내 하느님을 감싼 그 베일 자락에 입 맞추게만 해 다오. 비록 내가 악마를 따른다 해도, 주여, 저는 당신의 아들이고, 저는 당신을 사랑하며, 그것 없이는 세상이 설 수 없는 그 기쁨을 느낍니다.\n\n영원한 환희는 길러 내네 만물의 영혼을, 그것은 은밀한 효모가 되어 생명의 잔을 불꽃으로 채우네. 그 부름에 풀잎은 저마다 빛을 향해 고개 돌렸고, 태양계들도 진화하여 나왔네 혼돈과 어두운 밤으로부터, 현자의 눈길도 미치지 못하는 가없는 공간의 영역을 채우며. 너그러운 자연의 자애로운 품에서 숨 쉬는 모든 것이 환희를 마시고, 새도 짐승도 기어 다니는 것들도 모두 그녀가 이끄는 곳을 따르네. 인간에게 베푸는 그녀의 선물은 곤경 속의 벗, 화관과 거품 이는 새 술, 천사에게는—하느님 옥좌의 환영, 벌레에게는—육욕.\n\n하지만 시는 이만하면 됐다! 난 눈물을 흘리고 있구나. 울게 내버려 다오. 다들 비웃을 어리석은 짓일지도 모르지. 하지만 넌 비웃지 않겠지. 네 눈에도 빛이 어리는구나. 시는 이만하면 됐어. 이제 너한테 하느님이 '육욕'을 주신 그 벌레들에 관해 이야기하고 싶구나.\n\n벌레에게는—육욕.\n\n내가 바로 그 벌레다, 동생아, 그건 특히 나를 두고 하는 말이야. 우리 카라마조프 집안 사람들은 죄다 그런 벌레라서, 천사인 너한테도 그 벌레가 깃들어 있어, 언젠가 네 핏속에 폭풍을 일으킬 거다. 폭풍이라고 한 건, 육욕이란 폭풍이기 때문이야—폭풍보다 더 사나운 것이지! 아름다움이란 무섭고 끔찍한 것이다! 그것이 무서운 까닭은 헤아릴 수 없는 데다 결코 헤아려질 수도 없기 때문이야, 하느님은 우리에게 수수께끼만 던져 주시니까. 여기서 경계선들이 맞닿고 온갖 모순이 나란히 공존하지. 난 교양 있는 사람은 아니다, 동생아, 그래도 이것만은 많이 생각해 봤어. 세상에 얼마나 많은 신비가 있는지 무서울 지경이야! 너무도 많은 수수께끼가 이 땅의 인간을 짓누른다. 우리는 할 수 있는 데까지 그것들을 풀어 보면서, 물속에서도 살갗을 적시지 않으려 애써야지. 아름다움! 난 고결한 정신과 마음을 지닌 인간이 마돈나의 이상에서 시작해 소돔의 이상으로 끝나 버린다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없다. 더 끔찍한 건, 영혼 속에 소돔의 이상을 품은 인간이 마돈나의 이상을 버리지 않는다는 거고, 그의 가슴이 그 이상으로 불타오를 수도 있다는 거야, 진실로 불타오를 수도 있다는 거지, 마치 순결했던 젊은 날과 다름없이. 그래, 인간이란 넓다, 너무도 넓어, 정말이지. 차라리 더 좁았으면 좋겠다. 도무지 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 악마나 알겠지! 머리에는 수치스러운 것이 가슴에는 아름다움 그 자체로 보이거든. 소돔에 아름다움이 있을까? 내 말을 믿어라, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 인간에게 아름다움은 바로 소돔 속에 있단다. 너 그 비밀을 알고 있었냐? 무서운 건, 아름다움이 끔찍할 뿐 아니라 신비롭기까지 하다는 거야. 거기서 하느님과 악마가 싸우고 있고, 그 싸움터가 바로 인간의 마음이지. 하지만 사람이란 늘 제 아픔만을 떠벌리는 법. 자, 이제 사실을 이야기할 차례구나.\" [1] 미탸의 본명 드미트리는 그리스어로 '대지의 여신에게 바쳐진 자'를 뜻하나, 여기서는 발음의 유사함을 빗댄 말장난이다.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Anecdote\n\n\"그 무렵 나는 거친 생활을 하고 있었지. 아버지가 방금 내가 처녀들을 꾀느라 수천 루블을 썼다고 하시던데, 그건 돼지 같은 날조이고, 그런 일은 전혀 없었어. 설령 있었다 한들, 단지 그것을 위해서 돈이 필요했던 건 아니야. 나한테 돈이란 부속물이고, 마음의 넘쳐흐름이고, 뼈대 같은 거야. 오늘은 귀부인이 내 여자였다가, 내일은 그 자리에 길거리의 계집이 들어서기도 했지. 나는 그 둘 모두를 대접했어. 음악이며 흥청거림이며 집시들한테 돈을 한 줌씩 내던졌지. 더러는 귀부인들한테도 주었어, 그네들도 탐욕스럽게 받았으니까 말이야, 그건 인정해야지, 그러고는 좋아하고 고마워했단 말이야. 귀부인들은 나를 좋아하곤 했어. 다 그런 건 아니지만, 그런 일이 있었어, 있었다니까. 하지만 나는 언제나 샛길이 좋았어, 큰길 뒤편의 어둡고 자그마한 뒷골목 말이야—거기서 모험과 뜻밖의 일을 만나고, 진창 속에서 귀한 금속을 발견하지. 비유로 하는 말이야, 동생. 내가 있던 그 고을엔 글자 그대로의 그런 뒷골목은 없었지만, 도덕적으로는 있었어. 네가 나 같은 사람이라면 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알 텐데. 나는 악덕을 사랑했고, 악덕의 치욕을 사랑했어. 나는 잔혹함을 사랑했지. 내가 벌레가 아니냐, 해로운 곤충이 아니냐? 한마디로 카라마조프 아니냐! 한번은 우리 한 무리가 썰매 일곱 대에 나눠 타고 소풍을 갔지. 어둡고, 겨울이었는데, 나는 한 처녀의 손을 잡고 짓누르기 시작했고, 억지로 입을 맞추게 했어. 그 애는 어느 관리의 딸로, 다정하고 온순하고 고분고분한 아이였지. 그 애는 내가 하는 대로 두었어, 어둠 속에서 많은 걸 허락했어. 가엾게도 그 애는 내가 이튿날 청혼하러 올 거라고 생각했지(나도 꽤 괜찮은 혼처로 여겨지고 있었거든). 그런데 나는 다섯 달 동안 그 애한테 한마디도 하지 않았어. 무도회에서(우리는 늘 무도회를 열었거든) 한구석에 있는 그 애가, 그 눈이 나를 지켜보는 걸 보곤 했지. 그 눈이 불꽃으로 타오르는 걸 봤어—온순한 분노의 불꽃 말이야. 이 놀음은 다만 내 영혼 속에 품고 있던 그 곤충 같은 욕정을 간질였을 뿐이지. 다섯 달 뒤에 그 애는 어느 관리와 결혼해서 그 고을을 떠났어, 여전히 화가 난 채로, 그러면서도 어쩌면 나를 사랑한 채로 말이야. 지금은 행복하게 살고 있어. 잘 봐 두렴, 나는 아무한테도 말하지 않았어. 그걸 자랑하지도 않았어. 내가 비천한 욕망으로 가득 차 있고 비천한 걸 사랑한다고 해도, 나는 비열한 인간은 아니야. 너 얼굴을 붉히는구나, 눈이 번득였어. 너한테는 이런 더러운 얘기는 이쯤이면 됐다. 게다가 이런 건 별것도 아니야—폴 드 코크 풍의 길가에 핀 꽃이었지—이미 잔혹한 곤충이 내 영혼 속에서 무럭무럭 자라고 있긴 했지만. 나한테는 완벽한 추억의 앨범이 있어, 동생. 그 애들에게 신의 축복이 있기를, 가여운 것들. 나는 다투지 않고 관계를 끊으려고 애썼어. 그리고 그 애들을 한 번도 팔아넘긴 적이 없어. 그 애들 중 누구도 자랑거리로 삼은 적이 없지. 하지만 이쯤 하자. 내가 그저 이런 시시한 얘기나 하려고 너를 여기 데려왔다고 생각하진 않겠지. 아니야, 더 묘한 얘기를 들려줄 거야. 그리고 내가 부끄러워하기는커녕 들려주게 되어 기뻐한다고 놀라지 마라.\"\n\n\"형이 그렇게 말하는 건 내가 얼굴을 붉혔기 때문이지.\" 알료샤가 갑자기 말했다. \"나는 형이 한 말이나 형이 한 짓 때문에 얼굴을 붉힌 게 아니야. 나도 형과 똑같은 인간이라서 붉힌 거야.\"\n\n\"네가? 거참, 그건 좀 지나친데!\"\n\n\"아니, 지나치지 않아.\" 알료샤가 열을 띠며 말했다(분명 그것은 새삼스러운 생각이 아니었다). \"사다리는 똑같아. 나는 맨 아래 칸에 있고, 형은 위쪽, 한 열세 번째쯤 어딘가에 있지. 나는 그렇게 봐. 하지만 다 마찬가지야. 종류로 치면 완전히 똑같아. 맨 아래 칸에 있는 사람은 누구든 결국 맨 꼭대기까지 올라가게 마련이거든.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 아예 첫 발을 디디지 말아야겠군.\"\n\n\"피할 수 있는 사람이라면 디디지 않는 편이 낫지.\"\n\n\"그런데 너는 피할 수 있어?\"\n\n\"못 할 것 같아.\"\n\"쉿, 알료샤, 쉿, 사랑스러운 녀석! 네 손에 입이라도 맞추고 싶구나, 네가 이렇게 내 마음을 흔들어 놓으니. 그 깍쟁이 그루셴카는 남자 보는 눈이 있어. 언젠가 나한테 그러더군, 어느 날인가 너를 잡아먹어 버리겠다고. 알았어, 알았어, 안 그럴게! 파리가 들끓는 이 부패의 들판에서, 이제 나의 비극으로 넘어가자, 역시 파리가 들끓는 비극, 그러니까 온갖 종류의 비열함이 들끓는 비극으로 말이야. 영감이 내가 순결을 짓밟았다고 거짓말을 늘어놓긴 했지만, 사실 내 비극 속에 그 비슷한 일이 정말로 있긴 했어, 다만 단 한 번뿐이었고, 그나마도 실행에 옮기진 못했지. 일어나지도 않은 일로 나를 책망하는 그 영감은 이 사실조차 몰라. 나는 이 얘기를 아무한테도 한 적이 없으니까. 네가 처음이야, 물론 이반만 빼고—이반은 다 알지. 너보다 한참 전부터 알고 있었어. 하지만 이반은 무덤이야.\"\n\n\"이반이 무덤이라고?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n알료샤는 잔뜩 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"나는 보병 연대의 중위였지만, 그래도 일종의 죄수처럼 감시를 받고 있었어. 그래도 그 작은 고을에서는 엄청난 환대를 받았지. 나는 돈을 좌우로 펑펑 썼어. 사람들은 나를 부자로 여겼고, 나 자신도 그렇게 여겼어. 하지만 다른 점에서도 그들의 마음에 들었던 게 분명해. 다들 나를 두고 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서도 나를 좋아했거든. 노인인 우리 대령은 갑자기 나를 싫어하게 됐어. 늘 나를 못살게 굴었지만, 나한테는 든든한 친구들이 있었고, 게다가 온 고을이 내 편이었으니, 그도 나를 어쩌지 못했지. 그를 마땅한 예우로 대하기를 거부한 건 내 잘못이었어. 나는 거만했거든. 이 고집스러운 영감은 사실 아주 좋은 사람으로, 마음이 따뜻하고 손님을 잘 대접했는데, 두 번 결혼해서 두 아내를 다 잃었어. 첫 아내는 미천한 집안 출신이었는데, 자기처럼 꾸밈없는 딸 하나를 남겼지. 내가 거기 있을 때 그 처녀는 스물네 살이었고, 아버지와, 어머니의 여동생인 이모와 함께 살고 있었어. 이모는 순박하고 글을 모르는 사람이었고, 조카는 순박하면서도 활달했지. 나는 사람들에 대해 좋게 말하는 걸 좋아해. 아가피야보다 더 매력적인 성품을 지닌 여자는 만나 본 적이 없어—생각해 보렴, 이름이 아가피야 이바노브나였단다! 게다가 외모도 나쁘지 않았어, 러시아 식으로 말하자면 키가 크고 통통하고 풍만한 몸매에 아름다운 눈을 가졌지, 얼굴은 좀 투박했지만. 청혼하는 사람이 둘이나 있었는데도 그 애는 결혼하지 않았어. 그들을 거절하고도 여전히 명랑했지. 나는 그 애와 친하게 지냈어, '그런' 식이 아니라, 순수한 우정이었지. 나는 여자들과 아주 순수하게 친구로 지낸 적이 많아. 나는 그 애한테 충격적일 만큼 솔직하게 얘기하곤 했는데, 그 애는 그저 웃기만 했어. 많은 여자가 그런 격의 없음을 좋아하지, 게다가 그 애는 처녀였으니 그게 아주 재미있었어. 또 한 가지, 그 애를 두고 아가씨라고는 도무지 생각할 수가 없었지. 그 애와 이모는 아버지 집에서 일종의 자발적인 겸손으로 살았어, 다른 사람들과 동등한 위치에 서려 하지 않으면서 말이야. 그 애는 누구에게나 사랑받았고, 누구에게나 쓸모가 있었어, 솜씨 좋은 침모였거든. 그 일에 재주가 있었지. 보수를 요구하지 않고 기꺼이 일을 해 주었지만, 누가 보수를 내밀면 거절하지도 않았어. 물론 대령은 사정이 아주 달랐지. 그는 그 지방의 주요 인사 중 하나였어. 집 문을 활짝 열어 놓고 온 고을 사람들을 대접하며 만찬과 무도회를 열었지. 내가 도착해서 대대에 합류했을 무렵, 온 고을이 대령의 둘째 딸이 돌아온다는 소식으로 떠들썩했어, 대단한 미인인 그 딸이 막 수도의 어느 명문 학교를 졸업한 참이었거든. 이 둘째 딸이 바로 카테리나 이바노브나야, 그 애는 둘째 아내의 자식인데, 그 아내는 어느 명망 있는 장군 가문 출신이었지. 그렇긴 해도 내가 믿을 만한 소식통으로부터 알아낸 바로는, 그 아내 역시 대령에게 돈을 한 푼도 가져다주지 못했어. 연줄이 있었을 뿐, 그게 다였지. 어쩌면 무슨 유산에 대한 기대가 있었을지도 모르지만, 결국 아무것도 안 됐어.\n\n\"그래도 그 아가씨가 기숙학교에서 다니러 왔을 때, 온 고을이 되살아났어. 우리 고을에서 가장 지체 높은 부인들—'각하' 부인 두 분과 대령 부인 한 분—이며, 그 뒤를 따르는 나머지 사람들이 모두 단숨에 그 애를 떠받들며 그 애를 위해 연회를 열었지. 그 애는 무도회와 소풍의 꽃이었고, 사람들은 곤궁한 가정교사들을 돕는답시고 활인화까지 꾸몄어. 나는 거들떠보지도 않고 여전히 전처럼 거칠게 지냈는데, 그 무렵 내 무용담 하나가 온 고을을 떠들썩하게 했지. 어느 날 저녁 포병 중대장 집에서 그 애의 눈이 나를 가늠하는 걸 봤지만, 나는 그 애와 알고 지내기를 경멸하기라도 하듯 다가가지 않았어. 얼마 안 가 어느 저녁 모임에서는 다가가서 말을 걸긴 했지. 그 애는 나를 거들떠보지도 않고 비웃듯이 입술을 꼭 다물더군. '두고 보자. 복수해 주마.' 나는 그렇게 생각했어. 그 무렵 나는 여러 번 지독한 바보짓을 했고, 나 자신도 그걸 의식하고 있었지. 더 고약했던 건, '카첸카'가 순진한 기숙학교 아가씨가 아니라 성품을 갖춘 사람, 거만하고 정말로 고결한 원칙을 지닌 사람이라는 걸 느꼈다는 거야. 무엇보다도 그 애는 교양과 지성을 갖추었는데, 나는 그 어느 것도 없었거든. 너는 내가 청혼하려 했다고 생각하니? 아니야, 나는 그저 복수하고 싶었을 뿐이야, 나는 이렇게나 대단한 영웅인데 그 애는 그걸 느끼지도 못하는 것 같았으니까.\n\n\"그러는 사이에 나는 술과 흥청거림으로 세월을 보냈고, 마침내 중령이 나를 사흘간 영창에 가두었지. 바로 그 무렵 아버지가 나한테 육천 루블을 보냈는데, 내가 아버지에게 일체의 청구권을 포기한다는 증서를 보낸 데 대한 답이었어—말하자면 우리 사이의 셈을 매듭짓고, 더는 아무것도 바라지 않겠다는 거였지. 그때 나는 그 일을 한마디도 이해하지 못했어. 여기 오기 전까지, 알료샤, 정말로 바로 며칠 전까지, 어쩌면 지금까지도, 나는 아버지와의 돈 문제를 도무지 갈피를 못 잡고 있어. 하지만 그건 됐고, 그 얘긴 나중에 하자.\n\n\"바로 그 돈을 받았을 때, 한 친구한테서 편지를 받았는데 나한테 굉장히 흥미로운 소식을 알려 주었지. 알고 보니 당국이 우리 중령에게 불만을 품고 있었던 거야. 그가 부정을 저질렀다는 혐의를 받고 있었고, 사실 그의 적들이 그에게 한 방 먹일 준비를 하고 있었어. 그러더니 사단장이 와서 한바탕 난리법석을 피웠지. 얼마 안 가 그에게 퇴역 명령이 떨어졌어. 그 일이 다 어떻게 됐는지는 얘기하지 않으마. 분명 그에게는 적들이 있었지. 갑자기 고을 사람들이 그와 그의 온 가족에게 두드러지게 냉담해졌어. 친구들이 다 그에게 등을 돌렸지. 그때 나는 첫 발을 디뎠어. 나는 줄곧 친하게 지내 온 아가피야 이바노브나를 만나서 이렇게 말했어. '당신 아버지 장부에서 국고 돈이 사천오백 루블이나 모자란다는 걸 아시오?'\n\n\"'무슨 말씀이세요? 왜 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요? 얼마 전에 장군이 다녀가셨는데 다 괜찮았다고요.'\n\n\"'그때는 그랬지만, 지금은 그렇지 않소.'\n\n\"그 애는 몹시 겁에 질렸어.\n\n\"'저를 겁주지 마세요!' 그 애가 말했어. '누가 그런 말을 했나요?'\n\n\"'불안해할 것 없소.' 내가 말했지. '나는 아무한테도 말하지 않을 테니. 알다시피 나는 무덤처럼 입이 무거운 사람이오. 다만 「만일의 경우」를 생각해서 한마디 덧붙이고 싶은데, 그들이 당신 아버지한테 그 사천오백 루블을 내놓으라고 요구하고 그가 그걸 마련하지 못하면, 그는 재판을 받고 늘그막에 일개 졸병으로 복무하게 될 거요, 당신이 그 아가씨를 나한테 몰래 보내 줄 생각이 없다면 말이오. 마침 나한테 돈이 들어왔거든. 당신이 원한다면 그 애한테 사천 루블을 주고, 비밀은 신성하게 지켜 주겠소.'\n\n\"'아, 이 악당!'—그 애가 한 말이 그거였어. '이 사악한 악당! 어찌 감히!'\n\n\"그 애는 분노에 사로잡혀 가 버렸고, 나는 그 뒤에다 대고 다시 한번 비밀은 신성하게 지키겠다고 외쳤지. 미리 말해 두는데, 그 두 순박한 사람, 아가피야와 그 이모는 이 일이 진행되는 내내 완벽한 천사처럼 처신했어. 그네들은 진심으로 자기네 '카탸'를 떠받들었고, 그 애가 자기네보다 한참 위라고 여기며 손발이 되어 시중을 들었지. 그런데 아가피야가 우리가 나눈 대화를 그 애한테 말한 거야. 그건 나중에 알았어. 그 애가 그걸 숨기지 않았고, 물론 그게 바로 내가 바라던 거였지.\n\n\"갑자기 새 소령이 부임해서 대대의 지휘를 맡게 됐어. 늙은 중령은 즉시 병이 나서 이틀 동안 방에서 나오지도 못했고, 국고 돈을 인계하지도 못했지. 크라프첸코 의사는 그가 정말로 병이 났다고 단언했어. 하지만 나는 사실을 알고 있었고, 오래전부터 알고 있었지, 지난 사년간 그 돈이 사령관의 시찰 때를 빼고는 한 번도 그의 손에 있어 본 적이 없다는 걸 말이야. 그는 그 돈을 믿을 만한 사람한테 빌려주곤 했는데, 우리 고을의 트리포노프라는 상인으로, 늙은 홀아비에 수염이 덥수룩하고 금테 안경을 쓴 자였어. 그는 시장에 가서 그 돈으로 짭짤한 장사를 하고는 전액을 대령에게 돌려주었지, 시장에서 사 온 선물과 함께 대부에 대한 이자까지 얹어서 말이야. 그런데 이번에는(나는 이 모든 걸 트리포노프의 아들이자 상속자한테서 순전히 우연히 들었는데, 그놈은 침이나 질질 흘리는 애송이로 세상에서 가장 못된 부류 중 하나였지)—이번에는, 그러니까, 트리포노프가 시장에서 아무것도 가져오지 않았던 거야. 중령이 그에게 달려갔지. '나는 당신한테서 돈을 받은 적이 없고, 받을 수도 없었소.' 그가 들은 대답이라곤 그게 전부였어. 그래서 이제 우리 중령은 머리에 수건을 두른 채 집에 틀어박혀 있고, 셋이서 그 위에 얼음을 얹느라 부산을 떨었지. 그러던 차에 당번병이 장부를 들고 나타나서는 '대대 자금을 즉시, 두 시간 내에 인계하라'는 명령을 전했어. 그는 장부에 서명하고(나중에 그 장부에서 그 서명을 봤어), 일어서서 군복을 입겠다고 하고는 침실로 달려가, 쌍열 총에 실탄을 장전하고, 오른발의 군화를 벗고, 총을 가슴에 대고는, 발로 방아쇠를 더듬기 시작했어. 그런데 내가 한 말을 기억하고 있던 아가피야가 의심을 품고 있었지. 그 애는 몰래 다가가 마침맞게 방 안을 들여다봤어. 그 애는 달려들어 뒤에서 그에게 몸을 던지며 두 팔로 그를 끌어안았고, 총이 발사돼 천장을 맞혔지만 아무도 다치지 않았어. 다른 사람들이 뛰어들어 총을 빼앗고 그의 팔을 붙들었지. 나는 이 모든 걸 나중에 들었어. 나는 집에 있었는데, 땅거미가 지고 있었고, 나는 막 외출할 채비를 하던 참이었어. 옷을 차려입고, 머리를 빗고, 손수건에 향수를 뿌리고, 모자를 집어 들었는데, 갑자기 문이 열리더니, 방 안에서 카테리나 이바노브나가 나를 마주 보고 서 있는 거야.\n\n\"이따금 일이 어떻게 벌어지는지 참 묘하단 말이야. 거리에서 아무도 그 애를 본 사람이 없어서, 고을에서 아무도 그 일을 몰랐어. 나는 나를 돌봐 주는 늙고 쇠약한 두 노파와 함께 묵고 있었는데. 그네들은 무엇이든 들어주는 더없이 친절한 늙은이들이라 나를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 되어 있었고, 내 부탁에 나중에는 두 개의 무쇠 기둥처럼 입을 다물었지. 물론 나는 사정을 단번에 파악했어. 그 애는 걸어 들어와 나를 똑바로 바라봤는데, 그 검은 눈은 단호하다 못해 도전적이기까지 했지만, 입술에는, 입가에는 망설임이 보였어.\n\n\"'언니가 그러더군요.' 그 애가 입을 열었어. '제가 직접 받으러 오면 사천오백 루블을 주시겠다고요. 그래서 왔어요…… 돈을 주세요!'\n\n\"그 애는 그걸 버티지 못했어. 숨이 가쁘고, 겁에 질려, 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 입꼬리와 그 둘레의 주름이 떨렸지. 알료샤, 듣고 있니, 아니면 잠든 거니?\"\n\n\"미탸, 형이 모든 진실을 다 말하리라는 걸 알아.\" 알료샤가 흥분해서 말했다.\n\n\"말하고 있어. 일어난 그대로 모든 진실을 다 말한다면, 나 자신도 봐주지 않을 거야. 내 첫 생각은—카라마조프다운 거였어. 한번은 지네한테 물려서, 동생, 그 열병으로 보름이나 앓아누운 적이 있었지. 그런데 그때 지네 한 마리가 내 심장을 물어뜯는 게 느껴졌어—해로운 곤충 말이야, 알겠니? 나는 그 애를 위아래로 훑어봤어. 너 그 애를 봤지? 미인이야. 하지만 그때는 다른 식으로 아름다웠어. 그 순간 그 애가 아름다웠던 건 그 애가 고결했기 때문이고, 나는 악당이었기 때문이지. 그 애는 아버지를 위한 너그러움과 희생의 온갖 위엄을 두르고 있었고, 나는—한 마리 벌레였어! 그리고 악당이긴 했지만 그 애는 몸도 마음도 완전히 내 손안에 있었어. 그 애는 옴짝달싹할 수 없었지. 솔직히 말하는데, 그 생각이, 그 독기 어린 생각이 내 마음을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 긴장으로 거의 까무러칠 지경이었어. 도무지 거역할 수 없을 것만 같았어. 마치 내가 벌레처럼, 독거미처럼, 한 점의 연민도 없이 굴어야 할 것만 같았지. 나는 숨도 제대로 쉴 수 없었어. 알아 두렴, 나는 이튿날 그 애에게 청혼하러 가서, 말하자면 일이 명예롭게 끝나도록, 아무도 모르거나 알 수 없도록 했어야 마땅했어. 비록 내가 비천한 욕망을 지닌 인간이긴 해도, 나는 정직하거든. 그런데 바로 그 순간 어떤 목소리가 내 귓가에 속삭이는 것 같았어. '하지만 네가 내일 청혼하러 가면, 저 처녀는 너를 쳐다보지도 않을걸. 마부를 시켜 너를 마당에서 걷어차 내쫓으라고 하겠지. 「온 고을에 떠벌려라」고 그 애는 말할 거야, 「나는 네가 두렵지 않아」.' 나는 그 아가씨를 바라봤어, 그 목소리가 나를 속인 게 아니었지. 틀림없이 그렇게 됐을 거야, 의심할 여지가 없어. 이제 그 애의 얼굴에서 내가 집 밖으로 쫓겨나리라는 걸 알 수 있었어. 내 악의가 끓어올랐지. 나는 그 애한테 가장 야비한 돼지 같은 상놈의 수작을 부리고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었어. 비웃음을 띤 채 그 애를 바라보며, 내 앞에 서 있는 그 자리에서, 장사치나 쓸 법한 말투로 그 애를 멍하게 만들고 싶었던 거야.\n\n\"'사천 루블이라니! 무슨 말씀이오? 농담이었소. 셈을 너무 쉽게 헤아렸구려, 아가씨. 이백 루블이라면, 원한다면야 기꺼이 드리리다. 하지만 사천 루블은 그런 변덕에 내던질 액수가 아니오. 헛수고를 하셨군요.'\n\n\"물론 나는 그 놀음에서 졌을 거야. 그 애는 달아나 버렸겠지. 하지만 그건 지독한 복수였을 거야. 그럴 만한 값어치가 있었을 거고. 나는 그 수작을 부린 것만으로도 남은 평생을 후회로 울부짖었겠지. 믿어지니, 그 어떤 다른 여자한테도, 단 한 명한테도, 그런 순간에 증오를 품고 바라본 적은 한 번도 없었어. 하지만 맹세코, 나는 삼 초, 어쩌면 오 초쯤 끔찍한 증오를 품고 그 애를 바라봤어—사랑과는, 가장 미쳐 버린 사랑과는 머리카락 한 올 차이밖에 안 되는 그 증오 말이야!\n\n\"나는 창가로 가서 얼어붙은 유리창에 이마를 댔는데, 지금도 기억하지, 그 얼음이 불처럼 내 이마를 태우던 걸. 그 애를 오래 붙들어 두진 않았어, 걱정 마. 나는 몸을 돌려 탁자로 가서, 서랍을 열고 오천 루블짜리 지폐를 한 장 꺼냈어(프랑스어 사전 속에 끼워져 있었지). 그러고는 말없이 그걸 그 애한테 보여 주고, 접어서, 손에 쥐여 주고, 복도로 통하는 문을 열고는, 뒤로 물러서며 그 애한테 깊숙이 고개를 숙였어, 더없이 공손하고, 더없이 인상적인 절을, 정말이야! 그 애는 온몸을 부르르 떨더니, 잠시 나를 응시하다가, 무섭게 창백해져서—사실 시트처럼 새하얘져서—그러더니 갑자기, 격렬하게가 아니라 부드럽게, 다정하게, 내 발치에 머리를 조아렸어—기숙학교식 인사가 아니라, 이마를 바닥에 대는 러시아식 절을 말이야. 그 애는 벌떡 일어나 달아나 버렸어. 나는 칼을 차고 있었지. 나는 그 칼을 뽑아 그 자리에서 하마터면 내 몸을 찌를 뻔했어. 왜인지는 나도 몰라. 물론 그건 끔찍하게 어리석은 짓이었겠지. 아마 기쁨에서였던 것 같아. 사람이 기쁨에서 스스로 목숨을 끊을 수도 있다는 걸 이해할 수 있겠니? 하지만 나는 내 몸을 찌르진 않았어. 그저 칼에 입을 맞추고 칼집에 도로 꽂았을 뿐이지—하긴 그건 너한테 굳이 말할 필요가 없는 일이지만. 그리고 내 내면의 갈등을 너한테 얘기하면서 나 자신을 미화하려고 좀 과장해서 칠한 것 같기도 해. 하지만 그냥 넘어가자, 인간의 마음을 캐고 드는 자들은 다 지옥에나 가라지! 자, 카테리나 이바노브나와의 그 '모험'은 이쯤 해 두자. 그러니까 이제 이반이 그 일을 알고, 너도—그 밖엔 아무도 몰라.\"\n\n드미트리는 일어나서, 흥분한 채 한두 걸음 떼고는, 손수건을 꺼내 이마를 닦더니, 다시 앉았는데, 아까와 같은 자리가 아니라 맞은편에 앉아서, 알료샤가 그를 마주 보려면 완전히 돌아앉아야 했다.\n\n\"이제,\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"전반부는 알겠어요.\"\n\n\"전반부는 알겠다고. 그 절반은 한 편의 드라마였고, 거기 그곳에서 다 끝나 버렸지. 후반부는 비극인데, 그건 지금 여기서 벌어지고 있는 거다.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 후반부는 아직 하나도 모르겠어요.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 나는? 내가 그걸 안다고 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 드미트리 형. 한 가지 중요한 질문이 있어요. 말해 봐요, 형은 약혼한 사이였고, 지금도 약혼한 사이인가요?\"\n\n\"우리가 곧바로 약혼한 건 아니었다. 그 사건이 있고 나서 석 달이 지나서였지. 그 이튿날 나는 그 일이 다 끝나 버렸다고, 마무리됐다고, 뒷이야기는 없을 거라고 스스로에게 말했어. 그녀에게 청혼하는 건 비열한 짓 같았거든. 그녀 쪽에서도 읍내에 머문 여섯 주 동안 아무런 기척이 없었다. 단 한 가지 일만 빼면 말이지. 그녀가 다녀간 다음 날, 하녀가 슬그머니 내게 봉투 하나를 갖다주더구나. 뜯어 보니, 그 안에는 지폐를 헐어 거슬러 준 돈이 들어 있었다. 필요한 건 사천오백 루블뿐이었지만, 환전에 이백 루블쯤 수수료가 붙었지. 그녀가 보낸 건 이백육십 루블쯤이었다. 정확하진 않지만, 편지 한 장도, 설명 한마디도 없었어. 연필 자국이라도 있나 하고 봉투를 샅샅이 뒤졌지만, 아-무것도 없더구나! 뭐, 그래서 나는 남은 돈을 어찌나 흥청망청 술판에 다 써 버렸던지, 새로 온 소령이 나를 호되게 나무라지 않을 수 없었지.\n\n자, 그런데 그 중령이 대대 공금을 내놓더란 말이다. 모두가 깜짝 놀랐지. 그가 그 돈에 손도 안 댔으리라고는 아무도 믿지 않았으니까. 그런데 그 돈을 갚자마자 그가 병으로 쓰러져 자리에 눕더니, 석 주 뒤에 뇌가 물러지는 병이 와서 닷새 만에 죽고 말았다. 군장(軍葬)으로 묻혔지. 전역 처리를 받을 새도 없었으니까. 장례가 끝나고 열흘 뒤에 카테리나 이바노브나는 고모와 언니를 데리고 모스크바로 갔어. 그런데 어떻게 됐는지 아느냐, 바로 그들이 떠나던 그날(나는 그녀를 보지도 못했고, 배웅도 작별 인사도 못 했다) 나는 자그마한 쪽지 하나를 받았다. 얇은 푸른 종이 한 장에 연필로 단 한 줄만 적혀 있더구나. '편지 드리겠어요. 기다리세요. K.' 그게 다였다.\n\n이제 나머지는 두어 마디로 설명하마. 모스크바에서 그들의 운명은 번개 같은 속도로, 아라비아 동화 같은 뜻밖의 방식으로 뒤바뀌었다. 그들의 가장 가까운 친척인 그 장군 부인이 갑자기 상속녀이자 가장 가까운 핏줄인 두 조카딸을 잃었거든. 둘 다 같은 주에 천연두로 죽었지. 슬픔에 짓눌린 노부인은 카탸를 딸처럼, 자기 유일한 희망처럼 맞아들여 그녀에게 매달리고는, 유언장을 카탸에게 유리하게 고쳤어. 하지만 그건 장래의 일이었지. 그러는 한편 당장 쓰라고 결혼 지참금으로 팔만 루블을 주어 마음대로 하게 했다. 그 노부인은 신경질적인 여자였어. 나중에 모스크바에서 그녀를 좀 봤지.\n\n자, 그런데 갑자기 나는 우편으로 사천오백 루블을 받았다. 너도 짐작하겠지만, 너무 놀라 말문이 막혔지. 사흘 뒤에 약속했던 편지가 왔어. 지금도 내 품에 지니고 있다. 너도 읽어 봐야 해. 그녀는 내 아내가 되겠다고, 자신을 내게 바치겠다고 했어. '미치도록 당신을 사랑합니다.' 그녀는 이렇게 썼지. '당신이 나를 사랑하지 않아도 상관없어요. 내 남편이 되어 주세요. 두려워 마세요. 무슨 일로든 당신을 얽매지 않겠어요. 당신의 소유물이 되겠어요. 당신 발밑의 양탄자가 되겠어요. 영원히 당신을 사랑하고 싶어요. 당신을 당신 자신에게서 구해 내고 싶어요.' 알료샤, 나는 이 천박한 말투로, 이 천박한 어조로, 평생 고칠 수 없는 이 영원히 천박한 어조로 그 구절을 되읊을 자격이 없는 놈이다. 그 편지는 지금도 나를 찌른다. 내가 아무렇지 않을 것 같으냐? 지금도 아무렇지 않을 것 같아? 나는 곧장 답장을 썼다. 모스크바로 갈 수가 없었으니까. 눈물을 흘리며 그녀에게 썼지. 한 가지만은 영원히 부끄러워할 일이 있어. 나는 그녀가 부유하고 지참금이 있는데 나는 거만 떠는 거지에 불과하다는 얘길 꺼냈다! 돈 얘길 입에 올린 거야! 말없이 견뎠어야 했는데, 펜에서 그만 새어 나오고 말았어. 그러고는 곧장 이반에게 편지를 써서, 여섯 장에 걸쳐 그 일에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말을 다 적어, 그를 그녀에게 보냈다. 왜 그런 얼굴을 하느냐? 왜 나를 빤히 쳐다봐? 그래, 이반은 그녀를 사랑하게 됐다. 지금도 그녀를 사랑하고 있어. 나도 안다. 세상 사람들 눈으로 보면 나는 어리석은 짓을 한 거지. 하지만 어쩌면 그 어리석은 짓 하나가 지금 우리 모두를 구원할지도 몰라. 오! 그녀가 이반을 얼마나 높이 평가하는지, 얼마나 존경하는지 모르겠느냐? 그녀가 우리 둘을 견주어 보면, 나 같은 사내를, 더구나 여기서 일어난 그 모든 일을 겪은 뒤에 나 같은 사내를 사랑할 수 있을 것 같으냐?\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그녀가 형 같은 사람을 사랑하는 거지, 이반 형 같은 사람을 사랑하는 게 아니라고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그녀는 자기 자신의 미덕을 사랑하는 거지, 나를 사랑하는 게 아니다.\" 그 말이 드미트리의 입에서 저도 모르게, 거의 독기를 품은 채 튀어나왔다. 그는 웃었지만, 잠시 후 두 눈이 번득이더니 시뻘겋게 달아올라 주먹으로 탁자를 사납게 내리쳤다.\n\n\"맹세하건대, 알료샤,\" 그는 자기 자신을 향한 격렬하고도 진실한 분노로 부르짖었다. \"넌 나를 안 믿을지 몰라도, 하느님이 거룩하시고 그리스도가 하느님이심을 두고 맹세하건대, 방금 내가 그녀의 고결한 감정을 비웃기는 했다만, 나는 영혼으로 따지면 그녀보다 백만 배는 더 비천한 놈이고, 그녀의 그 고결한 감정은 천상의 천사처럼 진실하다는 걸 안다. 그게 바로 이 일의 비극이야. 내가 그걸 확실히 안다는 게 말이다. 누군가 좀 잘난 척한다고 한들 어떠냐? 나도 그러지 않느냐? 그래도 나는 진심이다, 진심이야. 이반으로 말하면, 그가 지금 자연을 얼마나 저주하고 있을지 나는 이해할 수 있어. 그만한 지성을 지니고서도 말이지! 누구에게, 무엇에게 그녀가 마음을 더 주는 꼴을 봐야 하느냐? 약혼한 몸이고 모두의 시선이 쏠려 있는데도 방탕을 절제하지 못하는 괴물에게, 그것도 제 약혼녀가 빤히 보는 앞에서 그러는 괴물에게! 그런데 나 같은 사내가 더 사랑받고, 그는 거부당하는 거야. 그게 왜냐고? 한 처녀가 감사하는 마음에서 제 인생과 운명을 희생하려 들기 때문이지. 우스꽝스럽지 않으냐! 나는 이반에게 이런 얘길 한마디도 한 적이 없고, 이반도 물론 내게 그런 기색을 비친 적이 없다. 하지만 운명은 이루어질 테고, 더 나은 사내는 제자리를 지킬 것이며, 자격 없는 놈은 제 뒷골목으로 영영 사라져 버릴 거다. 그 더러운 뒷골목으로, 그 사랑하는 뒷골목으로 말이지. 거기가 바로 제 집이고, 거기서 그놈은 제 자유 의지로, 그것도 즐기면서 오물과 악취 속에 잠겨 버릴 거야. 내가 어리석은 소리를 지껄였구나. 이제 할 말도 없다. 닥치는 대로 말을 내뱉지만, 결국 내가 말한 대로 될 거다. 나는 뒷골목에 빠져 죽을 테고, 그녀는 이반과 결혼하겠지.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 드미트리 형.\" 알료샤가 다시 몹시 불안해하며 말을 가로막았다. \"아직 분명히 안 밝힌 게 하나 있어요. 형은 그래도 여전히 약혼한 사이잖아요, 그렇죠? 형의 약혼녀가, 그녀가 원하지 않는다면, 어떻게 형이 약혼을 깰 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 격식을 갖춰 엄숙하게 약혼했지. 내가 모스크바에 도착하자마자 다 치러졌다. 성대한 의식에, 성상(聖像)까지 갖추고, 죄다 격식대로 했어. 장군 부인이 우리를 축복하고는, 너 믿겠느냐? 카탸를 축하해 줬다. '잘 골랐다.' 부인이 그러더구나. '내가 저 사람 속을 훤히 들여다본다.' 그리고 또, 너 믿겠느냐? 부인은 이반은 마음에 안 들어 해서 인사도 거의 안 했어. 나는 모스크바에서 카탸와 얘길 많이 나눴다. 나 자신에 대해 그녀에게 털어놓았지. 진실하게, 정직하게. 그녀는 다 들어 주었어.\n\n달콤한 혼란이 있었고, 다정한 말들이 있었네.\n\n자랑스러운 말들도 있긴 했다만. 그녀는 내게서 새사람이 되겠다는 굳은 약속을 짜냈다. 나는 약속했지, 그리고 여기—\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\"\n\n\"왜, 그래서 내가 오늘, 바로 오늘 너를 불러 여기로 데려온 거다. 기억해 둬라, 너를 보내려고 말이지. 바로 오늘 또다시, 카테리나 이바노브나에게로, 그리고—\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\"\n\n\"그녀에게 내가 다시는 그녀를 보러 가지 않겠다고 전하라는 거다. '그가 안부를 전한다더라'고 말해 줘.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 가능한 일이에요?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 불가능한 일이니까 너를, 나 대신 보내는 거다. 게다가 내가 어떻게 그 말을 직접 그녀에게 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"그럼 형은 어디로 갈 건데요?\"\n\n\"뒷골목으로.\"\n\n\"그럼 그루셴카에게로!\" 알료샤가 두 손을 맞잡으며 비통하게 외쳤다. \"라키틴이 정말 사실을 말한 거였군요? 나는 형이 그저 그녀를 한 번 찾아갔을 뿐, 그게 다인 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n\"약혼한 사내가 그런 식으로 찾아다닐 수 있느냔 말이다. 그게 가당키나 한 일이냐, 그것도 그런 약혼녀를 두고, 온 세상의 눈앞에서? 빌어먹을, 나도 명예란 게 있어! 그루셴카를 드나들기 시작한 순간부터 나는 약혼한 몸이기를, 정직한 사내이기를 그만둔 거다. 나도 그건 안다. 왜 나를 그렇게 보느냐? 봐라, 나는 애초에 그녀를 두들겨 패러 갔던 거다. 그 대위, 아버지의 앞잡이 노릇 하는 그놈이 내 차용증을 그루셴카에게 넘겨서, 그녀를 시켜 나를 상대로 빚 독촉 소송을 걸게 해 나를 끝장내려 한다는 얘길 들었거든. 이제는 그게 사실인 줄 확실히 안다. 그놈들은 나를 겁주려 했던 거야. 나는 그녀를 패러 갔지. 전에 그녀를 흘끗 본 적은 있었다. 첫눈에 사람을 사로잡는 여자는 아니야. 나는 그녀의 그 늙은 상인 정부(情夫) 얘긴 알고 있었다. 지금은 중풍으로 병석에 누워 있는데, 그래도 그녀에게 제법 짭짤한 돈을 남겨 줄 작자지. 그녀가 돈을 밝히고, 돈을 모아 두었다가 악랄한 이자로 빌려 주며, 무자비한 사기꾼에 협잡꾼이라는 것도 알고 있었어. 나는 그녀를 패러 갔다가, 눌러앉고 말았다. 폭풍이 몰아닥쳤지. 그게 역병처럼 나를 쓰러뜨렸어. 나는 지금도 역병에 걸린 채야. 그리고 모든 게 끝났다는 걸, 내게는 다시는 아무것도 없으리라는 걸 안다. 세월의 한 주기가 다 차 버린 거지. 그게 내 처지다. 그리고 비록 나는 운명의 장난으로 거지 신세지만, 그때만은 마침 주머니에 삼천 루블이 있었어. 나는 그루셴카를 데리고 모크로예로 마차를 몰고 갔다. 여기서 이십오 베르스타쯤 떨어진 곳이지. 거기서 집시들을 불러 모으고 샴페인을 들이고, 거기 농부들을 죄다 술에 취하게 했어. 여자들과 처녀들도 몽땅 말이다. 그렇게 수천 루블을 날려 버렸지. 사흘 만에 나는 알거지가 됐지만, 영웅이 됐어. 그래, 그 영웅이 제 뜻을 이뤘을 것 같으냐? 그녀 쪽에선 그런 기미라곤 손톱만큼도 없었다. 내 말하건대, 그 요부 그루셴카는 온몸에 나긋나긋한 곡선이 흐르는 여자야. 그 자그마한 발에서도, 새끼발가락에서도 그게 보인다. 나는 그걸 봤고, 입을 맞췄지. 하지만 그게 다였어, 맹세코! '원한다면 당신과 결혼해 드리죠.' 그녀가 그러더구나. '당신, 거지잖아요, 알죠. 나를 때리지 않겠다고, 내가 하고 싶은 대로 뭐든 하게 해 주겠다고 약속해요. 그럼 어쩌면 결혼해 드릴지도 몰라요.' 그녀는 웃었다, 지금도 웃고 있고!\"\n\n드미트리는 일종의 광기에 사로잡혀 벌떡 일어섰다. 그는 별안간 술에 취한 사람 같았다. 두 눈이 갑자기 시뻘겋게 충혈됐다.\n\n\"그래서 형은 정말 그녀와 결혼할 작정이에요?\"\n\n\"그녀만 원한다면 당장에라도. 그녀가 원치 않는다 해도, 나는 그대로 머물 거다. 그녀 집 문지기가 되어 줄 테야. 알료샤!\" 그가 외쳤다. 그는 알료샤 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 두 어깨를 붙잡고 사납게 흔들어 대기 시작했다. \"너 아느냐, 이 순진한 녀석아, 이게 다 헛소리란 걸, 정신 나간 헛소리란 걸 말이다. 여기엔 비극이 도사리고 있으니까. 알렉세이, 내 말 들어라, 나는 비천한 인간일지 모른다, 천박하고 타락한 정욕에 사로잡힌 인간일지 몰라. 하지만 도둑이나 소매치기, 드미트리 카라마조프는 결코 그런 놈은 될 수 없다. 자, 그런데 말이다, 내가 바로 그 도둑이요 소매치기라면 어쩌겠느냐. 바로 그날 아침, 내가 그루셴카를 패러 가기 직전에, 카테리나 이바노브나가 나를 불러서는 엄중히 비밀을 지키라며(왜 그랬는지는 모른다, 무슨 이유가 있었겠지) 도청 소재지로 가서 모스크바의 아가피야 이바노브나에게 삼천 루블을 부쳐 달라고, 여기 읍내에서는 아무도 모르게 해 달라고 부탁했어. 그래서 그루셴카를 보러 갈 때 나는 그 삼천 루블을 주머니에 지니고 있었고, 모크로예에서 우리가 흥청거리며 쓴 게 바로 그 돈이다. 그러고 나서 나는 도청에 다녀온 척했지만, 그녀에게 우체국 영수증은 보여 주지 않았어. 돈을 부쳤다고, 영수증을 가져오겠다고 말해 놓고는 여태 안 가져왔지. 잊어버린 척한 거야. 자, 이제 네가 오늘 그녀에게 가서 뭐라고 말할 것 같으냐? '그가 안부를 전한다더라.' 그러면 그녀가 묻겠지. '돈은 어떻게 됐나요?' 그래도 너는 이렇게는 말할 수 있었을 거다. '그는 타락한 호색한이고, 절제 못 하는 정욕에 사로잡힌 비천한 작자입니다. 그때 당신 돈을 부치지 않고 탕진해 버렸어요. 천한 짐승처럼 자제하지 못했기 때문이죠.' 그래도 이렇게 덧붙일 수는 있었겠지. '하지만 도둑은 아닙니다. 여기 당신의 삼천 루블이 있어요. 그가 돌려보냅니다. 아가피야 이바노브나에게는 당신이 직접 부치세요. 그리고 그가 「안부를 전한다」고 말하라더군요.' 하지만 사정이 이러하니, 그녀는 묻겠지. '그런데 그 돈은 어디 있나요?'\"\n\n\"미탸 형, 형은 불행해요, 그래요! 하지만 형이 생각하는 만큼 불행하진 않아요. 절망에 빠져 스스로를 죽도록 괴롭히지 말아요.\"\n\n\"뭐냐, 갚을 삼천 루블을 못 구한다고 내가 권총으로 자살이라도 할 것 같으냐? 바로 그게 문제야. 나는 자살하지 않을 거다. 지금은 그럴 기력이 없어. 나중에는 모르지, 어쩌면. 하지만 지금은 그루셴카에게로 갈 거다. 무슨 일이 벌어지든 상관없어.\"\n\n\"그럼 그다음은요?\"\n\n\"그녀가 나 따위를 받아 준다면 그녀의 남편이 되어 줄 테고, 정부들이 찾아오면 나는 옆방으로 물러가 주지. 그녀 정부들의 덧신을 닦아 주고, 사모바르에 불을 지펴 주고, 심부름을 다녀 주마.\"\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나는 다 이해할 거예요.\" 알료샤가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"이 고통이 얼마나 큰지 알아주고 용서할 거예요. 그녀는 고결한 마음을 지녔고, 형보다 더 불행한 사람은 없으니까요. 그녀 스스로 그걸 알아볼 거예요.\"\n\n\"그녀가 다 용서하진 못할 거다.\" 드미트리가 히죽 웃으며 말했다. \"여기엔 말이다, 동생아, 어떤 여자도 용서 못 할 무언가가 있어. 가장 좋은 방법이 뭔지 아느냐?\"\n\n\"뭔데요?\"\n\n\"삼천 루블을 갚는 거지.\"\n\n\"그 돈을 어디서 구해요? 저기, 나한테 이천 루블이 있어요. 이반 형이 천 루블을 더 줄 거예요. 그럼 삼천이 되잖아요. 그걸 받아서 갚으세요.\"\n\n\"그 삼천 루블을 네가 언제 마련하겠느냐? 게다가 넌 아직 미성년이고, 그리고 너는 오늘, 반드시, 무슨 일이 있어도 오늘 그녀에게 내 작별 인사를 전해야 한다. 돈이 있든 없든 말이다. 나는 더는 질질 끌 수가 없어, 일이 이 지경에 이르렀으니까. 내일은 너무 늦어. 너를 아버지에게 보내야겠다.\"\n\n\"아버지에게요?\"\n\n\"그래, 우선 아버지에게. 삼천 루블을 달라고 해 봐.\"\n\n\"하지만 미탸 형, 아버지는 안 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"줄 리가 있나! 안 줄 거라는 거 안다. 알렉세이, 너 절망이 뭔지 아느냐?\"\n\n\"알아요.\"\n\n\"들어 봐라. 법적으로 그는 내게 빚진 게 없다. 나는 받을 걸 그에게서 다 받았어, 나도 안다. 하지만 도의적으로는 그가 내게 빚진 게 있지 않으냐? 알다시피 그는 내 어머니의 돈 이만팔천 루블로 시작해서 그걸로 십만 루블을 벌었어. 그 이만팔천 가운데 단 삼천만 내게 돌려준다면, 그는 내 영혼을 지옥에서 끌어내 주는 셈이고, 그건 그가 지은 숱한 죄를 속죄해 줄 거다. 그 삼천 루블만 준다면, 내 엄숙히 약속하건대, 나는 모든 걸 끝내고 그는 다시는 내 소식을 듣지 못할 거야. 마지막으로 그에게 아버지 노릇 할 기회를 주는 거다. 하느님 그분께서 친히 이 기회를 그에게 보내신 거라고 전해 줘.\"\n\n\"미탸 형, 아버지는 무슨 일이 있어도 안 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"안 줄 거란 거 안다. 더없이 잘 알아. 지금은 특히 더. 그게 다가 아니다. 나는 더 알고 있는 게 있어. 자, 바로 며칠 전에, 어쩌면 바로 어제, 아버지는 처음으로 진지하게(진지하게라는 말에 밑줄을 그어라) 그루셴카가 정말 농담하는 게 아닐지도 모른다는 걸, 정말로 나와 결혼할 마음이라는 걸 알아챘다. 그는 그녀의 본성을 알아, 그 암고양이를 안단 말이다. 그런데 자기가 그녀에게 미쳐 있으면서, 그 일이 이뤄지도록 도우라고 내게 돈을 내줄 것 같으냐? 게다가 그게 또 다가 아니야. 그보다 더한 것도 말해 줄 수 있다. 나는 안다, 지난 닷새 동안 그가 은행에서 삼천 루블을 찾아다가, 백 루블짜리 지폐로 바꿔, 커다란 봉투에 넣고, 봉인을 다섯 개나 찍고, 붉은 끈으로 가위표를 쳐서 묶어 두었다는 걸 말이야. 봐라, 내가 얼마나 죄다 환히 알고 있는지! 봉투에는 이렇게 적혀 있다. '내 천사 그루셴카에게, 그녀가 내게 와 줄 때.' 그가 말없이, 몰래 직접 휘갈겨 쓴 거지. 그리고 그 돈이 거기 있다는 걸 아는 자라곤, 자기 분신처럼 믿는 하인 스메르댜코프밖에 없어. 그래서 지금 그는 지난 사나흘 동안 그루셴카를 기다려 온 거다. 그녀가 그 돈을 받으러 오기를 바라는 거지. 그가 그녀에게 그렇게 기별을 넣었고, 그녀는 어쩌면 가겠다고 답을 보냈어. 그런데 만일 그녀가 그 늙은이에게 가 버린다면, 그러고 나서 내가 어떻게 그녀와 결혼하겠느냐? 이제 알겠지, 내가 왜 여기 숨어 있는지, 내가 무얼 지켜보고 있는지 말이다.\"\n\n\"그녀를요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그녀를. 포마가 이 여기 갈보들 집에 방 하나를 얻어 산다. 포마는 우리 고향 사람이야. 우리 연대에서 군인이었지. 그 여자들의 잔심부름을 해 주고 있어. 밤에는 야경꾼 노릇을 하고, 낮에는 들꿩을 잡으러 다니지. 그렇게 먹고사는 거다. 나는 그놈 방에 자리를 잡았어. 그놈도, 이 집 여자들도 비밀은 모른다. 그러니까 내가 여기서 망을 보고 있다는 걸 말이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 스메르댜코프 말고는 아무도 모르는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아무도 몰라. 그녀가 그 늙은이에게 가면 그놈이 내게 알려 줄 거다.\"\n\n\"그럼 형한테 그 돈 얘길 해 준 게 그놈이었어요?\"\n\n\"그래. 그건 절대 비밀이야. 이반조차도 그 돈에 대해선, 아무것도 몰라. 그 늙은이는 이반을 체르마시냐로 이삼일 일정으로 보내려 하고 있어. 잡목림을 사겠다는 사람이 나타났거든. 목재 값으로 팔천 루블을 주겠다는 거야. 그래서 늙은이가 이반에게 그 일을 처리하러 가서 좀 도와 달라고 자꾸 부탁하는 거다. 이삼일은 걸릴 거야. 늙은이가 바라는 게 바로 그거지. 자기가 없는 사이에 그루셴카가 올 수 있도록 말이다.\"\n\n\"그럼 아버지는 오늘 그루셴카를 기다리는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니, 오늘은 안 올 거다. 그런 낌새가 있어. 그녀는 분명 오지 않을 거야.\" 미탸가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"스메르댜코프도 그렇게 생각해. 아버지는 지금 술을 마시고 있다. 이반과 함께 식탁에 앉아 있지. 그에게 가 봐라, 알료샤, 가서 삼천 루블을 달라고 해.\"\n\n\"미탸 형, 대체 왜 이래요?\" 알료샤가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 형의 광기 어린 얼굴을 날카롭게 바라보며 외쳤다. 한순간 드미트리가 미친 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 그를 스쳤다.\n\n\"왜 그러느냐? 나는 미치지 않았다.\" 드미트리가 진지하게 그를 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다. \"걱정 마. 나는 너를 아버지에게 보내는 거고, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 안다. 나는 기적을 믿어.\"\n\n\"기적을요?\"\n\n\"신의 섭리의 기적을 말이다. 하느님은 내 마음을 아셔. 내 절망을 보고 계셔. 그분은 이 모든 그림을 보고 계셔. 설마 그분께서 끔찍한 일이 벌어지도록 내버려 두시겠느냐. 알료샤, 나는 기적을 믿어. 가 봐라!\"\n\n\"갈게요. 말해 봐요, 형은 여기서 나를 기다릴 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래. 시간이 좀 걸릴 거라는 거 안다. 그에게 다짜고짜 들이댈 수는 없어. 지금 그는 취해 있으니까. 세 시간이든, 네 시간이든, 다섯, 여섯, 일곱 시간이든 기다리마. 다만 기억해라, 너는 오늘 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가야 한다. 한밤중이 되더라도 말이야, 돈이 있든 없든, 가서 '그가 당신에게 안부를 전한다더라'고 말해야 해. 나는 네가 그 구절을 그녀에게 전해 줬으면 한다. '그가 당신에게 안부를 전한다더라.'\"\n\n\"미탸 형! 그런데 만일 그루셴카가 오늘 오면 어쩌죠? 오늘이 아니면 내일, 아니면 모레라도?\"\n\n\"그루셴카가? 내가 그녀를 볼 거다. 뛰쳐나가서 막을 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 만일—\"\n\n\"만일이라는 게 있다면, 그건 살인이 될 거다. 나는 그걸 견딜 수 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"누가 죽는데요?\"\n\n\"그 늙은이. 나는 그녀를 죽이진 않을 거다.\"\n\n\"형, 무슨 소릴 하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아, 나도 모르겠다…… 모르겠어. 어쩌면 안 죽일지도, 어쩌면 죽일지도. 두려운 건, 그 순간 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 내게 너무도 역겹게 느껴질지 모른다는 거다. 나는 그의 그 추한 목구멍이, 그 코가, 그 두 눈이, 그 뻔뻔한 히죽거림이 증오스러워. 육체적인 혐오감이 느껴져. 그게 바로 내가 두려워하는 거다. 그게 바로 나로서는 감당 못 할 일일지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"갈게요, 미탸 형. 나는 하느님이 가장 좋은 쪽으로 일을 마련해 주셔서, 끔찍한 일이 하나도 안 일어나도록 해 주시리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 나는 앉아서 그 기적을 기다리마. 그런데 만일 그게 이루어지지 않는다면—\"\n\n알료샤는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 아버지의 집을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n# Chapter VI. Smerdyakov\n\n그는 과연 아버지가 아직 식탁에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 집에는 식당이 따로 있었으나 식탁은 여느 때처럼 응접실에 차려져 있었는데, 그곳이 집에서 가장 큰 방으로 옛날식의 과시적인 취향으로 꾸며져 있었다. 가구는 흰색이었고 몹시 낡았으며, 낡고 붉은 비단 천으로 씌워져 있었다. 창문과 창문 사이에는 흰색과 금색으로 정교하게 짠 옛날식 조각 틀에 끼운 거울들이 걸려 있었다. 흰 벽지를 발랐으나 군데군데 찢어진 벽에는 커다란 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었는데, 하나는 삼십 년 전 이 지역의 지사를 지낸 어느 공작의 초상이었고, 다른 하나는 역시 오래전에 죽은 어느 주교의 초상이었다. 문 맞은편 구석에는 성상 몇 점이 놓여 있었고, 그 앞에는 해가 지면 등불이 밝혀졌는데… 그것은 신앙심에서라기보다 방을 밝히기 위함이었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 늘 아주 늦게, 새벽 세 시나 네 시쯤에야 잠자리에 들었고, 밤이면 방 안을 거닐거나 안락의자에 앉아 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 이것은 그의 버릇이 되어 있었다. 그는 하인들을 별채로 보내고 집 안에서 혼자 자는 일이 잦았으나, 대개 스메르쟈코프만은 남아 현관의 긴 의자에서 잠을 잤다.\n\n알료샤가 들어섰을 때 저녁 식사는 끝나 있었으나 커피와 설탕에 절인 과일은 나와 있었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 식후에 브랜디와 더불어 단것을 즐겼다. 이반 역시 식탁에 앉아 커피를 홀짝이고 있었다. 하인인 그리고리와 스메르쟈코프는 그 곁에 서 있었다. 주인이건 하인이건 다들 유난히 기분이 좋아 보였다. 표도르 파블로비치는 너털웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다. 방에 들어서기 전부터 알료샤는 너무도 익숙한 그 새된 웃음소리를 들었고, 그 소리만으로도 아버지가 이제 막 거나하게 기분 좋아지는 단계에 이르렀을 뿐 아직 완전히 취하기에는 한참 멀었다는 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"여기 왔구나! 여기 왔어!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 알료샤를 보고 몹시 반가워하며 소리쳤다. \"이리 와 앉아라. 커피는 정진 음식이긴 하다만, 뜨겁고 맛이 좋단다. 너한테 브랜디는 권하지 않으마, 단식을 지키는 중이니까. 그래도 한 잔 하겠느냐? 아니지, 차라리 우리 집 그 유명한 리큐어를 좀 주는 게 낫겠구나. 스메르쟈코프, 찬장으로 가서 오른쪽 두 번째 선반을 열어라. 여기 열쇠다. 얼른얼른 움직여!\"\n\n알료샤는 리큐어를 사양하기 시작했다.\n\n\"괜찮아. 네가 안 마시겠다면 우리가 마시지.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그런데 가만있자, 저녁은 먹었느냐?\"\n\n\"네.\" 알료샤가 대답했으나, 사실은 수도원장의 부엌에서 빵 한 조각을 먹고 크바스 한 잔을 마신 게 전부였다. \"그래도 뜨거운 커피라면 기꺼이 마시겠어요.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 내 사랑하는 녀석! 커피를 마시겠다는구나. 데워야 하나? 아니, 펄펄 끓고 있어. 일품 커피지. 스메르쟈코프가 끓인 거다. 우리 스메르쟈코프는 커피 끓이는 데도, 생선 파이 만드는 데도, 생선 수프 끓이는 데도 예술가야. 언제 한번 와서 생선 수프를 좀 먹어 봐라. 미리 알려만 다오… 그런데 가만, 오늘 아침에 내가 너더러 요와 베개랑 다 챙겨서 집으로 들어오라 하지 않았느냐? 요는 가져왔어? 헤헤헤!\"\n\n\"아니요, 안 가져왔어요.\" 알료샤도 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아하, 그래도 너 겁이 났던 게지, 오늘 아침에 겁이 났던 거야, 안 그러냐? 자, 내 사랑하는 녀석아, 나는 너를 언짢게 할 짓은 도무지 못 하겠구나. 이반, 알겠느냐, 나는 저 녀석이 사람 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 웃는 그 표정을 당해 낼 재간이 없단다. 그걸 보면 온몸으로 웃음이 나. 나는 저 녀석이 그렇게 좋아. 알료샤, 내 너에게 축복을 내려 주마—아비의 축복을 말이다.\"\n\n알료샤가 일어섰으나, 표도르 파블로비치는 벌써 마음을 바꾼 뒤였다.\n\n\"아니, 아니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"지금은 그냥 네게 성호나 그어 주마. 가만히 앉아 있어라. 자, 이제 너한테 딱 어울리는, 네 취향에 맞는 즐길 거리가 하나 있다. 너도 웃음이 날 게야. 발람의 나귀가 여기서 우리한테 말을 하기 시작했단 말이지—게다가 얼마나 잘 지껄이는지! 얼마나 잘 지껄이는지 몰라!\"\n\n발람의 나귀란 알고 보니 하인 스메르쟈코프였다. 그는 스물네 살가량의 젊은이로, 놀라우리만치 비사교적이고 과묵했다. 그렇다고 수줍거나 부끄럼을 타는 것은 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대로, 거만하고 누구든 다 깔보는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 여기서 잠시 그에 관해 몇 마디 해 두어야겠다. 그는 그리고리와 마르파의 손에서 자랐으나, 그리고리의 말마따나 \"고마움이라고는 도무지 모르는\" 아이로 자라났으니, 정이 없는 아이였고 세상을 불신의 눈으로 바라보는 듯했다. 어릴 적에 그는 고양이를 목매다는 것을 무척 좋아했고, 그것을 거창한 의식을 갖춰 묻어 주곤 했다. 그는 시트를 몸에 둘러 마치 제의처럼 차려입고는 노래를 불렀으며, 죽은 고양이 위로 어떤 물건을 향로마냥 휘둘러 댔다. 이 모든 짓을 그는 남몰래, 더없이 은밀하게 했다. 한번은 그리고리가 이 장난질을 하던 그를 붙잡아 호되게 매질했다. 그러자 그는 구석으로 움츠러들어 일주일이나 골을 부리고 있었다. \"저 괴물 같은 녀석은 너도 나도 안중에 없어.\" 그리고리는 마르파에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"어느 누구도 안중에 없단 말이야. 너는 사람이냐?\" 그는 아이를 향해 대놓고 물었다. \"너는 사람도 아니야. 너는 목욕탕 곰팡이에서 생겨난 놈이야. 네가 그런 놈이라고.\" 나중에 드러난 바로는, 스메르쟈코프는 그 말만은 결코 용서할 수 없었다고 한다. 그리고리는 그에게 읽고 쓰는 법을 가르쳤고, 그가 열두 살이 되자 성경을 가르치기 시작했다. 그러나 이 가르침은 아무런 결실도 보지 못했다. 두 번째인가 세 번째 수업에서 그 아이가 별안간 히죽 웃었다.\n\n\"그건 또 무슨 짓이냐?\" 그리고리가 안경 너머로 위협하듯 그를 노려보며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 별거 아녜요. 하느님은 첫째 날에 빛을 지으셨고, 해와 달과 별은 넷째 날에 지으셨다잖아요. 그럼 첫째 날의 빛은 어디서 나온 거예요?\"\n\n그리고리는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다. 아이는 빈정대는 눈으로 제 선생을 바라보았다. 그 표정에는 분명 사람을 내려다보는 듯한 기색이 있었다. 그리고리는 참을 수가 없었다. \"어디서 나왔는지 보여 주마!\" 그는 이렇게 소리치며 아이의 뺨을 세차게 후려쳤다. 아이는 말없이 그 따귀를 맞았으나, 또다시 며칠 동안 제 구석으로 움츠러들었다. 일주일 뒤 그는 평생토록 그를 따라다니게 될 병, 곧 간질의 첫 발작을 일으켰다. 표도르 파블로비치가 이 일을 전해 듣자, 아이를 대하는 그의 태도가 단번에 달라진 듯했다. 그때까지만 해도 그는 아이를 야단친 적은 없었고 마주칠 때마다 늘 코페이카 한 닢을 쥐여 주기는 했으나, 별다른 관심을 두지는 않았다. 이따금 기분이 좋을 때면 자기 식탁에서 단것을 아이에게 보내 주기도 했다. 그러나 그의 병 이야기를 듣자마자 그는 아이에게 적극적인 관심을 보여 의사를 부르고 갖은 약을 써 보았으나, 그 병은 고칠 수 없는 것으로 드러났다. 발작은 평균하여 한 달에 한 번꼴로 일어났으되 그 간격은 들쭉날쭉했다. 발작의 정도도 제각각이어서 어떤 것은 가벼웠고 어떤 것은 몹시 심했다. 표도르 파블로비치는 그리고리에게 아이에게 손찌검하는 것을 엄히 금했고, 아이가 위층 자기 방으로 올라오는 것을 허락하기 시작했다. 한동안은 그에게 무엇이든 가르치는 것조차 금했다. 어느 날 아이가 열다섯 살쯤 되었을 무렵, 표도르 파블로비치는 그가 책장 곁에서 서성이며 유리 너머로 책 제목들을 읽고 있는 것을 보았다. 표도르 파블로비치에게는 제법 많은 책이—백 권이 넘게—있었으나, 그가 책을 읽는 모습을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그는 곧장 스메르쟈코프에게 책장 열쇠를 내주었다. \"자, 읽어 봐. 너를 내 사서로 삼으마. 마당이나 빈둥거리며 돌아다니느니 앉아서 책이나 읽는 편이 나을 게다. 자, 이걸 읽어 봐라.\" 그러면서 표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 『디칸카 근교 마을의 야회』를 건네주었다.\n\n그는 조금 읽었으나 좋아하지 않았다. 한 번도 미소 짓지 않았고, 결국에는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 말았다.\n\n\"왜? 우습지 않으냐?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 물었다.\n\n스메르쟈코프는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"대답을 해, 멍청한 놈아!\"\n\n\"죄다 거짓말이잖아요.\" 아이는 히죽거리며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그럼 악마한테나 가 버려라! 너는 하인 근성을 타고났구나. 가만있자, 여기 스마라그도프의 『세계사』가 있다. 이건 죄다 사실이야. 이걸 읽어.\"\n\n그러나 스메르쟈코프는 스마라그도프도 열 페이지를 채 넘기지 못했다. 그는 그것이 따분하다고 여겼다. 그리하여 책장은 다시 닫히고 말았다.\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 마르파와 그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치에게 스메르쟈코프가 차츰 유별난 결벽증을 보이기 시작했다고 일러바쳤다. 그는 수프 그릇 앞에 앉으면 숟가락을 집어 들고 수프 속을 들여다보았고, 몸을 숙여 자세히 살피더니, 한 술 떠서는 불빛에 비추어 보았다.\n\n\"그게 뭐냐? 딱정벌레라도 있어?\" 그리고리가 묻곤 했다.\n\n\"파리인지도 모르죠.\" 마르파가 한마디 했다.\n\n이 까다로운 젊은이는 결코 대답하는 법이 없었으나, 빵이든 고기든 무엇을 먹든 똑같이 그러했다. 한 조각을 포크에 꽂아 불빛에 비추어 현미경으로 들여다보듯 면밀히 살핀 다음, 한참을 곰곰이 따져 본 뒤에야 비로소 입에 넣기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"아이고! 어디서 저런 귀공자 티를 다 내는지!\" 그리고리는 그를 바라보며 투덜거렸다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 스메르쟈코프의 이런 변화를 전해 듣자 그를 요리사로 삼기로 작정하고 그를 모스크바로 보내 수련을 시켰다. 그는 그곳에서 몇 해를 보낸 뒤 외모가 놀랄 만큼 달라져서 돌아왔다. 나이에 비해 유난히 늙어 보였다. 얼굴에는 주름이 잡혔고, 누렇게 떴으며, 어딘지 거세된 사람처럼 묘하게 변해 있었다. 성품으로 말하자면 떠나기 전과 거의 똑같아 보였다. 여전히 비사교적이었고, 누구와도 어울리려는 기색이라고는 조금도 보이지 않았다. 나중에 들은 바로는 모스크바에서도 그는 늘 말이 없었다고 한다. 모스크바 자체도 그의 흥미를 거의 끌지 못했으니, 그는 그곳에서 본 것도 거의 없었고 무엇에든 좀처럼 눈길을 주지 않았다. 그는 한 번 극장에 갔으나 못마땅한 얼굴로 말없이 돌아왔다. 그래도 한편으로 그는 모스크바에서 옷차림을 말끔히 하고, 깨끗한 외투에 깨끗한 속옷을 입고 우리에게로 돌아왔다. 그는 하루도 빠짐없이 하루 두 번씩 더없이 꼼꼼하게 옷을 솔질했고, 영국제 특수 광택제로 자기 멋들어진 송아지 가죽 장화를 거울처럼 번쩍이도록 닦는 것을 무척 좋아했다. 그는 일류 요리사가 되었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 급료를 주었는데, 스메르쟈코프는 그 돈의 거의 전부를 옷이며 포마드며 향수 따위에 써 버렸다. 그러나 그는 남자에게 그러하듯 여자에게도 그만한 경멸을 품은 듯했으니, 여자들에게는 조심스럽고 거의 다가갈 수 없을 만큼 쌀쌀했다. 표도르 파블로비치는 그를 다소 다른 눈으로 보기 시작했다. 그의 발작은 점점 잦아졌고, 그가 앓아눕는 날이면 마르파가 요리를 했는데, 이것이 표도르 파블로비치에게는 영 입에 맞지 않았다.\n\n\"어째서 발작이 점점 더 심해지는 게냐?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 새 요리사를 흘겨보며 물었다. \"장가라도 들고 싶으냐? 내가 색싯감이라도 하나 찾아 줄까?\"\n\n그러나 스메르쟈코프는 화가 나서 새파랗게 질린 채 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 표도르 파블로비치는 짜증스러운 손짓을 하며 그를 내버려 두었다. 중요한 것은 그가 스메르쟈코프의 정직성만큼은 절대적으로 믿었다는 점이다. 한번은 표도르 파블로비치가 술에 취해 방금 받은 백 루블짜리 지폐 석 장을 진창이 된 마당에 떨어뜨린 일이 있었다. 그는 이튿날에야 그것이 없어진 것을 알아채고는 부랴부랴 주머니를 뒤지려는 참에 그 지폐들이 식탁 위에 놓여 있는 것을 보았다. 그것들이 어디서 나왔겠는가? 스메르쟈코프가 전날 그것을 주워 들여놓았던 것이다.\n\n\"이보게, 자네 같은 사람은 내 평생 처음일세.\" 표도르 파블로비치는 간단히 이렇게 말하며 그에게 십 루블을 주었다. 한마디 덧붙여 두자면, 그는 그의 정직성을 믿었을 뿐 아니라 어쩐 영문인지 그를 마음에 들어 했는데, 정작 그 젊은이는 다른 모든 사람을 대할 때처럼 침울한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았고 늘 말이 없었다. 그는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았다. 만일 그 무렵 누군가가 이 젊은이는 무엇에 관심을 두며 그의 머릿속에는 무엇이 들어 있을까 궁금해했다 한들, 그를 들여다본다고 해서 알 길은 없었으리라. 그러면서도 그는 이따금 집 안에서, 더러는 마당이나 거리에서까지 별안간 걸음을 멈추고 십 분이나 우두커니 선 채 생각에 잠겨 있곤 했다. 그의 얼굴을 살피는 관상가라면 거기에는 아무런 사색도, 성찰도 없고 오로지 일종의 응시만이 있다고 말했을 것이다. 화가 크람스코이가 그린 「응시」라는 주목할 만한 그림이 있다. 거기에는 겨울 숲이 있고, 그 숲을 가로지르는 길 위에 더할 나위 없는 고적함 속에서 한 농부가 찢어진 카프탄에 나무껍질 신을 신고 서 있다. 그는 흡사 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있다. 그러나 그는 생각하고 있는 것이 아니라 \"응시하고\" 있는 것이다. 만일 누군가가 그를 건드린다면 그는 화들짝 놀라며 잠에서 깨어난 듯 어리둥절한 눈으로 상대를 바라볼 것이다. 사실 그는 곧바로 정신을 차리기는 하겠으나, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었느냐고 묻는다면 아무것도 기억해 내지 못할 것이다. 그러나 십중팔구 그는 응시의 시간 동안 그를 사로잡았던 그 인상을 제 안에 깊이 감추고 있을 것이다. 그 인상들은 그에게 소중한 것이어서, 그는 분명 그것들을 알게 모르게, 심지어 무의식적으로 차곡차곡 쌓아 둘 것이다. 어떻게 그리고 왜 그러는지는 물론 그 자신도 알지 못한다. 그는 여러 해 동안 인상을 쌓아 두다가 별안간 모든 것을 버리고 영혼의 구원을 위해 예루살렘으로 순례를 떠날지도 모르고, 어쩌면 별안간 자기 고향 마을에 불을 지를지도 모르며, 어쩌면 그 둘을 다 저지를지도 모른다. 농민들 가운데는 이런 \"응시자\"가 적지 않다. 그런데 스메르쟈코프도 십중팔구 그런 부류 가운데 하나였고, 그 역시 무슨 까닭인지도 잘 모른 채 탐욕스럽게 자신의 인상을 쌓아 두고 있었던 것이리라.\n목욕탕 곰팡이: 러시아 민간 속설에서 사생아나 정체불명의 출생을 모욕적으로 이르던 표현으로, 목욕탕(바냐)은 부정한 기운이 깃든 장소로 여겨졌다.\n\n# Chapter VII. The Controversy\n\n그러나 발람의 나귀가 별안간 입을 열었다. 그 주제가 묘한 것이었다. 그리고리는 아침에 물건을 사러 나갔다가, 가게 주인 루키야노프에게서 그날 신문에 실린 어느 러시아 병사의 이야기를 전해 들었던 것이다. 이 병사는 아시아의 어느 외진 곳에서 포로로 잡혔는데, 기독교를 버리고 이슬람을 따르지 않으면 당장 끔찍한 죽임을 당하리라는 위협을 받았다. 그는 신앙을 부인하기를 거부했고, 그리하여 고문을 당하고 산 채로 살가죽이 벗겨진 끝에 그리스도를 찬양하고 영광 돌리며 죽었다. 그리고리는 식탁에서 이 이야기를 들려주었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 저녁 식사 후 후식을 들 때면 언제나, 비록 상대가 그리고리뿐이라 할지라도, 웃고 떠드는 것을 좋아했다. 그날 오후 그는 유달리 기분이 좋고 흥이 올라 있었다. 브랜디를 홀짝이며 그 이야기를 듣더니, 그는 그런 병사라면 마땅히 성인으로 떠받들고 그 살가죽을 어느 수도원에 갖다 두어야 한다고 한마디 했다. \"그러면 사람들이 떼 지어 몰려들 테고, 돈도 굴러들 게 아닌가.\"\n\n그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치가 조금도 감동하기는커녕 여느 때처럼 비웃기 시작하는 것을 보고 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 바로 그때 문가에 서 있던 스메르댜코프가 빙긋 웃었다. 스메르댜코프는 식사가 끝나갈 무렵이면 곧잘 시중을 들었는데, 이반이 우리 고장에 온 뒤로는 날마다 그렇게 하고 있었다.\n\n\"뭘 그렇게 히죽거리느냐?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 그 웃음을 대뜸 알아채고 그것이 그리고리를 두고 한 것임을 짐작하며 물었다.\n\n\"제 생각에는 말입니다,\" 스메르댜코프가 갑작스럽고도 뜻밖에 큰 소리로 입을 열었다, \"그 갸륵한 병사의 공적이 정말 그렇게 위대한 것이었다면, 제 소견으로는, 그가 그런 위급한 처지에서, 말하자면 그리스도의 이름과 제 세례를 부인하여 바로 그것으로 제 목숨을 건졌다 한들, 그래서 여러 해에 걸쳐 선행을 쌓아 제 비겁함을 속죄하려 했다 한들, 거기에 아무 죄도 없었을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"어찌 죄가 안 된단 말이냐? 헛소리를 지껄이는구나. 그딴 짓을 하면 곧장 지옥에 떨어져 양고기처럼 구워질 게다.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 끼어들었다.\n\n바로 이 대목에서 알료샤가 들어왔고, 표도르 파블로비치는 앞서 보았듯이 그의 등장에 몹시 기뻐했다.\n\n\"마침 네 이야기를 하던 참이다, 네 이야기 말이다.\" 그는 흐뭇하게 낄낄거리며 알료샤를 자리에 앉혀 듣게 했다.\n\n\"양고기로 말씀드리자면, 그건 그렇지가 않습니다. 거기엔 그런 벌이 없을 것이고, 정의에 따른다면 또한 마땅히 없어야 할 것입니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 꿋꿋이 주장했다.\n\n\"'정의에 따른다면'이라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 표도르 파블로비치가 한층 더 신이 나서 소리치며, 무릎으로 알료샤를 슬쩍 찔렀다.\n\n\"저놈은 악당입니다, 그게 저놈의 정체예요!\" 그리고리가 버럭 내뱉었다. 그는 분노에 차서 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 노려보았다.\n\n\"제가 악당이라는 건 잠시 미뤄 두십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치.\" 스메르댜코프가 더없이 침착하게 대꾸했다. \"당신 스스로 한번 헤아려 보시는 편이 나을 겁니다. 가령 제가 기독교 민족의 적들에게 포로로 잡혀, 그자들이 제게 하느님의 이름을 저주하고 거룩한 세례를 부인하라고 요구한다면, 저는 제 이성에 따라 행동할 온전한 권리가 있습니다. 거기엔 아무 죄도 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 아까도 한 소리다. 말을 허비하지 마라. 증명을 해 보아라.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"수프나 끓이는 놈이!\" 그리고리가 경멸조로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"수프나 끓이는 놈이라는 것도 잠시 미뤄 두시고, 저를 욕하지 마시고 직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치. 제가 그 적들에게 '아니다, 나는 기독교인이 아니다, 나는 나의 참된 하느님을 저주한다'고 말하는 순간, 곧바로 하느님의 지엄한 심판에 따라 저는 즉시 특별히 파문당해 저주받은 자가 되고, 거룩한 교회로부터 끊겨 나가, 흡사 이교도와 다름없게 됩니다. 그러니 그 바로 그 찰나에, 그것을 소리 내어 말할 때뿐만 아니라 그것을 말하려고 생각만 해도, 사분의 일 초가 지나기도 전에 저는 끊겨 나가는 것입니다. 그렇습니까, 안 그렇습니까, 그리고리 바실리예비치?\"\n\n그는 사실 표도르 파블로비치의 물음에 답하고 있으면서, 그 사실을 잘 알면서도, 짐짓 그리고리가 물어본 것인 양 꾸미며 그리고리를 향해 만족스러운 빛을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"이반,\" 표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 외쳤다, \"귓속말을 하게 좀 숙여 보아라. 저놈이 이걸 죄다 네 비위를 맞추려고 꾸민 게다. 네가 칭찬해 주기를 바라는 게야. 칭찬을 해 주려무나.\"\n\n이반은 아버지의 들뜬 귓속말을 더없이 진지하게 들었다.\n\n\"잠깐, 스메르댜코프, 잠시 가만히 있어 보아라.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 다시 한번 외쳤다. \"이반, 다시 귀 좀 대 보아라.\"\n\n이반은 또다시 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"나는 너를 알료샤만큼이나 사랑한단다. 내가 너를 사랑하지 않는다고 여기지 마라. 브랜디 한잔 하겠느냐?\"\n\n\"네. — 그런데 아버지가 좀 취하셨군요.\" 이반은 아버지를 가만히 바라보며 속으로 생각했다.\n\n그는 큰 호기심을 품고 스메르댜코프를 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n\"너는 이미 파문당해 저주받은 놈이야.\" 그리고리가 별안간 터뜨렸다. \"그런 주제에 어찌 감히 따지고 든단 말이냐, 이 악당아, 만약—\"\n\n\"그를 나무라지 마라, 그리고리, 나무라지 말라니까.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 그의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"잠깐만 기다려 주십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 잠시만이라도요. 그리고 들어 보십시오. 아직 할 말을 다 마치지 못했으니까요. 제가 저주받은 그 바로 그 순간, 바로 그 지엄한 순간에 저는 영락없이 이교도와 똑같이 되고, 제 세례는 벗겨져 아무 효력도 없게 됩니다. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"어서 마저 끝내거라, 이놈아.\" 표도르 파블로비치가 포도주 잔을 흐뭇하게 홀짝이며 그를 재촉했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 이미 기독교인이기를 그쳤다면, 적들이 제게 기독교인이냐 아니냐를 물었을 때 저는 적들에게 거짓말을 한 것이 아닙니다. 왜냐하면 적에게 한마디 내뱉기도 전에, 오로지 그 생각만으로, 저는 이미 하느님 친히 제 기독교 신앙을 거두어 가셨기 때문입니다. 그리고 제가 이미 면제받았다면, 저세상에서 무슨 도리로, 어떤 정의에 비추어, 제가 그리스도를 부인했다 하여 기독교인으로서 책임을 져야 한단 말입니까? 그분을 부인하기에 앞서, 오로지 그 생각 하나만으로 저는 이미 세례에서 풀려난 처지인데요. 제가 더는 기독교인이 아니라면, 저는 그리스도를 부인할 수도 없습니다. 그때는 부인할 것이 아무것도 없으니까요. 그리고리 바실리예비치, 부정한 타타르인을 누가, 천국에서라 한들, 기독교인으로 태어나지 못했다 하여 책임을 묻겠습니까? 또 누가 그를 그 일로 벌하겠습니까, 황소 한 마리에서 가죽을 두 장이나 벗길 수 없는 노릇인데 말입니다. 전능하신 하느님 친히, 설령 그 타타르인에게 책임을 물으신다 하더라도, 그가 죽으면 제 짐작으로는 가능한 한 가장 가벼운 벌을 내리실 겁니다(어쨌든 벌은 받아야 할 테니까요). 그가 이교도 부모에게서 부정한 이교도로 세상에 태어난 것은 그의 잘못이 아니라고 헤아리실 테니 말입니다. 주 하느님께서 설마 타타르인을 붙들고 그가 기독교인이었다고 말씀하시겠습니까? 그렇게 되면 전능하신 분께서 진짜 거짓말을 하시는 셈이 됩니다. 한데 하늘과 땅의 주님께서 단 한마디라도 거짓을 말씀하실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n그리고리는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯이 눈알이 거의 튀어나올 지경으로 그 웅변가를 바라보았다. 그는 무슨 말인지 또렷이 알아듣지는 못했으나, 그 횡설수설 속에서 뭔가를 붙잡았는지, 방금 벽에 머리를 들이받은 사람 같은 꼴로 우두커니 서 있었다. 표도르 파블로비치는 잔을 비우고는 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"알료샤! 알료샤! 너는 저 말을 어떻게 보느냐! 아, 이 궤변가 같으니! 저놈은 분명 어디선가 예수회 놈들과 어울려 지냈을 게다, 이반. 오, 이 구린내 나는 예수회 놈아, 누가 너를 가르쳤느냐? 하지만 너는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있는 게야, 이 궤변가야, 헛소리, 헛소리, 헛소리란 말이다. 울지 마라, 그리고리, 우리가 곧 저놈을 연기와 재로 만들어 버릴 테니. 자, 이놈, 나귀야, 이 말에 대답해 보아라. 네가 적들 앞에서는 옳을지 몰라도, 어쨌든 너는 네 마음속에서 신앙을 부인한 것이고, 게다가 네 입으로 바로 그 순간에 파문당해 저주받은 자가 되었다고 했잖느냐. 그리고 일단 파문당하고 나면 지옥에서 그 일로 너의 머리를 쓰다듬어 주지는 않을 게다. 그 말에는 뭐라 답하겠느냐, 이 멋진 예수회 놈아?\"\n\n\"제가 마음속에서 그것을 부인했다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 하지만 거기에 특별한 죄는 없었습니다. 설령 죄가 있었다 한들, 그건 더없이 평범한 죄였습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 어째서 더없이 평범한 죄란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"거짓말 마라, 이 저주받은 놈아!\" 그리고리가 쉭쉭거리며 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치.\" 스메르댜코프는 제 승리를 의식하면서도 패한 적에게 짐짓 너그러운 태도로, 차분하고 흔들림 없이 말을 이었다. \"직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치. 성경에 이르기를, 겨자씨만 한 믿음이라도 있어 산더러 바다로 옮겨 가라 명하면 그 산이 당신의 명에 따라 조금도 지체 없이 옮겨 가리라 하였습니다. 자, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 저는 믿음이 없고 당신은 줄곧 저를 욕하실 만큼 그토록 큰 믿음을 지니셨으니, 당신께서 친히 이 산더러 명해 보시지요. 바다는 너무 머니 거기까지 갈 것도 없고, 그저 우리 정원 아래로 흐르는 저 구린내 나는 작은 개울로라도 옮겨 가라고 해 보십시오. 당신은 산이 꿈쩍도 하지 않고, 당신이 아무리 소리쳐도 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있는 것을 직접 보시게 될 겁니다. 그것은, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 당신께 제대로 된 믿음이 없으며, 그저 그 일로 남을 욕하기만 한다는 것을 보여 줍니다. 게다가 오늘날 그 누구도, 당신뿐만 아니라 실로 그 누구도, 가장 높은 분에서부터 가장 미천한 농부에 이르기까지, 산을 바다로 떠밀어 넣을 수는 없다는 점을 헤아려 보십시오. 어쩌면 세상에 단 한 사람, 기껏해야 두 사람쯤은 그럴 수 있을지 모르나, 그들마저도 십중팔구 이집트 사막 어딘가에서 남몰래 제 영혼을 구하고 있을 테니 당신은 그들을 찾지도 못할 겁니다. 만일 그렇다면, 만일 나머지 모두가 믿음이 없다면, 하느님께서 나머지 모두를 저주하시겠습니까? 다시 말해, 사막의 은수자 두 사람쯤을 제외한 온 땅의 모든 백성을 저주하시고, 그 널리 알려진 자비로도 그중 단 한 사람조차 용서치 않으시겠습니까? 그러니 저는 확신합니다. 제가 한때 의심한 적이 있다 하더라도, 회개의 눈물을 흘린다면 용서받으리라고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 환희에 도취되어 외쳤다. \"그러니까 너는 산을 옮길 수 있는 자가 둘은 있다고 본단 말이지? 이반, 그걸 적어 두어라, 받아 적으라니까. 거기에 러시아인의 본색이 고스란히 드러나 있어!\"\n\n\"그것이 민중 신앙의 특징이라 하시는 건 지당하신 말씀입니다.\" 이반이 흡족한 미소를 띠며 동의했다.\n\n\"네가 동의하는구나. 네가 동의한다면 정녕 그런 게 틀림없지. 그렇지 않으냐, 알료샤? 저게 러시아 신앙 그대로지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 스메르댜코프는 결코 러시아 신앙을 지니고 있지 않습니다.\" 알료샤가 단호하고 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"나는 저놈의 신앙을 두고 하는 말이 아니다. 사막에 있는 그 두 사람, 그 발상 하나만을 두고 하는 말이지. 그건 분명 러시아적이지 않으냐?\"\n\n\"네, 그건 순전히 러시아적입니다.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"네 말은 금화 한 닢의 값어치가 있구나, 이 나귀야. 오늘 그걸 너에게 주마. 하지만 나머지로 말하면 너는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있어, 헛소리, 헛소리, 헛소리란 말이다. 이 멍청한 놈아, 내가 한마디 일러 주마. 우리는 여기 모두 믿음이 변변찮은데, 그건 그저 무심해서 그런 게야, 우리에게 시간이 없으니까. 일이 우리를 너무 짓누르고, 게다가 둘째로는, 주 하느님께서 우리에게 시간을 너무 적게, 하루에 고작 스물네 시간밖에 주지 않으셨으니, 잠도 실컷 잘 짬이 없는 판에 죄를 회개할 겨를이야 더 말할 것도 없지. 한데 너로 말하면, 오직 네 신앙을 드러내는 것 말고는 달리 생각할 거리도 없는 마당에 적들에게 신앙을 부인했단 말이다! 그러니 내 보기에는, 이 사람아, 그게 죄가 되는 게야.\"\n\n\"죄가 될지는 모릅니다만, 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 그것이 설령 죄가 된다 하더라도 그저 죄를 덜어 줄 뿐입니다. 만일 제가 그때 마땅히 믿었어야 할 만큼 참으로 믿고 있었다면, 그렇다면 정말이지 제 신앙을 위해 고문을 마주하지 않고 이교도인 마호메트교로 넘어갔다는 것은 죄가 되었을 겁니다. 하지만 물론 그때는 고문에 이르지도 않았을 겁니다. 저는 그 찰나에 산더러 '옮겨 가 저 고문자를 짓눌러라' 하고 말하기만 하면 되었을 테고, 그러면 산은 옮겨 가 바로 그 찰나에 그자를 검은 풍뎅이처럼 짓이겨 버렸을 테니까요. 그리고 저는 아무 일도 없었던 듯 하느님을 찬양하고 영광 돌리며 걸어 나왔을 겁니다. 한데 가령 제가 바로 그 순간에 그 모든 것을 시험해 보아, 그 산더러 '이 고문자들을 짓눌러라' 하고 외쳤는데도 산이 그들을 짓누르지 않았다면, 그런 때에, 그런 끔찍한 죽음의 공포의 순간에, 대체 제가 어찌 의심하지 않을 수 있었겠습니까? 그뿐 아니라 저는 이미, 제가 천국의 충만함에 이를 수 없다는 것을 알았을 겁니다(제 말 한마디에 산이 옮겨 가지 않았으니, 저 위에서는 제 믿음을 대단찮게 여겼을 테고, 저세상에서 저를 기다리는 큰 상도 없었을 테니까요). 그러니 무엇 하러 제가, 아무런 좋은 보람도 없이, 제 살가죽까지 벗기게 두겠습니까? 설령 그들이 제 등에서 살가죽을 절반쯤 벗겨 냈다 한들, 그래도 산은 제 말이나 외침에 옮겨 가지 않았을 테니까요. 게다가 그런 순간에는 의심이 덮쳐들 뿐만 아니라 두려움 때문에 정신을 잃어, 아무 생각도 못 하게 될 수 있습니다. 그러니 저세상에서든 이승에서든 제 이득도 상도 보이지 않는 마당에, 적어도 제 살가죽만이라도 건진다 한들, 어찌 제게 유달리 잘못이 있다 하겠습니까? 그래서 저는 주님의 은총을 온전히 믿고, 제가 죄다 용서받으리라는 희망을 품을 것입니다.\"\n\n# Chapter VIII. Over The Brandy\n\n논쟁은 끝났다. 그러나 이상하게도, 그토록 명랑하던 표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 얼굴을 찌푸리기 시작했다. 그는 인상을 쓰며 브랜디를 들이켰는데, 이미 한 잔이 지나친 양이었다.\n\n\"꺼져라, 이 예수회 놈들아!\" 그가 하인들에게 소리쳤다. \"물러가라, 스메르댜코프. 오늘 약속한 금화는 보내 줄 테니 어서 꺼져! 울지 마, 그리고리. 마르파한테 가거라. 그 사람이 너를 달래서 재워 줄 게다. 이 망나니 같은 놈들이 점심 먹고 나서도 우리를 편히 앉아 있게 내버려 두질 않는단 말이야.\" 그가 짜증스럽게 내뱉자, 하인들은 그 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 물러갔다.\n\n\"스메르댜코프 저 녀석은 요즘 들어 점심 먹고 나면 꼭 끼어든단 말이야. 저 녀석이 그토록 흥미를 갖는 게 바로 너야. 도대체 무슨 짓을 했길래 저 녀석을 그렇게 사로잡았느냐?\" 그가 이반에게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아무것도 한 게 없습니다.\" 이반이 대답했다. \"저 녀석은 멋대로 저를 높이 평가하는 거지요. 하인이고 비루한 영혼이니까요. 그래도 때가 오면 혁명의 원료로는 쓰일 만하지요.\"\n\n\"혁명이라고?\"\n\n\"다른 자들이, 더 나은 자들이 나올 겁니다. 하지만 저 녀석 같은 자들도 더러 있겠지요. 저런 부류가 먼저 나오고, 더 나은 자들이 그다음에 나오는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그 때라는 게 언제 오는데?\"\n\n\"로켓이 한번 솟구쳤다가 흐지부지 꺼져 버릴지도 모르지요. 농민들은 아직까지 이런 멀건 죽이나 끓이는 자들의 말에 귀 기울이는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 이 사람아, 그래도 저런 발람의 나귀 같은 놈은 생각하고 또 생각하다 보면, 어디까지 가닿을지 악마나 알 노릇이지.\"\n\n\"녀석은 머릿속에 생각을 쌓아 두고 있지요.\" 이반이 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 나는 저 녀석이 나를 견디지 못한다는 걸 알아. 누구든 다 못 견뎌 하지, 너까지도 말이야. 네가 자기를 높이 평가한다고 멋대로 상상하고 있겠지만 말이지. 알료샤한테는 더 심해서, 저 녀석은 알료샤를 경멸하거든. 하지만 도둑질은 안 한다, 그건 하나 인정할 만하지. 게다가 입이 가볍지도 않아서 입을 꾹 다물고, 우리 집 더러운 속사정을 밖에 떠벌리지도 않거든. 생선 파이도 기막히게 잘 만들고 말이야. 그래도 빌어먹을, 저 녀석이 이렇게나 떠들 만한 가치가 있는 놈이냔 말이지?\"\n\n\"물론 없지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저 녀석이 머릿속에 부화시키고 있을 생각들로 말하자면, 러시아 농민이란 대체로 두들겨 패야 마땅한 족속이야. 나는 늘 그렇게 주장해 왔어. 우리 농민들은 사기꾼이라 동정받을 자격이 없고, 가끔이라도 매를 맞는 건 잘된 일이지. 러시아엔 자작나무가 풍부하잖느냐. 만약 숲을 다 없애 버린다면 그거야말로 러시아의 파멸일 게다. 나는 영리한 사람들 편이야. 우리가 하도 영리해진 나머지 농민들을 패는 일은 그만뒀지만, 그놈들은 저희끼리 알아서 패고 있단 말이지. 그것도 잘된 일이야. '너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라', 뭐 그렇게 가는 거 아니냐? 아무튼 헤아림은 받게 돼 있어. 하지만 러시아란 죄다 돼지 같은 더러움뿐이야. 이봐, 내가 러시아를 얼마나 미워하는지 네가 안다면…… 아니, 러시아가 아니라 이 모든 악덕을 말이야! 하지만 어쩌면 러시아를 두고 하는 말일지도 모르지. 그 모든 게 다 돼지짓이야……. 내가 뭘 좋아하는지 아느냐? 나는 재치를 좋아한다.\"\n* 멀건 죽이나 끓이는 자들: 원문 '수프 만드는 자들(soup-makers)'. 사회주의·혁명 선동가들을 빈정대어 이르는 표현.\n\n\"또 한 잔 드셨군요. 그만하면 충분합니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만. 한 잔만 더 하고, 또 한 잔 하고, 그러고 나서 그만두마. 아니, 가만있어, 네가 내 말을 끊었잖느냐. 모크로예에서 어떤 늙은이랑 이야기를 나눴는데, 그자가 나한테 이러더구나. '우리한테 계집애들을 매질하라고 선고하는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없는데, 매질하는 일은 늘 사내놈들한테 맡깁죠. 그래서 오늘 매질당한 계집애한테 그 젊은 놈이 내일이면 청혼을 한답니다. 그러니 계집애들한테도 아주 잘 맞아떨어지는 셈이지요.' 자, 이거야말로 사드 후작들의 무리 아니냐! 어쨌든 영리하긴 하지. 우리 한번 가서 구경이라도 할까, 응? 알료샤, 너 얼굴이 붉어졌느냐? 부끄러워 말거라, 얘야. 내가 수도원장 식탁에 남아서 그 수도사들한테 모크로예 계집애들 얘기를 들려주지 못한 게 아쉽구나. 알료샤, 내가 오늘 아침 네 수도원장을 모욕했다고 화내지 마라. 그만 성질을 못 참았던 게야. 만약 신이 있다면, 정말로 존재한다면, 물론 그건 내 잘못이고 그 죄값을 치러야겠지. 하지만 신이라곤 아예 없다면, 네 그 신부 양반들이 무슨 대접을 받아 마땅하겠느냐? 머리를 잘라 내는 걸로도 모자라, 그자들은 진보를 가로막고 있단 말이야. 이반, 이게 내 감정을 갈가리 찢어 놓는다는 걸 너는 믿겠느냐? 아니, 넌 안 믿는구나, 네 눈을 보니 알겠다. 너는 사람들이 하는 말, 내가 어릿광대에 지나지 않는다는 그 말을 믿고 있는 게지. 알료샤, 너는 내가 어릿광대에 지나지 않는다고 믿느냐?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 그렇게 믿지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 네가 안 믿는다는 것도, 또 네가 진실을 말한다는 것도 나는 믿는다. 너는 진실해 보이고 진실하게 말하지. 하지만 이반은 아니야. 이반은 거만하단 말이지……. 그래도 어쨌든 나라면 네 수도사들을 끝장내 버리겠다. 그 신비주의 같은 잡것들을 죄다 거둬다가 러시아 전역에서 단번에 영원히 짓눌러 버려서, 그 바보들을 다 정신 차리게 하겠어. 그러면 조폐국으로 흘러들 그 금이며 은이 또 얼마겠느냐!\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 그걸 짓눌러야 하는데요?\" 이반이 물었다.\n\n\"진리가 널리 퍼지도록 하기 위해서지. 그게 이유야.\"\n\n\"그런데, 만약 진리가 널리 퍼지게 된다면 말이지요, 맨 먼저 약탈당하고 짓눌릴 사람은 바로 아버지일 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아! 네 말이 옳을지도 모르겠다. 아, 나는 멍청이로구나!\" 표도르 파블로비치가 자기 이마를 가볍게 탁 치며 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"그래, 사정이 그렇다면 네 수도원도 그냥 서 있게 둬야겠구나, 알료샤. 그리고 우리 영리한 사람들은 편히 앉아 브랜디나 즐기면 되겠지. 이봐, 이반, 이건 분명 전능하신 분이 친히 그렇게 정해 놓으신 게 틀림없어. 이반, 말해 봐라, 신이 있느냐 없느냐? 가만, 진실을 말해, 진지하게 말하란 말이다. 너 왜 또 웃느냐?\"\n\n\"방금 아버지가 스메르댜코프의 믿음에 대해 영리한 말씀을 하셨던 게 떠올라 웃은 겁니다. 산을 옮길 수 있는 두 성인이 존재한다는 그 믿음 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐냐, 내가 지금 저 녀석을 닮았다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"아주 많이요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그건 나도 러시아인이라는 걸, 나한테도 러시아인의 특성이 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거지. 그리고 너도 철학자라지만 똑같은 식으로 걸려들 수 있어. 내가 너를 한번 걸려들게 해 볼까? 내일 너를 걸려들게 할 테니 뭘 걸겠느냐? 그건 그렇고 어쨌든 말해 봐, 신이 있느냐 없느냐? 단, 진지하게 말이다. 지금만큼은 네가 진지했으면 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 신은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"알료샤, 신이 있느냐?\"\n\n\"있습니다.\"\n\n\"이반, 그럼 불멸이란 게, 어떤 종류라도, 아주 조금이라도, 정말 손톱만큼이라도 있느냐?\"\n\n\"불멸도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"전혀 없어?\"\n\n\"전혀 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 절대적인 무(無)뿐이로구나. 어쩌면 뭐라도 조금은 있지 않을까? 무엇이든 아무것도 없는 것보단 나으니 말이야!\"\n\n\"절대적인 무뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"알료샤, 불멸이 있느냐?\"\n\n\"있습니다.\"\n\n\"신도 불멸도?\"\n\n\"신도 불멸도요. 신 안에 불멸이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"흠! 아무래도 이반 말이 옳을 성싶구나. 맙소사! 인간이 그 꿈 하나에 온갖 종류의 믿음과 힘을 그렇게나 헛되이 쏟아부어 왔다니, 그것도 몇천 년 동안이나 말이야. 대체 누가 인간을 비웃고 있는 거냐? 이반! 마지막으로, 단번에 영원히 묻겠다, 신이 있느냐 없느냐? 마지막으로 묻는 게야!\"\n\n\"그리고 마지막으로 답하지만, 없습니다.\"\n\n\"인류를 비웃고 있는 게 누구냐, 이반?\"\n\n\"악마겠지요.\" 이반이 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 악마는? 그놈은 존재하느냐?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 악마도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 안됐구나. 빌어먹을, 신이란 걸 맨 처음 지어낸 놈한테 내가 무슨 짓인들 못 하겠느냐! 쓰디쓴 사시나무에 매달아 죽이는 것으로도 그놈한테는 과분하지.\"\n\n\"신을 지어내지 않았더라면 문명이라는 것도 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"없었을 거라고? 신이 없었더라면?\"\n\n\"없었을 겁니다. 그리고 브랜디라는 것도 없었을 거고요. 하지만 어쨌든 이 브랜디는 아버지한테서 거둬 가야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐, 잠깐만, 얘야, 딱 한 잔만 더. 내가 알료샤의 마음을 상하게 했구나. 너 나한테 화난 건 아니지, 알료샤? 내 사랑하는 어린 알렉세이야!\"\n\n\"아뇨, 화 안 났어요. 아버지 마음속 생각을 저는 알아요. 아버지는 머리보다 마음이 더 나은 분이세요.\"\n\n\"내 머리보다 마음이 더 낫다고? 오, 주여! 그것도 네 입에서 나온 말이라니. 이반, 너는 알료샤를 사랑하느냐?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"넌 저 애를 사랑해야 해.\" (이 무렵 표도르 파블로비치는 잔뜩 취해 있었다.) \"들어 봐라, 알료샤, 내가 오늘 아침 네 장로한테 무례하게 굴었지. 하지만 그땐 내가 흥분했던 거야. 그래도 그 장로한테는 재치가 있어, 그렇게 생각 안 하느냐, 이반?\"\n\n\"그럴 법도 하지요.\"\n\n\"있고말고, 있어. 그 안에는 피롱 같은 데가 있단 말이야. 그자는 예수회원이야, 러시아식 예수회원이지. 그래도 명예로운 인물인지라, 거룩한 척 위선을 떨고 신성한 체해야 하는 데 대한 숨은 분노가 그 속에서 부글부글 끓고 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 물론 그분은 신을 믿겠지요.\"\n\n\"천만에. 너 몰랐느냐? 아니, 그 사람이 만나는 사람마다 자기 입으로 그렇게 말하고 다니는걸. 그러니까 만나는 사람마다는 아니고, 자기를 찾아오는 영리한 사람들 모두한테 말이지. 얼마 전엔 슐츠 지사한테 대놓고 이러더라는구나. '나는 믿습니다. 하지만 무엇을 믿는지는 모릅니다.'\"\n\n\"정말로요?\"\n\n\"정말로 그랬다니까. 하지만 나는 그 사람을 존경해. 그 사람한테는 메피스토펠레스 같은 데가 있어, 아니 그보다 '우리 시대의 영웅'에 나오는…… 아르베닌이던가, 아니 그자 이름이 뭐더라?…… 알겠느냐, 그 사람은 호색한이야. 어찌나 지독한 호색한인지, 내 딸이나 아내가 그자한테 고해하러 간다면 나는 걱정이 돼서 못 견딜 게야. 글쎄, 그 사람이 옛날이야기를 시작하면 말이지……. 재작년엔 우리를 차 마시러 초대했는데, 술이 든 차였지(부인네들이 그 사람한테 술을 보내거든). 그러더니 옛 시절 이야기를 시작하는데 우리가 배꼽이 빠지도록 웃을 지경이었다니까……. 특히 한번은 그자가 중풍 걸린 여자를 고쳐 줬다는 얘긴데. '내 다리만 성하다면 당신들한테 춤을 한바탕 춰 보일 텐데' 하더라니까. 그런 말 어떻게 생각하느냐? '내가 한창때엔 별의별 재주를 다 부렸지요' 하더란 말이야. 데르니도프라는 상인한테서 육만 루블을 가로채기도 했고.\"\n* 피롱: 알렉시 피롱(1689~1773). 재치와 신랄한 풍자로 이름난 프랑스 시인. * '우리 시대의 영웅': 미하일 레르몬토프의 소설. 표도르 파블로비치는 작중 인물 페초린과 다른 작품 인물 아르베닌을 혼동하고 있다.\n\n\"뭐라고요, 그분이 그 돈을 훔쳤다고요?\"\n\n\"그자가 믿을 만한 사람한테 맡기는 척 그 돈을 가져다주면서 이랬다는 거야. '여보게, 이걸 나 대신 좀 맡아 주게. 내일 우리 집에 경찰 수색이 있을 거라네.' 그러고는 그걸 꿀꺽한 거지. '당신은 그 돈을 교회에 바친 거요'라고 그자가 딱 잘라 말하더란 거야. 내가 그자한테 그랬지. '당신은 악당이오'라고 말이야. 그랬더니 그자가 '아니오, 나는 악당이 아니라 도량이 넓은 거요'라고 하더라는 거야. 아니, 그건 그자가 아니라 다른 사람이었구나. 내가 그자를 누구랑…… 나도 모르게 헷갈렸어. 자, 한 잔만 더 하면 그걸로 충분하다. 이반, 그 병을 치워라. 내가 거짓말을 늘어놓고 있었구나. 이반, 너는 왜 나를 말리지 않고, 내가 거짓말을 하고 있다고 일러 주지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"아버지가 알아서 멈추실 줄 알았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 거짓말이야. 너는 악의로 그런 거야, 나에 대한 순전한 악의로 말이지. 너는 나를 경멸해. 너는 나를 찾아와서는 내 집에서 나를 경멸하고 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 가 보겠습니다. 브랜디를 너무 많이 드셨어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그리스도를 봐서라도 하루 이틀만 체르마시냐에 다녀와 달라고 빌었는데, 너는 가질 않는구나.\"\n\n\"그렇게 고집하신다면 내일 다녀오지요.\"\n\n\"넌 안 갈 거다. 너는 나를 감시하고 싶은 거야. 네가 원하는 게 바로 그거지, 이 심술궂은 녀석. 그래서 안 가려는 거야.\"\n\n노인은 끈질기게 물고 늘어졌다. 그는 그때까지 얌전하던 술꾼이 별안간 시비를 걸고 제 고집을 부리려 드는, 바로 그런 취기의 단계에 이르러 있었다.\n\n\"왜 나를 그렇게 쳐다보느냐? 왜 그런 눈으로 보느냔 말이다? 네 눈은 나를 보면서 '이 추악한 주정뱅이!' 하고 말하고 있어. 네 눈은 못 믿겠다는 눈빛이야. 경멸하는 눈빛이고……. 너는 무슨 꿍꿍이속을 품고 여기 온 거야. 여기 이 알료샤를 봐라, 나를 보는 눈이 빛나고 있잖느냐. 알료샤는 나를 경멸하지 않아. 알렉세이야, 너는 이반을 사랑해선 안 된다.\"\n\n\"제 형한테 그렇게 심술궂게 굴지 마세요. 형을 공격하는 건 그만하세요.\" 알료샤가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 알았다, 알았어. 윽, 머리가 아프구나. 브랜디를 치워라, 이반. 벌써 세 번째 말하는 거다.\"\n\n그는 잠시 생각에 잠기더니, 별안간 느리고 교활한 웃음이 그의 얼굴 위로 번져 갔다.\n\n\"기력 없는 늙은이한테 화내지 마라, 이반. 네가 나를 사랑하지 않는다는 건 안다만, 그래도 화는 내지 마. 너로선 나를 사랑할 이유가 없으니까. 너 체르마시냐에 다녀오너라. 내가 직접 너한테 가서 선물을 가져다주마. 거기서 어린 계집애 하나를 보여 주지. 오래전부터 눈여겨봐 둔 애야. 아직 맨발로 뛰어다니는 애지. 맨발 계집애들을 무서워 말거라, 깔보지도 말고 말이야. 그런 애들이야말로 진주거든!\"\n\n그러고는 자기 손에 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"내 생각에는 말이야,\" 그는 좋아하는 화제를 건드리자마자 단번에 술이 깨는 듯 곧장 생기를 되찾았다. \"내 생각에는…… 아, 이 애송이들! 이 어린것들, 젖먹이 새끼돼지들 같으니, 내 생각에는…… 나는 평생 단 한 번도 여자를 못생겼다고 여겨 본 적이 없어. 그게 내 신조였지! 너희가 그걸 이해할 수 있겠느냐? 어떻게 이해하겠어? 너희 핏줄엔 피가 아니라 젖이 흐르고 있잖느냐. 아직 알 껍데기도 못 벗은 것들이. 내 신조인즉, 어떤 여자한테서든 다른 여자한테선 결코 찾을 수 없는, 무언가 지독히도 흥미로운 점을 언제든 찾아낼 수 있다는 거야. 다만 그걸 찾아낼 줄 알아야 한다는 게 핵심이지! 그게 바로 재능이거든! 내가 보기엔 못생긴 여자란 없어. 여자라는 사실 그 자체가 이미 절반은 먹고 들어가는 거니까……. 하지만 너희가 그걸 어떻게 이해하겠느냐? 노처녀들한테서도, 심지어 그런 여자들한테서도, 사내놈들이 오죽 바보 같았으면 저런 여자를 못 알아보고 늙도록 내버려 뒀을까 싶어 그저 놀라게 되는 무언가를 발견할 수 있단 말이지. 맨발 계집애들이건 매력 없는 여자들이건, 너는 불시에 들이쳐서 사로잡아야 해. 너 그것도 몰랐느냐? 그런 여자들이 넋을 잃을 때까지, 어쩔 줄 몰라 할 때까지, 그런 보잘것없는 잡것을 신사 양반이 사랑한다는 게 부끄러워질 때까지 깜짝 놀라게 만들어야 하는 거야. 세상에 주인과 노예가 늘 있어 왔고 또 앞으로도 늘 있으리란 건 참으로 다행한 일이지. 그러니 잡일을 도맡아 하는 하녀 계집애와 그 주인이 언제나 있을 게고, 너도 알다시피 행복이라는 데 필요한 건 그게 전부거든. 가만…… 들어 봐라, 알료샤, 나는 늘 네 어미를 깜짝 놀라게 했지, 하지만 다른 방식으로 말이야. 평소엔 그 사람한테 아무 관심도 안 두다가, 별안간 때가 무르익으면 나는 그 사람한테 온통 헌신적으로 굴면서 무릎으로 기어가 발에 입을 맞추곤 했어. 그러면 나는 늘, 언제나—마치 오늘 일처럼 생생하구나—그 사람한테서 그 딸랑거리는, 조용하면서도 신경질적인, 묘하고 자그마한 웃음을 끌어내곤 했지. 그건 그 사람한테만 있는 독특한 웃음이었어. 나는 그 사람의 발작이 늘 그렇게 시작된다는 걸 알고 있었거든. 다음 날이면 그 사람은 히스테리를 부리며 비명을 질러 대기 시작했고, 그 자그마한 웃음은 기쁨의 표시가 아니었던 거야, 비록 기쁨인 척 감쪽같이 흉내를 내긴 했지만 말이지. 사람마다 어떻게 다뤄야 하는지를 아는 것, 그게 바로 가장 중요한 일이야. 한번은 벨랴프스키라는 자가—그자는 잘생긴 데다 부자였는데, 여기 와서 그 사람 주위를 어슬렁거리길 좋아했지—난데없이 그 사람 앞에서 내 따귀를 한 대 갈겼어. 그런데 그 사람이—그렇게 순한 양 같던 사람이—그 따귀 한 대 때문에 나를 때려눕히기라도 할 줄 알았다니까. 어찌나 나한테 달려들던지! '당신은 맞았어요, 이제 맞은 거예요.' 그 사람이 그랬어. '당신은 저 사람한테 따귀를 얻어맞았어요. 당신은 나를 저 사람한테 팔아넘기려 했어요.' 그 사람이 그러더라니까……. '그런데 저자가 어떻게 감히 내 앞에서 당신을 때린단 말이에요! 다시는 내 곁에 얼씬도 말아요, 절대로, 절대로! 당장 달려가서 저자한테 결투를 신청해요!' …… 그래서 나는 그때 그 사람을 제정신 차리게 하려고 수도원으로 데려갔지. 거룩한 신부님들이 기도를 올려 그 사람을 정신 차리게 했어. 하지만 맹세코, 신께 맹세코, 알료샤, 나는 그 가엾고 미친 여자를 단 한 번도 모욕한 적이 없다! 어쩌면 단 한 번, 첫해에 그랬을지 모르지. 그때 그 사람은 기도를 무척 좋아했어. 특히 성모님 축일들을 꼬박꼬박 지키면서, 그럴 때면 나를 자기 방에서 내쫓곤 했지. 그래서 내가 저 신비주의를 두들겨 쫓아내 버려야겠다고 생각했어! '자,' 내가 말했지. '당신의 거룩한 성상을 보시오. 여기 있군. 내가 이걸 끌어 내리겠소. 당신은 이게 기적을 일으킨다고 믿겠지만, 보시오, 내가 지금 당장 여기에 침을 뱉어도 나한텐 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 거요!' …… 그 사람이 그걸 보고는, 맙소사! 나는 그 사람이 나를 죽이는 줄 알았어. 그런데 그 사람은 그저 벌떡 일어나서 두 손을 비틀어 쥐더니, 별안간 그 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 온몸을 부들부들 떨다가 바닥에 쓰러져 버렸어……. 그대로 푹 고꾸라진 거지. 알료샤, 알료샤, 왜 그러느냐?\"\n\n노인이 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어났다. 그가 알료샤의 어머니 이야기를 꺼내기 시작한 순간부터 알료샤의 얼굴에 차츰 변화가 일고 있었던 것이다. 그의 얼굴은 새빨갛게 달아오르고 두 눈은 이글거렸으며 입술이 떨렸다. 늙은 주정뱅이는 아무것도 알아채지 못한 채 침을 튀기며 계속 지껄여 댔는데, 마침내 알료샤에게 아주 이상한 일이 벌어지는 그 순간이 닥쳤다. 그가 미친 여자에 대해 묘사하고 있던 바로 그 일이 별안간 알료샤한테서 똑같이 되풀이된 것이다. 알료샤는 제 어머니가 그랬다고 전해지던 것과 똑같이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 비틀어 쥐고 그 손에 얼굴을 파묻더니, 의자에 도로 쓰러져 갑작스레 격렬하고 소리 없는 흐느낌의 히스테리 발작에 휩싸여 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다. 그가 제 어머니와 유난히도 닮았다는 사실이 노인에게 특히 충격을 주었다.\n\n\"이반, 이반! 물이다, 어서 물! 똑 닮았어, 그때 저 애 어미 모습 그대로야. 입에 물을 머금어서 저 애한테 뿜어 줘라, 내가 그 사람한테 해 주던 그대로 말이야. 저 애가 제 어미 때문에, 제 어미 때문에 저렇게 상심한 거야.\" 그가 이반에게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"하지만 그분은 제 어머니이기도 했던 것 같은데요, 저 애 어머니가 말입니다. 아니었습니까?\" 이반이 억누를 수 없는 분노와 경멸을 담아 말했다. 노인은 번뜩이는 그의 눈빛 앞에서 움찔 움츠러들었다. 그런데 아주 이상한 일이 벌어졌으니, 비록 한순간에 지나지 않았으되, 알료샤의 어머니가 실은 이반의 어머니이기도 하다는 사실이 정말로 노인의 머릿속에서 빠져나가 버린 듯했던 것이다.\n\n\"네 어미라고?\" 그가 영문을 모르겠다는 듯 중얼거렸다. \"그게 무슨 소리냐? 무슨 어미를 두고 하는 말이야? 그 사람이?…… 아니, 빌어먹을! 물론 그 사람은 네 어미이기도 했지! 빌어먹을! 내 정신이 이렇게까지 흐려진 적은 한 번도 없었는데. 미안하구나, 글쎄 나는 생각하기를, 이반…… 헤헤헤!\" 그가 말을 멈췄다. 넓고 술 취한, 반쯤 넋 나간 듯한 웃음이 그의 얼굴 가득 번졌다.\n\n바로 그 순간 현관에서 무시무시한 소음과 아우성이 들려오더니, 거친 고함 소리가 일고 문이 활짝 젖혀지며 드미트리가 방 안으로 뛰어들었다. 노인은 공포에 질려 이반에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"저놈이 나를 죽일 거야! 저놈이 나를 죽인다고! 저놈이 나한테 못 덤비게 해 다오!\" 그가 이반의 외투 자락에 매달리며 비명을 질렀다.\n\n# Chapter IX. The Sensualists\n\n그리고리와 스메르쟈코프가 드미트리의 뒤를 따라 방 안으로 뛰어 들어왔다. 두 사람은 복도에서 그와 몸싸움을 벌이며 그를 들이지 않으려 했으니, 이는 며칠 전 표도르 파블로비치에게서 받은 지시에 따른 것이었다. 드미트리가 방에 들어서며 잠시 멈추어 주위를 둘러보는 틈을 타, 그리고리는 탁자를 돌아 안채로 통하는 맞은편의 이중문을 닫고는, 두 팔을 한껏 벌린 채 닫힌 문 앞에 버티고 섰으니, 말하자면 마지막 핏방울 한 점까지 다해 그 입구를 지킬 태세였다. 이 광경을 보자 드미트리는 외침이라기보다 비명에 가까운 소리를 지르며 그리고리에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그 여자가 거기 있구나! 거기 숨어 있어! 비켜, 이 악당아!\"\n\n그는 그리고리를 잡아끌어 떼어 내려 했으나, 늙은 하인이 그를 도로 밀쳐 냈다. 분노에 정신을 잃은 드미트리는 주먹을 휘둘러 온 힘을 다해 그리고리를 후려쳤다. 노인은 통나무처럼 쓰러졌고, 드미트리는 그를 뛰어넘어 문을 부수고 들어갔다. 스메르쟈코프는 방 저쪽 끝에서 창백하게 떨며 표도르 파블로비치에게 바싹 붙어 있었다.\n\n\"여기 있어!\" 드미트리가 소리쳤다. \"방금 그 여자가 이 집 쪽으로 돌아서는 걸 봤단 말이야. 그런데 따라잡지 못했어. 어디 있어? 어디 있느냐고?\"\n\n\"여기 있어!\" 하는 그 외침은 표도르 파블로비치에게 형언할 수 없는 영향을 미쳤다. 그를 사로잡고 있던 공포가 죄다 사라져 버린 것이다.\n\n\"붙잡아라! 붙잡아!\" 그는 소리치며 드미트리를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다. 그사이 그리고리는 바닥에서 일어나기는 했으나 아직도 정신이 멍한 듯했다. 이반과 알료샤가 아버지를 뒤따라 달려갔다. 세 번째 방에서 무언가가 쨍그랑 요란한 소리를 내며 바닥에 떨어지는 소리가 들렸으니, 그것은 대리석 받침대 위에 놓인 큼직한 유리 화병이었는데—값나가는 것은 아니었다—드미트리가 그 곁을 지나치며 넘어뜨린 것이었다.\n\n\"저놈을 잡아라!\" 노인이 소리쳤다. \"사람 살려!\"\n\n이반과 알료샤가 노인을 붙들어 억지로 도로 데려왔다.\n\n\"왜 저 사람을 쫓아가세요? 단번에 아버지를 죽여 버릴 거란 말입니다.\" 이반이 아버지를 향해 노기 띤 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"이반! 알료샤! 그 여자가 틀림없이 여기 있어. 그루셴카가 여기 있어. 저놈이 제 눈으로 그 여자가 뛰어가는 걸 봤다고 했잖느냐.\"\n\n그는 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 그는 그때 그루셴카를 기다리고 있지 않았으므로, 그녀가 여기 있다는 난데없는 소식에 제정신을 잃고 말았다. 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다. 미친 사람 같았다.\n\n\"하지만 아버지도 직접 보셨잖습니까, 그 여자는 오지 않았어요.\" 이반이 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 저 다른 입구로 들어왔는지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그 입구는 잠겨 있고, 열쇠는 아버지가 가지고 계시잖습니까.\"\n\n드미트리가 갑자기 응접실에 다시 나타났다. 물론 그는 다른 입구가 잠겨 있는 것을 알아냈고, 열쇠는 실제로 표도르 파블로비치의 호주머니에 들어 있었던 것이다. 모든 방의 창문도 죄다 닫혀 있었으니, 그루셴카가 어디로도 들어왔을 리 없었고 어디로도 빠져나갔을 리 없었다.\n\n\"저놈을 붙잡아라!\" 그를 다시 보자마자 표도르 파블로비치가 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"저놈이 내 침실에서 돈을 훔쳤다.\" 그러고는 이반에게서 몸을 뿌리치며 다시 드미트리에게 달려들었다. 그러나 드미트리는 두 손을 번쩍 쳐들더니 노인의 양쪽 관자놀이에 남아 있던 두 줌의 머리털을 와락 움켜쥐고는 잡아당겨 쾅 소리가 나도록 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 그는 발뒤꿈치로 노인의 얼굴을 두세 차례 걷어찼다. 노인은 날카롭게 신음했다. 이반은 드미트리만큼 힘이 세지는 않았으나, 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 온 힘을 다해 떼어 냈다. 알료샤도 그 가냘픈 힘이나마 보태어 드미트리를 앞에서 붙들었다.\n\n\"미친놈아! 아버지를 죽였잖아!\" 이반이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래도 싸지!\" 드미트리가 숨을 헐떡이며 소리쳤다. \"이번에 죽이지 못했다면, 다시 와서 죽이고 말 테다. 너희가 저 사람을 지켜낼 순 없어!\"\n\n\"드미트리 형님! 당장 나가요!\" 알료샤가 명령하듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"알렉세이! 네가 말해 다오. 내가 믿을 수 있는 건 너밖에 없으니까. 방금 그 여자가 여기 있었느냐, 없었느냐? 내 눈으로 그 여자가 골목길에서 울타리를 따라 이쪽으로 슬그머니 오는 걸 봤단 말이다. 내가 소리치니까 도망쳤어.\"\n\n\"맹세코 그 여자는 여기 온 적 없고, 아무도 그 여자를 기다리지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 봤다니까…… 그러니 틀림없이…… 당장 그 여자가 어디 있는지 알아내고 말겠다…… 잘 있어라, 알렉세이! 이젠 저 이솝한테 돈 얘긴 한마디도 하지 마라. 하지만 카체리나 이바노브나한테는 당장 가서, 꼭 이렇게 전해라. '그가 당신에게 안부를 전합니다!' 안부, 안부를 전한다고! 그저 안부와 작별 인사뿐이다! 그 여자한테 이 광경을 그대로 일러 주거라.\"\n\n그사이 이반과 그리고리는 노인을 부축해 일으켜 안락의자에 앉혔다. 그의 얼굴은 피로 뒤덮여 있었으나, 그는 의식이 있었고 드미트리의 외침에 게걸스레 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그는 여전히 그루셴카가 정말로 집 어딘가에 있다고 상상하고 있었다. 드미트리는 방을 나서면서 그를 증오에 찬 눈으로 노려보았다.\n\n\"네 피를 흘리게 한 걸 후회하지 않는다!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"조심해라, 늙은이여, 네 꿈을 조심해라. 나에게도 내 꿈이 있으니까. 나는 너를 저주하며, 너를 아주 의절하겠다.\"\n\n그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"그 여자가 여기 있어. 틀림없이 여기 있어. 스메르쟈코프! 스메르쟈코프!\" 노인이 가까스로 들릴락 말락 한 소리로 쌕쌕거리며 손가락으로 그를 불렀다.\n\n\"아니에요, 여기 없어요, 이 노망난 늙은이야!\" 이반이 그에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"아니, 기절하시는데! 물! 수건! 어서, 스메르쟈코프!\"\n\n스메르쟈코프가 물을 가지러 달려갔다. 마침내 그들은 노인의 옷을 벗겨 침대에 눕혔다. 그들은 그의 머리에 젖은 수건을 둘러 감았다. 브랜디에, 격렬한 흥분에, 거기에 얻어맞은 매질까지 겹쳐 기진맥진한 그는 눈을 감았고, 머리가 베개에 닿자마자 잠들어 버렸다. 이반과 알료샤는 응접실로 돌아갔다. 스메르쟈코프는 깨진 화병 조각을 치웠고, 그리고리는 탁자 곁에 서서 음울한 눈으로 바닥을 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n\"당신도 머리에 젖은 붕대를 감고 누우셔야 하지 않겠어요?\" 알료샤가 그에게 말했다. \"아버지는 우리가 돌볼게요. 형님이 당신을—머리를 호되게 때렸잖아요.\"\n\n\"그분이 저를 모욕했습니다!\" 그리고리가 음울하면서도 또렷하게 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그가 '모욕'한 건 자네뿐 아니라 제 아버지일세.\" 이반이 억지웃음을 지으며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"제가 그분을 목욕통에 넣고 씻겨 드리곤 했지요. 그런 그분이 저를 모욕했습니다.\" 그리고리가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 내가 떼어 놓지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그놈이 아버지를 죽여 버렸을지도 몰라. 이솝 하나 해치우는 데야 그리 힘들 것도 없지 않겠나?\" 이반이 알료샤에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"하느님 맙소사!\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"하느님이 왜 막으셔야 하는데?\" 이반이 여전히 속삭이는 목소리로, 악의에 찬 표정을 지으며 말을 이었다. \"한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는 거지. 그래야 둘 다 마땅한 벌을 받는 거고.\"\n\n알료샤는 몸서리를 쳤다.\n\n\"물론 방금처럼 나도 아버지가 살해당하도록 내버려 두진 않을 거다. 여기 있어라, 알료샤, 난 마당에 좀 나가 거닐어야겠다. 머리가 아프기 시작했어.\"\n\n알료샤는 아버지의 침실로 가서 칸막이 뒤 침대맡에 한 시간쯤 앉아 있었다. 노인이 갑자기 눈을 뜨더니 알료샤를 한참이나 바라보았는데, 분명 무언가를 기억해 내며 곰곰이 생각하는 듯했다. 별안간 그의 얼굴에 예사롭지 않은 흥분이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"알료샤,\" 그가 불안스레 속삭였다. \"이반은 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"마당에요. 머리가 아프대요. 망을 보고 있어요.\"\n\n\"저 거울을 다오. 저기 놓여 있는 거 말이다. 그걸 이리 다오.\"\n\n알료샤는 서랍장 위에 놓인 작고 둥근 접이식 거울을 그에게 건네주었다. 노인은 그 속에 비친 제 모습을 들여다보았다. 코가 상당히 부어 있었고, 이마 왼편에는 제법 큼직한 새빨간 멍이 들어 있었다.\n\n\"이반이 뭐라고 하더냐? 알료샤, 내 사랑하는, 내 하나뿐인 아들아, 나는 이반이 무섭구나. 다른 놈보다 이반이 더 무서워. 내가 무서워하지 않는 건 너 하나뿐이야……\"\n\n\"이반도 무서워하지 마세요. 화는 났지만, 그래도 아버지를 지켜 드릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"알료샤, 그럼 그 다른 놈은? 그놈이 그루셴카한테 달려갔어. 내 천사야, 진실을 말해 다오, 그 여자가 방금 여기 있었느냐 없었느냐?\"\n\n\"아무도 그 여자를 보지 못했어요. 착각이었어요. 그 여자는 여기 온 적 없어요.\"\n\n\"미탸가 그 여자랑 결혼하려는 거 알지, 그 여자랑 결혼하려고.\"\n\n\"그 여자는 형님과 결혼하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"안 하지. 안 하고말고. 안 하지. 무슨 일이 있어도 안 할 거야!\"\n\n노인은 마치 그보다 더 위안이 되는 말은 없으리라는 듯 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 그는 환희에 차서 알료샤의 손을 붙잡아 따뜻하게 제 가슴에 꼭 눌렀다. 두 눈에는 정말로 눈물이 반짝였다.\n\n\"방금 너한테 얘기했던 그 성모 마리아의 성상 말이다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그걸 집으로 가져가서 네가 간직하거라. 그리고 수도원으로 돌아가도록 해 주마…… 오늘 아침엔 농담을 한 거니, 나한테 화내지 마라. 머리가 아프구나, 알료샤…… 알료샤, 내 마음을 위로해 다오. 천사가 되어 진실을 말해 다오!\"\n\n\"아직도 그 여자가 여기 있었는지 없었는지를 물으시는 거예요?\" 알료샤가 서글프게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니다. 너를 믿어. 내가 뭘 바라는지 말해 주마. 네가 직접 그루셴카한테 가거라, 아니면 어떻게든 그 여자를 만나거라. 어서 서둘러 물어봐 다오. 네 눈으로 직접 확인해라, 그 여자가 그놈과 나 둘 중에 누굴 택할 작정인지. 응? 어떠냐? 그래 줄 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 그 여자를 만나면 물어보겠어요.\" 알료샤가 당황한 듯 우물거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 그 여잔 너한테 말 안 할 거다.\" 노인이 말을 가로막았다. \"그 여잔 교활한 계집이야. 너한테 입을 맞추기 시작하면서 자기가 원하는 건 너라고 할 거다. 그 여잔 거짓되고 뻔뻔스러운 화냥년이야. 넌 그 여자한테 가면 안 돼, 가면 안 된다!\"\n\n\"안 가요, 아버지, 그건 적절하지도 않고, 도무지 옳은 일이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"방금 그놈이 너를 어디로 보내려던 거냐? 달아나면서 '가라'고 소리치던데.\"\n\n\"카체리나 이바노브나한테요.\"\n\n\"돈 때문에? 그 여자한테 돈을 달라고 하려고?\"\n\n\"아뇨. 돈 때문은 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그놈은 돈이 없어. 한 푼도 없지. 난 자리에 누워 밤을 보내며 이런저런 일을 곰곰이 따져 봐야겠다. 너는 가도 좋다. 어쩌면 그 여자를 만나게 될지도 모르지…… 다만 내일 아침에는 꼭 나한테 와다오. 꼭 와야 한다. 내일 너한테 할 말이 있어. 와 주겠지?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"올 때는, 네가 제 발로 내 안부를 물으러 온 척해라. 내가 너한테 오라고 했다는 말은 아무한테도 하지 마라. 이반한테는 한마디도 하지 말고.\"\n\n\"알았어요.\"\n\n\"잘 가거라, 내 천사야. 너는 방금 나를 두둔해 줬지. 결코 잊지 않으마. 내일 너한테 할 말이 있다만—그건 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠다.\"\n\n\"지금 기분은 좀 어떠세요?\"\n\n\"내일이면 일어나서 나갈 거다, 멀쩡하게, 아주 멀쩡하게 말이야!\"\n\n마당을 가로지르던 알료샤는 대문께 벤치에 앉아 있는 이반을 발견했다. 그는 수첩에 연필로 무언가를 적고 있었다. 알료샤는 이반에게 아버지가 깨어나 의식을 차렸고, 자기에게 수도원으로 돌아가 자도 좋다고 허락했다고 전했다.\n\n\"알료샤, 내일 아침에 너를 만나면 정말 기쁘겠구나.\" 이반이 일어서며 다정하게 말했다. 그 다정함은 알료샤에게 전혀 뜻밖이었다.\n\n\"내일은 호흘라코바 부인 댁에 갈 거예요.\" 알료샤가 대답했다. \"지금 카체리나 이바노브나를 만나지 못하면, 그분 댁에도 갈지 몰라요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어쨌든 지금 그분한테 가는 거잖아? 그 '안부와 작별 인사' 때문에 말이야.\" 이반이 미소 지으며 말했다. 알료샤는 당황했다.\n\n\"방금 그가 외쳐 대던 말도, 그 전에 있었던 일의 일부도 내가 충분히 이해할 것 같다. 드미트리가 너한테 그 여자한테 가서 자기가—그러니까 결국—그 여자에게 작별을 고한다고 전하라고 부탁한 거지?\"\n\n\"형님, 아버지하고 드미트리 형님 사이의 이 끔찍한 일이 대체 어떻게 끝날까요?\" 알료샤가 부르짖었다.\n\n\"확실히는 알 수 없지. 어쩌면 아무 일도 없을지 몰라. 모두 흐지부지될 수도 있고. 그 여자는 짐승 같은 계집이야. 어쨌든 우린 아버지를 집 안에 붙들어 두고 드미트리를 집에 들이지 말아야 해.\"\n\n\"형님, 한 가지만 더 묻겠어요. 어떤 사람이 다른 사람들을 바라보면서 누가 살 만한 가치가 있는지 결정할 권리가 있을까요?\"\n\n\"왜 가치 문제를 끌어들이지? 그런 일은 대개 사람들 마음속에서 훨씬 더 자연스러운 다른 근거로 결정되는 법이야. 권리로 말하자면—바랄 권리가 없는 사람이 어디 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"다른 사람의 죽음을 바랄 권리도요?\"\n\n\"다른 사람의 죽음을 바란들 또 어떠냐? 모든 사람이 다 그렇게 살고, 어쩌면 그렇게 살 수밖에 없는데 굳이 자신을 속일 까닭이 뭐야? 너 방금 내가 한 말을 두고 그러는 거냐—한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는다고 한 말 말이다? 그렇다면 너한테 묻겠다, 너는 내가 드미트리처럼 이솝의 피를 흘리게 하고, 그를 죽일 수 있는 인간이라고 생각하느냐, 응?\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 이반 형님? 그런 생각은 한 번도 머리에 떠오른 적 없어요. 드미트리 형님도 그럴 수 있는 사람이라고 생각하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 그것만으로도.\" 이반이 미소 지었다. \"분명히 말하지만, 난 언제든 그를 두둔할 거다. 하지만 내가 무엇을 바라느냐 하는 점에서는, 이 경우 나 자신에게 완전한 여지를 남겨 두겠다. 내일까지 잘 있어라. 나를 단죄하지 말고, 나를 악당으로 보지도 마라.\" 그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n두 사람은 일찍이 없던 일로 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다. 알료샤는 형이 자기를 향해 첫걸음을 내디뎠음을, 그리고 그가 분명 어떤 뚜렷한 동기를 가지고 그렇게 했음을 느꼈다.\n\n# Chapter X. Both Together\n\n알료샤는 아버지의 집을 나설 때, 들어설 때보다 한층 더 지치고 마음마저 침울해진 상태였다. 그의 머릿속도 산산이 부서져 갈피를 잡지 못하는 것 같았고, 그날 겪은 온갖 괴롭고 모순된 경험의 흩어진 조각들을 한데 모아 어떤 전체적인 그림을 그려 보는 것조차 두렵게 느껴졌다. 그는 그때까지 한 번도 알지 못했던, 절망에 가까운 무언가를 느꼈다. 그 모든 것 위로 산처럼 우뚝 솟아 있는 것은 풀리지 않는 운명의 물음이었다. 아버지와 형 드미트리 사이의 일이 그 무서운 여자를 둘러싸고 대체 어떻게 끝날 것인가? 이제 그는 직접 그 광경의 증인이 되었고, 그 자리에 있었으며, 두 사람이 마주 보고 서 있는 것을 제 눈으로 보았다. 그러나 불행해질 수 있는 사람은, 무섭게, 완전히 불행해질 수 있는 사람은 오직 형 드미트리뿐이었다. 그에게는 닥쳐올 재앙이 기다리고 있었다. 게다가 알료샤가 미처 짐작하지 못했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 사람들이 이 일에 얽혀 있는 듯했다. 그 속에는 무언가 분명히 수수께끼 같은 것도 있었다. 이반이 그를 향해 한 발짝 다가서 주었는데, 그것은 알료샤가 오랫동안 바라 마지않던 일이었다. 그런데도 지금 그는 무슨 까닭에선지 그 일이 두렵게 느껴졌다. 그리고 이 여자들은? 이상한 말이지만, 그날 아침 그는 더없이 큰 거북함을 안고 카테리나 이바노브나의 집으로 향했었다. 그런데 지금은 그런 마음이 전혀 들지 않았다. 오히려 그는 마치 그녀에게서 어떤 인도를 받기를 기대하기라도 하듯 그곳으로 서둘러 가고 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게 이 전갈을 전하는 일은 분명 이전보다 더 어려웠다. 삼천 루블 문제는 돌이킬 수 없이 결정되어 버렸고, 명예를 잃었다고 느끼며 마지막 희망마저 잃은 드미트리는 어떤 나락으로든 떨어질 수 있었다. 게다가 그는 알료샤에게 방금 아버지와 벌어진 그 장면을 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그대로 전하라고 일러 두기까지 했다.\n\n어느덧 일곱 시였고, 알료샤가 카테리나 이바노브나가 사는, 큰길가에 자리한 매우 널찍하고 편리한 집으로 들어설 무렵에는 날이 어두워지고 있었다. 알료샤는 그녀가 두 고모와 함께 산다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그중 한 사람은 별로 배우지 못한 여자로, 그녀의 이복 언니 아가피야 이바노브나의 고모였는데, 그녀가 기숙학교를 마치고 돌아왔을 때 친정에서 그녀를 돌봐 준 사람이었다. 또 한 사람은 모스크바 출신의 격식 있고 명망 있는 부인이었으나 형편은 쪼들리는 처지였다. 들리는 말로는 두 고모가 매사에 카테리나 이바노브나의 뜻을 따랐으며, 그녀가 다만 보호자 격으로 두 사람을 곁에 두고 있을 뿐이라고 했다. 카테리나 이바노브나 자신은 그 누구의 뜻에도 굽히지 않았으되, 오직 자신의 후원자인 장군 부인에게만은 예외였으니, 그 부인은 병으로 모스크바에 발이 묶여 있었고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 일주일에 두 번씩 자신의 모든 행적을 빠짐없이 적어 편지로 보내야 했다.\n\n알료샤가 현관으로 들어서서 문을 열어 준 하녀에게 자기 이름을 위층에 전해 달라고 청했을 때, 사람들이 이미 그의 도착을 알고 있는 것이 분명했다. 어쩌면 창문으로 그를 발견했는지도 몰랐다. 적어도 알료샤는 무언가 부산스러운 소리를, 황급히 내닫는 발소리와 치맛자락 스치는 소리를 들었다. 어쩌면 두세 명의 여자가 방에서 뛰쳐나갔는지도 몰랐다.\n\n알료샤는 자신의 도착이 이런 소동을 일으킨다는 것이 이상하다고 생각했다. 그러나 그는 곧장 응접실로 안내되었다. 그곳은 넓은 방으로, 우아하고 풍성하게 꾸며져 있어 도무지 지방 도시풍이라고는 할 수 없었다. 소파며 안락의자며 긴 의자가 여러 개 있었고, 크고 작은 탁자들이 놓여 있었다. 벽에는 그림이 걸려 있었고, 탁자 위에는 꽃병과 등잔이 있었으며, 꽃이 무더기로 꽂혀 있었고, 창가에는 수족관까지 있었다. 황혼 무렵이라 방 안은 제법 어두웠다. 알료샤는 소파 위에 던져진 비단 망토를 알아보았는데, 분명 방금까지 사람들이 거기 앉아 있었던 모양이었다. 그리고 소파 앞 탁자 위에는 다 마시지 않은 초콜릿 잔 두 개와 과자, 푸른 건포도가 담긴 유리 접시, 그리고 사탕 과자가 담긴 또 하나의 접시가 놓여 있었다. 알료샤는 자기가 손님이 와 있던 자리를 방해했음을 깨닫고 이맛살을 찌푸렸다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 휘장이 걷히더니, 빠르고 다급한 발걸음으로 카테리나 이바노브나가 들어와, 기쁨으로 환하게 빛나는 미소를 띤 채 알료샤를 향해 두 손을 내밀었다. 그와 동시에 하인이 불 켜진 양초 두 개를 가져와 탁자 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n\"오, 다행이에요! 마침내 당신도 와 주셨군요! 온종일 당신을 위해 기도하다시피 했답니다! 앉으세요.\"\n\n알료샤는 세 주 전, 드미트리가 카테리나 이바노브나의 특별한 청에 따라 그를 처음으로 데리고 와 그녀에게 소개했을 때, 그녀의 아름다움에 충격을 받았었다. 그러나 그 첫 만남에서 두 사람 사이에 이렇다 할 대화는 없었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 알료샤를 몹시 수줍어하는 사람으로 여기고는, 그가 어색하지 않도록 줄곧 드미트리하고만 이야기를 나누었다. 알료샤는 말이 없었으나, 아주 많은 것을 또렷이 보아 두었다. 그는 그 도도한 처녀의 위압적인 태도와 거만한 여유로움, 그리고 자신감에 충격을 받았다. 그리고 그 모든 것은 분명한 사실이었으니, 알료샤는 자기가 그것을 과장하고 있는 것이 아니라고 느꼈다. 그는 그녀의 크고 이글거리는 검은 두 눈이 매우 아름답다고 여겼는데, 특히 창백하다 못해 다소 누르스름하기까지 한 갸름한 얼굴과 어우러져 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 그 두 눈에도, 또 그 우아한 입술의 윤곽에도, 형이 충분히 열렬하게 사랑할 만하면서도 어쩌면 오래도록 사랑하기는 어려울 듯한 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 그날 방문을 마친 뒤, 형이 약혼녀를 본 인상을 숨기지 말라고 애원하며 다그쳤을 때, 알료샤는 이 생각을 거의 그대로 드미트리에게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"형은 그녀와 행복할 거야. 하지만 어쩌면 마음 편한 행복은 아닐지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇다, 아우야. 그런 사람들은 늘 한결같지. 운명에 굴복하는 법이 없어. 그러니까 너는 내가 그녀를 영원히 사랑하지는 못할 거라고 생각하는구나.\"\n\n\"아니. 형은 어쩌면 그녀를 영원히 사랑할지도 몰라. 하지만 어쩌면 그녀와 늘 행복하지는 못할 거야.\"\n\n알료샤는 그때 얼굴을 붉히며 자기 의견을 말했고, 형의 간청에 못 이겨 그런 \"어리석은\" 생각을 입 밖에 낸 자신에게 화가 났다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그 말을 내뱉은 직후 자신의 의견이 끔찍하게 어리석게 느껴졌기 때문이다. 또한 여자에 대해 그토록 자신만만한 평가를 내린 것이 부끄럽기도 했다. 그랬기에 지금 그는 한결 더 놀라며 느꼈으니, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 달려 들어오는 것을 처음 본 순간, 자기가 어쩌면 완전히 잘못 보았는지도 모른다는 것이었다. 이번에 그녀의 얼굴은 꾸밈없이 우러나는 따뜻한 다정함과, 거짓 없이 정겨운 진실함으로 빛나고 있었다. 전에 그토록 알료샤를 놀라게 했던 그 \"긍지와 거만함\"은 이제 다만 솔직하고 너그러운 활기와, 어떤 밝고 굳센 자기 확신으로만 드러날 따름이었다. 알료샤는 첫눈에, 첫마디에 깨달았으니, 그녀가 그토록 애틋이 사랑하는 사내와 얽힌 자기 처지의 그 모든 비극이 그녀에게 결코 비밀이 아니라는 것을, 어쩌면 그녀가 이미 모든 것을, 정말 모든 것을 알고 있으리라는 것을 알아챘다. 그런데도 불구하고 그녀의 얼굴에는 그토록 환한 빛이, 미래에 대한 그토록 굳은 믿음이 있었다. 알료샤는 곧 자기가 마음속으로 그녀를 몹시 그릇되게 판단했음을 느꼈다. 그는 곧장 사로잡히고 매혹되었다. 그 모든 것에 더하여, 그는 그녀의 첫마디에서 그녀가 크나큰 흥분에 빠져 있음을, 어쩌면 아주 예사롭지 않은, 거의 환희에 가까운 흥분에 빠져 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"당신을 무척이나 만나고 싶었어요. 왜냐하면 당신에게서 모든 진실을 알 수 있으니까요. 당신에게서, 다른 누구도 아닌 당신에게서요.\"\n\n\"제가 온 것은,\" 알료샤가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 중얼거렸다. \"저는, 그 사람이 저를 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그 사람이 당신을 보냈군요! 저는 그럴 줄 예감했어요. 이제 저는 모든 것을 알아요, 모든 것을!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 두 눈을 번득이며 외쳤다. \"잠깐만요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 왜 그토록 당신을 보고 싶어 했는지 말씀드릴게요. 보세요, 저는 어쩌면 당신 자신보다도 훨씬 많은 것을 알고 있으니, 당신이 제게 무얼 말씀해 주실 필요는 없어요. 제가 당신께 바라는 것을 말씀드리죠. 저는 당신이 그 사람에게서 받은 마지막 인상을 알고 싶어요. 저는 당신이 가장 솔직하게, 가감 없이, 거칠게라도(오, 얼마든지 거칠게요!) 말씀해 주시기를 바라요. 오늘 그 사람을 만난 뒤, 당신이 그 사람을, 그리고 그 사람의 처지를 어떻게 보았는지를 말이에요. 그게 어쩌면 제가 그 사람과 직접 담판을 짓는 것보다 나을 거예요. 그 사람은 제게 오려 하지 않으니까요. 제가 당신께 무얼 바라는지 아시겠어요? 자, 그저 단순하게 말씀해 주세요. 그 사람이 당신 편에 보낸 전갈을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 말씀해 주세요(저는 그 사람이 당신을 보내리라는 걸 알고 있었어요).\"\n\n\"그분은 당신에게 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨습니다. 그리고 다시는 오지 않겠노라고 말해 달라고요. 다만 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"안부를요? 그 사람이 그렇게 말했나요, 그 사람 자신의 말로?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"혹시 우연히 말을 잘못 골랐던 게 아닐까요, 어쩌면 맞는 말을 쓰지 않았던 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 그분은 제게 그 말을 그대로 전하라고 분명히 일렀습니다. 잊지 말고 꼭 그렇게 전해 달라고 두세 번이나 당부하셨습니다.\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나의 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다.\n\n\"이제 저를 도와주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 지금 저는 정말로 당신의 도움이 필요해요. 제 생각을 말씀드릴 테니, 당신은 그저 그게 맞는지 아닌지만 말씀해 주세요. 들어 보세요! 만일 그 사람이 지나가는 말로 제게 안부를 전한 거라면, 당신더러 그 말을 그대로 옮기라고 고집하지도 않고, 그 말을 강조하지도 않았다면, 그건 모든 것의 끝이었을 거예요! 하지만 만일 그 사람이 그 말을 특별히 강조했다면, 그 말을 제게 그대로 전하는 것을 잊지 말라고 특별히 당부했다면, 그렇다면 어쩌면 그 사람은 흥분해 있었던 거예요, 제정신이 아니었던 거예요. 그 사람은 결심을 내려 놓고도 그 결심에 스스로 겁을 먹은 거예요. 그 사람은 결연한 발걸음으로 제게서 걸어 나간 게 아니라, 무작정 곤두박질치듯 뛰어나간 거예요. 그 말에 힘을 준 건 어쩌면 그저 허세였을 뿐일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요!\" 알료샤가 열을 띠며 외쳤다. \"제 생각에도 그런 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 그렇다면 그 사람은 아주 끝장난 게 아니에요. 저는 아직 그 사람을 구할 수 있어요. 가만! 그 사람이 돈 이야기는, 삼천 루블 이야기는 하지 않던가요?\"\n\n\"그 이야기도 하셨습니다. 그리고 무엇보다도 바로 그 일이 그분을 짓누르고 있습니다. 그분은 자기가 명예를 잃었으며 이제 아무것도 중요하지 않다고 하셨습니다.\" 알료샤가 열을 띠며 대답했는데, 그는 가슴속에 희망이 솟구치는 것을 느끼며, 정말로 형에게 벗어날 길이, 구원의 길이 있을지도 모른다고 믿었다. \"그런데 당신은 그 돈에 대해 알고 계십니까?\" 그는 이렇게 덧붙이다가 갑자기 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"저는 오래전부터 그 일을 알고 있었어요. 모스크바로 전보를 쳐서 알아보았고, 돈이 도착하지 않았다는 걸 벌써 오래전에 들었어요. 그 사람은 돈을 부치지 않았지만, 저는 아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 지난주에 저는 그 사람이 아직도 돈에 쪼들리고 있다는 걸 알게 됐어요. 이 모든 일에서 제 단 하나의 바람은, 그 사람이 누구에게 의지해야 할지, 누가 자신의 진정한 벗인지를 깨닫게 하는 것이었어요. 아니에요, 그 사람은 제가 자신의 가장 진실한 벗임을 인정하려 들지 않아요. 그 사람은 저를 알아보려 하지 않고, 그저 저를 한낱 여자로만 여겨요. 저는 한 주 내내 괴로워했어요. 그 사람이 그 삼천 루블을 써 버렸다고 해서 제 얼굴을 보기 부끄러워하지 않도록 하려면 어떻게 해야 할지 골몰하면서요. 그 사람더러 자기 자신에게 부끄러워하라고 해요, 다른 사람들이 아는 것을 부끄러워하라고 해요, 하지만 제가 아는 것은 부끄러워하지 말아요. 그 사람은 부끄러움 없이 하느님께는 모든 것을 다 말할 수 있잖아요. 그 사람은 어째서 아직도 모르는 걸까요, 제가 그 사람을 위해서라면 얼마나 견딜 각오가 되어 있는지를? 어째서, 어째서 그 사람은 저를 모르는 걸까요? 그 모든 일이 다 벌어진 마당에 어떻게 감히 저를 모를 수 있단 말인가요? 저는 그 사람을 영원히 구하고 싶어요. 그 사람더러 저를 약혼녀로는 잊으라고 해요. 그런데도 그 사람은 제 눈에 자기가 명예를 잃은 것으로 비칠까 봐 두려워하고 있어요. 아니, 그 사람은 당신에게는 솔직하기를 두려워하지 않았잖아요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 어째서 저는 그만한 대접도 받을 자격이 없다는 건가요?\"\n\n마지막 말을 그녀는 눈물 속에서 토해 냈다. 두 눈에서 눈물이 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n\"한 가지 말씀드려야겠습니다,\" 알료샤가 입을 열었는데, 그의 목소리 역시 떨리고 있었다. \"방금 그분과 제 아버지 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지를요.\"\n\n그리고 그는 그 모든 장면을 들려주었으니, 드미트리가 돈을 받아 오라고 자기를 보낸 일이며, 그가 어떻게 뛰어들어 아버지를 때려눕혔는지, 그러고 나서 다시금 각별히, 힘주어 자기더러 안부와 작별 인사를 전해 달라고 부탁한 일을 이야기했다. \"그분은 그 여자에게 갔습니다.\" 알료샤가 나직이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래서 당신은 제가 그 여자를 견뎌 내지 못할 거라고 생각하시나요? 그 사람은 제가 견디지 못하리라고 생각하나요? 하지만 그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못할 거예요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 신경질적으로 웃었다. \"카라마조프 집안 사람에게 그런 열정이 영원히 갈 수 있을까요? 그건 사랑이 아니라 욕정이에요. 그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못해요, 그 여자가 그 사람과 결혼하려 하지 않을 테니까요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나는 다시 이상하게 웃었다.\n\n\"그분은 어쩌면 그 여자와 결혼할지도 모릅니다.\" 알료샤가 시선을 떨군 채 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못한다니까요. 그 처녀는 천사예요. 당신은 그걸 아세요? 그걸 아세요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 별안간 유난한 열기를 띠며 외쳤다. \"그 여자는 환상적인 존재들 가운데서도 가장 환상적인 사람이에요. 저는 그 여자가 얼마나 사람을 홀리는지 알지만, 동시에 그 여자가 얼마나 다정하고 굳세고 고결한지도 알아요. 어째서 저를 그렇게 보시나요, 알렉세이 표도로비치? 어쩌면 제 말에 놀라셨나 보군요. 어쩌면 제 말을 믿지 못하시는 거예요? 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 내 천사!\" 그녀가 별안간 옆방을 들여다보며 누군가를 향해 소리쳤다. \"이리로 와요. 이분은 친구예요. 이분은 알료샤예요. 이분은 우리 일을 다 알고 계셔요. 어서 이분께 나와 봐요.\"\n\n\"부르시기만을 휘장 뒤에서 기다리고 있었답니다.\" 부드러운, 어쩌면 달짝지근하다고까지 할 만한 여자의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n휘장이 걷히고, 그루셴카가 미소를 머금고 환하게 빛나며 탁자 쪽으로 다가왔다. 알료샤의 마음속에 격렬한 반발감이 일었다. 그는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 시선을 고정했다. 바로 그 여자가 거기 있었으니, 반 시간 전에 이반이 \"짐승\"이라 부른 그 무서운 여자였다. 그런데도 그의 앞에 서 있는 그 사람은 더없이 단순하고 평범하며, 마음씨 좋고 다정한 여자처럼 보였으니, 분명 아름답기는 했으나 다른 여느 아름답고 평범한 여자들과 너무도 닮아 있었다! 사실 그녀는 매우, 매우 아름다웠으니, 수많은 사내들이 그토록 열렬히 사랑하는 그 러시아적인 미모를 지니고 있었다. 그녀는 키가 제법 큰 여자였으되, 유난히 키가 큰 카테리나 이바노브나보다는 조금 작았다. 그녀는 풍만한 몸매를 지녔고, 그 동작은 부드러워서, 말하자면 소리가 나지 않았으며, 그 목소리처럼 어떤 유별난 과도한 달콤함으로 누그러져 있었다. 그녀는 카테리나 이바노브나처럼 힘차고 대담한 걸음걸이가 아니라, 소리 없이 움직였다. 그녀의 발은 마룻바닥에서 전혀 소리를 내지 않았다. 그녀는 낮은 의자에 사뿐히 몸을 묻으며, 화려한 검은 비단 드레스를 부드럽게 살랑거렸고, 우윳빛 목덜미와 넓은 어깨를 값비싼 캐시미어 숄에 곱게 파묻었다. 그녀는 스물두 살이었고, 얼굴도 꼭 그 나이로 보였다. 얼굴빛은 매우 희었고, 두 뺨에는 옅은 분홍빛이 어려 있었다. 얼굴의 윤곽은 다소 지나치게 넓다고 할 만했고, 아래턱이 약간 앞으로 내밀려 있었다. 윗입술은 얇았으나, 살짝 도드라진 아랫입술은 적어도 그 두 배는 도톰하여 뾰로통해 보였다. 그러나 그 풍성하고 멋진 짙은 갈색 머리칼과, 검은담비 빛 눈썹, 그리고 긴 속눈썹에 둘러싸인 매혹적인 회청색 눈동자는, 거리의 인파 속에서 우연히 그녀와 마주친 더없이 무심한 사람조차도 그 얼굴을 보고는 걸음을 멈추게 하고 오래도록 기억에 남게 했을 것이다. 그 얼굴에서 알료샤를 가장 강하게 친 것은 어린아이 같은 천진함의 표정이었다. 그녀의 눈 속에는 어린아이 같은 눈빛이, 어린아이 같은 기쁨의 빛이 있었다. 그녀는 기쁨에 빛나며 탁자 쪽으로 다가왔는데, 마치 어린아이처럼 조바심 어린, 그러면서도 사람을 믿는 호기심으로 무언가를 기대하는 듯이 보였다. 그녀의 눈 속에 깃든 빛은 영혼을 기쁘게 했으니, 알료샤는 그것을 느꼈다. 그녀에게는 그가 이해할 수 없는, 아니 도저히 규정할 수 없을 또 다른 무언가가 있었는데, 그것이 어쩌면 무의식중에 그에게 영향을 미치고 있었다. 그것은 바로 그 부드러움, 그 육체적 동작의 관능, 그 고양이 같은 소리 없음이었다. 그러면서도 그것은 힘차고 풍만한 육체였다. 숄 아래로는 둥글고 넓은 어깨와, 높이 솟은, 아직은 자못 처녀다운 가슴이 비쳤다. 그녀의 몸매는 밀로의 비너스를 연상시키는 선을 지녔으되, 그 비례는 이미 다소 과장되어 있었다. 그것을 미루어 짐작할 수 있었다. 러시아적 미모를 아는 감식가라면 틀림없이 예언할 수 있었을 것이니, 이 싱싱하고 아직 젊은 미모가 서른 무렵이면 그 조화를 잃고 \"퍼져 버릴\" 것이며, 얼굴은 부풀어 오르고, 이마와 눈가에는 곧 주름이 생기며, 안색은 어쩌면 거칠고 불그레해질 것이라는 것을, 요컨대 그것이 한순간의 미모, 러시아 여인들에게서 그토록 흔히 마주치는 덧없는 미모라는 것을 말이다. 알료샤가 물론 이런 것을 생각한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 그는 매혹되면서도, 한편으로는 불쾌한 느낌을 품고서, 말하자면 아쉬운 듯이 의아해했으니, 어째서 그녀는 저런 식으로 말을 늘어뜨리며 자연스럽게 말하지 못할까 하는 것이었다. 그녀가 그렇게 하는 것은 분명, 음절을 과장되게 꿀처럼 굴리는 데 어떤 매력이 있다고 느끼기 때문이었다. 그것은 물론 좋지 못한, 천박한 버릇에 지나지 않았으니, 형편없는 교육과 예의범절에 대한 그릇된 관념을 드러낼 뿐이었다. 그러면서도 이 억양과 말투는 알료샤에게, 어린아이처럼 단순하고 행복한 그녀의 얼굴 표정, 그 눈 속의 부드럽고 천진한 기쁨과는 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 어긋나는 것으로 느껴졌다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 곧 그녀를 알료샤를 마주 보는 안락의자에 앉히고는, 미소 짓는 그 입술에 황홀한 듯 몇 번이나 입을 맞추었다. 그녀는 그녀에게 흠뻑 빠진 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"우리가 만나는 건 이번이 처음이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그녀가 황홀한 듯 말했다. \"저는 이 사람을 알고 싶었어요, 이 사람을 보고 싶었어요. 제가 이 사람을 찾아가려 했는데, 그 바람을 입에 올리기가 무섭게 이 사람이 제게 와 주었어요. 저는 우리가 함께라면 모든 것을, 모든 것을 해결하리라는 걸 알고 있었어요. 제 마음이 제게 그렇게 일러 주었어요. 사람들은 제게 그러지 말라고 말렸지만, 저는 이것이 이 어려움에서 벗어날 길이 되리라는 걸 예감했고, 제 예감은 틀리지 않았어요. 그루셴카가 제게 모든 것을 설명해 주었고, 자기가 어떻게 할 작정인지 다 말해 주었어요. 이 사람은 선의 천사처럼 이리로 날아와 우리에게 평화와 기쁨을 가져다주었어요.\"\n\n\"아가씨께서 저를 업신여기지 않으셨군요, 곱고 훌륭하신 아가씨.\" 그루셴카가 여전히 똑같은 매혹적인 기쁨의 미소를 머금은 채, 그 노래하는 듯한 목소리로 말을 늘어뜨렸다.\n\n\"감히 내게 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요, 이 마녀, 이 요녀 같으니! 내가 당신을 업신여기다니요! 자, 당신 아랫입술에 한 번 더 입을 맞춰야겠어요. 부어오른 것 같은데, 이러다 더 부어오를 거예요, 점점 더, 점점 더요. 저 사람이 어떻게 웃는지 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치! 이 천사를 보고 있으면 마음이 흐뭇해진다니까요.\"\n\n알료샤는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 희미한, 알아차릴 수 없는 떨림이 자꾸만 그의 온몸을 타고 흘렀다.\n\n\"저를 이토록 떠받들어 주시지만, 다정한 아가씨, 저는 어쩌면 당신의 친절을 받을 자격이 조금도 없을지 몰라요.\"\n\n\"자격이 없다니요! 이 사람이 자격이 없다니요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 또다시 똑같은 열기를 띠고 외쳤다. \"있잖아요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리는 변덕스럽고, 우리는 제멋대로이지만, 그 조그만 마음속에서는 자존심 강한 사람들 가운데서도 가장 자존심이 강하답니다. 우리는 고결하고, 우리는 너그러워요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 말씀드리지만요. 우리는 다만 불운했을 뿐이에요. 우리는 어쩌면 자격도 없거나 변덕스러운 사내를 위해 너무도 선뜻 온갖 희생을 다 바치려 했어요. 한 사내가 있었어요, 단 한 사람, 그 역시 장교였는데, 우리는 그를 사랑했고, 그를 위해 모든 것을 희생했어요. 그건 오래전, 오 년 전 일이고, 그는 우리를 잊었어요, 그는 결혼했어요. 지금 그는 홀아비가 되어 편지를 보냈고, 이리로 오고 있어요. 그리고 있잖아요, 우리는 이 모든 세월 동안 그를, 오직 그 사람만을 사랑했어요, 우리는 평생토록 그를 사랑했어요! 그가 올 거예요, 그러면 그루셴카는 다시 행복해질 거예요. 지난 오 년 동안 이 사람은 비참하게 지냈어요. 하지만 누가 이 사람을 나무랄 수 있겠어요, 누가 이 사람의 사랑을 받았노라 자랑할 수 있겠어요? 다만 그 병상에 누운 늙은 상인뿐인데, 그 사람은 이 사람에게 차라리 아버지 같고, 벗 같고, 보호자 같은 사람이에요. 그는 그때 이 사람이 절망에 빠져, 고통 속에서, 사랑하던 사내에게 버림받은 것을 발견했어요. 이 사람은 그때 물에 몸을 던지려 했지만, 그 늙은 상인이 이 사람을 구해 주었어요, 구해 주었어요!\"\n\n\"아가씨께서 저를 참 다정히도 변호해 주시는군요, 다정한 아가씨. 당신은 모든 일에 너무 서두르시는 분이에요.\" 그루셴카가 또다시 말을 늘어뜨렸다.\n\n\"당신을 변호한다고요! 내가 당신을 변호한다고요? 내가 감히 당신을 변호할 자격이라도 된단 말인가요? 그루셴카, 천사, 내게 손을 줘요. 이 곱고 부드러운 작은 손을 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치! 이 손을 보세요! 이 손이 제게 행복을 가져다주었고 저를 일으켜 세워 주었어요. 그래서 저는 이 손에 입을 맞추겠어요, 겉에도, 안에도, 여기, 여기, 여기에요!\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 일종의 황홀경에 빠져, 분명 곱기는 하나 다소 통통한 그루셴카의 손에 세 번이나 입을 맞추었다. 그루셴카는 매혹적이고 음악 같은, 신경질적인 작은 웃음소리를 내며 손을 내밀고는 그 \"고운 아가씨\"를 지켜보았는데, 손에 입맞춤을 받는 것이 분명 흐뭇한 모양이었다.\n\n'어쩌면 너무 지나친 황홀이 아닌가.' 알료샤는 생각했다. 그는 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그는 내내 마음속에 어떤 야릇한 거북함을 느꼈다.\n\n\"저를 부끄럽게 하지 말아 주세요, 다정한 아가씨, 알렉세이 표도로비치 앞에서 이렇게 제 손에 입을 맞추시다니요.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 부끄럽게 하려 한 줄 아세요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 다소 놀라며 말했다. \"아, 그루셴카, 당신은 어쩜 저를 이렇게도 몰라 줄까요!\"\n\n\"네, 그리고 당신도 어쩌면 저를 영 잘못 아시는지 몰라요, 다정한 아가씨. 어쩌면 저는 당신이 보시는 것만큼 그렇게 좋은 사람이 아닐지도 몰라요. 저는 못된 마음을 가졌고, 제멋대로 굴 거예요. 저는 그날 가엾은 드미트리 표도로비치를 한낱 장난삼아 홀려 버렸답니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 당신은 그분을 구해 주실 거예요. 당신은 제게 약속했잖아요. 당신은 그분께 모든 것을 설명해 주실 거예요. 당신은 그분께, 자신이 오래전부터 다른 사내를 사랑해 왔으며 그 사내가 지금 자신에게 청혼하고 있다는 것을 밝혀 주실 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요! 저는 그렇게 하겠다고 당신께 약속한 적 없어요. 그건 당신이 줄곧 말씀하신 거예요. 저는 당신께 약속한 게 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 당신을 잘못 이해한 거로군요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 약간 창백해지며 천천히 말했다. \"당신은 약속했어요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 천사 같은 아가씨, 저는 아무것도 약속하지 않았어요.\" 그루셴카가 여전히 똑같은 명랑하고 천진한 표정으로, 부드럽고 차분하게 말을 가로막았다. \"당신은 곧 아실 거예요, 다정한 아가씨, 제가 당신에 비하면 얼마나 제멋대로인 못된 계집인지를요. 저는 무얼 하고 싶으면 그냥 해 버려요. 어쩌면 방금 당신께 무슨 약속을 했을지도 모르죠. 하지만 지금 다시 이런 생각이 들어요. 저는 어쩌면 미탸에게 다시 끌릴지도 몰라요. 저는 한때 그이를 무척 좋아했거든요, 거의 한 시간 내내 좋아했어요. 어쩌면 지금이라도 가서 그이더러 오늘부터 영영 제 곁에 있어 달라고 할지도 몰라요. 보세요, 저는 이렇게 변덕스럽답니다.\"\n\n\"방금은 당신이, 전혀 다른 말을 했잖아요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 가냘프게 속삭였다.\n\n\"아, 방금은요! 하지만 있잖아요, 저는 이렇게 마음 여리고 어리석은 계집이랍니다. 그이가 저 때문에 어떤 일을 겪었는지 한번 생각해 보세요! 만일 제가 집에 돌아갔을 때 그이가 가엾어진다면 어쩌죠? 그러면 어떻게 되는 건가요?\"\n\n\"저는 전혀 생각지도 못했어요.\"\n\n\"아, 아가씨, 당신은 저에 비하면 어쩜 그리도 착하고 너그러우실까요! 이제 어쩌면 당신은 저 같은 어리석은 계집은 좋아하지 않으실 거예요, 이제 제 됨됨이를 아셨으니까요. 그 고운 작은 손을 제게 주세요, 천사 같은 아가씨.\" 그녀가 다정하게 말하며, 일종의 경의를 담아 카테리나 이바노브나의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"자, 다정한 아가씨, 당신이 제게 하셨던 것처럼 저도 당신의 손을 잡고 입을 맞추겠어요. 당신은 제 손에 세 번 입을 맞추셨지만, 저는 당신과 셈을 맞추려면 당신 손에 삼백 번은 입을 맞춰야 마땅하겠죠. 자, 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고요. 그다음 일은 하느님 뜻대로 될 거예요. 어쩌면 저는 온전히 당신의 노예가 되어, 노예처럼 당신이 시키는 대로 하고 싶어질지도 몰라요. 어떤 약조도 약속도 없이, 그저 하느님 뜻대로 되게 해요. 어쩜 이리도 고운 손일까요, 어쩜 이리도 고운 손을 가지셨을까요! 당신은 고운 아가씨예요, 당신은 믿기지 않을 만큼 아름다운 분이에요!\"\n\n그녀는 천천히 그 손을 입술로 들어 올렸는데, 그 야릇한 속셈은 정말로 입맞춤으로 그녀와 \"셈을 맞추려는\" 것이었다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 손을 빼지 않았다. 그녀는 그 마지막 말들을 수줍은 희망을 품고 들었는데, 비록 노예처럼 자기 뜻을 따르겠다는 그루셴카의 약속이 매우 이상하게 표현되기는 했어도 그러했다. 그녀는 그루셴카의 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다. 그녀는 그 눈 속에서 여전히 똑같은 순박하고 사람을 믿는 표정을, 여전히 똑같은 환한 명랑함을 보았다.\n\n'어쩌면 이 사람은 너무 천진한 거야.' 카테리나 이바노브나는 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 품고 생각했다.\n\n한편 그루셴카는 그 \"고운 손\"에 황홀해하는 듯이 보였다. 그녀는 그 손을 천천히 입술로 들어 올렸다. 그러나 그녀는 무언가를 다시 곰곰이 생각하기라도 하듯, 그 손을 두세 시간이나, 아니 두세 분이나 입술 가까이에 든 채로 멈춰 있었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 천사 같은 아가씨,\" 그녀가 별안간 한층 더 부드럽고 달짝지근한 목소리로 말을 늘어뜨렸다. \"있잖아요, 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 저는 아무래도 당신 손에 입을 맞추지 않는 게 좋겠어요.\" 그러고는 즐거운 듯 작은 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"마음대로 하세요. 무슨 일이세요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 갑자기 흠칫하며 말했다.\n\n\"그래야 당신은 자기가 내 손에는 입을 맞추었지만, 나는 당신 손에 입을 맞추지 않았다는 걸 기억하실 테니까요.\"\n\n그녀의 눈 속에 별안간 어떤 번득임이 일었다. 그녀는 무시무시한 골똘함으로 카테리나 이바노브나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이 뻔뻔한 것!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가, 마치 별안간 무언가를 깨닫기라도 한 듯 외쳤다. 그녀는 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n그루셴카도 일어섰으나, 서두르는 기색은 없었다.\n\n\"그러니까 저는 미탸에게 가서 일러 줄 거예요, 당신은 내 손에 입을 맞추었지만 나는 당신 손에는 도무지 입을 맞추지 않았다고요. 그이가 얼마나 웃어 댈까요!\"\n\n\"이 더러운 갈보 같으니! 썩 나가요!\"\n\n\"아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요, 아가씨! 아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요! 그런 말은 당신께 어울리지 않아요, 다정한 아가씨, 그런 말은요.\"\n\n\"나가요! 당신은 몸을 파는 계집이에요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 비명을 질렀다. 완전히 일그러진 그녀의 얼굴에서는 이목구비 하나하나가 씰룩거리고 있었다.\n\n\"몸을 파는 계집이라고요! 당신도 한때 돈을 받고 어스름녘에 신사들을 찾아다녔잖아요. 당신도 당신의 미모를 팔러 다녔어요. 보세요, 저는 다 알아요.\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나가 날카로운 비명을 지르며 그녀에게 덤벼들려 했으나, 알료샤가 온 힘을 다해 그녀를 붙들었다.\n\n\"한 발짝도, 한 마디도 안 됩니다! 말하지 마세요, 저 여자에게 대꾸하지 마세요. 저 여자는 가 버릴 겁니다, 곧 가 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 카테리나 이바노브나의 비명 소리에 두 고모가 뛰어 들어왔고, 그들과 함께 하녀도 들어왔다. 모두가 그녀에게로 달려들었다.\n\n\"저는 가겠어요.\" 그루셴카가 소파에서 망토를 집어 들며 말했다. \"알료샤, 자기, 집까지 바래다줘요!\"\n\n\"가요, 어서 가요, 서둘러서요!\" 알료샤가 두 손을 모아 애원하듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"귀여운 알료샤, 집까지 바래다줘요! 가는 길에 들려줄 재미난 이야기가 있어요. 저는 이 소동을 당신을 위해서 벌인 거예요, 알료샤. 집까지 바래다줘요, 자기, 나중에 그러길 잘했다 싶을 거예요.\"\n\n알료샤는 두 손을 비틀며 외면했다. 그루셴카는 음악 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 발작을 일으켰다. 그녀는 흐느꼈고, 경련으로 몸을 떨었다. 모두가 그녀를 둘러싸고 부산을 떨었다.\n\n\"내가 경고했잖니.\" 나이 든 고모가 말했다. \"내가 이러지 말라고 말렸잖니. 너는 너무 충동적이야. 어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수 있었니? 너는 그런 계집들을 몰라, 게다가 저 여자는 그중에서도 누구보다 고약하다고들 하더구나. 너는 너무 제멋대로야.\"\n\n\"저 여자는 암호랑이예요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 악을 썼다. \"어째서 저를 붙드셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치? 그러지 않았으면 저 여자를 때려 줬을 텐데, 흠씬 때려 줬을 텐데!\"\n\n그녀는 알료샤 앞에서 자제하지 못했다. 어쩌면 그녀는 정말로 자제할 마음조차 없었던 것이리라.\n\n\"저런 여자는 처형대에 세워 놓고 사람들이 보는 앞에서 매질을 해야 마땅해요!\"\n\n알료샤는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다.\n\n\"하지만, 오, 하느님!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그 사람이! 그 사람이! 그 사람이 그토록 비열할 수가, 그토록 인정머리 없을 수가! 아니, 그 사람이 그 운명의, 저주받은 날에 있었던 일을 저 계집에게 다 말해 버렸어요! '당신은 당신의 미모를 팔러 다녔어요, 다정한 아가씨.' 저 여자가 그걸 알고 있어요! 당신 형은 악당이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\"\n\n알료샤는 무슨 말이든 하고 싶었으나, 한 마디도 찾아낼 수가 없었다. 그의 가슴이 아렸다.\n\n\"가세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치! 부끄러워요, 제게는 끔찍한 일이에요! 내일, 무릎 꿇고 부탁드릴게요, 내일 와 주세요. 저를 나무라지 마세요. 저를 용서해 주세요. 저는 이제 제 자신을 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요!\"\n\n알료샤는 비틀거리며 거리로 걸어 나왔다. 그는 그녀가 그랬듯이 울 수도 있을 것 같았다. 별안간 하녀가 그를 뒤쫓아 왔다.\n\n\"아가씨께서 이 편지를 드리는 걸 깜빡하셨어요. 호흘라코바 부인께서 보내신 편지예요. 점심나절부터 저희가 맡아 두고 있었답니다.\"\n\n알료샤는 그 작은 분홍빛 봉투를 기계적으로 받아 들고는, 거의 무의식적으로 호주머니에 집어넣었다.\n\n# Chapter XI. Another Reputation Ruined\n\n수도원은 읍내에서 채 사 분의 삼 마일도 안 되는 거리에 있었다. 알료샤는 그 시각이면 인적이 끊긴 길을 따라 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다. 거의 밤이나 다름없어, 서른 걸음 앞도 또렷이 분간하기 어려울 만큼 어두웠다. 길 중간쯤에 갈림길이 있었다. 갈림길에 외따로 선 버드나무 아래로 한 사람의 그림자가 눈에 들어왔다. 알료샤가 갈림길에 다다르자마자 그 그림자가 불쑥 튀어나와 그에게 덤벼들며 사납게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"돈을 내놔라, 아니면 목숨을 내놔라!\"\n\n\"아니, 형이었어, 미탸.\" 알료샤가 놀라서 외쳤으나, 그래도 적잖이 가슴이 철렁한 터였다.\n\n\"하하하! 내가 나타날 줄은 몰랐지? 어디서 너를 기다릴까 궁리했단다. 그 여자 집 앞에서? 거기서 길이 셋으로 갈리니, 너를 놓칠 수도 있었겠지. 결국 여기서 기다리기로 했어, 네가 꼭 여기를 지나야 하니 말이야. 수도원으로 가는 길은 이것밖에 없거든. 자, 어서 사실대로 말해 봐. 나를 딱정벌레처럼 짓밟아 다오. 그런데 왜 그래?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니야, 형—형이 깜짝 놀라게 해서 그래. 아아, 드미트리! 조금 전엔 아버지의 피가.\" (알료샤는 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 오래전부터 눈물이 그렁그렁한 상태였는데, 이제 마음속에서 무언가가 툭 끊어진 것만 같았다.) \"형은 아버지를 거의 죽일 뻔했고—저주를 퍼부었고—그런데 지금—여기서—농담을 하고 있잖아—'돈을 내놔라, 아니면 목숨을 내놔라!' 하면서.\"\n\n\"흠, 그게 어쨌단 말이냐? 점잖지 못하다—그 말이지? 내 처지에 어울리지 않는다는 거지?\"\n\n\"아니—난 그저—\"\n\n\"가만있어 봐. 이 밤을 좀 봐. 얼마나 어두운 밤인지, 구름은 또 어떤지, 바람이 어떻게 일었는지 보이지? 나는 여기 버드나무 아래 숨어서 너를 기다렸다. 그런데, 하늘에 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 문득 이런 생각이 들지 뭐냐, 왜 이런 비참함을 더 끌고 가야 하나, 무얼 더 기다린단 말인가? 여기 버드나무가 있고, 손수건이 있고, 셔츠가 있으니, 단숨에 그것들을 비비 꼬아 밧줄을 만들 수 있고, 게다가 멜빵까지 있으니, 어찌하여 이 더러운 존재로 땅을 짓누르며, 땅을 욕보이면서 계속 살아간단 말인가? 그런데 그때 네가 오는 소리가 들렸어—맙소사, 마치 무언가가 갑자기 내게로 날아 내려온 것만 같았다. 그러니까, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 하나는 있는 거야. 바로 여기 그 사람이, 세상 누구보다도 사랑하는, 세상에서 유일하게 사랑하는 나의 사랑하는 동생이 있는 거야. 그 순간 나는 너를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 어찌나 사랑했던지, '당장 저 녀석 목을 끌어안아야지' 하고 생각했단다. 그러다가 어리석은 생각이 떠올랐어, 너한테 장난을 쳐서 깜짝 놀라게 해 주자고 말이야. 그래서 바보같이 '돈을 내놔라!' 하고 소리친 거야. 내 어리석은 짓을 용서해 다오—그저 헛소리였을 뿐, 내 영혼에는 아무런 점잖지 못한 구석도 없으니까…… 에라, 빌어먹을, 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 봐. 그 여자가 뭐라고 하더냐? 나를 후려쳐라, 짓밟아라, 봐주지 마라! 그 여자가 노발대발하더냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 건 아니야…… 그런 일은 전혀 없었어, 미탸. 그게—난 거기서 두 사람을 다 만났어.\"\n\n\"둘 다? 누구를?\"\n\n\"그루셴카가 카테리나 이바노브나 집에 와 있었어.\"\n\n드미트리는 할 말을 잃었다.\n\n\"그럴 리가!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"헛소리하는 거지! 그루셴카가 그 여자랑 같이?\"\n\n알료샤는 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에 들어선 순간부터 일어난 모든 일을 묘사했다. 이야기를 하는 데 십 분이 걸렸다. 그가 유창하고 조리 있게 이야기했다고는 할 수 없으나, 의미 있는 말이나 행동은 하나도 빠뜨리지 않고, 자기 자신의 감정까지 종종 한마디로 생생하게 그려 내며, 사정을 분명하게 전한 듯했다. 드미트리는 무서울 만큼 한곳에 못 박힌 눈길로 그를 응시한 채 잠자코 듣고 있었으나, 그가 모든 것을 이해했고 요점을 빠짐없이 파악했다는 것이 알료샤에게는 분명히 보였다. 그러나 이야기가 진행될수록 그의 얼굴은 단지 음울해지는 데 그치지 않고 위협적으로 변해 갔다. 그는 인상을 찌푸리고 이를 악물었으며, 못 박힌 눈길은 한층 더 굳어지고 한층 더 집중되고 한층 더 무서워졌는데, 그러다 문득, 믿기지 않을 만큼 빠르게, 분노에 찬 그 사나운 얼굴이 싹 바뀌더니, 꽉 다물려 있던 입술이 벌어지며 드미트리 표도로비치가 걷잡을 수 없이 절로 터져 나오는 웃음을 폭발시켰다. 그는 말 그대로 몸을 들썩이며 웃어 댔다. 한참 동안 그는 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"그래, 그 여자가 손에 입을 맞추려 하지 않았다는 거지! 입을 맞추지 않고, 달아나 버렸다는 거지!\" 그는 히스테릭한 환희에 차서 거듭 외쳐 댔는데, 그것이 그토록 절로 우러난 것이 아니었다면 뻔뻔스러운 환희라 불러도 좋았을 것이다. \"그래서 그쪽이 저 여자를 암호랑이라고 불렀다는 거지! 암호랑이가 맞고말고! 그러니 형틀에 묶어 매질을 당해야 한다고? 그래, 그래, 그래야지. 내 생각도 바로 그래. 진작에 그랬어야지. 이런 거야, 동생아, 그 여자를 벌하되, 나는 먼저 나아져야 해. 나는 그 뻔뻔함의 여왕을 잘 알아. 그게 바로 그 여자다워! 너도 그 손에 입맞춤 사건에서 그 여자의 진면목을 송두리째 봤겠지, 그 악녀 말이다! 그 여자는 제 분야에선 정말 대단하다니까! 그래서 집으로 달아났다고? 가야지—아—그 여자한테 달려가야지! 알료샤, 나를 탓하지 마라, 그런 여자한테는 목을 매다는 것도 과분하다는 데 나도 동의하니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는!\" 알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 여자도 보인다, 나는! 이제껏 한 번도 못 했던 것처럼 그 여자 속까지 훤히 꿰뚫어 보인다고! 이건 세계 사 대륙을 발견한 거나 마찬가지야, 아니, 오 대륙을 발견한 셈이지! 그런 짓을 하다니! 그게 바로 카탸다워, 너그러운 충동에 사로잡혀 아버지를 구하겠다고 거칠고 무례한 장교 앞에 마주 서서 치명적인 모욕을 무릅쓰기를 두려워하지 않던 그 카탸 말이야! 하지만 그 오만함, 그 무모함, 운명에 대한 그 도전, 끝 간 데 없는 도전이라니! 이모가 말리려 들었다고 했지? 그 이모란 사람도, 알다시피, 위세 부리기 좋아하는 여자야. 모스크바에 있는 장군 미망인의 동생인데, 그 언니보다도 더 콧대가 높지. 그런데 남편이 관청 돈을 빼돌리다 들켰단다. 영지를 비롯해 전 재산을 죄다 날렸고, 그 오만한 부인도 깃발을 내려야 했고, 그 후로 다시는 올리지 못했지. 그래서 카탸를 말리려 들었지만, 카탸가 들은 척이나 했겠어! 그 여자는 자기가 무엇이든 이겨 낼 수 있다고, 만사가 자기 앞에 굴복할 거라고 믿거든. 마음만 먹으면 그루셴카쯤 홀려 버릴 수 있다고 생각했고, 자기도 그렇게 믿었던 거야. 그 여자는 자기 자신을 향해 연극을 하는 거다, 그게 누구 탓이겠어? 그 여자가 일부러, 어떤 속셈을 품고 먼저 그루셴카의 손에 입을 맞췄다고 생각하느냐? 아니야, 그 여자는 정말로 그루셴카에게 매혹됐던 거다, 그러니까 그루셴카가 아니라 자기 자신의 꿈에, 자기 자신의 환상에 말이야—그게 그 여자 자신의 꿈이고, 그 여자 자신의 환상이었으니까! 알료샤, 얘야, 너는 그 여자들한테서 어떻게 빠져나왔니? 수도복 자락을 걷어 올리고 냅다 달아났냐? 하하하!\"\n\n\"형, 형은 그루셴카에게 그날 일을 떠벌려서 카테리나 이바노브나에게 얼마나 큰 모욕을 주었는지 알아채지도 못한 것 같네. 그루셴카는 조금 전 카테리나 이바노브나의 면전에다 대고, 그 여자가 자기 미모를 팔려고 몰래 신사들을 찾아다녔다고 쏘아붙였다고! 형, 그보다 더한 모욕이 어디 있겠어?\"\n\n알료샤를 무엇보다 괴롭힌 것은, 믿기지 않는 일이지만, 형이 카테리나 이바노브나의 굴욕을 도리어 기뻐하는 것처럼 보인다는 점이었다.\n\n\"이런!\" 드미트리가 사납게 얼굴을 찌푸리고는 손으로 제 이마를 쳤다. 알료샤가 방금 그 모욕에 대해, 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나가 \"당신 형은 비열한 인간이에요!\" 하고 외친 일까지 말해 주었건만, 그는 이제야 그것을 깨달은 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 어쩌면, 정말로 나는 그루셴카에게 그 '운명의 날' 이야기를 했어, 카탸가 부르는 대로 하자면 말이야. 그래, 내가 말했지, 기억나! 그게 모크로예에서였어. 나는 취해 있었고, 집시들이 노래를 부르고 있었지…… 하지만 나는 흐느끼고 있었어. 그때 나는 흐느끼면서, 무릎을 꿇고 카탸의 초상을 향해 기도하고 있었는데, 그루셴카가 그걸 알아챈 거야. 그 여자는 그때 모든 걸 이해했어. 기억나, 그 여자도 울었지…… 빌어먹을! 하지만 이제는 이렇게 될 수밖에 없는 거야…… 그때는 울었지만, 이제는 '가슴에 비수를 꽂는다'는 거지! 여자란 다 그래.\"\n\n그는 고개를 떨구고 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 비열한 놈이야, 철두철미한 비열한 놈이야!\" 그가 문득 음울한 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 울었든 안 울었든 그건 중요하지 않아, 나는 비열한 놈이야! 그 여자한테 전해 다오, 그 이름을 받아들이겠다고, 그게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면. 자, 이제 됐다. 잘 가라. 더 말해 봐야 소용없어! 즐거운 일도 아니고. 너는 네 갈 길로, 나는 내 갈 길로 가는 거다. 그리고 나는 너를, 최후의 수단이 아니고서는 다시 보고 싶지 않다. 잘 가라, 알렉세이!\"\n\n그는 알료샤의 손을 뜨겁게 움켜쥐고는, 여전히 고개를 떨군 채 한 번도 들지 않고, 마치 자신을 억지로 떼어 내듯이 빠르게 읍내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n알료샤는, 형이 그토록 갑작스레 가 버리리라고는 믿기지 않아 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다.\n\n\"가만, 알렉세이, 너한테만 한 가지 더 고백할 게 있다!\" 드미트리가 문득 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"나를 봐. 나를 잘 봐. 여기, 여기—나한테는 끔찍한 치욕이 기다리고 있어.\" (그는 \"여기\"라고 말하면서 묘한 표정으로 주먹을 쥐어 제 가슴을 쳤는데, 마치 그 치욕이 바로 그 가슴 어딘가, 어떤 자리, 이를테면 주머니 속에 들어 있거나 목에 걸려 있기라도 한 것 같았다.) \"이제 너는 나를 알지, 비열한 놈, 자타가 공인하는 비열한 놈이라고 말이야. 하지만 한 가지 말해 두마, 나는 이제껏 단 한 번도, 그리고 앞으로도 결코, 바로 이 순간 내 가슴에, 여기, 여기에 지고 있는 이 치욕에 비길 만큼 비열한 짓은 한 적도 없고 하지도 않을 거다. 이 치욕은 기어이 일어나고 말 거야, 내가 얼마든지 멈출 수 있는데도 말이지. 나는 그것을 멈출 수도 있고 끝까지 밀고 나갈 수도 있어, 명심해 둬. 그래, 내가 말해 두마, 나는 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 거다. 멈추지 않을 거야. 조금 전에 너한테 다 말했지만, 이것만은 말하지 않았어, 나조차도 그걸 입에 올릴 만큼 뻔뻔하지 못했으니까. 나는 아직도 멈출 수 있어. 멈추기만 하면 내일이라도 잃어버린 내 명예의 절반을 고스란히 되찾을 수 있지. 하지만 나는 멈추지 않을 거다. 나는 내 비열한 계획을 끝까지 해치울 거야, 그리고 너는 내가 미리 그렇게 말했다는 걸 증언할 수 있는 거다. 어둠과 파멸이라니! 설명할 필요 없어. 때가 되면 너도 알게 될 테니까. 그 더러운 뒷골목과 그 악녀 말이야. 잘 가라. 나를 위해 기도하지 마라, 나는 그럴 가치가 없어. 그리고 그럴 필요도 없고, 전혀 필요 없어…… 나한테는 그게 필요 없어! 저리 가!\"\n\n그러고는 그가 문득 물러갔는데, 이번에는 정말로 마지막이었다. 알료샤는 수도원 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"뭐? 다시는 형을 못 본다고? 형은 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거지?\" 그는 어지러운 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다. \"아니야, 내일이면 분명히 형을 보게 될 거야. 내가 형을 찾아갈 거야. 꼭 그러고 말 거야. 형은 대체 무슨 뜻으로 그런 말을 한 걸까?\"\n\n그는 수도원을 빙 돌아 솔숲을 가로질러 암자로 갔다. 그 시각이면 아무도 들이지 않는데도, 그에게는 문이 열렸다. 그가 조시마 장로의 암실로 들어설 때 가슴이 떨려 왔다.\n\n\"어찌하여, 어찌하여 장로님은 나를 밖으로 내보내셨던가? 어찌하여 나를 세상으로 보내셨던가? 여기에는 평화가 있다. 여기에는 거룩함이 있다. 그러나 저기에는 혼란이 있고, 자칫 길을 잃고 단번에 헤매게 되는 어둠이 있다…….\"\n\n암실에서 그는 수련 수사 포르피리와 파이시 신부를 만났는데, 파이시 신부는 한 시간에 한 번씩 조시마 장로의 안부를 살피러 오고 있었다. 알료샤는 장로의 병세가 점점 더 나빠지고 있다는 것을 알고 가슴이 철렁했다. 그날은 수사들과 늘 나누던 담화조차 열릴 수 없었다. 평소 매일 저녁 예배가 끝나면 수사들은 조시마 장로의 암실로 모여들어, 그날 지은 죄와 죄스러운 생각과 유혹을, 다툼이 있었다면 그 다툼까지도 모두 소리 내어 고백하곤 했다. 더러는 무릎을 꿇고 고백하기도 했다. 장로는 죄를 사하고, 화해시키고, 타이르고, 보속을 명하고, 축복한 뒤 그들을 돌려보냈다. \"장로 제도\"의 반대자들이 항의한 것이 바로 이 일반적인 \"고백\"이었으니, 그들은 그것이 고해성사를 모독하는 것이며, 비록 실은 전혀 다른 것이긴 했으나, 거의 신성모독에 가깝다고 주장했다. 그들은 심지어 교구 당국에 이런 고백이 아무런 선한 목적도 이루지 못할뿐더러 실제로는 도리어 죄와 유혹으로 크게 이끈다고까지 진정했다. 적잖은 수사들이 장로에게 가기를 꺼렸으나, 모두가 가는 데다 교만하다거나 반항적인 생각을 품었다는 비난을 받을까 두려워, 제 뜻과는 달리 마지못해 갔다. 사람들 말로는, 더러 수사들끼리 미리 입을 맞추어 \"내가 오늘 아침에 너한테 화를 냈다고 고백할 테니, 너는 그걸 맞장구쳐 다오\" 하고 약조했다는데, 그저 무슨 할 말이라도 있어야 했기 때문이었다. 알료샤는 실제로 가끔 그런 일이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 또한 그는 친지들에게서 온 편지를 으레 장로에게 가져가, 수신인이 읽기 전에 장로가 먼저 뜯어 읽는다는 사실에 깊이 분개하는 수사들이 더러 있다는 것도 알고 있었다.\n\n물론 이 모든 것은 자유롭게, 선의로, 자발적인 복종과 유익한 인도의 차원에서 이루어지는 것으로 여겨졌다. 그러나 실상 이 관습에는 때로 적잖은 위선이 있었고, 거짓되고 억지스러운 구석도 많았다. 그래도 더 나이 들고 노련한 수사들은 자기네 의견을 고수하며 이렇게 주장했다. \"진심으로 구원을 찾아 이 담장 안으로 들어온 이들에게는 그러한 순종과 희생이 반드시 유익하고 큰 도움이 될 것이다. 반면에 그것을 성가셔하고 투덜대는 이들은 참된 수도사가 아니며 수도원에 들어온 것이 잘못이니, 그런 자들이 있을 곳은 바로 세속이다. 성전 안에 있더라도 죄와 악마로부터 안전할 수는 없는 법이다. 그러니 그 일을 지나치게 마음에 두어 봐야 소용없다.\"\n\n\"장로님은 더 쇠약해지셨고, 졸음에 빠져드셨네.\" 파이시 신부가 알료샤를 축복하며 그에게 속삭였다. \"깨우기가 어렵네. 그리고 깨워서도 안 되네. 오 분 동안 잠을 깨셨다가, 수사들에게 축복을 보내시고 밤에 자기를 위해 기도해 달라 청하셨네. 아침에 다시 성체를 받으실 생각이라네. 자네를 기억하셨어, 알렉세이. 자네가 떠났느냐고 물으시기에 읍내에 가 있다고 여쭈었지. '나는 그 일을 위해 그를 축복해 보냈으니, 한동안 그가 있을 곳은 여기가 아니라 거기다' 하고 말씀하셨네. 자네에 관한 말씀이 그러했네. 자네를 다정하게, 걱정스러운 마음으로 기억하셨어. 장로님이 자네를 얼마나 귀히 여기셨는지 알겠는가? 그런데 어찌하여 자네가 한동안 세속에서 지내야 한다고 결정하신 걸까? 필시 자네의 운명에서 무언가를 미리 내다보신 게야! 알아 두게, 알렉세이, 자네가 세속으로 돌아간다면, 그것은 자네 장로께서 맡기신 의무를 다하기 위해서이지 경박한 허영이나 세속의 쾌락을 위해서가 아니라네.\"\n\n파이시 신부가 나갔다. 알료샤는 조시마 장로가 비록 하루 이틀쯤 더 사실 수는 있을지라도 임종을 앞두고 있다는 것을 의심치 않았다. 알료샤는, 아버지와 호흘라코바 모녀와 카테리나 이바노브나에게 한 약속에도 불구하고, 이튿날 수도원을 떠나지 않고 마지막까지 장로 곁에 남으리라 굳고도 뜨겁게 결심했다. 그의 가슴은 사랑으로 타올랐고, 임종의 자리에서 수도원에 남겨 두고 온, 세상 누구보다도 귀히 여기는 그분을 단 한순간이나마 잊을 수 있었다는 사실로 그는 자신을 쓰라리게 책망했다. 그는 조시마 장로의 침실로 들어가 무릎을 꿇고 장로 앞에 엎드려 땅에 이마를 대었는데, 장로는 미동도 없이 조용히, 고르게, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 숨을 쉬며 평온한 얼굴로 잠들어 있었다.\n\n알료샤는 아침에 조시마 장로가 손님들을 맞이했던 다른 방으로 돌아왔다. 그는 장화를 벗고, 오래전부터 침대로 써 온 딱딱하고 비좁은 가죽 소파에 누웠으니, 베개 하나 말고는 아무것도 가져다 두지 않은 자리였다. 그날 아침 아버지가 그에게 소리치며 운운하던 그 매트리스는, 그가 그 위에 눕는 것을 잊은 지 이미 오래였다. 그는 덮개로 쓰는 수도복을 벗었다. 그러나 잠자리에 들기 전에 그는 무릎을 꿇고 오랫동안 기도했다. 그 간절한 기도 속에서 그는 자신의 어둠을 밝혀 달라고 하느님께 빌지 않았고, 다만 그의 저녁 기도가 으레 그렇듯 찬미와 경배 끝에 언제나 그의 영혼을 찾아오던 그 기쁨에 찬 감격을 갈망할 뿐이었다. 그 기쁨은 늘 그에게 밝고 평온한 잠을 가져다주었다. 기도를 드리던 중, 그는 문득 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 나설 때 하인이 건네준 작은 분홍빛 쪽지가 주머니에 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 마음이 어수선해졌으나 기도를 마쳤다. 그러고는 잠시 망설인 끝에 봉투를 뜯었다. 그 안에는 그에게 보내는 편지가 있었는데, 그날 아침 장로 앞에서 그를 비웃었던 호흘라코바 부인의 어린 딸 리즈가 서명한 것이었다.\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그녀는 이렇게 썼다. \"저는 아무도, 엄마조차도 모르게 당신께 이 글을 씁니다, 이것이 얼마나 잘못된 일인지 알면서도요. 하지만 저는 제 마음속에 솟아난 이 감정을 당신께 말하지 않고는 살 수가 없어요, 그리고 이건 한동안 우리 둘 말고는 누구도 알아서는 안 돼요. 그런데 그토록 말하고 싶은 것을 어떻게 말해야 할까요? 종이는 얼굴을 붉히지 않는다고들 하지만, 단언컨대 그건 사실이 아니에요, 지금 이 종이도 꼭 지금의 저처럼 온통 새빨개져 있어요. 사랑하는 알료샤, 저는 당신을 사랑해요, 어린 시절부터, 당신이 지금과는 아주 달랐던 그 모스크바 시절부터 당신을 사랑해 왔어요, 그리고 평생토록 당신을 사랑할 거예요. 제 마음은 당신을 골랐어요, 우리 두 사람의 삶을 하나로 합쳐 늙어 갈 때까지 함께 보내려고요. 물론 당신이 수도원을 떠난다는 조건으로요. 나이로 말하자면 법이 정한 때까지 기다리면 되고요. 그때쯤이면 저는 분명히 아주 튼튼해져서, 걷고 춤도 출 거예요. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\n\n저 보세요, 제가 모든 걸 다 생각해 두었지요. 다만 한 가지만은 도무지 그려지지 않아요. 당신이 이걸 읽고 저를 어떻게 생각하실까 하는 거예요. 저는 늘 웃고 까불기만 하잖아요. 오늘 아침엔 당신을 화나게 했지만, 단언컨대 저는 펜을 들기 전에 성모님의 성상 앞에서 기도했고, 지금도 기도하면서 거의 울고 있어요.\n\n제 비밀은 당신 손안에 있어요. 내일 당신이 오면 제가 당신을 어떻게 바라볼 수 있을지 모르겠어요. 아아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 만일 제가 바보처럼 자제하지 못하고 오늘처럼 당신을 보며 웃어 버리면 어쩌죠. 그럼 당신은 제가 당신을 놀리는 못된 계집애라고 생각하고, 제 편지를 믿지 않으실 거예요. 그러니 부탁드려요, 사랑하는 분, 저를 조금이라도 가엾이 여기신다면, 내일 오실 때 제 얼굴을 똑바로 보지 마세요, 만일 당신과 눈이 마주치면 틀림없이 웃음이 터질 테니까요, 더구나 당신이 그 긴 옷을 입고 있을 테니 더욱 그럴 거예요. 그 생각만 해도 온몸이 오싹해지니, 오시거든 한동안 저를 아예 보지 마시고 엄마나 창문 쪽을 보세요……\n\n이렇게 저는 당신께 연애편지를 쓰고 말았네요. 어머나, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요? 알료샤, 저를 경멸하지 마세요, 그리고 제가 아주 끔찍한 짓을 해서 당신께 상처를 입혔다면 용서해 주세요. 이제 어쩌면 영영 망가져 버렸을지도 모를 제 평판의 비밀이 당신 손안에 있어요.\n\n저는 오늘 틀림없이 울 거예요. 우리가 만날 때까지, 그 무시무시한 만남까지, 안녕히.—리즈.\n\n추신.—알료샤! 당신은 꼭, 꼭, 꼭 와야 해요!—리즈.\"\n\n알료샤는 어리둥절해하며 그 쪽지를 읽었는데, 두 번을 거듭 읽고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 문득 부드럽고 다정한 웃음을 지었다. 그는 흠칫했다. 그 웃음이 그에게는 죄스럽게 느껴졌던 것이다. 그러나 잠시 후 그는 또다시 그처럼 부드럽고 행복하게 웃었다. 그는 천천히 쪽지를 봉투에 도로 넣고, 성호를 그은 뒤 자리에 누웠다. 가슴속의 동요는 단번에 가라앉았다. \"하느님, 그들 모두에게 자비를 베푸시고, 이 불행하고 소란스러운 영혼들을 모두 당신의 보호 아래 거두시어 바른길로 인도하소서. 모든 길은 당신의 것이옵니다. 당신의 지혜로 그들을 구원하소서. 당신은 사랑이옵니다. 당신께서 모두에게 기쁨을 보내 주실 것이옵니다!\" 알료샤는 성호를 그으며 이렇게 중얼거리고는, 평온한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n# PART II Book IV. Lacerations Chapter I. Father Ferapont\n\n알료샤는 동트기 전 이른 시각에 깨어났다. 조시마 장로는 잠에서 깨어 몹시 쇠약함을 느끼면서도 침대에서 일어나 안락의자에 앉기를 원했다. 정신만은 아주 또렷했고, 얼굴은 무척 지쳐 보였으나 그래도 환하고 거의 기쁨에 차 있었다. 그 얼굴에는 명랑함과 자애로움, 다정함이 어려 있었다. \"어쩌면 다가오는 하루를 넘기지 못할지도 모르겠구나.\" 그가 알료샤에게 말했다. 그러고는 곧바로 고해를 하고 성체를 받기를 원했다. 그는 늘 파이시 신부에게 고해했다. 영성체를 받은 뒤에는 종부성사가 이어졌다. 수도사들이 모여들었고, 암자는 차츰 은수처에 거하는 이들로 가득 찼다. 그러는 사이 날이 밝았다. 사람들이 수도원에서 찾아오기 시작했다. 예배가 끝나자 장로는 한 사람 한 사람에게 입맞추고 작별을 고하기를 원했다. 암자가 워낙 비좁은 탓에 먼저 온 방문객들은 자리를 비워 주려고 물러났다. 알료샤는 다시 안락의자에 앉은 장로의 곁에 서 있었다. 장로는 할 수 있는 한 많은 말을 했다. 목소리는 약했으나 제법 또렷했다.\n\n\"내가 그토록 여러 해 그대들을 가르쳐 왔고, 그렇기에 그토록 여러 해 소리 내어 말해 왔으니, 이제는 말하는 것이 습관이 되어 버려, 지금 이 쇠약한 처지에서도 입을 다물고 있기가 말하기보다 도리어 더 어려울 지경이라오, 사랑하는 신부님들과 형제들이여.\" 그는 둘러선 무리를 감격에 젖은 눈으로 바라보며 농을 던졌다.\n\n알료샤는 훗날 장로가 그들에게 한 말의 일부를 기억해 냈다. 그러나 장로가 또렷하게 말했고 그 목소리도 제법 한결같았건만, 그 말은 어딘가 두서가 없었다. 그는 여러 가지를 이야기했는데, 죽음의 순간을 앞두고 살아오는 동안 미처 하지 못한 말을 죄다 쏟아 내고 싶어 안달하는 듯했으니, 다만 그들을 가르치려는 뜻에서만이 아니라, 자신의 기쁨과 환희를 만인과 만물과 더불어 나누고, 일생에 한 번 더 자기 가슴 전부를 열어 보이고 싶어 목말라하는 듯했다.\n\n\"서로 사랑하시오, 신부님들.\" 알료샤가 훗날 기억해 낼 수 있는 한, 조시마 장로는 이렇게 말했다. \"하느님의 백성을 사랑하시오. 우리가 이곳에 와서 이 담장 안에 스스로를 가두었다 해서, 우리가 바깥에 있는 이들보다 더 거룩한 것은 아니며, 도리어 이곳에 왔다는 바로 그 사실에서, 우리 한 사람 한 사람은 스스로 다른 이들보다, 이 땅의 모든 사람보다 더 못한 자임을 자신에게 고백한 것이라오……. 그리고 수도사가 은둔 가운데 오래 살수록, 그것을 더욱 절실히 깨달아야만 하오. 그렇지 않다면 그가 이곳에 올 까닭이 없었을 테니. 그가 다른 이들보다 못할 뿐 아니라, 만인 앞에서 모든 것에 대해, 인류의 모든 죄에 대해, 민족의 죄든 개인의 죄든 그 모든 것에 책임이 있음을 깨달을 때, 비로소 우리 은둔의 목적이 이루어지는 것이라오. 사랑하는 이들이여, 알아 두시오. 우리 한 사람 한 사람은 의심할 바 없이 이 땅의 모든 사람과 모든 것에 대해 책임이 있으니, 그저 만물에 깃든 보편적 죄성을 통해서만이 아니라, 각자가 몸소 온 인류와 한 사람 한 사람에 대해 책임이 있는 것이라오. 이 깨달음이야말로 수도사에게나 모든 사람에게나 생명의 면류관이오. 수도사란 특별한 부류의 인간이 아니라, 다만 모든 사람이 마땅히 되어야 할 그것일 따름이기 때문이오. 오직 그 깨달음을 통해서만 우리 마음이 무한하고 보편적이며 다함이 없는 사랑으로 부드러워지는 것이오. 그리하면 그대들 한 사람 한 사람이 사랑으로써 온 세상을 얻고, 그대들의 눈물로써 세상의 죄를 씻어 낼 힘을 갖게 되리니……. 그대들 각자는 자신의 마음을 지켜보며, 끊임없이 자신에게 죄를 고백하시오. 자신의 죄를 두려워하지 마시오, 그것을 깨달았을 때라도, 다만 뉘우침만 있다면, 그러나 하느님과 흥정을 하지는 마시오. 거듭 말하노니, 교만하지 마시오. 작은 자에게도 큰 자에게도 교만하지 마시오. 그대들을 물리치고, 모욕하고, 욕하고, 헐뜯는 자들을 미워하지 마시오. 무신론자들을, 악을 가르치는 자들을, 유물론자들을 미워하지 마시오. 그 가운데 선한 자들만이 아니라, 그들 중에는 선한 자가 많으니, 특히 오늘날에는 더욱 그러하오. 악한 자들조차 미워하지 마시오. 기도할 때 그들을 이렇게 기억하시오. 주여, 그들을 위해 기도해 줄 이가 아무도 없는 자들을 모두 구원하소서, 기도하려 들지 않는 자들 또한 모두 구원하소서. 그리고 이렇게 덧붙이시오. 주여, 이 기도를 드림은 교만에서가 아니오니, 저야말로 모든 사람보다 못한 자이옵니다……. 하느님의 백성을 사랑하시오, 낯선 자들이 양 떼를 빼앗아 가지 못하게 하시오. 그대들이 게으름과 거만한 자존심에 빠져 잠들거나, 더 나쁘게는 탐욕에 빠져 잠든다면, 그자들이 사방에서 몰려와 그대들의 양 떼를 빼앗아 갈 것이니. 끊임없이 사람들에게 복음을 전하시오……. 갈취하지 마시오……. 금과 은을 사랑하지 말고, 그것을 쌓아 두지 마시오……. 믿음을 가지시오. 깃발을 붙들고 그것을 높이 치켜드시오.\"\n\n그러나 장로의 말은 알료샤가 훗날 전한 것보다 훨씬 더 두서가 없었다. 이따금 그는 숨을 고르며 기운을 되찾으려는 듯 아예 말을 끊기도 했으나, 일종의 환희에 잠겨 있었다. 사람들은 감격에 젖어 그의 말을 들었으되, 많은 이들은 그 말에 의아해하며 알아듣기 어렵다 여겼다……. 훗날 모두가 그 말들을 기억해 냈다.\n\n알료샤가 잠시 암자를 나섰을 때, 그는 암자 주위에 몰려든 수도사들 사이에 감도는 전반적인 흥분과 긴장에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 이 기대는 어떤 이들에게서는 불안으로, 어떤 이들에게서는 경건한 엄숙함으로 드러났다. 모두가 장로의 죽음 직후에 어떤 기적이 일어나리라 기다리고 있었다. 그들의 긴장은 어느 한편에서 보자면 거의 경박하다 할 만한 것이었으나, 가장 엄격한 수도사들조차 그것에 물들어 있었다. 파이시 신부의 얼굴이 그 가운데서도 가장 근엄해 보였다.\n\n알료샤는 한 수도사로부터 은밀히 부름을 받아 라키틴을 만나러 갔는데, 그는 호흘라코바 부인이 알료샤에게 보낸 기묘한 편지를 들고 읍내에서 막 도착한 참이었다. 그 편지에서 부인은 알료샤에게 어떤 이상하고도 무척 시의적절한 사건을 알려 왔다. 사연인즉, 전날 조시마 신부의 축복을 받으러 온 여인들 가운데 읍내에서 온 노파가 하나 있었으니, 프로호로브나라는 어느 하사관의 미망인이었다. 그녀는 이르쿠츠크로 떠나 일 년 넘도록 아무런 소식도 보내오지 않는 아들 바센카의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도해도 좋겠느냐고 물었다. 이에 조시마 신부는 엄하게 답하여 그러지 말라고 금하면서, 산 사람을 죽은 사람인 양 위해 기도하는 것은 일종의 주술이라고 말했다. 그 뒤 그는 그녀의 무지를 헤아려 용서하고는, 마치 \"미래의 책을 읽는 듯이\"(이것은 호흘라코바 부인의 표현이었다) 위로의 말을 덧붙였으니, \"그녀의 아들 바샤는 분명 살아 있으며, 머지않아 직접 돌아오거나 편지를 보낼 터이니, 집으로 돌아가 그를 기다리라\"는 것이었다. 그리고 \"이걸 믿으시겠어요?\" 호흘라코바 부인은 열에 들떠 외쳤다. \"그 예언이 그야말로 글자 그대로, 아니 그 이상으로 이루어졌답니다.\" 노파가 집에 채 닿기도 전에, 사람들이 그녀에게 시베리아에서 온, 그녀를 기다리고 있던 편지를 건네주었다는 것이다. 그러나 그뿐만이 아니었으니, 예카테린부르크에서 오는 길에 쓴 그 편지에서 바샤는 어머니에게, 자기가 어느 관리와 함께 러시아로 돌아가는 중이며, 어머니가 편지를 받은 지 삼 주 뒤면 \"어머니를 품에 안기를\" 바란다고 알려 왔던 것이다.\n\n호흘라코바 부인은 알료샤에게 이 새로운 \"예언의 기적\"을 원장 신부와 모든 형제에게 전해 달라고 간곡히 청했다. \"모두, 모두가 이것을 알아야만 해요!\" 그녀는 이렇게 끝맺었다. 편지는 황급히 쓰인 것으로, 쓴 이의 흥분이 줄줄이 배어 있었다. 그러나 알료샤가 수도사들에게 알릴 필요는 없었으니, 이미 모두가 그 일을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 라키틴은 자기 전갈을 가져온 수도사에게 \"더없이 공손히 파이시 신부님께 아뢰어, 자신, 곧 라키틴이 한순간도 미룰 수 없을 만큼 중대한 일을 그분과 의논하고자 하니, 외람됨을 삼가 용서해 주시기를 청한다\"고 일러두었던 것이다. 그 수도사가 알료샤보다 먼저 파이시 신부에게 전갈을 전했던 터라, 알료샤는 편지를 읽고 나서 그 이야기를 뒷받침할 수 있도록 파이시 신부에게 편지를 건네는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없음을 알게 되었다.\n\n그리고 그 엄격하고 신중한 사람조차, \"기적\"의 소식을 읽으며 눈살을 찌푸리기는 했으나, 어떤 내면의 동요를 온전히 억누르지는 못했다. 그의 눈이 반짝였고, 입가에는 진지하고도 엄숙한 미소가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"우리는 더 큰 일들을 보게 될 것이오!\" 그의 입에서 이런 말이 새어 나왔다.\n\n\"우리는 더 큰 일들을, 그보다 더 큰 일들을 보게 될 것이오!\" 둘러선 수도사들이 따라 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 파이시 신부는 다시 눈살을 찌푸리며 그들 모두에게, 적어도 한동안은 그 일을 입에 올리지 말기를 청했으니, \"더 충분히 확증될 때까지는, 이 세상 사람들 사이에는 쉬이 믿어 버리는 풍조가 워낙 많고, 또 실로 이런 일은 자연스럽게 일어났을 수도 있는 것\"이라고, 그는 마치 양심을 달래기라도 하려는 듯 신중하게 덧붙였으나, 정작 자기 자신의 부인을 거의 믿지 않고 있다는 것은 듣는 이들이 아주 또렷이 알아차린 바였다.\n\n한 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 그 \"기적\"은 물론 온 수도원에, 그리고 미사를 드리러 온 많은 방문객들에게까지 알려졌다. 그 누구도, 전날 머나먼 북쪽 옵도르스크의 작은 수도원, 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 온 수도사만큼 그 일에 감명받은 이는 없어 보였다. 전날 호흘라코바 부인 곁에 서서, 부인의 딸이 \"낫는\" 것을 두고 조시마 신부에게 \"어찌 감히 이런 일을 하실 수 있습니까?\"라고 진지하게 물었던 바로 그 사람이었다.\n\n그는 이제 어쩐지 어리둥절하여 누구를 믿어야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 전날 저녁 그는 양봉장 너머 외따로 떨어진 암자에 페라폰트 신부를 찾아갔는데, 그 방문에 크게 감명받고 압도되었던 것이다. 이 페라폰트 신부야말로 단식과 묵언 수행에 그토록 독실한, 앞서 이미 언급된 적이 있는 그 노수도사로서, 조시마 신부와 \"장로\" 제도 전체에 적대적인 인물이었으니, 그는 그것을 해롭고도 경박한 새 풍습으로 여겼다. 그는 묵언 수행 탓에 누구에게도 거의 한 마디도 하지 않았건만, 그래도 만만찮은 적수였다. 그를 만만찮게 만든 것은, 적잖은 수도사들이 그의 감정에 온전히 공감했고, 또 많은 방문객들이 그를 위대한 성자요 고행자로 우러러보았기 때문이니, 비록 그가 미쳤다는 것을 의심하지는 않으면서도 그러했다. 그러나 사람들을 끌어당긴 것은 바로 그 광기였다.\n\n페라폰트 신부는 결코 장로를 보러 가지 않았다. 그는 은수처에 살면서도 그곳의 규율을 지키라는 성가신 요구를 받지 않았으니, 이 또한 그가 미친 사람처럼 행동했기 때문이다. 그는 일흔다섯 살이 넘었고, 양봉장 너머 한 모퉁이에, 오래전 또 다른 위대한 고행자 이오나 신부를 위해 지어진 낡고 허물어져 가는 목조 암자에서 살았는데, 이오나 신부는 백다섯 살까지 살았으며 그 거룩한 행적에 관한 기이한 이야기들이 수도원과 인근에 아직도 숱하게 전해 내려오고 있었다.\n\n페라폰트 신부는 칠 년 전 이 외딴 암자에 스스로 자리 잡는 데 성공했다. 그것은 그저 농부의 오두막에 지나지 않았으나, 예배당처럼 보이기도 했으니, 그 안에는 엄청나게 많은 성상이 모셔져 있고 그 앞에는 등불이 끊임없이 타올랐기 때문이다. 그 성상들은 사람들이 하느님께 바치는 봉헌물로 수도원에 가져온 것이었다. 페라폰트 신부는 그것들을 돌보며 등불을 꺼뜨리지 않도록 임명되어 있었다. 사람들 말로는 (그리고 실제로 사실이었거니와) 그는 사흘에 빵 두 파운드밖에 먹지 않는다고 했다. 양봉장 가까이 사는 양봉지기가 사흘마다 빵을 갖다주었는데, 자기를 시중드는 이 사람에게조차 페라폰트 신부는 좀처럼 한 마디도 건네지 않았다. 빵 네 파운드와, 원장 신부가 주일마다 늦은 미사 뒤에 정기적으로 보내 주는 성체용 빵을 합한 것이 그의 한 주치 양식이었다. 항아리의 물은 날마다 갈아 주었다. 그는 좀처럼 미사에 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 그에게 경의를 표하러 온 방문객들은 더러 그가 하루 종일 주위도 돌아보지 않고 무릎 꿇어 기도하는 모습을 보았다. 그가 그들에게 말을 건넨다 해도, 짧고 퉁명스럽고 기이했으며, 거의 언제나 무례했다. 그러나 아주 드물게는 방문객들과 이야기를 나누기도 했는데, 대개는 도무지 풀 수 없는 수수께끼 같은 기이한 말 한 마디를 던질 뿐, 어떤 간청으로도 그에게 설명의 말을 한 마디 끌어낼 수는 없었다. 그는 사제가 아니라 평범한 수도사였다. 주로 가장 무지한 이들 사이에 떠도는 기이한 믿음이 하나 있었으니, 페라폰트 신부가 천상의 영들과 교통하며 오직 그들과만 대화를 나누기에 사람들에게는 입을 다문다는 것이었다.\n\n옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는, 역시 무척 과묵하고 무뚝뚝한 수도사인 양봉지기로부터 양봉장으로 가는 길을 안내받아, 페라폰트 신부의 암자가 서 있는 모퉁이로 갔다. \"당신이 낯선 사람이니 어쩌면 말을 할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 한 마디도 못 들을지도 모르오.\" 양봉지기는 그에게 이렇게 일러두었다. 그 수도사는 훗날 이야기하기를, 더없는 두려움을 안고 다가갔다고 했다. 저녁도 제법 깊은 무렵이었다. 페라폰트 신부는 암자 문가의 낮은 걸상에 앉아 있었다. 머리 위로는 거대한 고목 느릅나무가 가볍게 살랑거렸다. 공기에는 저녁의 서늘함이 감돌았다. 옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는 그 성자 앞에 엎드려 축복을 청했다.\n\n\"수도사여, 내가 그대에게 절이라도 하기를 바라는가?\" 페라폰트 신부가 말했다. \"일어나게!\"\n\n수도사는 일어났다.\n\n\"축복을, 복 받으시게! 내 곁에 앉게. 그대는 어디서 왔는가?\"\n\n이 가엾은 수도사가 무엇보다 놀란 것은, 그토록 엄격한 단식과 고령에도 불구하고 페라폰트 신부가 여전히 정정한 노인으로 보인다는 사실이었다. 그는 키가 컸고, 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠으며, 야위었으되 싱싱하고 건강한 얼굴이었다. 의심할 바 없이 그에게는 아직도 상당한 힘이 남아 있었다. 그는 다부진 체격이었다. 그 고령에도 머리가 아주 세지조차 않았고, 한때 모두 검었던 머리카락과 수염은 여전히 무척 숱이 많았다. 두 눈은 잿빛에 크고 형형했으나, 놀랍도록 툭 불거져 있었다. 그는 사투리가 짙은 말씨로 이야기했다. 그는 (예전에 일컫던 대로) 거친 죄수복 천으로 지은 농부의 불그스름하고 긴 외투를 걸쳤고, 허리에는 굵은 밧줄을 둘렀다. 목과 가슴은 드러나 있었다. 외투 안으로 보이는, 더없이 거친 삼베로 지은 셔츠는 몇 달째 갈아입지 않아 때에 절어 거의 시커멨다. 사람들 말로는 그가 외투 안에 서른 파운드짜리 쇠사슬을 두르고 있다고 했다. 양말도 신지 않은 발은 거의 다 해져 떨어질 지경인 낡은 슬리퍼에 꿰여 있었다.\n\n\"옵도르스크의 작은 수도원, 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 왔습니다.\" 수도사가 겸손히 답하는 동안, 그의 예리하고 캐묻기 좋아하면서도 자못 겁먹은 작은 눈은 줄곧 은수자를 지켜보았다.\n\n\"그대의 실베스테르에 가 본 적이 있네. 거기 머물곤 했지. 실베스테르는 잘 있는가?\"\n\n수도사는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"그대들은 분별없는 무리로다! 단식은 어떻게 지키는가?\"\n\n\"저희 식단은 옛 수도원 규칙을 따릅니다. 사순절 동안은 월요일과 수요일, 금요일에는 끼니가 마련되지 않습니다. 화요일과 목요일에는 흰 빵에 꿀을 넣어 졸인 과일, 산딸기, 또는 소금에 절인 양배추와 통밀죽을 먹습니다. 토요일에는 흰 양배춧국, 완두콩을 넣은 국수, 카샤를 먹는데, 죄다 삼씨 기름을 칩니다. 평일에는 말린 생선과 카샤를 양배춧국과 곁들여 먹습니다. 성주간에는 월요일부터 토요일 저녁까지 꼬박 엿새 동안 아무것도 익히지 않고, 빵과 물만, 그것도 아껴 먹으며, 가능하면 날마다 먹지 않으니, 사순절 첫 주에 정해진 것과 똑같이 합니다. 성금요일에는 아무것도 먹지 않습니다. 마찬가지로 토요일에도 세 시까지 단식한 다음, 약간의 빵과 물을 들고 포도주를 단 한 잔 마십니다. 성목요일에는 포도주를 마시고 기름을 치지 않은 음식을 익히거나 아예 익히지 않은 것을 먹으니, 라오디케이아 공의회가 성목요일에 관하여 '성목요일에 단식을 풀어 사순절 전체를 욕되게 함은 마땅치 않다!'고 정한 까닭입니다. 저희는 이렇게 단식을 지킵니다. 그러나 거룩하신 신부님, 그것이 신부님에 비하면 무엇이겠습니까.\" 수도사는 더욱 자신감을 얻어 덧붙였다. \"신부님께서는 일 년 내내, 부활절에조차 빵과 물밖에 들지 않으시고, 저희가 이틀에 먹을 것을 신부님께서는 꼬박 이레를 버티시니 말입니다. 참으로 놀랍습니다, 신부님의 그 위대한 절식이.\"\n\n\"버섯은?\" 페라폰트 신부가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"버섯이라고요?\" 어리둥절해진 수도사가 되물었다.\n\n\"그래. 나는 그들의 빵을 끊을 수 있으니, 빵 따위는 전혀 필요치 않아, 숲으로 들어가 거기서 버섯이나 산딸기로 살아갈 수 있네. 허나 그자들은 이곳의 빵을 끊지 못하니, 그래서 악마에게 매여 있는 게야. 요즘 들어 부정한 무리는 그런 단식이 필요하다는 것을 부인하지. 그들의 판단은 교만하고 부정하도다.\"\n\n\"오, 옳으신 말씀입니다.\" 수도사는 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"그래, 그자들 가운데서 악마를 본 적이 있는가?\" 페라폰트가 물었다.\n\n\"그자들 가운데서요? 누구 가운데서 말입니까?\" 수도사가 머뭇머뭇 물었다.\n\n\"작년 삼위일체 주일에 원장 신부에게 갔었는데, 그 뒤로는 가지 않았네. 한 사람의 가슴팍에 악마가 앉아서 수단 자락 밑에 숨어 있는 것을 보았지, 뿔만 삐죽 내밀고서. 또 한 사람은 호주머니에서 악마가 어찌나 날카로운 눈으로 빠끔히 내다보던지, 그놈이 나를 두려워하더군. 또 한 놈은 한 사람의 부정한 뱃속에 들어앉았고, 또 한 놈은 한 사람의 목에 매달려 있어서, 그 사람은 그놈을 알아보지도 못한 채 끌고 다니더란 말이야.\"\n\n\"신부님께서는, 영들을 보실 수 있습니까?\" 수도사가 물었다.\n\n\"내 말하지 않았는가, 나는 볼 수 있어, 그것들을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있지. 원장에게서 나오는데, 한 놈이 문 뒤에 숨어 나를 피하는 것을 보았네, 그것도 큰 놈이, 한 야드 반은 족히 넘는 키에, 굵고 긴 잿빛 꼬리가 달린 놈이었지. 그놈의 꼬리 끝이 문틈에 끼어 있기에, 내가 잽싸게 문을 쾅 닫아 거기에 꼬리를 짓눌렀단 말이야. 그놈이 꽥꽥대며 발버둥치기에, 나는 그 위에 성호를 세 번 그었지. 그러자 짓밟힌 거미처럼 그 자리에서 죽고 말았네. 그놈은 분명 그 모퉁이에서 썩어 악취를 풍기고 있을 텐데도, 그자들은 보지도 냄새 맡지도 못해. 거기 가지 않은 지 일 년이 됐지. 그대가 낯선 사람이기에 이걸 일러 주는 게야.\"\n\n\"신부님 말씀은 무섭습니다! 그러나 거룩하고 복되신 신부님,\" 수도사는 점점 더 대담해지며 말했다. \"먼 땅에까지 신부님에 관하여 떠도는 소문대로, 신부님께서 성령과 끊임없이 교통하신다는 것이 참말입니까?\"\n\n\"이따금 내려오시지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 내려오십니까? 어떤 모습으로요?\"\n\n\"새의 모습으로.\"\n\n\"비둘기의 모습으로 오시는 성령 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"성령(聖靈)이 따로 있고 거룩한 영(靈)이 따로 있네. 거룩한 영은 다른 새들의 모습으로도 나타날 수 있어. 어떤 때는 제비로, 어떤 때는 황금방울새로, 또 어떤 때는 푸른박새로 말이야.\"\n\n\"그것을 보통 박새와 어떻게 구별하십니까?\"\n\n\"말을 하거든.\"\n\n\"어떻게 말합니까, 어떤 언어로요?\"\n\n\"사람의 언어로.\"\n\n\"신부님께 무어라 이릅니까?\"\n\n\"이를테면 오늘은 나에게, 어리석은 자가 나를 찾아와 마땅찮은 질문들을 물어 댈 것이라고 일러 주더군. 수도사여, 그대는 너무 많은 것을 알려 드는구나.\"\n\n\"신부님 말씀은 무섭습니다, 더없이 거룩하고 복되신 신부님.\" 수도사는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다. 그러나 그 겁먹은 작은 눈에는 미심쩍어하는 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이 나무가 보이는가?\" 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 페라폰트 신부가 물었다.\n\n\"보입니다, 복되신 신부님.\"\n\n\"그대는 저것을 느릅나무로 여기겠지만, 나에게는 다른 형상으로 보이네.\"\n\n\"어떤 형상으로요?\" 부질없이 기다리던 끝에 수도사가 물었다.\n\n\"밤이면 그리 되지. 저 두 가지가 보이는가? 밤이면 저것이 그리스도께서 나에게 두 팔을 뻗으시어 그 팔로 나를 찾으시는 모습이라, 나는 그것을 똑똑히 보며 떠노라. 무섭도다, 무섭도다!\"\n\n\"그리스도 친히 오신 것이라면 무엇이 무섭습니까?\"\n\n\"나를 낚아채어 데려가실 테니 말이야.\"\n\n\"산 채로요?\"\n\n\"엘리야의 영광과 영으로 말일세, 듣지 못했는가? 그분께서 나를 두 팔에 안아 데려가시리라.\"\n\n옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는 한 형제와 함께 쓰는 암자로 돌아오면서 마음이 자못 어수선했으나, 그래도 마음속으로는 조시마 신부보다 페라폰트 신부를 더 크게 우러르고 있었다. 그는 단식을 열렬히 지지하는 사람이었으니, 페라폰트 신부처럼 그토록 엄격한 단식을 지키는 이가 \"기적을 본다\"는 것도 이상할 게 없었다. 그의 말이 분명 괴이쩍게 들리기는 했으나, 그 말 속에 무엇이 감추어져 있는지는 오직 하느님만이 아실 일이요, 하느님의 영광을 위해 제 지성을 바친 이들에게서 더 심한 말과 행동이 흔히 보이지 않던가? 악마의 꼬리를 짓눌렀다는 이야기를, 그는 그것도 비유적인 뜻으로만이 아니라 글자 그대로 기꺼이, 또 간절히 믿을 채비가 되어 있었다. 게다가 그는 수도원을 찾아오기 전부터 \"장로\" 제도에 대해 강한 편견을 품고 있었으니, 그것에 관해 그저 풍문으로만 알았고 해로운 새 풍습이라 믿어 왔다. 그는 수도원에 온 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 그 제도를 못마땅해하는 몇몇 천박한 형제들의 은밀한 수군거림을 알아챈 터였다. 게다가 그는 매사에 코를 들이미는, 오지랖 넓고 캐묻기 좋아하는 사람이었다. 그렇기에 조시마 신부가 행한 새로운 \"기적\"의 소식은 그를 극도의 혼란에 빠뜨렸다. 알료샤는 훗날, 옵도르스크에서 온 그 캐묻기 좋아하는 손님이 장로의 암자 안팎에 몰려든 수도사들 사이를 끊임없이 이 무리 저 무리로 옮겨 다니며 귀를 기울이고 질문을 던지던 것을 기억해 냈다. 그러나 그 당시에는 그에게 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 다만 훗날 그것을 떠올렸을 따름이다.\n\n실로 그는 그런 데 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었으니, 조시마 신부가 다시 피로를 느껴 침상으로 돌아간 뒤, 눈을 감으면서 알료샤를 떠올리고는 그를 불러오게 했기 때문이다. 알료샤는 곧장 달려왔다. 암자에는 파이시 신부와 이오시프 신부, 그리고 수련 수사 포르피리 말고는 아무도 없었다. 장로는 지친 눈을 뜨고 알료샤를 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"네 집안 사람들이 너를 기다리고 있느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n알료샤는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"그들이 너를 필요로 하지 않느냐? 어제 누군가에게 오늘 만나러 가겠다고 약속하지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"약속했습니다, 아버지께, 형들에게, 다른 이들에게도요.\"\n\n\"보아라, 너는 가야 한다. 슬퍼하지 마라. 분명히 일러두거니와, 네가 곁에서 내 마지막 말을 듣지 못한 채로는 나는 죽지 않을 게다. 너에게 그 말을 해 주마, 얘야, 그것이 너에게 주는 나의 마지막 선물이 될 게다. 너에게, 사랑하는 아들아, 네가 나를 사랑하기에 그러하다. 허나 이제 가서 네 약속을 지키거라.\"\n\n알료샤는 떠나기가 괴로웠으나 곧바로 따랐다. 그러나 그가 장로의 이 땅에서의 마지막 말을 들으리라는 약속, 그것이 알료샤 자신에게 주는 마지막 선물이 되리라는 약속은 그의 영혼에 환희의 전율을 일으켰다. 그는 읍내에서 해야 할 일을 마치고 어서 돌아오려고 서둘렀다. 파이시 신부 또한 그를 크게 감동시키고 놀라게 한 권면의 말을 몇 마디 건넸다. 그들이 함께 암자를 나서는데 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"젊은이여, 끊임없이 기억하시오.\" 파이시 신부는 서두도 없이 말문을 열었다. \"하나의 거대한 힘이 되어 버린 이 세상의 학문이, 특히 지난 한 세기 동안, 거룩한 책들을 통해 우리에게 전해 내려온 신성한 모든 것을 분석해 왔다는 사실을. 이 잔혹한 분석을 거치고 나니, 이 세상의 학자들에게는 예부터 거룩하던 그 모든 것 가운데 남은 것이 하나도 없게 되었소. 그러나 그들은 부분들만 분석했을 뿐 전체를 간과했으니, 실로 그들의 눈멂은 놀라울 지경이오. 그럼에도 그 전체는 여전히 그들의 눈앞에 굳건히 서 있으며, 지옥의 문이 그것을 이기지 못할 것이오. 그것이 십구 세기를 버텨 오지 않았소? 그것이 여전히 개개인의 영혼 속에서도, 무수한 백성들 속에서도 살아 움직이는 힘이 아니오? 그것은 모든 것을 파괴한 무신론자들의 영혼 속에서조차 여전히 그토록 강하고 살아 있소! 그리스도교를 저버리고 그것을 공격하는 이들조차, 그 가장 깊은 본성에서는 여전히 그리스도교의 이상을 따르고 있으니, 지금껏 그들의 영리함도, 그들 가슴의 열정도, 옛적 그리스도께서 주신 인간과 덕의 이상보다 더 높은 이상을 빚어내지 못했기 때문이오. 그것을 시도했을 때 그 결과란 그저 기괴한 것일 따름이었소. 젊은이여, 이것을 특별히 기억하시오, 그대는 떠나가는 그대의 장로에 의해 세상으로 보내지는 처지이니 말이오. 어쩌면 이 위대한 날을 기억하노라면, 그대는 진심에서 그대의 인도를 위해 들려준 내 말을 잊지 않을 것이오, 그대가 젊고, 세상의 유혹은 크며 그대의 힘으로 견뎌 내기에는 벅찬 까닭이오. 자, 이제 가시오, 나의 고아여.\"\n\n이 말과 더불어 파이시 신부는 그를 축복했다. 알료샤는 수도원을 나서며 그 말을 곱씹다가, 지금껏 자기를 엄하게만 대해 오던 이 근엄한 수도사 안에서, 새롭고도 뜻밖의 벗이자 따스한 사랑으로 가득한 스승을 만났음을 문득 깨달았다. 마치 조시마 신부가 임종에 이르러 그를 파이시 신부에게 물려준 것만 같았으니, \"어쩌면 두 사람 사이에 오간 것이 바로 그것이었는지도 몰라.\" 하고 알료샤는 문득 생각했다. 방금 그토록 뜻밖에 들은 철학적 사색은 파이시 신부 마음의 따스함을 증언하는 것이었다. 그는 소년의 마음을 유혹과의 싸움에 대비해 서둘러 무장시키고, 자신에게 맡겨진 그 젊은 영혼을 자기가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 굳센 방벽으로 지켜 주려 했던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter II. At His Father’s\n\n무엇보다 먼저 알료샤는 아버지에게로 갔다. 가는 길에 그는 어제 아버지가 형 이반의 눈에 띄지 않게 오라고 신신당부했던 것을 떠올렸다. \"어째서일까?\" 알료샤는 불현듯 의아해졌다. \"아버지가 나한테만 따로 할 말이 있다손 치더라도, 어째서 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 들어가야 한다는 것일까? 어제 흥분한 나머지 무언가 다른 말을 하려다 그리 된 것이겠지.\" 그는 이렇게 결론지었다. 그래도 정원 문을 열어 준 마르파 이그나티예브나가(그리고리는 별채에 몸져누워 있다는 것이었다) 그의 물음에 답하여 이반 표도로비치가 두 시간 전에 외출했다고 일러 주었을 때, 그는 무척 반가웠다.\n\n\"그럼 아버지는요?\"\n\n\"일어나서 커피를 드시는 중이지요.\" 마르파가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n알료샤는 안으로 들어갔다. 노인은 실내화에 낡은 외투를 걸치고 식탁에 홀로 앉아 있었다. 그는 무슨 장부 따위를 들여다보며 시간을 보내고 있었으나, 그다지 마음을 쏟지는 않았다. 스메르쟈코프마저 장을 보러 나가, 그는 집 안에 완전히 홀로 있었다. 일찍 일어나 짐짓 대범한 척하려 들었으나, 그는 지치고 약해 보였다. 간밤에 시퍼런 멍이 큼지막하게 솟아오른 이마에는 붉은 손수건이 동여매여 있었고, 코 역시 밤사이 끔찍하게 부어올라 자잘한 멍이 여기저기 얼룩져 온 얼굴에 유난히 심술궂고 짜증스러운 기색을 더하고 있었다. 노인도 그것을 알고 있던 터라, 알료샤가 들어서자 적의에 찬 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"커피가 식었구나.\" 그가 거칠게 소리쳤다. \"너한테는 권하지 않으마. 오늘은 사순절 생선 수프밖에 시키지 않았으니, 누구와도 나눠 먹을 생각이 없다. 무슨 일로 왔느냐?\"\n\n\"안부를 여쭈러 왔습니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 게다가 어제 내가 오라고 일러 두었지. 다 부질없는 일이다. 굳이 수고할 것 없었느니. 그래도 네가 곧장 코를 들이밀고 나타날 줄은 알고 있었다.\"\n\n그는 이 말을 거의 적의에 차서 내뱉었다. 그러면서 동시에 자리에서 일어나 거울 속의 제 코를 근심스레 들여다보았는데(그날 아침 들어 마흔 번째쯤이었으리라), 이마에 동여맨 붉은 손수건도 좀 더 보기 좋게 고쳐 매기 시작했다.\n\n\"빨간 게 낫지. 하얀 걸 매면 영락없이 병원 신세 같단 말이야.\" 그는 자못 점잖은 투로 한마디 했다. \"그래, 그쪽 형편은 어떠냐? 네 장로는 좀 어떠시냐?\"\n\n\"몹시 위중하십니다. 오늘이라도 돌아가실지 모릅니다.\" 알료샤가 대답했다. 그러나 아버지는 듣고 있지 않았으며, 제가 던진 물음조차 곧장 잊어버렸다.\n\n\"이반이 나갔다.\" 그가 불쑥 말했다. \"그놈은 미탸의 약혼녀를 가로채려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있어. 그게 바로 그놈이 여기 눌러앉아 있는 까닭이지.\" 그가 짓궂게 덧붙이고는 입을 비죽이며 알료샤를 바라보았다.\n\n\"설마 형이 아버지께 그렇게 말했을 리가요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 했다, 벌써 오래전에. 믿어지느냐, 삼 주 전에 그렇게 말했다니까? 설마 그놈도 나를 죽이러 온 거라고는 생각지 않겠지? 그놈도 무슨 속셈이 있어서 온 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요? 어째서 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\" 알료샤가 괴로운 듯 물었다.\n\n\"돈을 달라는 건 아니지, 그건 사실이다. 하지만 그래도 내게서는 한 푼도 못 얻어 갈 게다. 내 똑똑히 일러두마, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 되도록 오래오래 살 작정이고, 그러니 한 푼 한 푼이 다 필요하다. 그리고 오래 살면 살수록 그만큼 더 필요해질 게야.\" 그는 누런 무명천으로 지은 헐렁하고 기름때 전 외투 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 방 이 구석에서 저 구석으로 서성거리며 말을 이었다. \"쉰다섯이라도 아직 사내 구실은 할 만하지. 하지만 앞으로 이십 년은 더 사내 구실을 하고 싶단 말이다. 알다시피 늙어 가면 꼴이 볼품없어질 테고, 계집들이 제 발로 찾아오지는 않을 테니, 그때는 돈이 있어야 하지 않겠느냐. 그래서 나는 점점 더 모으는 게야, 오로지 나 자신을 위해서, 사랑하는 아들 알렉세이 표도로비치. 너도 알아 두는 게 좋을 게다. 똑똑히 말해 두마, 나는 끝까지 죄 속에서 살 작정이다. 죄란 달콤한 것이니까. 다들 죄를 욕하지만 다들 죄 속에서 살아간다. 다만 남들은 몰래 하고 나는 드러내 놓고 할 뿐이지. 그래서 다른 죄인 놈들이 다 나한테 달려드는 거야, 내가 그렇게 솔직하다고 말이다. 그리고 네 천국 말이다, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 똑똑히 말해 두지만 내 취향에는 도무지 맞지 않아. 설령 천국이라는 게 있다 한들, 그건 점잖은 사내가 갈 만한 데가 못 돼. 내 생각엔 잠들면 그뿐, 다시 깨어나지 않으면 그만인 게야, 그게 전부지. 네가 원한다면 내 영혼을 위해 기도해도 좋다. 싫으면 말고, 빌어먹을! 그게 내 철학이다. 어제 이반이 여기서 제법 그럴듯하게 떠들어 댔지, 우리가 다들 취해 있긴 했지만 말이다. 이반은 잘난 체하는 멋쟁이지만 별다른 학식은 없어…… 교양도 없고. 그저 잠자코 앉아 말없이 사람을 보고 빙긋이 웃을 뿐이지. 그걸로 그럭저럭 헤쳐 나가는 게야.\"\n\n알료샤는 잠자코 그의 말을 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 그놈은 나하고 말을 섞지 않는 거냐? 어쩌다 입을 열어도 거드름만 피우지. 네 이반은 비열한 놈이야! 나는 마음만 먹으면 당장에라도 그루셴카와 결혼할 수 있다. 알렉세이 표도로비치, 돈만 있으면 무얼 원하기만 하면 그걸 손에 넣을 수 있는 법이거든. 바로 그걸 이반이 두려워하는 게야. 그놈은 내가 결혼하는 걸 막으려고 망을 보고 있어. 그래서 미탸를 부추겨서 제가 직접 그루셴카와 결혼하게 만들려는 거지. 그렇게 해서 나를 그루셴카한테서 떼어 놓으려는 속셈이야(내가 그 여자와 결혼하지 않으면 그놈한테 돈을 남길 줄 알고!). 게다가 미탸가 그루셴카와 결혼해 버리면 이반은 그 부자 약혼녀를 가로챌 테고, 바로 그걸 노리고 있는 게야! 비열한 놈이라니까, 네 이반은!\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 심술이 잔뜩 나셨어요! 다 어제 일 때문이에요. 좀 누우시는 게 낫겠어요.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"옳거니! 네가 그런 말을 하는구나.\" 노인이 마치 처음으로 그 생각이 떠오른 듯 불쑥 말했다. \"그런데도 나는 너한테는 화가 나지 않아. 만약 이반이 그런 말을 했다면 나는 그놈한테 화를 냈을 게다. 너하고 있을 때만은 내게도 좋은 순간이 있단 말이지, 그렇지 않으면 너도 알다시피 나는 심보 고약한 인간이거든.\"\n\n\"심보가 고약하신 게 아니라 비뚤어지신 거예요.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"들어 보아라. 나는 오늘 아침 그 망나니 미탸 놈을 잡아 가두게 할 작정이었다만, 이제는 어찌 결판을 낼지 나도 모르겠구나. 물론 요즘 같은 시류에는 아비 어미를 한낱 케케묵은 편견쯤으로 여기지만, 그래도 법은 제 늙은 아비의 머리채를 끌고 다니거나, 제 집 안에서 아비 얼굴을 걷어차거나, 보란 듯이 아주 죽여 버리겠다고 떠벌리는 짓은 허락하지 않아—그것도 죄다 증인들이 보는 앞에서 말이다. 내가 마음만 먹으면 그놈을 짓뭉개 버릴 수도 있고, 어제 저지른 짓 때문에 당장 잡아 가두게 할 수도 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 고소하실 생각은 없으신 거예요?\"\n\n\"이반이 말렸다. 이반 따위야 나는 아랑곳하지 않다만, 다른 사정이 있어서 말이지.\"\n\n그러고는 알료샤에게 몸을 굽혀 은밀한 반쯤 속삭이는 투로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내가 그 망나니를 감옥에 처넣으면, 그 여자가 그 소식을 듣고 당장 그놈한테 면회를 가려고 달려갈 게다. 하지만 그놈이 이 약하디약한 늙은이를 거의 죽기 직전까지 두들겨 팼다는 소문을 들으면, 그 여자는 그놈을 내치고 내게로 올지도 몰라…… 그게 그 여자 방식이거든, 매사가 거꾸로지. 나는 그 여자를 속속들이 알아! 너 브랜디 한 모금 안 하겠느냐? 식은 커피라도 들어라, 거기다 브랜디를 사분의 일 잔쯤 부어 주마. 기막히게 맛있단다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 괜찮습니다. 괜찮으시다면 저 빵이나 하나 가져갈게요.\" 알료샤가 이렇게 말하고는 반 페니짜리 프랑스 빵을 집어 수도복 주머니에 넣었다. \"그리고 아버지도 브랜디는 안 드시는 게 좋겠어요.\" 그가 노인의 얼굴을 들여다보며 근심스레 권했다.\n\n\"네 말이 옳다. 그건 신경을 가라앉히기는커녕 도리어 곤두서게 하지. 딱 한 잔만이다. 찬장에서 꺼내 오마.\"\n\n그는 찬장을 열고 한 잔을 따라 마신 다음, 다시 찬장을 잠그고 열쇠를 도로 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐다. 한 잔쯤으로야 죽기야 하겠느냐.\"\n\n\"보세요, 이제 기분이 한결 좋아지셨잖아요.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"흠! 나는 브랜디가 없어도 너를 사랑한다만, 비열한 놈들한테는 나도 비열한 놈이 되는 게야. 이반은 체르마시냐에 가지 않아—그건 왜겠느냐? 그놈은 그루셴카가 오면 내가 그 여자한테 얼마나 주는지 염탐하려는 거야. 다들 비열한 놈들이지! 하지만 나는 이반이라는 놈을 도무지 모르겠어, 그놈을 통 알 수가 없어. 대체 어디서 굴러먹다 온 놈인지. 영혼부터가 우리 핏줄이 아니야. 내가 그놈한테 뭐라도 남겨 줄 성싶으냐! 너도 알아 두는 게 좋겠다, 나는 아예 유언장 따위는 남기지 않을 테다. 그리고 미탸 놈은 딱정벌레처럼 짓뭉개 버릴 게야. 나는 밤이면 실내화로 검은 딱정벌레를 으깨 버리거든. 밟으면 짓이겨지면서 찍 소리가 나지. 네 미탸도 그렇게 찍 소리를 낼 게다. 네 미탸 말이다, 네가 그놈을 사랑하니까. 그래, 너는 그놈을 사랑하지. 하지만 나는 네가 그놈을 사랑하는 건 두렵지 않아. 그러나 만약 이반이 그놈을 사랑한다면 나는 내 신상이 두려워질 게다, 그놈이 그놈을 사랑한다면 말이다. 하지만 이반은 아무도 사랑하지 않아. 이반은 우리 핏줄이 아니거든. 이반 같은 인간들은 우리 부류가 아니야, 얘야. 그런 자들은 한 줌 먼지 같은 것이지. 바람이 불면 그 먼지는 사라지고 마는 게야…… 오늘 너를 오라고 했을 때 내 머릿속에는 어리석은 생각이 하나 들어 있었다. 너한테서 미탸에 관해 좀 알아보려고 했지. 만일 내가 지금 그놈한테 천 루블, 어쩌면 이천 루블쯤 쥐여 준다면, 그 거지 같은 놈이 오 년, 아니 그보다 더 좋게는 삼십오 년쯤 아주 멀리 사라져 주는 데, 그것도 그루셴카 없이, 그 여자를 영영 단념하는 데 동의하겠느냐, 응?\"\n\n\"제가…… 제가 형한테 물어보겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"삼천 루블을 주신다면, 어쩌면 형이……\"\n\n\"그건 다 부질없는 소리다! 이제 그놈한테 물어볼 것 없다, 그럴 필요 없어! 내 마음이 바뀌었으니까. 그건 내 어리석은 생각이었어. 나는 그놈한테 아무것도 안 줄 테다, 한 푼도, 내 돈은 내가 쓸 테다.\" 노인이 손을 내저으며 소리쳤다. \"돈 없이도 그놈을 딱정벌레처럼 짓뭉개 버릴 게야. 그놈한테는 아무 말도 하지 마라, 안 그러면 또 헛된 기대를 품기 시작할 테니. 너는 여기서 할 일이 없으니, 더 머물 것 없다. 그 약혼녀라는 카테리나 이바노브나, 그놈이 이제껏 그토록 조심스레 나한테서 숨겨 온 그 여자가, 그놈하고 결혼하겠다더냐 아니더냐? 어제 그 여자를 만나러 갔었다지, 아마?\"\n\n\"그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 형을 버리지 않으실 거예요.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 그러니 그 잘난 아가씨들이 난봉꾼이며 비열한 놈을 어찌나 끔찍이도 사랑하는지 알 만하구나. 내 말하건대 그것들은 가엾은 것들이야, 그 핼쑥한 아가씨들 말이다, 도무지 다른 여자들하고는 딴판이지—아, 내게 그놈 같은 젊음이 있고, 한창때 내 인물이 그대로 남아 있었다면(나는 스물여덟에 그놈보다 인물이 더 훤했단 말이다) 나도 그놈 못지않은 정복의 영웅이 되었을 게야. 그놈은 천한 상놈이지! 하지만 어쨌든 그루셴카만은 못 차지하게 할 테다, 못 차지하게 하고말고! 내가 그놈을 짓뭉개 버릴 테다!\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 그의 노여움이 도로 치솟았다.\n\n\"이제 가도 좋다. 오늘은 여기서 네가 할 일이 없으니.\" 그가 거칠게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n알료샤는 작별 인사를 하려고 다가가 그의 어깨에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"그건 또 무슨 짓이냐?\" 노인이 조금 놀랐다. \"우리는 또 만날 텐데, 아니면 네 생각엔 다시는 못 만날 성싶으냐?\"\n\n\"천만에요, 아무 뜻도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇다, 아무 뜻도 없었어.\" 노인이 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"들어 봐라, 들어 봐.\" 그가 떠나는 알료샤의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤다. \"어서 또 오너라, 그러면 너한테 생선 수프를 해 주마, 오늘 같은 게 아니라 아주 근사한 걸로 말이다. 꼭 와야 한다! 내일 와라, 알겠느냐, 내일!\"\n\n그리고 알료샤가 문밖으로 나서기가 무섭게 그는 다시 찬장으로 가서 또 반 잔을 따랐다.\n\n\"더는 안 마실 테다!\" 그가 목청을 가다듬으며 중얼거리고는, 다시 찬장을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다. 그러고는 침실로 들어가 기진맥진한 채 침대에 드러누웠고, 일 분도 채 못 되어 잠이 들었다.\n\n# Chapter III. A Meeting With The Schoolboys\n\n「그루셴카 이야기를 묻지 않으셔서 천만다행이지.」 알료샤는 아버지의 집을 나서서 호흘라코바 부인의 집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기며 이렇게 생각했다. 「안 그랬으면 어제 그루셴카와 마주친 일을 털어놓아야 했을 테니까.」\n\n알료샤는 어제 이후로 두 싸움꾼이 다시금 기운을 차렸고, 그들의 마음이 또다시 모질어졌다는 것을 가슴 아프게 느끼고 있었다. 「아버지는 악의에 차서 화가 나 있고, 무슨 계획을 세워 놓고는 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나가실 테지. 그렇다면 드미트리는 어떨까? 그도 어제보다 더 모질어졌을 테고, 그도 악의에 차서 화가 나 있을 게 분명하며, 그 역시 틀림없이 무슨 계획을 세워 놓았을 거야. 아, 오늘은 무슨 일이 있어도 기어이 형을 찾아내야 한다.」\n\n그러나 알료샤가 그렇게 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있을 틈은 길지 않았다. 길 위에서 한 가지 사건이 벌어졌는데, 겉으로는 별것 아닌 듯 보였으나 그에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 광장을 가로질러 모퉁이를 돌아 미하일롭스키 거리로 막 접어들었을 때—이 거리는 작은 도랑 하나를 사이에 두고 큰길과 갈라져 있었으니(우리 고을 전체가 도랑으로 가로질려 있다)—그는 다리 곁에서 아홉 살에서 열두 살 사이의 학생 무리를 보았다. 그들은 학교에서 집으로 돌아가는 길이었는데, 더러는 책가방을 어깨에 메고, 더러는 가죽 책가방을 비스듬히 둘러메고 있었으며, 더러는 짧은 윗옷을, 더러는 작은 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 어떤 아이들은 발목 둘레로 주름이 잡힌 그 높은 장화까지 신고 있었는데, 부유한 아버지에게서 응석받이로 자란 사내아이들이 즐겨 신는 것이었다. 무리 전체가 무언가를 두고 열을 올리며 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 보아하니 회의라도 여는 모양이었다. 알료샤는 모스크바 시절부터 아이들을 보면 그냥 지나치지 못하는 사람이었으니, 세 살 안팎의 아이들을 유난히 좋아하기는 했으나 열 살, 열한 살짜리 학생들도 좋아했다. 그리하여 오늘 그토록 마음이 졸이는 와중에도 그는 곧장 옆으로 발길을 돌려 아이들에게 말을 걸고 싶어졌다. 그가 아이들의 상기되고 발그레한 얼굴을 들여다보다가 이내 알아차린 것은, 아이들 모두가 손에 돌멩이를 쥐고 있다는 사실이었다. 도랑 건너편 서른 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에는 또 다른 학생 하나가 울타리 곁에 서 있었다. 그 아이도 옆구리에 책가방을 차고 있었다. 열 살가량 되어 보이는, 창백하고 가냘프게 생긴 아이로, 까만 눈이 반짝거렸다. 그는 다른 여섯 아이를 주의 깊게, 그리고 불안한 눈빛으로 지켜보고 있었는데, 보아하니 그들은 방금 함께 학교에서 나온 동급생들로, 그와 무슨 다툼이 있었던 것이 분명했다.\n\n알료샤는 다가가서, 검은 윗옷을 입은 살결 희고 곱슬머리에 발그레한 사내아이에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"내가 너희 같은 책가방을 메고 다닐 때는 늘 왼쪽으로 메곤 했단다, 오른손을 자유롭게 쓰려고 말이지. 그런데 넌 오른쪽에 메고 있구나. 그러면 가방을 열기가 불편할 텐데.\"\n\n알료샤는 이런 실용적인 말로 이야기를 꺼낸 데에 무슨 기교나 미리 꾸민 속셈이 있었던 것은 아니다. 그러나 어른이 아이와, 더구나 한 무리의 아이들과 단번에 허물없는 사이가 되려면 이것이 유일한 방법이다. 완전히 대등한 처지에 서기 위해서는 진지하고 사무적인 태도로 말문을 열어야 하는 법이다. 알료샤는 그것을 본능으로 알고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 쟤는 왼손잡이인걸요.\" 곱고 건강해 보이는 열한 살짜리 다른 아이가 냉큼 대답했다. 나머지 아이들은 모두 알료샤를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"쟤는 돌멩이도 왼손으로 던져요.\" 세 번째 아이가 한마디 했다.\n\n바로 그 순간 돌멩이 하나가 무리 속으로 날아들었는데, 도랑 건너편에 서 있던 아이가 야무지고 힘차게 던졌는데도 왼손잡이 아이를 살짝 스치고 지나갔을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"한 방 먹여, 되받아쳐, 스무로프!\" 아이들이 모두 소리쳤다. 그러나 왼손잡이 아이 스무로프는 말할 것도 없이 곧바로 앙갚음에 나섰으니, 돌멩이를 하나 던졌으나 그 아이를 맞히지 못하고 땅바닥에 떨어지고 말았다. 도랑 건너편의 아이는 외투 주머니가 돌멩이로 불룩하니 눈에 띄게 부풀어 있었는데, 또 한 개의 돌멩이를 무리를 향해 내던졌다. 이번에는 그것이 알료샤를 향해 곧장 날아와 어깨를 호되게 맞혔다.\n\n\"쟤가 아저씨를 노린 거예요, 일부러 아저씨를 겨눈 거라고요. 아저씨는 카라마조프죠, 카라마조프!\" 아이들이 웃으며 소리쳤다. \"자, 다 함께 한꺼번에 쟤한테 던지자!\" 그러자 여섯 개의 돌멩이가 그 아이를 향해 날아갔다. 한 개가 아이의 머리를 맞혀 그가 쓰러졌으나, 곧장 벌떡 일어나서는 사납게 응사하기 시작했다. 양편이 쉴 새 없이 돌을 던져 댔다. 무리 가운데 많은 아이들도 주머니가 돌멩이로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 짓들이야! 부끄럽지도 않니? 여섯이서 하나를 상대하다니! 이러다 쟤를 죽이겠다.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n그는 앞으로 달려 나가 날아오는 돌멩이를 막아서며 외톨이 아이를 가려 주었다. 서너 명이 잠시 던지기를 멈추었다.\n\n\"쟤가 먼저 시작했어요!\" 빨간 셔츠를 입은 아이가 화난 어린아이의 목소리로 소리쳤다. \"쟤는 짐승 같은 놈이에요, 며칠 전엔 교실에서 크라소트킨을 주머니칼로 찔렀다고요. 피가 났어요. 크라소트킨은 일러바치진 않았지만, 저런 놈은 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 해요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 무엇 때문에? 너희가 쟤를 놀려 댄 거겠지.\"\n\n\"저것 봐요, 쟤가 또 아저씨 등에 돌을 날렸어요, 쟤는 아저씨를 알아요.\" 아이들이 소리쳤다. \"이젠 우리가 아니라 아저씨한테 던지는 거예요. 자, 다 같이 또 한 번 쟤한테, 빗맞히지 마, 스무로프!\" 그러더니 또다시 돌멩이의 응사가, 그것도 무척 사나운 응사가 시작되었다. 도랑 건너편의 아이는 가슴을 맞고 비명을 지르며 울음을 터뜨리더니 언덕 위 미하일롭스키 거리 쪽으로 달아났다. 아이들은 모두 소리쳤다. \"아하, 쟤가 겁을 먹었다, 도망친다. 검불 같은 놈!\"\n\n\"카라마조프 아저씨는 쟤가 얼마나 못된 놈인지 모르세요, 쟤는 죽여도 시원찮을 놈이에요.\" 윗옷을 입은 아이가 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다. 그 아이가 그중 맏이인 듯했다.\n\n\"쟤가 무슨 잘못을 했길래?\" 알료샤가 물었다. \"고자질쟁이냐, 아니면 뭐냐?\"\n\n아이들은 비웃기라도 하듯 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"아저씨도 그쪽으로, 미하일롭스키 쪽으로 가시는 거예요?\" 아까 그 아이가 말을 이었다. \"쟤를 따라잡아 보세요…… 보세요, 쟤가 또 멈춰 섰어요, 서서 아저씨를 쳐다보고 있다고요.\"\n\n\"쟤가 아저씨를 보고 있어요.\" 다른 아이들도 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"쟤한테 물어보세요, 헝클어진 검불을 좋아하느냐고요. 들으셨죠, 그렇게 물어보세요!\"\n\n와르르 웃음이 터졌다. 알료샤는 아이들을 바라보았고, 아이들도 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"가까이 가지 마세요, 다쳐요.\" 스무로프가 경고하듯 소리쳤다.\n\n\"검불 이야기는 묻지 않으마, 너희가 그 질문으로 어떻게든 쟤를 놀려 대는 모양이니까. 하지만 너희가 왜 그렇게 쟤를 미워하는지는 쟤한테 알아봐야겠다.\"\n\n\"그럼 알아보세요, 알아봐요.\" 아이들이 웃으며 소리쳤다.\n\n알료샤는 다리를 건너 울타리를 따라 언덕을 올라가 곧장 그 아이에게로 향했다.\n\n\"조심하는 게 좋을걸요.\" 아이들이 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다. \"쟤는 아저씨를 무서워하지 않을 거예요. 금세 슬그머니 찔러 버릴 거라고요, 크라소트킨한테 했던 것처럼요.\"\n\n아이는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 그를 기다렸다. 가까이 다가가 보니, 알료샤의 눈앞에 마주 선 것은 아홉 살가량 된 어린아이였다. 가냘프고 야윈 데다 창백한 얼굴에, 큼직한 까만 눈으로 그를 원한에 차서 응시하는, 키가 작고 허약한 사내아이였다. 그는 제법 낡고 해진 헌 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 몸이 자라 도무지 맞지 않을 만큼 작아져 있었다. 맨살이 드러난 두 팔이 소맷부리 밖으로 비죽 나와 있었다. 바지 오른쪽 무릎에는 커다란 헝겊 조각이 덧대어져 있었고, 오른쪽 장화는 발끝 바로 그 자리에 가죽이 크게 구멍 나 있었는데, 잉크로 정성껏 까맣게 칠해 놓은 것이었다. 그의 외투 양쪽 주머니는 돌멩이로 불룩하니 묵직해져 있었다. 알료샤는 그의 두 걸음 앞에서 멈춰 서서 묻고 싶은 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다. 아이는 알료샤의 눈빛에서 그가 자기를 때리지 않으리라는 것을 단번에 알아채고는 반항기가 다소 누그러져, 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n\"난 혼자고, 쟤네는 여섯이야. 그래도 나 혼자 쟤네를 다 때려눕힐 거야!\" 그가 별안간 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다.\n\n\"돌멩이 한 개가 너를 심하게 다치게 한 모양이구나.\" 알료샤가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"하지만 난 스무로프 머리통을 맞혔는걸!\" 아이가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"쟤네 말로는 네가 나를 알고 있고, 일부러 나한테 돌을 던졌다던데.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n아이는 음울한 눈으로 그를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"난 너를 몰라. 넌 나를 아니?\" 알료샤가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"날 좀 내버려 둬!\" 아이가 짜증스럽게 소리쳤다. 그러나 그는 무언가를 기다리기라도 하듯 움직이지 않았고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 원한에 찬 빛이 어렸다.\n\n\"좋아, 난 가마.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"다만 난 너를 모르고, 너를 놀리지도 않아. 쟤네가 너를 어떻게 놀리는지 들었지만, 난 너를 놀리고 싶지 않아. 잘 있어라!\"\n\n\"비단 바지를 입은 수도사 놈!\" 아이가 여전히 원한에 차서 반항하는 표정으로 알료샤를 뒤쫓듯 소리치고는, 이제 알료샤가 자기에게 덤벼들 것이 분명하다고 여겨 방어 자세를 취했다. 그러나 알료샤는 돌아서서 그를 한 번 바라보고는 걸음을 옮겼다. 세 걸음도 채 떼기 전에 아이가 주머니에 지니고 있던 가장 큰 돌멩이가 그의 등을 호되게 후려쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 사람을 등 뒤에서 치는구나! 그렇다면 네가 슬그머니 공격한다는 쟤네 말이 사실이었나 보다.\" 알료샤가 다시 돌아서며 말했다. 이번에는 아이가 돌멩이를 사납게 알료샤의 얼굴을 향해 곧바로 내던졌다. 그러나 알료샤는 가까스로 몸을 막을 틈이 있었고, 돌멩이는 그의 팔꿈치를 맞혔다.\n\n\"부끄럽지도 않니? 내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 했다고?\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n아이는 이제 알료샤가 자기를 덮칠 것이 분명하다고 확신하며 말없이 반항적인 자세로 기다렸다. 이제도 그가 덤비지 않는 것을 보자, 그의 분노는 작은 들짐승의 것과도 같았다. 그가 직접 알료샤에게 달려들었고, 알료샤가 미처 몸을 움직일 새도 없이 그 악에 받친 아이는 두 손으로 그의 왼손을 움켜잡고 가운뎃손가락을 깨물었다. 그는 거기에 이를 박아 넣고는 십 초가 지나서야 놓아주었다. 알료샤는 아픔에 비명을 지르며 있는 힘껏 손가락을 빼냈다. 아이는 마침내 손을 놓고 처음 있던 자리만큼 물러섰다. 알료샤의 손가락은 손톱 가까이까지 뼈에 닿도록 심하게 물려서 피가 흐르기 시작했다. 알료샤는 손수건을 꺼내어 다친 손에 단단히 동여맸다. 붕대를 감는 데 꼬박 일 분이 걸렸다. 아이는 그동안 내내 서서 기다리고 있었다. 마침내 알료샤가 온화한 눈을 들어 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"네가 나를 얼마나 심하게 물었는지 봐라. 이만하면 됐지, 안 그래? 자, 이제 말해 보렴, 내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 했니?\"\n\n아이는 어리둥절해하며 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"내가 너를 모르고, 오늘 처음 너를 본 거지만,\" 알료샤가 여전히 차분하게 말을 이었다. \"그래도 내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 한 게 분명해—아무 까닭도 없이 나를 이렇게 다치게 했을 리는 없으니까. 그러니 내가 무슨 짓을 했지? 내가 너한테 어떻게 잘못한 거니, 말해 보렴?\"\n\n아이는 대답하는 대신 큰 소리로 울음을 터뜨리며 달아났다. 알료샤는 미하일롭스키 거리 쪽으로 그의 뒤를 천천히 따라갔는데, 그 아이가 머리도 돌리지 않고 줄곧 있는 힘껏 멀리 달려가는 모습을, 그리고 틀림없이 여전히 울음을 그치지 않고 있는 모습을 한참 동안 바라보았다. 그는 짬이 나는 대로 곧 그 아이를 찾아내어 이 수수께끼를 풀어 보리라 마음먹었다. 다만 지금은 그럴 겨를이 없었다.\n\n# Chapter IV. At The Hohlakovs’\n\n알료샤는 곧 호흘라코바 부인의 집에 다다랐는데, 그것은 이 층짜리 멋들어진 석조 저택으로 우리 고을에서도 손꼽히는 훌륭한 집 가운데 하나였다. 호흘라코바 부인은 대개 영지가 있는 다른 현이나 자기 소유의 집이 있는 모스크바에서 시간을 보냈으나, 우리 고을에도 선조 대대로 물려받은 집을 한 채 가지고 있었다. 우리 군에 있는 영지는 그녀의 세 영지 가운데 가장 큰 것이었으되, 그녀는 이번 일이 있기 전까지는 우리 현에 거의 머문 적이 없었다. 그녀는 현관까지 달려 나와 알료샤를 맞았다.\n\n\"새로운 기적에 대해 쓴 내 편지는 받으셨어요?\" 그녀는 빠르고 초조하게 물었다.\n\"받았습니다.\"\n\n\"모든 사람한테 그걸 보여 주셨나요? 그분이 아들을 그 어머니에게 되돌려 주셨다니!\"\n\n\"그분은 오늘 임종하실 겁니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"들었어요, 알고 있어요, 아아, 이 모든 일에 대해 당신과, 당신이나 누군가와 얼마나 이야기를 나누고 싶었는지 몰라요. 아니, 당신과요, 바로 당신과요! 그리고 내가 그분을 뵙지 못하는 것이 어찌나 안타까운지요! 온 고을이 흥분에 들떠 있어요, 다들 마음을 졸이고 있다고요. 그건 그렇고—저, 카테리나 이바노브나가 지금 여기 와 계신 거 아세요?\"\n\n\"아, 잘됐군요.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"그럼 여기서 그분을 뵐 수 있겠네요. 어제 오늘 꼭 찾아와 달라고 하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"알아요, 다 알고 있어요. 어제 무슨 일이 있었는지도 죄다 들었답니다—그리고 그 끔찍한 계집의 망측한 행실까지도요. 세 트라지크(C'est tragique)예요, 내가 그분 처지였다면 어떻게 했을지 모르겠어요. 그리고 당신 형 드미트리 표도로비치는, 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하세요?—아이고 맙소사! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 깜빡했네요, 글쎄 생각 좀 해 보세요. 당신 형이 지금 그분과 함께 저 안에 있어요, 어제 그 무시무시하게 굴던 끔찍한 형이 아니라, 다른 형, 이반 표도로비치 말이에요, 그가 그분과 함께 앉아서 이야기를 나누고 있답니다. 두 사람이 자못 심각한 대화를 하고 있어요. 지금 그 두 사람 사이에 오가는 일을 상상이나 할 수 있겠어요—끔찍해요, 정말이지 가슴을 찢어 놓는다고요, 무슨 믿을 수 없는 공포담 같다니까요. 두 사람은 누구도 알 수 없는 까닭으로 자기네 인생을 망치고 있어요. 둘 다 그것을 알면서도 그 안에서 도취되어 있다니까요. 나는 당신을 줄곧 기다렸어요! 당신을 애타게 그리워했다고요! 나로서는 도무지 감당이 안 돼요, 그게 무엇보다 견디기 힘들어요. 곧 다 이야기해 드리겠지만, 지금은 다른 이야기를 해야겠어요, 가장 중요한 일을요—내가 가장 중요한 것을 깜빡 잊고 있었네요. 말해 보세요, 리즈가 왜 발작을 일으켰을까요? 당신이 왔다는 소릴 듣자마자 발작을 시작했지 뭐예요!\"\n\n\"마망, 지금 발작을 일으키는 건 어머니지 제가 아니에요.\" 옆쪽 문틈으로 리즈의 목소리가 노래하듯 새어 나왔다. 그녀의 목소리는 웃음을 터뜨리고 싶어 안달이 났으면서도 안간힘을 다해 참고 있는 듯이 들렸다. 알료샤는 그 문틈을 곧바로 알아차렸고, 틀림없이 리즈가 그 틈으로 엿보고 있으련만 그 모습까지는 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"그것도 무리는 아니지, 리즈, 무리는 아니야…… 네 변덕이 나까지 발작을 일으키게 만든다니까. 하지만 이 애가 어찌나 아픈지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 밤새 어찌나 앓았는지, 열에 들떠 신음을 했답니다! 아침이 오기를, 헤르첸슈투베가 오기를 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라요. 그런데 그이는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없다, 기다려 보는 수밖에 없다고만 하지 뭐예요. 헤르첸슈투베는 올 때마다 영문을 알 수 없다는 소리만 한다니까요. 당신이 이 집에 가까이 다가오자마자 이 애가 비명을 지르더니 발작을 일으켰고, 도로 이 방으로 들여보내 달라고 막무가내였답니다.\"\n\n\"엄마, 난 그가 온 줄도 몰랐어요. 그 사람 때문에 이 방으로 들어가고 싶어 한 게 아니라고요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이 아니야, 리즈, 율리야가 너한테 알렉세이 표도로비치가 오고 있다고 알리러 달려갔잖니. 그 애가 너를 살펴 주고 있었어.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 엄마, 그건 조금도 영리한 말씀이 못 돼요. 하지만 그걸 만회하시고 아주 영리한 말씀을 하고 싶으시면, 사랑하는 엄마, 우리의 귀하신 손님 알렉세이 표도로비치께 차라리 이렇게 말씀드리세요. 어제 그런 일이 있었고 다들 그분을 비웃고 있는데도 굳이 우리를 찾아왔으니 분별이 모자란 분이라고 말이에요.\"\n\n\"리즈, 너 너무 지나치다. 정말이지 단단히 야단을 쳐야겠구나. 누가 그분을 비웃는다고 그러니? 나는 그분이 와 주셔서 어찌나 기쁜지 몰라, 나는 그분이 필요해, 그분 없이는 안 된다고. 아아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 더없이 불행하답니다!\"\n\n\"그런데 무슨 일이 있으신 거예요, 사랑하는 엄마?\"\n\n\"아아, 네 변덕 말이다, 리즈, 네 안절부절못함, 네 병, 그 끔찍한 열병의 밤, 그 지긋지긋한 헤르첸슈투베, 끝도 없이, 끝도 없이 되풀이되는 그 사람, 그게 무엇보다 견디기 힘들어! 모든 게, 사실 모든 게 다…… 그 기적까지도 그래! 아아, 그 기적이 나를 얼마나 뒤흔들어 놓았는지, 얼마나 산산조각 냈는지 몰라요, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치! 그리고 응접실에서 벌어지는 그 비극이라니, 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요, 미리 말해 두지만요. 견딜 수가 없다고요. 어쩌면 그건 비극이 아니라 희극인지도 모르겠네요. 말해 보세요, 조시마 장로님이 내일까지 살아 계실까요, 그러실까요? 아아, 하느님! 내가 어떻게 되어 가는 걸까요? 시시각각 눈을 감으면 모든 게 다 부질없다, 죄다 부질없다는 생각만 들어요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 부탁드려도 될지요.\" 알료샤가 불쑥 말을 가로막았다. \"손가락을 동여맬 깨끗한 헝겊 조각을 좀 주신다면 정말 고맙겠습니다. 손가락을 다쳤는데 몹시 아프거든요.\"\n\n알료샤는 물어뜯긴 손가락을 풀어 보였다. 손수건은 피로 흥건히 젖어 있었다. 호흘라코바 부인은 비명을 지르며 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 이게 무슨 상처람, 어쩜 이리 끔찍할까!\"\n\n그러나 리즈는 문틈으로 알료샤의 손가락을 보자마자 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다.\n\n\"이리 오세요, 이리로요.\" 그녀가 위엄 있게 외쳤다. \"이젠 허튼소리 마시고요! 아이고 세상에, 어쩌면 여태 거기 서서 그 얘긴 한마디도 안 하고 계셨대요? 피가 다 빠져 죽을 수도 있었잖아요, 엄마! 어쩌다 이렇게 됐어요? 물, 물요! 무엇보다 먼저 씻어야 해요, 그냥 찬물에 담그고 있으면 아픈 게 가라앉아요, 그대로 담가 두세요, 담가 두시라니까요…… 어서요, 엄마, 세숫대야에 물 좀요. 어서 서두르세요.\" 그녀가 초조하게 말을 맺었다. 알료샤의 상처를 보고 그녀는 적잖이 겁에 질려 있었다.\n\n\"헤르첸슈투베를 부르러 보내야 하지 않겠니?\" 호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"엄마, 나를 죽일 작정이세요. 엄마의 그 헤르첸슈투베가 와서는 또 영문을 알 수 없다고 할 거잖아요! 물, 물요! 엄마, 제발 직접 가서 율리야 좀 재촉해 주세요, 그 애는 어찌나 굼뜬지 빨리 오는 법이 없다니까요! 어서요, 엄마, 안 그러면 나 죽을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 별것 아닌데요.\" 이 호들갑에 도리어 겁이 난 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n율리야가 물을 가지고 달려 들어오자 알료샤는 손가락을 그 안에 담갔다.\n\n\"무명실 좀요, 엄마, 제발 부탁이에요, 무명실하고 그 상처에 바르는 거무튀튀한 부식성 물약도요, 그게 뭐더라? 우리 집에 있잖아요. 그 약병이 어디 있는지 아시죠, 엄마. 엄마 침실 오른쪽 찬장에 있어요, 거기 무명실하고 함께 큰 병이 하나 있다고요.\"\n\n\"금방 다 가져오마, 리즈, 그러니 소리 좀 지르지 말고 부산 떨지 마라. 봐라, 알렉세이 표도로비치가 얼마나 의젓하게 견디고 계신지. 어쩌다 이렇게 끔찍한 상처를 입으셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?\"\n\n호흘라코바 부인은 서둘러 자리를 떴다. 리즈가 기다리던 것이 바로 이것이었다.\n\n\"우선 질문에 대답부터 하세요, 어쩌다 이렇게 다치셨어요?\" 그녀가 알료샤에게 다급히 물었다. \"그러고 나서 전혀 다른 이야기를 할 거예요. 자요?\"\n\n어머니가 자리를 비운 그 시간이 그녀에게 소중하다는 것을 본능적으로 느낀 알료샤는, 학생들과의 그 알 수 없는 마주침을 되도록 짧은 말로 서둘러 들려주었다. 리즈는 그의 이야기에 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"아니 어떻게, 그것도 그런 옷차림을 하고 학생들과 어울릴 수가 있어요?\" 그녀는 마치 그를 다스릴 권리라도 있는 양 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"그런 짓을 하시다니 당신도 한낱 어린애일 뿐이에요, 영락없는 어린애라고요! 하지만 그 고약한 아이에 대해 알아내서 죄다 말해 주셔야 해요, 거기엔 뭔가 수수께끼가 있으니까요. 자, 이제 두 번째 일인데, 그 전에 질문 하나요. 그 아픔 때문에 전혀 대수롭지 않은 이야기를, 그렇지만 진지한 이야기를 나누는 데 지장이 있나요?\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요, 게다가 지금은 별로 아프지도 않은걸요.\"\n\n\"그건 손가락을 물에 담그고 있어서예요. 곧바로 갈아 줘야 해요, 금방 미지근해질 테니까요. 율리야, 지하실에서 얼음 좀 가져오고 물도 한 대야 더 가져와. 자, 그 애가 갔으니 이제 말할 수 있겠네요. 어제 제가 보낸 편지를 돌려주시겠어요, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치—어서요, 엄마가 곧 돌아올 텐데 저는 싫거든요—\"\n\n\"편지를 가지고 있지 않은데요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이 아니에요, 가지고 계시잖아요. 그렇게 말씀하실 줄 알았어요. 그 주머니에 넣어 두셨죠. 밤새도록 그 장난을 후회했어요. 당장 편지를 돌려주세요, 어서 주시라니까요.\"\n\n\"집에 두고 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 어리석은 장난을 했다고 해서 저를 어린애 취급, 계집애 취급 하시면 안 돼요! 그 어리석은 짓에 대해서는 용서를 빌어요, 하지만 편지는 꼭 가져다주셔야 해요, 정말로 가지고 계시지 않다면요—오늘 가져오세요, 꼭, 꼭이요.\"\n\n\"오늘은 도저히 안 돼요, 수도원으로 돌아가야 하거든요, 그래서 앞으로 이틀은—어쩌면 사나흘쯤—당신을 찾아뵙지 못할 거예요, 조시마 장로님 때문에—\"\n\n\"나흘이라니, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소릴! 들어 보세요. 저를 많이 비웃으셨죠?\"\n\n\"전혀 비웃지 않았는데요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"당신이 쓴 말을 다 믿었으니까요.\"\n\n\"저를 모욕하시는군요!\"\n\n\"천만에요. 그걸 읽자마자 그 모든 일이 다 이루어지리라고 생각했어요, 조시마 장로님이 돌아가시면 저는 수도원을 떠나야 하니까요. 그러면 돌아가서 학업을 마칠 거고, 당신이 혼인할 나이가 되면 우리는 결혼하게 되겠죠. 저는 당신을 사랑하게 될 거예요. 아직 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 볼 겨를은 없었지만, 당신보다 더 나은 아내를 찾을 수는 없으리라 믿어요, 그리고 조시마 장로님도 제게 결혼을 하라고 하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 의자에 실려 다니는 불구의 몸인걸요.\" 리즈가 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다.\n\n\"제가 직접 의자를 밀어 드릴게요, 하지만 그때쯤이면 당신은 분명히 나을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 미쳤어요.\" 리즈가 초조하게 말했다. \"장난 하나로 이런 터무니없는 소릴 다 늘어놓다니요! 어쩌면 마침 잘 오셨네요, 엄마. 엄마, 어쩜 그렇게 늘 굼뜨세요, 어떻게 이리도 오래 걸리실 수가 있어요! 율리야도 얼음을 가지고 왔네요!\"\n\n\"아아, 리즈, 소리 좀 지르지 마라, 무엇보다 소리 지르지 말라니까. 네가 그렇게 소리를 지르면 내가 정신이 다…… 무명실을 딴 데 갖다 두니 내가 어쩌겠니? 한참을 찾고 또 찾았다—아무래도 네가 일부러 그런 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 그가 다친 손가락을 하고 올 줄을 제가 어떻게 알았겠어요, 그걸 알았다면 어쩌면 일부러 그랬을지도 모르죠. 사랑하는 엄마, 이제 정말로 재치 있는 말씀을 하기 시작하시네요.\"\n\n\"내 재치 따윈 아무래도 좋다만, 너야말로 알렉세이 표도로비치의 고통에 참 갸륵한 마음씨를 보이는구나! 아아, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나를 죽이는 건 어느 한 가지가 아니에요, 헤르첸슈투베도 아니고, 죄다 한꺼번에 몰아닥치는 거예요, 그게 바로 제가 감당 못 하는 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"이제 그만하세요, 엄마, 헤르첸슈투베 얘긴 그만이에요.\" 리즈가 명랑하게 웃었다. \"어서 무명실하고 물약을 발라 주세요, 엄마. 이건 그냥 굴라르 물약이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이제 이름이 생각나네요, 그래도 아주 훌륭한 물약이랍니다. 글쎄 믿으시겠어요, 엄마, 이분이 오는 길에 거리에서 사내애들하고 싸움을 했고, 어떤 아이가 손가락을 물어뜯었다지 뭐예요, 이분도 어린애지요, 그 자신이 어린애 아니에요? 그러고도 결혼할 자격이 있나요? 글쎄 생각 좀 해 보세요, 이분이 결혼을 하고 싶어 한대요, 엄마. 이분이 결혼한 모습을 한번 상상해 보세요, 우습지 않겠어요, 끔찍하지 않겠어요?\"\n\n리즈는 알료샤를 짓궂게 흘끔거리며 가냘프고 신경질적인 키득거림을 멈추지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 결혼이라니 무슨 소리니, 리즈? 어쩌다 그런 얘길 다 꺼낸 거야? 도무지 격에 맞지 않는 소리로구나—게다가 어쩌면 그 아이가 광견병에 걸렸을지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"어머나, 엄마! 광견병에 걸린 아이가 어디 있어요!\"\n\n\"왜 없겠니, 리즈, 마치 내가 무슨 어리석은 소리라도 한 양 그러는구나! 그 아이가 미친개한테 물렸을지도 모르잖니, 그러면 그 애도 미쳐서 가까이 있는 사람을 닥치는 대로 물어뜯을 거 아니냐. 정말 솜씨 좋게 잘도 동여맸구나, 알렉세이 표도로비치! 나라면 이렇게는 못 했을 거예요. 아직도 아프세요?\"\n\n\"이젠 별로 아프지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"물이 무섭진 않으세요?\" 리즈가 물었다.\n\n\"자, 그만하렴, 리즈, 어쩌면 내가 정말 그 아이가 광견병에 걸렸다는 말을 너무 성급하게 했나 보다, 너는 곧장 그 말꼬리를 물고 늘어지는구나. 카테리나 이바노브나가 당신이 여기 와 있다는 걸 방금 막 들었어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 부랴부랴 내게 달려왔다니까요, 당신을 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 안달이 나서요!\"\n\n\"아이, 엄마, 직접 그분들한테 가 보세요. 이분은 지금 갈 수가 없어요, 너무 아프시니까요.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 얼마든지 갈 수 있습니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 가시겠다고요? 정말 그렇게 말씀하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그분들을 뵙고 나서 다시 이리로 돌아올게요, 그러면 당신이 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 이야기를 나눌 수 있어요. 하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 지금 곧 뵙고 싶어요, 되도록 빨리 수도원으로 돌아가야 해서 마음이 몹시 급하거든요.\"\n\n\"엄마, 어서 이분을 데려가 주세요. 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그러고 나서 굳이 저를 찾아오느라 애쓸 것 없이 곧장 수도원으로 돌아가시는 게 차라리 잘된 일이에요. 저는 자고 싶어요, 밤새 한숨도 못 잤거든요.\"\n\n\"아아, 리즈, 또 장난을 치는구나, 하지만 정말이지 네가 좀 자 주었으면 얼마나 좋을까!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 잘못을 했는지 모르겠네요…… 삼 분만, 원하신다면 오 분만 더 있겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 우물거렸다.\n\n\"오 분씩이나요! 어서 이분을 데려가 주세요, 엄마, 정말 무서운 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"리즈, 너 미쳤구나. 자, 가시죠, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이 애가 오늘은 너무 변덕스러워서요. 비위를 거스를까 봐 겁이 나네요. 아아, 신경이 예민한 여자애를 데리고 사느라 겪는 고생이라니! 어쩌면 이 애가 당신을 보고 나서 정말로 잠들 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 어쩜 이렇게 빨리 이 애를 졸리게 만드셨는지, 정말 다행스러운 일이네요!\"\n\n\"아아, 엄마, 어쩜 그렇게 다정하게 말씀하세요! 그 말씀에 입을 맞춰 드려야겠어요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"나도 네게 입을 맞추마, 리즈. 들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 자못 비밀스럽고 엄숙하게, 빠른 속삭임으로 입을 열었다. \"저는 무언가를 넌지시 일러 주려는 것도, 베일을 들춰 보이려는 것도 아니에요, 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 당신 눈으로 직접 보시게 될 거예요. 정말이지 소름이 끼친답니다. 더없이 황당무계한 익살극이라니까요. 그 애는 당신 형 이반을 사랑하면서도, 당신 형 드미트리를 사랑한다고 자기 자신을 설득하려 갖은 애를 쓰고 있어요. 정말 소름 끼치는 일이죠! 저도 당신과 함께 들어갈 거예요, 그리고 저들이 저를 내쫓지만 않는다면 끝까지 남아 있겠어요.\"\n\n# Chapter V. A Laceration In The Drawing-Room\n\n그러나 응접실에서는 이미 이야기가 끝나 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 단호한 표정이었으되 몹시 흥분해 있었다. 알료샤와 호흘라코바 부인이 들어선 순간, 이반 표도로비치는 작별을 고하려고 자리에서 일어선 참이었다. 그의 얼굴은 다소 창백했고, 알료샤는 근심 어린 눈길로 그를 바라보았다. 바로 이 순간이, 얼마 전부터 알료샤를 줄곧 괴롭혀 온 의혹, 그 성가신 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 것이기 때문이었다. 지난 한 달 동안 그에게는 형 이반이 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하고 있으며, 더구나 그녀를 드미트리에게서 \"빼앗으려\" 한다는 암시가 여러 차례 던져졌다. 바로 얼마 전까지만 해도 알료샤는 그런 생각이 어처구니없게 여겨졌으나, 그래도 그것이 그를 몹시 불안하게 했다. 그는 두 형을 다 사랑했고, 그들 사이에 그런 경쟁이 벌어지는 것을 두려워했다. 그런데 드미트리는 바로 전날, 이반이 자기의 연적이 되어서 기쁘며 그것이 자기, 곧 드미트리에게 크나큰 도움이 된다고 대놓고 말했던 것이다. 그것이 어떻게 그에게 도움이 된다는 말인가? 그루셴카와 결혼하기 위해서? 그러나 그것이야말로 알료샤가 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 나쁜 일로 여기는 것이었다. 이 모든 것 외에도, 알료샤는 바로 어제저녁까지만 해도 카테리나 이바노브나가 드미트리를 향해 변함없고 열정적인 사랑을 품고 있다고 굳게 믿어 왔다. 그러나 그것을 믿은 것도 어제저녁까지였다. 그는 또한 그녀가 이반 같은 사내를 사랑할 수는 없으며, 그녀가 사랑하는 것은 드미트리이고, 그런 열정의 온갖 기이함에도 불구하고 그녀가 드미트리를 있는 그대로 사랑한다고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n그러나 어제 그루셴카와의 그 장면이 벌어지는 동안 또 다른 생각이 그의 머리를 쳤다. 호흘라코바 부인이 방금 입에 올린 \"찢어 발기는\"이라는 말에 그는 거의 흠칫 놀랄 뻔했으니, 그날 밤 동틀 무렵 반쯤 잠에서 깨면서 그가 \"찢어 발기는 고통, 찢어 발기는 고통\"이라고 외쳤기 때문인데, 아마 자기 꿈에 빗대어 한 말이었으리라. 그는 밤새도록 전날 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 벌어진 장면을 꿈꾸었던 것이다. 그런데 카테리나 이바노브나가 실은 이반을 사랑하고 있으며, 다만 일종의 가식 때문에, \"자기 학대\"에서 스스로를 속이고 있을 뿐이고, 무슨 상상 속의 보은의 의무 때문에 드미트리를 사랑하는 척 자신을 괴롭히고 있을 뿐이라는 호흘라코바 부인의 무뚝뚝하고 집요한 단언이 이제 알료샤에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. \"그래,\" 하고 그는 생각했다. \"어쩌면 모든 진실이 그 말 속에 들어 있는지도 모른다.\" 그러나 그렇다면 이반의 처지는 어떻게 되는가? 알료샤는 본능적으로 느꼈다. 카테리나 이바노브나 같은 성격은 반드시 상대를 지배해야 하며, 그녀가 지배할 수 있는 것은 오직 드미트리 같은 사내뿐이고 이반 같은 사내는 결코 아니라는 것을. 드미트리는 끝내 그녀의 지배에 \"자기 자신의 행복을 위해\" 굴복할 수도 있겠지만(그것이야말로 알료샤가 바라 마지않는 바였다), 이반은—아니, 이반은 그녀에게 굴복할 수 없으며, 그런 굴복은 그에게 행복을 주지도 못할 것이었다. 알료샤는 이반에 대해 그렇게 믿지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 이제 이 모든 의혹과 상념이 그가 응접실로 들어서는 동안 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 또 하나의 생각도 그에게 밀려들었다. \"만약 그녀가 둘 중 누구도—이반도 드미트리도—사랑하지 않는다면 어쩐단 말인가?\"\n\n여기서 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 지난 한 달 동안 그런 생각이 자꾸만 떠오를 때마다 알료샤가 자기 자신의 생각을 어쩐지 부끄러워하며 스스로를 책망했다는 점이다. \"사랑이며 여자에 대해 내가 무얼 안다고, 어떻게 그런 문제를 결정할 수 있단 말인가?\" 그런 의혹과 추측 끝에 그는 자책하듯 이렇게 생각하곤 했다. 그러면서도 그것을 생각하지 않기란 불가능했다. 그는 이 경쟁이 형들의 삶에서 엄청나게 중대한 일이며, 거기에 많은 것이 달려 있음을 본능적으로 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n\"한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹을 거다.\" 이반은 바로 전날 자기 아버지와 드미트리를 두고 화가 나서 이렇게 단언했다. 그러니까 이반은 드미트리를 한 마리 독사로 보고 있었고, 어쩌면 오래전부터 그래 왔는지도 몰랐다. 그것이 혹 카테리나 이바노브나를 알게 된 뒤부터였을까? 물론 그 말은 어제 이반이 무심코 흘린 것이었으되, 그렇기에 오히려 더 중대한 것이었다. 그가 그런 심정이라면, 평화의 가망이 어디 있겠는가? 오히려 그들 가문 안에 증오와 적대의 새로운 빌미가 생긴 것은 아닌가? 그리고 알료샤는 그들 가운데 누구의 편을 들어야 하는가? 또 그들 각자를 위해 무엇을 바라야 한단 말인가? 그는 둘 다 사랑했으나, 이렇게 서로 충돌하는 이해관계 한복판에서 각자를 위해 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가? 그는 이 미궁 속에서 영영 길을 잃을 수도 있었고, 게다가 알료샤의 마음은 불확실함을 견뎌 내지 못했으니, 그의 사랑은 언제나 능동적인 성격의 것이었기 때문이다. 그는 수동적인 사랑이라고는 할 줄 모르는 사람이었다. 누군가를 사랑하면 그는 곧장 그를 도우려고 나섰다. 그러려면 자기가 무엇을 겨누고 있는지를 알아야 했고, 각자에게 무엇이 최선인지를 확실히 알아야 했으며, 그것을 확인한 뒤에야 둘 다 돕는 것이 그에게는 자연스러운 일이었다. 그러나 뚜렷한 목표 대신 그가 발견한 것은 사방에 깔린 불확실함과 당혹뿐이었다. 방금 말한 대로 \"그것은 찢어 발기는 고통\"이었다. 그러나 그는 이 \"찢어 발기는 고통\"이라는 것에서조차 무엇을 이해할 수 있단 말인가? 그는 이 당혹스러운 미궁 속에서 첫마디조차 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n알료샤를 본 카테리나 이바노브나는, 벌써 가려고 일어선 이반을 향해 재빠르고 기쁜 목소리로 말했다. \"잠깐만요! 잠깐만 더 계세요! 제가 절대적으로 믿는 이분의 의견을 듣고 싶어요. 가지 마세요.\" 그러고는 호흘라코바 부인을 향해 덧붙였다. 그녀는 알료샤를 자기 곁에 앉히고, 호흘라코바 부인은 맞은편 이반 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"여러분은 모두 저의 벗이에요, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 전부예요, 소중한 벗들이에요.\" 그녀는 고통의 진솔한 눈물로 떨리는 목소리로 따스하게 입을 열었고, 알료샤의 마음은 단번에 그녀에게 누그러졌다. \"알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 어제 그 추악한 장면을 목격하셨고, 제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 보셨어요. 이반 표도로비치, 당신은 못 보셨지만 이분은 보셨답니다. 어제 그가 저를 어떻게 생각했는지는 모르겠어요. 다만 한 가지는 알아요. 만약 그 일이 오늘, 바로 이 순간 되풀이된다 해도, 저는 어제와 똑같은 감정을 다시 드러낼 거예요—똑같은 감정, 똑같은 말, 똑같은 행동을요. 제 행동을 기억하시죠, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신이 그중 하나를 막아 주셨잖아요…….\" (그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔고 두 눈이 반짝였다.) \"솔직히 말해 저는 그 일을 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요. 들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저는 제가 아직도 그를 사랑하는지조차 모르겠어요. 저는 그가 가엾어요. 그런데 그건 사랑의 보잘것없는 증표지요. 만약 제가 그를 사랑했다면, 아직도 사랑한다면, 어쩌면 지금 그를 가엾어하지 않고 미워했을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n그녀의 목소리가 떨렸고 눈물이 속눈썹 위에서 반짝였다. 알료샤는 속으로 몸서리쳤다. \"저 아가씨는 진실하고 진솔하다.\" 그는 생각했다. \"그리고 더는 드미트리를 사랑하지 않는다.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 부인. 아직 제가 가장 중요한 것, 간밤에 다다른 마지막 결심을 말씀드리지 않았어요. 어쩌면 제 결심이 끔찍한 것일지도 몰라요—저 자신에게는요. 하지만 저는 그 무엇으로도 제 마음을 돌릴 수 없으리라는 걸 예감하고 있어요—그 무엇으로도요. 평생 이대로일 거예요. 제 소중하고 다정하며, 늘 충실하고 너그러운 조언자, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 단 하나의 벗, 마음속을 깊이 꿰뚫어 보시는 이반 표도로비치께서 제 결심을 인정하고 칭찬해 주셨어요. 이분은 알고 계세요.\"\n\n\"네, 저는 그것을 인정합니다.\" 이반이 낮으나 단호한 목소리로 동의했다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 알료샤에게도(아! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 그냥 알료샤라고 부르는 걸 용서하세요), 알렉세이 표도로비치께도 제 두 벗 앞에서 제가 옳은지 말씀해 주십사 부탁드리고 싶어요. 저는 본능적으로 느껴요. 당신이, 알료샤, 제 소중한 동생이(당신은 제게 소중한 동생이니까요),\" 그녀는 다시 황홀하게 말하며 자기의 뜨거운 손으로 그의 차가운 손을 잡았다. \"당신의 결정, 당신의 인정이 제 온갖 고통에도 불구하고 제게 평화를 가져다주리라는 걸 예감해요. 당신의 말씀을 듣고 나면 저는 차분해지고 순순히 따를 거예요—그렇게 느껴져요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 제게 무엇을 묻고 계신지 모르겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"다만 제가 당신을 사랑하며, 이 순간 제 행복보다 당신의 행복을 더 바라고 있다는 것만은 압니다!…… 하지만 저는 그런 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다.\" 무언가에 떠밀린 듯 그가 황급히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그런 일에서는, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그런 일에서 가장 중요한 것은 명예와 의무, 그리고 무언가 더 높은 것이에요—그게 무언지는 모르겠지만—어쩌면 의무보다도 더 높은 것이지요. 저는 제 마음속에서 이 거역할 수 없는 감정을 느끼고, 그것이 저를 거역할 수 없이 떠밀어요. 하지만 그건 다 한두 마디로 말할 수 있어요. 저는 이미 결심했어요. 그가 그—여자와 결혼한다 해도,\" 그녀는 엄숙하게 입을 열었다. \"제가 결코, 결코 용서할 수 없는 그 여자와 결혼한다 해도, 그래도 저는 그를 버리지 않겠어요. 이제부터 저는 결코, 결코 그를 버리지 않겠어요!\" 그녀는 일종의 창백하고 발작적인 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤다. \"그렇다고 제가 줄곧 그를 쫓아다니거나, 그의 앞길을 가로막거나 괴롭히겠다는 게 아니에요. 오, 아니에요! 저는 다른 도시로—당신이 원하시는 곳으로 떠나겠어요—하지만 평생토록 그를 지켜보겠어요—한평생 끊임없이 그를 지켜보겠어요. 그가 그 여자와 불행해지면, 그건 머지않아 반드시 일어날 일이지만, 그가 제게 오면 그는 한 사람의 벗을, 누이를 발견하게 될 거예요…… 물론 그저 누이일 뿐이지요, 그리고 영원히 그럴 거예요. 하지만 적어도 그는 그 누이가 진정으로 자기의 누이이며, 그를 사랑하고 그에게 일평생을 바쳤다는 것을 알게 될 거예요. 저는 제 뜻을 이루고야 말겠어요. 그가 저를 알아주고, 거리낌 없이 온전히 제게 모든 걸 털어놓도록 기어이 만들겠어요.\" 그녀는 일종의 광란에 빠져 외쳤다. \"저는 그가 기도드릴 수 있는 신이 되겠어요—그리고 적어도 그것은 그가 저를 배신한 대가로, 어제 그 때문에 제가 겪은 고통의 대가로 제게 갚아야 할 빚이에요. 그리고 그가 보게 하겠어요. 그가 거짓되고 저를 배신했음에도, 제가 평생토록 그에게, 그리고 그에게 한 약속에 충실하리라는 것을요. 저는—저는 그의 행복을 위한 한낱 수단이, 아니면—뭐라 해야 할까요?—그의 행복을 위한 도구, 기계가 되겠어요. 그것도 제 평생, 제 한평생을요. 그리고 그가 그것을 평생 보게 하겠어요! 그게 제 결심이에요. 이반 표도로비치께서도 저를 전적으로 인정해 주셨어요.\"\n\n그녀는 숨이 가빴다. 어쩌면 그녀는 자기 생각을 좀 더 품위 있고 정교하고 자연스럽게 표현하려 했을지 모르나, 그 말은 너무 황급하고 거칠었다. 거기에는 젊은이다운 충동이 가득 차 있었고, 그녀가 아직 어제의 모욕에 쓰라려하고 있으며 그녀의 자존심이 만족을 갈망하고 있음이 드러났다. 그녀 자신도 그것을 느꼈다. 그녀의 얼굴이 별안간 어두워졌고, 눈에 불쾌한 기색이 떠올랐다. 알료샤는 곧장 그것을 알아채고 연민의 통증을 느꼈다. 그의 형 이반이 이렇게 덧붙여 사태를 더 악화시켰다.\n\n\"저는 다만 제 견해를 말했을 뿐입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"다른 누가 그랬다면 이건 꾸민 것이고 과장된 것이겠지요. 하지만 당신이 그러니—그렇지 않습니다. 다른 어떤 여자라면 틀렸을 테지만, 당신은 옳습니다. 어떻게 설명해야 할지 모르겠으나, 저는 당신이 더없이 진실하다는 것을, 그러므로 옳다는 것을 봅니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 그저 순간일 뿐이에요. 그리고 이 순간이라는 게 대체 무엇을 의미하나요? 어제의 모욕 말고는 아무것도 아니지요.\" 호흘라코바 부인은 분명 끼어들 생각이 없었으나, 이 더없이 옳은 한마디만은 참지 못했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 바로 그래요.\" 이반이 유난한 열의로 외쳤는데, 분명 말을 가로막힌 데에 짜증이 난 듯했다. \"다른 누구였다면 이 순간은 어제의 인상에서 비롯된 것에 불과했을 테고, 그저 한순간으로 끝났을 겁니다. 하지만 카테리나 이바노브나의 성격으로 보면 그 순간은 평생토록 지속될 겁니다. 다른 누구에게라면 한낱 약속에 지나지 않을 것이, 그녀에게는 영원토록 무겁고, 어쩌면 음울하지만 결코 시들지 않는 의무가 됩니다. 그리고 그녀는 그 의무를 다하고 있다는 느낌으로 지탱될 겁니다. 카테리나 이바노브나, 당신의 삶은 이제부터 당신 자신의 감정, 당신 자신의 영웅적 행위, 당신 자신의 고통을 괴롭게 곱씹는 데 바쳐질 겁니다. 하지만 끝내 그 고통은 누그러져, 대담하고 자랑스러운 결심을 완수했다는 달콤한 관조 속으로 옮아갈 겁니다. 그래요, 그것은 분명 자랑스러운 것이고, 아무튼 절망적인 것이지만, 당신에게는 하나의 승리입니다. 그리고 그것을 자각하는 것이 마침내 완전한 만족의 원천이 되어, 다른 모든 것을 체념하게 만들 겁니다.\"\n\n이것은 틀림없이 얼마간의 악의를 담아, 그리고 분명 의도적으로 한 말이었다. 어쩌면 자기가 비꼬듯이, 의도적으로 말하고 있음을 굳이 감추려 들지 않은 것이기도 했다.\n\n\"아아, 이게 다 얼마나 잘못된 건지!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 말씀해 주세요. 당신이 무슨 말씀을 하실지 너무나 알고 싶어요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외치더니 울음을 터뜨렸다. 알료샤는 소파에서 일어났다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도!\" 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 말을 이었다. \"제가 마음이 어지러워요, 간밤에 잠을 못 잤거든요. 하지만 당신과 당신 형님 같은 두 벗이 곁에 있으니 저는 그래도 강해진 걸 느껴요—두 분이 결코 저를 버리지 않으리라는 걸 아니까요.\"\n\n\"공교롭게도 저는 모스크바로 돌아가야 합니다—어쩌면 내일이라도—그래서 오랫동안 당신 곁을 떠나야 합니다—그리고 공교롭게도, 그건 피할 수 없는 일입니다.\" 이반이 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"내일—모스크바로요!\" 그녀의 얼굴이 별안간 일그러졌다. \"하지만—하지만, 어머나, 정말 잘됐네요!\" 그녀는 별안간 달라진 목소리로 외쳤다. 한순간에 그녀의 눈물은 흔적도 없이 사라졌다. 그녀는 순식간에 돌변했고, 그것이 알료샤를 놀라게 했다. 일종의 \"찢어 발기는 고통\" 속에서 우는, 가엾고 모욕당한 처녀 대신, 그는 무언가 흐뭇한 일이 막 벌어지기라도 한 듯 완전히 침착하고 심지어 더없이 기뻐하는 한 여자를 보았다.\n\n\"오, 물론 당신을 잃는 게 잘됐다는 건 아니에요.\" 그녀는 매혹적인 사교계의 미소를 띠며 불쑥 말을 고쳤다. \"당신 같은 벗이 그렇게 짐작하실 리 없지요. 저는 당신을 잃는 게 너무나 슬프답니다.\" 그녀는 충동적으로 이반에게 달려들어 그의 두 손을 잡고 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐었다. \"하지만 잘된 건, 당신이 모스크바에서 아주머니와 아가피야를 뵙고 제 지금 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 다 말씀드릴 수 있으리라는 거예요. 아가피야에게는 아주 솔직하게 말씀하셔도 되지만, 소중한 아주머니께는 좀 봐주셔야 해요. 당신은 그렇게 하실 줄 아시죠. 어제와 오늘 아침 제가 그 끔찍한 편지를 어떻게 써야 하나 고민하느라 얼마나 비참했는지 당신은 모르실 거예요—그런 일은 편지로는 도저히 전할 수가 없으니까요…… 이제 저는 편지 쓰기가 쉬워졌어요. 당신이 그분들을 만나 다 설명해 주실 테니까요. 오, 정말 기뻐요! 하지만 저는 그것만으로 기쁜 거예요, 믿어 주세요. 물론 그 누구도 당신을 대신할 수는 없어요…… 곧장 가서 편지를 쓰겠어요.\" 그녀는 불쑥 말을 맺고는 방을 나가려는 듯 한 걸음을 내디뎠다.\n\n\"그런데 그토록 애타게 듣고 싶어 하시던 알료샤의 의견은 어떻게 되나요?\" 호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다. 그녀의 목소리에는 비꼬는, 성난 가락이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"그걸 잊은 게 아니에요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 별안간 우뚝 멈추어 서며 외쳤다. \"그런데 부인은 어째서 이런 순간에 그토록 적대적이세요?\" 그녀는 뜨겁고도 쓰라린 책망을 담아 덧붙였다. \"제가 한 말은 다시 되풀이하겠어요. 저는 그분의 의견을 들어야 해요. 그뿐 아니라 그분의 결정이 필요해요! 그가 말하는 대로 될 거예요. 보세요, 제가 당신 말씀을 얼마나 애타게 기다리는지, 알렉세이 표도로비치…… 그런데 무슨 일이세요?\"\n\n\"저는 도무지 믿을 수가 없습니다. 이해할 수가 없어요!\" 알료샤가 별안간 괴로운 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐라고요? 뭐가요?\"\n\n\"그가 모스크바로 간다는데, 당신은 잘됐다고 외치셨어요. 일부러 그렇게 말씀하셨어요! 그러고는 그게 잘된 게 아니라 벗을—벗을 잃게 되어 슬프다고 설명하기 시작하셨지요. 하지만 그것 역시 연기였어요—당신은 한 배역을 연기하고 계셨어요—마치 극장에서처럼요!\"\n\n\"극장에서요? 뭐라고요? 그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤는데, 깊이 경악하여 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오르며 이맛살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"당신은 그에게서 벗을 잃게 되어 슬프다고 다짐하면서도, 그가 떠나서 잘됐다고 그의 면전에 대고 끈질기게 말씀하시잖아요.\" 알료샤가 숨 가쁘게 말했다. 그는 탁자 곁에 선 채 앉지 않았다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요? 저는 이해할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"저 자신도 이해가 안 됩니다…… 어쩐지 섬광처럼 본 것 같아요…… 제대로 말하지 못한다는 걸 알지만, 그래도 다 말하겠습니다.\" 알료샤는 여전히 떨리고 끊기는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"제가 보기에는, 어쩌면 당신은 드미트리를 전혀 사랑하지 않으시고…… 처음부터, 애초부터 사랑하신 적이 없는 것 같아요…… 그리고 드미트리도 당신을 결코 사랑한 적이 없고…… 다만 당신을 존경할 뿐이에요…… 제가 어떻게 감히 이 모든 걸 말하는지 정말 모르겠지만, 누군가는 진실을 말해야 하니까요…… 여기서는 아무도 진실을 말하지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 진실 말이에요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤는데, 그 목소리에는 발작적인 울림이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"말씀드리겠습니다.\" 알료샤는 마치 집 꼭대기에서 뛰어내리기라도 하듯 필사적으로 황급히 말을 이었다. \"드미트리를 부르세요. 제가 데려오겠습니다—그를 이리 오게 해서 당신의 손을 잡고 이반의 손도 잡아 두 분의 손을 맞잡게 하세요. 당신은 이반을 괴롭히고 계세요, 다름 아니라 당신이 그를 사랑하기 때문이에요—그리고 그를 괴롭히는 건, 당신이 '자기 학대'로 드미트리를 사랑하기 때문이고요—거짓된 사랑으로요—당신이 스스로를 그렇게 믿게 만들었으니까요.\"\n\n알료샤는 말을 끊고 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"당신…… 당신…… 당신은 신앙에 미친 조그만 바보로군요—그게 바로 당신이에요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 쏘아붙였다. 그녀의 얼굴은 새하얗고 입술은 분노로 떨리고 있었다.\n\n이반이 별안간 웃음을 터뜨리며 일어섰다. 손에는 모자가 들려 있었다.\n\n\"네 생각이 틀렸다, 나의 착한 알료샤.\" 그는 알료샤가 그의 얼굴에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정을 지으며 말했다—젊은이다운 진솔함과, 강렬하고 거역할 수 없이 솔직한 감정의 표정이었다. \"카테리나 이바노브나는 한 번도 나를 마음에 둔 적이 없어! 그녀는 줄곧 내가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 걸 알고 있었지—나는 내 사랑에 대해 그녀에게 한마디도 한 적이 없지만—그녀는 알면서도 나를 마음에 두지 않았어. 나는 그녀의 벗인 적도 없었어, 단 한순간도. 그녀는 너무 자존심이 강해서 내 우정 따위는 필요 없으니까. 그녀는 복수의 수단으로 나를 곁에 두었던 거야. 그녀는 나를 통해, 그리고 나에게, 첫 만남 이래로 드미트리에게서 줄곧 받아 온 온갖 모욕을 되갚은 거지. 그 첫 만남조차 그녀의 마음속에 모욕으로 사무쳐 있으니까—그녀의 마음이란 게 바로 그래! 그녀는 내게 그를 향한 자기 사랑 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않았어. 나는 이제 가겠소. 하지만 믿어요, 카테리나 이바노브나, 당신은 정말로 그를 사랑하고 있소. 그가 당신을 모욕하면 할수록 당신은 더욱 그를 사랑하지—그게 당신의 '찢어 발기는 고통'이오. 당신은 그를 있는 그대로 사랑하고 있소. 그가 당신을 모욕하기에 그를 사랑하는 거요. 만약 그가 새사람이 된다면 당신은 곧장 그를 버리고 사랑하기를 그칠 거요. 하지만 당신은 자신의 영웅적 정절을 끊임없이 관조하고, 그의 부정을 책망하기 위해 그가 필요한 거요. 그리고 그건 다 당신의 자존심에서 비롯된 거요. 오, 거기에는 굴욕과 자기 비하가 잔뜩 들어 있지만, 그것도 다 자존심에서 나온 거요…… 나는 너무 젊고, 당신을 너무 많이 사랑했소. 이런 말을 해서는 안 된다는 걸, 그냥 당신을 떠나는 편이 내게 더 품위 있는 일이고 당신에게도 덜 모욕적이리라는 걸 나도 알고 있소. 하지만 나는 멀리 떠나서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 거요…… 영원히 말이오. 나는 '찢어 발기는 고통' 곁에 앉아 있고 싶지 않소…… 그런데 이젠 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠군. 할 말은 다 했소…… 잘 있어요, 카테리나 이바노브나. 당신은 나에게 화낼 수 없을 거요. 나는 당신보다 백 배는 더 혹독하게 벌을 받고 있으니까, 다시는 당신을 보지 못하리라는 사실 하나만으로도. 잘 있어요! 당신 손은 필요 없소. 당신은 너무도 작정하고 나를 괴롭혔기에, 나는 이 순간 당신을 용서할 수가 없소. 나중에는 용서하겠지만, 지금은 당신 손이 필요 없소. '덴 단크, 다메, 베게어 이히 니히트.'\" 그는 억지웃음을 지으며 덧붙였는데, 그러나 그것은 그가 실러를 읽었으며, 그것도 외울 만큼 읽었음을 드러내는 것이었으니—알료샤로서는 도저히 믿지 못했을 일이었다. 그는 안주인인 호흘라코바 부인에게조차 작별 인사를 하지 않고 방을 나갔다. 알료샤는 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n* 덴 단크, 다메, 베게어 이히 니히트(Den Dank, Dame, begehr ich nicht): \"감사라면, 부인, 나는 바라지 않소\"라는 뜻의 독일어로, 실러의 발라드 「장갑」의 한 구절이다.\n\n\"이반 형!\" 그는 절망적으로 형의 뒤를 향해 외쳤다. \"돌아와요, 이반 형! 아니, 이젠 무슨 일이 있어도 형이 돌아오지 않을 거야!\" 그는 후회 어린 심정으로 그것을 깨달으며 다시 외쳤다. \"하지만 내 잘못이야, 내 잘못이야. 내가 시작한 거야! 이반 형은 화가 나서, 잘못 말한 거야. 부당하게, 화가 나서. 형은 이리로 돌아와야 해, 돌아와야 해.\" 알료샤는 미친 듯이 거듭 외쳐 댔다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 별안간 옆방으로 들어가 버렸다.\n\n\"당신은 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았어요. 천사처럼 훌륭하게 처신하셨어요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 알료샤에게 재빠르고 황홀하게 속삭였다. \"제가 이반 표도로비치가 떠나지 못하게 온 힘을 다하겠어요.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 환히 빛나 알료샤를 몹시 괴롭게 했는데, 바로 그때 카테리나 이바노브나가 불쑥 되돌아왔다. 그녀의 손에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 두 장이 들려 있었다.\n\n\"당신께 크게 부탁드릴 일이 있어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그녀는 마치 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 겉으로는 차분하고 고른 목소리로 알료샤에게 말을 건넸다. \"일주일—그래요, 일주일쯤 전인 것 같아요—드미트리 표도로비치가 성급하고 부당한 짓을—아주 추악한 짓을 저질렀어요. 여기 천한 선술집이 하나 있는데, 거기서 그가 퇴역 장교 하나, 당신 아버님이 무슨 일에 부리던 그 대위를 만났어요. 드미트리 표도로비치가 어쩌다 그 대위에게 화가 나서, 그의 수염을 움켜쥐고 거리로 끌어내어 그 모욕적인 꼴로 길을 따라 한참이나 끌고 다녔다는 거예요. 그런데 들으니, 여기 학교에 다니는 그의 아들, 아직 어린아이인 사내애가 그걸 보고 울면서 옆에 따라 뛰며 아버지를 살려 달라고, 모든 사람에게 아버지를 지켜 달라고 애원했는데, 다들 웃기만 했다지 뭐예요. 용서하세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 그가 저지른 그 수치스러운 짓을 떠올릴 때마다 치미는 분노를 억누를 수가 없어요…… 그건 드미트리 표도로비치가 화가 났을 때만…… 격정에 휩싸였을 때만 저지를 수 있는 그런 짓들 가운데 하나예요! 차마 묘사조차 할 수 없어요…… 무슨 말로 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 제가 그 피해자에 대해 알아보았더니, 그는 아주 가난한 사람이더군요. 이름은 스네기료프예요. 군에서 무슨 잘못을 저질러 퇴역당했다는데, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어요. 그러고는 지금은 가족과 함께 끔찍한 궁핍 속에 빠져 있어요—병든 아이들에, 제가 알기로는 미친 아내까지 딸린 불행한 가족이지요. 그는 여기 오래 살았고, 한때는 필경사로 일했지만 지금은 한 푼도 못 벌고 있어요. 저는 만약 당신이…… 그러니까 제 생각엔…… 모르겠어요. 제가 너무 어지러워서요. 보세요, 제가 당신께 부탁드리고 싶은 건, 소중한 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그에게 가셔서, 그들을 찾아갈 무슨 구실을 만드셔서—그러니까 그 대위 말이에요—오, 세상에, 제가 설명을 너무 못하네요!—그리고 섬세하게, 조심스럽게, 오직 당신만이 할 수 있는 그런 방식으로\" (알료샤는 얼굴을 붉혔다) \"그에게 이 도움을, 이 이백 루블을 어떻게든 건네주시는 거예요. 그는 틀림없이 받을 거예요…… 그러니까, 받도록 설득해 주세요…… 아니, 제가 무슨 말을 하는 거죠? 보세요, 이건 그가 소송을 걸지 못하게 하려는 보상금 같은 게 아니에요(그가 그럴 작정이었던 것 같으니까요). 그저 동정의 표시, 그를 돕고 싶다는 표시예요. 저, 드미트리 표도로비치의 약혼녀로서, 그 사람 본인이 아니라요…… 하지만 당신은 아실 거예요…… 제가 직접 갈 수도 있지만, 당신이 훨씬 더 잘하실 테니까요. 그는 호수 거리, 칼미코바라는 여자의 집에 살아요…… 부디, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저를 위해 그렇게 해 주세요, 그리고 이제…… 이제 저는 좀…… 지쳤어요. 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n그녀는 돌아서서 어찌나 재빨리 휘장 뒤로 사라졌던지, 알료샤는 말을 하고 싶었으나 한마디도 꺼낼 틈이 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 용서를 빌고, 자신을 책망하고, 무슨 말이든 하고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었으니, 그의 마음이 가득 차 있어 그러지 않고는 방을 나설 수가 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 호흘라코바 부인이 그의 손을 잡고 자기와 함께 그를 끌어냈다. 현관에서 그녀는 아까처럼 다시 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"저 애는 자존심이 강해서 자기 자신과 싸우고 있어요. 하지만 착하고, 매력적이고, 너그럽지요.\" 그녀가 반쯤 속삭이며 외쳤다. \"오, 제가 저 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 특히 가끔씩은요, 그리고 모든 게 다시 얼마나 기쁜지요! 소중한 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 모르셨겠지만 제가 꼭 말씀드려야겠어요. 우리 모두가, 모두가—저 애의 두 이모와 저, 그리고 우리 모두가, 심지어 리즈까지도—지난 한 달 동안 오직 한 가지만을 바라고 기도해 왔어요. 바로 저 애가, 자기를 거들떠보지도 않고 마음에 두지도 않는 당신의 그 사랑하는 드미트리를 포기하고, 이반 표도로비치와 결혼하는 거예요—그분은 그토록 훌륭하고 교양 있는 청년이고, 이 세상 그 무엇보다도 저 애를 사랑하시니까요. 우리는 그 일을 성사시키려고 아주 작당을 하고 있고, 어쩌면 저도 그 때문에 여기 머물고 있는 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저분은 울었어요—다시 상처를 받았어요.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여자의 눈물은 절대 믿지 마세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저는 이런 경우에 결코 여자 편을 들지 않아요. 저는 언제나 남자 편이랍니다.\"\n\n\"엄마, 그분을 버릇없게 만들고 계세요.\" 문 뒤에서 리즈의 가느다란 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니에요, 다 제 잘못이었어요. 제가 무섭도록 잘못했어요.\" 알료샤는 위로받지 못한 채, 자신의 경솔함을 뉘우치는 괴로움에 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요. 당신은 천사처럼, 천사처럼 처신하셨어요. 저는 천 번이라도 그렇게 말할 준비가 되어 있어요.\"\n\n\"엄마, 그분이 어떻게 천사처럼 처신했다는 거예요?\" 다시 리즈의 목소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"저는 어쩐지 별안간 이런 생각이 들었어요.\" 알료샤는 리즈의 말을 듣지 못한 듯 말을 이었다. \"저분이 이반 형을 사랑한다고요, 그래서 그런 어리석은 말을 한 거예요…… 이제 어떻게 될까요?\"\n\n\"누가요, 누가요?\" 리즈가 외쳤다. \"엄마, 정말 저를 죽이실 작정이세요. 제가 묻는데 대답을 안 하시잖아요.\"\n\n바로 그때 하녀가 뛰어 들어왔다.\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나 아씨께서 편찮으세요…… 울면서, 몸부림치며…… 발작을 일으키셨어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 리즈가 진정으로 걱정스러운 어조로 외쳤다. \"엄마, 발작은 그 사람이 아니라 제가 일으키겠어요!\"\n\n\"리즈, 제발 소리치지 마라, 나를 들볶지 말고. 네 나이에는 어른들이 아는 걸 다 알 수가 없는 거야. 가서 네가 알아야 할 건 다 말해 줄 테니까. 오, 세상에! 가요, 가요…… 발작은 좋은 징조예요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저 애가 발작을 일으키는 건 아주 잘된 일이에요. 바로 그래야 마땅한 거예요. 이런 경우에 저는 늘 여자 편이 아니에요, 이런 온갖 여성스러운 눈물이며 발작에 반대랍니다. 율리야, 어서 가서 내가 곧 날아가겠다고 전해라. 이반 표도로비치가 그런 식으로 떠나는 건 다 저 애 잘못이에요. 하지만 그분은 떠나지 않을 거예요. 리즈, 제발 소리치지 마! 아, 그래, 너는 소리치고 있지 않구나. 소리치는 건 나로구나. 엄마를 용서해 다오. 하지만 나는 기뻐, 기뻐, 기뻐 죽겠구나! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 보셨어요, 아까 이반 표도로비치가 나가실 때, 그 모든 말을 하고 나가실 때 얼마나 젊었는지, 얼마나 젊었는지를요? 저는 그분이 그토록 학식 높고 그토록 대단한 학자인 줄 알았는데, 별안간 그렇게 따뜻하고, 솔직하고, 젊은이답게, 그토록 젊은이다운 미숙함으로 처신하시니, 그 모든 게 어찌나 멋지던지요, 꼭 당신처럼요…… 그리고 그 독일어 시구를 읊으시던 모습이라니, 꼭 당신 같았어요! 하지만 저는 가야 해요, 가야 해요! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 어서 저 애의 부탁을 처리하시고, 그런 다음 어서 돌아오세요. 리즈, 지금 뭐 필요한 거 있니? 제발 알렉세이 표도로비치를 잠시도 붙들지 마라. 곧 네게 돌아올 테니까.\"\n\n호흘라코바 부인은 마침내 뛰어나갔다. 떠나기 전에 알료샤는 리즈를 보려고 문을 열려 했다.\n\n\"절대로 안 돼요.\" 리즈가 외쳤다. \"지금은 절대로 안 돼요. 문 너머로 말씀하세요. 어쩌다 천사가 되신 거예요? 제가 알고 싶은 건 오직 그것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"끔찍한 어리석음을 저질러서요, 리즈! 안녕히!\"\n\n\"감히 그런 식으로 가시면 안 돼요!\" 리즈가 입을 열기 시작했다.\n\n\"리즈, 저는 정말 슬픈 일이 있어요! 곧 돌아올게요. 하지만 저는 크나큰, 크나큰 슬픔이 있어요!\"\n\n그러고는 그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n# Chapter VI. A Laceration In The Cottage\n\n그는 분명 이전에는 좀처럼 겪어 본 적 없는 깊은 비탄에 잠겨 있었다. 바보처럼 뛰어들어, 대체 무슨 일에 끼어든 것인가? 연애 사건에. \"하지만 내가 그런 일에 대해 무엇을 안단 말인가? 이런 일을 두고 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있단 말인가?\" 그는 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 백 번째로 같은 말을 되뇌었다. \"오, 부끄러운 것쯤은 아무것도 아니다. 수치란 내가 마땅히 받아야 할 벌일 뿐이니. 문제는 내가 틀림없이 더 큰 불행을 불러일으키고 말았다는 것이다…… 게다가 조시마 장로께서는 나를 보내 두 사람을 화해시키고 한데 맺어 주라 하셨는데. 이것이 두 사람을 맺어 주는 방식이란 말인가?\" 그러다 문득 그는 자신이 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 주려 했던 일을 떠올리고는, 또다시 끔찍한 부끄러움에 휩싸였다. \"내 행동이 진심에서 우러난 것이었다 해도, 앞으로는 좀 더 분별 있게 처신해야겠다.\" 그는 불쑥 이렇게 결론을 내렸으나, 그 결론에 미소조차 짓지 못했다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나가 맡긴 심부름 때문에 그는 호수 거리로 가야 했는데, 형 드미트리는 그 부근, 호수 거리에서 갈라진 골목 안에 살고 있었다. 알료샤는 그 대위를 찾아가기 전에 어떻게든 먼저 형에게 들러야겠다고 마음먹었으나, 형을 만나지 못하리라는 예감이 들었다. 형이 지금 일부러 자기를 피하려 들 것이라고 짐작했지만, 어쨌든 반드시 형을 찾아내야 했다. 시간이 흐르고 있었다. 죽어 가는 장로에 대한 생각은 수도원을 나선 순간부터 단 한 순간도 알료샤의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나가 맡긴 심부름 중에서 그가 특히 마음에 걸리는 한 가지가 있었다. 그녀가 그 대위의 아들, 곧 아버지 곁에서 울며 뛰어가던 어린 학생 이야기를 꺼냈을 때, 알료샤는 곧바로 그 아이가 바로 자기 손가락을 깨문 그 학생이 틀림없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐던 것이다. 자기가, 곧 알료샤가 그에게 무슨 잘못을 했느냐고 물었을 때 손가락을 깨물었던 바로 그 아이 말이다. 이제 알료샤는 왜 그런지 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없었으나 거의 확신하고 있었다. 다른 일을 떠올리는 것은 마음의 위안이 되었고, 그리하여 그는 자기가 저질러 놓은 \"실수\"에 대해서는 더 이상 생각하지 말고, 후회로 스스로를 괴롭히지도 말며, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 해야 할 일을 하기로 결심했다. 그렇게 생각하니 마음이 완전히 가벼워졌다. 드미트리가 묵고 있는 거리로 접어들면서 그는 시장기를 느꼈고, 주머니에서 아버지 집에서 가져온 빵 한 덩이를 꺼내 먹었다. 그러자 기운이 좀 났다.\n\n드미트리는 집에 없었다. 그 집 식구들, 곧 늙은 가구장이와 그의 아들, 늙은 아내는 알료샤를 노골적으로 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보았다. \"사흘째 밤이나 여기서 안 잤습니다. 어디 가 버렸는지도 모르지요.\" 알료샤의 끈질긴 물음에 노인이 이렇게 대답했다. 알료샤는 그가 누군가의 지시대로 대답하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그가 형이 그루셴카네 집에 있는 것은 아닌지, 아니면 포마네 집에 숨어 있는 것은 아닌지 물었을 때(알료샤는 일부러 그렇게 거리낌 없이 말했다) 세 사람 모두 그를 놀란 눈으로 쳐다보았다. \"이 사람들은 형을 아끼고, 형을 위해 최선을 다하고 있구나.\" 알료샤는 생각했다. \"그거 잘된 일이다.\"\n\n마침내 그는 호수 거리에서 그 집을 찾아냈다. 한쪽으로 폭삭 주저앉은 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 집으로, 거리 쪽으로 창문 셋이 나 있고, 진흙투성이 마당 한복판에는 소 한 마리가 외따로 서 있었다. 그는 마당을 가로질러, 복도로 통하는 문을 찾아냈다. 복도 왼편에는 늙은 안주인이 역시 늙은 딸과 함께 살고 있었다. 둘 다 귀가 먼 듯했다. 대위를 찾는다는 그의 거듭된 물음에 마침내 둘 중 하나가 그가 세든 사람을 찾는다는 것을 알아듣고는, 복도 건너편의 한 문을 가리켰다. 대위의 거처는 알고 보니 평범한 시골집 방 한 칸이었다. 알료샤가 문을 열려고 쇠 빗장에 손을 얹었을 때, 그는 안에서 풍기는 묘한 정적에 멈칫했다. 그러나 카테리나 이바노브나의 말로 미루어 그 사내에게 가족이 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. \"다들 잠들었거나, 아니면 어쩌면 내가 오는 소리를 듣고 내가 문 열기를 기다리고 있는 것이리라. 먼저 노크하는 편이 낫겠다.\" 그러고는 문을 두드렸다. 대답이 들려왔으나, 곧바로는 아니었고, 아마 십 초쯤 사이를 둔 뒤였다.\n\n\"누구요?\" 누군가 크고 몹시 성난 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n그러자 알료샤는 문을 열고 문지방을 넘었다. 그는 영락없는 농가의 방 안에 들어와 있었다. 방은 컸지만 온갖 살림살이로 발 디딜 틈 없이 어수선했고, 그 안에는 몇 사람이 있었다. 왼편에는 커다란 러시아식 페치카가 있었다. 페치카에서 왼쪽 창문까지 방을 가로질러 끈 하나가 걸려 있었고, 그 위에는 누더기들이 널려 있었다. 좌우 양쪽 벽에는 각각 침대가 하나씩 붙어 있었는데, 뜨개질한 누비이불이 덮여 있었다. 왼쪽 침대 위에는 무명천을 씌운 베개 넷이 아래에서 위로 갈수록 작아지며 피라미드 모양으로 쌓여 있었다. 다른 쪽 침대에는 아주 작은 베개 하나만 놓여 있었다. 맞은편 구석은 끈에 걸친 커튼인지 홑이불인지로 가려져 있었다. 그 휘장 너머로는 긴 의자와 의자를 붙여 만든 잠자리가 보였다. 거칠게 짠 네모난 통나무 식탁이 가운데 창문 쪽으로 옮겨져 있었다. 창문 셋은 저마다 곰팡이 핀 푸르스름한 작은 유리 네 장으로 되어 있어 빛이 거의 들지 않았고, 게다가 꼭 닫혀 있어서 방 안은 그리 밝지 않고 다소 후텁지근했다. 식탁 위에는 달걀부침 찌꺼기가 남은 프라이팬과 반쯤 먹다 만 빵 한 조각, 그리고 보드카가 몇 방울 남은 작은 병이 놓여 있었다.\n\n무명 가운을 걸친, 점잖아 보이는 여인이 왼쪽 침대 곁의 의자에 앉아 있었다. 얼굴은 야위고 누렇게 떴으며, 움푹 꺼진 두 뺨은 한눈에 그녀가 병들었음을 드러냈다. 그러나 알료샤의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 것은 그 가엾은 여인의 눈빛이었다. 놀라움이 깃든 캐묻는 듯한 표정이면서도 동시에 오만한 자존심이 서린 눈빛이었다. 그가 그녀의 남편과 이야기를 나누는 동안에도, 그녀의 큼직한 갈색 눈은 똑같이 오만하고 캐묻는 듯한 표정으로 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 옮겨 다녔다. 창가의 그녀 곁에는 젊은 처녀가 서 있었는데, 인물은 그리 곱지 못하고 불그스름한 머리숱이 적었으며, 초라하지만 아주 단정하게 차려입고 있었다. 그녀는 알료샤가 들어서자 경멸하는 눈초리로 그를 쳐다보았다. 다른 침대 곁에는 또 다른 여인의 모습이 앉아 있었다. 그것은 몹시 애처로운 광경이었으니, 스무 살가량의 젊은 처녀였으나 곱사등이에 절름발이였고, 나중에 알료샤가 듣기로는 \"두 다리가 말라비틀어진\" 처녀였다. 그녀의 목발은 바로 곁 구석에 세워져 있었다. 이 가엾은 처녀의 눈은 놀랍도록 아름답고 부드러웠는데, 그 눈으로 온화하고 고요하게 알료샤를 바라보았다. 마흔다섯쯤 된 사내가 식탁에 앉아 달걀부침을 마저 먹고 있었다. 그는 깡마르고, 키가 작으며, 허약한 체구였다. 불그스름한 머리에 옅은 빛깔의 성긴 턱수염을 길렀는데, 영락없이 한 줌의 삼 부스러기 같았다(이 비유와 \"한 줌의 삼 부스러기\"라는 말이 무슨 까닭에선지 그 순간 대뜸 알료샤의 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그는 나중까지 그것을 기억했다). 방 안에 다른 사내라곤 없었으니, 그에게 소리친 것은 분명 이 양반이었다. 그러나 알료샤가 들어서자 그는 앉아 있던 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 누더기 같은 냅킨으로 황급히 입을 닦으며 알료샤에게로 쏜살같이 달려들었다.\n\n\"수도원에서 시주를 청하러 온 수도사로군. 찾아올 데가 따로 있지!\" 왼쪽 구석에 서 있던 처녀가 큰 소리로 말했다. 사내는 즉시 그녀 쪽으로 홱 돌아서더니, 흥분으로 갈라지는 목소리로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아니다, 바르바라, 네가 틀렸어. 실례지만 묻겠습니다.\" 그는 다시 알료샤를 돌아보았다. \"무슨 일로 이런—저희 누추한 거처까지 찾아오셨는지요?\"\n\n알료샤는 그를 유심히 바라보았다. 그를 보는 것은 처음이었다. 그에게는 어딘가 모나고, 허둥대며, 짜증스러운 데가 있었다. 방금 술을 마신 것이 분명했으나 취하지는 않았다. 표정에는 유별난 뻔뻔함이 서려 있으면서도, 이상하게도 그와 동시에 두려움이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 오랫동안 짓눌려 거기에 순종해 오다가 이제 별안간 돌아서서 자기를 내세워 보려고 애쓰는 사람 같았다. 아니, 더 정확히 말하자면, 끔찍이도 누군가를 한 대 치고 싶어 하면서도 그 상대가 자기를 칠까 봐 무섭도록 겁내는 사람 같았다. 그의 말과 날카로운 목소리의 억양에는 일종의 광기 어린 익살이 배어 있었는데, 때로는 악의에 차고 때로는 비굴하며, 끊임없이 이 어조에서 저 어조로 옮겨 다녔다. \"저희 누추한 거처\"에 관한 물음을 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨다시피 하며, 눈알을 굴리고, 알료샤에게 바짝 다가서며 던졌으므로, 알료샤는 저도 모르게 한 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다. 그는 몹시 낡고 거무튀튀한 무명 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 군데군데 기우고 얼룩이 져 있었다. 바지는 격자무늬에 빛깔이 지극히 옅고, 유행에 한참 뒤떨어진 데다, 천이 아주 얇았다. 어찌나 구깃구깃하고 짧았던지, 마치 사내아이가 자라 옷이 작아진 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"저는 알렉세이 카라마조프입니다.\" 알료샤가 대답을 시작했다.\n\n\"그건 익히 알고 있습지요, 선생.\" 그 양반은 이미 그가 누군지 알고 있노라 못 박으려는 듯 대뜸 말을 잘랐다. \"저는 스네기료프 대위올시다, 선생. 하지만 그래도 정확히 무엇이 선생을 이리로—\"\n\n\"오, 별 대단한 일로 온 건 아닙니다. 그저 한 말씀 나누고 싶어서요—허락해 주신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여기 의자가 있습니다, 선생. 부디 앉으시지요. 옛날 희극에서 늘 하던 대사 아닙니까, '부디 앉으시지요'라고.\" 그러고는 잽싼 동작으로 빈 의자 하나를 움켜쥐더니(쿠션도 없는 거친 나무 의자였다) 거의 방 한복판에 그것을 놓아 주고는, 자기도 그와 비슷한 의자를 하나 끌어다 알료샤를 마주 보고 앉았는데, 어찌나 바짝 붙어 앉았던지 두 사람의 무릎이 거의 맞닿을 지경이었다.\n\n\"니콜라이 일리치 스네기료프올시다, 선생. 한때 러시아 보병 대위였으나, 제 악행으로 망신을 당했지요. 그래도 어쨌든 대위입니다. 하기야 이렇게 말하는 꼴을 보면 지금은 대위가 아닐지도 모르지요. 후반생 내내 저는 '선생' 소리를 입에 달고 사는 법을 배웠으니까요. 그건 세상에서 영락한 사람이나 쓰는 말입지요.\"\n\n\"지당하신 말씀입니다.\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"그런데 그건 저절로 나오는 겁니까, 아니면 일부러 그러시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"하느님께 맹세코, 저절로 나오는 거지요. 예전엔 쓰지도 않았습니다! 평생 '선생'이란 말을 입에 담은 적이 없는데, 신세가 바닥으로 가라앉자마자 '선생' 소리를 하기 시작했단 말입니다. 이건 더 높은 힘이 하는 일이지요. 보아하니 선생께서는 요즘 세태에 관심이 있으신 모양인데, 손님 대접이라곤 할 수 없는 이런 형편에서 사는 제가 어찌 선생의 호기심을 자극할 수 있었겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제가 온 건—그 일 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"그 일이라니, 무슨 일 말입니까?\" 대위가 안달하며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"제 형 드미트리 표도로비치와의 그 일 때문입니다.\" 알료샤가 어색하게 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"무슨 일 말입니까, 선생? 설마 그 일을 말씀하시는 건 아니겠지요? 그럼 제 '한 줌의 삼 부스러기' 얘깁니까?\" 그는 더 가까이 다가앉아, 무릎이 정말로 알료샤의 무릎을 부딪쳤다. 그의 입술은 이상하게도 실처럼 앙다물려 있었다.\n\n\"한 줌의 삼 부스러기라니요?\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"저 형이 절 일러바치러 온 거예요, 아버지!\" 알료샤에게 낯익은 목소리가—그 학생의 목소리가—휘장 너머에서 외쳤다. \"제가 방금 저 사람 손가락을 깨물었거든요.\" 휘장이 젖혀지자, 알료샤는 자기를 습격했던 그 아이가 구석의 성상 아래, 긴 의자와 의자를 붙여 만든 작은 잠자리에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 아이는 외투와 낡은 솜이불을 덮고 누워 있었다. 보아하니 몸이 성치 않았고, 번들거리는 눈으로 미루어 열이 있는 듯했다. 그는 마치 제집에 있어서 아무도 자기를 건드리지 못한다고 여기는 듯, 두려움 없이 알료샤를 바라보았다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 저 애가 네 손가락을 깨물었다고?\" 대위가 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. \"저 애가 깨문 게 네 손가락이었어?\"\n\n\"네. 저 애가 다른 학생들과 돌을 던지고 있었습니다. 여섯이서 저 애 혼자를 상대로요. 제가 다가가니까 저 애가 제게 돌을 던지고, 또 한 번은 제 머리를 겨눠 던졌습니다. 제가 무슨 잘못을 했느냐고 물었지요. 그러자 저 애가 제게 달려들어 제 손가락을 사정없이 깨물었습니다. 왜 그랬는지는 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 저놈을 매질하겠습니다, 선생, 당장에—이 자리에서!\" 대위가 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어났다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 결코 일러바치려는 게 아닙니다. 그저 말씀드리는 것뿐이고…… 저는 저 애가 매를 맞는 걸 원치 않습니다. 게다가 보아하니 몸도 성치 않은 듯하고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 저놈을 매질하리라고 생각하십니까? 제 일류샤를 끌어다 선생 분풀이나 시켜 드리려고 선생 앞에서 매질하리라고요? 당장 그래 드릴까요, 선생?\" 대위가 별안간 알료샤를 향해, 마치 그에게 달려들 기세로 돌아섰다. \"선생 손가락은 안됐습니다만, 일류샤를 매질하는 대신, 선생의 정당한 분노를 풀어 드리려 여기 이 칼로 선생 눈앞에서 제 손가락 넷을 잘라 버리는 게 어떻겠습니까? 손가락 넷이면 선생의 복수심을 채우기에 족하리라 봅니다만. 설마 다섯째 손가락까지 달라고 하시진 않겠지요?\" 그는 목이 메어 말을 뚝 그쳤다. 얼굴의 근육이란 근육은 죄다 씰룩거리며 일그러졌고, 그는 극도로 도전적인 표정이었다. 일종의 광란 상태에 빠져 있었다.\n\n\"이제 다 알 것 같습니다.\" 알료샤가 여전히 자리를 지킨 채 부드럽고 슬픈 목소리로 말했다. \"그러니까 댁의 아드님은 착한 아이고, 제 아버지를 사랑하기에, 자기 아버지를 습격한 사람의 동생인 저를 공격한 것이군요…… 이제 알겠습니다.\" 그는 생각에 잠겨 거듭 말했다. \"하지만 제 형 드미트리 표도로비치는 자기 행동을 뉘우치고 있습니다. 저는 그걸 알고 있습니다. 그리고 만약 형이 댁으로 찾아올 수 있다면, 아니 그보다 바로 그 자리에서 댁을 만날 수만 있다면, 형은 여러 사람 앞에서 용서를 빌 겁니다—댁이 원하신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 제 수염을 뽑아 놓고 나서 용서를 빌겠다는 말씀이지요? 그러면 그것으로 깔끔하게 마무리되리라 여기는 게로군요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다! 오히려 형은 댁이 원하시는 것은 무엇이든, 댁이 원하시는 방식으로 다 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 제가 그 나리께 바로 그 술집에서—'수도'라는 이름의 술집이지요—아니면 장터에서 제 앞에 무릎을 꿇으라고 청하면, 그렇게 하리란 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"네, 형은 무릎까지도 꿇을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"선생은 제 가슴을 깊이 찌르셨습니다, 선생. 저를 눈물 나도록 감동시키고 제 가슴을 깊이 찌르셨어요! 저는 댁의 형님의 너그러움을 너무도 잘 헤아리고 있습니다. 제 식구를 소개하게 해 주십시오. 제 두 딸과 아들, 곧 제 새끼들 말입니다. 제가 죽으면 누가 이 애들을 돌봐 주겠으며, 제가 살아 있은들 이 애들 말고 누가 저 같은 가련한 인간을 보살펴 주겠습니까? 그건 주님께서 저 같은 부류의 인간마다에게 마련해 주신 크나큰 은총이지요, 선생. 저 같은 인간조차 사랑해 줄 수 있는 누군가는 반드시 있어야 하는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말 지당한 말씀입니다!\" 알료샤가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"오, 제발 어릿광대짓 좀 그만둬요! 무슨 바보 같은 작자가 들어왔다고 우리를 망신시키는 거예요!\" 창가의 처녀가 별안간 경멸하고 멸시하는 태도로 아버지를 돌아보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"잠깐만 기다려라, 바르바라!\" 아버지가 단호한 어조로 외쳤으나, 정작 그녀를 바라보는 눈길은 자못 흐뭇해하는 빛이었다. \"저 애의 성미가 저렇답니다.\" 그가 다시 알료샤를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 온 누리에 그 무엇도\n그의 눈에 드는 것은 없었더라—\n\n아니, 여성형으로 하자면, 그녀의 눈에 드는 것은 없었더라, 이 말입지요. 자, 그럼 이제 제 아내 아리나 페트로브나를 소개하겠습니다. 다리를 못 쓰고, 마흔셋이며, 움직이긴 하나 아주 조금밖에 못 움직입니다. 천한 집안 태생이지요. 아리나 페트로브나, 표정 좀 가다듬으시오. 이분은 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프올시다. 일어나시오, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그는 알료샤의 손을 잡더니 뜻밖의 힘으로 그를 끌어 일으켰다. \"부인께 소개받으려면 일어서셔야지요. 그 카라마조프가 아니라오, 여보, 그…… 흠…… 운운하는 그 양반 말고, 그 사람의 동생인데, 겸손한 미덕으로 빛나는 분이라오. 자, 아리나 페트로브나, 자, 여보, 먼저 손에 입을 맞추시도록.\"\n\n그러고는 아내의 손에 정중하게, 심지어 다정하게 입을 맞추었다. 창가의 처녀는 그 광경에 분개하여 등을 돌렸다. 오만하게 캐묻던 여인의 얼굴에 유난스러운 다정함이 어렸다.\n\n\"안녕하세요! 앉으세요, 체르노마조프 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"카라마조프요, 여보, 카라마조프. 우린 천한 집안 태생이지요.\" 그가 다시 속삭였다.\n\n\"그래요, 카라마조프든 뭐든, 난 늘 체르노마조프로 생각된단 말이지요…… 앉으세요. 저이는 왜 당신을 일으켜 세웠담? 저이는 날 보고 다리를 못 쓴다고 하지만, 못 쓰는 게 아니라 그저 다리가 술통처럼 퉁퉁 부었을 뿐이고, 나 자신은 비쩍 쪼그라들었답니다. 한때는 그렇게 살이 쪘었는데, 지금은 꼭 바늘이라도 삼킨 것 같지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"우린 천한 집안 태생이지요.\" 대위가 다시 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 아버지, 아버지!\" 그때까지 의자에 앉아 잠자코 있던 곱사등이 처녀가 별안간 입을 열더니, 손수건에 두 눈을 묻었다.\n\n\"어릿광대 같으니!\" 창가의 처녀가 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"우리 집 소식을 들으셨나요?\" 어머니가 두 딸을 가리키며 말했다. \"꼭 먹구름이 몰려오는 것 같답니다. 구름이 지나가면 또 음악이 울리지요. 우리가 군대에 있던 시절엔 이런 손님이 참 많이 드나들었답니다. 비교하려는 건 아니에요. 사람마다 취향이 다른 법이니까요. 그 시절엔 부제의 아내가 찾아와선 이러더군요. '알렉산드르 알렉산드로비치는 더없이 고귀한 마음을 지닌 분이지만, 나스타시야 페트로브나는 지옥의 종자라니까요.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '글쎄요, 그건 취향 문제고, 댁이야말로 성깔 사나운 여편네구려.' 그러니까 저쪽이 그래요. '당신은 분수를 좀 알아야겠소.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '이 시커먼 칼 같은 여편네야, 누가 당신더러 날 가르쳐 달라 했소?' 그러니까 이러는 거예요. '하지만 내 숨결은 깨끗하고, 당신 숨결은 더럽다오.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '내 숨결이 더러운지 어떤지 장교들한테 다 물어보구려.' 그날 이후로 그 말이 줄곧 마음에 걸렸답니다. 얼마 전에도 내가 지금처럼 여기 앉아 있는데, 부활절 때 여길 들렀던 바로 그 장군이 들어오지 뭐예요. 그래서 내가 물었지요. '각하,' 하고 말이에요, '귀부인의 숨결이 불쾌할 수도 있는 건가요?' 그랬더니 이러는 거예요. '그렇소. 창문이나 문을 좀 열어 두는 게 좋겠소. 여긴 공기가 신선하질 않으니.' 다들 그 모양이라니까요! 하지만 내 숨결이 그 사람들과 무슨 상관이람? 죽은 사람한테선 더 고약한 냄새가 나는데! 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '난 공기를 더럽히지 않겠어요. 슬리퍼나 한 켤레 맞춰서 떠나 버릴 테니.' 얘들아, 너희 어미를 탓하지 말아 다오! 니콜라이 일리치, 어쩌면 난 당신을 이렇게도 기쁘게 해 드리지 못한답니까? 학교에서 돌아와 날 사랑해 주는 건 일류샤뿐이에요. 어제는 사과를 하나 갖다 주더군요. 너희 어미를 용서해 다오—가련하고 외로운 인간을 용서해 다오! 어쩌다 내 숨결이 당신한테 불쾌한 것이 되어 버렸을까요?\"\n\n그러더니 그 가엾고 정신이 온전치 못한 여인은 흐느껴 울기 시작했고, 두 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다. 대위가 그녀에게로 달려갔다.\n\n\"여보, 여보, 진정해요! 당신은 외롭지 않아요. 다들 당신을 사랑하고, 다들 당신을 떠받든다오.\" 그는 다시 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추기 시작하더니 다정하게 그녀의 얼굴을 어루만졌고, 식탁의 냅킨을 집어 그녀의 눈물을 닦아 주었다. 알료샤가 보기에는 그의 눈에도 눈물이 고인 듯했다. \"자, 보셨지요, 들으셨지요?\" 그는 그 가엾은 백치를 가리키며 일종의 격분에 휩싸여 알료샤를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"보았고 들었습니다.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지, 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요—저런 작자 앞에서! 저 사람 좀 내버려 둬요!\" 아이가 침대에서 일어나 앉아 이글거리는 눈으로 아버지를 노려보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"제발 그 어릿광대짓 좀 그만둬요, 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 그 바보 같은 익살을 떨어 대면서!\" 바르바라가 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 격하게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이번만은 네 분노가 지당하구나, 바르바라. 어서 서둘러 네 비위를 맞춰 주마. 자, 모자를 쓰시지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저도 제 모자를 쓰겠습니다. 우리 밖으로 나갑시다. 진지하게 드릴 말씀이 있는데, 이 벽 안에서는 안 됩니다. 여기 앉아 있는 이 아이는 제 딸 니나입니다. 소개하는 걸 깜빡했군요. 이 애는 하늘에서 내려온 천사의 화신이지요…… 우리 같은 인간들 사이로 날아 내려온…… 이해하실 수 있다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"저것 봐, 마치 경련이라도 일으킨 듯 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있잖아!\" 바르바라가 분개하여 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그리고 방금 저기서 내게 발을 구르며 날 보고 바보라 한 저 애도, 역시 하늘에서 내려온 천사의 화신이랍니다. 그리고 저 애가 날 그렇게 부르는 데는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있지요. 갑시다, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리 이제 끝장을 봐야 합니다.\"\n\n그러고는 알료샤의 손을 와락 붙잡더니, 그를 방에서 거리로 끌어냈다.\n\n# Chapter VII. And In The Open Air\n\n\"공기야 신선하지만, 우리 집 안은 그 어떤 의미로도 그렇지가 못합니다. 천천히 걸으시지요, 나리. 이렇게 너그럽게 관심을 가져 주시니 기쁠 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 당신께 드릴 중요한 말씀이 있습니다.\" 알료샤가 입을 열었다. \"다만 어떻게 시작해야 할지 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"분명 저한테 무슨 용건이 있으셔서 오셨겠지요. 아무 목적도 없이 이런 사람을 찾아오실 리가 없으니까요. 그저 그 애 일로 불평하러 오신 것이 아니라면 말입니다만, 그건 좀처럼 있을 법한 일이 아니지요. 그건 그렇고, 그 애 이야기인데, 안에서는 도무지 설명드릴 수가 없었습니다만, 여기서라면 그 장면을 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다. 일주일 전만 해도 제 수세미가 좀 더 무성했지요. 제 수염 말씀입니다. 학생 녀석들이 무엇보다도 제 수염을 그렇게 별명으로 부른답니다. 그런데 말입니다, 당신 형님 드미트리 표도로비치께서 제 수염을 잡아끌었지요. 저는 아무 짓도 안 했는데, 그분이 노발대발하시던 차에 마침 저와 마주친 겁니다. 그분은 저를 술집에서 광장으로 끌어내셨는데, 바로 그때 학교에서 아이들이 나오던 참이었고, 그 속에 일류샤도 있었습니다. 그 애는 제가 그런 꼴을 하고 있는 걸 보자마자 저한테 달려왔지요. '아빠.' 그 애가 외쳤습니다. '아빠!' 그 애는 저를 붙들고 끌어안고는 떼어 놓으려 애쓰면서, 저를 붙든 사람한테 소리쳤습니다. '놓아 줘요, 놓아 줘요, 우리 아빠예요, 용서해 줘요!' 그렇습니다, 그 애가 정말로 '용서해 줘요'라고 외쳤지요. 그 애는 그 손을, 바로 그 손을 제 조그만 손으로 움켜쥐고 입을 맞췄습니다…… 저는 그 순간 그 애의 조그만 얼굴을 기억합니다, 잊지 않았고 앞으로도 영영 잊지 못할 겁니다!\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"제 형님은 같은 그 광장에서 무릎을 꿇는 한이 있더라도 가장 깊고 진실한 뉘우침을 표할 겁니다…… 제가 그렇게 시키겠습니다, 그러지 않는다면 제 형이 아니지요!\"\n\n\"아하, 그러니까 그건 그저 제안에 불과한 거로군요! 그것도 그분에게서 나온 게 아니라 그저 당신의 따뜻한 마음씨의 너그러움에서 나온 거고요. 진작 그렇게 말씀하셨어야지요. 아니지요, 그렇다면 당신 형님이 보여 주신 더없이 기사도적이고 군인다운 너그러움에 대해 제가 말씀드리도록 해 주십시오. 그분은 그때 정말로 그것을 드러내 보이셨으니까요. 그분은 제 수염을 잡아끌던 것을 그만두고 저를 놓아주셨습니다. '너도 장교고,' 그분이 말씀하셨지요. '나도 장교다. 만약 입회인이 되어 줄 점잖은 사람을 구할 수 있거든 나한테 결투장을 보내라. 네놈이 비록 악당이지만 결투에 응해 주마.' 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. 과연 기사도적인 정신이지요! 저는 일류샤를 데리고 물러났습니다만, 그 장면은 일류샤의 영혼에 영영 각인된 우리 집안의 기록이 되었습니다. 아니지요, 우리 같은 사람이 귀족의 특권을 주장할 처지는 못 됩니다. 직접 헤아려 보십시오. 방금 우리 저택에 들어와 보셨으니, 거기서 무엇을 보셨습니까? 여자 셋인데, 하나는 절름발이에 정신이 약하고, 또 하나는 절름발이에 곱사등이고, 셋째는 절름발이는 아니지만 지나치게 영리하지요. 그 애는 여학생인데, 페테르부르크로 돌아가서 네바 강변에서 러시아 여성 해방을 위해 일하고 싶어 안달이 나 있답니다. 일류샤 이야기는 하지 않겠습니다, 그 애는 겨우 아홉 살이니까요. 저는 이 세상에 혼자고, 제가 죽으면 저 사람들 모두는 어떻게 되겠습니까? 저는 그저 그것을 여쭙는 겁니다. 제가 그분에게 결투를 신청하고 그분이 그 자리에서 저를 죽인다면, 그땐 어떻게 됩니까? 저 사람들은 어떻게 되느냔 말입니다. 그보다 더 나쁘게는, 그분이 저를 죽이지 않고 그저 불구로만 만든다면요. 일도 못 하면서 여전히 먹여 살려야 할 입은 하나가 될 테지요. 누가 그 입을 먹이고, 누가 저들 모두를 먹입니까? 일류샤를 학교에서 빼내 길거리로 구걸을 보내야 하겠습니까? 제가 그분에게 결투를 신청한다는 건 저로서는 그런 의미입니다. 그건 어리석은 소리에 지나지 않지요.\"\n\n\"그분은 당신의 용서를 빌 겁니다, 광장 한복판에서 당신의 발치에 엎드려 절을 할 겁니다.\" 알료샤가 다시 두 눈을 빛내며 외쳤다.\n\n\"고소를 해 볼까도 생각했습니다.\" 이등 대위가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 우리 법전을 들여다보십시오, 제가 신체상의 상해로 무슨 보상을 많이 받을 수 있겠습니까? 게다가 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 저를 불러서는 소리를 질러 댔지요. '그런 꿈은 꾸지도 마! 그 사람을 걸고넘어졌다간, 네가 부정직해서 그가 너를 때렸다고 온 세상에 떠벌릴 테니까, 그럼 너야말로 고소를 당할 거다.' 그 부정직이 누구의 것이었으며 누구의 명령으로 제가 그 짓을 했는지, 하느님을 증인으로 세워 묻고 싶습니다. 바로 그 여자와 표도르 파블로비치의 명령이 아니었습니까? '한 가지 더,' 그 여자가 말을 이었지요. '아주 영영 너를 내쫓을 테니 다시는 나한테서 한 푼도 못 벌 줄 알아라. 내 상인한테도 말해 둘 거다.' (그 여자는 자기 영감을 그렇게 부른답니다.) '그럼 그이도 너를 내쫓을 테니까!' 만약 그 영감이 저를 내쫓으면, 그땐 제가 누구한테서 무엇을 벌 수 있겠습니까? 제가 기댈 데라곤 그 두 사람뿐입니다. 당신네 표도르 파블로비치께서는 다른 이유로 저를 부리는 일을 그만두셨을 뿐 아니라, 제가 서명한 서류를 가지고 저를 상대로 소송을 걸 작정이시니까요. 그래서 저는 잠자코 있었던 겁니다, 그리고 우리 피난처를 보신 거고요. 그런데 이제 제가 여쭙겠습니다. 일류샤가 당신 손가락을 많이 다치게 했습니까? 그 애 앞에서는 우리 저택에서 그 이야기를 꺼내기가 싫었습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 아주 심하게요, 그리고 그 애는 굉장히 격분해 있었습니다. 그 애는 저를 카라마조프로 여기고 당신의 원수를 갚은 거지요, 이제 알겠습니다. 하지만 그 애가 학교 친구들한테 돌을 던지는 걸 당신이 보셨더라면! 굉장히 위험합니다. 그 애를 죽일 수도 있어요. 다들 어린아이고 어리석으니까요. 돌이 날아가서 누군가의 머리를 깨뜨릴 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그런 일이 벌어졌지요. 오늘 그 애가 돌에 맞아 멍이 들었습니다. 머리는 아니고 가슴에, 심장 바로 위에요. 울고 신음하며 집으로 돌아왔고, 지금은 앓아누워 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 아시겠지만 그 애가 먼저 공격합니다. 당신 때문에 그 애가 그 애들한테 독이 올라 있는 거지요. 얼마 전에는 크라소트킨이라는 아이를 주머니칼로 찔렀다고들 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그 이야기도 들었습니다, 위험한 일이지요. 크라소트킨은 여기 관리니까, 그 일로 무슨 말이 더 나올지도 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 권해 드리고 싶은 건,\" 알료샤가 따뜻하게 말을 이었다. \"그 애가 좀 더 진정되고…… 분노가 가라앉을 때까지 당분간은 아예 학교에 보내지 않으시는 게…….\"\n\n\"분노라!\" 이등 대위가 되뇌었다. \"바로 그겁니다. 그 애는 조그만 녀석이지만 그 분노만은 어마어마하지요. 나리, 당신은 모든 걸 알지 못하십니다. 더 말씀드리겠습니다. 그 사건 이래로 아이들이 모두 '수세미' 운운하며 그 애를 놀려 댔습니다. 학생 녀석들이란 무자비한 족속이라, 하나하나 따로 보면 천사 같지만 한데 모이면, 특히 학교에서는 흔히 무자비해지지요. 그 녀석들의 놀림이 일류샤 안의 용맹한 정신을 들쑤셔 놓았습니다. 보통 아이라면, 약한 아들이라면 굴복하고 제 아비를 부끄러워했겠지요, 나리. 하지만 그 애는 그 모든 녀석들에 맞서 제 아비를 위해 일어섰습니다. 제 아비를 위해, 또 진실과 정의를 위해서요. 그 애가 당신 형님의 손에 입 맞추며 '아빠를 용서해 줘요, 용서해 줘요'라고 외쳤을 때 그 애가 겪은 고통이 어떠했는지, 그것은 오직 하느님만이 아십니다. 그리고 그 애의 아비인 저만이 알지요. 우리 아이들은, 당신네 아이들이 아니라 우리 아이들, 모든 사람에게 멸시받는 가난한 신사들의 아이들은, 나리, 아홉 살 나이에도 정의가 무엇을 뜻하는지 안답니다. 부자들이 어찌 그걸 알겠습니까? 그들은 평생 단 한 번도 그런 깊은 데를 들여다보지 않지요. 하지만 광장에서 그 손에 입을 맞추던 그 순간, 그 순간 제 일류샤는 정의가 무엇을 뜻하는지를 죄다 깨우쳤습니다. 그 진실이 그 애 속으로 파고들어 그 애를 영영 짓눌러 버렸지요, 나리.\" 이등 대위는 다시 일종의 광란에 휩싸여 뜨겁게 말하며, 마치 '진실'이 어떻게 일류샤를 짓눌렀는지를 보여 주려는 듯 오른 주먹으로 왼 손바닥을 내리쳤다. \"바로 그날, 나리, 그 애는 열병으로 앓아누워 밤새 헛소리를 했습니다. 그날 온종일 저한테는 거의 한마디도 하지 않았습니다만, 그 애가 구석에서 줄곧 저를 지켜보고 있는 걸 알아챘지요. 창문 쪽으로 돌아앉아 공부를 하는 척했지만 말입니다. 하지만 그 애의 마음이 공부에 있지 않다는 걸 저는 알 수 있었습니다. 이튿날 저는 제 시름을 잊으려고 술에 취했지요, 저야 죄 많은 인간이니까요, 그래서 별로 기억나는 게 없습니다. 엄마도 울기 시작했고요. 저는 엄마를 무척 아낍니다. 어쨌든 저는 제 시름을 술에 빠뜨리느라 마지막 한 푼까지 다 썼습니다. 그 일로 저를 멸시하지는 마십시오, 나리, 러시아에서는 술 마시는 사람들이 가장 좋은 사람들이지요. 우리네 가운데 가장 좋은 사람들이 가장 지독한 술꾼들이랍니다. 저는 드러누웠고, 일류샤에 대해서는 아무것도 기억나지 않습니다, 그날 온종일 아이들이 학교에서 그 애를 비웃었는데도 말입니다. '수세미야,' 그 녀석들이 소리쳤지요. '네 아비가 수세미를 잡혀 술집에서 끌려 나왔지, 그래서 너는 달려가서 용서를 빌었고.'\"\n\n\"사흘째 되던 날 그 애가 학교에서 돌아왔을 때, 그 애의 얼굴이 창백하고 처참해 보이는 걸 알았습니다. '무슨 일이냐?' 제가 물었지요. 그 애는 대답하려 들지 않았습니다. 그런데 우리 저택에서는 엄마와 딸들이 끼어들지 않고서는 무슨 이야기도 할 수가 없지요. 게다가 딸들은 바로 그 첫날 그 일을 들어 알고 있었습니다. 바르바라가 으르렁대기 시작했지요. '바보 같고 어릿광대 같은 인간들, 도대체 이치에 맞는 짓이라곤 한 가지도 못 하는 거예요?' '그래, 그렇구나,' 제가 말했지요. '우리가 도대체 이치에 맞는 짓을 한 가지라도 할 수 있겠느냐?' 그때는 그렇게 얼버무리고 넘어갔습니다. 그래서 저녁이 되자 저는 그 애를 데리고 산책을 나갔지요, 아시다시피 우리는 저녁마다 산책을 하니까요, 늘 똑같은 길로, 지금 우리가 걷고 있는 이 길로요. 우리 집 대문에서 저기 길가 울타리 밑에 홀로 놓여 있는 저 커다란 돌까지인데, 그 돌이 마을 목초지가 시작되는 데를 표시하지요. 아름답고 한적한 곳입니다, 나리. 일류샤와 저는 여느 때처럼 손을 맞잡고 걸었습니다. 그 애 손은 조그맣고 손가락은 가늘고 차갑지요. 아시다시피 가슴앓이를 하니까요. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '아빠!' '왜 그러냐?' 제가 말했지요. 그 애의 두 눈이 번득이는 게 보였습니다. '아빠, 그 사람이 그때 아빠한테 어떻게 했어요!' '어쩔 수 없는 일이란다, 일류샤.' 제가 말했지요. '아빠, 그 사람을 용서하지 마요, 용서하지 마요! 학교에서 그러는데, 그 사람이 그 대가로 아빠한테 십 루블을 줬다던데요.' '아니다, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '나는 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테서 돈을 받지 않는다.' 그러자 그 애는 온몸을 떨기 시작하더니 제 손을 제 두 손으로 잡고 다시 입을 맞췄습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '아빠, 그 사람한테 결투를 신청해요, 학교에서 그러는데 아빠가 겁쟁이라 결투를 신청하지 못하고, 그 사람한테서 십 루블을 받을 거라던데요.' '나는 그 사람한테 결투를 신청할 수 없단다, 일류샤.' 제가 대답했지요. 그러고는 방금 당신께 말씀드린 것을 간단히 들려줬습니다. 그 애는 듣고 있었지요. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '그래도 어쨌든 그걸 용서하지는 마요. 제가 크면 직접 그 사람을 불러내서 죽여 버릴 거예요.' 그 애의 두 눈이 빛나며 이글거렸습니다. 물론 저는 그 애의 아비이니, 한마디 끼워 넣어야 했지요. '사람을 죽이는 건 죄란다,' 제가 말했지요. '결투에서라도 말이다.' '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '제가 크면 그 사람을 때려눕히고, 그 사람 손에서 칼을 쳐서 떨어뜨리고, 그 사람한테 덤벼들어 그 사람 위로 칼을 휘두르며 말할 거예요. \"내가 너를 죽일 수도 있지만 용서해 주마, 자, 봐라!\"' 그 애의 조그만 머릿속에서 이 이틀 동안 어떤 일들이 벌어졌는지 아시겠지요. 그 애는 온종일 그 복수를 궁리하고, 밤이면 그 일로 헛소리를 했던 게 틀림없습니다.\n\n\"그런데 그 애가 학교에서 호되게 얻어맞고 돌아오기 시작했습니다, 그제야 그 사실을 알았지요, 그래서 당신 말씀이 옳습니다, 다시는 그 애를 그 학교에 보내지 않겠습니다. 들어 보니 그 애가 온 학급에 홀로 맞서 그 녀석들 모두에게 대들었고, 그 애 가슴이 원한과 쓰라림으로 가득 차 있다는 거였습니다. 저는 그 애가 걱정스러웠지요. 우리는 또 산책을 나갔습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 물었지요. '부자들이 세상 누구보다도 더 힘이 세요?' '그렇단다, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '세상에 부자보다 더 힘센 사람은 없단다.' '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '저는 부자가 될 거예요, 장교가 되어서 모두를 정복할 거예요. 황제께서 저한테 상을 내리실 거고, 저는 여기로 돌아올 거예요, 그러면 아무도 감히…….' 그러고는 입을 다물었는데, 그 애 입술은 여전히 떨리고 있었습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했지요. '이 동네는 정말 끔찍한 동네예요.' '그래, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '그리 좋은 동네는 못 되지.' '아빠, 우리 다른 동네로, 좋은 동네로 이사 가요,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '사람들이 우리를 모르는 데로요.' '이사 가자, 가고말고, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '다만 그러려면 돈을 모아야 한단다.' 저는 그 애의 마음을 괴로운 생각에서 돌려놓을 수 있게 되어 기뻤고, 우리는 어떻게 다른 동네로 이사 갈지, 어떻게 말과 수레를 살지 꿈꾸기 시작했습니다. '엄마랑 네 누나들을 안에 태우고, 덮어 주고, 우리는 걷는 거다, 너는 가끔씩 태워 주고, 나는 옆에서 걷고 말이다, 우리 말을 아껴야 하니까 다 같이 탈 수는 없거든. 그렇게 가는 거란다.' 그 애는 거기에 푹 빠졌지요, 무엇보다도 말을 갖고 그걸 몬다는 생각에 말입니다. 물론 러시아 아이라면 말들 속에서 태어나는 법이니까요. 우리는 한참 동안 재잘거렸습니다. 하느님, 감사합니다, 저는 생각했지요, 제가 그 애의 마음을 돌려놓고 위로해 주었구나, 하고요.\n\n\"그게 그제 저녁의 일이었습니다만, 어젯밤 모든 게 달라졌습니다. 그 애는 아침에 학교에 갔다가 풀이 죽어서, 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어서 돌아왔지요. 저녁에 저는 그 애 손을 잡고 산책을 나갔습니다. 그 애는 말을 하려 들지 않았지요. 바람이 불고 해는 없었고, 가을 기운이 느껴졌습니다. 땅거미가 내리고 있었지요. 우리는 둘 다 풀이 죽은 채 걸었습니다. '얘야,' 제가 말했지요. '우리 여행을 떠나는 건 어떠냐?' 그 애를 어제 했던 이야기로 다시 끌어들일 수 있을까 싶어서요. 그 애는 대답하지 않았지만, 제 손 안에서 그 애의 손가락이 떨리는 게 느껴졌습니다. 아, 이거 큰일이구나, 무언가 새로 생긴 게 있구나, 저는 생각했지요. 우리는 지금 우리가 있는 이 돌까지 다다랐습니다. 저는 돌 위에 앉았지요. 그리고 하늘에는 펄럭이며 빙빙 도는 연이 잔뜩 떠 있었습니다. 눈에 보이는 것만 서른 개는 됐지요. 물론 마침 연 날리는 철이니까요. '봐라, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '우리도 작년 연을 다시 꺼낼 때가 됐구나. 내가 고쳐 줄 테니, 어디다 치워 뒀느냐?' 그 애는 아무 대답이 없었습니다. 그 애는 눈길을 돌리고 제게서 옆으로 비스듬히 돌아앉았지요. 그러더니 한 줄기 바람이 모래를 불어 올렸습니다. 그 애는 별안간 제게 쓰러지듯 안기며 두 팔로 제 목을 감고는 저를 꽉 붙들었습니다. 아시다시피, 아이들이 말없이 자존심을 부리며 큰 고통 속에서도 눈물을 억누르려 애쓰다가 갑자기 무너질 때, 그 눈물은 줄기를 이루어 흘러내리지요. 그 따뜻한 눈물 줄기로 그 애는 별안간 제 얼굴을 적셨습니다. 그 애는 흐느끼며 마치 발작이라도 일어난 듯 떨었고, 돌 위에 앉은 제게 바짝 매달렸지요. '아빠,' 그 애는 계속 외쳤습니다. '아빠, 그 사람이 아빠를 얼마나 모욕했어요!' 그래서 저도 흐느꼈지요. 우리는 서로의 팔에 안겨 떨면서 앉아 있었습니다. '일류샤,' 제가 그 애한테 말했지요. '일류샤, 내 사랑.' 그때 아무도 우리를 보지 못했습니다. 오직 하느님만이 우리를 보셨지요, 하느님께서 그것을 제 공으로 적어 두시기를 바랍니다. 당신은 당신 형님께 감사드려야 합니다, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 아니지요, 나리, 저는 당신을 만족시켜 드리려고 제 아들을 매질하지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n그는 처음의 원한 어린 어릿광대 같은 어조로 되돌아가 있었다. 그래도 알료샤는 그가 자신을 믿고 있으며, 만약 자신, 곧 알료샤의 자리에 다른 누군가가 있었더라면 이 사내가 그토록 솔직하게 말하지도, 방금 말한 것을 들려주지도 않았으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 이것이 알료샤에게 용기를 주었으니, 그의 가슴은 눈물이 솟구치기 직전의 벼랑에서 떨리고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 제가 당신 아드님과 어떻게든 친해지고 싶습니다!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 주선해 주실 수 있다면…….\"\n\n\"그야 물론이지요, 나리.\" 이등 대위가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그런데 이제 전혀 다른 이야기를 들어 주십시오!\" 알료샤가 말을 이었다. \"당신께 전할 말이 있습니다. 바로 그 제 형 드미트리가 자기 약혼녀까지 모욕했습니다, 당신도 아마 들어 보셨을 고결한 마음씨를 지닌 아가씨지요. 저는 그분이 입은 부당함을 당신께 말씀드릴 권리가 있습니다, 아니 사실은 그래야 마땅하지요. 당신이 당한 모욕에 대해 듣고 당신의 불행한 처지를 죄다 알게 되자, 그분은 당장, 바로 방금 저더러 자기가 보내는 이 도움을 당신께 전해 달라 부탁하셨으니까요. 다만 오직 그분 한 분에게서지, 그분을 저버린 드미트리에게서가 아닙니다. 그분의 형제인 저에게서도, 그 누구에게서도 아니고, 오직 그분, 오직 그분에게서만입니다! 그분은 당신께 자기 도움을 받아 달라고 간청하고 계십니다…… 두 분은 같은 사람에게 모욕을 당하셨지요. 그분은 막 그 사람한테서 비슷한 모욕을, 잔혹함에서 비슷한 모욕을 당하고 나서야 비로소 당신을 떠올리셨습니다. 그분은 불행에 빠진 형제를 도우러 오는 누이처럼 오신 겁니다…… 그분은 저더러 당신을 설득해, 누이에게서 받듯 이 이백 루블을 받아 달라 하셨습니다, 당신이 그토록 궁핍하다는 걸 아시니까요. 아무도 그 일을 알지 못할 테니, 부당한 비방이 생겨날 여지도 없습니다. 여기 이백 루블이 있습니다, 그리고 맹세컨대 당신은 이걸 받으셔야 합니다, 만약 그러지 않으신다면, 이 세상 모든 사람이 서로 적이 되어야 한다는 것이 아니라면 말입니다! 그러나 이 세상에도 형제는 있지요…… 당신은 너그러운 마음을 지니셨습니다…… 당신은 그걸 아셔야 합니다, 꼭 아셔야 해요.\" 그러면서 알료샤는 무지갯빛 새 백 루블짜리 지폐 두 장을 내밀었다.\n\n그때 두 사람은 울타리 가까이 그 커다란 돌 옆에 서 있었고, 근처에는 아무도 없었다. 그 지폐는 이등 대위에게 어마어마한 인상을 주는 듯했다. 그는 흠칫했으나, 처음에는 그저 놀라움 때문이었다. 두 사람의 대화가 이런 결말에 이르리라고는 그가 가장 예상하지 못한 일이었다. 누군가에게서 도움을 받는다는 것은, 그것도 그런 액수를 받는다는 것은, 그의 꿈에서 더없이 멀리 떨어진 일이었던 것이다.\n\n그는 지폐를 받아 들었고, 한동안 거의 아무 대답도 하지 못했으니, 그의 얼굴에는 전혀 새로운 표정이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"저한테 이걸요? 이렇게 많은 돈을, 이백 루블이나! 맙소사! 아니, 지난 사 년 동안 이렇게 많은 돈은 구경도 못 했는데! 우리에게 자비를 베푸시는군요! 그런데 그분이 누이라 하셨다고요…… 그게 사실입니까?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 건 죄다 사실이라고 맹세합니다.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n이등 대위의 얼굴이 빨갛게 달아올랐다.\n\n\"들어 보십시오, 친애하는 분, 들어 보십시오. 제가 이걸 받으면, 악당처럼 구는 게 아니겠지요? 당신 눈에, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 악당으로 보이지는 않겠지요? 아니지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 들어 보십시오, 들어 보세요.\" 그는 두 손으로 알료샤를 만지며 서둘러 말했다. \"당신은 저더러 이걸 받으라고 설득하면서 누이가 보내는 것이라 하시지만, 속으로는, 당신 마음속으로는 제가 이걸 받으면 저를 경멸하시지 않겠습니까, 예?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아닙니다, 제 구원을 걸고 맹세컨대 그러지 않겠습니다! 그리고 저 말고는 아무도 알지 못할 겁니다. 저와 당신과 그분, 그리고 그분의 절친한 벗인 다른 한 부인뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"그 부인이야 아무래도 좋습니다! 들어 보십시오, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이런 순간에는 들어 주셔야 합니다, 당신은 이 이백 루블이 지금 저한테 어떤 의미인지 이해하실 수 없으니까요.\" 이 가엾은 사내는 차츰 일종의 두서없는, 거의 광적인 흥분으로 빠져들었다. 그는 평정을 잃고, 마치 하고 싶은 말을 다 하도록 허락받지 못할까 두려운 듯 몹시 빠르게 지껄였다.\n\n\"그토록 존경받고 우러름받는 '누이'에게서 정직하게 받은 것이라는 점은 둘째 치고, 이제 제가 엄마와, 곱사등이 천사 같은 제 딸 니나를 돌볼 수 있다는 걸 아십니까? 헤르첸슈투베 박사가 친절한 마음에서 저를 찾아와 둘을 꼬박 한 시간이나 진찰해 주셨지요. '도무지 알 수가 없군요.' 그분이 말씀하셨지만, 여기 약방에서 파는 광천수를 처방해 주셨습니다. 그게 틀림없이 그 애한테 좋을 거라 하시며, 무슨 약을 탄 목욕도 처방해 주셨고요. 광천수는 삼십 코페이카인데, 아마 마흔 병은 마셔야 할 겁니다. 그래서 저는 그 처방전을 받아다가 성상 아래 선반에 얹어 두었고, 거기 그대로 놓여 있지요. 그리고 니나에게는 무언가 녹인 물로 아침저녁 더운 목욕을 하라 하셨습니다. 하지만 하인도 없고, 도와줄 사람도 없고, 욕조도 없고, 물도 없는 우리 저택에서 어떻게 그런 치료를 한단 말입니까? 니나는 온몸이 류머티즘이지요, 그 이야긴 안 드린 것 같군요. 밤이면 오른쪽 옆구리가 죄다 쑤셔서 그 애는 괴로워 몸부림칩니다, 그런데 믿어지십니까, 그 천사는 우리를 깨울까 봐 신음 소리 한 번 내지 않고 그걸 견딘답니다. 우리는 구할 수 있는 걸 먹는데, 그 애는 남은 찌꺼기만, 개한테도 좀처럼 주지 않을 만한 것만 먹으려 들지요. '저는 그럴 자격이 없어요, 저는 식구들 것을 뺏어 먹는 거예요, 저는 짐이에요.' 그 애의 천사 같은 눈은 그렇게 말하려고 합니다. 우리가 그 애 시중을 들면 그 애는 그걸 싫어하지요. '저는 쓸모없는 불구라, 누구한테도 도움이 안 돼요.' 마치 그럴 자격이 없는 것처럼 말입니다, 그 애야말로 그 천사 같은 다정함으로 우리 모두를 구원하고 있는데도요. 그 애가 없다면, 그 애의 부드러운 말 한마디가 없다면 우리 사이는 지옥이 될 겁니다! 그 애는 바르바라마저 누그러뜨립니다. 그리고 바르바라도 모질게 보지는 말아 주십시오, 그 애도 천사니까요, 그 애 역시 부당한 일을 겪었지요. 그 애는 여름을 나러 우리한테 왔는데, 과외로 벌어 모은 십육 루블을 가지고 와서는, 구월에, 그러니까 지금쯤 그걸 가지고 페테르부르크로 돌아가려 했지요. 그런데 우리가 그 애 돈을 받아 그걸로 먹고살았으니, 이제 그 애는 돌아갈 밑천이 없는 겁니다. 하기야 사실 그 애는 돌아갈 수도 없지요, 우리를 위해 노예처럼 일해야 하니까요. 그 애는 우리 모두를 등에 짊어진, 혹사당하는 말 같은 신세입니다. 그 애는 우리 모두 시중을 들고, 깁고 빨래하고, 마루를 쓸고, 엄마를 잠자리에 뉘지요. 그리고 엄마는 변덕스럽고 눈물 많고 제정신이 아니랍니다! 그런데 이제 이 돈으로 하인을 둘 수 있고, 아시겠습니까, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그 사랑스러운 식구들에게 약을 구해 줄 수 있고, 제 여학생을 페테르부르크로 보낼 수 있고, 쇠고기를 살 수 있고, 그 애들을 제대로 먹일 수 있는 겁니다. 맙소사, 이건 꿈만 같군요!\"\n\n알료샤는 자신이 그에게 이런 행복을 가져다주었다는 것과, 이 가엾은 사내가 행복해지기로 마음먹어 주었다는 것이 기뻤다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 잠깐만요.\" 이등 대위는 새로운 백일몽에 휩쓸려 미친 듯이 빠른 속도로 지껄이기 시작했다. \"아십니까, 일류샤와 저는 어쩌면 정말로 우리 꿈을 이룰지도 모릅니다. 우리는 말과 수레를, 검은 말을 살 겁니다, 그 애는 검은 말이어야 한다고 우기지요, 그리고 며칠 전에 흉내만 냈던 그대로 떠나는 겁니다. 저한테 오랜 벗이 하나 있는데, K현의 변호사지요, 그리고 믿을 만한 사람을 통해 들었는데, 제가 가기만 하면 자기 사무실에 서기 자리를 하나 주겠다더군요, 그러니 누가 압니까, 어쩌면 정말 그럴지도 모르지요. 그러면 엄마와 니나를 수레에 태우고, 일류샤가 몰면, 저는 걷는 겁니다, 걷는 거지요…… 아니, 저한테 빚진 돈 한 가지만 받아 낼 수 있다면, 어쩌면 그것까지 할 만큼 돈이 모일지도 모릅니다!\"\n\n\"그것만으로도 충분할 겁니다!\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"카테리나 이바노브나가 당신께 필요한 만큼 더 보내 드릴 테고, 그리고 아시다시피 저한테도 돈이 있으니, 필요한 만큼 가져가십시오, 형제에게서 받듯, 벗에게서 받듯이요, 나중에 갚으셔도 됩니다…… (당신은 부자가 될 겁니다, 부자가 될 거예요!) 그리고 아시다시피 다른 현으로 이사 가는 것보다 더 나은 생각은 없을 겁니다! 그게 당신을, 특히 당신 아드님을 구하는 길일 테니까요. 그리고 서둘러 가셔야 합니다, 겨울이 오기 전에, 추위가 닥치기 전에요. 거기 도착하시거든 우리한테 편지를 보내 주세요, 그러면 우리는 언제까지나 형제일 겁니다…… 아니, 이건 꿈이 아니에요!\"\n\n알료샤는 어찌나 기쁜지 그를 끌어안고 싶을 지경이었다. 그러나 그를 흘낏 보고는 멈칫했다. 사내는 목을 길게 빼고 입술을 쑥 내민 채, 창백하고 광기 어린 얼굴로 서 있었다. 그의 입술은 무언가를 발음하려는 듯 움직이고 있었으나 아무 소리도 나지 않았고, 그래도 입술은 여전히 움직였다. 섬뜩한 광경이었다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 알료샤가 흠칫하며 물었다.\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치…… 저는…… 당신을.\" 이등 대위가 더듬거리며 중얼거렸는데, 기이하고 사납고 못 박힌 듯한 눈길로, 절망적인 결의를 띤 기색으로 그를 바라보았다. 그와 동시에 그의 입술에는 일종의 비웃음 같은 것이 어렸다. \"저는…… 당신께, 나리…… 제가 아는 자그마한 재주를 하나 보여 드릴까요?\" 그가 별안간, 더는 더듬지 않는 단호하고 빠른 속삭임으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"무슨 재주요?\"\n\n\"근사한 재주지요.\" 이등 대위가 속삭였다. 그의 입은 왼쪽으로 비뚤어졌고, 왼쪽 눈은 가늘게 찡그려졌다. 그는 여전히 알료샤를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까? 무슨 재주요?\" 이제 완전히 겁에 질린 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 보십시오.\" 이등 대위가 별안간 새된 소리를 지르더니, 대화 내내 엄지와 검지로 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡고 있던 그 두 장의 지폐를 그에게 내보이고는, 사납게 구겨서 오른손에 꽉 움켜쥐었다. \"보이지요, 보이지요?\" 그가 창백하게 격분하여 비명을 질렀다. 그러더니 별안간 손을 치켜들어 그 구겨진 지폐를 모래밭에 내던졌다. \"보이지요?\" 그가 그것을 가리키며 다시 비명을 질렀다. \"저기 보세요!\"\n\n그러고는 사나운 광기에 휩싸여 그것을 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟기 시작했고, 그러면서 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"네 돈은 이런 거다! 네 돈은 이런 거야! 네 돈은 이런 거다! 네 돈은 이런 거야!\"\n\n별안간 그는 펄쩍 뒤로 물러나 알료샤 앞에 꼿꼿이 섰는데, 그의 온몸이 이루 말할 수 없는 자존심을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"당신을 보낸 사람들한테 전하시오, 수세미는 제 명예를 팔지 않는다고!\" 그가 한 팔을 허공에 치켜들며 외쳤다. 그러고는 휙 돌아서서 달리기 시작했으나, 다섯 걸음도 채 못 가서 완전히 몸을 돌려 알료샤를 향해 제 손에 입을 맞춰 보였다. 그는 다시 다섯 걸음을 달리더니 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아섰다. 이번에 그의 얼굴은 웃음으로 일그러진 것이 아니라, 온통 눈물로 떨고 있었다. 그는 눈물에 젖어 더듬거리며 흐느끼는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리가 당한 치욕의 대가로 당신한테서 돈을 받는다면, 제 아들한테 뭐라고 말하란 말이오?\"\n\n그러고는 그는 돌아보지 않고 달려가 버렸다. 알료샤는 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔에 잠겨 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 아, 그는 보았다, 이 사내가 마지막 순간까지도 자신이 그 지폐를 구겨서 내던지리라는 것을 알지 못했음을. 그는 돌아보지 않았다. 알료샤는 그가 돌아보지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 그를 뒤쫓아 가서 불러 세우지 않을 작정이었으니, 왜 그런지도 알고 있었다. 그가 시야에서 사라지자, 알료샤는 그 두 장의 지폐를 주워 들었다. 지폐는 몹시 짓이겨지고 구겨진 데다 모래 속에 짓눌려 있었으나, 상한 데는 없었고 알료샤가 펴서 매만지니 새것처럼 바스락 소리까지 났다. 그는 그것을 다 펴고 나서 접어 주머니에 넣고는, 그분의 심부름을 잘 해냈는지 보고하러 카테리나 이바노브나를 찾아갔다.\n\n# Book V. Pro And Contra Chapter I. The Engagement\n\n호흘라코바 부인이 또다시 알료샤를 가장 먼저 맞이했다. 그녀는 안절부절못하고 있었으니, 무언가 중대한 일이 벌어진 모양이었다. 카테리나 이바노브나의 발작은 까무러침으로 끝이 났고, 그러더니 \"끔찍하고 무서운 탈진 상태가 뒤따라, 두 눈을 위로 치켜뜬 채 누워 헛소리를 했다. 지금은 열이 펄펄 끓고 있다. 게르첸슈투베를 부르러 사람을 보냈고, 고모들도 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 고모들은 벌써 와 있었지만 게르첸슈투베는 아직 오지 않았다. 다들 그녀의 방에 둘러앉아 기다리고 있다. 지금은 의식이 없는데, 혹시 뇌염으로 번지기라도 한다면!\"\n\n호흘라코바 부인은 자못 심각하게 겁에 질린 표정이었다. \"이건 보통 일이 아니에요, 보통 일이 아니라고요.\" 그녀는 마치 그전에 자기에게 일어난 어떤 일도 결코 보통 일이 아니었던 적이 없다는 듯, 말끝마다 이렇게 덧붙였다. 알료샤는 괴로운 마음으로 귀를 기울였고, 자기가 겪은 일을 이야기하려 했으나, 그녀는 첫마디부터 그의 말을 가로막았다. 그녀에게는 들을 겨를이 없었다. 그녀는 그에게 리즈 곁에 앉아 자기를 기다려 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n\"리즈가요.\" 그녀는 거의 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. \"리즈가 방금 저를 깜짝 놀라게 했어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 그 애가 제 마음을 움직여서, 제 마음은 그 애의 모든 걸 용서하고 있답니다. 글쎄, 당신이 가시자마자 그 애가 오늘이랑 어제 당신을 비웃은 걸 두고 진심으로 뉘우치기 시작했지 뭐예요, 사실 비웃은 게 아니라 그저 농담을 한 것뿐인데도요. 그런데 그걸 진지하게 미안해하면서 금방이라도 울 것처럼 굴어서 저는 정말 놀랐답니다. 그 애는 저를 비웃은 일로는 단 한 번도 진심으로 미안해한 적이 없고, 그저 농으로 넘겼거든요. 게다가 그 애는 시도 때도 없이 저를 비웃는답니다. 그런데 이번에는 진심이었어요. 그 애는 당신 의견을 무척 소중히 여기니까, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 되도록이면 그 애한테 마음 상하거나 기분 다치지 말아 주세요. 저는 그 애한테 결코 엄하게 굴지 못한답니다, 어찌나 영리한 아인지요. 글쎄 믿으시겠어요? 그 애가 방금 당신을 두고 자기 어린 시절의 친구라고, '어린 시절 가장 소중한 친구'라고 하지 뭐예요—생각해 보세요—'가장 소중한 친구'라니—그럼 저는 뭐가 되나요? 그 애는 감정도 추억도 아주 강한 데다, 게다가 이렇게 뜻밖의 말들을, 가장 예상치 못한 순간에 불쑥 튀어나오는 그런 표현들을 쓴답니다. 얼마 전엔 가령 소나무 이야기를 했어요. 그 애가 아주 어렸을 적 우리 정원에 소나무 한 그루가 서 있었거든요. 아마 지금도 거기 서 있을 거예요. 그러니 과거형으로 말할 필요는 없겠지요. 소나무는 사람과 달라서요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 빨리 변하질 않으니까요. '엄마,' 그 애가 그러더군요, '나는 이 소나무를 꿈속처럼 기억해.' 그러면서 어찌나 기발한 말을 덧붙였는지 저는 그대로 옮길 수가 없네요. 게다가 잊어버리기도 했고요. 자, 그럼 안녕히! 저는 너무 걱정이 되어서 제정신이 나갈 것만 같아요. 아! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 평생에 두 번이나 정신이 나간 적이 있답니다. 리즈한테 가서 그 애 기운을 북돋아 주세요, 당신은 늘 그렇게 사랑스럽게 해 주시잖아요. 리즈.\" 그녀는 딸의 방문으로 다가가며 소리쳤다. \"네가 그렇게 모욕했던 알렉세이 표도로비치를 데려왔단다. 이분은 조금도 화가 나지 않으셨어, 정말이야. 오히려 네가 그렇게 생각할 수 있다는 데 놀라고 계신단다.\"\n\n\"메르시, 마망. 들어오세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\"\n\n알료샤가 들어갔다. 리즈는 다소 당황한 기색이더니 이내 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔다. 무언가가 부끄러웠던 게 분명했고, 그런 경우에 사람들이 으레 그러듯, 그녀는 곧장 다른 이야기를, 마치 지금 이 순간 자기에게 더없이 흥미진진한 일인 양 떠벌리기 시작했다.\n\n\"엄마가 방금 이백 루블 이야기를 다 해 주셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신이 그 가엾은 장교한테 그 돈을 가져갔다는 거랑요…… 그리고 그 사람이 어떻게 모욕을 당했는지 그 끔찍한 사연도 다 말씀해 주셨고요…… 그런데 있잖아요, 엄마가 이야기를 좀 뒤죽박죽 섞으시긴 해도…… 이것저것 마구 건너뛰시거든요…… 저는 듣다가 울었어요. 그래서, 그 사람한테 돈을 주셨어요? 그 가엾은 사람은 어떻게 지내나요?\"\n\n\"실은 그 사람한테 돈을 주지 못했어요, 그게 긴 이야기랍니다.\" 알료샤는 마치 자기 역시 실패했다는 후회 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없다는 듯 대답했으나, 리즈는 그 역시 시선을 돌리고 있다는 것, 그 역시 다른 이야기를 하려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다.\n\n알료샤는 탁자 앞에 앉아 이야기를 시작했는데, 첫마디를 떼자마자 당혹감을 떨쳐 버리고 리즈의 온 주의마저 사로잡았다. 그는 방금 받은 강렬한 인상에 사로잡혀 깊은 감정을 담아 이야기했고, 자기 이야기를 잘, 그리고 자초지종을 빠짐없이 들려주는 데 성공했다. 모스크바 시절 그는 리즈를 찾아와 자기에게 방금 일어난 일이며 읽은 것이며 어린 시절의 기억을 들려주기를 즐겼다. 때로는 둘이서 함께 공상에 잠겨 온갖 이야기를 엮어 내기도 했는데, 대개는 밝고 즐거운 것들이었다. 이제 두 사람은 문득 이 년 전 모스크바 시절로 되돌아간 듯한 기분이 들었다. 리즈는 그의 이야기에 몹시 감동했다. 알료샤는 일류샤를 따뜻한 마음으로 묘사했다. 그 불운한 사내가 돈을 짓밟던 대목까지 다 이야기하자, 리즈는 두 손을 마주 잡고 외치지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그래서 그 사람한테 돈을 안 주신 거예요! 그래서 그 사람을 그냥 달아나게 두셨다고요! 아, 저런, 그 사람을 뒤쫓아 갔어야죠!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 리즈. 뒤쫓아 가지 않은 게 더 나아요.\" 알료샤는 의자에서 일어나 생각에 잠긴 채 방을 가로질러 거닐며 말했다.\n\n\"어째서요? 어째서 더 낫다는 거예요? 이제 그 사람들은 먹을 것도 없고 처지가 절망적인데요?\"\n\n\"절망적이지 않아요, 이백 루블은 결국 그 사람들 손에 들어갈 테니까요. 내일이면 그 사람이 그 돈을 받을 거예요. 내일이면 틀림없이 받을 겁니다.\" 알료샤는 생각에 잠겨 이리저리 거닐며 말했다. \"보세요, 리즈.\" 그는 갑자기 그녀 앞에 멈춰 서며 말을 이었다. \"내가 실수를 하나 저질렀어요, 그런데 그 실수마저도, 그 실수마저도 다 잘된 일이랍니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 실수인데요, 그리고 왜 잘된 일이라는 거죠?\"\n\n\"말씀드릴게요. 그 사람은 마음이 약하고 겁이 많은 사람이에요. 그렇게 많은 고생을 했으면서도 무척 선량하답니다. 나는 그 사람이 왜 그렇게 갑작스레 모욕감을 느꼈는지 자꾸만 생각하게 돼요, 정말이지 마지막 순간까지도 그 사람은 자기가 그 지폐를 짓밟으리라고는 몰랐거든요. 그리고 지금 생각해 보니 그 사람을 상하게 할 만한 일이 한두 가지가 아니었어요…… 그 사람 처지에서는 달리 될 수도 없었고요…… 우선 그 사람은 내 앞에서 그 돈을 그토록 기뻐하면서 그 기쁨을 나한테 감추지 못했다는 게 마음에 걸렸던 거예요. 기뻐는 하되 그렇게까지는 아니었더라면, 그걸 내색하지 않았더라면, 다른 사람들이 돈을 받을 때 으레 그러듯 짐짓 사양하는 척, 곤란한 척이라도 했더라면, 그래도 그 돈을 받아 챙길 수 있었을지 몰라요. 그런데 그 사람은 너무도 진심으로 기뻐했고, 바로 그게 굴욕스러웠던 거죠. 아, 리즈, 그 사람은 선하고 진실한 사람이에요—그게 바로 이 모든 일에서 가장 딱한 점이랍니다. 이야기하는 내내 그 사람 목소리는 어찌나 가냘프고 갈라지던지, 말도 어찌나 빠르고 빠르던지, 그렇게 줄곧 웃어 댔는데, 어쩌면 우는 것이었는지도—그래요, 그 사람은 분명 울고 있었어요, 그토록 기뻐서요—그리고 자기 딸들 이야기며—다른 고장에서 얻을 수 있을 일자리 이야기까지 했답니다…… 그렇게 마음을 다 쏟아 내고 나니, 나한테 자기 속마음을 그렇게 다 드러내 보인 게 부끄러워진 거예요. 그래서 단번에 나를 미워하기 시작한 거죠. 그 사람은 끔찍이도 예민한 가난한 사람들 가운데 하나예요. 그 사람을 가장 부끄럽게 만든 건, 너무 빨리 마음을 내주고 나를 친구로 받아들였다는 거였어요, 아시겠어요. 처음에 그 사람은 나한테 거의 덤벼들 듯 위협하려 들었는데, 돈을 보자마자 나를 끌어안기 시작했어요. 자꾸만 두 손으로 나를 만졌죠. 바로 그래서 이 모든 게 그토록 굴욕스럽게 느껴졌던 게 틀림없어요, 그러다 내가 그 실수를, 아주 중대한 실수를 저질렀어요. 만약 다른 고장으로 옮길 만큼 돈이 넉넉지 않으면 우리가 마련해 주겠노라고, 게다가 나 자신이 내 돈에서 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 주겠노라고 불쑥 말해 버렸거든요. 그 말이 단번에 그 사람을 찔렀어요. 어째서, 하고 그 사람은 생각했겠죠, 내가 나서서 자기를 도우려 드는 건가 하고요. 아세요, 리즈, 상처 입은 사람한테는, 다른 사람들이 자기를 마치 은인이라도 되는 양 바라보는 게 끔찍이도 힘든 일이거든요…… 저는 그런 말을 들은 적이 있어요, 조시마 장로님이 그렇게 말씀해 주셨죠. 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠지만, 저 자신도 그런 경우를 자주 봐 왔어요. 그리고 저 역시 그런 기분이 들고요. 그런데 무엇보다 딱한 건, 그 사람이 마지막 순간까지도 자기가 지폐를 짓밟으리라는 걸 몰랐으면서도 어쩐지 그걸 예감하고 있었다는 거예요, 저는 확신해요. 바로 그 예감이 그 사람을 그토록 황홀하게 만든 거죠…… 그리고 비록 이건 끔찍한 일이지만, 다 잘된 일이랍니다. 사실 저는 이보다 더 나은 결말은 없었으리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"왜요, 왜 이보다 더 나은 결말이 없었다는 거죠?\" 리즈가 알료샤를 몹시 놀란 눈으로 바라보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 만약 그 사람이 그 돈을 받았더라면, 집에 돌아가 한 시간도 안 되어 굴욕감에 울고 있었을 테니까요, 꼭 그렇게 됐을 거예요. 그리고 십중팔구 내일 일찍 나한테 찾아와서, 어쩌면 방금처럼 지폐를 내 얼굴에 내던지고 짓밟았겠죠. 그런데 지금은 그 사람이 비록 자기가 '제 신세를 망쳐 버렸다'는 걸 알면서도, 끔찍이도 자랑스럽고 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아갔어요. 그러니 이제 내일이면 그 사람한테 이백 루블을 받아들이게 하는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없을 거예요, 그 사람은 이미 제 명예를 지켜 냈고, 돈을 내팽개쳤고, 발밑에 짓밟아 버렸으니까요…… 그 사람은 그러면서도 내일 내가 그 돈을 다시 가져가리라는 건 몰랐을 테고, 그러면서도 그 돈이 절실히 필요하답니다. 지금은 비록 자기 자신이 자랑스럽겠지만, 오늘 당장에도 자기가 얼마나 큰 도움을 잃었는지 생각하게 될 거예요. 밤이 되면 그 어느 때보다도 그 생각이 더하겠죠, 꿈에서까지 그 생각을 할 테고요, 그러다 내일 아침이면 나한테 달려와 용서를 빌 채비가 되어 있을지도 몰라요. 바로 그때 내가 나타나는 거죠. '자, 당신은 자존심 강한 사람이오.' 하고 내가 말하는 거예요. '그걸 보여 줬으니, 이제는 이 돈을 받고 우리를 용서해 주시오!' 그러면 그 사람은 받을 거예요!\"\n\n마지막 말을 내뱉을 때 알료샤는 기쁨에 도취되어 있었다. \"그러면 그 사람은 받을 거예요!\" 리즈가 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n\"아, 정말 그래요! 이제 완전히 이해했어요. 아, 알료샤, 어떻게 이런 걸 다 아세요? 이렇게 젊은데도 사람 마음속에 뭐가 들었는지 다 아시네요…… 저라면 결코 거기까지 헤아리지 못했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"이제 무엇보다 중요한 건, 우리한테서 돈을 받더라도 그 사람이 우리와 똑같은 처지에 있다는 걸 납득시키는 거예요.\" 알료샤는 흥분하여 말을 이었다. \"그저 똑같은 처지일 뿐 아니라, 오히려 더 높은 처지에 있다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"'더 높은 처지'라니, 참 멋진 말이네요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 계속하세요, 계속요!\"\n\n\"'더 높은 처지'라는 표현 같은 건 없다는 뜻이군요. 하지만 그건 상관없어요, 왜냐하면—\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 물론 상관없죠. 용서하세요, 알료샤…… 있잖아요, 저는 지금까지 당신을 거의 존경하지 않았어요—그러니까 존경하긴 했지만 똑같은 처지에서 존경했던 거예요. 그런데 이제부터는 더 높은 처지에서 당신을 존경하기 시작할 거예요. 화내지 마세요, 제가 농담한다고요.\" 그녀는 곧 강한 감정을 담아 덧붙였다. \"저는 우습고 하찮은 사람인데, 당신은, 당신은요! 들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 우리의 이 모든 분석에는—그러니까 당신의 분석에는…… 아니, 차라리 우리의 것이라고 하는 게 낫겠네요—우리가 그 사람을, 그 가엾은 사람을 경멸하고 있는 건 아닐까요—그 사람의 영혼을 이렇게, 말하자면 위에서 내려다보며 분석하고 있으니 말이에요, 네? 그 사람이 돈을 받으리라고 그토록 확신에 차서 단정해 버리는 게요?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 리즈, 그건 경멸이 아니에요.\" 알료샤는 마치 그 물음에 미리 대비라도 한 듯 대답했다. \"저도 여기 오는 길에 그 생각을 했어요. 우리가 다 그 사람과 같은데, 우리가 다 그 사람과 똑같은데, 어떻게 그게 경멸일 수 있겠어요? 우린 정말로 똑같으니까요, 조금도 더 낫지 않아요. 설령 더 낫다 해도, 그 사람의 처지에 놓였더라면 우리도 똑같았을 거예요…… 리즈, 당신은 어떤지 모르겠지만, 저는 제가 여러모로 비루한 영혼을 지녔다고 여겨요, 그런데 그 사람의 영혼은 비루하지 않아요. 오히려 고운 감정으로 가득 차 있죠…… 아니에요, 리즈, 저는 그 사람을 경멸하지 않아요. 있잖아요, 리즈, 장로님께서 언젠가 제게 말씀하시기를, 대부분의 사람들은 꼭 아이를 돌보듯 보살피고, 더러는 병원의 환자를 돌보듯 보살피라고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리도 환자를 돌보듯 사람들을 보살펴요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 해요, 리즈. 저도 그럴 마음이 있어요. 비록 제 자신부터가 온전히 준비된 건 아니지만요. 저는 어떤 땐 몹시 조바심을 내고, 또 어떤 땐 일을 제대로 보지 못해요. 당신은 다르겠지만요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지 않아요! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 정말 행복해요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주시니 정말 기뻐요, 리즈.\"\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 놀랍도록 선한 분이지만, 가끔은 어딘가 딱딱하세요…… 그런데 사실은 조금도 딱딱하지 않으시고요. 문가로 가서 살그머니 문을 열고, 엄마가 엿듣고 있는지 좀 봐 주세요.\" 리즈가 초조하고 다급한 목소리로 속삭였다.\n\n알료샤는 가서 문을 열어 보고는 아무도 엿듣고 있지 않다고 알렸다.\n\n\"이리 오세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 리즈가 점점 더 빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 이었다. \"손을 주세요—그래요, 됐어요. 저, 큰 고백을 해야겠어요, 어제 당신한테 쓴 편지는 농담이 아니라 진심이었어요.\" 그러고는 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다. 그 고백이 몹시도 부끄러운 게 분명했다.\n\n별안간 그녀는 그의 손을 와락 붙잡더니 충동적으로 세 번이나 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"아, 리즈, 정말 잘됐어요!\" 알료샤가 기쁘게 외쳤다. \"있잖아요, 저는 당신이 진심이라는 걸 진작부터 확신하고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"확신했다고요? 어쩜!\" 그녀는 그의 손을 옆으로 밀어냈으나 놓지는 않은 채, 화끈하게 얼굴을 붉히며 행복한 웃음을 조금 흘렸다. \"내가 손에 입을 맞췄더니 '정말 잘됐어요'라고 하시다니.\"\n\n그러나 그 책망은 부당한 것이었다. 알료샤 역시 가슴이 벅차올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"저는 늘 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶은데, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\" 그도 얼굴을 붉히며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"알료샤, 당신은 차갑고 무뚝뚝해요. 보세요? 이분은 저를 아내로 골라 놓고는 그걸 아주 당연한 일로 여기고 계세요. 제가 진심이었다고 확신하셨다네요. 그런 말이 어디 있어요! 아니, 그건 무례한 거예요—바로 그거라고요.\"\n\n\"아니, 제가 확신한 게 잘못이었나요?\" 알료샤가 별안간 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 알료샤, 오히려 정반대로, 더없이 옳았어요.\" 리즈가 다정하고 행복한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 외쳤다.\n\n알료샤는 그녀의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채 가만히 서 있었다. 별안간 그는 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"어머, 뭐 하시는 거예요?\" 리즈가 외쳤다. 알료샤는 몹시 당황했다.\n\n\"아, 제가 그래선 안 됐다면 용서하세요…… 아마 제가 끔찍이 어리석은가 봐요…… 당신이 제가 차갑다고 하셔서, 그래서 입을 맞춘 건데…… 어리석은 짓이었군요.\"\n\n리즈는 웃음을 터뜨리며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다. \"게다가 그런 옷차림으로!\" 그녀가 웃음 속에서 불쑥 내뱉었다. 그러나 별안간 웃음을 그치더니 진지해졌고, 거의 엄숙해졌다.\n\n\"알료샤, 우리 입맞춤은 미뤄야 해요. 우린 아직 그럴 준비가 안 됐고, 한참을 더 기다려야 할 거예요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 말을 맺었다. \"그보다 말해 주세요, 당신처럼 영리하고, 지성 있고, 통찰력 있는 분이 어째서 저 같은 못난 바보, 환자를 고르시는 거죠? 아, 알료샤, 저는 끔찍이도 행복해요, 저는 당신을 조금도 받을 자격이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"당신은 자격이 있어요, 리즈. 저는 며칠 안으로 수도원을 아주 떠날 거예요. 세상으로 나간다면 결혼해야 해요. 저는 그걸 알아요. 그분도 결혼하라고 하셨고요. 당신보다 더 나은 사람과 어떻게 결혼할 수 있겠어요—그리고 당신 말고 누가 저를 받아 주겠어요? 저는 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요. 우선 당신은 어릴 적부터 저를 알았고, 제게 없는 좋은 점을 아주 많이 지녔어요. 당신은 저보다 마음이 밝고, 무엇보다 저보다 순수해요. 저는 벌써 너무도, 너무도 많은 일과 부딪쳐 왔거든요…… 아, 당신은 모르겠지만, 저 역시 카라마조프랍니다. 당신이 웃고 농담을 한들, 그것도 저를 두고 한들, 그게 무슨 상관이겠어요? 계속 웃으세요. 저는 당신이 웃어서 무척 기쁜걸요. 당신은 어린아이처럼 웃지만, 순교자처럼 생각하니까요.\"\n\n\"순교자처럼요? 어떻게요?\"\n\n\"네, 리즈, 방금 당신이 던진 물음 말이에요. 우리가 그 가엾은 사람의 영혼을 해부하면서 그 사람을 경멸하고 있는 건 아니냐던 그 물음—그건 고통받아 본 사람의 물음이에요…… 있잖아요, 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠지만, 그런 물음을 떠올리는 사람은 누구든 고통을 겪을 줄 아는 사람이에요. 환자용 의자에 앉아 있는 동안 당신은 이미 많은 것을 깊이 생각해 봤을 거예요.\"\n\n\"알료샤, 손을 주세요. 왜 손을 빼시는 거예요?\" 리즈가 행복에 겨워 힘없이 잦아드는 목소리로 중얼거렸다. \"들어 보세요, 알료샤. 수도원에서 나오면 무얼 입으실 거예요? 어떤 양복을요? 웃지 마세요, 화내지도 마시고요, 이건 저한테 정말, 정말 중요한 일이거든요.\"\n\n\"양복은 생각해 보지 않았어요, 리즈. 하지만 당신이 좋아하는 걸로 뭐든 입을게요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 짙은 청색 벨벳 코트에, 하얀 피케 조끼에, 부드러운 회색 펠트 모자를 쓰면 좋겠어요…… 말해 주세요, 제가 편지에 쓴 게 진심이 아니라고 했을 때, 정말로 제가 당신을 마음에 두지 않았다고 믿으셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 믿지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말 못 말리는 분이네요, 구제불능이세요.\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 저는 당신이—저를 마음에 두고 있는 것 같다는 걸 알고 있었지만, 일부러 당신이 저를 마음에 두지 않는다고 믿는 척한 거예요—당신 마음이 한결 편해지도록요.\"\n\n\"그건 더 나빠요! 그 무엇보다 더 나쁘고 또 더 좋아요! 알료샤, 저는 당신이 끔찍이 좋아요. 오늘 아침 당신이 오기 직전에 저는 제 운을 점쳐 봤어요. 당신한테 제 편지를 돌려 달라고 청하기로 마음먹었는데, 만약 당신이 (당신답게) 태연히 그걸 꺼내 제게 건네준다면, 그건 당신이 저를 조금도 사랑하지 않는다는 뜻, 아무 감정도 없고 그저 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 어리석은 소년에 지나지 않는다는 뜻이고, 저는 끝장이라는 뜻이었어요. 그런데 당신이 편지를 집에 두고 오신 덕에 저는 기운이 났답니다. 일부러 두고 오신 거죠, 돌려주지 않으려고, 제가 돌려 달라고 청할 줄 아셨으니까요? 그런 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아, 리즈, 그런 게 조금도 아니에요. 편지는 지금 제게 있어요, 오늘 아침에도 바로 이 주머니에 있었고요. 자, 여기 있어요.\"\n\n알료샤는 웃으며 편지를 꺼내 멀찍이서 그녀에게 보여 주었다.\n\n\"하지만 돌려주지는 않을 거예요. 여기서 보기만 하세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 거짓말을 하신 거네요? 수도사인 당신이 거짓말을 하다니!\"\n\n\"정 그렇게 말씀하신다면, 거짓말을 한 거죠.\" 알료샤도 웃었다. \"당신한테 편지를 돌려주지 않으려고 거짓말을 한 거예요. 이건 제게 무척 소중하거든요.\" 그가 강한 감정을 담아 별안간 덧붙이며 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"앞으로도 늘 그럴 거고, 그 누구한테도 내주지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n리즈는 기쁜 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. \"알료샤.\" 그녀가 다시 중얼거렸다. \"문 좀 봐 주세요. 엄마가 엿듣고 있지 않나요?\"\n\n\"좋아요, 리즈, 봐 드릴게요. 하지만 안 보는 게 낫지 않을까요? 어머님을 그런 비열한 짓이나 할 분이라고 의심할 게 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"비열한 짓이라뇨? 딸을 몰래 엿보는 건 엄마의 권리예요, 비열한 짓이 아니라고요!\" 리즈가 발끈하며 외쳤다. \"분명히 말씀드리는데요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 엄마가 되어서 저 같은 딸을 두게 된다면, 저는 틀림없이 그 애를 몰래 엿볼 거예요!\"\n\n\"정말이에요, 리즈? 그건 옳지 않아요.\"\n\n\"어머, 세상에! 비열한 짓이 거기서 무슨 상관이에요? 만약 엄마가 무슨 흔해 빠진 세속의 잡담이나 엿듣는다면 그건 비열한 짓이겠지만, 자기 딸이 젊은 남자와 단둘이 방에 틀어박혀 있는데…… 들어 보세요, 알료샤, 있잖아요, 저는 우리가 결혼하자마자 당신을 몰래 엿볼 거예요, 그리고 분명히 말해 두는데 당신 편지를 죄다 뜯어서 읽어 볼 거예요, 그러니 미리 단단히 각오해 두세요.\"\n\n\"네, 물론, 정 그러시다면—\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"다만 그건 옳지 않아요.\"\n\n\"아, 어쩜 그렇게 사람을 깔보세요! 알료샤, 우리 결혼 첫날부터 다투진 말아요. 차라리 사실대로 다 말씀드릴게요. 물론 남을 몰래 엿보는 건 아주 나쁜 짓이고, 물론 제가 틀렸고 당신이 옳지만, 그래도 저는 당신을 몰래 엿볼 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 하세요, 아무것도 알아내지 못할 테니까요.\" 알료샤가 웃었다.\n\n\"그리고 알료샤, 제 말에 양보해 주실 거예요? 그것도 정해 둬야 해요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그럴 거예요, 리즈, 틀림없이요, 다만 가장 중요한 일에서는 아니에요. 설령 당신이 제 뜻에 동의하지 않더라도, 저는 가장 중요한 일에서는 제 본분을 다할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그게 옳아요. 하지만 분명히 말씀드리는데 저는 가장 중요한 일에서뿐 아니라, 모든 일에서 당신한테 양보할 마음이 있어요. 그리고 지금 당장 그러기로 맹세할 수도 있어요—모든 일에서, 제 평생토록요!\" 리즈가 열렬히 외쳤다. \"그것도 기꺼이, 기꺼이 할 거예요! 게다가 저는 결코 당신을 몰래 엿보지 않겠다고, 단 한 번도, 당신 편지를 단 한 통도 읽지 않겠다고 맹세하겠어요. 당신이 옳고 제가 틀렸으니까요. 그리고 비록 몰래 엿보고 싶은 유혹에 끔찍이 시달리겠지만, 당신이 그걸 떳떳지 못한 일로 여기시니 저는 그러지 않으리라는 걸 알아요. 이제 당신은 제 양심이니까요…… 들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 요즘 왜 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요—어제도 오늘도요? 당신한테 걱정거리와 골칫거리가 많다는 건 저도 알지만, 그것 말고도 무슨 특별한 슬픔이, 어쩌면 무슨 남모를 슬픔이 있는 것 같아 보여요.\"\n\n\"네, 리즈, 저한테도 남모를 슬픔이 있어요.\" 알료샤가 침울하게 대답했다. \"당신이 그걸 알아챈 걸 보니, 저를 사랑하시는군요.\"\n\n\"무슨 슬픔인데요? 무엇에 관한 거예요? 제게 말해 주실 수 있어요?\" 리즈가 머뭇거리며 애원하듯 물었다.\n\n\"나중에 말씀드릴게요, 리즈—나중에요.\" 알료샤가 멋쩍어하며 말했다. \"지금은 당신이 이해하지 못할지도 몰라요—그리고 어쩌면 저도 그걸 설명하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"당신 형님들이랑 아버님도 당신을 괴롭히고 있다는 걸 알아요.\"\n\n\"네, 형님들도요.\" 알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"저는 당신 형님 이반이 싫어요, 알료샤.\" 리즈가 별안간 말했다.\n\n그는 다소 놀라며 이 말에 주목했으나, 대꾸하지는 않았다.\n\n\"제 형님들은 스스로를 파멸시키고 있어요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"아버지도 그렇고요. 그러면서 다른 사람들까지 함께 파멸시키고 있죠. 그건 며칠 전 파이시 신부님이 말씀하셨듯이 '카라마조프 가의 원초적인 힘'이에요, 거칠고, 고삐 풀린, 땅에 속한 힘이죠. 하느님의 영이 그 힘 위에서 움직이실까요? 그조차 저는 모르겠어요. 저는 다만 저 역시 카라마조프라는 것만 알 뿐이에요…… 제가 수도사라니, 수도사라니! 제가 수도사인가요, 리즈? 방금 당신이 그랬잖아요, 제가 수도사라고요.\"\n\n\"네, 그랬어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 어쩌면 저는 하느님조차 믿지 않는지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"믿지 않으신다고요? 무슨 일이세요?\" 리즈가 조용하고 부드럽게 말했다. 그러나 알료샤는 대답하지 않았다. 이 마지막 말 속에는 너무도 신비롭고, 너무도 주관적인 무언가가 있었으니, 어쩌면 자기 자신에게도 모호하면서도 그를 괴롭히는 것이었다.\n\n\"그런데 이제 그 모든 일에 더하여, 제 벗이자 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분이 떠나려 하세요, 이 땅을 떠나려 하신다고요! 리즈, 제 영혼이 그분과 얼마나 깊이 맺어져 있는지 당신이 안다면! 그러면 저는 홀로 남겨질 거예요…… 저는 당신한테 올게요, 리즈…… 앞으로 우리는 함께할 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 함께해요, 함께! 앞으로 우리는 늘 함께할 거예요, 평생토록요! 들어 보세요, 입을 맞춰 주세요, 제가 허락할게요.\"\n\n알료샤는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 가 보세요. 그리스도께서 함께하시길!\" 그러고는 그를 향해 성호를 그어 주었다. \"그분이 살아 계실 동안 어서 그분께 돌아가세요. 제가 당신을 너무 모질게 붙들었네요. 오늘 그분과 당신을 위해 기도할게요. 알료샤, 우리는 행복해질 거예요! 우리 행복해질까요, 그렇겠죠?\"\n\n\"그러리라 믿어요, 리즈.\"\n\n알료샤는 호흘라코바 부인에게는 들어가지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 그녀에게 작별 인사도 하지 않고 집을 나서려 했다. 그러나 문을 열기가 무섭게 그는 자기 앞에 호흘라코바 부인이 서 있는 것을 발견했다. 알료샤는 첫마디부터 그녀가 일부러 자기를 만나려고 기다리고 있었음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 이건 끔찍한 일이에요. 이건 죄다 어린애 같은 헛소리고 우스꽝스러운 짓이에요. 부디 당신은 꿈에도 생각하지—이건 어리석은 짓이에요, 어리석은 짓일 뿐이라고요!\" 그녀는 대뜸 그를 몰아붙였다.\n\n\"다만 그 애한테는 그런 말씀을 하지 마세요.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"안 그러면 그 애가 속상해할 텐데, 지금 그 애한테는 그게 안 좋으니까요.\"\n\n\"분별 있는 젊은이답게 분별 있는 충고로군요. 그렇다면 당신은 그저 그 애의 병약한 처지를 가엾이 여겨서, 반박해서 그 애를 자극하고 싶지 않아서 맞장구를 쳐 준 것이라고 이해하면 되나요?\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 전혀요. 저는 제가 한 말에 아주 진심이었어요.\" 알료샤가 단호하게 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"그 일을 진지하게 여긴다는 건 불가능해요, 어림도 없는 일이에요, 그리고 무엇보다 저는 두 번 다시 당신을 집에 들이지 않을 거고, 그 애를 멀리 데려가 버릴 거예요, 그건 분명히 알아 두세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어째서요?\" 알료샤가 물었다. \"그건 다 한참 먼 이야기예요. 우린 어쩌면 일 년 반은 더 기다려야 할지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그건 물론 맞는 말이에요, 그러면 일 년 반 동안 당신들은 천 번도 더 다투고 헤어질 시간이 있겠군요. 하지만 저는 너무도 불행해요! 비록 이게 다 헛소리지만, 저한테는 크나큰 타격이라고요. 저는 마치 〈지혜의 슬픔〉 마지막 장면의 파무소프가 된 기분이에요. 당신은 차츠키고, 그 애는 소피야예요, 그리고 글쎄, 저는 당신을 맞으려고 계단으로 달려 내려왔는데, 그 연극에서도 운명적인 장면이 계단에서 벌어지거든요. 저는 다 들었어요, 하마터면 쓰러질 뻔했답니다. 그래서 요즘 그 애의 그 끔찍한 밤이며 발작이 다 이렇게 설명되는 거였어요! 그건 딸한테는 사랑이지만 엄마한테는 죽음이에요. 차라리 저는 당장 무덤에 들어가는 게 낫겠어요. 그리고 그보다 더 중대한 문제가 있는데, 그 애가 쓴 이 편지는 대체 뭐예요? 당장 보여 주세요, 당장요!\"\n\n\"아니요, 그러실 필요 없어요. 말씀해 주세요, 카테리나 이바노브나는 지금 어떤가요? 저는 꼭 알아야 해요.\"\n\n\"그 애는 아직도 헛소리를 하며 누워 있어요, 의식을 되찾지 못했답니다. 고모들이 와 있긴 한데, 한숨이나 내쉬고 거드름이나 피울 뿐 아무것도 하지 않아요. 게르첸슈투베가 왔는데, 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지 저는 그 사람을 어떻게 해야 할지 몰랐다니까요. 하마터면 그 사람을 돌봐 줄 의사를 부를 뻔했어요. 결국 제 마차로 그 사람을 집까지 태워 보냈죠. 그런 마당에 당신까지, 게다가 이 편지까지! 정말이지 일 년 반 동안은 아무 일도 일어날 수 없겠죠. 신성한 모든 것의 이름으로, 당신의 임종을 앞둔 장로님의 이름으로 청하니, 그 편지를 제게 보여 주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저는 그 애의 어미예요. 정 그러시면 손에 쥐고 계세요, 그러면 제가 그렇게 읽을게요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 보여 드리지 않을 거예요. 설령 그 애가 허락한다 해도 그러지 않을 거예요. 내일 다시 올 테니, 원하신다면 그때 여러 가지를 함께 의논해요, 하지만 지금은 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n그러고는 알료샤는 계단을 뛰어 내려가 거리로 나섰다.\n\n# Chapter II. Smerdyakov With A Guitar\n\n그는 정말로 한시도 지체할 수 없었다. 리즈에게 작별 인사를 건네는 동안에도, 형 드미트리를 찾아내려면 무슨 계책이든 써 봐야겠다는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤으니, 형이 분명 자기를 피해 다니고 있었기 때문이다. 시간은 늦어져 거의 세 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다. 알료샤의 온 영혼은 수도원으로, 죽어 가는 그의 성자에게로 향해 있었으나, 드미트리를 만나야 한다는 절박함이 모든 것을 압도했다. 어떤 거대하고 피할 수 없는 파국이 닥쳐오리라는 확신이 시간이 갈수록 알료샤의 마음속에서 더 강해졌다. 그 파국이 무엇인지, 그리고 그 순간 형에게 무어라 말할 것인지는, 어쩌면 그 자신도 분명히 말할 수 없었으리라. \"설령 나의 은인께서 내가 없는 사이에 돌아가시게 된다 해도, 적어도 나는 무언가를 구할 수 있었는데 그러지 않고 지나쳐 집으로 서둘러 가 버렸다는 생각으로 평생 나 자신을 책망하지는 않게 되리라. 내가 마음먹은 대로 한다면, 나는 그분의 위대한 가르침을 따르는 것이다.\"\n\n그의 계획은 형 드미트리의 허를 찌르는 것이었으니, 곧 어제처럼 울타리를 넘어 정원에 들어가 정자에 앉아 있는 것이었다. 알료샤는 생각하기를, 만약 드미트리가 그곳에 없거든 포마에게나 집안의 여인들에게 자기 존재를 알리지 않고, 저녁까지 기다려야 하는 한이 있더라도 정자에 숨어 있겠다는 것이었다. 만약 어제처럼 드미트리가 그루셴카가 오기를 숨어서 기다리고 있다면, 그도 십중팔구 정자로 올 터였다. 그러나 알료샤는 계획의 세세한 부분까지는 별로 생각하지 않은 채, 설령 그날 수도원으로 돌아가지 못하는 한이 있더라도 그대로 실행하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n모든 일이 거침없이 풀려, 그는 어제와 거의 같은 자리에서 울타리를 넘어 아무에게도 들키지 않고 정자로 숨어들었다. 그는 눈에 띄고 싶지 않았다. 집안의 여인도, 혹시 포마가 여기 있다면 그 역시도, 형에게 충성스러워 형의 지시를 따를 터이니, 알료샤가 정원에 들어오는 것을 막거나, 혹은 누가 그를 찾으며 캐묻고 있다고 드미트리에게 일러바칠지도 몰랐다.\n\n정자에는 아무도 없었다. 알료샤는 앉아서 기다리기 시작했다. 그는 정자를 둘러보았는데, 어쩐 일인지 그것이 전보다 훨씬 더 낡아 보였다. 날씨는 어제와 다름없이 화창했건만, 이번에는 초라한 작은 자리로만 보였다. 탁자 위에는 둥근 자국이 남아 있었으니, 어제 브랜디 잔을 엎질러 생긴 것임에 틀림없었다. 따분하게 기다리는 시간에 으레 그렇듯, 어리석고 부질없는 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 떠돌았다. 가령 그는 어째서 다른 자리가 아니라 하필 어제와 똑같은 자리에 앉았을까 하는 생각을 했다. 마침내 그는 몹시 우울해졌다—초조함과 불확실함에 짓눌려 우울해진 것이었다. 그러나 그가 그곳에 앉은 지 십오 분도 채 못 되어, 문득 아주 가까운 어딘가에서 기타를 퉁기는 소리가 들려왔다. 누군가가, 스무 걸음도 채 떨어지지 않은 덤불 어딘가에 앉아 있거나 이제 막 앉은 참이었다. 알료샤는 문득 어제 정자에서 나오던 길에, 왼편 울타리 가까운 덤불 사이로 낡은 초록빛 낮은 정원용 의자를 언뜻 본 것이 떠올랐다. 사람들은 분명 지금 거기에 앉아 있을 터였다. 누구일까?\n\n한 남자의 목소리가 문득 달콤한 가성으로 노래를 부르기 시작했는데, 스스로 기타로 반주를 곁들였다.\n거역할 수 없는 힘으로\n\n나는 내 임에게 묶였네.\n오 주여, 자비를 베푸소서\n\n그녀와 나에게!\n그녀와 나에게!\n\n그녀와 나에게!\n목소리가 그쳤다. 그것은 하인의 테너였고 하인의 노래였다. 또 다른 목소리, 여자의 목소리가 문득 알랑거리듯 수줍게, 그러면서도 새치름한 교태를 부리며 물었다.\n\n\"파벨 표도로비치, 어째서 그토록 오래 우리를 보러 오시지 않으셨어요? 어째서 늘 우리를 깔보시는 거예요?\"\n\"천만에.\" 남자의 목소리가 정중하게, 그러나 도도한 위엄을 담아 대답했다. 남자가 우위를 점하고 있고, 여자가 추파를 던지고 있음이 분명했다. \"목소리로 보아 저 남자는 분명 스메르댜코프일 거야.\" 알료샤는 생각했다. \"그리고 저 아가씨는 이 집 딸, 모스크바에서 왔다는 그 여자, 자락이 달린 드레스를 입고 마르파에게 수프를 얻어먹으러 가는 여자일 테고.\"\n\n\"운만 맞으면 어떤 시구든 무척 좋아해요.\" 여자의 목소리가 이어졌다. \"왜 계속 안 부르세요?\"\n\n남자가 다시 노래했다.\n\n내 임이 무사하기만 하다면\n\n제왕의 부귀인들 무슨 상관이랴?\n\n주여, 자비를 베푸소서\n그녀와 나에게!\n\n그녀와 나에게!\n그녀와 나에게!\n\n\"지난번이 훨씬 더 좋았어요.\" 여자의 목소리가 말했다. \"그때는 '내 사랑이 무사하기만 하다면'이라고 부르셨잖아요. 그게 더 다정하게 들렸어요. 오늘은 잊어버리셨나 봐요.\"\n\"시 따위는 시시한 거요!\" 스메르댜코프가 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 아니에요! 저는 시를 무척 좋아하는걸요.\"\n\"시인 한에서야, 그건 본질적으로 시시한 거요. 생각해 보시오, 도대체 누가 운을 맞춰서 말을 한답디까? 우리 모두가 운을 맞춰서 말하라고, 설령 그게 정부의 명령이라 한들, 그리 많은 말을 하지는 못할 거 아니오? 시는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 거요, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 영리하세요! 어떻게 모든 일에 그토록 깊이 통달하셨어요?\" 여자의 목소리는 점점 더 알랑거렸다.\n\"그보다 더한 것도 할 수 있었을 거요. 그보다 더 많은 것을 알 수도 있었을 거요, 어릴 적부터의 내 운명만 아니었더라면. 누가 나더러 더러운 거지의 자식으로 태어나 아비도 없는 놈이라고 욕했다면, 나는 결투를 벌여 그자를 쏘아 죽였을 거요. 모스크바에서도 사람들이 그걸 내 얼굴에다 대고 들먹이곤 했지. 그게 여기서 거기까지 흘러간 건 그리고리 바실리예비치 덕분이오. 그리고리 바실리예비치는 내가 내 출생에 반항한다고 나를 나무라지만, 나는 차라리 태어나기 전에 그들이 나를 죽이는 걸 허락했을 거요, 그래서 아예 이 세상에 나오지나 않았으면 했단 말이오. 장터에서도 그런 말들을 하곤 했고, 당신네 어머니까지도 어찌나 무례하던지 내 어미의 머리털이 머리 위에 깔린 멍석 같았느니, 키가 다섯 자에서 쪼끔 모자랐느니 하고 나한테 떠벌리기 시작합디다. 어째서 '쪼끔'이라고 하는 거요, 남들처럼 '조금'이라고 말할 수도 있었을 텐데? 그 여자는 그걸 가슴 뭉클하게 만들고 싶었던 거요, 영락없는 촌놈의 감상이지. 러시아 촌놈한테 배운 사람에 견줄 만한 감정이 있다고 할 수 있겠소? 그 무지로 보아 감정이라고는 도무지 없다고 해야지. 어릴 적부터 나는 '쪼끔'이라는 말만 들으면 분통이 터질 지경이오. 나는 러시아 전체가 다 싫소, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\"\n\n\"당신이 사관 후보생이나 젊은 경기병이었다면 그렇게 말하지 않고 칼을 빼 들어 러시아 전체를 지키려 했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 경기병 따위가 되고 싶지 않소, 마리야 콘드라티예브나, 게다가 나라면 군인이란 군인은 죄다 없애 버리고 싶소.\"\n\n\"그러다 적이 쳐들어오면 누가 우리를 지켜 줘요?\"\n\n\"지켜 줄 필요 따위 없소. 1812년에 프랑스의 초대 황제, 지금 황제의 아비인 나폴레옹이 러시아를 대대적으로 침공했는데, 그때 그들이 우리를 정복했더라면 좋았을 거요. 영리한 민족이 아주 어리석은 민족을 정복해서 병합했을 테니까. 그랬으면 우리도 사뭇 다른 제도를 갖게 됐을 거요.\"\n\n\"그쪽 나라가 우리보다 그렇게나 더 나은가요? 나라면 내가 아는 멋쟁이 하나를 젊은 영국인 셋과도 안 바꾸겠어요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 다정하게 말했는데, 그 말끝에 분명 더없이 애교 어린 눈길을 곁들였을 터였다.\n\n\"그건 사람마다 취향 나름이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 영락없는 외국인 같아요—영락없이 더없이 신사다운 외국인 같다고요. 이런 말 하려니 부끄럽지만, 정말 그렇다고요.\"\n\n\"굳이 알고 싶다면 말이오만, 거기 사람들이나 여기 우리 사람들이나 악덕으로 치면 다 똑같소. 다들 사기꾼이지, 다만 거기서는 악당이 반들반들 광낸 부츠를 신고, 여기서는 진창 속에 뒹굴면서도 그게 나쁜 줄을 모른다는 차이뿐이오. 러시아 백성한테는 매질이 필요하오, 어제 표도르 파블로비치가 아주 옳은 말을 했지, 비록 그 양반이 미치광이고 그 자식들도 다 그렇긴 하지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"이반 표도로비치는 그토록 존경한다고 당신 입으로 말했잖아요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 양반은 나더러 구린내 나는 하인이라고 합디다. 내가 분수를 모르고 까불지도 모른다고 여기는 거요. 그건 그 양반이 잘못 본 거요. 내 주머니에 얼마간의 돈만 있었다면 나는 진작 여길 떠났을 거요. 드미트리 표도로비치는 그 행실로 보나, 그 머리로 보나, 그 가난으로 보나 어느 하인보다도 못한 위인이오. 할 줄 아는 게 아무것도 없는데도 다들 그를 떠받든단 말이오. 나야 한낱 수프나 끓이는 놈일지 모르지만, 운만 따라 준다면 모스크바 페트로프카에 카페 식당이라도 차릴 수 있소, 내 요리 솜씨가 보통이 아니거든, 모스크바에서 요리 솜씨가 변변한 자라고는 외국인들 말고는 아무도 없으니까. 드미트리 표도로비치는 거지나 다름없는데도, 이 나라 으뜸가는 백작의 아들에게 결투를 신청한다면 정말로 싸울 위인이오. 그렇지만 그가 나보다 나은 게 도대체 뭐가 있소? 나보다 훨씬 더 멍청한걸. 아무 까닭도 없이 돈을 얼마나 펑펑 날려 버렸는지 좀 보시오!\"\n\n\"결투란 건 분명 멋있을 거예요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 문득 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 그렇소?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 끔찍하면서도 용감할 거예요, 특히 젊은 장교들이 어떤 귀부인을 위해 손에 권총을 들고 서로를 향해 탕 쏠 때 말이에요. 영락없는 한 폭의 그림이죠! 아, 아가씨들한테도 구경하는 게 허락된다면, 한 번 보려고 무엇이든 다 내놓겠어요!\"\n\n\"당신이 누군가를 향해 쏠 때야 다 괜찮지만, 그자가 당신 상판대기를 정통으로 겨누고 쏠 때는 꽤나 멍청한 기분이 들 거요. 줄행랑이라도 치고 싶어질걸, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\"\n\n\"설마 당신이 줄행랑을 치겠다는 건 아니죠?\" 그러나 스메르댜코프는 굳이 대답하려 들지 않았다. 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 기타가 다시 딸랑거렸고, 그는 또다시 같은 가성으로 노래했다.\n당신이 무어라 하건\n\n나는 멀리 떠나가리.\n저 멀리 도시에서는\n\n삶이 밝고 즐거우리.\n나는 슬퍼하지 않으리,\n\n나는 결코 슬퍼하지 않으리,\n나는 도무지 슬퍼할 마음이 없네.\n\n그때 뜻밖의 일이 벌어졌다. 알료샤가 갑자기 재채기를 한 것이다. 그들은 입을 다물었다. 알료샤는 일어나 그들 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 가서 보니 스메르댜코프는 말쑥하게 차려입고 반들반들 광낸 부츠를 신었으며, 머리에는 포마드를 발라 어쩌면 곱슬곱슬하게 지졌을지도 몰랐다. 기타는 정원용 의자 위에 놓여 있었다. 그와 함께 있던 이는 이 집 딸로, 자락이 두 자나 되는 연한 하늘빛 드레스를 입고 있었다. 그녀는 젊었고 못생겼다고 할 수는 없었으나, 다만 얼굴이 어찌나 둥글고 주근깨가 끔찍이도 많은지 그게 흠이었다.\n\n\"형 드미트리는 곧 돌아오나요?\" 알료샤는 애써 침착하게 물었다.\n\n스메르댜코프는 느릿느릿 일어섰고, 마리야 콘드라티예브나도 따라 일어났다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치에 대해 제가 어찌 알겠습니까? 제가 그분 보호자도 아니고 말입니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 조용히, 또렷하게, 그리고 거만하게 대답했다.\n\"그저 혹시 알고 있는지 물었을 뿐이에요.\" 알료샤가 설명했다.\n\n\"그분이 어디 계신지 저는 아무것도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 형이 말하길, 자네가 이 집에서 일어나는 일을 다 알려 주고, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오면 알려 주기로 약속했다던데요.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 느릿하고 미동도 없는 눈길을 그에게로 돌렸다.\n\"그건 그렇고, 한 시간 전에 대문 빗장을 걸어 두었는데 이번엔 어떻게 들어오셨습니까?\" 그가 알료샤를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"뒷골목에서 울타리를 넘어 곧장 정자로 왔어요. 부디 용서해 주시길.\" 그는 마리야 콘드라티예브나를 향해 덧붙였다. \"형을 급히 찾아야 했거든요.\"\n\"어머, 저희가 그걸 가지고 언짢아할 리가 있나요!\" 알료샤의 사과에 우쭐해진 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 길게 빼는 말투로 말했다. \"드미트리 표도로비치도 곧잘 그렇게 정자로 오시는걸요. 저희는 그분이 와 계신 줄도 모르는데 정자에 앉아 계시곤 하지요.\"\n\n\"그분을 꼭 찾고 싶어요, 아니면 지금 어디 계신지 두 분께 알아내고 싶어요. 정말이지 그분한테 아주 중대한 일 때문이에요.\"\n\n\"그분은 저희한테 도통 말씀을 안 하셔요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 혀 짧은 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"제가 비록 친구로서 여길 드나들긴 했습니다만,\" 스메르댜코프가 다시 말문을 열었다, \"드미트리 표도로비치는 여기서까지 주인어른에 대해 끊임없이 캐물으며 사정없이 저를 들볶았습니다. '무슨 소식 없느냐?'고 묻지요. '지금 저 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지느냐? 누가 오가느냐?' 그러면서 좀 더 말해 줄 게 없냐고 채근하는 겁니다. 벌써 두 번이나 저를 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놨습죠.\"\n\n\"죽이겠다고요?\" 알료샤가 놀라 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그 성미로 보아 그쯤이야 대수롭게 여길 분이겠습니까, 어제 도련님께서도 직접 보실 기회가 있었지 않습니까? 제가 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 들이고 그녀가 거기서 하룻밤 묵게 되면 제가 제일 먼저 화를 당할 거랍니다. 저는 그분이 끔찍이 무섭습니다, 그러는 게 더 무섭지만 않았다면 경찰에 알려야 마땅할 지경입죠. 그분이 무슨 짓을 저지를지는 하느님만 아실 일입니다!\"\n\n\"나리께서도 며칠 전에 그분더러 '절구에 넣고 짓찧어 버리겠다!'고 하셨어요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 절구에 넣고 짓찧겠다는 거라면 그냥 해 보는 말일 수도 있겠죠.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"그분을 만날 수 있다면 그 일에 대해서도 말해 볼게요.\"\n\n\"음, 제가 도련님께 말씀드릴 수 있는 건 이것뿐입니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 마음을 고쳐먹은 듯 말했다. \"저야 오랜 친구이자 이웃으로서 여기 있는 거니, 제가 오지 않는다면 그게 오히려 이상한 일이겠지요. 한편으로, 이반 표도로비치께서 오늘 아침 맨 먼저 저를 도련님 형님이 묵으시는 오제르나야 거리 숙소로 보내셨습니다, 편지는 없이 그저 드미트리 표도로비치께 여기 장터에 있는 식당으로 와서 같이 식사하자는 전갈만 들려서요. 가 보았지만 드미트리 표도로비치는 댁에 안 계셨습니다, 여덟 시였는데도 말입니다. '여기 다녀가시긴 했는데 아주 떠나 버리셨다'고, 그 댁 안주인이 한 말이 바로 그것이었습니다. 마치 두 분 사이에 무슨 짠 게 있는 것 같았지요. 어쩌면 지금쯤 그분은 이반 표도로비치와 함께 식당에 계실지도 모릅니다, 이반 표도로비치께서도 식사하러 집에 안 들어오셨고, 표도르 파블로비치께서는 한 시간 전에 혼자 식사를 마치시고 누우러 가셨으니까요. 그렇지만 제발 부탁드립니다, 저에 대해서나 제가 말씀드린 것에 대해서는 입도 뻥끗 마십시오, 그분은 아무것도 아닌 일로도 저를 죽일 사람이니까요.\"\n\n\"이반 형이 오늘 드미트리 형을 식당으로 초대했다고요?\" 알료샤가 다급히 되물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"장터에 있는 메트로폴리스 선술집 말인가요?\"\n\n\"바로 그곳입니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 만하군요.\" 알료샤가 몹시 흥분하여 외쳤다. \"고마워요, 스메르댜코프, 이건 중요한 일이에요. 당장 거기로 가 봐야겠어요.\"\n\n\"저를 팔지 마십시오.\" 스메르댜코프가 그의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 우연히 들른 것처럼 선술집에 갈게요. 걱정 말아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 잠깐만요, 제가 대문을 열어 드릴게요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니에요, 이쪽이 지름길이라서요, 다시 울타리를 넘을게요.\"\n\n들은 이야기는 알료샤를 큰 동요에 빠뜨렸다. 그는 선술집으로 달려갔다. 수도복 차림으로는 선술집 안으로 들어갈 수 없었으나, 입구에서 형들을 찾아 불러내 달라고 청할 수는 있었다. 그런데 마침 선술집에 다다랐을 때 창문 하나가 활짝 열리더니, 형 이반이 그 창에서 그를 내려다보며 불렀다.\n\n\"알료샤, 이리로 올라올 수 없겠니? 그래 주면 정말 고맙겠다.\"\n\n\"그럼 올라가야죠, 다만 이런 차림으로 괜찮을지 잘 모르겠는데요—\"\n\n\"하지만 난 따로 떨어진 방에 있어. 계단으로 올라와, 내가 마중하러 내려갈게.\"\n\n잠시 후 알료샤는 형 곁에 앉아 있었다. 이반은 혼자서 식사를 하던 참이었다.\n\n# Chapter III. The Brothers Make Friends\n\n그러나 이반은 따로 마련된 방에 있는 것이 아니라 단지 칸막이로 가려진 자리에 있어서, 같은 방 안의 다른 사람들 눈에는 띄지 않았다. 그곳은 입구에서 첫 번째 방으로, 벽을 따라 식탁이 놓여 있었다. 종업원들이 끊임없이 그 안을 분주히 오갔다. 방 안의 손님이라고는 구석에서 차를 마시는, 퇴역한 늙은 군인 하나뿐이었다. 그러나 술집의 다른 방들에서는 여느 때와 다름없는 소란이 일고 있었으니, 종업원을 부르는 고함 소리, 병마개가 펑 터지는 소리, 당구공이 딱딱 부딪치는 소리, 손풍금이 웅웅대는 소리가 들려왔다. 알료샤는 이반이 평소 이 술집에 드나들지 않으며, 대체로 술집이라는 곳을 좋아하지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러니 형은 단지 약속에 따라 드미트리를 만나러 이곳에 온 것이리라고 그는 생각했다. 그런데도 드미트리는 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"생선이나 수프, 뭐 좀 시켜 줄까? 설마 차만 마시고 사는 건 아닐 테지.\" 이반이 외쳤는데, 알료샤를 붙잡은 것이 자못 반가운 기색이었다. 그는 식사를 마치고 차를 마시던 참이었다.\n\n\"수프를 먹고 나서 차를 마시겠어요, 배가 고파요.\" 알료샤가 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"버찌 잼은 어때? 여기 그게 있어. 어릴 적에 버찌 잼을 그렇게 좋아하던 거 기억나니?\"\n\n\"형이 그걸 다 기억해요? 잼도 주세요, 지금도 좋아하거든요.\"\n\n이반은 종을 울려 종업원을 부르더니 수프와 잼과 차를 주문했다.\n\n\"난 다 기억한다, 알료샤. 네가 열한 살이던 때까지 기억하지, 그때 난 열다섯이 다 되어 있었거든. 열다섯하고 열하나 사이에는 그토록 차이가 커서, 그 나이의 형제는 결코 친구가 될 수 없는 법이야. 내가 너를 좋아하기나 했는지도 모르겠다. 처음 모스크바로 떠나 몇 해 동안은 네 생각이라곤 아예 하지도 않았어. 그러다 네가 직접 모스크바에 왔을 때도, 어디선가 딱 한 번 만났던 것 같구나. 그리고 이제 내가 여기 온 지 석 달이 넘었는데, 여태껏 우리는 거의 한마디도 나누지 못했지. 내일이면 난 떠나는데, 여기 앉아 있으면서 어떻게 하면 너를 만나 작별 인사를 할 수 있을까 곰곰이 생각하던 차에, 마침 네가 지나가더구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 형은 나를 그렇게 만나고 싶었던 거예요?\"\n\n\"무척이나. 너라는 사람을 단번에 제대로 알아 두고 싶고, 또 너도 나를 알아 주었으면 했어. 그러고 나서 작별하려고. 사람을 알게 되기에는 헤어지기 직전이 언제나 가장 좋은 법이라고 나는 믿는다. 이 석 달 동안 네가 나를 어떻게 바라보았는지 나는 다 보았어. 네 눈에는 늘 어떤 기대의 빛이 어려 있었는데, 나는 그게 견딜 수가 없었지. 그래서 너를 피해 다녔던 거야. 하지만 결국에는 너를 존경하게 되었다. 이 조그만 녀석이 제법 야무지게 서 있구나, 하고 생각했지. 웃고는 있지만 진심으로 하는 말이야. 너는 정말 야무지게 서 있어, 그렇지 않니? 나는 무엇을 신념으로 삼든 그렇게 야무지게 서 있는 사람이 좋아, 비록 너처럼 조그만 녀석이라 해도 말이야. 너의 그 기대에 찬 눈도 더 이상 거슬리지 않게 되었고, 끝내는 그 기대에 찬 눈을 사랑하게 되었어. 너는 어쩐 일인지 나를 사랑하는 것 같구나, 알료샤?\"\n\n\"나는 형을 사랑해요, 이반. 드미트리 형은 형을 두고 이반은 무덤이라고 해요. 나는 형을 두고 이반은 수수께끼라고 해요. 형은 지금도 내게 수수께끼예요. 하지만 형 안의 무언가를 나는 이해해요, 오늘 아침까지만 해도 이해하지 못했던 것을요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐냐?\" 이반이 웃었다.\n\n\"화내지 않을 거죠?\" 알료샤도 웃었다.\n\n\"그래?\"\n\n\"형이 다른 스물세 살 청년들과 똑같이 젊다는 것, 그저 젊고 싱그럽고 착한 소년이라는 것, 사실은 풋내기라는 것 말이에요! 자, 내가 형을 끔찍하게 모욕한 건가요?\"\n\n\"천만에, 오히려 그게 우연의 일치라 놀랍구나.\" 이반이 다정하고 유쾌하게 외쳤다. \"믿을 수 있겠니, 그 여자와 그 장면을 겪은 뒤로 나는 다른 건 아무것도 생각하지 않고 오직 내 젊은 풋내기 같은 면만 생각하고 있었거든, 그런데 마치 네가 그걸 알아챈 것처럼 바로 그 얘기를 꺼내는구나. 알겠니, 나는 여기 앉아서 혼자 이런 생각을 하고 있었어. 만약 내가 삶을 믿지 않게 된다면, 내가 사랑하는 여자에 대한 믿음을 잃고, 사물의 질서에 대한 믿음을 잃고, 정녕 모든 것이 무질서하고 저주받은, 어쩌면 악마에 들린 혼돈에 지나지 않는다고 확신하게 된다면, 인간의 환멸이 자아내는 온갖 공포에 짓눌린다 해도—그래도 나는 살고 싶을 것이고, 일단 그 잔에 입을 댄 이상, 그것을 다 비우기 전에는 결코 외면하지 않을 거라고! 그렇지만 서른이 되면, 비록 잔을 다 비우지 못했다 해도 분명 그 잔을 내려놓고 돌아설 거야—어디로 갈지는 나도 모르겠지만. 하지만 서른이 되기 전까지는, 내 젊음이 모든 것을—온갖 환멸을, 삶에 대한 온갖 혐오를 이겨 내리라는 걸 나는 알아. 내 안에 있는 이 미친 듯한, 어쩌면 꼴사나운 삶에 대한 갈망을 압도할 만한 절망이 세상에 있는지 나는 수없이 자문해 봤지만, 결국 그런 건 없다는 결론에 이르렀지, 적어도 서른이 되기 전까지는 말이야. 그러고 나면 나는 저절로 그걸 잃게 되겠지, 그럴 것 같아. 침이나 질질 흘리는 폐병쟁이 도덕가들이—특히 시인들이—그 삶에 대한 갈망을 흔히 비루하다고 부르지. 모든 것을 아랑곳 않는 그 삶에 대한 갈망, 그게 카라마조프 집안의 특징인 것은 사실이고, 너에게도 틀림없이 있을 테지만, 어째서 그게 비루하다는 거냐? 우리 행성의 구심력은 아직도 무섭도록 강해, 알료샤. 나는 삶을 갈망하고, 논리에 어긋나면서도 계속 살아가고 있어. 비록 우주의 질서를 믿지 않는다 해도, 봄철에 돋아나는 끈적끈적한 어린 잎사귀를 나는 사랑해. 푸른 하늘을 사랑하고, 어떤 사람들을 사랑하지, 그게 누군가를 영문도 모른 채 사랑하는 그런 거 있잖니. 인간들이 이룬 어떤 위대한 행적들을 사랑해, 비록 오래전에 그것들에 대한 믿음을 잃어버렸을지도 모르지만, 그래도 오랜 습관 때문에 마음이 그것들을 소중히 여기지. 자, 네 수프가 나왔다, 먹어라, 몸에 좋을 거야. 일품 수프지, 여긴 이걸 잘 끓이거든. 나는 유럽을 여행하고 싶어, 알료샤, 여기서 출발할 거야. 그러면서도 나는 내가 그저 묘지로 가고 있을 뿐이라는 걸 알아, 하지만 그건 더없이 소중한 묘지지, 바로 그런 거야! 거기 누워 있는 죽은 자들은 소중해, 그들 위에 놓인 비석 하나하나가 지난날의 그토록 불타는 삶을, 자기 일과 자기 진리와 자기 투쟁과 자기 학문에 대한 그토록 열렬한 신념을 증언하고 있어서, 나는 땅에 엎드려 그 비석들에 입 맞추고 그 위에서 눈물을 흘릴 거라는 걸 알아. 비록 마음속으로는 그곳이 오래전부터 묘지에 지나지 않는다고 확신하면서도 말이야. 그리고 나는 절망 때문에 우는 게 아니라, 단지 내 눈물 속에서 행복할 것이기에 울 거야, 내 감정 속에 내 영혼을 푹 담글 거야. 봄철의 끈적끈적한 잎사귀를, 푸른 하늘을 나는 사랑해—그게 전부야. 그건 지성이나 논리의 문제가 아니라, 제 속으로, 제 배로 사랑하는 거지. 사람은 제 젊음의 첫 힘을 사랑하는 법이야. 내 장광설 가운데 뭐라도 알아듣겠니, 알료샤?\" 이반이 별안간 웃었다.\n\n\"너무도 잘 알아듣겠어요, 이반. 제 속으로, 제 배로 사랑하기를 갈망한다는 말씀. 그 말을 어찌나 멋지게 하시던지, 형이 그토록 삶을 갈망한다니 나는 정말이지 기뻐요.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"사람은 누구나 세상 그 무엇보다도 삶을 사랑해야 한다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"삶의 의미보다도 삶을 더 사랑하라고?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 삶을 사랑하세요, 형 말처럼 논리를 아랑곳하지 말고요, 논리를 아랑곳하지 말아야 해요, 그래야 비로소 삶의 의미를 이해하게 될 테니까요. 나는 오래전부터 그렇게 생각해 왔어요. 형 일의 절반은 이미 이루어진 셈이에요, 이반, 형은 삶을 사랑하니까요, 이제 나머지 절반만 해내려고 애쓰면 형은 구원받는 거예요.\"\n\n\"넌 나를 구원하려 드는구나, 하지만 어쩌면 난 길을 잃은 게 아닐지도 몰라! 그런데 네가 말하는 그 나머지 절반이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"그건, 형의 죽은 자들을 일으켜 세워야 한다는 거예요, 어쩌면 그들은 결국 죽지 않았는지도 모르니까요. 자, 차를 좀 마실게요. 우리가 이렇게 이야기를 나누니 나는 너무 기뻐요, 이반.\"\n\n\"네가 영감에 찬 기분인 게 보이는구나. 나는 너 같은—수련사한테서 이런 신앙 고백을 듣는 게 무척 좋아. 너는 흔들림 없는 사람이야, 알렉세이. 정말 수도원을 떠날 작정이라는 게 사실이냐?\"\n\n\"네, 장로님이 나를 세상으로 내보내신답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 세상에서 다시 만나겠구나. 내가 서른이 되어 잔에서 돌아서기 시작하기 전에 우리는 만나게 될 거야. 아버지는 일흔이 되도록 자기 잔에서 돌아설 마음이 없으시지, 실은 여든까지 매달려 버티는 게 꿈이라고 직접 말씀하시거든. 어릿광대이긴 해도 그 말씀만은 지나칠 만큼 진지하셔. 아버지도 단단한 바위 위에 서 계신데, 그게 바로 당신의 육욕이지—하기야 우리가 서른을 넘기고 나면, 정말이지 그것 말고는 발붙일 데가 아무것도 없을지도 모르지…… 하지만 일흔까지 매달리는 건 추접스러운 짓이야, 차라리 서른까지가 낫지. 그래야 자기 자신을 속여 가면서나마 '한 가닥 고결함의 그림자'쯤은 간직할 수 있거든. 오늘 드미트리를 봤니?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 하지만 스메르댜코프는 봤어요.\" 그러면서 알료샤는 스메르댜코프와의 만남을 빠르면서도 세세하게 들려주었다. 이반은 불안한 기색으로 귀를 기울이기 시작하더니 이것저것 캐물었다.\n\n\"하지만 그가 자기한테서 형 얘기를 들었다고 드미트리 형에게는 말하지 말아 달라고 신신당부했어요.\" 알료샤가 덧붙였다. 이반은 눈살을 찌푸리며 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"스메르댜코프 때문에 눈살을 찌푸리는 거예요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그 녀석 때문이지. 빌어먹을, 나는 분명 드미트리를 만나고 싶었지만, 이제는 그럴 필요가 없어졌어.\" 이반이 마지못해 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 형, 정말로 그렇게 빨리 떠나는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"드미트리 형과 아버지는 어쩌고요? 그 일이 어떻게 끝나려고요?\" 알료샤가 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"넌 늘 그 타령이구나! 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이야? 내가 내 동생 드미트리를 지키는 자라도 된단 말이냐?\" 이반이 짜증스럽게 쏘아붙였으나, 곧 별안간 씁쓸하게 웃었다. \"살해당한 동생을 두고 카인이 했던 대답이지, 안 그러냐? 어쩌면 지금 이 순간 네가 그걸 떠올리고 있는지도 모르겠구나? 그래, 빌어먹을, 내가 그들을 지키겠다고 여기 머물 수는 없잖니, 안 그래? 나는 내가 해야 할 일을 끝냈고, 이제 떠나는 거야. 너는 내가 드미트리를 질투한다고, 지난 석 달 동안 그의 아름다운 카테리나 이바노브나를 가로채려 했다고 생각하는 거냐? 말도 안 돼, 나는 내 나름의 볼일이 있었어. 그걸 끝냈고. 이제 떠나는 거야. 방금 그 일을 끝냈고, 네가 그 증인이었지.\"\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나 댁에서요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서 나는 단번에 나를 풀어 준 거야. 그리고 따지고 보면, 드미트리가 나하고 무슨 상관이야? 드미트리는 끼어들 자리가 없어. 나는 카테리나 이바노브나와 처리할 내 나름의 볼일이 있었던 거야. 너도 알다시피, 오히려 드미트리는 우리 둘 사이에 무슨 양해라도 있는 양 굴었어. 내가 그러라고 부탁한 적도 없는데, 그는 엄숙하게 그녀를 내게 넘겨주고 우리에게 축복까지 내렸지. 정말이지 우스워 죽겠어. 아, 알료샤, 지금 내 마음이 얼마나 가벼운지 네가 알기만 한다면! 믿을 수 있겠니, 나는 여기 앉아 밥을 먹으면서 내 자유의 첫 시간을 자축하려고 샴페인을 시킬 뻔했다니까. 체! 거의 여섯 달이나 끌어 온 일을, 단번에 훌훌 털어 버린 거야. 어제만 해도 나는 마음만 먹으면 이 일을 끝내는 게 이토록 쉬우리라고는 짐작도 못 했어.\"\n\n\"형의 사랑을 두고 하는 말이에요, 이반?\"\n\n\"내 사랑이라고 해도 좋아. 나는 그 아가씨와 사랑에 빠졌고, 그녀 때문에 속을 끓였고 그녀도 나 때문에 속을 끓였지. 나는 앉아서 그녀를 지켜보고 있었는데…… 그러던 게 별안간 와르르 무너져 버린 거야! 오늘 아침에는 영감에 사로잡혀 떠들었지만, 그 자리를 나오면서 나는 껄껄 웃어 댔어. 믿을 수 있겠니? 그래, 글자 그대로 사실이야.\"\n\n\"지금은 그 일을 두고 무척 즐거워 보이네요.\" 알료샤가 별안간 환해진 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 그녀를 손톱만큼도 사랑하지 않았다는 걸 어떻게 알 수 있었겠니! 하하! 알고 보니 결국 사랑하지 않았던 거야. 그런데도 그녀가 나를 어찌나 끌어당기던지! 방금 내가 연설을 늘어놓을 때 그녀가 어찌나 매력적이던지! 그리고 알겠니, 그녀는 지금도 나를 끔찍하게 끌어당겨, 그런데도 그녀를 떠나기란 어찌나 쉬운지. 내가 허풍을 떤다고 생각하니?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 다만 어쩌면 그건 사랑이 아니었나 봐요.\"\n\n\"알료샤,\" 이반이 웃었다, \"사랑을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하지 마라, 너한테는 어울리지 않아. 오늘 아침에 너 그 논쟁에 어찌나 불쑥 뛰어들던지! 그것 때문에 너한테 입맞춰 주는 걸 깜빡했구나…… 그건 그렇고 그녀가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히던지! 그건 정말이지 '생채기' 옆에 앉아 있는 거나 마찬가지였어. 아, 그녀는 내가 자기를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알고 있었어! 그녀가 사랑한 건 드미트리가 아니라 나였어.\" 이반이 명랑하게 우겼다. \"드미트리에 대한 그녀의 감정은 그저 자기 학대였을 뿐이야. 방금 내가 그녀에게 한 말은 죄다 더없이 옳은 말이었지만, 가장 고약한 건 그녀가 자기는 드미트리를 사랑하지 않으며 오히려 자기가 괴롭히는 나를 사랑한다는 걸 깨닫는 데 십오 년이나 이십 년이 걸릴지도 모른다는 점이야, 어쩌면 오늘 같은 교훈을 얻고도 끝내 깨닫지 못할지도 모르지. 뭐, 그게 차라리 나아. 나는 그저 영영 떠나 버리면 그만이니까. 그건 그렇고, 그녀는 지금 어떠냐? 내가 떠난 뒤에 무슨 일이 있었어?\"\n\n알료샤는 그녀가 발작을 일으켰으며, 듣자 하니 지금은 의식을 잃고 헛소리를 하고 있다고 형에게 말해 주었다.\n\n\"호흘라코바 부인이 일을 부풀리는 거 아니냐?\"\n\n\"아닌 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"알아봐야겠다. 하기야 발작으로 죽는 사람은 없지. 그런 건 아무것도 아니야. 하느님이 여자에게 발작을 주신 건 그게 위안이 되라고 그러신 거야. 나는 그녀에게 아예 가지 않겠어. 무엇하러 또 나서겠니?\"\n\n\"하지만 형은 그녀가 형을 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없다고 말했잖아요.\"\n\n\"일부러 그런 거야. 알료샤, 샴페인 좀 시킬까? 내 자유를 위해 건배하자. 아, 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 네가 알기만 한다면!\"\n\n\"아뇨, 형, 술은 마시지 않는 게 좋겠어요.\" 알료샤가 별안간 말했다. \"게다가 나는 어쩐지 마음이 울적해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 너는 오래전부터 울적해 있었지, 나도 눈치챘어.\"\n\n\"그럼 형은 내일 아침에 떠나기로 정한 거예요?\"\n\n\"아침에? 아침에 떠난다고는 말하지 않았는데…… 하지만 어쩌면 아침이 될지도 모르지. 믿을 수 있겠니, 오늘 여기서 밥을 먹은 것도 그저 그 영감하고 같이 먹기 싫어서였어, 어찌나 그가 역겨운지. 그 영감만 아니었다면 나는 진작에 떠났을 거야. 그런데 너는 어째서 내가 떠나는 일을 그토록 걱정하는 거냐? 내가 떠나기 전까지 우리한테는 시간이 넉넉해, 영원이 있다고!\"\n\n\"내일 떠난다면서 영원이라니 그게 무슨 말이에요?\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 우리한테 무슨 상관이야?\" 이반이 웃었다. \"우리가 이야기를 나누기에는, 우리를 여기로 데려온 그 일을 두고 이야기하기에는 시간이 넉넉하다는 거지. 왜 그렇게 놀란 얼굴을 하니? 대답해 봐, 우리가 왜 여기서 만났겠니? 내가 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑한 이야기를 하려고? 영감과 드미트리 이야기를 하려고? 외국 여행 이야기를? 러시아의 운명적 처지를? 나폴레옹 황제를? 그런 걸 두고 만난 거냐?\"\n\n\"아뇨.\"\n\n\"그럼 너도 무엇 때문인지 알겠지. 다른 사람들이야 사정이 다르지만, 우리처럼 풋내기 젊은이들은 무엇보다도 먼저 영원한 문제들을 해결해야 해. 우리가 마음 쓰는 게 바로 그거지. 요즘 젊은 러시아는 온통 영원한 문제들 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않아. 정작 늙은이들은 죄다 실제적인 문제에 매달려 있을 때 말이야. 너는 어째서 지난 석 달 동안 기대에 찬 눈으로 나를 바라보았겠니? 나한테 '형은 무엇을 믿어요, 아니면 아예 아무것도 믿지 않아요?'라고 물으려고 그런 거잖아. 이 석 달 동안 네 눈이 뜻하던 게 바로 그거 아니었니?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그럴지도요.\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"지금 나를 비웃는 거 아니죠, 이반?\"\n\n\"내가 비웃는다고! 석 달 동안이나 그토록 기대에 차서 나를 지켜본 내 동생에게 상처를 주고 싶지는 않아. 알료샤, 나를 똑바로 봐라! 물론 나도 너와 똑같은 조그만 소년이야, 다만 수련사가 아닐 뿐이지. 그런데 러시아 소년들은 지금껏 무얼 해 왔지, 그러니까 그중 일부 말이야? 가령 여기, 이 고약한 냄새 나는 술집 같은 데서, 그들은 만나 구석 자리에 앉아. 평생 한 번도 만난 적 없는 사이고, 이 술집을 나가면 사십 년이 지나도 다시는 못 만날 사이지. 그런데 그 잠깐의 술집 휴식 동안 그들이 무슨 이야기를 하겠니? 영원한 문제들, 하느님의 존재와 영혼 불멸에 관한 이야기지. 그리고 하느님을 믿지 않는 자들은 사회주의나 무정부주의 이야기를, 인류 전체를 새로운 틀로 개조하는 이야기를 하지, 그러니 결국 다 같은 거야, 그건 안팎을 뒤집어 놓은 똑같은 문제들이거든. 그리고 무수히, 무수히 많은 더없이 독창적인 러시아 소년들이 영원한 문제들 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않아! 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n\"네, 진정한 러시아인에게는 하느님의 존재와 영혼 불멸의 문제가, 형 말마따나 안팎을 뒤집어 놓은 똑같은 문제가, 물론 무엇보다도 먼저고, 또 마땅히 그래야 하지요.\" 알료샤가 여전히 똑같은 부드럽고 캐묻는 듯한 미소로 형을 지켜보며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 말이다, 알료샤, 도대체 러시아인으로 산다는 게 때로는 더없이 어리석은 일이지만, 러시아 소년들이 시간을 보내는 방식보다 더 어리석은 건 도무지 상상하기 어려워. 하지만 그중에 알료샤라는 러시아 소년이 하나 있는데, 나는 그 녀석을 끔찍이 좋아하지.\"\n\n\"그 말을 어쩜 그렇게 멋지게 끼워 넣어요!\" 알료샤가 별안간 웃었다.\n\n\"자, 어디서부터 시작할지 말해 봐, 명령을 내려라. 하느님의 존재부터, 어때?\"\n\n\"아무 데서나 시작하세요. 형은 어제 아버지 댁에서 하느님은 없다고 선언했잖아요.\" 알료샤가 형을 살피듯 바라보았다.\n\n\"어제 식사 자리에서 그렇게 말한 건 너를 약 올리려는 거였고, 네 눈이 이글거리는 걸 봤지. 하지만 지금은 너와 그 문제를 논하는 데 이의가 없어, 진심으로 하는 말이야. 나는 너와 친구가 되고 싶다, 알료샤, 나한테는 친구가 없거든, 그래서 한번 해 보고 싶어. 그런데, 한번 상상해 봐, 어쩌면 나도 하느님을 받아들이는지도 몰라.\" 이반이 웃었다. \"너한테는 뜻밖이지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"네, 물론이죠, 지금 형이 농담하는 게 아니라면요.\"\n\n\"농담이라고? 어제 장로한테서도 내가 농담을 한다는 말을 들었지. 그런데 말이다, 얘야, 십팔 세기에 어떤 늙은 죄인이 있었는데, 그가 선언하기를, 만약 하느님이 없다면 하느님을 발명해야 할 거라고 했어. '하느님이 존재하지 않는다면, 그분을 발명해야 하리라.' 그리고 인간은 실제로 하느님을 발명해 냈지. 그런데 이상한 건, 정말 놀라운 건, 하느님이 정녕 존재한다는 게 아니라, 그런 관념이, 곧 하느님의 필요성이라는 관념이 인간처럼 야만스럽고 사악한 짐승의 머릿속에 떠오를 수 있었다는 점이야. 그건 그토록 거룩하고, 그토록 가슴 뭉클하고, 그토록 지혜로워서 인간에게 그토록 큰 명예가 되는 일이지. 나로 말하자면, 인간이 하느님을 창조했는지 하느님이 인간을 창조했는지는 오래전부터 생각하지 않기로 작정했어. 그리고 그 주제를 두고 러시아 소년들이 정해 놓은 온갖 공리(公理)들을 일일이 짚어 보지는 않겠다, 죄다 유럽의 가설에서 끌어온 것들이거든. 거기서는 가설인 것이 러시아 소년에게는 공리고, 소년들뿐 아니라 그들을 가르치는 선생들에게도 그래, 우리 러시아 교수들도 흔히 그 소년들과 똑같으니까. 그래서 나는 모든 가설을 건너뛰겠어. 지금 우리가 노리는 게 뭐냐? 나는 되도록 빨리 내 본질을, 곧 내가 어떤 부류의 인간인지, 무엇을 믿는지, 무엇을 바라는지를 설명하려는 거야, 바로 그거지, 안 그러냐? 그러니 너한테 이렇게 말하겠다, 나는 하느님을 그냥 받아들인다고. 하지만 이건 새겨들어야 해. 만약 하느님이 존재하고 정녕 그분이 세계를 창조하셨다면, 우리 모두 알다시피 그분은 유클리드 기하학에 따라, 또 공간을 오직 삼차원으로만 파악하는 인간의 정신에 따라 세계를 창조하셨지. 그런데 예나 지금이나 기하학자와 철학자들이, 심지어 그중 가장 저명한 이들 중에도, 과연 온 우주가, 더 넓게 말해 존재 전체가 유클리드 기하학 안에서만 창조되었는지 의심하는 자들이 있었어. 그들은 심지어 유클리드에 따르면 지상에서는 결코 만날 수 없는 두 평행선이 무한 속 어딘가에서는 만날 수도 있다고 감히 꿈꾸기까지 하지. 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀어, 그것조차 이해하지 못하는 내가 하느님에 관해 이해하기를 바랄 수는 없다고 말이야. 나는 그런 문제들을 해결할 능력이 내게 없음을 겸허히 인정해, 나는 유클리드적인 지상의 정신을 지녔으니, 어떻게 이 세상의 것이 아닌 문제들을 풀 수 있겠니? 그러니 너에게도 충고하건대, 그런 건 절대로 생각하지 마라, 사랑하는 알료샤, 특히 하느님에 관해서는, 그분이 존재하는지 안 하는지 말이야. 그런 문제들은 죄다 오직 삼차원의 관념만 지니고 창조된 정신에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것들이거든. 그래서 나는 하느님을 받아들이고, 또 기꺼이 받아들이며, 게다가 그분의 지혜와 그분의 목적까지—우리의 이해를 완전히 넘어서는 것들이지만—받아들여. 나는 삶의 밑바닥에 깔린 질서와 의미를 믿어, 우리가 언젠가 그 속에 녹아들리라고들 말하는 그 영원한 조화를 믿어. 나는 온 우주가 그것을 향해 나아가는 그 말씀을 믿어, 그 자체로 '하느님과 함께' 있었고, 그 자체로 곧 하느님인 그 말씀을 말이야, 등등, 등등, 무한히. 그것을 가리키는 온갖 표현이 다 있지. 나는 올바른 길을 가고 있는 것 같지, 안 그래? 그런데 믿을 수 있겠니, 결국 나는 이 하느님의 세계를 받아들이지 않아, 그것이 존재한다는 걸 알면서도 도무지 받아들이지 못한다는 거야. 내가 하느님을 받아들이지 않는다는 게 아니야, 이걸 알아 둬야 해, 내가 받아들이지 못하고 받아들일 수 없는 건 그분이 창조하신 세계라는 거지. 분명하게 말해 줄게. 나는 어린아이처럼 믿어, 고통은 치유되고 보상받으리라고, 인간의 모순이 빚어내는 그 모든 굴욕적인 부조리가 가련한 신기루처럼, 무력하고 한없이 미소한 인간의 유클리드적 정신이 지어낸 비루한 날조처럼 사라져 버리리라고, 세계의 종막에 이르러 영원한 조화의 순간이 오면 그토록 소중한 무언가가 일어나서 그것이 모든 가슴을 채우고, 모든 원한을 달래고, 인류의 모든 죄악을, 그들이 흘린 모든 피를 속죄하기에 충분하리라고, 그리하여 인간들 사이에 일어난 모든 일을 용서할 수 있게 될 뿐 아니라 정당화할 수 있게까지 되리라고 말이야—하지만 그 모든 일이 일어난다 해도, 나는 받아들이지 않아. 받아들이지 않을 거야. 설령 평행선이 정말 만나고 내 눈으로 그걸 직접 본다 해도, 나는 그걸 보고 그것들이 만났다고 말은 하겠지만, 그래도 받아들이지는 않을 거야. 바로 그게 내 밑바닥에 깔려 있는 거야, 알료샤. 그게 내 신조지. 나는 진지하게 하는 말이야. 우리 이야기를 일부러 되도록 어리석게 시작했지만, 결국 내 고백으로 이끌어 온 거야, 네가 원하는 게 바로 그거였으니까. 너는 하느님에 관한 이야기를 듣고 싶었던 게 아니라, 단지 네가 사랑하는 형이 무얼 의지하며 살아가는지를 알고 싶었던 거지. 그래서 너한테 말해 준 거야.\"\n\n이반은 그 긴 장광설을 뜻밖에도 도드라진 감정으로 맺었다.\n\n\"그런데 형은 왜 '되도록 어리석게' 시작했어요?\" 알료샤가 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"우선은 러시아인이라는 이유에서지. 그런 주제를 두고 벌이는 러시아인의 대화는 언제나 상상도 못 할 만큼 어리석게 진행되거든. 그리고 둘째로, 어리석을수록 현실에 더 가까운 법이야. 어리석을수록 더 명료하지. 어리석음은 간결하고 꾸밈이 없는데, 영리함은 몸을 비틀어 자기를 숨기거든. 영리함은 악당이지만, 어리석음은 정직하고 솔직해. 나는 대화를 내 절망으로 이끌어 왔고, 그것을 어리석게 제시할수록 나한테는 더 나았던 거야.\"\n\n\"형이 왜 세계를 받아들이지 않는지 설명해 줄 거죠?\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"물론 설명하고말고, 그건 비밀이 아니야, 바로 그 이야기로 이끌어 온 거니까. 사랑하는 동생아, 나는 너를 타락시키거나 네 굳건한 보루에서 끌어내고 싶은 게 아니야, 어쩌면 나는 너한테서 치유받고 싶은 건지도 몰라.\" 이반이 별안간, 영락없이 어리고 온순한 아이처럼 미소 지었다. 알료샤는 형의 얼굴에서 그런 미소를 그전에는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다.\n\n# Chapter IV. Rebellion\n\n\"내가 너한테 한 가지 고백할 게 있다.\" 이반이 입을 열었다. \"나는 어떻게 사람이 제 이웃을 사랑할 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다. 내 생각으로는 멀리 떨어져 있는 사람이라면 또 몰라도, 바로 그 이웃이라는 자들이야말로 사랑할 수 없는 존재다. 언젠가 어디선가 자비로운 자 요한이라는 성인에 대해 읽은 적이 있는데, 굶주리고 얼어붙은 거지가 그를 찾아왔을 때 그는 거지를 제 잠자리에 받아들여 품에 안고, 무슨 끔찍한 병으로 썩어 문드러진 역겨운 입에 숨을 불어넣기 시작했다는 거다. 나는 확신한다, 그가 그렇게 한 것은 '자기 학대'에서, 거짓으로 자기를 괴롭히는 데서, 의무가 강제하는 자선을 위하여, 스스로에게 내린 속죄로서 그리한 것이라고. 누군가가 한 사람을 사랑하려면 그자는 모습을 감추고 있어야 한다, 왜냐하면 그가 얼굴을 드러내는 순간 사랑은 사라져 버리니까.\"\n\n\"조시마 장로께서도 그 이야기를 여러 번 하셨어요.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"장로님도 사랑에 익숙하지 못한 많은 사람들에게는 한 인간의 얼굴이 오히려 그를 사랑하는 데 방해가 되는 경우가 많다고 하셨거든요. 하지만 그래도 인류 안에는 사랑이 많이 있어요, 거의 그리스도와 같은 사랑이요. 나는 그걸 알아요, 이반 형.\"\n\n\"흠, 나는 아직 그런 건 전혀 모르겠고, 이해할 수도 없는데, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 인류가 그 점에서는 나와 한편이다. 문제는, 그게 인간의 나쁜 자질 때문인지 아니면 그들의 본성에 본래부터 박혀 있는 것인지 하는 거지. 내 생각으로는, 인간을 향한 그리스도와 같은 사랑이란 이 땅에서는 불가능한 기적이다. 그분은 신이셨다. 하지만 우리는 신이 아니지. 가령 내가 격심하게 고통받는다고 해 보자. 다른 사람은 내가 얼마나 고통받는지 결코 알 수 없다, 왜냐하면 그는 다른 사람이지 내가 아니니까. 게다가 더한 것은, 인간이란 좀처럼 남의 고통을 인정하려 들지 않는다는 거다(마치 그것이 무슨 영예라도 되는 양 말이지). 그가 왜 그걸 인정하지 않으려 하는지, 너는 짐작이 가니? 내게서 불쾌한 냄새가 나니까, 내 얼굴이 멍청하게 생겼으니까, 내가 언젠가 그의 발을 밟았으니까 그러는 거다. 게다가 고통에도 여러 종류가 있다. 나를 비천하게 만드는 모멸적이고 굴욕적인 고통—이를테면 굶주림 같은 것—이라면 나의 은인은 어쩌면 인정해 줄지도 모른다. 그러나 더 높은 고통—가령 어떤 이념을 위한 고통—에 이르면, 그는 그것을 좀처럼 인정하려 들지 않는다, 어쩌면 내 얼굴이, 이념을 위해 고통받는 사람이라면 마땅히 이러이러해야 한다고 그가 멋대로 상상하는 모습과는 전혀 다르게 보이기 때문이겠지. 그래서 그는 당장에 내게서 호의를 거두어 버린다, 그것도 결코 마음이 나빠서가 아니다. 거지들은, 특히 점잖은 거지들은, 결코 제 모습을 드러내서는 안 되고, 신문을 통해서나 자선을 청해야 한다. 사람은 추상적으로, 혹은 멀리 떨어져서라면 제 이웃을 사랑할 수 있지만, 가까이서는 그것이 거의 불가능하다. 만약 무대 위에서처럼, 발레에서처럼, 거지들이 비단 누더기와 해진 레이스를 걸치고 우아하게 춤추며 동냥을 청한다면, 그때야 사람은 그들을 바라보기를 좋아할지도 모른다. 하지만 그렇다 해도 우리는 그들을 사랑하지는 않을 거다. 아무튼 그 얘기는 이쯤 해 두자. 나는 그저 내 관점을 너한테 보여 주고 싶었을 뿐이니까. 나는 인류 전체의 고통에 대해 말하려 했지만, 차라리 아이들의 고통에 한정하는 편이 낫겠다. 그러면 내 논지의 범위가 본래의 십분의 일로 줄어들지. 그래도 아이들에게 국한하는 편이 낫다, 비록 그게 내 주장을 약화시키긴 하지만. 그러나 첫째로, 아이들은 가까이서도 사랑할 수 있다, 더럽혀져 있을 때조차, 못생겼을 때조차(하긴 아이들이 못생긴 경우란 없는 것 같지만). 내가 어른들에 대해 말하지 않으려는 두 번째 이유는, 그들은 역겹고 사랑받을 자격이 없는 데다가, 보상이라는 것을 가지고 있기 때문이다—그들은 사과를 먹었고 선과 악을 알게 되어, '신처럼' 되어 버렸으니까. 그들은 지금도 여전히 그 사과를 먹고 있지. 하지만 아이들은 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 그래서 아직은 무죄하다. 너는 아이들을 좋아하니, 알료샤? 네가 좋아한다는 걸 안다, 그러니 너는 내가 왜 아이들에 대해 말하기를 더 좋아하는지 이해할 거다. 만약 아이들마저 이 땅에서 끔찍하게 고통받는다면, 그것은 틀림없이 제 아비들의 죄 때문에 고통받는 것이고, 사과를 먹은 제 아비들을 대신해 벌받는 것이다. 그러나 그런 논리는 저세상의 것이며, 이 땅에 사는 인간의 마음으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 것이다. 무죄한 자가 남의 죄 때문에 고통받아서는 안 된다, 더구나 그렇게 무죄한 자들이라면 더욱! 너는 나를 보고 놀랄지도 모르겠다, 알료샤, 그러나 나 역시 아이들을 끔찍이 좋아한다. 그리고 잘 봐 둬라, 잔인한 사람들, 난폭한 자들, 탐욕스러운 자들, 카라마조프 집안 사람들도 더러는 아이들을 무척 좋아한단다. 아이들은 아주 어릴 적에는—가령 일곱 살까지는—어른들과 그토록 동떨어진 존재라서, 말하자면 다른 종에 속한 다른 피조물이다. 나는 감옥에서 한 범죄자를 알았는데, 그자는 강도질을 하는 동안 여러 아이들까지 포함해서 온 가족을 통째로 살해한 자였다. 그런데 막상 감옥에 들어와서는 아이들에게 묘한 애착을 보였다. 그는 온종일 창가에 붙어서서 감옥 마당에서 노는 아이들을 지켜보았다. 그는 한 어린 사내아이를 길들여 제 창가로 오게 만들고는 그 아이와 둘도 없는 친구가 되었지…… 내가 왜 너한테 이런 얘기를 다 늘어놓는지 모르겠지, 알료샤? 머리가 아프고 마음이 울적하구나.\"\n\n\"형 말투가 좀 이상해요.\" 알료샤가 불안해하며 말했다. \"마치 형이 형 자신이 아닌 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 얼마 전 모스크바에서 만난 어느 불가리아 사람이,\" 이반은 동생의 말을 듣지 못한 듯이 말을 이었다, \"슬라브인들이 전면적으로 봉기할까 두려워한 터키인과 체르케스인들이 불가리아 전역에서 저지른 범죄에 대해 내게 이야기해 주었다. 그들은 마을을 불태우고, 사람을 죽이고, 여자와 아이들을 능욕하고, 포로들의 귀를 울타리에 못 박아 아침까지 그대로 두었다가 아침이 되면 목매달아 죽인다—네가 상상도 못 할 온갖 짓들을 다 한다. 사람들은 더러 짐승 같은 잔인성이라고 말하지만, 그건 짐승들에 대한 크나큰 부당함이자 모욕이다. 짐승은 결코 인간만큼 잔인할 수가 없다, 그렇게 예술적으로 잔인할 수는 없다. 호랑이는 그저 찢고 물어뜯을 뿐, 그게 그놈이 할 수 있는 전부다. 호랑이는 설령 할 수 있다 한들 사람의 귀를 못 박을 생각 따위는 결코 하지 못할 거다. 이 터키인들은 아이들을 고문하는 데서도 쾌감을 느꼈다. 어머니의 자궁에서 아직 태어나지도 않은 아이를 잘라 내고, 갓난아기들을 공중으로 던져 올렸다가 그 어미들의 눈앞에서 총검 끝으로 받아 냈다. 어미들의 눈앞에서 그 짓을 한다는 것, 바로 그게 그 유희에 묘미를 더해 주었던 거지. 여기 내가 무척 흥미롭게 여긴 또 다른 장면이 있다. 한번 떠올려 봐라, 품에 아기를 안고 벌벌 떠는 어머니와, 그녀를 에워싼 침략자 터키인들의 무리를. 그들은 한 가지 여흥을 꾸민다. 아기를 어르고, 아기를 웃기려고 까르르 웃어 보인다. 마침내 성공해서 아기가 웃는다. 바로 그 순간 한 터키인이 아기 얼굴에서 네 치쯤 되는 거리에 권총을 겨눈다. 아기는 신이 나서 까르르 웃으며 권총을 향해 조그만 두 손을 뻗고, 그자는 아기 얼굴에 대고 방아쇠를 당겨 그 머리통을 날려 버린다. 예술적이지 않으냐? 그건 그렇고, 터키인들은 단것을 유난히 좋아한다더라.\"\n\n\"형, 대체 무슨 말을 하려는 거예요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔, 만약 악마가 존재하지 않고 인간이 악마를 창조해 낸 거라면, 인간은 제 형상과 모습을 본떠 악마를 창조한 거다.\"\n\n\"그럼 신을 창조했을 때와 똑같이요?\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"'어쩌면 그렇게 말을 잘도 비틀어 대는지', 『햄릿』에서 폴로니우스가 하는 말이지.\" 이반이 웃었다. \"너는 내 말을 거꾸로 돌려 나를 찌르는구나. 흠, 좋다, 기쁘다. 인간이 제 형상과 모습대로 신을 창조했다면, 네 신은 틀림없이 훌륭한 신일 거다. 너는 방금 내가 무슨 말을 하려는 거냐고 물었지. 알다시피 나는 어떤 종류의 사실들을 수집하기를 좋아한다, 그리고 믿어지겠니, 신문이며 책에서 어떤 종류의 일화들을 베껴 두기까지 하는데, 벌써 제법 그럴듯한 수집품이 모였단다. 물론 터키인들 얘기도 거기 들어갔지만, 그들은 외국인이지. 내겐 본국에서 나온, 터키인들보다 한층 더 나은 표본들이 있다. 너도 알다시피 우리는 매질을 더 즐긴다—회초리와 채찍 말이다—그게 우리 민족의 제도지. 귀를 못 박는 짓은 우리한테는 생각도 할 수 없는 일이다, 어쨌든 우리는 유럽인이니까. 하지만 회초리와 채찍은 우리가 늘 곁에 지니고 있고, 그건 우리한테서 빼앗아 갈 수도 없는 거다. 요즘 외국에서는 매질을 거의 하지 않지. 풍습이 더 인도적이 되었거나, 아니면 법이 제정되어 이제는 감히 사람을 매질하지 못하게 되었으니까. 하지만 그들은 우리 못지않게 민족적인 또 다른 방식으로 그걸 벌충한다. 그것도 어찌나 민족적인지 우리한테서는 거의 일어날 수 없는 일이지, 비록 우리 귀족 사회에 종교 운동이 시작된 이래로 우리도 거기에 물들어 가고 있는 것 같긴 하지만. 내겐 프랑스어에서 번역된 매혹적인 소책자가 한 권 있는데, 그 안에는 바로 얼마 전, 오 년 전에, 리샤르라는 살인자가 처형된 이야기가 적혀 있다—내 생각에 스물세 살쯤 된 젊은이로, 바로 그 단두대 위에서 회개하고 기독교 신앙으로 개종한 자였다. 이 리샤르는 사생아로서, 여섯 살 적에 부모에 의해 스위스 산속의 어느 목자들에게 넘겨진 아이였다. 목자들은 자기들을 위해 일을 시키려고 그를 길렀다. 그는 그들 틈에서 한 마리 작은 들짐승처럼 자라났다. 목자들은 그에게 아무것도 가르치지 않았고, 먹을 것도 입을 것도 거의 주지 않으면서, 일곱 살이 되자 추위와 빗속으로 내몰아 가축을 치게 했다. 누구 하나 그를 그렇게 대하는 것을 망설이거나 거리끼지 않았다. 오히려 그 반대로, 그들은 자기들에게 모든 권리가 있다고 여겼으니, 리샤르가 한낱 가축처럼 그들에게 넘겨진 물건이었던 까닭이며, 그들은 그를 먹여야 할 필요조차 느끼지 못했다. 리샤르 자신이 어떻게 그 시절을 보냈는지 이야기하는데, 복음서에 나오는 탕자처럼 그는 팔려고 살찌우는 돼지들에게 주는 죽이라도 먹고 싶어 애를 태웠다. 하지만 그들은 그것조차 주려 하지 않았고, 그가 돼지의 먹이를 훔쳐 먹으면 매질을 했다. 그렇게 그는 어린 시절과 젊은 시절을 다 보냈고, 마침내 자라나 달아나 도둑이 될 만큼 기운이 세지기에 이르렀다. 이 야만인은 제네바에서 날품팔이로 생계를 잇기 시작했다. 그는 벌어들인 것을 술로 탕진하고, 짐승처럼 살다가, 끝내 한 노인을 죽이고 강도질을 하기에 이르렀다. 그는 붙잡혀 재판을 받고 사형을 선고받았다. 그곳 사람들은 감상주의자가 아니거든. 그러자 감옥에서 그는 곧장 목사들, 기독교 형제단의 회원들, 자선을 베푸는 부인들, 그밖에 그런 부류의 사람들에게 둘러싸였다. 그들은 감옥에서 그에게 읽기와 쓰기를 가르치고 복음서를 풀이해 주었다. 그들은 그를 권면하고, 그에게 공을 들이고, 끊임없이 그를 두드려 대더니, 마침내 그가 자기 죄를 엄숙하게 고백하기에 이르렀다. 그는 개종했다. 그는 자기가 괴물이지만 마침내 신께서 자기에게 빛을 베푸시고 은총을 보여 주셨노라고 손수 법정에 편지를 써 보냈다. 제네바 전체가 그를 두고 흥분에 휩싸였다—자선과 신앙으로 가득 찬 제네바 전체가 말이다. 그 도시의 온갖 귀족과 교양 있는 사람들이 앞다투어 감옥으로 달려가 리샤르에게 입맞추고 그를 끌어안았다. '당신은 우리 형제요, 당신은 은총을 발견했소.' 그리고 리샤르는 감격에 겨워 울기만 할 뿐이다. '그렇소, 나는 은총을 발견했소! 내 어린 시절과 젊은 시절 내내 나는 돼지의 먹이라도 기꺼이 먹으려 했는데, 이제는 나 같은 자조차 은총을 발견했소. 나는 주 안에서 죽소.' '그렇소, 리샤르, 주 안에서 죽으시오. 당신은 피를 흘렸으니 마땅히 죽어야 하오. 비록 당신이 돼지의 먹이를 탐하고 그걸 훔쳤다 해서 매를 맞으며(훔치는 것은 금지된 일이니 그건 당신의 큰 잘못이었소) 주님을 알지 못한 것이 당신 잘못은 아니었다 해도, 어쨌든 당신은 피를 흘렸으니 죽어야 하오.' 그리고 마지막 날, 리샤르는 완전히 맥이 풀려서, 그저 울기만 하며 매 순간 되뇌었다. '오늘은 내 생애에서 가장 행복한 날이오. 나는 주님께로 가오.' '그렇소,' 목사들과 판사들과 자선을 베푸는 부인들이 외친다. '당신 생애에서 가장 행복한 날이오, 당신은 주님께로 가니 말이오!' 그들은 모두 죄수 호송 마차 뒤를 따라 행렬을 이루어 걷거나 마차를 타고 단두대로 향한다. 단두대에 이르자 그들은 리샤르를 향해 부르짖는다. '죽으시오, 형제여, 주 안에서 죽으시오, 당신마저 은총을 발견했으니!' 그리하여 형제들의 입맞춤에 뒤덮인 채 리샤르는 단두대 위로 끌려 올라가 기요틴 앞으로 인도된다. 그리고 그들은 형제다운 방식으로 그의 머리를 잘라 버렸다, 그가 은총을 발견했기 때문에 말이다. 그래, 그게 바로 특징적인 거다. 그 소책자는 귀족 신분이면서 복음주의적 열망을 품은 어느 러시아 자선가들에 의해 러시아어로 번역되어, 민중의 계몽을 위해 무료로 배포되었다. 리샤르의 사례가 흥미로운 까닭은 그것이 민족적이기 때문이다. 우리에게는 한 인간이 우리 형제가 되었고 은총을 발견했다 해서 그 목을 잘라 버린다는 게 터무니없는 일이지만, 그래도 우리에게는 우리만의 특기가 있으니, 그건 거의 그보다 더 나쁜 거다. 우리의 역사적 소일거리는 고통을 가하는 데서 직접적인 만족을 얻는 거다. 네크라소프의 시 가운데 한 농부가 말의 눈을, '그 온순한 눈을' 채찍질하는 광경을 묘사한 구절이 있는데, 누구든 그런 장면을 본 적이 있을 거다. 그건 유난히 러시아적이지. 그는 묘사한다, 어떻게 비실비실한 작은 말 한 마리가 너무 무거운 짐에 짓눌려 주저앉아 옴짝달싹 못 하게 되었는지를. 농부는 그놈을 때린다, 사납게 때린다, 마침내 잔인성에 도취되어 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채 때리고, 인정사정없이 몇 번이고 되풀이해 후려친다. '네가 아무리 약해 빠졌어도 끌어야 한다, 죽는 한이 있어도.' 말은 안간힘을 쓰고, 그러자 그는 그 가엾고 무력한 짐승의 울고 있는 눈을, 그 '온순한 눈을' 채찍질하기 시작한다. 미친 듯한 짐승은 온몸을 부들부들 떨고, 숨을 헐떡이며, 부자연스럽고 발작적인 동작으로 옆걸음을 치면서 짐을 끌어당긴다—네크라소프의 그 대목은 끔찍하다. 하지만 그건 한낱 말일 뿐이고, 신은 말을 매 맞으라고 주셨지. 타타르인들이 우리에게 그렇게 가르쳤고, 그 기념으로 채찍을 남겨 주었으니까. 하지만 사람도 매질당할 수 있다. 잘 배우고 교양 있는 한 신사와 그의 아내가 자기들의 친자식을, 일곱 살 난 계집아이를 자작나무 회초리로 때렸다. 나는 그 일의 정확한 기록을 가지고 있다. 그 아비는 회초리에 잔가지가 붙어 있는 걸 좋아했다. '이게 더 따끔하지', 하고 그는 말하고는, 제 딸을 따끔하게 만들기 시작했다. 나는 사실로 알고 있다, 매질을 한 대씩 할 때마다 관능으로, 글자 그대로의 관능으로 달아오르는 사람들이 있고, 그 관능은 매를 한 대씩 가할 때마다 점점 더 커져 간다는 것을. 그들은 일 분간, 오 분간, 십 분간 때린다, 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 사납게. 아이가 비명을 지른다. 마침내 아이는 비명조차 지르지 못하고 헐떡이며 외친다, '아빠! 아빠!' 무슨 악마 같은 점잖지 못한 우연으로 그 사건이 법정에까지 오게 되었다. 변호인이 선임된다. 러시아 민중은 오래전부터 변호사를 '돈으로 사는 양심'이라고 불러 왔다. 변호인은 자기 의뢰인을 변호하며 항변한다. '이건 아주 단순한 일입니다', 그는 말한다, '지극히 일상적인 가정사일 뿐입니다. 아버지가 제 자식을 바로잡아 준 것이지요. 부끄럽게도 이런 일이 법정에까지 끌려 나왔습니다.' 그에게 설득당한 배심원단은 무죄 평결을 내린다. 방청객은 그 고문자가 방면된 것에 환호하며 떠나갈 듯이 들끓는다. 아, 그 자리에 내가 없었던 게 분하구나! 나라면 그자를 기리는 모금이라도 하자고 제안했을 텐데! 매혹적인 그림이지.\n\n\"하지만 나한테는 아이들에 관한 더 좋은 얘깃거리가 있다. 나는 러시아 아이들에 관해 아주아주 많은 것을 수집해 두었다, 알료샤. 다섯 살 난 한 계집아이가 있었는데, 제 아비와 어미한테 미움을 받았다, '더없이 훌륭하고 존경받을 만한, 잘 배우고 교양 있는 사람들'한테 말이지. 알다시피, 나는 다시 한 번 되풀이해야겠다, 많은 사람들에게는 한 가지 독특한 특성이 있으니, 그게 바로 아이들을, 오직 아이들만을 고문하기를 즐기는 거다. 다른 모든 부류의 인간들에게는 이 고문자들이 교양 있고 인도적인 유럽인답게 온화하고 자애롭게 행동한다. 그러나 그들은 아이들을 괴롭히기를 무척 좋아하고, 그런 의미에서는 아이들 자체를 좋아하기까지 한다. 고문자를 유혹하는 것은 바로 그 아이들의 무방비함이고, 피할 곳도 호소할 데도 없는 아이의 그 천사 같은 신뢰야말로 그 비열한 피를 불타오르게 하는 거다. 물론 모든 인간 속에는 악마가 숨어 있다—분노의 악마, 고문당하는 희생자의 비명에 음탕하게 달아오르는 악마, 사슬에서 풀려난 무법의 악마, 악덕에 뒤따르는 온갖 질병의 악마, 통풍이며 신장병 따위의 악마가 말이지.\n\n\"이 가엾은 다섯 살배기 아이는 그 교양 있는 부모한테 온갖 고문이란 고문은 다 당했다. 그들은 아무 까닭도 없이 아이를 때리고, 후려치고, 발길질을 해서 마침내 그 몸뚱이가 온통 멍투성이가 되게 만들었다. 그러다가 그들은 한층 더 정교한 잔인성으로 나아갔다—추위와 서리 속에 밤새도록 아이를 변소에 가두어 두었는데, 아이가 밤중에 일으켜 달라고 청하지 않았다는 이유에서였다(마치 천사처럼 곤히 자는 다섯 살배기 아이가 깨어나서 청하도록 훈련될 수 있기라도 한 것처럼 말이지), 그러고는 아이의 얼굴에 똥을 처바르고 입에 똥을 가득 채웠다, 그것도 그 어미가, 그 어미가 이 짓을 했다. 그리고 그 어미는 가엾은 아이의 신음 소리를 들으면서도 잠을 잘 수 있었다! 너는 이해할 수 있겠니, 자기한테 무슨 짓이 가해지는지조차 이해하지 못하는 그 작은 피조물이, 어둠과 추위 속에서 조그만 주먹으로 제 아프고 작은 가슴을 두드리며, 자기를 지켜 달라고 다정하고 인자한 신께 그 온순하고 원망 없는 눈물을 흘렸다는 것을? 너는 그걸 이해할 수 있겠니, 벗이여 형제여, 경건하고 겸손한 수도 견습생이여? 너는 이 끔찍한 일이 왜 일어나야 하고 왜 용납되고 있는지 이해할 수 있겠니? 사람들 말로는, 그게 없었더라면 인간은 이 땅에 존재할 수 없었을 거라더라, 그게 없었더라면 인간이 선과 악을 알 수 없었을 테니까. 그렇게 비싼 대가를 치러야 하는 그 악마 같은 선과 악을 인간이 대체 왜 알아야 한단 말이냐? 아니, 그 모든 지식의 세계를 다 합쳐도 그 아이가 '다정하고 인자한 신'께 드린 기도 하나만 한 값어치도 없다! 어른들의 고통에 대해서는 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다, 그들은 사과를 먹었으니까, 빌어먹을 놈들, 죄다 악마한테나 잡혀가라지! 하지만 이 어린것들은! 내가 너를 괴롭히고 있구나, 알료샤, 너는 네 정신이 아니다. 네가 원하면 그만두마.\"\n\n\"괜찮아요. 나도 함께 괴로워하고 싶어요.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"한 장면만 더, 딱 한 장면만 더 얘기하마, 어찌나 기이하고 어찌나 특징적인지, 게다가 나도 방금에야 어떤 러시아 고문헌집에서 읽은 거니까. 제목은 잊어버렸다. 다시 찾아봐야겠어. 그건 금세기 초, 농노제가 가장 암울하던 시절의 일이었다, 인민 해방자께 만세를! 그 시절에 귀족 가문에 연줄이 닿고 광대한 영지를 거느린 한 장군이 있었는데, 그자는—내 생각엔 그 무렵에도 다소 예외적인 부류였지만—군 복무에서 물러나 한가로운 삶으로 접어들면서, 자기가 신민들의 목숨에 대해 절대적인 권력을 얻었다고 확신하는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 그 시절엔 그런 사람들이 있었지. 그래서 우리의 이 장군은 이천 명의 영혼을 거느린 영지에 자리를 잡고, 호화롭게 살면서, 마치 가난한 이웃들이 제 식객이나 어릿광대라도 되는 양 그들 위에 군림했다. 그에게는 수백 마리의 사냥개를 기르는 개집과, 거의 백 명에 이르는 개지기 소년들이 있었는데—다들 말을 타고 제복을 갖춰 입었다. 어느 날 한 농노 소년, 여덟 살 난 어린아이가 장난삼아 돌을 던졌는데 그게 장군이 아끼는 사냥개의 발을 다치게 했다. '왜 내가 아끼는 개가 절뚝거리느냐?' 누군가가 소년이 던진 돌이 개의 발을 다치게 했다고 그에게 아뢴다. '그래, 네놈이 했단 말이지.' 장군은 아이를 위아래로 훑어보았다. '저놈을 끌고 가라.' 아이는 끌려갔다—제 어미한테서 끌려가 밤새도록 갇혀 있었다. 이튿날 이른 아침에 장군은 사냥개들과 식객들, 개지기 소년들, 사냥꾼들을 거느리고 말을 타고 나오는데, 다들 그를 에워싸고 말에 올라 완벽한 사냥 행렬을 이루고 있다. 하인들은 교화를 받으라고 소집되고, 그들 모두의 앞에 그 아이의 어미가 서 있다. 아이가 유치장에서 끌려 나온다. 음울하고 춥고 안개 낀 가을날, 사냥하기에는 더없이 좋은 날이다. 장군은 아이의 옷을 벗기라고 명령한다. 아이는 발가벗겨진다. 아이는 공포로 몸이 굳은 채 부들부들 떨며, 감히 울지도 못한다…… '저놈을 뛰게 하라', 장군이 명령한다. '뛰어라! 뛰어!' 개지기 소년들이 소리친다. 아이가 뛴다…… '저놈을 물어라!' 장군이 고함을 지르며, 사냥개 떼를 통째로 그 아이에게 풀어 놓는다. 사냥개들이 아이를 붙잡아, 제 어미가 보는 앞에서 갈가리 찢어 버린다!…… 내 생각엔 그 장군은 나중에 영지를 관리할 능력이 없다는 판정을 받았던 것 같다. 자—그자가 마땅히 받아야 할 게 뭐였을까? 총살? 우리의 도덕적 감정을 만족시키기 위해 총살시켜야 할까? 말해 봐라, 알료샤!\"\n\n\"총살이요.\" 알료샤가 창백하게 일그러진 미소를 띤 채 이반에게로 눈을 들어 올리며 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 이반이 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"심지어 너까지 그렇게 말한다면…… 너도 어지간한 수도사로구나! 그러니까 네 마음속에도 작은 악마가 한 마리 앉아 있는 거다, 알료샤 카라마조프!\"\n\n\"내가 한 말은 터무니없는 거였어요, 하지만—\"\n\n\"바로 그 점이다, 그 '하지만'이!\" 이반이 외쳤다. \"들어 봐라, 견습생아, 이 땅에는 터무니없는 것이 너무도 필요하다는 걸 말이다. 세상은 터무니없는 것들 위에 서 있고, 어쩌면 그게 없었더라면 세상에서는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았을 거다. 우리는 우리가 아는 것을 안다!\"\n\n\"형이 뭘 아는데요?\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 이해 못 한다.\" 이반은 헛소리라도 하듯 말을 이었다. \"나는 이제 아무것도 이해하고 싶지 않다. 나는 사실에 매달리고 싶다. 나는 오래전에 이해하지 않기로 마음먹었다. 내가 무엇이든 이해하려 든다면 나는 그 사실을 배반하게 될 거고, 그래서 나는 그 사실에 매달리기로 작정했다.\"\n\n\"왜 자꾸 나를 시험하는 거예요?\" 알료샤가 갑작스러운 괴로움에 사로잡혀 외쳤다. \"이제 형이 무슨 말을 하려는지 좀 말해 줄래요?\"\n\n\"물론 말하고말고. 그게 바로 내가 여태 끌어 온 이야기다. 너는 나한테 소중한 사람이라, 나는 너를 놓아주고 싶지 않다, 너를 네 조시마한테 넘겨주지 않겠다.\"\n\n이반은 잠시 입을 다물었고, 그의 얼굴은 별안간 몹시 슬퍼졌다.\n\n\"들어 봐라! 나는 내 주장을 더 분명히 하려고 아이들의 경우만을 들었던 거다. 이 땅이 그 껍질에서 핵심에 이르기까지 흠뻑 젖어 있는 인류의 그 밖의 눈물에 대해서는 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 나는 일부러 내 주제를 좁혔다. 나는 한 마리 벌레이고, 세상이 왜 이런 식으로 짜여 있는지 내가 이해할 수 없다는 걸 더없이 겸허하게 인정한다. 인간 자신에게 잘못이 있는 거겠지, 아마. 그들에게는 낙원이 주어졌건만 그들은 자유를 원했고, 자기들이 불행해지리라는 걸 알면서도 하늘에서 불을 훔쳤다. 그러니 그들을 가엾이 여길 필요는 없다. 나의 가련하고 지상적이고 유클리드적인 이해력으로 내가 아는 거라곤, 고통이 존재하지만 죄지은 자는 아무도 없다는 것, 원인 뒤에 결과가 단순하고 직접적으로 뒤따른다는 것, 모든 것이 흘러가며 제 수준을 찾는다는 것뿐이다—하지만 그건 그저 유클리드적인 헛소리일 뿐이고, 나도 그걸 알지만, 나는 그걸로 살아가는 데 동의할 수 없다! 죄지은 자가 아무도 없고 원인 뒤에 결과가 단순하고 직접적으로 뒤따른다는 것, 게다가 내가 그걸 안다는 것이 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠느냐?—나는 정의를 얻어야 한다, 아니면 나 자신을 파멸시키겠다. 그것도 어딘가 아득한 무한의 시간과 공간 속에서의 정의가 아니라, 바로 여기 이 땅 위에서, 그것도 내 눈으로 직접 볼 수 있는 정의를 말이다. 나는 그것을 믿어 왔다. 나는 그것을 보고 싶고, 만약 그때 내가 죽어 있다면 다시 살아나게라도 해 다오, 왜냐하면 그 모든 일이 나 없이 일어난다면 그건 너무도 불공평할 테니까. 설마 내가 고통받은 게, 그저 나라는 인간이, 내 죄와 내 고통이, 다른 누군가를 위한 미래의 조화의 토양에 거름이나 되라고 그런 건 아니겠지. 나는 내 눈으로 직접 보고 싶다, 암사슴이 사자 곁에 눕고 살해당한 자가 일어나 제 살해자를 끌어안는 것을. 나는 모두가 별안간 그 모든 것이 무엇을 위한 것이었는지 깨닫는 그 자리에 있고 싶다. 세상의 모든 종교가 이 갈망 위에 세워져 있고, 나는 믿는 자다. 하지만 그렇다면 아이들이 있는데, 그 아이들에 대해서는 나는 어떻게 해야 한단 말이냐? 그건 내가 대답할 수 없는 물음이다. 백 번째로 되풀이하지만, 물음은 수없이 많다, 그러나 나는 오직 아이들의 경우만을 들었다, 그 경우에는 내가 말하려는 바가 반박할 길 없이 분명하니까. 들어 봐라! 만약 모두가 영원한 조화의 값을 치르려고 고통받아야 한다면, 아이들이 거기에 무슨 상관이란 말이냐, 어디 말 좀 해 봐라? 아이들이 왜 고통받아야 하는지, 왜 그 조화의 값을 치러야 하는지, 그건 도무지 이해할 수 없는 일이다. 그 아이들마저 왜 미래의 조화를 위해 토양을 기름지게 할 재료를 대 주어야 하느냐? 나는 인간들 사이의 죄에 있어서의 연대성은 이해한다. 응보에 있어서의 연대성도 이해한다. 하지만 아이들과는 그런 연대성이 있을 수 없다. 그리고 만약 아이들이 정말로 제 아비들의 모든 죄에 대한 책임을 나누어 져야 한다는 게 사실이라면, 그런 진리는 이 세상의 것이 아니며 내 이해력을 넘어서는 거다. 어떤 익살꾼은 어쩌면 이렇게 말할지도 모르지, 그 아이가 자라났다면 죄를 지었을 거라고. 하지만 알다시피 그 아이는 자라나지 못했고, 여덟 살의 나이에 개들한테 갈가리 찢기지 않았느냐. 오, 알료샤, 나는 신성을 모독하는 게 아니다! 나도 물론 이해한다, 하늘과 땅의 모든 것이 하나의 찬미의 노래로 어우러지고, 살아 있는 모든 것과 살았던 모든 것이 큰 소리로 부르짖을 때—'주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다, 당신의 길이 드러났음이니이다'—그것이 얼마나 우주를 뒤흔드는 일이 될지를. 자기 아이를 개들한테 던져 준 그 악귀를 어머니가 끌어안고, 셋이 다 함께 눈물을 흘리며 큰 소리로 부르짖을 때, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다!'라고—그때야 물론 지식의 면류관에 다다를 것이고 모든 것이 분명해질 거다. 하지만 여기서 나를 멈춰 세우는 건, 내가 그 조화를 받아들일 수 없다는 거다. 그래서 내가 이 땅 위에 있는 동안 나는 서둘러 내 나름의 대책을 강구하는 거다. 알다시피, 알료샤, 어쩌면 정말로 그런 일이 일어날지도 모르지, 만약 내가 그 순간까지 살아 있거나 다시 살아나 그것을 보게 된다면, 나 역시 어쩌면 다른 사람들과 함께 큰 소리로 부르짖을지도 모른다, 아이의 고문자를 끌어안고 있는 어머니를 바라보며, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다!'라고. 하지만 나는 그때 가서 부르짖고 싶지 않다. 아직 시간이 있는 동안에 나는 서둘러 나 자신을 지키려는 거고, 그래서 나는 그 더 높은 조화를 통째로 거부한다. 그건 똥내 나는 변소에서 조그만 주먹으로 제 가슴을 두드리며 '다정하고 인자한 신'께 그 속죄받지 못한 눈물로 기도한, 그 고문당한 아이 하나의 눈물만 한 값어치도 없다! 그건 그만한 값어치가 없다, 그 눈물이 속죄되지 않은 채로 남아 있기 때문이다. 그 눈물은 반드시 속죄되어야 한다, 그렇지 않으면 어떤 조화도 있을 수 없다. 그런데 어떻게? 너는 그 눈물을 어떻게 속죄하려느냐? 그게 가능키나 하냐? 그들의 원수를 갚음으로써? 하지만 내가 그들의 원수를 갚는 일에 무슨 관심이 있겠느냐? 압제자들을 위한 지옥이 나한테 무슨 상관이냐? 그 아이들이 이미 고문을 당해 버린 마당에, 지옥이 무슨 소용이 있단 말이냐? 그리고 지옥이 있다면, 조화는 대체 어떻게 되는 거냐? 나는 용서하고 싶다. 나는 끌어안고 싶다. 나는 더 이상의 고통을 원하지 않는다. 그리고 만약 아이들의 고통이 진리의 값을 치르는 데 필요했던 고통의 총량을 불리는 데 보태진다면, 나는 단언하건대 그 진리는 그만한 대가를 치를 값어치가 없다. 나는 어머니가 제 아들을 개들한테 던져 준 압제자를 끌어안기를 원치 않는다! 그녀는 감히 그자를 용서해서는 안 된다! 정 그러고 싶다면 자기 자신을 위해서라면 그자를 용서하라지, 어미의 가슴이 겪은 그 헤아릴 수 없는 고통에 대해서라면 그 고문자를 용서하라지. 그러나 고문당한 제 아이의 고통만큼은 그녀에게 용서할 권리가 없다. 설령 그 아이가 그자를 용서한다 해도, 그녀는 감히 그 고문자를 용서해서는 안 된다! 그리고 만약 그렇다면, 그들이 감히 용서할 수 없다면, 조화는 대체 어떻게 되는 거냐? 온 세상에 용서할 권리가 있고 또 용서할 수 있는 존재가 단 하나라도 있겠느냐? 나는 조화를 원하지 않는다. 인류에 대한 사랑에서 나는 그것을 원하지 않는다. 차라리 나는 원수 갚지 못한 고통과 함께 남겠다. 차라리 나는, 설령 내가 틀렸다 할지라도, 원수 갚지 못한 내 고통과 채워지지 못한 분노와 함께 남겠다. 게다가 조화를 위해서는 너무도 비싼 값이 요구된다. 그 안으로 들어가려고 그토록 많은 값을 치르는 건 우리 분수에 넘치는 일이다. 그래서 나는 서둘러 내 입장권을 돌려준다, 그리고 내가 정직한 사람이라면 나는 되도록 빨리 그것을 돌려줄 의무가 있다. 그리고 나는 그렇게 하고 있는 거다. 내가 받아들이지 않는 건 신이 아니다, 알료샤, 다만 나는 더없이 정중하게 그분께 입장권을 돌려드릴 뿐이다.\"\n\n\"그건 반역이에요.\" 알료샤가 눈을 내리깐 채 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"반역이라고? 네가 그걸 그렇게 부르다니 유감이구나.\" 이반이 진지하게 말했다. \"반역 속에서는 누구도 살아가기 어렵다, 그런데 나는 살고 싶거든. 어디 너 자신이 말해 봐라, 내가 너한테 던지는 도전이다—대답해 봐라. 네가 인간의 운명이라는 직물을 짜고 있다고 상상해 봐라, 결국 인간들을 행복하게 만들어 마침내 그들에게 평화와 안식을 주는 것이 목적인데, 다만 그러려면 단 하나의 작디작은 피조물을—이를테면 제 주먹으로 가슴을 두드리던 그 갓난아이를—고문해 죽이는 것이 꼭 필요하고 피할 수 없는 일이고, 그 속죄받지 못한 눈물 위에 그 건축물을 세워야만 한다고 해 보자, 그런 조건이라면 너는 그 건축가가 되기를 승낙하겠느냐? 말해 봐라, 그리고 진실을 말해라.\"\n\n\"아니요, 나는 승낙하지 않겠어요.\" 알료샤가 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 너는 이런 생각을 받아들일 수 있겠느냐, 네가 그걸 지어 주는 그 인간들이, 한 작은 희생자의 속죄받지 못한 피를 토대 삼아 제 행복을 받아들이는 데 동의하리라는 생각을? 그리고 그걸 받아들이고서도 영원토록 행복하게 남아 있으리라는 생각을?\"\n\n\"아니요, 나는 그걸 받아들일 수 없어요. 형,\" 알료샤가 별안간 눈을 번득이며 말했다, \"형은 방금 온 세상에 용서할 권리가 있고 또 용서할 수 있는 존재가 있겠느냐고 했죠? 하지만 그런 존재가 계세요, 그분은 모든 것을, 모든 이를, 모든 이를 위하여 용서하실 수 있어요, 왜냐하면 그분은 모든 이와 모든 것을 위하여 당신의 무죄한 피를 내어 주셨으니까요. 형은 그분을 잊어버렸어요, 그리고 그 건축물은 바로 그분 위에 세워져 있고, 사람들이 큰 소리로 부르짖는 것도 바로 그분을 향해서예요, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다, 당신의 길이 드러났음이니이다!'라고요.\"\n\n\"아! 죄 없으신 그분과 그분의 피란 말이지! 아니, 나는 그분을 잊지 않았다. 오히려 나는 줄곧 네가 어째서 그분을 더 일찍 끌어들이지 않는지 궁금해하고 있었다, 보통 네 쪽 주장은 죄다 그분을 맨 앞에 내세우니까 말이다. 그런데 알료샤—웃지 마라! 나는 한 일 년쯤 전에 서사시를 한 편 지었단다. 만약 네가 나한테 십 분만 더 허비할 수 있다면, 그걸 너한테 들려주마.\"\n\n\"형이 서사시를 썼다고요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니다, 쓴 건 아니다,\" 이반이 웃었다, \"나는 평생 시라곤 두 줄도 써 본 적이 없다. 하지만 이건 산문으로 지어 낸 서사시인데, 내가 그걸 기억하고 있단다. 그걸 지어 낼 때 나는 푹 빠져 있었지. 너는 내 첫 독자가—아니 청자가 되는 거다. 작가가 단 한 명의 청자인들 마다할 까닭이 어디 있겠느냐?\" 이반이 미소 지었다. \"어디 너한테 들려줄까?\"\n\n\"나는 온통 귀를 기울이고 있어요.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"내 서사시의 제목은 '대심문관'이다. 우스꽝스러운 물건이지만, 그래도 나는 그걸 너한테 들려주고 싶구나.\"\n\n# Chapter V. The Grand Inquisitor\n\n“이것 역시 무슨 머리말이 있어야겠지—그러니까 문학적인 머리말 말이야.” 이반이 웃으며 말했다. “하긴 나는 그런 걸 짓는 데는 영 서툰 사람이지만. 알다시피 내 이야기는 16세기를 배경으로 하는데, 그 무렵에는—아마 너도 학교에서 배웠겠지만—시 속에서 천상의 권능들을 지상으로 끌어내리는 것이 관례였어. 단테는 말할 것도 없거니와, 프랑스에서는 수도원의 수사들뿐 아니라 성직 서기들까지도 정식 공연을 벌이곤 했는데, 거기엔 성모와 성인들, 천사들, 그리스도, 그리고 하느님까지도 무대에 올랐어. 그 시절엔 이 모든 것이 더없이 소박하게 행해졌지. 빅토르 위고의 『노트르담 드 파리』를 보면, 루이 11세 치세에 파리 시청에서 황태자의 탄생을 기념하여 백성들에게 교훈적이고 무료한 구경거리가 마련되는 대목이 나오잖아. 그건 『지극히 거룩하고 자비로우신 동정녀 마리아의 선한 심판』이라고 불렸는데, 거기서 마리아가 친히 무대에 등장해 자신의 ‘선한 심판’을 선포하지. 이와 비슷한 극들은, 주로 구약을 소재로 삼은 것이긴 했으되, 모스크바에서도 표트르 대제 시대에 이르기까지 이따금 상연되었어. 그러나 이런 극들 말고도 온갖 전설과 민요가 세상에 흩어져 있었는데, 거기엔 필요에 따라 성인들과 천사들, 천상의 모든 권능이 등장했지. 우리 수도원들에서도 수사들이 그런 시들을 번역하고 베끼고, 심지어 직접 짓는 일에 매달렸는데—타타르족 치하에서까지도 그랬어. 가령 그런 시가 하나 있는데(물론 그리스어에서 옮긴 것이지) 『성모의 지옥 순례기』라는 것으로, 단테 못지않게 대담한 묘사가 담겨 있어. 성모께서 지옥을 찾아가시는데, 대천사 미카엘이 그분을 인도하며 온갖 형벌의 광경을 보여 드리지. 성모는 죄인들과 그들의 형벌을 목격하셔. 그곳에서 다른 무리들 가운데서도 한 무리의 주목할 만한 죄인들이 불타는 호수 속에 있는 것을 보시는데, 그중 어떤 자들은 호수 바닥으로 가라앉아 다시는 헤엄쳐 나오지 못하고, ‘이들은 하느님께서 잊으신 자들’이라는 거야—실로 비범한 깊이와 힘을 지닌 표현이지. 그리하여 성모는 충격을 받고 눈물을 흘리시며 하느님의 보좌 앞에 엎드려, 지옥에 있는 모든 자들을 위해—그곳에서 보신 모든 자들을 가리지 않고—자비를 베풀어 달라고 비셔. 그분과 하느님의 대화는 더없이 흥미롭지. 성모께서는 하느님께 간청하시며 결코 물러서려 하지 않으시는데, 하느님께서 십자가에 못 박힌 당신 아드님의 손과 발을 가리키시며 ‘내 어찌 그를 괴롭힌 자들을 용서하겠느냐?’ 하고 물으시자, 성모는 모든 성인과 모든 순교자, 모든 천사와 대천사들에게 자신과 함께 엎드려 차별 없이 모든 자를 위해 자비를 빌라고 명하셔. 마침내 성모는 하느님으로부터 해마다 성금요일부터 삼위일체 주일까지 형벌을 멈추어 주는 유예를 얻어 내시고, 그러자 죄인들은 곧장 지옥에서 감사의 함성을 올리며 ‘주여, 이 심판은 의로우십니다’ 하고 노래한다는 거야. 자, 내 시가 그 시절에 나왔더라면 바로 이런 부류였을 거야. 내 시에서도 그분이 무대에 등장하시는데, 다만 아무 말씀도 하지 않으시고 그저 나타났다가 지나가실 뿐이야. 그분이 영광 가운데 다시 오시겠다고 약속하신 지 열다섯 세기가 흘렀고, 그분의 예언자가 ‘보라, 내가 속히 오리라’고 쓴 지도 열다섯 세기가 지났지. ‘그날과 그때는 아무도 모르나니, 아들도 모르고 오직 아버지만 아시느니라’ 하고 그분 친히 지상에서 예언하셨던 그대로 말이야. 그러나 인류는 여전히 같은 믿음과 같은 사랑으로 그분을 기다리고 있어. 아니, 더 큰 믿음으로 기다리고 있지. 인간이 하늘로부터 오는 징표를 보지 못하게 된 지 어느덧 열다섯 세기가 지났으니까.\n\n오늘날 하늘에서는 아무런 징표도 내려오지 않으니 마음이 일러 주는 것에 무엇 하나 더할 길이 없도다.\n\n남은 것이라곤 오직 마음이 일러 주는 것에 대한 믿음뿐이었어. 하긴 그 시절에는 기적도 많았던 것이 사실이야. 기적 같은 치유를 행한 성인들도 있었고, 어떤 거룩한 이들은 그들의 전기에 따르면 하늘의 여왕께서 친히 찾아오신 일도 있었지. 그러나 악마도 잠들지 않았기에, 사람들 사이에서는 이미 그런 기적들의 진위를 의심하는 마음이 싹트고 있었어. 그리고 바로 그 무렵 독일 북부에 무시무시한 새 이단이 나타났지. ‘횃불 같은(즉 교회 같은) 큰 별 하나가 물의 근원들 위에 떨어지매 그 물들이 쓰게 되더라.’ 이 이단자들은 신성을 모독하며 기적을 부정하기 시작했어. 그러나 믿음을 지킨 자들은 그럴수록 더욱 뜨겁게 믿었지. 인류의 눈물은 예전처럼 그분께로 솟아올랐고, 그분의 오심을 기다리며 그분을 사랑하고 그분께 희망을 걸었으며, 예전처럼 그분을 위해 고난받고 죽기를 갈망했어. 그렇게 여러 세대에 걸쳐 인류는 믿음과 열정으로 ‘주 우리 하느님이시여, 어서 오소서’ 하고 기도했고, 그토록 오랜 세월 그분을 불렀기에, 마침내 그분께서는 그 무한한 자비로 당신의 종들에게로 내려오기를 허락하셨어. 그날 이전에도 그분은 내려오신 적이 있어, 어떤 거룩한 이들과 순교자들과 은수자들을 찾아오셨던 것이, 그들의 행전에 적혀 있는 그대로지. 우리네 사람으로는 튜체프가, 자기 말의 진실성을 절대적으로 믿으며 이렇게 증언했어.\n\n십자가를 지고 종의 모습으로 지치고 야위신 하늘의 임금님이 우리 어머니 러시아를 축복하시며 온 땅을 두루 헤매며 다니셨네.\n\n그리고 그것은 분명히 그러했어, 내가 장담하지.\n\n“그리하여 보라, 그분께서는 잠시 동안 백성들에게, 곧 죄악에 빠져 있으되 어린아이처럼 그분을 사랑하는, 고통받고 시달리는 백성들에게 나타나기를 허락하셨어. 내 이야기의 무대는 에스파냐, 세비야야. 종교 재판이 가장 끔찍했던 시절, 날마다 하느님의 영광을 위해 불길이 타올랐고 ‘화려한 화형식 속에서 사악한 이단자들이 불태워지던’ 그 시절이지. 오, 물론 이것은 약속하신 대로 시대의 종말에 그분이 온 천상의 영광 가운데 ‘번개가 동에서 서로 번쩍이는 것같이’ 홀연히 나타나실 그 오심은 아니야. 아니야, 그분은 다만 잠시 동안, 그것도 이단자들 주위로 불길이 탁탁 타오르는 바로 그곳으로 당신의 자녀들을 찾아오셨을 뿐이야. 그분은 무한한 자비로, 열다섯 세기 전 삼 년 동안 사람들 사이를 거니실 때 지니셨던 바로 그 인간의 모습으로 다시 한번 사람들 가운데 오셨어. 그분은 남쪽 도시의 ‘뜨거운 포석’ 위로 내려오셨는데, 바로 그 전날 그곳에서는 추기경이자 종교 재판소장이 ‘하느님의 더 큰 영광을 위하여’ 거의 백 명에 이르는 이단자들을, 임금과 궁정과 기사들과 추기경들과 더없이 아리따운 궁정 귀부인들과 세비야의 온 백성이 지켜보는 가운데, 장엄한 화형식을 거행하여 불태운 터였어.\n* 화형식: 종교 재판소가 이단자에게 판결을 선고하고 처형하던 공개 의식. 원어로는 ‘아우토 다 페(auto da fé)’, 곧 ‘신앙의 행위’라는 뜻.\n\n“그분은 가만히, 아무도 모르게 오셨지. 그런데도 이상한 일이지만, 모든 사람이 그분을 알아보았어. 이것이 어쩌면 이 시에서 가장 빼어난 대목 가운데 하나일지도 몰라. 그러니까, 사람들이 어째서 그분을 알아보았는가 하는 것 말이야. 백성들은 거역할 수 없는 힘에 이끌려 그분께로 다가가고, 그분을 둘러싸고, 그분 주위로 몰려들며 그분을 따르지. 그분은 끝없는 연민이 어린 부드러운 미소를 띠고 그들 한가운데를 말없이 거니셔. 그분의 가슴에서는 사랑의 태양이 타오르고, 두 눈에서는 빛과 권능이 비쳐 나오며, 그 광채가 백성들에게 쏟아져 그들의 가슴에 화답하는 사랑을 불러일으켜. 그분이 그들에게 손을 내밀어 축복하시니, 그분과의 접촉에서, 심지어 그분 옷자락과의 접촉에서도 병을 고치는 힘이 흘러나오지. 군중 속에서 어려서부터 눈먼 한 노인이 ‘오 주여, 저를 고쳐 주소서, 그러면 당신을 뵐 수 있겠나이다!’ 하고 외치니, 마치 그의 눈에서 비늘이 떨어지듯 눈먼 자가 그분을 보게 돼. 군중은 흐느끼며 그분 발밑의 땅에 입을 맞춰. 아이들은 그분 앞에 꽃을 뿌리며 노래하고 호산나를 외쳐. ‘저분이야—저분이셔!’ 모두가 입을 모아 되뇌어. ‘틀림없이 저분이셔, 저분 말고 다른 분일 리 없어!’ 그분은 세비야 대성당 계단 앞에서 걸음을 멈추시는데, 마침 흐느끼는 조문객들이 작고 뚜껑 없는 흰 관 하나를 들여오던 참이야. 그 안에는 어느 이름난 시민의 외동딸인 일곱 살배기 소녀가 누워 있어. 죽은 아이는 꽃에 파묻혀 보이지 않을 지경이야. ‘저분이 당신 아이를 살려 주실 거예요!’ 군중이 흐느끼는 어머니에게 외쳐. 관을 맞으러 나온 사제는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 눈살을 찌푸리지만, 죽은 아이의 어머니는 통곡하며 그분 발밑에 몸을 던져. ‘당신이 정녕 그분이시거든, 제 아이를 살려 주소서!’ 그녀가 그분께 두 손을 내밀며 부르짖어. 행렬이 멈추고, 관이 그분 발밑 계단 위에 놓여. 그분은 연민 어린 눈길로 바라보시고, 그 입술이 다시 한번 부드럽게 ‘소녀야, 일어나라!’ 하고 말씀하시니, 소녀가 일어나. 어린 소녀는 관 속에서 몸을 일으켜 두리번거리며, 손에 쥐여 준 흰 장미 한 다발을 든 채, 놀라 동그래진 눈으로 미소를 짓는 거야.\n\n“백성들 사이에서 외침과 흐느낌과 혼란이 일어. 바로 그 순간 추기경이자 종교 재판소장이 친히 대성당 곁을 지나가지. 그는 노인이야, 거의 아흔에 가깝고, 키가 크고 꼿꼿하며, 메마른 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 두 눈에는 아직도 한 줄기 빛이 남아 있어. 그는 어제, 로마 교회의 적들을 불태우던 그날처럼 화려한 추기경 예복을 걸치고 있지 않아—이 순간 그는 거칠고 낡은 수사의 법의를 입고 있어. 그 뒤로는 멀찍이서 음울한 시종들과 노예들, 그리고 ‘성스러운 호위대’가 따라오지. 그는 군중을 보고 걸음을 멈추어 멀리서 그 광경을 지켜봐. 그는 모든 것을 보지. 사람들이 그분 발밑에 관을 내려놓는 것을 보고, 아이가 일어나는 것을 보자, 그의 얼굴이 어두워져. 그는 짙은 잿빛 눈썹을 찌푸리고, 두 눈에서는 불길한 불꽃이 번뜩여. 그는 손가락을 들어 호위병들에게 그분을 붙잡으라고 명해. 그의 권세가 어찌나 대단한지, 백성들이 어찌나 철저히 겁에 질려 그에게 떨며 복종하는지, 군중은 즉시 호위병들에게 길을 터 주고, 죽음 같은 정적 속에서 그들이 그분께 손을 대어 끌고 가는 거야. 군중은 그 순간 한 사람처럼 일제히 늙은 종교 재판소장 앞에 땅에 엎드려. 그는 말없이 백성을 축복하고 지나가지. 호위병들은 그 죄수를 종교 재판소의 옛 궁전에 있는 비좁고 음침한 둥근 천장의 감옥으로 끌고 가 가두어. 하루가 지나고, 어둡고 후텁지근한 세비야의 ‘숨 막히는’ 밤이 뒤따라. 공기에는 ‘월계수와 레몬 향기가 그윽해.’ 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 별안간 감옥의 철문이 열리고, 종교 재판소장이 친히 손에 등불을 든 채 들어와. 그는 혼자야. 그의 등 뒤로 문이 곧 닫혀. 그는 문간에 서서 일이 분 동안 그분의 얼굴을 응시해. 마침내 그는 천천히 다가가 등불을 탁자 위에 내려놓고는 입을 열어.\n\n“‘당신인가? 당신이오?’ 그러나 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 곧 이렇게 덧붙여. ‘대답하지 마시오, 잠자코 계시오. 정말이지 당신이 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠소? 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있소. 게다가 당신은 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙일 권리가 없소. 그런데 어찌하여 우리를 방해하러 온 것이오? 당신은 우리를 방해하러 온 것이고, 당신 자신도 그것을 알고 있소. 한데 내일 무슨 일이 벌어질지 아시오? 나는 당신이 누구인지 모르고, 또 그것이 정말 당신인지 아니면 그저 그분의 허상인지 알고 싶지도 않으나, 내일 나는 당신을 가장 악한 이단자로 정죄하여 화형대에서 불태울 것이오. 그리고 오늘 당신의 발에 입을 맞춘 바로 그 백성들이, 내일 내가 가장 희미한 손짓 하나만 보내도 당신을 태우는 불에 숯을 쌓으려고 앞다투어 달려들 것이오. 그것을 아시오? 그래, 어쩌면 당신은 알고 있겠지.’ 그는 한순간도 죄수에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 깊이 꿰뚫는 듯한 사색에 잠겨 이렇게 덧붙여.”\n\n“잘 모르겠어, 이반 형. 그게 무슨 뜻이야?” 줄곧 말없이 듣고 있던 알료샤가 미소를 지으며 말했다. “그저 황당한 환상이라는 거야, 아니면 그 노인 쪽의 착각, 그러니까 있을 수 없는 사람 혼동이라는 거야?”\n\n“네가 현대의 사실주의에 그토록 물들어 환상적인 것이라곤 도무지 견디지 못하겠거든, 마지막 것으로 받아들이렴.” 이반이 웃으며 말했다. “그게 사람을 잘못 본 경우이길 바란다면, 그렇게 해도 좋아. 하긴 사실이지.” 그는 여전히 웃으며 말을 이었다. “그 노인은 아흔이었으니, 자기 고정관념에 사로잡혀 미쳐 있었다 해도 이상할 게 없어. 죄수의 모습에 충격을 받았을 수도 있고. 사실 그건 그저 그의 헛소리, 바로 전날 백 명의 이단자를 화형시키느라 지나치게 흥분한 아흔 살 노인의 망상에 지나지 않을 수도 있어. 그런데 그게 사람을 잘못 본 것이었든 황당한 환상이었든, 결국 우리에게 무슨 상관이 있겠어? 중요한 건 오직, 그 노인이 마침내 입을 연다는 것, 아흔 해 동안 말없이 품어 온 것을 드러내 놓고 말한다는 것뿐이야.”\n\n“그러면 죄수 역시 잠자코 있어? 그분은 노인을 바라보기만 하고 한마디도 하지 않는 거야?”\n\n“어쨌든 그건 불가피한 일이지.” 이반이 다시 웃었다. “노인이 그분께, 당신은 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙일 권리가 없다고 못 박았잖아. 적어도 내 생각으로는, 그것이 로마 가톨릭교의 가장 근본적인 특징이라고 말할 수 있어. ‘모든 것은 당신에 의해 교황에게 넘겨졌으니, 따라서 모든 것은 여전히 교황의 손안에 있고, 이제 당신은 굳이 다시 올 필요가 전혀 없다. 적어도 지금은 끼어들지 말라.’ 그들은 그렇게 말하고, 또 그렇게 쓰기까지 해—적어도 예수회 사람들은 그래. 나는 그들 신학자들의 저작에서 직접 읽었어. ‘당신은 우리에게, 당신이 떠나온 저 세계의 신비 가운데 하나를 드러낼 권리가 있는가?’ 하고 내 노인은 그분께 물은 뒤, 스스로 그 물음에 답해. ‘아니, 당신에게는 그럴 권리가 없다. 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙여서도 안 되고, 당신이 지상에 계실 때 그토록 떠받들었던 그 자유를 사람들에게서 빼앗아서도 안 된다. 당신이 새로이 드러내는 것은 무엇이든 사람들의 신앙의 자유를 침해할 것이니, 그것이 기적으로 드러날 것이기 때문이다. 한데 천오백 년 전 그 시절, 당신에게는 그들 신앙의 자유보다 더 소중한 것이 없었다. 그때 당신은 「내가 너희를 자유롭게 하리라」고 자주 말하지 않았는가? 그러나 이제 당신은 그 「자유로운」 자들을 보았다.’ 노인은 사색에 잠긴 미소를 띠고 별안간 이렇게 덧붙여. ‘그래, 우리는 그 대가를 톡톡히 치렀다.’ 그는 그분을 준엄하게 바라보며 말을 이어. ‘그러나 우리는 마침내 당신의 이름으로 그 일을 완수했다. 열다섯 세기 동안 우리는 당신의 자유와 씨름해 왔으나, 이제 그것은 끝났고 영영 마무리되었다. 그것이 영영 끝났다는 것을 믿지 못하겠는가? 당신은 온순하게 나를 바라볼 뿐, 나에게 노여워하는 것조차 마다하는구나. 그러나 내 말해 두겠는데, 바로 지금, 오늘날 사람들은 그 어느 때보다도 자기들이 완전한 자유를 누리고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있으나, 정작 그들은 제 자유를 우리에게 가져와 우리 발밑에 고분고분 내려놓았다. 그러나 그것은 우리가 이룬 일이다. 이것이 당신이 한 일이던가? 이것이 당신의 자유였던가?’”\n\n“또 모르겠어.” 알료샤가 끼어들었다. “노인이 빈정대는 거야, 농을 하는 거야?”\n\n“천만의 말씀! 노인은 마침내 자기와 자기 교회가 자유를 정복했다는 것을, 그것도 사람들을 행복하게 해 주려고 그렇게 했다는 것을 자기 공로로 내세우는 거야. ‘이제야’(물론 종교 재판을 두고 하는 말이지) ‘비로소 사람들의 행복을 생각할 수 있게 되었다. 인간은 반항자로 창조되었으니, 반항자들이 어찌 행복할 수 있겠는가? 당신은 경고를 받았다.’ 노인이 그분께 말해. ‘당신에게는 훈계와 경고가 부족하지 않았으나, 당신은 그 경고에 귀 기울이지 않았다. 당신은 사람들을 행복하게 할 수 있는 유일한 길을 물리쳤다. 그러나 다행히도, 떠나면서 당신은 그 일을 우리에게 맡겼다. 당신은 약속했고, 당신의 말로 정해 두었으며, 우리에게 매고 푸는 권리를 주었으니, 이제 와서 물론 그것을 도로 거두어 갈 수는 없다. 그런데 어찌하여 우리를 방해하러 온 것인가?’”\n\n“그런데 ‘훈계와 경고가 부족하지 않았다’는 건 무슨 뜻이야?” 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n“아, 그것이야말로 노인이 해야 할 말의 핵심이지.\n\n“‘저 슬기롭고 무시무시한 영, 자기 파멸과 비존재의 영이.’ 노인이 말을 이어. ‘그 위대한 영이 광야에서 당신과 이야기를 나누었고, 성경은 그가 당신을 「시험했다」고 전한다. 그런가? 그가 세 가지 물음으로 당신께 드러낸 것, 당신이 물리친 것, 그리고 성경에서 「시험」이라 불리는 그것보다 더 참된 말을 할 수 있겠는가? 만일 이 땅에 일찍이 진정 어마어마한 기적이 있었다면, 그것은 바로 그날, 그 세 가지 시험의 날에 일어난 것이다. 그 세 물음을 던졌다는 것 자체가 기적이었다. 단지 논의를 위해 이렇게 한번 상상해 보자. 그 무시무시한 영의 세 물음이 성경에서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버려, 우리가 그것을 복원하고 새로 지어내야만 하게 되었으며, 그러기 위해 이 땅의 모든 지혜로운 자들—통치자들, 대제사장들, 학자들, 철학자들, 시인들—을 한자리에 불러 모아, 단지 그 상황에 들어맞을 뿐 아니라 세 마디 말, 세 마디 인간의 문구로 세계와 인류의 미래 역사 전체를 표현해 낼 세 가지 물음을 지어내라는 과제를 그들에게 맡겼다고 치자. 그렇다면 당신은 정말로 믿는가, 이 땅의 모든 지혜를 한데 모은들, 그때 광야에서 슬기롭고 강대한 영이 실제로 당신께 던졌던 세 물음에 견줄 만큼 깊고 힘찬 무언가를 지어낼 수 있었으리라고? 오직 그 물음들만으로도, 그것이 던져졌다는 기적만으로도, 우리는 여기서 다루는 것이 덧없는 인간의 지성이 아니라 절대적이고 영원한 것임을 알 수 있다. 그 세 물음 속에는 인류의 그 후의 역사 전체가 말하자면 하나로 응축되어 예언되어 있고, 그 안에는 인간 본성의 풀리지 않은 역사적 모순들이 죄다 한데 엮여 있기 때문이다. 그 당시에는 미래가 알려지지 않았으니 그토록 분명할 수 없었으나, 이제 천오백 년이 지난 지금, 우리는 그 세 물음 속의 모든 것이 그토록 정확히 헤아려지고 예언되었으며 그토록 진실되게 이루어졌기에, 거기에 무엇 하나 더할 수도 뺄 수도 없음을 본다.\n\n“‘누가 옳았는지 당신 스스로 판단해 보라—당신인가, 아니면 그때 당신께 물음을 던진 자인가? 첫 번째 물음을 떠올려 보라. 다른 말로 하자면 그 뜻은 이러했다. 「너는 세상으로 나가려 하면서 빈손으로, 무슨 자유의 약속 따위를 들고 가는데, 그것은 사람들이 그 단순함과 타고난 무질서함으로 인해 도무지 이해조차 못 하는 것이요, 두려워하고 무서워하는 것이다—인간에게도 인간 사회에게도 자유보다 더 견디기 힘든 것은 일찍이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 메마르고 황량한 광야의 저 돌들이 보이느냐? 저것들을 빵으로 바꾸어라, 그러면 인류는 양 떼처럼 네 뒤를 따라 달려올 것이니, 감사하고 순종하면서, 다만 네가 손을 거두어 빵을 주지 않을까 늘 떨면서 그리할 것이다.」 그러나 당신은 인간에게서 자유를 빼앗으려 하지 않았기에 그 제안을 물리쳤다. 순종을 빵으로 산다면 그런 자유가 무슨 값어치가 있겠느냐고 생각하면서 말이다. 당신은 사람이 빵으로만 사는 것이 아니라고 대답했다. 그러나 아는가, 바로 그 지상의 빵을 빌미로 땅의 영이 당신을 거슬러 일어나 당신과 다투어 당신을 이길 것이며, 모든 자들이 그를 따르며 「누가 이 짐승에 견줄 수 있으랴? 그가 우리에게 하늘에서 불을 내려 주었도다!」 하고 외치게 되리라는 것을? 아는가, 세월이 흘러 인류가 제 현자들의 입을 빌려 죄악이란 없고 따라서 죄도 없으며 오직 굶주림만 있을 뿐이라고 선포하게 되리라는 것을? 「사람들을 먹인 다음에 그들에게 덕을 요구하라!」 그들이 당신을 거슬러 치켜들 깃발에, 그것으로 당신의 성전을 무너뜨릴 그 깃발에 그렇게 쓸 것이다. 당신의 성전이 섰던 자리에 새 건물이 일어설 것이니, 그 무시무시한 바벨탑이 다시 세워질 것이다. 그것 역시 옛것처럼 끝내 완성되지는 못하겠으나, 그래도 당신은 그 새 탑을 막아 천 년 동안 사람들의 고통을 줄일 수도 있었을 것이다. 그들은 천 년에 걸친 고뇌 끝에 자기네 탑을 들고 우리에게 되돌아올 것이기 때문이다. 그들은 다시 우리를 찾을 것이니, 땅속 지하 묘지에 숨은 우리를 찾을 것이다. 우리는 또다시 박해받고 고문당할 것이기 때문이다. 그들은 우리를 찾아내어 우리에게 부르짖으리라. 「우리를 먹여 주시오, 하늘에서 불을 주겠다고 약속한 자들이 그것을 주지 않았으니!」 그리하여 우리가 그들의 탑을 마저 짓게 되리니, 탑을 완성하는 자는 곧 그들을 먹이는 자이기 때문이다. 그리고 오직 우리만이 당신의 이름으로 그들을 먹이되, 당신의 이름이라 거짓을 말하며 먹일 것이다. 오, 결코, 결코 그들은 우리 없이는 제 힘으로 먹지 못한다! 그들이 자유로운 한, 어떤 과학도 그들에게 빵을 주지 못할 것이다. 결국 그들은 제 자유를 우리 발밑에 내려놓고 우리에게 이렇게 말할 것이다. 「우리를 당신들의 노예로 삼으시오, 그 대신 먹여만 주시오.」 그들도 마침내 깨닫게 되리라, 자유와 모두에게 넉넉한 빵이 한데 양립할 수 없음을, 그들끼리는 결코, 결코 그것을 나누어 가질 수 없으리라는 것을! 또한 그들은 자기들이 결코 자유로울 수 없음을 확신하게 되리니, 그들은 약하고 비루하며 쓸모없고 반항적이기 때문이다. 당신은 그들에게 하늘의 빵을 약속했으나, 거듭 말하거니와, 약하고 늘 죄짓고 비천한 인간 족속의 눈에 그것이 지상의 빵에 견줄 수 있겠는가? 그리고 하늘의 빵을 위해 수천 명이 당신을 따른다 한들, 하늘의 빵을 위해 지상의 빵을 포기할 힘이 없는 수백만, 수만 곱절의 수백만 피조물들은 어찌 되겠는가? 아니면 당신은 오직 그 위대하고 강한 자 수만 명만을 아끼고, 바다의 모래알처럼 수많은, 약하지만 당신을 사랑하는 그 수백만은 오직 위대하고 강한 자들을 위해서만 존재해야 한단 말인가? 아니, 우리는 약한 자들도 아낀다. 그들은 죄 많고 반항적이나, 결국은 그들 역시 순종하게 되리라. 그들은 우리를 경탄하며 신처럼 우러러볼 것이니, 그들이 그토록 무서워하던 그 자유를 우리가 기꺼이 떠맡아 그들을 다스리려 하기 때문이다—자유롭다는 것이 그들에게는 그토록 끔찍하게 여겨질 것이다. 그러나 우리는 그들에게, 우리가 당신의 종이며 당신의 이름으로 그들을 다스린다고 말하리라. 우리는 또다시 그들을 속일 것이니, 당신이 다시 우리에게 오도록 두지 않을 것이기 때문이다. 그 기만이 곧 우리의 고난이 되리니, 우리는 거짓말을 하지 않을 수 없을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n“‘이것이 광야에서의 첫 번째 물음의 의미이며, 이것이 바로 당신이 무엇보다도 떠받든 그 자유를 위해 물리친 것이다. 그러나 이 물음 속에 이 세상의 위대한 비밀이 숨어 있다. 「빵」을 택했더라면, 당신은 인류의 보편적이고 영원한 갈망을—곧 경배할 대상을 찾으려는 갈망을 채워 주었을 것이다. 인간은 자유로운 한, 경배할 대상을 찾는 일만큼 끊임없이, 그토록 고통스럽게 애쓰는 일이 달리 없다. 그러나 인간은 의심할 여지 없이 확립된 것을 경배하려 드니, 모든 사람이 즉시 한뜻으로 그것을 경배하도록 하기 위함이다. 이 가련한 피조물들은 단지 이 사람이나 저 사람이 경배할 수 있는 것을 찾는 데 그치지 않고, 모두가 믿고 경배할 만한 것을 찾는 데 골몰하기 때문이다. 무엇보다 중요한 것은 모두가 그 안에서 함께할 수 있어야 한다는 것이다. 이 경배의 공동체를 향한 갈망이야말로 태초부터 개개인의, 그리고 온 인류의 가장 큰 비참이다. 그 공통의 경배를 위해 그들은 칼로 서로를 죽여 왔다. 그들은 신들을 세워 놓고 서로 도전하며 외쳤다. 「너희 신들을 치워 버리고 와서 우리 신을 경배하라, 그러지 않으면 너희와 너희 신들을 죽이리라!」 그리고 세상 끝날까지 그러하리니, 땅에서 신들이 사라진다 해도 그들은 마찬가지로 우상 앞에 엎드릴 것이다. 당신은 인간 본성의 이 근본적인 비밀을 알고 있었다, 모를 수가 없었을 것이다. 그런데도 당신은 모든 사람이 오직 당신 한 분께 엎드리게 할 그 틀림없는 깃발을—지상의 빵이라는 깃발을 거절했다. 그리고 자유와 하늘의 빵을 위해 그것을 물리쳤다. 보라, 당신이 그다음에 한 일을. 그리고 그 또한 죄다 자유의 이름으로! 내 당신께 이르노니, 인간을 괴롭히는 것 가운데 그 불운한 피조물이 타고난 자유라는 선물을 어서 넘겨줄 누군가를 찾는 일보다 더 큰 근심은 없다. 그러나 그들의 양심을 달래 줄 수 있는 자만이 그들의 자유를 떠맡을 수 있다. 빵 속에는 당신께 무적의 깃발이 주어졌으니, 빵을 주라, 그러면 인간은 당신을 경배하리니, 빵보다 더 확실한 것은 없기 때문이다. 그러나 만일 다른 누군가가 그의 양심을 손에 넣는다면—오! 그때 그는 당신의 빵을 내던지고, 그의 양심을 사로잡은 자를 따라가리라. 그 점에서 당신은 옳았다. 인간 존재의 비밀은 단지 사는 데 있는 것이 아니라 무언가 살아갈 까닭을 갖는 데 있기 때문이다. 삶의 목적에 대한 확고한 관념이 없다면 인간은 계속 살기를 거부할 것이며, 빵이 넘쳐난다 해도 이 땅에 머무느니 차라리 제 목숨을 끊으려 할 것이다. 그것은 사실이다. 그러나 무슨 일이 일어났는가? 사람들에게서 자유를 빼앗기는커녕, 당신은 그것을 그 어느 때보다 더 크게 만들어 놓았다! 인간이 선악을 아는 가운데 선택하는 자유보다 평안을, 심지어 죽음을 더 좋아한다는 것을 당신은 잊었는가? 인간에게 양심의 자유보다 더 유혹적인 것은 없으나, 동시에 그보다 더 큰 고통의 원천도 없다. 그런데 보라, 인간의 양심을 영영 평안케 할 굳건한 토대를 주기는커녕, 당신은 온갖 예외적이고 막연하며 수수께끼 같은 것을 택했고, 인간의 힘에 도무지 부치는 것을 택했으니, 마치 그들을 조금도 사랑하지 않는 것처럼 행한 것이다—그들을 위해 목숨을 바치러 온 당신이! 사람들의 자유를 차지하기는커녕, 당신은 그것을 늘려 놓았고, 인류의 정신 왕국을 그 고통으로 영영 짓눌러 놓았다. 당신은 인간의 자유로운 사랑을 갈망했으니, 그가 당신께 매혹되고 사로잡혀 자유로이 당신을 따르기를 바랐다. 엄격한 옛 율법 대신, 인간은 이제부터 오직 당신의 형상만을 길잡이로 삼고서 자유로운 마음으로 무엇이 선이고 무엇이 악인지 스스로 결정해야 한다. 그러나 당신은 몰랐는가, 자유로운 선택이라는 무시무시한 짐에 짓눌리면 인간은 결국 당신의 형상마저, 당신의 진리마저 물리치게 되리라는 것을? 그들은 마침내 진리가 당신 안에 있지 않다고 소리 높여 부르짖으리니, 당신이 그토록 많은 근심과 풀리지 않는 문제들을 그들에게 지운 것보다 더 큰 혼란과 고통 속에 그들을 버려둘 수는 없었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n“‘그리하여 참으로, 당신은 친히 당신 왕국이 무너질 토대를 놓은 것이며, 그 누구도 당신보다 더 책임이 크지 않다. 그런데 당신께 주어진 것은 무엇이었던가? 세 가지 힘이 있다, 오직 세 가지 힘만이 이 무력한 반항자들의 양심을 그들의 행복을 위해 영영 정복하여 사로잡아 둘 수 있으니—그 힘이란 기적과 신비와 권위다. 당신은 그 셋을 모두 물리쳤고, 그렇게 함으로써 본을 보였다. 슬기롭고 무시무시한 영이 당신을 성전 꼭대기에 세우고 당신께 말했다. 「네가 하느님의 아들인지 알고 싶거든 뛰어내려라, 기록되었으되, 천사들이 그를 떠받들어 그가 떨어져 다치지 않게 하리라 하였으니, 그러면 너는 네가 하느님의 아들인지 알게 될 것이요, 네 아버지에 대한 네 믿음이 얼마나 큰지 증명하게 되리라.」 그러나 당신은 거절하고 뛰어내리려 하지 않았다. 오, 물론 당신은 신답게, 자랑스럽고 훌륭하게 처신했다. 그러나 약하고 무질서한 인간 족속이, 그들이 신인가? 오, 당신은 알았다, 한 걸음 내딛는 것만으로도, 뛰어내리려는 몸짓 하나만으로도 당신은 하느님을 시험하는 것이 되어 그분에 대한 믿음을 모조리 잃을 것이며, 당신이 구하러 온 바로 그 땅에 부딪쳐 산산이 부서지리라는 것을. 그리고 당신을 시험한 슬기로운 영은 기뻐했을 것이다. 그러나 다시 묻노니, 당신 같은 자가 그리 많겠는가? 그리고 당신은 한순간이라도 믿을 수 있었는가, 인간들 역시 그런 시험을 견뎌 낼 수 있으리라고? 인간의 본성이 그러한가, 기적을 물리치고, 그들 삶의 위대한 순간에, 가장 깊고 가장 괴로운 영적 고난의 순간에 오직 마음의 자유로운 판결에만 매달릴 수 있을 만큼? 오, 당신은 알았다, 당신의 행적이 책에 기록되어 머나먼 시대와 땅끝까지 전해지리라는 것을. 그리고 당신은 바랐다, 인간이 당신을 따라 하느님께 매달리고 기적을 구하지 않기를. 그러나 당신은 몰랐다, 인간이 기적을 물리치면 하느님마저 물리친다는 것을. 인간은 하느님보다도 기적을 더 찾기 때문이다. 그리고 인간은 기적 없이는 견디지 못하므로, 제 손으로 새 기적을 만들어 내어 요술과 마법의 행위를 경배하리니, 그가 백 번을 거듭 반항자요 이단자요 불신자라 하더라도 그러할 것이다. 그들이 당신을 조롱하고 욕하며 「십자가에서 내려오라, 그러면 네가 그분인 줄 믿겠다」고 외쳤을 때, 당신은 십자가에서 내려오지 않았다. 당신은 내려오지 않았으니, 또다시 기적으로 인간을 노예 삼으려 하지 않았고, 기적에 근거하지 않은, 자유로이 바쳐진 믿음을 갈망했기 때문이다. 당신은 자유로운 사랑을 갈망했지, 자신을 영영 압도해 버린 권능 앞에서 노예가 느끼는 비천한 황홀을 갈망하지 않았다. 그러나 그 점에서 당신은 인간을 너무 높이 평가했으니, 물론 그들은 본성이 반항적이긴 해도 결국 노예이기 때문이다. 둘러보고 판단해 보라. 열다섯 세기가 흘렀으니, 그들을 바라보라. 당신은 그들 가운데 누구를 당신께로 끌어올렸는가? 내 맹세컨대, 인간은 당신이 믿었던 것보다 본성이 더 약하고 더 비천하다! 그가, 그가 당신이 한 일을 할 수 있겠는가? 그를 그토록 존중함으로써 당신은 말하자면 그를 더는 가엾이 여기지 않게 된 것이니, 당신이 그에게 너무도 많은 것을 요구했기 때문이다—당신 자신보다 그를 더 사랑한 당신이! 그를 덜 존중했더라면 그에게 덜 요구했을 것이다. 그편이 오히려 사랑에 더 가까웠을 것이니, 그의 짐이 더 가벼웠을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 약하고 비루하다. 지금 그가 곳곳에서 우리의 권력에 반항하며 제 반항을 자랑한들 어떠랴? 그것은 어린아이와 학생의 자만에 지나지 않는다. 그들은 학교에서 소동을 부리며 선생을 내쫓는 어린아이들이다. 그러나 그들의 유치한 환희는 끝날 것이고, 그들은 그 대가를 톡톡히 치를 것이다. 그들은 성전을 무너뜨리고 피로 땅을 적실 것이다. 그러나 그 어리석은 아이들도 마침내 알게 되리니, 자기들이 반항자이긴 해도 무력한 반항자, 제 반항조차 끌고 나가지 못하는 자들임을 깨달을 것이다. 어리석은 눈물에 젖어, 그들은 마침내 자기들을 반항자로 창조한 그분이 필시 자기들을 조롱하려 하셨던 것이라고 깨달으리라. 그들은 절망 속에서 이렇게 말할 것이며, 그 말은 신성 모독이 되어 그들을 한층 더 불행하게 만들리니, 인간의 본성은 신성 모독을 견디지 못하여 결국 언제나 제 자신에게 그것을 되갚기 때문이다. 그리하여 불안과 혼란과 불행이—이것이 당신이 그들의 자유를 위해 그토록 많은 것을 견뎌 낸 끝에 인간에게 남겨진 오늘의 몫이다! 그 위대한 예언자는 환상과 형상으로 이르기를, 첫 번째 부활에 참여한 모든 자를 보았으며 각 지파에서 만 이천씩이 있었다고 했다. 그러나 그들이 그토록 많았다면 그들은 인간이 아니라 신이었을 것이다. 그들은 당신의 십자가를 졌고, 메마르고 굶주린 광야에서 메뚜기와 풀뿌리로 연명하며 수십 년을 견뎌 냈다—그러니 당신은 참으로 그 자유의 자녀들, 자유로운 사랑의 자녀들, 당신 이름을 위해 자유로이 빛나는 희생을 바친 자녀들을 자랑스레 가리킬 수도 있으리라. 그러나 잊지 말라, 그들은 고작 수천에 지나지 않았음을. 그렇다면 나머지는 어찌 되는가? 그리고 강한 자들이 견뎌 낸 것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다 하여, 다른 약한 자들에게 무슨 잘못이 있는가? 그토록 무시무시한 선물을 받아들이지 못한다 하여 약한 영혼에게 무슨 잘못이 있는가? 당신은 그저 선택된 자들에게, 선택된 자들을 위해서만 온 것인가? 그렇다면 그것은 신비이니 우리로서는 이해할 수 없다. 그리고 그것이 신비라면, 우리에게도 신비를 설교할 권리가 있으며, 그들에게 중요한 것은 그들 마음의 자유로운 판단도, 사랑도 아니라 그들이 제 양심을 거슬러서라도 맹목적으로 따라야 할 신비라고 가르칠 권리가 있다. 그리하여 우리는 그렇게 했다. 우리는 당신의 일을 바로잡아, 그것을 기적과 신비와 권위 위에 세웠다. 그러자 사람들은 자기들이 또다시 양 떼처럼 인도받는 것을, 그토록 큰 고통을 안겨 주었던 그 무시무시한 선물이 마침내 제 가슴에서 들리는 것을 기뻐했다. 우리가 그들에게 이렇게 가르친 것이 옳았는가? 말해 보라! 우리는 그토록 온순하게 그들의 연약함을 인정하고, 사랑으로 그들의 짐을 덜어 주며, 그들의 연약한 본성이 우리의 승인 아래 죄짓는 것마저 허락했으니, 인류를 사랑하지 않은 것이던가? 그런데 어찌하여 이제 와서 우리를 방해하러 온 것인가? 그리고 어찌하여 그 온화한 눈으로 말없이 나를 꿰뚫어 보는가? 노여워하라. 나는 당신의 사랑을 원치 않으니, 나 또한 당신을 사랑하지 않기 때문이다. 게다가 내가 당신께 무엇을 숨긴들 무슨 소용이겠는가? 내가 누구에게 말하고 있는지 내가 모를 줄 아는가? 내가 할 수 있는 모든 말은 이미 당신께 알려져 있다. 그러니 내가 우리의 비밀을 당신께 숨길 까닭이 있겠는가? 어쩌면 내 입으로 그것을 듣는 것이 당신의 뜻인지도 모르겠다. 그렇다면 들어 보라. 우리는 당신과 함께 일하는 것이 아니라 그와 함께 일하고 있다—그것이 우리의 비밀이다. 우리가 당신 편이 아니라 그의 편에 선 지도 오래되었으니, 어느덧 여덟 세기다. 꼭 여덟 세기 전, 당신이 멸시하며 물리친 것, 그가 당신께 이 땅의 모든 나라를 보여 주며 내놓았던 그 마지막 선물을 우리는 그에게서 받았다. 우리는 그에게서 로마와 카이사르의 칼을 받아, 우리야말로 이 땅의 유일한 통치자라고 선포했다. 비록 지금껏 우리 일을 완성하지는 못했으나 말이다. 그러나 그것이 누구의 잘못인가? 오, 일은 이제 겨우 시작되었으되, 그래도 시작되었다. 완성되기까지는 아직 멀었고 이 땅은 아직 많은 고통을 겪어야 하겠으나, 우리는 끝내 승리하여 카이사르가 될 것이며, 그때 비로소 인류의 보편적 행복을 설계하리라. 그러나 당신은 그때라도 카이사르의 칼을 받을 수 있었다. 어찌하여 그 마지막 선물을 물리쳤는가? 당신이 그 강대한 영의 마지막 조언을 받아들였더라면, 인간이 이 땅에서 찾는 모든 것을 이루어 주었을 것이다—곧 경배할 대상, 양심을 맡아 줄 자, 그리고 모두를 하나의 한뜻 되고 조화로운 개미집으로 묶어 줄 방편을. 보편적 통합을 향한 갈망이야말로 인간의 세 번째이자 마지막 고뇌이기 때문이다. 인류는 전체로서 언제나 보편적 국가를 세우려 애써 왔다. 위대한 역사를 지닌 위대한 민족이 많았으나, 더 높이 발전할수록 그들은 더 불행했으니, 다른 어떤 민족보다도 세계적 통합을 향한 갈망을 더 절절히 느꼈기 때문이다. 위대한 정복자들, 티무르와 칭기즈 칸은 이 땅의 백성을 굴복시키려 애쓰며 허리케인처럼 지표면을 휩쓸었는데, 그들 역시 다름 아닌 이 보편적 통합을 향한 갈망의 무의식적 표현이었다. 당신이 세계와 카이사르의 자줏빛 옷을 받았더라면, 보편적 국가를 세우고 보편적 평화를 주었을 것이다. 인간의 양심과 빵을 손에 쥔 자가 아니라면 누가 인간을 다스릴 수 있겠는가? 우리는 카이사르의 칼을 받았으며, 그것을 받음으로써 물론 당신을 물리치고 그를 따랐다. 오, 자유로운 사상의 혼란과 그들의 과학과 식인의 시대가 아직 닥쳐올 것이다. 그들은 우리 없이 제 바벨탑을 짓기 시작했으니, 물론 결국엔 식인으로 끝맺을 것이기 때문이다. 그러나 그때 그 짐승이 우리에게 기어와 우리 발을 핥으며 피눈물을 뿌릴 것이다. 그리고 우리는 그 짐승 위에 올라앉아 잔을 치켜들 것이니, 그 위에는 「신비」라고 쓰여 있으리라. 그러나 그때, 오직 그때에야 비로소 인간에게 평화와 행복의 시대가 도래하리라. 당신은 당신의 선택된 자들을 자랑하나, 당신에게는 오직 선택된 자들뿐인 반면, 우리는 모든 자에게 안식을 준다. 게다가 그 선택된 자들, 선택된 자가 될 수도 있었던 그 강한 자들 가운데서도 얼마나 많은 이가 당신을 기다리다 지쳐, 제 정신의 힘과 제 가슴의 온기를 다른 진영으로 옮겨 가고 또 옮겨 갈 것이며, 끝내 당신을 거슬러 제 「자유로운」 깃발을 치켜들 것인가. 당신이 친히 그 깃발을 들어 올린 것이다. 그러나 우리와 함께라면 모두가 행복할 것이며, 당신의 자유 아래서처럼 더는 반항하지도 서로를 죽이지도 않으리라. 오, 우리는 그들을 설득하리라, 자기 자유를 우리에게 내려놓고 우리에게 복종할 때에야 비로소 자유로워지리라고. 그러면 우리가 옳은 것인가, 거짓을 말하는 것인가? 그들은 우리가 옳다고 확신하리니, 당신의 자유가 그들을 몰아넣은 노예 상태와 혼란의 끔찍함을 기억할 것이기 때문이다. 자유와 자유로운 사상과 과학은 그들을 그토록 곤경으로 몰아넣고, 그토록 기이한 것들과 풀리지 않는 신비들 앞에 그들을 세워 놓을 것이니, 그들 가운데 사납고 반항적인 자들은 제 목숨을 끊을 것이요, 반항적이되 약한 자들은 서로를 죽일 것이며, 그 나머지 약하고 불행한 자들은 우리 발밑으로 알랑거리며 기어와 우리에게 칭얼대리라. 「그래요, 당신들이 옳았소, 오직 당신들만이 그분의 신비를 지니고 있소. 우리가 당신들께 돌아왔으니, 우리 자신에게서 우리를 구해 주시오!」\n\n“‘우리에게서 빵을 받으면서, 그들은 우리가 아무 기적도 없이 제 손으로 빚은 빵을 그들에게서 거두어 다시 그들에게 나누어 줄 뿐임을 똑똑히 보게 될 것이다. 그들은 우리가 돌을 빵으로 바꾸는 것이 아님을 보게 되겠으나, 정녕 그들은 그 빵 자체보다도 우리 손에서 그것을 받는 것에 더 감사하리라! 옛날에는 우리의 도움 없이 제 손으로 빚은 빵조차 그들 손안에서 돌로 변했던 것을, 그러나 우리에게 되돌아온 뒤로는 바로 그 돌이 그들 손안에서 빵으로 변했던 것을 그들은 너무도 잘 기억할 것이기 때문이다. 완전한 복종의 값어치를 그들은 너무도, 너무도 잘 알게 되리라! 그리고 인간이 그것을 알기 전까지 그들은 불행하리라. 그들이 그것을 알지 못하는 데에 누구의 책임이 가장 큰가?—말해 보라! 누가 양 떼를 흩어 놓고 알 수 없는 길로 헤매게 했는가? 그러나 양 떼는 다시 모여들어 또 한 번 복종할 것이며, 그때는 영영 그러하리라. 그때 우리는 그들에게, 그들이 본성상 그러한 약한 피조물에게 어울리는 조용하고 겸허한 행복을 주리라. 오, 우리는 마침내 그들을 설득하여 자만하지 않게 하리니, 당신이 그들을 치켜올려 자만하도록 가르쳤기 때문이다. 우리는 그들에게 보여 주리라, 그들이 약하다는 것, 그저 가련한 어린아이일 뿐이라는 것, 그러나 어린아이의 행복이야말로 그 무엇보다 달콤하다는 것을. 그들은 겁먹은 채 우리를 우러러보며, 병아리가 어미 닭에게 하듯 두려움에 떨며 우리에게 바싹 붙어들 것이다. 그들은 우리를 경탄하며 우리 앞에서 경외에 사로잡힐 것이고, 우리가 그토록 강하고 영리하여 수천만에 이르는 그 사나운 양 떼를 굴복시킬 수 있었다는 데에 자랑스러워하리라. 그들은 우리의 노여움 앞에 무력하게 떨 것이며, 그들의 마음은 겁에 질리고, 여자와 아이들처럼 쉬이 눈물을 흘릴 것이나, 우리의 손짓 하나에 그만큼이나 쉬이 웃음과 환희로, 행복한 즐거움과 어린아이의 노래로 옮아갈 것이다. 그렇다, 우리는 그들에게 일을 시키겠으나, 한가한 시간에는 그들의 삶을 어린아이의 놀이처럼, 어린아이의 노래와 천진한 춤이 어우러진 것으로 만들어 주리라. 오, 우리는 그들에게 죄짓는 것마저 허락하리니, 그들은 약하고 무력하며, 우리가 죄를 허락해 주기에 어린아이처럼 우리를 사랑할 것이다. 우리는 그들에게 이르리라, 우리의 허락 아래 저질러진 죄라면 어떤 죄든 사함을 받을 것이며, 우리가 그들을 사랑하기에 죄짓는 것을 허락하는 것이요, 그 죄에 대한 벌은 우리가 떠맡으리라고. 그리하여 우리는 그것을 떠맡을 것이며, 그들은 하느님 앞에서 제 죄를 대신 짊어진 구원자로 우리를 흠모하리라. 그리고 그들은 우리에게 아무 비밀도 두지 않으리라. 그들이 아내와 정부와 동침하는 것을, 자식을 두거나 두지 않는 것을—그들이 순종했는가 거역했는가에 따라—우리가 허락하거나 금하리니, 그들은 기꺼이 즐거이 우리에게 복종할 것이다. 그들 양심의 가장 괴로운 비밀들을, 모조리, 모조리 그들은 우리에게 가져올 것이며, 우리는 그 모든 것에 대한 답을 갖고 있으리라. 그러면 그들은 기꺼이 우리의 답을 믿으리니, 그것이 지금 그들이 스스로 자유로운 결정을 내리느라 겪는 큰 근심과 무시무시한 고뇌에서 그들을 구해 줄 것이기 때문이다. 그리하여 모두가 행복하리라, 그들을 다스리는 십만 명을 빼고는 그 수백만 피조물 모두가. 오직 우리, 신비를 지키는 우리만이 불행할 것이기 때문이다. 행복한 갓난아이들이 수천만이요, 선악을 아는 저주를 제 몸에 떠맡은 고난받는 자가 십만이리라. 그들은 평화로이 죽을 것이며, 평화로이 당신의 이름으로 숨을 거두겠으나, 무덤 너머에서 죽음 외에는 아무것도 찾지 못하리라. 그러나 우리는 그 비밀을 지킬 것이며, 그들의 행복을 위해 천국과 영생의 상을 미끼로 그들을 꾈 것이다. 비록 저세상에 무언가 있다 한들, 그것은 분명 그들 같은 자들을 위한 것은 아니겠으나 말이다. 당신이 승리 가운데 다시 오시리라, 당신의 선택받은 자들, 그 자랑스럽고 강한 자들과 함께 오시리라 예언되어 있으나, 우리는 말하리라, 그들은 오직 자기 자신만을 구했을 뿐, 우리는 모든 자를 구했노라고. 짐승 위에 앉아 손에 그 「신비」를 쥔 음녀가 수치를 당하리라고, 약한 자들이 다시 일어나 그녀의 왕다운 자줏빛 옷을 찢고 그 가증스러운 몸을 발가벗기리라고 우리는 듣는다. 그러나 그때 나는 일어서서, 죄라고는 모르고 살아온 그 수천만 행복한 어린아이들을 당신께 가리켜 보이리라. 그리고 그들의 행복을 위해 그들의 죄를 떠맡은 우리는 당신 앞에 일어서서 말하리라. 「할 수 있고 또 감히 그러하거든, 우리를 심판하라.」 알아 두라, 나는 당신이 두렵지 않다. 알아 두라, 나 또한 광야에 있었고, 나 또한 풀뿌리와 메뚜기로 연명했으며, 나 또한 당신이 사람들에게 복으로 내린 그 자유를 소중히 여겼고, 나 또한 「그 수를 채우려」 갈망하며 당신의 선택받은 자들, 그 강하고 권능 있는 자들 가운데 서려고 애썼다. 그러나 나는 깨어났고, 광기를 섬기려 하지 않았다. 나는 발길을 돌려, 당신의 일을 바로잡은 자들의 대열에 합류했다. 나는 자랑스러운 자들을 떠나 겸허한 자들에게로 돌아왔으니, 겸허한 자들의 행복을 위해서였다. 내가 당신께 하는 말은 그대로 이루어질 것이며, 우리의 지배는 세워질 것이다. 거듭 말하노니, 내일 당신은 보게 되리라, 내 손짓 하나에 당신을 태울 장작더미 둘레로 뜨거운 잿불을 앞다투어 쌓아 올릴 그 순종하는 양 떼를. 우리를 방해하러 왔다는 이유로 내가 당신을 불태울 그 장작더미 말이다. 일찍이 우리의 불에 처해 마땅한 자가 있었다면, 그것은 바로 당신이기 때문이다. 내일 나는 당신을 불태우리라. 내 할 말은 끝났다.’”\n* 디시(Dixi): 라틴어로 ‘내가 말했다’, 곧 ‘내 할 말은 다 했다’는 뜻의 변론 종결 상투어.\n\n이반은 말을 멈추었다. 그는 이야기하는 동안 격해져서 흥분하여 말했는데, 다 끝내고 나자 별안간 미소를 지었다.\n\n알료샤는 말없이 듣고 있었다. 끝 무렵에 그는 크게 마음이 동요하여 몇 번이나 끼어들려는 듯하다가도 가까스로 자제했다. 이제 그의 말이 봇물 터지듯 쏟아져 나왔다.\n\n“하지만…… 그건 터무니없어!” 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. “형의 시는 형이 의도한 것과 달리 예수를 비난하는 게 아니라 오히려 찬미하고 있어. 게다가 자유에 관한 형 말을 누가 믿겠어? 그걸 그렇게 이해해야 한단 말이야? 정교회에서는 그런 식으로 생각하지 않아…… 그건 로마야, 그것도 로마 전체도 아니고, 그건 거짓이야—그건 가톨릭교도 중에서도 가장 악질인 자들, 종교 재판관들, 예수회 사람들이라고!…… 그리고 형이 그린 그런 환상적인 인물은 있을 수가 없어. 그들이 떠맡는다는 인류의 죄라는 게 대체 뭐야? 인류의 행복을 위해 무슨 저주를 제 몸에 떠맡았다는, 그 신비의 수호자들은 또 누구야? 그런 자들을 언제 본 적이나 있어? 우리도 예수회를 알아, 사람들이 그들을 욕하긴 하지만, 그들이 정말 형이 묘사한 그런 자들이겠어? 그들은 전혀 그렇지 않아, 결코 그렇지 않다고…… 그들은 그저 장차 이 세계를 지상에서 다스리려는 로마의 군대일 뿐이고, 로마 교황을 황제로 떠받드는…… 그게 그들의 이상이지만, 거기엔 무슨 신비도 숭고한 우수도 없어…… 그저 권력욕, 더러운 지상의 이득에 대한 욕심, 지배욕일 뿐이야—저들을 주인으로 둔 일종의 보편적 농노제 같은 것 말이야. 그게 저들이 추구하는 전부야. 어쩌면 하느님조차 믿지 않을 거야. 형의 그 고뇌하는 종교 재판관은 한낱 환상일 뿐이야.”\n\n“가만, 가만.” 이반이 웃었다. “어쩜 그리 흥분하는지! 환상이라고? 그래, 그렇다고 치자! 물론 환상이지. 하지만 한 가지만 말하게 해 다오. 너는 정말로 지난 몇 세기 동안의 로마 가톨릭 운동이 실제로 그저 권력욕, 더러운 지상의 이득에 대한 욕심에 지나지 않는다고 생각해? 그게 파이시 신부님의 가르침이야?”\n\n“아니, 아니, 오히려 그 반대야. 파이시 신부님도 언젠가 형이랑 좀 비슷한 말씀을 하시긴 했지만…… 물론 그건 같은 게 아니야, 조금도 같지 않아.” 알료샤가 황급히 정정했다.\n\n“네가 ‘조금도 같지 않다’고 하긴 해도, 참으로 값진 인정이로구나. 나는 너에게 묻겠어, 어째서 너의 예수회와 종교 재판관들이 그저 비열한 물질적 이득을 위해 뭉쳤다는 거지? 어째서 그들 가운데 큰 슬픔에 짓눌리고 인류를 사랑하는 순교자 한 사람쯤 있을 수 없다는 거야? 보렴, 더러운 물질적 이득밖에 바라는 것이 없는 그 모든 자들 가운데 단 한 사람이라도 그런 인물이 있었다고 가정해 보자—내 늙은 종교 재판관처럼, 자기 자신을 자유롭고 완전하게 만들려고 광야에서 풀뿌리를 먹으며 제 육신을 굴복시키려 미친 듯이 애쓴, 그런 사람이 단 한 명이라도 있었다고 말이야. 그런데도 그는 평생 인류를 사랑했어. 그러다 별안간 그의 눈이 뜨여, 완전함과 자유에 이르는 것이 그리 대단한 도덕적 축복이 아님을 깨달은 거야. 만일 그와 동시에, 하느님의 피조물 수백만이 조롱거리로 창조되어 결코 제 자유를 쓸 줄 모르게 되었다는 것, 그 가련한 반항자들이 결코 탑을 완성할 거인으로 자라날 수 없다는 것, 그 위대한 이상주의자가 조화의 꿈을 꾼 것은 그런 거위들을 위해서가 아니었다는 것을 확신하게 된다면 말이야. 그 모든 것을 본 그는 발길을 돌려—영리한 자들에게 합류한 거지. 분명 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있지 않았겠어?”\n\n“누구에게 합류했다는 거야, 무슨 영리한 자들 말이야?” 완전히 휩쓸려 든 알료샤가 외쳤다. “그들에게는 그런 대단한 영리함도 없고, 무슨 신비도 비밀도 없어…… 어쩌면 무신론밖에 없을 거야, 그게 그들 비밀의 전부지. 형의 종교 재판관은 하느님을 믿지 않아, 그게 그의 비밀이야!”\n\n“설령 그렇다 한들 어떠랴! 마침내 네가 알아맞혔구나. 그건 완벽한 사실이야, 그게 비밀의 전부라는 건 정말이지. 하지만 그건 고통이 아니겠어, 적어도 그런 사람에게는 말이야, 평생을 광야에서 허비하고도 인류에 대한 그 고질적인 사랑을 끝내 떨쳐 버리지 못한 사람에게는? 노년에 이르러 그는 분명한 확신에 다다랐어, 오직 그 위대하고 무시무시한 영의 조언만이 그 연약하고 무질서한, ‘농담 삼아 창조된 미완의 경험적 피조물들’에게 그나마 견딜 만한 삶을 세워 줄 수 있다는 확신에 말이야. 그리하여 이를 확신한 그는 깨닫지, 자신은 그 슬기로운 영, 죽음과 파멸의 무시무시한 영의 조언을 따라야 하며, 따라서 거짓과 기만을 받아들여 사람들을 의식적으로 죽음과 파멸로 이끌되, 그러면서도 그들이 어디로 끌려가는지 알아채지 못하도록 내내 그들을 속여, 그 가련한 눈먼 피조물들이 적어도 가는 길에서나마 스스로 행복하다고 여기게 해야 한다는 것을. 그리고 명심해, 그 기만은 바로 그 노인이 평생토록 그토록 뜨겁게 믿어 온 이상의 주인이신 그분의 이름으로 행해지는 거야. 이것이 비극이 아니란 말이야? 그리고 만일 ‘오직 더러운 이득을 위해 권력욕에 가득 찬’ 그 온 군대의 수장에 그런 사람이 단 하나만 서 있어도—그런 사람 하나만으로도 비극을 빚어내기에 충분하지 않겠어? 그뿐 아니라, 그런 사람 하나가 수장에 서 있는 것만으로도 그 모든 군대와 예수회를 거느린 로마 교회의 실질적인 지도 이념, 그 최고의 이념을 만들어 내기에 충분해. 솔직히 말하지만, 나는 그 운동의 수장에 섰던 자들 가운데 언제나 그런 사람이 있었다고 굳게 믿어. 누가 알겠어, 로마 교황들 가운데에도 그런 자가 더러 있었을지. 누가 알겠어, 제 나름의 방식으로 그토록 끈질기게 인류를 사랑하는 그 저주받은 노인의 정신이, 어쩌면 지금도 그런 노인들의 무리 전체 속에 깃들어 있을지를. 우연이 아니라 합의로 존재하는, 신비를 지키기 위해 오래전에 결성된 비밀 동맹으로서, 약하고 불행한 자들을 행복하게 해 주려고 그들에게서 그 신비를 지키는 노인들의 무리 속에 말이야. 틀림없이 그러할 것이고, 또 정녕 그래야만 하지. 내 생각엔 프리메이슨들 사이에도 밑바닥에는 그와 똑같은 신비 비슷한 것이 있는 것 같아. 그래서 가톨릭교도들이 프리메이슨을 그토록 미워하는 거야, 이념의 통일을 깨뜨리는 경쟁자로 여겨서지. 한 무리, 한 목자가 있어야 한다는 것이 그토록 긴요한 마당에 말이야…… 하지만 내가 내 생각을 변호하는 꼴을 보아하니, 마치 자기 작품에 대한 비평을 못 견뎌 하는 작가 같구나. 이쯤 해 두자.”\n\n“형은 어쩌면 형 자신이 프리메이슨인지도 몰라!” 알료샤의 입에서 별안간 이 말이 튀어나왔다. “형은 하느님을 믿지 않아.” 그가 이번에는 무척 서글픈 어조로 덧붙였다. 게다가 그는 형이 자기를 빈정대는 눈으로 바라보는 것만 같았다. “형의 시는 어떻게 끝나?” 그가 별안간 고개를 떨구며 물었다. “아니면 그게 끝이었어?”\n\n“이렇게 끝맺을 작정이었어. 종교 재판관이 말을 마치고는 한동안 죄수가 자기에게 대답해 주기를 기다려. 그분의 침묵이 그를 짓눌러. 그는 죄수가 내내 온화하게 자기 얼굴을 바라보며 골똘히 귀 기울였을 뿐, 분명 대꾸하고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것을 알아차려. 노인은 그분이 무슨 말이든, 아무리 쓰디쓰고 무시무시한 말이라도 해 주기를 간절히 바라. 그런데 별안간 그분이 말없이 노인에게 다가가, 그 핏기 없는 늙은 입술에 부드럽게 입을 맞춰. 그것이 그분의 대답 전부였어. 노인은 몸을 떨어. 그의 입술이 움찔거려. 그는 문으로 가 문을 열고는 그분에게 말해. ‘가시오, 그리고 다시는 오지 마시오…… 다시는 절대 오지 마시오, 절대로, 절대로!’ 그러고는 그분을 도시의 어두운 골목길로 내보내. 죄수는 떠나가.”\n\n“그럼 노인은?”\n\n“그 입맞춤이 그의 가슴속에서 타오르지만, 노인은 자기 이념을 고수해.”\n\n“그리고 형도 그와 함께, 형도 그러는 거야?” 알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다.\n\n이반이 웃었다.\n\n“이런, 다 부질없는 소리야, 알료샤. 그건 그저 변변히 시 두 줄도 써 본 적 없는 한 어리석은 대학생이 지은 어리석은 시일 뿐이야. 그걸 왜 그리 진지하게 받아들이지? 설마 내가 곧장 예수회로 달려가, 그분의 일을 바로잡는 자들의 무리에 끼리라고 생각하는 건 아니겠지? 맙소사, 그건 내 알 바 아니야. 내가 말했잖아, 내가 바라는 거라곤 서른까지 살아남는 것뿐이고, 그다음엔…… 잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버리는 거라고!”\n\n“하지만 그 끈적끈적한 어린잎들과, 그 소중한 무덤들과, 푸른 하늘과, 형이 사랑하는 여인은 어쩌고! 형은 어떻게 살아갈 거야, 어떻게 그것들을 사랑할 거야?” 알료샤가 서글프게 외쳤다. “가슴속에도 머릿속에도 그런 지옥을 품고서 어떻게 그럴 수 있어? 아니, 형이 떠나려는 건 바로 그것 때문이야, 그들 무리에 끼려고…… 그러지 않으면 형은 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거야, 견디지 못할 테니까!”\n\n“무엇이든 견뎌 낼 힘이 있어.” 이반이 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n“무슨 힘?”\n\n“카라마조프의 힘—카라마조프적 비열함의 힘이지.”\n\n“방탕에 빠져들고, 타락으로 영혼을 질식시키는 것, 그거야?”\n\n“어쩌면 그것마저도…… 다만 서른까지는 어쩌면 그걸 피할 수 있겠지, 그다음엔—”\n\n“어떻게 그걸 피한다는 거야? 무엇으로 피할 건데? 형의 그런 생각으로는 불가능해.”\n\n“다시 카라마조프식으로지.”\n\n“‘모든 것이 허용된다’, 그 말이야? 모든 것이 허용된다, 그거지?”\n\n이반이 눈살을 찌푸리더니, 별안간 이상하리만치 창백해졌다.\n\n“아, 어제 미우소프를 그토록 발끈하게 만든 그 말을 너도 받아 옮겼구나—드미트리가 그토록 천진하게 덥석 물어 풀어 말한 그 말 말이야!” 그가 묘하게 미소 지었다. “그래, 네가 원한다면, 이미 그 말이 나온 이상 ‘모든 것이 허용된다’고 해 두지. 부정하지 않겠어. 게다가 미탸의 해석도 나쁘지 않고.”\n\n알료샤는 말없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n“나는 여기를 떠나도 적어도 너만은 있다고 생각했는데.” 이반이 별안간 뜻밖의 감정을 실어 말했다. “하지만 이제 보니, 내 사랑하는 은수자야, 네 가슴속에조차 나를 위한 자리는 없구나. ‘모든 것이 허용된다’는 그 명제를 나는 포기하지 않겠어—그렇다고 너는 그 때문에 나를 저버릴 거니, 응?”\n\n알료샤는 일어나 그에게 다가가서는 부드럽게 그의 입술에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n“그건 표절이야.” 이반이 몹시 기뻐하며 외쳤다. “내 시에서 훔쳤구나. 그래도 고맙다. 일어나, 알료샤, 우리 둘 다 이제 갈 시간이야.”\n\n그들은 밖으로 나왔으나, 음식점 입구에 이르렀을 때 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n“들어 봐, 알료샤.” 이반이 단호한 목소리로 입을 열었다. “만일 내가 정말로 그 끈적끈적한 어린잎들을 아낄 수 있다면, 그건 오직 너를 떠올리며 그것들을 사랑하기 때문일 거야. 네가 여기 어딘가에 있다는 것만으로 나에겐 충분하고, 그러면 아직은 삶에 대한 욕망을 잃지 않을 거야. 이만하면 너에게도 충분하지? 원한다면 사랑의 고백으로 받아들여도 좋아. 자, 너는 오른쪽으로 가고 나는 왼쪽으로 갈게. 그리고 이걸로 충분해, 알겠지, 충분하다고. 그러니까 설령 내일 내가 떠나지 않더라도(아무래도 떠날 것 같긴 하지만), 또 우리가 다시 만나더라도, 이런 문제들에 대해선 더는 한마디도 하지 마. 그것만은 특별히 부탁할게. 그리고 드미트리에 관해서도, 이건 각별히 부탁하는데, 다시는 나에게 말하지 마.” 그가 별안간 짜증을 내며 덧붙였다. “그건 다 동났어, 죄다 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말했잖아, 안 그래? 그 대신 내가 너에게 약속 하나를 하지. 서른이 되어 ‘잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이치고’ 싶어지면, 내가 어디에 있든 너와 한 번 더 이야기하러 올게, 설령 아메리카에 있더라도 말이야, 그건 믿어도 좋아. 일부러 찾아오겠어. 그때 너를 한번 보는 것, 네가 그 무렵 어떤 사람이 되어 있을지 보는 것도 무척 흥미로울 거야. 보다시피 제법 엄숙한 약속이지. 그리고 우리는 정말 칠 년이나 십 년쯤 헤어지게 될지도 몰라. 자, 이제 네 파테르 세라피쿠스에게 가 봐, 그분은 임종을 앞두고 계셔. 네가 없는 사이에 돌아가시기라도 하면, 너는 내가 너를 붙들었다고 나를 원망할 테니까. 잘 가, 한 번 더 입맞춰 줘. 옳지, 그래, 이제 가.\"\n* 파테르 세라피쿠스(Pater Seraphicus): 라틴어로 ‘천사 같은 아버지’라는 뜻으로, 본디 아시시의 성 프란체스코에게 붙던 칭호.\n\n이반은 별안간 몸을 돌려, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 제 갈 길을 갔다. 그것은 바로 전날 드미트리가 알료샤를 두고 떠났던 모습 그대로였다, 비록 그 작별은 사뭇 달랐지만. 그 야릇한 닮음이 그 괴로움과 낙담의 순간에 화살처럼 알료샤의 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그는 형의 뒷모습을 바라보며 잠시 서 있었다. 그는 문득 이반이 걸으면서 몸을 휘청거리는 것을, 그리고 오른쪽 어깨가 왼쪽보다 처져 보이는 것을 알아차렸다. 전에는 한 번도 눈여겨본 적이 없는 것이었다. 그러나 별안간 그 역시 몸을 돌려, 거의 뛰다시피 수도원으로 향했다. 날은 거의 어두워져 있었고, 그는 어쩐지 두려운 느낌마저 들었다. 무언가 새로운 것이 그의 안에서 자라나고 있었는데, 그것이 무엇인지 그는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다. 전날 저녁처럼 다시 바람이 일었고, 그가 암자 숲으로 들어서자 늙은 소나무들이 그의 주위에서 음울하게 술렁거렸다. 그는 거의 뛰다시피 했다. “파테르 세라피쿠스라—형은 어디선가 그 이름을 따왔는데, 대체 어디서일까?” 알료샤는 의아해했다. “이반, 가엾은 이반, 그러면 언제 다시 형을 보게 될까?…… 여기가 암자로구나. 그래, 그래, 바로 그분이셔, 파테르 세라피쿠스, 그분이 나를 구해 주실 거야—형에게서, 그리고 영원히!”\n\n그 뒤로 여러 번 그는, 이반과 헤어지면서 어떻게 형 드미트리를 그토록 까맣게 잊어버릴 수 있었는지 의아해하곤 했다. 바로 그날 아침, 불과 몇 시간 전만 해도 그는 형을 찾아내겠다고, 설령 그날 밤 수도원으로 돌아오지 못하는 한이 있더라도 결코 그 일을 포기하지 않겠다고 그토록 굳게 다짐했던 터였다.\n\n# Chapter VI. For Awhile A Very Obscure One\n\n이반은 알료샤와 헤어진 뒤 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 돌아갔다. 그러나 이상하게도 그는 견디기 힘든 우울에 사로잡혔는데, 그 우울은 집으로 향하는 걸음마다 점점 더 깊어졌다. 그가 우울해한다는 것 자체에는 이상할 것이 없었으나, 정작 이상한 것은 그 우울의 원인이 무엇인지 이반 자신도 말할 수 없었다는 점이다. 그는 전에도 자주 우울에 잠긴 적이 있었고, 자기를 이곳으로 끌어들인 모든 것과 인연을 끊고서 바로 그날 새 출발을 하여 미지의 새로운 미래로 들어서려는 그런 순간에 우울을 느낀다는 것에는 놀랄 일이 없었다. 그는 또다시 여느 때처럼 외톨이가 될 터였으나, 비록 인생에 큰 희망과 크나큰—지나치게 큰—기대를 품고 있었음에도, 그 희망이며 기대며 심지어 자신의 소망에 대해서조차 그는 무엇 하나 또렷하게 설명할 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 그 순간 새롭고 미지의 것에 대한 불안이 그의 가슴 한구석에 분명히 자리 잡고 있었음에도, 그를 괴롭히는 것은 전혀 다른 무엇이었다. \"아버지 집에 대한 혐오인가?\" 그는 의아해했다. \"그럴 법도 하지. 그 집이라면 이제 지긋지긋하니까. 비록 그 가증스러운 문턱을 넘는 것도 오늘이 마지막이긴 하지만, 그래도 나는 그 집이 역겨워…… 아니, 그것도 아니야. 알료샤와 헤어진 일, 그와 나눈 대화 때문인가? 그토록 오랜 세월 온 세상을 향해 입을 다물고 한마디도 할 가치를 못 느꼈던 내가, 느닷없이 그런 장광설을 줄줄이 늘어놓다니.\" 그것은 분명 젊은 미숙함과 허영심에서 비롯된 젊은이다운 분함—자기 마음을 제대로 표현하지 못한 데 대한 분함이었을 수도 있는데, 더구나 그가 진심으로 믿고 마음을 기대어 온 알료샤 같은 존재 앞에서였으니 더욱 그러했다. 그런 분함도 끼어 있었음에 틀림없고, 그래야 마땅했다. 그러나 그것도 아니었다, 그것도 아니었다. \"우울로 속이 메스껍건만, 정작 내가 무엇을 바라는지 알 수가 없군. 차라리 아무 생각도 하지 않는 편이 나으려나.\"\n\n이반은 \"아무 생각도 하지 않으려\" 애써 보았으나, 그것 또한 소용이 없었다. 그의 우울이 그토록 짜증스럽고 거슬렸던 것은, 그것이 어딘가 우발적이고 외부에서 비롯된 성격을 띠고 있었기 때문이다—그는 그렇게 느꼈다. 어떤 사람인지 사물인지가 어딘가에 불쑥 튀어나와 있는 듯했는데, 마치 무언가가 이따금 눈앞에 거슬리게 끼어들 때처럼, 일이나 대화에 골몰한 나머지 한참 동안 그것을 알아차리지 못하면서도 끝내 짜증이 나고 거의 괴로워지다가, 마침내 그 거슬리는 물건—흔히 아주 사소하고 우스꽝스러운 것, 즉 엉뚱한 자리에 놓인 어떤 물건이라든가 바닥에 떨어진 손수건이라든가 선반에 도로 꽂아 두지 않은 책 따위—을 깨닫고 치워 버리게 되는 것과 같았다.\n\n마침내 잔뜩 신경이 곤두서고 심기가 사나워진 채 이반은 집에 다다랐는데, 정원 문에서 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 문득 자신을 안달하게 하고 괴롭히던 것이 무엇인지를 깨달았다.\n\n대문 옆 벤치에는 하인 스메르댜코프가 앉아 저녁의 서늘함을 즐기고 있었는데, 그를 처음 본 순간 이반은 자기 마음에 걸린 것이 바로 그 하인 스메르댜코프이며, 자기 영혼이 역겨워하는 것이 바로 이 사내임을 알아차렸다. 그 모든 것이 별안간 머릿속에 떠오르며 분명해졌다. 바로 조금 전, 알료샤가 스메르댜코프와 마주친 이야기를 들려주었을 때 그는 문득 음울함과 혐오가 찌릿하게 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 그 즉시 가슴속에 분노가 맞받아 일었던 것이다. 그 뒤 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 스메르댜코프를 잠시 잊고 있었으나, 그래도 그는 줄곧 마음 한구석에 남아 있었고, 이반이 알료샤와 헤어져 집으로 걸어오자마자 그 잊고 있던 감각이 다시금 비집고 나오기 시작했다. \"저런 비참하고 하찮은 작자가 나를 이토록 괴롭히다니, 이게 말이 되는가?\" 그는 견딜 수 없는 짜증과 함께 의아해했다.\n\n사실 이반은 요사이, 특히 지난 며칠 사이에 그 사내에게 격심한 혐오감을 느끼게 되었던 것이다. 그는 심지어 그 작자를 향해 거의 증오에 가까운 감정이 자기 안에서 자라나고 있음을 알아차리기 시작했다. 어쩌면 이 증오는 이반이 처음 이 고장에 왔을 때 전혀 다른 감정을 느꼈다는 사실 때문에 더욱 도드라졌는지도 모른다. 그때만 해도 그는 스메르댜코프에게 뚜렷한 흥미를 보였고, 심지어 그를 아주 독창적인 인간이라고까지 여겼다. 그는 스메르댜코프에게 말을 걸어 보라고 부추기곤 했는데, 그러면서도 그의 정신에 어딘가 두서없음, 아니 그보다는 안절부절못함 같은 것이 있다는 점에 늘 의아해했고, 이 \"사색가\"의 머릿속을 그토록 끊임없이 집요하게 짓누르는 것이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수가 없었다. 두 사람은 철학적인 문제들을, 심지어 해와 달과 별이 나흘째 날에야 비로소 창조되었는데 어떻게 첫째 날에 빛이 있을 수 있었는지, 그것을 어떻게 이해해야 하는지 따위를 토론했다. 그러나 이반은 곧, 해와 달과 별이 흥미로운 주제일 수는 있어도 스메르댜코프에게는 그것이 한참 부차적인 것이며, 그가 노리는 것은 전혀 다른 무엇임을 알아차렸다. 이런저런 방식으로 그는 끝 모를 허영심, 그것도 상처 입은 허영심을 드러내기 시작했고, 이반은 그것이 못마땅했다. 그것이 바로 그의 반감을 처음으로 불러일으킨 것이었다. 그 뒤 집 안에 풍파가 일었다. 그루셴카가 등장했고 그의 형 드미트리와 얽힌 추문들이 벌어졌으니—그들은 그 일도 토론했다. 그러나 스메르댜코프는 늘 그 이야기를 굉장한 흥분에 차서 했음에도, 그가 그 일이 어떻게 결말나기를 바라는지는 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었다. 사실 무심코 드러나면서 늘 모호하게 표현되는 그의 어떤 욕망들에는 비논리성과 두서없음이 깃들어 있어 놀라울 정도였다. 스메르댜코프는 늘 캐물었고, 에둘러 가면서도 명백히 미리 궁리해 둔 어떤 질문들을 던졌으나, 그 목적이 무엇인지는 설명하지 않았고, 대개 가장 중요한 대목에 이르면 말을 뚝 끊고 침묵에 잠기거나 다른 화제로 넘어가 버리곤 했다. 그러나 끝내 이반을 가장 짜증스럽게 하고 그에 대한 혐오를 굳히게 만든 것은, 스메르댜코프가 점점 더 노골적으로 드러내기 시작한 그 묘하고도 역겨운 허물없음이었다. 그가 분수를 잊고 무례하게 굴었다는 것은 아니다. 오히려 그는 언제나 아주 공손하게 말했지만, 어찌 된 영문인지—신만이 알 일이지만!—자기와 이반 표도로비치 사이에 무슨 묵계 같은 것이 있다고 여기기 시작한 것이 분명했다. 그는 늘, 두 사람 사이에 어떤 약조가, 어떤 비밀이 있으며 그것이 언젠가 양쪽에서 표명된 바 있어 오직 둘만이 알고 주변 사람들은 도저히 헤아릴 수 없는 것이라는 듯한 어조로 말했다. 그러나 이반은 오랫동안 자라나는 혐오의 진짜 원인을 알아차리지 못했고, 그 뿌리에 무엇이 있는지는 최근에야 비로소 깨달은 터였다.\n\n그는 역겨움과 짜증을 느끼며 스메르댜코프에게 말도 걸지 않고 쳐다보지도 않은 채 문 안으로 들어가려 했다. 그러나 스메르댜코프가 벤치에서 일어섰고, 이반은 그 동작만으로도 그가 유독 자기에게 할 말이 있다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다. 이반은 그를 쳐다보며 걸음을 멈추었는데, 조금 전까지만 해도 그냥 지나치려 했던 자신이 멈춰 섰다는 사실이 그를 격분케 했다. 그는 분노와 혐오에 차서, 이마 위로 빗어 내린 자잘한 곱슬머리에 거세된 듯 병약한 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 사내는 왼쪽 눈을 찡긋하더니, 마치 이렇게 말하기라도 하듯 히죽 웃었다. \"어디 가십니까? 그냥은 못 지나가실 텐데요. 우리처럼 영리한 두 사람끼린 서로 나눌 이야기가 있다는 걸 잘 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n이반은 부르르 떨었다. \"꺼져, 이 한심한 멍청아. 내가 너 따위와 무슨 상관이냐\"라는 말이 혀끝까지 차올랐으나, 그는 자기 입에서 \"아버지는 아직 주무시느냐, 아니면 깨셨느냐?\" 하는 말이 흘러나오는 것을 듣고 소스라치게 놀랐다.\n\n그는 자기도 놀랄 만큼 나직하고 온순하게 그 질문을 던졌고, 곧이어 역시 자기도 놀랍게도 벤치에 가서 앉았다. 한순간 그는 거의 두려움마저 느꼈는데, 그것을 그는 훗날까지 기억했다. 스메르댜코프는 두 손을 뒷짐 진 채 그를 마주 보고 서서, 자신만만하고 거의 준엄하다 싶은 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나리께선 아직 주무십니다.\" 그는 한 마디 한 마디 또박또박 말했다(\"먼저 말을 건 건 제가 아니라 나리이십니다.\" 그는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다). \"전 나리께서 의아하기 짝이 없습니다.\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 짐짓 눈을 내리깔고 오른발을 앞으로 내밀어 반들반들 닦은 장화 코를 까닥거리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내가 왜 의아하다는 게냐?\" 이반은 안간힘을 다해 자신을 억누르며 퉁명스럽고 시무룩하게 물었는데, 문득 역겨움과 함께 자신이 격렬한 호기심을 느끼고 있으며 무슨 일이 있어도 그것을 채우지 않고는 자리를 뜨지 못하리라는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n\"나리께선 왜 체르마쉬냐로 가지 않으십니까?\" 스메르댜코프가 별안간 눈을 들며 허물없이 웃어 보였다. \"제가 왜 웃는지는, 영리하신 분이라면 나리께서 스스로 아셔야지요.\" 가늘게 뜬 그의 왼쪽 눈이 이렇게 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"내가 왜 체르마쉬냐로 가야 하느냐?\" 이반이 놀라며 물었다.\n\n스메르댜코프는 다시 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"표도르 파블로비치께서 친히 그토록 가시라고 청하지 않으셨습니까.\" 그는 마침내 천천히, 그러면서도 제 대답에 별 의미를 두지 않는 듯이 말했다. \"그저 무슨 말이라도 하려고 부차적인 핑계를 들어 둘러댄 것뿐입니다.\" 그는 이렇게 넌지시 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을! 원하는 게 뭔지 똑바로 말해!\" 이반은 마침내 온순함을 떨치고 사납게 소리쳤다.\n\n스메르댜코프는 오른발을 왼발 쪽으로 끌어당기고 몸을 곧추세웠으나, 여전히 똑같은 태연함과 똑같은 옅은 미소를 띤 채 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"별다른 건 없습니다—그저 이야깃거리 삼아 해 본 말씀이지요.\"\n\n또다시 침묵이 이어졌다. 두 사람은 거의 일 분가량 입을 열지 않았다. 이반은 자신이 일어나 화를 내야 마땅하다는 것을 알았고, 스메르댜코프는 그의 앞에 서서 그가 화를 낼지 말지를 지켜보기라도 하듯 기다리는 것 같았다. 적어도 이반에게는 그렇게 보였다. 마침내 그가 일어서려고 몸을 움직였다. 스메르댜코프는 그 틈을 놓치지 않으려는 듯했다.\n\n\"전 끔찍한 처지에 놓여 있습니다, 이반 표도로비치. 도무지 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다.\" 그는 단호하고 또렷하게 말했고, 마지막 말에 이르러 한숨을 내쉬었다. 이반 표도로비치는 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"두 분 다 완전히 제정신이 아니십니다, 어린애나 다름없으십니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 말을 이었다. \"나리이신 아버님과 형님이신 드미트리 표도로비치 말씀입니다. 곧 표도르 파블로비치께서 일어나셔서는 시도 때도 없이 절 들볶으실 겁니다. '그 여자가 왔느냐? 왜 안 오는 게냐?' 하면서 자정까지, 아니 자정이 넘도록 그러시겠지요. 그리고 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오지 않으면(아마 아예 올 생각이 없으실 테지만) 내일 아침에 또 절 붙들고 '왜 안 왔느냐? 언제 오는 게냐?' 하실 텐데—마치 그게 제 탓이라도 되는 양 말입니다. 다른 쪽도 나을 게 없습니다. 날이 어두워지자마자, 아니 그 전부터 형님께서 총을 손에 들고 나타나실 겁니다. '잘 봐 둬라, 이 악당아, 요리나 끓이는 놈아. 그 여자를 놓치고 다녀간 걸 내게 알리지 않으면—누구보다 먼저 너부터 죽여 버리겠다.' 밤이 지나고 아침이 오면 그분도 표도르 파블로비치처럼 절 죽도록 들볶기 시작하시지요. '왜 안 왔느냐? 곧 오겠느냐?' 그분도 자기 여자가 오지 않는 걸 제 탓으로 여기십니다. 그렇게 날이 갈수록 시간이 갈수록 두 분 다 점점 더 화를 내시니, 전 가끔 겁에 질린 나머지 차라리 죽어 버리는 게 낫겠다 싶을 정도입니다. 전 그분들을 믿을 수가 없습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"한데 넌 왜 끼어든 게냐? 왜 드미트리 표도로비치를 위해 염탐 노릇을 시작했어?\" 이반이 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어떻게 안 끼어들 수 있었겠습니까? 하긴, 정말 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 전 끼어든 적이 전혀 없습니다. 처음부터 감히 대꾸도 못 하고 잠자코만 있었는데, 그분이 절 자기 종으로 점찍어 버리신 거지요. 그 뒤로 그분이 하시는 말씀이라곤 한 가지뿐이었습니다. '그 여자를 놓치면, 이 악당아, 널 죽여 버리겠다.' 나리, 전 내일 긴 발작을 일으킬 게 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"'긴 발작'이라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\"\n\n\"긴 발작이란 오래 지속되는 발작입니다—몇 시간, 아니 하루이틀씩 가기도 하지요. 언젠가는 사흘이나 계속된 적도 있습니다. 그때 전 다락에서 떨어졌지요. 경련이 멎었다가 다시 시작되었고, 사흘 동안 전 정신을 차리지 못했습니다. 표도르 파블로비치께서 여기 의사 헤르첸슈투베를 부르셨고, 그분이 제 머리에 얼음을 얹고 또 다른 약도 써 보셨지요…… 하마터면 죽을 뻔했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 간질이란 발작이 언제 올지 알 수 없다고들 하던데. 어째서 내일 발작을 일으킨다고 말하는 게냐?\" 이반은 묘하고 짜증스러운 호기심에 차서 물었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 미리 알 수는 없지요.\"\n\n\"게다가 그때 넌 다락에서 떨어진 거잖느냐.\"\n\n\"전 매일 다락에 올라갑니다. 내일도 다락에서 떨어질 수 있지요. 그게 아니라도 지하실 계단에서 굴러떨어질 수도 있고요. 전 지하실에도 매일 들어가야 하니까요.\"\n\n이반은 그를 한참 바라보았다.\n\n\"내가 보기에 넌 지금 허튼소리를 하고 있고, 난 네 말을 도무지 못 알아듣겠다.\" 그는 나직하게, 그러나 어딘가 위협조로 말했다. \"그러니까 넌 내일 사흘 동안 앓는 척하겠다는 게냐, 응?\"\n\n다시 땅바닥을 내려다보며 오른발 발끝을 까닥거리던 스메르댜코프는 그 발을 내려놓고 왼발을 앞으로 옮긴 뒤, 히죽 웃으며 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"제가 만일 그런 술수를 부릴 수 있다면, 그러니까 발작을 일으킨 척할 수 있다면—발작에 이골이 난 사람에게야 어려운 일도 아니지요—제 목숨을 죽음에서 건지기 위해 그런 수단을 쓸 권리쯤은 충분히 있을 겁니다. 제가 앓아누워 있는 동안 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 아버님을 찾아오신다 해도, 나리께서 병든 사람더러 어째서 알리지 않았느냐고 탓하실 수는 없을 테니까요. 그러기엔 면구스러우실 테지요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 노릇이군!\" 이반은 분노로 얼굴을 일그러뜨리며 외쳤다. \"넌 어째서 늘 그렇게 제 목숨에 벌벌 떠는 게냐? 드미트리 형님의 협박이라야 다 성마른 말일 뿐 아무 의미도 없어. 그자는 널 죽이지 않아. 그자가 죽일 상대는 네가 아니야!\"\n\n\"무엇보다 먼저 절 파리 잡듯 죽이실 겁니다. 하지만 그보다 더 두려운 건, 그분이 아버님께 무슨 미친 짓을 저질렀을 때 제가 그 공범으로 몰리는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"네가 왜 공범으로 몰린다는 거냐?\"\n\n\"제가 그분께 큰 비밀로 그 신호를 알려 드렸으니, 사람들은 절 공범이라 여길 겁니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 신호 말이냐? 누구한테 말했어? 빌어먹을, 더 분명히 말해 봐.\"\n\n\"솔직히 인정할 수밖에 없는 사실이 있습니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 꼼꼼하고 침착하게 느릿느릿 말했다. \"이 일에 관해 제가 표도르 파블로비치와 한 가지 비밀을 나누고 있다는 것 말입니다. 나리께서도 친히 아시다시피(혹시 아신다면 말입니다만), 그분은 며칠째 밤이, 아니 저녁만 되어도 곧장 문을 걸어 잠그십니다. 요즈음 나리께선 저녁마다 일찍 위층 방으로 올라가셨고, 어제는 아예 내려오지도 않으셨으니, 그분이 밤마다 얼마나 조심스레 문을 걸어 잠그기 시작하셨는지 아마 모르실 겁니다. 그리고리 바실리예비치가 문 앞에 와도 그분은 목소리를 듣기 전에는 절대 열어 주지 않으십니다. 한데 그리고리 바실리예비치는 오지 않습니다, 이제 그분 방의 시중은 저 혼자 들고 있으니까요. 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 둘러싼 이 소동이 시작된 뒤로 그분이 친히 그렇게 정하셨습니다. 한데 밤이 되면 그분의 분부에 따라 저는 별채로 물러가는데, 그래서 자정까지 잠들지 못하고 망을 보며 일어나 마당을 거닐면서 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오기를 기다립니다. 요 며칠 그분은 그 여자를 기다리며 완전히 넋이 나가 계십니다. 그분 말씀인즉, 그 여자가 드미트리 표도로비치(그분은 미탸라고 부르시지요)를 무서워하니, '그래서 그 여자가 밤늦게 뒷길로 나를 찾아올 게다. 너는' 하고 그분이 말씀하십니다, '자정까지, 아니 그 뒤까지도 그 여자를 살펴라. 만약 정말 오거든 달려 올라와 내 문을, 아니면 정원 쪽 창문을 두드려라. 처음엔 두 번, 좀 살그머니 두드리고, 그다음엔 세 번을 좀 더 빠르게 두드려라. 그러면' 하고 그분이 말씀하시길, '나는 그 여자가 왔다는 걸 단번에 알아채고 너에게 조용히 문을 열어 주마.' 또 한 가지 신호는 무슨 뜻밖의 일이 생겼을 때를 위해 정해 주셨습니다. 처음엔 두 번 두드리고, 그다음 잠시 사이를 두었다가 한 번 훨씬 크게 두드리는 겁니다. 그러면 그분은 갑자기 무슨 일이 벌어졌고 제가 꼭 뵈어야 한다는 걸 알아채시고, 제가 들어가 말씀드릴 수 있게 문을 열어 주십니다. 이건 다 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 직접은 못 오고 전갈만 보낼 경우를 위한 겁니다. 게다가 드미트리 표도로비치가 들이닥칠 수도 있으니, 그분이 가까이 와 있다는 것도 제가 알려 드려야 하지요. 나리께선 드미트리 표도로비치를 끔찍이 두려워하셔서, 설령 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 와서 그분과 함께 안에 잠겨 있는데 그때 마침 드미트리 표도로비치가 어디 근처에라도 나타난다면, 저는 세 번을 두드려 그분께 즉시 알려 드려야만 합니다. 그러니까 다섯 번 두드리는 첫 번째 신호는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 왔다는 뜻이고, 세 번 두드리는 두 번째 신호는 '긴히 드릴 말씀이 있다'는 뜻입니다. 나리께선 제게 그 신호를 여러 번 보여 주시고 설명해 주셨지요. 그리고 온 세상에서 그 신호를 아는 사람은 저와 나리뿐이니, 그분은 조금도 망설이지 않고, 소리쳐 부르는 일도 없이(그분은 큰 소리로 부르짖는 걸 끔찍이 두려워하십니다) 문을 열어 주실 겁니다. 한데 이제 그 신호를 드미트리 표도로비치도 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 알았어? 네가 말한 게냐? 어떻게 감히 그자에게 말했어?\"\n\n\"겁에 질려서 그랬습니다. 제가 어떻게 감히 그분께 숨길 수 있었겠습니까? 드미트리 표도로비치는 날마다 끈질기게 다그치셨습니다. '넌 날 속이고 있어, 뭔가 감추고 있지! 두 다리를 분질러 놓겠다.' 그래서 전 제가 종처럼 충성을 다하고 있다는 걸 보여 드리고, 제가 그분을 속이는 게 아니라 아는 걸 죄다 말씀드리고 있다는 데 만족하시도록, 그 비밀 신호를 알려 드린 겁니다.\"\n\n\"만약 그자가 그 신호를 이용해 안으로 들어오려 한다고 생각된다면, 그자를 들여보내지 마라.\"\n\n\"하지만 제가 발작으로 드러눕는다면 그때는 어떻게 그분이 들어오시는 걸 막겠습니까. 설령 감히 막으려 한들, 그분이 얼마나 무모하신 분인지 잘 아는 처지에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을! 네가 어떻게 발작을 일으킬 거라고 그토록 확신하느냐, 이 망할 놈아? 날 비웃는 게냐?\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 감히 나리를 비웃겠습니까? 이렇게 공포에 짓눌려 있는데 웃을 기분이 어디 있겠습니까. 전 발작이 올 것만 같습니다. 예감이 듭니다. 공포만으로도 발작이 일어날 겁니다.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을! 네가 드러눕는다면 그리고리가 망을 볼 게다. 그리고리에게 미리 알려 둬라. 그러면 그자는 분명 그를 들여보내지 않을 테지.\"\n\n\"저는 주인 나리의 분부 없이는 그리고리 바실리예비치에게 그 신호 이야기를 감히 꺼낼 수 없습니다. 게다가 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그분의 소리를 듣고 들여보내지 않는 일로 말하자면, 그분은 어제부터 줄곧 앓아누워 계시고, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 내일 약을 드리려고 합니다. 방금 그렇게 정했지요. 그 약이라는 게 참 묘한 처방입니다. 마르파 이그나티예브나는 어떤 약을 조제할 줄 알고 늘 그걸 지니고 있습니다. 무슨 약초로 만든 독한 것이지요. 그 비방을 그분이 알고 있어서, 그리고리 바실리예비치가 요통이 너무 심해 거의 몸을 못 가눌 지경이 되면 일 년에 세 번씩 늘 그걸 먹입니다. 그러면 그분은 수건을 가져다 그 약을 적셔서는, 등이 온통 벌겋게 부어오를 때까지 반 시간이나 그분의 등 전체를 문지르고, 병에 남은 것은 특별한 기도와 함께 마시게 합니다. 하지만 죄다 마시게 하진 않습니다, 그런 때면 그분은 자기 몫을 좀 남겨 두었다가 자기도 마시거든요. 그리고 두 사람은 독한 술이라곤 입에도 안 대는 터라, 단언컨대 둘 다 그 자리에서 곯아떨어져 아주 오랫동안 깊이 잠듭니다. 그리고리 바실리예비치는 깨어나면 씻은 듯이 멀쩡해지지만, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 언제나 그 약 때문에 두통을 앓지요. 그러니 마르파 이그나티예브나가 내일 작정대로 한다면, 두 사람은 아무것도 못 듣고 드미트리 표도로비치를 막지 못할 겁니다. 잠들어 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 그런 장광설이 다 있느냐! 게다가 그 모든 게 마치 짜기라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 벌어지는군. 넌 발작을 일으키고 두 사람은 의식을 잃고.\" 이반이 외쳤다. \"한데 혹시 네가 일부러 그렇게 꾸미려는 건 아니냐?\" 하는 말이 불쑥 그의 입에서 튀어나왔고, 그는 위협하듯 이맛살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"제가 어찌 그러겠습니까?…… 그리고 모든 게 드미트리 표도로비치와 그분의 계획에 달려 있는데, 제가 무엇 때문에 그러겠습니까?…… 그분이 무슨 짓을 할 작정이라면 하실 테고, 그럴 마음이 없으면 제가 굳이 그분을 아버님께 떠밀어 보내지는 않겠지요.\"\n\n\"한데 네 말마따나 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 아예 오지도 않을 거라면, 그자가 무엇 때문에 아버지를 찾아가겠느냐, 그것도 몰래 말이야?\" 이반은 분노로 새파랗게 질려 가며 말을 이었다. \"그건 네가 직접 한 말이야. 내가 여기 있는 내내 그게 다 늙은이의 망상일 뿐이고 그 계집은 그에게 오지 않으리라고 확신했단 말이다. 여자가 오지 않는데 드미트리가 무엇 때문에 그에게 쳐들어가겠어? 말해 봐, 네가 무슨 생각을 하는지 알고 싶다!\"\n\n\"그분이 왜 오실지는 나리께서도 친히 아시잖습니까. 제 생각이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 그분은 그저 화가 나셨거나, 어쩌면 제 병 때문에 의심이 동하셔서 오실 겁니다. 그리고 어제처럼 안달이 나서, 그 여자가 자기 몰래 빠져나간 건 아닌지 살피려고 방들을 뒤지러 들이닥치실 테지요. 게다가 그분은 표도르 파블로비치께서 삼천 루블이 든 큰 봉투를 가지고 계시다는 것도 똑똑히 알고 있습니다. 리본으로 묶고 봉인을 세 개나 찍은 봉투지요. 거기엔 그분의 친필로 '나의 천사 그루셴카에게, 그녀가 와 준다면'이라고 적혀 있고, 사흘 뒤엔 '나의 병아리에게'라고 덧붙여 두셨습니다. 그게 무슨 일을 부를지 누가 알겠습니까.\"\n\n\"허튼소리!\" 이반은 거의 제정신이 아닌 채 외쳤다. \"드미트리는 돈을 훔치려고, 그것 때문에 아버지를 죽이러 오지는 않아. 어제는 그루셴카 때문에 그 미친 듯 사나운 바보처럼 그를 죽였을지 몰라도, 도둑질은 하지 않아.\"\n\n\"그분은 지금 돈이 몹시 궁하십니다—더없이 궁하시지요, 이반 표도로비치. 그분이 얼마나 궁한 처지인지 나리께선 모르십니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 더없이 침착하고 놀라우리만치 또렷하게 설명했다. \"그분은 그 삼천 루블도 자기 것이라 여기십니다. 제게 직접 그렇게 말씀하셨지요. '아버지는 아직도 내게 꼭 삼천 루블을 빚지고 있다'고 말입니다. 게다가 이반 표도로비치, 또 한 가지 틀림없는 사실을 헤아려 보십시오. 말하자면 거의 확실한 일인데, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 마음만 먹는다면 그분을—주인 나리, 그러니까 표도르 파블로비치 말씀입니다—자기와 결혼하게끔 몰아갈 수 있다는 겁니다. 마음만 먹는다면 말이지요, 물론 그럴 마음이 들 수도 있고요. 제가 한 말은 그저 그 여자가 오지 않으리라는 것뿐이었지만, 어쩌면 그 여자는 그 이상을 노리고 있는지도 모릅니다—그러니까 이 집의 안주인이 되는 것 말입니다. 저도 알다시피 그 여자의 상인인 삼소노프가 그걸 두고 그 여자와 웃으며, 그건 결코 어리석은 짓이 아닐 거라고 아주 대놓고 말했답니다. 게다가 그 여자는 머리가 비상하지요. 드미트리 표도로비치 같은 거지에게 시집갈 리가 없습니다. 그러니 이반 표도로비치, 그 점을 헤아려 보십시오. 그렇게 되면 주인 나리께서 돌아가신 뒤에 드미트리 표도로비치도, 나리도, 동생분이신 알렉세이 표도로비치도 한 푼 손에 넣지 못할 겁니다. 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오로지 전 재산을, 있는 돈 전부를 거머쥐려고 그분과 결혼할 테니까요. 하지만 아버님께서 지금 돌아가신다면, 적어도 사만 루블쯤은 확실히 남을 겁니다, 그분이 그토록 미워하시는 드미트리 표도로비치에게도 말입니다. 유언장을 안 만들어 두셨으니까요…… 드미트리 표도로비치는 그걸 죄다 훤히 알고 계십니다.\"\n\n이반의 얼굴 위로 일종의 전율이 스쳐 지나갔다. 그는 별안간 얼굴이 붉어졌다.\n\n\"그렇다면 도대체 무엇 때문에,\" 그는 갑자기 스메르댜코프의 말을 가로막았다. \"내게 체르마쉬냐로 가라고 권하는 거냐? 그 말은 또 무슨 뜻이야? 내가 떠나 버리면 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어질지 너도 알 거 아니냐.\" 이반은 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬었다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 나직하고 조리 있게 말했는데, 그러면서도 이반을 뚫어지게 살피고 있었다.\n\n\"'바로 그렇다'니, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 이반은 안간힘으로 자신을 억누르며, 눈에 위협의 빛을 띤 채 그에게 따져 물었다.\n\n\"나리가 딱하게 여겨져서 드린 말씀입니다. 제가 나리 처지라면 그저 죄다 내팽개쳐 버리고 말겠습니다…… 이런 처지에 남아 있느니 말입니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 이반의 번득이는 눈을 바라보며 더없이 솔직한 태도로 대답했다. 두 사람은 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"넌 아무래도 완전히 멍청이인 데다, 게다가…… 지독한 악당이기도 하구나.\" 이반이 별안간 벤치에서 일어났다. 그는 곧장 문을 지나려 했으나, 우뚝 멈춰 서서 스메르댜코프를 돌아보았다. 그러자 묘한 일이 벌어졌다. 이반은 별안간 발작하듯 입술을 깨물고 주먹을 불끈 쥐었으니, 한순간만 더 있었다면 스메르댜코프에게 달려들었을 것이다. 어쨌든 스메르댜코프도 그 순간 그것을 알아채고는 흠칫 놀라 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 그러나 그 순간은 스메르댜코프에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않은 채 지나갔고, 이반은 영문 모를 당혹감에 잠긴 듯 잠자코 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"네가 알고 싶다면 말해 두마, 난 내일 모스크바로 떠난다—내일 이른 아침에. 그게 다야!\" 그는 별안간 화가 나서 소리 내어 말했고, 훗날 그때 무엇 때문에 스메르댜코프에게 굳이 그런 말을 했는지 스스로도 의아해했다.\n\n\"그게 나리께서 하실 수 있는 가장 좋은 일이지요.\" 그는 그 말을 미리 예상하고 있었다는 듯 응수했다. \"다만 여기서 무슨 일이 생기면 모스크바로도 언제든 전보를 칠 수 있다는 것만 빼면 말입니다.\"\n\n이반은 다시 걸음을 멈추고, 또다시 휙 스메르댜코프를 돌아보았다. 그러나 그에게도 변화가 일어나 있었다. 그 모든 허물없음과 무심함이 씻은 듯이 사라져 버린 것이다. 그의 얼굴은 주의와 기대를, 그러면서도 골똘하면서도 겁먹은 듯 비굴한 빛을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"뭔가 더 하실 말씀이—뭔가 덧붙이실 말씀이 없으십니까?\" 그가 이반에게 고정한 골똘한 눈길에서는 이런 물음이 읽혔다.\n\n\"그럼 체르마쉬냐로도 사람을 보내 날 부를 수 있겠느냐—무슨 일이 생기면 말이다?\" 이반은 영문 모를 까닭에 목소리를 높이며 별안간 소리쳤다.\n\n\"체르마쉬냐로도…… 사람을 보내 부르실 수 있지요.\" 스메르댜코프가 거의 속삭이듯 중얼거렸는데, 당황한 기색이면서도 이반의 눈을 골똘히 들여다보았다.\n\n\"다만 모스크바는 더 멀고 체르마쉬냐는 더 가깝다는 것뿐이지. 네가 체르마쉬냐를 고집하는 건 내가 찻삯을 아끼게 하려는 거냐, 아니면 내가 그렇게 멀리 돌아가지 않게 하려는 거냐?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다……\" 스메르댜코프가 갈라지는 목소리로 중얼거렸다. 그는 역겨운 미소를 띤 채 이반을 바라보더니, 다시 뒷걸음질 칠 채비를 했다. 그러나 놀랍게도 이반은 웃음을 터뜨리며 여전히 웃은 채로 문을 지나갔다. 그 순간 그의 얼굴을 본 사람이라면 누구나 그가 마음이 가벼워 웃는 것이 아님을 알았을 터이고, 그 자신도 그 순간 자기가 무엇을 느끼고 있는지 설명할 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 마치 신경의 광란에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 움직이고 걸었다.\n\n# Chapter VII. “It’s Always Worth While Speaking To A Clever Man”\n\n그리고 그는 바로 그 신경질적인 광기에 휩싸인 채 말을 했다. 안으로 들어서자마자 응접실에서 표도르 파블로비치와 마주치자, 그는 두 손을 내저으며 소리쳤다. \"위층 제 방으로 가는 거지, 아버지한테 가는 게 아닙니다. 안녕히 계세요!\" 그러고는 아버지를 쳐다보지조차 않으려 애쓰며 지나쳐 갔다. 그 순간 노인이 그에게 너무도 가증스러웠던 것일 수도 있다. 그러나 이토록 거리낌 없는 적의의 표출은 표도르 파블로비치에게마저 뜻밖이었다. 게다가 노인은 분명 그에게 무언가 곧장 말하고 싶은 것이 있어서 일부러 응접실로 마중을 나온 참이었다. 이런 살가운 인사를 받고서, 노인은 잠자코 선 채 빈정거리는 기색으로 아들이 위층으로 올라가는 것을, 그 모습이 보이지 않게 될 때까지 지켜보았다.\n\n\"저놈이 왜 저러나?\" 노인은 이반을 뒤따라온 스메르댜코프에게 즉시 물었다.\n\n\"뭔가 화가 나신 모양입죠. 누가 알겠습니까?\" 하인은 얼버무리듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈! 그럼 화내라고 해라. 사모바르나 들여놓고 어서 꺼져. 동작 좀 빨리 해! 무슨 소식 없냐?\"\n\n그러고는 스메르댜코프가 방금 이반에게 하소연한 그런 질문들이 줄줄이 이어졌으니, 죄다 그가 기다리는 손님에 관한 것들이었거니와, 이런 질문들은 여기서 생략하기로 한다. 반 시간쯤 뒤에는 집이 잠겼고, 미친 노인은 약속된 다섯 번의 노크 소리를 매 순간 들을 양으로 들뜬 기대에 차서 이 방 저 방을 헤매고 다녔다. 이따금 그는 어둠 속을 내다보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n밤이 무척 깊었지만 이반은 여전히 잠들지 못한 채 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그날 밤 그는 두 시까지 늦도록 자리에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 우리는 그의 생각을 여기서 일일이 풀어놓지 않을 것이며, 그 영혼을 들여다볼 자리도 지금은 아니다—그 차례는 다가올 것이다. 그리고 설령 그러려고 애쓴다 한들 그것을 풀어놓기란 매우 어려운 일이니, 그의 머릿속에는 사실 아무 생각도 없었고, 다만 무언가 몹시 어렴풋한 것과, 무엇보다도 격렬한 흥분이 있었을 따름이다. 그는 자신이 방향을 잃었음을 스스로 느꼈다. 또한 그는 온갖 기이하고 거의 놀랍기까지 한 욕망들에 시달렸으니, 이를테면 자정이 지나자 그는 별안간 아래층으로 내려가 문을 열고 별채로 가서 스메르댜코프를 두들겨 패고 싶다는, 격렬하고 거역할 수 없는 충동에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 누가 그에게 왜냐고 물었다면, 그는 그 어떤 정확한 이유도 댈 수 없었으리니, 다만 그 하인이 세상 누구보다도 자기를 더 심하게 모욕한 자라서 그를 혐오한다는 것 정도가 고작이었으리라. 한편으로 그날 밤 그는 한두 번이 아니게 무어라 설명할 수 없는 굴욕적인 공포에 사로잡혔으니, 그 공포가 자신의 육체적 기력을 그야말로 마비시키는 것을 느꼈다. 머리가 아프고 어지러웠다. 마치 누군가에게 복수라도 하려는 듯, 증오의 감정이 가슴속에서 쓰라리게 곪아 갔다. 그는 방금 알료샤와 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 그마저 미워했다. 어떤 순간에는 자기 자신을 격렬하게 증오하기도 했다. 카테리나 이바노브나에 대해서는 거의 생각하는 것조차 잊었는데, 나중에 그는 이 사실에 크게 놀랐으니, 더욱이 자신이 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그토록 호기롭게 이튿날 모스크바로 떠나겠다고 다짐했을 때 가슴속에서 무언가가 이렇게 속삭였던 것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있었기 때문이다. \"그건 헛소리야, 너는 떠나지 않아, 그리고 지금 큰소리치는 만큼 그렇게 쉽게 떨치고 가지도 못할걸.\"\n\n오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 그날 밤을 회상할 때면, 이반은 자신이 어떻게 별안간 소파에서 일어나, 마치 누가 지켜볼까 봐 두려워하기라도 하듯 살그머니 문을 열고 계단으로 나가, 아래층에서 부스럭거리는 표도르 파블로비치의 기척에 귀를 기울였던가를 유난한 혐오감과 함께 떠올렸으니, 그는 그렇게 한참을—오 분쯤을—기이한 호기심 같은 것에 사로잡혀, 심장이 두근거리는 동안 숨을 죽인 채 엿들었던 것이다. 그리고 어째서 그 모든 짓을 했는지, 어째서 엿듣고 있었는지, 그는 끝내 말할 수 없었으리라. 그 \"행동\"을 그는 평생토록 \"수치스러운 짓\"이라 불렀고, 마음 밑바닥에서는 그것을 제 생애에서 가장 비열한 짓으로 여겼다. 그 순간 표도르 파블로비치 자신에 대해서는 아무런 증오도 느끼지 않았고, 다만 그가 저 아래서 어떻게 걸어 다니는지, 지금 무엇을 하고 있을지를 알고 싶은 격렬한 호기심에 사로잡혀 있었을 뿐이다. 그는 노인이 어두운 창밖을 어떻게 내다보고, 방 한가운데 멈춰 서서 누군가 노크하기를 듣고, 또 듣고 있을지를 궁금해하며 머릿속에 그려 보았다. 이렇게 엿듣기 위해 이반은 두 번이나 계단으로 나갔다.\n\n두 시쯤 모든 것이 잠잠해지고 표도르 파블로비치마저 잠자리에 들었을 때, 이반도 곧장 잠들기로 단단히 마음먹고 침대에 들었으니, 끔찍하게 지친 것을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 정말로 곧바로 잠들었고, 꿈도 꾸지 않고 깊이 잠들었으나, 일곱 시에 일찍, 날이 환히 밝았을 때 잠에서 깼다. 눈을 뜬 그는 자신이 유난히 기운차게 느껴지는 데 놀랐다. 그는 곧장 벌떡 일어나 부랴부랴 옷을 입었고, 그러고는 트렁크를 끌어내어 당장 짐을 꾸리기 시작했다. 그의 속옷가지는 전날 아침 세탁부에게서 돌아와 있었다. 모든 일이 자신의 돌연한 출발을 거들어 주고 있다는 생각에 이반은 정말로 빙긋 웃었다. 그리고 그의 출발은 분명 돌연한 것이었다. 이반이 전날 (카테리나 이바노브나와 알료샤와 스메르댜코프에게) 이튿날 떠나겠다고 말하기는 했으나, 그는 잠자리에 들 때만 해도 떠날 생각이라곤 없었으며, 적어도 아침에 일어나 가장 먼저 할 일이 트렁크를 꾸리는 것이리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했음을 떠올렸다. 마침내 트렁크와 가방이 준비되었다. 마르파 이그나티예브나가 늘 하던 대로 \"나리, 차는 어디서 드시겠습니까, 방에서요, 아래층에서요?\" 하고 물으러 들어온 것은 아홉 시쯤이었다. 그는 거의 명랑해 보였으나, 그의 태도에도, 말과 몸짓에도 무언가 서두르고 산만한 데가 있었다. 아버지에게 살갑게 인사하고 심지어 건강이 어떠냐고 특별히 안부까지 물었으되 그 대답을 끝까지 듣지도 않은 채, 그는 한 시간 뒤에 모스크바로 아주 돌아가기 위해 떠나겠노라 알리며 말을 준비시켜 달라고 청했다. 그의 아버지는 이 통고를 놀라는 기색 하나 없이 들었고, 무례하게도 아들을 잃는 데 대한 아쉬움을 내비치는 것마저 잊었다. 그러기는커녕 그는 무언가 제 중요한 용무가 떠올라 크게 부산을 떨기 시작했다.\n\n\"이런 녀석을 봤나! 어째서 어제 말하지 않았어! 괜찮다, 어쨌든 다 어떻게든 될 테니. 얘야, 나한테 큰 일 하나만 해 다오. 가는 길에 체르마시냐에 좀 들러라. 볼로뱌 역에서 왼쪽으로 꺾기만 하면 되는데, 거기서 겨우 십이 베르스타만 더 가면 체르마시냐가 나오거든.\"\n\n\"죄송하지만, 못 합니다. 철도까지가 팔십 베르스타인 데다, 모스크바행 기차가 오늘 밤 일곱 시에 떠나거든요. 겨우 시간 맞춰 탈 수 있을까 말까예요.\"\n\n\"기차야 내일이나 모레 타면 되고, 오늘은 체르마시냐로 꺾어 들어가라. 아비 비위 좀 맞춰 준다고 그리 손해 볼 것도 없잖느냐! 여기 발목 잡는 일만 없었어도 진작 내가 직접 다녀왔을 게다, 거기 급한 용무가 좀 있어서 말이다. 한데 지금 나는… 지금은 도무지 갈 형편이 못 돼서…. 저, 거기 내 잡목림이 두 자리 있단다. 마슬로프네, 그 늙은 상인과 그 아들놈이 목재 값으로 팔천을 주겠다는 게야. 한데 작년에는 만이천을 주겠다던 사람을 놓쳐 버렸지 뭐냐. 이 근방에서는 도무지 사 줄 사람을 구할 수가 없어. 마슬로프네가 죄다 제멋대로 주물러 대거든. 그놈들이 주는 대로 받을 수밖에 없으니, 여기서는 아무도 감히 그놈들과 맞붙어 값을 부르질 못한단 말이다. 일린스코예 신부가 지난 목요일에 편지를 보냈는데, 내가 아는 고르스트킨이라는 상인이 나타났다는 게야. 그자가 쓸모 있는 건 이 고장 사람이 아니라서 마슬로프네를 두려워하지 않는다는 점이지. 그자가 잡목림 값으로 만천을 주겠다는구나. 듣고 있느냐? 한데 신부 말로는 그자가 통틀어 일주일밖에 여기 머물지 않는다니, 네가 당장 가서 그자와 흥정을 매듭지어야 한다는 게야.\"\n\n\"그럼 신부님께 편지를 쓰세요, 신부님이 흥정을 매듭지으시면 되잖아요.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 못 한다. 그 사람은 장사에 영 눈이 없어. 그야 둘도 없는 보배 같은 인물이지, 영수증 한 장 없이도 이만을 맡겨 둘 만한 사람이야. 한데 장사에는 도통 눈이 없어, 영락없는 어린애라니까, 까마귀한테도 속아 넘어갈 위인이야. 그런데도 학식은 또 있는 사람이거든, 믿어지느냐? 이 고르스트킨이란 자는 겉보기엔 농사꾼 같고 파란 카프탄을 걸치고 다니지만, 천생 사기꾼이지. 그게 또 다들 골치 썩는 점이고. 그자는 거짓말쟁이야. 어떨 땐 어찌나 새빨간 거짓말을 늘어놓는지 대체 왜 저러나 싶을 정도란다. 재작년에는 나한테 마누라가 죽어서 새장가를 들었다고 하더구나, 한데 그게 한마디도 사실이 아니었으니 믿어지느냐? 마누라는 죽기는커녕 지금까지도 멀쩡히 살아서 일주일에 두 번씩 그자를 두들겨 팬다지 뭐냐. 그러니 네가 알아내야 할 건, 그자가 잡목림을 사겠다며 만천을 주겠다고 할 때, 그게 거짓말인지 참말인지 하는 거다.\"\n\n\"그런 일에는 제가 아무 쓸모도 없을 겁니다. 저도 그런 눈이 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"가만, 잠깐 기다려 봐라! 너는 쓸모가 있고말고, 고르스트킨을 판가름할 표시를 일러 줄 테니. 그자와 거래한 지 오래되었거든. 그게 말이다, 너는 그자의 턱수염을 잘 봐야 한다. 그자한텐 더럽고 가늘고 불그스름한 턱수염이 있거든. 그자가 말할 때 턱수염이 떨리고 화를 내면, 그건 됐다, 진심을 말하는 거고 거래를 하고 싶다는 게야. 한데 왼손으로 턱수염을 쓰다듬으면서 히죽거리면, 그건 너를 속여먹으려는 거지. 눈은 보지 마라, 그자 눈에서는 아무것도 알아낼 수 없으니, 그자는 속을 알 수 없는 깊은 위인, 사기꾼이거든—하지만 턱수염은 봐야 한다! 내가 쪽지를 한 장 써 줄 테니 그자한테 보여 줘라. 그자는 고르스트킨이라 불리지만 본명은 랴가비야. 그래도 그렇게 부르진 마라, 화를 낼 테니. 그자와 말이 통해서 다 됐다 싶거든 여기로 당장 편지를 써라. 그저 '거짓말이 아닙니다'라고만 쓰면 된다. 만천에서 버텨라. 천쯤은 깎아 줘도 좋지만 그 이상은 안 된다. 생각해 봐라! 팔천하고 만천은 차이가 크지 않으냐. 삼천을 그냥 줍는 거나 다름없어. 사 줄 사람을 구하기도 쉽지 않은 데다, 나는 지금 돈이 절박하게 필요하단 말이다. 그저 진짜다 싶거든 알려만 다오, 내가 직접 달려가서 매듭지을 테니. 어떻게든 짬을 내 보마. 한데 그게 다 신부 혼자만의 짐작이라면, 내가 달려간들 무슨 소용이겠느냐? 자, 가 주겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아, 저는 그럴 시간이 없습니다. 양해해 주세요.\"\n\n\"이봐라, 아비 청 한번 들어줘도 되잖느냐. 내 잊지 않으마. 너희들은 죄다 인정머리가 없어—바로 그게 문제다? 너한테 하루 이틀이 뭐 대수냐? 지금 어디로 가는 거냐—베네치아냐? 네 베네치아야 이틀쯤 더 기다려 줄 게다. 알료샤를 보낼까도 했다만, 이런 일에 알료샤가 무슨 소용이겠느냐? 너를 보내는 건 다름 아니라 네가 영리한 녀석이라서다. 내가 그걸 모를 성싶으냐? 너는 목재에 대해선 아무것도 모르지만, 보는 눈이 있어. 필요한 건 그저 그자가 진심인지 아닌지를 알아내는 것뿐이야. 그러니까 말이다, 턱수염을 봐라—턱수염이 떨리면 진심인 줄 알면 된다.\"\n\n\"그럼 아버지가 직접 저를 그 빌어먹을 체르마시냐로 떠밀어 보내시는 거군요?\" 이반이 음험한 미소를 지으며 외쳤다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 그 음험함을 알아채지 못했거나, 아니면 알아채려 들지 않았으나, 미소만은 알아챘다.\n\n\"그럼 가는 게냐, 가는 거야? 내 당장 쪽지를 끄적여 주마.\"\n\n\"갈지 안 갈지 모르겠어요. 모르겠다고요. 가는 길에 정하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리! 당장 정해라. 얘야, 정하라니까! 일을 매듭짓거든 나한테 몇 자 적어 보내라. 신부한테 건네주면 그 사람이 곧장 내게 부쳐 줄 게다. 그것 말고는 너를 더 붙들지 않으마. 베네치아엔 가도 좋아. 신부가 너를 볼로뱌 역까지 도로 데려다줄 말을 내줄 게다.\"\n\n노인은 무척 흡족해했다. 그는 쪽지를 써서 말을 부르러 보냈다. 가벼운 점심이 브랜디와 함께 들어왔다. 표도르 파블로비치는 기분이 좋으면 으레 수다스러워졌으나, 오늘은 자제하는 듯했다. 가령 드미트리에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 이별에 조금도 동요하지 않았고, 사실 무슨 말을 해야 할지 막막해하는 눈치였다. 이반은 이 점을 유독 눈여겨보았다. '내가 지겨워진 게로군.' 그는 생각했다. 다만 아들을 현관 계단까지 배웅하러 나가서야 노인은 부산을 떨기 시작했다. 그는 아들에게 입맞추려 했으나, 이반이 서둘러 손을 내밀어 보란 듯이 입맞춤을 피했다. 그의 아버지는 그것을 대번에 알아채고 즉시 자세를 가다듬었다.\n\n\"자, 잘 가거라, 잘 가!\" 노인은 계단에서 거듭 말했다. \"언제든 또 오겠지? 꼭 와야 한다. 나는 언제든 너를 보면 반가울 게다. 그럼, 그리스도께서 너와 함께하시기를!\"\n\n이반은 마차에 올라탔다.\n\n\"잘 가거라, 이반! 이 아비를 너무 모질게 대하지 말아 다오!\" 아버지가 마지막으로 외쳤다.\n\n온 집안 식구가 작별 인사를 하러 나왔으니—스메르댜코프와 마르파와 그리고리였다. 이반은 그들에게 십 루블씩 나눠 주었다. 그가 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉자, 스메르댜코프가 펄쩍 뛰어올라 무릎 덮개를 매만져 주었다.\n\n\"이봐… 나는 체르마시냐로 가네.\" 이반의 입에서 별안간 이런 말이 튀어나왔다. 전날과 마찬가지로 그 말은 저절로 흘러나오는 듯했고, 그는 또 기이하고 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 오래도록 그것을 기억했다.\n\n\"그러니까 '영리한 사람한테는 언제든 말을 붙여 볼 만하다'는 말이 참말입죠.\" 스메르댜코프가 이반을 의미심장하게 바라보며 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n마차가 굴러갔다. 이반의 영혼 속에는 무엇 하나 분명한 것이 없었으나, 그는 들판을, 언덕을, 나무들을, 환한 하늘 높이 날아가는 기러기 떼를 열심히 둘러보았다. 그러다 별안간 그는 무척 행복해졌다. 그는 마부에게 말을 붙여 보려 했고, 그 농사꾼이 한 대답에 격렬하게 흥미를 느꼈다. 그러나 잠시 뒤 그는 자기가 아무것도 알아듣지 못하고 있으며, 사실은 농사꾼의 대답을 제대로 새겨듣지도 않았음을 깨달았다. 그는 입을 다물었고, 그래도 마음은 흐뭇했다. 공기는 신선하고 맑고 서늘했으며, 하늘은 환했다. 알료샤와 카테리나 이바노브나의 모습이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그러나 그는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 그 다정한 환영들을 향해 살그머니 입김을 불었고, 그러자 그것들은 날아가 버렸다. '저들을 위한 시간은 얼마든지 있어.' 그는 생각했다. 그들은 이내 역에 닿아 말을 갈아매고 볼로뱌를 향해 내달렸다. '어째서 영리한 사람한테는 말을 붙여 볼 만하다는 거지? 그자가 그 말로 무슨 뜻을 비친 걸까?' 그 생각이 별안간 그의 숨통을 옥죄어 오는 듯했다. '그리고 나는 왜 그자한테 체르마시냐로 간다고 말했을까?' 그들은 볼로뱌 역에 닿았다. 이반은 마차에서 내렸고, 마부들이 그를 둘러싸고 체르마시냐까지 십이 베르스타 길의 삯을 두고 흥정을 벌였다. 그는 말을 매라고 일렀다. 그는 역사로 들어가 둘레를 살펴보고, 역장의 아내를 흘끗 보더니, 별안간 입구로 되돌아 나왔다.\n\n\"체르마시냐엔 안 가겠소. 이보게들, 일곱 시까지 철도에 닿기엔 너무 늦었나?\"\n\n\"꼭 맞춰 갈 수 있습니다. 마차를 끌어낼깝쇼?\"\n\n\"당장. 자네들 중에 내일 읍내로 갈 사람 있나?\"\n\n\"있고말고요. 여기 미트리가 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n\"미트리, 내 부탁 하나 들어주겠나? 우리 아버지, 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 댁에 가서, 내가 체르마시냐엔 가지 않았다고 전해 주게. 해 주겠나?\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠습니까. 표도르 파블로비치는 오래전부터 알고 지냈는걸요.\"\n\n\"그리고 이건 자네한테 주는 걸세, 아무래도 그 양반은 자네한테 한 푼도 안 줄 것 같으니 말이야.\" 이반이 유쾌하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그야 틀림없이 안 주실 겁니다.\" 미탸도 웃었다. \"고맙습니다, 나리. 꼭 전해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n일곱 시에 이반은 기차에 올라 모스크바로 떠났다. '지나간 것이여, 사라져라. 나는 옛 세계와 영영 끝장을 보았으니, 거기서 그 어떤 소식도, 그 어떤 메아리도 들려오지 않기를. 새로운 삶으로, 새로운 곳으로, 뒤돌아봄 없이!' 그러나 환희 대신 그의 영혼은 일찍이 평생 겪어 본 적 없는 그런 어둠으로 가득 찼고, 그의 가슴은 그런 비통으로 쓰라렸다. 그는 밤새도록 생각에 잠겼다. 기차는 날 듯이 달렸고, 동틀 녘 모스크바에 가까워질 무렵에야 그는 별안간 명상에서 깨어났다.\n\n\"나는 비열한 놈이다.\" 그는 혼잣말로 속삭였다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 아들을 떠나보내고 나서 흡족해했다. 그 뒤 두 시간 동안 그는 거의 행복하다시피 한 기분으로 앉아 브랜디를 마셨다. 그러나 별안간 집 안의 모두에게 몹시 성가시고 언짢은 일이 벌어졌으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 평정이 단번에 완전히 무너지고 말았다. 스메르댜코프가 무언가를 가지러 지하실에 내려갔다가 계단 꼭대기에서 굴러떨어진 것이다. 다행히 마르파 이그나티예브나가 마당에 있다가 때맞춰 그 소리를 들었다. 그녀는 떨어지는 것은 못 보았으나 그의 비명을—그 기이하고 독특한, 그녀에게 오래도록 익숙한 비명을—발작으로 쓰러지는 간질병자의 비명을 들었다. 발작이 계단을 내려가던 순간에 덮쳐 그가 정신을 잃고 굴러떨어진 것인지, 아니면 굴러떨어진 충격이 발작을 자아낸 것인지는 아무도 알 수 없었으니, 스메르댜코프가 평소 발작을 일으키기 쉬운 자라는 것은 익히 알려진 터였다. 그들은 그가 지하실 계단 밑바닥에서 경련으로 몸부림치며 입에 거품을 무는 것을 발견했다. 처음에는 어딘가—팔이나 다리를—부러뜨리고 다친 게 분명하리라 여겼으나, 마르파 이그나티예브나의 말마따나 \"하느님이 그를 지켜 주셔서\" 그런 일은 전혀 일어나지 않았다. 그러나 그를 지하실에서 끌어내기란 쉽지 않았다. 그들은 이웃에게 도움을 청해 어떻게든 일을 해냈다. 표도르 파블로비치 자신도 이 야단법석에 시종 자리를 지켰다. 그는 거들었으니, 보아하니 놀라고 심란한 기색이었다. 병자는 정신을 차리지 못했고, 경련은 한동안 멎었다가 다시 시작되었으니, 모두가 일 년 전 그가 어쩌다 다락에서 떨어졌을 때와 똑같은 일이 벌어지리라 단정했다. 그들은 그때 그의 머리에 얼음을 얹었던 것을 떠올렸다. 지하실에는 아직 얼음이 남아 있어, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 얼마쯤 가져오게 했다. 저녁에 표도르 파블로비치는 헤르첸슈투베 의사를 부르러 보냈고, 의사는 즉시 도착했다. 그는 더없이 존경받을 만한 노인으로, 이 지방에서 가장 세심하고 양심적인 의사였다. 그는 면밀히 진찰한 끝에, 발작이 매우 격심한 것이어서 중대한 결과를 빚을 수도 있다는 것, 다만 자기, 곧 헤르첸슈투베로서도 아직 그것을 온전히 이해하지는 못한다는 것, 그러나 내일 아침까지 지금 쓰는 처방이 듣지 않으면 다른 것을 한번 시도해 보겠다는 것을 결론지었다. 병자는 별채로, 그리고리와 마르파 이그나티예브나의 방 옆방으로 옮겨졌다.\n\n그러고 나서 표도르 파블로비치는 그날 하나둘 잇따른 불운을 견뎌야 했다. 마르파 이그나티예브나가 저녁을 지었는데, 그 수프는 스메르댜코프 것에 비하면 \"설거지 물이나 진배없었고\", 닭고기는 어찌나 바싹 말랐던지 도무지 씹을 수가 없었다. 주인의 쓰라린, 그래도 받아 마땅한 책망에, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 그 닭이 애당초 아주 늙은 놈이었던 데다 자기는 요리사로 배운 적도 없노라고 대꾸했다. 저녁에는 표도르 파블로비치에게 또 다른 골칫거리가 기다리고 있었으니, 지난 사흘간 몸이 성치 않던 그리고리가 요통으로 아주 드러눕고 말았다는 전갈이 온 것이다. 표도르 파블로비치는 되도록 일찍 차를 마저 마시고는 집 안에 홀로 틀어박혔다. 그는 끔찍한 흥분과 초조에 휩싸여 있었다. 그날 저녁 그는 그루셴카가 오리라는 것을 거의 확실한 일로 점치고 있었다. 그날 아침 그는 스메르댜코프에게서 \"그 여자가 틀림없이 오겠다고 약속했다\"는 다짐을 받아 둔 터였다. 이 구제 불능의 노인은 흥분으로 심장이 두근거렸다. 그는 텅 빈 방들을 오르내리며 귀를 기울였다. 잔뜩 경계 태세를 갖추어야 했다. 드미트리가 어딘가에서 그 여자를 노리고 지키고 있을지도 모르니, 그녀가 창을 두드리면 (스메르댜코프가 이틀 전에 어디를 어떻게 두드리라고 일러 두었다고 알려 온 터였다) 즉시 문을 열어 주어야 했다. 그녀가 통로에 한순간이라도 머물러서는 안 되었으니, 행여—그런 일이 있어서는 안 되겠지만!—그녀가 겁을 먹고 달아날까 두려웠던 것이다. 표도르 파블로비치는 궁리할 것이 많았으나, 그의 심장이 이토록 음탕한 희망에 흠뻑 젖어 본 적은 일찍이 없었다. 이번에야말로 그는 그녀가 오리라고 거의 확실하게 말할 수 있었던 것이다!\n* 베르스타: 옛 러시아의 거리 단위로, 약 1.067킬로미터에 해당한다.\n\n# Book VI. The Russian Monk Chapter I. Father Zossima And His Visitors\n\n불안과 아릿한 아픔을 안은 채 알료샤가 장로의 암자로 들어섰을 때, 그는 거의 놀라움에 사로잡혀 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 마지막 숨을 헐떡이며, 어쩌면 정신마저 잃었으리라 두려워했던 병자 대신, 그는 장로가 의자에 일어나 앉아 있는 모습을 보았는데, 비록 쇠약하고 기진해 있었으나 그 얼굴은 밝고 명랑했으며, 문병객들에 둘러싸여 조용하고도 기쁨에 찬 대화를 나누고 있었다. 그러나 그가 자리에서 일어난 것은 알료샤가 도착하기 겨우 십오 분 전이었다. 문병객들은 그보다 일찍 암자에 모여들어 그가 깨어나기를 기다리고 있었으니, 파이시 신부에게서 \"스승께서는 일어나실 것이며, 아침에 친히 약속하신 대로 마음으로 아끼는 이들과 다시 한 번 이야기를 나누실 것\"이라는 더없이 확고한 보증을 받았던 것이다. 이 약속을, 아니 죽어 가는 장로의 한마디 한마디를 파이시 신부는 절대적으로 믿었다. 설령 그가 의식을 잃은 장로를 보았더라도, 마지막 숨을 거두는 모습을 목격했더라도, 그러면서도 장로에게서 다시 일어나 작별을 고하겠노라는 약속을 받았더라면, 그는 어쩌면 죽음조차 믿지 않고 도리어 죽은 이가 되살아나 그 약속을 지키리라 기대했을 것이다. 아침에 잠자리에 들면서 조시마 장로는 그에게 분명히 일러두었다. \"내 마음의 사랑하는 이여, 나는 그대와 한 번 더 이야기를 나누는 기쁨 없이는 죽지 않을 것이오. 그대의 그리운 얼굴을 한 번 더 바라보고, 그대에게 또 한 번 내 마음을 쏟아 놓겠소.\" 조시마 장로와 아마도 마지막이 될 이 대화를 위해 모여든 수도사들은 모두 여러 해 동안 그의 헌신적인 벗이었다. 그들은 모두 넷이었으니, 이오시프 신부와 파이시 신부, 그리고 암자의 관리인인 미하일 신부였는데, 미하일 신부는 그리 늙지도 않았고 학식과도 거리가 먼 사람이었다. 그는 미천한 가문에서 태어나 의지가 굳고 신앙이 흔들림 없었으며, 겉모습은 준엄했으나 속으로는 깊은 다정함을 품고 있었는데, 다만 그것이 거의 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 분명히 감추려 들었다. 넷째인 안핌 신부는 더없이 가난한 농민 출신의, 아주 늙고 보잘것없는 작은 수도사였다. 그는 거의 글을 읽지 못했고, 무척 말이 없어 누구와도 좀처럼 이야기를 나누지 않았다. 그는 겸손한 이들 가운데서도 가장 겸손했으며, 자기 지력의 한계를 넘어서는 무언가 위대하고 두려운 것에 겁먹은 듯한 모습이었다. 조시마 장로는 이 소심한 사람에게 깊은 애정을 품고 언제나 각별한 존경으로 대했는데, 비록 그와 함께 여러 해 동안 거룩한 러시아 땅을 떠돌아다녔음에도, 어쩌면 그가 알던 누구에게보다도 이 사람에게 가장 적은 말을 건넸을 것이다. 그것은 아주 오래전, 사십 년 전의 일이었으니, 조시마 장로가 코스트로마의 가난하고 작은 수도원에서 수도사로서의 삶을 처음 시작했을 무렵, 그리고 그 직후 가난한 수도원을 위해 희사금을 모으러 떠나는 안핌 신부의 순례길에 동행했을 무렵의 일이었다.\n\n일행 모두는 침실에 있었는데, 앞서 말한 대로 침실은 무척 비좁아 거실에서 가져온 의자에 앉은 네 사람이(거기에 서 있는 견습 수도사 포르피리까지 더하면) 조시마 장로를 둘러싸기에도 자리가 모자랄 지경이었다. 이미 날이 어둑해지기 시작했고, 방 안은 등불과 성상 앞의 촛불로 밝혀져 있었다.\n\n알료샤가 어쩔 줄 몰라 문간에 서 있는 것을 보고, 조시마 장로는 기쁜 듯 미소 지으며 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"어서 오너라, 나의 조용한 아이야, 어서 오너라, 사랑하는 이여, 너도 왔구나. 네가 올 줄 알았다.\"\n\n알료샤가 그에게 다가가 땅에 닿도록 절을 올리고 울음을 터뜨렸다. 무언가가 가슴에서 북받쳐 올랐고, 영혼이 떨렸으며, 흐느껴 울고 싶었다.\n\n\"자, 아직은 나를 두고 울지 마라.\" 조시마 장로가 오른손을 그의 머리에 얹으며 미소 지었다. \"보다시피 나는 일어나 앉아 이야기를 하고 있지 않느냐. 어쩌면 나는 앞으로 이십 년은 더 살지도 모른다. 어제 비셰고리예에서 온 그 착하고 마음씨 고운 여인이, 어린 리자베타를 품에 안고 내게 빌어 준 그대로 말이다. 하느님께서 그 어미와 어린 딸 리자베타에게 복을 내리시기를.\" 그는 성호를 그었다. \"포르피리야, 그 여인의 헌금은 내가 일러둔 곳에 가져다주었느냐?\"\n\n그가 말한 것은, 전날 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이 \"나보다 더 가난한 누군가에게\" 주라며 가져온 육십 코페이카였다. 이런 헌금은 언제나 손수 땀 흘려 번 돈으로, 스스로 자청한 속죄의 한 방편으로 바쳐지는 것이었다. 장로는 전날 저녁 포르피리를 한 과부에게 보냈는데, 그 과부는 얼마 전 집이 불타 버린 뒤 아이들을 데리고 동냥을 다니던 처지였다. 포르피리는 일러 준 대로 \"어느 알 수 없는 자선가가 보냈다\"며 돈을 전했노라고 서둘러 대답했다.\n\n\"일어나거라, 사랑하는 아이야.\" 장로가 알료샤에게 말을 이었다. \"너를 좀 보자꾸나. 집에 다녀와 네 형을 만나 보았느냐?\" 그가 형들 가운데 오직 한 사람에 대해서만 그토록 확신에 차고 정확하게 묻는 것이 알료샤에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다. 그런데 그 형이 누구란 말인가? 그렇다면 어쩌면 그는 어제도 오늘도 바로 그 형을 위해 자기를 내보낸 것이리라.\n\n\"형 하나를 만났습니다.\" 알료샤가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 절을 올린 그 맏형 말이다.\"\n\n\"어제는 만났지만 오늘은 찾을 수가 없었습니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"서둘러 그를 찾거라. 내일 다시 가서 서둘러라. 모든 일을 제쳐 두고 서둘러라. 어쩌면 너는 아직 무언가 끔찍한 일을 막을 시간이 있을지도 모른다. 나는 어제 그에게 닥쳐올 크나큰 고난 앞에 절을 올렸느니라.\"\n\n그는 갑자기 입을 다물고 무언가를 곰곰이 헤아리는 듯했다. 그 말은 이상했다. 어제 그 장면을 목격했던 이오시프 신부가 파이시 신부와 눈길을 주고받았다. 알료샤는 묻지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"신부님, 스승님.\" 그는 북받치는 격정을 안고 입을 열었다. \"말씀이 너무 모호하옵니다……. 형에게 닥쳐올 그 고난이란 무엇이옵니까?\"\n\n\"묻지 마라. 나는 어제 무언가 끔찍한 것을 본 듯하구나……. 마치 그의 온 미래가 그의 두 눈에 드러나 있는 것 같았다. 그의 눈빛에 어떤 기색이 어렸는데—그 사람이 스스로를 위해 무엇을 준비하고 있는지를 보고 나는 순식간에 공포에 사로잡혔다. 내 평생에 한두 번, 어느 사람의 얼굴에서 그런 눈빛을 본 적이 있었으니……. 말하자면 그 사람의 미래의 운명을 비추는 눈빛이었고, 슬프게도 그 운명은 그대로 이루어졌다. 나는 너를 그에게 보냈다, 알렉세이야, 네 형제다운 얼굴이 그를 도와주리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 모든 것이, 우리 모두의 운명이 주님께로부터 오는 것이다. '한 알의 밀이 땅에 떨어져 죽지 아니하면 한 알 그대로 있고, 죽으면 많은 열매를 맺느니라.' 그것을 명심하거라. 알렉세이야, 나는 네 얼굴 때문에 여러 번 묵묵히 너를 축복했으니, 그것을 알아 두거라.\" 장로가 부드러운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"이것이 너에 대한 나의 생각이다. 너는 이 담장 밖으로 나가게 되겠지만, 세상 속에서 수도사처럼 살게 될 것이다. 너에게는 적이 많겠으나, 네 원수들조차 너를 사랑할 것이다. 삶은 너에게 많은 불행을 가져다주겠으나, 너는 바로 그 안에서 너의 행복을 찾을 것이며, 삶을 축복하고 또 남들로 하여금 삶을 축복하게 만들 것이니—그것이야말로 무엇보다 중요한 일이다. 자, 이것이 너의 천성이다. 신부들이여, 스승들이여.\" 그는 다정한 미소를 띠며 벗들에게 말을 건넸다. \"나는 오늘에 이르도록 이 젊은이의 얼굴이 어찌하여 내게 그토록 소중한지 이 아이에게조차 한 번도 말한 적이 없소. 이제 그대들에게 말하겠소. 그의 얼굴은 내게 말하자면 하나의 추억이자 하나의 예언이었소. 내 삶의 여명기, 내가 아이였을 때 내게는 열일곱 나이에 내 눈앞에서 죽은 형이 있었소. 그리고 훗날 살아가는 동안 나는 차츰 확신하게 되었으니, 그 형이 나를 위해 높은 곳에서 내려온 인도이자 표징이었다는 것이오. 만일 그가 내 삶에 나타나지 않았더라면, 적어도 내 짐작으로는, 나는 아마 결코 수도사가 되어 이 귀한 길에 들어서지 못했을 것이오. 그는 내 어린 시절에 처음 내게 나타났는데, 이제 내 순례의 끝자락에 이르러 다시금 내게 찾아온 듯하오. 신부들이여, 스승들이여, 참으로 놀라운 일은, 얼굴이 그다지 닮은 것도 아닌 이 알렉세이가 내게는 영적으로 그토록 그를 닮은 듯이 여겨져, 나는 여러 번 이 아이를 그 젊은이, 곧 내 순례의 끝자락에 하나의 일깨움이자 영감으로서 신비로이 내게 되돌아온 내 형으로 착각했다는 것이오. 그리하여 나는 내 안의 이토록 이상한 꿈에 정녕 놀라곤 했소. 포르피리야, 이 말을 듣고 있느냐?\" 그는 자기 시중을 드는 견습 수도사에게 몸을 돌렸다. \"여러 번 나는 네 얼굴에서, 내가 너보다 알렉세이를 더 사랑한다는 데 대한 서운함 같은 기색을 보았다. 이제 너는 그것이 어찌하여 그러했는지 알았을 것이다. 그러나 나는 너 또한 사랑한다, 그것을 알아 두거라, 그리고 여러 번 나는 너의 서운함에 마음 아파했다. 사랑하는 벗들이여, 나는 그대들에게 그 젊은이, 내 형에 대해 이야기하고 싶소, 내 삶에서 그보다 더 소중하고, 더 뜻깊고, 더 가슴 저미는 존재는 없었기 때문이오. 내 마음은 다정함으로 가득하고, 나는 지금 이 순간 내 온 생애를 마치 다시 한 번 살아 내는 것처럼 바라보고 있소.\"\n\n여기서 나는 이 점을 짚어 두어야겠다. 조시마 장로가 생애 마지막 날 자기를 찾아온 벗들과 나눈 이 마지막 대화는 일부가 글로 남아 보존되어 있다. 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 스승이 죽은 뒤 얼마간 지나 기억을 더듬어 적어 둔 것이다. 그러나 이것이 그때 오간 대화만을 옮긴 것인지, 아니면 그가 스승과 나눈 예전 대화들의 일부에 대한 자기 기록을 거기에 보탠 것인지, 나로서는 단정할 수 없다. 그의 기록에서는 조시마 장로의 이야기가 끊김 없이 이어져, 마치 그가 자기 생애를 한 편의 이야기 형식으로 벗들에게 들려준 것처럼 되어 있으나, 다른 증언들로 미루어 보건대 그날 저녁의 대화가 여럿이 함께 나눈 것이었음에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 손님들이 조시마 장로의 말을 자주 끊지는 않았다 해도, 그들 역시 이야기를 했고 어쩌면 스스로 무언가를 들려주기까지 했을 것이다. 게다가 조시마 장로가 끊김 없는 이야기를 이어 갈 수는 없었을 것이니, 그는 때로 숨을 헐떡였고 목소리가 잦아들었으며, 심지어 침상에 누워 쉬기까지 했기 때문이다. 다만 잠이 든 것은 아니었고 문병객들도 자리를 떠나지 않았다. 한두 번은 파이시 신부가 복음서를 읽음으로써 대화가 중단되었다. 또한 주목할 만한 점은, 그들 가운데 누구도 그가 그날 밤에 죽으리라고는 생각하지 않았다는 것이니, 그의 생애의 그 저녁, 낮에 깊이 잠든 뒤 그는 돌연 새로운 기력을 얻은 듯했고, 그 기력이 이 긴 대화 내내 그를 지탱해 주었기 때문이다. 그것은 마치 그에게 놀라운 활력을 안겨 준 사랑의 마지막 안간힘과도 같았다. 그러나 그것은 잠시뿐이었으니, 그의 생명은 곧바로 끊기고 말았던 것이다……. 그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하자. 지금은 다만, 내가 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 남긴 기록에만 의지하기로 했다는 것만 덧붙여 두겠다. 그편이 더 짧고 또 그리 지루하지 않을 것이다. 물론 거듭 말하지만, 알료샤가 예전 대화들에서 적잖은 것을 가져와 거기에 보탰음은 분명하지만 말이다.\n\n고인이 된 사제이자 수도사인 조시마 장로의 생애에 대한 기록, 그 자신의 말에서 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 받아 적다.\n\n전기적 기록\n(가) 조시마 장로의 형\n\n사랑하는 신부들이여, 스승들이여, 나는 북쪽 어느 먼 지방의 도시 V에서 태어났소. 내 아버지는 가문으로는 귀족이었으나 그리 대단하거나 높은 지위에 있지는 않았소. 그는 내가 겨우 두 살 때 세상을 떠나서, 나는 그를 전혀 기억하지 못하오. 그는 내 어머니에게 나무로 지은 작은 집 한 채와, 크지는 않으나 어머니와 자식들이 그럭저럭 편히 지내기에는 넉넉한 재산을 남겨 주었소. 우리는 둘이었으니, 나보다 여덟 살 위인 형 마르켈과 나였소. 형은 성미가 급하고 곧잘 짜증을 냈으나 마음씨는 따뜻했고 결코 빈정대는 법이 없었소. 그는 놀라우리만치 말이 없었는데, 특히 집에서 나와 어머니와 하인들 앞에서 그러했소. 학교에서는 성적이 좋았으나 동무들과는 잘 어울리지 못했소. 그래도 다투는 일은 없었다고, 적어도 어머니가 내게 그렇게 일러 주었소. 죽기 여섯 달 전, 열일곱 살이던 그는 자유사상 때문에 모스크바에서 우리 도시로 유배되어 그곳에서 외톨이로 지내던 한 정치적 유배자와 가까워졌소. 그는 대학에서 철학으로 두각을 나타낸 훌륭한 학자였소. 무슨 까닭에선지 그가 마르켈을 마음에 들어 해서, 형에게 자기를 보러 오라고 청하곤 했소. 형은 그해 겨울 내내 저녁마다 그와 함께 시간을 보냈는데, 마침내 그 유배자가 권세 있는 벗들을 둔 덕에 자기 청원으로 페테르부르크에 불려 가 다시 관직에 오르게 될 때까지 그러했소.\n\n마침 사순절의 시작이었는데, 마르켈은 금식을 하려 들지 않았소. 그는 무례하게 그것을 비웃었소. \"그딴 건 죄다 어리석은 헛소리야, 하느님 따위는 없어.\" 그가 이렇게 말해 어머니와 하인들을, 그리고 나까지 소스라치게 했소. 나는 겨우 아홉 살이었으나, 그런 말을 듣고 나 역시 아연실색했기 때문이오. 우리 집에는 하인이 넷 있었는데, 모두 농노였소. 나는 어머니가 그 넷 중 하나인, 다리를 절고 나이 든 요리사 아피미야를 종이돈 육십 루블에 팔고 그 자리에 자유로운 하인을 들이던 일을 기억하오.\n\n사순절 여섯째 주에, 본래 몸이 튼튼하지 못하고 폐병 기가 있던 형이 병으로 자리에 누웠소. 그는 키가 컸으나 야위고 가냘퍼 보였으며, 더없이 호감 가는 얼굴이었소. 짐작건대 그는 감기에 걸렸던 듯한데, 어쨌든 왕진을 온 의사는 곧 어머니에게 그것이 급성 폐병이며 봄을 넘기지 못할 것이라고 속삭였소. 어머니는 울기 시작했으나, 형이 놀랄까 조심하며 그에게 아직 몸을 움직일 수 있을 때 성당에 가서 고해하고 성체를 받으라고 간곡히 청했소. 이 말에 형은 화를 내며 성당에 대해 불경한 말을 내뱉었소. 그러나 그는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼소. 그는 자기가 중병에 걸렸으며, 그래서 어머니가 고해하고 성체를 받으라 애원하는 것임을 단번에 알아차렸던 것이오. 사실 그는 오래전부터 자기 몸이 영 좋지 못하다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 일 년 전에는 식탁에서 어머니와 내게 태연히 이런 말을 한 적이 있었소. \"내 목숨은 당신들 곁에 오래 머물지 못할 거예요, 한 해도 더 못 살지 모르죠.\" 그것이 이제는 하나의 예언처럼 여겨졌소.\n\n사흘이 지나 성주간이 다가왔소. 그리고 화요일 아침, 형이 성당에 다니기 시작했소. \"이건 오로지 어머니를 위해서, 어머니를 기쁘게 하고 위로해 드리려고 하는 거예요.\" 그가 말했소. 어머니는 기쁨과 슬픔으로 울었소. '저 애에게 저런 변화가 일었다면, 그 끝이 가까운 게 틀림없어' 하고 어머니는 생각했소. 그러나 그는 오래 성당에 다니지 못하고 자리에 눕고 말아서, 결국 집에서 고해하고 성체를 받아야 했소.\n\n그해는 부활절이 늦었는데, 날들은 밝고 화창했으며 향기로 가득했소. 나는 형이 밤새 기침을 하며 잠을 설치곤 했던 일을 기억하오. 그러나 아침이면 그는 옷을 차려입고 안락의자에 일어나 앉으려 애썼소. 내가 그를 기억하는 모습이 바로 그러하니, 그는 병중인데도 다정하고 부드럽게 미소 지으며, 얼굴은 밝고 기쁨에 차서 앉아 있었소. 그에게 놀라운 변화가 찾아왔으니, 그 정신이 마치 새로이 거듭난 듯했소. 늙은 유모가 들어와 말하곤 했소. \"거룩한 성상 앞에 등불을 켜게 해 다오, 우리 도련님.\" 예전 같으면 그는 그것을 허락하지 않고 불을 꺼 버렸을 것이오.\n\n\"켜요, 켜요, 할멈, 내가 그걸 못 하게 했던 건 못된 짓이었어요. 할멈은 등불을 켜며 기도하고, 나는 할멈을 보는 기쁨으로 기도하니까요. 그러니 우리는 같은 하느님께 기도하는 거예요.\"\n\n그 말들이 우리에게는 이상하게 들려서, 어머니는 제 방으로 가 울곤 했소. 그러나 형에게 들어갈 때면 눈물을 닦고 밝은 얼굴을 지었소. \"어머니, 울지 마세요, 사랑하는 어머니.\" 형이 말하곤 했소. \"나는 아직 오래 살 거예요, 어머니와 함께 오래 기뻐할 거예요. 삶이란 즐겁고 기쁜 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 사랑하는 아들아, 밤이면 열에 들떠 제 몸을 갈가리 찢기라도 할 듯 기침을 해 대면서 어떻게 기쁨을 이야기하느냐.\"\n\n\"울지 마세요, 어머니.\" 그가 대답하곤 했소. \"삶은 낙원이에요, 우리는 모두 낙원에 있어요, 다만 우리가 그걸 보려 들지 않을 뿐이죠. 우리가 보려고만 하면 바로 다음 날에 이 땅 위에 천국이 펼쳐질 거예요.\"\n\n다들 그의 말에 놀랐으니, 그가 어찌나 이상하고 단호하게 말하던지 우리 모두는 마음이 움직여 울었소. 벗들이 우리를 보러 왔소. \"사랑하는 이들이여.\" 그가 그들에게 말하곤 했소. \"내가 무엇을 했기에 당신들이 나를 이토록 사랑해 주는 거죠, 어떻게 나 같은 사람을 사랑할 수 있죠, 그리고 어찌하여 나는 전에 그걸 알지 못했죠, 그걸 귀하게 여기지 못했죠?\"\n\n하인들이 그에게 들어오면 그는 끊임없이 말하곤 했소. \"사랑하고 친절한 이들이여, 어찌하여 당신들은 나를 위해 이토록 애쓰는 거죠, 내가 시중받을 자격이 있나요? 하느님의 뜻으로 내가 살게 된다면, 나는 당신들의 시중을 들 거예요. 사람은 모두 서로의 시중을 들어야 하니까요.\"\n\n어머니는 그 말을 들으며 고개를 저었소. \"사랑하는 아들아, 그건 네 병 때문에 그런 말을 하는 거란다.\"\n\n\"어머니, 사랑하는 어머니.\" 그가 말하곤 했소. \"하인과 주인은 있어야 해요, 하지만 그렇다면 나는 내 하인들의 하인이 되겠어요, 그들이 내게 하듯 똑같이요. 그리고 또 한 가지, 어머니, 우리 하나하나가 모든 사람에게 죄를 지었어요, 그중에서도 내가 누구보다 더 그래요.\"\n\n어머니는 그 말에 정말로 미소를 지었으니, 눈물 속에서 미소 지었소. \"아니, 어떻게 네가 모든 사람에게, 그것도 누구보다 더 많이 죄를 지었단 말이냐? 강도나 살인자라면 그렇겠지만, 네가 무슨 죄를 지었기에 스스로를 누구보다 더 죄 많다고 여기느냐?\"\n\n\"어머니, 내 작은 심장 같은 어머니.\" 그가 말했소(그는 그 무렵부터 이런 이상하고 다정스러운 말을 쓰기 시작했소). \"내 작은 심장 같은 어머니, 나의 기쁨이여, 내 말을 믿어 주세요, 우리 하나하나가 참으로 모든 사람에게, 모든 일에 대해 책임이 있어요. 어떻게 그걸 어머니께 설명해야 할지 모르겠지만, 나는 그것이 그러하다고 느껴요, 아프도록 절절히요. 그런데 우리는 어찌하여 그토록 화를 내며, 그것을 알지도 못한 채 살아왔을까요?\"\n\n이렇게 그는 날마다 일어나, 더욱더 다정하고 기쁘며 사랑으로 충만해졌소. 아이젠슈미트라는 늙은 독일인 의사가 왕진을 오면 형이 농담조로 물었소.\n\n\"그래, 의사 선생, 나에게 이 세상의 하루가 더 남아 있나요?\"\n\n\"앞으로 여러 날을 더 사실 겁니다.\" 의사가 대답하곤 했소. \"여러 달, 여러 해도요.\"\n\n\"여러 달, 여러 해라니요!\" 형은 외치곤 했소. \"어찌하여 날수를 헤아리죠? 사람이 온갖 행복을 깨닫는 데는 하루로 족해요. 사랑하는 이들이여, 어찌하여 우리는 다투고, 서로를 누르려 하며, 서로에게 앙심을 품을까요? 곧장 정원으로 나가요, 그곳을 거닐고 노닐며, 사랑하고, 서로를 귀히 여기고, 입 맞추며, 삶을 찬미해요.\"\n\n\"댁의 아드님은 오래가지 못할 겁니다.\" 의사는 문까지 배웅하는 어머니에게 말했소. \"병이 뇌까지 침범하고 있어요.\"\n\n그의 방 창문은 정원으로 나 있었는데, 우리 정원은 그늘이 짙고, 막 움이 트기 시작한 오래된 나무들이 서 있었소. 봄의 첫 새들이 가지 사이를 옮겨 다니며 짹짹거리고 창가에서 노래했소. 그것들을 바라보며 감탄하던 형은 돌연 새들에게마저 용서를 빌기 시작했소. \"하늘의 새들이여, 행복한 새들이여, 나를 용서해 다오, 나는 너희에게도 죄를 지었으니.\" 그때 우리 가운데 누구도 그 말을 이해하지 못했으나, 형은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸소. \"그래.\" 그가 말했소. \"내 둘레에는 하느님의 영광이 이토록 가득했어. 새들, 나무들, 풀밭, 하늘이. 그런데 오직 나만이 수치 속에 살며 그 모든 것을 더럽혔고, 그 아름다움과 영광을 알아차리지 못했어.\"\n\n\"너는 너무 많은 죄를 스스로 짊어지는구나.\" 어머니는 울며 말하곤 했소.\n\n\"어머니, 사랑하는 어머니, 나는 슬퍼서가 아니라 기뻐서 우는 거예요. 어머니께 설명할 수는 없지만, 나는 그들 앞에 나를 낮추는 게 좋아요, 그들을 어떻게 충분히 사랑해야 할지 모르겠기 때문이에요. 내가 모든 이에게 죄를 지었어도, 그래도 모두가 나를 용서해 주니, 그것이 바로 천국이에요. 나는 지금 천국에 있는 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n그 밖에도 내가 기억하지 못하는 많은 말이 있었소. 한번은 아무도 없을 때 내가 그의 방에 들어갔던 일을 기억하오. 밝은 저녁이었고, 해가 지고 있어 온 방이 환히 물들어 있었소. 그가 나를 손짓해 불러 나는 그에게 다가갔소. 그는 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 다정하고 사랑스러운 눈길로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았소. 그는 한동안 아무 말 없이 그저 그렇게 나를 바라보기만 했소.\n\n\"자.\" 그가 말했소. \"이제 나가서 놀거라, 나를 위해서도 삶을 누리거라.\"\n\n그래서 나는 밖으로 나가 놀러 달려갔소. 그리고 그 뒤로 살아가는 동안 여러 번, 그가 나를 위해서도 삶을 누리라고 일러 준 일을 눈물마저 흘리며 떠올렸소. 그가 남긴 놀랍고 아름다운 말은 그 밖에도 많았으나, 그때 우리는 그것을 이해하지 못했소. 그는 부활절 셋째 주에 죽었소. 그는 말을 할 수는 없었으나 의식만은 또렷했고, 마지막 순간까지 변함이 없었소. 그는 행복해 보였고, 두 눈은 빛을 내며 우리를 찾았으며, 우리에게 미소 짓고 우리를 손짓해 불렀소. 그의 죽음에 대해서는 온 도시에서도 적잖은 이야기가 오갔소. 나는 그때 이 모든 일에 깊은 인상을 받았으나, 그렇게까지 깊지는 않았소. 그래도 그의 장례에서 나는 많이 울었소. 나는 그때 어렸으니, 어린아이였으나, 이 모든 일에 대한 오래도록 남는 인상, 숨겨진 감정이 내 마음에 머물러, 때가 오면 솟아올라 응답할 채비를 갖추고 있었소. 그리고 정녕 그렇게 되었소.\n\n(나) 조시마 장로의 생애에서 성경에 대하여\n\n나는 어머니와 단둘이 남게 되었소. 어머니의 벗들이 다른 부모들처럼 나를 페테르부르크로 보내라고 권하기 시작했소. \"이제 아드님이 하나뿐이고,\" 그들이 말했소. \"수입도 그만하니, 이 아이를 여기 붙들어 두면 어쩌면 빛나는 앞길을 빼앗는 셈이 될 거예요.\" 그들은 내가 페테르부르크의 유년사관학교로 보내져, 나중에 황실 근위대에 들어가게 하라고 일렀소. 어머니는 오래도록 망설였으니, 외아들과 헤어지는 일은 끔찍했기 때문이오. 그러나 마침내 어머니는 그것을 결심했소, 비록 숱한 눈물을 흘리기는 했으나, 그것이 내 행복을 위한 일이라 믿으면서 말이오. 어머니는 나를 페테르부르크로 데려가 유년사관학교에 넣었고, 나는 다시는 어머니를 보지 못했소. 어머니 또한 삼 년 뒤에 세상을 떠났기 때문이오. 어머니는 그 삼 년을 우리 둘을 위해 슬퍼하고 비통해하며 보냈소.\n\n내 어린 시절의 집에서 나는 소중한 추억밖에는 아무것도 가져오지 못했으니, 한 사람의 첫 보금자리에서 보낸 어린 시절의 추억보다 더 소중한 추억은 없기 때문이오. 그리고 가정에 조금이나마 사랑과 화목이 있다면 거의 언제나 그러하오. 참으로, 소중한 추억은 좋지 못한 가정에서도 남을 수 있으니, 다만 마음이 그 소중한 것을 찾아낼 줄 알기만 한다면 말이오. 내 집에 대한 추억과 더불어 나는 성경에 대한 추억도 헤아리오. 어린아이였던 나는 집에서 그것을 무척이나 읽고 싶어 했소. 그때 내게는 빼어난 그림이 곁들여진 성경 역사책이 있었는데, 『구약과 신약의 백네 가지 이야기』라는 책으로, 나는 그것으로 글 읽기를 배웠소. 나는 지금도 그 책을 내 서가에 두고, 지난날의 소중한 유물로 간직하고 있소. 그러나 글을 깨치기도 전, 나는 여덟 살 적에 처음으로 신심에 마음이 움직였던 일을 기억하오. 부활절 전 월요일에 어머니가 나만 데리고 미사에 갔소(그때 형이 어디 있었는지는 기억나지 않소). 화창한 날이었고, 나는 지금도, 마치 눈앞에 보이는 듯이, 향로에서 향연이 피어올라 위로 부드럽게 떠다니다가 머리 위 둥근 천장에서 작은 창으로 흘러드는 햇살과 솟구치는 물결처럼 뒤섞이던 모습을 기억하오. 그 광경에 내 마음이 흔들렸고, 나는 평생 처음으로 하느님 말씀의 씨앗을 의식하며 내 마음에 받아들였소. 한 젊은이가 커다란 책을 들고 성당 한가운데로 나왔는데, 책이 어찌나 컸던지 그때 나는 그가 그것을 들고 있기조차 버거우리라 여겼소. 그는 그것을 독서대에 올려놓고 펼쳐 읽기 시작했고, 그러자 돌연 나는 난생처음으로 하느님의 성당에서 읽히는 무언가를 알아들었소. 우스 땅에 한 사람이 살았는데, 의롭고 하느님을 두려워하는 이로, 큰 재물을 지녀 낙타도 숱하게, 양과 나귀도 숱하게 거느렸으며, 그의 자식들은 잔치를 벌였고, 그는 그들을 무척 사랑하여 그들을 위해 기도했소. \"혹시 내 아들들이 잔치를 벌이며 죄를 지었을지도 모른다.\" 그런데 마귀가 하느님의 아들들과 함께 주님 앞에 나아와, 자기가 땅 위와 땅 아래를 두루 오르내렸노라고 주님께 아뢰었소. \"네가 내 종 욥을 눈여겨보았느냐?\" 하느님이 그에게 물으셨소. 그리고 하느님은 자신의 위대하고 거룩한 종을 가리키며 마귀에게 자랑하셨소. 그러자 마귀는 하느님의 말씀을 비웃었소. \"그를 제게 넘기소서, 그러면 당신의 종이 당신을 거슬러 원망하고 당신의 이름을 저주하는 것을 보시리이다.\" 그리하여 하느님은 그토록 사랑하시던 의인을 마귀에게 넘기셨소. 그러자 마귀는 마치 하늘에서 떨어진 벼락처럼 느닷없이 그의 자식들과 가축을 쳐 죽이고 그의 재물을 흩어 버렸소. 그러자 욥은 자기 겉옷을 찢고 땅에 엎드려 큰 소리로 부르짖었소. \"내가 모태에서 알몸으로 나왔으니 또한 알몸으로 그리로 돌아가리이다. 주신 이도 주님이시요 거두신 이도 주님이시니, 주님의 이름이 영원무궁토록 찬송받으시기를 비나이다.\"\n\n신부들이여, 스승들이여, 지금 내 눈물을 용서하시오. 내 온 어린 시절이 다시금 내 앞에 떠올라, 나는 지금 그때 그러했듯 숨 쉬고 있으니, 여덟 살 어린아이의 가슴으로 숨 쉬며, 그때처럼 경외와 경탄과 기쁨을 느끼고 있소. 그때 낙타들이 내 상상을 사로잡았고, 하느님과 그렇게 이야기를 나누던 사탄이, 그리고 제 종을 파멸로 넘기신 하느님이, 또 \"당신께서 저를 벌하셔도 당신의 이름이 찬송받으시기를\" 하고 부르짖던 그분의 종이, 그리고 뒤이어 성당 안의 부드럽고 감미로운 노랫소리가, \"내 기도가 당신 앞에 향연처럼 피어오르게 하소서\" 하던 그 노래가, 또다시 사제의 향로에서 피어오르는 향연과 무릎 꿇음과 기도가 내 마음을 사로잡았소. 그때 이후로—바로 어제도 나는 그것을 집어 들었소—나는 그 거룩한 이야기를 눈물 없이는 한 번도 읽지 못했소. 그리고 그 안에 위대하고 신비로우며 헤아릴 수 없는 것이 얼마나 많은지! 훗날 나는 비웃음과 비난의 말, 오만한 말을 들었소. \"어찌하여 하느님은 마귀의 심심풀이를 위해 가장 사랑하는 성도를 넘기시고, 그에게서 자식들을 앗아 가시며, 그를 모진 종기로 치시어 그가 사금파리로 제 상처의 고름을 긁어내게 하셨단 말인가—그것도 마귀에게 자랑하려는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없이! '보아라, 내 성도가 나를 위해 어떤 고난을 견디는지를.'\" 그러나 그 위대함은 바로 그것이 하나의 신비라는 데 있으니—덧없이 스러지는 지상의 모습과 영원한 진리가 그 안에서 한데 모이는 것이오. 지상의 진리 앞에서 영원한 진리가 이루어지는 것이오. 창조주께서는, 마치 창조의 첫날들에 날마다 그 끝에 \"내가 지은 것이 좋도다\" 하고 찬미하셨던 그대로, 욥을 굽어보시며 다시금 자신의 피조물을 찬미하시오. 그리고 욥은 주님을 찬미함으로써 그분뿐 아니라 그분의 온 피조물을 대대손손, 영원무궁토록 섬기는 것이니, 그것을 위해 그가 정해졌기 때문이오. 오, 맙소사, 이 얼마나 위대한 책이며, 그 안에 얼마나 큰 가르침이 담겨 있는지! 성경이란 얼마나 위대한 책이며, 얼마나 큰 기적이며, 그것과 더불어 사람에게 얼마나 큰 힘이 주어지는지! 그것은 세상과 사람과 인간 본성을 떠 낸 거푸집과도 같아서, 모든 것이 그 안에 있고, 온 시대를 위한 모든 것의 법이 그 안에 있소. 그리고 얼마나 많은 신비가 그 안에서 풀리고 드러나는지! 하느님은 욥을 다시 일으키시고 다시 재물을 주시오. 여러 해가 지나 그에게 다른 자식들이 생기고 그는 그들을 사랑하오. 그러나 처음의 자식들이 더는 없는데, 그들을 잃었는데, 어찌 그가 그 새 자식들을 사랑할 수 있단 말이오? 그들을 떠올리면서 어찌 그가 그 새 자식들과 더불어 온전히 행복할 수 있단 말이오, 그 새 자식들이 아무리 사랑스럽다 한들? 그러나 그는 그럴 수 있었소, 그럴 수 있었소. 묵은 슬픔이 차츰 고요하고 다정한 기쁨으로 옮겨 가는 것, 그것이야말로 인간 삶의 위대한 신비요. 젊음의 들끓는 피를 대신해 노년의 잔잔한 평온이 찾아드는 것이오. 나는 날마다 떠오르는 해를 축복하고, 예전처럼 내 마음은 그것을 맞으러 노래하오. 그러나 이제는 그것이 저물 때를, 그 길게 비껴드는 빛살과 그것과 함께 찾아오는 부드럽고 다정스럽고 정겨운 추억들을, 내 길고 행복한 생애 전체에서 떠오르는 그리운 모습들을 더욱 사랑하오—그리고 그 모든 것 위로 부드럽게 하고, 화해시키고, 용서하는 하느님의 진리가 깃들어 있소! 내 생명이 끝나 가고 있음을, 나는 잘 알고 있소. 그러나 내게 남은 하루하루마다 나는 내 지상의 삶이 어떤 새롭고 무한하며 알 수 없는 삶, 다가오는 그 삶과 맞닿아 있음을 느끼니, 그 삶의 가까움이 내 영혼을 환희로 떨게 하고, 내 정신을 타오르게 하며, 내 마음을 기쁨으로 울게 하오.\n\n벗들이여, 스승들이여, 나는 한 번 아니라 여러 번 들었소, 그리고 요즈음에는 더 자주 들을 수 있으니, 사제들이, 그중에서도 특히 시골 사제들이 사방에서 자기들의 비참한 수입과 굴욕적인 처지를 한탄하고 있다는 것이오. 그들은 노골적으로, 심지어 활자로—나도 직접 읽었소—이르기를, 가진 것이 적은 탓에 백성에게 성경을 가르칠 수 없으며, 루터파나 이단자가 와서 양 떼를 그릇된 길로 이끌어도 자기들은 먹고살 것이 너무 적어 그들이 미혹하게 내버려 둔다고 하오. 주님께서 그들에게 그토록 소중한 양식을 늘려 주시기를, 그들의 하소연 또한 정당하기에 그러하오. 그러나 참으로 내가 말하노니, 이 일에 누군가 책임이 있다면 그 절반은 우리의 잘못이오. 그에게 시간이 모자랄 수 있고, 줄곧 일과 예배에 짓눌려 있다고 진정 말할 수 있으나, 그래도 늘 그런 것은 아니니, 그에게도 한 주에 한 시간쯤은 하느님을 기억할 짬이 있소. 또 그가 일 년 내내 쉼 없이 일하는 것도 아니오. 한 주에 한 번, 저녁의 어느 한 시간, 처음에는 아이들만이라도 그의 둘레에 모아 보게 하시오—아비들이 그 소식을 듣고 그들 또한 모여들기 시작할 것이오. 이를 위해 따로 회관을 지을 필요도 없으니, 그저 그들을 자기 오두막으로 들이게 하시오. 그들이 그의 오두막을 망가뜨리지 않을 것이니, 그저 한 시간 거기 있을 뿐이오. 그로 하여금 그 책을 펼쳐, 거창한 말이나 거만함 없이, 그들을 깔보는 기색 없이, 부드럽고 다정하게, 그들에게 읽어 주는 것을 기뻐하며, 그들이 귀 기울여 듣는 것을 기뻐하며, 그 자신이 그 말씀을 사랑하면서 읽기 시작하게 하시오. 다만 이따금 멈추어 농민들이 알아듣지 못하는 말들을 풀이해 주면서 말이오. 염려치 마시오, 그들은 모든 것을 알아들을 것이니, 정교를 믿는 마음은 모든 것을 알아들을 것이오! 그로 하여금 아브라함과 사라에 대해, 이사악과 레베카에 대해, 야곱이 어떻게 라반에게로 가서 꿈속에서 주님과 씨름하며 \"이곳은 거룩하도다\" 하고 말했는지를 읽어 주게 하시오—그러면 그는 농민의 경건한 마음에 깊은 자취를 새길 것이오. 특히 아이들에게는, 형들이 어떻게 다정한 소년이자 꿈꾸는 자요 예언자였던 요셉을 종으로 팔아넘기고, 아비에게는 들짐승이 그를 잡아먹었다 이르며 피 묻은 그의 옷을 보였는지를 읽어 주게 하시오. 또 그 형들이 어떻게 나중에 곡식을 구하러 이집트로 갔는지, 그리고 이미 큰 통치자가 된 요셉이, 형들이 알아보지 못하는 가운데 그들을 괴롭히고 추궁하며 아우 베냐민을 붙들어 두었는지를, 그것도 오직 사랑에서 그리했음을 읽어 주게 하시오. \"나는 너희를 사랑하기에, 사랑하기에 너희를 괴롭히노라.\" 그는 형들이 자기를 불타는 사막의 우물가에서 상인들에게 팔아넘기던 일을, 그리고 자기가 두 손을 비틀며 울면서 낯선 땅에 노예로 팔지 말아 달라고 형들에게 애원하던 일을 평생토록 기억했기 때문이오. 그리고 여러 해 만에 다시 그들을 보았을 때 그는 그들을 한없이 사랑했으나, 사랑하기에 그들을 들볶고 괴롭혔소. 그는 마침내 제 가슴의 고통을 견디지 못하고 그들 곁을 떠나, 침상에 몸을 던지고 울었소. 그러고는 눈물을 닦고 기쁜 얼굴로 그들에게 나아가 일렀소. \"형제들이여, 내가 그대들의 아우 요셉이오!\" 그로 하여금 더 나아가, 늙은 야곱이 사랑하는 아들이 아직 살아 있음을 알고 얼마나 행복해했는지, 그리고 그가 어떻게 제 고향을 떠나 이집트로 가서 타향에서 죽으며, 평생토록 그 온순하고 겁 많은 마음속에 신비로이 감추어 두었던 위대한 예언을—그의 후손에게서, 유다에게서 세상의 큰 소망인 메시아요 구세주가 나오리라는 예언을—남겼는지를 읽어 주게 하시오.\n\n신부들이여, 스승들이여, 어린아이처럼 그대들이 진작에 알고 있으며 나보다 백 배는 더 능란하게 가르칠 수 있는 것을 두고 내가 주절거린 것을 용서하시고 노여워 마시오. 나는 다만 환희에 겨워 말할 뿐이니, 내 눈물을 용서하시오, 나는 성경을 사랑하기 때문이오. 하느님의 사제 또한 울게 하시오, 그리하면 그 말을 듣는 이들의 마음이 그에 화답하여 두근거릴 것임을 확신하시오. 그저 아주 작은 씨앗 하나면 족하니—그것을 농민의 마음에 떨어뜨리면 그것은 죽지 않고 평생토록 그의 영혼 속에 살아, 그의 어둠과 죄의 한가운데에 밝은 점처럼, 위대한 일깨움처럼 숨어 있을 것이오. 그리고 많은 가르침이나 풀이가 필요치 않으니, 그는 모든 것을 그저 단순하게 알아들을 것이오. 그대들은 농민들이 알아듣지 못하리라 여기시오? 시험 삼아 아름다운 에스더와 오만한 와스디의 가슴 저미는 이야기를, 혹은 고래 속 요나의 기적 같은 이야기를 읽어 주어 보시오. 우리 주님의 비유들 또한 잊지 마시고, 특히 루카 복음에서 골라 읽으시오(내가 그렇게 했소), 그다음에는 사도행전에서 바울로의 회심을(그것은 무슨 일이 있어도 빼놓아서는 아니 되오), 그리고 『성인전』에서는, 이를테면 하느님의 사람 알렉세이의 생애를, 무엇보다도 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 빼어난, 복된 순교자요 하느님을 본 이집트의 마리아의 생애를 읽어 주시오—그러면 그대들은 이 소박한 이야기들로 그들의 마음에 스며들 것이오. 그대들의 가난에도 불구하고 한 주에 한 시간을, 오직 한 작은 시간을 거기에 바치시오. 그러면 그대들 스스로 보게 되리니, 우리 백성은 너그럽고 고마움을 아는지라, 그대들에게 백배로 갚을 것이오. 자기 사제의 친절과 그에게서 들은 감동적인 말을 기억하여, 그들은 제 스스로 그의 밭일과 집안일을 거들 것이며, 전보다 더 큰 존경으로 그를 대할 것이오—그리하여 그것은 그의 세속적인 형편마저 한결 나아지게 할 것이오. 그 이치가 어찌나 단순한지 때로는 비웃음을 살까 두려워 그것을 말로 옮기기조차 겁이 나오, 그러나 그것이 얼마나 참된지! 하느님을 믿지 않는 자는 하느님의 백성도 믿지 않을 것이오. 하느님의 백성을 믿는 자는 그분의 거룩함 또한 보게 되리니, 설령 그때까지 그것을 믿지 않았다 할지라도 그러하오. 오직 백성과 그들의 미래의 영적인 힘만이, 제 고향 땅에서 스스로를 떼어 낸 우리의 무신론자들을 회개시킬 것이오.\n\n그리고 우리가 본을 보이지 않는다면, 그리스도의 말씀이 무슨 소용이 있겠소? 백성은 하느님의 말씀이 없으면 길을 잃으니, 그 영혼이 말씀을, 그리고 온갖 선한 것을 목말라하기 때문이오.\n\n내 젊은 시절, 오래전, 거의 사십 년 전에, 나는 안핌 신부와 함께 우리 수도원을 위한 기금을 모으며 러시아 곳곳을 두루 돌아다녔는데, 어느 큰 배가 다닐 만한 강가에서 어부 몇과 더불어 하룻밤을 묵었소. 열여덟 살쯤 되어 보이는 잘생긴 농촌 청년 하나가 우리와 어울렸는데, 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 돌아가 강기슭을 따라 한 상인의 거룻배를 끌어야 하는 처지였소. 나는 그가 맑고 다정한 눈으로 앞을 똑바로 바라보는 것을 눈여겨보았소. 밝고 따뜻하며 고요한 칠월의 밤이었고, 너른 강에서 서늘한 안개가 피어올랐으며, 물고기가 첨벙대는 소리가 들리고, 새들은 잠잠했으며, 모든 것이 고요하고 아름다워, 만물이 하느님께 기도하고 있었소. 잠들지 않은 것은 오직 우리 둘, 그 청년과 나뿐이어서, 우리는 하느님의 이 세상의 아름다움과 그 위대한 신비에 대해 이야기를 나누었소. 풀잎 하나하나, 벌레 하나하나, 개미와 황금빛 꿀벌이, 모두 그토록 놀랍게 제 길을 알고 있으니, 비록 지력이 없을지언정 그것들은 하느님의 신비를 증언하며 끊임없이 스스로 그것을 이루어 가고 있소. 나는 그 어여쁜 청년의 마음이 움직이는 것을 보았소. 그는 자기가 숲과 숲의 새들을 사랑한다고 내게 말했소. 그는 새잡이여서, 새마다의 울음소리를 알았고, 어느 새든 불러낼 수 있었소. \"나는 숲속에 있는 것보다 더 좋은 게 없어요.\" 그가 말했소. \"물론 세상 모든 것이 다 좋지만요.\"\n\n\"참으로 그렇소.\" 나는 그에게 대답했소. \"세상 모든 것이 좋고 아름다우니, 모든 것이 진리이기 때문이오. 보시오.\" 내가 말했소. \"사람과 그토록 가까운 저 큰 짐승, 말을 보시오. 혹은 사람을 먹여 살리고 사람을 위해 일하는 저 보잘것없고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 소를 보시오. 그것들의 얼굴을 보시오, 그 얼마나 온순하며, 흔히 자기들을 무자비하게 매질하는 사람을 향해 그 얼마나 헌신적인지. 그 얼마나 부드럽고, 그 얼마나 믿음에 차 있으며, 그 얼마나 아름다운지! 그것들에게 죄가 없음을 안다는 것은 가슴 저미는 일이오, 사람을 빼고는 모든 것이, 모두가 죄가 없으며, 그리스도께서 우리보다 앞서 그들과 함께 계셨기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"어찌하여,\" 청년이 물었소. \"그리스도께서 그들과도 함께 계시나요?\"\n\n\"그러지 않을 수 없소.\" 나는 말했소. \"말씀은 모두를 위한 것이기 때문이오. 모든 피조물과 모든 생명이, 잎사귀 하나하나가 말씀을 향해 나아가며, 하느님께 영광의 노래를 부르고, 그리스도께 눈물지으며, 자기들의 죄 없는 삶의 신비를 통해 무심결에 이를 이루고 있소. 저기,\" 내가 말했소. \"저 숲속에는 무시무시한 곰이 떠도오, 사납고 위협적이나, 그 안에 또한 죄가 없소.\" 그러고 나서 나는, 언젠가 곰 한 마리가 숲속의 자그마한 암자에 몸을 의탁하고 있던 한 위대한 성자에게 찾아온 이야기를 그에게 들려주었소. 그 위대한 성자는 곰을 가엾이 여겨, 두려움 없이 그에게 다가가 빵 한 조각을 주었소. \"가거라.\" 그가 말했소. \"그리스도께서 너와 함께하시기를.\" 그러자 그 사나운 짐승은 아무런 해도 끼치지 않고 온순하고 고분고분하게 걸어가 버렸소. 청년은 곰이 성자를 해치지 않고 걸어가 버린 것을, 그리고 그리스도께서 곰과도 함께 계셨던 것을 기뻐했소. \"아아,\" 그가 말했소. \"그 얼마나 좋은지요, 하느님의 모든 일이 그 얼마나 좋고 아름다운지요!\" 그는 부드럽고 감미롭게 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었소. 나는 그가 알아들었음을 보았소. 그리고 그는 내 곁에서 가볍고 죄 없는 잠을 잤소. 하느님께서 젊음을 축복하시기를! 나는 잠들면서 그를 위해 기도했소. 주님이시여, 당신의 백성에게 평화와 빛을 내려 주소서!\n\n# Chapter II. The Duel\n\n(다) 조시마 장로가 수도자가 되기 전, 청년 시절의 회상.\n결투\n\n나는 페테르부르크의 사관학교에서 거의 팔 년에 이르는 오랜 세월을 보냈는데, 그 새로운 환경의 신기함 속에서 어린 시절의 인상들 가운데 많은 것이 점차 희미해져 갔으나, 그렇다고 무엇 하나 잊어버린 것은 아니었다. 나는 너무도 많은 새로운 습관과 관념을 익혀서 마침내 잔혹하고 어처구니없으며 거의 야만에 가까운 인간으로 변모하고 말았다. 예의범절과 사교계의 처신이라는 겉치레의 윤기만큼은 프랑스어와 더불어 익혀 두긴 했다.\n\n그러나 나를 포함한 우리 모두는 우리 밑에서 복무하는 병사들을 가축으로 여겼다. 그 점에서는 어쩌면 내가 나머지 사람들보다 더 심했을지도 모르니, 나는 동료들보다 훨씬 더 감수성이 예민한 인간이었기 때문이다. 우리가 사관학교를 졸업하고 장교가 되었을 무렵, 우리는 연대의 명예를 위해서라면 목숨을 내던질 각오가 되어 있었으나, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 명예의 참된 의미를 알지 못했고, 설령 누군가가 그것을 알았더라면 그자가 가장 먼저 그것을 비웃었을 것이다. 음주와 방탕과 난행, 그것이야말로 우리가 거의 자랑으로 삼던 것이었다. 우리가 천성적으로 나빴다는 말은 아니니, 이 모든 젊은이들은 다 좋은 친구들이었으나, 행실이 나빴을 따름이고, 그중에서도 내가 가장 나빴다. 게다가 나에게는 사정을 더욱 악화시키는 일이 있었으니, 내가 마침 내 몫의 재산을 손에 넣게 되었던 것이라, 나는 향락의 생활에 몸을 던졌고 젊음의 온갖 무모함 속으로 곤두박질치고 말았다.\n\n나는 책 읽기를 좋아했으나, 이상한 말이지만, 그 무렵 단 한 권 펼쳐 보지 않은 책이 바로 성경이었으니, 그러면서도 나는 늘 그것을 지니고 다녔고 한순간도 그것과 떨어진 적이 없었다. 실로 나는 그 책을 \"그날과 그 시각을 위하여, 그달과 그해를 위하여\" 간직하고 있었던 것이나, 정작 나 자신은 그것을 알지 못했다.\n\n이런 생활을 사 년 동안 보낸 끝에, 나는 우연히도 당시 우리 연대가 주둔하고 있던 K 시에 머물게 되었다. 우리는 그 고을 사람들이 손님 대접을 후하게 하고, 부유하며, 즐기기를 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다. 나는 어디서나 따뜻한 환대를 받았으니, 천성이 쾌활한 데다 살림이 넉넉하다고 알려져 있었기 때문인데, 이런 평판은 세상에서 언제나 큰 힘을 발휘하는 법이다. 그리고 그때 한 가지 사건이 벌어졌으니, 그것이 이 모든 일의 발단이었다.\n\n나는 아름답고 총명하며, 고결하고 드높은 성품을 지닌 한 젊은 처녀에게 마음을 빼앗기게 되었으니, 그녀는 사람들에게 크게 존경받는 집안의 딸이었다. 그들은 살림이 넉넉하고 세력과 지위가 있는 사람들이었다. 그들은 늘 나를 따뜻하고 다정하게 맞아 주었다. 나는 그 아가씨가 나를 호의로 바라본다고 여겼고, 그런 생각에 내 가슴은 불타올랐다. 나중에 가서야 나는 어쩌면 내가 그녀를 그토록 열렬히 사랑했던 것은 결코 아니었으며, 다만 그녀의 드높은 정신과 성품을 인정했을 따름임을 보고 또 온전히 깨달았으니, 사실 그것을 인정하지 않을 도리는 없었다. 그러나 그 무렵 나는 나의 이기심 때문에 그녀에게 청혼하기를 미루고 있었으니, 한창 젊은 나이에 주머니 가득 돈을 채운 채 누리던 자유롭고 방종한 독신 생활의 유혹과 작별하기가 못내 싫었던 것이다. 그래도 내 마음에 관해 넌지시 암시를 흘리기는 했으나, 결정적인 한 걸음을 내딛는 일만큼은 한동안 미루어 두었다. 그러던 차에, 별안간 우리에게 두 달 동안 다른 지방으로 떠나라는 명령이 내려졌다.\n\n두 달 뒤 돌아와 보니, 그 아가씨는 이미 이웃 고을의 부유한 지주와 결혼한 뒤였으니, 그는 매우 상냥한 사람으로, 나보다는 연상이었으나 아직 젊었고, 페테르부르크 최상류 사교계와 연줄이 닿아 있었으되 나는 그렇지 못했으며, 훌륭한 교육을 받은 사람이었으되 나는 또한 그렇지 못했다. 나는 이 뜻밖의 사태에 어찌나 압도되었던지 정신이 그야말로 흐려질 지경이었다. 무엇보다 견디기 힘들었던 것은, 그때 알게 된 사실인데, 그 젊은 지주가 오래전부터 그녀와 약혼한 사이였고, 나도 실은 그녀의 집에서 그를 여러 차례 마주쳤건만, 내 자만심에 눈이 멀어 아무것도 알아채지 못했다는 점이었다. 바로 이것이 나를 유난히 분하게 만들었으니, 거의 모든 사람이 그 일을 다 알고 있었는데 나만 아무것도 몰랐던 것이다. 나는 별안간 걷잡을 수 없는 분노에 사로잡혔다. 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 나는 내가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하려던 순간이 얼마나 잦았던가를 떠올리기 시작했고, 그녀가 나를 말리거나 일러 주려 하지 않았으니 결국 그녀가 내내 나를 비웃고 있었던 것이라고 단정 짓고 말았다. 물론 나중에 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그녀가 결코 나를 비웃었던 것이 아니었음이 기억났으며, 도리어 그녀는 내가 사랑을 내비칠라치면 농담으로 슬쩍 받아넘기고는 다른 이야기로 화제를 돌리곤 했던 것이다. 그러나 그 순간에 나는 곰곰이 생각할 능력이 없었고 오로지 복수심으로 들끓고 있었다. 지금 돌이켜 보면 놀라운 일이지만, 나의 분노와 복수의 감정은 본디 나의 천성에 지극히 거슬리는 것이었으니, 나는 성미가 무던한 사람이라 누군가에게 오래 화를 내기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었고, 그래서 일부러 억지로 감정을 부추겨야 했으며, 마침내는 역겹고 어처구니없는 꼴이 되고 말았다.\n\n나는 기회를 노린 끝에, 많은 사람이 모인 자리에서 나의 \"연적\"을 모욕하는 데 성공했다. 나는 전혀 엉뚱한 구실을 잡아 그를 모욕했으니, 어느 중대한 사회적 사건에 대한 그의 견해를 비웃은 것인데—그것은 1826년의 일이었다—사람들 말로는 나의 빈정거림이 제법 기지 넘치고 효과적이었다고 한다. 그러고는 나는 그가 해명을 요구하지 않을 수 없게끔 몰아붙였고, 어찌나 무례하게 굴었던지, 우리 둘 사이에 엄청난 격차가 있었음에도—나는 더 어리고, 보잘것없는 인간이며, 신분도 낮았건만—그가 나의 결투 신청을 받아들이고 말았다. 나는 나중에 가서야 한 가지 사실을 알게 되었으니, 그가 나의 신청을 받아들인 데에는 그쪽의 질투심도 한몫했다는 것이다. 그는 결혼 전부터 아내 때문에 나를 적잖이 질투하고 있었던 것이며, 이제 와서 그가 나에게 모욕당하고도 결투 신청을 거절했다는 것을 만약 그녀가 듣게 된다면, 그녀가 그를 경멸하기 시작하여 그에 대한 사랑이 흔들릴지도 모른다고 여겼던 것이다. 나는 곧 우리 연대의 한 동료, 한 소위에게서 입회인을 구했다. 그 시절에는 결투가 엄히 처벌받았음에도 불구하고, 장교들 사이에서는 결투가 일종의 유행이었으니—야만적인 편견이란 때로 이토록 강하고 뿌리 깊게 박혀 있는 법이다.\n\n때는 유월 말이었고, 우리의 결투는 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 고을 변두리에서 벌어지기로 되어 있었다—그런데 그때 한 가지 일이 벌어졌으니, 그것이 실로 내 생애의 전환점이었다. 그날 저녁, 사납고 거친 기분으로 집에 돌아온 나는 내 종졸 아파나시에게 벌컥 화를 내고는 있는 힘껏 그의 얼굴을 두 번 후려쳐서, 그 얼굴이 피투성이가 되도록 만들었다. 그는 나에게 온 지 얼마 되지 않았고, 나는 전에도 그를 때린 적이 있었으나, 그토록 잔인무도하게 친 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그리고 믿어 주시오, 비록 사십 년 전의 일이지만, 나는 지금도 그 일을 부끄러움과 아픔 속에서 떠올리고 있소. 나는 잠자리에 들어 세 시간쯤 잤는데, 깨어 보니 동이 트고 있었다. 나는 일어났다—더는 자고 싶지 않았다—창가로 다가가—창을 열었더니, 그것은 정원 쪽으로 나 있었다. 나는 해가 떠오르는 것을 보았다. 날은 따스하고 아름다웠으며, 새들이 지저귀고 있었다.\n\n\"대체 이게 무슨 영문일까?\" 나는 생각했다. \"내 마음속에 어쩐지 비열하고 수치스러운 무언가가 느껴진다. 내가 사람의 피를 흘리려 하기 때문일까? 아니,\" 나는 생각했다. \"그게 아닌 것 같다. 죽음이 두려운 것일까, 죽임을 당할까 봐 무서운 것일까? 아니, 그것도 아니다, 결코 그것이 아니다.\"…… 그러다 별안간 나는 그것이 무엇인지 깨달았으니, 그것은 바로 내가 어제저녁 아파나시를 때렸기 때문이었다! 그 모든 것이 내 마음속에 떠올랐고, 모든 것이 그대로 다시 한번 되풀이되는 듯했다. 그가 내 앞에 서 있고, 나는 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 후려치고 있는데, 그는 두 팔을 뻣뻣이 내려뜨린 채 고개를 꼿꼿이 세우고, 마치 사열대에 선 듯 두 눈을 내게 못 박고 있었다. 그는 매번 얻어맞을 때마다 비틀거렸으나, 제 몸을 가리려 손을 들어 올릴 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 인간이 이 지경에까지 이르렀구나, 그리고 그것은 인간이 같은 동족을 때리고 있는 꼴이었다! 이 무슨 죄악인가! 마치 예리한 단검이 내 몸을 그대로 꿰뚫고 지나간 것 같았다. 나는 벙어리가 된 듯 멍하니 서 있었고, 그동안 해는 빛나고, 나뭇잎은 환희에 차 있고, 새들은 하느님을 찬미하며 지저귀고 있었다.…… 나는 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻은 채 침대에 쓰러져 폭풍 같은 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 그때 나는 형 마르켈을, 그리고 그가 임종의 자리에서 하인들에게 했던 말을 떠올렸다. \"사랑하는 이들이여, 어찌하여 너희가 나의 시중을 드느냐, 어찌하여 너희가 나를 사랑하느냐, 내가 과연 너희의 시중을 받을 만한 사람이더냐?\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 과연 그럴 만한 사람인가?\" 하는 생각이 내 마음속을 스쳤다. \"도대체 나라는 인간이 무슨 값어치가 있기에, 다른 사람이, 하느님의 형상과 모양대로 지음받은 같은 동족이 나를 섬겨야 한단 말인가?\" 내 평생 처음으로 이 물음이 나에게 들이닥쳤다. 형은 이렇게 말했었다. \"어머니, 제 작은 심장이시여, 진실로 우리는 저마다 모든 이에 대해 모든 일에 책임이 있는 것입니다, 다만 사람들이 이것을 모를 뿐이지요. 만약 그들이 그것을 안다면, 세상은 당장 낙원이 될 텐데요.\"\n\n\"하느님, 그것마저도 거짓일 수 있을까?\" 나는 울면서 생각했다. \"진실로, 어쩌면 나야말로 모든 사람보다 더 모든 일에 책임이 있는 자, 세상 어느 누구보다도 더 큰 죄인일지도 모른다.\" 그러자 별안간 온전한 진실이 그 충만한 빛 속에서 내게 모습을 드러냈다. 내가 지금 무엇을 하려는 것인가? 나는 나에게 아무런 잘못도 저지르지 않은, 선량하고 영리하며 고결한 사람을 죽이려 하고 있으며, 그의 아내에게서 남은 평생의 행복을 앗아 감으로써 그녀까지도 괴롭혀 죽이려는 것이었다. 나는 그렇게 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채 침대에 누워, 시간이 어떻게 흐르는지도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그때 별안간 나의 입회인인 소위가 권총을 들고 나를 데리러 들어왔다.\n\n\"아,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"벌써 일어나 있으니 다행이군, 떠날 시간이네, 자 가세!\"\n\n나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 마음을 정하지 못한 채 이리저리 서성거렸으나, 그래도 마차가 있는 곳으로 나갔다.\n\n\"여기서 잠깐 기다려 주게,\" 나는 그에게 말했다. \"곧 돌아오겠네, 지갑을 두고 왔어.\"\n\n그러고는 나 혼자 아파나시의 작은 방으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"아파나시,\" 나는 말했다. \"내가 어제 네 얼굴을 두 번 때렸으니, 나를 용서해 다오,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n그는 깜짝 놀란 듯 흠칫하며 나를 바라보았다. 그래서 나는 그것만으로는 모자란다는 것을 깨닫고, 그 자리에서, 장교 정복을 차려입은 채, 그의 발치에 엎드려 이마가 땅에 닿도록 절을 했다.\n\n\"용서해 다오,\" 나는 말했다.\n\n그러자 그는 완전히 넋이 나가고 말았다.\n\n\"나리…… 나리, 무슨 짓을 하시는 겁니까? 제가 무슨 값어치가 있다고요?\"\n\n그러고는 그가, 조금 전 내가 그랬던 것처럼, 와락 울음을 터뜨리며 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 창 쪽으로 돌아서서는 흐느낌으로 온몸을 들썩였다. 나는 동료에게로 달려 나가 마차에 뛰어올랐다.\n\n\"준비됐네,\" 나는 외쳤다. \"자네 정복자라는 걸 본 적이 있나?\" 나는 그에게 물었다. \"여기 자네 앞에 하나 있네.\"\n\n나는 황홀경에 빠져, 가는 내내 웃고 떠들었는데, 무슨 이야기였는지는 기억나지 않는다.\n\n그는 나를 쳐다보았다. \"이 친구, 자네 참 배짱 두둑한 사내로군, 자네라면 군복의 명예를 지켜 내겠어, 내 눈에는 그게 보이네.\"\n\n그렇게 우리는 그곳에 다다랐고, 그들이 우리를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다. 우리는 열두 걸음 간격을 두고 마주 섰다. 첫 발은 그의 차례였다. 나는 즐거운 기색으로, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 서 있었다. 나는 눈썹 하나 까딱하지 않았고, 다정한 눈길로 그를 바라보았으니, 내가 무엇을 할지 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그가 쏜 총알은 내 뺨과 귀를 스쳐 지나갔을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다,\" 나는 외쳤다. \"아무도 죽지 않았습니다,\" 그러고는 내 권총을 움켜쥐고 돌아서서 그것을 숲 속으로 멀리 던져 버렸다. \"네가 있을 자리는 거기다,\" 나는 외쳤다.\n\n나는 나의 상대 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"용서해 주십시오, 나야말로 어리석은 풋내기입니다,\" 나는 말했다. \"까닭도 없이 당신을 모욕하고, 당신으로 하여금 나를 향해 총을 쏘게 만든 것을 용서해 주십시오. 나는 당신보다 열 곱절은 더 나쁜 인간이며, 어쩌면 그 이상일 겁니다. 부디 이 말을 당신이 세상에서 가장 소중히 여기는 분에게 전해 주십시오.\"\n\n내가 이 말을 마치기가 무섭게 세 사람이 일제히 나에게 소리를 질러 댔다.\n\n\"이런 말도 안 되는,\" 나의 상대가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"싸울 마음이 없었다면, 어째서 나를 그냥 내버려 두지 않았소?\"\n\n\"어제의 저는 바보였습니다만, 오늘의 저는 좀 더 분별이 생겼습니다,\" 나는 즐거운 기색으로 그에게 대답했다.\n\n\"어제의 일이라면 당신 말을 믿겠소만, 오늘의 일이라면 당신 의견에 동의하기 어렵구려,\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"브라보,\" 나는 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다. \"그 점에서도 나는 당신 말에 동의합니다. 나는 그런 소리를 들어 마땅합니다!\"\n\n\"쏘시겠소, 안 쏘시겠소?\"\n\n\"아니요, 쏘지 않겠습니다,\" 나는 말했다. \"원하신다면 다시 한번 나를 향해 쏘셔도 좋습니다만, 쏘지 않으시는 편이 당신에게 나을 겁니다.\"\n\n입회인들도, 특히 내 쪽의 입회인이 소리를 질렀다. \"이렇게 연대를 욕보일 수가 있나, 적수 앞에 서서 용서를 빌다니! 이럴 줄 알았더라면!\"\n\n나는 이제 웃지 않고, 그들 모두를 마주하고 섰다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 나는 말했다. \"요즘 세상에 자기의 어리석음을 뉘우치고 자기의 잘못을 사람들 앞에서 공공연히 고백할 수 있는 사람을 만나는 것이 그토록 놀라운 일입니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 결투에서는 안 될 말이지,\" 내 쪽 입회인이 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그 점이 이상하다는 것입니다,\" 나는 말했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 이곳에 도착하자마자, 그가 한 발 쏘기 전에, 그를 크고도 치명적인 죄악으로 끌어들이기 전에 내 잘못을 인정했어야 마땅했으니까요. 그러나 우리는 우리의 삶을 너무도 기괴하게 만들어 놓았기에, 그런 식으로 행동하기란 거의 불가능한 일이었던 겁니다. 오로지 열두 걸음 거리에서 그의 총탄을 마주한 뒤에야 비로소 내 말이 그에게 어떤 의미를 지닐 수 있었던 것이지, 만약 내가 그 전에 말을 꺼냈더라면 그는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '저자는 겁쟁이로군, 권총을 보고는 겁을 집어먹은 거야, 저런 자 말은 들을 것도 없지.' 여러분,\" 나는 별안간, 마음에서 우러나오는 그대로 외쳤다. \"여러분의 둘레에 있는 하느님의 선물들을 둘러보십시오, 저 맑은 하늘, 저 깨끗한 공기, 저 보드라운 풀, 저 새들을. 자연은 아름답고 죄가 없는데, 우리만이, 오직 우리만이 죄 많고 어리석으며, 우리는 삶이 곧 천국임을 깨닫지 못하고 있습니다. 우리가 그것을 깨닫기만 하면, 그 즉시 삶은 그 온갖 아름다움 속에서 이루어질 것이고, 우리는 서로를 끌어안고 눈물을 흘릴 것입니다.\"\n\n나는 더 말하고 싶었으나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 그 달콤함과 젊은 환희에 목이 메어 왔고, 내 평생 한 번도 맛본 적 없는 그런 더없는 행복이 내 가슴에 가득 차올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"이 모든 말이 다 이치에 맞고 교훈적이로군요,\" 나의 적수가 말했다. \"어쨌든 당신은 별난 사람이오.\"\n\n\"웃으셔도 좋습니다,\" 나도 함께 웃으며 그에게 말했다. \"하지만 나중에는 당신도 나를 좋게 여기게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 지금이라도 당신을 좋게 여길 마음이 있소,\" 그가 말했다. \"악수하지 않겠소? 내 보기에 당신은 진심으로 진실한 사람이니 말이오.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 나는 말했다. \"지금은 아닙니다, 나중에 내가 더 훌륭한 사람이 되어 당신의 존경을 받을 만해지면, 그때 악수하십시오, 그래야 옳을 겁니다.\"\n\n우리는 집으로 돌아갔는데, 내 쪽 입회인은 가는 내내 나를 나무라댔고, 나는 그에게 입을 맞췄다. 내 동료들은 모두 그 일을 곧바로 전해 듣고, 바로 그날 한자리에 모여 나에 대한 판결을 내리려 했다.\n\n\"저자가 군복을 욕보였다,\" 그들은 말했다. \"장교직을 사임하게 하라.\"\n\n몇몇은 나를 두둔했다. \"그는 총탄을 마주했어,\" 그들은 말했다.\n\n\"그렇긴 하지만, 그는 두 번째 총탄이 두려워 용서를 빈 거야.\"\n\n\"총에 맞을 게 두려웠다면, 용서를 빌기 전에 먼저 제 권총부터 쐈겠지, 그런데 그는 장전된 권총을 그대로 숲 속에 던져 버렸잖나. 아니, 이 일에는 뭔가 다른 게 있어, 뭔가 별난 데가 있다고.\"\n\n나는 그들의 말을 듣고 그들을 바라보는 것이 즐거웠다. \"친애하는 벗들이여, 동료들이여,\" 나는 말했다. \"내가 장교직을 사임하는 일이라면 걱정들 말게, 나는 이미 사임했으니까. 나는 오늘 아침 사직서를 제출했고, 제대 허가가 나오는 대로 수도원에 들어갈 작정이라네—바로 그 목적으로 연대를 떠나는 걸세.\"\n\n내가 이 말을 하자, 그들은 하나같이 폭소를 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"그 말을 먼저 했어야지, 그러면 모든 게 설명이 되잖나, 우리가 수도자를 판결할 수야 없지.\"\n\n그들은 웃으며 그칠 줄을 몰랐는데, 그것은 비웃음이 아니라 다정하고 흥겨운 웃음이었다. 그들은 모두 그 즉시 나에게 우정을 느꼈으니, 가장 매섭게 나를 비난하던 자들마저 그러했고, 제대 허가가 나오기 전까지의 한 달 내내 그들은 나를 더없이 떠받들어 주었다. \"이런, 우리 수도자 양반,\" 하고 그들은 말하곤 했다. 그리고 저마다 나에게 다정한 말을 한마디씩 건넸으며, 나를 만류하려 들고, 심지어 나를 가엾게 여기기까지 했다. \"자네 대체 스스로에게 무슨 짓을 하려는 건가?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 그들은 말하곤 했다. \"이 친구는 용감한 사내야, 포화를 마주하고도 제 권총을 쏠 수 있었는데, 그 전날 밤 수도자가 되는 꿈을 꾸었던 거지, 그래서 그렇게 한 거라고.\"\n\n고을의 사교계에서도 사정은 마찬가지였다. 그때까지 나는 따뜻한 환대를 받기는 했으나 특별한 주목의 대상은 아니었는데, 이제는 모두가 단번에 나를 알게 되어 나를 초대하기 시작했으니, 그들은 나를 비웃으면서도 나를 사랑했다. 한 가지 언급해 둘 것은, 비록 모두가 우리의 결투에 대해 공공연히 떠들어 댔으나, 당국은 그것을 문제 삼지 않았다는 사실이다. 나의 적수가 우리 장군의 가까운 친척이었던 데다, 유혈도 없고 심각한 후과도 없었으며, 게다가 내가 장교직을 사임했으므로, 그들은 그것을 한낱 농담쯤으로 받아들였던 것이다. 그래서 나는 그때부터 그들의 웃음에 아랑곳하지 않고 거리낌 없이 큰 소리로 이야기하기 시작했으니, 그것은 언제나 다정한 웃음이지 악의에 찬 웃음이 아니었기 때문이다. 이런 대화는 대개 저녁에, 부인들과 함께한 자리에서 이루어졌으니, 그 무렵 특히 여자들이 내 이야기 듣기를 좋아하여 남자들에게도 듣게끔 만들었다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 어떻게 모든 일에 책임이 있을 수 있단 말이에요?\" 저마다 내 면전에서 웃어 댔다. \"이를테면 제가 당신에 대해서까지 책임이 있다는 건가요?\"\n\n\"당신이 그것을 모르는 것도 무리는 아닙니다,\" 나는 대답하곤 했다. \"온 세상이 오래전부터 다른 길을 걸어왔고, 우리가 새빨간 거짓을 진실로 여기며 남들에게도 똑같은 거짓을 요구하고 있으니 말입니다. 자, 나는 내 평생 단 한 번 진심으로 행동했는데, 그랬더니, 보십시오, 당신들 모두 나를 미치광이로 여기고 있지 않습니까. 당신들이 나에게 우정을 품고 있으면서도, 보시다시피, 모두 나를 비웃고 있는 거지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 당신에게 우정을 품지 않을 수 있겠어요?\" 안주인이 웃으며 말했다. 방 안은 사람들로 가득했다. 그때 별안간, 우리가 결투를 벌인 까닭이 되었던, 그리고 얼마 전까지만 해도 내가 장차 아내로 삼으려 했던 그 젊은 부인이 자리에서 일어섰다. 나는 그녀가 방에 들어온 것을 알아채지 못하고 있었다. 그녀는 일어나, 나에게 다가와 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"제 말 좀 들어 주세요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 당신을 비웃지 않는 첫 번째 사람이에요, 오히려 그 반대로, 눈물을 흘리며 당신에게 감사드리고, 그때 당신이 보여 준 그 행동에 대해 존경을 표하고 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀의 남편도 다가왔고, 그러자 모두가 나에게로 몰려와 거의 입을 맞출 듯이 했다. 내 가슴은 기쁨으로 가득 찼으나, 다른 사람들과 함께 나에게 다가온 어느 중년 남자가 특히 내 주의를 끌었다. 나는 이미 이름으로는 그를 알고 있었으나, 그날 저녁까지 그와 사귀어 본 적도, 말 한마디 나누어 본 적도 없었다.\n\n(라) 수수께끼의 방문자\n\n그는 오래전부터 그 고을의 관리였으니, 두드러진 지위에 있어 모두에게 존경받았고, 부유했으며, 자선가라는 평판이 있었다. 그는 양로원과 고아원에 상당한 액수를 기부했고, 남몰래도 매우 자선을 베풀었는데, 이 사실은 그가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 알려졌다. 그는 쉰 살가량의 사람으로, 거의 근엄해 보이는 풍모에 말수가 많지 않았다. 그는 결혼한 지 십 년쯤 되었는데, 아직 젊은 그의 아내가 그에게 세 자녀를 낳아 주었다. 그런데 이튿날 저녁 내가 방에 홀로 앉아 있을 때, 별안간 문이 열리며 이 신사가 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n그건 그렇고 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 나는 더 이상 예전의 거처에서 살고 있지 않았다는 사실이다. 장교직을 사임하자마자 나는 어느 노부인의 집에 방을 얻었는데, 그녀는 어느 관청 서기의 미망인이었다. 집주인 노부인의 하녀가 내 시중을 들었으니, 내가 그녀의 집으로 옮긴 까닭은 다름이 아니라, 결투에서 돌아온 뒤 아파나시를 연대로 돌려보냈기 때문이었다. 마지막으로 그와 마주한 뒤로는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보기가 부끄러웠던 것이다. 세상 사람이란 이토록 의로운 행동을 부끄러워하기 쉬운 법이다.\n\n\"나는,\" 하고 나의 방문자가 말했다. \"지난 며칠 동안 여러 집에서 당신이 이야기하는 것을 깊은 관심을 가지고 들어 왔는데, 마침내 당신과 직접 사귀어 좀 더 허물없이 이야기를 나누고 싶어졌습니다. 선생, 부디 나에게 그런 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다, 오히려 그것을 영광으로 여기겠습니다.\" 나는 이렇게 말했으나, 거의 당혹스러운 심정이었으니, 첫 순간부터 이 사람의 풍모에 그토록 크게 압도되었던 것이다. 다른 사람들도 관심과 주의를 기울여 내 이야기를 듣기는 했으나, 그토록 진지하고 근엄하며 골똘한 표정으로 나에게 다가온 사람은 일찍이 없었던 것이다. 게다가 이제 그가 내 방으로 직접 나를 찾아온 것이었다. 그는 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"내가 보기에 당신은 대단히 강인한 성품을 지닌 사람이오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 그렇게 함으로써 만인의 멸시를 감수할 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 감히 진실을 섬길 줄 알았으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신의 칭찬은 어쩌면 지나치십니다,\" 나는 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요, 지나치지 않소,\" 그가 대답했다. \"내 말을 믿으시오, 그런 식으로 행동하기란 당신이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 어려운 일이오. 바로 그것이 나에게 깊은 인상을 주었고, 오로지 그 때문에 내가 당신을 찾아온 것이오,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"한 가지 말씀해 주시오, 혹시 나의, 어쩌면 무례할지도 모를 호기심에 언짢지 않으시다면 말입니다만, 당신이 결투에서 용서를 빌기로 마음을 정한 그 순간, 만약 그것을 기억해 낼 수 있다면, 당신의 정확한 심정이 어떠했는지 말이오. 나의 물음을 경박하게 여기지 마시오. 오히려 나는 이 물음을 던지는 데에 나 나름의 은밀한 동기가 있으며, 만약 우리가 더 가까이 사귀게 되는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라면, 어쩌면 나중에 그것을 당신에게 설명할 수도 있을 것이오.\"\n\n그가 이야기하는 내내, 나는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 별안간 그에 대한 온전한 신뢰와 더불어 나 자신도 큰 호기심을 느꼈으니, 그의 영혼 속에 무언가 기이한 비밀이 있다는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 적수에게 용서를 빌던 그 순간의 정확한 심정이 어떠했는지 물으시는군요,\" 나는 대답했다. \"하지만 그보다 먼저, 내가 아직 그 누구에게도 이야기한 적 없는 것을 처음부터 당신에게 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠습니다.\" 그러고는 나는 아파나시와 나 사이에 있었던 모든 일을, 그리고 내가 어떻게 그의 발치에 엎드려 이마가 땅에 닿도록 절했는지를 낱낱이 이야기했다. \"그러니 당신도 친히 아실 수 있을 겁니다,\" 나는 맺음말을 했다. \"결투할 때에는 오히려 한결 수월했다는 것을 말이지요, 나는 이미 집에서 그 첫걸음을 떼어 두었던 터라, 일단 그 길로 들어선 이상, 그 길을 더 멀리 나아가기란 결코 어렵기는커녕 도리어 기쁨과 행복의 샘이 되었던 것입니다.\"\n\n그가 내 이야기를 들으며 나를 바라보던 그 눈길이 나는 마음에 들었다. \"그 모든 것이,\" 그가 말했다. \"지극히 흥미롭소. 내 다시 또다시 당신을 찾아오겠소.\"\n\n그리고 그때부터 그는 거의 매일 저녁 나를 찾아왔다. 그리고 만약 그가 자기 자신에 관해 단 한 번이라도 이야기를 했더라면, 우리는 더 가까운 벗이 되었을 것이다. 그러나 자기 자신에 관해서는 그가 좀처럼 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그러면서도 끊임없이 나에 관해 물었다. 그럼에도 불구하고 나는 그를 무척 좋아하게 되어, 내 모든 감정에 관해 그에게 완전히 솔직하게 털어놓았다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 나는 생각했다. \"내가 무엇 때문에 그의 비밀을 알 필요가 있겠는가, 그것 없이도 그가 선량한 사람이라는 걸 알 수 있는데? 게다가 그는 그토록 진지한 사람이고 나보다 손위인데도, 나 같은 풋내기를 찾아와 나를 동등한 사람으로 대해 주지 않는가.\" 그리고 나는 그에게서 유익한 것을 매우 많이 배웠으니, 그는 드높은 정신을 지닌 사람이었던 것이다.\n\n\"삶이 곧 천국이라는 것,\" 하고 그가 별안간 나에게 말했다. \"그것을 나는 오래전부터 생각해 왔소.\" 그러고는 별안간 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"실로 나는 그것 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않소.\" 그는 나를 바라보며 미소 지었다. \"나는 당신보다 그것을 더 확신하고 있소, 그 까닭은 나중에 말해 주겠소.\"\n\n나는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그가 분명 나에게 무언가를 말하고 싶어 한다고 생각했다.\n\n\"천국은,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리 모두의 안에 숨겨져 있소—지금 내 안에도 그것이 숨겨져 있으며, 만약 내가 원하기만 하면, 그것은 내일이라도, 그리고 영원토록 나에게 드러날 것이오.\"\n\n나는 그를 바라보았다. 그는 깊은 격정에 차서 이야기하며, 마치 나에게 무언가를 묻기라도 하듯 수수께끼 같은 눈길로 나를 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 우리 모두가, 우리 자신의 죄와는 별개로, 모든 이에 대해 모든 일에 책임이 있다는 것, 당신이 그렇게 생각한 것은 지극히 옳았소, 그리고 당신이 그 온전한 의미를 단번에 깨달을 수 있었다는 것은 놀라운 일이오. 그리고 진실로, 사람들이 그것을 깨닫는 순간, 천국은 그들에게 한낱 꿈이 아니라 살아 있는 현실이 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 언제,\" 나는 그에게 비통하게 외쳤다. \"언제 그런 일이 일어날까요? 그리고 그런 일이 과연 일어나기는 할까요? 그것은 그저 우리의 꿈에 지나지 않는 것이 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 당신은 그것을 믿지 않는구려,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 그것을 설교하면서 정작 당신 자신은 믿지 않는 것이오. 내 말을 믿으시오, 당신이 꿈이라 부르는 이것은 의심할 바 없이 이루어질 것이오. 그것은 오기는 오겠으나, 지금은 아니니, 모든 과정에는 저마다의 법칙이 있기 때문이오. 그것은 영적이고 심리적인 과정이오. 세상을 변화시키고, 그것을 새로이 다시 빚으려면, 사람들은 심리적으로 다른 길로 돌아서야만 하오. 당신이 정말로, 실제로, 모든 사람에게 형제가 되기 전에는, 형제애란 이루어지지 않을 것이오. 그 어떤 과학적 가르침도, 그 어떤 공동의 이익도 사람들로 하여금 모두를 동등하게 배려하며 재산과 특권을 나누도록 가르치지는 못할 것이오. 저마다 자기 몫이 너무 적다고 여길 것이며, 그들은 늘 서로를 시기하고, 불평하고, 헐뜯을 것이오. 당신은 그것이 언제 이루어지겠느냐고 묻는데, 그것은 이루어질 것이나, 다만 그에 앞서 우리는 고립의 시대를 거쳐야만 하오.\"\n\n\"고립이라니, 무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀입니까?\" 나는 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"다름 아니라, 도처에, 무엇보다도 우리 시대에 만연한 그 고립 말이오—그것은 아직 온전히 무르익지 않았고, 아직 그 한계에 다다르지 않았소. 왜냐하면 저마다 자기의 개성을 되도록 떼어 놓으려 애쓰고, 자기 자신을 위해 되도록 충만한 삶을 누리고자 하기 때문이오. 그러나 그러는 동안 그의 온갖 노력은 삶의 충만함에 이르기는커녕 오히려 자기 파멸로 귀결되니, 자기 실현 대신 결국 완전한 고독에 다다르고 마는 것이오. 우리 시대의 온 인류는 단위들로 산산이 쪼개져, 저마다 따로따로 자기만의 골 속에 틀어박혀 있소. 저마다 남들과 거리를 두고, 자기 자신을 숨기며, 자기가 가진 것을 나머지 사람들에게서 감추다가, 마침내 남들에게 배척당하고 또 남들을 배척하기에 이르오. 그는 혼자서 재물을 쌓아 올리고는 '이제 나는 얼마나 강하고 얼마나 든든한가' 하고 생각하지만, 그 어리석음 속에서 정작 깨닫지 못하는 것은, 더 많이 쌓아 올릴수록 그만큼 더 자기 파멸의 무력함 속으로 가라앉는다는 사실이오. 왜냐하면 그는 오로지 자기 자신만을 의지하고 전체로부터 자기를 떼어 놓는 데 익숙해진 데다, 남들의 도움을, 사람을, 그리고 인류를 믿지 않도록 자기를 길들였으며, 오로지 자기가 손에 넣은 돈과 특권을 잃을까 봐 벌벌 떨 따름이기 때문이오. 요즘 세상 도처에서 사람들은, 그 조롱 속에서, 참된 든든함이란 고립된 개인의 노력보다는 사회적 결속 속에 있다는 사실을 이해하기를 그만두어 버렸소. 그러나 이 끔찍한 개인주의는 반드시 종말을 맞이할 수밖에 없을 것이며, 그러면 모두가 별안간 자기들이 서로에게서 얼마나 부자연스럽게 갈라져 있었는지를 깨달을 것이오. 그것은 시대의 정신이 될 것이며, 사람들은 자기들이 빛을 보지 못한 채 그토록 오래도록 어둠 속에 앉아 있었다는 사실에 경탄할 것이오. 그리고 그때 하늘에 인자(人子)의 표징이 나타나리라.…… 그러나 그때까지 우리는 깃발을 높이 들고 있어야만 하오. 때로는, 비록 그 일을 혼자서 해야 하고 그의 행동이 미친 짓처럼 보일지라도, 한 사람이 모범을 보여, 그리하여 사람들의 영혼을 고독으로부터 끌어내고, 그들에게 어떤 형제애의 행위로 박차를 가하여, 그 위대한 이념이 죽어 버리지 않도록 해야만 하는 것이오.\"\n\n우리의 저녁은 그렇게 하루하루, 가슴을 뒤흔드는 열정적인 대화로 채워졌다. 나는 사교계를 그만두고, 이웃들을 찾는 일도 훨씬 뜸해졌다. 게다가 나의 인기도 어느 정도 시들해진 터였다. 이런 말을 하는 것은 그들을 탓해서가 아니니, 그들은 여전히 나를 사랑하고 너그럽게 대해 주었으나, 유행이 사교계에서 큰 힘을 발휘한다는 것만큼은 부정할 수 없는 노릇이다. 나는 이 수수께끼 같은 방문자를 차츰 감탄의 눈으로 바라보기 시작했으니, 그의 총명함을 즐기는 것에 더해, 그가 마음속에 어떤 계획을 곱씹으며 어쩌면 위대한 행위를 준비하고 있다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했기 때문이다. 어쩌면 그는 내가 그의 비밀에 대해 호기심을 드러내지 않고, 직접적인 물음으로도 넌지시 떠보는 것으로도 그것을 캐내려 들지 않는 점을 마음에 들어 했는지도 모른다. 그러나 나는 마침내, 그가 나에게 무언가를 말하고 싶어 하는 기색을 내비치는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다. 실로 이것은, 그가 나를 처음 찾아오기 시작한 지 한 달쯤 지나자 제법 분명해졌다.\n\n\"당신도 아시오,\" 하고 그가 언젠가 나에게 말했다. \"고을 사람들이 우리에 대해 무척 궁금해하며, 내가 어째서 그토록 자주 당신을 찾아오는지 의아해한다는 것을 말이오. 하지만 의아해하라고 내버려 두시오, 머지않아 모든 것이 밝혀질 테니까.\"\n\n이따금 그에게 예사롭지 않은 동요가 엄습하곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 거의 언제나 그는 일어나서 가 버렸다. 이따금 그는 나를 오래도록 꿰뚫는 눈길로 응시하곤 했고, 나는 '이제 곧 무슨 말인가를 하겠구나' 하고 생각하곤 했다. 그러나 그는 별안간 무언가 평범하고 익숙한 이야기를 꺼내곤 했다. 그는 또 자주 두통을 호소했다.\n\n어느 날, 실로 전혀 뜻밖에도, 그가 오랫동안 깊은 열정에 차서 이야기를 하던 끝에, 나는 그가 별안간 창백해지는 것을 보았는데, 그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러지면서, 그는 줄곧 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 나는 말했다. \"어디 편찮으신가요?\"—그는 방금 두통을 호소하던 참이었다.\n\n\"나는…… 그러니까…… 나는 사람을 죽였소.\"\n\n그는 이렇게 말하고는, 백묵처럼 새하얀 얼굴로 미소를 지었다. '저 사람은 어째서 웃고 있는 걸까?' 다른 무엇을 깨닫기도 전에 이 생각이 내 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 나도 창백해졌다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까?\" 나는 외쳤다.\n\n\"보다시피,\" 그가 창백한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그 첫 마디를 입에 올리는 데 내가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치렀는지 말이오. 이제 그 말을 했으니, 나는 첫걸음을 떼었다는 느낌이 들고, 앞으로 계속 나아가게 될 것이오.\"\n\n나는 한참 동안 그의 말을 믿을 수가 없었고, 그때만 해도 그를 믿지 못했으나, 그가 사흘 연이어 나를 찾아와 그 모든 것을 낱낱이 이야기한 뒤에야 비로소 믿게 되었다. 나는 그가 미친 것이라고 생각했으나, 마침내, 크나큰 비통함과 경악 속에서, 확신하기에 이르렀다. 그의 죄는 크고도 끔찍한 것이었다.\n\n십사 년 전, 그는 어느 지주의 미망인을 죽였으니, 그녀는 우리 고을에 저택을 한 채 가지고 있던 부유하고 아름다운 젊은 여인이었다. 그는 그녀에게 열렬히 반하여, 자기 마음을 고백하고 그녀에게 자기와 결혼해 달라고 설득하려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 이미 다른 남자에게 마음을 주고 있었으니, 그는 명문 출신에 군에서 높은 계급에 있는 장교로, 당시에는 전선에 나가 있었으나, 그녀는 그가 머지않아 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀는 그의 청혼을 거절하고 자기를 찾아오지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 그가 그녀를 찾는 일을 그만둔 뒤, 그는 그 저택에 대해 자기가 알고 있는 바를 이용해, 발각될 위험이 큰데도 밤중에 정원을 통해 지붕으로 잠입했다. 그러나 흔히 그렇듯, 유달리 대담하게 저질러진 범행이 오히려 다른 경우보다 더 잘 성공하는 법이다.\n\n천창을 통해 다락방으로 들어선 그는 사다리를 타고 내려갔는데, 그 사다리 아래쪽의 문이 이따금 하인들의 부주의로 잠기지 않은 채 있다는 것을 알고 있었던 것이다. 그는 그러기를 바랐고, 과연 그러했다. 그는 어둠 속을 더듬어 그녀의 침실로 들어갔는데, 그곳에는 불이 켜져 있었다. 마치 일부러 그런 듯, 그녀의 두 하녀는 허락도 받지 않고 같은 거리에서 열린 생일잔치에 가 버린 뒤였다. 다른 하인들은 일층의 하인 방이나 부엌에서 잠들어 있었다. 잠든 그녀의 모습을 보자 그의 정욕이 활활 타올랐고, 그러다 앙심에 찬 질투의 분노가 그의 마음을 사로잡아, 마치 술에 취한 사람처럼 제정신이 아닌 채로, 그는 칼을 그녀의 심장에 찔러 넣었으니, 그녀는 비명조차 지르지 못했다. 그러고는 악마같이 교활한 범죄자의 간계로, 그는 하인들에게 혐의가 쏠리도록 꾸몄다. 그는 비열하게도 그녀의 지갑을 챙기고, 베개 밑에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 그녀의 궤를 열어 그 안에서 몇 가지 물건을 꺼냈는데, 무지한 하인이 했음 직하게 이 모든 일을 해냈으니, 값진 서류는 그대로 둔 채 돈만 챙긴 것이다. 그는 큼직한 금붙이 몇 가지를 가져갔으나, 그 열 곱절은 더 값진 자잘한 물건들은 그냥 두었다. 그는 또한 자기 자신을 위해 기념이 될 만한 몇 가지 물건도 가지고 갔는데, 그 이야기는 나중에 하겠다. 이 끔찍한 일을 저지른 뒤, 그는 왔던 길로 되돌아 나갔다.\n\n이튿날 소동이 벌어졌을 때에도, 그리고 그 후 그의 평생 어느 때에도, 그가 범인이라고 의심하는 꿈을 꾼 자는 단 한 사람도 없었다. 실로 아무도 그가 그녀를 사랑했다는 사실을 알지 못했으니, 그는 늘 말이 없고 과묵하여 자기 마음을 털어놓을 만한 벗조차 없었기 때문이다. 사람들은 그를 그저 살해된 여인의 지인, 그것도 그리 가깝지 않은 지인쯤으로 여겼으니, 지난 보름 동안 그는 그녀를 찾아간 적조차 없었던 것이다. 그녀의 농노인 표트르라는 자가 즉각 의심을 받았고, 모든 정황이 그 의심을 뒷받침했다. 그 사내는—실은 그 여주인이 그 사실을 숨기지도 않았으므로—자기 농노들 가운데 하나를 신병으로 보내야 하게 되자 그녀가 바로 그를 보내기로 마음먹었다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그에게 친척도 없고 행실도 변변치 못했기 때문이다. 사람들은 그가 어느 선술집에서 술에 취해 그녀를 죽여 버리겠다고 성난 목소리로 으름장 놓는 것을 들은 적이 있었다. 그녀가 죽기 이틀 전, 그는 달아나 버려, 고을 어디에 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다. 살인이 벌어진 이튿날, 그는 고을을 빠져나가는 길에서 발견되었는데, 곤드레만드레 취한 채 주머니에 칼을 지니고 있었고, 마침 그의 오른손이 피로 물들어 있었다. 그는 코피가 났던 것이라고 우겼으나, 아무도 그의 말을 믿지 않았다. 하녀들은 자기들이 잔치에 갔으며 자기들이 돌아올 때까지 한길 쪽 문이 열린 채로 있었다고 자백했다. 그리하여 이와 비슷한 갖가지 사정이 속속 드러나며 무고한 하인에게 혐의를 씌웠다.\n\n그들은 그를 체포했고, 그는 살인죄로 재판을 받았다. 그러나 체포된 지 일주일 만에, 그 죄수는 열병에 걸려 병원에서 의식을 잃은 채 죽고 말았다. 그것으로 사건은 마무리되었고, 판사들도, 당국도, 고을의 모든 사람도 그 범행은 병원에서 죽은 그 하인이 저지른 것임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다. 그리고 그 뒤로 형벌이 시작되었다.\n\n이제 나의 벗이 된 그 수수께끼의 방문자가 나에게 말하기를, 처음에는 양심의 가책에 조금도 시달리지 않았다고 했다. 그는 오랫동안 비참한 심정이었으나, 그것은 그 때문이 아니었다. 다만 자기가 사랑하던 여인을 죽였다는 것, 그녀가 이제는 더 이상 이 세상에 없다는 것, 정욕의 불길이 아직 핏줄 속에 남아 있는데도 그녀를 죽임으로써 자기 사랑마저 죽여 버렸다는 것에 대한 회한 때문이었다. 그러나 자기가 흘린 무고한 피에 대해서는, 같은 동족을 죽인 살인에 대해서는, 그는 거의 생각조차 하지 않았다. 자기 희생자가 다른 남자의 아내가 되었을지도 모른다는 생각이 그에게는 견딜 수 없는 것이었으니, 그리하여 그는 오랫동안 자기 양심 속에서, 자기로서는 달리 행동할 수 없었던 것이라고 확신하고 있었다.\n\n처음에 그는 그 하인이 체포된 것을 마음에 걸려 했으나, 그자의 병과 죽음이 곧 그의 마음을 가라앉혀 주었으니, 그자의 죽음은 (그가 당시에 그렇게 헤아린 바로는) 체포나 공포 때문이 아니라, 달아나던 날 밤 곤드레만드레 취해 축축한 땅바닥에 밤새 누워 있다가 걸린 오한 때문인 것이 분명했기 때문이다. 돈과 다른 물건들을 훔친 일은 그를 별로 괴롭히지 않았으니, 그 절도는 이득을 보려고 저지른 것이 아니라 혐의를 돌리기 위함이었다고 스스로 따져 댔던 것이다. 훔친 액수는 얼마 되지 않았고, 그는 그 직후 그 전액을, 그것도 훨씬 더 많은 액수를 보태어 고을의 양로원 유지 기금으로 기부했다. 그는 이 절도에 대해 자기 양심을 안심시키려고 일부러 그렇게 한 것이었는데, 한 가지 주목할 만한 사실은, 그가 오랫동안 정말로 마음의 평안을 누렸다는 점이다—이것은 그가 직접 나에게 한 말이다. 그러고 나서 그는 관직에서 대단히 활발한 경력을 쌓아 나갔으니, 어렵고 고된 임무를 자청하여 이 년 동안 그 일에 매달렸으며, 의지가 강한 사람이었던지라 거의 과거를 잊다시피 했다. 그것이 떠오를 때면 그는 그것에 대해 아예 생각하지 않으려 애썼다. 그는 또한 자선 사업에도 적극적으로 나서, 고을에 여러 시설을 세우고 그 유지를 도왔으며, 두 수도(首都)에서도 많은 일을 하여 모스크바와 페테르부르크 양쪽에서 자선 단체의 회원으로 선출되었다.\n\n그러나 마침내 그는 과거를 곱씹기 시작했으니, 그 부담이 그에게는 너무도 버거운 것이었다. 그때 그는 어느 곱고 총명한 처녀에게 마음이 끌려 머지않아 그녀와 결혼했으니, 결혼이 자기의 외로운 우울을 떨쳐 주리라고, 그리고 새로운 삶에 들어서서 아내와 자식들에 대한 자기 도리를 빈틈없이 다함으로써 옛 기억으로부터 아주 벗어날 수 있으리라고 기대했던 것이다. 그러나 그가 기대했던 것과는 정반대의 일이 벌어지고 말았다. 그는 결혼한 첫 달부터 벌써 '내 아내가 나를 사랑하지만—만약 그녀가 알게 된다면?' 하는 생각에 끊임없이 시달리기 시작했다. 그녀가 머지않아 그에게 아이를 낳아 주겠다고 처음 말했을 때, 그는 괴로웠다. '나는 생명을 주고 있다, 그러나 나는 생명을 앗은 자다.' 아이들이 태어났다. '내가 어찌 감히 이 아이들을 사랑하고, 가르치고, 길러 낸단 말인가, 내가 어찌 이 아이들에게 미덕에 대해 이야기할 수 있단 말인가? 나는 피를 흘린 자인데.' 그 아이들은 더없이 사랑스러운 아이들이라, 그는 아이들을 어루만지고 싶어 못 견딜 지경이었다. '그런데도 나는 이 아이들의 순진하고 맑은 얼굴을 차마 바라볼 수가 없으니, 나는 그럴 자격이 없는 인간이다.'\n\n마침내 그는 자기가 살해한 희생자의 피에, 자기가 짓밟아 버린 그 젊은 생명에, 복수를 부르짖는 그 피에 비통하고도 불길하게 시달리기 시작했다. 그는 끔찍한 꿈에 시달리게 되었다. 그러나 그는 굳센 사람이었던지라, 오랫동안 그 고통을 견디며 이렇게 생각했다. '나는 이 은밀한 고뇌로써 모든 것을 속죄하리라.' 그러나 그 희망 또한 부질없는 것이었으니, 세월이 흐를수록 그의 고통은 그만큼 더 격심해질 따름이었다.\n\n그는 활발한 자선 활동으로 사교계에서 존경을 받았으나, 모두가 그의 근엄하고 음울한 성품에 위압을 느꼈다. 그러나 존경을 받으면 받을수록, 그것은 그에게 더욱 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다. 그는 나에게, 자살을 생각한 적도 있었다고 고백했다. 그러나 그는 또 다른 생각에 시달리기 시작했으니—처음에는 불가능하고 생각조차 할 수 없는 것으로 여겼으나, 마침내 그 생각이 그의 마음을 어찌나 단단히 사로잡았던지 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었던 것이다. 그는 떨치고 일어나, 밖으로 나가, 만인 앞에서 자기가 살인을 저질렀노라고 고백하는 꿈을 꾸었다. 삼 년 동안 이 꿈은 갖가지 형태로 그를 사로잡으며 따라다녔다. 마침내 그는 진심으로, 만약 자기 죄를 고백한다면 자기 영혼이 치유되고 영원히 평안해지리라고 믿게 되었다. 그러나 이 믿음은 그의 마음을 공포로 가득 채웠으니, 어떻게 그것을 실행한단 말인가? 그리고 그때 나의 결투에서 벌어진 그 일이 일어났던 것이다.\n\n\"당신을 보고서, 나는 마음을 굳혔소.\"\n\n나는 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럴 수가 있습니까,\" 나는 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그토록 사소한 사건이 당신에게 그런 결심을 불러일으킬 수 있다는 것이?\"\n\n\"내 결심은 지난 삼 년 동안 자라 온 것이오,\" 그가 대답했다. \"당신의 이야기가 거기에 마지막 한 획을 더했을 뿐이오. 당신을 보면서, 나는 나 자신을 책망하고 또 당신을 부러워했소.\" 그는 거의 침울하다 싶을 만큼 이 말을 나에게 했다.\n\n\"하지만 사람들이 당신을 믿지 않을 겁니다,\" 나는 의견을 내놓았다. \"십사 년 전의 일이지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"내게는 증거가 있소, 확실한 증거가. 그것을 내보일 것이오.\"\n\n그러자 나는 눈물을 흘리며 그에게 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"한 가지만, 한 가지만 말해 주시오,\" 그가 (마치 그 모든 것이 나에게 달려 있기라도 하다는 듯) 말했다. \"내 아내, 내 자식들 말이오! 내 아내는 슬픔으로 죽을지도 모르고, 내 자식들은 비록 신분과 재산을 잃지는 않겠으나, 죄수의 자식으로, 그것도 영원토록 살아가야 하지 않겠소! 그리고 나에 대한 어떤 기억을, 어떤 기억을 그 어린 가슴들에 남기게 될 것이오!\"\n\n나는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그리고 그들과 헤어지는 것, 그들을 영원히 떠나는 것이라니? 그것은 영원이오, 알겠소, 영원이란 말이오!\"\n\n나는 가만히 앉아 소리 없는 기도를 거듭 올렸다. 마침내 나는 일어섰으니, 두려움을 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n\"어떻소?\" 그가 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"가십시오!\" 나는 말했다. \"고백하십시오. 모든 것은 지나가고, 오직 진실만이 남습니다. 당신의 자식들도 자라나면, 당신이 내린 그 결심의 고결함을 이해하게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n그때 그는 마치 마음을 굳힌 듯이 나에게서 떠나갔다. 그러나 그 뒤로도 보름이 넘도록, 그는 매일 저녁 나를 찾아와, 여전히 마음의 채비를 하면서도, 여전히 그 결단의 순간으로 자신을 몰아가지 못했다. 그는 나의 가슴을 아프게 했다. 어느 날은 단단히 작정한 채 찾아와 열정에 차서 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"고백하는 그 순간, 내게는 천국이 펼쳐지리라는 것을, 천국이 펼쳐지리라는 것을 나는 알고 있소. 십사 년 동안 나는 지옥에 있었소. 나는 고통받고 싶소. 나는 내 형벌을 받아들이고 새로이 살기 시작할 것이오. 사람이 잘못을 저지르며 세상을 살아갈 수는 있어도, 되돌릴 길은 없는 법이오. 지금 나는 내 이웃은커녕 내 자식조차도 감히 사랑할 수가 없소. 오, 하느님, 내 자식들은 어쩌면, 이 형벌이 나에게 어떤 대가를 치르게 했는지 이해하고 나를 단죄하지 않을 것이오! 하느님은 힘 안에 계신 것이 아니라 진실 안에 계시는 것이오.\"\n\n\"모두가 당신의 희생을 이해하게 될 겁니다,\" 나는 그에게 말했다. \"당장은 아니더라도, 나중에는 이해하게 될 겁니다. 왜냐하면 당신은 진실을, 그것도 땅의 것이 아닌 더 높은 진실을 섬긴 것이니까요.\"\n\n그러면 그는 위안을 얻은 듯이 떠나갔으나, 이튿날이면 다시 찾아오곤 했으니, 비통하고, 창백하고, 비아냥대는 모습으로였다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 찾아올 때마다, 당신은 마치 '이 사람이 아직도 고백을 하지 않았군!' 하고 말하기라도 하듯 그렇게 캐묻는 눈길로 나를 바라보는구려. 잠깐 기다려 주시오, 나를 너무 멸시하지는 마시오. 그것은 당신이 생각하는 것처럼 그리 쉬운 일이 아니오. 어쩌면 나는 끝내 그 일을 하지 않을지도 모르오. 그러면 당신은 가서 나를 고발하거나 하지는 않겠지요, 그렇소?\"\n\n나는 무례한 호기심으로 그를 바라보기는커녕, 차마 그를 바라보는 것조차 두려웠다. 나는 불안에 시달려 거의 병이 날 지경이었고, 내 가슴은 눈물로 가득 차 있었다. 나는 밤에 잠을 이룰 수가 없었다.\n\n\"나는 방금 아내에게서 오는 길이오,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"'아내'라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 당신이 이해하겠소? 내가 나서는데, 아이들이 나를 부르며 이러는 것이오. '아빠, 안녕히 다녀오세요, 어서 돌아오셔서 우리랑 같이 어린이 잡지를 읽어요.' 아니, 당신은 그것을 이해하지 못하오! 남의 슬픔으로 지혜로워지는 사람은 없는 법이니까.\"\n\n그의 두 눈은 번득이고 있었고, 그의 입술은 실룩거리고 있었다. 별안간 그가 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳐서 그 위의 모든 것이 들썩거렸는데—그가 그런 짓을 한 것은 처음이었으니, 그는 그토록 온화한 사람이었던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 꼭 그래야 하오?\" 그가 부르짖었다. \"내가 반드시 그래야만 하오? 아무도 유죄 선고를 받지 않았고, 아무도 나 대신 시베리아로 보내지지 않았으며, 그자는 열병으로 죽었소. 그리고 나는 내가 흘린 피에 대해 내 고통으로 형벌을 받아 왔소. 게다가 사람들은 나를 믿지 않을 것이고, 내 증거도 믿지 않을 것이오. 내가 고백을 해야 하오, 내가 꼭 그래야만 하오? 오로지 내 아내와 자식들만 화를 면할 수 있다면, 나는 내가 흘린 피에 대해 평생토록 고통받을 각오가 되어 있소. 그들을 나와 함께 파멸시키는 것이 과연 옳은 일이겠소? 우리가 잘못 생각하고 있는 것은 아니오? 이 경우에 무엇이 옳은 것이오? 그리고 사람들이 그것을 알아주기나 할 것이오, 그것을 인정해 주기나 할 것이오, 그것을 존중해 주기나 할 것이오?\"\n\n'맙소사,' 나는 속으로 생각했다. '이런 순간에도 이 사람은 남들의 존경을 생각하고 있구나!' 그러고는 그때 그가 어찌나 가엾던지, 만약 그것이 그에게 위안이 될 수 있다면 나는 그의 운명을 함께 나눠 졌으리라고 생각한다. 나는 그가 제정신이 아니라는 것을 알았다. 나는, 그런 결심이 무엇을 뜻하는지를 머리로뿐 아니라 가슴으로도 깨달으며, 아연실색하고 말았다.\n\n\"내 운명을 결정해 주시오!\" 그가 다시 부르짖었다.\n\n\"가서 고백하십시오,\" 나는 그에게 속삭였다. 목소리가 제대로 나오지 않았으나, 나는 단호하게 그것을 속삭였다. 나는 탁자에서 신약 성경을, 그 러시아어 번역본을 집어 들어, 그에게 요한복음 십이 장 이십사 절을 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n\"진실로 진실로 너희에게 이르노니, 한 알의 밀이 땅에 떨어져 죽지 아니하면 한 알 그대로 있고, 죽으면 많은 열매를 맺느니라.\"\n\n그가 들어오기 직전에 나는 마침 그 구절을 읽고 있던 참이었다. 그는 그것을 읽었다.\n\n\"그것은 진실이오,\" 그가 말했으나, 비통하게 미소 지었다. \"그런 책들 속에서 찾아내는 것들이란 참 무서운 것들이오,\" 그가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다. \"그런 것들을 남에게 들이밀기란 아주 쉬운 일이지요. 그런데 그것을 쓴 자는 누구요? 그것이 과연 사람들의 손으로 쓰일 수 있는 것이겠소?\"\n\n\"성령께서 그것을 쓰셨습니다,\" 나는 말했다.\n\n\"당신이야 떠벌리기 쉽겠지요,\" 그가 다시 미소 지었는데, 이번에는 거의 증오에 차서였다.\n\n나는 다시 그 책을 집어 들어, 다른 곳을 펼쳐 그에게 히브리서 십 장 삼십일 절을 보여 주었다. 그는 읽었다.\n\n\"살아 계신 하느님의 손에 빠져 들어가는 것이 무서울진저.\"\n\n그는 그것을 읽고는 그대로 책을 내던졌다. 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"끔찍한 구절이오,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 딱 들어맞는 것들만 골라낸 것은 부정할 수 없구려.\" 그는 의자에서 일어섰다. \"자!\" 그가 말했다. \"그럼 잘 있으시오, 어쩌면 나는 다시 오지 못할 거요…… 우리는 천국에서 만나게 될 것이오. 그러니까 나는 십사 년 동안 '살아 계신 하느님의 손에' 있었던 셈이오, 그 십사 년을 그렇게 생각해야 하는 것이지요. 내일 나는 그 손에 나를 놓아 달라고 간구하겠소.\"\n\n나는 그를 품에 안고 입 맞추고 싶었으나, 차마 그럴 수가 없었으니—그의 얼굴은 일그러지고 침울했던 것이다. 그는 떠나갔다.\n\n'맙소사,' 나는 생각했다. '이 사람이 무엇을 마주하러 간 것인가!' 나는 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 우리의 날랜 보호자이자 조력자이신 거룩하신 하느님의 어머니 앞에서 그를 위해 울었다. 나는 반 시간 동안 눈물을 흘리며 기도했는데, 시각은 늦은 자정 무렵이었다. 그때 별안간 나는 문이 열리는 것을 보았고, 그가 다시 들어왔다. 나는 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"어디를 다녀오셨습니까?\" 나는 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"내가 아무래도,\" 그가 말했다. \"무언가를 두고 간 것 같소…… 손수건을, 그런 것 같소.…… 뭐, 설령 두고 간 것이 없더라도, 잠깐 머무르게 해 주시오.\"\n\n그는 자리에 앉았다. 나는 그의 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"당신도 앉으시오,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 앉았다. 우리는 두어 시간 동안 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 그는 나를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 별안간 미소를 지었다—나는 그것을 기억하고 있다—그러더니 그는 일어나, 나를 따뜻하게 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"기억해 두시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 두 번째로 당신을 찾아왔다는 것을. 듣고 있소, 그것을 기억해 두시오!\"\n\n그러고는 그는 나가 버렸다.\n\n'내일이로구나,' 나는 생각했다.\n그리고 과연 그러했다. 그날 저녁 나는 이튿날이 그의 생일이라는 것을 알지 못했다. 지난 며칠 동안 나는 외출을 하지 않았던 터라, 누구에게서도 그것을 전해 들을 길이 없었던 것이다. 그날이면 그는 늘 큰 모임을 열었고, 고을의 모든 사람이 그 자리에 갔다. 이번에도 마찬가지였다. 만찬이 끝난 뒤, 그는 손에 종이 한 장을 든 채 방 한가운데로 걸어 나갔으니—그것은 그 자리에 와 있던 자기 부서의 장(長) 앞으로 쓴 정식 진술서였다. 그는 이 진술서를 모인 사람들 모두에게 큰 소리로 낭독했다. 그것에는 그 범행에 대한 온전한 전모가, 낱낱의 세부에 이르기까지 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"나는 괴물로서 사람들로부터 나 자신을 끊어 냈소. 하느님께서 나를 찾아오셨소,\" 그가 끝맺으며 말했다. \"나는 내 죄로 인해 고통받기를 원하오!\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는, 자기가 십사 년 동안 간직해 온, 자기 범행을 입증하리라고 여긴 모든 물건을 꺼내어 탁자 위에 늘어놓았으니, 혐의를 돌리려고 훔쳤던 살해당한 여인의 보석들, 그녀의 목에서 떼어 낸 십자가와 로켓—그 로켓 안에는 그녀의 약혼자의 초상이 들어 있었다—그녀의 수첩과 편지 두 통이었다. 그 가운데 한 통은 그녀의 약혼자가 보낸 것으로, 머지않아 그녀 곁으로 가겠다는 내용이었고, 다른 한 통은 그녀가 채 끝맺지 못한 답장으로, 이튿날 부치려고 탁자 위에 남겨 두었던 것이었다. 그는 이 두 통의 편지를 가져갔던 것이다—무엇 때문에? 어째서 그는 그것을 자기에게 불리한 증거로 없애 버리지 않고, 그 후로 십사 년 동안이나 간직하고 있었던 것일까?\n\n그리고 벌어진 일은 이러했다. 모두가 경악하고 소스라쳤으며, 모두가 그것을 믿기를 거부하고 그가 미친 것이라고 여기면서도, 다들 강렬한 호기심을 가지고 귀를 기울였다. 며칠 뒤, 그 불행한 사람이 미쳤다는 것이 집집마다 완전히 단정되고 합의되었다. 사법 당국은 그 사건을 맡지 않을 수 없었으나, 그들 역시 그것을 흐지부지 덮어 버렸다. 비록 그 장신구들과 편지들이 그들을 골똘히 생각하게 만들기는 했으나, 그들은 설령 그것들이 진짜로 판명된다 하더라도 그것만으로는 어떤 혐의도 성립시킬 수 없다고 결론 내렸다. 게다가 그녀가 벗으로서 그에게 그 물건들을 주었을 수도 있고, 자기 대신 보관해 달라고 부탁했을 수도 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 나중에, 그 물건들의 진위가 살해된 여인의 벗들과 친척들에 의해 입증되었으며, 그것에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없었다는 것을 전해 들었다. 그러나 결국 그 일에서는 아무것도 비롯되지 않을 운명이었다.\n\n닷새 뒤, 그가 병들었으며 목숨이 위태롭다는 소식을 모두가 들었다. 그의 병의 본질이 무엇인지 나로서는 설명할 수가 없는데, 사람들은 그것이 심장의 질환이라고들 했다. 그러나 의사들이 그의 아내의 권유로 그의 정신 상태까지 진찰하여 그것이 정신 착란의 경우라는 결론에 이르렀다는 사실이 알려졌다. 나는 사람들이 나에게 달려와 캐물어도 아무것도 발설하지 않았다. 그러나 내가 그를 찾아가려 했을 때, 나는 오랫동안, 무엇보다도 그의 아내에 의해 그러는 것을 금지당했다.\n\n\"바로 당신이 그이의 병을 불러온 거예요,\" 그녀가 나에게 말했다. \"그이는 늘 음울하긴 했지만, 지난 일 년 동안 사람들은 그이가 유난히 흥분해 이상한 짓을 한다는 것을 눈치챘는데, 이제 당신이 그이를 망쳐 놓은 거예요. 당신의 설교가 그이를 이 지경으로 몰고 온 거라고요. 지난 한 달 동안 그이는 늘 당신과 함께 있었으니까요.\"\n\n실로 그의 아내뿐만 아니라 온 고을이 나를 들들 볶으며 나를 탓했다. \"다 당신이 한 짓이오,\" 그들은 말했다. 나는 잠자코 있었으며, 실은 마음속으로 기뻐했으니, 자기 자신에게 맞서 자기 자신을 벌한 그 사람에게 하느님께서 베푸신 자비를 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다. 나는 그가 미쳤다는 것을 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n마침내 그들이 나에게 그를 만나게 해 주었으니, 그가 나에게 작별 인사를 하겠다고 고집했던 것이다. 나는 그에게로 들어가, 그에게 남은 것이 이제 며칠이 아니라 몇 시간뿐임을 단번에 알아보았다. 그는 쇠약하고, 누렇게 떴으며, 두 손이 떨리고, 숨을 헐떡거렸으나, 그의 얼굴은 다정하고 행복한 감정으로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n\"끝났소!\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 오랫동안 당신을 보고 싶어 애태웠는데, 어째서 오지 않았소?\"\n\n나는 그들이 나에게 그를 만나게 해 주지 않았다는 말은 하지 않았다.\n\n\"하느님께서 나를 가엾이 여기시어 나를 당신 곁으로 부르고 계시오. 나는 내가 죽어 가고 있음을 알지만, 그토록 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 처음으로 기쁨과 평안을 느끼고 있소. 내가 해야 할 일을 해낸 그 순간부터, 내 마음속에는 천국이 깃들었소. 이제 나는 감히 내 자식들을 사랑하고 그 아이들에게 입 맞출 수 있게 되었소. 내 아내도, 판사들도, 그 누구도 그것을 믿지 않았소. 내 자식들도 결코 그것을 믿지 않을 것이오. 나는 거기에서 그 아이들에게 베푸신 하느님의 자비를 보오. 나는 죽겠지만, 내 이름은 그 아이들에게 한 점 오점 없이 남을 것이오. 그리고 이제 나는 하느님이 가까이 계심을 느끼며, 내 마음은 천국에 있는 것처럼 기뻐하오…… 나는 내 도리를 다했소.\"\n\n그는 말을 잇지 못하고 숨을 헐떡였으며, 나의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥔 채 열렬한 눈길로 나를 바라보았다. 우리는 오래 이야기를 나누지는 못했으니, 그의 아내가 줄곧 우리 쪽을 엿보고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 그는 나에게 이렇게 속삭일 짬은 있었다.\n\n\"내가 그날 자정에 두 번째로 당신을 찾아갔던 것을 기억하시오? 나는 당신에게 그것을 기억해 두라고 했소. 내가 무엇 때문에 다시 찾아갔는지 아시오? 나는 당신을 죽이러 갔던 것이오!\"\n\n나는 흠칫했다.\n\n\"나는 그때 당신에게서 나와 어둠 속으로 나섰고, 나 자신과 싸우며 거리를 헤매고 다녔소. 그러다 별안간 나는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 당신이 미워졌소. '이제,' 하고 나는 생각했소. '나를 옭아매는 것은 오직 저자뿐이고, 저자가 나의 심판자다. 나는 내일 내 형벌을 마주하기를 거부할 수가 없으니, 저자가 모든 것을 알고 있기 때문이다.' 그것은 당신이 나를 밀고할까 봐 두려워서가 아니었소 (나는 그런 것은 생각조차 하지 않았소). 다만 나는 이렇게 생각했소. '내가 고백하지 않는다면, 어떻게 저자의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라볼 수 있단 말인가?' 그리고 설령 당신이 땅끝에 가 있더라도, 살아 있기만 하다면, 마찬가지였을 것이오. 당신이 모든 것을 알면서 살아서 나를 단죄하고 있다는 그 생각이 견딜 수 없었던 것이오. 나는 마치 당신이 그 모든 일의 원인이기라도 하듯, 마치 당신에게 그 모든 책임이 있기라도 하듯 당신을 미워했소. 나는 그때 당신의 탁자 위에 단검이 놓여 있던 것을 떠올리며 당신에게 되돌아갔던 것이오. 나는 자리에 앉아 당신에게도 앉으라고 청한 뒤, 꼬박 일 분 동안 곰곰이 생각했소. 만약 내가 당신을 죽였더라면, 비록 그 다른 죄는 고백하지 않았더라도 나는 그 살인으로 파멸하고 말았을 것이오. 그러나 나는 그런 것은 전혀 생각하지 않았고, 그 순간 그런 것을 생각하고 싶지도 않았소. 나는 그저 당신을 미워했고, 그 모든 것에 대해 당신에게 복수하기를 갈망했을 뿐이오. 주님께서 내 마음속의 악마를 물리치셨소. 그러나 한 가지 말해 두는데, 당신은 그때 일찍이 죽음에 그토록 가까이 다가간 적이 없었소.\"\n\n일주일 뒤 그는 죽었다. 온 고을이 그를 무덤까지 따라갔다. 수석 사제가 감동에 찬 추도사를 했다. 모두가 그의 생을 앗아 간 그 끔찍한 병을 애도했다. 그러나 장례식이 끝난 뒤, 온 고을이 나에게 들고일어났으며, 사람들은 심지어 나를 만나기조차 거부했다. 더러는, 처음에는 몇 사람이, 나중에는 더 많은 사람이 실제로 그의 이야기의 진실을 믿기 시작하여, 나를 찾아와 크나큰 관심과 열의를 가지고 캐물었으니, 사람이란 의로운 자의 몰락과 치욕을 보기를 좋아하는 법이기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 입을 다물고 있었고, 그 직후 곧 그 고을을 떠나, 다섯 달 뒤 하느님의 은총으로 안전하고 축복받은 길에 들어서서, 나를 그토록 또렷이 그 길로 인도해 주신 보이지 않는 손가락을 찬미했다. 그러나 나는 오늘날까지도 내 기도 속에서, 그토록 크나큰 고통을 겪은 하느님의 종 미하일을 기억하고 있다.\n\n신부님들과 스승님들이여, 수도승이란 무엇입니까? 교양 있는 세상에서는 요즈음 이 말을 어떤 이들은 비웃음을 담아 입에 올리고, 또 어떤 이들은 욕설로 쓰며, 수도승을 향한 이 경멸은 점점 더 커지고 있습니다. 슬프게도, 참으로 슬프게도, 수도승들 가운데 게으름뱅이며 식충이며 방탕한 자며 뻔뻔한 거지가 많은 것은 사실입니다. 배운 사람들은 이런 자들을 가리키며 말합니다. \"너희는 게으름뱅이며, 사회에 쓸모없는 존재이며, 남의 노동에 기대어 살아가는, 부끄러움을 모르는 거지들이다.\" 그러나 온유하고 겸손한 수도승, 고독과 평화 속에서 뜨거운 기도를 갈망하는 수도승은 또 얼마나 많습니까! 이런 이들은 덜 주목받거나, 침묵 속에 묻혀 버립니다. 그러니 만일 내가, 고독한 기도를 갈망하는 이 온유한 수도승들로부터 러시아의 구원이 어쩌면 다시 한 번 비롯되리라고 말한다면 사람들이 얼마나 놀라겠습니까! 참으로 이들은 평화와 고요 속에서 \"그날과 그 시각, 그달과 그해\"를 위하여 채비를 갖추고 있는 것입니다. 그러는 동안 그들은 고독 속에서, 옛 교부들과 사도들과 순교자들의 시대로부터 이어 온 하느님 진리의 순결함 가운데, 그리스도의 형상을 아름답고 더럽혀지지 않은 채 지켜 나갑니다. 그리고 때가 이르면 그들은 그 형상을 흔들리는 세상의 신앙들 앞에 내보일 것입니다. 이는 위대한 사상입니다. 그 별은 동방에서 떠오를 것입니다.\n\n이것이 수도승에 관한 나의 견해인데, 그것이 거짓입니까? 그것이 너무 교만합니까? 세속의 인간들과, 하느님의 백성 위에 스스로를 높이 세운 모든 자들을 보십시오. 그들 안에서 하느님의 형상과 그분의 진리가 일그러지지 않았습니까? 그들에게는 학문이 있습니다. 그러나 학문에는 감각의 대상이 되는 것밖에 아무것도 없습니다. 영적인 세계, 인간 존재의 더 높은 부분은 송두리째 거부당하고, 일종의 의기양양함으로, 심지어 증오로 내쳐집니다. 세상은, 특히 최근 들어 자유의 통치를 선포했으나, 그들의 그 자유 속에서 우리가 보는 것은 무엇입니까? 오직 노예 상태와 자기 파멸뿐입니다! 세상은 이렇게 말하기 때문입니다.\n\n\"너에게 욕망이 있으니 그것을 채워라, 너는 가장 부유하고 권세 있는 자들과 똑같은 권리를 지녔으니. 욕망을 채우기를 두려워 말고 도리어 네 욕망을 늘려라.\" 이것이 세상의 현대적 교리입니다. 그들은 거기서 자유를 봅니다. 그런데 이 욕망 증식의 권리에서 무엇이 따라 나옵니까? 부자에게는 고립과 영적 자살이, 가난한 자에게는 질투와 살인이 따라 나옵니다. 권리는 주어졌으되, 그 욕구를 채울 수단은 보여 주지 않았기 때문입니다. 그들은 세상이 거리를 정복하고 생각을 공중으로 날려 보내는 만큼, 점점 더 하나로 결합하고 점점 더 형제적 공동체로 묶여 가고 있다고 주장합니다.\n\n슬프게도, 그러한 결속의 유대를 믿지 마십시오. 자유를 욕망의 증식과 신속한 충족으로 해석함으로써 사람들은 자기 자신의 본성을 일그러뜨리는데, 무분별하고 어리석은 숱한 욕망과 습관과 가소로운 공상이 그들 안에서 길러지기 때문입니다. 그들은 오직 서로를 시기하기 위하여, 사치와 허영을 위하여 살아갑니다. 만찬을 베풀고, 방문을 다니고, 마차를 굴리고, 관등을 누리고, 시중들 노예를 거느리는 일이 하나의 필수로 여겨지며, 그것을 위하여 생명과 명예와 인간적 정감마저 희생되고, 그것을 채우지 못하면 사람들은 자살까지 합니다. 부유하지 않은 자들 사이에서도 우리는 똑같은 일을 보거니와, 가난한 자들은 채워지지 않은 욕구와 그 질투를 술에 빠져 잠재웁니다. 그러나 머지않아 그들은 포도주 대신 피를 마시게 될 것이니, 그들은 그리로 이끌려 가고 있습니다. 묻건대, 그런 인간이 자유롭습니까? 나는 어느 \"자유의 투사\"를 알고 있었는데, 그가 직접 내게 털어놓기를, 감옥에서 담배를 빼앗겼을 때 그 박탈이 어찌나 비참하던지 다시 담배를 얻기 위하여 하마터면 제 대의를 배신할 뻔했다는 것이었습니다! 그러면서도 그런 인간이 \"나는 인류의 대의를 위하여 싸운다\"고 말합니다.\n\n그런 자가 어찌 싸울 수 있겠습니까? 그가 무엇에 쓸모가 있겠습니까? 그는 어쩌면 금세 끝나 버리는 어떤 행동은 할 수 있을지 몰라도, 오래 견뎌 내지는 못합니다. 그러니 그들이 자유를 얻기는커녕 노예 상태로 가라앉고, 형제애와 인류 결합의 대의에 봉사하기는커녕 도리어 불화와 고립에 빠져 버린 것도 이상할 게 없으니, 이는 내 젊은 시절 나의 신비로운 방문자이자 스승이 내게 일러 준 그대로입니다. 그러므로 인류에 대한 봉사, 형제애, 인류의 연대라는 사상은 세상에서 점점 더 사그라들어 가고, 실로 이 사상은 때로 조롱거리로 취급되기까지 합니다. 인간이 어찌 자기 습관을 떨쳐 낼 수 있겠습니까? 스스로 만들어 낸 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 욕망을 채우는 습관에 그토록 얽매여 있다면 그가 어찌 되겠습니까? 그는 고립되어 있으니, 나머지 인류와 무슨 상관이 있겠습니까? 그들은 더 많은 물건을 쌓아 올리는 데는 성공했으나, 세상의 기쁨은 도리어 줄어들고 말았습니다.\n\n수도원의 길은 이와는 아주 다릅니다. 순종과 단식과 기도는 비웃음을 받지만, 오직 그것들을 통해서만 진정하고 참된 자유에 이르는 길이 열립니다. 나는 군더더기 같고 불필요한 욕망을 잘라 내고, 교만하고 방종한 내 의지를 다스리며 순종으로 그것을 매질하고, 하느님의 도우심으로 영혼의 자유와 그에 따른 영적 기쁨에 이릅니다. 위대한 사상을 품고 거기 봉사할 수 있는 이는 어느 쪽이겠습니까—고립 속의 부자입니까, 아니면 물질과 습관의 폭정에서 스스로를 해방한 사람입니까? 수도승은 자기 고독으로 인하여 비난받습니다. \"너는 네 자신의 구원을 위하여 수도원 담장 안에 스스로를 가두고, 인류를 향한 형제적 봉사는 잊어버렸다!\" 그러나 형제애의 대의에 누가 더 열심일지는 두고 보아야 할 것입니다. 고립되어 있는 것은 우리가 아니라 그들이건만, 그들은 그것을 보지 못하기 때문입니다. 옛날에는 백성의 지도자들이 우리 가운데서 나왔거늘, 어찌 다시 그러지 못하겠습니까? 똑같이 온유하고 겸손한 고행자들이 일어나 저 위대한 대의를 위하여 나아갈 것입니다. 러시아의 구원은 백성에게서 옵니다. 그리고 러시아의 수도승은 언제나 백성의 편에 서 있었습니다. 우리가 고립되는 것은 오직 백성이 고립될 때뿐입니다. 백성은 우리와 같이 믿으니, 믿음 없는 개혁가는 비록 마음이 진실하고 천재라 할지라도 러시아에서는 결코 아무 일도 이루지 못할 것입니다. 이것을 명심하십시오! 백성은 무신론자와 마주쳐 그를 이겨 낼 것이며, 러시아는 하나가 되어 정교를 받들 것입니다. 농민을 보살피고 그의 마음을 지키십시오. 조용히 그를 가르쳐 나가십시오. 그것이 수도승으로서 그대들의 의무이니, 농민의 마음속에는 하느님이 계시기 때문입니다.\n\n물론 나는 농민들 안에도 죄가 있음을 부정하지 않습니다. 그리고 타락의 불길은 눈에 보이게, 시시각각, 위에서 아래로 번져 내려가고 있습니다. 고립의 정신이 백성에게도 덮쳐 오고 있습니다. 고리대금업자와 공동체를 집어삼키는 자들이 일어나고 있습니다. 이미 상인은 점점 더 관등을 탐내고, 교양이라곤 한 자락도 없으면서 자기를 교양 있는 사람으로 내보이려 애쓰며, 그 목적을 위하여 비루하게도 제 오랜 전통을 멸시하고, 심지어 조상들의 신앙마저 부끄러워합니다. 그는 그저 타락한 농민일 뿐이면서도 공작들을 찾아다닙니다. 농민들은 술에 절어 썩어 가면서도 그 습관을 떨쳐 내지 못합니다. 그리고 제 아내에게, 심지어 제 자식에게 베푸는 그 잔혹함이라니! 모두가 술 때문입니다! 나는 공장에서 아홉 살배기 아이들을 보았는데, 허약하고 구루병에 걸려 허리가 굽고 벌써 타락해 있었습니다. 숨 막히는 작업장, 기계의 굉음, 온종일 이어지는 노동, 입에 담기 더러운 욕설과 술, 또 술—그것이 어린아이의 마음에 필요한 것입니까? 아이에게는 햇빛이, 어린아이다운 놀이가, 사방에 둘러싼 좋은 본보기가, 그리고 적어도 약간의 사랑이 필요합니다. 더는 이래서는 안 됩니다, 수도승들이여, 더는 아이들을 괴롭혀서는 안 됩니다. 일어나 그것을 설교하십시오, 서두르십시오, 서두르십시오!\n\n그러나 하느님께서 러시아를 구원하실 것이니, 농민들이 타락하여 그 더러운 죄를 버리지 못한다 할지라도, 그래도 그들은 그것이 하느님께 저주받은 일이며 죄를 지음으로써 자기가 그릇된 짓을 저지른다는 것을 알고 있기 때문입니다. 그러니 우리 백성은 여전히 의로움을 믿고, 하느님을 신뢰하며, 경건의 눈물을 흘립니다.\n\n상류 계급은 다릅니다. 그들은 학문을 좇으며 이전처럼 그리스도와 더불어서가 아니라 오직 이성에만 정의를 세우려 하고, 이미 범죄란 없으며 죄란 없다고 선포했습니다. 그것은 일관된 논리이니, 하느님이 없다면 범죄가 무슨 의미가 있겠습니까? 유럽에서는 백성이 이미 폭력으로 부자들에게 맞서 일어나고 있으며, 백성의 지도자들은 곳곳에서 그들을 유혈로 이끌고, 그들의 분노가 의롭다고 가르치고 있습니다. 그러나 그들의 \"분노는 저주받았으니, 그것이 잔혹하기 때문\"입니다. 그러나 하느님께서는 여러 번 그러하셨듯이 러시아를 구원하실 것입니다. 구원은 백성에게서, 그들의 신앙과 그들의 온유함에서 올 것입니다.\n\n신부님들과 스승님들이여, 백성의 신앙을 지켜보십시오, 그리하면 이것은 한낱 꿈이 아닐 것입니다. 나는 평생토록 우리 위대한 백성에게서 그들의 위엄, 그들의 참되고 단정한 위엄에 깊이 감동해 왔습니다. 나는 그것을 직접 보았고, 그것을 증언할 수 있으며, 그것을 보고 경탄했으니, 우리 농민의 타락한 죄와 빈궁에 찌든 행색에도 불구하고 나는 그것을 보았습니다. 그들은 비굴하지 않으며, 두 세기에 걸친 농노 생활 뒤에도 그 몸가짐과 태도가 자유로우면서도 무례하지 않고, 앙심을 품지도 시기하지도 않습니다. \"당신은 부유하고 고귀하며, 영리하고 재능이 있으니, 그래, 그러시오, 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를. 나는 당신을 존경하오, 그러나 나 또한 한 인간임을 압니다. 시기하지 않고 당신을 존경한다는 바로 그 사실로써 나는 인간으로서 내 위엄을 증명하는 것이오.\"\n\n참으로 그들이 이렇게 말하지는 않더라도(아직 이렇게 말할 줄 모르기 때문입니다), 그들은 그렇게 처신합니다. 나는 그것을 직접 보았고, 직접 겪어 알거니와, 믿어지겠습니까, 우리 러시아 농민은 가난할수록 그 잔잔한 선량함이 더욱 두드러집니다. 그들 가운데 부유한 자들은 대부분 이미 타락해 있으며, 그 많은 부분이 우리의 무관심과 무신경 탓이기 때문입니다. 그러나 하느님께서는 당신의 백성을 구원하실 것이니, 러시아는 그 겸손함으로 위대하기 때문입니다. 나는 우리의 미래를 보기를 꿈꾸며, 이미 그것을 또렷이 보는 듯합니다. 마침내 우리 부자들 가운데 가장 타락한 자조차 가난한 자들 앞에서 자기 부를 부끄러워하기에 이르고, 가난한 자들은 그의 겸손함을 보고 그것을 이해하여 그에게 길을 내주며, 그의 명예로운 부끄러움에 기쁘고 다정하게 응답하는 일이 이루어질 것입니다. 결국 그렇게 끝나리라는 것을 믿으십시오. 모든 것이 그리로 움직여 가고 있습니다. 평등은 오직 인간의 영적 위엄 안에서만 찾을 수 있으며, 그것은 우리 사이에서만 이해될 것입니다. 우리가 형제라면 형제애가 있을 것이나, 그 전에는 결코 부의 분배를 두고 합의하지 못할 것입니다. 우리는 그리스도의 형상을 간직하고 있으며, 그것은 귀한 금강석처럼 온 세상에 빛을 발할 것입니다. 부디 그렇게 되기를, 부디 그렇게 되기를!\n\n신부님들과 스승님들이여, 언젠가 내게 가슴 뭉클한 일이 있었습니다. 떠돌이 길에 나는 K시에서 옛 종졸이었던 아파나시를 만났습니다. 그와 헤어진 지 여덟 해가 되던 때였습니다. 그는 우연히 장터에서 나를 보고는 알아보고 달려왔는데, 얼마나 기뻐하던지요! 그는 그야말로 내게 와락 달려들었습니다. \"나리, 나리이십니까? 정말 제가 뵙는 분이 나리이십니까?\" 그러더니 그는 나를 자기 집으로 데려갔습니다.\n\n그는 더 이상 군에 있지 않았고, 결혼하여 벌써 어린 자식이 둘 있었습니다. 그와 그의 아내는 장터에서 행상으로 생계를 꾸리고 있었습니다. 그의 방은 가난했으나 밝고 깨끗했습니다. 그는 나를 앉히고 사모바르를 올리고 아내를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 내가 찾아온 것이 그들에게 마치 축제라도 되는 듯했습니다. 그는 자기 아이들을 데려왔습니다. \"이 아이들을 축복해 주십시오, 신부님.\"\n\n\"내가 이 아이들을 축복할 자격이 있겠는가? 나는 한낱 보잘것없는 수도승일 뿐일세. 이 아이들을 위해 기도하겠네. 그리고 아파나시 파블로비치, 그날 이후로 나는 날마다 자네를 위해 기도해 왔다네, 모든 것이 자네로부터 비롯되었으니 말일세.\" 나는 이렇게 말했습니다. 그리고 그것을 할 수 있는 데까지 그에게 설명해 주었습니다. 그런데 어찌 되었는지 아십니까? 그는 줄곧 나를 바라보면서, 한때 자기 주인이었고 장교였던 내가 이제 이런 행색과 처지로 자기 앞에 서 있다는 것을 믿지 못했습니다. 그것이 그로 하여금 눈물을 흘리게 했습니다.\n\n\"어찌하여 우는가?\" 내가 말했습니다. \"차라리 나를 두고 기뻐하게나, 결코 잊을 수 없는 정다운 벗이여, 나의 길은 즐겁고 기쁜 길이니 말일세.\"\n\n그는 말은 많이 하지 않았으나, 줄곧 한숨을 쉬며 다정하게 나를 향해 고개를 가로저었습니다.\n\n\"나리의 재산은 어찌 되었습니까?\" 그가 물었습니다.\n\n\"수도원에 내놓았다네.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"우리는 모든 것을 함께 나누며 산다네.\"\n\n차를 마신 뒤 나는 작별 인사를 하기 시작했는데, 갑자기 그가 수도원에 바치는 헌금으로 반 루블을 내놓았고, 또 다른 반 루블을 황급히 내 손에 쥐여 주는 것을 나는 보았습니다. \"이것은 떠돌이 길에 나리께 드리는 것입니다, 쓸모가 있을 겁니다, 신부님.\"\n\n나는 그의 반 루블을 받아 그와 그의 아내에게 절하고는 기쁜 마음으로 나왔습니다. 그리고 길을 가며 나는 생각했습니다. \"이제 우리 둘은, 그는 집에 있고 나는 길 위에 있으면서, 분명 한숨을 쉬고 고개를 가로젓고 있겠지만, 그래도 하느님께서 우리의 만남을 어떻게 이루어 주셨는가를 떠올리며 마음의 기쁨 속에 즐거이 미소 짓고 있으리라.\"\n\n그 뒤로 나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했습니다. 나는 그의 주인이었고 그는 나의 종이었으나, 이제 우리가 누그러진 마음으로 정다운 입맞춤을 나누었을 때 우리 사이에는 위대한 인간적 유대가 있었습니다. 나는 그것에 관하여 많은 것을 생각해 왔으며, 이제 내가 생각하는 바는 이러합니다. 그 장엄하고 소박한 마음의 하나됨이 때가 이르러 러시아 백성 가운데 보편적인 것이 된다는 것이 그토록 상상도 못 할 일이겠습니까? 나는 그것이 이루어지리라고, 그리고 그때가 가까이 왔다고 믿습니다.\n\n그리고 종들에 관하여 나는 이것을 덧붙이겠습니다. 옛날 내가 젊었을 때 나는 종들에게 자주 화를 냈습니다. \"요리사가 너무 뜨거운 것을 내왔다느니, 종졸이 내 옷을 솔질해 두지 않았다느니\" 하면서 말입니다. 그러나 그때 나를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 준 것은 어린 시절 내 사랑하는 형에게서 들었던 한 생각이었습니다. \"다른 사람이 그 가난과 무지 속에서 나를 섬기고 내게 부림을 받는다는 것이, 내가 과연 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 자인가?\" 그리고 나는 그토록 단순하고 자명한 생각이 어찌하여 우리 마음에 그처럼 더디게 떠오르는가를 그 무렵 의아하게 여겼습니다.\n\n세상에 종이 없을 수는 없는 일입니다. 그러나 그대의 종이 종이 아닐 때보다 영혼에 있어 더 자유롭도록 처신하십시오. 그리고 어찌하여 내가 내 종의 종이 되지 못하고, 게다가 그것을 그가 보도록 하지 못하겠습니까, 그것도 내 쪽에 아무런 교만도 없고 그의 쪽에 아무런 불신도 없이 말입니다? 어찌하여 내 종이 내 친족과 같아져서, 내가 그를 내 가족으로 받아들이고 그렇게 함을 기뻐하지 못하겠습니까? 지금도 이것은 이루어질 수 있으나, 이것은 장차 인류의 장엄한 하나됨으로 이끌어 갈 것이니, 그때에는 사람이 지금처럼 제 종을 구하거나 제 이웃을 종으로 만들고자 하지 않고, 도리어 복음이 가르치는 대로 온 마음으로 모든 이의 종이 되기를 갈망할 것입니다.\n\n그리고 마침내 인간이 지금처럼 잔혹한 쾌락, 곧 탐식과 음행과 허영과 자랑과 서로를 향한 시기 어린 경쟁에서가 아니라, 오직 빛과 자비의 행위에서만 자기 기쁨을 찾게 되리라는 것이 한낱 꿈일 수 있겠습니까? 나는 그것이 꿈이 아니며 그때가 가까이 왔음을 굳게 믿습니다. 사람들은 비웃으며 묻습니다. \"그때가 언제 오겠으며, 그것이 올 듯이나 보이는가?\" 나는 그리스도의 도우심으로 우리가 이 위대한 일을 이루어 내리라고 믿습니다. 그리고 인간의 역사에서, 나타나기 십 년 전만 해도 생각조차 할 수 없었던 사상이 이 땅에 얼마나 많았습니까! 그러나 정해진 때가 이르자 그것들은 나타나 온 땅에 퍼져 나갔습니다. 우리도 그러할 것이며, 우리 백성은 세상에 빛을 발할 것이고, 모든 사람이 이렇게 말할 것입니다. \"건축자들이 버린 돌이 집의 모퉁잇돌이 되었도다.\"\n\n그리고 우리는 비웃는 자들 자신에게도 물을 수 있습니다. 만일 우리의 희망이 한낱 꿈이라면, 그대들은 그리스도 없이 오직 그대들의 지성만으로 언제 그대들의 건물을 세우고 세상사를 의롭게 질서 지을 것입니까? 만일 그들이, 하나됨을 향해 나아가는 것은 바로 자기들이라고 단언한다면, 그들 가운데 오직 가장 순진한 자들만이 그것을 믿을 따름이니, 그 단순함에는 그야말로 경탄할 수밖에 없습니다. 참으로 그들은 우리보다 더 허황된 꿈을 품고 있습니다. 그들은 정의를 겨냥하나, 그리스도를 부정하므로 마침내 온 땅을 피로 적시고 말 것이니, 피는 피를 부르고, 칼을 잡는 자는 칼로 망할 것이기 때문입니다. 그리고 그리스도의 언약이 아니었던들, 그들은 이 땅에 마지막 두 사람이 남을 때까지 서로를 도륙했을 것입니다. 그리고 그 마지막 두 사람조차 제 교만 속에서 서로를 억제하지 못하여, 하나가 다른 하나를 죽이고 그런 다음 제 자신마저 죽였을 것입니다. 그리고 온유하고 겸손한 자들을 위하여 그날들을 줄이시리라는 그리스도의 약속이 아니었던들, 그런 일이 일어났을 것입니다.\n\n결투 후 아직 장교의 군복을 입고 있을 무렵, 나는 사교 모임에서 종들에 관하여 이야기했는데, 모두가 나를 보고 놀라워하던 것이 기억납니다. \"뭐라고요!\" 그들이 물었습니다. \"우리더러 종들을 소파에 앉히고 차를 대접하란 말입니까?\" 그래서 나는 그들에게 대답했습니다. \"어찌 안 되겠습니까, 적어도 가끔은 말입니다.\" 모두가 웃었습니다. 그들의 물음은 경박했고 내 대답도 명료하지 못했으나, 그 안에 담긴 생각은 어느 정도 옳았습니다.\n\n젊은이여, 기도를 잊지 마십시오. 그대가 기도할 때마다, 그 기도가 진실하다면, 거기에는 새로운 느낌과 새로운 의미가 깃들 것이며, 그것이 그대에게 새 용기를 줄 것이고, 그대는 기도가 하나의 교육임을 깨닫게 될 것입니다. 또한 날마다, 그리고 할 수 있을 때마다 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌십시오. \"주여, 오늘 당신 앞에 나아오는 모든 이에게 자비를 베푸소서.\" 매시간 매 순간 수천 명의 사람이 이 땅에서 생을 떠나며, 그 영혼들이 하느님 앞에 나아가기 때문입니다. 그리고 그들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 고독 속에, 알아주는 이도 없이, 슬프게, 아무도 자기를 애도해 주지 않고 자기가 살았는지조차 알지 못하는 채로 낙담하여 떠나갑니까! 그런데 보십시오, 어쩌면 땅의 저편 끝에서, 그대가 그들을 알지 못하고 그들도 그대를 알지 못했건만, 그들의 안식을 비는 그대의 기도가 하느님께로 올라갈 것입니다. 주님 앞에 두려움에 떨며 서 있는 한 영혼이, 바로 그 순간 자기를 위해서도 기도해 주는 이가 있음을, 이 땅에 자기를 사랑해 주는 한 동족이 남아 있음을 느낀다는 것이 얼마나 가슴 뭉클한 일이겠습니까! 그리고 하느님께서는 그대들 둘을 더 자비로이 굽어보실 것이니, 그대가 그를 그토록 가엾이 여겼다면, 그대보다 한없이 더 사랑이 많고 자비로우신 그분께서는 얼마나 더 가엾이 여기시겠습니까! 그리고 그분께서는 그대를 보아서 그를 용서하실 것입니다.\n\n형제들이여, 사람의 죄를 두려워하지 마십시오. 사람을 그 죄 가운데서도 사랑하십시오. 그것이 신적 사랑의 모상이며 이 땅에서 가장 높은 사랑이기 때문입니다. 하느님의 모든 피조물을, 그 전체와 그 안의 모래 한 알까지 사랑하십시오. 잎사귀 하나하나를, 하느님 빛의 빛줄기 하나하나를 사랑하십시오. 짐승들을 사랑하고, 식물들을 사랑하고, 모든 것을 사랑하십시오. 그대가 모든 것을 사랑하면, 사물들 속에 깃든 신적 신비를 깨닫게 될 것입니다. 일단 그것을 깨닫고 나면, 날마다 그것을 더 잘 이해하기 시작할 것입니다. 그리고 마침내 그대는 온 세상을 끌어안는 사랑으로 사랑하기에 이를 것입니다. 짐승들을 사랑하십시오. 하느님께서는 그들에게 사유의 단초와 흐트러짐 없는 기쁨을 주셨습니다. 그것을 어지럽히지 말고, 그들을 들볶지 말고, 그들의 행복을 앗아 가지 말고, 하느님의 뜻을 거슬러 일하지 마십시오. 인간이여, 짐승보다 우월하다고 우쭐대지 마십시오. 그들은 죄가 없으나, 그대는 그 위대함을 지니고서도 이 땅에 나타남으로써 땅을 더럽히고, 그대가 지나간 뒤에 그대의 추악한 자취를 남기니—슬프게도, 이것은 우리 거의 모두에게 사실입니다! 특히 아이들을 사랑하십시오. 그들 또한 천사들처럼 죄가 없기 때문입니다. 그들은 우리 마음을 누그러뜨리고 정화하기 위하여, 말하자면 우리를 이끌기 위하여 살아갑니다. 아이를 노엽게 하는 자에게 화가 있을진저! 안핌 신부는 내게 아이들을 사랑하라고 가르쳐 주었습니다. 그 친절하고 과묵한 사람은 우리의 떠돌이 길에서 사람들이 우리에게 준 동전들을 자주 아이들에게 줄 사탕이며 과자에 썼습니다. 그는 아이를 보면 마음이 동하지 않고서는 그냥 지나치지 못했습니다. 그것이 그 사람의 천성이었습니다.\n\n어떤 생각들 앞에서는 사람이 어찌할 바를 몰라 망설이게 되니, 특히 사람의 죄를 목격할 때, 힘을 써야 할지 겸손한 사랑을 써야 할지 의아해집니다. 언제나 겸손한 사랑을 쓰기로 결단하십시오. 그것을 단번에 영영 결단한다면, 그대는 온 세상을 굴복시킬 수 있습니다. 사랑이 깃든 겸손은 경이로울 만큼 강하며, 모든 것 가운데 가장 강하니, 그것에 견줄 만한 것은 달리 없습니다.\n\n날마다 매시간 매분, 그대 자신을 돌아보며 그대 자신을 살피고, 그대의 형상이 단정한지 보십시오. 그대가 어린아이 곁을 지나는데, 악의를 품고, 추한 말을 내뱉으며, 분노한 마음으로 지나친다 합시다. 그대는 그 아이를 알아채지 못했을지 몰라도 그 아이는 그대를 보았으며, 단정치 못하고 비루한 그대의 형상이 그 무방비한 마음에 남을지도 모릅니다. 그대는 그것을 알지 못하지만, 어쩌면 그대는 그 아이 안에 악의 씨앗을 뿌렸을지 모르며, 그것이 자라날 수도 있으니, 모두가 그대가 아이 앞에서 조심하지 않았기 때문이요, 그대 안에 조심스럽고 능동적으로 자애로운 사랑을 길러 두지 않았기 때문입니다. 형제들이여, 사랑은 스승이지만, 그것을 어떻게 얻는지 알아야 합니다. 그것은 얻기 어렵고, 비싼 값을 치러야 하며, 오랜 수고로 더디게 쟁취되기 때문입니다. 우리는 어쩌다 한 번, 한순간만 사랑할 것이 아니라 영원토록 사랑해야 하기 때문입니다. 어쩌다 한 번 사랑하는 것이라면 누구나 할 수 있고, 악한 자조차 할 수 있습니다.\n\n내 형은 새들에게 자기를 용서해 달라고 빌었습니다. 그것은 분별없는 소리로 들리지만 옳은 일입니다. 만물은 바다와 같아서 모든 것이 흐르며 섞이고, 한곳을 건드리면 땅의 저편 끝에서 움직임이 일어나기 때문입니다. 새들에게 용서를 빈다는 것이 분별없는 일일지 모르나, 그대가 지금보다 더 고결하다면 새들도 그대 곁에서 더 행복할 것이요—어쨌든 조금은 더 행복할 것이며—아이들도, 모든 짐승도 그러할 것입니다. 거듭 말하건대, 만물은 바다와 같습니다. 그러면 그대 또한 모든 것을 끌어안는 사랑에 사로잡혀, 일종의 황홀경 속에서 새들에게도 기도하며, 그들 또한 그대의 죄를 용서해 주기를 빌게 될 것입니다. 사람들에게는 그것이 아무리 분별없어 보일지라도, 그 환희를 소중히 간직하십시오.\n\n벗들이여, 기쁨을 얻고자 하느님께 기도하십시오. 어린아이처럼, 하늘의 새들처럼 기뻐하십시오. 그리고 그대들이 하는 일에서 사람의 죄가 그대들을 어지럽히지 못하게 하십시오. 그것이 그대들의 일을 닳아 없애고 그 성취를 가로막으리라고 두려워하지 마십시오. \"죄는 강력하고, 사악함은 강력하며, 악한 환경은 강력하건만, 우리는 외롭고 무력하니, 악한 환경이 우리를 닳아 없애며 우리의 선한 일이 이루어지는 것을 가로막는다\"고 말하지 마십시오. 그 낙담에서 달아나십시오, 아이들이여! 구원의 길은 오직 하나뿐이니, 그대 자신을 붙들어 모든 사람의 죄에 대하여 스스로 책임을 지도록 하십시오. 그것이 진리입니다, 벗들이여. 그대가 진실로 모든 것에 대하여, 모든 사람에 대하여 스스로 책임을 지자마자, 그대는 그것이 정말로 그러하며 그대가 모든 이에게, 모든 일에 대하여 죄가 있음을 단번에 알게 될 것이기 때문입니다. 그러나 그대 자신의 나태와 무력을 남에게 떠넘기다 보면, 그대는 결국 사탄의 교만에 가담하여 하느님께 불평을 늘어놓기에 이를 것입니다.\n\n사탄의 교만에 관하여 내가 생각하는 바는 이러합니다. 그것은 이 땅의 우리로서는 헤아리기 어려운 것이므로, 거기에 빠져 그것에 가담하기가 그토록 쉬우며, 심지어 우리가 무언가 장엄하고 훌륭한 일을 하고 있다고 착각하기까지 합니다. 실로 우리 본성의 가장 강한 감정과 충동 가운데 많은 것을 우리는 이 땅에서 헤아릴 수 없습니다. 그것이 걸림돌이 되게 하지 말며, 그것이 그대에게 무엇에 대한 변명으로 쓰일 수 있다고 생각하지 마십시오. 영원하신 심판자께서는 그대에게 헤아릴 수 없는 것이 아니라 헤아릴 수 있는 것을 물으시기 때문입니다. 그대는 훗날 그것을 스스로 알게 될 것이니, 그때에는 모든 것을 참되이 보게 되어 그것들에 시비를 걸지 않을 것이기 때문입니다. 참으로 이 땅에서 우리는 말하자면 길 잃은 자와 같으며, 우리 앞에 그리스도의 귀한 형상이 없었던들 우리는 홍수 이전의 인류가 그러했듯이 파멸하여 송두리째 길을 잃었을 것입니다. 이 땅에서는 많은 것이 우리에게 감추어져 있으나, 그것을 메우고자 우리에게는 다른 세계, 더 높은 천상의 세계와 우리가 살아 있는 채로 맺은 유대에 대한 귀한 신비적 감각이 주어졌으며, 우리 생각과 느낌의 뿌리는 여기가 아니라 다른 세계들에 있습니다. 철학자들이 우리가 이 땅에서 사물의 실재를 파악할 수 없다고 말하는 것은 바로 그 때문입니다.\n\n하느님께서는 여러 세계로부터 씨앗들을 가져다 이 땅에 뿌리셨고, 그분의 정원이 자라나 자라날 수 있는 모든 것이 돋아났으나, 자라나는 것은 다른 신비로운 세계들과 맞닿아 있다는 느낌을 통해서만 살아 있고 또 살아갑니다. 만일 그대 안에서 그 느낌이 약해지거나 사라진다면, 그대 안의 천상적 성장도 시들어 버릴 것입니다. 그러면 그대는 삶에 무관심해지고, 심지어 삶을 미워하기에 이를 것입니다. 그것이 내 생각입니다.\n\n그대가 누구도 심판할 수 없음을 특히 명심하십시오. 자기가 자기 앞에 서 있는 사람과 똑같은 죄인이며, 어쩌면 그 죄에 대하여 자기야말로 모든 사람보다 더 책임이 있음을 깨닫기 전까지는, 그 누구도 죄인을 심판할 수 없기 때문입니다. 그것을 이해할 때에야 그는 심판자가 될 수 있을 것입니다. 그것이 터무니없게 들릴지라도 그것은 참입니다. 만일 나 자신이 의로웠던들, 어쩌면 내 앞에 서 있는 죄인은 없었을 것입니다. 만일 그대 마음이 심판하고 있는 그 죄인의 죄를 그대가 떠맡을 수 있다면, 당장 그것을 떠맡아 그를 위하여 그대 자신이 고통받고, 그를 책망 없이 보내 주십시오. 그리고 설령 법 자체가 그대를 그의 심판자로 세운다 할지라도, 할 수 있는 한 같은 정신으로 행하십시오. 그는 떠나가 그대가 한 것보다 더 쓰라리게 자기 자신을 단죄할 것이기 때문입니다. 만일 그대의 입맞춤에도 그가 마음 동하지 않은 채 그대를 비웃으며 떠나간다 해도, 그것이 그대에게 걸림돌이 되게 하지 마십시오. 그것은 그의 때가 아직 이르지 않았음을 보여 줄 뿐이며, 그 때는 머지않아 올 것입니다. 그리고 설령 그 때가 오지 않더라도 상관없으니, 그가 아니면 그를 대신할 또 다른 이가 이해하고 고통받으며, 자기 자신을 심판하고 단죄할 것이요, 진리는 이루어질 것입니다. 그것을 믿으십시오, 의심 없이 믿으십시오. 성인들의 모든 희망과 신앙이 바로 그 안에 있기 때문입니다.\n\n쉼 없이 일하십시오. 밤에 잠자리에 들며 \"나는 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하지 않았다\"고 떠오른다면, 당장 일어나 그것을 하십시오. 그대 주위의 사람들이 악의에 차고 냉담하여 그대의 말을 들으려 하지 않거든, 그들 앞에 엎드려 용서를 비십시오. 참으로 그들이 그대의 말을 들으려 하지 않는 것은 그대에게 책임이 있기 때문입니다. 그리고 그들의 쓰라림 속에서 그들에게 말을 건넬 수 없거든, 침묵과 겸손 가운데 그들을 섬기되 결코 희망을 잃지 마십시오. 만일 모든 사람이 그대를 저버리고 심지어 힘으로 그대를 내쫓는다 해도, 그때 홀로 남거든 땅에 엎드려 그것에 입 맞추고 그대의 눈물로 그것을 적시십시오, 그리하면 비록 그대의 고독을 본 이도 들은 이도 없을지라도 땅은 열매를 맺을 것입니다. 끝까지 믿으십시오, 설령 모든 사람이 길을 잃고 그대만이 신실한 자로 남았다 할지라도. 그때에도 그대의 제물을 바치고 그대의 고독 속에서 하느님을 찬양하십시오. 그리고 그대들 둘이 함께 모인다면—그곳에는 온전한 하나의 세계가, 살아 있는 사랑의 세계가 있습니다. 서로를 다정히 끌어안고 하느님을 찬양하십시오. 비록 그대들 둘 안에서만이라도 그분의 진리가 이루어졌기 때문입니다.\n\n만일 그대 자신이 죄를 짓고 그대의 죄로 인하여, 또는 그대의 갑작스러운 죄로 인하여 죽을 듯이 슬퍼한다면, 그때에는 다른 이들을 위하여 기뻐하고, 의로운 사람을 위하여 기뻐하며, 그대가 죄를 지었어도 그는 의로워 죄를 짓지 않았음을 기뻐하십시오.\n\n만일 사람들의 악행이 그대를 분노와 압도적인 비탄으로, 심지어 악행자들에 대한 복수의 욕망으로까지 몰아간다면, 무엇보다도 그 감정을 피하십시오. 당장 가서, 마치 그대 자신이 그 악행의 죄를 지은 것인 양 그대 자신을 위한 고통을 구하십시오. 그 고통을 받아들이고 견디면 그대의 마음은 위안을 얻을 것이며, 그대 또한 죄가 있음을 깨닫게 될 것이니, 그대가 죄 없는 한 사람으로서 악행자들에게 빛이 되어 줄 수도 있었건만 그들에게 빛이 되어 주지 못했기 때문입니다. 만일 그대가 빛이었던들, 다른 이들을 위해서도 길을 밝혀 주었을 것이요, 그 악행자도 어쩌면 그대의 빛으로 인하여 자기 죄에서 구원받았을지 모릅니다. 그리고 비록 그대의 빛이 빛나고 있었음에도 사람들이 그것으로 구원받지 못한 것을 그대가 본다 할지라도, 굳건히 버티며 천상의 빛의 힘을 의심하지 마십시오. 만일 그들이 구원받지 못했다면 훗날에 구원받으리라고 믿으십시오. 그리고 만일 그들이 훗날에도 구원받지 못한다면 그들의 자손들이 구원받을 것이니, 그대의 빛은 그대가 죽은 뒤에도 사라지지 않을 것이기 때문입니다. 의로운 사람은 떠나가도 그의 빛은 남습니다. 사람들은 언제나 구원자가 죽은 뒤에 구원을 받습니다. 사람들은 자기 예언자들을 거부하고 그들을 죽이지만, 자기 순교자들은 사랑하며 자기가 죽인 이들을 떠받듭니다. 그대는 전체를 위하여 일하고 있으며, 미래를 위하여 행하고 있는 것입니다. 보상을 구하지 마십시오. 이 땅에서 그대의 보상은 크기 때문이니, 곧 의로운 사람에게만 허락되는 영적 기쁨입니다. 위대한 자도 권세 있는 자도 두려워하지 말고, 지혜로우며 늘 평온하십시오. 분수를 알고, 때를 알며, 그것을 익히십시오. 홀로 남거든 기도하십시오. 땅에 몸을 던져 그것에 입 맞추기를 즐기십시오. 땅에 입 맞추고 쉼 없이 타오르는 사랑으로 그것을 사랑하십시오. 모든 사람을 사랑하고, 모든 것을 사랑하십시오. 그 환희와 황홀경을 구하십시오. 그대의 기쁨의 눈물로 땅을 적시고 그 눈물을 사랑하십시오. 그 황홀경을 부끄러워하지 말고 소중히 여기십시오. 그것은 하느님의 선물이며 위대한 선물이기 때문이니, 그것은 많은 이에게가 아니라 오직 택함받은 자에게만 주어집니다.\n\n신부님들과 스승님들이여, 나는 \"지옥이란 무엇인가?\"를 곰곰이 생각합니다. 나는 그것이 사랑할 수 없음의 고통이라고 단언합니다. 시간과 공간으로 헤아릴 수 없는 무한한 존재 가운데, 어느 영적 피조물이 이 땅에 나아올 때 \"나는 존재하며 나는 사랑한다\"고 말할 능력을 한 번 부여받았습니다. 한 번, 오직 한 번, 그에게는 능동적으로 살아 있는 사랑의 한순간이 주어졌고, 그것을 위하여 그에게 지상의 삶이 주어졌으며, 그와 더불어 때와 시절이 주어졌습니다. 그런데 그 행복한 피조물은 그 값을 매길 수 없는 선물을 거부하고, 그것을 소중히 여기지도 사랑하지도 않으며, 그것을 멸시하고 냉담한 채로 남았습니다. 그런 자는 이 땅을 떠난 뒤, 부자와 나사로의 비유에서 우리에게 일러 주는 대로, 아브라함의 품을 보고 아브라함과 이야기를 나누며, 천국을 바라보고 주님께로 올라갈 수도 있습니다. 그러나 바로 그것이 그의 고통이니, 한 번도 사랑해 본 적 없이 주님께로 올라가는 것이요, 그들의 사랑을 멸시해 놓고서 사랑했던 이들 가까이로 이끌려 가는 것입니다. 그는 똑똑히 보면서 스스로에게 말하기 때문입니다. \"이제 나는 깨달았으나, 비록 내가 지금 사랑하기를 갈망한들, 내 사랑에는 아무런 위대함도, 아무런 희생도 없을 것이니, 나의 지상의 삶이 끝났고, 아브라함은 지금 내 안에 타오르는 영적 사랑의 불 같은 갈증을 식혀 줄 살아 있는 물 한 방울조차(그것이 곧 지상의 능동적 삶이라는 선물이다) 들고 오지 않을 것이기 때문이다. 비록 내가 이 땅에서 그것을 멸시했을지라도 말이다. 내게는 더 이상 삶이 없으며 더 이상 때도 없으리라! 비록 내가 기꺼이 다른 이들을 위하여 내 생명을 바치고자 한들 그것은 결코 이루어질 수 없으니, 사랑을 위하여 희생될 수 있었던 그 삶은 이미 지나갔고, 이제 그 삶과 이 존재 사이에는 건널 수 없는 심연이 가로놓여 있기 때문이다.\"\n\n사람들은 물질적인 의미에서의 지옥불을 이야기합니다. 나는 그 신비 속으로 들어가지 않으며 그것을 피합니다. 그러나 내 생각에, 만일 물질적인 의미의 불이 있다면 그들은 도리어 그것을 반길 것이니, 짐작건대 물질적 고통 속에서라면 그들의 더욱더 큰 영적 고통이 한순간이나마 잊힐 것이기 때문입니다. 더욱이 그 영적 고통은 그들에게서 거두어 갈 수 없으니, 그 고통은 외부의 것이 아니라 그들 안에 있는 것이기 때문입니다. 그리고 설령 그것을 그들에게서 거두어 갈 수 있다 해도, 내 생각에 그것은 그 불행한 피조물들에게 더욱더 쓰라린 일이 될 것입니다. 비록 낙원의 의로운 자들이 그들의 고통을 굽어보며 그들을 용서하고, 그 무한한 사랑으로 그들을 천국으로 불러올린다 해도, 그것은 그들의 고통을 곱절로 늘릴 따름이기 때문이니, 이제는 불가능해진, 응답하고 능동적이며 감사할 줄 아는 사랑을 향한 타오르는 갈증을 그들 안에 더욱 날카롭게 불러일으킬 것이기 때문입니다. 그러나 마음의 소심함 속에서나마 나는, 바로 이 불가능함을 인정하는 것이 마침내 그들에게 위안이 되어 주리라고 상상합니다. 의로운 자들의 사랑을 그것을 갚을 수 없다는 불가능함과 더불어 받아들임으로써, 이 순종과 이 겸손함의 작용으로, 그들은 마침내, 말하자면 그들이 생전에 멸시했던 그 능동적 사랑의 어떤 모상에, 그 사랑의 외적 표현 같은 무언가에 이르게 될 것이기 때문입니다…… 벗들이여, 형제들이여, 내가 이것을 명료하게 표현하지 못하는 것이 미안합니다. 그러나 이 땅에서 스스로 목숨을 끊은 자들에게 화가 있을진저, 자살한 자들에게 화가 있을진저! 나는 그들보다 더 비참한 이는 있을 수 없다고 믿습니다. 사람들은 우리에게 그들을 위하여 기도하는 것이 죄라고 말하며, 겉으로 교회는 말하자면 그들을 내치는 듯하지만, 나는 내 은밀한 마음속으로 우리가 그들을 위해서도 기도할 수 있다고 믿습니다. 사랑이 그리스도께 거스르는 일이 될 수는 결코 없습니다. 그런 이들을 위하여 나는 평생토록 마음속으로 기도해 왔음을, 신부님들과 스승님들이여, 나는 고백하며, 지금도 날마다 그들을 위하여 기도합니다.\n\n오, 지옥에서조차, 절대적 진리를 분명히 알고 바라보면서도 여전히 교만하고 사나운 채로 남아 있는 자들이 있으며, 사탄과 그의 교만한 정신에 자기를 송두리째 내맡긴 무서운 자들이 있습니다. 그런 자들에게 지옥은 자발적이며 끝없이 타오르는 것이니, 그들은 자기 자신의 선택으로 고통받습니다. 그들은 하느님과 삶을 저주하며 자기 자신을 저주했기 때문입니다. 그들은 마치 사막에서 굶주린 자가 제 몸에서 피를 빨아 먹듯이 자기의 앙심에 찬 교만을 양식 삼아 살아갑니다. 그러나 그들은 결코 만족하지 못하며 용서를 거부하고, 자기를 부르시는 하느님을 저주합니다. 그들은 살아 계신 하느님을 증오 없이는 바라보지 못하며, 생명의 하느님이 소멸되어야 한다고, 하느님께서 당신 자신과 당신 자신의 피조물을 멸하셔야 한다고 부르짖습니다. 그리고 그들은 영원토록 자기 자신의 분노의 불 속에서 타오르며 죽음과 소멸을 갈망할 것입니다. 그러나 그들은 죽음에 이르지 못할 것입니다……\n\n여기에서 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프의 수기가 끝난다. 거듭 말하거니와, 그것은 미완이며 단편적이다. 이를테면 전기적 세부 사항은 조시마 신부의 가장 이른 젊은 시절만을 다루고 있다. 그의 가르침과 견해에 관해서는 분명 서로 매우 다른 자리에서 발설된 말들이 한데 묶여 있는 것을 보게 된다. 마지막 몇 시간 동안의 그의 발언들은 나머지와 따로 구분되어 있지 않으나, 그 전반적 성격은 알렉세이 표도로비치의 수기에 담긴 내용에서 미루어 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n장로의 죽음은 결국 전혀 예기치 못하게 찾아왔다. 그 마지막 저녁 그의 곁에 모여 있던 이들이 그의 죽음이 다가오고 있음을 알아차리기는 했으나, 그래도 그것이 그토록 갑작스레 닥치리라고는 상상하기 어려웠던 것이다. 도리어 그의 벗들은, 내가 앞서 말한 대로, 그날 밤 그가 보아하니 그토록 쾌활하고 말이 많은 것을 보고는, 적어도 그의 상태에 일시적인 호전이 있는 것이라고 확신했다. 그가 죽기 오 분 전까지도 그것을 내다보기란 불가능했다고 그들은 나중에 의아해하며 말했다. 그는 갑자기 가슴에 날카로운 통증을 느끼는 듯하더니, 얼굴이 창백해지며 두 손을 심장에 갖다 댔다. 모두가 자리에서 일어나 그에게로 달려갔다. 그러나 고통스러워하면서도 그는 여전히 미소를 띤 채 그들을 바라보았고, 의자에서 천천히 미끄러져 무릎을 꿇더니, 얼굴을 땅에 숙이고 두 팔을 뻗어, 마치 기쁨에 찬 황홀경에 잠긴 듯 기도하며 땅에 입 맞추고는, 조용하고 기쁘게 자기 영혼을 하느님께 바쳤다.\n\n그의 죽음에 관한 소식은 곧장 암자 전체로 퍼져 수도원에까지 이르렀다. 고인의 가장 가까운 벗들과 그 지위로 보아 그것이 임무인 이들이 옛 의식에 따라 시신을 안치하기 시작했고, 모든 수도승이 성당에 한데 모였다. 그리고 동이 트기 전에 그 죽음의 소식이 읍내에 다다랐다. 아침이 되자 온 읍내가 그 일을 이야기했고, 사람들이 읍내에서 수도원으로 떼 지어 몰려들었다. 그러나 이 화제는 다음 권에서 다루기로 하겠다. 여기서는 다만 하루가 채 지나기도 전에, 수도승들과 읍내 사람들에게 미친 영향에 있어 너무도 예기치 못하고, 너무도 기이하며, 동요를 일으키고 어리둥절하게 만드는 어떤 일이 벌어졌으며, 그래서 이 모든 세월이 지난 지금까지도 그 온통 긴장에 휩싸였던 날이 읍내에서 여전히 생생히 기억되고 있다는 것만을 덧붙여 두겠다.\n\n# PART III Book VII. Alyosha Chapter I. The Breath Of Corruption\n\n조시마 장로의 유해는 정해진 의식에 따라 매장 준비가 이루어졌다. 널리 알려진 대로 죽은 수도사와 은수자의 시신은 씻기지 않는다. 교회 의식서의 말을 빌리면 이러하다. \"수도사들 가운데 누구든 주 안에서 잠들거든, 지명을 받은 수도사가(곧 그 일을 맡은 자가) 따뜻한 물로 시신을 닦되, 먼저 해면으로 고인의 이마와 가슴과 두 손과 두 발과 무릎에 십자 표시를 그으면 그것으로 족하니라.\" 이 모든 일을 파이시 신부가 손수 행했고, 그런 다음 고인에게 수도복을 입히고 망토로 감쌌는데, 그 망토는 관습에 따라 십자 모양으로 시신을 감싸 접을 수 있도록 일부가 트여 있었다. 머리에는 여덟 모 십자가가 달린 두건을 씌웠다. 두건은 열어 둔 채, 죽은 이의 얼굴은 검은 망사로 덮었다. 두 손에는 구세주의 이콘을 쥐어 주었다. 새벽녘에 이르러 고인은 오래전에 이미 마련해 둔 관 속에 안치되었다. 관은 하루 종일 암자에, 곧 장로가 평소 방문객들과 동료 수도사들을 맞이하던 더 큰 방에 그대로 두기로 결정되었다. 고인이 가장 엄격한 계율을 지키는 사제이자 수도사였으므로, 그 시신 위에서는 시편이 아니라 복음서를 사제 서품을 받은 수도사들이 읽어야만 했다. 낭독은 위령미사가 끝난 직후 이오시프 신부가 시작했다. 파이시 신부는 나중에 죽은 벗의 시신 위에서 밤낮으로 복음서를 읽고 싶어 했으나, 당장은 그도 은수처의 원장 신부와 마찬가지로 몹시 바쁘고 분주했으니, 무언가 심상치 않은, 전에 없던, 심지어 \"온당치 못한\" 흥분과 안달 난 기대가 수도사들 사이에서, 또 수도원 숙소에서 온 방문객들과 읍내에서 떼 지어 몰려든 군중 사이에서 드러나기 시작했기 때문이다. 그리고 시간이 흐를수록 이런 분위기는 점점 더 뚜렷해졌다. 원장도 파이시 신부도 이 전반적인 소란과 동요를 가라앉히려고 온 힘을 다했다.\n\n날이 완전히 밝자, 어떤 이들은 읍내에서 병자들을, 대개는 아이들을 데리고 오기 시작했으니—마치 일부러 이 순간을 기다리기라도 한 듯했고, 죽은 장로의 유해에 치유의 힘이 깃들어 있어 자기들의 믿음에 따라 즉시 그 힘이 나타나리라고 분명히 확신하는 모양이었다. 우리 고장 사람들 모두가 생전의 조시마 장로를 의심할 바 없이 위대한 성자로 받아들이고 있었다는 사실이 그제야 비로소 분명히 드러난 것이다. 그리고 찾아온 이들이 결코 하나같이 미천한 계층만은 아니었다.\n\n이토록 서둘러, 이토록 노골적으로, 심지어 안달과 거의 강요에 가까운 태도로 드러나는 신자들의 이 강렬한 기대가 파이시 신부에게는 온당치 못하게만 비쳤다. 그는 이런 일이 있으리라고 진작부터 어느 정도 내다보고는 있었으나, 막상 그 감정이 실제로 표출되는 양상은 그가 예상했던 것을 훨씬 넘어서는 것이었다. 이런 흥분을 드러내는 수도사를 마주칠 때마다 파이시 신부는 그들을 나무라기 시작했다. \"무언가 비범한 일을 그렇게 곧장 기대하는 것은,\" 그가 말했다, \"세속 사람들에게나 있을 법한 경박함이지 우리에게는 온당치 못한 짓이오.\"\n\n그러나 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 이는 거의 없었고, 파이시 신부는 그것을 불안하게 알아차렸다. 그러면서도 정작 그 자신은(모든 진실을 털어놓아야 한다면) 남몰래 마음 깊은 곳에서 거의 같은 희망을 품고 있었고, 자기 주위의 너무도 안달 난 기대에 분개하며 그 안에서 경박함과 허영을 보면서도, 자기 안의 그 희망을 의식하지 않을 수 없었다. 그럼에도 그에게 특히 불쾌했던 것은 어떤 사람들과 마주치는 일이었으니, 그들의 존재는 그의 마음속에 커다란 의구심을 불러일으켰다. 죽은 이의 암자에 모여든 군중 가운데서 그는 내심 거부감을 느끼며(그러고는 곧장 스스로를 책망하며) 라키틴과, 아직 수도원에 머물고 있는 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사가 와 있음을 알아차렸다. 어쩐 일인지 파이시 신부는 그 두 사람에 대해 갑자기 의심을 품게 되었는데—하기야 다른 이들에게도 똑같은 의심을 품을 만했을 것이다.\n\n오브도르스크에서 온 수도사는 흥분한 군중 가운데서도 가장 부산스러운 자로 눈에 띄었다. 그는 어디서나 모습을 드러냈고, 어디서나 이것저것 캐물었으며, 어디서나 귀를 기울였고, 사방에서 묘하고 비밀스러운 기색으로 속닥거렸다. 그의 얼굴에는 더없는 안달과 심지어 일종의 짜증마저 떠올라 있었다.\n\n라키틴으로 말하자면, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 그가 그렇게 일찍 은수처에 온 것은 호흘라코바 부인의 특별한 부탁 때문이었다. 마음씨는 착하나 머리가 모자란 이 부인은—자기 몸으로는 은수처에 들어올 수도 없었거니와—잠에서 깨어 조시마 장로의 죽음을 전해 듣자마자 어찌나 강렬한 호기심에 사로잡혔던지, 즉시 라키틴을 은수처로 보내어 주의 깊게 동정을 살피고 반 시간마다 한 번씩 \"일어나는 일을 모조리\" 편지로 보고하게 했다. 부인은 라키틴을 더없이 신앙심 깊고 독실한 청년으로 여기고 있었다. 그는 사람들의 비위를 맞추고, 거기서 조금이라도 자기에게 득 될 것을 알아채면 그들 입맛에 가장 맞으리라 여겨지는 배역이라면 무엇이든 능청스레 연기하는 데 유난히 능했다.\n\n화창하고 맑은 날이었고, 방문객들 가운데 많은 이들이 무덤들 주위로 몰려들었는데, 그 무덤들은 특히 교회 둘레에 빽빽이 모여 있었고 은수처 여기저기에도 흩어져 있었다. 파이시 신부는 은수처를 한 바퀴 돌다가 알료샤를 떠올렸고, 그를 못 본 지 한참 되었다는 것을, 그날 밤 이후로 보지 못했다는 것을 생각해 냈다. 그런데 그를 떠올리기가 무섭게 곧 은수처 정원의 가장 외진 구석에서, 오래전 성덕으로 이름났던 어느 수도사의 묘비 위에 앉아 있는 그를 알아보았다. 알료샤는 은수처를 등지고 담벼락을 향해 앉아 있어서, 묘비 뒤에 숨어 있는 듯이 보였다. 가까이 다가간 파이시 신부는 그가 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 없이, 그러나 비통하게 울고 있으며 온몸이 흐느낌으로 떨리고 있는 것을 보았다. 파이시 신부는 잠시 그를 굽어보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"됐다, 얘야, 됐어, 이 사람아.\" 마침내 그가 정에 겨운 목소리로 말했다. \"왜 우느냐? 기뻐할 일이지 울 일이 아니다. 오늘이 그분에게는 가장 큰 날이라는 것을 모르느냐? 지금 이 순간 그분이 어디에 계신지를 생각해 보아라!\"\n\n알료샤는 그를 흘긋 쳐다보며 얼굴을 드러냈는데, 아이처럼 울어서 퉁퉁 부어 있는 그 얼굴을 보이더니, 곧 한마디도 하지 못한 채 다시 고개를 돌리고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그것도 좋은 일이겠지.\" 파이시 신부가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"울어야겠거든 울려무나, 그리스도께서 너에게 그 눈물을 보내 주신 것이니라.\"\n\n\"네 가슴 저린 눈물은 네 영혼을 달래 주고 네 갸륵한 마음을 기쁘게 해 줄 따름이다.\" 그는 알료샤에게서 물러나며 속으로 이렇게 덧붙였고, 그를 애틋하게 생각했다. 그러나 그는 빠르게 자리를 떴으니, 그를 바라보다가는 자기마저 울고 말 것 같았기 때문이다.\n\n그러는 동안 시간은 흘러갔다. 수도원의 예배와 고인을 위한 위령미사가 순서에 따라 차례로 이어졌다. 파이시 신부는 다시 이오시프 신부를 대신해 관 곁에 자리를 잡고 복음서를 읽기 시작했다. 그러나 오후 세 시도 되기 전에, 내가 앞 권 말미에서 넌지시 비친 그 일이 벌어졌으니, 우리 모두에게는 너무도 뜻밖이었고 모두의 기대와는 너무도 어긋나는 것이어서, 거듭 말하거니와 이 사소한 사건은 오늘날까지도 우리 읍내와 인근 온 동네에서 시시콜콜하게 기억되고 있다. 여기서 나 개인으로서 덧붙여 두자면, 그토록 경박한 동요를 일으키고 많은 이들에게 그토록 큰 걸림돌이 된 그 사건을 떠올리는 것이 거의 역겹게까지 느껴지거니와, 실상 그것은 더없이 자연스럽고 사소한 일이었을 뿐이다. 물론 나는 이 이야기에서 그 일을 일절 언급하지 않고 넘어갔을 것이나, 그것이 내 이야기의 주인공이자—비록 장차의 일이지만—주인공인 알료샤의 가슴과 영혼에 매우 강한 영향을 끼쳐, 그의 정신적 성장에 한 위기이자 전환점을 이루었고 그의 지성에 충격을 가하여, 그 충격이 마침내 그의 남은 평생토록 지성을 단단하게 다지고 그것에 분명한 목표를 부여했기 때문이다.\n\n자, 그러면 우리 이야기로 돌아가 보자. 동트기 전에 조시마 장로의 유해를 관에 누이고 그것을 앞방으로 옮겨 왔을 때, 관 주위에 있던 이들 사이에서 창문을 열어야 하지 않겠느냐는 문제가 제기되었다. 그러나 누군가가 무심코 던진 이 제안에는 아무도 대답하지 않았고 거의 주목하지도 않았다. 그 자리에 있던 이들 가운데 몇몇은 어쩌면 속으로 그 말을 새겼을지도 모르나, 그저 이만한 성자의 시신에서 부패와 썩는 냄새를 예상한다는 것은 그야말로 터무니없는 일이며, 그것이 내비치는 믿음의 부족과 경박함에 대해 측은함을(웃음까지는 아니더라도) 자아낼 만하다고 마음속으로 생각했을 따름이다. 사람들이 기대한 것은 전혀 다른 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n그런데 보라, 정오가 조금 지나자 무언가의 징후가 나타났으니, 처음에는 드나들던 이들이 말없이 알아차리기만 할 뿐, 저마다 제 머릿속의 생각을 입 밖에 내기를 분명히 두려워하는 눈치였다. 그러나 세 시에 이르자 그 징후들이 너무도 또렷하고 의심할 여지가 없게 되어, 그 소식은 삽시간에 은수처의 모든 수도사와 방문객에게 퍼졌고, 곧장 수도원까지 파고들어 모든 수도사를 경악에 빠뜨렸으며, 마침내 그야말로 눈 깜짝할 사이에 읍내로 번져, 신자건 불신자건 그곳의 모든 사람을 들끓게 했다. 불신자들은 기뻐했고, 신자들로 말하자면 그 가운데 어떤 이들은 불신자보다 더 기뻐하기까지 했으니, 죽은 장로가 어느 설교에서 말했듯이 \"사람들은 의인의 몰락과 치욕을 사랑하기\" 때문이다.\n\n사실인즉, 관에서 썩는 냄새가 풍기기 시작했고 그 냄새가 차츰 짙어지더니 세 시쯤에는 도무지 의심할 수 없을 지경이 되었던 것이다. 우리 수도원의 지난 역사를 통틀어 이런 추문은 떠올릴 수조차 없었고, 다른 어떤 상황에서도 이런 추문은 일어날 수 없었을 터인데, 이 발견이 있은 직후 다름 아닌 수도사들 자신 사이에서 온당치 못한 무질서로 그것이 모습을 드러냈던 것이다. 훗날, 여러 해가 지난 뒤에까지도, 분별 있는 몇몇 수도사들은 그날을 돌이켜 볼 때마다 추문이 어찌 그토록 큰 규모에 이를 수 있었는지 놀라고 또 몸서리쳤다. 지난날에도 더없이 거룩한 삶을 산 수도사들이, 모두가 성덕을 인정하던 경건한 노인들이 죽어 갔고, 그들의 소박한 관에서도 여느 시신과 마찬가지로 자연스레 썩는 냄새가 풍겼지만, 그것이 추문은커녕 조금의 동요조차 일으킨 적이 없었다. 물론 지난날에도 수도원에는, 그 기억이 정성껏 보존되고 전승에 따르면 그 유골에 부패의 흔적이 전혀 없었다는 성자들이 있었다. 수도사들은 이 사실을 가슴 뭉클하고 신비로운 일로 여겼고, 그 전승을 무언가 복되고 기적적인 것으로, 또 하느님의 은총으로 그 무덤들에서 장차 더욱 큰 영광이 비롯되리라는 약속으로 소중히 간직했다.\n\n그런 이들 가운데 특히 기억이 소중히 간직된 한 사람은 욥이라는 노수도사로, 칠십 년 전에 백다섯 살의 나이로 세상을 떠난 인물이었다. 그는 단식과 침묵에 엄격하기로 이름난 고행자였고, 그의 무덤은 새로 도착한 모든 방문객에게 각별한 경의와 함께, 그것과 결부된 큰 희망에 대한 신비로운 암시와 함께 가리켜 보여지곤 했다.(바로 그 무덤이, 아침에 파이시 신부가 알료샤가 앉아 있는 것을 발견한 그 무덤이었다.) 수도원에서 소중히 간직된 또 하나의 기억은 이름난 바르소노피 신부에 관한 것이었으니, 그는 비교적 최근에 세상을 떠나 조시마 장로보다 앞서 장로직을 맡았던 인물이었다. 그는 생전에 수도원을 찾는 모든 순례자들로부터 광인 같은 성자로 추앙받았다. 전승에 따르면 이 두 사람은 마치 살아 있는 듯이 관 속에 누워 있었고, 묻힐 때에도 부패의 흔적이 전혀 없었으며 그 얼굴에는 거룩한 빛이 어려 있었다고 했다. 더러는 그 시신에서 향긋한 내음마저 풍겼다고 우기는 이들까지 있었다.\n\n그러나 이렇듯 교훈적인 기억들이 있었음에도, 조시마 장로의 관 곁에서 드러난 경박함과 부조리와 악의를 설명하기란 쉽지 않을 것이다. 나 개인의 생각으로는 서로 다른 여러 원인이 동시에 작용하고 있었으니, 그 가운데 하나는 장로 제도를 해로운 폐단으로 여기는 뿌리 깊은 반감, 곧 수많은 수도사들의 가슴 깊숙이 숨어 있던 적의였다. 그보다 더 강력했던 것은 죽은 이의 성덕에 대한 시샘이었으니, 그 성덕이 생전에 너무도 확고히 자리 잡은 나머지 그것을 의심한다는 것이 거의 금기에 가까운 일이 되어 버렸기 때문이다. 죽은 장로가 기적보다는 사랑으로 수많은 이들의 마음을 사로잡았고 자기 둘레에 그를 따르는 무리를 가득 모았다고는 하나, 그럼에도, 아니 오히려 바로 그 까닭에 그는 시샘을 불러일으켰고, 그리하여 수도원 안에서뿐 아니라 바깥세상에서도 은밀한, 또 드러난 모진 원수들을 두게 되었던 것이다. 그는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않았건만, \"어째서 저자를 그토록 거룩하다고 여기는 거지?\" 하는 것이었다. 그리고 차츰 되풀이된 그 물음 하나가 마침내 그에 대한 격렬하고 채울 길 없는 증오를 낳았다. 내가 믿기로는, 그토록 빠르게 풍긴 썩는 냄새에—그가 죽은 지 하루도 채 지나지 않았으니—수많은 사람들이 더없이 기뻐한 까닭도 바로 거기에 있었다. 한편으로는 그때까지 장로를 경건히 따르던 이들 가운데서도, 이 사건에 거의 굴욕을 느끼고 자기가 직접 모욕이라도 당한 듯이 여기는 자들도 있었다. 그 일은 이렇게 벌어졌다.\n\n부패의 징후가 나타나기 시작하자마자, 수도사들의 행색은 그들이 암자에 들어가는 은밀한 속셈을 죄다 드러내 보였다. 그들은 안으로 들어가 잠시 머물다가는, 밖에서 기다리고 있는 다른 수도사 무리에게 소식을 확인해 주려고 서둘러 빠져나왔다. 그 가운데 어떤 이들은 침울하게 고개를 내저었으나, 또 어떤 이들은 심술궂은 눈에 또렷이 번득이는 기쁨을 감추려 들지조차 않았다. 그런데도 이제 아무도 그들을 나무라지 않았고, 아무도 항의의 목소리를 높이지 않았으니, 이는 기이한 일이었다. 대다수 수도사들은 죽은 장로를 따르던 이들이었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 경우에는 하느님께서 한동안 소수가 우위를 차지하도록 내버려 두신 듯했다.\n\n바깥에서 온 방문객들, 특히 교양 있는 계층의 사람들도 곧 똑같이 염탐하려는 속셈으로 암자 안으로 들어갔다. 농민들 가운데서는 암자 안으로 들어간 이가 거의 없었으나, 은수처 문간에는 그들이 떼 지어 몰려 있었다. 세 시가 지나자 세속 방문객들의 발길이 부쩍 늘었는데, 이는 두말할 것 없이 그 충격적인 소식 때문이었다. 그렇지 않았다면 그날 오지 않았을, 올 생각조차 없었던 사람들이 이끌려 왔고, 그 가운데에는 지체 높은 인사들도 더러 있었다. 그러나 겉으로의 예의범절은 그래도 지켜지고 있었고, 파이시 신부는 엄숙한 얼굴로 자기 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 알아차리지 못하는 양 굳건하고 또렷하게 복음서를 소리 내어 계속 읽어 나갔는데, 실은 진작부터 무언가 심상치 않은 낌새를 알아챈 터였다. 그러나 마침내 처음에는 죽인 듯 낮았다가 차츰 커지고 대담해진 수군거림이 그의 귀에까지 닿았다. \"이것은 하느님의 심판이 사람의 심판과 같지 않음을 보여 주는 거요.\" 파이시 신부는 문득 이런 말을 들었다. 이 말을 처음 입 밖에 낸 사람은 평신도, 곧 읍내에서 온 나이 지긋한 관리였는데 신앙심이 매우 깊기로 알려진 인물이었다. 그러나 그는 수도사들이 진작부터 수군거려 온 말을 소리 내어 되풀이했을 뿐이다. 그들은 이미 오래전에 이 단죄의 결론을 정해 두었고, 무엇보다 고약한 것은 그 결론에 대한 일종의 의기양양한 만족감이 순간순간 점점 더 또렷이 드러나고 있었다는 점이다. 머지않아 그들은 겉으로의 예의범절마저 내팽개치기 시작했고, 거의 그것을 내던질 무슨 권리라도 있는 듯이 느끼는 것 같았다.\n\n\"대체 무슨 까닭으로 이런 일이 생긴 걸까.\" 몇몇 수도사들이 처음에는 안타까운 기색을 꾸미며 말했다. \"그분은 몸집도 자그마했고 살이라곤 뼈에 말라붙어 있었는데, 썩을 거리가 뭐가 있다고?\"\n\n\"이건 하늘이 내린 징조임에 틀림없소.\" 다른 이들이 서둘러 덧붙였고, 그들의 의견은 아무런 반대 없이 곧장 받아들여졌다. 그도 그럴 것이, 만일 부패가 죄 지은 모든 죽은 자의 경우처럼 자연스러운 것이었다면 적어도 스물네 시간은 지난 뒤에야 나타났을 텐데, 이렇게 너무 이른 부패는 \"자연의 도를 넘어선\" 것이므로 거기에는 하느님의 손길이 분명하다는 지적까지 나왔던 것이다. 그것은 하나의 징조로 내린 것이었다. 이 결론은 거역할 수 없는 것으로 보였다.\n\n죽은 이가 몹시 아끼던 온화한 사서 이오시프 신부가 험담을 일삼는 자들 가운데 몇몇에게 대꾸하려 애쓰며, \"그건 어디서나 한결같이 받아들여지는 것이 아니오\"라고, 또 의인의 시신이 썩지 않는다는 것은 정교회의 교리가 아니라 다만 하나의 견해일 뿐이며, 가장 정교다운 지방에서도, 이를테면 아토스에서도 부패의 냄새에 그리 당황하지 않으며, 그곳에서는 구원받은 자의 영광을 드러내는 으뜸가는 징표가 육신이 썩지 않는 데 있는 것이 아니라 시신이 여러 해 땅속에 누워 썩은 뒤 그 유골의 빛깔에 있다고 했다. \"그래서 유골이 밀랍처럼 누렇다면, 그것은 주께서 죽은 성자에게 영광을 내리셨다는 위대한 징표이고, 누렇지 않고 검다면, 그것은 하느님께서 그를 그런 영광에 합당하다고 여기지 않으셨음을 보여 주는 것이오—이것이 아토스의 믿음으로, 그곳은 위대한 곳이며 정교의 교리가 예로부터 끊김 없이 가장 순수하게 보존되어 온 곳이오.\" 이오시프 신부가 끝으로 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 온순한 신부의 말은 별다른 효력이 없었고 도리어 비웃는 대꾸만 자아냈다. \"그건 죄다 현학이고 새로 들여온 헛소리니 귀담아들을 게 못 되오.\" 수도사들은 이렇게 단정 지었다. \"우리는 옛 교리를 지킬 뿐이오, 요즘에는 별의별 새로운 것들이 다 나도는데, 그걸 죄다 따라가야 한단 말이오?\" 다른 이들이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"우리에게도 그들 못지않게 거룩한 신부님들이 계셨소. 저들은 투르크인들 틈에 끼여 모든 걸 잊어버렸소. 저들의 교리는 오래전부터 불순해졌고 종조차 없지 않소.\" 가장 비아냥대는 자들이 덧붙였다.\n\n이오시프 신부는 자리를 떴는데, 자기 의견을 그토록 자신 없이, 자기 스스로조차 거의 믿지 않는 듯이 내세웠던 만큼 더욱 마음이 아팠다. 그는 무언가 매우 온당치 못한 일이 시작되고 있으며 분명한 불복종의 징후들이 보인다는 것을 괴롭게 내다보았다. 차츰차츰 분별 있는 수도사들은 모두 이오시프 신부처럼 입을 다물게 되었다. 그리하여 결국, 장로를 사랑하고 경건한 순종으로 장로 제도를 받아들였던 이들은 모두 한순간에 무섭게 풀이 죽어, 서로 마주칠 때면 겁먹은 눈으로 슬며시 상대의 얼굴을 살피게 되었다. 장로 제도를 새것이라 하여 적대하던 자들은 거만하게 고개를 쳐들었다. \"돌아가신 바르소노피 장로한테서는 썩는 냄새가 아니라 향긋한 내음이 풍겼지.\" 그들은 심술궂게 떠올렸다. \"하지만 그분이 그 영광을 얻은 건 장로여서가 아니라 거룩한 분이어서였어.\"\n\n그리고 뒤이어 조시마 장로에 대한 비난, 심지어 책망이 쏟아졌다. \"그분의 가르침은 그릇된 것이었어. 삶이란 눈물의 골짜기가 아니라 큰 기쁨이라고 가르치셨거든.\" 좀 더 사리에 어두운 자들이 이렇게 말했다. \"유행하는 믿음을 따르며 지옥에 실제로 불이 있다는 걸 인정하지 않으셨지.\" 한층 더 사리에 어두운 다른 이들이 덧붙였다. \"단식에도 엄격하지 못해서 단것을 입에 대셨고, 차에 버찌잼을 타 드셨지. 부인네들이 그걸 보내 드리곤 했거든. 엄격한 계율을 지키는 수도사가 차를 마실 일이오?\" 시샘하는 자들 사이에서 이런 말이 들렸다. \"그분은 거만하게 앉아 계셨어.\" 가장 심술궂은 자들이 앙심을 품고 단언했다. \"스스로를 성자로 여기고, 사람들이 무릎 꿇는 걸 당연한 일로 받아들이셨지.\" \"그분은 고해성사를 함부로 다루셨어.\" 장로 제도를 가장 격렬히 반대하는 자들이 심술궂은 속삭임으로 덧붙였다. 그리고 이들 가운데에는 신앙에 가장 엄격한 가장 늙은 수도사들, 진짜 고행자들도 끼여 있었으니, 그들은 죽은 장로가 살아 있는 동안에는 입을 다물고 있다가 이제 와서 느닷없이 입을 연 것이었다. 그리고 이것은 무서운 일이었으니, 그들의 말은 아직 신념이 굳지 못한 젊은 수도사들에게 큰 영향을 끼쳤기 때문이다. 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고 고개를 끄덕이며 이 모든 이야기를 주의 깊게 들었다. \"그래, 어제 페라폰트 신부님의 판단이 옳았던 게 분명해.\" 바로 그때 페라폰트 신부 자신이, 마치 일부러 혼란을 키우려는 듯이 모습을 나타냈다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 그는 양봉장 옆 통나무 암자를 좀처럼 떠나지 않았다. 그는 교회에서조차 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 사람들은 그의 광기를 핑계로 이 태만을 눈감아 주었고, 나머지 모두에게 매인 규칙을 그에게는 적용하지 않았다. 그러나 모든 진실을 다 말하자면, 그들에게는 달리 선택의 여지가 거의 없었다. 밤낮으로 기도하는(그는 무릎 꿇은 채로 잠이 들기까지 했다) 이만한 고행자에게 공동의 규율을 굳이 짊어지라고 우긴다는 것은 체면 깎이는 일이었을 터이기 때문이다. 만일 우겼다면 수도사들은 이렇게 말했을 것이다. \"저분은 우리 모두보다 거룩하시고, 우리보다 더 혹독한 계율을 따르신다. 교회에 안 가시는 것도 언제 가야 할지를 스스로 아시기 때문이지. 저분께는 저분만의 계율이 있는 게야.\" 바로 이런 죄스러운 수군거림이 일어날 빌미를 피하려고 사람들은 페라폰트 신부를 가만히 내버려 두었던 것이다.\n\n누구나 알다시피 페라폰트 신부는 조시마 장로를 유난히 싫어했다. 그런데 이제 그의 오두막에까지 \"하느님의 심판은 사람의 심판과 같지 않다\"는, 그리고 \"자연의 도를 넘어선\" 무슨 일이 벌어졌다는 소식이 닿았던 것이다. 그 소식을 들고 가장 먼저 그에게 달려간 이들 가운데 하나가 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사였으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하니, 그는 전날 저녁 그를 찾아갔다가 겁에 질려 그 암자를 떠났던 인물이다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 파이시 신부는 관 위에서 흔들림 없이 굳건하게 복음서를 읽고 있어 암자 밖에서 벌어지는 일을 듣지도 보지도 못했으나, 그 대부분을 마음속으로 정확히 헤아리고 있었으니, 자기를 둘러싼 사람들을 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 그 일에 흔들리지 않았고, 두려움 없이 다음에 무슨 일이 닥칠지를 기다리며 이 전반적인 흥분의 결말을 꿰뚫는 통찰로 지켜보았다.\n\n그런데 별안간 예법을 노골적으로 무시하는 어마어마한 소란이 복도에서 터져 나와 그의 귀를 때렸다. 문이 벌컥 열리더니 문간에 페라폰트 신부가 나타났다. 그의 뒤로는 그를 따르는 수도사 무리가, 거기에 읍내에서 온 많은 사람들과 함께 보였다. 그러나 그들은 암자 안으로 들어오지는 않고 계단 아래에 서서 페라폰트 신부가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 할지 지켜보았다. 그도 그럴 것이 그들은 그 대담함에도 불구하고, 그가 까닭 없이 온 것이 아님을 어떤 외경심과 함께 느끼고 있었던 것이다. 문간에 선 페라폰트 신부가 두 팔을 치켜들자, 그의 오른팔 아래로 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사의 날카롭고 캐기 좋아하는 작은 눈이 안을 들여다보았다. 그 강렬한 호기심에 그 혼자만은 페라폰트 신부를 뒤따라 계단을 뛰어 올라가지 않고는 배길 수 없었던 것이다. 반대로 다른 이들은 문이 요란하게 벌컥 열리자 화들짝 놀라 더 뒤로 물러섰다. 페라폰트 신부는 두 손을 높이 쳐든 채 별안간 우렁차게 외쳤다.\n\n\"쫓아내고 또 쫓아내노라!\" 그러고는 사방으로 몸을 돌려, 암자의 네 벽과 네 귀퉁이에 차례차례 곧장 십자 표시를 긋기 시작했다. 페라폰트 신부를 따라온 이들은 모두 그 행동을 곧 알아차렸다. 그가 어디를 가든 늘 이렇게 했고, 악령을 쫓아내기 전에는 자리에 앉지도, 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"사탄아, 물러가라! 사탄아, 물러가라!\" 그는 십자 표시를 그을 때마다 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"쫓아내고 또 쫓아내노라.\" 그가 다시 우렁차게 외쳤다.\n\n그는 밧줄로 동여맨 거친 수도복을 걸치고 있었다. 삼베 셔츠 아래로 잿빛 털이 덮인 맨가슴이 드러나 보였다. 두 발은 맨발이었다. 그가 팔을 휘젓기 시작하자마자, 수도복 아래에 차고 있는 잔혹한 쇠사슬이 절그렁거리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n파이시 신부는 낭독을 멈추고 앞으로 나서서 그 앞에 서서 기다렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 오셨소, 존경하는 신부님? 어찌하여 좋은 질서를 어지럽히시오? 어찌하여 양 떼의 평화를 어지럽히시오?\" 마침내 그가 엄하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 일로 왔느냐? 어찌하여 왔느냐고 묻는 게냐? 네 믿음이 어떤 것이냐?\" 페라폰트 신부가 실성한 듯 소리쳤다. \"나는 네 손님들, 그 부정한 마귀들을 쫓아내러 여기 왔다. 내가 없는 동안 여기 얼마나 많이 모여들었는지 보러 왔지. 자작나무 빗자루로 싹 쓸어내려고 말이다.\"\n\n\"신부님은 악령을 쫓아낸다고 하시나, 어쩌면 신부님 자신이 그를 섬기고 계신지도 모르오.\" 파이시 신부가 두려움 없이 말을 이었다. \"그리고 누가 제 입으로 '나는 거룩하다'고 말할 수 있겠소? 신부님은 그러실 수 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 부정한 자이지 거룩한 자가 아니다. 나라면 안락의자에 앉지도 않을 것이며, 사람들이 나를 우상처럼 떠받들게 하지도 않을 것이다.\" 페라폰트 신부가 천둥같이 호통쳤다. \"요즘 사람들은 참된 믿음을 망가뜨리고 있어. 저 죽은 자, 너희의 성자는,\" 그가 군중을 향해 손가락으로 관을 가리키며 말했다, \"마귀를 믿지 않았어. 마귀를 막는답시고 약을 줬지. 그러니 마귀들이 구석의 거미들만큼이나 흔해진 게야. 그리고 이제는 제놈이 직접 썩는 냄새를 풍기기 시작했어. 거기서 우리는 하느님께서 내리신 위대한 징조를 보는 게야.\"\n\n그가 가리킨 사건은 이러했다. 어느 수도사가 꿈에서, 또 나중에는 깨어 있는 순간에까지 악령의 환영에 시달렸다. 더없는 공포에 사로잡혀 그가 이를 조시마 장로에게 털어놓자, 장로는 끊임없는 기도와 엄격한 단식을 권했다. 그러나 그것이 아무 소용이 없자, 장로는 기도와 단식을 계속하되 특별한 약을 한 가지 쓰라고 일렀다. 그 무렵 많은 사람들이 충격을 받고 이 일을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 떠들며 고개를 내저었거니와—그 가운데서도 페라폰트 신부가 가장 심했으니, 험담 좋아하는 자들 몇몇이 장로의 이 \"기상천외한\" 충고를 부랴부랴 그에게 일러바쳤던 것이다.\n\n\"가시오, 신부님!\" 파이시 신부가 명령조로 말했다. \"심판하는 것은 사람의 몫이 아니라 하느님의 몫이오. 어쩌면 우리는 여기서 신부님도, 나도, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 헤아릴 수 없는 '징조'를 보고 있는 것인지도 모르오. 가시오, 신부님, 양 떼를 어지럽히지 마시오!\" 그가 힘주어 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그자는 계율대로 단식을 지키지 않았고, 그래서 징조가 내린 게야. 그건 분명한 일이고, 그걸 숨기는 건 죄야.\" 이성을 앞지른 열광에 휩쓸린 광신자는 좀처럼 가라앉으려 하지 않았다. \"그자는 단것에 빠져 부인네들이 그걸 주머니에 넣어 갖다 바쳤고, 차를 홀짝거렸으며, 제 배때기를 단것으로 채우고 제 머릿속은 오만한 생각으로 채우며 그 배때기를 떠받들었어……. 그래서 이렇게 치욕을 당하는 게야…….\"\n\n\"신부님은 함부로 말씀하시오.\" 파이시 신부도 목소리를 높였다. \"나는 신부님의 단식과 고행은 우러러보지만, 신부님은 변덕스럽고 철없는 경박한 젊은이처럼 함부로 말씀하시오. 가시오, 신부님, 내가 명하오!\" 파이시 신부가 끝으로 천둥같이 호통쳤다.\n\n\"가지.\" 페라폰트가 다소 주춤하는 기색이었으나 여전히 모질게 말했다. \"너희 배운 자들! 너희는 어찌나 영리한지 내 비천함을 깔보는구나. 나는 가진 배움이 변변찮은 채로 여기 왔다가, 알던 것마저 잊어버렸지. 하느님 친히 나를 내 약함 가운데 두시어 너희의 잔꾀로부터 지켜 주셨다.\"\n\n파이시 신부는 단호히 기다리며 그를 굽어보고 서 있었다. 페라폰트 신부는 잠시 입을 다물더니, 별안간 풀 죽은 듯 한 손에 뺨을 괴고는 죽은 장로의 관을 바라보며 가락을 붙인 목소리로 읊조렸다.\n\n\"내일이면 저자를 두고는 '우리의 도움이요 방패시여'를—그 거룩한 성가를 부르겠지—그런데 내가 죽으면 사람들이 부를 거라곤 고작 '무슨 세상의 기쁨이'라는 짤막한 송가뿐일 테지.\" 그가 눈물 어린 회한과 함께 덧붙였다. \"너희는 거만하고 우쭐대는구나, 여기는 헛된 곳이야!\" 그가 별안간 미치광이처럼 소리치더니, 손을 한 번 내젓고는 휙 돌아서서 빠르게 계단을 내려갔다. 아래에서 그를 기다리던 군중이 술렁였다. 더러는 곧장 그를 뒤따랐고 더러는 머뭇거렸으니, 암자가 아직 열려 있었고 파이시 신부가 페라폰트 신부를 따라 계단까지 나와 그를 지켜보고 서 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 흥분한 늙은 광신자는 아직 완전히 잠잠해진 것이 아니었다. 스무 걸음쯤 걸어가더니, 그는 별안간 지는 해를 향해 돌아서서 두 팔을 치켜들고는, 마치 누군가가 그를 베어 쓰러뜨리기라도 한 듯이 큰 비명을 지르며 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"내 하느님께서 이기셨다! 그리스도께서 지는 해를 이기셨다!\" 그가 미친 듯이 소리치며 해를 향해 두 손을 뻗었고, 땅바닥에 엎어져 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈으니, 눈물에 온몸을 떨며 두 팔을 땅 위에 활짝 벌렸다. 그러자 모두가 그에게 달려갔다. 여기저기서 탄성이 터지고 동정 어린 흐느낌이 일었으니…… 일종의 광란이 그들 모두를 사로잡은 듯했다.\n\n\"이분이야말로 성자다! 이분이야말로 거룩한 분이다!\" 두려움을 잊은 몇몇이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이분이야말로 장로가 되셔야 할 분이다.\" 다른 이들이 심술궂게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저분은 장로가 안 되실 거야……. 마다하실 테지……. 저주받은 새 제도를 받들지 않으실 거야……. 저들의 어릿광대짓을 흉내 내지 않으실 거야.\" 또 다른 목소리들이 곧장 맞장구쳤다. 그들이 어디까지 갔을지는 말하기 어려우나, 바로 그때 예배를 알리는 종이 울렸다. 모두가 일제히 성호를 긋기 시작했다. 페라폰트 신부도 일어나 성호를 긋고는 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 자기 암자로 돌아갔는데, 여전히 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 탄성을 내뱉고 있었다. 몇몇이 그를 따랐으나, 대다수는 흩어져 예배를 향해 서둘러 갔다. 파이시 신부는 이오시프 신부에게 자기 대신 낭독을 맡기고 아래로 내려갔다. 광신자들의 광란에 찬 외침도 그를 흔들지 못했으나, 그의 마음은 어떤 특별한 까닭으로 별안간 우수에 잠겼고 그는 그것을 느꼈다. 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 문득 의아해했다. \"어찌하여 나는 의기소침할 만큼 슬픈가?\" 그러고는 그 갑작스러운 슬픔이 매우 사소하고 특별한 까닭에서 비롯되었음을 놀라움과 함께 곧 깨달았다. 암자 입구에 몰려든 군중 가운데서 그는 알료샤를 보았고, 그를 본 순간 가슴에 통증을 느꼈음을 떠올렸던 것이다. \"저 아이가 지금 내 마음에 그토록 큰 자리를 차지하고 있단 말인가?\" 그는 의아해하며 자문했다.\n\n바로 그 순간 알료샤가 그의 곁을 서둘러 지나갔는데, 교회 쪽이 아니었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 알료샤는 얼른 눈길을 돌려 땅바닥으로 떨구었고, 그 눈빛 하나만으로도 파이시 신부는 지금 이 순간 그의 안에서 얼마나 큰 변화가 일어나고 있는지를 짐작했다.\n\n\"너마저 유혹에 빠진 게냐?\" 파이시 신부가 외쳤다. \"너마저 믿음이 약한 자들 틈에 끼려는 게냐?\" 그가 침울하게 덧붙였다.\n\n알료샤는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 파이시 신부를 멍하니 바라보았으나, 곧 다시 눈길을 돌려 또 땅바닥을 내려다보았다. 그는 옆으로 비스듬히 서서 파이시 신부에게 얼굴을 돌리지 않았는데, 신부는 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보았다.\n\n\"어디로 그리 서두르느냐? 종이 예배를 알리고 있다.\" 그가 다시 물었으나, 알료샤는 또다시 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"은수처를 떠나려는 게냐? 무어라, 허락도 구하지 않고, 축복도 청하지 않고?\"\n\n알료샤는 별안간 일그러진 미소를 짓더니, 죽어 가며 자신의 옛 인도자, 자신의 마음과 정신의 옛 군주, 사랑하는 장로가 그를 맡긴 그 신부에게 기이한, 너무도 기이한 눈길을 던졌다. 그러고는 별안간 여전히 한마디 말도 없이, 예의를 차릴 마음조차 없는 듯 손을 한 번 내젓고는, 빠른 걸음으로 은수처를 벗어나 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\"너는 다시 돌아올 게다!\" 파이시 신부가 슬픈 놀라움과 함께 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 중얼거렸다.\n\n각주: 여기서 '무슨 세상의 기쁨이'는 정교회 장례식에서 평수도사를 위해 부르는 짧은 송가의 첫 구절이고, '우리의 도움이요 방패시여'는 장로나 사제처럼 높은 위격의 고인을 위해 부르는 장중한 성가다.\n\n# Chapter II. A Critical Moment\n\n파이시 신부가 자기의 \"사랑하는 아이\"가 다시 돌아오리라고 판단한 것은 물론 틀린 일이 아니었다. 어쩌면 실로 그는 어느 정도까지 알료샤의 영적 상태가 지닌 참된 의미를 통찰의 눈으로 꿰뚫어 보았을 것이다. 그러나 솔직히 고백하건대, 내가 그토록 사랑하는 이 젊은 주인공의 생애에서 벌어진 그 기이하고도 막연한 순간을 명료하게 풀어내기란 나로서는 무척이나 어려운 일이다. 파이시 신부의 슬픔 어린 물음, \"그대 또한 믿음이 얕은 자들 가운데 끼어 있는가?\"에 대해 나는 물론 알료샤를 대신해 자신 있게 대답할 수 있다. \"아니다, 그는 믿음이 얕은 자들 가운데 있지 않다. 오히려 정반대다.\" 사실 그의 모든 괴로움은 그가 큰 믿음을 지녔다는 바로 그 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그렇지만 괴로움은 엄연히 거기 있었고, 그것이 어찌나 사무치는 것이었던지 알료샤는 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에도 그 슬픔의 날을 자기 생애에서 가장 쓰라리고 가장 운명적이었던 날의 하나로 떠올렸다. 만약 누군가 이렇게 묻는다면, \"그의 모든 비탄과 동요가 그저 그의 장로의 시신이 곧장 기적을 행하기는커녕 때 이른 부패의 징후를 드러냈다는 사실 하나 때문이었단 말인가?\"라고 묻는다면, 나는 변죽을 울리지 않고 곧바로 답해야 하리라. \"그렇다, 분명 그러했다.\" 다만 나는 독자가 내 젊은 주인공의 순결한 마음을 비웃는 일에 너무 서두르지 말기를 청할 뿐이다. 나는 그를 변명하려거나, 그의 천진한 믿음을 그의 어린 나이나 학업에서 거둔 보잘것없는 진척이나 그 밖의 어떤 이유로도 정당화하려는 의도가 전혀 없다. 오히려 나는 선언해야 하리니, 나는 그의 마음이 지닌 품성에 진정한 경의를 품고 있다. 의심할 바 없이, 인상을 조심스럽게만 받아들이고, 그 사랑이 미적지근하며, 그 정신이 제 나이에 견주어 지나치게 신중하여 그만큼 값어치가 적은 그런 젊은이라면, 인정하건대, 내 주인공에게 일어난 일을 피할 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 어떤 경우에는, 비록 분별없을지언정 큰 사랑에서 솟아오른 감정에 사로잡히는 편이 도리어 무감각한 것보다 더 떳떳한 법이다. 그리고 이는 젊은 시절에는 더욱 그러하니, 언제나 분별 있기만 한 젊은이란 의심스러운 자이며 값어치가 적은 자이기 때문이다. 이것이 내 견해다!\n\n\"그렇지만,\" 분별 있는 사람들은 어쩌면 이렇게 외치리라. \"젊은이라고 누구나 다 그런 미신을 믿을 수 있는 건 아니며, 당신의 주인공은 다른 이들의 본보기가 못 되오.\"\n\n이에 나는 다시 답하겠다. \"그렇다! 내 주인공은 믿음을, 거룩하고 굳건한 믿음을 지니고 있었다. 그렇지만 그래도 나는 그를 변명하지는 않으련다.\"\n\n위에서 나는, 어쩌면 너무 성급하게, 내 주인공을 설명하거나 정당화하지 않겠노라 선언했으나, 내 이야기의 나머지를 이해하기 위해서는 얼마간의 설명이 필요하다는 것을 깨닫는다. 그러면 이렇게 말해 두자, 그것은 기적의 문제가 아니었다. 그의 마음속에 기적을 향한 경박하고 조급한 기대 따위는 없었다. 그리고 알료샤에게는 그때 어떤 선입견을 굳히기 위한 기적 따위가 필요치 않았으니—오, 아니다, 결코 아니다—그가 무엇보다 먼저 본 것은 한 인물의 모습, 자기가 사랑하는 장로의 모습, 그가 그토록 흠모하며 우러르던 그 거룩한 사람의 모습이었다. 사실인즉, 그의 순결하고 젊은 가슴속에 만인과 만물을 향해 감추어져 있던 그 모든 사랑이, 지난 한 해 동안 한 존재, 곧 그가 사랑하는 장로에게로 집중되어 있었으며—어쩌면 그것은 잘못된 일이었으리라. 그 존재가 그토록 오래도록 그에게 자신의 이상으로 받아들여져 온 것은 사실이니, 그리하여 그의 모든 젊은 힘과 정력이 그 이상을 향하지 않을 수 없었고, 마침내는 그 순간 \"만인과 만물을\" 잊을 정도에까지 이르렀던 것이다. 그는 훗날 떠올렸다, 그 끔찍한 날에 자기가 형 드미트리를 까맣게 잊고 있었음을, 바로 전날만 해도 그토록 마음 졸이며 애를 태우던 그 형을 말이다. 또 그는 일류샤의 아버지에게 이백 루블을 가져다주는 일도 잊고 있었으니, 전날 저녁 그토록 간절히 그러기로 마음먹었건만 그러했다. 그러나 거듭 말하지만, 그가 필요로 한 것은 기적이 아니라 오로지 \"더 높은 정의\"였으며, 그토록 갑작스럽고 잔인하게 그의 가슴을 후려친 일격으로 인해 그 정의가 짓밟혔다고 그는 믿었던 것이다. 그리고 알료샤가 찾던 이 \"정의\"가 필연적으로, 흠모하던 스승의 유해로 말미암아 즉각 일어날 기적의 형태를 띠었다 한들 그것이 무슨 대수랴? 어차피 수도원의 모든 사람이 똑같은 생각과 똑같은 희망을 품고 있었으니, 알료샤가 그 지성을 우러르던 이들조차, 이를테면 파이시 신부 자신마저도 그러하지 않았던가. 그리하여 알료샤도 아무런 의심에 흔들림 없이 자기의 꿈을 나머지 모든 사람과 똑같은 형태로 옷 입혔던 것이다. 그리고 수도원에서 보낸 한 해의 세월이 그의 가슴속에 이런 기대의 습관을 빚어 놓았던 것이다. 그러나 그가 목말라했던 것은 정의, 정의였지, 단순한 기적이 아니었다.\n\n그런데 그가 믿기로는 마땅히 온 세상 누구보다도 높이 떠받들어졌어야 할 그 사람이, 그 사람이 응당 받아야 할 영광을 받기는커녕 별안간 격하되고 욕을 당하다니! 무엇 때문에? 누가 그를 심판했단 말인가? 누가 이런 일을 명할 수 있었단 말인가? 이것이야말로 그의 미숙하고 순결한 가슴을 쥐어짜는 물음이었다. 거룩한 자들 가운데서도 가장 거룩한 분이, 그분보다 그토록 못한 경박한 무리의 비웃음과 악의 어린 조롱에 내던져졌다는 사실을, 그는 분함 없이, 아니 원망조차 없이는 견딜 수 없었다. 설령 아무 기적도 없었다 한들, 그의 희망을 정당화할 만한 그 어떤 경이도 없었다 한들, 어찌하여 이런 모욕인가, 어찌하여 이런 굴욕인가, 어찌하여 악의 어린 수도사들의 말마따나 \"자연의 도를 넘은\" 이런 때 이른 부패란 말인가? 어찌하여 그들이 페라폰트 신부와 더불어 그토록 의기양양하게 환호하던 이 \"하늘의 징표\"란 말인가, 또 어찌하여 그들은 그것을 환호할 권리를 얻었노라 믿었단 말인가? 섭리의 손가락은 어디에 있는가? 어찌하여 섭리는 마치 눈멀고 말 없고 무자비한 자연의 법칙에 스스로 굴복하기라도 하듯이 \"가장 위태로운 순간에\"(알료샤는 그렇게 여겼다) 그 얼굴을 가렸단 말인가?\n\n그것이 바로 알료샤의 가슴이 피 흘린 까닭이었으니, 물론 이미 말했듯이, 그 모든 일의 가시는 그가 이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑하는 사람이 수치와 굴욕을 당해야 했다는 데 있었던 것이다! 내 주인공의 이런 원망이 얕고 분별없는 것이었는지는 모르나, 나는 세 번째로 거듭 말하거니와—그리고 내 이 감정을 옹호하기란 어려운 일임을 인정할 준비가 되어 있거니와—나는 내 주인공이 그 순간 그다지 분별 있는 모습을 보이지 않았다는 것이 기쁘다. 분별 있는 사람이라면 때가 되면 언제든 분별로 되돌아오게 마련이지만, 만약 그런 예외적인 순간에 소년의 가슴속에서 사랑이 우위를 차지하지 못한다면 대체 언제 그러겠는가? 그러나 나는 이 운명적이고 어두운 순간에 한동안 알료샤의 마음 표면으로 떠오른 어떤 기이한 것을 빼놓지 않고 언급해 두겠다. 이 새로운 무언가란 이반과 나눈 대화가 남긴 괴로운 인상으로, 그것이 이제 끈질기게 알료샤의 마음을 따라다녔다. 바로 이 순간 그것이 그를 따라다녔다. 오, 그의 영혼이 지닌 이른바 근본적이고 본질적인 믿음이 흔들렸다는 것은 아니었다. 그는 자기의 하느님을 사랑했고 굳건히 그분을 믿었으니, 비록 별안간 그분을 원망하고 있기는 했어도 그러했다. 그렇지만 전날 이반과 나눈 대화가 남긴 막연하면서도 괴롭고 사악한 그 인상이 이제 그의 영혼 속에서 별안간 다시 되살아나 그의 의식 표면으로 비집고 올라오려 하는 듯했다.\n\n라키친이 암자에서 수도원으로 가는 솔숲을 가로질러 갈 무렵 날이 어슴푸레 저물기 시작했는데, 그는 별안간 알료샤가 나무 아래 땅바닥에 얼굴을 묻고 엎드린 채 미동도 없이, 보아하니 잠든 듯이 누워 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 다가가 그의 이름을 불렀다.\n\n\"여기 있었군, 알렉세이? 자네 설마—\" 그는 의아해하며 말을 꺼냈다가 뚝 그쳤다. 그가 하려던 말은 \"자네 설마 이 지경에까지 이른 건가?\"였다.\n\n알료샤는 그를 쳐다보지 않았으나, 라키친은 그 미미한 움직임 하나로 그가 자기 말을 듣고 알아들었음을 단번에 알아챘다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 그는 말을 이었으나, 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 놀라움은 차차 미소로 바뀌더니 점점 더 빈정대는 빛을 띠어 갔다.\n\n\"이봐, 내가 자넬 두 시간이나 찾아다녔단 말일세. 자네가 별안간 사라져 버렸으니. 대체 뭘 하고 있는 건가? 이게 무슨 어리광이야? 나 좀 쳐다보기라도 하지 그래…….\"\n\n알료샤는 고개를 들고 일어나 앉더니 나무에 등을 기댔다. 울고 있지는 않았으나 그의 얼굴에는 괴로움과 짜증의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그렇지만 그는 라키친을 쳐다보지 않고 그의 한쪽 옆으로 시선을 돌렸다.\n\n\"자네 얼굴이 아주 딴판이 된 거 아나? 그 유명한 온화함이라곤 눈을 씻고 봐도 없네그려. 누구한테 화라도 났나? 누가 자넬 못살게 굴기라도 했어?\"\n\n\"날 좀 내버려 두게.\" 알료샤가 별안간 말하며 지친 듯 한 손을 내저었으나, 여전히 그에게서 시선을 돌린 채였다.\n\n\"오호! 그래, 우리 기분이 그렇단 말이지! 자네도 다른 인간들처럼 남한테 소리를 지를 줄 아는군. 천사 신분에서 한 단계 추락한 셈이야. 이봐, 알료샤, 자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군, 듣고 있나? 진심으로 하는 말일세. 내가 이곳에서 무슨 일에든 놀라 본 게 참 오랜만이야. 난 늘 자넬 교양 있는 사람으로 여겨 왔는데…….\"\n\n알료샤가 마침내 그를 쳐다보았으나, 그 눈길은 그가 하는 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하는 양 멍한 것이었다.\n\n\"자네 정말 그저 자네의 그 늙은이가 썩는 냄새를 풍기기 시작했다는 것 하나 때문에 이렇게 속이 뒤집힐 수 있단 말인가? 설마 그분이 기적을 행하시리라고 진심으로 믿었다는 건 아니겠지?\" 라키친이 다시 진심으로 놀라워하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"난 믿었고, 믿고 있고, 믿고 싶고, 또 믿을 걸세, 더 무얼 바라는가?\" 알료샤가 짜증스레 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아무것도 바라지 않아, 이 사람아. 빌어먹을! 아니, 요즘은 열세 살짜리 학생도 그런 걸 믿지 않아. 하지만 그래……. 그래서 지금 자네는 자네 하느님께 부아가 났고, 그분께 반항하고 있다 이거지. 그분이 승진을 안 시켜 주셨다, 훈장을 안 내려 주셨다 이거야! 허, 자네들 참 별종들이야!\"\n\n알료샤는 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 한참 동안 라키친을 응시했는데, 그 눈에서 별안간 어떤 빛이 번뜩였다…… 그러나 라키친을 향한 분노의 빛은 아니었다.\n\n\"난 내 하느님께 반항하는 게 아닐세. 다만 '그분의 세계를 받아들이지 않을' 뿐이지.\" 알료샤가 별안간 억지웃음을 지어 보였다.\n\n\"세계를 받아들이지 않는다니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\" 라키친은 그 대답을 두고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. \"이게 무슨 얼빠진 소리야?\"\n\n알료샤는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 헛소린 그만하고, 이제 본론으로 들어가지. 오늘 뭐라도 먹긴 했나?\"\n\n\"기억이 안 나는군…… 먹은 것 같기도 하고.\"\n\n\"자네 얼굴을 보아하니 기운을 좀 차려야겠어. 보고 있자니 딱하구먼. 듣자 하니 밤새 한숨도 못 잤다며, 거기서 회합이 있었다지. 게다가 그 뒤로 이 야단법석까지. 십중팔구 성찬 빵 한 입밖에 못 먹었겠지. 내 주머니에 소시지가 좀 있네. 만약을 대비해 읍내에서 가져온 건데, 자넨 소시지를 안 먹을 테고…….\"\n\n\"좀 주게.\"\n\n\"아니 이런! 자네 갈 데까지 가는군! 아니, 이거야말로 바리케이드까지 친 진짜 반란일세! 그래, 이 사람아, 기왕 이렇게 된 거 최대한 누려 봐야지. 우리 집으로 가세…… 나도 보드카 한 모금쯤은 마다하지 않겠어, 죽도록 피곤하니까. 보드카는 자네한텐 너무 지나친 일이겠지…… 아니면 자네도 좀 마시려나?\"\n\n\"보드카도 좀 주게.\"\n\n\"이런! 자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군, 이 사람아!\" 라키친이 어안이 벙벙해서 그를 바라보았다. \"그래, 어찌 됐든, 보드카든 소시지든, 이거야말로 둘도 없는 좋은 기회인데 놓쳐선 안 되지. 자, 가세.\"\n\n알료샤는 말없이 일어나 라키친을 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"자네 동생 이반이 이 꼴을 봤다면—얼마나 놀라겠나! 그건 그렇고, 자네 형 이반이 오늘 아침 모스크바로 떠났는데, 알고 있었나?\"\n\n\"응.\" 알료샤가 맥없이 대답하는데, 별안간 그의 형 드미트리의 모습이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그러나 그것은 단 일 분뿐이었으니, 그 모습이 단 한순간도 미룰 수 없는 무언가를, 어떤 의무를, 어떤 끔찍한 책무를 그에게 일깨워 주었음에도, 그 일깨움조차 그에게 아무런 인상도 남기지 못한 채, 그의 가슴에 가닿지 못한 채 곧장 그의 머릿속에서 흐릿하게 사라져 잊히고 말았다. 그러나 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에 알료샤는 이 일을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"자네 형 이반은 언젠가 내가 '아무런 재능도 없는 자유주의 얼간이'라고 단언한 적이 있지. 자네도 언젠가 내가 '명예를 모르는 자'라고 기어이 내게 일러 주지 않고는 못 배기더군. 그래! 이제 자네의 그 재능과 명예심이 자네한테 무슨 도움이 되는지 어디 한번 보고 싶구먼.\" 이 마지막 말을 라키친은 속으로 나직이 중얼거리며 끝맺었다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"수도원 너머 오솔길로 해서 곧장 읍내로 가세. 흠! 가는 길에 호흘라코바 부인 댁에 들러야 하는데. 글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보게, 내가 그분께 일어난 일을 죄다 적어 보냈더니, 자네 믿겠나, 그분이 당장에 연필로 답장을 보내왔지 뭔가(그 부인은 쪽지 쓰는 데 사족을 못 쓰거든). '조시마 신부처럼 그토록 존경받는 인품을 지닌 분에게서 그런 처신을 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다'는 거야. 그분이 쓴 말 그대로일세, '처신'이라니. 그분도 화가 난 거지. 허, 자네들 참 별종들이야! 가만!\" 그가 별안간 다시 외쳤다. 그는 느닷없이 걸음을 멈추더니 알료샤의 어깨를 붙들어 그도 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"이봐 알료샤, 자네 아나,\" 그는 자기에게 막 떠오른 어떤 새로운 생각에 골똘히 빠져들어 알료샤의 눈을 캐묻듯 들여다보았는데, 겉으로는 웃고 있었지만 그 새로운 생각을 소리 내어 입에 담기는 분명 두려운 모양이었으니, 그가 지금 알료샤에게서 보고 있는 이 기이하고 뜻밖의 기분을 도무지 믿기 어려웠던 까닭이다. \"알료샤, 자네 우리가 어디로 가는 게 제일 좋을지 아나?\" 그가 마침내 머뭇거리며, 슬며시 구슬리듯 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"아무래도 좋아…… 자네 좋을 대로 하게.\"\n\n\"그루셴카한테 가세, 어때? 같이 가겠나?\" 라키친이 마침내 머뭇거리는 마음으로 가슴을 졸이며 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그루셴카한테 가세.\" 알료샤가 차분하게, 단번에 대답했는데, 이 선뜻하고 차분한 동의가 라키친에게는 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지 그는 하마터면 흠칫 뒤로 물러설 뻔했다.\n\n\"이런! 어럽쇼!\" 그가 놀라 외쳤으나, 알료샤의 팔을 단단히 움켜쥐고는 그가 마음을 바꿀까 여전히 두려워하며 오솔길을 따라 그를 이끌었다.\n\n그들은 말없이 걸어갔으니, 라키친은 입을 떼는 것조차 정녕 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n\"그 여자가 얼마나 기뻐할까, 얼마나 좋아 날뛸까!\" 그가 중얼거렸으나 이내 다시 침묵에 잠겼다. 사실 그가 알료샤를 그녀에게 데려가는 것은 그루셴카를 기쁘게 해 주려는 것이 아니었다. 그는 실속을 차리는 인간이라 자기에게 이득이 될 전망이 없으면 무슨 일에도 결코 손을 대지 않았다. 이 경우 그의 목적은 두 가지였으니, 첫째는 \"의로운 자의 몰락\"과 알료샤가 \"성자의 자리에서 죄인의 자리로\" 떨어지는 꼴을 보고 싶은 앙심 품은 욕망으로, 그는 벌써부터 그 광경을 상상하며 회심의 미소를 짓고 있었고, 둘째로는 자기에게 돌아올 어떤 물질적 이득을 염두에 두고 있었는데, 그에 관해서는 나중에 더 이야기하기로 하겠다.\n\n\"드디어 결정적인 순간이 온 거야,\" 그는 앙큼한 희열에 잠겨 속으로 생각했다. \"이참에 단숨에 낚아채 버리는 거지, 바로 우리가 바라던 바이니 말이야.\"\n\n# Chapter III. An Onion\n\n그루셴카는 시내에서 가장 번잡한 구역, 대성당 광장 가까이에 살았으니, 모로조바 미망인 소유의 저택 안뜰에 딸린 자그마한 목조 별채가 그녀의 거처였다. 본채는 이층짜리 커다란 석조 건물로, 낡은 데다 보기에도 흉했다. 미망인은 역시 나이 든 처녀인 결혼하지 않은 두 조카딸과 더불어 은둔하다시피 살았다. 별채를 세놓을 필요가 전혀 없는 처지였으나, 그녀가 사 년 전 그루셴카를 세입자로 들인 것은 오로지 친척인 상인 삼소노프의 비위를 맞추기 위해서였음을 누구나 알고 있었으니, 삼소노프가 이 처녀의 후견인이라는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이었다. 질투심 많은 이 노인이 자기의 \"총애하는 여자\"를 모로조바 미망인의 곁에 둔 까닭은, 늙은 여인으로 하여금 새 세입자의 품행을 매서운 눈으로 감시하게 하려 함이었다는 말이 있었다. 그러나 이 매서운 눈은 이내 쓸모없는 것으로 드러났고, 결국 모로조바 미망인은 그루셴카와 마주치는 일조차 드물었으며 어떤 식으로도 그녀를 살피거나 귀찮게 굴지 않았다. 노인이 호리호리하고 가냘프며 수줍고 겁 많고 몽상에 잠긴 듯 슬픈 열여덟 살 처녀를 도(道)의 중심 도시에서 데려온 지 사 년이 지났고, 그 사이에 적잖은 일이 벌어진 것은 사실이었다. 이 처녀의 내력에 관하여 시내에 알려진 것은 거의 없었으며, 그나마 알려진 것도 어렴풋했다. 그 사이 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 자라나 빚어낸 아름다운 젊은 여인에게 많은 이들이 관심을 가지게 된 뒤에도, 지난 사 년 동안 더 알아낸 것은 아무것도 없었다. 소문에 따르면 그녀는 열일곱 살에 누군가에게, 무슨 장교라는 자에게 농락당했고, 그 직후 그에게 버림받았다고 했다. 그 장교는 떠나 버린 뒤 결혼했고, 그루셴카는 가난과 치욕 속에 남겨졌다는 것이었다. 그러나 비록 그루셴카가 노인 삼소노프 덕분에 궁핍에서 벗어나기는 했으나, 그녀가 성직자 계급에 속한 점잖은 가문 출신으로, 부제(副祭)인지 그 비슷한 무엇인지 하는 사람의 딸이라는 말도 있었다.\n\n그리고 이제 사 년이 지나, 상처 입고 가련하던 예민한 어린 고아는 통통하고 발그레한 러시아풍 미인으로, 대담하고 단호한 성격에 거만하고 오만한 여인으로 변해 있었다. 그녀는 장사에 밝은 머리를 지녔고, 욕심이 많으며 알뜰하고 빈틈이 없었으니, 수단이 옳건 그르건 가리지 않고 제법 재산을 모으는 데 성공했다는 말이 돌았다. 다만 한 가지 점에 대해서만큼은 모두의 의견이 일치했다. 그루셴카에게는 쉬이 다가갈 수 없었고, 늙은 후견인을 제외하면 지난 사 년 동안 그녀의 총애를 받았노라 자랑할 수 있는 사내는 단 한 명도 없었다는 점이었다. 그것은 엄연한 사실이었으니, 특히 지난 이 년 동안 그 총애를 얻으려 시도한 자가 적지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그들의 온갖 노력은 헛수고로 돌아갔고, 이 구애자들 중 더러는 의지가 굳센 이 젊은 여인에게서 단호하고 빈정대는 거절을 당한 끝에 위신을 잃은, 심지어 우스꽝스럽기까지 한 퇴각을 할 수밖에 없었다. 또한 알려진 바로는, 이 젊은 여인이 특히 근래 들어 이른바 \"투기\"라는 것에 손을 댔고, 그 방면에서 두드러진 재능을 보였기에, 적잖은 사람들이 그녀가 유대인과 다를 바 없다고 입에 올리기 시작했다는 것이다. 그녀가 이자를 받고 돈을 빌려준 것은 아니었으나, 이를테면 얼마 전부터 늙은 카라마조프와 손을 잡고 부실 채권을 명목 가치의 십분의 일에 불과한 헐값으로 사들였다가, 나중에 그것으로 본래 가치의 열 배를 벌어들였다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이었다.\n\n홀아비 노인 삼소노프는 막대한 재산을 지닌 사람으로, 인색하고 무자비했다. 그는 장성한 아들들을 폭군처럼 휘둘렀으나, 지난 일 년 동안 병으로 퉁퉁 부은 다리를 쓰지 못하게 되면서부터는 자기가 보살피던 여자의 영향력에 크게 휘둘리게 되었으니, 처음에는 그녀를 엄히 단속하며 변변찮은 처지에, 당시 익살꾼들이 일컫던 대로 \"사순절 음식이나 먹는\" 형편에 묶어 두었던 터였다. 그러나 그루셴카는 스스로를 해방시키는 데 성공했고, 동시에 노인에게는 자기 정절에 대한 끝없는 믿음을 심어 놓았다. 이제는 죽은 지 오래된 그 노인은 한 시절 큰 사업을 했고, 또한 인색하고 부싯돌처럼 단단한, 주목할 만한 인물이기도 했다. 그루셴카가 그를 사로잡은 힘이 어찌나 강했던지 그는 그녀 없이는 살 수 없을 지경이었으나(특히 지난 이 년간은 더욱 그러했다), 그렇다고 그녀에게 상당한 재산을 떼어 주지는 않았고, 설령 그녀가 떠나겠노라 위협했더라도 마음이 흔들리지 않았을 것이다. 다만 그는 그녀에게 적은 액수를 선물했는데, 그것조차 알려졌을 때 모두를 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"너는 머리가 잘 돌아가는 계집이야.\" 노인은 그녀에게 팔천 루블을 주면서 말했다. \"그러니 네 앞가림은 네가 알아서 하고, 다만 한 가지 일러두자면, 전처럼 해마다 받는 생활비를 빼고는 내가 죽는 날까지 나한테서 더 받을 건 아무것도 없을 게다. 그리고 내 유언에도 너한테 남길 건 하나 없을 게야.\"\n\n그리고 그는 그 말을 지켰다. 그는 죽으면서 모든 것을 아들들에게 남겼는데, 평생 그 아들들을 아내와 자식들까지 싸잡아 종 부리듯 다루어 왔던 터였다. 그루셴카는 그의 유언에 이름조차 언급되지 않았다. 이 모든 사실은 나중에야 알려졌다. 그는 그루셴카에게 자본을 불릴 조언을 해 주고 장사할 길을 터 주는 것으로 도움을 주었다.\n\n표도르 파블로비치가 처음 어느 투기 거래를 두고 그루셴카와 인연을 맺었다가 스스로도 놀랍게 그녀에게 미친 듯이 빠져드는 것으로 끝나고 말았을 때, 늙은 삼소노프는 중병을 앓는 처지였으면서도 더없이 재미있어했다. 주목할 만한 점은, 둘이 알고 지내는 내내 그루셴카가 노인에게만큼은 완전히 또 거리낌 없이 속을 터놓았다는 사실이며, 노인은 그녀가 그토록 마음을 연 세상에 단 하나뿐인 사람이었던 듯하다. 근래에 드미트리마저 자기 사랑을 품고 무대에 등장하자, 노인은 웃음을 거두었다. 도리어 한번은 그루셴카에게 엄하고도 진지한 충고를 한마디 던졌다.\n\n\"아비냐 아들이냐, 둘 중에 골라야 한다면 늙은 쪽을 고르는 게 낫지. 다만 그 늙은 악당이 너랑 결혼하고 미리 재산도 좀 떼어 줄 게 확실해야 한다. 하지만 그 대위 놈하고는 계속 어울리지 마라, 그래 봤자 좋을 게 하나도 없으니까.\"\n\n이것이 바로, 이미 제 죽음이 멀지 않았음을 느끼던, 그리고 실제로 다섯 달 뒤에 죽은 그 늙은 난봉꾼의 말이었다.\n\n또 한 가지 지나가는 김에 일러두겠는데, 우리 시내의 많은 사람들이 그루셴카를 두고 벌어진 카라마조프 부자(父子)의 기괴하고 흉측한 경쟁을 알고 있었으나, 그녀가 두 사람을 대하는 태도의 밑바닥에 정작 무엇이 깔려 있는지를 이해하는 자는 거의 없었다. 그루셴카의 두 하녀조차(나중에 이야기할 그 참극이 벌어진 뒤) 법정에서 증언하기를, 여주인이 드미트리 표도로비치를 받아들인 것은 순전히 두려움 때문이었다고 했으니, \"그가 자기를 죽이겠다고 협박했기\" 때문이라는 것이었다. 이 하녀들이란 그루셴카의 옛집에서 따라온, 병약하고 귀가 거의 먹은 늙은 식모와, 하녀 일을 맡아 보는 스무 살의 영리한 젊은 처녀인 그 손녀였다. 그루셴카는 매우 검소하게 살았고, 그녀의 살림살이는 결코 호화롭다고 할 수 없었다. 그녀의 별채는 방 세 칸으로 이루어졌고, 집주인 소유의, 1820년대 양식을 따른 마호가니 가구가 놓여 있었다.\n\n라키틴과 알료샤가 그녀의 방에 들어섰을 때는 이미 사위가 캄캄했는데도 불을 켜 두지 않은 상태였다. 그루셴카는 거실의, 마호가니 등받이가 달린 크고 딱딱하며 투박한 소파에 누워 있었다. 소파에는 닳고 해진 가죽이 씌워져 있었다. 머리 밑에는 침대에서 가져온 하얀 솜털 베개 두 개를 받쳐 두었다. 그녀는 두 손을 머리 뒤로 한 채 똑바로 누워 꼼짝도 않고 몸을 쭉 뻗고 있었다. 마치 누군가를 기다리기라도 하는 듯한 차림새였으니, 검은 비단 드레스를 입고 머리에는 매우 잘 어울리는 앙증맞은 레이스 두건을 쓰고 있었다. 어깨에는 묵직한 금 브로치로 여민 레이스 숄을 걸쳤다. 그녀는 분명 누군가를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀는 애가 타고 지친 듯이 누워 있었는데, 얼굴은 다소 핼쑥했으나 입술과 눈은 달아올랐고, 오른발 끝으로 소파 팔걸이를 안절부절못하며 톡톡 두드리고 있었다. 라키틴과 알료샤가 나타나자 그녀는 약간 동요했다. 현관에서 두 사람은 그루셴카가 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 겁먹은 목소리로 외치는 소리를 들었다. \"누구세요?\" 그러나 하녀가 손님들을 맞이하고는 곧장 여주인에게 되받아 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그분이 아니에요, 아무것도 아니에요, 그냥 다른 손님들이세요.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 일이람?\" 라키틴은 중얼거리며 알료샤를 데리고 거실로 들어섰다.\n\n그루셴카는 여전히 놀란 듯이 소파 곁에 서 있었다. 짙은 갈색 머리채 한 가닥이 레이스 두건에서 풀려나와 오른쪽 어깨로 흘러내렸으나, 그녀는 그것을 알아차리지도, 손님들을 찬찬히 들여다보고 누구인지 알아볼 때까지는 다시 매만지지도 않았다.\n\n\"아, 너였구나, 라키틴? 깜짝 놀랐잖아. 누구를 데려온 거야? 같이 온 이 사람은 누구지? 오, 세상에, 그분을 데려왔구나!\" 그녀는 알료샤를 알아보고는 소리쳤다.\n\n\"촛불 좀 가져오라고 해요!\" 라키틴은 이 집에서 명령을 내릴 자격이 있는 더없이 막역한 친구다운, 거리낌 없고 스스럼없는 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"촛불…… 그래, 촛불…… 페냐, 촛불 좀 가져다드려라…… 거참, 하필 이런 때를 골라 이분을 데려오다니!\" 그녀는 알료샤 쪽으로 고갯짓을 하며 다시 소리치더니, 거울 쪽으로 돌아서서 두 손으로 부랴부랴 머리를 매만지기 시작했다. 못마땅한 기색이었다.\n\n\"내가 영 마음에 안 들게 했나 보군요?\" 라키틴은 대뜸 거의 화가 난 듯이 물었다.\n\n\"날 놀라게 했잖아, 라키틴, 그 말이야.\" 그루셴카는 미소를 지으며 알료샤에게로 돌아섰다. \"날 무서워 마요, 사랑스러운 알료샤, 뜻밖에 찾아와 준 당신을 보니 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 당신은 모를 거예요. 하지만 넌 날 놀라게 했어, 라키틴, 미탸가 쳐들어오는 줄 알았다고. 알겠지만 내가 방금 그를 속였거든, 날 믿겠다는 약속을 받아 내고는 거짓말을 한 거야. 오늘 저녁은 나의 노인장, 쿠지마 쿠지미치하고 함께 보내며 늦게까지 그분 돈 계산을 도와드릴 거라고 했지. 난 일주일에 한 번은 꼬박 저녁 내내 그분과 함께 장부를 정리해. 우린 문을 걸어 잠그고, 그분이 주판알을 튕기면 나는 앉아서 장부에 적어 넣는 거야. 그분이 믿는 사람은 나밖에 없거든. 미탸는 내가 거기 있는 줄 알고 있지만, 사실 나는 돌아와서 여기 문을 걸어 잠그고 앉아 무슨 소식을 기다리고 있던 참이야. 페냐가 어쩌다 너희를 들여보냈을까? 페냐, 페냐, 어서 문간으로 달려가서 문을 열고 그 대위가 보이지 않나 살펴봐라! 어쩌면 어딘가 숨어서 엿보고 있을지도 몰라, 난 무서워 죽겠어.\"\n\n\"거기 아무도 없어요, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 방금 내다봤어요, 저도 자꾸만 틈으로 달려가 엿보고 있는걸요, 저 역시 무서워서 벌벌 떨고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"덧문은 잠갔니, 페냐? 그리고 커튼도 쳐야겠다—그게 낫겠어!\" 그녀는 손수 묵직한 커튼을 쳤다. \"불빛을 보면 당장 뛰어들 테니까. 오늘은 당신 형 미탸가 무서워, 알료샤.\"\n\n그루셴카는 큰 소리로 말했는데, 비록 겁에 질려 있긴 했으나 무언가에 대해 무척 행복해하는 듯했다.\n\n\"오늘따라 왜 그렇게 미탸를 무서워하는 거요?\" 라키틴이 물었다. \"당신은 그자 앞에서 겁낼 사람이 아닌 줄 알았는데, 손가락 하나로 그자를 쥐락펴락하잖소.\"\n\n\"분명히 말하지만, 난 소식을 기다리고 있어, 더없이 귀한 소식을, 그러니 미탸는 조금도 곁에 두고 싶지 않다고. 게다가 그는 믿지 않았어, 느낌으로 알아, 내가 쿠지마 쿠지미치네 머물러 있을 거라는 걸 믿지 않았다고. 지금쯤 그는 분명 표도르 파블로비치네 뒷마당 정원에 매복해서 날 노리고 있을 거야. 거기 있다면 여기로는 오지 않을 테니, 그럴수록 더 잘된 일이지! 하지만 난 정말로 쿠지마 쿠지미치네 다녀왔어, 미탸가 거기까지 날 바래다줬거든. 자정까지 거기 있겠노라 일러두고, 자정에 꼭 데리러 와 달라고 부탁했지. 그가 떠나고 나서 나는 쿠지마 쿠지미치와 십 분쯤 앉아 있다가 여기로 다시 돌아온 거야. 어휴, 무서워서, 그를 마주칠까 봐 부랴부랴 달려왔다고.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 그렇게 차려입은 거요? 머리에 쓴 그 두건은 또 뭐가 그리 유별나오!\"\n\n\"넌 너대로 어찌나 유난을 떠는지, 라키틴! 말했잖아, 난 전갈을 기다리고 있다고. 전갈이 오면 난 날아갈 거야, 말을 달려 떠나 버릴 테니 너희는 다시는 내 모습을 못 볼걸. 그래서 채비를 갖추려고 이렇게 차려입은 거라고.\"\n\n\"그래, 어디로 날아간다는 거요?\"\n\n\"너무 많이 알면 그만큼 일찍 늙는 법이야.\"\n\n\"이거 참! 무척이나 들떠 있군…… 당신이 이런 모습은 처음 보오. 무도회에라도 가는 것처럼 차려입었구려.\" 라키틴은 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다.\n\n\"무도회에 대해 아주 많이도 아는구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 무도회를 많이 아오?\"\n\n\"무도회를 본 적이 있어. 재작년에 쿠지마 쿠지미치 아들이 결혼했을 때 회랑에서 구경했지. 라키틴, 이런 왕자님 같은 분이 여기 서 계신데 내가 너 따위와 떠들고 있고 싶겠니. 이런 손님이 오셨는데! 알료샤, 사랑스러운 분, 당신을 보고 있어도 내 눈을 믿을 수가 없어요. 오, 세상에, 정말 날 보러 여기 와 주신 거예요! 솔직히 말하면, 난 당신을 만나리라곤 한 번도 생각지 못했고, 당신이 날 보러 와 줄 거라고도 생각지 못했어요. 지금이 그럴 때는 아니지만, 그래도 당신을 보니 정말이지 너무 기뻐요. 소파에 앉아요, 여기, 그래요, 나의 환히 빛나는 어린 달님. 지금도 도무지 믿기지가 않네요…… 아, 라키틴, 네가 어제나 그제 이분을 데려왔더라면! 하지만 이대로도 기뻐! 어쩌면 그제가 아니라 지금, 바로 이런 순간에 오신 게 더 잘된 일인지도 몰라.\"\n\n그녀는 더없이 즐거운 듯이 알료샤 곁 소파에 앉아 진심 어린 기쁨으로 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 정말로 기뻤고, 기쁘다고 말한 것은 거짓이 아니었다. 그녀의 눈은 빛났고 입술은 웃고 있었으나, 그것은 선량하고 명랑한 웃음이었다. 알료샤는 그녀의 얼굴에서 그토록 다정한 표정을 보게 되리라고는 미처 예상치 못했었다…… 그는 어제 처음 그녀를 만났다시피 했고, 그녀에 대해 무시무시한 인상을 품고 있었으며, 어제는 그녀가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 부린 악랄하고 음흉한 농간 때문에 끔찍이도 괴로워했던 터였다. 이제 그녀가 자기가 예상했던 것과는 전혀 딴판인 모습임을 알고 그는 몹시 놀랐다. 그리고 자신의 슬픔에 짓눌린 처지였으면서도, 그의 눈은 저도 모르게 주의를 기울여 그녀에게 머물렀다. 어제 이후 그녀의 태도는 통째로 더 나은 쪽으로 바뀐 듯했으니, 그녀의 말투에서는 그 느끼하리만치 달콤하던 기색이, 몸짓에서는 그 관능적인 나긋함이 거의 자취를 감추고 없었다. 모든 것이 소박하고 선량했으며, 그녀의 몸짓은 재고 직선적이고 거리낌이 없었으나, 다만 그녀는 몹시 흥분해 있었다.\n\n\"어머나, 오늘은 어쩜 이렇게 모든 일이 한꺼번에 겹치는지!\" 그녀는 다시 재잘거리기 시작했다. \"그리고 알료샤, 당신을 보는 게 왜 이리도 기쁜지, 나도 알 수가 없어요! 누가 물어도 대답할 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"이런, 당신은 자기가 왜 기쁜지도 모르오?\" 라키틴이 이를 드러내고 히죽거리며 말했다. \"당신은 늘 나를 들볶으며 이 사람을 데려오라고 하지 않았소, 무슨 속셈이 있었던 게지.\"\n\n\"한때는 다른 속셈이 있었지만, 이제 그건 다 끝났어, 지금은 그럴 때가 아니야. 저기, 내가 너희한테 뭐 좋은 걸 좀 대접하고 싶어. 난 지금 마음이 이렇게 너그러워졌거든. 너도 앉아, 라키틴, 왜 서 있어? 벌써 앉았네? 라키틴이 제 앞가림하는 걸 잊을까 봐 걱정할 필요는 없지. 봐요, 알료샤, 저기 우리 맞은편에 앉아서는, 내가 당신보다 먼저 앉으라고 권하지 않았다고 어찌나 토라졌는지. 어휴, 라키틴은 정말이지 토라지기 선수라니까!\" 그루셴카는 웃었다. \"화내지 마, 라키틴, 난 오늘 마음이 너그러우니까. 알료샤, 당신은 왜 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있어요? 내가 무서워요?\" 그녀는 명랑한 장난기를 담아 그의 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이 사람은 슬픈 거요. 승진이 안 됐거든.\" 라키틴이 굵은 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 승진?\"\n\n\"이 사람 장로가 썩어 가고 있잖소.\"\n\n\"뭐? 무슨 말도 안 되는 소릴 하는 거야, 무슨 고약한 소리를 하고 싶은 게지. 닥쳐, 이 멍청아! 당신 무릎에 앉게 해 줘요, 알료샤, 이렇게.\" 그녀는 갑자기 깡충 다가와 웃으면서, 둥지 속 새끼 고양이처럼 그의 무릎 위로 뛰어올라 오른팔로 그의 목을 감았다. \"당신 기운을 북돋아 줄게요, 나의 경건한 분. 그래요, 정말로, 당신 무릎에 앉아도 될까요? 화나지 않을 거예요? 화나면 말해요, 내려갈 테니까?\"\n\n알료샤는 입을 열지 않았다. 그는 꼼짝하기조차 두려운 듯이 앉아 \"화나면 말해요, 내려갈게요\"라는 그녀의 말을 들었으나 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 그의 마음속에는, 이를테면 한구석에서 그를 음흉하게 지켜보던 라키틴이 예상하거나 짐작했을 법한 그 어떤 것도 없었다. 그의 마음속에 자리한 크나큰 슬픔이 일어날 법한 온갖 욕정을 죄다 집어삼켜 버렸으니, 만약 그 순간 그가 또렷이 생각할 수 있었다면, 자신이 이제 온갖 정욕과 유혹으로부터 자기를 지켜 줄 가장 든든한 갑옷을 갖추었음을 깨달았으리라. 그러나 그의 영적 상태가 어렴풋이 무감각한 데다 그를 짓누르는 슬픔에도 불구하고, 그는 자기 마음속에 일어난 새롭고 야릇한 감정에 놀라지 않을 수 없었다. 이 여자, 이 \"무시무시한\" 여자가 이제는 그에게 아무런 두려움도 일으키지 않았으니, 여자라는 것을 잠깐 떠올리기만 해도 그의 영혼 속에 일렁이던 그 두려움이 조금도 없었다. 도리어 모든 여자 가운데 가장 두려워하던 이 여자가, 지금 그의 무릎에 앉아 그를 두 팔로 안고 있는 이 여자가, 그의 안에 지금껏과는 사뭇 다른, 뜻밖의, 묘한 감정을 일으켰으니, 그것은 두려움이라고는, 예전의 그 공포라고는 흔적조차 없는, 더없이 강렬하고 더없이 순수한 관심의 감정이었다. 바로 그 점이 본능적으로 그를 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"허튼소리는 그쯤 했으면 됐소.\" 라키틴이 외쳤다. \"그보다 우리한테 샴페인이나 좀 내오시구려. 당신이 내게 진 빚이잖소, 잘 알면서!\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그렇지. 알료샤, 알아요? 내가 당신을 데려와 주면 다른 것에 더해 샴페인까지 주겠다고 이 사람한테 약속했거든요. 나도 좀 마실래! 페냐, 페냐, 미탸가 두고 간 그 병을 가져와! 어서 서둘러! 내가 이렇게 인색하긴 해도 한 병 정도는 내겠어, 너 때문이 아니라, 라키틴, 넌 독버섯이지만 이분은 매니까! 그리고 내 마음은 전혀 딴것으로 가득 차 있지만, 그래, 그러라지, 너희와 함께 마시겠어. 흥청거리며 한바탕 놀고 싶어서 못 견디겠으니.\"\n\n\"한데 대체 무슨 일이오? 그리고 그 전갈이라는 게 뭐요, 물어봐도 될까, 아니면 비밀인가?\" 라키틴은 자기를 향해 끊임없이 날아드는 면박을 못 본 척하려 애쓰며 캐묻듯 끼어들었다.\n\n\"에이, 비밀도 아니고, 너도 다 알잖아.\" 그루셴카는 갑자기 불안해진 목소리로, 라키틴 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며, 여전히 팔로 알료샤의 목을 감은 채 무릎에 앉아 있으면서도 그에게서 약간 물러나 앉으며 말했다. \"내 장교가 오고 있어, 라키틴, 내 장교가 오고 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그자가 온다는 말은 들었지만, 그렇게 가까이 와 있소?\"\n\n\"지금 모크로예에 있어. 거기서 사람을 보내겠다고, 그렇게 편지에 썼더라고. 오늘 그에게서 편지를 받았어. 시시각각 그 심부름꾼을 기다리고 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"설마! 왜 하필 모크로예에 있다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그건 긴 이야기야, 너한테 할 만큼 했어.\"\n\n\"미탸가 이제 무슨 일을 저지를 텐데—거참! 그자가 아는 거요, 모르는 거요?\"\n\n\"그가 안다고! 당연히 몰라. 알았다면 살인이 났겠지. 하지만 이제 난 그게 두렵지 않아, 그의 칼도 두렵지 않다고. 조용히 해, 라키틴, 내게 드미트리 표도로비치 이야기는 꺼내지 마, 그는 내 가슴에 멍이 들도록 했으니까. 그리고 난 지금 이 순간 그 생각은 하고 싶지 않아. 여기 알료샤를 생각할 수 있어, 알료샤를 바라볼 수 있다고…… 웃어 봐요, 사랑스러운 분, 기운 내요, 내 어리석음을, 내 기쁨을 보고 웃어 줘요…… 아, 웃는다, 웃어요! 어쩜 저렇게 다정하게 날 봐 주는지! 그리고 있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 그제 일 때문에, 그 아가씨 일 때문에 당신이 줄곧 내게 화가 나 있을 거라고 생각했어요. 난 못된 년이었어, 그게 사실이지요…… 하지만 그렇게 된 게 차라리 잘된 일이에요. 끔찍한 일이었지만, 좋은 일이기도 했어요.\" 그루셴카는 꿈꾸듯 미소 지었는데, 그 미소에 약간 잔인한 주름이 비쳤다. \"미탸가 그러는데, 그 아가씨가 내가 '매를 맞아야 한다'고 비명을 질렀다더군요. 내가 그녀를 끔찍하게 모욕하긴 했지요. 그녀가 날 부른 건, 나를 자기편으로 만들려고, 초콜릿으로 날 구슬려 자기 사람으로 만들려는 거였어요…… 아니, 그렇게 끝나 버린 게 잘된 일이에요.\" 그녀는 다시 미소 지었다. \"하지만 난 여전히 당신이 화가 났을까 봐 두려워요.\"\n\n\"맞아, 정말 그렇구려.\" 라키틴이 갑자기 진심으로 놀라며 끼어들었다. \"알료샤, 이 여자가 정말로 당신 같은 햇병아리를 무서워하다니.\"\n\n\"이분이 너한테나 햇병아리지, 라키틴…… 그건 네게 양심이라곤 없기 때문이야, 바로 그거라고! 봐, 난 이분을 온 영혼을 다해 사랑해, 그게 진실이라고! 알료샤, 내가 당신을 온 영혼을 다해 사랑한다는 게 믿겨요?\"\n\n\"이런, 부끄러움도 모르는 여자 같으니! 당신한테 사랑 고백을 하고 있구려, 알렉세이!\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 어때서, 난 이분을 사랑해!\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 장교는 어쩌고? 그리고 모크로예에서 온다는 그 더없이 귀한 전갈은?\"\n\n\"그건 전혀 다른 거야.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 여자의 사고방식이지!\"\n\n\"날 화나게 하지 마, 라키틴.\" 그루셴카가 발끈하며 그의 말을 가로챘다. \"이건 전혀 달라. 난 알료샤를 다른 식으로 사랑한다고. 사실이에요, 알료샤, 난 전에 당신에게 음흉한 속셈을 품었어요. 난 끔찍하고 사나운 인간이니까요. 하지만 다른 때에는, 알료샤, 난 당신을 내 양심처럼 여겨 왔어요. '저런 사람이라면 나처럼 더러운 것을 얼마나 경멸할까' 하고 줄곧 생각했지요. 그제 그 아가씨네서 집으로 달려오면서도 그런 생각을 했어요. 알료샤, 난 오랫동안 당신을 그렇게 생각해 왔고, 미탸도 알아요, 그에게 그 이야기를 했거든요. 미탸는 이해해요. 믿어지나요, 난 가끔 당신을 바라보면 부끄러워져요, 나 자신이 한없이 부끄러워진다고요…… 그리고 어쩌다가, 언제부터 당신을 그렇게 생각하게 됐는지는 말할 수가 없어요, 기억이 안 나요……\"\n\n페냐가 들어와 마개를 딴 병 하나와 샴페인이 담긴 잔 세 개를 쟁반에 받쳐 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n\"샴페인이 왔구려!\" 라키틴이 외쳤다. \"당신은 흥분해서, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 제정신이 아니구려. 샴페인 한 잔 들이켜고 나면 당장 춤이라도 출 기세요. 에잇, 이런 것조차 제대로 할 줄 모르다니.\" 그는 병을 보며 덧붙였다. \"노파가 부엌에서 따라 놓는 바람에 병이 미지근해진 데다 마개도 없이 들어왔구려. 뭐, 그래도 어쨌든 좀 마셔 봅시다.\"\n\n그는 탁자로 다가가 잔 하나를 들고 단숨에 비운 뒤 자기 잔에 다시 따랐다.\n\n\"샴페인이란 게 흔히 마주칠 수 있는 게 아니지.\" 그는 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"자, 알료샤, 잔을 들어요, 당신 솜씨 좀 보여 주시오! 무엇을 위해 마실까? 천국의 문을 위해? 잔을 들어요, 그루셴카, 당신도 천국의 문을 위해 마시구려.\"\n\n\"무슨 천국의 문?\"\n\n그녀가 잔을 들었고, 알료샤도 자기 잔을 들어 한 모금 맛보고는 도로 내려놓았다.\n\n\"아니, 안 마시는 게 좋겠어요.\" 그는 부드럽게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그렇게 큰소리를 쳐 놓고!\" 라키틴이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면 나도 안 마실래.\" 그루셴카가 맞장구쳤다. \"사실 난 조금도 마시고 싶지 않아. 라키틴, 너 혼자 병을 다 비워도 돼. 알료샤가 마시면 나도 마시겠어.\"\n\n\"이 무슨 가슴 뭉클한 감상이람!\" 라키틴이 빈정대며 말했다. \"게다가 저 사람 무릎에까지 앉아 있고! 이 사람이야 슬퍼할 일이 있다지만, 당신은 대체 무슨 일이오? 이 사람은 제 하느님께 반항하고 소시지라도 먹어 치울 판인데……\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"이 사람 장로가 오늘 죽었거든, 조시마 신부, 그 성자 말이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 조시마 신부님이 돌아가셨다는 거야.\" 그루셴카가 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님, 난 몰랐어!\" 그녀는 경건하게 성호를 그었다. \"세상에, 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거람, 이런 순간에 이렇게 이분 무릎에 앉아 있다니!\" 그녀는 소스라치게 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어나, 곧장 그의 무릎에서 미끄러져 내려와 소파에 앉았다.\n\n알료샤는 의아한 눈길로 한참 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴에 무언가 빛이 동트는 듯했다.\n\n\"라키틴.\" 그는 갑자기 단호하고 큰 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 하느님께 반항했다고 빈정대지 마. 나는 너에게 화를 내고 싶지 않으니, 너도 좀 더 너그러워져야 해. 나는 너 같은 사람은 가져 본 적도 없는 보물을 잃었으니, 너는 지금 나를 판단할 수 없어. 차라리 이분을 봐—이분이 나를 얼마나 가엾이 여기는지 보이지 않아? 나는 사악한 영혼을 찾아 여기 왔어—나 자신이 비루하고 사악했기에 악에 이끌렸던 거야, 그런데 진정한 누이를 찾았어, 보물을 찾았다고—사랑하는 마음을. 이분은 방금 나를 가엾이 여겨 주었어…… 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 당신을 두고 하는 말입니다. 당신이 내 영혼을 깊은 나락에서 들어 올려 주었어요.\"\n\n알료샤의 입술이 떨렸고, 그는 숨을 삼켰다.\n\n\"이 여자가 당신을 구원했다는 말씀이로군.\" 라키틴이 악의에 차서 웃었다. \"이 여자가 당신을 제 손아귀에 넣으려 했다는 걸 당신은 알기나 하오?\"\n\n\"그만해, 라키틴.\" 그루셴카가 벌떡 일어섰다. \"둘 다 조용히 해. 이제 내가 다 이야기할게. 조용히 해요, 알료샤, 당신 말은 나를 부끄럽게 해요, 난 나쁘지 좋은 사람이 아니거든요—내가 바로 그런 사람이에요. 그리고 너도 조용히 해, 라키틴, 넌 거짓말을 하고 있으니까. 그를 내 손아귀에 넣으려는 비열한 생각을 품었던 건 맞지만, 지금 넌 거짓말을 하고 있어, 이제는 모든 게 달라졌으니까. 그리고 라키틴, 네 입에서 더는 아무 소리도 듣고 싶지 않아.\"\n\n이 모든 말을 그루셴카는 극도로 격앙된 채 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"둘 다 미쳤군.\" 라키틴은 어이없다는 듯 두 사람을 바라보며 말했다. \"꼭 정신병원에 와 있는 기분이오. 둘 다 어찌나 마음이 약해졌는지 곧 울음이라도 터뜨릴 판이군.\"\n\n\"울 거야, 정말 울 테야.\" 그루셴카가 거듭 말했다. \"이분이 날 누이라고 불렀어, 난 그걸 결코 잊지 못할 거야. 다만 너한테 한마디 해 두지, 라키틴, 내가 나쁜 인간이긴 해도, 양파 한 알은 베푼 적이 있어.\"\n\n\"양파라고? 빌어먹을, 당신 정말 미쳤군.\"\n\n라키틴은 두 사람의 열띤 모습에 어리둥절했다. 그는 모욕감을 느끼고 짜증이 났는데, 사실 두 사람이 저마다 평생에 흔히 찾아오지 않는 영적 위기를 지나고 있는 중임을 헤아렸어야 마땅했다. 그러나 라키틴은 자기 자신에 관한 일에는 매우 예민했으되, 타인의 감정과 느낌에 대해서는 무척이나 둔감했으니—그것은 한편으로는 그의 젊음과 미숙함 때문이었고, 한편으로는 그의 극심한 이기심 때문이었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 알료샤.\" 그루셴카는 신경질적인 웃음을 지으며 그에게로 돌아섰다. \"내가 양파 한 알을 베풀었다고 라키틴한테 말한 건 으스대려고 한 거지만, 당신한테 이 이야기를 하는 건 으스대려는 게 아니에요. 그냥 한 이야기일 뿐인데, 좋은 이야기예요. 내가 어릴 적, 지금도 나와 함께 사는 식모 마트료나한테서 들었던 거예요. 이런 이야기랍니다. 옛날 옛적에 한 농사꾼 아낙이 있었는데, 어찌나 악독한 여자였는지 몰라요. 그 여자가 죽었는데, 평생 선행이라곤 단 하나도 남기지 못했어요. 악마들이 그 여자를 붙잡아 불바다 호수에 처넣었지요. 그래서 그 여자의 수호천사가 서서, 하느님께 아뢸 그 여자의 선행을 무엇이든 떠올려 보려 애를 썼어요. '저 여자가 한번은 제 텃밭에서 양파 한 알을 뽑아서' 하고 천사가 말했어요, '거지 여인에게 준 적이 있습니다.' 그러자 하느님이 대답하셨지요. '그러면 그 양파를 가져다가 호수 속의 저 여자에게 내밀어 붙잡게 하여 끌어내 보아라. 네가 그 여자를 호수에서 끌어낼 수 있거든 천국으로 오게 하고, 만일 양파가 끊어지거든 저 여자는 있던 자리에 그대로 두어라.' 천사가 그 여자에게 달려가 양파를 내밀었어요. '자.' 천사가 말했지요, '이걸 붙잡아라, 내가 끌어내 주겠다.' 그러고는 조심조심 그 여자를 끌어올리기 시작했어요. 막 그 여자를 다 끌어낸 참인데, 호수에 있던 다른 죄인들이 그 여자가 끌려 나오는 걸 보고는 자기들도 함께 끌려 나가려고 그 여자에게 매달리기 시작했어요. 그런데 그 여자는 어찌나 악독한지 그들을 발로 차 대기 시작했어요. '끌려 나가는 건 나지 너희가 아니야. 이건 내 양파야, 너희 게 아니라고.' 그 여자가 그 말을 하자마자 양파가 뚝 끊어져 버렸어요. 그래서 그 여자는 호수로 떨어졌고, 지금까지도 거기서 타고 있답니다. 그래서 천사는 울면서 떠나갔지요. 자, 이게 그 이야기예요, 알료샤. 난 이걸 외우고 있어요, 내가 바로 그 악독한 여자거든요. 라키틴한테는 양파 한 알을 베풀었다고 으스댔지만, 당신한테는 이렇게 말하겠어요. '난 평생 양파 한 알 베푼 것 말고는 한 일이 아무것도 없어요, 그게 내가 한 단 하나의 선행이에요.' 그러니 날 칭찬하지 말아요, 알료샤, 날 좋은 사람이라 여기지 말아요, 난 나쁜 인간이에요, 난 악독한 여자라고요, 당신이 날 칭찬하면 난 부끄러워져요. 에잇, 모든 걸 다 털어놔야겠어요. 들어 봐요, 알료샤. 난 당신을 손에 넣고 싶어서 안달이 난 나머지, 라키틴한테 당신을 데려와 주면 이십오 루블을 주겠다고 약속했어요. 가만있어, 라키틴, 기다려!\"\n\n그녀는 빠른 걸음으로 탁자로 가서 서랍을 열고 지갑을 꺼내더니 이십오 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"무슨 헛소리요! 무슨 헛소리야!\" 라키틴이 당황하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"받아, 라키틴, 내가 너한테 진 빚이니 거절할 까닭이야 없겠지, 네가 직접 달라고 한 거잖아.\" 그러고는 지폐를 그에게 던졌다.\n\n\"내가 거절할 리가 있나.\" 라키틴은 굵은 소리로 말했는데, 분명 멋쩍어하면서도 그 거북함을 허세로 얼버무렸다. \"이거 아주 요긴하게 쓰겠군. 어리석은 자들이란 영리한 자들 잇속 채우라고 생기는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"이제 입 다물어, 라키틴, 지금 내가 할 이야기는 네 귀에 들으라는 게 아니야. 저 구석에 앉아서 조용히 있어. 넌 우리를 좋아하지도 않으니 입을 다물고 있으라고.\"\n\n\"내가 당신들을 좋아할 까닭이 어디 있다고?\" 라키틴은 못마땅한 심기를 감추지 않고 으르렁댔다. 그는 이십오 루블짜리 지폐를 호주머니에 넣었는데, 알료샤가 그걸 본 것이 부끄러웠다. 그는 알료샤 모르게 나중에 돈을 받을 작정이었던 터라, 이제 부끄러운 나머지 화가 치밀었다. 그 순간까지 그는, 그루셴카에게서 뜯어낼 것이 있었으므로 그녀가 면박을 주어도 너무 노골적으로 맞받아치지 않는 편이 분별 있다고 여겼다. 그러나 이제는 그 역시 화가 났다.\n\n\"사람이란 무슨 까닭이 있어서 사랑하는 법인데, 당신들 둘 중 누가 나한테 해 준 게 뭐가 있다고?\"\n\n\"사람은 까닭 없이 사랑해야 하는 거야, 알료샤가 그러는 것처럼.\"\n\n\"이 사람이 당신을 어떻게 사랑한다고? 당신이 그렇게 호들갑을 떨 만큼 이 사람이 뭘 어떻게 보여 줬소?\"\n\n그루셴카는 방 한가운데 서 있었다. 그녀는 격앙되어 말했고, 그 목소리에는 신경질적인 가락이 서려 있었다.\n\n\"입 다물어, 라키틴, 넌 우리에 대해 아무것도 몰라! 그리고 다시는 내게 감히 그런 식으로 지껄이지 마. 어떻게 그렇게 함부로 굴 수가 있어! 저 구석에 앉아서 내 하인이라도 된 양 조용히 있으라고! 그리고 이제, 알료샤, 당신한테 모든 진실을 다 말할게요, 내가 얼마나 못된 인간인지 알 수 있도록 말이에요! 라키틴한테 하는 말이 아니라, 당신한테 하는 말이에요. 난 당신을 파멸시키고 싶었어요, 알료샤, 그게 거룩한 진실이에요. 정말 그러려고 했어요. 어찌나 그러고 싶었던지 라키틴한테 뇌물을 줘서 당신을 데려오게 한 거예요. 그런데 내가 왜 그런 짓을 하고 싶었을까요? 알료샤, 당신은 그런 걸 전혀 몰랐어요, 당신은 나한테서 고개를 돌렸고, 나를 지나칠 때면 눈을 내리깔았지요. 그런데 난 오늘 이전에도 당신을 백 번은 바라봤어요. 사람들한테 당신에 대해 묻기 시작했지요. 당신 얼굴이 내 가슴을 떠나지 않았어요. '저 사람은 날 경멸해.' 난 그렇게 생각했어요. '나를 쳐다보지도 않아.' 그리고 마침내 그게 어찌나 사무치던지, 사내아이 하나를 그토록 무서워하는 나 자신이 의아할 정도였어요. 저 사람을 내 손아귀에 넣어서 비웃어 주겠어. 난 악의와 분노로 가득 차 있었어요. 믿어지나요, 여기서는 누구도 감히 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 무슨 흉계를 품고 다가올 엄두를 못 내요. 여기서 내가 상대하는 남자라곤 늙은 쿠지마뿐이에요. 난 그에게 매여 팔린 몸이니까요. 사탄이 우리를 맺어 준 거지요, 하지만 그 밖에는 아무도 없었어요. 그런데 당신을 보면서, 저 사람을 내 손아귀에 넣어 비웃어 주겠다고 생각했어요. 보다시피 난 이렇게 악독한 년이에요, 그런데도 당신은 날 누이라고 불렀지요! 그리고 이제 나를 농락했던 그 남자가 돌아왔어요. 난 여기 앉아 그에게서 올 전갈을 기다리는 중이에요. 그런데 그 남자가 나한테 어떤 존재였는지 알아요? 오 년 전, 쿠지마가 날 여기 데려왔을 때, 난 누구도 내 모습을 보거나 내 목소리를 듣지 못하도록 문을 걸어 잠그고 틀어박혀 지냈어요. 난 어리석은 풋내기 계집애였어요. 여기 앉아 흐느껴 울곤 했지요. 밤새 잠 못 이루며 생각했어요. '날 농락한 그 남자는 지금 어디 있을까? 십중팔구 다른 여자랑 날 비웃고 있겠지. 그를 한 번이라도 볼 수 있다면, 다시 만날 수만 있다면, 본때를 보여 줄 텐데, 본때를 보여 줄 텐데!' 밤이면 어둠 속에서 베개에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼며 그 일을 곱씹곤 했어요. 일부러 내 가슴을 쥐어뜯으며 내 분노를 고소해하며 음미했지요. '본때를 보여 줄 테야, 본때를 보여 줄 테야!' 어둠 속에서 그렇게 외쳐 댔어요. 그러다 문득, 사실 난 그에게 아무 짓도 못 할 거라는 생각, 그는 그때 날 비웃고 있거나 어쩌면 날 까맣게 잊었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들면, 난 바닥에 몸을 던져 하염없이 눈물에 잠긴 채 새벽이 올 때까지 거기 누워 떨곤 했어요. 아침이면 개보다 더 사나운 꼴로 일어나, 온 세상을 갈가리 찢어발길 기세였지요. 그런데 그다음 어찌 됐는지 알아요? 난 돈을 모으기 시작했고, 마음이 모질어졌고, 살이 올랐어요—더 영리해졌다고 할까요? 아니, 온 세상 누구도 그걸 알지 못해요, 아무도 모르지만, 밤이 찾아오면 난 가끔 오 년 전 어리석은 계집애였을 때처럼 누워, 이를 악물고 밤새 울며 생각해요. '본때를 보여 줄 테야, 본때를 보여 줄 테야!' 들려요? 자, 이제 당신은 나를 이해할 거예요. 한 달 전 내게 편지 한 통이 왔어요—그가 온다고, 그는 홀아비가 됐고, 나를 보고 싶어 한다고요. 난 숨이 멎는 것 같았어요. 그러다 문득 이런 생각이 들었지요. '그가 와서 휘파람을 불어 날 부르면, 난 매 맞은 개처럼 그에게 기어 돌아가겠지.' 나 자신을 믿을 수가 없었어요. 내가 이토록 비굴한가? 그에게 달려갈 것인가 말 것인가? 그래서 난 이 한 달 내내 나 자신에게 어찌나 화가 났던지 오 년 전보다 더 못한 꼴이 됐어요. 이제 알겠어요, 알료샤, 내가 얼마나 난폭하고 앙심 깊은 인간인지? 당신한테 모든 진실을 다 보여 줬어요! 난 그 다른 남자에게 달려가지 않으려고 미탸를 가지고 놀았던 거예요. 입 다물어, 라키틴, 나를 판단하는 건 네가 할 일이 아니야, 너한테 하는 말이 아니라고. 너희가 들어오기 전에 난 여기 누워 기다리며, 골똘히 생각하며, 내 앞날 전부를 정하고 있었어, 그러니 너희는 내 가슴속에 무엇이 있었는지 결코 알 수 없을 거야. 그래요, 알료샤, 당신 아가씨한테 전해 줘요, 그제 일어난 일로 나한테 화내지 말라고요…… 온 세상 누구도 내가 지금 어떤 일을 겪고 있는지 모르고, 또 결코 알 수도 없어요…… 어쩌면 난 오늘 칼을 품고 갈지도 몰라요, 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없으니……\"\n\n그리고 이 \"비극적인\" 말끝에 그루셴카는 무너지듯 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 소파 베개에 몸을 던지며 어린아이처럼 흐느껴 울었다.\n\n알료샤는 일어나 라키틴에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"미샤.\" 그가 말했다. \"화내지 마. 이 여자가 너한테 상처를 주긴 했지만, 화내지 마. 방금 이 여자가 한 말을 들었잖아? 인간의 인내심에 너무 많은 걸 요구해선 안 돼, 자비로워야 하는 거야.\"\n\n알료샤는 자기 마음이 본능적으로 시키는 대로 이렇게 말했다. 그는 무언가 말하지 않을 수 없어 라키틴에게로 돌아섰다. 만일 라키틴이 거기 없었더라면, 그는 허공에 대고라도 말했으리라. 그러나 라키틴이 그를 빈정대듯 바라보자 알료샤는 말을 뚝 멈추었다.\n\n\"어젯밤 네 장로의 가르침을 잔뜩 채워 넣고 와서는, 이제 그걸 나한테 쏟아 내야 직성이 풀리는 모양이군, 하느님의 사람 알렉세이!\" 라키틴은 증오에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"웃지 마, 라키틴, 비웃지 마, 죽은 분에 대해 함부로 말하지 마—그분은 세상 누구보다 훌륭한 분이었어!\" 알료샤가 목멘 소리로 외쳤다. \"난 심판자로서가 아니라 심판받는 자들 가운데 가장 낮은 자로서 너한테 말한 거야. 이 여자에 비하면 내가 다 뭐란 말이야? 난 내 파멸을 찾아 여기 왔고, 비겁하게도 속으로 '그게 무슨 대수랴?' 하고 되뇌었어. 그런데 이 여자는, 오 년을 고통 속에 보낸 끝에, 누군가 가슴에서 우러난 한마디만 건네도—그 한마디에 모든 걸 잊고, 모든 걸 용서하며 눈물짓는 거야! 자기를 농락했던 남자가 돌아와 사람을 보내 부르니, 이 여자는 그에게 모든 걸 용서하고 기쁘게 그를 맞으러 서둘러 가면서도 칼은 품고 가지 않겠다는 거야. 안 품을 거라고! 아니, 난 그렇지 못해. 너는 어떤지 모르겠다, 미샤, 하지만 난 그렇지 못해. 이건 내게 하나의 가르침이야…… 이 여자는 우리보다 더 사랑할 줄 아는 사람이야…… 너는 이 여자가 방금 우리한테 들려준 이야기를 전에도 들은 적이 있어? 아니, 없을 거야. 들었더라면 진작 이 여자를 이해했을 테니까…… 그리고 그제 모욕당한 그 사람도 이 여자를 용서해야 해! 알게 되면 용서할 거야…… 그리고 그녀는 알게 될 거야…… 이 영혼은 아직 스스로와 화해하지 못했어, 다정하게 대해 줘야 해…… 그 영혼 속에 보물이 있을지도 몰라……\"\n\n알료샤는 숨이 가빠 말을 멈추었다. 못마땅한 심기에도 불구하고 라키틴은 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그는 온순한 알료샤에게서 그런 일장 연설이 나오리라곤 미처 예상치 못했던 것이다.\n\n\"이 여자가 자기 변호를 해 줄 사람을 하나 찾았군! 아니, 당신 이 여자한테 반한 거요? 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 우리 수도사가 정말로 당신한테 반했소, 당신이 그를 정복한 거요!\" 그는 천박하게 웃으며 외쳤다.\n\n그루셴카는 베개에서 고개를 들고, 눈물에 젖은 얼굴에 다정한 미소를 빛내며 알료샤를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내버려 둬요, 알료샤, 나의 천사여. 보다시피 저런 인간이니, 당신이 말 섞을 상대가 못 돼요. 미하일 오시포비치.\" 그녀는 라키틴에게로 돌아섰다. \"너한테 무례하게 군 걸 사과하려고 했는데, 이제는 그러고 싶지 않아. 알료샤, 이리 와요, 여기 앉아요.\" 그녀는 행복한 미소를 지으며 그를 손짓해 불렀다. \"그래요, 여기 앉아요. 말해 줘요.\" 그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 미소 띤 얼굴로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"말해 줘요, 내가 그 남자를 사랑하는 거예요, 아닌 거예요? 나를 농락한 그 남자를, 내가 사랑하는 거예요, 아니에요? 당신이 오기 전에 난 여기 어둠 속에 누워, 내 마음에게 그를 사랑하는지 묻고 있었어요. 날 위해 결정해 줘요, 알료샤, 때가 왔어요, 당신이 말하는 대로 따를게요. 그를 용서해야 하나요, 말아야 하나요?\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 이미 그를 용서했잖아요.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 난 정말로 그를 용서했어요.\" 그루셴카가 생각에 잠겨 중얼거렸다. \"어쩌면 이렇게 비굴한 마음일까! 이 비굴한 내 마음을 위하여!\" 그녀는 탁자에서 잔을 낚아채 단숨에 비우더니, 허공으로 치켜들었다가 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다. 잔은 요란한 소리를 내며 깨졌다. 약간 잔인한 주름이 그녀의 미소에 어렸다.\n\n\"하긴 어쩌면 난 그를 용서하지 않은 건지도 몰라.\" 그녀는 목소리에 어떤 위협 같은 것을 담아 말하더니, 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 눈을 바닥으로 떨구었다. \"어쩌면 내 마음은 이제 막 용서할 채비를 하고 있을 뿐인지도 몰라. 난 내 마음과 싸울 거예요. 있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 이 오 년 동안 내 눈물을 사랑하게 됐어요…… 어쩌면 난 그가 아니라 내 원한을 사랑하는 건지도 몰라요……\"\n\n\"흥, 난 그자 처지가 되고 싶진 않군.\" 라키틴이 쉭쉭거리듯 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그래, 넌 그렇게 안 될 거야, 라키틴, 넌 결코 그자 처지가 못 돼. 넌 내 구두나 닦으면 돼, 라키틴, 그게 너한테 어울리는 자리지. 넌 나 같은 여자는 결코 얻지 못할 거야…… 그리고 어쩌면 그도 못 얻겠지……\"\n\n\"못 얻는다고? 그럼 왜 그렇게 차려입었소?\" 라키틴이 독기 어린 비웃음을 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"차려입었다고 날 비웃지 마, 라키틴, 넌 내 가슴속에 든 걸 다 알지 못해! 내가 이 단장한 차림을 벗어 던지기로 마음먹으면, 지금 당장, 이 순간이라도 벗어 던질 거야.\" 그녀는 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이 단장이 무엇을 위한 건지 넌 모를 거야, 라키틴! 어쩌면 난 그를 만나 이렇게 말할지도 몰라. '당신은 전에 내가 이런 모습인 걸 본 적 있어요?' 그는 비쩍 마르고 폐병이나 앓는, 울보 같은 열일곱 살짜리 계집애를 버리고 떠났지. 난 그의 곁에 앉아 그를 홀리고 애를 태워 줄 거야. '지금 내 모습이 어때 보여요?' 그에게 이렇게 말할 테야. '자, 그걸로 충분하겠지요, 그런데 친애하는 양반, 잔과 입술 사이에서도 미끄러지는 일은 허다하답니다!' 어쩌면 이 단장은 그걸 위한 건지도 몰라, 라키틴.\" 그루셴카는 악의에 찬 웃음으로 말을 맺었다. \"난 난폭하고 앙심이 깊어요, 알료샤, 난 이 단장을 벗어 던지고, 내 아름다움을 망가뜨리고, 내 얼굴을 지지고, 칼로 그어 거지가 될 거예요. 마음만 먹으면 이제 누구를 보러 어디로도 가지 않을 거예요. 마음만 먹으면 쿠지마가 내게 준 모든 걸 그에게 돌려보낼 거예요, 내일이라도, 그의 돈도 죄다 돌려주고, 남은 평생 남의 집 허드렛일이나 하러 다닐 거라고요. 내가 그러지 못할 거라고 생각하지, 라키틴, 내가 그럴 엄두도 못 낼 거라고? 난 할 거야, 할 거라고, 당장이라도 할 수 있어, 다만 날 더 약 올리지나 마…… 그러고는 그를 쫓아내 버릴 거야, 그의 면전에 손가락을 튕겨 줄 테니, 그는 다시는 날 보지 못할 거야!\"\n\n그녀는 마지막 말을 신경질적인 비명처럼 내질렀으나, 다시 무너지듯 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 베개에 파묻은 채 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었다.\n\n라키틴이 일어섰다.\n\n\"이제 갈 시간이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"늦었소, 수도원 문이 닫혀 우린 못 들어가게 될 거요.\"\n\n그루셴카는 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"설마 가려는 건 아니지요, 알료샤!\" 그녀가 슬픔에 찬 놀라움으로 외쳤다. \"나한테 무슨 짓을 하는 거예요? 내 마음을 휘저어 놓고, 날 괴롭혀 놓고, 이제 이 밤을 혼자 견디라고 날 두고 가려는 거예요!\"\n\n\"이 사람이 당신과 함께 밤을 보낼 수야 없지! 하긴 그러고 싶다면야, 그렇게 하라지! 난 혼자 가겠소.\" 라키틴이 비아냥대듯 조롱했다.\n\n\"닥쳐, 못된 혓바닥아!\" 그루셴카가 그에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"넌 이분이 와서 해 준 그런 말을 내게 한마디도 해 준 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"이 사람이 당신한테 무슨 그리 특별한 말을 했다는 거요?\" 라키틴이 짜증스레 물었다.\n\n\"말할 수 없어, 나도 몰라. 이분이 내게 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠지만, 그건 곧장 내 가슴으로 파고들었어. 이분이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰단 말이야…… 이분은 나를 가엾이 여겨 준 첫 번째, 단 한 사람이야, 바로 그거라고. 왜 진작 오지 않았어요, 천사 같은 분?\" 그녀는 갑자기 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. \"난 평생 당신 같은 사람을 기다려 왔어요, 당신 같은 사람이 와서 날 용서해 주리라는 걸 알고 있었어요. 난 믿었어요, 내가 이토록 못된 인간이라도, 누군가 정말로 날 사랑해 주리라는 걸, 부끄러운 사랑만이 아닌 사랑으로요!\"\n\n\"내가 당신한테 무얼 해 줬다고요?\" 알료샤가 다정한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 몸을 숙이고 가만히 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"난 그저 당신한테 양파 한 알을 주었을 뿐이에요, 자그마한 양파 한 알, 그게 다예요!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그 자신도 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 바로 그 순간 복도에서 갑자기 소란한 소리가 나며 누군가 현관으로 들어섰다. 그루셴카는 몹시 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어났다. 페냐가 요란하게 방으로 뛰어들며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아씨, 사랑하는 아씨, 심부름꾼이 말을 달려 왔어요.\" 그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다. \"모크로예에서 아씨한테 마차가 왔어요, 마부 티모페이가 말 세 필을 끌고요, 지금 막 새 말로 갈아매고 있어요…… 편지예요, 여기 편지가 왔어요, 아씨.\"\n\n손에 편지가 들려 있었고, 그녀는 이야기하는 내내 그것을 허공에 흔들어 댔다. 그루셴카는 그녀에게서 편지를 낚아채 촛불 곁으로 가져갔다. 그것은 그저 몇 줄짜리 짧은 쪽지였다. 그녀는 단숨에 그것을 읽었다.\n\n\"그가 날 부르고 있어.\" 그녀는 핼쑥하게 일그러진 얼굴에 파리한 미소를 띠며 외쳤다. \"휘파람을 부는구나! 기어 돌아가라, 강아지야!\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 단 한순간 망설이듯 서 있었을 뿐이었다. 별안간 피가 머리로 솟구쳐 두 뺨에 홍조가 번졌다.\n\n\"갈 거야.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"내 인생 오 년! 잘 있어요! 잘 있어요, 알료샤, 내 운명은 정해졌어요. 가요, 가, 다들 날 두고 떠나요, 다시는 내 모습을 보이지 않을게요! 그루셴카는 새로운 삶을 향해 날아가요…… 너도 내게 악감정 품지 마, 라키틴. 난 어쩌면 죽으러 가는 건지도 몰라! 어휴! 꼭 취한 것만 같아!\"\n\n그녀는 별안간 그들을 두고 자기 침실로 달려 들어갔다.\n\n\"이런, 이제 우리 따위는 안중에도 없군!\" 라키틴이 투덜거렸다. \"갑시다, 안 그러면 저 여자의 비명을 또 듣게 될지도 모르니. 이놈의 눈물이며 울음소리에 진절머리가 나는군.\"\n\n알료샤는 기계적으로 이끌려 밖으로 나갔다. 마당에는 포장마차 한 대가 서 있었다. 사람들이 말들을 채에서 풀어내고 있었고, 등불을 든 자들이 이리저리 분주히 오갔다. 열린 문으로 새 말 세 필이 끌려 들어오고 있었다. 그러나 알료샤와 라키틴이 층계 아래에 다다랐을 때, 그루셴카의 침실 창문이 갑자기 열리더니 그녀가 낭랑한 목소리로 알료샤를 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"알료샤, 당신 형 미탸한테 내 인사를 전해 주고, 내가 그에게 비참함을 안겨 주긴 했어도 날 나쁘게 기억하진 말라고 전해 줘요. 그리고 또 내 말이라며 이렇게도 전해 줘요. '그루셴카는 한낱 악당의 차지가 됐을 뿐, 고결한 마음을 지닌 당신의 차지가 되지는 못했어요.' 그리고 이것도 덧붙여 줘요, 그루셴카는 그를 단 한 시간 사랑했노라고, 단 한 시간, 그 짧은 한 시간을 사랑했으니, 그 한 시간을 평생토록 기억하라고요—'그루셴카가 당신에게 그러라고 했다'고 말해 줘요!\"\n\n그녀는 흐느낌으로 가득 찬 목소리로 말을 맺었다. 창문이 쾅 하고 닫혔다.\n\n\"흠, 흠!\" 라키틴이 웃으며 으르렁댔다. \"당신 형 미탸를 죽여 놓고는 그걸 평생 기억하라니! 어쩌면 저리도 표독스러울까!\"\n\n알료샤는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 마치 그 말을 듣지 못한 듯했다. 그는 끔찍이도 다급한 사람처럼 라키틴 곁에서 빠르게 걸었다. 그는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 기계적으로 움직였다. 라키틴은 갑자기 벌어진 상처를 건드리기라도 한 듯한 따끔한 통증을 느꼈다. 그는 그루셴카와 알료샤를 맺어 줌으로써 전혀 다른 무언가를 기대했었다. 그가 바라던 것과는 사뭇 다른 일이 벌어진 것이다.\n\n\"저 여자 장교라는 자는 폴란드인이오.\" 그는 자제하며 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"게다가 이제는 사실 장교도 뭣도 아니지. 시베리아 어딘가 중국 국경 근처에서 세관 일을 했다는데, 짐작건대 비쩍 마른 보잘것없는 폴란드인 거지나 다름없는 자일 거요. 일자리도 잃었다더군. 이제 그루셴카가 돈푼깨나 모았다는 소문을 듣고 다시 나타난 거지—그게 그 수수께끼의 진상이오.\"\n\n이번에도 알료샤는 듣지 못한 듯했다. 라키틴은 자신을 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n\"흥, 그래, 죄인을 구원하셨나?\" 그가 악의에 차서 웃었다. \"막달라 마리아를 참된 길로 돌려놓으셨나? 일곱 마귀를 쫓아냈나, 어? 자, 보시오, 방금 당신이 학수고대하던 그 기적이 일어났구려!\"\n\n\"그만해, 라키틴.\" 알료샤가 아픈 마음으로 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그 이십오 루블 때문에 이제 나를 경멸하는 건가? 친구를 팔아넘겼다, 그 말이지. 하지만 알다시피 당신은 그리스도가 아니고, 나도 유다가 아니야.\"\n\n\"아, 라키틴, 정말이지 난 그 일을 잊고 있었어.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"네가 직접 그 일을 일깨워 주는구나……\"\n\n그러나 이것이 라키틴에게는 마지막 한 방이 되고 말았다.\n\n\"네놈들 모두, 하나하나 다 뒈져 버려라!\" 그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"내가 무슨 빌어먹을 마음으로 너 따위와 어울렸을까? 이제부터 너 같은 건 알고 싶지도 않다. 혼자 가라, 저기가 네 갈 길이다!\"\n\n그러고는 홱 다른 골목으로 꺾어 들며, 알료샤를 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨 둔 채 떠나 버렸다. 알료샤는 시내를 벗어나 들판을 가로질러 수도원을 향해 걸었다.\n\n# Chapter IV. Cana Of Galilee\n\n수도원의 관습으로 따지자면 알료샤가 암자로 돌아온 것은 무척 늦은 시각이었으니, 문지기는 특별한 출입구로 그를 들여보내 주었다. 시계가 아홉 시를 친 뒤였는데—모두에게 그토록 격동의 하루가 지나간 끝의 휴식과 안식의 시각이었다. 알료샤는 조심스레 문을 열고 장로의 암실로 들어섰으니, 그곳에는 이제 그의 관이 놓여 있었다. 암실에는 관 곁에서 홀로 복음서를 읽고 있는 파이시 신부와, 어린 수련자 포르피리 외에는 아무도 없었는데, 포르피리는 전날 밤의 담화와 그날의 어수선한 사건들에 지친 나머지 옆방 마룻바닥에서 젊음의 깊고 곤한 잠에 빠져 있었다. 파이시 신부는 알료샤가 들어오는 소리를 들었으면서도 그쪽으로 눈길조차 돌리지 않았다. 알료샤는 문에서 오른쪽 구석으로 돌아가 무릎을 꿇고 기도하기 시작했다.\n\n그의 영혼은 차고 넘쳤으나 그것은 뒤섞인 감정들이었으니, 단 하나의 감각도 뚜렷하게 두드러지지 않았고, 오히려 느리고 끊임없는 순환 속에서 하나가 다른 하나를 밀어내고 있었다. 그러나 그의 마음에는 어떤 감미로움이 깃들어 있었으며, 이상하게도 알료샤는 그것을 두고 놀라지 않았다. 그는 또다시 자기 앞의 그 관을, 그토록 소중한 망자의 가려진 형상을 바라보았으나, 아침나절의 그 울음과 사무치는 슬픔은 더 이상 그의 영혼을 쑤시지 않았다. 들어서자마자 그는 성스러운 성소 앞에 엎드리듯 관 앞에 엎드렸으나, 기쁨이, 기쁨이 그의 마음과 가슴 속에서 타오르고 있었다. 암실의 단 하나뿐인 창문이 열려 있어 공기는 신선하고 서늘했다. \"창문을 열어 둔 걸 보면 냄새가 더 심해진 모양이군.\" 하고 알료샤는 생각했다. 그러나 몇 시간 전만 해도 그토록 끔찍하고 모욕적으로 여겨졌던 그 부패의 냄새에 대한 이 생각조차도, 이제는 그를 비참하게 만들거나 분개하게 만들지 않았다. 그는 조용히 기도하기 시작했으나, 곧 자신이 거의 기계적으로 기도하고 있음을 느꼈다. 단편적인 생각들이 그의 영혼을 떠돌며 별처럼 반짝이다가 이내 다시 꺼지고는, 또 다른 생각들에 자리를 내주었다. 그러나 그래도 그의 영혼에는 만물의 온전함에 대한 어떤 감각이—굳건하고 위안을 주는 무언가가—군림하고 있었으며, 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었다. 때때로 그는 열렬히 기도하기 시작했고, 자신의 감사와 사랑을 쏟아 내고 싶어 했다……\n\n그러나 기도를 시작하자마자 그는 문득 다른 무언가로 옮겨 가, 기도도 그것을 가로막은 것도 다 잊은 채 상념에 잠겨 들었다. 그는 파이시 신부가 읽는 것에 귀를 기울이기 시작했으나, 기진맥진한 탓에 차츰 졸기 시작했다.\n\n\"사흘째 되던 날에 갈릴리 가나에 혼인이 있었으니,\" 하고 파이시 신부가 읽었다. \"예수의 어머니도 거기 계시고, 예수와 그 제자들도 그 혼인에 청함을 받았더라.\"\n\n\"혼인? 그게 무엇이지?…… 혼인이라!\" 하는 생각이 알료샤의 머릿속을 빙글빙글 떠돌았다. \"그녀에게도 행복이 있구나…… 그녀는 잔치에 갔어…… 아니, 그녀는 칼을 들지 않았어…… 그건 그저 비극적인 말이었을 뿐…… 그래…… 비극적인 말은 용서받아야 해, 그래야만 해. 비극적인 말은 마음을 위로해 주니까…… 그것이 없다면 슬픔은 사람이 견디기에 너무 무거울 거야. 라키틴은 뒷골목으로 가 버렸지. 라키틴이 제 억울함을 곱씹는 한, 그는 언제나 뒷골목으로 가 버릴 거야…… 하지만 큰길은…… 그 길은 넓고 곧으며 수정처럼 밝고, 그 끝에는 태양이 있어…… 아!…… 지금 뭘 읽고 있는 거지?\"……\n\n\"포도주가 떨어진지라 예수의 어머니가 예수에게 이르되, 저들에게 포도주가 없다 하니\"…… 하고 알료샤는 들었다.\n\n\"아, 그래, 이 대목을 놓치고 있었는데, 놓치고 싶지 않았어, 이 구절을 나는 사랑하니까. 갈릴리 가나, 첫 번째 기적이야…… 아, 그 기적! 아, 그 감미로운 기적! 그리스도께서 찾아오신 것은 사람들의 슬픔이 아니라 그들의 기쁨이었으니, 그분은 사람들의 즐거움을 도우시려고 첫 기적을 행하셨어…… '사람을 사랑하는 자는 그들의 즐거움도 사랑한다'…… 그분은 늘 그 말씀을 되풀이하셨고, 그것은 그분의 핵심 사상 가운데 하나였지…… '기쁨 없이는 살아갈 수 없다'고 미탸는 말하지…… 그래, 미탸…… '참되고 선한 모든 것은 언제나 용서로 가득 차 있다'고 그분은 그 말씀도 하시곤 했어\"……\n\n\"예수께서 이르시되, 여자여, 나와 당신이 무슨 상관이 있나이까, 내 때가 아직 이르지 아니하였나이다.\n\n그 어머니가 하인들에게 이르되, 그가 너희에게 무슨 말을 하든지 그대로 하라 하니라\"……\n\n\"그대로 하라…… 즐거움, 어느 가난한, 무척이나 가난한 사람들의 즐거움이여…… 물론 그들은 가난했지, 혼인 잔치에서조차 포도주가 넉넉하지 못했으니…… 역사가들은 그 시절 게네사렛 호숫가에 살던 사람들이 도무지 상상할 수 없을 만큼 가난한 자들이었다고 쓰고 있어…… 그리고 또 하나의 위대한 마음이, 저 또 다른 위대한 존재인 그분의 어머니께서는, 그분이 단지 자신의 위대하고 두려운 희생을 치르려고 오신 것만은 아님을 알고 계셨어. 그분의 마음이 그분을 가난한 혼인 잔치에 따뜻이 청한 어느 이름 없고 무지한 사람들의, 그 소박하고 꾸밈없는 흥겨움에까지도 열려 있음을 그분의 어머니는 알고 계셨던 거야. '내 때가 아직 이르지 아니하였나이다.' 그분은 부드러운 미소와 함께 그렇게 말씀하셨지(그분은 어머니께 다정하게 미소 지으셨을 거야). 그리고 정말이지, 가난한 혼인 잔치에 포도주를 넉넉히 하시려고 그분이 이 땅에 내려오셨던가? 그런데도 그분은 가셔서 어머니가 청하신 대로 행하셨어…… 아, 또 읽고 있구나\"……\n\n\"예수께서 그들에게 이르시되, 항아리에 물을 채우라 하시니, 그들이 아귀까지 채우니라.\n\n이에 이르시되, 이제는 떠서 잔치를 맡은 자에게 갖다 주라 하시매, 그들이 갖다 주었더니.\n\n잔치를 맡은 자가 포도주가 된 물을 맛보고도 어디서 났는지 알지 못하되(물 떠 온 하인들은 알더라), 잔치를 맡은 자가 신랑을 불러,\n\n말하되, 사람마다 먼저 좋은 포도주를 내고 취한 후에 낮은 것을 내거늘, 그대는 지금까지 좋은 포도주를 두었도다 하니라.\"\n\n\"그런데 이게 뭐지, 이게 뭐야? 어째서 방이 점점 넓어지는 거지?…… 아, 그래…… 혼인이야, 혼인 잔치…… 그래, 물론이지. 여기 손님들이 있고, 여기 젊은 부부가 앉아 있고, 흥겨운 무리들이 있고, 그리고…… 잔치를 맡은 지혜로운 자는 어디 있지? 그런데 이분은 누구지? 누구지? 또 벽들이 물러나는구나…… 저기 큰 식탁에서 일어나는 이는 누구지? 뭐라고!…… 저분도 여기 계신단 말인가? 하지만 저분은 관 속에 계신데…… 그런데도 여기 계셔. 일어나셨어, 나를 보고 계셔, 이리로 오시는구나…… 하느님!\"……\n\n그렇다, 그분이 그에게로, 바로 그에게로 다가오셨으니, 얼굴에 자잘한 주름이 잡힌 그 작고 여윈 노인이, 기쁨에 젖어 부드럽게 웃으면서 다가오셨다. 이제 관은 없었고, 그분은 어제 사람들이 둘러 모였을 때 그들과 함께 앉아 있던 그 옷차림 그대로였다. 그분의 얼굴은 가려져 있지 않았고, 눈은 빛나고 있었다. 그렇다면 이것은 어찌 된 일인가? 그분도 잔치에 청함을 받았던 것이다. 그분도 갈릴리 가나의 혼인 잔치에……\n\n\"그렇다, 얘야, 나도 청함을 받았단다, 청함을 받고 부름을 받았어.\" 그는 자기 머리 위에서 들려오는 부드러운 목소리를 들었다. \"어찌하여 여기 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 숨어 있느냐? 너도 와서 우리와 함께하려무나.\"\n\n그것은 그분의 목소리, 조시마 장로의 목소리였다. 그분이 그를 불렀으니, 분명 그분이 틀림없었다!\n\n장로가 알료샤의 손을 잡아 일으키니 그는 무릎에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"우리는 기뻐하고 있단다.\" 그 작고 여윈 노인이 말을 이었다. \"우리는 새 포도주를, 새롭고 위대한 기쁨의 포도주를 마시고 있어. 손님이 얼마나 많은지 보이느냐? 여기 신부와 신랑이 있고, 여기 잔치를 맡은 지혜로운 자가 있어, 그가 새 포도주를 맛보고 있구나. 어찌하여 나를 보고 놀라느냐? 나는 거지에게 파 한 뿌리를 주었기에, 그래서 나도 여기 있는 거란다. 그리고 여기 있는 많은 이들이 저마다 파 한 뿌리씩만을—오직 하나의 자그마한 파 한 뿌리만을 주었을 뿐이야…… 우리의 행적이란 다 무엇이겠느냐? 그리고 너, 나의 온순한 아이야, 너, 나의 착한 아이야, 너도 오늘 굶주린 여인에게 파 한 뿌리를 줄 줄 알았구나. 네 일을 시작하여라, 사랑하는 아이야, 그것을 시작하여라, 온순한 아이야!…… 우리의 태양이 보이느냐, 그분이 보이느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 두렵습니다…… 감히 바라보지 못하겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 속삭였다.\n\n\"그분을 두려워하지 말아라. 그분은 그 위대하심으로 두렵고, 그 숭고하심으로 경외로우시나, 한없이 자비로우시단다. 그분은 사랑으로 우리와 같이 되셨고 우리와 함께 기뻐하신단다. 그분은 손님들의 즐거움이 끊기지 않도록 물을 포도주로 바꾸고 계셔. 그분은 새 손님들을 기다리시며, 새 손님들을 영원무궁토록 끊임없이 부르고 계셔…… 저기 그들이 새 포도주를 가져오는구나. 그들이 그릇들을 가져오는 것이 보이느냐……\"\n\n알료샤의 가슴 속에서 무언가가 타올랐고, 무언가가 그것을 아릴 만큼 가득 채웠으며, 환희의 눈물이 그의 영혼에서 솟구쳤다…… 그는 두 손을 뻗으며 외마디 소리를 지르고는 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n다시 관이, 열린 창문이, 그리고 부드럽고 엄숙하며 또렷한 복음서 낭독 소리가 거기 있었다. 그러나 알료샤는 그 낭독에 귀를 기울이지 않았다. 이상한 일이었으니, 그는 무릎을 꿇은 채 잠들었으나 이제는 일어서 있었고, 갑자기 앞으로 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 세 걸음의 굳건하고 빠른 발걸음으로 곧장 관으로 다가갔다. 그의 어깨가 파이시 신부를 스쳤으나 그는 알아차리지도 못했다. 파이시 신부는 책에서 잠시 눈을 들었으나, 이 소년에게 무언가 이상한 일이 벌어지고 있음을 보고는 곧 다시 눈길을 돌렸다. 알료샤는 반 분쯤 관을, 관 속에 누운 그 가려진, 미동도 없는 망자를 바라보았으니, 그 망자의 가슴에는 성상이 놓여 있고 머리에는 팔각 십자가가 새겨진 뾰족한 모자가 씌워져 있었다. 그는 방금 전까지 그분의 목소리를 듣고 있었고, 그 목소리는 여전히 그의 귓전에 울리고 있었다. 그는 또 다른 말씀을 기대하며 귀를 기울이고 있었으나, 갑자기 홱 돌아서서 암실 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n그는 층계에서도 멈추지 않고 빠르게 내려갔으니, 환희로 차고 넘치는 그의 영혼은 자유를, 공간을, 탁 트인 것을 갈망하고 있었다. 부드럽게 빛나는 별들로 가득한 하늘의 궁륭이 그의 머리 위로 광대하고도 가늠할 길 없이 펼쳐져 있었다. 은하수가 천정에서 지평선까지 두 줄기 창백한 흐름으로 흘러내렸다. 신선하고 미동도 없는 고요한 밤이 대지를 감싸고 있었다. 대성당의 흰 탑들과 황금빛 둥근 지붕들이 사파이어빛 하늘을 배경으로 반짝였다. 집 둘레의 화단에 핀 화사한 가을꽃들이 아침까지 잠들어 있었다. 대지의 침묵이 하늘의 침묵 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다. 대지의 신비가 별들의 신비와 하나였다……\n\n알료샤는 서서 바라보다가, 갑자기 땅 위로 몸을 던졌다. 그는 왜 그것을 끌어안는지 알지 못했다. 그는 어째서 그토록 억누를 수 없이 땅에 입 맞추고 싶은지, 온 땅에 입 맞추고 싶은지 말로 설명할 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 울면서, 흐느끼면서, 자신의 눈물로 그것을 적시며 입을 맞추었고, 그것을 사랑하리라고, 영원무궁토록 그것을 사랑하리라고 열렬히 맹세했다. \"네 기쁨의 눈물로 땅을 적시고 그 눈물을 사랑하여라.\" 하는 말이 그의 영혼에서 메아리쳤다.\n\n그는 무엇을 두고 울고 있었던가?\n\n오! 그는 환희에 젖어 저 별들을 두고도 울고 있었으니, 그 별들은 공간의 심연으로부터 그에게 빛을 비추고 있었고, \"그는 그 황홀경을 부끄러워하지 않았다.\" 하느님의 저 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세계들로부터 뻗어 나온 실들이 그의 영혼을 그 세계들과 잇고 있는 듯했으며, 그의 영혼은 \"다른 세계들과 닿아\" 온통 떨고 있었다. 그는 모든 이를, 모든 것을 용서하고 싶었고, 또 용서를 빌고 싶었다. 오, 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니라 모든 사람을 위해, 모두를 위하고 모든 것을 위해서였다. \"다른 이들도 나를 위해 기도하고 있어.\" 하는 말이 그의 영혼에서 또다시 메아리쳤다. 그러나 매 순간 그는, 저 하늘의 궁륭처럼 굳건하고 흔들림 없는 무언가가 자신의 영혼 속으로 들어왔음을 분명히, 말하자면 손에 잡힐 듯 느꼈다. 마치 어떤 사상이 그의 마음의 주권을 사로잡은 듯했으니—그것은 그의 평생을 위한 것이요 영원무궁토록 이어질 것이었다. 그는 연약한 소년으로 땅에 엎드렸으나, 결연한 투사로 일어섰으니, 그는 바로 그 황홀경의 순간에 그것을 문득 알고 또 느꼈다. 그리고 알료샤는 평생토록 결코, 결코 그 순간을 잊을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그 시각에 누군가 내 영혼을 찾아왔었지.\" 하고 그는 훗날 자기 말에 절대적인 믿음을 담아 말하곤 했다.\n\n사흘이 채 지나지 않아 그는 \"세상에 머물러 살라\"고 명하신 장로의 말씀에 따라 수도원을 떠났다.\n\n# Book VIII. Mitya Chapter I. Kuzma Samsonov\n\n그러나 새 삶을 향해 날아가며 그루셴카가 마지막 인사를 남기고, 자신을 사랑했던 그 시간을 영원히 기억해 달라 당부했던 그 상대 드미트리는, 그녀에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 아무것도 알지 못했고, 바로 그 순간 열병에 들뜬 듯한 흥분과 분주함 속에 빠져 있었다. 지난 이틀 동안 그는 도무지 상상할 수 없을 만큼 심란한 상태였으니, 뒷날 그 자신이 말했듯이, 하마터면 뇌염으로 드러눕고 말았을 정도였다. 알료샤는 그 전날 아침 그를 찾아내지 못했고, 이반 역시 같은 날 선술집에서 그를 만나는 데 성공하지 못했다. 그의 하숙집 사람들은 그의 지시에 따라 그의 행적을 숨겼다.\n\n그는 그 이틀을 글자 그대로 사방으로 내달리며 보냈으니, 뒷날 그 자신이 표현한 대로 \"운명과 씨름하며 자기 자신을 구하려 애쓰며\" 지냈고, 그루셴카에게서 한순간이라도 눈을 떼는 것이 그토록 끔찍한 일이었음에도, 몇 시간 동안은 화급한 용무로 읍내 밖까지 달려가기도 했다. 이 모든 것은 뒷날 상세히 밝혀졌고 문서 증거로도 확인되었으나, 지금으로서는 다만, 그토록 갑작스레 그를 덮친 끔찍한 파국 직전의 그 무시무시한 이틀 동안 벌어진 가장 본질적인 사건들만을 짚어 두기로 하겠다.\n\n그루셴카가 한 시간만큼은 진정으로, 또 진심으로 그를 사랑한 것은 사실이었으나, 그녀는 때로 잔인하고 무자비하게 그를 괴롭혔다. 무엇보다 견디기 힘든 것은, 그녀가 무엇을 하려는 것인지 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 점이었다. 힘으로든 다정함으로든 그녀를 꺾는 것 또한 불가능했으니, 그녀는 어떤 것에도 굴하지 않았다. 그저 화를 내고 그에게서 아주 등을 돌려 버릴 뿐이라는 것을, 그는 이미 잘 알고 있었다. 그는 아주 정확하게도, 그녀 역시 내면의 갈등을 겪고 있으며 유달리 우유부단한 상태에 빠져, 무언가를 마음먹으려 하면서도 끝내 결단을 내리지 못하고 있다고 짐작했다. 그리하여 그는, 까닭이 없지 않게, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 심정으로, 순간순간 그녀가 자기와 자기의 열정을 그저 미워할 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 알아차렸다. 어쩌면 정말 그러했을지도 모른다. 그러나 무엇이 그루셴카를 괴롭히고 있는지 그는 이해하지 못했다. 그에게 이 모든 괴로운 문제는 자신과 표도르 파블로비치 사이에 놓여 있었다.\n\n여기서 우리는, 지나가는 김에, 한 가지 분명한 사실을 짚어 두어야 한다. 그는 표도르 파블로비치가 그루셴카에게 정식 결혼을 제안하리라고, 어쩌면 이미 제안했으리라고 굳게 믿었으며, 그 늙은 호색한이 삼천 루블로 제 목적을 이룰 수 있으리라 기대하고 있다고는 한순간도 믿지 않았다. 미탸가 이런 결론에 이른 것은 그루셴카와 그녀의 성격을 알기 때문이었다. 그래서 그는, 그루셴카의 온갖 불안이 둘 가운데 누구를 택해야 할지, 누가 자기에게 가장 이로운지 알지 못하는 데서 비롯된 것이라고 때때로 믿을 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n이상하게도, 그 며칠 동안 그는 다가오는 \"장교\"의 귀환, 다시 말해 그루셴카의 삶에 그토록 치명적인 영향을 끼쳤고, 그녀가 그토록 동요와 두려움 속에서 그 도착을 기다리고 있던 그 사내에 관해서는 단 한 번도 생각이 미치지 않았다. 근래 들어 그루셴카가 그 일에 관해 매우 입을 다물어 온 것은 사실이었다. 그렇지만 그는 한 달 전 그녀가 자신을 유혹했던 그 사내로부터 편지 한 통을 받았다는 사실을 똑똑히 알고 있었고, 그것을 그녀의 입으로 직접 들었다. 그 편지에 무엇이 적혀 있었는지도 그는 어느 정도 알았다. 그루셴카가 홧김에 그 편지를 그에게 보여 준 적이 있었으나, 그녀가 놀랍게도 그는 그것을 거의 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 어째서 그러했는지는 말하기 어렵다. 어쩌면 이 여자를 두고 친아버지와 벌이는 흉측하고 끔찍한 싸움에 짓눌려, 그로서는 적어도 당분간은 그보다 더 무서운 위험이라곤 상상할 수조차 없었던 것이리라. 그는 오 년 동안이나 종적을 감추었다가 느닷없이 다시 나타난 구혼자라는 것을 도무지 믿지 않았고, 그가 곧 도착하리라는 것은 더더욱 믿지 않았다. 게다가 미탸에게 보여 준 그 \"장교\"의 첫 편지에서는 새 연적이 찾아올 가능성이 매우 모호하게만 암시되어 있었다. 그 편지는 더없이 막연하고, 거창하며, 감상으로 가득 차 있었다. 한 가지 짚어 둘 것은, 그루셴카가 그에게 편지의 마지막 줄들을 감추었다는 점인데, 그 부분에서는 그의 귀환이 한층 분명하게 언급되어 있었다. 그뿐 아니라 그는 그 순간—뒷날 기억해 낸 바로는—그루셴카의 얼굴에 시베리아에서 온 그 편지를 향한 어떤 무심결의 오만한 경멸이 떠올라 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 그루셴카는 그 후 자기와 이 연적 사이에 오간 일에 관해서는 그에게 아무것도 말하지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 차츰 그 장교의 존재 자체를 까맣게 잊어버리고 말았다.\n\n그는 뒷날 무슨 일이 닥치든, 일이 어떻게 굴러가든, 표도르 파블로비치와의 마지막 대결이 코앞에 닥쳐 있으며, 그것이 무엇보다 먼저 결판나야 한다고 느꼈다. 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 심정으로 그는 그루셴카의 결단을 시시각각 기다렸으니, 그것이 어느 순간의 충동에 따라 느닷없이 찾아오리라고 줄곧 믿었다. 별안간 그녀가 그에게 이렇게 말하리라. \"저를 데려가세요, 저는 영원히 당신 거예요.\" 그러면 모든 것이 끝나는 것이다. 그는 당장 그녀를 붙들어 땅끝까지라도 데리고 달아나리라. 오, 그때는 곧장 그녀를 데리고 멀리, 멀리, 될 수 있는 한 멀리 떠나리라. 땅끝까지는 아닐지라도 러시아의 가장 먼 변두리까지라도 가서, 그녀와 혼인하고 아무도 모르게 자리 잡으리니, 그리하여 그곳에서든 이곳에서든 그 어디에서든 아무도 그들에 관해 알지 못하리라. 그러면, 오, 그때는, 새 삶이 당장 시작되리라!\n\n이 다르고, 개과천선한, \"덕스러운\" 삶을(\"그것은 반드시, 반드시 덕스러워야 한다\") 그는 매 순간 열에 들뜬 듯 꿈꾸었다. 그는 그 개심과 갱생을 목말라했다. 제 발로 빠져든 그 더러운 수렁이 너무도 역겨웠고, 이런 처지에 놓인 숱한 사람들이 그러하듯, 그는 무엇보다 장소를 바꾸는 데 믿음을 걸었다. 이 사람들만 아니라면, 이 정황만 아니라면, 이 저주받은 곳에서 날아가 버릴 수만 있다면—그는 완전히 거듭나, 새로운 길로 들어서리라. 바로 그것이 그가 믿는 바였고, 그가 갈망하는 바였다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것은 오로지 그 첫 번째, 그 행복한 해결이라는 조건에서만 가능한 일이었다. 또 다른 가능성, 그와는 다른 끔찍한 결말이 있었다. 별안간 그녀가 그에게 이렇게 말할지도 몰랐다. \"가 버리세요. 저는 방금 표도르 파블로비치와 합의를 보았어요. 저는 그분과 결혼할 거고 당신은 필요 없어요.\" 그러면 그땐… 그러나 그땐…. 그러나 미탸는 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알지 못했다. 마지막 순간까지도 그는 알지 못했다. 이 점만은 그를 위해 말해 두어야 한다. 그에게는 뚜렷한 의도가 없었고, 어떤 범죄도 계획하지 않았다. 그는 그저 고통 속에서 지켜보고 염탐하며, 제 운명의 첫 번째, 행복한 해결을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었을 뿐이다. 사실 그는 그 밖의 다른 생각은 모조리 떨쳐 버렸다. 그러나 그 결말을 위해 전혀 다른 종류의 근심이 생겨났으니, 새롭고, 부수적이면서도, 그럼에도 치명적이고 풀 길 없는 난관이 그 앞에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n만일 그녀가 그에게 \"저는 당신 거예요, 저를 데려가세요\"라고 말한다면, 그가 어떻게 그녀를 데려간단 말인가? 그럴 방편이, 그럴 돈이 그에게 어디 있단 말인가? 그런데 바로 이때에, 그토록 오랜 세월 끊임없이 이어져 오던 표도르 파블로비치의 적선, 그 온갖 수입원이 죄다 끊겨 버렸다. 물론 그루셴카에게는 돈이 있었으나, 이에 관해서는 미탸가 별안간 유달리 큰 자존심을 드러냈으니, 그는 그녀를 데리고 가서 그녀의 돈이 아니라 제 손으로, 제 비용으로 그녀와 함께 새 삶을 시작하고 싶었다. 그녀의 돈을 받는다는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없었고, 그런 생각만으로도 격렬한 혐오의 통증이 그를 찔렀다. 나는 여기서 이 사실을 늘어놓거나 분석하지는 않겠고, 다만 그 순간 그의 태도가 이러했다는 것만 짚어 두겠다. 이 모든 것은 어쩌면 그가 부정하게 가로챈 카테리나 이바노브나의 돈을 두고 양심이 은밀히 찔러 대는 데서 간접적으로, 또 무의식적으로 비롯된 것이었으리라. \"나는 그 여자 하나에게 비열한 놈이었는데, 곧 또 다른 여자에게도 비열한 놈이 되겠구나.\" 뒷날 그가 설명한 대로, 그때 그의 심정은 이러했다. \"그리고 그루셴카가 그걸 알면, 그런 비열한 놈 따위는 거들떠보지도 않을 거야.\"\n\n그렇다면 그는 그 방편을 어디서 구한단 말인가, 그 운명의 돈을 어디서 구한단 말인가? 그것이 없으면 모든 것이 허사가 되고 아무것도 이룰 수 없으리라. \"그것도 다 내게 돈이 없다는 이유 하나 때문이라니. 오, 이 수치스러움이라니!\"\n\n미리 말해 두자면, 그는 어쩌면 그 돈을 어디서 구해야 할지 알고 있었을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그 순간 그것이 어디에 놓여 있는지도 알고 있었을지 모른다. 이에 관해서는 모든 것이 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 터이므로 여기서는 더 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 그의 가장 큰 고민은—아무리 어렴풋하게라도 설명해 두어야겠는데—그가 아는 그 금액을 손에 넣으려면, 그것을 취할 권리를 가지려면, 무엇보다 먼저 카테리나 이바노브나의 삼천 루블부터 갚아야 한다는 사실에 있었다. 그러지 않으면, \"나는 한낱 소매치기요, 비열한 놈이니, 비열한 놈인 채로 새 삶을 시작하고 싶지는 않다\"라고 미탸는 작정했다. 그리하여 그는 무슨 수를 써서라도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그 삼천 루블을 돌려주기로, 그것도 무엇보다 먼저 그러기로 마음을 굳혔다. 말하자면 이 결심의 마지막 단계에 그가 이른 것은 불과 지난 몇 시간 동안의 일이었으니, 곧 이틀 전 큰길에서 알료샤와 마지막으로 만난 뒤, 그루셴카가 카테리나 이바노브나를 모욕한 그날 저녁, 미탸가 알료샤에게서 그 이야기를 듣고 자신이 비열한 놈이라고 인정하며, 그것이 그녀에게 조금이라도 위로가 된다면 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그렇게 전하라고 일렀던 그 일이 있은 뒤였다. 그날 밤 동생과 헤어진 뒤, 그는 광기에 사로잡힌 채 \"카탸에게 진 빚을 갚지 못할 바에야 차라리 누군가를 죽이고 털어 버리는 편이 낫다\"고 느꼈다. \"남들이 모두 나를 강도요 살인자로 여기는 편이 낫겠다, 카탸가 내가 자기를 속이고 그 돈을 훔쳤으며 그 돈으로 그루셴카와 달아나 새 삶을 시작했다고 말할 권리를 갖느니 차라리 시베리아로 가는 편이 낫겠다! 그것만은 할 수 없다!\" 미탸는 이를 갈며 이렇게 작정했고, 때때로 정말이지 머리가 돌아 버릴 것만 같았다. 그러나 그러면서도 그는 계속 발버둥쳤다….\n\n이상하게도, 그에게 남은 것이라곤 절망밖에 없으리라 여겨질 만했는데—세상에 아무것도 가진 것 없는 그가 그만한 큰돈을 마련할 가망이 무엇이 있었겠는가?—그럼에도 그는 끝까지, 그 삼천 루블을 손에 넣게 되리라고, 그 돈이 마치 하늘에서 떨어지기라도 하듯 어떻게든 제 발로 굴러들어 오리라고 끈질기게 믿었다. 드미트리처럼, 돈이란 것을 제 노력 하나 들이지 않고 상속받은 것을 흥청망청 써 버리는 일 말고는 도무지 다뤄 본 적이 없고, 돈이 어떻게 마련되는지도 전혀 모르는 사람들이 바로 그러한 법이다. 이틀 전 알료샤와 헤어진 직후부터 더없이 황당무계한 망상들이 회오리치며 그의 머릿속을 사로잡아, 그의 생각을 온통 뒤엉킨 혼란에 빠뜨렸다. 그래서 그는 맨 먼저 완전히 터무니없는 계획에 매달리게 된 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 그런 처지에 놓인 그런 부류의 사람들에게는, 가장 불가능하고 환상적인 계획이 맨 먼저 떠올라 가장 현실적으로 보이는 법인지도 모른다.\n\n그는 별안간 그루셴카의 후견인인 상인 삼소노프를 찾아가 그에게 \"사업안\"을 제안하고, 그것을 통해 당장 그에게서 필요한 돈 전부를 얻어 내기로 작정했다. 자기 사업안의 상업적 가치에 관해서는 그는 추호도, 털끝만큼도 의심하지 않았고, 다만 삼소노프가 이를 순전히 상업적인 관점이 아닌 다른 시각에서 본다면 자기의 이 엉뚱한 발상을 어떻게 받아들일지가 미덥지 않을 따름이었다. 미탸는 그 상인을 얼굴로는 알았으나, 인사를 나눈 사이도 아니었고 단 한마디도 말을 섞어 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 무슨 까닭에선지 그는 오래전부터, 죽음의 문턱에 누운 그 늙은 난봉꾼이라면 이제 그루셴카가 번듯한 처지를 얻어 \"믿을 만한\" 사내와 혼인하는 것에 전혀 반대하지 않으리라는 확신을 품어 왔다. 그리고 그는 그가 반대하지 않을 뿐 아니라 바로 그것을 바라고 있으며, 기회만 닿는다면 기꺼이 거들어 주리라고까지 믿었다. 어떤 소문에서, 아니면 어쩌면 그루셴카가 무심코 흘린 한마디에서, 그는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 노인이 그루셴카의 짝으로 표도르 파블로비치보다 자기를 더 마음에 들어 할지도 모른다는 것까지 짐작해 냈다.\n\n내 소설을 읽는 독자들 가운데 많은 이들은, 그런 도움을 기대하고 말하자면 신부를 그 후견인의 손에서 넘겨받을 채비를 했다는 점에서, 드미트리가 대단한 조야함과 섬세함의 결여를 드러냈다고 느낄 것이다. 나는 다만, 미탸가 그루셴카의 과거를 완전히 끝나 버린 무엇으로 여겼다는 것만 짚어 두겠다. 그는 그 과거를 끝없는 연민으로 바라보았고, 일단 그루셴카가 자기를 사랑하며 자기와 혼인하겠다고 말하기만 하면, 그것은 곧 온갖 악덕에서 벗어난 새로운 그루셴카와 새로운 드미트리의 시작을 뜻하리라고 제 열정의 온 열기를 다해 굳게 마음먹었다. 두 사람은 서로를 용서하고 삶을 처음부터 새로 시작하리라. 쿠지마 삼소노프로 말하자면, 드미트리는 그를, 비록 그루셴카가 한 번도 사랑한 적은 없으나 그녀의 그 머나먼 과거에 운명적인 영향을 끼쳤던 사내이자, 이제는 그 자신이 과거의 일이 되어 완전히 끝나 버린, 말하자면 존재하지 않는 사람으로 여겼다. 게다가 미탸는 그를 사람으로조차 거의 여기지 않았으니, 그가 한낱 부서져 버린 폐물에 지나지 않으며, 그루셴카와의 관계도 그 성격이 바뀌어 이제는 단지 부녀간의 정과 같은 것이 된 지 오래라는 사실을 읍내 사람이라면 누구나 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n어쨌든 이 모든 일에는 미탸 쪽의 순박함이 적잖이 깃들어 있었으니, 그는 온갖 악덕에도 불구하고 매우 순박한 마음을 지닌 사내였기 때문이다. 미탸가, 저세상으로 떠날 날을 앞둔 늙은 쿠지마라면 그루셴카와의 지난 관계를 진심으로 뉘우치고 있으리라고, 그리고 이제 무해해진 이 노인보다 더 헌신적인 벗이자 보호자를 그녀가 세상에 둘 수 없으리라고 진지하게 믿었다는 것이야말로 그 순박함의 한 본보기였다.\n\n네거리에서 알료샤와 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 밤새 거의 잠을 이루지 못했고, 이튿날 아침 열 시에 삼소노프의 집에 이르러 하인에게 자기가 왔음을 알리라고 일렀다. 그것은 별채와 헛간들이 딸린, 매우 크고 음울한 이 층짜리 낡은 저택이었다. 아래층에는 삼소노프의 결혼한 두 아들이 그 가족과 함께, 그리고 그의 늙은 누이와 시집 안 간 딸이 살았다. 별채에는 그의 점원 둘이 살았는데, 그중 하나도 대가족을 거느리고 있었다. 별채도 아래층도 사람들로 빽빽했으나, 노인은 위층을 제 혼자 차지하고는, 딸이 시중을 들고 천식이 있는 몸으로 정해진 시각마다, 또 부르기만 하면 아무 때나 아래층에서 그에게로 부랴부랴 뛰어 올라가야 했음에도, 딸조차 거기서 함께 살지 못하게 했다.\n\n이 위층에는 순전히 보여 주기 위한 큰 방들이 여럿 있었는데, 옛 상인풍으로 꾸며져 벽을 따라 투박한 마호가니 의자들이 길고 단조롭게 줄지어 늘어서 있었고, 갓을 씌운 유리 샹들리에들과 벽에 걸린 음울한 거울들이 있었다. 이 방들은 죄다 텅 비어 쓰이지 않았으니, 노인은 한 방에만, 외딴 작은 침실에만 틀어박혀 머리에 수건을 두른 늙은 하녀와 복도의 궤짝에 걸터앉아 있곤 하는 사내아이의 시중을 받았다. 다리가 퉁퉁 부어 노인은 도무지 걷지 못했고, 늙은 하녀가 부축해 방 안을 한두 번 오르내리게 할 때나 어쩌다 한 번씩 가죽 안락의자에서 일으켜질 따름이었다. 그는 이 늙은 하녀에게마저 시무룩하고 말이 없었다.\n\n\"대위\"가 왔다는 전갈을 받자 그는 대뜸 만나기를 거절했다. 그러나 미탸는 물러서지 않고 다시 제 이름을 올려 보냈다. 삼소노프는 사내아이에게 꼬치꼬치 캐물었다. 그 사람 행색이 어떠냐? 술에 취했더냐? 소란을 피울 것 같더냐? 돌아온 대답은, 그가 멀쩡한데 도무지 가려 들지 않는다는 것이었다. 노인은 또다시 만나기를 거절했다. 그러자 이를 미리 내다보고 일부러 연필과 종이를 가지고 온 미탸는, 그 종이에 또박또박 이렇게 적었다. \"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에 관한 더없이 중대한 용무로.\" 그러고는 그것을 노인에게 올려 보냈다.\n\n잠시 생각한 끝에 삼소노프는 사내아이에게 손님을 응접실로 안내하라 이르고, 늙은 하녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 작은아들에게 당장 위층으로 올라오라는 전갈을 전하게 했다. 이 작은아들은 키가 육 척이 넘고 남달리 힘이 센 사내로, 아비는 아직도 카프탄을 입고 수염을 기르고 있었으나 그는 말끔히 면도하고 유럽식으로 차려입은 사람이었는데, 군말 없이 곧장 올라왔다. 온 식구가 아비 앞에서 벌벌 떨었다. 노인이 이 거구를 부른 것은 \"대위\"가 두려워서가 아니라(그는 결코 겁 많은 성미가 아니었다), 무슨 일이 벌어질 경우에 대비해 증인을 두기 위함이었다. 아들과 하인 아이의 부축을 받아 그는 마침내 뒤뚱뒤뚱 응접실로 들어섰다. 짐작건대 그는 적잖은 호기심을 느꼈으리라. 미탸가 그를 기다리고 있던 응접실은 가슴에 짓누르는 듯한 우울을 안기는, 휑하고 음산한 방이었다. 거기엔 이중으로 늘어선 창들과 회랑, 대리석을 바른 벽, 그리고 갓을 씌운 유리 장식이 매달린 거대한 샹들리에 셋이 있었다.\n\n미탸는 입구의 작은 의자에 앉아, 초조한 조바심 속에서 제 운명을 기다리고 있었다. 일흔 자나 떨어진 맞은편 문에 노인이 모습을 드러내자, 미탸는 대뜸 벌떡 일어나 길고 군인다운 걸음걸이로 그를 맞으러 걸어갔다. 미탸는 단정히 차려입고 있었으니, 단추를 채운 프록코트 차림에 둥근 모자와 검은 장갑을 손에 든 모양새가, 사흘 전 장로의 처소에서 아버지며 형제들과 함께한 가족 모임 때 그대로였다. 노인은 위엄 있게, 꼿꼿이 서서 그를 기다렸고, 미탸는 자기가 다가가는 동안 그가 자신을 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보았음을 단번에 느꼈다. 미탸는 또한 삼소노프의 엄청나게 부어오른 얼굴에 크게 압도되었다. 늘 두툼하던 아랫입술이 이제는 빵 덩어리처럼 축 늘어져 있었다. 노인은 위엄 있게 잠자코 손님에게 고개를 숙여 보이고는, 소파 옆의 낮은 의자를 손짓으로 가리킨 뒤, 아들의 팔에 기대어 맞은편 소파에 몸을 낮추기 시작하며 괴롭게 신음했으니, 그 고통스러운 안간힘을 본 미탸는 그 즉시 가책을 느끼며, 감히 폐를 끼친 이 위엄 있는 인물 앞에서 제 하찮음을 예민하게 의식했다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 나를 찾았소, 선생?\" 마침내 자리에 앉자 노인이 천천히, 또박또박, 엄하게, 그러나 정중하게 말했다.\n\n미탸는 흠칫하며 벌떡 일어섰다가 다시 앉았다. 그러더니 곧장 큰 소리로, 신경질적으로 다급하게, 손짓을 섞어 가며, 그야말로 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 말하기 시작했다. 그는 누가 봐도 궁지에 몰려, 파멸의 벼랑 끝에서 마지막 지푸라기를 붙드는, 실패하면 그대로 가라앉을 채비가 된 사내였다. 늙은 삼소노프는 얼굴은 조각상처럼 차갑고 미동도 없었으나, 십중팔구 이 모든 것을 순식간에 알아챘으리라.\n\n\"더없이 존경하옵는 선생님, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 선생님께서도 제가 아버지 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프와 다투고 있다는 것을 분명 한두 번은 들으셨을 줄 압니다. 그자가 어머니에게서 물려받은 제 유산을 가로챘는데… 온 읍내가 그 일을 입에 올리고 있으니 말입니다… 여기서는 다들 입에 담아서는 안 될 것들을 떠벌리니까요… 게다가 그루셴카를 통해 그 이야기가 선생님 귀에 들어갔을 수도 있겠지요… 죄송합니다, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 통해서 말입니다… 제가 더없이 깊이 존경하고 우러르는 분, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 통해서요…\"\n\n이렇게 미탸는 입을 떼었으나 첫 문장에서부터 말이 막히고 말았다. 우리는 그의 말을 한 마디 한 마디 옮기지는 않고, 다만 그 요지만을 간추리기로 하겠다. 그는 석 달 전에 모종의 작정을 품고(미탸는 \"의도적으로\"라는 말 대신 일부러 이런 표현을 썼다) 도의 중심 도시에서 한 변호사와 상의했다고 했다. \"쿠지마 쿠지미치, 명망 높은 변호사인데, 파벨 파블로비치 코르네플로도프라는 사람입니다. 혹시 들어 보셨는지요? 어마어마한 지력에, 정치가의 두뇌를 지닌 사람으로… 그가 선생님도 알더군요… 선생님을 더없이 칭송하던데요…\" 미탸는 또다시 말이 막혔다. 그러나 이런 끊김들이 그를 멈추게 하지는 못했다. 그는 그 틈새들을 단숨에 뛰어넘어 끊임없이 발버둥치며 말을 이어 나갔다.\n\n이 코르네플로도프라는 자는 그에게 꼬치꼬치 캐묻고 그가 가져갈 수 있었던 서류들을 살펴본 끝에(미탸는 이 서류들에 관해서는 다소 모호하게 둘러대며 유달리 서둘러 그 대목을 얼버무렸다), 어머니에게서 자기, 곧 미탸에게로 마땅히 넘어왔어야 할 체르마시냐 마을에 관해 소송을 걸어 그 늙은 악당인 아비에게 한 방 먹일 수 있다고 알려 주었다. \"왜냐하면 모든 문이 닫힌 것은 아니어서 정의가 아직 빠져나갈 구멍을 찾을 수 있기 때문\"이라는 것이었다. 사실 그는 표도르 파블로비치에게서 육천, 아니 칠천 루블까지도 더 받아 낼 셈을 칠 수 있었으니, 체르마시냐가 적어도 이만오천 루블, 아니 이만팔천 루블, 사실은 \"삼만, 삼만은 나가는데, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 그런데 믿어지십니까, 저는 그 매정한 자한테서 만 칠천도 못 받았답니다!\" 그래서 그, 미탸는 법에 관해 아는 것이 없는 터라 그 일을 당분간 접어 두었는데, 막상 이리로 와 보니 자기에게 걸린 맞소송에 그만 말문이 막혀 버렸다는 것이었다(여기서 미탸는 또다시 갈피를 잃고 다시 한 번 앞으로 훌쩍 건너뛰었다). \"그러니 훌륭하고 존경하옵는 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 그 천륜을 저버린 괴물을 상대로 한 제 모든 청구권을 떠맡으시고, 제게 단돈 삼천만 한꺼번에 내주시지 않겠습니까?… 보십시오, 선생님은 어떤 경우에도 손해 보실 일이 없습니다. 제 명예를 걸고, 제 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다. 외려 그 반대로, 삼천 대신 육천이나 칠천을 버시게 될 겁니다.\" 무엇보다 그는 이 일을 바로 그날 안에 매듭짓고 싶어 했다.\n\n\"공증인 앞에서든 뭐든 선생님과 일을 마무리짓겠습니다… 사실 저는 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 되어 있습니다…. 모든 증서를 넘겨드리고… 무엇이든 원하시는 대로, 뭐든 서명하고… 계약서도 당장 작성할 수 있습니다… 그리고 가능하다면, 정말 가능하기만 하다면, 바로 오늘 아침에라도…. 선생님께서 제게 그 삼천을 내주실 수 있으십니다. 이 읍내에 선생님께 견줄 자본가는 없으니까요. 그러면 저를 구해 주시는 겁니다… 사실 저를 구해 주시는 거지요… 선하고, 명예롭다 할 만한 일을 위해서 말입니다…. 왜냐하면 저는 선생님께서도 잘 아시고 아버지처럼 아끼시는 어떤 분을 향해 더없이 명예로운 감정을 품고 있기 때문입니다. 사실 아버지 같은 분이 아니었다면 저는 찾아오지도 않았을 겁니다. 게다가 이건 셋이 얽힌 싸움이거든요, 운명이란 말입니다—그게 무서운 거예요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치! 비극입니다, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 비극이라고요! 그런데 선생님은 오래전에 발을 빼셨으니, 이건 둘 사이의 줄다리기지요. 어쩌면 제가 서툴게 말하고 있는지도 모르겠습니다만, 저는 문필가가 아니라서요. 보십시오, 한쪽엔 제가 있고, 다른 한쪽엔 그 괴물이 있습니다. 그러니 선생님께서 택하셔야 합니다. 저냐, 그 괴물이냐예요. 이 모든 게 선생님 손에 달렸습니다—세 목숨의 운명과 두 사람의 행복이…. 죄송합니다, 제가 횡설수설하고 있습니다만, 선생님은 아실 겁니다… 선생님의 존엄한 눈빛을 보니 아시는 게 분명합니다… 그런데 만일 모르신다면, 저는 끝장입니다… 자, 그러니까요!\"\n\n미탸는 이 \"자, 그러니까요!\"라는 말로 그 어설픈 연설을 뚝 그치고는, 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 자신의 어리석은 제안에 대한 답을 기다렸다. 마지막 구절에 이르러 그는 별안간, 모든 것이 빗나가 버렸다는 것을, 무엇보다도 자기가 순전히 헛소리를 지껄여 댔다는 것을 절망적으로 깨달았다.\n\n\"참 이상도 하구나! 여기 오는 길엔 멀쩡해 보였는데, 막상 와 보니 죄다 헛소리뿐이라니.\" 그 절망에 빠진 머릿속에 별안간 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 그가 말하는 내내 노인은 미동도 없이 앉아, 얼음장 같은 표정을 눈에 담은 채 그를 지켜보았다. 잠시 그를 애태우게 내버려 둔 끝에, 쿠지마 쿠지미치는 마침내 더없이 단호하고 싸늘한 어조로 이렇게 못 박았다.\n\n\"미안하오만, 우리는 그런 일은 맡지 않소.\"\n\n미탸는 별안간 다리에 힘이 풀리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"이제 저는 어쩌면 좋습니까, 쿠지마 쿠지미치?\" 그가 창백한 미소를 띠며 중얼거렸다. \"이제 다 끝장난 것 같은데—어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"미안하오만….\"\n\n미탸는 선 채로, 꼼짝 않고 멍하니 쳐다보았다. 문득 그는 노인의 얼굴에 무언가 변화가 이는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 흠칫했다.\n\n\"보시오, 선생, 그런 종류의 일은 우리 분야가 아니오.\" 노인이 느릿느릿 말했다. \"법정이며 변호사들이 있긴 하지만—그건 그야말로 고역이오. 하지만 원한다면, 여기 찾아가 볼 만한 사람이 하나 있긴 하오.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 누구입니까? 선생님이 저를 구원하시는군요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치.\" 미탸가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그는 여기 살지 않고, 지금 여기 있지도 않소. 농민인데, 목재 일을 하는 자요. 이름은 랴가비라고 하오. 지난 한 해 동안 표도르 파블로비치와 체르마시냐에 있는 당신네 숲을 두고 흥정을 벌이고 있소. 값이 안 맞아 합의를 못 보는 모양인데, 혹시 들어 봤소? 지금 그가 다시 돌아와서 일린스코예의 사제 집에 묵고 있소, 볼로뱌 역에서 십이 베르스타쯤 되는 곳이오. 그 숲 일로 내게도 편지를 보내 조언을 청했소. 표도르 파블로비치가 직접 그를 찾아가 만날 셈이라고 하더이다. 그러니 만일 당신이 표도르 파블로비치보다 한발 앞서, 내게 한 그 제안을 랴가비에게 한다면, 어쩌면 그가—\"\n\n\"기막힌 생각이군요!\" 미탸가 황홀해하며 말을 가로챘다. \"그 사람이야말로 딱 맞는 사람입니다, 그에겐 그게 안성맞춤이지요. 값을 너무 부른다고 흥정을 벌이고 있는데, 여기 그 재산 자체에 대한 권리를 입증하는 서류가 죄다 그의 손에 들어오는 거니까요. 하하하!\"\n\n그러고는 미탸가 별안간 짧고 메마른 웃음을 터뜨려 삼소노프를 흠칫하게 했다.\n\n\"이 은혜를 어떻게 갚아야 할지요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치?\" 미탸가 넘치는 감정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"별말씀을.\" 삼소노프가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 선생님은 모르십니다, 선생님이 저를 구하신 겁니다. 오, 저를 선생님께 데려온 건 정말이지 참된 예감이었습니다…. 그럼 이제 그 사제에게로!\"\n\n\"고마워할 것 없소.\"\n\n\"서둘러 그리로 날아가겠습니다. 선생님 기력을 너무 축내 드린 건 아닌지 모르겠습니다. 결코 잊지 않겠습니다. 이렇게 말하는 건 러시아 사람이랍니다, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 러-러-러시아 사람이요!\"\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고!\"\n\n미탸는 노인의 손을 잡아 쥐려 했으나, 그 눈에 악의에 찬 번득임이 어렸다. 미탸는 손을 도로 거두었지만, 이내 제 의심 많음을 스스로 나무랐다.\n\n\"노인장이 지치셨기 때문이지.\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그분을 위해서입니다! 그분을 위해서예요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치! 이게 그분을 위한 일이라는 걸 아시겠지요.\" 그가 외치자, 그 목소리가 방 안에 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸다. 그는 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 홱 돌아서서, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 똑같은 그 긴 걸음걸이로 문을 향해 걸어갔다. 그는 환희에 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"모든 것이 파멸의 벼랑에 놓였었는데 내 수호천사가 나를 구해 주셨구나.\" 그의 머릿속에 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. 그리고 삼소노프 같은 사업가(더없이 훌륭한 노인장, 게다가 그 위엄이라니!)가 이런 방도를 일러 주었다면, 그렇다면… 그렇다면 성공은 보장된 것이나 다름없었다. 그는 당장 날아가리라. \"밤이 되기 전에 돌아오마, 밤에는 돌아올 거고, 그러면 일은 다 된 거다. 노인장이 나를 놀리고 있었던 건 아닐까?\" 미탸는 제 하숙을 향해 성큼성큼 걸으며 소리쳤다. 그로서는 물론, 그 충고가 \"그런 사업가에게서\" 나온, 그 일의 내막을 훤히 아는, 그 랴가비라는 자(참 묘한 성씨로군!)를 잘 아는 사람의 실속 있는 조언이라는 것 말고는 달리 아무것도 떠올릴 수 없었다. 아니면—노인장이 자기를 놀리고 있었거나.\n\n아아! 두 번째 쪽이 맞는 것이었다. 한참 뒤, 파국이 벌어지고 난 다음, 늙은 삼소노프 자신이 껄껄 웃으며, 자기가 그 \"대위\"를 바보로 만들었노라 털어놓았다. 그는 차갑고 심술궂으며 빈정대기 좋아하는 사람으로, 격렬한 반감에 곧잘 사로잡히는 위인이었다. 그 \"대위\"의 흥분한 얼굴이었는지, 아니면 그 \"난봉꾼이자 탕아\"가 자기, 곧 삼소노프 같은 사람이 그따위 황당무계한 계획에 넘어가리라 어리석게 굳게 믿은 탓이었는지, 아니면 이 \"망나니\"가 돈을 우려내겠다고 그런 이야기를 들고 제게 뛰어들면서 들먹인 그루셴카에 대한 질투 탓이었는지, 그것이 그 노인의 마음을 건드린 것인지는 나로서도 알 수 없다. 그러나 미탸가 그 앞에 서서 다리에 힘이 풀리는 것을 느끼며 자기는 끝장났다고 미친 듯이 외쳐 대던 바로 그 순간, 노인은 격렬한 악의를 품고 그를 바라보며 그를 웃음거리로 만들기로 작정했던 것이다. 미탸가 떠나고 나자, 쿠지마 쿠지미치는 분노로 새파랗게 질려 아들을 돌아보고는, 그 거지가 다시는 얼씬도 못 하게, 마당에조차 들이지 말도록 단단히 단속하라, 그러지 않으면 가만두지—\n\n그는 그 위협을 끝내 입에 올리지 않았다. 그러나 아비가 격노하는 모습을 자주 보아 온 아들조차 두려움에 떨었다. 그 후로도 노인은 꼬박 한 시간 동안 분노로 부들부들 떨었고, 저녁이 되자 더 나빠져 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다.\n\n# Chapter II. Lyagavy\n\n그리하여 그는 전속력으로 달려가야만 했으나, 말을 빌릴 돈이 없었다. 그에게 남은 것은 사십 코페이카가 전부였으니, 그토록 오랜 세월 누려 온 풍족함 끝에 남은 것이라곤 고작 그것뿐이었다! 그러나 그에게는 집에 오래전부터 멈춰 버린 낡은 은시계가 하나 있었다. 그는 그것을 낚아채어 시장 광장에 가게를 차린 유대인 시계공에게 들고 갔다. 유대인은 그것을 받고 육 루블을 내주었다.\n\n\"그건 나도 기대 못 했던 거야.\" 미탸가 황홀에 겨워 소리쳤다. (그는 여전히 황홀경에 빠져 있는 상태였다.) 그는 육 루블을 거머쥐고 집으로 달려갔다. 집에서는 셋집 사람들에게서 삼 루블을 빌렸는데, 그들은 그를 어찌나 좋아했던지 가진 돈 전부였음에도 기꺼이 내주었다. 미탸는 흥분한 나머지 그 자리에서, 오늘 자기 운명이 결판날 것이라고 그들에게 일러 주었고, 다급하기 짝이 없는 어조로 삼소노프에게 내놓았던 계획 전부와 그 노인의 결정, 자신이 품은 앞날의 희망 따위를 죄다 늘어놓았다. 이 사람들은 전부터 셋방살이하는 그의 비밀을 적잖이 들어 온 터라, 그를 조금도 거만하지 않은, 거의 자기네 식구나 다름없는 신사로 여기고 있었다. 그렇게 해서 구 루블을 그러모은 미탸는 볼로뱌 역까지 데려다줄 역마를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 바로 이런 연유로 \"사건이 벌어지기 전날 한낮에 미탸는 한 푼도 없었으며, 돈을 마련하려고 시계를 팔았고 집주인에게서 삼 루블을 빌렸는데, 이 모든 일이 증인들이 보는 앞에서 벌어졌다\"는 사실이 사람들 기억에 남고 또 확증되기에 이르렀다.\n\n내가 이 사실을 적어 두는 까닭은, 뒤에 가서 분명해질 것이다.\n\n마침내 자신의 모든 난관을 풀어 줄 것이라는 기쁜 기대로 그는 환하게 빛나고 있었으나, 막상 볼로뱌 역에 가까워지자 자기가 없는 동안 그루센카가 무슨 짓을 하고 있을지 생각하니 몸이 떨려 왔다. 만일 그녀가 오늘 표도르 파블로비치에게로 가기로 마음을 굳힌다면 어쩐단 말인가? 바로 이 때문에 그는 그녀에게 알리지도 않고 떠나왔으며, 누구든 자기를 찾아 묻거든 어디로 갔는지 발설하지 말라고 하숙집 안주인에게 일러두었던 것이다.\n\n\"오늘 밤에는, 오늘 밤에는 반드시 돌아가야 해.\" 그는 마차에 실려 덜컹덜컹 흔들리며 거듭 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 아마도 이 라가비를 여기로 데려와야 할 테지… 증서를 작성하자면 말이야.\" 미탸는 가슴을 두근거리며 이렇게 골똘히 생각했으나, 아아! 그의 꿈은 이루어질 운명이 못 되었다.\n\n우선 그는 늦고 말았으니, 볼로뱌 역에서 지름길로 들어섰던 것이 십이 베르스타가 아니라 십팔 베르스타로 드러났던 것이다. 둘째로 그는 일리인스코예에서 사제를 집에서 만나지 못했으니, 사제는 이웃 마을로 떠나 버린 뒤였다. 미탸가 그 지친 말들을 이끌고 그리로 길을 잡아 사제를 찾아 헤매는 동안 날은 거의 어두워져 있었다.\n\n수줍고 상냥해 보이는 자그마한 사내인 사제는, 라가비가 처음에는 자기 집에 묵었으나 지금은 수호이 포숄로크에 가 있으며, 그곳에서도 목재를 사들이느라 산지기 오두막에서 하룻밤을 묵고 있다고 곧바로 일러 주었다. 자기를 당장 라가비에게 데려다주어 \"말하자면 자기를 구해 달라\"는 미탸의 절박한 청에, 사제는 얼마간 망설인 끝에 수호이 포숄로크까지 안내하기로 응낙했으니, 그의 호기심이 동했음이 분명했다. 그러나 공교롭게도 사제는 걸어가자고 권했는데, 거리가 한 베르스타에서 \"그리 멀지 않다\"는 것이었다. 미탸는 물론 동의하고 한 발에 한 마장씩 성큼성큼 내디뎠으니, 가엾은 사제는 그를 뒤쫓아 거의 뛰다시피 했다. 그는 늙지는 않았으나 무척 조심스러운 사람이었다.\n\n미탸는 그에게도 곧장 자기 계획을 이야기하기 시작했고, 라가비에 관해 안절부절못하며 흥분한 어조로 조언을 구하면서 가는 내내 떠들어 댔다. 사제는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였으나 조언이라곤 별로 해 주지 못했다. 그는 미탸의 물음을 \"글쎄요, 잘 모르겠습니다. 뭐라 말할 수가 없군요. 제가 어찌 알겠습니까?\" 하는 식으로 받아넘기곤 했다. 미탸가 유산 문제로 아버지와 벌인 다툼을 이야기하기 시작하자, 사제는 어떤 식으로든 표도르 파블로비치에게 신세를 지고 있던 터라 대놓고 경계하는 빛을 보였다. 그래도 그는 의아하다는 듯, 어째서 고르스트킨이라는 농부 장사꾼을 라가비라고 부르느냐고 물었고, 친절하게도 미탸에게 설명하기를, 그 사내의 이름이 실제로 라가비이긴 하나 누구도 그렇게 부르지 않으니, 그 이름을 들으면 몹시 기분이 상하기 때문이며, 반드시 고르스트킨이라고 불러야 한다고 했다. \"안 그러면 그자와는 아무 일도 못 하실 겁니다. 당신 말은 들으려고도 안 할 테니까요.\" 사제가 마무리 삼아 이렇게 말했다.\n\n미탸는 잠깐 다소 어리둥절했다가, 삼소노프가 그렇게 불렀던 것이라고 설명했다. 이 사실을 듣고 사제는 그 화제를 더는 입에 올리지 않았으나, 만일 삼소노프가 그 농부에게 그를 보내면서 라가비라고 불렀다면 거기에 뭔가 석연찮은 데가 있는 것이며 그를 조롱거리로 삼은 것은 아닌지, 그런 의심을 말로 꺼냈더라면 좋았을 것이다. 그러나 미탸로서는 그런 사소한 일에 잠시 머무를 겨를이 없었다. 그는 성큼성큼 걸음을 재촉했고, 수호이 포숄로크에 다다라서야 비로소 자기들이 한 베르스타도, 한 베르스타 반도 아니고 적어도 삼 베르스타는 걸어왔음을 깨달았다. 이 일이 짜증스러웠으나 그는 꾹 참았다.\n\n그들은 오두막 안으로 들어섰다. 산지기는 오두막 한쪽에 살고 있었고, 고르스트킨은 다른 쪽, 통로 건너편의 더 나은 방에 묵고 있었다. 그들은 그 방으로 들어가 수지로 만든 양초에 불을 붙였다. 오두막 안은 지나치게 후끈했다. 탁자 위에는 불이 꺼진 사모바르 하나와 찻잔이 놓인 쟁반, 빈 럼주 병 하나, 절반쯤 든 보드카 병 하나, 그리고 반쯤 베어 먹다 만 밀빵 조각 몇 개가 놓여 있었다. 손님 본인은 외투를 둘둘 말아 머리 밑에 베개 삼아 받친 채 긴 의자 위에 길게 뻗고 누워 요란하게 코를 골고 있었다. 미탸는 어찌할 바를 몰라 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"당연히 깨워야지. 내 용건이 너무도 중대하니까. 이렇게 다급하게 달려왔잖아. 오늘 안으로 돌아가야 한단 말이야.\" 그가 몹시 동요하며 말했다. 그러나 사제와 산지기는 아무 의견도 내놓지 않고 잠자코 서 있었다. 미탸는 다가가 직접 그를 깨워 보려 했고, 힘껏 흔들어 댔으나 잠든 사내는 깨어나지 않았다.\n\n\"취했군.\" 미탸는 단정했다. \"맙소사! 어떻게 해야 하나? 어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\" 그러고는 참다못해 그의 팔을, 다리를 잡아당기고 머리를 흔들어 대며 그를 들어 올려 긴 의자에 앉히려 했다. 그러나 한참을 용을 쓴 끝에 얻어 낸 것이라곤, 술 취한 사내가 터무니없는 끙끙 소리와 사납지만 알아들을 수 없는 욕설을 내뱉게 한 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"안 되겠습니다, 조금 기다리시는 편이 낫겠어요.\" 마침내 사제가 입을 열었다. \"보아하니 도무지 제정신이 아닌 상태이니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"하루 종일 마셔 댔거든요.\" 산지기가 거들었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"이게 나한테 얼마나 중대한 일인지, 또 내가 얼마나 절박한지 두 분이 아신다면!\"\n\n\"안 됩니다, 아침까지 기다리시는 게 낫겠어요.\" 사제가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"아침까지요? 원, 그건 말도 안 됩니다!\"\n\n그리고 절망에 빠진 그는 또다시 잠든 사내에게 달려들려다가, 그런 짓이 부질없음을 깨닫고 곧장 멈춰 섰다. 사제는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 졸음에 겨운 산지기는 침울한 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"현실이라는 게 사람한테 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 비극을 꾸며 내는지.\" 미탸가 완전히 절망에 잠겨 말했다. 땀이 그의 얼굴을 타고 줄줄 흘러내렸다. 사제는 이 틈을 놓치지 않고, 설령 그를 깨우는 데 성공한다 해도 여전히 취해 있어 대화를 나눌 형편이 못 될 것이라고 지극히 사리에 맞게 일러 주었다. \"게다가 당신 용건이 중대하니,\" 그가 말했다. \"필시 아침까지 미루시는 편이 나을 겁니다.\" 미탸는 절망의 몸짓을 하며 그 말에 동의했다.\n\n\"신부님, 저는 여기 불을 켜 놓고 남아서 좋은 기회를 잡겠습니다. 저자가 깨어나는 즉시 시작할 겁니다. 불값은 치르겠습니다.\" 그가 산지기에게 말했다. \"하룻밤 묵은 값도요. 드미트리 카라마조프를 기억해 두십시오. 다만 신부님, 신부님은 어떻게 해 드려야 할지 모르겠군요. 어디서 주무시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 저는 집으로 돌아가겠습니다. 저 사람 말을 빌려 집으로 가지요.\" 그가 산지기를 가리키며 말했다. \"그럼 이만 작별 인사를 드리겠습니다. 모든 일이 잘되기를 빕니다.\"\n\n그렇게 해서 매듭이 지어졌다. 사제는 산지기의 말을 타고 떠났으니, 빠져나가게 된 것을 기뻐하면서도 불안한 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 이튿날 자기 은인인 표도르 파블로비치에게 이 기묘한 사건을 알려야 하는 것은 아닐까 하고 생각했다. \"안 그러면 그분이 재수 없게 어디서 듣고는 화를 내며 베풀던 호의를 거두어 버릴지도 모르니까.\"\n\n산지기는 몸을 긁적이며 한마디 말도 없이 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 미탸는 본인 표현대로 \"좋은 기회를 잡으려고\" 긴 의자에 앉았다. 깊은 낙담이 짙은 안개처럼 그의 영혼에 들러붙었다. 깊고 사무치는 낙담이! 그는 앉아서 골똘히 생각했으나 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못했다. 촛불은 침침하게 타올랐고 귀뚜라미가 울었다. 후끈하게 달궈진 방 안은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 숨이 막혔다. 문득 그의 머릿속에 정원이, 정원 뒤편의 오솔길이, 아버지 집의 문이 은밀히 열리고 그루센카가 뛰어들어 가는 모습이 떠올랐다. 그는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"이건 비극이야!\" 그는 이를 갈며 말했다. 무심결에 그는 잠든 사내에게 다가가 그 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그는 깡마른 중년 농부로, 얼굴이 무척 길쭉하고 머리는 아마 빛 곱슬이었으며 길고 가느다란 불그스레한 수염을 길렀고, 파란 무명 셔츠에 검은 조끼를 걸쳤는데 그 조끼 주머니에서는 은시계 줄이 삐죽이 내다보였다. 미탸는 격렬한 증오를 품고 그의 얼굴을 노려보았는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그 곱슬머리가 유난히 그의 신경을 거슬렀다.\n\n견딜 수 없이 굴욕스러운 것은, 그토록 중대한 일들을 내팽개치고 그토록 큰 희생을 치른 끝에, 이 미탸가 기진맥진한 몸으로 이토록 다급한 용건을 안고서 자기 운명 전부가 걸린 이 멍청이 위에 우두커니 서 있다는 사실이었으니, 정작 그자는 아무 일도 없다는 듯, 마치 다른 별에서 뚝 떨어지기라도 한 듯 코를 골고 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"오, 운명의 농간이여!\" 미탸가 소리쳤고, 완전히 이성을 잃은 채 다시금 술 취한 농부를 깨우는 데 달려들었다. 그는 일종의 광포함을 띠고 그를 흔들어 깨웠으니, 잡아당기고 떠밀고 심지어 두들겨 패기까지 했다. 그러나 오 분 동안 헛되이 용을 쓴 끝에, 그는 힘없는 절망에 잠겨 자기 긴 의자로 돌아와 주저앉았다.\n\n\"멍청한 놈! 멍청한 놈!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"게다가 이게 다 얼마나 떳떳치 못한 짓이냐!\" 무언가가 그로 하여금 이 말을 덧붙이게 했다. 머리가 끔찍하게 아프기 시작했다. \"이 일을 내던지고 아주 떠나 버릴까?\" 그는 자문했다. \"아니, 이제 내일까지 기다려야지. 일부러라도 남아 있겠어. 안 그러면 뭐 하러 왔단 말인가? 게다가 떠날 방도도 없잖아. 이제 와서 어떻게 여기를 벗어난단 말인가? 아, 이 멍청한 짓거리라니!\"\n\n그러나 머리는 점점 더 아파 왔다. 그는 꼼짝도 않고 앉아 있다가 자기도 모르게 꾸벅꾸벅 졸더니 앉은 채로 잠이 들고 말았다. 그렇게 두 시간 남짓 잔 듯했다. 그는 머리가 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 아픈지 소리라도 지르고 싶을 지경이 되어 잠에서 깨어났다. 관자놀이가 쿵쾅거리고 정수리가 욱신거렸다. 한참이 지나서야 그는 비로소 완전히 정신을 차리고 자기에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 깨달을 수 있었다.\n\n마침내 그는 방 안이 난로에서 새어 나온 숯 가스로 가득 차서 자칫 질식해 죽을 수도 있음을 알아차렸다. 그리고 술 취한 농부는 여전히 코를 골며 누워 있었다. 촛불은 가물거리며 막 꺼지려 하고 있었다. 미탸는 소리를 지르며 비틀비틀 통로를 가로질러 산지기 방으로 뛰어 들어갔다. 산지기는 곧 잠에서 깨어났으나, 옆방이 가스로 가득 찼다는 말을 듣고도 미탸가 놀라고 짜증스럽게도 묘하게 무덤덤하게 그 사실을 받아들였으니, 그래도 살펴보러 가기는 했다.\n\n\"하지만 저 사람이 죽었다고요, 죽었어요! 그러면… 난 대체 어떻게 하란 말입니까?\" 미탸가 미친 듯이 소리쳤다.\n\n그들은 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 창과 굴뚝을 텄다. 미탸는 통로에서 물 한 통을 길어 왔다. 먼저 자기 머리를 적신 다음, 헝겊 조각 같은 것을 찾아내 물에 적셔서는 라가비의 머리에 얹었다. 산지기는 여전히 이 일을 대수롭잖게 여겼고, 창문을 열면서 퉁명스레 말했다.\n\n\"이제 괜찮을 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 미탸에게 불 켜진 등을 남겨 두고 다시 자러 갔다. 미탸는 술 취한 농부를 두고 반 시간 동안 부산을 떨며 그의 머리를 적셨고, 밤새도록 자지 않으리라 굳게 마음먹었다. 그러나 그는 너무도 기진맥진한 터라, 잠깐 숨을 돌리려고 앉은 순간 두 눈이 감겼고, 자기도 모르게 긴 의자에 길게 몸을 뻗고 죽은 듯이 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n그가 깨어났을 때는 끔찍이도 늦은 시각이었다. 아홉 시쯤이었다. 오두막의 자그마한 두 창문으로 햇살이 환하게 비쳐 들고 있었다. 곱슬머리 농부는 긴 의자에 앉아 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 그 앞에는 또 다른 사모바르와 또 다른 병이 놓여 있었다. 어제의 병은 이미 비워졌고, 새 병도 절반 넘게 줄어 있었다. 미탸는 벌떡 일어나, 그 빌어먹을 농부가 다시, 도무지 어찌해 볼 도리 없이 곤드레만드레 취해 있음을 단번에 알아차렸다. 그는 잠시 두 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 그를 노려보았다. 농부는 모욕적인 침착함을 띠고, 미탸가 보기에는 심지어 일종의 경멸 어린 거드름까지 피우며 잠자코 능청스레 그를 지켜보고 있었다. 미탸는 그에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"이보시오, 실은… 나는… 당신도 여기 오두막의 산지기한테서 들었겠지만. 나는 드미트리 카라마조프 중위요, 당신이 숲을 사들이고 있는 그 카라마조프 영감의 아들이란 말이오.\"\n\n\"거짓말이군.\" 농부가 침착하고 자신만만하게 말했다.\n\n\"거짓말이라니? 당신 표도르 파블로비치를 알잖소?\"\n\n\"당신네 표도르 파블로비치 따위는 난 모르오.\" 농부가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 그 사람하고 숲을, 그 숲을 두고 흥정하고 있잖소. 좀 깨어나서 정신을 차려 보시오. 일리인스코예의 파벨 신부님이 나를 여기로 데려왔단 말이오. 당신이 삼소노프한테 편지를 썼고, 그가 나를 당신한테 보냈잖소.\" 미탸가 숨을 헐떡이며 다급하게 말했다.\n\n\"거, 거짓말이야!\" 라가비가 또다시 불쑥 내뱉었다. 미탸는 다리가 싸늘해졌다.\n\n\"제발 좀! 이건 농담이 아니란 말이오! 당신 취했나 본데. 그래도 말은 하고 알아듣잖소… 아니면… 난 도무지 영문을 모르겠군!\"\n\n\"당신은 환쟁이야!\"\n\n\"제발 좀! 나는 카라마조프요, 드미트리 카라마조프란 말이오. 당신한테 제안할 게 있소, 솔깃한 제안이지… 아주 솔깃한 제안이오, 그 숲에 관한!\"\n\n농부는 거드름을 피우며 수염을 쓰다듬었다.\n\n\"아니지, 당신은 일을 맡아 놓고 사기꾼으로 드러난 거야. 당신은 악당이라고!\"\n\n\"맹세컨대 당신이 잘못 안 거요.\" 미탸가 절망에 빠져 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다. 농부는 여전히 수염을 쓰다듬다가, 갑자기 교활하게 두 눈을 가늘게 떴다.\n\n\"아니지, 그럼 어디 이걸 내게 보여 봐. 사기를 쳐도 된다는 법이 어디 있는지 말해 보란 말이야. 알아들어? 당신은 악당이야! 그걸 알아듣겠어?\"\n\n미탸는 침울하게 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 그 순간 그가 나중에 한 말마따나 \"무언가가 머리를 후려치는 것 같았다.\" 한순간에 그의 머릿속에 한 줄기 빛이 동트는 듯했으니, \"빛이 켜지면서 나는 모든 것을 깨달았다\"는 것이다. 그는 멍하니 선 채로, 명색이 분별 있는 인간이라는 자기가 어떻게 이런 어리석음에 굴복하고, 어떻게 이런 모험에 끌려들어, 거의 스물네 시간 동안이나 이 라가비를 둘러싸고 부산을 떨며 그 머리를 적셔 가면서까지 이 짓을 이어 왔는지 의아해했다.\n\n\"이런, 저 작자는 취했어, 곤죽이 되도록 취했고, 이제 일주일은 줄곧 마셔 댈 텐데. 여기서 기다린들 무슨 소용이람? 그리고 만일 삼소노프가 일부러 나를 여기로 보낸 거라면? 만일 그녀가…? 오, 하느님, 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지?\"\n\n농부는 앉아서 그를 지켜보며 히죽거렸다. 다른 때 같았으면 미탸는 격분하여 그 멍청이를 죽여 버렸을지도 모르나, 지금은 어린아이처럼 무력하게 느껴졌다. 그는 조용히 긴 의자로 가서 외투를 집어 들고 한마디 말도 없이 걸쳐 입고는 오두막을 나섰다. 옆방에서 산지기는 보이지 않았으니, 거기엔 아무도 없었다. 그는 주머니에서 잔돈으로 오십 코페이카를 꺼내, 하룻밤 묵은 값과 촛값과 끼친 폐에 대한 값으로 탁자 위에 놓아두었다. 오두막을 나서자 사방에 온통 숲뿐이었다. 그는 오두막에서 오른쪽으로 가야 할지 왼쪽으로 가야 할지도 모른 채 무작정 걸었다. 전날 저녁 사제와 함께 그리로 서둘러 오느라 길을 눈여겨보지 못했던 것이다. 그의 마음속에는 누구에 대해서도, 심지어 삼소노프에 대해서도 복수심이라곤 없었다. 그는 좁다란 숲길을 따라 정처 없이, 얼떨떨한 채로, 어디로 가는지도 아랑곳없이 성큼성큼 걸었다. 어린아이라도 그를 쓰러뜨릴 수 있었을 만큼, 그는 몸도 마음도 쇠약해져 있었다. 그래도 그는 어찌어찌 숲을 빠져나왔고, 추수가 끝나 휑하게 드러난 들판이 눈길 닿는 데까지 멀리 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n\"이 무슨 절망인가! 사방이 온통 죽음뿐이로구나!\" 그는 걷고 또 걸으며 거듭 중얼거렸다.\n\n그는 세를 낸 마차를 타고 들판을 가로질러 가던 한 늙은 상인을 만난 덕분에 구원을 받았다. 그를 따라잡은 미탸가 길을 묻자, 그 늙은 상인 또한 볼로뱌로 가는 길임이 드러났다. 얼마간 이야기를 주고받은 끝에 미탸는 그 마차에 올라탔다. 세 시간 뒤 그들은 도착했다. 볼로뱌에서 미탸는 곧장 읍내로 갈 역마를 청해 두고는, 문득 자기가 무시무시하게 배고프다는 것을 깨달았다. 말에 마구를 채우는 동안 그를 위해 오믈렛이 마련되었다. 그는 그것을 눈 깜짝할 새에 먹어 치웠고, 커다란 빵 한 덩이를 먹고 소시지를 먹고 보드카 석 잔을 들이켰다. 먹고 나자 기분이 한결 가벼워지고 마음도 누그러졌다. 그는 읍내를 향해 날 듯이 달리며 마부를 재촉했고, 문득 저녁이 되기 전에 그 \"빌어먹을 돈\"을 마련하겠다는 새롭고도 \"결코 번복할 수 없는\" 계획을 세웠다. \"생각해 보면, 글쎄 생각해 보면 사람의 인생이 그까짓 하찮은 삼천 루블 때문에 망가져야 한다니!\" 그가 경멸조로 외쳤다. \"오늘 안에 결판을 내겠어.\" 그리고 한순간도 그를 떠나지 않는 그루센카 생각과 그녀에게 무슨 일이 일어났을지도 모른다는 생각만 아니었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 다시 제법 명랑해졌을지도 모른다… 그러나 그녀 생각은 시시각각 날카로운 칼처럼 그의 가슴을 찔러 댔다.\n\n마침내 그들은 도착했고, 미탸는 곧장 그루센카에게로 달려갔다.\n\n# Chapter III. Gold-Mines\n\n그것은 그루셴카가 라키친에게 그토록 진저리를 치며 이야기했던 미탸의 방문이었다. 그녀는 마침 그 \"전갈\"을 기다리고 있던 참이라, 미탸가 그날도 그 전날도 자기를 찾아오지 않은 것을 무척 다행으로 여기던 터였다. 그녀는 \"제발 내가 떠날 때까지 그 사람이 오지 않게 해 주십사\" 하고 빌었건만, 그가 느닷없이 그녀에게 들이닥친 것이었다. 그다음의 일은 우리도 이미 아는 바와 같다. 그를 떼어 놓으려고 그녀는 곧장 자기와 함께 삼소노프의 집까지 걸어가자고 제안했는데, 그곳에 가서 \"그의 셈을 정리해야\" 한다고 했고, 반드시 가야만 한다고 말했다. 미탸가 곧장 그녀를 따라나서자, 그녀는 대문 앞에서 작별을 고하며 열두 시에 다시 와서 자기를 집으로 데려다 달라고 약속을 받아 두었다. 미탸 또한 이 약속에 흡족해했다. 그녀가 삼소노프의 집에 앉아 있다면 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 갈 수는 없을 테니 말이다. \"그저 그 여자가 거짓말만 하지 않는다면야.\" 그는 곧바로 이렇게 덧붙였다. 그러나 자기가 본 바로는 그녀가 거짓말을 하는 것 같지 않다고 생각했다.\n\n그는 사랑하는 여자가 곁에 없을 때면 당장에 그녀에게 무슨 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있을지, 그녀가 어떻게 자기를 배신하고 있을지 온갖 무서운 공상을 지어내면서도, 막상 마음이 흔들리고 가슴이 찢어지며 그녀의 부정을 확신하게 되면 다시 그녀에게로 달려가는 그런 부류의 질투하는 사내였다. 그녀의 얼굴을 처음 보는 순간, 그 명랑하고 웃음 띤 다정한 얼굴을 보는 순간 그는 단숨에 되살아나 모든 의심을 내려놓고는 기쁘면서도 부끄러운 마음으로 제 질투를 스스로 꾸짖는 것이었다.\n\n그루셴카를 대문 앞에 남겨 두고 그는 집으로 달려갔다. 아아, 그날 그에게는 아직도 해야 할 일이 그토록 많았던 것이다! 그래도 어쨌든 가슴에서 짐 하나는 내려간 셈이었다.\n\n\"이제 서둘러서 스메르댜코프에게 어젯밤 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 혹시라도 그녀가 표도르 파블로비치한테 갔던 것은 아닌지 알아보기만 하면 된다, 어휴!\" 이런 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다.\n\n그러나 그가 미처 하숙집에 다다르기도 전에 그 불안한 가슴속에는 또다시 질투가 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n질투라니! \"오셀로는 질투하지 않았다, 그는 사람을 믿었던 것이다.\" 푸시킨은 이렇게 말했다. 그 한마디만으로도 우리의 위대한 시인이 지닌 깊은 통찰을 넉넉히 엿볼 수 있다. 오셀로의 영혼이 산산이 부서지고 그의 온 세계관이 흐려진 것은 다만 그의 이상이 파괴되었기 때문이었다. 그러나 오셀로는 숨거나 엿보거나 몰래 살피기 시작하지는 않았다. 오히려 그는 사람을 믿었다. 그는 누가 이끌어 주고 떠밀어 주고 갖은 애를 써서 부추겨 준 뒤에야 비로소 배신이라는 생각을 품을 수 있었다. 진정으로 질투하는 사내는 그렇지 않다. 질투하는 사내가 양심의 거리낌 하나 없이 빠져들 수 있는 수치와 도덕적 타락을 누구도 상상할 수 없을 것이다. 그렇다고 질투하는 자들이 모두 천박하고 비루한 영혼인 것도 아니다. 오히려 고귀한 감정을 지닌 사람, 순결하고 자기희생으로 가득 찬 사랑을 품은 사람도 식탁 밑에 숨고, 가장 더러운 자들을 매수하고, 염탐과 도청이라는 가장 천한 치욕에 익숙해질 수 있는 법이다.\n\n오셀로는 결코 부정을 받아들이기로 마음먹지 못했으니—용서하지 못한 것이 아니라 그것을 받아들이기로 마음먹지 못한 것이다—그의 영혼이 갓난아이처럼 순결하고 악의가 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 진정으로 질투하는 사내는 그렇지 않다. 어떤 질투하는 사내들이 무엇을 받아들이고 무엇을 못 본 체할 수 있으며, 또 무엇을 용서할 수 있는지는 상상조차 하기 어렵다! 질투하는 자들이야말로 누구보다 선뜻 용서하는 자들이며, 이것은 모든 여자가 다 아는 사실이다. 질투하는 사내는 (물론 한바탕 격렬한 소동을 치른 뒤이긴 하지만) 놀라우리만치 빨리 용서할 수 있으며, 거의 결정적으로 입증된 부정마저, 제 눈으로 본 입맞춤과 포옹마저 용서할 수 있으니, 다만 그 모든 것이 \"마지막\"이었으며 그날 이후로 연적이 사라져 땅끝으로 떠나 버리리라고, 혹은 자기 자신이 그녀를 어딘가 그 두려운 연적이 가까이 다가오지 못할 곳으로 데려가 버리리라고 어떻게든 확신할 수만 있다면 그렇게 한다. 물론 그 화해란 고작 한 시간짜리다. 설령 연적이 다음 날 사라진다 해도 그는 또 다른 연적을 지어내어 그를 질투할 테니 말이다. 그렇게까지 감시해야 하는 사랑이라면 대체 그 안에 무엇이 들어 있는 것인지, 그토록 안간힘을 써서 지켜야 하는 사랑이 대체 무슨 값어치가 있는 것인지 의아할 법도 하다. 그러나 그것을 질투하는 자는 결코 이해하지 못한다. 그런데도 그들 가운데는 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사내들이 있다. 게다가 주목할 만한 것은, 바로 그 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사내들이 어느 벽장 속에 숨어 귀를 기울이고 엿보면서도, 적어도 그 순간만은 양심의 가책을 조금도 느끼지 않는다는 점이니, 그들의 \"고귀한 마음\"으로도 자신이 스스로 가라앉은 그 수치스러운 나락이 어느 정도인지를 똑똑히 헤아리고 있으면서도 그러한 것이다.\n\n그루셴카를 보는 순간 미탸의 질투는 사라졌고, 한순간 그는 사람을 믿는 너그러운 사내가 되어 자신의 사악한 감정을 진정으로 경멸했다. 그러나 그것은 다만, 그 여자를 향한 그의 사랑 속에 그 자신이 상상하던 것보다 훨씬 더 높은 무언가가 깃들어 있음을, 그것이 그저 관능적인 정념만이, 알료샤에게 늘어놓았던 그 \"몸의 곡선\"만이 아님을 증명할 따름이었다. 그러나 그루셴카가 떠나자마자 미탸는 그녀가 부정한 여자의 온갖 저열한 술수를 부리고 있을 것이라 의심하기 시작했고, 그러면서도 양심의 가책이라곤 조금도 느끼지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 그의 안에서 또다시 질투가 솟구쳐 올랐다. 어쨌거나 그는 서둘러야만 했다. 먼저 해야 할 일은 적어도 얼마간이라도 임시로 돈을 빌리는 일이었다. 그 구 루블은 이번 여정에 거의 다 써 버린 터였다. 그리고 누구나 알다시피, 돈 없이는 한 발짝도 움직일 수 없는 법이다. 그러나 그는 마차 안에서 어디서 돈을 빌릴 수 있을지 곰곰이 생각해 둔 바가 있었다. 그에게는 상자에 든 훌륭한 결투용 권총 한 쌍이 있었는데, 그것은 자기 소유물 중에서 무엇보다 아끼는 물건이었기에 그때까지 전당포에 잡히지 않고 있던 것이었다.\n\n그는 얼마 전 \"메트로폴리스\" 선술집에서 한 젊은 관리와 알게 되었고, 이 매우 부유한 독신 사내가 무기를 열렬히 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그는 권총이며 회전식 연발총이며 단검을 사들여 벽에 걸어 두고 지인들에게 보여 주곤 했다. 그는 그것들을 자랑으로 여겼고, 연발총의 구조에 관해서는 어엿한 전문가였다. 미탸는 더 생각해 볼 것도 없이 곧장 그를 찾아가 권총을 십 루블에 잡혀 달라고 제안했다. 관리는 기뻐하며 아예 통째로 팔라고 설득하려 들었다. 그러나 미탸가 응하지 않자, 젊은 사내는 십 루블을 내주면서 이자 따위는 결코 받지 않겠노라고 우겼다. 두 사람은 친구처럼 헤어졌다.\n\n미탸는 서둘렀다. 그는 되도록 빨리 스메르댜코프를 붙들려고 표도르 파블로비치의 집 뒷길을 통해 그 정자 쪽으로 달려갔다. 이리하여, 이제 곧 이야기할 어떤 사건이 일어나기 서너 시간 전만 해도 미탸에게는 한 푼도 없었으며, 자기가 아끼던 소유물을 십 루블에 잡혔다는 사실이 확인된 셈이다. 그러나 세 시간 뒤에 그는 수천 루블을 손에 쥐고 있었다…… 그러나 내가 너무 앞서 가고 있다. 마리야 콘드라티예브나(표도르 파블로비치의 집 근처에 사는 여자)에게서 그는 스메르댜코프가 병이 났다는 매우 걱정스러운 사실을 알게 되었다. 그가 지하실에서 굴러떨어진 이야기, 발작을 일으킨 일, 의사가 다녀간 일, 표도르 파블로비치가 노심초사하던 일을 들었으며, 또한 형 이반이 그날 아침 모스크바로 떠났다는 소식도 흥미롭게 들었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 형이 나보다 먼저 볼로뱌를 지나갔겠군.\" 드미트리는 이렇게 생각했으나, 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 몹시 마음이 어지러웠다. \"이제 어떻게 되는 거지? 누가 나를 위해 망을 봐 준단 말인가? 누가 내게 소식을 전해 준단 말인가?\" 그는 이렇게 생각했다. 그는 전날 저녁에 무언가 본 것이 없느냐고 여자들에게 다급히 캐묻기 시작했다. 그들은 그가 무엇을 알아내려 하는지 충분히 알아차렸고, 그를 완전히 안심시켜 주었다. 아무도 거기 오지 않았다는 것이었다. 이반 표도로비치가 밤에 거기 있었고, 모든 것이 평소와 다름없이 지나갔다고 했다. 미탸는 생각에 잠겼다. 그는 오늘 틀림없이 망을 봐야 할 텐데, 어디서 봐야 한단 말인가? 여기서? 아니면 삼소노프의 대문 앞에서? 그는 여기든 저기든 양쪽을 다 살피기로 마음먹었으나, 그러는 동안에…… 그러는 동안에…… 문제는 돌아오는 길에 세운 새로운 계획을 실행에 옮겨야 한다는 것이었다. 그는 그 성공을 확신했으나, 지체 없이 행동에 나서야만 했다. 미탸는 거기에 한 시간을 바치기로 결심했다. \"한 시간이면 모든 것을 알게 되고 모든 것을 매듭지을 수 있을 테니, 그런 다음에, 그런 다음에 무엇보다 먼저 삼소노프의 집으로 가는 거다. 그루셴카가 거기 있는지 알아보고는 곧장 다시 여기로 돌아와 열한 시까지 머물다가, 그런 뒤에 다시 삼소노프의 집으로 가서 그녀를 집으로 데려오는 거다.\" 이것이 그가 내린 결정이었다.\n\n그는 집으로 날아가 몸을 씻고 머리를 빗고 옷에 솔질을 한 다음, 옷을 차려입고 호흘라코바 부인의 집으로 갔다. 아아! 그는 그 부인에게 희망을 걸어 두었던 것이다. 그는 그 부인에게서 삼천 루블을 빌리기로 결심했다. 게다가 그는 그녀가 결코 빌려 주기를 거절하지 않으리라는 확신이 문득 들었다. 그가 그토록 확신했다면, 어째서 처음부터 그녀, 말하자면 자기와 같은 부류의 사람에게 가지 않고, 알지도 못하는 데다 자기와 같은 계층도 아니어서 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지조차 막막한 삼소노프에게 갔던 것인지 의아할 법도 하다.\n\n그러나 사실인즉 그는 호흘라코바 부인을 한 번도 제대로 알았던 적이 없었고, 지난 한 달 동안 그녀를 통 본 적도 없었으며, 게다가 그녀가 자기를 못 견뎌 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 처음부터 그를 몹시 싫어했는데, 그가 카테리나 이바노브나와 약혼한 사이였기 때문이었다. 그런데 그녀는 무슨 까닭에선지 문득, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그를 버리고 \"매력적이고 기사답게 세련된 데다 그토록 흠잡을 데 없는 예의범절을 갖춘 이반\"과 결혼했으면 하는 바람을 품게 된 것이었다. 미탸의 예의범절을 그녀는 몹시 싫어했다. 미탸 또한 대놓고 그녀를 비웃었고, 한번은 그녀를 두고, 교양이 없는 만큼이나 활달하고 격의가 없는 여자라고 말한 적도 있었다. 그러나 그날 아침 마차 안에서 그에게 한 가지 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐던 것이다. \"그 여자가 내가 카테리나 이바노브나와 결혼하지 않기를 그토록 바란다면,\" (그는 그녀가 이 문제에 관해서는 거의 신경질적일 정도임을 알고 있었다) \"내가 카탸를 떠나 영영 그녀에게서 벗어날 수 있도록 그 삼천 루블을 거절할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가. 응석받이로 자란 이런 귀부인들은 일단 무엇엔가 마음을 쏟으면 제 변덕을 채우기 위해 비용을 아끼지 않는 법이지. 게다가 그 여자는 어찌나 부유한가.\" 미탸는 이렇게 따져 보았다.\n\n그의 \"계획\"으로 말하자면 그것은 전과 똑같은 것이었으니, 곧 체르마쉬냐에 대한 자기 권리를 내놓겠다는 것이었다. 다만 삼소노프에게 했던 것처럼 장삿속으로, 즉 육칠천 루블의 이익을 볼 가능성으로 부인을 꾀려는 것이 아니라, 그저 빚에 대한 담보로 내놓겠다는 것이었다. 이 새로운 착상을 다듬어 가면서 미탸는 그것에 매료되었으나, 그가 벌이는 모든 일, 그가 문득 내리는 모든 결정에서 늘 그러했듯이 이번에도 마찬가지였다. 그는 새로운 착상이 떠오를 때마다 그것에 열렬한 열정으로 빠져들었다. 그러나 호흘라코바 부인의 집 계단을 올라설 때 그는 등줄기를 타고 두려움의 전율이 흘러내리는 것을 느꼈다. 그 순간 그는, 이것이 마지막 희망이며, 이것마저 무너지면 이 세상에 자기에게 남은 것이라곤 \"그 삼천 루블을 위해 누군가를 털고 죽이는\" 일밖에 없으리라는 것을, 수학적 확실성처럼 분명히 깨달았던 것이다. 그가 초인종을 울렸을 때는 일곱 시 반이었다.\n\n처음에는 운이 그에게 미소를 짓는 듯했다. 그가 왔다는 전갈이 들어가자마자 그는 놀라우리만치 빠르게 맞아들여졌다. \"마치 나를 기다리고 있었던 것 같군.\" 미탸는 이렇게 생각했고, 응접실로 안내되자마자 안주인이 몸소 달려 나와 자기가 그를 기다리고 있었노라고 대뜸 선언했다.\n\n\"기다리고 있었어요! 기다리고 있었답니다! 당신이 나를 찾아오리라 짐작할 만한 이유라곤 없었는데도 말이에요, 그건 당신 자신도 인정하실 테죠. 그런데도 나는 당신을 기다렸어요. 내 직감에 놀라셔도 좋아요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 하지만 나는 아침 내내 당신이 오리라고 확신하고 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"그것 참 놀라운 일이로군요, 부인.\" 미탸는 맥없이 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"그러나 저는 대단히 중대한 일로 부인을 찾아왔습니다…… 제게는 더없이 중대한 일이지요, 부인…… 오직 저 혼자만의 일입니다…… 그래서 서둘러—\"\n\n\"당신이 무척 중대한 용건으로 오셨다는 건 알아요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 이건 예감 같은 게 아니에요, 기적 따위로 되돌아가는 반동적인 일도 아니고요(조시마 장로 이야기는 들으셨나요?). 이건 수학의 문제예요. 카테리나 이바노브나와 그 모든 일이 있은 뒤이니, 당신은 오지 않을 수가 없었던 거예요. 오지 않을 수가, 오지 않을 수가 없었어요, 그건 수학적 확실성이랍니다.\"\n\n\"실제 삶의 현실주의지요, 부인, 바로 그겁니다. 그러나 제 말씀을 좀 들려 드리도록 해 주십시오—\"\n\n\"현실주의라, 그래요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 나는 이제 온통 현실주의 편이에요. 기적이라면 지긋지긋하게 봐 왔으니까요. 조시마 장로께서 돌아가셨다는 소식은 들으셨나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 부인. 처음 듣는 이야깁니다.\" 미탸는 조금 놀랐다. 알료샤의 모습이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"어젯밤이었어요, 그런데 글쎄 상상해 보세요—\"\n\n\"부인,\" 미탸가 말했다. \"제가 절망적인 처지에 놓여 있다는 것, 그리고 부인께서 저를 도와주지 않으시면 모든 것이 파국에 이르고 무엇보다 저 자신이 먼저 그리되리라는 것 말고는 아무것도 상상할 수가 없습니다. 표현이 변변찮은 것을 용서해 주십시오, 그러나 저는 지금 열병에 들떠 있어서—\"\n\n\"알아요, 당신이 열병에 들떠 있다는 거 다 알아요. 그러지 않을 수가 없겠지요. 당신이 내게 무슨 말씀을 하시든 나는 미리 다 알고 있어요. 나는 오래전부터 당신의 운명을 곰곰이 생각해 왔답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 나는 그것을 지켜보고 연구하고 있어요…… 오, 믿어 주세요, 나는 영혼의 노련한 의사랍니다, 드미트리 표도로비치.\"\n\n\"부인, 부인께서 노련한 의사시라면 저야말로 노련한 환자임에 틀림없지요.\" 미탸는 애써 정중하게 말했다. \"그리고 부인께서 그처럼 제 운명을 지켜봐 주신다면, 제가 파멸하는 마당에 저를 도와주시리라 믿습니다, 그러니 적어도 제가 부인을 감히 찾아오게 만든 그 계획만이라도…… 그리고 부인께 바라는 것이 무엇인지만이라도 설명드릴 수 있게 해 주십시오…… 저는 찾아왔습니다, 부인—\"\n\n\"설명하실 것 없어요. 그건 그다지 중요한 일이 아니니까요. 그런데 돕는 것으로 말하자면, 당신이 내가 도운 첫 번째 사람도 아니랍니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 내 사촌 벨메소바 부인 이야기는 아마 들으셨겠지요. 그 남편이 파산해서, 당신이 그 특유의 표현으로 말씀하셨듯이 '파국에 이르렀더랬어요', 드미트리 표도로비치. 내가 그 사람에게 말 사육을 해 보라고 권했더니, 지금은 아주 잘 지내고 있답니다. 당신은 말 사육에 대해 좀 아시나요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\"\n\n\"전혀, 조금도 모릅니다, 부인. 아, 부인, 정말이지 조금도 모릅니다!\" 미탸는 초조한 조바심에 사로잡혀 외쳤고, 실제로 자리에서 벌떡 일어날 뻔했다. \"그저 간청드리오니, 부인, 제 말씀을 좀 들어 주십시오. 그저 제가 모든 것을, 제가 가지고 온 계획 전부를 설명드릴 수 있도록 자유롭게 말할 이 분만 주십시오. 게다가 저는 시간이 없습니다. 저는 무섭게 서두르고 있어요.\" 미탸는 그녀가 또다시 말을 시작하려는 것을 느끼고는 말을 끊어 보려는 심산으로 신경질적으로 외쳤다. \"저는 절망에 빠져 찾아왔습니다…… 절망의 마지막 숨결에 매달려, 삼천 루블이라는 돈을 꾸어 주십사 간청드리러 온 것입니다, 빌리는 것입니다, 그러나 확실하고도 더없이 확실한 담보로, 부인, 가장 믿을 만한 보증을 걸고서 말입니다! 그저 설명드리게만—\"\n\n\"그건 다 나중에, 나중에 말씀하셔야 해요!\" 호흘라코바 부인은 이번에는 자기 차례라는 듯 손짓으로 침묵을 요구했다. \"당신이 무슨 말씀을 하시든 나는 미리 다 알고 있어요. 아까 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요. 당신은 일정한 금액을, 삼천 루블을 청하시지만, 나는 그보다 더, 헤아릴 수 없이 더 많은 것을 드릴 수 있어요. 내가 당신을 구해 드리겠어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그러나 당신은 내 말을 들으셔야 해요.\"\n\n미탸는 또다시 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"부인, 정말로 그토록 친절을 베풀어 주시는 겁니까!\" 그는 북받치는 감정으로 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 부인께서 저를 구하셨군요! 부인께서는 한 사내를 비참한 죽음에서, 총탄에서 구해 주신 겁니다…… 영원토록 감사드리겠—\"\n\n\"삼천 루블보다 더, 한없이 더 많은 것을 드리겠어요!\" 호흘라코바 부인은 미탸의 환희를 환한 미소로 바라보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"한없이요? 하지만 저는 그렇게 많이는 필요 없습니다. 저는 그저 그 운명적인 삼천 루블만 있으면 됩니다. 그리고 저로서는 한없는 감사와 함께 그 금액에 대한 담보를 드릴 수 있으며, 한 가지 계획을 제안드리는데 그것은—\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 말은 이미 끝났고 일은 다 된 거예요.\" 호흘라코바 부인은 선행을 베푸는 자의 겸손한 승리감에 젖어 그의 말을 잘랐다. \"내가 당신을 구해 드리겠노라고 약속했으니, 구해 드리겠어요. 벨메소프에게 해 준 것처럼 당신을 구해 드릴 거예요. 금광에 대해서는 어떻게 생각하세요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\"\n\n\"금광 말입니까, 부인? 그런 건 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 당신을 위해 그걸 생각해 왔어요. 몇 번이고 거듭 생각했답니다. 나는 지난 한 달 동안 당신을 지켜봐 왔어요. 당신이 지나가는 모습을 백 번도 더 보면서 혼자 이렇게 되뇌었지요. 저 사람은 정력적인 사람이니 금광에 가 있어야 마땅하다고요. 나는 당신의 걸음걸이를 연구한 끝에 이런 결론에 이르렀답니다. 저 사람은 금을 찾아낼 사람이라고요.\"\n\n\"제 걸음걸이로 말입니까, 부인?\" 미탸는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 당신의 걸음걸이로요. 걸음걸이를 보면 사람의 성품을 알 수 있다는 걸 설마 부정하시진 않겠지요, 드미트리 표도로비치? 과학이 그 견해를 뒷받침하고 있어요. 나는 이제 온통 과학과 현실주의 편이랍니다. 나를 그토록 뒤흔들어 놓은 조시마 장로의 그 모든 일을 겪고 나서, 바로 오늘부터 나는 현실주의자가 되어 실질적인 유익에 헌신하고 싶어요. 나는 다 나았답니다. 투르게네프의 말마따나 '이제 그만!'이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 부인, 부인께서 그토록 너그러이 꾸어 주마 약속하신 그 삼천 루블은—\"\n\n\"그건 당신 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 호흘라코바 부인은 곧장 그의 말을 잘랐다. \"그 돈은 이미 당신 호주머니에 든 거나 다름없어요, 삼천 루블이 아니라 삼백만 루블이, 그것도 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말이에요. 내가 당신께 그 착상을 선물로 드리지요. 당신은 금광을 찾아내어 수백만 루블을 벌고 돌아와 지도적인 인물이 되어, 우리를 일깨우고 더 나은 길로 이끌게 될 거예요. 우리가 그 모든 걸 유대인들 손에 내맡겨야 하겠어요? 당신은 온갖 기관과 사업을 세우게 될 거예요. 당신은 가난한 이들을 도울 것이고, 그들은 당신을 축복하겠지요. 지금은 철도의 시대예요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 당신은 유명해질 것이고, 지금 그토록 형편이 어려운 재무부에 없어서는 안 될 사람이 될 거예요. 루블화의 가치 하락 때문에 나는 밤잠을 못 이룬답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 사람들은 나의 그런 면을 모르지요—\"\n\n\"부인, 부인!\" 드미트리가 불안한 예감에 사로잡혀 말을 가로막았다. \"정말이지 저는 부인의 충고를, 그 현명한 충고를 따를지도 모릅니다, 부인…… 어쩌면 저는 떠날지도 모르지요…… 금광으로 말입니다…… 그 일에 관해서는 다시 부인을 찾아뵙겠습니다…… 정말이지 여러 번이라도요…… 그러나 지금은, 부인께서 그토록 너그러이 약속하신 그 삼천 루블이…… 오, 그것이 저를 자유롭게 해 줄 텐데요, 오늘 안에 부인께서 해 주실 수만 있다면…… 보십시오, 저는 오늘 일 분도, 일 분도 허비할 틈이 없습니다—\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그만하세요!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 단호하게 말을 가로막았다. \"문제는, 당신이 금광에 가시겠느냐 안 가시겠느냐예요. 완전히 마음을 정하셨나요? 예, 아니요로 답해 주세요.\"\n\n\"가겠습니다, 부인, 나중에요…… 부인께서 원하시는 곳이라면 어디든 가겠습니다…… 그러나 지금은—\"\n\n\"기다려요!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다. 그러고는 벌떡 일어나 작은 서랍이 잔뜩 달린 멋들어진 책상으로 달려가더니, 서랍을 하나씩 차례로 잡아 빼면서 무언가를 미친 듯이 허둥지둥 찾기 시작했다.\n\n\"삼천 루블이다.\" 미탸는 심장이 멎을 것만 같은 심정으로 생각했다. \"게다가 당장…… 무슨 서류나 절차도 없이…… 이거야말로 신사답게 일을 처리하는 방식이로군! 저 부인은 훌륭한 여자야, 그저 말만 그렇게 많이 하지 않는다면 말이지!\"\n\n\"여기 있어요!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 미탸에게 기쁘게 달려오며 외쳤다. \"내가 찾던 게 여기 있네요!\"\n\n그것은 끈에 매단 자그마한 은제 성상으로, 때로는 십자가와 함께 살갗에 닿게 걸치곤 하는 그런 종류의 것이었다.\n\n\"이건 키예프에서 가져온 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 그녀는 경건하게 말을 이었다. \"거룩한 순교자 바르바라의 성유물에서 나온 것이랍니다. 내가 손수 당신 목에 걸어 드리지요, 그리고 이것과 더불어 당신을 새로운 삶에, 새로운 길에 봉헌하겠어요.\"\n\n그러고는 실제로 그 끈을 그의 목에 둘러 매무새를 다듬기 시작했다. 미탸는 몹시 당혹스러워하며 몸을 숙여 그녀를 도왔고, 마침내 그것을 넥타이와 옷깃 밑으로, 셔츠를 지나 가슴께까지 집어넣었다.\n\n\"이제 떠나셔도 돼요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 다시 의기양양하게 제자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"부인, 저는 정말 감동했습니다. 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다, 정말이지…… 이런 친절을 베풀어 주시니, 하지만…… 제게 시간이 얼마나 소중한지 부인께서 아신다면…… 부인의 너그러움에 신세를 지게 될 그 돈 말입니다…… 오, 부인, 부인께서 제게 이토록 친절하시고 이토록 가슴 뭉클하게 너그러우시니,\" 미탸는 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 부인께 털어놓겠습니다…… 물론 부인께서는 오래전부터 알고 계셨겠지만…… 제가 이 고장의 어떤 여인을 사랑하고 있다는 사실을요…… 저는 카탸를 배신했습니다…… 카테리나 이바노브나라고 해야겠군요…… 오, 저는 그녀에게 비인간적으로, 명예롭지 못하게 굴었습니다, 그러나 저는 이곳에서 다른 여인을 사랑하게 되었습니다…… 부인께서는, 부인, 어쩌면 경멸하실지도 모를 여인을, 부인께서는 이미 모든 것을 다 알고 계시니까요, 그러나 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그 여인을 떠날 수 없는 까닭에, 그 삼천 루블이 지금—\"\n\n\"모든 걸 떠나세요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 더없이 단호한 어조로 말을 가로막았다. \"모든 걸 떠나세요, 특히 여자들을요. 금광이 당신의 목표이고, 거기에 여자가 들어설 자리는 없어요. 나중에 부유하고 유명해져서 돌아오면, 당신은 가장 높은 사교계에서 당신 마음에 드는 처녀를 찾게 될 거예요. 그 처녀는 현대적인 처녀, 교양과 진보적인 사상을 갖춘 처녀일 거예요. 그때쯤이면 이제 막 동트기 시작한 여성 문제가 자리를 잡았을 테고, 새로운 여성이 나타나 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인, 그게 핵심이 아닙니다, 전혀 아니에요…….\" 미탸는 간청하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"아니에요, 바로 그거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 바로 당신께 필요한 거예요. 당신 자신은 깨닫지 못하고 있지만, 바로 당신이 갈망하고 있는 그거란 말이에요. 나는 오늘날의 여성 운동에 결코 반대하지 않아요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 여성의 발전, 그리고 머지않은 장래에 이루어질 여성의 정치적 해방까지—그것이 내 이상이랍니다. 나에게도 딸이 하나 있어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 사람들은 나의 그런 면을 모르지요. 나는 그 문제에 관해 작가 시체드린에게 편지를 한 통 보낸 적이 있어요. 그분은 여성의 사명에 관해 내게 참 많은 것을, 정말 많은 것을 가르쳐 주셨거든요. 그래서 작년에 나는 그분께 두 줄짜리 익명의 편지를 보냈답니다. '현대 여성을 위하여, 스승이신 당신께 입맞춤과 포옹을 보냅니다. 부디 굽히지 마소서.' 그러고는 '한 어머니로부터'라고 서명했지요. '한 동시대의 어머니로부터'라고 서명할까 망설이다가, 결국 그냥 '어머니'로 했어요. 그편이 도덕적으로 더 아름답거든요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 게다가 '동시대'라는 말은 그분께 '동시대인'을—검열 때문에 떠올리기 괴로운 기억을—떠올리게 했을지도 모르니까요…… 맙소사, 대체 무슨 일이세요!\"\n\n\"부인!\" 미탸가 마침내 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 그녀 앞에서 어쩔 줄 모르고 두 손을 맞잡아 간청했다. \"부인께서 그토록 너그러이 약속하신 것을 미루신다면 저는 울고 말 겁니다—\"\n\n\"오, 우세요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 어서 우세요! 그건 고귀한 감정이에요…… 당신 앞에는 그토록 활짝 열린 길이 있는걸요! 눈물은 당신의 마음을 편하게 해 줄 거예요, 그리고 나중에 당신은 기뻐하며 돌아오겠지요. 당신은 그 기쁨을 나와 나누려고 일부러 시베리아에서부터 내게로 달려올 거예요—\"\n\n\"하지만 저에게도 말할 기회를 좀 주십시오!\" 미탸가 느닷없이 외쳤다. \"마지막으로 간청드립니다, 말씀해 주십시오, 부인께서 약속하신 그 돈을 제가 오늘 받을 수 있습니까, 그게 안 된다면 언제 받으러 와야 합니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 돈 말인가요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\"\n\n\"부인께서 약속하신 그 삼천 루블 말입니다…… 그토록 너그러이—\"\n\n\"삼천 루블이요? 루블 말씀이세요? 오, 아니에요, 나에게는 삼천 루블이 없는걸요.\" 호흘라코바 부인은 태연하게 놀라워하며 선언했다. 미탸는 얼이 빠지고 말았다.\n\n\"아니, 방금 말씀하시기를…… 말씀하시기를…… 그것이 제 손에 든 거나 다름없다고 하셨잖습니까—\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 당신이 나를 오해하신 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 그렇다면 당신이 나를 오해하신 거죠. 나는 금광 이야기를 하고 있었던 거예요. 당신께 그보다 더, 삼천 루블보다 한없이 더 많은 것을 약속한 건 사실이에요, 이제 다 기억이 나는군요, 하지만 나는 금광을 두고 한 말이었답니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 돈은요? 그 삼천 루블은요?\" 미탸가 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 돈을 말씀하시는 거라면, 나에게는 한 푼도 없어요. 나에게는 땡전 한 푼 없답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 그 일로 지금 집사와 다투고 있는 중이고, 나 자신도 방금 미우소프에게서 오백 루블을 빌렸는걸요. 아니, 아니에요, 나에게는 돈이 없어요. 그리고 말이지요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 설령 내게 돈이 있다 한들 당신께는 드리지 않겠어요. 우선 첫째로 나는 결코 돈을 빌려 주지 않아요. 돈을 빌려 준다는 건 친구를 잃는다는 뜻이니까요. 게다가 당신께는 더더욱 드리지 않을 거예요. 당신을 좋아하고 당신을 구하고 싶어서 드리지 않겠다는 거예요, 당신께 필요한 건 오직 금광, 금광, 금광뿐이니까요!\"\n\n\"오, 빌어먹을!\" 미탸는 고함을 지르며 있는 힘껏 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳤다.\n\n\"아이고! 아이고!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 기겁하여 외치며 응접실 저쪽 끝으로 달아났다.\n\n미탸는 바닥에 침을 뱉고는 성큼성큼 방을 빠져나가 집을 나서서, 거리로, 어둠 속으로 나섰다! 그는 무언가에 홀린 사람처럼 걸으며 가슴을, 이틀 전 알료샤 앞에서, 마지막으로 그를 보았던 그 어두운 길 위에서 스스로 내리쳤던 바로 그 자리를 주먹으로 쳤다. 가슴팍 그 자리를 친 그 주먹질이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그가 그것으로 무엇을 말하려 했는지는 그때까지만 해도 이 세상 누구도 알지 못하는 비밀이었으니, 알료샤에게조차 털어놓지 않은 것이었다. 그러나 그 비밀은 그에게 치욕 이상의 것을, 곧 파멸과 자살을 뜻했다. 카테리나 이바노브나에게 진 빚을 갚아 줄 그 삼천 루블을 손에 넣지 못한다면, 그리하여 자기 가슴에서, 가슴팍 그 자리에서, 양심을 짓누르는 그 짊어진 수치를 떨쳐 내지 못한다면, 그렇게 하기로 그는 작정해 두었던 것이다. 이 모든 것은 나중에 독자에게 충분히 설명될 테지만, 지금 마지막 희망마저 사라지자, 겉보기에 그토록 건장하던 이 사내는 호흘라코프 댁에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다. 그는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지도 모른 채 걸으며 주먹으로 눈물을 훔쳤다. 그렇게 하여 그는 광장에 이르렀고, 문득 무언가에 부딪혔다는 것을 깨달았다. 그가 하마터면 쓰러뜨릴 뻔한 어느 노파의 째지는 듯한 비명이 들려왔다.\n\n\"이런 맙소사, 사람 잡을 뻔했네! 어디 눈을 어디다 두고 다니는 거야, 이 망나니야?\"\n\n\"아니, 당신이군!\" 미탸가 어둠 속에서 그 노파를 알아보고 외쳤다. 그녀는 삼소노프의 시중을 드는 그 늙은 하녀로, 미탸가 전날 유심히 눈여겨봐 둔 여자였다.\n\n\"그런데 댁은 뉘시오, 나리?\" 노파가 사뭇 다른 목소리로 물었다. \"어두워서 댁을 못 알아보겠구려.\"\n\n\"당신은 쿠즈마 쿠즈미치 댁에 살지요. 거기 하녀 아니오?\"\n\n\"그렇습죠, 나리, 잠깐 프로호리치 댁에 다녀오려던 참이었어요…… 그래도 영 댁을 못 알아보겠네요.\"\n\n\"여보시오, 말 좀 해 주시오, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 지금 거기 있소?\" 미탸가 초조함에 제정신이 아닌 채로 물었다. \"내가 좀 전에 그 댁까지 바래다주었는데 말이오.\"\n\n\"거기 와 계셨더랬죠, 나리. 잠깐 머무시더니 또 가셨어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 가 버렸다고?\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"언제 갔소?\"\n\n\"아니, 오시자마자요. 그저 잠깐 계셨을 뿐인걸요. 쿠즈마 쿠즈미치께 우스운 이야기 하나를 들려 드려서 그분을 웃기시고는, 곧장 달아나듯 가 버리셨어요.\"\n\n\"거짓말 마, 이 빌어먹을 할멈아!\" 미탸가 고함쳤다.\n\n\"아이고! 아이고!\" 노파가 비명을 질렀으나, 미탸는 이미 사라지고 없었다.\n\n그는 그루셴카가 사는 집으로 있는 힘껏 달려갔다. 그가 그곳에 다다른 바로 그 순간, 그루셴카는 모크로예로 가는 길이었다. 그녀가 떠난 지 채 십오 분도 지나지 않은 때였다.\n\n페냐는 부엌에서 자기 할머니인 늙은 식모 마트료나와 함께 앉아 있었는데, 그때 \"그 대위\"가 뛰어 들어왔다. 페냐는 그를 보고 째지는 듯한 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"비명을 지르다니?\" 미탸가 고함쳤다. \"그 여자는 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n그러나 공포에 질린 페냐가 한마디 내뱉을 틈도 주지 않고 그는 그녀의 발치에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"페냐, 제발 부탁이다, 말해 다오, 그 여자는 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"몰라요. 드미트리 표도로비치, 나리, 정말 몰라요. 절 죽이셔도 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\" 페냐는 맹세하며 항변했다. \"조금 전에 나리께서 직접 그분이랑 같이 나가셨잖아요—\"\n\n\"그 여자가 돌아왔어!\"\n\n\"정말 안 돌아오셨어요. 하느님께 맹세코 안 돌아오셨다니까요.\"\n\n\"거짓말 마!\" 미탸가 고함쳤다. \"네가 무서워하는 걸 보니 그 여자가 어디 있는지 알겠다.\"\n\n그는 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다. 페냐는 겁에 질린 채로 이렇게 쉽게 벗어난 것을 다행으로 여겼다. 그러나 그녀는, 그것이 다만 그가 그토록 서둘렀기 때문일 뿐, 그러지 않았더라면 자기가 이렇게 무사하지 못했을지도 모른다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그런데 그는 달려 나가면서 뜻밖의 행동으로 페냐와 늙은 마트료나를 둘 다 놀라게 했다. 탁자 위에는 절굿공이가 꽂힌 놋쇠 절구가 놓여 있었는데, 그것은 길이가 여섯 치 남짓밖에 안 되는 자그마한 놋쇠 절굿공이였다. 미탸는 한 손으로 이미 문을 연 채로, 다른 한 손으로 그 절굿공이를 낚아채어 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 저 양반이 누굴 죽이려나 봐!\" 페냐가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 외쳤다.\n\n# Chapter IV. In The Dark\n\n그는 어디로 달려가고 있었던가? \"그녀가 표도르 파블로비치의 집 말고 또 어디에 있겠는가? 그녀는 삼소노프의 집에서 곧장 그에게로 달려간 것이 틀림없다, 이제는 그것이 분명해졌다. 이 음모 전체가, 이 기만 전체가 환히 드러난 것이다.\" ... 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속에서 소용돌이치며 휘몰아쳤다. 그는 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 집으로 달려가지 않았다. \"그곳에 갈 필요는 없다 ... 조금도 그럴 필요가 없다 ... 소란을 피워서는 안 된다 ... 그러면 그들이 즉시 달려가 일러바칠 것이다.... 마리야 콘드라티예브나도 이 음모에 가담한 것이 분명하고, 스메르댜코프도, 그놈도, 모두가 매수당한 것이다!\"\n\n그는 다른 행동 계획을 세웠다. 표도르 파블로비치의 집을 멀찍이 빙 돌아, 골목을 가로질러 드미트롭스키 거리를 따라 내려간 다음 작은 다리를 건너, 그렇게 해서 집 뒤편의 인적 없는 좁은 길로 곧장 나왔는데, 그 길은 텅 비어 사람이 살지 않았으며, 한쪽에는 이웃집 채마밭의 섶나무 울타리가, 다른 한쪽에는 표도르 파블로비치의 정원을 빙 둘러 친 튼튼하고 높은 울타리가 서 있었다. 여기서 그는 한 자리를 골랐는데, 보아하니 바로 그 자리, 전해 내려오는 이야기에 따르면 리자베타가 언젠가 그곳을 타넘었다고 그가 알고 있던 바로 그 지점이었다. \"그 여자가 타넘을 수 있었다면,\" 어찌 된 영문인지 그런 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다, \"나라고 못 할 리 없지.\" 과연 그는 펄쩍 뛰어올라 단숨에 울타리 꼭대기를 붙잡는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 힘차게 몸을 끌어올려 그 위에 걸터앉았다. 바로 가까이, 정원 안에는 목욕간이 서 있었으나, 울타리 위에서는 집의 불 켜진 창문들도 보였다.\n\n\"그래, 노인의 침실에 불이 켜져 있다. 그 여자가 거기 있다!\" 그러고는 울타리에서 정원 안으로 뛰어내렸다. 그리고리가 앓고 있고 스메르댜코프 역시 십중팔구 그러하며, 자기 소리를 들을 사람이 아무도 없다는 것을 알면서도 그는 본능적으로 몸을 숨겨 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 그러나 사방은 죽은 듯이 고요했고, 마치 일부러 그런 듯이 완전한 정적이었으며, 한 점의 바람기도 없었다.\n\n\"속삭이는 듯한 정적밖에는 아무것도 없네.\" 어쩐 일인지 그 시구가 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. \"내가 울타리를 넘는 소리를 아무도 못 들었어야 할 텐데! 못 들었을 것이다.\" 잠시 가만히 서 있다가 그는 나무와 덤불을 피해 가며 정원 풀밭 위를 살그머니 걸었다. 그는 천천히 걸으며 한 걸음 한 걸음 살금살금 기듯이 나아갔고, 자기 발소리에 귀를 기울였다. 불 켜진 창문에 이르기까지 오 분이 걸렸다. 그는 그 창문 바로 아래에 딱총나무와 마가목의 무성하고 키 큰 덤불 몇 그루가 있었다는 것을 기억해 냈다. 집에서 정원으로 통하는 왼편의 문은 닫혀 있었으니, 그는 지나가면서 일부러 주의 깊게 살펴 두었던 것이다. 마침내 그는 덤불에 이르러 그 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다. 그는 숨을 죽였다. \"이제 기다려야 한다.\" 그는 생각했다. \"그들을 안심시키기 위해서, 혹여 그들이 내 발소리를 듣고 귀를 기울이고 있다면 말이다 ... 기침이나 재채기만 하지 않으면 된다.\"\n\n그는 이 분을 기다렸다. 심장이 격렬하게 뛰어, 순간순간 거의 숨을 쉴 수 없을 지경이었다. \"안 되겠다, 이 심장의 두근거림은 멎질 않는다.\" 그는 생각했다. \"더는 기다릴 수 없다.\" 그는 그늘 속 덤불 뒤에 서 있었다. 창문의 불빛이 덤불의 앞부분에 떨어졌다.\n\n\"마가목 열매가 어쩌면 저리도 붉을까!\" 그는 까닭도 모른 채 중얼거렸다. 소리도 없이 살그머니, 한 걸음 한 걸음 그는 창문에 다가가 발끝으로 몸을 일으켜 세웠다. 표도르 파블로비치의 침실 전체가 그의 눈앞에 활짝 드러났다. 그리 큰 방은 아니었고, 표도르 파블로비치가 늘 \"중국식\"이라고 부르던 붉은 칸막이로 둘로 나뉘어 있었다. \"중국식\"이라는 말이 미탸의 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다. \"그리고 저 칸막이 뒤에 그루셴카가 있다.\" 미탸는 생각했다. 그는 표도르 파블로비치를 살펴보기 시작했는데, 노인은 미탸가 한 번도 본 적 없는 새 줄무늬 비단 실내복을 입고, 허리에는 술 달린 비단 끈을 두르고 있었다. 깨끗하고 멋스러운 고운 아마포 셔츠가 금단추를 단 채 실내복 깃 아래로 살짝 내비쳤다. 표도르 파블로비치는 머리에 알료샤가 보았던 것과 똑같은 붉은 띠를 두르고 있었다.\n\n\"잔뜩 차려입었군.\" 미탸는 생각했다.\n\n그의 아버지는 창가에 서서 보아하니 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 별안간 그는 고개를 번쩍 쳐들고 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 탁자로 가서 유리병에서 브랜디를 반 잔쯤 따라 단숨에 들이켰다. 그러고는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 다시 잠시 가만히 서 있다가, 무심한 걸음으로 벽에 걸린 거울 앞으로 가더니 오른손으로 이마의 붉은 띠를 조금 들어 올리고는, 아직 사라지지 않은 멍과 상처를 살펴보기 시작했다.\n\n\"혼자다.\" 미탸는 생각했다. \"십중팔구 혼자다.\"\n\n표도르 파블로비치는 거울에서 물러나 별안간 창문 쪽으로 돌아서서 밖을 내다보았다. 미탸는 순식간에 그늘 속으로 몸을 숨겼다.\n\n\"그 여자가 어쩌면 저 칸막이 뒤에 있을지도 모른다. 어쩌면 벌써 잠들었는지도.\" 그는 가슴이 찔리는 듯한 아픔을 느끼며 생각했다. 표도르 파블로비치가 창가에서 물러났다. \"저자가 창밖으로 그 여자를 찾고 있다, 그러니 여기에는 없는 것이다. 왜 어둠 속을 저렇게 노려보겠는가? 초조함에 미칠 지경인 게다.\" ... 미탸는 곧바로 다시 다가가 창 안을 들여다보기 시작했다. 노인은 보아하니 실망한 듯 탁자에 앉아 있었다. 마침내 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 오른쪽 뺨을 손에 갖다 댔다. 미탸는 그를 열심히 지켜보았다.\n\n\"혼자다, 혼자야!\" 그는 다시 되뇌었다. \"그 여자가 여기 있다면 저자의 얼굴이 다를 것이다.\"\n\n이상한 말이지만, 그녀가 여기 없다는 사실에 묘하고도 까닭 없는 분함이 그의 가슴속에서 솟구쳤다. \"그 여자가 여기 없다는 게 문제가 아니다.\" 그는 곧장 스스로에게 변명했다. \"그 여자가 있는지 없는지 확실히 알 수 없다는 게 문제다.\" 미탸는 훗날 기억하기를, 그 순간 자기 정신이 유난히 또렷했으며, 사소한 세부까지 죄다 받아들여 한 가지도 놓치지 않았다고 했다. 그러나 비참한 감정이, 곧 불확실함과 망설임의 비참함이 매 순간 그의 가슴속에서 자라나고 있었다. \"그 여자가 여기 있는가, 없는가?\" 성난 의혹이 그의 가슴을 채웠고, 별안간 마음을 정하자 그는 손을 내밀어 살그머니 창틀을 두드렸다. 그는 노인이 스메르댜코프와 약속해 둔 신호를, 곧 천천히 두 번 두드린 다음 조금 더 빠르게 세 번 더 두드리는, \"그루셴카가 여기 왔다!\"는 뜻의 신호를 두드렸다.\n\n노인은 흠칫 놀라 고개를 번쩍 쳐들더니, 재빨리 벌떡 일어나 창문으로 달려왔다. 미탸는 그늘 속으로 몸을 숨겼다. 표도르 파블로비치는 창문을 열고 머리를 통째로 밖으로 내밀었다.\n\n\"그루셴카, 너냐? 너야?\" 그는 떨리는 듯한 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리로 말했다. \"어디 있느냐, 내 천사야, 어디 있어?\" 그는 무섭도록 흥분해 숨을 헐떡였다.\n\n\"혼자다.\" 미탸는 단정했다.\n\n\"어디 있느냐?\" 노인이 다시 외쳤다. 그러고는 머리를 더 멀리, 어깨까지 내밀고 좌우 사방을 두리번거렸다. \"이리 오너라, 너에게 줄 작은 선물이 있다. 오너라, 보여 주마....\"\n\n\"삼천 루블을 말하는 게로군.\" 미탸는 생각했다.\n\n\"한데 어디 있느냐? 문 앞에 있는 게냐? 곧 열어 주마.\"\n\n그러고는 노인은 정원으로 통하는 문이 있는 오른쪽을 내다보며 어둠 속을 살피려 들면서 거의 창밖으로 기어 나오다시피 했다. 한 순간만 더 지났더라면 그는 분명 그루셴카의 대답도 기다리지 않고 문을 열러 뛰쳐나갔을 것이다.\n\n미탸는 꼼짝도 않고 옆에서 그를 바라보았다. 그토록 진저리 치던 노인의 옆모습, 축 늘어진 울대뼈, 갈고리 같은 코, 탐욕스러운 기대에 미소 짓는 입술이, 방 안에서 왼쪽으로 비스듬히 떨어지는 등불에 밝게 비쳐 있었다. 무시무시한 증오의 격분이 별안간 미탸의 가슴속에서 치솟았다. \"저기 그가 있다, 자기의 연적, 자기를 괴롭히고 자기 인생을 망쳐 놓은 사내가!\" 그것은 나흘 전 정자에서 알료샤에게, 마치 이를 예감한 듯이 말했던 그 갑작스럽고 격렬한 복수의 분노가 솟구치는 것이었으니, 그때 알료샤가 \"어떻게 형이 아버지를 죽이겠다는 말을 할 수 있어요?\"라고 묻자 그는 이렇게 말했었다. \"나도 모른다, 나도 몰라.\" 그는 그때 그렇게 말했다. \"어쩌면 안 죽일지도 모르고, 어쩌면 죽일지도 모른다. 두려운 건 그 순간 그자가 갑자기 너무도 역겹게 느껴질까 봐 하는 거야. 나는 그자의 이중 턱이, 코가, 눈이, 그 뻔뻔스러운 히죽거림이 싫다. 나는 개인적인 혐오를 느낀다. 그게 두렵다, 그것이 나를 어쩌지 못하게 만들까 봐 두렵다.\" ... 이 개인적인 혐오가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 자라나고 있었다. 미탸는 제정신이 아니었고, 별안간 호주머니에서 놋쇠 절굿공이를 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"그때 하느님이 나를 지켜보고 계셨다.\" 미탸 자신이 훗날 말했다. 바로 그 순간 그리고리가 병석에서 잠이 깼다. 그날 저녁 일찍이 그는 스메르댜코프가 이반에게 묘사했던 그 치료를 받았던 것이다. 그는 어떤 비방의 아주 독한 달인 물을 섞은 보드카로 온몸을 문질렀고, 아내가 그의 머리맡에서 \"어떤 기도\"를 되뇌는 동안 남은 혼합액을 마신 다음 잠자리에 들었다. 마르파 이그나티예브나도 그 물을 맛보았는데, 독한 술에 익숙지 않은 터라 남편 곁에서 죽은 듯이 잠들었다.\n\n그러나 그리고리는 한밤중에 아주 갑작스레 잠이 깼고, 잠시 생각에 잠긴 끝에, 곧바로 등에 날카로운 통증을 느꼈음에도 침대에 일어나 앉았다. 그러고는 다시 한 번 곰곰이 따져 본 뒤 일어나 허둥지둥 옷을 입었다. 어쩌면 \"이런 위태로운 시절에\" 집을 지키는 이 없이 잠을 잤다는 생각에 양심이 편치 않았는지도 모른다. 발작에 지친 스메르댜코프는 옆방에 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다. 마르파 이그나티예브나도 미동도 없었다. \"그 물이 마누라에게는 너무 셌던 게로군.\" 그리고리는 아내를 흘긋 보며 생각했고, 신음 소리를 내며 현관 층계로 나갔다. 분명 그는 층계에서 밖을 내다볼 작정이었을 뿐이었으니, 등과 오른쪽 다리의 통증이 참을 수 없어 거의 걸을 수조차 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 그날 저녁 정원으로 통하는 작은 쪽문을 잠그지 않았다는 것이 별안간 생각났다. 그는 더없이 시간을 잘 지키고 빈틈없는 사람이었으며, 변치 않는 일과와 여러 해 이어 온 습관을 고수하는 사람이었다. 다리를 절며 통증에 몸을 뒤틀면서 그는 층계를 내려가 정원 쪽으로 향했다. 과연 쪽문은 활짝 열려 있었다. 그는 기계적으로 정원 안으로 발을 들여놓았다. 어쩌면 무언가가 눈에 어른거렸는지도, 어쩌면 무슨 소리를 들었는지도 모른다, 그리고 왼쪽을 흘긋 보니 주인의 창문이 열려 있는 것이 보였다. 그때 그 창문 밖을 내다보는 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n\"저 창문이 왜 열려 있지? 지금이 여름도 아닌데.\" 그리고리는 생각했는데, 별안간 바로 그 순간 정원 저 앞에서 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 그의 눈에 언뜻 들어왔다. 그의 앞 마흔 걸음쯤 되는 곳에서 한 사내가 어둠 속을 달려가는 것 같았고, 어떤 그림자가 아주 빠르게 움직이고 있었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그리고리는 제정신이 아닌 채로 외쳤고, 등의 통증도 잊은 채 달려가는 그림자를 가로막으려고 서둘렀다. 그는 지름길을 택했는데, 보아하니 정원을 더 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 달아나는 그림자는 목욕간 쪽으로 가서 그 뒤로 돌아 정원 울타리로 내달았다. 그리고리는 그를 시야에서 놓치지 않고 뒤쫓으며, 모든 것을 잊은 채 달렸다. 그는 사내가 울타리를 막 타넘던 바로 그 순간 울타리에 다다랐다. 그리고리는 제정신이 아닌 채로 소리치며 그에게 덤벼들어 두 손으로 그의 다리를 움켜잡았다.\n\n과연 그의 예감은 그를 속이지 않았다. 그는 그를 알아보았으니, 바로 그였다, 그 \"괴물\", 그 \"아비를 죽인 놈\"이었다.\n\n\"아비를 죽인 놈!\" 노인은 온 이웃이 들을 만큼 큰 소리로 외쳤으나, 더 외칠 겨를도 없이 마치 벼락에 맞은 듯 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말았다.\n\n미탸는 정원 안으로 도로 뛰어내려 쓰러진 사내 위로 몸을 굽혔다. 미탸의 손에는 놋쇠 절굿공이가 들려 있었는데, 그는 그것을 기계적으로 풀밭에 내던졌다. 절굿공이는 그리고리에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 풀밭이 아니라 길 위, 더없이 눈에 띄는 자리에 떨어졌다. 그는 몇 초 동안 자기 앞에 엎어진 형체를 살펴보았다. 노인의 머리는 피로 뒤덮여 있었다. 미탸는 손을 내밀어 그것을 만져 보기 시작했다. 그는 훗날 또렷이 기억하기를, 자기가 노인의 두개골을 깨뜨린 것인지 아니면 그저 절굿공이로 기절시킨 것인지 무섭도록 확인하고 싶어 했다고 했다. 그러나 피가 끔찍하게 흘러나왔고, 한순간 미탸의 손가락은 그 뜨거운 핏줄기에 흠뻑 젖었다. 그는 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아갈 때 챙겨 두었던 깨끗하고 흰 손수건을 호주머니에서 꺼내 노인의 머리에 갖다 대고는, 분별없이 그의 얼굴과 관자놀이에서 피를 닦아 내려 했던 것을 기억했다. 그러나 손수건은 순식간에 피에 흠뻑 젖었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 내가 무엇 때문에 이 짓을 하고 있나?\" 미탸는 별안간 정신을 차리며 생각했다. \"내가 그의 두개골을 깨뜨렸다 한들, 지금 그걸 어떻게 알아낸단 말인가? 게다가 이제 와서 무슨 차이가 있단 말인가?\" 그는 절망스럽게 덧붙였다. \"내가 그를 죽였다면, 죽인 것이다.... 영감, 당신은 화를 자초했으니, 거기 그렇게 누워 있어야지!\" 그는 소리 내어 말했다. 그러고는 별안간 울타리 쪽으로 돌아서 그것을 뛰어넘어 좁은 길로 내려서더니 달리기 시작했다 — 피에 젖은 손수건을 오른손 주먹에 꽉 움켜쥔 채로, 그리고 달리면서 그것을 외투 뒷주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다. 그는 정신없이 달렸고, 어둠 속 거리에서 그와 마주친 몇 안 되는 행인들은 훗날 그날 밤 한 사내가 달려가는 것을 보았다고 기억했다. 그는 다시 모로조바 미망인의 집으로 날듯이 되돌아갔다.\n\n그날 저녁 그가 그 집을 떠나자마자, 페냐는 수석 문지기 나자르 이바노비치에게 달려가, 제발 그리스도를 봐서라도 \"오늘이든 내일이든 그 대위를 다시 들여보내지 말아 달라\"고 간청했다. 나자르 이바노비치는 약속했으나, 별안간 그를 부른 여주인에게 가려고 위층으로 올라가다가, 도중에 최근에 시골에서 올라온 스무 살짜리 조카를 만나 자기 자리를 대신 맡으라고 일렀는데, \"그 대위\"에 관해 일러두는 것은 깜박 잊고 말았다. 미탸가 대문으로 달려와 두드렸다. 그 젊은이는 단번에 그를 알아보았으니, 미탸가 한두 번 그에게 행하를 주었기 때문이다. 그는 곧장 대문을 열어 그를 들여보내고는, 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 \"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나는 지금 댁에 안 계세요, 아시잖아요\" 하고 서둘러 알려 주었다.\n\n\"그럼 그 여자는 어디 있느냐, 프로호르?\" 미탸는 우뚝 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁에 길을 떠나셨어요, 두 시간쯤 전에 티모페이와 함께 모크로예로요.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에?\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그건 저도 모르겠습니다. 어떤 장교를 만나러요. 누군가가 그분을 초대했고 모시러 말까지 보내왔거든요.\"\n\n미탸는 그를 두고 미친 사람처럼 페냐에게로 달려갔다.\n\n# Chapter V. A Sudden Resolution\n\n그녀는 부엌에서 할머니와 함께 앉아 있었는데, 두 사람 다 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다. 나자르 이바노비치를 믿고서 그들은 문을 잠가 두지 않았다. 미탸가 뛰어 들어와 페냐에게 달려들더니 그녀의 목을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"당장 말해! 그 여자 어디 있어? 지금 누구하고 있어, 모크로예에서?\" 그는 미친 듯이 울부짖었다.\n\n두 여자가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"아이고! 말씀드릴게요. 아이고! 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 다 말씀드릴게요, 하나도 안 숨길게요.\" 페냐가 죽도록 겁에 질려 횡설수설했다. \"아씨는 모크로예로, 그 장교한테 가셨어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 장교야?\" 미탸가 고함을 질렀다.\n\n\"그 장교요, 예전에 알던 바로 그 사람, 오 년 전에 아씨를 버린 그 사람이요.\" 페냐는 입에서 나오는 대로 빠르게 종알거렸다.\n\n미탸는 그녀의 목을 조르던 두 손을 거두었다. 그는 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로 그녀를 마주 보고 서서 한마디도 내뱉지 못했으나, 그의 눈은 첫마디에서부터 모든 것을, 모든 것을 깨닫고 사태의 전모를 짐작했음을 드러내고 있었다. 가엾은 페냐는 그 순간 그가 알아들었는지 어떤지 살필 형편이 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 방으로 뛰어 들어왔을 때 그대로 궤짝 위에 앉은 채로 온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 마치 제 몸을 막으려는 듯이 두 손을 앞으로 내밀고 있었다. 그녀는 그 자세 그대로 굳어 버린 것 같았다. 휘둥그레지고 겁에 질린 두 눈은 그에게 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 게다가 사태를 더 끔찍하게 만든 것은, 그의 두 손이 온통 피로 얼룩져 있었다는 점이다. 달려오는 길에 그는 땀을 닦느라 그 손으로 이마를 만진 모양이어서, 이마와 오른쪽 뺨에 피로 물든 자국이 묻어 있었다. 페냐는 막 발작을 일으킬 지경이었다. 늙은 식모는 벌떡 일어나 미친 여자처럼 그를 노려보았는데, 공포로 거의 정신을 잃을 지경이었다.\n\n미탸는 한순간 서 있다가, 기계적으로 페냐 옆 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다. 그는 무엇을 곰곰이 생각하는 것이 아니라, 말하자면 공포에 사로잡혀 마비된 채로 앉아 있었다. 그러면서도 모든 것이 대낮처럼 분명했다. 그 장교, 그는 그자에 관해 알고 있었고, 죄다 완벽하게 알고 있었으며, 그루셴카 자신에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 한 달 전에 그에게서 편지가 왔다는 것까지 알고 있었던 것이다. 그러니까 한 달 동안, 꼬박 한 달 동안 이 일이 그에게는 비밀로 진행되어 왔던 것이다, 이 새로운 사내가 들이닥치는 바로 그 순간까지. 그런데도 그는 그자를 한 번도 떠올린 적이 없었다! 그러나 어떻게, 어떻게 그자를 떠올리지 못할 수 있었단 말인가? 어찌하여 그는 이 장교를 그렇게, 그자에 대해 듣자마자 그렇게 까맣게 잊어버렸던 것인가? 그것이 무슨 괴물처럼 그의 앞에 들이닥친 물음이었다. 그리고 그는 그 괴물 같은 것을 공포에 사로잡혀 바라보았고, 공포로 온몸이 싸늘하게 식어 갔다.\n\n그러나 별안간, 온순하고 다정한 어린아이처럼 부드럽고 상냥하게 그는 페냐에게 말을 걸기 시작했는데, 마치 방금 자신이 그녀를 겁주고 아프게 했던 것을 깡그리 잊은 듯했다. 그는 그의 처지로서는 놀라우리만치 극도로 정확하게 페냐에게 캐묻기 시작했고, 처녀는 그의 피투성이 손을 겁에 질려 바라보면서도 역시 놀랄 만큼 선뜻하고 빠르게 모든 물음에 대답했는데, 마치 진실을, 오로지 진실만을 그의 앞에 죄다 펼쳐 보이고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 차츰차츰 그녀는 어떤 즐거움마저 느끼며 시시콜콜한 사정을 하나하나 설명하기 시작했으니, 그를 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라 말하자면 그에게 최대한 도움이 되고 싶어 안달하는 것 같았다. 그녀는 그날 하루의 일을 아주 자세히, 라키틴과 알료샤가 찾아온 일, 자기 페냐가 어떻게 망을 보고 있었는지, 아씨가 어떻게 길을 떠났는지, 그리고 아씨가 어떻게 창밖으로 알료샤를 불러 미탸에게 안부를 전해 달라고, \"한 시간 동안 그를 사랑했던 것을 영원히 기억하라\"고 전해 달라 했는지를 늘어놓았다.\n\n그 전갈을 듣고 미탸는 별안간 빙긋 웃었고, 그의 창백한 뺨에 붉은 기운이 번졌다. 바로 그 순간 페냐는 이제 전혀 두려워하지 않고 호기심에 차서 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"손을 보세요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리. 온통 피투성이예요!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 미탸는 기계적으로 대답했다. 그는 무심히 자기 손을 들여다보다가 곧 그 손도, 페냐의 물음도 잊어버렸다.\n\n그는 다시 침묵 속으로 가라앉았다. 그가 뛰어 들어온 지 이십 분이 지나 있었다. 처음의 공포는 가셨으나, 분명 무슨 새로운 굳은 결심이 그를 사로잡고 있었다. 그는 별안간 일어서서 꿈꾸듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있으셨어요, 나리?\" 페냐가 다시 그의 손을 가리키며 말했다. 그녀는 가엾어하는 투로 말했는데, 마치 슬픔에 잠긴 그가 이제는 아주 가깝게 느껴지는 듯했다. 미탸는 다시 자기 손을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이건 피야, 페냐.\" 그는 야릇한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"이건 사람의 피인데, 아아, 하느님! 어쩌다 이게 흘려졌단 말인가? 하지만…… 페냐…… 여기 울타리가 하나 있는데\" (그는 그녀에게 수수께끼라도 내듯 그녀를 바라보았다) \"높다란 울타리라, 보기에도 무시무시한 거야. 그렇지만 내일 새벽, 해가 떠오르면 미탸는 그 울타리를 뛰어넘을 거야…… 무슨 울타리인지 너는 모를 거야, 페냐, 하지만 괜찮아…… 내일이면 듣고 알게 될 테니…… 그럼 이제 잘 있어. 나는 그 여자의 길을 막지 않겠어. 나는 옆으로 비켜설 거야, 나는 비켜설 줄 안다고. 잘 살아라, 내 기쁨이여…… 너는 한 시간 동안 나를 사랑했으니, 미텐카 카라마조프를 그렇게 영원히 기억해 줘…… 그 여자는 늘 나를 미텐카라고 불렀지, 기억나니?\"\n\n그렇게 말하고 그는 별안간 부엌을 나가 버렸다. 페냐는 그가 뛰어 들어와 자기에게 달려들었을 때보다도 이 느닷없는 떠남에 거의 더 겁을 먹었다.\n\n꼭 십 분 뒤에 드미트리는 표트르 일리치 페르호틴에게로 들어갔으니, 그는 미탸가 권총을 저당 잡혔던 그 젊은 관리였다. 어느덧 여덟 시 반이 되어 있었고, 표트르 일리치는 저녁 차를 마치고 막 다시 외투를 걸치고서 \"메트로폴리스\"로 당구를 치러 가려던 참이었다. 미탸는 막 나오는 그를 붙들었다.\n\n얼굴이 온통 피로 얼룩진 그를 보고 젊은 관리는 놀라 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이오! 어찌 된 거요?\"\n\n\"내 권총을 찾으러 왔소.\" 미탸가 말했다. \"그리고 돈을 가져왔소. 정말 고맙소. 내가 좀 급하오, 표트르 일리치, 부디 서둘러 주시오.\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 점점 더 놀랐다. 그는 문득 미탸의 손에 한 뭉치의 지폐가 쥐어져 있는 것을 알아챘고, 더구나 그는 아무도 그렇게 들어오지 않고 아무도 그렇게 돈을 들고 다니지 않는 모양새로 지폐를 쥐고 걸어 들어왔던 것이다. 그는 오른손에 지폐를 들고서, 마치 보여 주려는 듯 앞으로 쭉 내민 채였다. 통로에서 미탸를 맞은 페르호틴의 사환 아이는 나중에 말하기를, 그가 통로로 들어올 때도 그런 식으로, 돈을 손에 쭉 내민 채 걸어 들어왔다고 했으니, 그렇다면 그는 거리에서조차 그렇게 들고 다녔던 것이 분명하다. 죄다 무지갯빛이 도는 백 루블짜리 지폐였고, 그것을 쥔 손가락은 피로 뒤덮여 있었다.\n\n표트르 일리치는 나중에 그 액수에 관해 질문을 받았을 때, 한눈에 가늠하기는 어려웠지만 이천 루블쯤, 혹은 어쩌면 삼천 루블쯤 되었을 것이라고, 어쨌든 큼지막한 \"두툼한\" 뭉치였다고 말했다. \"드미트리 표도로비치는\" 하고 그는 나중에 증언했다. \"어딘지 평소의 그 사람 같지 않았습니다. 취한 것은 아니었으나 말하자면 들떠 있었고, 모든 것을 잊은 듯하면서도 동시에 무언가에 골똘히 빠진 듯, 마치 무언가를 곰곰 생각하며 찾고 있는데 결단을 내리지 못하는 것 같았습니다. 그는 몹시 서둘렀고, 무뚝뚝하면서도 아주 야릇하게 대답했으며, 어떤 순간에는 전혀 풀이 죽은 기색이 아니라 도리어 아주 쾌활해 보였습니다.\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 일이오? 무슨 일이 난 거요?\" 표트르 일리치가 손님을 놀란 눈으로 바라보며 외쳤다. \"어쩌다 온통 피투성이가 됐소? 넘어지기라도 했소? 자기 꼴 좀 보시오!\"\n\n그는 미탸의 팔꿈치를 잡아 거울 앞으로 이끌었다.\n\n피로 얼룩진 자기 얼굴을 보고 미탸는 흠칫하더니 노여운 듯 얼굴을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"제기랄! 이런 일까지.\" 그는 화가 나서 중얼거리며 오른손의 지폐를 황급히 왼손으로 옮기고는, 충동적으로 주머니에서 손수건을 홱 꺼냈다. 그러나 손수건도 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었으니 (그것은 그가 그리고리의 얼굴을 닦는 데 쓴 손수건이었다) 흰 자국이라곤 거의 남아 있지 않았고, 마르기 시작했을 뿐 아니라 구깃구깃한 덩어리로 빳빳하게 굳어 펼칠 수조차 없었다. 미탸는 화가 나서 그것을 바닥에 내던졌다.\n\n\"아, 빌어먹을!\" 그가 말했다. \"헝겊 쪼가리라도 없소…… 얼굴 좀 닦게?\"\n\n\"그러니까 다친 게 아니라 그냥 묻은 거로군. 씻는 게 낫겠소.\" 표트르 일리치가 말했다. \"여기 세면대가 있소. 내가 물을 부어 주리다.\"\n\n\"세면대? 그거 좋군…… 그런데 이건 어디다 두지?\"\n\n그는 더없이 야릇하게 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 가리켰고, 마치 자기, 미탸가 자기 돈을 어떻게 해야 할지 표트르 일리치더러 정해 달라는 듯 그를 미심쩍게 바라보았다.\n\n\"주머니에 넣든지, 아니면 여기 탁자 위에 두시오. 없어지지 않을 테니.\"\n\n\"주머니에? 그래, 주머니에. 좋아…… 그런데 말이오, 이건 다 부질없는 짓이지.\" 그는 별안간 골똘한 상념에서 깨어난 듯 외쳤다. \"이봐요, 먼저 그 권총 일부터 끝냅시다. 그걸 내게 돌려주시오. 여기 당신 돈이오…… 내가 그게 몹시 필요하거든…… 게다가 나는 잠시도, 단 한순간도 지체할 틈이 없소.\"\n\n그리고 뭉치에서 맨 위의 지폐를 빼서 표트르 일리치에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"하지만 내겐 거스를 잔돈이 모자랄 거요. 더 작은 건 없소?\"\n\n\"없소.\" 미탸가 다시 뭉치를 들여다보며 말했고, 마치 제 말이 미덥지 않은 듯 맨 위의 두세 장을 들춰 보았다.\n\n\"없군, 다 똑같은 거요.\" 그가 덧붙이며 다시 표트르 일리치를 미심쩍게 바라보았다.\n\n\"어쩌다 이렇게 부자가 됐소?\" 상대가 물었다. \"잠깐, 우리 집 아이를 플로트니코프 가게로 보내리다. 거긴 늦게까지 문을 여니까. 거스를 수 있는지 보지요. 미샤, 이리 와!\" 그가 통로 쪽으로 불렀다.\n\n\"플로트니코프 가게로! 그거 아주 좋군!\" 미탸가 무슨 생각에 퍼뜩 떠오른 듯 외쳤다. \"미샤,\" 그는 들어오는 아이에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이봐, 플로트니코프 가게로 달려가서, 드미트리 표도로비치가 안부를 전한다고, 곧 갈 거라고 일러라…… 그런데 잘 들어, 잘 들어, 샴페인을 준비해 두라고 해, 세 다스를, 내가 가기 전에 마련해서, 모크로예로 가져갈 때처럼 꾸려 놓으라고 해. 그때 나는 네 다스를 가져갔지.\" 그가 (별안간 표트르 일리치를 향해) 덧붙였다. \"그쪽이 다 알고 있으니, 너는 걱정 말아라, 미샤.\" 그는 다시 아이에게로 돌아섰다. \"가만, 들어 봐. 치즈하고, 스트라스부르 파이하고, 훈제 생선하고, 햄하고, 캐비아하고, 죄다, 가게에 있는 건 죄다 넣으라고 해, 백 루블어치, 아니 예전처럼 백이십 루블어치까지…… 가만, 후식도 잊지 말라고 해, 사탕이며 배며 수박이며, 두세 개, 아니 네 개—아니, 수박은 하나면 충분해, 그리고 초콜릿, 캔디, 토피, 봉봉, 하여간 예전에 모크로예로 가져간 건 죄다, 샴페인 합쳐서 삼백 루블어치로…… 꼭 그때와 똑같이 하라고 해. 그리고 미샤, 기억해 둬, 네 이름이 미샤라면 말이지—이 애 이름이 미샤 맞소?\" 그는 다시 표트르 일리치에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 표트르 일리치가 불안하게 그를 듣고 지켜보다가 끼어들었다. \"당신이 직접 가서 일러 주는 게 낫겠소. 이 애는 다 헷갈리게 할 테니.\"\n\n\"그러겠지, 그럴 거란 걸 알아! 어이, 미샤! 아니, 심부름을 시키려고 너한테 입이라도 맞추려던 참인데…… 틀리지만 않으면, 너한테 십 루블을 주마, 어서 달려가, 서둘러…… 샴페인이 제일 중요해, 샴페인을 올려 보내라고 해. 그리고 브랜디도, 적포도주와 백포도주도, 그때 내가 가져간 건 죄다…… 그쪽이 그때 뭘 가져갔는지 다 알고 있어.\"\n\n\"하지만 들어 보시오!\" 표트르 일리치가 약간 짜증스레 말을 끊었다. \"그러니까, 그냥 이 애더러 달려가서 돈을 거슬러 오고, 문 닫지 말라고만 이르게 합시다. 그리고 당신이 가서 일러 주시오…… 이 애한테 지폐를 주시오. 가거라, 미샤! 어서 발 빠르게!\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 일부러 미샤를 서둘러 내보내려는 듯했으니, 아이가 입과 눈을 휘둥그렇게 벌린 채 멍하니 서서 미탸의 분부를 도통 알아듣지 못하는 듯, 그의 피투성이 얼굴과 지폐를 쥔 피투성이의 떨리는 손가락을 놀라움과 공포에 차서 올려다보고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"자, 이제 와서 씻으시오.\" 표트르 일리치가 엄하게 말했다. \"돈은 탁자 위에 두든지 주머니에 넣든지 하고…… 그렇지, 이리 오시오. 그런데 외투를 벗으시오.\"\n\n그리고 그의 외투 벗는 것을 거들기 시작하다가 그는 또 외쳤다.\n\n\"이것 보시오, 외투에도 온통 피가 묻었잖소!\"\n\n\"그건…… 외투가 아니오. 그건 그냥 여기 소매에 조금…… 그리고 그것도 손수건이 닿았던 데뿐이오. 배어 나온 게 틀림없소. 페냐네 집에서 손수건 위에 앉았던 모양이라, 피가 배어 나온 거요.\" 미탸는 놀라우리만치 어린아이 같은 천진함으로 곧장 설명했다. 표트르 일리치는 눈살을 찌푸리며 들었다.\n\n\"그러니까 무슨 일을 저지른 게 분명하군. 누군가와 싸운 게 틀림없소.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n두 사람은 씻기 시작했다. 표트르 일리치가 물주전자를 들고 물을 부었다. 미탸는 죽도록 서두르며 손에 비누칠도 제대로 하지 않았다 (손이 떨리고 있었는데, 표트르 일리치는 나중에 그것을 기억해 냈다). 그러나 젊은 관리는 손에 비누칠을 꼼꼼히 하고 더 문지르라고 우겼다. 시간이 갈수록 그는 미탸에게 점점 더 영향력을 행사하는 듯했다. 그러고 보면 그는 다부진 성격의 젊은이였다는 점을 덧붙여 둘 만하다.\n\n\"이것 보시오, 손톱이 깨끗하지 않잖소. 이제 얼굴을 문지르시오. 자, 여기 관자놀이, 귀 옆에다…… 그 셔츠를 입고 갈 거요? 어디로 가는 거요? 보시오, 오른쪽 소맷부리가 온통 피투성이잖소.\"\n\n\"그래, 다 피투성이로군.\" 미탸가 셔츠 소맷부리를 들여다보며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그럼 셔츠를 갈아입으시오.\"\n\n\"그럴 시간이 없소. 그러니까 나는……\" 미탸는 여전히 그 미더운 천진함으로 말을 이으며 수건으로 얼굴과 손을 닦고 외투를 걸쳤다. \"소맷부리를 걷어 올리면 돼요. 외투 밑에 가려서 안 보일 테니…… 봐요!\"\n\n\"그러지 말고 이제 말해 보시오, 무슨 짓을 벌인 거요? 누군가와 싸웠소? 또 전처럼 술집에서? 또 그 대위를 두들겨 팼소?\" 표트르 일리치가 나무라듯 물었다. \"이번엔 누구를 두들겨 팬 거요…… 아니, 어쩌면 죽인 거요?\"\n\n\"헛소리!\" 미탸가 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 '헛소리'요?\"\n\n\"걱정 마시오.\" 미탸가 말하고는 별안간 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"내가 방금 시장 바닥에서 노파 하나를 박살 냈거든.\"\n\n\"박살을? 노파를?\"\n\n\"노인을!\" 미탸가 표트르 일리치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며, 웃으면서, 마치 그가 귀머거리라도 되는 양 그에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"빌어먹을! 노파다, 노인이다…… 누군가를 죽인 거요?\"\n\n\"우린 화해했소. 우린 다투었고—화해했소. 내가 아는 어떤 데서. 우린 친구로 헤어졌소. 바보 같은 자였지…… 그자는 날 용서했소…… 지금쯤은 틀림없이 날 용서했을 거요…… 일어났다면 날 용서하지 않았겠지만\"—미탸가 별안간 눈을 찡긋했다—\"하지만 빌어먹을, 알겠소, 이봐요, 표트르 일리치, 그자 따위는 집어치우시오! 그자 걱정은 마시오! 지금은 그러고 싶지 않으니까!\" 미탸가 단호하게 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"대체 무엇하러 아무하고나 시비를 거는 거요?…… 꼭 그 대위하고 그랬듯이, 별것도 아닌 일로 말이오…… 싸움질을 해 놓고 이제 또 술판을 벌이러 내달리다니—영락없는 당신답군! 샴페인 세 다스라니—그 많은 게 다 무슨 소용이오?\"\n\n\"좋았어! 자, 이제 권총을 주시오. 맹세코 지금은 시간이 없소. 자네하고 좀 이야기를 나누고 싶지만, 친구, 시간이 없소. 게다가 그럴 필요도 없지, 이야기하기엔 너무 늦었으니. 내 돈이 어디 있지? 어디다 뒀더라?\" 그가 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신이 탁자 위에 뒀잖소…… 직접…… 여기 있소. 잊었소? 당신한텐 돈이 흙이나 물 같은 모양이군. 자, 당신 권총이오. 참 묘한 일이군, 여섯 시엔 이걸 십 루블에 저당 잡히더니, 지금은 수천 루블이 생겼으니. 이삼천 루블은 되겠소.\"\n\n\"삼천이지, 틀림없이.\" 미탸가 웃으며 지폐를 바지 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다.\n\n\"그러다 잃어버리겠소. 금광이라도 찾은 거요?\"\n\n\"금광? 금광 말이오?\" 미탸가 목청껏 소리치더니 한바탕 큰 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"금광에 가고 싶소, 페르호틴? 여기 어떤 부인이 있는데, 당신이 가겠다고만 하면 당신한테 삼천을 척 내놓을 거요. 그 부인이 나한테 그랬거든, 금광을 어찌나 좋아하는지. 호흘라코바 부인을 아시오?\"\n\n\"알지는 못하지만, 소문은 들었고 본 적도 있소. 정말로 당신한테 삼천을 줬소? 정말로?\" 표트르 일리치가 미심쩍게 그를 훑어보며 말했다.\n\n\"내일 해가 떠오르면, 언제나 젊은 포이보스가 하느님을 찬미하고 영광 돌리며 솟아오르거든, 그때 그 부인한테, 그 호흘라코바 부인한테 가서 정말로 그 삼천을 척 내놓았는지 아닌지 물어보시오. 한번 알아보란 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신네가 어떤 사이인지 나야 모르오만…… 당신이 그렇게 딱 잘라 말하니, 정말로 줬나 보군. 돈을 손에 쥐었으면서 시베리아로 갈 생각은 않고 죄다 흥청망청 쓰려 하다니…… 지금 정말로 어디로 가는 거요, 응?\"\n\n\"모크로예로.\"\n\n\"모크로예로? 한밤중에?\"\n\n\"한때 사내는 다 가졌으나, 이제 사내는 빈털터리로다.\" 미탸가 별안간 외쳤다.\n\n\"어째서 '빈털터리'요? 그 수천 루블을 쥐고서 그런 소리를!\"\n\n\"수천 루블 얘기가 아니오. 수천 루블 따위 집어치워! 나는 여자의 마음을 말하는 거요.\n\n변덕스럽기는 여인의 마음, 믿을 수 없고 악덕으로 가득하니.\n\n나는 율리시스에게 동감이오. 바로 그가 한 말이지.\"\n\n\"무슨 소린지 모르겠소!\"\n\n\"내가 취했소?\"\n\n\"취하진 않았지만, 그보다 더하오.\"\n\n\"나는 마음이 취했소, 표트르 일리치, 마음이 취했지! 하지만 그쯤 하면 됐고!\"\n\n\"뭘 하는 거요, 권총에 총알을 재는 거요?\"\n\n\"권총에 총알을 재고 있소.\"\n\n권총집을 끄르고서 미탸는 정말로 화약통을 열어 조심스레 화약을 뿌려 넣고 다져 넣었다. 그런 다음 총알을 집어, 끼워 넣기 전에 두 손가락으로 촛불 앞에 들어 보였다.\n\n\"왜 총알을 들여다보는 거요?\" 표트르 일리치가 불안한 호기심으로 그를 지켜보며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그냥 변덕이오. 그게, 이 총알을 당신 머릿속에 박아 넣을 작정이라면, 들여다보겠소, 안 보겠소?\"\n\n\"왜 들여다본단 말이오?\"\n\n\"이게 내 머릿속으로 들어갈 거니까, 어떻게 생겼는지 들여다보고 알아 두는 게 흥미롭지 않겠소. 하지만 이런 건 바보짓이지, 한순간의 바보짓이오. 자, 이제 됐군.\" 그가 총알을 끼워 넣고 꽂을대로 다져 넣으며 덧붙였다. \"표트르 일리치, 이보게, 이건 부질없는 짓이오, 죄다 부질없지, 얼마나 부질없는지 당신이 알기만 한다면! 자, 이제 종이 한 장만 주시오.\"\n\n\"여기 종이가 있소.\"\n\n\"아니, 깨끗한 새것, 편지지로. 그래, 그거요.\"\n\n그리고 탁자에서 펜을 집어 미탸는 재빨리 두 줄을 적고, 종이를 네 겹으로 접어 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣었다. 그는 권총을 권총집에 넣고 잠근 다음, 그것을 손에 쥐었다. 그러고는 느릿하고 사색에 잠긴 미소로 표트르 일리치를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 갑시다.\"\n\n\"어디로 가는 거요? 아니, 잠깐만…… 혹시 그 총알을 당신 머릿속에 박을 생각인 거요?\" 표트르 일리치가 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"총알 얘기는 그냥 장난이었소! 나는 살고 싶소. 나는 삶을 사랑하오! 그건 믿어도 좋소. 나는 금발의 포이보스와 그 따스한 빛을 사랑하지…… 이보게, 표트르 일리치, 당신은 옆으로 비켜설 줄 아오?\"\n\n\"'옆으로 비켜선다'는 게 무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"길을 내주는 거요. 어떤 사랑스러운 존재를 위해, 그리고 내가 미워하는 자를 위해 길을 내주는 거지. 그리고 내가 미워하는 자가 사랑받게 만드는 것—그게 길을 내준다는 뜻이오! 그리고 그들에게 이렇게 말하는 거지. 하느님이 당신들을 축복하시기를, 당신들 길을 가시오, 지나가시오, 그동안 나는—\"\n\n\"그동안 당신은—?\"\n\n\"그쯤이면 됐소, 갑시다.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 누군가한테 일러서 당신이 거기 가는 걸 막게 하겠소.\" 표트르 일리치가 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"지금 모크로예엔 무엇하러 가는 거요?\"\n\n\"거기 한 여자가 있소, 한 여자가. 당신한텐 그거면 됐소. 입 다무시오.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 당신이 이런 야만인이긴 해도 나는 늘 당신을 좋아했소…… 마음이 놓이질 않는구려.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 친구. 내가 야만인이라고 했지. 야만인, 야만인이라! 그게 바로 내가 늘 하는 말이오. 야만인! 아니, 미샤가 왔군! 깜빡 잊고 있었네.\"\n\n미샤가 잔돈으로 바꾼 지폐를 한 움큼 쥐고 부랴부랴 뛰어 들어와, 플로트니코프네에선 다들 부산을 떨고 있다고 알렸다. \"병이며 생선이며 차를 들고 내려오고 있어요. 곧 다 준비될 거래요.\" 미탸는 십 루블을 집어 표트르 일리치에게 건네고, 또 다른 십 루블짜리 지폐를 미샤에게 던져 주었다.\n\n\"그런 짓은 하지 마시오!\" 표트르 일리치가 외쳤다. \"내 집에선 그런 짓 못 보겠소, 그건 나쁜, 못된 버릇을 들이는 짓이오. 돈을 거두시오. 자, 여기 두시오, 뭣하러 낭비하는 거요? 내일이면 요긴할 텐데, 또 내게 와서 십 루블만 빌려 달라고 할 게 뻔하지 않소. 어째서 자꾸 지폐를 옆주머니에 넣는 거요? 아, 그러다 잃어버리겠소!\"\n\n\"이보게, 친구, 함께 모크로예로 갑시다.\"\n\n\"내가 뭣하러 가겠소?\"\n\n\"이봐요, 당장 한 병 따서 삶을 위해 건배합시다! 나는 마시고 싶소, 특히 당신하고 마시고 싶소. 당신하고는 한 번도 마셔 본 적이 없잖소, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"좋소, '메트로폴리스'로 가면 되겠군. 마침 거기 가려던 참이었으니.\"\n\n\"그럴 시간은 없소. 플로트니코프네 뒷방에서 마십시다. 수수께끼 하나 내볼까?\"\n\n\"내보시오.\"\n\n미탸는 조끼 주머니에서 종이쪽지를 꺼내, 펼쳐서 보여 주었다. 큼지막하고 또렷한 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"나는 내 평생을 두고 나 자신을 벌하노라, 평생토록 나를 벌하노라!\"\n\n\"내 반드시 누군가한테 일러두겠소, 지금 당장 가겠소.\" 표트르 일리치가 종이를 읽고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 시간 없소, 친구, 와서 한잔합시다. 행진!\"\n\n플로트니코프 가게는 거리 모퉁이에, 표트르 일리치의 집에서 한 집 건너에 있었다. 그것은 우리 고장에서 제일 큰 식료품 가게로, 결코 변변찮은 가게가 아니었고, 어느 부유한 상인들의 소유였다. 그들은 페테르부르크 가게에서 구할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 갖춰 두었으니, 온갖 종류의 식료품, \"옐리세예프 형제가 병에 담은\" 포도주, 과일, 시가, 차, 커피, 설탕 따위였다. 점원이 셋, 심부름꾼 아이가 둘 늘 일하고 있었다. 우리 고장이 한결 가난해지고 지주들이 떠나가고 장사가 더 나빠지긴 했으나, 식료품 가게만은 예전처럼 번창하여 해마다 형편이 나아졌으니, 그들의 물건을 사려는 손님이 넘쳐났던 것이다.\n\n가게에서는 미탸를 애타게 기다리고 있었다. 그들은 그가 서너 주 전에 포도주며 온갖 물건을 수백 루블어치나 사서 현금으로 치렀던 일을 생생히 기억하고 있었다 (물론 그들이 그에게 외상으로 물건을 줄 리는 결코 없었다). 그들은 그때도 지금처럼 그가 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고서, 흥정도 하지 않고, 곰곰 따져 보지도, 그렇게 많은 포도주며 식료품이 자기에게 무슨 소용이 있을지 따져 볼 마음도 없이 마구 뿌려 댔던 것을 기억하고 있었다. 온 고을에 이런 이야기가 퍼져 있었으니, 그때 그루셴카와 함께 모크로예로 마차를 몰고 가서 \"하룻밤과 이튿날에 삼천 루블을 써 버리고, 술판에서 한 푼도 없이 돌아왔다\"는 것이었다. 그는 (그 무렵 우리 동네에 진을 치고 있던) 집시 한 떼를 통째로 불러들였는데, 그들은 그가 술에 취해 있는 이틀 동안 그에게서 아낌없이 돈을 뜯어내고 값비싼 포도주를 실컷 들이켰다. 사람들은 미탸를 비웃으며 이야기하곤 했으니, 그가 손이 새까만 농부들에게 샴페인을 먹이고 마을 아낙들과 처녀들에게 사탕이며 스트라스부르 파이로 잔치를 베풀었다는 것이었다. 미탸의 면전에서 그를 비웃는 것은 다소 위험한 일이긴 했으나, 등 뒤에서는, 특히 술집에서는 그를 두고 적잖은 웃음거리가 오갔으니, 이 \"탈선\"으로 그루셴카에게서 얻어 낸 것이라곤 \"그녀의 발에 입 맞추는 허락뿐이었고, 그것이 그녀가 허락한 전부였다\"고 그가 스스로 천진하게 사람들 앞에서 털어놓았기 때문이다.\n\n미탸와 표트르 일리치가 가게에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 방울을 단 채 세 마리 말이 나란히 매인 짐마차가, 마부 안드레이와 함께 입구에서 미탸를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다. 가게에서는 식료품 한 상자의 꾸리기를 거의 다 끝내고, 못질을 해서 짐마차에 실으려고 미탸가 오기만을 기다리고 있던 참이었다. 표트르 일리치는 어안이 벙벙했다.\n\n\"이 짐마차는 이렇게 급히 어디서 난 거요?\" 그가 미탸에게 물었다.\n\n\"당신한테 달려오다가 안드레이를 만나서, 곧장 여기 가게로 몰고 오라고 일렀소. 지체할 시간이 없으니까. 지난번엔 티모페이하고 갔는데, 티모페이는 지금 그 마녀하고 나보다 먼저 떠나 버렸소. 우리가 많이 늦을까, 안드레이?\"\n\n\"기껏해야 한 시간, 그것도 안 될지 모릅죠. 저쪽보다 한 시간 먼저 닿을까 말까 합니다. 제가 티모페이를 채비시켜 출발시켰는뎁쇼. 그자가 어떻게 갈지 제가 압죠. 그 사람들 속도가 우리만 하겠습니까요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리. 어떻게 그러겠어요? 한 시간 먼저는 못 닿을 겁니다요!\" 안드레이는 키 크고 빨간 머리에 중년인 마부로, 자락이 긴 외투를 입고 팔에는 카프탄을 걸친 채 신이 나서 대답했다.\n\n\"저쪽보다 한 시간만 뒤처진다면 보드카 값으로 오십 루블이다.\"\n\n\"제가 시간을 책임집죠, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리. 에헤, 한 시간은커녕 반 시간도 저쪽이 우리보다 앞서진 못할 겁니다요.\"\n\n미탸는 부산을 떨며 일들을 챙기긴 했으나, 분부를 야릇하게, 말하자면 두서없이 앞뒤도 맞지 않게 내렸다. 그는 한 문장을 시작해 놓고 끝을 잊어버렸다. 표트르 일리치는 거들고 나서지 않을 수 없게 되었다.\n\n\"사백 루블어치다, 사백 루블어치보다 적어선 안 돼, 꼭 그때처럼.\" 미탸가 명령했다. \"샴페인 네 다스, 한 병도 모자라선 안 돼.\"\n\n\"그 많은 게 다 무슨 소용이오? 뭣에 쓰려고? 잠깐!\" 표트르 일리치가 외쳤다. \"이 상자는 뭐요? 안에 뭐가 들었소? 설마 여기 사백 루블어치가 들어 있는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n설치기 좋아하는 점원들이 느물느물한 공손함으로 설명하기 시작하기를, 첫 번째 상자에는 샴페인 여섯 병과 안주며 사탕이며 토피 따위 \"꼭 있어야 할 물건들\"만 들어 있고, 주문하신 물건의 대부분은 지난번처럼 역시 세 마리 말이 끄는 특별한 짐마차에 꾸려서 전속력으로 보낼 터이니, 드미트리 표도로비치 본인보다 한 시간 이상 늦지 않게 도착할 거라고 했다.\n\n\"한 시간 이상 늦으면 안 돼! 한 시간 이상은 안 돼! 그리고 토피와 봉봉을 더 넣어. 거기 처녀들이 그걸 어찌나 좋아하는지.\" 미탸가 열을 올려 우겼다.\n\n\"봉봉이야 좋소. 하지만 샴페인 네 다스는 다 무슨 소용이오? 한 다스면 충분할 텐데.\" 표트르 일리치가 거의 화를 내며 말했다. 그는 흥정을 시작해 물건 명세서를 청하고는 좀처럼 수긍하려 들지 않았다. 그러나 그가 절약한 것은 고작 백 루블뿐이었다. 결국 삼백 루블어치만 보내기로 합의가 되었다.\n\n\"에이, 빌어먹을, 당신 마음대로 하시오!\" 표트르 일리치가 다시 생각해 보고 외쳤다. \"내가 알 바요? 당신 돈을 내버리든 말든, 어차피 거저 생긴 거니까.\"\n\n\"이리 오시오, 우리 절약가 양반, 이리, 화내지 마시오.\" 미탸가 그를 가게 뒤편 방으로 끌어들였다. \"여기서 곧 한 병 내줄 거요. 우리 맛 좀 봅시다. 에헤, 표트르 일리치, 나하고 같이 갑시다, 당신은 좋은 사람이거든, 내가 좋아하는 그런 부류요.\"\n\n미탸는 더러운 식탁보가 덮인 작은 탁자 앞 등나무 의자에 앉았다. 표트르 일리치는 맞은편에 앉았고, 이내 샴페인이 나왔으며, 신사분들께 굴이 어떠시냐는 권유가 따랐다. \"최상품 굴입니다, 마지막으로 들어온 거예요.\"\n\n\"굴은 집어치워. 나는 그런 거 안 먹소. 그리고 우린 아무것도 필요 없소.\" 표트르 일리치가 거의 화를 내며 외쳤다.\n\n\"굴 따위에 쓸 시간 없소.\" 미탸가 말했다. \"게다가 나는 배고프지 않소. 이보게, 친구,\" 그가 별안간 감정을 실어 말했다. \"나는 이 모든 무질서가 한 번도 마음에 든 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"누군들 그게 마음에 들겠소? 농부들한테 샴페인 세 다스라니, 정말이지 누구라도 화가 날 노릇이오!\"\n\n\"그런 뜻이 아니오. 나는 더 높은 질서를 말하는 거요. 내 안엔 질서가 없소, 더 높은 질서가 없지. 하지만…… 그것도 다 끝났소. 슬퍼할 것 없지. 너무 늦었소, 빌어먹을! 내 평생이 무질서였으니, 이제 그걸 질서 잡아야지. 이게 말장난이오, 응?\"\n\n\"말장난이 아니라 헛소리를 지껄이는 거요!\"\n\n\"하늘에 계신 하느님께 영광을, 내 안에 계신 하느님께 영광을……\n\n이 시구는 언젠가 내 가슴에서 우러나온 거요, 시가 아니라 눈물이지…… 내가 직접 지었소…… 대위의 수염을 잡아 뜯던 때는 아니었지만……\"\n\n\"왜 그자를 이렇게 느닷없이 끌어들이는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 왜 그자를 끌어들이냐고? 부질없는 짓이지! 만물은 끝이 있고, 만물은 똑같아지는 법이오. 그게 다요, 한마디로.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 나는 자꾸 당신 권총이 마음에 걸리오.\"\n\n\"그것도 다 부질없는 짓이오! 마시기나 하시오, 공상에 빠지지 말고. 나는 삶을 사랑하오. 나는 삶을 너무 사랑했지, 부끄러우리만치 많이. 그만하면 됐소! 삶을 위해 마십시다, 친구, 내가 건배를 제의하리다. 내가 왜 나 자신이 흡족하냐고? 나는 비열한 놈이지만, 나 자신에게 만족하오. 그러면서도 내가 비열한 놈이라는 생각에, 그런데도 나 자신에게 만족한다는 생각에 괴로워하지. 나는 이 창조를 축복하오. 나는 당장이라도 하느님과 그분의 창조를 축복할 준비가 되어 있소, 하지만…… 나는 해로운 벌레 한 마리를 죽여야만 하오, 그게 기어다니며 남들의 삶을 망칠까 봐서…… 삶을 위해 마십시다, 친애하는 형제여. 삶보다 더 귀한 것이 어디 있겠소? 아무것도 없지! 삶을 위해, 그리고 여왕 중의 여왕을 위해!\"\n\n\"삶을 위해, 그리고 당신이 원한다면 당신의 여왕을 위해서도 마십시다.\"\n\n두 사람은 각자 한 잔씩 마셨다. 미탸는 흥분하여 들떠 있었으나, 한편으로 우울하기도 했다. 마치 무슨 무겁고 짓누르는 듯한 불안이 그를 짓누르고 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"미샤…… 너희 미샤가 왔구나! 미샤, 이리 오너라, 얘야, 내일 아침의 금발 포이보스를 위해 이 잔을 마셔라……\"\n\n\"왜 그 애한테 그걸 주는 거요?\" 표트르 일리치가 짜증스레 외쳤다.\n\n\"괜찮아요, 괜찮아, 괜찮으니 그냥 두시오! 내가 그러고 싶소!\"\n\n\"에—이!\"\n\n미샤는 잔을 비우고 절을 하더니 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"이 애는 나중에 이걸 기억할 거요.\" 미탸가 말했다. \"여자, 나는 여자를 사랑하오! 여자란 무엇이오? 창조의 여왕이지! 내 마음은 슬프오, 내 마음은 슬프오, 표트르 일리치. 햄릿을 기억하시오? '나는 참으로 가슴이 아프다, 착한 호레이쇼여! 아아, 가엾은 요릭!' 어쩌면 그게 나, 요릭이 아닐까? 그래, 나는 지금은 요릭이고, 나중엔 해골이 되겠지.\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 잠자코 들었다. 미탸도 한동안 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"여기 있는 저 개는 무슨 개요?\" 그가 구석에 앉아 있는, 눈이 까만 예쁘장한 작은 발바리를 알아채고 점원에게 무심히 물었다.\n\n\"바르바라 알렉세예브나 마님 거예요.\" 점원이 대답했다. \"그분이 데려왔다가 여기 두고 가셨어요. 마님께 도로 갖다 드려야지요.\"\n\n\"군대에서…… 저런 걸 한 마리 봤지……\" 미탸가 꿈꾸듯 중얼거렸다. \"그놈은 뒷다리가 부러져 있었지만…… 그건 그렇고, 표트르 일리치, 당신한테 물어보고 싶은 게 있소. 당신은 평생 무언가를 훔쳐 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"별 질문을 다 하는군!\"\n\n\"아, 뭐 별 뜻은 없소. 누군가의 주머니에서 말이오. 관청 돈을 말하는 게 아니오, 그건 다들 훔치는 거고, 당신도 분명 그러겠지……\"\n\n\"꺼지시오.\"\n\n\"나는 남의 돈을 말하는 거요. 주머니에서 곧장 훔치는 것 말이오. 지갑에서, 응?\"\n\n\"아홉 살 때 어머니한테서 이십 코페이카를 훔친 적이 있소. 몰래 탁자에서 집어 손에 꽉 쥐었지.\"\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 됐소?\"\n\n\"아, 아무것도. 사흘 동안 가지고 있다가, 부끄러워져서 고백하고 돌려줬소.\"\n\n\"그래서 그다음엔?\"\n\n\"당연히 매를 맞았지. 그런데 왜 묻는 거요? 당신이 뭘 훔쳤소?\"\n\n\"훔쳤지.\" 미탸가 능청스레 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 훔쳤소?\" 표트르 일리치가 궁금해서 물었다.\n\n\"아홉 살 때 어머니한테서 이십 코페이카를 훔쳤다가, 사흘 뒤에 돌려줬지.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 미탸는 별안간 일어섰다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 이제 안 오십니까요?\" 안드레이가 가게 문간에서 불렀다.\n\n\"준비됐나? 가자!\" 미탸가 흠칫했다. \"마지막으로 몇 마디만 더 하고—안드레이, 출발하기 전에 보드카 한 잔. 브랜디도 좀 줘라! 저 상자는\" (권총이 든 상자였다) \"내 자리 밑에 넣어 둬. 잘 있으시오, 표트르 일리치, 나를 나쁘게 기억하지 마시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 내일은 돌아오는 거요?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\"\n\n\"이 자그마한 계산은 지금 치러 주시겠습니까?\" 점원이 앞으로 튀어나오며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 그래, 계산. 물론이지.\"\n\n그는 다시 주머니에서 지폐 뭉치를 꺼내 삼백 루블을 골라내 계산대 위에 던지고는 허둥지둥 가게를 뛰쳐나갔다. 모두가 머리를 조아리고 행운을 빌며 그를 뒤따라 나왔다. 안드레이는 방금 들이켠 브랜디에 기침을 하며 마부석에 뛰어 올라탔다. 그러나 미탸가 막 자리에 앉으려는 순간, 뜻밖에도 그는 자기 앞에 페냐가 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며 달려와, 그의 앞에서 두 손을 맞잡고 부르짖더니, 그의 발치에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 친절하신 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 제발 우리 아씨를 해치지 마세요. 그리고 그걸 다 일러바친 건 저예요…… 그리고 그분을 죽이지 마세요, 그분이 먼저였고, 아씨는 그분 거예요! 그분은 이제 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 결혼할 거예요. 그래서 시베리아에서 돌아온 거라고요. 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 제발, 사람의 목숨을 빼앗지 마세요!\"\n\n\"쯧—쯧—쯧! 그렇게 된 거로군, 그래? 그러니까 거기로 사달을 내러 가는 거였군!\" 표트르 일리치가 중얼거렸다. \"이제 다 분명해졌소, 대낮처럼 분명하군. 드미트리 표도로비치, 사내답게 처신할 생각이라면 당장 그 권총을 내게 주시오.\" 그가 미탸에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"들리오, 드미트리?\"\n\n\"권총? 잠깐만, 친구, 가는 길에 웅덩이에 던져 버릴 거요.\" 미탸가 대답했다. \"페냐, 일어나라, 내게 무릎 꿇지 마라. 미탸는 아무도 해치지 않아, 이 어리석은 바보는 다시는 아무도 해치지 않을 거다. 하지만 이봐, 페냐,\" 그가 자리에 앉고 나서 외쳤다. \"내가 방금 너를 아프게 했으니, 나를 용서하고 가엾이 여겨 다오, 비열한 놈을 용서해 다오…… 하지만 용서하지 않아도 괜찮아. 이제 다 마찬가지니까. 자 그럼, 안드레이, 정신 차리고, 전속력으로 날려라!\"\n\n안드레이가 말을 채찍질하자, 방울이 울리기 시작했다.\n\n\"잘 있으시오, 표트르 일리치! 내 마지막 눈물은 당신을 위한 거요!……\"\n\n\"취하진 않았는데, 미친놈처럼 자꾸 횡설수설하는군.\" 표트르 일리치는 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보며 생각했다. 그는 남아서 남은 포도주며 식료품을 짐마차에 꾸리는 것을 지켜볼까 하는 생각이 반쯤 들었으니, 그들이 미탸를 속여 등쳐 먹으리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 별안간 자기 자신에게 짜증이 나서, 그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 돌아서서 당구를 치러 술집으로 갔다.\n\n\"좋은 녀석이긴 하지만 바보야.\" 그는 걸어가며 중얼거렸다. \"그 장교, 그루셴카의 옛 정인 얘기는 들은 적이 있지. 그래, 그자가 정말로 나타났다면…… 에이, 그 권총! 빌어먹을! 내가 그자의 보모도 아니고! 멋대로 하라지! 게다가 다 아무 일 없이 끝날 거야. 그자들은 그저 한 무리의 싸움꾼들일 뿐이지. 마시고 싸우고, 싸우고 또 화해하겠지. 진짜로 무슨 일을 저지를 위인들이 아니야. '나는 비켜서겠소, 나 자신을 벌하겠소'라니 그게 무슨 소리야? 다 아무 일 없이 끝날 거라고! 그런 문구야 술집에서 취해서 천 번도 더 외쳐 댄 거지. 하지만 지금은 취하지도 않았어. '마음이 취했다'—그자들은 그럴싸한 문구를 좋아하지, 그 악당들은. 내가 그자의 보모야? 분명 싸움질을 한 거야, 얼굴이 온통 피투성이였으니. 누구하고? '메트로폴리스'에서 알아봐야지. 그리고 그자의 손수건은 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었어…… 아직도 내 집 바닥에 떨어져 있겠군…… 에이, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n그는 언짢은 기분으로 술집에 다다라 곧 한판 어울렸다. 게임은 그의 기분을 풀어 주었다. 그는 두 번째 판을 치다가, 별안간 같이 치는 사람 가운데 하나에게 드미트리 카라마조프가 또 큰돈이—삼천 루블쯤 되는 돈이 생겨서, 그루셴카와 함께 그것을 쓰러 또 모크로예로 갔다고 이야기하기 시작했다…… 이 소식은 듣는 이들에게 유난한 흥미를 불러일으켰다. 그들은 모두 그 이야기를 했는데, 웃지도 않고 야릇하게 진지했다. 그들은 게임을 멈추었다.\n\n\"삼천이라고? 한데 그자가 어디서 삼천을 구했단 말이야?\"\n\n질문이 쏟아졌다. 호흘라코바 부인이 선물했다는 이야기는 미심쩍게 받아들여졌다.\n\n\"제 늙은 아비를 털어먹은 거 아냐?—그게 문제지.\"\n\n\"삼천이라! 뭔가 수상쩍은데.\"\n\n\"그자는 제 아비를 죽이겠다고 큰소리쳤지. 우리 모두 들었잖아, 여기서. 그리고 그자가 말한 게 삼천이었어……\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 듣고 있었다. 그는 별안간 대답이 짤막하고 무뚝뚝해졌다. 그는 미탸의 얼굴과 손에 묻은 피에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 처음엔 그 이야기를 할 작정이었지만 말이다.\n\n그들은 세 번째 판을 시작했고, 미탸에 관한 이야기는 차츰 잦아들었다. 그러나 세 번째 판이 끝날 무렵, 표트르 일리치는 더는 당구 칠 마음이 없었다. 그는 큐를 내려놓고, 작정했던 저녁 식사도 하지 않은 채 술집을 걸어 나왔다. 시장에 다다랐을 때 그는 어쩔 줄 몰라 멈춰 서서, 스스로 의아해했다. 그는 자기가 원하는 것이 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 가서 거기서 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아보는 것임을 깨달았다. \"어차피 뻔히 그렇게 드러날 무슨 어리석은 헛소리 때문에—내가 온 집안을 깨워서 소동을 벌인단 말인가? 흥! 빌어먹을, 그자들을 돌보는 게 내 일이야?\"\n\n몹시 언짢은 기분으로 그는 곧장 집으로 향하다가, 별안간 페냐를 떠올렸다. \"에잇, 빌어먹을! 아까 그 여자한테 캐물었어야 했는데.\" 그는 분이 나서 생각했다. \"그랬으면 다 들었을 텐데.\" 그리고 그녀에게 말을 걸어 알아보고 싶은 욕구가 어찌나 절박하고 끈질기게 밀려오는지, 집으로 가는 길 절반쯤에서 그는 홱 돌아서서 그루셴카가 묵고 있는 집 쪽으로 향했다. 대문 앞으로 다가가 그는 문을 두드렸다. 밤의 정적 속에 울리는 그 노크 소리에 그는 정신이 들면서 짜증이 났다. 그리고 아무도 대답하지 않았으니, 집안 사람들이 모두 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"내가 또 야단법석을 떠는구나!\" 그는 영락없이 거북한 기분으로 생각했다. 그러나 아주 가 버리는 대신, 그는 다시 온 힘을 다해 문을 두드리기 시작해, 거리를 떠들썩하게 메웠다.\n\n\"안 나와? 좋아, 내가 깨워 주지, 깨워 주고말고!\" 그는 두드릴 때마다 자기 자신에게 화를 내며 중얼거렸으나, 그러면서도 대문을 두드리는 손길을 더욱 거세게 했다.\n\n# Chapter VI. “I Am Coming, Too!”\n\n그러나 드미트리 표도로비치는 길 위를 쏜살같이 달리고 있었다. 모크로예까지는 이십 베르스타 남짓이었으나, 안드레이의 세 마리 말은 워낙 빠른 속도로 내달려 그 거리는 한 시간 십오 분이면 닿을 수 있었다. 빠른 움직임이 미탸를 되살아나게 했다. 공기는 신선하고 서늘했으며, 하늘에는 커다란 별들이 빛나고 있었다. 바로 그날 밤, 어쩌면 바로 그 시각이었으니, 알료샤가 땅 위로 쓰러져 황홀경 속에서 영원토록 이 땅을 사랑하리라 맹세하던 그때였다.\n\n* 베르스타(верста): 제정 러시아의 거리 단위로 약 1.067킬로미터.\n미탸의 영혼은 온통 혼란, 또 혼란이었으나, 비록 많은 것들이 그의 가슴을 찌르고 있었어도, 그 순간 그의 온 존재는 그녀를, 자신의 여왕을 향해 사무치게 갈망하고 있었으니, 마지막으로 한 번 그녀를 보려고 그곳으로 날아가고 있는 것이었다. 한 가지만은 단언할 수 있다. 그의 가슴은 한순간도 흔들리지 않았다. 어쩌면 사람들은 믿지 않을지도 모르나, 이 질투 많은 연인은 땅에서 솟아난 듯한 이 새로운 연적에게 추호의 질투도 느끼지 않았던 것이다. 만약 다른 누군가가 무대 위에 나타났더라면 그는 당장 질투에 사로잡혔을 것이고, 어쩌면 그 사나운 두 손을 또다시 피로 물들였을지도 모른다. 그러나 밤을 가르며 날아가는 동안, 그는 그녀의 첫사랑이었던 그 사내에게 선망도, 적의조차도 느끼지 않았다…… 하기야 그는 아직 그자를 본 적이 없기는 했다.\n\n\"여기에는 다툴 여지가 없다. 그것은 그녀의 권리요, 그의 권리다. 이것은 그녀의 첫사랑이고, 오 년이 지나도록 그녀가 잊지 못한 사랑이다. 그러니 그녀는 그 오 년 동안 오직 그자만을 사랑한 것이고, 나는, 나는 대체 무슨 자격으로 끼어든단 말인가? 무슨 권리가 있단 말인가? 비켜라, 미탸, 길을 내주어라! 이제 나는 무엇인가? 이제 저 장교만 제외하면 모든 것이 끝났다. 설령 그자가 나타나지 않았더라도 모든 것은 끝났을 것이다……\"\n\n이 말들은 만약 그가 이치를 따질 줄 아는 상태였더라면 그의 심정을 대강 표현했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 순간 이치를 따질 수 없었다. 지금 그가 하려는 행동의 계획은 따짐 없이 솟아난 것이었다. 페냐의 첫마디에서 그것은 감정으로부터 솟구쳐 나와, 그 모든 결과와 함께 순식간에 채택되었다. 그러나 그 결심에도 불구하고 그의 영혼 안에는 혼란이, 괴로움에 찬 혼란이 있었으니, 그 결심은 그에게 평안을 주지 못했다. 그를 괴롭히는 것이 그 뒤에 너무도 많이 도사리고 있었다. 그리고 펜과 종이로 자신의 사형 선고문을 직접 써 두었다는 생각이 이따금 그에게 기이하게 다가왔다. \"나는 나 자신을 벌하노라\"라고 쓴 그 종이는 그의 주머니 속에 준비된 채 들어 있었고, 권총은 장전되어 있었으며, 그는 이미 다음 날 아침 \"황금빛 머리칼의 포이보스\"의 첫 따스한 빛살을 어떻게 맞이할 것인지 결심해 두고 있었다.\n\n* 포이보스(Phœbus): 그리스 신화에서 태양신 아폴론을 일컫는 별칭.\n그럼에도 그는 과거에서, 자신이 뒤에 남겨 두고 온 모든 것, 자신을 괴롭히는 모든 것에서 벗어날 수 없었다. 그는 그것을 비참하게 느꼈고, 그 생각은 절망과 함께 그의 가슴속으로 가라앉았다. 한순간 그는 안드레이를 멈춰 세우고 마차에서 뛰어내려, 장전된 권총을 뽑아 들고 새벽을 기다릴 것도 없이 모든 것을 끝장내고 싶은 충동을 느꼈다. 그러나 그 순간은 불꽃처럼 스쳐 날아가 버렸다. 말들은 \"공간을 집어삼키며\" 줄곧 내달렸고, 목적지에 가까워질수록 그녀에 대한 생각이, 오직 그녀만에 대한 생각이 점점 더 완전하게 그의 영혼을 사로잡아, 그것을 떠나지 않고 맴돌던 무시무시한 형상들을 쫓아내었다. 오, 그는 단 한순간만이라도, 단지 멀리서나마 그녀를 바라보기를 얼마나 갈망했던가!\n\n\"그녀는 지금 그자와 함께 있다.\" 그는 생각했다. \"이제 나는 그녀가 그자와, 그 첫사랑과 함께 있을 때 어떤 모습인지 보게 될 것이다. 내가 바라는 건 그것뿐이다.\" 그의 생애에서 그토록 운명적인 영향을 끼친 이 여자가 그의 가슴속에 이런 사랑을, 이토록 새롭고 알 수 없는 감정을, 그 자신에게조차 놀라운, 경건에 이르도록 다정하고 그녀 앞에서 스스로를 지워 버리고 싶을 만큼 헌신적인 감정을 불러일으킨 적은 일찍이 없었다. \"나는 나를 지워 버리겠다!\" 그는 거의 발작에 가까운 환희에 휩싸여 말했다.\n\n그들은 거의 한 시간을 내달렸다. 미탸는 말이 없었고, 안드레이는 본래 수다스러운 농사꾼이었으나 그 역시 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그는 말하기를 두려워하는 듯, 다만 비쩍 마르긴 했어도 혈기 넘치는 세 마리 적갈색 말에게 야무지게 채찍질만 가했다. 그러다 별안간 미탸가 끔찍한 불안에 사로잡혀 소리쳤다.\n\n\"안드레이! 만약 그들이 잠들어 있으면 어쩌지?\"\n\n이 생각이 한 대 얻어맞은 것처럼 그에게 떨어졌다. 그전에는 미처 떠오르지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"지금쯤이면 잠자리에 들었을 법도 합니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리.\"\n\n미탸는 고통스러운 듯 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 그렇다, 정말이지…… 자신은 이런 심정으로 이렇게 그곳으로 달려가고 있는데…… 그들은 잠들어 있다니…… 어쩌면 그녀도 그곳에서 잠들어 있을지 모른다…… 분노의 감정이 그의 가슴속에서 치솟았다.\n\n\"몰아라, 안드레이! 채찍질해! 정신 차려!\" 그는 제정신이 아닌 채 소리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 잠자리에 안 들었을지도 모르지요!\" 안드레이가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말을 이었다. \"티모페이 말로는 거기 사람들이 한 무리 와 있다고 하던뎁쇼—\"\n\n\"역참에?\"\n\n\"역참이 아니라, 플라스투노프네 여인숙에 말입니다요, 거기서 말도 빌려주는뎁쇼.\"\n\n\"안다. 그러니까 사람들이 많다는 거냐? 어찌 된 일이야? 누구누구냐?\" 미탸가 이 뜻밖의 소식에 몹시 당황하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 티모페이 말로는 죄다 신사 양반들이라던뎁쇼. 우리 고장 사람 둘은—누군지는 저도 모르겠습니다요—그리고 다른 둘은 외지 사람이고, 어쩌면 더 있을지도 모르지요. 자세히는 안 물어봤습니다요. 카드놀이를 시작했다고 티모페이가 그러더군요.\"\n\n\"카드놀이?\"\n\n\"그러니 카드놀이를 하고 있다면야 잠자리에 안 들었을 겁니다요. 아직 열한 시도 안 됐을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"빨리, 안드레이! 더 빨리!\" 미탸가 또다시 신경질적으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"한 가지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까요, 나리?\" 안드레이가 잠시 뒤에 말했다. \"다만 나리를 노엽게 할까 봐 겁이 납니다만요.\"\n\n\"무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 페냐가 아까 나리 발치에 몸을 던지며 자기 여주인을 해치지 말아 달라고, 또 다른 누군가도 해치지 말아 달라고 빌지 않았습니까요…… 그러니 보십쇼, 나리— 그곳으로 나리를 모시고 가는 건 다름 아닌 접니다요…… 용서하십쇼, 나리, 양심에 걸려서요…… 이런 말씀을 드리는 게 어리석은 짓일지도 모릅니다만—\"\n\n미탸가 별안간 뒤에서 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"네가 마부냐?\" 그가 미친 듯이 물었다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 길을 비켜 줘야 한다는 걸 알 테지. 누구에게도 길을 비켜 주지 않고 그저 마차를 몰아 사람을 깔아뭉개는 마부를 두고 뭐라 하겠느냐? 안 되지, 마부가 사람을 치고 지나가서는 안 되는 법이다. 사람을 깔아뭉개서는 안 돼. 사람의 인생을 망쳐서는 안 된다. 그리고 만약 네가 한 인생을 망쳤다면—네 자신을 벌하거라…… 만약 네가 누군가의 인생을 망쳤다면, 파멸시켰다면—네 자신을 벌하고 떠나가거라.\"\n\n이 말들이 거의 발작하듯 미탸에게서 터져 나왔다. 안드레이는 그에게 놀랐으면서도 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 지당하신 말씀입니다요, 사람을 깔아뭉개거나 괴롭혀서는 안 되지요, 어떤 생물이든 마찬가집니다요, 모든 생물은 하느님이 지으신 것이니까요. 말을 예로 들어도 그렇습니다요, 어떤 사람들은, 우리 마부들 중에서도, 막무가내로 몰아댄단 말입니다요. 무엇으로도 막을 수가 없지요, 그냥 억지로 몰고 가니까요.\"\n\n\"지옥으로 말이냐?\" 미탸가 말을 가로채더니 특유의 짧고 퉁명스러운 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"안드레이, 순박한 사람아.\" 그는 다시 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다. \"말해 보아라, 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프는 지옥에 갈까, 안 갈까, 네 생각은 어떠냐?\"\n\n\"저야 모르지요, 나리, 그건 나리께 달린 거니까요, 나리께서는…… 보십쇼, 나리, 하느님의 아드님이 십자가에 못 박혀 돌아가셨을 때, 그분은 십자가에서 곧장 지옥으로 내려가시어 거기서 고통받고 있던 죄인들을 모두 풀어 주셨답니다요. 그러자 악마가 신음했지요, 이제 지옥에는 죄인을 더 받지 못하겠다고 생각했으니까요. 그래서 하느님이 그자에게 말씀하셨답니다요, '신음하지 말라, 너는 땅 위의 권세 있는 자들을, 통치자들과 으뜸 재판관들과 부자들을 모조리 갖게 될 것이며, 내가 다시 올 때까지 그 모든 세월 동안 그래 왔듯이 가득 채워질 것이다.' 그것이 바로 그분이 하신 말씀이랍니다요……\"\n\n\"농사꾼의 전설이로구나! 기막히다! 왼쪽 말을 채찍질해라, 안드레이!\"\n\n\"그러니 보십쇼, 나리, 지옥이 누구를 위한 것인지 말입니다요.\" 안드레이가 왼쪽 말에 채찍질하며 말했다. \"한데 나리께서는 어린아이 같으십니다요…… 우리는 나리를 그렇게 봅죠…… 그리고 비록 성미는 급하셔도, 나리, 하느님께서는 그 어진 마음씨를 봐서 나리를 용서하실 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 너는, 너는 나를 용서하느냐, 안드레이?\"\n\n\"제가 나리를 무얼 용서한단 말입니까요, 나리? 나리께서는 제게 해를 끼치신 적이 한 번도 없는뎁쇼.\"\n\n\"아니다, 모든 사람을 위해서, 모든 사람을 위해서다, 여기 길 위에서 너 혼자라도, 모든 사람을 대신해 나를 용서해 주겠느냐? 말해 보아라, 순박한 농사꾼의 마음아!\"\n\n\"오, 나리! 나리를 모시고 가는 게 겁이 납니다요, 나리 말씀이 어찌나 이상한지요.\"\n\n그러나 미탸는 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 미친 듯이 기도하며 혼자 중얼거리고 있었다.\n\n\"주여, 이 모든 무법한 죄와 함께 저를 거두시고, 저를 정죄하지 마옵소서. 당신의 심판을 그냥 지나치게 하옵소서…… 저를 정죄하지 마옵소서, 제가 이미 스스로를 정죄하였나이다, 저를 정죄하지 마옵소서, 제가 당신을 사랑하나이다, 오 주여. 저는 비루한 자이오나 당신을 사랑하나이다. 당신께서 저를 지옥으로 보내신다 해도, 저는 거기서도 당신을 사랑하겠나이다, 그리고 그곳에서 영원토록 당신을 사랑한다고 부르짖겠나이다…… 그러나 끝까지 사랑하게 하옵소서…… 지금 여기서 단지 다섯 시간만…… 당신의 날의 첫 빛이 비칠 때까지만…… 제가 제 영혼의 여왕을 사랑하기 때문이옵니다…… 저는 그녀를 사랑하옵고, 사랑하지 않을 수 없나이다. 당신께서는 저의 온 마음을 보고 계시나이다…… 저는 말을 몰아 달려가, 그녀 앞에 엎드려 말하겠나이다, '당신이 그대로 떠나 저를 버리는 것이 옳습니다. 잘 가시오, 그리고 당신의 희생물을 잊으시오…… 결코 나 때문에 마음 쓰지 마시오!'\"\n\n\"모크로예입니다!\" 안드레이가 채찍으로 앞쪽을 가리키며 외쳤다.\n\n밤의 희뿌연 어둠 사이로 건물들의 검고 단단한 덩어리가, 마치 광막한 평원 위에 내던져진 듯이 어렴풋이 떠올랐다. 모크로예 마을은 주민이 이천 명이었으나, 그 시각에는 모두 잠들어 있었고, 다만 여기저기에서 몇 점의 불빛만이 깜박이고 있었다.\n\n\"몰아라, 안드레이, 내가 왔다!\" 미탸가 열에 들떠 소리쳤다.\n\n\"안 잤군요.\" 안드레이가 또다시 마을 어귀에 있는 플라스투노프네 여인숙을 채찍으로 가리키며 말했다. 거리 쪽으로 난 여섯 개의 창문이 모두 환하게 불을 밝히고 있었다.\n\n\"안 잤어.\" 미탸가 기쁘게 되뇌었다. \"더 빨리, 안드레이! 내달려라! 한달음에 들이닥쳐라! 방울을 울려라! 내가 왔다는 걸 모두에게 알려라. 내가 간다! 나도 간다!\"\n\n안드레이는 지친 말떼를 채찍질해 질주시켰고, 한달음에 내달아 김이 무럭무럭 나며 헐떡이는 말들을 높은 층계 앞에 세웠다.\n\n미탸가 마차에서 뛰어내리는 바로 그때, 여인숙 주인이 잠자리에 들려다가 누가 왔는지 궁금하여 층계에서 빼꼼히 내다보았다.\n\n\"트리폰 보리소비치, 자넨가?\"\n\n여인숙 주인은 몸을 굽혀 유심히 들여다보더니, 층계를 뛰어 내려와 굽실거리는 환희에 차서 손님에게로 달려들었다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치, 나리! 또 뵙게 되다니요!\"\n\n트리폰 보리소비치는 다부지고 건장한 농사꾼으로, 중키에 다소 살진 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 그 표정은 엄하고 가차 없었는데, 특히 모크로예의 농사꾼들을 대할 때 그러했으나, 자기 잇속에 보탬이 되겠다 싶은 낌새를 채면 더없이 굽실거리는 낯빛을 꾸며낼 줄도 알았다. 그는 러시아식으로, 한쪽으로 단추를 채우는 셔츠에 자락이 넓은 외투를 걸치고 다녔다. 그는 꽤 많은 돈을 모아 두었으면서도 형편을 더 끌어올릴 궁리만 줄곧 하고 있었다. 농사꾼들의 절반 이상이 그의 손아귀에 잡혀 있었고, 인근의 누구나 그에게 빚을 지고 있었다. 인근의 지주들에게서 그는 땅을 사들이거나 빌려, 그것을 농사꾼들이 부치게 하고는, 그들이 결코 벗어날 수 없는 빚 대신 그 일을 시켰다. 그는 홀아비였고 장성한 딸 넷을 두었다. 그중 하나는 이미 과부가 되어 두 아이, 곧 그의 손주들과 함께 여인숙에서 살며 그를 위해 허드렛일을 하는 여자처럼 일했다. 또 다른 딸은 하급 관리에게 시집을 갔는데, 여인숙의 어느 방 벽에는 가족사진들 사이에, 제복에 관등 견장을 단 이 관리의 자그마한 사진 한 장이 걸려 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 아래 두 딸은 교회 축일이나 누군가를 방문하러 갈 때면 등 쪽이 꼭 끼고 한 야드나 되는 긴 자락이 달린, 유행하는 파랑이나 초록 드레스를 입곤 했다. 그러나 다음 날 아침이면 여느 때처럼 동틀 녘에 일어나 자작나무 빗자루로 방을 쓸고, 구정물을 비우고, 투숙객이 머물다 간 자리를 치웠다.\n\n수천 루블을 모아 두었음에도 트리폰 보리소비치는 술 취한 손님의 주머니를 털어내는 것을 무척 즐겼다. 한 달도 채 안 된 전날, 드미트리가 그루셴카와 함께 한바탕 흥청거리러 왔을 때 그에게서 스물네 시간 만에 이삼백 루블은 아니어도 이백 루블은 우려냈던 것을 떠올리며, 그는 미탸가 층계 앞으로 마차를 몰고 다가오는 순간 먹잇감 냄새를 맡고서 이제 그를 살갑게 맞이했다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치, 나리, 또 뵙게 되다니요!\"\n\n\"가만, 트리폰 보리소비치.\" 미탸가 입을 열었다. \"무엇보다 먼저, 그녀는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나 말씀입니까요?\" 여인숙 주인은 미탸의 얼굴을 날카롭게 들여다보며 단번에 알아차렸다. \"그분도 여기 계십니다요……\"\n\n\"누구와 함께? 누구와 함께?\"\n\n\"외지 사람들과 함께지요. 하나는 관리 양반인데, 말투로 보아 폴란드 사람입니다요. 그 사람이 여기서 그분을 모시러 말을 보냈지요. 그리고 그와 함께 또 한 사람이 있는데, 그 친구인지 길동무인지는 알 수 없습니다요. 둘 다 평복 차림입죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그들은 잔치를 벌이고 있나? 돈이 있던가?\"\n\n\"잔치라고 할 것도 없습죠! 내세울 것 하나 없습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리.\"\n\n\"내세울 게 없다고? 그럼 나머지 사람들은 누구냐?\"\n\n\"고장에서 온 신사 두 분입니다요…… 체르니에서 돌아오는 길에 여기 묵고 계십죠. 하나는 아주 젊은 양반인데, 미우소프 씨의 친척인 듯합니다요, 한데 이름은 잊어버렸군요…… 그리고 다른 한 분은 나리께서도 아실 듯합니다요, 막시모프라는 양반입니다요. 자기 말로는 고장에 있는 수도원으로 순례를 다녀오는 길이라더군요. 미우소프 씨의 그 젊은 친척과 함께 다닌답니다요.\"\n\n\"그게 전부인가?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"가만, 들어 보게, 트리폰 보리소비치. 가장 중요한 걸 말해 주게. 그녀는 어떤가? 그녀의 상태는 어떠냐 말일세?\"\n\n\"오, 그분은 이제 막 오셨습니다요. 그들과 함께 앉아 계십죠.\"\n\n\"명랑한가? 웃고 있나?\"\n\n\"아니요, 별로 웃지 않으시는 것 같습니다요. 아주 시무룩하게 앉아 계십죠. 그 젊은 양반의 머리칼을 빗겨 주고 계십니다요.\"\n\n\"폴란드 사람—그 장교 말인가?\"\n\n\"그 사람은 젊지도 않고, 장교도 아닙니다요. 그 사람이 아니라요, 나리. 미우소프 씨의 친척인 그 젊은 양반입니다요…… 이름을 잊어버렸습니다만.\"\n\n\"칼가노프로군.\"\n\n\"맞습니다요, 칼가노프!\"\n\n\"좋아. 내가 직접 보지. 그들은 카드놀이를 하고 있나?\"\n\n\"하고 있다가 그만뒀습니다요. 차를 마셨고, 그 관리 양반이 리큐어를 청했습죠.\"\n\n\"가만, 트리폰 보리소비치, 가만, 이 사람아, 내가 직접 보겠네. 자, 한 가지만 더 대답해 주게. 집시들이 여기 와 있나?\"\n\n\"지금은 집시를 부르실 수 없습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리. 당국에서 그들을 쫓아냈거든요. 하지만 마을에 심벌즈하고 바이올린을 켜는 유대인들이 있으니, 그들을 부르면 됩니다요. 그들이라면 올 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"불러 오게. 꼭 불러 와!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"그리고 그때처럼 처녀들도 모아 올 수 있겠지, 특히 마리야, 스테파니다도, 그리고 아리나도. 합창에 이백 루블이다!\"\n\n\"오, 그만한 돈이라면 온 마을 사람들을 다 모아 올 수 있습죠, 비록 지금쯤은 다들 잠들어 있겠지만요. 한데 여기 농사꾼들이, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 그만한 후의를 받을 만한 자들입니까요, 처녀들인들 그렇겠습니까요? 그런 거칠고 무례한 것들에게 그만한 돈을 쓰시다니요! 농사꾼에게 시가를 물려 피우게 한들 무슨 소용입니까요, 그 고약한 냄새 나는 무뢰한들에게 말입니다요! 처녀들이래야 죄다 이투성이고요. 게다가 제 딸들이라면 그만한 돈은커녕 거저라도 일으켜 세우겠습니다요. 이제 막 잠자리에 들었으니, 한번 걷어차서 나리를 위해 노래를 부르게 하겠습니다요. 나리께서는 지난번에 농사꾼들에게 샴페인까지 먹이지 않았습니까요, 에—이!\"\n\n미탸를 동정하는 척하면서도, 트리폰 보리소비치는 그 지난번에 샴페인 여섯 병을 몰래 빼돌렸고, 식탁 밑에서 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 주워 자기 손아귀에 넣어 두었던 것이다.\n\n\"트리폰 보리소비치, 지난번에 내가 여기 왔을 때 천 루블도 넘게 날려 버렸지. 기억하나?\"\n\n\"날려 버리셨고말굽쇼. 똑똑히 기억하고 있습니다요. 삼천 루블은 뿌리고 가셨을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그래, 이번에도 똑같이 하러 왔지, 알겠나?\"\n\n그러고는 그가 지폐 뭉치를 꺼내 여인숙 주인의 코앞에 치켜들었다.\n\n\"자, 들어 두고 기억하게. 한 시간 뒤에 술이 도착할 걸세, 안주에 파이에 사탕까지—그것들을 죄다 한꺼번에 올려 주게. 안드레이가 가지고 있는 저 상자도 당장 올려 와야 해. 그걸 열어서 샴페인을 즉시 내오게. 그리고 처녀들, 처녀들이 있어야 하네, 특히 마리야 말일세.\"\n\n그가 마차로 돌아서서 권총 상자를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"자, 안드레이, 셈을 치르자. 여기 마차삯으로 십오 루블, 보드카 값으로 오십 루블이다…… 네 정성에 대해, 네 충정에 대해서지…… 카라마조프를 잊지 말거라!\"\n\n\"무섭습니다요, 나리.\" 안드레이가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"오 루블만 더 얹어 주십쇼, 그 이상은 받지 않겠습니다요. 트리폰 보리소비치, 증인이 되어 주시오. 제 어리석은 말들을 용서하십쇼……\"\n\n\"뭐가 무섭다는 게냐?\" 미탸가 그를 훑어보며 물었다. \"그래, 정 그렇다면 악마에게나 가거라!\" 그가 오 루블을 던져 주며 외쳤다. \"자, 트리폰 보리소비치, 조용히 나를 데려가 주게, 그들이 나를 보지 못하게 먼저 살펴볼 수 있도록 말일세. 그들이 어디 있나? 파란 방에 있나?\"\n\n트리폰 보리소비치는 불안한 듯 미탸를 쳐다보았으나, 곧바로 순순히 그가 시키는 대로 했다. 그를 복도로 안내한 뒤, 자신이 먼저 손님들이 앉아 있는 방에 잇닿은 첫 번째 큰 방으로 들어가 불을 치웠다. 그러고는 살그머니 미탸를 들여보내, 들키지 않고도 그 무리를 마음껏 살펴볼 수 있도록 어두운 구석에 세워 두었다. 그러나 미탸는 오래 보지 않았다, 아니, 실은 그들을 볼 수가 없었으니, 그는 그녀를 보았고, 그의 심장이 격렬하게 두근거렸으며, 눈앞이 온통 캄캄해졌다.\n\n그녀는 식탁 옆으로 비스듬히 낮은 의자에 앉아 있었고, 그 곁의 소파에는 잘생긴 젊은이 칼가노프가 있었다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 웃고 있는 듯했는데, 그는 짜증이 난 듯 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않은 채, 식탁 맞은편에서 그루셴카를 마주 보고 앉은 막시모프에게 큰 소리로 무언가 말하고 있었다. 막시모프는 무언가에 격하게 웃고 있었다. 소파에는 바로 그자가 앉아 있었고, 소파 옆 의자에는 또 다른 외지 사람이 있었다. 소파에 앉은 자는 등을 뒤로 젖힌 채 파이프를 빨고 있었는데, 미탸는 다소 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 넓적한, 키 작은 사내라는 인상을 받았으며, 그자는 무언가에 화가 나 있는 듯했다. 그의 친구인 또 다른 외지 사람은 미탸에게 유달리 키가 크다는 느낌을 주었으나, 그 이상은 분간할 수 없었다. 그는 숨이 막혔다. 한순간도 더는 견딜 수 없어, 그는 권총 상자를 궤짝 위에 올려 두고, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 온몸에 한기를 느끼며, 곧장 파란 방으로 걸어 들어가 그 무리와 마주했다.\n\n\"아이쿠!\" 그를 맨 먼저 알아본 그루셴카가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n# Chapter VII. The First And Rightful Lover\n\n미탸는 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 곧장 탁자 앞으로 다가갔다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그는 거의 고함에 가까운 큰 소리로, 그러면서도 말마디마다 더듬거리며 말했다. \"나, 나는 괜찮습니다! 두려워 마세요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"나는, 아무 일도 없습니다.\" 그는 의자에 앉은 채 칼가노프 쪽으로 몸을 움츠리고 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 있던 그루센카를 향해 불쑥 돌아섰다. \"나, 나도 함께 있겠소. 아침까지 여기 있겠소. 여러분, 아침까지 여러분과 함께 있어도 되겠습니까? 그저 아침까지만, 마지막으로, 바로 이 방에서?\"\n\n그렇게 말을 맺으며 그는 파이프를 문 채 소파에 앉아 있는 작고 뚱뚱한 사내를 향했다. 그 사내는 위엄을 갖추어 입에서 파이프를 떼더니 엄하게 한마디 했다.\n\n\"여보시오, 우리는 여기서 사사로이 모여 있는 거요. 다른 방들이 있잖소.\"\n\n\"아니, 당신이군요, 드미트리 표도로비치! 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 칼가노프가 불쑥 대답했다. \"우리와 함께 앉으세요. 안녕하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"만나서 반갑소, 친애하는… 소중한 친구여, 나는 늘 당신을 높이 여겨 왔소.\" 미탸가 기쁨에 들떠 다급하게 응수하며, 곧장 탁자 너머로 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"아얏! 어찌 그리 꽉 쥐시오! 손가락이 다 부러지겠소.\" 칼가노프가 웃었다.\n\n\"이이는 늘 저렇게 꽉 쥔답니다, 늘 그래요.\" 그루센카가 수줍은 미소를 띠며 명랑하게 끼어들었으니, 미탸의 얼굴을 보고 그가 소동을 부리지는 않으리라는 것을 문득 확신한 듯했다. 그녀는 강한 호기심을, 그러면서도 여전히 약간의 불안을 품고 그를 지켜보고 있었다. 그에게서 무언가가 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았으니, 실로 그녀가 그에게서 가장 예상치 못한 것은 그가 이런 순간에 들어와 이런 식으로 말하는 것이었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오.\" 막시모프가 왼편에서 부드럽게 인사를 건넸다. 미탸는 그에게도 달려갔다.\n\n\"안녕하시오. 당신도 여기 계셨군요! 당신까지 여기서 만나다니 얼마나 기쁜지요! 여러분, 여러분, 나는—\" (그는 다시 파이프를 문 폴란드 신사에게 말을 건넸으니, 분명 그를 그 자리에서 가장 중요한 인물로 여기는 모양이었다.) \"나는 여기로 날아왔소…. 나는 내 마지막 날을, 내 마지막 시간을 이 방에서, 바로 이 방에서… 나도 흠모했던… 내 여왕을…. 용서하시오, 여보시오.\" 그가 격하게 외쳤다. \"나는 여기로 날아와 맹세했소— 오, 두려워 마시오, 이건 내 마지막 밤이오! 우리의 화목을 위해 한잔합시다. 곧 술을 가져올 거요…. 이걸 가지고 왔소.\" (무언가에 떠밀린 듯 그는 지폐 뭉치를 꺼냈다.) \"허락해 주시오, 여보시오! 나는 음악을, 노래를, 흥청거리는 잔치를 벌이고 싶소, 예전처럼 말이오. 하지만 그 벌레는, 쓸데없는 벌레는 기어 사라질 것이고, 다시는 그런 놈이 없을 거요. 나는 내 마지막 밤에 내 기쁨의 날을 기리겠소.\"\n\n그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 말하고 싶은 것이 그토록 많고도 많았건만, 입에서 나오는 것이라곤 기이한 외침뿐이었다. 폴란드인은 그를, 그의 손에 든 지폐 뭉치를 뚫어지게 바라보더니 그루센카를 쳐다보며 역력한 당혹감에 빠져 있었다.\n\n\"내 군주이신 마님께서 허락하신다면—\" 그가 입을 떼기 시작했다.\n\n\"'군주'가 무슨 뜻이에요? '주권자' 말씀인가요?\" 그루센카가 말을 가로챘다. \"당신 말투를 들으면 웃음이 나서 견딜 수가 없어요. 앉아요, 미탸, 무슨 소리를 하는 거예요? 제발 우리를 놀라게 하지 말아요. 우리를 놀라게 하진 않을 거죠? 그러지만 않는다면 당신을 보게 돼서 기뻐요….\"\n\n\"내가, 내가 당신을 놀라게 한다고?\" 미탸가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"오, 나를 지나쳐 가시오, 당신 갈 길을 가시오, 나는 방해하지 않겠소!…\"\n\n그러더니 그는 별안간 의자에 몸을 던지고 울음을 터뜨려, 머리를 맞은편 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 두 팔로 의자 등받이를 끌어안듯 꽉 부둥켜안아, 모두를, 그리고 틀림없이 자기 자신마저 놀라게 만들었다.\n\n\"이런, 이런, 당신은 정말 어쩔 수 없는 사람이군요!\" 그루센카가 나무라듯 외쳤다. \"꼭 저런 식으로 나를 보러 온다니까요—말을 시작하는데 무슨 소린지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없어요. 전에도 한 번 저렇게 울더니, 이제 또 우는군요! 부끄럽지도 않아요? 왜 우는 거예요? 마치 울 일이라도 있는 것처럼!\" 그녀는 짜증 섞인 어조로 말마디마다 힘을 주며 수수께끼 같은 말을 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나, 나는 울지 않소…. 자, 안녕하시오!\" 그는 곧장 의자에서 몸을 돌리더니 별안간 웃었는데, 그 퉁명스럽고 나무토막 같은 웃음이 아니라 길게 떨리는, 소리 없는 신경질적인 웃음이었다.\n\n\"자, 또 시작이네요…. 자, 기운 내요, 기운 내요!\" 그루센카가 그를 달래듯 말했다. \"당신이 와 줘서 정말 기뻐요, 정말로요, 미탸, 듣고 있어요, 정말 기쁘다니까요! 이이가 여기 우리와 함께 있었으면 좋겠어요.\" 그녀는 좌중 전체를 향해 단호하게 말했지만, 그 말이 분명 소파에 앉은 사내를 겨냥한 것임은 누가 봐도 알 수 있었다. \"그게 내 바람이에요, 내 바람이라고요! 그리고 만약 이이가 가 버린다면 나도 가겠어요!\" 그녀는 눈을 번득이며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내 여왕께서 분부하시는 것이 곧 법이외다!\" 폴란드인이 그루센카의 손에 정중히 입을 맞추며 선언했다. \"여보시오, 우리 자리에 함께해 주시기를 청합니다.\" 그가 미탸를 향해 정중하게 덧붙였다.\n\n미탸는 또 한바탕 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 작정으로 벌떡 일어났지만, 말이 나오지 않았다.\n\n\"한잔합시다, 여보시오.\" 그는 연설을 늘어놓는 대신 이렇게 불쑥 내뱉었다. 모두가 웃었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 또 시작하는 줄 알았네요!\" 그루센카가 신경질적으로 외쳤다. \"들어요, 미탸.\" 그녀가 끈질기게 말을 이었다. \"껑충거리며 설치지 말아요. 그래도 샴페인을 가져온 건 잘했어요. 나도 좀 마시고 싶었는데, 리큐어는 질색이거든요. 그리고 무엇보다도, 당신이 직접 와 줬으니까요. 여기 정말 지루해 죽을 지경이었거든요…. 또 흥청거리려고 온 거죠? 그건 그렇고 돈은 주머니에 넣어 둬요. 그렇게 많은 돈을 어디서 났어요?\"\n\n미탸는 그동안 줄곧 구겨진 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 모두의, 특히 폴란드인들의 시선이 거기에 못 박혀 있었다. 그는 당황하여 황급히 그것을 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다. 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 바로 그때 여관 주인이 마개를 딴 샴페인 한 병과 쟁반에 받친 잔들을 들고 들어왔다. 미탸가 병을 낚아챘지만 너무도 얼떨떨한 나머지 그것을 어찌해야 할지 몰랐다. 칼가노프가 그에게서 병을 받아 샴페인을 따랐다.\n\n\"한 병 더! 한 병 더 가져오시오!\" 미탸가 여관 주인에게 외치고는, 그토록 엄숙하게 화목을 위해 건배하자고 청했던 폴란드인과 잔을 부딪치는 것도 잊은 채, 누구도 기다리지 않고 제 잔을 단숨에 비웠다. 그의 표정이 별안간 온통 달라졌다. 들어올 때 띠고 있던 엄숙하고 비극적인 표정은 완전히 사라지고, 무언가 어린아이 같은 기색이 얼굴에 떠올랐다. 그는 별안간 온순하고 잠잠해진 듯했다. 그는 수줍고도 행복한 표정으로 모두를 바라보며 끊임없이 신경질적인 작은 웃음을 흘렸으니, 잘못을 저질러 벌을 받았다가 용서받은 개의 더없이 행복한 표정 같았다. 그는 모든 것을 잊어버린 듯, 어린아이처럼 기쁨에 찬 미소를 띠고 모두를 둘러보았다. 그는 그루센카를 바라보며 끊임없이 웃었고, 의자를 그녀 가까이로 바짝 끌어당겼다. 차차 그는 두 폴란드인에 대해 어느 정도 감을 잡게 되었으나, 아직 그들에 대한 명확한 인상을 갖추지는 못했다.\n\n소파에 앉은 폴란드인은 그 위엄 있는 태도와 폴란드 억양으로, 그리고 무엇보다 그 파이프로 미탸에게 강한 인상을 주었다. \"뭐, 그게 어떻다는 거지? 파이프를 피우는 거야 좋은 일이지.\" 그는 이렇게 생각했다. 그 폴란드인의 부석부석하고 중년에 접어든 얼굴은 자그마한 코와, 가늘고 뾰족하게 다듬어 염색한 두 가닥의 뻔뻔스러워 보이는 콧수염을 달고 있었지만, 아직 미탸의 마음속에 일말의 의심도 불러일으키지 못했다. 그는 그 폴란드인이 시베리아에서 만든 우스꽝스러운 가발을 쓰고, 관자놀이 위로 어리석게 빗어 내린 곱슬머리 타래를 늘어뜨린 것조차 별로 눈여겨보지 않았다. \"가발을 쓰고 있으니 뭐 괜찮겠지.\" 그는 황홀하게 상념에 잠긴 채 생각을 이어 갔다. 좌중을 뻔뻔스럽고 도전적으로 노려보며 침묵 속의 경멸을 담아 대화를 듣고 있던 또 한 사람, 더 젊은 폴란드인은 그의 엄청난 키로만 미탸에게 인상을 주었으니, 그것은 소파에 앉은 폴란드인과는 두드러진 대조를 이루었다. \"저자가 일어선다면 여섯 자 석 치는 되겠군.\" 이런 생각이 미탸의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 또한 그는 이 폴란드인이 다른 자의 친구, 말하자면 \"경호원\"이며, 이 큰 폴란드인은 틀림없이 파이프를 문 작은 폴란드인의 처분에 맡겨진 신세이리라는 생각이 들었다. 그러나 이 모든 것이 미탸에게는 더없이 당연하고 의심할 여지가 없어 보였다. 개처럼 순종하는 그의 기분 속에서 경쟁심이라는 감정은 죄다 사그라들어 있었다.\n\n그루센카의 기분과 그녀가 내뱉은 몇몇 말의 수수께끼 같은 어조를 그는 전혀 알아채지 못했다. 그가 가슴 떨리는 마음으로 이해한 것이라곤 오로지, 그녀가 자기에게 다정하다는 것, 자기를 용서하고 자기 곁에 앉게 해 주었다는 것뿐이었다. 그는 그녀가 샴페인 잔을 홀짝이는 모습을 지켜보며 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 그러나 좌중의 침묵이 어쩐지 그의 마음에 걸린 듯, 그는 기대에 찬 눈으로 모두를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"그런데 여러분, 우리는 왜 이렇게 앉아만 있는 거요? 왜 뭔가 시작하지 않는 거요?\" 그의 웃음 띤 눈이 이렇게 묻는 듯했다.\n\n\"이 사람이 자꾸 허튼소리를 늘어놓아서 다들 웃고 있었답니다.\" 칼가노프가 그의 생각을 알아챈 듯 별안간 입을 열며 막시모프를 가리켰다.\n\n미탸는 곧장 칼가노프를, 그러고 나서 막시모프를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"이분이 허튼소리를 한다고?\" 그가 짧고 나무토막 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며, 무언가에 별안간 즐거워진 듯 웃었다. \"하하!\"\n\n\"그렇다니까요. 글쎄, 믿어지시겠어요, 이 사람은 20년대에 우리 기병 장교들이 죄다 폴란드 여자와 결혼했다고 우긴다니까요. 어처구니없는 허풍 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"폴란드 여자라고?\" 미탸가 완전히 황홀경에 빠져 되뇌었다.\n\n칼가노프는 미탸가 그루센카에게 품은 마음을 잘 알고 있었고, 폴란드인에 대해서도 짐작하고 있었지만, 그런 것은 그다지 그의 관심을 끌지 못했으니, 어쩌면 전혀 관심이 없었는지도 몰랐다. 그의 관심을 끄는 것은 막시모프였다. 그는 우연히 막시모프와 함께 이곳에 왔고, 폴란드인들은 이 여관에서 난생처음 만났다. 그루센카는 전부터 알고 지냈으며, 한번은 누군가와 함께 그녀를 보러 온 적도 있었지만, 그녀는 그를 마음에 들어 하지 않았다. 그러나 여기서 그녀는 그를 무척 다정하게 바라보았으니, 미탸가 도착하기 전까지 그녀는 그를 한껏 추어올렸으나, 그는 어쩐지 거기에 마음이 동하지 않는 듯했다. 그는 스무 살이 채 안 된 소년으로, 멋쟁이처럼 차려입었고, 살결이 희고 무척 매력적인 얼굴에 숱 많고 멋진 금발을 지니고 있었다. 그 흰 얼굴에서는 아름다운 옅은 푸른 눈이 내다보았으니, 그 눈에는 영리하고 때로는 심지어 깊이까지 어린 표정이, 실로 그 나이를 넘어서는 표정이 깃들어 있었다. 비록 이 젊은이는 이따금 어린아이처럼 보이고 어린아이처럼 말했으며, 스스로 그것을 깨달았을 때조차 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았지만 말이다. 대체로 그는 무척 제멋대로이고 심지어 변덕스러웠으나, 늘 다정했다. 때로는 그의 표정에 무언가 고정되고 완고한 데가 있었다. 그는 당신을 바라보고 귀를 기울이면서도, 그러는 내내 무언가 다른 것을 끈질기게 꿈꾸는 것처럼 보였다. 흔히 그는 무기력하고 나른했으나, 때로는 흥분하기도 했으니, 그것도 더러는 보기에 더없이 하찮은 일에 그러했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보세요, 내가 나흘째 이 사람을 데리고 다니는 중이랍니다.\" 그는 나른하게 말을 끌면서, 그러나 조금도 꾸밈없이 아주 자연스럽게 말을 이었다. \"당신 형님이, 기억하시죠, 이 사람을 마차에서 밀쳐 내동댕이친 그날 이후로 말입니다. 그 일로 그때 이 사람한테 관심이 생겨서 시골로 데려왔는데, 어찌나 허튼소리를 늘어놓는지 함께 다니기가 창피할 지경이에요. 그래서 다시 데려다주는 길이랍니다.\"\n\n\"이 양반은 폴란드 부인들을 본 적이 없으니 있을 수 없는 소리를 하는 거외다.\" 파이프를 문 폴란드인이 막시모프를 향해 말했다.\n\n그는 러시아어를 제법 잘했으니, 아무튼 짐짓 꾸며 보이는 것보다는 훨씬 잘했다. 러시아어 단어를 쓸 때면 그는 늘 그것을 폴란드식으로 비틀어 댔다.\n\n\"하지만 나도 폴란드 부인과 결혼했었는걸요.\" 막시모프가 킬킬거렸다.\n\n\"그렇지만 기병으로 복무하긴 했소? 당신은 기병 이야기를 하고 있었잖소. 기병 장교였소?\" 칼가노프가 곧장 끼어들었다.\n\n\"정말로 기병 장교였답니까? 하하!\" 미탸가 열심히 귀를 기울이며 외쳤으니, 말하는 동안 그는 캐묻는 눈길을 한 사람 한 사람에게로 돌렸으며, 마치 각자에게서 무슨 말을 듣게 될지 알 수 없다는 투였다.\n\n\"아니요, 그게 말이죠,\" 막시모프가 그를 향해 돌아섰다. \"내 말은, 그 어여쁜 폴란드 부인들이… 우리 창기병들과 마주르카를 출 때면… 그중 하나가 창기병과 마주르카를 추다가 새끼고양이처럼 그 무릎 위로 폴짝 뛰어오른답니다… 자그마한 흰 고양이처럼요… 그러면 그 댁 아버님이며 어머님이 보고도 그냥 내버려 두지요…. 내버려 둔답니다… 그러고는 이튿날 창기병이 와서 그녀에게 청혼을 하는 겁니다…. 바로 그런 식이지요… 청혼을 한다니까요, 헤헤!\" 막시모프가 킬킬거리며 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"이 양반은 비열한 놈이오!\" 의자에 앉은 키 큰 폴란드인이 별안간 으르렁대며 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 꼬아 얹었다. 미탸의 눈에 두껍고 더러운 밑창이 달린 그의 커다란 기름투성이 장화가 들어왔다. 두 폴란드인의 옷차림은 어딘지 기름때가 낀 듯했다.\n\n\"이젠 비열한 놈이라고요! 대체 무얼 가지고 욕설을 퍼붓는 거예요?\" 그루센카가 별안간 짜증을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"아그리피나 마님, 이 양반이 폴란드에서 본 건 하녀들이지 양가의 부인들이 아니었던 거외다.\" 파이프를 문 폴란드인이 그루센카를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"그건 장담할 수 있소.\" 키 큰 폴란드인이 경멸하듯 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"별꼴이야! 이야기하게 놔둬요! 사람들이 이야기하는데 왜 막는 거예요? 그래야 흥겹잖아요.\" 그루센카가 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"막는 게 아니외다, 마님.\" 가발을 쓴 폴란드인이 그루센카를 길게 바라보며 말하고는, 다시 위엄 있는 침묵 속으로 가라앉아 파이프를 빨았다.\n\n\"아니, 아니. 폴란드 양반 말이 옳아요.\" 칼가노프가 마치 무슨 대단히 중요한 문제라도 되는 양 다시 흥분했다. \"이 사람은 폴란드에 가 본 적이 없는데 어떻게 폴란드 이야기를 할 수 있겠어요? 당신은 폴란드에서 결혼한 게 아니었죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아니요, 스몰렌스크 현(縣)에서였어요. 다만 그 전에 어느 창기병이 그녀를, 내 장차 아내가 될 사람을 어머니와 이모, 그리고 또 다 큰 아들을 둔 다른 여자 친척과 함께 러시아로 데려온 거지요. 그가 그녀를 폴란드에서 곧장 데려와 나한테 넘겨준 거예요. 그는 우리 연대의 중위였는데, 아주 좋은 청년이었지요. 처음엔 자기가 그녀와 결혼할 작정이었어요. 하지만 그녀가 절름발이로 드러나는 바람에 결혼하지 않았답니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 절름발이 여자랑 결혼했단 말이에요?\" 칼가노프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇답니다. 그때 두 사람이 나를 조금 속이고 그걸 감췄던 거지요. 나는 그녀가 깡충거리는 줄로만 알았어요. 자꾸 깡충거리길래…. 그저 장난삼아 그러는 줄 알았답니다.\"\n\n\"당신과 결혼하게 돼서 그렇게나 좋았던 거군요!\" 칼가노프가 어린아이처럼 낭랑한 목소리로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 그렇게나 좋았던 거지요. 그런데 알고 보니 영 다른 까닭이었던 겁니다. 나중에 우리가 결혼하고 나서, 식을 올린 바로 그날 저녁에 그녀가 고백을 하면서 무척이나 애처롭게 용서를 빌더군요. '제가 어렸을 적에 한번은 웅덩이를 뛰어넘다가 다리를 다쳤어요'라고 말이지요. 헤헤!\"\n\n칼가노프가 더없이 어린아이 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며 거의 소파에 쓰러질 지경이었다. 그루센카도 웃었다. 미탸는 행복의 절정에 올라 있었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 그건 사실이에요, 이번엔 거짓말이 아니라고요.\" 칼가노프가 미탸를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"그리고 말이죠, 이 사람은 두 번 결혼했는데, 지금 말하는 건 첫 아내 이야기예요. 그런데 두 번째 아내는요, 글쎄, 달아나 버려서 지금도 살아 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그게 가능한 일이오?\" 미탸가 더없는 경악의 표정을 띠고 막시모프를 향해 휙 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요. 정말로 달아나 버렸지요. 나는 그런 불쾌한 일을 겪었답니다.\" 막시모프가 겸손하게 인정했다. \"어떤 양반과 말이지요. 게다가 더 나빴던 건, 그 여자가 미리 내 자그마한 재산을 죄다 자기 앞으로 돌려놓았던 겁니다. '당신은 배운 사람이잖아요'라고 그 여자가 나한테 말하더군요. '언제든 벌어먹고 살 수 있잖아요.' 그렇게 한마디로 내 일을 끝장내 버린 거지요. 어느 존경할 만한 주교님이 언젠가 나한테 이런 말씀을 하셨답니다. '자네 아내 하나는 절름발이였지만, 다른 하나는 발이 너무 가벼웠구먼.' 헤헤!\"\n\n\"들어 봐요, 들어 봐요!\" 칼가노프가 들떠서 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다. \"만약 이 사람이 거짓말을 하는 거라면—실은 종종 그러지만—그건 그저 우리 모두를 즐겁게 해 주려는 거예요. 그게 뭐 나쁠 거 있나요, 안 그래요? 있잖아요, 난 가끔 이 사람이 좋아져요. 형편없이 비루한 사람이지만, 그게 타고난 천성이거든요, 안 그래요? 그렇게 생각 안 하세요? 어떤 사람들은 잇속 때문에 비루해지지만, 이 사람은 그저 천성이 그런 거예요. 글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보세요, 이 사람은 고골이 자기를 두고 《죽은 혼》을 썼다고 우긴다니까요(어제 내내 그걸 두고 옥신각신했지요). 기억하시죠, 거기 막시모프라는 지주가 나오는데, 노즈드료프한테 흠씬 두들겨 맞잖아요. 그 사람이 기소된 게, 기억나세요, '술에 취한 상태에서 지주 막시모프에게 회초리로 신체적 상해를 가한 죄'였지요. 그런데 글쎄, 이 사람이 자기가 바로 그 막시모프이고 자기가 매를 맞은 거라고 우긴다니까요! 자, 그게 말이 됩니까? 치치코프가 여행을 한 게 아무리 늦어도 20년대 초니까 시기가 도무지 안 맞잖아요. 그때 이 사람이 두들겨 맞았을 리가 없어요, 그럴 리가요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n칼가노프가 무엇 때문에 흥분하는 것인지 헤아리기는 어려웠으나, 그 흥분만큼은 진심이었다. 미탸는 군말 없이 그의 말장단을 따라갔다.\n\n\"뭐, 하지만 정말로 두들겨 팼다면 어떻소!\" 그가 웃으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"날 두들겨 팼다기보다는, 내 말은 그게—\" 막시모프가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"무슨 말이오? 두들겨 맞았든지 안 맞았든지 둘 중 하나 아니오.\"\n\n\"몇 시나 됐소, 여보시오?\" 파이프를 문 폴란드인이 따분한 표정으로 키 큰 친구에게 물었다. 상대는 대답 대신 어깨를 으쓱했다. 둘 다 시계가 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"이야기 좀 하면 어때서? 남들이 이야기하게 놔두라고요. 당신이 따분하다고 남들도 이야기하면 안 된단 말이에요?\" 그루센카가 트집을 잡을 작정이 역력한 채 그에게 달려들었다. 미탸의 머릿속에 처음으로 무언가가 번뜩이는 듯했다. 이번에 폴란드인은 누가 봐도 분명한 짜증을 내며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"마님, 나는 막지 않았소. 아무 말도 안 했소이다.\"\n\n\"그럼 됐어요. 자, 당신 이야기를 들려줘요.\" 그루센카가 막시모프에게 외쳤다. \"왜들 죄다 입을 다물고 있는 거예요?\"\n\n\"들려드릴 게 없는걸요, 죄다 어리석은 이야기뿐이라서요.\" 막시모프가 곧장 대답했는데, 역력히 만족스러운 기색으로 약간 점잔을 뺐다. \"게다가 그게 다 고골이 빗대어 쓴 거라서요, 이름마다 죄다 무슨 뜻이 있게 만들어 놓았거든요. 노즈드료프는 실은 노소프라는 이름이었고, 쿠브시니코프는 영 다른 이름, 시크보르네프였답니다. 페나르디는 정말로 페나르디였지만, 이탈리아인이 아니라 러시아인이었고, 마드무아젤 페나르디는 살색 타이츠 속에 예쁘장한 작은 다리를 드러낸 어여쁜 처녀로, 반짝이 장식을 단 짤막한 치마를 입고 빙글빙글 돌아 댔지요, 다만 네 시간이 아니라 딱 사 분 동안만요, 그래도 모두를 홀려 버렸답니다….\"\n\n\"그런데 당신은 뭣 때문에 두들겨 맞은 거예요?\" 칼가노프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"피롱 때문이지요!\" 막시모프가 대답했다.\n\n\"피롱이 누구요?\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 유명한 프랑스 작가, 피롱 말입니다. 그때 우리가 다들 술을 마시고 있었어요, 한 무리가 잔뜩 모여서, 바로 그 장터 어느 선술집에서요. 사람들이 날 불러서, 나는 우선 풍자시 몇 구절을 읊기 시작했지요. '거기 자네 부알로 아닌가? 무슨 우스꽝스러운 차림이람!' 그러자 부알로가 자기는 가장무도회에 가는 길이라고, 그러니까 목욕탕에 간다고 대답하는 거지요, 헤헤! 그런데 사람들이 그걸 자기들한테 한 소리로 받아들이는 바람에, 나는 부랴부랴 또 다른 시를, 아주 신랄하고 배운 사람이라면 누구나 다 아는 시를 읊었답니다.\n\n그렇소, 우리는 사포와 파온이라오! 허나 한 가지 시름이 나를 짓누른다오. 그대는 바다로 가는 길을 모르나니!\n\n그러자 사람들이 더더욱 화를 내며 더없이 망측한 말로 날 욕해 대기 시작하더군요. 그런데 운수 사납게도, 일을 바로잡으려고 나는 피롱에 관한 아주 교양 있는 일화를 들려주기 시작했지요, 그가 프랑스 학술원에 받아들여지지 못해서, 그 앙갚음으로 자기 묘비명을 직접 지었다는 이야기 말입니다.\n여기 아무것도 아니었던 피롱이 잠들다, 학술원 회원조차 못 되었던 자가.\n\n그랬더니 사람들이 날 붙잡아 흠씬 두들겨 패는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데 뭣 때문에요? 뭣 때문에?\"\n\n\"내 교양 때문이지요. 사람이란 무슨 이유로든 남을 두들겨 팰 수 있는 법이랍니다.\" 막시모프가 짤막하게, 격언조로 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"에, 그만하면 됐어요! 죄다 어리석은 소리뿐이라 더는 듣고 싶지 않아요. 재미있을 줄 알았더니만.\" 그루센카가 별안간 그들의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n미탸가 움찔하더니 곧장 웃음을 거두었다. 키 큰 폴란드인이 일어서더니, 따분하고 자기 자리가 아닌 데 끼인 듯한 사내 특유의 오만한 태도로 뒷짐을 진 채 방 이 구석 저 구석을 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n\"아, 저이는 가만히 앉아 있질 못하네요.\" 그루센카가 그를 경멸하듯 바라보며 말했다. 미탸는 불안을 느끼기 시작했다. 게다가 그는 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 짜증스러운 표정으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"한잔합시다! 저쪽 양반도 함께! 한잔합시다.\"\n\n그는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔 세 개를 끌어당겨 샴페인으로 가득 채웠다.\n\n\"폴란드를 위하여, 여러분, 나는 당신네 폴란드를 위해 마시겠소!\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n\"기꺼이 받겠소, 여보시오.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 위엄과 상냥한 겸양을 갖추어 말하며 잔을 들었다.\n\n\"그리고 저쪽 양반, 성함이 뭐였더라? 드시오, 더없이 고명하신 분, 잔을 드시오!\" 미탸가 재촉했다.\n\n\"브루블레프스키 씨요.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 일러 주었다.\n\n브루블레프스키 씨가 휘청거리는 걸음으로 탁자로 다가왔다.\n\n\"폴란드를 위하여, 여러분!\" 미탸가 잔을 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"만세!\"\n\n셋이 다 마셨다. 미탸가 병을 움켜쥐고 다시 잔 세 개를 채웠다.\n\n\"이번엔 러시아를 위하여, 여러분, 그리고 우리 형제가 됩시다!\"\n\n\"우리한테도 좀 따라 줘요.\" 그루센카가 말했다. \"나도 러시아를 위해 마시겠어요!\"\n\n\"나도요.\" 칼가노프가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 마시지요… 러시아를 위하여, 늙은 할머니를 위하여!\" 막시모프가 킬킬거렸다.\n\n\"모두! 모두!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"트리폰 보리소비치, 술 좀 더 가져오시오!\"\n\n미탸가 가지고 온 나머지 세 병이 탁자 위에 놓였다. 미탸가 잔을 채웠다.\n\n\"러시아를 위하여! 만세!\" 그가 다시 소리쳤다. 폴란드인들을 빼고는 모두가 건배를 들이켰고, 그루센카는 제 잔을 단숨에 쭉 비웠다. 폴란드인들은 자기들 잔에 손도 대지 않았다.\n\n\"이게 어찌 된 일이오, 여러분?\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"마시지 않겠소?\"\n\n브루블레프스키 씨가 잔을 들어 치켜올리더니 우렁찬 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"1772년 이전의 러시아를 위하여.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 그게 낫구려!\" 다른 폴란드인이 외쳤고, 두 사람이 단숨에 잔을 비웠다.\n\n\"당신네는 바보들이오, 여러분.\" 미탸의 입에서 별안간 이런 말이 튀어나왔다.\n\n\"여보시오!\" 두 폴란드인이 한 쌍의 수탉처럼 미탸에게 달려들 듯 위협적으로 외쳤다. 브루블레프스키 씨가 특히 길길이 날뛰었다.\n\n\"제 조국을 사랑하는 게 어찌 잘못이란 말이오?\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"닥쳐요! 다투지 말아요! 다투는 건 못 봐줘요!\" 그루센카가 위압적으로 외치며 발로 바닥을 쾅 굴렀다. 그녀의 얼굴이 달아오르고 눈이 빛났다. 방금 마신 술기운이 역력했다. 미탸는 끔찍하게 당황했다.\n\n\"여러분, 용서하시오! 내 잘못이었소, 미안하오. 브루블레프스키, 브루블레프스키 씨, 미안하오.\"\n\n\"당신부터 입 좀 다물어요, 아무튼! 앉아요, 이 멍청한 양반아!\" 그루센카가 성난 짜증을 내며 나무랐다.\n\n모두가 자리에 앉았고, 다들 입을 다문 채 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"여러분, 이게 다 내 탓이었소.\" 미탸가 그루센카의 말뜻을 도무지 헤아리지 못한 채 다시 입을 열었다. \"자, 우리가 왜 이렇게 앉아만 있는 거요? 무얼 하면… 우리 다시 즐겨 볼까요?\"\n\n\"아흐, 정말이지 도무지 즐겁지가 않네요!\" 칼가노프가 나른하게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아까처럼 다시 파로 게임을 합시다.\" 막시모프가 별안간 킬킬거렸다.\n\n\"파로? 좋소!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"여러분만 좋다면야—\"\n\n\"늦었소이다, 여러분.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 마지못한 듯 응수했다.\n\n\"맞는 말이오.\" 브루블레프스키 씨가 동의했다.\n\n\"늦었다고요? '늦었다'니 무슨 말이에요?\" 그루센카가 물었다.\n\n\"늦은 시각이라는 말이외다, 마님! '깊은 밤'이라는 뜻이지요.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 설명했다.\n\n\"이 사람들은 늘 늦었다지 뭐예요. 도무지 아무것도 못 한다니까요!\" 그루센카가 성이 나서 거의 비명을 지르듯 했다. \"자기들이 따분하니까 남들도 따분하길 바라는 거예요. 당신이 오기 전에도, 미탸, 이 사람들은 꼭 저렇게 입을 다물고 나한테 콧대만 세우고 있었다니까요.\"\n\n\"내 여신이여!\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 외쳤다. \"당신이 나를 탐탁잖게 여기는 걸 알겠소이다, 그래서 내가 우울한 거외다. 나는 준비됐소, 여보시오.\" 그가 미탸를 향해 덧붙였다.\n\n\"시작합시다, 여보시오.\" 미탸가 동의하며 주머니에서 지폐를 꺼내 백 루블짜리 두 장을 탁자 위에 내려놓았다. \"나는 당신에게 잔뜩 잃고 싶소. 패를 잡으시오. 물주를 맡으시오.\"\n\n\"여관 주인한테서 패를 가져옵시다, 여보시오.\" 작은 폴란드인이 진지하고 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"그게 훨씬 좋은 방법이외다.\" 브루블레프스키 씨가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"여관 주인한테서? 좋소, 알겠소, 그한테서 가져옵시다. 카드!\" 미탸가 여관 주인에게 소리쳤다.\n\n여관 주인이 새것 그대로 봉하지 않은 패 한 벌을 들고 들어와, 처녀들이 채비를 차리고 있고 심벌즈를 든 유대인들도 곧 당도할 듯하다고 미탸에게 일러 주었으나, 먹거리를 실은 짐수레는 아직 도착하지 않았다고 했다. 미탸가 탁자에서 벌떡 일어나 분부를 내리러 옆방으로 달려갔지만, 처녀는 셋만 와 있을 뿐 마리야는 아직 없었다. 게다가 그는 무슨 분부를 내려야 할지, 무엇 때문에 달려 나왔는지 자기도 알지 못했다. 그는 다만 상자에서 처녀들에게 줄 선물들, 사탕과 토피와 봉봉을 꺼내라고만 일렀다. \"그리고 안드레이한테 보드카를, 안드레이한테 보드카를!\" 그가 다급하게 외쳤다. \"내가 안드레이한테 무례하게 굴었지!\"\n\n별안간 그를 따라 나온 막시모프가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다.\n\n\"오 루블만 주세요.\" 그가 미탸에게 속삭였다. \"나도 파로에 좀 걸어 보게요, 헤헤!\"\n\n\"좋고말고요! 훌륭하오! 십 루블 받으시오, 여기!\"\n\n그는 다시 주머니에서 지폐를 죄다 꺼내 십 루블짜리 한 장을 골랐다. \"그걸 잃거든 또 오시오, 또 와요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 막시모프가 기쁘게 속삭이고는 다시 안으로 달려 들어갔다. 미탸도 그들을 기다리게 해서 미안하다고 사과하며 돌아왔다. 폴란드인들은 이미 자리에 앉아 패를 펼쳐 놓고 있었다. 그들은 한결 상냥해진, 거의 다정한 기색이었다. 소파에 앉은 폴란드인은 또 다른 파이프에 불을 붙이고 패를 던질 채비를 하고 있었다. 그는 엄숙한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다.\n\n\"자리에들 앉으시오, 여러분.\" 브루블레프스키 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니요, 나는 더 안 할 겁니다.\" 칼가노프가 한마디 했다. \"방금 이 사람들한테 오십 루블을 잃었거든요.\"\n\n\"이 양반은 운이 없었던 거외다, 어쩌면 이번엔 운이 트일지도 모르지요.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 그쪽을 향해 한마디 했다.\n\n\"물주 판돈은 얼마요? 거기에 맞추겠소.\" 미탸가 물었다.\n\n\"형편 닿는 대로지요, 여보시오, 백이 될 수도, 이백이 될 수도 있소, 당신이 거는 만큼 말이오.\"\n\n\"백만!\" 미탸가 웃었다.\n\n\"대위 양반은 어쩌면 포드비소츠키 씨에 대해 들어 보셨을지 모르겠소?\"\n\n\"포드비소츠키가 누구요?\"\n\n\"바르샤바에 도박판이 하나 있었는데, 누구든 와서 물주를 상대로 돈을 거는 거외다. 포드비소츠키가 와서 금화 천 닢을 보고는 물주를 상대로 걸었지요. 물주가 말하기를, '포드비소츠키 씨, 금화를 내려놓으시려오, 아니면 우리가 당신 명예를 믿어야 하오?' '내 명예를 믿으시오, 여보시오' 하고 포드비소츠키가 말했소. '그게 더 좋소.' 물주가 주사위를 던졌소. 포드비소츠키가 이겼지요. '가져가시오, 여보시오' 하고 물주가 말하며 서랍을 빼서 그에게 백만을 내주었소. '가져가시오, 여보시오, 이게 당신이 딴 거외다.' 도박판에 백만이 걸려 있었던 거지요. '난 그런 줄 몰랐소' 하고 포드비소츠키가 말했소. '포드비소츠키 씨,' 물주가 말하기를, '당신은 명예를 걸었고 우리도 명예를 걸었소이다.' 포드비소츠키가 백만을 가져갔지요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이 아니오.\" 칼가노프가 말했다.\n\n\"칼가노프 씨, 신사들 사이에서는 그런 말을 하는 게 아니외다.\"\n\n\"폴란드 노름꾼이 백만을 거저 내줬다니, 어림도 없는 소리지!\" 미탸가 외쳤다가 곧장 스스로를 다잡았다. \"용서하시오, 여보시오, 또 내 잘못이오, 그렇소, 그는 내줬을 거요, 명예를 위해, 폴란드의 명예를 위해서라면 백만을 내줬을 거요. 보시오, 내가 이렇게 폴란드 말을 한다오, 하하! 자, 나는 십 루블을 걸겠소, 잭을 내겠소.\"\n\n\"그러면 나는 퀸에다 일 루블을 걸겠어요, 하트 퀸에다, 그 어여쁜 아가씨한테요, 헤헤!\" 막시모프가 자기 퀸을 꺼내며 웃었고, 마치 모두에게 그것을 감추려는 듯 바짝 다가앉아 탁자 밑에서 황급히 성호를 그었다. 미탸가 이겼다. 일 루블도 이겼다.\n\n\"코너!\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n\"나는 일 루블 더 걸겠소, '단일' 판돈으로요.\" 막시모프가 일 루블을 딴 것에 잔뜩 신이 나서 기쁘게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"잃었소!\" 미탸가 소리쳤다. \"칠에다 '더블'!\"\n\n칠도 잡아먹혔다.\n\n\"그만!\" 칼가노프가 별안간 외쳤다.\n\n\"더블! 더블!\" 미탸가 판돈을 곱절로 올렸고, 곱절로 올릴 때마다 그가 곱절을 건 패는 폴란드인들에게 잡아먹혔다. 일 루블짜리 판돈은 계속 이겼다.\n\n\"곱절로!\" 미탸가 길길이 날뛰며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이백을 잃으셨소, 여보시오. 백을 더 거시겠소?\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 물었다.\n\n\"뭐요? 벌써 이백을 잃었다고? 그럼 또 이백! 죄다 곱절로!\"\n\n그러고서 미탸가 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 이백 루블을 퀸에다 내던지려는데, 칼가노프가 손으로 그것을 덮었다.\n\n\"그만하면 됐어요!\" 그가 낭랑한 목소리로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 미탸가 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"그만하면 됐어요! 더는 노름 안 했으면 좋겠어요. 하지 말아요!\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"내가 싫으니까요. 젠장, 그만 일어나요. 그래서예요. 더는 노름하게 놔두지 않겠어요.\"\n\n미탸가 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그만둬요, 미탸. 이 사람 말이 옳을지도 몰라요. 이미 많이 잃었잖아요.\" 그루센카가 묘한 어조를 담아 말했다. 폴란드인 둘이 몹시 모욕당한 기색으로 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"농담이오, 여보시오?\" 작은 사내가 칼가노프를 엄하게 노려보며 말했다.\n\n\"네놈이 감히!\" 브루블레프스키 씨도 칼가노프에게 으르렁댔다.\n\n\"감히 그렇게 소리 지르지 말아요.\" 그루센카가 외쳤다. \"아휴, 이 칠면조 수컷들 같으니!\"\n\n미탸가 그들을 차례로 둘러보았다. 그러나 그루센카의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 별안간 그의 마음을 쳤고, 바로 그 순간 무언가 새로운 것이 그의 머릿속에 번뜩였으니—기이하고도 새로운 생각이었다!\n\n\"아그리피나 마님,\" 작은 폴란드인이 분노로 시뻘게진 얼굴로 입을 떼는데, 미탸가 별안간 그에게 다가가 어깨를 툭 쳤다.\n\n\"더없이 고명하신 분, 당신과 두 마디만 합시다.\"\n\n\"무얼 원하시오?\"\n\n\"옆방에서, 당신과 두 마디 나눌 게 있소, 무언가 흐뭇한, 아주 흐뭇한 일이오. 듣고 나면 기뻐할 거요.\"\n\n작은 양반이 흠칫 놀라 미탸를 미심쩍게 바라보았다. 그래도 그는 브루블레프스키 씨가 함께 간다는 조건으로 곧 응했다.\n\n\"경호원 말이오? 같이 옵시다, 나도 그가 함께 갔으면 하오. 그가 꼭 있어야 하오!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"갑시다, 여러분!\"\n\n\"어디로 가는 거예요?\" 그루센카가 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"금방 돌아올 거요.\" 미탸가 대답했다.\n\n그의 눈에는 무언가 대담함이, 별안간 솟은 자신감이 빛나고 있었다. 한 시간 전 이 방에 들어섰을 때 그의 얼굴은 사뭇 달라 보였더랬다.\n\n그는 폴란드인들을 처녀들의 합창대가 모여들고 식탁이 차려지고 있는 큰 방이 아니라, 오른편 침실로 데려갔으니, 그곳에는 트렁크와 짐 꾸러미가 놓여 있고, 큼직한 침대 두 개에 솜 베개가 저마다 피라미드처럼 쌓여 있었다. 구석의 자그마한 전나무 탁자 위에는 불 켜진 양초가 놓여 있었다. 작은 사내와 미탸가 이 탁자에 마주 보고 앉았고, 거구의 브루블레프스키는 뒷짐을 진 채 그들 곁에 섰다. 폴란드인들은 엄한 표정이었으나 역력히 궁금해하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 여보시오?\" 작은 폴란드인이 혀짤배기소리로 물었다.\n\n\"자, 이보시오, 여보시오, 길게 붙들지 않겠소. 당신한테 줄 돈이 있소.\" 그가 지폐를 꺼냈다. \"삼천 루블, 어떻소? 그걸 받고 당신 갈 길을 가시오.\"\n\n폴란드인이 캐묻는 눈초리로 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 미탸를 바라보았다.\n\n\"삼천 루블이라, 여보시오?\" 그가 브루블레프스키와 눈빛을 주고받았다.\n\n\"삼천이오, 여러분, 삼천! 들어 보시오, 여보시오, 당신이 분별 있는 사람인 걸 알겠소. 삼천을 받고 악마한테로 꺼지시오, 브루블레프스키도 함께—알겠소? 다만, 당장, 바로 이 순간, 그리고 영원히 말이오. 알아들었소, 여보시오, 영원히. 여기 문이 있소, 그리로 나가면 되오. 거기 무얼 가졌소, 외투, 모피 코트? 내가 가져다주겠소. 곧 말을 끌어낼 테니, 그러고는—잘 가시오, 여보시오!\"\n\n미탸가 자신만만하게 대답을 기다렸다. 그는 일말의 의심도 없었다. 폴란드인의 얼굴에 비상한 결연함의 빛이 스쳐 갔다.\n\n\"그래서 돈은, 여보시오?\"\n\n\"돈 말이오, 여보시오? 노자로, 그리고 첫 몫으로 오백 루블을 이 순간 당신에게 주겠소, 그리고 나머지 이천오백은 내일, 읍내에서—내 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 무슨 수를 써서라도 마련하겠소, 마련하고말고!\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n폴란드인들이 다시 눈빛을 주고받았다. 작은 사내의 얼굴이 한층 험상궂어 보였다.\n\n\"칠백, 칠백이오, 오백이 아니라, 당장, 이 순간, 현찰로!\" 미탸가 무언가 잘못되어 가는 것을 느끼며 덧붙였다. \"무슨 일이오, 여보시오? 나를 못 믿겠소? 삼천을 한꺼번에 다 줄 수는 없소. 그걸 다 주면 당신이 내일 그녀한테 되돌아올지도 모르잖소…. 게다가 지금 수중에 삼천이 없소. 읍내 집에 두고 왔소.\" 미탸가 자기 입에서 말이 한 마디씩 나올 때마다 기가 꺾인 채 더듬거렸다. \"맹세코, 돈은 거기 있소, 감춰 두었소.\"\n\n순식간에 비상한 자존감이 작은 사내의 얼굴에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그래서 어쩌라는 거요?\" 그가 비꼬듯 물었다. \"수치스러운 줄 아시오!\" 그러고는 바닥에 침을 뱉었다. 브루블레프스키 씨도 침을 뱉었다.\n\n\"당신이 그렇게 구는 건, 여보시오,\" 미탸가 절망 속에 모든 것이 끝났음을 깨달으며 말했다. \"그루센카한테서 더 많이 우려낼 속셈이기 때문이지? 당신네는 한 쌍의 거세된 수탉들이오, 그게 바로 당신네 꼴이오!\"\n\n\"이건 죽도록 모욕적인 일이오!\" 작은 폴란드인이 게처럼 시뻘게져서는, 한 마디도 더 들을 수 없다는 듯 잰걸음으로 방을 나갔다. 브루블레프스키가 그 뒤를 따라 휭하니 나갔고, 미탸도 당황하여 풀이 죽은 채 따라 나섰다. 그는 그루센카가 두려웠고, 폴란드 양반이 당장 고함을 질러 댈까 봐 두려웠다. 과연 그가 그렇게 했다. 폴란드인이 방 안으로 걸어 들어와 그루센카 앞에서 연극조의 자세를 취했다.\n\n\"아그리피나 마님, 나는 죽도록 모욕당했소!\" 그가 외쳤다. 그러나 그루센카는 별안간 인내심을 잃었으니, 마치 그들이 그녀의 가장 여린 데를 찔러 상처라도 입힌 듯했다.\n\n\"러시아 말로 해요! 러시아 말로 하라고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"폴란드 말은 한 마디도 더 하지 말아요! 전에는 러시아 말을 했잖아요. 오 년 사이에 잊어버렸을 리가 없잖아요.\"\n\n그녀가 격정으로 시뻘게져 있었다.\n\n\"아그리피나 마님—\"\n\n\"내 이름은 아그라페나, 그루센카예요, 러시아 말로 하든지 아니면 듣지 않겠어요!\"\n\n폴란드인이 모욕당한 자존심에 숨을 헐떡이더니, 서툰 러시아어로 빠르고 거드름스럽게 떠벌렸다.\n\n\"아그라페나 마님, 나는 지난날을 잊고 그것을 용서하려고, 오늘까지 일어난 모든 일을 잊으려고 여기로 온 것이오—\"\n\n\"용서한다고요? 나를 용서하러 여기 왔다고요?\" 그루센카가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 그의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"바로 그렇소, 마님, 나는 옹졸한 사람이 아니라 도량이 넓은 사람이오. 허나 당신의 정부(情夫)들을 보고 나는 아연실색했소. 미탸 양반이 옆방에서 내게 삼천 루블을 줄 테니 떠나라고 하더이다. 나는 그 양반 면상에 침을 뱉었소.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 이이가 나 때문에 당신한테 돈을 줬다고요?\" 그루센카가 히스테릭하게 외쳤다. \"사실이에요, 미탸? 어떻게 감히 그래요? 내가 팔아 치울 물건이라도 돼요?\"\n\n\"여보시오, 여보시오!\" 미탸가 소리쳤다. \"그녀는 순결하고 빛나는 사람이오, 나는 한 번도 그녀의 정부였던 적이 없소! 그건 거짓말이오….\"\n\n\"어떻게 감히 저자 앞에서 날 두둔하는 거예요?\" 그루센카가 비명을 질렀다. \"내가 순결을 지킨 건 미덕 때문도 아니었고, 쿠지마가 두려워서도 아니었어요, 그저 저자를 만났을 때 고개를 빳빳이 들고 저자더러 비열한 놈이라고 말해 주고 싶어서였다고요. 그래, 저자가 정말로 그 돈을 거절했다고요?\"\n\n\"받았소! 받았다고요!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"다만 삼천을 한꺼번에 다 받으려 했을 뿐이오, 그런데 나는 당장은 칠백밖에 줄 수가 없었던 거요.\"\n\n\"알겠어요. 저자가 나한테 돈이 있다는 소릴 듣고, 나랑 결혼하려고 여기 온 거였군요!\"\n\n\"아그리피나 마님!\" 작은 폴란드인이 외쳤다. \"나는—기사요, 나는—귀족이지 비열한 놈이 아니오. 나는 당신을 아내로 삼으려고 여기 왔건만, 보니 당신은 영 딴사람이 되어 있구려, 비뚤어지고 부끄러움을 모르는 여자로 말이오.\"\n\n\"오, 당신 온 데로 도로 돌아가요! 사람을 시켜 당신을 쫓아내라고 할 테니, 쫓겨나게 될 거예요.\" 그루센카가 길길이 날뛰며 외쳤다. \"내가 바보였어요, 바보였다고요, 오 년 동안이나 비참해하다니! 그것도 저자 때문이 아니라, 내 분노 때문에 비참했던 거예요. 그런데 이건 저 사람이 전혀 아니에요! 저 사람이 이런 모습이었던가요? 저 사람 아버지뻘이나 되겠네! 그 가발은 어디서 구해 왔어요? 그 사람은 매였는데, 이건 수거위잖아요. 그 사람은 나한테 웃고 노래를 불러 줬는데…. 그런데 나는 오 년 동안이나 울었으니, 빌어먹을 바보 같으니, 비굴하고 부끄러움도 모르는 계집이었어!\"\n\n그녀는 나지막한 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다. 바로 그 순간, 왼편 방에서 모크로예의 합창대가 노래를 부르기 시작했으니—흥겹게 들썩이는 춤 노래였다.\n\n\"완전히 소돔이로구먼!\" 브루블레프스키가 별안간 고함쳤다. \"주인장, 저 부끄러움도 모르는 계집들을 내보내시오!\"\n\n여관 주인은 한동안 문틈으로 궁금한 듯 안을 엿보고 있었는데, 고함 소리를 듣고 손님들이 다투고 있음을 짐작하고는 곧장 방 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"뭣 때문에 고래고래 소리를 지르는 거요? 목구멍이라도 찢어 놓을 작정이오?\" 그가 놀라우리만치 무례하게 브루블레프스키를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"짐승 같은 놈!\" 브루블레프스키 씨가 으르렁댔다.\n\n\"짐승 같은 놈이라고? 그러는 당신은 방금 무슨 패로 노름을 한 거요? 내가 새 패 한 벌을 줬는데 당신이 그걸 감췄잖소. 표시해 둔 패로 노름을 한 거요! 가짜 패로 노름한 죄로 당신을 시베리아로 보낼 수도 있소, 그거 아시오, 그건 가짜 지폐랑 똑같은 거니까….\"\n\n그러고는 소파로 다가가 등받이와 방석 사이에 손가락을 찔러 넣어 봉하지 않은 패 한 벌을 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"여기 내 패가 봉한 그대로 있소!\"\n\n그가 그것을 치켜들어 방 안의 모두에게 내보였다. \"내가 서 있던 데서, 저자가 내 패를 슬쩍 빼돌리고 그 자리에 제 패를 끼워 넣는 걸 봤단 말이오—당신은 사기꾼이지 신사가 아니오!\"\n\n\"나도 이 양반이 패를 바꿔치는 걸 두 번이나 봤어요!\" 칼가노프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"부끄러워라! 부끄러워라!\" 그루센카가 두 손을 마주 잡고 진정 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 저 사람이 저런 지경까지 됐다니!\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각했소!\" 미탸가 말했다. 그러나 그가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 브루블레프스키가 당황하고 격분한 얼굴로 그루센카에게 주먹을 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이 천한 갈보 같으니!\"\n\n미탸가 곧장 그에게 달려들어 두 손으로 그를 움켜쥐고는 번쩍 들어 올려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 방금 둘이 나왔던 오른편 방으로 그를 옮겨다 놓았다.\n\n\"저자를 저쪽 바닥에 눕혀 놨소.\" 그가 흥분에 헐떡이며 곧장 돌아와 알렸다. \"버둥거리고 있소, 저 비열한 놈이! 하지만 다시 나오진 못할 거요, 그건 염려 마시오!…\"\n\n그는 여닫이문의 한쪽을 닫고, 다른 한쪽을 빠끔히 연 채 작은 폴란드인을 향해 소리쳤다.\n\n\"더없이 고명하신 분, 당신도 물러가 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리,\" 트리폰 보리소비치가 말했다. \"잃으신 돈을 도로 받아 내십시오. 그건 나리한테서 훔쳐 간 거나 다름없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 오십 루블은 도로 받고 싶지 않아요.\" 칼가노프가 별안간 선언했다.\n\n\"내 이백도 받고 싶지 않소.\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 받지 않겠소! 위로 삼아 그자가 갖게 놔둡시다.\"\n\n\"잘했어요, 미탸! 당신은 정말 멋진 사람이에요, 미탸!\" 그루센카가 외쳤는데, 그 외침에는 사나운 분노의 가락이 서려 있었다.\n\n작은 양반은 분노로 시뻘게졌으면서도 여전히 제 위엄을 잊지 않은 채 문 쪽으로 향하다가, 별안간 멈춰 서더니 그루센카를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"마님, 나와 함께 가고 싶으면 갑시다. 아니라면, 잘 있으시오.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 분개와 거드름으로 잔뜩 부풀어 문 쪽으로 갔다. 이자는 줏대 있는 인간이었다. 그는 자기 자신을 어찌나 높이 여겼던지, 그 모든 일이 있고 난 뒤에도 여전히 그녀가 자기와 결혼하리라 기대하고 있었던 것이다. 미탸가 그의 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다.\n\n\"잠가요.\" 칼가노프가 말했다. 그러나 열쇠가 저쪽에서 찰칵 돌아갔으니, 그들이 안에서 문을 잠가 버린 것이었다.\n\"잘됐네요!\" 그루센카가 매정하게 외쳤다. \"그래도 싸지!\"\n\n* 1772년: 제1차 폴란드 분할이 일어난 해로, 이후 폴란드는 러시아·프로이센·오스트리아에 의해 영토가 잠식되어 끝내 소멸했다. 브루블레프스키의 건배는 분할 이전의 온전한 폴란드를 기리는 것으로, 러시아에 대한 폴란드 민족주의자의 적개심을 드러낸다.\n\n# Chapter VIII. Delirium\n\n그 뒤에 이어진 것은 거의 광란의 잔치였으니, 누구든 환영받는 향연이었다. 술을 가장 먼저 청한 사람은 그루셴카였다.\n\n\"마시고 싶어요. 예전처럼 흠뻑 취하고 싶어요. 기억나요, 미탸, 우리가 지난번에 여기서 얼마나 의기투합했는지 기억나요!\"\n\n미탸 자신도 거의 정신이 나가 있었으니, 자신의 행복이 손에 잡힐 듯 가까이 있음을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 그루셴카는 끊임없이 그를 제 곁에서 멀리 내쫓았다.\n\n\"가서 즐겨요. 사람들한테 춤추라고, 흥겹게 놀라고 일러요, '난로도 오두막도 다 춤추게' 하라고요. 지난번에 그랬던 것처럼요.\" 그녀는 연신 이렇게 외쳐 댔다. 그녀는 어마어마하게 들떠 있었다. 미탸는 서둘러 그녀의 말에 따랐다. 합창대는 옆방에 있었다. 그때까지 그들이 앉아 있던 방은 너무 좁았는데, 무명천 커튼으로 둘로 나뉘어 있었고, 그 뒤에는 푹신한 깃털 매트리스와 무명 베개를 피라미드처럼 쌓아 올린 거대한 침대가 놓여 있었다. 손님용으로 마련된 네 개의 방에는 침대가 갖춰져 있었다. 그루셴카는 바로 문가에 자리를 잡았다. 미탸가 그녀에게 안락의자를 가져다주었다. 그녀는 \"지난번\"에 그들이 여기서 흥겹게 놀았을 때에도 춤과 노래를 구경하느라 바로 그 자리에 앉아 있었다. 그때 왔던 처녀들이 모두 그 자리에 있었고, 바이올린과 치터를 든 유대인 악단도 와 있었으며, 마침내 그토록 고대하던, 술과 음식을 실은 짐수레가 당도했던 것이다.\n\n미탸는 부산스레 돌아다녔다. 온갖 부류의 사람들이 구경하러 방 안으로 몰려들기 시작했으니, 잠에서 깨어난 농부들과 그 아낙들이었는데, 한 달 전에 누렸던 것 같은 또 한 번의 기막힌 잔치를 바라는 마음에 이끌려 온 것이었다. 미탸는 그들의 얼굴을 알아보고, 아는 사람마다 일일이 인사를 건네고 끌어안았다. 그는 병마개를 따서 자기 앞에 나서는 사람마다 술을 따라 주었다. 다만 샴페인을 몹시 탐낸 것은 처녀들뿐이었다. 사내들은 럼주와 브랜디, 무엇보다도 뜨거운 펀치를 더 좋아했다. 미탸는 처녀들 모두에게 초콜릿을 만들어 주게 했고, 사모바르 세 개를 밤새도록 끓여 두어 누구든 차와 펀치를 마음껏 마실 수 있게 하라고 일렀다.\n\n터무니없고 어수선한 혼란이 뒤따랐으나, 미탸는 제 본바탕의 물 만난 격이었으니, 그 꼴이 어리석어질수록 그의 기분은 더욱 들떠 올랐다. 만약 그 순간 농부들이 그에게 돈을 달라고 했다면, 그는 지폐를 꺼내어 좌우로 마구 뿌려 주었을 것이다. 아마 그래서였을 것이니, 여관 주인 트리폰 보리소비치는 미탸를 지키느라 그의 주위를 줄곧 맴돌았다. 그는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들 생각을 아예 접은 듯했으나, 정작 술은 거의 마시지 않아 펀치 한 잔만 들었을 뿐이고, 제 나름의 방식으로 미탸의 잇속에 날카롭게 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그는 적절한 때에 끼어들어, 공손하고 굽실거리는 태도로 미탸에게 예전처럼 \"시가와 라인산 포도주\"를, 무엇보다도 돈을 농부들에게 뿌리지 말라고 타일렀다. 그는 또한 농가 처녀들이 리큐어를 마시고 사탕을 먹는 것에도 몹시 분개했다.\n\n\"저것들은 하찮은 무리입니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 그가 말했다. \"저런 것들은 죄다 발길로 한 번 걷어차 주면, 오히려 그걸 영광으로 여길 겁니다요. 딱 그만한 값어치밖에 안 되는 것들이죠!\"\n\n미탸는 다시금 안드레이를 떠올리고, 그에게 펀치를 내보내라고 일렀다. \"방금 그자한테 무례했어.\" 그는 가라앉고 누그러진 목소리로 거듭 중얼거렸다. 칼가노프는 술을 마시고 싶어 하지 않았고, 처음에는 처녀들의 노래에도 관심이 없었으나, 샴페인을 두어 잔 들이켜고 나자 유난히 활기가 돌아, 방 안을 거닐며 웃고 음악과 노래를 칭찬하고 모든 사람과 모든 것을 감탄하며 보았다. 더할 나위 없이 행복하게 취한 막시모프는 그의 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았다. 그루셴카 역시 취기가 오르기 시작했다. 그녀는 칼가노프를 가리키며 미탸에게 말했다.\n\n\"저 애 참 귀엽고 사랑스러운 도련님이지요!\"\n\n그러자 미탸는 기뻐하며 달려가 칼가노프와 막시모프에게 입을 맞추었다. 오, 그의 희망은 크기도 했다! 그녀는 아직 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 실로 일부러 입을 다물고 있는 듯했다. 그러나 이따금 그녀는 어루만지는 듯한, 정열에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 마침내 그녀는 별안간 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 힘껏 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그 순간 그녀는 문가의 낮은 의자에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"방금 어떻게 들어왔어요, 응? 걸어 들어왔어요!… 난 무서웠어요. 그래서 날 저 사람한테 넘겨주려고 했던 거예요, 그랬어요? 정말로 그러고 싶었어요?\"\n\n\"당신의 행복을 망치고 싶지 않았어!\" 미탸는 행복에 겨워 더듬더듬 말했다. 그러나 그녀에게 그의 대답 따위는 필요치 않았다.\n\n\"자, 가서 즐겨요…\" 그녀는 다시 한번 그를 내쫓았다. \"울지 말아요, 다시 불러 줄 테니까.\"\n\n그는 달려가곤 했고, 그녀는 노래에 귀를 기울이고 춤을 바라보았으나, 그녀의 눈은 그가 어디로 가든 그를 좇았다. 그러나 다시 십오 분쯤 지나면 그녀는 또 한 번 그를 불렀고, 그러면 그는 또다시 그녀에게 달려가곤 했다.\n\n\"이리 와요, 내 곁에 앉아요. 말해 봐요, 어떻게 내 얘기를 들었어요, 어제 내가 여기 온 걸 말이에요? 누구한테서 처음 들었어요?\"\n\n미탸는 두서없이, 종잡을 수 없이, 열에 들뜬 채 그 모든 이야기를 그녀에게 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그는 이상하게 이야기했으니, 자주 이맛살을 찌푸리고 불쑥불쑥 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"뭘 그렇게 찡그려요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니야… 거기 한 사람을 앓아눕게 하고 왔어. 그자가 다 나아서 무사하다는 걸 알 수만 있다면 내 수명을 십 년이라도 내놓겠어!\"\n\n\"그자가 앓건 말건 신경 쓰지 말아요. 그러니까 당신은 내일 자기를 쏘려고 했단 말이지요! 어쩌면 그렇게 어리석은 도련님일까! 무엇 때문에요? 난 당신처럼 막무가내인 사람이 좋아요.\" 그녀는 다소 꼬부라진 혀로 혀짤배기소리를 했다. \"그러니까 나를 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 하겠다는 거지요, 응? 정말로 내일 자기를 쏘려고 했어요, 이 바보 같은 사람아? 아니, 잠깐만 기다려요. 내일은 당신한테 할 말이 있을지도 몰라요… 오늘은 안 하고 내일 할 거예요. 당신은 오늘 듣고 싶지요? 아니에요, 난 오늘은 싫어요. 자, 이제 가요, 가서 즐겨요.\"\n\n그러나 한번은 그녀가 그를 불렀는데, 어쩐지 어리둥절하고 불안해 보였다.\n\n\"왜 슬퍼해요? 슬퍼 보여요… 그래요, 보여요.\" 그녀는 그의 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 덧붙였다. \"당신이 자꾸 농부들한테 입을 맞추고 소리를 질러 대지만, 난 뭔가가 보여요. 아니, 즐거워해요. 난 즐거우니까, 당신도 즐거워해야 해요… 난 여기서 어떤 사람을 사랑해요. 그게 누군지 알아맞혀 봐요. 아, 봐요, 우리 도련님이 잠들었네, 가엾기도 하지, 취했어요.\"\n\n그녀가 가리킨 것은 칼가노프였다. 그는 실제로 취해 소파에 앉은 채 잠시 잠이 들었던 것이다. 그러나 그는 단지 술기운에 졸린 것만이 아니었으니, 갑자기 의기소침해진, 그 자신의 말로는 \"따분한\" 기분이 들었던 것이다. 그는 처녀들의 노래에 몹시 우울해졌는데, 그 노래는 술판이 무르익을수록 점점 더 천박하고 무분별해졌다. 춤도 그에 못지않게 형편없었다. 처녀 둘이 곰으로 분장하고, 스테파니다라는 활달한 처녀가 손에 막대기를 들고 곰 부리는 사람 역을 맡아 \"곰들을 부리기\" 시작했다.\n\n\"기운차게 굴어, 마리야, 안 그러면 막대기로 한 대 맞을 줄 알아!\"\n\n마침내 곰들은 빽빽이 들어찬 남녀 무리의 폭소 속에서 더없이 꼴사나운 모양으로 바닥을 뒹굴었다.\n\n\"자, 내버려 둬요! 내버려 두라니까!\" 그루셴카는 황홀한 표정을 얼굴에 담은 채 격언이라도 읊듯 말했다. \"저들도 하루쯤 즐길 날을 얻었는데, 사람들이 행복해하면 안 될 게 뭐예요?\"\n\n칼가노프는 마치 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"이런 농부들의 짓거리는 죄다 돼지 같아.\" 그는 자리를 옮기며 중얼거렸다. \"여름에 밤새도록 환할 때나 하는 놀음이지.\"\n\n그가 특히 싫어한 것은 경쾌한 춤곡에 맞춘 어느 \"새\" 노래였다. 그 노래는 어떤 나리가 와서 처녀들이 자기를 사랑할지 어떨지 운을 떼어 보는 내용이었다.\n\n나리가 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?\n\n그러나 처녀들은 나리를 사랑할 수 없었으니.\n\n저이는 날 모질게 때릴 테니 그런 사랑은 내겐 싫어라.\n\n그다음에는 집시가 와서 그 역시 떠보았다.\n\n집시가 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?\n\n그러나 처녀들은 집시도 사랑할 수 없었으니.\n\n저이는 도둑이 될까 두려워 내게 눈물깨나 흘리게 할 테지.\n\n그리고 더 많은 사내들이 운을 떼어 보러 왔는데, 그중에는 군인도 있었다.\n\n군인이 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?\n\n그러나 군인은 외설스러운 두 행의 노랫말로 경멸 속에 퇴짜를 맞았는데, 그 노랫말은 거리낌 없이 적나라하게 불려 청중을 열광시켰다. 노래는 상인으로 끝을 맺는다.\n\n상인이 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?\n\n그리고 그가 처녀들의 사랑을 얻는 것으로 드러나는데, 그 까닭인즉.\n\n상인은 내게 금을 벌어다 주리니 나는 기꺼이 그의 여왕이 되리라.\n\n칼가노프는 단단히 분개했다.\n\n\"저런 건 그저 어제 만든 노래일 뿐이야.\" 그가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"누가 저들을 위해 저런 걸 짓는담? 철도 인부나 유대인을 불러내어 처녀들에게 운을 떼게 했어도 매한가지였을걸. 그자들이 죄다 휩쓸어 갔겠지.\"\n\n그러고는 마치 개인적인 모욕이라도 당한 듯, 그는 그 자리에서 따분하다고 선언하더니, 소파에 앉아 이내 잠들어 버렸다. 그의 예쁘장한 작은 얼굴은 소파 쿠션에 젖혀지자 다소 핼쑥해 보였다.\n\n\"이것 봐요, 얼마나 예쁜지.\" 그루셴카가 미탸를 그에게 데려가며 말했다. \"방금 내가 이 애 머리를 빗겨 줬는데, 머리칼이 아마 실 같고 어찌나 숱이 많은지…\"\n\n그러고는 다정하게 그에게 몸을 굽혀 그의 이마에 입을 맞추었다. 칼가노프는 그 즉시 눈을 뜨고 그녀를 바라보더니, 일어나 더없이 근심스러운 표정으로 막시모프가 어디 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신이 찾는 게 바로 그 사람이로군요.\" 그루셴카가 웃었다. \"잠깐만 내 곁에 있어요. 미탸, 가서 이 애의 막시모프를 찾아 줘요.\"\n\n알고 보니 막시모프는 처녀들 곁을 도무지 떠나지 못하고, 이따금씩만 빠져나와 리큐어를 한 잔씩 따라 마실 뿐이었다. 그는 초콜릿을 두 잔이나 마셨다. 그의 얼굴은 붉었고, 코는 진홍빛이었으며, 눈은 축축하니 느끼하리만치 달콤했다. 그는 달려와 자기가 \"사부티에르\"를 추겠노라고 알렸다.\n\n\"어렸을 때 그 점잖고 귀족적인 춤들을 죄다 배웠지요…\"\n\n\"가요, 저이랑 같이 가요, 미탸, 난 여기서 저이가 춤추는 걸 구경할 테니.\" 그루셴카가 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 나도 구경하러 갈래요.\" 칼가노프가 곁에 앉아 있으라는 그루셴카의 권유를 더없이 천진하게 뿌리치며 외쳤다. 모두들 구경하러 갔다. 막시모프는 자기 춤을 추었다. 그러나 그것은 미탸 외에는 아무에게서도 큰 감탄을 자아내지 못했다. 그것은 그저 깡충깡충 뛰고 발을 차올리는 것이 전부였으니, 막시모프는 한 번씩 뛸 때마다 위로 젖힌 발바닥을 철썩철썩 손바닥으로 쳤다. 칼가노프는 그것을 전혀 마음에 들어 하지 않았으나, 미탸는 그 춤꾼에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"고맙소. 혹시 지치셨소? 여기서 뭘 찾고 있소? 사탕이라도 드릴까? 시가는 어떻소?\"\n\n\"담배 한 대 주시오.\"\n\n\"술은 안 드시겠소?\"\n\n\"리큐어나 한 잔 하지요… 초콜릿은 좀 있소?\"\n\n\"그럼, 저기 탁자 위에 잔뜩 쌓여 있소. 하나 고르시오, 이 친구!\"\n\n\"난 바닐라가 든 게 좋소… 노인네들한테 어울리지. 헤헤!\"\n\n\"안 됐네, 이 사람아, 그런 특별한 건 없는데.\"\n\n\"이보시오.\" 노인이 미탸의 귀에 대고 속삭이려고 몸을 굽혔다. \"저기 저 처녀, 어린 마리야 말이오, 헤헤! 당신이 나하고 저 애를 친해지게 좀 주선해 주면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 당신 속셈이었구려! 안 되오, 이 사람아, 그건 안 될 말이지!\"\n\n\"난 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않아.\" 막시모프가 풀이 죽어 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아, 좋소, 좋아. 저 애들은 그저 춤추고 노래하러 여기 온 거요, 알겠소, 이 사람아. 하지만 에라, 잠깐만 기다리시오!… 그동안 먹고 마시고 즐기시오. 돈이 필요하진 않소?\"\n\n\"나중에라면 몰라도.\" 막시모프가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"좋소, 좋아…\"\n\n미탸의 머리는 불덩이 같았다. 그는 건물 안쪽 둘레를 따라 안마당을 굽어보며 빙 둘러쳐진 나무 발코니로 나갔다. 신선한 공기가 그를 되살려 놓았다. 그는 어두운 구석에 홀로 서 있다가, 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다. 흩어졌던 생각들이 한데 모이고, 갖가지 감각이 하나로 어우러져 그의 마음속에 갑작스러운 빛을 던졌다. 무섭고도 끔찍한 빛이었다! \"기왕 자결할 거라면, 지금이라고 안 될 게 뭐냐?\" 하는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다. \"권총을 가지러 가서 이리로 가져와, 바로 여기, 이 어둡고 더러운 구석에서 끝장내 버리는 게 어떤가?\" 거의 일 분 동안 그는 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다. 몇 시간 전, 이곳으로 달려올 때만 해도 그는 치욕에, 자기가 저지른 도둑질에, 그리고 그 피, 그 피에 쫓기고 있었다!… 그러나 그래도 그때는 그에게 더 수월했다. 그때는 모든 것이 끝나 있었으니, 그는 그녀를 잃었고 그녀를 포기했던 것이다. 그녀는 그에게서 떠나 버렸으니—오, 그때는 그의 사형 선고가 그에게 더 수월했고, 적어도 그것이 필연적이고 불가피한 일로 여겨졌으니, 그가 이 땅에 더 머무를 이유가 무엇이었겠는가?\n\n그러나 지금은? 지금이 그때와 같은가? 지금은 적어도 하나의 망령, 하나의 공포만은 끝장이 났으니, 그 첫 번째 정당한 연인, 그 숙명적인 인물이 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸던 것이다. 그 무서운 망령은 너무도 보잘것없고 너무도 우스꽝스러운 무언가로 변하여, 침실로 옮겨져 자물쇠가 채워졌다. 그것은 다시는 돌아오지 않을 것이다. 그녀는 부끄러워했고, 그녀의 눈에서 그는 이제 그녀가 누구를 사랑하는지 알 수 있었다. 이제 그는 인생을 행복하게 할 모든 것을 갖추었건만… 그러나 그는 계속 살아갈 수 없었다, 그럴 수 없었다. 오, 빌어먹을! \"오, 하느님! 제가 울타리에서 때려눕힌 그자를 되살려 주소서! 이 무서운 잔을 제게서 거두어 주소서! 주여, 당신은 저 같은 죄인들을 위해서도 기적을 행하셨나이다! 하지만 만약, 만약 그 노인이 살아 있다면? 오, 그렇다면 저 다른 치욕의 부끄러움은 제가 씻어 내겠나이다. 훔친 돈은 돌려놓겠나이다. 다시 갚겠나이다. 어떻게든 마련해서…… 그 치욕의 흔적이라곤 영원토록 제 가슴속에만 남으리라! 하지만 아니, 아니야. 오, 가당찮고 비겁한 꿈이로다! 오, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n그래도 그의 어둠 속에는 한 줄기 빛과 희망이 있었다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 방으로—그녀에게로, 그녀에게로, 영원한 그의 여왕에게로 달려 돌아갔다! 그녀의 사랑이라면 단 한순간이라도, 비록 치욕의 고뇌 속에서일망정 남은 평생을 다 바칠 만한 값어치가 있지 않은가? 이 거친 물음이 그의 가슴을 움켜쥐었다. \"그녀에게로, 오직 그녀에게로, 그녀를 보고, 그녀의 목소리를 듣고, 아무것도 생각하지 않고, 모든 것을 잊는 거다, 단 하룻밤만이라도, 한 시간만이라도, 한순간만이라도!\" 발코니에서 통로로 막 돌아서던 참에, 그는 여관 주인 트리폰 보리소비치와 마주쳤다. 미탸가 보기에 그는 침울하고 근심스러워 보였는데, 어쩐지 자기를 찾아온 듯싶었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 트리폰 보리소비치? 날 찾고 있었소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리.\" 여관 주인은 당황한 듯했다. \"제가 왜 나리를 찾겠습니까요? 어디 계셨습니까요?\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 시무룩하오? 화난 건 아니겠지, 응? 잠깐만 기다리시오, 곧 잠자리에 들게 해 주겠소…… 지금 몇 시요?\"\n\n\"세 시쯤 됐을 겁니다요. 세 시는 넘었을 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"우리도 곧 끝낼 거요. 곧 끝내겠소.\"\n\n\"천만의 말씀입니다요. 괜찮습니다요. 마음껏 즐기십시오……\"\n\n\"저 사람이 왜 저러지?\" 미탸는 한순간 의아해하다가, 처녀들이 춤추고 있는 방으로 달려 돌아갔다. 그러나 그녀는 거기 없었다. 푸른 방에도 그녀는 없었으니, 소파에 잠든 칼가노프 말고는 아무도 없었다. 미탸는 커튼 뒤를 들여다보았다—그녀는 거기 있었다. 그녀는 구석에 놓인 궤짝 위에 앉아 있었다. 앞으로 몸을 숙이고 머리와 두 팔을 바로 옆 침대에 기댄 채, 그녀는 흐느낌 소리가 새어 나가 들키지 않도록 안간힘을 다해 억누르면서 서럽게 울고 있었다. 미탸를 보자 그녀는 손짓해 그를 불렀고, 그가 그녀에게 달려가자 그녀는 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"미탸, 미탸, 난 그이를 사랑했어요, 알지요. 이 다섯 해 동안, 그 세월 내내 내가 그이를 얼마나 사랑했는지! 내가 그이를 사랑한 거였을까요, 아니면 그저 내 분노만 사랑한 걸까요? 아니, 그이를, 그이를 사랑했어요! 내가 사랑한 게 그이가 아니라 내 분노였다는 건 거짓말이에요. 미탸, 그때 난 겨우 열일곱이었어요. 그이는 내게 그렇게 다정하고 그렇게 명랑했어요. 내게 노래를 불러 주곤 했어요…… 아니, 나처럼 어리석은 계집애한테 그렇게 보였던 것뿐이겠지요…… 그런데 이제, 오, 주여, 그이는 그때 그 사람이 아니에요. 얼굴마저 그때 그 얼굴이 아니에요. 아주 딴사람이 됐어요. 알아보지도 못할 뻔했어요. 난 티모페이의 마차를 타고 여기로 오면서, 오는 내내 그이를 어떻게 만날지, 그이한테 무슨 말을 할지, 우리가 서로 어떻게 바라볼지 생각했어요. 내 영혼은 까무러칠 듯했는데, 갑자기 그이가 더러운 물 한 통을 내게 끼얹은 것만 같았어요. 그이는 마치 학교 선생처럼 내게 말했어요, 어찌나 근엄하고 학자연하던지요. 어찌나 거드름을 피우며 날 맞던지 난 입을 다물어 버렸어요. 한마디도 끼워 넣을 수가 없었어요. 처음엔 그 덩치 큰 폴란드 사람 앞이라 말하기가 부끄러운가 보다 생각했어요. 난 멍하니 그이를 바라보며 어째서 이제는 그이한테 한마디도 못 하는 걸까 의아해했어요. 분명 그이를 망쳐 놓은 건 그이 아내일 거예요. 알지요, 그이는 결혼하려고 날 버렸잖아요. 그 여자가 그이를 저렇게 바꿔 놓은 게 틀림없어요. 미탸, 어쩌면 이렇게 부끄러울까요! 오, 미탸, 부끄러워요, 평생토록 부끄러워요. 빌어먹을, 빌어먹을, 그 다섯 해가 저주스러워요!\"\n\n그러고는 그녀는 다시 울음을 터뜨렸으나, 미탸의 손을 꼭 붙든 채 놓지 않았다.\n\n\"미탸, 사랑하는 사람, 가지 말고 있어요. 당신한테 한마디 하고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 속삭이더니, 갑자기 그를 향해 얼굴을 들었다. \"들어요, 말해 봐요, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 누구지요? 난 여기서 한 사람을 사랑해요. 그 사람이 누구지요? 그걸 당신이 말해 줘야 해요.\"\n\n울어서 부어오른 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 환하게 피어났고, 그녀의 눈은 어스름 속에서 빛났다.\n\n\"매 한 마리가 날아들자 내 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았어요. '바보야! 저 사람이 네가 사랑하는 사람이야!' 그게 그 순간 내 가슴이 내게 속삭인 말이었어요. 당신이 들어오자 온통 환해졌어요. 저이는 뭘 두려워하는 걸까, 난 의아했어요. 당신이 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 말도 못 하고 있었으니까요. 저이가 두려워하는 건 저들이 아니야—당신이 누군가를 두려워할 리가 있어요? 저이가 두려워하는 건 나야, 오직 나뿐이야, 난 그렇게 생각했어요. 그래, 페냐가 당신한테 일러바쳤군요, 이 바보 같은 양반아, 내가 창밖으로 알료샤한테 한 시간 동안 미텐카를 사랑했지만 이제 다른 사람을 사랑하러 간다고 외친 걸 말이에요. 미탸, 미탸, 어떻게 내가 당신 다음으로 누군가를 사랑할 수 있다고 생각할 만큼 어리석을 수 있었을까요? 날 용서해 주겠어요, 미탸? 날 용서해요, 안 해요? 날 사랑해요? 날 사랑해요?\" 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 두 손으로 그의 두 어깨를 붙들었다. 미탸는 환희에 차 말을 잃은 채 그녀의 눈을, 그녀의 얼굴을, 그녀의 미소를 응시하다가, 별안간 그녀를 두 팔로 꼭 끌어안고 정열적으로 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 괴롭힌 걸 용서해 주겠지요? 난 심술이 나서 당신들을 다 괴롭혔어요. 심술이 나서 그 노인을 미치게 만들었어요…… 기억해요, 언젠가 당신이 우리 집에서 술을 마시다가 술잔을 깬 거? 난 그걸 기억해 뒀다가 오늘 잔을 깨고 '내 더러운 마음을 위하여' 마셨어요. 미탸, 내 매여, 왜 내게 입을 맞추지 않아요? 한 번 입을 맞추더니, 이제는 물러나서 바라보기만 하고 귀만 기울이네요. 왜 내 말에 귀를 기울여요? 입을 맞춰요, 세게 입을 맞춰요, 그래요. 사랑한다면, 그럼, 사랑해요! 이제 난 당신의 노예가 될 거예요, 평생토록 당신의 노예가 될 거예요. 노예가 되는 건 달콤한 일이에요. 입을 맞춰요! 날 때려요, 날 학대해요, 나한테 마음대로 해요…… 난 정말 고통받아 마땅해요. 잠깐, 기다려요, 나중에, 난 그건 싫어요……\" 그녀는 별안간 그를 밀쳐 냈다. \"가요, 미탸, 난 가서 술을 마실래요, 취하고 싶어요, 취해서 춤출 거예요. 그래야 해요, 그래야 한다고요!\" 그녀는 그에게서 몸을 뿌리치고 커튼 뒤로 사라졌다. 미탸는 술 취한 사람처럼 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 되든—이제 무슨 일이 일어나든, 단 일 분을 위해서라면 온 세상이라도 내놓겠어.\" 그는 생각했다. 그루셴카는 실제로 샴페인 한 잔을 단숨에 들이켜고는 곧장 몹시 취해 버렸다. 그녀는 행복에 겨운 미소를 얼굴에 담은 채 아까와 같은 의자에 앉았다. 그녀의 두 뺨은 달아올랐고, 입술은 타는 듯했으며, 번득이는 눈은 촉촉했으니, 그 눈에는 정열적인 호소가 담겨 있었다. 칼가노프조차 가슴이 술렁여 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"방금 당신이 잠들었을 때 내가 입 맞춘 거 느꼈어요?\" 그녀가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다. \"난 지금 취했어요, 그게 문제예요…… 당신은 안 취했어요? 그런데 미탸는 왜 술을 안 마셔요? 왜 안 마셔요, 미탸? 난 취했는데, 당신은 안 마시네요……\"\n\n\"난 취했어! 이미 취했다고…… 당신한테 취했어…… 이제 술에도 취할 거야.\"\n\n그는 또 한 잔을 들이켰는데—스스로도 이상하게 여겼지만—그 한 잔이 그를 완전히 취하게 만들었다. 그 순간까지만 해도 그는 멀쩡히 맨정신이었음을 그는 기억하고 있었건만, 그는 갑자기 취해 버렸다. 그 순간부터 모든 것이 그의 주위에서 빙빙 돌았으니, 마치 그가 헛것을 보는 것만 같았다. 그는 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 걷고, 웃고, 누구에게나 말을 걸었다. 다만 한 가지 집요하게 타오르는 감각만이 끊임없이 느껴졌으니, 훗날 그의 말로는 \"가슴속의 시뻘건 숯덩이 같은\" 것이었다. 그는 그녀에게 다가가 곁에 앉아 그녀를 응시하고 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였다…… 그녀는 몹시 수다스러워져서, 누구든 자기에게 불러들이고, 합창대에서 이런저런 처녀들에게 손짓했다. 처녀가 다가오면 그녀는 그 처녀에게 입을 맞추거나 성호를 그어 주었다. 다시 일 분쯤 지나면 울음을 터뜨릴 것만 같았다. 그녀는 자기가 \"꼬마 노인네\"라고 부르는 막시모프를 보고 무척 즐거워했다. 그는 끊임없이 달려와 그녀의 두 손에, \"손가락 하나하나에\" 입을 맞추었고, 마침내 자기가 직접 부르는 옛 노래에 맞춰 또 한 곡 춤을 추었다. 그는 후렴 대목에서 특히 힘차게 춤추었다.\n\n새끼 돼지는 꿀꿀, 꿀꿀, 꿀꿀! 송아지는 음매, 음매, 음매, 새끼 오리는 꽥꽥, 꽥꽥, 꽥꽥, 새끼 거위는 가가, 가가, 가가. 암탉은 현관을 으스대며 거닐다가 꼬꼬댁 꼬꼬, 노래하리니, 꼬꼬댁 꼬꼬, 노래하리라!\n\n\"저이한테 뭐라도 줘요, 미탸.\" 그루셴카가 말했다. \"저이한테 선물을 줘요, 가난한 사람이잖아요. 아, 가난한 사람들, 모욕받는 사람들!…… 알아요, 미탸, 난 수녀원에 들어갈 거예요. 아니, 정말로 언젠가 그럴 거예요, 알료샤가 오늘 내게 평생 잊지 못할 말을 했거든요…… 그래요…… 하지만 오늘은 춤을 춰요. 내일은 수녀원으로, 하지만 오늘은 춤을 추는 거예요. 난 오늘 놀고 싶어요, 선량한 분들이여, 그게 뭐 어때서요? 하느님이 우릴 용서해 주실 거예요. 내가 하느님이라면 모두를 용서할 거예요. '내 사랑하는 죄인들아, 오늘부터 너희를 용서하노라.' 난 용서를 빌 거예요. '용서해 주세요, 선량한 분들이여, 어리석은 계집을.' 난 짐승이에요, 그래요. 하지만 난 기도하고 싶어요. 난 양파 한 뿌리를 줬어요. 내가 아무리 못된 짓을 했어도, 난 기도하고 싶어요. 미탸, 사람들이 춤추게 둬요, 막지 말아요. 세상 사람은 누구나 다 선해요. 누구나 다—그중 가장 못된 사람조차도요. 세상은 좋은 곳이에요. 우리가 나쁘다 해도 세상은 괜찮아요. 우리는 선하기도 하고 악하기도 해요, 선하기도 하고 악하기도…… 자, 말해 봐요, 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어요. 다들 이리 와요, 물어볼 게 있으니까. 난 왜 이렇게 착할까요? 내가 착한 거 알지요. 난 아주 착해요…… 자, 난 왜 이렇게 착하지요?\"\n\n이렇게 그루셴카는 점점 더 취해 가며 횡설수설 지껄였다. 마침내 그녀는 자기도 춤을 추겠노라고 선언했다. 그녀는 비틀거리며 의자에서 일어났다. \"미탸, 이젠 술을 더 주지 말아요—내가 달라고 해도 주지 말아요. 술은 평안을 주지 않아요. 모든 게 빙빙 돌아요, 난로도, 다른 것도 죄다. 난 춤추고 싶어요. 다들 내가 춤추는 걸 보게 둬요…… 내가 얼마나 아름답게 춤추는지 보게 둬요……\"\n\n그녀는 진심이었다. 그녀는 주머니에서 흰 무명 손수건을 꺼내어, 춤추며 흔들려고 오른손으로 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡았다. 미탸는 이리저리 뛰어다녔고, 처녀들은 조용해진 채 신호가 떨어지면 곧장 춤곡을 부르려고 채비했다. 막시모프는 그루셴카가 춤추고 싶어 한다는 말을 듣자 기뻐서 꽥 소리를 지르며 그녀 앞에서 깡충깡충 뛰어다니며 흥얼거렸다.\n\n다리는 가늘고 옆구리는 날씬한 데다 조그만 꼬리는 야무지게 말려 있네.\n\n그러나 그루셴카는 그에게 손수건을 휘둘러 쫓아 버렸다.\n\n\"쉬잇! 미탸, 저 사람들은 왜 안 와요? 다들 오게 해요…… 구경하라고요. 갇혀 있는 사람들도 불러들여요…… 왜 그 사람들을 가둬 놨어요? 내가 춤출 거라고 일러요. 그 사람들도 구경하게 둬요……\"\n\n미탸는 술 취해 거들먹거리는 걸음걸이로 잠긴 문으로 걸어가, 주먹으로 폴란드 사람들을 향해 문을 두드리기 시작했다.\n\n\"여보시오, 당신들…… 포드비소츠키 양반들! 자, 그녀가 춤을 출 거요. 당신들을 부르고 있소.\"\n\n\"라이다크(lajdak)!\" 폴란드 사람 하나가 대꾸하며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"당신이야말로 라이다크요! 당신이야말로 하찮은 악당이지, 바로 그거요.\"\n\n\"폴란드를 비웃는 건 그만둬요.\" 칼가노프가 격언이라도 읊듯 말했다. 그 역시 취해 있었다.\n\n\"조용히 해, 도련님! 내가 저자를 악당이라 부른다고 해서, 폴란드 전체를 악당이라 부른 건 아니야. 라이다크 하나가 폴란드를 이루는 건 아니니까. 조용히 해, 내 예쁜 도련님, 사탕이나 하나 먹어.\"\n\n\"아, 어쩌면 저런 작자들이! 마치 사람도 아닌 것 같아요. 왜 화해하려 들지 않을까요?\" 그루셴카는 이렇게 말하며 춤추러 앞으로 나섰다. 합창대가 \"아, 나의 현관, 나의 새 현관!\"을 부르기 시작했다. 그루셴카는 고개를 뒤로 젖히고, 입술을 반쯤 벌리고, 미소 지으며, 손수건을 흔들다가, 별안간 휘청 심하게 비틀거리더니 방 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"기운이 없어요……\" 그녀가 지친 목소리로 말했다. \"용서해요…… 기운이 없어요, 못 추겠어요…… 미안해요.\"\n\n그녀는 합창대를 향해 머리를 숙이더니, 이어 사방을 향해 머리를 숙이기 시작했다.\n\n\"미안해요…… 용서해요……\"\n\n\"아씨가 술을 드셨군. 예쁜 아씨가 술을 드셨어.\" 사람들의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"아씨가 너무 많이 드셨어.\" 막시모프가 킥킥거리며 처녀들에게 설명했다.\n\n\"미탸, 날 데려가요…… 날 데려가요.\" 그루셴카가 힘없이 말했다. 미탸는 그녀에게 달려들어 두 팔로 와락 안아 들고는, 그 소중한 짐을 안고 커튼을 지나갔다.\n\n\"자, 이제 난 가야겠군.\" 칼가노프는 이렇게 생각하고, 푸른 방에서 나가며 두 짝 문을 뒤로 닫았다. 그러나 더 큰 방에서 벌어진 광란의 잔치는 계속되었고, 점점 더 시끌벅적해졌다. 미탸는 그루셴카를 침대에 눕히고 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"날 건드리지 말아요……\" 그녀가 애원하는 목소리로 더듬더듬 말했다. \"내가 당신 것이 되기 전에는 날 건드리지 말아요…… 난 당신 것이라고 말했지만, 그래도 날 건드리지 말아요…… 날 봐줘요…… 저들이 여기 있는데, 저들이 가까이 있는데 그러면 안 돼요. 그이가 여기 있어요. 여긴 추잡해요……\"\n\n\"당신 말대로 하겠어! 그런 생각은 하지 않겠어…… 난 당신을 떠받드는 거야!\" 미탸가 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 여긴 추잡해, 끔찍해.\"\n\n그러고는 여전히 그녀를 두 팔로 안은 채, 그는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다.\n\n\"알아요, 당신은 짐승이긴 해도 너그러워요.\" 그루셴카가 힘겹게 말을 이었다. \"이건 떳떳해야 해요…… 앞으로는 떳떳할 거예요…… 우리 정직하게 살아요, 우리 선하게 살아요, 짐승이 아니라 선한 사람으로…… 날 데려가요, 멀리 데려가요, 알겠어요? 여기선 싫어요, 멀리, 멀리 떨어진 곳에서……\"\n\n\"오, 그래, 그래, 꼭 그래야지!\" 미탸가 그녀를 두 팔로 꽉 끌어안으며 말했다. \"당신을 데리고 날아가 버릴 거야…… 오, 그 피에 관해 알 수만 있다면 단 일 년을 위해서라도 내 평생을 다 내놓겠어!\"\n\n\"무슨 피요?\" 그루셴카가 어리둥절해서 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니야.\" 미탸가 이를 악문 채 중얼거렸다. \"그루샤, 당신은 정직하게 살고 싶다지만, 난 도둑이야. 난 카탸한테서 돈을 훔쳤어…… 치욕이야, 치욕이라고!\"\n\n\"카탸한테서, 그 아가씨한테서요? 아니, 당신은 그걸 훔친 게 아니에요. 그 여자한테 돌려줘요, 내게서 가져다가…… 뭘 그렇게 야단이에요? 이제 내 것은 다 당신 거예요. 돈이 무슨 대수예요? 어차피 우린 다 써 버릴 텐데…… 우리 같은 사람들은 돈을 헤프게 쓰게 마련이에요. 하지만 우린 차라리 땅을 일구러 가는 게 좋겠어요. 난 내 손으로 흙을 파고 싶어요. 우린 일해야 해요, 알겠어요? 알료샤가 그렇게 말했어요. 난 당신 정부 노릇은 안 할 거예요, 난 당신한테 충실할 거예요, 당신의 노예가 될 거예요, 당신을 위해 일할 거예요. 우리 그 아가씨한테 함께 가서 같이 머리를 숙여요, 그 여자가 우릴 용서하게요, 그러고 나서 떠나요. 그 여자가 용서해 주지 않는다 해도 우린 어쨌든 떠날 거예요. 그 여자한테 돈을 가져다주고 날 사랑해요…… 그 여자는 사랑하지 말아요…… 이제 더는 그 여자를 사랑하지 말아요. 당신이 그 여자를 사랑하면 난 그 여자 목을 졸라 버릴 거예요…… 바늘로 두 눈을 다 찔러 버릴 거예요……\"\n\n\"난 당신을 사랑해. 난 오직 당신만 사랑해. 시베리아에서도 당신을 사랑할 거야……\"\n\n\"왜 시베리아예요? 괜찮아요, 시베리아라도 좋아요, 당신만 원한다면. 난 상관없어요…… 우린 일할 거예요…… 시베리아에는 눈이 있어요…… 난 눈 속에서 마차 모는 게 좋아요…… 그리고 방울이 있어야 해요…… 들려요, 방울이 울리고 있어요? 저 방울은 어디서 울리는 거지요? 사람들이 오고 있어요…… 이제 멎었네요.\"\n\n그녀는 기진해 눈을 감더니, 갑자기 한순간 잠들어 버렸다. 멀리서 방울 소리가 들려온 것은 분명했으나, 그 울림은 이미 멎어 있었다. 미탸는 그녀의 가슴에 머리를 떨구었다. 그는 방울 소리가 멎은 것을 알아차리지 못했고, 노래가 그친 것도, 노래와 술 취한 떠들썩함 대신 집 안에 완전한 정적이 깃든 것도 알아차리지 못했다. 그루셴카가 눈을 떴다.\n\n\"무슨 일이지요? 내가 잠들었어요? 그래요…… 방울…… 잠이 들어 꿈을 꿨어요, 방울을 단 채 눈밭 위를 마차로 달리는 꿈을요, 그러다 깜빡 졸았어요. 난 내가 사랑하는 누군가와, 당신과 함께 있었어요. 그리고 멀리, 멀리 떨어진 곳에 있었어요. 난 당신을 끌어안고 입 맞추며, 당신한테 바싹 파고들었어요. 난 추웠고, 눈은 반짝였어요…… 알지요, 달빛이 비칠 때 밤에 눈이 어떻게 반짝이는지. 마치 내가 이 땅에 있지 않은 것 같았어요. 깨어 보니 내 사랑하는 사람이 곁에 있네요. 어쩌면 이렇게 달콤할까요!……\"\n\n\"당신 곁에.\" 미탸가 그녀의 옷에, 그녀의 가슴에, 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추며 중얼거렸다. 그런데 갑자기 그에게 이상한 느낌이 들었으니, 그녀가 자기 앞을 똑바로 바라보고 있는 것 같았는데, 자기를 보는 것도, 자기 얼굴을 보는 것도 아니라, 그의 머리 너머를, 골똘하게, 거의 섬뜩하리만치 고정된 시선으로 바라보고 있는 듯했다. 별안간 그녀의 얼굴에 놀라움, 거의 경악에 가까운 표정이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"미탸, 우릴 보고 있는 저게 누구지요?\" 그녀가 속삭였다.\n\n미탸가 돌아보니, 과연 누군가가 커튼을 젖히고 그들을 지켜보고 있는 듯했다. 그것도 한 사람만이 아닌 듯했다.\n\n그는 벌떡 일어나 그 침입자에게 빠른 걸음으로 다가갔다.\n\n\"이리 와요, 우리 쪽으로, 이리.\" 한 목소리가 크지는 않으나 단호하고 위압적으로 말했다.\n\n미탸는 커튼 저편으로 건너가더니 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 방은 사람들로 가득했으나, 아까 거기 있던 사람들이 아니었다. 순간 그의 등줄기로 오한이 훑고 지나가, 그는 몸을 부르르 떨었다. 그는 그 사람들을 모두 단번에 알아보았다. 외투를 걸치고 모표가 달린 정모를 쓴 저 키 크고 살집 있는 노인은—경찰서장 미하일 마카로비치였다. 그리고 \"늘 그렇게 반들반들한 장화를 신고 다니는\", \"폐병 환자 같은\" 말쑥한 멋쟁이는—검사보였다. \"저 사람은 사백 루블짜리 정밀 회중시계를 갖고 있지, 내게 보여 준 적이 있어.\" 그리고 안경을 쓴 저 자그마한 젊은이는…… 미탸는 그를 알고 있었고, 본 적도 있건만 그 성은 잊어버렸으니, 그는 최근에야 읍내에 부임한, \"법학교\" 출신의 \"예심 판사\"였다. 그리고 이 사람은—경찰 감독관 마브리키 마브리키예비치로, 그가 잘 아는 사람이었다. 그리고 가슴에 놋쇠 휘장을 단 저 친구들은, 어째서 여기 있는 걸까? 그리고 저 다른 두 사람은…… 농부들이로군…… 그리고 저쪽 문가에는 칼가노프와 트리폰 보리소비치가……\n\n\"여러분! 이게 무슨 일입니까, 여러분?\" 미탸가 입을 열었으나, 별안간 정신이 나간 듯, 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채, 목청껏 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"알—겠—습니다!\"\n\n안경 쓴 젊은이가 갑자기 앞으로 나서서 미탸에게 다가서더니, 서두르면서도 위엄을 갖추어 입을 열었다.\n\n\"우리는…… 간단히 말씀드리자면, 이쪽으로, 이쪽 소파로 와 주시기를 청합니다…… 당신은 반드시 진술을 하셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"노인이!\" 미탸가 미친 듯이 외쳤다. \"노인과 그 피가!…… 알겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고는 그는 낫에 베어 쓰러지기라도 한 듯, 바로 곁의 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 거의 쓰러지다시피 했다.\n\n\"알겠다고? 저자가 알겠다고 하는군! 괴물, 친부 살해자! 네 아비의 피가 너를 향해 울부짖고 있다!\" 늙은 경찰서장이 별안간 미탸에게 다가서며 고함을 질렀다.\n\n그는 제정신이 아니었으니, 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"이러시면 안 됩니다!\" 자그마한 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"미하일 마카로비치, 미하일 마카로비치, 이러시면 안 됩니다!…… 부디 제가 말하도록 허락해 주십시오. 서장님께 이런 처사는 결코 기대하지 않았습니다……\"\n\n\"이건 헛소리요, 여러분, 미친 헛소리야.\" 경찰서장이 외쳤다. \"저자를 보시오. 술에 취해서, 이 야밤에, 행실 더러운 계집과 어울려, 제 아비의 피를 손에 묻힌 채로…… 이건 헛소리야!……\"\n\n\"간곡히 부탁드립니다, 친애하는 미하일 마카로비치, 감정을 자제해 주십시오.\" 검사가 늙은 경찰서장에게 빠른 말로 속삭였다. \"안 그러시면 저로서는 부득이……\"\n\n그러나 자그마한 판사는 그의 말을 끝까지 듣지 않았다. 그는 미탸에게 돌아서서, 크고 단호하며 위엄 있는 목소리로 말을 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"전직 중위 카라마조프, 본관은 당신에게 알리는 바이오. 당신은 오늘 밤 자행된 당신의 아버지 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 살해 혐의로 기소되었소……\"\n\n그는 무슨 말인가를 더 했고, 검사 역시 무슨 말인가를 보탰으나, 미탸는 그 말을 듣기는 했지만 알아듣지는 못했다. 그는 사나운 눈으로 그들 모두를 멍하니 바라보았다.\n\n우리가 모로조바 미망인의 집, 굳게 잠긴 튼튼한 대문을 두드리던 자리에 남겨 두고 온 표트르 일리치 페르호틴은 물론 마침내 인기척을 알리는 데 성공했다. 두 시간 전에 겪은 공포로 아직도 마음이 들떠 있던 페냐는, 어찌나 \"심란\"한지 잠자리에 들지도 못한 채 있다가, 대문을 사납게 두드리는 소리를 듣고는 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 만큼 겁에 질렸다. 그가 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 제 눈으로 보고서도, 그녀는 그것이 또다시 드미트리 표도로비치가 두드리는 것이라고 여겼으니, 그토록 거칠게 두드릴 수 있는 사람은 달리 없으리라 싶었던 것이다. 그녀는 이미 잠에서 깨어 대문 쪽으로 나가 있던 문지기에게로 달려가, 제발 문을 열지 말라고 사정했다. 그러나 표트르 일리치를 캐물어 그가 페냐를 매우 \"중대한 용건\"으로 만나고자 한다는 것을 알게 되자, 문지기는 마침내 문을 열어 주기로 마음먹었다. 표트르 일리치는 페냐의 부엌으로 들여보내졌으나, 처녀는 \"마음이 놓이지 않으니\" 문지기를 곁에 두게 해 달라고 청했다. 그는 그녀를 캐묻기 시작했고, 곧장 가장 중요한 사실을 알아냈으니, 다름 아니라 드미트리 표도로비치가 그루셴카를 찾으러 뛰쳐나갈 때 절구에서 절굿공이를 집어 들었다는 것, 그리고 돌아왔을 때 절굿공이는 그의 손에 없었고 두 손이 피로 얼룩져 있었다는 것이었다.\n\n\"게다가 피가 그냥 줄줄, 뚝뚝 떨어지고 있었어요, 뚝뚝!\" 페냐는 거듭 외쳐 댔다. 이 끔찍한 세부는 그저 어지러워진 그녀의 상상이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이었다. 그러나 \"뚝뚝 떨어지지\"는 않았다 해도, 표트르 일리치는 피로 물든 그 두 손을 제 눈으로 보았고, 그것을 씻는 일까지 거들었다. 더구나 그가 판가름해야 할 문제는 피가 얼마나 빨리 말랐느냐가 아니라, 드미트리 표도로비치가 절굿공이를 들고 어디로 달려갔느냐, 아니 그보다 그것이 정말로 표도르 파블로비치의 집이었느냐, 그리고 그것을 어떻게 흡족하게 확인할 수 있느냐 하는 것이었다. 표트르 일리치는 끈질기게 이 점으로 되돌아갔으나, 결정적인 것은 아무것도 알아내지 못했음에도, 드미트리 표도로비치가 제 아버지의 집 말고는 어디로도 갈 수 없었으리라는 것, 따라서 그곳에서 무슨 일인가가 틀림없이 벌어졌으리라는 확신을 안고 떠났다.\n\n\"그런데 그분이 돌아왔을 때,\" 페냐가 흥분해서 덧붙였다, \"제가 그분께 자초지종을 다 말했어요, 그러고는 묻기 시작했죠, '드미트리 표도로비치, 손에 어째서 피가 묻어 있어요?' 하고요, 그러니까 그분은 그게 사람의 피라고, 방금 누군가를 죽였다고 대답했어요. 저한테 다 털어놓고는 갑자기 미친 사람처럼 뛰쳐나갔어요. 저는 주저앉아서 생각하기 시작했죠, 지금 저 양반이 미친 사람처럼 어디로 달려간 걸까? 모크로예로 가겠지, 하고 생각했어요, 거기 가서 우리 아씨를 죽이려는 거구나. 저는 아씨를 죽이지 말라고 빌려고 뛰쳐나갔어요. 그분 숙소로 달려가던 참인데, 플로트니코프 가게 쪽을 보니까 그분이 막 떠나려는 게 보였어요, 그때는 손에 피가 없었어요.\" (페냐는 이것을 알아채고 기억해 두었다.) 페냐의 늙은 할머니도 제 능력껏 그녀의 진술을 거들어 확인해 주었다. 몇 가지를 더 캐물은 뒤, 표트르 일리치는 들어왔을 때보다 한층 더 심란하고 불안한 마음으로 그 집을 나섰다.\n\n그로서 가장 직접적이고 가장 손쉬운 길은 곧장 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 가서 거기서 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 일어났다면 무엇인지 알아보고, 그 사실을 확인한 뒤에야—표트르 일리치가 굳게 작정한 대로—경찰서장에게 가는 것이었으리라. 그러나 밤은 어두웠고, 표도르 파블로비치의 대문은 튼튼했으며, 그는 또다시 두드려야 할 판이었다. 표도르 파블로비치와의 친분이라고는 지극히 얕은 터에, 만약 한참 두드린 끝에 사람들이 문을 열어 주었는데 아무 일도 없었다면 어쩔 것인가? 그러면 표도르 파블로비치는 그 빈정대는 투로 온 고을을 돌아다니며 이 이야기를 떠벌릴 것이니, 페르호틴이라는 낯선 사내가 한밤중에 자기 집에 쳐들어와 누가 자기를 죽였느냐고 물었다고 할 게 아닌가. 그것은 추문거리가 될 것이었다. 그리고 추문이야말로 표트르 일리치가 이 세상 무엇보다도 두려워하는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그를 사로잡은 감정이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그는 화가 나서 발을 구르고 제 자신에게 욕설을 퍼부으면서도, 다시 길을 나섰으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 집이 아니라 호흘라코바 부인의 집으로였다. 그는 마음먹기를, 만약 그녀가 방금 드미트리 표도로비치에게 삼천 루블을 주었다는 것을 부인한다면 곧장 경찰서장에게로 가겠고, 만약 그 돈을 주었다고 인정한다면 집으로 돌아가 이 일을 이튿날 아침까지 덮어 두겠다고 했다.\n\n물론, 한밤 열한 시에 생면부지의 낯선 상류층 부인을 찾아가, 어쩌면 잠자리에서 깨워 가며 어처구니없는 질문을 던지는 편이, 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 가는 것보다 추문을 일으킬 공산이 한층 더 크다는 것은 더없이 자명한 일이었다. 그러나 더없이 빈틈없고 냉정한 사람들의 결정이라는 것도, 때로는, 특히 이번과 같은 경우에는, 바로 그런 식으로 흘러가는 법이다. 표트르 일리치는 그 순간 결코 냉정하지 못했다. 그는 평생토록 기억했으니, 어떻게 끈질긴 불안이 차츰차츰 그를 사로잡아, 점점 더 괴로워지며 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 그를 내몰았던가를. 그래도 그는 물론 이 부인에게 가는 내내 제 자신을 저주했으나, \"내 끝까지 파헤치고야 말겠다, 끝까지!\" 하고 이를 갈며 열 번째로 되뇌고는, 제 작정을 실행에 옮겼다.\n\n그가 호흘라코바 부인의 집에 들어선 것은 정확히 열한 시였다. 그는 제법 빨리 안마당으로 들여보내졌으나, 부인이 아직 깨어 있느냐는 그의 물음에 문지기는 대답해 줄 도리가 없었고, 다만 보통 그 시각이면 잠자리에 든다고만 했다.\n\n\"위층 계단에서 물어보시오. 마님이 손님을 맞고 싶으시면 맞으실 거고, 안 그러시면 안 맞으실 거요.\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 위로 올라갔으나, 여기서는 일이 그리 수월하지 않았다. 하인은 그의 이름을 안에 전하기를 꺼렸으나, 마침내 하녀를 불렀다. 표트르 일리치는 정중하지만 집요하게, 이 고을에 사는 페르호틴이라는 관리가 특별한 용건으로 찾아왔으며, 그것이 더없이 중대한 일이 아니었다면 감히 오지 않았으리라고 마님께 전해 달라고 그녀에게 청했다. \"그 말 그대로 전해 주시오, 그 말 그대로 정확히요.\" 그가 처녀에게 부탁했다.\n\n그녀가 물러갔다. 그는 현관에서 기다렸다. 호흘라코바 부인은 이미 침실에 있었으나, 아직 잠들지는 않았다. 그녀는 미탸가 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 심란해하고 있었고, 늘 그러한 흥분 끝에 따라오는 그 지긋지긋한 두통 없이는 이 밤을 넘기지 못하리라는 예감에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그녀는 하녀의 전갈을 듣고 놀랐다. 그러나 그녀는 짜증스럽게 만나기를 거절했으니, 비록 이런 시각에 생면부지의 \"이 고을에 사는 관리\"가 뜻밖에 찾아온 것이 그녀의 여성다운 호기심을 한껏 부추겼음에도 그러했다. 그러나 이번만은 표트르 일리치가 노새처럼 고집스러웠다. 그는 하녀에게 더없이 간곡히, 바로 이 말 그대로 다시 한번 전해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"그가 더없이 중대한 용건으로 찾아왔으며, 만약 지금 그를 만나기를 거절하신다면 호흘라코바 부인께서 나중에 후회하실 까닭이 생길지도 모른다고요.\"\n\n\"나는 무작정 뛰어들었습니다.\" 그는 나중에 그렇게 묘사했다.\n\n하녀는 놀라움에 차서 그를 바라보더니, 다시 한번 그의 전갈을 전하러 갔다. 호흘라코바 부인은 마음이 움직였다. 그녀는 잠시 생각하더니, 그가 어떻게 생겼느냐고 물었고, 그가 \"옷차림이 아주 단정하고 젊으며 무척 정중하다\"는 것을 알게 되었다. 여담으로 적어 두자면, 표트르 일리치는 제법 풍채 좋은 청년이었고, 그 자신도 그 사실을 잘 알고 있었다. 호흘라코바 부인은 그를 만나기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림이었으나, 어깨에 검은 숄을 걸쳤다. \"관리\"는 응접실로 들도록 청해졌으니, 바로 얼마 전 미탸를 맞이했던 그 방이었다. 부인은 엄하게 캐묻는 낯빛으로 손님을 맞으러 나왔고, 앉으라는 말도 없이 대뜸 질문부터 시작했다.\n\n\"무슨 용건이시죠?\"\n\n\"부인, 우리 두 사람의 공통된 지인인 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프에 관한 일로 감히 폐를 끼치게 되었습니다.\" 페르호틴이 입을 열었다.\n\n그러나 그가 그 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 부인의 얼굴에는 격심한 짜증의 기색이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 거의 비명을 지르다시피 하며 분에 차서 그의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"내가 도대체 언제까지 그 끔찍한 사내 때문에 시달려야 한단 말이오?\" 그녀가 발작하듯 외쳤다. \"당신, 어찌 감히, 어떻게 생면부지의 부인을, 그것도 제 집에서, 이런 시각에 감히 방해할 수 있단 말이오!… 게다가, 불과 세 시간 전에 바로 이 응접실로 나를 죽이러 찾아와서는, 멀쩡한 집에서라면 누구도 그렇게 나가지 않을 만큼 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 방을 나가 버린 그 사내 이야기를 하겠다고 억지로 들이밀다니. 분명히 말해 두겠는데, 나는 당신을 고소하겠소, 이 일을 그냥 넘기지 않겠어요. 당장 나가 주시오…. 나는 어미 된 사람이오…. 나는… 나는—\"\n\n\"죽이다니! 그러면 그자가 부인까지 죽이려 했단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 그가 누구 또 다른 사람을 죽였단 말이오?\" 호흘라코바 부인이 충동적으로 물었다.\n\n\"부인, 부디 잠깐만, 반 순간만 들어 주신다면, 제가 두어 마디로 모두 설명해 드리겠습니다.\" 페르호틴이 단호하게 대답했다. \"오늘 오후 다섯 시에 드미트리 표도로비치가 제게서 십 루블을 빌렸는데, 저는 그에게 돈이 한 푼도 없었다는 것을 분명히 알고 있습니다. 그런데 아홉 시에 그가 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고 저를 찾아왔으니, 어림잡아 이삼천 루블쯤 되었습니다. 그의 두 손과 얼굴은 온통 피투성이였고, 미친 사람 같은 몰골이었습니다. 제가 그 많은 돈을 어디서 났느냐고 물었더니, 그는 방금 부인께 받았다고, 부인이 금광에 가라고 그에게 삼천 루블을 주셨다고 대답하더군요….\"\n\n호흘라코바 부인의 얼굴에 격심하고도 고통스러운 흥분의 기색이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"맙소사! 그가 제 늙은 아비를 죽인 게 틀림없어요!\" 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"나는 그에게 돈을 준 적이 없어요, 단 한 번도! 오, 어서 가세요, 가요!… 더는 한마디도 마세요! 그 노인을 구하세요… 그의 아버지에게로 달려가세요… 어서요!\"\n\n\"실례지만, 부인, 그러면 그에게 돈을 주지 않으셨다는 거군요? 그에게 한 푼도 주지 않으셨다는 것을 분명히 기억하신단 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"네, 안 줬어요, 안 줬다고요! 나는 주기를 거절했어요, 그가 그것을 알아볼 위인이 못 되니까요. 그는 분에 차서 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 뛰쳐나갔어요. 나한테 달려들었지만, 내가 슬쩍 비켜 빠져나왔지요…. 그리고 이제 당신에게 아무것도 숨기고 싶지 않으니 말해 두겠는데, 그가 정말로 내게 침을 뱉었어요. 상상이 가시오! 한데 우리는 왜 서 있는 거죠? 아, 앉으세요.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 저는….\"\n\n\"아니, 그보다 어서 가세요, 가요, 가서 그 가엾은 노인을 끔찍한 죽음에서 구해 내야 해요!\"\n\n\"하지만 만약 그가 이미 죽였다면요?\"\n\n\"아, 세상에, 그래요! 그러면 우리가 이제 어떻게 해야 하지요? 우리가 지금 무엇을 해야 한다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n그러는 사이에 그녀는 표트르 일리치를 앉히고 자신도 그를 마주 보고 앉았다. 표트르 일리치는 간결하지만 제법 또렷하게, 적어도 자기가 직접 목격한 부분만큼은, 그 사건의 전말을 그녀에게 들려주었다. 그는 페냐를 찾아갔던 일도 묘사하고, 절굿공이에 대해서도 이야기했다. 이 모든 세부는 넋이 나간 부인에게 압도적인 충격을 안겨 주었으니, 그녀는 연신 비명을 질러 대고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리곤 했다….\n\n\"믿으시겠어요, 나는 이 모든 걸 예감하고 있었어요! 나한테는 그런 특별한 능력이 있어서, 내가 상상하는 것은 무엇이든 그대로 이루어진답니다. 그리고 내가 그 끔찍한 사내를 볼 때마다 얼마나 자주 생각했는지, 저 사내는 결국 나를 죽이고 말 거라고요. 그런데 이제 그 일이 벌어진 거예요… 다시 말해, 그가 나를 죽이지 않고 제 아비만 죽인 거라면, 그건 오로지 하느님의 손가락이 나를 지켜 주신 덕분이고, 더구나 그가 나를 죽이기를 부끄러워한 건, 바로 이 자리에서 내가 거룩한 순교자 성녀 바르바라의 성유물에서 나온 거룩한 성상을 그의 목에 걸어 주었기 때문이에요…. 그리고 그 순간 내가 죽음에 얼마나 가까이 다가가 있었는지 생각해 보세요, 나는 그에게 바짝 다가갔고 그는 내게 목을 쭉 내밀었답니다!… 아세요, 표트르 일리치 씨(이름이 표트르 일리치라고 하셨던 것 같은데), 나는 기적 같은 건 믿지 않아요, 하지만 그 성상과 지금 나에게 일어난 이 틀림없는 기적은—그게 나를 뒤흔들어서, 이제 무엇이든 당신이 바라는 대로 믿을 채비가 되어 있어요. 조시마 장로에 대해 들어 보셨어요?… 한데 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 모르겠네요… 그리고 생각해 보세요, 성상을 목에 건 채로 그가 내게 침을 뱉었다니까요…. 침만 뱉은 건 사실이에요, 나를 죽이지는 않았고… 그러고는 쏜살같이 달아났어요! 한데 우리가 무엇을 해야 하죠, 이제 무엇을 해야 할까요? 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n표트르 일리치는 일어서며, 곧장 경찰서장에게 가서 이 일을 전부 알리고, 그가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처리하도록 맡기겠다고 알렸다.\n\n\"오, 그분은 훌륭한 분이에요, 훌륭하고말고요! 미하일 마카로비치, 나도 그분을 알아요. 물론 그분이야말로 찾아갈 만한 분이지요. 표트르 일리치 씨, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 빈틈이 없으세요! 어쩌면 그렇게 모든 걸 잘 생각해 내셨어요! 나라면 당신 처지에서 결코 그런 생각을 못 했을 거예요!\"\n\n\"더구나 저는 경찰서장도 아주 잘 알고 있습니다.\" 표트르 일리치가 여전히 선 채로 말했으니, 그는 작별 인사를 하고 떠나도록 내버려 두지 않는 이 충동적인 부인에게서 되도록 빨리 벗어나고 싶어 안달이 난 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"그리고 꼭, 꼭,\" 그녀가 재잘재잘 늘어놓았다, \"다시 와서 거기서 무엇을 보셨는지, 무엇을 알아내셨는지 내게 말씀해 주세요… 무엇이 밝혀지는지… 그를 어떻게 재판하는지… 그가 어떤 형을 선고받는지를요…. 말씀해 보세요, 우리나라엔 사형 제도가 없지요, 그렇죠? 하지만 꼭 와 주세요, 설령 그게 새벽 세 시든, 네 시든, 네 시 반이든…. 하인들에게 일러서 나를 깨우게, 깨우게, 내가 일어나지 않으면 흔들어서라도 깨우게 하세요…. 하지만, 세상에, 나는 잠들지 못할 거예요! 한데 잠깐만요, 차라리 내가 당신과 함께 가는 게 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"아—아니요. 하지만 부인께서 친필로 세 줄만, 드미트리 표도로비치에게 돈을 주지 않으셨다고 적어 주신다면, 어쩌면 쓸모가 있을지도 모릅니다… 필요할 경우에 대비해서요….\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요!\" 호흘라코바 부인은 기뻐서 깡충 뛰며 책상으로 갔다. \"그리고 있잖아요, 나는 이런 일에서 당신이 보여 주는 그 임기응변, 그 분별력에 그저 놀랐답니다, 경탄했어요. 이곳에서 관직에 계신가요? 당신이 이곳에서 관직에 계신다니 정말 기뻐요!\"\n\n그러면서도 줄곧 말을 멈추지 않고, 그녀는 편지지 반 장에 다음과 같은 글귀를 갈겨썼다.\n\n저는 오늘 그 불행한 사내 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프에게(이러나저러나, 그는 불행한 사람이니까요) 삼천 루블을 빌려준 적이 평생 단 한 번도 없습니다. 저는 그에게 돈을 준 적이 결코 없어요, 단 한 번도. 거룩한 모든 것에 두고 맹세합니다!\n카. 호흘라코바.\n\n# K. HOHLAKOV.\n\n\"여기 쪽지예요!\" 그녀가 표트르 일리치 쪽으로 휙 돌아서며 말했다. \"가세요, 그를 구하세요. 당신 입장에서 보면 참으로 고귀한 일이에요!\"\n\n그러고는 그를 향해 세 번 성호를 그었다. 그녀는 그를 복도까지 배웅하려고 뛰쳐나왔다.\n\n\"당신께 얼마나 감사한지! 당신이 제일 먼저 나를 찾아와 주신 게 얼마나 고마운지 당신은 모르실 거예요. 어쩌면 내가 전에 당신을 만나 뵙지 못했을까요? 앞으로 우리 집에서 당신을 뵙게 된다면 영광으로 알겠어요. 당신이 이곳에 사신다니 얼마나 기쁜지요!… 그토록 빈틈없으시다니! 그토록 실무에 밝으시다니!… 사람들이 당신을 알아봐 드려야 해요, 당신을 알아드려야 해요. 내가 도울 수 있는 일이 있다면, 믿어 주세요… 오, 나는 젊은이들을 사랑해요! 젊은이들에게 반해 있답니다! 젊은 세대야말로 고통받는 우리 조국의 단 하나뿐인 버팀목이에요. 그 유일한 희망이고요…. 오, 가세요, 가요!…\"\n\n그러나 표트르 일리치는 이미 달아나 버린 뒤였으니, 그러지 않았더라면 그녀가 그를 그리 일찍 보내 주지 않았을 것이다. 그렇지만 호흘라코바 부인은 그에게 제법 흐뭇한 인상을 남겼고, 그것이 이런 불쾌한 사건에 휘말려 든 그의 불안을 얼마간 누그러뜨려 주었다. 사람마다 취향이 다른 법이라는 것은 우리 모두 아는 바이다. \"저 부인이 그리 늙은 것도 아니로군.\" 그는 흐뭇한 마음으로 생각했다, \"오히려 제 딸뻘이라고 봤을 정도야.\"\n\n호흘라코바 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 그 청년에게 그저 홀딱 반해 버렸다. \"그런 분별력이라니! 그런 빈틈없음이라니! 저렇게 젊은 사람이! 요즘 세상에! 게다가 그 모든 게 저런 매너와 풍채까지 갖추고서! 사람들은 요즘 젊은이들이 아무짝에도 쓸모없다고 하지만, 여기 그렇지 않다는 본보기가 있지 않은가!\" 운운. 그리하여 그녀는 그 \"끔찍한 사건\"을 까맣게 잊어버렸고, 잠자리에 들면서야 비로소 문득 \"내가 죽음에 얼마나 가까이 갔던가\"를 떠올리고는 이렇게 외쳤다. \"아, 끔찍해, 끔찍하기도 하지!\" 그러나 그녀는 곧장 깊고 달콤한 잠에 빠져들었다.\n\n그러나 만약 이 젊은 관리와 결코 늙었다고 할 수 없는 미망인의 기이한 만남이, 훗날 그 실무에 밝고 빈틈없는 청년의 출세 전체의 토대가 되었던 것이 아니었다면, 나는 이런 사소하고 본 줄거리와 무관한 세부에 이토록 머무르지는 않았으리라. 그의 이야기는 오늘날까지도 우리 고을에서 경탄과 함께 회자되거니와, 카라마조프가의 형제들에 관한 이 기나긴 이야기를 끝마치고 나면 나는 어쩌면 그에 관해 한마디 할 거리가 생길지도 모른다.\n\n# Chapter II. The Alarm\n\n우리 고장의 경찰서장 미하일 마카로비치 마카로프는 퇴역한 중령으로, 홀아비였고 더없이 훌륭한 인물이었다. 그가 우리 고장에 온 것은 삼 년 전의 일에 지나지 않았으나, 그는 이미 두루 존경을 받고 있었으니, 무엇보다도 \"사람들을 한데 어울리게 하는 재주\"를 지녔기 때문이었다. 그의 집에는 손님이 끊일 날이 없었고, 그 자신도 손님 없이는 도무지 지낼 수 없는 사람이었다. 늘 누군가가 그와 함께 식사를 했으며, 그는 손님 없이 식탁에 앉는 법이 없었다. 게다가 그는 온갖 구실을 붙여, 때로는 더없이 뜻밖의 구실로 정기적인 만찬을 베풀었다. 음식이 썩 세련된 것은 아니었으나 양만은 푸짐했다. 생선 파이는 일품이었고, 포도주는 질에서 모자란 것을 양으로 메웠다.\n\n손님들이 처음 들어서는 방은 잘 꾸며진 당구실로, 벽에는 검은 액자에 끼운 영국 경주마 그림들이 걸려 있었으니, 다들 알다시피 독신자의 당구실에 빠질 수 없는 장식이었다. 그의 집에서는 매일 저녁 카드놀이가 벌어졌으며, 비록 탁자 하나뿐일 때도 있었다. 그러나 빈번하게 우리 고을의 온 사교계가 어머니들과 젊은 처녀들을 거느리고 그의 집에 모여 춤을 추곤 했다. 미하일 마카로비치는 홀아비였으나 혼자 사는 것은 아니었다. 그에게는 역시 과부가 된 딸이 함께 살고 있었는데, 그 딸에게는 결혼하지 않은 두 딸, 곧 학업을 마친 다 자란 처녀들이 있었다. 이들은 보기에 마음에 들고 성격도 발랄하여, 지참금이 한 푼도 없으리라는 것을 누구나 알면서도 외할아버지의 집으로 멋쟁이 젊은이들을 죄다 끌어모았다.\n\n미하일 마카로비치는 일에 있어 결코 썩 유능한 편은 아니었으나, 직무를 수행하는 데는 다른 많은 이들에 비해 못할 것이 없었다. 솔직히 말하자면 그는 교양이 다소 좁은 인물이었다. 자기 행정 권한의 한계를 헤아리는 그의 분별력은 늘 믿을 만한 것이 못 되었다. 그것은 그가 당대의 치세에 제정된 어떤 개혁들을 이해하지 못했다기보다, 그것들을 해석하는 데서 두드러진 실수를 저질렀다는 쪽이었다. 이는 특별히 지능이 모자라서가 아니라 부주의에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그는 언제나 너무 서둘러 매사를 그저 건너뛰려 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"나는 문관이라기보다는 군인의 심장을 지닌 사람이지.\" 그는 자기 자신을 두고 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그는 농노 해방과 관련된 개혁의 근본 원리에 대해 뚜렷한 관념조차 세우지 못한 채, 말하자면 해가 갈수록 어깨너머로 주워들으며 실무를 통해 본의 아니게 지식을 늘려 가는 형편이었다. 그러면서도 그 자신이 지주였던 것이다. 표트르 일리치는 그날 저녁 그곳에서 미하일 마카로비치의 손님들 가운데 몇몇과 마주치게 되리라는 것을 확실히 알았으나, 누구일지는 알지 못했다. 마침 그 순간에는 검사와, 우리 고을의 군의관 바르빈스키가 경찰서장과 휘스트를 치고 있었는데, 바르빈스키는 의학 아카데미에서 뛰어난 성적으로 학위를 따고 페테르부르크에서 막 우리 고장으로 온 젊은이였다. 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치는(실은 검사보였으나 우리는 늘 그를 검사라고 불렀다) 서른다섯 살가량의 다소 별난 사람으로, 폐병 기운이 있었고, 뚱뚱하고 자식이 없는 여자를 아내로 두고 있었다. 그는 허영심이 많고 성마른 사람이었으나, 머리가 좋았고 마음씨조차 따뜻한 편이었다. 그에게 흠이 있다면 오로지, 제 능력이 보증하는 것 이상으로 자기 자신을 높이 평가한다는 점뿐인 듯싶었다. 그래서 그는 늘 안절부절못하는 사람처럼 보였다. 게다가 그는 어떤 한층 높은, 심지어 예술적이라 할 만한 취향을 지니고 있었으니, 이를테면 심리학이라든가, 인간의 마음에 대한 각별한 연구라든가, 범죄자와 그 범죄에 대한 각별한 식견 같은 것이었다. 그는 이 점에서 한 가닥 원한을 품고 있었으니, 자신이 관직에서 부당하게 외면당했다고 여겼으며, 윗자리에서 자기를 제대로 인정해 주지 않았고 적들까지 있다고 굳게 믿고 있었다. 울적할 때면 그는 직위를 내던지고 형사 사건 변호사로 개업하겠노라고 으르대기까지 했다. 뜻밖에 터진 카라마조프 사건은 그를 깊이 뒤흔들어 놓았으니, \"이것이야말로 온 러시아에 두루 회자될 만한 사건이었다.\" 그러나 이는 앞질러 가는 이야기다.\n\n두 달 전에야 페테르부르크에서 온 젊은 예심판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치 넬류도프는 옆방에서 젊은 처녀들과 함께 앉아 있었다. 사람들은 나중에 이 일을 두고 입방아를 찧으며, 마치 일부러 짠 것처럼 \"그 범행\"이 있던 저녁에 이 신사들이 죄다 행정 당국자의 집에 모여 있었다는 것을 의아해했다. 그러나 그것은 더없이 단순한 일이었고 지극히 자연스럽게 벌어진 일이었다.\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치의 아내는 지난 이틀 동안 치통을 앓고 있었으므로, 그는 아내의 신음 소리를 피해 집을 나설 수밖에 없었다. 의사는 그 천성으로 보아 카드놀이 없이는 저녁을 보낼 수 없는 위인이었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치 넬류도프는 사흘 전부터 그날 저녁 미하일 마카로비치의 집에 말하자면 우연인 척 들를 작정이었으니, 맏손녀 올가 미하일로브나가 감추려 드는 비밀, 곧 그날이 그녀의 생일이며, 무도회를 열지 않으려고 일부러 그것을 숨기고 있다는 사실을 자기가 알고 있음을 넌지시 드러내어 짓궂게 그녀를 놀래 주려는 속셈이었다. 그는 한바탕 큰 웃음판이 벌어지리라고 기대했으니, 그녀의 나이를 두고, 그리고 그것을 밝히기를 겁내는 것을 두고, 또 자기가 그 비밀을 알아서 모두에게 떠벌리겠다는 것을 두고 갖은 익살을 부릴 작정이었다. 이 매력적인 젊은이는 그런 놀림에 능수능란한 사람이었으니, 부인들은 그를 \"심술꾸러기\"라고 별명 붙였는데, 그는 그 별명을 도리어 흐뭇해하는 듯했다. 그렇지만 그는 더없이 점잖은 사람으로, 좋은 가문에 교양과 감수성을 갖추었으며, 향락의 삶을 살면서도 그의 재담은 언제나 순수하고 품위가 있었다. 그는 키가 작고 가냘파 보였다. 희고 가느다란 작은 손가락마다 그는 늘 번쩍이는 큼직한 반지를 여러 개 끼고 다녔다. 공무에 임할 때면 그는 자기 위치와 자기에게 지워진 책무의 신성함을 깨닫기라도 한 듯 유난히 엄숙해졌다. 그는 심문 도중 살인범이나 농민 계층의 다른 범죄자들을 어리둥절하게 만드는 데 각별한 재주가 있었으니, 그들의 존경까지 얻지는 못할지언정 그들의 경탄을 자아내는 데는 분명 성공하곤 했다.\n\n표트르 일리치는 경찰서장의 집에 들어섰을 때 그저 어안이 벙벙해질 따름이었다. 그는 모두가 이미 알고 있음을 단번에 알아차렸다. 그들은 카드를 내팽개친 채 죄다 일어서서 떠들어 대고 있었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치마저 젊은 처녀들을 버려두고 달려 나와, 잔뜩 긴장한 채 당장이라도 행동에 나설 태세였다. 표트르 일리치는, 늙은 표도르 파블로비치가 정말로 그날 저녁 제 집에서 살해되었다는, 살해되고 강도까지 당했다는 놀라운 소식을 전해 들었다. 그 소식이 그들에게 전해진 경위는 다음과 같았다.\n\n울타리 곁에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌던 늙은 그리고리의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나는 자기 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 그녀가 마신 탕약을 생각하면 능히 아침까지도 내처 잘 만했다. 그러나 별안간 그녀는 잠에서 깼는데, 옆방에 의식을 잃고 누워 있던 스메르댜코프가 내지른 무시무시한 간질 발작의 비명에 놀라 깬 것이 틀림없었다. 그 비명은 언제나 그의 발작에 앞서 터져 나왔고, 언제나 마르파 이그나티예브나를 소스라치게 하고 마음을 뒤흔들어 놓았다. 그녀는 결코 그 소리에 익숙해질 수 없었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 잠이 덜 깬 채로 스메르댜코프의 방으로 달려갔다. 그러나 그곳은 캄캄했고, 다만 그 병자가 헐떡이며 몸부림치기 시작하는 소리만 들릴 뿐이었다. 그러자 마르파 이그나티예브나 자신도 비명을 지르며 남편을 부르려 했으나, 문득 자기가 일어났을 때 그가 침대에서 곁에 없었다는 것을 깨달았다. 그녀는 침대로 되달려 가 손으로 더듬어 보았으나, 침상은 정말로 비어 있었다. 그렇다면 그는 밖으로 나간 것이 틀림없는데—대체 어디로 갔단 말인가? 그녀는 현관 층계로 달려 나가 겁먹은 목소리로 그를 불렀다. 물론 아무 대답도 없었으나, 어둠 속 정원 저 멀리서 신음 소리가 들려오는 것을 그녀는 알아챘다. 그녀는 귀를 기울였다. 신음 소리가 거듭 들려왔고, 그것이 정원에서 나는 소리임이 분명했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 꼭 리자베타 스메르댜시차야 때하고 똑같구먼!\" 그녀는 정신없이 그렇게 생각했다. 그녀는 겁먹은 채로 층계를 내려갔고, 정원으로 통하는 쪽문이 열려 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"저 양반이 저기 나가 있는 게로구나, 가엾어라.\" 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다. 그녀가 쪽문으로 다가가는데, 별안간 그리고리가 자기 이름을 부르는 소리가 또렷이 들렸으니, \"마르파! 마르파!\" 하고 힘없이 신음하는 듯한 끔찍한 목소리였다.\n\n\"주여, 우리를 화로부터 지켜 주소서!\" 마르파 이그나티예브나는 중얼거리며 그 목소리를 향해 달려갔고, 그렇게 해서 그리고리를 찾아냈다. 그러나 그녀가 그를 찾아낸 곳은 그가 얻어맞고 쓰러졌던 울타리 곁이 아니라, 거기서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 자리였다. 나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 그가 정신을 차리고서 기어서 그곳까지 갔으며, 거기까지 오는 데 아마도 오랜 시간이 걸렸고 도중에 몇 번이나 의식을 잃었던 모양이었다. 그녀는 그가 온통 피투성이가 된 것을 단번에 알아채고 목청껏 비명을 질렀다. 그리고리는 두서없이 중얼거리고 있었다.\n\n\"그놈이 죽였어… 제 아비를 죽였어… 비명은 왜 질러, 이 멍청아… 어서 달려가… 누구든 데려와…\"\n\n그러나 마르파는 계속 비명을 질러 댔고, 주인의 창문이 열려 있고 창가에 촛불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고는 그쪽으로 달려가 표도르 파블로비치를 부르기 시작했다. 그러나 창 안을 들여다보다가 그녀는 무시무시한 광경을 보았다. 그녀의 주인이 바닥에 반듯이 누워 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었던 것이다. 그의 옅은 빛깔 실내복과 흰 셔츠는 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 탁자 위의 촛불이 그 피와, 표도르 파블로비치의 꼼짝 않는 죽은 얼굴을 환히 비추고 있었다. 공포에 사로잡힌 마르파는 창가에서 뛰쳐나와 정원 밖으로 달려 나갔고, 큰 대문의 빗장을 빼고는 뒷길로 곤두박질치듯 이웃집 마리야 콘드라티예브나에게로 내달렸다. 어머니와 딸은 둘 다 잠들어 있었으나, 마르파가 덧문을 두드리며 필사적으로 끈질기게 비명을 질러 대는 통에 잠에서 깼다. 마르파는 째질 듯한 소리로 두서없이 외쳐 대면서도 가까스로 핵심만은 일러 주고 도움을 청했다. 마침 떠돌이 생활을 하던 포마가 돌아와 그 집에서 밤을 보내고 있던 참이었다. 그들은 곧장 그를 깨웠고, 셋이서 범행 현장으로 달려갔다. 가는 길에 마리야 콘드라티예브나는, 여덟 시쯤 자기네 정원에서 끔찍한 비명이 들렸던 것을 떠올렸으니, 이는 그리고리가 미탸의 다리를 붙들었을 때 내질렀던 \"아비를 죽인 놈!\"이라는 비명이 틀림없었다.\n\n\"누군가 한 사람이 비명을 지르고는 곧 잠잠해졌어요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 달려가면서 설명했다. 그리고리가 누워 있는 곳으로 달려간 두 여인은 포마의 도움을 받아 그를 별채로 옮겼다. 그들이 촛불을 켜고 보니 스메르댜코프는 조금도 나아지지 않은 채 경련으로 몸을 뒤틀고 있었고, 두 눈은 사팔뜨기처럼 한곳에 굳어 있었으며, 입술에서는 거품이 흘러내리고 있었다. 그들은 식초를 탄 물로 그리고리의 이마를 적셨고, 그 물에 그는 이내 정신을 차렸다. 그는 즉시 물었다.\n\n\"주인님이 살해당하셨소?\"\n\n그러자 포마와 두 여인은 본채로 달려갔고, 이번에는 창문뿐 아니라 정원으로 통하는 문까지 활짝 열려 있는 것을 보았으니, 표도르 파블로비치는 지난 한 주 내내 밤마다 문을 안에서 걸어 잠그고 어떤 핑계로도 그리고리조차 들이지 않던 터였다. 그 문이 열려 있는 것을 본 그들은 \"나중에 무슨 일이 생길까 두려워\" 표도르 파블로비치에게 들어가기를 꺼렸다. 그들이 그리고리에게 돌아오자, 노인은 곧장 경찰서장에게 가라고 일렀다. 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 그리로 달려가 경찰서장 집에 모인 사람들 전체에게 변고를 알렸다. 그녀는 표트르 일리치보다 단 오 분 앞서 당도했으니, 그리하여 그의 이야기는 그 자신의 추측이나 견해가 아니라, 범인의 신원에 관해 모두가 품고 있던 견해를(그가 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 그 순간까지도 믿기를 거부해 온 견해를) 한 목격자가 직접 확증해 주는 것으로 받아들여졌다.\n\n힘차게 움직이기로 결정이 났다. 고을의 경찰부서장이 증인 네 사람을 데리고 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 들어가 그 자리에서 정식 절차에 따라 조사를 개시하라는 임무를 맡았으니, 그 절차에 관해서는 여기서 시시콜콜 늘어놓지 않겠다. 군의관은 열성적인 사람이자 일이 손에 익지 않은 신참이었던 터라, 경찰서장과 검사와 예심판사를 따라가겠다고 거의 우기다시피 했다.\n\n간략히 적어 두자면, 표도르 파블로비치는 두개골이 박살난 채 완전히 숨이 끊어진 것으로 밝혀졌다. 그런데 무엇으로 그랬단 말인가? 십중팔구 그리고리를 내리쳤던 바로 그 흉기였다. 그리고 곧 그 흉기가 발견되었으니, 곧바로 가능한 모든 의료 처치를 받은 그리고리가 힘없이 끊어지는 목소리로 자기가 어떻게 얻어맞고 쓰러졌는지를 진술한 덕분이었다. 그들이 등불을 들고 울타리 곁을 살피기 시작하자, 정원 오솔길의 더없이 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 떨어져 있는 놋쇠 절굿공이가 발견되었다. 표도르 파블로비치가 누워 있던 방에는 다투거나 흐트러진 흔적이 없었다. 그러나 침대 곁, 가리개 뒤편에서 그들은 바닥에 떨어진 크고 두툼한 봉투 하나를 주워 들었는데, 거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"나의 천사 그루셴카가 와 주겠다면, 그녀에게 줄 삼천 루블 선물.\" 그리고 그 아래에는 표도르 파블로비치가 덧붙여 쓴 글이 있었으니, \"나의 귀여운 병아리에게\"라고 적혀 있었다. 봉투에는 붉은 봉랍으로 찍은 봉인이 세 개 있었으나, 이미 뜯겨 텅 비어 있었으니, 돈은 빼내어진 뒤였다. 그들은 또한 봉투를 묶었던 가느다란 분홍 리본 한 조각을 바닥에서 발견했다.\n\n표트르 일리치의 진술 가운데 한 대목이 검사와 예심판사에게 큰 인상을 남겼으니, 곧 드미트리 표도로비치가 동트기 전에 스스로 목숨을 끊으리라는 그의 생각이었다. 미탸가 그렇게 하기로 작정했으며, 그것을 일리치에게 말했고, 권총을 챙겨 그가 보는 앞에서 장전했으며, 편지를 써서 호주머니에 넣었다는 등의 이야기였다. 표트르 일리치가, 그것을 믿고 싶지 않으면서도, 자살을 막으려고 누군가에게 알리겠다고 으르대자, 미탸는 히죽 웃으며 이렇게 대답했다는 것이다. \"너무 늦을걸.\" 그러니 그가 정말로 스스로를 쏘아 버리기 전에 서둘러 모크로예로 가서 범인을 찾아야만 했다.\n\n\"분명하군, 분명해!\" 검사는 잔뜩 흥분하여 거듭 외쳤다. \"그런 미치광이 같은 자들이 꼭 그렇지. '내일 자살할 테니, 죽기 전에 한바탕 흥청거려 보자!' 하는 식이거든.\"\n\n그가 어떻게 포도주와 음식을 사들였는가 하는 이야기는 검사를 그 어느 때보다 흥분시켰다.\n\n\"여러분, 올수피예프라는 상인을 죽인 그 작자를 기억하시오? 그놈은 천오백 루블을 훔치고는 곧장 이발소로 가서 머리를 지졌고, 그러고는 돈을 숨기지도 않은 채, 그것을 거의 손에 든 그대로 들고서 계집들한테로 가 버렸지요.\"\n\n그러나 표도르 파블로비치의 집에서 진행된 조사와 수색, 갖가지 절차 따위 때문에 모두가 발이 묶였다. 그 일에는 시간이 걸렸으므로, 출발하기 두 시간 전에 그들은 시골 경찰관 마브리키 마브리키예비치 시메르초프를 모크로예로 먼저 보냈는데, 그는 봉급을 타려고 그 전날 아침 고을에 와 있던 참이었다. 그는 모크로예에 닿거든 소동을 일으키지 말고, 정식 당국자들이 도착할 때까지 \"범인\"을 끊임없이 감시하며, 아울러 체포에 필요한 증인과 순경 따위를 확보해 두라는 지시를 받았다. 마브리키 마브리키예비치는 지시받은 대로, 신분을 감춘 채 오랜 지기인 트리폰 보리소비치 외에는 아무에게도 자기 비밀 임무에 대해 손톱만큼의 낌새도 내비치지 않았다. 그가 그에게 말을 건넨 것은, 미탸가 어둠 속에서 그를 찾다가 발코니에서 그 주인과 마주치기 바로 직전이었으니, 미탸는 그 즉시 트리폰 보리소비치의 얼굴빛과 목소리가 달라진 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 미탸도, 그 누구도 자기가 감시당하고 있다는 것은 알지 못했다. 권총이 든 상자는 트리폰 보리소비치가 가져가 적당한 곳에 치워 두었다. 네 시가 지나서야, 거의 해 뜰 무렵에야 모든 관리들, 곧 경찰서장과 검사와 예심판사가 각각 세 마리 말이 끄는 두 대의 마차에 나누어 타고 당도했다. 군의관은 이튿날 시신을 부검하기 위해 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 남았다. 그러나 그가 각별히 흥미를 느낀 것은 하인 스메르댜코프의 상태였다.\n\n\"이토록 격렬하고 오래 끄는 간질 발작이 스물네 시간 동안 끊임없이 되풀이되는 경우는 좀처럼 보기 드물어서, 학문적으로 흥미로운 사례입니다.\" 그는 동행들에게 열을 올려 단언했고, 그들은 떠나면서 그의 진귀한 발견을 두고 웃으며 축하해 주었다. 검사와 예심판사는, 스메르댜코프가 이 밤을 넘기지 못하리라던 군의관의 말을 또렷이 기억하고 있었다.\n\n이처럼 길었으나 내 생각으로는 꼭 필요했던 설명을 마쳤으니, 이제 우리는 이야기를 끊었던 그 대목으로 되돌아가기로 하자.\n\n# Chapter III. The Sufferings Of A Soul, The First Ordeal\n\n그리하여 미탸는 주위 사람들을 멍하니 바라보며 앉아 있었고, 자기에게 무슨 말을 하는지조차 알아듣지 못했다. 갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 치켜들고 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"나는 결백하오! 그 피에는 죄가 없소! 아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없단 말이오……. 죽이려고는 했소. 하지만 나는 결백하오. 내가 한 짓이 아니오.\"\n\n그러나 그가 미처 이 말을 끝맺기도 전에 그루셴카가 휘장 뒤에서 뛰쳐나와 경찰서장의 발치에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n\"이건 제 탓이에요! 제 탓! 제가 사악해서예요!\" 그녀는 눈물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 가슴이 미어지는 목소리로 외치며, 맞잡은 두 손을 그들에게 내밀었다. \"이이는 저 때문에 그런 거예요. 제가 이이를 들볶아서 이 지경으로 몰아넣었어요. 제 사악함 때문에 저 가엾은 노인까지, 이제는 죽고 만 그 양반까지 들볶아서 이 꼴로 만든 거예요! 제 탓이에요, 무엇보다 제 탓, 가장 큰 제 탓, 제 죄예요!\"\n\n\"그래, 네 탓이지! 네가 주범이야! 이 독부 같은 것! 이 음탕한 계집! 죄가 가장 큰 건 너야!\" 경찰서장이 손을 들어 그녀를 위협하며 소리쳤다. 그러나 그는 곧장 단호하게 제지당했다. 검사가 그를 다짜고짜 붙들어 버린 것이다.\n\n\"이건 전혀 규정에 어긋납니다, 미하일 마카로비치!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 지금 심리를 방해하고 있습니다……. 사건을 망치고 있단 말이오…….\" 그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다.\n\n\"규정대로 합시다! 규정대로!\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치도 몹시 흥분하여 소리쳤다. \"그러지 않으면 전혀 진행이 불가능하단 말입니다!…….\"\n\n\"우리를 함께 심판하세요!\" 그루셴카가 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 미친 듯이 외쳤다. \"우리를 함께 벌하세요. 지금 당장 이이와 함께 가겠어요, 그게 죽음이라 해도!\"\n\n\"그루샤, 내 목숨, 내 피, 내 성스러운 사람!\" 미탸가 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇으며 두 팔로 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다. \"이 사람 말을 믿지 마시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"이 사람은 아무 죄도 없소, 어떤 피에 대해서도, 그 무엇에 대해서도!\"\n\n그는 나중에야 자기가 몇몇 사내들의 손에 힘으로 그녀에게서 떼어내어졌다는 것과, 그녀가 끌려 나갔다는 것과, 정신을 차리고 보니 자기가 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었다는 것을 기억해냈다. 그의 곁과 등 뒤에는 금속 표찰을 단 사내들이 서 있었다. 탁자 맞은편에는 예심판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 앉아 있었다. 그는 미탸에게 탁자 위에 놓인 잔의 물을 좀 마시라고 거듭 권했다.\n\n\"이걸 마시면 기운이 날 거고, 마음이 가라앉을 겁니다. 진정하세요, 겁먹지 마시고.\" 그가 더없이 정중하게 덧붙였다. 미탸는(이것도 나중에 기억해냈다) 갑자기 그의 커다란 반지들에 무서울 만큼 정신이 팔렸으니, 하나에는 자수정이, 다른 하나에는 투명하고 밝은 노란 보석이 박혀 눈부시게 빛나고 있었다. 그리고 훗날 그는 그 반지들이 그 끔찍한 심문의 시간 내내 어쩌면 그렇게도 자기 주의를 사로잡았는지, 그래서 자기 처지와는 아무 상관도 없는 물건이건만 도무지 거기서 시선을 떼어 떨쳐낼 수 없었던 것을 의아한 마음으로 회상하곤 했다. 미탸의 왼쪽, 그날 저녁 처음에 막시모프가 앉아 있던 자리에는 이제 검사가 앉아 있었고, 미탸의 오른쪽, 그루셴카가 앉아 있던 자리에는 허름한 사냥 재킷 같은 것을 걸친 발그레한 뺨의 젊은이가 앞에 잉크와 종이를 놓고 앉아 있었다. 이 사람은 예심판사가 데려온 서기였다. 경찰서장은 이제 방 반대편 끝 창가에 서 있었고, 그 옆에는 칼가노프가 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"물을 좀 드세요.\" 예심판사가 벌써 열 번째로 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"마셨소, 여러분, 마셨다니까……. 그러나……, 자, 여러분, 나를 짓밟으시오, 벌하시오, 내 운명을 결정하시오!\" 미탸가 무섭게 고정된 채 크게 뜬 눈으로 예심판사를 노려보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신은 부친 표도르 파블로비치의 죽음에 대해 결백하다고 단호히 선언하는 거지요?\" 예심판사가 부드럽지만 집요하게 물었다.\n\n\"나는 결백하오. 나는 다른 노인의 피에 대해서는 죄가 있으나 아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없소. 그리고 그 일로 통곡하고 있소! 내가 죽였소, 내가 그 노인을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸소……. 하지만 그 살인을 또 다른 살인, 내가 결백한 그 끔찍한 살인까지 뒤집어쓰고 대답해야 한다니 이건 견디기 힘든 일이오……. 끔찍한 혐의요, 여러분, 사람을 때려눕히는 일격이오. 그런데 누가 내 아버지를 죽였단 말이오, 대체 누가? 내가 아니라면 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소? 이건 기이하고, 터무니없고, 있을 수 없는 일이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소?\" 예심판사가 입을 떼려 했으나, 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 그를 흘긋 보더니 미탸에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"늙은 하인 그리고리 바실리예비치에 대해서는 걱정할 것 없습니다. 그는 살아 있고, 회복했으며, 자신과 당신의 진술에 따르면 당신이 가한 끔찍한 타격에도 불구하고 살아날 것이 거의 틀림없어 보입니다, 적어도 의사 말로는 그렇소.\"\n\n\"살아 있다고? 그가 살아 있어?\" 미탸가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다. 그의 얼굴이 환하게 빛났다. \"주여, 죄인이요 악행을 저지른 저를 위해 베푸신 기적에 감사드리나이다. 이것은 제 기도에 대한 응답입니다. 저는 밤새도록 기도했습니다.\" 그러고는 세 번 성호를 그었다. 그는 거의 숨이 차오를 지경이었다.\n\n\"그래서 이 그리고리한테서 우리는 당신에 관한 매우 중요한 증언을 받았는데, 그것은—\" 검사가 말을 이으려 했으나, 미탸가 갑자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"잠깐만, 여러분, 제발, 잠깐만; 그녀에게 달려가게 해주시오—\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 지금 이 순간엔 전혀 불가능합니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 거의 비명을 지르듯 말했다. 그도 벌떡 일어났다. 미탸는 금속 표찰을 단 사내들에게 붙들렸으나, 스스로 자리에 앉았다…….\n\n\"여러분, 참으로 안타깝구려! 단 일 분만 그녀를 보고 싶었는데; 내 가슴을 밤새도록 짓누르던 그 피가 씻겨 내려갔다고, 사라졌다고, 그래서 이제 나는 살인자가 아니라고 그녀에게 말해주고 싶었소! 여러분, 그녀는 내 약혼녀요!\" 그는 그들 모두를 둘러보며 황홀하고도 경건하게 말했다. \"아, 고맙소, 여러분! 아, 단 일 분 만에 여러분은 내게 새 생명을, 새 가슴을 주셨소!……. 그 노인은 나를 두 팔로 안아주곤 했소, 여러분. 내가 세 살배기 아기였을 때, 모두에게 버림받았을 때 그는 함지에 나를 씻겨주곤 했소, 그는 내게 아버지나 다름없었소!…….\"\n\n\"그래서 당신은—\" 예심판사가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 여러분, 일 분만 더 주시오.\" 미탸가 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 끼어들었다. \"잠시 생각할 틈을 주시오, 숨 좀 쉬게 해주시오, 여러분. 이 모든 게 끔찍하게 사람을 뒤흔들어 놓는구려, 끔찍하게도. 사람은 북이 아니란 말이오, 여러분!\"\n\n\"물을 좀 더 드세요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 중얼거렸다.\n\n미탸는 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼고 웃었다. 그의 눈에는 자신감이 어려 있었다. 그는 한순간에 완전히 딴사람이 된 듯했다. 태도가 송두리째 달라졌으니, 그는 다시 한번 이 사람들과 대등한 사람이 되었던바, 그들 모두와 안면이 있었으며, 마치 아무 일도 없던 어제 어느 사교 모임에서 다 함께 만났던 것만 같았다. 지나가는 김에 짚어두자면, 미탸가 처음 이 고장에 왔을 때는 경찰서장 댁에서 무척 환대를 받았으나, 나중에, 특히 지난 한 달 동안은 거의 발길을 끊다시피 했고, 경찰서장이 이를테면 길에서 그를 마주칠 때면 미탸는 그가 눈살을 찌푸리며 예의상으로만 인사하는 것을 알아챘다. 검사와의 친분은 그보다 못했으나, 미탸는 가끔 그의 아내를, 신경질적이고 공상이 많은 부인을 까닭도 잘 모른 채 예의상 찾아가곤 했고, 그녀는 언제나 그를 정중히 맞아주었으며 무슨 까닭에선지 마지막까지 그에게 관심을 두고 있었다. 예심판사와는 친해질 겨를이 없었으나, 두 번 만나 이야기를 나눈 적이 있었고, 그때마다 화제는 여인들에 관한 것이었다.\n\n\"보아하니 당신은 대단히 노련한 법관이시군요, 니콜라이 파르페노비치.\" 미탸가 유쾌하게 웃으며 외쳤다. \"하지만 이제 내가 당신을 도울 수 있소. 아, 여러분, 나는 새사람이 된 기분이오, 내가 이렇게 허물없이 직설적으로 말을 거는 걸 언짢게 여기지 마시오. 게다가 나는 좀 취했소, 솔직히 말해두겠소. 니콜라이 파르페노비치, 당신을 내 친척 미우소프의 집에서 뵙는 영광과 기쁨을 누린 적이 있는 것 같소. 여러분, 여러분, 나는 당신들과 대등하다고 자처하지는 않소. 물론 내가 어떤 처지로 당신들 앞에 앉아 있는지 잘 알고 있소. 아, 물론, 끔찍한 혐의가……, 내게 씌워져 있소……, 그리고리가 증언을 했다면 말이오……. 끔찍한 혐의요! 무섭소, 무서워, 나도 그건 잘 알고 있소! 하지만 본론으로 들어갑시다, 여러분, 나는 준비가 되어 있고, 우리는 단숨에 이걸 끝낼 수 있소; 들어보시오, 들어보시오, 여러분! 내가 결백하다는 걸 알고 있는 이상, 우리는 단번에 끝낼 수 있소. 안 그렇소? 안 그렇소?\"\n\n미탸는 많은 말을 빠르게, 신경질적이면서도 격정적으로 쏟아냈으니, 마치 듣는 사람들을 자기의 둘도 없는 벗으로 단단히 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"그럼 우선, 당신은 자신에게 씌워진 혐의를 단호히 부인한다고 적겠습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 무게 있게 말하더니, 서기 쪽으로 몸을 숙여 무엇을 쓸지 낮은 목소리로 받아쓰게 했다.\n\n\"적는다고? 그걸 적겠다는 거요? 좋소, 적으시오; 동의하오, 전적으로 동의하오, 여러분, 다만……, 알겠소?……. 가만, 가만, 이것도 적으시오. 난동을 부린 죄라면 나는 유죄요, 가엾은 노인에게 폭력을 휘두른 죄라면 나는 유죄요. 그리고 내 가슴 밑바닥에 또 다른, 내가 죄지은 무언가가 있소—하지만 그건 적을 필요 없소\"(그는 갑자기 서기 쪽으로 돌아섰다); \"그건 내 사적인 삶이오, 여러분, 당신들과는 상관없는 일이오, 그러니까 내 가슴 밑바닥의 일이란 말이오……. 하지만 내 늙은 아버지를 죽인 죄에 대해서는 나는 결백하오. 그건 터무니없는 생각이오. 정말이지 터무니없는 생각이오!……. 내 그걸 당신들에게 증명해 보이겠소, 그럼 곧장 납득하게 될 거요……. 당신들은 웃을 거요, 여러분. 당신들 자신이 품은 혐의를 두고 스스로 웃게 될 거요!…….\"\n\n\"진정하세요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 예심판사가 자신의 침착함으로 미탸의 흥분을 가라앉히려는 듯이 말했다. \"심리를 계속하기 전에, 만약 대답해주신다면, 한 가지 진술을 당신 입으로 확인해 듣고 싶소, 곧 당신이 부친 표도르 파블로비치를 싫어했고, 그와 끊임없는 다툼에 휘말려 있었다는 것 말이오. 적어도 여기서, 십오 분 전에, 당신은 그를 죽이고 싶었다고 외쳤소. '내가 그를 죽이지는 않았다'고 당신은 말했소, '그러나 죽이고 싶었다'고 말이오.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게 외쳤소? 아아, 그랬을지도 모르겠소, 여러분! 그렇소, 불행히도 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소……. 여러 번이나 죽이고 싶었소……, 불행하게도, 불행하게도!\"\n\n\"죽이고 싶었다고요. 정확히 어떤 동기에서 그렇게 친부에 대한 증오의 감정에 이르게 되었는지 설명해주시겠소?\"\n\n\"설명할 게 뭐 있겠소, 여러분?\" 미탸가 시무룩하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 눈을 내리깔았다. \"나는 한 번도 내 감정을 숨긴 적이 없소. 온 고을이 그걸 알고 있고—술집에서는 누구나 다 알고 있소. 바로 얼마 전에도 나는 조시마 장로의 암자에서 그 감정을 털어놨소……. 그리고 바로 그날, 저녁에 나는 아버지를 두들겨 팼소. 죽일 뻔했고, 다시 와서 죽이겠다고 증인들 앞에서 맹세까지 했소……. 아, 천 명의 증인이 있소! 나는 지난 한 달 내내 그걸 큰 소리로 떠들고 다녔소, 누구든 당신들에게 말해줄 거요!……. 사실은 당신들 눈앞에 빤히 드러나 있고, 그 자체로 말하고 있으며, 크게 외쳐대고 있소, 하지만 감정은, 여러분, 감정은 별개의 문제요. 보시오, 여러분\"—미탸는 눈살을 찌푸렸다—\"감정에 관해서는 당신들에게 나를 심문할 권리가 없는 것 같소. 당신들이 직무에 매여 있다는 건 나도 아오, 그건 충분히 이해하오, 하지만 그건 내 일이오, 내 사적이고 내밀한 일이오, 그렇긴 하나……, 과거에 나는 내 감정을 숨기지 않았으니……, 이를테면 술집에서 누구에게나 다 떠들어댔으니까, 그러니……, 그러니 지금도 그걸 비밀로 하지는 않겠소. 보시오, 여러분, 나도 이 사건에서 나한테 불리한 끔찍한 사실들이 있다는 걸 이해하오. 나는 그를 죽이겠다고 누구에게나 말하고 다녔고, 이제 별안간 그가 살해됐소. 그러니 내가 한 짓임에 틀림없다는 거지! 하하! 나는 당신들을 이해할 수 있소, 여러분, 충분히 이해할 수 있단 말이오. 나 자신도 망연자실하오, 내가 아니라면 대체 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소? 결국 그렇게 되는 거 아니오? 내가 아니라면 누구일 수 있겠소, 누구? 여러분, 나도 알고 싶소, 꼭 알아야겠소!\" 그가 별안간 외쳤다. \"그는 어디서 살해됐소? 어떻게 살해됐소? 어떻게, 무엇으로? 말해주시오.\" 그가 두 법관을 바라보며 다급하게 물었다.\n\n\"우리는 그를 서재에서 발견했는데, 머리가 박살난 채 바닥에 반듯이 누워 있었소.\" 검사가 말했다.\n\n\"끔찍하구려!\" 미탸가 몸서리를 치며 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 오른손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"계속합시다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다. \"그래서 무엇이 당신을 그런 증오의 감정으로 몰아갔소? 당신은 그것이 질투에 근거한 것이라고 공공연히 주장했다고 알고 있는데요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 질투요. 그리고 질투만은 아니오.\"\n\n\"돈을 둘러싼 다툼이었소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 돈에 관한 것이기도 했소.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 삼천 루블을 둘러싼 다툼이 있었던 것 같은데, 당신이 그걸 상속분의 일부라고 주장했지요?\"\n\n\"삼천이라! 더, 더 되오.\" 미탸가 격하게 외쳤다. \"육천도 넘고, 어쩌면 만 루블도 넘소. 누구에게나 그렇게 말했고, 그들에게 소리쳐댔소. 하지만 나는 삼천 루블로 끝내기로 마음먹었소. 그 삼천 루블이 절박하게 필요했소……, 그래서 그가 그루셴카를 위해 베개 밑에 넣어두고 있던 그 삼천 루블 지폐 뭉치를 나는 그냥 나한테서 도둑맞은 것으로 여겼소. 그렇소, 여러분, 나는 그걸 내 것으로, 내 재산으로 여겼소…….\"\n\n검사는 의미심장하게 예심판사를 바라보았고, 슬쩍 그에게 눈짓을 보낼 틈도 있었다.\n\n\"그 문제는 나중에 다시 다루겠소.\" 예심판사가 곧바로 말했다. \"그 점을 적어두는 걸 허락해주시겠소; 곧 당신이 그 돈을 자기 재산으로 여겼다는 점 말이오?\"\n\n\"얼마든지 적으시오. 그것 역시 나한테 불리한 또 하나의 사실이라는 걸 알지만, 나는 사실을 두려워하지 않고 나 스스로 나에게 불리한 사실을 말하는 거요. 듣고 있소? 알겠소, 여러분, 당신들은 나를 실제의 나와는 다른 부류의 인간으로 여기고 있소.\" 그가 갑자기 침울하고 풀이 죽어 덧붙였다. \"당신들이 상대하는 건 명예를 아는 사람, 더없이 명예를 아는 사람이오; 무엇보다도—이걸 놓치지 마시오—더러운 짓을 많이 저질렀으나 그 밑바닥, 그 내면에서는 언제나 명예로웠고 지금도 명예로운 사람이란 말이오. 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠소. 바로 그게 내 평생을 비참하게 만든 거요, 내가 명예롭게 되기를 갈망했다는 것, 말하자면 명예 의식의 순교자였다는 것, 등불을 들고, 디오게네스의 등불을 들고 그걸 찾아 헤맸다는 것이오, 그러면서도 평생 우리 모두처럼 더러운 짓을 저질러왔다는 것이오, 여러분……, 그러니까 나 혼자만 그랬다는 거요. 그게 잘못이었소, 나 혼자만, 나 혼자만!……. 여러분, 머리가 아프구려…….\" 그의 눈썹이 고통으로 찡그러졌다. \"보시오, 여러분, 나는 그자의 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없었소, 그자에게는 무언가 비천하고, 뻔뻔하고, 온갖 신성한 것을 짓밟는, 무언가 비웃는 듯하고 불경한, 역겨운, 역겨운 데가 있었소. 하지만 이제 그가 죽고 나니, 느낌이 다르구려.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"느낌이 다르다기보다는, 그를 그렇게 미워하지 않았더라면 좋았을 거라는 거요.\"\n\n\"뉘우치는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 뉘우치는 건 아니오, 그건 적지 마시오. 나 자신도 별로 잘난 게 없고, 그리 아름다운 인간도 못 되니, 그자를 역겹다고 여길 권리가 없었다는 거요. 내 말은 그거요. 정 그렇다면 그걸 적으시오.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 미탸는 무척 침울해졌다. 심리가 이어질수록 그는 점점 더 우울해졌다.\n\n바로 그때 또 한 차례 뜻밖의 장면이 벌어졌다. 그루셴카는 끌려 나가긴 했으나 멀리 데려가지는 않았고, 심문이 진행 중인 파란 방에서 한 칸 건넛방에 있었다. 그곳은 창이 하나 달린 작은 방으로, 그들이 그토록 흥청거리며 춤추고 잔치를 벌인 큰 방 바로 옆이었다. 그녀는 그곳에 앉아 있었는데 곁에는 막시모프밖에 없었으니, 그는 몹시 의기소침하고 무섭게 겁에 질려, 마치 안전을 구하듯 그녀 옆에 바싹 붙어 있었다. 그들의 문가에는 가슴에 금속 표찰을 단 농부 하나가 서 있었다. 그루셴카는 울고 있었는데, 갑자기 슬픔을 주체하지 못하고 벌떡 일어나 두 팔을 치켜들더니, 비통하게 큰 소리로 울부짖으며 방에서 뛰쳐나가 그에게로, 자기의 미탸에게로 달려갔으니, 어찌나 갑작스러웠던지 그들이 그녀를 막을 틈도 없었다. 미탸는 그녀의 울음소리를 듣자 부르르 떨더니 벌떡 일어나, 비명을 지르며 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 맹렬히 그녀를 맞으러 달려갔다. 그러나 두 사람은 서로를 보기는 했어도 가까이 가는 것은 허락되지 않았다. 그는 양팔이 붙들렸다. 그는 몸부림치며 떨쳐내려 했다. 그를 붙드는 데 서너 명이 달라붙어야 했다. 그녀도 붙들렸는데, 그는 그녀가 자기를 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 끌려가는 동안 큰 소리로 울부짖는 모습을 보았다. 그 소동이 끝나자 그는 다시 정신을 차렸으니, 이전과 같은 자리에, 예심판사 맞은편에 앉은 채 그들을 향해 외치고 있었다.\n\n\"그녀를 어쩌겠다는 거요? 왜 그녀를 괴롭히는 거요? 그녀는 아무 짓도 하지 않았소, 아무 짓도!…….\"\n\n법관들은 그를 달래려 했다. 그렇게 십 분쯤 지났다. 마침내 자리를 비웠던 미하일 마카로비치가 황급히 방으로 들어와, 크고 흥분된 목소리로 검사에게 말했다.\n\n\"그녀는 옮겨놨소, 아래층에 있소. 여러분, 이 가엾은 사람에게 한마디만 하게 해주시겠소? 당신들이 보는 앞에서 말이오, 여러분, 당신들이 보는 앞에서.\"\n\n\"얼마든지요, 미하일 마카로비치.\" 예심판사가 대답했다. \"지금 경우에는 그것을 반대할 이유가 없소.\"\n\n\"이보게, 드미트리 표도로비치, 여보게.\" 경찰서장이 입을 열었는데, 흥분한 그의 얼굴에는 이 불운한 피고를 향한 따뜻한, 거의 부정(父情)에 가까운 감정이 어려 있었다. \"내가 직접 자네의 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 아래층으로 데려가서 주인집 딸들에게 보살피라고 맡겨두었고, 그 막시모프라는 노인이 줄곧 그녀 곁에 붙어 있네. 그리고 내가 그녀를 달랬네, 알겠나? 달래고 진정시켜 주었어. 자네가 결백을 밝혀야 하니 그녀가 자네를 방해해선 안 되고, 자네 기를 꺾어선 안 된다고, 안 그러면 자네가 정신을 잃고 증언에서 엉뚱한 말을 할 수도 있다고 단단히 일러두었네. 사실 그녀와 이야기를 나눴더니 그녀가 알아듣더군. 그녀는 분별 있는 처자야, 여보게, 마음씨 고운 처자야, 자네를 도와달라며 이 늙은 손에 입이라도 맞출 기세였다네. 그녀가 직접 나를 보냈네, 자기 걱정은 말라고 자네에게 전해달라고 말일세. 그러니 나는 가봐야 하네, 여보게, 가서 자네가 그녀 때문에 마음 놓고 진정해 있다고 전해줘야겠네. 그러니 자네도 진정해야 하네, 알겠나? 내가 그녀에게 부당하게 굴었어; 그녀는 그리스도교도의 영혼을 지녔어, 여러분, 그렇소, 분명히 말하지만, 그녀는 온순한 영혼이고 아무 잘못도 없소. 그래, 그녀에게 뭐라고 전할까, 드미트리 표도로비치? 가만히 앉아 있겠나, 안 그러겠나?\"\n\n이 마음씨 좋은 경찰서장은 규정에 어긋나는 말을 잔뜩 했지만, 그루셴카의 고통이, 한 동포의 고통이 그의 마음씨 고운 가슴을 울렸고, 그의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다. 미탸는 벌떡 일어나 그에게 달려갔다.\n\n\"용서하시오, 여러분, 아, 허락해주시오, 허락해주시오!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신은 천사의 마음을 지녔소, 천사 말이오, 미하일 마카로비치, 그녀를 위해 감사드리오. 진정하겠소, 정말로 진정하고 명랑해지겠소. 부디 그 다정한 마음으로 그녀에게 전해주시오, 내가 명랑하다고, 아주 명랑하다고, 그녀에게 당신 같은 수호천사가 있다는 걸 알았으니 곧 웃게 되리라고. 나는 이걸 곧 끝낼 거고, 풀려나는 대로 그녀에게 갈 거요, 두고 보면 알 거요, 기다리라고 하시오. 여러분.\" 그가 두 법관 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이제 당신들에게 내 영혼을 송두리째 열어 보이겠소; 모든 걸 쏟아내겠소. 우리는 이걸 곧장 끝낼 거요, 명랑하게 끝낼 거요. 결국 우리는 이걸 두고 웃게 될 거요, 안 그렇소? 그러나 여러분, 저 여인은 내 마음의 여왕이오. 아, 이 말만은 하게 해주시오. 지금 이 한 가지만은 말해두겠소……. 보아하니 나는 명예로운 분들과 함께 있구려. 그녀는 내 빛이고, 내 성스러운 사람이오, 당신들이 알기만 한다면! 그녀가 '당신과 함께 죽음으로 가겠다'고 외치는 걸 들었소? 그런데 한 푼 없는 거지인 내가 그녀를 위해 무얼 해주었단 말이오? 어째서 나를 그토록 사랑하는 거요? 나처럼 굼뜨고 못생긴 짐승 같은 인간이, 이 추한 얼굴을 하고서 어떻게 그런 사랑을 받을 자격이 있어, 그녀가 나와 함께 유형길에 오를 각오까지 한단 말이오? 그리고 조금 전에 나를 위해 어떻게 당신들 발치에 엎드렸는지!……. 그런데도 그녀는 자존심이 강하고 아무 잘못도 없소! 내가 어찌 그녀를 흠모하지 않을 수 있겠소, 어찌 외치며 조금 전처럼 그녀에게 달려가지 않을 수 있겠소? 여러분, 용서하시오! 그러나 이제, 이제 나는 위안을 얻었소.\"\n\n그러고는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 그것은 행복한 눈물이었다. 그는 곧장 정신을 차렸다. 늙은 경찰서장은 무척 흐뭇해 보였고, 법관들도 그러했다. 그들은 심문이 새로운 국면으로 접어들고 있음을 느꼈다. 경찰서장이 나가자 미탸는 정말이지 명랑해졌다.\n\n\"자, 여러분, 이제 나는 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소, 전적으로 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소. 그리고 이 사소한 곁가지들만 없다면, 우리는 단번에 서로를 이해할 수 있을 거요. 또 그 곁가지 타령이군. 나는 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소, 여러분, 다만 우리는 서로 믿어야 한다는 걸 분명히 해두겠소, 당신들이 나를, 내가 당신들을 믿어야 한다는 말이오, 안 그러면 이건 끝이 없을 거요. 나는 당신들을 위해 하는 말이오. 본론으로 갑시다, 여러분, 본론으로, 그리고 내 영혼을 함부로 뒤지지 마시오; 사소한 것들로 나를 들볶지 말고, 다만 사실과 중요한 것만 물으시오, 그러면 당장 흡족하게 해드리겠소. 곁가지 따위는 집어치웁시다!\"\n\n미탸는 이렇게 말했다. 심문이 다시 시작되었다.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Second Ordeal\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치, 당신이 이렇게 선뜻 대답해 주시니 우리가 얼마나 힘을 얻는지 모르실 겁니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 활기 띤 태도로 말했는데, 조금 전에 안경을 벗은 그 유난히 불거진, 근시의, 연회색 눈에는 만족감이 또렷이 빛나고 있었다. \"그리고 당신은 상호 신뢰에 관해 아주 옳은 지적을 하셨습니다. 이렇게 중대한 사건에서는, 혐의를 받는 자가 진심으로 자기를 변호하기를 바라고 또 그럴 처지에 있다면, 그 신뢰 없이는 일을 진척시키기가 때로 도저히 불가능하지요. 우리 쪽에서도 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 다 할 것이며, 우리가 이 사건을 어떻게 다루는지는 당신 스스로도 보실 수 있습니다. 동의하십니까, 이폴리트 키릴로비치?\" 그는 검사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"오, 두말할 나위가 없지요.\" 검사가 대답했다. 그의 어조는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 충동적인 태도에 견주면 다소 차가운 데가 있었다.\n\n여기서 한 번에 일러 두어야 할 것이 있다. 얼마 전에야 우리 고장으로 부임해 온 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 처음부터 우리 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치에게 각별한 존경심을 품었고, 거의 그의 막역한 벗이 되어 있었다. 그는 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 심리학자이자 웅변가로서의 비범한 재능과, 그가 품은 불만의 정당함을 전적으로 믿어 의심치 않은 거의 유일한 사람이었다. 그는 페테르부르크에 있을 적에 이미 그의 소문을 들었던 것이다. 한편 우리의 이 \"인정받지 못한\" 검사가 온 세상에서 진정으로 좋아한 단 한 사람이 바로 이 젊은 니콜라이 파르페노비치였다. 모크로예로 오는 길에 두 사람은 이번 사건에 관해 의견을 맞출 시간이 있었다. 그래서 지금 탁자에 마주 앉은 자리에서, 명민한 후배는 선배 동료의 얼굴에 떠오르는 온갖 기색을—반쯤 흘린 말이며, 눈짓이며, 윙크 하나하나까지—놓치지 않고 알아채어 그 뜻을 헤아렸다.\n\n\"여러분, 제 이야기를 제 식대로 하게만 해 주시고 시시한 질문으로 끊지만 마십시오. 그러면 당장 모든 걸 다 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 미탸가 흥분하여 말했다.\n\n\"좋습니다! 감사합니다. 그런데 당신의 진술을 듣기에 앞서, 우리에게 무척 흥미로운 또 다른 자그마한 사실 하나를 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까? 다름이 아니라 어제 다섯 시 무렵 당신이 친구인 표트르 일리치 페르호틴에게서 권총을 담보로 십 루블을 빌리셨다는 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"전당을 잡혔지요, 여러분. 십 루블에 전당을 잡혔습니다. 더 무엇이 있겠습니까? 그게 전부입니다. 시내로 돌아오자마자 전당을 잡혔지요.\"\n\n\"시내로 돌아오셨다고요? 그럼 시외로 나가 계셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 사십 베르스타나 떨어진 시골로 갔다 왔지요. 모르셨습니까?\"\n\n검사와 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다.\n\n\"그럼 어제 아침부터 차례차례, 당신이 한 일을 빠짐없이 조리 있게 묘사하는 것으로 이야기를 시작하시는 게 어떻겠습니까? 가령 당신이 어째서 시내를 비웠는지, 또 언제 떠났다가 언제 돌아왔는지—그런 사실들을 우리가 여쭙도록 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"처음부터 그렇게 물으셨어야지요.\" 미탸가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"정 원하신다면 어제부터가 아니라 그저께 아침부터 시작합시다. 그러면 내가 어떻게, 왜, 어디로 갔는지를 아시게 될 겁니다. 여러분, 나는 그저께 이 고을의 삼소노프라는 상인에게 갔습니다. 확실한 담보를 잡히고 그에게서 삼천 루블을 빌리려 했지요. 급한 일이었습니다, 여러분. 별안간 닥친 필요였지요.\"\n\n\"잠깐 끊겠습니다.\" 검사가 정중하게 끼어들었다. \"어째서 하필 그 액수, 삼천 루블이 그렇게 급하게 필요하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"오, 여러분, 어떻게, 언제, 왜, 어째서 하필 그만큼의 돈이고 그보다 적지도 많지도 않은가, 그런 잡다한 사정까지 들추실 건 없습니다. 그러다간 세 권짜리 책이 될 테고, 그러고도 후기까지 붙여야 할 겁니다!\"\n\n미탸는 이 모든 말을, 온전한 진실을 다 털어놓으려는 마음에 더없이 선의로 가득 찬 사람 특유의 사람 좋으면서도 조바심 어린 허물없는 투로 했다.\n\n\"여러분!\" 그가 황급히 자신을 고쳐 잡으며 말했다. \"내가 가만있지 못하는 걸 두고 노여워 마시기를 다시 한번 청합니다. 거듭 믿어 주십시오, 나는 여러분에게 더없는 존경을 품고 있으며 사태의 참된 형편을 이해하고 있습니다. 내가 취했다고는 여기지 마십시오. 지금은 아주 말짱합니다. 게다가 취했다 한들 거리낄 것도 없지요. 나로 말하자면, 아시다시피 그 속담 그대로입니다. '맨정신일 땐 바보요, 취하면 현자라.' 하하! 그런데 여러분, 보아하니 우리의 해명이 끝나기 전엔, 그러니까 그 전엔 여러분께 농담을 늘어놓는 게 마땅한 일이 아니로군요. 또 나도 지켜야 할 체면이라는 게 있고요. 지금 이 순간의 처지가 다르다는 건 잘 압니다. 어쨌거나 나는 죄인의 처지에 놓여 있고, 그러니 여러분과 대등한 처지와는 거리가 멉니다. 그리고 나를 감시하는 것이 여러분의 직무지요. 내가 그리고리에게 한 짓을 두고 머리를 쓰다듬어 주길 바랄 수야 없는 노릇입니다. 노인의 머리통을 무사히 깨뜨릴 수는 없는 법이니까요. 짐작컨대 그 일로 나를 반년쯤, 아니면 한 일 년쯤 교화소에 처넣으시겠지요. 형벌이 어떻게 되는지는 모르겠습니다만—그래도 신분상의 권리를 잃거나, 내 신분을 잃지는 않겠지요? 그러니 보십시오, 여러분, 나는 우리 사이의 차이를 잘 알고 있습니다…… 하지만 그런 질문들로는 하느님조차 쩔쩔매게 만들 수 있다는 걸 여러분도 아셔야 합니다. '어떻게 발을 디뎠나? 어디에 발을 디뎠나? 언제 발을 디뎠나? 무엇을 밟았나?' 이런 식으로 계속하시면 나는 뒤죽박죽이 될 테고, 여러분은 그걸 모조리 나에게 불리하게 적어 두시겠지요. 그러면 그게 무엇으로 이어지겠습니까? 아무것도 아닙니다! 그리고 설령 지금 내가 떠드는 게 헛소리라 해도, 끝까지 말하게 해 주십시오. 그러면 여러분은, 명예롭고 교양 있는 분들이시니, 나를 용서해 주실 겁니다! 끝으로 여러분께 한 가지 청하건대, 그 틀에 박힌 심문 방식은 좀 버려 주십시오. 그러니까 무슨 보잘것없는 사소한 것에서부터 시작해서, 내가 어떻게 일어났는지, 아침으로 무엇을 먹었는지, 어떻게 침을 뱉었는지, 어디에 침을 뱉었는지, 그렇게 죄인의 주의를 흐트러뜨려 놓고는 느닷없이 압도적인 질문으로 머리를 후려치는 거 말입니다. '누구를 죽였느냐? 누구를 털었느냐?' 하하! 그게 여러분의 규정된 방식이고, 거기에 여러분의 온갖 술수가 들어 있지요. 그런 식으로 농사꾼들의 경계를 풀어 놓을 수야 있겠지만, 나한테는 안 통합니다. 나도 그 수법을 다 압니다. 나도 관직에 있어 봤거든요. 하하하! 노여워하지 않으시지요, 여러분? 내 무례를 용서하시겠지요?\" 그는 거의 놀라울 정도의 사람 좋은 낯빛으로 그들을 바라보며 외쳤다. \"그저 미탸 카라마조프니까요, 아시다시피, 그러니 너그러이 봐주시면 됩니다. 분별 있는 사람이라면 변명의 여지가 없겠지만, 미탸에게는 용서가 되는 거지요. 하하!\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 귀를 기울이며 함께 웃었다. 검사는 웃지 않았으나, 한 음절도, 아주 미세한 움직임도, 그의 얼굴 어느 한구석의 가장 작은 경련 하나도 놓치지 않으려는 듯 미탸에게서 날카로운 시선을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"우리는 처음부터 당신을 그렇게 대해 왔습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 여전히 웃으면서 말했다. \"아침에 어떻게 일어났는지, 아침으로 무얼 먹었는지 따위를 물어 당신을 곤혹스럽게 만들려 들지 않았지요. 우리는 오히려 가장 중대한 질문들로 시작하지 않았습니까.\"\n\n\"이해합니다. 그것을 보았고 또 고맙게 여겼지요. 그리고 지금 나에게 베푸시는 친절은 더더욱 고맙게 여깁니다. 그건 여러분의 고결한 마음에 어울리는, 유례없는 친절입니다. 여기 우리 셋은 다 신사이니, 모든 것을 교양 있고 잘 자란 사람들 사이의 상호 신뢰의 바탕 위에 놓읍시다. 고귀한 혈통과 명예라는 공통의 끈으로 맺어진 사람들 말입니다. 어쨌든 이 순간만큼은, 내 명예가 공격받고 있는 내 생애의 이 순간만큼은, 여러분을 나의 가장 좋은 벗으로 여기게 해 주십시오. 그렇다고 여러분께 무례가 되는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"천만에요. 모든 걸 참으로 훌륭하게 표현하셨습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 위엄 있는 동의를 담아 대답했다.\n\n\"그러니 그 시시한 질문들은 이제 그만, 여러분, 그 까다로운 질문들은 다 그만둡시다!\" 미탸가 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"안 그러면 도대체 우리가 어디까지 가게 될지 알 수가 없지요! 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"당신의 현명한 충고를 전적으로 따르겠습니다.\" 검사가 미탸에게 말하며 끼어들었다. \"하지만 내 질문을 거두지는 않겠습니다. 어째서 당신에게 그 액수가, 그러니까 바로 삼천 루블이 필요했는지를 정확히 아는 것이 지금 우리에게는 무엇보다 중요합니다.\"\n\n\"왜 그게 필요했느냐고요?…… 오, 이런저런 일 때문에…… 뭐, 빚을 갚으려던 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"누구에게 진 빚이지요?\"\n\n\"그건 절대로 대답하지 않겠습니다, 여러분. 대답할 수 없어서도, 감히 못 해서도, 또 그게 나에게 해가 되어서도 아닙니다. 그건 다 하찮은 일이고 더없이 사소한 거니까요. 다만—말하지 않겠습니다. 그건 원칙의 문제이기 때문이지요. 그건 내 사생활이고, 나는 내 사생활에 그 누구도 끼어드는 걸 허락하지 않습니다. 그게 내 원칙입니다. 당신의 질문은 이 사건과 아무 상관이 없고, 사건과 상관없는 것은 무엇이든 내 사사로운 일입니다. 나는 빚을 갚으려 했습니다. 명예의 빚을 갚으려 했지요. 하지만 누구에게인지는 말하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그것을 기록해 두는 걸 허락하시지요.\" 검사가 말했다.\n\n\"얼마든지요. 내가 말하지 않겠다고, 말하지 않겠노라고 적으십시오. 말하는 걸 불명예로 여길 거라고 적으십시오. 에잇! 적으셔도 됩니다. 어차피 시간을 달리 쓸 데도 없으실 테니.\"\n\n\"한 가지 주의를 드리고, 혹 모르고 계실까 하여 다시 한번 일러 드리겠습니다.\" 검사가 묘하게 준엄한 무게를 실어 입을 열었다. \"지금 당신에게 던지는 질문들에 대답하지 않을 완전한 권리가 당신에게 있으며, 우리 쪽에서는 당신이 이런저런 까닭으로 대답하기를 거절할 경우 그 답을 강요할 권리가 없습니다. 그것은 전적으로 당신 개인의 판단에 달린 문제입니다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 지금과 같은 경우에 이런저런 증언을 거절함으로써 당신이 스스로에게 얼마만 한 손해를 입히게 되는지를 설명하고 일러 드리는 것이 우리의 의무이지요. 그러고 나서 다시 말씀을 이어 가시기를 청하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"여러분, 나는 노여운 게 아닙니다…… 나는……\" 미탸가 다소 머쓱해진 어조로 중얼거렸다. \"자, 여러분, 그러니까 그때 내가 찾아간 그 삼소노프 말입니다……\"\n\n물론 우리는 이미 독자가 아는 이야기를 그의 진술로 되풀이하지는 않겠다. 미탸는 조바심을 내며 아주 사소한 것 하나도 빠뜨리지 않으려 애썼다. 동시에 그는 그것을 어서 끝내 버리고 싶어 서둘렀다. 그러나 그가 증언을 하는 동안 그것이 받아 적혔으므로, 그들은 줄곧 그를 멈춰 세워야 했다. 미탸는 이것을 싫어했으나 따랐고, 화를 내면서도 여전히 기분은 좋게 유지했다. 사실 그는 이따금 \"여러분, 이건 천사라도 인내심을 잃게 만들 노릇이오!\"라거나 \"여러분, 나를 약 올려 봐야 소용없소\"라고 외치기는 했다.\n\n그러나 그렇게 외치면서도 그는 한동안 너그럽고 활달한 기분을 지켜 갔다. 그렇게 그는 이틀 전에 삼소노프가 자기를 어떻게 바보로 만들었는지를 그들에게 이야기했다. (이제 그는 자기가 농락당했다는 것을 완전히 깨달은 터였다.) 여비를 마련하려고 시계를 육 루블에 판 일은 법관들에게 처음 듣는 사실이었다. 그들은 곧 크게 흥미를 보였으며, 심지어—미탸의 격렬한 분노를 자아내게도—그 사실을 당시 그의 주머니에 한 푼도 거의 없었다는 정황을 보강해 주는 부차적 증거로 적어 두어야 한다고 여겼다. 미탸는 차츰 무뚝뚝해지기 시작했다. 그러고는 랴가비를 만나러 간 여정과, 숨 막히는 오두막에서 보낸 밤 따위를 묘사한 뒤, 시내로 돌아온 대목에 이르렀다. 여기서 그는 별로 다그치지도 않았는데 그루셴카 때문에 겪은 질투의 고통을 시시콜콜 들려주기 시작했다.\n\n사람들은 잠자코 주의를 기울여 그의 말을 들었다. 그들은 특히 그가 그루셴카를 감시하려고 표도르 파블로비치의 정원 뒤편 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 집에 잠복할 자리를 마련했던 정황과, 스메르댜코프가 그에게 정보를 가져다주던 정황을 캐물었다. 그들은 이 점을 유독 강조하며 받아 적었다. 자기 질투에 관해 그는 열을 올려 길게 이야기했는데, 자기의 더없이 은밀한 감정을 이를테면 \"만인의 치욕거리\"로 드러내는 것이 속으로 부끄러웠으나, 진실을 말하기 위해 그 수치심을 분명히 이겨 내려 했다. 그가 이야기하는 동안 예심판사가, 그리고 그보다 더욱 검사가 그를 뚫어지게 응시하던 그 싸늘한 준엄함은 마침내 그를 상당히 머쓱하게 만들었다.\n\n'며칠 전만 해도 내가 여자 얘기로 시답잖은 소리를 늘어놓던 저 애송이 니콜라이 파르페노비치며, 저 비실거리는 검사 따위에게 이런 이야기를 들려줄 만한 가치가 없는데.' 그는 우울하게 생각했다. '치욕스러운 일이지. 참아라, 겸손하라, 입을 다물라.' 그는 그 시구로 상념을 맺었다. 그러나 그는 다시 마음을 다잡고 이야기를 이어 갔다. 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아간 대목에 이르자 그는 다시 기운을 되찾았고, 심지어 사건과는 아무 상관도 없는 그 부인에 관한 자그마한 일화 하나를 들려주고 싶어 했다. 그러나 예심판사가 그를 막고는, \"좀 더 본질적인 사안\"으로 넘어가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 정중히 권했다. 마침내 그가 자기 절망을 묘사하며, 호흘라코바 부인의 집을 나섰을 때 \"누군가를 죽여서라도 삼천 루블을 손에 넣겠다\"고 생각했다고 말하자, 그들은 다시 그를 멈춰 세우고 그가 \"누군가를 죽이려 했다\"고 적어 두었다. 미탸는 항의 없이 그것을 적게 두었다. 마침내 그는 이야기에서, 그루셴카가 자기를 속였다는 것과, 자정까지 그곳에 머물겠다고 해 놓고는 자기가 떠나자마자 삼소노프의 집에서 돌아왔다는 것을 알게 된 대목에 이르렀다.\n\n\"여러분, 그때 내가 페냐를 죽이지 않은 건 오로지 그럴 시간이 없었기 때문입니다.\" 이야기의 그 대목에서 그에게서 불쑥 이런 말이 터져 나왔다. 그것 또한 꼼꼼히 받아 적혔다. 미탸가 침울하게 기다리다가 아버지의 정원으로 뛰어든 경위를 막 이야기하려는데, 예심판사가 별안간 그를 멈춰 세우고는, 자기 옆 소파에 놓인 커다란 서류 가방을 열어 놋쇠 절굿공이를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이 물건을 알아보시겠습니까?\" 그가 그것을 미탸에게 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요.\" 그가 침울하게 웃었다. \"물론 알아봅니다. 어디 좀 봅시다…… 제기랄, 됐어요!\"\n\n\"이것을 언급하는 걸 잊으셨더군요.\" 예심판사가 지적했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 여러분께 이걸 숨길 까닭이야 없지요. 내가 이것 없이 일을 치를 수 있었으리라고 보십니까? 그저 깜빡 잊었을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 해서 이것으로 무장하게 되었는지 정확히 말씀해 주시지요.\"\n\n\"그러지요, 기꺼이 그러겠습니다, 여러분.\"\n\n그리하여 미탸는 자기가 절굿공이를 집어 들고 달려 나간 경위를 묘사했다.\n\n\"하지만 그런 무기로 무장하면서 무슨 목적을 품고 계셨습니까?\"\n\n\"목적이요? 아무 목적도 없었습니다. 그냥 집어 들고 달려 나갔지요.\"\n\n\"목적이 없었다면 무엇 때문에요?\"\n\n미탸의 분노가 확 타올랐다. 그는 \"그 애송이\"를 뚫어지게 바라보며 침울하고 악의에 찬 미소를 지었다. 그는 \"이런 자들\"에게 자기 질투 이야기를 그토록 진솔하고 거리낌 없이 털어놓은 것이 점점 더 부끄러워지고 있었다.\n\n\"그놈의 절굿공이야 아무래도 좋소!\" 그에게서 불쑥 이런 말이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"그래도—\"\n\n\"오, 개를 쫓으려고…… 오, 어두웠으니까…… 무슨 일이라도 생길까 봐.\"\n\n\"하지만 어둠이 무섭다면서, 전에 외출하실 때도 무기를 지니고 다닌 적이 있으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"으윽! 빌어먹을, 여러분! 당신들하고는 도무지 말이 안 통하는군!\" 미탸가 더는 참을 수 없을 만큼 약이 올라 외쳤고, 분노로 새빨개진 얼굴로 서기 쪽으로 돌아서서, 격분이 서린 목소리로 빠르게 말했다.\n\n\"당장 적으시오…… 당장…… '내가 아버지를…… 표도르 파블로비치를…… 절굿공이로 머리통을 후려쳐 죽이려고 그걸 움켜쥐었다!'고 말이오. 자, 이제 만족하십니까, 여러분? 마음이 후련해지셨소?\" 그는 도전하듯 법관들을 노려보며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 방금 그 진술을 한 것은, 우리와 우리가 던진 질문들에 대한 격분 때문이라는 걸 잘 압니다. 당신은 그 질문들을 시시하다고 여기시지만, 실은 그것이 본질적인 것이지요.\" 검사가 메마르게 응수했다.\n\n\"원, 참, 여러분! 그래요, 절굿공이를 집어 들었소…… 그런 순간에 사람이 물건을 집어 드는 게 무엇 때문이겠소? 무엇 때문인지 나도 모르겠소. 그냥 움켜쥐고 달려 나갔소—그게 다요. 나로서는, 여러분, 그건 넘어갑시다. 안 그러면 정말이지 더는 한마디도 안 하겠소.\"\n\n그는 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 짚고 한 손으로 머리를 괴고 앉았다. 그는 그들에게 옆으로 비스듬히 앉아 벽을 바라보며, 치미는 구역질을 억누르고 있었다. 사실 그는 일어서서 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠노라고, \"이 일로 나를 목매단다 해도\" 그러겠노라고 선언해 버리고 싶은 끔찍한 충동에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"보십시오, 여러분.\" 그는 가까스로 자신을 다스리며 마침내 입을 열었다. \"보십시오. 나는 여러분의 말을 듣고 있노라니 어떤 꿈에 사로잡힙니다…… 그게 내가 가끔 꾸는 꿈이거든요, 아시겠습니까…… 자주 꾸는데—언제나 똑같습니다…… 누군가가 나를 쫓고 있어요. 내가 무섭도록 두려워하는 누군가가…… 그자가 어둠 속에서, 밤중에 나를 쫓는 겁니다…… 내 뒤를 밟아 오고, 나는 어딘가에 그자를 피해 숨지요. 문 뒤나 찬장 뒤에, 비굴하게 숨는 겁니다. 그런데 가장 끔찍한 것은, 그자가 내가 어디 있는지 늘 알고 있으면서도, 일부러 모르는 척한다는 거예요. 내 고통을 늘리고 내 공포를 즐기려고 말입니다…… 지금 여러분이 하시는 게 바로 그겁니다. 꼭 그것과 똑같아요!\"\n\n\"그런 종류의 꿈을 꾸신다는 말씀이군요?\" 검사가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그것도 적어 두고 싶지 않으십니까?\" 미탸가 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 적을 필요는 없습니다. 하지만 그래도 참 별난 꿈을 꾸시는군요.\"\n\n\"지금은 꿈이 문제가 아닙니다, 여러분—이건 현실이고, 이건 실제 삶이오! 나는 늑대고 여러분은 사냥꾼이지요. 자, 그놈을 잡아 보시오!\"\n\n\"그런 비유를 드시는 건 잘못입니다……\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 유난히 부드러운 목소리로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아니요, 잘못이 아니오, 결코 아니지!\" 미탸가 다시 발끈했으나, 분노의 폭발은 분명히 그의 가슴을 후련하게 풀어 준 모양이었다. 그는 말 한마디 한마디마다 점점 기분이 누그러졌다. \"여러분의 질문에 시달리는 죄인이나 피고는 못 믿을 수 있겠지요. 하지만 명예로운 사람은, 마음에서 우러나는 명예로운 충동은(이건 떳떳하게 말합니다!)—안 되지요! 그건 여러분이 믿어야 합니다. 그러지 않을 권리는 정녕 없으니까요…… 그러나—\n\n잠잠하라, 가슴아,\n참아라, 겸손하라, 입을 다물라.\n\n자, 계속할까요?\" 그가 침울하게 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"그래 주신다면 고맙겠습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 대답했다.\n\n# Chapter V. The Third Ordeal\n\n미탸는 무뚝뚝하게 말하면서도, 자기 이야기의 사소한 대목 하나라도 잊거나 빠뜨리지 않으려고 그 어느 때보다 애쓰고 있는 것이 역력했다. 그는 어떻게 울타리를 뛰어넘어 아버지의 정원으로 들어갔는지, 어떻게 창가로 다가갔는지 들려주었고, 그 창문 아래에서 벌어진 일을 죄다 이야기했다. 정원에서 보낸 그 순간들 동안, 그루셴카가 아버지와 함께 있는지 아닌지를 그토록 애타게 알고 싶어 했던 자신을 괴롭히던 감정들을, 그는 또렷하고 정확하고 분명하게 묘사했다. 그러나 이상하게도 두 법관은 이제 무언가 무시무시한 신중함을 띠고 듣고 있었으며, 그를 차갑게 바라보면서 질문은 거의 던지지 않았다. 미탸는 그들의 얼굴에서 아무것도 읽어 낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"이자들은 화가 나서 토라진 거야.\" 그는 생각했다. \"흥, 마음대로 하라지!\"\n\n마침내 그루셴카가 왔다는 \"신호\"를 아버지에게 보내어 창문을 열게 하기로 결심한 대목을 묘사할 때, 두 법관은 \"신호\"라는 말에 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았으니, 마치 이런 맥락에서 그 말이 지니는 의미를 도무지 파악하지 못한 듯했다. 미탸가 그것을 눈치챌 정도였다. 마침내 아버지가 창밖을 내다보는 것을 보고 증오가 치밀어 호주머니에서 절굿공이를 꺼내 든 순간에 이르자, 그는 마치 일부러 그런 듯 별안간 말을 뚝 멈추었다. 그는 벽을 응시하며 앉아 있었으나, 그들의 눈길이 자기에게 못박혀 있다는 것을 느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 예심판사가 말했다. \"흉기를 꺼내 들었고…… 그래서 그다음엔 무슨 일이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"그다음? 흥, 그다음엔 내가 그를 죽인 거지요…… 머리를 후려쳐서 두개골을 박살냈고요…… 그게 당신들 각본인 것 같은데. 바로 그거지요!\"\n\n그의 눈이 별안간 번뜩였다. 짓눌려 있던 모든 분노가 갑자기 그의 영혼 속에서 엄청난 격렬함으로 타올랐다.\n\n\"우리 각본이라고요?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 되받았다. \"그래서—당신 각본은요?\"\n\n미탸는 눈을 떨어뜨리고 한참 동안 말이 없었다.\n\n\"내 각본 말이오, 여러분? 자, 그건 이랬소.\" 그는 나직이 입을 열었다. \"누군가의 눈물 때문이었는지, 어머니가 하느님께 기도를 드린 덕분인지, 아니면 바로 그 순간 선한 천사가 내게 입을 맞춰 준 것인지는 모르겠소. 하지만 악마가 패배했지요. 나는 창가에서 뛰쳐나와 울타리 쪽으로 달렸소. 아버지는 깜짝 놀라, 그제야 처음으로 나를 보더니 소리를 지르며 창에서 펄쩍 물러났소. 나는 그걸 아주 똑똑히 기억하오. 나는 정원을 가로질러 울타리로 달려갔고…… 그러다 거기서, 내가 울타리에 걸터앉아 있을 때 그리고리에게 붙들렸소.\"\n\n그 대목에서 그는 마침내 눈을 들어 듣는 이들을 바라보았다. 그들은 더없이 흔들림 없는 주의를 기울이며 그를 응시하고 있는 것 같았다. 일종의 분노의 발작이 미탸의 영혼을 사로잡았다.\n\n\"아니, 당신들은 지금 이 순간에도 날 비웃고 있군, 여러분!\" 그가 별안간 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"무엇 때문에 그렇게 생각하십니까?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"단 한마디도 안 믿으니까요—바로 그래서지요! 물론 나도 내가 결정적인 대목에 이르렀다는 건 알고 있소. 노인네는 지금 두개골이 박살난 채 저기 누워 있는데, 나는—그를 죽이고 싶었던 심정이며 절굿공이를 움켜쥔 경위를 극적으로 묘사해 놓고는—별안간 창가에서 도망쳐 버린다. 한 편의 로맨스로군! 시(詩)고! 마치 사내가 제 말 하나로 믿어지기라도 할 것처럼 말이오. 하하! 당신들은 비웃는 자들이오, 여러분!\"\n\n그러더니 그는 의자 위에서 홱 몸을 돌려 의자가 삐걱거렸다.\n\n\"그런데 혹시 알아챘습니까,\" 검사가 미탸의 흥분에는 아랑곳하지 않는 듯 별안간 물었다. \"창가에서 도망쳐 나올 때, 정원으로 통하는 문이 열려 있던지 어떤지 알아챘습니까?\"\n\"아니오, 열려 있지 않았소.\"\n\n\"열려 있지 않았다고요?\"\n\n\"닫혀 있었소. 게다가 누가 그걸 열 수 있었겠소? 흥! 그 문이라니. 잠깐만!\" 그는 별안간 무언가 생각이 미친 듯, 거의 흠칫 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 당신들은 문이 열려 있는 걸 발견했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 열려 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 당신들이 직접 연 게 아니라면 대체 누가 그 문을 열 수 있었단 말이오?\" 미탸가 몹시 놀라 소리쳤다.\n\n\"문은 열려 있었고, 당신 아버지를 살해한 자는 의심할 바 없이 바로 그 문으로 들어가, 범행을 마친 뒤 같은 문으로 다시 나갔소.\" 검사가 마치 한마디 한마디를 따로따로 새겨 내듯 신중하게 말했다. \"그건 더없이 명백합니다. 살인은 방 안에서 저질러졌고 창문을 통해서가 아니오. 그건 실시된 검시로 보나, 시신의 자세며 모든 것으로 보나 절대적으로 확실합니다. 그 정황에는 의심의 여지가 없소.\"\n\n미탸는 완전히 어안이 벙벙해졌다.\n\n\"하지만 그건 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이오!\" 그가 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 소리쳤다. \"나는…… 나는 안으로 들어가지 않았소…… 단언컨대, 분명히 말하지만, 내가 정원에 있던 내내, 그리고 정원에서 뛰쳐나올 때까지 문은 닫혀 있었소. 나는 그저 창가에 서서 창문 너머로 그를 보았을 뿐이오. 그게 다요, 그게 전부란 말이오…… 나는 마지막 순간까지 다 기억하고 있소. 설령 기억하지 못한다 해도 마찬가지요. 나는 그걸 아니까. 그 신호를 아는 사람은 스메르댜코프와 나, 그리고 죽은 그 양반밖에 없었으니 말이오. 그리고 그 양반은 신호 없이는 세상 그 누구에게도 문을 열어 주지 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"신호라고요? 무슨 신호 말입니까?\" 검사가 탐욕스럽다 못해 거의 발작적인 호기심으로 물었다. 그는 단번에 신중함과 위엄의 흔적을 죄다 잃어버렸다. 그는 일종의 비굴한 소심함으로 그 질문을 던졌다. 그는 자신이 전혀 모르고 있던 중대한 사실의 냄새를 맡았고, 미탸가 그것을 밝히지 않으려 할까 봐 벌써부터 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신들은 몰랐던 거로군!\" 미탸가 심술궂고 비웃는 미소를 지으며 그에게 눈을 찡긋해 보였다. \"내가 말해 주지 않는다면 어떻게 할 거요? 누구한테서 알아낼 수 있겠소? 그 신호에 관해 아는 사람은 아버지와 스메르댜코프, 그리고 나밖에 없었소. 그게 다요. 하늘도 알기야 하지만, 하늘이 당신들한테 말해 주진 않을 거요. 하지만 그건 흥미로운 사실이지. 그걸 가지고 무슨 탑을 쌓아 올릴지 누가 알겠소. 하하! 안심하시오, 여러분, 내가 밝혀 주겠소. 당신들 마음속엔 무슨 어리석은 생각이 들어 있군. 당신들은 지금 상대하고 있는 사람이 어떤 위인인지 모르고 있어! 당신들이 상대하고 있는 건 자기에게 불리하게, 제 손해를 무릅쓰고 증언하는 피의자란 말이오! 그렇소, 나는 명예를 아는 사람이고 당신들은—아니니까.\"\n\n검사는 군말 없이 이 말을 꿀꺽 삼켰다. 그는 새로운 사실을 듣고 싶어 안달이 나서 몸을 떨고 있었다. 미탸는 표도르 파블로비치가 스메르댜코프를 위해 고안해 낸 신호에 관한 모든 것을 시시콜콜 장황하게 들려주었다. 그는 창문을 두드리는 신호 하나하나가 무엇을 뜻하는지 정확히 일러 주었고, 그 신호를 탁자 위에 두드려 보였으며, 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 그가, 곧 미탸가 아버지에게 신호를 두드릴 때 \"그루셴카가 왔다\"는 신호를 두드렸을 거라고 하자, 그는 바로 그 신호, 곧 \"그루셴카가 왔다\"는 신호를 두드렸다고 정확히 답했다.\n\n\"자, 이제 당신들의 탑을 쌓아 올리시구려.\" 미탸가 말을 뚝 끊고는 다시 그들에게서 경멸하듯 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그러니까 그 신호를 안 사람은 돌아가신 당신 아버지와 당신, 그리고 하인 스메르댜코프뿐이었다는 거지요? 그밖에 다른 사람은 없고요?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 다시 한번 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 하인 스메르댜코프와, 그리고 하늘이오. 하늘에 관해서도 적어 두시오. 그게 쓸모가 있을지도 모르니. 게다가 당신들도 하느님이 필요해질 거요.\"\n\n그리고 그들은 물론 벌써 그것을 받아 적기 시작한 터였다. 그런데 받아 적는 동안 검사가 별안간 새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 만약 스메르댜코프도 이 신호를 알고 있었고, 당신은 부친의 죽음에 대한 모든 책임을 절대적으로 부인한다면, 어쩌면 그가 약속된 신호를 두드려 당신 부친으로 하여금 문을 열게 한 뒤…… 범행을 저지른 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n미탸는 깊은 빈정거림과 격렬한 증오가 어린 시선을 그에게 돌렸다. 그의 말없는 응시가 어찌나 길게 이어졌던지 검사가 눈을 깜박일 정도였다.\n\n\"또다시 여우를 잡았군요.\" 마침내 미탸가 말했다. \"당신들은 그 짐승의 꼬리를 붙들었어요. 하하! 검사 양반, 나는 당신 속내를 다 꿰뚫어 보고 있소. 당신은 물론, 내가 그 미끼에 덥석 달려들어 당신 부추김에 넘어가서는 있는 힘껏 '아이고! 스메르댜코프야, 그놈이 살인자라고' 하고 소리칠 줄 알았겠지. 그게 당신 속셈이었다고 실토하시구려. 실토하면 내가 계속 말해 주겠소.\"\n\n그러나 검사는 실토하지 않았다. 그는 입을 다물고 기다렸다.\n\n\"오산이오. 나는 '스메르댜코프 짓이다' 하고 소리칠 생각이 없으니까.\" 미탸가 말했다.\n\n\"그를 의심조차 하지 않는단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"흥, 당신들은 그를 의심하오?\"\n\n\"그도 의심받고 있소.\"\n\n미탸는 바닥에 시선을 못박았다.\n\n\"농담은 접어 두고.\" 그가 침울하게 내뱉었다. \"들어 보시오. 맨 처음부터, 그러니까 거의 내가 휘장 뒤에서 당신들한테로 뛰쳐나온 순간부터 내 머릿속엔 스메르댜코프 생각이 박혀 있었소. 여기 이렇게 앉아 내가 결백하다고 소리치면서도 줄곧 '스메르댜코프!' 하고 생각하고 있었던 거요. 스메르댜코프를 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었소. 사실 나도 방금 스메르댜코프를 떠올렸소. 하지만 그저 한순간이었지요. 거의 곧바로 '아니야, 스메르댜코프가 아니야' 하고 생각했소. 그건 그의 짓이 아니오, 여러분.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 달리 의심 가는 사람이라도 있습니까?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 조심스레 물었다.\n\n\"그게 누구일 수 있을지는 모르겠소. 하늘의 손인지 사탄의 손인지도. 하지만…… 스메르댜코프는 아니오.\" 미탸가 단호하게 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 무엇 때문에 그토록 자신 있게, 그토록 단호하게 그가 아니라고 단언하는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"내 확신에서, 내 인상에서 그러는 거요. 스메르댜코프는 더할 나위 없이 비굴한 성품의 인간이고 겁쟁이니까. 겁쟁이 정도가 아니라, 세상의 온갖 비겁함을 죄다 모아 두 다리로 걸어 다니게 한 표본이오. 그놈은 닭의 간을 가진 자요. 나와 이야기할 때면 늘 내가 자기를 죽일까 봐 벌벌 떨었지요. 내가 그놈한테 손찌검 한 번 한 적 없는데도 말이오. 그놈은 내 발치에 엎드려 질질 짜면서, 글자 그대로 바로 이 장화에 입을 맞추며 '제발 겁주지 말아 달라'고 애원했소. 들었소? '겁주지 말아 달라'고. 그런 말을 다 하다니! 흥, 나는 그놈한테 돈까지 줬소. 그놈은 칭얼대는 병아리요—병약하고, 간질에 걸렸고, 머리가 모자란—여덟 살짜리 아이라도 흠씬 두들겨 팰 수 있는 위인이오. 입에 올릴 만한 성품이라고는 없소. 스메르댜코프가 아니오, 여러분. 그놈은 돈에 관심도 없소. 내가 주는 선물도 받지 않았으니까. 게다가 그놈이 노인네를 죽일 무슨 동기가 있었겠소? 그놈은 어쩌면 그 양반의 아들일 가능성이 아주 높단 말이오, 알겠소—그 양반의 사생아 말이오. 그걸 알고 있소?\"\n\n\"우리도 그 풍문은 들었소. 하지만 당신도 부친의 아들이긴 마찬가지 아니오. 그런데도 당신 스스로 만나는 사람마다 부친을 죽이겠다고 떠벌렸잖소.\"\n\n\"그건 한 방 먹이는 소리로군! 게다가 더럽고 비열한 한 방이오! 나는 두렵지 않소! 오, 여러분, 그 말을 내 면전에 대고 하다니 너무 비열하지 않소? 비열하지요, 그건 내가 직접 당신들한테 말한 거니까. 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었을 뿐만 아니라, 죽일 수도 있었소. 게다가 한술 더 떠서, 내가 그를 거의 죽일 뻔했다고 자진해서 당신들한테 일부러 말해 주기까지 했단 말이오. 하지만 보다시피 나는 그를 죽이지 않았소. 보다시피 내 수호천사가 나를 구했지요—바로 그걸 당신들이 셈에 넣지 않은 거요. 그래서 그토록 비열하다는 거요. 나는 그를 죽이지 않았으니까, 죽이지 않았으니까! 들었소, 나는 그를 죽이지 않았다고.\"\n\n그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 심문 내내 그가 이토록 격앙된 적은 없었다.\n\n\"그런데 그놈은 당신들한테 뭐라고 말했소, 여러분—스메르댜코프 말이오.\" 그가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 별안간 덧붙였다. \"그 질문을 해도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"무슨 질문이든 하셔도 됩니다.\" 검사가 냉랭하고 엄격하게 대답했다. \"사건의 사실관계에 관한 질문이라면 무엇이든, 그리고 거듭 말하지만 우리는 당신이 하는 모든 질문에 답할 의무가 있소. 당신이 묻는 그 하인 스메르댜코프를, 우리는 침대에 의식을 잃고 누워 있는 채로 발견했소. 극도로 심한 간질 발작 상태였는데, 어쩌면 열 번쯤 거듭 일어났던 모양이오. 우리와 동행한 의사가 그를 진찰한 뒤, 어쩌면 오늘 밤을 넘기지 못할지도 모른다고 우리한테 말했소.\"\n\n\"흠, 그렇다면 그를 죽인 건 악마가 틀림없겠군.\" 미탸가 별안간 내뱉었으니, 마치 그 순간까지 \"스메르댜코프였을까 아니었을까?\" 하고 스스로에게 묻고 있던 듯했다.\n\n\"그 문제는 나중에 다시 다루기로 하지요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 결정을 내렸다. \"자, 이제 진술을 계속하시겠습니까?\"\n\n미탸는 잠시 쉬게 해 달라고 청했다. 그의 청은 정중하게 받아들여졌다. 쉬고 난 뒤 그는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 그는 눈에 띄게 의기소침해 있었다. 그는 기진맥진했고, 굴욕감에 빠졌으며, 정신적으로 흔들리고 있었다. 설상가상으로 검사가 마치 일부러 그러는 듯 \"사소한 대목\"을 두고 성가시게 끼어들어 그를 더욱 약 올렸다. 미탸가 노인이 자기 왼쪽 다리를 붙들고 있는 동안 담장에 걸터앉은 채 절굿공이로 그리고리의 머리를 후려쳤고, 그러고는 뛰어내려 그를 들여다본 경위를 채 다 묘사하기도 전에, 검사가 그를 멈춰 세우고 담장에 정확히 어떻게 걸터앉아 있었는지 묘사해 달라고 청했다. 미탸는 어리둥절했다.\n\n\"오, 나는 이렇게, 말 타듯 두 다리를 벌려서 한쪽 다리는 담장 이쪽에, 다른 쪽 다리는 저쪽에 걸치고 앉아 있었소.\"\n\n\"그러면 절굿공이는요?\"\n\n\"절굿공이는 손에 들고 있었소.\"\n\n\"호주머니에 들어 있던 게 아니고요? 그걸 정확히 기억합니까? 당신이 그에게 가한 건 세찬 일격이었소?\"\n\n\"세찬 일격이 틀림없었겠지요. 한데 왜 묻는 거요?\"\n\n\"수고스럽겠지만 그때 담장에 걸터앉았던 것과 똑같이 의자에 앉아서, 팔을 어떻게, 어느 방향으로 휘둘렀는지 우리한테 보여 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"나를 놀리는 거요, 그렇지 않소?\" 미탸가 거만하게 상대를 노려보며 물었다. 그러나 상대는 움찔하지도 않았다.\n\n미탸는 홱 몸을 돌려 의자에 두 다리를 벌리고 걸터앉더니 팔을 휘둘렀다.\n\n\"바로 이렇게 그를 후려쳤소! 이렇게 그를 때려눕혔단 말이오! 더 뭘 바라는 거요?\"\n\n\"고맙소. 그럼 이제 수고스럽지만 왜 뛰어내렸는지, 무슨 목적으로, 무엇을 염두에 두고 그랬는지 설명해 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 빌어먹을!…… 내가 다치게 한 자를 들여다보려고 뛰어내렸소…… 뭣 때문이었는지는 나도 모르겠소!\"\n\n\"그토록 흥분해서 도망치던 와중에도 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 흥분해서 도망치던 와중에도 그랬소.\"\n\n\"그를 도와주려고 한 거요?\"\n\n\"도와주려고!…… 그렇소, 어쩌면 그를 도와주고 싶었는지도 모르겠소…… 기억이 안 나오.\"\n\n\"기억이 안 난다고요? 그럼 당신이 무얼 하고 있는지 제대로 알지도 못했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않소. 나는 모든 걸 기억하고 있소—세세한 대목 하나하나까지. 나는 그를 들여다보려고 뛰어내렸고, 손수건으로 그의 얼굴을 닦아 주었소.\"\n\n\"우리도 당신 손수건을 봤소. 그를 다시 의식이 들게 하려는 바람이었소?\"\n\n\"그걸 바랐는지 어땠는지는 모르겠소. 나는 그저 그가 살아 있는지 아닌지 확인하고 싶었을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"아! 확인하고 싶었다고요? 그래서, 그다음엔요?\"\n\n\"나는 의사가 아니오. 판단할 수가 없었소. 나는 그를 죽였구나 생각하며 도망쳤소. 그런데 지금 보니 그가 살아났더군요.\"\n\n\"훌륭하오.\" 검사가 말했다. \"고맙소. 내가 알고 싶었던 건 그게 전부요. 그럼 계속해 주시오.\"\n\n아아! 미탸의 머릿속에는, 비록 그것을 기억하고 있었으면서도, 자기가 연민에서 뛰어내렸으며 쓰러진 사람 위에 서서 후회의 말까지 몇 마디 내뱉었다는 것을 그들에게 말할 생각이 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. \"영감, 당신은 화를 입었구려—어쩔 수가 없소. 자, 거기 누워 계셔야겠소.\"\n\n검사가 끌어낼 수 있는 결론은 단 하나뿐이었다. 곧 그자가 \"그런 순간에, 그런 흥분 상태에서, 오로지 자기 범행의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 어떤지를 확인할 목적으로 뛰어내렸으며, 따라서 그런 순간에조차 대단한 완력과 냉정함과 결단력과 선견지명을 갖춘 인간이 틀림없다\"는…… 따위의 결론이었다. 검사는 흡족했다. \"'사소한 것들'로 이 신경질적인 작자를 자극했더니, 의도한 것보다 더 많은 말을 했군.\"\n\n미탸는 고통스러운 노력을 기울이며 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 이번에는 곧바로 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게 제지당했다.\n\n\"어쩌다가 두 손에 그토록 피를 잔뜩 묻힌 채로, 게다가 보아하니 얼굴에도 피가 묻은 채로 하녀 페도시야 마르코브나한테 달려가게 된 거요?\"\n\n\"흥, 나는 그때 피라곤 전혀 알아채지 못했소.\" 미탸가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 충분히 있을 법한 일이오. 그런 일이 가끔 일어나지요.\" 검사가 니콜라이 파르페노비치와 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"나는 그저 알아채지 못했소. 그 점은 당신 말이 옳소, 검사 양반.\" 미탸가 별안간 수긍했다.\n\n다음으로는 미탸가 \"비켜서서\" 두 사람의 행복에 길을 터 주기로 별안간 결심한 사연이 이어졌다. 그러나 그는 전처럼 그들에게 자기 속내를 털어놓고 \"제 영혼의 여왕\"에 관해 이야기할 결심이 서지 않았다. 그는 \"빈대처럼 자기한테 들러붙는\" 이 냉랭한 위인들 앞에서 그녀를 입에 올리기가 싫었다. 그래서 그들이 거듭 묻는 말에 그는 짧고 퉁명스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"흥, 나는 스스로 목숨을 끊기로 결심했소. 내게 무슨 살아갈 까닭이 남아 있었겠소? 그 물음이 내 면전을 빤히 들여다보고 있었소. 그녀의 첫 정당한 연인이 돌아왔으니, 그녀에게 몹쓸 짓을 했으나 오 년이 지난 뒤 부랴부랴 돌아와 사랑을 바치고, 결혼으로 그 잘못을 속죄하겠다는 사내가 말이오…… 그래서 나는 이제 내겐 모든 게 끝났다는 걸 알았소…… 게다가 내 등 뒤에는 치욕이, 그리고 그 피—그리고리의 피가 있었지요…… 내게 무슨 살아갈 까닭이 있었겠소? 그래서 나는 저당 잡혀 둔 권총을 되찾으러 갔던 거요. 거기에 총알을 재어 내일 내 머리통에 한 발 박아 넣으려고.\"\n\n\"그러면서 그 전날 밤엔 성대한 잔치를 벌였다?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그 전날 밤엔 성대한 잔치를 벌였지. 빌어먹을, 여러분! 제발 서둘러서 끝내 주시오. 나는 여기서 멀지 않은 곳, 마을 너머에서 총으로 자결할 작정이었고, 새벽 다섯 시에 그 일을 치를 계획이었소. 그리고 호주머니엔 이미 쪽지가 한 장 들어 있었소. 페르호틴의 집에서 권총에 총알을 잴 때 쓴 거요. 자, 여기 그 편지가 있소. 읽어 보시오! 당신들 들으라고 하는 말은 아니지만.\" 그가 경멸하듯 덧붙였다. 그는 그것을 조끼 호주머니에서 꺼내 탁자 위로 내던졌다. 두 법관은 호기심 어린 눈으로 그것을 읽었고, 으레 그렇듯 사건에 관련된 서류에 그것을 끼워 넣었다.\n\n\"그런데 페르호틴의 집에서 손을 씻을 생각조차 안 한 거요? 그때는 의심을 살까 봐 두렵지 않았소?\"\n\n\"무슨 의심 말이오? 의심을 사든 안 사든, 나는 어차피 똑같이 이리로 말을 몰아 다섯 시에 자결했을 테고, 당신들은 무슨 일을 하기엔 너무 늦었을 거요. 만약 아버지한테 그런 일이 벌어지지 않았더라면, 당신들은 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐을 테고 이리로 오지도 않았을 거요. 오, 이건 악마의 짓이오. 아버지를 죽인 건 악마요, 당신들이 그토록 빨리 알아낸 것도 악마를 통해서지. 대체 어떻게 이렇게 빨리 여기까지 온 거요? 놀랍군, 한 편의 꿈 같소!\"\n\n\"페르호틴 씨의 말로는, 당신이 그를 찾아갔을 때 두 손에…… 피 묻은 두 손에…… 돈을…… 큰돈을…… 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 쥐고 있었고, 그 집 사환 아이도 그걸 봤다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"사실이오, 여러분. 그랬던 기억이 나오.\"\n\n\"자, 그런데 여기 한 가지 자그마한 의문점이 떠오르오. 사실관계로 보나, 시간을 따져 보나 당신은 집에 들르지 않았던 것으로 보이는데, 어떻게 별안간 그렇게 많은 돈이 생겼는지 우리한테 알려 줄 수 있겠소?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 더없이 부드럽게 운을 떼었다.\n\n이렇게 단도직입으로 질문이 던져지자 검사는 눈썹을 찌푸렸으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 말을 가로막지는 않았다.\n\n\"아니, 나는 집에 들르지 않았소.\" 미탸가 겉보기엔 완벽하게 차분한 모습으로, 그러나 바닥을 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 내 질문을 다시 한번 되풀이하도록 허락해 주시오.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마치 슬며시 본론으로 다가가듯 말을 이었다. \"당신 스스로 실토했듯이, 같은 날 다섯 시에 당신은—\"\n\n\"나는 십 루블이 아쉬워서 권총을 페르호틴에게 저당 잡혔고, 그러고는 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아가 삼천 루블을 빌리려 했으나 그녀가 주지 않았고, 운운, 그밖에 죄다 그런 식이었지요.\" 미탸가 날카롭게 가로막았다. \"그렇소, 여러분, 나는 그게 아쉬웠는데, 별안간 수천 루블이 생겨났다, 그렇지요? 당신들 말이오, 여러분, 두 사람 다 지금 '저자가 돈을 어디서 났는지 말하지 않으면 어쩌나' 하고 두려워하고 있군. 바로 그렇소. 나는 당신들한테 말하지 않을 거요, 여러분. 당신들이 옳게 짚었소. 당신들은 영영 알아내지 못할 거요.\" 미탸가 비상한 결의로 한마디 한마디를 깎아 내듯 말했다. 두 법관은 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"이해하셔야 하오, 카라마조프 씨, 우리로서는 그걸 아는 게 사활이 걸린 중대한 일이오.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 부드럽고 상냥하게 말했다.\n\n\"이해하오. 하지만 그래도 말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n검사 또한 끼어들어, 피의자가 자기에게 이롭다고 판단되면 질문에 답하기를 거부할 자유가 있다는 점 따위를 다시금 일러 주었다. 그러나 침묵으로 인해 그가 스스로에게 끼칠 수 있는 손해를 고려할 때, 특히 이토록 중대한 사건에서는—\n\n\"운운, 여러분, 운운. 됐소! 그 장광설이라면 전에도 들었소.\" 미탸가 또다시 가로막았다. \"그게 얼마나 중대한지, 이게 결정적인 대목이라는 것쯤은 나도 직접 알 수 있소. 그래도 말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"그게 우리한테 무슨 상관이오? 그건 우리 일이 아니라 당신 일이오. 당신 스스로에게 해를 끼치고 있는 거요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 신경질적으로 말했다.\n\n\"이거 보시오, 여러분, 농담은 접어 두고\"—미탸가 눈을 들어 두 사람을 똑바로 바라보았다—\"나는 맨 처음부터 우리가 바로 이 대목에서 충돌하게 되리라는 예감이 들었소. 하지만 처음 증언을 시작할 때만 해도 그건 다 아직 까마득하고 흐릿했소. 다 둥둥 떠다니고 있었고, 나는 어찌나 순진했던지 우리 사이에 서로 간의 신뢰가 존재한다는 가정에서 출발했소. 이제 보니 그런 신뢰란 애당초 말도 안 되는 것임을 나 스스로 알겠소. 어차피 우리는 이 빌어먹을 걸림돌에 부딪히게 되어 있었으니까. 그리고 이제 거기에 다다른 거요! 그건 불가능하고, 그걸로 끝이오! 하지만 당신들을 탓하지는 않겠소. 당신들은 그저 내 말 하나만으로 그걸 다 믿을 수는 없을 테니까. 물론 나도 그건 이해하오.\"\n\n그는 다시 침울한 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n\"핵심 대목에 관해 침묵하겠다는 결심은 버리지 않으면서도, 그와 동시에, 이토록 당신에게 위험천만한 고비에서 답변을 거부하게 만들 만큼 강력한 그 동기의 성격에 대해, 우리에게 약간의 실마리라도 줄 수는 없겠소?\"\n\n미탸는 슬픈 듯, 거의 꿈꾸는 듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"나는 당신들이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 마음씨가 좋은 사람이오, 여러분. 그 까닭을 말해 주고 그 실마리를 주겠소. 당신들은 그럴 자격이 없지만 말이오. 나는 그것에 관해 말하지 않을 거요, 여러분, 왜냐하면 그건 내 명예에 오점이 될 테니까. 돈을 어디서 났느냐는 물음에 대한 답은, 설령 내가 아버지를 죽이고 그 돈을 털었다 한들 그 살인과 강도질보다도 훨씬 더 큰 치욕에 나를 빠뜨릴 거요. 그래서 당신들한테 말할 수 없는 거요. 치욕이 두려워서 말이오. 어떻소, 여러분, 그걸 받아 적을 작정이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 받아 적겠소.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다.\n\n\"'치욕'에 관한 그 대목은 적지 말아야 하오. 그건 그저 내 마음이 좋아서 말해 준 거요. 굳이 말할 필요도 없었지. 말하자면 내가 그걸 당신들한테 선물로 준 셈인데, 당신들은 곧바로 그걸 덮치는군. 오, 그래, 적으시오—적고 싶은 대로 적으란 말이오.\" 그가 경멸 어린 혐오를 띠고 결론지었다. \"나는 당신들이 두렵지 않고, 당신들 앞에서 아직도 고개를 빳빳이 들 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 치욕의 성격이 어떤 것인지 우리한테 말해 줄 수는 없겠소?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 조심스레 떠보았다.\n\n검사가 어둡게 얼굴을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 다 끝났소, 애쓰지들 마시오. 손을 더럽힐 만한 가치도 없는 일이오. 나는 당신들 때문에 이미 충분히 나를 더럽혔소. 당신들은 그럴 가치가 없는 자들이오—그 누구도 마찬가지지만…… 됐소, 여러분. 나는 더 이상 말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n이 말은 너무도 단호하게 내뱉어졌다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 더 이상 우기지 않았으나, 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 눈빛에서 그가 아직 희망을 포기하지 않았다는 것을 읽어 냈다.\n\n\"그렇다면 적어도, 페르호틴 씨의 집에 들어섰을 때 당신 손에 쥐고 있던 돈이 얼마였는지—정확히 몇 루블이었는지는 말해 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그건 말할 수 없소.\"\n\n\"당신은 페르호틴 씨에게 호흘라코바 부인한테서 삼천 루블을 받았다고 말한 것으로 알고 있는데요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 말했을지도 모르지. 됐소, 여러분. 내가 얼마를 가지고 있었는지는 말하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 황송하지만 어떻게 이리로 왔는지, 그리고 도착한 뒤로 무슨 일을 했는지는 말해 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"오! 그거라면 여기 사람들한테 물어보면 될 거요. 하지만 원한다면 내가 말해 주겠소.\"\n\n그는 그렇게 하기 시작했으나, 그 이야기는 여기서 되풀이하지 않겠다. 그는 무미건조하고 짤막하게 이야기했다. 자기 사랑의 황홀경에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 그저 \"사건의 새로운 정황들\" 때문에 자결하려던 결심을 버렸다고만 말했다. 그는 동기나 세부 사항으로 파고드는 일 없이 그 이야기를 들려주었다. 그리고 이번에는 두 법관도 그를 그다지 들볶지 않았다. 여기엔 그들에게 흥미로운 본질적 대목이 없다는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"그건 다 우리가 확인하겠소. 증인 신문 때 그 문제로 다시 돌아오게 될 텐데, 물론 당신이 입회한 자리에서 진행될 거요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마무리 짓듯 말했다. \"그럼 이제 부탁하건대, 당신이 지니고 있는 모든 것, 특히 아직 몸에 지니고 있는 돈을 전부 탁자 위에 올려놓아 주시오.\"\n\n\"내 돈 말이오, 여러분? 좋소. 그게 필요하다는 건 이해하오. 사실 진작 그걸 묻지 않은 게 오히려 놀랍군. 하기야 나야 어디로 달아날 수도 없었지. 이렇게 빤히 보이는 자리에 앉아 있으니. 자, 여기 내 돈이 있소—세어 보고—가져가시오. 이게 다인 것 같소.\"\n\n그는 호주머니에서 그것을 죄다 끄집어냈다. 잔돈까지—이십 코페이카짜리 동전 두 닢을—조끼 호주머니에서 끌어냈다. 그들은 돈을 세었는데, 모두 합쳐 팔백삼십육 루블 사십 코페이카였다.\n\n\"이게 다요?\" 예심판사가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"방금 증언에서 당신은 플로트니코프 상점에서 삼백 루블을 썼다고 진술했소. 페르호틴에게 십 루블을 주었고, 마부에게 이십 루블, 여기서 이백 루블을 잃었고, 그다음에……\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그 모든 것을 셈해 나갔다. 미탸는 선뜻 그를 거들었다. 그들은 한 푼까지 죄다 떠올려 셈에 넣었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 서둘러 총액을 합산했다.\n\n\"이 팔백 루블까지 치면 당신은 처음에 천오백 루블쯤 가지고 있었던 게 틀림없겠군요?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같소.\" 미탸가 퉁명스레 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"한데 어째서 다들 훨씬 더 많았다고 주장하는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"주장하라지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신 스스로도 그렇게 주장했잖소.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 나도 그랬지요.\"\n\n\"이건 다 아직 신문하지 않은 다른 사람들의 증언과 대조해 보겠소. 당신 돈에 대해서는 염려 마시오. 잘 보관해 두었다가, 이제 막 시작되는…… 이것이…… 만약 당신에게 그것에 대한 다툼의 여지 없는 권리가 있는 것으로 드러난다면, 아니, 말하자면 입증된다면, 그게 끝날 때 당신 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 될 거요. 자, 그럼 이제……\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치가 별안간 일어서더니, 미탸에게 \"당신의 옷과 그밖에 모든 것\"에 대해 세밀하고 철저한 수색을 하는 것이 자기의 직무이자 의무라고 단호히 알렸다.\n\n\"얼마든지 하시오, 여러분. 원한다면 호주머니를 죄다 뒤집어 보이겠소.\"\n\n그러고는 실제로 호주머니를 뒤집어 까기 시작했다.\n\n\"옷도 벗어야 할 거요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 옷을 벗으라고? 윽! 빌어먹을! 이대로 나를 수색하면 안 되겠소! 그럴 수 없는 거요?\"\n\n\"그건 절대로 불가능하오, 드미트리 표도로비치. 옷을 벗으셔야 하오.\"\n\n\"좋을 대로 하시오.\" 미탸가 침울하게 따랐다. \"다만, 제발, 여기서 말고 휘장 뒤에서 했으면 하오. 누가 수색하는 거요?\"\n\n\"물론 휘장 뒤에서 하지요.\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치가 동의의 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다. 그의 자그마한 얼굴에는 유난히 엄숙한 표정이 어려 있었다.\n\n# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor Catches Mitya\n\n미탸로서는 도무지 예상치 못한, 놀랍기 짝이 없는 일이 뒤따랐다. 그는 단 일 분 전까지만 해도 누군가가 자기에게, 다름 아닌 미탸 카라마조프에게 이런 식으로 굴 수 있으리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 무엇보다 견디기 어려웠던 것은, 그 속에 어딘가 모욕적인 무언가가 깃들어 있었고, 그들 쪽에서도 무언가 \"거만하고 경멸하는\" 기색이 있었다는 점이다. 외투를 벗는 것쯤이야 아무것도 아니었으나, 그는 그 이상으로 옷을 벗으라는 요구를 받았으니, 아니 요구가 아니라 \"명령\"을 받은 것이었고, 그도 그것을 똑똑히 알아차렸다. 자존심과 경멸에서 그는 한마디 말도 없이 따랐다. 농부 몇 사람이 검사 일행을 따라와 휘장 안쪽 그 편에 남아 있었다. \"혹시 완력이 필요할 때를 대비해서겠지,\" 미탸는 생각했다, \"어쩌면 또 다른 이유 때문일 수도 있고.\"\n\n\"흠, 셔츠까지 벗어야 하오?\" 그가 날카롭게 물었으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 대답하지 않았다. 그는 검사와 함께 외투며 바지며 조끼며 모자를 살펴보느라 여념이 없었고, 두 사람 모두 그 검사(檢査)에 자못 깊은 흥미를 쏟고 있는 것이 분명했다. \"거리낌이라곤 없군,\" 미탸는 생각했다, \"가장 기본적인 예의조차 차리지 않는단 말이지.\"\n\n\"두 번째로 묻겠소—셔츠를 벗어야 하오, 말아야 하오?\" 그가 더욱 날카롭고 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"수고하실 것 없습니다. 어찌해야 할지는 저희가 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말했는데, 그 목소리는 단연 명령조였거나, 적어도 미탸에게는 그렇게 들렸다.\n\n그러는 동안 두 법조인 사이에 낮은 목소리로 의논이 오갔다. 알고 보니 외투에는, 특히 뒤쪽 왼편에 커다란 핏자국이 말라붙어 아직도 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있었다. 바지에도 핏자국이 있었다. 게다가 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 농부 증인들이 보는 앞에서 외투와 바지의 깃이며 소맷부리며 솔기란 솔기를 모조리 손가락으로 더듬어 보았으니, 무언가를—물론 돈을—찾고 있는 것이 분명했다. 그는 미탸가 옷 속에 돈을 꿰매어 넣을 만한 위인이라는 의심조차 미탸 앞에서 감추지 않았다.\n\n\"이자는 나를 장교가 아니라 도둑놈 취급하는군,\" 미탸는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. 그들은 서로 제 생각을 놀라우리만치 거리낌 없이 주고받았다. 이를테면, 역시 휘장 뒤에서 부산을 떨며 엿듣고 있던 서기가 그들이 마찬가지로 만지작거리던 모자를 두고 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 주의를 끌었다.\n\n\"필사 서기 그리졘코를 기억하시지요,\" 서기가 말했다. \"지난여름에 사무소 전체 봉급을 받아 들고는, 술에 취했을 때 그 돈을 잃어버린 척했지 않습니까. 한데 그 돈이 어디서 나왔게요? 글쎄, 바로 이런 모자의 가두리 안에서 나왔답니다. 백 루블짜리 지폐를 돌돌 말아 가두리에 꿰매 넣었더라고요.\"\n\n두 법조인은 그리졘코 사건을 똑똑히 기억해 내고는, 미탸의 모자를 한쪽에 치워 두면서, 그의 모든 옷가지는 나중에 좀 더 철저히 조사해야겠다고 결정했다.\n\n\"실례합니다만,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미탸의 셔츠 오른쪽 소맷부리가 안으로 접혀 들어가 피로 물들어 있는 것을 문득 알아채고 외쳤다. \"실례합니다, 이게 뭡니까, 피입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 미탸가 툭 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그러니까, 무슨 피냐는 말씀입니다…… 그리고 어째서 소맷부리가 안으로 접혀 있습니까?\"\n\n미탸는 그리고리를 돌보다가 소매에 피가 묻었으며, 페르호틴의 집에서 손을 씻을 때 그것을 안으로 접어 넣었던 사정을 그에게 이야기했다.\n\n\"셔츠도 벗으셔야겠습니다. 그건 물증으로서 대단히 중요하니까요.\"\n\n미탸는 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오르더니 격노했다.\n\n\"뭐요, 발가벗고 있으란 말이오?\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"심려치 마십시오. 저희가 어떻게든 마련하겠습니다. 그리고 그동안 양말도 벗으십시오.\"\n\n\"농담은 아니겠지요? 그게 정말 필요하단 말이오?\" 미탸의 두 눈이 번쩍였다.\n\n\"저희는 농담할 처지가 못 됩니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 엄하게 대답했다.\n\n\"흠, 정 그래야 한다면—\" 미탸는 중얼거리며 침대에 걸터앉아 양말을 벗었다. 그는 견딜 수 없이 어색했다. 모두가 옷을 입고 있는데 자기만 발가벗은 꼴이었고, 이상한 노릇이지만 옷을 벗고 나니 그들 앞에서 어쩐지 죄지은 사람이 된 듯한 기분이 들었으며, 자기가 그들보다 못한 인간이고 이제 그들이 자기를 경멸할 완전한 권리를 지녔다고 거의 스스로 믿어 버릴 지경이었다.\n\n\"모두가 옷을 벗고 있으면 어쩐지 부끄럽지 않은데, 혼자만 벗고 있고 모두가 쳐다보고 있으면 그건 굴욕이야,\" 그는 거듭거듭 혼잣말을 되뇌었다. \"꿈을 꾸는 것 같군, 나는 가끔 이런 굴욕스러운 처지에 빠지는 꿈을 꾸곤 했지.\" 양말을 벗는 것은 그에게 고역이었다. 양말은 몹시 더러웠고, 속옷도 마찬가지였는데, 이제 누구나 그것을 볼 수 있게 된 것이다. 게다가 더 고약한 것은, 그가 자기 발을 싫어한다는 점이었다. 그는 평생토록 두 엄지발가락이 흉측하다고 여겨 왔다. 특히 오른쪽 발의 거칠고 납작하며 비뚤어진 발톱을 끔찍이도 혐오했는데, 이제 그들 모두가 그것을 보게 될 판이었다. 참을 수 없이 부끄럽다는 느낌이 그를 단숨에, 또 일부러, 더 거칠게 만들었다. 그는 셔츠를 손수 벗어 던졌다.\n\n\"부끄럽지 않으시다면 더 살펴보고 싶은 데가 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 지금으로서는 그럴 필요가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"흠, 그럼 이렇게 발가벗고 있으란 말이오?\" 그가 사납게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네, 당분간은 어쩔 수가 없습니다…… 잠시 이쪽에 앉아 계시지요. 침대의 누비이불을 둘러도 좋습니다. 그리고 저는…… 제가 이 모든 걸 처리하겠습니다.\"\n\n모든 물건이 증인들에게 제시되었다. 수색 조서가 작성되었고, 마침내 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 밖으로 나가자, 옷가지도 그 뒤를 따라 들려 나갔다. 이폴리트 키릴로비치도 나갔다. 미탸는 농부들과 단둘이 남았는데, 그들은 잠자코 서서 그에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 미탸는 누비이불을 둘렀다. 그는 추웠다. 맨발이 비죽 삐져나왔으나, 그것을 덮을 만큼 이불을 끌어당길 수가 없었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 오래도록, \"견딜 수 없으리만치 오래\" 돌아오지 않는 듯했다. \"저자는 나를 강아지쯤으로 여기는군,\" 미탸는 이를 갈며 생각했다. \"저 썩어 빠진 검사놈도 가 버렸어, 분명 경멸하는 거지, 내 발가벗은 꼴을 보는 게 역겨운 거야!\"\n\n그래도 미탸는 자기 옷이 조사된 뒤에 도로 돌아오리라 짐작하고 있었다. 그러나 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 전혀 다른 옷을, 그 뒤를 따라 들어온 농부의 손에 들려서 가지고 돌아왔을 때, 그의 분노가 어떠했겠는가.\n\n\"여기 입으실 옷입니다.\" 그는 자기 임무의 성공에 자못 흡족해하는 기색으로 가뿐하게 말했다. \"이 뜻밖의 비상사태에 칼가노프 씨가 친절히 이것들을 마련해 주셨고, 깨끗한 셔츠까지 내주셨습니다. 다행히 그가 그것들을 모두 자기 트렁크에 가지고 있더군요. 양말과 속옷은 본인 것을 그대로 입으셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n미탸는 발끈 성을 냈다.\n\n\"남의 옷은 입지 않겠소!\" 그가 위협하듯 소리쳤다, \"내 옷을 내놓으시오!\"\n\n\"그건 불가능합니다!\"\n\n\"내 옷을 내놓으시오. 칼가노프도, 그자의 옷도 빌어먹을!\"\n\n그들이 그를 설득하기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 그래도 그들은 어떻게든 그를 진정시키는 데 성공했다. 그들은 그의 옷이 피로 물들어 있어 \"다른 물증들과 함께 포함되어야\" 하며, \"사건의 있을 법한 결말을 고려할 때…… 지금 그것을 내드릴 권리조차 없다\"는 점을 그에게 거듭 일깨웠다. 미탸도 마침내 이를 납득했다. 그는 침울한 침묵 속으로 가라앉아 서둘러 옷을 입었다. 그는 다만 옷을 입으면서, 이 옷이 자기 헌 옷보다 훨씬 좋다는 것과, 자기가 \"이런 교환으로 득을 보는\" 것이 싫다는 말만 했다. 게다가 외투는 \"우스꽝스러우리만치 꽉 끼었다. 내가 무슨 바보처럼 차려입어야 하오…… 당신네들 구경거리가 되라고?\"\n\n그들은 그가 과장하고 있을 뿐이라고, 칼가노프가 키가 조금 클 따름이라 바지만 약간 길지도 모른다고 다시 그를 다독였다. 그러나 외투는 정말로 어깨가 꽉 끼었다.\n\n\"제기랄! 단추도 겨우 채우겠군,\" 미탸가 투덜거렸다. \"내가 칼가노프 씨한테 옷을 달라고 한 적도 없고, 나를 어릿광대처럼 꾸며 놓은 게 내 짓도 아니라고, 부디 그분께 내 말을 전해 주시오.\"\n\n\"그분도 그걸 알고 있고, 미안해하고 있습니다…… 그러니까, 옷을 빌려준 게 미안하다는 게 아니라, 이 모든 사태가 안타깝다는 거지요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 우물거렸다.\n\n\"그자의 동정 따위 집어치우라지! 자, 이제 어디로 가오? 여기 계속 앉아 있어야 하오?\"\n\n그는 \"다른 방\"으로 돌아가 달라는 청을 받았다. 미탸는 분노로 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 누구와도 눈을 마주치지 않으려 애쓰며 들어갔다. 남의 옷을 입은 그는 농부들의 눈에도, 또 무슨 까닭인지 문간에 얼굴을 비쳤다가 이내 사라져 버린 트리폰 보리소비치의 눈에도 자기가 망신을 당한 듯 느껴졌다. \"이자는 내가 차려입은 꼴을 구경하러 온 거로군,\" 미탸는 생각했다. 그는 아까와 같은 의자에 앉았다. 그는 마치 제정신이 아닌 양 터무니없는 악몽 같은 기분에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"흠, 이제 어쩌겠소? 나를 매질이라도 하겠소? 당신들한테 남은 건 그것뿐일 텐데,\" 그가 이를 악물고 검사에게 말했다. 그는 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게는, 그와는 말을 섞기조차 꺼린다는 듯, 몸을 돌리려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"저자는 내 양말을 너무 빤히 들여다보더니, 그게 얼마나 더러운지 모두에게 보여 주려고 일부러 뒤집어 놓았어—저 비열한 놈이!\"\n\n\"자, 이제 증인 신문으로 넘어가야겠습니다,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마치 미탸의 물음에 답하기라도 하듯 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 검사가 무언가를 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 사려 깊게 말했다.\n\n\"저희는 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 했습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말을 이었다. \"그러나 당신 몸에서 발견된 돈의 출처를 설명하기를 그토록 단호히 거부하셨으니, 저희로서는 지금 이 순간—\"\n\n\"당신 반지에 박힌 돌이 뭐요?\" 미탸가 마치 몽상에서 깨어나기라도 한 듯 불쑥 말을 가로막았다. 그는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 오른손을 장식한 큼직한 반지 세 개 가운데 하나를 가리켰다.\n\n\"반지요?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀라서 되물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그거 말이오…… 가운뎃손가락에 낀, 자잘한 실금이 들어 있는 거, 그게 무슨 돌이오?\" 미탸가 짜증 난 어린아이처럼 집요하게 캐물었다.\n\n\"그건 연수정입니다,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"보고 싶으십니까? 빼 드리지요……\"\n\n\"아니, 빼지 마시오,\" 미탸가 사납게 외치더니, 문득 정신을 차리고 자기 자신에게 화를 냈다. \"빼지 마시오…… 그럴 필요 없소…… 제기랄!…… 여러분, 당신들은 내 마음을 더럽혀 놓았소! 내가 정말로 아버지를 죽였다면, 그걸 당신들한테 감추고, 발뺌하고, 거짓말하고, 몸을 숨길 거라고 생각하시오? 아니, 그건 드미트리 카라마조프답지 않소, 그런 짓은 그가 할 수 없는 일이오, 만약 내가 죄인이었다면, 맹세컨대 나는 당신들이 오기를, 처음에 작정했던 대로 해가 뜨기를 기다리지 않고, 동이 트기도 전에 진작 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 거요! 이제 나는 나 자신에 대해 그걸 알고 있소. 나는 이 저주받은 하룻밤에 깨달은 것만큼, 이십 년 동안에도 배우지 못했을 거요!…… 그리고 내가 정말로 아버지를 죽인 자였다면, 실수로 그리고리를 죽였을지도 모른다는 생각만으로도 밤새도록 마음이 편치 못했던 내가—그게 두려움 때문이 아니라, 오, 그저 당신들의 형벌이 두려워서가 아니라! 그 치욕 때문이었는데—이 밤에, 이 순간에, 당신들과 마주 앉아, 이렇게 이야기하고, 이렇게 몸을 움직이고, 당신들과 이 세상을 이렇게 바라볼 수 있었겠소? 그러니 당신들 같은, 아무것도 보지 못하고 아무것도 믿지 않는, 눈먼 두더지에 조롱꾼인 자들에게 내가 속을 털어놓고, 그것이 당신들의 고발에서 나를 구해 준다 할지라도, 내가 저지른 또 다른 추악한 짓, 또 다른 치욕을 당신들에게 이야기하기를 바란단 말이오! 아니, 차라리 시베리아가 낫소! 내 아버지에게 문을 열어 주고 그 문으로 들어간 자, 그자가 아버지를 죽였고, 아버지의 것을 빼앗았소. 그자가 누구냐고? 머리를 쥐어짜 봐도 누구인지 떠오르지 않소. 하지만 그게 드미트리 카라마조프가 아니라는 것만은 당신들한테 말할 수 있고, 그게 내가 당신들한테 말할 수 있는 전부이며, 그것으로 충분하오, 충분해, 나를 좀 내버려 두시오…… 나를 유형 보내든 벌하든 마음대로 하시오, 하지만 더는 나를 들볶지 마시오. 더는 아무 말도 하지 않겠소. 증인들이나 부르시오!\"\n\n미탸는 앞으로는 절대로 입을 다물기로 작정한 사람처럼 이 느닷없는 독백을 쏟아 냈다. 검사는 그동안 줄곧 그를 지켜보다가, 그가 말을 그친 뒤에야, 마치 그것이 더없이 예사로운 일이라는 듯, 더없이 냉랭하고 침착한 태도로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"오, 방금 말씀하신 그 열린 문에 대해서, 마침 이 자리에서 알려 드려도 좋겠군요, 당신께도 저희에게도 더없이 중대한, 무척 흥미로운 증언 하나를요. 당신이 부상을 입힌 노인, 그리고리가 그것을 진술했습니다. 그는 회복된 뒤 저희 질문에 답하여, 또렷하고 단호하게 진술했습니다. 즉 그가 현관 계단으로 나와 정원에서 나는 소리를 듣고, 열려 있던 쪽문을 통해 정원으로 들어가기로 마음먹었을 때, 당신이 이미 말씀하신 대로 어둠 속에서 당신 아버지를 보았던 그 열린 창문에서 달아나는 것을 알아채기에 앞서, 그가, 그리고리가 왼쪽을 흘끗 보았는데, 그 열린 창문을 알아채는 동시에, 자기 가까이에서 활짝 열린 채 서 있는 그 문을—당신이 정원에 있는 내내 닫혀 있었다고 진술한 바로 그 문을—보았다는 겁니다. 당신께 감추지 않겠습니다만, 그리고리 본인은 당신이 그 문에서 달려 나왔음이 틀림없다고 확신에 차 단언하며 증언하고 있습니다. 물론 그가 그 광경을 제 눈으로 직접 본 것은 아닙니다, 그가 당신을 처음 알아챈 것은 이미 정원 저편 멀찍이서, 당신이 울타리를 향해 달려가던 때였으니까요.\"\n\n미탸는 이 말이 절반쯤 진행될 무렵 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리!\" 그가 별안간 광란에 빠져 고래고래 외쳤다, \"뻔뻔스러운 거짓말이오. 그 문은 닫혀 있었으니 그자가 열린 걸 봤을 리가 없소. 그자가 거짓말을 하는 거요!\"\n\n\"그가 그 진술을 굳게 고수하고 있다는 점을 거듭 말씀드리는 게 제 의무라고 봅니다. 그는 흔들리지 않습니다. 그 진술을 고수하고 있어요. 저희가 그를 여러 차례 추궁해 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 제가 그를 여러 차례 추궁했지요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 열을 띠며 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"거짓이오, 거짓! 그건 나를 모함하려는 수작이거나, 미친 자의 헛것이오,\" 미탸가 여전히 소리쳤다. \"그자는 피를 흘려서, 상처 때문에 헛소리를 하는 것뿐이오. 정신이 들었을 때 헛것을 본 게 틀림없소…… 그자는 헛소리를 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그는 부상을 입고 정신이 든 뒤가 아니라, 그 전에, 곧 별채에서 정원으로 들어서자마자 열린 문을 알아챘습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 거짓이오, 거짓! 그럴 리가 없소! 그자는 앙심을 품고 나를 모함하는 거요…… 그자가 그걸 봤을 리 없소…… 나는 그 문에서 나오지 않았소,\" 미탸가 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n검사는 니콜라이 파르페노비치 쪽으로 몸을 돌리고 의미심장하게 말했다.\n\n\"그것을 들이대 보시오.\"\n\n\"이 물건을 알아보시겠습니까?\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 봉인 세 개가 아직 멀쩡히 남아 있는, 크고 두툼한 공문서용 봉투를 탁자 위에 놓았다. 봉투는 비어 있었고, 한쪽 끝이 칼로 베이듯 뜯겨 있었다. 미탸는 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 그것을 응시했다.\n\n\"그건…… 그건 우리 아버지의 봉투가 틀림없소, 삼천 루블이 들어 있던 그 봉투…… 그리고 거기 적혀 있다면, 어디 봅시다, '내 귀여운 병아리에게'라고…… 그렇소—삼천 루블!\" 그가 소리쳤다, \"보이시오, 삼천 루블, 보이시오?\"\n\n\"물론 보입니다. 하지만 저희는 그 안에서 돈을 찾지 못했습니다. 그것은 비어 있었고, 칸막이 뒤 침대 곁 바닥에 떨어져 있었습니다.\"\n\n몇 초 동안 미탸는 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 서 있었다.\n\n\"여러분, 그건 스메르댜코프요!\" 그가 별안간 목청을 다해 외쳤다. \"그자가 아버지를 죽인 거요! 그자가 아버지를 턴 거요! 노인이 그 봉투를 어디 숨겼는지 아는 자는 그자 말고 아무도 없었소. 그건 스메르댜코프요, 이제 분명해졌소!\"\n\n\"하지만 당신도 그 봉투를 알고 있었고, 그게 베개 밑에 있다는 것도 알고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"나는 결코 그걸 몰랐소. 한 번도 본 적이 없소. 그걸 들여다본 건 이번이 처음이오. 그저 스메르댜코프한테 들었을 뿐이오…… 노인이 그걸 어디 숨겨 두는지 아는 자는 그자뿐이었소, 나는 몰랐단 말이오……\" 미탸는 완전히 숨이 차 헐떡였다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 그 봉투가 돌아가신 부친의 베개 밑에 있었다고 직접 말씀하셨습니다. 베개 밑에 있었다고 특별히 진술하셨으니, 당신은 그것을 알고 있었던 게 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"저희가 그걸 기록해 두었습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 확인해 주었다.\n\n\"말도 안 되오! 터무니없는 소리요! 나는 그게 베개 밑에 있는 줄 전혀 몰랐소. 어쩌면 애초에 베개 밑에 있지도 않았을지 모르오…… 그게 베개 밑에 있다는 건 그저 우연히 한 짐작이었소. 스메르댜코프는 뭐라고 하오? 그자한테 그게 어디 있었는지 물어봤소? 스메르댜코프가 뭐라고 하느냐, 그게 핵심이오…… 그런데 나는 일부러 나서서 내게 불리한 거짓말을 한 셈이오…… 나는 아무 생각 없이 그게 베개 밑에 있었다고 당신들한테 말한 건데, 이제 당신들은—오, 사람이 어떻게 본의 아니게 엉뚱한 소리를 하는지 당신들도 알 거 아니오. 스메르댜코프 말고는 아무도 몰랐소, 오직 스메르댜코프뿐, 다른 누구도 아니오…… 그자는 그게 어디 있는지 나한테 말해 주지도 않았소! 하지만 이건 그자의 짓이오, 그자의 짓이오, 의심할 여지가 없소, 그자가 아버지를 죽인 거요, 이제 그건 대낮처럼 분명하오,\" 미탸는 갈수록 미친 듯이 외쳐 대며, 두서없이 같은 말을 되풀이했고, 점점 더 격분하고 흥분해 갔다. \"당신들은 그걸 깨닫고 당장 그자를 체포해야 하오…… 내가 달아나는 동안, 그리고리가 정신을 잃은 동안 그자가 아버지를 죽인 게 틀림없소, 이제 그건 분명하오…… 그자가 신호를 보냈고 아버지가 그자에게 문을 열어 준 거요…… 그자 말고는 아무도 그 신호를 몰랐고, 신호가 없으면 아버지는 결코 문을 열어 주지 않았을 테니까……\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 또 그 사정을 잊고 계시는군요,\" 검사가 여전히 똑같이 절제된 어조로, 그러나 어딘가 승리의 가락을 담아 말했다. \"당신이 거기 있었을 때, 당신이 정원에 있는 동안 그 문이 이미 열려 있었다면 신호를 보낼 필요가 없었다는 사정 말입니다……\"\n\n\"문, 그 문이……\" 미탸가 중얼거리며, 아무 말도 못 한 채 검사를 멍하니 바라보았다. 그는 맥없이 의자에 도로 주저앉았다. 모두가 잠잠했다.\n\n\"그래, 그 문이!…… 이건 악몽이야! 하느님이 나를 적대하시는구나!\" 그가 완전히 얼이 빠진 채 앞을 멍하니 응시하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 보십시오,\" 검사가 위엄 있게 말을 이었다. \"당신 스스로 판단해 보실 수 있습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 한편으로는 당신이 달려 나온 그 열린 문에 대한 증언이 있는데, 이는 당신도 저희도 압도하는 사실입니다. 다른 한편으로는, 불과 세 시간 전만 해도, 당신 자신의 진술대로 십 루블 때문에 권총을 저당 잡혔던 당신의 손에 그토록 느닷없이 나타난 돈, 그 출처에 대한 당신의 납득할 수 없고, 집요하며, 말하자면 완강한 침묵이 있습니다! 이 모든 사실에 비추어, 스스로 판단해 보십시오. 저희가 무엇을 믿어야 하며, 무엇에 의지할 수 있겠습니까? 그리고 저희를, 당신 마음속의 너그러운 충동을 믿을 줄 모르는 '냉랭하고, 냉소적이고, 조롱이나 일삼는 자들'이라고 탓하지 마십시오…… 저희 처지가 되어 헤아려 보십시오……\"\n\n미탸는 형언할 수 없으리만치 동요했다. 그는 얼굴이 창백해졌다.\n\n\"좋소!\" 그가 별안간 외쳤다. \"내 비밀을 말하겠소. 내가 그 돈을 어디서 손에 넣었는지 말하겠소!…… 내 치욕을 드러내겠소, 그래야 앞으로 나 자신도, 당신들도 탓하지 않게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"믿어 주십시오, 드미트리 표도로비치,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 거의 애처로우리만치 기쁨에 찬 목소리로 끼어들었다. \"이 순간 당신이 진실하고 온전한 고백을 하신다면, 그것이 나중에 당신께 더없이 유리하게 작용할 수 있고, 실로, 게다가—\"\n\n그러나 검사가 탁자 밑으로 그를 살짝 떠밀자, 그는 때맞춰 말을 삼켰다. 사실 미탸는 그의 말을 듣지 못했다.\n\n# Chapter VII. Mitya’s Great Secret. Received With Hisses\n\n\"여러분,\" 그는 여전히 같은 흥분에 사로잡힌 채 입을 열었다. \"나는 모든 것을 낱낱이 고백하고 싶소. 그 돈은 내 것이었소.\" 두 법관의 얼굴이 길어졌다. 그것은 그들이 예상하던 바와는 전혀 달랐던 것이다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 머뭇거리며 물었다. \"당신 자신의 진술에 따르면 같은 날 다섯 시에—\"\n\n\"같은 날 다섯 시고 내 진술이고 다 집어치우시오! 그건 이제 아무 상관도 없소! 그 돈은 내 것이었소, 내 것, 다시 말해 내가 훔친 것 …… 내 것은 아니지만, 내가 훔친 것이고, 천오백 루블이었으며, 그걸 줄곧 내 몸에 지니고 있었소, 줄곧 ……\"\n\n\"하지만 그 돈을 어디서 얻으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"내 목에서 끄집어냈소, 여러분, 바로 이 목에서 …… 여기 있었소, 내 목에 두르고, 헝겊에 꿰매 넣은 채로, 오랫동안 목에 걸고 다녔소, 목에 두른 지 한 달이 되었소 …… 내 수치이자 치욕으로!\"\n\n\"그러면 그것을 누구에게서 …… 가져오신 것입니까?\"\n\n\"'훔쳤느냐'는 말씀이오? 이제 솔직하게 말하시오. 그렇소, 나는 그것을 사실상 훔친 것이나 다름없다고 생각하오만, 정 그러시다면 내가 그것을 '가져왔다'고 해 둡시다. 나는 그것을 훔쳤다고 생각하오. 그리고 어젯밤에 마침내 완전히 훔치고 만 거요.\"\n\n\"어젯밤이라고요? 하지만 그것을 …… 손에 넣은 지 한 달이 되었다고 하시지 않았습니까?……\"\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 아버지에게서 훔친 건 아니오. 아버지에게서가 아니니 마음 놓으시오. 나는 아버지에게서 훔친 게 아니라 그 여자에게서 훔쳤소. 끼어들지 말고 들어 주시오. 이건 정말 말하기 어려운 거요. 보시오, 한 달 전에 나는 카테리나 이바노브나, 한때 내 약혼녀였던 그 여자에게 불려 갔소. 그 여자를 아시오?\"\n\n\"네, 물론입니다.\"\n\n\"당신들이 그 여자를 안다는 건 나도 아오. 그녀는 고귀한 여인이오, 고귀한 이들 가운데서도 가장 고귀하지. 하지만 그녀는 오래도록 나를 미워해 왔소, 오, 정말 오래도록 …… 그것도 그럴 만한 이유가 있어 미워했지, 그럴 만한 이유가!\"\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나라고요!\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀라움을 감추지 못하고 외쳤다. 검사도 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"오, 그녀의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지 마시오! 그녀를 이 일에 끌어들이다니 나는 비열한 놈이오. 그렇소, 나는 그녀가 나를 미워한다는 걸 알고 있었소 …… 오래전부터 …… 처음부터, 내 숙소에서의 그날 저녁부터 말이오 …… 하지만 됐소, 됐어. 당신들은 그 일을 알 자격조차 없소. 그런 건 전혀 필요 없는 일이오 …… 다만 이것만 말해 두겠소. 그녀가 한 달 전에 나를 불러, 모스크바에 있는 자기 언니와 또 다른 친척에게 부쳐 달라며 삼천 루블을 내게 주었던 것이오(마치 자기가 직접 부치지 못하기라도 하는 것처럼!). 그런데 나는 …… 그건 바로 내 인생의 그 운명적인 순간이었소, 내가 …… 그러니까, 내가 막 다른 여자를, 그녀를, 지금 아래층에 앉아 있는 그루셴카를 사랑하게 된 바로 그때였소. 나는 그때 그녀를 이곳 모크로예로 데려와 이틀 동안 그 빌어먹을 삼천 루블의 절반을 여기서 탕진했지만, 나머지 절반은 내 몸에 지니고 있었소. 그렇소, 나는 그 나머지 절반, 그 천오백 루블을 목에 건 작은 패물처럼 지니고 있다가 어제 그것을 풀어 써 버린 거요. 그러고 남은 것이, 팔백 루블이 지금 당신 손에 있소, 니콜라이 파르페노비치. 그게 어제 내가 가지고 있던 천오백 루블에서 거슬러 남은 돈이오.\"\n\n\"실례합니다만. 어떻게 그럴 수가 있지요? 아니, 당신이 한 달 전에 여기 왔을 때 천오백이 아니라 삼천을 쓰셨다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실인데요.\"\n\n\"누가 안다는 거요? 누가 그 돈을 세어 봤소? 내가 누구에게 세어 보라고 했소?\"\n\n\"아니, 당신 스스로 모두에게 정확히 삼천을 썼다고 말씀하셨지요.\"\n\n\"맞소, 그랬소. 나는 온 고을에 그렇게 말했고, 온 고을이 그렇게 떠들었소. 그리고 여기 모크로예에서도 모두가 삼천이라고 계산했지. 하지만 나는 삼천을 쓴 게 아니라 천오백을 쓴 거요. 그리고 나머지 천오백은 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣었소. 그렇게 된 거요, 여러분. 어제 내가 그 돈을 얻은 곳이 바로 거기요 ……\"\n\n\"이건 거의 기적이나 다름없군요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\" 검사가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"당신은 이 정황을, 그러니까 한 달 전에 천오백을 몸에 지니고 있었다는 사실을, 이전에 누구에게든 알린 적이 있습니까?\"\n\"아무에게도 말하지 않았소.\"\n\n\"그것 참 이상하군요. 정말 단 한 사람에게도 말하지 않았다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"단 한 사람에게도. 누구에게도, 아무에게도.\"\n\n\"이렇게 입을 다물고 있은 까닭이 무엇입니까? 무슨 동기로 그것을 그토록 비밀로 삼으셨습니까? 좀 더 정확히 말씀드리지요. 당신은 마침내 그 비밀을, 당신 말로는 그토록 '치욕스러운' 비밀을 우리에게 털어놓으셨소만, 실은—물론 어디까지나 상대적으로 말해서—이 행위는, 그러니까 남의 것인 삼천 루블을, 그것도 물론 잠깐 동안 가로챈 행위는, 적어도 내가 보기에는 그저 더없이 무모한 짓일 뿐 그리 치욕스러운 일은 아니오. 당신의 성격을 고려해 본다면 말이오 …… 설령 그것이 더없이 떳떳지 못한 행위였음을 인정한다 하더라도, 떳떳지 못한 것이 곧 '치욕스러운' 것은 아니오 …… 이미 적잖은 사람들이 지난 한 달 동안 당신이 카테리나 이바노브나의 삼천을 써 버렸다고 짐작하고 있었고, 나 역시 당신의 고백과는 별개로 그 이야기를 들었소 …… 가령 미하일 마카로비치도 그것을 들었으니, 사실 그건 이야깃거리라고 할 것도 없이 온 고을의 입소문이었던 거요. 게다가 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 당신이 누군가에게 이것을 직접 고백했다는, 즉 그 돈이 카테리나 이바노브나의 것이라고 털어놓았다는 정황도 있소. 그러니 지금까지, 다시 말해 이 순간까지 당신이 따로 떼어 두었다는 그 천오백을 그토록 유별나게 비밀로 삼아 왔다는 것이, 게다가 그 비밀에 거의 공포에 가까운 감정까지 결부시키고 있다는 것이 나로서는 몹시 놀라울 따름이오 …… 그런 비밀을 고백하는 일이 당신에게 그토록 큰 괴로움이 된다는 것은 도무지 믿기 어려운 일이오 …… 당신은 방금 전 그것을 고백하느니 차라리 시베리아가 낫겠다고 외치지 않았소 ……\"\n\n검사는 말을 멈췄다. 그는 화가 나 있었다. 거의 분노에 가까운 짜증을 굳이 감추지 않고, 쌓이고 쌓인 울화를 두서없고 갈피 잡히지 않게, 말을 가려 쓰지도 않은 채 죄다 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"치욕스러운 것은 그 천오백이 아니라, 그것을 나머지 삼천에서 따로 떼어 둔 그 행위요.\" 미탸가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇지요?\" 검사가 신경질적으로 웃었다. \"당신이 떳떳지 못하게, 정 그러시다면 '치욕스럽게' 가로챈 삼천 가운데 절반을 따로 떼어 두었다는 것이, 당신 생각에 대체 무엇이 그리 치욕스럽다는 거요? 당신이 그 삼천을 가져간 것이 그 돈으로 무엇을 했느냐보다 더 중요한 일이오. 그건 그렇고, 어째서 당신은 그렇게 했소—어째서 그 절반을 따로 떼어 두었으며, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 목적으로 그렇게 했소? 우리에게 설명해 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 여러분, 바로 그 목적이 핵심이오!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"내가 그것을 따로 떼어 둔 것은 내가 비열했기 때문이오, 다시 말해 내가 잇속을 따졌기 때문이며, 그런 경우에 잇속을 따진다는 것 자체가 비열한 일이오 …… 그리고 그 비열함이 꼬박 한 달 동안이나 계속되었던 거요.\"\n\n\"도무지 이해할 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"당신들이 이상하구려. 하지만 좀 더 분명하게 설명해 드리겠소. 어쩌면 정말로 이해하기 어려운 일일지도 모르지. 자, 내 말을 잘 들어 보시오. 내가 명예를 걸고 맡은 삼천을 가로채어 흥청망청 써 버렸다 칩시다, 죄다 써 버렸다 칩시다. 그러고 이튿날 아침 그녀에게 가서 이렇게 말하는 거요. '카탸, 내가 잘못했소, 당신의 삼천을 탕진해 버렸소.' 자, 이게 옳은 일이오? 아니, 옳지 않소—이건 부정직하고 비겁한 짓이오, 나는 짐승이오, 짐승 정도밖에 자제력이 없는 놈이지, 그렇지 않소? 하지만 그래도 나는 도둑은 아니지 않소? 적어도 완전한 도둑은 아니라는 걸 당신들도 인정할 거요! 나는 탕진했지만 훔치지는 않았소. 자, 이번엔 좀 더 나은 두 번째 경우요. 잘 따라오시오, 안 그러면 또 헷갈릴지도 모르니—머리가 빙빙 도는구려—그래서, 두 번째 경우는 이렇소. 나는 삼천 가운데 천오백만, 그러니까 절반만 여기서 쓰는 거요. 이튿날 나는 그 절반을 그녀에게 가져가 이렇게 말하지. '카탸, 내게서 이 천오백을 받아 두시오, 나는 비열한 짐승이고 믿을 수 없는 악당이오, 절반을 탕진해 버렸고 이것마저 탕진할 테니, 부디 나를 유혹에서 지켜 주시오!' 자, 이 경우는 어떻소? 나는 짐승이요 악당이요, 당신들 좋을 대로 무엇이든 되겠지만, 도둑은 아니오, 완전한 도둑은 아니지, 안 그랬다면 나는 남은 것을 돌려주지 않고 그것마저 가지고 있었을 테니 말이오. 그녀는 곧바로 알아챘을 거요, 내가 절반을 돌려준 이상 쓴 것도 갚을 것이며, 그 노력을 결코 포기하지 않을 것이고, 일을 해서라도 돈을 마련해 갚으리라는 것을. 그러니 그 경우에 나는 악당일지언정 도둑은 아닌 거요, 당신들이 뭐라고 하든, 도둑은 아니란 말이오!\"\n\n\"어느 정도 구분이 있다는 건 인정하겠소.\" 검사가 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 당신이 거기서 그토록 본질적인 차이를 본다는 게 이상하군요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 나는 본질적인 차이를 보오! 누구든 악당이 될 수 있고, 어쩌면 모든 사람이 악당일지도 모르지만, 누구나 다 도둑이 될 수 있는 건 아니오, 그건 더없이 악랄한 자라야 될 수 있는 거요. 오, 물론 나는 이런 미묘한 구분을 제대로 할 줄은 모르오 …… 하지만 도둑은 악당보다 더 비천하다는 것, 그게 내 확신이오. 들어 보시오, 나는 그 돈을 꼬박 한 달이나 몸에 지니고 다녔소, 당장 내일이라도 돌려주기로 마음을 정할 수도 있었고, 그러면 나는 더 이상 악당이 아니게 되는 거요, 하지만 보다시피 나는 좀처럼 결심을 못 했소, 날마다 결심을 하고, 날마다 스스로를 다그쳐 그렇게 하려 했건만, 그래도 꼬박 한 달 동안 차마 그것을 실행에 옮기지 못했단 말이오. 당신들 생각에 이게 옳은 일이오, 이게 옳소?\"\n\n\"분명 옳지 않소, 그건 나도 충분히 이해할 수 있고, 그 점은 따지지 않겠소.\" 검사가 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"그러니 이런 미묘한 구분이나 차이에 관한 이야기는 모두 그만두기로 하고, 부디 본론으로 돌아갑시다. 그런데 그 본론이란, 당신이 아직도 우리에게 말하지 않은 것이오, 그러니까 우리가 거듭 물었는데도, 첫째로 어째서 그 돈을 절반으로 나누어 한쪽은 탕진하고 다른 한쪽은 감추었는가 하는 것이오. 정확히 무슨 목적으로 그것을 감추었으며, 그 천오백으로 무엇을 하려 했소? 나는 이 질문을 고집하겠소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 물론이오!\" 미탸가 이마를 탁 치며 외쳤다. \"용서하시오, 내가 당신들을 괴롭히면서 정작 핵심은 설명하지 않고 있구려, 그것만 알면 당신들도 당장 이해할 텐데, 바로 그 동기야말로 치욕스러운 거니까! 보시오, 이건 다 그 늙은이, 죽은 내 아버지와 관련된 일이오. 그자는 늘 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 치근덕거렸고, 나는 질투에 사로잡혔소. 그때 나는 그녀가 나와 그자 사이에서 망설이고 있다고 생각했지. 그래서 날마다 이런 생각이 들었소, 만일 그녀가 별안간 마음을 정한다면, 만일 그녀가 나를 괴롭히는 일을 그만두고 갑자기 내게 이렇게 말한다면, '내가 사랑하는 건 그가 아니라 당신이에요, 나를 세상 저 끝으로 데려가 줘요'라고. 그런데 내겐 사십 코페이카밖에 없었소. 그러니 어떻게 그녀를 데려갈 수 있단 말이오, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠소? 그야말로 나는 끝장날 판이었지. 보시오, 그때 나는 그녀를 알지 못했고, 그녀를 이해하지 못했소, 나는 그녀가 돈을 바란다고, 내 가난을 용서하지 않으리라고 생각했던 거요. 그래서 나는 악마처럼 그 삼천의 절반을 세어 내어 꿰매 넣었소, 그걸 노리고 계산해서, 술에 취하기 전에 미리 꿰매 두고, 다 꿰매 놓은 다음에야 나머지를 가지고 취하러 갔던 거요. 그렇소, 그건 비열한 짓이었소. 이제 이해가 가시오?\"\n\n두 법관이 소리 내어 웃었다.\n\n\"그걸 죄다 탕진하지 않았다니, 나라면 오히려 분별 있고 도덕적인 처사라고 했을 거요.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 빙긋이 웃었다. \"어쨌든 그게 무슨 대단한 일이라고요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 내가 그걸 훔쳤다는 거요, 그게 바로 그거란 말이오! 오, 맙소사, 당신들이 이해를 못 하니 나는 소름이 끼치는구려! 그 천오백을 목에 꿰매어 두르고 있던 날마다, 매일 매시간 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌었소. '너는 도둑이다! 너는 도둑이다!' 그렇소, 그래서 이 한 달 내내 내가 그토록 사나웠던 거요, 그래서 술집에서 싸움을 벌였고, 그래서 아버지에게 덤벼들었던 거요, 그건 다 내가 도둑이라고 느꼈기 때문이오. 나는 차마 결심을 못 했고, 내 동생 알료샤에게조차 그 천오백 이야기를 감히 꺼내지 못했소. 내가 그토록 악당 같고 소매치기 같은 놈이라고 느꼈던 거요. 하지만 들어 보시오, 그것을 지니고 다니는 동안 나는 매시간 동시에 이렇게도 되뇌었소. '아니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 너는 아직 도둑이 아닐 수도 있다'라고. 왜냐고? 이튿날 당장 가서 그 천오백을 카탸에게 돌려줄 수도 있었으니까. 그런데 바로 어제, 페냐의 집에서 페르호틴에게 가는 길에 나는 그 부적을 목에서 떼어 내기로 마음을 정한 거요. 그 순간까지도 나는 차마 그렇게 하지 못하고 있었던 거지. 그리고 그것을 떼어 낸 바로 그 순간에야 나는 완전한 도둑이, 남은 평생토록 도둑이요 부정직한 인간이 되고 만 거요. 왜냐고? 그것으로 나는 카탸에게 찾아가 '나는 악당이지만 도둑은 아니오!'라고 말할 꿈마저 무너뜨려 버렸으니까. 이제 이해가 가시오? 이해가 가시오?\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 어제 그렇게 하기로 결심하셨습니까?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"왜냐고? 그건 물어볼 것도 없는 일이오. 오늘 새벽 다섯 시에, 여기서, 동틀 무렵에 나는 스스로 죽기로 작정했기 때문이오. 도둑으로 죽건 명예로운 사람으로 죽건 아무 차이 없다고 생각했소. 하지만 그게 그렇지가 않더군, 차이가 있다는 게 드러났소. 믿어 주시오, 여러분, 이 밤 내내 나를 가장 괴롭힌 것은 내가 그 늙은 하인을 죽였다는 생각도, 또 내 사랑이 막 보답을 받고 천국이 다시 내게 열리려는 바로 그때 시베리아로 끌려갈 위기에 처했다는 생각도 아니었소. 오, 그것도 나를 괴롭히긴 했지만 같은 식은 아니었소. 마침내 그 빌어먹을 돈을 내 가슴에서 떼어 내어 써 버리고 완전한 도둑이 되고 말았다는 그 저주받을 자각만큼은 아니었던 거요! 오, 여러분, 다시 한번 피눈물 흘리는 심정으로 말하겠소, 나는 이 밤에 많은 것을 깨달았소. 악당으로 살아가는 것이 불가능할 뿐 아니라 악당으로 죽는 것마저 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았소 …… 아니, 여러분, 사람은 명예롭게 죽어야 하는 거요 ……\"\n\n미탸는 창백했다. 그는 극도로 흥분해 있었음에도 그 얼굴에는 초췌하고 기진맥진한 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이제 당신을 이해하기 시작했소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 검사가 부드럽고 거의 연민에 가까운 어조로 천천히 말했다. \"하지만 이 모든 것이, 이런 말을 해서 미안하오만, 내 생각에는 다 신경 탓이오 …… 당신의 과민해진 신경 말이오, 바로 그게 문제요. 그건 그렇고, 가령 어째서 당신은 그 천오백을 가지고 가서 그것을 당신에게 맡긴 부인에게 돌려줌으로써, 거의 한 달 동안이나 자신을 그런 고통에서 구해 내지 않았던 거요? 어째서 당신은 그녀에게 사정을 설명할 수 없었던 거요, 그리고 당신이 그토록 끔찍하다고 묘사하는 당신의 처지를 생각하면, 누구라도 자연스레 떠올렸을 그 방법에 의지할 수 있지 않았소, 즉 그녀에게 자신의 과오를 정직하게 고백한 다음, 어째서 당신은 자신의 비용에 필요한 그 액수를 빌려 달라고 그녀에게 부탁할 수 없었던 거요, 그토록 너그러운 마음을 지닌 그녀라면 당신의 곤경을 두고 분명 거절하지 않았을 텐데 말이오, 더구나 어떤 담보라도 있었다면, 아니면 당신이 상인 삼소노프와 호흘라코바 부인에게 제안했던 그 담보라도 있었다면 더욱 그러했을 거요. 당신은 아직도 그 담보를 값어치 있는 것으로 여기고 있겠지요?\"\n\n미탸의 얼굴이 별안간 새빨개졌다.\n\n\"설마 나를 그토록 철저한 악당으로 여기는 건 아니겠지요? 진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠지요?\" 그는 분개하여, 검사의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며, 제 귀를 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"분명히 말하지만 나는 진심이오 …… 어째서 내가 진심이 아니라고 생각하는 거요?\" 이번엔 검사가 놀랄 차례였다.\n\n\"오, 그게 얼마나 비열한 짓이 되었겠소! 여러분, 알겠소, 당신들은 나를 괴롭히고 있소! 다 털어놓겠소, 좋소. 내 지옥 같은 사악함을 죄다 고백하겠소, 다만 당신들을 부끄럽게 하기 위해서요, 그러면 당신들도 인간의 온갖 정념이 뒤엉켰을 때 얼마나 깊은 치욕의 나락으로 가라앉을 수 있는지를 보고 스스로 놀라게 될 거요. 알아 두시오, 검사 양반, 방금 당신이 말한 그 방법, 그건 이미 나 자신도 품고 있던 것이오! 그렇소, 여러분, 나 역시 이번 한 달 내내 그 생각을 마음에 품고 있었고, 그래서 카탸에게 찾아가기로 거의 마음을 정할 뻔했소—나는 그만큼 비열한 놈이었던 거요. 하지만 그녀에게 찾아가, 내 배신을 그녀에게 털어놓고, 바로 그 배신을 위해, 그 배신을 실행에 옮기기 위해, 그 배신에 드는 비용을 위해, 그녀에게, 카탸에게 돈을 구걸하고(구걸이오, 듣고 있소, 구걸 말이오), 그러고는 그녀에게서 곧장 나와 그 여자를, 그녀를 미워하고 모욕한 연적과 함께 달아난다—그걸 한번 생각해 보시오! 당신 제정신이 아니구려, 검사 양반!\"\n\n\"제정신이 아닌 건 아니오만, 내가 깊이 생각하지 않고 성급하게 말한 건 사실이오 …… 그 여자의 질투에 관해서 말이오 …… 만일 당신 말대로 이 경우에 질투라는 게 있을 수 있다면 …… 그렇소, 어쩌면 그런 것이 좀 있을지도 모르겠군요.\" 검사가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그건 너무도 파렴치한 짓이 되었을 거요!\" 미탸가 거칠게 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳤다. \"그건 무엇보다도 더없이 추악한 짓이 되었을 거란 말이오! 그렇소, 알겠소, 그녀라면 내게 그 돈을 줬을지도 모르오, 그렇소, 정말 주었을 거요, 틀림없이 줬을 거요, 내게 복수하기 위해서, 그녀는 자신의 복수심을 채우려고, 나를 향한 경멸을 드러내려고 그 돈을 주었을 거요, 그녀 역시 지옥 같은 천성을 지녔고 분노가 대단한 여자니까. 나도 그 돈을 받았을 거요, 오, 틀림없이 받았겠지, 받고서, 그러고 나면 남은 평생토록 …… 오, 맙소사! 용서하시오, 여러분, 내가 이렇게 소리를 질러 대는 건 바로 얼마 전에, 겨우 그저께, 랴가비와 그 온갖 실랑이를 벌이던 그날 밤에, 그리고 그다음 어제, 어제 하루 종일, 그 일이 일어나기 전까지 그 생각을 마음에 품고 있었기 때문이오 ……\"\n\n\"그 일이라니, 무슨 일이 일어나기 전 말입니까?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 궁금한 듯 끼어들었으나, 미탸는 그것을 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"나는 당신들에게 끔찍한 고백을 했소.\" 미탸가 침울하게 끝맺었다. \"당신들은 그것을 마땅히 알아주어야 하고, 더 나아가 그것을 존중해 주어야 하오, 만일 그러지 않는다면, 만일 그것이 당신들의 영혼을 조금도 건드리지 못한다면, 그건 당신들이 나를 도무지 존중하지 않는다는 뜻이오, 분명히 말해 두겠소, 그렇다면 나는 당신들 같은 사람들에게 그것을 고백했다는 부끄러움으로 죽고 말 거요! 오, 나는 권총으로 내 머리를 쏘아 버릴 거요! 그렇소, 알겠소, 당신들이 나를 믿지 않는다는 걸 이미 다 알겠소. 뭐요, 당신들 그것마저 받아 적으려는 거요?\" 그가 경악하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 방금 하신 말씀 말입니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀란 듯 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러니까 당신이 마지막 순간까지도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 찾아가 그 액수를 구걸할 생각을 하고 있었다는 말씀 말입니다 …… 분명히 말씀드리지만, 그건 우리에게 매우 중요한 증거입니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그러니까 이 사건 전체에 …… 그리고 특히 당신에게, 당신에게 특히 중요한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"제발 자비를 베푸시오, 여러분!\" 미탸가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들었다. \"어쨌거나 그것만은 받아 적지 마시오, 부끄러운 줄 좀 아시오. 여기 내가 당신들 앞에서 내 심장을 두 쪽으로 찢어 보였더니, 당신들은 그 틈을 타 두 쪽 다 그 상처를 후벼 파고 있구려 …… 오, 맙소사!\"\n\n절망에 빠져 그는 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n\"그리 괴로워하지 마시오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 검사가 말했다. \"받아 적은 것은 나중에 모두 당신에게 읽어 드릴 거요, 그리고 당신이 동의하지 않는 부분은 당신 뜻대로 고쳐 드리겠소. 다만 지금 한 가지 사소한 질문을 두 번째로 드리겠소. 당신이 꿰매 넣었다는 그 돈에 관해, 정말 아무도, 단 한 사람도 당신에게서 들은 적이 없다는 거요? 그건, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 도무지 믿기 어려운 일이라서 말이오.\"\n\n\"아무도, 아무도 없소, 아까도 그렇게 말하지 않았소, 아니면 당신들이 아무것도 못 알아들은 거요! 나를 좀 내버려 두시오!\"\n\n\"좋소, 이 문제는 언젠가 밝혀질 게 분명하고 그럴 시간도 충분하오, 하지만 그동안 한번 생각해 보시오. 당신 자신이 여기서 쓴 삼천에 관해 떠들고 다녔으며, 거의 어디서나 그것을 소리쳐 외치다시피 했다는 증인이 우리에게 어쩌면 열두어 명은 될 거요. 천오백이 아니라 삼천 말이오. 그리고 지금도, 어제 그 돈을 손에 넣었을 때 당신은 많은 사람들에게 삼천을 가지고 왔다는 인상을 주었소.\"\n\n\"열두어 명이 아니라 수백 명, 이백 명의 증인이 있을 거요, 이백 명이 들었고, 수천 명이 들었소!\" 미탸가 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 보시오, 모두가 그것을 증언하는 거요. 그리고 모두라는 말에는 무언가 의미가 있는 법이오.\"\n\n\"아무 의미도 없소. 내가 헛소리를 지껄였더니 모두가 그걸 따라 떠들기 시작한 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신 말마따나 '헛소리를 지껄일' 무슨 필요가 있었소?\"\n\n\"악마나 알겠지. 아마 허세였을 거요 …… 그렇게 많은 돈을 탕진한 데 대한 허세 …… 어쩌면 내가 꿰매 넣어 둔 그 돈을 잊어 보려고 그랬는지도 모르오 …… 그렇소, 바로 그래서였소 …… 빌어먹을 …… 도대체 그 질문을 몇 번이나 더 할 셈이오? 자, 나는 거짓말을 했고, 그걸로 끝난 거요, 일단 입 밖에 내고 나니 굳이 바로잡고 싶지 않았던 거요. 사람이 때때로 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하겠소?\"\n\n\"사람이 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하는가, 그건 참 판단하기 어려운 일이오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 검사가 의미심장하게 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 한 가지 묻겠소, 당신 목에 두르고 있었다는 그 '부적'이라는 것은 큰 물건이었소?\"\n\n\"아니, 크지 않았소.\"\n\n\"예를 들어 어느 정도였소?\"\n\n\"백 루블짜리 지폐를 반으로 접으면 딱 그만한 크기일 거요.\"\n\n\"그 남은 것을 우리에게 보여 주는 게 좋겠소. 어딘가에 분명 가지고 있을 테니.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 무슨 헛소리요! 그게 어디 있는지 나도 모르오.\"\n\n\"하지만 실례지만, 그것을 언제 어디서 목에서 떼어 냈소? 당신 자신의 진술에 따르면 집에는 가지 않았는데 말이오.\"\n\n\"페냐의 집에서 페르호틴에게 가던 길에, 도중에 그것을 목에서 떼어 내어 돈을 꺼냈소.\"\n\n\"어둠 속에서 말이오?\"\n\n\"불빛이 무슨 필요가 있겠소? 손가락으로 일 분 만에 해치웠소.\"\n\n\"가위도 없이, 길거리에서 말이오?\"\n\n\"아마 장터였던 것 같소. 가위는 무슨. 낡은 헝겊이었소. 순식간에 찢어졌지.\"\n\n\"그걸 그 뒤에 어디다 두었소?\"\n\n\"거기 떨어뜨렸소.\"\n\n\"정확히 어디였소?\"\n\n\"장터였소, 장터! 어디쯤이었는지는 악마나 알겠지. 그걸 알아서 뭣 하려는 거요?\"\n\n\"그게 대단히 중요한 일이오, 드미트리 표도로비치. 그건 당신에게 유리한 물증이 될 수도 있단 말이오. 어째서 그걸 이해하지 못하는 거요? 한 달 전에 그걸 꿰매는 일을 누가 도와주었소?\"\n\n\"아무도 도와주지 않았소. 내가 직접 했소.\"\n\n\"바느질을 할 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"군인이라면 바느질할 줄 알아야 하는 법이오. 그런 걸 하는 데 무슨 대단한 솜씨가 필요한 것도 아니고.\"\n\n\"그 재료는, 그러니까 돈을 꿰매 넣은 그 헝겊은 어디서 났소?\"\n\n\"지금 나를 비웃는 거요?\"\n\n\"천만에. 그리고 우리는 지금 웃을 기분이 아니오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\"\n\n\"그 헝겊을 어디서 났는지 모르겠소—어딘가에서 났겠지.\"\n\n\"설마 그걸 잊어버렸을 리가 없을 텐데요?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 기억이 안 나오. 어쩌면 내 속옷에서 한 조각 찢어 냈을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그것 참 흥미롭군요. 우리가 내일 당신 숙소에서 당신이 헝겊을 찢어 낸 그 셔츠인지 뭔지를 찾아낼 수도 있겠소. 그게 어떤 헝겊이었소, 천이었소 아니면 아마포였소?\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이었는지는 하느님만 아실 일이오. 잠깐 …… 아무 데서도 찢어 낸 게 아닌 것 같소. 옥양목 조각이었소 …… 내 하숙집 여주인의 보닛에 꿰매 넣었던 것 같소.\"\n\n\"하숙집 여주인의 보닛에 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그 여자한테서 가져왔소.\"\n\n\"어떻게 손에 넣었소?\"\n\n\"보시오, 언젠가 보닛 하나를 헝겊으로 쓰려고, 아마 펜을 닦으려고 가져온 일이 있소. 보잘것없는 헝겊이라 묻지도 않고 가져왔지. 그걸 찢어서 지폐를 그 안에 꿰매 넣었소. 바로 그 헝겊에 꿰맨 것 같소. 천 번은 빨았을 낡은 옥양목 조각이었소.\"\n\n\"이제 그것만은 확실히 기억하시오?\"\n\n\"확실한지는 모르겠소. 보닛이었던 것 같긴 한데. 하지만 젠장, 그게 무슨 상관이오?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신 하숙집 여주인이 그 물건이 없어진 걸 기억하고 있겠군요?\"\n\n\"아니, 기억 못 할 거요, 없어진 줄도 몰랐을 테니. 낡은 헝겊이었다지 않소, 한 푼어치도 안 되는 낡은 헝겊 말이오.\"\n\n\"그러면 바늘과 실은 어디서 났소?\"\n\n\"이제 그만하겠소. 더는 말하지 않겠소. 이젠 됐소!\" 미탸가 마침내 화를 터뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 장터의 어디쯤에 그 조각들을 던졌는지 그렇게 깡그리 잊어버렸다는 게 이상하군요.\"\n\n\"내일 장터를 쓸어 보라고 명령을 내리시오, 그러면 어쩌면 찾아낼지도 모르지.\" 미탸가 비웃으며 말했다. \"됐소, 여러분, 됐소!\" 그가 기진맥진한 목소리로 단언했다. \"당신들이 나를 믿지 않는다는 걸 알겠소! 단 한순간도 믿지 않는다는 걸! 그건 내 잘못이지 당신들 잘못이 아니오. 내가 그렇게 선뜻 입을 열지 말았어야 했소. 어째서, 어째서 나는 당신들에게 내 비밀을 고백해 스스로를 욕보였단 말인가? 당신들에게야 이건 한낱 우스갯거리지. 당신들 눈빛을 보면 알겠소. 검사 양반, 당신이 나를 거기에 끌어들인 거요? 할 수 있다면 어디 승리의 찬가나 불러 보시오 …… 빌어먹을, 이 고문자들 같으니!\"\n\n그는 고개를 숙이고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다. 두 법관은 잠자코 있었다. 잠시 후 그는 고개를 들어 거의 멍한 눈길로 그들을 바라보았다. 그의 얼굴은 이제 완전한, 가망 없는 절망을 드러내고 있었고, 그는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 거의 의식하지 못하는 듯 말없이 무기력하게 앉아 있었다. 그러는 동안 그들은 하던 일을 마저 끝내야만 했다. 곧바로 증인 신문을 시작해야 했던 것이다. 어느덧 아침 여덟 시였다. 등불은 진작에 꺼져 있었다. 신문이 진행되는 내내 줄곧 방을 들락거리던 미하일 마카로비치와 칼가노프는 이제 둘 다 다시 밖으로 나가고 없었다. 두 법관도 몹시 지쳐 보였다. 음울한 아침이었으니, 온 하늘이 잔뜩 흐려 있었고 비가 양동이로 퍼붓듯 쏟아져 내렸다. 미탸는 멍하니 창밖을 내다보았다.\n\n\"창밖을 좀 내다봐도 되겠소?\" 그가 별안간 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게 물었다.\n\n\"오, 얼마든지 보시오.\" 상대가 대답했다.\n\n미탸는 일어나 창가로 다가갔다 …… 비가 자그마한 녹빛 유리창을 후려치고 있었다. 그는 바로 집 아래의 진창길과, 그 너머 빗속과 안개 속으로 늘어선 가난하고 거뭇하고 음울한 오두막 한 줄을 볼 수 있었는데, 빗속에서 그것들은 한층 더 거뭇하고 가난해 보였다. 미탸는 \"황금빛 머리칼의 포이보스\"를 떠올렸고, 그 첫 햇살이 비칠 때 권총으로 자신을 쏘려 했던 것을 떠올렸다. \"어쩌면 이런 아침이 오히려 더 나을지도 모르지.\" 그는 미소를 띤 채 생각하다가, 별안간 손을 아래로 내저으며 자신의 \"고문자들\"을 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내가 끝장났다는 건 알겠소! 하지만 그녀는? 제발 부탁이오, 그녀에 관해 말해 주시오. 설마 그녀까지 나와 함께 파멸해야 하는 건 아니겠지요? 그녀는 죄가 없소, 알겠소, 어젯밤 '다 내 잘못이에요!'라고 외쳤을 때 그녀는 제정신이 아니었던 거요. 그녀는 아무 짓도 하지 않았소, 아무 짓도! 나는 당신들과 함께 앉아 있는 내내 밤새 그녀를 생각하며 가슴 아파했소 …… 이제 그녀를 어떻게 하려는지 내게 말해 줄 수는, 말해 줄 마음은 없는 거요?\"\n\n\"그 점이라면 마음을 완전히 놓으셔도 좋소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 검사가 눈에 띄게 선뜻 즉각 대답했다. \"지금까지는 당신이 그토록 마음을 쓰는 그 부인에게 간섭할 만한 근거가 우리에게 전혀 없소. 사건이 앞으로 전개되어 가더라도 그러하기를 바라오 …… 오히려 우리는 그 문제에 관해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다할 거요. 부디 완전히 마음을 놓으시오.\"\n\n\"여러분, 고맙소. 나는 당신들이 무슨 일이 있어도 정직하고 올곧은 사람들이라는 걸 알고 있었소. 당신들이 내 가슴에서 무거운 짐을 덜어 주었구려 …… 자, 이제 우리는 무엇을 해야 하오? 나는 준비됐소.\"\n\n\"자, 서둘러야겠소. 지체 없이 증인 신문으로 넘어가야 하오. 그건 반드시 당신이 입회한 자리에서 이루어져야 하니, 그러므로—\"\n\n\"우선 차라도 좀 마시는 게 어떻겠습니까?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다. \"우리도 그만한 자격은 있는 것 같은데요!\"\n\n그들은 아래층에 차가 준비되어 있다면(미하일 마카로비치가 틀림없이 차를 가지러 내려갔을 터였다) 한 잔씩 마시고 나서 \"계속, 계속\" 진행하기로, 정작 제대로 된 아침 식사는 좀 더 적당한 기회로 미루기로 정했다. 차는 정말로 아래층에 준비되어 있었고, 이내 위로 올라왔다. 미탸는 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 정중하게 권한 잔을 처음엔 사양했으나, 나중엔 직접 청해서 게걸스럽게 들이켰다. 그는 놀라우리만치 지쳐 보였다. 그 헤라클레스 같은 체력으로 보아 하룻밤의 술잔치쯤은, 그것이 아무리 격렬한 감정을 동반했다 하더라도, 그에게 별다른 영향을 미치지 못했으리라 짐작할 수도 있었다. 그러나 그는 머리조차 가누기 어려울 지경이었고, 때때로 주위의 온갖 사물이 눈앞에서 출렁이며 춤추는 것만 같았다. \"조금만 더 있으면 나는 헛소리를 지껄이기 시작할 거야.\" 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Evidence Of The Witnesses. The Babe\n\n증인 신문이 시작되었다. 그러나 우리는 앞서처럼 그렇게 시시콜콜하게 이야기를 이어 가지는 않겠다. 그러므로 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 호출된 증인 한 사람 한 사람에게 진실과 양심에 따라 증언해야 하며, 나중에 선서한 후 자신의 증언을 되풀이해야 한다고 일러둔 일이라든가, 증인마다 자기 증언의 조서에 서명하도록 요구받은 일 따위는 더 이상 다루지 않겠다. 다만 신문에서 주로 집요하게 파고든 것이 삼천 루블에 관한 문제였다는 점만 짚어 두겠다. 즉 한 달 전 처음 이곳 모크로예에서 미탸가 쓴 돈이 삼천 루블이었는가, 아니면 천오백 루블이었는가? 또한 어제 그가 쓴 돈은 삼천이었는가, 천오백이었는가? 아아, 모든 이가 내놓은 증언은 죄다 미탸에게 불리하게 드러나고 말았다. 그에게 유리한 증언은 단 하나도 없었으며, 어떤 증인들은 그, 미탸의 진술과 어긋나는, 거의 결정적인 새 사실들까지 들고 나왔다.\n\n처음 신문을 받은 증인은 트리폰 보리소비치였다. 그는 법관들 앞에 서면서도 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다. 오히려 그는 피고에 대해 준엄하고 가혹한 분노의 기색을 띠었으며, 그 때문에 그에게는 진실됨과 인격적 위엄의 풍모가 감돌았다. 그는 말수가 적었고 조심스러웠으며, 질문을 기다렸다가 정확하고 신중하게 대답했다. 그는 한 달 전에 쓰인 돈이 삼천 루블보다 적었을 리 없다고 단호하고 망설임 없이 증언했으며, 이 근방의 농부들이 모두 드미트리 표도로비치 자신의 입에서 삼천이라는 액수가 언급되는 것을 들었다고 증언할 것이라고 했다. \"집시 계집들한테만도 어찌나 돈을 뿌려 댔는지! 그것들한테만 천 루블쯤은 날렸을 겝니다.\"\n\n\"내가 그것들한테 오백 루블이라도 줬을 성싶지 않은데.\" 미탸는 이에 대해 침울하게 한마디 했다. \"그때 돈을 세어 두지 않은 게 안타깝긴 하군. 하지만 난 취해 있었으니까…….\"\n\n미탸는 옆으로 비스듬히 앉아 커튼을 등지고 있었다. 그는 침울하게, 우울하고 진이 빠진 기색으로 귀를 기울이고 있었는데, 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯했다.\n\n\"오, 마음대로들 떠들라지. 이제 와선 아무래도 상관없으니까.\"\n\n\"천 루블도 넘게 그것들한테 들어갔습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\" 트리폰 보리소비치가 단호하게 받아쳤다. \"되는대로 뿌려 대셨고, 그것들이 그걸 주워 갔지요. 천하의 망나니에 도둑놈들, 말 도둑들이었는데, 이젠 여기서 다 쫓겨났습죠. 안 그랬으면 그것들이 직접 당신한테서 얼마를 받았는지 증언했을 텐데요. 나도 그 돈을 당신 손에서 직접 봤습니다요. 세어 보진 못했지요, 당신이 못 세게 했으니까, 그건 사실입니다만, 그래도 어림잡아 보아도 천오백보다는 훨씬 많았다고 하겠습니다…… 천오백이라니, 원! 우리도 돈을 본 적이 있는 사람들입니다. 액수쯤은 가늠할 줄 압죠…….\"\n\n어제 쓴 돈에 관해서는, 드미트리 표도로비치가 도착하자마자 삼천 루블을 가져왔다고 자기에게 말했다고 주장했다.\n\n\"이봐요, 정말 그랬소, 트리폰 보리소비치?\" 미탸가 대꾸했다. \"내가 정말로 삼천을 가져왔다고 그렇게 딱 잘라 말했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀하셨습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 안드레이 앞에서 그러셨지요. 안드레이가 아직 여기 있습니다. 그를 불러오십쇼. 그리고 홀에서 합창단에게 한턱내실 때, 여섯째 천을 여기다 두고 가겠다고 대놓고 소리치셨습니다요. 즉 전에 쓰신 것까지 합쳐서 그렇단 얘기로 알아들어야겠지요. 스테판이랑 세묜이 그걸 들었고, 표트르 포미치 칼가노프도 그때 당신 곁에 서 있었습니다요. 아마 그분도 기억하실 겝니다…….\"\n\n이 \"여섯째\" 천 루블에 관한 증언은 두 법관에게 엄청난 인상을 남겼다. 그들은 이 새로운 계산법에 흡족해했다. 삼에 삼을 더하면 육, 그때의 삼천에 지금의 삼천을 더하면 육천, 그것은 명백한 일이었다.\n\n그들은 트리폰 보리소비치가 지목한 농부들, 즉 스테판과 세묜, 마부 안드레이, 그리고 칼가노프를 모두 신문했다. 농부들과 마부는 망설임 없이 트리폰 보리소비치의 증언을 확인해 주었다. 그들은 안드레이가 길에서 미탸와 나눈 대화에 관한 진술을 특별히 신경 써서 받아 적었다. \"'안드레이, 난 말이다,' 하고 그분이 묻습디다, '나 드미트리 표도로비치는 어디로 가는 거냐, 천국이냐 지옥이냐, 그리고 저세상에서 용서받겠느냐 못 받겠느냐?'\"\n\n심리학자 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 이 말을 묘한 미소를 지으며 들었고, 마침내 드미트리 표도로비치가 어디로 가게 될지에 관한 이 발언을 \"사건 기록에 포함\"하도록 권고하는 것으로 마무리했다.\n\n칼가노프는 호출되자 마지못해, 얼굴을 찌푸리고 언짢은 기색으로 들어왔으며, 두 법관과는 오래전부터 매일같이 만나 온 지인 사이였음에도 마치 평생 처음 보는 사람들에게 말하듯 대했다. 그는 우선 \"그 일에 대해선 아무것도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않다\"고 운을 뗐다. 그러나 그가 \"여섯째\" 천 루블에 관한 말을 들었다는 것이 드러났고, 그 순간 바로 곁에 서 있었다는 사실도 인정했다. 그가 본 바로는 미탸의 손에 돈이 얼마나 있었는지 \"모른다\"고 했다. 그는 폴란드인들이 카드에서 속임수를 썼다고 단언했다. 거듭된 질문에 대해, 폴란드인들이 쫓겨난 후 미탸의 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와의 관계가 분명 나아졌으며, 그녀가 그를 사랑한다고 말했다고 진술했다. 그는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에 대해 마치 그녀가 최상류 사교계의 귀부인이기라도 한 듯 조심스럽고 정중하게 말했으며, 단 한 번도 그녀를 그루셴카라고 부르려 들지 않았다. 그 젊은이가 증언하기를 분명히 꺼리는데도 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 그를 아주 길게 신문했으며, 그날 밤 미탸의 이른바 \"로맨스\"를 이룬 온갖 세부 사정을 오직 그에게서만 알아냈다. 미탸는 단 한 번도 칼가노프의 말을 막지 않았다. 마침내 그들은 그 젊은이를 놓아주었고, 그는 감추지 못한 분노를 품은 채 방을 나섰다.\n\n폴란드인들도 신문을 받았다. 그들은 자기들 방에서 자리에 눕긴 했으나 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했고, 경찰관들이 도착하자 분명 호출되리라는 것을 깨닫고는 허둥지둥 옷을 차려입고 채비를 갖추었다. 그들은 다소 불안한 기색이 없지는 않았으나 위엄을 갖추고 증언했다. 키 작은 폴란드인은 시베리아에서 수의사로 복무했던 십이등관 퇴직 관리로 드러났다. 이름은 무샬로비치였다. 브루블레프스키 씨는 무자격 치과 의사로 밝혀졌다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 방에 들어서며 그들에게 질문을 던졌건만, 둘은 모두 한쪽에 서 있던 미하일 마카로비치를 향해 답했는데, 무지한 탓에 그를 가장 중요하고 지휘권을 쥔 인물로 여겨 말끝마다 그를 \"대령 나리\"라고 불렀다. 미하일 마카로비치 자신에게서 몇 차례 핀잔을 듣고 나서야 둘은 답변을 오직 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게만 해야 한다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그들은 몇몇 단어의 발음을 빼면 러시아어를 꽤 정확하게 구사할 줄 아는 것으로 드러났다. 그루셴카와의 과거와 현재의 관계에 대해 무샬로비치 씨는 자랑스럽고 열을 올려 말하는 바람에 미탸가 대번에 발끈하여, 자기가 보는 앞에서 그 \"비열한 놈\"이 그런 식으로 말하도록 내버려 두지 않겠다고 선언했다! 무샬로비치 씨는 즉시 \"비열한 놈\"이라는 말에 주의를 환기하며 그것을 조서에 기록해 달라고 청했다. 미탸는 노여움에 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"저놈은 비열한 놈이야! 비열한 놈이라고! 그걸 기록하시오. 그리고 조서에 적든 말든 나는 여전히 저놈이 비열한 놈이라고 선언한다는 것도 적으시오!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 이것을 조서에 적어 넣기는 했으나, 더없이 칭찬할 만한 재치와 수완을 발휘했다. 그는 미탸를 준엄하게 나무란 후, 사건의 낭만적인 측면에 관한 추가 심문을 모조리 잘라 버리고 서둘러 본질적인 문제로 넘어갔다. 폴란드인들이 내놓은 증언 가운데 한 대목이 법관들의 각별한 관심을 불러일으켰는데, 그것은 미탸가 바로 그 방에서 무샬로비치 씨를 매수하려 들었던 일, 즉 그에게 청구권을 포기하는 대가로 삼천 루블을 제안하며 그 자리에서 칠백 루블을 내놓고 나머지 이천삼백 루블은 \"이튿날 읍내에서 지불\"하겠다고 한 일이었다. 그때 그는 모크로예에서 전액을 가지고 있지는 않으나 자기 돈이 읍내에 있다고 맹세했다는 것이다. 미탸는 이튿날 읍내에서 나머지를 반드시 갚겠다고 말한 적은 없다고 발끈하여 지적했다. 그러나 브루블레프스키 씨가 그 진술을 확인해 주었고, 미탸는 잠시 생각한 후 얼굴을 찌푸리며, 아마 폴란드인들이 진술한 대로일 것이며, 그때 자기가 흥분해 있었으니 정말로 그렇게 말했을 수도 있다고 인정했다.\n\n검사는 이 증언에 단숨에 달려들었다. 그것은 미탸의 손에 들어온 삼천 루블 가운데 절반, 혹은 일부가 정말로 읍내 어딘가에, 어쩌면 여기 모크로예 어딘가에 감춰진 채 남아 있을지도 모른다는 것을 검찰 측에 입증해 주는 듯했다(실제로 그들은 이 추론을 그것에 근거하여 세웠다). 이것은 미탸의 손에서 고작 팔백 루블밖에 발견되지 않았다는, 검찰 측으로서는 그토록 당혹스러운 정황을 설명해 줄 터였다. 이 정황은 비록 사소하기는 했으나, 그때까지 어느 정도는 미탸에게 유리하게 작용해 온 유일한 증거였다. 그런데 이제 그에게 유리한 이 단 하나의 증거마저 무너지고 만 것이다. 자기 자신이 천오백 이상은 가지고 있지 않았다고 부인해 놓고서 나머지 이천삼백 루블은 어디서 마련하려 했느냐는 검사의 추궁에, 미탸는 그 \"꼬마 친구\"에게 돈이 아니라 체르마시냐 마을에 대한 자기 권리의 정식 양도 증서를 내놓을 작정이었다고 자신만만하게 대답했으니, 그것은 이미 삼소노프와 호흘라코바 부인에게도 제안했던 바로 그 권리였다. 검사는 \"이 발뺌의 천진함\"에 대놓고 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"그러면 당신은 그 사람이 이천삼백 루블 현금 대신 그런 증서를 받아들였으리라고 생각하는 거요?\"\n\n\"그자라면 틀림없이 받아들였을 거요.\" 미탸가 열을 올리며 단언했다. \"이거 보시오, 그자라면 그걸 가지고 이천이 아니라 사천이고 육천이고 거머쥘 수 있었을 테니까. 그자는 자기 변호사들, 폴란드인이며 유대인들을 그 일에 붙여서, 삼천이 아니라 그 영감한테서 재산을 통째로 뜯어낼 수도 있었을 거란 말이오.\"\n\n무샬로비치 씨의 증언은 물론 더없이 자세하게 조서에 기록되었다. 그런 다음 그들은 폴란드인들을 놓아주었다. 카드 속임수 사건은 거의 건드려지지 않았다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그것만으로도 그들에게 충분히 만족했던 터라 시시한 일로 그들을 성가시게 하고 싶지 않았으며, 더구나 그것은 카드를 둘러싼 어리석고 술 취한 다툼에 지나지 않았다. 그날 밤은 술판과 소동만으로도 이미 충분했던 것이다…… 그리하여 이백 루블은 폴란드인들의 주머니 속에 그대로 남게 되었다.\n\n이어서 늙은 막시모프가 불려 왔다. 그는 잔걸음으로 머뭇머뭇 다가왔으며, 몹시 헝클어지고 풀이 죽은 모습이었다. 그는 그동안 내내 아래층에서 그루셴카 곁에 피신해 말없이 앉아 있었는데, \"이따금 그녀를 붙들고 훌쩍거리며 파란 체크무늬 손수건으로 눈물을 닦곤 했다\"고 미하일 마카로비치가 나중에 묘사했다. 그래서 오히려 그녀가 그를 달래고 위로하려 들 정도였다. 늙은이는 대뜸 자기가 잘못을 저질렀다고, \"곤궁한 처지라 십 루블을\" 드미트리 표도로비치에게서 빌렸으며 그것을 갚을 용의가 있다고 고백했다. 드미트리 표도로비치에게서 지폐를 받을 때 누구보다 그 액수를 잘 보았을 터이니 그의 손에 돈이 얼마나 들려 있었는지 보았느냐는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 직접적인 질문에, 막시모프는 더없이 단호한 태도로 이만 루블이 있었다고 단언했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 이만 루블이나 되는 돈을 전에 본 적이라도 있단 말이오?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"물론 본 적이 있고말굽쇼, 이만은 아니어도 칠천을요, 우리 마누라가 내 자그마한 영지를 저당 잡혔을 때 말입니다. 멀찍이서 구경만 시켜 주고는 나한테 그걸 자랑하더군요. 아주 두툼한 뭉치였는데, 죄다 무지갯빛 지폐였습죠. 그런데 드미트리 표도로비치의 것도 죄다 무지갯빛이더란 말입니다…….\"\n\n그는 오래 붙들려 있지 않았다. 마침내 그루셴카의 차례가 되었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그녀의 등장이 미탸에게 미칠 영향을 분명히 우려하여 그에게 몇 마디 주의를 중얼거렸으나, 미탸는 말없이 고개를 숙여 \"소란을 피우지 않겠다\"는 뜻을 내비쳤다. 미하일 마카로비치가 친히 그루셴카를 데리고 들어왔다. 그녀는 준엄하고 침울한, 거의 평정해 보이는 얼굴로 들어와서는 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 권한 의자에 조용히 앉았다. 그녀는 몹시 창백했고, 추워하는 듯 자신의 화려한 검은 숄로 몸을 단단히 감쌌다. 그녀는 가벼운 오한에 시달리고 있었는데, 그것은 그날 밤 뒤에 찾아온 긴 병의 첫 징후였다. 그녀의 진중한 태도, 솔직하고 진지한 눈빛, 조용한 거동은 모든 이에게 매우 호의적인 인상을 남겼다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 심지어 다소 \"매료\"되기까지 했다. 그는 나중에 그 일을 이야기할 때, 비록 전에도 그녀를 여러 번 보긴 했으나 늘 그녀를 일종의 \"시골 창부\" 정도로 여겨 왔던 터라 그제야 비로소 \"그 여자가 얼마나 아름다운지\" 보았노라고 스스로 인정했다. \"그녀에겐 최상류 사교계의 몸가짐이 있더군.\" 그는 부인들의 모임에서 그녀에 관한 잡담을 늘어놓으며 열을 올려 말했다. 그러나 이 말은 부인들에게서 노골적인 분노를 샀고, 그들은 대번에 그를 \"엉큼한 양반\"이라고 불렀는데, 그는 그것을 무척 흡족해했다.\n\n그루셴카는 방에 들어서며 미탸를 잠깐 한번 흘끗 쳐다보았을 뿐인데, 미탸는 불안한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 표정은 대번에 그를 안심시켰다. 으레 거쳐야 할 첫 질문과 경고가 끝난 후, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 다소 머뭇거리면서도 더없이 정중한 태도를 잃지 않은 채, 퇴역 중위 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프와 어떤 사이냐고 그녀에게 물었다. 이에 그루셴카는 단호하고 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"지인이었어요. 지난 한 달 동안 지인으로서 저를 찾아오곤 했지요.\" 캐묻는 듯한 추가 질문에 대해 그녀는 거리낌 없이 완전히 솔직하게, 비록 \"때때로\" 그가 매력적이라고 여기긴 했으나 그를 사랑한 적은 없으며, \"고약한 심술에서\" 그와 그의 늙은 아버지의 마음을 모두 사로잡았다고, 미탸가 표도르 파블로비치며 그 밖의 모든 이를 몹시 질투하는 것을 보았으나 그것이 그저 재미있었을 뿐이라고 대답했다. 그녀는 표도르 파블로비치에게 갈 마음 따위는 결코 없었으며, 그저 그를 놀려 댔을 뿐이라고 했다. \"지난 한 달 동안 저는 그 두 사람 어느 쪽에도 마음이 없었어요. 저는 저를 모욕했던 다른 남자를 기다리고 있었으니까요. 하지만 제 생각엔,\" 그녀가 끝맺으며 말했다. \"그 일에 대해서는 당신들이 캐물을 필요도, 제가 답할 필요도 없을 것 같아요. 그건 제 개인적인 일이니까요.\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 곧바로 이 암시에 따랐다. 그는 다시 사건의 \"낭만적인\" 측면을 접어 두고 진지한 측면으로, 즉 삼천 루블에 관한 가장 중요한 문제로 넘어갔다. 그루셴카는 모크로예에서의 첫 번째 술잔치에 삼천 루블이 분명히 쓰였다는 진술을 확인해 주었으며, 비록 자신이 직접 돈을 세어 보지는 않았으나 드미트리 표도로비치 자신의 입에서 삼천이라는 말을 들었다고 했다.\n\n\"그가 당신에게만 그렇게 말했소, 아니면 다른 누군가 앞에서 그랬소, 그것도 아니면 그저 당신이 있는 자리에서 그가 남들에게 그 말을 하는 것을 들었을 뿐이오?\" 검사가 곧바로 물었다.\n\n이에 대해 그루셴카는, 다른 사람들 앞에서 그가 그렇게 말하는 것도 들었고, 둘만 있을 때 그렇게 말하는 것도 들었다고 답했다.\n\n\"그가 당신에게만 그렇게 말한 것이 한 번이오, 아니면 여러 번이오?\" 검사가 물었고, 미탸가 그루셴카에게 그렇게 말한 것이 여러 번이었다는 것을 알아냈다.\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치는 이 증언에 매우 흡족해했다. 추가 신문을 통해, 그루셴카 역시 그 돈이 어디서 났는지 알고 있었으며, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그것을 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 받았다는 것이 밝혀졌다.\n\n\"그러면 당신은 한 달 전에 쓰인 돈이 삼천이 아니라 그보다 적었으며, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그 절반을 자기가 쓰려고 따로 챙겨 두었다는 말을 단 한 번도 들은 적이 없소?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 그런 말은 전혀 들은 적이 없어요.\" 그루셴카가 대답했다.\n\n미탸가 오히려 그녀에게 자기는 한 푼도 없다고 자주 말했다는 것도 밝혀졌다.\n\n\"그이는 늘 아버지한테서 돈을 좀 받게 되기를 기대하고 있었어요.\" 그루셴카가 끝맺으며 말했다.\n\n\"그가 당신 앞에서 한 번도…… 무심코, 혹은 발끈한 순간에,\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 불쑥 끼어들었다. \"자기 아버지의 목숨을 노릴 작정이라고 말한 적이 없소?\"\n\n\"아흐, 그런 말은 했어요.\" 그루셴카가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"한 번이오, 아니면 여러 번이오?\"\n\n\"여러 번 그 말을 했어요, 언제나 화가 났을 때였지요.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은 그가 정말 그러리라고 믿었소?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 저는 그런 건 결코 믿지 않았어요.\" 그녀가 단호하게 대답했다. \"저는 그이의 고귀한 마음을 믿었으니까요.\"\n\n\"여러분, 허락해 주십시오.\" 미탸가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"여러분이 보는 앞에서 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 한마디만 하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"말해도 좋소.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 동의했다.\n\n\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나!\" 미탸가 의자에서 일어섰다. \"하느님을 믿고, 또 나를 믿어 주오. 나는 아버지의 살해에 죄가 없소!\"\n\n이 말을 내뱉고 미탸는 다시 의자에 앉았다. 그루셴카는 일어서서 성상 앞에 경건하게 성호를 그었다. \"주여, 당신께 감사드립니다.\" 그녀는 감격에 떨리는 목소리로 말했고, 여전히 선 채로 니콜라이 파르페노비치를 향해 돌아서서 덧붙였다.\n\n\"방금 저이가 말한 그대로니, 그걸 믿으세요! 저는 저이를 알아요. 저이는 농담이나 고집으로는 무슨 말이든 하지만, 양심을 거슬러 당신들을 속이는 일은 결코 없어요. 저이는 모든 진실을 말하고 있으니, 그걸 믿으셔도 돼요.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 당신이 내게 새 용기를 주었소.\" 미탸가 떨리는 목소리로 화답했다.\n\n전날 쓴 돈에 관해서는, 그것이 얼마였는지 모르지만 그가 여러 사람에게 삼천을 가지고 있다고 말하는 것을 들었다고 그녀는 진술했다. 그리고 그 돈을 어디서 났느냐는 질문에는, 그가 그것을 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 \"훔쳤다\"고 자기에게 말했으며, 자기는 그에게 그건 훔친 게 아니니 이튿날 그 돈을 갚아야 한다고 대꾸했다고 했다. 그가 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 훔쳤다고 한 돈이 어제 쓴 돈인지, 아니면 한 달 전 이곳에서 탕진한 돈인지 검사가 힘주어 묻자, 그녀는 그가 한 달 전에 쓴 돈을 두고 한 말이며, 자기는 그렇게 알아들었다고 단언했다.\n\n마침내 그루셴카는 풀려났고, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 충동적으로 그녀에게 곧장 읍내로 돌아가도 좋다고, 그리고 자기가 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 이를테면 말을 마련해 주는 일이라든가, 혹은 호위가 필요하다면 자기가…… 기꺼이 하겠노라고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"진심으로 감사드려요.\" 그루셴카가 그에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"저는 이 노신사분과 함께 갈 거예요, 저이를 제 마차에 태워 읍내로 모시고 갈 참이거든요. 그리고 그동안, 허락해 주신다면, 드미트리 표도로비치에 관해 당신들이 무슨 결정을 내리는지 듣기 위해 아래층에서 기다리겠어요.\"\n\n그녀가 나갔다. 미탸는 차분했고, 심지어 한결 밝아 보이기까지 했으나, 그것도 잠깐뿐이었다. 그는 점점 더 기이한 육체적 무력감에 짓눌렸다. 두 눈은 피로로 감겨 왔다. 증인 신문은 마침내 끝났다. 그들은 조서의 최종 검토에 들어갔다. 미탸는 일어나 의자에서 커튼 옆 구석으로 자리를 옮겨, 깔개가 덮인 커다란 궤짝 위에 드러눕더니, 곧바로 잠이 들었다.\n\n그는 그 장소와 시간에 전혀 어울리지 않는 기이한 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n그는 오래전에 주둔했던 어느 초원 지대를 마차로 달리고 있었는데, 한 농부가 두 마리 말이 끄는 짐마차에 그를 태우고 눈과 진눈깨비 속을 헤치고 갔다. 그는 추웠고, 때는 십일월 초였으며, 눈은 큼직하게 젖은 송이로 내려 땅에 닿자마자 녹아 버렸다. 농부는 그를 활기차게 몰아갔는데, 희끗한 긴 수염을 기르고 있었다. 늙은이는 아니고 쉰쯤 되어 보였으며, 잿빛 농부 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 멀지 않은 곳에 마을이 있어 검은 오두막들이 보였는데, 오두막 절반은 불에 타 버려 그을린 들보들만 삐죽삐죽 솟아 있었다. 그들이 마을로 들어서자, 길을 따라 농가 아낙들이 줄지어 서 있었는데, 한 무리의 여인들이, 죽 늘어선 한 줄이, 모두 야위고 핼쑥했으며, 얼굴은 거뭇한 빛을 띠고 있었고, 특히 맨 끝에 선 한 여인은 키가 크고 뼈가 앙상하여 마흔쯤으로 보였으나 어쩌면 스물밖에 안 되었을지도 모를, 길고 야윈 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 품에는 울고 있는 어린 갓난아기가 안겨 있었다. 그녀의 젖가슴은 어찌나 바싹 말라붙었는지 젖이 한 방울도 나지 않을 듯했다. 아이는 울고 또 울었으며, 추위로 새파래진 작은 주먹을 쥔, 발가벗은 작은 두 팔을 내뻗고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 저들이 울고 있지? 왜 저들이 울지?\" 미탸가 그 곁을 경쾌하게 내달리며 물었다.\n\n\"애기예요.\" 마부가 대답했다. \"애기가 우는 겁니다.\"\n\n미탸는 마부가 농부답게 \"애기\"라고 말하는 것에 마음이 찔렸고, 그 농부가 그것을 \"애기\"라고 부르는 것이 마음에 들었다. 거기에는 더 많은 연민이 담겨 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"한데 왜 우는 거지?\" 미탸가 어리석게도 끈질기게 물었다. \"왜 저 작은 팔이 발가벗었지? 왜 감싸 주질 않는 거지?\"\n\n\"애기가 춥거든요, 애기 옷이 얼어붙어서 따뜻하질 않은 거지요.\"\n\n\"한데 왜 그렇지? 왜?\" 어리석은 미탸가 여전히 끈질기게 물었다.\n\n\"왜라뇨, 가난한 사람들이거든요, 불타 버렸어요. 빵도 없습죠. 불타 버렸으니 구걸을 하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐.\" 미탸는 여전히 알아듣지 못하겠다는 듯했다. \"왜 저 가난한 어미들이 저기 서 있는 건지 말해 봐. 왜 사람들은 가난한 거지? 왜 애기는 가난한 거지? 왜 초원은 황량한 거지? 왜 저들은 서로 부둥켜안고 입 맞추지 않는 거지? 왜 저들은 기쁨의 노래를 부르지 않지? 왜 저들은 시커먼 비참함으로 저토록 거뭇한 거지? 왜 저들은 애기에게 젖을 먹이지 않지?\"\n\n그리고 그는 비록 자신의 물음들이 이치에 닿지 않고 터무니없긴 하나, 그래도 바로 그것을 묻고 싶었고, 또 바로 그런 식으로 물어야만 한다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 일찍이 한 번도 알지 못했던 연민의 격정이 자기 가슴속에 솟구치는 것을, 울고 싶고, 저들 모두를 위해 무언가를 하고 싶다는 것을 느꼈으니, 애기가 더는 울지 않도록, 거뭇한 얼굴의 바싹 마른 어미가 울지 않도록, 그 순간부터 다시는 아무도 눈물 흘리지 않도록 하고 싶었고, 또 그것을 지금 당장, 당장에, 온갖 장애물에도 아랑곳없이, 카라마조프 일가다운 그 모든 무모함을 다해 해내고 싶었다.\n\n\"그리고 저도 당신과 함께 가겠어요. 이제 평생토록 당신 곁을 떠나지 않겠어요, 저도 당신과 함께 가겠어요.\" 그는 바로 곁에서 감격에 떨리는 그루셴카의 다정한 목소리를 들었다. 그러자 그의 가슴은 뜨겁게 타올랐고, 그는 빛을 향해 안간힘을 쓰며 나아갔으며, 살고 싶고, 살고 싶고, 그 새로운, 손짓하는 빛을 향해 자꾸자꾸 나아가고 싶었고, 서두르고, 서두르고 싶었으니, 지금, 당장에!\n\n\"뭐야! 어디지?\" 그가 두 눈을 뜨고 궤짝 위에 일어나 앉으며 외쳤는데, 마치 졸도에서 깨어난 듯 환하게 미소 짓고 있었다. 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 그를 굽어보고 서서, 조서를 낭독하는 것을 듣고 거기에 서명하라고 권하고 있었다. 미탸는 자기가 한 시간 남짓 잠들어 있었음을 알아차렸으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 말은 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 그는 진이 빠진 채 궤짝에 등을 기댔을 때는 없던 베개가 자기 머리 밑에 놓여 있다는 사실에 문득 마음이 찔렸다.\n\n\"누가 내 머리 밑에 이 베개를 놓아 주었지? 누가 이토록 친절을 베풀었지?\" 그는 마치 자기에게 무슨 크나큰 친절이 베풀어지기라도 한 듯, 일종의 황홀한 감사에 젖어 목소리에 눈물을 머금고 외쳤다.\n\n그는 이 친절한 사람이 누구였는지 끝내 알아내지 못했다. 어쩌면 증인으로 나온 농부들 가운데 하나, 혹은 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 어린 서기가 가엾이 여겨 그의 머리 밑에 베개를 놓아 줄 생각을 했는지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그의 온 영혼이 눈물로 떨리고 있었다. 그는 탁자로 가서, 그들이 원하는 것이라면 무엇이든 서명하겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 나는 좋은 꿈을 꾸었소.\" 그는 얼굴에 기쁨인 듯한 새로운 빛을 띤 채 기이한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n# Chapter IX. They Carry Mitya Away\n\n조서에 서명이 끝나자, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 엄숙하게 피고를 향해 돌아서서 \"구류 결정서\"를 읽어 내려갔으니, 그 내용인즉 모년 모월 모일 모소에서 모 지방 법원의 예심 판사가 이러저러한 혐의로 기소된 모씨(곧 미탸)를 심문하였고(모든 혐의가 빠짐없이 적혀 있었다), 피고가 자신에게 제기된 혐의에 대해 유죄를 인정하지 않으면서도 변호를 위해 아무것도 제시하지 못한 점, 그리고 증인 모씨와 모씨, 또한 이러저러한 정황이 그에게 불리하게 증언하는 점을 헤아려, 법전의 이러저러한 조항에 따라 모씨(미탸)가 추적과 재판을 피할 모든 수단을 차단하기 위하여 그를 모 감옥에 구류함을 판결하며, 이로써 피고에게 이를 고지하고 동일한 \"구류 결정서\"의 사본 한 부를 검사보에게 전달한다는, 그러한 내용이 이러쿵저러쿵 이어졌다.\n\n요컨대 미탸는 그 순간부터 자신이 죄수의 신세이며, 곧장 읍내로 끌려가 거기서 아주 불쾌한 곳에 갇히게 되리라는 통고를 받은 것이었다. 미탸는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였고, 다만 어깨를 한번 으쓱했을 뿐이다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 여러분, 나는 여러분을 탓하지 않소. 나는 준비가 되어 있소…. 여러분으로서도 달리 어쩔 도리가 없다는 것을 이해하니까.\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치는 부드러운 어조로, 마침 그 자리에 있던 시골 경찰관 마브리키 마브리키예비치가 곧 그를 호송할 것이라고 알려 주었다….\n\n\"잠깐만.\" 미탸가 갑자기 말을 가로막았고, 억누를 수 없는 격정에 떠밀려 방 안의 모든 사람을 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"여러분, 우리는 모두 잔인한 인간이며, 우리는 모두 괴물이오. 우리는 모두 사람들을 울리고, 어머니들을 울리고, 젖먹이 아기들을 울리지만, 그 모든 사람들 가운데서도, 여기서 지금 결판을 내자면, 그 모두 가운데 내가 가장 비천한 벌레요! 나는 고치겠노라 맹세하고도 날마다 똑같은 추악한 짓을 거듭해 왔소. 나 같은 인간에게는 한 방의 타격이, 올가미로 옭아매듯 사로잡아 외부의 힘으로 결박해 줄 운명의 타격이 필요하다는 것을 이제야 알겠소. 나는 결코, 결코 스스로의 힘으로는 일어서지 못했을 것이오! 그러나 벼락이 떨어졌소. 나는 고발이라는 형벌을, 그리고 만인 앞에서의 치욕을 받아들이겠소. 나는 고통받기를 원하며, 고통을 통해 정화될 것이오. 어쩌면 나는 정화될지도 모르오, 여러분? 그러나 들어 주시오, 마지막으로 하는 말이오, 나는 아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없소. 나는 내 벌을 받아들이지만, 그것은 내가 아버지를 죽였기 때문이 아니라, 죽이려는 마음을 품었기 때문이며, 어쩌면 정말로 죽였을지도 모르기 때문이오. 그래도 나는 여러분과 끝까지 싸울 작정이오. 그것을 미리 일러두겠소. 나는 여러분과 끝까지 싸울 것이며, 그런 다음에는 하느님께서 판결하실 것이오. 안녕히 계시오, 여러분, 심문하는 동안 여러분에게 고함을 질렀다고 노여워하지 마시오. 아, 그때만 해도 나는 여전히 그토록 어리석은 바보였소…. 일 분만 지나면 나는 죄수가 되겠지만, 지금은 마지막으로, 자유로운 인간으로서, 드미트리 카라마조프가 여러분에게 손을 내미는 것이오. 여러분에게 작별을 고하면서, 나는 그것을 모든 사람에게 고하는 것이오.\"\n\n그의 목소리는 떨렸고 그는 손을 내밀었으나, 마침 그에게 가장 가까이 서 있던 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 별안간, 거의 신경질적인 동작으로 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추었다. 미탸는 그것을 즉각 알아채고 흠칫했다. 그는 내밀었던 손을 그 자리에서 떨구었다.\n\n\"예심은 아직 끝나지 않았습니다.\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 다소 멋쩍어하며 더듬더듬 말했다. \"읍내에서 그것을 계속할 것이며, 또 나로서는 물론 당신에게 모든 행운을… 당신의 변호에… 빌어 마지않습니다…. 사실인즉, 드미트리 표도로비치, 나는 늘 당신을 이를테면 죄인이라기보다는 불행한 사람으로 여기는 편이었습니다. 여기 있는 우리 모두가, 내가 감히 모두를 대신해 말해도 좋다면, 우리는 모두 당신이 본바탕은 명예를 아는 청년이지만, 아아, 어떤 격정에 다소 지나치게 휩쓸려 버린 사람임을 기꺼이 인정할 마음이 있습니다….\"\n\n니콜라이 파르페노비치의 자그마한 몸집은 말을 끝마칠 무렵에는 그야말로 위엄에 차 있었다. 미탸에게는 문득 이 \"소년\"이 곧 자기 팔을 붙들고 다른 구석으로 데려가, \"처녀들\"에 관한 대화를 다시 시작할 것만 같았다. 그러나 사형장으로 끌려나가는 죄수에게조차 더러는 영 동떨어지고 엉뚱한 생각들이 떠오르는 법이다.\n\n\"여러분, 여러분은 선량하고, 여러분은 인정이 있으니, 그 여자를 만나 마지막으로 작별 인사를 해도 되겠소?\" 미탸가 물었다.\n\n\"물론입니다, 그러나 여러 가지 사정을 고려하면… 실은, 지금으로서는 다음과 같은 사람의 입회 없이는 불가능합니다만—\"\n\n\"오, 그래요, 그래야만 한다면 그래야겠지요!\"\n\n그루셴카가 안으로 불려 들어왔으나, 작별은 짧았고, 말수도 적었으며, 니콜라이 파르페노비치를 전혀 만족시키지 못했다. 그루셴카는 미탸에게 깊이 허리를 숙여 절했다.\n\n\"당신에게 말했잖아요, 나는 당신의 것이고, 앞으로도 당신의 것이 되겠다고. 나는 그들이 당신을 어디로 보내든 영원히 당신을 따라가겠어요. 안녕히 가세요. 당신은 죄가 없어요. 비록 당신 스스로 자기 자신을 망쳐 버렸을지언정요.\"\n\n그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"용서해 줘, 그루샤, 내 사랑을, 내 사랑으로 너까지 망쳐 버린 것을.\"\n\n미탸는 무언가 더 말하려다가 그만 입을 다물고 밖으로 나갔다. 그는 곧장 끊임없이 자신을 감시하는 사람들에게 에워싸였다. 어제 안드레이의 세 마리 말을 몰아 그토록 기세 좋게 들이닥쳤던 그 계단 아래에, 마차 두 대가 채비를 갖추고 서 있었다. 다부지고 떡 벌어진 체격에 주름투성이 얼굴을 한 마브리키 마브리키예비치는 무언가에, 어떤 갑작스러운 차질에 짜증이 나 있었다. 그는 성난 목소리로 고함을 지르고 있었다. 그는 다소 지나치게 무뚝뚝한 태도로 미탸에게 마차에 오르라고 일렀다.\n\n'주막에서 내가 이 사람에게 술을 사 주었을 때는 얼굴이 영 딴판이었는데.' 미탸는 마차에 오르며 이렇게 생각했다. 대문께에는 사람들이, 농부들과 아낙네들과 마부들이 무리 지어 있었다. 트리폰 보리소비치도 계단을 내려왔다. 모두가 미탸를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"헤어지는 마당에, 부디 나를 용서해 주시오, 선량한 사람들이여!\" 미탸가 갑자기 마차에서 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리도 용서해 주시오!\" 두세 사람의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"당신에게도 작별을 고하오, 트리폰 보리소비치!\"\n\n그러나 트리폰 보리소비치는 돌아보지조차 않았다. 그는 어쩌면 너무 바빴는지도 모른다. 그 역시 무언가 때문에 고함을 지르고 부산을 떨고 있었다. 알고 보니 마브리키 마브리키예비치를 따라갈 순경 두 사람이 탈 두 번째 마차의 채비가 아직 다 갖추어지지 않았던 것이다. 두 번째 마차를 몰라는 명을 받은 농부는 작업복을 꿰입으면서, 이번엔 제 차례가 아니라 아킴의 차례라고 한사코 우겨댔다. 그러나 아킴은 코빼기도 보이지 않았다. 사람들이 그를 찾으러 뛰어갔다. 농부는 고집을 꺾지 않으며 제발 기다려 달라고 사정했다.\n\n\"우리 농부들 꼴이 어떤지 좀 보시오, 마브리키 마브리키예비치. 부끄러운 줄을 몰라요!\" 트리폰 보리소비치가 외쳤다. \"아킴이 그저께 당신한테 이십오 코페이카를 줬잖소. 그걸 죄다 술로 처마시고는 이제 와서 떠들어 대다니. 우리 천한 농부들을 데리고 그렇게 너그러우신 게, 나로선 그저 놀라울 따름이오, 마브리키 마브리키예비치, 내가 할 말은 그뿐이오.\"\n\n\"한데 우리한테 두 번째 마차가 무슨 소용이오?\" 미탸가 끼어들었다. \"한 대로 출발합시다, 마브리키 마브리키예비치. 나는 난동을 부리지 않을 테고, 당신한테서 달아나지도 않을 거요, 영감. 호송이 무슨 소용이란 말이오?\"\n\n\"이보시오, 미안하지만, 나한테 어떻게 말하는 건지 배운 적이 없거든 이참에 좀 배우시지. 나는 당신한테 '영감'이 아니오, 그리고 당신 충고 따위는 다음번을 위해 고이 간직해 두시오!\" 마브리키 마브리키예비치는 마치 분풀이라도 하게 되어 기쁘다는 듯 사납게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n미탸는 입을 다물 수밖에 없었다. 그는 온몸이 붉게 달아올랐다. 잠시 뒤 그는 문득 몹시 한기를 느꼈다. 비는 그쳤으나 잔뜩 흐린 하늘은 여전히 구름으로 뒤덮여 있었고, 매서운 바람이 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 후려쳤다.\n\n'몸이 으슬으슬하군.' 미탸는 어깨를 움찔거리며 생각했다.\n\n마침내 마브리키 마브리키예비치도 마차에 올라 무겁게 주저앉으면서, 미처 알아채지도 못했다는 듯 미탸를 구석으로 밀어붙였다. 그가 심기가 틀어져 자기에게 떨어진 임무를 몹시 못마땅해하고 있었던 것은 사실이었다.\n\n\"잘 있으시오, 트리폰 보리소비치!\" 미탸가 다시 외쳤고, 이번에는 자기도 그것이 선의에서 나온 외침이 아니라, 원망에서 저도 모르게 터져 나온 외침임을 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 트리폰 보리소비치는 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 거만하게 서서, 험상궂고 성난 얼굴로 미탸를 똑바로 노려보며 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"안녕히 가세요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 안녕히 가세요!\" 별안간 칼가노프의 목소리가 들려왔는데, 그가 불쑥 뛰어나온 것이었다. 마차로 달려온 그는 미탸에게 손을 내밀었다. 그는 모자도 쓰지 않은 차림이었다.\n\n미탸는 가까스로 그 손을 붙잡아 꼭 쥘 수 있었다.\n\n\"잘 있게, 친애하는 친구! 자네의 너그러움을 잊지 않겠네.\" 그가 따뜻하게 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 마차가 움직이며 두 사람의 손은 떨어졌다. 방울 소리가 울리기 시작했고 미탸는 실려 갔다.\n\n칼가노프는 도로 뛰어가 한구석에 주저앉더니, 고개를 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻은 채 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 오랫동안 그렇게 앉아, 스무 살 청년이 아니라 마치 어린 소년이기라도 한 양 울었다. 아, 그는 미탸의 유죄를 거의 의심 없이 믿고 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람들은 대체 뭐란 말인가? 이런 일이 있고서야 인간이란 대체 무엇일 수 있단 말인가?\" 그는 쓰디쓴 낙담 속에서, 거의 절망에 빠져 두서없이 외쳐 댔다. 그 순간 그에게는 살고 싶은 마음조차 없었다.\n\n\"이게 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가? 이게 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가?\" 슬픔에 잠긴 청년이 외쳤다.\n\n# PART IV Book X. The Boys Chapter I. Kolya Krassotkin\n\n11월 초였다. 매서운 추위가 몰아쳐 레오뮈르* 영하 11도였으나 눈은 내리지 않았는데, 밤사이 얼어붙은 땅 위에 메마른 눈이 조금 흩날려 쌓였고, 날카롭고 건조한 바람이 그 눈을 들어 올려 우리 고을의 쓸쓸한 거리를 따라, 특히 장터 언저리로 휘몰아 가고 있었다. 흐릿한 아침이었으나 눈은 이미 그쳐 있었다.\n\n* 레오뮈르: 물의 어는점을 0도, 끓는점을 80도로 정한 옛 온도 단위. 영하 11도는 섭씨로 약 영하 14도에 해당한다.\n장터에서 그리 멀지 않은, 플로트니코프의 가게 가까운 곳에 작은 집 한 채가 서 있었는데, 안팎이 모두 무척 정갈했다. 그 집은 크라소트킨 부인의 소유로, 그녀는 십사 년 전에 세상을 떠난 전직 도청 서기의 미망인이었다. 그 미망인은 서른두 살로 아직 제법 고운 여인이었으며, 단정한 그 작은 집에서 자기 재산으로 살아가고 있었다. 그녀는 점잖게 세상과 거리를 두고 살았으니, 성품은 부드러우면서도 제법 명랑한 편이었다. 남편이 죽었을 때 그녀는 열여덟 살가량이었고, 결혼한 지 겨우 일 년 만에 아들 하나를 막 낳은 참이었다. 남편이 죽은 그날부터 그녀는 자신의 소중한 보물, 아들 콜랴를 키우는 일에 온 마음과 영혼을 바쳤다. 그 십사 년 동안 그녀는 아들을 열렬히 사랑했으나, 아들은 그녀에게 행복보다는 고통을 훨씬 더 많이 안겨 주었다. 그녀는 거의 날마다 공포에 떨며 졸도할 지경이었으니, 아들이 병이 들지나 않을까, 감기에 걸리지나 않을까, 무슨 못된 짓을 하지나 않을까, 의자에 올라갔다가 떨어지지나 않을까, 그리고 이러쿵저러쿵 끊임없이 마음을 졸였다. 콜랴가 학교에 다니기 시작하자, 어머니는 아들을 돕고 함께 공부를 따라가려고 온갖 학문을 그와 더불어 익히는 일에 매달렸다. 그녀는 부랴부랴 교사들과 그 아내들과 친분을 텄고, 콜랴의 학교 친구들에게까지 비위를 맞추며 아첨하였으니, 그렇게 함으로써 콜랴가 그들에게 놀림받거나 비웃음당하거나 얻어맞는 일을 막아 보려는 심산이었다. 그녀가 어찌나 그러했던지, 급기야 아이들은 그녀 때문에 콜랴를 조롱하기 시작하여 \"엄마의 귀염둥이\"라고 놀려 대기에 이르렀다.\n\n그러나 그 소년은 제 앞가림을 할 줄 아는 아이였다. 그는 의지가 굳은 소년이었고, 그가 속한 반에서 소문이 났듯 \"엄청나게 힘이 센\" 아이였는데, 그 소문이 곧 사실로 드러났으니, 그는 날렵하고 의지가 강하며 대담하고 진취적인 기질을 지니고 있었다. 공부도 잘하여, 학교에는 그가 산수와 만국사에서 교사 다르다넬로프를 이길 수 있다는 소문이 돌았다. 그는 누구든 깔보기는 했어도 좋은 동무였고 거만하지는 않았다. 학교 친구들의 존경을 마땅한 것으로 받아들이면서도 그들과는 다정하게 지냈다. 무엇보다도 그는 어디서 선을 그어야 하는지를 알았다. 필요한 때에는 스스로를 억제할 줄 알았고, 교사들과의 관계에서도 장난이 용서받지 못할 규율 위반으로 넘어가는 그 마지막 신비로운 한계선만은 결코 넘지 않았다. 그러면서도 그는 기회만 있으면 학교에서 가장 어린 아이 못지않게 장난을 좋아했는데, 장난 자체를 위해서라기보다 무언가 화제를 불러일으키고, 무언가를 꾸며 내고, 무언가 효과적이고 눈에 띄는 일을 벌이고 싶어서였다. 그는 허영심이 대단히 강했다. 그는 제 어머니마저 자기에게 굴복하게 만들 줄 알았으니, 어머니를 거의 폭군처럼 휘둘렀다. 어머니는 그에게 양보했고, 아, 그녀는 몇 해 동안 줄곧 그에게 양보해 온 것이었다. 그녀가 도저히 견딜 수 없었던 단 한 가지 생각은, 제 아들이 자기를 그리 사랑하지 않는다는 것이었다. 그녀는 늘 콜랴가 자기에게 \"무정하다\"고 여겼고, 때때로 히스테릭한 눈물을 쏟으며 그의 냉담함을 나무라곤 했다. 소년은 이것을 싫어했고, 감정의 표현을 요구받으면 받을수록 일부러 그것을 피하려는 듯 보였다. 그러나 그것은 그의 의도에서 나온 것이 아니라 본능에서 나온 것이었으니—그것이 그의 성격이었다. 어머니는 잘못 알고 있었다. 그는 어머니를 무척 사랑했다. 다만 그가 자기네 학생들 말투로 표현하듯 \"양처럼 칠칠치 못한 감상벽\"을 싫어할 뿐이었다.\n\n집에는 책장이 하나 있었는데, 그 안에는 그의 아버지가 남긴 책 몇 권이 들어 있었다. 콜랴는 책 읽기를 좋아하여 그 가운데 몇 권을 혼자 읽어 두었다. 어머니는 그것을 개의치 않았고, 다만 이따금 아들이 놀러 나가는 대신 몇 시간씩 책장 곁에 서서 책에 골몰해 있는 모습을 보고 의아해할 뿐이었다. 그런 식으로 콜랴는 제 나이에 어울리지 않는 책들을 더러 읽기도 했다.\n\n소년은 대체로 장난에서 어디까지가 한계인지를 알고 있었으나, 근래에 들어 어머니를 진정으로 놀라게 하는 장난을 치기 시작했다. 사실 그가 한 짓에 악의가 깃든 것은 없었으나, 거기에는 거칠고 미친 듯한 무모함이 있었다.\n\n그해 칠월, 여름방학 동안의 일이었다. 어머니와 아들은 사십오 마일 떨어진 다른 군으로 가서, 한 먼 친척네 집에서 일주일을 보냈는데, 그 친척의 남편은 철도역의 관리였다(바로 그 역, 우리 고을에서 가장 가까운 그 역으로, 한 달 뒤에 이반 표도로비치 카라마조프가 그곳에서 모스크바로 떠나게 될 그 역이었다). 그곳에서 콜랴는 우선 철도와 관련된 온갖 세부 사항을 꼼꼼히 조사하는 일부터 시작했으니, 새로 익힌 지식으로 집에 돌아가 학교 친구들의 코를 납작하게 해 줄 수 있으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그런데 마침 그곳에는 다른 소년들 몇이 있어서 그는 곧 그들과 친해졌다. 그 가운데 더러는 역에서 살았고, 더러는 인근에서 살았는데, 모두 여섯이나 일곱쯤 되었고 죄다 열두 살에서 열다섯 살 사이였으며, 그중 둘은 우리 고을 출신이었다. 소년들은 함께 어울려 놀았는데, 콜랴가 역에 머문 지 나흘인가 닷새째 되던 날, 그 어리석은 소년들 사이에서 미친 내기 하나가 벌어졌다. 콜랴는 그 무리에서 거의 가장 어린 축에 들었고 그런 까닭에 다른 아이들에게 다소 무시당했는데, 허영심에서인지 무모한 객기에서인지 그는 두 루블을 걸고, 밤에 열한 시 기차가 들어올 시각에 철로 사이에 누워, 기차가 전속력으로 제 몸 위를 지나가는 동안 꼼짝 않고 그대로 누워 있겠노라고 내기를 걸었다. 사실 그들은 미리 조사를 해 보았는데, 철로 사이에 몸을 바싹 붙여 누우면 기차가 몸에 닿지 않고 지나갈 수 있다는 것이 밝혀지기는 했으나, 거기에 가만히 누워 있다는 것은 결코 우스운 일이 아니었다! 콜랴는 자기는 해내겠다고 단단히 우겼다. 처음에 아이들은 그를 비웃으며 거짓말쟁이니 허풍선이니 하고 불렀으나, 그것은 오히려 그를 부추길 뿐이었다. 무엇보다 그의 자존심을 건드린 것은, 그 열다섯 살짜리들이 자기를 너무도 거만하게 깔보며 처음에는 자기들과 어울릴 자격이 없는 \"꼬마\"로 취급하려 든 점이었으니, 그것은 도저히 참을 수 없는 모욕이었다.\n\n그리하여 저녁에 역에서 반 마일 떨어진 곳으로 가기로 정해졌으니, 그래야 기차가 역을 떠난 뒤 충분히 속력을 낼 여유가 있기 때문이었다. 소년들이 모였다. 달도 없는 칠흑같이 어두운 밤이었다. 정해진 시각에 콜랴는 철로 사이에 드러누웠다. 내기를 건 나머지 다섯은 둑 아래 덤불 속에서 기다렸는데, 가슴은 조마조마한 긴장으로 두근거렸고, 이어 그 긴장은 두려움과 후회로 바뀌었다. 마침내 그들은 멀리서 기차가 역을 떠나는 우르릉 소리를 들었다. 어둠 속에서 빨간 불빛 두 개가 번득였고, 괴물이 다가오며 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"달아나, 철로에서 비켜!\" 소년들이 덤불 속에서 공포에 숨이 막힌 채 콜랴에게 소리쳤다. 그러나 이미 늦었으니, 기차가 쏜살같이 달려와 휙 지나가 버렸다. 소년들은 콜랴에게로 달려갔다. 그는 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다. 그들은 그를 잡아당기며 일으켜 세우려 했다. 그는 별안간 일어나더니 한마디 말도 없이 걸어가 버렸다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기들을 겁주려고 의식을 잃은 척하고 누워 있었던 것이라고 둘러댔으나, 사실인즉 그는 정말로 의식을 잃었던 것이니, 이는 그가 한참 뒤에 어머니에게 고백한 대로였다. 이런 식으로 \"겁 없는 인물\"이라는 그의 명성이 영영 확립되었다. 그는 종잇장처럼 새하얗게 질려서 역의 친척집으로 돌아왔다. 이튿날 그는 가벼운 신경성 열병을 앓았으나, 기분만은 한껏 들떠 있었고 제 자신이 무척 흐뭇했다. 그 사건은 곧바로 알려지지는 않았으나, 그들이 고을로 돌아왔을 때 학교에까지 스며들어 마침내 교사들 귀에까지 들어가고 말았다. 그러나 그때 콜랴의 어머니가 부랴부랴 교사들에게 제 아들을 위해 사정사정하며 매달렸고, 끝내는 존경받고 영향력 있는 교사인 다르다넬로프가 그를 위해 힘써 주어, 그 일은 그냥 묻혀 버렸다.\n\n다르다넬로프는 중년의 독신 남자로, 여러 해 전부터 크라소트킨 부인을 열렬히 사모해 왔으며, 이미 한 번, 일 년쯤 전에 두려움과 자기 감정의 섬세함에 떨면서 그녀에게 더없이 정중하게 청혼을 감행한 적이 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 단호히 그를 거절했으니, 그를 받아들이는 것은 제 아들에 대한 배신이라고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 하기야 다르다넬로프는, 어떤 알 수 없는 징후들로 미루어 보건대, 자기가 그 매력적이면서도 너무나 정숙하고 정 많은 미망인에게 혐오의 대상은 아니라고 믿을 만한 근거를 가지고 있었다. 콜랴의 미친 장난이 그 얼음을 깨뜨린 듯하였으니, 다르다넬로프는 자신이 중재해 준 보답으로 한 가닥 희망의 암시를 얻었다. 사실 그 암시는 희미한 것이었으나, 다르다넬로프는 워낙 순수함과 섬세함의 본보기 같은 사람이었던 터라, 당장은 그것만으로도 더없이 행복해지기에 충분했다. 그는 그 소년을 좋아했으나, 그를 구슬려 제 편으로 끌어들이려 드는 것은 자신의 격에 어긋나는 일이라 여겼고, 수업 시간에는 그에게 엄격하고 단호하게 대했다. 콜랴 역시 그를 정중한 거리에 두었다. 그는 수업을 완벽하게 익혀 반에서 이등이었고, 다르다넬로프에게는 말수가 적었는데, 반 전체가 굳게 믿기를 콜랴는 만국사에 어찌나 능한지 다르다넬로프조차 \"이길\" 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 콜랴는 실제로 그에게 \"트로이를 세운 자가 누구냐?\"고 물은 적이 있었는데, 다르다넬로프는 이에 매우 막연한 대답을 내놓아, 민족들의 이동과 이주, 그 시대의 아득함, 신화적 전설 따위를 들먹였다. 그러나 \"트로이를 세운 자가 누구냐?\", 즉 어떤 개인들이 세웠느냐 하는 물음에는 그가 대답하지 못했고, 게다가 무슨 까닭에선지 그 질문을 한가롭고 시시한 것으로 여겼다. 그러나 소년들은 다르다넬로프가 트로이를 세운 자가 누구인지 모른다고 굳게 믿게 되었다. 콜랴는 트로이를 세운 자들에 관한 이야기를 스마라그도프에서 읽었는데, 그 역사책은 그의 아버지 책장에 꽂혀 있던 책들 가운데 하나였다. 결국 모든 소년들이 트로이를 세운 자가 대체 누구냐 하는 문제에 관심을 갖게 되었으나, 크라소트킨은 제 비밀을 일러 주지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 박식하다는 명성은 흔들림 없이 유지되었다.\n\n철도 사건이 있은 뒤로 어머니에 대한 콜랴의 태도에 어떤 변화가 일어났다. 아나 표도로브나(크라소트킨 부인)는 아들의 그 무용담을 전해 들었을 때 공포로 거의 정신이 나갈 뻔했다. 그녀는 며칠 동안 간헐적으로 이어지는 끔찍한 히스테리 발작을 일으켰고, 마침내 콜랴는 정말로 겁을 집어먹고서 다시는 그런 장난을 되풀이하지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속했다. 크라소트킨 부인의 요구에 따라 그는 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 맹세하였으며 아버지의 영전에 두고도 맹세하였는데, 그 \"사내다운\" 콜랴가 여섯 살배기 어린아이처럼 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그날 하루 종일 어머니와 아들은 끊임없이 서로의 품에 뛰어들어 흐느꼈다. 이튿날 콜랴는 전과 다름없이 \"무정한\" 모습으로 잠에서 깨었으나, 한결 말수가 적어지고 한결 얌전해졌으며, 한결 엄격하고 한결 생각이 깊어져 있었다.\n\n사실 그로부터 여섯 주 뒤에 그는 또 다른 말썽을 일으켜, 그 이름이 우리 고을 치안 판사의 귀에까지 들어가기에 이르렀으나, 그것은 전혀 다른 종류의 말썽으로, 우스꽝스럽고 어리석은 것이었으며, 알고 보니 그가 그 일에서 주도적인 역할을 한 것이 아니라 그저 거기에 연루되었을 뿐이었다. 그러나 이에 관해서는 나중에 이야기하기로 한다. 어머니는 여전히 마음을 졸이며 떨었으나, 그녀가 불안해할수록 다르다넬로프의 희망은 그만큼 더 커져 갔다. 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 콜랴가 다르다넬로프의 마음속에 무엇이 들어 있는지를 알아채고 헤아렸으며, 물론 그의 그 \"감정\" 때문에 그를 속으로 깊이 경멸했다는 점이다. 그는 전에 어머니 앞에서 그 경멸을 드러낼 만큼 눈치 없이 굴어, 다르다넬로프가 무엇을 노리는지 자기가 안다고 어렴풋이 내비치기까지 했었다. 그러나 철도 사건 이후로는 이 점에서도 그의 태도가 바뀌어, 그는 그 일에 대해 아주 먼 암시조차 입에 담지 않게 되었고, 어머니 앞에서 다르다넬로프를 더욱 정중하게 말하기 시작하니, 그 예민한 여인은 곧장 그것을 한없는 감사의 마음으로 알아차렸다. 그러나 콜랴가 있는 자리에서 손님이 다르다넬로프를 조금이라도 입에 올리면 그녀는 장미처럼 발갛게 얼굴을 붉히곤 했다. 그런 순간이면 콜랴는 인상을 찌푸리며 창밖을 노려보거나, 제 장화 상태를 살피거나, 아니면 \"페레즈본\"을 성난 목소리로 불러 대곤 했는데, 그것은 그가 한 달 전에 주워다 집으로 데려온 크고 털북숭이에 옴이 오른 개로, 무슨 까닭에선지 집 안에 몰래 가두어 두고 학교 친구들 누구에게도 보여 주지 않았다. 그는 그 개를 끔찍하게 들볶으며 온갖 재주를 가르쳤는데, 그래서 가엾은 개는 그가 학교에 가서 자리에 없으면 그를 그리워하며 울부짖었고, 그가 들어오면 기뻐 낑낑대며 미친 듯이 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 앞발을 들어 빌고, 땅에 누워 죽은 시늉을 하는 따위, 요컨대 그가 가르친 온갖 재주를 다 부려 보였는데, 그것은 명령을 받아서가 아니라 그저 흥분하고 고마워하는 제 마음의 열정에서 우러나온 것이었다.\n\n그런데 말이 나온 김에 깜빡 잊고 빠뜨린 것이 있으니, 콜랴 크라소트킨이 바로, 독자들이 이미 스네기료프 대위의 아들로 알고 있는 그 소년에게 주머니칼로 찔린 그 소년이라는 점이다. 일류샤는 학교 아이들이 자기 아버지를 두고 \"수세미 같은 머리\"라는 별명을 외쳐 대며 조롱했을 때 아버지를 두둔하다가 그리한 것이었다.\n\n# Chapter II. Children\n\n그리하여 그 살을 에는 듯 춥고 눈보라 휘몰아치는 십일월의 어느 날, 콜랴 크라소트킨은 집에 앉아 있었다. 그날은 일요일이라 학교가 없었다. 마침 열한 시를 친 참이었고, 그는 무슨 \"아주 긴급한 용무\"로 어딘가에 나가고 싶어 안달이었으나, 어쩌다 보니 집안의 손위 식구들이 갑작스럽고 별난 사건 탓에 모두 자리를 비워 그 혼자 집을 지키는 신세가 되고 말았다. 크라소트킨 부인은 복도 하나를 사이에 두고 나머지 집과 떨어진 작은 방 두 칸을, 어린아이 둘을 둔 어느 의사의 아내에게 세놓고 있었다. 이 부인은 안나 표도로브나와 동갑이었고, 그녀와 막역한 사이였다. 의사인 그녀의 남편은 일 년 전에 떠나 처음에는 오렌부르크로, 다음에는 타슈켄트로 가더니, 지난 반년 동안 그녀에게는 그에게서 한마디 소식도 없었다. 버림받은 이 부인에게 그나마 얼마간 위안이 되어 준 크라소트킨 부인과의 우정이 아니었던들, 그녀는 십중팔구 눈물에 녹아 흔적도 없이 사라지고 말았을 것이다. 그런데 이제 그녀의 불행에 한술 더 떠, 그녀의 단 하나뿐인 하녀 카테리나가 바로 전날 저녁 느닷없이 마음이 동하여, 안주인이 깜짝 놀라게도 동트기 전에 아이 하나를 세상에 내놓을 작정이라고 알리고 나섰다. 미리 그럴 낌새를 아무도 알아채지 못했다는 것이 누구에게나 거의 기적처럼 여겨졌다. 어안이 벙벙해진 의사의 아내는 아직 시간이 있을 때 카테리나를 읍내에서 그런 급한 일을 맡아 주는 산파의 집으로 옮기기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 하녀를 무척 소중히 여겼기에 곧장 이 계획을 실행에 옮겼고, 그곳에 남아 그녀를 돌보았다. 아침이 되자 이 일에 거들고 누군가에게 도움을 청하느라 크라소트킨 부인의 정다운 동정과 기력이 죄다 동원되기에 이르렀다.\n\n그리하여 두 부인은 모두 집을 비웠고, 크라소트킨 집안의 하녀 아가피야는 장을 보러 나간 터라, 콜랴는 잠시 \"꼬맹이들\", 곧 둘만 덩그러니 남겨진 의사 아내의 아들과 딸을 지키고 돌보는 처지가 된 것이었다. 콜랴는 집을 지키는 일이 두렵지 않았고, 게다가 그에게는 페레즈본이 있었으니, 그 개는 현관 의자 밑에 옴짝달싹 말고 납작 엎드려 있으라는 명령을 받은 터였다. 콜랴가 방들을 이리저리 오가다 현관으로 들어설 때마다 개는 고개를 흔들고 꼬리로 마루를 두 번 크게 알랑거리듯 두드렸으나, 아아! 그를 풀어 줄 휘파람 소리는 들려오지 않았다. 콜랴는 그 가엾은 개를 매섭게 노려보았고, 개는 다시금 고분고분하게 굳은 자세로 돌아갔다. 콜랴를 괴롭히는 단 한 가지는 \"꼬맹이들\"이었다. 물론 그는 카테리나의 뜻밖의 모험을 더없는 경멸의 눈초리로 바라보았으나, 그 버림받은 \"꼬맹이들\"만큼은 무척 귀여워해 이미 그림책 한 권을 가져다준 터였다. 손위인 여덟 살배기 계집아이 나스탸는 글을 읽을 줄 알았고, 일곱 살배기 사내아이 코스탸는 누이가 글을 읽어 주는 것을 무척 좋아했다. 크라소트킨은 물론 그들에게 더 신나는 놀잇거리를 마련해 줄 수도 있었다. 둘을 나란히 세워 놓고 병정놀이를 하거나, 온 집 안을 뒤져 가며 숨바꼭질을 시킬 수도 있었다. 그는 전에도 한두 번 그렇게 한 적이 있었고 또 그 정도쯤 마다하지 않는 터라, 한번은 학교에 크라소트킨이 집에서 어린 셋방 아이들과 말놀이를 하며 곁말처럼 고개를 한쪽으로 갸우뚱한 채 날뛰더라는 소문까지 퍼졌을 정도였다. 그러나 크라소트킨은 도도하게 이 빈정거림을 받아넘기며, 제 또래의 사내아이들, 곧 열세 살짜리들과 말놀이를 한다면야 \"이 나이에\" 분명 망신스러운 일이겠지만, 자기는 \"꼬맹이들\"이 좋아서 그들을 위해 그러는 것이며, 자기 감정을 두고 시비를 걸 권리는 누구에게도 없다고 못 박았다. 두 \"꼬맹이\"는 그를 무척 따랐다.\n\n그러나 이번만큼은 그도 놀이를 할 기분이 아니었다. 그의 앞에는 거의 무슨 비밀에 가까운, 제 나름의 대단히 중요한 용무가 놓여 있었던 것이다. 그러는 사이에도 시간은 자꾸 흘렀고, 아이들을 맡길 수 있을 아가피야는 장에서 돌아오지 않았다. 그는 벌써 몇 번이고 복도를 가로질러 셋방 문을 열고는, 일러둔 대로 책을 펴 놓고 앉아 있는 \"꼬맹이들\"을 안절부절못하며 들여다보았다. 그가 문을 열 때마다 아이들은 그가 들어와 무언가 즐겁고 재미난 짓을 해 주기를 바라며 그를 보고 히죽 웃었다. 그러나 콜랴는 마음이 어수선해 들어가지 않았다.\n\n마침내 열한 시를 알리는 소리가 울리자 그는 단번에 결심을 굳혔다. 그 \"빌어먹을\" 아가피야가 십 분 안에 돌아오지 않는다면 더는 기다리지 않고 나가 버리되, 물론 \"꼬맹이들\"에게는 자기가 없는 동안 용감하게 굴고, 말썽 부리지 말고, 무서워서 울지 말라고 다짐을 받아 두기로 한 것이다. 이런 생각에 그는 고양이 털 깃이 달린 솜 둔 겨울 외투를 걸치고 책가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 뒤, 이렇게 추운 날에는 늘 덧신을 신으라고 어머니가 입이 닳도록 당부하던 것도 아랑곳없이, 현관을 가로지르며 그 덧신을 경멸하듯 한 번 쳐다보고는 장화만 신은 채 밖으로 나섰다. 페레즈본은 그가 외출복 차림인 것을 보더니 안절부절못하면서도 힘차게 꼬리로 마루를 두드리기 시작했다. 온몸을 떨며 심지어 애처로운 낑낑 소리까지 냈다. 그러나 콜랴는 개가 그토록 흥분해 들뜬 것을 보고 이것이 규율 위반이라 판단해, 다시 일 분 동안 의자 밑에 붙들어 두었다가, 복도로 통하는 문을 열고 나서야 비로소 휘파람을 불어 불러냈다. 개는 미친 것처럼 펄쩍 뛰어올라 황홀해하며 그의 앞으로 깡충깡충 달려 나갔다.\n\n콜랴는 문을 열고 \"꼬맹이들\"을 들여다보았다. 둘은 여전히 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었으나, 책을 읽는 것이 아니라 무언가를 두고 열을 올리며 다투고 있었다. 아이들은 인생의 갖가지 흥미진진한 문제를 두고 곧잘 함께 입씨름을 벌였는데, 나스탸가 손위였던 까닭에 늘 이겼다. 코스탸는 누이와 의견이 맞지 않으면 거의 언제나 콜랴 크라소트킨에게 판가름을 청했고, 그의 판결은 둘 다에게 한 치도 틀림없는 것으로 여겨졌다. 이번에 \"꼬맹이들\"의 토론은 크라소트킨의 흥미를 제법 끌었던 터라, 그는 복도에 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 아이들은 그가 엿듣고 있는 것을 알아채자 더더욱 기를 쓰며 다투었다.\n\n\"난 절대로, 절대로 안 믿어.\" 나스탸가 재잘거렸다. \"할머니들이 텃밭의 양배추 속에서 아기를 찾아낸다는 거 말이야. 지금은 겨울이라 양배추도 없잖아, 그러니까 할머니가 카테리나한테 딸을 갖다줬을 리가 없어.\"\n\n콜랴는 혼자 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n\"아니면 어쩌면 어디선가 아기를 데려오기는 하는데, 그게 결혼한 사람들한테만 그러는 거겠지.\"\n\n코스탸는 나스탸를 빤히 바라보며 듣더니, 깊이 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"나스탸, 너 참 바보구나!\" 마침내 그가 단호하고 차분하게 말했다. \"결혼도 안 한 카테리나가 어떻게 아기를 가질 수가 있어?\"\n\n나스탸는 약이 올랐다.\n\n\"넌 아무것도 모르면서.\" 그녀가 신경질을 부리며 톡 쏘았다. \"어쩌면 남편이 있는데, 다만 감옥에 가 있는지도 모르잖아, 그래서 이제 아기를 가진 거지.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 여자 남편이 감옥에 있어?\" 따지기 좋아하는 코스탸가 진지하게 캐물었다.\n\n\"아니, 내가 말해 줄게.\" 나스탸가 충동적으로 말을 가로채며, 처음에 내세운 가설은 깡그리 버리고 잊은 채 말했다. \"그 여잔 남편이 없어, 그건 네 말이 맞아. 그런데 결혼하고 싶어서, 그래서 결혼할 생각을 했는데, 생각하고 또 생각하다 보니까 이제 그걸 갖게 된 거야, 그러니까 남편이 아니라 아기를 말이야.\"\n\n\"음, 어쩌면 그럴지도 몰라.\" 완전히 두 손 든 코스탸가 수긍했다. \"그런데 넌 아까는 그 말 안 했잖아. 그러니 내가 어떻게 알았겠어?\"\n\n\"자, 꼬맹이들.\" 콜랴가 방 안으로 들어서며 말했다. \"보아하니 너희들 대단한 사람들이로구나.\"\n\n\"페레즈본도 같이 왔다!\" 코스탸가 히죽 웃으며 손가락을 딱딱 튕겨 페레즈본을 부르기 시작했다.\n\n\"꼬맹이들, 나한테 골치 아픈 일이 생겼는데,\" 크라소트킨이 엄숙하게 운을 떼었다. \"너희가 좀 도와줘야겠다. 아가피야가 다리라도 부러진 게 분명해, 지금까지 안 돌아오는 걸 보면 말이야, 그건 틀림없어. 난 나가 봐야 해. 나 좀 나가게 해 줄래?\"\n\n아이들은 불안한 듯 서로를 마주 보았다. 웃고 있던 그들의 얼굴에 근심의 빛이 떠올랐으나, 자기들에게 무엇이 요구되는지는 아직 온전히 헤아리지 못했다.\n\n\"내가 없는 동안 말썽 안 부릴 거지? 찬장에 기어올라가 다리 부러뜨리지 않을 거지? 혼자 무섭다고 울지 않을 거지?\"\n\n아이들의 얼굴에 깊은 낙담의 기색이 어렸다.\n\n\"그러면 상으로 너희한테 뭔가 보여 줄 수도 있는데, 진짜 화약으로 쏠 수 있는 자그마한 구리 대포 말이야.\"\n\n아이들의 얼굴이 대뜸 환해졌다. \"대포 보여 줘.\" 코스탸가 온 얼굴에 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n크라소트킨은 책가방에 손을 넣어 자그마한 청동 대포를 끄집어내더니 탁자 위에 세워 놓았다.\n\n\"아, 그건 물어볼 줄 알았지! 봐, 바퀴가 달렸잖아.\" 그는 그 장난감을 탁자 위로 굴렸다. \"그리고 쏠 수도 있어. 산탄을 재워서 쏠 수 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구든 죽일 수 있어?\"\n\n\"누구든 죽일 수 있지. 누구한테든 겨누기만 하면 돼.\" 그러고는 크라소트킨이 화약을 어디에 넣고 산탄을 어디에 굴려 넣어야 하는지 설명하며 점화구 같은 자그마한 구멍을 보여 주었고, 쏠 때면 뒤로 튄다고 일러 주었다.\n\n아이들은 무척이나 흥미진진하게 귀를 기울였다. 특히 그들의 상상을 사로잡은 것은 대포가 뒤로 튄다는 점이었다.\n\n\"그럼 화약도 있어?\" 나스탸가 물었다.\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"화약도 보여 줘.\" 그녀가 애원하는 미소를 띠며 길게 늘여 말했다.\n\n크라소트킨은 다시 책가방을 뒤져 진짜 화약이 조금 든 작은 약병을 꺼냈다. 종이로 비틀어 싼 산탄도 얼마간 있었다. 그는 약병 마개까지 뽑아 손바닥에 화약을 조금 털어 보였다.\n\n\"가까이에 불이 없도록 조심해야 해, 안 그러면 터져서 우리 모두 죽고 말 테니까.\" 크라소트킨이 짐짓 으스스하게 그들을 일렀다.\n\n아이들은 두려움에 사로잡힌 눈빛으로 화약을 바라보았는데, 그 두려움이 도리어 그들의 즐거움을 한층 돋우었다. 그러나 코스탸는 산탄이 더 마음에 들었다.\n\n\"그럼 산탄도 타?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 그건 안 타.\"\n\n\"나 산탄 조금만 줘.\" 그가 애원하는 목소리로 청했다.\n\n\"산탄 조금 줄게. 자, 받아, 그런데 내가 돌아올 때까지 너희 엄마한테는 보여 주면 안 돼, 안 그러면 분명히 화약인 줄 알고 놀라서 죽을 지경이 돼서 너희를 매질할 테니까.\"\n\n\"엄마는 우리 한 번도 매질 안 해.\" 나스탸가 곧바로 한마디 했다.\n\n\"알아, 그냥 말을 마무리하려고 한 거야. 그리고 너흰 절대로 엄마를 속이면 안 돼, 딱 이번 한 번만, 내가 돌아올 때까지만이야. 그래서, 꼬맹이들, 나 나가도 될까? 내가 없어도 무서워서 울지 않을 거지?\"\n\n\"우우—울 거야.\" 코스탸가 벌써 울먹울먹하며 길게 늘여 말했다.\n\n\"우리 울 거야, 분명히 울 거야.\" 나스탸도 겁먹은 듯 조급하게 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"오, 애들아, 애들아, 너희 나이란 어찌 이리도 위태로운지! 어쩔 수가 없구나, 병아리들아, 내가 얼마나 걸릴지도 모르면서 너희와 함께 있어야겠다. 그런데 시간은 자꾸 흐르고, 시간은 자꾸 흐르는데, 어휴!\"\n\n\"페레즈본더러 죽은 척하라고 시켜 줘!\" 코스탸가 졸랐다.\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없지, 페레즈본의 힘을 빌려야겠구나. 이시, 페레즈본.\" 그러고는 콜랴가 개에게 명령을 내리기 시작했고, 개는 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다.\n\n페레즈본은 털이 뻣뻣하고 몸집이 중간쯤 되는 개로, 털빛은 일종의 보랏빛 도는 잿빛이었다. 오른쪽 눈은 멀었고 왼쪽 귀는 찢어져 있었다. 그는 낑낑거리고 펄쩍 뛰고, 일어서서 뒷다리로 걷고, 죽은 듯 뻣뻣하게 발을 허공에 든 채 벌렁 드러누웠다. 이 마지막 재주를 부리고 있을 때 문이 열리고, 크라소트킨 부인의 하녀 아가피야가, 마흔 살에 천연두 자국이 얽은 뚱뚱한 여자가 문간에 나타났다. 그녀는 장에서 돌아오는 길이라 먹을거리가 가득 든 자루를 손에 들고 있었다. 왼손에 그 먹을거리 자루를 든 채 그녀는 가만히 멈춰 서서 개를 지켜보았다. 콜랴는 그토록 그녀가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다렸으면서도 그 재주 부리기를 도중에 그치게 하지 않았고, 페레즈본을 으레 그러던 만큼 죽은 채로 붙들어 두었다가 마침내 휘파람을 불었다. 개는 벌떡 일어나 제 임무를 다한 기쁨에 겨워 깡충깡충 뛰어다니기 시작했다.\n\n\"세상에, 개라는 게 말이야!\" 아가피야가 점잔을 빼며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"왜 이리 늦었어, 여편네?\" 크라소트킨이 매섭게 물었다.\n\n\"여편네라니, 무슨! 저리 가, 이 꼬마 녀석아.\"\n\n\"꼬마 녀석?\"\n\"그래, 꼬마 녀석. 내가 늦는 게 너랑 무슨 상관이람. 내가 늦으면 다 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그런 거지.\" 아가피야가 화덕 주위를 부산스레 오가며, 목소리에는 노여움이나 언짢음의 기색이라곤 한 점도 없이 중얼거렸다. 사실 그녀는 짓궂은 젊은 주인과 한바탕 옥신각신하는 것이 자못 즐거운 듯 보였다.\n\n\"이봐, 이 경박한 아낙네야,\" 크라소트킨이 소파에서 일어서며 입을 열었다. \"세상에서 네가 신성하게 여기는 모든 것에 걸고, 게다가 그것 말고 또 무엇에든 걸고 맹세할 수 있겠어? 내가 없는 동안 이 꼬맹이들을 한눈팔지 않고 잘 지키겠다고 말이야. 난 나가 봐야 해.\"\n\n\"내가 무엇 때문에 맹세를 한담?\" 아가피야가 웃었다. \"그런 거 없이도 잘 봐줄 텐데.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 영원한 구원에 걸고 맹세해야 해. 안 그러면 난 안 나갈 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 나가지 말려무나. 그게 나랑 무슨 상관이야? 밖은 추우니, 집에나 있어.\"\n\n\"꼬맹이들,\" 콜랴가 아이들 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 아줌마가 내가 돌아오거나 너희 엄마가 올 때까지 너희랑 있어 줄 거야, 엄마는 진작 돌아왔어야 하는데 말이야. 점심도 차려 줄 거야. 얘들한테 뭐 좀 차려 줄 거지, 아가피야, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"그쯤이야 할 수 있지.\"\n\n\"잘 있어라, 병아리들아, 난 마음 놓고 간다. 그리고 할멈은,\" 그가 아가피야 곁을 지나며 나직한 소리로 진지하게 덧붙였다. \"이 어린것들을 봐서라도 카테리나에 관해 할멈의 그 노파다운 허튼소리 따위는 한마디도 안 했으면 좋겠어. 이시, 페레즈본!\"\n\n\"가, 어서 가 버려!\" 아가피야가 이번에는 정말로 화가 나서 쏘아붙였다. \"어처구니없는 녀석 같으니! 그런 소리를 하다니 매를 좀 맞아야 정신을 차리겠어, 바로 그거라고!\"\n\n# Chapter III. The Schoolboy\n\n그러나 콜랴는 어머니의 말을 듣지 못했다. 마침내 그는 밖으로 나설 수 있었다. 대문을 나서며 그는 사방을 둘러보고 어깨를 으쓱하더니, \"꽝꽝 얼겠는걸\" 하고 중얼거리며 곧장 거리를 따라 걷다가 오른쪽으로 꺾어 장터 쪽으로 향했다. 장터 바로 앞에서 두 번째 집에 이르렀을 때 그는 대문 앞에 멈춰 서서 주머니에서 호루라기를 꺼내, 마치 신호라도 보내듯 있는 힘껏 불었다. 일 분도 채 기다리기 전에 따뜻하고 깔끔한, 그것도 제법 멋스러운 외투를 걸친 열한 살가량의 발그레한 볼을 가진 소년이 그를 맞으러 쏜살같이 달려 나왔다. 이 아이는 예비반(콜랴 크라소트킨보다 두 학년 아래였다)의 소년 스무로프로, 형편이 넉넉한 관리의 아들이었다. 보아하니 그의 부모는 소문난 망나니 크라소트킨과 어울리는 것을 금했던 모양이라, 스무로프는 분명 몰래 빠져나온 것이었다. 그는—독자가 잊지 않았다면—두 달 전 일류샤에게 돌을 던졌던 소년 패거리 가운데 하나였다. 일류샤에 관해 알렉세이 카라마조프에게 일러바친 것도 바로 그였다.\n\n\"한 시간이나 기다렸어, 크라소트킨.\" 스무로프가 무덤덤하게 말하자, 두 소년은 장터 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어갔다.\n\n\"늦었지.\" 크라소트킨이 대답했다. \"사정이 있어 붙들렸어. 나하고 같이 다닌다고 두들겨 맞진 않겠지?\"\n\n\"아니, 정말이지 난 한 번도 안 맞아! 그런데 페레즈본도 데려왔어?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"걔도 데려가?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"아! 주치카였으면 좋았을 텐데!\"\n\n\"그건 불가능해. 주치카는 이제 없어. 주치카는 어둠의 안갯속으로 사라져 버렸다고.\"\n\n\"아! 이렇게 하면 안 될까?\" 스무로프가 갑자기 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. \"있잖아, 일류샤가 그러는데 주치카는 페레즈본처럼 털북숭이에 거무스름한 잿빛 개였대. 이게 주치카라고 하면 어때, 걔가 믿을지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"이봐, 거짓말은 피하라는 게 첫째고, 설령 좋은 뜻이라 해도 그래선 안 된다는 게 둘째야. 무엇보다, 내가 간다는 걸 그쪽에 한마디도 안 했길 바란다.\"\n\n\"천만에! 내가 알아서 하지. 그래도 페레즈본으로는 걔를 위로하지 못할 거야.\" 스무로프가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"걔 아버지, 그 대위, '삼 부스러기' 말이야, 오늘 코가 까만 진짜 마스티프 새끼를 데려다주겠다고 하셨거든. 그러면 일류샤가 위로받을 거라고 생각하시는데, 글쎄 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"일류샤는 좀 어때?\"\n\n\"아, 안 좋아, 아주 안 좋아! 폐병에 걸린 것 같아. 정신은 또렷한데, 숨이! 숨 쉬는 게 영 잘못됐어. 며칠 전엔 장화를 신겨 달라고 하더니 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 싶어 했어. 걸어 보려고 했지만 서질 못했어. '아, 아버지, 진작 말했잖아요. 이 장화는 못쓴다고요. 이걸론 도무지 똑바로 걸을 수가 없어요.' 이러는 거야. 장화 때문에 비틀거린다고 여겼지만, 사실은 그냥 기운이 없는 거였어. 일주일도 못 넘길 거야. 게르첸슈투베가 봐주고 있어. 이제 그 집은 다시 부자가 됐어. 돈이 산더미처럼 생겼대.\"\n\n\"사기꾼들이지.\"\n\n\"누가 사기꾼이야?\"\n\n\"의사들, 그 돌팔이 무리 전부 통틀어서 말이야, 물론 하나하나 따져도 그렇고. 난 의학을 안 믿어. 쓸데없는 제도야. 이 문제는 내가 죄다 파헤쳐 볼 작정이야. 그런데 너희가 저기서 부리는 그 감상은 다 뭐냐? 매일 반 전체가 거기 가는 모양이던데.\"\n\n\"반 전체는 아니야. 우리 애들 가운데 열 명쯤이 매일 걔를 보러 가는 거야. 그게 뭐 대수도 아니고.\"\n\n\"이 일에서 내가 도무지 이해 못 하는 건 알렉세이 카라마조프가 거기 끼어 있다는 거야. 자기 형이 그런 죄로 내일이나 모레면 재판을 받는데, 어린애들하고 감상에 젖을 시간이 어찌 그리도 넉넉한지.\"\n\n\"감상 같은 거 없어. 너도 지금 일류샤하고 화해하러 가는 거잖아.\"\n\n\"화해라고? 무슨 그런 어이없는 말을! 하지만 내 행동을 누가 분석하는 건 용납 못 해.\"\n\n\"일류샤가 널 보면 얼마나 기뻐하겠어! 걘 네가 온다는 걸 까맣게 몰라. 도대체 왜, 왜 넌 이때까지 안 온 거야?\" 스무로프가 갑자기 따뜻한 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"이봐, 그건 내 일이지 네 일이 아니야. 난 내가 가고 싶어서 가는 거고, 너희는 죄다 알렉세이 카라마조프한테 끌려간 거야. 거기엔 차이가 있단 말이야, 알겠어? 게다가 네가 어떻게 알아? 난 화해 같은 건 아예 안 할지도 몰라. 어처구니없는 말이야.\"\n\n\"카라마조프 일이 절대 아니야. 그 사람이 시킨 게 아니라고. 우리 애들이 스스로 가기 시작한 거야. 물론 처음엔 카라마조프하고 같이 갔지. 그래도 그런 거 전혀 없었어. 어리석은 짓 같은 건. 하나가 가고, 또 하나가 가고 그랬어. 걔 아버지는 우리를 보고 어찌나 기뻐하시는지. 너도 알지, 일류샤가 죽으면 그분은 그냥 미쳐 버리실 거야. 그분도 일류샤가 죽어 간다는 걸 알아. 그래서 우리가 일류샤하고 화해한 걸 무척 기뻐하시는 것 같아. 일류샤가 네 안부를 물었어, 그게 다야. 그냥 묻기만 하고 더는 말 안 해. 걔 아버지는 미쳐 버리거나 목을 매실 거야. 전에는 정말 미친 사람처럼 굴었어. 너도 알다시피 그분은 아주 점잖은 분이야. 그때 우리가 잘못한 거야. 그건 죄다 그때 그분을 때린 그 살인자 탓이지.\"\n\n\"그래도 카라마조프는 내겐 수수께끼야. 진작 사귀어 둘 수도 있었지만, 난 경우에 따라선 응당한 자존심을 지키는 걸 좋아해. 게다가 그 사람에 대해선 내가 세워 두고 풀어 가며 검증해야 할 이론이 하나 있거든.\"\n\n콜랴는 위엄 어린 침묵에 잠겼다. 스무로프 역시 입을 다물었다. 스무로프는 물론 크라소트킨을 떠받들었고, 자기를 그와 같은 반열에 놓는다는 건 꿈도 꾸지 못했다. 지금 그는 콜랴가 일류샤를 보러 \"내가 가고 싶어서 간다\"고 한 말에 굉장한 흥미를 느꼈다. 콜랴가 갑자기 그날 그를 찾아갈 마음을 먹은 데에는 무언가 비밀이 있으리라 짐작했다. 그들은 장터를 가로질렀는데, 그 시각에는 시골에서 온 짐수레가 잔뜩 있었고 살아 있는 가금도 수두룩했다. 장터 아낙들은 좌판에서 빵이며 무명천, 실 따위를 팔고 있었다. 읍내에서는 이런 일요 장을 순박하게도 \"큰장\"이라 불렀고, 한 해에 그런 큰장이 여러 차례 섰다.\n\n페레즈본은 더없이 신이 나서, 처음엔 이쪽, 다음엔 저쪽 냄새를 맡으며 쏘다녔다. 다른 개들을 만나면 개들끼리의 예법에 따라 열심히 서로의 냄새를 맡았다.\n\n\"난 저런 실감 나는 광경을 보는 게 좋아, 스무로프.\" 콜랴가 불쑥 말했다. \"개들이 마주치면 서로 냄새를 맡는 거 봤어? 그건 그놈들 본성의 법칙인 모양이야.\"\n\n\"응, 우스운 버릇이지.\"\n\n\"아니, 우습지 않아. 거기선 네가 틀렸어. 자연에는 우스운 게 없어, 편견에 사로잡힌 인간 눈엔 아무리 우스워 보여도 말이야. 개들이 이성을 갖고 우릴 비판할 수 있다면, 그놈들 주인인 인간들의 사회적 관계 속에서 분명 그만큼이나, 아니 훨씬 더 우스운 걸 찾아낼 거야. 정말이지 훨씬 더 많이. 내가 이 말을 거듭하는 건, 우리 사이에 어리석음이 훨씬 더 많다고 확신하기 때문이야. 그건 라키틴의 생각인데, 탁월한 생각이지. 난 사회주의자야, 스무로프.\"\n\n\"사회주의자가 뭔데?\" 스무로프가 물었다.\n\n\"그건 모두가 평등하고 재산을 다 함께 갖고, 결혼이라는 게 없고, 누구나 제일 마음에 드는 종교와 법을 가질 수 있고, 뭐 그 밖의 모든 게 그렇다는 거야. 넌 아직 그걸 이해할 나이가 못 돼. 그건 그렇고, 춥다.\"\n\n\"응, 영하 십이 도래. 아빠가 방금 온도계를 봤어.\"\n\n\"넌 눈치챘니, 스무로프, 한겨울엔 영하 십오 도나 십팔 도여도 지금만큼 춥게 느껴지지 않는데, 초겨울에 별안간 영하 십이 도까지 떨어지면, 특히 눈이 많이 안 왔을 때, 이렇게 춥게 느껴진다는 거? 사람들이 아직 거기 익숙해지지 않아서 그래. 인간한텐 모든 게 습관이야, 사회적이고 정치적인 관계에서까지도 죄다 그렇지. 습관이야말로 위대한 추진력이라고. 저 농부 꼴 좀 봐, 우습게 생겼네!\"\n\n콜랴는 키 큰 농부 하나를 가리켰는데, 긴 양가죽 외투를 입고 선량한 얼굴을 한 그는 짐수레 옆에 서서 볼품없는 가죽 장갑을 낀 두 손을 마주 쳐 가며 녹이고 있었다. 그의 길고 옅은 빛깔의 수염은 서리로 온통 하얗게 덮여 있었다.\n\n\"저 농부 수염이 얼어붙었네.\" 콜랴는 그를 지나치며 일부러 도발하듯 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"수염 얼어붙은 사람이 한둘인가.\" 농부가 침착하고 점잖게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"건드리지 마.\" 스무로프가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"괜찮아, 화 안 낼 거야. 좋은 사람이거든. 잘 가요, 마트베이.\"\n\n\"잘 가게.\"\n\n\"이름이 마트베이예요?\"\n\n\"그래. 어떻게 알았나?\"\n\n\"몰랐어요. 그냥 찍어 본 거예요.\"\n\n\"허, 그러게! 학생인가 보군?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"매도 좀 맞겠구먼?\"\n\n\"뭐, 별것 아니에요. 가끔요.\"\n\n\"아프나?\"\n\n\"음, 네, 아프죠.\"\n\n\"에휴, 사는 게 다 그렇지!\" 농부가 가슴속 깊은 데서 한숨을 토해 냈다.\n\n\"잘 가요, 마트베이.\"\n\n\"잘 가게. 자넨 참 괜찮은 녀석이야, 정말이지.\"\n\n소년들은 계속 걸어갔다.\n\n\"괜찮은 농부였어.\" 콜랴가 스무로프에게 말했다. \"난 농부들하고 얘기하는 게 좋아. 늘 기꺼이 그들을 공정하게 대접하려고 하지.\"\n\n\"근데 왜 거짓말했어, 우리가 매 맞는 척하면서?\" 스무로프가 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람을 흐뭇하게 해 주려고 그래야 했어.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n\"있잖아, 스무로프, 난 같은 걸 두 번 묻는 게 싫어. 사람들이 첫마디에 알아듣는 게 좋아. 어떤 건 설명이 안 돼. 농부의 관념대로라면 학생은 매를 맞고, 또 응당 맞아야 하는 거야. 매도 안 맞는 학생이 무슨 학생이겠어? 우린 안 맞는다고 했다면 그 사람은 실망했을 거라고. 하지만 넌 그걸 이해 못 해. 농부들하곤 어떻게 얘기해야 하는지 알아야 하는 거야.\"\n\n\"제발 놀려 먹지만 마, 안 그러면 그 거위 때처럼 또 무슨 사달이 날 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래서 겁이 나?\"\n\n\"웃지 마, 콜랴. 당연히 겁나지. 우리 아빠가 엄청 화내실 거야. 너랑 같이 나다니는 건 엄하게 금지당했단 말이야.\"\n\n\"걱정 마, 이번엔 아무 일도 없을 거야. 어이, 나타샤!\" 그는 좌판에 있던 한 장터 아낙에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"누구더러 나타샤래! 별꼴이야! 내 이름은 마리야다.\" 중년의 장터 아낙이 그에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"마리야라니 정말 다행이네요. 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n\"아이고, 이 망나니 같은 놈아! 코흘리개 같은 게 까불기는!\"\n\n\"바빠서요. 지금은 못 있어요. 다음 일요일에 얘기해 줘요.\" 콜랴는, 마치 자기가 아낙을 건드린 게 아니라 아낙이 자기를 건드리기라도 한 것처럼 그녀에게 손을 흔들었다.\n\n\"다음 일요일에 너한테 해 줄 얘기 따위 없다. 네놈이 시비를 걸었지, 이 뻔뻔한 원숭이 새끼야. 난 아무 말도 안 했어.\" 마리야가 악을 썼다. \"넌 매가 필요해, 그게 네놈한테 필요한 거야, 이 건방진 풋내기 녀석!\"\n\n그녀를 둘러싼 다른 장터 아낙들 사이에서 웃음이 와르르 터졌다. 그때 별안간 한 사내가 가까운 가게 회랑에서 잔뜩 성이 나서 튀어나왔다. 그는 젊은 사내로, 이 읍내 토박이는 아니었으며, 검고 곱슬곱슬한 머리에 길고 창백한 얼굴엔 마맛자국이 있었다. 긴 파란 외투를 입고 챙 달린 모자를 썼는데, 상인의 점원처럼 보였다. 그는 어리석은 흥분에 휩싸여 콜랴를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다.\n\n\"네놈을 알아!\" 그가 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"네놈을 안다고!\"\n\n콜랴는 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 자기가 이 사내와 언제 다툰 적이 있었는지 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 거리에서 하도 많은 다툼을 벌인 터라 그걸 다 기억하기란 어려운 노릇이었다.\n\n\"안다고요?\" 그가 비꼬듯 물었다.\n\n\"네놈을 알아! 네놈을 안다니까!\" 사내가 멍청하게 같은 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그거 잘됐네요. 그럼, 난 이제 가 봐야겠어요. 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n\"또 그 까부는 장난질이지?\" 사내가 외쳤다. \"또 그 까부는 장난질이야? 알아, 또 그 짓이지!\"\n\n\"형씨, 내가 또 까부는 장난질을 하건 말건 그게 당신이 상관할 바는 아니죠.\" 콜랴는 멈춰 서서 그를 찬찬히 훑어보며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 상관할 바가 아니라고?\"\n\n\"네, 당신이 상관할 바가 아니죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 누구 상관이야? 누구 상관? 누구 상관이냐고?\"\n\n\"트리폰 니키티치가 상관할 바지 당신은 아니에요.\"\n\n\"트리폰 니키티치가 누구야?\" 젊은 사내는 얼간이처럼 어리둥절해 콜랴를 빤히 쳐다보면서도 여전히 성이 나 있었다.\n\n콜랴는 진지하게 그를 훑어보았다.\n\n\"승천 교회엔 가 봤어요?\" 그가 갑자기 엄숙하게 힘주어 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 승천 교회? 뭣 하러? 아니, 안 가 봤어.\" 젊은 사내가 좀 당황해서 대꾸했다.\n\n\"사바네예프는 알아요?\" 콜랴가 한층 더 힘주어, 한층 더 근엄하게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"무슨 사바네예프? 아니, 난 그런 사람 몰라.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 지옥에나 가시오.\" 콜랴는 이렇게 대화를 끊어 버리고, 사바네예프조차 모르는 멍청이와 더 말을 섞는 건 자기 격에 안 맞는다는 듯 홱 오른쪽으로 돌아 잰걸음으로 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n\"멈춰, 어이! 무슨 사바네예프?\" 젊은 사내는 잠깐의 멍한 상태에서 깨어나 다시 전처럼 흥분했다. \"저 녀석이 뭐랬어?\" 그는 멍청한 눈초리로 장터 아낙들을 돌아보았다.\n\n아낙들이 웃었다.\n\n\"저 애가 뭘 노리는지 누가 알겠어.\" 그중 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"대체 무슨 사바네예프 얘길 하는 거야?\" 젊은 사내는 여전히 길길이 날뛰며 오른팔을 휘둘러 대고 같은 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"쿠즈미초프네 집에서 일하던 사바네예프겠지, 분명 그럴 거야.\" 아낙 하나가 넌지시 일러 주었다.\n\n젊은 사내가 그녀를 거칠게 노려보았다.\n\n\"쿠즈미초프네 집?\" 다른 아낙이 받았다. \"근데 그 사람 이름은 트리폰이 아니었어. 이름이 쿠즈마지 트리폰이 아니야. 그런데 저 애는 트리폰 니키티치라고 했으니, 같은 사람일 리가 없지.\"\n\n\"그 사람 이름은 트리폰도 사바네예프도 아니라 치조프야.\" 여태 잠자코 진지하게 듣고만 있던 세 번째 아낙이 불쑥 끼어들었다. \"알렉세이 이바니치라는 사람이라고. 치조프, 알렉세이 이바니치.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 치조프야.\" 네 번째 아낙이 그 말을 힘주어 거들었다.\n\n어리둥절해진 젊은 사내는 이 사람 저 사람을 번갈아 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"그래도 저 녀석이 뭘 물은 거요, 뭘 물은 거냐고요, 여러분?\" 그가 거의 절망에 빠져 외쳤다. \"'사바네예프를 아느냐'고 했단 말이오. 사바네예프가 누군지 대체 누가 안담?\"\n\n\"이런 답답한 양반 같으니. 사바네예프가 아니라 치조프, 알렉세이 이바니치 치조프라니까, 바로 그 사람이라고!\" 아낙 하나가 그에게 인상 깊게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"치조프가 누군데? 그게 누구냐고? 알면 말해 봐요.\"\n\n\"여름이면 장터에 앉아 있던, 키 크고 코나 훌쩍거리던 그 사람 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 치조프인지 뭔지가 나하고 무슨 상관이오, 여러분, 엉?\"\n\n\"걔가 자네하고 무슨 상관인지 내가 어찌 알아?\" 또 다른 아낙이 끼어들었다. \"그 사람한테 뭘 바라는지는 자네가 알아야지, 그렇게 야단법석을 떨면서. 그 애가 자네한테 말한 거지 우리한테 말한 게 아니잖아, 이 멍청아. 정말로 그 사람 몰라?\"\n\n\"누굴 몰라요?\"\n\n\"치조프 말이야.\"\n\n\"치조프고 너희들이고 다 지옥에나 가라지. 내가 그놈을 흠씬 패 주겠어, 꼭 그러고 말겠어. 그놈이 날 비웃었어!\"\n\n\"치조프를 흠씬 패 줘? 오히려 그 사람이 자넬 패 주겠지. 자넨 바보야, 그게 바로 자네라고!\"\n\n\"치조프가 아니야, 치조프가 아니라고, 이 심술궂고 못된 여편네야. 그 애새끼를 흠씬 패 주겠어. 저놈 잡아라, 잡아, 저놈이 날 비웃었어!\"\n\n아낙이 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 콜랴는 이미 한참 멀리 가서 의기양양한 태도로 걸어가고 있었다. 스무로프는 그 곁에서 걸으며 저 멀리 뒤편에서 아우성치는 무리를 돌아보았다. 그 역시 신이 났지만, 콜랴와 어울리다 무슨 사달에 휘말릴까 여전히 겁이 났다.\n\n\"무슨 사바네예프 말이었어?\" 그는 어떤 대답이 나올지 짐작하면서 콜랴에게 물었다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 알아? 이제 저들 사이에선 온종일 한바탕 소동이 벌어질 거야. 난 어느 계층에서든 바보들을 들쑤셔 놓는 게 좋아. 저기 또 다른 멍청이가 있군, 저 농부 말이야. 너도 알지, '멍청한 프랑스인보다 더 멍청한 자는 없다'는 말이 있잖아. 그런데 멍청한 러시아인도 얼굴에 똑같이 다 드러난단 말이야. 저 농부가 바보라는 게 얼굴에 온통 다 쓰여 있는 거 안 보여, 엉?\"\n\n\"내버려 둬, 콜랴. 그냥 가자.\"\n\n\"한번 시작한 이상 아무것도 날 못 말려. 어이, 안녕하시오, 농부 양반!\"\n\n마침 곁을 지나가던, 둥글고 순박한 얼굴에 희끗희끗한 수염을 기른 건장한 농부가 고개를 들어 소년을 바라보았다. 그는 그다지 멀쩡한 정신은 아닌 듯했다.\n\n\"안녕하신가, 자네가 날 비웃는 게 아니라면 말일세.\" 그가 천천히 대꾸했다.\n\n\"비웃는 거라면요?\" 콜랴가 웃었다.\n\n\"뭐, 농담은 농담이니까. 실컷 웃게. 난 상관없어. 농담에 해될 거야 없지.\"\n\n\"미안해요, 형씨, 농담이었어요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 하느님이 자넬 용서하시길!\"\n\n\"당신도 날 용서해 줘요?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 용서하지. 어서 가게.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 당신 똑똑한 농부 같은데요.\"\n\n\"자네보단 똑똑하지.\" 농부가 뜻밖에도 여전히 진지한 얼굴로 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 콜랴가 좀 당황해서 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 사실인걸.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"그렇다니까, 형씨.\"\n\n\"잘 가요, 농부 양반!\"\n\n\"잘 가게!\"\n\n\"농부에도 별의별 농부가 다 있어.\" 콜랴가 잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가 스무로프에게 말했다. \"내가 하필 똑똑한 농부를 골랐을 줄 어찌 알았겠어? 난 언제든 농민층의 지성을 인정할 준비가 돼 있어.\"\n\n저 멀리서 대성당의 시계가 열한 시 반을 쳤다. 소년들은 발걸음을 서둘러, 제법 먼 스네기료프 대위의 거처까지 빠르게, 거의 말없이 걸어갔다. 그 집에서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 콜랴는 멈춰 서더니, 스무로프에게 먼저 가서 카라마조프더러 나와 달라고 청하라고 일렀다.\n\n\"먼저 좀 냄새를 맡아 봐야지.\" 그가 스무로프에게 한마디 했다.\n\n\"뭣 하러 나오라고 해?\" 스무로프가 항변했다. \"그냥 들어가. 다들 널 보면 엄청 기뻐할 거야. 이 추위에 여기서 화해하는 게 무슨 소용이야?\"\n\n\"이 추위에 여기 밖에서 그 사람을 보려는 데엔 내 나름의 이유가 있어.\" 콜랴는 '꼬맹이들'을 상대할 때 즐겨 쓰던 그 거만한 어조로 그의 말을 잘랐고, 스무로프는 그가 시키는 대로 하려고 달려갔다.\n\n# Chapter IV. The Lost Dog\n\n콜랴는 위엄을 갖춘 태도로 울타리에 기댄 채 알료샤가 나타나기를 기다렸다. 그렇다, 그는 오래전부터 그를 만나고 싶었다. 아이들에게서 그에 관한 이야기를 숱하게 들어 왔으나, 지금껏 그의 이름이 입에 오르면 늘 경멸 어린 무관심을 가장했고, 알료샤에 관해 들은 바를 두고 \"비판\"까지 곁들이곤 했다. 그러나 속으로는 그와 알고 지내고 싶은 마음이 간절했으니, 알료샤에 관해 들은 모든 이야기에는 무언가 마음을 끄는, 호감 가는 데가 있었던 것이다. 그러니 지금 이 순간이 중요했다. 우선 그는 자신의 가장 좋은 모습을 보여 주어야 했고, 자신의 독립성을 내보여야 했다. \"안 그러면 저 사람은 나를 열세 살짜리로 여기고, 다른 사람들처럼 어린애 취급을 하겠지. 게다가 저 애들이 저 사람한테 다 뭐란 말인가? 친해지면 물어봐야겠다. 그나저나 내가 이렇게 키가 작은 건 안타깝군. 투지코프는 나보다 어린데도 머리통 반쯤은 더 크지 않은가. 하지만 나는 영리한 얼굴을 가졌어. 잘생기진 않았지. 내가 못생긴 줄은 알지만, 영리한 얼굴이야. 너무 함부로 떠들어선 안 되겠다. 단번에 그의 품에 와락 안기기라도 하면 저 사람이 생각하기를—체! 저 사람이 그렇게 생각한다면 얼마나 끔찍한가—!\"\n\n그런 생각들이, 더없이 독립적인 태도를 갖추려고 안간힘을 쓰는 콜랴의 마음을 들끓게 했다. 그를 가장 괴롭히는 것은 키가 작다는 점이었으니, \"못생긴\" 얼굴은 키가 작은 것만큼 신경 쓰이지 않았다. 작년에 그는 집 한구석 벽에다 연필로 제 키를 표시해 두었고, 그 뒤로 두 달마다 한 번씩 거기에 자기 키를 대 보며 얼마나 자랐는지 초조하게 재 보곤 했다. 그러나 아아! 그는 너무도 더디게 자랐고, 그 때문에 더러는 절망에 가까운 심정에 빠지기까지 했다. 그의 얼굴은 실상 결코 \"못생긴\" 것이 아니었으니, 오히려 제법 매력적이었다. 희고 창백한 살갗에 주근깨가 박혀 있었다. 작고 생기 넘치는 잿빛 두 눈은 겁이 없어 보였고, 더러는 감정으로 불타올랐다. 광대뼈는 다소 높은 편이었고, 입술은 작고 무척 붉으나 그다지 두텁지는 않았으며, 코는 자그마하면서 영락없이 위로 들려 있었다. \"내 코는 영락없는 들창코야, 영락없는 들창코라니까.\" 콜랴는 거울을 들여다볼 때면 이렇게 혼잣말로 중얼거렸고, 늘 분개하며 거울 앞을 떠났다. \"그런데 어쩌면 나는 영리한 얼굴마저 없는 게 아닐까?\" 그는 때때로 그것마저 의심하며 이렇게 생각하기도 했다. 그러나 그의 마음이 제 얼굴과 키에만 사로잡혀 있었다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 오히려 거울 앞에서 보내는 순간들이 그에게 아무리 쓰라렸더라도, 그는 그것을 금세 잊었고, 오래도록 잊었으니, 그 자신이 스스로 표현한 바와 같이 \"온통 사상과 실제 삶에 자신을 내맡겼던\" 것이다.\n\n알료샤가 빠른 걸음으로 나와 콜랴에게 서둘러 다가왔다. 그에게 다다르기도 전에, 콜랴는 그가 기뻐하는 얼굴이라는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다. \"나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워한단 말인가?\" 콜랴는 흐뭇한 기분이 들어 의아해했다. 여기서 잠깐 짚고 넘어가자면, 알료샤의 모습은 우리가 마지막으로 그를 보았을 때와 견주어 완전히 달라져 있었다. 그는 수도복을 벗고, 이제는 잘 재단된 외투에 부드럽고 둥근 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 머리도 짧게 깎은 차림이었다. 이 모든 것이 그에게 무척 잘 어울려, 그는 제법 잘생겨 보였다. 그 매력적인 얼굴에는 언제나 쾌활한 표정이 어려 있었으나, 그 쾌활함에는 부드러움과 평온함이 깃들어 있었다. 콜랴가 놀랍게도, 알료샤는 외투조차 걸치지 않은 차림 그대로 그에게 나왔다. 보아하니 그는 급히 나온 모양이었다. 그는 곧장 콜랴에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"드디어 왔구나! 우리가 얼마나 너를 보고 싶어 했는지 모른다!\"\n\n\"곧 알게 되실 사정이 좀 있었어요. 어쨌든 알게 되어 기쁩니다. 오래전부터 기회를 바라 왔고, 형님에 관한 이야기도 무척 많이 들었어요.\" 콜랴가 다소 숨을 헐떡이며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"우리야 어차피 만났을 거야. 나도 네 이야기를 많이 들었거든. 그런데 너는 여기 오는 데 시간이 꽤 걸렸구나.\"\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가고 있나요?\"\n\n\"일류샤가 무척 위중하단다. 틀림없이 죽어 가고 있어.\"\n\n\"끔찍하군요! 의학이라는 게 사기라는 걸 형님도 인정하셔야 합니다, 카라마조프.\" 콜랴가 격하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"일류샤가 네 이야기를 자주, 아주 자주 했어. 잠결에도, 헛소리 속에서도 말이야. 네가 그 애한테 아주, 아주 소중한 사람이었다는 걸 알 수 있더구나… 그 사건이 있기 전까지는… 그 칼 사건 말이야…. 그리고 또 다른 이유도 있고…. 말해 봐, 저게 네 개냐?\"\n\n\"네, 페레즈본이에요.\"\n\n\"주치카가 아니고?\" 알료샤가 가엾음이 가득한 눈으로 콜랴를 바라보았다. \"그 개는 영영 사라진 거니?\"\n\n\"형님들 모두 그게 주치카이기를 바란다는 거 알아요. 그 이야긴 죄다 들었어요.\" 콜랴가 수수께끼처럼 빙긋 웃었다. \"들어 보세요, 카라마조프, 제가 그 일을 죄다 말씀드릴게요. 그러려고 온 거예요. 안으로 들어가기 전에 그 사건 전부를 설명해 드리려고, 형님더러 여기 나와 달라고 한 거예요.\" 그가 활기를 띠며 말문을 열었다. \"그러니까요, 카라마조프, 일류샤는 지난봄에 예비반에 들어왔어요. 우리 예비반이 어떤지 아시잖아요—꼬마들 천지죠. 애들이 곧장 일류샤를 놀려 대기 시작했어요. 저는 두 학년 위라서, 당연히 멀찍이서 그 애들을 지켜보기만 했죠. 그 애가 약하고 작은데도 애들한테 굴복하지 않는 걸 봤어요. 맞서 싸우더군요. 자존심이 강하고, 눈에는 불꽃이 가득하다는 걸 알아봤죠. 저는 그런 아이들이 좋거든요. 그런데 애들은 그럴수록 더 놀려 댔어요. 무엇보다 고약했던 건, 그 애가 그때 형편없는 옷차림이었다는 거예요. 바지는 너무 작아 끼었고, 장화에는 구멍이 나 있었죠. 애들이 그걸 가지고 그 애를 들볶고 비웃었어요. 저는 그런 꼴은 못 참아요. 곧장 그 애 편을 들어, 애들한테 따끔하게 본때를 보여 줬죠. 애들을 때려 줬는데, 그래도 걔들은 저를 떠받든답니다, 아세요, 카라마조프?\" 콜랴가 충동적으로 으스댔다. \"저는 늘 아이들을 좋아하거든요. 지금 집에는 제 손에 병아리 두 마리가 있는데—그것 때문에 오늘 늦은 거예요. 어쨌든 그래서 애들이 일류샤 때리는 걸 그만뒀고, 제가 그 애를 보호 아래 두게 됐죠. 그 애가 자존심이 강하다는 걸 알아봤어요. 정말이지 그 애는 자존심이 강했어요. 그런데도 결국엔 저한테 노예처럼 헌신하게 됐죠. 제 사소한 분부도 다 따랐고, 저를 하느님처럼 떠받들었고, 저를 흉내 내려고 했어요. 수업 사이사이엔 곧장 제게 달려왔고, 저는 그 애를 데리고 돌아다녔어요. 일요일에도 그랬죠. 윗학년 애가 그렇게 아랫학년 애랑 친구가 되면 다들 비웃지만, 그건 편견이에요. 제 마음이 그렇다면, 그걸로 충분한 거죠. 저는 그 애를 가르치고, 키워 주고 있어요. 제가 그 애를 좋아하는데 왜 못 키워 줘요? 자, 카라마조프, 형님도 저 어린것들을 전부 떠맡으셨잖아요. 형님이 젊은 세대에 영향을 끼치고 싶어 하는 걸 알겠어요—그들을 키워 주고, 그들에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶으신 거죠. 단언컨대 풍문으로만 알던 형님 성격의 그런 점이, 무엇보다도 저를 끌어당겼어요. 그건 그렇고, 본론으로 들어가죠. 그 애한테 일종의 물러 터진 감상벽 같은 게 스며드는 걸 알아챘는데, 아시다시피 저는 그런 양 같은 감상벽을 정말이지 질색해요. 어릴 적부터 그랬죠. 그 애한테는 모순도 있었어요. 자존심이 강하면서도 저한테는 노예처럼 헌신했는데, 그러다가도 별안간 눈을 번뜩이며 제 말에 동의하길 거부하곤 했어요. 따지고 들고, 버럭 화를 냈죠. 제가 가끔 어떤 사상들을 펼쳐 보이면, 그게 사상에 동의하지 않아서라기보다 그저 저한테 반항하는 거라는 걸 알 수 있었어요. 제가 그 애의 살가운 표현에 냉담하게 대했기 때문이죠. 그래서 그 애를 제대로 길들이려고, 그 애가 다정하게 굴수록 저는 더 차갑게 굴었어요. 일부러 그런 거예요. 그게 제 생각이었죠. 제 목적은 그 애의 성격을 빚어 내고, 다듬어서, 한 사람의 사내로 만드는 거였어요… 게다가… 한마디면 형님은 분명 제 말을 알아들으시겠죠. 그러던 어느 날, 사흘 내리 그 애가 풀이 죽어 침울해하는 걸 알아챘어요. 제 냉담함 때문이 아니라 다른 무언가 때문에, 더 중대한 무언가 때문에요. 무슨 비극이 있나 싶었죠. 캐물어서 알아냈는데, 그 애가 어쩌다 스메르댜코프와 알게 됐다는 거예요. 형님 돌아가신 아버님 댁 하인 말이에요—물론 그분이 돌아가시기 전 일이죠—그자가 그 어린 바보한테 못된 장난을 가르쳤더라고요. 그러니까, 잔인하고 더러운 장난이죠. 빵 한 조각을 가져다 거기 핀을 찔러 넣은 다음, 아무거나 씹지도 않고 덥석 삼키는 굶주린 개한테 던져 주고는, 무슨 일이 벌어지나 지켜보라고 한 거예요. 그래서 그렇게 빵 한 조각을 마련해서, 그렇게 한바탕 소동을 일으켰던 그 주치카라는 텁수룩한 개한테 던져 줬어요. 그 개가 딸린 집 사람들은 종일 짖어 대는데도 통 먹이를 안 줬거든요. (형님은 그런 멍청한 짖는 소리가 좋으세요, 카라마조프? 저는 못 참아요.) 그래서 그 개가 빵으로 달려들어 꿀꺽 삼키더니, 깨갱거리기 시작했어요. 빙빙 돌다가, 깨갱거리며 달아나서는 시야에서 사라졌죠. 그게 일류샤가 직접 들려준 이야기예요. 그 애가 저한테 고백하면서 서럽게 울었어요. 온몸을 부들부들 떨며 저를 끌어안았죠. '그 개가 깨갱거리며 달아났어'라고 자꾸만 되뇌었어요. 그 광경이 그 애를 떠나지 않았던 거예요. 죄책감에 시달리고 있었어요. 그게 보였죠. 저는 그걸 심각하게 받아들였어요. 다른 일들까지 곁들여 그 애한테 따끔한 교훈을 주기로 마음먹었죠. 그래서 솔직히 말하면, 제가 완전히 정직하지는 않았고, 실제보다 더 분개한 척했을지도 몰라요. '넌 못된 짓을 한 거야,' 제가 말했죠, '넌 악당이야. 물론 일러바치진 않겠지만, 한동안은 너랑 더는 상종하지 않겠어. 생각해 보고 스무로프를 통해 알려 줄게'—스무로프는 방금 저랑 같이 온 애인데, 저를 위해서라면 뭐든 할 준비가 돼 있는 녀석이죠—'앞으로 너랑 상종할지, 아니면 악당이라고 영영 너를 버릴지 말이야.' 그 애는 엄청나게 충격을 받았어요. 솔직히 말하면 제가 너무 심하게 굴었다는 느낌이 들긴 했지만, 어쩔 수 없었어요. 그땐 그게 최선이라고 생각한 대로 한 거예요. 하루 이틀 뒤에, 스무로프를 보내 다시는 그 애한테 말을 걸지 않겠다고 전했어요. 학교 친구 둘이 더는 서로 상종하지 않기로 할 때 우리가 그렇게 부르거든요. 사실 속으로는 며칠만 그 애를 따돌렸다가, 뉘우치는 기색이 보이면 다시 손을 내밀 작정이었어요. 그게 제 의도였죠. 그런데 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 아세요? 그 애가 스무로프의 전갈을 듣더니 눈을 번뜩였어요. '크라소트킨한테 전해,' 그 애가 소리쳤죠, '내가 핀 박은 빵을 개란 개한테 다 던져 줄 거라고—전부—전부 다한테!' '아, 성깔 좀 부리는군. 저걸 좀 빼 줘야겠어.' 그래서 저는 그 애를 경멸하듯 대하기 시작했어요. 마주칠 때마다 외면하거나 비꼬듯 웃었죠. 그러던 바로 그 무렵에 그 애 아버지와 관련된 그 일이 벌어졌어요. 기억하시죠? 그 애가 이미 벌어진 일로 끔찍하게 흥분해 있었다는 걸 아셔야 해요. 애들은 제가 그 애를 버린 걸 보고 달려들어 약을 올렸어요. '수세미 같은 놈, 수세미 같은 놈!' 하고 외치면서요. 그러다 곧 그 애는 애들과 본격적으로 드잡이질을 벌이게 됐는데, 그게 저는 정말 안타까워요. 애들이 그 애를 한 번 아주 호되게 두들겨 팼던 모양이에요. 어느 날 그 애가 학교에서 나오는 애들 모두한테 달려들었어요. 저는 몇 걸음 떨어진 데 서서 지켜보고 있었죠. 그리고, 맹세컨대, 제가 웃었던 기억은 없어요. 오히려 정반대로, 그 애가 너무 가여워서, 조금만 더 있었으면 달려가 그 애 편을 들어 줬을 거예요. 그런데 그 애가 별안간 제 눈과 마주쳤어요. 그 애가 뭘 떠올렸는지 모르겠어요. 어쨌든 주머니칼을 꺼내 저한테 달려들더니, 제 허벅지를, 여기 오른쪽 다리를 찔렀어요. 저는 꿈쩍도 안 했어요. 솔직히 말하면 저도 가끔은 배짱이 두둑하답니다, 카라마조프. 저는 그저 그 애를 경멸하듯 바라봤어요. 마치 '이게 내 호의에 보답하는 꼴이냐? 하고 싶으면 또 해 봐, 얼마든지 받아 주마' 하는 것처럼요. 그런데 그 애는 다시 찌르지 않았어요. 무너져 내렸죠. 제가 저지른 짓에 겁을 먹고는 칼을 내던지더니 와락 울음을 터뜨리며 달아났어요. 물론 저는 그 애를 일러바치지 않았고, 선생님들 귀에 들어가지 않게 다들 입을 다물게 했어요. 어머니한테도 상처가 아물 때까지 말하지 않았죠. 게다가 그 상처는 그저 긁힌 정도였어요. 그러고 나서 그 애가 같은 날 돌을 던지고 형님 손가락을 물었다는 이야기를 들었는데—이제 형님도 그 애가 어떤 상태였는지 아시겠죠! 뭐, 어쩔 수 없죠. 그 애가 앓아누웠을 때 제가 찾아가 용서해 주지 않은 것—그러니까 화해하지 않은 것—은 어리석은 짓이었어요. 지금은 후회해요. 하지만 저한테는 특별한 이유가 있었어요. 자, 이제 다 말씀드렸어요… 그래도 제가 어리석었던 것 같아 걱정이네요.\"\n\n\"아, 안타깝구나.\" 알료샤가 감정을 담아 외쳤다. \"네가 그 애와 그런 사이였다는 걸 진작 알았더라면, 벌써 너를 찾아가 나와 함께 그 애한테 가 달라고 부탁했을 텐데. 믿어지니, 그 애가 열에 들떠 헛소리 속에서 네 이야기를 했단다. 네가 그 애한테 그렇게 소중한 사람인 줄은 몰랐어! 그런데 너는 정말 그 개를 못 찾은 거니? 그 애 아버지와 아이들이 온 읍내를 뒤지며 찾아다녔는데. 믿어지니, 그 애가 앓아누운 뒤로 눈물을 흘리며 이렇게 되뇌는 걸 세 번이나 들었어. '아버지, 제가 주치카를 죽여서 지금 이렇게 앓는 거예요. 하느님이 그 벌을 내리시는 거예요.' 그 생각을 머릿속에서 떨치질 못해. 그러니 만약 그 개를 찾아내서 살아 있다는 게 밝혀진다면, 어쩌면 그 기쁨이 그 애를 낫게 하지 않을까 싶을 정도야. 우리 모두 너한테 희망을 걸어 왔단다.\"\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 어째서 제가 그 개를 찾아낼 사람이라고 기대하셨어요?\" 콜랴가 큰 호기심을 품고 물었다. \"왜 다른 누구도 아닌 저를 믿으셨죠?\"\n\n\"네가 그 개를 찾고 있고, 찾으면 데려올 거라는 소문이 있었거든. 스무로프가 비슷한 말을 했어. 우리 모두 일류샤한테 그 개가 살아 있다고, 누가 봤다고 설득하려 애써 왔단다. 아이들이 그 애한테 살아 있는 산토끼를 갖다줬는데, 그 애는 그저 희미하게 웃으며 바라보더니 들판에 풀어 주라고 하더구나. 그래서 그렇게 했지. 그 애 아버지가 방금 막 돌아왔어. 마스티프 강아지를 데려와서 그걸로 위로해 줄까 했는데, 내 생각엔 외려 더 나빠지기만 하는 것 같아.\"\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 카라마조프, 그 애 아버지는 어떤 사람이에요? 저도 알긴 하지만, 형님이 보시기엔 어떤가요—협잡꾼인가요, 어릿광대인가요?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지 않아. 깊은 감정을 지니고도 어쩌다 짓밟혀 버린 사람들이 있단다. 그런 이들의 어릿광대짓이란, 여러 해 모욕당하고 위협당한 끝에 차마 진실을 말하지 못하는 자들을 향한, 원한 어린 빈정거림의 한 형태야. 믿어 다오, 크라소트킨, 그런 어릿광대짓은 때로 더없이 비극적이란다. 그 사람의 삶은 이제 온통 일류샤에게 쏠려 있어. 만약 일류샤가 죽으면, 그 사람은 슬픔에 미쳐 버리거나 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거야. 지금 그 사람을 보고 있으면 그게 거의 틀림없다는 느낌이 들어.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 카라마조프. 형님은 인간 본성을 꿰뚫어 보시는군요.\" 콜랴가 감정을 담아 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그리고 네가 개를 데리고 있는 걸 보자마자, 나는 네가 주치카를 데려오는 줄 알았어.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 카라마조프, 어쩌면 아직 찾아낼 수도 있어요. 하지만 이건 페레즈본이에요. 지금 이 녀석을 안으로 들여보낼게요. 어쩌면 마스티프 강아지보다 일류샤를 더 즐겁게 해 줄지도 몰라요. 잠깐만요, 카라마조프, 곧 뭔가 알게 되실 거예요. 그런데, 참, 제가 형님을 여기 붙들어 놓고 있네요!\" 콜랴가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"이 매서운 추위에 외투도 안 걸치셨는데. 보세요, 제가 얼마나 이기적인지. 아, 우린 다 이기주의자예요, 카라마조프!\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마라. 춥긴 해도 나는 감기에 잘 걸리지 않아. 그래도 들어가자. 그런데, 참, 네 이름이 뭐니? 콜랴라고 불리는 건 아는데, 그 밖엔?\"\n\n\"니콜라이—니콜라이 이바노비치 크라소트킨이에요. 아니면 공식 문서에서 부르는 대로 하자면 '크라소트킨의 아들'이고요.\" 콜랴가 무슨 까닭에선지 웃더니, 별안간 덧붙였다. \"물론 저는 니콜라이라는 제 이름이 싫어요.\"\n\n\"왜 그러니?\"\n\n\"너무 흔해 빠지고, 너무 평범하잖아요.\"\n\n\"열세 살이지?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 열네 살이에요—그러니까, 곧, 보름 뒤면 열네 살이 돼요. 형님한테만 제 약점 하나를 고백할게요, 카라마조프, 이게 우리 첫 만남이니까, 형님이 단번에 제 성격을 알 수 있게요. 저는 나이를 묻는 게 싫어요. 그것보다 더한 건… 사실은… 저에 관해 떠도는 험담이 하나 있는데, 지난주에 제가 예비반 애들이랑 강도놀이를 했다는 거예요. 제가 걔들이랑 논 건 사실이지만, 제 재미로 그랬다는 건 완전히 모함이에요. 형님이 그 이야기를 들으셨으리라고 믿을 만한 이유가 있는데, 저는 제 재미로 논 게 아니라 아이들을 위해서 그런 거예요. 걔들끼리는 뭘 하고 놀지 도무지 생각해 내질 못하니까요. 하지만 사람들은 늘 무슨 어처구니없는 이야기를 만들어 내요. 정말이지 여긴 험담으로 치면 끔찍한 읍내예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 만약 네 재미로 놀았다 한들, 그게 무슨 잘못이니?\"\n\n\"이거 참, 제 재미라뇨! 형님은 말타기 놀이 같은 건 안 하시잖아요?\"\n\n\"하지만 이렇게 봐야 해.\" 알료샤가 빙긋 웃으며 말했다. \"어른들도 극장에 가는데, 거기서는 온갖 영웅들의 모험이 펼쳐지지—때로는 강도와 전투도 나오고—그것도 결국 형식만 다를 뿐 똑같은 것 아니겠니? 그리고 젊은이들이 놀이 시간에 하는 병정놀이나 강도놀이도 첫 단계의 예술이란다. 알다시피, 그건 자라나는 젊은이들의 예술적 본능에서 솟아나는 거야. 그리고 때로는 이런 놀이가 극장의 공연보다 훨씬 나아. 유일한 차이라면, 극장에는 배우를 보러 가는 거지만, 이런 놀이에서는 젊은이들 자신이 배우라는 점이지. 하지만 그건 지극히 자연스러운 일이야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요? 그게 형님 생각이에요?\" 콜랴가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. \"아, 그거 참, 제법 흥미로운 견해네요. 집에 가면 곰곰이 생각해 봐야겠어요. 솔직히 형님한테 뭔가 배울 수 있겠다 싶었거든요. 저는 형님께 배우러 온 거예요, 카라마조프.\" 콜랴가 절로 우러나는 감정이 가득한 목소리로 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"나도 너한테 배우러 온 거란다.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 그의 손을 꼭 잡았다.\n\n콜랴는 알료샤가 무척 마음에 들었다. 그를 가장 강하게 사로잡은 것은, 알료샤가 그를 꼭 동등한 사람처럼 대하고, 마치 그가 \"다 큰 어른\"이라도 되는 것처럼 그에게 이야기한다는 점이었다.\n\n\"제가 곧 형님께 뭔가 보여 드릴게요, 카라마조프. 이것도 일종의 연극 공연이에요.\" 그가 초조하게 웃으며 말했다. \"그것 때문에 온 거예요.\"\n\n\"먼저 왼쪽의 집주인네로 가자. 아이들이 다들 외투를 거기 벗어 두거든. 방이 좁고 더워서 말이야.\"\n\n\"아, 저는 잠깐만 들어갈 거예요. 외투는 그냥 입고 있을게요. 페레즈본은 여기 복도에서 죽은 시늉을 하고 있을 거예요. 이시,* 페레즈본, 엎드려 죽은 척해! 보세요, 이렇게 죽은 시늉을 하잖아요. 제가 먼저 들어가서 살펴볼게요. 그러다 적당하다 싶을 때 이 녀석한테 휘파람을 불 텐데, 그러면 보세요, 미친 듯이 달려 들어올 거예요. 다만 스무로프가 그 순간에 문 여는 걸 잊으면 안 돼요. 제가 다 준비해 둘 테니, 형님은 뭔가 구경하시게 될 거예요.\"\n* 이시(Ici): '여기로'라는 뜻의 프랑스어.\n\n# Chapter V. By Ilusha’s Bedside\n\n퇴역 이등 대위 스네기료프의 가족이 사는 그 방은 독자에게 이미 낯익은 곳이다. 그때 그 방은 답답하고 비좁았으며 여러 손님들로 북적이고 있었다. 사내아이 몇이 일류샤 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 스무로프를 비롯한 그들 모두가 자기들을 데려와 일류샤와 화해시킨 것이 알료샤라는 사실을 부인하려 들었으나, 실은 분명한 사실이었다. 그가 부린 재간이라곤 \"양 떼처럼 질질 짜는 감상\" 따위 없이, 마치 우연인 듯 아무런 꿍꿍이도 없는 척하며 아이들을 하나씩 하나씩 일류샤에게 데려간 것뿐이었다. 그것은 고통 속에 있던 일류샤에게 크나큰 위안이 되었다. 한때 자기의 적이었던 이 아이들이 보여 주는 거의 다정하다 할 애정과 동정에 그는 깊이 감동했다. 오직 크라소트킨 한 사람만이 보이지 않았고, 그의 부재는 일류샤의 가슴을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 그의 쓰라린 기억들 가운데서도 가장 쓰라린 것은, 자기의 유일한 친구이자 보호자였던 크라소트킨을 칼로 찌른 일이었을 것이다. 일류샤와 가장 먼저 화해한 영리한 꼬마 스무로프도 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 스무로프가 크라소트킨에게 알료샤가 무슨 일로 너를 보러 오고 싶어 한다고 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 크라소트킨은 그의 말을 잘라 버리며, \"카라마조프\"에게 당장 가서, 자기는 어떻게 해야 할지 누구보다 잘 알고 있고, 누구의 충고도 필요 없으며, 일류샤를 보러 간다면 그건 \"나름의 이유가 있어서\" 자기가 정한 때에 갈 것이라고 전하라고 스무로프에게 일렀다.\n\n그것은 이번 일요일보다 두 주 전의 일이었다. 그래서 알료샤는 가려고 마음먹었으면서도 그를 보러 가지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 기다리면서도 스무로프를 두 번 더 그에게 보냈다. 두 번 다 크라소트킨은 무뚝뚝하고 짜증스러운 거절로 그를 대했고, 알료샤에게 더는 자기를 귀찮게 하지 말라는, 만일 그가 직접 온다면 자기, 크라소트킨은 일류샤에게 아예 가지 않겠다는 전갈을 보냈다. 바로 그 마지막 날까지도 스무로프는 콜랴가 그날 아침 일류샤에게 갈 작정인 줄 몰랐고, 다만 바로 전날 저녁 스무로프와 헤어지면서 콜랴가 불쑥, 다음 날 아침 집에서 자기를 기다리라고, 함께 스네기료프네에 가겠다고 일렀을 뿐이었는데, 다만 자기가 간다는 말은 절대로 하지 말라고, 우연히 들른 것처럼 하고 싶다고 단단히 일러두었다. 스무로프는 그대로 따랐다. 콜랴가 잃어버린 개를 데려올 것이라는 스무로프의 짐작은, \"그 개가 살아 있는데도 찾아내지 못한다면 그놈들은 멍청이가 틀림없다\"고 콜랴가 흘린 말에 근거한 것이었다. 스무로프가 기회를 엿보다가 그 개에 관한 자기 짐작을 머뭇거리며 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 크라소트킨은 불같이 화를 냈다. \"나한테도 개가 있는데, 남의 개를 찾겠다고 온 동네를 쏘다닐 만큼 멍청이는 아니란 말이야! 게다가 핀을 삼킨 개가 살아 있을 거라고 어떻게 상상이나 할 수 있어? 양 떼처럼 질질 짜는 감상, 바로 그게 그거라고!\"\n\n지난 두 주 동안 일류샤는 구석 성상(聖像) 아래의 자그마한 침대를 떠나지 못했다. 알료샤를 만나 그 손가락을 깨문 그날 이후로 그는 학교에 가지 못했다. 바로 그날부터 그는 앓아누웠는데, 그 뒤로 한 달가량은 이따금 일어나 방 안과 복도를 걸어 다닐 수 있기도 했다. 그러나 근래에는 너무 쇠약해져서 아버지의 부축 없이는 꼼짝도 할 수 없게 되었다. 아버지는 그를 두고 무섭도록 애를 태웠다. 그는 술까지 끊었고, 제 아들이 죽을지도 모른다는 공포에 거의 미칠 지경이었다. 그리고 흔히, 특히 아들을 팔에 안다시피 부축해 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 다시 침대에 누인 뒤에는, 복도의 어두운 구석으로 달려가 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 일류샤가 듣지 못하도록 흐느낌을 억누르며 격렬한 통곡의 발작에 몸을 맡기곤 했다.\n\n방으로 돌아오면 그는 으레 그 귀한 아들을 즐겁게 하고 위로하느라 무언가를 하기 시작했다. 옛이야기며 우스운 일화를 들려주거나, 어쩌다 만났던 우스꽝스러운 사람들을 흉내 내고, 심지어 짐승들의 울부짖음과 울음소리까지 흉내 내곤 했다. 그러나 일류샤는 아버지가 어릿광대 짓을 하며 익살을 부리는 꼴을 차마 볼 수 없었다. 아이는 자기가 그것을 싫어한다는 티를 내지 않으려 애썼으나, 아버지가 멸시의 대상이라는 사실을 가슴 저미는 심정으로 알았고, \"삼 부스러기\"와 그 \"끔찍한 날\"의 기억에 끊임없이 시달렸다.\n\n일류샤의 온순하고 다리를 저는 누이 니나도 아버지의 어릿광대 짓을 좋아하지 않았다(바르바라는 얼마 전부터 대학에서 공부하느라 페테르부르크에 가 있었다). 그러나 반쯤 정신이 나간 어머니는 남편이 깡충거리며 뛰어다니거나 무슨 재주를 부리기 시작하면 무척 즐거워하며 깔깔 웃었다. 그것이 그녀를 즐겁게 할 수 있는 유일한 방법이었으니, 그 밖의 시간에는 줄곧 투덜대며 이제는 다들 자기를 잊어버렸다느니, 아무도 자기를 존중해 주지 않는다느니, 자기를 업신여긴다느니 하며 불평을 늘어놓았다. 그러나 지난 며칠 사이 그녀는 완전히 달라졌다. 구석의 일류샤 침대를 줄곧 바라보기 시작했고, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다. 그녀는 말수가 줄고 조용해졌으며, 운다 해도 남이 듣지 못하도록 소리 죽여 울었다. 대위는 그 변화를 침울한 당혹감 속에서 알아차렸다. 아이들의 방문은 처음에는 그녀를 짜증나게 했을 뿐이지만, 나중에는 그들의 명랑한 외침과 이야기가 그녀를 즐겁게 하기 시작했고, 마침내 그녀는 그들을 어찌나 좋아하게 되었던지, 아이들이 오기를 그만두었더라면 그들 없이는 적막했을 것이다. 아이들이 무슨 이야기를 하거나 놀이를 할 때면 그녀는 웃으며 손뼉을 쳤다. 몇몇 아이를 곁으로 불러 입을 맞추기도 했다. 그녀는 특히 스무로프를 귀여워했다.\n\n대위로 말하자면, 일류샤의 기운을 북돋우러 오는 아이들이 자기 방에 모여 있다는 사실이 처음부터 그의 가슴을 황홀한 기쁨으로 가득 채웠다. 그는 일류샤가 이제 그 우울을 떨쳐 버릴 것이고, 그것이 회복을 앞당겨 줄 것이라는 희망까지 품었다. 일류샤에 대한 불안에도 불구하고 그는 근래까지만 해도 아들이 끝내 회복하리라는 것을 단 한순간도 의심하지 않았다.\n\n그는 어린 손님들을 깍듯이 맞이하며 그들의 시중을 정성껏 들었다. 그들의 말이 되어 줄 채비까지 되어 있어 등에 올라타게 하기 시작했으나, 일류샤가 그 놀이를 좋아하지 않아 그만두었다. 그는 아이들에게 줄 자잘한 것들, 생강과자와 호두를 사들이기 시작했고, 차를 따라 주며 샌드위치를 만들어 주었다. 짚어 둘 것은, 이 무렵 그에게는 돈이 넉넉했다는 점이다. 알료샤가 예언했던 그대로 그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 이백 루블을 받았던 것이다. 그리고 그 뒤 카테리나 이바노브나는 그들의 형편과 일류샤의 병에 대해 더 자세히 알게 되자 직접 그들을 찾아와 가족과 가까워졌고, 반쯤 정신이 나간 어머니의 마음을 사로잡는 데 성공했다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 아낌없이 그들을 도왔고, 제 아들이 죽어 갈지도 모른다는 생각에 공포에 질린 대위는 자존심을 버리고 그녀의 도움을 겸허히 받아들였다.\n\n이 무렵 내내, 카테리나 이바노브나가 불러들인 헤르첸슈투베 의사가 하루걸러 한 번씩 어김없이 찾아왔으나, 그의 왕진으로 얻는 것은 별로 없었고, 그는 가차 없이 환자에게 약을 들이부었다. 그런데 그 일요일 아침에는 모스크바에서 온, 그곳에서 명성이 자자한 새 의사가 오기로 되어 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 큰 비용을 들여 모스크바에서 그를 불러들였는데, 일류샤를 위해서가 아니라 다른 목적이 있어서였고, 그 일에 관해서는 적절한 대목에서 따로 더 이야기하기로 하겠다. 그러나 그가 온 김에 그녀가 일류샤도 봐 달라고 청했고, 대위에게는 그를 기다리라는 말이 전해져 있었다. 그는 일류샤가 애타게 그리워하던 그 아이의 방문을 오래도록 바라 왔으면서도, 콜랴 크라소트킨이 온다는 것은 까맣게 모르고 있었다.\n\n크라소트킨이 문을 열고 방으로 들어선 바로 그 순간, 대위와 아이들은 모두 일류샤의 침대를 둘러싸고서, 바로 전날 태어난 자그마한 마스티프 강아지를 들여다보고 있었다. 대위가 그 강아지를 일주일 전에 미리 부탁해 두었던 것은, 잃어버려 십중팔구 죽었을 주치카를 두고 여전히 애를 태우는 일류샤를 위로하고 즐겁게 해 주기 위해서였다. 사흘 전부터 강아지를, 그것도 보통 강아지가 아니라 혈통 있는 마스티프를(물론 이건 아주 중요한 점이었다) 선물 받게 되리라는 말을 들은 일류샤는, 섬세한 마음씨에서 기쁜 척하려 애썼다. 그러나 아버지와 아이들은, 그 강아지가 자기가 죽인 그 가엾은 개의 기억을 도리어 그 어린 가슴에 불러일으킨다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다. 강아지는 그의 곁에 누워 힘없이 꿈틀거렸고, 그는 슬픈 미소를 띠며 여위고 창백하게 메마른 손으로 강아지를 쓰다듬었다. 그가 그 강아지를 좋아하는 것은 분명했으나… 그것은 주치카가 아니었다. 만일 주치카와 그 강아지를 둘 다 가질 수 있었더라면 그는 더없이 행복했을 것이다.\n\n\"크라소트킨이다!\" 한 아이가 갑자기 소리쳤다. 그가 들어오는 것을 맨 먼저 본 것이다.\n\n크라소트킨의 등장은 좌중에 술렁임을 일으켰다. 아이들이 비켜서서 침대 양옆에 늘어서자 그는 일류샤를 똑똑히 볼 수 있게 되었다. 대위가 콜랴를 맞으러 부리나케 달려왔다.\n\n\"어서 들어오게… 잘 왔네!\" 그가 다급하게 말했다. \"일류샤, 크라소트킨 군이 너를 보러 왔구나!\"\n\n그러나 크라소트킨은 그와 황급히 악수를 나누고는, 곧바로 자기가 상류 사회의 예법을 완벽하게 익혔음을 드러냈다. 그는 먼저, 안락의자에 앉은 채 아이들이 자기와 일류샤의 침대 사이를 가로막아 새 강아지를 못 보게 한다고 그 순간 잔뜩 심술이 나 투덜대던 대위 부인에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 더없이 정중하게 그는 한쪽 발을 끌며 그녀에게 절을 했고, 니나에게로 몸을 돌려, 그 자리에 있는 또 한 사람의 부인에게 하듯 똑같이 절을 했다. 이 예의 바른 행동은 그 정신 나간 부인에게 더없이 호의적인 인상을 주었다.\n\n\"저것 보세요, 한눈에 알 수 있잖아요, 잘 자란 도련님이라는 걸.\" 그녀가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 소리 내어 평했다. \"그런데 우리 다른 손님들로 말하자면, 하나가 또 하나 위에 포개 타고 들어온다니까요.\"\n\n\"하나가 또 하나 위에 포개 타고라니, 그게 무슨 말이오, 여보? 그게 어떻게 그렇다는 거요?\" 대위가 다정하게, 그러면서도 그녀를 두고 조금 걱정스러운 듯 웅얼거렸다.\n\n\"바로 그렇게 타고 들어온다니까요. 복도에서 서로 어깨에 올라타서는, 그렇게 점잖은 집안에 깡충깡충 뛰어든다고요. 별스러운 손님들이지!\"\n\n\"하지만 누가 그렇게 들어왔다는 거요, 여보?\"\n\n\"아니, 저 애가 저 애 등에 타고 들어오고, 또 이 애가 저 애 등에 타고 들어왔잖아요.\"\n\n콜랴는 어느새 일류샤의 머리맡에 와 있었다. 병든 아이는 눈에 띄게 창백해졌다. 그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 콜랴를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 콜랴는 두 달 동안 그 어린 친구를 보지 못했던 터라, 그를 보는 순간 망연자실했다. 그렇게 여위고 누렇게 뜬 얼굴, 그렇게 크고 열에 들떠 번들거리는 눈, 그렇게 가느다란 손을 보게 되리라고는 상상조차 못 했던 것이다. 그는 슬픈 놀라움 속에서 일류샤의 가쁘고 거친 숨결과 메마른 입술을 보았다. 그는 가까이 다가서서 손을 내밀었고, 거의 압도된 채 말했다.\n\n\"음, 친구… 좀 어때?\" 그러나 목소리가 잠겨 버려, 그는 태연한 척하지 못했다. 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 씰룩거렸고 입가가 떨렸다. 일류샤는 여전히 한마디도 못 한 채 가엾고 자그마한 미소를 지었다. 무언가에 이끌려 콜랴는 손을 들어 일류샤의 머리카락을 쓸어 주었다.\n\n\"괜찮아!\" 그는 그를 위로하려고, 아니 어쩌면 왜 그런 말을 하는지도 모른 채, 그에게 나직이 속삭였다. 잠시 그들은 다시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"어이, 새 강아지를 얻었구나?\" 콜랴가 더없이 무심한 목소리로 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"으응.\" 일류샤가 숨을 헐떡이며 길게 끄는 속삭임으로 대답했다.\n\n\"코가 검은 걸 보니 사납겠는걸, 좋은 집 지키는 개가 되겠어.\" 콜랴가, 마치 자기가 신경 쓰는 것이라곤 오직 그 강아지와 그 검은 코뿐이라는 듯, 진지하고 무덤덤하게 한마디 했다. 그러나 사실 그는 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 여전히 안간힘을 다해 감정을 억눌러야 했고, 아무리 애써도 그것을 다스릴 수가 없었다. \"이놈이 다 자라면 사슬에 매어 둬야 할걸, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"엄청나게 큰 개가 될 거예요!\" 한 아이가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"당연하지.\" \"마스티프인걸.\" \"큼지막하게.\" \"이만큼.\" \"송아지만큼 크게.\" 여러 목소리가 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"송아지만큼, 진짜 송아지만큼 크게요.\" 대위가 맞장구를 쳤다. \"내가 일부러 그런 놈을 골랐지요, 가장 사나운 종으로 말입니다. 어미 아비가 다 어마어마하게 크고 무척 사납거든요, 바닥에서 이만큼이나 높이 선다니까요… 여기 앉게, 일류샤 침대에 앉든지, 아니면 여기 의자에 앉게. 잘 왔네, 오래도록 자네가 오기를 바라고 있었지… 알렉세이 표도로비치와 함께 와 줬구먼, 고맙기도 하지?\"\n\n크라소트킨은 일류샤의 발치, 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다. 오는 길에 어쩌면 대화를 트는 격의 없는 말머리를 준비해 두었을지도 모르나, 이제 그는 그 실마리를 완전히 놓쳐 버렸다.\n\n\"아뇨… 저는 페레즈본하고 같이 왔어요. 제게 이제 개가 한 마리 있는데, 페레즈본이라고 불러요. 슬라브식 이름이죠. 저 밖에 있어요… 제가 휘파람을 불면 달려 들어올 거예요. 저도 개를 한 마리 데려왔거든요.\" 그가 별안간 일류샤를 향해 말했다. \"주치카 기억나니, 친구야?\" 그가 불쑥 그 질문을 그에게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n일류샤의 자그마한 얼굴이 떨렸다. 그는 괴로움이 가득한 표정으로 콜랴를 바라보았다. 문가에 서 있던 알료샤가 눈살을 찌푸리며 주치카 이야기는 하지 말라고 콜랴에게 신호를 보냈으나, 그는 알아차리지 못했거나 알아차리려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"주치카는… 어디 있어?\" 일류샤가 끊어지는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그게 말이지, 친구야, 네 주치카는 없어졌고 끝장나 버렸어!\"\n\n일류샤는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 다시 한번 콜랴에게 뚫어질 듯한 눈길을 못 박았다. 알료샤가 콜랴의 눈길을 붙잡아 또 한 번 힘주어 신호를 보냈으나, 그는 알아채지 못한 척 눈길을 돌려 버렸다.\n\n\"어딘가로 달아나서 죽었을 거야. 그런 걸 삼켰으니 죽었겠지.\" 콜랴가 가차 없이 단언했는데, 다만 숨이 조금 가빠 보였다. \"하지만 나한테는 개가 있어, 페레즈본이라고… 슬라브식 이름이지… 너한테 보여 주려고 데려왔어.\"\n\n\"보기 싫어!\" 일류샤가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 정말로 한번 봐야 해… 재미있을 거야. 일부러 데려온 거란 말이야… 똑같이 털북숭이 개거든… 제 개를 불러들여도 될까요, 부인?\" 그가 갑자기, 까닭 모를 흥분이 어린 태도로 스네기료프 부인을 향해 말했다.\n\n\"보기 싫어, 보기 싫단 말이야!\" 일류샤가 목소리에 구슬픈 떨림을 담아 외쳤다. 그의 눈에는 원망의 빛이 어렸다.\n\n\"그러는 게 좋겠어.\" 대위가 방금 걸터앉았던 벽가의 궤짝에서 벌떡 일어났다. \"그러는 게 좋겠네… 다음에 하지.\" 그가 웅얼거렸으나, 콜랴는 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다. 그는 황급히 스무로프에게 \"문 열어!\" 하고 소리쳤고, 문이 열리자마자 휘파람을 불었다. 페레즈본이 쏜살같이 방 안으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"뛰어, 페레즈본, 빌어! 빌어!\" 콜랴가 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤고, 개는 일류샤의 머리맡에서 뒷다리로 곧추섰다. 그다음에 벌어진 일은 모두에게 뜻밖이었다. 일류샤가 흠칫하더니 격렬하게 앞으로 휘청 쏠려 페레즈본 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 숨이 멎을 듯한 긴장 속에서 그를 응시했다.\n\n\"이건… 주치카야!\" 그가 기쁨과 고통으로 갈라지는 목소리로 별안간 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 누구인 줄 알았어?\" 크라소트킨이 낭랑하고 행복에 겨운 목소리로 있는 힘껏 외치고는, 몸을 굽혀 개를 붙잡아 일류샤에게로 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"봐, 친구야, 봐, 한쪽 눈이 멀었고 왼쪽 귀가 찢어졌지, 네가 나한테 말해 준 그 자국 그대로야. 바로 그걸로 찾아낸 거야. 단번에 찾아냈지. 임자 없는 개였어!\" 그가 대위에게로, 그 부인에게로, 알료샤에게로, 그러고는 다시 일류샤에게로 잽싸게 몸을 돌리며 설명했다. \"이놈은 페도토프네 뒤뜰에 살고 있었어. 거기 자리를 잡고 살긴 했어도 먹이를 주진 않더라고. 마을에서 도망쳐 나온 들개였던 거야… 내가 찾아냈지… 봐, 친구야, 네가 준 걸 이놈이 삼켰을 리가 없어. 삼켰다면 죽었을 거야, 틀림없이 죽었겠지! 그러니 살아 있는 걸 보면 도로 뱉어 낸 게 분명해. 네가 그걸 못 봤을 뿐이지. 핀이 혀를 찔러서 깽깽거린 거고. 깽깽거리며 달아났으니까 네가 삼킨 줄로 알았던 거야. 깽깽거릴 만도 하지, 개 입 속 살갗이 얼마나 여린데… 사람보다 여려, 훨씬 더 여리다고!\" 콜랴가 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환하게 빛나며 격하게 외쳐 댔다. 일류샤는 말을 할 수가 없었다. 백지장처럼 하얘진 얼굴로, 거의 튀어나올 듯한 커다란 눈을 한 채 입을 벌리고 콜랴를 응시했다. 그리고 만일 그것을 까맣게 모르던 크라소트킨이, 그런 순간이 병든 아이의 건강에 얼마나 파멸적이고 치명적인 결과를 미칠 수 있는지 알았더라면, 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 그런 장난을 치지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그 방에서 그것을 깨달은 사람은 어쩌면 알료샤 한 사람뿐이었을 것이다. 대위로 말하자면 그는 어린아이처럼 굴었다.\n\n\"주치카야! 주치카라고!\" 그가 더없이 행복한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"일류샤, 이게 주치카야, 네 주치카라고! 여보, 이게 주치카요!\" 그는 거의 울먹였다.\n\n\"난 짐작도 못 했어!\" 스무로프가 분하다는 듯 소리쳤다. \"잘했다, 크라소트킨! 내가 그랬잖아, 쟤가 개를 찾아낼 거라고, 그런데 정말 찾아냈어.\"\n\n\"정말 찾아냈어!\" 다른 아이가 신이 나서 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"크라소트킨은 멋진 녀석이야!\" 또 다른 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"멋진 녀석이야, 멋진 녀석!\" 다른 아이들도 외치며 손뼉을 치기 시작했다.\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐.\" 크라소트킨이 그들 모두를 누르고 외치려 안간힘을 썼다. \"어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄게, 그게 바로 핵심이야. 내가 이놈을 찾아내서 집으로 데려와 곧장 숨겨 뒀어. 집에 가둬 두고 오늘까지는 아무한테도 안 보여 줬지. 지난 두 주 동안 스무로프만 알고 있었지만, 이 개는 페레즈본이라는 놈이라고 둘러댔더니 짐작도 못 하더라고. 그러는 사이에 나는 이놈한테 온갖 재주를 가르쳤어. 이놈이 할 줄 아는 걸 죄다 봐야 하는데! 잘 길들여진, 상태 좋은 개를 너한테 데려다주려고 훈련시킨 거야, 친구야, '봐, 친구야, 네 주치카가 이제 얼마나 훌륭한 개가 됐는지!' 하고 너한테 말할 수 있게 말이야! 고기 한 점 없어요? 배꼽 빠지게 웃을 재주를 보여 줄 텐데. 고기 한 조각, 없어요?\"\n\n대위가 음식 만드는 곳인 주인 여자네로 복도를 가로질러 달려갔다. 귀한 시간을 허비하지 않으려고 콜랴는 다급하기 짝이 없게 페레즈본에게 \"죽어!\" 하고 외쳤다. 그러자 개는 즉시 몸을 굴려 네 발을 허공으로 쳐든 채 벌렁 드러누웠다. 아이들이 웃었다. 일류샤는 여전히 고통스러운 그 미소를 띤 채 바라보았는데, 개의 재주에 누구보다 기뻐한 사람은 \"엄마\"였다. 그녀는 개를 보며 웃다가 손가락을 딱딱 튀기며 \"페레즈본, 페레즈본!\" 하고 부르기 시작했다.\n\n\"무슨 짓을 해도 이놈은 일어나지 않아요, 절대로!\" 콜랴가 제 성공이 자랑스러워 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"세상 누가 아무리 소리쳐도 꿈쩍 안 하지만, 제가 부르면 단박에 벌떡 일어날 거예요. 이리 와, 페레즈본!\" 개가 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 기뻐서 낑낑대며 이리저리 뛰어다녔다. 대위가 구운 쇠고기 한 점을 들고 달려 돌아왔다.\n\n\"뜨거운가요?\" 콜랴가 고기를 받으며, 일을 다루는 듯한 태도로 다급히 물었다. \"개는 뜨거운 걸 싫어하거든요. 아뇨, 괜찮네요. 다들 봐요, 봐, 일류샤, 봐, 친구야. 왜 안 보고 있어? 모처럼 데려왔는데 얘가 이놈을 안 보네.\"\n\n새 재주란, 개가 코를 쭉 내민 채 꼼짝 않고 서 있게 하고는 그 코끝에 먹음직스러운 고기 한 조각을 올려놓는 것이었다. 가엾은 개는 코에 고기를 얹은 채 꼼짝도 않고, 주인이 붙잡아 두려는 동안 내내 미동도 없이 서 있어야 했으니, 어쩌면 반 시간이라도 그래야 했다. 그러나 그는 페레즈본을 잠깐만 그렇게 붙잡아 두었다.\n\n\"받아!\" 콜랴가 외치자, 고기가 눈 깜짝할 사이에 개의 코에서 입으로 옮겨 갔다. 좌중은 물론 열광과 감탄을 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"정말로 그동안 안 온 게 단지 개를 훈련시키느라 그런 거니?\" 알료샤가 저도 모르게 책망의 가락을 목소리에 담아 소리쳤다.\n\n\"오직 그것 때문이었죠!\" 콜랴가 더없이 천연덕스럽게 대답했다. \"이놈을 영광스러운 모습으로 보여 주고 싶었거든요.\"\n\n\"페레즈본! 페레즈본.\" 일류샤가 가느다란 손가락을 딱딱 튀기며 개를 손짓해 부르며 별안간 불렀다.\n\n\"왜 그래? 이놈을 침대 위로 올라오게 해! 이리 와, 페레즈본!\" 콜랴가 침대를 탁탁 치자 페레즈본이 일류샤 곁으로 쏜살같이 뛰어올랐다. 아이가 두 팔로 개의 머리를 끌어안자 페레즈본은 즉시 그의 뺨을 핥았다. 일류샤는 개에게 바싹 다가들어 침대에 길게 몸을 뻗고는 개의 털북숭이 몸에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n\"아이고, 아이고!\" 대위가 연신 탄성을 질렀다. 콜랴는 다시 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다.\n\n\"일류샤, 다른 재주를 하나 더 보여 줄게. 너한테 줄 자그마한 대포를 가져왔어. 기억하지, 전에 내가 이 얘기를 했더니 네가 그걸 무척 보고 싶다고 했잖아. 자, 여기, 너한테 가져왔어.\"\n\n그러고서 콜랴는 가방에서 황급히 그 자그마한 청동 대포를 꺼냈다. 자기 스스로가 행복했기에 서두른 것이다. 다른 때 같았으면 페레즈본이 일으킨 흥분이 가라앉을 때까지 기다렸을 테지만, 이제 그는 아무것도 따지지 않고 서둘렀다. \"다들 이제 행복하니까,\" 그는 느꼈다, \"자, 여기 더 행복하게 해 줄 게 있다고!\" 그 자신이 더없이 황홀해 있었다.\n\n\"이걸 오래도록 탐내 왔는데, 이건 네 거야, 친구야, 네 거라고. 이건 모로조프 거였는데, 걔한테는 쓸모가 없었지, 제 형한테서 받은 거거든. 내가 이걸 얻으려고 아버지 책장에서 책 한 권을 바꿨어. 『마호메트의 친척, 또는 이로운 어리석음』이라고, 백 년 전 모스크바에서, 검열이라는 게 있기도 전에 나온 망측한 책이지. 그런데 모로조프는 그런 걸 좋아하거든. 걔도 나한테 고마워했어….\"\n\n콜랴는 모두가 보고 감탄할 수 있도록 대포를 손에 받쳐 들었다. 일류샤가 몸을 일으켰고, 여전히 오른팔로는 개를 끌어안은 채, 그 장난감을 황홀하게 바라보았다. 콜랴가 화약도 가져왔다고, 그래서 \"부인들이 놀라지 않으신다면\" 당장 쏘아 볼 수도 있다고 밝히자 흥분은 더욱 커졌다. \"엄마\"가 곧장 그 장난감을 더 가까이서 보겠다고 청했고, 그 청은 받아들여졌다. 그녀는 바퀴 달린 그 자그마한 청동 대포가 무척 마음에 들어, 그것을 제 무릎 위에서 앞뒤로 굴리기 시작했다. 그녀는 자기가 무슨 부탁을 받았는지도 모른 채 선뜻 대포를 쏘아도 좋다고 허락했다. 콜랴가 화약과 탄알을 보여 주었다. 군인이었던 대위가 화약을 아주 조금 넣어 장전하는 일을 맡았다. 그는 탄알 쏘는 일은 다음으로 미루자고 청했다. 대포를 방의 빈 쪽을 겨누어 바닥에 놓고, 화약 세 알을 점화 구멍에 밀어 넣은 다음 거기에 불을 댔다. 굉장한 폭발이 뒤따랐다. 엄마가 흠칫 놀랐으나 곧 기뻐서 깔깔 웃었다. 아이들은 말없는 환희 속에서 바라보았다. 그러나 일류샤를 바라보는 대위가 그들 누구보다 더 황홀해했다. 콜랴는 대포를 집어 들어, 화약과 탄알과 함께 즉시 일류샤에게 선사했다.\n\n\"너 주려고 얻은 거야, 너 주려고! 너 주려고 오래도록 간직해 뒀어.\" 그가 기쁨에 겨워 다시 한번 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그걸 나 줘! 아니, 그 대포를 나한테 줘!\" 엄마가 어린아이처럼 조르기 시작했다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 그것을 못 얻을까 봐 애처로운 두려움이 떠올라 있었다. 콜랴는 당황했다. 대위는 안절부절못하며 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다.\n\n\"여보, 여보.\" 그가 그녀에게로 달려갔다. \"물론 그 대포는 당신 거요, 하지만 일류샤가 갖게 합시다, 그건 그 애한테 준 선물이니까. 그래도 당신 거나 마찬가지요. 일류샤가 언제든 당신이 갖고 놀게 해 줄 테니까. 그건 두 사람 다의 것이 되는 거요, 두 사람 다의.\"\n\n\"싫어요, 난 두 사람 거가 되는 건 싫어요, 통째로 내 것, 일류샤 게 아니라 내 것이었으면 좋겠어요.\" 엄마가 금세 울음을 터뜨릴 듯이 고집을 부렸다.\n\n\"가지세요, 어머니, 자, 가지세요!\" 일류샤가 외쳤다. \"크라소트킨, 이걸 어머니께 드려도 될까?\" 그는 마치 제가 받은 선물을 다른 사람에게 줘서 그가 기분 상하지나 않을까 두려운 듯, 애원하는 얼굴로 크라소트킨을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"물론 되고말고.\" 크라소트킨이 흔쾌히 응했고, 일류샤에게서 대포를 받아 정중히 절을 하며 그것을 직접 엄마에게 건넸다. 그녀는 어찌나 감동했던지 울었다.\n\n\"일류샤, 우리 아가, 이 애야말로 제 엄마를 사랑하는 애구나!\" 그녀가 다정하게 말하고는, 곧 다시 대포를 제 무릎 위에서 앞뒤로 굴리기 시작했다.\n\n\"여보, 당신 손에 입 맞추게 해 주오.\" 대위가 즉시 그녀에게로 달려가 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"그리고 이 착한 도련님처럼 사랑스러운 사람은 본 적이 없어요.\" 고마움에 찬 부인이 크라소트킨을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 화약도 얼마든지 가져다줄게, 일류샤. 이제 화약은 우리가 직접 만들거든. 보로비코프가 만드는 법을 알아냈어. 초석 스물네 몫에 황 열 몫, 그리고 자작나무 숯 여섯 몫. 그걸 다 함께 빻아서 물에 섞어 반죽으로 만든 다음, 고운 체로 걸러 내는 거야. 그렇게 만드는 거지.\"\n\n\"화약 얘기는 스무로프한테 들었어, 그런데 아버지는 그게 진짜 화약이 아니래.\" 일류샤가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"진짜가 아니라고?\" 콜랴가 발끈했다. \"타기는 잘 타는데. 뭐, 나도 잘은 모르지만.\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 뜻이 아니었네.\" 대위가 면구스러운 얼굴로 끼어들었다. \"다만 진짜 화약은 그렇게 만드는 게 아니라는 말이었지, 하지만 그건 별것 아니야, 그렇게 만들어도 되고말고.\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요, 어른께서 더 잘 아시겠죠. 우리가 포마드 통에다 좀 불을 붙여 봤더니 멋지게 타더라고요, 아주 자그마한 재만 남기고 다 타 버렸어요. 하지만 그건 반죽 그대로였고, 그걸 체에 걸러 내면… 뭐 어쨌든 어른께서 더 잘 아시겠죠, 저야 모르고요… 그리고 불킨은 우리 화약 때문에 아버지한테 두들겨 맞았어, 그거 들었어?\" 그가 일류샤를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"응.\" 일류샤가 대답했다. 그는 콜랴의 이야기를 엄청난 흥미와 즐거움 속에서 들었다.\n\n\"우리가 한 병 가득 만들어 놨었는데, 걔가 그걸 침대 밑에 둬 두곤 했거든. 걔 아버지가 그걸 본 거야. 그게 터질지도 모른다면서 그 자리에서 두들겨 팼지. 나를 선생님들한테 일러바치겠다고 하더라고. 이제 걔는 나하고 어울리는 게 금지됐어, 이제 아무도 나하고 못 어울리게 돼 버렸지. 스무로프도 안 되고, 다들 나를 나쁜 놈으로 여기거든. 내가 '막돼먹은 녀석'이라는 거야.\" 콜랴가 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃었다. \"이게 다 그 철길 사건에서 시작된 거지.\"\n\n\"아, 우리도 그 무용담을 들었네.\" 대위가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 선로 위에 가만히 누워 있을 수가 있었나? 정말 조금도 안 무서웠단 말인가, 기차 밑에 그렇게 누워 있으면서? 겁나지 않았나?\"\n\n대위는 콜랴를 추어올리는 데 비굴할 지경이었다.\n\n\"그…그렇게까지 무섭진 않았어요.\" 콜랴가 무심하게 대답했다. \"여기서 무엇보다 제 평판을 망쳐 놓은 건 그 빌어먹을 거위 사건이었죠.\" 그가 다시 일류샤를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. 그러나 이야기하는 동안 짐짓 무심한 태도를 꾸미면서도 그는 여전히 자제하지 못해, 유지하려던 가락을 끊임없이 놓치곤 했다.\n\n\"아! 그 거위 얘기는 들었어!\" 일류샤가 온 얼굴에 환한 웃음을 띠며 웃었다. \"사람들이 얘기해 줬는데, 난 잘 못 알아들었어. 정말로 너를 법정에까지 데려갔어?\"\n\n\"세상에서 제일 멍청하고 하찮은 일을, 그치들이 늘 하듯이 침소봉대한 거지.\" 콜랴가 무심하게 운을 뗐다. \"어느 날 여기 장터를 지나가는데, 마침 거위들을 몰아넣었을 때였어. 내가 걸음을 멈추고 거위들을 보고 있었지. 그런데 별안간 지금 플로트니코프네 심부름꾼으로 있는 어떤 녀석이 나를 보더니, '뭣 하러 거위를 보고 있냐?' 하더라고. 내가 그 녀석을 봤지. 멍청하고 둥근 달덩이 같은 얼굴의 스무 살짜리였어. 나는 늘 농민 편이거든, 알다시피. 농민들하고 얘기하는 걸 좋아해… 우리는 농민들한테 뒤떨어져 있어, 그건 자명한 이치지. 너 웃는 것 같은데, 카라마조프?\"\n\n\"아냐, 천만에, 듣고 있어.\" 알료샤가 더없이 선량한 태도로 말했고, 예민한 콜랴는 즉시 안심했다.\n\n\"내 이론은, 카라마조프, 명료하고 단순해.\" 그가 흡족한 표정으로 다시 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"나는 민중을 믿고, 늘 그들에게 마땅한 몫을 주고 싶지만, 그렇다고 그들을 응석받이로 만드는 건 반대야, 그건 절대적인 전제 조건이지… 그런데 거위 얘기를 하고 있었지. 그래서 내가 그 바보한테 몸을 돌리고 대답했어. '저 거위가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 궁금해서.' 그 녀석이 아주 멍청하게 나를 보더니, '거위가 무슨 생각을 한다고요?' 하고 묻더라고. '저기 귀리가 가득 실린 짐수레 보이지?' 하고 내가 말했지. '귀리가 자루에서 떨어지고 있는데, 거위가 그걸 주워 먹으려고 바퀴 바로 밑에다 목을 들이밀고 있잖아, 보여?' '잘 보이는데요.' 하더라고. '그럼,' 하고 내가 말했어, '저 짐수레가 조금만 앞으로 움직이면 거위 목이 부러질까, 안 부러질까?' '틀림없이 부러지죠.' 하면서 그 녀석이 무척 신이 나서 얼굴 가득 히죽거리는 거야. '그럼 가서,' 하고 내가 말했어, '한번 해 보자.' '해 봅시다.' 하더라고. 그러고는 채비하는 데 오래 걸리지도 않았어. 그 녀석이 눈에 안 띄게 고삐 곁에 서고, 나는 한쪽에 서서 거위를 몰아갔지. 그런데 주인은 안 보고 있었어, 누군가하고 얘기를 하고 있었거든, 그러니 나는 할 게 없었어, 거위가 제 발로 귀리를 좇아 짐수레 밑, 바퀴 바로 밑으로 머리를 들이밀더라고. 내가 그 녀석한테 눈짓을 했더니, 녀석이 고삐를 홱 잡아당겼고, 우두둑. 거위 목이 두 동강 났지. 그런데 운수 사납게도, 바로 그 순간 농민들이 죄다 우리를 봤고 당장 한바탕 난리가 났어. '일부러 그랬지!' '아뇨, 일부러가 아니에요.' '아냐, 일부러 그랬어!' 그래서 그치들이 외쳐 댔지, '저놈을 치안 판사한테 끌고 가!' 나도 끌고 갔어. '너도 거기 있었잖아,' 하면서, '네가 거들었어, 너야 온 장터가 다 아는 놈이지!' 그런데 무슨 영문인지 나는 정말로 온 장터에 다 알려져 있거든.\" 콜랴가 으스대며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 다들 치안 판사한테 갔고, 거위도 가져갔지. 그 녀석은 잔뜩 겁에 질려 울고 있었어, 그냥 여편네처럼 엉엉 울더라고. 그리고 그 농사꾼은 그런 식으로 거위를 얼마든지 죽일 수 있겠다고 줄곧 소리쳤지. 뭐, 물론 증인들도 있었고. 치안 판사가 단박에 처결했어, 농사꾼한테 거위 값으로 일 루블을 물어 주고, 거위는 그 녀석이 갖는 걸로. 그리고 다시는 그런 장난을 치지 말라고 주의를 줬지. 그런데 그 녀석은 여편네처럼 계속 엉엉 우는 거야. '제가 한 게 아니에요,' 하면서, '저 애가 부추긴 거예요.' 하고 나를 가리키더라고. 나는 더없이 침착하게, 내가 부추긴 게 아니라 그저 일반적인 명제를 말했을 뿐이라고, 가정해서 말한 것뿐이라고 대답했어. 치안 판사가 빙긋 웃더니, 웃었다는 게 즉시 분해진 모양이더라고. '앞으로는 책상 앞에 앉아 책을 보고 공부나 할 것이지, 그런 일반적인 명제 따위에 시간을 허비하지 못하도록 너희 선생님들한테 일러바치겠다.' 선생님들한테 일러바치진 않았어, 그건 농담이었지, 하지만 그 일이 소문나서 선생님들 귀에까지 들어갔어. 그분들 귀는 길거든, 알다시피! 고전어 선생인 콜바스니코프가 특히 그 일에 펄펄 뛰었는데, 다르다넬로프가 또 나를 구해 줬지. 그런데 콜바스니코프는 요즘 어디서 풀이 죽은 당나귀처럼 모두한테 사납게 군다니까. 너 알아, 일류샤, 그 사람이 얼마 전에 결혼했는데, 천 루블 지참금을 받았다지만, 신부가 일등급으로 추하고 최하등으로 못생긴 진짜 흉물이래. 삼 학년 애들이 거기에 풍자시를 지었어.\n\n놀라운 소식이 학급에 닿았네,\n콜바스니코프가 당나귀 됐다네.\n\n이런 식으로, 무척 우스워, 나중에 가져다줄게. 다르다넬로프를 두고는 험담할 게 없어, 그분은 학식 있는 사람이야, 그건 의심할 여지가 없지. 나는 그런 사람들을 존경해, 그분이 나를 변호해 줘서가 아니라.\"\n\n\"하지만 형은 트로이를 세운 사람들 문제로 그 사람을 코 납작하게 만들었잖아!\" 스무로프가 별안간 끼어들었는데, 그런 순간 크라소트킨이 자랑스럽기 짝이 없는 기색이 역력했다. 그는 거위 이야기가 특히 마음에 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"정말로 그를 코 납작하게 만들었나?\" 대위가 추어올리듯 물었다. \"누가 트로이를 세웠느냐는 문제로 말이지? 우리도 그 얘긴 들었네, 그때 일류샤가 내게 얘기해 줬거든.\"\n\n\"이 형은 뭐든지 다 알아요, 아버지, 우리 중 누구보다 많이 안다고요!\" 일류샤가 끼어들었다. \"겉으로만 저런 척하지, 사실은 무슨 과목이든 일등이에요….\"\n\n일류샤는 그지없는 행복에 찬 눈길로 콜랴를 바라보았다.\n\n\"오, 트로이 얘기는 다 시시한 거야, 하찮은 일이지. 나는 그걸 대수롭지 않은 문제로 여겨.\" 콜랴가 거만한 겸손을 담아 말했다. 이제 그는 위엄을 완전히 되찾았으나, 그래도 여전히 조금은 마음이 편치 않았다. 자기가 무척 흥분해 있다는 것을, 가령 거위 이야기를 너무 거리낌 없이 떠벌렸다는 것을 느꼈는데, 그동안 알료샤는 시종 진지한 얼굴로 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다. 그러자 그 허영심 많은 아이는 차츰, 알료샤가 잠자코 있는 것은 자기를 경멸하기 때문이며, 자기가 그 앞에서 잘난 척한다고 여기는 게 아닐까 하는 쓰라린 두려움을 품기 시작했다. 만일 알료샤가 감히 그런 생각을 한다면 콜랴는—\n\n\"나는 그 문제를 아주 하찮은 거로 본다고.\" 그가 거만하게 다시 한번 딱 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 누가 트로이를 세웠는지 알아.\" 그때껏 한마디도 하지 않던 한 아이가 모두를 놀라게 하며 별안간 말했다. 그는 말이 없었고 수줍어하는 듯 보였다. 카르타쇼프라는 열한 살가량의 예쁘장한 아이로, 문 가까이에 앉아 있었다. 콜랴가 위엄 어린 놀라움으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n사실 트로이를 세운 사람들이 누구냐 하는 것은 온 학교에 비밀이 되어 있었으니, 그 비밀은 오로지 스마라그도프를 읽어야만 알아낼 수 있는 것이었고, 콜랴 말고는 아무도 스마라그도프를 갖고 있지 않았다. 어느 날 콜랴가 등을 돌리고 있을 때, 카르타쇼프가 콜랴의 책들 틈에 놓인 스마라그도프를 잽싸게 펼쳤고, 곧장 트로이 건국에 관한 대목을 짚어 냈던 것이다. 이것은 한참 전의 일이었으나, 그는 무슨 일이 벌어질지, 또 크라소트킨이 어떻게든 그 일로 자기를 망신 주지나 않을지 두려워, 자기도 누가 트로이를 세웠는지 안다고 차마 공공연히 밝히지 못한 채 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 이제 그는 그 말을 참을 수가 없었다. 몇 주 동안이나 그 말을 하고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 누가 세웠는데?\" 콜랴가 거만한 오만함을 담아 그를 향해 돌아서며 물었다. 그는 그 아이의 얼굴에서 그가 정말로 알고 있다는 것을 알아채고는 즉시 어떻게 받아넘길지 마음을 정했다. 말하자면 전체의 화음에 불협화음 한 가락이 끼어든 셈이었다.\n\n\"트로이는 테우케르, 다르다노스, 일로스, 트로스가 세웠어.\" 그 아이가 단번에 딱 잘라 말하고는, 그 순간 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 어찌나 붉혔던지 보기에 딱할 지경이었다. 그러나 아이들은 그를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 꼬박 일 분 동안 그를 쳐다보다가, 이윽고 그 응시하던 눈들이 일제히 콜랴에게로 쏠려 못 박혔으니, 콜랴는 여전히 그 당돌한 아이를 멸시 어린 침착함으로 훑어보고 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻으로 그들이 세웠다는 거지?\" 마침내 그가 한마디 던지는 영광을 베풀었다. \"그리고 도시나 국가를 세운다는 건 무슨 뜻이야? 그 사람들이 뭘 했다는 건데? 한 사람씩 가서 벽돌을 한 장씩 놓았다, 그렇게 생각하는 거야?\"\n\n웃음이 터졌다. 무안을 당한 아이는 분홍빛에서 진홍빛으로 변했다. 그는 입을 다문 채 금세 울음을 터뜨릴 듯했다. 콜랴는 잠시 그를 그대로 붙들어 두었다.\n\n\"한 민족의 건국 같은 역사적 사건을 입에 올리려면, 우선 그게 무슨 뜻인지부터 알아야 해.\" 그가 준엄하고 날카로운 어조로 그를 타일렀다. \"하지만 나는 이런 노파들의 옛이야기 따위엔 아무 의미도 두지 않고, 세계사라는 것 전반을 대수롭잖게 여겨.\" 그가 좌중 전체를 향해 무심하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"세계사를요?\" 대위가 거의 겁먹은 표정으로 물었다.\n\n\"네, 세계사요! 그건 인류가 차례로 저질러 온 어리석은 짓들에 대한 연구일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요. 제가 존중하는 학문은 오직 수학과 자연 과학뿐이죠.\" 콜랴가 말했다. 그는 잘난 척하면서 알료샤를 흘끔 곁눈질했다. 그곳에서 그가 두려워하는 의견은 오직 알료샤의 것뿐이었다. 그러나 알료샤는 여전히 잠자코, 여전히 전처럼 진지했다. 알료샤가 한마디만 했더라도 그를 멈추게 했을 텐데, 알료샤는 입을 다물고 있었고 \"그건 경멸의 침묵일지도 몰라\" 싶었으니, 그것이 끝내 콜랴를 자극했다.\n\n\"고전어들도 그래요… 그건 그저 미친 짓일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요. 또 제 말에 동의하지 않으시는 것 같은데, 카라마조프?\"\n\n\"동의하지 않아.\" 알료샤가 엷은 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"고전어 공부란, 제 의견을 묻는다면, 그저 경찰 행정상의 방책일 뿐이고, 그게 바로 그게 우리 학교에 도입된 까닭이죠.\" 차츰 콜랴가 다시 숨이 가빠지기 시작했다. \"라틴어와 그리스어가 도입된 건 그것이 따분하고 지력을 마비시키기 때문이에요. 전부터 지루했으니, 더 지루하게 만들려고 무슨 수를 쓸 수 있었겠어요? 전부터 충분히 무의미했으니, 더 무의미하게 만들려고 무슨 수를 쓸 수 있었겠냐고요? 그래서 그리스어와 라틴어를 생각해 낸 거죠. 그게 제 의견이에요, 부디 이 생각을 절대 바꾸지 않았으면 좋겠어요.\" 콜랴가 불쑥 말을 맺었다. 그의 두 뺨은 발갛게 달아올라 있었다.\n\n\"맞는 말이야.\" 스무로프가 별안간 낭랑하고 확신에 찬 어조로 맞장구쳤다. 그는 주의 깊게 듣고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그런데 형은 자기가 라틴어에서 일등이잖아.\" 아이들 무리 가운데 하나가 별안간 외쳤다.\n\n\"맞아요, 아버지, 형은 그렇게 말하면서도 라틴어에서 일등이라고요.\" 일류샤가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"그게 어쨌다는 거야?\" 콜랴가 변명해야겠다고 여겼는데, 그 칭찬이 그에게는 무척 달콤하기는 했다. \"내가 라틴어에 죽어라 매달리는 건 그래야만 하기 때문이야, 어머니한테 시험에 합격하겠다고 약속했거든, 그리고 나는 무슨 일을 하든 제대로 할 만한 값어치가 있다고 생각하니까. 하지만 마음속으로는 고전어니 그 사기극이니 하는 것들을 깊이 경멸하고 있어… 동의하지 않아, 카라마조프?\"\n\n\"왜 '사기극'이야?\" 알료샤가 다시 미소 지었다.\n\n\"아니, 고전 작가들은 죄다 온갖 언어로 번역돼 있잖아, 그러니 고전을 공부하자고 라틴어를 도입한 게 아니라, 오로지 경찰 행정상의 방책으로, 지력을 마비시키려고 도입한 거지. 그러니 그걸 사기극 말고 뭐라고 부를 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 건 다 누가 가르쳐 줬어?\" 알료샤가 끝내 놀라며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"첫째로 나는 누가 가르쳐 주지 않아도 스스로 생각할 줄 알아. 게다가 방금 고전이 번역돼 있다고 한 말은, 우리 선생 콜바스니코프가 삼 학년 전체한테 한 얘기야.\"\n\n\"의사 선생님이 오셨어요!\" 그때껏 잠자코 있던 니나가 외쳤다.\n\n호흘라코바 부인의 마차 한 대가 대문 앞으로 다가왔다. 아침 내내 의사를 기다리던 대위가 그를 맞으러 부리나케 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. \"엄마\"는 정신을 가다듬고 위엄 있는 태도를 꾸몄다. 알료샤는 일류샤에게 다가가 베개를 바로 놓아 주기 시작했다. 니나는 환자용 의자에 앉은 채, 그가 침대를 정돈하는 것을 근심스레 지켜보았다. 아이들은 황급히 작별을 고했다. 그중 몇몇은 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 약속했다. 콜랴가 페레즈본을 부르자 개가 침대에서 뛰어내렸다.\n\n\"나는 안 갈게, 안 가.\" 콜랴가 일류샤에게 다급히 말했다. \"복도에서 기다리다가 의사 선생님이 가시면 다시 올게, 페레즈본하고 같이 돌아올게.\"\n\n그러나 그사이에 의사가 들어와 있었는데, 길고 검은 구레나룻과 윤이 나도록 면도한 턱을 한, 곰가죽 외투를 걸친 거드름 피우는 인물이었다. 그는 문지방을 넘어서자 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 아마도 자기가 엉뚱한 곳에 온 줄로 여긴 모양이었다. \"이게 어찌 된 일이지? 여기가 어디야?\" 그가 외투도 끝이 뾰족한 물개가죽 모자도 벗지 않은 채 웅얼거렸다. 북적이는 사람들이며, 방의 궁색함이며, 구석에 줄을 매고 널어놓은 빨래가 그를 어리둥절하게 했다. 대위는 허리를 한껏 굽힌 채 그 앞에서 굽실거리며 절을 했다.\n\n\"여깁니다, 선생님, 여기예요, 선생님.\" 그가 굽실거리며 웅얼거렸다. \"여기가 맞습니다, 제대로 오셨어요, 저희 집에 오시던 길이니까요….\"\n\n\"스네-기-료프?\" 의사가 큰 소리로 거드름을 피우며 말했다. \"스네기료프 씨, 당신이오?\"\n\n\"접니다, 선생님!\"\n\n\"아!\"\n\n의사는 비위 상한다는 듯한 기색으로 다시 한번 방을 둘러보고는 외투를 벗어 던졌고, 그러자 목에 걸린 큼지막한 훈장이 모든 이의 눈에 드러났다. 대위가 허공에서 그 모피 외투를 받아 들었고, 의사는 모자를 벗었다.\n\n\"환자는 어디 있소?\" 그가 힘주어 물었다.\n\n# Chapter VI. Precocity\n\n\"의사가 그 애한테 뭐라고 할 것 같으세요?\" 콜랴가 다급하게 물었다. \"그건 그렇고, 저 상판대기가 어찌나 역겨운지요? 저는 의학이라면 도무지 견딜 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"일류샤는 죽어 가고 있어. 그건 틀림없는 사실인 것 같아.\" 알료샤가 침울하게 대답했다.\n\n\"다들 사기꾼이에요! 의학은 협잡질이라고요! 그래도 카라마조프, 당신과 알게 되어 기쁩니다. 오래전부터 당신을 알고 싶었어요. 다만 이렇게 슬픈 처지에서 만나게 된 것이 유감스러울 뿐이에요.\"\n\n콜랴는 그보다 더 따뜻하고 더 마음을 드러내는 무언가를 말하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 어쩐지 거북했다. 알료샤는 그것을 눈치채고 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"저는 오래전부터 당신을 보기 드문 사람으로 존경해 왔어요.\" 콜랴가 더듬거리며 머뭇머뭇 다시 중얼거렸다. \"당신이 신비주의자이고 수도원에 있었다는 말을 들었어요. 당신이 신비주의자라는 건 알아요. 하지만… 그것 때문에 마음이 식지는 않았어요. 실제 삶과 부딪히다 보면 당신도 그게 고쳐질 거예요…. 당신 같은 성격은 늘 그렇거든요.\"\n\n\"신비주의자라니 무슨 뜻이지? 무엇이 고쳐진다는 거고?\" 알료샤는 다소 놀랐다.\n\n\"아, 신이라든가 그 밖의 모든 것 말이에요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 너는 신을 믿지 않니?\"\n\n\"아니요, 저는 신에 대해 반감을 가진 건 아니에요. 물론 신이란 하나의 가설일 뿐이지만… 그래도 신이 필요하다는 건 인정해요… 우주의 질서라든가 그런 모든 것을 위해서요… 그리고 만약 신이 없다면 신을 만들어 내야 했을 거예요.\" 콜랴가 얼굴을 붉히기 시작하며 덧붙였다. 그는 문득 알료샤가 자기더러 지식을 자랑하려 들고 자기가 \"어른\"임을 증명하려 한다고 여길지 모른다는 생각이 들었다. '나는 그 사람한테 지식을 자랑할 마음 따위는 눈곱만큼도 없는데.' 콜랴는 분하게 생각했다. 그러자 갑자기 견딜 수 없이 짜증이 치밀었다.\n\n\"솔직히 말해서 저는 이런 논의에 끼어드는 걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그가 매듭을 짓는 듯한 어조로 말했다. \"신을 믿지 않는 사람도 인류를 사랑할 수 있는 거 아니겠어요? 볼테르는 신을 믿지 않았지만 인류를 사랑했잖아요?\" ('또 시작이군.' 그는 속으로 생각했다.)\n\n\"볼테르는 신을 믿었어. 그다지 깊이는 아니었던 것 같지만 말이야. 그리고 인류를 그렇게 사랑한 것 같지도 않아.\" 알료샤가 조용하고 부드럽게, 마치 자기 또래나 더 나이 든 누군가와 이야기를 나누듯이 아주 자연스럽게 말했다. 콜랴는 볼테르에 관한 자기 견해를 두고 알료샤가 보이는 조심스러운 태도에 유난히 마음이 끌렸다. 마치 그 문제를 어린 콜랴 자신이 결판내도록 남겨 두는 듯했다.\n\n\"너는 볼테르를 읽어 봤니?\" 알료샤가 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"아니요, 읽었다고 할 정도는…. 하지만 《캉디드》를 러시아어 번역본으로 읽긴 했어요… 우스꽝스럽고 괴상한, 케케묵은 번역본이었지만요… (또 시작이군! 또!)\"\n\n\"그래서 이해는 했고?\"\n\n\"아, 네, 다 이해했어요…. 그러니까… 어째서 제가 이해 못 했으리라고 생각하세요? 물론 그 안에 추잡한 게 잔뜩 들어 있긴 하죠…. 물론 그게 철학 소설이고 하나의 사상을 옹호하기 위해 쓰인 거라는 건 이해할 수 있고요….\" 콜랴는 이제 횡설수설하기 시작했다. \"저는 사회주의자예요, 카라마조프. 저는 구제 불능의 사회주의자라고요.\" 그가 느닷없이, 아무 맥락도 없이 선언했다.\n\n\"사회주의자라고?\" 알료샤가 웃었다. \"그런데 대체 언제 사회주의자가 될 틈이 있었지? 아니, 나는 네가 겨우 열세 살인 줄 알았는데?\"\n\n콜랴가 움찔했다.\n\n\"우선 저는 열세 살이 아니라 열네 살이에요. 보름만 지나면 열네 살이라고요.\" 그가 화가 나서 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"그리고 둘째로, 제 나이가 이 일과 무슨 상관인지 도무지 모르겠어요. 문제는 제 신념이 무엇이냐 하는 거지, 제 나이가 몇이냐 하는 게 아니잖아요. 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"네가 좀 더 나이가 들면, 나이가 신념에 미치는 영향을 스스로 깨닫게 될 거야. 그리고 나는 네가 네 자신의 생각을 말하고 있는 게 아니라는 인상도 받았고.\" 알료샤가 차분하고 겸손하게 대답했으나, 콜랴는 발끈하여 그의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"이봐요, 당신은 복종과 신비주의를 원하는 거예요. 가령 그리스도교라는 종교가 오직 부자와 권력자들이 하층 계급을 노예 상태로 묶어 두는 데에만 쓸모가 있었다는 걸 인정해야 한다고요. 그렇잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"아, 네가 그걸 어디서 읽었는지 알겠다. 분명 누군가 너한테 그렇게 말해 줬을 거야!\" 알료샤가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이봐요, 어째서 제가 그걸 읽었다고 생각하세요? 그리고 분명히 아무도 저한테 그렇게 말해 주지 않았어요. 저도 스스로 생각할 줄 안다고요…. 정 그러시다면, 저는 그리스도를 반대하지 않아요. 그분은 더없이 인간적인 분이었고, 만약 그분이 오늘날 살아 계셨다면 혁명가들의 대열에 끼셨을 테고, 어쩌면 두드러진 역할을 하셨을지도 몰라요…. 거기엔 의심의 여지가 없어요.\"\n\n\"오, 어디서, 대체 어디서 그런 걸 주워들었니? 어떤 멍청이하고 친구가 된 거야?\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이봐요, 진실은 드러나기 마련이죠! 어쩌다 보니 라키친 씨하고 자주 이야기를 나눈 건 사실이에요. 물론 그렇지만… 옛날에 벨린스키도 그렇게 말했다고들 하던데요.\"\n\n\"벨린스키가? 기억에 없는데. 그는 그런 걸 어디에도 쓴 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"쓰지는 않았어도 그렇게 말했다고들 해요. 저는 어떤 사람한테서 들었는데… 뭐, 아무래도 상관없죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 너는 벨린스키를 읽어 보긴 했니?\"\n\n\"음, 아니요… 그를 다 읽지는 못했어요. 하지만… 타티야나에 관한 대목은 읽었어요. 그녀가 어째서 오네긴과 떠나지 않았는가 하는 부분 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오네긴과 떠나지 않았다고? 설마 너는… 벌써 그런 것까지 이해한단 말이니?\"\n\n\"이봐요, 당신은 저를 어린 스무로프쯤으로 여기시는 모양이군요.\" 콜랴가 짜증 섞인 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 제발 제가 그런 혁명가라고 생각하지는 말아 주세요. 저는 라키친 씨하고 의견이 안 맞을 때가 많거든요. 제가 타티야나를 언급하긴 했지만, 저는 여성 해방 같은 건 결코 찬성하지 않아요. 저는 여자가 종속된 종족이고 복종해야 한다는 걸 인정해요. 나폴레옹이 말했듯이, '여자들은 뜨개질이나 하라지(Les femmes tricottent)'예요.\" 콜랴가 무슨 까닭에서인지 빙긋 웃었다. \"적어도 그 문제에서만큼은 저도 그 사이비 위인하고 완전히 한마음이에요. 또 저는 제 조국을 버리고 미국으로 달아나는 건 비열한 짓이라고 생각해요. 비열하다 못해 더 나쁜, 어리석은 짓이죠. 여기서도 인류를 위해 크게 봉사할 수 있는데 무엇하러 미국에 간단 말이에요? 특히 지금 같은 때에요. 우리에게는 결실 있는 활동거리가 산더미처럼 활짝 열려 있단 말이에요. 저는 그렇게 대답했어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이지? 누구한테 대답했다는 거야? 누군가 벌써 너한테 미국에 가라고 부추기기라도 했니?\"\n\n\"사실 저더러 가라고 들볶긴 했지만, 저는 거절했어요. 물론 이건 우리끼리 얘기예요, 카라마조프. 듣고 있죠, 누구한테도 한마디도 하면 안 돼요. 이건 당신한테만 하는 말이에요. 저는 비밀경찰의 손아귀에 떨어져서 '사슬 다리'에서 수업을 받을 마음은 전혀 없거든요.\n\n오래도록 너는 기억하리라 사슬 다리 옆의 그 집을.\n\n기억나세요? 멋지죠. 왜 웃으세요? 설마 제가 허풍을 떤다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\" ('만약 내가 아버지 책장에 《종(鐘)》 그 한 호밖에 없고 그 이상은 더 읽어 보지도 못했다는 걸 저 사람이 알아채기라도 하면 어쩌지?' 콜랴는 몸서리치며 생각했다.)\n\n\"오, 아니야, 나는 웃는 게 아니고, 네가 거짓말을 한다고는 한순간도 생각하지 않아. 정말이지 그렇게 생각할 수가 없어. 슬프게도 이 모든 게 너무도 사실이거든. 그런데 말해 보렴, 너는 푸시킨을, 가령 《오네긴》을 읽어 봤니?… 방금 타티야나 이야기를 했잖아.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아직 안 읽었지만 읽고 싶긴 해요. 저는 편견이 없거든요, 카라마조프. 저는 양쪽 이야기를 다 들어 보고 싶어요. 왜 그런 걸 물으세요?\"\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니야.\"\n\n\"말해 주세요, 카라마조프, 당신은 저를 끔찍이 경멸하시나요?\" 콜랴가 느닷없이 쏘아붙이듯 묻고는, 마치 훈련이라도 받는 듯이 알료샤 앞에서 몸을 꼿꼿이 폈다. \"부디 에둘러 말씀하지 마시고 솔직히 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"내가 너를 경멸한다고?\" 알료샤가 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. \"무엇 때문에? 나는 다만 너처럼 매력적인 천성이 인생을 시작하기도 전에 이런 거친 헛소리들로 비뚤어져 버린 것이 슬플 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"제 천성에 대해선 염려하지 마세요.\" 콜랴가 자못 흡족한 기색을 감추지 않으며 말을 가로챘다. \"하지만 제가 어리석게 민감하다는 건, 거칠게 민감하다는 건 사실이에요. 방금 당신이 미소를 지으셨을 때, 저는 당신이 마치—\"\n\n\"오, 내 미소는 전혀 다른 뜻이었어. 왜 웃었는지 말해 줄게. 얼마 전에 나는 러시아에서 살았던 어느 독일 사람이 오늘날 우리 대학생과 학생들에 대해 쓴 비평을 읽었어. '러시아 학생에게,' 그는 이렇게 썼어. '아무것도 모르는 별자리 지도를 보여 줘 보라. 그러면 다음 날 그 지도에 수정까지 가해서 돌려줄 것이다.' 아무 지식도 없으면서 끝없는 자만심뿐이라는 것—그 독일 사람이 러시아 학생에 대해 하려던 말이 바로 그거였어.\"\n\n\"네, 그건 완전히 맞는 말이에요.\" 콜랴가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"정말 그래요! 그 독일 사람, 브라보! 하지만 그는 좋은 면은 못 본 거예요. 어떻게 생각하세요? 자만심이야 있을 수 있죠. 그건 젊음에서 나오는 거고, 필요하다면 바로잡힐 테니까요. 하지만 다른 한편으로는, 거의 어린 시절부터 독립적인 정신이 있고, 사상과 신념의 대담함이 있단 말이에요. 권위 앞에 굽실거리는 그 소시지 장수들 같은 정신이 아니라요…. 그래도 그 독일 사람 말은 옳긴 해요. 그 독일 사람, 브라보! 하지만 독일인들은 어쨌든 목 졸라 죽여야 해요. 학문과 학식에는 그렇게 뛰어나다 해도 목 졸라 죽여야 한다고요.\"\n\n\"목 졸라 죽이다니, 무엇 때문에?\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"음, 어쩌면 제가 허튼소리를 하는 건지도 모르겠어요. 인정해요. 저는 가끔 끔찍이 유치해지거든요. 무언가가 기쁘면 저는 자제를 못 하고 무슨 헛소리든 떠벌릴 채비가 돼 있어요. 그런데 이봐요, 우리가 여기서 쓸데없는 잡담이나 늘어놓고 있는 사이에 저 의사가 안에 들어간 지 너무 오래됐어요. 하지만 어쩌면 어머니랑 그 가엾은 불구의 니나를 진찰하고 있는지도 모르겠네요. 저는 그 니나가 마음에 들었어요, 아세요. 제가 나오려는데 그 애가 갑자기 저한테 속삭이더라고요. '왜 진작 안 오셨어요?' 게다가 그런 목소리로, 어찌나 원망하는 투였는지요! 저는 그 애가 끔찍이 착하고 가엾다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래! 그럼 너는 자주 오게 될 테고, 그 애가 어떤 애인지 알게 될 거야. 그런 사람들을 알게 되는 건 너한테 큰 도움이 될 거야. 그 사람들을 알게 됨으로써 비로소 깨닫게 될 많은 것의 가치를 배우게 될 테니까.\" 알료샤가 따뜻하게 말했다. \"그게 그 무엇보다도 너한테 큰 영향을 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"오, 진작 오지 못한 것이 어찌나 후회스럽고 제 자신이 원망스러운지요!\" 콜랴가 쓰라린 심정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 안타까운 일이야. 너도 직접 봤잖아, 그 가엾은 아이가 너를 보고 얼마나 기뻐했는지. 그리고 네가 오기를 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지!\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마세요! 더 괴로워지잖아요! 하지만 당해도 싸죠. 제가 오지 못하게 막은 건 제 자만심이었어요. 제 이기적인 허영심, 그리고 그 빌어먹을 고집—평생 싸워 왔는데도 도무지 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없는 그 고집 말이에요. 이제야 그게 보여요. 저는 여러모로 짐승 같은 놈이에요, 카라마조프!\"\n\n\"아니야, 너는 매력적인 천성을 지녔어. 비록 비뚤어지긴 했지만 말이야. 그리고 네가 어째서 이 너그럽고 병적으로 민감한 아이한테 그토록 큰 영향을 끼쳤는지 충분히 이해가 돼.\" 알료샤가 따뜻하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그런 말씀을 저한테 하시다니요!\" 콜랴가 외쳤다. \"믿어지세요, 저는 생각했었어요—여기 온 뒤로도 몇 번이나 그렇게 생각했어요—당신이 저를 경멸한다고요! 제가 당신의 의견을 얼마나 소중히 여기는지 아신다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\"\n\n\"그런데 너는 정말로 그렇게 민감하니? 그 나이에! 믿어지니, 방금 네가 네 이야기를 들려줄 때, 나는 너를 지켜보면서 네가 무척 민감한 아이임이 틀림없다고 생각했어!\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하셨어요? 정말 눈썰미가 대단하시네요! 분명 제가 그 거위 이야기를 할 때였을 거예요. 바로 그때 저는 당신이 제가 그렇게 서둘러 잘난 척하려 든다고 저를 잔뜩 경멸한다고 여기고 있었거든요. 그래서 한순간 그것 때문에 당신이 정말 미워졌고, 바보처럼 떠들어 대기 시작했던 거예요. 그러고 나서 또—방금, 여기서—제가 신이 없다면 신을 만들어 내야 했을 거라고 말했을 때, 제가 제 지식을 뽐내려고 너무 서두르고 있다고 여겼어요. 특히 그 구절을 책에서 따온 거였으니까요. 하지만 맹세코 저는 허영심 때문에 잘난 척한 게 아니에요. 정말이지 왜 그랬는지 모르겠지만요. 너무 기뻐서였을까요? 네, 너무 기뻐서 그랬던 것 같아요…. 기쁘다고 곧장 호들갑을 떠는 건 누구한테나 더없이 창피한 짓이라는 걸 알면서도요. 하지만 이제 저는 당신이 저를 경멸하지 않는다고 확신해요. 그건 다 제 망상이었던 거예요. 오, 카라마조프, 저는 뼈저리게 불행해요. 저는 가끔 별별 상상을 다 한단 말이에요. 모두가, 온 세상이 저를 비웃고 있다고요. 그러면 저는 세상의 질서 전체를 뒤엎어 버리고 싶은 마음이 들어요.\"\n\n\"그러면서 너는 네 주위의 모든 사람을 들볶는구나.\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"네, 저는 제 주위의 모두를, 특히 어머니를 들볶아요. 카라마조프, 말해 주세요, 제가 지금 아주 우스꽝스러운가요?\"\n\n\"그런 건 생각하지 마, 아예 생각도 하지 마!\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"그리고 우스꽝스럽다는 게 대체 뭐지? 사람은 누구나 끊임없이 우스꽝스럽거나 우스꽝스러워 보이지 않니? 게다가 요즘 영리한 사람들은 거의 다 우스꽝스러워 보일까 봐 끔찍이 두려워하고, 그 때문에 불행해진단 말이야. 다만 내가 놀라는 건, 네가 그걸 이렇게 일찍부터 느끼고 있다는 거야. 사실 나는 얼마 전부터 그걸 눈여겨봐 왔어. 너뿐만이 아니라 말이야. 요즘은 어린아이들까지 그것 때문에 괴로워하기 시작했거든. 그건 거의 일종의 광기야. 악마가 그 허영심의 모습을 띠고 온 세대 안으로 들어와 버린 거지. 그건 그야말로 악마야.\" 알료샤가 덧붙였는데, 콜랴가 빤히 바라보며 기대했던 그 미소는 조금도 띠고 있지 않았다. \"너도 다른 모든 사람과 똑같아.\" 알료샤가 마무리 지어 말했다. \"그러니까, 아주 많은 다른 사람들과 똑같다는 거야. 다만 너는 다른 모든 사람과 똑같아져서는 안 돼. 그게 다야.\"\n\n\"모두가 다 그렇다 해도요?\"\n\n\"그래, 모두가 다 그렇다 해도. 너만은 그렇지 않은 유일한 사람이 되어야 해. 너는 정말로 다른 모든 사람과는 달라. 봐, 너는 무언가 나쁜 것, 심지어 우스꽝스러운 것까지 고백하기를 부끄러워하지 않잖아. 요즘 누가 그만큼 인정하겠어? 아무도 안 해. 사람들은 자기 자신을 비판하려는 충동조차 느끼지 않게 돼 버렸어. 다른 모든 사람과 똑같아지지 마. 설령 네가 유일한 사람이 된다 해도 말이야.\"\n\n\"훌륭해요! 제가 당신을 잘못 본 게 아니었어요. 당신은 사람을 위로할 줄 아시는군요. 오, 제가 당신을 얼마나 알고 싶어 했는지요, 카라마조프! 저는 오래전부터 이 만남을 간절히 바라 왔어요. 정말로 당신도 저에 대해 생각하셨던 거예요? 방금 당신도 저를 생각했다고 하셨잖아요?\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 네 이야기를 들었고 너에 대해 생각도 했어…. 그리고 설령 그걸 묻는 게 어느 정도는 허영심에서 나온 거라 해도, 상관없어.\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 카라마조프, 우리 대화는 마치 사랑의 고백 같았어요.\" 콜랴가 수줍고 녹아드는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. \"이건 우스꽝스럽지 않죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"전혀 우스꽝스럽지 않아. 설령 그렇다 해도 상관없어. 좋은 일이었으니까.\" 알료샤가 환하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그런데 있잖아요, 카라마조프, 당신도 지금 조금은 부끄러워하고 있다는 걸 인정하셔야 해요…. 당신 눈을 보면 알 수 있거든요.\" 콜랴가 짓궂은 행복감 같은 것을 띠며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"왜 부끄러워한다는 거지?\"\n\n\"음, 그럼 왜 얼굴을 붉히시는데요?\"\n\n\"네가 나를 붉히게 만든 거잖아.\" 알료샤가 웃었는데, 그는 정말로 얼굴을 붉히고 있었다. \"오, 그래, 조금 그렇긴 한데, 왜 그런지는 정말이지 나도 모르겠다….\" 그가 거의 당황한 듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 바로 당신이 조금 부끄러워하고 있다는 그 이유 때문에 지금 이 순간 제가 당신을 얼마나 사랑하고 우러러보는지요! 당신이 바로 저와 똑같으니까요!\" 콜랴가 그야말로 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤다. 그의 두 뺨이 달아오르고 두 눈이 빛났다.\n\n\"있잖아, 콜랴, 너는 살아가는 동안 무척 불행할 거야.\" 무언가가 알료샤로 하여금 불쑥 그렇게 말하게 했다.\n\n\"알아요, 알아요. 어쩌면 그렇게 모든 걸 미리 다 아세요!\" 콜랴가 곧바로 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 그래도 너는 인생 전체를 두고 보면 삶을 축복하게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요, 만세! 당신은 예언자예요. 오, 우리는 잘 지낼 거예요, 카라마조프! 있잖아요, 무엇보다 저를 기쁘게 하는 건, 당신이 저를 완전히 대등한 사람처럼 대해 주신다는 거예요. 하지만 우리는 대등하지 않아요, 아니에요, 우리는 대등하지 않아요, 당신이 더 나은 사람이에요! 그래도 우리는 잘 지낼 거예요. 있잖아요, 지난 한 달 내내 저는 제 자신한테 이렇게 말해 왔어요. '우리는 단번에 영원한 친구가 되거나, 아니면 무덤까지 가는 원수로 갈라서거나, 둘 중 하나다!'라고요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그렇게 말했다는 건, 물론 너는 나를 사랑한 거지.\" 알료샤가 유쾌하게 웃었다.\n\n\"그랬어요. 저는 당신을 끔찍이 사랑했어요. 저는 줄곧 당신을 사랑하고 당신을 꿈꿔 왔어요. 그런데 어떻게 그 모든 걸 미리 다 아세요? 아, 의사가 나오네요. 맙소사! 그가 우리한테 뭐라고 할까요? 저 얼굴 좀 보세요!\"\n\n# Chapter VII. Ilusha\n\n의사가 모피 외투를 단단히 여미고 머리에 모자를 쓴 채 다시 방에서 나왔다. 그의 얼굴은 무언가에 더럽혀질까 두려워하는 사람처럼 거의 성이 나고 역겨워하는 표정이었다. 그는 복도를 휙 둘러보았는데, 그러면서 알료샤와 콜랴를 매섭게 쏘아보았다. 알료샤는 문가에서 마부에게 손짓을 했고, 의사를 태우고 왔던 마차가 다가왔다. 대위가 의사를 뒤쫓아 뛰어나가, 사죄하듯 허리를 굽히며 그를 멈춰 세우고는 마지막 한마디를 들으려 했다. 가엾은 사내는 완전히 으스러진 듯 보였고, 그 눈에는 겁에 질린 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"각하, 각하…… 그럴 수가 있습니까?\" 그는 입을 떼었으나 더는 말을 잇지 못하고 절망에 차 두 손을 맞잡았다. 그러면서도 그는 여전히 애원하듯 의사를 바라보고 있었으니, 마치 그의 말 한마디가 아직도 이 가엾은 아이의 운명을 바꿔 줄 수 있기라도 하다는 듯이.\n\n\"나로서는 어쩔 수가 없소, 나는 신이 아니란 말이오!\" 의사는 무심하게 대꾸했으나, 그래도 늘 그렇듯 위엄을 곁들였다.\n\n\"선생님…… 각하…… 그럼 곧, 곧 그렇게 됩니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이든 각오를 해 두셔야 할 거요.\" 의사는 단호하고 날카로운 어조로 말하더니, 눈을 내리깔고는 마차로 발을 내디디려 했다.\n\n\"각하, 제발 부탁입니다!\" 공포에 사로잡힌 대위가 다시 그를 가로막았다. \"각하! 정말 이제는 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 저 애를 살릴 수 없는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이제 내 손을 떠난 일이오.\" 의사가 짜증스레 말했다. \"하지만 흠!……\" 그는 갑자기 말을 멈췄다. \"만약 가령…… 환자를…… 지금 당장, 한시도 지체 없이\"(이 \"지금 당장, 한시도 지체 없이\"라는 말을 의사는 거의 노여움이 어린 엄격함으로 내뱉어, 대위가 흠칫 놀랐다)\"시라쿠사로 보낼 수 있다면, 새로운 유-익한 기후 조건으로의 변화가 어쩌면 효과를……\"\n\n\"시라쿠사로요!\" 대위는 들은 말을 미처 알아듣지 못하고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"시라쿠사는 시칠리아에 있어요.\" 콜랴가 불쑥 끼어들어 설명했다. 의사가 그를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"시칠리아요! 각하,\" 대위는 더듬거렸다. \"하지만 보셨다시피\"—그는 두 손을 펼쳐 주변을 가리켰다—\"안사람이며 제 식구들을……?\"\n\n\"아—니, 시칠리아는 식구들이 갈 곳이 아니오. 가족은 이른 봄에 캅카스로 가야 하오…… 따님은 캅카스로 가야 하고, 부인은…… 류머티즘 때문에 캅카스에서 온천 요법을 받은 다음…… 곧장 파리의 정신과 전문의 르펠르티에에게 보내야 하오. 내가 그에게 보내는 소개장을 써 드릴 수도 있소. 그렇게 하면…… 차도가 있을지도 모르오……\"\n\n\"선생님, 선생님! 하지만 보십시오!\" 대위는 다시 절망에 차 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 복도의 휑한 나무 벽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"흠, 그건 내 일이 아니지요.\" 의사는 히죽 웃었다. \"나는 다만 가능한 치료에 관한 당신의 물음에 의학이 내놓는 답을 말해 주었을 뿐이오. 그 나머지로 말하면, 유감스럽게도……\"\n\n\"걱정 마쇼, 약장수 양반, 우리 개는 안 물어요.\" 콜랴가 큰 소리로 쏘아붙였으니, 문간에 서 있는 페레즈본을 향해 의사가 다소 불안한 눈길을 던지는 것을 알아챘던 것이다. 콜랴의 목소리에는 노여움이 서려 있었다. 그는 의사라는 말 대신 일부러 약장수라는 말을 썼고, 나중에 스스로 밝힌 대로 \"그를 모욕하려고\" 그 말을 쓴 것이었다.\n\n\"뭐야, 이게?\" 의사는 고개를 번쩍 쳐들고 놀란 눈으로 콜랴를 노려보았다. \"이건 또 누군가?\" 그는 설명을 구하기라도 하듯 알료샤를 향해 물었다.\n\n\"이건 페레즈본의 주인이올시다, 내 걱정은 마쇼.\" 콜랴가 다시 날카롭게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"페레즈본?\" 의사가 어리둥절해하며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"종소리는 들려도 어디서 나는지는 모른다 이거지. 잘 가쇼, 시라쿠사에서 만납시다.\"\n\n\"이건 누구야? 누구냐고?\" 의사는 무서운 분노에 휩싸였다.\n\n\"그저 학생입니다, 선생님, 장난기 많은 애예요, 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\" 알료샤가 눈살을 찌푸리며 빠르게 말했다. \"콜랴, 입 다물어!\" 그는 크라소트킨에게 소리쳤다. \"신경 쓰지 마십시오, 선생님.\" 그는 다소 조바심을 내며 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"저놈은 매를 맞아야 해, 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 한다고!\" 의사가 그야말로 길길이 날뛰며 발을 굴렀다.\n\n\"그런데 말이오, 약장수 양반, 우리 페레즈본이 물지도 몰라!\" 콜랴는 얼굴이 새파래진 채 목소리를 떨고 눈을 번득이며 말했다. \"이리 와, 페레즈본!\"\n\n\"콜랴, 한마디만 더 하면 나는 너와 두 번 다시 상종하지 않겠다.\" 알료샤가 단호하게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"이 세상에 니콜라이 크라소트킨에게 명령할 수 있는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이야—바로 이 사람이지.\" 콜랴가 알료샤를 가리켰다. \"나는 이 사람한테 복종한다. 잘 가쇼!\"\n\n그는 앞으로 나서서 문을 열고 안쪽 방으로 잰걸음으로 들어갔다. 페레즈본이 그 뒤를 쫓아 달려갔다. 의사는 어이가 없어 오 초쯤 꼼짝 않고 서서 알료샤를 바라보다가, 욕설을 내뱉으며 황급히 마차로 나가서는 큰 소리로 거듭 중얼거렸다. \"이건…… 이건…… 대체 이게 뭔지 모르겠군!\" 대위가 앞으로 달려 나가 그가 마차에 오르는 것을 거들었다. 알료샤는 콜랴를 따라 방으로 들어갔다. 콜랴는 이미 일류샤의 머리맡에 가 있었다. 병든 아이는 그의 손을 잡고 아버지를 부르고 있었다. 잠시 후 대위도 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지, 이리 와요…… 우리……\" 일류샤는 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 더듬거렸으나, 더는 말을 잇지 못하고 야윈 팔로 아버지와 콜랴를 한꺼번에 끌어안아 하나로 모으고는, 있는 힘껏 그들을 꼭 껴안았다. 대위가 갑자기 소리 없는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨기 시작했고, 콜랴의 입술과 턱도 실룩거렸다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지! 저 때문에 얼마나 마음이 아프세요!\" 일류샤가 비통하게 신음했다.\n\n\"일류샤…… 우리 아가…… 의사 선생이 말했어…… 너는 괜찮을 거라고…… 우리는 행복해질 거야…… 의사 선생이……\" 대위가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아, 아버지! 새로 온 의사가 저에 대해서 아버지한테 뭐라고 했는지 다 알아요…… 봤단 말이에요!\" 일류샤가 소리치며, 다시 온 힘을 다해 두 사람을 끌어안고는 아버지의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"아버지, 울지 마세요. 그리고 제가 죽으면 좋은 아이를, 다른 아이를 하나 데려오세요…… 아이들 중에서 좋은 아이를 골라서, 일류샤라고 부르고 제 대신 그 애를 사랑해 주세요……\"\n\n\"쉿, 이 친구야, 너는 나을 거야.\" 크라소트킨이 갑자기 성난 듯한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 절 절대 잊지는 마세요, 아버지.\" 일류샤가 말을 이었다. \"제 무덤에 와 주세요…… 그리고 아버지, 우리가 산책하러 다니던 그 큰 바위 옆에 저를 묻어 주세요. 저녁이면 크라소트킨이랑 같이 그리로 저를 찾아와 주세요…… 페레즈본도요…… 저는 아버지를 기다릴 거예요…… 아버지, 아버지!\"\n\n그의 목소리가 끊겼다. 세 사람은 여전히 서로를 껴안은 채 모두 말이 없었다. 니나는 의자에 앉아 조용히 울고 있었는데, 마침내 모두가 우는 것을 보고는 \"엄마\"마저 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"일류샤! 일류샤!\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n크라소트킨이 갑자기 일류샤의 품에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n\"잘 있어, 이 친구야, 어머니가 저녁때 돌아오라고 하셨거든.\" 그가 황급히 말했다. \"어머니한테 말씀을 못 드리고 나온 게 정말 후회되는걸! 무척 걱정하실 거야…… 하지만 저녁을 먹고 나면 다시 너한테 와서 하루 종일, 저녁 내내 같이 있을게. 그리고 온갖 이야기를, 별의별 이야기를 다 들려줄게. 페레즈본도 데려올게, 지금은 데리고 갈 거야. 내가 없으면 저놈이 짖어 대기 시작해서 너를 성가시게 할 테니까. 잘 있어!\"\n\n그러고는 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다. 울고 싶지 않았으나, 복도에 나서자 그는 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다. 알료샤가 우는 그를 발견했다.\n\n\"콜랴, 꼭 약속을 지키고 와야 해, 안 그러면 저 애가 몹시 실망할 거야.\" 알료샤가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"올 거예요! 아아, 진작 오지 않은 제 자신이 얼마나 원망스러운지!\" 콜랴는 울먹이며 중얼거렸고, 이제는 그것을 부끄러워하지도 않았다.\n\n바로 그 순간 대위가 방에서 뛰쳐나오며 곧장 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 그의 얼굴은 광기에 휩싸인 듯했고, 입술은 떨리고 있었다. 그는 두 사람 앞에 서서 두 팔을 치켜들었다.\n\n\"나는 좋은 아이 따위 싫어! 다른 아이는 싫단 말이야!\" 그는 이를 악물고 거칠게 속삭이듯 중얼거렸다. \"예루살렘아, 내가 너를 잊을진대, 내 혀가—\" 그는 흐느낌으로 말을 끊고는 나무 의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. 두 주먹을 머리에 짓누른 채, 그는 우스꽝스럽게 칭얼대는 듯한 소리로 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 그 울음소리가 방 안까지 들리지 않게 하려고 안간힘을 다했다.\n\n콜랴는 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"잘 있어요, 카라마조프? 형도 직접 올 거죠?\" 그가 알료샤에게 날카롭고 성난 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"저녁에 꼭 올게.\"\n\n\"그런데 아저씨가 예루살렘에 대해서 한 말은 뭐예요?…… 그게 무슨 뜻이죠?\"\n\n\"성경에 나오는 말이야. '예루살렘아, 내가 너를 잊을진대'라는 건, 곧 내가 나에게 가장 소중한 모든 것을 잊는다면, 그 무엇이든 그 자리를 대신하게 한다면, 그때는—\"\n\"알았어요, 그만하면 됐어요! 꼭 와요! 이리 와, 페레즈본!\" 그는 개를 향해 그야말로 사납게 소리치고는, 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 집으로 향했다.\n\n* 페레즈본(Перезвон): 러시아어로 '여러 종이 어우러져 울리는 소리'를 뜻하며, 콜랴가 의사에게 읊은 \"종소리는 들려도 어디서 나는지는 모른다\"라는 말장난과 연결된다.\n\n# Book XI. Ivan Chapter I. At Grushenka’s\n\n알료샤는 대성당 광장 쪽으로, 미망인 모로조바의 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다. 그루셴카를 만나러 가는 길이었으니, 그녀가 이른 아침 페냐를 보내어 부디 와 달라는 절박한 전갈을 전했던 것이다. 페냐에게 이것저것 묻는 동안 알료샤는 자기 여주인이 전날부터 유난히 마음 졸이고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 미탸가 체포된 후 두 달이 흐르는 사이 알료샤는 모로조바의 집에 자주 들렀으니, 그 자신의 마음에서이기도 했고 미탸의 전갈을 전하기 위해서이기도 했다. 미탸가 체포되고 사흘이 지났을 때 그루셴카는 몹시 앓아누워 거의 다섯 주 동안이나 병석에 있었다. 그 가운데 꼬박 한 주일은 의식을 잃은 채였다. 그녀는 몰라보게 변해서, 더 야위었고 안색도 다소 누렇게 떴으나, 지난 두 주 동안에는 외출할 수 있을 만큼은 회복되어 있었다. 그러나 알료샤가 보기에 그녀의 얼굴은 예전보다 오히려 더 매력적이었고, 그녀에게 들어설 때 그 눈과 마주치는 것이 그는 좋았다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 굳건함과 지혜로운 결의의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그녀에게는 어떤 정신적 변모의 자취가 엿보였고, 무엇으로도 흔들 수 없는 한결같고 고결하며 겸허한 결심이 분간되었다. 두 눈썹 사이에는 작은 세로 주름이 한 줄 잡혀 있었는데, 그것이 그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯한, 첫눈에는 거의 준엄해 보이는 인상을 더했다. 예전의 그 경박함은 거의 자취도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n알료샤에게는 또 한 가지 이상하게 여겨지는 것이 있었으니, 가엾은 처녀를 덮친 재난에도 불구하고—약혼한 지 거의 그 순간에 약혼자가 끔찍한 죄목으로 체포되었고, 그녀 자신의 병환에 더해 미탸에게 거의 피할 수 없는 선고가 드리워져 있는데도—그루셴카가 아직 젊은이다운 명랑함을 잃지 않았다는 점이었다. 한때 오만하던 그 두 눈에는 이제 부드러운 빛이 어려 있었으나, 마음속에서 그 어느 때보다 강하게 그녀를 괴롭히는 한 가지 생각이 찾아들 때면 더러는 옛날의 그 앙심 어린 불길로 번득이기도 했다. 그 불안의 대상은 변함없이 똑같았으니, 곧 카테리나 이바노브나였고, 그루셴카는 헛소리를 하며 누워 있을 때조차 그녀의 이름을 부르며 광란했던 것이다. 알료샤는 그녀가 그녀를 끔찍하게 질투하고 있음을 알고 있었다. 그런데도 카테리나 이바노브나는 마음만 먹으면 언제든 갈 수 있었음에도, 옥에 갇힌 미탸를 단 한 번도 찾지 않았다. 이 모든 것이 알료샤에게는 풀기 어려운 문제였으니, 그는 그루셴카가 속마음을 털어놓는 유일한 사람이자 그녀가 끊임없이 조언을 구하는 사람이었기 때문이다. 더러는 그도 아무 말도 해 줄 수 없었다.\n\n근심에 가득 차 그는 그녀의 숙소로 들어섰다. 그녀는 집에 있었다. 그녀는 반 시간 전에 미탸를 만나고 돌아온 참이었는데, 그를 맞으려 의자에서 벌떡 일어서는 그 재빠른 몸짓을 보고 그는 그녀가 몹시 애타게 자기를 기다리고 있었음을 알아챘다. 탁자 위에는 \"바보 놀이\"를 하려고 돌려 놓은 카드 한 벌이 놓여 있었다. 맞은편 가죽 소파 위에는 잠자리가 마련되어 있었고, 그 위에 막시모프가 반쯤 기대어 누워 있었다. 그는 실내복에 무명 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 보아하니 병들어 기운이 없었으나, 그래도 더없이 행복한 듯 미소를 짓고 있었다. 두 달 전 그루셴카와 함께 모크로예에서 돌아온 이 의지가지없는 늙은이는, 그저 눌러앉아 지금껏 그녀와 함께 머물고 있는 것이었다. 그는 비와 진눈깨비를 맞으며 그녀와 함께 도착해서는, 흠뻑 젖고 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 소파에 주저앉아, 소심하면서도 애원하는 듯한 미소를 띠고 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다. 끔찍한 슬픔에 잠긴 데다 열병의 초기 증세까지 겹쳐 있던 그루셴카는, 도착한 뒤 첫 반 시간 동안 처리할 일이 많아 그의 존재를 거의 잊고 있었다. 그러다 문득 그를 뚫어지게 바라보게 되었는데, 그는 가엾고 무력한 작은 웃음을 흘렸다. 그녀는 페냐를 불러 그에게 먹을 것을 좀 주라고 일렀다. 그날 하루 종일 그는 거의 꼼짝도 않고 같은 자리에 앉아 있었다. 날이 어두워지고 덧창이 닫혔을 때 페냐가 여주인에게 물었다.\n\n\"마님, 저 어른은 여기서 밤을 보내실 건가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 소파에 잠자리를 봐 드려라.\" 그루셴카가 대답했다.\n\n좀 더 자세히 캐물어, 그루셴카는 그가 그야말로 갈 데가 한 군데도 없다는 것과, \"제 은인이신 칼가노프 나리께서 다시는 저를 거두지 않겠노라 대놓고 말씀하시고는 오 루블을 주셨습죠\" 하는 사정을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"그래요, 그럼 하느님의 가호가 함께하시길, 여기 머무는 게 낫겠네요.\" 슬픔에 잠긴 채 그루셴카는 가엾이 여기는 미소를 그에게 지어 보이며 그렇게 결정했다. 그녀의 미소는 늙은이의 가슴을 후벼 팠고, 그의 입술은 고마운 눈물로 떨렸다. 그리하여 이 빈털터리 떠돌이는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그녀와 함께 머물게 되었다. 그는 그녀가 앓아누웠을 때조차 그 집을 떠나지 않았다. 페냐와 그 할머니인 식모도 그를 내쫓지 않고, 줄곧 끼니를 차려 주고 소파에 잠자리를 봐 주었다. 그루셴카도 그에게 익숙해져서, 미탸를 만나고 돌아오면(그녀는 채 몸이 낫기도 전부터 옥에 갇힌 그를 찾기 시작했다) 자리에 앉아 슬픔에 골몰하지 않으려고 \"막시무시카\"와 시시콜콜한 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 늙은이는 이따금 제법 그럴듯하게 이야기를 풀어 놓는 재주가 있어, 마침내 그녀에게 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되었다. 그루셴카는 알료샤 말고는 거의 아무도 만나지 않았는데, 알료샤조차 매일 오는 것도 아니었고 오래 머무는 법도 없었다. 그녀의 옛 상인은 이 무렵 중병으로 누워 있었으니, 고을 사람들 말마따나 \"마지막 숨을 헐떡이고\" 있었고, 실제로 그는 미탸의 재판이 끝난 일주일 뒤에 죽었다. 죽기 세 주 전, 임종이 가까워 옴을 느낀 그는 마침내 아들들과 며느리들, 손주들을 위층 자기 방으로 불러올려 다시는 곁을 떠나지 말라고 명했다. 그 순간부터 그는 하인들에게 엄명을 내려 그루셴카를 절대 들이지 말고, 만일 그녀가 오면 이렇게 전하라고 했다. \"주인 어른께서는 부인이 오래 건강히 행복하게 사시기를 바라시며, 자신을 잊어 달라고 하셨습니다.\" 그래도 그루셴카는 거의 날마다 사람을 보내 그의 안부를 물었다.\n\n\"드디어 오셨네요!\" 그녀는 카드를 내던지고 알료샤를 기쁘게 맞으며 외쳤다. \"막시무시카가, 어쩌면 안 오실지도 모른다고 자꾸 겁을 주잖겠어요. 아, 당신이 얼마나 필요한지! 어서 탁자에 앉으세요. 뭘 드시겠어요, 커피?\"\n\n\"네, 주세요.\" 알료샤가 탁자에 앉으며 말했다. \"몹시 시장하네요.\"\n\n\"잘됐네요. 페냐, 페냐, 커피!\" 그루셴카가 외쳤다. \"당신 드리려고 진작 끓여 놨어요. 그리고 작은 파이도 좀 가져오너라, 꼭 뜨겁게 해서. 그거 아세요, 오늘 그 파이 때문에 한바탕 난리가 났답니다. 그이한테 주려고 옥으로 가져갔는데, 글쎄 그걸 도로 저한테 내던지지 뭐예요. 먹지 않겠다는 거예요. 그중 하나는 바닥에 내동댕이치고 발로 짓밟더라고요. 그래서 제가 그랬죠. '간수한테 맡겨 두고 가겠어요. 저녁때까지 안 드시면, 그건 당신 그 독한 심술만으로도 배가 부르다는 거겠죠!' 그러고는 나와 버렸어요. 또 싸웠지 뭐예요, 글쎄. 갈 때마다 싸운다니까요.\"\n\n그루셴카는 격앙된 나머지 이 모든 것을 단숨에 쏟아냈다. 막시모프는 마음이 불안해져서 이내 미소를 지으며 바닥으로 눈을 떨구었다.\n\n\"이번엔 무슨 일로 다투셨는데요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 생각지도 못한 일이었어요. 글쎄, 그이가 그 폴란드 사람을 질투하지 뭐예요. '왜 그자를 거두고 있는 거야?' 그러더라고요. '그래, 이제 아주 그자를 데리고 사는군.' 질투, 줄곧 저를 질투해요, 먹는 것 자는 것까지 질투한다니까요! 지난주에는 또 쿠즈마를 질투할 생각까지 다 하더라고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분도 그 폴란드 사람에 대해 진작부터 알고 있었잖아요?\"\n\n\"네, 그런데 그게 문제예요. 처음부터 다 알고 있었으면서, 오늘은 느닷없이 벌떡 일어나서 그자에 대해 욕을 퍼붓기 시작하는 거예요. 무슨 말을 했는지 입에 담기도 부끄러워요. 어리석은 사람! 제가 나올 때 라키틴이 들어가더라고요. 어쩌면 라키틴이 부추기는지도 몰라요. 어떻게 생각하세요?\" 그녀는 아무렇지 않은 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그분은 당신을 사랑하는 겁니다, 그게 다예요. 그토록 당신을 사랑하는 거죠. 그런데 지금은 유난히 마음이 불안한 거고요.\"\n\n\"그야 그럴 만도 하겠죠, 내일이 재판이니까요. 그래서 제가 내일 일을 두고 뭐라도 좀 말하려고 갔던 거예요. 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각만 해도 두려우니까요. 당신은 그이가 불안해한다고 하시지만, 제가 얼마나 불안한지 아세요! 그런데 폴란드 사람 이야기나 하고 있다니! 너무 어리석어요! 그래도 막시무시카는 아직 질투하지 않으니 다행이죠.\"\n\n\"제 아내도 저를 두고 어찌나 질투를 하던지요.\" 막시모프가 한마디 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신을 질투해요?\" 그루셴카는 자기도 모르게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"대체 누굴 질투할 게 있다고요?\"\n\n\"하녀들을 두고요.\"\n\n\"입 다물어요, 막시무시카, 지금은 웃을 기분이 아니에요. 화가 나 있다고요. 파이를 그렇게 눈독 들이지 말아요. 안 줄 거예요. 당신 몸에 안 좋아요. 보드카도 안 줄 거고요. 나는 이이까지 보살펴야 한다니까요, 마치 양로원이라도 차린 것처럼.\" 그녀가 웃었다.\n\n\"저는 마님의 친절을 받을 자격이 없습니다. 저는 쓸모없는 인간이지요.\" 막시모프가 목멘 소리로 말했다. \"저 같은 것보다 더 쓸모 있는 사람들에게 친절을 베푸시는 게 나을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"에, 누구나 다 쓸모가 있는 거예요, 막시무시카, 게다가 누가 제일 쓸모 있는지 우리가 어찌 알겠어요? 그 폴란드 사람만 없으면 좋으련만, 알료샤. 그자도 오늘 또 병이 날 생각을 했지 뭐예요. 그래서 그자한테도 다녀왔어요. 그자한테도 일부러 파이를 좀 보낼 거예요. 보낸 적이 없었는데, 미탸가 그렇다고 저를 몰아세웠으니, 그러니 이제 보낼 거예요! 아, 페냐가 편지를 들고 오네요! 네, 그 폴란드 사람들한테서 온 거예요. 또 구걸하는 거죠!\"\n\n과연 무샬로비치 씨는 더없이 길고도 그답게 화려한 편지를 보내왔는데, 그 안에서 그는 그녀에게 삼 루블만 빌려 달라고 간청하고 있었다. 편지에는 그 금액에 대한 차용증이 동봉되어 있었고, 석 달 안에 갚겠다는 약속과 함께 브루블렙스키 씨의 서명까지 들어 있었다. 그루셴카는 회복기에 접어든 두 주 동안 옛 정인에게서 이런 차용증을 곁들인 편지를 여러 통 받았다. 그러나 그녀는 그 두 폴란드 사람이 그녀가 앓는 동안 안부를 물으러 왔었다는 것은 알고 있었다. 그루셴카가 그들에게서 받은 첫 번째 편지는 길고 긴 것으로, 큰 편지지에 쓰여 봉인에는 커다란 가문(家紋)이 찍혀 있었다. 그것이 어찌나 알아먹기 어렵고 미사여구로 가득하던지, 그루셴카는 절반도 읽기 전에 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없어 내려놓고 말았다. 그때는 편지 따위에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었던 것이다. 첫 번째 편지에 이어 이튿날 두 번째 편지가 왔는데, 거기서 무샬로비치 씨는 아주 짧은 기간만 이천 루블을 빌려 달라고 간청했다. 그루셴카는 그 편지에도 답하지 않았다. 그 뒤로 일련의 편지가 줄을 이었으니, 날마다 한 통씩 모두 한결같이 거창하고 화려했으나, 빌려 달라는 액수는 차츰 줄어들어 백 루블로, 다음엔 이십오 루블로, 십 루블로 떨어졌고, 마침내 그루셴카는 두 폴란드 사람이 단 일 루블만 간청하면서 둘 다 서명한 차용증을 동봉한 편지를 받게 되었다.\n\n그러자 그루셴카는 문득 그들이 가엾게 여겨져, 해 질 녘에 손수 그들의 숙소로 찾아갔다. 가 보니 두 폴란드 사람은 극심한 가난, 거의 알거지나 다름없는 처지에 빠져 있었으니, 먹을 것도 땔감도 담배도 없는 데다 셋집 안주인에게 빚까지 지고 있었다. 모크로예에서 미탸에게서 챙겨 온 이백 루블은 금세 사라져 버린 것이다. 그러나 그루셴카가 놀란 것은, 그들이 거만한 위엄과 자기 과시를 부리며, 더없이 까다로운 격식과 거창한 언사로 그녀를 맞이했다는 점이었다. 그루셴카는 그저 웃으며 옛 흠모자에게 십 루블을 주었다. 그러고는 웃으며 미탸에게 그 이야기를 했더니 그는 조금도 질투하지 않았다. 그러나 그때부터 폴란드 사람들은 그루셴카에게 들러붙어 날마다 돈 요구로 그녀를 들볶았고, 그녀는 늘 얼마씩 적은 돈을 보내 주었다. 그런데 바로 그날 미탸가 끔찍하게 질투를 부릴 생각을 했던 것이다.\n\n\"바보처럼, 미탸를 보러 가는 길에 잠깐만 들르려고 그자한테 갔지 뭐예요, 그 사람도 아프거든요, 그 폴란드 사람 말이에요.\" 그루셴카는 다시 안절부절못하며 황급히 말을 이었다. \"그러고는 웃으면서 미탸한테 그 이야기를 했어요. '글쎄,' 그랬죠, '그 폴란드 사람이 좋다고 기타를 치면서 옛날 노래를 불러 주지 뭐예요. 그러면 내가 감동해서 자기랑 결혼이라도 할 줄 알았던 거예요!' 그랬더니 미탸가 욕을 퍼부으며 벌떡 일어서더라고요…… 그러니까, 봐요, 그래서 그들한테 파이를 보낼 거예요! 페냐, 저 애가 그자들이 보낸 그 계집애냐? 자, 얘한테 삼 루블 주고 파이 열두 개를 종이에 싸서 가져가라고 일러라. 그리고 당신, 알료샤, 미탸한테 내가 그들한테 파이를 정말로 보냈다고 꼭 전해 주세요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 그 말은 전하지 않겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"에! 당신은 그이가 그 일로 괴로워한다고 생각하시죠. 천만에요, 일부러 질투하는 거예요. 아무렇지도 않으면서.\" 그루셴카가 쓰라리게 말했다.\n\n\"일부러요?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어 봐요, 당신은 어리석어요, 알료샤. 그렇게 똑똑하면서도 이런 일은 도통 모른다니까요. 나는 그이가 나 같은 계집을 질투한다고 화가 나는 게 아니에요. 질투하지 않는다면 오히려 화가 나겠죠. 나는 그런 사람이에요. 질투에는 화가 안 나요. 나도 사나운 마음을 가졌거든요. 나도 질투할 줄 알아요. 다만 내가 화가 나는 건, 그이가 나를 조금도 사랑하지 않는다는 거예요. 지금 그이가 일부러 질투하고 있다니까요. 내가 눈이 멀었나요? 내가 못 볼 것 같아요? 그이가 방금 나한테 그 여자 이야기를, 카테리나 이야기를 꺼내더라고요, 그 여자가 이렇네 저렇네, 자기를 살려 보겠다고 모스크바에서 의사를 불러왔다느니, 가장 훌륭한, 가장 박식한 변호사까지 구해 줬다느니. 그러니 그이는 그 여자를 사랑하는 거예요, 내 면전에서 그 여자를 칭찬해 대다니, 그런 뻔뻔한 사람 같으니! 자기가 나한테 못되게 굴어 놓고는, 오히려 나를 몰아세우는 거예요, 내가 먼저 잘못한 걸로 꾸며서 죄다 나한테 뒤집어씌우려고요. '당신은 나보다 먼저 그 폴란드 사람이랑 같이 있었으니, 카테리나 일로 나를 탓할 수는 없지,' 결국 그런 얘기예요. 모든 죄를 나한테 뒤집어씌우려는 거죠. 일부러 나를 몰아세운 거예요, 일부러라니까요, 내 말 들어요, 하지만 나는——\"\n\n그루셴카는 자기가 어쩌겠다는 말을 끝맺지 못했다. 그녀는 손수건에 눈을 묻고 격하게 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"그분은 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하지 않아요.\" 알료샤가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 사랑하든 안 하든, 내가 곧 직접 알아낼 거예요.\" 그루셴카가 위협이 담긴 목소리로 말하며 눈에서 손수건을 떼었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 일그러져 있었다. 알료샤는 온화하고 평온하던 그 얼굴이 음울하고 앙심으로 가득해진 것을 슬프게 바라보았다.\n\n\"이런 어리석은 얘기는 이제 그만.\" 그녀가 갑자기 말했다. \"그것 때문에 당신을 부른 게 아니에요. 알료샤, 그대여, 내일—내일은 무슨 일이 벌어질까요? 그게 나를 괴롭혀요! 그리고 그걸 괴로워하는 건 나뿐이에요! 다들 둘러봐도 아무도 그 생각을 안 해요. 아무도 신경 쓰지 않아요. 당신은 그 생각이라도 하나요? 내일이면 그이가 재판을 받잖아요. 말해 봐요, 그이가 어떻게 재판을 받게 될까요? 그 하인이, 그 하인이 죽인 거 알잖아요! 맙소사! 하인 대신 그이에게 죄를 뒤집어씌울 수 있는 건가요, 아무도 그이 편을 들어 주지 않는 건가요? 그 하인은 아예 건드리지도 않은 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"그자는 호되게 심문을 받았어요.\" 알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"하지만 모두가 그자는 아니라는 결론에 이르렀어요. 지금 그자는 몹시 앓아누워 있어요. 그 발작 이후로 줄곧 앓고 있어요. 정말로 앓고 있어요.\" 알료샤가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오, 저런! 당신이 직접 그 변호사한테 가서 모든 걸 손수 다 이야기할 수는 없나요? 그 사람을 페테르부르크에서 삼천 루블에 데려왔다던데요.\"\n\n\"그 삼천 루블은 우리가 함께 냈어요—이반과 카테리나 이바노브나와 내가요—하지만 모스크바에서 의사를 부르는 데 든 이천 루블은 그녀가 혼자서 냈어요. 변호사 페튜코비치라면 더 많이 청구했겠지만, 이 사건이 온 러시아에 알려졌잖아요. 모든 신문과 잡지에서 떠들어 대니까요. 페튜코비치는 명성을 위해서 오겠다고 한 면이 더 커요, 사건이 그토록 유명해졌으니까요. 어제 그 사람을 만났어요.\"\n\n\"그래서요? 그 사람과 이야기는 나눠 봤나요?\" 그루셴카가 다급히 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그는 듣기만 하고 아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 자기는 이미 의견을 정했다고 하더군요. 그래도 내 말을 참작해 보겠다고는 약속했어요.\"\n\n\"참작이라니! 아, 다들 사기꾼이에요! 그들이 그이를 망쳐 놓을 거예요. 그런데 그 여자는 왜 의사를 불렀대요?\"\n\n\"감정인으로요. 그들은 미탸가 미쳤고, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르는 상태에서 살인을 저질렀다는 걸 입증하려는 거예요.\" 알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다. \"하지만 미탸는 거기에 동의하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그게 만일 그이가 정말 죽였다면 사실이었을 거예요!\" 그루셴카가 외쳤다. \"그때 그이는 미쳐 있었어요, 완전히 미쳐 있었고, 그건 다 내 잘못이에요, 못난 나 같은 것 때문에! 하지만 물론 그이는 그러지 않았어요, 그러지 않았다고요! 그런데도 다들 그이를 적으로 돌리고 있어요, 온 고을이 다요. 페냐의 증언조차 그이가 저질렀다는 걸 입증하는 쪽으로 갔어요. 가게 사람들도, 그 관리도, 술집에서도, 전에 사람들이 그이가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었다지 뭐예요! 다들, 다들 그이를 적으로 돌리고, 다들 그이를 향해 소리치고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 증거가 무섭게 쌓여 있어요.\" 알료샤가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그리고리—그리고리 바실리예비치—그 사람은 문이 열려 있었다는 자기 이야기를 끝까지 고집하고, 자기가 직접 봤다고 우겨 대고 있어요—도무지 그 사람을 흔들 수가 없어요. 내가 직접 가서 이야기해 봤거든요. 그 일에 대해서는 무례하기까지 하더라고요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그게 어쩌면 그분에게 가장 불리한 증거일 거예요.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 미탸가 미쳤다는 거 말인데요, 정말이지 지금 보면 그래 보이긴 해요.\" 그루셴카가 유난히 불안하고 알 수 없는 기색으로 입을 뗐다. \"그거 아세요, 알료샤, 이 얘기를 당신한테 하고 싶었던 게 오래됐어요. 나는 날마다 그이를 찾아가는데, 그저 그이가 의아할 뿐이에요. 자, 말해 봐요, 그이가 늘 무슨 이야기를 하는 것 같아요? 말하고 또 말하는데, 나는 도무지 갈피를 못 잡겠어요. 처음엔 내가 어리석어 알아듣지 못하는 무슨 어려운 얘기를 하는가 싶었어요. 그런데 갑자기 어떤 아기에 대해서, 그러니까 무슨 어린아이에 대해서 말하기 시작하는 거예요. '왜 그 아기는 가난한 거지?' 그러더라고요. '바로 그 아기 때문에 내가 이제 시베리아로 가는 거야. 나는 살인자가 아니지만, 시베리아로 가야만 해!' 그게 무슨 뜻인지, 무슨 아기인지, 나는 죽었다 깨어나도 알 수가 없었어요. 다만 그이가 그 말을 할 때 나는 울었어요, 어찌나 다정하게 말하던지요. 그이도 울고, 나도 울었어요. 그이는 갑자기 나한테 입을 맞추고 내 위로 성호를 그어 주었어요. 그게 무슨 뜻일까요, 알료샤, 말해 봐요? 그 아기가 뭘까요?\"\n\n\"분명 라키틴일 거예요, 요즘 그분을 찾아가고 있으니까요.\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"하긴…… 그건 라키틴이 시킨 일은 아니지만요. 어제는 미탸를 못 봤어요. 오늘 만나 봐야겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 라키틴이 아니에요. 그건 그이 동생인 이반 표도로비치가 그이를 뒤흔들어 놓는 거예요. 그 사람이 그이를 찾아가는 것, 그게 문제예요.\" 그루셴카가 입을 떼다가 갑자기 말을 뚝 끊었다. 알료샤는 놀라서 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이반이 찾아간다고요? 그가 그분을 만나러 왔었나요? 미탸가 직접 나한테 이반은 한 번도 안 왔다고 했는데요.\"\n\n\"저런…… 저런! 내가 무슨 계집애람! 함부로 입을 놀리다니!\" 그루셴카가 당황해서 갑자기 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"잠깐만요, 알료샤, 쉿! 이왕 이만큼 말했으니 진실을 다 말할게요—그 사람은 그이를 두 번 찾아갔어요, 첫 번째는 도착하자마자였죠. 물론 모스크바에서 단숨에 말을 달려 이리로 왔어요, 내가 앓아눕기도 전이었죠. 그리고 두 번째는 일주일 전이었어요. 그 사람은 미탸한테 무슨 일이 있어도 당신한테 이 얘기를 하지 말라고, 사실 아무한테도 말하지 말라고 했대요. 그 사람은 몰래 온 거예요.\"\n\n알료샤는 생각에 잠겨 무언가를 곰곰이 따져 보며 앉아 있었다. 그 소식은 분명 그에게 깊은 인상을 남긴 것이었다.\n\n\"이반은 나한테 미탸의 사건 이야기를 하지 않아요.\" 그가 천천히 말했다. \"요 두 달 동안 나한테 거의 말이 없었어요. 그리고 내가 찾아갈 때마다 내가 오는 걸 언짢아하는 것 같아서, 지난 세 주 동안은 그를 찾아가지 않았어요. 흠!…… 일주일 전에 그가 거기 있었다면…… 정말이지 이번 주에 미탸에게 변화가 있긴 했어요.\"\n\n\"변화가 있었어요.\" 그루셴카가 재빨리 맞장구쳤다. \"그들에겐 비밀이 있어요, 비밀이 있다고요! 미탸가 직접 나한테 비밀이 있다고 말했어요, 그것도 미탸가 도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없을 만큼 큰 비밀이라고요. 전에는 명랑했어요—사실 지금도 명랑하긴 해요—그런데 그렇게 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서, 알죠, 방 안을 성큼성큼 거닐고 오른손으로 오른쪽 관자놀이 머리카락을 자꾸 잡아당기면, 나는 그이 마음속에 그이를 괴롭히는 무언가가 있다는 걸 알아요…… 안다고요! 전에는 명랑했는데, 하긴 오늘도 명랑하긴 해요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분이 불안해한다고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, 불안해하면서도 또 명랑해요. 잠깐 짜증을 내다가 또 명랑해지고 또 짜증을 내요. 그리고 그거 아세요, 알료샤, 나는 늘 그이가 의아해요—그렇게 끔찍한 일이 그이 머리 위에 드리워져 있는데도, 가끔은 마치 자기가 아기라도 된 양 그런 시시한 일에 깔깔 웃거든요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그분이 정말로 당신한테 이반에 대해 나한테 말하지 말라고 했나요? '그한테 말하지 마'라고 했어요?\"\n\n\"네, 그랬어요, '그한테 말하지 마.' 미탸가 가장 두려워하는 게 바로 당신이에요. 그게 비밀이니까요. 그이 자신이 비밀이라고 했어요. 알료샤, 그대여, 그이한테 가서 그들의 비밀이 뭔지 알아내고 와서 나한테 말해 줘요.\" 그루셴카가 갑자기 간절하게 그에게 애원했다. \"나한테 닥칠 최악의 일이 뭔지 알 수 있게 내 마음을 편하게 해 줘요. 그래서 당신을 부른 거예요.\"\n\n\"그게 당신과 관련된 일이라고 생각하세요? 만일 그렇다면, 그분이 당신한테 비밀이 있다는 말조차 하지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"모르겠어요. 어쩌면 나한테 말하고 싶은데 차마 못 하는 건지도 모르죠. 나한테 미리 일러두는 거예요. 비밀이 있다고는 하면서, 그게 뭔지는 말해 주지 않아요.\"\n\n\"당신 생각은 어때요?\"\n\n\"내 생각이요? 나한테는 끝장이에요, 그게 내 생각이에요. 그들 셋이서 내 끝장을 꾸미고 있어요, 카테리나가 거기 끼어 있으니까요. 모두 카테리나 때문이에요, 모두 그 여자한테서 비롯된 거예요. 그 여자가 이렇네 저렇네 하는 건, 곧 내가 아니라는 뜻이죠. 그이는 그걸 미리 나한테 일러두는 거예요—경고하는 거죠. 나를 내팽개칠 작정인 거예요, 그게 비밀의 전부예요. 그들이 함께 꾸민 거예요, 셋이서요—미탸, 카테리나, 그리고 이반 표도로비치가요. 알료샤, 오래전부터 당신한테 묻고 싶었어요. 일주일 전에 그이가 갑자기 나한테 이반이 카테리나를 사랑한다고 말했어요, 자주 그 여자를 만나러 간다면서요. 그이가 나한테 사실을 말한 건가요, 아닌가요? 말해 봐요, 양심을 걸고, 최악의 것을 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"거짓말은 하지 않을게요. 내 생각에 이반은 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"오, 나도 그렇게 생각했어요! 그이가 나한테 거짓말을 한 거예요, 뻔뻔한 사기꾼 같으니, 바로 그거예요! 그리고 방금 나를 질투한 것도, 나중에 죄를 나한테 뒤집어씌우려는 거였어요. 어리석은 사람이라 자기가 하는 짓을 숨기지도 못해요. 워낙 속이 다 들여다보이는 사람이거든요, 알죠…… 하지만 내가 본때를 보여 주겠어요, 본때를 보여 주겠다고요! '당신은 내가 그랬다고 믿잖아,' 그러더라고요. 나한테, 나한테 그런 말을 했어요. 나를 그걸로 나무라다니! 하느님이 그이를 용서하시길! 두고 봐요, 재판에서 카테리나를 혼쭐을 내 주겠어요! 그때 한마디만 하면…… 그때 다 까발릴 거예요!\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 다시 쓰라리게 울었다.\n\n\"이것만은 확실히 말해 줄 수 있어요, 그루셴카.\" 알료샤가 일어서며 말했다. \"첫째, 그분은 당신을 사랑해요, 세상 누구보다도 당신을 사랑하고, 오직 당신만을 사랑해요, 내 말을 믿어요. 나는 알아요. 정말로 알아요. 둘째는, 나는 그분에게서 비밀을 캐낼 생각은 없지만, 만일 그분이 오늘 자기 입으로 말해 준다면, 나는 당신한테 전하기로 약속했다고 그분한테 솔직히 말하겠어요. 그러고 나서 오늘 당신한테 와서 말해 줄게요. 다만…… 내 생각엔…… 카테리나 이바노브나와는 아무 상관이 없고, 그 비밀은 뭔가 다른 일에 관한 거예요. 그건 분명해요. 내가 보기엔 카테리나 이바노브나에 관한 일일 것 같지가 않아요. 그럼 이만 가 볼게요.\"\n\n알료샤는 그녀와 악수했다. 그루셴카는 여전히 울고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 자신의 위로를 거의 믿지 않는다는 것을 알았으나, 그래도 슬픔을 다 털어놓고 그것을 입 밖에 낸 덕에 그녀의 마음은 한결 나아져 있었다. 그런 심정의 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 그는 안타까웠으나, 그는 마음이 급했다. 아직 해야 할 일이 산더미처럼 많았던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter II. The Injured Foot\n\n그 첫 번째 일은 호흘라코바 부인의 집에서 있을 터였고, 그는 미탸에게 너무 늦지 않으려고 가능한 한 빨리 끝내 버릴 작정으로 그리로 서둘러 갔다. 호흘라코바 부인은 지난 삼 주 동안 가벼운 병을 앓고 있었으니, 무슨 까닭에서인지 발이 부어올라, 자리보전을 하지는 않았으나 종일토록 자기 규방의 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기대어 누운 채로, 매혹적이면서도 단정한 실내복 차림으로 지내고 있었다. 알료샤는 언젠가 천진한 재미를 느끼며 알아챈 적이 있었는데, 호흘라코바 부인이 병중임에도 불구하고 제법 멋을 부리기 시작했다는 것이니—머리 위로 틀어 올린 장식이며 리본이며 헐렁한 실내 가운 따위가 등장했던 것이라, 그는 그 까닭을 어렴풋이 짐작하면서도 그런 생각들은 경박한 것이라 여겨 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버렸다. 지난 두 달 동안 젊은 관리 페르호틴이 이 집의 단골손님이 되어 있었다.\n\n알료샤는 나흘 동안 발걸음을 하지 않았던 터라 곧장 리즈에게로 가려고 서둘렀으니, 그가 이야기를 나눠야 할 상대는 바로 그녀였기 때문이었다. 리즈가 전날 그에게 하녀를 보내어 특별히 \"아주 중요한 일로\" 찾아와 달라고 부탁했던 것인데, 그 청은 어떤 이유에서인지 알료샤의 관심을 끌었다. 그러나 하녀가 그의 이름을 리즈에게 전하러 간 사이에, 호흘라코바 부인이 누군가에게서 그가 왔다는 소식을 듣고는 즉시 사람을 보내어 \"잠깐만이라도\" 자기에게 들러 달라고 청했다. 알료샤는 어머니 쪽의 청을 들어주는 편이 낫겠다고 판단했으니, 그렇지 않으면 그가 머무는 내내 매 순간 리즈의 방으로 사람을 내려보낼 것이기 때문이었다. 호흘라코바 부인은 긴 의자에 누워 있었다. 그녀는 유난히 맵시 있게 차려입고 있었고 누가 보아도 극도의 신경 흥분 상태에 빠져 있었다. 그녀는 환희에 찬 외침으로 알료샤를 맞이했다.\n\n\"오래, 정말 오래, 한참 만이로군요, 당신을 못 본 지! 꼬박 일주일이에요—생각 좀 해 보세요! 아니, 그런데 당신은 나흘 전, 수요일에 여기 오셨던가요. 리즈를 보러 오신 거지요. 분명 내가 못 듣게 발끝으로 살금살금 그 애 방으로 빠져나갈 작정이셨을 거예요. 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 그 애 때문에 얼마나 애를 태우는지 당신이 알기만 한다면! 하지만 그건 나중에, 그게 제일 중요한 일이긴 해도, 그건 나중에요. 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 내 리즈를 당신께 온전히 믿고 맡기겠어요. 조시마 장로님이 돌아가신 뒤로—그분의 영혼에 평안이 깃들기를!\"(그녀는 성호를 그었다)\"—나는 당신을 수도자처럼 여기고 있어요, 그 새 옷을 입으니 참 매력적이긴 하지만요. 이런 시골에서 어디서 그런 재단사를 찾으셨어요? 아니, 아니에요, 그게 중요한 게 아니지요—그건 나중에요. 내가 가끔 당신을 알료샤라고 부르는 걸 용서해 줘요. 나처럼 늙은 여자야 좀 허물없이 굴어도 되지 않겠어요.\" 그녀는 교태 어린 미소를 지었다. \"하지만 그것도 나중에 하기로 해요. 중요한 건 내가 중요한 것을 잊지 말아야 한다는 거예요. 부디 당신이 직접 일깨워 줘요. 내 혀가 마구 달아나기 시작하거든, 그냥 '중요한 일은요?' 하고 말해 줘요. 아아! 지금 무엇이 가장 중요한지 내가 어찌 알겠어요? 리즈가 당신과 결혼하겠다던 약속을—그 애다운 약속이었지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치—거두어들인 뒤로, 당신도 물론 깨달으셨겠지만, 그건 그토록 오래 의자에 매여 있던 병든 아이의 장난스러운 공상에 지나지 않았던 거예요—다행히도, 이제 그 애는 걸을 수 있어요!… 카탸가 당신의 불행한 형님을 위해 모스크바에서 불러온 그 새 의사 말이에요, 내일이면—아니, 내일 얘기를 왜 하겠어요? 나는 내일을 생각하기만 해도 죽을 것만 같아요. 궁금해서 죽을 지경이에요…. 그 의사가 어제 우리 집에 와서 리즈를 봤어요…. 그 진찰비로 오십 루블을 줬지요. 하지만 그게 요점이 아니에요, 또 그게 요점이 아니라니까요. 보세요, 나는 모든 걸 뒤죽박죽 섞고 있어요. 너무 마음이 급해서요. 내가 왜 이리 서두를까요? 모르겠어요. 무얼 도무지 이해할 수 없게 되어 가는 것 같아서 끔찍해요. 모든 게 일종의 엉킨 실타래처럼 뒤섞여 버린 것 같아요. 당신이 너무 지루해서 벌떡 일어나 달아나 버릴까 봐 겁이 나요, 그러면 내가 당신을 보는 것도 그게 마지막일 테니까요. 어머나! 우리가 왜 여기 앉아 있는데 커피가 없을까요? 율리야, 글라피라, 커피!\"\n\n알료샤는 서둘러 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고는, 방금 막 커피를 마시고 왔다고 말했다.\n\n\"어디서요?\"\n\n\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나 댁에서요.\"\n\n\"그… 그 여자 집에서요? 아아, 그 여자가 모든 사람을 파멸로 몰아넣었지요. 하긴 나는 그 일에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요. 사람들 말로는 그 여자가 성녀가 됐다더군요, 좀 때늦은 감이 있긴 하지만요. 진작에 그랬으면 좋았을걸. 이제 와서 무슨 소용이겠어요? 쉿, 쉿, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신에게 할 말이 너무 많아서 결국엔 아무것도 못 할까 봐 겁이 나요. 이 끔찍한 재판이…. 나는 꼭 갈 거예요, 지금 준비를 하고 있어요. 의자에 실려서라도 그리로 갈 거예요. 게다가 나는 앉아 있을 수도 있어요. 사람들도 데려갈 거고요. 그리고, 아시겠지만, 나는 증인이거든요. 내가 어떻게 말해야 하지요, 어떻게 말해야 하지요? 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 선서를 해야 하지요, 그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n\"예. 하지만 부인께서 가실 수 있을 것 같지는 않은데요.\"\n\n\"나는 앉아 있을 수 있어요. 아아, 당신이 날 헷갈리게 하잖아요! 아아! 이 재판, 이 야만스러운 짓, 그러고는 모두 시베리아로 가고, 누구는 결혼을 하고, 이 모든 게 이리도 빨리, 이리도 빨리, 모든 게 변해 버리고, 결국엔—아무것도 남지 않아요. 다들 늙어 가고 죽음만을 앞두게 되지요. 뭐, 그렇다면 그러라죠! 나는 지쳤어요. 이 카탸, 그 사랑스러운 사람이, 내 모든 희망을 저버렸어요. 이제 그 애는 당신의 형님들 중 하나를 따라 시베리아로 가려 하고, 또 다른 형님은 그 애를 따라가 가장 가까운 읍내에 살려 한다니, 그러면 다들 서로를 괴롭히게 될 거예요. 그 생각만 하면 미칠 것만 같아요. 무엇보다도—그 세상의 입방아 말이에요. 그 이야기가 모스크바와 페테르부르크의 온갖 신문에 백만 번도 더 실렸어요. 아아! 그래, 당신은 믿을 수 있겠어요, 내가 당신 형님의 '정다운 벗'이었다는 구절이 실렸다니까요——그 끔찍한 단어는 차마 입에 담을 수가 없어요. 상상이나 해 보세요, 상상이나!\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요! 그 구절이 어디 실렸지요? 뭐라고 쓰여 있었어요?\"\n\n\"곧 보여 드릴게요. 어제 그 신문을 구해서 읽었어요. 여기, 페테르부르크 신문 〈소문〉이에요. 그 신문이 올해부터 나오기 시작했지요. 나는 소문이라면 무척 좋아해서 그 신문을 구독하는데, 이제 와서 그것이 내게 앙갚음을 하는군요—소문이란 게 결국 이 꼴이 되는 거예요! 여기 있어요, 여기, 이 대목이에요. 읽어 보세요.\"\n\n그러면서 그녀는 베개 밑에 넣어 두었던 신문 한 장을 알료샤에게 건넸다.\n\n그녀는 정확히 말해 흥분했다기보다는 압도당한 듯이 보였고, 어쩌면 정말로 머릿속의 모든 것이 한데 뒤엉켜 있는지도 몰랐다. 그 구절은 아주 전형적인 것이라 그녀에게는 큰 충격이었을 게 분명하지만, 어쩌면 다행스럽게도 그녀는 그 순간 어느 한 가지 주제에 정신을 붙들어 둘 수가 없었으니, 그래서 잠깐 사이에 다른 것으로 달아나 버려 신문 따위는 까맣게 잊어버릴 수도 있는 것이었다.\n\n알료샤는 그 끔찍한 사건의 이야기가 온 러시아에 퍼졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고, 오 하느님! 자기 형에 관하여, 카라마조프 집안에 관하여, 또 자기 자신에 관하여, 그 두 달 동안 그가 읽은 그 밖의 똑같이 믿을 만한 항목들 가운데 얼마나 터무니없는 소문들이 떠돌았던가! 어느 신문은 심지어 그가 형의 범죄에 경악한 나머지 수도원에 들어가 수도자가 되었다고까지 했다. 또 다른 신문은 이를 반박하며, 그와 그의 장로인 조시마 신부가 수도원의 금고를 부수고 들어가 \"수도원에서 줄행랑을 쳤다\"고 했다. 〈소문〉지에 실린 이번 구절은 \"스코토프리고니옙스크의 카라마조프 사건\"이라는 표제 아래 실려 있었다.(아아, 그것이 우리 작은 읍의 이름이었다. 나는 지금껏 그 이름을 숨겨 왔다.) 그것은 짤막했고, 호흘라코바 부인이 직접 언급되지는 않았다. 사실 어떤 이름도 등장하지 않았다. 다만 이렇게만 적혀 있었으니, 곧 임박한 재판으로 그토록 큰 화제를 불러일으키고 있는 그 범인—퇴역한 육군 대위로서, 빈둥거리는 허풍쟁이이자 반동적인 망나니인 그자가—끊임없이 연애 사건에 휘말려 들었고, 특히 \"고독 속에서 시들어 가던\" 어떤 부인들에게 유달리 인기가 있었다는 것이었다. 그런 부인들 가운데 하나, 곧 시들어 가던 어느 과부는, 다 자란 딸이 있으면서도 젊어 보이려 애쓰는 여자였는데, 그자에게 어찌나 홀딱 빠졌던지 범행이 있기 불과 두 시간 전에 자기와 함께 금광으로 달아나 준다면 삼천 루블을 주겠다고 그자에게 제안했다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 범인은 처벌을 모면할 속셈으로, 시들어 가던 그 부인의 중년의 매력과 함께 시베리아로 떠나느니 차라리 삼천 루블을 손에 넣으려고 제 아비를 죽이는 편을 택했다는 것이었다. 이 장난기 어린 구절은 물론, 친부 살해의 사악함과 근래 폐지된 농노제에 대한 너그러운 분노의 폭발로 마무리되었다. 호기심에 차서 그것을 읽고 난 알료샤는 신문을 접어 호흘라코바 부인에게 도로 건넸다.\n\n\"자, 그게 분명 나일 거예요.\" 그녀가 다시 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"물론 나를 가리키는 거예요. 한 시간 남짓밖에 안 됐어요, 내가 그이에게 금광 얘기를 꺼낸 게, 그런데 여기서는 그게 마치 내 속셈이었던 것처럼 '중년의 매력'이라고 떠들어 대는군요! 그이는 앙심을 품고 그걸 쓴 거예요! 전능하신 하느님께서 그 중년의 매력 운운한 것을 용서해 주시길, 나도 그이를 용서하듯이! 당신도 알겠지만, 그게—그게 누군지 아세요? 당신 친구 라키틴이에요.\"\n\n\"아마도요.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"하긴 저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 듣지 못했지만요.\"\n\n\"그자예요, 그자라니까요! '아마도'랄 게 없어요. 알다시피 내가 그자를 집에서 쫓아냈잖아요…. 당신도 그 이야기를 다 알고 있지요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"부인께서 그에게 앞으로는 찾아오지 말라고 하셨다는 건 알지만, 무슨 까닭에서였는지는 듣지 못했습니다… 적어도 부인에게서는요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 그자한테서 들었군요! 그자가 나를 헐뜯었겠지요, 끔찍하게 헐뜯었겠지요?\"\n\n\"예, 그러긴 합니다. 하지만 그는 누구든 다 헐뜯으니까요. 그래도 부인께서 왜 그와 인연을 끊으셨는지는 그에게서도 듣지 못했습니다. 실은 요즘 그를 아주 가끔밖에 만나지 않아요. 우리는 친구 사이가 아니거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 좋아요, 내가 다 이야기해 드리지요. 어쩔 수 없는 일이니, 고백할게요, 어쩌면 내가 잘못한 점이 한 가지 있었으니까요. 다만 아주 작은, 작은 점이라, 너무 작아서 어쩌면 셈에도 안 들어갈 정도예요. 보세요, 사랑하는 나의 도련님—\" 호흘라코바 부인은 갑자기 짓궂은 표정을 짓더니 매혹적이면서도 알 수 없는 미소를 입가에 띄웠다. \"—보세요, 내 생각엔 말이에요… 용서해 줘야 해요, 알료샤. 나는 당신에게 어머니나 다름없으니까요…. 아니, 아니에요, 정반대예요. 지금 나는 당신을 마치 내 아버지인 양 여기고 말하는 거예요—어머니라는 건 영 어울리지 않아요. 자, 그러니까 마치 내가 조시마 신부님께 고해를 하는 것 같다고 해 두지요, 바로 그거예요. 아까 내가 당신을 수도자라고 불렀잖아요. 자, 그 가엾은 젊은이, 당신 친구 라키틴이(저런, 가엾어라! 나는 그자한테 화를 낼 수가 없네요. 부아가 치밀긴 해도 그리 심하진 않아요), 그 경박한 젊은이가, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 나에게 반해 버릴 생각을 머릿속에 품은 모양이에요. 나중에야 그걸 알아챘지요. 처음에는—한 달 전이었어요—그저 나를 더 자주 보러 오기 시작했어요, 거의 매일같이요. 물론 우리는 전부터 아는 사이였지만요. 나는 아무것도 몰랐어요… 그러다 갑자기 깨달았고, 놀라면서 이것저것 눈여겨보기 시작했지요. 알다시피, 두 달 전에 그 점잖고 매력적이고 훌륭한 젊은이, 여기서 관직에 있는 표트르 일리치 페르호틴이 이 집의 단골손님이 되기 시작했어요. 당신도 여기서 그를 여러 번 만났잖아요. 그는 훌륭하고 진지한 젊은이지요, 안 그래요? 그는 사흘에 한 번씩 와요, 매일은 아니고요(나야 매일 봐도 좋겠지만요), 그리고 늘 그렇게 잘 차려입고 오지요. 대체로 나는 젊은 사람들을 좋아해요, 알료샤, 당신처럼 재능 있고 겸손한 사람들 말이에요, 그리고 그는 거의 정치가다운 두뇌를 지녔어요, 어찌나 매력적으로 말을 하는지, 나는 꼭, 반드시 그가 승진하도록 힘써 볼 거예요. 그는 장래의 외교관이에요. 그 끔찍한 날에 그는 밤중에 찾아와 줘서 나를 죽음에서 거의 구해 준 셈이지요. 그런데 당신 친구 라키틴은 그런 장화를 신고 와서는, 늘 그걸 양탄자 위에 쭉 뻗어 놓는다니까요…. 어쨌든 그자는 자기 속마음을 넌지시 비치기 시작했고, 어느 날 돌아가려다가 내 손을 어찌나 세게 꽉 쥐던지요. 그자가 그렇게 내 손을 누른 직후에 곧바로 내 발이 부어오르기 시작했어요. 그자는 전에도 여기서 표트르 일리치를 만난 적이 있었는데, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 무슨 까닭에선지 늘 그를 빈정거리고 으르렁대지 뭐예요. 나는 그저 그 둘이서 그렇게 하는 꼴을 지켜보면서 속으로 웃었지요. 그래서 내가 여기 혼자 앉아 있었는데—아니, 그때는 자리보전을 하고 있었지요. 자, 내가 여기 혼자 누워 있는데 갑자기 라키틴이 들어와서는, 글쎄 상상해 보세요! 자기가 직접 지은 시를 가져왔지 뭐예요—내 아픈 발에 관한 짤막한 시였어요. 그러니까 내 발을 시로 묘사한 거예요. 잠깐만요—어떻게 됐더라?\n\n매혹적인 작은 발이여.\n\n그렇게 시작했던 것 같아요. 나는 도통 시를 외우질 못해요. 여기 가지고 있어요. 나중에 보여 드릴게요. 하지만 매력적인 거예요—매력적이지요. 그리고, 알다시피, 발에 관한 것만이 아니라 훌륭한 교훈도 담겨 있었어요, 매력적인 발상이었는데, 다만 내가 잊어버렸어요. 사실 꼭 앨범에나 어울릴 만한 것이었지요. 그래서 물론 나는 고맙다고 했고, 그자는 보아하니 우쭐해진 모양이었어요. 내가 미처 고마움을 다 표하기도 전에 표트르 일리치가 들어왔는데, 그 순간 라키틴의 얼굴이 갑자기 밤처럼 시커메지지 뭐예요. 표트르 일리치가 거치적거린다는 걸 나는 알 수 있었어요, 라키틴이 시를 건네준 뒤에 분명 무슨 말을 하고 싶어 했으니까요. 나는 그걸 예감했지요. 그런데 표트르 일리치가 들어온 거예요. 나는 표트르 일리치에게 그 시를 보여 주면서 누가 쓴 것인지는 말하지 않았어요. 하지만 나는 그가 알아챘다고 확신해요, 비록 오늘날까지도 그걸 인정하지 않고, 전혀 짐작도 못 했다고 우기지만요. 하지만 그는 일부러 그러는 거예요. 표트르 일리치는 곧장 웃기 시작하더니 그것을 헐뜯기 시작했어요. '형편없는 엉터리 시로군요' 하고 그가 말하더군요, '어느 신학생이 쓴 게 분명해요' 하면서, 어찌나 격하게, 어찌나 격하게 그러던지요! 그러자 당신 친구는 웃기는커녕 버럭 화를 내며 발끈했지요. '저런!' 나는 생각했어요, '저러다 서로 달려들겠어.' '그건 내가 쓴 거요' 하고 그자가 말했어요. '농담 삼아 쓴 거요' 하고 말이지요, '나는 시 따위 짓는 게 천박하다고 생각하니까…. 하지만 그건 좋은 시요. 사람들이 여자 발에 대해 썼다고 당신네 푸시킨한테는 기념비를 세워 주려 하면서, 나는 교훈적인 목적으로 썼는데도, 당신은' 하고 그자가 말했어요, '농노제 옹호자요. 당신에겐 인도주의적인 사상이라곤 없소' 하고 말이에요. '당신에겐 현대의 계몽된 감정이 없고, 진보의 영향도 받지 않았으며, 그저 한낱 관리에 불과하오' 하고 그자가 말했어요, '게다가 당신은 뇌물이나 받아먹지.' 그래서 나는 비명을 지르며 두 사람에게 애원하기 시작했지요. 그런데, 알다시피, 표트르 일리치는 겁쟁이라곤 도무지 아니에요. 그는 즉시 더없이 신사다운 어조를 취하고는, 그자를 비웃듯 바라보면서, 가만히 듣다가 사과를 했어요. '몰랐습니다' 하고 그가 말했어요. '알았더라면 그런 말을 하지 않았을 겁니다. 칭찬을 했겠지요. 시인이란 다들 그렇게 신경이 예민하니까요' 하고 말이에요. 한마디로, 그는 더없이 신사다운 어조로 그자를 비웃은 거예요. 나중에 그는 그게 다 빈정거린 거였다고 내게 설명해 주더군요. 나는 그가 진심인 줄로만 알았는데 말이에요. 다만 내가 거기 누워서, 꼭 지금 당신 앞에서처럼, 이런 생각을 했어요, '내 집에서 손님에게 그토록 무례하게 소리를 질러 댄다고 라키틴을 쫓아내는 것이 마땅한 일일까, 아닐까?' 하고요. 그리고, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 나는 여기 누워서 눈을 감고는, 그게 마땅한 일일까 아닐까 곰곰이 생각했어요. 자꾸자꾸 마음을 졸였고, 심장이 마구 뛰기 시작했는데, 소리를 내질러야 할지 말아야 할지 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었어요. 한쪽 목소리는 내게 '말해' 하는 것 같았고, 다른 쪽은 '아니, 말하지 마' 하는 것 같았어요. 그런데 그 두 번째 목소리가 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게 나는 비명을 지르고 기절해 버렸지요. 물론 한바탕 소동이 났어요. 나는 벌떡 일어나서 라키틴에게 말했어요, '이런 말을 하기는 괴롭지만, 다시는 내 집에서 당신을 보고 싶지 않아요.' 그렇게 그자를 쫓아냈지요. 아아! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 잘못했다는 건 나 자신도 알아요. 나는 꾸민 거였어요. 사실은 그자에게 조금도 화가 나지 않았거든요. 하지만 갑자기 그런 생각이 들었어요—바로 그게 일을 그르친 거예요—그게 참 멋진 장면이 될 거라는 생각이요…. 그런데도, 믿어 주세요, 그건 아주 자연스러운 일이었어요, 정말로 나는 눈물을 흘렸고 그 뒤로 며칠이나 울었으니까요, 그러다 갑자기, 어느 날 오후, 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버렸지요. 그래서 그자가 여기 오지 않은 지 보름이 됐는데, 나는 그자가 다시 올까 자꾸 궁금해했어요. 어제도 궁금해했지요, 그런데 갑자기 어젯밤에 이 〈소문〉이 온 거예요. 나는 그걸 읽고 숨이 막혔어요. 누가 그걸 썼겠어요? 분명 그자가 쓴 거예요. 집에 가서, 앉아서, 그 자리에서 써서, 보냈고, 신문사에서 그걸 실은 거예요. 그게 보름 전이었잖아요, 아시겠어요. 하지만, 알료샤, 끔찍하게도 나는 자꾸 떠들기만 하고 정작 하고 싶은 말은 하지 못하고 있네요. 아아! 말들이 제멋대로 쏟아져 나와요!\"\n\n\"오늘은 제때에 형님을 만나는 것이 저로서는 무척 중요합니다.\" 알료샤가 우물거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요! 당신이 그걸 다시 떠올려 주는군요. 들어 봐요, 정신착란이라는 게 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"무슨 정신착란 말씀입니까?\" 알료샤가 의아해하며 물었다.\n\n\"법률상의 의미로요. 모든 게 용서받을 수 있는 그런 정신착란 말이에요. 무슨 짓을 했든 즉시 무죄로 풀려나게 되는 거요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"내가 이야기해 드리지요. 이 카탸가… 아아! 그 애는 사랑스럽고 또 사랑스러운 사람이에요, 다만 그 애가 대체 누구를 사랑하는지 도무지 알아낼 수가 없어요. 얼마 전에 그 애가 나한테 왔었는데 도통 아무것도 캐낼 수가 없었어요. 더구나 그 애는 이제 나하고는 겉도는 이야기밖에 안 하니까요. 늘 내 건강 얘기만 하고 다른 건 일절 안 해요, 게다가 나한테 그런 어조까지 취한다니까요. 나는 그저 혼잣말을 했지요, '그래, 그러라지. 난 상관 안 해'… 아, 그래요. 정신착란 얘기를 하고 있었지요. 그 의사가 왔어요. 의사가 왔다는 거 아세요? 물론 아시겠지요—미치광이를 가려내는 그 의사 말이에요. 당신이 사람을 보내 불렀잖아요. 아니, 당신이 아니라 카탸였지요. 다 카탸가 한 일이에요. 자, 보세요, 사람이 멀쩡하게 제정신으로 앉아 있다가도 갑자기 정신착란을 일으킬 수가 있는 거예요. 의식이 또렷하고 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알면서도 정신착란 상태에 빠질 수 있다는 거지요. 그리고 드미트리 표도로비치가 정신착란을 앓고 있었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요. 사람들이 정신착란이라는 걸 알아낸 건 법원이 개혁되자마자였어요. 다 개혁된 법원의 좋은 효과인 거지요. 그 의사가 여기 와서 그날 저녁 일을, 금광 얘기를 나한테 캐물었어요. '그때 그가 어떻게 보이던가요?' 하고 묻더군요. 그는 분명 정신착란 상태였을 거예요. 그가 들어와서 소리쳤거든요, '돈, 돈, 삼천 루블! 삼천 루블을 주시오!' 하고요, 그러고는 나가서 곧장 살인을 저질렀지요. '나는 그를 죽이고 싶지 않아' 하고 그가 말했는데, 그러고는 갑자기 가서 그를 죽인 거예요. 그래서 사람들이 그를 무죄로 풀어 줄 거예요, 그가 그것에 맞서 발버둥 쳤는데도 결국 그를 죽였으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 형님은 아버지를 죽이지 않았습니다.\" 알료샤가 다소 날카롭게 끼어들었다. 그는 불안과 초조함으로 점점 더 속이 메스꺼워지고 있었다.\n\n\"그래요, 나도 알아요, 그를 죽인 건 그 늙은이 그리고리였지요.\"\n\n\"그리고리가요?\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 그리고리였어요. 드미트리 표도로비치가 그를 쳐서 쓰러뜨린 자리에 누워 있다가, 그러고는 일어나, 문이 열려 있는 걸 보고, 안으로 들어가 표도르 파블로비치를 죽인 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜요, 어째서요?\"\n\n\"정신착란을 앓고 있었으니까요. 드미트리 표도로비치가 머리를 후려친 그 충격에서 깨어났을 때, 그는 정신착란을 앓고 있었던 거예요. 그래서 가서 살인을 저지른 거지요. 그가 자기는 안 그랬다고 하는 건, 아마 기억을 못 하는 거겠지요. 다만, 알다시피, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그를 죽인 편이 더 나아요, 훨씬 더 나아요. 그리고 그렇게 된 게 분명해요, 비록 내가 그리고리라고 말하긴 하지만요. 분명 드미트리 표도로비치였고, 그게 더 나아요, 훨씬 더 나아요! 아아! 아들이 제 아비를 죽이는 게 더 낫다는 말은 아니에요, 나는 그걸 두둔하는 게 아니에요. 자식은 마땅히 부모를 공경해야지요, 그래도 그가 그랬다면 더 나을 거예요, 그러면 당신이 슬퍼할 게 없어질 테니까요, 그는 무의식 상태에서, 아니 차라리 의식은 있었으나 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지는 몰랐던 상태에서 그 짓을 한 거니까요. 사람들이 그를 무죄로 풀어 주라죠—그건 참으로 인도적인 일이고, 개혁된 법원이 얼마나 큰 축복인지를 보여 줄 거예요. 나는 그런 건 아무것도 몰랐는데, 사람들 말로는 오래전부터 그래 왔다더군요. 그리고 어제 그 얘기를 들었을 때 나는 어찌나 큰 충격을 받았던지 당장 당신을 부르고 싶었어요. 그리고 만일 그가 무죄로 풀려나거든, 그를 법원에서 곧장 우리 집으로 데려와 나와 함께 저녁을 들게 해 줘요, 그러면 나는 친구들을 불러 모아 잔치를 벌이고, 우리 다 함께 개혁된 법원을 위해 축배를 들 거예요. 그가 위험할 거라곤 생각하지 않아요. 게다가 나는 아주 많은 친구들을 초대할 테니, 그가 무슨 짓을 하더라도 언제든 끌어낼 수 있을 거예요. 그리고 그다음엔 그를 다른 읍의 치안판사 같은 자리에 앉히면 될 거예요, 자기 자신이 곤경을 겪어 본 사람이야말로 가장 훌륭한 재판관이 되는 법이니까요. 게다가, 요즘 정신착란을 앓지 않는 사람이 어디 있겠어요?—당신도, 나도, 우리 모두가 정신착란 상태에 있는 거예요, 그런 예는 얼마든지 있어요. 어떤 사람이 노래를 부르며 앉아 있다가, 갑자기 무언가에 짜증이 나서, 권총을 집어 들고 맨 처음 마주친 사람을 쏘아 버리는데도, 아무도 그를 탓하지 않아요. 나는 최근에 그런 걸 읽었는데, 의사들이 다들 그걸 확인해 줬어요. 의사들은 늘 확인해 주지요. 뭐든 다 확인해 준다니까요. 글쎄, 우리 리즈도 정신착란 상태예요. 그 애가 어제 또 나를 울렸고, 그저께도 그랬고, 그래서 오늘 나는 문득 그게 다 정신착란 탓이라는 걸 깨달았지요. 아아, 리즈 때문에 내가 얼마나 마음을 졸이는지! 그 애는 완전히 미쳐 버린 것 같아요. 그 애가 왜 당신을 불렀을까요? 그 애가 당신을 부른 건가요, 아니면 당신이 제 발로 온 건가요?\"\n\n\"예, 그 애가 저를 불렀습니다, 그래서 지금 막 그 애에게 가려는 참입니다.\" 알료샤는 단호하게 일어섰다.\n\n\"오, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 어쩌면 그게 가장 중요한 일일 거예요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"하느님께서 아시지만, 나는 온 마음으로 리즈를 당신께 믿고 맡겨요, 그 애가 제 어미한테는 말도 없이 몰래 당신을 부른 거야 아무래도 좋아요. 하지만 용서해요, 나는 내 딸을 당신의 형님 이반 표도로비치에게는 그렇게 쉽사리 맡길 수가 없어요, 비록 나는 여전히 그를 더없이 기사도적인 젊은이라고 여기긴 하지만요. 그런데 글쎄 상상해 보세요, 그가 리즈를 보러 왔었는데 나는 아무것도 몰랐다니까요!\"\n\n\"어떻게요? 무슨 말씀이세요? 언제요?\" 알료샤는 몹시 놀랐다. 그는 다시 앉지 않고 선 채로 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 이야기해 드리지요. 어쩌면 그래서 내가 당신에게 와 달라고 청한 건지도 몰라요, 사실 이제는 내가 왜 당신더러 와 달라고 했는지도 모르겠으니까요. 자, 이반 표도로비치는 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 두 번 나를 보러 왔어요. 처음에는 친구로서 나를 찾아온 거였고, 두 번째는 카탸가 여기 있을 때였는데 그 애가 여기 있다는 걸 듣고 온 거였지요. 물론 나는 그가 자주 오리라곤 기대하지 않았어요, 안 그래도 그가 할 일이 얼마나 많은지 아니까요, 당신도 이해하겠지요, 그 사건이며 당신 아버님의 그 끔찍한 죽음이며 말이에요. 그런데 갑자기 듣자 하니 그가 또 여기 왔었다는 거예요, 나를 보러 온 게 아니라 리즈를 보러요. 이제 엿새 전 일이에요. 그가 와서, 오 분쯤 머물다가, 가 버렸어요. 그리고 나는 사흘이 지나서야 글라피라한테서 그 얘기를 들었으니, 그게 나한테는 큰 충격이었지요. 나는 곧장 리즈를 불렀어요. 그 애는 웃더군요. '그분은 어머니가 주무시는 줄 아셨대요' 하고 그 애가 말했어요, '그래서 어머니 안부를 여쭈러 내 방에 들르신 거예요.' 물론 그렇게 된 거겠지요. 하지만 리즈, 리즈가, 저런, 그 애가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히는지! 글쎄 믿어지세요, 어느 날 밤, 나흘 전이에요, 당신이 그 애를 마지막으로 보고 떠난 직후에, 그 애가 갑자기 발작을 일으켰어요, 비명을 지르고, 악을 쓰고, 히스테리를 부리고! 나는 어째서 한 번도 히스테리를 일으키지 않는 걸까요? 그러더니, 다음 날 또 발작, 사흘째에도 마찬가지, 어제도 그랬고, 그러고는 어제 그 정신착란이지요. 그 애가 갑자기 소리를 질렀어요, '나는 이반 표도로비치가 싫어요. 다시는 그분을 집에 들이지 말라고 어머니께 당부해요.' 나는 그 놀라운 말에 입이 딱 막혀서 대답했지요, '그렇게 훌륭한 젊은이를, 더구나 그렇게 학식 있고, 또 그렇게 불행한 젊은이를 무슨 명목으로 안 만나겠다고 하겠니?'—이 모든 일이 다 불행이잖아요, 안 그래요? 그 애는 내 말에 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸어요, 그것도 어찌나 무례하게요. 그래도 나는 기뻤지요, 내가 그 애를 즐겁게 해 줘서 발작이 가실 거라고 생각했으니까요, 더구나 나는 그가 나도 모르게 그런 이상한 방문을 한 일 때문에 어차피 이반 표도로비치를 안 만날 작정이었고, 그에게 해명을 요구할 생각이었거든요. 그런데 오늘 이른 아침에 리즈가 잠에서 깨더니 율리야에게 버럭 화를 내고는, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 그 애의 뺨을 후려쳤지 뭐예요. 그건 끔찍한 짓이에요. 나는 늘 하인들에게 정중하게 대하거든요. 그러더니 한 시간 뒤에는 율리야의 발을 끌어안고 입을 맞추더라니까요. 그 애가 나한테 전갈을 보내왔어요, 나한테는 도무지 오지 않겠다고, 다시는 나를 보러 오지 않겠다고요, 그래서 내가 몸을 끌고 그 애에게 내려갔더니, 그 애는 나한테 달려들어 입을 맞추며 울었고, 그렇게 입을 맞추면서, 한마디 말도 없이 나를 방에서 밀어내는 바람에, 무슨 일인지 알아낼 수가 없었어요. 자, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 모든 희망을 당신에게 걸겠어요, 그리고, 물론, 내 온 삶이 당신 손에 달려 있어요. 부디 리즈에게 가서 그 애한테서 모든 걸 알아내 줘요, 당신만이 할 수 있는 일이니까요, 그러고는 돌아와 나한테—그 애 어미인 나한테 이야기해 줘요, 당신도 알겠지만 이대로 가다간 내가 죽고 말 거예요, 그냥 죽어 버릴 거예요, 아니면 도망쳐 버리든가요. 나는 더는 견딜 수가 없어요. 나도 참을성이 있지만, 그 참을성을 잃어버릴지도 몰라요, 그러면… 그러면 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지겠지요. 아아, 어머나! 드디어 오셨군요, 표트르 일리치!\" 호흘라코바 부인은 페르호틴이 방으로 들어오는 것을 보고는 온 얼굴에 환한 빛을 띠며 외쳤다. \"늦으셨어요, 늦으셨어요! 자, 앉으세요, 말씀해 보세요, 우리를 이 애타는 마음에서 풀어 주세요. 변호인이 뭐라고 하던가요. 어디로 가시려고요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?\"\n\n\"리즈에게요.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요. 잊지 않을 거지요, 내가 부탁한 걸 잊지 않을 거지요? 이건 죽느냐 사느냐의 문제예요!\"\n\n\"물론 잊지 않겠습니다, 할 수 있다면요… 하지만 제가 너무 늦어서요.\" 알료샤가 황급히 물러나며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 꼭, 꼭 들러 줘야 해요. '할 수 있다면'이라고 하지 말아요. 당신이 안 오면 나는 죽고 말 거예요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 그의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤으나, 알료샤는 이미 방을 나선 뒤였다.\n\n# Chapter III. A Little Demon\n\n리즈에게 들어서니, 그녀는 걸을 수 없을 때 누가 밀어 주던 바로 그 환자용 의자에 반쯤 기대어 있었다. 그를 맞으려고 몸을 움직이지는 않았으나, 날카롭고 매서운 두 눈만은 그저 그의 얼굴에 못 박힌 듯 고정되어 있었다. 그 눈에는 열에 들뜬 빛이 어려 있었고, 얼굴은 창백하고 누르께했다. 알료샤는 사흘 사이에 그녀에게 일어난 변화에 깜짝 놀랐다. 그녀는 확연히 야위어 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀지 않았다. 그는 옷자락 위에 미동도 없이 놓인, 가늘고 긴 손가락을 만져 보고는, 말없이 그녀를 마주하고 앉았다.\n\n\"오빠가 교도소에 가려고 서두르고 있는 거 알아요.\" 리즈가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"엄마가 거기서 몇 시간이나 오빠를 붙들어 뒀죠. 방금 나랑 율리야에 관해서 늘어놓았을 테고요.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 알아?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"엿듣고 있었거든요. 왜 그렇게 빤히 쳐다봐요? 엿듣고 싶어서 엿들었어요, 그게 무슨 잘못이라고요. 사과 같은 건 안 해요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일로 속이 상한 거니?\"\n\n\"그 반대예요, 난 아주 행복해요. 오빠를 거절하고 오빠 아내가 되지 않게 된 게 얼마나 잘한 일인지, 방금 서른 번째로 곱씹어 보고 있던 참이라고요. 오빠는 남편감이 못 돼요. 만약 내가 오빠랑 결혼해서, 오빠 다음으로 사랑하게 된 남자한테 전해 달라고 쪽지를 건네면, 오빠는 그걸 받아서 틀림없이 그 사람한테 전해 주고, 답장까지 받아다 줄 거예요. 오빠가 마흔 살이 돼도, 여전히 내 연애편지를 날라다 주겠죠.\"\n\n그녀는 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"너에게는 심술궂으면서도 동시에 솔직한 데가 있구나.\" 알료샤가 그녀에게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"솔직하다는 건, 오빠 앞에서는 내 자신이 부끄럽지 않다는 거예요. 게다가 난 오빠 앞에서는 부끄럽고 싶지도 않아요, 딱 오빠 앞에서만요. 알료샤, 왜 난 오빠를 존경하지 않는 걸까요? 오빠를 무척 좋아하는데도, 존경은 안 돼요. 만약 오빠를 존경한다면, 부끄러움도 없이 이런 얘길 늘어놓진 못하겠죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\"그렇겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 오빠 앞에서 부끄럽지 않다는 거, 믿어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 안 믿어.\"\n\n리즈는 또다시 신경질적으로 웃었다. 그녀는 빠르게 말했다.\n\n\"오빠 형, 드미트리 표도로비치한테 교도소로 사탕을 좀 보냈어요. 알료샤, 있잖아요, 오빠는 참 예뻐요! 오빠를 사랑하지 않아도 되게끔 그렇게 빨리 허락해 준 걸로, 난 오빠를 끔찍이 사랑할 거예요.\"\n\n\"오늘 왜 나를 부른 거니, 리즈?\"\n\n\"내게 있는 어떤 갈망을 오빠한테 말하고 싶었어요. 누군가 나를 괴롭혀 줬으면 좋겠어요, 나랑 결혼하고 나서 나를 괴롭히고, 나를 속이고 떠나 버렸으면 해요. 난 행복해지고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"넌 무질서를 사랑하는구나?\"\n\n\"그래요, 난 무질서를 원해요. 자꾸만 집에 불을 지르고 싶어요. 몰래 살금살금 다가가 슬쩍 집에 불을 놓는 장면을 계속 떠올린다고요. 반드시 몰래여야 해요. 다들 불을 끄려 들겠지만, 불은 계속 타오를 거예요. 그리고 난 알면서도 아무 말도 안 하겠죠. 아, 이 무슨 바보 같은 소린지! 그리고 난 어찌나 따분한지!\"\n\n그녀는 혐오스럽다는 표정으로 손을 내저었다.\n\n\"네 사치스러운 생활 탓이야.\" 알료샤가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 가난한 게 더 낫다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 더 낫지.\"\n\n\"그건 오빠네 수도사가 가르쳐 준 거잖아요. 그건 사실이 아니에요. 나만 부자고 다른 사람들은 다 가난뱅이라면, 난 사탕을 먹고 크림을 마시면서 아무한테도 한 입 안 나눠 줄 거예요. 아, 말하지 마요, 아무 말도 하지 마요.\" 알료샤가 입도 떼지 않았는데도, 그녀는 그에게 손을 내저었다. \"그런 얘긴 전에 다 해 줬잖아요, 난 죄다 외우고 있어요. 따분하다니까요. 만약 내가 언젠가 가난해진다면, 난 누군가를 죽여 버릴 거예요. 부자라 해도 누군가를 죽일지도 몰라요, 어쩌면요. 왜 아무것도 안 하고 있어야 하죠! 그런데 있잖아요, 난 곡식을 거두고 호밀을 베어 보고 싶어요. 오빠랑 결혼해서, 오빠를 농사꾼으로, 진짜 농사꾼으로 만들 거예요. 망아지도 한 마리 기르는 거예요, 어때요? 칼가노프 알죠?\"\n\n\"알지.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 늘 이리저리 헤매며 공상에 잠겨 있어요. 그가 그러더라고요. '왜 현실 속에서 살아야 하지? 꿈꾸는 편이 더 낫지. 더없이 황홀한 것들을 꿈꿀 수 있는데, 현실의 삶은 따분하기만 하잖아.' 그래도 그는 곧 결혼할 거예요. 벌써 나한테 구애했거든요. 오빠는 팽이 돌릴 줄 알아요?\"\n\n\"알지.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그는 꼭 팽이 같아요. 감아 올려서 돌리고는 채찍으로 후려치고, 후려치고, 또 후려쳐 주기를 바라죠. 내가 그와 결혼하면, 평생토록 그를 빙빙 돌게 만들 거예요. 오빠는 나랑 같이 있는 게 부끄럽지 않아요?\"\n\"부끄럽지 않아.\"\n\n\"오빠는 내가 거룩한 것들에 관해 이야기하지 않으니까 무척이나 못마땅하겠죠. 난 거룩해지고 싶지 않아요. 가장 큰 죄를 지으면 저세상에서 사람한테 무슨 짓을 할까요? 오빠는 그런 걸 다 알 거 아니에요.\"\n\n\"하느님이 너를 꾸짖으시겠지.\" 알료샤는 그녀를 가만히 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 바라는 게 바로 그거예요. 내가 올라가면 그들이 나를 꾸짖을 테고, 난 그들 면전에서 와락 웃음을 터뜨릴 거예요. 난 집에 불을 지르고 싶어 죽겠어요, 알료샤, 우리 집에 말이에요. 아직도 내 말을 안 믿죠?\"\n\n\"왜? 무언가에 불을 지르고 싶은 갈망을 품은 열두 살짜리 아이들도 있고, 실제로 불을 놓기도 해. 그건 일종의 병이야.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이 아니에요, 사실이 아니라고요. 그런 아이들이야 있겠죠, 하지만 내가 말하는 건 그런 게 아니에요.\"\n\n\"넌 악을 선으로 착각하고 있어. 그건 지나가는 위기일 뿐이야, 어쩌면 네 병에서 비롯된 것이고.\"\n\n\"그래도 오빠는 날 경멸하는군요! 그냥 난 선을 행하고 싶지 않고 악을 행하고 싶을 뿐이에요, 그건 병하고는 아무 상관이 없어요.\"\n\n\"왜 악을 행하려는 거니?\"\n\n\"모든 게 파괴되도록 하기 위해서요. 아, 모든 게 파괴된다면 얼마나 좋을까요! 있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 가끔 어마어마하게 해악을 끼치고 온갖 나쁜 짓을 저지르는 걸 상상해요. 그걸 오래도록 몰래 저지르다가, 어느 날 갑자기 모두가 그걸 알아채는 거예요. 다들 빙 둘러서서 나를 손가락질하겠죠, 그러면 난 그들 모두를 바라보는 거예요. 그건 정말 근사할 거예요. 왜 그렇게 근사할까요, 알료샤?\"\n\n\"모르겠어. 그건 선한 무언가를 파괴하고 싶은, 아니 네 말마따나 무언가에 불을 지르고 싶은 갈망이야. 가끔 그런 일이 일어나지.\"\n\n\"난 말만 하는 게 아니라, 정말로 그렇게 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"믿어.\"\n\"아, 날 믿는다고 말해 주다니, 그래서 오빠를 어찌나 사랑하는지. 그리고 오빠는 손톱만큼도 거짓말을 안 해요. 하지만 어쩌면 오빠는, 내가 오빠를 약 올리려고 일부러 이런 말들을 다 한다고 생각할지도 몰라요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 생각하진 않아…… 하지만 어쩌면 그 안에 그러고 싶은 마음이 조금은 있을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"조금은 있어요. 난 오빠한테는 절대 거짓말을 못 해요.\" 그녀가 두 눈에 묘한 불꽃을 담고 단언했다.\n\n무엇보다도 알료샤를 놀라게 한 것은 그녀의 진지함이었다. 지금 그녀의 얼굴에는 익살이나 농담의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없었는데, 예전 같으면 가장 \"진지한\" 순간에도 그녀에게서 장난기와 명랑함이 떠난 적이 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"사람들이 죄악을 사랑하는 순간들이 있어.\" 알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아요! 오빠가 내 생각을 그대로 말했어요. 사람들은 죄악을 사랑해요, 누구나 죄악을 사랑하고, '어떤 순간들'이 아니라 늘 사랑한다고요. 있잖아요, 그건 마치 사람들이 그것에 관해 거짓말하기로 약속이라도 한 것 같아요, 그래서 그 이래로 줄곧 거짓말을 해 온 거죠. 다들 자기는 악을 미워한다고 떠벌리지만, 속으로는 다들 그걸 사랑한다고요.\"\n\n\"넌 아직도 그 못된 책들을 읽고 있는 거니?\"\n\n\"네, 읽어요. 엄마가 그것들을 읽고는 베개 밑에 숨겨 두는데, 내가 슬쩍 훔쳐요.\"\n\n\"네 자신을 망가뜨리는 게 부끄럽지 않니?\"\n\n\"난 내 자신을 망가뜨리고 싶어요. 여기 어떤 남자애가, 기차가 지나갈 때 철로 사이에 누워 있었대요. 운 좋은 녀석이죠! 들어 봐요, 지금 오빠 형이 아버지를 죽인 죄로 재판을 받고 있는데, 다들 그가 아버지를 죽인 걸 사랑해요.\"\n\n\"그가 아버지를 죽인 걸 사랑한다고?\"\n\n\"네, 사랑해요. 다들 그걸 사랑한다고요! 다들 그게 너무 끔찍하다고 말하지만, 속으로는 그냥 그걸 사랑하는 거예요. 나부터가 그걸 사랑해요.\"\n\n\"누구나 다 그렇다는 네 말에는 어느 정도 진실이 담겨 있어.\" 알료샤가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 오빠는 어쩌면 그런 생각을 다 해요!\" 리즈가 기뻐 날카롭게 소리쳤다. \"게다가 오빠는 수도사잖아요! 거짓말이라곤 절대 안 하는 오빠를, 내가 얼마나 존경하는지 오빠는 믿지 못할 거예요, 알료샤. 아, 내 우스운 꿈 얘기를 해 줘야겠어요. 난 가끔 악마들 꿈을 꿔요. 밤이고, 난 촛불을 켜고 내 방에 있는데, 갑자기 사방에 악마들이 우글거려요, 온갖 구석마다, 탁자 밑에도요, 그러더니 문을 열어요. 문 뒤로 한 떼가 몰려 있는데, 그놈들이 다가와서 나를 붙잡으려고 해요. 그놈들이 막 다가와서, 막 나를 붙잡으려는 거예요. 그런데 내가 갑자기 성호를 그으면 그놈들이 죄다 물러서요, 그렇다고 아주 가 버리는 건 아니고, 문가랑 구석에 서서 기다려요. 그러다 갑자기 난 하느님을 소리 내어 욕하고 싶은 무시무시한 갈망에 사로잡혀서 욕을 시작해요, 그러면 그놈들이 신이 나서 다시 우르르 내게 몰려들어 또 나를 붙잡고, 난 또 성호를 그어요, 그러면 그놈들이 죄다 물러서요. 정말 끔찍하게 재미나요. 숨이 다 멎을 지경이라니까요.\"\n\n\"나도 똑같은 꿈을 꾼 적이 있어.\" 알료샤가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"정말요?\" 리즈가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다. \"있잖아요, 알료샤, 웃지 마요, 이건 정말 중요한 일이에요. 서로 다른 두 사람이 똑같은 꿈을 꿀 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"꿀 수 있나 봐.\"\n\n\"알료샤, 정말이지, 이건 정말 중요한 일이에요.\" 리즈는 정말로 지나치다 싶을 만큼 경탄하며 말을 이었다. \"중요한 건 그 꿈이 아니라, 오빠가 나랑 똑같은 꿈을 꿨다는 거예요. 오빠는 나한테 절대 거짓말을 안 하잖아요, 지금도 거짓말하지 마요. 정말이에요? 웃고 있는 거 아니죠?\"\n\n\"정말이야.\"\n\n리즈는 어지간히 깊은 인상을 받은 듯, 삼십 초쯤 말이 없었다.\n\n\"알료샤, 나를 보러 와요, 좀 더 자주 나를 보러 와요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 애원하는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"언제든 너를 보러 올게, 평생토록.\" 알료샤가 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n\"오빠는 내가 얘기를 나눌 수 있는 유일한 사람이에요, 알아요?\" 리즈가 다시 입을 열었다. \"난 나 자신하고 오빠 말고는 아무하고도 이야기를 안 해요. 온 세상에 오빠 하나뿐이라고요. 게다가 나 자신한테보다 오빠한테 더 선뜻 털어놓아요. 그리고 난 오빠 앞에서는 조금도 부끄럽지 않아요, 조금도요. 알료샤, 난 왜 오빠 앞에서는 부끄럽지 않을까요, 조금도요? 알료샤, 부활절 때 유대인들이 아이를 훔쳐다가 죽인다는 게 정말이에요?\"\n\n\"난 몰라.\"\n\n\"여기 어떤 책에서 한 유대인의 재판에 관해 읽었는데, 그자가 네 살짜리 아이를 데려다가 양손의 손가락을 죄다 잘라 내고는, 벽에 십자가형으로 못 박았대요, 못을 박아 넣어 십자가에 매단 거예요. 그러고 나서 재판을 받을 때, 그자가 말하길 아이가 곧 죽었다고, 네 시간 안에 죽었다고 했대요. 그게 '곧'이라니! 그자 말로는, 아이가 신음했다고, 계속 신음하기에 자기는 서서 그걸 흐뭇하게 바라보고 있었다고 했대요. 정말 근사하죠!\"\n\"근사하다고?\"\n\n\"근사해요. 난 가끔 그 아이를 십자가에 매단 게 나라고 상상해요. 그 아이가 거기 매달려 신음하고 있으면, 난 맞은편에 앉아 파인애플 콩포트를 먹는 거예요. 난 파인애플 콩포트를 끔찍이 좋아하거든요. 오빠도 좋아해요?\"\n\n알료샤는 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀의 창백하고 누르께한 얼굴이 갑자기 일그러지고, 두 눈이 불타올랐다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 그 유대인 얘기를 읽고 나서 난 밤새 흐느끼며 떨었어요. 그 가엾은 것이 어떻게 울부짖고 신음했을지 자꾸만 떠올랐어요(네 살짜리 아이도 다 알잖아요), 그러면서도 내내 파인애플 콩포트 생각이 나를 떠나지 않았어요. 아침이 되자 난 어떤 사람한테 편지를 써서, 꼭 좀 나를 보러 와 달라고 간곡히 부탁했어요. 그가 왔고, 난 갑자기 그 아이랑 파인애플 콩포트 얘기를 죄다 그에게 털어놨어요. 전부 다요, 전부요, 그러고는 그게 근사하다고 말했어요. 그가 웃으면서, 정말로 근사하다고 했어요. 그러더니 일어나서 가 버렸어요. 그는 여기 딱 오 분 있었어요. 그가 나를 경멸했을까요? 나를 경멸했을까요? 말해 줘요, 말해 줘요, 알료샤, 그가 나를 경멸했을까요, 아닐까요?\" 그녀는 두 눈을 번득이며 긴 의자에서 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n\"말해 봐.\" 알료샤가 근심스레 물었다. \"네가 그 사람을 부른 거니?\"\n\n\"네, 그랬어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 편지를 보냈고?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그저 그걸 묻기 위해서, 그 아이 얘기를 하려고?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그것 때문은 전혀 아니에요. 하지만 그가 왔을 때, 난 대뜸 그걸 물었어요. 그가 대답하고는, 웃고, 일어나서 가 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 점잖게 처신한 거야.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그가 나를 경멸했을까요? 나를 비웃은 걸까요?\"\n\n\"아니, 어쩌면 그 사람 자신도 파인애플 콩포트를 믿고 있을 테니까. 그 사람도 지금 몹시 아프단다, 리즈.\"\n\n\"네, 그는 그걸 믿어요.\" 리즈가 두 눈을 번득이며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람은 누구도 경멸하지 않아.\" 알료샤가 말을 이었다. \"다만 누구도 믿지 않을 뿐이야. 사람을 믿지 않으니, 당연히 사람을 경멸하는 거지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그가 나를 경멸하는군요, 나를?\"\n\n\"너도.\"\n\n\"좋아요.\" 리즈는 이를 가는 듯했다. \"그가 웃으면서 나가 버렸을 때, 난 경멸당하는 게 근사하다고 느꼈어요. 손가락이 잘린 그 아이도 근사하고, 경멸당하는 것도 근사하고…….\"\n\n그러고는 알료샤의 면전에 대고 웃었으니, 열에 들뜬 악의에 찬 웃음이었다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 알료샤, 있잖아요, 난…… 알료샤, 나를 구해 줘요!\" 그녀가 갑자기 긴 의자에서 벌떡 뛰어내려 그에게 달려들더니 두 손으로 그를 붙잡았다. \"나를 구해 줘요!\" 그녀는 거의 신음하듯 말했다. \"내가 오빠한테 한 얘기를 털어놓을 수 있는 사람이 이 세상에 또 누가 있겠어요? 난 오빠한테 진실을, 진실을 말했어요. 난 자살할 거예요, 모든 게 다 역겨우니까요! 살고 싶지 않아요, 모든 게 다 역겨우니까요! 모든 게 다 역겨워요, 전부 다요. 알료샤, 오빠는 왜 나를 손톱만큼도 사랑하지 않는 거예요?\" 그녀가 광기에 사로잡혀 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"하지만 난 너를 사랑해!\" 알료샤가 따뜻하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나를 위해 울어 줄 거예요, 그럴 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"내가 오빠 아내가 되지 않아서가 아니라, 그냥 나를 위해 울어 줄 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"고마워요! 난 오직 오빠의 눈물만 바라요. 다른 사람들은 다 나를 벌하고 발밑에 짓밟아도 돼요, 누구나, 누구나, 단 한 사람도 빼놓지 않고요. 난 누구도 사랑하지 않으니까요. 들었죠, 누구도요! 오히려, 난 그를 미워해요! 가요, 알료샤, 오빠 형한테 갈 시간이에요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 그에게서 몸을 떼어 냈다.\n\n\"이런 너를 두고 어떻게 떠나니?\" 알료샤가 거의 놀라다시피 말했다.\n\n\"오빠 형한테 가요, 교도소 문이 닫힐 거예요. 가요, 여기 오빠 모자요. 미탸한테 내 사랑을 전해 줘요, 가요, 가요!\"\n\n그러고는 거의 떠밀다시피 알료샤를 문밖으로 내보냈다. 그가 고통스러운 놀라움에 잠겨 그녀를 바라보던 그때, 문득 오른손에 편지 한 통이 쥐여 있다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 단단히 접어 봉한 자그마한 편지였다. 그가 그것을 흘끗 보고 그 자리에서 수신인을 읽으니, \"이반 표도로비치 카라마조프에게\"라고 적혀 있었다. 그는 재빨리 리즈를 보았다. 그녀의 얼굴은 거의 위협적으로 변해 있었다.\n\n\"그한테 전해 줘요, 반드시 그한테 전해 줘야 해요!\" 그녀가 부들부들 떨며 제정신이 아닌 채로 그에게 명령했다. \"오늘, 당장요, 안 그러면 난 독을 마실 거예요! 그래서 오빠를 부른 거란 말이에요.\"\n\n그러고는 재빨리 문을 쾅 닫았다. 빗장이 철컥 채워졌다. 알료샤는 쪽지를 주머니에 넣고 호흘라코바 부인에게 들르지도 않은 채 곧장 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 실은 그녀를 까맣게 잊은 것이었다. 알료샤가 떠나자마자 리즈는 빗장을 풀고 문을 조금 연 다음, 손가락을 문틈에 끼워 넣고는 있는 힘껏 문을 쾅 닫아 손가락을 짓이겼다. 십 초쯤 지나 손가락을 빼낸 그녀는, 부드럽고 느릿한 걸음으로 의자로 걸어가 거기에 꼿꼿이 앉아서는, 시커멓게 변한 손가락과 손톱 밑에서 배어 나오는 피를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그녀의 입술은 바르르 떨렸고, 그녀는 줄곧 빠르게 혼자 속삭였다.\n\n\"난 비열한 년이야, 비열한 년, 비열한 년, 비열한 년!\"\n\n# Chapter IV. A Hymn And A Secret\n\n알료샤가 감옥 정문에서 초인종을 울렸을 때는 이미 제법 늦은 시각이었다(십일월의 낮은 짧았다). 어스름이 깔리기 시작하고 있었다. 그러나 알료샤는 자기가 별다른 어려움 없이 안으로 들어갈 수 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 우리 작은 고장에서도 일은 다른 곳과 마찬가지로 돌아갔다. 물론 처음에는, 예심이 끝난 직후라 친척이나 그 밖의 몇몇 사람들도 정해진 절차를 어쩔 수 없이 거쳐야만 미탸와 면회할 수 있었다. 그러나 나중에는, 절차 자체가 느슨해진 것은 아니었으되, 적어도 미탸를 찾아오는 방문객 가운데 몇몇에게는 예외가 인정되었다. 어찌나 그러했던지, 면회용으로 따로 마련된 방에서 죄수와 만나는 자리가 사실상 단둘이서 마주 앉는 것이나 다름없을 때도 더러 있었다.\n\n그렇지만 이런 예외는 몇 사람에게만 허락되었으니, 오로지 그루셴카와 알료샤, 그리고 라키틴만이 이런 대접을 받았다. 그런데 경찰서장 미하일 미하일로비치는 그루셴카에게 무척 호의적이었다. 모크로예에서 그녀에게 퍼부었던 욕설이 노인의 양심을 짓눌렀고, 자초지종을 다 알게 되자 그는 그녀에 대한 생각을 완전히 바꾸었다. 그리고 이상하게도, 그는 미탸가 유죄라고 굳게 믿고 있었으면서도 미탸가 일단 감옥에 들어간 뒤로는 점점 더 너그러운 시선으로 그를 바라보게 되었다. \"어쩌면 마음씨 착한 사람이었던 게야.\" 그는 생각했다. \"술과 방탕 때문에 신세를 망친 게지.\" 처음에 느꼈던 공포는 어느새 연민으로 바뀌어 있었다. 알료샤로 말하자면, 경찰서장은 그를 무척 아꼈고 오래전부터 알고 지내던 사이였다. 라키틴은 요즘 들어 죄수를 매우 자주 보러 오게 되었는데, 그는 자신이 \"경찰서장 댁 아가씨들\"이라 부르던 이들과 가장 가깝게 지내는 사람 가운데 하나로서 늘 그 집 언저리를 맴돌았다. 그는 감옥 소장의 집에서도 가정교사 노릇을 하고 있었는데, 그 소장은 직무 수행에는 빈틈이 없었으나 마음씨는 따뜻한 노인이었다. 알료샤 또한 소장과는 오래도록 가깝게 지낸 사이였고, 소장은 그와 이야기 나누기를, 대개 성스러운 주제에 관해 이야기 나누기를 좋아했다. 그는 이반 표도로비치를 존경했고 그의 견해 앞에서는 경외감을 품었는데, 정작 자기 자신도 대단한 철학자였다. 물론 \"독학\"으로 그렇게 된 것이었다. 그러나 알료샤에게는 그가 거역할 수 없는 끌림을 느꼈다. 지난 한 해 동안 노인은 외경(外經) 복음서를 공부하기 시작했고, 자신이 받은 인상을 끊임없이 이 젊은 벗과 더불어 이야기했다. 그는 수도원으로 알료샤를 찾아와 그와, 또 수도사들과 더불어 몇 시간씩 토론을 벌이곤 했다. 그러니 설령 알료샤가 감옥에 늦게 도착한다 해도 소장에게로 가기만 하면 모든 일이 수월하게 풀렸다. 게다가 감옥 안에서는 가장 말단 간수에 이르기까지 모두가 알료샤에게 익숙해져 있었다. 보초병 또한, 물론, 윗선이 만족하는 한 그를 귀찮게 굴지 않았다.\n\n미탸는 감방에서 호출을 받을 때면 언제나 아래층으로, 면회를 위해 따로 마련된 자리로 내려갔다. 알료샤가 방으로 들어서니 마침 미탸와 작별 인사를 나누고 있던 라키틴과 마주쳤다. 두 사람 다 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었다. 미탸는 그를 배웅하며 유쾌하게 웃고 있었으나, 라키틴은 무언가 투덜대는 듯했다. 라키틴은 알료샤와 마주치는 것을 좋아하지 않았는데, 특히 요즘 들어 더욱 그랬다. 그는 알료샤에게 말을 거의 건네지 않았고, 뻣뻣하게 고개만 까딱했다. 알료샤가 들어오는 것을 보자 그는 인상을 찌푸리며 시선을 돌리더니, 모피로 가장자리를 두른 크고 따뜻한 외투의 단추를 채우는 데 온통 정신이 팔린 척했다. 그러고는 곧 우산을 찾기 시작했다.\n\n\"내 물건 잊지 않도록 조심해야지.\" 그는 그저 무슨 말이라도 하려고 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"남의 물건 잊지 않도록 조심하시게.\" 미탸가 농담조로 말하고는 곧 제 재치에 스스로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라키틴은 대번에 발끈했다.\n\n\"그런 충고라면 라키틴이 아니라 자네 집안사람들한테나 하지그래, 늘 종 부리듯 사람들을 부려먹던 족속들 아닌가.\" 그는 갑자기 분노로 부들부들 떨며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"왜 이러나? 농담이었네.\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"에잇, 빌어먹을! 다들 저 모양이라니까.\" 그는 알료샤를 돌아보며 황급히 물러가는 라키틴의 뒷모습을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다. \"여기 앉아서 웃고 떠들며 즐거워하더니, 갑자기 저렇게 부글부글 끓어오른단 말이야. 너한테는 고갯짓 한 번 안 하던데. 저 친구하고 완전히 갈라선 거냐? 그런데 왜 이렇게 늦었어? 난 그저 너를 기다린 게 아니라, 아침 내내 너를 목말라하며 기다렸다. 하지만 됐다. 이제 그걸 만회하면 되지.\"\n\n\"저 사람은 왜 여기 이렇게 자주 오는 거야? 설마 그렇게까지 친한 사이는 아닐 텐데?\" 알료샤가 물었다. 그 역시 라키틴이 사라진 문 쪽을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"라키틴하고 친한 사이냐고? 아니, 그 정도는 아니야. 말이 되냐, 그런 돼지 같은 놈하고? 그자는 나를… 비열한 놈이라고 여긴다. 농담도 못 알아듣지, 그게 바로 그런 인간들의 가장 못된 점이야. 농담이라곤 도무지 알아듣지 못하고, 영혼이 메말랐어, 메마르고 납작해빠졌지. 그자를 보면 내가 여기 처음 끌려왔을 때의 감옥 벽이 떠올라. 하지만 똑똑한 놈이야, 아주 똑똑하지. 그건 그렇고, 알렉세이, 이제 나는 다 끝났다.\"\n\n그는 벤치에 앉으며 알료샤를 제 곁에 앉혔다.\n\n\"그래, 재판이 내일이지. 형은 그렇게도 가망이 없는 거야?\" 알료샤가 불안한 마음으로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리를 하는 거냐?\" 미탸가 다소 멍한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"아, 재판 말이로구나! 빌어먹을! 지금까지 우리는 별것 아닌 것들, 이 재판 얘기만 하고 있었지, 정작 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 너한테 한마디도 안 했다. 그래, 재판은 내일이야. 하지만 내가 다 끝났다고 했을 때 말한 건 재판이 아니었어. 왜 그렇게 따지는 눈으로 나를 보는 거냐?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이야, 미탸?\"\n\n\"관념, 관념, 그게 전부야! 윤리학 말이다! 윤리학이란 게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"윤리학?\" 알료샤가 어리둥절해하며 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그게 학문이냐?\"\n\n\"그래, 그런 학문이 있긴 한데… 하지만… 솔직히 그게 어떤 학문인지 형한테 설명할 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"라키틴은 알아. 그놈은 아는 게 많지, 제기랄! 그놈은 수도사가 되지 않을 거야. 페테르부르크로 갈 작정이지. 거기서 고상한 경향의 비평이라는 걸 할 거다. 누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 쓸모도 있고 제 출셋길도 닦을지. 으! 정말 일류라니까, 그런 인간들은, 출세하는 데는 말이야. 윤리학이고 뭐고 다 집어치워, 난 끝장났어, 알렉세이, 끝장났다고, 너 같은 하느님의 사람아! 난 그 누구보다 너를 사랑해. 너를 보면 내 가슴이 사무친다. 그런데 카를 베르나르가 누구였더라?\"\n\n\"카를 베르나르?\" 알료샤가 또다시 놀랐다.\n\n\"아니, 카를이 아니지. 가만, 내가 틀렸군. 클로드 베르나르 말이야. 그 사람이 뭐였더라? 화학자였나, 뭐였나?\"\n\n\"학자임에는 틀림없겠지.\" 알료샤가 대답했다. \"하지만 솔직히 그 사람에 대해서도 내가 형한테 별로 해 줄 말이 없네. 학자라는 얘기는 들었는데, 어떤 사람인지는 몰라.\"\n\n\"그럼 그자도 빌어먹으라지! 나도 모르겠다.\" 미탸가 욕설을 퍼부었다. \"필시 무슨 악당 같은 놈이겠지. 그놈들은 죄다 악당이야. 그리고 라키틴은 출세할 거다. 라키틴은 어디서든 잘나갈 놈이야. 그자도 또 하나의 베르나르지. 으, 이놈의 베르나르들! 천지에 깔렸어.\"\n\n\"그런데 대체 무슨 일이야?\" 알료샤가 끈질기게 물었다.\n\n\"그놈이 나에 대해, 내 사건에 대해 글을 써서 그걸로 제 문필 경력을 시작하려는 거야. 그래서 여기 오는 거지. 자기 입으로 그렇게 말하더라. 무슨 이론인가를 증명하고 싶어 해. '그는 아버지를 죽이지 않을 수 없었다, 환경 탓에 타락한 것이다' 어쩌고저쩌고하고 싶다는 거지. 나한테 전부 설명하더군. 거기에다 사회주의 색채를 좀 가미하겠다는 거야. 하지만 뭐, 그놈은 빌어먹으라지, 색채를 가미하고 싶으면 가미하라지, 난 상관없어. 그놈은 이반을 못 견뎌 해, 이반을 미워하지. 너도 좋아하지 않아. 하지만 난 그놈을 내쫓지 않는다, 똑똑한 놈이니까. 그래도 끔찍하게 자만심이 강하지. 방금 그놈한테 이렇게 말해 줬다. '카라마조프 집안 사람들은 비열한 놈들이 아니라 철학자들이야. 참된 러시아인은 다 철학자거든. 그런데 자네는 공부를 했어도 철학자가 아니야 ― 자네는 천박한 놈이지.' 그놈이 웃더라, 어찌나 악의에 차서 웃던지. 그래서 내가 또 이렇게 말해 줬지. '데 이데아부스 논 에스트 디스푸탄둠.' 제법 그럴듯하지 않냐? 봐라, 나도 고전학자 행세를 할 수 있다고!\" 미탸가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n* 데 이데아부스 논 에스트 디스푸탄둠(De ideabus non est disputandum): \"관념에 관해서는 논쟁할 것이 없다\"라는 뜻의 라틴어. \"취향에 관해서는 논쟁할 것이 없다(De gustibus non est disputandum)\"라는 격언을 미탸가 익살스럽게 바꾼 것이다.\n\n\"형은 왜 다 끝났다는 거야? 방금 그렇게 말했잖아.\" 알료샤가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 왜 끝장났느냐고? 흠!… 사실인즉슨… 통틀어 말하자면, 난 하느님을 잃는 게 안타까워 ― 그래서 그런 거다.\"\n\n\"'하느님을 잃는 게 안타깝다'니, 그게 무슨 말이야?\"\n\n\"한번 상상해 봐라. 몸 안에, 신경 속에, 머릿속에 ― 그러니까 이 신경이라는 게 뇌 속에 있는데… (빌어먹을 것들!) 거기에 무슨 작은 꼬리 같은 게 있어, 그 신경들의 작은 꼬리들 말이야, 그게 떨리기 시작하면… 그러니까, 봐라, 내가 무언가를 눈으로 보면 그것들이 떨리기 시작하는 거야, 그 작은 꼬리들이… 그리고 그것들이 떨리면 어떤 상(像)이 떠오르지… 곧바로 떠오르는 건 아니고, 한순간, 일 초가 지나야… 그러고 나서 무슨 한 찰나 같은 게 떠올라. 그러니까, 찰나가 아니라 ― 찰나 따위 악마한테나 잡아먹히라지! ― 상이지. 그러니까 대상, 아니면 행동, 빌어먹을! 그래서 나는 보고, 그다음에 생각하는 거야, 그 꼬리들 때문에 말이지, 내게 영혼이 있어서가 결코 아니고, 내가 무슨 하느님의 형상이자 모상이라서가 아니란 말이다. 그딴 건 죄다 헛소리야! 라키틴이 어제 나한테 전부 설명해 줬는데, 형제여, 그게 그냥 나를 나가떨어지게 만들었어. 굉장하더라, 알료샤, 이 과학이라는 게! 새로운 인간이 일어서고 있다 ― 그건 나도 알겠어… 그런데도 난 하느님을 잃는 게 안타깝단 말이야!\"\n\n\"뭐, 어쨌든 그건 좋은 일이네.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 하느님을 잃는 걸 안타까워하는 게? 그건 화학이야, 형제여, 화학이라고! 어쩔 도리가 없지, 신부님, 화학에 자리를 비켜 줘야 한다 이거야. 그리고 라키틴은 정말 하느님을 싫어해. 으! 어찌나 싫어하는지! 그게 바로 그놈들 모두의 아픈 데지. 그런데 다들 그걸 감춰. 거짓말을 한다고. 시치미를 떼. '자네 비평에 그런 걸 설파할 텐가?' 내가 물었지. '오, 글쎄, 대놓고 그러면 통과시켜 주질 않을 텐데.' 하더라. 웃으면서 말이야. '그럼 사람들은 어떻게 되는 건가?' 내가 물었어. '하느님도, 영생도 없이? 그렇다면 모든 것이 허용되는 거 아닌가, 마음대로 해도 되는 거 아니냐고?' '몰랐나?' 그놈이 웃으며 말하더라. '똑똑한 자는 뭐든 마음대로 할 수 있어. 똑똑한 자는 처세에 밝지. 그런데 자네는 살인이나 저지르고 발을 헛디뎌서, 지금 감옥에서 썩고 있는 거 아닌가.' 내 면전에 대고 그러는 거야! 영락없는 돼지지! 예전 같으면 그런 놈들은 발로 차서 내쫓았을 텐데, 지금은 그놈들 말을 들어 준다. 사리에 맞는 말도 꽤 하긴 해. 글도 잘 쓰고. 지난주에 나한테 글 한 편을 읽어 주기 시작하던데. 그중 세 줄을 베껴 뒀어. 잠깐만. 여기 있다.\"\n\n미탸는 황급히 주머니에서 종이쪽지를 꺼내 읽었다.\n\n\"'이 문제를 규명하기 위해서는 무엇보다도 자신의 인격을 자신의 현실과 모순 관계에 놓는 것이 긴요하다.' 이게 무슨 소린지 알겠냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 모르겠는데.\" 알료샤가 말했다. 그는 미탸를 바라보며 호기심 어린 눈으로 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"나도 모르겠어. 어둡고 모호하지만, 어쨌든 지적이지. '요즘은 다들 이렇게 써.' 그놈이 그러더라. '환경의 영향이지.' 그놈들은 환경을 두려워해. 그놈은 시도 쓴다, 그 악당 같은 놈이. 호흘라코바 부인의 발을 기리는 시를 썼지. 하하하!\"\n\n\"그 얘기는 들었어.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"들었어? 그 시도 들어 봤냐?\"\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"나한테 있어. 여기 있다. 너한테 읽어 줄게. 너는 모르지 ― 내가 말 안 했으니까 ― 거기엔 제법 사연이 있어. 그놈은 악당이야! 삼 주 전에 나를 놀려대기 시작하더라. '자네는 고작 삼천 루블 때문에 바보같이 곤경에 빠졌지만, 나는 십오만 루블을 거머쥘 거야. 과부와 결혼해서 페테르부르크에 집을 살 거라고.' 그러고는 자기가 호흘라코바 부인한테 구애하고 있다고 털어놓더군. 그 부인은 젊었을 때도 별로 머리가 없었는데, 이제 마흔이 되니 그나마 있던 것마저 잃어버렸다는 거야. '하지만 그 여자는 끔찍하게 감상적이거든.' 그놈이 그러더라. '그 점을 노려서 사로잡을 거야. 결혼하면 그 여자를 페테르부르크로 데려가서 거기서 신문을 창간할 거다.' 그러면서 그놈은 입에 침이 마를 지경이었어, 그 짐승 같은 놈이, 과부가 탐나서가 아니라 십오만 루블이 탐나서 말이야. 그러고는 나도 그 말을 믿게 만들었지. 날마다 나를 보러 왔어. '여자가 넘어오고 있어.' 하고 큰소리쳤지. 기쁨에 겨워 환하게 빛나더라. 그런데 그러다가, 별안간 그 집에서 쫓겨났지 뭐냐. 페르호틴이 모든 걸 휩쓸어 버린 거야, 잘했다! 그놈을 집에서 내쫓아 준 그 어리석은 늙은 멍청이한테 입이라도 맞춰 주고 싶다. 아무튼 그놈이 이 엉터리 시를 썼던 거야. '내가 시를 쓰느라 손을 더럽힌 건 이번이 처음이야.' 그놈이 그러더라. '여자의 마음을 얻으려는 거니까 좋은 명분이지. 그 어리석은 여자의 재산을 손에 넣으면 사회에 크게 이바지할 수 있을 거다.' 그놈들은 자기네가 저지르는 온갖 더러운 짓마다 이런 사회적 명분을 갖다 붙인다니까! '어쨌든 이건 자네가 떠받드는 푸시킨의 시보다는 낫지.' 그놈이 그러더라. '난 이 시에서까지 계몽을 옹호하는 데 성공했으니까 말이야.' 푸시킨에 대해 그놈이 무슨 말을 하려는 건지는 나도 알아, 충분히 알겠어. 푸시킨이 정말 재능 있는 사람이었으면서도 고작 여자 발 따위나 노래했다면 말이지. 그런데 라키틴 그놈, 자기 엉터리 시를 어찌나 우쭐대던지! 그런 놈들의 허영이라니! '내 사랑하는 임의 부어오른 발의 쾌유에 부쳐' ― 그게 그놈이 생각해 낸 제목이야. 익살맞은 놈이지.\n\n매혹적인 작은 발이여, 부어오르고 붉어지고 쓰라릴지언정! 의사들이 와서 고약을 붙여 보지만 그래도 그녀를 고치지 못하네.\n\n허나 내가 두려운 건 그녀의 발이 아니라네 ― 그것은 차라리 푸시킨의 시신(詩神)에 어울릴 주제, 그녀의 발이 아니라 그녀의 머리일세, 나는 그녀가 정신을 잃을까 떠노라!\n\n이상하게도 그녀의 발이 부어오를수록 그녀의 지력은 시들어 가니 ― 오, 나는 기도하노라, 무슨 묘약이 있어 발과 두뇌를 함께 되살려 주기를!\n\n그놈은 돼지야, 영락없는 돼지지, 하지만 아주 능청맞다, 그 악당 같은 놈! 게다가 정말로 진보적인 사상을 집어넣었단 말이야. 그런데 그 부인이 그놈을 쫓아냈을 때 어찌나 화를 내던지! 이를 갈더라니까!\"\n\n\"그놈은 벌써 복수를 했어.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"호흘라코바 부인에 대해 단평을 한 편 썼지.\"\n\n그러고 나서 알료샤는 〈소문〉지에 실린 그 단평에 대해 간략하게 이야기해 주었다.\n\n\"그놈 짓이야, 그놈 짓이라고!\" 미탸가 인상을 찌푸리며 맞장구쳤다. \"그놈이 한 거지! 그 단평들이라는 게… 나도 알아… 이를테면 그루셴카에 대해 쓰인 모욕적인 글들 말이야… 그리고 카탸에 대해서도… 흠!\"\n\n그는 시달린 듯한 얼굴로 방을 가로질러 거닐었다.\n\n\"형, 나 오래 있을 수가 없어.\" 잠시 침묵하다가 알료샤가 말했다. \"내일은 형에게 크고도 두려운 날이 될 거야, 하느님의 심판이 이루어질 거라고… 난 형이 놀라워, 이렇게 여기서 거닐며 뭔지 모를 얘기들만 하고 있으니…\"\n\n\"아니, 나를 놀라워하지 마라.\" 미탸가 따뜻하게 가로막았다. \"내가 그 악취 나는 개 같은 놈 얘기를 해야겠냐? 그 살인자 얘기를? 그놈 얘기는 할 만큼 했어. 그 악취 풍기는 리자베타의 악취 나는 자식 얘기는 더는 하고 싶지 않다고! 하느님이 그놈을 죽이실 거야, 두고 봐라. 쉿!\"\n\n그는 흥분하여 알료샤에게 다가가더니 그에게 입을 맞추었다. 그의 두 눈이 이글거렸다.\n\n\"라키틴은 이걸 이해하지 못할 거야.\" 그는 일종의 황홀경에 빠져 입을 열었다. \"하지만 너는, 너는 이 모든 걸 이해할 거야. 그래서 너를 목말라하며 기다린 거다. 봐라, 정말 오래전부터 너한테 하고 싶었던 얘기가 어찌나 많았던지, 바로 여기, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 말이야, 그런데도 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 한마디도 못 했어. 도무지 그럴 순간이 오지 않는 것 같았거든. 이제는 더 기다릴 수가 없다. 너한테 내 마음을 다 쏟아 내야겠어. 형제여, 지난 두 달 동안 나는 내 안에서 새로운 인간을 발견했다. 내 안에서 새로운 인간이 일어선 거야. 그건 내 안에 숨어 있었는데, 하늘에서 내려온 이 일격이 없었다면 결코 표면으로 떠오르지 못했을 거다. 나는 두려워! 그런데 내가 광산에서 망치로 광석을 깨며 이십 년을 보낸들 그게 무슨 대수겠냐? 그런 건 조금도 두렵지 않아 ― 지금 내가 두려운 건 다른 거야, 그 새로운 인간이 나를 떠나 버릴까 봐 두려운 거지. 거기서도, 광산에서도, 땅속에서도, 내 곁에 있는 또 다른 죄수, 또 다른 살인자 안에서 인간의 마음을 발견할 수 있을 거야, 그리고 그자와 벗이 될 수도 있겠지, 거기서도 사람은 살고 사랑하고 고통받을 수 있으니까. 그 죄수의 얼어붙은 마음을 녹이고 되살릴 수도 있어, 몇 해고 그를 정성껏 돌볼 수도 있고, 마침내는 그 어두운 심연으로부터 고결한 영혼을, 느끼고 고통받는 존재를 끌어올릴 수도 있는 거야. 천사를 길러 낼 수도 있고, 영웅을 만들어 낼 수도 있어! 그런 자들이 어찌나 많은지, 수백 명이나 되지, 그리고 우리 모두에게 그들에 대한 책임이 있는 거다. 어째서 나는 하필 그런 순간에 그 '갓난아이' 꿈을 꾸었을까? '어째서 갓난아이는 저토록 가난한가?' 그건 그 순간 나에게 주어진 하나의 징표였어. 나는 그 갓난아이를 위해 가는 거다. 우리 모두가 모두에 대해 책임이 있으니까. 모든 '갓난아이들'에 대해서 말이야, 큰 아이들도 있고 작은 아이들도 있으니까. 모두가 '갓난아이들'이지. 나는 모두를 위해 간다, 누군가는 모두를 위해 가야 하니까. 나는 아버지를 죽이지 않았지만, 그래도 가야만 해. 나는 그걸 받아들이겠어. 이 모든 게 여기서, 바로 여기서, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 나에게 찾아온 거야. 거기엔 그런 자들이 숱하게 있어, 땅속에 수백 명씩, 손에 망치를 들고 말이지. 오, 그래, 우리는 쇠사슬에 묶일 테고 자유라곤 없겠지, 하지만 그때, 그 크나큰 슬픔 속에서 우리는 다시 기쁨으로 일어설 거야, 그 기쁨 없이는 인간도 살 수 없고 하느님도 존재하실 수 없는 그 기쁨으로 말이야, 하느님이 기쁨을 주시니까. 그건 그분의 특권이지 ― 위대한 특권이야. 아, 인간은 기도 속에 녹아들어야 해! 하느님이 없다면 거기 땅속에서 내가 무엇이 되겠냐? 라키틴은 비웃지! 그놈들이 하느님을 이 땅에서 몰아낸다 해도, 우리는 그분을 땅속에 모실 거야. 감옥 안에서는 하느님 없이 존재할 수 없어. 감옥 밖에서보다 더 불가능한 일이지. 그리고 그때 우리 땅속의 인간들은 대지의 깊은 곳에서 하느님께, 기쁨이 함께하시는 그분께 영광스러운 찬가를 부를 거야. 하느님과 그분의 기쁨에 영광 있으라! 나는 그분을 사랑한다!\"\n\n미탸는 이 격정적인 말을 쏟아 내며 거의 숨을 헐떡일 지경이었다. 그의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 입술이 떨렸으며, 두 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"그래, 삶은 충만한 거야, 땅속에도 삶은 있어.\" 그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"넌 못 믿을 거다, 알렉세이, 내가 지금 얼마나 살고 싶은지, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 존재와 의식에 대한 갈증이 내 안에 얼마나 솟구쳤는지 말이야. 라키틴은 그걸 이해 못 해. 그놈이 신경 쓰는 거라곤 집을 짓고 셋방을 놓는 것뿐이지. 하지만 나는 너를 그리워해 왔어. 그리고 고통이 뭐란 말이냐? 난 그게 두렵지 않아, 설령 그게 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 크다 해도 말이지. 이제 난 그게 두렵지 않아. 전에는 두려웠지만. 알아 두렴, 어쩌면 나는 재판에서 아예 입을 열지 않을지도 몰라… 그리고 지금 내 안에는 무슨 힘이 가득 차 있는 것 같아, 어떤 것이든, 어떤 고통이든 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같다고, 다만 매 순간 나 자신에게 이렇게 말하고 또 말할 수만 있다면 말이야. '나는 존재한다.' 수천 가지 고뇌 속에서도 ― 나는 존재한다. 고문대 위에서 괴로워하면서도 ― 그래도 나는 존재한다! 기둥 위에 홀로 앉아 있어도 ― 나는 존재한다! 나는 태양을 본다, 설령 태양을 보지 못한다 해도 그것이 거기 있음을 안다. 그리고 거기에, 태양이 거기 있음을 안다는 그 사실 안에 온전한 삶 하나가 담겨 있는 거야. 알료샤, 내 천사야, 이 모든 철학이 나를 죽이고 있어. 빌어먹을 것들! 이반 형은―\"\n\n\"이반 형이 어쨌다는 거야?\" 알료샤가 끼어들었으나 미탸는 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"봐라, 전에는 이런 의심을 조금도 품지 않았는데, 사실은 그 모든 게 내 안에 숨어 있었던 거야. 어쩌면 바로 내가 이해하지 못하는 관념들이 내 안에서 들끓고 있었기 때문에, 내가 술을 마시고 싸우고 미쳐 날뛰곤 했던 건지도 몰라. 그것들을 내 안에서 짓누르고, 잠재우고, 질식시키려고 말이지. 이반은 라키틴 같은 놈이 아니야, 그 안에는 관념이 있어. 이반은 스핑크스고 입을 다물고 있어. 늘 침묵하지. 나를 괴롭히는 건 하느님이야. 오직 그것만이 나를 괴롭히는 거지. 만약 그분이 존재하지 않는다면? 만약 라키틴 말이 옳다면 ― 그게 인간들이 지어낸 관념일 뿐이라면? 그렇다면 그분이 존재하지 않는다면, 인간이 이 땅의, 우주의 으뜸이지. 굉장하지! 다만 하느님 없이 어떻게 선하게 될 수 있을까? 그게 문제야. 나는 늘 그리로 되돌아온다. 그렇다면 인간은 누구를 사랑하게 될까? 누구에게 감사하게 될까? 누구에게 찬가를 부르게 될까? 라키틴은 비웃지. 라키틴은 하느님 없이도 인류를 사랑할 수 있다고 해. 흥, 그런 소리는 콧물이나 흘리는 멍청이나 늘어놓을 수 있는 거지. 난 도무지 이해할 수가 없어. 라키틴한테는 삶이 쉽지. '자네는 차라리 시민권 확대를 고민하거나, 아니면 고기값을 잡아 두는 게 낫지. 그게 철학보다 더 단순하고 직접적으로 인류에 대한 사랑을 보여 주는 길이야.' 내가 그놈한테 대꾸했지. '흥, 그렇지만 자네는 하느님이 없으니, 자네한테 유리하다면 오히려 고기값을 올려서, 한 코페이카마다 일 루블씩 남겨 먹기가 더 쉬울걸.' 그놈이 발끈하더라. 그런데 결국, 선이란 게 뭐냐? 그것 좀 대답해 봐라, 알렉세이. 선이라는 게 나한테는 이런 것이고 중국인한테는 저런 것이라면, 그건 상대적인 거잖아. 안 그러냐? 상대적인 게 아니야? 음흉한 질문이지! 이 질문 때문에 내가 이틀 밤이나 잠을 못 이뤘다고 하면 너는 웃지 않겠지. 난 그저 어떻게 사람들이 살아가면서 그것에 대해 아무 생각도 안 하는지 그게 궁금할 뿐이야. 다 허영이지! 이반은 하느님이 없어. 이반한테는 관념이 있지. 그건 내 능력 밖이야. 그런데 그는 침묵해. 난 그가 프리메이슨이라고 믿는다. 물어봤지만 입을 다물더라. 나는 그의 영혼의 샘에서 물을 마시고 싶었어 ― 그런데 그는 침묵했어. 하지만 딱 한 번 말 한마디를 흘리긴 했지.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 했는데?\" 알료샤가 얼른 받아 물었다.\n\n\"내가 그한테 물었어. '그렇다면 만약 그렇다면, 모든 것이 허용되는 거 아닌가?' 그가 인상을 찌푸리더라. '우리 아버지 표도르 파블로비치는' 하고 그가 말했어. '돼지였지만, 그분의 생각은 충분히 옳았어.' 그가 흘린 게 그거였어. 그가 한 말은 그게 다였지. 라키틴보다 한 수 위였던 거야.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 알료샤가 씁쓸하게 동의했다. \"그 형이 언제 형한테 왔었어?\"\n\n\"그건 나중에 얘기하자. 지금은 다른 걸 말해야 해. 전에 너한테 이반에 대해서는 한마디도 안 했지. 맨 마지막으로 미뤄 뒀어. 여기 내 일이 끝나고 판결이 내려지면, 그때 너한테 뭔가를 말해 줄게. 전부 말해 줄 거야. 우리한테는 엄청난 일이 걸려 있어… 그리고 그 일에서 네가 나의 심판자가 되어 줘야 해. 하지만 지금은 그 얘기를 꺼내지 마, 잠자코 있어. 너는 내일 얘기를, 재판 얘기를 하지만, 믿어지겠냐, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다.\"\n\n\"변호사하고는 얘기해 봤어?\"\n\n\"변호사가 무슨 소용이냐? 그한테 죄다 말했지. 도시에서 자란 무르고 교활한 놈이야 ― 또 하나의 베르나르지! 그런데 그놈은 나를 믿지 않아 ― 눈곱만큼도. 한번 상상해 봐라, 그놈은 내가 했다고 믿는다니까. 다 보여. '그렇다면' 하고 내가 물었지. '뭣 하러 나를 변호하러 온 거요?' 그놈들 다 뒈져 버려라! 게다가 의사까지 불러왔어, 내가 미쳤다는 걸 증명하려고 말이야. 난 그딴 건 받아들이지 않아! 카테리나 이바노브나는 무슨 일이 있어도 끝까지 자기 '의무'를 다하고 싶어 해, 그게 아무리 무리한 일이라도!\" 미탸가 씁쓸하게 웃었다. \"그 고양이 같은 여자! 매정한 것! 그 여자는 내가 모크로예에서 자기를 두고 '진노가 대단한' 여자라고 말한 걸 알고 있어. 그놈들이 그 말을 옮긴 거지. 그래, 나한테 불리한 사실들이 바다의 모래알처럼 불어났어. 그리고리는 자기 주장을 굽히지 않아. 그리고리는 정직하지만 바보지. 많은 사람들이 바보라서 정직한 거야. 그게 라키틴의 지론이지. 그리고리는 내 적이야. 그리고 어떤 사람들은 벗보다 적일 때가 더 나아. 카테리나 이바노브나 말이다. 나는 두려워, 오, 두렵다, 그 여자가 그 사천 루블 때문에 나한테 땅에 닿도록 절했던 일을 입에 올릴까 봐. 그 여자는 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 거야. 난 그 여자의 희생을 원하지 않아. 그놈들은 재판에서 나에게 수치를 안길 거야. 내가 어떻게 그걸 견딜지 모르겠다. 그 여자한테 가 봐라, 알료샤, 법정에서 그 얘기를 하지 말아 달라고 부탁해 줘, 그럴 수 있겠냐? 하지만 다 빌어먹으라지, 상관없어! 어떻게든 헤쳐 나갈 테니까. 난 그 여자를 동정하지 않아. 그건 자업자득이야. 받을 만한 대접을 받는 거지. 나한테도 내 나름대로 할 얘기가 있을 테니까, 알렉세이.\" 그가 또다시 씁쓸하게 웃었다. \"다만… 다만 그루샤, 그루샤가 문제야! 맙소사! 어째서 그 여자가 그런 고통을 짊어져야 하는 거냐?\" 그가 갑자기 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다. \"그루샤가 나를 죽이고 있어. 그 여자 생각이 나를 죽여, 죽인다고. 방금 전까지 그 여자가 나하고 함께 있었어…\"\n\n\"오늘 형 때문에 무척 마음이 상했다고 그러던데.\"\n\n\"알아. 빌어먹을 내 성질! 질투였어. 미안했지, 그래서 떠날 때 입을 맞춰 줬어. 용서를 빌지는 않았지만.\"\n\n\"왜 안 빌었어?\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n갑자기 미탸가 거의 즐겁다는 듯 웃었다.\n\n\"하느님이 너를 지켜 주시기를, 얘야, 네가 사랑하는 여자한테 잘못을 저질렀다고 용서를 비는 일이 결코 없도록 말이다. 특히 네가 사랑하는 여자한테는, 네가 아무리 큰 잘못을 저질렀다 해도 말이야. 여자란 ― 여자라는 건 악마나 알까 무슨 수로 알겠냐! 어쨌든 나는 여자에 대해 좀 알지. 한번 여자한테 네 잘못을 인정해 봐라. '미안해, 용서해 줘.' 하고 말해 봐, 그러면 비난이 소나기처럼 쏟아질걸! 무슨 수를 써도 여자가 너를 단순하고 직접적으로 용서해 주는 일은 없을 거야, 너를 흙먼지 속까지 짓밟아 놓고, 일어나지도 않은 일들을 끄집어내고, 죄다 들춰내고, 아무것도 잊지 않고, 거기에 제멋대로 뭔가를 보태고, 그러고 나서야 너를 용서할 거다. 가장 나은 여자, 가장 나은 여자라도 그렇게 한다고. 부스러기란 부스러기는 죄다 긁어모아서 네 머리 위에 쏟아부을 거야. 그 여자들은 너를 산 채로 가죽을 벗길 태세야, 정말이지, 하나같이 다, 그 천사들 없이는 우리가 살 수 없는 바로 그 천사들이 말이지! 분명하고 솔직하게 말하마, 얘야, 점잖은 사내라면 누구나 어떤 여자의 손아귀에 잡혀 있어야 하는 거야. 그게 내 신념이다 ― 신념이 아니라 느낌이지. 사내는 너그러워야 해, 그건 사내한테 수치가 아니야! 영웅한테도, 카이사르한테조차도 수치가 아니지! 하지만 그래도 무슨 일이 있어도 절대로 여자한테 용서를 빌지는 마라. 여자들 때문에 신세를 망친 형 미탸가 너한테 일러 주는 그 규칙을 잊지 마. 아니, 차라리 용서를 빌지 않고 어떻게든 그루샤한테 만회를 해야겠어. 난 그 여자를 떠받든다, 알렉세이, 떠받들어. 다만 그 여자가 그걸 못 볼 뿐이야. 아니, 그 여자는 여전히 내가 자기를 충분히 사랑하지 않는다고 여겨. 그래서 나를 괴롭혀, 자기 사랑으로 나를 괴롭힌다고. 지난날은 아무것도 아니었어! 지난날에는 그저 그 여자의 그 지옥 같은 몸의 곡선이 나를 괴롭혔지만, 지금은 그 여자의 영혼을 통째로 내 영혼 안에 받아들였고, 그 여자를 통해 나 자신이 비로소 사람이 된 거야. 우리를 결혼시켜 줄까? 안 시켜 주면 나는 질투로 죽고 말 거야. 날마다 별생각이 다 든다고… 그 여자가 나에 대해 너한테 뭐라고 하던?\"\n\n알료샤는 그날 그루셴카가 자기에게 했던 말을 전부 되풀이해 들려주었다. 미탸는 귀를 기울이며 몇몇 대목은 다시 말해 보라고 하더니, 흡족해하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그럼 그 여자는 내가 질투한 걸 화내지 않는 거구나?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"영락없는 여자라니까! '나도 마음이 거센 여자예요!' 아, 난 그런 거센 마음을 사랑해, 누가 나를 질투하는 건 못 견디지만 말이야. 그건 도무지 참을 수가 없어. 우린 싸우게 될 거야. 하지만 난 그 여자를 사랑할 거야, 한없이 사랑할 거다. 우리를 결혼시켜 줄까? 죄수들도 결혼을 허락해 주나? 그게 문제야. 그 여자 없이는 나는 살 수가 없어…\"\n\n미탸는 인상을 찌푸린 채 방을 가로질러 거닐었다. 방 안은 거의 어두워져 있었다. 그는 갑자기 끔찍하게 괴로워하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그래, 비밀이 있다고 그 여자가 그러더라, 비밀이? 우리가 자기를 두고 음모를 꾸미고 있고, 거기에 카탸가 한몫 끼어 있다고 여기는 거지. 아니야, 사랑스러운 내 그루셴카, 그게 아니란다. 너는 그 어리석은 여자 특유의 방식으로 한참 빗나간 거야. 알료샤, 얘야, 자, 어디 해 보자! 너한테 우리 비밀을 말해 주마!\"\n\n그는 주위를 둘러보더니 자기 앞에 서 있던 알료샤에게 재빨리 바짝 다가가, 무언가 은밀한 기색으로 속삭였다. 사실은 아무도 그들의 말을 들을 수 없었는데도 말이다. 늙은 간수는 구석에서 졸고 있었고, 보초를 서는 병사들의 귀에는 단 한마디도 닿을 수 없었다.\n\n\"우리 비밀을 전부 말해 줄게.\" 미탸가 황급히 속삭였다. \"나중에 말하려고 했어, 너 없이 내가 어떻게 무슨 결정을 내릴 수 있겠냐? 너는 내게 모든 것이야. 내가 이반이 우리보다 한 수 위라고 말하긴 하지만, 너는 나의 천사다. 그건 너의 결정으로 결판날 일이야. 어쩌면 한 수 위인 건 이반이 아니라 너일지도 몰라. 봐라, 이건 양심의 문제야, 더 높은 양심의 문제지 ― 그 비밀이 어찌나 중대한지 나 혼자서는 결판을 낼 수가 없어서, 너한테 말할 수 있을 때까지 미뤄 둔 거다. 하지만 어쨌든 지금 결정하기엔 너무 일러, 판결을 기다려야 하니까. 판결이 내려지는 즉시 네가 내 운명을 결정해 줘. 지금은 결정하지 마. 지금 너한테 말은 해 줄게. 너는 듣기만 하고, 결정은 하지 마. 가만히 서서 잠자코 있어. 전부 말해 주진 않을 거야. 그저 그 생각만, 세세한 건 빼고 말해 줄 테니, 너는 잠자코 있어. 질문도 하지 말고, 꼼짝도 하지 마. 알겠지? 그런데, 맙소사, 네 그 눈은 어쩐다? 네가 말은 안 해도 그 눈이 네 결정을 나한테 말해 줄까 봐 두렵구나. 으! 무서워! 알료샤, 들어라! 이반이 나더러 탈주하라고 권한다. 세세한 건 말 안 할게. 다 짜 놓았어, 다 손쓸 수 있다. 쉿, 결정하지 마. 나는 그루샤와 함께 미국으로 가는 거야. 알다시피 난 그루샤 없이는 못 살아! 만약 그놈들이 그루샤가 나를 따라 시베리아로 오는 걸 허락하지 않으면 어떡하냐? 죄수들도 결혼을 허락해 주나? 이반은 안 된다고 봐. 그런데 그루샤 없이 거기 땅속에서 망치나 들고 내가 뭘 하겠냐? 그저 망치로 내 머리통이나 박살 내고 말겠지! 하지만, 다른 한편으로, 내 양심은? 나는 고통으로부터 달아난 셈이 되잖아. 징표가 내려왔는데, 내가 그 징표를 내치는 거야. 구원의 길이 있는데, 거기에 등을 돌리는 거지. 이반은 미국에서, '선의를 가지고' 일하면 땅속에서보다 내가 더 쓸모 있을 수 있다고 해. 하지만 우리의 땅속 찬가는 어떻게 되는 거냐? 미국이 뭐란 말이야? 미국도 또다시 허영일 뿐이지! 게다가 미국에도 사기가 수두룩할 거다, 내 짐작에는. 나는 십자가 처형으로부터 달아난 셈이 되는 거야! 너한테 말하는 거다, 알렉세이, 너만이 이걸 이해할 수 있는 유일한 사람이니까. 다른 사람은 아무도 없어. 내가 너한테 찬가에 대해 말한 그 모든 게 다른 사람들한테는 어리석음이고, 미친 소리일 거야. 그놈들은 내가 정신이 나갔거나 바보라고 하겠지. 난 정신이 나가지도 않았고 바보도 아니야. 이반도 찬가에 대해서는 이해해. 이해하긴 하는데, 다만 대답을 안 해 ― 입을 다물 뿐이지. 그는 찬가를 믿지 않아. 말하지 마, 말하지 마. 네가 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 다 보여! 너는 벌써 결정을 내렸어. 결정하지 마, 나를 좀 봐주렴! 난 그루샤 없이는 살 수 없어. 재판이 끝날 때까지 기다려 줘!\"\n\n미탸는 제정신이 아닌 채로 말을 맺었다. 그는 두 손으로 알료샤의 두 어깨를 붙들었고, 갈망에 찬 열에 들뜬 두 눈을 동생에게 못 박았다.\n\n\"죄수들은 결혼을 허락받지 못하지, 그렇지?\" 그는 애원하는 목소리로 세 번째 같은 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n알료샤는 극도의 놀라움에 휩싸여 듣고 있었고, 깊이 마음이 움직였다.\n\n\"한 가지만 말해 줘.\" 그가 말했다. \"이반 형이 그 일에 무척 적극적이야, 그리고 그건 누구 생각이었어?\"\n\n\"그의 생각이야, 그의 생각, 그리고 그가 무척 적극적이지. 처음엔 나를 보러 오지도 않더니, 일주일 전에 갑자기 와서는 다짜고짜 그 얘기를 꺼내더라. 끔찍하게 열을 올려. 나한테 묻는 게 아니라 탈주하라고 명령하는 거야. 내가 너한테 하듯 그한테 내 마음을 다 보여 주고, 찬가에 대해서도 말해 줬는데도, 그는 내가 자기 말에 따르리라는 걸 의심치 않아. 자기가 다 손써 주겠다고 하더라. 모든 걸 알아봐 뒀다는 거야. 하지만 그건 나중 얘기다. 그는 그냥 거기에 단단히 마음을 굳혔어. 다 돈 문제지. 탈주에 만 루블을 내고, 미국 갈 자금으로 이만 루블을 주겠대. 그리고 만 루블이면 근사한 탈주를 꾸밀 수 있다는 거야.\"\n\n\"그러면서 무슨 일이 있어도 나한테는 말하지 말라고 했어?\" 알료샤가 다시 물었다.\n\n\"아무한테도 말하지 말라고, 특히 너한테는 더더욱. 무슨 일이 있어도 너한테는 말하지 말라고 했어. 틀림없이, 네가 내 양심처럼 내 앞을 가로막고 설까 봐 두려운 거지. 내가 너한테 말했다는 건 그한테 알리지 마. 무슨 일이 있어도 그한테는 말하지 마.\"\n\n\"형 말이 맞아.\" 알료샤가 단언했다. \"재판이 끝나기 전에는 아무것도 결정할 수가 없어. 재판이 끝나면 형 스스로 결정하게 될 거야. 그때 형 안에서 그 새로운 인간을 발견하게 될 테고, 그가 결정할 거야.\"\n\n\"새로운 인간이든, 아니면 베르나르식으로 결정할 베르나르든 간에 말이지, 사실 나 자신도 경멸스러운 베르나르라고 믿으니까.\" 미탸가 씁쓸하게 이를 드러내며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 형, 그럼 무죄로 풀려날 가망은 전혀 없는 거야?\"\n\n미탸는 초조하게 어깨를 으쓱하고는 고개를 가로저었다. \"알료샤, 얘야, 이제 너 갈 시간이다.\" 그가 갑자기 서둘러 말했다. \"마당에서 소장이 소리치는구나. 곧 여기로 올 거야. 우린 너무 늦었어. 규칙에 어긋나는 거지. 어서 나를 안아 다오. 입을 맞춰 줘! 십자 성호를 그어 다오, 얘야, 내일 내가 짊어져야 할 그 십자가를 위해서 말이야.\"\n\n그들은 서로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"이반은,\" 미탸가 갑자기 말했다. \"나더러 탈주하라고 권하지. 하지만, 물론, 그는 내가 했다고 믿어.\"\n\n그의 입가에 서글픈 미소가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"형이 했다고 믿느냐고 그한테 물어봤어?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 못 물어봤어. 묻고 싶었지만, 그럴 수가 없었어. 그럴 용기가 없었지. 하지만 그의 눈에서 봤어. 자, 그럼, 잘 가라!\"\n\n그들은 다시 한번 황급히 입을 맞추었고, 알료샤가 막 나가려는 참에 미탸가 갑자기 그를 불러 세웠다.\n\n\"나를 마주 보고 서 봐라! 그래, 그렇지!\" 그러고는 다시 알료샤를 붙들어 두 손을 그의 두 어깨에 얹었다. 그의 얼굴이 별안간 새파랗게 질려, 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서도 그것이 무섭도록 또렷이 드러났다. 그의 입술이 실룩거렸고, 두 눈이 알료샤에게 못 박혔다.\n\n\"알료샤, 하느님 앞에서 하듯 나한테 진실을 다 말해 다오. 너는 내가 했다고 믿느냐? 정말, 네 속으로 정말 그걸 믿어? 진실을 다 말해, 거짓말은 하지 말고!\" 그가 필사적으로 외쳤다.\n\n알료샤에게는 눈앞의 모든 것이 일렁이는 듯했고, 가슴에 무언가 칼에 찔리는 듯한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"쉿! 무슨 소리야?\" 그가 어쩔 줄 몰라하며 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"진실을 다, 전부 다, 거짓말은 하지 마!\" 미탸가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"난 단 한순간도 형이 살인자라고 믿은 적이 없어!\" 알료샤의 가슴속에서 떨리는 목소리가 터져 나왔고, 그는 마치 하느님을 불러 자기 말의 증인으로 삼으려는 듯 오른손을 허공으로 들어 올렸다.\n\n미탸의 얼굴 전체가 환희로 밝아졌다.\n\n\"고맙다!\" 그는 마치 기절했다가 한숨을 토해 내듯 천천히 말했다. \"이제 너는 나에게 새 생명을 줬어. 믿어지냐, 이 순간까지 나는 너한테, 너한테조차 물어보기가 두려웠다. 자, 어서 가! 너는 나한테 내일을 위한 힘을 주었어. 하느님의 축복이 있기를! 자, 어서 가렴! 이반을 사랑해라!\" 이것이 미탸의 마지막 말이었다.\n\n알료샤는 눈물을 흘리며 밖으로 나왔다. 미탸 안에 그런 불신이, 자기에게조차, 알료샤에게조차 그런 못 미더움이 있었다니 ― 이 모든 것이 갑자기 알료샤 앞에, 자기 불행한 형의 영혼 속에 자리한, 미처 짐작도 못 했던 절망과 가망 없는 슬픔의 심연을 열어 보였다. 강렬하고 한없는 연민이 순식간에 그를 사로잡았다. 그의 찢긴 가슴에 사무치는 통증이 일었다. \"이반을 사랑해라!\" ― 그는 문득 미탸의 말을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그는 이반에게로 가려는 참이었다. 하루 종일 이반을 몹시 만나고 싶었다. 그는 미탸 못지않게 이반에 대해서도 마음을 졸이고 있었고, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더욱 그랬다.\n\n# Chapter V. Not You, Not You!\n\n이반에게 가는 길에 그는 카테리나 이바노브나가 사는 집을 지나가야 했다. 창문에 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었다. 그는 문득 걸음을 멈추고 들어가 보기로 마음먹었다. 그는 카테리나 이바노브나를 못 본 지 일주일이 넘었다. 그러나 지금 문득, 이반이 그녀와 함께 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었으니, 더욱이 그 끔찍한 날을 하루 앞둔 밤이었기 때문이다. 초인종을 누르고, 중국식 등롱이 희미하게 비추는 계단을 올라가다가, 그는 한 사내가 내려오는 것을 보았고, 두 사람이 마주치는 순간 그가 자기 형임을 알아보았다. 그러니까 형은 방금 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 나오는 길이었다.\n\n\"아, 너로구나.\" 이반이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"그럼, 잘 가라! 그녀에게 가는 거냐?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"권하고 싶지는 않다. 그녀는 마음이 어지러우니, 너까지 가면 더 어지러워질 게다.\"\n\n순간 위쪽에서 문이 활짝 열리며, 별안간 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요! 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그이한테서 오시는 길인가요?\"\n\n\"네, 형님과 함께 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그이가 제게 무슨 전갈이라도 남겼나요? 올라오세요, 알료샤. 그리고 당신, 이반 표도로비치, 당신은 돌아오셔야 해요, 꼭이요. 들리세요?\"\n\n카탸의 목소리에는 어찌나 단호한 기색이 서려 있던지, 이반은 잠시 망설이다가 알료샤와 함께 돌아가기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n\"엿듣고 있었군.\" 그는 성난 목소리로 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸으나, 알료샤가 그 말을 들었다.\n\n\"외투를 입은 채로 있는 걸 양해해 주시오.\" 이반이 응접실로 들어서며 말했다. \"앉지는 않겠소. 일 분 이상 머물지도 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"앉으세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 카테리나 이바노브나는 정작 자신은 선 채로 말했다. 그동안 그녀는 거의 변하지 않았으나, 검은 눈동자에는 불길한 광채가 어려 있었다. 알료샤는 훗날, 그 순간 그녀가 유난히 아름답게 보였던 것을 기억해 냈다.\n\n\"그이가 제게 뭘 전하라고 하던가요?\"\n\n\"오직 한 가지뿐이었습니다.\" 알료샤가 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"그게 무엇이냐 하면, 재판에서 부디 자신을 아끼시고, 그러니까……\" 그는 조금 당황했다. \"처음 알게 되었을 무렵, 그 도시에서 두 분 사이에 있었던 일을…… 발설하지 마시라는 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"아! 그 돈 때문에 제가 땅바닥에 엎드려 절했던 일 말이군요!\" 그녀는 쓰디쓴 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"그래, 그이가 두려워하는 게 저인가요, 자기 자신인가요? 누구를 아끼라는 거죠—그이를, 아니면 저를? 말씀해 주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치!\"\n\n알료샤는 그녀를 이해하려 애쓰며 골똘히 바라보았다.\n\n\"두 분 다입니다.\" 그가 나직이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군요.\" 그녀는 심술궂게 쏘아붙였고, 갑자기 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"당신은 아직 저를 모르세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그녀가 위협하듯 말했다. \"저 자신조차도 저를 모르는걸요. 내일 제가 심문을 받고 나면, 당신은 어쩌면 저를 발밑에 짓밟고 싶어질지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"당신은 떳떳하게 증언하실 겁니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"필요한 건 그것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"여자란 흔히 떳떳하지 못한 법이에요.\" 그녀가 으르렁거리듯 내뱉었다. \"바로 한 시간 전만 해도 저는 그 괴물을 만지는 것조차 두렵다고 느꼈어요…… 마치 그가 파충류라도 되는 양…… 하지만 아니에요, 제겐 그이가 여전히 한 인간인걸요! 그런데 정말 그이가 그랬을까요? 그이가 살인자인가요?\" 그녀는 별안간 히스테릭하게 소리치며, 이반에게 홱 돌아섰다. 알료샤는 그녀가 이반에게 그 질문을 이미 던졌었다는 것을, 어쩌면 그가 들어오기 바로 직전에, 그것도 처음이 아니라 수백 번째로 던졌고, 두 사람이 끝내 다투고 말았다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다.\n\n\"저는 스메르쟈코프를 만나러 갔었어요…… 그이가 아버지를 죽였다고 저를 설득한 사람이 바로 당신이에요. 저는 오직 당신만을 믿었다고요!\" 그녀는 여전히 이반을 향해 말을 이었다. 그는 어딘가 억지스러운 미소를 그녀에게 지어 보였다. 알료샤는 그녀의 어조에 흠칫했다. 두 사람 사이에 그토록 허물없는 친밀함이 있으리라고는 짐작도 못 했던 것이다.\n\n\"뭐, 어쨌든 이쯤 하면 충분하오.\" 이반이 대화를 잘라 버렸다. \"나는 가겠소. 내일 다시 오리다.\" 그러고는 그 자리에서 곧장 돌아서서, 방을 나가 곧바로 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 명령하듯 단호한 몸짓으로 알료샤의 두 손을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"저이를 따라가세요! 따라잡으세요! 단 일 분도 혼자 두지 마세요!\" 그녀가 다급히 속삭였다. \"저이는 미쳤어요! 저이가 미쳤다는 걸 모르세요? 저이는 열에 들떠 있어요, 신경성 열병이에요. 의사가 제게 그렇게 말했어요. 어서, 저이를 쫓아가세요……\"\n\n알료샤는 벌떡 일어나 이반을 뒤쫓아 달려갔는데, 그는 쉰 걸음도 채 앞서가 있지 않았다.\n\n\"뭘 바라는 거냐?\" 그는 알료샤가 자기를 쫓아오는 것을 보고 홱 돌아섰다. \"내가 미쳤으니 따라잡으라고 그녀가 시킨 거겠지. 다 외울 만큼 잘 알고 있다.\" 그가 짜증스럽게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그분이 잘못 안 거예요, 물론. 하지만 형님이 아프다는 건 옳은 말이에요.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"방금 형님 얼굴을 봤어요. 몹시 아파 보여요, 형.\"\n\n이반은 멈추지 않고 계속 걸었다. 알료샤가 그를 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"그런데 알겠느냐, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 사람이 어떻게 미쳐 가는지를?\" 이반이 별안간 차분한 목소리로, 짜증이라곤 흔적도 없이, 더없이 단순한 호기심의 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 몰라요. 미치는 데도 갖가지 종류가 있겠죠.\"\n\n\"그러면 자기가 미쳐 가고 있다는 걸 스스로 알아챌 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"그런 처지에서 자기 자신을 또렷이 볼 수는 없을 것 같은데요.\" 알료샤가 놀라며 대답했다.\n\n이반은 반 분쯤 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"나하고 이야기를 하고 싶거든, 제발 화제를 바꿔라.\" 그가 갑자기 말했다.\n\n\"아, 생각난 김에 말하자면, 형님께 드릴 편지가 있어요.\" 알료샤가 머뭇거리며 말하고는, 주머니에서 리즈의 쪽지를 꺼내 이반에게 내밀었다. 두 사람은 마침 가로등 바로 아래에 있었다. 이반은 단번에 필체를 알아보았다.\n\n\"아, 그 작은 악마한테서 온 거로군!\" 그는 심술궂게 웃고는, 봉투를 뜯지도 않고 갈기갈기 찢어 공중에 던져 버렸다. 찢긴 조각들이 바람에 흩날렸다.\n\n\"아직 열여섯도 안 됐을 텐데, 벌써 제 몸을 내던지고 있다니.\" 그가 경멸하듯 말하며, 다시 거리를 성큼성큼 걸어갔다.\n\n\"제 몸을 내던지다니, 그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n\"음탕한 여자들이 제 몸을 내던지듯이, 그렇다는 거다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그러실 수 있어요, 형, 어떻게 그러실 수 있어요?\" 알료샤가 비통한 목소리로 격하게 부르짖었다. \"그앤 아이예요. 형님은 지금 아이를 모욕하는 거예요! 그앤 아파요. 게다가 몹시 아파요. 어쩌면 그애도 광기의 벼랑 끝에 서 있는지도 몰라요…… 저는 형님한테서 그애를 구할 무슨 말을 들을 수 있기를 바랐어요.\"\n\n\"내게서 들을 건 아무것도 없다. 그애가 아이라면, 나는 그애의 유모가 아니야. 조용히 해라, 알렉세이. 그애 얘기는 그만둬. 나는 그애에 대해 생각조차 하고 있지 않다.\"\n\n두 사람은 다시 한동안 말이 없었다.\n\n\"그녀는 지금 밤새도록 성모님께 기도를 드리겠지, 내일 재판에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알려 달라고 말이야.\" 그가 다시 날카롭고 성난 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"형님은…… 카테리나 이바노브나 말씀이에요?\"\n\n\"그래. 미탸를 구할지 파멸시킬지를 두고 말이야. 그녀는 위로부터 빛이 내려오기를 기도할 거다. 알겠느냐, 그녀는 스스로 마음을 정하지 못하거든. 아직 결심할 틈이 없었던 거야. 그녀는 나까지도 자기 유모로 여기지. 내가 그녀에게 자장가를 불러 주기를 바란다고.\"\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나는 형님을 사랑하고 있어요, 형.\" 알료샤가 슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 그녀에게 그다지 끌리지 않는다.\"\n\n\"그분은 괴로워하고 있어요. 그런데 어째서 형님은…… 이따금 그분에게 희망을 품게 하는 말을 하시는 거죠?\" 알료샤가 머뭇거리는 책망조로 말을 이었다. \"형님이 그분에게 희망을 주었다는 걸 저는 알아요. 이렇게 말씀드리는 걸 용서하세요.\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나는 그녀에게 마땅히 해야 할 대로 행동할 수가 없어—완전히 관계를 끊고 그렇다고 그녀에게 곧이곧대로 말해 버리는 일 말이야.\" 이반이 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"나는 그 살인자에게 판결이 내려질 때까지 기다려야 해. 지금 그녀와 관계를 끊어 버리면, 그녀는 내일 재판에서 그 비열한 놈을 파멸시킴으로써 내게 복수할 거야. 그녀는 그를 증오하고, 자기가 그를 증오한다는 걸 알고 있으니까. 그건 죄다 거짓이야—거짓 위에 거짓이지! 내가 그녀와 관계를 끊지 않는 한, 그녀는 계속 희망을 품을 테고, 내가 그 괴물을 곤경에서 빼내고 싶어 한다는 걸 알기에 그를 파멸시키지 않을 거야. 그 빌어먹을 판결이 어서 나기만 한다면!\"\n\n\"살인자\"와 \"괴물\"이라는 말이 알료샤의 가슴속에 고통스럽게 메아리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 그분이 어떻게 미탸를 파멸시킬 수 있다는 거죠?\" 그가 이반의 말을 곱씹으며 물었다. \"그분이 미탸를 파멸시킬 무슨 증언을 할 수 있다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"너는 아직 그걸 모르지. 그녀는 미탸가 직접 쓴 문서를 손에 쥐고 있는데, 그게 미탸가 표도르 파블로비치를 죽였다는 걸 결정적으로 증명한단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요!\" 알료샤가 부르짖었다.\n\n\"어째서 있을 수 없는 일이라는 거냐? 내가 직접 읽어 봤는데.\"\n\n\"그런 문서는 있을 수 없어요!\" 알료샤가 격하게 거듭 말했다. \"있을 수 없어요, 형은 살인자가 아니니까요. 아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니에요, 형이 아니라고요!\"\n\n이반이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 네 생각엔 누가 살인자라는 거냐?\" 그가 짐짓 냉랭하게 물었다. 그의 목소리에는 거만한 기색마저 서려 있었다.\n\n\"누군지 형님도 아시잖아요.\" 알료샤가 낮고도 파고드는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"누구? 그 미치광이 백치, 간질쟁이 스메르쟈코프에 관한 그 신화 말이냐?\"\n\n알료샤는 별안간 온몸이 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"누군지 형님도 아시잖아요.\" 어쩔 수 없다는 듯 그의 입에서 터져 나왔다. 그는 숨조차 쉴 수 없을 지경이었다.\n\n\"누구? 누구냐고?\" 이반이 거의 사납게 소리쳤다. 그의 모든 자제력이 별안간 사라졌다.\n\n\"저는 단 한 가지만 알아요.\" 알료샤가 여전히 거의 속삭이듯 말을 이었다. \"아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니라는 거예요.\"\n\n\"'형이 아니라니'! '형이 아니라니' 그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 이반은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 굳어졌다.\n\n\"아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니에요, 형이 아니라고요!\" 알료샤가 단호하게 거듭 말했다.\n\n침묵이 반 분간 이어졌다.\n\n\"내가 죽이지 않은 건 나도 안다. 너 헛소리를 하는 거냐?\" 이반이 창백하게 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그의 눈은 알료샤에게 못 박혀 있었다. 두 사람은 다시 가로등 아래 서 있었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 이반 형. 형님은 자신이 살인자라고 여러 번 스스로에게 말했어요.\"\n\n\"내가 언제 그런 말을 했다는 거냐? 나는 모스크바에 있었어…… 내가 언제 그런 말을 했다고?\" 이반이 어쩔 줄 몰라 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"형님은 그 끔찍한 두 달 동안 혼자 있을 때, 스스로에게 그 말을 수도 없이 했어요.\" 알료샤가 여전히 부드럽고 또렷하게 말을 이었다. 그러나 그는 이제, 말하자면 자기 자신에 대해서가, 자기 자신의 뜻으로가 아니라, 거역할 수 없는 어떤 명령에 따라 말하고 있었다. \"형님은 자기 자신을 단죄했고, 살인자는 다름 아닌 형님 자신이라고 스스로에게 고백했어요. 하지만 형은 하지 않았어요. 형은 잘못 알고 있어요. 형은 살인자가 아니에요. 들리세요? 그건 형이 아니에요! 하느님이 형님께 그 말을 전하라고 저를 보내신 거예요.\"\n\n두 사람 다 말이 없었다. 침묵은 꼬박 일 분 동안 이어졌다. 둘 다 가만히 선 채 서로의 눈을 응시했다. 둘 다 창백했다. 갑자기 이반이 온몸을 부들부들 떨기 시작하며 알료샤의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"너, 내 방에 들어왔었구나!\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다. \"밤에, 그자가 왔을 때 거기 있었던 거야…… 실토해라…… 너 그자를 봤지, 그자를 봤지?\"\n\n\"누구 말씀이에요—미탸요?\" 알료샤가 어리둥절해하며 물었다.\n\n\"그놈 말이 아냐, 그 괴물은 빌어먹을!\" 이반이 광란하듯 소리쳤다. \"그자가 나를 찾아온다는 걸 너는 알고 있지? 어떻게 알아낸 거냐? 말해!\"\n\n\"그자가 누구예요! 형님이 누구 얘기를 하는 건지 모르겠어요.\" 알료샤가 더듬거리며, 차츰 겁에 질리기 시작했다.\n\n\"아니, 너는 알고 있어…… 안 그러면 어떻게 네가——? 네가 모를 리가 없어.\"\n\n별안간 그는 스스로를 억누르는 듯했다. 그는 가만히 서서 무언가를 곰곰이 생각하는 것 같았다. 야릇한 비웃음이 그의 입술을 일그러뜨렸다.\n\n\"형.\" 알료샤가 떨리는 목소리로 다시 입을 열었다. \"제가 형님께 이 말을 한 건, 형님이 제 말을 믿어 주실 줄 알기 때문이에요, 저는 그걸 알아요. 단번에 영영 말씀드릴게요, 그건 형이 아니에요. 들리세요, 단번에 영영! 비록 이 순간부터 형님이 저를 미워하게 될지라도, 하느님이 이 말을 형님께 하라고 제 마음속에 넣어 주신 거예요.\"\n\n그러나 이제 이반은 분명 자제력을 되찾은 듯했다.\n\n\"알렉세이 표도로비치.\" 그가 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"나는 예언자나 간질쟁이는 견딜 수가 없어—특히 하느님의 사자라는 자들은 더더욱—그건 너도 너무나 잘 알고 있지. 이 순간부터 너와는 모든 관계를 끊겠다, 아마 영원히. 여기 갈림길에서 나를 놓아주기 바란다. 네 숙소로 가는 길이기도 하지. 오늘은 특히 내게 오지 않도록 각별히 조심하는 게 좋을 거다! 들었느냐?\"\n\n그는 돌아서서, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 단호한 걸음으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"형.\" 알료샤가 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다. \"오늘 형님께 무슨 일이 생기거든, 그 누구보다 먼저 제게로 오세요!\"\n\n그러나 이반은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 알료샤는 이반이 어둠 속으로 사라질 때까지 갈림길 가로등 아래 서 있었다. 그러고 나서 그는 돌아서서 천천히 집을 향해 걸었다. 알료샤도 이반도 셋방살이를 하고 있었으니, 둘 다 표도르 파블로비치의 텅 빈 집에서 살고 싶어 하지 않았던 것이다. 알료샤는 어느 노동자 집에 가구 딸린 방 하나를 얻어 들어 있었다. 이반은 그에게서 좀 떨어진 곳에 살았다. 그는 어느 부유한 부인, 곧 한 관리의 미망인 소유인 번듯한 집에 딸린, 널찍하고 제법 안락한 별채를 빌려 쓰고 있었다. 그러나 그의 시중을 드는 사람이라고는 귀먹고 류머티즘을 앓는 늙은 노파 한 명뿐이었는데, 그녀는 저녁마다 여섯 시면 잠자리에 들고 아침마다 여섯 시면 일어났다. 이반은 근래 들어 자기 안락에 놀라우리만치 무심해졌고, 혼자 있기를 무척 좋아하게 되었다. 그는 자기가 사는 단칸방에서 무엇이든 손수 해결했고, 거처의 다른 방에는 좀처럼 발을 들이지 않았다.\n\n그는 집 대문 앞에 이르러 초인종에 손을 얹었다가, 별안간 멈춰 섰다. 그는 분노로 온몸이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 갑자기 그는 초인종에서 손을 떼고, 욕설을 내뱉으며 돌아서서, 빠른 걸음으로 반대 방향으로 걸어갔다. 그는 일 베르스타 반쯤 걸어, 작고 비스듬히 기운, 거의 오두막이나 다름없는 목조 가옥에 다다랐는데, 그곳에는 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 세 들어 살고 있었다. 그녀는 한때 표도르 파블로비치의 부엌으로 수프를 얻으러 드나들던 이웃 여자로, 스메르쟈코프가 언젠가 노래를 불러 주고 기타를 쳐 주었던 바로 그 여인이었다. 그녀는 자기네 작은 집을 팔고, 지금은 어머니와 함께 이곳에서 살고 있었다. 거의 죽어 가다시피 앓고 있던 스메르쟈코프는 표도르 파블로비치가 죽은 이래로 줄곧 그들과 함께 지내고 있었다. 이반이 지금 갑작스럽고도 거역할 수 없는 충동에 이끌려 찾아가는 사람이 바로 그였다.\n* 베르스타(верста): 약 1.067킬로미터에 해당하는 옛 러시아의 거리 단위.\n\n# Chapter VI. The First Interview With Smerdyakov\n\n이반이 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 스메르댜코프를 찾아간 것은 이번이 세 번째였다. 처음 그를 만나 이야기를 나눈 것은 도착한 첫날이었고, 그 후 다시 한 번, 두 주가 지나서 찾아간 적이 있었다. 그러나 그의 방문은 그 두 번째를 끝으로 막을 내렸으니, 이제 그를 본 지도 한 달이 넘었다. 그리고 그에 관한 소식이라고는 거의 듣지 못했다.\n\n이반은 아버지가 죽고 닷새가 지나서야 돌아왔으므로, 그가 돌아오기 바로 전날 치러진 장례식에는 참석하지 못했다. 그가 늦어진 까닭은 이러했다. 알료샤가 그의 모스크바 주소를 몰라 카테리나 이바노브나에게 전보를 쳐 달라고 부탁할 수밖에 없었는데, 그녀 역시 주소를 알지 못해 자기 언니와 고모에게 전보를 쳤고, 이반이 모스크바에 도착하는 대로 그들을 찾아가리라 짐작했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 도착하고 나흘이 지나서야 그들을 찾아갔다. 전보를 받자마자 그는 물론 부랴부랴 우리 고을로 달려왔다. 가장 먼저 그를 맞은 것은 알료샤였는데, 이반은 알료샤가 온 고을의 일반적인 견해와는 달리 미탸를 향한 의심을 한사코 받아들이지 않으며 스메르댜코프를 살인자라고 거리낌 없이 말하는 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐다. 나중에 경찰서장과 검사를 만나 혐의와 체포에 관한 자세한 내막을 듣고 나서 그는 더욱더 알료샤에게 놀랐고, 그의 견해를 다만 형제로서의 지나친 정과 미탸에 대한 동정 탓으로 돌렸으니, 이반이 알기로 알료샤는 그 형을 무척이나 따랐던 것이다.\n\n여기서 이반이 형 드미트리에게 품은 감정에 대해 한두 마디 해 두기로 하자. 그는 형을 노골적으로 싫어했으니, 기껏해야 이따금 연민을 느낄 뿐이었고, 그나마도 그 연민에는 커다란 경멸이, 거의 혐오에 가까운 것이 뒤섞여 있었다. 미탸의 인간됨 전부가, 심지어 그 생김새까지도 이반에게는 더없이 매력 없게 비쳤다. 이반은 형을 향한 카테리나 이바노브나의 사랑을 분개의 눈으로 바라보았다. 그러면서도 그는 도착한 첫날 미탸를 찾아갔는데, 그 면회는 형이 유죄라는 이반의 확신을 흔들기는커녕 오히려 더욱 굳혀 놓았다. 그가 본 형은 동요하고 신경이 곤두서 흥분해 있었다. 미탸는 말이 많았으나 몹시 산만하고 두서가 없었다. 그는 거친 말을 내뱉으며 스메르댜코프를 비난했고 끔찍하리만치 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 그가 주로 떠든 것은 삼천 루블에 관한 것이었으니, 그것을 아버지가 자기에게서 \"훔쳤다\"는 것이었다.\n\n\"그 돈은 내 것이었어, 내 돈이었단 말이야.\" 미탸는 거듭 되뇌었다. \"설령 내가 그걸 훔쳤다 한들, 나에게는 그럴 권리가 있었어.\"\n\n그는 자기에게 불리한 증거를 거의 반박하지 않았고, 어떤 사실을 자기에게 유리하게 돌려 보려 할 때면 그것도 터무니없고 두서없는 방식이었다. 그는 이반에게든 그 누구에게든 자신을 변호하고 싶어 하는 기색조차 거의 없었다. 오히려 그 반대로, 그는 자기에게 걸린 혐의에 화를 내며 거만하게 경멸했고, 끊임없이 발끈하여 모두를 욕했다. 열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언에 대해서도 그는 경멸하듯 웃을 뿐, \"문을 연 것은 악마였다\"고 단언했다. 그러나 그 사실에 대해 그는 조리 있는 설명이라곤 단 하나도 내놓지 못했다. 그는 첫 면회 자리에서 이반을 모욕하기까지 했으니, \"모든 것이 합법이다\"라고 떠드는 자들이 자기를 의심하고 추궁할 자격은 없다고 날카롭게 쏘아붙였던 것이다. 어쨌든 그날 그는 이반에게 결코 우호적이지 않았다. 미탸와의 그 면회를 마치자마자 이반은 처음으로 스메르댜코프를 찾아갔다.\n\n모스크바에서 돌아오는 기차 안에서 그는 줄곧 스메르댜코프와, 떠나기 전날 저녁 그와 나눈 마지막 대화를 곱씹었다. 여러 가지가 그에게는 알 수 없고 미심쩍게 여겨졌다. 그러나 그는 수사 판사에게 진술할 때 그 대화에 관해서는 당분간 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 그것을 스메르댜코프를 만난 뒤로 미뤄 두었으니, 그자는 그 무렵 병원에 있었다.\n\n헤르첸슈투베 의사와, 병원에서 만난 의사 바르빈스키는 이반의 집요한 물음에 대해, 스메르댜코프의 간질 발작은 의심할 여지 없이 진짜였다고 자신 있게 단언했으며, 참사가 일어난 날 그가 꾀병을 부린 것은 아니냐는 이반의 물음에는 오히려 의아해했다. 그들은 그 발작이 예사롭지 않은 것이었으며, 경련이 거듭하여 여러 차례 되풀이되는 통에 환자의 목숨이 실로 위태로웠고, 이제야, 약을 쓰고 난 지금에야 환자가 살아남으리라고 자신 있게 말할 수 있게 되었음을 이해시켰다. \"다만 충분히 있을 수 있는 일이지요.\" 헤르첸슈투베 의사가 덧붙였다. \"그의 이성이 영구히는 아니더라도 상당 기간 손상될 수 있다는 것 말입니다.\" 그렇다면 지금 그가 미친 것이냐고 이반이 조바심을 내며 묻자, 그들은 아직 온전한 의미에서 그런 것은 아니지만 어떤 비정상적인 징후는 감지된다고 일러 주었다. 이반은 그 비정상이라는 것이 무엇인지 직접 알아내기로 마음먹었다.\n\n병원에서 그는 곧바로 환자를 만나도록 허락받았다. 스메르댜코프는 별도의 병실에 놓인 간이침대에 누워 있었다. 그 방에는 침대가 하나 더 있을 뿐이었는데, 거기에는 수종으로 퉁퉁 부어 누가 봐도 거의 죽어 가는 고을의 한 상인이 누워 있었으니, 그가 두 사람의 대화를 방해할 일은 없었다. 스메르댜코프는 이반을 보자 미덥지 못하게 씩 웃었고, 처음 한순간은 긴장한 듯 보였다. 적어도 이반에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다. 그러나 그것은 잠깐뿐이었다. 그 후로는 오히려 스메르댜코프의 침착함에 그는 충격을 받았다. 첫눈에 이반은 그가 몹시 아프다는 것을 의심치 않았다. 그는 무척 쇠약했고, 혀를 움직이기조차 힘든 듯 천천히 말했으며, 한결 야위고 누렇게 떠 있었다. 이십 분간 이어진 면회 내내 그는 두통과 온몸의 통증을 끊임없이 호소했다. 거세된 듯한 그 야윈 얼굴은 어찌나 조그매졌는지, 머리칼은 헝클어졌고 앞이마에 솟은 곱슬머리는 가느다란 한 줌으로 곤두서 있었다. 그러나 무언가를 넌지시 암시하는 듯 가늘게 찌푸린 왼쪽 눈만은, 스메르댜코프가 조금도 변치 않았음을 드러냈다. \"영리한 자와 이야기하는 것은 언제나 보람이 있는 법이지요.\" 이반은 이내 그 말이 떠올랐다. 그는 침대 발치의 걸상에 앉았다. 스메르댜코프는 고통스레 안간힘을 쓰며 자리에서 몸을 옮겼으나, 먼저 입을 떼지는 않았다. 그는 묵묵히 입을 다물었고, 별로 흥미를 느끼는 기색조차 없었다.\n\n\"나와 얘기 좀 할 수 있겠나?\" 이반이 물었다. \"오래 지치게 하지는 않을 테니.\"\n\n\"물론 할 수 있습죠.\" 스메르댜코프가 가느다란 목소리로 웅얼거렸다. \"도련님께서는 돌아오신 지 오래되셨나요?\" 그는 마치 긴장한 손님을 다독이기라도 하듯 거들먹거리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오늘에야 도착했네……. 자네가 여기서 이 꼴이 된 걸 보러 온 거지.\"\n\n스메르댜코프가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"어째서 한숨을 쉬는 건가? 자네는 처음부터 다 알고 있지 않았나.\" 이반이 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n스메르댜코프는 한동안 무뚝뚝하게 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 어찌 모를 수 있었겠습니까? 미리부터 뻔한 일이었지요. 하지만 그게 그렇게 끝날 줄을 제가 어찌 알았겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무엇이 그렇게 끝나다니? 둘러대지 말게! 자네는 발작이 일어날 거라고 예언했어. 지하실로 내려가는 길에 말이야, 알지. 바로 그 자리까지 짚어 말했지.\"\n\n\"그걸 벌써 심문 때 말씀하셨습니까?\" 스메르댜코프가 침착하게 캐물었다.\n\n이반은 갑자기 화가 치밀었다.\n\n\"아니, 아직은 말하지 않았어. 하지만 반드시 말할 거야. 자네는 나에게 설명해야 할 게 잔뜩 있어, 이 사람아. 그리고 똑똑히 말해 두지, 나는 자네가 나를 가지고 놀게 내버려 두지 않을 테니까!\"\n\n\"제가 어찌 도련님을 가지고 놀겠습니까, 전능하신 하느님께 그러하듯 도련님께 온 믿음을 걸고 있는 마당에 말입니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 한순간 눈을 감을 뿐, 여전히 침착하게 말했다.\n\n\"우선 첫째로.\" 이반이 입을 열었다. \"간질 발작이란 미리 예언할 수 없는 거라는 걸 나는 알고 있어. 물어봤거든. 나를 속이려 들지 마. 날짜며 시각을 미리 알 수는 없는 법이야. 그런데 어떻게 자네는 나에게 날짜와 시각을, 게다가 지하실까지 미리 일러 줬던 거지? 일부러 발작을 꾸민 게 아니라면 어떻게 발작이 일어나 지하실 계단에서 굴러떨어질 거라고 알 수 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"어차피 저는 지하실에 내려가야 했습니다, 실은 하루에도 몇 번씩 말이지요.\" 스메르댜코프가 일부러 느릿느릿 말을 끌었다. \"꼭 그처럼 일 년 전에도 다락방에서 떨어졌습죠. 발작의 날과 시각을 미리 알 수 없다는 건 정말 맞는 말씀입니다만, 예감만은 늘 들 수 있는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 자네는 날짜와 시각을 분명히 예언했어!\"\n\n\"제 간질에 관해서라면, 도련님, 여기 의사 선생들께 여쭤보시는 편이 훨씬 나을 겁니다. 진짜 발작이었는지 꾸민 것이었는지 물어보시면 되지요. 제가 더 이상 무슨 말을 해 봐야 소용없습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지하실은? 어떻게 지하실 일을 미리 알 수 있었나?\"\n\n\"그놈의 지하실에서 영 헤어나질 못하시는군요! 제가 지하실로 내려갈 때 저는 무서운 두려움과 의심에 사로잡혀 있었습니다. 무엇보다 저를 떨게 한 것은, 도련님을 잃고 이 세상천지에 아무 보호자도 없이 남겨진다는 것이었지요. 그래서 지하실로 내려가면서 이렇게 생각했습니다. '자, 이제 곧 닥쳐올 거야, 당장 나를 쓰러뜨릴 거야, 내가 떨어지려나?' 바로 이 두려움 때문에 저는 늘 찾아오는 그 경련을 갑자기 느꼈고…… 그렇게 굴러떨어졌습니다. 그 모든 것을, 그리고 전날 저녁 대문 앞에서 도련님과 나눈 이전의 대화 전부를, 제가 얼마나 두려워했는지, 지하실 얘기를 했던 것까지 다 헤르첸슈투베 의사와 수사 판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치께 말씀드렸고, 그게 전부 조서에 적혔습니다. 그리고 여기 의사 바르빈스키 선생께서도 모두에게, 바로 그 생각이, 떨어질지도 모른다는 그 불안이 발작을 일으킨 거라고 확언하셨지요. 바로 그 순간 발작이 저를 덮친 겁니다. 그래서 그들은 그렇게, 틀림없이 그런 식으로 일어났을 거라고, 순전히 제 두려움 때문에 그리 됐을 거라고 적어 두었습니다.\"\n\n말을 마치자 스메르댜코프는 마치 기진맥진한 듯 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"그럼 자네는 그 모든 걸 진술에서 다 말했단 말이지?\" 이반이 다소 당황하여 말했다. 그는 두 사람의 대화를 그대로 옮기겠다는 위협으로 그를 겁주려 했는데, 알고 보니 스메르댜코프가 이미 그 모두를 스스로 진술해 버린 것이었다.\n\n\"제가 무얼 두려워해야 합니까? 온전한 진실을 죄다 적게 두면 그만이지요.\" 스메르댜코프가 단호하게 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 대문 앞에서 나눈 우리 대화를 한마디도 빼놓지 않고 다 말했나?\"\n\n\"아니요, 한마디도 빼놓지 않고는 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그때 자네가 떠벌렸듯이, 발작을 꾸며 낼 수 있다는 것도 그들에게 말했나?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그건 말하지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제 말해 보게, 자네는 어째서 그때 나를 체르마시냐로 보냈지?\"\n\n\"도련님께서 모스크바로 가 버리실까 봐 두려웠습니다. 어쨌든 체르마시냐가 더 가까우니까요.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야. 자네가 직접 떠나라고 권했어. 화를 피하라고 나에게 말했지.\"\n\n\"그건 그저 도련님을 향한 애정과 진실한 충정에서, 집안에 화가 닥칠 것을 내다보고 도련님을 지켜 드리려는 마음이었습니다. 다만 저는 저 자신을 지키고 싶은 마음이 더 컸지요. 그래서 화를 피하시라고 말씀드린 겁니다, 집안에 화가 있으리라는 걸 알아채시고 댁에 남아 아버님을 지켜 드리시라고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좀 더 직접적으로 말했어야지, 이 멍청아!\" 이반이 갑자기 발끈했다.\n\n\"그때 어떻게 더 직접적으로 말씀드릴 수 있었겠습니까? 그저 두려움 때문에 그렇게 말한 것이었고, 도련님께서 화를 내셨을지도 모를 일이지요. 드미트리 표도로비치께서 한바탕 소동을 부리며 그 돈을 가져가실까 봐 저는 충분히 겁이 났습니다, 그분은 그 돈을 거의 자기 것이나 다름없다고 여기셨으니까요. 하지만 그게 이런 살인으로 끝나리라고 누가 알았겠습니까? 저는 그분이 주인님 침대 요 밑 봉투에 든 삼천 루블만 가져가시리라고 생각했는데, 보십시오, 그분이 그분을 죽여 버린 겁니다. 도련님인들 그걸 어찌 짐작하셨겠습니까?\"\n\n\"하지만 자네 스스로 짐작할 수 없었다고 말하면서, 어떻게 내가 짐작하고 집에 남아 있을 수 있었겠나? 자네는 앞뒤가 안 맞아!\" 이반이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 도련님을 모스크바가 아니라 체르마시냐로 보낸 것에서 짐작하실 수 있었지요.\"\n\n\"그걸로 어떻게 짐작하란 말인가?\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 무척 지친 듯 보였고, 다시 한동안 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"제가 도련님께 모스크바로 가지 마시고 체르마시냐로 가시라고 청한 사실에서, 제가 도련님을 더 가까이 두고 싶어 했다는 걸 짐작하실 수 있었지요. 모스크바는 멀고, 드미트리 표도로비치께서도 도련님이 멀리 있지 않다는 걸 아시면 그토록 대담하게는 굴지 못하셨을 테니까요. 그리고 무슨 일이 생기면 도련님께서 저까지 지키러 와 주실 수 있었지요. 제가 그리고리 바실리예비치의 병환을 알려 드렸고, 발작이 일어날까 봐 두렵다고도 말씀드렸으니까요. 게다가 그 신호에 대해 설명해 드렸을 때, 그걸로 고인께 들어갈 수 있다는 것과 드미트리 표도로비치께서 저를 통해 그걸 죄다 알고 계시다는 것까지 말씀드렸으니, 저는 도련님께서 그분이 틀림없이 무슨 일을 저지르시리라는 걸 스스로 짐작하시고, 체르마시냐로도 가지 않으시고 남아 계시리라 여겼던 겁니다.\"\n\n'녀석은 웅얼거리기는 해도, 무척 조리 있게 말하는군.' 이반은 생각했다. '헤르첸슈투베가 말한 정신의 이상이라는 게 대체 뭐란 말이야?'\n\n\"자네는 나에게 잔꾀를 부리고 있어, 이 빌어먹을 놈!\" 그는 화가 치밀어 소리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 그때 도련님께서 진작에 다 짐작하셨다고 생각했습니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 더없이 천연덕스러운 태도로 응수했다.\n\n\"내가 짐작했더라면 남아 있었을 거야.\" 이반이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 저는 도련님께서 짐작하셨기 때문에, 화를 피하시려고, 그저 도망쳐 두려움 속에서 자신을 지키시려고 그토록 급히 떠나신 거라고 생각했지요.\"\n\n\"자네는 모든 사람이 자네처럼 그렇게 비겁하다고 여기나?\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 저는 도련님께서 저와 같은 줄로만 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"물론 나는 짐작했어야 했어.\" 이반이 동요하며 말했다. \"그리고 나는 자네 쪽에서 무슨 흉계가 꾸며지고 있다는 걸 정말로 짐작했지……. 다만 자네는 거짓말을 하고 있어, 또 거짓말을 하고 있어.\" 그는 문득 떠올라 소리쳤다. \"기억하나, 자네가 마차로 다가와 나에게 '영리한 자와 이야기하는 것은 언제나 보람이 있는 법이지요'라고 말했던 거? 그럼 자네는 나를 칭찬한 걸 보니 내가 떠나서 기뻤던 거로군?\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 거듭 한숨을 쉬었다. 그의 얼굴에 핏기가 한 줄 떠올랐다.\n\n\"제가 기뻤다면.\" 그는 다소 숨가쁘게 또박또박 말했다. \"그건 그저 도련님께서 모스크바가 아니라 체르마시냐로 가시기로 하셨기 때문입니다. 어쨌든 거기가 더 가까우니까요. 다만 그 말씀을 드린 것은 칭찬으로가 아니라 책망으로 드린 겁니다. 도련님께서는 그걸 이해하지 못하셨지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 책망이란 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 재앙을 내다보시고도 친아버님을 버리시고, 우리를 지켜 주려 하지 않으셨다는 책망이지요. 저는 그 삼천 루블을 훔쳤다는 죄로 언제든 끌려갈 수 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈!\" 이반이 다시 욕을 내뱉었다. \"잠깐, 자네는 검사와 수사 판사에게 그 신호 얘기를 했나?\"\n\n\"있는 그대로 죄다 말했습니다.\"\n\n이반은 또다시 속으로 의아해했다.\n\n\"그때 내가 무언가를 생각했다면.\" 그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그건 오로지 자네 쪽의 어떤 흉계에 관한 것이었어. 드미트리가 그를 죽일 수는 있어도, 그가 도둑질을 하리라고는—그땐 그렇게 믿지 않았지……. 하지만 자네에게서는 어떤 흉계든 나올 거라고 각오하고 있었어. 자네 스스로 발작을 꾸며 낼 수 있다고 나에게 말했지. 무엇 때문에 그런 말을 한 건가?\"\n\n\"그건 그저 저의 순박함 때문이었고, 저는 평생 일부러 발작을 꾸민 적이 한 번도 없습니다. 그저 그때 도련님 앞에서 뻐기느라고 그렇게 말한 것뿐이지요. 한낱 어리석은 짓이었습니다. 저는 그때 도련님이 어찌나 좋았던지, 도련님께 마음을 활짝 열었던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내 형은 대놓고 자네를 살인과 절도로 고발하고 있어.\"\n\n\"그분에게 달리 무슨 수가 있겠습니까?\" 스메르댜코프가 쓰디쓴 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그 많은 증거가 죄다 그분에게 불리한 마당에, 누가 그분의 말을 믿겠습니까? 그리고리 바실리예비치께서 문이 열린 걸 보셨다지요. 그러니 그분이 그 다음에 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠습니까? 하지만 그분이야 어쩔 수 없지요! 자기 목숨을 구하려고 벌벌 떨고 계신 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 천천히 말을 멈추더니, 갑자기 무슨 생각이라도 떠오른 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그리고 또 이걸 보십시오. 그분은 그걸 저에게 뒤집어씌워 제 손으로 한 짓인 양 꾸미려 하십니다—저도 벌써 그 얘기를 들었지요. 하지만 제가 발작을 꾸미는 데 능하다는 것으로 말하자면, 만일 제가 정말로 아버님께 그런 흉계를 품고 있었다면, 제가 발작을 꾸밀 수 있다고 미리 도련님께 말씀드렸겠습니까? 만일 제가 그런 살인을 꾸미고 있었다면, 그토록 어리석게 미리 제 입으로 저에게 불리한 증거를 댔겠습니까? 그것도 그분의 아드님께 말입니다! 정말이지! 그게 있을 법한 일입니까? 그런 일은 일찍이 일어난 적이 없습니다. 지금 우리의 이 이야기는 섭리 그 자체 말고는 아무도 듣는 이가 없으니, 만일 도련님께서 이걸 검사와 니콜라이 파르페노비치께 말씀하신다면 그것으로 저를 완전히 변호해 주시는 셈이 됩니다. 미리부터 이토록 마음을 활짝 여는 자가 어떻게 그런 죄인일 수 있겠습니까? 누구라도 그건 알 수 있는 일이지요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 이반은 스메르댜코프의 마지막 논리에 충격을 받아, 대화를 끝맺으려고 자리에서 일어섰다. \"나는 자네를 조금도 의심하지 않네. 실은 자네를 의심한다는 게 터무니없다고 생각해. 오히려 내 마음을 놓이게 해 줘서 고맙네. 이제 가 보겠지만, 다시 오겠네. 그동안 잘 있게. 어서 낫고. 무어 필요한 게 있나?\"\n\n\"모든 게 그저 고마울 따름입니다. 마르파 이그나티예브나께서 저를 잊지 않으시고, 그분의 너그러움대로 제가 원하는 건 무엇이든 챙겨 주십니다. 착한 분들이 날마다 저를 찾아오시고요.\"\n\n\"잘 있게. 하지만 자네가 발작을 꾸밀 수 있다는 얘기는 내가 하지 않겠네. 자네에게도 그러지 말라고 권하고 싶군.\" 무언가가 이반으로 하여금 불쑥 그렇게 말하게 했다.\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다. 그리고 도련님께서 그걸 말씀하지 않으신다면, 저도 대문 앞에서 나눈 우리의 그 대화에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 이반은 밖으로 나갔는데, 복도를 따라 열 걸음 남짓 걸어가서야 그는 문득 스메르댜코프의 마지막 말에 모욕적인 뜻이 담겨 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 하마터면 돌아설 뻔했으나, 그것은 한순간의 충동에 지나지 않았고, \"터무니없는 소리!\"라고 중얼거리며 그는 병원을 나섰다.\n\n그의 가장 큰 감정은, 살인을 저지른 것이 스메르댜코프가 아니라 미탸라는 사실에서 오는 안도감이었으니, 마땅히 그 반대의 감정을 느꼈으리라 짐작할 만한데도 그러했다. 그는 이 감정의 까닭을 분석하고 싶지 않았고, 자기 감각을 캐어 들여다보는 데 대해 노골적인 혐오마저 느꼈다. 그는 마치 무언가를 서둘러 잊어버리고 싶은 것 같았다. 그 후 며칠 동안 그는 미탸에게 걸린 증거의 무게를 모조리 알게 되면서 형의 유죄를 확신하게 되었다. 보잘것없는 사람들, 가령 페냐와 그녀의 어머니 같은 이들의 증언도 있었으나, 그것이 미치는 영향은 거의 압도적이었다. 페르호틴, 선술집 사람들, 플로트니코프의 가게 사람들, 그리고 모크로예의 증인들로 말하자면 그들의 증언은 결정적으로 보였다. 그토록 치명적인 것은 바로 그 자질구레한 정황들이었다. 그 신호의 비밀은 열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언만큼이나 법조인들에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 그리고리의 아내 마르파는 이반의 물음에 답하여, 스메르댜코프가 밤새도록 칸막이벽 저편에 누워 있었다고 단언했다. \"우리 침대에서 세 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳이었지요.\" 그러면서 자기는 잠귀가 어두운 편인데도 몇 번이나 깨어 그가 신음하는 소리를 들었다고 했다. \"그이는 내내 신음했어요, 끊임없이 신음했지요.\"\n\n헤르첸슈투베와 이야기하면서, 스메르댜코프는 미친 게 아니라 그저 조금 쇠약할 뿐이라는 견해를 내놓자, 이반은 그 노인에게서 미묘한 웃음을 자아냈을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"그가 요즘 어떻게 시간을 보내는지 아십니까?\" 노인이 물었다. \"프랑스어 단어 목록을 외우고 있지요. 베개 밑에 공책을 두고 있는데, 거기엔 누군가가 그를 위해 러시아 글자로 적어 준 프랑스어 단어들이 쓰여 있더군요, 히히히!\"\n\n이반은 결국 모든 의혹을 떨쳐 버렸다. 그는 혐오 없이는 드미트리를 떠올릴 수 없었다. 다만 한 가지 이상한 점이 있었다. 알료샤는 드미트리가 살인자가 아니며 \"십중팔구\" 스메르댜코프가 살인자라고 고집했다. 이반은 늘 알료샤의 견해가 자기에게 큰 의미가 있다고 느껴 왔으므로, 이번에는 그 견해에 놀랐다. 또 한 가지 이상한 점은, 알료샤가 미탸에 관해 이반과 이야기하려는 시도를 전혀 하지 않으며, 결코 먼저 그 화제를 꺼내지 않고 다만 그의 물음에만 답할 뿐이라는 것이었다. 이 점 역시 이반에게는 유난히 충격적이었다.\n\n그러나 그는 그 무렵 그것과는 전혀 동떨어진 무언가에 무척 골몰해 있었다. 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 그는 카테리나 이바노브나를 향한 미친 듯하고 사람을 집어삼키는 정념에 가망 없이 자신을 내맡겼다. 이반의 남은 평생에 흔적을 남긴 이 새로운 정념에 관해 이야기를 시작할 때는 지금이 아니다. 이는 또 다른 소설의 소재가 될 만한 것으로, 어쩌면 나는 끝내 그 소설을 쓰지 못할지도 모른다. 그러나 여기서 짚어 두지 않을 수 없는 것은, 이반이 앞서 내가 이야기했듯 알료샤를 데리고 카테리나 이바노브나의 곁을 떠나며 그에게 \"나는 그 여자에게 마음이 없어\"라고 말했을 때, 그것은 완전한 거짓말이었다는 점이다. 그는 그녀를 미치도록 사랑했으니, 때로는 그녀를 죽이고 싶을 만큼 미워하기도 했지만 그러했다. 여러 가지 원인이 이 감정을 빚어 내는 데 한몫했다. 미탸와의 일로 산산이 부서진 그녀는, 이반이 돌아오자 그를 유일한 구원으로 여겨 그에게 달려들었다. 그녀는 감정에 상처를 입었고, 모욕당했으며, 굴욕을 겪었다. 그런데 바로 그 사내가 그녀에게 돌아온 것이니, 일찍이 그토록 열렬히 그녀를 사랑했고(오! 그녀는 그것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다), 그 마음과 지성이 자기보다 훨씬 뛰어나다고 그녀가 여겨 온 사내였다. 그러나 엄격하리만치 정숙한 이 처녀는, 카라마조프 특유의 격정과 그가 그녀에게 끼치는 커다란 매혹에도 불구하고 사랑하는 사내에게 자신을 송두리째 내맡기지는 않았다. 그녀는 동시에 미탸를 버렸다는 회한에 끊임없이 시달렸고, 불화와 격한 분노의 순간(그런 순간은 숱하게 많았다)이면 이반에게 그것을 노골적으로 말하곤 했다. 이것이 바로 이반이 알료샤에게 \"거짓말에 거짓말을 쌓는다\"고 일컬은 것이었다. 거기에는 물론 거짓된 것이 많았고, 그것이 무엇보다도 이반을 화나게 했다……. 그러나 이 모든 것에 대해서는 나중에 다루기로 하자.\n\n사실 그는 한동안 스메르댜코프의 존재를 거의 잊다시피 했는데, 그를 처음 찾아간 지 두 주가 지나서부터 그는 전과 똑같은 기이한 생각들에 다시 시달리기 시작했다. 그는 자기 자신에게 끊임없이 이렇게 물었다고만 말해 두면 충분할 것이다. 어찌하여 그날 밤 표도르 파블로비치의 집에서, 그는 도둑처럼 계단으로 살금살금 기어 나가 아버지가 아래층에서 무엇을 하는지 엿들었던가? 어찌하여 그 일을 나중에 혐오로써 떠올렸던가? 어찌하여 그 이튿날 아침, 그는 길에서 그토록 갑작스레 우울해졌던가? 어찌하여 모스크바에 다다랐을 때, 그는 스스로에게 \"나는 비열한 놈이다\"라고 말했던가? 그리고 이제 그는 이 괴로운 생각들이 다시금 자신을 송두리째 사로잡았으므로, 그것들이 카테리나 이바노브나마저 잊게 만들 것만 같은 기분이 들 지경이었다. 바로 이런 기분이 든 직후, 그는 길에서 알료샤를 만났다. 그는 곧장 그를 멈춰 세우고 한 가지 물음을 던졌다.\n\n\"드미트리가 저녁 식사 후에 들이닥쳐 아버지를 때렸을 때, 그리고 그 뒤에 내가 마당에서 너에게 '바랄 권리'를 남겨 둔다고 말했던 거 기억하니?…… 말해 봐라, 너는 그때 내가 아버지의 죽음을 바랐다고 생각했니, 아니면 아니라고 생각했니?\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각했어요.\" 알료샤가 나직하게 대답했다.\n\n\"실제로 그랬어. 짐작의 문제가 아니었지. 그런데 너는 그때 내가 바라던 게 바로 '한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는 것', 그러니까 바로 드미트리가 아버지를 죽이는 것, 그것도 되도록 빨리…… 그리고 나 자신이 그렇게 되도록 거들 채비까지 되어 있었다고 여기지는 않았니?\"\n\n알료샤는 다소 창백해지더니, 말없이 형의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"말해 봐!\" 이반이 소리쳤다. \"나는 네가 그때 무얼 생각했는지 무엇보다도 알고 싶어. 나는 진실을 원해, 진실을!\"\n\n그는 대답이 나오기 전에 알료샤를 노여운 눈으로 바라보며 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"용서하세요, 저는 그때 그것도 생각했어요.\" 알료샤가 속삭였고, 누그러뜨리는 말은 한마디도 덧붙이지 않았다.\n\n\"고맙다.\" 이반은 쏘아붙이듯 말하고는, 알료샤를 남겨 둔 채 빠른 걸음으로 제 갈 길을 갔다. 그때부터 알료샤는 이반이 눈에 띄게 자기를 피하기 시작했고 심지어 자기를 싫어하게 된 것 같다는 것을 알아차렸는데, 어찌나 그러했던지 알료샤는 그를 찾아가는 것을 그만두었다. 그를 만난 직후 이반은 집으로 돌아가지 않고 곧장 다시 스메르댜코프에게로 갔다.\n\n# Chapter VII. The Second Visit To Smerdyakov\n\n그 무렵 스메르댜코프는 병원에서 퇴원해 있었다. 이반은 그의 새 거처를 알고 있었는데, 그것은 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 목조 가옥으로, 복도 하나를 사이에 두고 둘로 나뉘어 한쪽에는 마리야 콘드라티예브나와 그 어머니가 살았고 다른 쪽에는 스메르댜코프가 살았다. 그가 그들과 어떤 사이로 지내는지는 아무도 몰랐으니, 친구로서인지 세 들어 사는 사람으로서인지조차 알 수 없었다. 나중에 추측하기로는 그가 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 약혼자 자격으로 함께 들어와 살게 되었으며, 한동안은 숙식비를 한 푼도 내지 않고 지냈다는 것이었다. 어머니와 딸은 둘 다 그를 더없이 떠받들었고, 자기들보다 한참 윗길의 사람으로 우러러보았다.\n\n이반은 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열리자 곧장 복도 안으로 들어섰다. 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 일러 준 대로 그는 왼편에 있는, 스메르댜코프가 쓰는 더 나은 방으로 곧장 갔다. 방에는 타일을 붙인 난로가 있어 몹시 더웠다. 벽은 푸른빛 벽지로 화사하게 발려 있었으나 적잖이 낡았고, 그 아래 갈라진 틈에는 바퀴벌레가 놀랄 만큼 우글거려 끊임없이 사각거리는 소리를 냈다. 가구라곤 별것이 없어서, 벽마다 의자 둘이 붙어 있고 식탁 곁에 의자 둘이 놓여 있을 뿐이었다. 거친 나무로 짠 식탁에는 분홍빛 무늬가 있는 식탁보가 덮여 있었다. 자그마한 두 창문에는 저마다 제라늄 화분이 하나씩 놓여 있었다. 한쪽 구석에는 성상함이 있었다. 식탁 위에는 여기저기 우그러진 자그마한 구리 사모바르가 놓이고, 찻잔 두 개가 얹힌 쟁반이 있었다. 그러나 스메르댜코프는 차를 다 마셔서 사모바르의 불은 꺼져 있었다. 그는 식탁 앞 의자에 앉아 있었다. 그는 공책을 들여다보며 펜으로 천천히 무언가를 쓰고 있었다. 그의 곁에는 잉크병과 납작한 철제 촛대가 있었는데, 촛대에는 짜 맞춘 양초가 꽂혀 있었다. 이반은 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 보자마자 그가 병에서 완전히 회복되었음을 알아차렸다. 그의 얼굴은 한결 생기 있고 살이 올라 있었으며, 앞머리는 멋들어지게 곤두서 있고 양옆은 매끈하게 빗어 붙여져 있었다. 그는 알록달록한 솜 누비 실내복을 걸치고 있었는데, 다만 제법 때가 묻고 너덜너덜했다. 코에는 안경을 걸치고 있었으니, 이반은 그가 안경 쓴 모습을 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 이 하찮은 사실이 별안간 이반의 분노를 곱절로 부추겼다. \"저런 따위 위인이 안경을 다 쓰다니!\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 천천히 고개를 들어 안경 너머로 손님을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러더니 천천히 안경을 벗고 의자에서 일어섰는데, 결코 공손하다고는 할 수 없는, 거의 굼뜬 태도로, 흔한 예의가 요구하는 최소한의 것만을 마지못해 갖추었다. 이 모든 것이 이반의 눈에 단번에 들어왔다. 그는 그것을 죄다 받아들이고 즉시 새겨 두었으니, 무엇보다도 스메르댜코프의 눈빛이 그러했는데, 그것은 영락없이 악의에 차 있고 무뚝뚝하며 거만했다. \"뭐 하러 끼어드는 거요?\" 하고 그 눈빛이 말하는 듯했다. \"그때 다 정리하지 않았소. 왜 또 온 거요?\" 이반은 좀처럼 자신을 억누를 수가 없었다.\n\n\"여기는 덥군.\" 그는 여전히 선 채로 말하며 외투의 단추를 풀었다.\n\n\"외투를 벗으시지요.\" 스메르댜코프가 마지못해 허락하듯 말했다.\n\n이반은 외투를 벗어 떨리는 손으로 의자 위에 내던졌다. 그는 의자 하나를 잡아 재빨리 식탁 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고 앉았다. 스메르댜코프는 그보다 앞서 제 의자에 가까스로 앉았다.\n\n\"우선 묻겠는데, 여기엔 우리 둘뿐인가?\" 이반이 엄하면서도 충동적으로 물었다. \"저 안에서 우리 얘기를 엿들을 수 있나?\"\n\n\"아무도 아무것도 들을 수 없습니다. 직접 보셨잖습니까. 복도가 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"잘 들어라, 이 사람아. 내가 병원에서 나올 때 자네가 지껄인 그 소리는 대체 무슨 뜻이었나? 내가 자네의 발작 흉내 내는 재주에 관해 입을 다물어 주면, 자네도 대문에서 우리가 나눈 얘기를 전부 예심판사에게 말하지 않겠다고 했잖나. 그 전부라는 게 무슨 뜻이야? 그게 도대체 무슨 뜻일 수가 있나? 자네 나를 협박한 건가? 내가 자네와 무슨 거래라도 맺었단 말인가? 내가 자네를 두려워하기라도 한다는 건가?\"\n\n이반은 완전히 격분한 채로 이렇게 말했는데, 자신은 어떤 둘러대기나 에두름도 경멸하며 패를 다 드러내 보일 작정임을 분명한 의도로 그에게 알리려 했다. 스메르댜코프의 눈이 원망스럽게 번득였고, 그의 왼쪽 눈이 찡긋했으며, 그는 곧바로 여느 때와 다름없는 침착함과 신중함으로 대답을 내놓았다. \"다 까놓고 하길 바라신다면, 좋습니다, 그렇게 해드리지요.\" 하고 그가 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"제가 그때 뜻한 바는 이것이고, 또 제가 그 말을 한 까닭도 이것입니다. 도련님께서는 친아버지가 살해되리라는 것을 미리 아셨으면서도 그분을 운명에 내맡겨 두셨고, 그렇기에 사람들이 그 일로 도련님의 심사에 관해, 또 어쩌면 다른 무엇에 관해서도 좋지 못한 결론을 내릴까 봐, 바로 그것을 제가 관헌에 말하지 않겠다고 약속한 겁니다.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 서두르지 않고, 또 분명히 자신을 억누르며 말했지만, 그래도 그 목소리에는 무언가 단호하고 힘준, 원망에 차고 뻔뻔스레 도전적인 것이 담겨 있었다. 그는 이반을 무엄하게 노려보았다. 한순간 이반의 눈앞에 안개가 끼었다.\n\n\"어째서? 뭐라고? 자네 정신이 나갔나?\"\n\n\"제 정신은 멀쩡합니다.\"\n\n\"내가 그 살인을 알고 있었다고 생각한단 말이냐?\" 이반이 마침내 소리치며 주먹으로 식탁을 사납게 내리쳤다. \"'다른 무엇에 관해서도'라는 건 또 무슨 소리냐? 말해, 이 악당아!\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 잠자코, 여전히 똑같이 무엄한 눈초리로 이반을 살펴보았다.\n\n\"말하라니까, 이 구린내 나는 악당아, 그 '다른 무엇에 관해서도'라는 게 뭐야?\"\n\n\"제가 말한 그 '다른 무엇'이란, 도련님 역시 친아버지가 죽기를 무던히도 바라셨으리라는 겁니다.\"\n\n이반은 벌떡 일어나 있는 힘껏 그의 어깨를 후려쳤고, 그 바람에 그는 벽에 부딪혀 나동그라졌다. 순식간에 그의 얼굴이 눈물로 흠뻑 젖었다. \"병자를 때리시다니, 너무하십니다요.\" 하고 말하며 그는 몹시 더러운 푸른 격자무늬 손수건으로 눈물을 닦더니 가만히 흐느껴 울기 시작했다. 일 분이 흘렀다.\n\n\"그만! 됐어.\" 이반이 다시 앉으며 단호하게 말했다. \"더 이상 내 인내심을 시험하지 마라.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 눈에서 손수건을 떼었다. 잔뜩 일그러진 그의 얼굴 구석구석에는 방금 받은 모욕이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네는 그때 생각한 건가, 이 악당아, 내가 드미트리와 한패가 되어 아버지를 죽이려 한다고?\"\n\n\"저는 그때 도련님 마음속에 무슨 생각이 들어 있는지 몰랐습니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 원망스레 말했다. \"그래서 그때 대문에서 도련님을 붙들어 바로 그 점을 떠보려 한 겁니다.\"\n\n\"무엇을 떠봐, 무엇을?\"\n\n\"아니, 바로 그 사정 말입니다, 도련님께서 아버지가 살해되기를 바라시는지 아닌지를요.\"\n\n이반을 무엇보다 격노하게 한 것은, 스메르댜코프가 끈질기게 고수하는 그 공격적이고 무엄한 말투였다.\n\n\"네가 아버지를 죽인 거지?\" 그가 별안간 소리쳤다.\n\n스메르댜코프는 경멸하듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"제가 그분을 죽인 게 아니라는 건 도련님께서도 사실로 잘 아시잖습니까. 그리고 분별 있는 분이라면 그 얘기를 또 입에 올리실 필요가 없으리라 생각했습니다요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어째서, 어째서 그때 자네는 나를 그렇게 의심했나?\"\n\n\"이미 아시다시피, 그저 두려움 때문이었습니다. 저는 그런 처지에 놓여, 두려움에 벌벌 떨며 누구든 다 의심했으니까요. 도련님도 떠보기로 마음먹은 건, 만일 도련님이 형님과 똑같은 것을 바라신다면 그 일은 이미 다 끝난 거나 진배없고, 저도 파리처럼 짓이겨지리라 생각했기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네는 보름 전에는 그런 말을 하지 않았어.\"\n\n\"병원에서 도련님과 얘기할 때도 똑같은 뜻이었습니다요. 다만 도련님이 말을 허비하지 않아도 알아들으시리라, 또 그토록 분별 있는 분이니 그걸 대놓고 말하길 꺼리시리라 생각했을 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그다음은! 대답해, 대답하라니까, 내가 명한다. 그게 무엇이었나… 내가 무엇을 했기에 자네의 그 비열한 영혼 속에 그런 모욕적인 의심을 품게 했단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"살인으로 말하자면, 도련님은 그걸 하실 수도 없었고 바라지도 않으셨지요. 하지만 누군가 다른 사람이 그 일을 해주기를 바라는 것으로 말하면, 그거야말로 도련님이 정말로 바라시던 바였습니다.\"\n\n\"한데 어쩌면 저토록 태연하게, 저토록 태연하게 말하는가! 그런데 내가 왜 그것을 바랐겠나. 내가 무슨 근거로 그것을 바랐겠어?\"\n\n\"무슨 근거냐고요? 유산은 어쩌고요?\" 스메르댜코프가 비아냥대듯, 그리고 흡사 앙심을 품은 듯이 말했다. \"아니, 아버님이 돌아가시면 도련님들 각자에게 적어도 사만 루블씩은 돌아올 테고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 많겠지요. 하지만 만일 표도르 파블로비치께서 그 마님, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 그때 결혼하셨다면, 그 마님은 머리가 잘 돌아가는 분이니 혼인하자마자 그분 재산을 죄다 제 명의로 옮겨 놓았을 겁니다. 그랬다면 아버님은 세 분께 통틀어 이 루블도 남기지 않으셨겠지요. 게다가 결혼이 머나먼 일이었던가요? 머리카락 한 올만큼도 멀지 않았습니다요. 그 마님이 새끼손가락만 까딱하면 아버님은 혀를 빼물고 교회로 쫓아 달려가셨을 테니까요.\"\n\n이반은 괴로운 노력으로 자신을 억눌렀다.\n\n\"좋다.\" 그가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"보다시피 나는 벌떡 일어나지도, 너를 때려눕히지도, 죽이지도 않았다. 계속 말해라. 그래, 네 말대로라면 나는 드미트리가 그 일을 하도록 점찍어 두었다, 나는 그를 믿고 셈을 하고 있었다는 거냐?\"\n\n\"어찌 그분을 믿고 셈하지 않으셨겠습니까? 만일 그분이 아버님을 죽이면 귀족으로서의 모든 권리와 신분과 재산을 잃고 유형을 떠나게 되지요. 그러면 그분 몫의 유산이 도련님과 아우 알렉세이 표도로비치께 똑같이 나뉘어 돌아올 테니, 두 분이 각각 사만이 아니라 육만씩 갖게 되는 겁니다. 도련님이 드미트리 표도로비치를 믿고 셈하셨다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다요.\"\n\n\"내가 너한테 별꼴을 다 당하는구나! 잘 들어라, 이 악당아, 내가 그때 누군가를 믿고 셈했다면, 그건 드미트리가 아니라 바로 너였을 거다. 그리고 맹세컨대 나는 너에게서 무슨 흉악한 짓을 예감하고 있었어… 그때… 그 인상이 지금도 기억난다!\"\n\n\"저 역시 그때 한순간, 도련님이 저까지 믿고 셈하시는 게 아닌가 생각했습니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 비아냥대는 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"그러니 다른 무엇보다도 바로 그 점으로 도련님은 제게 마음속을 내보이신 겁니다. 저에 대해 무슨 예감을 품으시고도 떠나 버리셨으니, 도련님은 제게 '내 아버지를 죽여도 좋다, 나는 너를 막지 않겠다!' 하고 말씀하신 거나 마찬가지니까요.\"\n\n\"이 악당아! 그래, 그런 식으로 알아들었단 말이냐!\"\n\n\"죄다 그 체르마시냐로 가신 일 때문입니다. 아니! 도련님은 모스크바로 가려 하시면서 아버님이 체르마시냐로 가달라고 그토록 간청해도 죄다 마다하시더니, 그저 저의 어리석은 한마디에 당장 응하시지 않았습니까! 무슨 까닭으로 체르마시냐 행에 응하셨습니까? 아무 까닭도 없이, 그저 저의 한마디에 체르마시냐로 가셨다는 건, 도련님이 저에게서 무언가를 기대하셨다는 걸 보여 주는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니다, 맹세코 나는 기대하지 않았어!\" 이반이 이를 갈며 외쳤다.\n\n\"기대하지 않으셨다고요? 그렇다면 아버님의 아들로서, 그때 저의 그런 말 때문에라도 저를 당장 유치장에 처넣고 매질을 시키셨어야지요… 적어도 그 자리에서 제 얼굴을 한 대 후려치셨어야 합니다. 한데 도련님은, 미안한 말씀이지만, 조금도 화내지 않으시고 곧장 다정하게 저의 어리석은 말을 따라 떠나 버리셨으니, 이건 더없이 어처구니없는 일입니다. 도련님은 아버님의 목숨을 구하려고 남아 계셨어야 마땅하니까요. 그런데 제가 어찌 그런 결론을 내리지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?\"\n\n이반은 두 주먹을 무릎에 경련하듯 짓누른 채 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 네 얼굴을 한 대 후려치지 않은 게 후회스럽다.\" 그가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그때 너를 유치장에 처넣을 수는 없었지. 누가 내 말을 믿어 주었겠으며, 너에게 무슨 죄목을 씌울 수 있었겠나? 하지만 얼굴을 한 대 친다는 건… 아, 그 생각을 못 한 게 분하구나. 손찌검이 금지돼 있긴 하지만, 네 그 흉한 얼굴을 짓이겨 곤죽을 만들어 놓았어야 했는데.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 거의 흐뭇한 듯이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"평범한 일상사에서는,\" 그는 일찍이 표도르 파블로비치의 식탁에서 그리고리를 비웃고 그와 종교를 두고 입씨름하던 때와 똑같이 자족적이고 잘난 체하는 말투로 말했다. \"평범한 일상사에서는 얼굴을 치는 것이 요즘 법으로 금지되어 있어 사람들이 다들 그만두었습지요. 하지만 예외적인 경우에는 사람들이 여전히 주먹질에 의지하는데, 그건 우리나라만 그런 게 아니라 온 세상이 다, 설령 그게 가장 자유로운 프랑스 공화국이라 해도, 아담과 이브의 시절과 똑같이 그러하며, 결코 그만두는 법이 없을 겁니다. 그런데 도련님은 예외적인 경우에도 감히 그렇게 하지 못하셨군요.\"\n\n\"프랑스어 단어는 뭐 하러 배우는 거냐?\" 이반이 식탁 위에 놓인 공책 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다.\n\n\"제 교양을 좀 닦자고 배우는 게 어째서 안 됩니까, 언젠가 저 자신이 유럽의 그 행복한 고장에 가게 될 기회가 올지도 모르는데요?\"\n\n\"잘 들어라, 이 괴물 같은 놈.\" 이반의 눈이 번득였고 그는 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. \"나는 네 고발 따위 두렵지 않다. 나에 대해 무슨 말이든 멋대로 지껄여라. 그리고 내가 너를 때려죽이지 않는 건, 오로지 네가 그 범죄를 저질렀다고 의심하기 때문이며, 나는 너를 법의 심판대로 끌고 갈 것이다. 네 정체를 까발리고 말겠다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 도련님은 가만히 계시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 제 결백이 이토록 완전한데 무슨 죄목으로 저를 고발하시겠습니까? 게다가 누가 도련님 말을 믿겠습니까? 다만 도련님이 시작하시면 저도 죄다 털어놓겠습니다, 제 몸은 제가 지켜야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 이제 와서 너를 두려워한다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"법정이 제가 방금 도련님께 한 말을 다 믿지 않더라도, 세상 사람들은 믿을 테고, 그러면 도련님이 부끄러워지실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"결국 '분별 있는 사람과는 언제든 얘기할 만하다'는 소리로구나, 응?\" 이반이 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"과연 정곡을 찌르셨습니다. 그러니 도련님도 분별 있게 처신하시는 게 좋을 겁니다.\"\n\n이반은 분개에 온몸을 떨며 일어나 외투를 걸쳤고, 스메르댜코프에게 더는 대꾸하지 않고, 거들떠보지도 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 오두막을 나섰다. 서늘한 저녁 공기가 그를 상쾌하게 했다. 하늘에는 밝은 달이 떠 있었다. 갖가지 상념과 감각의 악몽이 그의 영혼을 가득 메웠다. \"당장 가서 스메르댜코프를 고발할까? 하지만 내가 무슨 고발을 할 수 있단 말인가? 어쨌든 그는 죄가 없다. 오히려 그가 나를 고발할 것이다. 그리고 사실, 그때 나는 왜 체르마시냐로 떠났던가? 무엇 때문에? 무엇 때문에?\" 이반은 스스로에게 물었다. \"그래, 물론, 나는 무언가를 기대하고 있었다, 그리고 그의 말이 옳다….\" 그러고는 그날 아버지 집에서 보낸 마지막 밤에 자신이 계단에서 어떻게 귀를 기울이고 있었는지를 백 번째로 떠올렸다. 그러나 이제 그것을 어찌나 괴롭게 떠올렸던지, 그는 마치 칼에 찔리기라도 한 것처럼 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. \"그래, 나는 그때 그것을 기대하고 있었어, 그건 사실이다! 나는 그 살인을 바랐다, 나는 정말로 그 살인을 바랐어! 내가 그 살인을 바랐던가? 내가 그것을 바랐던가? 스메르댜코프를 죽여야 한다! 지금 스메르댜코프를 죽일 용기조차 없다면, 산다는 것이 다 무슨 소용이란 말인가!\"\n\n이반은 집으로 가지 않고 곧장 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 갔으며, 그의 몰골에 그녀는 깜짝 놀랐다. 그는 미친 사람 같았다. 그는 스메르댜코프와 나눈 대화를 한 마디 한 마디 빠짐없이 죄다 되풀이했다. 그녀가 아무리 달래 보아도 그는 진정되지 않았으니, 그는 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 기이하고 두서없이 지껄여 댔다. 마침내 그는 자리에 앉아 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고 두 손에 머리를 떨군 채 이런 기이한 말을 내뱉었다. \"만일 드미트리가 아니라 스메르댜코프가 살인자라면, 나는 그의 죄를 함께 진 것이오, 내가 그를 부추겨 그 일을 하게 했으니까. 내가 정말 그랬는지는 아직 나도 모르오. 하지만 만일 드미트리가 아니라 그가 살인자라면, 그렇다면 물론, 나도 살인자인 것이오.\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 그 말을 듣자 말없이 자리에서 일어나 자신의 책상으로 가서, 그 위에 놓인 상자를 열고 종이 한 장을 꺼내 이반 앞에 내려놓았다. 이것이 바로 훗날 이반이 알료샤에게 드미트리가 아버지를 죽였다는 \"결정적 증거\"라고 말한 그 문서였다. 그것은 미탸가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 술에 취해 쓴 편지로, 바로 그날 저녁 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 그루셴카가 그녀를 모욕한 그 소동이 있은 뒤, 수도원으로 가던 길의 갈림길에서 알료샤와 마주쳤던 그날 밤에 쓴 것이었다. 그때 알료샤와 헤어진 미탸는 그루셴카에게로 달려갔다. 그가 그녀를 만났는지는 모르겠으나, 저녁에 그는 '메트로폴리스'에 있었고 거기서 곤드레만드레 취하도록 마셨다. 그러고는 펜과 종이를 청해 제 자신에게 중대한 결과를 가져올 문서 한 장을 썼다. 그것은 장황하고 두서없으며 광란에 찬 편지, 한마디로 술 취한 편지였다. 그것은 마치 술 취한 사내의 넋두리 같았으니, 집에 돌아온 그가 아내나 식구 가운데 누군가를 붙들고 자기가 방금 어떤 모욕을 당했는지, 어떤 악당이 방금 자기를 모욕했는지, 반면에 자기는 또 얼마나 훌륭한 사내인지, 그리고 자기가 그 악당에게 어떻게 앙갚음할 것인지를 엄청난 열기로 늘어놓기 시작하는 것과 같았다. 그 모든 것을 한없이 길게, 굉장한 흥분과 횡설수설로, 술 취한 눈물과 식탁을 내리치는 주먹질을 곁들여 가며 떠벌리는 것이다. 편지는 가장 싸구려 보통 종이의 더러운 한 장에 쓰여 있었다. 그것은 술집에서 내준 것으로, 뒷면에는 숫자가 갈겨져 있었다. 분명 그의 술 취한 장광설을 담기엔 자리가 모자랐던지, 미탸는 여백까지 죄다 채우고도 마지막 줄은 나머지 글 위로 가로질러 써 놓았다. 편지의 내용은 다음과 같았다.\n\n운명의 카탸. 내일 나는 돈을 마련해 당신의 삼천 루블을 갚을 것이오, 그러니 잘 있으시오, 진노에 가득 찬 여인이여, 그래도 잘 있으시오, 내 사랑이여! 우리 이제 끝장을 냅시다! 내일 나는 누구한테서든 그것을 마련해 보려 애쓸 것이고, 만일 빌리지 못하면, 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 나는 아버지에게 가서 그 두개골을 쪼개고 베개 밑에서 돈을 꺼내 가겠소, 이반만 떠나고 없다면 말이오. 그것 때문에 시베리아로 가야 한다 해도, 나는 당신의 삼천 루블을 돌려주겠소. 그리고 잘 있으시오. 나는 당신 앞에 땅바닥에 엎드려 절하오, 내가 당신에게 악당 노릇을 했으니. 나를 용서해 주오! 아니, 차라리 용서하지 마오, 그래야 당신도 나도 더 행복할 테니! 당신의 사랑보다는 차라리 시베리아가 낫소, 나는 다른 여자를 사랑하며, 당신도 오늘 그 여자를 너무 잘 알게 되었으니, 어찌 당신이 용서할 수 있겠소? 나는 내게서 훔쳐 간 그 사내를 죽일 것이오! 나는 당신들 모두를 버리고 동방으로 떠나 다시는 아무도 보지 않으리다. 그 여자도 보지 않겠소, 당신만이 나를 괴롭히는 게 아니라 그 여자도 그러하니. 잘 있으시오! 추신. 나는 저주를 쓰면서도, 당신을 흠모하오! 내 가슴속에서 그것이 들려오오. 줄 하나가 남아 떨리고 있소. 차라리 내 가슴을 둘로 찢어 버리는 게 낫겠소! 나는 스스로 목숨을 끊겠지만, 무엇보다 먼저 그 개 같은 놈부터. 나는 그놈에게서 삼천을 뜯어내 당신에게 내던지겠소. 내가 당신에게 악당 노릇을 했을망정, 나는 도둑은 아니오! 당신은 삼천을 기다려도 좋소. 그 개 같은 놈은 그걸 자기 요 밑에, 분홍빛 리본에 싸서 간직하고 있소. 나는 도둑이 아니지만, 내 것을 훔친 도둑은 죽이겠소. 카탸, 경멸하는 눈으로 보지 마오. 드미트리는 도둑이 아니라 살인자요! 그는 당신의 오만을 견디느니 차라리 제 입지를 지키려고 아버지를 죽이고 자신을 망쳤소. 그리고 그는 당신을 사랑하지 않소. 재추신. 당신의 발에 입 맞추오, 잘 있으시오! 재재추신. 카탸, 누군가 내게 그 돈을 주도록 하느님께 기도해 주오. 그러면 나는 피에 물들지 않을 테지만, 아무도 주지 않는다면 나는 물들고 말 것이오! 나를 죽여 주오!\n\n당신의 종이자 적, 드미트리 카라마조프.\n\n이반은 이 \"문서\"를 읽고 확신했다. 그러니까 살인을 저지른 것은 스메르댜코프가 아니라 그의 형이었던 것이다. 그리고 스메르댜코프가 아니라면, 그 자신, 이반도 아니었다. 이 편지는 그의 눈에 단번에 논리적 증거의 모양새를 띠었다. 미탸의 죄에 관해서는 이제 추호의 의심도 있을 수 없었다. 그런데 미탸가 스메르댜코프와 한패가 되어 살인을 저질렀을지도 모른다는 의혹은 이반의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오르지 않았으며, 실제로 그런 가설은 여러 정황과 들어맞지도 않았다. 이반은 완전히 안심했다. 이튿날 아침 그는 스메르댜코프와 그의 빈정거림을 그저 경멸하는 마음으로 떠올릴 뿐이었다. 며칠 뒤에는 어떻게 자신이 그런 의혹 때문에 그토록 끔찍하게 괴로워할 수 있었는지 정말로 의아해할 정도였다. 그는 그를 경멸로 떨쳐 내고 잊어버리기로 작정했다. 그렇게 한 달이 지나갔다. 그는 스메르댜코프에 관해 더는 알아보지 않았으나, 두 차례 우연히 그가 몹시 병들어 정신이 온전치 못하다는 말을 들었다.\n\n\"저 사람은 미쳐 버리고 말 겁니다.\" 젊은 의사 바르빈스키가 그를 두고 한 말이었는데, 이반은 이 말을 기억해 두었다. 그달의 마지막 주에 이반 자신도 몹시 몸이 아프기 시작했다. 그는 재판이 열리기 직전에 카테리나 이바노브나가 불러온 모스크바의 의사를 찾아가 진찰을 받았다. 그리고 바로 그 무렵 카테리나 이바노브나와의 관계가 첨예하게 틀어졌다. 두 사람은 마치 서로 사랑하는 두 원수와도 같았다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 미탸에게로 \"되돌아가는 것\", 곧 그에게 유리한 쪽으로 짧지만 격렬하게 감정이 뒤바뀌는 것은 이반을 완전한 광란으로 몰아넣었다. 기이한 노릇이지만, 앞서 묘사한 그 마지막 장면, 곧 알료샤가 미탸의 부탁으로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 찾아간 그때에 이르기까지, 이반은 그달 내내 그토록 가증스럽던 그 \"되돌아감\"에도 불구하고 그녀가 미탸의 죄를 의심하는 말을 단 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었다. 또 한 가지 주목할 만한 것은, 그가 미탸를 날이 갈수록 점점 더 미워한다고 느끼면서도, 그 미움이 카탸의 \"되돌아감\" 때문이 아니라 바로 그가 아버지를 죽인 살인자이기 때문임을 깨달았다는 점이다. 그는 이를 의식하고 있었고 스스로에게도 그것을 온전히 인정했다.\n\n그럼에도 그는 재판이 열리기 열흘 전에 미탸를 찾아가 그에게 탈출 계획을 제안했으니, 그것은 분명 그가 오랫동안 곰곰이 궁리해 온 계획이었다. 그가 이렇게 하게 된 데에는, 형이 유죄를 선고받는 것이 이반 자신에게 이로운 일이라던, 그렇게 되면 자기와 알료샤의 유산이 사만에서 육만 루블로 불어난다던 스메르댜코프의 한마디가 여전히 그의 가슴속에 쓰라린 상처로 남아 있던 것이 한몫했다. 그는 미탸의 탈출을 주선하는 데 삼만 루블을 내놓기로 작정했다. 그를 만나고 돌아오는 길에 그는 몹시 침울하고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그는 별안간, 자신이 미탸의 탈출을 바라는 것이 삼만을 내놓아 그 쓰라린 상처를 치유하기 위해서일 뿐만 아니라 또 다른 이유 때문임을 느끼기 시작했다. \"그건 내 마음속도 그만큼 살인자이기 때문인가?\" 그는 스스로에게 물었다. 무언가 아주 깊은 곳에서 그의 영혼을 태우고 쓰라리게 좀먹는 듯했다. 무엇보다도 그의 자존심이 그달 내내 잔혹하게 고통받았다. 그러나 그것은 나중에 이야기하기로 하자….\n\n알료샤와 대화를 나눈 뒤, 이반이 자기 거처의 초인종에 손을 얹은 채 별안간 스메르댜코프에게 가기로 결심했을 때, 그는 갑작스럽고 기이한 분노의 충동에 따른 것이었다. 그는 카테리나 이바노브나가 방금 알료샤가 보는 앞에서 자기를 향해 이렇게 외쳤던 것이 불현듯 떠올랐다. \"그의(곧 미탸의) 죄를 내게 믿게 한 건 당신이에요, 바로 당신이라고요!\" 이반은 그 말을 떠올리고 벼락을 맞은 듯 멍해졌다. 그는 미탸가 살인자라고 그녀를 설득하려 한 적이 단 한 번도 없었다. 오히려 그는 스메르댜코프에게서 돌아왔던 그때, 그녀가 보는 앞에서 자기 자신을 의심하기까지 했었다. 그 \"문서\"를 내놓아 형의 죄를 입증한 것은 바로 그녀, 그녀였다. 그런데 이제 그녀가 별안간 이렇게 외친 것이다. \"나도 스메르댜코프한테 직접 다녀왔다고요!\" 그녀가 언제 거기에 갔단 말인가? 이반은 그것을 전혀 모르고 있었다. 그러니 그녀는 미탸의 죄를 결코 그렇게 확신하고 있던 게 아니었던 것이다! 그리고 스메르댜코프가 대체 그녀에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있었을까? 무슨, 무슨 말을 그녀에게 했단 말인가? 그의 가슴은 격렬한 분노로 불타올랐다. 그는 자신이 어떻게 반 시간 전에 그 말을 그냥 흘려보내고 그 순간 소리쳐 따지지 않았는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다. 그는 초인종에서 손을 떼고 스메르댜코프에게로 달려갔다. \"이번에는 어쩌면 그를 죽이게 될지도 모른다.\" 그는 가는 길에 이렇게 생각했다.\n\n# Chapter VIII. The Third And Last Interview With Smerdyakov\n\n그가 그곳으로 절반쯤 갔을 무렵, 그날 이른 아침부터 불어 대던 매섭고 건조한 바람이 다시 일더니 가늘고 메마른 눈발이 빽빽하게 쏟아지기 시작했다. 눈은 땅에 쌓이지 않고 바람에 휘날려 이내 본격적인 눈보라로 변했다. 스메르댜코프가 사는 동네에는 가로등이라고는 거의 없었다. 이반은 어둠 속을 홀로 성큼성큼 걸으며, 폭풍 따위는 의식하지 못한 채 본능적으로 길을 가려냈다. 머리가 욱신거렸고 관자놀이가 고통스럽게 지끈거렸다. 두 손이 발작적으로 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다. 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 오두막에서 멀지 않은 곳에서, 이반은 문득 술에 취한 외톨이 작은 농부와 마주쳤다. 그는 거칠고 누덕누덕 기운 외투를 걸친 채 비틀비틀 갈지자걸음을 하며 혼자 투덜대고 욕설을 내뱉고 있었다. 그러다 별안간 술에 잠긴 쉰 목소리로 노래를 부르기 시작했다.\n\n\"아, 반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났네,\n돌아올 때까지 난 기다리지 않으리.\"\n\n그러나 그는 매번 둘째 줄에 이르러 노래를 끊고는 다시 욕설을 늘어놓았고, 그러고 나면 또 같은 노래를 처음부터 부르기 시작했다. 이반은 그 사내에 관해 미처 무엇을 생각하기도 전부터 그를 향해 격렬한 증오를 느꼈다. 별안간 그는 그 사내의 존재를 의식했고, 그를 쓰러뜨리고 싶다는 거역할 수 없는 충동을 느꼈다. 바로 그 순간 두 사람이 마주쳤고, 농부는 거세게 휘청거리며 이반에게 정면으로 부딪쳐 왔는데, 이반은 그를 사납게 밀쳐 냈다. 농부는 뒤로 나가떨어지며 통나무처럼 얼어붙은 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그는 애처롭게 \"오—오!\" 하고 한마디 신음을 내뱉더니 이내 잠잠해졌다. 이반은 그에게 다가갔다. 사내는 움직임도 의식도 없이 등을 대고 누워 있었다. \"이러다 얼어 죽겠군.\" 이반은 그렇게 생각하며 스메르댜코프의 집으로 가던 길을 계속 갔다.\n\n복도에서 촛불을 손에 들고 문을 열려고 뛰어나온 마리야 콘드라티예브나는, 스메르댜코프가 몹시 아프다고 속삭였다. \"자리에 누운 건 아닌데요, 어쩐지 사람이 제정신이 아닌 것 같아요, 차도 가져가라고 했답니다, 한 모금도 안 들겠다고요.\"\n\n\"왜, 소란이라도 피우던가?\" 이반이 거칠게 물었다.\n\n\"어머, 아니에요, 오히려 정반대예요, 아주 조용하답니다. 그저 너무 오래 말을 붙이진 마세요.\" 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 그에게 부탁했다. 이반은 문을 열고 방으로 들어섰다.\n\n방 안은 전처럼 지나치게 더웠으나, 방에는 달라진 데가 있었다. 옆에 놓였던 긴 의자 하나가 치워지고, 그 자리에 마호가니 가죽을 씌운 크고 낡은 소파가 놓였는데, 그 위에는 제법 깨끗한 흰 베개를 갖춘 잠자리가 마련되어 있었다. 스메르댜코프는 전과 같은 실내복 차림으로 그 소파에 앉아 있었다. 탁자가 소파 앞으로 끌어내어져 있어서 몸을 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다. 탁자 위에는 노란 표지의 두툼한 책이 놓여 있었으나, 스메르댜코프는 그 책을 읽고 있지 않았다. 그는 아무것도 하지 않고 그저 앉아 있는 듯했다. 그는 느릿하고 말없는 시선으로 이반을 맞이했는데, 이반이 찾아온 것에 조금도 놀라지 않는 기색이 역력했다. 그의 얼굴에는 큰 변화가 있었으니, 훨씬 야위고 누렇게 떠 있었다. 두 눈은 움푹 꺼지고 그 밑에는 푸르스름한 자국이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 자네 정말로 아픈 게로군?\" 이반이 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. \"오래 붙들진 않겠네, 외투도 벗지 않겠어. 어디 좀 앉을 데가 없나?\"\n\n그는 탁자 반대편 끝으로 가서 의자 하나를 끌어다 놓고 거기에 앉았다.\n\n\"왜 말없이 나만 쳐다보고 있나? 난 그저 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어서 왔을 뿐이고, 맹세컨대 대답을 듣기 전엔 가지 않겠네. 그 아가씨, 카체리나 이바노브나가 자네를 찾아왔었나?\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 여전히 잠자코 전처럼 조용히 이반을 바라보고 있었다. 별안간 그는 손을 한 번 움직이더니 얼굴을 돌려 버렸다.\n\n\"자네 대체 왜 그러나?\" 이반이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"'아무것도 아니'라니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"네, 다녀갔습니다. 나리하곤 상관없는 일이지요. 그냥 좀 내버려 두십시오.\"\n\n\"아니, 그냥 내버려 두지 않겠네. 말해 봐, 그 여자가 언제 여기 왔었나?\"\n\n\"원, 그 여자에 관한 일은 까맣게 잊고 있었습니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 비웃는 듯한 미소를 지으며 그렇게 말하더니, 다시 이반에게로 얼굴을 돌려 광적인 증오의 눈빛으로 그를 응시했는데, 그것은 한 달 전 마지막으로 만났을 때 그에게 박아 넣었던 바로 그 눈빛이었다.\n\n\"나리야말로 몹시 편찮아 보이는데요, 얼굴이 핼쑥하군요, 도무지 평소의 나리 같지가 않습니다.\" 그가 이반에게 말했다.\n\n\"내 건강 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없고, 내가 묻는 거나 대답해.\"\n\n\"한데 어째서 눈이 그렇게 누렇습니까? 흰자위가 온통 노랗군요. 그렇게 근심이 많으신가요?\" 그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓더니 별안간 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"잘 들어, 대답을 듣기 전엔 가지 않겠다고 말했잖나!\" 이반이 극도로 짜증이 나서 소리쳤다.\n\n\"왜 자꾸 절 들볶으십니까? 어째서 절 괴롭히시는 거예요?\" 스메르댜코프가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"제기랄! 난 자네하곤 아무 상관도 없네. 그저 내 질문에 대답만 하면 곧 가겠어.\"\n\n\"드릴 대답이 없습니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 다시 눈을 내리깔며 말했다.\n\n\"기어이 대답하게 만들고 말 테니 두고 봐!\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 안절부절못하십니까?\" 스메르댜코프는 단순한 경멸이 아니라 거의 혐오에 가까운 눈으로 그를 응시했다. \"내일 재판이 시작되기 때문입니까? 나리한테는 아무 일도 없을 겁니다, 그것조차 끝내 못 믿으시겠어요? 집에 가서 자리에 누워 편히 주무세요, 아무것도 두려워하실 것 없습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소린지 모르겠군…… 내가 내일 두려워할 게 뭐가 있단 말인가?\" 이반은 어리둥절해서 그렇게 내뱉었는데, 그 순간 별안간 차디찬 공포의 기운이 정말로 그의 영혼을 스쳐 지나갔다. 스메르댜코프는 눈으로 그를 찬찬히 훑어보았다.\n\n\"모르신다고요?\" 그가 책망하듯 느릿느릿 말을 끌었다. \"분별 있는 분이 이런 익살극을 즐기려 드시다니, 참 별일이군요!\"\n\n이반은 말없이 그를 바라보았다. 한때 자기 하인이었던 이 사내가 던지는, 놀라우리만치 믿기 어려울 만큼 거만한 그 말투는 그 자체로 예사롭지 않았다. 지난번 마지막으로 만났을 때조차 그는 이런 말투를 쓴 적이 없었다.\n\n\"말씀드리지만, 나리는 아무것도 두려워하실 게 없습니다. 전 나리에 관해선 한마디도 하지 않을 거고, 나리를 옭아맬 증거 따위도 없으니까요. 한데 어쩌면 그렇게 손을 떠시는지! 손가락은 또 왜 그렇게 떠시는 겁니까? 집에 가세요, 그분을 죽인 건 나리가 아닙니다.\"\n\n이반은 흠칫 놀랐다. 그는 알료샤를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"내가 아니란 건 나도 알아.\" 그가 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"아신다고요?\" 스메르댜코프가 다시 그 말꼬리를 낚아챘다.\n\n이반은 벌떡 일어나 그의 어깨를 움켜잡았다.\n\n\"전부 말해, 이 독사 같은 놈아! 죄다 말하라고!\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 조금도 겁먹지 않았다. 그저 광적인 증오의 눈길을 이반에게 못 박을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"그러니까, 정 그러시다면, 그분을 죽인 건 바로 나리입니다.\" 그가 사납게 속삭였다.\n\n이반은 무언가를 곰곰이 헤아리듯 의자에 도로 주저앉았다. 그는 악의에 차서 웃었다.\n\n\"내가 떠난 일을 말하는 게로군. 지난번에 자네가 떠벌렸던 그 얘기 말이야?\"\n\n\"나리는 지난번에 제 앞에 서서 죄다 알아들으셨고, 지금도 알아들으시는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 알아듣는 건 자네가 미쳤다는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"이젠 진력도 안 나십니까? 우리 둘이 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 있는데, 무엇 하러 계속 서로 익살극을 부린답니까? 아직도 그걸 죄다 제게, 제 면전에 떠넘기려고 애쓰시는 겁니까? 그분을 죽인 건 나리예요, 진짜 살인자는 나리고, 저는 그저 나리의 도구이자 충실한 하인이었을 뿐, 나리 말씀을 따라 그 일을 해치운 것뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"해치워? 아니, 자네가 그분을 죽였단 말인가?\" 이반은 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었다.\n\n뇌리에서 무언가가 툭 끊어지는 듯하더니, 그는 차디찬 전율로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. 그러자 이번에는 스메르댜코프 쪽이 의아한 듯 그를 바라보았으니, 아마도 이반의 경악이 진짜라는 사실이 그를 놀라게 한 모양이었다.\n\n\"설마 정말로 모르고 계셨다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\" 그가 미심쩍은 투로 더듬거리며, 억지웃음을 머금은 채 이반의 눈을 들여다보았다. 이반은 여전히 그를 응시할 뿐, 말을 잇지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n아, 반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났네,\n돌아올 때까지 난 기다리지 않으리.\n\n별안간 이 노랫말이 그의 머릿속에서 메아리쳤다.\n\n\"이보게, 난 자네가 내 앞에 앉은 한낱 꿈이고 허깨비일까 봐 두렵네.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"여기 허깨비 따위는 없습니다, 다만 우리 둘과 또 하나가 있을 뿐이지요. 틀림없이 그분도 여기 계실 겁니다, 그 셋째 분 말입니다, 우리 사이에요.\"\n\n\"그게 누군가? 누가 여기 있다는 거야? 무슨 셋째란 말이냐?\" 이반은 놀라 소리치며 사방을 둘러보았고, 그의 눈은 다급하게 구석구석을 뒤졌다.\n\n\"그 셋째 분이 바로 하느님이십니다—섭리지요. 그분이 지금 우리 곁에 셋째로 계시는 겁니다. 다만 그분을 찾으려 들진 마세요, 찾아내지 못하실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"자네가 그분을 죽였다는 건 거짓말이야!\" 이반이 미친 듯이 소리쳤다. \"자넨 미쳤거나, 아니면 또 날 놀려 먹는 거지!\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 전처럼 두려운 기색도 없이 호기심에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그는 좀처럼 의혹을 떨치지 못한 채, 여전히 이반이 죄다 알면서도 \"그걸 죄다 제 면전에 떠넘기려\" 애쓰는 것이라고 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 그가 마침내 힘없는 목소리로 말하더니, 별안간 탁자 밑에서 왼쪽 다리를 끌어 올려 바짓단을 걷어 올리기 시작했다. 그는 흰 긴 양말에 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 그는 천천히 양말 대님을 풀더니 양말 속 깊숙이 손을 더듬어 넣었다. 이반은 그를 바라보다가 별안간 발작적인 공포로 몸을 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"미친놈이야!\" 그가 소리쳤고, 잽싸게 벌떡 일어나 뒷걸음질을 치다가 등을 벽에 쿵 부딪고는, 뻣뻣하고 꼿꼿하게 그 벽에 기대섰다. 그는 광적인 공포의 눈으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보았는데, 스메르댜코프는 그의 공포에는 아랑곳없이 계속 양말 속을 더듬으며, 마치 손가락으로 무언가를 붙잡아 끄집어내려고 애쓰는 듯했다. 마침내 그는 그것을 붙잡아 끌어내기 시작했다. 이반의 눈에 그것은 종잇조각, 어쩌면 종이를 말아 놓은 뭉치 같은 것으로 보였다. 스메르댜코프는 그것을 끄집어내어 탁자 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 그가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 이반이 떨면서 물었다.\n\n\"좀 보십시오.\" 스메르댜코프는 여전히 낮은 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n이반은 탁자로 다가가 그 종이 뭉치를 집어 들고 펴기 시작했으나, 별안간 무슨 징그러운 파충류라도 만진 듯이 손가락을 움츠렸다.\n\n\"손이 자꾸 떨리시는군요.\" 스메르댜코프는 그렇게 말하고는, 손수 천천히 그 뭉치를 펼쳤다. 포장 안에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 세 다발이 들어 있었다.\n\n\"전부 여기 있습니다, 삼천 루블 고스란히요, 세어 보실 것도 없습니다. 가져가세요.\" 스메르댜코프가 지폐를 향해 고갯짓을 하며 이반에게 권했다. 이반은 의자에 도로 털썩 주저앉았다. 그는 손수건처럼 새하얗게 질려 있었다.\n\n\"날 놀라게 했군…… 그 양말로 말이야.\" 그가 묘한 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"정말로 지금까지 모르고 계셨다는 말씀인가요?\" 스메르댜코프가 다시 한번 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 난 몰랐어. 줄곧 드미트리만 생각하고 있었네. 형님, 형님! 아!\" 그는 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"이봐. 자네가 혼자서 죽인 건가? 우리 형의 도움을 받았나, 아니면 혼자였나?\"\n\n\"오직 나리와, 나리의 도움으로 제가 그분을 죽인 겁니다, 드미트리 표도로비치는 아무 죄도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아. 내 얘긴 나중에 하지. 난 왜 자꾸 이렇게 떨리는 거지? 말도 제대로 못 하겠군.\"\n\n\"그때만 해도 그렇게 대담하셨는데요. '모든 것이 허용된다'고 말씀하시더니, 지금은 어쩌면 그렇게 겁을 내십니까.\" 스메르댜코프가 놀란 듯 중얼거렸다. \"레모네이드라도 좀 드시겠습니까? 당장 가져오라고 하지요. 아주 시원하답니다. 다만 이걸 먼저 감춰 둬야겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 다시 지폐를 향해 손짓했다. 그는 막 일어나 문간으로 가서 마리야 콘드라티예브나에게 레모네이드를 만들어 가져오라고 부르려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 보지 못하도록 지폐를 덮을 만한 것을 찾다가, 먼저 손수건을 꺼냈으나 그것이 몹시 더러웠던 터라, 이반이 처음에 탁자 위에 놓인 것을 보았던 그 노란 큰 책을 집어 들어 지폐 위에 덮었다. 그 책은 「시리아 사람 성 이사악 성인의 어록」이었다. 이반은 무심코 그 제목을 읽었다.\n\n\"레모네이드는 됐네.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 얘긴 나중에 하고. 앉아서 어떻게 그 일을 저질렀는지 말해 봐. 처음부터 끝까지 다.\"\n\n\"외투를 벗으시는 게 좋겠습니다, 안 그러면 너무 더우실 텐데요.\" 이반은 그제야 비로소 생각이 났다는 듯 외투를 벗어, 의자에서 일어나지도 않은 채 긴 의자 위로 던져 놓았다.\n\n\"말해, 어서, 말하라고.\"\n\n그는 한결 차분해진 듯했다. 그는 스메르댜코프가 그 일의 자초지종을 죄다 들려주리라 확신하며 기다렸다.\n\n\"어떻게 저질렀느냐고요?\" 스메르댜코프가 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"더없이 자연스러운 방식으로, 나리께서 하신 바로 그 말씀을 따라서 했지요.\"\n\n\"내 말 얘긴 나중에 하고.\" 이반이 다시 말을 가로막았는데, 보아하니 완전히 평정을 되찾은 듯, 전처럼 고함치는 대신 또박또박 다부지게 말을 내뱉었다. \"그저 자네가 어떻게 했는지 낱낱이 말해 봐. 일어난 그대로 전부 다. 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 말고. 무엇보다도 세세한 것들을, 그 세세한 것들을, 제발 부탁이야.\"\n\n\"나리께서 떠나시고 나서, 전 지하실에 굴러떨어졌습니다.\"\n\n\"발작 때문이었나, 아니면 꾸민 거였나?\"\n\n\"꾸민 거지요, 물론. 죄다 꾸며 낸 겁니다. 전 조용히 계단을 따라 맨 밑바닥까지 내려가서 조용히 누웠고, 그렇게 누운 채로 비명을 지르며 발버둥을 쳤는데, 결국 사람들이 절 들어내 갈 때까지 그랬지요.\"\n\n\"잠깐! 그러면 그 뒤로도, 병원에서도 줄곧 꾸민 거였나?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 천만에요. 이튿날 아침, 사람들이 절 병원으로 데려가기 전에, 진짜로 발작이 일어났는데 몇 해 동안 겪어 본 것 중 가장 심한 발작이었습니다. 이틀 동안이나 완전히 의식이 없었지요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아. 계속해.\"\n\n\"사람들이 절 침대에 눕혔습니다. 전 칸막이 저편에 눕게 되리란 걸 알고 있었지요, 제가 아플 때면 마르파 이그나티예브나가 늘 거기, 그분들 가까이에 절 뉘었으니까요. 그 여자는 제가 태어났을 때부터 줄곧 제게 무척 잘해 줬습니다. 밤이 되자 전 신음을 했지만, 나직하게 했지요. 줄곧 드미트리 표도로비치가 오기를 기다리면서요.\"\n\n\"기다렸다고? 자네한테 오기를?\"\n\n\"제게가 아닙니다. 그분이 집 안으로 들어오시기를 기다린 거지요, 그날 밤 반드시 오시리라 의심치 않았으니까요, 왜냐하면 제가 없어서 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터라, 그분은 틀림없이 와서 늘 그러시던 대로 담을 넘어 들어와 무슨 일이든 저지르실 게 분명했거든요.\"\n\n\"그가 오지 않았다면?\"\n\n\"그러면 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요. 그분 없이는 저 혼자선 도저히 결행하지 못했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아…… 좀 더 알아듣게 말해, 서두르지 말고, 무엇보다 하나도 빠뜨리지 마!\"\n\n\"전 그분이 표도르 파블로비치를 죽이리라 기대했습니다. 그건 확실하다고 생각했지요, 제가 그분을 그렇게 몰아세워 뒀으니까요…… 지난 며칠 동안에요…… 그분은 그 신호 소리를 알고 계셨는데, 그게 무엇보다 중요했습니다. 그 의심 많은 성미에다 그날들 내내 그분 속에서 부풀어 오른 분노까지 더해졌으니, 그분은 틀림없이 그 신호 소리를 빌미로 집 안에 들어오실 수밖에 없었지요. 그건 피할 수 없는 일이었고, 그래서 전 그분을 기다린 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐.\" 이반이 말을 끊었다. \"만약 그가 그분을 죽였다면, 돈을 챙겨서 가져가 버렸을 텐데, 자네도 그건 생각해 봤을 거 아닌가. 그러면 그 뒤에 자넨 뭘 얻겠다는 거였지? 도무지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분은 그 돈을 결코 찾아내지 못했을 겁니다. 돈이 매트리스 밑에 있다고 한 건 제가 그분께 그렇게 말한 것뿐이지요. 한데 그건 사실이 아니었습니다. 돈은 상자 안에 들어 있었거든요. 그러고 나서 전 표도르 파블로비치께, 그분이 믿는 사람이라곤 저뿐이었으니, 지폐가 든 봉투를 성상들 뒤 구석에 숨겨 두시라고 일러 드렸지요, 아무도 그 자리는 짐작하지 못할 테니까요, 더구나 다급하게 들이닥친다면 말입니다. 그래서 봉투는 거기, 성상들 뒤 구석에 놓여 있었던 겁니다. 매트리스 밑에 둔다는 건 어처구니없는 일이었지요, 어쨌거나 상자는 자물쇠를 채울 수 있으니까요. 그래도 다들 돈이 매트리스 밑에 있다고 믿었지요. 어리석은 믿음이었습니다. 그러니 드미트리 표도로비치가 살인을 저질렀다 해도, 아무것도 찾지 못한 채 살인자들이 으레 그러듯 작은 소리에도 겁을 먹고 황급히 달아나거나, 아니면 붙잡혔을 겁니다. 그러면 전 언제든 성상들 위로 기어 올라가 이튿날 아침에, 아니 바로 그날 밤에라도 돈을 빼낼 수 있었을 테고, 죄는 죄다 드미트리 표도로비치에게 뒤집어씌워졌을 겁니다. 전 그걸 믿고 계산한 거지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 만약 그가 그분을 죽이지 않고 그저 때려눕히기만 했다면?\"\n\n\"그분이 죽이지 않았다면, 물론 전 감히 돈에 손을 대지 못했을 테고, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요. 하지만 전 그분이 그분을 정신을 잃을 만큼 때려 줄 거라고 계산했고, 그러면 그 틈에 돈을 빼낼 시간이 있을 테고, 그런 다음 표도르 파블로비치께는 드미트리 표도로비치가 그분을 때린 뒤 돈을 가져간 거라고, 그 사람 말고는 아무도 아니라고 둘러댈 작정이었지요.\"\n\n\"잠깐…… 점점 헷갈리는군. 그러니까 결국 그분을 죽인 건 드미트리고, 자넨 돈만 가져간 거란 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그분이 죽인 게 아닙니다. 뭐, 이제 와서 그분이 살인자라고 나리께 말씀드릴 수도 있었겠지요…… 하지만 전 지금 나리께 거짓말을 하고 싶지 않습니다, 왜냐하면…… 왜냐하면 만약 나리께서 정말로 지금까지 모르고 계셨다면, 제 눈으로 직접 보아하니 그런 것 같고, 또 죄를 제 면전에 떠넘기려 시치미를 떼시는 게 아니라면, 그래도 나리는 이 모든 일에 책임이 있으십니다, 살인을 알면서도 제게 그 일을 맡기고 죄다 아시면서 떠나 버리셨으니까요. 그래서 전 오늘 저녁 나리 면전에 증명해 보이고 싶은 겁니다, 이 모든 사건에서 진짜 살인자는 나리 한 분뿐이고, 비록 제가 그분을 죽이긴 했어도 진짜 살인자는 제가 아니란 것을요. 나리야말로 마땅한 살인자이십니다.\"\n\n\"어째서, 어째서 내가 살인자란 말이냐? 오, 하느님!\" 이반은 마침내 자제력을 잃고 소리쳤는데, 자기 얘기는 대화의 맨 끝으로 미뤄 두기로 한 것조차 까맣게 잊고 있었다. \"자넨 아직도 그 체르마시냐 얘길 하는 건가? 잠깐, 말해 봐, 자네가 정말로 체르마시냐를 동의의 표시로 받아들였다면, 어째서 굳이 내 동의가 필요했지? 그걸 이제 어떻게 설명할 텐가?\"\n\n\"나리의 동의를 확신하고 나면, 전 알 수 있었지요, 설령 드미트리 표도로비치 대신, 혹은 그 공범으로 제가 의심을 받는다 해도, 나리께서 그 삼천 루블이 없어진 일로 떠들썩하게 소동을 일으키진 않으시리란 걸요. 오히려 나리는 남들로부터 절 감싸 주셨을 겁니다…… 그리고 유산을 받으시면 형편이 되는 대로, 평생토록 제게 보답해 주셨겠지요. 왜냐하면 나리는 저를 통해 그 유산을 받으시는 셈이니까요, 만약 그분이 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 결혼하셨다면, 나리는 단 한 푼도 손에 넣지 못하셨을 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아! 그러니까 자넨 그 뒤로 평생토록 날 들볶을 작정이었군.\" 이반이 으르렁거렸다. \"그럼 만약 내가 그때 떠나지 않고 자넬 밀고했더라면?\"\n\n\"무얼 밀고하실 수 있었겠습니까? 제가 나리께 체르마시냐로 가시라고 권했다는 걸요? 그건 죄다 헛소리지요. 게다가 우리가 그 얘기를 나눈 뒤로, 나리는 떠나시거나 머무시거나 둘 중 하나였습니다. 머무셨다면 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요. 전 나리께서 그 일이 일어나길 원치 않으신다는 걸 알았을 테고, 아무것도 시도하지 않았을 테니까요. 한데 나리께서 떠나셨다는 건, 곧 재판에서 절 밀고할 엄두를 내지 않으시고 제가 삼천 루블을 가지는 것도 눈감아 주시겠노라 제게 확약하신 셈이지요. 그리고 사실 나리는 그 뒤에 절 고발하실 수도 없었습니다, 그랬다면 전 법정에서 죄다 까발렸을 테니까요. 그러니까 제가 돈을 훔쳤다거나 그분을 죽였다는 게 아니라—그런 얘긴 하지 않았겠지요—나리께서 절 부추겨 절도와 살인을 시키셨다고요, 비록 제가 동의하진 않았지만 말입니다. 그래서 전 나리의 동의가 필요했던 겁니다, 그래야 나리께서 나중에 절 궁지로 몰아넣지 못하실 테니까요, 무슨 증거가 있으셨겠습니까? 하지만 전 언제든 나리를 궁지로 몰 수 있었지요, 나리께서 부친의 죽음을 얼마나 갈망하셨는지 폭로하면서요, 말씀드리지만 세상 사람들은 그걸 죄다 곧이들었을 테고, 나리는 평생토록 부끄러움 속에 사셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 내가 그렇게나 갈망했단 말이냐, 정말로?\" 이반이 다시 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"그럼요, 그러셨고말고요, 그리고 동의하심으로써 제가 그 일을 저지르는 걸 말없이 묵인하신 거지요.\" 스메르댜코프는 단호하게 이반을 바라보았다. 그는 몹시 쇠약해서 느릿하고 힘없이 말했으나, 무언가 숨겨진 내면의 힘이 그를 떠밀고 있었다. 그에게 어떤 속셈이 있는 것이 분명했다. 이반은 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"계속해.\" 그가 말했다. \"그날 밤 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 봐.\"\n\n\"더 말씀드릴 게 뭐가 있겠습니까! 전 거기 누워 있었는데, 주인 나리가 소리치시는 게 들리는 것 같았습니다. 그보다 앞서 그리고리 바실리예비치가 별안간 일어나 밖으로 나가더니, 갑자기 비명을 지르고, 그러고는 온통 적막과 어둠뿐이었지요. 전 거기 누운 채 심장을 두근거리며 기다렸지만, 도저히 견딜 수가 없었습니다. 마침내 일어나 밖으로 나갔지요. 왼편으로 정원 쪽 창문이 열려 있는 게 보여서, 그분이 거기 살아서 앉아 계신지 귀를 기울이려고 왼쪽으로 다가갔는데, 주인 나리가 이리저리 움직이며 한숨을 쉬시는 소리가 들리길래, 그분이 살아 계시다는 걸 알았지요. '에잇!' 전 생각했습니다. 창가로 다가가 주인 나리께 소리쳤지요, '접니다.' 그러자 그분이 제게 소리치셨습니다, '왔었다, 왔었어, 달아났다.' 드미트리 표도로비치가 다녀갔다는 뜻이었지요. '그놈이 그리고리를 죽였어!' '어디서요?' 전 속삭였습니다. '저기, 저 구석에서.' 그분이 손가락으로 가리키셨지요. 그분도 속삭이고 계셨습니다. '잠깐만요.' 전 그렇게 말하고 정원 구석으로 가서 살펴봤는데, 거기 그리고리 바실리예비치가 담장 옆에 온통 피투성이가 된 채 정신을 잃고 누워 있더군요. 그래서 정말 드미트리 표도로비치가 다녀갔구나, 하는 생각이 제 머리를 스쳤고, 전 그 자리에서 이 일을 끝장내기로 마음먹었습니다, 그리고리 바실리예비치는 설령 살아 있다 해도 정신을 잃고 누워 있으니 아무것도 보지 못할 테니까요. 단 하나 위험한 건 마르파 이그나티예브나가 깨어날지도 모른다는 것이었지요. 전 그 순간 그걸 느꼈지만, 그 일을 해치우고 싶은 갈망이 어찌나 솟구치던지 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없을 지경이었습니다. 전 창가로 돌아가 주인 나리께 말씀드렸지요, '그 여자가 여기 왔습니다, 왔어요, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 왔는데, 들여보내 달랍니다.' 그러자 그분은 어린아이처럼 흠칫하시더군요. '어디 있느냐?' 그분은 숨이 턱 막힌 듯 물으셨지만, 좀처럼 믿지 못하셨습니다. '저기 서 있습니다.' 전 말했지요. '문 여세요.' 그분은 반쯤 믿고 반쯤 못 미더워하시며 창문 너머로 절 내다보셨는데, 문을 여시기는 두려워하셨습니다. '아니, 이제 나를 무서워하시는군.' 전 생각했지요. 그게 우스웠습니다. 전 그분 면전에서, 그분 눈앞에서, 그루셴카가 왔다는 신호로 우리가 약속해 둔 그 신호 소리를 창틀에 두드려 보일 생각이 떠올랐습니다. 그분은 제 말은 믿지 않으시는 듯했지만, 그 신호 소리를 듣자마자 곧장 문을 열러 달려가셨지요. 그분은 문을 여셨습니다. 전 안으로 들어가려 했지만, 그분이 길을 막고 서서 제가 지나가지 못하게 하시더군요. '어디 있느냐? 어디 있어?' 그분은 온몸을 떨면서 절 쳐다보셨습니다. '이런,' 전 생각했지요, '이렇게나 날 무서워하시다니, 일이 고약하게 됐군!' 그분이 절 들여보내지 않거나 소리를 지르거나, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 달려오거나, 무슨 다른 일이 벌어질까 봐 겁이 나서 다리가 후들거렸습니다. 지금은 기억나지 않지만, 전 분명 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 그분 앞에 서 있었을 겁니다. 전 그분께 속삭였지요, '아니, 저기 있잖습니까, 저기, 창문 아래요, 어째서 안 보이십니까?' 전 말했습니다. '그럼 데려와라, 데려와.' '겁을 먹었습니다.' 전 말했지요, '소리에 놀라서 덤불 속에 숨었습니다, 서재에서 직접 그 여자를 불러 보세요.' 그분은 창가로 달려가 촛불을 창턱에 올려놓으셨습니다. '그루셴카,' 그분이 외치셨지요, '그루셴카, 거기 있느냐?' 그렇게 외치시면서도 그분은 창밖으로 몸을 내밀려 하지 않으셨고, 제게서 떨어지려 하지 않으셨지요, 공포에 사로잡혀 계셨으니까요. 너무 무서운 나머지 감히 제게 등을 돌리지 못하셨던 겁니다. '아니, 여기 있잖습니까.' 전 말했지요. 전 창가로 다가가 그 밖으로 몸을 쑥 내밀었습니다. '여기 있어요, 덤불 속에서 나리를 비웃고 있잖습니까, 안 보이세요?' 그분은 별안간 그 말을 믿으셨습니다. 온몸을 부들부들 떠셨지요—그 여자에게 미칠 듯이 빠져 계셨으니까요—그러더니 창밖으로 몸을 쑥 내미셨습니다. 전 그분 책상에서 그 무쇠 문진을 잽싸게 집어 들었지요, 기억하십니까, 삼 파운드쯤 나가던 그것 말입니다? 전 그걸 휘둘러 그 모서리로 그분 정수리를 내리쳤습니다. 그분은 비명조차 지르지 않으셨지요. 그저 별안간 풀썩 주저앉으셨고, 전 다시 한번, 그리고 세 번째로 내리쳤습니다. 세 번째에 전 그분 두개골이 부서진 걸 알았지요. 그분은 별안간 벌렁 나자빠지셨는데, 얼굴을 위로 한 채 온통 피투성이가 되어 계셨습니다. 전 사방을 둘러봤지요. 제 몸에는 피 한 점 묻지 않았더군요, 단 한 방울도요. 전 문진을 닦아 제자리에 돌려놓고, 성상들 쪽으로 다가가 봉투에서 돈을 꺼낸 뒤, 봉투는 바닥에 내던지고 그 곁에 분홍색 끈도 던져 놨습니다. 전 온몸을 떨며 정원으로 나가, 곧장 구멍이 파인 사과나무로 갔지요—그 구멍을 아시죠. 전 오래전부터 그걸 봐 두고 거기에 헝겊 한 조각과 종이 한 장을 미리 넣어 뒀습니다. 전 지폐를 죄다 헝겊에 싸서 그 구멍 속 깊숙이 쑤셔 넣었지요. 그리고 그건 보름 넘게 거기 그대로 있었습니다. 나중에, 제가 병원에서 나왔을 때 그걸 꺼냈지요. 전 제 침상으로 돌아가 누워서 생각했습니다, '만약 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그 자리에서 죽어 버렸다면 나한테 고약한 일이 되겠지만, 죽지 않고 회복된다면 그야말로 더할 나위 없겠다, 그러면 그가 드미트리 표도로비치가 여기 다녀갔다고 증언할 테고, 그러니 그분이 그분을 죽이고 돈을 가져간 게 될 테니까.' 그러고 나서 전 마르파 이그나티예브나를 되도록 빨리 깨우려고 조바심과 안달 속에 신음을 하기 시작했지요. 마침내 그 여자가 일어나 제게 달려왔는데, 그리고리 바실리예비치가 없는 걸 보더니 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔고, 전 그 여자가 정원에서 비명을 지르는 소리를 들었습니다. 그래서 모든 게 굴러가기 시작했고, 제 마음도 놓였지요.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈췄다. 이반은 그동안 내내 미동도 없이, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 스메르댜코프는 이야기를 하면서 이따금 그를 흘끗거렸으나, 대개는 눈길을 딴 데로 돌리고 있었다. 이야기를 마쳤을 때 그는 분명 동요한 기색이었고 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬고 있었다. 얼굴에는 땀이 송골송골 맺혀 있었다. 그러나 그가 느끼는 것이 회한인지, 아니면 무엇인지는 도무지 알 길이 없었다.\n\n\"잠깐.\" 이반이 곰곰이 헤아리며 소리쳤다. \"그 문은 어떻게 된 거지? 만약 그가 자네한테만 문을 열어 줬다면, 어떻게 그리고리가 그보다 먼저 문이 열려 있는 걸 봤을 수 있단 말인가? 그리고리는 자네가 가기 전에 그걸 봤다는데.\"\n\n이반이 사뭇 다정하게, 전과는 달리 화내지 않는 어조로 말하는 것이 눈에 띄었으니, 만약 그 순간 누군가 문을 열고 두 사람을 들여다봤더라면, 그는 분명 두 사람이 흥미롭긴 해도 그저 평범한 어떤 화제를 두고 평화롭게 이야기를 나누고 있다고 단정했을 것이다.\n\n\"그 문이며 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그게 열려 있는 걸 봤다는 거며, 그건 죄다 그 사람 망상일 뿐입니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 입을 비뚜름히 일그러뜨리며 말했다. \"장담컨대 그자는 사람이 아니라 고집불통 노새지요. 보지도 않았으면서 봤다고 우기는 거고, 도무지 그 고집을 꺾을 수가 없습니다. 그자가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 박아 넣은 게 우리한테는 그야말로 천운이지요, 그 덕에 드미트리 표도로비치는 영락없이 유죄 판결을 받을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이봐……\" 이반이 다시 어리둥절해지는 듯하더니, 무언가를 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이봐. 자네한테 물어볼 게 많은데, 자꾸 잊어버리는군…… 자꾸 잊어버리고 헷갈려. 그래. 적어도 이것만은 말해 봐, 자넨 어째서 봉투를 뜯어서 그걸 바닥에 내버려 둔 거지? 어째서 그냥 봉투째 들고 가지 않았나?…… 자네가 얘기할 때 보니, 마치 그게 마땅히 해야 할 일이었다는 듯이 말하더군…… 하지만 어째서인지, 난 도무지 모르겠어……\"\n\n\"전 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그렇게 한 겁니다. 만약 어떤 자가 그 일을 죄다 알고 있었다면, 이를테면 저처럼요, 그 지폐를 전에 본 적이 있고, 어쩌면 손수 그 봉투에 넣었으며, 봉투가 봉해지고 겉봉이 쓰이는 걸 제 눈으로 직접 봤다면, 그런 자가 살인을 저질렀을 경우, 무엇하러 나중에 봉투를 뜯겠습니까, 더구나 그렇게 다급하기 짝이 없는 와중에 말입니다, 지폐가 봉투 안에 들어 있다는 걸 분명히 알고 있을 텐데요? 그렇지요, 만약 도둑이 저 같은 자였다면, 그냥 봉투를 곧장 주머니에 넣고 되도록 빨리 들고 달아났을 겁니다. 하지만 드미트리 표도로비치라면 사정이 전혀 다르지요. 그분은 봉투에 관해 그저 풍문으로만 알았을 뿐, 한 번도 본 적이 없었으니, 만약 그걸, 이를테면 매트리스 밑에서 찾아냈다면, 지폐가 안에 들었는지 확인하려고 되도록 빨리 그걸 뜯었을 겁니다. 그러고는 그게 자기한테 불리한 증거가 되리란 걸 미처 생각할 겨를도 없이 봉투를 내던졌겠지요. 왜냐하면 그분은 상습 도둑이 아닌 데다 전에 직접 무얼 훔쳐 본 적도 없으니까요, 태생이 양반이시니 말입니다. 설령 도둑질을 결심하셨다 해도 그건 제대로 된 도둑질이 아니라 그저 제 것을 되찾는 것일 뿐이었을 테니, 그분은 그러겠다고 전부터 온 고을에 떠벌리셨고, 표도르 파블로비치에게서 제 재산을 찾아오겠노라 모두 앞에서 큰소리로 자랑까지 하셨거든요. 전 심문을 받을 때 검사에게 그걸 대놓고 말하지는 않았고, 오히려 정반대로, 제 입으로는 그걸 미처 깨닫지 못한 척, 그리고 그분 스스로 그런 생각을 떠올린 척, 제가 부추긴 게 아닌 척하면서 슬쩍 암시를 흘려 검사를 그쪽으로 이끌었지요. 그랬더니 검사 나리는 제 암시에 그야말로 군침을 흘리시더군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네가 그 모든 걸 그 자리에서 생각해 냈단 말인가?\" 이반은 경악에 사로잡혀 소리쳤다. 그는 다시 놀란 눈으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보았다.\n\n\"원, 별말씀을! 그렇게 다급하기 짝이 없는 와중에 누가 그 모든 걸 생각해 내겠습니까? 죄다 미리 궁리해 둔 거지요.\"\n\n\"그래…… 그래, 그건 악마가 자넬 도운 거야!\" 이반이 다시 소리쳤다. \"아니, 자넨 바보가 아니야, 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 영리한 놈이군……\"\n\n그는 자리에서 일어났는데, 보아하니 방을 가로질러 거닐 작정인 듯했다. 그는 끔찍한 고뇌에 빠져 있었다. 그러나 탁자가 길을 막고 있는 데다 탁자와 벽 사이로 빠져나갈 틈이 거의 없는 터라, 그는 선 자리에서 그저 몸을 돌려 도로 주저앉고 말았다. 어쩌면 움직일 수 없다는 사실이 그를 짜증나게 했는지, 그는 별안간 거의 전처럼 사납게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"잘 들어, 이 비참하고 천하기 짝이 없는 놈아! 내가 널 죽이지 않은 건 그저 내일 재판에서 답하게 하려고 널 살려 두는 것뿐이란 걸 모르겠나? 하느님이 보고 계신다.\" 이반이 손을 들어 올렸다. \"어쩌면 나도 죄가 있을지 몰라, 어쩌면 정말로 내 아버지의…… 죽음을 은밀히 바랐을지도 모르지, 하지만 맹세컨대 난 네가 생각하는 만큼 죄가 있진 않아, 어쩌면 난 널 부추긴 적이 전혀 없을지도 몰라. 아냐, 아냐, 난 널 부추기지 않았어! 하지만 상관없다, 난 내일 재판에서 나 자신에게 불리한 증언을 하겠어. 결심했다고! 죄다 말하겠어, 죄다 말이야. 하지만 우린 함께 출두하는 거다. 그리고 네가 재판에서 나에게 무슨 불리한 말을 하든, 무슨 증언을 하든, 난 맞서겠어. 난 네가 두렵지 않아. 죄다 내 입으로 확인해 주마! 하지만 너도 자백해야 해! 해야 한다, 반드시, 우린 함께 가는 거야. 그렇게 될 거다!\"\n\n이반은 이 말을 엄숙하고 단호하게 내뱉었고, 번득이는 그 눈빛만 보아도 정말 그렇게 되리란 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"나리는 편찮으십니다, 보아하니 그래요, 아주 편찮으십니다. 눈이 누렇잖습니까.\" 스메르댜코프는 조금의 빈정거림도 없이, 오히려 동정하는 기색을 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"우린 함께 간다.\" 이반이 거듭 말했다. \"그리고 네가 가지 않겠다면, 상관없어, 나 혼자 가겠다.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"그런 일은 절대 없을 거고, 나리는 가지 않으실 겁니다.\" 그가 마침내 단정적으로 결론을 내렸다.\n\n\"넌 날 이해하지 못해.\" 이반이 책망하듯 소리쳤다.\n\n\"죄다 자백하시면 나리는 너무도 부끄러우실 겁니다. 게다가 무엇보다 그래 봐야 아무 소용이 없지요, 전 단번에 잘라 말할 테니까요, 나리께 그런 얘긴 한 적이 없다고요, 그리고 나리는 편찮으시거나(보아하니 정말 그래 보입니다만), 아니면 형님이 하도 가여운 나머지 그분을 구하려고 자신을 희생하시느라 죄다 절 모함해 지어내신 거라고요, 나리는 늘 절 파리 한 마리만도 못하게 여기셨으니까요. 한데 누가 나리 말을 믿겠습니까, 게다가 나리한테 무슨 증거가 단 하나라도 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"이봐, 자넨 방금 날 납득시키려고 그 지폐를 보여 줬잖나.\"\n\n스메르댜코프는 지폐 위에 덮어 둔 책을 들어 한쪽으로 치워 놨다.\n\n\"그 돈은 가지고 가십시오.\" 스메르댜코프가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n\"물론 가져가지. 한데 자네가 그 돈 때문에 살인을 저질렀다면서, 어째서 그걸 나한테 주는 거지?\" 이반은 몹시 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"전 그게 필요 없습니다.\" 스메르댜코프는 거절하는 손짓을 하며, 떨리는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다. \"전 그 돈을 가지고 모스크바에서, 아니 그보다는 외국에서 새 삶을 시작해 볼 생각이 있긴 했지요. 정말 그런 꿈을 꿨습니다, 무엇보다 '모든 것이 허용된다'고 했으니까요. 나리께서 제게 가르쳐 주신 게 전적으로 옳았지요, 나리는 제게 그 얘길 숱하게 하셨으니까요. 만약 영원하신 하느님이 없다면, 미덕이란 것도 없고, 그런 게 필요하지도 않으니 말입니다. 나리 말씀이 옳았어요. 그래서 전 그렇게 본 거지요.\"\n\n\"그걸 자네 혼자 깨우쳤나?\" 이반이 입을 비뚜름히 일그러뜨리며 물었다.\n\n\"나리의 가르침을 받아서지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제, 돈을 돌려주는 걸 보니, 하느님을 믿는 모양이군?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 전 안 믿습니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 속삭였다.\n\n\"그럼 어째서 그걸 돌려주는 거지?\"\n\n\"그만두세요…… 그만하면 됐습니다!\" 스메르댜코프가 다시 손을 내저었다. \"모든 것이 허용된다고 나리께서 손수 말씀하시지 않았습니까, 그런데 이제 와서 나리는 왜 그렇게 안절부절못하시는 겁니까? 심지어 가서 자신에게 불리한 증언까지 하시겠다니요…… 하지만 그런 일은 절대 없을 겁니다! 나리는 증언하러 가지 않으실 거예요.\" 스메르댜코프가 확신에 차서 단정했다.\n\n\"두고 보면 알겠지.\" 이반이 말했다.\n\n\"있을 수 없는 일입니다. 나리는 무척 영리하시지요. 돈을 좋아하시고요, 그건 제가 압니다. 또 존경받는 것도 좋아하시지요, 자존심이 무척 세시니까요. 게다가 여자의 매력도 지나치게 밝히시고요, 무엇보다 아무에게도 기대지 않고 마음 편히 안락하게 사는 걸 가장 중히 여기시지요—그게 나리가 가장 소중히 여기시는 겁니다. 나리는 그런 치욕을 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 걸로 평생을 영영 망쳐 버리고 싶진 않으실 겁니다. 나리는 표도르 파블로비치를 닮으셨어요, 그분의 어느 자식보다도 나리가 그분을 더 닮으셨지요. 나리한테는 그분과 똑같은 영혼이 깃들어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"넌 바보가 아니군.\" 이반은 충격을 받은 듯 말했다. 얼굴로 피가 솟구쳐 올랐다. \"이제 진지하게 말하는군!\" 그는 별안간 달라진 표정으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"제가 바보라고 여기신 건 나리의 자존심 때문이었지요. 돈을 가지고 가십시오.\"\n\n이반은 지폐 세 다발을 집어, 아무것에도 싸지 않은 채 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다.\n\n\"이걸 내일 법정에서 내보이겠다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아무도 나리 말을 믿지 않을 겁니다, 나리한테는 본디 돈이 넉넉하니까요. 그저 나리가 금고에서 꺼내 법정에 들고 오신 거라고 여기겠지요.\"\n\n이반은 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"다시 말하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 널 죽이지 않은 단 하나의 까닭은 내일을 위해 네가 필요해서다, 그걸 기억해 둬, 잊지 말라고!\"\n\n\"그럼, 절 죽이세요. 지금 죽여 보십시오.\" 스메르댜코프가 별안간 묘한 눈빛으로 이반을 바라보며 말했다. \"나리는 그것조차 감히 못 하실 겁니다!\" 그가 쓰디쓴 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"나리는 아무것도 감히 못 하실 거예요, 한때 그토록 대담하시던 나리가 말입니다!\"\n\n\"내일 보자.\" 이반이 소리치며 나가려고 몸을 움직였다.\n\n\"잠깐만요…… 그 지폐를 다시 한번 보여 주십시오.\"\n\n이반은 지폐를 꺼내 그에게 보여 주었다. 스메르댜코프는 십 초쯤 그것을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럼, 가셔도 됩니다.\" 그가 손을 한 번 내저으며 말했다. \"이반 표도로비치!\" 그가 다시 그를 불러 세웠다.\n\n\"뭘 원하나?\" 이반은 걸음을 멈추지 않은 채 돌아보았다.\n\n\"안녕히 가십시오!\"\n\"내일 보자!\" 이반이 다시 소리쳤고, 오두막을 걸어 나왔다.\n\n눈보라는 여전히 사납게 몰아치고 있었다. 그는 처음 몇 걸음은 당당하게 내디뎠으나, 별안간 비틀거리기 시작했다. \"몸 탓이군.\" 그는 쓴웃음을 지으며 생각했다. 무언가 기쁨 비슷한 것이 그의 가슴속에서 솟아오르고 있었다. 그는 한없는 결의를 자각하고 있었으니, 요즘 들어 그토록 그를 괴롭히던 망설임을 끝장내려는 것이었다. 결심은 섰고, \"이젠 바뀌지 않을 거야.\" 그는 안도하며 생각했다. 바로 그 순간 그는 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리다 하마터면 넘어질 뻔했다. 우뚝 멈춰 선 그는 자기 발치에서, 자기가 쓰러뜨린 그 농부가 여전히 정신을 잃고 꼼짝없이 누워 있는 것을 알아보았다. 눈이 그의 얼굴을 거의 다 덮고 있었다. 이반은 그를 붙들어 두 팔로 안아 일으켰다. 오른편의 작은 집에서 불빛이 새어 나오는 것을 보고, 그는 그리로 다가가 덧창을 두드린 뒤, 집주인에게 삼 루블을 주겠노라 약속하며 그 농부를 경찰서까지 옮기는 걸 거들어 달라고 청했다. 사내는 채비를 하고 밖으로 나왔다. 이반이 어떻게 자기 뜻을 이루어, 그 농부를 경찰서로 데려가 의사가 당장 그를 진찰하도록 조처하고, 후한 손길로 그 비용을 댔는지를 일일이 적지는 않겠다. 다만 이 일에 꼬박 한 시간이 걸렸다는 것, 그러나 이반은 그것에 흡족해했다는 것만 말해 두겠다. 그의 마음은 쉴 새 없이 헤매고 또 움직였다.\n\n\"내가 내일 일을 그토록 단단히 결심해 두지 않았더라면,\" 그는 만족스레 곱씹었다. \"꼬박 한 시간이나 머물며 저 농부를 돌보지 않고, 저자가 얼어 죽든 말든 아랑곳없이 그냥 지나쳐 버렸겠지. 그건 그렇고 난 내 자신을 충분히 살필 줄 아는군.\" 그는 바로 그 순간 한층 더 큰 만족감에 젖어 생각했다. \"사람들은 내가 미쳐 가고 있다고 단정 지었지만 말이야!\"\n\n자기 집에 다다른 바로 그때 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 별안간 지금 당장 검사에게 가서 죄다 털어놓는 편이 낫지 않을까 자문했다. 그는 집으로 발길을 되돌리는 것으로 그 물음에 답을 내렸다. \"내일 죄다 함께!\" 그는 혼자 속삭였는데, 이상하게도 그 기쁨과 자기만족이 거의 죄다 한순간에 사라져 버렸다.\n\n자기 방으로 들어서면서 그는 가슴에 얼음이 닿는 듯한 느낌을 받았는데, 그것은 지금 이 순간 그 방 안에 있고 또 전에도 있었던, 어떤 괴롭고 역겨운 것에 대한 회상, 더 정확히 말하면 일깨움 같은 것이었다. 그는 지친 몸을 소파에 털썩 묻었다. 노파가 그에게 사모바르를 가져왔고, 그는 차를 끓였으나 입에 대지는 않았다. 그는 소파에 앉아 어질어질한 현기증을 느꼈다. 그는 자신이 병들고 무력하다는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 막 잠에 빠져들려다가도 불안하게 일어나, 졸음을 떨쳐 내려고 방을 가로질러 거닐었다. 이따금 그는 자기가 헛것을 보고 있는 게 아닌가 싶었으나, 그가 가장 골똘히 생각한 것은 병이 아니었다. 다시 자리에 앉은 그는 무언가를 찾기라도 하듯 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다. 이런 일이 여러 차례 되풀이되었다. 마침내 그의 눈은 한 지점에 골똘히 못 박혔다. 이반은 미소를 지었으나, 그의 얼굴에는 성난 홍조가 번졌다. 그는 머리를 두 팔에 괸 채 오랫동안 제자리에 앉아 있었는데, 그러면서도 눈은 곁눈질로 줄곧 그 한 지점을, 맞은편 벽에 붙어 있는 소파를 바라보고 있었다. 거기에는 분명 무언가가, 어떤 물건이 있어, 그것이 그를 짜증나게 하고, 안달하게 하고, 또 괴롭히고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter IX. The Devil. Ivan’s Nightmare\n\n나는 의사가 아니지만, 그래도 이쯤에서 독자에게 이반의 병이 어떤 성질의 것이었는지 부득이 어느 정도 설명을 해 두어야 할 순간이 왔다고 느낀다. 앞으로 벌어질 일을 미리 짚어 두자면 적어도 한 가지는 말할 수 있으니, 그는 바로 그 순간 뇌염이 발작하기 직전의 상태에 놓여 있었던 것이다. 그의 건강은 오래전부터 나빠져 있었으나, 끝내 그를 완전히 사로잡고 만 그 열병에 대해 끈질기게 버텨 왔던 것이다. 의학에 관해서는 아는 바가 없으나, 나는 감히 이런 추측을 던져 보려 한다. 그는 어쩌면 무서운 의지의 힘으로 한동안 발작을 늦추는 데 정말로 성공했던 것이며, 물론 그것을 완전히 억눌러 버리기를 바랐던 것이리라. 그는 자신이 몸이 성치 않다는 것을 알고 있었으나, 하필이면 그 운명적인 시기, 자기 생애의 다가오는 위기 앞에서 병에 걸린다는 생각만은 진저리가 났으니, 그때야말로 모든 정신을 또렷이 차리고서, 자신이 해야 할 말을 대담하고 단호하게 내뱉어 \"스스로에게 자신을 정당화\"해야 할 때였기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 기상천외한 발상으로 모스크바에서 불려 온, 앞서 내가 언급한 그 새로운 의사에게 진찰을 받기는 했다. 의사는 그의 말을 듣고 진찰을 마친 끝에, 그가 실제로 어떤 뇌 질환을 앓고 있다는 결론에 이르렀으며, 이반이 마지못해 털어놓은 고백에도 조금도 놀라지 않았다. \"당신 상태에서 환각이 나타나는 것은 충분히 있을 수 있는 일입니다.\" 의사는 이렇게 소견을 밝혔다. \"다만 그것을 확인해 보는 편이 좋겠지요…… 당장, 한순간도 지체하지 말고 조치를 취해야 합니다. 그러지 않으면 일이 좋지 않게 흘러갈 겁니다.\" 그러나 이반은 이 분별 있는 충고를 따르지 않았고, 자리에 누워 간병을 받으려 하지 않았다. \"이렇게 걸어 다니고 있으니 견딜 만큼은 튼튼한 거지. 쓰러지면 그때는 다르겠지, 그땐 누구든 원하는 사람이 간병하면 그만이고.\" 그는 이렇게 마음을 정하고는 그 문제를 떨쳐 버렸다.\n\n그리하여 그는 거의 자신의 헛소리를 스스로 의식하다시피 한 채 앉아 있었고, 앞서 말한 대로 맞은편 벽에 붙은 소파 위의 어떤 물체를 끈질기게 바라보고 있었다. 누군가가 거기 앉아 있는 듯했는데, 도대체 어떻게 들어왔는지는 알 도리가 없었으니, 이반이 스메르댜코프에게서 돌아와 방으로 들어왔을 때만 해도 거기엔 아무도 없었기 때문이다. 그것은 한 사람, 더 정확히 말하자면 어떤 특별한 부류에 속하는 러시아 신사로, 이미 젊지는 않은, 프랑스인들이 말하듯 \"쉰 줄에 접어든(qui faisait la cinquantaine)\" 자였는데, 제법 길고 아직 숱이 많은 검은 머리에는 희끗희끗 새치가 섞여 있었고, 끝이 뾰족한 작은 턱수염을 기르고 있었다. 그는 갈색이 도는 리퍼 재킷을 걸치고 있었는데, 제법 낡기는 했으나 솜씨 좋은 재단사가 지은 것이 분명했고, 적어도 삼 년은 묵은 유행을 따른 것으로, 멋쟁이에 부유한 자들이 최근 이 년 동안에는 내다 버린 그런 양식이었다. 그의 셔츠와 목도리처럼 긴 넥타이는 모두 멋을 부리려는 자들이 두르는 그런 것이었으나, 가까이서 들여다보니 셔츠는 그리 깨끗하지 못했고 폭 넓은 목도리는 올이 다 드러나 너덜너덜했다. 손님의 체크무늬 바지는 재단이 훌륭했으나, 색깔이 지나치게 밝고 요즘 유행에 비추어 너무 몸에 꼭 끼었다. 부드럽고 보송보송한 흰 모자는 계절과는 영 어울리지 않았다.\n\n요컨대 살림이 쪼들린 신사의 면모가 구석구석 배어 있었다. 그 신사는 농노제 시절에 번성하던 그런 한가로운 지주 부류에 속한 인물처럼 보였다. 그가 한때 좋은 사교계, 그것도 세련된 사교계에 드나들었으며, 한때는 좋은 인맥을 지녔고, 어쩌면 지금도 그것을 그대로 간직하고 있을지도 모른다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었으나, 흥청대는 젊은 시절을 보낸 뒤 농노제 폐지와 함께 차츰 가산이 기울어, 마침내 상류층의 가난한 식객 신세로 전락하여, 옛 친구 이 집 저 집을 떠돌며 그 붙임성 있고 무던한 성품 덕에, 그리고 어쨌든 누구와 함께 자리에 앉혀도 무방한 신사라는 이유로 환대를 받는 처지가 된 것이었다. 물론 상석에 앉히지는 못할 신사였지만 말이다. 이렇게 무던한 성품에 남에게 얹혀사는 처지의 신사들, 이야기를 늘어놓을 줄 알고, 카드 한 판쯤 거들 줄도 알며, 자기에게 떠넘겨질 만한 의무라면 무엇이든 질색하는 그런 신사들은 대개 외톨이로, 독신이거나 홀아비다. 더러는 자식이 있기도 하나, 있다 한들 그 아이들은 늘 멀리 떨어진 어느 이모 집 같은 데서 자라며, 그 신사들은 좋은 사교계에서는 그 인연을 입에 올리는 법이 없으니, 그런 관계를 부끄러워하는 듯하다. 그들은 차츰 자식들을 아예 시야에서 놓쳐 버리고 마는데, 그래도 이따금 자식들에게서 생일이나 성탄절 편지를 받으며, 때로는 답장을 보내기까지 한다.\n\n이 뜻밖의 손님의 얼굴은 선량하다기보다는 무던하니, 경우에 따라 어떤 상냥한 표정이든 능히 지어 보일 채비가 되어 있는 그런 얼굴이었다. 회중시계는 없었으나, 검은 리본에 매단 별갑 오페라글라스를 지니고 있었다. 오른손 가운뎃손가락에는 값싼 오팔이 박힌 묵직한 금반지를 끼고 있었다.\n\n이반은 화가 난 듯 입을 다문 채 먼저 말을 꺼내려 하지 않았다. 손님은 마치 차를 마시는 주인의 말동무가 되려고 제 방에서 내려온 가난한 식객처럼 가만히 기다리며 앉아 있었는데, 주인이 이맛살을 찌푸리고 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 조심스레 침묵을 지켰다. 그러면서도 주인이 입을 열기만 하면 언제든 상냥하게 대화를 받아 줄 채비가 되어 있었다. 별안간 그의 얼굴에 갑작스러운 근심의 빛이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"이봐요.\" 그가 이반에게 말을 꺼냈다. \"실례하지만, 그저 일깨워 주려고 하는 말입니다만. 당신은 카테리나 이바노브나에 관해 알아보려고 스메르댜코프에게 갔는데, 그녀에 대해서는 아무것도 알아내지 못한 채 그냥 나와 버렸어요. 아마 잊어버린 모양이지요……\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지.\" 이반의 입에서 이 말이 터져 나왔고, 그의 얼굴은 불안으로 어두워졌다. \"그래, 깜빡 잊고 있었어…… 하지만 이제 와선 상관없어, 아무래도 좋아, 내일까지는.\" 그가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 당신,\" 그가 손님 쪽을 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"그건 나도 금방 혼자 떠올렸을 거야, 바로 그게 나를 괴롭히고 있었으니까! 왜 끼어드는 거지, 마치 그걸 당신이 일러 주었고 내가 혼자서는 떠올리지 못했을 거라고 내가 믿기라도 할 것처럼?\"\n\n\"그럼 믿지 마시오.\" 신사가 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마음에 없는데 억지로 믿어 봤자 무슨 소용이겠소? 게다가 증거란 믿음에 아무 도움도 안 되는 법이지요, 특히나 물질적인 증거란. 토마스가 믿은 것은 부활한 그리스도를 보았기 때문이 아니라, 보기도 전에 이미 믿고 싶어 했기 때문이었소. 가령 강신술사들을 보시오…… 나는 그자들을 무척 좋아하는데…… 글쎄, 그자들은 저세상에서 악마들이 자기네한테 뿔을 보여 준다는 이유로 종교의 대의에 봉사하고 있다고 여긴단 말입니다. 그게 이를테면 또 다른 세상이 존재한다는 물질적 증거라는 거지요. 또 다른 세상에 물질적 증거라니, 별소릴 다 듣겠소! 게다가 따지고 보면, 악마가 있다는 걸 증명한다고 해서 신이 있다는 게 증명되는 거요? 나는 관념론자 협회에 가입하고 싶소, 그 안에서 반대파를 이끌면서 이렇게 말할 거요, 나는 사실주의자지 유물론자는 아니라고, 헤헤!\"\n\n\"들어 봐.\" 이반이 별안간 식탁에서 일어섰다. \"나는 헛것을 보고 있는 것 같아…… 사실 헛것을 보고 있는 거지, 무슨 헛소리든 마음대로 지껄여, 난 상관없으니까! 지난번처럼 나를 광분으로 몰아넣지는 못할 거야. 하지만 어쩐지 부끄럽군…… 방 안을 좀 걸어 다녀야겠어…… 지난번처럼 가끔은 당신이 보이지도 않고 목소리조차 들리지 않지만, 그래도 당신이 무슨 헛소리를 지껄이는지는 늘 알아챈다고, 왜냐하면 그건 나니까, 당신이 아니라 나, 나 자신이 말하는 거니까. 다만 지난번에 내가 꿈을 꾼 건지 정말로 당신을 본 건지는 모르겠어. 수건을 적셔서 머리에 얹어야겠어, 그러면 당신은 허공으로 사라져 버릴지도 모르지.\"\n\n이반은 구석으로 가서 수건을 집어 들고 말한 대로 했고, 젖은 수건을 머리에 얹은 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신이 나를 이렇게 허물없이 대해 주니 참 기쁘오.\" 손님이 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"바보 같으니.\" 이반이 웃었다. \"내가 당신한테 격식을 차릴 줄 알았나? 지금은 기분이 좋다고, 이마가 좀 쑤시긴 해도…… 정수리도 그렇고…… 다만 제발 지난번처럼 철학 같은 건 늘어놓지 마. 썩 꺼지지 못하겠거든 뭐든 재미있는 이야기나 해. 잡담이나 늘어놓으라고, 당신은 가난한 식객이니 잡담쯤은 해야지. 이런 악몽이 다 있나! 하지만 난 당신이 두렵지 않아. 내가 당신을 이기고 말 거야. 정신병원에는 끌려가지 않을 거라고!\"\n\n\"이거 매력적이군(C'est charmant), 가난한 식객이라. 그렇소, 이게 나의 타고난 모습이오. 내가 이 땅에서 가난한 식객 아니고 뭐겠소? 그나저나 당신 말을 듣고 있자니 제법 놀랍구려, 당신이 정말로 나를 무언가 실재하는 것으로, 지난번에 그토록 우기던 것처럼 그저 당신의 망상이 아니라 실재로 여기기 시작하다니 말이오……\"\n\n\"단 한순간도 당신을 실재로 여긴 적은 없어.\" 이반이 일종의 광기 어린 어조로 외쳤다. \"당신은 거짓이고, 당신은 내 병이고, 당신은 허깨비야. 다만 내가 당신을 어떻게 없애야 할지 모를 뿐이고, 한동안은 괴로워해야 하리라는 것도 알아. 당신은 내 환각이야. 당신은 나 자신의 화신이지만, 그저 내 한쪽 면일 뿐이지…… 내 생각과 감정 가운데서도 가장 추악하고 어리석은 것들만의 화신 말이야. 그런 관점에서 보면 당신은 나에게 흥미로운 존재일 수도 있겠지, 당신한테 허비할 시간이 있기만 하다면 말이야……\"\n\n\"미안하오, 미안하오만, 내가 한번 짚어 보겠소. 오늘 저녁 당신이 가로등 밑에서 알료샤에게 버럭 화를 내며 이렇게 외쳤지요, '너 그걸 그자한테서 배웠구나! 그자가 나를 찾아온다는 걸 네가 어떻게 알아?' 그때 당신은 나를 생각하고 있었던 거요. 그러니 그 짧은 한순간만큼은 당신도 내가 정말로 존재한다고 믿었던 셈이지.\" 신사가 부드럽게 웃었다.\n\n\"그래, 그건 약해진 순간이었어…… 하지만 당신을 믿을 수는 없었지. 지난번에 내가 잠들어 있었는지 깨어 있었는지 모르겠어. 어쩌면 그땐 그저 꿈을 꾸고 있었을 뿐, 당신을 정말로 본 건 아니었는지도……\"\n\n\"그런데 아까는 어째서 알료샤한테 그렇게 퉁명스럽게 굴었소? 그 애는 참 사랑스럽잖소. 나는 조시마 신부 일로 그 애한테 못되게 굴었지만.\"\n\n\"알료샤 얘긴 꺼내지 마! 어딜 감히, 이 비천한 종놈 주제에!\" 이반이 다시 웃었다.\n\n\"나를 욕하면서도 웃는구려, 좋은 징조요. 하지만 당신은 지난번보다 훨씬 더 정중해졌소, 그 까닭도 내가 알지. 당신의 그 위대한 결심 때문이……\"\n\n\"내 결심 얘긴 입에 올리지 마.\" 이반이 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n\"알겠소, 알겠소, 그건 고귀하고 매력적인 일이지요(c'est noble, c'est charmant), 당신은 형을 변호하고 자신을 희생하려는 거요…… 참으로 기사도적이군요(C'est chevaleresque).\"\n\n\"입 닥쳐, 안 그러면 걷어차 버릴 테다!\"\n\n\"그래도 나로서는 그리 섭섭하지 않을 거요, 그러면 내 목적이 이루어지는 셈이니까. 당신이 나를 걷어찬다면, 당신은 내가 실재한다고 믿어야 하는 거요, 사람들이 유령을 걷어차지는 않으니까 말이오. 농담은 그만두고, 나야 아무래도 좋소, 욕하고 싶으면 욕하시오, 그래도 나한테까지 조금쯤은 더 정중하게 구는 편이 낫겠지만. '바보다, 종놈이다' 하니, 무슨 말본새가 그렇소!\"\n\n\"당신을 욕하는 건 나 자신을 욕하는 거야.\" 이반이 다시 웃었다. \"당신은 나야, 나 자신이라고, 다만 얼굴만 다를 뿐이지. 당신은 그저 내가 생각하는 걸 말할 뿐이고…… 뭔가 새로운 건 한마디도 말하지 못해!\"\n\n\"내 사고방식이 당신과 닮았다면, 그건 다 내 공로지요.\" 신사가 우아하고 위엄 있게 단언했다.\n\n\"당신은 내 생각 중에서도 가장 나쁜 것들만, 게다가 어리석은 것들만 골라내. 당신은 어리석고 천박해. 지독하게 어리석다고. 안 되겠어, 더는 당신을 견딜 수가 없어! 어쩌면 좋지, 대체 어쩌면 좋단 말인가?\" 이반이 이를 악문 채 말했다.\n\n\"여보게, 무엇보다도 나는 신사답게 처신하고 또 그렇게 인정받고 싶을 뿐이오.\" 손님이 가난한 식객 특유의, 겸양과 순박한 자존심이 넘쳐 나는 어조로 말을 꺼냈다. \"나는 가난하지만…… 아주 정직하다고는 못해도…… 사교계에서 일반적으로 통하는 공리에 따르면 나는 타락한 천사라오. 내가 어떻게 천사였을 수 있었는지는 도무지 상상이 안 가오. 설령 그랬다 한들 너무도 까마득한 옛날 일이라 잊어버린들 탈 될 게 없을 거요. 이제 나는 그저 신사다운 인물이라는 평판이나 소중히 여기며, 되도록 남의 비위를 맞추려고 애쓰면서 형편 닿는 대로 살아가오. 나는 진심으로 사람을 사랑하건만, 무던히도 모함을 받아 왔소! 이렇게 이따금 당신 곁에 머물면, 내 삶이 일종의 실재성을 얻게 되는데, 나는 그게 무엇보다도 좋소. 알다시피 나도 당신처럼 환상적인 것 때문에 괴로워하기에, 이 땅의 사실주의를 사랑하는 거요. 여기, 당신들 곁에서는 모든 것이 한계가 분명하고, 여기서는 모든 것이 공식화되고 기하학적인데, 우리한테는 부정방정식밖에 없거든! 나는 여기서 몽상에 잠긴 채 떠돌아다니오. 나는 몽상하기를 좋아하지요. 게다가 이 땅에서는 나도 미신을 믿게 되오. 제발 웃지 마시오, 그게 바로 내가 좋아하는 거니까, 미신을 믿게 되는 것 말이오. 나는 여기서 당신들의 온갖 습관을 받아들이오. 글쎄, 대중탕에 가는 걸 좋아하게 됐다면 믿겠소? 가서 상인들이며 사제들과 함께 몸을 푹푹 찌곤 하지요. 내가 꿈꾸는 건 단번에, 그것도 돌이킬 수 없이, 백팔십 근쯤 나가는 어느 상인 마누라의 몸으로 화신하여 그 여자가 믿는 걸 죄다 믿어 버리는 것이오. 내 이상은 교회에 가서 순박한 신앙으로 촛불 하나 바치는 것, 정말이지 그렇소. 그러면 내 고통도 끝장이 날 텐데. 나는 치료받는 것도 좋아하오. 봄에 천연두가 돌았을 때 나는 고아원에 가서 종두를 맞았는데, 그날 내가 얼마나 즐거웠는지 당신이 알기나 하겠소. 슬라브인들을 위한다는 명목으로 십 루블을 기부하기도 했지!…… 한데 당신은 듣고 있지 않군. 그거 아오, 당신 오늘 저녁 영 상태가 좋지 않소. 어제 그 의사한테 다녀온 거 알고 있소…… 그래, 건강은 어떻소? 의사가 뭐라 합디까?\"\n\n\"바보 같으니!\" 이반이 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"하지만 어쨌든 당신은 영리하잖소. 또 욕이오? 동정해서 물어본 게 아니오. 대답 안 해도 좋소. 또 류머티즘이 도졌단 말이지……\"\n\n\"바보!\" 이반이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"같은 말만 자꾸 하는구려. 하지만 작년에 어찌나 류머티즘 발작이 심했던지 지금까지도 또렷이 기억난다오.\"\n\n\"악마가 류머티즘에 걸린다고!\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐요, 이따금 육신의 형상을 걸칠 때면? 나도 육신의 형상을 걸치면 그 대가를 치르는 거요. 나는 사탄이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇도 나와 무관하지 않다고 여기오(Satan sum et nihil humanum a me alienum puto).\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 뭐, 나는 사탄이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇도(Satan sum et nihil humanum)…… 악마치고는 제법 그럴듯한데!\"\n\n\"드디어 당신 마음에 들었다니 기쁘오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 나한테서 얻은 게 아니야.\" 이반이 무언가에 한 대 맞은 듯 별안간 멈춰 섰다. \"그건 내 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없어, 이상하군.\"\n\n\"이건 새로운 거지요, 그렇지 않소(C'est du nouveau, n'est-ce pas)? 이번에는 정직하게 처신하면서 당신에게 설명해 주겠소. 들어 보시오, 꿈속에서, 특히 악몽 속에서, 소화불량 때문이든 뭐 때문이든, 사람은 이따금 그토록 예술적인 환상을, 그토록 복잡하고 생생한 현실을, 그토록 무수한 사건들, 그것도 사건들로 이루어진 온 세계를, 그토록 짜임새 있는 줄거리로 엮어 내어, 더없이 숭고한 일들에서부터 소맷부리의 마지막 단추 하나에 이르기까지 그토록 뜻밖의 세부를 곁들여 보는 법인데, 맹세컨대 레프 톨스토이도 그런 건 결코 지어낸 적이 없소. 한데 그런 꿈을 꾸는 건 작가들이 아니라, 가장 평범한 사람들, 관리들, 신문 기자들, 사제들이란 말이오…… 이건 완전히 수수께끼지요. 실제로 어느 정치가가 내게 고백하기를, 자기의 가장 훌륭한 발상은 죄다 잠들었을 때 떠올랐다고 합디다. 그래, 지금이 바로 그런 경우요, 나는 당신의 환각이지만 그래도 악몽 속에서와 꼭 마찬가지로, 전에 당신 머릿속에 떠오른 적 없는 독창적인 것들을 말한단 말이오. 그러니 나는 당신의 생각을 되풀이하는 게 아니라, 그러면서도 그저 당신의 악몽일 뿐이오, 그 이상은 아무것도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"거짓말 마, 당신의 목적은 당신이 따로 존재하는 거지 내 악몽이 아니라고 나를 설득하는 거잖아, 그런데 지금은 당신이 꿈이라고 우기고 있군.\"\n\n\"여보게, 나는 오늘 특별한 방법을 택했으니, 나중에 그걸 당신에게 설명하리다. 가만, 내가 어디서 말을 끊었더라? 아, 그렇지! 그때 내가 감기에 걸렸는데, 다만 여기가 아니라 저쪽에서였소.\"\n\n\"저쪽이 어디야? 말해 봐, 당신 여기 오래 있을 거야? 좀 가 줄 수는 없나?\" 이반이 거의 절망에 빠져 외쳤다. 그는 이리저리 거닐기를 멈추고 소파에 앉아, 다시 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고서 두 손으로 머리를 단단히 움켜쥐었다. 그는 젖은 수건을 잡아채어 짜증스레 내던졌다. 그것은 아무 소용도 없는 게 분명했다.\n\n\"당신은 신경이 다 망가졌소.\" 신사가 무심하리만치 편안하면서도 더없이 정중한 태도로 말했다. \"내가 감기에 걸릴 수 있다는 것마저도 당신은 못마땅한 모양인데, 그건 더없이 자연스러운 경위로 일어난 일이라오. 그때 나는 페테르부르크의, 부처(府處)에 영향력을 노리던 어느 지체 높은 부인의 저택에서 열리는 외교 야회에 가느라 서두르고 있었소. 그래, 야회복에 흰 넥타이, 장갑까지 갖춰 입었는데, 정작 그때 내가 있던 곳은 신만이 아는 어디였고, 당신네 지구에 닿으려면 우주 공간을 가로질러 날아와야 했단 말이오…… 물론 그야 눈 깜짝할 새였지만, 그래도 알다시피 태양에서 오는 빛줄기 하나도 꼬박 팔 분이 걸리는데, 야회복에 조끼 앞섶을 풀어 헤친 꼴을 한번 상상해 보시오. 영혼이야 얼지 않지만, 육신의 형상을 걸쳤을 때는, 글쎄…… 요컨대 나는 미처 생각도 못 하고 길을 나섰는데, 알다시피 그 천공(天空)의 공간이라는 데가, 창공 위에 있는 그 물속이라는 데가, 어찌나 추운지…… 하긴 추위라고 부를 수도 없을 정도라, 한번 상상해 보시오, 영하 백오십 도라니까! 시골 처녀들이 하는 장난 알지요, 영하 삼십 도쯤 될 때 멋모르는 녀석을 꾀어 도끼를 핥게 하면, 혀가 그 자리에서 쩍 들러붙고, 속아 넘어간 녀석이 살갗을 뜯어내는 바람에 피가 나잖소. 한데 그건 삼십 도쯤일 때 얘기고, 백오십 도라면 도끼에 손가락만 갖다 대도 그걸로 끝장날 것 같소…… 하긴 거기에 도끼라는 게 있을 수만 있다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"그런데 거기에 도끼라는 게 있을 수 있나?\" 이반이 무심하고 경멸하듯 말허리를 잘랐다. 그는 그 헛것을 믿지 않으려고, 완전한 광기 속으로 가라앉지 않으려고 안간힘을 다하고 있었다.\n\n\"도끼라?\" 손님이 놀라서 되물었다.\n\n\"그래, 도끼가 거기서 어떻게 되겠느냐고?\" 이반이 별안간 일종의 사납고도 집요한 고집을 부리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"우주 공간에서 도끼가 어떻게 되겠느냐고? 거참 별 생각을 다 하는구려(Quelle idée)! 그게 어느 거리만큼 떨어져 나간다면, 내 생각엔 영문도 모른 채 위성처럼 지구 둘레를 빙빙 돌기 시작할 거요. 천문학자들은 그 도끼가 뜨고 지는 시각을 계산해 내고, 가추크가 그걸 자기 달력에 적어 넣겠지, 그게 다요.\"\n\n\"당신은 어리석어, 지독하게 어리석다고.\" 이반이 짜증스레 말했다. \"허풍을 떨려거든 좀 더 영리하게 떨든가, 아니면 안 들을 테야. 당신은 사실주의로 나를 이겨 먹으려고, 당신이 존재한다고 나를 설득하려 들지만, 나는 당신이 존재한다는 걸 믿고 싶지 않아! 믿지 않겠다고!\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 허풍을 떠는 게 아니라, 죄다 진실이오. 진실이란 안타깝게도 좀처럼 재미있는 법이 없지. 보아하니 당신은 나한테서 뭔가 거창한 것을, 어쩌면 뭔가 근사한 것을 기대하기를 고집하는군. 그거 참 딱한 일이오, 나는 내가 줄 수 있는 것만 줄 뿐이니까……\"\n\n\"철학 얘기 하지 마, 이 멍청아!\"\n\n\"철학이라니, 내 오른쪽 옆구리가 죄다 마비되어 신음하고 끙끙대는 판에. 의학자란 의학자는 죄다 찾아가 봤소. 그들은 진단은 기막히게 잘하고, 당신 병을 손금 들여다보듯 훤히 꿰뚫지만, 정작 어떻게 고치는지는 깜깜이오. 여기 열성에 찬 어린 의학도가 하나 있었는데, '당신은 죽을지도 모르지요.' 하더니 '하지만 무슨 병으로 죽는지는 완벽하게 아실 겁니다!' 하지 뭐요. 게다가 사람을 전문의한테 떠넘기는 그 작태라니! '우리는 진단만 합니다.' 하면서 '아무개 전문의한테 가시오, 그가 고쳐 줄 겁니다.' 한다오. 온갖 병을 다 고치던 옛날 의사는 이제 자취를 싹 감추었소, 정말이오, 이제는 전문의들밖에 없고 죄다 신문에 광고를 낸다오. 코에 무슨 탈이 났다 하면 당신을 파리로 보내지요, 거기 코를 고치는 유럽 전문의가 있다나. 파리에 가면 그 전문의가 당신 코를 들여다보고는, 나는 당신 오른쪽 콧구멍밖에 못 고치오, 왼쪽 콧구멍은 안 고치오, 그건 내 전문이 아니라서, 빈으로 가시오, 거기 당신 왼쪽 콧구멍을 고쳐 줄 전문의가 있소, 이러는 거요. 어쩌겠소? 나는 민간요법에 기댈 수밖에 없었지, 어느 독일 의사가 목욕탕에서 꿀과 소금을 몸에 문질러 보라고 일러 줍디다. 목욕이나 한 번 더 하려고 갔다가 온몸에 떡칠을 했는데, 아무 효험도 없었소. 절망에 빠진 나는 밀라노의 마테이 백작에게 편지를 썼소. 그가 책 한 권과 물약 몇 방울을 보내 줬는데, 그 양반 복받을지어다, 한데 글쎄, 정작 나를 고쳐 준 건 호프 맥아 진액이었지 뭐요! 우연히 사서 한 병 반쯤 마셨더니, 춤이라도 출 만큼 멀쩡해지고 씻은 듯이 나아 버렸소. 나는 고마운 마음에 떠밀려 신문에 편지를 써서 그에게 감사를 표하기로 마음먹었는데, 글쎄, 그게 끝도 없는 골칫거리가 되고 말았소. 어느 신문 하나 내 편지를 실어 주려 하지 않았다오. '그건 무척 반동적인 일이오.' 하면서 '아무도 믿지 않을 거요. 악마는 존재하지 않으니까(Le diable n'existe point). 그러니 익명으로 남는 편이 낫겠소.' 이렇게 충고합디다. 익명이라면 감사 편지가 무슨 소용이오? 나는 신문사 사람들과 함께 웃으며 이렇게 말했소, '요즘 세상에 신을 믿는 건 반동적인 일이지만, 나는 악마니까 나쯤은 믿어 줘도 되지 않겠소.' 그러자 그들이 말합디다, '그야 충분히 이해합니다. 악마를 안 믿는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까? 그래도 안 될 일입니다, 우리 명성에 누가 될 수도 있어서요. 농담이라면 또 모를까.' 하지만 농담으로 하기엔 그리 재치 있을 것 같지 않더란 말이오. 그래서 결국 실리지 못했소. 그런데 그거 아오, 나는 지금까지도 그 일이 못내 서운하다오. 내 가장 고결한 감정, 가령 감사 같은 것조차도, 그저 내 사회적 처지 때문에 글자 그대로 거부당하는 거란 말이오.\"\n\n\"또 철학적 사색인가?\" 이반이 악의에 차 으르렁댔다.\n\n\"신께서 나를 그런 일에서 지켜 주시기를, 하지만 사람이 어찌 가끔 하소연 한번 못 하겠소. 나는 모함당한 사람이오. 당신은 시도 때도 없이 나더러 어리석다고 나무라지요. 보아하니 당신은 젊군. 여보게, 지성이 다는 아니라오! 나는 본디 다정하고 명랑한 마음을 타고났소. '나도 온갖 보드빌을 쓰는 사람이오.' 당신은 나를 늙어 버린 흘레스타코프쯤으로 여기는 모양이지만, 내 운명은 그보다 훨씬 더 심각한 거요. 태초가 있기도 전에, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 명령에 따라, 나는 '부정하도록' 예정되었소, 그러면서도 나는 진심으로 마음씨가 곱고 부정과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 자란 말이오. '아니, 너는 가서 부정해야 한다, 부정이 없으면 비판도 없는 법이거늘, 비판 난(欄)이 없는 잡지가 무슨 잡지란 말이냐?' 비판이 없으면 그저 '호산나' 한마디뿐일 거요. 하지만 삶에는 호산나 하나만으로는 충분치 않으니, 그 호산나는 의심의 도가니에서 단련되어야 하고, 그런 식으로 계속되는 거지요. 하지만 나는 거기에 끼어들지 않소, 내가 만든 것도 아니고, 나한테 책임이 있는 것도 아니니까. 그래, 그들은 속죄양을 골라 나더러 비판 난을 쓰게 만들었고, 그리하여 삶이 가능해진 거요. 우리는 그 희극을 이해하오. 가령 나는 그저 소멸을 청하지요. 그러나 안 된다, 살아라, 너 없이는 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 테니, 이렇게 명령받는단 말이오. 만일 우주의 모든 것이 사리에 맞는다면, 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 거요. 당신이 없으면 아무런 사건도 없을 텐데, 사건은 반드시 있어야 하니까. 그래서 나는 비위에 거슬리면서도 사건을 일으키는 데 봉사하며, 명령받은 대로 비합리적인 짓을 하는 거요. 사람들은 그 의심할 바 없는 지성에도 불구하고 이 광대극을 무언가 심각한 것으로 받아들이는데, 바로 그게 그들의 비극이오. 물론 그들은 괴로워하지요…… 하지만 그래도 그들은 살아가오, 환상이 아닌 진짜 삶을 살아가지요, 고통이야말로 삶이니까. 고통이 없다면 무슨 즐거움이 있겠소? 그건 끝도 없는 예배로 변해 버릴 거요, 거룩하긴 하나 따분하기 짝이 없는. 한데 나는 어떻소? 나는 괴로워하지만, 그래도 살지는 못한단 말이오. 나는 부정방정식 속의 x요. 나는 모든 시작도 끝도 잃어버린, 심지어 제 이름조차 잊어버린, 삶 속의 일종의 허깨비란 말이오. 당신은 웃는군. 아니, 웃는 게 아니라 또 화가 났구려. 당신은 늘 화만 내지, 당신이 신경 쓰는 건 오로지 지성뿐이지만, 다시 한번 말하건대, 나는 이 모든 별 너머의 삶을, 온갖 관등과 영예를 죄다 내던지고서라도, 그저 백팔십 근 나가는 어느 상인 마누라의 영혼으로 변신하여 신의 제단에 촛불을 바칠 수만 있다면 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신조차도 신을 믿지 않는단 말인가?\" 이반이 증오에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 뭐라 답할 수 있겠소? 그러니까, 당신이 진지하게 묻는 거라면 말이오……\"\n\n\"신이 있어, 없어?\" 이반이 여전히 사납고 격렬한 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 그럼 당신 진지하게 묻는 거구려! 여보게, 정말이지 나도 모르오. 자! 이제 말해 버렸소!\"\n\n\"모른다면서 신을 본다고? 아니, 당신은 따로 떨어진 존재가 아니야, 당신은 나 자신이고, 당신은 나, 그 이상도 아무것도 아니야! 당신은 쓰레기야, 당신은 내 망상이라고!\"\n\n\"글쎄, 정 원한다면, 나도 당신과 같은 철학을 지녔다고 해 두지요, 그게 사실일 테니. 나는 생각한다, 고로 존재한다(Je pense, donc je suis), 그것만큼은 나도 확실히 아오. 나머지 전부, 이 모든 세계며, 신이며, 심지어 사탄까지도, 내가 보기엔 죄다 증명되지 않은 것들이오. 그 모든 게 스스로 존재하는 것인지, 아니면 그저 나 자신의 발현, 영원토록 홀로 존재해 온 내 자아의 논리적 전개일 뿐인지…… 하지만 여기서 서둘러 그만두겠소, 안 그러면 당신이 당장 벌떡 일어나 나를 두들겨 팰 것 같으니.\"\n\n\"차라리 무슨 일화나 들려주는 게 낫겠어!\" 이반이 비참하게 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 우리 화제에 들어맞는 일화가 하나 있긴 하오, 일화라기보다 전설이라고 해야겠지만. 알다시피 당신은 불신을 나무라며, 그러면서도 너는 믿지 않는다고 하잖소. 하지만 여보게, 그런 건 나 혼자만이 아니라오. 우리는 지금 저쪽에서 다들 뒤죽박죽이 되어 있는데, 죄다 당신네 과학 탓이오. 한때는 원자가 있고, 오감이 있고, 네 가지 원소가 있어서, 만사가 그럭저럭 들어맞았소. 원자야 고대 세계에도 있었지. 한데 당신네가 화학 분자니 원형질이니 악마나 알 법한 것들을 발견했다는 걸 우리가 알게 된 다음부터는, 우리도 콧대를 꺾을 수밖에 없었소. 영락없는 뒤죽박죽이고, 무엇보다도 미신이며 추문이 판을 치오. 우리한테도 당신네 못지않게 추문이 들끓는다오, 알다시피, 사실 좀 더 많다고 해야겠지, 게다가 염탐질까지 있소, 정말로, 우리한테는 사사로운 정보를 받아 챙기는 비밀경찰 부서까지 있으니까. 그래, 이 황당한 전설은 우리 중세에 속한 것인데, 당신네 중세가 아니라 우리 중세 말이오, 우리들 사이에서조차 아무도 믿지 않는 거요, 백팔십 근 나가는 노파들이나 믿을까, 당신네 노파들이 아니라 우리 노파들 말이오. 당신네한테 있는 건 우리한테도 죄다 있소, 당신과의 우정을 봐서 우리 비밀 하나를 누설하는 거요, 금지된 일이긴 하지만. 이 전설은 천국에 관한 거요. 듣자 하니, 이 땅에 어떤 사상가요 철학자가 하나 있었다 합디다. 그자는 모든 것을 부정했소, '법도, 양심도, 신앙도' 죄다, 그중에서도 특히 내세를 부정했지요. 그자가 죽었소. 그자는 곧장 어둠과 죽음 속으로 가리라 여겼는데, 정작 눈앞에 내세가 펼쳐진 거요. 그자는 아연실색하며 분개했소. '이건 내 원칙에 어긋난다!' 이렇게 말했지요. 그래서 그자는 벌을 받았소…… 그러니까, 미안하오만, 나는 그저 내가 들은 걸 그대로 옮기는 것뿐이오, 어디까지나 전설이니까…… 그자는 어둠 속을 백경(百京) 킬로미터 걷도록 선고받았소(알다시피 우리는 미터법을 채택했거든), 그래서 그 백경 킬로미터를 다 걷고 나면 천국의 문이 그자에게 열리고 그자는 용서받게 되리라는 거였소……\"\n\n\"그럼 그 백경 킬로미터 말고 저세상에 또 무슨 고문이 있지?\" 이반이 묘한 열의를 띠고 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 고문이냐고? 아, 묻지 마시오. 옛날에는 별의별 게 다 있었지만, 이제는 주로 도덕적 형벌, '양심의 가책'이니 뭐니 하는 그런 헛소리로 옮겨 갔소. 그것도 당신네한테서, 당신네 풍속이 물러진 데서 얻어 온 거요. 한데 그래서 누가 덕을 본단 말이오? 그저 양심이라곤 없는 자들이나 덕을 보지, 양심이 없는데 어떻게 양심으로 고통받겠소? 하지만 양심과 명예심이 있는 점잖은 사람들은 그것 때문에 괴로워한다오. 토양이 마련되지도 않았는데 들이미는 개혁이란, 특히 외국에서 베껴 온 제도라는 것은, 해악밖에 끼치지 못하오! 옛날의 불(火)이 차라리 나았소. 그래, 백경 킬로미터를 선고받은 이 사내는, 우뚝 멈춰 서서 둘레를 둘러보더니 길 한복판을 가로질러 벌렁 드러눕고 말았소. '안 가겠다, 원칙적으로 거부한다!' 어느 계몽된 러시아 무신론자의 영혼을, 고래 뱃속에서 사흘 밤낮을 뾰로통해 있던 예언자 요나의 영혼과 뒤섞어 보시오, 그러면 길을 가로질러 드러누운 그 사상가의 성격이 나오는 거요.\"\n\n\"그자는 거기서 무엇 위에 드러누웠지?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 드러누울 만한 무언가가 있었겠지요. 안 웃는구려?\"\n\n\"브라보!\" 이반이 여전히 그 묘한 열의를 띤 채 외쳤다. 이제 그는 뜻밖의 호기심을 품고 귀를 기울이고 있었다. \"그래, 그자는 지금도 거기 드러누워 있나?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 요점이오, 그자는 그러고 있지 않다는 거. 그자는 거의 천 년을 거기 드러누워 있다가, 결국 일어나 다시 걷기 시작했소.\"\n\n\"이런 멍청이!\" 이반이 신경질적으로 웃으면서도 여전히 무언가를 골똘히 곱씹는 듯 외쳤다. \"영영 거기 드러누워 있는 거나 백경 킬로미터를 걷는 거나 무슨 차이가 있겠어? 그걸 걸으려면 십억 년은 걸릴 텐데?\"\n\n\"그보다 훨씬 더 걸리지. 연필과 종이가 있으면 계산해 볼 수 있을 텐데. 하지만 그자는 벌써 오래전에 도착했고, 바로 거기서 이야기가 시작되는 거요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그자가 도착했다고? 한데 어떻게 그걸 다 걸을 십억 년을 얻었지?\"\n\n\"아니, 당신은 자꾸 우리의 지금 이 지구만 생각하는군! 하지만 우리의 지금 이 지구도 어쩌면 십억 번이나 되풀이됐을지 모르오. 아니, 이 지구는 다 타 버리고, 얼어붙고, 쩍쩍 갈라지고, 산산조각 나고, 제 원소로 분해되었다가, 다시 '창공 위의 물'이 되고, 그다음엔 또 혜성이 되고, 또 태양이 되고, 또 태양에서 지구가 되고, 그렇게 똑같은 순서가 끝없이, 그것도 세세한 데까지 토씨 하나 다르지 않게, 더없이 꼴사납고 견딜 수 없으리만치 따분하게 되풀이됐을지도 모른단 말이오……\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그자가 도착했을 땐 무슨 일이 벌어졌지?\"\n\n\"아니, 천국의 문이 열려 그자가 안으로 걸어 들어간 순간, 거기 머문 지 채 이 초도 되기 전에, 그자는 제 회중시계를 보고는(하긴 내 생각엔 그자의 시계는 오는 길에 진작 제 원소로 녹아 버렸겠지만) 그 이 초야말로 백경 킬로미터가 아니라 백경의 백경 곱하기에 다시 백경 제곱을 한 만큼을 걸을 가치가 있다고 외쳤소! 사실 그자는 '호산나'를 불렀는데, 어찌나 도가 지나쳤던지, 거기 있던 고매한 사상을 지닌 몇몇이 처음엔 그자와 악수조차 하려 들지 않을 정도였소. 그자가 너무 빨리 반동으로 돌아섰다나, 이렇게들 말하면서. 러시아 기질이지. 거듭 말하지만 이건 전설이오. 들은 그대로 전하는 거요. 그러니 이런 문제에 관해선 우리도 지금까지 그런 식의 생각을 품고 있는 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"이제 당신 꼬리를 잡았어!\" 이반이 마침내 무언가를 떠올리는 데 성공하기라도 한 듯 거의 어린아이 같은 환희에 차 외쳤다. \"그 백경 년 얘긴 내가 직접 지어낸 거라고! 그땐 내가 열일곱 살이었고, 고등학교에 다니고 있었지. 내가 그 일화를 지어내서 코로프킨이라는 학교 친구한테 들려줬어, 모스크바에서 있었던 일이지…… 그 일화는 어찌나 특이한지 내가 어디서 가져왔을 리가 없어. 까맣게 잊은 줄 알았는데…… 나도 모르게 그게 떠오른 거야, 내가 혼자 떠올린 거라고, 당신이 들려준 게 아니야! 사람들은 사형장에 끌려갈 때조차 그렇게 무수한 것들을 무의식중에 떠올리곤 하지…… 그게 꿈속에서 다시 떠오른 거야. 당신이 바로 그 꿈이야! 당신은 꿈이지, 살아 있는 존재가 아니라고!\"\n\n\"당신이 내 존재를 부정하는 그 격렬함을 보니,\" 신사가 웃었다. \"당신이 나를 믿고 있다는 확신이 드오.\"\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 안 믿어! 당신에 대한 믿음이라곤 알갱이 하나의 백분의 일도 없단 말이야!\"\n\n\"하지만 천분의 일은 있군. 동종요법 약은 어쩌면 희석할수록 더 강해지는 법이지요. 솔직히 인정해 보시오, 알갱이 하나의 만분의 일만큼은 믿음이 있다고.\"\n\n\"단 한순간도 없어.\" 이반이 광분하여 외쳤다. \"하지만 당신을 믿고 싶기는 해.\" 그가 묘하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아하! 자백이 나왔군! 하지만 나는 마음씨가 곱지요. 다시 한번 당신을 도와주겠소. 들어 보시오, 당신을 붙잡은 건 나지 당신이 나를 붙잡은 게 아니오. 당신이 까맣게 잊은 그 일화를 내가 일부러 들려준 거요, 나에 대한 당신의 믿음을 완전히 깨부수려고 말이오.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야. 당신이 찾아온 목적은 당신이 존재한다고 나를 설득하는 거잖아!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 하지만 망설임, 미결, 믿음과 불신 사이의 갈등이라는 것은, 당신 같은 양심적인 인간에게는 이따금 차라리 당장 목을 매는 편이 나을 만큼 큰 고문이 되지요. 당신이 나를 믿는 쪽으로 기우는 걸 알았기에, 나는 그 일화를 들려주어 불신을 좀 처방한 거요. 나는 당신을 믿음과 불신 사이로 번갈아 이끄는데, 거기엔 내 나름의 동기가 있소. 이게 바로 그 새로운 방법이오. 당신이 나를 완전히 불신하게 되는 즉시, 당신은 내 면전에 대고 내가 꿈이 아니라 실재라고 우기기 시작할 거요. 나는 당신을 알지. 그러면 나는 내 목적을 이루는 셈인데, 그건 명예로운 목적이오. 나는 당신 안에 그저 자그마한 믿음의 알갱이 하나를 심어 둘 거요, 그러면 그게 떡갈나무로 자라날 거고, 그것도 어찌나 큰 떡갈나무가 될지, 당신은 그 위에 올라앉아 '광야의 은수자들과 거룩한 여인들'의 대열에 끼어들기를 갈망하게 될 거요, 그게 바로 당신이 남몰래 갈망하는 거니까. 당신은 메뚜기로 끼니를 때우고, 영혼을 구원하겠다고 광야로 떠돌아 나설 거란 말이오!\"\n\n\"그럼 당신이 애쓰는 게 내 영혼의 구원을 위해서라는 거냐, 이 악당아?\"\n\n\"사람이 가끔은 선한 일도 해야지요. 당신은 어찌 그리 심사가 뒤틀렸소!\"\n\n\"바보 같으니! 당신이 언제 그 거룩한 사람들을, 메뚜기를 먹으며 온몸이 이끼로 뒤덮이도록 광야에서 십칠 년이나 기도하던 그들을 유혹해 본 적이라도 있어?\"\n\n\"여보게, 나는 그 짓밖에 안 했소. 사람은 온 세상을, 모든 세계를 다 잊어버리고서 그런 성자 하나에게 들러붙는 법이오, 그자가 더없이 귀한 다이아몬드니까. 알다시피 그런 영혼 하나가 이따금 별자리 하나 전체만큼의 값어치가 있단 말이오. 우리한테는 우리 나름의 계산법이 있거든. 그런 자를 정복하면 그건 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀한 거요! 게다가 그중 더러는, 정말이지, 당신은 안 믿겠지만, 교양으로 따져도 당신한테 결코 뒤지지 않소. 그들은 같은 순간에 그토록 깊은 믿음과 불신을 동시에 응시할 수 있어서, 이따금 배우 고르부노프 말마따나 정말이지 머리카락 한 올 차이로 '뒤집어질' 지경에 이른단 말이오.\"\n\n\"그래서, 당신 코는 잡아당겨졌나?\"\n\n* 코를 잡아당기다: 속아 넘어가다, 우롱당하다를 뜻하는 러시아어 관용 표현.\n\"여보게,\" 손님이 경구를 읊듯 말했다. \"코가 잡아당겨지는 게 코를 통째로 잃는 것보다야 낫지요. 얼마 전에 어느 가엾은 후작이(그자는 분명 전문의한테 치료를 받은 모양이오) 자기 영적 아버지인 예수회 신부에게 고해성사를 하면서 한 말이오. 나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 정말이지 매력적이었소. '제 코를 돌려주십시오!' 그자가 이러면서 제 가슴을 쳤소. '내 아들아,' 신부가 얼버무리며 말합디다, '만물은 헤아릴 수 없는 섭리의 명령에 따라 이루어지는 법이며, 불행처럼 보이는 일도 이따금 비범한, 비록 눈에 띄지는 않으나, 은혜로 이어진단다. 모진 운명이 그대에게서 코를 앗아 갔다면, 그건 이제 아무도 그대의 코를 잡아당길 수 없으니 도리어 그대에게 이로운 일이니라.' '거룩한 신부님, 그건 위로가 안 됩니다.' 절망에 빠진 후작이 외칩디다. '제자리에 붙어만 있다면, 저는 평생 날마다 코를 잡아당겨져도 기꺼이 좋겠습니다.' '내 아들아,' 신부가 한숨을 쉬며 말합디다, '모든 축복을 한꺼번에 바랄 수는 없는 법이니라. 이는 섭리에 대한 불평이로다, 섭리는 이 일에 있어서조차 그대를 잊지 않으셨거늘, 그대가 방금 그러했듯이 평생 코를 잡아당겨져도 기쁘겠노라 외치며 불평한다면, 그대의 소원은 이미 에둘러 이루어진 셈이니, 그대가 코를 잃었을 때 그대는 이미 코가 꿰여 끌려다닌 것이기 때문이니라.'\"\n\n\"바보 같으니, 무슨 그런 어처구니없는 소릴!\" 이반이 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보게, 나는 그저 당신을 즐겁게 해 주고 싶었을 뿐이오. 하지만 맹세컨대 그게 바로 진짜 예수회식 궤변이고, 또 맹세컨대 모든 게 내가 들려준 그대로 토씨 하나 다르지 않게 일어난 일이오. 얼마 전에 벌어진 일인데, 나한테 적잖은 골칫거리를 안겨 주었지. 그 가엾은 청년은 집에 돌아온 바로 그날 밤 권총으로 자살했소. 나는 마지막 순간까지 그자 곁을 지켰지. 그 예수회 고해소야말로 우울할 때 나의 가장 즐거운 소일거리라오. 여기 바로 며칠 전에 벌어진 또 다른 사건이 있소. 스무 살 난 자그마한 금발의 노르망디 처녀가―군침이 절로 돌 만큼 풍만하고 순진한 미인이오―늙은 신부를 찾아왔소. 그녀가 몸을 굽히고 격자창에 대고 자기 죄를 속삭였소. '아니, 내 딸아, 벌써 또 타락했단 말이냐?' 신부가 외칩디다. '오 거룩하신 마리아여, 이게 무슨 소린가! 이번엔 같은 사내도 아니라니, 대체 이게 언제까지 계속될 셈이냐? 부끄럽지도 않으냐!' '아, 신부님(Ah, mon père),' 죄지은 여자가 참회의 눈물을 흘리며 대답합디다, '그분께는 그토록 큰 기쁨이 되고, 저한테는 그토록 작은 괴로움일 뿐인걸요(ça lui fait tant de plaisir, et à moi si peu de peine)!' 상상해 보시오, 그런 대답이라니! 나는 흠칫 물러섰소. 그건 자연의 외침이었고, 굳이 말하자면 순결 그 자체보다도 더 나은 것이었소. 나는 그 자리에서 그녀의 죄를 사해 주고 돌아서 나오려 했는데, 다시 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었소. 격자창 너머로 그 신부가 그녀와 저녁에 만날 약속을 잡는 소리가 들렸으니 말이오―부싯돌처럼 굳은 늙은이였건만, 순식간에 넘어가 버린 거요! 그게 자연이었소, 자연의 진리가 제 권리를 주장한 거지! 뭐요, 또 코를 찡긋거리는구려? 또 화가 났소? 도무지 당신 비위를 어떻게 맞춰야 할지 모르겠군……\"\n\n\"날 좀 내버려 둬, 당신은 따라다니는 악몽처럼 내 머릿속을 두드려 대고 있어.\" 이반이 제 환영 앞에서 속수무책으로 비참하게 신음했다. \"당신한텐 진절머리가 나, 괴롭고 견딜 수 없을 만큼. 당신을 떨쳐 낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 다 내주겠어!\"\n\n\"거듭 말하지만, 기대를 좀 누그러뜨리시오, 나한테 '위대하고 고귀한 것'을 다 내놓으라 하지 마시오, 그러면 우리가 얼마나 잘 지낼 수 있는지 알게 될 거요.\" 신사가 인상 깊게 말했다. \"당신은 내가 천둥 번개를 거느리고 날개를 그을린 채 시뻘건 불빛 속에서 나타나지 않고, 이렇게 수수한 모습으로 나타났다고 정말로 화가 난 거요. 첫째로 당신은 미적 감정에 상처를 입었고, 둘째로 자존심에 상처를 입은 거지요. 어떻게 이렇게 천박한 악마가 당신처럼 위대한 인물을 찾아올 수 있느냔 말이오! 그래, 당신한테는 그 낭만적인 기질이 있소, 벨린스키가 그토록 비웃던 그 기질 말이오. 어쩔 수 없지요, 젊은이, 당신을 찾아오려고 채비하면서 나도 농담 삼아 카프카스에서 복무한 퇴역 장군의 모습으로, 외투에 사자와 태양 훈장을 단 채 나타날까 생각해 봤소. 하지만 그러기엔 정말이지 겁이 났소, 사자와 태양 훈장 따위를, 적어도 북극성 훈장이나 시리우스 훈장도 아니고, 그걸 외투에 감히 달았다고 당신이 나를 흠씬 두들겨 팼을 테니 말이오. 게다가 당신은 자꾸 나더러 어리석다고 하는데, 원, 천만에! 나는 지성에서 당신과 맞먹겠다고 주장하지도 않소. 메피스토펠레스는 파우스트에게 자기는 악을 바라면서도 선만 행한다고 선언했지요. 글쎄, 그자야 무슨 말이든 하라지, 나는 정반대요. 나는 어쩌면 온 천지에서 진리를 사랑하고 진심으로 선을 바라는 유일한 존재일 거요. 나는 그 자리에 있었소, 십자가에서 죽으신 말씀이, 회개한 도둑의 영혼을 품에 안고 하늘로 솟아오르실 때 말이오. 나는 케루빔들이 호산나를 노래하고 외치는 환희에 찬 절규를 들었고, 하늘과 온 천지를 뒤흔드는 세라핌들의 우레 같은 환희를 들었소, 그리고 거룩한 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 나도 그 합창에 끼어들어 그들 모두와 함께 호산나를 외치고 싶었소. 그 말이 하마터면 내게서 새어 나올 뻔했소, 하마터면 내 입술에서 터져 나올 뻔했지…… 알다시피 나는 무척 예민하고 미적으로 쉽게 감응하는 자니까. 하지만 상식이―오, 내 성격의 가장 불행한 특질인―나를 적당한 선에서 붙들어 두었고, 그래서 나는 그 순간을 그냥 흘려보내고 말았소! 무슨 일이 벌어졌겠는가, 나는 곰곰이 생각해 봤소, 내가 호산나를 외쳤다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌겠는가? 이 땅의 모든 것이 당장 꺼져 버리고 아무 사건도 일어날 수 없게 됐을 거요. 그래서 나는 오로지 의무감과 내 사회적 처지 때문에, 그 선한 순간을 억누르고 내 추악한 소임에 매달릴 수밖에 없었소. 누군가가 선한 것에 대한 공은 죄다 제 것으로 가로채고, 나한테는 추악한 것밖에 남겨 주지 않는 거요. 하지만 나는 한가로이 사기나 치며 사는 영예 따위는 부럽지 않소, 나는 야심이 없으니까. 어째서 나는, 온 세상 모든 피조물 가운데 하필이면, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 저주하고 심지어 발길질까지 당하도록 운명 지어진 거요, 내가 죽을 육신의 형상을 걸치면 이따금 그런 대가를 치를 수밖에 없으니 말이오? 물론 거기에 어떤 비밀이 있다는 건 나도 알지만, 그들은 무슨 일이 있어도 나한테 그 비밀을 일러 주지 않을 거요, 왜냐하면 내가 그 의미를 알게 되면 어쩌면 호산나를 목청껏 외쳐 댈 테고, 그러면 그 없어서는 안 될 마이너스가 당장 사라져 버려, 온 세상에 양식(良識)이 군림하게 될 테니까. 그러면 물론 만물의 종말을 뜻하게 되겠지, 잡지며 신문까지도 말이오, 그런 걸 누가 구독하려 들겠소? 만물의 끝에 가서는 나도 화해하게 되리라는 것을 나는 알고 있소. 나 역시 내 백경 킬로미터를 걷고 그 비밀을 알게 될 거요. 하지만 그렇게 되기까지 나는 뾰로통한 채로, 비위에 거슬리면서도 내 운명을 다하는 거요―그러니까, 하나를 구원하기 위해 수천을 파멸시키는 일 말이오. 옛날에 그들이 나를 그토록 우롱했던 그 의로운 사람 욥 하나를 위해, 얼마나 많은 영혼이 파멸되어야 했고 얼마나 많은 명예로운 평판이 무너져야 했던지! 그렇소, 그 비밀이 밝혀지기 전까지 나한테는 두 가지 진리가 있소―하나는 저쪽에 있는 그들의 진리로, 나로서는 아직 아무것도 모르는 것이고, 다른 하나는 나 자신의 진리요. 그리고 어느 쪽이 더 나은 걸로 드러날지는 알 수 없는 노릇이오…… 당신 잠들었소?\"\n\n\"잠들 만도 하지.\" 이반이 화난 듯 신음했다. \"내 어리석은 생각들―진작에 벗어났고, 오래전에 두들겨 부숴 송장처럼 내팽개쳐 버린 것들을―당신은 무슨 새로운 것인 양 나한테 들이미는군!\"\n\n\"당신 비위는 도무지 못 맞추겠소! 나는 내 문체로 당신을 매료시킬 줄 알았는데. 그 하늘의 호산나 대목은 정말이지 나쁘지 않았소, 안 그렇소? 그다음 그 하이네풍의 반어적인 어조는 또 어떻고, 응?\"\n\n\"천만에, 나는 결코 그런 종놈이었던 적이 없어! 그러니 어떻게 내 영혼이 당신 같은 종놈을 낳을 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"여보게, 나는 더없이 매력적이고 호감 가는 어느 젊은 러시아 신사를 하나 아는데, 젊은 사상가요 문학과 예술을 무척 사랑하는 자로, 〈대심문관〉이라는 제목의 장래가 촉망되는 서사시의 작가요. 나는 그저 그자를 생각하고 있었을 뿐이오!\"\n\n\"〈대심문관〉 얘긴 입에 올리지 마.\" 이반이 수치심으로 시뻘게져 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 〈지질학적 대변동〉은? 기억하오? 그건 정말 한 편의 시였지!\"\n\n\"입 닥쳐, 안 그러면 죽여 버릴 테다!\"\n\n\"나를 죽인다고? 천만에, 미안하지만, 나는 말하겠소. 나는 그 즐거움을 만끽하려고 찾아온 거니까. 오, 나는 삶을 향한 열망으로 부르르 떠는 내 열렬한 젊은 친구들의 꿈을 사랑하오! '새로운 인간들이 있다.' 당신은 작년 봄, 여기 오려고 마음먹었을 때 이렇게 단정했소, '그들은 모든 것을 파괴하고 식인(食人)부터 시작하자고 한다. 어리석은 자들 같으니! 그들은 내게 조언을 구하지도 않았다! 나는 주장한다, 아무것도 파괴할 필요가 없으며, 우리는 다만 인간 안의 신이라는 관념을 파괴하기만 하면 된다고, 그것이 바로 우리가 일에 착수해야 할 방식이라고. 바로 그것, 그것에서부터 시작해야 한다. 오, 아무런 이해도 없는 눈먼 인류여! 인간들이 모조리 신을 부정하게 되는 즉시―나는 지질학적 시대에 견줄 만한 그런 시대가 도래하리라 믿는다―우주에 대한 낡은 관념은 식인 없이도 저절로 무너질 것이며, 게다가 낡은 도덕까지도 무너질 것이고, 모든 것이 새로 시작될 것이다. 인간들은 삶이 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 거기서 취하려고 단결할 것이나, 그것은 오로지 현세에서의 기쁨과 행복만을 위해서다. 인간은 신적인, 거인 같은 자긍심으로 드높여질 것이며, 그리하여 인신(人神)이 출현할 것이다. 자신의 의지와 과학으로 자연에 대한 정복을 시시각각 무한히 넓혀 가면서, 인간은 그 일을 하며 시시각각 그토록 드높은 기쁨을 느낄 것이고, 그것이 천국의 기쁨에 대한 그의 모든 낡은 꿈을 보상해 줄 것이다. 누구나 자신이 죽을 존재임을 알 것이며, 신처럼 당당하고 평온하게 죽음을 받아들일 것이다. 그의 자긍심은 삶이 한순간임을 한탄해 봐야 부질없음을 그에게 가르칠 것이고, 그는 보답을 바랄 것도 없이 제 형제를 사랑할 것이다. 사랑은 삶의 한순간에만 족할 테지만, 바로 그 순간성의 의식이야말로 그 불길을 더욱 거세게 타오르게 할 것이니, 그 불길은 지금이야 무덤 너머 영원한 사랑의 꿈속에서 흩어져 버리고 있지만'…… 운운, 그런 식으로 계속되는 거요. 매력적이지!\"\n\n이반은 두 눈을 마룻바닥에 떨군 채 두 손으로 귀를 틀어막고 앉아 있었으나, 온몸을 부들부들 떨기 시작했다. 그 목소리는 계속됐다.\n\n\"이제 문제는, 하고 내 젊은 사상가가 곰곰이 생각했소, 과연 그런 시대가 도래할 수 있겠는가 하는 것이다. 만일 그렇게 된다면, 모든 것이 결판나고 인류는 영원히 안정되리라. 하지만 인간의 고질적인 어리석음 탓에 그것이 적어도 천 년 안에는 이루어질 수 없는 노릇이니, 지금이라도 그 진리를 깨달은 자라면 누구든 그 새로운 원칙에 따라 제멋대로 자기 삶을 꾸려도 정당하리라. 그런 의미에서 그에게는 '모든 것이 허용된다.' 더 나아가, 설령 그런 시대가 영영 도래하지 않는다 해도, 어차피 신도 없고 불멸도 없는 이상, 새로운 인간은 능히 인신이 될 수 있으니, 설령 그가 온 세상에 단 하나뿐인 인신이라 할지라도, 그 새로운 지위로 격상되어, 필요하다면 낡은 노예 인간의 낡은 도덕의 온갖 장벽을 가뿐히 넘어서도 좋으리라. 신에게는 법이 없다. 신이 서 있는 곳, 그곳이 곧 거룩한 곳이다. 내가 서는 곳이 당장 으뜸가는 곳이 되리라…… '모든 것이 허용된다', 그걸로 끝이다! 그거야 다 매력적이오. 하지만 사기를 치고 싶거든 무엇하러 거기에 도덕적 명분까지 필요하단 말이오? 한데 그게 바로 요즘 러시아인의 본색이오. 도덕적 명분 없이는 사기조차 칠 수가 없는 거지요. 그자는 진리를 그토록 사랑하니까……\"\n\n손님은 보아하니 제 능변에 도취된 듯 점점 더 목청을 높여 가며, 주인을 반어적인 눈길로 바라보면서 떠들어 댔다. 그러나 그는 말을 끝맺지 못했으니, 이반이 별안간 식탁에서 유리잔을 낚아채어 그 웅변가에게 내던졌던 것이다.\n\n\"아, 이거 영 어처구니없군(Ah, mais c'est bête enfin).\" 후자가 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 제 몸에 묻은 찻방울을 털어 내며 외쳤다. \"루터의 잉크병이 생각나는 모양이군! 나를 꿈으로 여기면서 그 꿈한테 유리잔을 던지다니! 꼭 여자 같소! 당신이 귀를 틀어막는 척만 하고 있다고 짐작은 했지.\"\n\n별안간 창문에서 요란하고 집요하게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 이반은 소파에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"들리오? 어서 열어 주는 게 좋겠소.\" 손님이 외쳤다. \"당신 동생 알료샤가 더없이 흥미롭고 놀라운 소식을 들고 온 거요, 내 장담하지!\"\n\n\"입 닥쳐, 이 사기꾼아, 알료샤라는 건 나도 알았어, 그 애가 오고 있다는 걸 느꼈다고, 물론 그 애가 헛걸음하러 온 건 아니겠지, 물론 '소식'을 들고 온 거겠지.\" 이반이 미친 듯이 외쳤다.\n\n\"열어 주시오, 그 애한테 문을 열어 주시오. 눈보라가 치는데 그 애는 당신 동생이잖소. 무슈, 지금 날씨가 어떤지 아시오? 이런 날씬 개도 밖에 내놓지 못할 지경이오(Monsieur sait-il le temps qu'il fait? C'est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors).\"\n\n두드리는 소리는 계속됐다. 이반은 창가로 달려가려 했으나, 무언가가 그의 팔다리를 옭아매는 것만 같았다. 그는 그 쇠사슬을 끊으려고 안간힘을 다했으나, 헛수고였다. 창문을 두드리는 소리는 점점 더 커졌다. 마침내 쇠사슬이 끊어지고 이반은 소파에서 벌떡 뛰어 일어났다. 그는 미친 듯이 둘레를 둘러보았다. 두 자루의 촛불은 거의 다 타 버렸고, 방금 손님에게 던진 유리잔이 식탁 위 제 앞에 놓여 있었으며, 맞은편 소파에는 아무도 없었다. 창틀을 두드리는 소리는 집요하게 계속됐으나, 결코 꿈속에서 들렸던 것만큼 요란하지는 않았다. 오히려 아주 잦아든 소리였다.\n\n\"꿈이 아니었어! 아니, 맹세컨대 꿈이 아니었어, 모든 게 방금 정말로 일어난 일이야!\" 이반이 외쳤다. 그는 창가로 달려가 여닫이 유리창을 열었다.\n\n\"알료샤, 오지 말라고 했잖아.\" 그가 동생에게 사납게 외쳤다. \"두 마디로, 무슨 일이야? 두 마디로, 알겠어?\"\n\n\"한 시간 전에 스메르댜코프가 목을 맸어요.\" 알료샤가 마당에서 대답했다.\n\n\"현관 계단 쪽으로 돌아와, 곧 열어 줄게.\" 이반은 이렇게 말하며 알료샤에게 문을 열어 주러 갔다.\n\n# Chapter X. “It Was He Who Said That”\n\n알료샤가 들어서며 한 시간 남짓 전에 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 자기 거처로 달려와 스메르댜코프가 스스로 목숨을 끊었다고 알려 왔다고 이반에게 말했다. \"사모바르를 치우러 들어갔더니 그이가 벽에 박힌 못에 매달려 있지 뭐예요.\" 경찰에는 알렸느냐고 알료샤가 묻자, 그녀는 아무에게도 말하지 않았다고, \"곧장 도련님께로 날아왔어요, 줄곧 뛰어왔어요\"라고 대답했다. 그녀는 완전히 제정신이 아닌 것 같았다고 알료샤는 전했고, 나뭇잎처럼 떨고 있었다는 것이다. 알료샤가 그녀와 함께 오두막으로 달려갔을 때 스메르댜코프는 아직도 매달려 있었다. 탁자 위에는 쪽지 한 장이 놓여 있었다. \"나는 누구에게도 죄를 씌우지 않으려고 내 의지와 바람에 따라 내 목숨을 끊는다.\" 알료샤는 쪽지를 탁자 위에 그대로 두고 곧장 경찰서장에게 가서 일의 자초지종을 다 이야기했다. \"그러고는 거기서 곧장 형한테로 온 거야.\" 알료샤는 이반의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 이야기를 맺었다. 그는 이야기를 하는 내내 형에게서 눈을 떼지 못했으니, 그 표정 속의 무언가에 충격을 받은 듯했다.\n\n\"형.\" 그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"형 정말 몹시 아픈 거 아냐. 보고 있으면서도 내 말을 알아듣지 못하는 것 같아.\"\n\n\"네가 와 줘서 다행이다.\" 이반은 무언가에 골똘히 잠긴 듯, 알료샤의 외침은 듣지도 못한 채 말했다. \"나는 그자가 목을 맨 걸 알고 있었어.\"\n\n\"누구한테서 들었는데?\"\n\n\"몰라. 그래도 알고 있었지. 내가 알았나? 그래, 그자가 말해 줬어. 방금 그렇게 말해 줬다고.\"\n\n이반은 방 한가운데 서서 여전히 같은 골똘한 어조로, 바닥을 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그자가 누군데?\" 알료샤가 저도 모르게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"빠져나가 버렸어.\"\n\n이반이 고개를 들고 부드럽게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그자는 너를 무서워했어, 너 같은 비둘기를 말이야. 너는 '순결한 천사'지. 드미트리가 너더러 천사라고 부르잖아. 천사라!… 천사들의 우레 같은 황홀경이라니. 천사가 대체 뭐냐? 어쩌면 별자리 하나일지도 모르지. 그렇지만 어쩌면 그 별자리가 화학 분자 하나에 지나지 않을지도. 사자자리와 태양자리라는 게 있지. 너 그거 모르냐?\"\n\n\"형, 좀 앉아.\" 알료샤가 놀라서 말했다. \"제발 좀, 소파에 앉으라니까! 형 헛소리를 하고 있어. 베개에 머리를 좀 대, 그래, 그렇게. 머리에 젖은 수건을 얹어 줄까? 그러면 좀 나아질지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"수건 좀 줘. 의자 위에 있어. 방금 내가 거기 던져 놨거든.\"\n\n\"여긴 없는데. 걱정 마. 어디 있는지 내가 알아, 여기 있네.\" 알료샤가 방 반대편 구석 이반의 화장대 옆에서, 개켜진 채 쓰지 않은 깨끗한 수건을 찾아내며 말했다. 이반은 그 수건을 기이한 눈빛으로 바라보았으니, 한순간 기억이 되돌아오는 듯했다.\n\n\"가만\"—그가 소파에서 일어났다—\"한 시간 전에 내가 저기서 저 새 수건을 가져다 적셨어. 그걸 머리에 둘렀다가 여기다 던져 놨다고… 그게 어떻게 말라 있지? 다른 건 없었는데.\"\n\n\"형이 그 수건을 머리에 얹었다고?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 한 시간 전에 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하면서 말이야… 양초가 왜 이렇게 다 타 버렸지? 지금 몇 시야?\"\n\n\"거의 열두 시야.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\" 이반이 갑자기 외쳤다. \"그건 꿈이 아니었어. 그자가 여기 있었어. 바로 여기, 저 소파에 앉아 있었다고. 네가 창문을 두드렸을 때 내가 그자한테 유리잔을 던졌어… 이거 말이야. 잠깐만. 지난번엔 내가 잠들어 있었는데, 이번 꿈은 꿈이 아니었어. 전에도 있었던 일이야. 나는 요즘 꿈을 꿔, 알료샤… 그런데 그건 꿈이 아니라 현실이야. 나는 돌아다니고, 말하고, 보고… 그러면서도 잠들어 있어. 그렇지만 그자는 여기 앉아 있었어, 바로 저기 저 소파에…. 그자는 끔찍하게 멍청해, 알료샤, 끔찍하게 멍청하다고.\" 이반이 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨리고는 방 안을 서성이기 시작했다.\n\n\"누가 멍청한데? 누구 얘기를 하는 거야, 형?\" 알료샤가 또다시 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"악마 말이야! 그자가 나를 찾아오기 시작했어. 두 번, 거의 세 번이나 다녀갔지. 그자가 천둥 번개 속에서 날개를 그을린 사탄이 아니라 한낱 평범한 악마라는 데 내가 화를 낸다고 나를 비웃더군. 하지만 그자는 사탄이 아니야. 그건 거짓말이지. 그자는 사기꾼이야. 그냥 악마, 보잘것없고 하찮은 악마라고. 그자는 목욕탕에도 가. 그자 옷을 벗겨 보면 틀림없이 꼬리가 달려 있을 거야, 덴마크 개 꼬리처럼 길고 매끈한, 한 야드는 되는, 우중충한 갈색 꼬리가 말이야…. 알료샤, 너 춥겠구나. 눈 속을 헤치고 왔으니. 차라도 좀 마실래? 뭐? 추워? 그 여자한테 좀 가져오라고 할까? 세 아 느 파 메트르 욍 시앵 드오르….\"\n\n알료샤는 세면대로 달려가 수건을 적시고는 이반을 다시 자리에 앉히도록 설득해, 젖은 수건을 그의 머리에 둘러 주었다. 그러고는 그 옆에 앉았다.\n\n\"아까 네가 리즈에 대해 무슨 얘기를 했었지?\" 이반이 다시 입을 열었다. (그는 몹시 수다스러워지고 있었다.) \"나는 리즈가 좋아. 그 애에 대해 못된 소리를 했지. 그건 거짓말이었어. 나는 그 애가 좋아… 나는 내일 카탸가 두려워. 그 무엇보다도 그 여자가 더 두렵다고. 앞일 때문이지. 그 여자는 내일 나를 내쳐 버리고 발밑에 짓밟을 거야. 내가 자기 때문에 질투에 사로잡혀 미탸를 파멸시키려 한다고 여기거든! 그래, 그렇게 생각한단 말이야! 하지만 그게 아냐. 내일은 십자가일 뿐, 교수대는 아니지. 아니, 나는 목을 매지 않을 거야. 알겠니, 나는 결코 자살 같은 건 할 수 없어, 알료샤. 그게 내가 비열해서일까? 나는 겁쟁이가 아니야. 삶에 대한 애착 때문일까? 내가 스메르댜코프가 목을 맸다는 걸 어떻게 알았겠어? 그래, 바로 그자가 나한테 말해 준 거야.\"\n\n\"그러니까 형은 여기 누군가가 있었다고 완전히 확신하는 거야?\" 알료샤가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 저기 구석 소파에 말이야. 너라면 그자를 쫓아냈겠지. 실제로 네가 쫓아냈잖아. 네가 오니까 그자가 사라져 버렸으니까. 나는 네 얼굴이 좋아, 알료샤. 내가 네 얼굴을 좋아하는 걸 너 알았니? 그리고 그자는 바로 나 자신이야, 알료샤. 내 안의 비열한 것, 천박하고 경멸스러운 것 전부 말이야. 그래, 나는 낭만주의자야. 그자가 그걸 알아챘지… 하지만 그건 중상모략이야. 그자는 끔찍하게 멍청해. 하지만 그게 그자한테는 유리하게 작용하지. 그자한테는 교활함이, 짐승 같은 교활함이 있어서, 어떻게 나를 격분하게 만드는지를 알았어. 그자는 내가 자기를 믿는다고 계속 비웃었는데, 그렇게 해서 나로 하여금 자기 말에 귀를 기울이게 만든 거야. 나를 어린애처럼 놀려 댔지. 그래도 나 자신에 관해 진실인 것도 잔뜩 말해 주긴 했어. 나라면 결코 스스로 인정하지 못했을 것들을 말이야. 알겠니, 알료샤.\" 이반이 더없이 진지하고 은밀한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그게 내가 아니라 바로 그자였다고 생각할 수만 있다면 나는 정말 더없이 기쁠 거야.\"\n\n\"형은 그자한테 시달려서 기진맥진해진 거야.\" 알료샤가 형을 가엾게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그자가 나를 놀려 댔어. 그런데 말이야, 그자는 어찌나 교묘하게, 어찌나 교묘하게 그러는지. '양심! 양심이 뭐냐? 나 스스로 지어내는 거지. 왜 내가 그것 때문에 고통받지? 습관 때문이야. 칠천 년 동안 이어진 인류의 보편적인 습관 때문이라고. 그러니 그런 건 내던져 버리자, 그러면 우리는 신이 될 거야.' 그자가 그렇게 말했어, 바로 그자가 그렇게 말했다고!\"\n\n\"형이 아니라, 형이 아니라?\" 알료샤가 형을 솔직하게 바라보며 외치지 않을 수 없었다. \"어쨌든 그자는 신경 쓰지 마. 그자랑은 끝장을 내고 잊어버려. 그리고 형이 지금 저주하는 모든 걸 그자가 다 짊어지고 가게 해, 다시는 돌아오지 못하게!\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그자는 악의에 차 있어. 나를 비웃었지. 뻔뻔스러웠어, 알료샤.\" 이반이 모욕감에 몸서리치며 말했다. \"그런데 그자는 나한테 부당했어, 많은 일에서 나한테 부당했다고. 내 면전에 대고 거짓말을 늘어놨어. '오, 너는 영웅적인 미덕의 행위를 하려는 거지. 네가 아버지를 죽였다고, 하인이 네 사주를 받아 죽였다고 자백하려는 거잖아.'\"\n\n\"형.\" 알료샤가 끼어들었다. \"정신 차려. 형이 아버지를 죽인 게 아니야. 그건 사실이 아니라고!\"\n\n\"그자가 하는 말이 바로 그거야, 그자가, 그러면서도 그자는 그걸 알고 있어. '너는 영웅적인 미덕의 행위를 하려고 하지만 미덕 따위는 믿지도 않아. 바로 그게 너를 괴롭히고 너를 화나게 하는 거고, 그래서 네가 그토록 앙심을 품는 거지.' 그자가 나에 관해 나한테 그렇게 말했고, 그자는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고 있어.\"\n\n\"그건 형이 하는 말이지, 그자가 아니야.\" 알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다. \"형이 그런 말을 하는 건 형이 아프고 헛소리를 하며 스스로를 괴롭히고 있기 때문이야.\"\n\n\"아니야, 그자는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아. '너는 자존심 때문에 가는 거야.' 그자가 말하지. '너는 떨치고 일어나서 내가 그를 죽였다고 말할 텐데, 그러면서 왜 공포에 몸부림치는 거지? 너는 거짓말을 하고 있어! 나는 너의 그 잘난 생각을 경멸해, 너의 그 공포를 경멸한다고!' 그자가 나에 관해 그렇게 말했어. '그리고 너 알아, 너는 그자들이 너를 칭송해 주기를 갈망하고 있어—\"그자는 범죄자다, 살인자다, 하지만 얼마나 너그러운 영혼인가. 동생을 구하려고 자백을 했으니\"라고 말이야.' 그건 거짓말이야, 알료샤!\" 이반이 눈을 번득이며 갑자기 외쳤다. \"나는 그 천한 무리들이 나를 칭송하는 걸 원하지 않아, 맹세코 원하지 않아! 그건 거짓말이야! 그래서 내가 그자한테 유리잔을 던졌고 그게 그자의 추악한 낯짝에 부딪혀 깨진 거라고.\"\n\n\"형, 진정해, 그만해!\" 알료샤가 그에게 간청했다.\n\n\"그래, 그자는 사람을 괴롭힐 줄 알아. 잔인하지.\" 이반이 아랑곳없이 말을 이었다. \"나는 처음부터 그자가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 어렴풋이 알아챘어. '네가 자존심 때문에 간다고 치자, 그래도 너는 스메르댜코프가 유죄 선고를 받아 시베리아로 보내지고 미탸는 무죄로 풀려나며, 너는 그저 도덕적 비난이라는 벌만 받기를 바라는 희망을 품고 있었지' ('알아듣겠냐?' 그자는 그러면서 웃었어)—'그리고 어떤 사람들은 너를 칭송할 거고. 하지만 이제 스메르댜코프는 죽었어, 목을 맸지, 그러니 너 혼자만의 말을 누가 믿겠어? 그런데도 너는 가고 있어, 가고 있어, 어쨌든 갈 거야, 가기로 마음먹었으니까. 그런데 이제 와서 뭐 하러 가는 거지?' 그건 끔찍한 일이야, 알료샤. 나는 그런 질문은 견딜 수가 없어. 누가 감히 나한테 그런 질문을 한단 말이야?\"\n\n\"형.\" 알료샤가 끼어들었다—공포로 가슴이 내려앉았지만, 그래도 이반을 이성으로 돌려놓을 수 있으리라는 희망을 여전히 품은 듯했다—\"아무도 모르고, 누구도 알아낼 시간이 없었는데, 내가 오기도 전에 그자가 어떻게 형한테 스메르댜코프의 죽음을 이야기할 수 있었겠어?\"\n\n\"그자가 말해 줬다니까.\" 이반이 의심의 여지를 인정하지 않으려 단호하게 말했다. \"따지고 보면 그자가 한 얘기라곤 온통 그것뿐이었어. '네가 미덕을 믿기만 한다면야 다 괜찮을 텐데 말이야.' 그자가 그러더군. '사람들이 너를 믿지 않는대도 상관없어, 너는 신념을 위해 가는 거니까. 하지만 너는 표도르 파블로비치 같은 한 마리 돼지 새끼인데, 미덕 따위가 너한테 무슨 소용이람? 네 희생이 누구에게도 쓸모가 없는데 왜 가서 참견하려 드는 거지? 너 자신조차 네가 왜 가는지 모르니까 그래! 오, 네가 왜 가는지를 스스로 알 수만 있다면 너는 무엇이든 다 내줄 텐데! 그런데 너 정말 마음을 정하기는 한 거야? 너는 마음을 정하지 못했어. 밤새도록 앉아서 갈까 말까 궁리만 하겠지. 하지만 너는 갈 거야. 네가 가리란 걸 너도 알아. 어느 쪽으로 결정하든 그 결정이 너한테 달려 있지 않다는 걸 너도 알지. 너는 갈 거야, 감히 가지 않을 엄두가 안 나니까. 왜 엄두가 안 나느냐고? 그건 네가 알아서 짐작해 봐. 너한테 던지는 수수께끼다!' 그자가 일어나서 가 버렸어. 네가 오니까 그자가 가 버린 거야. 그자가 나더러 겁쟁이라고 했어, 알료샤! 르 모 드 레니그므, 그 수수께끼의 답은 내가 겁쟁이라는 거야. '그런 독수리들이나 땅 위로 솟구쳐 날아오르는 거지.' 그자가 그 말을 덧붙였어—그자가! 그리고 스메르댜코프도 똑같은 말을 했어. 그자는 죽어 마땅해! 카탸는 나를 경멸해. 나는 한 달 전부터 그걸 알고 있었어. 리즈마저 나를 경멸하기 시작할 거야! '너는 칭송받으려고 가는 거다.' 그건 잔인한 거짓말이야! 그리고 너도 나를 경멸하잖아, 알료샤. 이제 나는 너를 또다시 미워하게 될 거야! 그리고 그 괴물도 미워해! 나는 그 괴물이 미워! 나는 그 괴물을 구하고 싶지 않아. 시베리아에서 썩어 버리라지! 그자는 찬송가나 부르기 시작했으니! 오, 내일 나는 가서, 그자들 앞에 서서, 그자들 낯짝에 침을 뱉어 줄 테다!\"\n\n그는 광란에 사로잡혀 벌떡 일어나더니 수건을 내던지고 다시금 방 안을 이리저리 서성이기 시작했다. 알료샤는 형이 방금 한 말을 떠올렸다. \"나는 깨어 있으면서 잠들어 있는 것 같아…. 나는 걷고, 말하고, 보지만, 잠들어 있어.\" 지금이 바로 그런 모양이었다. 알료샤는 형의 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 의사를 부르러 달려갈까 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤지만, 형을 혼자 두기가 두려웠다. 형을 맡길 만한 사람이 아무도 없었던 것이다. 마침내 이반은 차츰차츰 완전히 의식을 잃어 갔다. 그는 여전히 끊임없이 말을 하고 또 했지만, 도무지 두서가 없었고, 발음조차 힘겹게 했다. 갑자기 그가 몹시 비틀거렸다. 그러나 알료샤가 때맞춰 그를 부축했다. 이반은 그가 자기를 침대로 이끄는 대로 몸을 맡겼다. 알료샤는 그럭저럭 형의 옷을 벗겨 침대에 눕혔다. 그러고는 두 시간을 더 형을 지켜보며 앉아 있었다. 병자는 미동도 없이, 부드럽고 고르게 숨을 쉬며 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 알료샤는 베개 하나를 가져다 옷도 벗지 않은 채 소파에 누웠다.\n\n잠이 들면서 그는 미탸와 이반을 위해 기도했다. 그는 이반의 병을 차츰 이해하기 시작했다. \"오만한 결단이 빚어내는 고뇌. 진실한 양심!\" 그가 믿지 않던 하느님과 그분의 진리가, 여전히 굴복하기를 거부하는 그의 마음을 사로잡아 가고 있는 것이었다. \"그래.\" 베개를 벤 알료샤의 머릿속으로 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"그래, 스메르댜코프가 죽었으니 아무도 이반의 증언을 믿지 않겠지. 하지만 형은 가서 증언할 거야.\" 알료샤는 부드럽게 미소 지었다. \"하느님이 승리하실 거야!\" 그는 생각했다. \"형은 진리의 빛 속에 떨쳐 일어나거나, 아니면… 자기가 믿지 않는 대의를 위해 봉사했다는 것 때문에 자기 자신과 모든 사람에게 복수하면서 증오 속에서 스러져 가겠지.\" 알료샤는 씁쓸하게 덧붙이고는 다시금 이반을 위해 기도했다.\n\n# Book XII. A Judicial Error Chapter I. The Fatal Day\n\n내가 앞서 서술한 사건들이 있은 이튿날 아침 열 시, 드미트리 카라마조프의 재판이 우리 고을의 지방 법원에서 시작되었다.\n\n서둘러 밝혀 두거니와, 나는 재판에서 벌어진 일을 빠짐없이 세세하게, 아니 실제로 벌어진 순서 그대로 보고할 능력을 지녔다고는 결코 자부하지 않는다. 모든 것을 충분한 설명을 곁들여 언급하자면 한 권의 책, 그것도 아주 두꺼운 책으로도 모자라리라 짐작한다. 그러니 내가 인상 깊었던 대목에만 매달린다 하여 책망받지 않기를 바랄 뿐이다. 어쩌면 나는 부차적인 것을 가장 흥미로운 것으로 골라냈을 수도 있고, 가장 두드러지고 본질적인 세부들을 빠뜨렸을 수도 있다. 그러나 변명을 늘어놓지 않는 편이 나으리라는 것을 안다. 나는 최선을 다할 것이고, 독자는 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했음을 스스로 알게 될 것이다.\n\n그리고 우선, 법정에 들어서기에 앞서, 그날 나를 가장 놀라게 한 것부터 언급하고자 한다. 사실 나중에 드러난 바로는 누구나 그 점에 놀랐다. 우리는 이 사건이 큰 관심을 불러일으켰다는 것을, 누구나 재판이 시작되기를 애타게 고대하고 있다는 것을, 지난 두 달 동안 그것이 우리 고을 사교계에서 화제와 억측과 탄성과 추측의 소재가 되어 왔다는 것을 모두 알고 있었다. 또한 이 사건이 러시아 전역에 알려졌다는 것도 누구나 알았으나, 그것이 그토록 뜨겁고 그토록 격렬한 관심을, 그것도 우리뿐 아니라 러시아 전역에 걸쳐 누구에게나 불러일으켰을 줄은 미처 상상하지 못했다. 그것이 바로 이날 재판에서 분명하게 드러났다.\n\n방청객들은 우리 도의 도청 소재지뿐 아니라 다른 여러 러시아 도시에서, 게다가 모스크바와 페테르부르크에서도 몰려왔다. 그들 가운데에는 법률가들과 귀부인들, 심지어 몇몇 저명한 인사들도 끼어 있었다. 입장권은 한 장도 남김없이 채여 나갔다. 세 명의 판사가 앉는 탁자 뒤편에는 가장 저명하고 중요한 남자 방청객들을 위한 특별석이 따로 마련되었으니, 그곳에는 안락의자 한 줄이 놓였는데, 이는 전례 없는 이례적인 조치로서 예전에는 결코 허용된 적이 없는 일이었다. 방청객 가운데 상당수가, 적어도 절반은 귀부인이었다. 각지에서 몰려온 법률가들이 어찌나 많았던지 그들을 어디에 앉혀야 할지 몰랐으니, 입장권은 이미 오래전에 다투어 신청되고 배분된 터였다. 나는 법정 끝, 단상 뒤편에 급히 세워진 특별 칸막이를 보았는데, 그 뒤로 이 모든 법률가들이 들어가도록 허락되었고, 그들은 거기 서 있을 자리만이라도 얻은 것을 다행으로 여겼으니, 공간을 마련하느라 의자가 죄다 치워진 까닭에 칸막이 뒤의 무리는 재판 내내 어깨를 맞댄 채 빽빽이 들어차 서 있었다.\n\n귀부인들 가운데 일부는, 특히 먼 곳에서 온 이들은 회랑에 아주 맵시 있게 차려입고 모습을 드러냈으나, 대다수 귀부인은 옷차림 따위는 안중에도 없었다. 그들의 얼굴에는 발작적이고 격렬하며 거의 병적이기까지 한 호기심이 드러나 있었다. 한 가지 기묘한 사실은—나중에 여러 차례 관찰로 확인된 것이지만—거의 모든 귀부인이, 적어도 그 압도적 다수가 미탸 편이었고 그가 무죄로 풀려나기를 바랐다는 점이다. 이는 어쩌면 그가 여인의 마음을 사로잡는 정복자로 이름이 났던 탓이 가장 클 것이다. 두 명의 연적이 이 사건에 등장하리라는 것은 알려진 바였다. 그중 한 사람인 카테리나 이바노브나는 세간의 관심의 표적이었다. 그녀에 관해서는 온갖 기이한 이야기가, 그가 저지른 범죄에도 불구하고 미탸를 향한 그녀의 열정에 얽힌 놀라운 일화들이 떠돌았다. 사람들은 특히 그녀의 자존심과 \"귀족 가문의 연줄\"을 강조했는데(그녀는 고을에서 거의 누구도 찾아가 인사한 적이 없었다), 그녀가 정부에 청원을 넣어 그 죄인을 따라 시베리아로 가서 어느 광산에서든 그와 결혼할 작정이라고들 했다. 그루셴카가 법정에 나타나기를 기다리는 마음도 그에 못지않게 조바심으로 가득했다. 방청객들은 두 연적의 대면을, 곧 그 도도한 귀족 처녀와 \"그 화류계 여자\"의 맞닥뜨림을 애타는 호기심으로 고대하고 있었다. 그러나 그루셴카는 카테리나 이바노브나보다 이 고을 귀부인들에게 한결 낯익은 인물이었다. 그들은 이미 \"표도르 파블로비치와 그 불행한 아들을 파멸시킨 여자\"를 본 적이 있었고, 거의 예외 없이 모두가, 어떻게 아버지와 아들이 \"예쁘지도 않은 그토록 흔하디흔한 평범한 러시아 처녀\"를 그토록 사랑할 수 있었는지 의아해했다.\n\n요컨대 말이 많았다. 나는 우리 고을에서 미탸 때문에 여러 차례 심각한 집안싸움이 벌어졌다는 것을 사실로 알고 있다. 적잖은 귀부인이 이 끔찍한 사건을 두고 의견이 엇갈려 남편들과 격렬하게 다투었으니, 이런 부인의 남편들이 죄수에게 호의를 품기는커녕 도리어 그에게 모진 편견을 품고 법정에 들어선 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다. 사실상 청중 가운데 여성과 구별되는 남성 쪽은 대체로 죄수에게 적대적이었다고 거의 단언할 수 있다. 엄하고 찌푸린, 심지어 앙심에 찬 얼굴이 수두룩했다. 실로 미탸는 고을에 머무는 동안 많은 이들의 비위를 거슬러 놓은 터였다. 물론 방청객들 가운데에는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 기분으로 미탸 개인의 운명 따위에는 전혀 무관심한 이들도 있었다. 그러나 모두가 재판에 흥미를 느꼈고, 남자들 대다수는 분명 죄인이 유죄 판결을 받기를 바라고 있었으니, 다만 법률가들만은 예외였을 텐데, 이들은 사건의 도덕적 측면보다 법률적 측면에 더 관심이 쏠려 있었다.\n\n모두가 저명한 변호사 페튜코비치의 등장에 술렁였다. 그의 재능은 익히 알려져 있었고, 그가 지방에서 세상을 떠들썩하게 한 형사 사건을 변호하는 것은 이번이 처음도 아니었다. 그리고 그가 변호를 맡으면 그런 사건은 명성을 얻어 러시아 전역에서 오래도록 기억되곤 했다. 우리 고을 검사와 재판장에 관한 이야기도 떠돌았다. 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 페튜코비치와 마주하게 되어 떨고 있다는 말이 있었고, 두 사람은 페테르부르크에서 경력을 쌓기 시작한 이래로 줄곧 원수지간이었다는 것이다. 또한 우리 고을의 예민한 검사는, 자기 재능이 제대로 인정받지 못했다 하여 페테르부르크의 누군가에게 늘 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여겨 왔으나, 이 카라마조프 사건에 자못 흥분해 있었고 심지어 이 사건을 발판 삼아 기울어 가는 자기 운명을 다시 일으켜 세우는 꿈까지 꾸고 있었는데, 페튜코비치야말로 그의 유일한 근심거리라고들 했다. 그러나 이런 소문은 그다지 정확하지 못했다. 우리 검사는 위험 앞에서 기가 꺾이는 그런 부류의 인간이 아니었다. 오히려 위험이 커질수록 그의 자신감도 커졌다. 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 우리 검사가 대체로 지나치게 성급하고 병적으로 예민한 사람이었다는 점이다. 그는 어떤 사건에 자기 온 영혼을 쏟아부어, 마치 자기 운명 전체와 신상 전체가 그 결과에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 매달리곤 했다. 이는 법조계에서 약간의 웃음거리가 되었으니, 바로 이런 기질 덕분에 우리 검사는 그 변변찮은 지위에서 기대할 수 있는 것보다 더 널리 이름을 떨치게 되었던 것이다. 사람들은 특히 그의 심리학에 대한 열정을 두고 비웃었다. 내 생각에 그들이 틀렸으니, 우리 검사는 세간에서 짐작하는 것보다 한층 깊이 있는 인물이었다고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 그는 허약한 건강 탓에 경력의 출발에서 두각을 나타내지 못했고, 나중에도 끝내 그것을 만회하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n우리 고을 재판장으로 말하자면, 다만 그가 인간적이고 교양 있는 사람으로서 자기 일에 실무적인 지식을 갖추고 진보적인 견해를 지녔다고만 말할 수 있겠다. 그는 다소 야심이 있었으나 자기 앞날의 출세 따위에는 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그의 인생의 큰 목표는 진보적 사상을 지닌 사람이 되는 것이었다. 그는 또한 연줄과 재산을 갖춘 사람이었다. 우리가 나중에 알게 된 바로는 그는 카라마조프 사건에 대해 자못 강한 감정을 품고 있었으나, 그것은 개인적 관점에서가 아니라 사회적 관점에서였다. 그는 이 사건을 하나의 사회 현상으로서, 그 분류와 우리 사회 조건의 산물로서의 성격, 또 민족성의 전형으로서 등등 그런 식으로 흥미롭게 여겼다. 사건의 개인적 측면에 대해서는, 그 비극적 의미와 죄수를 포함해 거기 얽힌 인물들에 대해서는 다소 무관심하고 추상적인 태도를 지녔으니, 실로 어쩌면 그것이 마땅한 일이었을 것이다.\n\n판사들이 모습을 드러내기 한참 전부터 법정은 빈틈없이 들어차 넘쳐흘렀다. 우리 고을 법정은 고을에서 가장 좋은 홀로, 널찍하고 천장이 높으며 소리가 잘 울렸다. 높은 단상에 자리한 판사들의 오른편에는 배심원을 위한 탁자 하나와 의자 두 줄이 마련되어 있었다. 왼편은 죄수와 변호인의 자리였다. 법정 한가운데 판사들 가까이에는 \"물적 증거\"를 올려놓은 탁자가 있었다. 그 위에는 표도르 파블로비치의 피 묻은 흰 비단 실내복, 추정컨대 살인이 저질러졌다는 그 운명의 놋쇠 공이, 소맷자락에 피가 묻은 미탸의 셔츠, 손수건을 넣어 두었던 호주머니 위로 군데군데 피가 얼룩진 그의 외투, 피로 빳빳하게 굳어 이제는 누렇게 변해 버린 그 손수건 자체, 미탸가 자살할 작정으로 페르호틴의 집에서 장전했다가 모크로예에서 트리폰 보리소비치에게 몰래 빼앗긴 권총, 그루셴카에게 주려고 삼천 루블을 넣어 두었던 봉투와 그것을 묶었던 가느다란 분홍 리본, 그 밖에 내가 기억하지 못하는 여러 물건이 놓여 있었다. 홀의 본체에는, 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에 방청객 좌석이 있었다. 그러나 난간 앞쪽에는 증언을 마친 뒤에도 법정에 남은 증인들을 위해 의자 몇 개가 놓여 있었다.\n\n열 시에 세 명의 판사가 도착했으니, 재판장과 명예 치안판사 한 명, 그리고 또 한 명이었다. 물론 검사는 그 직후에 들어왔다. 재판장은 키가 작고 통통하며 다부진 쉰 살가량의 사내로, 위장이 나쁜 듯한 안색에 희끗희끗해진 검은 머리를 짧게 깎았고, 무슨 훈장인지 기억나지 않는 붉은 리본을 달고 있었다. 검사는 나에게도, 다른 이들에게도 유난히 창백해 보였으니, 거의 새파랗다 할 정도였다. 그의 얼굴은 갑자기, 어쩌면 단 하룻밤 사이에 부쩍 야위어 버린 듯했으니, 이틀 전만 해도 나는 그가 평소와 다름없는 모습인 것을 보았던 것이다. 재판장은 배심원이 모두 출석했는지를 법정에 묻는 것으로 시작했다.\n\n그러나 나는 더 이상 이런 식으로 이어 갈 수 없음을 깨닫는다. 한편으로는 내가 듣지 못한 것도, 알아채지 못한 것도, 잊어버린 것도 있기 때문이고, 무엇보다 앞서 말했듯이 거기서 오간 말과 행동을 죄다 언급할 시간도 지면도 정말이지 없기 때문이다. 다만 내가 아는 것은, 어느 쪽도 배심원 다수에 대해서는 별다른 이의를 제기하지 않았다는 사실이다. 나는 열두 명의 배심원을 기억하니, 넷은 고을의 하급 관리였고, 둘은 상인, 여섯은 고을의 농민과 직공이었다. 재판이 시작되기 한참 전부터, 특히 귀부인들 사이에서 약간 놀란 듯한 물음이 끊임없이 던져지던 것을 나는 기억한다. \"이토록 미묘하고 복잡한 심리적 사건을 하급 관리들, 심지어 농민들의 판단에 맡길 수 있단 말인가?\" 그리고 \"관리가, 더구나 농민이 이런 사건에서 무엇을 이해할 수 있겠는가?\" 하는 것이었다. 사실 배심원에 든 네 명의 관리는 죄다 보잘것없고 직급이 낮은 자들이었다. 그중 비교적 젊은 한 명을 빼면 모두 백발이 성성한 사내들로, 사교계에는 거의 알려지지 않았고, 쥐꼬리만 한 봉급으로 근근이 연명해 왔으며, 십중팔구는 나이 들고 사람들 앞에 내놓기 민망한 아내와, 어쩌면 신발도 양말도 없는 한 떼의 아이들을 거느리고 있을 터였다. 여가가 나면 기껏해야 카드놀이로 시간을 보낼 뿐, 물론 책이라곤 단 한 권도 읽어 본 적이 없었다. 두 상인은 점잖아 보였으나 묘하게 말이 없고 무뚝뚝했다. 그중 한 사람은 수염을 말끔히 깎고 유럽풍으로 차려입었고, 다른 한 사람은 희끗한 잔수염을 길렀으며 목에 무슨 메달인지가 달린 붉은 리본을 두르고 있었다. 직공과 농민에 대해서는 말할 필요도 없다. 스코토프리고니옙스크의 직공이란 거의 농민이나 다름없고, 더러는 땅을 부치기까지 한다. 그중 둘 또한 유럽풍으로 차려입었는데, 어쩌면 그 때문에 다른 이들보다 더 꾀죄죄하고 더 볼품없어 보였다. 그러니 내가 그들을 보자마자 그러했듯, \"저런 사내들이 대체 이런 사건을 어찌 이해할 수 있겠는가?\" 하고 의아해할 만도 했다. 그런데도 그들의 얼굴은 묘하게 위엄 있는, 거의 위협적이기까지 한 인상을 풍겼으니, 그들은 엄숙하고 잔뜩 찌푸린 표정이었다.\n\n마침내 재판장이 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 살해 사건의 심리를 개시했다. 그가 그를 어떻게 묘사했는지는 잘 기억나지 않는다. 정리(廷吏)에게 죄수를 데려오라는 지시가 내려졌고, 미탸가 모습을 드러냈다. 법정에 정적이 흘렀다. 파리 한 마리가 날아도 들릴 만했다. 다른 이들은 어떠했는지 모르겠으나, 미탸는 나에게 더없이 좋지 못한 인상을 주었다. 그는 새것이 분명한 프록코트 차림으로 굉장한 멋쟁이처럼 보였다. 나중에 들으니 그는 이 자리를 위해 일부러 모스크바에서, 제 치수를 아는 단골 재단사에게 그것을 주문했다고 했다. 그는 흠 하나 없는 검은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 빼어난 아마포 옷을 받쳐 입고 있었다. 그는 한 발에 일 야드는 됨 직한 성큼성큼한 걸음으로 들어와, 정면을 뻣뻣하게 똑바로 응시한 채 더없이 태연한 태도로 제자리에 앉았다.\n\n바로 그 순간 변호인인 저 저명한 페튜코비치가 들어섰고, 일종의 나직한 술렁임이 법정을 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 키가 크고 깡마른 사내로, 길고 가는 다리에, 유난히 길고 가늘고 핏기 없는 손가락을 지녔으며, 말끔히 면도한 얼굴에, 얌전히 빗어 넘긴 다소 짧은 머리, 그리고 때때로 비웃음과 미소의 중간쯤 되는 무엇인가로 휘어지는 얇은 입술의 소유자였다. 마흔쯤 되어 보였다. 그의 얼굴은 그 눈만 아니었더라면 호감을 주었을 것이니, 그 눈은 그 자체로 작고 무표정한 데다 놀라우리만치 서로 바짝 붙어 있어, 가늘고 긴 코만이 둘 사이의 경계선 노릇을 할 뿐이었다. 사실 그의 얼굴에는 어딘가 새를 빼닮은 데가 두드러졌다. 그는 야회복에 흰 넥타이 차림이었다.\n\n나는 재판장이 미탸에게 던진 첫 질문들을, 그의 이름과 직업 따위에 관한 것이었음을 기억한다. 미탸는 또렷하게 대답했는데, 그 목소리가 어찌나 뜻밖에 우렁찼던지 재판장이 흠칫 놀라며 의아한 눈빛으로 죄수를 바라볼 정도였다. 그다음으로는 심리에 참여할 사람들의, 곧 증인과 감정인의 명단이 이어졌다. 긴 명단이었다. 증인 가운데 네 사람은 출석하지 않았으니, 예심에서 진술했으나 지금은 파리에 가 있는 미우소프, 병환으로 불참한 호흘라코바 부인과 막시모프, 그리고 급작스러운 죽음으로 불참한 스메르댜코프였는데, 그에 관해서는 경찰의 공식 진술서가 제출되었다. 스메르댜코프의 죽음 소식은 법정에 돌연 동요와 수군거림을 불러일으켰다. 물론 방청객 가운데 많은 이들이 이 갑작스러운 자살에 대해 듣지 못했던 것이다. 사람들을 가장 놀라게 한 것은 미탸의 돌연한 격발이었다. 스메르댜코프의 죽음에 관한 진술서가 낭독되자마자 그는 제자리에서 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"개 같은 놈이 개처럼 뒈진 거지!\"\n\n나는 그의 변호인이 그에게 달려가던 모습을, 그리고 재판장이 만약 그런 무례한 행동이 되풀이된다면 엄중한 조치를 취하겠다고 위협하며 그에게 이르던 것을 기억한다. 미탸는 고개를 끄덕이며 후회하는 기색이라곤 없이 나직한 목소리로 변호인을 향해 여러 번 무뚝뚝하게 되뇌었다.\n\n\"다시는 안 그러겠소, 안 그래요. 그만 입에서 새 나간 거요. 다시는 안 그러겠소.\"\n\n그리고 물론 이 짧은 소동은 배심원에게나 방청객에게나 그에게 아무런 득이 되지 못했다. 그의 성격이 고스란히 드러났고, 그것은 스스로 말해 주고 있었다. 이 사건의 영향이 가시지 않은 가운데 공소장이 낭독되었다. 그것은 다소 짧았으나 정황을 빠짐없이 담고 있었다. 거기에는 다만 그가 체포된 주된 이유와 그가 재판에 부쳐져야 하는 까닭 따위만이 적혀 있었다. 그런데도 그것은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 서기는 그것을 크고 또렷한 목소리로 낭독했다. 비극 전체가 응축되어, 또렷한 윤곽을 띤 채, 운명적이고 가차 없는 빛 속에서 돌연 우리 앞에 펼쳐졌다. 나는 그것이 낭독되자마자 재판장이 크고 인상적인 목소리로 미탸에게 묻던 것을 기억한다.\n\n\"피고는 유죄를 인정하는가?\"\n\n미탸가 돌연 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"나는 술과 방탕에 대해서는 유죄를 인정하오.\" 그는 또다시 깜짝 놀랄 만한, 거의 광란에 가까운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"나태와 방종에 대해서도 인정하오. 나는 운명의 일격을 받아 쓰러진 바로 그 순간, 영영 정직한 사람이 되려고 마음먹은 참이었소. 그러나 그 노인의, 내 원수이자 내 아버지인 그자의 죽음에 대해서는 나는 죄가 없소. 아니, 아니오, 나는 그를 턴 죄도 없소! 나는 그럴 수 없었소. 드미트리 카라마조프는 비열한 놈이지만 도둑은 아니오.\"\n\n그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 온몸이 눈에 띄게 떨리고 있었다. 재판장은 또다시 짤막하지만 인상 깊게, 묻는 말에만 대답하고 부질없는 외침으로 빠지지 말라고 그를 타일렀다. 그러고는 심리를 진행하라고 명했다. 모든 증인이 선서를 위해 앞으로 인도되었다. 그제야 나는 그들 모두를 한자리에서 보았다. 다만 죄수의 형제들은 선서 없이 증언하도록 허락받았다. 사제와 재판장의 훈계가 있은 뒤, 증인들은 따로 인도되어 되도록 서로 멀찍이 떨어져 앉도록 조치되었다. 그러고 나서 그들을 한 사람씩 불러내기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter II. Dangerous Witnesses\n\n피고 측 증인과 검찰 측 증인이 재판장의 손으로 무리 지어 갈라졌는지, 또 일정한 순서를 정해 호명하도록 짜여 있었는지 나로서는 알 수 없다. 그러나 분명 그렇게 되어 있었을 것이다. 다만 내가 아는 것은 검찰 측 증인들이 먼저 호명되었다는 사실뿐이다. 거듭 말하거니와, 나는 모든 신문(訊問) 과정을 하나하나 차례대로 기술할 생각은 없다. 게다가 내 설명은 어느 정도 군더더기가 될 터이니, 검찰 측과 피고 측의 논고와 변론에서 증거의 전 과정이 한데 모여 강렬하고 의미심장한 빛 속에 놓이게 되었기 때문이며, 나는 그 두 편의 주목할 만한 변설을 부분적으로 빠짐없이 받아 적어 두었기에 적절한 대목에서 인용하려 하는데, 최후 변론 직전에 일어나 이 재판의 음울하고 치명적인 결말에 의심할 바 없이 영향을 끼친 어느 기이하고 전혀 예기치 못한 사건도 함께 옮길 것이다.\n\n다만 나는 재판이 시작된 첫 순간부터 이 사건의 한 가지 독특한 특징이 두드러져 모두의 눈에 들어왔다는 점만은 짚어 두려 하는데, 그것은 곧 피고 측이 의지할 수 있는 논거에 비해 검찰 측의 위력이 압도적이었다는 사실이다. 사실들이 한 점을 중심으로 모여들기 시작하고 그 끔찍하고 피비린내 나는 범죄 전모가 차츰 드러나는 첫 순간부터 모두가 그것을 깨달았다. 어쩌면 모두가 처음부터 이 사건은 논쟁의 여지가 없으며, 의심할 것이 없고, 실로 토론할 거리가 없으며, 변론이란 형식에 지나지 않고, 피고는 유죄, 명백하고도 결정적으로 유죄라고 느꼈을 것이다. 짐작건대 그토록 안달하며 이 흥미로운 피고의 무죄 방면을 갈망하던 부인네들조차도, 동시에 예외 없이 그의 죄를 확신하고 있었으리라. 더욱이 나는 그들이, 만약 그의 죄가 그토록 확고하게 입증되지 않았더라면 도리어 분개했으리라고 믿는데, 그렇게 되면 죄인의 무죄 방면이라는 마지막 장면의 효과가 줄어들었을 것이기 때문이다. 그가 무죄로 풀려나리라는 것을, 이상한 말이지만, 모든 부인네들은 바로 마지막 순간까지 굳게 믿고 있었다. \"그는 유죄지만, 인도주의적 동기에서, 유행하게 된 새로운 사상과 새로운 정서에 따라 무죄로 풀려날 거예요.\" 이런 식으로 그들은 끝없이 떠들어 댔다. 그래서 그들은 그토록 안달하며 법정으로 몰려들었던 것이다. 남자들은 검사와 저명한 페튜코비치의 대결에 더 큰 관심을 기울였다. 모두가 페튜코비치 같은 재능이라 한들 이토록 절망적인 사건을 두고 무엇을 해낼 수 있을지 의아해하며 스스로 묻고 있었으니, 그래서 그들은 정신을 집중하여 그의 수완을 한 걸음 한 걸음 좇았다.\n\n그러나 페튜코비치는 끝까지, 그의 변론에 이르기까지 모두에게 수수께끼로 남아 있었다. 경험 많은 이들은 그가 무슨 복안을 품고 있으며, 어떤 목표를 향해 일을 꾸미고 있다고 짐작했으나, 그것이 무엇인지 알아맞히기란 거의 불가능했다. 그러나 그의 확신과 자신감만큼은 누가 봐도 분명했다. 게다가 모두가 흐뭇하게 알아챈 것은, 그가 우리 고장에 머문 지 기껏해야 사흘 남짓밖에 안 되는 그토록 짧은 기간 만에 놀랍도록 사건을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 \"세세한 부분까지 빈틈없이 연구해 두었다\"는 사실이었다. 사람들은 훗날 그가 검찰 측 증인들을 얼마나 영리하게 \"코를 납작하게\" 만들고, 가능한 한 그들을 당황시켰으며, 더욱이 그들의 평판에 먹칠을 하여 증언의 가치를 떨어뜨렸는지 신이 나서 묘사하곤 했다. 그러나 사람들은 그가 이런 짓을 말하자면 일종의 유희 삼아, 직업적 영예를 위해, 통상적인 방법에서 빠뜨린 것이 하나도 없음을 보이려고 한 것이라 짐작했으니, 모두가 그런 증인 깎아내리기로는 실질적인 득을 볼 수 없다고 확신했고, 그 자신도 누구보다 잘 알고 있을 것이며, 배후에 그만의 어떤 복안, 곧 때가 되면 불쑥 드러낼 어떤 감추어진 변호의 무기를 지니고 있으리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 그동안 그는 자기 힘을 의식한 채 그저 즐기고 있는 듯 보였다.\n\n이를테면 표도르 파블로비치의 늙은 하인으로서 열린 문에 관해 가장 결정적인 증언을 한 그리고리가 신문을 받을 때, 자기 차례가 되어 질문할 때가 되자 변호인은 그를 그야말로 물고 늘어졌다. 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그리고리가 침착하고 거의 위엄에 찬 태도로 법정에 들어섰으며, 법정의 위엄이나 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 방대한 청중에 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다는 점이다. 그는 마치 자기 아내 마르파와 이야기하듯, 다만 어쩌면 조금 더 공손하게, 자신만만하게 증언했다. 그를 모순에 빠뜨리기란 불가능했다. 검사는 먼저 카라마조프 집안의 가정생활에 관해 세세하게 그를 신문했다. 그 가정의 모습이 음산한 빛깔로 또렷이 드러났다. 이 증인이 꾸밈없고 공정하다는 것이 귀로 들어도 눈으로 보아도 분명했다. 그는 작고한 주인의 기억에 깊은 경의를 품고 있었음에도, 주인이 미탸에게 부당했으며 \"마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 자식들을 키우지 않았다\"고 증언했다. \"도련님이 어렸을 적, 제가 없었더라면 이가 들끓어 잡아먹혔을 겁니다.\" 그는 미탸의 어린 시절을 묘사하며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 아버지가 권리상 마땅히 아들 것이었던 어미의 재산을 두고 아들에게 부당하게 군 것도 옳지 못한 일이었지요.\"\n\n표도르 파블로비치가 금전 관계에서 아들에게 부당하게 굴었다고 단언하는 근거가 무엇이냐는 검사의 물음에, 그리고리는 모두를 놀라게도 내놓을 만한 증거라곤 하나도 없었으나, 그래도 아들과의 재산 처리가 \"공정치 못했고\" 주인이 \"수천 루블은 더 주었어야 했다\"고 고집스레 주장했다. 곁들여 짚어 둘 것은, 검사가 표도르 파블로비치가 정말로 미탸의 유산 일부를 떼어먹었는지를 묻는 이 질문을, 알료샤와 이반까지 빼놓지 않고 물어볼 수 있는 모든 증인에게 두드러진 집요함으로 던졌으나, 누구에게서도 정확한 정보를 얻지 못했다는 사실이다. 모두가 그렇다고 주장은 했지만, 누구도 뚜렷한 증거 하나 내놓지 못했다. 그리고리가 묘사한 만찬 식탁에서의 장면, 곧 드미트리가 쳐들어와 아버지를 때리고 다시 와서 죽이겠노라 위협하던 그 장면은 법정에 불길한 인상을 남겼으니, 특히 그것을 이야기하는 늙은 하인의 침착함과 말의 인색함, 독특한 말투가 웅변 못지않게 효과적이었기 때문이다. 그는 미탸가 자기를 때려눕히고 얼굴을 친 것을 두고 화나지 않았으며, 이미 오래전에 용서했노라고 말했다. 작고한 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 성호를 그으며, 능력은 있는 녀석이었으나 어리석고 병약했고, 더 나쁘게는 신을 믿지 않는 자였으며, 그렇게 만든 것은 표도르 파블로비치와 그 맏아들이라고 말했다. 그러나 그는 스메르댜코프의 정직함만큼은 거의 열을 내며 변호했으니, 언젠가 스메르댜코프가 마당에서 주인의 돈을 발견했을 때 그것을 감추기는커녕 주인에게 가져갔고, 주인은 그 대가로 \"금화 한 닢\"을 상으로 주고 그때부터 그를 전적으로 신임하게 되었다는 이야기를 들려주었다. 그는 정원으로 통하는 문이 열려 있었노라고 고집스레 주장했다. 그러나 그에게 던져진 질문이 어찌나 많았던지 나로서는 그것을 다 떠올릴 수가 없다.\n\n마침내 변호인이 그를 반대신문하기 시작했는데, 그가 처음 던진 질문은 표도르 파블로비치가 \"어떤 사람\"을 위해 삼천 루블을 넣어 두었다는 그 봉투에 관한 것이었다. \"그토록 오랜 세월 주인을 가까이 모셔 온 당신은, 그것을 본 적이 있습니까?\" 그리고리는 그것을 본 적이 없으며 \"모두가 그 말을 떠들기 전까지는\" 누구에게서도 그 돈에 관해 들어 본 적이 없다고 대답했다. 봉투에 관한 이 질문을, 페튜코비치는 검사가 드미트리의 유산에 관해 캐묻던 것만큼이나 집요하게, 그것을 알 만한 모든 사람에게 던졌고, 모두에게서 똑같은 답을 얻었으니, 곧 많은 이가 그에 관해 듣기는 했으나 봉투를 본 사람은 아무도 없다는 것이었다. 처음부터 모두가 이 문제에 대한 페튜코비치의 집요함을 알아챘다.\n\n\"자, 허락하신다면 한 가지 묻겠습니다.\" 페튜코비치가 불쑥 뜻밖의 말을 꺼냈다. \"그날 저녁, 예심 조서에서 알 수 있듯, 당신이 요통을 고쳐 볼까 하여 문지르는 데 썼다는 그 향유, 아니 정확히는 그 달인 물은 무엇으로 만든 것입니까?\"\n\n그리고리는 멍하니 질문자를 바라보다가, 잠시 침묵한 끝에 중얼거렸다. \"사프란이 들어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"사프란뿐이오? 다른 재료는 기억나지 않소?\"\n\n\"톱풀도 들어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 후추도 들어 있었던 것 아니오?\" 페튜코비치가 캐물었다.\n\n\"예, 후추도 들어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"기타 등등. 그리고 그 모든 것을 보드카에 녹였소?\"\n\n\"독한 술에 녹였습니다.\"\n법정에 희미한 웃음소리가 일었다.\n\n\"그렇군요, 독한 술에 말이지요. 등을 문지른 뒤에는, 내가 알기로, 당신 아내만 아는 어떤 경건한 기도를 외고서 병에 남은 것을 마셨다지요?\"\n\n\"마셨습니다.\"\n\n\"많이 마셨소? 대충 말해서, 포도주 잔으로 한두 잔쯤?\"\n\n\"큰 잔으로 가득 한 잔쯤 됐을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"큰 잔으로 가득 한 잔이라. 어쩌면 한 잔 반쯤?\"\n\n그리고리는 대답하지 않았다. 무슨 뜻인지 알아챈 듯했다.\n\n\"독한 술을 그대로 한 잔 반이면—제법 적지 않은 양이지요, 그렇지 않소? 정원으로 통하는 문이 아니라 천국의 문이 열리는 것을 보았을 수도 있지 않겠소?\"\n\n그리고리는 잠자코 있었다. 법정에 또 한 차례 웃음이 일었다. 재판장이 몸을 움직였다.\n\n\"당신이 열린 문을 보았을 때 깨어 있었는지 아닌지, 확실히 압니까?\" 페튜코비치가 집요하게 물었다.\n\n\"두 발로 서 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 당신이 깨어 있었다는 증거가 못 됩니다.\" (법정에 다시 웃음이 일었다.) \"그 순간 누가 당신에게 무언가를 물었더라면 대답할 수 있었겠소—이를테면 올해가 몇 년도냐고 물었다면?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금이 그리스도 기원 몇 년도인지는 아시오?\"\n\n그리고리는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 자기를 괴롭히는 자를 똑바로 바라보며 서 있었다. 이상한 일이지만, 그는 정말로 올해가 몇 년도인지 모르는 것 같았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 혹시 당신 손에 손가락이 몇 개인지는 말할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"저는 하인입니다.\" 그리고리가 불쑥, 크고 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. \"윗분들께서 저를 놀림감으로 삼는 게 마땅하다 여기신다면, 저는 그것을 참는 게 도리지요.\"\n\n페튜코비치는 다소 멈칫했고, 재판장이 끼어들어 좀 더 사건과 관련된 질문을 해야 한다고 일깨웠다. 페튜코비치는 위엄 있게 고개를 숙이고, 증인에게 더는 물을 것이 없다고 말했다. 물론 방청객과 배심원들의 마음속에는 한 가닥 의심이 남았으니, 어떤 치료를 하던 와중에 \"천국의 문\"을 보았을지도 모르고 자기가 사는 해가 몇 년도인지조차 모르는 사내의 증언에 대한 의심이었다. 그러나 그리고리가 증언대를 떠나기 전에 또 하나의 사건이 벌어졌다. 재판장이 피고를 향해, 마지막 증인의 증언에 대해 할 말이 있느냐고 물었던 것이다.\n\n\"문에 관한 것만 빼면, 저 사람이 한 말은 다 사실입니다.\" 미탸가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이를 잡아 준 것, 감사합니다. 제 매질을 용서해 준 것, 감사합니다. 저 노인은 평생 정직했고, 푸들 칠백 마리만큼이나 제 아버지에게 충직했습니다.\"\n\n\"피고는 말을 삼가시오.\" 재판장이 그를 타일렀다.\n\n\"저는 푸들이 아닙니다.\" 그리고리가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"좋아요, 푸들은 바로 나요.\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"그게 모욕이라면, 그건 내게 돌리고 그에게 용서를 빌겠소. 나는 저 사람에게 짐승처럼 잔인하게 굴었소. 이솝에게도 잔인했지요.\"\n\n\"이솝이라니, 무슨 이솝 말이오?\" 재판장이 다시 엄하게 물었다.\n\n\"아, 피에로 말입니다…… 제 아버지, 표도르 파블로비치 말입니다.\"\n\n재판장은 미탸에게 거듭, 인상 깊게 그리고 매우 엄하게 말을 더 삼가라고 경고했다.\n\n\"당신은 재판관들의 눈에 스스로 해를 끼치고 있소.\"\n\n변호인은 라키틴의 증언을 다루는 데도 똑같이 영리했다. 짚어 둘 것은, 라키틴이 주요 증인 가운데 하나였으며, 검사가 큰 의미를 두던 인물이었다는 사실이다. 그는 모든 것을 다 아는 듯했고, 그의 박식함은 놀라웠으니, 어디든 가 보았고 무엇이든 보았으며 누구와도 이야기를 나눴고, 표도르 파블로비치와 카라마조프 집안 사람들 전부의 일대기를 세세한 데까지 꿰고 있었다. 봉투에 관한 한, 사실 그는 미탸 본인에게서 들었을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그는 미탸가 \"메트로폴리스\"에서 벌인 행적, 평판을 떨어뜨릴 만한 온갖 행동과 말들을 세세하게 묘사했고, 스네기료프 이등 대위의 \"삼 부스러기\" 이야기도 들려주었다. 그러나 라키틴조차 미탸의 유산에 관해서는 확정적인 것을 아무것도 말하지 못한 채, 경멸 어린 일반론에 그쳤다.\n\n\"누가 그들 가운데 누가 잘못했고 누가 누구에게 빚졌는지를 알 수 있겠습니까, 아무도 갈피를 잡을 수 없게끔 모든 일을 뒤죽박죽으로 만드는 그 미치광이 같은 카라마조프 식 행태로 말입니다.\" 그는 이 비극적 범죄를, 오랜 농노제 세월 동안 뿌리 깊이 박힌 습성과, 적절한 제도의 부재에서 비롯된 러시아의 곤궁한 처지 탓으로 돌렸다. 사실 그에게는 다소 자유로운 발언이 허용되었다. 라키틴이 자기 역량을 펼쳐 보이며 주목을 끈 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 검사는 이 증인이 이 사건에 관한 잡지 기고문을 준비하고 있음을 알고 있었고, 나중에 보겠지만 자기 논고에서 그 기고문의 몇몇 견해를 인용하여, 자신이 이미 그것을 읽었음을 드러냈다. 이 증인이 그린 그림은 음울하고 불길한 것이어서, 검찰 측의 입지를 크게 강화했다. 대체로 라키틴의 변설은 그 독자성과 사상의 비범한 고결함으로 방청객을 사로잡았다. 그가 농노제와 러시아의 곤궁한 처지를 이야기할 때는 두세 차례 박수가 터지기까지 했다.\n\n그러나 라키틴은 젊은 혈기에 사소한 실책을 저질렀고, 변호인은 그것을 즉시 교묘하게 이용했다. 그루셴카에 관한 몇 가지 질문에 답하면서, 자신의 드높은 정서와 스스로도 물론 의식하고 있던 성공에 도취된 나머지, 그는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 두고 다소 경멸하듯 \"삼소노프의 첩\"이라고까지 말해 버렸다. 그는 나중에 그 말을 거두어들이는 데 무엇이든 내놓았을 테지만, 페튜코비치가 즉시 그 말꼬리를 잡았기 때문이다. 그리고 그 모든 것은 라키틴이, 그 변호사가 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 세세한 부분까지 그토록 속속들이 알게 되었으리라고는 미처 헤아리지 못한 탓이었다.\n\n\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\" 변호인이 더없이 상냥한, 심지어 공손하기까지 한 미소를 띠며 입을 열었다. \"당신은 물론, 교구 당국에서 펴낸 저 소책자 『작고한 장로 조시마 신부의 생애』의 그 라키틴 씨가 맞으시지요? 깊고 종교적인 성찰로 가득하고, 주교님께 바치는 훌륭하고 경건한 헌사가 앞에 붙은, 제가 방금 그토록 즐겁게 읽은 그 책 말입니다.\"\n\n\"저는 출판하려고 쓴 게 아닙니다…… 나중에 출판된 겁니다.\" 라키틴은 무슨 까닭인지 무섭게 당황하며, 거의 부끄러워하면서 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오, 그것 참 훌륭합니다! 당신 같은 사상가라면 모든 사회 문제를 가장 폭넓은 시각으로 바라볼 수 있고, 또 마땅히 그래야지요. 당신의 더없이 유익한 그 소책자는 주교님의 후원으로 널리 보급되어 적잖은 공헌을 했습니다…… 그런데 제가 당신에게서 알고 싶은 가장 중요한 것은 이겁니다. 당신은 방금, 스베틀로바 부인과 아주 친밀한 사이라고 진술했지요.\" (짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그루셴카의 성이 스베틀로바였다는 점이다. 나는 그날, 이 사건을 다루는 중에 그것을 처음 들었다.)\n\n\"제 모든 지인에 대해 책임질 수는 없습니다…… 저는 젊은 사람이고…… 누가 자기가 만나는 사람 하나하나에 대해 책임을 질 수 있겠습니까?\" 라키틴이 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다.\n\n\"이해합니다, 충분히 이해하고말고요.\" 페튜코비치가 마치 자기도 당황하여 황급히 변명이라도 하려는 듯 외쳤다. \"당신도 누구나 그렇듯, 인근의 젊은 명사들을 기꺼이 대접하던 젊고 아름다운 여인과 친분을 맺는 데 충분히 관심을 가질 만하지요. 하지만…… 제가 알고 싶은 것은 다만 이겁니다…… 제가 알게 된 바로는, 스베틀로바 부인은 두어 달 전 카라마조프 집안의 막내, 알렉세이 표도로비치와 알고 지내기를 유독 간절히 바랐고, 당신이 수도복 차림의 그를 자기에게 데려와 준다면 이십오 루블을 주겠노라 약속했다지요. 그리고 그 일은 실제로, 지금 이 심리의 대상이 된 그 끔찍한 범죄가 저질러진 바로 그날 저녁에 일어났습니다. 당신은 알렉세이 카라마조프를 스베틀로바 부인에게 데려갔고, 그 대가로 스베틀로바 부인에게서 이십오 루블을 받았습니까, 바로 그것을 당신에게서 듣고 싶었던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그건 농담이었습니다…… 그게 당신에게 무슨 흥밋거리가 되는지 모르겠군요…… 저는 그걸 농담으로 받았고…… 나중에 돌려줄 작정이었습니다……\"\n\n\"그렇다면 받기는 받았군요. 하지만 아직 돌려주지는 않았고…… 아니면 돌려주었소?\"\n\n\"그건 중요한 게 아닙니다.\" 라키틴이 중얼거렸다. \"그런 질문에는 대답하지 않겠습니다…… 물론 돌려줄 겁니다.\"\n\n재판장이 끼어들었으나, 페튜코비치는 증인에게 더는 물을 것이 없다고 선언했다. 라키틴 씨는 명예에 흠집 하나 없이 증언대를 떠나지는 못했다. 그의 변설이 풍기던 드높은 이상주의의 효과는 다소 흐려졌고, 그가 걸어 나가는 것을 지켜보는 페튜코비치의 표정은 방청객에게 \"이것이 바로 그를 고발하는 그 고결한 인사들의 표본이올시다\" 하고 일러 주는 듯했다. 기억하건대 이 사건 역시 미탸의 한바탕 폭발 없이는 지나가지 않았다. 라키틴이 그루셴카를 가리키던 그 어조에 격분한 그는, 별안간 \"베르나르!\" 하고 소리쳤다. 라키틴의 반대신문이 끝난 뒤, 재판장이 피고에게 할 말이 있느냐고 묻자, 미탸가 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 체포된 뒤로, 저자는 내게서 돈을 꿔 갔소! 저자는 비열한 베르나르요 기회주의자고, 신을 믿지 않습니다. 주교님을 감쪽같이 속여먹었지요!\"\n\n물론 미탸는 그 무절제한 말 때문에 또다시 제지당했으나, 라키틴은 끝장이 난 셈이었다. 스네기료프 이등 대위의 증언도 실패였지만, 그것은 전혀 다른 이유에서였다. 그는 누덕누덕한 더러운 옷에 진흙투성이 장화 차림으로 나타났는데, 경관들의 경계와 능숙한 관찰에도 불구하고 가망 없이 곤드레만드레 취해 있는 것으로 드러났다. 미탸가 자기에게 가한 폭행에 관한 질문을 받자, 그는 대답하기를 거부했다.\n\n\"그분께 신의 가호가 있기를. 일류샤가 말하지 말라고 했습니다. 신께서 저 세상에서 제게 갚아 주실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"누가 말하지 말라고 했소? 누구 이야기를 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"제 어린 아들 일류샤 말입니다. '아버지, 아버지, 저 사람이 아버지를 어찌나 모욕했는지!' 그 애가 바위 옆에서 그렇게 말했습니다. 지금 그 애는 죽어 가고 있어요……\"\n\n이등 대위는 별안간 흐느끼기 시작하더니, 재판장 앞에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었다. 그는 방청객의 웃음 속에 황급히 끌려 나갔다. 검사가 준비해 둔 효과는 전혀 거두지 못했다.\n\n페튜코비치는 계속해서 온갖 기회를 최대한 이용했고, 사건에 대한 세세한 지식으로 사람들을 점점 더 놀라게 했다. 이를테면 트리폰 보리소비치는 물론 미탸에게 매우 불리한 큰 인상을 남겼다. 그는 거의 손가락을 꼽아 가며, 미탸가 모크로예를 처음 찾았을 때 삼천 루블, \"아니면 그보다 아주 조금 적은\" 돈을 썼음이 틀림없다고 계산했다. \"그 집시 계집들한테만 얼마나 흥청망청 뿌렸는지 한번 생각해 보십시오! 그리고 우리 천한 농부들로 말하자면, 길바닥에 반 루블이나 던져 주는 정도가 아니라, 한 사람 앞에 적어도 이십오 루블씩 선물로 안겼지요, 그보다 적게 주진 않았습니다. 게다가 얼마나 많은 돈이 그냥 도둑맞았는지! 누가 훔쳐 갔든, 영수증을 남길 리가 있나요. 돈을 노상 그렇게 뿌려 대는데 어떻게 도둑을 잡겠습니까? 아시다시피 우리 농부들은 도둑놈들이라, 제 영혼은 안중에도 없으니까요. 게다가 그가 계집애들, 우리 마을 계집애들하고 놀아난 꼴이라니! 분명히 말씀드리지만, 그것들은 그 뒤로 아주 팔자가 폈습니다. 전에는 가난했었거든요.\" 사실 그는 지출 항목 하나하나를 떠올려 죄다 합산했다. 그러니 천오백 루블만 쓰고 나머지는 작은 주머니에 따로 챙겨 두었다는 가설은 도무지 믿기지 않게 여겨졌다.\n\n\"제 두 눈으로 그의 손에 삼천 루블이 동전 한 닢처럼 또렷이 있는 걸 봤습니다. 돈 셀 줄은 제가 알 텐데요.\" 트리폰 보리소비치는 \"윗분들\"의 비위를 맞추려고 안간힘을 쓰며 외쳤다.\n\n페튜코비치는 그를 반대신문할 차례가 되자, 그의 증언을 반박하려는 시도는 거의 하지 않고, 체포 한 달 전 모크로예에서의 첫 술판 때 일어난 어떤 일에 관해 묻기 시작했다. 곧 티모페이와 아킴이라는 또 다른 농부가 미탸가 취해서 통로 바닥에 떨어뜨린 백 루블을 주워 트리폰 보리소비치에게 건넸고, 그렇게 한 대가로 그에게서 각각 일 루블씩을 받은 일이었다. \"자,\" 변호사가 물었다. \"당신은 그 백 루블을 카라마조프 씨에게 돌려주었소?\" 트리폰 보리소비치는 부질없이 발뺌하려 했다…… 농부들의 신문이 끝나자, 그는 백 루블을 주운 사실을 인정할 수밖에 없었으나, 다만 자기는 그것을 \"더없이 정직하게\" 드미트리 표도로비치에게 고스란히 돌려주었으며, \"다만 그때 나리께서 취해 계셨던지라 그걸 기억 못 하실 뿐\"이라고 덧붙였다. 그러나 그가 농부들이 불려 나와 증명하기 전까지 백 루블 건을 부인했던 터라, 미탸에게 돈을 돌려주었다는 그의 증언은 당연히 큰 의심을 받았다. 그리하여 검찰 측이 내세운 가장 위험한 증인 가운데 하나가 또다시 신빙성을 잃었다.\n\n폴란드인들에게도 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다. 그들은 거만하고 도도한 태도를 취하며, 둘 다 국가에 봉직한 바 있고 \"판 미탸\"가 그들에게 \"명예를 매수하려고\" 삼천 루블을 제의했으며, 그의 손에 큰돈이 있는 것을 보았노라고 큰 소리로 떠벌렸다. 판 무샬로비치는 자기 말 속에 폴란드어 단어를 무시무시하게 많이 끼워 넣었는데, 이것이 재판장과 검사의 눈에 자기 위신을 높여 줄 뿐임을 알아채자 점점 더 거드름을 피우다가, 끝내는 아예 폴란드어로만 떠들기에 이르렀다. 그러나 페튜코비치는 그들마저 자기 올가미에 걸려들게 했다. 다시 불려 나온 트리폰 보리소비치는 둘러대려 했으나, 판 브루블레프스키가 자기가 마련해 준 카드 한 벌을 다른 것으로 바꿔치기했으며, 판 무샬로비치가 노름판에서 속임수를 썼다는 사실을 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 칼가노프가 이를 확인해 주었고, 두 폴란드인은 방청객의 웃음 속에 평판을 망친 채 증언대를 떠났다.\n\n그러더니 가장 위험한 증인들 거의 모두에게 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다. 페튜코비치는 그들 모두에게 흠집을 내고, 일종의 조롱과 함께 물러가게 하는 데 성공했다. 변호사들과 전문가들은 감탄을 금치 못했으나, 다만 그것이 무슨 유익한 목적에 보탬이 될 수 있는지를 도무지 이해하지 못했으니, 거듭 말하거니와 모두가 검찰 측의 입지는 반박될 수 없으며 오히려 점점 더 비극적일 만큼 압도적으로 커져 간다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러나 그 \"위대한 마술사\"의 자신감에서 그들은 그가 태연자약함을 보았고, \"그런 사람\"이 까닭 없이 페테르부르크에서 온 것이 아니며, 빈손으로 돌아갈 사내가 아님을 느끼며 기다렸다.\n\n# Chapter III. The Medical Experts And A Pound Of Nuts\n\n의료 전문가들의 증언 역시 피고에게는 별 도움이 되지 못했다. 그리고 페튜코비치 또한 그 증언에 큰 기대를 걸지 않았음이 나중에 드러났다. 의학적 변호 노선은 오로지 카테리나 이바노브나의 고집 때문에 채택되었던 것으로, 그녀는 일부러 모스크바에서 이름난 의사 한 사람을 불러왔던 것이다. 물론 그 변호 노선이 피고 측에 손해를 끼칠 일은 없었고, 운이 좋으면 무언가 얻을 수도 있었다. 그러나 의사들의 의견이 서로 엇갈리는 바람에 거기엔 희극적인 요소가 깃들어 있었다. 의료 전문가는 모스크바에서 온 유명한 의사와 우리 고장의 의사 헤르첸슈투베, 그리고 젊은 의사 바르빈스키였다. 뒤의 두 사람은 검찰 측 증인으로도 나섰다.\n\n전문가 자격으로 가장 먼저 호명된 이는 헤르첸슈투베 박사였다. 그는 일흔 살의 백발에 머리가 벗어진 노인으로, 중키에 다부진 체격이었다. 그는 온 고장 사람들에게서 두루 존경과 신망을 받았다. 그는 양심적인 의사였고 더없이 훌륭하고 경건한 사람으로, 헤른후트파인지 모라비아 형제단원인지, 정확히 어느 쪽인지는 나도 잘 모르겠다. 그는 여러 해 동안 우리 사이에서 살아왔으며 놀라우리만큼 위엄 있게 처신했다. 그는 마음이 따뜻하고 인정 많은 사람이었다. 그는 가난한 환자들과 농민들을 무료로 치료해 주었고, 그들의 빈민굴과 오두막까지 찾아가 약값으로 쓰라며 돈을 놓고 오기까지 했으나, 노새처럼 고집이 셌다. 한번 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박히면 도무지 흔들리는 법이 없었다. 그건 그렇고, 고장 사람들 거의 모두가 알고 있던 사실인데, 그 유명한 의사는 우리 사이에 온 지 사나흘도 채 되지 않아 헤르첸슈투베 박사의 자질에 대해 극도로 모욕적인 빈정거림을 늘어놓았던 것이다. 모스크바 의사는 왕진 한 번에 이십오 루블을 요구했음에도, 고장 사람 여럿이 그의 도착을 반겨 비용을 가리지 않고 앞다투어 진찰을 받으러 몰려들었다. 그들은 물론 하나같이 이전에 헤르첸슈투베 박사의 환자였던 자들이었고, 그 유명한 의사는 그의 치료를 극도로 가혹하게 비판했다. 급기야 그는 환자들을 보자마자 이렇게 묻기까지 했다. \"그래, 누가 당신한테 이런 엉터리 약을 잔뜩 먹였소? 헤르첸슈투베? 헤, 헤!\" 헤르첸슈투베 박사도 물론 이 모든 말을 들어 알고 있었는데, 이제 세 의사가 차례로 모습을 드러내 심문을 받게 되었다.\n\n헤르첸슈투베 박사는 피고의 정신 능력에 이상이 있다는 것은 자명하다고 단호히 선언했다. 그러고 나서 이 견해의 근거를 댔는데, 여기서는 그 부분을 생략하기로 한다, 그는 그 이상이 피고의 과거 행동 가운데 여럿에서 드러났을 뿐만 아니라 바로 지금 이 순간에도 분명히 나타나고 있다고 덧붙였다. 지금 이 순간 어떻게 그것이 드러나느냐는 질문을 받자, 노의사는 순박하리만큼 솔직하게, 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 \"이 상황에서는 주목할 만한, 범상치 않은 태도\"를 보였다고 지적했다. 즉 그가 \"군인처럼 곧장 앞만 바라보며 행진하듯 들어왔는데, 정작 그로서는 왼쪽을, 방청석에 부인들이 앉아 있는 쪽을 바라보는 것이 더 자연스러웠을 것이니, 그가 여성을 대단히 흠모하는 사람인 데다 지금 부인들이 자기에 대해 무어라 말하고 있을지 무척 신경을 쓰고 있을 것이 분명하기 때문\"이라고, 노인은 자기 특유의 어법으로 결론을 맺었다.\n\n덧붙이자면 그는 러시아어를 거침없이 구사했으나 모든 구절이 독일식으로 짜여 있었는데, 그렇다고 그것이 그에게 걸림돌이 되지는 않았으니, 자기가 러시아어를 완벽하게, 실로 러시아 사람들보다도 더 잘한다고 믿는 것이 늘 그의 약점이었기 때문이다. 그는 러시아 속담을 즐겨 썼고, 러시아 속담이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 훌륭하고 가장 표현력이 풍부한 격언이라고 항상 단언했다. 또한 덧붙여 말해 두자면, 그는 대화 중에 정신이 딴 데 팔려 더없이 평범한 낱말조차 잊어버리곤 했는데, 그 낱말을 완벽하게 알고 있으면서도 더러 머릿속에서 사라져 버리는 것이었다. 그런 일은 독일어로 말할 때도 똑같이 일어났는데, 그럴 때면 그는 늘 잃어버린 낱말을 붙잡으려는 듯 얼굴 앞에서 손을 휘저었고, 그 사라진 낱말을 찾아내기 전에는 누구도 그에게 말을 계속하게 할 수 없었다. 피고가 들어설 때 부인들 쪽을 바라봤어야 했다는 그의 말에 방청석에서는 재미있어하는 수군거림이 일었다. 우리 고장 부인들은 모두 노의사를 무척 좋아했다. 또한 그들은 그가 평생 독신으로 모범적인 품행을 지켜 온 신앙심 깊은 사람으로서 여성을 고귀한 존재로 우러러본다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그래서 그의 뜻밖의 소견은 모두에게 무척 기이하게 여겨졌다.\n\n이번에는 모스크바 의사가 심문을 받았는데, 그는 피고의 정신 상태가 극도로 비정상이라고 본다고 분명하고 단호하게 거듭 말했다. 그는 \"착란\"과 \"광증\"에 대해 박식하게 길게 늘어놓으며, 수집된 모든 사실로 미루어 피고가 체포되기 며칠 전부터 의심할 바 없이 착란 상태에 있었고, 만일 범행이 그에 의해 저질러졌다면 설령 그가 그것을 의식하고 있었다 할지라도 거의 무의식적으로 행해진 것일 수밖에 없으니, 그것은 그가 자신을 사로잡은 병적 충동을 억제할 힘이 없었기 때문이라고 주장했다.\n\n그러나 일시적 착란과는 별개로, 의사는 광증을 진단했으며, 그의 말에 따르면 이는 장차 완전한 정신착란으로 이어질 조짐이었다. (여기서 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이것은 내가 내 나름의 표현으로 옮긴 것이며, 의사는 매우 학술적이고 전문적인 용어를 구사했다는 점이다.) \"그의 모든 행동은 상식과 논리에 어긋납니다.\" 그는 말을 이었다. \"제가 직접 보지 못한 것, 즉 범행 자체와 사건의 전모는 차치하더라도, 그저께 그가 저와 이야기를 나눌 때 그의 눈에는 영문 모를 고정된 눈빛이 어려 있었습니다. 그는 웃을 일이 전혀 없는데도 느닷없이 웃었습니다. 그는 끊임없이, 설명할 길 없는 짜증을 드러내며 '베르나르!' '윤리!' 같은, 그리고 그에 못지않게 엉뚱한 이상한 말들을 내뱉었습니다.\" 그러나 의사는 무엇보다도, 피고가 자기를 속여 빼앗아 갔다고 여기는 삼천 루블에 관해서는 다른 불운이나 원한에 대해서는 비교적 가볍게 이야기할 수 있으면서도 유독 그 이야기만 나오면 유난한 짜증 없이는 입에 올리지도 못한다는 사실에서 광증을 간파해 냈다. 여러 사람의 진술에 따르면, 그는 과거에도 삼천 루블 이야기만 나오면 그때마다 완전히 광란에 빠지곤 했는데, 그러면서도 욕심 없고 물욕에 매이지 않은 사람으로 전해지고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"제 학식 높은 동료의 견해, 즉 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 자연스럽게 곧장 앞을 바라보지 않고 부인들 쪽을 바라봤을 것이라는 견해에 대해서는,\" 모스크바 의사가 마무리하며 비꼬듯 덧붙였다. \"이 이론의 재기 발랄함은 차치하더라도, 근본적으로 잘못되었다고만 말해 두겠습니다. 저로서는 자기 운명이 결정될 법정에 들어서는 피고가 그렇게 고정된 눈빛으로 곧장 앞만 바라보는 것이 자연스럽지 않으며, 그것이 실로 그의 비정상적인 정신 상태의 징후일 수 있다는 데에는 전적으로 동의하는 바이나, 동시에 그가 자연스럽게 바라봤을 곳은 왼쪽의 부인들이 아니라 오히려 오른쪽이었으리라고, 즉 자기의 모든 희망이 걸려 있고 자기의 모든 장래가 그 변호에 달려 있는 법률 고문을 찾았으리라고 주장하는 바입니다.\" 의사는 자기 견해를 확신에 차서 단호하게 피력했다.\n\n그러나 전문가들 사이의 의견 차이에 마지막 희극적 색채를 더한 것은 바르빈스키 박사의 뜻밖의 단언이었다. 그의 견해로는 피고가 지금도, 그리고 줄곧 완벽하게 정상적인 상태에 있었으며, 비록 체포되기 전에는 분명 신경이 곤두서고 극도로 흥분한 상태였음이 틀림없으나, 이는 질투, 분노, 끊임없는 음주 따위의 지극히 명백한 여러 원인에서 비롯된 것일 수 있다고 했다. 그러나 그러한 신경 상태가 방금 거론된 정신 착란을 수반하는 것은 아니라고 했다. 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 왼쪽을 바라봤어야 했는가, 오른쪽을 바라봤어야 했는가 하는 문제에 대해서는, \"자기의 변변찮은 소견으로는,\" 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 곧장 앞을 바라보는 것이 자연스러우며 실제로 그렇게 했는데, 바로 그곳에 자기 운명을 좌우할 판사들이 앉아 있었기 때문이라고 했다. 그러니 곧장 앞을 바라봤다는 바로 그 사실이야말로 그가 현재 더없이 정상적인 정신 상태에 있음을 보여 주는 것이라고 했다. 젊은 의사는 다소 열을 올려 자기의 \"변변찮은\" 증언을 끝맺었다.\n\n\"브라보, 의사 선생!\" 미탸가 자기 자리에서 외쳤다. \"바로 그거요!\"\n\n미탸는 물론 제지당했으나, 젊은 의사의 견해는 판사들과 방청객들에게 결정적인 영향을 미쳤고, 나중에 드러난 바로는 모두가 그의 의견에 동의했다. 그런데 헤르첸슈투베 박사는 증인으로 호명되었을 때 전혀 뜻밖에도 미탸에게 도움이 되어 주었다. 고장의 오랜 주민으로서 카라마조프 집안을 여러 해 알고 지내 온 그는 검찰 측에 매우 중요한 사실 몇 가지를 제공했으나, 갑자기 무언가가 떠오른 듯 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"하지만 저 가엾은 젊은이는 전혀 다른 삶을 살 수도 있었습니다, 그 애는 어린 시절에도 그 뒤에도 마음씨가 고왔으니까요, 그건 제가 압니다. 그런데 러시아 속담에 이런 말이 있지요. '사람에게 머리가 하나 있으면 좋은 일이지만, 영리한 사람이 또 한 명 찾아와 준다면 더욱 좋을 것이니, 그러면 머리가 하나가 아니라 둘이 되기 때문이다.'\"\n\n\"머리 하나보다는 둘이 낫다는 말이군요.\" 검사가 조바심을 내며 끼어들었다. 그는 노인이 자기가 주는 인상이나 자기가 일으키는 지체에는 아랑곳없이 느릿느릿 신중하게 말하는 버릇이 있으며, 그 밋밋하고 둔하면서도 늘 흐뭇하게 자족하는 독일식 재담을 대단히 자랑스러워한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 노인은 농담하기를 좋아했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 제 말이 바로 그겁니다.\" 그는 고집스레 말을 이었다. \"머리 하나도 좋지만 둘이면 훨씬 더 좋지요, 그런데 그 애는 영리한 머리를 또 하나 만나지 못했고, 그래서 그 애의 머리가 그만... 어디로 가버렸느냐 하면, 아, 그 단어를 잊어버렸군요.\" 그는 손으로 눈앞을 가리며 말을 이었다. \"오, 그렇지, 슈파치렌(spazieren).\"\n\n\"방랑한다는 말입니까?\"\n\"오, 그렇소, 방랑이지요, 바로 그 말입니다. 그러니까, 그 애의 머리가 방랑을 떠나 그만 너무도 깊은 구멍에 빠져 자기 자신을 잃어버린 거지요. 그렇지만 그 애는 고마움을 아는 다정한 아이였습니다. 오, 저는 그 애를 아주 잘 기억합니다, 요만한 꼬마였는데, 아비한테 버림받아 뒤뜰에 방치된 채 발에 장화도 신지 않고 뛰어다녔고, 작은 바지는 단추 하나에 간신히 걸려 있었지요.\"\n\n정직한 노인의 목소리에 갑자기 정감 어린 따스함이 깃들었다. 페튜코비치는 무언가 냄새를 맡은 듯 흠칫하더니 곧장 그것을 낚아챘다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 그때 저는 젊은이였지요... 저는... 글쎄요, 그때 마흔다섯이었고, 막 이곳에 온 참이었습니다. 그때 저는 그 애가 너무도 가엾어서 이렇게 자문했지요, 내가 저 애한테 한 파운드의... 한 파운드의 뭐였더라? 그 이름을 잊어버렸군요. 아이들이 무척 좋아하는 그것 한 파운드를, 그게 뭐였더라, 뭐였지?\" 의사는 다시 손을 휘젓기 시작했다. \"나무에서 자라고, 따서 모두에게 나눠 주는 그것...\"\n\n\"사과 말입니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아니, 아니오. 사과라면 한 다스를 헤아리지 한 파운드라고 하지는 않지요... 아니오, 그건 잔뜩 있고, 죄다 자그마합니다. 입에 넣고 깨물면 똑 하고...\"\n\n\"호두 말입니까?\"\n\"바로 그겁니다, 호두지요, 제 말이 그겁니다.\" 의사는 마치 그 낱말을 까먹은 적도 없었다는 듯 더없이 태연하게 되풀이했다. \"그래서 저는 그 애한테 호두 한 파운드를 사 주었습니다, 그때까지 아무도 그 애한테 호두 한 파운드 사 준 사람이 없었으니까요. 그러고는 손가락을 치켜들고 그 애한테 말했지요, '얘야, 고트 데어 파터(Gott der Vater).' 그러자 그 애가 웃으며 말했습니다, '고트 데어 파터.'... '고트 데어 존(Gott der Sohn).' 그 애는 또 웃더니 혀 짧은 소리로 말했지요, '고트 데어 존.' '고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트(Gott der heilige Geist).' 그러자 그 애가 웃으며 제 딴에는 최선을 다해 말했습니다, '고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트.' 저는 떠났고, 이틀 뒤에 마침 그 앞을 지나가게 되었는데, 그 애가 먼저 저를 보고 소리쳤습니다, '아저씨, 고트 데어 파터, 고트 데어 존,' 그런데 '고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트'만 잊어버렸더군요. 하지만 제가 그걸 일러 주었고, 또다시 그 애가 무척 가여웠습니다. 그런데 그 애는 다른 데로 보내졌고, 저는 다시는 그 애를 보지 못했습니다. 이십삼 년이 흘렀지요. 어느 날 아침 제가 머리가 하얗게 센 노인이 되어 서재에 앉아 있는데, 한 혈색 좋은 젊은이가 방으로 걸어 들어왔습니다, 저로서는 도무지 알아볼 수 없는 얼굴이었지요, 그런데 그가 손가락을 치켜들고 웃으며 말하더군요, '고트 데어 파터, 고트 데어 존, 그리고 고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트. 막 도착해서 그 호두 한 파운드에 감사드리러 왔습니다, 저한테 호두 한 파운드 사 준 사람은 그 누구도 없었고, 사 주신 분은 선생님이 유일하니까요.' 그러자 저는 제 행복했던 젊은 시절과 발에 장화도 신지 못한 채 뒤뜰에 있던 그 가엾은 아이가 떠올랐고, 가슴이 뭉클해져서 말했습니다, '자네는 고마움을 아는 젊은이로군, 어린 시절에 내가 사 준 호두 한 파운드를 평생 기억하고 있었으니 말일세.' 그러고는 그를 껴안고 축복해 주었습니다. 그리고 저는 눈물을 흘렸지요. 그도 웃었지만, 역시 눈물을 흘렸습니다... 러시아 사람은 마땅히 울어야 할 때 곧잘 웃는 법이니까요. 하지만 그는 정말로 울었습니다, 제가 보았으니까요. 그런데 지금은, 아아!...\"\n\n\"저도 지금 울고 있습니다, 독일 양반, 저도 지금 울고 있어요, 성자 같은 분이여.\" 미탸가 갑자기 외쳤다.\n\n어쨌든 이 일화는 방청객들에게 어느 정도 호의적인 인상을 주었다. 그러나 미탸에게 유리한 가장 큰 반향을 불러일으킨 것은 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언이었으니, 그것은 곧이어 묘사하기로 하겠다. 실로 변호 측 증인들, 즉 변호인이 부른 증인들이 증언을 시작하자 운세가 갑자기 미탸에게 눈에 띄게 더 유리해진 듯했고, 특히 놀라운 것은 이것이 변호인 자신에게조차 뜻밖이었다는 점이다. 그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 호명되기 전에 알료샤가 심문을 받았는데, 그는 검찰이 내세운 한 가지 중요한 논점에 대해 확실한 반증이 될 만한 사실 하나를 떠올렸다.\n\n# Chapter IV. Fortune Smiles On Mitya\n\n그것은 알료샤 자신에게조차 자못 뜻밖의 일이었다. 그는 선서를 요구받지 않았으며, 양측 모두 그를 무척 부드럽고 동정 어린 태도로 대했던 것으로 나는 기억한다. 그의 선량함에 관한 평판이 그보다 먼저 이곳에 당도해 있었던 것이 분명했다. 알료샤는 겸손하게, 그리고 절제된 어조로 증언했으나, 불행한 형을 향한 그의 따뜻한 동정만큼은 누가 보아도 틀림이 없었다. 어느 질문에 답하면서 그는 형의 성품을 이렇게 묘사했다. 어쩌면 격정적이고 자신의 정념에 휩쓸리기 쉬운 사람이지만, 동시에 명예를 알고 자존심이 강하며 너그럽고, 필요하다면 자기를 희생할 줄도 아는 사람이라고. 그러나 그는, 그루셴카를 향한 정념과 아버지를 둘러싼 경쟁 때문에 형이 근래에 견딜 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다는 점은 인정했다. 다만 형이 이득을 노리고 살인을 저질렀을지도 모른다는 암시에 대해서는 분개하며 물리쳤으니, 그러면서도 삼천 루블이 미탸에게 거의 강박이 되어 있었다는 것, 그가 그 돈을 아버지에게서 사취당한 유산의 일부로 여겼다는 것, 그리고 평소에는 돈에 무심한 사람이면서도 그 삼천 루블에 관해서만큼은 분노 없이는 입에 올리지도 못했다는 것은 인정했다. 검사의 표현을 빌리면 두 \"숙녀\"의, 즉 그루셴카와 카탸의 경쟁에 관해서는 그는 얼버무리며 답했고, 한두 가지 질문에는 아예 대답하기를 꺼리기까지 했다.\n\n\"어쨌거나 형이 당신에게, 아버지를 죽일 작정이라고 말한 적이 있습니까?\" 검사가 물었다. \"필요하다고 여기시면 답을 거부하셔도 됩니다.\" 그는 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제게 그렇게 대놓고 말한 적은 없습니다.\" 알료샤가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 어떻게요? 에둘러서 말했습니까?\"\n\n\"형은 언젠가 제게 아버지에 대한 증오를 말했고, 극단적인 순간에… 분노에 사로잡힌 순간에 어쩌면 아버지를 죽일지도 모른다는 두려움을 토로했습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 당신은 그 말을 믿었습니까?\"\n\n\"믿었다고 말하기가 두렵습니다. 하지만 저는 그 운명의 순간에 언제나 어떤 더 높은 감정이 형을 구해 주리라는 것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었고, 실제로 그것이 형을 구했습니다. 제 아버지를 죽인 사람은 형이 아니니까요.\" 알료샤는 법정 전체에 울려 퍼지는 큰 목소리로 단호하게 말했다.\n\n검사는 나팔 소리를 들은 군마처럼 움찔하고 일어섰다.\n\n\"당신의 확신이 전적으로 진실하다는 것을 제가 충분히 믿고 있으며, 그것을 불행한 형에 대한 애정으로 설명하거나 그것과 동일시하지 않는다는 점을 분명히 말씀드립니다. 이 비극적인 사건 전체에 대한 당신의 독특한 견해는 이미 예심에서부터 저희가 알고 있습니다. 그것이 지극히 개인적이며 검찰이 수집한 다른 모든 증거와 모순된다는 점을 당신에게 굳이 숨기지는 않겠습니다. 그러므로 저는, 도대체 어떤 사실들이 당신으로 하여금 형의 무죄와, 예심에서 당신이 증언하여 지목한 또 다른 인물의 유죄를 이토록 확신하게 만들었는지를 캐묻는 것이 반드시 필요하다고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"저는 예심에서 제게 던져진 질문들에만 답했을 뿐입니다.\" 알료샤가 천천히, 그리고 차분하게 대답했다. \"제 입으로 스메르댜코프를 고발하지는 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 그에게 불리한 증언을 하지 않았습니까?\"\n\n\"제 형 드미트리의 말에 이끌려 그렇게 한 것입니다. 저는 형이 체포될 때 무슨 일이 있었는지, 형이 제가 심문받기도 전에 어떻게 스메르댜코프를 지목했는지를 전해 들었습니다. 저는 제 형이 무죄라고 절대적으로 믿으며, 만약 형이 살인을 저지르지 않았다면, 그렇다면…\"\n\n\"그렇다면 스메르댜코프란 말입니까? 왜 스메르댜코프죠? 그리고 당신은 어째서 형의 무죄를 그토록 완전히 확신합니까?\"\n\n\"저는 제 형을 믿지 않을 수가 없습니다. 형이 제게 거짓말을 하지 않으리라는 것을 압니다. 형의 얼굴을 보고 그가 거짓말을 하고 있지 않다는 것을 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"오로지 그 얼굴에서 말입니까? 당신이 가진 증거라곤 그것이 전부입니까?\"\n\n\"다른 증거는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 스메르댜코프의 유죄에 대해서도 형의 말과 그 얼굴 표정 외에는 아무런 증거가 없는 거로군요?\"\n\n\"네, 다른 증거는 없습니다.\"\n\n검사는 이 대목에서 신문을 중단했다. 알료샤의 증언이 방청객에게 남긴 인상은 더없이 실망스러운 것이었다. 재판이 열리기 전부터 스메르댜코프에 관한 이야기가 떠돌았으니, 누군가는 무언가를 들었고, 누군가는 또 다른 무언가를 지적했으며, 알료샤가 형의 무죄와 스메르댜코프의 유죄를 입증할 어떤 비범한 증거들을 한데 모아 두었다는 소문도 있었다. 그러나 결국 아무것도 없었고, 형이라면 당연히 품을 법한 어떤 도덕적 확신 외에는 아무 증거도 없었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다. 그가 알료샤에게, 피고가 아버지에 대한 증오와 그를 죽일지도 모른다는 말을 그에게 한 것이 언제였는지, 가령 그 참사가 있기 전 마지막 만남에서 들은 것인지를 묻자, 알료샤는 마치 이제야 비로소 무언가를 기억해 내고 깨닫는 듯 움찔하며 대답했다.\n\n\"이제야 한 가지 정황이 떠오르는데, 저 자신도 까맣게 잊고 있던 일입니다. 그때는 제게 분명하지 않았으나, 지금은…\"\n\n그리고 분명 이제야 처음으로 어떤 생각에 부딪힌 듯, 그는 열을 올리며 이야기했다. 그날 저녁 수도원으로 가는 길의 나무 아래에서 미탸와 마지막으로 만났을 때, 미탸가 자기 가슴을, \"가슴 위쪽을\" 치면서 자신의 명예를 되찾을 방도가 있다고, 그 방도가 여기, 바로 이 가슴에 있다고 몇 번이나 되풀이해 말했다는 것이었다. \"형이 가슴을 쳤을 때 저는 그것이 마음속에 있다는 뜻인 줄로만 알았습니다.\" 알료샤가 계속했다. \"형이 자기를 기다리고 있는 어떤 끔찍한 치욕에서, 제게조차 차마 털어놓지 못하는 그 치욕에서 자기를 구해 낼 힘을 마음속에서 찾을 수 있으리라는 뜻으로요. 솔직히 고백하건대, 저는 그때 형이 우리 아버지에 관해 말하고 있으며, 형이 몸서리치는 그 치욕이란 아버지에게 찾아가 무슨 폭행을 저지른다는 생각이라고 여겼습니다. 그런데 바로 그때 형이 자기 가슴의 어딘가를 가리켰고, 그래서 저는 그때 이런 생각이 떠올랐던 것을 기억합니다. 심장은 가슴의 그 부위가 아니라 그 아래에 있는데 형은 너무 높은 곳을, 바로 목 아래쪽을 치며 자꾸 그 자리를 가리키는구나, 하고요. 그때는 제 생각이 어리석게 느껴졌지만, 형은 어쩌면 그때 천오백 루블이 든 바로 그 작은 주머니를 가리키고 있었던 겁니다!\"\n\n\"바로 그거야.\" 미탸가 제자리에서 외쳤다. \"맞다, 알료샤, 내가 주먹으로 친 건 그 작은 주머니였어.\"\n\n페튜코비치는 황급히 그에게 날아가듯 다가가 제발 조용히 해 달라고 간청하는 한편, 같은 순간 알료샤에게 달려들었다. 알료샤 자신도 기억에 휩쓸려, 그 치욕이란 아마도 형의 몸에 지니고 있던 바로 그 천오백 루블이었으리라는 자신의 추론을 열렬히 펼쳤다. 형은 그 돈을 카테리나 이바노브나에게 빚진 금액의 절반으로 돌려줄 수도 있었지만, 그것을 갚지 않고 다른 목적에—다시 말해 그루셴카가 응한다면 그녀와 함께 달아나는 데 쓰기로 작정하고 있었다는 것이었다.\n\n\"그래요, 분명히 그럴 겁니다.\" 알료샤가 갑자기 흥분하여 외쳤다. \"형은 그 치욕의 절반을, 절반을(형은 절반이라고 몇 번이나 말했습니다) 당장에라도 벗어 버릴 수 있는데, 의지가 약해 너무도 불행하게 그것을 하지 못하겠노라고… 그것을 할 수 없는 인간임을 미리부터 알고 있노라고 몇 번이나 외쳤습니다!\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은 형이 가슴의 바로 이 부위를 쳤다는 것을 분명하게, 확신을 가지고 기억하는 겁니까?\" 페튜코비치가 열을 올려 물었다.\n\n\"분명하게, 확신을 가지고 기억합니다. 그때 제가 '심장은 더 아래에 있는데 어째서 저렇게 위쪽을 칠까?' 하고 생각했고, 그 생각이 그때는 제게 어리석게 느껴졌으니까요… 그게 어리석게 느껴졌던 것을 기억합니다… 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔습니다. 바로 그것이 방금 제게 이 일을 되살려 준 겁니다. 어떻게 지금까지 그걸 잊고 있었을까요? 형이 방도가 있는데도 그 천오백 루블을 돌려주지 않겠다고 했을 때 가리킨 것이 바로 그 작은 주머니였습니다. 그리고 형은 모크로예에서 체포되었을 때 이렇게 외쳤다고 합니다—저는 압니다, 전해 들었습니다—카테리나 이바노브나에게 빚진 돈의 절반을(절반입니다, 유의하십시오!) 갚을 방도가 있었으면서도 차마 그 돈을 돌려주지 못했고, 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 그녀의 눈에 도둑으로 남기를 택했다는 것이야말로 자기 인생에서 가장 수치스러운 짓이라고요. 그리고 그 빚이 형에게 얼마나 큰 고통, 얼마나 큰 고통이었던지!\" 알료샤가 끝맺으며 외쳤다.\n\n물론 검사가 끼어들었다. 그는 알료샤에게 그 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지 다시 한번 묘사해 달라고 했고, \"피고가 무언가를 가리키는 것처럼 보였습니까? 어쩌면 그저 주먹으로 가슴을 친 것은 아니었습니까?\" 하는 질문을 몇 번이나 거듭 다그쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 주먹은 아니었습니다.\" 알료샤가 외쳤다. \"형은 손가락으로 가리켰고, 여기, 아주 높은 곳을 가리켰습니다…. 어떻게 제가 이 순간까지 그것을 그토록 까맣게 잊고 있었을까요?\"\n\n재판장이 미탸에게 마지막 증인의 증언에 대해 할 말이 있느냐고 물었다. 미탸는 그것을 확인해 주며, 자기는 가슴에, 바로 목 아래에 지니고 있던 천오백 루블을 가리키고 있었으며, 그것이 물론 그 치욕이었다고 말했다. \"부정할 수 없는 치욕입니다, 제 평생에 가장 수치스러운 짓이지요.\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"갚을 수 있었는데도 갚지 않았습니다. 그것을 돌려주느니 차라리 그녀의 눈에 도둑으로 남기를 택했습니다. 그리고 가장 수치스러운 대목은, 내가 그것을 돌려주지 않으리라는 걸 미리부터 알고 있었다는 겁니다! 네 말이 맞다, 알료샤! 고맙다, 알료샤!\"\n\n이렇게 하여 알료샤에 대한 반대신문이 끝났다. 그 중요하고 인상적인 점은, 적어도 한 가지 사실이 밝혀졌다는 것이었으니, 그것이 비록 증거의 자그마한 한 조각, 증거의 한낱 실마리에 지나지 않더라도, 그 주머니가 실제로 존재했고 그 안에 천오백 루블이 들어 있었다는 것, 그리고 모크로예에서 그 천오백 루블이 \"자기 것\"이라고 주장했을 때 피고가 예심에서 거짓말을 하고 있던 것이 아니라는 것을 어느 정도 입증하는 데 보탬이 되었기 때문이다. 알료샤는 기뻤다. 그는 상기된 얼굴로 자기에게 배정된 자리로 물러났다. 그는 줄곧 속으로 되뇌었다. \"어쩌다 잊고 있었을까? 어떻게 그걸 잊을 수 있었을까? 그리고 무엇이 지금 그걸 되살려 냈을까?\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나가 증언대로 불려 나왔다. 그녀가 들어서자 법정에서 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어졌다. 부인들은 손잡이 안경과 오페라글라스를 움켜쥐었다. 남자들 사이에서도 술렁임이 일어, 더 잘 보려고 자리에서 일어서는 이들도 있었다. 사람들은 훗날 입을 모아, 그녀가 들어서는 순간 미탸의 얼굴이 \"백지장처럼 하얗게\" 질렸다고 말했다. 온통 검은 옷을 입은 그녀는 겸손하게, 거의 머뭇거리듯 앞으로 나아갔다. 그녀가 동요하고 있는지는 그 얼굴로는 도무지 알 수 없었으나, 어둡고 음울한 두 눈에는 단호한 빛이 어려 있었다. 많은 사람이 그 순간 그녀가 유난히 아름다워 보였다고 언급했다는 것을 나는 짚어 둘 만하다. 그녀는 나직하면서도 또렷하게 말했고, 그래서 법정 전체에 그 소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 침착하게, 적어도 침착해 보이려 애쓰며 자신의 생각을 표현했다. 재판장은 \"어떤 현(絃)\"을 건드리기를 두려워하듯, 또 그녀의 크나큰 불행을 배려하듯, 신중하고 매우 정중하게 신문을 시작했다. 그러나 처음 던진 질문들 가운데 하나에 답하면서 카테리나 이바노브나는, 자기가 한때 피고와 약혼했었다고 단호하게 대답하고는 \"그가 제 발로 저를 떠날 때까지요…\" 하고 조용히 덧붙였다. 그녀가 친척에게 부쳐 달라고 미탸에게 맡긴 삼천 루블에 관해 물어보자, 그녀는 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 그저 부쳐 달라고 그 돈을 그에게 준 것이 아닙니다. 저는 그때 그가 돈이 몹시 절실히 필요하다는 것을 느끼고 있었어요…. 저는 그가 원한다면 한 달 안에 부치면 된다는 양해 아래 삼천 루블을 주었습니다. 그가 나중에 그 빚을 두고 스스로를 괴롭힐 필요는 전혀 없었어요.\"\n\n그녀에게 던져진 모든 질문과 그녀의 모든 대답을 일일이 옮기지는 않겠다. 그저 그녀 증언의 요지만을 전하겠다.\n\n\"저는 그가 아버지에게서 돈을 받는 즉시 그 액수를 부치리라고 굳게 확신하고 있었습니다.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"저는 그의 무사무욕함과 정직함을… 돈 문제에 있어서의… 그 빈틈없는 정직함을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없습니다. 그는 아버지에게서 돈을 받으리라고 굳게 믿었고, 그것에 대해 제게 여러 번 이야기했어요. 저는 그가 아버지와 반목하고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그가 아버지에게 부당한 대우를 받아 왔다고 늘 믿어 왔습니다. 그가 아버지를 향해 어떤 위협을 입에 올린 것은 기억나지 않아요. 적어도 제 앞에서는 결코 그런 위협을 한 적이 없습니다. 만약 그가 그때 제게 찾아왔더라면, 저는 그 불운한 삼천 루블에 대한 그의 불안을 당장 덜어 주었을 거예요. 하지만 그는 저를 찾아오기를 그만두었고… 또 저 자신도 그를 부를 수 없는 그런 처지에 놓여 있었습니다…. 그리고 사실 저는 그 돈에 대해 까다롭게 굴 권리도 없었어요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 덧붙였고, 그 목소리에는 결연한 울림이 있었다. \"저는 한때 삼천 루블이 넘는 돈을 그에게서 도움받아 빚진 적이 있고, 그때는 제가 그 빚을 갚을 처지가 되리라고 미처 내다볼 수 없었음에도 그 돈을 받았으니까요.\"\n\n그녀의 목소리에는 도전적인 가락이 묻어났다. 바로 그때 페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다.\n\n\"그것은 이곳에서가 아니라, 두 분이 알게 된 초기에 있었던 일입니까?\" 페튜코비치가 무언가 유리한 것의 냄새를 단숨에 맡고는, 조심스럽게 길을 더듬듯 넌지시 물었다. 여기서 한마디 곁들여 두어야 할 것은, 페튜코비치가 일부는 카테리나 이바노브나 자신의 청에 따라 페테르부르크에서 불려 온 것이었음에도, 미탸가 그녀에게 준 사천 루블의 일화와 그녀가 \"그에게 땅에 닿도록 절을 한\" 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고 있었다는 사실이다. 그녀는 그것을 그에게 숨기고 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 이는 기이한 일이었다. 그녀 자신도 마지막 순간까지 법정에서 그 일화를 말할지 말지 알지 못한 채, 그 순간의 영감을 기다리고 있었던 것이라고 거의 확실하게 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n아니, 나는 그 순간들을 결코 잊을 수 없다. 그녀는 자기 이야기를 시작했다. 미탸가 알료샤에게 들려주었던 그 일화 전부를, 자기가 땅에 닿도록 절한 일과 그 이유까지 죄다 털어놓았다. 그녀는 자기 아버지에 관해, 그리고 자기가 미탸에게 찾아간 일에 관해 이야기했으나, 미탸가 자기 누이를 통해 돈을 가지러 \"카테리나 이바노브나를 보내자\"고 직접 제안했다는 것은 단 한마디로도, 단 한 가닥 암시로도 비치지 않았다. 그녀는 너그럽게 그것을 감추었고, 마치 자기가 무언가를 믿고서 제 충동에 따라 그 젊은 장교에게 달려가… 돈을 구걸한 것처럼 보이게 하면서도 부끄러워하지 않았다. 그것은 실로 엄청난 일이었다! 나는 그 이야기를 들으며 온몸이 싸늘해지고 떨려 왔다. 법정은 한마디 한마디를 놓치지 않으려 숨을 죽였다. 그것은 전례 없는 일이었다. 그녀처럼 제멋대로이고 경멸하듯 오만한 처녀에게서조차, 그토록 극도로 솔직한 고백, 그토록 큰 희생, 그토록 큰 자기 헌신은 도무지 믿기지 않는 것이었다. 그리고 무엇을 위해, 누구를 위해? 자기를 속이고 모욕한 사내를 구하기 위해, 그리고 그에게 유리한 강렬한 인상을 자아내어, 단 한 점이나마 그를 구하는 데 보탬이 되기 위해서였다. 그리고 실로, 순결한 처녀에게 공손히 절을 하며 자기 수중의 마지막 사천 루블을—세상에 가진 전부를—건넨 그 젊은 장교의 모습은 무척 동정 어리고 매혹적인 빛 속에 던져졌으나, 그러나… 내 마음에는 괴로운 불안이 자리했다! 나는 훗날 이것에서 중상이 빚어질 수 있으리라는 것을 느꼈다(그리고 실제로 그렇게 되었다, 정말로). 훗날 온 고을에 심술궂은 웃음과 함께 되풀이된 이야기는, 그 사연이 어쩌면 그리 완전하지 않으리라는 것—즉 장교가 그 젊은 아가씨를 \"공손한 절 하나만으로\" 돌려보냈다는 진술 말이다. 여기에는 무언가 빠진 것이 있다는 암시가 떠돌았다.\n\n\"설령 빠진 것이 없다 한들, 이것이 사연의 전부라 한들,\" 우리 고을에서 가장 존경받는 부인이 주장했다. \"그렇더라도 젊은 처녀가 제 아비를 구하기 위해서라지만 그런 식으로 처신하는 것이 과연 떳떳한 일이었는지는 매우 의심스럽지요.\"\n\n그러니 카테리나 이바노브나가, 그 명석함과 그 병적인 예민함을 지닌 그녀가, 사람들이 이렇게 수군대리라는 것을 헤아리지 못했을 리가 있겠는가? 그녀는 분명 그것을 헤아렸으면서도 모든 것을 털어놓기로 결심한 것이다. 물론 그녀 이야기의 진실성을 둘러싼 이 모든 추잡하고 자질구레한 의혹은 죄다 훗날에야 일어난 것이고, 첫 순간에는 모두가 깊이 감동했다. 판사들과 변호인들로 말하자면, 그들은 경건하게, 거의 면구스러운 침묵 속에서 카테리나 이바노브나의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 검사는 그 일에 대해 단 하나의 질문도 감히 꺼내지 못했다. 페튜코비치는 그녀에게 깊이 고개를 숙여 절했다. 오, 그는 거의 승리감에 차 있었다! 많은 발판이 마련된 것이었다. 한 사내가 너그러운 충동에 휩쓸려 자기 수중의 마지막 사천 루블을 내주었던 그 사내가, 삼천 루블을 빼앗으려고 제 아버지를 죽인다는 것은—그 발상부터가 너무도 앞뒤가 맞지 않게 여겨졌다. 페튜코비치는 이제 적어도 절도의 혐의만큼은 사실상 반증된 거나 다름없다고 느꼈다. \"사건\"은 사뭇 다른 빛 속으로 던져졌다. 미탸에 대한 동정의 물결이 일었다. 그 자신으로 말하자면…. 카테리나 이바노브나가 증언하는 동안 그가 한두 번 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다가 다시 주저앉으며 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다고 나는 전해 들었다. 그러나 그녀가 말을 마치자, 그는 별안간 흐느끼는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"카탸, 어째서 당신은 나를 파멸시킨 거요?\" 그의 흐느낌은 법정 전체에 들렸다. 그러나 그는 이내 자제하더니 다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"이제 나는 끝장이오!\"\n\n그러고는 이를 악물고 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 얹은 채 제자리에 굳은 듯 앉아 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 법정에 남아 자기 자리에 앉았다. 그녀는 창백한 낯빛으로 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있었다. 그녀 가까이 앉아 있던 사람들은, 그녀가 한참 동안 마치 열병이라도 앓듯 온몸을 떨었다고 전했다. 그루셴카가 불려 나왔다.\n\n나는 어쩌면 미탸의 파멸을 결정지은 최종 원인이었을지도 모를 그 돌연한 파국에 다가가고 있다. 나는—아니 모두가—그 일화가 일어나지 않았더라면 피고는 적어도 선처 권고는 받았으리라고 확신하고 있으며, 훗날 변호사들도 모두 같은 말을 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하고, 먼저 그루셴카에 관해 몇 마디 해 두겠다.\n\n그녀 또한 온통 검은 옷차림에, 어깨에는 그 화려한 검은 숄을 두르고 있었다. 그녀는 매끄럽고 소리 없는 걸음걸이로, 풍만한 몸매의 여인들에게 흔한 살짝 흔들리는 듯한 자태로 증언대를 향해 걸어갔다. 그녀는 시선을 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 돌리지 않고 재판장을 똑바로 바라보았다. 내가 보기에 그녀는 그 순간 무척 아름다워 보였고, 훗날 부인들이 주장한 것처럼 결코 창백하지 않았다. 그들은 또한 그녀의 표정이 굳어 있고 심술궂었다고도 말했다. 나는 그녀가 그저 짜증이 났을 뿐이며, 추문이나 좋아하는 우리 방청객들의 경멸 어리고 캐묻는 듯한 시선을 괴롭게 의식하고 있었던 거라고 믿는다. 그녀는 자존심이 강했고 경멸을 견디지 못했다. 그녀는 경멸의 기미만 비쳐도 발끈 성을 내며 앙갚음하려 드는 그런 부류의 사람이었다. 물론 거기에는 소심함의 요소도, 그리고 자기 소심함에 대한 내심의 부끄러움도 있었으니, 그녀의 어조가 자꾸 바뀐 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다. 어느 순간에는 성나고 경멸에 차고 거칠다가도, 또 어느 순간에는 진심 어린 자책의 가락이 묻어났다. 때로 그녀는 마치 필사적으로 몸을 던지는 것처럼, 마치 \"무슨 일이 벌어지든 상관없어, 말해 버리겠어…\" 하고 느끼기라도 하는 것처럼 말했다. 표도르 파블로비치와 알게 된 사정에 관해서는 그녀가 퉁명스럽게 한마디 했다. \"그건 다 헛소리예요, 그 사람이 절 귀찮게 따라다닌 게 내 잘못이었나요?\" 그러나 잠시 뒤 그녀는 덧붙였다. \"다 제 잘못이었어요. 제가 그 둘 다를—그 영감이며 그 사람이며—다 가지고 놀았고, 그래서 둘 다 이 지경으로 끌고 온 거예요. 다 저 때문에 일어난 일이에요.\"\n\n어쩌다 삼소노프의 이름이 거론되었다. \"그건 누구도 상관할 일이 아니에요.\" 그녀가 일종의 뻔뻔스러운 반항조로 당장 쏘아붙였다. \"그분은 제 은인이세요. 제 식구들이 절 내쫓아 발에 신을 신발조차 없을 때 절 거두어 준 분이라고요.\" 재판장이, 비록 매우 정중하게나마, 그녀에게 곁가지 같은 자질구레한 이야기로 빠지지 말고 질문에 곧이곧대로 답해야 한다고 일러 주었다. 그루셴카는 새빨개졌고 두 눈이 번뜩였다.\n\n지폐가 든 봉투를 그녀는 본 적이 없으며, 다만 \"그 사악한 놈\"에게서 표도르 파블로비치가 삼천 루블어치 지폐가 든 봉투를 가지고 있다는 말을 들었을 뿐이라고 했다. \"하지만 그건 다 어리석은 짓이었어요. 저는 그저 비웃고 있었을 뿐, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테는 가지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"'그 사악한 놈'이라고 하셨는데 누구를 가리키는 겁니까?\" 검사가 물었다.\n\n\"하인이던 스메르댜코프죠, 제 주인을 죽이고 어젯밤 목매달아 죽은 그놈요.\"\n\n물론 그녀는 곧 그토록 단정적인 고발을 하는 무슨 근거가 있느냐는 질문을 받았으나, 그녀에게도 역시 아무런 근거가 없는 것으로 드러났다.\n\n\"드미트리 표도로비치가 제게 직접 그렇게 말했어요. 그 사람 말을 믿으셔도 됩니다. 우리 사이에 끼어든 그 여자가 그 사람을 파멸시킨 거예요. 그 여자가 이 모든 일의 원흉이라고요, 똑똑히 말씀드리지요.\" 그루셴카가 덧붙였다. 그녀는 증오로 부르르 떨고 있는 듯했고, 그 목소리에는 앙심에 찬 가락이 묻어 있었다.\n\n그녀는 누구를 가리키는 것이냐는 질문을 다시 받았다.\n\n\"저기 저 아가씨, 카테리나 이바노브나요. 그 여자가 절 불러다 초콜릿을 권하며 절 호리려고 들었어요. 그 여자한테 진짜 수치심 같은 건 별로 없다고요, 제가 똑똑히 말씀드리지요….\"\n\n이 대목에서 재판장이 그녀를 준엄하게 제지하며 말을 삼가 달라고 청했다. 그러나 질투에 사로잡힌 여자의 가슴은 활활 타오르고 있었으니, 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하든 개의치 않았다.\n\n\"피고가 모크로예에서 체포될 때,\" 검사가 물었다. \"모두가 당신이 옆방에서 뛰쳐나와 '이건 다 제 잘못이에요. 우리 함께 시베리아로 가요!' 하고 외치는 것을 보고 들었습니다. 그렇다면 당신은 이미 그가 제 아버지를 죽였다고 믿고 있었던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그때 제가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 기억나지 않아요.\" 그루셴카가 대답했다. \"다들 그가 제 아버지를 죽였다고 소리쳐 대고 있었고, 저는 그게 제 잘못이며 저 때문에 그가 그를 죽인 거라고 느꼈어요. 하지만 그가 자기는 무죄라고 말하자, 저는 곧장 그를 믿었고, 지금도 믿으며, 앞으로도 늘 믿을 거예요. 그 사람은 거짓말할 사람이 아니에요.\"\n\n페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다. 그가 여러 가지 중에서도 라키틴에 관해, 그리고 \"당신이 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프를 데려와 만나게 해 준 대가로 그에게 치른\" 이십오 루블에 관해 물었던 것을 나는 기억한다.\n\n\"그 사람이 돈을 받은 게 뭐 이상할 거 있나요.\" 그루셴카가 성난 경멸을 담아 비웃었다. \"그 사람은 늘 제게 돈을 받으러 왔어요. 한 달에 적어도 삼십 루블씩은 제게서 뜯어 갔는데, 주로 사치품에 쓰려는 거였죠. 제 도움 없이도 제 앞가림은 할 만한 사람이었어요.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 당신은 라키틴 씨에게 그토록 후하게 굴었습니까?\" 페튜코비치가, 재판장이 불안한 듯 몸을 움직이는데도 아랑곳없이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 그 사람은 제 사촌인걸요. 그 사람 어머니가 제 어머니의 동생이거든요. 그런데도 그 사람은 자길 너무도 부끄러워하는 통에, 여기 누구한테도 그 얘길 하지 말아 달라고 늘 제게 간청했어요.\"\n\n이 사실은 모두에게 완전히 뜻밖이었다. 고을에서도 수도원에서도, 심지어 미탸조차 그것을 아는 사람이 없었다. 라키틴이 자기 자리에서 부끄러움에 시뻘게졌다고 나는 전해 들었다. 그루셴카는 법정에 들어오기 전에 어떻게 들어 알고 있었으니, 그가 미탸에게 불리한 증언을 했다는 것을, 그래서 그녀는 성이 나 있었던 것이다. 라키틴의 연설이며 그 고결한 정서며 농노제와 러시아 정치의 무질서를 향한 그의 공격이 방청객에게 미친 효과는, 이번에야말로 결정적으로 망가지고 말았다. 페튜코비치는 흡족했다. 그것은 또 하나의 하늘이 내린 선물이었다. 그루셴카에 대한 반대신문은 오래 이어지지 않았고, 물론 그녀의 증언에 특별히 새로울 것은 있을 수 없었다. 그녀는 방청객에게 매우 불쾌한 인상을 남겼다. 그녀가 증언을 마치고 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 한참 떨어진 곳, 법정의 자기 자리에 다시 앉을 때, 수백의 경멸 어린 눈길이 그녀에게 쏟아졌다. 미탸는 그녀의 증언 내내 입을 다물고 있었다. 그는 마치 돌처럼 굳어 두 눈을 땅바닥에 못 박은 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n이반이 증언하라고 불려 나왔다.\n\n# Chapter V. A Sudden Catastrophe\n\n한 가지 덧붙여 둘 것은, 그가 알료샤보다 먼저 호명되었었다는 점이다. 그러나 법정의 정리(廷吏)가 재판장에게 알리기를, 그 증인은 병이 도졌거나 무슨 발작 같은 것 때문에 당장은 출두할 수 없으나 회복되는 대로 곧 진술할 채비가 되어 있다고 했다. 그런데 아무도 그 말을 듣지 못한 듯했고, 그것은 한참 뒤에야 비로소 드러났다.\n\n그가 들어선 첫 순간에는 거의 아무도 그를 알아차리지 못했다. 주요 증인들, 특히 두 연적(戀敵)인 부인들은 이미 신문을 받은 뒤였다. 호기심은 당분간 충족되었고, 방청객들은 거의 지쳐 있는 기색이었다. 아직 몇몇 증인이 더 진술해야 했으나, 이미 나온 이야기들에 비추어 보면 그들이 새로 보탤 정보는 아마 별로 없을 듯했다. 시간이 흘러갔다. 이반은 누구도 쳐다보지 않은 채 유난히 느릿느릿 걸어 나왔는데, 고개를 숙이고 있어 마치 음울한 생각에 잠긴 사람 같았다. 그는 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입었으나, 그 얼굴은 적어도 내게는 고통스러운 인상을 주었으니, 그 얼굴에는 흙빛이, 죽어 가는 사람 같은 빛이 어려 있었다. 그의 두 눈은 광채가 없었는데, 그가 그것을 들어 천천히 법정을 둘러보았다. 알료샤가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 \"아!\" 하고 신음하던 것을 나는 기억하지만, 그것을 알아챈 이는 거의 없었다.\n\n재판장은 먼저 그에게 알리기를, 그는 선서하지 않은 증인이므로 답할 수도 답하기를 거부할 수도 있으나, 물론 자기 양심에 따라 증언해야 한다는 등등을 일러 주었다. 이반은 그 말을 들으며 멍하니 그를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 차츰 풀리며 미소가 번졌고, 재판장이 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말을 맺자마자 그는 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"자, 그래서, 또 뭡니까?\" 그가 큰 소리로 물었다.\n\n법정 안에 정적이 흘렀고, 무언가 기이한 기운이 감돌았다. 재판장이 불안한 기색을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"증인은…… 아직 몸이 편치 않으신 모양이오?\" 그가 정리를 사방으로 찾으며 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"염려 마십시오, 재판장님, 저는 충분히 멀쩡하고 흥미로운 얘기 하나를 들려드릴 수 있습니다.\" 이반이 돌연 차분하고 공손하게 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 특별히 전할 말씀이라도 있는 것이오?\" 재판장이 여전히 미심쩍어하며 말을 이었다.\n\n이반은 눈을 내리뜨고 몇 초간 뜸을 들이더니, 고개를 들어 거의 더듬거리듯 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요…… 없습니다. 특별한 건 아무것도 없습니다.\"\n\n사람들이 그에게 질문을 던지기 시작했다. 그는 마지못한 듯, 극도로 짧게, 점점 더 뚜렷해지는 일종의 혐오를 담아 대답했는데, 그래도 답변만큼은 조리가 있었다. 여러 질문에 그는 모른다고 답했다. 아버지와 드미트리 사이의 금전 관계에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다. \"저는 그 문제에 관심이 없었습니다.\" 그가 덧붙였다. 아버지를 죽이겠다는 위협이라면 피고에게서 들은 적이 있었다. 봉투 속의 돈에 관해서는 스메르댜코프에게서 들었다.\n\n\"똑같은 얘기를 자꾸 되풀이하는군요.\" 그가 돌연 지친 기색으로 말을 끊었다. \"법정에 특별히 들려드릴 얘기는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"증인이 편치 않은 줄 알겠고, 그 심정도 이해하오.\" 재판장이 입을 열었다.\n\n그는 검사와 변호인 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 필요하다면 증인을 신문하라고 권하려 했는데, 그때 이반이 돌연 기진맥진한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"보내 주십시오, 재판장님, 몸이 몹시 좋지 않습니다.\"\n\n그리고 이 말과 함께 그는 허락을 기다리지도 않고 법정을 나가려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그러나 네 걸음쯤 떼고는 마치 결심이라도 선 듯 멈춰 서더니, 천천히 미소 짓고는 도로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"저는 그 시골 처녀와 같습니다, 재판장님…… 아시지요. 그게 어떻게 되더라? '내키면 일어나고, 안 내키면 안 일어나겠어요.' 사람들이 그 처녀에게 사라판을 입혀 교회로 데려가 혼인시키려 하니까 그 애가 말했다지요, '내키면 일어나고, 안 내키면 안 일어나겠어요.'…… 농민들에 관한 무슨 책에 나오는 얘깁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 재판장이 준엄하게 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐면, 이런 겁니다.\" 이반이 돌연 지폐 뭉치 하나를 꺼냈다. \"여기 돈이 있습니다…… 그 봉투 안에 들어 있던 지폐들입니다.\" (그는 물증이 놓인 탁자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다.) \"우리 아버지가 이것 때문에 살해당한 그 돈입니다. 어디에 둘까요? 정리 양반, 받으시오.\"\n\n법정의 정리가 그 뭉치를 통째로 받아 재판장에게 건넸다.\n\n\"만일 이것이 그 돈이라면, 어떻게 이 돈이 증인의 수중에 들어올 수 있었소?\" 재판장이 의아해하며 물었다.\n\n\"어제 스메르댜코프에게서, 그 살인범에게서 받았습니다…… 그가 목을 매기 직전에 그와 함께 있었지요. 우리 아버지를 죽인 건 그놈입니다, 제 형이 아니라. 그놈이 죽였고, 제가 그러도록 부추겼습니다…… 누군들 제 아비가 죽기를 바라지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"증인은 제정신이오?\" 재판장의 입에서 절로 튀어나왔다.\n\n\"제정신이고말고요…… 여러분 모두와 똑같이 추악한 정신이지요…… 이 모든…… 추한 얼굴들과 똑같이.\" 그가 돌연 방청석을 향해 돌아섰다. \"내 아버지가 살해당했는데, 저들은 끔찍해하는 척이나 하고 있어.\" 그가 사나운 경멸을 담아 으르렁거렸다. \"저들은 서로서로 거짓 시늉을 계속하고 있지. 거짓말쟁이들! 저들 모두 제 아비가 죽기를 바라고 있어. 한 마리 파충류가 다른 파충류를 삼키는 거야…… 살인이 없었더라면 저들은 화가 나서 언짢은 기분으로 집에 돌아갔을걸. 저들이 원하는 건 구경거리야! 빵과 서커스(Panem et circenses) 말이지. 하기야 내가 누굴 탓하겠나마는! 물 있습니까? 제발, 그리스도의 이름으로 마실 것 좀 주시오!\" 그가 돌연 제 머리를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n정리가 곧장 그에게 다가갔다. 알료샤가 벌떡 일어나 소리쳤다. \"저 사람은 병에 걸렸습니다. 믿지 마십시오, 뇌염을 앓고 있습니다.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 충동적으로 자리에서 일어나, 공포로 굳어진 채 이반을 응시했다. 미탸가 일어서서 형을 게걸스레 바라보며, 거칠고 기이한 미소를 띤 채 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"동요들 마십시오. 나는 미친 게 아니라, 그저 살인자일 뿐이오.\" 이반이 다시 입을 열었다. \"살인자에게 웅변을 기대할 수야 없지요.\" 그가 무슨 까닭에선지 돌연 덧붙이고는 기묘한 웃음을 웃었다.\n\n검사가 명백히 당황한 기색으로 재판장에게 몸을 굽혔다. 다른 두 판사가 흥분한 속삭임으로 의견을 주고받았다. 페튜코비치는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었으며, 법정은 기대 속에 숨을 죽였다. 재판장이 돌연 정신을 차린 듯했다.\n\n\"증인, 증인의 말은 여기서 이해할 수도 없고 있을 수도 없는 것이오. 할 수 있다면 진정하고, 정말 할 말이 있거든…… 증인의 이야기를 해 보시오. 어떻게 증인의 진술을 입증할 수 있소…… 만일 정말로 헛소리를 하는 게 아니라면 말이오?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 문제입니다. 저는 증거가 없습니다. 그 개 같은 스메르댜코프가 저세상에서…… 봉투에 담아 증거를 부쳐 줄 리는 없으니까요. 여러분은 봉투 생각밖에 안 하시는군요—하나로 충분한데. 저는 증인이 없습니다…… 하나라면 모를까, 아마.\" 그가 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"증인이 누구요?\"\n\n\"그놈은 꼬리가 달렸습니다, 재판장님, 그러니 격식에 맞질 않겠지요! 악마는 존재하지 않는다(Le diable n'existe point)! 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 그놈은 하찮고 가련한 악마입니다.\" 그가 돌연 덧붙였다. 그는 웃음을 그치고, 마치 은밀히 털어놓듯 말했다. \"그놈은 분명 여기 어딘가에 있습니다—저 물증이 놓인 탁자 밑쯤일지도 모르지요. 거기가 아니면 어디 앉아 있겠습니까? 자, 들어 보십시오. 제가 그놈에게 잠자코 있고 싶지 않다고 말했더니, 그놈은 지질학적 격변 얘기를 늘어놓더군요…… 멍청한 소리! 자, 저 괴물을 풀어 주십시오…… 그놈이 찬송가를 부르고 있었거든요. 제 마음이 가벼워서 그런 겁니다! 마치 길거리의 술 취한 사내가 '반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났다네' 하고 떠들어대는 것 같은데, 나라면 단 이 초의 기쁨을 위해 천조 곱하기 천조라도 내놓겠소. 여러분은 나를 모릅니다! 아, 이 모든 일이 어찌 이리 멍청한지! 자, 그놈 대신 나를 잡아가시오! 나는 괜히 온 게 아니란 말이오…… 어째서, 어째서 모든 게 이토록 멍청한 거요?……\"\n\n그러고서 그는 다시 주위를 둘러보며 느릿느릿, 마치 골똘히 생각하듯 말하기 시작했다. 그러나 이제 법정은 온통 술렁이고 있었다. 알료샤가 그에게 달려갔으나, 법정의 정리가 이미 이반의 팔을 붙든 뒤였다.\n\n\"무슨 짓이오?\" 그가 정리의 얼굴을 노려보며 소리치더니, 돌연 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐고는 바닥에 거칠게 내동댕이쳤다. 그러나 경관들이 곧장 달려들어 그를 제압했다. 그는 미친 듯이 비명을 질러댔다. 그리고 끌려 나가는 내내 그는 무언가 알아들을 수 없는 말을 고래고래 외쳐댔다.\n\n법정 전체가 아수라장이 되었다. 나는 그 일이 일어난 그대로 모두를 기억하지는 못한다. 나 자신도 흥분해 있어 미처 따라가지 못했다. 다만 내가 아는 것은, 그 뒤 모든 것이 다시 잠잠해지고 무슨 일이 있었는지 다들 알게 되었을 때, 법정의 정리가 견책을 받았다는 사실이다. 비록 그는 매우 조리 있게 해명하기를, 그 증인은 멀쩡했고 한 시간 전에 의사가 그를 진찰했을 때 가벼운 현기증 발작이 있었을 뿐이며, 법정에 들어오기 전까지는 아주 또박또박 말을 했으므로 무슨 일이 벌어질지 예견할 도리가 없었다고, 게다가 그가 한사코 증언하겠노라 고집했다고 했지만 말이다. 그러나 모두가 평정을 완전히 되찾고 이 광경에서 벗어나기도 전에, 또 다른 광경이 뒤따랐다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 히스테리 발작을 일으킨 것이다. 그녀는 크게 비명을 지르며 흐느꼈으나, 법정을 나가기를 거부한 채 몸부림치며 자기를 끌어내지 말아 달라고 애원했다. 그러더니 돌연 재판장을 향해 소리쳤다.\n\n\"제가 당장 더 드릴 증언이 있습니다…… 당장이요! 여기 문서가, 편지가 있습니다…… 받으세요, 어서, 어서 읽어 보세요! 저 괴물이 보낸 편지예요…… 저기, 저기 있는 저 남자가!\" 그녀가 미탸를 가리켰다. \"저 사람이 제 아비를 죽인 거예요, 곧 아시게 될 거예요. 어떻게 제 아비를 죽일지 저한테 써 보냈다고요! 하지만 저 다른 사람은 병에 걸렸어요, 병에 걸려서, 헛소리를 하는 거예요!\" 그녀가 제정신이 아닌 채로 거듭 외쳐댔다.\n\n법정의 정리가 그녀가 내민 문서를 받아 재판장에게 건넸고, 그녀는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고는, 온몸을 떨며 발작적으로 소리 없이 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 법정에서 쫓겨날까 두려워 모든 소리를 억눌렀다. 그녀가 건넨 문서는 미탸가 '수도(首都)' 선술집에서 쓴, 이반이 \"수학적 증거\"라 일컬었던 바로 그 편지였다. 아아! 그 수학적 확실성은 인정되고 말았으니, 그 편지만 없었던들 미탸는 어쩌면 파멸을 면했거나, 적어도 그 파멸이 덜 끔찍했을지도 모른다. 거듭 말하거니와, 모든 세부를 일일이 알아채기란 어려웠다. 그 뒤에 벌어진 일은 지금도 내 머릿속에서 어수선하기만 하다. 짐작건대 재판장은 그 문서를 곧장 판사들과 배심원, 그리고 양쪽 변호인에게 넘겼을 것이다. 나는 다만 그들이 증인을 신문하기 시작하던 광경만 기억한다. 재판장이 충분히 진정되었느냐고 부드럽게 묻자, 카테리나 이바노브나는 격하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"준비됐어요, 준비됐다고요! 충분히 답할 수 있어요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였는데, 어쩐지 자신이 증언을 못 하도록 방해받을까 봐 여전히 두려워하는 기색이 역력했다. 사람들이 그녀에게 이 편지가 무엇이며 어떤 정황에서 받게 되었는지 자세히 설명하라고 청했다.\n\n\"범행이 저질러지기 전날 받았지만, 그가 쓴 건 그보다 하루 전, 그 선술집에서였어요—그러니까 범행 이틀 전이지요. 보세요, 무슨 계산서 같은 종이에 쓰여 있어요!\" 그녀가 숨가쁘게 외쳤다. \"그 무렵 그 사람은 저를 미워했어요, 자기가 비열하게 굴면서 그 계집 꽁무니를 쫓아다니고 있었으니까요…… 게다가 저한테 그 삼천 루블을 빚지고 있었으니까요…… 아! 그 사람은 제 비열함 탓에 그 삼천 루블 때문에 굴욕을 느꼈던 거예요! 그 삼천 루블 일은 이렇게 된 거예요. 부탁이에요, 간청하건대, 제 말을 들어 주세요. 그가 제 아비를 죽이기 삼 주 전 어느 날 아침, 그가 저를 찾아왔어요. 저는 그가 돈이 궁한 걸, 또 그 돈을 어디에 쓰려는지를 알고 있었어요. 그래요, 그래요—그 계집을 차지해 데리고 달아나려는 거였지요. 저는 그때 이미 그가 저를 배신했고 저를 버릴 작정인 걸 알고 있었는데, 그런데도 저는, 바로 제가 그에게 그 돈을 줬어요, 모스크바에 있는 제 언니에게 부쳐 달라는 핑계로 그 돈을 내밀었던 거예요. 그러고서 돈을 건네면서 저는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 보며 말했어요, 내키는 대로 아무 때나 보내면 된다고, '한 달 뒤라도 괜찮다'고요. 어떻게, 어떻게 그가 모를 수가 있었겠어요, 제가 사실상 그의 면전에 대고 이렇게 말하고 있었다는 걸요, '당신은 그 계집과 함께 나를 배신하는 데 쓸 돈이 필요하지, 그러니 여기 돈이 있어. 내가 직접 주는 거야. 받을 만큼 염치가 없거든 받아!' 저는 그가 어떤 사람인지 증명하고 싶었던 거예요, 그런데 어떻게 됐을까요? 그는 받았어요, 받아서, 그 계집과 함께 하룻밤 새 탕진해 버렸어요…… 하지만 그는 알았어요, 제가 그 모든 걸 안다는 걸 알고 있었어요. 단언컨대 그도 알았어요, 제가 그를 시험하려고, 저한테서 돈을 받을 만큼 염치를 깡그리 잃은 사람인지 보려고 그 돈을 줬다는 걸요. 저는 그의 눈을 들여다봤고 그도 제 눈을 들여다봤는데, 그는 그 모든 걸 알면서도 받아 갔어요—제 돈을 가지고 가 버린 거예요!\"\n\n\"맞소, 카탸.\" 미탸가 돌연 우렁차게 외쳤다. \"나는 당신 눈을 들여다봤고, 당신이 나를 모욕하고 있다는 걸 알면서도, 그래도 당신 돈을 받았소. 나를 비열한 놈이라고 경멸하시오, 경멸해, 여러분 모두! 나는 그래 마땅하오!\"\n\n\"피고,\" 재판장이 외쳤다. \"한마디만 더 하면 퇴정시키겠소.\"\n\n\"그 돈은 그에게 고통이었어요.\" 카탸가 충동적으로 다급하게 말을 이었다. \"그는 저한테 그 돈을 갚고 싶어 했어요. 갚고 싶어 했지요, 그건 사실이에요. 하지만 그 계집한테도 돈이 필요했으니까요. 그래서 그는 제 아비를 죽였지만, 저한테는 갚지 않고 그 여자와 함께 그가 체포된 그 마을로 떠나 버렸어요. 거기서 또 그는 제 아비를 죽인 뒤 훔친 돈을 탕진했지요. 그리고 살인 하루 전에 그는 저한테 이 편지를 썼어요. 그걸 쓸 때 그는 취해 있었어요. 저는 그때 당장 알아챘지요. 그는 앙심에서 그걸 썼고, 제가 그걸 절대로 누구에게도 보이지 않으리라고 확신하면서, 설령 그가 정말 아비를 죽이더라도 그러리라고 단단히 믿으면서 썼던 거예요, 안 그랬으면 쓰지도 않았겠지요. 제가 복수를 하려 들거나 그를 파멸시키려 들지 않으리란 걸 알았으니까요! 하지만 읽어 보세요, 주의 깊게 읽어 보세요—더 주의 깊게, 부디—그러면 그가 편지에 모든 걸 미리, 어떻게 제 아비를 죽일지, 또 그 돈이 어디 숨겨져 있는지를 다 써 놓은 걸 아시게 될 거예요. 보세요, 부디 그 대목을 놓치지 마세요, 거기 이런 구절이 있어요, '이반이 떠나는 즉시 나는 그를 죽일 것이다.' 그러니까 그는 어떻게 죽일지를 모두 미리 궁리해 놓았던 거예요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 독기 어린 악의에 찬 승리감으로 법정을 향해 지적했다. 아아! 그녀가 그 편지의 한 줄 한 줄을 샅샅이 연구해 그 행간에 깔린 의미를 죄다 간파했음이 분명했다. \"취하지만 않았던들 그는 저한테 쓰지 않았을 거예요. 하지만 보세요, 모든 게 거기 미리 쓰여 있어요, 나중에 그가 살인을 저지른 그대로 말예요. 완벽한 계획서라고요!\" 그녀가 미친 듯이 외쳤다.\n\n이제 그녀는 자신에게 닥칠 온갖 결과에 아랑곳하지 않았으나, 의심할 바 없이 그녀는 한 달 전에 이미 그 결과를 내다보았을 것이니, 그때에도 어쩌면 분노에 떨면서 이것을 재판에서 내놓을지 말지를 곰곰이 헤아렸을 것이다. 이제 그녀는 그 치명적인 도약을 감행한 것이었다. 내 기억으로 그 편지는 직후에 곧바로 서기에 의해 낭독되었던 듯하다. 그것은 압도적인 인상을 주었다. 사람들이 미탸에게 이 편지를 쓴 것을 인정하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"제 거예요, 제 거!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"취하지만 않았던들 쓰지 않았을 텐데!…… 우리는 여러 가지로 서로를 미워했지, 카탸, 하지만 맹세코, 맹세코 나는 당신을 미워하면서도 사랑했소, 그런데 당신은 나를 사랑하지 않았소!\"\n\n그는 절망에 빠져 두 손을 비틀며 자리에 풀썩 주저앉았다. 검사와 변호인이 그녀를 반대신문하기 시작했는데, 주로 무엇이 그녀로 하여금 그런 문서를 숨기고 또 조금 전까지 전혀 다른 어조와 정신으로 증언하게 만들었는지를 알아내려는 것이었다.\n\n\"네, 네. 저는 방금 거짓말을 하고 있었어요. 제 명예와 양심을 저버리고 거짓을 말했어요, 하지만 그를 구하고 싶었어요, 그가 저를 그토록 미워하고 경멸했으니까요!\" 카탸가 미친 듯이 외쳤다. \"아, 그는 저를 끔찍하게 경멸했어요, 늘 저를 경멸했지요, 그리고 아세요, 제가 그 돈 때문에 그에게 머리를 조아린 바로 그 순간부터 저를 경멸했어요. 저는 봤어요…… 그때 당장 그걸 느꼈지만, 오랫동안 믿으려 하지 않았어요. 제가 그의 눈 속에서 얼마나 자주 그걸 읽었던지요, '그래도 네가 제 발로 왔잖아.' 아, 그는 몰랐어요, 제가 왜 그에게 달려갔는지 전혀 몰랐어요, 그는 비열함밖에는 아무것도 의심할 줄 모르고, 자기 잣대로 저를 판단했어요, 누구나 다 자기 같은 줄로 알았던 거예요!\" 카탸가 완전히 광란에 빠져 사납게 쉭쉭거렸다. \"그리고 그가 저와 결혼하려 한 건 오로지 제가 재산을 물려받았기 때문이에요, 그것 때문에, 그것 때문이라고요! 저는 늘 그것 때문이라고 의심했어요! 아, 그는 짐승이에요! 그는 제가 그때 그에게 갔다는 이유로 평생토록 그 앞에서 수치심에 떨리라고, 그리고 그 일로 자기는 저를 영원히 경멸할 권리가 있어 저보다 우월하리라고—언제나 그렇게 확신했어요, 그래서 저와 결혼하려 했던 거예요! 바로 그거예요, 모두 그래요! 저는 제 사랑으로 그를 정복하려 했어요—한이 없는 사랑으로요. 심지어 그의 불성실까지 용서하려고 애썼어요. 하지만 그는 아무것도, 아무것도 몰랐어요! 하기야 그가 어떻게 알았겠어요? 그는 괴물인걸요! 저는 그 편지를 바로 그 이튿날 저녁에야 받았어요. 선술집에서 제게 전해진 거예요—그런데 바로 그날 아침, 바로 그날 아침에 저는 그에게 모든 걸, 모든 걸 용서하고 싶었어요—그의 배신마저도요!\"\n\n물론 재판장과 검사는 그녀를 진정시키려 애썼다. 그들이 그녀의 히스테리를 이용해 그런 고백을 듣고 있는 것에 부끄러움을 느꼈으리라는 생각을 나는 떨칠 수가 없다. 그들이 그녀에게 \"당신에게 이것이 얼마나 괴로운지 우리도 압니다, 부디 우리도 당신의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있다고 믿으십시오\" 하는 둥, 이런저런 말을 하던 것을 나는 기억한다. 그러면서도 그들은 광란에 빠진 그 히스테리컬한 여자에게서 증언을 끌어냈다. 마침내 그녀는, 이런 극도의 흥분 상태에서 잠시나마 흔히 보이곤 하는 그 비상한 명료함으로, 이반이 지난 두 달 동안 자기 형인 \"그 괴물이자 살인자\"를 구하려다 어떻게 거의 실성할 지경에 이르렀는지를 묘사했다.\n\n\"그는 스스로를 괴롭혔어요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그는 늘 형의 죄를 줄여 보려 애쓰면서, 자기 역시 한 번도 아버지를 사랑한 적이 없으며 어쩌면 자기 스스로도 아버지의 죽음을 바랐을지 모른다고 제게 고백했어요. 아, 그는 여리디여린 양심을 지녔어요! 그는 제 양심에 스스로를 짓이겼어요! 그는 제게 모든 걸, 모든 걸 털어놨어요! 날마다 찾아와 유일한 벗에게 하듯 제게 이야기했지요. 저는 그의 유일한 벗이라는 영광을 누리고 있어요!\" 그녀가 돌연 일종의 반항기를 띠고 외쳤고, 그녀의 두 눈이 번득였다. \"그는 스메르댜코프를 두 번 찾아갔어요. 어느 날 그가 제게 와서 말했어요, '만일 내 형이 아니라 스메르댜코프가 그 살인을 저지른 거라면'(스메르댜코프가 했다는 소문이 사방에 떠돌고 있었으니까요), '어쩌면 나도 죄가 있는지 몰라, 스메르댜코프는 내가 아버지를 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 알았고 어쩌면 내가 아버지의 죽음을 바란다고 믿었을지도 모르니까.' 그래서 저는 그 편지를 꺼내 그에게 보여 줬어요. 그는 형이 저질렀다고 완전히 확신했고, 그것에 압도당했어요. 자기 친형이 친부 살해범이라는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 거예요! 불과 일주일 전에 저는 그것이 그를 병들게 하고 있는 걸 봤어요. 지난 며칠 동안 그는 제 앞에서 횡설수설했어요. 저는 그의 정신이 무너져 가는 걸 봤어요. 그는 헛소리를 하며 돌아다녔고, 거리에서 혼자 중얼거리는 게 사람들 눈에 띄기도 했어요. 모스크바에서 온 의사가, 제 청을 받고 그저께 그를 진찰하고는, 그가 뇌염 직전이라고 제게 말했어요—이게 다 그 사람 때문이에요, 이 괴물 때문이라고요! 그리고 어젯밤 그는 스메르댜코프가 죽었다는 걸 알게 됐어요! 그 충격이 어찌나 컸던지 그를 실성하게 만들었어요…… 이게 다 이 괴물 때문이에요, 다 이 괴물을 구하겠다고 한 일 때문이라고요!\"\n\n아, 물론 이런 토로, 이런 고백이란 평생에 단 한 번이나 가능한 것이니—이를테면 죽음의 시각, 단두대로 끌려가는 길 위에서 같은 때 말이다! 그러나 그것은 카탸의 기질에 들어 있는 것이었고, 그녀의 생애에서 바로 그런 순간이었다. 그녀는 아버지를 구하려고 어느 젊은 난봉꾼의 자비에 제 몸을 던졌던 바로 그 격정적인 카탸였고, 조금 전 자존심과 정결함 속에서 미탸의 운명을 조금이나마 누그러뜨려 보려는 바람으로 미탸의 너그러운 처신을 이야기하며 이 모든 사람들 앞에 자신과 처녀로서의 수치심을 희생했던 바로 그 카탸였다. 그리고 이제 다시 그녀는 자신을 희생했으나, 이번에는 다른 사람을 위해서였고, 어쩌면 바로 지금에야—어쩌면 바로 이 순간에야—그녀는 그 다른 사람이 자기에게 얼마나 소중한지를 느끼고 깨달은 것이었다! 그녀는 그를 위한 공포 속에서 자신을 희생했으니, 살인을 저지른 것이 형이 아니라 자기라는 그 고백으로 그가 스스로를 파멸시켰다고 돌연 단정하고는, 그를 구하려고, 그의 명예와 평판을 구하려고 자신을 희생한 것이었다!\n\n그런데도 한 가지 끔찍한 의혹이 떠올랐으니—미탸와의 지난 관계를 묘사하면서 그녀가 거짓을 말한 것은 아닐까?—그것이 문제였다. 아니, 미탸가 자기의 절을 두고 자기를 경멸했다고 외쳤을 때, 그녀는 고의로 그를 중상한 것이 아니었다! 그녀 자신이 그것을 믿고 있었다. 그녀는, 어쩌면 그 절을 한 순간부터 줄곧, 그때조차도 자기를 흠모하던 그 순박한 미탸가 자기를 비웃고 경멸하고 있다고 굳게 믿어 왔던 것이다. 그녀는 오로지 자존심에서, 상처 입은 자존심에서, 히스테리컬하고 \"갈가리 찢긴\" 사랑으로 그를 사랑해 온 것이었으니, 그 사랑은 사랑이라기보다는 복수에 더 가까웠다. 아! 어쩌면 그 갈가리 찢긴 사랑이 참된 사랑으로 자라났을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 카탸가 다른 무엇보다 바란 것이 바로 그것이었을지도 모르지만, 미탸의 불성실이 그녀의 마음을 밑바닥까지 후벼 놓았고, 그녀의 마음은 그를 용서할 수 없었다. 복수의 순간이 돌연 그녀에게 들이닥쳤고, 모욕당한 여인의 가슴속에 그토록 오래 그토록 고통스럽게 쌓여 온 모든 것이 별안간 예기치 못하게 한꺼번에 터져 나온 것이었다. 그녀는 미탸를 배신했으나, 자기 자신마저 배신했다. 그리고 자신의 감정을 남김없이 쏟아내자마자, 물론 그 긴장은 끝났고 그녀는 수치심에 압도되었다. 히스테리가 다시 시작되었으니, 그녀는 흐느끼며 비명을 지르고 바닥에 쓰러졌다. 사람들이 그녀를 들어 내갔다. 바로 그 순간 그루셴카가 통곡하며, 사람들이 미처 막을 새도 없이 미탸에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"미탸,\" 그녀가 통곡했다. \"당신의 그 독사가 당신을 파멸시켰어요! 자, 봤지요, 저 여자가 제 본색을 드러냈잖아요!\" 그녀가 분노에 떨며 판사들을 향해 외쳤다. 재판장의 신호에 따라 사람들이 그녀를 붙들어 법정에서 끌어내려 했다. 그녀는 응하지 않았다. 미탸에게로 되돌아가려고 몸부림치며 발버둥쳤다. 미탸가 비명을 지르며 그녀에게 가려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그는 제압당했다.\n\n그렇다, 구경거리를 보러 온 부인네들은 틀림없이 만족했을 것이니—그 볼거리는 참으로 다채로운 것이었다. 그러고서 모스크바의 그 의사가 등장하던 장면이 내 기억에 떠오른다. 짐작건대 재판장이 미리 정리를 보내 이반에게 의료 조치를 마련하도록 했던 모양이다. 의사는 법정에 알리기를, 그 환자는 위험한 뇌염 발작을 앓고 있으니 당장 옮겨야 한다고 했다. 검사와 변호인의 질문에 그는 답하기를, 그저께 그 환자가 제 발로 자기를 찾아왔으며, 그런 발작이 닥쳐오고 있다고 미리 경고해 주었으나 그가 보살핌을 받기를 마다했다고 했다. \"그는 분명 정상적인 정신 상태가 아니었습니다. 그는 깨어 있을 때 환영을 본다고, 거리에서 이미 죽은 여러 사람을 만난다고, 그리고 매일 저녁 사탄이 찾아온다고 제게 직접 말했습니다.\" 의사가 끝으로 말했다. 증언을 마치자 그 저명한 의사는 물러났다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 제출한 편지는 물증에 추가되었다. 얼마간 심의한 끝에 판사들은 재판을 계속 진행하고, 예기치 못한 두 증언(이반과 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언)을 모두 조서에 올리기로 결정했다.\n\n그러나 다른 증인들의 증언은 일일이 적지 않겠으니, 그들은 저마다 특유의 면면을 지니기는 했어도 그저 앞서 나온 이야기를 되풀이하고 확인했을 뿐이다. 거듭 말하거니와, 모든 것은 검사의 논고에 한데 모였고, 그것을 나는 곧 인용하려 한다. 다들 흥분해 있었고, 다들 방금의 파국에 감전된 듯했으며, 모두가 검사 측 논고와 변호 측 변론을 격렬한 조바심으로 기다리고 있었다. 페튜코비치는 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언에 명백히 동요한 기색이었다. 그러나 검사는 의기양양했다. 모든 증언이 끝나자 법정은 거의 한 시간가량 휴정되었다. 내 기억으로는 재판장이 자리로 돌아오고 우리 고장의 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 논고를 시작한 것이 막 여덟 시였을 것이다.\n\n# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor’s Speech. Sketches Of Character\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치는 신경의 떨림에 사로잡힌 채, 이마에 식은땀을 흘리며, 온몸이 번갈아 달아올랐다 식었다 하는 것을 느끼며 논고를 시작했다. 그는 훗날 이것을 스스로 그렇게 묘사했다. 그는 이 논고를 자신의 걸작, 평생을 통틀어 가장 뛰어난 걸작이자 자신의 백조의 노래로 여겼다. 사실 그는 아홉 달 뒤 급성 폐결핵으로 세상을 떠났으니, 결국에 가서는 자신을 마지막 노래를 부르는 백조에 견줄 권리가 있었던 셈이다. 그는 그 논고에 자신의 온 마음과 가진 두뇌를 죄다 쏟아부었다. 그리고 가엾은 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 적어도 공공의 안녕과 \"영원한 문제\"에 대한 어떤 감정이 자기 안에 감춰져 있었음을 뜻밖에도 드러냈다. 그의 논고가 진정으로 빛을 발한 대목은 그 진실함이었다. 그는 피고의 유죄를 진심으로 믿었으니, 단지 공무상의 의무로서 그를 단죄한 것이 아니었고, 복수를 부르짖을 때에도 \"사회의 안전을 위하여\"라는 진정한 열정으로 부르르 떨었다. 청중석의 부인들조차, 비록 이폴리트 키릴로비치에게 적대적인 태도를 거두지 않았으나, 그가 자신들에게 비상한 인상을 남겼다는 것을 인정했다. 그는 갈라지는 목소리로 시작했으나, 이내 그 목소리는 힘을 얻어 논고가 끝날 때까지 법정을 가득 채웠다. 그러나 말을 마치자마자 그는 거의 실신할 지경이었다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분,\" 검사가 입을 열었다. \"이 사건은 온 러시아를 떠들썩하게 만들었습니다. 그러나 여기에 놀랄 것이 무엇이며, 우리에게 무엇이 그토록 유난히 끔찍하단 말입니까? 우리는 이런 범죄에 이미 너무도 익숙해져 있습니다! 바로 그것이 가장 끔찍한 일이니, 이토록 어두운 행위들이 더 이상 우리를 전율케 하지 못한다는 것입니다. 우리를 전율케 해야 할 것은 우리가 그것에 이토록 익숙해져 있다는 사실이지, 이런저런 개별 범죄가 아닙니다. 이런 행위들, 부러워할 것 없는 미래를 예고하는 이런 시대의 징후들에 대한 우리의 무관심, 우리의 미온적인 태도의 원인은 무엇입니까? 그것은 우리의 냉소주의입니까, 아니면 젊음에도 불구하고 쇠락의 늪으로 가라앉고 있는 사회에서 지성과 상상력이 때 이르게 고갈된 탓입니까? 우리의 도덕적 원칙이 그 토대까지 산산이 부서진 탓입니까, 아니면 어쩌면 우리에게 그런 원칙이 아예 완전히 결여된 탓입니까? 나는 이런 물음들에 답할 수 없습니다. 그럼에도 이 물음들은 마음을 어지럽히는 것이며, 모든 시민은 마땅히 이 물음들에 시달려야 할 뿐 아니라 시달리지 않을 수 없는 것입니다. 갓 태어나 아직 소심한 우리의 언론은 이미 대중에게 좋은 봉사를 해 왔으니, 그것이 없었던들 우리는 억제되지 않은 폭력과 도덕적 타락의 참상을 결코 알지 못했을 것입니다. 그런 일들은 언론을 통해 끊임없이 알려지는데, 단지 현 치세에 설립된 새로운 배심 재판정에 참석하는 이들에게만이 아니라 모든 사람에게 전해지는 것입니다. 그렇다면 우리는 거의 날마다 무엇을 읽고 있습니까? 그것에 비하면 지금 이 사건이 빛이 바래고 거의 평범하게까지 보일 만한 일들입니다. 그러나 가장 중요한 것은, 우리나라에서 벌어지는 폭력 범죄의 대다수가 이제 우리 사이에 너무도 널리 퍼져 맞서 싸우기조차 어려운 어떤 만연한 악을 증언하고 있다는 점입니다.\n\n\"어느 날 우리는 상류 사회의 한 빛나는 젊은 장교가, 그 출세 가도의 바로 첫걸음에서, 비겁하고 음험한 방식으로, 양심의 가책 한 점 없이, 한때 자신의 은인이었던 관리와 그 하녀를 살해하는 것을 봅니다. 그것도 자기가 써 준 차용증과 그에게서 찾아낼 수 있는 현금을 훔치기 위해서였으니, '상류 사교계에서의 내 쾌락과 앞으로의 출세에 요긴하게 쓰이겠지' 하는 것입니다. 그들을 죽인 뒤 그는 희생자들 각자의 머리 밑에 베개를 받쳐 놓고는 그곳을 떠납니다. 다음으로, '용맹으로 훈장을 받은' 한 젊은 영웅이 자기 상관이자 은인인 사람의 어머니를 노상강도처럼 죽이고는, 자기 패거리를 끌어들이려고 '그 노파는 자기를 아들처럼 사랑하니 자기 말이라면 무엇이든 따를 것이고 아무런 경계도 하지 않을 것'이라고 장담합니다. 그가 괴물이라는 것은 인정합시다. 그러나 요즘 세상에 나는 감히 그가 유일무이한 존재라고는 말하지 못하겠습니다. 또 다른 누군가는 살인을 저지르지는 않으나 그와 똑같이 느끼고 똑같이 생각하며, 그 영혼이 그만큼이나 명예를 모릅니다. 침묵 속에서, 홀로 제 양심과 마주한 채 그는 어쩌면 스스로에게 묻고 있을 것입니다. '명예란 대체 무엇이며, 피 흘림을 단죄하는 것은 한낱 편견이 아닌가?'\n\n\"어쩌면 사람들은 나를 향해, 내가 병적이고 신경질적이라느니, 이것은 터무니없는 중상이라느니, 내가 과장하고 있다느니 하며 소리칠지도 모릅니다. 그렇게 말하라지요—그리고 맙소사! 만약 정말 그렇다면 나야말로 가장 먼저 기뻐할 사람입니다! 오, 부디 나를 믿지 마시고 나를 병적인 자로 여기십시오. 다만 내 말을 기억해 주십시오. 만약 내가 하는 말의 십분의 일, 아니 이십분의 일만이라도 진실이라면—그렇다 해도 끔찍한 일입니다! 보십시오, 우리 젊은이들이 어떻게 자살하는지를. 그 너머에 무엇이 있는지 묻는 햄릿의 물음조차 스스로에게 던지지 않은 채, 그런 물음의 기색조차 없이, 마치 영혼에 관한 모든 것, 무덤 너머에서 우리를 기다리는 모든 것이 그들의 마음속에서 오래전에 지워져 모래 밑에 묻혀 버리기라도 한 듯이 말입니다. 우리의 악덕을 보십시오, 우리의 방탕한 자들을 보십시오. 이 사건의 불운한 희생자인 표도르 파블로비치는 그들 가운데 많은 자들에 견주면 거의 순진무구한 갓난아기나 다름없었습니다. 그런데도 우리는 모두 그를 알았으니, '그는 우리 사이에서 살았습니다!'……\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어쩌면 언젠가 러시아와 유럽의 으뜸가는 지성들이 러시아 범죄의 심리를 연구할 날이 올 것이니, 그 주제는 그럴 만한 값어치가 있습니다. 그러나 이 연구는 훗날, 여유가 생겼을 때, 오늘날의 이 모든 비극적 혼돈이 우리에게서 더 멀어져서, 내가 할 수 있는 것보다 더 깊은 통찰과 더 큰 공정함으로 그것을 살펴볼 수 있을 때 이루어질 것입니다. 지금 우리는 전율하거나, 아니면 전율하는 척하지만, 실은 그 광경을 보며 은근히 고소해하고, 우리의 냉소적이고 응석받이로 자란 나태를 간질이는 강렬하고 기괴한 자극을 좋아합니다. 그렇지 않으면 어린아이들처럼 그 무서운 유령들을 쫓아 버리고 베개 속에 머리를 파묻었다가, 유령이 사라지기 무섭게 다시 놀이와 흥겨움으로 돌아갑니다. 그러나 우리는 언젠가 진지하고 엄숙하게 삶을 시작해야 하며, 우리 자신을 하나의 사회로서 바라보아야 합니다. 우리의 사회적 처지를 조금이라도 파악하려고, 적어도 그 방향으로 첫발을 떼려고 시도할 때가 된 것입니다.\n\n\"지난 시대의 한 위대한 작가는, 러시아를 알 수 없는 목적지를 향해 질주하는 빠른 트로이카에 견주며 이렇게 외쳤습니다. '오, 트로이카여, 새와도 같은 트로이카여, 누가 너를 만들었느냐!' 그리고 그는 자랑스러운 환희에 차서, 세상 만민이 경의를 표하며 비켜서서 그 무모하게 질주하는 트로이카가 지나가도록 길을 내준다고 덧붙입니다. 그럴지도 모릅니다, 그들은 경의를 표하든 안 표하든 비켜설지도 모릅니다. 그러나 내 변변찮은 소견으로는, 그 위대한 작가가 자기 책을 이렇게 끝맺은 것은 유치하고 순진한 낙관주의의 발작 탓이거나, 아니면 그저 당대 검열에 대한 두려움 탓이었습니다. 왜냐하면 만약 그 트로이카를 그의 주인공들, 곧 소바케비치, 노즈드료프, 치치코프가 끌고 있다면, 누가 모는들 그것은 어떤 합리적인 목적지에도 다다를 수 없을 것이기 때문입니다. 그런데 그들은 앞선 세대의 주인공들이었고, 우리 세대의 견본들은 그보다도 더 못한 것들입니다……\"\n\n이 대목에서 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 논고는 박수갈채에 가로막혔다. 이 비유가 담은 자유주의적 의미가 호응을 얻은 것이다. 사실 박수는 잠깐 사이에 그쳤으므로, 재판장은 굳이 청중에게 주의를 줄 필요를 느끼지 못하고 그저 박수를 친 자들 쪽을 엄하게 노려보았을 뿐이다. 그러나 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 고무되었으니, 그는 전에는 한 번도 박수를 받아 본 적이 없었던 것이다! 그는 평생토록 자기 말에 귀를 기울이게 하지 못했는데, 이제 갑자기 온 러시아의 귀를 사로잡을 기회를 얻은 셈이었다.\n\n\"대체 이 카라마조프 집안이란 무엇이기에, 온 러시아에 걸쳐 이토록 부러울 것 없는 악명을 떨치게 되었을까요?\" 그는 말을 이었다. \"어쩌면 내가 과장하는 것인지도 모르나, 내가 보기에는 오늘날 교양 계급의 어떤 근본적인 특징들이 이 가족의 초상에 비쳐 있는 듯합니다—물론 축소된 모습으로, '물방울에 비친 태양처럼' 말입니다. 그 불행하고 사악하며 절제를 모르던 노인을, 그토록 음울한 최후를 맞은 그 한 가정의 가장을 떠올려 보십시오! 귀족으로 태어났으나 가난하여 남에게 빌붙는 처지로 삶을 시작한 그는, 뜻밖의 결혼을 통해 약간의 재산을 손에 넣었습니다. 좀스러운 악당이요 아첨꾼이며 어릿광대였던 그는, 제법 괜찮으나 갈고닦이지 않은 지능을 지닌 자였으되, 무엇보다도 고리대금업자였고, 살림이 늘어 갈수록 점점 더 대담해졌습니다. 그의 비굴하고 굽실거리던 특성은 사라지고, 악의에 차고 빈정대는 냉소주의만이 남았습니다. 영적인 면에서 그는 미숙했던 반면, 그 생명력은 지나치게 넘쳤습니다. 그는 삶에서 관능적 쾌락 외에는 아무것도 보지 못했고, 제 자식들도 똑같이 그렇게 되도록 길렀습니다. 그는 아버지로서의 의무에 대해 아무런 감정도 없었습니다. 그는 그 의무를 비웃었습니다. 그는 어린 자식들을 하인들에게 내맡기고는, 그들을 떼어 낸 것을 다행으로 여기며 그들을 까맣게 잊어버렸습니다. 그 노인의 좌우명은 '내가 죽은 뒤에는 홍수가 나든 말든'이었습니다. 그는 시민적 의무에 대립하는 모든 것의, 가장 철저하고 악의에 찬 개인주의의 본보기였습니다. '나만 무사하다면 세상이야 불타든 말든 내 알 바 아니다'—그리고 그는 무사했고, 만족했으며, 앞으로도 이삼십 년쯤 똑같은 방식으로 계속 살아가기를 열망했습니다. 그는 제 친아들을 등쳐 먹고, 그 어머니가 남긴 유산인 그의 돈을 그에게서 정부를 빼앗으려는 데 써 버렸습니다. 아닙니다, 나는 피고의 변론을 페테르부르크에서 오신 재능 있는 동료에게 죄다 떠넘길 생각은 없습니다. 진실은 나 자신이 말하겠습니다. 나는 아버지가 아들의 마음속에 얼마나 큰 원한을 쌓아 올렸는지 충분히 이해할 수 있습니다.\n\n\"그러나 그 불행한 노인에 관해서는 이쯤 해 둡시다, 이쯤이면 충분합니다. 그는 그 대가를 치렀습니다. 그러나 한 가지만은 기억해 둡시다. 그는 한 사람의 아버지였으며, 오늘날의 전형적인 아버지들 가운데 하나였다는 것을. 그가 오늘날 수많은 아버지의 전형이라고 말하는 것이 과연 부당한 일입니까? 아아! 그들 가운데 많은 자들은 다만 그런 냉소주의를 드러내 놓고 떠벌리지 않는다는 점에서만 다를 뿐이니, 그들이 더 잘 교육받고 더 교양 있는 까닭입니다. 그러나 그들의 철학은 본질적으로 그의 것과 똑같습니다. 어쩌면 나는 비관주의자인지도 모릅니다. 그러나 여러분은 나를 용서하기로 합의해 주셨습니다. 미리 이렇게 약속해 둡시다, 여러분은 나를 믿지 않으셔도 좋으니, 다만 내가 말하도록 내버려 두십시오. 내가 해야 할 말을 하게 해 주시고, 내 말 가운데 무언가를 기억해 주십시오.\n\n\"이제 이 아버지의, 이 한 가정 가장의 자식들로 넘어갑시다. 그중 하나가 우리 앞에 선 피고이며, 내 논고의 나머지 전부는 그를 다룰 것입니다. 나머지 두 사람에 관해서는 그저 간략하게만 말하겠습니다.\n\n\"맏이는 빛나는 교양과 왕성한 지력을 갖춘, 그러면서도 모든 것에 대한 일체의 신앙을 잃어버린 오늘날의 젊은이들 가운데 하나입니다. 그는 제 아버지처럼 이미 많은 것을 부정하고 배척했습니다. 우리는 모두 그의 말을 들은 적이 있으며, 그는 이 고장 사교계에서 환영받는 손님이었습니다. 그는 결코 자기 견해를 감추지 않았으니, 사실은 정반대였습니다. 바로 그 때문에 나는 지금 그에 관해 다소 거리낌 없이 이야기해도 무방하다고 봅니다. 물론 한 개인으로서가 아니라 카라마조프 집안의 한 사람으로서 말입니다. 이 사건과 밀접하게 얽힌 또 다른 인물이 어젯밤 이곳에서 제 손으로 목숨을 끊었습니다. 나는 표도르 파블로비치의 하인이었으며 어쩌면 그 사생아였을지도 모르는, 병든 백치 스메르댜코프를 가리키는 것입니다. 예심에서 그는 신경질적인 눈물을 흘리며, 젊은 이반 카라마조프가 자신의 영적 대담함으로 자기를 얼마나 전율케 했는지를 내게 이야기했습니다. '그분에 따르면 세상의 모든 것이 허용되며, 앞으로는 그 무엇도 금지되어서는 안 된다는 겁니다—그분이 늘 저에게 가르치신 게 그것입니다.' 나는 그 백치가 이 이론 때문에 정신이 나가 버린 것이라고 믿습니다. 물론 그가 앓던 간질 발작과 이 끔찍한 참사가 그의 정신을 흐트러뜨리는 데 한몫했겠지만 말입니다. 그러나 그는 한 가지 매우 흥미로운 견해를 흘렸으니, 그것은 더 영민한 관찰자가 했더라면 그에게 명예가 되었을 만한 것이었고, 사실 내가 그것을 언급하는 까닭도 거기에 있습니다. '아들들 가운데 표도르 파블로비치를 성격으로 빼닮은 사람이 있다면, 그건 이반 표도로비치입니다.'\n\n\"이 한마디로 나는 그의 성격에 관한 묘사를 끝맺겠습니다. 더 깊이 들어가는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이라 여기는 까닭입니다. 오, 나는 더 이상 어떤 결론도 끌어내어 까마귀처럼 그 젊은이의 미래를 두고 불길하게 까악거리고 싶지 않습니다. 우리는 오늘 이 법정에서, 그의 젊은 가슴속에 아직 선한 충동이 남아 있다는 것을, 신앙의 결핍과 냉소주의가—그것도 독립적인 사고의 행사를 통해서라기보다는 차라리 유전을 통해 그에게 물려진 것입니다만—그 안의 가족애를 파괴하지는 못했다는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"다음으로 셋째 아들입니다. 오, 그는 경건하고 겸손한 청년으로서, 형의 음울하고 파괴적인 인생관을 공유하지 않습니다. 그는 '민중의 이상'에, 혹은 우리 지식 계급의 어떤 무리들 사이에서 그렇게 일컫는 것에 매달리고자 애써 왔습니다. 그는 수도원에 매달렸고, 하마터면 수도사가 될 뻔했습니다. 내가 보기에 그는, 그토록 이른 나이에 무의식중에 어떤 소심한 절망을 드러내 보인 듯합니다. 그 절망이란, 냉소주의와 그 타락시키는 영향을 두려워하고 모든 해악을 유럽의 계몽 탓으로 잘못 돌리며, 그들이 말하듯 자신의 '본래의 토양'으로, 말하자면 어머니 대지의 품으로 돌아가도록 우리의 불행한 사회에서 그토록 많은 이들을 이끄는 것이니, 마치 겁에 질린 아이들이 늙고 쇠약한 어머니의 메마른 가슴 위에서 잠들기를, 자신들을 떨게 하는 공포에서 벗어나고자 그곳에서 영원히 잠들기를 갈망하는 것과도 같습니다.\n\n\"나 자신으로 말하자면, 이 훌륭하고 재능 있는 청년이 온갖 성공을 거두기를 바랍니다. 나는 그의 젊은 이상주의와 민중의 이상을 향한 충동이, 흔히 그러하듯, 도덕적인 측면에서는 음울한 신비주의로, 정치적인 측면에서는 맹목적인 배외주의로 변질되지 않기를 바랍니다. 이 두 요소야말로, 그의 형이 앓고 있는—유럽 사상의 몰이해와 무분별한 수용에서 비롯된—때 이른 쇠락보다도 러시아에 한층 더 큰 위협이 되는 것입니다.\"\n\n배외주의와 신비주의가 언급되자 두세 사람이 박수를 쳤다. 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 사실 자기 자신의 웅변에 도취되어 있었던 것이다. 이 모든 이야기는 다소 모호하다는 점은 차치하더라도 지금 다루는 사건과는 별 관련이 없었으나, 병들고 폐결핵에 걸린 이 사내는 평생에 단 한 번이라도 자기를 표현하고 싶다는 욕망에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다. 사람들은 훗날 그가 이반을 비판한 데에는 떳떳지 못한 동기가 작용했다고 수군거렸으니, 이반이 한두 차례 논쟁에서 그를 눌렀던 일이 있었던 까닭에, 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 그것을 기억하고 이제 앙갚음을 하려 든 것이라는 말이었다. 그러나 그것이 사실이었는지는 나도 모르겠다. 어쨌든 이 모든 것은 서론에 불과했으며, 논고는 이제 사건에 대한 좀 더 직접적인 고찰로 넘어갔다.\n\n\"그러나 맏아들 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\" 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 말을 이었다. \"그가 바로 우리 앞에 선 피고입니다. 우리 앞에는 그의 삶과 그의 행적 또한 놓여 있습니다. 그 숙명의 날이 닥쳐왔고 모든 것이 수면 위로 떠올랐습니다. 그의 형제들이 '유럽주의'와 '민중의 원리'를 대변하는 듯하다면, 그는 있는 그대로의 러시아를 대표하는 듯합니다. 오, 러시아 전체는 아닙니다, 전체는 아닙니다! 만약 그렇다면 하느님이 우리를 지켜 주시기를! 그러나 여기 우리에게는 그녀가, 우리 어머니 러시아가, 그녀의 바로 그 냄새와 그 소리가 있습니다. 오, 그는 천성이 거침없고, 선과 악이 놀랍도록 뒤섞인 존재이며, 교양과 실러를 사랑하는 자이면서도 술집에서 난동을 부리고 술친구들의 수염을 쥐어뜯습니다. 오, 그 역시 선하고 고결할 수 있으나, 다만 만사가 그에게 순조롭게 풀릴 때에만 그렇습니다. 게다가 그는 고결한 이상에 휩쓸릴 수도, 그야말로 발이 땅에서 떨어질 만큼 휩쓸릴 수도 있으나, 다만 그 이상이 제 발로 찾아올 때에만, 그를 위해 하늘에서 떨어질 때에만, 대가를 치를 필요가 없을 때에만 그렇습니다. 그는 무엇에든 대가를 치르기를 싫어하나 받는 것은 무척 좋아하니, 매사가 그에게는 그러합니다. 오, 그에게 삶에서 가능한 온갖 좋은 것을 주고(그는 그보다 못한 것으로는 만족하지 못할 것입니다), 그의 앞길에 아무런 장애도 두지 마십시오. 그러면 그도 고결할 수 있음을 보여 줄 것입니다. 그는 탐욕스럽지는 않습니다, 그렇습니다. 그러나 그에게는 돈이, 그것도 아주 많은 돈이 있어야만 하며, 그러면 여러분은 그가 얼마나 너그럽게, 더러운 돈을 얼마나 경멸하며, 하룻밤의 무모한 방탕 속에 그 모든 것을 죄다 내던져 버리는지 보게 될 것입니다. 그러나 만약 그에게 돈이 없다면, 그것이 절실히 필요한 처지에 몰렸을 때 그것을 손에 넣으려고 그가 무슨 짓까지 할 각오가 되어 있는지를 보여 줄 것입니다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 뒤로 미루고, 사건들을 시간 순서대로 짚어 봅시다.\n\n\"먼저, 우리 앞에는 가엾게 버려진 한 아이가, 방금 우리의 존경스럽고 훌륭한—아아, 외국 태생의!—동료 시민이 표현한 대로 '발에 신발도 신지 못한 채' 뒷마당을 뛰어다니던 한 아이가 있습니다. 다시 한 번 말하거니와, 나는 이 범죄자의 변론을 누구에게도 양보하지 않겠습니다. 나는 그를 고발하러 이 자리에 섰으나, 또한 그를 변호하러 선 것이기도 합니다. 그렇습니다, 나 역시 인간입니다. 나 역시 가정과 어린 시절이 성격에 미치는 영향을 가늠할 줄 압니다. 그러나 그 소년은 자라나 장교가 됩니다. 결투와 그 밖의 무모한 행실 때문에 그는 러시아의 외딴 변경 마을 가운데 한 곳으로 유배됩니다. 그곳에서 그는 장교로서 거친 삶을 살았습니다. 그리고 물론 그에게는 돈이, 무엇보다도 돈이 필요했으니, 그리하여 오랜 분쟁 끝에 그는 아버지와 합의에 이르렀고, 마지막 육천 루블을 그에게 부쳐 주게 되었습니다. 그가 사실상 나머지에 대한 청구권을 포기하고 이 육천 루블의 지급으로 유산을 둘러싼 아버지와의 갈등을 마무리한다는 내용의 편지가 한 통 남아 있습니다.\n\n\"그다음에 그는 고결한 성품과 빛나는 교양을 갖춘 한 젊은 처녀와 만나게 됩니다. 오, 나는 감히 그 상세한 내막을 되풀이하지 않겠습니다. 여러분은 방금 그것을 들으셨습니다. 거기에는 명예와 자기희생이 드러나 있었으니, 나는 입을 다물겠습니다. 경박하고 방탕한 젊은 장교가 진정한 고결함과 드높은 이상에 경의를 표하던 그 모습은, 우리 앞에 매우 호감 가는 빛으로 비쳤습니다. 그러나 메달의 다른 면이 그 직후 바로 이 법정에서 뜻밖에도 우리에게 돌려졌습니다. 다시 한 번 나는 왜 일이 그렇게 되었는지를 감히 추측하지 않겠으나, 거기에는 원인이 있었습니다. 그 똑같은 여인이, 오래도록 감춰 온 분노의 눈물에 젖은 채, 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 그가, 비록 경솔하고 어쩌면 무모했을지언정 그래도 드높고 너그러운 동기에서 비롯된 자기 행동을 두고 자기를 멸시했다고 주장했습니다. 그가, 바로 그가, 그 처녀의 약혼자였던 그가, 다른 누구의 것보다도 더 견디기 힘든 그 조롱의 미소를 띠고 그녀를 바라보았다는 것입니다. 그리고 그가 이미 자기를 속였다는 것을 알면서도(그는 그녀를 속였으니, 그녀가 자기에게서라면 배신마저도 무엇이든 견뎌 낼 수밖에 없으리라 믿었던 것입니다), 그녀는 일부러 그에게 삼천 루블을 내밀었고, 그것이 자기를 속이라고 주는 돈임을 그가 알아차리도록 분명하게, 너무도 분명하게 내비쳤습니다. '자, 받겠어요, 안 받겠어요, 그토록 수치를 모르는 사람인가요?'—이것이 그녀의 살피는 눈에 담긴 무언의 물음이었습니다. 그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 있는지를 분명히 알아차렸으며(그는 여기 여러분 앞에서 그 모든 것을 이해했다고 인정했습니다), 그 삼천 루블을 아무 조건 없이 제 것으로 만들고는, 이틀 만에 새로운 애정의 대상과 함께 그것을 흥청망청 써 버렸습니다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 무엇을 믿어야 합니까? 마지막 한 푼까지 너그러운 고결함의 충동에서 내놓고 미덕에 경의를 표하던 젊은 장교에 관한 첫 번째 전설입니까, 아니면 이 또 다른 역겨운 그림입니까? 흔히 두 극단 사이에서는 그 중간을 찾아야 마땅하나, 지금 이 경우에는 그것이 맞지 않습니다. 십중팔구 첫 번째 경우에 그는 진정으로 고결했고, 두 번째 경우에는 그만큼이나 진정으로 비열했습니다. 그것은 왜입니까? 그가 폭넓은 카라마조프적 성격을 지녔기 때문이니—바로 이것이 내가 이끌어 가려는 바입니다—가장 어울리지 않는 모순들을 한데 결합할 수 있고, 가장 높은 정점에도 가장 깊은 나락에도 다다를 수 있는 성격이기 때문입니다. 카라마조프 집안을 가까이에서 지켜본 한 젊은 관찰자, 라키틴 씨가 했던 빛나는 한마디를 기억하십시오. '그 무모하고 절제를 모르는 천성에게는 제 자신의 비천함에 대한 자각이, 제 드높은 너그러움에 대한 자각만큼이나 본질적으로 필요한 것이다.' 그리고 그것은 사실이니, 그들에게는 이 부자연스러운 혼합이 끊임없이 필요합니다. 두 극단을 같은 순간에 누리지 못하면 그들은 비참해하고 만족하지 못하며 그들의 존재는 미완으로 남습니다. 그들은 넓디넓으니, 어머니 러시아만큼이나 넓습니다. 그들은 모든 것을 품고 모든 것을 견뎌 냅니다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 배심원 여러분, 우리는 방금 그 삼천 루블 이야기를 잠깐 건드렸으니, 나는 감히 이야기를 조금 앞당겨 보고자 합니다. 여러분은 그런 사내가, 그런 식으로, 그토록 큰 수치와 그토록 큰 치욕과 그토록 철저한 비천함을 대가로 그 돈을 받아 든 자가, 바로 그날 그 절반을 따로 떼어 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣고, 그 뒤로 온갖 유혹과 그것에 대한 극심한 궁핍에도 불구하고 꼬박 한 달 동안 그것을 몸에 지니고 다닐 만한 성격의 굳셈을 지녔으리라고 상상하실 수 있겠습니까! 술집에서 곤드레만드레 흥청거릴 때에도, 혹은 자기 애정의 대상이 제 아버지의 유혹에 넘어가지 못하도록 그녀를 빼돌리는 데 필요한 그 절실한 돈을 누구에게서인지도 모를 사람에게서 구하려고 시골로 내달릴 때에도, 그는 차마 그 작은 주머니에 손을 대지 못했습니다! 아니, 그토록 질투하던 연적에게 제 정부를 내주지 않기 위해서라도, 그는 분명히 그 주머니를 열고 집에 머물며 그녀를 지키고, 마침내 그녀가 그에게 '나는 당신의 것이에요'라고 말해 줄 순간을 기다렸다가 그 숙명적인 환경에서 멀리 그녀와 함께 달아났을 것이 틀림없습니다.\n\n\"그러나 아닙니다, 그는 자기 부적에 손을 대지 않았습니다. 그러면 그가 그 까닭으로 대는 이유는 무엇입니까? 방금 말한 대로 가장 큰 이유는, 그녀가 '나는 당신의 것이에요, 어디로든 데려가 줘요'라고 말할 때 그녀를 데려갈 밑천을 갖고 있기 위해서였다는 것입니다. 그러나 피고 자신의 말에 따르면, 그 첫 번째 이유는 두 번째 이유에 비하면 별 무게가 없었습니다. 이 돈을 몸에 지니고 있는 한 나는 악당이지 도둑은 아니다, 라고 그는 말했습니다. 왜냐하면 나는 언제든 모욕당한 약혼녀에게 찾아가, 내가 부정하게 가로챈 돈의 절반을 내려놓으며 언제든 그녀에게 이렇게 말할 수 있기 때문이라는 것입니다. '보시오, 나는 당신 돈의 절반을 탕진했고, 내가 나약하고 부도덕한 인간이라는 것을, 원한다면 악당이라는 것을(나는 피고 자신의 표현을 그대로 쓰고 있습니다) 보여 주었소. 그러나 비록 내가 악당일지언정 도둑은 아니오. 만약 내가 도둑이었다면 당신에게 이 절반의 돈을 도로 가져오지 않고 나머지 절반처럼 가져 버렸을 테니까!' 참으로 놀라운 변명입니다! 그런 치욕을 대가로 삼천 루블을 받아 드는 유혹을 이겨 내지 못했던 이 광적이면서도 나약한 사내가, 바로 그 사내가 갑자기 더없이 금욕적인 굳셈을 발휘하여, 감히 손도 대지 못하면서 천 루블을 몸에 지니고 다닌다는 것입니다. 그것이 과연 우리가 분석한 그 성격과 조금이라도 들어맞습니까? 아닙니다, 그래서 나는 감히 여러분께 말씀드리겠습니다. 진짜 드미트리 카라마조프라면, 만약 그가 정말로 그 돈을 따로 떼어 둘 마음을 먹었다 하더라도, 그런 상황에서 어떻게 행동했을지를 말입니다.\n\n\"첫 번째 유혹이 닥치면—이를테면 그가 이미 절반의 돈을 함께 탕진했던 그 여자를 대접하려는 유혹 말입니다—그는 자기 작은 주머니의 실밥을 뜯어 백 루블쯤 꺼냈을 것입니다. 어째서 그가 하필 돈의 정확히 절반을, 곧 천오백 루블을 도로 가져가야 한단 말입니까? 어째서 천사백 루블은 안 됩니까? 그래도 그는 천사백 루블을 도로 가져왔으니 자기는 도둑이 아니라고 똑같이 말할 수 있었을 것입니다. 그러고는 또 한 번 주머니의 실밥을 뜯어 다시 백 루블을 꺼내고, 그다음에는 또 한 번, 그다음에는 또 한 번, 그리하여 한 달이 채 가기 전에 마지막에서 두 번째 지폐까지 꺼냈을 것이니, 백 루블만 도로 가져가도 목적에는 충분하리라 여겼을 것입니다. 도둑이라면 그 모두를 훔쳤을 테니까요. 그러고 나서 그는 이 마지막 지폐를 바라보며 스스로에게 이렇게 말했을 것입니다. '백 루블 도로 돌려주는 건 정말 그럴 가치도 없지. 이것마저 써 버리자!' 우리가 아는 진짜 드미트리 카라마조프라면 바로 이렇게 행동했을 것입니다. 그 작은 주머니에 관한 이 전설만큼 실제 사실과 어울리지 않는 것은 상상할 수 없습니다. 그보다 더 믿기 어려운 것은 없을 것입니다. 그러나 이에 관해서는 나중에 다시 다루도록 하겠습니다.\"\n\n아버지와 아들의 금전 관계에 관해 재판 과정에서 드러난 것들을 짚고, 알려진 사실들로는 어느 쪽이 잘못이었는지 도무지 판가름할 수 없다고 거듭거듭 주장한 끝에, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 미탸가 자신에게 마땅히 돌아와야 할 삼천 루블에 대해 품었던 고정관념과 관련한 의료 전문가들의 증언으로 넘어갔다.\n\n# Chapter VII. An Historical Survey\n\n\"의료 전문가들은 피고가 정신이 나갔으며, 사실상 광인이라고 우리를 설득하려 애써 왔습니다. 저는 그가 제정신이라고 주장하며, 만약 제정신이 아니었다면 오히려 더 영리하게 처신했으리라고 봅니다. 그가 광인이라는 점에 대해서는, 다만 한 가지 점에서만 동의할 수 있으니, 곧 그 삼천 루블에 관한 고정관념이 그것입니다. 그러나 저는 그의 정신착란 성향보다 훨씬 더 단순한 원인을 찾을 수 있으리라고 생각합니다. 저로서는 피고의 정신 기능이 언제나 정상이었으며, 다만 신경이 곤두서고 격앙되어 있었을 뿐이라고 주장한 젊은 의사의 견해에 전적으로 동의하는 바입니다. 피고가 끊임없이 격렬한 분노를 쏟아부은 대상은 그 돈 자체가 아니었으니, 그 밑바닥에는 특별한 동기가 깔려 있었습니다. 그 동기란 바로 질투입니다!\"\n\n여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 그루셴카를 향한 피고의 치명적 정념을 장황하게 묘사했다. 그는 피고가 그 \"젊은 여인\"의 거처로 \"그녀를 때리러\" 갔던 순간부터 이야기를 시작했다. \"이것은 피고 자신의 표현을 그대로 쓰는 것입니다.\" 하고 검사가 설명했다. \"그러나 그는 그녀를 때리기는커녕 그곳에 머물러, 그녀의 발치에 주저앉고 말았습니다. 이것이 그 정념의 시작이었습니다. 같은 시기에 피고의 아버지 또한 같은 젊은 여인에게 사로잡혔으니, 참으로 기이하고도 숙명적인 우연이었습니다. 두 사람은 모두 그녀를 전부터 알고 있었으면서도 동시에 그녀에게 마음을 빼앗기고 말았던 것입니다. 그리고 그녀는 두 사람 모두에게서 가장 격렬한, 카라마조프 집안 특유의 정념을 불러일으켰습니다. 우리는 그녀 자신의 고백을 가지고 있습니다. '저는 두 사람을 모두 놀리고 있었어요.' 그렇습니다, 갑자기 두 사람을 가지고 장난을 치고 싶은 욕망이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그리하여 그녀는 단숨에 두 사람을 모두 정복해 버렸습니다. 돈을 숭배하던 그 늙은이는 즉시 그녀의 한 번 방문에 대한 대가로 삼천 루블을 마련해 두었으나, 그 직후에는 그녀가 자신의 합법적인 아내가 되어 주기만 한다면 자신의 재산과 이름을 기꺼이 그녀의 발치에 바쳤을 것입니다. 우리는 이에 관한 확실한 증거를 가지고 있습니다. 피고로 말하자면, 그 운명의 비극은 명백하니, 바로 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있습니다. 그러나 그것이야말로 그 젊은 여인의 '유희'였던 것입니다. 그 요부는 불행한 청년에게 마지막 순간까지 아무런 희망도 주지 않았으니, 마침내 그가 이미 아버지이자 연적의 피로 물든 두 손을 내밀며 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇었던 그 순간까지도 그러했습니다. 바로 그 자세로 그는 체포되었습니다. '저도 그이와 함께 시베리아로 보내 주세요, 제가 그이를 이 지경으로 만들었어요, 가장 큰 죄인은 저예요.' 하고 그 여자 자신이, 그의 체포 순간에 진정한 회한에 잠겨 외쳤던 것입니다.\n\n\"제가 이미 언급한 바 있는 그 재능 있는 청년, 라키틴 군은 이 여주인공을 간결하고도 인상적인 말로 규정했습니다. '그녀는 일찍이 인생에서 환멸을 겪었으니, 그녀를 유혹하고 버린 약혼자에게 기만당하고 파멸했습니다. 그녀는 빈궁 속에 남겨졌고, 점잖은 가족에게 저주받았으며, 어느 부유한 노인의 보호 아래 들어갔으되, 그러면서도 그녀는 여전히 그 노인을 자신의 은인으로 여기고 있습니다. 그 젊은 마음속에는 어쩌면 선한 것이 많이 있었을지 모르나, 그것은 너무 일찍 쓰디쓰게 변해 버렸습니다. 그녀는 타산적으로 변해 돈을 모았습니다. 그녀는 빈정거리게 되었고 사회를 향해 원한을 품게 되었습니다.' 이러한 성격 묘사를 듣고 나면, 그녀가 그저 장난삼아, 악의에서 두 사람을 모두 비웃을 수도 있었으리라는 것을 충분히 이해할 수 있습니다.\n\n\"한 달에 걸친 희망 없는 사랑과 도덕적 타락 속에서, 그동안 그는 자신의 약혼녀를 배신하고 자신의 명예에 맡겨진 돈을 가로챘으며, 끊임없는 질투로 인해 거의 광란에 가까운, 거의 광기에 가까운 상태로 내몰렸습니다. 그런데 그 질투의 상대가 누구였습니까? 그의 아버지였습니다! 그리고 그중에서도 가장 끔찍한 것은, 그 미친 늙은이가 다름 아닌 그 삼천 루블을 미끼로 그가 애정을 품은 대상을 유혹하고 꾀어내고 있었다는 사실이니, 그 돈을 아들은 자신의 소유물로, 어머니로부터 받을 유산의 일부로 여겼으며, 아버지가 그것을 가로채 자신을 속이고 있다고 보았던 것입니다. 그렇습니다, 저도 그것이 견디기 어려웠으리라는 점은 인정합니다! 그것은 능히 사람을 광기로 몰아갈 만했습니다. 문제는 돈이 아니라, 그 돈이 그토록 역겨운 냉소와 함께 그의 행복을 파멸시키는 데 쓰였다는 사실이었습니다!\"\n\n이어 검사는 아버지를 살해한다는 생각이 어떻게 피고의 머릿속에 들어왔는지 묘사하기 시작했으며, 자신의 이론을 사실로써 뒷받침했다.\n\n\"처음에 그는 그저 술집에서 그 일을 입에 올리기만 했으니, 그는 그달 내내 그 이야기를 떠벌렸습니다. 아, 그는 언제나 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있기를 좋아하며, 동료들에게 무엇이든, 심지어 자신의 가장 악마적이고 위험한 생각까지도 죄다 털어놓기를 좋아합니다. 그는 모든 생각을 남들과 나누기를 좋아하며, 무슨 까닭에서인지 자기가 속내를 털어놓는 사람들이 자신을 완벽한 공감으로 맞아 주고, 자신의 온갖 고민과 불안 속으로 함께 들어와 주며, 자신의 편을 들고 무슨 일에서든 반대하지 않으리라 기대합니다. 만약 그렇지 않으면, 그는 격분하여 술집에 있는 모든 것을 때려 부숩니다. (그러고는 스네기료프 대위에 관한 일화가 이어졌다.) 피고의 말을 들은 자들은 마침내, 그것이 단순한 위협 이상의 의미를 담고 있을지도 모르며, 그러한 광란이 위협을 행동으로 바꿔 놓을 수도 있으리라고 생각하기 시작했습니다.\"\n\n여기서 검사는 수도원에서의 가족 모임과 알료샤와 나눈 대화들, 그리고 피고가 식사 직후 아버지의 집으로 뛰어들었을 때 벌어진 끔찍한 폭력의 장면을 묘사했다.\n\n\"저는 피고가 그 사건 이전에 아버지를 살해할 명백한 의도를 완전히 품고 있었다고 단정할 수는 없습니다.\" 하고 검사가 말을 이었다. \"그러나 그 생각은 여러 차례 그에게 떠올랐고, 그는 그것을 곱씹어 보았으니, 이에 관해서는 우리에게 사실과 증인과 그 자신의 말이 있습니다. 배심원 여러분, 저는 고백하건대,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다. \"오늘에 이르기까지 저는 피고에게 의식적인 사전 계획을 인정해야 할지 확신하지 못하고 있었습니다. 저는 그가 그 운명의 순간을 미리 머릿속에 그려 보았으되, 다만 그려 보았을 뿐이며, 그것을 하나의 가능성으로서 떠올렸을 뿐이라고 굳게 믿고 있었습니다. 그는 언제 어떻게 그 범죄를 저지를지 명확히 따져 보지는 않았던 것입니다.\n\n\"그러나 저의 망설임은 오늘까지였을 뿐, 방금 그 운명적인 문서가 법정에 제출되기 전까지였습니다. 여러분 스스로 저 젊은 여성의 외침을 들으셨습니다. '이것은 살인의 계획, 살인의 청사진이에요!' 그녀는 그 불행한 피고가 취중에 쓴 가련한 편지를 그렇게 규정했습니다. 그리고 실제로 그 편지에서 우리는 살인의 전모가 사전에 계획되었음을 알 수 있습니다. 그것은 이틀 전에 쓰인 것이니, 그리하여 우리는 이제 그 끔찍한 계획을 실행에 옮기기 마흔여덟 시간 전에, 피고가 이튿날 돈을 구하지 못한다면 이반이 떠나는 즉시 아버지를 살해하여 베개 밑에서 지폐가 든 봉투를 빼앗겠노라고 맹세했다는 사실을 확실히 알고 있습니다. '이반이 떠나가는 즉시'라고 했으니, 여러분도 들으셨다시피, 그는 모든 것을 따져 보았고, 모든 정황을 저울질했으며, 자신이 써 놓은 그대로 그 모든 것을 실행에 옮긴 것입니다. 사전 계획의 증거는 결정적입니다. 그 범죄는 돈을 위해 저질러진 것임에 틀림없으니, 그것은 명백히 진술되어 있고, 글로 쓰여 서명까지 되어 있습니다. 피고는 자신의 서명을 부인하지 않습니다.\n\n\"누군가는 그가 그것을 쓸 때 취해 있었다고 말할 것입니다. 그러나 그것이 이 편지의 가치를 떨어뜨리지는 않으며, 오히려 정반대입니다. 그는 술에 취해서, 맨정신일 때 계획했던 것을 쓴 것입니다. 만약 맨정신일 때 그것을 계획하지 않았더라면, 취중에 그것을 쓰지도 않았을 것입니다. 그러면 어찌하여 그가 술집에서 그 이야기를 떠벌렸는가 하고 물으실 것입니다. 그런 범죄를 미리 계획하는 자라면 입을 다물고 혼자만 간직하는 법이라고 말입니다. 그렇습니다, 그러나 그가 그 이야기를 떠벌린 것은 아직 계획을 세우기 전, 그저 그러한 욕망만을, 그저 그러한 충동만을 품고 있었을 때입니다. 그 후로 그는 그 이야기를 덜 했습니다. 그가 '메트로폴리스' 술집에서 그 편지를 쓰던 날 저녁, 그는 자신의 습관과는 달리 입을 다물고 있었으니, 비록 술을 마시고는 있었지만 말입니다. 그는 당구도 치지 않았고, 한구석에 앉아 아무와도 이야기하지 않았습니다. 물론 그는 한 점원을 그 자리에서 쫓아내기는 했으나, 그것은 거의 무의식적으로 한 짓이었으니, 그는 술집에 들어서면 소동을 일으키지 않고는 못 배기는 위인이었기 때문입니다. 최종 결심을 내린 뒤에 그가 자신의 계획을 미리 너무 많이 떠벌린 탓에 그것이 나중에 자신의 체포와 기소로 이어질지도 모른다는 불안을 느꼈으리라는 것은 사실입니다. 그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었으니, 이미 내뱉은 말을 도로 거둬들일 수는 없는 노릇이었지만, 운이 전에도 그를 도왔으니 다시 한번 도와주리라 여겼던 것입니다. 그는, 아시다시피, 자신의 별을 믿고 있었습니다! 저는 또한 그가 그 운명의 파국을 피하고자 무던히도 애를 썼다는 점을 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다. '내일 나는 모든 사람에게 돈을 빌려 보려 애쓸 것이다.' 하고 그는 자신의 독특한 말투로 쓰고 있으니, '그래도 그들이 내게 돈을 주지 않는다면, 피를 보게 될 것이다'라고 말입니다.\"\n\n여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 돈을 빌리려던 미탸의 모든 노력에 대한 상세한 묘사로 넘어갔다. 그는 삼소노프를 찾아간 일과 랴가비를 찾아간 여정을 묘사했다. \"시달리고, 조롱당하고, 굶주린 채로, 여비를 마련하느라 자신의 시계를 팔고 나서—비록 그는 자신에게 천오백 루블이 있었다고 우리에게 말하지만, 참으로 그럴듯한 이야기입니다만—애정의 대상을 읍내에 남겨 두고 떠난 데 대한 질투로 괴로워하며, 자신이 없는 동안 그녀가 표도르 파블로비치에게 가지 않을까 의심하면서, 그는 마침내 읍내로 돌아왔고, 그녀가 자신의 아버지 근처에 얼씬도 하지 않았음을 알고는 기뻐했습니다. 그는 직접 그녀를 그 보호자에게 바래다주었습니다. (이상하게도 그는 삼소노프에 대해서는 질투하지 않은 듯한데, 이는 심리적으로 흥미로운 대목입니다.) 그러고 나서 그는 뒤뜰에 마련한 자신의 매복 장소로 서둘러 돌아왔고, 거기서 스메르댜코프가 발작을 일으켰으며 다른 하인은 병이 났다는 사실을 알게 됩니다. 길은 훤히 트여 있고, 그는 '신호'를 알고 있으니, 이 얼마나 큰 유혹입니까! 그래도 그는 그 유혹을 뿌리치고, 한동안 읍내에 거주하며 우리 사이에서 높은 존경을 받아 온 한 부인, 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아갑니다. 오래도록 연민을 품고 그의 행적을 지켜봐 온 그 부인은 그에게 더없이 분별 있는 충고를 주었으니, 곧 방탕한 생활과 꼴사나운 연애, 술집의 난봉질로 청춘과 정력을 허비하는 짓을 그만두고 시베리아의 금광으로 떠나라는 것이었습니다. '그것이야말로 당신의 들끓는 정력과 낭만적인 성격, 모험을 향한 갈증을 풀어 줄 출구가 될 거예요'라고 말입니다.\"\n\n이 대화의 결말과, 피고가 그루셴카가 삼소노프의 집에 머물러 있지 않았음을 알게 된 순간, 그리고 그녀가 자신을 속이고 이제 자신의 아버지와 함께 있다는 생각에 질투와 신경의 탈진으로 기진맥진한 그 불운한 사내를 사로잡은 돌연한 광란을 묘사하고 난 뒤, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 우연이라는 것의 치명적인 영향력을 곱씹는 것으로 결론을 맺었다. \"만약 그 하녀가 자신의 여주인이 옛 연인과 함께 모크로예에 있다고 그에게 말했더라면, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았을 것입니다. 그러나 그녀는 정신을 잃고, 그저 맹세하며 자신은 모른다고 항변할 수밖에 없었으니, 피고가 그 자리에서 그녀를 죽이지 않은 것은 오로지 자신을 배신한 정부의 뒤를 쫓아 날아가 버렸기 때문일 뿐입니다.\n\n\"그러나 주목하십시오, 그토록 광란에 빠져 있으면서도 그는 놋쇠 절굿공이를 챙겨 들고 갔습니다. 어찌하여 그것을? 어찌하여 다른 무기가 아니라? 그러나 그는 한 달 내내 자신의 계획을 곱씹으며 그것에 대비해 왔으므로, 눈에 띈 무기 비슷한 것이라면 무엇이든 움켜쥐었을 것입니다. 그는 지난 한 달 동안 그런 종류의 물건이라면 무엇이든 무기로 쓸 수 있으리라는 것을 깨닫고 있었기에, 즉시, 망설임 없이 그것이 자신의 목적에 쓸모가 있으리라는 것을 알아차렸습니다. 그러니 그가 그 운명의 절굿공이를 움켜쥔 것은 결코 무의식적인 일도, 결코 자기도 모르게 한 일도 아니었습니다. 그러고 나서 우리는 그가 아버지의 정원에 있는 것을 발견합니다. 길은 훤히 트여 있고, 증인도 없으며, 어둠과 질투뿐입니다. 그녀가 거기에, 그와 함께, 자신의 연적과 함께, 그 품 안에 안겨, 어쩌면 바로 그 순간 자신을 비웃고 있을지도 모른다는 의심이 그의 숨을 멎게 했습니다. 그리고 그것은 단순한 의심이 아니었으니, 기만은 노골적이고 명백한 것이었습니다. 그녀는 틀림없이 거기에, 저 불 켜진 방에 있을 것이며, 틀림없이 칸막이 뒤에 있을 것입니다. 그런데도 그 불행한 사내는, 자기가 창가로 살그머니 다가가 공손히 안을 들여다본 뒤, 무언가 끔찍하고 부도덕한 일이 벌어질까 두려워 조심스레 물러났다고 우리더러 믿어 달라는 것입니다. 더욱이 그는 우리를, 곧 그의 성격을 이해하고 있고, 그 순간 그의 심리 상태를 알고 있으며, 그가 그 집에 즉시 들어갈 수 있는 신호를 알고 있었다는 것을 아는 우리를 그렇게 설득하려 드는 것입니다.\" 이 대목에서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 말을 멈추고, 스메르댜코프가 살인에 연루되었다는 의혹을 낱낱이 논하기 시작했다. 그는 이를 매우 세세하게 다루었으니, 비록 그가 그 의혹을 경멸하는 척하기는 했으나 그 문제를 대단히 중요하게 여기고 있다는 것을 모두가 깨달았다.\n\n# Chapter VIII. A Treatise On Smerdyakov\n\n\"우선, 이 의혹은 대체 어디에서 비롯된 것입니까?\"(이폴리트 키릴로비치가 입을 열었다.) \"스메르댜코프가 살인을 저질렀다고 처음으로 부르짖은 사람은 다름 아닌 피고 자신으로, 그가 체포되던 바로 그 순간이었습니다. 그런데도 그때부터 지금에 이르기까지 그는 이 혐의를 뒷받침할 단 하나의 사실도, 사실의 희미한 암시조차도 내놓지 못했습니다. 이 혐의를 뒷받침하는 사람은 단 세 명뿐이니—피고의 두 형제와 스베틀로바 부인입니다. 이들 형제 가운데 형이 자신의 의혹을 입에 담은 것은 다름 아닌 오늘이었는데, 그때 그는 의심할 바 없이 뇌염을 앓고 있었습니다. 그러나 우리가 아는 바로는 그가 지난 두 달 동안 동생의 유죄에 관한 우리의 확신을 완전히 함께해 왔고, 그 생각에 맞서려 들지 않았다는 것입니다. 그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하겠습니다. 동생은 스메르댜코프의 유죄에 관한 자기 생각을 뒷받침할 사실이라곤 손톱만큼도 없으며, 다만 피고 자신의 말과 그 얼굴 표정으로부터 그런 결론에 이르렀을 뿐이라고 인정했습니다. 그렇습니다, 그 어이없는 증거를 그는 오늘 두 차례나 내놓았습니다. 스베틀로바 부인은 한층 더 어이없었습니다. '피고가 여러분께 하는 말은 믿으셔야 합니다. 그분은 거짓말을 할 사람이 아닙니다.' 스메르댜코프에게 불리한 증거라고는 이 세 사람이 내놓은 것이 전부인데, 이들은 모두 피고의 운명에 깊이 얽혀 있는 사람들입니다. 그런데도 스메르댜코프의 유죄라는 가설이 떠들썩하게 퍼져 나갔고, 지금도 여전히 주장되고 있습니다. 이것이 믿을 만한 일입니까? 이것이 생각할 수나 있는 일입니까?\"\n\n여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 \"광기의 발작 속에서 스스로 목숨을 끊은\" 스메르댜코프의 인물됨을 묘사할 필요를 느꼈다. 그는 스메르댜코프를 지력이 약하고 어설픈 교양을 갖춘 사내로 그렸으니, 자기 수준을 넘어서는 철학적 관념들과 어떤 현대적 의무론에 휘둘려 균형을 잃은 자였는데, 그 의무론을 그는 실천으로는 자기 주인이자 어쩌면 친아버지였을 표도르 파블로비치의 무절제한 삶에서 배웠고, 이론으로는 주인의 큰아들 이반 표도로비치와 나눈 갖가지 기이한 철학적 대화에서 배웠다고 했다. 이반 표도로비치는 아마도 따분함을 느꼈거나 하인을 상대로 자신을 즐겁게 하고자 했던 탓에 이런 소일거리에 기꺼이 빠져들었던 것이다. \"그자는 자기 아버지 집에서 보낸 마지막 며칠 동안의 정신 상태에 관해 제게 직접 이야기했습니다.\" 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 설명했다. \"그러나 다른 이들 또한 이를 증언하였으니—피고 자신과 그 형제, 그리고 하인 그리고리, 다시 말해 그를 잘 알던 사람들 모두가 그러했습니다.\n\n\"더욱이 간질 발작으로 건강이 흔들리던 스메르댜코프는 닭만큼의 용기도 없었습니다. '그자는 내 발치에 엎드려 발에 입을 맞추었다.' 피고 자신이 우리에게 이렇게 말했는데, 그런 진술이 자신에게 얼마나 불리한지 미처 깨닫기도 전이었습니다. '그자는 간질 걸린 닭이다.' 그는 자기 특유의 말투로 그를 두고 이렇게 단언했습니다. 그리고 피고는 그를 자신의 심복으로 골랐으며(이것은 그 자신의 입에서 나온 말입니다), 겁을 주어 마침내 자기를 위한 첩자 노릇에 동의하도록 만들었습니다. 그 자격으로 그는 주인을 속였고, 지폐가 든 봉투의 존재와 집 안으로 들어올 수 있는 신호를 피고에게 일러바쳤습니다. 사실 어떻게 그가 말하지 않을 수 있었겠습니까? '그자는 나를 죽였을 겁니다, 저는 그자가 나를 죽일 것임을 알 수 있었습니다.' 그는 심문 자리에서 우리 앞에서마저 부들부들 떨고 벌벌 떨며 이렇게 말했으니, 그를 괴롭히던 자가 그 무렵 이미 체포되어 그에게 아무런 해도 끼칠 수 없었는데도 그러했습니다. '그자는 시시각각 저를 의심했습니다. 저는 두려움에 떨며 서둘러 모든 비밀을 그자에게 털어놓아 그를 달랬으니, 제가 그를 속이지 않았음을 알고 저를 살려 보내 주기를 바랐던 것입니다.' 이것이 그 자신의 말입니다. 저는 그것을 받아 적었고 또렷이 기억합니다. '그자가 제게 소리를 지르기 시작하면 저는 무릎을 꿇곤 했습니다.'\n\n\"그는 본디 매우 정직했고, 주인이 잃어버린 돈을 되찾아 준 이래 주인의 완전한 신임을 누리고 있었습니다. 그러므로 이 가엾은 사내가 은인으로 여겨 사랑하던 주인을 속인 데 대해 회한의 고통을 느꼈으리라고 짐작할 수 있습니다. 가장 노련한 의사들이 우리에게 말해 주는 바에 따르면, 간질을 심하게 앓는 사람은 언제나 끊임없고 병적인 자책에 빠지기 쉽다고 합니다. 그들은 자신의 '사악함'을 두고 괴로워하고, 흔히 아무런 이유도 없이 양심의 가책에 시달리며, 온갖 종류의 허물과 죄악을 부풀리고 흔히 지어내기까지 합니다. 그리고 여기, 정말로 공포와 협박에 떠밀려 잘못을 저지른 그런 유형의 사내가 있는 것입니다.\n\n\"게다가 그는 자기 눈앞에서 전개되어 가는 상황으로부터 무언가 끔찍한 일이 비롯될 것이라는 강한 예감을 품고 있었습니다. 이반 표도로비치가 그 참사 직전에 모스크바로 떠나려 할 때, 스메르댜코프는 자기가 무엇을 두려워하는지 분명히 말할 만큼 대담하지는 못했지만 그에게 남아 달라고 애원했습니다. 그는 넌지시 암시하는 데 그쳤으나, 그 암시는 이해받지 못했습니다.\n\n\"여기서 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 그가 이반 표도로비치를 보호자로 여겼다는 점이니, 그가 집 안에 있다는 것은 아무런 해도 일어나지 않으리라는 보증과도 같았던 것입니다. 드미트리 카라마조프가 술에 취해 쓴 편지의 한 구절을 떠올려 보십시오. '이반이 떠나기만 한다면 나는 그 늙은이를 죽이겠다.' 그러니 이반 표도로비치가 있다는 것은 누구에게나 집안의 평화와 질서에 대한 보증으로 보였던 것입니다.\n\n\"그러나 그는 떠났고, 젊은 주인이 떠난 지 한 시간도 못 되어 스메르댜코프는 간질 발작을 일으켰습니다. 그러나 이것은 충분히 이해할 만한 일입니다. 여기서 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 스메르댜코프가 일종의 공포와 절망에 짓눌린 채 그 마지막 며칠 동안, 긴장의 순간에 전에 그를 괴롭히던 발작 가운데 하나가 다시 닥쳐올지도 모른다고 느꼈다는 점입니다. 물론 그러한 발작의 날과 시각을 미리 알 수는 없으나, 모든 간질 환자는 자신에게 발작이 일어날 듯하다는 것을 미리 느낄 수 있다고 합니다. 의사들이 우리에게 그렇게 일러 줍니다. 그리하여 이반 표도로비치가 마차로 마당을 빠져나가자마자 스메르댜코프는 외롭고 보호받지 못하는 자기 처지에 의기소침해져 지하 저장고로 내려갔습니다. 그는 발작이 일어날지 어떨지, 또 만일 당장 닥쳐온다면 어쩔까 생각하며 계단을 내려갔습니다. 바로 그 불안, 바로 그 의심이 그런 발작에 늘 앞서는 목구멍의 경련을 불러일으켰고, 그는 정신을 잃고 저장고로 굴러떨어졌습니다. 그런데 이 더할 나위 없이 자연스러운 사건에서 사람들은 그가 일부러 발작을 가장했다는 의혹, 그 암시를 찾아내려 합니다. 그러나 만일 일부러였다면 곧바로 이런 의문이 떠오릅니다. 그의 동기가 무엇이었습니까? 그는 무엇을 노렸습니까? 무엇을 겨냥했습니까? 의학에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 듣자 하니 과학도 길을 잃을 수 있다고 합니다. 의사들은 가짜와 진짜를 가려내지 못했습니다. 그럴 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 제게 한 가지 질문에만 답해 주십시오. 그런 가장을 할 무슨 동기가 그에게 있었습니까? 만일 그가 살인을 꾸미고 있었다면, 바로 직전에 발작을 일으켜 온 집안의 주의를 끌고 싶어 했겠습니까?\n\n\"보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 살인이 일어난 그날 밤 표도르 파블로비치의 집에는 다섯 사람이 있었습니다—표도르 파블로비치 자신(그러나 그가 자기를 죽이지 않았음은 분명합니다), 그다음으로 그의 하인 그리고리, 그러나 그 자신이 거의 죽임을 당할 뻔했습니다. 세 번째 인물은 그리고리의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나이나, 그녀가 주인을 살해했다고 상상하는 것은 그저 부끄러운 일일 따름입니다. 그러면 두 사람이 남습니다—피고와 스메르댜코프입니다. 그러나 만일 자기가 살인자가 아니라는 피고의 진술을 믿어야 한다면, 스메르댜코프가 살인자였을 수밖에 없으니, 다른 대안이 없고 달리 찾을 사람이 없기 때문입니다. 바로 이것이, 어제 스스로 목숨을 끊은 그 불행한 백치에 대한 교묘하고도 어이없는 고발의 까닭입니다. 만일 누구든 다른 사람에게 한 가닥 의혹의 그림자라도 드리워졌더라면, 만일 여섯 번째 인물이 있었더라면, 저는 확신컨대 피고조차 스메르댜코프를 고발하기를 부끄러워했을 것이며 그 여섯 번째 인물을 고발했을 것입니다. 스메르댜코프에게 그 살인의 혐의를 씌우는 것은 완전히 터무니없는 일이기 때문입니다.\n\n\"여러분, 심리학은 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 의학도 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 논리학마저도 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 오로지 사실로만 눈을 돌려 사실이 우리에게 무엇을 말해 주는지 봅시다. 만일 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽였다면 어떻게 죽였습니까? 혼자서입니까, 아니면 피고의 도움을 받아서입니까? 첫 번째 대안을 따져 봅시다—그가 혼자 했다는 것 말입니다. 만일 그가 죽였다면 거기에는 어떤 목적이, 자기에게 돌아올 어떤 이득이 있었어야 합니다. 그러나 피고가 살인을 저지른 동기—증오, 질투 따위—라고는 그림자만큼도 없었던 스메르댜코프로서는 오로지 이득을 위해, 주인이 봉투에 넣는 것을 자기 눈으로 본 그 삼천 루블을 가로채기 위해서만 그를 죽일 수 있었을 것입니다. 그런데도 그는 다른 사람에게—그것도 가장 깊이 이해관계가 얽힌 사람, 곧 피고에게—그 돈과 신호에 관한 모든 것을, 봉투가 어디에 놓여 있는지, 거기에 무엇이 적혀 있는지, 무엇으로 묶여 있는지, 그리고 무엇보다도 집 안으로 들어올 수 있는 그 신호까지 일러 주었습니다. 그가 이렇게 한 것이 그저 자신을 폭로하기 위해서였습니까, 아니면 그 봉투를 손에 넣고 싶어 안달할 사람을 같은 일에 끌어들이기 위해서였습니까? '그렇지만,' 사람들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요, '그는 두려워서 그것을 털어놓은 것이다.' 그러나 이것을 어떻게 설명하시겠습니까? 그토록 대담하고 흉포한 행위를 머릿속에 그리고 또 실행에 옮길 수 있는 사내가, 세상 누구도 모르는, 그리고 그가 입을 다물고만 있었다면 그 누구도 결코 짐작조차 못 했을 사실들을 털어놓는단 말입니까!\n\n\"아닙니다, 그가 아무리 겁쟁이였다 한들, 만일 그런 범죄를 꾸미고 있었다면 봉투와 신호에 관해 누구에게도 발설하도록 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었을 터이니, 그것은 미리 자신을 폭로하는 것이나 다름없었기 때문입니다. 만일 무엇이라도 알려 주도록 강요당했다면 그는 무언가 지어냈을 것이고 어떤 거짓말이라도 했을 것이나, 그 점에 관해서는 입을 다물었을 것입니다. 왜냐하면 다른 한편으로, 만일 그가 돈에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않고 살인을 저질러 돈을 훔쳤다면, 세상 누구도 그를 강도살인의 혐의로 고발할 수 없었을 것이니, 그 돈을 본 사람도 그 외에는 없었고 그것이 집 안에 있다는 것을 아는 사람도 그 외에는 없었기 때문입니다. 설령 그가 살인 혐의로 고발되었다 하더라도, 다만 다른 어떤 동기에서 저질렀으리라고만 여겨졌을 것입니다. 그러나 아무도 미리 그에게서 그런 동기를 보지 못했고, 도리어 모두가 주인이 그를 아끼고 신임으로 그를 존중했음을 보았으니, 그는 물론 가장 마지막으로 의심받았을 사람입니다. 사람들은 동기를 지닌 사람을, 자기에게 그런 동기가 있다고 스스로 떠벌리고 그것을 조금도 숨기지 않은 사람을 가장 먼저 의심했을 것입니다. 사실상 그들은 살해당한 자의 아들 드미트리 표도로비치를 의심했을 것입니다. 만일 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽이고 돈을 훔쳤는데 그 아들이 그 죄로 고발되었더라면, 그것이야말로 물론 스메르댜코프에게 안성맞춤이었을 것입니다. 그런데도 우리는, 그가 살인을 꾸미면서도 그 아들 드미트리에게 돈과 봉투와 신호에 관해 일러 주었다고 믿어야 합니까? 그것이 논리에 맞습니까? 그것이 분명한 일입니까?\n\n\"스메르댜코프가 계획한 살인의 날이 닥쳐오자, 우리는 그가 가장된 발작을 일으켜 계단에서 굴러떨어지는 것을 봅니다—무슨 목적으로 말입니까? 첫째로, 약을 먹으려 작정하고 있던 그리고리가 집을 지킬 사람이 없음을 보고 그것을 미루고 망을 보며 남도록 하기 위해서요, 둘째로, 짐작건대 자기를 지켜 줄 사람이 없음을 본 주인이 아들의 방문을 두려워한 나머지 경계와 조심을 갑절로 더하도록 하기 위해서일 것입니다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 짐작건대 발작으로 몸을 가누지 못하게 된 그 자신, 곧 스메르댜코프가, 그가 늘 다른 모든 이들과 떨어져 자고 마음대로 드나들 수 있던 부엌에서, 별채 반대편 끝에 있는 그리고리의 방으로 옮겨지기 위해서일 것이니, 그곳은 그가 늘 눕혀지던 곳으로, 그들 부부의 침대에서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 칸막이로 가려져 있었습니다. 이것은 그가 발작을 일으킬 때마다 주인과 인정 많은 마르파 이그나티예브나가 세워 둔 오래된 관습이었습니다. 거기, 칸막이 뒤에 누워서 그는 십중팔구 시늉을 계속하느라 신음을 내기 시작하여 (그리고리와 그의 아내가 증언했듯이) 밤새도록 그들을 잠 못 들게 했을 것입니다. 그리고 이 모든 것이, 우리더러 믿으라는 바로는, 그가 더 편리하게 일어나 주인을 살해하기 위해서였다는 것입니다!\n\n\"그러나 사람들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요. 그가 의심받지 않기 위해 일부러 병을 가장했고, 살인을 저지르도록 유혹하려고 피고에게 돈과 신호를 일러 주었으며, 피고가 그를 살해하고 돈을 가지고 떠난 뒤—십중팔구 소란을 피워 사람들을 깨우면서—스메르댜코프가 일어나 들어갔다고, 제가 믿어야 한단 말입니까? 무엇 하러 말입니까? 주인을 두 번째로 살해하고 이미 훔쳐 간 돈을 빼내 가려고요? 여러분, 웃으십니까? 저는 이런 추측을 내놓는 것이 부끄럽습니다만, 믿기 어려운 일이긴 해도, 바로 그것이 피고가 주장하는 바입니다. 피고가 집을 떠나 그리고리를 쓰러뜨리고 소동을 일으킨 뒤에 스메르댜코프가 일어나 들어가 주인을 살해하고 돈을 훔쳤다는 것입니다! 스메르댜코프가 이것을 미리 헤아렸을 리 없다는 점은, 또 격분하여 노발대발한 아들이 신호를 알면서도 그저 공손히 안을 들여다보려고만 와서 스메르댜코프에게 노획물을 남겨 둔 채 물러나리라고 미리 내다보았을 리 없다는 점은 굳이 강조하지 않겠습니다. 배심원 여러분, 저는 여러분께 이 질문을 진지하게 던집니다. 스메르댜코프가 자기 범죄를 저지를 수 있었던 순간은 언제였습니까? 그 순간을 말해 보십시오, 그러지 못한다면 여러분은 그를 고발할 수 없습니다.\n\n\"그러나 어쩌면 발작은 진짜였고, 병든 그자가 갑자기 정신을 차려 외침 소리를 듣고 밖으로 나갔을 수도 있습니다. 그래서요—그다음은? 그는 주위를 둘러보고 이렇게 말했다는 겁니까, '주인을 죽이러 가면 어떨까?' 그런데 그 순간까지 정신을 잃고 누워 있었던 그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 어떻게 알았겠습니까? 그러나 이런 공상의 비약에도 한계가 있는 법입니다.\n\n\"'바로 그렇소.' 어떤 약삭빠른 이들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요, '하지만 만일 그들이 공모했다면 어떻소? 만일 그들이 함께 그를 살해하고 돈을 나눠 가졌다면—그땐 어떻소?' 참으로 묵직한 질문입니다! 그리고 그것을 뒷받침할 사실들이라는 게 가관입니다. 한 사람은 살인을 저지르며 온갖 수고를 떠맡는데, 그 공범은 한쪽에 드러누워 발작을 가장하니, 보아하니 모든 사람에게 의혹을 불러일으키고 주인에게 경각심을, 그리고리에게 경각심을 불러일으키기 위해서랍니다. 두 공범이 그런 정신 나간 계획을 짜도록 만든 동기가 무엇이었을지 알아본다면 흥미로울 것입니다.\n\n\"그러나 어쩌면 스메르댜코프 편에서는 능동적인 공모가 아니라 그저 수동적인 묵인의 경우였을지도 모릅니다. 어쩌면 스메르댜코프는 겁을 먹고 살인을 막지 않기로 동의했을 것이며, 비명을 지르거나 저항하지 않고 주인이 살해당하도록 내버려 둔 데 대해 자기가 책망받으리라 내다보고, 발작을 가장하여 자리를 피해도 좋다는 허락을 드미트리 카라마조프에게서 얻었을지도 모릅니다—'마음대로 그를 죽여라, 그건 내 알 바 아니다.' 그러나 스메르댜코프의 이 발작은 온 집안을 혼란에 빠뜨릴 수밖에 없었으니, 드미트리 카라마조프가 그런 계획에 결코 동의했을 리 없습니다. 그렇지만 그 점은 일단 접어 두겠습니다. 설령 그가 동의했다 하더라도, 그래도 결국 드미트리 카라마조프가 살인자이자 교사자이며, 스메르댜코프는 그저 수동적인 공범에 지나지 않으니, 아니, 공범도 아니고 다만 공포에 떠밀려 제 뜻과 달리 묵인했을 뿐이라는 결론이 나옵니다.\n\n\"그러나 우리가 보는 것은 무엇입니까? 체포되자마자 피고는 즉각 모든 죄를 스메르댜코프에게 떠넘기는데, 그를 공범으로 고발하는 것이 아니라 그가 바로 살인자라고 고발합니다. '그자가 혼자 했다.' 그는 말합니다. '그자가 그를 죽이고 돈을 훔쳤다. 그자의 손이 한 짓이다.' 곧바로 서로를 고발하기 시작하는 참으로 별난 공범들입니다! 게다가 카라마조프로서는 그것이 얼마나 위험한지 생각해 보십시오. 공범이 침대에 누워 있는 동안 살인을 저지른 뒤에, 그는 그 병자에게 죄를 떠넘기는데, 병자라면 충분히 그것에 분개하여 자기를 지키고자 진실을 자백할 만했습니다. 왜냐하면 법정이 곧 그가 어디까지 책임이 있는지 가려내리라는 것을 그가 충분히 알았을 것이고, 그래서 만일 처벌받는다 해도 진짜 살인자보다는 훨씬 가벼우리라고 충분히 헤아릴 만했기 때문입니다. 그러나 그렇다면 그는 틀림없이 자백을 했을 터인데도 그렇게 하지 않았습니다. 진짜 살인자가 끈질기게 그를 고발하며 자기 혼자 범죄를 저질렀다고 단언했는데도, 스메르댜코프는 그들의 공모를 결코 입에 올리지 않았습니다.\n\n\"더욱이 스메르댜코프는 심문 자리에서, 피고에게 지폐가 든 봉투와 신호에 관해 일러 준 사람이 바로 자기이며, 자기가 없었다면 피고는 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐을 것이라고 자청하여 진술했습니다. 만일 그가 정말 죄 있는 공범이었다면, 심문 자리에서 그토록 선뜻 이런 진술을 했겠습니까? 도리어 그는 그것을 감추려, 사실을 왜곡하거나 축소하려 했을 것입니다. 그러나 그는 사실을 왜곡하거나 축소하기는커녕 도리어 거리가 멀었습니다. 공모 혐의로 고발될까 봐 두려워할 것이 없는 결백한 사내가 아니고서는 누구도 그처럼 행동할 수 없었을 것입니다. 그리고 자기 병과 이 참사에서 비롯된 우울의 발작 속에서 그는 어제 목을 매었습니다. 그는 자기 특유의 말투로 쓴 쪽지를 남겼습니다. '나는 누구에게도 죄를 씌우지 않으려고 내 뜻과 의향에 따라 스스로 목숨을 끊는다.' '나는 살인자다, 카라마조프가 아니다'라고 덧붙이는 것이 그에게 무슨 대수였겠습니까? 그러나 그는 그것을 덧붙이지 않았습니다. 양심이 그를 자살로는 이끌면서 죄의 고백으로는 이끌지 못했단 말입니까?\n\n\"그래서 그다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌습니까? 방금 삼천 루블어치의 지폐가 법정으로 들어왔고, 우리는 그것이 지금 우리 앞 탁자 위에 놓인 봉투 속에 들어 있던 바로 그 지폐이며, 증인이 그것을 하루 전에 스메르댜코프에게서 받았다는 말을 들었습니다. 그러나 저는 그 괴로운 장면을 다시 떠올릴 필요는 없겠고, 다만 한두 가지 논평을 덧붙이려 하는데, 처음에는 누구에게나 한눈에 드러나지 않아서 간과되기 쉬운 그런 사소한 것들을 골라 보겠습니다. 첫째로, 스메르댜코프는 회한 때문에 돈을 돌려주고 어제 목을 매었음이 분명합니다. 그리고 그가 자기 죄를 이반 카라마조프에게 고백한 것도 바로 어제로, 이반 카라마조프가 우리에게 그렇게 알려 주었습니다. 만일 정말 그렇지 않다면, 대체 왜 이반 표도로비치는 지금까지 입을 다물고 있었겠습니까? 그러니 만일 그가 고백했다면, 죄 없는 피고가 이튿날 이 끔찍한 시련에 맞서야 한다는 것을 알면서도, 어째서, 거듭 묻건대, 그는 뒤에 남긴 마지막 쪽지에서 모든 진실을 털어놓지 않았단 말입니까?\n\n\"돈만으로는 아무런 증거가 되지 못합니다. 일주일 전, 그야말로 우연히도, 이 법정에 있는 저와 다른 두 사람의 귀에 한 가지 사실이 들어왔는데, 이반 표도로비치가 각각 오천 루블짜리 오 퍼센트 이표 두 장—다시 말해 도합 일만 루블을—환전하기 위해 도청 소재지로 보냈다는 것입니다. 저는 다만 누구에게나 돈이 있을 수 있다는 것, 그리고 이 지폐가 표도르 파블로비치의 봉투 속에 들어 있던 것과 같은 것임이 증명될 수 없다는 것을 지적하기 위해 이를 언급할 따름입니다.\n\n\"이반 카라마조프는 어제 진짜 살인자에게서 그토록 중대한 통보를 받고서도 꼼짝하지 않았습니다. 왜 그는 그것을 곧바로 신고하지 않았습니까? 왜 그 모든 것을 아침까지 미루었습니까? 저는 그 까닭을 추측할 권리가 있다고 봅니다. 그의 건강은 지난 일주일 동안 무너져 가고 있었습니다. 그는 한 의사에게, 그리고 가장 가까운 친구들에게, 자기가 환각에 시달리며 죽은 자들의 환영을 본다고 털어놓았습니다. 그는 오늘 그를 쓰러뜨린 뇌염의 발작 직전에 있었습니다. 이런 상태에서 그는 갑자기 스메르댜코프의 죽음을 전해 들었고, 곧바로 이렇게 생각한 것입니다. '그자는 죽었으니 그자에게 죄를 떠넘겨 동생을 구할 수 있다. 내게는 돈이 있다. 지폐 한 뭉치를 가져다가 스메르댜코프가 죽기 전에 내게 주었다고 말하자.' 여러분은 그것이 떳떳지 못한 일이라고 하시겠지요. 죽은 자를 헐뜯는 것은, 비록 동생을 구하기 위해서라 해도 떳떳지 못한 일입니다. 옳은 말씀입니다. 그러나 만일 그가 무의식중에 그를 헐뜯은 것이라면 어떻습니까? 만일 하인의 갑작스러운 죽음이라는 소식에 끝내 정신이 무너져, 정말로 그러했다고 그가 상상한 것이라면 어떻습니까? 여러분은 조금 전의 장면을 보셨습니다. 여러분은 그 증인의 상태를 보셨습니다. 그는 일어서서 말을 하고 있었으나, 그의 정신은 어디에 있었습니까?\n\n\"그다음에는 문서가, 범죄 이틀 전에 쓰인 피고의 편지가 뒤따랐는데, 거기에는 살인의 완전한 청사진이 담겨 있었습니다. 그렇다면 우리가 무엇 하러 다른 청사진을 찾고 있는 것입니까? 범죄는 정확히 이 청사진대로, 다름 아닌 그것을 쓴 자에 의해 저질러졌습니다. 그렇습니다, 배심원 여러분, 일은 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 진행되었습니다! 그는 자기 애정의 대상이 함께 있다고 굳게 믿었으면서도, 아버지의 창문에서 공손하게 머뭇거리며 달아나지 않았습니다. 아닙니다, 그것은 터무니없고 있을 법하지 않은 일입니다! 그는 안으로 들어가 그를 살해했습니다. 십중팔구 그는 분노에 휩싸여, 증오하던 연적을 보자마자 원한에 불타올라 그를 죽였을 것입니다. 그러나 그를 죽이고 나서, 아마도 놋쇠 절굿공이로 단 한 차례 내리쳐 죽인 뒤에, 그리고 꼼꼼히 살펴 그녀가 거기에 없음을 확인하고 나서도, 그는 베개 밑으로 손을 넣어 봉투를—지금 우리 앞 탁자 위에 그 찢긴 껍데기가 놓여 있는 그 봉투를—꺼내는 것은 잊지 않았습니다.\n\n\"제가 이 사실을 언급하는 것은, 제 생각으로는 매우 특징적인 한 가지 정황을 여러분께서 주목하시도록 하기 위해서입니다. 만일 그가 노련한 살인자였고 오로지 이득을 위해 살인을 저질렀더라면, 발견된 그대로 시신 곁 바닥에 찢긴 봉투를 남겨 두었겠습니까? 가령 그것이 스메르댜코프였더라면, 주인을 죽여 그를 털려 했더라면, 그는 그저 봉투를 통째로 가지고 떠났을 것이고, 굳이 희생자의 시신 위에서 그것을 뜯어 보는 수고는 하지 않았을 것이니, 지폐가 봉투 속에 들어 있음을—그것은 그가 보는 앞에서 넣어지고 봉인되었으니—틀림없이 알았을 것이고, 봉투를 통째로 가지고 갔더라면 그 누구도 도난 사실을 결코 알지 못했을 것이기 때문입니다. 여러분께 묻습니다, 스메르댜코프가 그런 식으로 행동했겠습니까? 그가 봉투를 바닥에 남겨 두었겠습니까?\n\n\"아닙니다, 이것은 광란에 빠진 살인자의 행동, 도둑이 아니며 그날 이전에는 한 번도 도둑질해 본 적 없는 살인자의 행동이었으니, 그는 베개 밑에서 지폐를 낚아챘으되, 그것을 훔치는 도둑처럼이 아니라, 마치 그것을 훔쳐 간 도둑에게서 제 것을 빼앗는 것처럼 그리한 것입니다. 왜냐하면 바로 그것이 그 돈에 관하여 드미트리 카라마조프 속에서 거의 미친 집착이 되어 버린 관념이었기 때문입니다. 그래서 그는 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 봉투에 덤벼들어, 그 속에 돈이 들어 있는지 확인하려고 그것을 뜯어 열고는, 돈을 주머니에 넣은 채 달아났으니, 바닥에 그 찢긴 봉투로 자신에게 불리한 어이없는 증거를 남겨 두었다는 사실을 헤아릴 생각조차 하지 못했습니다. 이 모든 것은 그가 스메르댜코프가 아니라 카라마조프였기 때문이니, 그는 생각하지 않았고, 곱씹지 않았으며, 또 어찌 그럴 수 있었겠습니까? 그는 달아났습니다. 그는 등 뒤에서 하인이 외치는 소리를 들었습니다. 늙은이가 그를 붙들어 멈춰 세웠다가 놋쇠 절굿공이에 맞아 땅바닥에 쓰러졌습니다.\n\n\"피고는 연민에 사로잡혀 그를 보려고 뛰어내렸습니다. 믿어지십니까, 그는 자기가 연민에서, 동정심에서, 그를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 있을까 보려고 뛰어내렸다고 우리에게 말합니다. 그때가 동정심을 드러낼 순간이었습니까? 아닙니다. 그는 그저 자기 범죄의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 살았는지 확인하려고 뛰어내린 것입니다. 그 밖의 다른 감정, 다른 동기라면 무엇이든 부자연스러울 것입니다. 그가 그리고리에게 수고를 들여 손수건으로 머리를 닦아 주었고, 그가 죽었다고 스스로 확신하고서, 얼이 빠진 채 피투성이가 되어 자기 애인의 집으로 달려갔다는 점을 주목하십시오. 자기가 피투성이여서 곧바로 발각되리라는 것을 그가 어떻게 한 번도 생각하지 못했단 말입니까? 그러나 피고 자신은 자기가 피투성이라는 것조차 알아차리지 못했다고 우리에게 단언합니다. 그것은 믿을 만한 일이며, 충분히 있을 수 있는 일이고, 그런 순간에 범죄자들에게는 늘 그런 일이 일어납니다. 한 가지 점에서는 그들이 악마 같은 교활함을 드러내는가 하면, 다른 점은 그들에게서 송두리째 빠져나가 버립니다. 그러나 그는 그 순간 오로지 한 가지만을 생각하고 있었습니다—그녀는 어디에 있는가? 그는 즉각 그녀가 어디 있는지 알아내고 싶었기에 그녀의 거처로 달려갔다가, 뜻밖의 어이없는 소식을 알게 되었습니다—그녀가 첫 연인을 만나러 모크로예로 떠나 버렸다는 것이었습니다.\"\n\n# Chapter IX. The Galloping Troika. The End Of The Prosecutor’s Speech.\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치는 역사적 서술법을 택했는데, 이는 신경질적인 웅변가라면 누구나 즐겨 쓰는 방식으로, 그들은 그 방식의 제약 속에서 제 분방한 수사에 대한 고삐를 발견하는 것이다. 연설의 이 대목에 이르러 그는 그루셴카의 \"첫사랑 남자\"에 관한 논평으로 흘러들어, 이 주제를 두고 흥미로운 생각 몇 가지를 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n\"누구에게나 미친 듯이 질투를 느끼던 카라마조프는, 말하자면 이 첫사랑 남자 앞에서 단숨에 무너지고 스스로를 지워 버렸습니다. 더욱 기이한 것은, 그가 이 무서운 연적을 거의 생각해 본 적조차 없었던 듯하다는 점입니다. 그는 그자를 먼 곳의 위험쯤으로만 여겼고, 카라마조프란 언제나 현재 속에서 사는 인간입니다. 어쩌면 그는 그자를 한낱 허구로 보았는지도 모릅니다. 그러나 상처 입은 그의 마음은 이 새로운 연적을 그 여자가 감추고 자기를 속여 왔다는 사실을 단번에 알아챘으니, 그자는 그 여자에게만은 결코 허구가 아니었고, 도리어 그녀의 삶에서 단 하나뿐인 희망이었기 때문입니다. 이를 단번에 깨닫자 그는 체념하고 말았습니다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 저는 피고의 성격에서 드러난 이 뜻밖의 면모를 짚고 넘어가지 않을 수 없습니다. 그는 별안간 거부할 수 없는 정의에의 갈망과, 여인에 대한 존중과, 그녀가 사랑할 권리를 지녔다는 인정을 드러냅니다. 그것도 바로 그녀를 위해 제 손을 아비의 피로 더럽힌 바로 그 순간에 말입니다! 사실인즉 그가 흘린 피는 이미 복수를 외치고 있었으니, 이 세상에서 제 영혼과 제 삶을 망가뜨려 놓은 그는 바로 그 순간 스스로에게 물을 수밖에 없었던 것입니다. 자기 영혼보다 더 소중한 그 존재, 그녀에게 이제 자기란 무엇이며 무엇이 될 수 있겠는가를 말입니다. 한때 자기가 배신했던 여인에게 새로운 사랑을 품고 참회하여 돌아온, 명예로운 청혼과 함께 개과천선한 행복한 삶을 약속하는 그 옛 연인과 견주어서 말입니다. 그런데 그, 이 불운한 사내가 이제 그녀에게 무엇을 줄 수 있으며, 무엇을 바칠 수 있겠습니까?\n\n\"카라마조프는 이 모든 것을 느꼈고, 자신의 범죄로 인해 모든 길이 막혀 버렸으며, 자기가 앞날을 가진 인간이 아니라 선고를 받은 죄인이라는 것을 알았습니다! 이 생각이 그를 짓눌렀습니다. 그리하여 그는 즉시 어떤 미친 듯한 계획 하나로 날아들었으니, 카라마조프 같은 성격의 인간에게는 그것이야말로 자신의 끔찍한 처지에서 벗어날 단 하나의 불가피한 출구로 보였을 것이 분명합니다. 그 출구란 곧 자살이었습니다. 그는 친구 페르호틴에게 저당 잡혀 둔 권총을 찾으러 달려갔고, 달려가는 도중에 주머니에서 돈을 꺼냈으니, 바로 그 돈을 위해 그는 제 손을 아비의 핏물로 더럽혔던 것입니다. 오, 이제 그에게는 그 어느 때보다도 돈이 절실했습니다. 카라마조프는 죽으리라, 카라마조프는 제 손으로 총을 쏘리라, 그리고 그것이 사람들의 기억에 남으리라! 과연 그는 시인이었으니, 평생토록 촛불을 양 끝에서 동시에 태워 온 인간이었습니다. '그녀에게로, 그녀에게로! 그리고 거기서, 오 거기서 나는 온 세상에 잔치를 베풀어 주리라, 일찍이 없던 그런 잔치를, 두고두고 기억되고 회자될 그런 잔치를! 거친 환락의 함성 속에서, 무모한 집시의 노래와 춤 속에서 나는 잔을 들어 내가 사모하는 여인과 그녀가 새로이 찾은 행복을 위해 건배하리라! 그러고는 그 자리에서, 그녀의 발치에서, 그녀 앞에 내 머리통을 박살 내어 나 자신을 벌하리라! 그녀는 이따금 미탸 카라마조프를 떠올리리라, 미탸가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑했는지 알리라, 미탸를 가엾이 여기리라!'\n\n\"여기서 우리는 효과를 노리는 지나친 욕심과, 낭만적인 절망과 감상, 그리고 카라마조프 일가 특유의 거친 무모함을 봅니다. 그렇습니다, 그러나 그 밖에도 무언가가 더 있습니다, 배심원 여러분, 영혼 속에서 부르짖고, 끊임없이 마음속에서 고동치며, 가슴을 죽음에 이르도록 좀먹는 무언가가 말입니다. 그 무언가란 바로 양심입니다, 배심원 여러분, 양심의 심판이요, 그 무서운 고통입니다! 권총이 모든 것을 매듭지으리라, 권총만이 유일한 출구다! 그러나 그 너머는—나는 카라마조프가 그 순간 '그 너머에 무엇이 있는가'를 의아해했는지, 또 카라마조프가 햄릿처럼 '그 너머에 무엇이 있는가'를 의아해할 수 있었는지 모릅니다. 아닙니다, 배심원 여러분, 그들에게는 그들의 햄릿이 있으나, 우리에게는 아직도 우리의 카라마조프가 있을 따름입니다!\"\n\n여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 미탸가 채비를 갖추던 광경을, 페르호틴의 집에서, 가게에서, 마부들과 함께 벌어진 장면을 세밀하게 그려 보였다. 그는 증인들이 확인해 준 수많은 말과 행동을 인용했고, 그 묘사는 청중에게 끔찍한 인상을 남겼다. 사실들을 한데 모아 놓으니, 이 시달리고 절망에 빠진 사내의 죄상이 또렷하고도 설득력 있게 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그가 무엇 때문에 조심할 필요가 있었겠습니까? 그는 두세 번이나 거의 자백할 뻔했고, 넌지시 비추었으며, 하마터면 다 털어놓을 뻔했습니다.\" (이어서 증인들의 진술이 따라 나왔다.) \"그는 심지어 자기를 태우고 달리던 농부에게 이렇게 외치기까지 했습니다. '이보게, 자네는 지금 살인자를 태우고 가는 줄 아나!' 그러나 그로서는 다 털어놓는 것이 불가능했으니, 그는 모크로예에 당도하여 거기서 자신의 로맨스를 끝맺어야만 했던 것입니다. 그렇다면 이 불운한 사내를 기다리고 있던 것은 무엇이었겠습니까? 모크로예에 당도한 거의 첫 순간부터 그는, 자신의 무적의 연적이 어쩌면 결코 그토록 무적은 아닐지도 모른다는 것을, 그들이 새로이 찾은 행복을 위한 건배 따위는 바라지도 않고 받아들여지지도 않으리라는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그러나 배심원 여러분, 여러분은 예심을 통해 이미 그 사실들을 알고 계십니다. 연적에 대한 카라마조프의 승리는 완벽했고, 그의 영혼은 전혀 새로운 국면으로, 어쩌면 그의 영혼이 지나왔거나 앞으로 지나갈 가운데 가장 끔찍한 국면으로 옮아갔습니다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 단언컨대,\" 검사가 말을 이었다. \"능욕당한 본성과 죄지은 가슴은 그 어떤 지상의 정의보다도 더 완벽하게 스스로 복수를 가합니다. 뿐만 아니라 지상의 정의와 형벌은 도리어 본성의 형벌을 덜어 주며, 실로 그런 순간에 죄인의 영혼에게는 절망으로부터의 구원으로서 없어서는 안 될 것입니다. 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것을, 그를 위해 그녀가 첫사랑 남자를 물리쳤다는 것을, 그녀가 그를, 미탸를 새로운 삶으로 부르고 있다는 것을, 그녀가 그에게 행복을 약속하고 있다는 것을 알았을 때—더구나 언제 말입니까? 그에게는 모든 것이 끝나 버리고 아무것도 가능하지 않게 된 바로 그때 말입니다!—카라마조프가 느꼈을 공포와 도덕적 고뇌를 나는 차마 상상조차 할 수 없습니다!\n\n\"덧붙여, 그 순간 피고의 처지를 비추어 주는 중요한 한 가지를 괄호 안에 넣어 두는 셈으로 짚어 두겠습니다. 이 여인, 그의 이 사랑은 마지막 순간까지, 그가 체포되는 바로 그 찰나까지도 그에게는 도저히 닿을 수 없는 존재, 열렬히 갈망하면서도 닿을 수 없는 존재였습니다. 그런데 어찌하여 그는 그때 제 손으로 총을 쏘지 않았으며, 어찌하여 자신의 계획을 단념하고 심지어 권총이 어디 있는지조차 잊어버렸겠습니까? 그를 붙들어 둔 것은 바로 그 열렬한 사랑에의 갈망과 그것을 채우리라는 희망이었던 것입니다. 흥청거리는 내내 그는 자신이 흠모하는 정부 곁에 바짝 붙어 있었으니, 그녀는 그와 함께 그 잔치 자리에 있었고 그에게 그 어느 때보다도 매혹적이고 사로잡는 존재였습니다—그는 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않고, 그녀 앞에서 자신을 낮추며 경배를 바쳤습니다.\n\n\"그의 정념은 한순간 체포의 두려움뿐 아니라 양심의 고통마저도 충분히 잠재울 만했습니다. 한순간, 오, 그저 한순간만은 말입니다! 나는 이러한 영향에 속절없이 사로잡힌 죄인의 마음 상태를 그려 볼 수 있습니다—첫째로 술과 소음과 흥분의 영향, 춤의 쿵쿵거림과 노래의 비명 같은 외침의 영향, 그리고 술기운에 발그레해진 채 그를 향해 노래하고 춤추고 웃어 보이는 그녀의 영향입니다! 둘째로는, 그 숙명적인 종말이 아직 멀리 있을지도 모른다는, 적어도 이튿날 아침까지는 그들이 와서 자기를 잡아가지는 않으리라는 막연한 희망입니다. 그러니 그에게는 몇 시간이 남아 있었고, 그것은 큰 것입니다, 아주 큰 것입니다! 몇 시간이면 사람은 많은 것을 생각할 수 있습니다. 나는 그가 사형대로 끌려가는 죄수들이 느끼는 것과 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈으리라 상상합니다. 그들 앞에는 또 하나의 길고 긴 거리가 펼쳐져 있고, 수천 명의 사람들 곁을 지나 천천히 걸어가야 합니다. 그러고는 또 다른 거리로 꺾어 들어, 그 거리의 끝에 이르러서야 비로소 무시무시한 처형 장소가 나타나는 것입니다! 나는 그 길의 처음에 그 죄인이 치욕스러운 수레에 앉아 자기 앞에 아직도 무한한 삶이 남아 있다고 느끼리라 짐작합니다. 집들이 뒤로 물러나고 수레는 나아갑니다—오, 그것은 아무것도 아니다, 두 번째 거리로 꺾어드는 모퉁이까지는 아직 멀다, 그리고 그는 여전히 대담하게 좌우를 둘러보며, 무심하면서도 호기심에 찬 눈길로 그를 응시하는 수천 명의 사람들을 바라보고, 자기도 그들과 똑같은 사람이라고 여전히 여깁니다. 그러나 이제 다음 거리로 꺾어드는 모퉁이가 다가옵니다. 오, 그것은 아무것도 아니다, 아무것도 아니다, 아직 거리 하나가 통째로 남아 있다, 그리고 아무리 많은 집을 지나쳤어도 그는 여전히 남은 집이 많다고 생각할 것입니다. 그렇게 마지막 순간까지, 바로 그 사형대에 이르기까지 말입니다.\n\n\"카라마조프도 그때 그러했으리라고 나는 상상합니다. '저들에게는 아직 짬이 없었다,' 그는 분명 이렇게 생각했을 것입니다. '나는 아직 어떤 출구를 찾을 수 있을지 모른다, 오, 아직 무슨 방어책을 세울 시간이 있다, 그런데 지금, 지금—그녀가 이토록 매혹적이지 않은가!'\n\n\"그의 영혼은 혼란과 두려움으로 가득했으나, 그래도 그는 가까스로 돈의 절반을 따로 떼어 어딘가에 감추었습니다—그렇지 않고서는 그가 방금 아비의 베개 밑에서 빼낸 삼천 루블 가운데 꼭 절반이 사라진 것을 달리 설명할 길이 없습니다. 그는 전에도 한두 번 모크로예에 가 본 적이 있었고, 이미 그곳에서 이틀이나 내리 흥청거린 적이 있었으니, 통로며 별채가 딸린 그 오래되고 커다란 집을 잘 알고 있었습니다. 나는 그 돈의 일부가 체포되기 얼마 전에 그 집 어딘가에, 어떤 틈새에, 어떤 마룻장 밑에, 어떤 구석에, 지붕 밑에 감추어졌으리라 상상합니다. 무슨 목적으로 그랬느냐고 물으실 것입니다. 아니, 파국은 당장에라도 닥칠 수 있는 것입니다, 물론이지요. 그는 아직 그것에 어떻게 대처할지 궁리하지 못했고, 그럴 시간도 없었으며, 그의 머리는 욱신거렸고 그의 마음은 그녀에게 가 있었으나, 돈은—돈은 어쨌거나 없어서는 안 될 것이었습니다! 돈이 있으면 사람은 언제나 사람 구실을 하는 법입니다. 그런 순간에 그런 선견지명을 부린다는 것이 여러분에게는 부자연스럽게 비칠지도 모르겠습니다. 그러나 그는 한 달 전 어느 위태롭고 흥분된 순간에 자기 돈을 반으로 나누어 작은 주머니에 꿰매어 두었다고 우리에게 직접 장담하고 있습니다. 그것이 사실이 아니라는 것은 곧 증명해 보이겠습니다만, 그것은 적어도 그 발상이 카라마조프에게 낯익은 것이었음을, 그가 그것을 마음속에 그려 본 적이 있음을 보여 줍니다. 뿐만 아니라 그가 예심에서 천오백 루블을 (실재하지도 않은) 주머니에 넣어 두었노라고 진술했을 때, 그는 어쩌면 그 자리에서 영감처럼 떠오른 대로 그 작은 주머니를 지어낸 것인지도 모릅니다. 그는 두 시간 전에 자기 돈을 나누어 그 절반을 만일에 대비하여 아침까지 모크로예에 감추어 두었으니, 그저 그것을 몸에 지니고 있지 않으려 했던 것입니다. 두 극단, 배심원 여러분, 카라마조프는 두 극단을, 그것도 동시에 둘 다 마음에 품을 수 있는 인간임을 기억해 두십시오.\n\n\"우리는 그 집을 뒤졌으나 돈을 찾아내지는 못했습니다. 그것은 아직 거기 있을 수도 있고, 이튿날 사라져 지금 피고의 수중에 있을 수도 있습니다. 어쨌든 그는 그녀 곁에서 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었고, 그녀는 침대에 누워 있었으며, 그는 그녀에게 두 손을 뻗은 채 모든 것을 어찌나 까맣게 잊고 있었던지 자기를 체포하러 오는 사람들의 기척조차 듣지 못했습니다. 그는 마음속에 그 어떤 방어선도 미처 마련해 두지 못했습니다. 그는 불시에 붙들려 자신의 심판관들, 곧 자기 운명의 결정자들과 맞닥뜨리게 되었습니다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 우리가 직무를 수행하는 가운데 한 인간과 마주하는 것이 끔찍스럽게 느껴지는 순간들이 있으니, 그 인간을 생각해 보아도 끔찍한 일입니다! 죄인이 모든 것이 끝장났음을 보면서도 여전히 발버둥 치고, 여전히 발버둥 치려 들 때의 그 짐승 같은 공포를 지켜보는 순간들, 자기 보존의 본능이란 본능이 그의 안에서 한꺼번에 곤두서서, 그가 묻는 듯하고 고통에 찬 눈으로 여러분을 바라보며, 여러분을, 여러분의 얼굴을, 여러분의 생각을 살피고, 여러분이 어느 쪽에서 내리칠지 가늠하지 못한 채, 그 어지러운 머릿속으로 순식간에 수천 가지 계획을 짜내면서도, 여전히 입을 떼기를 두려워하고, 제 속을 드러낼까 두려워하는 그 순간들 말입니다! 이 영혼의 연옥, 이 짐승 같은 자기 보존의 갈증, 인간 영혼의 이 굴욕적인 순간들은 무시무시한 것이며, 때로는 변호사에게마저 죄인에 대한 공포와 연민을 불러일으킵니다. 그리고 그것이 바로 그때 우리 모두가 목격한 것이었습니다.\n\n\"처음에 그는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했고, 두려움에 사로잡혀 몹시 불리한 말들을 몇 마디 흘렸습니다. '피다! 나는 그것을 받아 마땅하다!' 그러나 그는 이내 자제했습니다. 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 미리 준비해 두지 못했으니, 그에게 준비된 것이라곤 노골적인 부인뿐이었습니다. '나는 아버지의 죽음에 죄가 없다.' 그것이 당장의 그의 방패막이였고, 그 뒤에서 그는 어떻게든 방벽을 쌓아 올리기를 바랐습니다. 그는 자신이 처음 흘린 그 불리한 외침들을, 하인 그리고리의 죽음에 대해서만 책임이 있을 뿐이라고 선언함으로써 서둘러 둘러댔습니다. '그 피흘림에 대해서는 내가 죄가 있으나, 그렇다면 누가 내 아버지를 죽였단 말입니까, 여러분, 누가 그를 죽였단 말입니까? 내가 아니라면 대체 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠습니까?' 들으셨습니까, 그는 우리에게 그것을 물었습니다, 바로 그것을 그에게 물으러 온 우리에게 말입니다! 그토록 성급하게 앞질러 튀어나온 그 말, '내가 아니라면'이라는 그 말, 그 속에 깃든 짐승 같은 교활함, 그 순진함, 그 카라마조프 특유의 조바심을 들으셨습니까? '나는 그를 죽이지 않았소, 그러니 내가 죽였다고 여겨서는 안 되오! 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소, 여러분, 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소,' 그는 서둘러 시인합니다 (그는 서둘렀으니, 끔찍이도 서둘렀습니다), '그러나 그래도 나는 죄가 없소, 그를 죽인 것은 내가 아니오.' 그는 자기가 그를 죽이고 싶었다는 것은 우리에게 인정하니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯합니다. 내가 얼마나 솔직한지 여러분 스스로 보시오, 그러니 내가 그를 죽이지 않았다는 것도 그만큼 더 빨리 믿어 주시오. 오, 이런 경우에 죄인이란 흔히 놀라우리만치 얕고 잘 속아 넘어가는 법입니다.\n\n\"바로 그 대목에서 우리 측 한 사람이 그에게, 마치 지나가는 말처럼, 더없이 단순한 질문을 던졌습니다. '스메르댜코프가 죽인 것은 아니오?' 그러자 우리가 예상한 대로 그는 우리가 자기를 앞질러 불시에 붙든 데 대해 무섭게 화를 냈으니, 그로서는 미처 발판을 마련해 스메르댜코프의 이름을 끌어들이기에 가장 자연스러운 순간을 골라 낚아챌 겨를이 없었던 까닭입니다. 그는 늘 그렇듯이 곧장 반대편 극단으로 치달아, 스메르댜코프는 그를 죽일 수 없었고, 그럴 만한 위인이 못 된다고 우리에게 장담하기 시작했습니다. 그러나 그를 믿어서는 안 됩니다, 그것은 한낱 그의 교활함이었을 뿐입니다. 그는 실은 스메르댜코프라는 발상을 단념한 것이 아니었으니, 도리어 그를 다시 끌어들일 작정이었던 것입니다. 사실 그가 끌어들일 수 있는 사람이라곤 달리 없었으나, 다만 그것을 나중으로 미루었을 뿐이니, 당장은 그 노선이 그에게는 망가져 버린 까닭이었습니다. 그는 어쩌면 이튿날, 또는 며칠 뒤에 기회를 보아 그를 끌어들여 우리에게 이렇게 외쳤을 것입니다. '여러분도 알다시피 나는 스메르댜코프에 대해 여러분보다 더 회의적이었소, 그 점은 여러분 스스로 기억할 것이오, 그러나 이제 나는 확신하오. 그가 죽인 것이오, 그가 죽인 것이 틀림없소!' 그리고 당장은 음울하고 신경질적인 부인으로 물러나 버립니다. 그러나 조바심과 분노에 떠밀려 그는, 자기가 어떻게 아버지의 창문을 들여다보았고 또 어떻게 공손하게 물러났는지에 대해 더없이 서툴고 믿을 수 없는 설명을 늘어놓았습니다. 무엇보다 고약한 것은, 그가 사태의 형편을, 그리고리가 진술한 증거를 까맣게 모르고 있었다는 점입니다.\n\n\"우리는 이어서 그의 몸을 수색했습니다. 그 수색은 그를 화나게 했으나 한편으로는 그를 고무했으니, 삼천 루블이 통째로 그의 몸에서 나오지 않고 그 절반만 나왔기 때문입니다. 그리고 분명 화가 나서 입을 다물고 있던 바로 그 순간에 그 작은 주머니라는 허구가 처음으로 그에게 떠올랐던 것입니다. 분명 그 자신도 그 이야기가 그럴듯하지 않다는 것을 의식하고 있었으며, 그것을 좀 더 그럴싸하게 들리도록, 제법 그럴듯한 로맨스로 엮어 내려고 안간힘을 다해 애썼습니다. 이런 경우 수사를 맡은 자들의 첫째 임무, 으뜸가는 과업은 죄인이 미리 채비를 갖추지 못하게 막는 것, 불시에 덮쳐서 그가 제 속에 품은 생각들을 그 온갖 단순함과 허무맹랑함과 모순 그대로 불쑥 내뱉게 하는 것입니다. 죄인의 입을 열게 하려면, 그가 미처 알지 못했고 예상할 수도 없었던 어떤 새로운 사실, 사건에 매우 중대한 어떤 정황을 별안간, 그러면서도 짐짓 지나가는 말처럼 알려 주는 수밖에 없습니다. 우리에게는 그런 사실이 마련되어 있었으니—그것은 피고가 빠져나간 그 열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언이었습니다. 그는 그 문에 대해 까맣게 잊고 있었고, 그리고리가 그것을 볼 수 있었으리라고는 의심조차 하지 못했습니다.\n\n\"그 효과는 놀라웠습니다. 그는 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 우리에게 소리쳤습니다. '그렇다면 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽인 것이오, 스메르댜코프였소!' 그리하여 그는 감추어 두었던 변호의 토대를 스스로 드러내고 말았으니, 그것도 가장 그럴듯하지 못한 모양새로 드러낸 것입니다. 스메르댜코프가 살인을 저지를 수 있었다면 그것은 오직 그가 그리고리를 쓰러뜨리고 달아난 뒤라야 했기 때문입니다. 우리가 그에게, 그리고리는 쓰러지기 전에 문이 열려 있는 것을 보았고, 자기 침실에서 나오던 길에 칸막이 뒤에서 스메르댜코프의 기척을 들었다고 말해 주자—카라마조프는 그야말로 무너지고 말았습니다. 나의 존경하는 재치 있는 동료 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 나중에 내게, 그 모습을 보고 거의 눈물이 날 뻔했노라고 털어놓았습니다. 게다가 사태를 더 그르치려는 듯, 피고는 서둘러 우리에게 그 말 많던 작은 주머니 이야기를 늘어놓았습니다—좋습니다, 그렇다면 여러분도 이 로맨스를 들어 보시지요!\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 나는 이 로맨스를 어째서 한낱 터무니없는 소리일 뿐 아니라 그 정황에서 끌어댈 수 있는 가장 그럴듯하지 못한 지어낸 이야기로 여기는지, 이미 여러분께 말씀드렸습니다. 누군가가 내기 삼아 가장 있을 법하지 않은 이야기를 지어내려 애쓴다 해도, 이보다 더 믿기 어려운 것은 좀처럼 찾아내지 못할 것입니다. 이런 이야기들의 가장 고약한 점은, 의기양양한 그 이야기꾼들이 언제나, 실제 삶이 그토록 풍부하게 갖추고 있으나 이 가련하고 본의 아닌 이야기꾼들이 하찮은 사소한 일이라고 소홀히 여기는 바로 그 세부에 걸려 혼쭐이 나고 무너지고 만다는 데 있습니다. 오, 그들에게는 그런 세부에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없으니, 그들의 마음은 자신의 거창한 지어낸 이야기 전체에만 쏠려 있는지라, 어느 누가 감히 사소한 일로 자기들의 말허리를 자른단 말인가 하는 것입니다! 그러나 그들이 덜미를 잡히는 것은 바로 그 때문입니다. 피고는 이런 질문을 받았습니다. '그 작은 주머니의 천은 어디서 났으며, 누가 만들어 주었소?' '내가 직접 만들었소.' '그렇다면 그 천은 어디서 났소?' 피고는 대놓고 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 기색이었으니, 그토록 하찮은 질문을 던지는 것을 거의 무례한 짓으로 여겼고, 여러분은 믿으시겠습니까, 그 분개는 진심이었습니다! 그러나 그들은 다들 그렇습니다. '셔츠에서 찢어 냈소.' '그렇다면 내일 당신의 빨랫감 가운데서 한 조각이 찢겨 나간 그 셔츠를 찾아낼 수 있겠군요.' 그리고 한번 상상해 보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 만일 우리가 정말로 그 찢긴 셔츠를 찾아냈다면 (그리고 그의 서랍장이나 트렁크에서 그것을 어찌 찾아내지 못할 수 있었겠습니까?) 그것은 하나의 사실, 그의 진술을 뒷받침하는 물증이 되었을 것입니다! 그러나 그는 거기까지 헤아릴 줄을 몰랐습니다. '기억나지 않소, 셔츠에서 떼어 낸 게 아니었는지도 모르오, 나는 그것을 내 하숙집 안주인의 두건 하나에 꿰매어 넣었소.' '어떤 두건이오?' '그 여자가 아무렇게나 굴러다니게 둔 낡은 무명 헝겊이었소.' '그것을 똑똑히 기억하오?' '아니, 기억나지 않소.' 그러면서도 그는 화를 냈습니다, 몹시 화를 냈습니다, 그런데도 한번 상상해 보십시오, 그것이 기억나지 않는다니요! 인간의 삶에서 가장 끔찍한 순간들에, 이를테면 처형장으로 끌려갈 때조차 사람은 바로 그런 사소한 것들을 기억하는 법입니다. 그는 무엇이든 다 잊어버려도, 길가에 휙 스쳐 지나간 어떤 초록 지붕이나, 십자가 위에 앉은 갈까마귀 한 마리만은—그것만은 기억할 것입니다. 그는 그 작은 주머니를 만든 일을 식구들에게 감추었으니, 누군가 들어와 바늘을 손에 쥔 자기를 보지나 않을까 하던 그 굴욕적인 두려움을, 아주 작은 소리에도 칸막이 뒤로 슬쩍 몸을 숨기던 일을(그의 하숙방에는 칸막이가 있습니다) 분명 기억하고 있었을 것입니다.\n\n\"그러나 배심원 여러분, 내가 어찌하여 여러분께 이 모든 것을, 이 모든 세부와 사소한 일들을 늘어놓는 것이겠습니까?\" 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 별안간 소리쳤다. \"그것은 다름 아니라 피고가 이 순간까지도 여전히 이런 터무니없는 소리들을 고집하고 있기 때문입니다. 두 달 전 그 숙명적인 밤 이래로 그는 아무것도 설명하지 못했으며, 자신의 예전 환상적인 진술에 단 하나의 실질적이고 환히 밝혀 주는 사실도 보태지 못했습니다. 그 모든 것은 부질없는 군말일 뿐입니다. '내 명예를 걸고 그것을 믿어 주셔야 합니다.' 오, 우리는 기꺼이 믿고 싶습니다, 우리는 그것을 믿고 싶어 안달입니다, 설령 그의 명예를 건 말 하나에 지나지 않더라도 말입니다! 우리가 인간의 피에 목말라하는 승냥이라도 된단 말입니까? 피고에게 유리한 사실 단 하나라도 우리에게 내보여 주십시오, 그러면 우리는 기뻐할 것입니다. 그러나 그것은 실질적이고 진정한 사실이어야 하며, 피고의 표정에서 그의 친동생이 끌어낸 추측이라든가, 그가 제 가슴을 쳤을 때 그것은 분명, 그것도 어둠 속에서, 그 작은 주머니를 가리키려는 뜻이었으리라는 따위여서는 안 됩니다. 우리는 그 새로운 사실에 기뻐할 것이며, 우리야말로 맨 먼저 우리의 기소를 철회할 것이고, 서둘러 그것을 철회할 것입니다. 그러나 지금은 정의가 부르짖고 있으니, 우리는 고집할 수밖에 없으며, 그 무엇도 철회할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치는 마지막 결론으로 넘어갔다. 그는 열병에라도 걸린 듯한 모습이었고, 복수를 부르짖는 피에 대해, 비천한 강도질의 동기로 아들의 손에 살해당한 아비의 피에 대해 이야기했다! 그는 사실들이 빚어내는 비극적이고도 명백한 일관성을 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"그리고 여러분이 저 재능 있고 명망 높은 변호인에게서 어떤 말을 듣더라도,\" 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다. \"여러분의 감수성에 호소하는 그 어떤 능변의 절절한 호소가 펼쳐지더라도, 여러분은 지금 이 순간 정의의 전당에 계시다는 것을 기억하십시오. 여러분은 우리의 정의를 지키는 투사이며, 우리의 거룩한 러시아를, 그 원칙을, 그 가정을, 러시아가 신성히 여기는 그 모든 것을 지키는 투사임을 기억하십시오! 그렇습니다, 여러분은 지금 이 순간 이 자리에서 러시아를 대표하는 것이며, 여러분의 평결은 이 법정 안에서만 들리는 것이 아니라 온 러시아에 메아리쳐 울려 퍼질 것이고, 온 러시아가 여러분을, 자신을 지키는 투사이자 심판관인 여러분의 말을 들을 것이며, 여러분의 평결에 따라 러시아는 힘을 얻기도 하고 낙담하기도 할 것입니다. 러시아와 그 기대를 저버리지 마십시오. 우리의 숙명적인 트로이카는 어쩌면 파멸을 향하여 무서운 기세로 내달리고 있으며, 온 러시아에서는 이미 오래전부터 사람들이 애원하듯 손을 내밀어 그 광포하고 무모한 질주를 멈추라고 외쳐 왔습니다. 그리고 만일 다른 나라들이 그 트로이카에서 비켜선다면, 그것은 어느 시인이 기꺼이 믿고 싶어 하듯 존경에서 비켜서는 것이 아니라, 그저 공포에서 비켜서는 것일지도 모릅니다. 공포에서, 어쩌면 혐오에서 말입니다. 그들이 비켜서 주는 것만으로도 다행이나, 어쩌면 그들도 언젠가는 비켜서기를 그치고, 그 내달리는 환영 앞에 굳건한 벽을 이루어 맞서서, 자신들의 안전과 계몽과 문명을 위하여 우리의 무법한 질주를 가로막아 버릴지도 모릅니다. 우리는 이미 유럽에서 경종을 울리는 목소리들을 들었으며, 그것은 이미 울리기 시작했습니다. 그들을 시험하지 마십시오! 아들이 아비를 살해한 일을 정당화하는 판결로써 그들의 자라나는 증오를 더 쌓아 올리지 마십시오!\"\n\n이폴리트 키릴로비치는 진심으로 격앙되어 있었으나, 이런 수사적인 호소로 연설을 끝맺었다—그리고 그가 빚어낸 효과는 자못 비상한 것이었다. 연설을 마치자 그는 황급히 밖으로 나갔고, 앞서 말한 대로 옆방에서 거의 졸도할 뻔했다. 법정 안에 박수는 없었으나, 진중한 사람들은 흡족해했다. 부인들은 그만큼 만족스러워하지는 않았으되, 그래도 그 능변에는 그들조차 기뻐했으니, 더구나 그들은 재판의 결말에 대해 아무런 걱정이 없었고 페튜코비치를 온전히 믿고 있던 까닭이었다. \"마침내 그가 입을 열 것이고, 당연히 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n모두가 미탸를 쳐다보았다. 그는 검사의 연설이 이어지는 내내 이를 악물고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 고개를 숙인 채 말없이 앉아 있었다. 다만 이따금 고개를 들고 귀를 기울였는데, 특히 그루셴카에 대한 이야기가 나올 때가 그러했다. 검사가 그녀에 대한 라키틴의 견해를 입에 올리자, 경멸과 분노가 어린 미소가 그의 얼굴을 스쳤고, 그는 제법 또렷이 들리도록 중얼거렸다. \"베르나르 같은 작자들이로군!\" 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 자신이 모크로예에서 그를 어떻게 심문하고 다그쳤는지를 묘사할 때는, 미탸가 고개를 들고 강렬한 호기심에 차서 귀를 기울였다. 한 대목에서는 그가 벌떡 일어나 소리라도 지를 듯했으나, 스스로를 억누르고 그저 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐이다. 사람들은 나중에 그 연설의 끝부분에 대해, 검사가 모크로예에서 피고를 심문한 그 공적에 대해 이야기하며 이폴리트 키릴로비치를 비웃었다. \"그 사람은 제 영리함을 자랑하지 않고는 못 배기는 작자야.\" 그들은 이렇게 말했다.\n\n법정은 휴정에 들어갔으나, 잠깐 동안일 뿐이었으니, 길어야 십오 분이나 이십 분이었다. 방청석에서는 대화와 탄성이 웅성거렸다. 그 가운데 몇몇이 기억난다.\n\n\"묵직한 연설이었어.\" 한 무리에 끼어 있던 신사가 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"심리 분석을 너무 많이 끌어들였어.\" 또 다른 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 죄다 사실이었지, 한 치도 틀림없는 사실 말이오!\"\n\n\"그래, 그 사람은 그런 데는 일류야.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 모든 걸 정리해 버렸어.\"\n\n\"그래, 우리까지 싸잡아서 정리해 버렸지.\" 또 다른 목소리가 끼어들었다. \"연설 첫머리에서 우리가 죄다 표도르 파블로비치 같은 인간이라고 몰아붙인 거 기억나오?\"\n\n\"끝에서도 그랬지. 하지만 그건 죄다 허튼소리였어.\"\n\n\"게다가 알아먹기도 힘들었고.\"\n\n\"좀 지나치게 흥분했더군.\"\n\n\"부당해, 부당하다고.\"\n\n\"아니, 어쨌든 솜씨는 영리하게 부렸지. 오래도록 기다려 온 끝에 드디어 제 할 말을 한 거야, 하하!\"\n\n\"변호인은 뭐라고 할까?\"\n\n또 다른 무리에서는 이런 말이 들렸다.\n\n\"페테르부르크에서 온 그 사람을 그런 식으로 찌를 까닭은 없었는데, '여러분의 감수성에 호소하는'이라니—기억나오?\"\n\n\"그래, 그건 그 사람답지 않게 어설펐어.\"\n\n\"너무 서둘렀던 거야.\"\n\n\"신경질적인 위인이니까.\"\n\n\"우리야 웃지만, 정작 피고는 어떤 심정일까?\"\n\n\"그래, 미탸로서는 어떤 심정이겠나?\"\n\n세 번째 무리에서는.\n\n\"저 부인은 누구요, 저 끝에 앉은, 손잡이 안경을 든 뚱뚱한 여자 말이오?\"\n\n\"장군 부인인데, 이혼했지, 내가 아는 여자야.\"\n\n\"그래서 손잡이 안경을 들고 있군.\"\n\n\"별 볼 일 없는 여자야.\"\n\n\"오, 천만에, 톡 쏘는 데가 있는 앙증맞은 여자라네.\"\n\n\"그 여자 두 자리 너머에 자그마한 금발 여자가 있는데, 그쪽이 더 예쁘오.\"\n\n\"모크로예에서 그 사람을 솜씨 좋게 옭아맸지, 안 그렇소, 응?\"\n\n\"오, 솜씨는 제법이었지. 하지만 전에도 들은 얘기야, 그 사람이 남의 집을 돌며 그 이야기를 얼마나 자주 늘어놓았는데!\"\n\n\"그러니 지금도 그 짓을 하지 않고는 못 배긴 거지. 그게 다 허영이야.\"\n\n\"불만이 잔뜩 쌓인 위인이라니까, 헤헤!\"\n\n\"그래, 게다가 걸핏하면 발끈하지. 그리고 수사가 너무 많았어, 그런 긴긴 문장들 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 사람은 우리를 겁주려 들었어, 줄곧 우리를 겁주려고 했지. 트로이카 얘기 기억나오? '그들에게는 햄릿이 있으나, 우리에게는 아직 카라마조프밖에 없다'느니 하던 거 말이오! 그건 영리하게 말한 거였어!\"\n\n\"그건 자유주의자들 비위를 맞추려는 거였지. 그 사람은 그자들을 두려워하니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 게다가 변호사도 두려워하고 있어.\"\n\n\"그래, 페튜코비치는 뭐라고 할까?\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 하든, 우리 농부들 마음을 돌려놓지는 못할걸.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하시오?\"\n\n네 번째 무리에서는.\n\n\"트로이카에 대해 한 말은 좋았어, 다른 나라들에 관한 그 대목 말이야.\"\n\n\"다른 나라들이 그걸 가만 두고 보지 않으리라고 한 것도 사실이었지.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리요?\"\n\n\"아니, 지난주에 영국 의회에서 한 의원이 일어나 허무주의자들에 대해 말하면서, 이 야만스러운 민족을 교화하기 위해 이제 끼어들 때가 되지 않았느냐고 정부에 물었다지 않소. 이폴리트는 바로 그 사람을 염두에 두고 있었던 거야, 분명히 그래. 지난주에 그 얘기를 하고 있었거든.\"\n\n\"쉬운 일은 아니지.\"\n\n\"쉬운 일이 아니라니? 어째서요?\"\n\n\"아니, 우리가 크론슈타트를 봉쇄해서 곡물을 한 톨도 못 넘기게 하면 되지. 저들이 그걸 어디서 구하겠소?\"\n\n\"미국에서지. 요즘은 미국에서 들여오니까.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리!\"\n\n그러나 종이 울리자 모두가 제자리로 우르르 몰려갔다. 페튜코비치가 연단에 올랐다.\n* 트로이카(тройка): 말 세 필이 끄는 러시아의 전통적인 마차나 썰매. 고골의 「죽은 혼」에서 질주하는 러시아를 상징하는 형상으로 그려진 이래, 러시아 자체를 비유하는 표현으로 쓰였다.\n\n# Chapter X. The Speech For The Defense. An Argument That Cuts Both Ways\n\n이름난 변호인의 첫마디가 울려 퍼지자 장내는 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해졌다. 방청객의 시선이 일제히 그에게 못 박혔다. 그는 아주 단순하고 직접적으로, 확신에 찬 태도로 입을 열었으나 자만하는 기색이라고는 털끝만큼도 없었다. 그는 웅변을 부리려 하지도, 비장한 어조나 감상적인 미사여구를 동원하려 하지도 않았다. 그는 마치 친밀하고 마음 맞는 벗들의 무리 안에서 이야기를 나누는 사람 같았다. 그의 음성은 훌륭했으니, 낭랑하면서도 호소력이 있었고, 그 울림 자체에 진실하고 소박한 무언가가 깃들어 있었다. 그러나 듣는 이들은 곧 깨달았으니, 이 화자가 어느 순간 문득 진정한 비장함으로 솟구쳐 올라 \"이루 말할 수 없는 힘으로 가슴을 꿰뚫을\" 수도 있으리라는 것을. 그의 화법은 어쩌면 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 것보다 더 들쭉날쭉했으나, 장황한 문구 없이, 도리어 더 정확하게 말했다. 다만 한 가지가 부인들의 마음에 들지 않았으니, 그는 특히 연설 초입에서 자꾸만 앞으로 몸을 굽혔는데, 정확히 절을 하는 것은 아니었으나 마치 청중을 향해 달려들기라도 할 것처럼, 그 긴 등뼈를 반으로 접듯 굽혔으니, 마치 등 한가운데 용수철이라도 박혀 있어 거의 직각으로 꺾일 수 있게 해 주는 것만 같았다.\n\n연설을 시작할 무렵 그는 다소 두서없이, 말하자면 체계 없이 이야기하며 사실들을 따로따로 다루었으나, 끝에 이르자 이 사실들이 하나의 전체를 이루었다. 그의 연설은 두 부분으로 나눌 수 있었으니, 첫째 부분은 공소 사실을 반박하는 비판으로 이루어져 있었고, 때로는 악의적이고 빈정거리는 데가 있었다. 그러나 후반부에 이르러 그는 돌연 어조를, 심지어 태도까지 바꾸어 단숨에 비장함으로 솟구쳤다. 청중은 마치 그것을 기다리고 있었던 듯, 열광으로 몸을 떨었다.\n\n그는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자신이 비록 페테르부르크에서 변호 활동을 하고 있으나 그동안 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례 지방 도시를 찾아 피고를 변호해 왔으며, 그 피고들에 대해서는 무죄를 확신하거나 적어도 그러리라는 예단을 품고 있었다고 운을 떼었다. \"이번 사건에서 제게 일어난 것이 바로 그런 일입니다.\" 그는 이렇게 설명했다. \"신문에 처음 실린 보도들에서부터 저는 어떤 점에 강하게 마음이 끌려 피고에게 호의를 품게 되었습니다. 무엇보다 저의 흥미를 끈 것은 법정 실무에서 자주 일어나기는 하나, 제 생각으로는 이번 사건에서처럼 그토록 극단적이고 기이한 형태로 나타나는 일은 드문 어떤 사실이었습니다. 본래 그 특이점은 제 연설의 맨 끝에 가서야 정리해야 마땅하겠으나, 저는 그것을 맨 처음에 말씀드리겠습니다. 곧장 일에 착수하고 효과를 비축해 두거나 자료를 아껴 두지 못하는 것이 저의 약점이기 때문입니다. 이는 제 쪽에서 보면 경솔한 일일지 모르나, 적어도 진실한 태도이기는 합니다. 제가 마음에 두고 있는 바는 이것입니다. 피고에게는 압도적인 증거의 사슬이 얽혀 있으나, 동시에 그 어느 사실도 따로 떼어 살펴보면 비판을 견뎌 낼 만한 것이 하나도 없다는 점입니다. 신문을 통해 사건을 더 가까이 좇아갈수록 저의 생각은 점점 더 굳어졌고, 그러던 차에 문득 피고의 친지들로부터 그를 변호해 달라는 청을 받았습니다. 저는 곧장 이곳으로 달려왔고, 와서 보니 완전히 확신하게 되었습니다. 제가 이 사건을 맡은 것은 바로 이 무서운 사실의 사슬을 끊어 내고, 따로 떼어 놓고 보면 증거의 한 조각 한 조각이 입증되지 않은 것이며 허황된 것임을 보여 드리기 위함입니다.\"\n\n페튜코비치는 이렇게 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분.\" 그가 돌연 항변하듯 말했다. \"저는 이 고장에 처음 온 사람입니다. 저에게는 선입견이 없습니다. 피고는 거칠고 제멋대로인 성미의 사내이나, 저를 모욕한 적은 없습니다. 그러나 그는 어쩌면 이 도시에서 수백 명에 이르는 사람들을 모욕했고, 그리하여 미리부터 많은 이들로 하여금 그에게 편견을 품게 만들었습니다. 물론 저는 이 고장 사회의 도덕적 감정이 그를 향해 정당하게 들끓고 있음을 인정합니다. 피고는 거칠고 난폭한 성미의 사내입니다. 그럼에도 그는 이곳 사교계에 받아들여졌고, 심지어 제 재능 있는 벗인 검사의 가정에서도 환영을 받았습니다.\"\n\n(주의: 이 말에 방청석에서 두세 번 웃음이 터졌으나 곧 억눌렸지만, 모두가 알아챘다. 우리는 모두 알고 있었으니, 검사가 미탸를 마지못해 받아들인 것은 오로지 그가 어찌어찌 검사의 부인의 흥미를 끌었기 때문이었다. 그 부인은 더없이 고결하고 도덕적 품격을 갖춘 여인이었으나, 공상이 심하고 변덕스러우며, 특히 사소한 일에서 남편에게 맞서기를 좋아하는 사람이었다. 그러나 미탸의 방문이 잦았던 것은 아니었다.)\n\n\"그렇기는 하나 저는 감히 이렇게 말씀드리고자 합니다.\" 페튜코비치가 말을 이었다. \"독립적인 정신과 공정한 성품을 지닌 분임에도, 제 상대편이신 검사께서는 어쩌면 저의 불행한 의뢰인에게 그릇된 편견을 품게 되셨을지도 모릅니다. 오, 그것은 너무도 자연스러운 일입니다. 이 불행한 사내는 그런 편견을 받아 마땅할 만큼의 짓을 너무도 충분히 저질렀으니까요. 모욕당한 도덕, 그리고 그보다 더욱 모욕당한 취향이란 흔히 가차 없는 법입니다. 우리는 재능 있는 검사의 연설에서 피고의 성격과 행실에 대한 준엄한 분석을 들었고, 그가 이 사건을 대하는 엄격하고 비판적인 태도가 역력히 드러났습니다. 게다가 그는 심리학적 미묘함에까지 파고들었으니, 만약 그가 피고에게 조금이라도 의식적이고 악의적인 편견을 품고 있었다면 결코 그렇게까지 들어갈 수 없었을 것입니다. 그러나 이런 사건들에서는 가장 악의적이고 의식적으로 불공정한 태도보다도 더 나쁘고 더 치명적인 것들이 있습니다. 우리가 예술가적 본능에, 말하자면 한 편의 소설을 지어내고 싶은 욕망에 휩쓸려 버린다면 그것이 더 나쁜 일이며, 특히 신께서 우리에게 심리적 통찰력을 부여하셨을 때 더욱 그렇습니다. 제가 이곳으로 길을 떠나기 전에 저는 페테르부르크에서 경고를 들었고, 또 저 자신도 알고 있었으니, 이곳에 와서 재능 있는 상대를 만나게 되리라는 것이었습니다. 그의 심리적 통찰과 섬세함은 근년에 법조계에서 그에게 남다른 명성을 안겨 주었습니다. 그러나 심리학이란 아무리 심오하다 한들 양쪽을 다 베는 칼입니다.\" (방청석에서 웃음.) \"물론 여러분, 제 비유를 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 저는 웅변에 능하다고 뽐낼 처지가 못 됩니다. 그러나 한 가지 예로 검사 연설 중의 아무 대목이나 들어 보겠습니다.\n\n\"피고는 어둠 속 정원을 가로질러 달아나다가 담을 넘었고, 하인에게 붙들리자 놋쇠 절굿공이로 그를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸습니다. 그러고는 다시 정원 안으로 뛰어 내려와 그 사내를 죽였는지 아닌지 알아내려고 오 분이나 매달려 있었습니다. 그런데 검사께서는 피고가 측은한 마음에 늙은 그리고리에게 달려갔다는 진술을 믿으려 하지 않습니다. 검사께서는 이렇게 말씀하십니다. '아니다, 그런 순간에 그런 감수성이란 있을 수 없으며 부자연스럽다. 그는 자기 범행의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 살았는지 알아보러 달려간 것이며, 그리하여 자신이 살인을 저질렀음을 스스로 드러낸 것이니, 다른 어떤 이유로도 되돌아 달려가지는 않았을 것이다.'\n\n\"여기 여러분 앞에 심리학이 있습니다. 그러나 같은 방법을 가져다 이 사건에 거꾸로 적용해 보면, 우리가 얻는 결과 또한 그에 못지않게 그럴듯합니다. 그 살인자가 만일을 위해 목격자가 살았는지 죽었는지 알아보려고 뛰어내렸다고들 합니다. 그런데 검사 자신이 주장하는 바와 같이, 그는 살해당한 아버지의 서재에 삼천 루블이 들어 있었다는 글이 적힌 찢어진 봉투라는 놀라운 증거물을 남겨 두었습니다. '만약 그가 그 봉투를 가지고 가 버렸다면, 그 봉투와 그 안에 들었던 지폐에 대해, 그리고 그 돈을 피고가 훔쳐 갔다는 사실에 대해 세상 누구도 알지 못했을 것이다.' 이것은 검사 자신의 말입니다. 그러니 한편으로는 완전히 경계심을 잃은 모습, 곧 정신이 나가 겁에 질려 달아나며 그 단서를 바닥에 남겨 둔 사내가 보이는데, 이 분 뒤에는, 또 다른 사내를 죽였을 때는, 우리가 그에게서 더없이 무정하고 치밀한 계산과 선견지명을 인정해야 한다는 것입니다. 그러나 설령 그것이 사실이라 하더라도, 어떤 상황에서는 제가 캅카스의 독수리처럼 피에 굶주리고 눈이 날카로워지다가, 그다음 순간에는 두더지처럼 겁 많고 눈먼 자가 된다고 분별해 내는 것이 바로 심리학적 섬세함이라는 것이겠지요. 그러나 만일 제가 그토록 피에 굶주리고 잔혹하게 계산적이어서, 사람을 죽인 뒤에 오로지 그가 나를 고발할 증인으로 살아 있는지 알아보려고만 되돌아 달려간 자라면, 어찌하여 다른 목격자들과 마주칠 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 오 분이나 제 희생자를 살피며 보냈겠습니까? 어찌하여 손수건을 적셔 가며 그의 머리에서 피를 닦아 내어 훗날 저에게 불리한 증거가 되게 했겠습니까? 만일 그가 그토록 냉혹하고 계산적이었다면, 어찌하여 그 절굿공이로 하인의 머리를 거듭거듭 내리쳐 아예 죽여 버리고 목격자에 대한 온갖 근심을 떨쳐 버리지 않았겠습니까?\n\n\"또한 그는 목격자가 살아 있는지 보려고 달려갔으면서도, 또 다른 증거를 길에 남겨 두었으니, 곧 그가 두 여인에게서 가져온 놋쇠 절굿공이입니다. 그들은 훗날 언제든 그것이 제 것임을 알아보고 그가 그것을 자기들에게서 가져갔음을 입증할 수 있었습니다. 게다가 그것은 그가 길에 잊고 떨어뜨린 것이거나 부주의나 황급함 탓에 떨어뜨린 것이 아닙니다. 아닙니다, 그는 자기 무기를 내던진 것이니, 그것이 그리고리가 쓰러져 있던 자리에서 열다섯 걸음이나 떨어진 곳에서 발견되었기 때문입니다. 어찌하여 그가 그렇게 했겠습니까? 다름이 아니라 사람을, 그것도 늙은 하인을 죽인 것이 슬펐기 때문이며, 그래서 그는 사람 죽인 흉기인 그 절굿공이를 저주를 내뱉으며 내던진 것입니다. 분명 그러했을 것이니, 그렇지 않고서야 무슨 다른 까닭으로 그것을 그토록 멀리 던졌겠습니까? 그리고 만일 그가 사람을 죽인 것에 슬픔과 연민을 느낄 수 있는 자였다면, 이는 그가 아버지를 죽인 죄에서 결백하다는 것을 보여 줍니다. 만일 그가 아버지를 살해했다면, 그는 결코 측은한 마음에 또 다른 희생자에게 달려가지 않았을 것입니다. 그랬다면 그는 다른 감정을 느꼈을 것이니, 그의 생각은 오로지 제 한 몸 지키는 데로 쏠렸을 것입니다. 연민에 내어 줄 마음의 여유라고는 한 점도 없었을 것이니, 이는 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 도리어 그는 오 분 동안 그를 살피며 보내는 대신 그의 머리통을 깨부수었을 것입니다. 연민과 선한 감정이 들어설 자리가 있었던 것은 바로 그때까지 그의 양심이 깨끗했기 때문입니다. 여기서 우리는 전혀 다른 심리학을 마주하게 됩니다. 배심원 여러분, 저는 이 방법으로는 무엇이든 입증해 낼 수 있음을 보여 드리려고 일부러 그것에 의지해 본 것입니다. 모든 것은 누가 그것을 써먹느냐에 달려 있습니다. 심리학은 더없이 진지한 사람들마저 소설을 지어내도록, 그것도 전혀 의식하지 못하는 사이에 꾀어내고 맙니다. 여러분, 저는 심리학의 오용에 대해 말씀드리는 것입니다.\"\n\n검사를 빗댄 동의의 소리와 웃음소리가 다시금 법정 안에 들려왔다. 나는 그 연설을 자세히 되풀이하지는 않겠고, 다만 거기서 몇몇 대목, 몇몇 요긴한 요점만을 인용하기로 하겠다.\n\n# Chapter XI. There Was No Money. There Was No Robbery\n\n돈은 없었다. 강도질도 없었다\n페튜코비치의 변론에는 모든 이의 마음을 강타한 한 가지 논점이 있었다. 그는 운명을 가른 그 삼천 루블이라는 돈의 존재 자체를, 따라서 그것이 도둑맞았을 가능성마저 단호히 부인하고 나섰다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"새롭고 선입견 없는 관찰자라면 누구나 이번 사건의 한 가지 특징적인 면모에, 곧 강도질이라는 죄목과, 도둑맞을 만한 무언가가 있었음을 증명하기란 완전히 불가능하다는 사실에 충격을 받지 않을 수 없을 것입니다. 우리는 돈이, 삼천 루블이 도둑맞았다는 말을 듣고 있습니다만, 정작 그 루블이라는 것이 실제로 존재하기는 했는지 아는 사람이 아무도 없습니다. 한번 생각해 보십시오. 우리가 어떻게 그 액수에 관해 듣게 되었으며, 또 그 지폐를 본 사람이 누구입니까? 그것을 보고, 봉투 속에 넣어 두었노라고 진술한 유일한 사람은 하인 스메르자코프였습니다. 그는 이 사건이 벌어지기 전에 피고와 그 동생 이반 표도로비치에게 그 이야기를 했습니다. 스베틀로바 부인 역시 그 이야기를 전해 들었습니다. 그러나 이 세 사람 가운데 그 누구도 실제로 그 지폐를 보지는 못했고, 오직 스메르자코프만이 그것을 보았던 것입니다.\n\n여기서 한 가지 의문이 생깁니다. 만약 그 돈이 정말로 존재했고, 또 스메르자코프가 그것을 보았다는 것이 사실이라면, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 본 것은 언제였습니까? 만약 그의 주인이 침대 밑에서 지폐를 꺼내어 그에게는 알리지 않은 채 다시 자기 돈궤에 집어넣었다면 어쩌겠습니까? 유념하십시오. 스메르자코프의 이야기에 따르면 지폐는 매트리스 밑에 보관되어 있었습니다. 그렇다면 피고는 그것을 끄집어냈을 텐데, 정작 침대는 조금도 흐트러진 데가 없었습니다. 이 점은 조서에 세심하게 기록되어 있습니다. 피고가 어떻게 침대를 흐트러뜨리지 않고서 지폐를 찾아낼 수 있었겠습니까? 그가 어떻게 피로 물든 손으로, 일부러 매끈하게 정돈해 두었던 그 곱고 티 없는 침대보를 더럽히지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?\n\n그러나 이런 질문을 받을 것입니다. 바닥에 떨어져 있던 봉투는 어찌 된 것이냐? 그렇습니다, 그 봉투에 관해서는 한두 마디 짚고 넘어갈 만합니다. 저는 방금 그 대단히 유능하신 검사께서 스스로—자기 입으로 말입니다, 유의하십시오—만약 그 봉투가 없었더라면, 그것이 바닥에 떨어져 있지 않았더라면, 세상 그 누구도 그 봉투와 그 안에 든 지폐의 존재를, 따라서 피고가 그것을 훔쳤다는 사실을 알지 못했을 것이라고 단언하시는 것을 듣고 적잖이 놀랐습니다. 그러니 그 찢어진 종잇조각이야말로 검사 자신의 인정에 따르건대 강도질이라는 죄목이 떠받쳐지는 유일한 증거인 셈입니다. '그렇지 않았다면 아무도 그 강도질을, 어쩌면 그 돈조차 알지 못했을 것이다'라는 것이지요. 그러나 그 종잇조각이 바닥에 떨어져 있었다는 단순한 사실 하나가, 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었으며 그 돈이 도둑맞았다는 증거가 되겠습니까? 그래도 스메르자코프가 봉투 안의 돈을 보았다고 반박할 것입니다. 그러나 언제, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 본 것이 언제였느냐고 저는 여러분께 묻는 바입니다. 저는 스메르자코프와 이야기를 나누었는데, 그는 사건이 벌어지기 이틀 전에 그 지폐를 보았노라고 말했습니다. 그렇다면 늙은 표도르 파블로비치가, 자기가 흠모하는 대상을 애가 타고 발작적인 심정으로 기다리며 홀로 틀어박혀 있던 차에, 봉투를 뜯어 지폐를 꺼내며 시간을 때웠으리라고 어찌 상상하지 못하겠습니까. '봉투 따위가 무슨 소용인가?' 그는 스스로 이렇게 물었을지 모릅니다. '그 여자는 안에 지폐가 있다는 것을 믿지 않겠지. 하지만 무지갯빛 지폐 서른 장을 한 다발로 보여 주면 더 깊은 인상을 줄 게 틀림없어, 군침을 흘리고 말 거야.' 그리하여 그는 봉투를 찢어 열고 돈을 꺼낸 뒤, 자신이 그 주인임을 의식하고 증거를 남길 걱정 따위 하지도 않은 채 봉투를 바닥에 내던졌을 것입니다.\n\n들어 보십시오, 여러분. 이런 추론과 이런 행동보다 더 그럴듯한 것이 또 있겠습니까? 어째서 이것이 논외라는 말입니까? 만약 이와 비슷한 일이 일어났을 수 있다면 강도질이라는 죄목은 무너지고 맙니다. 돈이 없었다면 그것을 훔친 일도 없는 것입니다. 바닥에 떨어진 봉투를 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었다는 증거로 받아들일 수 있다면, 어찌하여 저는 그 반대를, 곧 그 주인이 봉투에서 돈을 꺼냈기 때문에 봉투가 바닥에 떨어져 있었다고 주장할 수 없다는 말입니까?\n\n그러나 이런 질문을 받을 것입니다. 표도르 파블로비치가 봉투에서 돈을 꺼냈다면 그 돈은 어찌 되었느냐, 경찰이 집을 수색했을 때는 발견되지 않았는데? 첫째로, 그 돈의 일부는 돈궤에서 발견되었습니다. 그리고 둘째로, 그는 그날 아침이나 그 전날 저녁에 그것을 다른 데 쓰려고, 누구에게 주거나 보내려고 꺼냈을 수 있습니다. 그는 자기 생각을, 행동 계획을 완전히 바꾸었으되 그 사실을 미리 스메르자코프에게 알릴 필요까지는 없다고 여겼을지 모릅니다. 그리고 만약 이런 설명이 조금이라도 가능하다면, 어떻게 피고가 강도질을 위해 살인을 저질렀으며 실제로 그 강도질을 실행에 옮겼다고 그토록 단정적으로 고발될 수 있겠습니까? 이는 공상 소설의 영역을 침범하는 일입니다. 무언가가 도둑맞았다고 주장하려면 그 물건을 제시하거나, 적어도 그것이 존재했음을 의심의 여지 없이 증명해야 합니다. 그러나 이 지폐를 본 사람은 아무도 없었던 것입니다.\n\n얼마 전 페테르부르크에서 열여덟 살, 소년이나 다름없는 어느 젊은이가, 행상으로 자그마한 장사를 하던 자가, 백주 대낮에 도끼를 들고 환전상의 가게로 들어가, 유례없으리만치 전형적인 대담함으로 가게 주인을 죽이고 천오백 루블을 가지고 달아났습니다. 다섯 시간 뒤 그는 체포되었는데, 이미 써 버린 십오 루블을 제외한 전액이 그의 몸에서 발견되었습니다. 게다가 가게의 점원은 살인이 벌어진 뒤 가게로 돌아와, 도둑맞은 정확한 액수뿐 아니라 그 액수를 이루고 있던 지폐와 금화까지 경찰에 알렸으며, 바로 그 지폐와 금화가 범인에게서 발견되었습니다. 그 뒤로 살인자 측의 완전하고 진실된 자백이 이어졌습니다. 배심원 여러분, 저는 이런 것을 증거라고 부르는 바입니다! 그 경우에는 제가 돈을 알고, 보고, 만져 볼 수 있으니 그 존재를 부인할 수가 없습니다. 이번 사건도 그러합니까? 그러면서도 이것은 한 사람의 생사가 걸린 문제입니다.\n\n물론 이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그러나 그는 그날 밤 흥청망청 술판을 벌이며 돈을 펑펑 썼고, 천오백 루블을 지니고 있었던 것으로 드러나지 않았는가, 그 돈은 어디서 났단 말이냐? 그러나 바로 천오백밖에 발견되지 않았고 나머지 절반은 어디서도 찾아내지 못했다는 사실 자체가, 그 돈이 같은 돈이 아니었으며 애초에 어떤 봉투에도 들어 있던 적이 없었음을 보여 줍니다. 예심에서는 시간을 엄밀히 계산하여, 피고가 그 하녀들에게서 곧장 페르호틴의 집으로 달려갔고 집에는 들르지 않았으며 그 어디에도 가지 않았음이 증명되었습니다. 그러니 그는 줄곧 사람들과 함께 있었던 셈이고, 따라서 삼천 루블을 절반으로 나누어 절반을 읍내에 숨겨 둘 수가 없었습니다. 바로 이 점 때문에 검사는 그 돈이 모크로예 어딘가의 틈새에 숨겨져 있다고 가정하기에 이른 것입니다. 차라리 우돌포 성의 지하 감옥에 숨겨 두었다고 하는 게 어떻겠습니까, 여러분? 이 추측이야말로 너무도 환상적이고 너무도 소설 같지 않습니까? 그리고 유의하십시오. 만약 그 추측이 무너지면 강도질이라는 죄목 전체가 바람에 흩어지고 마는데, 그렇게 되면 나머지 천오백 루블은 대체 어찌 되었겠습니까? 피고가 그 어디에도 가지 않았음이 증명된 마당에, 그것이 무슨 기적으로 사라질 수 있었겠습니까? 그런데도 우리는 이런 이야기를 가지고 한 사람의 인생을 파멸시킬 채비를 하고 있습니다!\n\n이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그는 자신이 지니고 있던 천오백 루블을 어디서 얻었는지 설명하지 못했으며, 그날 밤 전까지 그에게 돈이 없었다는 것을 누구나 알고 있었다고 말입니다. 대체 그것을 누가 알았다는 것입니까? 피고는 그 돈의 출처에 관해 명확하고 흔들림 없는 진술을 했으며, 여러분이 굳이 따져 보고자 한다면, 배심원 여러분, 그 진술만큼 개연성이 높고 피고의 기질과 정신에 들어맞는 것은 없습니다. 검사는 자신의 소설에 도취되어 있습니다. 자신의 약혼녀가 그토록 모욕적으로 내놓은 삼천 루블을 차마 받아 든 의지박약한 사내가, 우리가 듣기로는, 그 절반을 따로 떼어 꿰매 넣었을 리 없으며, 설령 그리했다 하더라도 이틀에 한 번씩 그것을 뜯어 백 루블씩 꺼내 한 달 만에 죄다 써 버렸으리라는 것입니다. 여러분도 기억하시겠지만, 이 모든 것이 반박을 허용하지 않는 어조로 제시되었습니다. 그러나 만약 그 일이 전혀 다르게 벌어졌다면 어쩌겠습니까? 만약 여러분이 소설을 한 편 짜내고 있었던 것이라면, 그것도 전혀 다른 종류의 인간에 관해서라면? 바로 그렇습니다, 여러분은 전혀 다른 인간을 지어낸 것입니다!\n\n어쩌면 이런 말을 들을지도 모릅니다. 그가 사건이 벌어지기 한 달 전 약혼녀가 준 그 삼천 루블 전부를 하루 만에 써 버렸다는 증인들이 있으니, 그가 그 액수를 절반으로 나누었을 리 없다고 말입니다. 그러나 그 증인들이란 대체 누구입니까? 그들 증언의 가치는 이미 법정에서 드러났습니다. 게다가 남의 손에 들린 빵 조각은 늘 더 커 보이는 법이며, 그 증인들 가운데 누구도 그 돈을 세어 본 적이 없으니, 모두가 그저 눈대중으로 짐작했을 뿐입니다. 그리고 증인 막시모프는 피고가 이만 루블을 손에 쥐고 있었다고 증언했습니다. 보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 심리학이란 양날의 검입니다. 이제 그 다른 쪽 날로 돌려서 무슨 결과가 나오는지 살펴보겠습니다.\n\n사건이 벌어지기 한 달 전, 피고는 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 우편으로 부쳐 달라며 삼천 루블을 위탁받았습니다. 그러나 문제는 이것입니다. 그 돈이 방금 선언된 것처럼 그토록 모욕적이고 굴욕적인 방식으로 그에게 맡겨졌다는 것이 사실입니까? 그 처녀가 이 문제에 관해 처음 한 진술은 달랐습니다, 완전히 달랐습니다. 두 번째 진술에서 우리가 들은 것은 다만 원한과 복수의 외침, 오래 감추어 온 증오의 외침뿐이었습니다. 그리고 그 증인이 처음 증언을 부정확하게 했다는 사실 자체가, 그 두 번째 증언 또한 부정확했을 수 있다는 결론을 내릴 권리를 우리에게 줍니다. 검사는 그 이야기를 건드리려 하지 않을 것이고, 감히 건드리지도 못할 것입니다(그 자신의 말입니다). 그렇다면 그러라지요. 저 역시 그것을 건드리지 않겠습니다만, 다만 이 한 가지만 감히 지적하고자 합니다. 만약 의심할 바 없이 그토록 존경받는 처녀처럼 고결하고 도덕적인 사람이, 그런 사람이 말입니다, 피고를 파멸시키려는 뻔한 동기를 품고 법정에서 돌연 자신의 첫 진술을 뒤집는 것을 스스로에게 허용한다면, 그 증언이 공정하지도, 냉정하지도 않게 행해졌음이 분명합니다. 복수심에 불타는 여인이라면 많은 것을 과장했으리라고 가정할 권리가 우리에게 없겠습니까? 그렇습니다, 그녀는 능히 과장했을 만하며, 특히 그에게 돈을 내밀 때의 그 모욕과 굴욕을 과장했을 것입니다. 아닙니다, 그 돈은 받을 수 있을 만한 방식으로 내밀어졌으니, 특히 피고처럼 무던한 사내에게는 그러했고, 무엇보다 그는 머지않아 아버지에게서 자기 몫이라고 여기는 삼천 루블을 받으리라 기대하고 있었습니다. 그것은 그의 경솔함이었습니다만, 바로 그 무책임한 생각 없음이야말로 그로 하여금 아버지가 돈을 주리라고, 자기가 그것을 받게 되리라고, 그래서 자기에게 맡겨진 돈을 언제든 부쳐 빚을 갚을 수 있으리라고 그토록 확신하게 만든 것입니다.\n\n그러나 검사는 그가 같은 날 그 돈의 절반을 따로 떼어 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣었을 수 있다는 것을 인정하지 않으려 합니다. 그건 그의 성격이 아니다, 그는 그런 감정을 품을 수 없었다고 우리에게 말합니다. 그러면서도 검사 자신이 카라마조프 집안의 광활한 본성에 관해 떠들었으며, 카라마조프라면 두 극단을 한꺼번에 품을 수 있다고 외쳤습니다. 카라마조프란 바로 그렇게 양면적인 본성을 지녀 두 극단 사이를 오가는 인간이니, 광란의 환락을 향한 가장 격렬한 갈망에 휩싸여 있을 때조차도, 다른 한편에서 무언가가 그의 마음을 치면 스스로를 다잡을 수 있는 것입니다. 그리고 그 다른 한편에는 사랑이—그의 가슴에서 타오른 그 새로운 사랑이 있었으며, 그 사랑을 위해 그는 돈이 필요했습니다. 아, 정부와 흥청대며 노는 것보다 훨씬 더 절실하게 말입니다. 만약 그녀가 그에게 '나는 당신 거예요, 표도르 파블로비치는 싫어요'라고 말한다면, 그는 그녀를 데리고 떠날 돈이 있어야 했습니다. 그것이 흥청거리는 것보다 더 중요했습니다. 카라마조프가 그것을 이해하지 못했을 리 있겠습니까? 그가 시달리고 있던 것은 바로 그 불안이었으니, 그가 만일의 사태에 대비해 그 돈을 따로 떼어 숨겨 두었다 한들 무엇이 그리 있을 법하지 않은 일이겠습니까?\n\n그러나 시간이 흘러도 표도르 파블로비치는 피고에게 기대했던 삼천 루블을 주지 않았습니다. 오히려 피고는 아버지가 그 돈을 써서 자기가, 곧 피고가 사랑하는 여인을 유혹할 작정이라는 말을 들었습니다. '표도르 파블로비치가 돈을 주지 않으면,' 그는 생각했습니다. '나는 카테리나 이바노브나 앞에서 도둑 신세가 되고 마는 거야.' 그러자 이런 생각이 떠올랐습니다. 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가서, 여태 목에 걸고 다니던 천오백 루블을 그녀 앞에 내놓고 이렇게 말하겠다고. '나는 비열한 놈이지만, 도둑은 아니오.' 그러니 여기서 우리는 이미 그가 어째서 그 돈을 눈동자처럼 지켜야 했는지, 어째서 그 작은 주머니를 뜯어 백 루블씩 써 버려서는 안 되었는지에 대한 이중의 이유를 갖게 됩니다. 어찌하여 여러분은 피고에게 명예 감각이 있음을 부정하려 하십니까? 그렇습니다, 그에게는 명예 감각이 있으니, 그것이 빗나간 것이라 치고, 흔히 그릇된 것이라 친다 해도, 그래도 그것은 엄연히 존재하며 하나의 정념에까지 이르렀고, 그는 그것을 증명해 보였습니다.\n\n그러나 이제 사태는 한층 더 복잡해집니다. 그의 질투의 고통이 절정에 이르고, 바로 그 두 가지 의문이 그의 열병에 들뜬 머릿속을 점점 더 괴롭힙니다. '카테리나 이바노브나에게 돈을 갚는다면, 그루셴카와 떠날 돈은 어디서 마련한단 말인가?' 그가 그 한 달 동안 거칠게 굴고, 술을 마시고, 선술집에서 소동을 부렸다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그가 비참한 처지에서 견딜 수 있는 한계를 넘도록 시달렸기 때문일 것입니다. 이 두 가지 의문이 너무도 날카로워져 마침내 그를 절망으로 몰아넣었습니다. 그는 동생을 보내 마지막으로 삼천 루블을 청해 보려 했으나, 답을 기다리지도 못한 채 직접 뛰어들어, 결국 증인들이 보는 앞에서 늙은 아버지를 두들겨 패고 말았습니다. 그 뒤로 그는 누구에게서도 그 돈을 얻을 가망이 없었습니다. 그렇게 매질을 당한 뒤로 아버지가 그에게 돈을 줄 리 없었던 것입니다.\n\n바로 그날 저녁, 그는 자기 가슴을, 그 작은 주머니가 있던 가슴의 윗부분을 치면서 동생에게 맹세하기를, 자기에게는 비열한 놈이 되지 않을 방도가 있으나 그래도 자기는 비열한 놈으로 남으리라고 했습니다. 자기가 그 방도를 쓰지 않으리라는 것을, 그럴 만한 성격도, 그럴 만한 의지력도 없으리라는 것을 스스로 내다보았기 때문입니다. 어찌하여, 어찌하여 검사는 알렉세이 카라마조프의 증언을, 그토록 진실되고 성실하게, 그토록 거짓 없고 설득력 있게 행해진 그 증언을 믿기를 거부하는 것입니까? 그러면서 어찌하여 도리어 저더러 틈새에 숨겨진 돈을, 우돌포 성의 지하 감옥에 숨겨진 돈을 믿으라고 강요하는 것입니까?\n\n바로 그날 저녁, 동생과 이야기를 나눈 뒤 피고는 그 운명의 편지를 썼으니, 그 편지야말로 피고가 강도질을 저질렀다는 가장 주요하고도 가장 어마어마한 증거라는 것입니다! '나는 모든 사람에게 구걸해 보고, 그래도 얻지 못하면 아버지를 죽이고 이반이 떠나는 즉시 매트리스 밑에서 분홍 리본이 달린 봉투를 꺼내겠다.' 우리가 듣기로는 살인의 완벽한 계획서라는 것이니, 따라서 범인은 그일 수밖에 없다는 것입니다. '그가 쓴 그대로 모든 것이 실행되었다'고 검사는 외칩니다.\n\n그러나 첫째로, 그것은 술 취한 자의 편지이며 극도의 흥분 상태에서 쓰인 것입니다. 둘째로, 그가 봉투에 관해 쓴 것은 또다시 스메르자코프에게서 들은 것에 따른 것이니, 그는 봉투를 자기 눈으로 본 적이 없기 때문입니다. 그리고 셋째로, 그가 그것을 쓴 것은 사실이지만, 그가 그 일을 실제로 행했다는 것을 어떻게 증명할 수 있겠습니까? 피고가 베개 밑에서 봉투를 꺼냈습니까, 그가 돈을 찾아냈습니까, 그 돈이 실제로 존재하기는 했습니까? 그리고 기억하신다면, 피고가 달려간 것이 돈을 얻기 위해서였습니까? 그는 훔치려고 그토록 황급히 달려간 것이 아니라, 그녀가, 자신을 짓밟아 놓은 그 여인이 어디 있는지 알아내려고 달려간 것입니다. 그는 어떤 계획을 실행하려고, 자기가 쓴 것을 실행하려고 달려간 것이 아니었습니다. 다시 말해 미리 계획된 강도질의 행위를 위해서가 아니라, 돌연히, 충동적으로, 질투의 광란에 휩싸여 달려간 것입니다. 그렇습니다! 이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그러나 그가 그곳에 이르러 아버지를 죽였을 때 돈마저 움켜쥐지 않았느냐고 말입니다. 그러나 그가 정말로 아버지를 죽이기는 했습니까? 강도질이라는 죄목을 저는 분개하며 부인하는 바입니다. 무엇을 훔쳤는지 정확하게 진술할 수 없다면 그 사람을 강도질로 고발할 수 없으니, 그것은 하나의 공리입니다. 그러나 그가 강도질 없이 아버지를 죽였습니까, 아니 도대체 아버지를 죽이기는 했습니까? 그것이 증명되었습니까? 그것 또한 소설이 아니겠습니까?\"\n\n# Chapter XII. And There Was No Murder Either\n\n배심원 여러분, 여기서 한 사람의 목숨이 걸려 있으며 여러분께서 신중하셔야 한다는 점을 다시금 일깨워 드리는 것을 허락해 주십시오. 우리는 검사 자신이, 오늘에 이르기까지 피고가 이 범죄를 온전히, 의식적으로 미리 계획했다고 단정하기를 망설였노라고 시인하는 것을 들었습니다. 그는 오늘 법정에 제출된 그 운명적인 취중의 편지를 보기 전까지 망설였던 것입니다. ‘쓰인 그대로 모두 행해졌다’는 것이지요. 그러나 거듭 말씀드리거니와, 그는 그녀에게로, 그녀를 찾으러, 오로지 그녀가 어디 있는지 알아내려고 달려갔던 것입니다. 이것은 누구도 반박할 수 없는 사실입니다. 만일 그녀가 집에 있었다면 그는 달아나지 않고 그녀 곁에 머물렀을 것이며, 따라서 편지에서 약속한 일을 저지르지 않았을 것입니다. 그는 뜻밖에, 우연히 달려간 것이며, 그때쯤이면 십중팔구 자기가 쓴 취중의 편지조차 기억하지 못했을 것입니다. ‘그는 절굿공이를 움켜쥐었다’고들 하며, 여러분께서는 그 절굿공이를 토대로 어떻게 심리학의 전 구조물이 세워졌는지를 기억하실 것입니다. 어찌하여 그가 그 절굿공이를 무기로 바라보고 그것을 움켜쥐어야 했는가, 운운, 운운 말입니다. 바로 이 대목에서 지극히 평범한 생각 하나가 제 머리에 떠오릅니다. 만일 그 절굿공이가 눈에 띄지 않았더라면, 피고가 그것을 움켜쥐었다는 그 선반 위에 놓여 있지 않고 찬장 속에 치워져 있었더라면 어찌 되었겠습니까? 그것은 피고의 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이고, 그는 무기도 없이 빈손으로 달려갔을 것이며, 그랬다면 그는 분명 아무도 죽이지 않았을 것입니다. 그렇다면 제가 어찌 그 절굿공이를 사전 계획의 증거로 볼 수 있겠습니까?\n\n그렇습니다, 그러나 그는 술집에서 자기 아버지를 죽이겠다고 떠들었으며, 이틀 전, 곧 취중의 편지를 쓴 그날 저녁에는 조용했고 다만 술집에서 한 점원과 다투었을 뿐인데, 그것도 카라마조프란 어쩔 수 없이 다투게 마련이기 때문이라고들 합니다그려! 그러나 이에 대한 제 대답은 이렇습니다. 만일 그가 편지에 적힌 대로 그런 살인을 계획하고 있었다면, 그는 점원하고조차 다투지 않았을 것이며, 아마 술집에 발도 들이지 않았을 것입니다. 그런 범죄를 꾸미는 자는 고요함과 은둔을 찾고, 자기를 지우려 하며, 남의 눈과 귀에 띄지 않으려 하는 법이니, 그것도 계산에서가 아니라 본능에서 그러하기 때문입니다. 배심원 여러분, 심리학적 방법이란 양날의 무기이며, 우리 역시 그것을 쓸 수 있습니다. 한 달 내내 술집에서 떠들어댔다는 그 모든 일로 말하자면, 우리는 어린아이들이나 술집에서 나오는 취객들이 ‘너를 죽여 버리겠다!’고 외치는 것을 흔히 듣지 않습니까? 그러나 그들은 아무도 죽이지 않습니다. 그리고 그 운명적인 편지—그것 또한 그저 취중의 짜증에 지나지 않는 것이 아니겠습니까? 그것이 그저 술집 앞에서 주정뱅이가 내지르는 고함, ‘너를 죽이겠다! 너희를 모조리 죽이겠다!’와 다를 것이 무엇입니까? 어찌 그럴 수 없겠습니까, 어찌 그럴 수 없겠습니까? 우리가 그 편지를 ‘터무니없다’가 아니라 ‘운명적이다’라고 부를 무슨 근거를 가지고 있습니까? 그의 아버지가 살해된 채 발견되었기 때문이며, 한 증인이 피고가 손에 무기를 들고 정원에서 달려 나오는 것을 보았고 그에게 쓰러뜨려졌기 때문입니다. 그러니 우리는 모든 것이 그가 글로 계획한 그대로 행해졌으며, 그 편지는 ‘터무니없는’ 것이 아니라 ‘운명적인’ 것이라고 듣게 되는 것이지요.\n\n자, 이제 하느님께 감사하게도! 우리는 진짜 요점에 이르렀습니다. ‘그가 정원에 있었으니, 틀림없이 그가 죽였다.’ 이 몇 마디 말, 곧 ‘그가 있었으니, 그렇다면 그가 틀림없이’라는 말 속에 검찰 측 주장의 전부가 들어 있습니다. 그가 거기 있었으니, 틀림없이 그가 했다는 것이지요. 그런데 만일 그가 거기 있었다 한들, 거기에 ‘틀림없이’라는 것이 없다면 어찌 됩니까? 오, 저는 증거의 사슬이—그 우연의 일치들이—실로 암시적이라는 점을 인정합니다. 그러나 이 모든 사실을 그 연결고리와 무관하게 하나하나 따로 검토해 보십시오. 가령 어찌하여 검찰 측은 피고가 아버지의 창문에서 달아났다는 진술의 진실성을 인정하기를 거부합니까? 그 살인자에게 갑자기 찾아들었다는 그 공손하고 ‘경건한’ 감정을 두고 검사가 늘어놓은 빈정거림을 기억하십시오. 그러나 만일 정말로 그런 무언가가, 자식 된 도리에서 우러난 존경까지는 아니더라도 종교적 경외의 감정 같은 것이 있었다면 어찌 됩니까? ‘바로 그 순간 어머니가 저를 위해 기도하고 계셨던 것이 틀림없습니다’라고 한 것이 예심에서 피고가 한 말이었으며, 그리하여 그는 스베틀로바 부인이 아버지의 집에 없다는 것을 스스로 확인하자마자 달아났던 것입니다. ‘그러나 그는 창문을 들여다본다고 그것을 확인할 수 없었을 것이다’라고 검사는 반박합니다. 그러나 어찌 못 했겠습니까? 어찌하여 말입니까? 그 창문은 피고가 보낸 신호에 따라 열렸습니다. 표도르 파블로비치가 무슨 말을, 그녀가 거기 없다는 것을 피고에게 알려 주는 어떤 외침을 내뱉었을 수도 있습니다. 어찌하여 우리는 모든 것을 우리가 상상하는 대로, 우리가 마음먹고 상상하기로 한 대로 단정해야 합니까? 가장 정교한 상상력조차 비껴가는 일이 현실에서는 무수히 일어나는 법입니다.\n\n‘그렇다, 그러나 그리고리는 문이 열려 있는 것을 보았으니 피고는 분명 집 안에 있었고, 따라서 그가 죽인 것이다.’ 자, 그 문에 관해서 말입니다, 배심원 여러분…… 그 문에 관해서는 우리에게 단 한 증인의 진술밖에 없으며, 그 증인은 그 당시 이러이러한 상태에 있었다는 점에 유념하십시오, 그러니까— 그러나 그 문이 열려 있었다고 칩시다. 피고가 자기 처지에서 자연스러운 자기방어의 본능에서 그것을 부인하며 거짓말했다고 칩시다. 그가 집 안에 들어갔다고 칩시다. 자, 그래서 어떻다는 것입니까? 그가 거기 있었다고 해서 어찌 그가 살인을 저질렀다는 결론이 나옵니까? 그는 안으로 뛰어들어 방들을 가로질러 달렸을 수도 있고, 아버지를 밀쳤을 수도 있고, 아버지를 쳤을 수도 있습니다. 그러나 스베틀로바 부인이 거기 없다는 것을 확인하자마자, 그는 그녀가 없다는 것에, 그리고 자기가 아버지를 죽이지 않았다는 것에 기뻐하며 달아났을 수도 있습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 그가 바로 아버지를 죽이려는 유혹에서 벗어났기 때문에, 떳떳한 양심을 가지고 아버지를 죽이지 않은 것을 기뻐하고 있었기 때문에, 그는 순수한 감정, 곧 연민과 동정의 감정을 느낄 수 있었고, 흥분한 나머지 그리고리를 쓰러뜨린 뒤 일 분쯤 지나 담장에서 뛰어내려 그를 도우러 갔던 것입니다.\n\n검사는 무서운 웅변으로, 모크로예에서 사랑이 다시 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 새 삶으로 그를 부르고 있었으나 그에게는 사랑이 불가능했던, 피고의 마음의 끔찍한 상태를 우리에게 묘사해 주었습니다. 그의 등 뒤에는 피투성이가 된 아버지의 시신이 있었고, 그 시신 너머에는—응보가 있었기 때문이라는 것이지요. 그러면서도 검사는 그에게 사랑을 허락했는데, 그는 자기 방법에 따라 그것을 설명하면서 그가 취해 있었던 상태에 대해, 처형장으로 끌려가는 죄수에 대해, 그것이 아직 멀었다는 것에 대해, 운운, 운운 늘어놓았습니다. 그러나 저는 다시 묻습니다, 검사님, 당신은 새로운 인격을 지어내신 것이 아닙니까? 피고가, 만일 그의 손이 정말로 아버지의 피로 물들어 있었다면, 바로 그 순간에 사랑을 생각하고 처벌을 모면할 잔꾀를 생각할 수 있을 만큼 그토록 거칠고 무정한 자입니까? 아니요, 아니요, 아닙니다! 그녀가 그를 사랑하며 그를 자기 곁으로 부르고 새로운 행복을 약속한다는 것이 그에게 분명해지자마자, 오! 단언컨대 그는 자살하고 싶은 충동을 두 배로, 세 배로 느꼈을 것이며, 만일 아버지의 살해를 양심에 짊어지고 있었다면 틀림없이 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 것입니다. 오, 아닙니다! 그는 자기 권총이 어디 놓여 있는지 잊지 않았을 것입니다! 저는 피고를 압니다. 검사가 그에게 돌린 야만적이고 돌처럼 차가운 무정함은 그의 성격과 맞지 않습니다. 그는 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 것입니다, 그것은 분명합니다. 그가 자살하지 않은 것은 다만 ‘어머니의 기도가 그를 구했기’ 때문이며, 그가 아버지의 피에 죄가 없었기 때문입니다. 그날 밤 모크로예에서 그가 괴로워하고 슬퍼한 것은 오로지 늙은 그리고리 때문이었으며, 그 노인이 회복되기를, 자기가 가한 일격이 치명적이지 않기를, 그래서 자기가 그 때문에 고통받지 않아도 되기를 하느님께 기도하고 있었던 것입니다. 어찌하여 사실에 대한 이런 해석을 받아들이지 못한단 말입니까? 피고가 거짓말을 하고 있다는 무슨 믿을 만한 증거를 우리가 가지고 있습니까?\n\n그러나 우리는 또 즉각 이런 말을 듣게 될 것입니다. ‘저기 그의 아버지의 시신이 있지 않은가! 그가 죽이지 않고 달아났다면, 대체 누가 그를 죽였단 말인가?’ 여기에, 거듭 말씀드리지만, 검찰 측 논리의 전부가 있습니다. 그가 아니라면 누가 그를 죽였느냐는 것이지요. 그의 자리에 세울 사람이 아무도 없다는 것입니다.\n\n배심원 여러분, 정말로 그러합니까? 정말로, 실제로, 다른 사람이 아무도 없다는 것이 사실입니까? 우리는 검사가 그날 밤 그 집에 있던 모든 사람을 손가락으로 꼽는 것을 들었습니다. 그들은 다섯 명이었으며, 그중 셋은—살해된 본인, 늙은 그리고리, 그리고 그의 아내는—책임이 있을 수 없다는 데 저도 동의합니다. 그러면 피고와 스메르댜코프가 남는데, 검사는 극적으로 외치기를, 피고가 달리 지목할 사람이 없었기에 스메르댜코프를 가리킨 것이며, 만일 여섯 번째 사람이, 여섯 번째 사람의 환영이라도 있었다면 그는 부끄러움에 당장 스메르댜코프에 대한 고발을 거두고 그 다른 사람을 고발했을 것이라고 합니다. 그러나 배심원 여러분, 어찌하여 저는 정반대의 결론을 끌어내서는 안 됩니까? 두 사람이 있습니다—피고와 스메르댜코프입니다. 어찌하여 저는, 당신들이 달리 고발할 사람이 없기에 다만 제 의뢰인을 고발하는 것이라고 말해서는 안 됩니까? 그리고 당신들에게 달리 고발할 사람이 없는 것은 오로지 당신들이 스메르댜코프를 모든 혐의에서 배제하기로 작정했기 때문입니다.\n\n스메르댜코프를 고발하는 사람이 피고와 그의 두 형제, 그리고 스베틀로바 부인뿐인 것은 사실입니다. 그러나 그를 고발하는 다른 것들이 있습니다. 막연한 의문의 소문, 의혹, 흐릿한 풍문, 무언가 기대하는 느낌이 있습니다. 끝으로, 우리에게는 비록 결정적이지는 않다는 점을 인정하지만 매우 암시적인 정황들의 결합이라는 증거가 있습니다. 첫째로, 다름 아닌 그 참사의 날에 일어난 그 발작이 있는데, 검사는 무슨 까닭에선지 그 진정성을 위해 애써 변호해야 한다고 느꼈습니다. 다음으로, 재판 전날 스메르댜코프가 별안간 자살한 일이 있습니다. 다음으로, 그의 유죄를 믿어 왔으나 오늘 한 묶음의 지폐를 내놓으며 스메르댜코프를 살인자로 선언한, 피고의 큰형이 오늘 법정에서 한 못지않게 놀라운 증언이 있습니다. 오, 저는 이반 카라마조프가 뇌염을 앓고 있으며, 그의 진술이 정말로 섬망 속에서 꾸며진, 죽은 사람에게 죄를 떠넘겨 동생을 구하려는 필사적인 노력일 수 있다는 법정과 검사의 확신에 전적으로 동의합니다. 그러나 또다시 스메르댜코프의 이름이 입에 오르고, 또다시 어떤 수수께끼의 암시가 있습니다. 설명되지 않은, 미완의 무언가가 있는 것입니다. 그리고 그것은 어쩌면 언젠가 밝혀질지도 모릅니다. 그러나 지금은 그 문제로 들어가지 않겠습니다. 그것은 나중에 이야기하지요.\n\n법정은 재판을 계속 진행하기로 결정했으나, 그동안에 저는 검사가 정교함과 재능으로 그려낸 스메르댜코프의 성격 묘사에 대해 몇 마디 말씀드리고자 합니다. 그러나 저는 그의 재능에 감탄하면서도 그에게 동의할 수는 없습니다. 저는 스메르댜코프를 찾아가, 그를 보고 그와 이야기를 나누었는데, 그는 제게 사뭇 다른 인상을 주었습니다. 그가 건강이 허약했던 것은 사실입니다. 그러나 성격에서, 정신에서, 그는 결코 검사가 그려낸 것처럼 나약한 인간이 아니었습니다. 저는 그에게서 검사가 그토록 강조한 그 소심함의 흔적을 전혀 찾지 못했습니다. 그에게는 단순함 또한 없었습니다. 오히려 저는 그에게서, 순박함의 가면 아래 감추어진 극도의 불신과 상당히 넓은 범위에 걸친 지력을 발견했습니다. 검사는 그를 어리석은 자로 여긴 점에서 너무 단순했습니다. 그는 제게 매우 뚜렷한 인상을 주었습니다. 저는 그가 분명히 심술궂은 인간이며, 지나치게 야심에 차 있고, 앙심이 깊고, 몹시 시샘이 많은 자라는 확신을 안고 그와 헤어졌습니다. 저는 몇 가지를 알아보았습니다. 그는 자기 출신을 분하게 여겼고, 그것을 부끄러워했으며, 자기가 ‘냄새나는 리자베타’의 아들이라는 것을 떠올릴 때면 이를 악물곤 했습니다. 그는 어린 시절 자기를 돌봐 준 하인 그리고리와 그의 아내에게 무례하게 굴었습니다. 그는 러시아를 저주하고 비웃었습니다. 그는 프랑스로 가서 프랑스인이 되기를 꿈꾸었습니다. 그는 자기에게 그럴 만한 재력이 없다고 자주 말하곤 했습니다. 짐작건대 그는 자기 자신밖에는 누구도 사랑하지 않았으며, 자기 자신에 대해 이상하리만치 높은 평가를 품고 있었습니다. 그의 교양에 대한 관념이라야 좋은 옷, 깨끗한 셔츠 앞섶, 잘 닦인 장화에 국한되어 있었습니다. 자기가 표도르 파블로비치의 사생아라고 믿고 있었으니(이를 뒷받침하는 증거가 있습니다), 그는 주인의 적자들의 처지와 견주어 자기 처지를 분하게 여겼을 만합니다. 그들은 모든 것을 가졌고, 그는 아무것도 갖지 못했습니다. 그들은 모든 권리를, 상속을 가졌으나, 그는 일개 요리사에 지나지 않았습니다. 그는 자기가 표도르 파블로비치를 도와 봉투에 지폐를 넣었노라고 제게 직접 말했습니다. 그 돈의 행방은—자기 앞날을 열어 줄 만한 액수의 돈이—그에게는 증오스러웠을 것이 틀림없습니다. 게다가 그는 삼천 루블을 새 무지갯빛 지폐로 보았습니다.(저는 일부러 그것에 대해 그에게 물었습니다.) 오, 야심 많고 시샘 많은 사람에게 한꺼번에 큰돈을 보여 주는 것을 경계하십시오! 그리고 그가 한 사람의 손에 그토록 많은 돈이 들려 있는 것을 본 것은 그것이 처음이었습니다. 그 무지갯빛 지폐의 광경은 그의 상상력에 병적인 인상을 남겼을 수 있으나, 당장의 결과를 낳지는 않았습니다.\n\n재능 있는 검사는 비상한 정교함으로 스메르댜코프의 유죄라는 가설에 찬성하는 논거와 반대하는 논거를 모두 우리에게 그려 보여 주었으며, 특히 그가 발작을 가장할 무슨 동기가 있었느냐고 우리에게 물었습니다. 그러나 그는 발작을 전혀 가장하지 않았을 수도 있으니, 그 발작은 지극히 자연스럽게 일어났을 수도 있으며, 또한 지극히 자연스럽게 지나갔을 수도 있고, 그 병자는 회복되었을 수도, 어쩌면 완전히는 아니지만 그래도 간질 환자에게 흔히 그러하듯 의식을 되찾았을 수도 있는 것입니다.\n\n검사는 어느 순간에 스메르댜코프가 살인을 저지를 수 있었겠느냐고 묻습니다. 그러나 그 순간을 지목하기란 매우 쉽습니다. 그는 늙은 그리고리가 목청껏 ‘아비 죽인 놈!’ 하고 외친 바로 그 순간에 깊은 잠에서 깨어났을 수 있습니다.(그는 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐이니—간질 발작 뒤에는 언제나 깊은 잠이 따르는 법입니다.) 어둠과 정적 속의 그 외침이 그 순간 잠이 덜 깊었을 수도 있는 스메르댜코프를 깨웠을 수 있습니다. 그는 한 시간쯤 전에 자연스럽게 깨어났을 수도 있는 것입니다.\n\n침대에서 일어난 그는 거의 무의식적으로, 뚜렷한 동기도 없이, 무슨 일인가 보려고 그 소리 쪽으로 갑니다. 그의 머리는 아직 발작으로 흐려 있고, 그의 정신력은 반쯤 잠들어 있습니다. 그러나 일단 정원에 나서자 그는 불 켜진 창문 쪽으로 걸어가, 물론 그를 보면 반가워할 주인에게서 끔찍한 소식을 듣습니다. 그의 머리는 즉각 돌아가기 시작합니다. 그는 겁에 질린 주인에게서 온갖 세세한 사정을 듣고, 그의 어지러운 머릿속에 차츰 한 생각이—끔찍하지만, 유혹적이고 거역할 수 없이 논리적인 한 생각이—모습을 갖춰 갑니다. 노인을 죽이고, 삼천 루블을 빼앗고, 모든 죄를 자기 젊은 주인에게 뒤집어씌우자는 것이지요. 발각될 염려가 없다는 것을 깨달으면서, 돈과 노획물에 대한 무서운 탐욕이 그를 사로잡았을 수 있습니다. 오! 이런 갑작스럽고 거역할 수 없는 충동은 호기가 찾아왔을 때 너무도 자주 일어나는 법이며, 더욱이 미리 살인을 저지를 생각이 전혀 없었던 살인자들에게서 그러합니다. 그리하여 스메르댜코프는 안으로 들어가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮겼을 수 있습니다. 무슨 무기로 말입니까? 그야, 정원에서 주운 아무 돌멩이로 그랬겠지요. 그러나 무엇 때문에, 무슨 목적으로 말입니까? 그야, 자기에게 앞날을 열어 줄 그 삼천 루블 때문이지요. 오, 저는 제 말을 뒤집고 있는 것이 아닙니다—그 돈은 실재했을 수 있습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 스메르댜코프만이 그것을 어디서 찾을지, 주인이 그것을 어디 두는지 알고 있었을 것입니다. 그렇다면 그 돈을 싸고 있던 것은—바닥에 떨어진 그 찢긴 봉투는 어찌 된 일입니까?\n\n조금 전, 검사가 카라마조프처럼 미숙한 도둑이라야 봉투를 바닥에 남겨 두었을 것이며, 자기에게 불리한 증거 한 조각도 남기기를 피했을 스메르댜코프 같은 자라면 그러지 않았을 것이라는 자기의 정교한 논리를 설명하고 있을 때, 저는 그것을 들으며 무언가 매우 낯익은 것을 듣고 있다는 생각이 들었습니다. 그리고, 믿으시겠습니까, 저는 카라마조프가 어떻게 행동했을 것인가에 관한 바로 그 논거, 바로 그 추측을, 정확히 이틀 전에 스메르댜코프 자신에게서 들었던 것입니다. 게다가 그것은 당시 제 마음에 걸렸습니다. 저는 그에게 무언가 꾸며 낸 듯한 단순함이 있다는 느낌을 받았습니다. 그가, 제가 그것을 제 생각이라고 여기도록, 그 생각을 제게 서둘러 암시하고 있다는 느낌을 받았던 것입니다. 그는 말하자면 그것을 슬그머니 심어 놓았던 것입니다. 그가 예심에서도 같은 생각을 슬그머니 심어 재능 있는 검사에게 암시하지 않았겠습니까?\n\n저는 이런 질문을 받게 될 것입니다. ‘그 노파, 그리고리의 아내는 어찌 된 일이냐? 그녀는 바로 가까이서 그 병자가 밤새도록 신음하는 소리를 들었다.’ 그렇습니다, 그녀는 그것을 들었습니다, 그러나 그 증언은 지극히 믿을 수 없는 것입니다. 저는 마당의 개 한 마리 때문에 밤새도록 잠을 못 이루었다고 호되게 불평하던 어느 부인을 알고 있었습니다. 그런데 알고 보니 그 가엾은 짐승은 밤새 겨우 한두 번 짖었을 뿐이었습니다. 그리고 그것은 당연한 일입니다. 누군가 잠들어 있다가 신음을 들으면, 그는 잠을 깬 데 짜증을 내며 깨어났다가도 곧바로 다시 잠들어 버립니다. 두 시간 뒤에 또 한 번 신음이 들리면, 그는 깨어났다가 다시 잠듭니다. 그리고 또 두 시간 뒤에 똑같은 일이—밤새 도합 세 번 일어납니다. 다음 날 아침이면 그 잠든 이는 깨어나 누군가 밤새도록 신음하며 자기 잠을 방해했다고 불평합니다. 그리고 그에게는 으레 그렇게 여겨질 수밖에 없으니, 두 시간씩의 잠의 간격은 기억하지 못하고 깨어 있던 순간만을 기억하는지라, 그는 밤새도록 잠을 깨우는 소리에 시달렸다고 느끼는 것입니다.\n\n그러나 어찌하여, 어찌하여, 하고 검사는 묻습니다, 스메르댜코프는 마지막 편지에서 자백하지 않았는가? 어찌하여 그의 양심은 그를 한 걸음으로는 이끌면서 두 걸음 모두로는 이끌지 않았는가? 그러나 죄송하지만, 양심이란 회개를 전제하는데, 그 자살자는 회개가 아니라 다만 절망을 느꼈을 수도 있습니다. 절망과 회개는 매우 다른 두 가지입니다. 절망은 앙심에 차고 화해할 줄 모를 수 있으며, 그 자살자는 제 손으로 제 목숨을 끊으면서, 평생토록 시샘해 온 자들에 대한 증오를 곱절로 느꼈을 만합니다.\n\n배심원 여러분, 오심을 경계하십시오! 제가 방금 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 이 모든 것에 무엇이 있을 법하지 않은 것이 있습니까? 제 추론에서 오류를 찾아내십시오. 불가능함을, 터무니없음을 찾아내십시오. 그리고 제 명제 속에 한 가닥의 가능성이라도, 한 가닥의 개연성이라도 있다면, 그를 단죄하지 마십시오. 그런데 과연 한 가닥뿐이겠습니까? 저는 신성한 모든 것에 걸고 맹세하거니와, 제가 방금 제시한 그 살인에 관한 설명을 온전히 믿습니다. 저를 괴롭히고 분노케 하는 것은, 검찰 측이 피고에게 불리하게 쌓아 올린 그 산더미 같은 사실들 가운데 확실하고 반박할 수 없는 것이 단 하나도 없다는 점입니다. 그런데도 이 불행한 사람은 이 사실들의 누적으로 인해 파멸당하려 하고 있습니다. 그렇습니다, 그 누적된 효과는 무시무시합니다. 피, 그의 손가락에서 뚝뚝 떨어지는 피, 피로 물든 셔츠, ‘아비 죽인 놈!’ 하는 외침으로 울리는 어두운 밤, 그리고 머리가 깨진 채 쓰러지는 노인. 그리고 그 산더미 같은 말들, 진술들, 몸짓들, 외침들! 오! 이것은 그토록 큰 영향을 미치며, 그토록 사람의 마음을 기울게 할 수 있습니다. 그러나 배심원 여러분, 그것이 여러분의 마음을 기울게 할 수 있겠습니까? 기억하십시오, 여러분께는 묶고 푸는 절대적인 권능이 주어졌으나, 그 권능이 클수록 그 책임은 더욱 무서운 법입니다.\n\n저는 방금 말씀드린 것에서 한 치도 물러서지 않습니다. 그러나 한순간, 제 불운한 의뢰인이 자기 아버지의 피로 손을 더럽혔다는 검찰 측 주장에 제가 동의한다고 가정해 봅시다. 거듭 말씀드리지만 이것은 어디까지나 가설입니다. 저는 단 한순간도 그의 무죄를 의심하지 않습니다. 그러나 그렇다 치고, 제 의뢰인이 아비 살해의 죄를 지었다고 상정해 봅시다. 그렇다 하더라도, 제가 드릴 말씀을 들어 주십시오. 저는 여러분께 무언가 더 말씀드리고 싶은 것이 제 가슴속에 있으니, 여러분의 가슴과 머릿속에 큰 갈등이 있을 것임을 느끼기 때문입니다…… 여러분의 가슴과 머리를 거론하는 것을 용서해 주십시오, 배심원 여러분, 그러나 저는 끝까지 진실하고 진솔하고자 합니다. 우리 모두 진솔해집시다!”\n\n이 대목에서 연설은 제법 큰 박수 소리에 가로막혔다. 실로 마지막 말은 어찌나 진솔한 가락으로 발음되었던지, 저마다 그가 정말로 할 말이 있으며 그가 막 하려는 말이 더없이 중대한 것이리라고 느꼈다. 그러나 재판장은 박수 소리를 듣고 큰 목소리로, 만일 그런 일이 되풀이된다면 법정을 비우게 하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 모든 소리가 잠잠해졌고, 페튜코비치는 여태껏 써 온 어조와는 사뭇 다른, 감정이 가득한 목소리로 입을 열었다.\n\n# Chapter XIII. A Corrupter Of Thought\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 제 의뢰인을 파멸로 몰아가는 것은 비단 정황 증거들이 쌓였기 때문만이 아닙니다.\" 그는 입을 열었다. \"제 의뢰인에게 정말로 치명적인 것은 단 하나의 사실, 곧 그 아버지의 시신입니다. 만약 이것이 평범한 살인 사건이었다면, 여러분은 그 증거들을 하나하나 따로 떼어 살펴볼 적에 그 하찮음과 불완전함과 환상적인 성격을 헤아려 공소를 기각하셨을 것입니다. 적어도 그자에 대한 편견만으로—아, 그가 너무도 마땅히 받아 마땅한 그 편견만으로—한 인간의 생애를 망가뜨리는 데에는 주저하셨을 것입니다. 그러나 이것은 평범한 살인이 아니라 친부 살해 사건입니다. 그것이 사람들의 마음에 강한 인상을 남기되, 어찌나 강하게 남기는지 바로 그 증거의 하찮음과 불완전함조차도 편견 없는 마음에마저 덜 하찮고 덜 불완전한 것으로 비치는 것입니다. 어떻게 이런 피고를 무죄로 풀어 줄 수 있단 말인가? 만약 그가 살인을 저질렀는데 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나간다면 어쩔 것인가? 이것이 거의 모든 사람이 본의 아니게, 본능적으로 마음속에서 느끼는 바입니다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 아버지의 피를 흘리게 한다는 것은 무서운 일입니다. 나를 낳아 주고, 나를 사랑해 주고, 나를 위해 제 목숨을 아끼지 않았으며, 어린 시절부터 내 병을 두고 애태우고, 평생토록 내 행복을 위해 노심초사하고, 내 기쁨과 내 성공 속에서 함께 살아온 아버지를. 그런 아버지를 죽인다는 것—그것은 상상조차 할 수 없는 일입니다. 배심원 여러분, 아버지란 무엇입니까—참된 아버지란? 그 위대한 단어의 뜻은 무엇입니까? 그 이름 안에 담긴 위대한 관념은 무엇입니까? 참된 아버지란 무엇이며 마땅히 어떠해야 하는가를 우리는 방금 부분적으로나마 짚어 보았습니다. 우리가 지금 이토록 깊이 매달려 있고 우리 마음을 아프게 하는 이 사건에서—이 사건에서, 아버지인 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프는 우리가 방금 언급한 그 아버지의 관념에 부합하지 않았습니다. 그것이 바로 불행입니다. 실로 어떤 아버지들은 하나의 불행입니다. 이 불행을 좀 더 가까이 들여다보기로 합시다. 배심원 여러분, 여러분이 내려야 할 결정의 중대함을 헤아린다면 우리는 그 무엇 앞에서도 움츠러들어서는 안 됩니다. 재능 있는 검사께서 멋지게 표현하셨듯이, 어린아이나 겁먹은 여자들처럼 그 어떤 생각 앞에서도 움츠러들지 않는 것이 우리의 각별한 의무입니다.\n\n\"그러나 저의 존경하는 적수께서는—그분은 제가 입을 열기도 전에 이미 저의 적수였습니다만—그 열변을 토하시는 중에 몇 번이나 이렇게 외치셨습니다. '오, 나는 페테르부르크에서 내려온 변호사에게 피고의 변호를 양보하지 않겠다. 나는 고발하지만 또한 변호도 하노라!' 그분은 그렇게 여러 번 외치셨으나, 한 가지만은 언급하기를 잊으셨습니다. 만약 이 무시무시한 피고가 어린 시절 아버지의 집에서 자기에게 친절을 베푼 유일한 사람에게 받은 한 줌의 호두 때문에 이십삼 년 동안이나 그토록 감사하는 마음을 품었다면, 그런 사람이라면 아버지의 뒷마당을 '발에는 장화도 신지 않고 작은 바지는 단추 하나에 매달린 채로' 뛰어다니던 일을—마음씨 고운 의사 게르첸슈투베의 표현을 빌리자면—이십삼 년 동안 능히 기억하고 있지 않았겠습니까?\n\n\"오, 배심원 여러분, 우리가 어찌하여 이 불행을 더 자세히 들여다봐야 하며, 어찌하여 우리 모두가 이미 알고 있는 것을 되풀이해야겠습니까? 제 의뢰인이 이곳, 아버지의 집에 당도했을 때 무엇과 마주쳤습니까? 그런데 어찌하여 제 의뢰인을 무정한 이기주의자요 괴물로 그려 내려 하는 것입니까? 그는 자제할 줄 모르고, 거칠고 다스리기 어려운 사람입니다—우리는 지금 그 점을 두고 그를 재판하고 있습니다—그러나 그의 삶에 책임이 있는 자는 누구입니까? 그가 그토록 빼어난 천성과 감사할 줄 아는 예민한 마음을 지녔음에도 그토록 꼴사나운 양육을 받게 된 데에 책임이 있는 자는 누구입니까? 누가 그를 사리에 밝은 사람으로 길렀습니까? 누가 그를 학문으로 깨우쳐 주었습니까? 어린 시절 누가 그를 조금이라도 사랑해 주었습니까? 제 의뢰인은 들판의 짐승처럼 섭리의 손에 내맡겨졌습니다. 그는 어쩌면 오랜 세월의 이별 끝에 아버지를 보고 싶어 목말라했을 것입니다. 어쩌면 천 번이라도, 자신의 어린 시절을 떠올리며 어린 꿈을 따라다니던 혐오스러운 환영들을 쫓아내고, 온 마음을 다해 아버지를 끌어안고 용서하기를 갈망했을 것입니다! 그런데 그를 기다리고 있던 것은 무엇이었습니까? 그를 맞이한 것은 냉소적인 조롱과 의심과 돈을 둘러싼 다툼이었습니다. 그는 날마다 브랜디 잔을 기울이며 내뱉어지는 역겨운 말과 사악한 훈계 외에는 아무것도 듣지 못했고, 마침내 자기 아버지가 자기 돈으로 자기 정부를 빼앗아 가는 것을 보았습니다. 오, 배심원 여러분, 그것은 잔혹하고 역겨운 일이었습니다! 그런데도 그 늙은이는 아들의 불효와 잔인함을 늘 한탄했습니다. 사교계에서 그를 헐뜯고, 그에게 해를 끼치고, 그를 모함하고, 그를 감옥에 처넣으려고 그가 갚지 못한 빚을 사들이기까지 했습니다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 제 의뢰인과 같은 사람들, 겉으로는 사납고 다스리기 어렵고 자제할 줄 모르는 사람들은 때로, 아니 실로 아주 흔히, 더없이 다정한 마음을 지니고 있되 다만 그것을 드러내지 못할 뿐입니다. 웃지 마십시오, 제 생각을 비웃지 마십시오! 재능 있는 검사께서는 방금 제 의뢰인이 실러를 사랑한다고—숭고하고 아름다운 것을 사랑한다고 해서 가차 없이 비웃으셨습니다! 제가 그분의 처지였다면 그것을 비웃지 않았을 것입니다. 그렇습니다, 그런 천성들은—오, 그토록 자주 그토록 가혹하게 오해받는 그런 천성들을 변호하게 해 주십시오—이런 천성들은 흔히 자기 자신과, 자신의 다스릴 수 없음과 자신의 사나움과 대비되는 듯한 다정함과 선함과 정의를 갈망하되, 그것을 무의식적으로 갈망합니다. 겉으로는 격정적이고 사납지만, 이를테면 여자를 사랑할 때면 고통스러우리만치 깊이, 그것도 정신적이고 고결한 사랑으로 사랑할 수 있는 것입니다. 거듭 말하지만 저를 비웃지 마십시오. 이는 그런 천성들에서 아주 흔히 있는 일입니다. 그러나 그들은 자기 격정을—때로는 아주 거친 격정을—숨기지 못하니, 바로 그것이 눈에 띄고 사람들의 주목을 받지만, 그 속사람은 보이지 않는 것입니다. 그들의 격정은 빠르게 소진됩니다. 그러나 고귀하고 드높은 존재 곁에서 그 겉보기에 거칠고 투박한 사람은 새로운 삶을 찾으며, 자신을 바로잡고 더 나아지고 고귀하고 명예로운 사람이 되어, 그 표현이 아무리 조롱당했을지라도 '숭고하고 아름다운' 사람이 되기를 추구합니다.\n\n\"저는 방금 제 의뢰인의 약혼에 관해서는 감히 건드리지 않겠다고 말씀드렸습니다. 그러나 한마디만 하겠습니다. 우리가 방금 들은 것은 증언이 아니라 광란에 빠진 복수심에 찬 한 여인의 비명에 지나지 않았으며, 그를 배신으로 책망할 자격은—오, 그녀에게는 결코 없었습니다!—다름 아닌 그녀였으니, 그녀야말로 그를 배신한 것이기 때문입니다! 그녀에게 조금이라도 곰곰이 생각할 시간이 있었더라면 그런 증언은 하지 않았을 것입니다. 오, 그녀의 말을 믿지 마십시오! 아닙니다, 제 의뢰인은 그녀가 부른 것처럼 괴물이 아닙니다!\n\n\"인류를 사랑하신 분께서 십자가에 못 박히시기 전날 말씀하셨습니다. '나는 선한 목자라. 선한 목자는 양들을 위하여 목숨을 버리나니, 그중 하나도 잃지 않게 하려 함이라.' 우리로 말미암아 한 인간의 영혼이 잃어지지 않게 하소서!\n\n\"저는 방금 '아버지'란 무엇을 뜻하는가를 물으며, 그것이 위대한 단어요 소중한 이름이라고 외쳤습니다. 그러나 여러분, 우리는 말을 정직하게 써야 하니, 저는 감히 사물을 그 마땅한 이름으로 부르고자 합니다. 카라마조프 노인과 같은 아버지는 아버지라 부를 수 없으며 그럴 자격도 없습니다. 무가치한 아버지에 대한 자식의 사랑이란 부조리요, 불가능입니다. 사랑은 무에서 만들어질 수 없으니, 오직 하느님만이 무에서 무언가를 창조하실 수 있습니다.\n\n\"'아비들아, 너희 자녀를 노엽게 하지 말라.' 사도는 사랑으로 타오르는 마음에서 이렇게 적었습니다. 제가 이 거룩한 말씀을 인용하는 것은 제 의뢰인을 위해서가 아니라 모든 아버지를 위해서입니다. 누가 제게 아버지들에게 설교할 권한을 주었습니까? 아무도 없습니다. 그러나 한 사람으로서, 한 시민으로서 저는 호소합니다—비보스 보코! 우리는 이 땅에 오래 머물지 않으며, 수많은 악한 일을 행하고 수많은 악한 말을 합니다. 그러니 우리 모두 함께 모인 이 좋은 기회를 붙잡아 서로에게 좋은 말 한마디를 건넵시다. 저는 지금 그것을 하고 있습니다. 이 자리에 서 있는 동안 저는 제 기회를 이용하는 것입니다. 이 연단이 최고의 권위로부터 우리에게 주어진 것은 헛된 일이 아니니—온 러시아가 우리의 말을 듣고 있습니다! 저는 이 자리에 계신 아버지들에게만 말하는 것이 아니라, 모든 아버지에게 큰 소리로 외칩니다. '아비들아, 너희 자녀를 노엽게 하지 말라.' 그렇습니다, 우리가 먼저 그리스도의 계명을 스스로 이행하고 난 다음에야 비로소 우리 자녀에게 그것을 기대할 수 있습니다. 그러지 않으면 우리는 자녀의 아버지가 아니라 자녀의 원수이며, 그들은 우리의 자녀가 아니라 우리의 원수이니, 우리 스스로 그들을 우리의 원수로 만든 것입니다. '너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라'—이는 제가 하는 말이 아니라 복음서의 가르침으로, 남이 너희를 헤아리는 만큼 너희도 남을 헤아리라는 것입니다. 자녀들이 우리가 헤아린 만큼 우리를 헤아린다 한들 어찌 우리가 그들을 탓할 수 있겠습니까?\n\n\"얼마 전 핀란드에서 한 하녀가 몰래 아이를 낳았다는 의심을 받았습니다. 사람들이 그녀를 감시했는데, 다락방 한구석 벽돌 더미 뒤에서 아무도 모르던 상자 하나가 발견되었습니다. 그것을 열어 보니 안에는 그녀가 죽인 갓난아이의 시신이 들어 있었습니다. 그 같은 상자 안에서 다른 두 아기의 유골도 발견되었는데, 그녀 자신의 자백에 따르면 그 아기들도 태어나는 순간에 죽였다는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 그 여자가 제 아이들에게 어머니였습니까? 그녀가 아이들을 낳기는 했습니다. 그러나 그녀가 아이들에게 어머니였습니까? 누가 감히 그녀에게 어머니라는 거룩한 이름을 붙이겠습니까? 여러분, 우리 대담해집시다, 아니 과감해지기까지 합시다. 지금 이 순간 그렇게 하는 것이 우리의 의무이며, 오스트롭스키의 희곡에 나오는 모스크바 여인들처럼 어떤 말이나 생각 앞에서 두려워해서는 안 됩니다. 그들은 어떤 말소리에만 들어도 겁을 집어먹지요. 아니, 우리는 지난 몇 해의 진보가 우리에게까지 닿았음을 증명하고, 분명히 말합시다. 아버지란 단지 아이를 낳은 자가 아니라, 아이를 낳고 그 아이에 대한 의무를 다하는 자라고.\n\n\"오, 물론 다른 의미도, '아버지'라는 단어의 다른 해석도 있습니다. 그것은 어떤 아버지든, 비록 그가 괴물일지라도, 비록 그가 제 자녀의 원수일지라도, 나를 낳았다는 이유만으로 여전히 나의 아버지라고 주장합니다. 그러나 이것은, 말하자면, 제 지성으로는 헤아릴 수 없고 오직 믿음으로, 아니 더 정확히 말하자면 믿음에 기대어 받아들일 수밖에 없는 신비한 의미입니다. 제가 이해하지는 못하나 종교가 믿으라 명하는 다른 많은 것들이 그러하듯 말입니다. 그러나 그렇다면 그것은 실제 삶의 영역 바깥에 두기로 합시다. 실제 삶의 영역은, 실로 그 나름의 권리를 지니면서도 또한 우리에게 큰 의무와 책무를 지우거니와, 만약 우리가 인간적이고자 한다면—실로 그리스도교적이고자 한다면—그 영역에서 우리는 이성과 경험으로 정당화되고 분석의 도가니를 거친 확신에 따라서만 행동해야 하며, 또 마땅히 그래야 합니다. 한마디로 우리는 합리적으로 행동해야 하며, 꿈속이나 헛소리 속에서 하듯이 해서는 안 됩니다. 그래야만 우리가 한 인간에게 해를 끼치지 않고, 학대하지 않고, 망가뜨리지 않을 수 있습니다. 그래야 비로소 그것이 신비적일 뿐만 아니라 합리적이고 박애적인, 참된 그리스도교적 행위가 될 것입니다…….\"\n\n이 대목에서 법정 곳곳에서 격렬한 박수가 터져 나왔으나, 페튜코비치는 방해받지 않고 끝까지 말하게 해 달라고 간청하듯 두 손을 내저었다. 법정은 곧 다시 정적에 잠겼다. 변사는 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"여러분, 우리 자녀들이 자라나 사리를 따지기 시작할 때 이런 질문들을 피할 수 있다고 생각하십니까? 아닙니다, 그들은 피할 수 없으며, 우리도 그들에게 불가능한 제약을 강요하지 않을 것입니다. 무가치한 아버지를 보는 것은 어린 존재에게 본의 아니게 괴로운 질문들을 떠올리게 합니다. 더욱이 자기 친구들의 훌륭한 아버지들과 그를 견주어 볼 때면 그러합니다. 이 질문에 대한 통상적인 대답은 이렇습니다. '그가 너를 낳았으니 너는 그의 살이요 피라, 그러므로 너는 마땅히 그를 사랑해야 한다.' 그러면 그 젊은이는 본의 아니게 곰곰이 생각합니다. '그러나 그가 나를 낳을 때 나를 사랑했던가?' 그는 점점 더 의아해하며 묻습니다. '그가 나를 위해 나를 낳았던가? 그 순간, 어쩌면 술에 달아오른 정욕의 순간에 그는 나를 알지 못했으며, 심지어 내 성별조차 알지 못했다. 그리고 그가 내게 물려준 것이라곤 술에 빠지는 기질뿐이니—그것이 나를 위해 그가 한 일의 전부다……. 그 후 평생토록 나를 조금도 돌보지 않은 그를, 단지 나를 낳았다는 이유만으로 내가 어찌 사랑할 의무가 있단 말인가?'\n\n\"오, 어쩌면 이런 질문들이 여러분에게는 거칠고 잔인하게 들릴지 모릅니다. 그러나 어린 마음에서 불가능한 자제를 기대하지 마십시오. '천성을 문밖으로 내쫓으면 그것은 창문으로 날아 들어온다'고 합니다. 그러니 무엇보다도 말을 두려워하지 말고, 이 질문을 신비한 관념이 아니라 이성과 인간애의 명령에 따라 판단합시다. 어떻게 판단해야 할까요? 바로 이렇습니다. 아들이 아버지 앞에 서서 이렇게 묻게 하십시오. '아버지, 제가 왜 아버지를 사랑해야 하는지 말씀해 주십시오. 아버지, 제가 아버지를 사랑해야 한다는 것을 보여 주십시오.' 만약 그 아버지가 아들에게 대답하여 정당한 이유를 보여 줄 수 있다면, 우리는 신비한 편견에 기댄 것이 아니라 합리적이고 책임 있으며 엄정하게 인도주의적인 토대 위에 선, 참되고 정상적인 부자 관계를 갖게 되는 것입니다. 그러나 그가 보여 주지 못한다면, 가족의 유대는 거기서 끝입니다. 그는 아들에게 아버지가 아니며, 아들은 그를 타인으로, 심지어 원수로 여길 권리가 있습니다. 배심원 여러분, 우리의 이 연단은 참되고 건전한 사상의 학교가 되어야 합니다.\"\n\n(이 대목에서 변사는 억누를 수 없는, 거의 광적인 박수 때문에 말을 멈춰야 했다. 물론 청중 전체는 아니었으나 그 절반쯤은 박수를 보냈다. 자리에 있던 아버지들과 어머니들이 박수를 쳤다. 부인들이 앉아 있던 위층 관람석에서는 비명과 탄성이 터져 나왔다. 손수건들이 나부꼈다. 재판장은 있는 힘껏 종을 울리기 시작했다. 그는 청중의 행태에 분명 짜증이 나 있었으나, 앞서 위협했던 대로 법정을 비우게 할 엄두는 내지 못했다. 재판관들 뒤편 특별석에 앉아 있던, 가슴에 훈장을 단 고관대작 노인들마저 변사에게 박수를 보내며 손수건을 흔들었다. 그리하여 소란이 잦아들자 재판장은 법정을 비우게 하겠다는 준엄한 위협을 되풀이하는 데 그쳤고, 흥분하여 의기양양해진 페튜코비치는 연설을 이어 갔다.)\n\n\"배심원 여러분, 오늘 그토록 많이 거론된 그 무시무시한 밤을 여러분은 기억하실 것입니다. 그날 밤 아들은 울타리를 넘어, 자기를 낳은 그 원수요 박해자와 얼굴을 마주하고 섰습니다. 저는 가장 단호하게 주장합니다. 그가 아버지의 집으로 달려간 것은 돈 때문이 아니었습니다. 강도질이라는 혐의는, 제가 앞서 증명했듯이 부조리입니다. 그리고 그가 집 안으로 침입한 것도 아버지를 죽이기 위해서가 아니었습니다, 오, 아닙니다! 만약 그런 의도가 있었다면 그는 적어도 미리 무장하는 신중함이라도 보였을 것입니다. 그 놋쇠 절굿공이는 그가 왜 그러는지도 모른 채 본능적으로 집어 든 것입니다. 그가 창문을 두드려 아버지를 속였다고 칩시다, 그가 안으로 들어갔다고 칩시다—저는 그 전설을 한순간도 믿지 않는다고 이미 말씀드렸으나, 그렇다고 칩시다, 잠시 그렇게 가정해 봅시다. 여러분, 저는 거룩한 모든 것을 두고 여러분께 맹세합니다. 만약 그가 아버지가 아니라 평범한 원수였다면, 그는 방들을 두루 살펴 그 여자가 거기 없음을 확인한 뒤, 자기 연적에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않고 부리나케 달아났을 것입니다. 그는 그를 한 대 치거나 어쩌면 밀쳐 냈을지언정 그 이상은 하지 않았을 것이니, 그럴 생각도 그럴 겨를도 없었기 때문입니다. 그가 알고 싶었던 것은 그녀가 어디 있느냐 하는 것뿐이었습니다. 그러나 그의 아버지, 그의 아버지였습니다! 어린 시절부터 그를 미워했고, 그의 원수였고, 그의 박해자였으며, 이제는 그의 패륜적인 연적이 된 그 아버지를 보는 것만으로도 충분했습니다! 본의 아니게, 거역할 수 없이 증오의 감정이 그를 덮쳐 그의 이성을 흐려 놓았습니다. 모든 것이 한순간에 솟구쳐 올랐던 것입니다! 그것은 광기와 정신착란의 충동이었으나, 동시에 자연의 충동이기도 했으니, (자연 속의 모든 것이 그러하듯) 거역할 수 없이 무의식적으로 제 영원한 법칙이 침해된 것에 복수하는 충동이었습니다.\n\n\"그러나 피고는 그때조차 그를 죽이지 않았습니다—저는 그것을 주장합니다, 저는 그것을 큰 소리로 외칩니다!—아닙니다, 그는 다만 분노에 찬 혐오감이 북받쳐 절굿공이를 휘둘렀을 뿐, 그를 죽일 생각도 없었고 자기가 그를 죽이리라는 것도 알지 못했습니다. 그의 손에 그 운명적인 절굿공이가 들려 있지 않았더라면 그는 어쩌면 아버지를 때려눕히기만 했을 뿐 죽이지는 않았을 것입니다. 달아나면서도 그는 자기가 노인을 죽였는지 어떤지 알지 못했습니다. 그런 살인은 살인이 아닙니다. 그런 살인은 친부 살해가 아닙니다. 아닙니다, 그런 아버지를 죽인 것은 친부 살해라 부를 수 없습니다. 그런 살인은 오직 편견에 의해서만 친부 살해로 셈해질 수 있을 따름입니다.\n\n\"그러나 저는 제 영혼 깊은 곳으로부터 거듭거듭 여러분께 호소합니다. 이 살인이 실제로 일어나기는 했습니까? 배심원 여러분, 만약 우리가 그를 유죄로 판결하고 처벌한다면, 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 것입니다. '이 사람들은 나를 길러 주기 위해서도, 나를 가르치기 위해서도 아무것도 하지 않았고, 내 처지를 낫게 하려고도, 나를 더 나은 인간으로 만들려고도, 나를 사람답게 만들려고도 아무것도 하지 않았다. 이 사람들은 내게 먹을 것과 마실 것도 주지 않았고, 옥에 갇히고 헐벗었을 때 나를 찾아보지도 않았으면서, 이제 와서는 나를 징역살이로 보내는구나. 이제 나는 셈을 다 치렀으니, 더는 그들에게 빚진 것이 없고, 앞으로 영원히 그 누구에게도 빚진 것이 없다. 그들이 악하니 나도 악해지리라. 그들이 잔인하니 나도 잔인해지리라.' 배심원 여러분, 그는 그렇게 말할 것입니다. 그리고 저는 맹세합니다. 그를 유죄로 판결함으로써 여러분은 오직 그를 더 편하게 해 줄 뿐입니다. 여러분은 그의 양심을 가볍게 해 줄 것이며, 그는 자기가 흘린 피를 저주하면서도 그것을 뉘우치지는 않을 것입니다. 동시에 여러분은 그가 새사람이 될 가능성을 그 안에서 파괴할 것이니, 그는 평생토록 자신의 악함과 눈멂 속에 머물러 있을 것이기 때문입니다.\n\n\"그러나 여러분은 그를 무섭게, 끔찍하게, 상상할 수 있는 가장 무서운 형벌로 처벌하면서도, 동시에 그를 구원하고 그 영혼을 다시 태어나게 하기를 원하십니까? 그렇다면 여러분의 자비로 그를 압도하십시오! 여러분은 보실 것이며, 그가 어떻게 떨며 두려움에 사로잡히는지를 들으실 것입니다. '내가 어떻게 이 자비를 감당할 수 있단 말인가? 내가 어떻게 이토록 큰 사랑을 감당할 수 있단 말인가? 내가 그럴 자격이 있는가?' 그는 그렇게 외칠 것입니다.\n\n\"오, 저는 압니다, 저는 그 마음을, 그 거칠지만 감사할 줄 아는 마음을 압니다, 배심원 여러분! 그 마음은 여러분의 자비 앞에 머리를 숙일 것입니다. 그것은 위대하고 사랑에 찬 행위를 갈망하니, 녹아내려 위로 솟아오를 것입니다. 어떤 영혼들은 그 편협함 때문에 온 세상을 탓합니다. 그러나 그런 영혼을 자비로 다스리고 사랑을 보여 주면, 그것은 제 과거를 저주할 것이니, 그 안에는 선한 충동들이 많이 깃들어 있기 때문입니다. 그런 마음은 활짝 열려, 하느님은 자비로우시며 사람들은 선하고 의롭다는 것을 깨달을 것입니다. 그는 두려움에 사로잡힐 것이며, 회한과 이제부터 자기에게 지워진 그 막대한 책무에 짓눌릴 것입니다. 그리고 그때 그는 '나는 셈을 다 치렀다'고 말하지 않고, '나는 모든 사람 앞에서 죄인이며, 그 누구보다도 무가치한 자다'라고 말할 것입니다. 참회의 눈물과 애절하고 다정한 고뇌에 젖어 그는 외칠 것입니다. '다른 사람들은 나보다 낫구나, 그들은 나를 망치려 한 것이 아니라 나를 구하려 한 것이었구나!' 오, 이 자비의 행위는 여러분에게 참으로 쉬운 일이니, 진정한 증거라 할 만한 것이 아무것도 없는 마당에 '그렇다, 그는 유죄다'라고 선고하기란 여러분에게 너무도 무서운 일일 것이기 때문입니다.\n\n\"죄인 열 명을 풀어 주는 것이 무고한 한 사람을 벌하는 것보다 낫습니다! 들리십니까, 우리의 영광스러운 역사의 지난 세기로부터 울려오는 저 장엄한 목소리가 들리십니까? 러시아의 법정이 처벌만을 위해서가 아니라 죄인의 구원을 위해서도 존재한다는 것을 여러분께 일깨우는 일이 저 같은 보잘것없는 사람에게 어울리는 바는 아닙니다! 다른 민족들이 응보와 법조문에 매달리게 두십시오. 우리는 그 정신과 그 의미에—잃어버린 자의 구원과 갱생에 매달릴 것입니다. 만약 이것이 참이라면, 만약 러시아와 그 정의가 그러하다면, 러시아는 기쁜 마음으로 앞으로 나아가도 좋습니다! 모든 민족이 혐오스럽게 비켜서는 여러분의 광란하는 트로이카로 우리를 겁주려 하지 마십시오. 폭주하는 트로이카가 아니라 위풍당당한 러시아의 마차가 침착하고 장엄하게 그 목표를 향해 나아갈 것입니다. 여러분의 손에 제 의뢰인의 운명이, 여러분의 손에 러시아 정의의 운명이 달려 있습니다. 여러분은 그것을 지켜 낼 것이며, 그것을 구원할 것이며, 그것을 지키고 보살필 사람들이 있다는 것을, 그것이 좋은 손에 맡겨져 있다는 것을 증명할 것입니다!\"\n* 비보스 보코(vivos voco): \"나는 산 자들을 부른다\"는 뜻의 라틴어로, 실러의 시 「종의 노래」에 인용되어 널리 알려진 교회 종에 새긴 명문에서 따온 것이다.\n\n# Chapter XIV. The Peasants Stand Firm\n\n페튜코비치는 이렇게 연설을 맺었고, 청중의 열광은 걷잡을 수 없는 폭풍처럼 터져 나왔다. 그것을 가라앉힌다는 것은 도무지 불가능한 일이었다. 여자들은 울었고, 남자들 가운데서도 적잖은 이들이 울었으며, 심지어 지체 높은 인사 두 사람마저 눈물을 흘렸다. 재판장도 결국 손을 들고는 종을 울리는 것조차 미루었다. 그런 열광을 억누른다는 것은 무언가 신성한 것을 억누르는 일이라고, 부인들은 나중에 외쳐 댔다. 연설가 자신도 진심으로 감동한 모습이었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 몇 가지 반론을 펴려고 자리에서 일어섰다. 사람들은 증오 어린 눈초리로 그를 바라보았다. \"뭐라고? 저게 무슨 짓이야? 정말로 반론을 펼 작정인가 봐.\" 부인들이 종알거렸다. 그러나 그 순간만큼은 온 세상 부인들이, 그의 아내까지 합세하여 항의한다 한들 그를 멈출 수는 없었으리라. 그는 창백했고, 격정에 떨고 있었으며, 첫마디는 아예 알아들을 수도 없었고, 숨을 헐떡이며 또렷하게 말하지 못한 채 갈피를 잃었다. 그러나 그는 곧 평정을 되찾았다. 그의 이 새로운 연설에서 나는 다만 몇 문장만을 인용하기로 하겠다.\n\n\"…저는 한 편의 소설을 지어냈다고 비난받고 있습니다. 그러나 이 변론이야말로 한 편의 소설 위에 또 한 편의 소설을 쌓아 올린 것이 아니고 무엇이겠습니까? 거기에 빠진 것이라곤 운문뿐이었습니다. 표도르 파블로비치는 정부를 기다리던 중에 봉투를 찢어 마룻바닥에 던졌습니다. 우리는 그가 이 기이한 짓을 하면서 무슨 말을 했는지까지 듣게 되었습니다. 이것이야말로 상상의 비약이 아니겠습니까? 그가 돈을 꺼냈다는 무슨 증거라도 우리에게 있습니까? 그가 한 말을 들은 자가 누구입니까? 저 정신박약의 백치 스메르자코프가 사생아로 태어난 데 대해 사회에 복수하는 바이런풍 영웅으로 둔갑하다니—이것이야말로 바이런식 소설이 아니겠습니까? 그리고 아비의 집에 침입하여 죽이되 죽이지 않으면서 살해했다는 그 아들은, 소설조차도 못 됩니다—이것은 제 손으로도 풀지 못할 수수께끼를 우리에게 던지는 스핑크스입니다. 그가 죽였다면 죽인 것이지, 죽이지 않으면서 죽였다는 것이 도대체 무슨 말입니까—이걸 무슨 수로 앞뒤를 가려낼 수 있단 말입니까?\n\n\"그다음 우리는 우리의 법정이 진실하고 건전한 사상의 법정이라는 훈계를 받았으며, 이 '건전한 사상'의 법정에서 아비를 죽이는 일을 '친부 살해'라 부르는 것이 한낱 편견에 지나지 않는다는 엄숙한 선언이 들려왔습니다! 그러나 친부 살해가 편견이라면, 그리고 모든 자식이 제 아비에게 어찌하여 당신을 사랑해야 하느냐고 따져 묻게 된다면, 우리는 대체 어찌 되겠습니까? 사회의 기초는 어찌 되겠습니까? 가정은 어찌 되겠습니까? 친부 살해란, 알고 보니, 모스크바 상인 마누라들이나 무서워하는 도깨비에 불과하다는 것입니다. 러시아 사법의 운명과 미래를 위한 가장 소중하고 가장 신성한 보증이, 한 가지 목적을 이루기 위해서—곧 도저히 정당화될 수 없는 무언가를 정당화하기 위해서—왜곡되고 경박한 모습으로 우리 앞에 제시되고 있습니다. '오, 자비로써 그를 짓눌러 주소서.' 변호인은 이렇게 외칩니다. 그러나 그것이야말로 바로 그 범죄자가 바라는 바이며, 내일이면 그가 얼마나 짓눌렸는지 드러날 것입니다. 게다가 변호인은 한낱 피고인의 무죄 방면만을 청하다니, 너무 겸손한 것이 아니겠습니까? 차라리 이 친부 살해자를 기려 그 위업을 후세에 길이 전할 자선 사업이라도 세우는 게 어떻겠습니까? 종교와 복음서는 수정되었습니다—그런 것은 죄다 신비주의이며, 이성과 상식의 분석을 거친 우리의 것이야말로 유일하게 참된 기독교라고 우리는 듣습니다. 그리하여 그들은 우리 앞에 그리스도의 거짓 형상을 세워 놓는 것입니다! '너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라.' 변호인은 이렇게 외치고는, 곧장 그리스도께서 우리가 헤아림을 받는 대로 헤아리라고 가르치셨다는 결론을 끌어냅니다—그것도 진실과 건전한 양식의 법정에서 말입니다! 우리가 복음서를 들여다보는 것은 오직 연설을 앞둔 전날 밤뿐이니, 어쨌거나 제법 독창적인 작품에 대한 우리의 식견을 과시하여 청중의 눈을 어지럽히고, 어떤 효과를 거두는 데 써먹어 보려는 것입니다—죄다 그 목적에 봉사하려는 것이지요! 그러나 그리스도께서 우리에게 명하신 바는 그것과는 전혀 다른 것입니다. 그분은 우리에게 이렇게 하기를 삼가라 이르셨으니, 사악한 세상이 이렇게 하는 까닭이며, 우리는 마땅히 용서하고 다른 뺨을 돌려 대야지, 우리를 박해하는 자들이 우리를 헤아리는 대로 그들을 헤아려서는 안 된다 하셨습니다. 이것이야말로 우리의 하느님께서 우리에게 가르치신 바이며, 자식들이 제 아비를 죽이는 것을 금하는 것이 편견이라는 따위가 아닙니다. 그리고 우리는 진실과 건전한 양식의 법정에서 우리 주님의 복음서를 수정하지 않을 것이니, 변호인은 정통 러시아 전체에 맞서 그분을 그저 '십자가에 못 박힌 인류의 연인'이라고만 부르기를 황송하게도 좋아하지만, 정통 러시아는 그분을 향해 이렇게 부르짖습니다. '이는 당신이 우리의 하느님이시기 때문입니다!'\"\n\n이 대목에서 재판장이 끼어들어 지나치게 열을 올리는 연설가를 제지하며, 과장하지 말 것을, 도를 넘지 말 것을 청했고, 그 밖에도 그런 경우에 재판장들이 으레 하는 말들을 늘어놓았다. 청중 역시 술렁였다. 방청객들은 안절부절못했고, 분개하는 외침마저 터져 나왔다. 페튜코비치는 굳이 답하지도 않았다. 그는 다만 단상에 올라 가슴에 손을 얹고는, 모욕당한 목소리로 위엄에 찬 몇 마디를 내뱉었다. 그는 다만 \"소설 짓기\"와 \"심리\"를 다시금 가볍고 빈정대는 투로 건드렸을 뿐이며, 적절한 대목에서 \"유피테르여, 당신은 화를 내시니 곧 그르신 것입니다\"라는 말을 인용했는데, 이는 청중 가운데서 한바탕 찬동하는 웃음을 자아냈으니, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 결코 유피테르 같은 인물이 못 되었기 때문이다. 그러고는 자기가 젊은 세대에게 제 아비를 죽이라고 가르친다는 비난과 관련하여, 페튜코비치는 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 그런 것에는 답조차 하지 않겠노라고 말했다. 검사가 그가 정통에 어긋나는 견해를 입에 담았다고 비난한 것에 대해서는, 페튜코비치는 그것이 개인적인 빈정거림이라는 점을 넌지시 비치며, 자신은 이 법정에서만큼은 \"한 시민이자 충성스러운 신민으로서의 내 명예를 훼손하는\" 비난으로부터 안전하리라 기대했었다고 말했다. 그러나 이 말에 이르러 재판장이 그 역시 제지했고, 페튜코비치는 법정 안에 찬동의 웅성거림이 퍼지는 가운데 머리를 숙이며 연설을 맺었다. 그리고 이폴리트 키릴로비치는, 우리 부인들의 견해로는 \"아주 박살이 나고\" 말았다.\n\n이어 피고인에게 발언이 허락되었다. 미탸는 일어섰으나 거의 말을 하지 않았다. 그는 육체적으로나 정신적으로나 무섭도록 지쳐 있었다. 아침에 들어설 때 보였던 그 힘차고 당당한 기색은 거의 사라지고 없었다. 그는 그날 어떤 경험을 통과하면서, 그때까지 미처 깨닫지 못했던 무언가 지극히 중요한 것을 남은 평생을 두고 배운 사람처럼 보였다. 그의 목소리는 힘이 없었고, 전처럼 소리치지도 않았다. 그의 말에는 겸허와 패배와 굴복의 새로운 가락이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 말을 하겠습니까, 배심원 여러분? 제게 심판의 시간이 닥쳐왔으니, 저는 하느님의 손길이 제게 임하신 것을 느낍니다! 길을 잘못 든 한 인간에게 종말이 온 것입니다! 그러나 하느님 앞에서 다시 한번 여러분께 말씀드립니다, 저는 제 아비의 피에 대해서는 결백합니다! 마지막으로 다시 말씀드립니다, 그를 죽인 것은 제가 아닙니다! 저는 길을 잘못 들었으나, 선한 것을 사랑했습니다. 매 순간 저는 새사람이 되려 애썼으나, 짐승처럼 살았습니다. 저는 검사님께 감사드립니다, 그분은 제 자신에 대해 제가 알지 못했던 많은 것을 말씀해 주셨습니다. 그러나 제가 제 아비를 죽였다는 것은 사실이 아니며, 검사님은 잘못 알고 계십니다. 저는 제 변호인께도 감사드립니다. 저는 그분의 말씀을 들으며 울었습니다. 그러나 제가 제 아비를 죽였다는 것은 사실이 아니니, 그분은 그렇게 짐작하시지 않으셨어야 했습니다. 그리고 의사들의 말도 믿지 마십시오. 저는 더없이 멀쩡한 정신이며, 다만 가슴이 무거울 뿐입니다. 저를 살려 주신다면, 저를 풀어 주신다면, 저는 여러분을 위해 기도하겠습니다. 저는 더 나은 사람이 되겠습니다. 하느님 앞에서 여러분께 맹세코 그리하겠습니다! 그러나 만일 저를 단죄하신다면, 저는 제 손으로 제 머리 위에서 칼을 부러뜨리고 그 조각들에 입 맞추겠습니다. 그러나 저를 살려 주십시오, 제게서 제 하느님을 앗아 가지 마십시오! 저는 제 자신을 압니다, 저는 반역할 것입니다! 가슴이 무겁습니다, 여러분… 저를 살려 주십시오!\"\n\n그는 거의 제자리에 쓰러지듯 주저앉았고, 목소리가 갈라졌으며, 마지막 말은 거의 또렷하게 발음하지 못했다. 이어 재판관들은 질문을 던지기 시작했고, 양측에 각각의 결론을 정식으로 진술하라고 요구하기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 나는 그 시시콜콜한 절차를 묘사하지는 않겠다. 마침내 배심원들이 협의를 위해 자리에서 일어났다. 재판장은 무척 지쳐 있었던 터라, 배심원에게 내린 마지막 당부도 다소 맥이 빠진 것이었다. \"공정하게 임하고, 변론의 웅변에 휘둘리지 말되, 그래도 논거를 잘 따져 보십시오. 여러분에게 막중한 책임이 지워졌음을 명심하십시오.\" 운운하는 식이었다.\n\n배심원들이 물러갔고 법정은 휴정에 들어갔다. 사람들은 일어서서 돌아다니고, 쌓인 소감을 주고받으며, 매점에서 요기를 할 수 있었다. 시간은 무척 늦어 거의 새벽 한 시에 가까웠으나, 아무도 자리를 뜨지 않았다. 긴장이 어찌나 팽팽했던지 누구도 쉴 생각을 할 수 없었다. 모두가 가슴을 졸이며 기다렸다. 하기야 그것은 다소 지나친 말일지도 모르니, 부인들로 말하자면 히스테릭한 초조함에 사로잡혀 있었을 뿐 그 가슴은 조금도 불안하지 않았던 까닭이다. 무죄 방면은 피할 수 없다고 그녀들은 생각했다. 그녀들은 모두 다 함께 열광할 극적인 순간에 대비하고 있었다. 솔직히 인정하건대, 남자들 가운데서도 무죄 방면이 틀림없다고 확신하는 이가 적지 않았다. 어떤 이는 흐뭇해했고, 어떤 이는 인상을 찌푸렸으며, 또 어떤 이는 그가 방면되기를 바라지 않아 그저 풀이 죽어 있었다. 페튜코비치 자신은 자신의 성공을 확신했다. 그의 주위로 사람들이 몰려들어 축하하고 알랑거렸다.\n\n\"이런 것이 있습니다.\" 나중에 들은 바로, 그는 한 무리에게 이렇게 말했다고 한다. \"변호인과 배심원을 잇는 보이지 않는 실이 있는 법입니다. 연설을 하는 동안 그 실이 짜이고 있는지 어떤지를 느낄 수 있지요. 저는 그것을 느꼈습니다. 그것은 분명히 존재합니다. 우리의 사건은 이긴 것입니다. 마음 놓으십시오.\"\n\n\"이제 우리 농민들이 뭐라고 할까요?\" 인근의 한 지주인, 살집 좋고 심술궂은 표정에 곰보 자국이 있는 신사가, 한창 이야기에 열을 올리는 신사들 무리에 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그들이 다 농민은 아니지요. 그중에 관청 서기가 넷이나 끼어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 서기들이 있습니다.\" 군 자치회 의원 하나가 무리에 끼어들며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 메달을 단 상인 나자리예프가 배심원인 것을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"그가 어떻다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"머리가 비상한 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그는 도통 말이 없잖습니까.\"\n\n\"말수가 적긴 하지요, 하지만 오히려 그 편이 낫습니다. 그 페테르부르크 양반이 그를 가르칠 필요가 없어요. 그가 페테르부르크 전체를 가르치고도 남을 위인이지요. 그는 열두 자식의 아비랍니다. 한번 생각해 보십시오!\"\n\n\"맙소사, 설마 그들이 그를 무죄로 방면하지 않으리라고 보시는 건 아니겠지요?\" 우리 고장의 한 젊은 관리가 다른 무리에서 외쳤다.\n\n\"틀림없이 무죄로 방면할 겁니다.\" 단호한 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"그를 방면하지 않는다면 그건 부끄럽고 망신스러운 일이지요!\" 관리가 소리쳤다. \"설령 그가 정말로 죽였다 칩시다—그래도 아비에도 여러 아비가 있는 법 아닙니까! 게다가 그는 그토록 광란에 사로잡혀 있었으니… 사실은 그저 절굿공이를 허공에 휘둘렀을 뿐인데 그 바람에 노인을 쓰러뜨렸을지도 모르는 일입니다. 다만 그 하인 놈을 끌어들인 건 안타까운 일이었어요. 그건 그저 터무니없는 가설이었지요! 내가 페튜코비치 처지였다면, 나는 그냥 대놓고 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '그가 그를 죽였소. 하지만 그는 죄가 없소, 빌어먹을!'\"\n\n\"그가 한 게 바로 그겁니다, 다만 '빌어먹을!'이라는 말만 안 붙였을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 미하일 세묜오비치, 그는 그 말까지도 거의 한 거나 다름없었습니다.\" 세 번째 목소리가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"이보십시오, 여러분, 사순절에 우리 고장에서 어떤 여배우가 무죄로 방면된 적이 있는데, 그 여자가 제 정부의 정식 마누라 목을 그어 놓았었지요.\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 그 여자는 끝까지 긋지는 못했잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 차이가 있습니까. 어쨌든 긋기 시작은 한 거지요.\"\n\n\"그가 자식들에 대해 한 말은 어떻게 보셨습니까? 일품이었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"일품이었습니다!\"\n\n\"신비주의에 대한 것도 그렇고요!\"\n\n\"오, 신비주의 얘기는 그만 좀 합시다!\" 또 다른 누군가가 외쳤다. \"오늘부터 이폴리트와 그의 신세나 한번 생각해 보십시오. 내일이면 그의 아내가 미탸 때문에 그의 두 눈을 후벼 팔 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 부인이 여기 와 있습니까?\"\n\"무슨 그런 생각을! 여기 와 있었더라면 법정에서 진작 후벼 팠을 겁니다. 그 부인은 치통이 나서 집에 있답니다. 헤헤헤!\"\n\n\"헤헤헤!\"\n\n또 다른 무리에서는 이런 말이 오갔다.\n\n\"아무래도 미텐카는 결국 방면될 것 같소.\"\n\n\"내일이면 그가 '메트로폴리스'를 발칵 뒤집어 놓는다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠소. 열흘은 줄곧 퍼마실 테니!\"\n\n\"오, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈이 끼어들지 않을 리 없지. 여기가 아니면 그놈이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n\"이보시오, 여러분, 나도 그게 웅변이었다는 건 인정하오. 하지만 그래도 아비의 머리통을 절굿공이로 깨부순다는 건 될 법한 일이 아니잖소! 안 그러면 우리가 대체 어찌 되겠소?\"\n\n\"전차 말이오! 그 전차 기억하시오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그가 짐수레를 전차로 둔갑시켰지요!\"\n\n\"그러고는 내일이면 제 속셈에 맞춰 전차를 다시 짐수레로 둔갑시킬 거요.\"\n\n\"요즘 세상엔 어찌 그리 약삭빠른 작자들이 많은지! 러시아에 정의라는 게 남아 있기는 한 거요?\"\n\n그러나 종이 울렸다. 배심원들은 더도 덜도 없이 꼭 한 시간 동안 숙의했다. 방청객들이 자리에 앉자마자 법정에는 깊은 정적이 깔렸다. 나는 배심원들이 법정으로 걸어 들어오던 모습을 기억한다. 드디어! 나는 질문들을 차례대로 되풀이하지는 않겠으며, 실은 그것들을 잊어버리고 말았다. 다만 재판장의 첫 번째이자 가장 중요한 질문에 대한 답만은 기억한다. \"피고인은 강도질을 목적으로 또 미리 계획하여 살인을 저질렀는가?\"(정확한 문구는 기억나지 않는다.) 완전한 정적이 감돌았다. 배심원 대표인, 서기들 가운데 가장 젊은 사내가, 죽음 같은 법정의 적막 속에서 또렷하고 우렁찬 목소리로 선고했다.\n\n\"그렇다, 유죄!\"\n\n그리고 모든 질문에 똑같은 답이 되풀이되었다. \"그렇다, 유죄!\" 그것도 정상참작에 대한 일언반구의 언급도 없이. 이것은 아무도 예상하지 못한 일이었으니, 거의 모든 이가 적어도 자비를 권하는 단서 정도는 붙으리라 헤아리고 있었던 것이다. 죽음 같은 법정의 적막은 깨지지 않았다—그를 유죄로 만들고 싶어 했던 자들이나 그의 방면을 간절히 바랐던 자들이나 다 함께 돌처럼 굳어 버린 듯했다. 그러나 그것은 다만 첫 한순간뿐이었고, 곧이어 무시무시한 소란이 잇따랐다. 방청객 가운데 남자들 다수는 흐뭇해했다. 어떤 이들은 기쁨을 숨기려는 기색도 없이 두 손을 비벼 댔다. 평결에 동의하지 않는 자들은 짓눌린 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 수군거렸으나, 그래도 여전히 이 사태를 실감하지 못하는 듯했다. 그러나 부인들이 처한 상태를 내가 어찌 묘사할 수 있겠는가? 나는 그녀들이 폭동이라도 일으킬 줄 알았다. 처음에 그녀들은 제 귀를 좀처럼 믿지 못했다. 그러다 별안간 온 법정이 외침으로 울려 퍼졌다. \"이게 무슨 영문이야? 다음엔 또 뭐람?\" 그녀들은 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어났다. 그녀들은 이 평결이 당장이라도 재고되어 뒤집힐 수 있다고 공상하는 듯했다. 바로 그 순간 미탸가 별안간 일어서더니 두 손을 앞으로 내뻗으며 가슴을 찢는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"하느님과 저 무서운 심판의 날에 두고 맹세하노니, 저는 제 아비의 피에 대해 죄가 없습니다! 카탸, 나는 당신을 용서하오! 형제들이여, 벗들이여, 저 다른 여인을 가엾이 여겨 주오!\"\n\n그는 더 말을 잇지 못한 채, 제 것이라고는 믿기지 않는 기이하고 부자연스러운 목소리로 온 법정에 울려 퍼지는 끔찍한 통곡을 터뜨렸다. 방청석 맨 뒤 가장 먼 구석에서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나왔다—그루셴카였다. 그녀는 변호인들의 연설이 시작되기 전에 다시금 간청하여 법정에 들어오는 데 성공했던 것이다. 미탸는 끌려 나갔다. 선고는 이튿날로 미루어졌다. 온 법정이 소란에 휩싸였으나 나는 더 들으려 머물지 않았다. 다만 밖으로 나가며 계단에서 들은 몇 마디 외침만이 기억에 남아 있다.\n\"저 친구, 광산에서 이십 년짜리 여행을 하게 생겼군!\"\n\n\"그보다 덜하지는 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"흠, 우리 농민들이 단단히 버텼군.\"\n\n\"그래서 우리 미탸를 끝장내 버렸지.\"\n\n# Chapter I. Plans For Mitya’s Escape\n\n재판이 끝난 지 닷새째 되던 날 아침 아주 이른 시각, 아홉 시에 알료샤는 두 사람 모두에게 더없이 중대한 어떤 문제를 의논하고 또 전할 말을 건네고자 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 찾았다. 그녀는 한때 그루셴카를 맞이했던 바로 그 방에 앉아 그와 이야기를 나누었다. 옆방에는 이반 표도로비치가 고열에 시달리며 의식을 잃은 채 누워 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 법정에서 그 장면이 벌어지자마자, 피할 수 없는 뒷공론과 세간의 노골적인 비난 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고 병들어 의식을 잃은 그를 자기 집으로 옮기게 했다. 그녀와 함께 살던 두 친척 가운데 한 사람은 법정에서 그 장면이 벌어진 직후 모스크바로 떠났고, 다른 한 사람은 남았다. 그러나 설령 둘 다 떠나 버렸다 해도 카테리나 이바노브나는 자신의 결심을 굽히지 않고 밤낮없이 그의 곁에 앉아 병자를 계속 간호했을 것이었다. 바르빈스키와 게르첸슈투베가 그를 진찰하고 있었다. 그 유명한 의사는 병의 결말이 어찌 될지에 대한 견해 밝히기를 거부한 채 모스크바로 돌아가 버렸다. 의사들이 카테리나 이바노브나와 알료샤를 안심시키려 했으나, 그들이 아직 회복에 대한 확실한 희망을 줄 수는 없다는 것이 분명했다.\n\n알료샤는 하루에 두 번 앓아누운 형을 보러 왔다. 그러나 이번에는 특별히 다급한 용무가 있어서, 이 화제를 꺼내기가 얼마나 어려울지 미리 헤아리면서도 마음만은 몹시 바빴다. 그날 아침에는 미룰 수 없는 또 다른 약속이 있어 서둘러야만 했던 것이다.\n\n두 사람은 십오 분째 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 창백하고 끔찍하게 지쳐 보였으나, 동시에 히스테릭한 흥분에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그녀는 알료샤가 무엇 때문에 자기를 찾아왔는지 어렴풋이 짐작하고 있었다.\n\n\"그이의 결심은 걱정하지 마세요.\" 그녀는 확신에 찬 어조로 알료샤에게 못 박듯 말했다. \"이러나저러나 그이는 결국 그 결심에 이를 수밖에 없어요. 그이는 달아나야 해요. 그 불행한 사람, 명예와 신념의 영웅인 그 사람이—드미트리 표도로비치가 아니라, 저 문 너머에 누워 있는 사람, 형을 위해 제 몸을 희생한 그 사람 말이에요—\" 카탸는 눈을 번쩍이며 덧붙였다. \"오래전에 제게 탈출 계획을 송두리째 다 들려주었답니다. 아시겠지만 그이는 이미 교섭에 들어갔어요…. 제가 이미 얼마쯤은 말씀드렸지요…. 보세요, 그건 아마 죄수 일행이 시베리아로 이송되는 길에 여기서 세 번째 역참에 이르렀을 때 이뤄질 거예요. 아, 아직은 한참 먼 일이지요. 이반 표도로비치는 벌써 세 번째 역참의 책임자를 찾아갔어요. 하지만 누가 그 일행을 인솔하게 될지는 아직 모르고, 그렇게 한참 전부터 그걸 알아내기란 불가능해요. 내일쯤이면 이반 표도로비치가 재판 전날 만일을 대비해 제게 남겨 둔 계획 전체를 자세히 보여 드릴 수 있을 거예요…. 그때가 언제냐 하면—기억하세요?—당신이 우리가 다투는 걸 보았던 그때예요. 그이는 막 아래층으로 내려간 참이었지만, 당신을 보고서 제가 그이를 다시 불러올렸지요. 기억하세요? 그때 우리가 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 아세요?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 모릅니다.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그이는 당신께 말하지 않았겠지요. 바로 그 탈출 계획 때문이었어요. 그이는 사흘 전에 제게 핵심을 들려주었고, 우리는 곧장 그 일로 다투기 시작해 사흘을 내리 다퉜답니다. 우리가 다툰 까닭은, 그이가 만약 드미트리 표도로비치가 유죄 판결을 받으면 그 계집과 함께 외국으로 달아날 거라고 말하자, 제가 대번에 격분했기 때문이에요—왜 그랬는지 말씀드릴 수도 없고, 저 자신도 왜 그랬는지 몰라요…. 아, 물론 그때 저는 그 계집 때문에 분노했어요, 그 여자까지 드미트리와 함께 외국으로 가다니!\" 카테리나 이바노브나는 갑자기 소리쳤고, 입술이 분노로 파르르 떨렸다. \"이반 표도로비치는 제가 그 여자 때문에 격분하는 걸 보자마자, 곧장 제가 드미트리를 질투하는 거라고, 제가 아직도 드미트리를 사랑하는 거라고 단정해 버렸어요. 우리의 첫 다툼은 그렇게 시작됐답니다. 저는 해명하려 들지 않았고, 용서를 빌 수도 없었어요. 그런 사람이 제가 아직도 그…를 사랑한다고 의심하다니, 차마 견딜 수가 없었지요. 게다가 저는 이미 오래전에 그이에게, 드미트리는 사랑하지 않으며 오직 그이만을 사랑한다고 말했는데도 말이에요! 그 계집을 향한 분함만이 그이에게 화를 내게 한 거였어요. 사흘 뒤, 당신이 찾아온 그날 저녁에 그이는 봉인된 봉투 하나를 가져다주며, 자기에게 무슨 일이 생기면 곧장 열어 보라고 했어요. 아, 그이는 자기 병을 예감했던 거예요! 그 봉투 안에는 탈출의 세부 사항이 들어 있으며, 만약 자기가 죽거나 위중한 병에 걸리면 저 혼자서라도 미탸를 구해 내야 한다고 했어요. 그러고는 제게 돈을 남겼지요, 거의 만 루블이나 되는—검사가 논고에서 언급한 그 지폐들 말이에요, 누군가에게서 그이가 환전하려고 보냈다는 걸 알아낸 거지요. 이반 표도로비치가 형을 구하려는 생각을 포기하지 않았으며, 여전히 저를 질투하고 제가 미탸를 사랑한다고 확신하면서도 이 탈출 계획을 제게 맡겼다는 사실에 저는 엄청난 감동을 받았어요. 아, 그건 희생이었어요! 아니에요, 당신은 그런 자기희생의 위대함을 이해하지 못해요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저는 경외심에 차 그이의 발치에 엎드리고 싶었지만, 곧 그이가 그걸 그저 미탸가 구원받는다는 생각에 제가 기뻐하는 것으로만 받아들이리라는 생각이 들었어요(그이는 분명 그렇게 상상했을 거예요!). 그이가 그렇게 부당한 생각을 품을 가능성이 있다는 것만으로도 어찌나 화가 나던지, 저는 또 이성을 잃고, 그이의 발에 입맞추는 대신 다시 격노에 휩싸이고 말았어요! 아, 저는 불행한 여자예요! 이게 제 성격이에요, 제 끔찍하고 불행한 성격이라고요! 아, 두고 보세요, 저는 결국 그이마저 드미트리처럼 저보다 더 잘 맞는 다른 여자에게로 떠나가게 만들 거예요. 하지만… 아니에요, 저는 그걸 견딜 수 없어요, 차라리 목숨을 끊고 말 거예요. 그때 당신이 들어오고, 제가 당신을 불러 세우며 그이더러 돌아오라고 했을 때, 그이가 제게 던진 그 경멸과 증오의 눈빛에 어찌나 격분했던지—기억하세요?—저는 당신을 향해 소리쳤지요, 그이가, 오직 그이만이 자기 형 드미트리가 살인자라고 저를 설득했다고 말이에요! 저는 그이를 다시 한번 상처 입히려고 일부러 그 악의에 찬 말을 했어요. 그이는 결코, 결코 자기 형이 살인자라고 저를 설득한 적이 없어요. 오히려 그이를 설득한 건 저였어요! 아, 제 더러운 성미가 모든 것의 원인이었어요! 제가 그 재판정의 끔찍한 장면으로 가는 길을 닦아 놓은 거예요. 그이는 제게 자기가 명예로운 사람이라는 걸, 설령 제가 자기 형을 사랑한다 해도 복수나 질투 때문에 형을 파멸시키지는 않으리라는 걸 보여 주고 싶었던 거예요. 그래서 법정에 나간 거고요…. 모든 게 제 탓이에요, 오직 저 하나만이 죄인이에요!\"\n\n카탸가 알료샤에게 이런 고백을 한 적은 전에 없었으니, 그는 그녀가 이제 가장 오만한 마음조차 제 오만을 고통스레 짓밟으며 슬픔에 굴복해 쓰러지는, 그 견딜 수 없는 괴로움의 단계에 이르렀음을 느꼈다. 아, 알료샤는 지금 그녀가 겪는 비참함의 또 다른 무서운 까닭을 알고 있었으나, 그녀는 재판이 끝난 뒤로 그 며칠 동안 그것을 그에게서 조심스레 감추어 왔다. 다만 그녀가 그토록 비참한 지경으로 떨어져 지금 그 일을 그에게 입에 올린다면, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그에게도 너무나 고통스러운 일이 되었으리라. 그녀는 법정에서 저지른 자신의 \"배신\" 때문에 괴로워하고 있었고, 알료샤는 그녀의 양심이 그것을 그에게, 바로 자기 알료샤에게 눈물과 절규와 바닥에 몸부림치는 히스테리로 고백하라고 떠밀고 있음을 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 그 순간이 두려웠고 그녀를 그것으로부터 지켜 주고 싶었다. 그 때문에 그가 떠맡고 온 임무는 더더욱 어려워졌다. 그는 다시 미탸에 대한 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"괜찮아요, 괜찮아요, 그이 걱정은 마세요!\" 그녀가 날카롭고 고집스럽게 다시 입을 열었다. \"그 모든 건 한순간일 뿐이에요, 저는 그이를 알아요, 그이의 마음을 너무도 잘 안다고요. 분명히 말하건대 그이는 탈출에 동의할 거예요. 당장이라는 게 아니에요. 그이에겐 마음을 정할 시간이 있을 테니까요. 그때쯤이면 이반 표도로비치가 건강을 되찾아 모든 걸 직접 처리할 테니, 저는 거기에 관여할 일이 없을 거예요. 걱정 마세요, 그이는 달아나는 데 동의할 거예요. 이미 동의했어요. 그이가 그 계집을 포기하겠어요? 그리고 그들은 그 여자가 그이에게 가도록 내버려 두지 않을 테니, 그이는 달아날 수밖에 없어요. 그이가 가장 두려워하는 건 바로 당신이에요, 당신이 도덕적인 이유로 자기 탈출을 인정하지 않을까 봐 무서워하는 거지요. 하지만 당신의 승인이 그렇게나 꼭 필요하다면, 당신은 너그럽게 그걸 허락해 주셔야 해요.\" 카탸가 독기 어린 어조로 덧붙였다. 그녀는 말을 멈추고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그이는 무슨 찬가가 어쩌고 떠들어요.\" 그녀가 다시 말을 이었다. \"자기가 짊어져야 할 무슨 십자가가 어쩌고, 무슨 의무가 어쩌고 하면서요. 이반 표도로비치가 그 일에 대해 제게 많은 이야기를 들려주었던 게 기억나요, 그이가 어떻게 이야기했는지 당신이 아셨다면!\" 카탸가 갑자기, 억누를 수 없는 감정에 차 소리쳤다. \"그이가 제게 그 이야기를 들려주던 그 순간 그 가엾은 사람을 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그러면서도 어쩌면 그 똑같은 순간에 그를 얼마나 미워했는지 당신이 아셨다면 말이에요. 그런데 저는 그이의 이야기와 눈물을 비웃는 듯한 멸시로 들었어요. 짐승 같으니! 그래요, 저는 짐승이에요. 그이의 열병은 제 탓이에요. 하지만 감옥에 있는 저 사람은 괴로워할 줄을 몰라요.\" 카탸가 짜증스레 결론지었다. \"그런 사람이 괴로워할 수 있겠어요? 그이 같은 사람들은 절대로 괴로워하지 않아요!\"\n\n그녀의 말에는 증오와 경멸 어린 혐오의 가락이 서려 있었다. 그러면서도 정작 그를 배신한 것은 그녀였다. '어쩌면 자기가 그이에게 얼마나 잘못했는지 느끼기 때문에 순간순간 그이를 미워하는 거겠지.' 알료샤는 속으로 생각했다. 그는 그것이 단지 '순간순간'에 지나지 않기를 바랐다. 카탸의 마지막 말에서 그는 도전하는 듯한 가락을 알아챘으나, 거기에 응하지는 않았다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 당신을 부른 건, 당신이 직접 그이를 설득하겠다고 약속하게 하려는 거예요. 아니면 당신도, 탈출이 명예롭지 못한 일, 비겁한 일, 아니면 무슨… 어쩌면 기독교도답지 못한 일이라고 여기시나요?\" 카탸가 한층 더 도발적으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요. 그분께 다 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"그분은 당신이 오늘 자기를 보러 와 주기를 청하고 있어요.\" 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 불쑥 내뱉었다. 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 소파에서 그에게서 조금 물러났다.\n\n\"저더러요? 그럴 수가 있나요?\" 그녀가 창백해지며 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"그럴 수 있고, 또 그러셔야 해요!\" 알료샤가 점점 활기를 띠며 힘주어 말하기 시작했다. \"그분은 지금 특별히 당신을 필요로 하고 있어요. 꼭 필요한 일이 아니었다면 저도 이 화제를 꺼내 당신을 괴롭히지 않았을 거예요. 그분은 병들었고, 제정신이 아니에요, 줄곧 당신을 찾고 있어요. 당신과 화해하려고 당신을 원하는 게 아니라, 다만 당신이 가서 그 문간에 모습을 보여 주기를 바라는 거예요. 그날 이후로 그분에게 많은 일이 일어났어요. 그분은 자기가 당신에게 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 깊은 상처를 입혔다는 걸 깨닫고 있어요. 그분은 당신의 용서를 구하지 않아요—'나를 용서하기란 불가능하다'고 스스로 말하면서요—다만 당신이 그 문간에 모습을 보여 주기만을 바랄 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"너무 갑작스러워요….\" 카탸가 더듬거렸다. \"그동안 줄곧, 당신이 그 전갈을 가지고 오리라는 예감이 들었어요. 그이가 제게 와 달라고 청하리라는 걸 알았지요. 그건 불가능해요!\"\n\n\"불가능하더라도, 그렇게 해 주세요. 생각해 보세요, 그분은 평생 처음으로 자기가 당신에게 얼마나 깊은 상처를 입혔는지 깨닫고 있어요, 난생처음으로요. 전에는 그렇게 온전히 헤아린 적이 없었던 거예요. 그분은 이렇게 말했어요, '만약 그녀가 오기를 거부한다면 나는 평생토록 불행할 것이다'라고요. 들으셨어요? 이십 년의 징역형을 선고받았으면서도 그분은 여전히 행복해지려고 마음먹고 있어요—이거야말로 가엾지 않나요? 생각해 보세요—당신은 그분을 찾아가야 해요. 그분은 파멸했지만, 죄가 없어요.\" 알료샤의 입에서 도전하듯 말이 터져 나왔다. \"그분의 손은 깨끗해요, 거기엔 피가 묻어 있지 않아요! 앞으로 그분이 겪을 그 끝없는 고통을 위해서라도 지금 그분을 찾아가 주세요. 가셔서, 어둠 속으로 향하는 그분의 길에 인사를 건네 주세요—그 문간에 서 주세요, 그게 다예요…. 당신은 그렇게 하셔야 해요, 그렇게 하셔야 한다고요!\" 알료샤는 \"하셔야\"라는 말에 엄청난 무게를 실으며 끝맺었다.\n\n\"저는 그렇게 해야 하죠… 하지만 할 수가 없어요….\" 카탸가 신음했다. \"그이가 저를 바라볼 거예요…. 저는 그럴 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"두 분의 눈이 마주쳐야 해요. 지금 마음을 정하지 못한다면, 당신은 평생을 어떻게 살아가시겠어요?\"\n\n\"차라리 평생을 괴로워하는 편이 나아요.\"\n\n\"가셔야 해요, 가셔야 해요.\" 알료샤가 가차 없이 힘주어 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 왜 하필 오늘이죠, 왜 당장이어야 하죠?… 저는 우리 환자를 두고 떠날 수 없어요—\"\n\n\"잠깐이면 됩니다. 그저 한순간일 뿐이에요. 당신이 오지 않으면, 그분은 오늘 밤 헛소리에 빠질 거예요. 거짓말을 하는 게 아니에요. 부디 그분을 가엾이 여겨 주세요!\"\n\n\"저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요!\" 카탸가 쓰라린 원망을 담아 말하고는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"그럼 와 주시겠지요.\" 알료샤가 그녀의 눈물을 보며 단호히 말했다. \"제가 가서 당신이 곧 오실 거라고 그분께 전하겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 무슨 일이 있어도 그이에게 그렇게 말하진 마세요.\" 카탸가 놀라 소리쳤다. \"갈게요, 하지만 미리 그이에게 말하진 마세요. 어쩌면 제가 가더라도, 안으로 들어가지 못할지도 모르니까요…. 저도 아직 모르겠어요—\"\n\n그녀의 목소리가 끊겼다. 그녀는 숨을 헐떡였다. 알료샤가 가려고 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"그런데 만약 누군가와 마주치면 어쩌죠?\" 그녀가 다시 새파랗게 질리며 갑자기 나직한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그래서 지금 가셔야 하는 거예요, 누구와도 마주치지 않으려면요. 거기엔 아무도 없을 거예요, 그건 제가 확실히 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 저희가 기다리겠습니다.\" 그는 힘주어 끝맺고는 방에서 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter II. For A Moment The Lie Becomes Truth\n\n그는 미탸가 누워 있는 병원으로 서둘러 갔다. 운명이 결정된 이튿날 미탸는 신경성 열병에 걸려 시립병원의 죄수 병동으로 보내졌다. 그러나 몇몇 사람들(알료샤, 호흘라코바 부인, 리즈 등)의 청에 따라 바르빈스키 의사는 미탸를 다른 죄수들과 함께 두지 않고 따로 떼어 둔 작은 방, 곧 한때 스메르댜코프가 있던 그 방에 들였다. 사실 복도 저쪽 끝에는 보초가 서 있었고 창문에는 쇠창살이 쳐져 있었으니, 바르빈스키로서는 자신이 베푼 호의가, 엄밀히 말하면 그다지 합법적이지 않은 그 호의에 대해 마음을 놓을 수 있었던 것이다. 어쨌든 그는 마음씨 곱고 동정심 많은 젊은이였다. 그는 미탸 같은 사람이 그토록 느닷없이 한꺼번에 강도와 살인자들 무리 속으로 들어가는 것이 얼마나 견디기 힘든 일인지를 알았고, 그가 차츰차츰 거기에 익숙해져야 한다는 것도 알고 있었다. 친척과 친구들의 면회는 의사와 감독관은 물론, 경찰서장에게서까지 비공식적으로 허락을 받았다. 그러나 미탸를 찾아온 사람은 알료샤와 그루셴카뿐이었다. 라키틴이 두 번이나 억지로 비집고 들어오려 했으나, 미탸는 한사코 바르빈스키에게 그를 들이지 말라고 사정했다.\n\n알료샤가 들어서니 미탸는 병원 가운을 걸친 채 침대에 앉아 있었는데, 다소 열에 들떠 머리에는 식초와 물에 적신 수건을 얹고 있었다. 그는 들어오는 알료샤를 알 수 없는 표정으로 바라보았으나, 그 표정에는 두려움 비슷한 그늘이 어른거렸다. 그는 재판 이후로 끔찍하게 골똘해져서, 어떤 때는 반 시간이나 입을 다문 채 주위의 모든 것을 잊고 무언가를 무겁고 괴롭게 곱씹는 듯했다. 깊은 생각에서 깨어나 말을 시작하면, 그는 언제나 어딘가 퉁명스러운 투로, 정작 하고 싶은 말은 결코 하지 않았다. 이따금 그는 고통에 찬 얼굴로 동생을 바라보았다. 그는 알료샤보다 그루셴카와 함께 있을 때 더 마음이 편해 보였다. 사실 그는 그루셴카와는 거의 말을 나누지 않았지만, 그녀가 들어서기만 하면 그의 온 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환해지는 것이었다.\n\n알료샤는 말없이 그의 곁 침대에 걸터앉았다. 이번에 미탸는 마음을 졸이며 알료샤를 기다리고 있었으나, 차마 그에게 무엇도 물어보지 못했다. 카탸가 와 주기로 마음먹는다는 것은 거의 생각조차 할 수 없는 일로 여겨졌고, 그러면서도 동시에 그녀가 오지 않는다면 무언가 상상도 못 할 일이 벌어지리라는 느낌이 들었다. 알료샤는 그의 심정을 헤아렸다.\n\n\"트리폰 보리소비치가 말이야,\" 미탸가 초조하게 입을 열었다, \"제 여인숙을 죄다 부숴 놓았다는 거야, 그렇게 들었어. 바닥을 뜯어내고 널빤지를 떼어 내고 회랑이란 회랑은 다 쪼개 놓았다는 거지. 보물을 줄곧 찾고 있는 거야—검사가 내가 거기 숨겨 놓았다고 한 천오백 루블 말이야. 집에 돌아오자마자 그런 짓을 시작했다는군. 사기꾼 놈, 쌤통이지! 여기 간수가 어제 일러 줬어, 그자가 거기 출신이거든.\"\n\n\"들어 봐,\" 알료샤가 입을 열었다. \"그분은 올 거야. 다만 언제일지는 모르겠어. 어쩌면 오늘일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 며칠 후일 수도 있고, 그건 알 수가 없어. 하지만 올 거야, 틀림없이 와.\"\n\n미탸는 흠칫하더니 무언가 말하려다가 입을 다물었다. 그 소식은 그에게 엄청난 충격을 주었다. 그녀가 무슨 말을 했는지 끔찍이도 알고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했으나, 그는 또다시 차마 묻지 못했다. 카탸에게서 나온 잔인하고 경멸 어린 말 한마디라도 있었다면 그 순간 그것은 칼처럼 그를 베었을 것이다.\n\n\"그분이 다른 여러 가지 가운데서도 이런 말을 했어. 탈주에 관해서 형의 양심을 반드시 편안하게 해 주어야 한다고. 그때까지 이반 형이 낫지 않으면 자기가 모든 걸 손수 처리하겠다고.\"\n\n\"그건 벌써 했던 얘기잖아,\" 미탸가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 형은 그걸 그루샤한테 되풀이했지,\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 미탸가 인정했다. \"그 사람은 오늘 아침엔 안 와.\" 그는 동생을 조심스레 바라보았다. \"저녁때나 돼야 올 거야. 어제 내가 카탸가 조처를 취하고 있다고 말했더니, 그 사람은 잠자코 있다가 입을 꾹 다물더라고. 그저 '그러라죠!' 하고 속삭일 뿐이었어. 그게 중요한 일이라는 걸 알아챈 거지. 난 더는 그 사람을 떠볼 엄두가 안 났어. 이제 그 사람은 카탸가 더 이상 날 마음에 두지 않고 이반 형을 사랑한다는 걸 알아챈 것 같아.\"\n\n\"정말 그래?\" 알료샤가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"아닐지도 모르지. 다만 그 사람이 오늘 아침엔 안 온다는 거야,\" 미탸가 또다시 서둘러 변명하듯 말했다. \"그 사람한테 내가 부탁을 좀 했거든. 알다시피 이반 형은 우리 모두보다 뛰어난 사람이야. 살아야 할 건 우리가 아니라 그 형이지. 그 형은 회복될 거야.\"\n\n\"믿어지겠어, 카탸가 그 형 때문에 그렇게 걱정하면서도 회복을 거의 의심하지 않는단 말이지,\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 그 사람이 그 형이 죽을 거라고 확신하고 있다는 뜻이야. 두려워서 그렇게 나으리라고 단단히 믿는 거라고.\"\n\n\"이반 형은 체질이 튼튼하니까, 나도 회복될 가망이 충분하다고 믿어,\" 알료샤가 근심스레 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그 형은 나을 거야. 하지만 그 사람은 그 형이 죽을 거라고 확신해. 짊어져야 할 슬픔이 그 사람에겐 많아……\" 침묵이 흘렀다. 무거운 불안이 미탸를 좀먹고 있었다.\n\n\"알료샤, 난 그루샤를 끔찍이 사랑해,\" 그가 별안간 눈물이 가득한, 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람들은 그 여자가 거기 형한테 따라가는 걸 허락하지 않을 거야,\" 알료샤가 곧바로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그리고 또 한 가지 너한테 하고 싶은 말이 있어,\" 미탸가 별안간 목소리에 울림을 띠며 말을 이었다. \"가는 길에서든 거기서든 놈들이 날 때린다면, 난 가만있지 않을 거야. 누군가를 죽일 거고, 그 때문에 총살당하겠지. 그런데 그게 이십 년 동안이나 이어질 거란 말이지! 지금도 놈들은 나한테 함부로 말을 해. 밤새도록 여기 누워서 나 자신에게 판결을 내려 봤어. 난 준비가 안 됐어! 난 스스로 받아들일 수가 없어. '찬가'를 부르고 싶었지만, 간수 하나가 나한테 함부로 말을 걸면 난 그걸 견뎌 낼 힘이 없어. 그루샤를 위해서라면 무엇이든 견디겠어…… 매질만 빼고는 무엇이든…… 하지만 그 여잔 거기엔 따라오지 못할 거야.\"\n\n알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"들어 봐, 형, 단번에 다 말할게,\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"이게 그 일에 대한 내 생각이야. 그리고 형도 알지, 내가 형한테 거짓말은 하지 않으리라는 걸. 들어 봐. 형은 준비가 안 됐고, 그런 십자가는 형을 위한 게 아니야. 게다가 형은 준비도 안 된 그런 순교자의 십자가가 필요하지도 않아. 만일 형이 우리 아버지를 죽였다면, 형이 자기 형벌을 거부하는 게 나로선 가슴 아팠을 거야. 하지만 형은 죄가 없고, 그런 십자가는 형에게 너무 과해. 형은 고통을 통해 자신을 다른 사람으로 만들려고 했지. 내 말은, 그저 그 다른 사람을 늘, 평생토록, 어디를 가든 잊지 말라는 거야. 그것만으로도 형에겐 충분할 거야. 형이 그 위대한 십자가를 거부한다는 것은, 오히려 평생토록 한층 더 큰 의무를 느끼게 하는 구실이 될 뿐이고, 그 끊임없는 느낌이 어쩌면 형이 거기로 가는 것보다 형을 새사람으로 만드는 데 더 큰 몫을 할 거야. 거기서라면 형은 견뎌 내지 못하고 원망하다가, 어쩌면 끝내는 '이젠 빚을 다 갚았다'고 말하게 될 테니까. 변호사가 그 점에 대해선 옳았어. 그렇게 무거운 짐은 모든 사람을 위한 게 아니야. 어떤 사람들에겐 그건 불가능한 일이지. 이게 그 일에 대한 내 생각이야, 형이 그렇게나 듣고 싶어 하니까. 만일 형의 탈주에 대해 다른 사람들이, 장교나 병사들이 책임져야 한다면, 난 형을 '허락하지' 않았을 거야,\" 알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"그런데 그 사람들 말로는—그 호송 중계소 책임자가 이반 형한테 직접 말했다는데—일만 잘 처리하면 별다른 조사도 없을 거고, 쉽게 빠져나갈 수 있다는 거야. 물론 이런 경우라도 뇌물을 쓰는 건 떳떳지 못한 일이지만, 난 그걸 두고 판단할 처지가 못 돼. 왜냐면 이반 형과 카탸가 나더러 형을 위해 나서 달라고 맡긴다면, 난 분명히 가서 뇌물을 쓸 테니까. 형한테 진실을 말해야겠어. 그러니 나로선 형 자신의 행동을 판단할 수가 없어. 다만 내가 형을 결코 비난하지 않으리라는 것만은 장담할게. 게다가 내가 이 일을 두고 형을 판단할 수 있다면 그건 이상한 노릇이지. 자, 이제 모든 걸 다 짚어 본 것 같아.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 나 자신을 비난해!\" 미탸가 외쳤다. \"난 탈주할 거야, 그건 너와는 별개로 이미 정해진 일이지. 미탸 카라마조프가 달아나는 것 말고 무슨 짓을 할 수 있겠어? 하지만 난 나 자신을 단죄할 거고, 내 죄를 영원히 기도로 빌 거야. 예수회 수사들이 꼭 이렇게 말하지 않나? 우리가 지금 하는 것처럼 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"난 네가 늘 온전한 진실만 말하고 아무것도 숨기지 않아서 널 사랑해,\" 미탸가 즐거운 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"그러니까 내가 우리 알료샤가 예수회 수사처럼 구는 걸 딱 잡은 거지. 그 일로 너한테 입을 맞춰야겠어. 자, 이제 나머지도 들어 봐. 내 마음의 다른 한쪽도 너한테 열어 보일게. 내가 계획하고 결정한 게 바로 이거야. 만일 내가 돈과 여권을 갖고, 더구나 아메리카로까지 달아난다 해도, 난 내가 즐거움을 위해서, 행복을 위해서 달아나는 게 아니라 또 다른 유배지로, 어쩌면 시베리아만큼이나 못한 곳으로 가는 거라는 생각에 위안을 얻을 거야. 그래, 거기도 똑같이 끔찍해, 알료샤, 정말 그래! 난 벌써 그 아메리카가, 빌어먹을, 싫어. 그루샤가 나와 함께 있다 해도 말이지. 그 여잘 좀 봐. 그 여자가 아메리카 사람이야? 그 여잔 러시아 사람이야, 뼛속까지 러시아 사람이라고. 모국을 그리워하며 향수에 젖을 거고, 난 그 여자가 날 위해 괴로워한다는 걸, 날 위해 그 십자가를 짊어졌다는 걸 시시각각 보게 되겠지. 게다가 그 여자가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀단 말이야? 그리고 나 또한 거기 천한 무리들을 어떻게 견뎌 내겠어, 비록 그놈들이 하나같이 나보다 나은 인간이라 해도? 난 벌써 그 아메리카가 싫어! 그놈들이 하나같이 기계에는 기막히게 능하다 해도, 빌어먹을, 그놈들은 내 영혼의 사람들이 아니야. 난 러시아를 사랑해, 알료샤, 난 러시아의 신을 사랑해, 비록 나 자신은 비열한 놈이지만. 거기선 난 숨이 막혀 죽을 거야!\" 그가 별안간 눈을 번득이며 외쳤다. 그의 목소리는 눈물로 떨리고 있었다. \"그래서 내가 이렇게 결정했어, 알료샤, 들어 봐,\" 그가 감정을 가다듬으며 다시 입을 열었다. \"그루샤랑 거기 도착하는 즉시 우리는 곧장 땅을 부쳐 일을 시작할 거야, 외따로, 아주 외진 어딘가에서, 사나운 곰들이나 있는 곳에서 말이야. 거기에도 분명 외진 구석이 있겠지. 듣자니 거기엔 아직도 어딘가, 지평선 끄트머리에 인디언들이 남아 있다는 거야. 그러니 '모히칸족의 최후'의 고장으로 가서, 거기서 그루샤랑 나랑 곧장 문법을 붙들고 늘어지는 거지. 노동과 문법—그렇게 삼 년을 보내는 거야. 그때쯤이면 우린 영국 사람처럼 영어를 할 줄 알게 될 거야. 그리고 그걸 다 익히는 즉시—아메리카여, 안녕! 우린 아메리카 시민이 되어 여기 러시아로 달려올 거야. 걱정 마—이 작은 읍내로는 안 올 테니까. 우린 어딘가, 멀리 떨어진 곳, 북쪽이든 남쪽이든 거기 숨어 살 거야. 그때쯤이면 난 변해 있을 거고, 그 여자도 아메리카에서 변할 거야. 의사들이 내 얼굴에 무슨 사마귀 같은 걸 만들어 주겠지—그놈들이 그렇게 기계에 능하다는 게 다 무슨 소용이겠어!—아니면 내 눈 하나를 멀게 하거나, 수염을 한 자나 기르거나, 러시아를 그리며 애태우다 머리가 세어 버리겠지. 아마 놈들은 우릴 못 알아볼 거야. 설령 알아본다 한들, 시베리아로 보내라지. 난 상관없어. 그게 우리 운명이라는 걸 보여 주는 거니까. 우린 여기서도 땅을 부치며, 어딘가 황량한 곳에서, 평생토록 아메리카인 행세를 하는 거지. 하지만 우린 우리 땅에서 죽을 거야. 그게 내 계획이고, 이건 바꾸지 않을 거야. 찬성하니?\"\n\n\"그래,\" 알료샤는 그의 말을 거스르고 싶지 않아 대답했다. 미탸는 잠시 입을 다물었다가 별안간 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 재판에서 놈들이 그걸 어떻게 꾸며 댔는지! 정말 어떻게 꾸며 댔는지 봤지!\"\n\n\"그렇게 안 했어도 형은 마찬가지로 유죄가 됐을 거야,\" 알료샤가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 여기 사람들은 내가 지긋지긋한 거야! 그들에게 신의 가호가 있기를, 하지만 괴롭구나,\" 미탸가 비참하게 신음했다. 다시 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"알료샤, 당장 날 이 괴로움에서 꺼내 줘!\" 그가 별안간 외쳤다. \"말해 줘, 그 사람이 지금 오는 거야, 아니야? 말해 줘. 그 사람이 뭐라고 했어? 어떻게 말했어?\"\n\n\"그분은 오겠다고 했어. 다만 오늘 올지는 모르겠어. 알다시피 그분으로선 힘든 일이니까,\" 알료샤가 동생을 조심스레 바라보았다.\n\n\"그 사람한테 힘든 일이고말고! 알료샤, 이러다 미쳐 버리겠어. 그루샤가 자꾸 날 쳐다봐. 그 여잔 다 알아채고 있어. 맙소사, 내 마음을 가라앉혀 줘. 내가 원하는 게 대체 뭐야? 난 카탸를 원해! 내가 뭘 원하는지 내가 알기나 하는 거야? 이건 그 고집스럽고 사악한 카라마조프의 기질이야! 아니, 난 고통을 감당할 위인이 못 돼. 난 비열한 놈이야, 그 한마디로 다야.\"\n\n\"저기 오신다!\" 알료샤가 외쳤다.\n\n바로 그 순간 카탸가 문간에 나타났다. 그녀는 잠시 멈춰 서서 멍한 표정으로 미탸를 바라보았다. 그는 충동적으로 벌떡 일어났고, 그의 얼굴에는 겁먹은 빛이 떠올랐다. 그는 새파랗게 질렸으나, 이내 그의 입가에는 소심하고 애원하는 듯한 미소가 떠올랐고, 그는 억누를 수 없는 충동에 두 손을 카탸에게 내밀었다. 그것을 본 그녀는 격정에 휩싸여 그에게 달려왔다. 그녀는 그의 두 손을 붙잡고는 거의 억지로 그를 침대에 앉혔다. 그녀는 그의 곁에 앉아, 여전히 그의 손을 잡은 채 세차게 움켜쥐었다. 두 사람은 여러 번 말을 하려 했으나 도중에 멈추고는, 다시 묘한 미소를 띤 채 말없이 서로에게 눈길을 박고 바라보았다. 그렇게 이 분이 흘렀다.\n\n\"날 용서했어?\" 미탸가 마침내 더듬더듬 말했고, 동시에 알료샤를 돌아보며, 기쁨으로 일그러진 얼굴로 외쳤다, \"내가 무얼 묻고 있는지 들리니, 들려?\"\n\n\"바로 그 점 때문에 내가 당신을 사랑했어요, 당신이 마음이 너그럽다는 점 때문에!\" 카탸의 입에서 말이 터져 나왔다. \"내 용서는 당신에게 아무 소용 없고, 당신 용서도 내게 아무 소용 없어요. 당신이 날 용서하든 안 하든, 당신은 언제까지나 내 마음속의 아픈 자리로 남을 것이고, 나 또한 당신 마음속에 그렇게 남을 거예요—그렇게 될 수밖에 없어요……\" 그녀는 숨을 고르려 말을 멈췄다. \"내가 무엇 때문에 왔겠어요?\" 그녀가 다시 초조하게 서두르며 입을 열었다. \"당신의 발을 끌어안고, 이렇게 당신의 손을 아플 때까지 꼭 쥐려고—모스크바에서 내가 어떻게 당신 손을 꼭 쥐곤 했는지 기억하죠—당신이 내 신이고, 내 기쁨이라고 다시 말하려고, 당신을 미치도록 사랑한다고 말하려고 왔어요,\" 그녀는 고뇌에 차서 신음하더니, 별안간 게걸스럽게 그의 손을 제 입술에 갖다 댔다. 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 알료샤는 말문이 막힌 채 어찌할 바를 몰라 서 있었다. 그는 자신이 보고 있는 광경을 결코 예상하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n\"사랑은 끝났어요, 미탸!\" 카탸가 다시 입을 열었다. \"하지만 지난날은 내게 가슴 저미도록 소중해요. 알아 두세요, 당신은 언제까지나 그렇게 남으리라는 걸. 하지만 이제, 그럴 수도 있었을 일이 단 일 분 동안만이라도 이루어지게 해 줘요,\" 그녀는 일그러진 미소를 띤 채 더듬거리며, 다시 기쁜 듯이 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"당신은 다른 여자를 사랑하고, 나는 다른 남자를 사랑하지만, 그래도 난 당신을 영원히 사랑할 것이고, 당신도 날 사랑할 거예요. 그걸 알겠어요? 들려요? 날 사랑해 줘요, 평생토록 날 사랑해 줘요!\" 그녀가 목소리에 거의 위협에 가까운 떨림을 띠며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신을 사랑할게…… 그런데 말이야, 카탸,\" 미탸가 한마디 한마디마다 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 입을 열었다, \"그런데 말이야, 닷새 전 그날 저녁에 난 당신을 사랑했어…… 당신이 쓰러져 실려 나갔을 때…… 평생토록! 그렇게 될 거야, 언제까지나 그렇게 될 거야—\"\n\n그렇게 두 사람은 광란에 찬, 거의 아무 뜻도 없는, 어쩌면 진실조차 아닌 말들을 서로에게 속삭였으나, 그 순간만큼은 그것이 모두 진실이었고, 두 사람 모두 자기들이 하는 말을 한 점 의심 없이 믿었다.\n\n\"카탸,\" 미탸가 별안간 외쳤다, \"당신은 내가 그분을 죽였다고 믿어? 지금은 안 믿는다는 걸 알아, 하지만 그때는…… 당신이 증언했을 때는…… 정말, 정말 안 믿었던 거야!\"\n\n\"그때조차 난 안 믿었어요. 단 한 번도 믿은 적 없어요. 난 당신이 미웠고, 그래서 한순간 나 자신을 그렇게 믿게끔 설득했던 거예요. 증언하는 동안 난 나 자신을 설득해서 그걸 믿었지만, 말을 끝마치자마자 곧바로 믿기를 그만뒀어요. 그건 의심하지 마세요! 난 내가 나 자신을 벌하려고 여기 왔다는 걸 잊었어요,\" 그녀가 조금 전의 사랑스럽던 어조와는 전혀 다른, 새로운 빛깔을 띤 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"여인이여, 당신이 지닌 짐은 무겁구려,\" 미탸의 입에서 마치 저도 모르게인 듯 말이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"보내 줘요,\" 그녀가 속삭였다. \"다시 올게요. 지금은 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요.\"\n\n그녀는 자리에서 일어서려다가, 별안간 날카로운 비명을 지르며 비틀비틀 물러섰다. 그루셴카가 느닷없이 소리도 없이 방 안으로 걸어 들어온 것이다. 아무도 그녀를 예상하지 못했다. 카탸가 잽싸게 문 쪽으로 움직였으나, 그루셴카에게 이르자 별안간 걸음을 멈추더니 백지장처럼 새하얗게 질려 거의 속삭이듯 나직이 신음했다.\n\n\"용서해 주세요!\"\n\n그루셴카는 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 앙심에 찬, 독기 어린 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"우린 서로 미움으로 가득 차 있어요, 아가씨, 당신이나 나나! 우리 둘 다 미움으로 가득 차 있다고요! 우리가 서로를 용서할 수나 있겠어요! 저이를 구해 주세요, 그러면 난 평생토록 당신을 떠받들겠어요.\"\n\n\"넌 저 사람을 용서하지 않으려는구나!\" 미탸가 미친 듯한 질책을 담아 외쳤다.\n\n\"걱정 마세요, 당신을 위해 저이를 구해 줄게요!\" 카탸가 빠르게 속삭이고는 방을 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"저 사람이 제 입으로 용서를 빌었는데도 넌 용서하기를 마다할 수가 있어?\" 미탸가 다시 쓰라리게 외쳤다.\n\n\"미탸, 감히 그 사람을 탓하지 마요. 형한텐 그럴 자격이 없어요!\" 알료샤가 격하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 여자의 오만한 입술이 한 말이지, 마음에서 우러난 게 아니에요,\" 그루셴카가 역겨운 듯한 어조로 내뱉었다. \"저 여자가 당신을 구해 준다면, 난 저 여자의 모든 걸 용서하겠어요—\"\n\n그녀는 무언가를 억누르는 듯 말을 멈췄다. 그녀는 아직 마음을 추스르지 못하고 있었다. 나중에 드러난 바로, 그녀는 우연히 들어온 것이었고 자기가 무엇을 마주치게 될지 전혀 짐작도 못 했던 것이다.\n\n\"알료샤, 저 사람을 쫓아가!\" 미탸가 동생에게 외쳤다. \"저 사람한테 전해 줘…… 뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어…… 저렇게 가 버리게 하지 마!\"\n\n\"날이 저물 무렵에 형한테 다시 올게,\" 알료샤는 이렇게 말하고는 카탸를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다. 그는 병원 구내를 벗어난 곳에서 그녀를 따라잡았다. 그녀는 빠르게 걷고 있었으나, 알료샤가 따라붙기 무섭게 빠르게 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 저 여자 앞에선 난 나 자신을 벌할 수가 없어요! 내가 저 여자에게 용서를 빈 건 끝까지, 쓰라리도록 나 자신을 벌하고 싶어서였어요. 저 여잔 날 용서하려 들지 않았어요…… 난 그 점이 마음에 들어요!\" 그녀가 부자연스러운 목소리로 덧붙였고, 그녀의 눈은 사나운 원한으로 번득였다.\n\n\"우리 형은 이런 걸 조금도 예상하지 못했어요,\" 알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"형은 그분이 오지 않으리라고 확신했거든요—\"\n\n\"그렇겠죠. 그 얘긴 그만둬요,\" 그녀가 쏘아붙였다. \"들어요. 지금은 당신과 함께 장례식에 갈 수가 없어요. 그 집에 꽃은 보냈어요. 그 사람들한테 아직 돈이 남아 있을 거예요. 필요하면, 내가 결코 그들을 저버리지 않겠다고 전해 줘요…… 이제 날 내버려 둬요, 제발 날 내버려 둬요. 당신은 이미 늦었어요—예배를 알리는 종이 울리고 있어요…… 날 내버려 둬요, 제발!\"\n\n# Chapter III. Ilusha’s Funeral. The Speech At The Stone\n\n그는 정말로 늦었다. 사람들은 그를 기다렸으나, 결국 그가 없이도 꽃으로 곱게 단장한 자그마한 관을 교회로 옮기기로 이미 결정한 참이었다. 그것은 가엾은 어린 일류샤의 관이었다. 그는 미탸의 판결이 내려진 지 이틀 뒤에 죽었다. 집 대문에서 알료샤를 맞이한 것은 소년들의, 곧 일류샤의 학교 친구들의 외침이었다. 그들은 모두 그를 애타게 기다리고 있던 터라 그가 마침내 와 준 것을 기뻐했다. 모두 열두 명쯤 되었고, 하나같이 책가방이나 배낭을 어깨에 메고 있었다. \"아버지가 우실 거야, 아버지 곁에 있어 줘.\" 일류샤가 죽어 가며 그들에게 그렇게 일렀고, 소년들은 그 말을 기억하고 있었다. 콜랴 크라소트킨이 그들 중 맨 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"네가 와 줘서 정말 기뻐, 카라마조프!\" 그가 알료샤에게 손을 내밀며 소리쳤다. \"여기 정말 끔찍해. 차마 보기 힘들 만큼 끔찍해. 스네기료프는 취하지 않았어, 오늘은 한 모금도 마시지 않은 게 분명한데, 그런데도 마치 취한 사람 같아…… 나는 늘 사내답게 굴려고 하지만, 이건 정말 끔찍해. 카라마조프, 붙드는 게 아니라면, 들어가기 전에 한 가지만 물어봐도 될까?\"\n\n\"뭔데, 콜랴?\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"네 형은 무죄야, 유죄야? 너희 아버지를 죽인 게 그 사람이야, 아니면 하인이었어? 네가 말하는 대로 나는 믿을 거야. 그 생각 때문에 나는 지난 나흘 밤을 한숨도 못 잤어.\"\n\n\"하인이 죽인 거야, 우리 형은 무죄야.\" 알료샤가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 그렇게 말했잖아!\" 스무로프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 형은 무죄한 희생자로 스러지는 거구나!\" 콜랴가 외쳤다. \"비록 신세를 망쳤어도 행복한 사람이야! 나는 그가 부럽기까지 해!\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어? 왜?\" 알료샤가 놀라 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아, 나도 언젠가 진실을 위해 내 한 몸을 바칠 수 있다면!\" 콜랴가 열을 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그런 명분으로는 안 돼, 그런 치욕과 그런 끔찍함을 안고서는 안 돼!\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지…… 나는 온 인류를 위해 죽고 싶어, 그리고 치욕으로 말하자면, 그런 건 상관없어—우리 이름이야 스러져도 좋아. 나는 네 형을 존경해!\"\n\n\"나도 그래!\" 한때 트로이를 누가 세웠는지 자기가 안다고 큰소리쳤던 소년이 갑작스럽고 뜻밖에 외치고는, 그때 그랬듯이 작약처럼 귀밑까지 새빨개졌다.\n\n알료샤는 방 안으로 들어갔다. 일류샤는 흰 주름 장식이 둘린 파란 관 속에 두 손을 모으고 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었다. 그 야윈 얼굴은 거의 변하지 않았고, 기이하게도 시신에서는 썩는 냄새가 나지 않았다. 그 얼굴의 표정은 엄숙했고, 말하자면 무언가를 골똘히 생각하는 듯했다. 가슴 위로 포개진 두 손은 유난히 아름다워 보였으니, 마치 대리석으로 깎아 놓은 것 같았다. 그 손에는 꽃이 들려 있었고, 관 안팎은 꽃으로 단장되어 있었는데, 그것은 아침 일찍 리즈 호흘라코바가 보내온 것이었다. 그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 보낸 꽃도 있어서, 알료샤가 문을 열었을 때 이등 대위는 떨리는 두 손에 한 다발을 쥐고 사랑하는 아들 위에 다시 흩뿌리고 있었다. 그는 알료샤가 들어왔을 때 그를 거의 쳐다보지도 않았고, 누구도 보려 하지 않았으니, 죽은 아들을 더 가까이서 보려고 절름거리는 다리로 끊임없이 일어서려 애쓰는 미친 듯 우는 아내, \"엄마\"조차 거들떠보지 않았다. 니나는 소년들이 의자째 밀어 관 가까이로 옮겨 놓았다. 그녀는 관에 머리를 댄 채 앉아 있었고, 그녀 역시 의심할 바 없이 소리 없이 울고 있었다. 스네기료프의 얼굴은 다급해 보였으나, 동시에 어리둥절하고 격앙되어 있었다. 그의 몸짓에도, 그의 입에서 터져 나오는 말에도 무언가 실성한 듯한 데가 있었다. \"꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야!\" 그는 일류샤를 바라보며 시시각각 외쳤다. 일류샤가 살아 있을 때 그를 \"꼬마\"라고 부르는 것이 애정의 표현으로서 그의 버릇이었다.\n\n\"여보, 나한테도 꽃 한 송이 줘요. 저 애 손에서 흰 꽃을 빼내서 나한테 줘요.\" 미친 어머니가 흐느끼며 졸랐다. 일류샤의 손에 들린 그 작은 흰 장미가 마음에 들었던 것인지, 아니면 그를 기리며 간직하려고 그의 손에서 나온 꽃을 갖고 싶었던 것인지, 그녀는 꽃을 향해 두 손을 뻗으며 안절부절못했다.\n\n\"누구한테도 안 줘, 당신한테는 아무것도 안 줘.\" 스네기료프가 매정하게 소리쳤다. \"이건 저 애 꽃이야, 당신 것이 아니라고! 전부 저 애 거야, 당신 건 아무것도 없어!\"\n\n\"아빠, 엄마한테 꽃 한 송이 줘요!\" 니나가 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 들며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 아무것도 안 내줘, 더구나 저 여자한테는 누구보다도 안 줘! 저 여자는 일류샤를 사랑하지 않았어. 저 여자가 저 애의 작은 대포를 빼앗아 갔는데, 저 애가 그걸 줬던 거지.\" 일류샤가 어머니에게 자기 대포를 내주었던 일을 떠올리며 이등 대위는 큰 소리로 흐느끼기 시작했다. 가엾고 실성한 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 없는 눈물로 흠뻑 젖어 있었다.\n\n소년들은 아버지가 관에서 떨어지려 하지 않는 것을 보고, 또 이제 관을 운구할 때가 되었음을 알고는, 관 둘레에 빽빽이 둘러서서 그것을 들어 올리기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 애를 묘지에 묻고 싶지 않아.\" 스네기료프가 갑자기 통곡했다. \"나는 그 돌 옆에, 우리 돌 옆에 묻을 거야! 일류샤가 나한테 그렇게 일렀어. 저 애를 못 내가게 하겠어!\"\n\n그는 지난 사흘 동안 아들을 그 돌 옆에 묻겠다고 말해 왔으나, 알료샤와 크라소트킨, 집주인 여자와 그 자매, 그리고 모든 소년들이 가로막았다.\n\n\"무슨 그런 생각을, 마치 목이라도 맨 사람처럼 부정한 돌 옆에 묻다니!\" 늙은 집주인 여자가 단호하게 말했다. \"저기 묘지에는 땅이 축성되어 있어요. 거기서라야 그 애를 위해 기도를 드릴 수 있지요. 교회에서 부르는 성가도 들리고, 부제가 어찌나 또렷하고 분명하게 읽는지, 마치 그 애 무덤 위에서 읽어 주는 것처럼 그때마다 그 애한테 가닿을 거예요.\"\n\n마침내 이등 대위는 \"마음대로 데려가라\"고 말하듯 절망적인 몸짓을 했다. 소년들이 관을 들어 올렸으나, 어머니 곁을 지날 때 잠시 멈춰 서서 관을 내려 그녀가 일류샤에게 작별을 고할 수 있게 했다. 그러나 지난 사흘 동안 멀찍이서만 바라보았던 그 소중한 작은 얼굴을 보자, 그녀는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 그 희끗희끗한 머리가 관 위에서 경련하듯 씰룩거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"엄마, 저 애한테 성호를 그어 줘요, 축복해 줘요, 입을 맞춰 줘요.\" 니나가 그녀에게 외쳤다. 그러나 그녀의 머리는 여전히 자동인형처럼 씰룩거렸고, 비통한 슬픔으로 일그러진 얼굴로 그녀는 아무 말 없이 주먹으로 제 가슴을 두드리기 시작했다. 사람들이 관을 들고 그녀 앞을 지나갔다. 그들이 관을 들고 곁을 지날 때 니나는 마지막으로 오빠에게 입술을 댔다. 알료샤는 집을 나서며 집주인 여자에게 뒤에 남는 이들을 돌보아 달라고 부탁했으나, 그가 말을 미처 끝내기도 전에 그녀가 가로막았다.\n\n\"물론이지요, 내가 저들과 함께 있을게요, 우리도 기독교인인걸요.\" 늙은 여인은 그렇게 말하며 울었다.\n\n교회까지 관을 운구하는 길은 멀지 않아서, 삼백 걸음 남짓이었다. 고요하고 맑은 날에 가벼운 서리가 내려 있었다. 교회 종소리가 여전히 울리고 있었다. 스네기료프는 낡고 짧은 여름 외투를 걸치고, 머리에는 아무것도 쓰지 않은 채 부드럽고 낡은 챙 넓은 모자를 손에 든 채로, 어쩔 줄 모르고 안달하며 관 뒤를 따라 달렸다. 그는 어리둥절한 불안에 사로잡힌 듯했다. 한순간에는 손을 뻗어 관의 머리 쪽을 받치려다 운구하는 이들을 방해할 뿐이었고, 또 한순간에는 관 옆을 나란히 달리며 거기서 제가 설 자리를 찾으려 했다. 꽃 한 송이가 눈 위로 떨어지자 그는 마치 그 꽃 한 송이를 잃느냐 마느냐에 온 세상이 달려 있기라도 한 듯 그것을 주우려고 달려들었다.\n\n\"그리고 빵 조각, 우리가 빵 조각을 깜빡했어!\" 그가 갑자기 당황하여 외쳤다. 그러나 소년들이 곧바로, 그가 빵 조각을 벌써 챙겨 주머니에 넣었다고 일깨워 주었다. 그는 즉시 그것을 꺼내 보고서 안심했다.\n\n\"일류샤가 나한테 일렀어, 일류샤가.\" 그가 곧바로 알료샤에게 설명했다. \"어느 날 밤 내가 저 애 곁에 앉아 있는데 저 애가 갑자기 나한테 말하더군. '아버지, 제 무덤에 흙이 다 덮이거든 그 위에 빵 한 조각을 부숴 뿌려 주세요. 참새들이 날아 내려오게요, 그러면 제가 그 소리를 듣고 혼자 누워 있지 않게 되어 기분이 좋아질 거예요.'\"\n\n\"그것 참 좋은 일이오.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"우리가 자주 빵을 좀 가져와야겠소.\"\n\n\"날마다, 날마다요!\" 이등 대위가 그 생각에 기운이 나는 듯 얼른 말했다.\n\n마침내 그들은 교회에 이르러 관을 한가운데에 놓았다. 소년들은 관을 둘러싸고 예배가 진행되는 내내 경건하게 서 있었다. 그곳은 오래되고 제법 가난한 교회였으니, 많은 성상들이 장식 틀도 없었다. 그러나 그런 교회야말로 기도하기에는 가장 좋은 곳이다. 미사가 진행되는 동안 스네기료프는 다소 진정되었으나, 때때로 똑같이 무의식적이고, 말하자면 종잡을 수 없는 불안에 사로잡혀 발작을 일으켰다. 어느 순간에는 관으로 다가가 덮개나 화환을 바로잡았고, 촛대에서 초가 떨어지자 그것을 다시 꽂으려고 달려들어 한참을 더듬거리며 애를 먹었으며, 그러다가 가라앉아서는 멍한 불안과 당혹의 빛을 띤 채 관 곁에 가만히 서 있었다. 사도 서간이 봉독된 뒤에 그는 갑자기 옆에 서 있던 알료샤에게 사도 서간이 제대로 봉독되지 않았다고 속삭였으나, 무슨 뜻인지는 설명하지 않았다. \"거룩한 천사들처럼\"이라는 기도가 진행되는 동안 그는 성가에 끼어들었으나 끝까지 부르지는 않았다. 그는 무릎을 꿇더니 돌바닥에 이마를 댄 채 오랫동안 그렇게 엎드려 있었다.\n\n마침내 장례 예식 자체가 시작되었고 초가 나누어졌다. 정신을 못 차리는 아버지는 다시 안절부절못하기 시작했으나, 가슴을 울리고 감동을 자아내는 장례 기도가 그의 영혼을 흔들어 일깨웠다. 그는 갑자기 몸이 오그라드는 듯하더니 빠르고 짧은 흐느낌을 터뜨렸고, 처음에는 그것을 억누르려 했으나 끝내 소리 내어 흐느꼈다. 죽은 이에게 작별을 고하고 관을 닫으려 하자, 그는 마치 일류샤를 덮는 것을 허락하지 않으려는 듯 두 팔을 휘저으며 죽은 아들의 입술에 게걸스럽고 집요하게 입을 맞추기 시작했다. 마침내 사람들이 그를 설득해 관 옆에서 물러나게 하는 데 성공했으나, 그는 갑자기 충동적으로 손을 뻗어 관에서 꽃 몇 송이를 낚아챘다. 그는 그것을 들여다보았고, 무언가 새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯해서 잠시 동안 자기 슬픔을 잊은 것처럼 보였다. 차츰 그는 깊은 상념에 잠기는 듯하더니 관이 들어 올려져 무덤으로 운구될 때에도 저항하지 않았다. 그것은 교회 가까운 묘지의 값비싼 자리였으니, 카테리나 이바노브나가 비용을 치렀다. 관례적인 의식이 끝난 뒤 인부들이 관을 내렸다. 스네기료프는 손에 꽃을 쥔 채 파헤쳐진 무덤 위로 어찌나 깊이 몸을 숙였던지, 소년들이 놀라서 그의 외투를 붙잡아 끌어당겼다. 그는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 온전히 이해하지 못하는 듯했다. 사람들이 무덤을 메우기 시작하자, 그는 갑자기 떨어지는 흙을 불안하게 가리키며 무언가를 말하려 했으나, 아무도 그가 무슨 뜻인지 알아듣지 못했고, 그는 별안간 멈추었다. 그러자 빵을 부숴 뿌려야 한다는 것을 누군가 일깨워 주었고, 그는 몹시 흥분하여 빵을 움켜쥐고는 그것을 잘게 부수어 그 부스러기를 무덤 위로 흩뿌리기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 날아 내려와라, 새들아, 날아 내려와라, 참새들아!\" 그는 불안하게 중얼거렸다.\n\n소년 중 하나가, 손에 꽃을 쥔 채로 빵을 부수려니 불편해 보인다며, 잠시 누군가에게 꽃을 들고 있게 하라고 권했다. 그러나 그는 그러려 하지 않았고, 실은 마치 누군가 자기 꽃을 영영 빼앗아 가려는 것처럼 갑자기 꽃을 두고 놀라는 듯했다. 그리고 무덤을 들여다보고 나서, 말하자면 모든 일이 끝났고 빵도 다 부수어 뿌렸음을 스스로 확인한 뒤, 그는 모두를 놀라게 하면서 별안간, 게다가 자못 침착하게 돌아서서 집을 향해 걷기 시작했다. 그러나 그의 발걸음은 점점 더 다급해져, 거의 달리다시피 했다. 소년들과 알료샤가 그를 따라붙었다.\n\n\"이 꽃은 엄마한테 줄 거야, 이 꽃은 엄마 거야! 내가 엄마한테 매정하게 굴었어.\" 그가 갑자기 외쳐 대기 시작했다.\n\n누군가 그에게 날이 추우니 모자를 쓰라고 했다. 그러나 그는 마치 화가 난 듯 모자를 눈 위로 내던지며 \"모자는 안 써, 모자는 안 써\"라고 거듭 말했다. 스무로프가 그것을 주워 들고 그를 따라가며 들고 갔다. 소년들은 모두 울고 있었고, 그 가운데서도 콜랴와 트로이에 관해 알아냈다는 그 소년이 가장 심하게 울었다. 이등 대위의 모자를 손에 든 스무로프 역시 비통하게 울면서도, 달리는 와중에 길의 눈 위에 놓인 붉은 벽돌 조각 하나를 집어 들어, 마침 날아가는 참새 떼를 향해 던질 틈을 냈다. 물론 그는 빗맞혔고, 달리면서 계속 울었다. 길을 절반쯤 갔을 때 스네기료프는 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 무언가에 부딪힌 듯 반 분쯤 우뚝 서 있다가, 별안간 교회 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 인적 없는 무덤을 향해 달렸다. 그러나 소년들이 곧바로 그를 따라잡아 사방에서 그를 붙들었다. 그러자 그는 마치 누가 떠밀어 쓰러뜨리기라도 한 듯 힘없이 눈 위로 쓰러졌고, 몸부림치고 흐느끼고 통곡하면서 \"일류샤, 꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야!\" 하고 외치기 시작했다. 알료샤와 콜랴가 그를 일으키려 하며 어르고 달랬다.\n\n\"대위님, 그만하세요, 용감한 사내라면 꿋꿋함을 보여야지요.\" 콜랴가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"꽃이 상하겠어요.\" 알료샤가 말했다. \"엄마가 기다리고 계세요, 아까 당신이 꽃을 한 송이도 주지 않아서 앉아 우신다고요. 일류샤의 작은 침대도 아직 그대로 있고요—\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 엄마!\" 스네기료프가 갑자기 생각이 났는지 외쳤다. \"저들이 침대를 가져갈 거야, 가져갈 거라고.\" 그는 정말로 사람들이 그럴까 봐 놀라는 듯 덧붙였다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 다시 집을 향해 달렸다. 그러나 거리가 멀지 않아 모두 함께 도착했다. 스네기료프는 황급히 문을 열고, 바로 조금 전에 그토록 모질게 다투었던 아내를 불렀다.\n\n\"엄마, 가여운 절름발이 여보, 일류샤가 당신한테 이 꽃을 보냈어.\" 그가, 그가 눈 속에서 몸부림치는 동안 얼어붙고 부러진 작은 꽃다발을 그녀에게 내밀며 외쳤다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 작은 침대 옆 구석에 집주인 여자가 가지런히 나란히 놓아 둔 일류샤의 작은 장화를 보았다. 낡고 기운 자국이 있는, 녹슨 듯한 빛깔의 뻣뻣한 그 장화를 보자 그는 두 손을 치켜들고 그쪽으로 달려가 무릎을 꿇더니, 장화 한 짝을 움켜쥐고 거기에 입술을 댄 채 게걸스럽게 입 맞추기 시작했고, \"일류샤, 꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야, 네 작은 발은 어디 있느냐?\" 하고 외쳐 댔다.\n\n\"저 애를 어디로 데려간 거야? 어디로 데려갔어?\" 실성한 여인이 가슴을 찢는 목소리로 외쳤다. 니나 역시 흐느껴 울기 시작했다. 콜랴가 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 소년들이 그를 따라 나갔다. 마침내 알료샤도 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n\"저들이 울게 두자.\" 그가 콜랴에게 말했다. \"지금은 위로해 봐야 소용없어. 잠깐 기다렸다가 다시 들어가자.\"\n\n\"그래, 소용없어, 끔찍해.\" 콜랴가 동의했다. \"있잖아, 카라마조프.\" 그가 아무도 듣지 못하게 목소리를 낮추었다. \"나는 너무나 슬퍼, 그리고 만약 그 애를 다시 살려 낼 수만 있다면, 나는 이 세상의 무엇이든 다 내놓겠어.\"\n\n\"아, 나도 그래.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각해, 카라마조프? 우리 오늘 밤에 여기 다시 오는 게 좋을까? 알다시피 그 사람은 취해 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"아마 그럴 거야. 너랑 나, 우리 둘이서만 함께 오자, 그거면 충분해, 어머니와 니나하고 한 시간쯤 보내려면. 우리가 다 함께 오면 저들에게 또다시 모든 걸 떠올리게 할 테니까.\" 알료샤가 제안했다.\n\n\"집주인 아주머니가 지금 저들에게 상을 차리고 있어—장례 만찬이라든가 뭐 그런 게 있을 거야, 신부님도 오시고. 우리 거기 다시 갈까, 카라마조프?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\" 알료샤가 말했다.\n\n\"다 너무 이상해, 카라마조프, 그런 슬픔이 있고 나서 곧장 블린이라니, 우리 종교에선 다 그렇게 부자연스러워 보여.\"\n\n\"연어도 나올 거야.\" 트로이에 관해 알아냈다는 그 소년이 큰 소리로 한마디 했다.\n\n\"카르타쇼프, 제발 진심으로 부탁하는데, 그 멍청한 소리로 또 끼어들지 좀 마, 더구나 아무도 너한테 말 거는 사람이 없고 네가 존재하든 말든 알 바도 아닐 때는!\" 콜랴가 짜증스럽게 쏘아붙였다. 소년은 얼굴이 새빨개졌으나 감히 대꾸하지는 못했다.\n\n그러는 사이 그들은 길을 따라 천천히 거닐고 있었는데, 별안간 스무로프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저기 일류샤의 돌이야, 저들이 그 밑에 일류샤를 묻으려 했던.\"\n\n그들은 모두 그 큰 돌 옆에 멈춰 섰다. 알료샤는 그것을 바라보았고, 그날 스네기료프가 그에게 들려주었던 광경 전체가, 곧 일류샤가 울면서 아버지를 끌어안고 \"아버지, 아버지, 저자가 아버지를 얼마나 모욕했는지!\" 하고 외쳤다던 그 광경이 단번에 그의 상상 속에 떠올랐다.\n\n문득 어떤 충동이 그의 영혼 속에 차오르는 듯했다. 그는 진지하고 간곡한 표정으로 일류샤의 학교 친구들의 밝고 정다운 얼굴을 하나하나 둘러보다가, 별안간 그들에게 말했다.\n\n\"얘들아, 바로 이 자리에서 너희에게 한마디 하고 싶구나.\"\n\n소년들은 그를 둘러싸고 곧바로 주의 깊고 기대에 찬 눈길을 그에게 쏟았다.\n\n\"얘들아, 우리는 곧 헤어지게 될 거야. 나는 한동안 두 형과 함께 지낼 텐데, 한 사람은 시베리아로 가게 됐고 다른 한 사람은 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있단다. 하지만 머지않아 나는 이 도시를 떠날 거야, 어쩌면 오랫동안 말이야, 그러니 우리는 헤어지게 되는 거지. 자, 여기 일류샤의 돌 옆에서 약속을 하자, 우리는 일류샤를, 그리고 서로를 결코 잊지 않겠다고. 그리고 앞으로 살면서 우리에게 무슨 일이 일어나든, 설령 그 뒤로 이십 년 동안 만나지 못한다 해도, 우리가 한때 돌을 던졌던 그 가여운 아이를 어떻게 묻었는지 늘 기억하자, 기억나니, 다리 옆에서 말이야? 그러고 나서 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 좋아하게 됐지. 그 애는 훌륭한 아이였고, 마음씨 곱고 용감한 아이였어, 아버지의 명예를 위해 가슴 아파했고 아버지가 받은 잔인한 모욕에 분개해 아버지 편에 서서 맞섰지. 그러니 무엇보다 먼저, 우리는 평생토록 그 애를 기억하자, 얘들아. 그리고 설령 우리가 더없이 중요한 일에 몰두하게 되더라도, 명예를 얻든 큰 불행에 빠지든—그래도 한때 여기서 우리 모두가 함께 모여 어떤 선하고 따뜻한 마음으로 하나가 되어, 그 가여운 아이를 사랑하던 그동안만큼은 어쩌면 지금의 우리보다 더 나은 사람이 되었던 그 시절이 얼마나 좋았는지 기억하자. 나의 작은 비둘기들아—너희를 그렇게 부르게 해 다오, 지금 너희의 선하고 정다운 얼굴들을 바라보고 있노라니 너희가 그 어여쁜 파랑새들을 꼭 닮았으니 말이다. 나의 사랑하는 아이들아, 어쩌면 너희는 내가 너희에게 하는 말을 이해하지 못할지도 모르겠다, 나는 흔히 아주 알아듣기 어렵게 말하니까. 하지만 너희는 그래도 이 말을 기억할 테고, 언젠가는 내 말에 동의하게 될 거야. 너희가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 앞으로의 삶을 위해 어떤 좋은 추억보다, 특히 어린 시절의 추억, 집에 대한 추억보다 더 높고 더 강하고 더 건전하고 좋은 것은 없다는 사실이야. 사람들은 너희에게 교육에 관해 많은 이야기를 하지만, 어린 시절부터 간직해 온 어떤 선하고 거룩한 추억이야말로 어쩌면 가장 훌륭한 교육이란다. 만약 사람이 그런 추억을 많이 지니고 삶 속으로 들어간다면, 그는 죽는 날까지 안전한 거야. 그리고 마음속에 좋은 추억이 단 하나만 남아 있어도, 그것조차 언젠가 우리를 구원하는 수단이 될 수 있어. 어쩌면 우리는 훗날 사악해질 수도 있고, 나쁜 짓을 저지르지 않고는 못 배기게 될 수도 있고, 사람들의 눈물을, 그리고 방금 콜랴가 그랬듯이 '나는 온 인류를 위해 고통받고 싶다'고 말하는 그런 사람들을 비웃을 수도 있고, 심지어 그런 사람들을 악의에 차서 조롱할 수도 있어. 하지만 우리가 아무리 나빠진다 해도—하느님께서 막아 주시기를—그래도 우리가 일류샤를 어떻게 묻었는지, 그 애의 마지막 날들에 우리가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그리고 이 돌 옆에서 우리 모두 친구처럼 어떻게 이야기를 나누었는지 떠올린다면, 우리 가운데 가장 잔인하고 가장 비웃기 좋아하는 자라도—혹시 우리가 그렇게 된다 한들—이 순간 우리가 선하고 따뜻했다는 사실을 속으로 비웃지는 못할 거야! 그뿐 아니라, 어쩌면 그 하나의 추억이 그를 큰 악에서 지켜 줄 테고, 그는 곰곰이 생각하며 이렇게 말하겠지, '그래, 나는 그때 선하고 용감하고 정직했어!'라고. 그가 혼자 비웃게 두어도 좋아, 그건 상관없어, 사람은 흔히 선하고 따뜻한 것을 비웃으니까. 그건 그저 생각이 모자라서 그러는 거야. 하지만 얘들아, 내가 장담하건대, 그는 비웃으면서도 곧 마음속으로 이렇게 말할 거야, '아니야, 내가 비웃는 건 잘못이야, 이건 비웃을 일이 아니니까.'\"\n\n\"그렇게 될 거예요, 나는 형님 말을 알겠어요, 카라마조프!\" 콜랴가 눈을 반짝이며 외쳤다.\n\n소년들은 흥분했고, 그들 역시 무언가 말하고 싶었으나 자제하면서, 골똘하고 감격에 찬 눈길로 화자를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내가 이 말을 하는 건 혹시 우리가 나빠질까 봐서야.\" 알료샤가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 우리가 나빠질 까닭이 어디 있겠니, 그렇지, 얘들아? 무엇보다 우선 우리는 선하게 살자, 그다음에는 정직하게, 그리고 결코 서로를 잊지 말자! 다시 한번 말하마. 나로서는 너희 중 누구도 결코 잊지 않겠다고 너희에게 약속하마. 지금 나를 바라보는 이 얼굴들을 하나하나, 나는 삼십 년이 지나도 기억할 거야. 방금 콜랴가 카르타쇼프에게, 그가 존재하든 말든 우리는 알 바 아니라고 말했지. 하지만 나는 카르타쇼프가 존재한다는 것을, 그리고 그가 트로이를 세운 사람들에 대해 알아냈을 때처럼 지금 얼굴을 붉히고 있는 게 아니라, 명랑하고 다정한, 사랑스러운 작은 눈으로 나를 바라보고 있다는 것을 잊을 수가 없어. 얘들아, 나의 사랑하는 아이들아, 우리 모두 일류샤처럼 너그럽고 용감해지자, 콜랴처럼 영리하고 용감하고 너그러워지자(하긴 콜랴는 다 자라면 훨씬 더 영리해지겠지만), 그리고 우리 모두 카르타쇼프처럼 겸손하고 영리하고 사랑스러워지자. 그런데 내가 왜 그 둘만 이야기하고 있지? 너희 모두가 나에게 소중해, 얘들아, 오늘 이날부터 내 마음속에 너희 모두를 위한 자리가 있단다, 그러니 너희도 너희 마음속에 나를 위한 자리를 간직해 주기를 부탁한다! 자, 그렇다면 우리가 기억할 테고 또 평생 기억하려 마음먹은 이 선하고 따뜻한 마음으로 우리를 하나로 묶어 준 이가 누구냐? 일류샤가 아니라면 누구겠니, 그 선한 아이, 그 사랑스러운 아이, 영원히 우리에게 소중할 그 아이가 아니라면! 우리 결코 그 애를 잊지 말자. 이날 이후로 그 애에 대한 기억이 우리 마음속에 영원히 살아 있기를!\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 영원히, 영원히요!\" 소년들이 부드러워진 얼굴로 낭랑한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"그 애의 얼굴과 옷과 가여운 작은 장화를, 그 애의 관과 불행하고 죄 많은 아버지를, 그리고 그 애가 온 학교를 상대로 혼자서 얼마나 용감하게 아버지 편에 서서 맞섰는지를 기억하자.\"\n\n\"기억할 거예요, 기억할 거예요.\" 소년들이 외쳤다. \"그 애는 용감했어요, 착했어요!\"\n\n\"아, 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했는지!\" 콜랴가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 아이들아, 아, 사랑하는 친구들아, 삶을 두려워하지 말아라! 무언가 선하고 의로운 일을 할 때 삶은 얼마나 아름다운지!\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 소년들이 열렬히 따라 말했다.\n\n\"카라마조프, 우리는 형님을 사랑해요!\" 한 목소리, 아마도 카르타쇼프의 목소리가 충동적으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"우리는 형님을 사랑해요, 사랑해요!\" 모두가 그 말을 받아 외쳤다. 그들 가운데 많은 이의 눈에 눈물이 어렸다.\n\n\"카라마조프 만세!\" 콜랴가 황홀하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 죽은 아이에 대한 기억이 영원히 살아 있기를!\" 알료샤가 다시 한번 감정을 담아 덧붙였다.\n\n\"영원히!\" 소년들이 또다시 화답했다.\n\n\"카라마조프.\" 콜랴가 외쳤다. \"종교에서 우리에게 가르치는 게, 곧 우리 모두 죽음에서 다시 살아나 다시 살게 되고 서로를, 모두를, 일류샤까지도 다시 보게 된다는 게 정말일까요?\"\n\n\"분명히 우리는 모두 다시 살아날 거야, 분명히 우리는 서로를 다시 보게 될 테고, 그동안 있었던 모든 일을 기쁘고 즐거운 마음으로 서로에게 이야기하게 될 거야!\" 알료샤가 반은 웃으며, 반은 열에 차서 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 얼마나 멋질까요!\" 콜랴의 입에서 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"자, 이제 이야기는 그만하고 그 애의 장례 만찬에 가자. 우리가 블린을 먹는다고 언짢아하지 마—그건 아주 오래된 풍습이고, 거기엔 무언가 좋은 데가 있으니까!\" 알료샤가 웃었다. \"자, 가자! 그리고 이제 우리는 손에 손을 잡고 가는 거야.\"\n\n\"그리고 언제까지나 그렇게, 평생토록 손에 손을 잡고! 카라마조프 만세!\" 콜랴가 다시 한번 황홀하게 외쳤고, 소년들은 또다시 그의 외침을 받아 외쳤다. \"카라마조프 만세!\"\n\n끝\n\n블린: 러시아식 팬케이크로, 장례 후 추모 만찬에 내는 전통 음식이다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-28054"]=[[["Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov was the third son of Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, a land owner well known in our district in his own day, and still remembered among us owing to his gloomy and tragic death, which happened thirteen years ago, and which I shall describe in its proper place.", "알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프는 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프의 셋째 아들이었는데, 그 아버지는 한 시절 우리 고장에서 꽤 이름이 알려졌던 지주로, 십삼 년 전에 벌어진 음울하고 비극적인 죽음 때문에 지금도 우리 사이에서 회자되는 인물이거니와, 그 죽음에 관해서는 적절한 대목에서 따로 이야기하기로 하겠다."], ["For the present I will only say that this “landowner”—for so we used to call him, although he hardly spent a day of his life on his own estate—was a strange type, yet one pretty frequently to be met with, a type abject and vicious and at the same time senseless.", "지금으로서는 다만 이렇게만 말해 두겠다. 이 \"지주\"는—우리는 그를 그렇게 부르곤 했으나, 실은 평생토록 자기 영지에서 보낸 날이 거의 하루도 없었거니와—기이한 부류의 인간이었으되, 그래도 제법 흔히 마주칠 수 있는 부류로서, 비루하고 사악하면서 동시에 분별이 없는 자였다."], ["But he was one of those senseless persons who are very well capable of looking after their worldly affairs, and, apparently, after nothing else.", "그러나 그는 그런 분별없는 인간들 가운데서도 자기 세속의 잇속만큼은 아주 잘 챙길 줄 아는, 보아하니 그 외에는 아무것도 못 하는 그런 부류였다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch, for instance, began with next to nothing; his estate was of the smallest; he ran to dine at other men’s tables, and fastened on them as a toady, yet at his death it appeared that he had a hundred thousand roubles in hard cash.", "가령 표도르 파블로비치는 거의 무일푼으로 시작했고, 영지라야 보잘것없이 작았으며, 남의 식탁에 끼어 밥을 얻어먹으려 들고 식객 노릇으로 빌붙어 살았으나, 정작 죽고 나니 현금으로만 십만 루블이 있었던 것이 드러났다."], ["At the same time, he was all his life one of the most senseless, fantastical fellows in the whole district.", "그러면서도 그는 평생토록 온 고장에서 가장 분별없고 종잡을 수 없는 사내 가운데 하나였다."], ["I repeat, it was not stupidity—the majority of these fantastical fellows are shrewd and intelligent enough—but just senselessness, and a peculiar national form of it.", "거듭 말하지만 그것은 어리석음이 아니었으니—이런 종잡을 수 없는 사내들의 대다수는 제법 영민하고 똑똑하다—오로지 분별없음이었고, 그것도 우리 민족 특유의 한 형태였다."]], [["He was married twice, and had three sons, the eldest, Dmitri, by his first wife, and two, Ivan and Alexey, by his second.", "그는 두 번 결혼했고 세 아들을 두었는데, 맏이 드미트리는 첫 아내의 소생이었고, 이반과 알렉세이 두 사람은 둘째 아내의 소생이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch’s first wife, Adelaïda Ivanovna, belonged to a fairly rich and distinguished noble family, also landowners in our district, the Miüsovs.", "표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내 아델라이다 이바노브나는 제법 부유하고 명망 있는 귀족 가문, 역시 우리 고장의 지주인 미우소프 집안의 사람이었다."], ["How it came to pass that an heiress, who was also a beauty, and moreover one of those vigorous, intelligent girls, so common in this generation, but sometimes also to be found in the last, could have married such a worthless, puny weakling, as we all called him, I won’t attempt to explain.", "어쩌다가 상속녀이자 미인인 데다, 게다가 이 세대에 흔하고 더러는 앞 세대에서도 볼 수 있던 그런 활달하고 똑똑한 처녀가, 우리가 모두 일컫듯이 그렇게 변변찮고 비실비실한 약골과 결혼하게 되었는지는 굳이 설명하려 들지 않겠다. 나는 지난 \"낭만적\""], ["I knew a young lady of the last “romantic” generation who after some years of an enigmatic passion for a gentleman, whom she might quite easily have married at any moment, invented insuperable obstacles to their union, and ended by throwing herself one stormy night into a rather deep and rapid river from a high bank, almost a precipice, and so perished, entirely to satisfy her own caprice, and to be like Shakespeare’s Ophelia.", "세대에 속한 어느 젊은 아가씨를 알고 있었는데, 그녀는 마음만 먹으면 언제든 손쉽게 결혼할 수 있었던 한 신사를 향해 몇 해 동안이나 수수께끼 같은 열정을 품은 끝에, 두 사람의 결합에 넘을 수 없는 장애물을 스스로 지어내더니, 마침내 어느 폭풍우 치는 밤에 거의 벼랑이나 다름없는 높은 강기슭에서 제법 깊고 물살 빠른 강물 속으로 몸을 던져 스스로 목숨을 끊고 말았으니, 이는 오로지 제 변덕을 채우기 위해서이자 셰익스피어의 오필리아를 닮고자 함이었다."], ["Indeed, if this precipice, a chosen and favorite spot of hers, had been less picturesque, if there had been a prosaic flat bank in its place, most likely the suicide would never have taken place.", "실로 그녀가 골라 두고 즐겨 찾던 이 벼랑이 그토록 그림 같지 않았더라면, 그 자리에 무미건조하고 평평한 강기슭이 있었더라면, 십중팔구 그 자살은 결코 일어나지 않았을 것이다."], ["This is a fact, and probably there have been not a few similar instances in the last two or three generations.", "이것은 엄연한 사실이며, 아마 지난 두세 세대 동안 이와 비슷한 사례가 적지 않았을 것이다."], ["Adelaïda Ivanovna Miüsov’s action was similarly, no doubt, an echo of other people’s ideas, and was due to the irritation caused by lack of mental freedom.", "아델라이다 이바노브나 미우소바의 행동 역시 의심할 바 없이 다른 사람들의 관념의 메아리였고, 정신의 자유가 결핍된 데서 비롯된 짜증의 산물이었다."], ["She wanted, perhaps, to show her feminine independence, to override class distinctions and the despotism of her family.", "그녀는 어쩌면 자신의 여성적 독립을 보이고, 신분의 차별과 가족의 전횡을 짓밟아 버리고 싶었던 것이리라."], ["And a pliable imagination persuaded her, we must suppose, for a brief moment, that Fyodor Pavlovitch, in spite of his parasitic position, was one of the bold and ironical spirits of that progressive epoch, though he was, in fact, an ill-natured buffoon and nothing more.", "그리고 짐작건대 잘 휘둘리는 상상력이 잠깐 동안 그녀를 설득했을 것이니, 표도르 파블로비치가 그 기생충 같은 처지에도 불구하고 그 진보적인 시대의 대담하고 냉소적인 정신의 소유자 가운데 하나라고 믿게 한 것이리라. 사실인즉 그는 심술궂은 어릿광대에 지나지 않았지만 말이다."], ["What gave the marriage piquancy was that it was preceded by an elopement, and this greatly captivated Adelaïda Ivanovna’s fancy.", "이 결혼에 묘미를 더한 것은 그것이 사랑의 도피로 시작되었다는 점이었고, 이는 아델라이다 이바노브나의 공상을 크게 사로잡았다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch’s position at the time made him specially eager for any such enterprise, for he was passionately anxious to make a career in one way or another.", "당시 표도르 파블로비치의 처지는 그로 하여금 이런 종류의 모험이라면 무엇이든 유난히 열을 올리게 만들었으니, 그는 무슨 수로든 출세하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었기 때문이다."], ["To attach himself to a good family and obtain a dowry was an alluring prospect.", "좋은 가문에 빌붙어 지참금을 얻는다는 것은 솔깃한 전망이었다."], ["As for mutual love it did not exist apparently, either in the bride or in him, in spite of Adelaïda Ivanovna’s beauty.", "서로 간의 사랑으로 말하자면, 아델라이다 이바노브나의 미모에도 불구하고 신부에게나 그에게나 보아하니 그런 것은 존재하지 않았다."], ["This was, perhaps, a unique case of the kind in the life of Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was always of a voluptuous temper, and ready to run after any petticoat on the slightest encouragement.", "이는 어쩌면 표도르 파블로비치의 생애에서 그런 종류로는 유일무이한 경우였을 것이니, 그는 언제나 호색적인 기질을 타고나 조금만 부추기면 어떤 치마폭이든 쫓아다닐 채비가 되어 있는 사내였다."], ["She seems to have been the only woman who made no particular appeal to his senses.", "그녀만은 유독 그의 감각에 별다른 호소력을 발휘하지 못한 단 한 명의 여자였던 모양이다."]], [["Immediately after the elopement Adelaïda Ivanovna discerned in a flash that she had no feeling for her husband but contempt.", "사랑의 도피가 끝나자마자 아델라이다 이바노브나는 자신이 남편에게 품은 감정이라곤 경멸밖에 없음을 단숨에 알아차렸다."], ["The marriage accordingly showed itself in its true colors with extraordinary rapidity.", "그리하여 이 결혼은 놀라우리만치 빠르게 그 본색을 드러내었다."], ["Although the family accepted the event pretty quickly and apportioned the runaway bride her dowry, the husband and wife began to lead a most disorderly life, and there were everlasting scenes between them.", "가족이 제법 빨리 이 사태를 받아들이고 달아난 신부에게 지참금을 떼어 주기는 했으나, 부부는 더없이 난잡한 생활을 시작했고 두 사람 사이에는 끊일 줄 모르는 소동이 이어졌다."], ["It was said that the young wife showed incomparably more generosity and dignity than Fyodor Pavlovitch, who, as is now known, got hold of all her money up to twenty-five thousand roubles as soon as she received it, so that those thousands were lost to her for ever.", "젊은 아내가 표도르 파블로비치보다 비할 데 없이 더 너그럽고 기품이 있었다는 말이 있었으니, 지금은 다 알려진 대로 그는 그녀가 받은 돈을 이만오천 루블에 이르기까지 받자마자 죄다 가로채어, 그 수천 루블은 그녀에게서 영영 사라지고 말았다."], ["The little village and the rather fine town house which formed part of her dowry he did his utmost for a long time to transfer to his name, by means of some deed of conveyance.", "그녀의 지참금 일부였던 자그마한 마을과 제법 번듯한 읍내 저택을, 그는 오랫동안 무슨 양도 증서 따위를 동원해 제 명의로 옮기려고 갖은 애를 다 썼다."], ["He would probably have succeeded, merely from her moral fatigue and desire to get rid of him, and from the contempt and loathing he aroused by his persistent and shameless importunity.", "아마 그는 성공했을 것이니, 그녀가 정신적으로 지쳐 그에게서 벗어나고 싶어 했고, 또 그가 집요하고 뻔뻔스럽게 들러붙어 자아낸 경멸과 혐오 때문에라도 그러했을 것이다."], ["But, fortunately, Adelaïda Ivanovna’s family intervened and circumvented his greediness.", "그러나 다행히도 아델라이다 이바노브나의 가족이 끼어들어 그의 탐욕을 가로막았다."], ["It is known for a fact that frequent fights took place between the husband and wife, but rumor had it that Fyodor Pavlovitch did not beat his wife but was beaten by her, for she was a hot-tempered, bold, dark-browed, impatient woman, possessed of remarkable physical strength.", "부부 사이에 잦은 싸움이 벌어졌다는 것은 엄연한 사실로 알려져 있으나, 소문에 따르면 표도르 파블로비치가 아내를 때린 것이 아니라 오히려 아내에게 두들겨 맞았다고 하니, 그녀는 성미가 불같고 대담하며 눈썹이 짙고 참을성이 없는 여자로서 놀라운 완력을 지니고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Finally, she left the house and ran away from Fyodor Pavlovitch with a destitute divinity student, leaving Mitya, a child of three years old, in her husband’s hands.", "마침내 그녀는 집을 나와, 세 살배기 어린 미탸를 남편의 손에 남겨 둔 채 한 가난뱅이 신학생과 함께 표도르 파블로비치에게서 달아나 버렸다."], ["Immediately Fyodor Pavlovitch introduced a regular harem into the house, and abandoned himself to orgies of drunkenness.", "그러자 표도르 파블로비치는 곧장 집 안에 버젓이 하렘을 들여놓고 곤드레만드레 술판에 빠져 지냈다."], ["In the intervals he used to drive all over the province, complaining tearfully to each and all of Adelaïda Ivanovna’s having left him, going into details too disgraceful for a husband to mention in regard to his own married life.", "그러다 짬이 나면 온 고을을 마차로 휘젓고 다니며 만나는 사람마다 붙들고 눈물을 짜내며 아델라이다 이바노브나가 자기를 버렸다고 하소연했는데, 남편이 자기 부부 생활을 두고 입에 담기에는 너무도 수치스러운 시시콜콜한 사연까지 늘어놓았다."], ["What seemed to gratify him and flatter his self-love most was to play the ridiculous part of the injured husband, and to parade his woes with embellishments.", "그가 가장 흐뭇해하고 그의 자존심을 가장 우쭐하게 만든 것은, 상처 입은 남편이라는 우스꽝스러운 배역을 연기하면서 자신의 비애를 갖은 윤색을 곁들여 떠벌리는 일이었던 듯하다."]], [["“One would think that you’d got a promotion, Fyodor Pavlovitch, you seem so pleased in spite of your sorrow,”", "\"표도르 파블로비치, 자네는 슬픔에 잠겼다면서도 어찌나 기뻐 보이는지, 누가 보면 승진이라도 한 줄 알겠네그려.\""], ["scoffers said to him.", "조롱꾼들이 그에게 이렇게 빈정거렸다."], ["Many even added that he was glad of a new comic part in which to play the buffoon, and that it was simply to make it funnier that he pretended to be unaware of his ludicrous position.", "심지어 적잖은 이들은, 그가 어릿광대 노릇을 할 새로운 희극 배역이 생겨 기뻐하는 것이며, 더 우스꽝스럽게 만들려고 일부러 제 가소로운 처지를 모르는 체하는 것이라고 덧붙이기까지 했다."], ["But, who knows, it may have been simplicity.", "그러나 누가 알겠는가, 어쩌면 그것은 순박함이었을지도 모른다."], ["At last he succeeded in getting on the track of his runaway wife.", "마침내 그는 달아난 아내의 행방을 알아내는 데 성공했다."], ["The poor woman turned out to be in Petersburg, where she had gone with her divinity student, and where she had thrown herself into a life of complete emancipation.", "가엾은 여인은 페테르부르크에 있는 것으로 드러났는데, 그곳으로 신학생과 함께 가서는 완전한 해방의 삶에 몸을 던지고 있었던 것이다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch at once began bustling about, making preparations to go to Petersburg, with what object he could not himself have said.", "표도르 파블로비치는 당장 부산을 떨며 페테르부르크로 갈 채비를 차렸으나, 무슨 목적으로 가려는 것인지는 자기 자신도 말할 수 없었으리라."], ["He would perhaps have really gone; but having determined to do so he felt at once entitled to fortify himself for the journey by another bout of reckless drinking.", "어쩌면 그는 정말로 갔을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇게 마음먹고 나자 그는 곧 그 여행에 대비해 기운을 돋운답시고 또 한바탕 무절제한 술판을 벌일 자격이 있다고 느꼈다."], ["And just at that time his wife’s family received the news of her death in Petersburg.", "그리고 바로 그 무렵 아내의 가족은 그녀가 페테르부르크에서 죽었다는 소식을 받았다."], ["She had died quite suddenly in a garret, according to one story, of typhus, or as another version had it, of starvation.", "한 이야기에 따르면 그녀는 어느 다락방에서 발진티푸스로, 또 다른 설에 따르면 굶주림으로 아주 갑작스레 죽었다고 했다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch was drunk when he heard of his wife’s death, and the story is that he ran out into the street and began shouting with joy, raising his hands to Heaven: “Lord, now lettest Thou Thy servant depart in peace,”", "표도르 파블로비치는 아내의 죽음을 전해 들었을 때 술에 취해 있었는데, 전하는 이야기로는 그가 거리로 뛰쳐나가 두 손을 하늘로 치켜들고 기뻐 소리쳤다고 하니, \"주여, 이제는 당신의 종을 평안히 놓아 보내시나이다\"라고 외쳤다는 것이다."], ["but others say he wept without restraint like a little child, so much so that people were sorry for him, in spite of the repulsion he inspired.", "그러나 어떤 이들은 그가 어린아이처럼 거침없이 울었다고 하는데, 어찌나 서럽게 울던지 그가 자아내는 혐오감에도 불구하고 사람들이 그를 가엾게 여길 정도였다고 한다."], ["It is quite possible that both versions were true, that he rejoiced at his release, and at the same time wept for her who released him.", "두 가지 설이 다 사실이었을 가능성도 충분하니, 곧 그는 풀려난 것을 기뻐하면서 동시에 자기를 풀어 준 여자를 위해 울었을 수도 있는 것이다."], ["As a general rule, people, even the wicked, are much more naïve and simple-hearted than we suppose.", "대체로 사람이란, 비록 사악한 자라 할지라도, 우리가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 더 순진하고 천진한 법이다."], ["And we ourselves are, too.", "그리고 우리 자신 또한 그러하다."]], [["# Chapter II. He Gets Rid Of His Eldest Son", "# Chapter II. He Gets Rid Of His Eldest Son"]], [["You can easily imagine what a father such a man could be and how he would bring up his children.", "그런 사내가 어떤 아버지일 수 있었으며, 제 자식들을 어떻게 길렀을지는 쉬이 짐작할 수 있을 것이다."], ["His behavior as a father was exactly what might be expected.", "아버지로서의 그의 행실은 능히 예상할 만한 그대로였다."], ["He completely abandoned the child of his marriage with Adelaïda Ivanovna, not from malice, nor because of his matrimonial grievances, but simply because he forgot him.", "그는 아델라이다 이바노브나와의 결혼에서 얻은 자식을 완전히 내팽개쳤으니, 악의에서도 아니요 부부간의 원한 때문도 아니라, 그저 그 아이를 잊어버렸기 때문이었다."], ["While he was wearying every one with his tears and complaints, and turning his house into a sink of debauchery, a faithful servant of the family, Grigory, took the three-year-old Mitya into his care.", "그가 눈물과 하소연으로 모든 사람을 진절머리 나게 하고 제 집을 난봉의 소굴로 만들어 가던 동안, 이 집안에 충직하게 봉직하던 하인 그리고리가 세 살배기 미탸를 거두어 보살폈다."], ["If he hadn’t looked after him there would have been no one even to change the baby’s little shirt.", "만일 그가 돌보지 않았더라면 그 어린것의 작은 속옷 하나 갈아입혀 줄 사람조차 없었을 것이다."]], [["It happened moreover that the child’s relations on his mother’s side forgot him too at first.", "게다가 어머니 쪽 친척들조차 처음에는 그 아이를 잊고 말았다."], ["His grandfather was no longer living, his widow, Mitya’s grandmother, had moved to Moscow, and was seriously ill, while his daughters were married, so that Mitya remained for almost a whole year in old Grigory’s charge and lived with him in the servant’s cottage.", "외할아버지는 이미 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 미망인이 된 미탸의 외할머니는 모스크바로 옮겨 가 중병을 앓고 있었으며, 딸들은 모두 시집을 가 버린 터라, 미탸는 거의 꼬박 한 해 동안을 늙은 그리고리의 손에 맡겨진 채 하인 오두막에서 그와 함께 지냈다."], ["But if his father had remembered him (he could not, indeed, have been altogether unaware of his existence) he would have sent him back to the cottage, as the child would only have been in the way of his debaucheries.", "그러나 설령 아버지가 그를 기억해 냈더라도(사실 그가 아이의 존재를 아주 모를 리는 없었으니), 아이는 제 난봉질에 거치적거리기만 할 터였으므로 그는 다시 오두막으로 돌려보냈을 것이다."], ["But a cousin of Mitya’s mother, Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, happened to return from Paris.", "그런데 마침 미탸의 어머니 쪽 사촌인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 파리에서 돌아왔다."], ["He lived for many years afterwards abroad, but was at that time quite a young man, and distinguished among the Miüsovs as a man of enlightened ideas and of European culture, who had been in the capitals and abroad.", "그는 그 뒤로도 오랜 세월을 외국에서 보냈으나 당시에는 아직 제법 젊은 사내였고, 두 수도와 외국을 두루 다녀온, 계몽된 사상과 유럽적 교양을 갖춘 인물로서 미우소프 집안에서도 단연 두드러졌다."], ["Towards the end of his life he became a Liberal of the type common in the forties and fifties.", "만년에 이르러 그는 사십 년대와 오십 년대에 흔하던 부류의 자유주의자가 되었다."], ["In the course of his career he had come into contact with many of the most Liberal men of his epoch, both in Russia and abroad.", "그는 생애를 거치는 동안 러시아 안팎에서 당대의 가장 진보적인 인사들과 두루 교분을 맺었다."], ["He had known Proudhon and Bakunin personally, and in his declining years was very fond of describing the three days of the Paris Revolution of February 1848, hinting that he himself had almost taken part in the fighting on the barricades.", "프루동과 바쿠닌을 몸소 알고 지냈으며, 노년에 접어들어서는 1848년 2월 파리 혁명의 사흘간을 즐겨 늘어놓으면서, 자기도 바리케이드 전투에 거의 한몫 끼었던 듯이 넌지시 비추기를 좋아했다."], ["This was one of the most grateful recollections of his youth.", "이것은 그의 젊은 시절을 통틀어 가장 흐뭇하게 떠올리는 추억 가운데 하나였다."], ["He had an independent property of about a thousand souls, to reckon in the old style.", "그는 옛 방식으로 헤아려 약 천 명의 농노를 거느린 독립된 재산을 지니고 있었다."], ["His splendid estate lay on the outskirts of our little town and bordered on the lands of our famous monastery, with which Pyotr Alexandrovitch began an endless lawsuit, almost as soon as he came into the estate, concerning the rights of fishing in the river or wood-cutting in the forest, I don’t know exactly which.", "그의 훌륭한 영지는 우리 작은 읍내의 변두리에 자리 잡고 있었으며 우리 고장의 이름난 수도원 영지와 맞닿아 있었는데, 표트르 알렉산드로비치는 영지를 물려받자마자 거의 곧장 그 수도원을 상대로 끝 모를 소송을 시작했으니, 강에서의 어업권을 두고서였는지 숲에서의 벌목권을 두고서였는지 나로서는 정확히 알 수 없다."], ["He regarded it as his duty as a citizen and a man of culture to open an attack upon the “clericals.”", "그는 \"성직자들\"을 향해 공세를 펼치는 것을 시민이자 교양인으로서의 제 의무로 여겼다."], ["Hearing all about Adelaïda Ivanovna, whom he, of course, remembered, and in whom he had at one time been interested, and learning of the existence of Mitya, he intervened, in spite of all his youthful indignation and contempt for Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그는 물론 기억하고 있었고 한때 마음을 두기도 했던 아델라이다 이바노브나에 관한 자초지종을 전해 듣고, 또 미탸가 존재한다는 사실을 알게 되자, 표도르 파블로비치를 향한 온갖 젊은이다운 분노와 경멸에도 불구하고 끼어들었다."], ["He made the latter’s acquaintance for the first time, and told him directly that he wished to undertake the child’s education.", "그는 그제야 처음으로 표도르 파블로비치와 면식을 트고는, 그 아이의 교육을 자기가 맡고 싶다고 단도직입으로 말했다."], ["He used long afterwards to tell as a characteristic touch, that when he began to speak of Mitya, Fyodor Pavlovitch looked for some time as though he did not understand what child he was talking about, and even as though he was surprised to hear that he had a little son in the house.", "그는 그 사람됨을 보여 주는 일화로서 훗날 오래도록 이렇게 이야기하곤 했으니, 자기가 미탸 이야기를 꺼내기 시작하자 표도르 파블로비치는 한동안 도대체 무슨 아이를 두고 하는 말인지 모르겠다는 표정을 짓더니, 심지어 제 집에 어린 아들이 있다는 말에 놀라기까지 하더라는 것이었다."], ["The story may have been exaggerated, yet it must have been something like the truth.", "이 이야기는 다소 과장되었을지도 모르나, 그래도 어지간히는 사실에 가까웠을 것이다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch was all his life fond of acting, of suddenly playing an unexpected part, sometimes without any motive for doing so, and even to his own direct disadvantage, as, for instance, in the present case.", "표도르 파블로비치는 평생토록 연기하기를 좋아하여, 때로는 아무런 동기도 없이, 심지어 지금 이 경우처럼 제게 곧장 손해가 되는데도 느닷없이 뜻밖의 배역을 연기하곤 했다."], ["This habit, however, is characteristic of a very great number of people, some of them very clever ones, not like Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그런데 이런 습성은 실로 수많은 사람들에게서, 그것도 더러는 표도르 파블로비치 따위와는 다른 아주 영민한 이들에게서도 흔히 볼 수 있는 특성이다."], ["Pyotr Alexandrovitch carried the business through vigorously, and was appointed, with Fyodor Pavlovitch, joint guardian of the child, who had a small property, a house and land, left him by his mother.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치는 그 일을 정력적으로 밀어붙여, 표도르 파블로비치와 더불어 아이의 공동 후견인으로 지명되었으니, 아이는 어머니가 남긴 자그마한 재산, 곧 집 한 채와 약간의 토지를 가지고 있었던 것이다."], ["Mitya did, in fact, pass into this cousin’s keeping, but as the latter had no family of his own, and after securing the revenues of his estates was in haste to return at once to Paris, he left the boy in charge of one of his cousins, a lady living in Moscow.", "미탸는 과연 이 사촌의 손으로 넘어갔으나, 그에게는 제 가정이 없었던 데다 영지의 수입을 확보하자마자 서둘러 곧장 파리로 돌아가고자 한 까닭에, 그는 아이를 자기 사촌 가운데 한 사람, 모스크바에 사는 어느 부인에게 맡겨 버렸다."], ["It came to pass that, settling permanently in Paris he, too, forgot the child, especially when the Revolution of February broke out, making an impression on his mind that he remembered all the rest of his life.", "그런데 파리에 영영 눌러앉게 되면서 그 또한 아이를 잊고 말았으니, 특히 2월 혁명이 터져 그의 마음에 평생토록 기억할 인상을 남긴 뒤로는 더욱 그러했다."], ["The Moscow lady died, and Mitya passed into the care of one of her married daughters.", "모스크바의 부인은 세상을 떠났고, 미탸는 그 부인의 출가한 딸들 가운데 한 사람의 손으로 넘어갔다."], ["I believe he changed his home a fourth time later on.", "그 뒤로 그는 네 번째로 또 거처를 옮겼던 것 같다."], ["I won’t enlarge upon that now, as I shall have much to tell later of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s firstborn, and must confine myself now to the most essential facts about him, without which I could not begin my story.", "지금 그 일을 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 맏아들에 관해서는 나중에 들려줄 이야기가 많은 데다, 지금으로서는 이 이야기를 시작하기 위해 빼놓을 수 없는 가장 요긴한 사실들만 추려 두어야 하겠기 때문이다."]], [["In the first place, this Mitya, or rather Dmitri Fyodorovitch, was the only one of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s three sons who grew up in the belief that he had property, and that he would be independent on coming of age.", "우선 이 미탸, 아니 정확히 말해 드미트리 표도로비치는 표도르 파블로비치의 세 아들 가운데 유일하게 자신에게 재산이 있어 성년이 되면 자립할 수 있으리라 믿으며 자라난 인물이었다."], ["He spent an irregular boyhood and youth.", "그는 들쭉날쭉하고 종잡을 수 없는 소년기와 청년기를 보냈다."], ["He did not finish his studies at the gymnasium, he got into a military school, then went to the Caucasus, was promoted, fought a duel, and was degraded to the ranks, earned promotion again, led a wild life, and spent a good deal of money.", "김나지움 학업을 마치지 못한 채 사관학교에 들어갔다가, 그다음에는 카프카스로 가서 진급했고, 결투를 벌여 사병으로 강등되었다가, 다시 진급했으며, 방탕한 생활을 일삼고 적지 않은 돈을 탕진했다."], ["He did not begin to receive any income from Fyodor Pavlovitch until he came of age, and until then got into debt.", "그는 성년이 되기 전까지는 표도르 파블로비치에게서 한 푼의 수입도 받지 못했고, 그때까지 빚더미에 올라앉아 있었다."], ["He saw and knew his father, Fyodor Pavlovitch, for the first time on coming of age, when he visited our neighborhood on purpose to settle with him about his property.", "그가 아버지 표도르 파블로비치를 처음으로 보고 알게 된 것은 성년이 되었을 때로, 제 재산 문제를 아버지와 매듭짓고자 일부러 우리 고장을 찾아왔을 무렵이었다."], ["He seems not to have liked his father.", "그는 아버지가 그다지 마음에 들지 않았던 모양이다."], ["He did not stay long with him, and made haste to get away, having only succeeded in obtaining a sum of money, and entering into an agreement for future payments from the estate, of the revenues and value of which he was unable (a fact worthy of note), upon this occasion, to get a statement from his father.", "그는 아버지 곁에 오래 머물지 않고 서둘러 떠나 버렸는데, 다만 약간의 돈을 손에 넣는 데 성공하고, 또 영지로부터 앞으로 지급받기로 하는 약정을 맺었을 따름이었으니, 정작 그 영지의 수입과 가치가 어느 정도인지에 대해서는(주목할 만한 사실인바) 이번 기회에도 아버지에게서 명세를 받아 내지 못했다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch remarked for the first time then (this, too, should be noted) that Mitya had a vague and exaggerated idea of his property.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그제야 처음으로(이 또한 새겨 둘 만하다) 미탸가 제 재산에 대해 막연하고 부풀려진 관념을 품고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch was very well satisfied with this, as it fell in with his own designs.", "표도르 파블로비치는 이를 두고 매우 흡족해했으니, 그것이 제 속셈과 딱 맞아떨어졌기 때문이다."], ["He gathered only that the young man was frivolous, unruly, of violent passions, impatient, and dissipated, and that if he could only obtain ready money he would be satisfied, although only, of course, for a short time.", "그는 다만 그 젊은이가 경박하고 다루기 힘들며 격정에 사로잡히기 쉽고 참을성이 없는 방탕한 자라는 것, 그리고 당장 손에 쥘 돈만 얻을 수 있다면, 물론 잠시뿐일망정, 그것으로 만족하리라는 것을 짐작했다."], ["So Fyodor Pavlovitch began to take advantage of this fact, sending him from time to time small doles, installments.", "그리하여 표도르 파블로비치는 이 점을 이용하기 시작하여, 이따금 푼돈을 분할로 조금씩 던져 주었다."], ["In the end, when four years later, Mitya, losing patience, came a second time to our little town to settle up once for all with his father, it turned out to his amazement that he had nothing, that it was difficult to get an account even, that he had received the whole value of his property in sums of money from Fyodor Pavlovitch, and was perhaps even in debt to him, that by various agreements into which he had, of his own desire, entered at various previous dates, he had no right to expect anything more, and so on, and so on.", "결국 사 년이 지나 미탸가 참다못해 두 번째로 우리 작은 읍내를 찾아와 아버지와 단번에 깨끗이 결판을 지으려 했을 때, 놀랍게도 자기에게는 아무것도 남은 것이 없으며, 셈을 따지기조차 어렵고, 자기는 제 재산의 값어치를 전부 표도르 파블로비치에게서 돈으로 받아 챙긴 데다 어쩌면 오히려 그에게 빚을 진 처지이며, 자신이 이전 여러 시기에 스스로 원하여 맺은 갖가지 약정에 따라 더 이상 아무것도 기대할 권리가 없다는 것 따위가 드러났다."], ["The young man was overwhelmed, suspected deceit and cheating, and was almost beside himself.", "젊은이는 망연자실하여 속임수와 사기를 의심하고는 거의 제정신이 아니었다."], ["And, indeed, this circumstance led to the catastrophe, the account of which forms the subject of my first introductory story, or rather the external side of it.", "그리고 실로 이 사정이야말로 그 파국으로 이어졌으니, 그 전말이 바로 내 첫 도입 이야기의 주제, 아니 정확히는 그 외면을 이루는 것이다."], ["But before I pass to that story I must say a little of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s other two sons, and of their origin.", "그러나 그 이야기로 넘어가기에 앞서, 나는 표도르 파블로비치의 다른 두 아들과 그들의 출생에 관해 잠깐 이야기해 두어야 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter III. The Second Marriage And The Second Family", "# Chapter III. The Second Marriage And The Second Family"]], [["Very shortly after getting his four-year-old Mitya off his hands Fyodor Pavlovitch married a second time.", "네 살배기 미탸를 자기 손에서 떼어 놓고 나서 얼마 지나지 않아 표도르 파블로비치는 두 번째로 결혼했다."], ["His second marriage lasted eight years.", "이 두 번째 결혼은 팔 년 동안 이어졌다."], ["He took this second wife, Sofya Ivanovna, also a very young girl, from another province, where he had gone upon some small piece of business in company with a Jew.", "그가 둘째 아내로 맞은 소피야 이바노브나 역시 아주 젊은 처녀로, 어떤 유대인과 함께 자그마한 사업 거리를 좇아 다른 도(道)로 갔다가 거기서 데려온 여자였다."], ["Though Fyodor Pavlovitch was a drunkard and a vicious debauchee he never neglected investing his capital, and managed his business affairs very successfully, though, no doubt, not over-scrupulously.", "표도르 파블로비치는 술꾼이며 사악한 난봉꾼이었으나 자기 자본을 굴리는 일만큼은 결코 게을리하지 않았고, 비록 의심할 바 없이 그리 양심적이지는 못했으되 자기 사업을 매우 능란하게 꾸려 나갔다."], ["Sofya Ivanovna was the daughter of an obscure deacon, and was left from childhood an orphan without relations.", "소피야 이바노브나는 이름 없는 부제(副祭)의 딸로, 어려서부터 일가붙이 하나 없는 고아로 남겨졌다."], ["She grew up in the house of a general’s widow, a wealthy old lady of good position, who was at once her benefactress and tormentor.", "그녀는 어느 장군의 미망인 집에서 자랐는데, 그 노부인은 신분 높고 부유한 늙은 귀부인으로서, 그녀에게는 은인이자 동시에 학대자였다."], ["I do not know the details, but I have only heard that the orphan girl, a meek and gentle creature, was once cut down from a halter in which she was hanging from a nail in the loft, so terrible were her sufferings from the caprice and everlasting nagging of this old woman, who was apparently not bad-hearted but had become an insufferable tyrant through idleness.", "자세한 내막은 알지 못하나, 다만 이런 이야기를 들었을 뿐이다. 곧 이 고아 처녀는 온순하고 다정한 사람이었는데, 한번은 다락방 못에 목을 매단 올가미에서 끌어내려진 적이 있었다는 것이니, 그것은 분명 심성이 모질지는 않으되 무위(無爲) 탓에 견딜 수 없는 폭군이 되어 버린 이 노파의 변덕과 끝없는 잔소리에 시달리던 그녀의 고통이 그토록 끔찍했기 때문이었다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch made her an offer; inquiries were made about him and he was refused.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그녀에게 청혼했다. 그를 두고 사람들이 알아보았고, 그는 거절당했다."], ["But again, as in his first marriage, he proposed an elopement to the orphan girl.", "그러나 첫 번째 결혼 때와 마찬가지로 그는 이번에도 이 고아 처녀에게 사랑의 도피를 제안했다."], ["There is very little doubt that she would not on any account have married him if she had known a little more about him in time.", "만약 그녀가 제때에 그에 관해 조금만 더 알았더라면 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 그와 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것은 거의 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["But she lived in another province; besides, what could a little girl of sixteen know about it, except that she would be better at the bottom of the river than remaining with her benefactress.", "그러나 그녀는 다른 도에 살고 있었고, 더구나 열여섯 살 소녀가 그런 일에 대해 무엇을 알 수 있었겠는가, 은인 곁에 남아 있느니 차라리 강바닥에 가라앉는 편이 낫다는 것 외에는."], ["So the poor child exchanged a benefactress for a benefactor.", "그리하여 이 가엾은 아이는 은인을 은인과 맞바꾼 셈이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch did not get a penny this time, for the general’s widow was furious.", "이번에 표도르 파블로비치는 한 푼도 받지 못했으니, 장군의 미망인이 노발대발했기 때문이다."], ["She gave them nothing and cursed them both.", "그녀는 두 사람에게 아무것도 주지 않았고 둘 다 저주했다."], ["But he had not reckoned on a dowry; what allured him was the remarkable beauty of the innocent girl, above all her innocent appearance, which had a peculiar attraction for a vicious profligate, who had hitherto admired only the coarser types of feminine beauty.", "그러나 그는 애초에 지참금을 셈에 넣지 않았다. 그를 사로잡은 것은 이 순결한 처녀의 빼어난 미모, 무엇보다도 그 순결한 자태였으니, 그것은 지금껏 거칠고 천박한 여성의 미만을 탐해 온 사악한 난봉꾼에게 각별한 매력을 발휘했다."]], [["“Those innocent eyes slit my soul up like a razor,”", "\"그 순결한 눈동자가 면도날처럼 내 영혼을 베어 가르더란 말이지.\""], ["he used to say afterwards, with his loathsome snigger.", "훗날 그는 그 역겨운 낄낄거림과 함께 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["In a man so depraved this might, of course, mean no more than sensual attraction.", "이토록 타락한 사내에게 이 말은 물론 육욕적 끌림 이상의 것을 뜻하지는 않았으리라."], ["As he had received no dowry with his wife, and had, so to speak, taken her “from the halter,”", "그는 아내에게서 지참금을 한 푼도 받지 못했고, 말하자면 그녀를 \"올가미에서\""], ["he did not stand on ceremony with her. Making her feel that she had “wronged”", "데려온 셈이었으므로, 그녀에게 격식을 차리려 들지 않았다."], ["him, he took advantage of her phenomenal meekness and submissiveness to trample on the elementary decencies of marriage.", "그는 그녀가 자기에게 \"잘못을 저질렀다\"고 느끼게 만들면서, 그녀의 놀라운 온순함과 순종을 이용하여 결혼 생활의 기본적인 예절마저 짓밟았다."], ["He gathered loose women into his house, and carried on orgies of debauchery in his wife’s presence.", "그는 헤픈 여자들을 집 안으로 끌어들였고, 아내가 보는 앞에서 난잡한 술판을 벌였다."], ["To show what a pass things had come to, I may mention that Grigory, the gloomy, stupid, obstinate, argumentative servant, who had always hated his first mistress, Adelaïda Ivanovna, took the side of his new mistress.", "사태가 어느 지경에까지 이르렀는지 보이기 위해 이런 일을 언급해 두겠다. 곧 음울하고 우둔하며 고집스럽고 따지기 좋아하는 하인 그리고리는, 첫 번째 여주인 아델라이다 이바노브나를 늘 미워했으면서도 이번에는 새 여주인 편을 들었다."], ["He championed her cause, abusing Fyodor Pavlovitch in a manner little befitting a servant, and on one occasion broke up the revels and drove all the disorderly women out of the house.", "그는 그녀를 두둔하며 하인답지 않은 태도로 표도르 파블로비치에게 욕설을 퍼부었고, 한번은 그 술판을 깨부수고 음탕한 여자들을 모조리 집 밖으로 쫓아내기까지 했다."], ["In the end this unhappy young woman, kept in terror from her childhood, fell into that kind of nervous disease which is most frequently found in peasant women who are said to be “possessed by devils.”", "결국 어려서부터 공포 속에서 자란 이 불행한 젊은 여인은, 흔히 \"악령에 씌었다\"고들 하는 농촌 아낙들에게 가장 자주 나타나는 그런 종류의 신경병에 걸리고 말았다."], ["At times after terrible fits of hysterics she even lost her reason.", "때로는 끔찍한 히스테리 발작 끝에 정신마저 잃곤 했다."], ["Yet she bore Fyodor Pavlovitch two sons, Ivan and Alexey, the eldest in the first year of marriage and the second three years later.", "그러면서도 그녀는 표도르 파블로비치에게 이반과 알렉세이 두 아들을 낳아 주었으니, 맏이는 결혼한 첫해에, 둘째는 그로부터 삼 년 뒤에 태어났다."], ["When she died, little Alexey was in his fourth year, and, strange as it seems, I know that he remembered his mother all his life, like a dream, of course.", "그녀가 죽었을 때 어린 알렉세이는 네 살이었는데, 이상하게 들리겠지만 나는 그가 평생토록 어머니를 기억했음을 알고 있으니, 물론 꿈결 같은 기억이기는 했다."], ["At her death almost exactly the same thing happened to the two little boys as to their elder brother, Mitya.", "그녀가 죽자 두 어린 사내아이에게는 그들의 형 미탸에게 일어났던 것과 거의 똑같은 일이 일어났다."], ["They were completely forgotten and abandoned by their father.", "두 아이는 아버지에게서 완전히 잊히고 버림받았다."], ["They were looked after by the same Grigory and lived in his cottage, where they were found by the tyrannical old lady who had brought up their mother.", "그들은 같은 그리고리의 손에 맡겨져 그의 오두막에서 살았는데, 그들의 어머니를 키운 그 폭군 같은 노부인이 거기서 그들을 찾아냈다."], ["She was still alive, and had not, all those eight years, forgotten the insult done her.", "그녀는 아직 살아 있었고, 그 팔 년 내내 자기가 당한 모욕을 잊지 않고 있었던 것이다."], ["All that time she was obtaining exact information as to her Sofya’s manner of life, and hearing of her illness and hideous surroundings she declared aloud two or three times to her retainers:", "그 세월 동안 그녀는 자기 소피야가 어떻게 사는지 정확한 소식을 줄곧 캐내고 있었고, 그녀의 병과 끔찍한 처지를 전해 듣고는 자기 하인들 앞에서 두세 번 큰 소리로 단언했다."]], [["“It serves her right.", "\"그래도 싸지."], ["God has punished her for her ingratitude.”", "하느님께서 그 배은망덕을 벌하신 게야.\""]], [["Exactly three months after Sofya Ivanovna’s death the general’s widow suddenly appeared in our town, and went straight to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house.", "소피야 이바노브나가 죽은 지 꼭 석 달 만에 장군의 미망인이 느닷없이 우리 고을에 나타나 곧장 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 갔다."], ["She spent only half an hour in the town but she did a great deal.", "그녀는 고을에서 반 시간밖에 머물지 않았으나 적지 않은 일을 해냈다."], ["It was evening.", "때는 저녁이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch, whom she had not seen for those eight years, came in to her drunk.", "그 팔 년 동안 그녀가 보지 못했던 표도르 파블로비치가 술에 취해 그녀 앞에 나왔다."], ["The story is that instantly upon seeing him, without any sort of explanation, she gave him two good, resounding slaps on the face, seized him by a tuft of hair, and shook him three times up and down.", "전하는 이야기로는, 그녀가 그를 보자마자 아무 설명도 없이 그의 뺨을 두 차례 철썩철썩 후려갈기고, 한 줌의 머리채를 움켜쥐고 위아래로 세 번 흔들어 댔다는 것이다."], ["Then, without a word, she went straight to the cottage to the two boys.", "그러고는 한마디 말도 없이 곧장 두 사내아이가 있는 오두막으로 갔다."], ["Seeing, at the first glance, that they were unwashed and in dirty linen, she promptly gave Grigory, too, a box on the ear, and announcing that she would carry off both the children she wrapped them just as they were in a rug, put them in the carriage, and drove off to her own town.", "한눈에 그 아이들이 씻지도 못하고 더러운 속옷을 걸치고 있는 것을 본 그녀는 즉시 그리고리에게도 따귀를 한 대 올려붙이고, 두 아이를 모두 데려가겠노라 선언하더니, 입은 그대로 아이들을 양탄자에 둘둘 말아 마차에 싣고 자기 고을로 떠나 버렸다."], ["Grigory accepted the blow like a devoted slave, without a word, and when he escorted the old lady to her carriage he made her a low bow and pronounced impressively that, “God would repay her for the orphans.”", "그리고리는 충직한 종답게 한마디 없이 그 매를 받아들였고, 노부인을 마차까지 배웅하면서 깊숙이 절을 올리고 \"하느님께서 고아들을 위해 마님께 갚아 주실 겁니다\"라고 묵직하게 아뢰었다."], ["“You are a blockhead all the same,”", "\"그래도 너는 멍청이로구나.\""], ["the old lady shouted to him as she drove away.", "노부인은 마차를 몰아 떠나면서 그에게 이렇게 소리쳤다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch, thinking it over, decided that it was a good thing, and did not refuse the general’s widow his formal consent to any proposition in regard to his children’s education.", "표도르 파블로비치는 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 잘된 일이라 판단했고, 제 아이들의 교육에 관한 어떤 제안에도 장군의 미망인에게 정식 동의를 거절하지 않았다."], ["As for the slaps she had given him, he drove all over the town telling the story.", "그녀에게 얻어맞은 따귀로 말하자면, 그는 온 고을을 휘젓고 다니며 그 이야기를 떠벌렸다."]], [["It happened that the old lady died soon after this, but she left the boys in her will a thousand roubles each “for their instruction, and so that all be spent on them exclusively, with the condition that it be so portioned out as to last till they are twenty-one, for it is more than adequate provision for such children.", "마침 노부인은 이 일이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 죽었으나, 유언으로 두 사내아이에게 각각 천 루블씩을 남겼는데, \"그들의 교육을 위함이며, 오로지 그들에게만 전부 쓰일 것이되, 다만 스물한 살이 될 때까지 가도록 분배할 것을 조건으로 하노니, 이만하면 그런 아이들에게는 차고 넘치는 몫이니라."], ["If other people think fit to throw away their money, let them.”", "다른 사람들이 제 돈을 함부로 내던지고 싶다면 그러라지.\""], ["I have not read the will myself, but I heard there was something queer of the sort, very whimsically expressed.", "나는 그 유언장을 직접 읽어 보지는 못했으나, 그런 종류의 무언가 이상하고도 몹시 변덕스럽게 표현된 대목이 있었다고 들었다."], ["The principal heir, Yefim Petrovitch Polenov, the Marshal of Nobility of the province, turned out, however, to be an honest man.", "그러나 주된 상속자인 그 도의 귀족 회장(會長) 예핌 페트로비치 폴레노프는 정직한 사람으로 드러났다."], ["Writing to Fyodor Pavlovitch, and discerning at once that he could extract nothing from him for his children’s education (though the latter never directly refused but only procrastinated as he always did in such cases, and was, indeed, at times effusively sentimental), Yefim Petrovitch took a personal interest in the orphans.", "그는 표도르 파블로비치에게 편지를 써 보냈으나, 그에게서 아이들 교육을 위해 한 푼도 끌어낼 수 없으리라는 것을 단번에 알아차렸으니(다만 그자는 결코 대놓고 거절하지는 않고 늘 그런 경우에 하듯 차일피일 미루기만 했고, 실은 때때로 주체할 수 없이 감상에 젖기도 했다), 예핌 페트로비치는 그 고아들에게 몸소 관심을 기울였다."], ["He became especially fond of the younger, Alexey, who lived for a long while as one of his family.", "그는 특히 어린 쪽인 알렉세이를 아끼게 되었고, 그 아이는 오랫동안 그의 가족 가운데 한 사람처럼 그 집에서 살았다."], ["I beg the reader to note this from the beginning.", "부디 독자들께서는 이 점을 처음부터 유념해 주시기 바란다."], ["And to Yefim Petrovitch, a man of a generosity and humanity rarely to be met with, the young people were more indebted for their education and bringing up than to any one.", "그리고 좀처럼 찾아보기 힘든 너그러움과 인정을 지닌 이 예핌 페트로비치에게, 이 젊은이들은 그 누구보다도 더 많은 교육과 양육의 빚을 졌다."], ["He kept the two thousand roubles left to them by the general’s widow intact, so that by the time they came of age their portions had been doubled by the accumulation of interest.", "그는 장군의 미망인이 그들에게 남긴 이천 루블을 한 푼도 손대지 않고 고스란히 간직했으므로, 그들이 성년에 이르렀을 무렵에는 이자가 불어 그 몫이 두 배로 늘어나 있었다."], ["He educated them both at his own expense, and certainly spent far more than a thousand roubles upon each of them.", "그는 두 아이를 모두 자기 돈으로 교육시켰고, 분명 각자에게 천 루블을 훌쩍 넘는 돈을 들였다."], ["I won’t enter into a detailed account of their boyhood and youth, but will only mention a few of the most important events.", "그들의 소년 시절과 청년 시절을 시시콜콜 늘어놓지는 않겠고, 다만 가장 중요한 사건 몇 가지만 언급하겠다."], ["Of the elder, Ivan, I will only say that he grew into a somewhat morose and reserved, though far from timid boy.", "맏이 이반에 관해서는 이것만 말해 두겠으니, 그는 다소 침울하고 속을 드러내지 않는, 그러나 결코 소심하지는 않은 소년으로 자라났다."], ["At ten years old he had realized that they were living not in their own home but on other people’s charity, and that their father was a man of whom it was disgraceful to speak.", "열 살 무렵 그는 자기들이 제 집이 아니라 남의 자선에 기대어 살고 있으며, 자기들 아버지가 입에 올리기조차 부끄러운 인간임을 깨달았다."], ["This boy began very early, almost in his infancy (so they say at least), to show a brilliant and unusual aptitude for learning.", "이 소년은 아주 일찍부터, 거의 갓난아이 적부터(적어도 사람들 말로는 그렇다고 한다) 학업에 비범하고 남다른 재능을 드러내기 시작했다."], ["I don’t know precisely why, but he left the family of Yefim Petrovitch when he was hardly thirteen, entering a Moscow gymnasium, and boarding with an experienced and celebrated teacher, an old friend of Yefim Petrovitch.", "정확히 무슨 까닭인지는 모르나, 그는 열세 살이 채 못 되어 예핌 페트로비치의 가족을 떠나 모스크바의 한 김나지움에 들어갔고, 예핌 페트로비치의 오랜 벗인 노련하고 이름난 교사의 집에서 하숙하게 되었다."], ["Ivan used to declare afterwards that this was all due to the “ardor for good works”", "이반은 훗날 이것이 모두 예핌 페트로비치의 \"선행을 향한 열정\""], ["of Yefim Petrovitch, who was captivated by the idea that the boy’s genius should be trained by a teacher of genius.", "덕분이었다고 단언하곤 했으니, 그는 그 소년의 천재적 재능을 천재적인 교사가 길러야 한다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다."], ["But neither Yefim Petrovitch nor this teacher was living when the young man finished at the gymnasium and entered the university.", "그러나 그 젊은이가 김나지움을 마치고 대학에 들어갔을 때는 예핌 페트로비치도 그 교사도 이미 세상을 떠나고 없었다."], ["As Yefim Petrovitch had made no provision for the payment of the tyrannical old lady’s legacy, which had grown from one thousand to two, it was delayed, owing to formalities inevitable in Russia, and the young man was in great straits for the first two years at the university, as he was forced to keep himself all the time he was studying.", "예핌 페트로비치가 폭군 같은 노부인의 유산—천 루블에서 이천 루블로 불어난—을 지급할 채비를 미처 해 두지 못한 탓에, 러시아에서는 피할 수 없는 갖가지 형식 절차로 인해 그 지급이 지연되었고, 이 젊은이는 대학에서의 처음 두 해 동안 몹시 궁핍한 처지에 놓였으니, 공부하는 내내 스스로 생계를 꾸려 가지 않으면 안 되었기 때문이다."], ["It must be noted that he did not even attempt to communicate with his father, perhaps from pride, from contempt for him, or perhaps from his cool common sense, which told him that from such a father he would get no real assistance.", "여기서 짚어 둘 것은, 그가 아버지와 연락을 취하려는 시도조차 하지 않았다는 점인데, 이는 아마도 자존심에서, 아버지에 대한 경멸에서, 혹은 그런 아버지에게서는 진정한 도움을 얻지 못하리라 일러 주는 그의 냉정한 분별에서 비롯된 것이리라."], ["However that may have been, the young man was by no means despondent and succeeded in getting work, at first giving sixpenny lessons and afterwards getting paragraphs on street incidents into the newspapers under the signature of “Eye-Witness.”", "어찌 됐든 이 젊은이는 결코 낙담하지 않았고 일자리를 구하는 데 성공했으니, 처음에는 한 시간에 몇 푼 받는 과외를 했고 나중에는 \"목격자\"라는 서명을 달고 거리에서 일어난 사건들을 다룬 단신 기사를 신문에 실었다."], ["These paragraphs, it was said, were so interesting and piquant that they were soon taken.", "사람들 말로는, 이 단신들이 어찌나 흥미롭고 톡톡 쏘는 맛이 있던지 곧 받아들여졌다고 한다."], ["This alone showed the young man’s practical and intellectual superiority over the masses of needy and unfortunate students of both sexes who hang about the offices of the newspapers and journals, unable to think of anything better than everlasting entreaties for copying and translations from the French.", "이것만으로도 이 젊은이가, 신문사와 잡지사 사무실 언저리를 어슬렁거리며 프랑스어 번역이나 필사 일거리를 끝없이 애걸하는 것 외에는 더 나은 궁리라곤 못 하는, 가난하고 불운한 남녀 학생 무리보다 실무적으로나 지적으로나 더 뛰어남을 보여 주었다."], ["Having once got into touch with the editors Ivan Fyodorovitch always kept up his connection with them, and in his latter years at the university he published brilliant reviews of books upon various special subjects, so that he became well known in literary circles.", "일단 편집자들과 인연을 맺은 이반 표도로비치는 줄곧 그들과의 관계를 유지했고, 대학 말년에는 여러 전문 분야의 서적에 대해 빼어난 서평을 발표하여 문단에서 제법 이름이 알려지게 되었다."], ["But only in his last year he suddenly succeeded in attracting the attention of a far wider circle of readers, so that a great many people noticed and remembered him.", "그러나 그가 훨씬 더 폭넓은 독자층의 관심을 느닷없이 끌어모으는 데 성공한 것은 마지막 학년에 이르러서였으니, 그리하여 수많은 사람이 그를 주목하고 기억하게 되었다."], ["It was rather a curious incident.", "그것은 다소 기이한 사건이었다."], ["When he had just left the university and was preparing to go abroad upon his two thousand roubles, Ivan Fyodorovitch published in one of the more important journals a strange article, which attracted general notice, on a subject of which he might have been supposed to know nothing, as he was a student of natural science.", "막 대학을 졸업하고 이천 루블을 가지고 외국으로 떠날 채비를 하던 무렵, 이반 표도로비치는 더 비중 있는 잡지 가운데 하나에 어떤 기묘한 논문을 발표했는데, 자연과학을 전공한 학생이었던 그가 아무것도 모르리라 여겨질 만한 주제를 다룬 것이어서 세간의 이목을 끌었다."], ["The article dealt with a subject which was being debated everywhere at the time—the position of the ecclesiastical courts.", "그 논문은 당시 도처에서 논쟁이 되던 주제—교회 재판소의 위상—를 다루었다."], ["After discussing several opinions on the subject he went on to explain his own view.", "그는 그 문제에 관한 여러 견해를 논한 뒤, 이어서 자신의 견해를 펼쳐 보였다."], ["What was most striking about the article was its tone, and its unexpected conclusion.", "그 논문에서 가장 두드러진 것은 그 어조와 뜻밖의 결론이었다."], ["Many of the Church party regarded him unquestioningly as on their side.", "교회파에 속한 많은 이들이 그를 의심 없이 자기네 편으로 여겼다."], ["And yet not only the secularists but even atheists joined them in their applause.", "그러면서도 세속주의자들뿐 아니라 무신론자들까지 그들과 함께 그에게 갈채를 보냈다."], ["Finally some sagacious persons opined that the article was nothing but an impudent satirical burlesque.", "마침내 몇몇 명민한 사람들은 그 논문이 뻔뻔스러운 풍자적 익살에 지나지 않는다는 견해를 내놓았다."], ["I mention this incident particularly because this article penetrated into the famous monastery in our neighborhood, where the inmates, being particularly interested in the question of the ecclesiastical courts, were completely bewildered by it.", "내가 이 사건을 특별히 언급하는 까닭은, 이 논문이 우리 이웃에 있는 그 유명한 수도원에까지 파고들었기 때문인데, 교회 재판소 문제에 각별한 관심을 두고 있던 그곳 수도사들은 그 논문에 완전히 어리둥절해졌다."], ["Learning the author’s name, they were interested in his being a native of the town and the son of “that Fyodor Pavlovitch.”", "글쓴이의 이름을 알게 된 그들은, 그가 이 고을 태생이며 \"바로 그 표도르 파블로비치\"의 아들이라는 데 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["And just then it was that the author himself made his appearance among us.", "그리고 바로 그 무렵 글쓴이 본인이 우리 사이에 모습을 나타냈던 것이다."]], [["Why Ivan Fyodorovitch had come amongst us I remember asking myself at the time with a certain uneasiness.", "이반 표도로비치가 무슨 까닭으로 우리 사이에 찾아왔는지, 나는 그때 어떤 불안감을 품고 스스로 물어보았던 것을 기억한다."], ["This fateful visit, which was the first step leading to so many consequences, I never fully explained to myself.", "그토록 많은 결과로 이어진 첫걸음이었던 이 운명적인 방문을, 나는 끝내 나 자신에게 온전히 설명해 내지 못했다."], ["It seemed strange on the face of it that a young man so learned, so proud, and apparently so cautious, should suddenly visit such an infamous house and a father who had ignored him all his life, hardly knew him, never thought of him, and would not under any circumstances have given him money, though he was always afraid that his sons Ivan and Alexey would also come to ask him for it.", "겉으로 보기에 그것은 이상한 일이었으니, 그토록 학식 있고 그토록 거만하며 보아하니 그토록 신중한 젊은이가, 그처럼 추잡한 집과, 평생 그를 거들떠보지도 않고 거의 알지도 못하며 한 번도 마음에 두지 않았고 어떤 경우에도 그에게 돈을 주려 하지 않았을—그러면서도 늘 두 아들 이반과 알렉세이가 자기에게 돈을 요구하러 올까 봐 전전긍긍하던—그런 아버지를 느닷없이 찾아왔으니 말이다."], ["And here the young man was staying in the house of such a father, had been living with him for two months, and they were on the best possible terms.", "그런데 이 젊은이는 그런 아버지의 집에 머물며 두 달째 함께 살고 있었고, 두 사람은 더없이 좋은 사이였다."], ["This last fact was a special cause of wonder to many others as well as to me.", "이 마지막 사실은 나뿐 아니라 다른 많은 이들에게도 각별한 놀라움의 까닭이 되었다."], ["Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, of whom we have spoken already, the cousin of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s first wife, happened to be in the neighborhood again on a visit to his estate.", "앞서 이미 이야기한 바 있는, 표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내의 사촌인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 마침 자기 영지를 둘러보러 다시 이 근방에 와 있었다."], ["He had come from Paris, which was his permanent home.", "그는 자신의 영구한 거처인 파리에서 왔다."], ["I remember that he was more surprised than any one when he made the acquaintance of the young man, who interested him extremely, and with whom he sometimes argued and not without an inner pang compared himself in acquirements.", "그가 그 젊은이와 알게 되었을 때 누구보다도 더 놀라워했던 것을 나는 기억하니, 그 젊은이는 그의 흥미를 몹시 자아냈고, 그는 때때로 젊은이와 논쟁을 벌이며 내심 적잖은 아픔과 더불어 자신의 학식을 그와 견주어 보기도 했다."]], [["“He is proud,”", "\"저 친구는 자존심이 강해.\""], ["he used to say, “he will never be in want of pence; he has got money enough to go abroad now.", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"저 친구는 결코 푼돈이 아쉬울 일이 없을 거야."], ["What does he want here?", "지금 외국으로 떠날 만한 돈도 충분히 갖고 있고."], ["Every one can see that he hasn’t come for money, for his father would never give him any.", "그런데 여기서 도대체 무얼 바라는 거지? 돈 때문에 온 게 아니라는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하지, 제 아비가 한 푼도 줄 리 없으니 말이야."], ["He has no taste for drink and dissipation, and yet his father can’t do without him.", "술이나 방탕에 취미가 있는 것도 아닌데, 그런데도 제 아비가 그 친구 없이는 못 견디거든."], ["They get on so well together!”", "둘이 어찌나 죽이 잘 맞는지!\""]], [["That was the truth; the young man had an unmistakable influence over his father, who positively appeared to be behaving more decently and even seemed at times ready to obey his son, though often extremely and even spitefully perverse.", "그것은 사실이었다. 이 젊은이는 자기 아버지에게 틀림없는 영향력을 미치고 있었으니, 아버지는 실제로 더 점잖게 처신하는 듯 보였고 때로는 아들의 말에 따를 채비가 된 것처럼 보이기까지 했는데, 그러면서도 종종 극도로, 심지어 심술궂게 비뚤어지게 굴곤 했다."]], [["It was only later that we learned that Ivan had come partly at the request of, and in the interests of, his elder brother, Dmitri, whom he saw for the first time on this very visit, though he had before leaving Moscow been in correspondence with him about an important matter of more concern to Dmitri than himself.", "이반이 부분적으로는 형 드미트리의 부탁으로, 또 그 형의 이익을 위해 찾아왔다는 사실을 우리가 알게 된 것은 나중의 일이었으니, 그는 바로 이번 방문에서 그 형을 난생처음 만났으나, 모스크바를 떠나기 전에 이미 자기보다는 드미트리에게 더 중대한 어떤 문제를 두고 그와 편지를 주고받은 터였다."], ["What that business was the reader will learn fully in due time.", "그 일이 무엇이었는지는 독자들께서 때가 되면 소상히 알게 될 것이다."], ["Yet even when I did know of this special circumstance I still felt Ivan Fyodorovitch to be an enigmatic figure, and thought his visit rather mysterious.", "그러나 내가 이 특별한 사정을 알게 된 뒤에도, 나는 여전히 이반 표도로비치를 수수께끼 같은 인물로 느꼈고, 그의 방문이 어쩐지 의문스럽다고 여겼다."]], [["I may add that Ivan appeared at the time in the light of a mediator between his father and his elder brother Dmitri, who was in open quarrel with his father and even planning to bring an action against him.", "덧붙이자면, 이반은 당시 자기 아버지와, 아버지와 공공연히 다투며 심지어 소송까지 걸려고 별렀던 형 드미트리 사이를 중재하는 사람으로 비쳤다."]], [["The family, I repeat, was now united for the first time, and some of its members met for the first time in their lives.", "거듭 말하거니와, 이 가족은 이제 처음으로 한자리에 모인 것이었고, 그 구성원 가운데 몇몇은 난생처음 서로 얼굴을 마주했다."], ["The younger brother, Alexey, had been a year already among us, having been the first of the three to arrive.", "막내 알렉세이는 세 형제 가운데 가장 먼저 도착하여 이미 한 해 전부터 우리 사이에 와 있었다."], ["It is of that brother Alexey I find it most difficult to speak in this introduction.", "이 머리말에서 가장 말하기 어려운 것이 바로 그 동생 알렉세이에 관해서다."], ["Yet I must give some preliminary account of him, if only to explain one queer fact, which is that I have to introduce my hero to the reader wearing the cassock of a novice.", "그러나 한 가지 기이한 사실, 곧 내가 나의 주인공을 수련 수사의 법의를 입은 모습으로 독자들께 소개해야만 한다는 점을 설명하기 위해서라도, 나는 그에 관해 미리 얼마간 이야기해 두지 않으면 안 된다."], ["Yes, he had been for the last year in our monastery, and seemed willing to be cloistered there for the rest of his life.", "그렇다, 그는 지난 한 해 동안 우리 수도원에 머물러 왔고, 남은 평생을 거기서 은둔하며 보낼 마음인 듯했다."]], [["# Chapter IV. The Third Son, Alyosha", "# Chapter IV. The Third Son, Alyosha"]], [["He was only twenty, his brother Ivan was in his twenty-fourth year at the time, while their elder brother Dmitri was twenty-seven.", "알료샤는 그때 겨우 스무 살이었고, 그의 형 이반은 스물네 살이었으며, 그들의 맏형 드미트리는 스물일곱이었다."], ["First of all, I must explain that this young man, Alyosha, was not a fanatic, and, in my opinion at least, was not even a mystic.", "우선 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이 젊은이 알료샤가 광신자가 아니었으며, 적어도 내 견해로는 신비주의자조차 아니었다는 점이다."], ["I may as well give my full opinion from the beginning.", "차라리 내 견해를 처음부터 온전히 털어놓는 편이 낫겠다."], ["He was simply an early lover of humanity, and that he adopted the monastic life was simply because at that time it struck him, so to say, as the ideal escape for his soul struggling from the darkness of worldly wickedness to the light of love.", "그는 그저 일찍부터 인류를 사랑하는 사람이었을 뿐이며, 그가 수도 생활을 택한 것도 단지 그 무렵 그것이, 이를테면 세속적 사악함의 어둠 속에서 사랑의 빛을 향해 발버둥치는 자신의 영혼을 위한 이상적인 도피처로 그에게 비쳤기 때문이었다."], ["And the reason this life struck him in this way was that he found in it at that time, as he thought, an extraordinary being, our celebrated elder, Zossima, to whom he became attached with all the warm first love of his ardent heart.", "그리고 이 생활이 그에게 그토록 비친 까닭은, 그가 그때 그곳에서, 그가 생각하기에, 한 비범한 존재를—우리 고장의 이름난 조시마 장로를—발견했기 때문이었으니, 그는 자신의 뜨거운 마음의 온정 어린 첫사랑을 죄다 바쳐 그 장로에게 매달렸다."], ["But I do not dispute that he was very strange even at that time, and had been so indeed from his cradle.", "그러나 그가 그 시절에도 매우 기이했으며, 사실 요람에 있을 때부터 그러했다는 점만은 나도 부인하지 않겠다."], ["I have mentioned already, by the way, that though he lost his mother in his fourth year he remembered her all his life—her face, her caresses, “as though she stood living before me.”", "그건 그렇고, 나는 이미 말한 바 있거니와, 그는 네 살 때 어머니를 잃었음에도 평생토록 어머니를, 곧 그 얼굴과 그 어루만짐을 \"마치 살아 계신 채로 내 앞에 서 계신 듯이\""], ["Such memories may persist, as every one knows, from an even earlier age, even from two years old, but scarcely standing out through a whole lifetime like spots of light out of darkness, like a corner torn out of a huge picture, which has all faded and disappeared except that fragment.", "기억했다. 누구나 알다시피 이런 기억은 더 이른 나이에서, 심지어 두 살 무렵에서도 남아 있을 수 있으나, 어둠 속의 빛 점처럼, 온통 빛이 바래 사라져 버린 거대한 그림에서 그 조각 하나만 찢겨 나온 한 귀퉁이처럼, 한평생을 꿰뚫고 또렷이 떠오르는 일은 좀처럼 없다."], ["That is how it was with him.", "그런데 그의 경우가 바로 그러했다."], ["He remembered one still summer evening, an open window, the slanting rays of the setting sun (that he recalled most vividly of all); in a corner of the room the holy image, before it a lighted lamp, and on her knees before the image his mother, sobbing hysterically with cries and moans, snatching him up in both arms, squeezing him close till it hurt, and praying for him to the Mother of God, holding him out in both arms to the image as though to put him under the Mother’s protection ...", "그는 어느 고요한 여름 저녁을, 열린 창문을, 지는 해의 비스듬한 햇살을(무엇보다도 이것을 가장 생생하게 떠올렸다) 기억했으니, 방 한 귀퉁이에는 성상이 있었고 그 앞에는 등불이 켜져 있었으며, 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 어머니가 외침과 신음으로 발작하듯 흐느끼면서, 그를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안아 아프도록 꼭 죄어 안고는 그를 위해 성모님께 기도하며, 마치 그를 성모님의 보호 아래 맡기려는 듯 그를 두 팔로 성상을 향해 내밀고 있었다……"], ["and suddenly a nurse runs in and snatches him from her in terror.", "그러다 갑자기 유모가 달려 들어와 질겁하며 그를 어머니의 품에서 낚아챘다."], ["That was the picture!", "바로 그것이 그 그림이었다!"], ["And Alyosha remembered his mother’s face at that minute.", "그리고 알료샤는 그 순간의 어머니 얼굴을 기억했다."], ["He used to say that it was frenzied but beautiful as he remembered.", "그는 기억 속의 그 얼굴이 광기에 차 있었으나 아름다웠다고 말하곤 했다."], ["But he rarely cared to speak of this memory to any one.", "그러나 그는 이 기억을 누군가에게 이야기하고 싶어 하는 일이 좀처럼 없었다."], ["In his childhood and youth he was by no means expansive, and talked little indeed, but not from shyness or a sullen unsociability; quite the contrary, from something different, from a sort of inner preoccupation entirely personal and unconcerned with other people, but so important to him that he seemed, as it were, to forget others on account of it.", "어린 시절과 소년 시절에 그는 결코 속내를 잘 드러내는 편이 아니었고 실로 말수가 적었으나, 그것은 수줍음이나 음울한 비사교성 때문이 아니라, 오히려 정반대로, 무언가 다른 것에서, 곧 전적으로 개인적이며 다른 사람들과는 무관하지만 그에게는 너무도 중대한 어떤 내면의 골몰에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그는 그것 때문에 이를테면 남들을 잊어버리는 듯이 보일 지경이었다."], ["But he was fond of people: he seemed throughout his life to put implicit trust in people: yet no one ever looked on him as a simpleton or naïve person.", "그러나 그는 사람들을 좋아했다. 그는 평생토록 사람들을 무턱대고 믿는 듯이 보였으나, 누구도 그를 바보나 순진한 사람으로 여기지 않았다."], ["There was something about him which made one feel at once (and it was so all his life afterwards) that he did not care to be a judge of others—that he would never take it upon himself to criticize and would never condemn any one for anything.", "그에게는 무언가 사람으로 하여금 단번에 느끼게 하는 것이 있었으니(그리고 그것은 그 후로도 평생토록 그러했다), 곧 그가 남을 심판하는 자가 되기를 바라지 않으며—결코 남을 비판하는 일을 자청하지 않고 무슨 일로든 누구도 단죄하지 않으리라는 것이었다."], ["He seemed, indeed, to accept everything without the least condemnation though often grieving bitterly: and this was so much so that no one could surprise or frighten him even in his earliest youth.", "실로 그는 더러 쓰라리게 슬퍼하면서도 모든 것을 조금의 단죄도 없이 받아들이는 듯이 보였으니, 그 정도가 어찌나 심했던지 가장 어린 소년 시절에도 누구 하나 그를 놀라게 하거나 겁먹게 할 수 없었다."], ["Coming at twenty to his father’s house, which was a very sink of filthy debauchery, he, chaste and pure as he was, simply withdrew in silence when to look on was unbearable, but without the slightest sign of contempt or condemnation.", "스무 살에 더러운 방탕의 소굴이나 다름없는 아버지의 집에 와서도, 정결하고 순결한 그는 차마 두고 볼 수 없을 때면 그저 잠자코 물러났을 뿐, 멸시나 단죄의 기색이라고는 손톱만큼도 내비치지 않았다."], ["His father, who had once been in a dependent position, and so was sensitive and ready to take offense, met him at first with distrust and sullenness.", "한때 남에게 빌붙어 살던 처지였던 까닭에 예민하고 걸핏하면 발끈하던 그의 아버지는, 처음에는 미심쩍어하며 음울하게 그를 맞았다. \"이 녀석은 말은 별로 없으면서,\""], ["“He does not say much,” he used to say, “and thinks the more.”", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"속으로는 더 많이 생각한단 말이야.\""], ["But soon, within a fortnight indeed, he took to embracing him and kissing him terribly often, with drunken tears, with sottish sentimentality, yet he evidently felt a real and deep affection for him, such as he had never been capable of feeling for any one before.", "그러나 곧, 실로 보름도 채 못 되어, 그는 술 취한 눈물을 흘리며 술주정뱅이의 감상에 젖어 무섭도록 자주 그를 끌어안고 입을 맞추기 시작했는데, 그것은 분명 일찍이 어느 누구에게도 품어 본 적 없는 진실하고 깊은 애정이었다."]], [["Every one, indeed, loved this young man wherever he went, and it was so from his earliest childhood.", "실로 어디를 가든 누구나 이 젊은이를 사랑했으니, 그것은 그의 가장 어린 시절부터 그러했다."], ["When he entered the household of his patron and benefactor, Yefim Petrovitch Polenov, he gained the hearts of all the family, so that they looked on him quite as their own child.", "그가 자신의 후원자이자 은인인 예핌 페트로비치 폴레노프의 집안에 들어갔을 때, 그는 온 가족의 마음을 사로잡았고, 그리하여 그들은 그를 아주 제 자식처럼 여기게 되었다."], ["Yet he entered the house at such a tender age that he could not have acted from design nor artfulness in winning affection.", "그러나 그가 그 집에 들어간 것은 너무도 어린 나이였으므로, 애정을 얻으려는 술수나 잔꾀를 부렸을 리는 없었다."], ["So that the gift of making himself loved directly and unconsciously was inherent in him, in his very nature, so to speak.", "그러니 곧장 무의식적으로 사랑받게 되는 재주가 그에게는, 이를테면 그 본성에 타고난 것이었다."], ["It was the same at school, though he seemed to be just one of those children who are distrusted, sometimes ridiculed, and even disliked by their schoolfellows.", "학교에서도 마찬가지였으니, 그는 동무들에게 미덥지 못한 아이, 때로는 놀림감이 되고 심지어 미움까지 받는 그런 부류의 아이일 법도 했다."], ["He was dreamy, for instance, and rather solitary.", "가령 그는 몽상적이었고 다소 외톨이였다."], ["From his earliest childhood he was fond of creeping into a corner to read, and yet he was a general favorite all the while he was at school.", "가장 어린 시절부터 그는 한 귀퉁이에 틀어박혀 책 읽기를 좋아했으나, 그러면서도 학교에 다니는 내내 모두의 귀염둥이였다."], ["He was rarely playful or merry, but any one could see at the first glance that this was not from any sullenness.", "그는 좀처럼 장난치거나 들떠 떠드는 일이 없었으나, 누구든 첫눈에 그것이 음울함에서 비롯된 것이 아님을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["On the contrary he was bright and good-tempered.", "오히려 그는 밝고 성격이 좋았다."], ["He never tried to show off among his schoolfellows.", "그는 동무들 사이에서 결코 잘난 체하려 들지 않았다."], ["Perhaps because of this, he was never afraid of any one, yet the boys immediately understood that he was not proud of his fearlessness and seemed to be unaware that he was bold and courageous.", "어쩌면 그래서인지 그는 누구도 두려워하지 않았으나, 소년들은 그가 자기의 그 대담함을 자랑스러워하지 않으며 자신이 담대하고 용감하다는 것조차 모르는 듯하다는 점을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["He never resented an insult.", "그는 결코 모욕에 앙심을 품지 않았다."], ["It would happen that an hour after the offense he would address the offender or answer some question with as trustful and candid an expression as though nothing had happened between them.", "모욕을 당한 지 한 시간이 지나면, 그는 두 사람 사이에 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 미덥고 솔직한 표정으로 그 모욕한 자에게 말을 걸거나 무슨 물음에 대답하는 일이 더러 있었다."], ["And it was not that he seemed to have forgotten or intentionally forgiven the affront, but simply that he did not regard it as an affront, and this completely conquered and captivated the boys.", "그것은 그가 그 모욕을 잊어버린 듯이 보이거나 일부러 용서한 듯이 보여서가 아니라, 단지 그가 그것을 모욕으로 여기지 않았기 때문이었으니, 이것이 소년들을 완전히 정복하고 사로잡았다."], ["He had one characteristic which made all his schoolfellows from the bottom class to the top want to mock at him, not from malice but because it amused them.", "그에게는 한 가지 특성이 있어서, 가장 낮은 학년부터 가장 높은 학년에 이르기까지 모든 동무가 그를 놀려 주고 싶게 만들었는데, 그것은 악의에서가 아니라 그것이 그들을 즐겁게 했기 때문이었다."], ["This characteristic was a wild fanatical modesty and chastity.", "이 특성이란 곧 정도가 지나친 광적인 수줍음과 순결함이었다."], ["He could not bear to hear certain words and certain conversations about women. There are “certain”", "그는 여자에 관한 어떤 말과 어떤 대화를 차마 들을 수가 없었다."], ["words and conversations unhappily impossible to eradicate in schools.", "학교에는 불행히도 뿌리 뽑을 수 없는 \"어떤\" 말과 대화가 있는 법이다."], ["Boys pure in mind and heart, almost children, are fond of talking in school among themselves, and even aloud, of things, pictures, and images of which even soldiers would sometimes hesitate to speak.", "마음과 정신이 순결하며 거의 어린애나 다름없는 소년들이, 학교에서 자기네끼리, 심지어 소리 높여, 군인들조차 더러 입에 담기를 망설일 만한 것들과 그림들과 형상들에 관해 이야기하기를 즐긴다."], ["More than that, much that soldiers have no knowledge or conception of is familiar to quite young children of our intellectual and higher classes.", "그뿐 아니라 군인들이 알지도 못하고 짐작조차 못 하는 많은 것을 우리네 지식 계층과 상류 계층의 제법 어린 아이들이 익히 알고 있다."], ["There is no moral depravity, no real corrupt inner cynicism in it, but there is the appearance of it, and it is often looked upon among them as something refined, subtle, daring, and worthy of imitation.", "거기에 도덕적 타락이나 진정으로 썩어 빠진 내면의 냉소가 있는 것은 아니지만, 그런 겉모양은 있으며, 그것은 그들 사이에서 흔히 세련되고 미묘하며 대담하고 본받을 만한 무엇으로 여겨지곤 한다."], ["Seeing that Alyosha Karamazov put his fingers in his ears when they talked of “that,” they used sometimes to crowd round him, pull his hands away, and shout nastiness into both ears, while he struggled, slipped to the floor, tried to hide himself without uttering one word of abuse, enduring their insults in silence.", "알료샤 카라마조프가 그들이 \"그것\"에 관해 이야기할 때 손가락으로 귀를 틀어막는 것을 보고, 그들은 더러 그를 빙 둘러싸고 그의 두 손을 잡아떼어 양쪽 귀에다 추잡한 소리를 외쳐 대곤 했는데, 그동안 그는 발버둥치다 바닥으로 미끄러지면서도 욕설 한마디 내뱉지 않고 몸을 숨기려 애쓰며 그 모욕을 묵묵히 견뎌 냈다."], ["But at last they left him alone and gave up taunting him with being a “regular girl,” and what’s more they looked upon it with compassion as a weakness.", "그러나 마침내 그들은 그를 내버려 두었고, 그를 \"영락없는 계집애\"라고 놀려 대기를 그만두었으며, 더구나 그것을 일종의 약점으로 여겨 가엾게 보아 주기까지 했다."], ["He was always one of the best in the class but was never first.", "그는 늘 반에서 우등생 가운데 하나였으나 결코 일등을 한 적은 없었다."]], [["At the time of Yefim Petrovitch’s death Alyosha had two more years to complete at the provincial gymnasium.", "예핌 페트로비치가 죽었을 때 알료샤는 지방 김나지움에서 아직 이태를 더 마쳐야 했다."], ["The inconsolable widow went almost immediately after his death for a long visit to Italy with her whole family, which consisted only of women and girls.", "슬픔을 가눌 길 없던 미망인은 그가 죽은 직후 거의 곧바로 온 가족을—오직 여자들과 처녀들뿐인 가족을—데리고 이탈리아로 오랜 여행을 떠났다."], ["Alyosha went to live in the house of two distant relations of Yefim Petrovitch, ladies whom he had never seen before.", "알료샤는 일찍이 한 번도 본 적 없는 예핌 페트로비치의 먼 친척인 두 부인의 집으로 가서 살게 되었다."], ["On what terms he lived with them he did not know himself.", "자신이 그들과 어떤 조건으로 사는지 그 자신도 알지 못했다."], ["It was very characteristic of him, indeed, that he never cared at whose expense he was living.", "실로 그가 누구의 신세를 지며 사는가에 도무지 마음을 쓰지 않았다는 것은 그의 매우 두드러진 특징이었다."], ["In that respect he was a striking contrast to his elder brother Ivan, who struggled with poverty for his first two years in the university, maintained himself by his own efforts, and had from childhood been bitterly conscious of living at the expense of his benefactor.", "이 점에서 그는 자신의 형 이반과 뚜렷이 대조를 이루었으니, 이반은 대학에서의 첫 두 해 동안 가난과 씨름하며 제 힘으로 생계를 꾸렸고, 어릴 적부터 은인의 신세를 지며 산다는 것을 쓰라리게 의식해 왔던 것이다."], ["But this strange trait in Alyosha’s character must not, I think, be criticized too severely, for at the slightest acquaintance with him any one would have perceived that Alyosha was one of those youths, almost of the type of religious enthusiast, who, if they were suddenly to come into possession of a large fortune, would not hesitate to give it away for the asking, either for good works or perhaps to a clever rogue.", "그러나 알료샤 성격의 이 기이한 특질을 너무 가혹하게 나무라서는 안 되리라고 나는 생각하는바, 그를 조금이라도 사귀어 본 사람이라면 누구든 알료샤가 그런 부류의 젊은이, 곧 거의 종교적 열광가에 가까운 부류로서, 만일 갑자기 큰 재산이 손에 들어온다면 누가 청하기만 해도 선행을 위해서든 혹 어쩌면 약삭빠른 사기꾼에게든 그것을 서슴없이 내주어 버릴 그런 젊은이임을 알아보았을 것이기 때문이다."], ["In general he seemed scarcely to know the value of money, not, of course, in a literal sense.", "대체로 그는 돈의 가치를 거의 모르는 듯이 보였는데, 물론 글자 그대로의 뜻에서는 아니다."], ["When he was given pocket-money, which he never asked for, he was either terribly careless of it so that it was gone in a moment, or he kept it for weeks together, not knowing what to do with it.", "그는 결코 달라고 조른 적이 없는 용돈을 받으면, 무섭도록 함부로 다루어 순식간에 다 써 버리거나, 아니면 그것을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 몇 주일씩 그대로 지니고 있었다."]], [["In later years Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, a man very sensitive on the score of money and bourgeois honesty, pronounced the following judgment, after getting to know Alyosha:", "후년에 돈과 부르주아적 정직함의 문제에 매우 예민한 사람인 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프는 알료샤를 알게 된 뒤에 다음과 같은 평을 내렸다."]], [["“Here is perhaps the one man in the world whom you might leave alone without a penny, in the center of an unknown town of a million inhabitants, and he would not come to harm, he would not die of cold and hunger, for he would be fed and sheltered at once; and if he were not, he would find a shelter for himself, and it would cost him no effort or humiliation.", "\"여기 어쩌면 세상에서 단 하나뿐인 사람이 있으니, 그를 백만 인구의 낯선 도시 한복판에 한 푼도 없이 홀로 내버려 두더라도 그는 해를 입지 않을 것이며, 추위와 굶주림으로 죽지도 않을 것이오. 누군가가 당장 그를 먹이고 재워 줄 테니 말이오."], ["And to shelter him would be no burden, but, on the contrary, would probably be looked on as a pleasure.”", "설령 그렇지 않더라도 그는 제 힘으로 거처를 찾아낼 테고, 그것은 그에게 아무런 수고도 굴욕도 들지 않을 것이오. 그리고 그를 재워 주는 것이 짐이 되기는커녕 오히려 즐거움으로 여겨질 게 분명하오.\""]], [["He did not finish his studies at the gymnasium.", "그는 김나지움 학업을 마치지 못했다."], ["A year before the end of the course he suddenly announced to the ladies that he was going to see his father about a plan which had occurred to him.", "과정이 끝나기 한 해 전에 그는 두 부인에게 문득 자기에게 떠오른 어떤 계획에 관해 아버지를 만나러 가겠다고 알렸다."], ["They were sorry and unwilling to let him go.", "그들은 안타까워하며 그를 보내기를 꺼렸다."], ["The journey was not an expensive one, and the ladies would not let him pawn his watch, a parting present from his benefactor’s family.", "여비는 비싸지 않았고, 부인들은 그가 은인의 가족에게서 작별 선물로 받은 시계를 전당 잡히도록 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["They provided him liberally with money and even fitted him out with new clothes and linen.", "그들은 그에게 넉넉히 돈을 마련해 주었고 심지어 새 옷과 속옷까지 갖추어 주었다."], ["But he returned half the money they gave him, saying that he intended to go third class.", "그러나 그는 받은 돈의 절반을 돌려주며 삼등 칸으로 가겠다고 말했다."], ["On his arrival in the town he made no answer to his father’s first inquiry why he had come before completing his studies, and seemed, so they say, unusually thoughtful.", "그 고을에 도착한 뒤, 그는 어찌하여 학업도 마치기 전에 왔느냐는 아버지의 첫 물음에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으며, 사람들의 말로는 유난히 생각에 잠긴 듯이 보였다고 한다."], ["It soon became apparent that he was looking for his mother’s tomb.", "곧 그가 어머니의 무덤을 찾고 있음이 드러났다."], ["He practically acknowledged at the time that that was the only object of his visit.", "그는 그때 실제로 그것이 자기가 찾아온 유일한 목적임을 인정했다."], ["But it can hardly have been the whole reason of it.", "그러나 그것이 그 전부의 이유였을 리는 없다."], ["It is more probable that he himself did not understand and could not explain what had suddenly arisen in his soul, and drawn him irresistibly into a new, unknown, but inevitable path.", "차라리 그 자신도 자기 영혼 속에 무엇이 갑자기 일어나, 자신을 거역할 수 없이 새롭고 알 수 없으나 피할 수 없는 길로 끌어당겼는지 이해하지도 못하고 설명할 수도 없었다고 보는 편이 옳을 것이다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch could not show him where his second wife was buried, for he had never visited her grave since he had thrown earth upon her coffin, and in the course of years had entirely forgotten where she was buried.", "표도르 파블로비치는 자신의 둘째 아내가 어디에 묻혔는지 알료샤에게 알려 줄 수 없었으니, 그는 아내의 관 위에 흙을 뿌린 이래 한 번도 그 무덤을 찾은 적이 없었고, 세월이 흐르는 동안 그녀가 어디에 묻혔는지 까맣게 잊어버렸기 때문이다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch, by the way, had for some time previously not been living in our town.", "그런데 표도르 파블로비치는 얼마 전부터 우리 고을에 살고 있지 않았다."], ["Three or four years after his wife’s death he had gone to the south of Russia and finally turned up in Odessa, where he spent several years.", "아내가 죽고 서너 해가 지난 뒤 그는 러시아 남부로 떠났다가 결국 오데사에 모습을 드러내었고, 그곳에서 몇 해를 보냈다."], ["He made the acquaintance at first, in his own words, “of a lot of low Jews, Jewesses, and Jewkins,”", "그는 처음에 제 말마따나 \"비천한 유대인 놈들과 유대인 여편네들과 유대인 새끼들을 잔뜩\""], ["and ended by being received by “Jews high and low alike.”", "알게 되었고, 마침내 \"지체 높은 유대인이든 낮은 유대인이든 가리지 않고\""], ["It may be presumed that at this period he developed a peculiar faculty for making and hoarding money.", "그들에게 받아들여지는 데까지 이르렀다. 짐작건대 이 시기에 그는 돈을 벌어 그러모으는 독특한 재주를 키운 모양이다."], ["He finally returned to our town only three years before Alyosha’s arrival.", "그가 마침내 우리 고을로 돌아온 것은 알료샤가 도착하기 겨우 세 해 전이었다."], ["His former acquaintances found him looking terribly aged, although he was by no means an old man.", "그의 옛 지인들은 그가 결코 노인이라 할 나이가 아닌데도 무섭도록 늙어 보인다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["He behaved not exactly with more dignity but with more effrontery.", "그의 처신은 위엄이 더해졌다기보다는 차라리 뻔뻔함이 더해진 것이었다."], ["The former buffoon showed an insolent propensity for making buffoons of others.", "한때의 어릿광대는 이제 남을 어릿광대로 만들려는 오만한 버릇을 드러내었다."], ["His depravity with women was not simply what it used to be, but even more revolting.", "여자에 대한 그의 방탕은 그저 예전 그대로가 아니라 더욱 역겨워졌다."], ["In a short time he opened a great number of new taverns in the district.", "얼마 안 가서 그는 그 일대에 새 술집을 여럿 열었다."], ["It was evident that he had perhaps a hundred thousand roubles or not much less.", "그가 십만 루블, 혹은 그에 그리 못지않은 돈을 지녔으리라는 것이 분명했다."], ["Many of the inhabitants of the town and district were soon in his debt, and, of course, had given good security.", "고을과 일대의 많은 주민이 곧 그에게 빚을 졌고, 물론 든든한 담보를 잡혔다."], ["Of late, too, he looked somehow bloated and seemed more irresponsible, more uneven, had sunk into a sort of incoherence, used to begin one thing and go on with another, as though he were letting himself go altogether.", "또 근래에 그는 어쩐지 부어오른 듯이 보였고 한층 무책임하고 더 변덕스러워졌으며, 일종의 종잡을 수 없는 상태에 빠져, 한 가지 일을 시작했다가 딴 일로 넘어가곤 하는 것이 마치 자신을 송두리째 내버려 두는 듯했다."], ["He was more and more frequently drunk.", "그는 점점 더 자주 술에 취했다."], ["And, if it had not been for the same servant Grigory, who by that time had aged considerably too, and used to look after him sometimes almost like a tutor, Fyodor Pavlovitch might have got into terrible scrapes.", "그리고 만일 그 무렵 역시 상당히 늙어 버려 때로는 거의 가정교사처럼 그를 돌봐 주던 바로 그 하인 그리고리가 없었더라면, 표도르 파블로비치는 끔찍한 곤경에 빠졌을지도 모른다."], ["Alyosha’s arrival seemed to affect even his moral side, as though something had awakened in this prematurely old man which had long been dead in his soul.", "알료샤가 온 것은 그의 도덕적인 면까지도 건드린 듯했으니, 마치 이 일찍 늙어 버린 사내의 영혼 속에 오래도록 죽어 있던 무언가가 깨어난 것만 같았다."]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"이거 아느냐,\""], ["he used often to say, looking at Alyosha, “that you are like her, ‘the crazy woman’”—that was what he used to call his dead wife, Alyosha’s mother.", "그는 알료샤를 바라보며 자주 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"너는 그 여자를, '그 미친 여자'를 꼭 닮았다.\" 그것은 그가 죽은 아내, 곧 알료샤의 어머니를 부르던 말이었다."], ["Grigory it was who pointed out the “crazy woman’s” grave to Alyosha.", "\"그 미친 여자\"의 무덤을 알료샤에게 가리켜 준 것은 그리고리였다."], ["He took him to our town cemetery and showed him in a remote corner a cast-iron tombstone, cheap but decently kept, on which were inscribed the name and age of the deceased and the date of her death, and below a four-lined verse, such as are commonly used on old-fashioned middle-class tombs.", "그는 알료샤를 우리 고을 묘지로 데려가, 외진 한 귀퉁이에서 값은 싸지만 제법 단정하게 손질된 무쇠 묘비 하나를 보여 주었는데, 거기에는 고인의 이름과 나이와 사망 날짜가 새겨져 있었고 그 아래에는 옛날 중류층 무덤에 흔히 쓰이는 그런 넉 줄짜리 시구가 적혀 있었다."], ["To Alyosha’s amazement this tomb turned out to be Grigory’s doing.", "알료샤가 놀랍게도 이 무덤은 그리고리가 손수 마련한 것으로 드러났다."], ["He had put it up on the poor “crazy woman’s” grave at his own expense, after Fyodor Pavlovitch, whom he had often pestered about the grave, had gone to Odessa, abandoning the grave and all his memories.", "표도르 파블로비치가, 그토록 무덤 일로 자주 졸라 댔건만, 무덤도 모든 추억도 다 내버린 채 오데사로 떠나 버린 뒤, 그가 제 돈을 들여 가엾은 \"미친 여자\"의 무덤 위에 그것을 세워 놓았던 것이다."], ["Alyosha showed no particular emotion at the sight of his mother’s grave.", "알료샤는 어머니의 무덤을 보고도 별다른 감정을 드러내지 않았다."], ["He only listened to Grigory’s minute and solemn account of the erection of the tomb; he stood with bowed head and walked away without uttering a word.", "그는 다만 무덤을 세운 경위에 관한 그리고리의 자상하고 엄숙한 이야기에 귀를 기울였을 뿐, 고개를 숙인 채 서 있다가 한마디 말도 없이 돌아서 갔다."], ["It was perhaps a year before he visited the cemetery again.", "그가 다시 묘지를 찾기까지는 아마 한 해쯤 걸렸을 것이다."], ["But this little episode was not without an influence upon Fyodor Pavlovitch—and a very original one.", "그러나 이 자그마한 일화도 표도르 파블로비치에게 영향을 미치지 않은 것은 아니었으니—그것도 아주 별난 영향이었다."], ["He suddenly took a thousand roubles to our monastery to pay for requiems for the soul of his wife; but not for the second, Alyosha’s mother, the “crazy woman,” but for the first, Adelaïda Ivanovna, who used to thrash him.", "그는 문득 우리 수도원에 천 루블을 가져가 죽은 아내의 영혼을 위한 진혼 미사를 부탁했는데, 그것은 둘째 아내인 알료샤의 어머니, 곧 \"미친 여자\"를 위한 것이 아니라, 그를 두들겨 패곤 하던 첫 아내 아델라이다 이바노브나를 위한 것이었다."], ["In the evening of the same day he got drunk and abused the monks to Alyosha.", "같은 날 저녁 그는 술에 취해 알료샤 앞에서 수도사들을 욕해 댔다."], ["He himself was far from being religious; he had probably never put a penny candle before the image of a saint.", "그 자신은 신앙심과는 거리가 멀었으니, 아마 성인의 성상 앞에 동전 한 푼짜리 양초조차 바쳐 본 적이 없었을 것이다."], ["Strange impulses of sudden feeling and sudden thought are common in such types.", "갑작스러운 감정과 갑작스러운 생각의 기이한 충동은 그런 부류에게 흔한 일이다."]], [["I have mentioned already that he looked bloated.", "나는 이미 그가 부어오른 듯이 보였다고 말한 바 있다."], ["His countenance at this time bore traces of something that testified unmistakably to the life he had led.", "이 무렵 그의 얼굴에는 그가 살아온 삶을 틀림없이 증언하는 무언가의 흔적이 새겨져 있었다."], ["Besides the long fleshy bags under his little, always insolent, suspicious, and ironical eyes; besides the multitude of deep wrinkles in his little fat face, the Adam’s apple hung below his sharp chin like a great, fleshy goiter, which gave him a peculiar, repulsive, sensual appearance; add to that a long rapacious mouth with full lips, between which could be seen little stumps of black decayed teeth.", "늘 오만하고 의심스럽고 비웃는 듯한 그 작은 두 눈 밑에 늘어진 길쭉한 살주머니 말고도, 그 작고 살진 얼굴에 깊이 팬 무수한 주름살 말고도, 뾰족한 턱 아래에는 커다란 살덩이 갑상선종처럼 울대뼈가 달랑거려 그에게 유난히 역겨운 호색의 인상을 풍겼으니, 거기에 두툼한 입술을 가진 길쭉하고 탐욕스러운 입까지 보태면, 그 입술 사이로는 까맣게 썩은 이의 자그마한 그루터기들이 들여다보였다."], ["He slobbered every time he began to speak.", "그는 말을 시작할 때마다 침을 질질 흘렸다."], ["He was fond indeed of making fun of his own face, though, I believe, he was well satisfied with it.", "그는 실로 자기 얼굴을 두고 농지거리하기를 좋아했으나, 내 생각에 그는 자기 얼굴에 흡족해하고 있었다."], ["He used particularly to point to his nose, which was not very large, but very delicate and conspicuously aquiline.", "그는 특히 자기 코를 가리켜 보이곤 했는데, 그리 크지는 않으나 매우 섬세하고 두드러지게 매부리 모양이었다."], ["“A regular Roman nose,”", "\"영락없는 로마식 코지,\""], ["he used to say, “with my goiter I’ve quite the countenance of an ancient Roman patrician of the decadent period.”", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했다, \"이 갑상선종까지 곁들이면 나는 영판 쇠퇴기 고대 로마 귀족의 상판이라니까.\""], ["He seemed proud of it.", "그는 그것을 자랑스러워하는 듯했다."]], [["Not long after visiting his mother’s grave Alyosha suddenly announced that he wanted to enter the monastery, and that the monks were willing to receive him as a novice.", "어머니의 무덤을 찾은 지 얼마 지나지 않아 알료샤는 문득 자기가 수도원에 들어가고 싶으며, 수도사들도 자기를 수련 수사로 받아들일 뜻이 있다고 알렸다."], ["He explained that this was his strong desire, and that he was solemnly asking his consent as his father.", "그는 이것이 자신의 간절한 소망이며, 아버지로서 그의 허락을 정중히 구한다고 설명했다."], ["The old man knew that the elder Zossima, who was living in the monastery hermitage, had made a special impression upon his “gentle boy.”", "노인은 수도원 암자에 살고 있는 조시마 장로가 자신의 \"온순한 아들\"에게 특별한 감화를 주었음을 알고 있었다."]], [["“That is the most honest monk among them, of course,”", "\"하기야 그 사람이야 거기서 가장 정직한 수도사지,\""], ["he observed, after listening in thoughtful silence to Alyosha, and seeming scarcely surprised at his request.", "그는 생각에 잠긴 채 잠자코 알료샤의 말에 귀를 기울이고 나서, 그 청에 별로 놀라지도 않은 듯이 이렇게 말했다."], ["“H’m!...", "\"흠!……"], ["So that’s where you want to be, my gentle boy?”", "그래, 거기가 네가 가고 싶은 데란 말이지, 내 온순한 아들아?\""]], [["He was half drunk, and suddenly he grinned his slow half-drunken grin, which was not without a certain cunning and tipsy slyness.", "그는 반쯤 취해 있었는데, 갑자기 그 느릿한 반취반성의 히죽거림을 지었으니, 거기에는 어딘가 교활하고 술기운 어린 능청스러움이 없지 않았다."], ["“H’m!...", "\"흠!……"], ["I had a presentiment that you would end in something like this.", "나는 네가 결국 이런 식으로 끝장날 거라는 예감이 들었더랬다."], ["Would you believe it?", "믿어지느냐?"], ["You were making straight for it.", "너는 곧장 그리로 향하고 있었어."], ["Well, to be sure you have your own two thousand.", "그래, 하긴 너에게는 네 몫의 이천 루블이 있지."], ["That’s a dowry for you.", "그게 네 지참금인 셈이야."], ["And I’ll never desert you, my angel.", "그리고 나는 결코 너를 저버리지 않으마, 내 천사야."], ["And I’ll pay what’s wanted for you there, if they ask for it.", "그리고 거기서 너를 위해 무엇이든 필요한 게 있으면, 저들이 청하기만 하면 내가 치러 주마."], ["But, of course, if they don’t ask, why should we worry them?", "하지만 물론 저들이 청하지 않으면야, 우리가 무엇하러 들볶겠느냐? 어떠냐?"], ["What do you say? You know, you spend money like a canary, two grains a week.", "너야 알다시피 카나리아처럼 한 주에 좁쌀 두 알밖에 안 쓰는 녀석이니 말이다."], ["H’m!...", "흠!……"], ["Do you know that near one monastery there’s a place outside the town where every baby knows there are none but ‘the monks’ wives’ living, as they are called.", "너 아느냐, 어느 수도원 가까이, 그 고을 밖에 '수도사의 마누라들'이라 불리는 여편네들만 산다는 걸 갓난애도 다 아는 그런 데가 있다는 걸 말이다."], ["Thirty women, I believe.", "내 알기로 서른 명쯤 돼."], ["I have been there myself.", "나도 거기 가 봤다."], ["You know, it’s interesting in its own way, of course, as a variety.", "너 알다시피, 그것도 그것대로 흥미롭거든, 별미라면 별미니까."], ["The worst of it is it’s awfully Russian.", "무엇보다 고약한 건 그게 지독하게 러시아적이라는 거다."], ["There are no French women there.", "거기엔 프랑스 여자가 없어."], ["Of course they could get them fast enough, they have plenty of money.", "물론 마음만 먹으면야 금세 데려올 수 있지, 돈이야 넘쳐 나니까."], ["If they get to hear of it they’ll come along.", "소문만 들으면 당장 몰려올걸."], ["Well, there’s nothing of that sort here, no ‘monks’ wives,’ and two hundred monks.", "자, 여기는 그런 게 전혀 없어, '수도사의 마누라들' 같은 건 없고 수도사가 이백 명이지."], ["They’re honest. They keep the fasts.", "그자들은 정직해."], ["I admit it....", "단식도 지키고."], ["H’m.... So you want to be a monk?", "인정한다……흠……그래, 너는 수도사가 되겠단 말이지?"], ["And do you know I’m sorry to lose you, Alyosha; would you believe it, I’ve really grown fond of you?", "그런데 너 아느냐, 나는 너를 떠나보내는 게 섭섭하구나, 알료샤. 믿어지느냐, 내가 정말로 너를 좋아하게 됐단 말이다?"], ["Well, it’s a good opportunity.", "뭐, 그래도 좋은 기회지."], ["You’ll pray for us sinners; we have sinned too much here.", "너는 우리 죄인들을 위해 기도해 주겠지. 우리는 여기서 너무 많은 죄를 지었으니까."], ["I’ve always been thinking who would pray for me, and whether there’s any one in the world to do it.", "나는 늘 누가 나를 위해 기도해 줄까, 세상에 그래 줄 사람이 하나라도 있을까 생각해 왔다."], ["My dear boy, I’m awfully stupid about that.", "사랑하는 아들아, 나는 그 일에 대해선 지독하게 멍청하구나."], ["You wouldn’t believe it.", "믿어지지 않겠지. 지독하게 말이다."], ["Awfully. You see, however stupid I am about it, I keep thinking, I keep thinking—from time to time, of course, not all the while.", "너도 알다시피, 내가 그 일에 아무리 멍청해도 자꾸 생각하게 되는 거야, 자꾸 생각하게 돼—물론 늘 그러는 건 아니고 이따금이다만."], ["It’s impossible, I think, for the devils to forget to drag me down to hell with their hooks when I die.", "내 생각엔 말이다, 내가 죽을 때 악마들이 그 갈고리로 나를 지옥에 끌어내리는 걸 잊어버릴 리가 없을 것 같단 말이야."], ["Then I wonder—hooks? Where would they get them?", "그러면 또 이런 의문이 들거든—갈고리라고?"], ["What of? Iron hooks?", "그걸 어디서 구한담?"], ["Where do they forge them?", "무얼로 만들었을까?"], ["Have they a foundry there of some sort?", "쇠 갈고리? 그걸 어디서 벼려 낸담?"], ["The monks in the monastery probably believe that there’s a ceiling in hell, for instance.", "거기 무슨 주물 공장이라도 있는 걸까? 수도원 수도사들이야 아마 가령 지옥에 천장이 있다고 믿겠지."], ["Now I’m ready to believe in hell, but without a ceiling.", "한데 나는 지옥은 믿을 채비가 돼 있다만, 천장 없는 지옥이라야 해."], ["It makes it more refined, more enlightened, more Lutheran that is.", "그래야 한결 세련되고 한결 계몽되고 한결 루터교다워지거든."], ["And, after all, what does it matter whether it has a ceiling or hasn’t?", "그리고 따지고 보면 천장이 있건 없건 그게 무슨 대수냐?"], ["But, do you know, there’s a damnable question involved in it?", "한데 너 아느냐, 거기엔 망할 놈의 문제가 하나 얽혀 있단 말이다."], ["If there’s no ceiling there can be no hooks, and if there are no hooks it all breaks down, which is unlikely again, for then there would be none to drag me down to hell, and if they don’t drag me down what justice is there in the world?", "천장이 없으면 갈고리도 있을 수 없고, 갈고리가 없으면 죄다 무너지는 거야. 한데 그것도 영 그럴듯하지가 않은 게, 그러면 나를 지옥으로 끌어내릴 자가 아무도 없을 거고, 나를 끌어내리지 않는다면 세상에 무슨 정의가 있겠느냐?"], ["Il faudrait les inventer, those hooks, on purpose for me alone, for, if you only knew, Alyosha, what a blackguard I am.”", "그 갈고리들을 나 하나만을 위해 일부러라도 만들어 내야 할 판이지(Il faudrait les inventer), 왜냐하면 알료샤야, 네가 알기만 한다면 말이다, 내가 얼마나 못돼 먹은 놈인지 말이다.\""]], [["“But there are no hooks there,”", "\"하지만 거기엔 갈고리 같은 건 없어요.\""], ["said Alyosha, looking gently and seriously at his father.", "알료샤가 아버지를 부드럽고 진지하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, only the shadows of hooks, I know, I know.", "\"그래, 그래, 갈고리의 그림자뿐이겠지, 알아, 알아."], ["That’s how a Frenchman described hell: ‘J’ai vu l’ombre d’un cocher qui avec l’ombre d’une brosse frottait l’ombre d’une carrosse.’ How do you know there are no hooks, darling?", "어느 프랑스 사람이 지옥을 그렇게 묘사했더랬다. '나는 한 마부의 그림자가 솔 한 자루의 그림자로 마차 한 대의 그림자를 닦고 있는 것을 보았노라(J'ai vu l'ombre d'un cocher qui avec l'ombre d'une brosse frottait l'ombre d'une carrosse).'"], ["When you’ve lived with the monks you’ll sing a different tune.", "한데 거기 갈고리가 없다는 걸 네가 어찌 아느냐, 얘야? 수도사들하고 좀 살아 보면 너도 딴소리를 하게 될걸."], ["But go and get at the truth there, and then come and tell me.", "하지만 거기 가서 진리를 찾아내거든, 그러거든 와서 나한테 말해 다오."], ["Anyway it’s easier going to the other world if one knows what there is there.", "어쨌든 저세상에 무엇이 있는지 알고 가는 편이 한결 수월할 테니까."], ["Besides, it will be more seemly for you with the monks than here with me, with a drunken old man and young harlots ...", "게다가 술 취한 늙은이하고 젊은 갈보들하고 여기 나하고 있는 것보다는 수도사들하고 있는 게 너한테 더 어울리기도 하고……"], ["though you’re like an angel, nothing touches you.", "하긴 너야 천사 같으니, 무엇 하나 너를 더럽히지 못하지."], ["And I dare say nothing will touch you there.", "그리고 거기서도 아마 무엇 하나 너를 더럽히지 못할 거다."], ["That’s why I let you go, because I hope for that.", "내가 너를 보내 주는 것도 그래서야, 그걸 바라기 때문이지."], ["You’ve got all your wits about you.", "너는 정신이 멀쩡하니까."], ["You will burn and you will burn out; you will be healed and come back again.", "너는 타오를 테고 또 다 타 버릴 테지. 그러면 치유되어 다시 돌아올 거야."], ["And I will wait for you.", "그러면 나는 너를 기다리마."], ["I feel that you’re the only creature in the world who has not condemned me.", "나는 느낀단다, 네가 나를 단죄하지 않은 세상의 단 하나뿐인 존재라는 걸 말이다."], ["My dear boy, I feel it, you know.", "사랑하는 아들아, 나는 그걸 느껴, 알다시피."], ["I can’t help feeling it.”", "느끼지 않을 수가 없구나.\""]], [["And he even began blubbering.", "그러고는 그는 엉엉 울기까지 했다."], ["He was sentimental.", "그는 감상적이었다."], ["He was wicked and sentimental.", "그는 사악하면서도 감상적이었다."]], [["# Chapter V. Elders", "# Chapter V. Elders"]], [["Some of my readers may imagine that my young man was a sickly, ecstatic, poorly developed creature, a pale, consumptive dreamer.", "내 독자들 가운데 더러는 이 젊은이가 병약하고 황홀경에 잠긴, 제대로 자라지 못한 위인, 곧 창백하고 폐병이나 앓을 법한 몽상가라고 상상할지도 모른다."], ["On the contrary, Alyosha was at this time a well-grown, red-cheeked, clear-eyed lad of nineteen, radiant with health.", "그러나 그와는 반대로, 알료샤는 이 무렵 건강이 넘쳐흐르는 열아홉 살의 잘 자란 청년으로, 뺨이 붉고 눈이 맑았다."], ["He was very handsome, too, graceful, moderately tall, with hair of a dark brown, with a regular, rather long, oval-shaped face, and wide-set dark gray, shining eyes; he was very thoughtful, and apparently very serene.", "그는 또한 무척 잘생겼고, 몸매가 곱고, 키는 알맞게 컸으며, 머리칼은 짙은 갈색이었고, 얼굴은 단정하고 다소 갸름한 타원형이었으며, 좌우로 멀찍이 자리한 짙은 잿빛 눈은 반짝였다. 그는 무척 생각이 깊었고, 보아하니 무척 평온한 사람이었다."], ["I shall be told, perhaps, that red cheeks are not incompatible with fanaticism and mysticism; but I fancy that Alyosha was more of a realist than any one. Oh!", "어쩌면 누군가는 붉은 뺨이 광신이나 신비주의와 양립하지 못할 까닭은 없다고 내게 말할지도 모른다."], ["no doubt, in the monastery he fully believed in miracles, but, to my thinking, miracles are never a stumbling-block to the realist.", "그러나 내가 보기에 알료샤는 누구보다도 더한 현실주의자였다. 오, 물론 수도원에서 그는 기적을 전적으로 믿었다."], ["It is not miracles that dispose realists to belief.", "그러나 내 생각으로는 기적이란 현실주의자에게 결코 걸림돌이 되지 못한다."], ["The genuine realist, if he is an unbeliever, will always find strength and ability to disbelieve in the miraculous, and if he is confronted with a miracle as an irrefutable fact he would rather disbelieve his own senses than admit the fact.", "현실주의자를 믿음으로 이끄는 것은 기적이 아니다. 진정한 현실주의자란, 만일 그가 믿지 않는 자라면 언제나 기적적인 것을 믿지 않을 힘과 능력을 찾아낼 것이며, 또 기적이 반박할 수 없는 사실로 그 앞에 들이닥친다 해도 그는 그 사실을 인정하느니 차라리 제 감각을 믿지 않으려 들 것이다."], ["Even if he admits it, he admits it as a fact of nature till then unrecognized by him.", "설령 그가 그것을 인정한다 해도, 그것을 그때껏 자신이 알지 못했던 자연의 한 사실로서 인정하는 것이다."], ["Faith does not, in the realist, spring from the miracle but the miracle from faith.", "현실주의자에게 믿음은 기적에서 솟아나는 것이 아니라, 도리어 기적이 믿음에서 솟아난다."], ["If the realist once believes, then he is bound by his very realism to admit the miraculous also.", "현실주의자가 일단 믿게 되면, 그는 바로 그 현실주의 때문에 기적 또한 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["The Apostle Thomas said that he would not believe till he saw, but when he did see he said, “My Lord and my God!”", "사도 토마스는 보기 전에는 믿지 않겠노라고 했으나, 막상 보고 나서는 \"나의 주, 나의 하느님!\" 하고 외쳤다."], ["Was it the miracle forced him to believe?", "그를 믿게 만든 것이 기적이었던가?"], ["Most likely not, but he believed solely because he desired to believe and possibly he fully believed in his secret heart even when he said, “I do not believe till I see.”", "십중팔구 아니었으니, 그는 다만 믿고자 갈망했기에 믿었던 것이며, 어쩌면 \"보기 전에는 믿지 않겠다\"고 말하던 바로 그 순간에도 마음 깊은 곳에서는 이미 온전히 믿고 있었을 것이다."]], [["I shall be told, perhaps, that Alyosha was stupid, undeveloped, had not finished his studies, and so on.", "어쩌면 누군가는 내게 알료샤가 어리석고 미숙하며 학업도 마치지 못했다는 따위의 말을 할지도 모른다."], ["That he did not finish his studies is true, but to say that he was stupid or dull would be a great injustice.", "그가 학업을 마치지 못한 것은 사실이지만, 그를 어리석다거나 둔하다고 한다면 그것은 큰 부당함이 될 것이다."], ["I’ll simply repeat what I have said above.", "나는 그저 위에서 한 말을 다시 되풀이하겠다."], ["He entered upon this path only because, at that time, it alone struck his imagination and presented itself to him as offering an ideal means of escape for his soul from darkness to light.", "그가 이 길로 들어선 것은, 오로지 그 무렵에 그것만이 그의 상상을 사로잡았고, 어둠에서 빛으로 그의 영혼이 벗어날 이상적인 탈출구로 그에게 비쳤기 때문이다."], ["Add to that that he was to some extent a youth of our last epoch—that is, honest in nature, desiring the truth, seeking for it and believing in it, and seeking to serve it at once with all the strength of his soul, seeking for immediate action, and ready to sacrifice everything, life itself, for it.", "거기에 덧붙이자면 그는 어느 정도 우리 지난 시대의 청년이었으니—곧 본성이 정직하고, 진리를 갈망하며, 그것을 찾고 또 믿으며, 자기 영혼의 온 힘을 다해 당장 그것에 헌신하고자 하고, 즉각적인 행동을 추구하며, 그것을 위해서라면 무엇이든, 목숨 그 자체까지도 바칠 채비가 된 그런 청년이었다."], ["Though these young men unhappily fail to understand that the sacrifice of life is, in many cases, the easiest of all sacrifices, and that to sacrifice, for instance, five or six years of their seething youth to hard and tedious study, if only to multiply tenfold their powers of serving the truth and the cause they have set before them as their goal—such a sacrifice is utterly beyond the strength of many of them.", "비록 이런 젊은이들은 안타깝게도, 목숨을 바치는 일이 많은 경우 모든 희생 가운데 가장 손쉬운 것임을, 그리고 이를테면 자기들이 목표로 세운 진리와 대의를 섬길 힘을 열 배로 키우기 위해서라도 끓어오르는 청춘의 다섯 해나 여섯 해를 고되고 따분한 공부에 바치는 일—그런 희생이야말로 그들 가운데 많은 이의 힘으로는 도저히 감당할 수 없는 것임을 깨닫지 못하지만 말이다."], ["The path Alyosha chose was a path going in the opposite direction, but he chose it with the same thirst for swift achievement.", "알료샤가 택한 길은 그와는 정반대 방향으로 난 길이었으되, 그는 그 길 또한 빠른 성취를 향한 똑같은 갈망으로 택했다."], ["As soon as he reflected seriously he was convinced of the existence of God and immortality, and at once he instinctively said to himself: “I want to live for immortality, and I will accept no compromise.”", "진지하게 사색하기 시작하자마자 그는 신과 불멸의 존재를 확신했고, 곧장 본능적으로 자신에게 일렀다. \"나는 불멸을 위해 살고 싶다, 그리고 어떤 타협도 받아들이지 않겠다.\""], ["In the same way, if he had decided that God and immortality did not exist, he would at once have become an atheist and a socialist.", "마찬가지로, 만일 그가 신과 불멸이 존재하지 않는다고 결론지었다면, 그는 곧장 무신론자가 되고 사회주의자가 되었을 것이다."], ["For socialism is not merely the labor question, it is before all things the atheistic question, the question of the form taken by atheism to-day, the question of the tower of Babel built without God, not to mount to heaven from earth but to set up heaven on earth.", "사회주의란 한낱 노동 문제가 아니라, 무엇보다도 먼저 무신론의 문제, 곧 오늘날 무신론이 띠게 된 형태의 문제이며, 신 없이 세워진 바벨탑의 문제, 곧 땅에서 하늘로 오르기 위함이 아니라 땅 위에 하늘을 세우려는 문제인 것이다."], ["Alyosha would have found it strange and impossible to go on living as before.", "알료샤로서는 예전처럼 살아간다는 것이 기이하고 불가능한 일로 여겨졌으리라."], ["It is written: “Give all that thou hast to the poor and follow Me, if thou wouldst be perfect.”", "이렇게 기록되어 있지 않은가. \"네가 온전하고자 하거든, 네게 있는 모든 것을 가난한 자들에게 주고 나를 따르라.\""]], [["Alyosha said to himself: “I can’t give two roubles instead of ‘all,’ and only go to mass instead of ‘following Him.’”", "알료샤는 자신에게 일렀다. \"나는 '모든 것'"], ["Perhaps his memories of childhood brought back our monastery, to which his mother may have taken him to mass.", "대신 두 루블만 줄 수도, '그분을 따르는' 대신 미사에만 다닐 수도 없다.\""], ["Perhaps the slanting sunlight and the holy image to which his poor “crazy” mother had held him up still acted upon his imagination.", "어쩌면 어린 시절의 기억이 우리 수도원을 떠올리게 했는지도 모르니, 그의 어머니가 그를 그곳 미사에 데려갔을 수도 있는 것이다."], ["Brooding on these things he may have come to us perhaps only to see whether here he could sacrifice all or only “two roubles,”", "어쩌면 비스듬히 비쳐 들던 햇살과, 가엾고 \"정신 나간\" 어머니가 그를 높이 들어 올려 보이던 성상이 여전히 그의 상상에 작용하고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["and in the monastery he met this elder. I must digress to explain what an “elder”", "이런 것들을 깊이 곱씹다가 그는 어쩌면 이곳에서 자신이 모든 것을 바칠 수 있을지, 아니면 그저 \"두 루블\"만 바칠 수 있을지 알아보려는 마음만으로 우리에게 왔는지도 모르며, 그리하여 수도원에서 이 장로를 만난 것이다."], ["is in Russian monasteries, and I am sorry that I do not feel very competent to do so. I will try, however, to give a superficial account of it in a few words.", "여기서 나는 러시아 수도원에서 \"장로\"가 무엇인지 설명하기 위해 곁길로 빠지지 않을 수 없는데, 그 일에 그리 능하지 못함이 유감스럽다."], ["Authorities on the subject assert that the institution of “elders”", "그래도 몇 마디로 겉핥기식 설명이나마 해 보고자 한다. 이 문제의 권위자들은 \"장로\""], ["is of recent date, not more than a hundred years old in our monasteries, though in the orthodox East, especially in Sinai and Athos, it has existed over a thousand years.", "제도가 근래에 생긴 것으로, 우리 수도원에서는 백 년이 채 안 되었다고 단언하나, 정교회의 동방, 특히 시나이와 아토스에서는 천 년이 넘도록 존속해 왔다고 한다."], ["It is maintained that it existed in ancient times in Russia also, but through the calamities which overtook Russia—the Tartars, civil war, the interruption of relations with the East after the destruction of Constantinople—this institution fell into oblivion.", "그것이 러시아에서도 옛날에 존재했었다는 주장이 있으나, 러시아를 덮친 재앙들—타타르의 침략, 내전, 콘스탄티노플 함락 이후 동방과의 관계가 끊긴 일—로 말미암아 이 제도는 망각 속에 묻혀 버렸다고 한다."], ["It was revived among us towards the end of last century by one of the great “ascetics,”", "그것이 우리 사이에서 되살아난 것은 지난 세기말, 사람들이 일컫던 대로 위대한 \"고행자\""], ["as they called him, Païssy Velitchkovsky, and his disciples.", "가운데 한 사람인 파이시 벨리치콥스키와 그 제자들에 의해서였다."], ["But to this day it exists in few monasteries only, and has sometimes been almost persecuted as an innovation in Russia.", "그러나 그것은 오늘날까지도 몇몇 수도원에만 존재하며, 러시아에서는 이따금 새로운 폐단이라 하여 거의 박해받다시피 한 일도 있었다."], ["It flourished especially in the celebrated Kozelski Optin Monastery.", "그것은 특히 저 유명한 코젤스카야 옵틴 수도원에서 융성했다."], ["When and how it was introduced into our monastery I cannot say.", "그것이 언제 어떻게 우리 수도원에 도입되었는지는 나도 알 수 없다."], ["There had already been three such elders and Zossima was the last of them.", "이미 그런 장로가 셋이나 있었고, 조시마는 그 마지막 사람이었다."], ["But he was almost dying of weakness and disease, and they had no one to take his place.", "그러나 그는 쇠약과 병으로 거의 죽어 가고 있었고, 그의 자리를 대신할 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["The question for our monastery was an important one, for it had not been distinguished by anything in particular till then: they had neither relics of saints, nor wonder-working ikons, nor glorious traditions, nor historical exploits.", "이는 우리 수도원으로서는 중대한 문제였으니, 그때껏 이렇다 할 무엇으로도 두드러진 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그곳에는 성인의 유물도, 기적을 일으키는 성상도, 영광스러운 전통도, 역사적 위업도 없었다."], ["It had flourished and been glorious all over Russia through its elders, to see and hear whom pilgrims had flocked for thousands of miles from all parts.", "그곳이 온 러시아에 두루 융성하고 영광을 누린 것은 오로지 그 장로들 덕분이었으니, 그들을 보고 그 말씀을 듣고자 사방 천 리 길에서 순례자들이 떼 지어 몰려들었던 것이다."]], [["What was such an elder?", "그런 장로란 무엇이었던가?"], ["An elder was one who took your soul, your will, into his soul and his will.", "장로란 그대의 영혼과 그대의 의지를 자기 영혼과 자기 의지 속으로 받아들이는 사람이었다."], ["When you choose an elder, you renounce your own will and yield it to him in complete submission, complete self-abnegation.", "그대가 한 장로를 택할 때, 그대는 자신의 의지를 버리고 완전한 복종, 완전한 자기 부정으로써 그것을 그에게 내맡기는 것이다."], ["This novitiate, this terrible school of abnegation, is undertaken voluntarily, in the hope of self-conquest, of self-mastery, in order, after a life of obedience, to attain perfect freedom, that is, from self; to escape the lot of those who have lived their whole life without finding their true selves in themselves.", "이 수련, 이 무서운 자기 부정의 학교는 자발적으로 받아들이는 것으로, 자기 정복과 자기 통제의 희망 속에서, 복종의 생애를 거친 뒤에 완전한 자유, 곧 자기 자신으로부터의 자유에 이르기 위함이며, 평생을 살면서도 끝내 자기 안에서 참된 자아를 찾아내지 못한 자들의 운명을 면하기 위함이다."], ["This institution of elders is not founded on theory, but was established in the East from the practice of a thousand years.", "이 장로 제도는 이론에 근거를 둔 것이 아니라, 동방에서 천 년에 걸친 실천으로부터 확립된 것이다."], ["The obligations due to an elder are not the ordinary “obedience” which has always existed in our Russian monasteries.", "장로에 대한 의무는 우리 러시아 수도원에 늘 있어 온 보통의 \"복종\"과는 다르다."], ["The obligation involves confession to the elder by all who have submitted themselves to him, and to the indissoluble bond between him and them.", "그 의무에는 그에게 자신을 내맡긴 모든 이가 장로에게 고해를 바치는 일과, 그와 그들 사이의 끊을 수 없는 유대가 포함된다."]], [["The story is told, for instance, that in the early days of Christianity one such novice, failing to fulfill some command laid upon him by his elder, left his monastery in Syria and went to Egypt.", "이를테면 이런 이야기가 전해진다. 그리스도교 초창기에 한 수련 수사가 자기 장로가 내린 어떤 명령을 이행하지 않고서 시리아의 수도원을 떠나 이집트로 갔다."], ["There, after great exploits, he was found worthy at last to suffer torture and a martyr’s death for the faith.", "그곳에서 그는 큰 위업을 쌓은 끝에 마침내 신앙을 위해 고문과 순교의 죽음을 당할 만한 자로 인정받았다."], ["When the Church, regarding him as a saint, was burying him, suddenly, at the deacon’s exhortation, “Depart all ye unbaptized,”", "교회가 그를 성인으로 여겨 장사 지내려 할 때, 부제가 \"세례받지 않은 자들은 모두 물러가라\""], ["the coffin containing the martyr’s body left its place and was cast forth from the church, and this took place three times.", "하고 권고하는 순간 별안간 순교자의 시신이 든 관이 제자리를 떠나 교회 밖으로 내던져졌는데, 이런 일이 세 번이나 거듭되었다."], ["And only at last they learnt that this holy man had broken his vow of obedience and left his elder, and, therefore, could not be forgiven without the elder’s absolution in spite of his great deeds.", "그리고 마침내 사람들은 이 거룩한 사람이 복종의 서약을 깨고 자기 장로를 떠났던 까닭에, 그 위대한 행적에도 불구하고 장로의 사면 없이는 용서받을 수 없음을 알게 되었다."], ["Only after this could the funeral take place.", "오직 그러고 난 뒤에야 장례를 치를 수 있었다."], ["This, of course, is only an old legend.", "물론 이것은 한낱 옛 전설에 지나지 않는다."], ["But here is a recent instance.", "그러나 여기 근래의 한 실례가 있다."]], [["A monk was suddenly commanded by his elder to quit Athos, which he loved as a sacred place and a haven of refuge, and to go first to Jerusalem to do homage to the Holy Places and then to go to the north to Siberia: “There is the place for thee and not here.”", "한 수사가 어느 날 갑자기 자기 장로로부터, 거룩한 곳이요 피난처로 사랑해 마지않던 아토스를 떠나라는 명령을 받았으니, 먼저 예루살렘으로 가서 성지에 경배하고, 그다음에는 북녘 시베리아로 가라는 것이었다. \"거기가 네가 있을 곳이지 이곳이 아니다.\""], ["The monk, overwhelmed with sorrow, went to the Œcumenical Patriarch at Constantinople and besought him to release him from his obedience.", "슬픔에 짓눌린 그 수사는 콘스탄티노플로 세계 총대주교를 찾아가 복종의 의무에서 풀어 달라고 간청했다."], ["But the Patriarch replied that not only was he unable to release him, but there was not and could not be on earth a power which could release him except the elder who had himself laid that duty upon him.", "그러나 총대주교는 자기로서는 그를 풀어 줄 수 없을 뿐 아니라, 그 의무를 친히 그에게 지운 장로 외에는 그를 풀어 줄 수 있는 권능이 이 땅 위에 있지도 않거니와 있을 수도 없다고 대답했다."], ["In this way the elders are endowed in certain cases with unbounded and inexplicable authority.", "이렇듯 장로들은 어떤 경우에는 한없고 헤아릴 수 없는 권위를 부여받고 있다."], ["That is why in many of our monasteries the institution was at first resisted almost to persecution.", "바로 그런 까닭에 우리네 수많은 수도원에서 이 제도는 처음에 박해에 가까울 만큼 저항을 받았던 것이다."], ["Meantime the elders immediately began to be highly esteemed among the people.", "그러는 사이 장로들은 곧 백성들 사이에서 크게 존경받기 시작했다."], ["Masses of the ignorant people as well as men of distinction flocked, for instance, to the elders of our monastery to confess their doubts, their sins, and their sufferings, and ask for counsel and admonition.", "이를테면 무지한 백성의 무리는 물론 명망 높은 인사들까지도 우리 수도원의 장로들에게로 떼 지어 몰려와 자기들의 의혹과 죄와 고뇌를 고백하고 충고와 훈계를 청했다."], ["Seeing this, the opponents of the elders declared that the sacrament of confession was being arbitrarily and frivolously degraded, though the continual opening of the heart to the elder by the monk or the layman had nothing of the character of the sacrament.", "이를 본 장로 제도의 반대자들은, 비록 수사든 속인이든 장로에게 끊임없이 마음을 여는 일이 성사(聖事)의 성격이라고는 조금도 없는 것이었음에도, 고해성사가 함부로, 또 경솔하게 격하되고 있다고 떠들어 댔다."], ["In the end, however, the institution of elders has been retained and is becoming established in Russian monasteries.", "그러나 결국 장로 제도는 유지되었고 러시아 수도원들에서 차츰 자리를 잡아 가고 있다."], ["It is true, perhaps, that this instrument which had stood the test of a thousand years for the moral regeneration of a man from slavery to freedom and to moral perfectibility may be a two-edged weapon and it may lead some not to humility and complete self-control but to the most Satanic pride, that is, to bondage and not to freedom.", "노예 상태에서 자유로, 그리고 도덕적 완성으로 사람을 도덕적으로 거듭나게 하는 데 천 년의 시련을 견뎌 낸 이 방편이 어쩌면 양날의 무기일 수 있고, 또 어떤 이들을 겸손과 완전한 자기 통제로가 아니라 가장 악마적인 교만으로, 곧 자유가 아니라 속박으로 이끌 수도 있다는 것은, 어쩌면 사실일 것이다."]], [["The elder Zossima was sixty-five.", "조시마 장로는 예순다섯이었다."], ["He came of a family of landowners, had been in the army in early youth, and served in the Caucasus as an officer.", "그는 지주 집안 출신으로, 젊은 시절에는 군에 몸담아 캅카스에서 장교로 복무했다."], ["He had, no doubt, impressed Alyosha by some peculiar quality of his soul.", "그는 분명 어떤 독특한 영혼의 자질로써 알료샤에게 깊은 인상을 남겼을 것이다."], ["Alyosha lived in the cell of the elder, who was very fond of him and let him wait upon him.", "알료샤는 장로의 암자에서 지냈는데, 장로는 그를 무척 아껴 곁에서 시중들게 했다."], ["It must be noted that Alyosha was bound by no obligation and could go where he pleased and be absent for whole days.", "짚어 둘 것은, 알료샤가 어떤 의무에도 매여 있지 않아 마음 내키는 대로 어디든 갈 수 있었고 며칠씩 자리를 비울 수도 있었다는 점이다."], ["Though he wore the monastic dress it was voluntarily, not to be different from others.", "그가 수도복을 입긴 했으나 그것은 자발적인 것이었지, 남들과 달라 보이려는 것이 아니었다."], ["No doubt he liked to do so.", "분명 그는 그렇게 하는 것을 좋아했다."], ["Possibly his youthful imagination was deeply stirred by the power and fame of his elder.", "어쩌면 그의 젊은 상상력이 장로의 권능과 명성에 깊이 흔들렸는지도 모른다."], ["It was said that so many people had for years past come to confess their sins to Father Zossima and to entreat him for words of advice and healing, that he had acquired the keenest intuition and could tell from an unknown face what a new-comer wanted, and what was the suffering on his conscience.", "듣자 하니 지난 여러 해 동안 그토록 많은 사람이 조시마 신부에게 죄를 고백하고 조언과 치유의 말씀을 간구하러 왔던 까닭에, 그는 더없이 예리한 직관을 얻어, 낯선 얼굴만 보고도 새로 온 이가 무엇을 원하는지, 그 양심을 짓누르는 고뇌가 무엇인지를 알아맞힐 수 있었다고 한다."], ["He sometimes astounded and almost alarmed his visitors by his knowledge of their secrets before they had spoken a word.", "그는 이따금 찾아온 이들이 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그들의 비밀을 알아챔으로써 그들을 깜짝 놀라게 하고 거의 두렵게까지 만들었다."]], [["Alyosha noticed that many, almost all, went in to the elder for the first time with apprehension and uneasiness, but came out with bright and happy faces.", "알료샤가 보니 많은 사람이, 거의 모든 사람이 처음에 장로에게 들어갈 때는 두려움과 불안을 품고 들어갔으나, 나올 때는 밝고 행복한 얼굴이 되어 나왔다."], ["Alyosha was particularly struck by the fact that Father Zossima was not at all stern.", "알료샤가 특히 놀란 것은 조시마 신부가 조금도 엄격하지 않다는 사실이었다."], ["On the contrary, he was always almost gay.", "그와는 반대로 그는 늘 거의 쾌활하다시피 했다."], ["The monks used to say that he was more drawn to those who were more sinful, and the greater the sinner the more he loved him.", "수사들이 하는 말로는, 그는 죄가 더 많은 자들에게 더 마음이 끌렸고, 죄인일수록 더욱 사랑했다고 한다."], ["There were, no doubt, up to the end of his life, among the monks some who hated and envied him, but they were few in number and they were silent, though among them were some of great dignity in the monastery, one, for instance, of the older monks distinguished for his strict keeping of fasts and vows of silence.", "물론 그의 생애가 끝날 때까지도 수사들 가운데는 그를 미워하고 시기하는 이들이 더러 있었으나, 그 수는 적었고 또 그들은 침묵을 지켰는데, 그중에는 수도원에서 큰 위엄을 지닌 이들도 있었으니, 이를테면 단식과 침묵의 서약을 엄히 지키기로 이름난 한 늙은 수사가 그러했다."], ["But the majority were on Father Zossima’s side and very many of them loved him with all their hearts, warmly and sincerely.", "그러나 대다수는 조시마 신부 편이었고, 그들 가운데 아주 많은 이가 따뜻하고 진실하게 온 마음으로 그를 사랑했다."], ["Some were almost fanatically devoted to him, and declared, though not quite aloud, that he was a saint, that there could be no doubt of it, and, seeing that his end was near, they anticipated miracles and great glory to the monastery in the immediate future from his relics.", "어떤 이들은 거의 광신에 가깝도록 그에게 헌신하여, 비록 큰 소리로는 아니었으나, 그가 성인이며 거기에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 단언했고, 그의 임종이 가까워 옴을 보고는 머지않은 장래에 그의 유물에서 기적이 일어나고 수도원에 큰 영광이 임하리라 미리 기대했다."], ["Alyosha had unquestioning faith in the miraculous power of the elder, just as he had unquestioning faith in the story of the coffin that flew out of the church.", "알료샤는 교회 밖으로 날아가 버린 관 이야기를 의심 없이 믿었던 것과 똑같이, 장로의 기적적인 권능을 의심 없이 믿었다."], ["He saw many who came with sick children or relatives and besought the elder to lay hands on them and to pray over them, return shortly after—some the next day—and, falling in tears at the elder’s feet, thank him for healing their sick.", "그는 병든 아이나 친지를 데리고 와서 장로에게 그들 위에 손을 얹고 기도해 달라고 간청한 많은 이가, 얼마 뒤에—어떤 이들은 이튿날—다시 와서 눈물을 흘리며 장로의 발치에 엎드려 병자를 낫게 해 준 것에 감사하는 것을 보았다."]], [["Whether they had really been healed or were simply better in the natural course of the disease was a question which did not exist for Alyosha, for he fully believed in the spiritual power of his teacher and rejoiced in his fame, in his glory, as though it were his own triumph.", "그들이 정말로 나은 것인지, 아니면 그저 병이 자연히 차도를 보인 것인지 하는 물음은 알료샤에게는 아예 존재하지 않았으니, 그는 스승의 영적 권능을 온전히 믿었고 마치 제 자신의 승리인 양 스승의 명성과 영광을 기뻐했기 때문이다."], ["His heart throbbed, and he beamed, as it were, all over when the elder came out to the gates of the hermitage into the waiting crowd of pilgrims of the humbler class who had flocked from all parts of Russia on purpose to see the elder and obtain his blessing.", "장로를 보고 그 축복을 받고자 일부러 러시아 방방곡곡에서 몰려든 비천한 신분의 순례자들의 무리 속으로 장로가 암자 문께로 나설 때면, 그의 심장은 두근거렸고 온몸이 환히 빛나는 듯했다."], ["They fell down before him, wept, kissed his feet, kissed the earth on which he stood, and wailed, while the women held up their children to him and brought him the sick “possessed with devils.”", "그들은 그의 앞에 엎드려 울며 그의 발에 입 맞추고, 그가 서 있는 땅에 입 맞추며 통곡했고, 여인들은 아이들을 그에게 들어 올려 보이고 \"마귀 들린\" 병자들을 데려왔다."], ["The elder spoke to them, read a brief prayer over them, blessed them, and dismissed them.", "장로는 그들에게 말을 건네고, 그들 위에 짧은 기도를 외워 주고, 축복한 뒤 돌려보냈다."], ["Of late he had become so weak through attacks of illness that he was sometimes unable to leave his cell, and the pilgrims waited for him to come out for several days.", "근래 들어 그는 병의 발작으로 어찌나 쇠약해졌던지 이따금 암자를 나서지조차 못했고, 순례자들은 그가 나오기를 며칠씩 기다리곤 했다."], ["Alyosha did not wonder why they loved him so, why they fell down before him and wept with emotion merely at seeing his face.", "알료샤는 그들이 어찌하여 그토록 그를 사랑하는지, 어찌하여 그저 그 얼굴을 보는 것만으로 그 앞에 엎드려 감격의 눈물을 흘리는지 의아해하지 않았다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["he understood that for the humble soul of the Russian peasant, worn out by grief and toil, and still more by the everlasting injustice and everlasting sin, his own and the world’s, it was the greatest need and comfort to find some one or something holy to fall down before and worship.", "그는 알고 있었으니, 슬픔과 노역에 지치고, 더욱이 자기 자신과 세상의 영원한 부정(不正)과 영원한 죄에 지친 러시아 농민의 겸손한 영혼에게는, 그 앞에 엎드려 경배할 거룩한 누군가 혹은 무엇인가를 찾는 것이야말로 가장 큰 갈망이요 위안이라는 것을."]], [["“Among us there is sin, injustice, and temptation, but yet, somewhere on earth there is some one holy and exalted.", "\"우리 가운데는 죄와 불의와 유혹이 있으나, 그래도 이 땅 어딘가에는 거룩하고 고결한 누군가가 있다."], ["He has the truth; he knows the truth; so it is not dead upon the earth; so it will come one day to us, too, and rule over all the earth according to the promise.”", "그분께는 진리가 있고, 그분은 진리를 아시니, 그러므로 진리는 이 땅에서 죽지 않았으며, 그러므로 그것은 언젠가 우리에게도 임하여 약속하신 대로 온 땅을 다스리게 되리라.\""]], [["Alyosha knew that this was just how the people felt and even reasoned.", "알료샤는 백성이 바로 이렇게 느끼고 또 그렇게 헤아리기까지 한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He understood it, but that the elder Zossima was this saint and custodian of God’s truth—of that he had no more doubt than the weeping peasants and the sick women who held out their children to the elder.", "그는 그것을 이해했으나, 조시마 장로야말로 바로 이 성인이요 하느님의 진리를 지키는 수호자라는 것—그 점에 대해서만큼은 울며 매달리는 농민들이나 아이를 장로에게 내미는 병든 여인들에 못지않게 그도 추호의 의심이 없었다."], ["The conviction that after his death the elder would bring extraordinary glory to the monastery was even stronger in Alyosha than in any one there, and, of late, a kind of deep flame of inner ecstasy burnt more and more strongly in his heart.", "장로가 죽은 뒤에 수도원에 비범한 영광을 가져오리라는 확신은 그곳의 누구보다도 알료샤에게서 더욱 강했으며, 근래 들어 일종의 깊은 내면적 황홀의 불길이 그의 가슴속에서 점점 더 세차게 타올랐다."], ["He was not at all troubled at this elder’s standing as a solitary example before him.", "이 장로가 자기 앞에 홀로 우뚝 선 본보기로 서 있다는 사실은 그를 조금도 괴롭히지 않았다."]], [["“No matter.", "\"상관없다."], ["He is holy.", "그분은 거룩하시다."], ["He carries in his heart the secret of renewal for all: that power which will, at last, establish truth on the earth, and all men will be holy and love one another, and there will be no more rich nor poor, no exalted nor humbled, but all will be as the children of God, and the true Kingdom of Christ will come.”", "그분은 만인을 위한 갱생의 비밀을 가슴에 품고 계시니, 곧 마침내 이 땅 위에 진리를 세울 그 권능이며, 그리하여 모든 사람이 거룩해지고 서로 사랑하며, 더는 부자도 가난한 자도 없고, 높은 자도 낮은 자도 없이, 모두가 하느님의 자녀처럼 되어 참된 그리스도의 나라가 임하리라.\""], ["That was the dream in Alyosha’s heart.", "그것이 알료샤의 가슴속 꿈이었다."]], [["The arrival of his two brothers, whom he had not known till then, seemed to make a great impression on Alyosha.", "그때껏 알지 못했던 두 형의 도착은 알료샤에게 큰 인상을 남긴 듯했다."], ["He more quickly made friends with his half-brother Dmitri (though he arrived later) than with his own brother Ivan.", "그는 친형 이반보다 이복형 드미트리와 (드미트리가 더 늦게 왔는데도) 더 빨리 가까워졌다."], ["He was extremely interested in his brother Ivan, but when the latter had been two months in the town, though they had met fairly often, they were still not intimate.", "그는 형 이반에게 지극한 관심을 품었으나, 이반이 읍에 온 지 두 달이 지나 제법 자주 만났는데도 두 사람은 여전히 가까워지지 못했다."], ["Alyosha was naturally silent, and he seemed to be expecting something, ashamed about something, while his brother Ivan, though Alyosha noticed at first that he looked long and curiously at him, seemed soon to have left off thinking of him.", "알료샤는 본디 말수가 적었고, 무언가를 기다리는 듯, 무언가를 부끄러워하는 듯 보였으며, 한편 형 이반은—알료샤가 처음에는 그가 자기를 오래도록 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보는 것을 알아챘으나—이내 그에 대한 생각을 거둬 버린 듯했다."], ["Alyosha noticed it with some embarrassment.", "알료샤는 다소 당혹스러운 마음으로 그것을 알아챘다."], ["He ascribed his brother’s indifference at first to the disparity of their age and education.", "그는 처음에 형의 무관심을 두 사람의 나이와 교육의 차이 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["But he also wondered whether the absence of curiosity and sympathy in Ivan might be due to some other cause entirely unknown to him.", "그러나 그는 또한 이반에게 호기심도 공감도 없는 것이 자신은 전혀 알지 못하는 어떤 다른 까닭에서 비롯된 것은 아닐까 의아해하기도 했다."], ["He kept fancying that Ivan was absorbed in something—something inward and important—that he was striving towards some goal, perhaps very hard to attain, and that that was why he had no thought for him.", "그는 이반이 무언가에—내면적이고 중대한 무언가에—골몰해 있고, 어쩌면 도달하기 몹시 어려운 어떤 목표를 향해 애쓰고 있어서, 바로 그 때문에 자기에게 마음을 둘 겨를이 없는 것이리라는 생각이 자꾸만 들었다."], ["Alyosha wondered, too, whether there was not some contempt on the part of the learned atheist for him—a foolish novice.", "알료샤는 또한 학식 있는 무신론자인 형이 자기 같은—어리석은 수련 수사를—얼마간 경멸하고 있는 것은 아닐까 의아해하기도 했다."], ["He knew for certain that his brother was an atheist.", "그는 형이 무신론자임을 확실히 알고 있었다."], ["He could not take offense at this contempt, if it existed; yet, with an uneasy embarrassment which he did not himself understand, he waited for his brother to come nearer to him.", "그런 경멸이 설령 있다 한들 그는 그것에 기분 상할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 자기 자신도 이해하지 못하는 불안한 당혹감을 품은 채, 형이 자기에게 더 가까이 다가와 주기를 기다렸다."], ["Dmitri used to speak of Ivan with the deepest respect and with a peculiar earnestness.", "드미트리는 이반을 입에 올릴 때면 더없이 깊은 존경과 유별난 진지함을 담곤 했다."], ["From him Alyosha learnt all the details of the important affair which had of late formed such a close and remarkable bond between the two elder brothers.", "알료샤는 그에게서, 근래 두 형 사이에 그토록 긴밀하고 두드러진 유대를 맺어 준 중대한 사건의 자초지종을 낱낱이 들었다."], ["Dmitri’s enthusiastic references to Ivan were the more striking in Alyosha’s eyes since Dmitri was, compared with Ivan, almost uneducated, and the two brothers were such a contrast in personality and character that it would be difficult to find two men more unlike.", "드미트리가 이반을 두고 열을 올려 가며 늘어놓는 말들은 알료샤의 눈에 더욱 인상적이었으니, 드미트리는 이반에 비하면 거의 못 배운 사람이나 다름없었고, 두 형은 인품과 성격에서 어찌나 대조적이었던지 이보다 더 닮지 않은 두 사람을 찾아내기란 어려울 정도였기 때문이다."]], [["It was at this time that the meeting, or, rather gathering of the members of this inharmonious family took place in the cell of the elder who had such an extraordinary influence on Alyosha.", "알료샤에게 그토록 비상한 영향을 끼친 장로의 암자에서, 이 화목하지 못한 집안 식구들의 회동, 아니 차라리 집결이라 할 만한 모임이 이루어진 것은 바로 이 무렵이었다."], ["The pretext for this gathering was a false one.", "이 집결의 구실은 거짓된 것이었다."], ["It was at this time that the discord between Dmitri and his father seemed at its acutest stage and their relations had become insufferably strained.", "드미트리와 그 아버지 사이의 불화가 가장 극심한 단계에 이른 듯했고 두 사람의 관계가 견디기 힘들 만큼 팽팽해진 것도 바로 이 무렵이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch seems to have been the first to suggest, apparently in joke, that they should all meet in Father Zossima’s cell, and that, without appealing to his direct intervention, they might more decently come to an understanding under the conciliating influence of the elder’s presence.", "다 함께 조시마 신부의 암자에 모이자고, 또 장로의 직접적인 중재를 청하지 않더라도 장로가 그 자리에 있다는 그 화해의 영향력 아래 좀 더 점잖게 서로 합의에 이를 수 있으리라고 처음 제안한 사람은—겉보기에는 농담조였으나—표도르 파블로비치였던 듯하다."], ["Dmitri, who had never seen the elder, naturally supposed that his father was trying to intimidate him, but, as he secretly blamed himself for his outbursts of temper with his father on several recent occasions, he accepted the challenge.", "장로를 한 번도 본 적이 없던 드미트리는 당연히 아버지가 자기를 위협하려 드는 것이라고 여겼으나, 근래 여러 차례 아버지 앞에서 울컥 성을 낸 일을 두고 속으로 자신을 책망하고 있던 터라 그 도전을 받아들였다."], ["It must be noted that he was not, like Ivan, staying with his father, but living apart at the other end of the town.", "짚어 둘 것은, 그가 이반과는 달리 아버지와 함께 지내고 있던 것이 아니라 읍의 반대편 끝에 따로 떨어져 살고 있었다는 점이다."], ["It happened that Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, who was staying in the district at the time, caught eagerly at the idea.", "마침 그 무렵 이 고장에 머물고 있던 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프가 그 생각에 덥석 달려들었다."], ["A Liberal of the forties and fifties, a freethinker and atheist, he may have been led on by boredom or the hope of frivolous diversion.", "사오십 년대의 자유주의자요, 자유사상가이자 무신론자였던 그는, 권태에 이끌렸거나 아니면 시시한 심심풀이에 대한 기대에 이끌렸는지도 모른다."], ["He was suddenly seized with the desire to see the monastery and the holy man.", "그는 별안간 수도원과 그 거룩한 사람을 보고 싶은 욕구에 사로잡혔다."], ["As his lawsuit with the monastery still dragged on, he made it the pretext for seeing the Superior, in order to attempt to settle it amicably.", "수도원과의 소송이 여전히 질질 끌고 있었으므로, 그는 그것을 원만히 매듭짓고자 시도한다는 명분으로 원장을 만나려는 구실로 삼았다."], ["A visitor coming with such laudable intentions might be received with more attention and consideration than if he came from simple curiosity.", "그처럼 가상한 의도를 품고 찾아온 방문객이라면 단순한 호기심으로 온 경우보다 더한 관심과 배려로 맞아질 만했다."], ["Influences from within the monastery were brought to bear on the elder, who of late had scarcely left his cell, and had been forced by illness to deny even his ordinary visitors.", "근래 들어 거의 암자를 떠나지 못하고 병 때문에 평소의 방문객들조차 사절해야 했던 장로에게, 수도원 안쪽에서 손길이 미쳐 들어왔다."], ["In the end he consented to see them, and the day was fixed.", "결국 그는 그들을 만나기로 응낙했고, 날짜가 정해졌다."]], [["“Who has made me a judge over them?”", "\"누가 나를 그들의 재판관으로 세웠던가?\""], ["was all he said, smilingly, to Alyosha.", "그는 알료샤에게 빙그레 웃으며 그렇게만 말했다."]], [["Alyosha was much perturbed when he heard of the proposed visit.", "알료샤는 그 방문 제안에 대해 듣고서 몹시 심란해졌다."], ["Of all the wrangling, quarrelsome party, Dmitri was the only one who could regard the interview seriously.", "이 옥신각신하는 다툼꾼 일행 가운데 그 만남을 진지하게 여길 수 있는 사람은 드미트리 하나뿐이었다."], ["All the others would come from frivolous motives, perhaps insulting to the elder.", "나머지는 모두 시시한 동기에서, 어쩌면 장로에게 모욕이 될 만한 동기에서 올 터였다."], ["Alyosha was well aware of that.", "알료샤는 그것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Ivan and Miüsov would come from curiosity, perhaps of the coarsest kind, while his father might be contemplating some piece of buffoonery.", "이반과 미우소프는 호기심에서, 어쩌면 가장 천박한 종류의 호기심에서 올 것이고, 한편 그의 아버지는 어떤 익살극이라도 꾸미고 있을지 몰랐다."], ["Though he said nothing, Alyosha thoroughly understood his father.", "비록 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나, 알료샤는 자기 아버지를 속속들이 꿰뚫고 있었다."], ["The boy, I repeat, was far from being so simple as every one thought him.", "거듭 말하거니와, 이 소년은 모든 이가 여기던 것처럼 그렇게 단순한 사람이 결코 아니었다."], ["He awaited the day with a heavy heart.", "그는 무거운 마음으로 그날을 기다렸다."], ["No doubt he was always pondering in his mind how the family discord could be ended.", "분명 그는 어떻게 하면 이 집안의 불화를 끝낼 수 있을지 마음속으로 늘 골똘히 궁리하고 있었다."], ["But his chief anxiety concerned the elder.", "그러나 그의 가장 큰 근심은 장로에 관한 것이었다."], ["He trembled for him, for his glory, and dreaded any affront to him, especially the refined, courteous irony of Miüsov and the supercilious half-utterances of the highly educated Ivan.", "그는 장로를 위해, 또 그 영광을 위해 마음을 졸였고, 그에게 어떤 모욕이 가해질까 두려워했으니, 특히 미우소프의 세련되고 정중한 비꼼과, 학식 깊은 이반의 거만한 어중간한 말투가 그러했다."], ["He even wanted to venture on warning the elder, telling him something about them, but, on second thoughts, said nothing.", "그는 장로에게 미리 경고를 해 그들에 관해 무언가 일러 두는 모험까지 해 볼까도 했으나, 다시 생각한 끝에 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He only sent word the day before, through a friend, to his brother Dmitri, that he loved him and expected him to keep his promise.", "그는 다만 전날 한 친구를 통해 형 드미트리에게, 자기는 형을 사랑하며 형이 약속을 지켜 주기를 기대한다는 전갈만을 보냈다."], ["Dmitri wondered, for he could not remember what he had promised, but he answered by letter that he would do his utmost not to let himself be provoked “by vileness,” but that, although he had a deep respect for the elder and for his brother Ivan, he was convinced that the meeting was either a trap for him or an unworthy farce.", "드미트리는 자기가 무엇을 약속했는지 도무지 기억나지 않아 의아해했으나, 답장으로 자기는 \"비열한 짓\"에 도발당하지 않도록 온 힘을 다하겠노라고, 다만 장로와 형 이반을 깊이 존경하긴 하지만 이 모임이 자기에게는 함정이거나 아니면 천박한 익살극이라고 확신한다고 적어 보냈다."]], [["“Nevertheless I would rather bite out my tongue than be lacking in respect to the sainted man whom you reverence so highly,”", "\"그렇지만 자네가 그토록 떠받드는 그 거룩한 분께 결례를 범하느니 차라리 내 혀를 깨물어 버리겠네.\""], ["he wrote in conclusion.", "그는 끝맺음으로 그렇게 적었다."], ["Alyosha was not greatly cheered by the letter.", "알료샤는 그 편지에 그리 크게 기운이 나지는 않았다."]], [["# Book II. An Unfortunate Gathering Chapter I. They Arrive At The Monastery", "# Book II. An Unfortunate Gathering Chapter I. They Arrive At The Monastery"]], [["It was a warm, bright day at the end of August.", "따뜻하고 화창한 8월 말의 하루였다."], ["The interview with the elder had been fixed for half-past eleven, immediately after late mass.", "장로와의 면담은 늦은 미사가 끝난 직후인 열한 시 반으로 정해져 있었다."], ["Our visitors did not take part in the service, but arrived just as it was over.", "우리의 방문객들은 예배에는 참례하지 않고 마침 그것이 끝날 무렵에 도착했다."], ["First an elegant open carriage, drawn by two valuable horses, drove up with Miüsov and a distant relative of his, a young man of twenty, called Pyotr Fomitch Kalganov.", "먼저 값비싼 말 두 필이 끄는 우아한 사륜 포장마차가 들이닥쳤는데, 거기엔 미우소프와 그의 먼 친척으로 스무 살 난 표트르 포미치 칼가노프라는 청년이 타고 있었다."], ["This young man was preparing to enter the university.", "이 청년은 대학에 들어갈 준비를 하고 있었다."], ["Miüsov, with whom he was staying for the time, was trying to persuade him to go abroad to the university of Zurich or Jena.", "그가 당분간 함께 지내고 있던 미우소프는 그에게 외국으로 나가 취리히나 예나 대학에 진학하라고 설득하는 중이었다."], ["The young man was still undecided.", "청년은 아직 마음을 정하지 못한 채였다."], ["He was thoughtful and absent-minded.", "그는 생각이 깊으면서도 멍한 데가 있었다."], ["He was nice-looking, strongly built, and rather tall.", "인물이 곱고 다부지게 생겼으며 키도 제법 컸다."], ["There was a strange fixity in his gaze at times.", "이따금 그 눈길에는 야릇한 응시가 어렸다."], ["Like all very absent-minded people he would sometimes stare at a person without seeing him.", "몹시 멍한 사람들이 다 그렇듯 그는 때로 어떤 사람을 보고 있으면서도 실은 보지 못하고 있을 때가 있었다."], ["He was silent and rather awkward, but sometimes, when he was alone with any one, he became talkative and effusive, and would laugh at anything or nothing.", "그는 말수가 적고 다소 어색했으나, 더러 누군가와 단둘이 있을 때면 수다스럽고 격정적으로 변해서, 별것 아닌 일에도, 아무 까닭 없이도 웃음을 터뜨리곤 했다."], ["But his animation vanished as quickly as it appeared.", "그러나 그의 그런 활기는 나타날 때와 마찬가지로 순식간에 사라져 버렸다."], ["He was always well and even elaborately dressed; he had already some independent fortune and expectations of much more.", "그는 늘 잘, 심지어 공들여 차려입었다. 이미 어느 정도 독립된 재산을 가지고 있었고, 앞으로 훨씬 더 많은 유산을 받을 전망이었다."], ["He was a friend of Alyosha’s.", "그는 알료샤의 친구였다."]], [["In an ancient, jolting, but roomy, hired carriage, with a pair of old pinkish-gray horses, a long way behind Miüsov’s carriage, came Fyodor Pavlovitch, with his son Ivan.", "낡고 덜컹거리지만 널찍한 삯마차에, 늙고 불그스름한 잿빛 말 두 필을 매단 채, 미우소프의 마차보다 한참 뒤처져서 표도르 파블로비치가 아들 이반과 함께 왔다."], ["Dmitri was late, though he had been informed of the time the evening before.", "드미트리는 전날 저녁에 시각을 통보받았는데도 늦었다."], ["The visitors left their carriage at the hotel, outside the precincts, and went to the gates of the monastery on foot.", "방문객들은 경내 바깥, 여관 앞에 마차를 두고 걸어서 수도원 정문으로 향했다."], ["Except Fyodor Pavlovitch, none of the party had ever seen the monastery, and Miüsov had probably not even been to church for thirty years.", "표도르 파블로비치를 빼고는 일행 가운데 누구도 이 수도원을 본 적이 없었고, 미우소프로 말하자면 삼십 년 동안 교회 근처에도 가 본 일이 없을 듯했다."], ["He looked about him with curiosity, together with assumed ease.", "그는 짐짓 태연한 척하면서도 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["But, except the church and the domestic buildings, though these too were ordinary enough, he found nothing of interest in the interior of the monastery.", "그러나 교회와 부속 건물들을 빼면—그것들조차 변변찮을 만큼 평범했지만—수도원 내부에서 그의 흥미를 끄는 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다."], ["The last of the worshippers were coming out of the church, bareheaded and crossing themselves.", "마지막 예배자들이 모자를 벗고 성호를 그으며 교회에서 나오고 있었다."], ["Among the humbler people were a few of higher rank—two or three ladies and a very old general.", "신분이 낮은 사람들 사이에 지체 높은 이들도 몇 끼어 있었으니—두세 명의 부인과 매우 늙은 장군이 그러했다."], ["They were all staying at the hotel.", "그들은 모두 여관에 묵고 있었다."], ["Our visitors were at once surrounded by beggars, but none of them gave them anything, except young Kalganov, who took a ten-copeck piece out of his purse, and, nervous and embarrassed—God knows why!—hurriedly gave it to an old woman, saying: “Divide it equally.”", "우리의 방문객들은 곧 거지들에게 둘러싸였으나, 누구 하나 적선하는 이가 없었고 다만 젊은 칼가노프만이 지갑에서 십 코페이카짜리 한 닢을 꺼내, 까닭 모를 초조함과 당혹감에 휩싸여—신만이 그 이유를 아시리라!—한 노파에게 황급히 건네며 \"고루 나누세요\" 하고 말했다."], ["None of his companions made any remark upon it, so that he had no reason to be embarrassed; but, perceiving this, he was even more overcome.", "일행 가운데 누구도 그 일을 두고 한마디 하지 않았으니 그가 무안해할 까닭은 없었으나, 정작 그는 그 점을 알아차리고 더욱 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."]], [["It was strange that their arrival did not seem expected, and that they were not received with special honor, though one of them had recently made a donation of a thousand roubles, while another was a very wealthy and highly cultured landowner, upon whom all in the monastery were in a sense dependent, as a decision of the lawsuit might at any moment put their fishing rights in his hands.", "이상하게도 그들의 방문은 미리 알려져 기다려진 것 같지 않았고, 특별한 예우로 맞아들여지지도 않았다. 일행 가운데 하나는 최근에 천 루블을 헌금했으며, 또 한 사람은 매우 부유하고 교양 높은 지주로서, 송사의 판결이 어느 순간에든 어업권을 그의 손에 넘겨줄 수도 있는 까닭에 수도원 전체가 어떤 의미로는 그에게 매여 있는 처지였는데도 그러했다."], ["Yet no official personage met them.", "그런데도 직책 있는 인물 하나 그들을 맞으러 나오지 않았다."]], [["Miüsov looked absent-mindedly at the tombstones round the church, and was on the point of saying that the dead buried here must have paid a pretty penny for the right of lying in this “holy place,”", "미우소프는 교회 둘레의 묘비들을 멍하니 바라보다가, 여기 묻힌 망자들이 이 \"거룩한 곳\"에 누울 권리를 얻으려 꽤나 비싼 값을 치렀을 거라고 한마디 하려다가 참았다."], ["but refrained. His liberal irony was rapidly changing almost into anger.", "그의 자유주의적 빈정거림은 어느새 분노에 가까운 것으로 빠르게 바뀌고 있었다."]], [["“Who the devil is there to ask in this imbecile place?", "\"이 멍청한 곳에서 도대체 누구한테 물어봐야 한단 말인가?"], ["We must find out, for time is passing,”", "알아내야지, 시간이 자꾸 가니까.\""], ["he observed suddenly, as though speaking to himself.", "그가 혼잣말하듯 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["All at once there came up a bald-headed, elderly man with ingratiating little eyes, wearing a full, summer overcoat.", "그때 별안간 대머리에 알랑거리는 작은 눈을 가진 중년 사내가 다가왔는데, 헐렁한 여름 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["Lifting his hat, he introduced himself with a honeyed lisp as Maximov, a landowner of Tula.", "그는 모자를 들어 올리며, 꿀처럼 끈적이는 혀짤배기소리로 자신을 툴라의 지주 막시모프라고 소개했다."], ["He at once entered into our visitors’ difficulty.", "그는 곧장 우리 방문객들의 곤란한 처지에 끼어들었다."]], [["“Father Zossima lives in the hermitage, apart, four hundred paces from the monastery, the other side of the copse.”", "\"조시마 신부님은 수도원에서 떨어져, 사백 보쯤 가서 잡목 숲 너머 은수처에 계십니다.\""]], [["“I know it’s the other side of the copse,”", "\"잡목 숲 너머라는 건 알아요.\""], ["observed Fyodor Pavlovitch, “but we don’t remember the way.", "표도르 파블로비치가 말했다. \"한데 길을 통 기억할 수가 없구려."], ["It is a long time since we’ve been here.”", "여기 와 본 지가 하도 오래라서.\""]], [["“This way, by this gate, and straight across the copse ...", "\"이쪽으로, 이 문으로, 잡목 숲을 곧장 가로질러서……"], ["the copse.", "잡목 숲을요."], ["Come with me, won’t you?", "저랑 같이 가시지요, 예?"], ["I’ll show you.", "제가 안내해 드리겠습니다."], ["I have to go....", "저도 가야 하니까요……"], ["I am going myself.", "저도 가는 길이에요."], ["This way, this way.”", "이쪽, 이쪽입니다.\""]], [["They came out of the gate and turned towards the copse.", "그들은 문을 나서 잡목 숲 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Maximov, a man of sixty, ran rather than walked, turning sideways to stare at them all, with an incredible degree of nervous curiosity.", "예순 살의 막시모프는 걷는다기보다 차라리 뛰다시피 하며, 도무지 믿기 어려울 만큼 신경질적인 호기심으로 몸을 옆으로 돌려 그들 모두를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["His eyes looked starting out of his head.", "두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 듯했다."]], [["“You see, we have come to the elder upon business of our own,”", "\"보시오, 우리는 우리 나름의 용무가 있어 장로를 뵈러 온 거요.\""], ["observed Miüsov severely.", "미우소프가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다."], ["“That personage has granted us an audience, so to speak, and so, though we thank you for showing us the way, we cannot ask you to accompany us.”", "\"그분이 말하자면 우리에게 면담을 허락해 주신 터라, 길을 안내해 주신 데 대해서는 고맙긴 합니다만, 동행을 청할 수는 없는 노릇이오.\""]], [["“I’ve been there.", "\"저도 가 봤습니다."], ["I’ve been already; un chevalier parfait,”", "벌써 다녀왔어요. 운 슈발리에 파르페(완벽한 기사)지요.\""], ["and Maximov snapped his fingers in the air.", "그러더니 막시모프가 허공에서 손가락을 딱 튕겼다."]], [["“Who is a chevalier?”", "\"누가 기사라는 거요?\""], ["asked Miüsov.", "미우소프가 물었다."]], [["“The elder, the splendid elder, the elder!", "\"장로님이죠, 그 훌륭한 장로님, 그 장로님!"], ["The honor and glory of the monastery, Zossima.", "수도원의 명예이자 영광인 조시마 신부님 말입니다."], ["Such an elder!”", "그런 장로님이 또 어디 있겠어요!\""]], [["But his incoherent talk was cut short by a very pale, wan-looking monk of medium height, wearing a monk’s cap, who overtook them.", "그러나 그의 두서없는 수다는, 키가 중간쯤 되는 몹시 창백하고 수척한 수사가 수사 모자를 쓰고 그들을 따라잡으며 끼어드는 바람에 뚝 끊겼다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch and Miüsov stopped.", "표도르 파블로비치와 미우소프는 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["The monk, with an extremely courteous, profound bow, announced:", "수사는 더없이 정중하게 허리를 깊이 숙여 절하며 알렸다."]], [["“The Father Superior invites all of you gentlemen to dine with him after your visit to the hermitage.", "\"원장 신부님께서 여러분 모두를 은수처 방문이 끝난 뒤 함께 식사하자고 청하십니다."], ["At one o’clock, not later.", "한 시에, 늦지 않게요."], ["And you also,”", "그리고 당신도요.\""], ["he added, addressing Maximov.", "그가 막시모프를 향해 덧붙였다."]], [["“That I certainly will, without fail,”", "\"암, 그러고말고요, 꼭 가지요.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, hugely delighted at the invitation.", "표도르 파블로비치가 그 초대에 몹시 기뻐하며 외쳤다."], ["“And, believe me, we’ve all given our word to behave properly here....", "\"그리고 정말이지, 우리는 다들 여기서 예의 바르게 처신하기로 단단히 약속했답니다……"], ["And you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, will you go, too?”", "그래, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 자네도 가겠지?\""]], [["“Yes, of course.", "\"그럼요, 물론이죠."], ["What have I come for but to study all the customs here?", "내가 여기 온 게 다 여기 풍습을 두루 살펴보려는 것 아니겠습니까?"], ["The only obstacle to me is your company....”", "단 하나 걸리는 것이라곤 당신과 동석하는 일이지만……\""]], [["“Yes, Dmitri Fyodorovitch is non-existent as yet.”", "\"그래, 드미트리 표도로비치는 아직 코빼기도 안 보이는군.\""]], [["“It would be a capital thing if he didn’t turn up.", "\"그 친구가 안 나타나면 더할 나위 없이 좋겠소만."], ["Do you suppose I like all this business, and in your company, too?", "내가 이 모든 일을, 그것도 당신네와 함께하는 걸 즐기는 줄 아시오?"], ["So we will come to dinner.", "아무튼 식사하러 가겠소."], ["Thank the Father Superior,”", "원장 신부님께 고맙다고 전하시오.\""], ["he said to the monk.", "그가 수사에게 말했다."]], [["“No, it is my duty now to conduct you to the elder,”", "\"아닙니다, 이제는 제가 여러분을 장로님께로 모시는 게 제 소임입니다.\""], ["answered the monk.", "수사가 대답했다."]], [["“If so I’ll go straight to the Father Superior—to the Father Superior,”", "\"그렇다면 저는 곧장 원장 신부님께로—원장 신부님께로 가겠습니다.\""], ["babbled Maximov.", "막시모프가 더듬더듬 지껄였다."]], [["“The Father Superior is engaged just now.", "\"원장 신부님은 지금 일이 있으십니다."], ["But as you please—”", "하지만 정 원하신다면야—\""], ["the monk hesitated.", "수사가 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Impertinent old man!”", "\"건방진 영감 같으니!\""], ["Miüsov observed aloud, while Maximov ran back to the monastery.", "막시모프가 수도원 쪽으로 도로 뛰어가자 미우소프가 들으라는 듯 내뱉었다."]], [["“He’s like von Sohn,”", "\"폰 존을 빼닮았군.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch said suddenly.", "표도르 파블로비치가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Is that all you can think of?...", "\"고작 생각해 낸 게 그거요?……"], ["In what way is he like von Sohn?", "대체 어디가 폰 존을 닮았다는 거요?"], ["Have you ever seen von Sohn?”", "폰 존을 본 적이나 있소?\""]], [["“I’ve seen his portrait.", "\"초상화를 봤지."], ["It’s not the features, but something indefinable.", "이목구비가 아니라, 뭐랄까 딱 꼬집을 수 없는 무언가요."], ["He’s a second von Sohn.", "저자는 제2의 폰 존이오."], ["I can always tell from the physiognomy.”", "나는 늘 인상만 보면 알아맞힌다니까.\""]], [["“Ah, I dare say you are a connoisseur in that.", "\"흥, 과연 그 방면엔 일가견이 있으시구려."], ["But, look here, Fyodor Pavlovitch, you said just now that we had given our word to behave properly.", "한데 이보시오, 표도르 파블로비치, 방금 우리가 예의 바르게 처신하기로 약속했다고 했잖소."], ["Remember it.", "그 말 잊지 마시오."], ["I advise you to control yourself.", "당신더러 자제하라고 권하는 거요."], ["But, if you begin to play the fool I don’t intend to be associated with you here....", "하지만 만일 광대짓을 시작하면 나는 여기서 당신과 한패로 엮이고 싶지 않소……"], ["You see what a man he is”—he turned to the monk—“I’m afraid to go among decent people with him.”", "보십시오, 이 사람이 어떤 위인인지를\"—그가 수사 쪽으로 돌아섰다—\"이런 자와 함께 점잖은 분들 사이에 끼는 게 영 두렵단 말입니다.\""], ["A fine smile, not without a certain slyness, came on to the pale, bloodless lips of the monk, but he made no reply, and was evidently silent from a sense of his own dignity.", "창백하고 핏기 없는 수사의 입술에 어떤 교활함이 어린 옅은 미소가 떠올랐으나, 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으니, 분명 제 위엄을 지키려는 마음에서 입을 다문 것이었다."], ["Miüsov frowned more than ever.", "미우소프는 한층 더 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Oh, devil take them all!", "\"오, 빌어먹을, 죄다 악마에게나 가 버려라!"], ["An outer show elaborated through centuries, and nothing but charlatanism and nonsense underneath,”", "몇 세기에 걸쳐 공들여 다듬은 겉치레일 뿐, 그 속이라곤 야바위와 허튼소리밖에 없잖은가.\""], ["flashed through Miüsov’s mind.", "이런 생각이 미우소프의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“Here’s the hermitage.", "\"여기가 은수처군."], ["We’ve arrived,”", "도착했어.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다."], ["“The gates are shut.”", "\"문이 닫혀 있군.\""]], [["And he repeatedly made the sign of the cross to the saints painted above and on the sides of the gates.", "그러더니 그는 문 위와 양옆에 그려진 성인들을 향해 거듭 성호를 그었다."]], [["“When you go to Rome you must do as the Romans do.", "\"로마에 가면 로마법을 따라야지."], ["Here in this hermitage there are twenty-five saints being saved.", "이 은수처에는 스물다섯 명의 성인이 구원받겠다고 들어앉아 있다지."], ["They look at one another, and eat cabbages.", "서로 얼굴만 마주 보면서 양배추나 먹는다는군."], ["And not one woman goes in at this gate.", "그리고 이 문으로는 여자라곤 한 사람도 못 들어가지."], ["That’s what is remarkable.", "그게 바로 진기한 점이라네."], ["And that really is so.", "그리고 그게 정말 그렇다는군."], ["But I did hear that the elder receives ladies,”", "그런데 듣자 하니 장로는 부인네들을 맞아들인다던데?\""], ["he remarked suddenly to the monk.", "그가 불쑥 수사에게 물었다."]], [["“Women of the people are here too now, lying in the portico there waiting.", "\"평민 여인들도 지금 여기 있습니다. 저기 회랑에 누워 기다리고 있지요."], ["But for ladies of higher rank two rooms have been built adjoining the portico, but outside the precincts—you can see the windows—and the elder goes out to them by an inner passage when he is well enough.", "한데 지체 높은 부인들을 위해서는 회랑에 잇닿아, 다만 경내 바깥에 방 두 칸을 지어 두었는데—창문이 저기 보이실 겁니다—장로님께서 몸이 괜찮으실 때면 안쪽 통로를 통해 그분들께 나가십니다."], ["They are always outside the precincts.", "그 방들은 늘 경내 바깥에 있지요."], ["There is a Harkov lady, Madame Hohlakov, waiting there now with her sick daughter.", "지금도 하리코프에서 온 호흘라코바 부인이 병든 따님을 데리고 거기서 기다리고 있습니다."], ["Probably he has promised to come out to her, though of late he has been so weak that he has hardly shown himself even to the people.”", "아마 그분은 나가 보겠다고 약속하셨을 텐데, 요즘은 워낙 기력이 쇠하셔서 평민들에게도 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않으십니다.\""]], [["“So then there are loopholes, after all, to creep out of the hermitage to the ladies.", "\"그렇다면 결국 은수처에서 슬그머니 빠져나가 부인네들에게 갈 구멍이 있긴 있군그래."], ["Don’t suppose, holy father, that I mean any harm.", "거룩한 신부님, 내가 무슨 나쁜 뜻으로 하는 말이라곤 생각지 마시오."], ["But do you know that at Athos not only the visits of women are not allowed, but no creature of the female sex—no hens, nor turkey-hens, nor cows.”", "한데 혹시 아시오, 아토스에서는 여자의 방문이 금지된 것은 물론이거니와, 암컷이라곤 그 어떤 짐승도—암탉이건 암칠면조건 암소건—발 못 들이게 한다는 걸?\""]], [["“Fyodor Pavlovitch, I warn you I shall go back and leave you here.", "\"표도르 파블로비치, 경고하는데 나는 돌아가서 당신을 여기 내버려 두겠소."], ["They’ll turn you out when I’m gone.”", "내가 가고 나면 사람들이 당신을 쫓아낼 거요.\""]], [["“But I’m not interfering with you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "\"하지만 나는 당신을 방해하는 게 아니잖소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치."], ["Look,”", "보시오.\""], ["he cried suddenly, stepping within the precincts, “what a vale of roses they live in!”", "그가 별안간 경내로 발을 들여놓으며 외쳤다. \"이 사람들이 얼마나 장미 골짜기 같은 데서 사는지를!\""]], [["Though there were no roses now, there were numbers of rare and beautiful autumn flowers growing wherever there was space for them, and evidently tended by a skillful hand; there were flower-beds round the church, and between the tombs; and the one-storied wooden house where the elder lived was also surrounded with flowers.", "지금은 장미가 없었으나, 자리만 나면 어디고 진귀하고 아름다운 가을 꽃들이 자라고 있었고, 분명 솜씨 좋은 손길이 보살핀 흔적이 역력했다. 교회 둘레와 무덤들 사이에 화단이 꾸며져 있었고, 장로가 사는 단층 목조 가옥 또한 꽃들에 에워싸여 있었다."]], [["“And was it like this in the time of the last elder, Varsonofy?", "\"전 장로 바르소노피 시절에도 이랬소?"], ["He didn’t care for such elegance.", "그분은 이런 우아한 것 따위엔 통 관심이 없었다던데."], ["They say he used to jump up and thrash even ladies with a stick,”", "듣자 하니 그분은 벌떡 일어나 부인네들까지도 지팡이로 두들겨 패곤 했다더군.\""], ["observed Fyodor Pavlovitch, as he went up the steps.", "표도르 파블로비치가 계단을 올라가며 말했다."]], [["“The elder Varsonofy did sometimes seem rather strange, but a great deal that’s told is foolishness.", "\"바르소노피 장로님께서 더러 좀 별나 보이실 때가 있긴 했지요. 하지만 떠도는 얘기 가운데는 어리석은 것이 태반입니다."], ["He never thrashed any one,”", "그분은 누구도 매질하신 적이 없습니다.\""], ["answered the monk.", "수사가 대답했다."], ["“Now, gentlemen, if you will wait a minute I will announce you.”", "\"자, 여러분, 잠시 기다려 주시면 제가 알려 드리고 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Fyodor Pavlovitch, for the last time, your compact, do you hear?", "\"표도르 파블로비치, 마지막으로 한 번 더, 그 약속 말이오, 알아들었소?"], ["Behave properly or I will pay you out!”", "점잖게 처신하시오, 안 그러면 가만 안 둘 테니!\""], ["Miüsov had time to mutter again.", "미우소프가 다시 한번 중얼거릴 짬이 있었다."]], [["“I can’t think why you are so agitated,”", "\"당신이 왜 이렇게 안절부절못하는지 도무지 모르겠구려.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch observed sarcastically.", "표도르 파블로비치가 빈정거리듯 말했다."], ["“Are you uneasy about your sins?", "\"당신 죄가 켕기는 거요?"], ["They say he can tell by one’s eyes what one has come about.", "사람들 말이, 그 양반은 눈만 보고도 무슨 일로 왔는지 알아맞힌다는데."], ["And what a lot you think of their opinion!", "한데 당신은 그들의 견해를 어찌 그리 대단하게 여기시오!"], ["you, a Parisian, and so advanced.", "파리 물 먹고 그토록 앞서간다는 당신이 말이오."], ["I’m surprised at you.”", "새삼 놀랍구려.\""]], [["But Miüsov had no time to reply to this sarcasm.", "그러나 미우소프는 이 빈정거림에 대꾸할 겨를이 없었다."], ["They were asked to come in.", "그들에게 들어오라는 청이 떨어졌던 것이다."], ["He walked in, somewhat irritated.", "그는 다소 약이 오른 채로 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“Now, I know myself, I am annoyed, I shall lose my temper and begin to quarrel—and lower myself and my ideas,”", "\"이거 봐, 나는 내 속을 잘 알지, 지금 짜증이 나 있고, 분통을 터뜨려 말다툼을 벌이고는—나 자신과 내 신념까지 깎아내리고 말 거야.\""], ["he reflected.", "그가 속으로 생각했다."]], [["# Chapter II. The Old Buffoon", "# Chapter II. The Old Buffoon"]], [["They entered the room almost at the same moment that the elder came in from his bedroom.", "그들은 거의 장로가 침실에서 나오는 바로 그 순간에 방으로 들어섰다."], ["There were already in the cell, awaiting the elder, two monks of the hermitage, one the Father Librarian, and the other Father Païssy, a very learned man, so they said, in delicate health, though not old.", "암자에는 이미 장로를 기다리는 두 수도사가 있었으니, 한 사람은 도서 담당 신부였고, 다른 한 사람은 파이시 신부였는데, 소문에 따르면 대단히 학식이 깊은 인물로서 늙지는 않았으나 몸이 허약하다고 했다."], ["There was also a tall young man, who looked about two and twenty, standing in the corner throughout the interview.", "그 밖에도 스물두 살쯤 되어 보이는 키 큰 젊은이가 있어, 면담이 진행되는 내내 한구석에 서 있었다."], ["He had a broad, fresh face, and clever, observant, narrow brown eyes, and was wearing ordinary dress.", "그는 넓적하고 혈색 좋은 얼굴에, 영리하고 관찰력이 날카로운 가느다란 갈색 눈을 지녔으며, 평상복을 입고 있었다."], ["He was a divinity student, living under the protection of the monastery.", "그는 수도원의 보호 아래 살아가는 신학생이었다."], ["His expression was one of unquestioning, but self-respecting, reverence.", "그의 표정에는 의심을 모르되 자존심을 잃지 않은 경건함이 어려 있었다."], ["Being in a subordinate and dependent position, and so not on an equality with the guests, he did not greet them with a bow.", "종속적이고 의탁하는 처지였던 까닭에, 손님들과 동등한 위치가 아니었으므로 그는 절을 하여 그들을 맞이하지 않았다."]], [["Father Zossima was accompanied by a novice, and by Alyosha.", "조시마 장로는 한 수련 수사와 알료샤를 데리고 들어왔다."], ["The two monks rose and greeted him with a very deep bow, touching the ground with their fingers; then kissed his hand.", "두 수도사는 일어서서 손가락으로 땅을 짚으며 매우 깊이 절을 올려 그를 맞이한 뒤, 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Blessing them, the elder replied with as deep a reverence to them, and asked their blessing.", "장로는 그들을 축복하면서 그들에게도 그만큼 깊이 머리를 숙여 답례하고, 그들의 축복을 청했다."], ["The whole ceremony was performed very seriously and with an appearance of feeling, not like an everyday rite.", "이 모든 의식은 매우 엄숙하게, 그리고 진심 어린 모습으로 행해졌으니, 여느 날의 일상적인 예식 같지 않았다."], ["But Miüsov fancied that it was all done with intentional impressiveness.", "그러나 미우소프는 이 모든 것이 일부러 인상을 남기려는 연출이라고 여겼다."], ["He stood in front of the other visitors.", "그는 다른 방문객들 앞에 서 있었다."], ["He ought—he had reflected upon it the evening before—from simple politeness, since it was the custom here, to have gone up to receive the elder’s blessing, even if he did not kiss his hand.", "그는—전날 저녁에 이미 곰곰이 생각해 둔 바였으나—이곳의 관습이 그러하니 단순한 예의로라도 다가가 장로의 축복을 받았어야 했을 것이니, 비록 그 손에 입을 맞추지는 않더라도 말이다."], ["But when he saw all this bowing and kissing on the part of the monks he instantly changed his mind.", "그러나 수도사들이 절하고 입 맞추는 그 모든 광경을 보자 그는 곧장 마음을 바꾸었다."], ["With dignified gravity he made a rather deep, conventional bow, and moved away to a chair.", "위엄 있고 진지한 태도로 그는 제법 깊숙이, 형식적인 절을 한 다음 의자 쪽으로 물러났다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch did the same, mimicking Miüsov like an ape.", "표도르 파블로비치도 똑같이 했는데, 마치 원숭이처럼 미우소프를 흉내 냈다."], ["Ivan bowed with great dignity and courtesy, but he too kept his hands at his sides, while Kalganov was so confused that he did not bow at all.", "이반은 대단히 위엄 있고 정중하게 절을 했으나, 그 역시 두 손을 옆구리에 붙인 채였고, 칼가노프는 너무도 당황한 나머지 아예 절을 하지 못했다."], ["The elder let fall the hand raised to bless them, and bowing to them again, asked them all to sit down.", "장로는 축복하려고 들어 올렸던 손을 떨구고는 다시 한번 그들에게 절을 한 뒤 모두에게 앉기를 청했다."], ["The blood rushed to Alyosha’s cheeks.", "알료샤의 두 뺨으로 피가 솟구쳤다."], ["He was ashamed.", "그는 수치스러웠다."], ["His forebodings were coming true.", "그의 불길한 예감이 그대로 들어맞고 있었다."]], [["Father Zossima sat down on a very old-fashioned mahogany sofa, covered with leather, and made his visitors sit down in a row along the opposite wall on four mahogany chairs, covered with shabby black leather.", "조시마 장로는 가죽을 씌운 아주 고풍스러운 마호가니 소파에 앉아, 손님들에게는 맞은편 벽을 따라 한 줄로 놓인 마호가니 의자 넷에 나란히 앉도록 했는데, 그 의자에는 낡고 검은 가죽이 씌워져 있었다."], ["The monks sat, one at the door and the other at the window.", "수도사들은 하나는 문가에, 다른 하나는 창가에 자리를 잡았다."], ["The divinity student, the novice, and Alyosha remained standing.", "신학생과 수련 수사, 그리고 알료샤는 선 채로 있었다."], ["The cell was not very large and had a faded look.", "암자는 그다지 넓지 않았고 빛바랜 모습이었다."], ["It contained nothing but the most necessary furniture, of coarse and poor quality.", "그 안에는 거칠고 조악한 품질의, 가장 긴요한 가구 외에는 아무것도 없었다."], ["There were two pots of flowers in the window, and a number of holy pictures in the corner.", "창가에는 화분이 둘 놓여 있었고, 한구석에는 성화가 여럿 걸려 있었다."], ["Before one huge ancient ikon of the Virgin a lamp was burning.", "거대하고 오래된 성모상 하나 앞에는 등불이 타오르고 있었다."], ["Near it were two other holy pictures in shining settings, and, next them, carved cherubims, china eggs, a Catholic cross of ivory, with a Mater Dolorosa embracing it, and several foreign engravings from the great Italian artists of past centuries.", "그 곁에는 번쩍이는 틀에 끼운 다른 성화가 둘 있었고, 그 옆으로는 조각된 천사상들과 도자기 달걀, 상아로 만든 가톨릭 십자가가 있었는데 그 십자가에는 슬픔에 잠긴 성모가 끌어안고 있었으며, 또 지난 세기 위대한 이탈리아 화가들의 외국 판화 몇 점이 있었다."], ["Next to these costly and artistic engravings were several of the roughest Russian prints of saints and martyrs, such as are sold for a few farthings at all the fairs.", "이 값비싸고 예술적인 판화들 곁에는 모든 장터에서 몇 푼이면 살 수 있는, 성인과 순교자를 새긴 가장 조잡한 러시아 판화 몇 점이 나란히 걸려 있었다."], ["On the other walls were portraits of Russian bishops, past and present.", "다른 벽에는 과거와 현재의 러시아 주교들 초상이 걸려 있었다."]], [["Miüsov took a cursory glance at all these “conventional”", "미우소프는 이 모든 \"판에 박힌\""], ["surroundings and bent an intent look upon the elder.", "주변을 스치듯 한번 둘러본 뒤, 장로에게 골똘한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["He had a high opinion of his own insight, a weakness excusable in him as he was fifty, an age at which a clever man of the world of established position can hardly help taking himself rather seriously.", "그는 자신의 통찰력을 매우 높이 평가하고 있었는데, 그것은 그에게서는 용서받을 만한 약점이었으니, 그도 이미 쉰의 나이로, 세상 물정에 밝고 지위가 확고한 영민한 사내가 자기 자신을 제법 진지하게 여기지 않기란 그 나이에 이르러서는 좀처럼 어려운 일이기 때문이다."], ["At the first moment he did not like Zossima.", "첫 순간부터 그는 조시마가 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["There was, indeed, something in the elder’s face which many people besides Miüsov might not have liked.", "사실 장로의 얼굴에는 미우소프 말고도 많은 이들이 마음에 들어 하지 않을 만한 무언가가 있었다."], ["He was a short, bent, little man, with very weak legs, and though he was only sixty-five, he looked at least ten years older.", "그는 키가 작고 등이 굽은 자그마한 사람으로, 다리가 몹시 약했으며, 비록 예순다섯밖에 되지 않았으나 적어도 열 살은 더 늙어 보였다."], ["His face was very thin and covered with a network of fine wrinkles, particularly numerous about his eyes, which were small, light-colored, quick, and shining like two bright points.", "그의 얼굴은 매우 야위었고 가느다란 주름이 그물처럼 뒤덮고 있었는데, 특히 눈가에 주름이 유난히 많았으니, 그 눈은 작고 빛깔이 옅으며 재빠르고, 두 개의 반짝이는 점처럼 빛나고 있었다."], ["He had a sprinkling of gray hair about his temples.", "그의 관자놀이 언저리에는 흰머리가 듬성듬성 나 있었다."], ["His pointed beard was small and scanty, and his lips, which smiled frequently, were as thin as two threads.", "뾰족한 턱수염은 작고 성겼으며, 자주 미소 짓는 그의 입술은 두 가닥 실처럼 얇았다."], ["His nose was not long, but sharp, like a bird’s beak.", "그의 코는 길지는 않았으나 새의 부리처럼 날카로웠다."]], [["“To all appearances a malicious soul, full of petty pride,”", "\"누가 봐도 악의에 찬 영혼, 자잘한 자만심으로 가득한 자로군.\""], ["thought Miüsov.", "미우소프는 생각했다."], ["He felt altogether dissatisfied with his position.", "그는 자신의 처지가 도무지 못마땅하게 느껴졌다."]], [["A cheap little clock on the wall struck twelve hurriedly, and served to begin the conversation.", "벽에 걸린 값싼 작은 시계가 서둘러 열두 시를 알렸고, 그것이 대화를 시작하는 계기가 되었다."]], [["“Precisely to our time,”", "\"정확히 시간 맞춰 왔습니다그려.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, “but no sign of my son, Dmitri.", "표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다. \"한데 제 아들 드미트리는 코빼기도 안 보이는군요."], ["I apologize for him, sacred elder!”", "그 녀석을 대신해 사죄드립니다, 거룩하신 장로님!\""], ["(Alyosha shuddered all over at “sacred elder.”) “I am always punctual myself, minute for minute, remembering that punctuality is the courtesy of kings....”", "(알료샤는 \"거룩하신 장로님\"이라는 말에 온몸이 부르르 떨렸다.) \"저로 말할 것 같으면 늘 시간을 정확히, 일 분도 어김없이 지키지요. 시간 엄수란 제왕의 예의라는 말을 명심하고 있기에…….\""]], [["“But you are not a king, anyway,”", "\"하지만 댁은 어쨌든 제왕이 아니지 않소.\""], ["Miüsov muttered, losing his self-restraint at once.", "미우소프가 곧장 자제심을 잃고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Yes; that’s true.", "\"옳소, 그건 맞는 말입니다."], ["I’m not a king, and, would you believe it, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, I was aware of that myself.", "나는 제왕이 아니지요. 한데 믿어 주시겠소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 나도 그쯤은 스스로 알고 있었답니다."], ["But, there!", "한데 이것 보시오!"], ["I always say the wrong thing.", "나는 늘 엉뚱한 소리만 한단 말입니다."], ["Your reverence,”", "신부님,\""], ["he cried, with sudden pathos, “you behold before you a buffoon in earnest!", "그는 별안간 비장한 어조로 외쳤다. \"당신 앞에 진짜배기 어릿광대가 서 있는 걸 보고 계십니다!"], ["I introduce myself as such.", "나 스스로 그렇게 소개하는 바입니다."], ["It’s an old habit, alas!", "오래된 버릇이지요, 아아!"], ["And if I sometimes talk nonsense out of place it’s with an object, with the object of amusing people and making myself agreeable.", "그리고 때때로 자리에 어울리지 않게 허튼소리를 늘어놓는 것도 다 속셈이 있어서랍니다. 사람들을 즐겁게 하고 나 자신을 호감 가게 만들려는 속셈이지요."], ["One must be agreeable, mustn’t one?", "사람이란 호감을 사야 하지 않겠소, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["I was seven years ago in a little town where I had business, and I made friends with some merchants there.", "칠 년 전에 볼일이 있어 어느 작은 읍내에 갔다가 그곳 상인들 몇몇과 친해졌지요. 우리가 무슨 일로 경찰서장을 만날 일이 생겨서, 함께 식사나 하자고 청하러 그를 찾아갔습니다."], ["We went to the captain of police because we had to see him about something, and to ask him to dine with us.", "그는 키 크고 뚱뚱하며 머리가 희끄무레하고 시무룩한 사내였는데, 이런 경우에는 가장 위험한 부류지요. 다 간 때문이랍니다."], ["He was a tall, fat, fair, sulky man, the most dangerous type in such cases.", "나는 곧장 그에게 다가가, 세상 물정에 밝은 사내답게 여유롭게, 아시겠소, 이렇게 말했지요. '이스프라브니크* 나리, 우리의 나프라브니크가 되어 주시구려.'"], ["It’s their liver.", "그러자 그가 묻습디다. '나프라브니크라니 그게 무슨 소리요?'"], ["I went straight up to him, and with the ease of a man of the world, you know, ‘Mr.", "나는 첫 반 초 만에 농담이 빗나갔다는 걸 알아챘지요. 그가 그렇게 시무룩하게 서 있는 걸 보고 말입니다."], ["Ispravnik,’ said I, ‘be our Napravnik.’ ‘What do you mean by Napravnik?’ said he.", "나는 말했지요. '농담을 좀 해 보려던 거올시다."], ["I saw, at the first half-second, that it had missed fire.", "다 함께 흥겨우라고요. 나프라브니크 씨로 말할 것 같으면 우리 러시아의 유명한 오케스트라 지휘자이고, 우리 일이 화합을 이루려면 바로 그런 인물 같은 사람이 필요하지 않겠소.'"], ["He stood there so glum.", "이만하면 제법 그럴듯하게 비유를 설명한 거 아닙니까? 한데 그가 이러더군요."], ["‘I wanted to make a joke,’ said I, ‘for the general diversion, as Mr.", "'실례지만, 나는 이스프라브니크요. 내 직무를 두고 말장난하는 건 용납하지 못하겠소.'"], ["Napravnik is our well-known Russian orchestra conductor and what we need for the harmony of our undertaking is some one of that sort.’ And I explained my comparison very reasonably, didn’t I?", "그러고는 돌아서 가 버렸습니다. 나는 뒤따라가며 외쳤지요."], ["‘Excuse me,’ said he, ‘I am an Ispravnik, and I do not allow puns to be made on my calling.’ He turned and walked away.", "'옳소, 옳소, 당신은 이스프라브니크요, 나프라브니크가 아니라.' 그러자 그가 이러더군요."], ["I followed him, shouting, ‘Yes, yes, you are an Ispravnik, not a Napravnik.’ ‘No,’ he said, ‘since you called me a Napravnik I am one.’ And would you believe it, it ruined our business!", "'아니, 당신이 나를 나프라브니크라고 불렀으니 나는 나프라브니크요.' 그래, 믿어지십니까, 그 일로 우리 볼일이 다 망쳐졌단 말입니다!"], ["And I’m always like that, always like that.", "나는 늘 이 모양이지요, 늘 이 모양이라니까요. 언제나 내 예의 때문에 스스로를 다치게 한단 말입니다."], ["Always injuring myself with my politeness.", "한번은, 여러 해 전 일입니다만, 어느 세도가에게 이렇게 말했지요."], ["Once, many years ago, I said to an influential person: ‘Your wife is a ticklish lady,’ in an honorable sense, of the moral qualities, so to speak.", "'댁의 부인은 간지럼을 잘 타는 분이시군요.' 점잖은 뜻으로, 말하자면 도덕적 품성을 두고 한 말이었지요."], ["But he asked me, ‘Why, have you tickled her?’ I thought I’d be polite, so I couldn’t help saying, ‘Yes,’ and he gave me a fine tickling on the spot.", "한데 그가 내게 묻더군요. '아니, 그럼 당신이 내 아내를 간질여 봤단 말이오?'"], ["Only that happened long ago, so I’m not ashamed to tell the story.", "나는 예의를 차리겠답시고 '예' 하고 대답하지 않을 수 없었는데, 그가 그 자리에서 나를 톡톡히 간질여 주더이다."], ["I’m always injuring myself like that.”", "다만 그건 오래전 일이니 부끄러움 없이 이야기할 수 있군요. 나는 늘 이런 식으로 스스로를 다치게 한단 말입니다.\""]], [["“You’re doing it now,”", "\"지금도 그러고 있지 않소.\""], ["muttered Miüsov, with disgust.", "미우소프가 역겹다는 듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["Father Zossima scrutinized them both in silence.", "조시마 장로는 말없이 두 사람을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."]], [["“Am I?", "\"내가요?"], ["Would you believe it, I was aware of that, too, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, and let me tell you, indeed, I foresaw I should as soon as I began to speak.", "믿어지십니까, 그것도 나는 알고 있었답니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 게다가 말이지요, 사실 나는 입을 떼는 순간 이미 이렇게 되리란 걸 내다보고 있었지요."], ["And do you know I foresaw, too, that you’d be the first to remark on it.", "그리고 당신이 제일 먼저 그 점을 지적하리란 것도 내다봤단 걸 아시오?"], ["The minute I see my joke isn’t coming off, your reverence, both my cheeks feel as though they were drawn down to the lower jaw and there is almost a spasm in them.", "신부님, 내 농담이 먹히지 않는다 싶은 순간이면 양쪽 뺨이 마치 아래턱까지 끌려 내려가는 것 같고 거의 경련이 일 지경이지요. 젊을 적부터 그랬답니다, 귀족 집안에서 농담을 해 가며 밥벌이를 해야 했던 그 시절부터요."], ["That’s been so since I was young, when I had to make jokes for my living in noblemen’s families.", "나는 뼛속까지 어릿광대이고, 태어날 때부터 그랬답니다, 신부님. 마치 광기처럼 내 안에 박혀 있는 것 같지요."], ["I am an inveterate buffoon, and have been from birth up, your reverence, it’s as though it were a craze in me.", "아마 내 안에 마귀가 들어 있는 게지요. 다만 아주 자그마한 놈이올시다."], ["I dare say it’s a devil within me. But only a little one.", "좀 더 거물급 마귀라면 다른 거처를 골랐을 테니까요."], ["A more serious one would have chosen another lodging.", "하지만 당신 영혼은 아니올시다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치. 당신 역시 들어앉을 만한 거처는 못 되거든요."], ["But not your soul, Pyotr Alexandrovitch; you’re not a lodging worth having either.", "그래도 나는 믿습니다—나는 하느님을 믿어요, 요즘 들어 의심이 들기는 했습니다만. 한데 이제 나는 여기 앉아 지혜로운 말씀을 기다리고 있지요."], ["But I do believe—I believe in God, though I have had doubts of late.", "나는 철학자 디드로 같은 사람이올시다, 신부님. 거룩하신 신부님, 혹시 들어 보신 적 있으신지요, 디드로가 예카테리나 여제 시절에 대주교 플라톤을 찾아간 이야기 말입니다."], ["But now I sit and await words of wisdom.", "그가 들어서서 대뜸 이렇게 말했답니다. '하느님은 없소.'"], ["I’m like the philosopher, Diderot, your reverence.", "그러자 그 위대한 주교가 손가락을 치켜들며 답했지요."], ["Did you ever hear, most Holy Father, how Diderot went to see the Metropolitan Platon, in the time of the Empress Catherine?", "'어리석은 자는 그 마음에 하느님이 없다 하였도다.' 그러자 그가 그 자리에서 주교의 발치에 엎드렸답니다."], ["He went in and said straight out, ‘There is no God.’ To which the great bishop lifted up his finger and answered, ‘The fool hath said in his heart there is no God.’ And he fell down at his feet on the spot.", "'나는 믿습니다,' 그가 외쳤지요."], ["‘I believe,’ he cried, ‘and will be christened.’ And so he was.", "'세례를 받겠습니다.' 그래서 그는 세례를 받았답니다."], ["Princess Dashkov was his godmother, and Potyomkin his godfather.”", "다시코바 공작부인이 그의 대모였고, 포툠킨이 대부였지요.\""]], [["“Fyodor Pavlovitch, this is unbearable!", "\"표도르 파블로비치, 이건 정말 못 참겠소!"], ["You know you’re telling lies and that that stupid anecdote isn’t true.", "댁이 거짓말을 하고 있고 그 어이없는 일화가 사실이 아니란 걸 댁도 알지 않소."], ["Why are you playing the fool?”", "어째서 그렇게 광대 짓을 하는 거요?\""], ["cried Miüsov in a shaking voice.", "미우소프가 떨리는 목소리로 소리쳤다."]], [["“I suspected all my life that it wasn’t true,”", "\"나는 평생 그게 사실이 아닐 거라고 의심해 왔습니다.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch cried with conviction.", "표도르 파블로비치가 확신에 찬 어조로 외쳤다."], ["“But I’ll tell you the whole truth, gentlemen.", "\"한데 여러분, 솔직히 다 털어놓겠습니다."], ["Great elder!", "위대한 장로님!"], ["Forgive me, the last thing about Diderot’s christening I made up just now.", "용서해 주십시오, 디드로의 세례 운운한 마지막 대목은 방금 막 지어낸 겁니다."], ["I never thought of it before.", "전에는 생각해 본 적도 없는 얘기지요."], ["I made it up to add piquancy.", "좀 더 톡 쏘는 맛을 더하려고 꾸며 댄 거올시다."], ["I play the fool, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, to make myself agreeable.", "나는 호감을 사려고 광대 짓을 하는 거랍니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치."], ["Though I really don’t know myself, sometimes, what I do it for.", "그래도 솔직히 가끔은 나 스스로도 왜 그러는지 모르겠어요."], ["And as for Diderot, I heard as far as ‘the fool hath said in his heart’ twenty times from the gentry about here when I was young.", "디드로로 말하자면, '어리석은 자는 그 마음에' 하는 대목까지는 젊을 적 이 고장 양반들에게서 스무 번도 더 들었지요."], ["I heard your aunt, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, tell the story.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신 고모님이 그 이야기를 하는 것도 들었답니다."], ["They all believe to this day that the infidel Diderot came to dispute about God with the Metropolitan Platon....”", "그들은 다들 지금까지도 무신론자 디드로가 하느님을 두고 따지러 대주교 플라톤을 찾아갔다고 믿고 있지요…….\""]], [["Miüsov got up, forgetting himself in his impatience.", "미우소프는 조바심에 자제심을 잃고 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["He was furious, and conscious of being ridiculous.", "그는 격분해 있었고, 자기가 우스꽝스러운 꼴이 되었다는 것도 의식하고 있었다."]], [["What was taking place in the cell was really incredible.", "암자 안에서 벌어지고 있는 일은 실로 믿기 어려운 광경이었다."], ["For forty or fifty years past, from the times of former elders, no visitors had entered that cell without feelings of the profoundest veneration.", "지난 사오십 년 동안, 이전 장로들의 시대로부터 이 암자에 들어선 방문객 가운데 가장 깊은 경외심을 품지 않은 자는 없었다."], ["Almost every one admitted to the cell felt that a great favor was being shown him.", "이 암자에 들어오도록 허락받은 사람치고 자기가 큰 은혜를 입고 있다고 느끼지 않은 이가 거의 없었다."], ["Many remained kneeling during the whole visit.", "적잖은 이들이 방문하는 내내 무릎을 꿇고 있었다."], ["Of those visitors, many had been men of high rank and learning, some even freethinkers, attracted by curiosity, but all without exception had shown the profoundest reverence and delicacy, for here there was no question of money, but only, on the one side love and kindness, and on the other penitence and eager desire to decide some spiritual problem or crisis.", "그 방문객들 가운데는 높은 지위와 학식을 갖춘 인사들도 많았고, 더러는 호기심에 끌려 찾아온 자유사상가도 있었으나, 하나같이 예외 없이 더없이 깊은 경외심과 섬세한 예의를 보였으니, 이곳에서는 돈 문제 따위는 있을 수 없었고 오직 한편에는 사랑과 친절이, 다른 한편에는 참회와, 어떤 영혼의 문제나 위기를 풀고자 하는 간절한 열망이 있을 뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["So that such buffoonery amazed and bewildered the spectators, or at least some of them.", "그러니 이런 광대 짓은 구경꾼들을, 적어도 그들 가운데 일부를 깜짝 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 만들었다."], ["The monks, with unchanged countenances, waited, with earnest attention, to hear what the elder would say, but seemed on the point of standing up, like Miüsov.", "수도사들은 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않은 채 진지하게 귀를 기울이며 장로가 무어라 말할지 기다리고 있었으나, 미우소프처럼 막 일어서려는 듯 보였다."], ["Alyosha stood, with hanging head, on the verge of tears.", "알료샤는 고개를 떨군 채 눈물을 쏟기 직전이었다."], ["What seemed to him strangest of all was that his brother Ivan, on whom alone he had rested his hopes, and who alone had such influence on his father that he could have stopped him, sat now quite unmoved, with downcast eyes, apparently waiting with interest to see how it would end, as though he had nothing to do with it.", "그에게 무엇보다 이상하게 여겨진 것은, 그가 유일하게 희망을 걸었던 형 이반이, 그리고 아버지에게 그를 막을 만한 영향력을 지닌 유일한 사람이었던 이반이, 지금은 눈을 내리깐 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아, 마치 자기와는 아무 상관도 없다는 듯 이 일이 어떻게 끝날지 흥미롭게 지켜보며 기다리고 있는 것 같았다는 점이었다."], ["Alyosha did not dare to look at Rakitin, the divinity student, whom he knew almost intimately.", "알료샤는 신학생 라키틴 쪽으로는 차마 눈길을 돌리지 못했는데, 그와는 거의 막역한 사이였다."], ["He alone in the monastery knew Rakitin’s thoughts.", "수도원에서 라키틴의 속생각을 아는 자는 그뿐이었다."]], [["“Forgive me,”", "\"용서하십시오.\""], ["began Miüsov, addressing Father Zossima, “for perhaps I seem to be taking part in this shameful foolery.", "미우소프가 조시마 장로를 향해 입을 열었다. \"어쩌면 제가 이 수치스러운 어릿광대 짓에 한몫 끼는 것처럼 보일지도 모르겠습니다만."], ["I made a mistake in believing that even a man like Fyodor Pavlovitch would understand what was due on a visit to so honored a personage.", "표도르 파블로비치 같은 사람이라도 이토록 존경받는 어른을 찾아뵐 때 갖추어야 할 도리쯤은 알 줄로 믿었던 것이 제 잘못이었습니다."], ["I did not suppose I should have to apologize simply for having come with him....”", "그저 이 사람과 함께 왔다는 것만으로 사죄를 드려야 할 줄은 미처 생각지 못했습니다…….\""]], [["Pyotr Alexandrovitch could say no more, and was about to leave the room, overwhelmed with confusion.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치는 더는 말을 잇지 못한 채, 당혹감에 짓눌려 방을 나서려 했다."]], [["“Don’t distress yourself, I beg.”", "\"부디 괴로워 마십시오.\""], ["The elder got on to his feeble legs, and taking Pyotr Alexandrovitch by both hands, made him sit down again.", "장로가 후들거리는 다리로 일어서더니, 표트르 알렉산드로비치의 두 손을 잡아 다시 앉혔다."], ["“I beg you not to disturb yourself.", "\"제발 마음을 어지럽히지 마십시오."], ["I particularly beg you to be my guest.”", "특히 당신께 부디 제 손님으로 남아 주시기를 청합니다.\""], ["And with a bow he went back and sat down again on his little sofa.", "그러고는 절을 한 뒤 돌아가 다시 자기의 작은 소파에 앉았다."]], [["“Great elder, speak!", "\"위대한 장로님, 말씀해 주십시오!"], ["Do I annoy you by my vivacity?”", "제 활달함이 장로님을 성가시게 합니까?\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch cried suddenly, clutching the arms of his chair in both hands, as though ready to leap up from it if the answer were unfavorable.", "표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 소리치며, 답이 신통치 않으면 당장이라도 의자에서 펄쩍 뛰어오를 듯 두 손으로 의자 팔걸이를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“I earnestly beg you, too, not to disturb yourself, and not to be uneasy,”", "\"당신께도 간곡히 청하건대, 부디 마음을 어지럽히지 말고 불안해하지 마십시오.\""], ["the elder said impressively.", "장로가 무게 있게 말했다."], ["“Do not trouble.", "\"괴로워 마십시오."], ["Make yourself quite at home.", "편안히 계십시오."], ["And, above all, do not be so ashamed of yourself, for that is at the root of it all.”", "그리고 무엇보다도 스스로를 그토록 부끄러워하지 마십시오. 모든 일의 뿌리가 바로 거기에 있으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Quite at home?", "\"편안히 있으라고요?"], ["To be my natural self?", "본디의 나 자신이 되라고요?"], ["Oh, that is much too much, but I accept it with grateful joy.", "오, 그건 너무도 과분한 말씀입니다만, 고마운 기쁨으로 받아들이겠습니다."], ["Do you know, blessed Father, you’d better not invite me to be my natural self.", "한데 아시겠습니까, 복되신 신부님, 저더러 본디의 나 자신이 되라고는 권하지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다."], ["Don’t risk it....", "그건 무릅쓰지 마십시오……."], ["I will not go so far as that myself.", "저 스스로도 거기까지는 가지 않을 작정이니까요."], ["I warn you for your own sake.", "당신을 위해서 미리 경고해 두는 겁니다."], ["Well, the rest is still plunged in the mists of uncertainty, though there are people who’d be pleased to describe me for you.", "자, 나머지 일은 아직도 불확실의 안개 속에 잠겨 있습니다만, 저에 관해 기꺼이 묘사해 드리려는 사람들도 있긴 하지요."], ["I mean that for you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "그건 당신더러 들으라고 하는 말입니다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치."], ["But as for you, holy being, let me tell you, I am brimming over with ecstasy.”", "한데 거룩하신 분이여, 당신께는 말씀드리지요, 저는 환희로 넘쳐흐르고 있습니다.\""]], [["He got up, and throwing up his hands, declaimed, “Blessed be the womb that bare thee, and the paps that gave thee suck—the paps especially.", "그는 일어서서 두 손을 치켜들고 낭송하듯 외쳤다. \"당신을 밴 태와 당신을 먹인 젖가슴이 복되도다—특히 그 젖가슴이 말입니다."], ["When you said just now, ‘Don’t be so ashamed of yourself, for that is at the root of it all,’ you pierced right through me by that remark, and read me to the core.", "방금 전 당신이 '스스로를 그토록 부끄러워하지 마라, 모든 일의 뿌리가 거기에 있으니' 하고 말씀하셨을 때, 그 한마디가 저를 꿰뚫고 들어와 제 속을 속속들이 읽어 내셨습니다."], ["Indeed, I always feel when I meet people that I am lower than all, and that they all take me for a buffoon.", "정말이지 저는 사람들을 만날 때마다 제가 누구보다 못난 자라고, 그리고 그들 모두가 저를 어릿광대로 여긴다고 느끼지요. 그래서 저는 이렇게 말합니다."], ["So I say, ‘Let me really play the buffoon.", "'그래, 진짜 광대 노릇을 해 주겠다."], ["I am not afraid of your opinion, for you are every one of you worse than I am.’ That is why I am a buffoon.", "너희들 평판 따위 두렵지 않다. 너희들 하나하나가 다 나보다 더 못된 자들이니까.'"], ["It is from shame, great elder, from shame; it’s simply over-sensitiveness that makes me rowdy.", "그래서 제가 어릿광대인 겁니다. 다 수치심에서 비롯된 거지요, 위대한 장로님, 수치심에서요."], ["If I had only been sure that every one would accept me as the kindest and wisest of men, oh, Lord, what a good man I should have been then!", "그저 지나친 예민함 탓에 제가 이렇게 소란을 떠는 거랍니다. 모두가 저를 가장 친절하고 가장 지혜로운 사람으로 받아들여 주리란 확신만 있었더라면, 오, 주님, 그때 저는 얼마나 훌륭한 사람이 되었겠습니까!"], ["Teacher!”", "스승님!\""], ["he fell suddenly on his knees, “what must I do to gain eternal life?”", "그는 별안간 무릎을 꿇었다. \"영생을 얻으려면 저는 무엇을 해야 합니까?\""]], [["It was difficult even now to decide whether he was joking or really moved.", "지금도 그가 농담을 하는 것인지 정말로 감동한 것인지는 가늠하기 어려웠다."]], [["Father Zossima, lifting his eyes, looked at him, and said with a smile:", "조시마 장로는 눈을 들어 그를 바라보더니, 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“You have known for a long time what you must do.", "\"무엇을 해야 할지는 당신도 이미 오래전부터 알고 있습니다. 당신에게는 분별력이 충분히 있으니까요."], ["You have sense enough: don’t give way to drunkenness and incontinence of speech; don’t give way to sensual lust; and, above all, to the love of money.", "폭음에 빠지지 말고, 함부로 입을 놀리지 마십시오. 육체의 정욕에 빠지지 말고, 무엇보다도 돈을 사랑하는 데 빠지지 마십시오."], ["And close your taverns.", "그리고 당신의 술집들을 닫으십시오."], ["If you can’t close all, at least two or three.", "다 닫을 수 없다면 적어도 두셋이라도 닫으십시오."], ["And, above all—don’t lie.”", "그리고 무엇보다도—거짓말을 하지 마십시오.\""]], [["“You mean about Diderot?”", "\"디드로 얘기 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“No, not about Diderot.", "\"아니, 디드로 얘기가 아닙니다."], ["Above all, don’t lie to yourself.", "무엇보다도 자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하지 마십시오."], ["The man who lies to himself and listens to his own lie comes to such a pass that he cannot distinguish the truth within him, or around him, and so loses all respect for himself and for others.", "자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하고 제가 한 거짓말에 귀 기울이는 자는, 마침내 제 안에서도 제 주위에서도 진실을 분간하지 못하는 지경에 이르고, 그리하여 자기 자신과 남들에 대한 존경을 모두 잃고 맙니다."], ["And having no respect he ceases to love, and in order to occupy and distract himself without love he gives way to passions and coarse pleasures, and sinks to bestiality in his vices, all from continual lying to other men and to himself.", "그리고 존경할 줄 모르게 되면 사랑하기를 그치고, 사랑 없이 제 마음을 채우고 달래려고 온갖 정념과 거친 쾌락에 몸을 맡기다가, 끝내 그 악덕 속에서 짐승의 수준으로까지 가라앉고 마는데, 이 모두가 남들에게도 자기 자신에게도 끊임없이 거짓말을 한 데서 비롯되는 겁니다."], ["The man who lies to himself can be more easily offended than any one.", "자기 자신에게 거짓말을 하는 자는 누구보다도 쉽게 모욕을 느낄 수 있지요."], ["You know it is sometimes very pleasant to take offense, isn’t it?", "더러는 모욕을 당하는 일이 참으로 기분 좋은 법이라는 걸 아시지요, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["A man may know that nobody has insulted him, but that he has invented the insult for himself, has lied and exaggerated to make it picturesque, has caught at a word and made a mountain out of a molehill—he knows that himself, yet he will be the first to take offense, and will revel in his resentment till he feels great pleasure in it, and so pass to genuine vindictiveness.", "어떤 사람은 아무도 자기를 모욕하지 않았다는 걸, 자기가 그 모욕을 스스로 지어냈다는 걸, 그럴듯하게 보이려고 거짓말로 부풀렸다는 걸, 말 한마디를 붙잡아 도토리만 한 일을 태산처럼 키웠다는 걸—그 자신도 다 알면서, 그러면서도 누구보다 먼저 모욕을 느끼고, 제 원한 속에서 마냥 흐뭇해하다가 마침내 거기서 큰 쾌감을 느끼는 지경에 이르러, 그렇게 진짜 앙심으로 옮겨 가는 겁니다."], ["But get up, sit down, I beg you.", "한데 일어나십시오, 앉으세요, 제발."], ["All this, too, is deceitful posturing....”", "이것도 다 거짓된 몸짓이올시다…….\""]], [["“Blessed man!", "\"복되신 분이여!"], ["Give me your hand to kiss.”", "입 맞추도록 손을 내어 주십시오.\""]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch skipped up, and imprinted a rapid kiss on the elder’s thin hand.", "표도르 파블로비치는 펄쩍 일어나, 장로의 가느다란 손에 잽싸게 입을 맞추었다."], ["“It is, it is pleasant to take offense.", "\"그렇습니다, 모욕을 당하는 일은 기분 좋은 거지요."], ["You said that so well, as I never heard it before.", "그 말씀을 어찌나 잘하시는지, 그런 말은 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Yes, I have been all my life taking offense, to please myself, taking offense on esthetic grounds, for it is not so much pleasant as distinguished sometimes to be insulted—that you had forgotten, great elder, it is distinguished!", "그렇습니다, 저는 평생토록 모욕을 당해 왔지요, 제 즐거움을 위해, 심미적인 이유로 모욕을 당해 왔습니다. 모욕을 당하는 일이란 기분 좋다기보다 때로는 품위 있는 일이거든요—그건 잊으셨군요, 위대한 장로님, 품위 있는 일이란 말입니다!"], ["I shall make a note of that.", "그건 메모해 두겠습니다."], ["But I have been lying, lying positively my whole life long, every day and hour of it.", "한데 저는 거짓말을 해 왔습니다, 평생토록 줄곧 거짓말을 해 왔지요, 하루도, 한 시간도 빠짐없이요."], ["Of a truth, I am a lie, and the father of lies.", "진실로 저는 거짓말 그 자체요, 거짓의 아비올시다."], ["Though I believe I am not the father of lies.", "하기야 제가 거짓의 아비는 아닌 것 같습니다만."], ["I am getting mixed in my texts.", "제가 성경 구절을 자꾸 헷갈리는군요."], ["Say, the son of lies, and that will be enough.", "거짓의 아들이라고 해 두지요, 그러면 충분할 겁니다."], ["Only ...", "다만……"], ["my angel ...", "나의 천사여……"], ["I may sometimes talk about Diderot!", "저는 가끔 디드로 얘기쯤은 할 수도 있는 거지요!"], ["Diderot will do no harm, though sometimes a word will do harm.", "디드로는 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 겁니다, 가끔은 말 한마디가 해를 끼치기도 합니다만. 위대한 장로님, 그건 그렇고, 깜빡 잊을 뻔했군요."], ["Great elder, by the way, I was forgetting, though I had been meaning for the last two years to come here on purpose to ask and to find out something.", "지난 이 년 동안 일부러 여기 찾아와 묻고 알아보려고 작정했던 일이 있습니다. 다만 표트르 알렉산드로비치더러 제 말을 막지 말라고 일러 주십시오."], ["Only do tell Pyotr Alexandrovitch not to interrupt me.", "제 질문은 이렇습니다. 위대한 신부님, 정말로 어딘가 성인전에 이런 이야기가 전해진다는 게 사실입니까."], ["Here is my question: Is it true, great Father, that the story is told somewhere in the Lives of the Saints of a holy saint martyred for his faith who, when his head was cut off at last, stood up, picked up his head, and, ‘courteously kissing it,’ walked a long way, carrying it in his hands.", "신앙 때문에 순교한 어느 거룩한 성인이 마침내 목이 잘리자 일어서서 제 머리를 주워 들고, '정중히 입을 맞추며' 그것을 두 손에 받쳐 든 채 먼 길을 걸어갔다는 이야기 말입니다."], ["Is that true or not, honored Father?”", "그게 사실입니까, 아닙니까, 존경하는 신부님?\""]], [["“No, it is untrue,”", "\"아니, 그건 사실이 아니오.\""], ["said the elder.", "장로가 말했다."]], [["“There is nothing of the kind in all the lives of the saints.", "\"성인전 어디에도 그런 이야기는 없소."], ["What saint do you say the story is told of?”", "그 이야기가 어느 성인에 관한 것이라고 하셨소?\""], ["asked the Father Librarian.", "도서 담당 신부가 물었다."]], [["“I do not know what saint.", "\"무슨 성인인지는 모르오."], ["I do not know, and can’t tell.", "모릅니다, 말씀드릴 수가 없군요."], ["I was deceived.", "제가 속은 겁니다."], ["I was told the story.", "누가 그 이야기를 해 주더이다."], ["I had heard it, and do you know who told it?", "그걸 들었지요, 한데 누가 해 주었는지 아십니까?"], ["Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov here, who was so angry just now about Diderot.", "바로 여기 계신 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프올시다, 방금 디드로 얘기로 그토록 화를 내시던 그분 말입니다."], ["He it was who told the story.”", "그 이야기를 해 준 사람이 바로 저분이었지요.\""]], [["“I have never told it you, I never speak to you at all.”", "\"나는 당신에게 그런 이야기를 한 적이 없소, 당신과는 도통 말을 섞지도 않으니까.\""]], [["“It is true you did not tell me, but you told it when I was present.", "\"당신이 내게 직접 말한 건 아니지요, 하지만 내가 그 자리에 있을 때 그 이야기를 했잖소."], ["It was three years ago.", "삼 년 전 일이올시다."], ["I mentioned it because by that ridiculous story you shook my faith, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "내가 이 얘길 꺼낸 건, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 그 어이없는 이야기로 당신이 내 신앙을 뒤흔들어 놓았기 때문이오."], ["You knew nothing of it, but I went home with my faith shaken, and I have been getting more and more shaken ever since.", "당신은 까맣게 몰랐겠지만, 나는 신앙이 흔들린 채로 집에 돌아갔고, 그 뒤로 점점 더 흔들려 왔단 말입니다."], ["Yes, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, you were the cause of a great fall.", "그렇소, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신이 바로 큰 타락의 원인이었소."], ["That was not a Diderot!”", "그건 디드로 따위와는 차원이 다른 일이었단 말이오!\""]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch got excited and pathetic, though it was perfectly clear to every one by now that he was playing a part again.", "표도르 파블로비치는 흥분하여 비장한 어조가 되었으나, 이제는 누구의 눈에도 그가 또다시 연극을 하고 있다는 것이 빤히 드러나 있었다."], ["Yet Miüsov was stung by his words.", "그런데도 미우소프는 그의 말에 따끔하게 찔렸다."]], [["“What nonsense, and it is all nonsense,”", "\"무슨 헛소리요, 이건 죄다 헛소리란 말이오.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."], ["“I may really have told it, some time or other ...", "\"내가 정말로 언젠가 그 얘기를 했을 수는 있지요…… 하지만 당신에게 한 건 아니오."], ["but not to you. I was told it myself.", "나도 누군가에게서 들은 얘기란 말입니다."], ["I heard it in Paris from a Frenchman.", "파리에서 어느 프랑스인에게 들었지요."], ["He told me it was read at our mass from the Lives of the Saints ...", "그자는 그게 우리 미사 때 성인전에서 낭독되는 이야기라고 하더군요……"], ["he was a very learned man who had made a special study of Russian statistics and had lived a long time in Russia....", "그는 러시아 통계를 특별히 연구한 매우 학식 깊은 사람으로, 러시아에서 오래 살았던 인물이었소……"], ["I have not read the Lives of the Saints myself, and I am not going to read them ...", "나 자신은 성인전을 읽어 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 읽을 생각도 없소……"], ["all sorts of things are said at dinner—we were dining then.”", "식사 자리에서야 온갖 얘기가 다 나오는 법 아니오—그때 우리는 식사를 하던 중이었으니까.\""]], [["“Yes, you were dining then, and so I lost my faith!”", "\"그렇소, 당신은 그때 식사를 하고 있었고, 그 바람에 나는 신앙을 잃었지요!\""], ["said Fyodor Pavlovitch, mimicking him.", "표도르 파블로비치가 그를 흉내 내며 말했다."]], [["“What do I care for your faith?”", "\"당신 신앙 따위가 나와 무슨 상관이란 말이오?\""], ["Miüsov was on the point of shouting, but he suddenly checked himself, and said with contempt, “You defile everything you touch.”", "미우소프는 막 소리를 지르려다가 별안간 자제하고는, 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"당신은 손대는 것마다 죄다 더럽혀 놓는군.\""]], [["The elder suddenly rose from his seat.", "장로가 별안간 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["“Excuse me, gentlemen, for leaving you a few minutes,”", "\"여러분, 몇 분간 자리를 비우는 것을 양해해 주십시오.\""], ["he said, addressing all his guests.", "그가 모든 손님을 향해 말했다."], ["“I have visitors awaiting me who arrived before you.", "\"여러분보다 먼저 도착해 기다리고 있는 방문객들이 있습니다."], ["But don’t you tell lies all the same,”", "그래도 당신은 거짓말을 하지 마십시오.\""], ["he added, turning to Fyodor Pavlovitch with a good-humored face.", "그가 너그러운 낯빛으로 표도르 파블로비치를 돌아보며 덧붙였다."], ["He went out of the cell.", "그는 암자를 나섰다."], ["Alyosha and the novice flew to escort him down the steps.", "알료샤와 수련 수사가 그를 모시고 계단을 내려가려 날듯이 따라나섰다."], ["Alyosha was breathless: he was glad to get away, but he was glad, too, that the elder was good-humored and not offended.", "알료샤는 숨이 가빴으니, 그 자리에서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤고, 또 장로가 너그러운 기분으로 노여워하지 않은 것도 기뻤다."], ["Father Zossima was going towards the portico to bless the people waiting for him there.", "조시마 장로는 거기서 자기를 기다리는 사람들을 축복하려고 현관 쪽으로 가고 있었다."], ["But Fyodor Pavlovitch persisted in stopping him at the door of the cell.", "그러나 표도르 파블로비치는 기어이 암자 문 앞에서 그를 붙들어 세웠다."]], [["“Blessed man!”", "\"복되신 분이여!\""], ["he cried, with feeling.", "그가 감격에 차서 외쳤다."], ["“Allow me to kiss your hand once more.", "\"한 번만 더 손에 입 맞추게 해 주십시오."], ["Yes, with you I could still talk, I could still get on.", "그렇습니다, 당신과는 아직도 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠습니다, 아직도 잘 지낼 수 있겠어요."], ["Do you think I always lie and play the fool like this?", "제가 늘 이렇게 거짓말이나 하고 광대 짓이나 하는 줄 아십니까?"], ["Believe me, I have been acting like this all the time on purpose to try you.", "믿어 주십시오, 줄곧 일부러 이렇게 굴어 본 건 당신을 시험해 보려는 거였습니다."], ["I have been testing you all the time to see whether I could get on with you.", "당신과 잘 지낼 수 있을지 알아보려고 줄곧 떠본 거지요."], ["Is there room for my humility beside your pride?", "당신의 그 자긍심 곁에 제 겸손이 들어설 자리가 있을까요?"], ["I am ready to give you a testimonial that one can get on with you!", "당신은 더불어 지낼 만한 분이라고 기꺼이 보증서라도 써 드리지요! 한데 이제는 잠자코 있겠습니다."], ["But now, I’ll be quiet; I will keep quiet all the time.", "줄곧 입을 다물고 있겠어요. 의자에 앉아 혀를 묶어 두지요."], ["I’ll sit in a chair and hold my tongue.", "이제 말씀하실 차례는 당신이올시다, 표트르 알렉산드로비치."], ["Now it is for you to speak, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "이제 여기 남은 주인공은 당신이니까요—앞으로 십 분 동안 말입니다.\""], ["You are the principal person left now—for ten minutes.”", "* 이스프라브니크(исправник): 제정 러시아의 군(郡) 경찰서장."]], [["# Chapter III. Peasant Women Who Have Faith", "# Chapter III. Peasant Women Who Have Faith"]], [["Near the wooden portico below, built on to the outer wall of the precinct, there was a crowd of about twenty peasant women.", "아래쪽, 경내의 바깥담에 잇대어 세운 나무 현관 곁에는 농사꾼 아낙네가 스무 명쯤 무리지어 모여 있었다."], ["They had been told that the elder was at last coming out, and they had gathered together in anticipation.", "장로가 마침내 나오신다는 말을 전해 듣고 다들 그 기대 속에 한데 모여든 것이었다."], ["Two ladies, Madame Hohlakov and her daughter, had also come out into the portico to wait for the elder, but in a separate part of it set aside for women of rank.", "호흘라코바 부인과 그 딸, 두 귀부인도 장로를 기다리려고 현관으로 나와 있었으나, 신분 있는 여인들을 위해 따로 마련된 자리에 떨어져 있었다."]], [["Madame Hohlakov was a wealthy lady, still young and attractive, and always dressed with taste.", "호흘라코바 부인은 부유한 부인으로, 아직 젊고 매력이 있었으며, 늘 취향 있게 차려입었다."], ["She was rather pale, and had lively black eyes.", "얼굴빛은 다소 창백했고, 검고 생기 넘치는 눈을 지녔다."], ["She was not more than thirty-three, and had been five years a widow.", "나이는 서른셋이 넘지 않았고, 과부가 된 지는 다섯 해가 되었다."], ["Her daughter, a girl of fourteen, was partially paralyzed.", "그 딸은 열네 살 난 소녀로, 몸 한쪽이 마비되어 있었다."], ["The poor child had not been able to walk for the last six months, and was wheeled about in a long reclining chair.", "가엾은 아이는 지난 반년 동안 걷지 못해, 길게 누울 수 있는 의자에 실려 이리저리 옮겨 다녔다."], ["She had a charming little face, rather thin from illness, but full of gayety.", "병으로 다소 야위기는 했으나 그 얼굴은 사랑스럽고 명랑함이 가득했다."], ["There was a gleam of mischief in her big dark eyes with their long lashes.", "긴 속눈썹이 드리운 크고 검은 눈에는 장난기가 어른거렸다."], ["Her mother had been intending to take her abroad ever since the spring, but they had been detained all the summer by business connected with their estate.", "어머니는 봄부터 줄곧 딸을 데리고 외국으로 가려고 마음먹고 있었으나, 영지와 관련된 일 때문에 여름 내내 발이 묶여 있었다."], ["They had been staying a week in our town, where they had come more for purposes of business than devotion, but had visited Father Zossima once already, three days before.", "두 사람은 우리 고을에 일주일째 머무는 중이었는데, 신앙보다는 사무를 보러 온 것이었으나 사흘 전에 이미 한 차례 조시마 신부를 찾아뵌 바 있었다."], ["Though they knew that the elder scarcely saw any one, they had now suddenly turned up again, and urgently entreated “the happiness of looking once again on the great healer.”", "장로가 거의 아무도 만나지 않는다는 것을 알면서도, 두 사람은 이제 다시 불쑥 나타나 \"그 위대한 치유자를 다시 한번 뵙는 기쁨\"을 간절히 청했던 것이다."]], [["The mother was sitting on a chair by the side of her daughter’s invalid carriage, and two paces from her stood an old monk, not one of our monastery, but a visitor from an obscure religious house in the far north.", "어머니는 딸의 병자용 수레 곁에 놓인 의자에 앉아 있었고, 그녀로부터 두어 걸음 떨어진 곳에 한 늙은 수도사가 서 있었는데, 우리 수도원의 사람이 아니라 머나먼 북방의 어느 이름 없는 수도원에서 온 객승이었다."], ["He too sought the elder’s blessing.", "그 역시 장로의 축복을 구하러 온 것이었다."]], [["But Father Zossima, on entering the portico, went first straight to the peasants who were crowded at the foot of the three steps that led up into the portico.", "그러나 조시마 신부는 현관에 들어서자 먼저 곧장, 현관으로 오르는 세 계단 발치에 빽빽이 모여든 농사꾼들에게로 다가갔다."], ["Father Zossima stood on the top step, put on his stole, and began blessing the women who thronged about him.", "조시마 신부는 맨 윗계단에 서서 영대를 두르고는, 자기 둘레로 몰려든 아낙네들을 축복하기 시작했다."], ["One crazy woman was led up to him.", "미친 여자 하나가 그의 앞으로 인도되어 왔다."], ["As soon as she caught sight of the elder she began shrieking and writhing as though in the pains of childbirth.", "장로를 보자마자 여자는 마치 산고에 시달리기라도 하는 듯 비명을 지르며 몸을 비틀어 댔다."], ["Laying the stole on her forehead, he read a short prayer over her, and she was at once soothed and quieted.", "그가 영대를 그 이마에 얹고 짧은 기도를 읽어 주자, 여자는 이내 가라앉아 잠잠해졌다."]], [["I do not know how it may be now, but in my childhood I often happened to see and hear these “possessed”", "지금은 어떤지 모르겠으나, 내 어린 시절에는 시골 마을과 수도원에서 이런 \"들린\""], ["women in the villages and monasteries.", "여자들을 보고 듣는 일이 흔했다."], ["They used to be brought to mass; they would squeal and bark like a dog so that they were heard all over the church.", "그들은 미사에 끌려 나오곤 했는데, 교회 안이 떠나가도록 개처럼 깩깩거리고 짖어 댔다."], ["But when the sacrament was carried in and they were led up to it, at once the “possession” ceased, and the sick women were always soothed for a time.", "그러나 성체가 옮겨져 들어와 그들이 그 앞으로 인도되면, 그 \"들림\"은 즉시 멎었고, 병든 여자들은 언제나 한동안 가라앉았다."], ["I was greatly impressed and amazed at this as a child; but then I heard from country neighbors and from my town teachers that the whole illness was simulated to avoid work, and that it could always be cured by suitable severity; various anecdotes were told to confirm this.", "어린 나는 이 광경에 크게 놀라고 감명을 받았다. 그러나 그 뒤로 시골 이웃들과 고을의 선생들에게서, 그 병은 모두 일을 피하려고 꾸며 낸 것이며 적당히 호되게 다루면 언제든 고칠 수 있다는 말을 들었고, 이를 뒷받침하는 갖가지 일화도 들려주었다."], ["But later on I learnt with astonishment from medical specialists that there is no pretense about it, that it is a terrible illness to which women are subject, specially prevalent among us in Russia, and that it is due to the hard lot of the peasant women.", "그러나 훗날 나는 의학 전문가들에게서 놀랍게도 거기엔 거짓이라곤 없으며, 그것은 여자들이 걸리는 무서운 병으로 특히 우리 러시아에서 유난히 흔하며, 농사꾼 아낙네들의 고달픈 처지에서 비롯된다는 것을 배웠다."], ["It is a disease, I was told, arising from exhausting toil too soon after hard, abnormal and unassisted labor in childbirth, and from the hopeless misery, from beatings, and so on, which some women were not able to endure like others.", "그것은 산고라는, 힘겹고 비정상적이며 아무 도움도 받지 못한 출산을 치른 뒤 너무 일찍 기진맥진하도록 노역에 시달린 데서, 또 가망 없는 비참함에서, 매질 따위에서 생겨나는 병으로, 어떤 여자들은 다른 이들처럼 그것을 견뎌 내지 못한다는 것이었다."], ["The strange and instant healing of the frantic and struggling woman as soon as she was led up to the holy sacrament, which had been explained to me as due to malingering and the trickery of the “clericals,” arose probably in the most natural manner.", "발광하며 몸부림치던 여자가 거룩한 성체 앞으로 인도되자마자 기이하게도 단번에 낫는 그 현상을, 나는 꾀병이며 \"성직자들\"의 속임수 탓이라고 들어 왔으나, 실은 십중팔구 지극히 자연스러운 방식으로 일어난 것이었다."], ["Both the women who supported her and the invalid herself fully believed as a truth beyond question that the evil spirit in possession of her could not hold out if the sick woman were brought to the sacrament and made to bow down before it.", "그녀를 부축한 여자들이나 병든 여자 자신이나, 병든 여자를 성체 앞으로 데려가 그 앞에 엎드리게 하면 그녀에게 들린 악령이 버티지 못한다는 것을 의심할 여지 없는 진리로 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다."], ["And so, with a nervous and psychically deranged woman, a sort of convulsion of the whole organism always took place, and was bound to take place, at the moment of bowing down to the sacrament, aroused by the expectation of the miracle of healing and the implicit belief that it would come to pass; and it did come to pass, though only for a moment.", "그리하여 신경이 곤두서고 정신이 어지러운 여자에게는, 성체 앞에 엎드리는 순간 온몸에 일종의 경련이 언제나 일어났고 또 일어날 수밖에 없었으니, 그것은 치유의 기적에 대한 기대와 그것이 반드시 이루어지리라는 무조건적인 믿음에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그리고 그것은 실제로 이루어졌다, 비록 한순간뿐일지언정."], ["It was exactly the same now as soon as the elder touched the sick woman with the stole.", "지금도 장로가 영대로 병든 여자를 건드리자마자 똑같은 일이 벌어진 것이었다."]], [["Many of the women in the crowd were moved to tears of ecstasy by the effect of the moment: some strove to kiss the hem of his garment, others cried out in sing-song voices.", "무리 가운데 많은 여자들이 그 순간의 효험에 황홀한 눈물을 쏟았다. 어떤 이는 그의 옷자락에 입을 맞추려 애썼고, 어떤 이는 가락을 넣어 울부짖었다."]], [["He blessed them all and talked with some of them.", "그는 그들 모두를 축복하고 몇몇과는 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The “possessed”", "그 \"들린\""], ["woman he knew already.", "여자는 이미 그가 아는 사람이었다."], ["She came from a village only six versts from the monastery, and had been brought to him before.", "수도원에서 겨우 육 베르스타 떨어진 마을에서 온 여자로, 전에도 그에게 인도되어 온 적이 있었다."]], [["“But here is one from afar.”", "\"그러나 여기 멀리서 온 사람이 있구나.\""], ["He pointed to a woman by no means old but very thin and wasted, with a face not merely sunburnt but almost blackened by exposure.", "그가 결코 늙지는 않았으나 몹시 야위고 수척한 한 여자를 가리켰는데, 그녀의 얼굴은 햇볕에 그을린 정도가 아니라 풍상에 거의 새카맣게 탄 빛이었다."], ["She was kneeling and gazing with a fixed stare at the elder; there was something almost frenzied in her eyes.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 장로를 뚫어지게 응시하고 있었으니, 그 눈에는 거의 광기 어린 무언가가 서려 있었다."]], [["“From afar off, Father, from afar off!", "\"멀리서, 신부님, 멀리서요!"], ["From two hundred miles from here.", "여기서 이백 베르스타나 되는 데서요."], ["From afar off, Father, from afar off!”", "멀리서, 신부님, 멀리서 왔어요!\""], ["the woman began in a sing-song voice as though she were chanting a dirge, swaying her head from side to side with her cheek resting in her hand.", "여자는 마치 만가라도 읊조리듯 가락을 넣어 운을 떼며, 한쪽 뺨을 손에 괸 채 머리를 좌우로 흔들었다."]], [["There is silent and long-suffering sorrow to be met with among the peasantry.", "농사꾼들 사이에서는 말없이 오래 견디는 슬픔을 마주칠 수 있다."], ["It withdraws into itself and is still.", "그것은 제 안으로 움츠러들어 잠잠하다."], ["But there is a grief that breaks out, and from that minute it bursts into tears and finds vent in wailing.", "그러나 터져 나오는 비탄도 있어, 그 순간부터 울음이 복받쳐 통곡으로 발산된다."], ["This is particularly common with women.", "이는 특히 여자들에게 흔한 일이다."], ["But it is no lighter a grief than the silent.", "그러나 그것이 말없는 슬픔보다 가벼운 것은 결코 아니다."], ["Lamentations comfort only by lacerating the heart still more.", "통곡은 마음을 더욱 갈가리 찢음으로써만 위안을 준다."], ["Such grief does not desire consolation.", "그런 슬픔은 위안을 바라지 않는다."], ["It feeds on the sense of its hopelessness.", "그것은 제 가망 없음을 양식으로 삼아 자란다."], ["Lamentations spring only from the constant craving to reopen the wound.", "통곡은 오로지 그 상처를 다시 헤집고픈 끊임없는 갈망에서 솟아나는 것이다."]], [["“You are of the tradesman class?”", "\"그대는 상인 계층 사람인가?\""], ["said Father Zossima, looking curiously at her.", "조시마 신부가 그녀를 신기한 듯 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Townfolk we are, Father, townfolk.", "\"읍내 사람입니다, 신부님, 읍내 사람이지요."], ["Yet we are peasants though we live in the town.", "그래도 읍에 살지만 농사꾼이랍니다."], ["I have come to see you, O Father!", "저는 신부님을 뵈러 왔어요, 오, 신부님!"], ["We heard of you, Father, we heard of you.", "신부님에 대한 소문을 들었어요, 신부님, 들었지요."], ["I have buried my little son, and I have come on a pilgrimage.", "어린 자식을 묻고서 순례 길에 나섰답니다."], ["I have been in three monasteries, but they told me, ‘Go, Nastasya, go to them’—that is to you.", "수도원을 세 군데나 다녀 봤는데, 그분들이 그러시더군요, '가라, 나스타시야, 그분들께로 가라'고요—바로 신부님께로요."], ["I have come; I was yesterday at the service, and to-day I have come to you.”", "그래서 왔지요. 어제는 예배에 참석했고, 오늘은 신부님을 뵈러 왔습니다.\""]], [["“What are you weeping for?”", "\"무엇 때문에 울고 있느냐?\""]], [["“It’s my little son I’m grieving for, Father.", "\"어린 자식 때문에 슬퍼하는 거예요, 신부님."], ["He was three years old—three years all but three months.", "세 살이었답니다—석 달만 모자란 세 살이었지요."], ["For my little boy, Father, I’m in anguish, for my little boy.", "제 어린 사내아이 때문에, 신부님, 가슴이 미어집니다, 제 어린 사내아이 때문에요."], ["He was the last one left.", "마지막 남은 아이였어요."], ["We had four, my Nikita and I, and now we’ve no children, our dear ones have all gone.", "저와 제 남편 니키타 사이에 넷이 있었는데, 이제 자식이라곤 하나도 없답니다, 우리 귀한 것들이 모두 가 버렸어요."], ["I buried the first three without grieving overmuch, and now I have buried the last I can’t forget him.", "처음 셋은 그리 깊이 슬퍼하지 않고 묻었는데, 이제 마지막 아이를 묻고 나니 그 애를 잊을 수가 없어요."], ["He seems always standing before me.", "늘 제 앞에 서 있는 것만 같아요."], ["He never leaves me.", "한시도 제게서 떠나질 않아요."], ["He has withered my heart.", "그 애가 제 가슴을 다 말려 버렸어요."], ["I look at his little clothes, his little shirt, his little boots, and I wail.", "그 애의 자그마한 옷가지를, 자그마한 셔츠를, 자그마한 신발을 들여다보면 통곡이 나옵니다."], ["I lay out all that is left of him, all his little things.", "그 애가 남긴 것을, 그 애의 자그마한 물건들을 죄다 펼쳐 놓아요."], ["I look at them and wail.", "그것들을 들여다보면 통곡이 나오지요."], ["I say to Nikita, my husband, ‘Let me go on a pilgrimage, master.’ He is a driver.", "제 남편 니키타한테 말했어요, '여보, 순례 길을 떠나게 해 줘요'라고요. 그이는 마부랍니다."], ["We’re not poor people, Father, not poor; he drives our own horse.", "저희는 가난한 사람들이 아니에요, 신부님, 가난하지 않아요."], ["It’s all our own, the horse and the carriage.", "그이는 우리 말을 부린답니다. 다 우리 것이에요, 말도 마차도요."], ["And what good is it all to us now?", "그런데 이제 와서 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요?"], ["My Nikita has begun drinking while I am away.", "제가 없는 동안 우리 니키타는 술을 마시기 시작했답니다."], ["He’s sure to.", "틀림없이 그럴 거예요."], ["It used to be so before.", "전에도 그랬으니까요."], ["As soon as I turn my back he gives way to it.", "제가 등을 돌리기만 하면 그이는 술에 빠지지요."], ["But now I don’t think about him.", "하지만 이제는 그이 생각을 안 해요."], ["It’s three months since I left home.", "집을 떠난 지 석 달이 됐어요."], ["I’ve forgotten him.", "그이를 잊었답니다."], ["I’ve forgotten everything.", "모든 걸 잊었어요."], ["I don’t want to remember.", "기억하고 싶지 않아요."], ["And what would our life be now together?", "이제 와서 우리가 함께 산들 그게 무슨 삶이겠어요?"], ["I’ve done with him, I’ve done.", "그이와는 끝났어요, 끝났지요."], ["I’ve done with them all.", "다 끝났어요."], ["I don’t care to look upon my house and my goods.", "우리 집도 우리 살림도 보고 싶지 않아요."], ["I don’t care to see anything at all!”", "아무것도 보고 싶지 않아요!\""]], [["“Listen, mother,”", "\"들어 보거라, 어미여.\""], ["said the elder.", "장로가 말했다."], ["“Once in olden times a holy saint saw in the Temple a mother like you weeping for her little one, her only one, whom God had taken.", "\"옛적 한 거룩한 성인이 성전에서, 그대처럼 하느님이 데려가신 자기 어린것, 하나뿐인 자식을 위해 우는 어미를 보았느니라."], ["‘Knowest thou not,’ said the saint to her, ‘how bold these little ones are before the throne of God?", "성인이 그 어미에게 말했지. '그대는 모르는가, 이 어린것들이 하느님의 보좌 앞에서 얼마나 담대한지를?"], ["Verily there are none bolder than they in the Kingdom of Heaven.", "진실로 천국에서 그들보다 더 담대한 자는 없느니라."], ["“Thou didst give us life, O Lord,” they say, “and scarcely had we looked upon it when Thou didst take it back again.”", "그들은 말한다네, \"주여, 당신께서 우리에게 생명을 주셨으나, 우리가 미처 그것을 바라보기도 전에 도로 거두어 가셨나이다\"라고."], ["And so boldly they ask and ask again that God gives them at once the rank of angels.", "그리하여 그들은 어찌나 담대하게 묻고 또 묻는지, 하느님은 그들에게 곧바로 천사의 지위를 내려 주시느니라. 그러니'"], ["Therefore,’ said the saint, ‘thou, too, O mother, rejoice and weep not, for thy little son is with the Lord in the fellowship of the angels.’ That’s what the saint said to the weeping mother of old.", "성인이 말했지, '그대 또한, 오 어미여, 기뻐하고 울지 말라, 그대의 어린 자식은 천사들의 무리 가운데서 주님과 함께 있느니라.' 옛적에 그 성인이 우는 어미에게 한 말이 이러하였느니라."], ["He was a great saint and he could not have spoken falsely.", "그는 위대한 성인이었으니 거짓을 말했을 리 없다."], ["Therefore you too, mother, know that your little one is surely before the throne of God, is rejoicing and happy, and praying to God for you, and therefore weep not, but rejoice.”", "그러니 그대 또한, 어미여, 그대의 어린것이 분명히 하느님의 보좌 앞에서 기뻐하며 행복하게 그대를 위해 하느님께 기도하고 있음을 알지니, 그러므로 울지 말고 기뻐하라.\""]], [["The woman listened to him, looking down with her cheek in her hand.", "여자는 한쪽 뺨을 손에 괸 채 눈을 내리뜨고 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["She sighed deeply.", "그녀는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“My Nikita tried to comfort me with the same words as you.", "\"우리 니키타도 신부님과 똑같은 말로 저를 달래려 했어요. '어리석은 사람아,'"], ["‘Foolish one,’ he said, ‘why weep?", "그이가 그러더군요. '왜 우는 거야?"], ["Our son is no doubt singing with the angels before God.’ He says that to me, but he weeps himself.", "우리 아들은 틀림없이 하느님 앞에서 천사들과 함께 노래하고 있을 거야.' 그이가 제게 그렇게 말하면서도 정작 자기도 우는 거예요."], ["I see that he cries like me.", "그이도 저처럼 우는 게 보였어요. '알아요, 니키타,'"], ["‘I know, Nikita,’ said I.", "제가 그랬지요. '하느님 곁이 아니면 그 애가 어디 있겠어요?"], ["‘Where could he be if not with the Lord God?", "다만 지금 여기 우리 곁에, 전처럼 우리 옆에 앉아 있지 못할 뿐이지요.' 단 한 번만이라도 그 애를 바라볼 수 있다면, 단 한 번만이라도 그 애를 살짝 엿볼 수 있다면, 다가가지 않고, 말도 걸지 않고, 구석에 숨어서 단 일 분만이라도 그 애를 볼 수 있다면, 그 애가 마당에서 노는 소리를, 그 자그마한 목소리로 '엄마, 어디 있어?'"], ["Only, here with us now he is not as he used to sit beside us before.’ And if only I could look upon him one little time, if only I could peep at him one little time, without going up to him, without speaking, if I could be hidden in a corner and only see him for one little minute, hear him playing in the yard, calling in his little voice, ‘Mammy, where are you?’ If only I could hear him pattering with his little feet about the room just once, only once; for so often, so often I remember how he used to run to me and shout and laugh, if only I could hear his little feet I should know him!", "하고 부르는 소리를 들을 수 있다면. 그 애가 자그마한 발로 방 안을 종종거리며 돌아다니는 소리를 단 한 번만, 딱 한 번만 들을 수 있다면."], ["But he’s gone, Father, he’s gone, and I shall never hear him again.", "그 애가 어찌나 자주, 어찌나 자주 제게 달려와 소리치며 웃어 댔는지 떠오르거든요. 그 자그마한 발소리만 들을 수 있다면 그게 그 앤 줄 알 텐데요!"], ["Here’s his little sash, but him I shall never see or hear now.”", "하지만 그 애는 가 버렸어요, 신부님, 가 버렸어요, 다시는 그 애 소리를 듣지 못할 거예요. 여기 그 애의 자그마한 띠가 있는데, 그 애만은 이제 다시는 보지도 듣지도 못할 거예요.\""]], [["She drew out of her bosom her boy’s little embroidered sash, and as soon as she looked at it she began shaking with sobs, hiding her eyes with her fingers through which the tears flowed in a sudden stream.", "그녀는 품에서 사내아이의 자그마한 수놓은 띠를 꺼냈는데, 그것을 보자마자 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨기 시작하더니 손가락으로 눈을 가렸고, 그 손가락 사이로 눈물이 갑작스레 줄줄 흘러내렸다."]], [["“It is Rachel of old,”", "\"옛적의 라헬이로구나.\""], ["said the elder, “weeping for her children, and will not be comforted because they are not.", "장로가 말했다. \"자식들 때문에 울며, 그들이 없으므로 위로받기를 마다하는도다."], ["Such is the lot set on earth for you mothers.", "그대들 어미에게 이 땅 위에 정해진 몫이 이러하니라."], ["Be not comforted.", "위로받으려 하지 말라."], ["Consolation is not what you need.", "위안은 그대에게 필요한 것이 아니다."], ["Weep and be not consoled, but weep.", "울되 위로받지 말고, 다만 울라."], ["Only every time that you weep be sure to remember that your little son is one of the angels of God, that he looks down from there at you and sees you, and rejoices at your tears, and points at them to the Lord God; and a long while yet will you keep that great mother’s grief.", "다만 울 때마다 그대의 어린 자식이 하느님의 천사들 가운데 하나임을, 그가 저 위에서 그대를 내려다보며 그대를 지켜보고, 그대의 눈물을 보고 기뻐하며 그것을 주 하느님께 가리켜 보이고 있음을 반드시 기억하라. 그리고 그대는 아직 오랫동안 그 큰 어미의 슬픔을 간직하리라."], ["But it will turn in the end into quiet joy, and your bitter tears will be only tears of tender sorrow that purifies the heart and delivers it from sin.", "그러나 그것은 끝내 고요한 기쁨으로 바뀔 것이며, 그대의 쓰라린 눈물은 다만 마음을 정화하여 죄에서 건져 주는 애틋한 슬픔의 눈물이 되리라."], ["And I shall pray for the peace of your child’s soul.", "나도 그대 아이의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도하겠노라."], ["What was his name?”", "그 애의 이름이 무엇이었느냐?\""]], [["“Alexey, Father.”", "\"알렉세이였어요, 신부님.\""]], [["“A sweet name.", "\"고운 이름이로구나."], ["After Alexey, the man of God?”", "하느님의 사람 알렉세이를 따른 것이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Father.”", "\"네, 신부님.\""]], [["“What a saint he was!", "\"그분은 얼마나 거룩한 성인이셨던가!"], ["I will remember him, mother, and your grief in my prayers, and I will pray for your husband’s health.", "기도 가운데 그 애를, 그리고 그대의 슬픔을 기억하겠노라, 어미여. 또한 그대 남편의 건강을 위해 기도하겠다."], ["It is a sin for you to leave him.", "그를 버리고 떠나는 것은 그대에게 죄가 된다."], ["Your little one will see from heaven that you have forsaken his father, and will weep over you.", "그대의 어린것이 하늘에서 그대가 제 아비를 저버린 것을 보고 그대를 위해 울 것이다."], ["Why do you trouble his happiness?", "어찌하여 그 애의 행복을 어지럽히려 하느냐?"], ["He is living, for the soul lives for ever, and though he is not in the house he is near you, unseen.", "그 애는 살아 있으니, 영혼은 영원히 사는 까닭이라. 비록 그가 집 안에 있지 않을지라도 보이지 않게 그대 곁에 있느니라."], ["How can he go into the house when you say that the house is hateful to you?", "그대가 그 집이 밉다고 말하는데, 어찌 그 애가 집으로 들어가겠느냐?"], ["To whom is he to go if he find you not together, his father and mother?", "제 아비와 어미가 함께 있지 않은 것을 보면, 그 애가 누구에게로 가겠느냐?"], ["He comes to you in dreams now, and you grieve.", "지금은 그 애가 그대의 꿈에 찾아오니 그대가 슬퍼하는 것이라."], ["But then he will send you gentle dreams.", "그러나 그때가 되면 그 애가 그대에게 부드러운 꿈을 보내 주리라."], ["Go to your husband, mother; go this very day.”", "남편에게로 가거라, 어미여. 바로 오늘 가거라.\""]], [["“I will go, Father, at your word.", "\"가겠어요, 신부님, 신부님 말씀대로요."], ["I will go.", "가겠어요."], ["You’ve gone straight to my heart.", "신부님 말씀이 곧장 제 가슴에 사무쳤어요."], ["My Nikita, my Nikita, you are waiting for me,”", "우리 니키타, 우리 니키타, 당신이 나를 기다리고 있군요.\""], ["the woman began in a sing-song voice; but the elder had already turned away to a very old woman, dressed like a dweller in the town, not like a pilgrim.", "여자가 가락을 넣어 읊조리기 시작했으나, 장로는 이미 몸을 돌려 아주 늙은 한 여인에게로 향했는데, 그 여인은 순례자가 아니라 읍내에 사는 사람처럼 차려입고 있었다."], ["Her eyes showed that she had come with an object, and in order to say something.", "그 눈을 보니 무슨 목적이 있어, 또 무언가 할 말이 있어 온 것임을 알 수 있었다."], ["She said she was the widow of a non-commissioned officer, and lived close by in the town.", "그녀는 자기가 하사관의 과부이며 이 읍내 가까이에 산다고 말했다."], ["Her son Vasenka was in the commissariat service, and had gone to Irkutsk in Siberia.", "아들 바센카는 군수품 부대에서 복무하다가 시베리아의 이르쿠츠크로 갔다고 했다."], ["He had written twice from there, but now a year had passed since he had written.", "거기서 두 번 편지를 보내왔으나, 이제 마지막 편지를 보낸 지 한 해가 지났다는 것이었다."], ["She did inquire about him, but she did not know the proper place to inquire.", "그녀는 아들의 소식을 알아보려 했으나, 어디다 알아봐야 마땅한지를 알지 못했다."]], [["“Only the other day Stepanida Ilyinishna—she’s a rich merchant’s wife—said to me, ‘You go, Prohorovna, and put your son’s name down for prayer in the church, and pray for the peace of his soul as though he were dead.", "\"엊그제 스테파니다 일리이니시나가—그이는 부유한 상인의 아내랍니다—제게 그러더군요. '프로호로브나, 가서 교회에 아들 이름을 적어 기도를 올리고, 마치 그 애가 죽은 사람인 양 그 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도하시구려."], ["His soul will be troubled,’ she said, ‘and he will write you a letter.’ And Stepanida Ilyinishna told me it was a certain thing which had been many times tried.", "그러면 그 애 영혼이 심란해져서,' 그러는 거예요."], ["Only I am in doubt....", "'당신한테 편지를 보낼 거요'라고요."], ["Oh, you light of ours!", "그리고 스테파니다 일리이니시나가 그러는데, 그건 여러 번 시험해 본 확실한 방법이라더군요. 다만 저는 미심쩍어요……"], ["is it true or false, and would it be right?”", "오, 우리의 빛이신 분이여! 그게 참말인가요 거짓인가요, 또 그래도 괜찮은 일일까요?\""]], [["“Don’t think of it.", "\"그런 생각일랑 말거라."], ["It’s shameful to ask the question.", "그런 것을 묻다니 부끄러운 일이로다."], ["How is it possible to pray for the peace of a living soul?", "어찌 산 사람의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도할 수 있단 말이냐?"], ["And his own mother too!", "더구나 친어미가 말이다!"], ["It’s a great sin, akin to sorcery.", "그것은 요술과 다름없는 큰 죄로다."], ["Only for your ignorance it is forgiven you.", "다만 그대가 무지한 까닭에 용서받는 것이니라."], ["Better pray to the Queen of Heaven, our swift defense and help, for his good health, and that she may forgive you for your error.", "차라리 우리의 날랜 방벽이요 도움이신 하늘의 여왕께 그 애의 건강을 위해, 또 그분이 그대의 잘못을 용서해 주시기를 기도하라."], ["And another thing I will tell you, Prohorovna.", "그리고 한 가지 더 일러 두겠다, 프로호로브나."], ["Either he will soon come back to you, your son, or he will be sure to send a letter.", "그대의 아들은 머잖아 그대에게 돌아오거나, 아니면 틀림없이 편지를 보내올 것이다."], ["Go, and henceforward be in peace.", "가거라, 그리고 이제부터 마음을 편히 가지거라."], ["Your son is alive, I tell you.”", "그대의 아들은 살아 있느니라, 내 그대에게 이르노라.\""]], [["“Dear Father, God reward you, our benefactor, who prays for all of us and for our sins!”", "\"고마우신 신부님, 하느님께서 갚아 주시기를, 우리 모두를 위해, 우리의 죄를 위해 기도해 주시는 우리의 은인이시여!\""]], [["But the elder had already noticed in the crowd two glowing eyes fixed upon him.", "그러나 장로는 이미 무리 가운데서 자기를 향해 못박힌 듯 타오르는 두 눈을 알아차렸다."], ["An exhausted, consumptive-looking, though young peasant woman was gazing at him in silence.", "기진맥진하고 폐병이라도 든 듯한, 그러나 아직 젊은 농사꾼 아낙네 하나가 말없이 그를 응시하고 있었다."], ["Her eyes besought him, but she seemed afraid to approach.", "그 눈은 그에게 애원하고 있었으나, 여자는 다가오기를 두려워하는 듯했다."]], [["“What is it, my child?”", "\"무슨 일이냐, 내 딸아?\""]], [["“Absolve my soul, Father,”", "\"제 영혼을 사하여 주세요, 신부님.\""], ["she articulated softly, and slowly sank on her knees and bowed down at his feet.", "여자가 나직이 또박또박 말하고는, 천천히 무릎을 꿇고 그의 발치에 엎드렸다."], ["“I have sinned, Father.", "\"저는 죄를 지었어요, 신부님."], ["I am afraid of my sin.”", "제 죄가 두렵습니다.\""]], [["The elder sat down on the lower step.", "장로는 아래쪽 계단에 앉았다."], ["The woman crept closer to him, still on her knees.", "여자는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 그에게로 좀 더 바싹 기어왔다."]], [["“I am a widow these three years,”", "\"과부가 된 지 세 해째예요.\""], ["she began in a half-whisper, with a sort of shudder.", "여자가 일종의 전율과 함께 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 운을 뗐다."], ["“I had a hard life with my husband.", "\"남편과 사는 동안 고된 삶을 살았어요."], ["He was an old man.", "늙은이였답니다."], ["He used to beat me cruelly.", "저를 모질게 때리곤 했지요."], ["He lay ill; I thought looking at him, if he were to get well, if he were to get up again, what then?", "그이가 앓아누웠어요. 그이를 들여다보면서 생각했지요, 만약 저이가 나아 버리면, 만약 저이가 다시 일어나면, 그러면 어쩌나?"], ["And then the thought came to me—”", "그러다 문득 이런 생각이 들었어요—\""]], [["“Stay!”", "\"멈추거라!\""], ["said the elder, and he put his ear close to her lips.", "장로가 말하며, 그녀의 입술에 귀를 바싹 갖다 댔다."]], [["The woman went on in a low whisper, so that it was almost impossible to catch anything.", "여자는 나직이 속삭이며 말을 이었는데, 무슨 말인지 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["She had soon done.", "이내 말을 마쳤다."]], [["“Three years ago?”", "\"세 해 전이라고?\""], ["asked the elder.", "장로가 물었다."]], [["“Three years.", "\"세 해예요."], ["At first I didn’t think about it, but now I’ve begun to be ill, and the thought never leaves me.”", "처음엔 그 일을 생각하지 않았는데, 이제 몸이 아프기 시작하면서 그 생각이 한시도 떠나질 않아요.\""]], [["“Have you come from far?”", "\"먼 데서 왔느냐?\""]], [["“Over three hundred miles away.”", "\"삼백 베르스타도 넘는 데서요.\""]], [["“Have you told it in confession?”", "\"고해성사로 그것을 고백했느냐?\""]], [["“I have confessed it.", "\"고백했어요."], ["Twice I have confessed it.”", "두 번이나 고백했지요.\""]], [["“Have you been admitted to Communion?”", "\"영성체는 받았느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["I am afraid.", "두려워요."], ["I am afraid to die.”", "죽는 게 두려워요.\""]], [["“Fear nothing and never be afraid; and don’t fret.", "\"아무것도 두려워하지 말고 결코 겁내지 말라. 그리고 애태우지 말라."], ["If only your penitence fail not, God will forgive all.", "그대의 뉘우침이 거짓되지만 않다면, 하느님께서 모든 것을 용서하시리라."], ["There is no sin, and there can be no sin on all the earth, which the Lord will not forgive to the truly repentant!", "온 땅에 진실로 뉘우치는 자에게 주께서 용서하지 않으실 죄란 없으며, 있을 수도 없느니라!"], ["Man cannot commit a sin so great as to exhaust the infinite love of God.", "사람은 하느님의 무한한 사랑을 다 길어 낼 만큼 큰 죄를 지을 수 없느니라."], ["Can there be a sin which could exceed the love of God?", "하느님의 사랑을 넘어설 죄가 어디 있겠느냐?"], ["Think only of repentance, continual repentance, but dismiss fear altogether.", "오로지 뉘우침만을, 끊임없는 뉘우침만을 생각하고, 두려움일랑 송두리째 떨쳐 버려라."], ["Believe that God loves you as you cannot conceive; that He loves you with your sin, in your sin.", "하느님께서 그대가 헤아릴 수도 없을 만큼 그대를 사랑하심을, 그대의 죄째로, 그대의 죄 속에 있는 그대를 사랑하심을 믿으라."], ["It has been said of old that over one repentant sinner there is more joy in heaven than over ten righteous men.", "옛적에 이르기를, 의인 열 명보다 회개하는 죄인 하나를 두고 하늘에서 더 큰 기쁨이 있다 하였느니라."], ["Go, and fear not.", "가거라, 그리고 두려워 말라."], ["Be not bitter against men.", "사람들에게 모질게 굴지 말라."], ["Be not angry if you are wronged.", "그대가 부당한 일을 당하더라도 노여워하지 말라."], ["Forgive the dead man in your heart what wrong he did you.", "죽은 그 사람이 그대에게 끼친 잘못을 마음속으로 용서하라. 그와 진실로 화해하라."], ["Be reconciled with him in truth.", "그대가 뉘우친다면 그대는 사랑하는 것이라."], ["If you are penitent, you love. And if you love you are of God.", "그리고 그대가 사랑한다면 그대는 하느님께 속한 것이라."], ["All things are atoned for, all things are saved by love.", "만물이 사랑으로 속죄되며, 만물이 사랑으로 구원받느니라."], ["If I, a sinner, even as you are, am tender with you and have pity on you, how much more will God.", "그대와 똑같이 죄인인 나조차 그대에게 다정하며 그대를 가엾이 여기거늘, 하물며 하느님이야 얼마나 더하시겠느냐."], ["Love is such a priceless treasure that you can redeem the whole world by it, and expiate not only your own sins but the sins of others.”", "사랑은 그것 하나로 온 세상을 구할 수 있고, 그대 자신의 죄뿐 아니라 남들의 죄까지 갚을 수 있는, 그토록 값을 매길 수 없는 보배니라.\""]], [["He signed her three times with the cross, took from his own neck a little ikon and put it upon her.", "그는 그녀에게 세 번 십자가를 그어 주고, 자기 목에서 작은 성상을 벗어 그녀에게 걸어 주었다."], ["She bowed down to the earth without speaking.", "그녀는 아무 말 없이 땅에 엎드려 절했다."]], [["He got up and looked cheerfully at a healthy peasant woman with a tiny baby in her arms.", "그는 일어나, 자그마한 갓난아기를 품에 안은 건강한 농사꾼 아낙네를 흐뭇하게 바라보았다."]], [["“From Vyshegorye, dear Father.”", "\"비셰고리예에서 왔어요, 고마우신 신부님.\""]], [["“Five miles you have dragged yourself with the baby.", "\"갓난것을 데리고 오 베르스타나 걸어왔구나."], ["What do you want?”", "무엇을 바라느냐?\""]], [["“I’ve come to look at you.", "\"신부님을 뵈러 왔어요."], ["I have been to you before—or have you forgotten?", "전에도 신부님을 찾아뵌 적이 있는데—혹 잊으셨나요?"], ["You’ve no great memory if you’ve forgotten me.", "절 잊으셨다면 신부님 기억력이 신통찮으신 거예요."], ["They told us you were ill.", "신부님이 편찮으시다고들 하더군요."], ["Thinks I, I’ll go and see him for myself.", "그래서 제가 생각했지요, 내가 직접 가서 뵈어야겠다고요."], ["Now I see you, and you’re not ill!", "이제 뵈니 편찮으신 게 아니네요!"], ["You’ll live another twenty years.", "앞으로 스무 해는 더 사시겠어요."], ["God bless you!", "하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를!"], ["There are plenty to pray for you; how should you be ill?”", "신부님을 위해 기도하는 사람이 이렇게 많은데, 어찌 편찮으실 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“I thank you for all, daughter.”", "\"모든 것에 고맙구나, 딸아.\""]], [["“By the way, I have a thing to ask, not a great one.", "\"그건 그렇고, 청이 하나 있는데, 대단한 건 아니에요."], ["Here are sixty copecks.", "여기 육십 코페이카가 있어요."], ["Give them, dear Father, to some one poorer than me.", "고마우신 신부님, 이걸 저보다 더 가난한 누군가에게 주세요."], ["I thought as I came along, better give through him.", "오는 길에 생각했는데, 신부님을 통해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어요."], ["He’ll know whom to give to.”", "신부님은 누구한테 줘야 할지 아실 테니까요.\""]], [["“Thanks, my dear, thanks!", "\"고맙구나, 내 딸아, 고마워!"], ["You are a good woman.", "그대는 착한 여인이로다."], ["I love you.", "그대를 사랑하노라."], ["I will do so certainly.", "꼭 그리하겠다."], ["Is that your little girl?”", "저 아이가 그대의 딸이냐?\""]], [["“My little girl, Father, Lizaveta.”", "\"제 딸이에요, 신부님, 리자베타랍니다.\""]], [["“May the Lord bless you both, you and your babe Lizaveta!", "\"주께서 그대 둘 다, 그대와 그대의 갓난것 리자베타를 축복하시기를!"], ["You have gladdened my heart, mother.", "그대가 내 마음을 기쁘게 하였구나, 어미여."], ["Farewell, dear children, farewell, dear ones.”", "잘 가거라, 사랑스러운 아이들아, 잘 가거라, 귀한 사람들아.\""]], [["He blessed them all and bowed low to them.", "그는 그들 모두를 축복하고 그들에게 깊이 허리 굽혀 절했다."]], [["# Chapter IV. A Lady Of Little Faith", "# Chapter IV. A Lady Of Little Faith"]], [["A visitor looking on the scene of his conversation with the peasants and his blessing them shed silent tears and wiped them away with her handkerchief.", "그가 농부들과 대화를 나누고 그들을 축복하는 광경을 지켜보던 한 방문객은 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 손수건으로 그것을 닦아 냈다."], ["She was a sentimental society lady of genuinely good disposition in many respects.", "그녀는 여러 면에서 진정으로 선량한 천성을 지닌, 감상적인 사교계 부인이었다."], ["When the elder went up to her at last she met him enthusiastically.", "마침내 장로가 그녀에게 다가가자 그녀는 열렬하게 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“Ah, what I have been feeling, looking on at this touching scene!...”", "\"아아, 이 가슴 뭉클한 광경을 지켜보면서 제가 얼마나 마음이 벅찼는지요!……\""], ["She could not go on for emotion.", "그녀는 감격에 겨워 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["“Oh, I understand the people’s love for you.", "\"오, 저는 백성들이 당신을 사랑하는 마음을 이해합니다."], ["I love the people myself.", "저 자신도 백성을 사랑하지요."], ["I want to love them.", "저는 그들을 사랑하고 싶어요."], ["And who could help loving them, our splendid Russian people, so simple in their greatness!”", "그리고 누군들 그들을 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어요, 그토록 위대하면서도 그토록 소박한 우리의 훌륭한 러시아 백성을!\""]], [["“How is your daughter’s health?", "\"따님의 건강은 어떻습니까?"], ["You wanted to talk to me again?”", "다시 저와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하셨다지요?\""]], [["“Oh, I have been urgently begging for it, I have prayed for it!", "\"오, 저는 그것을 간절히 청해 왔고, 그것을 위해 기도했답니다!"], ["I was ready to fall on my knees and kneel for three days at your windows until you let me in.", "당신이 저를 들여보내 주실 때까지 무릎을 꿇고 사흘이라도 당신의 창 밑에 엎드릴 각오였어요."], ["We have come, great healer, to express our ardent gratitude.", "위대한 치유자시여, 저희는 뜨거운 감사의 마음을 표하고자 찾아왔습니다."], ["You have healed my Lise, healed her completely, merely by praying over her last Thursday and laying your hands upon her.", "당신은 제 리즈를, 완전히 치유해 주셨어요, 지난 목요일에 그 아이를 위해 기도해 주시고 그 아이에게 손을 얹어 주신 것만으로 말이지요."], ["We have hastened here to kiss those hands, to pour out our feelings and our homage.”", "저희는 그 손에 입 맞추고, 저희의 감정과 경의를 쏟아 내고자 부랴부랴 달려왔답니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by healed?", "\"치유라니, 무슨 말씀이신지요?"], ["But she is still lying down in her chair.”", "하지만 따님은 아직도 의자에 누워 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["“But her night fevers have entirely ceased ever since Thursday,”", "\"하지만 목요일 이후로 그 아이의 밤 열이 완전히 가셨답니다.\""], ["said the lady with nervous haste.", "부인이 들뜬 듯 다급하게 말했다."], ["“And that’s not all.", "\"그것뿐이 아니에요."], ["Her legs are stronger.", "그 아이의 다리에 힘이 붙었답니다."], ["This morning she got up well; she had slept all night.", "오늘 아침엔 멀쩡하게 일어났고, 밤새도록 잘 잤지요."], ["Look at her rosy cheeks, her bright eyes!", "저 발그레한 뺨을, 저 반짝이는 눈을 보세요!"], ["She used to be always crying, but now she laughs and is gay and happy.", "예전엔 노상 울기만 하더니, 이젠 웃고 명랑하고 행복하답니다."], ["This morning she insisted on my letting her stand up, and she stood up for a whole minute without any support.", "오늘 아침엔 자기를 일으켜 세워 달라고 우기더니, 아무 데도 기대지 않고 꼬박 일 분 동안이나 서 있었어요. 그 아이는 보름 안에 카드리유를 추겠다고 장담한답니다."], ["She wagers that in a fortnight she’ll be dancing a quadrille.", "헤르첸슈투베 박사를 불렀더니, 그는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말하더군요, '놀랍습니다."], ["I’ve called in Doctor Herzenstube.", "도무지 영문을 모르겠어요.'"], ["He shrugged his shoulders and said, ‘I am amazed; I can make nothing of it.’ And would you have us not come here to disturb you, not fly here to thank you?", "이러한데 저희더러 당신을 성가시게 하러 이곳에 오지 말라고, 당신께 감사드리러 날아오지 말라고 하실 수 있겠어요?"], ["Lise, thank him—thank him!”", "리즈, 이분께 감사드리렴ㅡ감사드려!\""]], [["Lise’s pretty little laughing face became suddenly serious.", "리즈의 예쁘장한 작은 웃음 띤 얼굴이 별안간 진지해졌다."], ["She rose in her chair as far as she could and, looking at the elder, clasped her hands before him, but could not restrain herself and broke into laughter.", "그녀는 의자에서 일어설 수 있는 데까지 몸을 일으키더니, 장로를 바라보며 그 앞에서 두 손을 모았으나, 자신을 억누르지 못하고 그만 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["“It’s at him,”", "\"저분 때문이에요.\""], ["she said, pointing to Alyosha, with childish vexation at herself for not being able to repress her mirth.", "그녀가 알료샤를 가리키며 말했는데, 자신의 웃음을 참지 못하는 스스로에게 어린아이처럼 약이 오른 투였다."]], [["If any one had looked at Alyosha standing a step behind the elder, he would have caught a quick flush crimsoning his cheeks in an instant.", "만약 누군가 장로 뒤로 한 걸음 물러서 있던 알료샤를 바라보았다면, 순식간에 그의 뺨이 새빨갛게 달아오르는 것을 보았으리라."], ["His eyes shone and he looked down.", "그의 눈이 빛났고, 그는 시선을 내리깔았다."]], [["“She has a message for you, Alexey Fyodorovitch. How are you?”", "\"이 아이가 당신께 전할 말이 있답니다, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["the mother went on, holding out her exquisitely gloved hand to Alyosha.", "그동안 안녕하셨어요?\" 어머니가 우아하게 장갑을 낀 손을 알료샤에게 내밀며 말을 이었다."]], [["The elder turned round and all at once looked attentively at Alyosha.", "장로가 몸을 돌리더니 별안간 알료샤를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["The latter went nearer to Lise and, smiling in a strangely awkward way, held out his hand to her too.", "알료샤는 리즈에게 가까이 다가가, 묘하게 어색한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게도 손을 내밀었다."], ["Lise assumed an important air.", "리즈는 짐짓 거드름을 피우는 태를 지었다."]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna has sent you this through me.”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나가 저를 통해 이것을 당신께 보냈어요.\""], ["She handed him a little note.", "그녀가 그에게 쪽지를 건넸다."], ["“She particularly begs you to go and see her as soon as possible; that you will not fail her, but will be sure to come.”", "\"그분이 특별히 부탁하시기를, 되도록 빨리 자기를 찾아와 달라고, 그분을 저버리지 말고 꼭 와 달라고 하셨답니다.\""]], [["“She asks me to go and see her?", "\"그분이 저더러 찾아와 달라고요? 저를요?"], ["Me? What for?”", "무슨 일로요?\""], ["Alyosha muttered in great astonishment.", "알료샤가 몹시 놀라 중얼거렸다."], ["His face at once looked anxious.", "그의 얼굴이 단번에 불안한 빛을 띠었다."], ["“Oh, it’s all to do with Dmitri Fyodorovitch and—what has happened lately,”", "\"오, 그건 모두 드미트리 표도로비치와ㅡ요즘 벌어진 일과 관련된 거예요.\""], ["the mother explained hurriedly.", "어머니가 황급히 설명했다."], ["“Katerina Ivanovna has made up her mind, but she must see you about it....", "\"카테리나 이바노브나가 마음을 굳혔는데, 그 일에 관해 당신을 꼭 만나야 한답니다……"], ["Why, of course, I can’t say.", "무슨 일인지는, 글쎄요, 저로선 말할 수 없네요."], ["But she wants to see you at once.", "하지만 그분은 당장 당신을 보고 싶어 해요."], ["And you will go to her, of course.", "그러니 당신도 물론 그분께 가시겠지요."], ["It is a Christian duty.”", "그건 그리스도교인의 도리니까요.\""]], [["“I have only seen her once,”", "\"저는 그분을 단 한 번 뵈었을 뿐인데요.\""], ["Alyosha protested with the same perplexity.", "알료샤가 여전히 어리둥절해하며 항변했다."]], [["“Oh, she is such a lofty, incomparable creature!", "\"오, 그분은 어쩌면 그렇게도 고결하고 비길 데 없는 분인지요!"], ["If only for her suffering....", "그 고통만으로도 충분하답니다……"], ["Think what she has gone through, what she is enduring now!", "그분이 무엇을 겪어 왔는지, 지금 무엇을 견디고 있는지 생각해 보세요!"], ["Think what awaits her!", "그분 앞에 무엇이 기다리고 있는지 생각해 보세요!"], ["It’s all terrible, terrible!”", "모든 게 끔찍하고, 또 끔찍하답니다!\""]], [["“Very well, I will come,”", "\"좋습니다, 가지요.\""], ["Alyosha decided, after rapidly scanning the brief, enigmatic note, which consisted of an urgent entreaty that he would come, without any sort of explanation.", "알료샤는 간결하면서도 알 수 없는 그 쪽지를 재빨리 훑어본 뒤 그렇게 결정했는데, 거기에는 아무런 설명도 없이 그저 와 달라는 간절한 애원만이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Oh, how sweet and generous that would be of you!”", "\"오, 그렇게 해 주시면 얼마나 다정하고 너그러운 일인지요!\""], ["cried Lise with sudden animation.", "리즈가 별안간 활기를 띠며 외쳤다."], ["“I told mamma you’d be sure not to go.", "\"엄마한테는 분명 안 가실 거라고 했어요."], ["I said you were saving your soul.", "당신은 영혼을 구하느라 바쁘다고 했지요."], ["How splendid you are!", "당신은 얼마나 훌륭한지!"], ["I’ve always thought you were splendid.", "저는 늘 당신이 훌륭하다고 생각했답니다."], ["How glad I am to tell you so!”", "그걸 당신께 말씀드릴 수 있어서 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요!\""]], [["“Lise!”", "\"리즈!\""], ["said her mother impressively, though she smiled after she had said it.", "그녀의 어머니가 위엄을 담아 말했으나, 말을 마친 뒤에는 미소를 지었다."]], [["“You have quite forgotten us, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"당신은 저희를 까맣게 잊으셨군요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["she said; “you never come to see us.", "그녀가 말했다. \"한 번도 저희를 보러 오지 않으시니."], ["Yet Lise has told me twice that she is never happy except with you.”", "그런데 리즈는 두 번이나 제게 말했답니다, 당신과 함께 있을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없다고요.\""]], [["Alyosha raised his downcast eyes and again flushed, and again smiled without knowing why.", "알료샤는 내리깔았던 눈을 들었다가 또다시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 또다시 까닭도 모르게 미소를 지었다."], ["But the elder was no longer watching him.", "그러나 장로는 더 이상 그를 바라보고 있지 않았다."], ["He had begun talking to a monk who, as mentioned before, had been awaiting his entrance by Lise’s chair.", "그는 앞서 말했듯 리즈의 의자 곁에서 자신이 나오기를 기다리고 있던 한 수도사와 이야기를 나누기 시작했던 것이다."], ["He was evidently a monk of the humblest, that is of the peasant, class, of a narrow outlook, but a true believer, and, in his own way, a stubborn one.", "그는 분명 가장 비천한, 다시 말해 농민 계층 출신의 수도사로서, 견식은 좁으나 진실한 신자였고, 나름대로 고집스러운 사람이었다."], ["He announced that he had come from the far north, from Obdorsk, from Saint Sylvester, and was a member of a poor monastery, consisting of only ten monks.", "그는 자신이 머나먼 북방, 오브도르스크의 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 왔으며, 겨우 열 명의 수도사로 이루어진 가난한 수도원의 일원이라고 밝혔다."], ["The elder gave him his blessing and invited him to come to his cell whenever he liked.", "장로는 그에게 축복을 내리고 언제든 마음 내킬 때 자신의 암자로 찾아오라고 청했다."]], [["“How can you presume to do such deeds?”", "\"어찌 감히 그런 일을 행하실 수 있습니까?\""], ["the monk asked suddenly, pointing solemnly and significantly at Lise.", "수도사가 별안간, 엄숙하고 의미심장하게 리즈를 가리키며 물었다."], ["He was referring to her “healing.”", "그는 그녀의 \"치유\"를 두고 하는 말이었다."]], [["“It’s too early, of course, to speak of that.", "\"물론 그것을 논하기엔 아직 이릅니다."], ["Relief is not complete cure, and may proceed from different causes.", "차도는 완전한 치유가 아니며, 여러 다른 원인에서 비롯될 수도 있지요."], ["But if there has been any healing, it is by no power but God’s will.", "그러나 만약 어떤 치유가 일어났다면, 그것은 다른 어떤 힘이 아니라 오직 하느님의 뜻에 의한 것입니다."], ["It’s all from God.", "모든 게 하느님으로부터 온 것이지요."], ["Visit me, Father,”", "저를 찾아오십시오, 신부님.\""], ["he added to the monk.", "그가 수도사에게 덧붙였다."], ["“It’s not often I can see visitors.", "\"제가 손님을 맞을 수 있는 날이 그리 많지 않답니다."], ["I am ill, and I know that my days are numbered.”", "저는 병들었고, 제 날이 얼마 남지 않았음을 알고 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, no!", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!"], ["God will not take you from us.", "하느님께서 당신을 저희에게서 데려가시지 않을 거예요."], ["You will live a long, long time yet,”", "당신은 아직 오래오래 사실 거예요.\""], ["cried the lady.", "부인이 외쳤다."], ["“And in what way are you ill?", "\"한데 어디가 그리 편찮으신가요?"], ["You look so well, so gay and happy.”", "이토록 정정하시고, 이토록 명랑하고 행복해 보이시는데요.\""]], [["“I am extraordinarily better to-day.", "\"오늘은 유난히 좋아진 것이지요."], ["But I know that it’s only for a moment.", "하지만 그것이 한순간에 지나지 않음을 저는 압니다."], ["I understand my disease now thoroughly.", "이제 저는 제 병을 속속들이 알고 있어요."], ["If I seem so happy to you, you could never say anything that would please me so much.", "제가 당신께 그토록 행복해 보인다면, 당신은 제게 그보다 더 기쁜 말을 결코 해 줄 수 없을 겁니다."], ["For men are made for happiness, and any one who is completely happy has a right to say to himself, ‘I am doing God’s will on earth.’ All the righteous, all the saints, all the holy martyrs were happy.”", "인간은 행복을 위해 지어졌고, 온전히 행복한 사람은 누구나 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 권리가 있으니까요, '나는 이 땅에서 하느님의 뜻을 행하고 있다'고요. 모든 의인이, 모든 성인이, 모든 거룩한 순교자가 행복했답니다.\""]], [["“Oh, how you speak!", "\"오, 어찌 그리 말씀하시는지요!"], ["What bold and lofty words!”", "얼마나 대담하고 고결한 말씀인지!\""], ["cried the lady.", "부인이 외쳤다."], ["“You seem to pierce with your words.", "\"당신의 말씀은 마음을 꿰뚫는 듯합니다."], ["And yet—happiness, happiness—where is it?", "그런데도ㅡ행복이, 행복이ㅡ대체 어디에 있을까요?"], ["Who can say of himself that he is happy?", "누가 스스로 행복하다고 말할 수 있을까요?"], ["Oh, since you have been so good as to let us see you once more to-day, let me tell you what I could not utter last time, what I dared not say, all I am suffering and have been for so long!", "오, 당신께서 오늘 한 번 더 저희를 만나 주시는 은혜를 베푸셨으니, 지난번에는 차마 입에 담지 못했던 것, 감히 말하지 못했던 것, 제가 그토록 오랫동안 겪어 온 모든 괴로움을 말씀드리게 해 주세요!"], ["I am suffering!", "저는 괴로워하고 있어요!"], ["Forgive me!", "용서하세요!"], ["I am suffering!”", "저는 괴로워하고 있답니다!\""]], [["And in a rush of fervent feeling she clasped her hands before him.", "그러고는 북받쳐 오르는 열렬한 감정에 휩싸여 그녀는 그 앞에서 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“From what specially?”", "\"특히 무엇 때문에 그러십니까?\""]], [["“I suffer ...", "\"저는 괴로워하고 있어요……"], ["from lack of faith.”", "믿음이 부족해서요.\""]], [["“Lack of faith in God?”", "\"하느님에 대한 믿음이 부족하다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no, no!", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요!"], ["I dare not even think of that.", "저는 감히 그런 건 생각조차 할 수 없어요."], ["But the future life—it is such an enigma!", "하지만 내세ㅡ그건 너무도 알 수 없는 수수께끼랍니다!"], ["And no one, no one can solve it. Listen!", "그리고 아무도, 아무도 그것을 풀 수 없어요."], ["You are a healer, you are deeply versed in the human soul, and of course I dare not expect you to believe me entirely, but I assure you on my word of honor that I am not speaking lightly now.", "들어 보세요! 당신은 치유자시고, 인간의 영혼에 깊이 통달하신 분이니, 물론 제가 당신께 저를 온전히 믿어 달라고 감히 바랄 수는 없지만, 제 명예를 걸고 단언컨대 저는 지금 결코 가벼이 말하고 있는 게 아니랍니다."], ["The thought of the life beyond the grave distracts me to anguish, to terror.", "무덤 너머의 삶에 대한 생각이 저를 번민으로, 공포로 몰아넣어요."], ["And I don’t know to whom to appeal, and have not dared to all my life.", "그런데 저는 누구에게 호소해야 할지 모르겠고, 평생토록 감히 그러지도 못했어요."], ["And now I am so bold as to ask you.", "그리고 이제 이렇게 대담하게 당신께 여쭙고 있는 거예요."], ["Oh, God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["What will you think of me now?”", "당신은 이제 저를 어떻게 생각하실까요?\""]], [["She clasped her hands.", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Don’t distress yourself about my opinion of you,”", "\"제가 당신을 어떻게 생각할지로 괴로워하지 마십시오.\""], ["said the elder.", "장로가 말했다."], ["“I quite believe in the sincerity of your suffering.”", "\"저는 당신이 겪는 고통의 진실함을 충분히 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, how thankful I am to you!", "\"오, 당신께 얼마나 감사한지 몰라요!"], ["You see, I shut my eyes and ask myself if every one has faith, where did it come from?", "보세요, 저는 눈을 감고 스스로에게 물어본답니다, 만약 모두가 믿음을 지니고 있다면 그건 대체 어디서 온 것일까 하고요."], ["And then they do say that it all comes from terror at the menacing phenomena of nature, and that none of it’s real.", "그런데 사람들이 말하기를, 그건 모두 자연의 위협적인 현상에 대한 공포에서 비롯된 것이며, 그 어느 것도 실재하지 않는다고 하지요."], ["And I say to myself, ‘What if I’ve been believing all my life, and when I come to die there’s nothing but the burdocks growing on my grave?’ as I read in some author.", "그래서 저는 스스로에게 말한답니다, '내가 평생토록 믿어 왔는데, 막상 죽고 나면 내 무덤 위에 우엉이나 자라날 뿐이라면 어쩌지?' 하고요, 어느 작가의 글에서 읽은 대로 말이에요."], ["It’s awful!", "끔찍한 일이지요!"], ["How—how can I get back my faith?", "어떻게ㅡ어떻게 하면 제 믿음을 되찾을 수 있을까요?"], ["But I only believed when I was a little child, mechanically, without thinking of anything.", "하지만 저는 어린아이였을 적에만, 아무것도 생각하지 않고 그저 기계적으로 믿었을 뿐이에요."], ["How, how is one to prove it?", "어떻게, 어떻게 그것을 증명할 수 있을까요?"], ["I have come now to lay my soul before you and to ask you about it.", "저는 이제 제 영혼을 당신 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 그것에 관해 여쭙고자 찾아온 거예요."], ["If I let this chance slip, no one all my life will answer me.", "만약 이 기회를 놓친다면, 평생토록 아무도 제게 답해 주지 않을 거예요."], ["How can I prove it?", "어떻게 그것을 증명할 수 있을까요?"], ["How can I convince myself?", "어떻게 스스로를 납득시킬 수 있을까요?"], ["Oh, how unhappy I am!", "오, 저는 얼마나 불행한지!"], ["I stand and look about me and see that scarcely any one else cares; no one troubles his head about it, and I’m the only one who can’t stand it.", "저는 서서 주위를 둘러보며, 다른 사람은 거의 아무도 그것에 신경 쓰지 않는다는 걸 깨닫는답니다. 아무도 그 일로 골머리를 앓지 않는데, 그것을 견딜 수 없는 건 오직 저 하나뿐이에요."], ["It’s deadly—deadly!”", "죽을 것만 같아요ㅡ죽을 것만 같다고요!\""]], [["“No doubt.", "\"의심할 바 없는 일입니다."], ["But there’s no proving it, though you can be convinced of it.” “How?”", "그러나 그것은 증명할 수는 없으되, 확신할 수는 있습니다.\""]], [["“By the experience of active love.", "\"어떻게요?\" \"능동적인 사랑의 경험을 통해서지요."], ["Strive to love your neighbor actively and indefatigably.", "그대의 이웃을 능동적으로, 지칠 줄 모르고 사랑하려 애쓰십시오."], ["In as far as you advance in love you will grow surer of the reality of God and of the immortality of your soul.", "사랑 안에서 나아가는 만큼, 그대는 하느님의 실재와 그대 영혼의 불멸에 대한 확신이 더욱 굳어질 것입니다."], ["If you attain to perfect self-forgetfulness in the love of your neighbor, then you will believe without doubt, and no doubt can possibly enter your soul.", "만약 그대가 이웃에 대한 사랑 속에서 완전한 자기 망각에 이른다면, 그때 그대는 의심 없이 믿게 될 것이며, 어떤 의심도 그대의 영혼에 결코 비집고 들어올 수 없을 것입니다."], ["This has been tried. This is certain.”", "이는 이미 시험된 것이며, 분명한 것입니다.\""]], [["“In active love?", "\"능동적인 사랑 안에서요?"], ["There’s another question—and such a question!", "거기에 또 다른 문제가 있어요ㅡ그것도 이런 문제가요!"], ["You see, I so love humanity that—would you believe it?—I often dream of forsaking all that I have, leaving Lise, and becoming a sister of mercy.", "보세요, 저는 인류를 너무도 사랑한 나머지ㅡ믿으시겠어요?ㅡ제가 가진 모든 것을 버리고, 리즈도 떠나, 자선 수녀가 되는 꿈을 자주 꾼답니다."], ["I close my eyes and think and dream, and at that moment I feel full of strength to overcome all obstacles.", "저는 눈을 감고 생각하고 꿈꾸는데, 그 순간엔 모든 장애를 이겨 낼 힘이 가득 찬 것만 같아요."], ["No wounds, no festering sores could at that moment frighten me.", "그 어떤 상처도, 그 어떤 곪은 종기도 그 순간만큼은 저를 두렵게 하지 못해요."], ["I would bind them up and wash them with my own hands.", "저는 제 손으로 그것을 싸매고 씻어 줄 거예요."], ["I would nurse the afflicted.", "저는 병든 이들을 간호할 거예요."], ["I would be ready to kiss such wounds.”", "저는 그런 상처에 기꺼이 입 맞출 각오가 되어 있답니다.\""]], [["“It is much, and well that your mind is full of such dreams and not others.", "\"대단한 일이며, 그대의 마음이 다른 것이 아닌 그러한 꿈으로 가득 차 있다는 것은 좋은 일입니다."], ["Sometime, unawares, you may do a good deed in reality.”", "언젠가 그대도 모르는 사이에, 그대는 현실에서 선한 일을 행하게 될지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["But could I endure such a life for long?”", "하지만 제가 그런 삶을 오래 견뎌 낼 수 있을까요?\""], ["the lady went on fervently, almost frantically.", "부인이 거의 미친 듯이, 열렬하게 말을 이었다."], ["“That’s the chief question—that’s my most agonizing question.", "\"그게 가장 큰 문제예요ㅡ그게 저를 가장 번민케 하는 문제랍니다."], ["I shut my eyes and ask myself, ‘Would you persevere long on that path?", "저는 눈을 감고 스스로에게 물어요, '너는 그 길을 오래 꿋꿋이 갈 수 있겠느냐?"], ["And if the patient whose wounds you are washing did not meet you with gratitude, but worried you with his whims, without valuing or remarking your charitable services, began abusing you and rudely commanding you, and complaining to the superior authorities of you (which often happens when people are in great suffering)—what then?", "그리고 만약 네가 그 상처를 씻어 주는 환자가 너를 감사로 맞기는커녕, 변덕으로 너를 괴롭히고, 네 자비로운 봉사를 가치 있게 여기지도 알아주지도 않으면서, 너를 욕하고 무례하게 부려 먹고, 윗사람에게 너를 일러바치기 시작한다면(사람이 큰 고통에 처했을 때 흔히 일어나는 일이지요)ㅡ그땐 어쩔 테냐? 너는 그 사랑을 꿋꿋이 이어 가겠느냐, 그러지 못하겠느냐?'"], ["Would you persevere in your love, or not?’ And do you know, I came with horror to the conclusion that, if anything could dissipate my love to humanity, it would be ingratitude.", "하고요. 그런데 아세요, 저는 소름 끼치는 결론에 이르렀답니다, 만약 인류를 향한 제 사랑을 흩어 버릴 수 있는 것이 있다면, 그건 바로 배은망덕이라는 결론에요."], ["In short, I am a hired servant, I expect my payment at once—that is, praise, and the repayment of love with love.", "한마디로 저는 삯을 받는 종이라, 제 보수를 당장 받기를 바라는 거예요ㅡ그러니까 칭찬과, 사랑에 대한 사랑의 보답을 말이지요."], ["Otherwise I am incapable of loving any one.”", "그렇지 않으면 저는 누구도 사랑할 수 없답니다.\""]], [["She was in a very paroxysm of self-castigation, and, concluding, she looked with defiant resolution at the elder.", "그녀는 그야말로 자기 질책의 발작에 빠져 있었으며, 말을 맺으면서 도전적인 결의를 담아 장로를 바라보았다."]], [["“It’s just the same story as a doctor once told me,”", "\"그것은 언젠가 한 의사가 제게 들려준 이야기와 꼭 같습니다.\""], ["observed the elder.", "장로가 말했다."], ["“He was a man getting on in years, and undoubtedly clever.", "\"나이가 지긋한 사람이었는데, 의심할 바 없이 영리한 자였지요."], ["He spoke as frankly as you, though in jest, in bitter jest.", "그는 그대처럼 솔직하게 말했습니다, 비록 농담조였으나, 그것도 쓰디쓴 농담조였지만요. '나는 인류를 사랑하오.'"], ["‘I love humanity,’ he said, ‘but I wonder at myself.", "그가 말했지요. '하지만 나 자신이 의아할 따름이오."], ["The more I love humanity in general, the less I love man in particular.", "인류 전체를 사랑하면 할수록, 개개의 인간은 그만큼 덜 사랑하게 되니 말이오. 내 꿈속에서는,'"], ["In my dreams,’ he said, ‘I have often come to making enthusiastic schemes for the service of humanity, and perhaps I might actually have faced crucifixion if it had been suddenly necessary; and yet I am incapable of living in the same room with any one for two days together, as I know by experience.", "그가 말했습니다, '나는 인류에 봉사하기 위한 열렬한 계획을 세우는 데까지 곧잘 이르렀고, 만약 갑자기 그래야만 한다면 어쩌면 실제로 십자가에 못 박히는 일도 마다하지 않았을 거요. 그런데도 나는 누구와도 단 이틀을 한방에서 함께 지낼 수가 없으니, 이는 내가 경험으로 아는 바요."], ["As soon as any one is near me, his personality disturbs my self-complacency and restricts my freedom.", "누군가 내 곁에 다가오기만 하면, 그의 인격이 내 자기만족을 어지럽히고 내 자유를 옭아매오."], ["In twenty-four hours I begin to hate the best of men: one because he’s too long over his dinner; another because he has a cold and keeps on blowing his nose.", "스물네 시간 안에 나는 아무리 훌륭한 사람이라도 미워하기 시작하오. 어떤 자는 저녁 식사를 너무 오래 끈다고, 또 어떤 자는 감기에 걸려 줄곧 코를 푼다고 말이오."], ["I become hostile to people the moment they come close to me.", "사람들이 내게 가까이 다가오는 순간, 나는 그들에게 적의를 품게 되오."], ["But it has always happened that the more I detest men individually the more ardent becomes my love for humanity.’”", "그러나 늘 그러했듯, 내가 개개의 인간을 혐오하면 할수록, 인류를 향한 내 사랑은 그만큼 더 뜨거워지는 거요.'\""]], [["“But what’s to be done?", "\"하지만 어찌해야 하나요?"], ["What can one do in such a case?", "그런 경우엔 어떻게 해야 하지요?"], ["Must one despair?”", "절망해야만 하나요?\""]], [["“No. It is enough that you are distressed at it.", "\"아닙니다."], ["Do what you can, and it will be reckoned unto you.", "그대가 그 일로 괴로워한다는 것만으로도 충분합니다."], ["Much is done already in you since you can so deeply and sincerely know yourself.", "할 수 있는 일을 하십시오, 그러면 그것이 그대에게 헤아려질 것입니다."], ["If you have been talking to me so sincerely, simply to gain approbation for your frankness, as you did from me just now, then of course you will not attain to anything in the achievement of real love; it will all get no further than dreams, and your whole life will slip away like a phantom.", "그대가 그토록 깊고 진실하게 자신을 알 수 있으니, 이미 그대 안에서 많은 일이 이루어진 셈입니다. 만약 그대가 제게 그토록 진실하게 이야기한 것이, 단지 방금 제게서 그러했듯 자신의 솔직함에 대한 칭찬을 얻기 위함이었다면, 물론 그대는 참된 사랑을 이루는 일에 아무것도 도달하지 못할 것입니다."], ["In that case you will naturally cease to think of the future life too, and will of yourself grow calmer after a fashion in the end.”", "그 모든 것이 꿈에 머물고 말 것이며, 그대의 온 생애가 환영처럼 스러져 갈 것입니다. 그런 경우 그대는 자연히 내세에 대해서도 더는 생각하지 않게 될 것이며, 끝내는 어떤 식으로든 스스로 더 평온해질 것입니다.\""]], [["“You have crushed me!", "\"당신은 저를 무너뜨리셨어요!"], ["Only now, as you speak, I understand that I was really only seeking your approbation for my sincerity when I told you I could not endure ingratitude.", "이제야, 당신이 말씀하시는 이 순간에야, 제가 배은망덕을 견딜 수 없다고 당신께 말씀드렸을 때 정말이지 제 진실함에 대한 당신의 칭찬을 구하고 있었을 뿐임을 깨닫는답니다."], ["You have revealed me to myself.", "당신은 저를 제 자신에게 드러내 보이셨어요."], ["You have seen through me and explained me to myself!”", "당신은 저를 꿰뚫어 보시고, 저를 제 자신에게 설명해 주셨어요!\""]], [["“Are you speaking the truth?", "\"진실을 말씀하시는 겁니까?"], ["Well, now, after such a confession, I believe that you are sincere and good at heart.", "자, 그럼, 그런 고백을 하셨으니, 저는 그대가 진실하고 마음이 선한 사람임을 믿습니다."], ["If you do not attain happiness, always remember that you are on the right road, and try not to leave it.", "비록 그대가 행복에 이르지 못한다 하더라도, 그대가 옳은 길 위에 있음을 늘 기억하고, 그 길에서 벗어나지 않도록 애쓰십시오."], ["Above all, avoid falsehood, every kind of falsehood, especially falseness to yourself.", "무엇보다 거짓을 피하십시오, 온갖 종류의 거짓을, 특히 자기 자신에 대한 거짓을요."], ["Watch over your own deceitfulness and look into it every hour, every minute.", "그대 자신의 기만을 살피고, 매시간, 매분 그것을 들여다보십시오."], ["Avoid being scornful, both to others and to yourself.", "남에게든 자신에게든 경멸하는 태도를 피하십시오."], ["What seems to you bad within you will grow purer from the very fact of your observing it in yourself.", "그대 안에서 나쁘게 보이는 것은, 그대가 그것을 자신 안에서 관찰한다는 바로 그 사실로 인해 더욱 정결해질 것입니다."], ["Avoid fear, too, though fear is only the consequence of every sort of falsehood.", "두려움도 피하십시오, 비록 두려움이 온갖 거짓의 결과에 지나지 않는다 해도요."], ["Never be frightened at your own faint-heartedness in attaining love.", "사랑에 이르는 데 있어 자신의 나약함을 결코 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["Don’t be frightened overmuch even at your evil actions.", "자신의 악행조차도 지나치게 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["I am sorry I can say nothing more consoling to you, for love in action is a harsh and dreadful thing compared with love in dreams.", "더 위안이 될 말을 해 드릴 수 없어 미안합니다만, 행동하는 사랑은 꿈속의 사랑에 비하면 가혹하고 무서운 것이니까요."], ["Love in dreams is greedy for immediate action, rapidly performed and in the sight of all.", "꿈속의 사랑은 즉각적인 행동을, 모두가 지켜보는 가운데 신속히 이루어지는 행동을 갈망합니다."], ["Men will even give their lives if only the ordeal does not last long but is soon over, with all looking on and applauding as though on the stage.", "사람들은 시련이 오래 끌지 않고 곧 끝나기만 한다면, 마치 무대 위에서처럼 모두가 지켜보며 갈채를 보내는 가운데서라면, 심지어 제 목숨까지도 내놓을 것입니다."], ["But active love is labor and fortitude, and for some people too, perhaps, a complete science.", "그러나 능동적인 사랑은 노고이자 인내이며, 어떤 이들에게는 어쩌면 하나의 완전한 학문이기까지 합니다."], ["But I predict that just when you see with horror that in spite of all your efforts you are getting farther from your goal instead of nearer to it—at that very moment I predict that you will reach it and behold clearly the miraculous power of the Lord who has been all the time loving and mysteriously guiding you.", "그러나 제가 예언하건대, 바로 그대가 온갖 노력에도 불구하고 목표에 가까워지기는커녕 오히려 멀어지고 있음을 소름 끼치도록 깨닫게 되는 그 순간ㅡ제가 예언하건대 바로 그 순간에 그대는 목표에 도달할 것이며, 그 모든 시간 동안 그대를 사랑하고 신비로이 인도해 오신 주님의 기적 같은 권능을 똑똑히 보게 될 것입니다."], ["Forgive me for not being able to stay longer with you.", "더 오래 그대와 함께 있어 드리지 못하는 것을 용서하십시오."], ["They are waiting for me.", "사람들이 저를 기다리고 있군요."], ["Good-by.”", "그럼 안녕히.\""]], [["The lady was weeping.", "부인은 울고 있었다."]], [["“Lise, Lise!", "\"리즈, 리즈!"], ["Bless her—bless her!”", "이 아이를 축복해 주세요ㅡ축복해 주세요!\""], ["she cried, starting up suddenly.", "그녀가 별안간 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."]], [["“She does not deserve to be loved.", "\"이 아이는 사랑받을 자격이 없습니다."], ["I have seen her naughtiness all along,”", "저는 줄곧 이 아이의 짓궂음을 보아 왔으니까요.\""], ["the elder said jestingly.", "장로가 농담조로 말했다."], ["“Why have you been laughing at Alexey?”", "\"어찌하여 알렉세이를 놀려 댔느냐?\""]], [["Lise had in fact been occupied in mocking at him all the time.", "리즈는 사실 내내 그를 조롱하는 데 골몰해 있었다."], ["She had noticed before that Alyosha was shy and tried not to look at her, and she found this extremely amusing.", "그녀는 알료샤가 수줍어하며 자기를 보지 않으려 애쓰는 것을 진작에 알아챘고, 그것이 더없이 재미있었던 것이다."], ["She waited intently to catch his eye.", "그녀는 그의 눈길을 붙잡으려고 골똘히 기다렸다."], ["Alyosha, unable to endure her persistent stare, was irresistibly and suddenly drawn to glance at her, and at once she smiled triumphantly in his face.", "알료샤는 그녀의 집요한 응시를 견디지 못하고, 거역할 수 없이 별안간 그녀를 흘끗 보고 싶은 마음에 이끌렸는데, 그러자 그녀는 곧장 그의 얼굴에 대고 의기양양하게 미소를 지었다."], ["Alyosha was even more disconcerted and vexed.", "알료샤는 더욱 당황하고 약이 올랐다."], ["At last he turned away from her altogether and hid behind the elder’s back.", "마침내 그는 그녀에게서 완전히 몸을 돌려 장로의 등 뒤로 숨었다."], ["After a few minutes, drawn by the same irresistible force, he turned again to see whether he was being looked at or not, and found Lise almost hanging out of her chair to peep sideways at him, eagerly waiting for him to look.", "몇 분 뒤, 똑같이 거역할 수 없는 힘에 이끌려, 그는 자기가 응시당하고 있는지 아닌지 보려고 다시 몸을 돌렸는데, 리즈가 그를 보려고 의자에서 거의 몸을 내밀다시피 곁눈질하며, 그가 자기를 봐 주기를 애타게 기다리고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Catching his eye, she laughed so that the elder could not help saying, “Why do you make fun of him like that, naughty girl?”", "그의 눈길을 붙잡자 그녀가 어찌나 웃어 대던지, 장로도 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"어찌하여 그렇게 저 아이를 놀려 대느냐, 짓궂은 아가씨야?\""]], [["Lise suddenly and quite unexpectedly blushed.", "리즈가 별안간, 전혀 뜻밖에도 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Her eyes flashed and her face became quite serious.", "그녀의 눈이 번뜩였고 얼굴이 자못 진지해졌다."], ["She began speaking quickly and nervously in a warm and resentful voice:", "그녀는 따뜻하면서도 원망스러운 목소리로 빠르고 격하게 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“Why has he forgotten everything, then?", "\"그럼 저 사람은 어째서 모든 걸 잊어버린 거예요?"], ["He used to carry me about when I was little.", "제가 어렸을 적엔 저를 안고 다녔어요."], ["We used to play together.", "저희는 함께 놀곤 했지요."], ["He used to come to teach me to read, do you know.", "저 사람은 제게 글 읽기를 가르쳐 주러 오곤 했답니다, 아세요."], ["Two years ago, when he went away, he said that he would never forget me, that we were friends for ever, for ever, for ever!", "이 년 전, 떠나갈 적에 저 사람은 저를 결코 잊지 않겠다고, 저희가 영원히, 영원히, 영원히 친구라고 말했어요!"], ["And now he’s afraid of me all at once.", "그런데 이제 와선 별안간 저를 두려워하잖아요."], ["Am I going to eat him?", "제가 저 사람을 잡아먹기라도 한대요?"], ["Why doesn’t he want to come near me?", "어째서 제 곁에 오려 하지 않는 거죠?"], ["Why doesn’t he talk?", "어째서 말을 안 하는 거예요?"], ["Why won’t he come and see us?", "어째서 저희를 보러 오지 않는 거죠?"], ["It’s not that you won’t let him.", "당신이 못 오게 하시는 것도 아닌데."], ["We know that he goes everywhere.", "저 사람이 어딜 가든 다 돌아다닌다는 걸 저희도 알아요."], ["It’s not good manners for me to invite him.", "제가 저 사람을 초대하는 건 예의에 어긋나는 일이지요."], ["He ought to have thought of it first, if he hasn’t forgotten me.", "저를 잊지 않았다면 저 사람이 먼저 그 생각을 했어야죠."], ["No, now he’s saving his soul!", "아뇨, 이제 저 사람은 영혼을 구하느라 바쁘대요!"], ["Why have you put that long gown on him?", "어째서 저 사람한테 저런 긴 가운을 입히신 거예요?"], ["If he runs he’ll fall.”", "뛰다간 넘어지고 말 텐데.\""]], [["And suddenly she hid her face in her hand and went off into irresistible, prolonged, nervous, inaudible laughter.", "그러더니 그녀는 별안간 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 거역할 수 없이, 오래도록, 격하게, 소리 없는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The elder listened to her with a smile, and blessed her tenderly.", "장로는 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 말을 듣고 있다가, 다정하게 그녀를 축복했다."], ["As she kissed his hand she suddenly pressed it to her eyes and began crying.", "그녀는 그의 손에 입 맞추다가 별안간 그것을 자기 두 눈에 갖다 대고는 울기 시작했다."]], [["“Don’t be angry with me.", "\"저한테 화내지 마세요."], ["I’m silly and good for nothing ...", "저는 어리석고 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 아이예요……"], ["and perhaps Alyosha’s right, quite right, in not wanting to come and see such a ridiculous girl.”", "그리고 어쩌면 알료샤가 옳은지도, 아주 옳은지도 몰라요, 이렇게 우스꽝스러운 여자애를 보러 오고 싶어 하지 않는 게요.\""]], [["“I will certainly send him,”", "\"내가 반드시 저 아이를 보내마.\""], ["said the elder.", "장로가 말했다."]], [["# Chapter V. So Be It! So Be It!", "# Chapter V. So Be It! So Be It!"]], [["The elder’s absence from his cell had lasted for about twenty-five minutes.", "장로가 암자를 비운 시간은 이십오 분쯤이었다."], ["It was more than half-past twelve, but Dmitri, on whose account they had all met there, had still not appeared.", "벌써 열두 시 반이 넘었으나, 그들 모두가 그 때문에 이 자리에 모였던 드미트리는 아직 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["But he seemed almost to be forgotten, and when the elder entered the cell again, he found his guests engaged in eager conversation.", "그러나 그는 거의 잊힌 듯했으니, 장로가 다시 암자로 들어섰을 때 손님들은 열띤 대화에 빠져 있었다."], ["Ivan and the two monks took the leading share in it.", "이반과 두 수도사가 그 대화를 주도하고 있었다."], ["Miüsov, too, was trying to take a part, and apparently very eagerly, in the conversation.", "미우소프 또한 끼어들려고, 그것도 자못 열을 올리며 애를 쓰고 있었다."], ["But he was unsuccessful in this also.", "그러나 이 일에서도 그는 뜻을 이루지 못했다."], ["He was evidently in the background, and his remarks were treated with neglect, which increased his irritability.", "그는 분명히 뒷전으로 밀려나 있었고, 그의 말은 무시당하기 일쑤여서 그 짜증은 점점 더 부풀었다."], ["He had had intellectual encounters with Ivan before and he could not endure a certain carelessness Ivan showed him.", "그는 전에도 이반과 지적인 충돌을 겪은 적이 있었고, 이반이 자기에게 내보이는 어떤 무심함을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["“Hitherto at least I have stood in the front ranks of all that is progressive in Europe, and here the new generation positively ignores us,”", "\"적어도 지금까지 나는 유럽의 모든 진보적인 것들 가운데 맨 앞줄에 서 있었거늘, 여기 새 세대는 우리를 아예 거들떠보지도 않는구나.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 이렇게 생각했다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch, who had given his word to sit still and be quiet, had actually been quiet for some time, but he watched his neighbor Miüsov with an ironical little smile, obviously enjoying his discomfiture.", "가만히 앉아 입을 다물고 있겠노라 약속했던 표도르 파블로비치는 정말 한동안 잠잠히 있었으나, 옆자리의 미우소프를 조롱하는 듯한 엷은 웃음을 띠고 지켜보면서, 그의 낭패를 분명히 즐기고 있었다."], ["He had been waiting for some time to pay off old scores, and now he could not let the opportunity slip.", "그는 묵은 빚을 갚을 기회를 한참 기다려 왔던 터라, 이제 그 기회를 놓칠 수가 없었다."], ["Bending over his shoulder he began teasing him again in a whisper.", "그의 어깨 너머로 몸을 숙이며 그는 다시 속삭임으로 그를 놀려 대기 시작했다."]], [["“Why didn’t you go away just now, after the ‘courteously kissing’?", "\"아까 '공손히 입을 맞춘' 다음에 왜 떠나지 않았소?"], ["Why did you consent to remain in such unseemly company?", "어쩌자고 이런 꼴사나운 패거리 속에 남아 있기로 했소?"], ["It was because you felt insulted and aggrieved, and you remained to vindicate yourself by showing off your intelligence.", "그건 당신이 모욕을 당하고 속이 상해서, 자기 똑똑함을 뽐내어 명예를 회복해 보려고 남았기 때문이지."], ["Now you won’t go till you’ve displayed your intellect to them.”", "이제 당신은 저들에게 자기 지성을 펼쳐 보이기 전에는 떠나지 못할 거요.\""]], [["“You again?...", "\"또 당신이군요?……"], ["On the contrary, I’m just going.”", "천만에, 나는 지금 막 떠나려던 참이오.\""]], [["“You’ll be the last, the last of all to go!”", "\"당신은 마지막에, 누구보다도 맨 나중에 떠나게 될 거요!\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch delivered him another thrust, almost at the moment of Father Zossima’s return.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 또 한 번 찌르듯 말을 던졌으니, 그것은 거의 조시마 장로가 돌아오는 바로 그 순간이었다."]], [["The discussion died down for a moment, but the elder, seating himself in his former place, looked at them all as though cordially inviting them to go on.", "논의는 잠시 사그라들었으나, 장로는 본래 자리에 앉으며 좌중을 두루 둘러보았는데, 마치 그들에게 어서 이야기를 이어 가라고 다정하게 권하는 듯했다."], ["Alyosha, who knew every expression of his face, saw that he was fearfully exhausted and making a great effort.", "그의 얼굴 표정 하나하나를 다 알고 있던 알료샤는, 그가 끔찍이도 지쳐 있으면서 안간힘을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Of late he had been liable to fainting fits from exhaustion.", "근래에 그는 탈진으로 인한 졸도 발작을 일으키기 쉬운 상태였다."], ["His face had the pallor that was common before such attacks, and his lips were white.", "그의 얼굴에는 그런 발작이 닥치기 전에 흔히 나타나는 창백함이 어려 있었고, 입술은 하얗게 질려 있었다."], ["But he evidently did not want to break up the party.", "그러나 그는 분명 이 모임을 끝맺으려 하지 않았다."], ["He seemed to have some special object of his own in keeping them.", "그들을 붙들어 두는 데에 무슨 자기 나름의 특별한 목적이 있는 듯했다."], ["What object?", "무슨 목적일까?"], ["Alyosha watched him intently.", "알료샤는 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보았다."]], [["“We are discussing this gentleman’s most interesting article,”", "\"저희는 이 신사분의 무척 흥미로운 논문을 두고 이야기하던 참이었습니다.\""], ["said Father Iosif, the librarian, addressing the elder, and indicating Ivan.", "사서를 맡은 이오시프 신부가 장로에게 말하며 이반을 가리켰다."], ["“He brings forward much that is new, but I think the argument cuts both ways.", "\"여러 가지 새로운 점을 내놓고 계시지만, 제가 보기에는 그 논리가 양날의 칼인 듯합니다."], ["It is an article written in answer to a book by an ecclesiastical authority on the question of the ecclesiastical court, and the scope of its jurisdiction.”", "이것은 교회 재판소의 문제와 그 관할 범위에 관해 어느 교회 권위자가 쓴 책에 대한 반론으로 쓰인 논문입니다.\""]], [["“I’m sorry I have not read your article, but I’ve heard of it,”", "\"안타깝게도 그대의 논문을 읽지는 못했으나, 소문은 들었소.\""], ["said the elder, looking keenly and intently at Ivan.", "장로가 이반을 날카롭고 골똘하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“He takes up a most interesting position,”", "\"이분은 무척 흥미로운 입장을 취하고 계십니다.\""], ["continued the Father Librarian.", "사서 신부가 말을 이었다."], ["“As far as Church jurisdiction is concerned he is apparently quite opposed to the separation of Church from State.”", "\"교회의 관할권에 관한 한, 이분은 보아하니 교회와 국가의 분리에 전적으로 반대하시는 듯합니다.\""]], [["“That’s interesting.", "\"그것 참 흥미롭구려."], ["But in what sense?”", "하지만 어떤 의미에서 그렇소?\""], ["Father Zossima asked Ivan.", "조시마 장로가 이반에게 물었다."]], [["The latter, at last, answered him, not condescendingly, as Alyosha had feared, but with modesty and reserve, with evident goodwill and apparently without the slightest arrière-pensée.", "이반은 마침내 그에게 대답했는데, 알료샤가 우려했던 것처럼 거드름을 피우는 투가 아니라, 겸손하고 절제된 태도로, 분명한 호의를 담아, 보아하니 한 점의 속셈도 없이 답했다."]], [["“I start from the position that this confusion of elements, that is, of the essential principles of Church and State, will, of course, go on for ever, in spite of the fact that it is impossible for them to mingle, and that the confusion of these elements cannot lead to any consistent or even normal results, for there is falsity at the very foundation of it.", "\"저는 이러한 요소들의 혼동, 곧 교회와 국가의 본질적 원리들의 혼동이, 양자가 결코 뒤섞일 수 없음에도 불구하고 당연히 영원히 계속되리라는 입장에서 출발합니다. 그리고 이 요소들의 혼동은 일관되거나 정상적인 결과조차 결코 낳을 수 없으니, 그것은 바로 그 근저에 거짓이 깔려 있기 때문입니다."], ["Compromise between the Church and State in such questions as, for instance, jurisdiction, is, to my thinking, impossible in any real sense.", "가령 관할권 같은 문제에서 교회와 국가 사이의 타협이란, 제 생각으로는 어떤 진정한 의미로도 불가능합니다."], ["My clerical opponent maintains that the Church holds a precise and defined position in the State.", "저와 맞서는 성직자 측은 교회가 국가 안에서 정확하고 한정된 지위를 차지한다고 주장합니다."], ["I maintain, on the contrary, that the Church ought to include the whole State, and not simply to occupy a corner in it, and, if this is, for some reason, impossible at present, then it ought, in reality, to be set up as the direct and chief aim of the future development of Christian society!”", "저는 그와 반대로, 교회가 단지 국가 안의 한 귀퉁이를 차지할 것이 아니라 국가 전체를 포괄해야 한다고 주장합니다. 그리고 만일 이것이 무슨 이유로 지금 당장은 불가능하다면, 그것은 마땅히 그리스도교 사회의 미래 발전이 지향할 직접적이고 으뜸가는 목표로서 세워져야 마땅합니다!\""]], [["“Perfectly true,”", "\"지극히 옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["Father Païssy, the silent and learned monk, assented with fervor and decision.", "말수가 적고 학식이 깊은 수도사 파이시 신부가 열의와 단호함을 담아 동의했다."]], [["“The purest Ultramontanism!”", "\"순전한 교황지상주의로군!\""], ["cried Miüsov impatiently, crossing and recrossing his legs.", "미우소프가 참다못해 외치며, 다리를 꼬았다 다시 풀었다."]], [["“Oh, well, we have no mountains,” cried Father Iosif, and turning to the elder he continued: “Observe the answer he makes to the following ‘fundamental and essential’ propositions of his opponent, who is, you must note, an ecclesiastic. First, that ‘no social organization can or ought to arrogate to itself power to dispose of the civic and political rights of its members.’ Secondly, that ‘criminal and civil jurisdiction ought not to belong to the Church, and is inconsistent with its nature, both as a divine institution and as an organization of men for religious objects,’ and, finally, in the third place, ‘the Church is a kingdom not of this world.’”", "\"아니, 우리에게는 산이라는 게 없는걸요.\" 이오시프 신부가 외치더니, 장로를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. \"이분이 상대편의 다음과 같은 '근본적이고 본질적인' 명제들에, 유념하셔야 할 것은 그 상대편이 성직자라는 점입니다만, 어떤 답을 내놓는지 보십시오. 첫째, '어떠한 사회 조직도 그 구성원의 시민적·정치적 권리를 좌우할 권력을 스스로에게 참칭할 수 없으며 또 그래서도 안 된다'는 것. 둘째, '형사 및 민사 관할권은 교회에 속해서는 안 되며, 신적 제도로서나 종교적 목적을 위한 인간들의 조직으로서나 교회의 본성과 양립할 수 없다'는 것. 그리고 마지막으로 셋째, '교회는 이 세상에 속한 나라가 아니다'라는 것입니다.\""]], [["“A most unworthy play upon words for an ecclesiastic!”", "\"성직자로서는 더없이 못난 말장난이오!\""], ["Father Païssy could not refrain from breaking in again.", "파이시 신부가 또다시 끼어들지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“I have read the book which you have answered,”", "\"나는 그대가 반박한 그 책을 읽었소만,\" 그는 이반을 향해 덧붙였다."], ["he added, addressing Ivan, “and was astounded at the words ‘the Church is a kingdom not of this world.’ If it is not of this world, then it cannot exist on earth at all.", "\"'교회는 이 세상에 속한 나라가 아니다'라는 그 말에 경악을 금치 못했소. 만일 그것이 이 세상에 속한 것이 아니라면, 그렇다면 그것은 이 땅에 도무지 존재할 수가 없는 것이오."], ["In the Gospel, the words ‘not of this world’ are not used in that sense.", "복음서에서 '이 세상에 속하지 않은'이라는 말은 그런 뜻으로 쓰이지 않았소."], ["To play with such words is indefensible.", "그런 말을 가지고 농을 하는 것은 변명의 여지가 없는 일이오."], ["Our Lord Jesus Christ came to set up the Church upon earth.", "우리 주 예수 그리스도께서는 이 땅에 교회를 세우러 오셨소."], ["The Kingdom of Heaven, of course, is not of this world, but in Heaven; but it is only entered through the Church which has been founded and established upon earth.", "천국은 물론 이 세상에 속한 것이 아니라 하늘에 있는 것이나, 그곳에는 오로지 이 땅에 세워지고 견고히 다져진 교회를 통해서만 들어갈 수 있는 것이오."], ["And so a frivolous play upon words in such a connection is unpardonable and improper.", "그러니 그런 문제를 두고 경박하게 말장난을 하는 것은 용서할 수 없고 온당치 못한 짓이오."], ["The Church is, in truth, a kingdom and ordained to rule, and in the end must undoubtedly become the kingdom ruling over all the earth.", "교회는 진실로 하나의 나라이며 다스리도록 정해진 것이고, 마침내는 틀림없이 온 땅을 다스리는 나라가 되어야 하오."], ["For that we have the divine promise.”", "그것에 대해서는 우리에게 신의 약속이 있소.\""]], [["He ceased speaking suddenly, as though checking himself.", "그는 마치 스스로를 다잡듯 갑자기 말을 멈추었다."], ["After listening attentively and respectfully Ivan went on, addressing the elder with perfect composure and as before with ready cordiality:", "이반은 주의 깊고 공손하게 귀 기울인 뒤, 더없이 침착하게, 그리고 앞서와 마찬가지로 선뜻한 다정함을 담아 장로를 향해 말을 이었다."]], [["“The whole point of my article lies in the fact that during the first three centuries Christianity only existed on earth in the Church and was nothing but the Church.", "\"제 논문의 요점은 그리스도교가 처음 삼백 년 동안 이 땅에서 오로지 교회 안에서만 존재했고, 교회 그 자체에 지나지 않았다는 사실에 있습니다."], ["When the pagan Roman Empire desired to become Christian, it inevitably happened that, by becoming Christian, it included the Church but remained a pagan State in very many of its departments.", "이교도였던 로마 제국이 그리스도교 국가가 되고자 했을 때, 필연적으로 일어난 일은, 그것이 그리스도교 국가가 됨으로써 교회를 그 안에 포함하면서도 매우 많은 부문에서 여전히 이교 국가로 남았다는 것입니다."], ["In reality this was bound to happen.", "사실 이것은 일어날 수밖에 없는 일이었습니다."], ["But Rome as a State retained too much of the pagan civilization and culture, as, for example, in the very objects and fundamental principles of the State.", "그러나 국가로서의 로마는 가령 국가의 바로 그 목적과 근본 원리에 이르기까지 이교 문명과 문화를 너무도 많이 간직하고 있었습니다."], ["The Christian Church entering into the State could, of course, surrender no part of its fundamental principles—the rock on which it stands—and could pursue no other aims than those which have been ordained and revealed by God Himself, and among them that of drawing the whole world, and therefore the ancient pagan State itself, into the Church.", "그 국가 안으로 들어간 그리스도교 교회는, 물론 자신이 딛고 선 반석인 근본 원리의 어떤 부분도 내어줄 수 없었고, 신께서 친히 정하시고 계시하신 목표들 외에 다른 어떤 목표도 추구할 수 없었으니, 그 가운데 하나가 바로 온 세계를, 따라서 저 오랜 이교 국가 자체를 교회 안으로 끌어들이는 것이었습니다."], ["In that way (that is, with a view to the future) it is not the Church that should seek a definite position in the State, like ‘every social organization,’ or as ‘an organization of men for religious purposes’ (as my opponent calls the Church), but, on the contrary, every earthly State should be, in the end, completely transformed into the Church and should become nothing else but a Church, rejecting every purpose incongruous with the aims of the Church.", "그런 까닭에 (다시 말해 미래를 내다볼 때) 교회가 '모든 사회 조직'처럼, 혹은 (제 상대편이 교회를 일컫는 대로) '종교적 목적을 위한 인간들의 조직'처럼 국가 안에서 어떤 한정된 지위를 구해야 하는 것이 아니라, 오히려 그 반대로 이 땅의 모든 국가가 마침내 온전히 교회로 변모하여 교회 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어야 하며, 교회의 목적과 어긋나는 온갖 목적을 버려야 하는 것입니다."], ["All this will not degrade it in any way or take from its honor and glory as a great State, nor from the glory of its rulers, but only turns it from a false, still pagan, and mistaken path to the true and rightful path, which alone leads to the eternal goal.", "이 모든 일은 결코 국가의 품격을 떨어뜨리거나 위대한 국가로서의 명예와 영광을, 혹은 그 통치자들의 영광을 앗아 가는 것이 아니라, 다만 거짓되고 여전히 이교적이며 그릇된 길에서 참되고 마땅한 길로, 오직 영원한 목표로만 이끄는 그 길로 돌려세우는 것일 뿐입니다."], ["This is why the author of the book On the Foundations of Church Jurisdiction would have judged correctly if, in seeking and laying down those foundations, he had looked upon them as a temporary compromise inevitable in our sinful and imperfect days.", "바로 이런 까닭에 『교회 관할권의 토대에 관하여』라는 책의 저자가, 만일 그 토대를 찾고 정립함에 있어 그것을 우리의 죄 많고 불완전한 시대에 불가피한 일시적 타협으로 여겼더라면, 옳게 판단한 것이 되었을 것입니다."], ["But as soon as the author ventures to declare that the foundations which he predicates now, part of which Father Iosif just enumerated, are the permanent, essential, and eternal foundations, he is going directly against the Church and its sacred and eternal vocation.", "그러나 그 저자가 감히, 방금 이오시프 신부께서 일부 열거하신, 그가 지금 단언하는 그 토대들을 영구하고 본질적이며 영원한 토대라고 선언하는 순간, 그는 곧장 교회와 교회의 거룩하고 영원한 사명을 거스르는 셈이 됩니다."], ["That is the gist of my article.”", "이것이 제 논문의 골자입니다.\""]], [["“That is, in brief,”", "\"다시 말하면, 요컨대,\""], ["Father Païssy began again, laying stress on each word, “according to certain theories only too clearly formulated in the nineteenth century, the Church ought to be transformed into the State, as though this would be an advance from a lower to a higher form, so as to disappear into it, making way for science, for the spirit of the age, and civilization.", "파이시 신부가 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주며 다시 입을 열었다. \"십구 세기에 너무도 분명하게 정식화된 어떤 이론들에 따르면, 교회가 마치 낮은 형태에서 높은 형태로 나아가듯 국가로 변모하여 그 안으로 사라져 버려야 하며, 과학과 시대정신과 문명에 자리를 내주어야 한다는 것이오."], ["And if the Church resists and is unwilling, some corner will be set apart for her in the State, and even that under control—and this will be so everywhere in all modern European countries.", "그리고 만일 교회가 저항하며 응하지 않으면, 국가 안에 무슨 한 귀퉁이가 떼어져 그녀에게 주어지되, 그것조차 통제 아래 놓이게 되는 것이오. 그리고 이것이 오늘날 모든 유럽 국가에서 도처에 그러할 것이오."], ["But Russian hopes and conceptions demand not that the Church should pass as from a lower into a higher type into the State, but, on the contrary, that the State should end by being worthy to become only the Church and nothing else.", "그러나 러시아의 희망과 관념은, 교회가 낮은 유형에서 높은 유형으로 옮아가듯 국가로 넘어가야 한다는 것이 아니라, 그와 반대로 국가가 마침내 오로지 교회가 될 자격을 갖추고 그 외에 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어야 한다고 요구하는 것이오. 그렇게 될지어다!"], ["So be it! So be it!”", "그렇게 될지어다!\""]], [["“Well, I confess you’ve reassured me somewhat,”", "\"음, 솔직히 말해 당신은 내 마음을 어느 정도 놓이게 해 주었소.\""], ["Miüsov said smiling, again crossing his legs.", "미우소프가 다시 다리를 꼬며 미소를 띠고 말했다."], ["“So far as I understand, then, the realization of such an ideal is infinitely remote, at the second coming of Christ.", "\"그러니까 내가 이해한 바로는, 그러한 이상의 실현이란 한없이 먼 일이로군, 그리스도의 재림 때나 가능한 일 말이오."], ["That’s as you please.", "그것이야 당신들 좋을 대로 하시오."], ["It’s a beautiful Utopian dream of the abolition of war, diplomacy, banks, and so on—something after the fashion of socialism, indeed.", "전쟁과 외교와 은행 따위를 폐지하자는, 아름다운 유토피아의 꿈이로군요, 정말이지 사회주의 비슷한 어떤 것 말이오."], ["But I imagined that it was all meant seriously, and that the Church might be now going to try criminals, and sentence them to beating, prison, and even death.”", "그러나 나는 그것이 죄다 진지하게 한 말인 줄로, 그래서 교회가 지금 당장이라도 죄인들을 재판하여 태형이나 투옥, 심지어 사형까지 선고하려는 줄로 알았소그려.\""]], [["“But if there were none but the ecclesiastical court, the Church would not even now sentence a criminal to prison or to death.", "\"그러나 만일 교회 재판소만이 있다면, 교회는 지금이라도 죄인에게 투옥이나 사형을 선고하지 않을 것입니다."], ["Crime and the way of regarding it would inevitably change, not all at once of course, but fairly soon,”", "범죄와 그것을 보는 방식이 필연적으로 달라질 테니까요, 물론 한꺼번에는 아니겠으나 제법 머지않아 그리될 것입니다.\""], ["Ivan replied calmly, without flinching.", "이반이 조금도 움츠러들지 않고 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["“Are you serious?”", "\"진심으로 하는 말이오?\""], ["Miüsov glanced keenly at him.", "미우소프가 그를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“If everything became the Church, the Church would exclude all the criminal and disobedient, and would not cut off their heads,”", "\"만일 모든 것이 교회가 된다면, 교회는 모든 범죄자와 불순종하는 자를 배제하되 그들의 목을 베지는 않을 것입니다.\""], ["Ivan went on.", "이반이 말을 이었다."], ["“I ask you, what would become of the excluded?", "\"그렇다면 묻겠습니다만, 배제된 자는 어찌 되겠습니까? 그때 그는 지금처럼 인간들로부터만이 아니라 그리스도로부터도 끊겨 나가게 되는 것입니다."], ["He would be cut off then not only from men, as now, but from Christ.", "그는 자기 범죄로 인하여 인간들에게만이 아니라 그리스도의 교회에게도 죄를 범한 셈이 되니까요. 물론 엄밀히 말하면 지금도 그러하나, 그것이 분명하게 천명되어 있지 않아서, 오늘날의 범죄자는 아주아주 자주 제 양심과 타협하고 맙니다."], ["By his crime he would have transgressed not only against men but against the Church of Christ.", "'나는 도둑질을 한다,' 그는 이렇게 말하지요."], ["This is so even now, of course, strictly speaking, but it is not clearly enunciated, and very, very often the criminal of to-day compromises with his conscience: ‘I steal,’ he says, ‘but I don’t go against the Church.", "'그러나 나는 교회를 거스르지는 않는다. 나는 그리스도의 적이 아니다.'"], ["I’m not an enemy of Christ.’ That’s what the criminal of to-day is continually saying to himself, but when the Church takes the place of the State it will be difficult for him, in opposition to the Church all over the world, to say: ‘All men are mistaken, all in error, all mankind are the false Church.", "오늘날의 범죄자는 끊임없이 자기 자신에게 이렇게 되뇌는 것입니다. 그러나 교회가 국가를 대신하게 되면, 그가 온 세계의 교회에 맞서서 '모든 인간이 잘못 알고 있고, 모두가 오류에 빠져 있으며, 온 인류가 거짓 교회다."], ["I, a thief and murderer, am the only true Christian Church.’ It will be very difficult to say this to himself; it requires a rare combination of unusual circumstances.", "도둑이요 살인자인 나야말로 유일한 참된 그리스도의 교회다'라고 말하기란 어려울 것입니다. 자기 자신에게 그렇게 말하기란 무척 어려운 일이니, 거기에는 보기 드물게 기이한 상황들이 한데 겹쳐야 합니다."], ["Now, on the other side, take the Church’s own view of crime: is it not bound to renounce the present almost pagan attitude, and to change from a mechanical cutting off of its tainted member for the preservation of society, as at present, into completely and honestly adopting the idea of the regeneration of the man, of his reformation and salvation?”", "자, 이번에는 다른 쪽에서, 교회 자신이 범죄를 보는 관점을 들어 봅시다. 교회는 마땅히 지금의 거의 이교적인 태도를 버리고, 현재처럼 사회를 지키기 위해 오염된 지체를 기계적으로 잘라 내는 방식에서 벗어나, 인간의 갱생, 그의 교화와 구원이라는 사상을 온전하고 정직하게 받아들이는 쪽으로 변해야 하지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말씀이오?"], ["I fail to understand again,”", "또 못 알아듣겠소그려.\""], ["Miüsov interrupted.", "미우소프가 가로막았다."], ["“Some sort of dream again.", "\"또 무슨 꿈 같은 소리로군."], ["Something shapeless and even incomprehensible.", "형체도 없고 도무지 알 수도 없는 어떤 것 말이오."], ["What is excommunication?", "파문이란 게 대체 뭐요?"], ["What sort of exclusion?", "어떤 식의 배제란 말이오?"], ["I suspect you are simply amusing yourself, Ivan Fyodorovitch.”", "이반 표도로비치, 내 보기에 당신은 그저 혼자 재미 삼아 즐기고 있는 게 아닌가 싶소.\""]], [["“Yes, but you know, in reality it is so now,”", "\"그렇소, 하지만 실은 지금도 그러하다오.\""], ["said the elder suddenly, and all turned to him at once.", "장로가 불쑥 말하자 모두가 일제히 그에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“If it were not for the Church of Christ there would be nothing to restrain the criminal from evil-doing, no real chastisement for it afterwards; none, that is, but the mechanical punishment spoken of just now, which in the majority of cases only embitters the heart; and not the real punishment, the only effectual one, the only deterrent and softening one, which lies in the recognition of sin by conscience.”", "\"만일 그리스도의 교회가 없다면, 죄인이 악행을 저지르지 못하도록 막아 줄 것이 아무것도 없을 테고, 그 뒤에 그것에 대한 참된 징벌도 없을 것이오. 다시 말해 방금 이야기된 저 기계적인 형벌, 대다수의 경우 마음만 더 모질게 만들 뿐인 그 형벌 말고는 없을 것이며, 참된 형벌, 곧 유일하게 효력 있고 유일하게 억제하며 누그러뜨려 주는 그 형벌, 양심에 의한 죄의 자각 속에 깃든 그 형벌은 없을 것이오.\""]], [["“How is that, may one inquire?”", "\"어찌하여 그렇습니까, 여쭈어도 되겠는지요?\""], ["asked Miüsov, with lively curiosity.", "미우소프가 생생한 호기심을 띠며 물었다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"이를테면,\""], ["began the elder, “all these sentences to exile with hard labor, and formerly with flogging also, reform no one, and what’s more, deter hardly a single criminal, and the number of crimes does not diminish but is continually on the increase.", "장로가 말을 시작했다. \"이 모든 중노동 유형의 선고, 예전에는 거기에 태형까지 곁들였던 그 선고들이 아무도 교화하지 못하고, 더구나 죄인 한 사람조차 좀처럼 막지 못하며, 범죄의 수는 줄어들기는커녕 끊임없이 늘어만 가고 있소."], ["You must admit that.", "그대도 이 점은 인정해야 할 것이오."], ["Consequently the security of society is not preserved, for, although the obnoxious member is mechanically cut off and sent far away out of sight, another criminal always comes to take his place at once, and often two of them.", "따라서 사회의 안전은 지켜지지 않으니, 비록 그 해로운 지체가 기계적으로 잘려 나가 멀리 눈에 안 띄는 곳으로 보내진다 해도, 언제나 또 다른 죄인이 곧장 그 자리를 메우러 오고, 더러는 둘씩이나 오기 때문이오."], ["If anything does preserve society, even in our time, and does regenerate and transform the criminal, it is only the law of Christ speaking in his conscience.", "만일 무엇인가가, 우리 시대에라도, 사회를 지키고 또 죄인을 갱생시키고 변모시킨다면, 그것은 오로지 그의 양심 속에서 말하는 그리스도의 법뿐이오."], ["It is only by recognizing his wrong-doing as a son of a Christian society—that is, of the Church—that he recognizes his sin against society—that is, against the Church.", "그가 그리스도교 사회의, 곧 교회의 아들로서 자기 죄과를 자각함으로써만, 그는 사회에 대한, 곧 교회에 대한 자기 죄를 자각하는 것이오."], ["So that it is only against the Church, and not against the State, that the criminal of to-day can recognize that he has sinned.", "그러므로 오늘날의 죄인이 자기가 죄를 지었음을 자각할 수 있는 것은 오로지 교회에 대해서이지 국가에 대해서가 아니오."], ["If society, as a Church, had jurisdiction, then it would know when to bring back from exclusion and to reunite to itself.", "만일 사회가 교회로서 관할권을 지닌다면, 언제 배제로부터 다시 데려와 자신에게 합칠지를 알 것이오."], ["Now the Church having no real jurisdiction, but only the power of moral condemnation, withdraws of her own accord from punishing the criminal actively.", "지금은 교회가 실질적인 관할권은 없고 다만 도덕적 단죄의 힘만을 지닌 채, 죄인을 능동적으로 벌하는 일에서 스스로 물러나 있소."], ["She does not excommunicate him but simply persists in motherly exhortation of him.", "교회는 그를 파문하는 것이 아니라, 그저 어머니의 마음으로 그를 타이르기를 그치지 않을 따름이오."], ["What is more, the Church even tries to preserve all Christian communion with the criminal.", "더구나 교회는 죄인과의 온갖 그리스도교적 교류마저 지키려 애쓰오."], ["She admits him to church services, to the holy sacrament, gives him alms, and treats him more as a captive than as a convict.", "교회는 그를 예배에 받아들이고, 거룩한 성찬에 받아들이며, 그에게 자선을 베풀고, 그를 죄수라기보다 포로처럼 대하오."], ["And what would become of the criminal, O Lord, if even the Christian society—that is, the Church—were to reject him even as the civil law rejects him and cuts him off?", "오 주여, 만일 그리스도교 사회마저, 곧 교회마저 세속의 법이 그러하듯 그를 내치고 잘라 낸다면, 죄인은 대체 어찌 되겠소?"], ["What would become of him if the Church punished him with her excommunication as the direct consequence of the secular law?", "만일 교회가 세속법의 직접적 귀결로서 그를 파문으로 벌한다면, 그는 대체 어찌 되겠소?"], ["There could be no more terrible despair, at least for a Russian criminal, for Russian criminals still have faith.", "적어도 러시아의 죄인에게는 그보다 더 끔찍한 절망이 없을 것이니, 러시아의 죄인들은 아직 믿음을 지니고 있기 때문이오."], ["Though, who knows, perhaps then a fearful thing would happen, perhaps the despairing heart of the criminal would lose its faith and then what would become of him?", "하기야 누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 그때 끔찍한 일이 벌어져, 죄인의 절망에 빠진 마음이 그 믿음마저 잃어버릴는지도, 그러면 그는 또 어찌 되겠소?"], ["But the Church, like a tender, loving mother, holds aloof from active punishment herself, as the sinner is too severely punished already by the civil law, and there must be at least some one to have pity on him.", "그러나 교회는 자애롭고 사랑이 깊은 어머니처럼 능동적인 형벌에서 스스로 비켜서 있으니, 그 죄인은 이미 세속의 법으로 너무도 혹독하게 벌받고 있는 데다, 적어도 누군가 한 사람은 그를 가엾이 여겨야 하기 때문이오."], ["The Church holds aloof, above all, because its judgment is the only one that contains the truth, and therefore cannot practically and morally be united to any other judgment even as a temporary compromise.", "무엇보다도 교회가 비켜서 있는 까닭은, 교회의 판단만이 진리를 담고 있어, 따라서 실제로도 도덕적으로도 다른 어떤 판단과, 일시적 타협으로조차 결합될 수 없기 때문이오."], ["She can enter into no compact about that.", "교회는 그것에 관하여 어떠한 협정도 맺을 수 없소."], ["The foreign criminal, they say, rarely repents, for the very doctrines of to-day confirm him in the idea that his crime is not a crime, but only a reaction against an unjustly oppressive force.", "외국의 죄인은 좀처럼 뉘우치지 않는다고들 하는데, 오늘날의 바로 그 교리들이 그에게, 제 범죄는 범죄가 아니라 부당하게 억압하는 힘에 대한 반작용일 뿐이라는 생각을 굳혀 주기 때문이오."], ["Society cuts him off completely by a force that triumphs over him mechanically and (so at least they say of themselves in Europe) accompanies this exclusion with hatred, forgetfulness, and the most profound indifference as to the ultimate fate of the erring brother.", "사회는 기계적으로 그를 짓이기는 힘으로써 그를 완전히 잘라 내고, (적어도 유럽에서는 자기들 스스로 그렇게 말하거니와) 이 배제에 증오와 망각을, 그리고 길 잃은 형제의 궁극적 운명에 대한 더없이 깊은 무관심을 곁들이오."], ["In this way, it all takes place without the compassionate intervention of the Church, for in many cases there are no churches there at all, for though ecclesiastics and splendid church buildings remain, the churches themselves have long ago striven to pass from Church into State and to disappear in it completely.", "이런 식으로 모든 일이 교회의 자비로운 개입 없이 일어나니, 거기에는 많은 경우 교회라는 것이 아예 없기 때문이오. 비록 성직자들과 으리으리한 교회 건물은 남아 있을지언정, 교회 자체는 이미 오래전에 교회에서 국가로 옮아가 그 안으로 완전히 사라지려 애써 왔기 때문이오."], ["So it seems at least in Lutheran countries.", "적어도 루터교 나라들에서는 그러해 보이오."], ["As for Rome, it was proclaimed a State instead of a Church a thousand years ago.", "로마로 말하자면, 그것은 천 년 전에 이미 교회 대신 국가로 선포되었소."], ["And so the criminal is no longer conscious of being a member of the Church and sinks into despair.", "그리하여 죄인은 더 이상 자기가 교회의 일원임을 의식하지 못하고 절망에 빠지오."], ["If he returns to society, often it is with such hatred that society itself instinctively cuts him off.", "만일 그가 사회로 돌아가면, 흔히 그 증오가 어찌나 깊은지 사회 자체가 본능적으로 그를 잘라 내고 마오."], ["You can judge for yourself how it must end.", "그것이 어떻게 끝날지는 그대 스스로 판단할 수 있을 것이오."], ["In many cases it would seem to be the same with us, but the difference is that besides the established law courts we have the Church too, which always keeps up relations with the criminal as a dear and still precious son.", "많은 경우 우리에게도 사정은 마찬가지인 듯 보이나, 다른 점이 있다면, 우리에게는 확립된 법정 외에 교회 또한 있어서, 그것이 죄인을 늘 소중하고 여전히 귀한 아들로서 관계를 이어 간다는 것이오."], ["And besides that, there is still preserved, though only in thought, the judgment of the Church, which though no longer existing in practice is still living as a dream for the future, and is, no doubt, instinctively recognized by the criminal in his soul.", "그뿐 아니라, 비록 생각 속에서만일지언정 교회의 판단이 아직 보존되어 있으니, 그것이 실제로는 더 이상 존재하지 않더라도 미래를 향한 꿈으로서 여전히 살아 있고, 죄인 또한 그 영혼 속에서 본능적으로 그것을 알아본다는 데 의심의 여지가 없소."], ["What was said here just now is true too, that is, that if the jurisdiction of the Church were introduced in practice in its full force, that is, if the whole of the society were changed into the Church, not only the judgment of the Church would have influence on the reformation of the criminal such as it never has now, but possibly also the crimes themselves would be incredibly diminished.", "방금 여기서 한 말 또한 진실이오, 다시 말해 만일 교회의 관할권이 그 온전한 힘으로 실제로 도입된다면, 곧 사회 전체가 교회로 바뀐다면, 교회의 판단이 지금으로서는 결코 미치지 못하는 식으로 죄인의 교화에 영향을 미칠 뿐 아니라, 어쩌면 범죄 자체도 믿기 어려울 만큼 줄어들 것이오."], ["And there can be no doubt that the Church would look upon the criminal and the crime of the future in many cases quite differently and would succeed in restoring the excluded, in restraining those who plan evil, and in regenerating the fallen.", "그리고 교회가 미래의 죄인과 범죄를 많은 경우 사뭇 다르게 바라보고, 배제된 자를 다시 받아들이고, 악을 꾀하는 자를 억제하며, 타락한 자를 갱생시키는 데 성공하리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없소."], ["It is true,”", "사실,\""], ["said Father Zossima, with a smile, “the Christian society now is not ready and is only resting on some seven righteous men, but as they are never lacking, it will continue still unshaken in expectation of its complete transformation from a society almost heathen in character into a single universal and all-powerful Church.", "조시마 장로가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"지금의 그리스도교 사회는 아직 준비가 되어 있지 않고, 다만 일곱 명쯤의 의인들에게 의지하여 버티고 있을 따름이나, 그들이 결코 끊이지 않는 만큼, 거의 이교적 성격을 띤 사회에서 하나의 보편적이고 전능한 교회로 온전히 변모하기를 기다리며 여전히 흔들림 없이 이어질 것이오."], ["So be it, so be it!", "그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다!"], ["Even though at the end of the ages, for it is ordained to come to pass!", "비록 세상 끝에 가서라도 말이오, 그것은 반드시 이루어지도록 정해진 일이니!"], ["And there is no need to be troubled about times and seasons, for the secret of the times and seasons is in the wisdom of God, in His foresight, and His love.", "그리고 그 시기와 때를 두고 마음 졸일 필요는 없으니, 시기와 때의 비밀은 신의 지혜 안에, 그 예지 안에, 그 사랑 안에 있기 때문이오."], ["And what in human reckoning seems still afar off, may by the Divine ordinance be close at hand, on the eve of its appearance.", "인간의 셈으로는 아직 까마득해 보이는 것이, 신의 섭리로는 바로 코앞에, 그 출현의 전야에 와 있을 수도 있는 것이오."], ["And so be it, so be it!”", "그러니 그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다!\""]], [["“So be it, so be it!”", "\"그렇게 될지어다, 그렇게 될지어다!\""], ["Father Païssy repeated austerely and reverently.", "파이시 신부가 엄숙하고 경건하게 되뇌었다."]], [["“Strange, extremely strange!”", "\"이상하군요, 참으로 이상하오!\""], ["Miüsov pronounced, not so much with heat as with latent indignation.", "미우소프가, 격분이라기보다 억눌린 분노를 담아 내뱉었다."]], [["“What strikes you as so strange?”", "\"무엇이 그리도 이상하게 여겨지십니까?\""], ["Father Iosif inquired cautiously.", "이오시프 신부가 조심스럽게 물었다."]], [["“Why, it’s beyond anything!”", "\"아니, 이건 도무지 말도 안 되는 소리요!\""], ["cried Miüsov, suddenly breaking out; “the State is eliminated and the Church is raised to the position of the State.", "미우소프가 불쑥 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"국가가 없어지고 교회가 국가의 자리로 올라선다니."], ["It’s not simply Ultramontanism, it’s arch-Ultramontanism!", "이건 단순한 교황지상주의가 아니라, 극도의 교황지상주의요!"], ["It’s beyond the dreams of Pope Gregory the Seventh!”", "교황 그레고리우스 칠세의 꿈마저 넘어서는 것이오!\""]], [["“You are completely misunderstanding it,”", "\"당신은 그것을 완전히 잘못 이해하고 있소.\""], ["said Father Païssy sternly.", "파이시 신부가 준엄하게 말했다."], ["“Understand, the Church is not to be transformed into the State.", "\"잘 들으시오, 교회가 국가로 변모하는 것이 아니오."], ["That is Rome and its dream.", "그것은 로마이며 로마의 꿈이오."], ["That is the third temptation of the devil.", "그것은 악마의 세 번째 유혹이오."], ["On the contrary, the State is transformed into the Church, will ascend and become a Church over the whole world—which is the complete opposite of Ultramontanism and Rome, and your interpretation, and is only the glorious destiny ordained for the Orthodox Church.", "그와 반대로, 국가가 교회로 변모하여, 위로 올라 온 세계를 아우르는 교회가 되는 것이니, 이는 교황지상주의나 로마와는, 또 당신의 해석과는 정반대되는 것이며, 오직 정교회를 위해 정해진 영광스러운 운명일 따름이오."], ["This star will arise in the east!”", "이 별은 동방에서 떠오를 것이오!\""]], [["Miüsov was significantly silent.", "미우소프는 의미심장하게 침묵했다."], ["His whole figure expressed extraordinary personal dignity.", "그의 온 자태가 비범한 자존감을 드러냈다."], ["A supercilious and condescending smile played on his lips.", "그의 입가에는 거만하고 내려다보는 듯한 미소가 감돌았다."], ["Alyosha watched it all with a throbbing heart.", "알료샤는 가슴을 두근거리며 이 모든 것을 지켜보았다."], ["The whole conversation stirred him profoundly.", "그 대화 전체가 그의 마음을 깊이 뒤흔들었다."], ["He glanced casually at Rakitin, who was standing immovable in his place by the door listening and watching intently though with downcast eyes.", "그는 무심코 라키틴 쪽을 흘끗 보았는데, 그는 문 옆 제자리에 꼼짝도 않고 선 채, 눈은 내리뜨고 있었으나 골똘히 귀 기울이며 지켜보고 있었다."], ["But from the color in his cheeks Alyosha guessed that Rakitin was probably no less excited, and he knew what caused his excitement.", "그러나 그 두 뺨의 홍조로 미루어, 알료샤는 라키틴 역시 못지않게 흥분해 있으리라 짐작했고, 그 흥분의 까닭이 무엇인지도 알고 있었다."]], [["“Allow me to tell you one little anecdote, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분, 한 가지 자그마한 일화를 들려드리는 것을 허락해 주시지요.\""], ["Miüsov said impressively, with a peculiarly majestic air.", "미우소프가 유난히 위엄 있는 태도로, 무게를 잡으며 말했다."], ["“Some years ago, soon after the coup d’état of December, I happened to be calling in Paris on an extremely influential personage in the Government, and I met a very interesting man in his house.", "\"몇 해 전, 십이월의 그 쿠데타가 있은 직후에, 나는 마침 파리에서 정부의 지극히 영향력 있는 한 인사를 방문하던 차에, 그의 집에서 무척 흥미로운 사람을 만난 적이 있소."], ["This individual was not precisely a detective but was a sort of superintendent of a whole regiment of political detectives—a rather powerful position in its own way.", "이 사람은 딱히 형사라기보다는, 정치 담당 형사들로 이루어진 한 부대 전체를 감독하는 일종의 책임자였는데, 그 나름으로 제법 막강한 자리였소."], ["I was prompted by curiosity to seize the opportunity of conversation with him.", "호기심에 끌린 나는 그와 대화할 기회를 놓치지 않으려 했소."], ["And as he had not come as a visitor but as a subordinate official bringing a special report, and as he saw the reception given me by his chief, he deigned to speak with some openness, to a certain extent only, of course.", "그가 손님으로서가 아니라 특별 보고서를 가지고 온 하급 관리로서 와 있었던 데다, 그 상관이 나를 어떻게 맞이하는지를 보았던 까닭에, 그는 어느 정도 마음을 터놓고 이야기해 주는 영광을 베풀었소, 물론 어느 선까지만 말이오."], ["He was rather courteous than open, as Frenchmen know how to be courteous, especially to a foreigner.", "그는 솔직하다기보다는 정중한 편이었으니, 프랑스인들이, 특히 외국인에게 어떻게 정중할 줄 아는지는 다들 아는 바요."], ["But I thoroughly understood him.", "하지만 나는 그를 속속들이 이해했소."], ["The subject was the socialist revolutionaries who were at that time persecuted.", "화제는 그 무렵 박해받고 있던 사회주의 혁명가들이었소."], ["I will quote only one most curious remark dropped by this person.", "이 사람이 무심코 흘린 더없이 기묘한 말 한 마디만 인용하겠소."], ["‘We are not particularly afraid,’ said he, ‘of all these socialists, anarchists, infidels, and revolutionists; we keep watch on them and know all their goings on.", "'우리는 이 모든 사회주의자, 무정부주의자, 무신론자, 혁명가들을 그리 두려워하지 않습니다.' 그가 말했소."], ["But there are a few peculiar men among them who believe in God and are Christians, but at the same time are socialists.", "'우리는 그들을 감시하고 그들의 동향을 죄다 알고 있으니까요. 그러나 그들 중에는 신을 믿고 그리스도교도이면서 동시에 사회주의자인 몇몇 기이한 자들이 있습니다."], ["These are the people we are most afraid of.", "바로 이런 자들을 우리는 가장 두려워합니다."], ["They are dreadful people!", "그들은 무서운 사람들이지요!"], ["The socialist who is a Christian is more to be dreaded than a socialist who is an atheist.’ The words struck me at the time, and now they have suddenly come back to me here, gentlemen.”", "그리스도교도인 사회주의자는 무신론자인 사회주의자보다 더 두려운 법입니다.' 그 말은 당시 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼는데, 여러분, 지금 여기서 그 말이 불현듯 다시 떠오르는구려.\""]], [["“You apply them to us, and look upon us as socialists?”", "\"그 말을 우리에게 빗대시는군요, 그래서 우리를 사회주의자로 보시는 게요?\""], ["Father Païssy asked directly, without beating about the bush.", "파이시 신부가 에둘러 가지 않고 곧장 물었다."]], [["But before Pyotr Alexandrovitch could think what to answer, the door opened, and the guest so long expected, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, came in.", "그러나 표트르 알렉산드로비치가 무어라 답할지 미처 생각하기도 전에, 문이 열리며 그토록 오래 기다리던 손님 드미트리 표도로비치가 들어섰다."], ["They had, in fact, given up expecting him, and his sudden appearance caused some surprise for a moment.", "사실 그들은 그가 오기를 단념하고 있던 터라, 그 갑작스러운 등장은 한순간 적잖은 놀라움을 자아냈다."]], [["# Chapter VI. Why Is Such A Man Alive?", "# Chapter VI. Why Is Such A Man Alive?"]], [["Dmitri Fyodorovitch, a young man of eight and twenty, of medium height and agreeable countenance, looked older than his years.", "드미트리 표도로비치는 스물여덟 살의 청년으로, 중키에 호감 가는 용모를 지녔으나 나이보다 늙어 보였다."], ["He was muscular, and showed signs of considerable physical strength.", "근육질의 체격에 상당한 완력을 지닌 듯한 흔적이 엿보였다."], ["Yet there was something not healthy in his face.", "그런데도 그의 얼굴에는 어딘가 건강하지 못한 구석이 있었다."], ["It was rather thin, his cheeks were hollow, and there was an unhealthy sallowness in their color.", "얼굴은 다소 야위었고, 두 뺨은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 거기에는 건강하지 못한 누르께한 빛이 감돌았다."], ["His rather large, prominent, dark eyes had an expression of firm determination, and yet there was a vague look in them, too.", "다소 크고 불거진 검은 눈동자에는 굳은 결의의 표정이 서려 있으면서도, 동시에 어딘가 막연한 기색도 깃들어 있었다."], ["Even when he was excited and talking irritably, his eyes somehow did not follow his mood, but betrayed something else, sometimes quite incongruous with what was passing.", "그가 흥분해서 짜증스럽게 떠들 때조차도 그의 두 눈은 어쩐 일인지 그의 기분을 그대로 따라가지 않고 무언가 다른 것을, 때로는 그 순간에 벌어지는 일과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 무언가를 드러내곤 했다."], ["“It’s hard to tell what he’s thinking,”", "\"저 사람이 무슨 생각을 하는지 도무지 종잡을 수가 없어.\""], ["those who talked to him sometimes declared.", "그와 이야기를 나눠 본 사람들이 더러 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["People who saw something pensive and sullen in his eyes were startled by his sudden laugh, which bore witness to mirthful and light-hearted thoughts at the very time when his eyes were so gloomy.", "그의 눈에서 무언가 우수에 잠긴, 침울한 기색을 읽어 낸 사람들은 그의 느닷없는 웃음에 흠칫 놀라곤 했는데, 눈은 그토록 음울한 바로 그 순간에 그 웃음은 즐겁고 경쾌한 생각이 일고 있음을 증언했기 때문이다."], ["A certain strained look in his face was easy to understand at this moment.", "이 순간 그의 얼굴에 어린 어떤 긴장된 빛은 이해하기 어렵지 않았다."], ["Every one knew, or had heard of, the extremely restless and dissipated life which he had been leading of late, as well as of the violent anger to which he had been roused in his quarrels with his father.", "그가 근래 보내 온 더없이 들떠 있고 방탕한 생활에 대해서는, 또 아버지와의 다툼에서 그가 일으킨 격렬한 분노에 대해서는 누구나 알고 있거나 들어 본 적이 있었다."], ["There were several stories current in the town about it.", "그 일을 두고 고을에는 여러 가지 이야기가 떠돌았다."], ["It is true that he was irascible by nature, “of an unstable and unbalanced mind,” as our justice of the peace, Katchalnikov, happily described him.", "사실 그는 천성이 성마른 사람으로, 우리 고을의 치안판사 카찰니코프가 그를 두고 적절하게 표현했듯이 \"불안정하고 균형이 잡히지 않은 정신\"의 소유자였다."]], [["He was stylishly and irreproachably dressed in a carefully buttoned frock-coat.", "그는 정성껏 단추를 채운 프록코트 차림에 멋스럽고 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입었다."], ["He wore black gloves and carried a top-hat.", "검은 장갑을 끼고 실크해트를 손에 들고 있었다."], ["Having only lately left the army, he still had mustaches and no beard.", "군대를 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그는 아직 콧수염만 기르고 턱수염은 없었다."], ["His dark brown hair was cropped short, and combed forward on his temples.", "짙은 갈색 머리는 짧게 깎아 관자놀이 쪽으로 앞으로 빗어 넘겼다."], ["He had the long, determined stride of a military man.", "그는 군인 특유의 길고 단호한 보폭으로 걸었다."], ["He stood still for a moment on the threshold, and glancing at the whole party went straight up to the elder, guessing him to be their host.", "그는 문턱에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 좌중을 한번 훑어보더니 곧장 장로에게로 다가갔는데, 그를 이 자리의 주인이라고 짐작했던 것이다."], ["He made him a low bow, and asked his blessing.", "그는 장로에게 깊이 절하고 축복을 청했다."], ["Father Zossima, rising in his chair, blessed him.", "조시마 장로가 의자에서 일어나 그를 축복했다."], ["Dmitri kissed his hand respectfully, and with intense feeling, almost anger, he said:", "드미트리는 공손하게 그의 손에 입을 맞추고, 격한 감정으로, 거의 노여움에 가까운 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Be so generous as to forgive me for having kept you waiting so long, but Smerdyakov, the valet sent me by my father, in reply to my inquiries, told me twice over that the appointment was for one.", "\"이토록 오래 기다리시게 한 저를 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 그러나 아버지께서 제게 보내신 하인 스메르자코프가 제 물음에 거듭 약속 시간이 한 시라고 일러 주었습니다."], ["Now I suddenly learn—”", "그런데 이제 와서 갑자기 알고 보니—\""]], [["“Don’t disturb yourself,”", "\"마음 쓰지 마시오.\""], ["interposed the elder.", "장로가 말을 가로막았다."], ["“No matter.", "\"괜찮소."], ["You are a little late.", "조금 늦은 것뿐이오."], ["It’s of no consequence....”", "대수로운 일이 아니오…….\""]], [["“I’m extremely obliged to you, and expected no less from your goodness.”", "\"참으로 감사합니다. 어른의 너그러우심에 그 이상은 바라지도 않았습니다.\""]], [["Saying this, Dmitri bowed once more.", "이렇게 말하며 드미트리는 한 번 더 절했다."], ["Then, turning suddenly towards his father, made him, too, a similarly low and respectful bow.", "그러더니 갑자기 아버지 쪽으로 돌아서서 아버지에게도 똑같이 깊고 공손한 절을 올렸다."], ["He had evidently considered it beforehand, and made this bow in all seriousness, thinking it his duty to show his respect and good intentions.", "그는 분명 미리 마음먹어 둔 듯, 자신의 존경과 선의를 보이는 것이 도리라 여기며 이 절을 자못 진지하게 올렸다."]], [["Although Fyodor Pavlovitch was taken unawares, he was equal to the occasion.", "표도르 파블로비치는 불시에 허를 찔렸으나 그 자리에서 능히 대처했다."], ["In response to Dmitri’s bow he jumped up from his chair and made his son a bow as low in return.", "드미트리의 절에 화답하여 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 아들에게 그에 못지않게 깊은 절을 돌려주었다."], ["His face was suddenly solemn and impressive, which gave him a positively malignant look.", "그의 얼굴은 갑자기 엄숙하고 그럴듯해졌는데, 그 때문에 도리어 음흉하기 짝이 없는 표정이 되고 말았다."], ["Dmitri bowed generally to all present, and without a word walked to the window with his long, resolute stride, sat down on the only empty chair, near Father Païssy, and, bending forward, prepared to listen to the conversation he had interrupted.", "드미트리는 자리에 있는 모든 사람에게 두루 인사한 뒤 한마디 말도 없이 그 길고 단호한 보폭으로 창가로 걸어가, 파이시 신부 곁의 단 하나뿐인 빈 의자에 앉더니, 몸을 앞으로 숙여 자기가 끊어 놓은 대화를 들을 채비를 했다."]], [["Dmitri’s entrance had taken no more than two minutes, and the conversation was resumed.", "드미트리가 들어선 일은 채 이 분도 걸리지 않았고, 대화는 다시 이어졌다."], ["But this time Miüsov thought it unnecessary to reply to Father Païssy’s persistent and almost irritable question.", "그러나 이번에는 미우소프가 파이시 신부의 집요하고도 거의 짜증스럽기까지 한 물음에 굳이 대답할 필요를 느끼지 못했다."]], [["“Allow me to withdraw from this discussion,”", "\"이 논의에서는 저를 좀 빼 주십시오.\""], ["he observed with a certain well-bred nonchalance.", "그가 점잖은 무관심을 곁들여 말했다."], ["“It’s a subtle question, too.", "\"게다가 까다로운 문제이기도 하고요."], ["Here Ivan Fyodorovitch is smiling at us.", "보아하니 이반 표도로비치가 우리를 보고 미소를 짓고 있군요."], ["He must have something interesting to say about that also.", "저 사람도 거기에 대해 무언가 흥미로운 할 말이 있을 겁니다."], ["Ask him.”", "저 사람에게 물어보시지요.\""]], [["“Nothing special, except one little remark,”", "\"별다른 건 없습니다. 한 가지 자그마한 말씀만 드리지요.\""], ["Ivan replied at once.", "이반이 즉시 대답했다."], ["“European Liberals in general, and even our liberal dilettanti, often mix up the final results of socialism with those of Christianity.", "\"유럽의 자유주의자들 전반은, 심지어 우리네 자유주의 애호가들조차도, 사회주의의 궁극적인 결과를 그리스도교의 결과와 곧잘 뒤섞곤 합니다."], ["This wild notion is, of course, a characteristic feature.", "이런 터무니없는 생각은 물론 그 무리의 특징적인 면모지요."], ["But it’s not only Liberals and dilettanti who mix up socialism and Christianity, but, in many cases, it appears, the police—the foreign police, of course—do the same.", "하지만 사회주의와 그리스도교를 뒤섞는 것은 자유주의자나 애호가들뿐만이 아니라, 많은 경우 경찰까지도—물론 외국 경찰 말입니다—똑같이 그러는 모양입니다."], ["Your Paris anecdote is rather to the point, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.”", "당신의 파리 일화는 제법 들어맞는 이야기로군요, 표트르 알렉산드로비치.\""]], [["“I ask your permission to drop this subject altogether,”", "\"이 주제는 아예 접도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.\""], ["Miüsov repeated.", "미우소프가 거듭 말했다."], ["“I will tell you instead, gentlemen, another interesting and rather characteristic anecdote of Ivan Fyodorovitch himself.", "\"그 대신, 여러분, 이반 표도로비치 본인에 관한 또 다른 흥미롭고 자못 특징적인 일화를 들려드리지요."], ["Only five days ago, in a gathering here, principally of ladies, he solemnly declared in argument that there was nothing in the whole world to make men love their neighbors.", "닷새 전, 여기서 주로 부인네들이 모인 한 자리에서, 이 사람은 논쟁 중에 이 세상 어디에도 사람으로 하여금 이웃을 사랑하게 만들 만한 것이 없다고 자못 엄숙하게 선언했습니다."], ["That there was no law of nature that man should love mankind, and that, if there had been any love on earth hitherto, it was not owing to a natural law, but simply because men have believed in immortality.", "사람이 인류를 사랑해야 한다는 자연의 법칙 따위는 없으며, 만일 지금까지 이 땅에 사랑이라는 것이 있었다면 그것은 자연의 법칙 때문이 아니라 단지 사람들이 영혼 불멸을 믿어 왔기 때문이라는 거지요."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch added in parenthesis that the whole natural law lies in that faith, and that if you were to destroy in mankind the belief in immortality, not only love but every living force maintaining the life of the world would at once be dried up.", "이반 표도로비치는 덧붙여 모든 자연의 법칙이 바로 그 믿음 안에 있으며, 만일 인류에게서 불멸의 믿음을 없애 버린다면 사랑뿐만 아니라 세계의 생명을 지탱하는 온갖 살아 있는 힘이 단숨에 말라 버릴 것이라고 했습니다. 그뿐만이 아닙니다."], ["Moreover, nothing then would be immoral, everything would be lawful, even cannibalism.", "그렇게 되면 그 무엇도 부도덕할 것이 없으며, 모든 것이, 식인 행위마저도 허용될 것이라고 했습니다."], ["That’s not all.", "그게 다가 아닙니다."], ["He ended by asserting that for every individual, like ourselves, who does not believe in God or immortality, the moral law of nature must immediately be changed into the exact contrary of the former religious law, and that egoism, even to crime, must become not only lawful but even recognized as the inevitable, the most rational, even honorable outcome of his position.", "그는 마지막에 가서, 신도 불멸도 믿지 않는 우리 같은 사람 하나하나에게는 도덕적 자연 법칙이 곧장 종전의 종교적 법칙과 정반대의 것으로 바뀔 수밖에 없으며, 이기주의는, 심지어 범죄에 이르는 이기주의마저도, 그저 허용되는 것에 그치지 않고 오히려 그의 처지에서 비롯되는 불가피하고 가장 합리적이며 심지어 떳떳하기까지 한 귀결로 인정될 수밖에 없다고 단언했습니다."], ["From this paradox, gentlemen, you can judge of the rest of our eccentric and paradoxical friend Ivan Fyodorovitch’s theories.”", "이 역설만 보아도, 여러분, 우리의 그 별나고 역설적인 친구 이반 표도로비치의 나머지 이론들도 능히 짐작하실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["Dmitri cried suddenly; “if I’ve heard aright, crime must not only be permitted but even recognized as the inevitable and the most rational outcome of his position for every infidel!", "드미트리가 갑자기 소리쳤다. \"제가 제대로 들은 것이라면, 범죄는 비단 허용될 뿐만 아니라 신을 믿지 않는 모든 자에게 그 처지에서 비롯되는 불가피하고 가장 합리적인 귀결로 인정되어야 한다는 거로군요!"], ["Is that so or not?”", "그렇습니까, 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["said Father Païssy.", "파이시 신부가 말했다."]], [["“I’ll remember it.”", "\"명심해 두지요.\""]], [["Having uttered these words Dmitri ceased speaking as suddenly as he had begun.", "이 말을 내뱉고 나서 드미트리는 시작할 때처럼 갑작스레 입을 다물었다."], ["Every one looked at him with curiosity.", "모든 사람이 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Is that really your conviction as to the consequences of the disappearance of the faith in immortality?”", "\"불멸의 믿음이 사라진 결과에 대한 그것이 정말로 그대의 확신이오?\""], ["the elder asked Ivan suddenly.", "장로가 갑자기 이반에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes. That was my contention.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["There is no virtue if there is no immortality.”", "그것이 제 주장이었습니다. 불멸이 없다면 미덕도 없습니다.\""]], [["“You are blessed in believing that, or else most unhappy.”", "\"그렇게 믿는다면 그대는 복된 사람이거나, 아니면 더없이 불행한 사람이오.\""]], [["“Why unhappy?”", "\"어째서 불행하다는 겁니까?\""], ["Ivan asked smiling.", "이반이 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["“Because, in all probability you don’t believe yourself in the immortality of your soul, nor in what you have written yourself in your article on Church jurisdiction.”", "\"왜냐하면 십중팔구 그대는 자기 영혼의 불멸도, 교회 재판권에 관해 그대 스스로 쓴 논문의 내용조차도 정작 믿지 않고 있기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Perhaps you are right!", "\"어쩌면 옳은 말씀일지도 모르지요!"], ["...", "……"], ["But I wasn’t altogether joking,”", "하지만 제가 아주 농담만 한 것은 아니었습니다.\""], ["Ivan suddenly and strangely confessed, flushing quickly.", "이반이 갑자기 묘한 어조로 고백하며 금세 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“You were not altogether joking.", "\"아주 농담만 한 것은 아니었지."], ["That’s true.", "그건 사실이오."], ["The question is still fretting your heart, and not answered.", "그 문제는 아직도 그대의 마음을 들쑤시고 있으며, 답을 얻지 못한 채로 있소."], ["But the martyr likes sometimes to divert himself with his despair, as it were driven to it by despair itself.", "그러나 순교자란 때때로 제 절망을 가지고 스스로를 달래기를 좋아하는 법이니, 마치 절망 그 자체에 떠밀려 그러는 것과도 같지."], ["Meanwhile, in your despair, you, too, divert yourself with magazine articles, and discussions in society, though you don’t believe your own arguments, and with an aching heart mock at them inwardly....", "그러는 사이 그대 역시 절망 속에서 잡지 기고문이며 사교계의 논쟁을 가지고 스스로를 달래고 있는데, 정작 자기 자신의 논리는 믿지 않으면서, 쓰라린 마음으로 속으로 그것을 비웃고 있소……."], ["That question you have not answered, and it is your great grief, for it clamors for an answer.”", "그 문제에 그대는 답하지 못했고, 그것이 그대의 크나큰 슬픔이오. 그 문제가 답을 외쳐 부르고 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“But can it be answered by me?", "\"그러나 그것에 제가 답할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Answered in the affirmative?”", "긍정으로 답할 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["Ivan went on asking strangely, still looking at the elder with the same inexplicable smile.", "이반은 여전히 그 알 수 없는 미소를 띤 채 장로를 바라보며 묘한 어조로 계속 물었다."]], [["“If it can’t be decided in the affirmative, it will never be decided in the negative.", "\"그것이 긍정으로 결판날 수 없다면, 부정으로도 결코 결판나지 않을 것이오."], ["You know that that is the peculiarity of your heart, and all its suffering is due to it.", "그대는 그것이 그대 마음의 특이한 점이며 그 마음의 온갖 고뇌가 거기서 비롯됨을 알고 있을 거요."], ["But thank the Creator who has given you a lofty heart capable of such suffering; of thinking and seeking higher things, for our dwelling is in the heavens.", "그러나 그토록 고뇌할 수 있는, 더 높은 것을 사색하고 추구할 수 있는 드높은 마음을 그대에게 주신 창조주께 감사드리시오. 우리의 거처는 하늘에 있으니 말이오."], ["God grant that your heart will attain the answer on earth, and may God bless your path.”", "부디 그대의 마음이 이 땅에서 답에 다다르기를, 또 신께서 그대의 길을 축복하시기를 비오.\""]], [["The elder raised his hand and would have made the sign of the cross over Ivan from where he stood.", "장로는 손을 들어 서 있던 자리에서 이반에게 십자가 성호를 그어 주려 했다."], ["But the latter rose from his seat, went up to him, received his blessing, and kissing his hand went back to his place in silence.", "그러나 이반은 자리에서 일어나 그에게 다가가 축복을 받고, 그 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 말없이 제자리로 돌아갔다."], ["His face looked firm and earnest.", "그의 얼굴은 굳고 진지해 보였다."], ["This action and all the preceding conversation, which was so surprising from Ivan, impressed every one by its strangeness and a certain solemnity, so that all were silent for a moment, and there was a look almost of apprehension in Alyosha’s face.", "이반답지 않은 이 행동과 그에 앞선 온갖 대화가 그 기이함과 어떤 엄숙함으로 모든 사람에게 깊은 인상을 남겨, 한순간 다들 입을 다물었고, 알료샤의 얼굴에는 거의 근심에 찬 빛이 떠올랐다."], ["But Miüsov suddenly shrugged his shoulders.", "그러나 미우소프는 느닷없이 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["And at the same moment Fyodor Pavlovitch jumped up from his seat.", "그리고 바로 그 순간 표도르 파블로비치가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Most pious and holy elder,”", "\"더없이 경건하고 거룩하신 장로님.\""], ["he cried, pointing to Ivan, “that is my son, flesh of my flesh, the dearest of my flesh!", "그가 이반을 가리키며 소리쳤다. \"저것이 제 아들, 제 살 중의 살이요, 제 살붙이 중에서도 가장 사랑스러운 놈입니다!"], ["He is my most dutiful Karl Moor, so to speak, while this son who has just come in, Dmitri, against whom I am seeking justice from you, is the undutiful Franz Moor—they are both out of Schiller’s Robbers, and so I am the reigning Count von Moor!", "말하자면 저놈은 제게 더없이 효성스러운 카를 모어요, 방금 들어온 저 아들 드미트리, 제가 장로님께 정의를 구하고자 하는 저 불효한 놈은 프란츠 모어인 셈이지요—둘 다 실러의 「군도」에 나오는 인물들이니, 그렇다면 저는 군림하는 모어 백작이 되는 셈입니다!"], ["Judge and save us!", "저희를 심판하시고 구원해 주십시오!"], ["We need not only your prayers but your prophecies!”", "저희에게는 장로님의 기도뿐만 아니라 예언도 필요합니다!\""]], [["“Speak without buffoonery, and don’t begin by insulting the members of your family,”", "\"광대짓은 그만두시오. 또한 댁의 가족부터 모욕하며 시작하지 마시오.\""], ["answered the elder, in a faint, exhausted voice.", "장로가 가냘프고 지친 목소리로 대답했다."], ["He was obviously getting more and more fatigued, and his strength was failing.", "그는 보아하니 점점 더 피로해지고 있었고, 기력이 다해 가고 있었다."]], [["“An unseemly farce which I foresaw when I came here!”", "\"제가 여기 올 때 이미 내다보았던 꼴사나운 광대극이로구나!\""], ["cried Dmitri indignantly.", "드미트리가 분개해서 소리쳤다."], ["He too leapt up.", "그 역시 벌떡 일어났다."], ["“Forgive it, reverend Father,”", "\"용서하십시오, 존경하는 신부님.\""], ["he added, addressing the elder.", "그가 장로를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["“I am not a cultivated man, and I don’t even know how to address you properly, but you have been deceived and you have been too good-natured in letting us meet here.", "\"저는 교양 있는 사람이 못 되어 어른을 어떻게 불러야 옳은지조차 모릅니다. 그러나 어른은 속으셨고, 또 마음씨가 지나치게 좋으신 탓에 저희가 여기서 만나도록 허락하셨습니다."], ["All my father wants is a scandal.", "제 아버지가 바라는 것이라곤 오직 한바탕 추문뿐입니다."], ["Why he wants it only he can tell.", "어째서 그것을 바라는지는 본인만이 말할 수 있겠지요."], ["He always has some motive.", "저 사람에게는 늘 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있으니까요."], ["But I believe I know why—”", "하지만 저는 그 까닭을 알 것 같습니다—\""]], [["“They all blame me, all of them!”", "\"다들 나를 탓하는구나, 죄다 나를 탓해!\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch in his turn.", "이번에는 표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다."], ["“Pyotr Alexandrovitch here blames me too.", "\"여기 이 표트르 알렉산드로비치도 나를 탓하고 있어."], ["You have been blaming me, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, you have!”", "자네는 나를 탓해 왔지, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 자네 말이야!\""], ["he turned suddenly to Miüsov, although the latter was not dreaming of interrupting him.", "그가 갑자기 미우소프 쪽으로 돌아섰는데, 정작 미우소프는 그의 말을 가로막을 생각조차 없었다."], ["“They all accuse me of having hidden the children’s money in my boots, and cheated them, but isn’t there a court of law?", "\"다들 나더러 애들 몫의 돈을 장화 속에 숨기고 애들을 속여먹었다고 몰아붙이지. 하지만 법정이라는 것이 있지 않은가?"], ["There they will reckon out for you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, from your notes, your letters, and your agreements, how much money you had, how much you have spent, and how much you have left.", "거기서 드미트리 표도로비치, 자네의 차용증서며 편지며 계약서를 가지고 따져 줄 걸세, 자네가 돈을 얼마나 가졌었고, 얼마나 썼으며, 얼마나 남았는지를 말이야."], ["Why does Pyotr Alexandrovitch refuse to pass judgment?", "어째서 표트르 알렉산드로비치가 판단하기를 마다하는 걸까?"], ["Dmitri is not a stranger to him.", "드미트리가 그에게 남남도 아닌데 말이야."], ["Because they are all against me, while Dmitri Fyodorovitch is in debt to me, and not a little, but some thousands of which I have documentary proof.", "그건 다들 나한테 등을 돌리고 있는데, 정작 드미트리 표도로비치는 나한테 빚을 졌기 때문이지. 그것도 적지 않은, 수천 루블이나 되는 빚을, 게다가 내겐 그걸 증명할 서류까지 있어."], ["The whole town is echoing with his debaucheries.", "저놈의 방탕질로 온 고을이 떠들썩하다네."], ["And where he was stationed before, he several times spent a thousand or two for the seduction of some respectable girl; we know all about that, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, in its most secret details.", "게다가 전에 주둔하던 곳에서는 변변한 처녀 하나를 유혹하려고 천 루블이고 이천 루블이고 여러 번 뿌려 댔지. 우리는 그 일을 죄다 알고 있어, 드미트리 표도로비치, 가장 은밀한 속속들이까지 말이야."], ["I’ll prove it....", "내가 증명해 보이지……."], ["Would you believe it, holy Father, he has captivated the heart of the most honorable of young ladies of good family and fortune, daughter of a gallant colonel, formerly his superior officer, who had received many honors and had the Anna Order on his breast.", "거룩하신 신부님, 믿어지십니까, 저놈이 좋은 가문에 재산까지 갖춘, 더없이 명예로운 한 처녀의 마음을 사로잡았답니다. 그 애비는 용맹한 대령이요 한때 저놈의 상관이었던 사람으로, 숱한 훈장을 받고 가슴에 안나 훈장까지 단 분이지요."], ["He compromised the girl by his promise of marriage, now she is an orphan and here; she is betrothed to him, yet before her very eyes he is dancing attendance on a certain enchantress.", "저놈은 결혼하겠다는 약속으로 그 처녀의 처지를 곤란하게 만들어 놓았는데, 이제 그 애는 고아가 되어 여기 와 있고, 저놈과 약혼한 사이입니다. 그런데도 저놈은 그 애가 빤히 보는 앞에서 어떤 요부의 비위를 맞추며 알랑거리고 있단 말입니다."], ["And although this enchantress has lived in, so to speak, civil marriage with a respectable man, yet she is of an independent character, an unapproachable fortress for everybody, just like a legal wife—for she is virtuous, yes, holy Fathers, she is virtuous.", "이 요부가 말하자면 어떤 점잖은 사내와 내연 관계로 살아오기는 했어도, 어쨌거나 독립적인 성품의 여자라 누구도 범접 못 할 요새 같은 것이, 꼭 법적인 아내나 다름없지요—그 여자는 정숙하니까요, 그렇습니다, 거룩하신 신부님들, 그 여자는 정숙합니다."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch wants to open this fortress with a golden key, and that’s why he is insolent to me now, trying to get money from me, though he has wasted thousands on this enchantress already.", "드미트리 표도로비치는 그 요새를 황금 열쇠로 열어젖히려 하는 거고, 그래서 지금 나한테 이리 무례하게 굴며 돈을 우려내려 드는 겁니다. 그 요부한테 이미 수천 루블을 탕진해 놓고도 말이지요."], ["He’s continually borrowing money for the purpose.", "저놈은 그 목적으로 끊임없이 돈을 빌려 대고 있어요."], ["From whom do you think?", "그게 누구한테서겠습니까?"], ["Shall I say, Mitya?”", "말해 줄까, 미탸?\""]], [["“Be silent!”", "\"입 다무세요!\""], ["cried Dmitri, “wait till I’m gone.", "드미트리가 소리쳤다. \"제가 나갈 때까지 기다리세요."], ["Don’t dare in my presence to asperse the good name of an honorable girl!", "제 앞에서 감히 명예로운 처녀의 좋은 이름을 더럽히지 마세요!"], ["That you should utter a word about her is an outrage, and I won’t permit it!”", "그 사람에 관해 단 한마디라도 입에 올리는 것은 모욕이고, 저는 그것을 용납하지 않겠습니다!\""]], [["He was breathless.", "그는 숨이 막혀 했다."]], [["“Mitya!", "\"미탸!"], ["Mitya!”", "미탸!\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch hysterically, squeezing out a tear.", "표도르 파블로비치가 신경질적으로 소리치며 눈물을 한 방울 쥐어짰다."], ["“And is your father’s blessing nothing to you?", "\"그래, 네게는 애비의 축복이 아무것도 아니란 말이냐?"], ["If I curse you, what then?”", "내가 너를 저주한다면, 그땐 어쩔 테냐?\""]], [["“Shameless hypocrite!”", "\"뻔뻔스러운 위선자!\""], ["exclaimed Dmitri furiously.", "드미트리가 격분하여 외쳤다."]], [["“He says that to his father!", "\"제 애비한테 저런 소릴 하다니!"], ["his father!", "애비한테 말이오!"], ["What would he be with others?", "그러니 남들한테야 오죽하겠습니까? 여러분, 한번 상상해들 보십시오."], ["Gentlemen, only fancy; there’s a poor but honorable man living here, burdened with a numerous family, a captain who got into trouble and was discharged from the army, but not publicly, not by court-martial, with no slur on his honor.", "여기 가난하지만 명예로운 사내가 살고 있는데, 식솔이 줄줄이 딸린 데다, 한때 곤경에 처해 군에서 면직당한 대위입니다. 그래도 공개적으로, 군법 회의를 거쳐, 명예에 흠집이 남은 채로 쫓겨난 건 아니지요."], ["And three weeks ago, Dmitri seized him by the beard in a tavern, dragged him out into the street and beat him publicly, and all because he is an agent in a little business of mine.”", "그런데 삼 주 전에 드미트리가 술집에서 그자의 수염을 움켜잡고 거리로 끌어내 사람들이 보는 앞에서 두들겨 팼습니다. 그것도 그자가 내 자그마한 일거리에 심부름꾼 노릇을 했다는 이유 하나 때문에 말입니다.\""]], [["“It’s all a lie!", "\"그건 죄다 거짓말입니다!"], ["Outwardly it’s the truth, but inwardly a lie!”", "겉으로는 사실이지만 속을 들여다보면 거짓말이에요!\""], ["Dmitri was trembling with rage.", "드미트리는 분노로 부들부들 떨고 있었다."], ["“Father, I don’t justify my action.", "\"아버지, 저는 제 행동을 정당화하지 않겠습니다."], ["Yes, I confess it publicly, I behaved like a brute to that captain, and I regret it now, and I’m disgusted with myself for my brutal rage.", "그래요, 사람들 앞에서 인정하지요. 저는 그 대위에게 짐승처럼 굴었고, 지금은 그것을 후회하며, 제 짐승 같은 분노에 스스로 진저리가 납니다."], ["But this captain, this agent of yours, went to that lady whom you call an enchantress, and suggested to her from you, that she should take I.O.U.’s of mine which were in your possession, and should sue me for the money so as to get me into prison by means of them, if I persisted in claiming an account from you of my property.", "하지만 그 대위는, 아버지의 그 심부름꾼은, 아버지가 요부라고 부르는 그 부인에게 찾아가, 아버지의 뜻을 받들어 이렇게 부추겼습니다. 아버지 수중에 있는 제 차용증서를 받아 두었다가, 만일 제가 아버지께 제 재산에 대한 셈을 끈질기게 요구하거든, 그것을 이용해 저를 상대로 소송을 걸어 감옥에 처넣으라고 말입니다."], ["Now you reproach me for having a weakness for that lady when you yourself incited her to captivate me!", "그래 놓고 이제 와서 아버지가 그 부인을 향한 제 마음의 약점을 두고 저를 나무라는데, 정작 아버지 자신이 그 부인더러 저를 사로잡으라고 부추겼잖습니까!"], ["She told me so to my face....", "그 사람이 제 얼굴에 대고 그렇게 말해 줬습니다……."], ["She told me the story and laughed at you....", "제게 그 이야기를 다 들려주며 아버지를 비웃었어요……."], ["You wanted to put me in prison because you are jealous of me with her, because you’d begun to force your attentions upon her; and I know all about that, too; she laughed at you for that as well—you hear—she laughed at you as she described it.", "아버지는 저를 감옥에 처넣으려 했는데, 그건 그 사람을 두고 저를 질투했기 때문이고, 아버지 자신이 그 사람에게 치근덕대기 시작했기 때문이지요. 저는 그것까지도 죄다 알고 있습니다."], ["So here you have this man, this father who reproaches his profligate son!", "그 사람은 그 일을 두고서도 아버지를 비웃었어요—들으셨습니까—그 일을 이야기하며 아버지를 비웃었다고요."], ["Gentlemen, forgive my anger, but I foresaw that this crafty old man would only bring you together to create a scandal.", "자, 그러니 여기 계신 것이 바로 이런 사내, 방탕한 아들을 나무라는 이런 애비입니다! 여러분, 제 노여움을 용서하십시오."], ["I had come to forgive him if he held out his hand; to forgive him, and ask forgiveness!", "하지만 저는 이 교활한 늙은이가 그저 한바탕 추문을 일으키려고 여러분을 한자리에 불러 모았을 뿐이라는 것을 미리 내다보았습니다. 저는 저 사람이 손을 내민다면 용서하려고 왔던 겁니다."], ["But as he has just this minute insulted not only me, but an honorable young lady, for whom I feel such reverence that I dare not take her name in vain, I have made up my mind to show up his game, though he is my father....”", "용서하고, 또 용서를 구하려고요! 하지만 저 사람이 방금 이 순간 저뿐만 아니라, 제가 너무도 깊이 우러러 감히 그 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지도 못하는 한 명예로운 처녀까지 모욕했으니, 비록 제 애비라 할지라도 저 사람의 수작을 까발리기로 마음먹었습니다…….\""]], [["He could not go on.", "그는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["His eyes were glittering and he breathed with difficulty.", "두 눈은 번득였고 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬었다."], ["But every one in the cell was stirred.", "그러나 암자 안의 모든 사람이 술렁였다."], ["All except Father Zossima got up from their seats uneasily.", "조시마 신부를 제외하고는 다들 불안한 기색으로 자리에서 일어났다."], ["The monks looked austere but waited for guidance from the elder.", "수도사들은 근엄한 표정이었으나 장로의 지시를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He sat still, pale, not from excitement but from the weakness of disease.", "그는 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 창백한 그 안색은 흥분 때문이 아니라 병으로 인한 쇠약 때문이었다."], ["An imploring smile lighted up his face; from time to time he raised his hand, as though to check the storm, and, of course, a gesture from him would have been enough to end the scene; but he seemed to be waiting for something and watched them intently as though trying to make out something which was not perfectly clear to him.", "그의 얼굴에는 애원하는 듯한 미소가 떠올랐고, 그는 이따금 마치 폭풍을 잠재우려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸으니, 물론 그의 손짓 하나면 이 소동을 끝내기에 충분했을 테지만, 그는 무언가를 기다리고 있는 듯, 자신에게 아직 온전히 또렷하지 않은 무언가를 헤아려 보려는 듯 그들을 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었다."], ["At last Miüsov felt completely humiliated and disgraced.", "마침내 미우소프는 자신이 완전히 모욕당하고 망신을 당했다고 느꼈다."]], [["“We are all to blame for this scandalous scene,”", "\"이 추잡한 꼴은 우리 모두의 탓입니다.\""], ["he said hotly.", "그가 격하게 말했다."], ["“But I did not foresee it when I came, though I knew with whom I had to deal. This must be stopped at once!", "\"그러나 저는 여기 올 때 이 사태를 미리 내다보지는 못했습니다, 제가 누구를 상대해야 하는지는 알고 있었지만 말입니다."], ["Believe me, your reverence, I had no precise knowledge of the details that have just come to light, I was unwilling to believe them, and I learn for the first time....", "이건 당장 멈춰야 합니다! 믿어 주십시오, 신부님, 저는 방금 드러난 시시콜콜한 사정을 정확히 알지 못했고, 그것을 믿고 싶지도 않았으며, 지금 처음으로 알게 된 겁니다……."], ["A father is jealous of his son’s relations with a woman of loose behavior and intrigues with the creature to get his son into prison!", "애비가 행실이 헤픈 여자와 얽힌 제 아들의 관계를 질투하여, 그 여자와 작당해 자기 아들을 감옥에 처넣으려 하다니요!"], ["This is the company in which I have been forced to be present!", "이런 무리 속에 제가 어쩔 수 없이 끼게 된 겁니다!"], ["I was deceived.", "저는 속았습니다."], ["I declare to you all that I was as much deceived as any one.”", "여러분 모두에게 분명히 말씀드리건대, 저는 그 누구 못지않게 속았던 겁니다.\""]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["yelled Fyodor Pavlovitch suddenly, in an unnatural voice, “if you were not my son I would challenge you this instant to a duel ...", "표도르 파블로비치가 갑자기 부자연스러운 목소리로 악을 썼다. \"네가 내 아들만 아니라면 이 자리에서 당장 결투를 신청할 텐데……"], ["with pistols, at three paces ...", "권총으로, 세 걸음 거리에서……"], ["across a handkerchief,”", "손수건을 사이에 두고 말이야.\""], ["he ended, stamping with both feet.", "그가 두 발을 쿵쿵 구르며 말을 맺었다."]], [["With old liars who have been acting all their lives there are moments when they enter so completely into their part that they tremble or shed tears of emotion in earnest, although at that very moment, or a second later, they are able to whisper to themselves, “You know you are lying, you shameless old sinner!", "평생토록 연기를 해 온 늙은 거짓말쟁이들에게는, 제 배역에 어찌나 완전히 몰입하는지 정말로 부들부들 떨거나 감정에 겨워 눈물까지 흘리는 순간이 있는 법인데, 정작 바로 그 순간이나 그 일 초 뒤에는 스스로에게 이렇게 속삭일 수 있는 것이다. \"너도 알다시피 넌 거짓말을 하고 있는 거야, 이 뻔뻔스러운 늙은 죄인아!"], ["You’re acting now, in spite of your ‘holy’ wrath.”", "넌 지금 그 '거룩한' 분노에도 불구하고 연기를 하고 있는 거라고.\""]], [["Dmitri frowned painfully, and looked with unutterable contempt at his father.", "드미트리는 고통스럽게 얼굴을 찡그리며 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸의 눈초리로 아버지를 바라보았다."]], [["“I thought ...", "\"저는 생각했습니다…… 저는 생각했어요.\""], ["I thought,”", "그가 부드럽고, 말하자면 억누른 목소리로 말했다."], ["he said, in a soft and, as it were, controlled voice, “that I was coming to my native place with the angel of my heart, my betrothed, to cherish his old age, and I find nothing but a depraved profligate, a despicable clown!”", "\"제 마음의 천사, 제 약혼녀와 함께 고향으로 돌아와 늙은 아버지의 노년을 보살펴 드리려는 줄로요. 그런데 와서 보니 타락한 방탕아, 비열한 어릿광대밖에 없군요!\""]], [["“A duel!”", "\"결투다!\""], ["yelled the old wretch again, breathless and spluttering at each syllable.", "그 늙은 비렁뱅이가 또다시 악을 썼는데, 음절마다 숨이 차서 침을 튀겨 가며 외쳤다."], ["“And you, Pyotr Alexandrovitch Miüsov, let me tell you that there has never been in all your family a loftier, and more honest—you hear—more honest woman than this ‘creature,’ as you have dared to call her!", "\"그리고 자네, 표트르 알렉산드로비치 미우소프, 내 말해 두지만, 자네 온 집안을 통틀어도 자네가 감히 그렇게 불러 댄 그 '계집'보다 더 드높고 더 정직한—들었나—더 정직한 여자는 단 한 명도 없었어!"], ["And you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, have abandoned your betrothed for that ‘creature,’ so you must yourself have thought that your betrothed couldn’t hold a candle to her.", "또 드미트리 표도로비치 너는 그 '계집'을 위해 약혼녀를 내팽개쳤으니, 결국 네 약혼녀가 그 여자한테 발끝에도 못 미친다고 너 스스로 생각했다는 거 아니냐."], ["That’s the woman called a ‘creature’!”", "바로 그런 여자가 '계집'이라 불린 거란 말이다!\""]], [["“Shameful!”", "\"수치스럽습니다!\""], ["broke from Father Iosif.", "이오시프 신부의 입에서 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Shameful and disgraceful!”", "\"수치스럽고 망측합니다!\""], ["Kalganov, flushing crimson, cried in a boyish voice, trembling with emotion.", "칼가노프가 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 소년 같은 목소리로, 감정에 겨워 떨면서 외쳤다."], ["He had been silent till that moment.", "그는 그 순간까지 줄곧 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["“Why is such a man alive?”", "\"어째서 저런 인간이 살아 있는 겁니까?\""], ["Dmitri, beside himself with rage, growled in a hollow voice, hunching up his shoulders till he looked almost deformed.", "드미트리가 분노로 제정신을 잃은 채 공허한 목소리로 으르렁대며, 어깨를 잔뜩 웅크려 거의 기형처럼 보일 지경이었다."], ["“Tell me, can he be allowed to go on defiling the earth?”", "\"말씀해 주십시오, 저자가 계속 이 땅을 더럽히도록 내버려 두어도 되는 겁니까?\""], ["He looked round at every one and pointed at the old man.", "그는 모든 사람을 둘러보며 늙은이를 손가락질했다."], ["He spoke evenly and deliberately.", "그는 차분하고 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“Listen, listen, monks, to the parricide!”", "\"들어들 보시오, 들어 보시오, 수도사 여러분, 애비를 죽이겠다는 소리를!\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, rushing up to Father Iosif.", "표도르 파블로비치가 이오시프 신부에게 달려들며 소리쳤다."], ["“That’s the answer to your ‘shameful!’ What is shameful?", "\"그게 바로 당신의 그 '수치스럽다!'는 말에 대한 대답이오!"], ["That ‘creature,’ that ‘woman of loose behavior’ is perhaps holier than you are yourselves, you monks who are seeking salvation!", "무엇이 수치스럽단 말이오? 그 '계집', 그 '행실 헤픈 여자'가 어쩌면 구원을 찾는다는 당신네 수도사들보다 더 거룩할지도 모르지요!"], ["She fell perhaps in her youth, ruined by her environment.", "그 여자는 어쩌면 젊은 시절에 타락했을지도 모르오, 제 환경에 망가져서 말이오."], ["But she loved much, and Christ himself forgave the woman ‘who loved much.’”", "하지만 그 여자는 많이 사랑했고, 그리스도께서도 친히 '많이 사랑한' 그 여인을 용서하셨지 않소.\""]], [["“It was not for such love Christ forgave her,”", "\"그리스도께서 그 여인을 용서하신 것은 그런 사랑 때문이 아니었습니다.\""], ["broke impatiently from the gentle Father Iosif.", "온화한 이오시프 신부의 입에서 참다못해 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Yes, it was for such, monks, it was!", "\"아니오, 바로 그런 사랑 때문이었소, 수도사 여러분, 그랬다니까요!"], ["You save your souls here, eating cabbage, and think you are the righteous.", "당신들은 여기서 양배추나 뜯어 먹으며 영혼을 구한답시고, 스스로 의롭다고 여기고 있소."], ["You eat a gudgeon a day, and you think you bribe God with gudgeon.”", "하루에 모샘치 한 마리를 먹으면서 그 모샘치로 신을 매수한다고 생각하는 거요.\""]], [["“This is unendurable!”", "\"이건 참을 수 없습니다!\""], ["was heard on all sides in the cell.", "암자 사방에서 이런 소리가 터져 나왔다."]], [["But this unseemly scene was cut short in a most unexpected way.", "그러나 이 꼴사나운 소동은 더없이 뜻밖의 방식으로 끝이 났다."], ["Father Zossima rose suddenly from his seat.", "조시마 신부가 갑자기 자리에서 일어났다."], ["Almost distracted with anxiety for the elder and every one else, Alyosha succeeded, however, in supporting him by the arm.", "장로와 다른 모든 사람을 향한 염려로 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었던 알료샤가 그래도 가까스로 장로의 팔을 부축했다."], ["Father Zossima moved towards Dmitri and reaching him sank on his knees before him.", "조시마 신부는 드미트리 쪽으로 다가가, 그에게 이르러 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["Alyosha thought that he had fallen from weakness, but this was not so.", "알료샤는 그가 쇠약해서 쓰러진 줄 알았으나 그렇지 않았다."], ["The elder distinctly and deliberately bowed down at Dmitri’s feet till his forehead touched the floor.", "장로는 또렷하고도 의연하게 드미트리의 발치에 몸을 굽혀 이마가 바닥에 닿도록 절했다."], ["Alyosha was so astounded that he failed to assist him when he got up again.", "알료샤는 너무도 놀란 나머지 그가 다시 일어설 때 부축하는 것조차 잊었다."], ["There was a faint smile on his lips.", "장로의 입가에는 희미한 미소가 어려 있었다."]], [["“Good-by!", "\"잘들 계시오!"], ["Forgive me, all of you!”", "모두들 나를 용서하시오!\""], ["he said, bowing on all sides to his guests.", "그가 손님들에게 사방으로 절하며 말했다."]], [["Dmitri stood for a few moments in amazement.", "드미트리는 한동안 어안이 벙벙해서 서 있었다."], ["Bowing down to him—what did it mean?", "자기에게 절을 하다니—그게 대체 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Suddenly he cried aloud, “Oh, God!”", "그는 갑자기 \"오, 하느님!\""], ["hid his face in his hands, and rushed out of the room.", "하고 큰 소리로 외치며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["All the guests flocked out after him, in their confusion not saying good-by, or bowing to their host.", "다른 손님들도 모두 그를 뒤따라 우르르 밖으로 나갔는데, 당황한 나머지 작별 인사도, 주인에 대한 절도 하지 못했다."], ["Only the monks went up to him again for a blessing.", "다만 수도사들만이 다시 그에게 다가가 축복을 청했다."]], [["“What did it mean, falling at his feet like that?", "\"저렇게 발치에 엎드린 게 대체 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["Was it symbolic or what?”", "무슨 상징인가, 뭔가?\""], ["said Fyodor Pavlovitch, suddenly quieted and trying to reopen conversation without venturing to address anybody in particular.", "표도르 파블로비치가 갑자기 잠잠해져서, 누구를 콕 집어 말할 엄두는 내지 못한 채 대화를 다시 트려고 애쓰며 말했다."], ["They were all passing out of the precincts of the hermitage at the moment.", "그들은 마침 암자 경내를 빠져나오고 있는 참이었다."]], [["“I can’t answer for a madhouse and for madmen,”", "\"나는 미친 집이며 미친 자들에 대해서는 책임질 수 없소.\""], ["Miüsov answered at once ill-humoredly, “but I will spare myself your company, Fyodor Pavlovitch, and, trust me, for ever.", "미우소프가 즉시 언짢은 기색으로 대답했다. \"하지만 표도르 파블로비치, 당신과의 동행만은 면하도록 하겠소, 그것도 영영, 내 말을 믿으시오."], ["Where’s that monk?”", "그 수도사는 어디 있소?\""]], [["“That monk,” that is, the monk who had invited them to dine with the Superior, did not keep them waiting.", "\"그 수도사\", 곧 그들을 원장과의 만찬에 초대했던 그 수도사는 그들을 기다리게 하지 않았다."], ["He met them as soon as they came down the steps from the elder’s cell, as though he had been waiting for them all the time.", "그들이 장로의 암자 계단을 내려서기가 무섭게, 마치 내내 그들을 기다리고 있었다는 듯 그가 그들을 맞이했다."]], [["“Reverend Father, kindly do me a favor.", "\"존경하는 신부님, 부디 제 청을 하나 들어주십시오."], ["Convey my deepest respect to the Father Superior, apologize for me, personally, Miüsov, to his reverence, telling him that I deeply regret that owing to unforeseen circumstances I am unable to have the honor of being present at his table, greatly as I should desire to do so,”", "원장 신부님께 저의 깊은 경의를 전해 주시고, 미우소프 본인이 직접 사죄드린다고, 뜻하지 않은 사정으로 그분의 식탁에 함께하는 영광을 누리지 못하게 되어 진심으로 유감스러우며, 그러기를 그토록 간절히 바라면서도 어쩔 수 없다고 신부님께 말씀드려 주십시오.\""], ["Miüsov said irritably to the monk.", "미우소프가 짜증스레 수도사에게 말했다."]], [["“And that unforeseen circumstance, of course, is myself,”", "\"그 뜻하지 않은 사정이란 게 물론 나를 두고 하는 말이지요.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch cut in immediately.", "표도르 파블로비치가 즉시 끼어들었다."], ["“Do you hear, Father; this gentleman doesn’t want to remain in my company or else he’d come at once.", "\"들으셨소, 신부님? 이 양반은 나하고 같이 있기 싫은 거요, 그게 아니면 당장 가셨겠지요."], ["And you shall go, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, pray go to the Father Superior and good appetite to you.", "그러니 가시오, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 어서 원장님께 가시구려, 그리고 맛있게 드시오."], ["I will decline, and not you.", "사양하는 건 당신이 아니라 나일 테니."], ["Home, home, I’ll eat at home, I don’t feel equal to it here, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, my amiable relative.”", "집으로, 집으로 가겠소, 나는 집에서 먹지. 여기서는 도무지 그럴 마음이 안 나는구려, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 내 다정한 친척 양반.\""]], [["“I am not your relative and never have been, you contemptible man!”", "\"나는 당신 친척이 아니고, 여태껏 친척이었던 적도 없소, 이 비열한 인간!\""]], [["“I said it on purpose to madden you, because you always disclaim the relationship, though you really are a relation in spite of your shuffling.", "\"내가 당신을 약 올리려고 일부러 그렇게 말한 거요. 당신이야 늘 그 인척 관계를 부인하지만, 아무리 발뺌해도 당신은 실은 내 친척이거든."], ["I’ll prove it by the church calendar.", "교회 달력으로 증명해 보이지."], ["As for you, Ivan, stay if you like.", "이반, 너는 남고 싶으면 남거라."], ["I’ll send the horses for you later.", "나중에 말을 보내 주마."], ["Propriety requires you to go to the Father Superior, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, to apologize for the disturbance we’ve been making....”", "격식상 원장님께 가야 하는 거요, 표트르 알렉산드로비치, 우리가 일으킨 소란을 사죄하러 말이오…….\""]], [["“Is it true that you are going home?", "\"정말로 집으로 가는 거요?"], ["Aren’t you lying?”", "거짓말은 아니겠지?\""]], [["“Pyotr Alexandrovitch!", "\"표트르 알렉산드로비치!"], ["How could I dare after what’s happened!", "이런 일이 벌어진 마당에 내가 어찌 감히 가겠소!"], ["Forgive me, gentlemen, I was carried away!", "용서들 하시오, 여러분, 내가 그만 흥분했던 거요!"], ["And upset besides!", "게다가 속까지 뒤집어졌고!"], ["And, indeed, I am ashamed.", "그리고 솔직히 부끄럽기도 하오."], ["Gentlemen, one man has the heart of Alexander of Macedon and another the heart of the little dog Fido.", "여러분, 어떤 사람은 마케도니아의 알렉산드로스 같은 심장을 지녔고, 또 어떤 사람은 강아지 피도 같은 심장을 지녔지요."], ["Mine is that of the little dog Fido.", "내 것은 그 강아지 피도의 심장이오."], ["I am ashamed!", "부끄럽소이다!"], ["After such an escapade how can I go to dinner, to gobble up the monastery’s sauces?", "그런 추태를 부려 놓고 무슨 낯으로 만찬에 가서 수도원의 소스를 게걸스레 먹어 치우겠소?"], ["I am ashamed, I can’t.", "부끄러워서, 도저히 못 하겠소."], ["You must excuse me!”", "부디 양해해 주시구려!\""]], [["“The devil only knows, what if he deceives us?”", "\"빌어먹을, 만일 저자가 우리를 속이는 거라면 어쩐다?\""], ["thought Miüsov, still hesitating, and watching the retreating buffoon with distrustful eyes.", "미우소프는 여전히 망설이며, 멀어져 가는 그 어릿광대를 미심쩍은 눈으로 지켜보면서 생각했다."], ["The latter turned round, and noticing that Miüsov was watching him, waved him a kiss.", "어릿광대는 뒤를 돌아보더니, 미우소프가 자기를 지켜보고 있는 것을 알아채고는 그에게 손으로 입맞춤을 날려 보냈다."]], [["“Well, are you coming to the Superior?”", "\"그래, 자네는 원장께 가겠나?\""], ["Miüsov asked Ivan abruptly.", "미우소프가 이반에게 퉁명스레 물었다."]], [["“Why not?", "\"안 갈 까닭이 있나요?"], ["I was especially invited yesterday.”", "어제 특별히 초대받았는걸요.\""]], [["“Unfortunately I feel myself compelled to go to this confounded dinner,”", "\"유감스럽게도 나는 이 빌어먹을 만찬에 가야만 할 것 같군.\""], ["said Miüsov with the same irritability, regardless of the fact that the monk was listening.", "미우소프가 수도사가 듣고 있다는 사실도 아랑곳없이 똑같은 짜증스러운 어조로 말했다."], ["“We ought, at least, to apologize for the disturbance, and explain that it was not our doing.", "\"우리는 적어도 소란을 일으킨 데 대해 사죄하고, 그게 우리 탓이 아니었음을 해명해야 하니까."], ["What do you think?”", "자네 생각은 어떤가?\""]], [["“Yes, we must explain that it wasn’t our doing.", "\"그래요, 그게 우리 탓이 아니었음을 해명해야지요."], ["Besides, father won’t be there,”", "게다가 아버지도 거기 안 계실 테고요.\""], ["observed Ivan.", "이반이 말했다."]], [["“Well, I should hope not!", "\"그렇겠지, 정말 그래야 할 거야!"], ["Confound this dinner!”", "이 빌어먹을 만찬 같으니!\""]], [["They all walked on, however.", "그래도 그들은 모두 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["The monk listened in silence.", "수도사는 잠자코 듣고 있었다."], ["On the road through the copse he made one observation however—that the Father Superior had been waiting a long time, and that they were more than half an hour late.", "잡목 숲을 가로지르는 길에서 그가 한 가지만은 일러 주었으니—원장 신부님이 오래도록 기다리고 계시며, 그들이 삼십 분도 넘게 늦었다는 것이었다. 그는 아무 대답도 듣지 못했다."], ["He received no answer. Miüsov looked with hatred at Ivan.", "미우소프는 증오에 찬 눈으로 이반을 바라보았다."]], [["“Here he is, going to the dinner as though nothing had happened,”", "\"저자는 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 만찬에 가는군.\" 그는 생각했다."], ["he thought. “A brazen face, and the conscience of a Karamazov!”", "\"뻔뻔스러운 낯짝에다, 카라마조프의 양심을 지닌 놈이야!\""]], [["# Chapter VII. A Young Man Bent On A Career", "# Chapter VII. A Young Man Bent On A Career"]], [["Alyosha helped Father Zossima to his bedroom and seated him on his bed.", "알료샤는 조시마 장로를 부축해 침실로 모시고 침대에 앉혀 드렸다."], ["It was a little room furnished with the bare necessities.", "방은 꼭 필요한 세간만 갖추어진 자그마한 곳이었다."], ["There was a narrow iron bedstead, with a strip of felt for a mattress.", "좁은 철제 침대가 놓여 있었고, 요라고는 펠트 한 조각이 깔려 있을 뿐이었다."], ["In the corner, under the ikons, was a reading-desk with a cross and the Gospel lying on it.", "한쪽 구석, 이콘 아래에는 십자가와 복음서가 얹힌 독서대가 있었다."], ["The elder sank exhausted on the bed.", "장로는 기진맥진하여 침대에 주저앉았다."], ["His eyes glittered and he breathed hard.", "그의 두 눈은 번득였고 숨결은 거칠었다."], ["He looked intently at Alyosha, as though considering something.", "그는 마치 무언가를 곰곰 헤아리는 듯, 알료샤를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Go, my dear boy, go.", "\"가거라, 얘야, 가거라."], ["Porfiry is enough for me.", "내게는 포르피리만 있으면 충분하다."], ["Make haste, you are needed there, go and wait at the Father Superior’s table.”", "어서 가거라, 거기서 네가 필요하니, 가서 원장 신부님 식탁에서 시중을 들어야 한다.\""]], [["“Let me stay here,”", "\"여기 남게 해 주십시오.\""], ["Alyosha entreated.", "알료샤가 애원했다."]], [["“You are more needed there.", "\"네가 더 필요한 곳은 거기다."], ["There is no peace there.", "거기엔 평안이 없으니."], ["You will wait, and be of service.", "너는 시중을 들며 도움이 되어야 한다."], ["If evil spirits rise up, repeat a prayer.", "악령이 일어나거든 기도문을 외워라."], ["And remember, my son”—the elder liked to call him that—“this is not the place for you in the future.", "그리고 잊지 말아라, 얘야\"—장로는 그를 그렇게 부르기를 좋아했다—\"이곳은 앞으로 네가 머물 자리가 아니다."], ["When it is God’s will to call me, leave the monastery.", "하느님께서 나를 부르실 뜻을 세우시거든, 수도원을 떠나거라."], ["Go away for good.”", "영영 떠나거라.\""]], [["Alyosha started.", "알료샤가 흠칫했다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"왜 그러느냐?"], ["This is not your place for the time.", "지금으로서는 이곳이 네 자리가 아니다."], ["I bless you for great service in the world.", "나는 너에게 세상에서 큰 봉사를 하도록 축복을 내린다."], ["Yours will be a long pilgrimage.", "너의 앞에는 기나긴 순례가 있을 것이다."], ["And you will have to take a wife, too.", "너는 또한 아내를 맞아야 할 것이다."], ["You will have to bear all before you come back.", "너는 돌아오기 전에 모든 것을 견뎌 내야 한다."], ["There will be much to do.", "할 일이 많을 것이다."], ["But I don’t doubt of you, and so I send you forth.", "그러나 나는 너를 의심하지 않으니, 그래서 너를 내보내는 것이다."], ["Christ is with you.", "그리스도께서 너와 함께하신다."], ["Do not abandon Him and He will not abandon you.", "그분을 저버리지 말아라, 그러면 그분도 너를 저버리지 않으실 것이다."], ["You will see great sorrow, and in that sorrow you will be happy.", "너는 큰 슬픔을 보게 될 것이며, 그 슬픔 속에서 너는 행복할 것이다."], ["This is my last message to you: in sorrow seek happiness.", "이것이 너에게 남기는 나의 마지막 당부다. 슬픔 속에서 행복을 찾아라."], ["Work, work unceasingly.", "일하라, 쉬지 말고 일하라."], ["Remember my words, for although I shall talk with you again, not only my days but my hours are numbered.”", "나의 말을 기억하여라, 비록 너와 다시 이야기를 나누게 되겠지만, 나의 날뿐만 아니라 나의 시간까지도 이미 다 헤아려졌으니.\""]], [["Alyosha’s face again betrayed strong emotion.", "알료샤의 얼굴에 또다시 격한 감정이 드러났다."], ["The corners of his mouth quivered.", "입꼬리가 떨렸다."]], [["“What is it again?”", "\"또 왜 그러느냐?\""], ["Father Zossima asked, smiling gently.", "조시마 장로가 부드럽게 미소 지으며 물었다."], ["“The worldly may follow the dead with tears, but here we rejoice over the father who is departing.", "\"세속 사람들은 죽은 자를 눈물로 배웅하지만, 이곳에서는 떠나가는 아버지를 두고 기뻐한다. 우리는 기뻐하며 그를 위해 기도한다."], ["We rejoice and pray for him. Leave me, I must pray.", "나를 두고 가거라, 나는 기도를 드려야 한다."], ["Go, and make haste.", "가거라, 어서 서둘러라."], ["Be near your brothers.", "네 형들 곁에 있어라."], ["And not near one only, but near both.”", "한 사람 곁이 아니라, 두 사람 모두의 곁에 있어라.\""]], [["Father Zossima raised his hand to bless him.", "조시마 장로가 그를 축복하려고 손을 들어 올렸다."], ["Alyosha could make no protest, though he had a great longing to remain.", "알료샤는 그토록 남아 있고 싶었으나 거역할 수 없었다."], ["He longed, moreover, to ask the significance of his bowing to Dmitri, the question was on the tip of his tongue, but he dared not ask it.", "게다가 그는 드미트리에게 절을 한 것이 무슨 뜻인지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수 없었으니, 그 물음이 혀끝까지 차올랐으나 감히 입 밖에 내지 못했다."], ["He knew that the elder would have explained it unasked if he had thought fit.", "그는 장로가 적절하다고 여겼다면 묻지 않아도 설명해 주었으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["But evidently it was not his will.", "그러나 그것은 분명 장로의 뜻이 아니었다."], ["That action had made a terrible impression on Alyosha; he believed blindly in its mysterious significance.", "그 행동은 알료샤에게 무서운 인상을 남겼으니, 그는 거기에 깃든 신비로운 의미를 맹목적으로 믿었다."], ["Mysterious, and perhaps awful.", "신비롭고, 어쩌면 끔찍한 의미를."]], [["As he hastened out of the hermitage precincts to reach the monastery in time to serve at the Father Superior’s dinner, he felt a sudden pang at his heart, and stopped short.", "원장 신부의 만찬에서 시중을 들기 위해 제때 수도원에 닿으려고 암자 경내를 서둘러 빠져나오던 그는, 문득 가슴이 미어지는 듯한 통증을 느끼며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["He seemed to hear again Father Zossima’s words, foretelling his approaching end.", "그는 다가올 자신의 임종을 예언하던 조시마 장로의 말이 다시금 들려오는 것만 같았다."], ["What he had foretold so exactly must infallibly come to pass.", "그토록 정확히 예언하신 것은 틀림없이 그대로 이루어지리라."], ["Alyosha believed that implicitly.", "알료샤는 그것을 추호도 의심 없이 믿었다."], ["But how could he be left without him?", "그러나 그를 잃고서 어찌 살아간단 말인가?"], ["How could he live without seeing and hearing him? Where should he go?", "그를 보지도 못하고 그의 말을 듣지도 못한 채 어찌 살아간단 말인가?"], ["He had told him not to weep, and to leave the monastery.", "어디로 가야 한단 말인가? 그는 울지 말라고, 수도원을 떠나라고 했다."], ["Good God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["It was long since Alyosha had known such anguish.", "알료샤가 이런 비통함을 맛본 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다."], ["He hurried through the copse that divided the monastery from the hermitage, and unable to bear the burden of his thoughts, he gazed at the ancient pines beside the path.", "그는 수도원과 암자를 가르는 작은 숲을 가로질러 황급히 걸으면서, 제 생각의 무게를 견딜 수 없어 길가의 늙은 소나무들을 물끄러미 바라보았다."], ["He had not far to go—about five hundred paces.", "갈 길은 멀지 않았으니—오백 걸음쯤이었다."], ["He expected to meet no one at that hour, but at the first turn of the path he noticed Rakitin.", "그는 그 시각에 아무도 마주치지 않으리라 여겼으나, 길의 첫 모퉁이에서 라키틴을 발견했다."], ["He was waiting for some one.", "그는 누군가를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Are you waiting for me?”", "\"나를 기다리는 건가?\""], ["asked Alyosha, overtaking him.", "알료샤가 그를 따라잡으며 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["grinned Rakitin.", "라키틴이 히죽 웃었다."], ["“You are hurrying to the Father Superior, I know; he has a banquet.", "\"원장 신부님께 서둘러 가는 길이지, 나도 알아."], ["There’s not been such a banquet since the Superior entertained the Bishop and General Pahatov, do you remember?", "거기 잔치가 벌어졌으니까. 원장이 주교님과 파하토프 장군을 대접하던 그때 이후로 이런 잔치는 처음이지, 기억나나?"], ["I shan’t be there, but you go and hand the sauces.", "나야 거기 가지 않겠지만, 자넨 가서 소스나 받쳐 들고 다니게."], ["Tell me one thing, Alexey, what does that vision mean?", "한 가지만 묻세, 알렉세이, 그 광경은 대체 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["That’s what I want to ask you.”", "그걸 묻고 싶어서 그래.\""]], [["“What vision?”", "\"무슨 광경 말인가?\""]], [["“That bowing to your brother, Dmitri.", "\"자네 형 드미트리에게 절을 한 거 말일세."], ["And didn’t he tap the ground with his forehead, too!”", "게다가 이마로 땅을 톡톡 두드리기까지 했잖나!\""]], [["“You speak of Father Zossima?”", "\"조시마 장로님 이야기인가?\""]], [["“Yes, of Father Zossima.”", "\"그래, 조시마 장로 이야기일세.\""]], [["“Tapped the ground?”", "\"땅을 두드렸다고?\""]], [["“Ah, an irreverent expression!", "\"아, 불경한 표현이로군!"], ["Well, what of it?", "그래, 그게 뭐 어때서?"], ["Anyway, what does that vision mean?”", "어쨌든 그 광경이 무슨 뜻이냔 말일세.\""]], [["“I don’t know what it means, Misha.”", "\"무슨 뜻인지 나도 모르네, 미샤.\""]], [["“I knew he wouldn’t explain it to you!", "\"자네한테 설명해 주지 않을 줄 알았어!"], ["There’s nothing wonderful about it, of course, only the usual holy mummery.", "물론 거기에 신통할 건 하나도 없지, 흔해 빠진 성스러운 광대놀음일 뿐이니까."], ["But there was an object in the performance.", "하지만 그 연극에는 목적이 있었다네."], ["All the pious people in the town will talk about it and spread the story through the province, wondering what it meant.", "고을의 신심 깊은 사람들이 죄다 그 이야기를 떠들어 대며 무슨 뜻이었을까 궁금해하면서 온 고장에 퍼뜨릴 거야."], ["To my thinking the old man really has a keen nose; he sniffed a crime.", "내 생각에 그 늙은이는 정말이지 코가 예민해. 범죄 냄새를 맡은 거지."], ["Your house stinks of it.”", "자네 집안에서 그 냄새가 풍기거든.\""]], [["“What crime?”", "\"무슨 범죄 말인가?\""]], [["Rakitin evidently had something he was eager to speak of.", "라키틴은 분명 무언가 이야기하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다."]], [["“It’ll be in your family, this crime. Between your brothers and your rich old father. So Father Zossima flopped down to be ready for what may turn up. If something happens later on, it’ll be: ‘Ah, the holy man foresaw it, prophesied it!’ though it’s a poor sort of prophecy, flopping like that. ‘Ah, but it was symbolic,’ they’ll say, ‘an allegory,’ and the devil knows what all! It’ll be remembered to his glory: ‘He predicted the crime and marked the criminal!’ That’s always the way with these crazy fanatics; they cross themselves at the tavern and throw stones at the temple. Like your elder, he takes a stick to a just man and falls at the feet of a murderer.”", "\"자네 집안에서 벌어질 범죄 말이야, 이 범죄가. 자네 형들과 그 돈 많은 늙은 아버지 사이에서 말일세. 그래서 조시마가 무슨 일이 터질지 대비하려고 넙죽 엎드린 거야. 나중에 무슨 일이 벌어지면 이렇게들 말하겠지. '아, 그 성자께서 미리 내다보셨다, 예언하셨다!' 하지만 그렇게 넙죽 엎드리는 것쯤이야 시시한 예언이지. '아, 하지만 그건 상징이었어,' 사람들은 이렇게 말할 거야. '하나의 알레고리였지,' 그러고는 또 무슨 소릴 늘어놓을지 귀신이나 알겠지! 그것이 그의 영광으로 길이 기억될 거야. '그분은 범죄를 예언하셨고 범인을 지목하셨다!' 하고 말이지. 이런 미치광이 광신도들은 늘 그런 식이야. 술집에서 성호를 긋고 성전에 돌을 던진다니까. 자네네 장로처럼 말이지, 의인에게는 몽둥이를 들이대고 살인자의 발치엔 엎드리거든.\""]], [["“What crime?", "\"무슨 범죄 말인가?"], ["What murderer?", "무슨 살인자 말이야?"], ["What do you mean?”", "대체 무슨 소릴 하는 건가?\""]], [["Alyosha stopped dead.", "알료샤가 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Rakitin stopped, too.", "라키틴도 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“What murderer?", "\"무슨 살인자라니?"], ["As though you didn’t know!", "자넨 모르는 척하는군!"], ["I’ll bet you’ve thought of it before.", "자네도 진작에 그 생각을 했을 거라고 장담하네."], ["That’s interesting, too, by the way.", "게다가 그것 또한 흥미롭단 말이지."], ["Listen, Alyosha, you always speak the truth, though you’re always between two stools.", "들어 보게, 알료샤, 자넨 늘 진실을 말하면서도 늘 이쪽도 저쪽도 아닌 어정쩡한 처지에 있잖아."], ["Have you thought of it or not?", "그 생각을 했나, 안 했나?"], ["Answer.”", "대답해 보게.\""]], [["“I have,”", "\"했네.\""], ["answered Alyosha in a low voice.", "알료샤가 낮은 목소리로 대답했다."], ["Even Rakitin was taken aback.", "라키틴마저 흠칫했다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Have you really?”", "정말로?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“I ...", "\"나는…"], ["I’ve not exactly thought it,”", "정확히 그 생각을 한 건 아니야.\""], ["muttered Alyosha, “but directly you began speaking so strangely, I fancied I had thought of it myself.”", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 자네가 그렇게 이상한 말을 꺼내자마자, 나도 그 생각을 했었구나 싶더군.\""]], [["“You see?", "\"봤지?"], ["(And how well you expressed it!) Looking at your father and your brother Mitya to-day you thought of a crime.", "(게다가 표현도 어찌나 잘했는지!) 오늘 자네 아버지와 형 미탸를 보면서 자넨 범죄를 떠올린 거야."], ["Then I’m not mistaken?”", "그럼 내가 틀린 게 아니지?\""]], [["“But wait, wait a minute,”", "\"하지만 잠깐, 잠깐만 기다려.\""], ["Alyosha broke in uneasily.", "알료샤가 불안한 듯 말을 막았다."], ["“What has led you to see all this?", "\"자네는 어떻게 이 모든 걸 알아챈 거지?"], ["Why does it interest you?", "왜 그게 자네 관심사인가?"], ["That’s the first question.”", "그게 첫 번째 물음일세.\""]], [["“Two questions, disconnected, but natural.", "\"두 가지 물음이로군, 서로 동떨어졌지만 자연스러운 물음이야."], ["I’ll deal with them separately.", "따로따로 답해 주지."], ["What led me to see it?", "내가 어떻게 알아챘느냐고?"], ["I shouldn’t have seen it, if I hadn’t suddenly understood your brother Dmitri, seen right into the very heart of him all at once.", "자네 형 드미트리를 별안간 이해하고, 단번에 그 속마음 깊은 곳까지 꿰뚫어 보지 못했더라면 나도 알아채지 못했을 거야."], ["I caught the whole man from one trait.", "나는 단 하나의 특징에서 그 사람 전부를 잡아냈거든."], ["These very honest but passionate people have a line which mustn’t be crossed.", "이렇게 아주 정직하지만 격정적인 사람들에게는 결코 넘어서는 안 될 선이 하나 있단 말이지."], ["If it were, he’d run at your father with a knife.", "만약 그 선을 넘으면, 그는 칼을 들고 자네 아버지에게 달려들 거야."], ["But your father’s a drunken and abandoned old sinner, who can never draw the line—if they both let themselves go, they’ll both come to grief.”", "그런데 자네 아버지로 말하자면 술에 절어 막돼먹은 늙은 죄인이라 도무지 선을 그을 줄 모르거든—두 사람이 다 제멋대로 굴면, 둘 다 파멸하고 마는 거지.\""]], [["“No, Misha, no.", "\"아니야, 미샤, 아니야."], ["If that’s all, you’ve reassured me.", "그게 전부라면, 자넨 나를 안심시킨 거네."], ["It won’t come to that.”", "그런 일은 일어나지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“But why are you trembling?", "\"그럼 왜 떨고 있나?"], ["Let me tell you; he may be honest, our Mitya (he is stupid, but honest), but he’s—a sensualist.", "내 말해 주지. 우리 미탸가 정직할 순 있어 (그는 어리석지만 정직하지), 하지만 그는—호색한이야."], ["That’s the very definition and inner essence of him.", "그게 바로 그의 정의이자 본질적인 속성이지."], ["It’s your father has handed him on his low sensuality.", "자네 아버지가 그 천박한 호색을 그에게 물려준 거야."], ["Do you know, I simply wonder at you, Alyosha, how you can have kept your purity.", "정말이지 난 자네가 신기하단 말이야, 알료샤, 어떻게 그 순결함을 지킬 수 있었는지."], ["You’re a Karamazov too, you know!", "자네도 카라마조프잖나!"], ["In your family sensuality is carried to a disease.", "자네 집안에선 호색이 병적인 지경까지 치닫는단 말이지."], ["But now, these three sensualists are watching one another, with their knives in their belts.", "그런데 지금, 이 세 호색한이 허리춤에 칼을 차고 서로를 노려보고 있어."], ["The three of them are knocking their heads together, and you may be the fourth.”", "셋이서 머리를 맞부딪치고 있는데, 자넨 어쩌면 그 넷째가 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["“You are mistaken about that woman.", "\"자넨 그 여자를 잘못 알고 있어."], ["Dmitri—despises her,”", "드미트리는—그 여자를 경멸한다네.\""], ["said Alyosha, with a sort of shudder.", "알료샤가 일종의 몸서리를 치며 말했다."]], [["“Grushenka?", "\"그루셴카를?"], ["No, brother, he doesn’t despise her.", "아니야, 형제, 그는 그 여자를 경멸하지 않아."], ["Since he has openly abandoned his betrothed for her, he doesn’t despise her.", "그 여자 때문에 약혼녀를 버린 마당에, 그 여자를 경멸할 리가 없지."], ["There’s something here, my dear boy, that you don’t understand yet.", "여기엔 말일세, 친구, 자네가 아직 이해하지 못하는 무언가가 있어."], ["A man will fall in love with some beauty, with a woman’s body, or even with a part of a woman’s body (a sensualist can understand that), and he’ll abandon his own children for her, sell his father and mother, and his country, Russia, too.", "사내란 어떤 미녀에게, 여자의 몸에, 심지어 여자 몸의 어느 한 부분에까지 반할 수 있는 법이거든 (호색한이라면 이걸 이해하지), 그러면 그 여자를 위해 제 자식들도 버리고, 제 아비 어미도 팔아넘기고, 제 조국 러시아까지도 팔아넘기는 거야."], ["If he’s honest, he’ll steal; if he’s humane, he’ll murder; if he’s faithful, he’ll deceive.", "정직한 자라면 도둑질을 할 거고, 인정 많은 자라면 살인을 할 거고, 신의 있는 자라면 배신을 할 거야."], ["Pushkin, the poet of women’s feet, sung of their feet in his verse.", "여인의 발을 노래한 시인 푸시킨은 시구에서 그 발을 찬미했지."], ["Others don’t sing their praises, but they can’t look at their feet without a thrill—and it’s not only their feet.", "다른 자들은 발을 찬미하진 않아도, 그 발을 보면 전율하지 않을 수 없거든—그리고 그게 발만은 아니지."], ["Contempt’s no help here, brother, even if he did despise Grushenka.", "여기서는 경멸도 소용이 없어, 형제, 설령 그가 그루셴카를 경멸한다 해도 말이지."], ["He does, but he can’t tear himself away.”", "정말 경멸하긴 해, 하지만 그 여자에게서 헤어나질 못한단 말이야.\""]], [["“I understand that,”", "\"나는 그걸 이해하네.\""], ["Alyosha jerked out suddenly.", "알료샤가 별안간 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“Really?", "\"정말로?"], ["Well, I dare say you do understand, since you blurt it out at the first word,”", "흠, 첫마디에 그렇게 불쑥 내뱉는 걸 보니 정말 이해하는 모양이로군.\""], ["said Rakitin, malignantly.", "라키틴이 심술궂게 말했다."], ["“That escaped you unawares, and the confession’s the more precious.", "\"그건 자네도 모르게 새어 나온 거니, 그 고백이 더더욱 값진 거지."], ["So it’s a familiar subject; you’ve thought about it already, about sensuality, I mean!", "그러니 익숙한 주제로군. 자넨 이미 그 생각을 했었던 거야, 호색에 관해서 말이지!"], ["Oh, you virgin soul!", "오, 이 동정의 영혼아!"], ["You’re a quiet one, Alyosha, you’re a saint, I know, but the devil only knows what you’ve thought about, and what you know already!", "자넨 얌전한 친구야, 알료샤, 자넨 성자지, 나도 알아, 하지만 자네가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 이미 무엇을 알고 있는지는 귀신이나 알겠지!"], ["You are pure, but you’ve been down into the depths....", "자넨 순결하지만, 심연의 밑바닥까지 내려갔다 왔어…"], ["I’ve been watching you a long time.", "난 오래전부터 자넬 지켜봐 왔다네."], ["You’re a Karamazov yourself; you’re a thorough Karamazov—no doubt birth and selection have something to answer for.", "자네 자신도 카라마조프야. 자넨 철두철미한 카라마조프거든—혈통과 선택이라는 게 분명 책임져야 할 몫이 있는 거지."], ["You’re a sensualist from your father, a crazy saint from your mother.", "자넨 아버지에게서 호색을, 어머니에게서 미치광이 같은 성자의 기질을 물려받은 거야."], ["Why do you tremble?", "왜 떠는 건가?"], ["Is it true, then?", "그럼 정말인가?"], ["Do you know, Grushenka has been begging me to bring you along.", "자네 알아? 그루셴카가 나더러 자넬 데려오라고 졸라 댔다네."], ["‘I’ll pull off his cassock,’ she says.", "'내가 그자의 사제복을 벗겨 줄게,' 이러더군."], ["You can’t think how she keeps begging me to bring you.", "그 여자가 자넬 데려오라고 얼마나 졸라 대는지 자넨 짐작도 못 할 거야."], ["I wondered why she took such an interest in you.", "난 그 여자가 왜 자네한테 그렇게 관심을 두는지 의아했지."], ["Do you know, she’s an extraordinary woman, too!”", "자네 알아, 그 여자도 보통 여자가 아니거든!\""]], [["“Thank her and say I’m not coming,”", "\"고맙다고 전하고 난 가지 않겠다고 하게.\""], ["said Alyosha, with a strained smile.", "알료샤가 억지 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Finish what you were saying, Misha.", "\"하던 이야기를 마저 해 보게, 미샤."], ["I’ll tell you my idea after.”", "내 생각은 그다음에 말해 줄 테니.\""]], [["“There’s nothing to finish.", "\"마저 할 것도 없네."], ["It’s all clear.", "다 뻔하니까."], ["It’s the same old tune, brother.", "늘 똑같은 가락이지, 형제."], ["If even you are a sensualist at heart, what of your brother, Ivan?", "자네마저도 속으로는 호색한이라면, 자네 형 이반은 또 어떻겠나?"], ["He’s a Karamazov, too.", "그자도 카라마조프잖아."], ["What is at the root of all you Karamazovs is that you’re all sensual, grasping and crazy!", "자네들 카라마조프 일족의 뿌리에 박힌 게 바로 그거란 말이야, 자네들은 죄다 음탕하고 탐욕스럽고 미쳐 있다는 거!"], ["Your brother Ivan writes theological articles in joke, for some idiotic, unknown motive of his own, though he’s an atheist, and he admits it’s a fraud himself—that’s your brother Ivan.", "자네 형 이반은 무신론자면서도, 무슨 알 수 없는 멍청한 동기로 신학 논문을 장난삼아 쓰고, 게다가 자기 입으로 그게 사기라고 인정하지—그게 자네 형 이반이야."], ["He’s trying to get Mitya’s betrothed for himself, and I fancy he’ll succeed, too.", "그자는 미탸의 약혼녀를 제 것으로 차지하려 하고 있고, 내 보기엔 성공할 것도 같아."], ["And what’s more, it’s with Mitya’s consent.", "더구나 그게 미탸의 동의를 얻어서란 말이지."], ["For Mitya will surrender his betrothed to him to be rid of her, and escape to Grushenka.", "미탸야 그 여자를 떨쳐 버리고 그루셴카에게 도망치려고 약혼녀를 그자에게 넘겨줄 테니까."], ["And he’s ready to do that in spite of all his nobility and disinterestedness.", "그리고 그자는 그 온갖 고결함과 무사무욕에도 불구하고 기꺼이 그럴 채비가 돼 있단 말이야."], ["Observe that.", "잘 보게."], ["Those are the most fatal people!", "그런 자들이야말로 가장 끔찍한 인간들이지!"], ["Who the devil can make you out?", "자넨 도대체 그자를 알아볼 수가 없어!"], ["He recognizes his vileness and goes on with it!", "그자는 제 비열함을 인정하면서도 계속 그걸 밀고 나간단 말이야!"], ["Let me tell you, too, the old man, your father, is standing in Mitya’s way now.", "한 가지 더 말해 주지, 자네 아버지, 그 늙은이가 지금 미탸의 앞길을 가로막고 있다네."], ["He has suddenly gone crazy over Grushenka.", "그 늙은이가 그루셴카에게 갑자기 미쳐 버렸거든."], ["His mouth waters at the sight of her.", "그 여자만 보면 입에 군침을 흘린다니까."], ["It’s simply on her account he made that scene in the cell just now, simply because Miüsov called her an ‘abandoned creature.’ He’s worse than a tom-cat in love.", "아까 방에서 그런 소동을 벌인 것도 순전히 그 여자 때문이었어, 단지 미우소프가 그 여자를 '막돼먹은 계집'이라고 불렀다는 이유로 말이지. 그자는 사랑에 빠진 수고양이보다도 못한 작자야."], ["At first she was only employed by him in connection with his taverns and in some other shady business, but now he has suddenly realized all she is and has gone wild about her.", "처음에 그 여자는 그저 그자의 술집 일이나 다른 구린 사업에 부려졌을 뿐인데, 이제 그자는 그 여자가 어떤 존재인지 별안간 깨닫고 그 여자에게 미쳐 날뛰는 거지."], ["He keeps pestering her with his offers, not honorable ones, of course.", "그자는 그 여자에게 끊임없이 제안을 들이밀며 귀찮게 굴어, 물론 떳떳한 제안은 아니고 말이지."], ["And they’ll come into collision, the precious father and son, on that path!", "그러니 이 잘난 부자가 그 길 위에서 정면으로 충돌하고 말 거란 말일세!"], ["But Grushenka favors neither of them, she’s still playing with them, and teasing them both, considering which she can get most out of.", "하지만 그루셴카는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 편들지 않아, 여전히 둘을 가지고 놀면서 어느 쪽에게서 더 많이 우려낼 수 있을까 따져 보며 둘 다 애를 태우고 있지."], ["For though she could filch a lot of money from the papa he wouldn’t marry her, and maybe he’ll turn stingy in the end, and keep his purse shut.", "아비한테서야 큰돈을 우려낼 수 있겠지만 그자가 그 여자와 결혼하진 않을 테고, 어쩌면 결국 인색하게 굴며 지갑을 꽁꽁 닫아 버릴지도 모르거든."], ["That’s where Mitya’s value comes in; he has no money, but he’s ready to marry her.", "바로 거기서 미탸의 값어치가 빛을 발하는 거야. 그자는 돈은 없어도 그 여자와 결혼할 채비가 돼 있으니까."], ["Yes, ready to marry her!", "그래, 결혼할 채비가 돼 있단 말이지!"], ["to abandon his betrothed, a rare beauty, Katerina Ivanovna, who’s rich, and the daughter of a colonel, and to marry Grushenka, who has been the mistress of a dissolute old merchant, Samsonov, a coarse, uneducated, provincial mayor.", "부유하고 대령의 딸인, 보기 드문 미녀 카테리나 이바노브나라는 약혼녀를 버리고, 방탕한 늙은 상인 삼소노프—거칠고 무식한 시골 시장 나부랭이의 정부 노릇을 하던 그루셴카와 결혼하겠다는 거야."], ["Some murderous conflict may well come to pass from all this, and that’s what your brother Ivan is waiting for.", "이 모든 일에서 살인의 충돌이 벌어질 만도 하고, 바로 그것이 자네 형 이반이 기다리는 바라네."], ["It would suit him down to the ground.", "그자에게는 더없이 안성맞춤일 테니까."], ["He’ll carry off Katerina Ivanovna, for whom he is languishing, and pocket her dowry of sixty thousand.", "그자는 애타게 그리는 카테리나 이바노브나를 채어 가고, 그 여자의 육만 루블 지참금을 챙길 거야."], ["That’s very alluring to start with, for a man of no consequence and a beggar.", "그것만으로도 보잘것없고 거지 신세인 사내에겐 무척 솔깃한 일이지."], ["And, take note, he won’t be wronging Mitya, but doing him the greatest service.", "게다가 잘 보게, 그자는 미탸에게 해를 끼치는 게 아니라 도리어 더없이 큰 봉사를 베푸는 셈이 되거든."], ["For I know as a fact that Mitya only last week, when he was with some gypsy girls drunk in a tavern, cried out aloud that he was unworthy of his betrothed, Katya, but that his brother Ivan, he was the man who deserved her.", "사실인즉 바로 지난주에 미탸가 술에 취해 어느 술집에서 집시 계집들과 어울리다가 큰 소리로 외쳤다는 걸 내가 똑똑히 아는데, 자기는 약혼녀 카탸에게 어울리지 않는 자이고 형 이반이야말로 그 여자를 차지할 만한 사내라고 했다는 거야."], ["And Katerina Ivanovna will not in the end refuse such a fascinating man as Ivan.", "그리고 카테리나 이바노브나도 결국에는 이반처럼 매혹적인 사내를 거절하진 못할 거란 말이지."], ["She’s hesitating between the two of them already.", "그 여자는 이미 둘 사이에서 망설이고 있거든."], ["And how has that Ivan won you all, so that you all worship him?", "그런데 그 이반이란 자가 어떻게 자네들 모두를 사로잡아서, 자네들이 다 그자를 떠받들게 만든 걸까?"], ["He is laughing at you, and enjoying himself at your expense.”", "그자는 자네들을 비웃으며 자네들을 발판 삼아 즐기고 있단 말이야.\""]], [["“How do you know?", "\"자넨 그걸 어떻게 아나?"], ["How can you speak so confidently?”", "어떻게 그렇게 장담할 수 있지?\""], ["Alyosha asked sharply, frowning.", "알료샤가 미간을 찌푸리며 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["“Why do you ask, and are frightened at my answer?", "\"왜 묻는 건가, 게다가 내 대답을 두려워하면서 말이지?"], ["It shows that you know I’m speaking the truth.”", "그건 내가 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 자네가 안다는 증거야.\""]], [["“You don’t like Ivan.", "\"자넨 이반을 좋아하지 않아."], ["Ivan wouldn’t be tempted by money.”", "이반은 돈에 넘어갈 사람이 아니야.\""]], [["“Really?", "\"정말로?"], ["And the beauty of Katerina Ivanovna?", "그럼 카테리나 이바노브나의 미모는?"], ["It’s not only the money, though a fortune of sixty thousand is an attraction.”", "육만이라는 재산이 솔깃한 거야 사실이지만, 비단 돈만은 아니란 말이지.\""]], [["“Ivan is above that.", "\"이반은 그런 것을 초월한 사람이야."], ["He wouldn’t make up to any one for thousands.", "그는 몇 천 루블 때문에 누구한테 아부할 사람이 아니야."], ["It is not money, it’s not comfort Ivan is seeking.", "이반이 좇는 건 돈도 아니고 안락함도 아니야."], ["Perhaps it’s suffering he is seeking.”", "어쩌면 그가 좇는 건 고통일지도 몰라.\""]], [["“What wild dream now?", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 허황된 꿈인가?"], ["Oh, you—aristocrats!”", "오, 자네들—귀족 나리들이란!\""]], [["“Ah, Misha, he has a stormy spirit.", "\"아, 미샤, 그는 폭풍 같은 영혼을 지녔어."], ["His mind is in bondage.", "그의 정신은 속박돼 있지."], ["He is haunted by a great, unsolved doubt.", "그는 거대한, 풀리지 않는 의문에 시달리고 있어."], ["He is one of those who don’t want millions, but an answer to their questions.”", "그는 수백만 루블을 바라는 게 아니라 제 물음에 대한 답을 바라는 그런 부류의 사람이라네.\""]], [["“That’s plagiarism, Alyosha.", "\"그건 표절이야, 알료샤."], ["You’re quoting your elder’s phrases.", "자넨 자네네 장로의 말투를 그대로 인용하고 있잖아."], ["Ah, Ivan has set you a problem!”", "아하, 이반이 자네에게 문제를 하나 던졌구먼!\""], ["cried Rakitin, with undisguised malice.", "라키틴이 노골적인 악의를 드러내며 소리쳤다. 그의 얼굴빛이 변하고 입술이 씰룩거렸다."], ["His face changed, and his lips twitched.", "\"게다가 그 문제라는 게 멍청하기 짝이 없어. 알아맞히려고 애쓸 것도 없지."], ["“And the problem’s a stupid one.", "골머리를 쥐어짜 보게나—이해하게 될 테니."], ["It is no good guessing it.", "그자의 논문은 터무니없고 우스꽝스러워."], ["Rack your brains—you’ll understand it.", "자네도 아까 그자의 멍청한 이론을 들었잖나."], ["His article is absurd and ridiculous.", "영혼의 불멸이 없다면 미덕도 없고, 모든 것이 허용된다는 거 말이야."], ["And did you hear his stupid theory just now: if there’s no immortality of the soul, then there’s no virtue, and everything is lawful.", "(그건 그렇고, 자네 형 미탸가 '나는 기억하겠다!'고 외쳤던 걸 기억하나?) 악당들에게 솔깃한 이론이지!—(내가 욕을 하는군, 멍청한 짓이야.) 악당들이 아니라, '심오하고 풀리지 않는 의문에 시달리는' 현학적인 허세꾼들에게나 솔깃한 거지."], ["(And by the way, do you remember how your brother Mitya cried out: ‘I will remember!’) An attractive theory for scoundrels!—(I’m being abusive, that’s stupid.) Not for scoundrels, but for pedantic poseurs, ‘haunted by profound, unsolved doubts.’ He’s showing off, and what it all comes to is, ‘on the one hand we cannot but admit’ and ‘on the other it must be confessed!’ His whole theory is a fraud!", "그자는 잘난 척을 하는 거고, 결국 그게 다 무슨 소리냐 하면, '한편으로는 인정하지 않을 수 없으나' '다른 한편으로는 고백해야만 한다!'"], ["Humanity will find in itself the power to live for virtue even without believing in immortality.", "따위 헛소리란 말일세. 그자의 이론은 통째로 사기야!"], ["It will find it in love for freedom, for equality, for fraternity.”", "인류는 불멸을 믿지 않고도 미덕을 위해 살아갈 힘을 제 안에서 찾아낼 거야. 자유와 평등과 형제애에 대한 사랑 속에서 그걸 찾아낼 거란 말이지.\""]], [["Rakitin could hardly restrain himself in his heat, but, suddenly, as though remembering something, he stopped short.", "라키틴은 그 격정에 못 이겨 좀처럼 자신을 억누르지 못하다가, 별안간 무언가 생각난 듯 뚝 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Well, that’s enough,”", "\"자, 이쯤 하면 됐네.\""], ["he said, with a still more crooked smile.", "그가 한층 더 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Why are you laughing?", "\"왜 웃나?"], ["Do you think I’m a vulgar fool?”", "내가 천박한 바보로 보이나?\""]], [["“No, I never dreamed of thinking you a vulgar fool.", "\"아니, 자넬 천박한 바보라고는 꿈에도 생각지 않았네."], ["You are clever but ...", "자넨 영리해, 하지만…"], ["never mind, I was silly to smile.", "아무튼 신경 쓰지 말게, 웃은 건 내가 어리석었어."], ["I understand your getting hot about it, Misha.", "자네가 그 일에 흥분하는 걸 이해하네, 미샤."], ["I guess from your warmth that you are not indifferent to Katerina Ivanovna yourself; I’ve suspected that for a long time, brother, that’s why you don’t like my brother Ivan.", "그 열을 보니 자네 자신도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 무관심하지 않은 모양이군. 진작부터 그런 의심을 했었네, 형제, 그래서 자네가 내 형 이반을 좋아하지 않는 거지."], ["Are you jealous of him?”", "자넨 그를 질투하는 건가?\""]], [["“And jealous of her money, too?", "\"게다가 그 여자의 돈까지 질투한다고?"], ["Won’t you add that?”", "그 말은 안 덧붙이려나?\""]], [["“I’ll say nothing about money.", "\"돈에 대해선 아무 말도 않겠네."], ["I am not going to insult you.”", "자넬 모욕하려는 게 아니니까.\""]], [["“I believe it, since you say so, but confound you, and your brother Ivan with you.", "\"자네가 그렇게 말하니 믿어 주지, 하지만 자네도 자네 형 이반도 다 빌어먹을 인간들이야."], ["Don’t you understand that one might very well dislike him, apart from Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나와는 별개로 그자를 충분히 싫어할 수 있다는 걸 모르겠나?"], ["And why the devil should I like him?", "대체 내가 무슨 까닭으로 그자를 좋아해야 한단 말인가?"], ["He condescends to abuse me, you know.", "그자는 황송하게도 나를 깎아내리시거든."], ["Why haven’t I a right to abuse him?”", "그렇담 나라고 그자를 깎아내릴 권리가 없으란 법이 어디 있나?\""]], [["“I never heard of his saying anything about you, good or bad.", "\"난 그가 자네에 대해 좋게든 나쁘게든 무슨 말을 했단 걸 들어 본 적이 없네."], ["He doesn’t speak of you at all.”", "그는 자네 얘기를 아예 입에 올리질 않아.\""]], [["“But I heard that the day before yesterday at Katerina Ivanovna’s he was abusing me for all he was worth—you see what an interest he takes in your humble servant.", "\"하지만 그저께 카테리나 이바노브나 댁에서 그자가 나를 있는 대로 욕해 댔다는 걸 들었네—자, 그자가 이 미천한 종놈한테 얼마나 관심이 많은지 보게나."], ["And which is the jealous one after that, brother, I can’t say.", "그러니 둘 중 누가 질투하는 건지, 형제, 난 모르겠네."], ["He was so good as to express the opinion that, if I don’t go in for the career of an archimandrite in the immediate future and don’t become a monk, I shall be sure to go to Petersburg and get on to some solid magazine as a reviewer, that I shall write for the next ten years, and in the end become the owner of the magazine, and bring it out on the liberal and atheistic side, with a socialistic tinge, with a tiny gloss of socialism, but keeping a sharp look out all the time, that is, keeping in with both sides and hoodwinking the fools.", "그자는 황송하게도 이런 의견을 늘어놓으셨다더군. 내가 가까운 장래에 수도원장 자리를 노리는 길에 들어서서 수도사가 되지 않는다면, 분명 페테르부르크로 가서 어느 탄탄한 잡지에 평론가로 들어앉을 거고, 앞으로 십 년 동안 글을 쓰다가 끝내는 그 잡지의 주인이 되어, 사회주의의 옅은 빛깔을 입힌, 아주 자그마한 사회주의의 광택을 곁들인 자유주의적이고 무신론적인 노선으로 그 잡지를 펴내되, 내내 양쪽을 다 살피며—그러니까 양쪽 진영에 발을 걸쳐 두고 멍청이들을 속여 가며—날카롭게 눈치를 살필 거라더군."], ["According to your brother’s account, the tinge of socialism won’t hinder me from laying by the proceeds and investing them under the guidance of some Jew, till at the end of my career I build a great house in Petersburg and move my publishing offices to it, and let out the upper stories to lodgers.", "자네 형 말로는, 그 옅은 사회주의 빛깔도 내가 수익을 챙겨 어느 유대인의 지도 아래 투자하는 데에는 아무 방해도 되지 않을 거고, 그렇게 해서 내 경력의 끝에 가서는 페테르부르크에 으리으리한 저택을 짓고 거기로 출판사를 옮긴 다음, 위층은 세놓아 먹게 될 거라는 거지."], ["He has even chosen the place for it, near the new stone bridge across the Neva, which they say is to be built in Petersburg.”", "그자는 그 저택 자리까지 골라 놓았다더군, 페테르부르크에 새로 놓는다는 네바강의 새 돌다리 근처로 말이야.\""]], [["“Ah, Misha, that’s just what will really happen, every word of it,”", "\"아, 미샤, 그건 정말로 그대로 일어날 일이야, 한마디 한마디 죄다.\""], ["cried Alyosha, unable to restrain a good-humored smile.", "알료샤가 흐뭇한 미소를 참지 못하고 소리쳤다."]], [["“You are pleased to be sarcastic, too, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”", "\"자네도 빈정거리시는군그래, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""]], [["“No, no, I’m joking, forgive me.", "\"아니, 아니야, 농담이야, 용서하게."], ["I’ve something quite different in my mind.", "내 머릿속엔 전혀 다른 생각이 있었어."], ["But, excuse me, who can have told you all this?", "그런데 말이지, 미안하지만, 누가 그 얘기를 다 자네한테 해 줄 수 있었던 거지?"], ["You can’t have been at Katerina Ivanovna’s yourself when he was talking about you?”", "그자가 자네 얘기를 할 때 설마 자네가 직접 카테리나 이바노브나 댁에 있었던 건 아닐 테고?\""]], [["“I wasn’t there, but Dmitri Fyodorovitch was; and I heard him tell it with my own ears; if you want to know, he didn’t tell me, but I overheard him, unintentionally, of course, for I was sitting in Grushenka’s bedroom and I couldn’t go away because Dmitri Fyodorovitch was in the next room.”", "\"난 거기 없었지만, 드미트리 표도로비치가 있었네. 그리고 난 내 두 귀로 그가 그 얘기를 하는 걸 들었지. 정 알고 싶다면 말해 주는데, 그가 내게 해 준 게 아니라 내가 우연히 엿들은 거야, 물론 일부러는 아니고. 내가 그루셴카의 침실에 앉아 있었는데 드미트리 표도로비치가 옆방에 있는 바람에 자리를 뜰 수가 없었거든.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I’d forgotten she was a relation of yours.”", "\"아, 그래, 그 여자가 자네 친척이라는 걸 깜빡했군.\""]], [["“A relation!", "\"친척이라니!"], ["That Grushenka a relation of mine!”", "그 그루셴카가 내 친척이라고!\""], ["cried Rakitin, turning crimson.", "라키틴이 새빨개지며 소리쳤다."], ["“Are you mad?", "\"자네 미쳤나?"], ["You’re out of your mind!”", "정신이 나갔군!\""]], [["“Why, isn’t she a relation of yours?", "\"아니, 그 여자가 자네 친척 아닌가?"], ["I heard so.”", "난 그렇게 들었는데.\""]], [["“Where can you have heard it?", "\"대체 어디서 그런 소릴 들었나?"], ["You Karamazovs brag of being an ancient, noble family, though your father used to run about playing the buffoon at other men’s tables, and was only admitted to the kitchen as a favor.", "자네네 카라마조프 집안은 유서 깊은 귀족 가문이라고 으스대지만, 자네 아버지로 말하자면 남의 집 식탁이나 전전하며 광대 노릇을 하고 다녔고, 그것도 선심 써서 부엌에나 들여보내 줬던 작자란 말이지."], ["I may be only a priest’s son, and dirt in the eyes of noblemen like you, but don’t insult me so lightly and wantonly.", "나야 한낱 사제의 아들로, 자네 같은 귀족 나리들 눈엔 흙먼지에 지나지 않을지 모르지만, 그렇게 함부로 까닭 없이 나를 모욕하진 말게."], ["I have a sense of honor, too, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I couldn’t be a relation of Grushenka, a common harlot.", "나에게도 명예심이라는 게 있어, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 천한 갈보 그루셴카 따위의 친척일 수가 없네."], ["I beg you to understand that!”", "그 점을 좀 알아 두게나!\""]], [["Rakitin was intensely irritated.", "라키틴은 몹시 발끈해 있었다."]], [["“Forgive me, for goodness’ sake, I had no idea ...", "\"제발 용서하게, 난 전혀 몰랐어… 게다가…"], ["besides ... how can you call her a harlot?", "어떻게 자네는 그 여자를 갈보라고 부를 수가 있나?"], ["Is she ...", "그 여자가…"], ["that sort of woman?”", "그런 부류의 여자인가?\""], ["Alyosha flushed suddenly.", "알료샤가 별안간 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“I tell you again, I heard that she was a relation of yours.", "\"다시 말하지만, 난 그 여자가 자네 친척이라고 들었어."], ["You often go to see her, and you told me yourself you’re not her lover.", "자넨 그 여자를 자주 찾아가고, 게다가 자네 입으로 그 여자의 정부는 아니라고 했잖아."], ["I never dreamed that you of all people had such contempt for her!", "다른 누구도 아닌 자네가 그 여자를 그렇게 경멸할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐네!"], ["Does she really deserve it?”", "정말 그 여자가 그런 경멸을 받아 마땅한가?\""]], [["“I may have reasons of my own for visiting her.", "\"내가 그 여자를 찾아가는 데엔 나름의 이유가 있을 수도 있지."], ["That’s not your business.", "그건 자네가 상관할 바 아니야."], ["But as for relationship, your brother, or even your father, is more likely to make her yours than mine.", "하지만 친척으로 말하자면, 나보다는 자네 형이, 아니 자네 아버지가 그 여자를 자네 친척으로 만들 가능성이 더 크지."], ["Well, here we are.", "자, 다 왔군."], ["You’d better go to the kitchen.", "자넨 어서 부엌으로 가는 게 좋겠어."], ["Hullo!", "어라!"], ["what’s wrong, what is it?", "무슨 일이지, 이게 뭐야?"], ["Are we late?", "우리가 늦은 건가?"], ["They can’t have finished dinner so soon!", "만찬이 이렇게 빨리 끝났을 리가 없는데!"], ["Have the Karamazovs been making trouble again?", "카라마조프 집안에서 또 한바탕 소동을 벌인 건가?"], ["No doubt they have.", "틀림없이 그랬겠지."], ["Here’s your father and your brother Ivan after him.", "저기 자네 아버지가 있고 그 뒤로 자네 형 이반이 따라 나오는군."], ["They’ve broken out from the Father Superior’s.", "둘이 원장 신부님 처소에서 뛰쳐나왔어."], ["And look, Father Isidor’s shouting out something after them from the steps.", "그리고 저것 봐, 이시도르 신부님이 계단에서 그들 뒤에다 대고 뭐라고 소리치고 있잖아."], ["And your father’s shouting and waving his arms.", "자네 아버지도 소리치며 팔을 휘저어 대는군."], ["I expect he’s swearing.", "욕을 퍼붓고 있는 모양이야."], ["Bah, and there goes Miüsov driving away in his carriage.", "어허, 저기 미우소프가 마차를 타고 떠나는군."], ["You see, he’s going.", "봐, 가 버리잖아."], ["And there’s old Maximov running!—there must have been a row.", "그리고 저기 늙은 막시모프가 달려가네!—틀림없이 한바탕 난리가 났던 거야."], ["There can’t have been any dinner.", "만찬이 제대로 됐을 리가 없지."], ["Surely they’ve not been beating the Father Superior!", "설마 원장 신부님을 두들겨 패기야 했겠나!"], ["Or have they, perhaps, been beaten?", "아니면 혹시, 그들이 도리어 얻어맞은 건가?"], ["It would serve them right!”", "그래도 싸지!\""]], [["There was reason for Rakitin’s exclamations.", "라키틴의 이런 탄성에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있었다."], ["There had been a scandalous, an unprecedented scene.", "추문이라 할 만한, 전례 없는 소동이 벌어졌던 것이다."], ["It had all come from the impulse of a moment.", "그것은 모두 한순간의 충동에서 비롯된 일이었다."]], [["# Chapter VIII. The Scandalous Scene", "# Chapter VIII. The Scandalous Scene"]], [["Miüsov, as a man of breeding and delicacy, could not but feel some inward qualms, when he reached the Father Superior’s with Ivan: he felt ashamed of having lost his temper.", "미우소프는 교양 있고 섬세한 사람인지라, 이반과 함께 수도원장의 처소에 이르렀을 때 내심 적잖은 가책을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으니, 그는 자제력을 잃은 것이 부끄러웠다."], ["He felt that he ought to have disdained that despicable wretch, Fyodor Pavlovitch, too much to have been upset by him in Father Zossima’s cell, and so to have forgotten himself.", "그 비열한 작자 표도르 파블로비치 따위는 멸시하고 무시했어야 마땅했거늘, 조시마 장로의 암자에서 그자에게 휘둘려 자기 자신을 잊어버린 것이 부끄러웠다."], ["“The monks were not to blame, in any case,”", "\"어쨌든 수도사들에게야 무슨 잘못이 있겠는가.\""], ["he reflected, on the steps.", "그는 층계를 오르며 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“And if they’re decent people here (and the Father Superior, I understand, is a nobleman) why not be friendly and courteous with them?", "\"여기 사람들이 점잖은 이들이라면—듣자 하니 수도원장도 귀족 출신이라고 하지 않던가—어찌 그들에게 친절하고 정중하게 대하지 않을 까닭이 있겠는가?"], ["I won’t argue, I’ll fall in with everything, I’ll win them by politeness, and ...", "따지지 않으리라, 무엇이든 맞춰 주리라, 예의로써 그들의 호의를 사리라, 그리고…"], ["and ...", "그리고…"], ["show them that I’ve nothing to do with that Æsop, that buffoon, that Pierrot, and have merely been taken in over this affair, just as they have.”", "저 이솝 같은 작자, 저 어릿광대, 저 피에로와 나는 아무 상관이 없으며, 나 역시 저들과 똑같이 이 일에 그저 끌려들었을 뿐임을 보여 주리라.\""]], [["He determined to drop his litigation with the monastery, and relinquish his claims to the wood-cutting and fishery rights at once.", "그는 수도원과의 소송을 그만두고 벌목권과 어업권에 대한 자기 청구를 당장 포기하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He was the more ready to do this because the rights had become much less valuable, and he had indeed the vaguest idea where the wood and river in question were.", "그가 이리하기로 한 데에는 그 권리들이 이제는 훨씬 값어치가 떨어진 데다, 정작 문제가 되는 숲과 강이 어디쯤에 있는지조차 자기로서는 도무지 가늠이 가지 않는다는 사정도 한몫했다."]], [["These excellent intentions were strengthened when he entered the Father Superior’s dining-room, though, strictly speaking, it was not a dining-room, for the Father Superior had only two rooms altogether; they were, however, much larger and more comfortable than Father Zossima’s.", "이 갸륵한 결심은 수도원장의 식당으로 들어서자 한층 굳어졌다. 하기야 엄밀히 말하면 그것은 식당이라 할 수 없었으니, 수도원장의 거처라야 도합 방이 둘뿐이었기 때문이다."], ["But there was no great luxury about the furnishing of these rooms either.", "그러나 그 방들은 조시마 장로의 암자보다 훨씬 넓고 안락했다."], ["The furniture was of mahogany, covered with leather, in the old-fashioned style of 1820; the floor was not even stained, but everything was shining with cleanliness, and there were many choice flowers in the windows; the most sumptuous thing in the room at the moment was, of course, the beautifully decorated table.", "그렇다 해도 이 방들의 세간이 그리 호사스러운 것은 아니었다. 가구는 가죽을 씌운 마호가니로, 1820년대의 고풍스러운 양식이었고, 마룻바닥에는 칠조차 되어 있지 않았으나, 모든 것이 청결하게 빛나고 있었으며, 창가에는 진귀한 꽃들이 즐비했다."], ["The cloth was clean, the service shone; there were three kinds of well-baked bread, two bottles of wine, two of excellent mead, and a large glass jug of kvas—both the latter made in the monastery, and famous in the neighborhood.", "지금 이 방에서 가장 호화로운 것은 물론 아름답게 차려진 식탁이었다. 식탁보는 정갈했고 식기는 반짝였으며, 잘 구운 빵이 세 종류, 포도주가 두 병, 빼어난 봉밀주가 두 병, 그리고 크바스가 큼직한 유리 단지로 하나 놓여 있었는데—이 뒤의 두 가지는 수도원에서 직접 빚은 것으로 인근에서 이름이 자자한 것이었다."], ["There was no vodka.", "보드카는 없었다."], ["Rakitin related afterwards that there were five dishes: fish-soup made of sterlets, served with little fish patties; then boiled fish served in a special way; then salmon cutlets, ice pudding and compote, and finally, blanc-mange.", "라키틴이 나중에 전한 바로는 요리가 다섯 가지였다고 한다. 쇠철갑상어로 끓인 생선 수프에 자그마한 생선 파이를 곁들인 것, 그다음에는 특별한 방식으로 삶아 낸 생선, 그다음에는 연어 커틀릿, 얼음 푸딩과 콤포트, 그리고 마지막으로 블랑망제였다."], ["Rakitin found out about all these good things, for he could not resist peeping into the kitchen, where he already had a footing.", "라키틴이 이 온갖 좋은 음식들을 죄다 알아낸 것은, 자기가 이미 발을 들여놓은 부엌을 기어이 들여다보지 않고는 못 배기는 위인이었기 때문이다."], ["He had a footing everywhere, and got information about everything.", "그는 어디에나 발을 들여놓았고 무엇이든 정보를 캐냈다."], ["He was of an uneasy and envious temper.", "그는 불안하고 시기심 많은 성미였다."], ["He was well aware of his own considerable abilities, and nervously exaggerated them in his self-conceit.", "그는 자기에게 제법 대단한 재능이 있음을 잘 알았고, 자만심에서 그것을 신경질적으로 부풀려 생각했다."], ["He knew he would play a prominent part of some sort, but Alyosha, who was attached to him, was distressed to see that his friend Rakitin was dishonorable, and quite unconscious of being so himself, considering, on the contrary, that because he would not steal money left on the table he was a man of the highest integrity.", "그는 자기가 무언가 두드러진 역할을 하게 되리라는 것을 알았으나, 그에게 정을 붙이고 있던 알료샤로서는 자기 친구 라키틴이 비루한 인간이면서도 정작 자신은 그런 줄을 전혀 깨닫지 못한 채, 도리어 식탁에 놓인 돈을 훔치지 않는다는 이유만으로 자기를 더없이 청렴한 사람이라 여기는 것을 보며 마음 아파했다."], ["Neither Alyosha nor any one else could have influenced him in that.", "그 점에서는 알료샤도, 그 누구도 그를 어찌해 볼 도리가 없었다."]], [["Rakitin, of course, was a person of too little consequence to be invited to the dinner, to which Father Iosif, Father Païssy, and one other monk were the only inmates of the monastery invited.", "물론 라키틴은 만찬에 초대받기에는 너무도 보잘것없는 인물이었으니, 수도원의 식구들 가운데 초대받은 이는 이오시프 신부와 파이시 신부, 그리고 또 다른 수도사 한 사람뿐이었다."], ["They were already waiting when Miüsov, Kalganov, and Ivan arrived.", "미우소프와 칼가노프와 이반이 당도했을 때 그들은 이미 기다리고 있었다."], ["The other guest, Maximov, stood a little aside, waiting also.", "또 한 사람의 손님인 막시모프는 한쪽에 비켜선 채 역시 기다리고 있었다."], ["The Father Superior stepped into the middle of the room to receive his guests.", "수도원장은 손님들을 맞이하려고 방 한가운데로 걸어 나왔다."], ["He was a tall, thin, but still vigorous old man, with black hair streaked with gray, and a long, grave, ascetic face.", "그는 키가 크고 야위었으나 아직 정정한 노인으로, 검은 머리에 희끗희끗 흰 것이 섞여 있었고, 길쭉하고 근엄한 고행자의 얼굴을 지니고 있었다."], ["He bowed to his guests in silence.", "그는 말없이 손님들에게 머리를 숙였다."], ["But this time they approached to receive his blessing.", "그러나 이번에는 손님들 쪽에서 그의 축복을 받으려고 다가갔다."], ["Miüsov even tried to kiss his hand, but the Father Superior drew it back in time to avoid the salute.", "미우소프는 그의 손에 입을 맞추려고까지 했으나, 수도원장이 제때 손을 거두어 그 인사를 피했다."], ["But Ivan and Kalganov went through the ceremony in the most simple-hearted and complete manner, kissing his hand as peasants do.", "그러나 이반과 칼가노프는 더없이 순박하고 빈틈없는 태도로 그 의식을 치러, 농부들이 하듯 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“We must apologize most humbly, your reverence,”", "\"저희가 황공하옵게도 머리 숙여 사죄드려야겠습니다, 신부님.\""], ["began Miüsov, simpering affably, and speaking in a dignified and respectful tone.", "미우소프가 싹싹하게 미소 지으며 위엄 있고 공손한 어조로 입을 열었다."], ["“Pardon us for having come alone without the gentleman you invited, Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "\"초대받으신 분, 표도르 파블로비치를 모시지 못하고 저희만 온 것을 용서해 주십시오."], ["He felt obliged to decline the honor of your hospitality, and not without reason.", "그분은 환대의 영광을 사양해야겠다고 느끼셨는데, 그럴 만한 까닭이 없지 않았습니다."], ["In the reverend Father Zossima’s cell he was carried away by the unhappy dissension with his son, and let fall words which were quite out of keeping ...", "존경하옵는 조시마 장로님의 암자에서 그분은 아들과의 불행한 불화에 휩쓸려, 도무지 어울리지 않는…"], ["in fact, quite unseemly ...", "사실인즉 매우 점잖지 못한 말씀을…"], ["as”—he glanced at the monks—“your reverence is, no doubt, already aware.", "하셨으니\"—그는 수도사들을 흘끗 보았다—\"신부님께서도 이미 알고 계실 줄 압니다."], ["And therefore, recognizing that he had been to blame, he felt sincere regret and shame, and begged me, and his son Ivan Fyodorovitch, to convey to you his apologies and regrets.", "그리하여 자기가 잘못했음을 깨닫고 진심으로 후회하며 부끄러워하시며, 저와 그 아드님 이반 표도로비치에게 부디 사죄와 유감의 뜻을 신부님께 전해 달라고 간청하셨습니다."], ["In brief, he hopes and desires to make amends later.", "한마디로 말씀드리면, 그분은 훗날 그 허물을 보상하기를 바라고 또 그러기를 원하십니다."], ["He asks your blessing, and begs you to forget what has taken place.”", "신부님의 축복을 청하며, 일어난 일은 부디 잊어 주십사 빌고 계십니다.\""]], [["As he uttered the last word of his tirade, Miüsov completely recovered his self-complacency, and all traces of his former irritation disappeared.", "이 일장 연설의 마지막 말을 내뱉을 즈음 미우소프는 완전히 자기만족을 되찾았고, 조금 전의 노여움은 자취도 없이 사라졌다."], ["He fully and sincerely loved humanity again.", "그는 다시금 온전하고 진심으로 인류를 사랑하게 되었다."]], [["The Father Superior listened to him with dignity, and, with a slight bend of the head, replied:", "수도원장은 위엄 있게 그의 말을 듣고 나서, 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다."]], [["“I sincerely deplore his absence.", "\"그분이 오시지 못한 것을 진심으로 애석하게 여깁니다."], ["Perhaps at our table he might have learnt to like us, and we him.", "어쩌면 우리 식탁에서 그분이 우리를 좋아하게 되고, 우리 또한 그분을 좋아하게 되었을지도 모르는 일이지요."], ["Pray be seated, gentlemen.”", "자, 앉으시지요, 여러분.\""]], [["He stood before the holy image, and began to say grace, aloud.", "그는 성상 앞에 서서 소리 내어 식전 기도를 올리기 시작했다."], ["All bent their heads reverently, and Maximov clasped his hands before him, with peculiar fervor.", "모두가 경건히 머리를 숙였고, 막시모프는 유난스러운 열성으로 두 손을 가슴 앞에 모았다."]], [["It was at this moment that Fyodor Pavlovitch played his last prank.", "바로 이 순간 표도르 파블로비치가 마지막 장난을 벌였다."], ["It must be noted that he really had meant to go home, and really had felt the impossibility of going to dine with the Father Superior as though nothing had happened, after his disgraceful behavior in the elder’s cell.", "한 가지 짚어 둘 것은, 그가 정말로 집에 돌아가려 했었고, 장로의 암자에서 그 망신스러운 추태를 부린 뒤에 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 수도원장과 마주 앉아 만찬을 든다는 것은 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라고 진정으로 느꼈었다는 점이다."], ["Not that he was so very much ashamed of himself—quite the contrary perhaps.", "자기 자신이 그토록 부끄러웠던 것은 아니었으니—어쩌면 도리어 그 반대였으리라."], ["But still he felt it would be unseemly to go to dinner.", "그래도 만찬에 가는 것은 점잖지 못하다고 느끼기는 했다."], ["Yet his creaking carriage had hardly been brought to the steps of the hotel, and he had hardly got into it, when he suddenly stopped short.", "그러나 삐걱거리는 그의 마차가 여관 층계 앞에 닿기가 무섭게, 그가 그 안에 올라타기가 무섭게, 그는 문득 동작을 딱 멈추었다. 그는 장로의 암자에서 자기가 했던 말을 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered his own words at the elder’s: “I always feel when I meet people that I am lower than all, and that they all take me for a buffoon; so I say let me play the buffoon, for you are, every one of you, stupider and lower than I.”", "\"나는 사람들을 만날 때면 늘 내가 누구보다도 못난 놈이고, 다들 나를 어릿광대로 여긴다고 느낀단 말이오. 그러니 어디 한번 어릿광대 노릇을 해 보자, 너희야말로 하나같이 나보다 더 어리석고 더 못난 놈들이니까, 하고 마음먹는단 말이오.\""], ["He longed to revenge himself on every one for his own unseemliness.", "그는 자기 자신의 추태에 대한 앙갚음을 모든 사람에게 하고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["He suddenly recalled how he had once in the past been asked, “Why do you hate so and so, so much?”", "그는 문득 언젠가 누군가가 자기에게 \"당신은 어째서 아무개를 그토록 미워하시오?\" 하고 물었던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["And he had answered them, with his shameless impudence, “I’ll tell you.", "그러자 그는 뻔뻔스럽고 염치없이 이렇게 대답했었다. \"말해 주리다."], ["He has done me no harm.", "그자가 나에게 해코지를 한 적은 없소."], ["But I played him a dirty trick, and ever since I have hated him.”", "다만 내가 그자에게 더러운 짓을 한 번 했더니, 그때부터 줄곧 그자가 미워지더란 말이오.\""]], [["Remembering that now, he smiled quietly and malignantly, hesitating for a moment.", "이제 그것을 떠올리며 그는 조용히, 그리고 음흉하게 미소 지으며 잠시 망설였다."], ["His eyes gleamed, and his lips positively quivered.", "그의 두 눈이 번득였고, 입술은 영락없이 부르르 떨렸다."], ["“Well, since I have begun, I may as well go on,”", "\"그래, 이왕 시작한 김에 끝까지 가 보는 게 낫지.\""], ["he decided.", "그는 그렇게 작정했다."], ["His predominant sensation at that moment might be expressed in the following words, “Well, there is no rehabilitating myself now.", "그 순간 그를 사로잡은 지배적인 감정은 다음과 같은 말로 표현할 수 있을 것이다. \"이제 와서 내 명예를 회복하기는 글렀어."], ["So let me shame them for all I am worth.", "그러니 있는 힘껏 저들에게 망신을 주자."], ["I will show them I don’t care what they think—that’s all!”", "저들이 나를 어떻게 생각하든 나는 아랑곳하지 않는다는 걸 보여 주는 거야—그뿐이다!\""]], [["He told the coachman to wait, while with rapid steps he returned to the monastery and straight to the Father Superior’s.", "그는 마부에게 기다리라고 일러 둔 채, 빠른 걸음으로 수도원으로, 곧장 수도원장의 처소로 되돌아갔다."], ["He had no clear idea what he would do, but he knew that he could not control himself, and that a touch might drive him to the utmost limits of obscenity, but only to obscenity, to nothing criminal, nothing for which he could be legally punished.", "무엇을 어찌하겠다는 분명한 작정이 있는 것은 아니었으나, 자기로서는 도무지 자제할 수 없으며, 한 번 건드려지기만 하면 외설의 극한까지 치달을 수 있다는 것은 알고 있었다—그러나 어디까지나 외설일 뿐, 범죄에 이르는 짓이나 법으로 처벌받을 만한 짓은 결코 아니었다."], ["In the last resort, he could always restrain himself, and had marveled indeed at himself, on that score, sometimes.", "막판에 가서는 그도 늘 자제할 수 있었고, 그 점에 있어서는 때때로 스스로도 자기 자신에게 감탄하곤 했었다."], ["He appeared in the Father Superior’s dining-room, at the moment when the prayer was over, and all were moving to the table.", "그는 기도가 막 끝나고 모두가 식탁으로 옮겨 가려던 바로 그 순간에 수도원장의 식당에 나타났다."], ["Standing in the doorway, he scanned the company, and laughing his prolonged, impudent, malicious chuckle, looked them all boldly in the face.", "문간에 선 채 그는 좌중을 죽 훑어보더니, 길게 늘어지는 뻔뻔스럽고 음흉한 너털웃음을 터뜨리며 모두의 얼굴을 대담하게 마주 보았다."], ["“They thought I had gone, and here I am again,”", "\"다들 내가 가 버린 줄 알았겠지, 그런데 여기 다시 왔소이다.\""], ["he cried to the whole room.", "그는 방 안 전체를 향해 소리쳤다."]], [["For one moment every one stared at him without a word; and at once every one felt that something revolting, grotesque, positively scandalous, was about to happen.", "한순간 모두가 말없이 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 곧 모두가, 무언가 역겹고 괴이하며 영락없이 망측한 일이 벌어지려 한다는 것을 직감했다."], ["Miüsov passed immediately from the most benevolent frame of mind to the most savage.", "미우소프는 더없이 자비로운 심경에서 단숨에 더없이 사나운 심경으로 돌변했다."], ["All the feelings that had subsided and died down in his heart revived instantly.", "그의 가슴속에서 가라앉아 사그라들었던 온갖 감정이 일시에 되살아났다."]], [["“No!", "\"안 돼!"], ["this I cannot endure!”", "나는 이것만은 참을 수 없소!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“I absolutely cannot!", "\"도무지 참을 수 없단 말이오!"], ["and ...", "그리고…"], ["I certainly cannot!”", "정말이지 참을 수 없소!\""]], [["The blood rushed to his head.", "피가 그의 머리로 솟구쳤다."], ["He positively stammered; but he was beyond thinking of style, and he seized his hat.", "그는 영락없이 말을 더듬었으나, 이미 말투를 따질 처지가 아니었으므로 모자를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“What is it he cannot?”", "\"저자가 참을 수 없다는 게 대체 무엇이오?\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, “that he absolutely cannot and certainly cannot?", "표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다. \"도무지 참을 수 없고 정말이지 참을 수 없다는 게 뭐란 말이오?"], ["Your reverence, am I to come in or not?", "신부님, 제가 들어가도 됩니까, 안 됩니까?"], ["Will you receive me as your guest?”", "저를 손님으로 받아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“You are welcome with all my heart,”", "\"진심으로 환영합니다.\""], ["answered the Superior.", "수도원장이 대답했다."], ["“Gentlemen!”", "\"여러분!\""], ["he added, “I venture to beg you most earnestly to lay aside your dissensions, and to be united in love and family harmony—with prayer to the Lord at our humble table.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"간곡히 청하건대, 부디 다툼을 내려놓으시고, 사랑과 가족의 화목 속에서 하나가 되시기를—주님께 기도드리며, 우리의 변변찮은 식탁에 함께하시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“No, no, it is impossible!”", "\"안 됩니다, 안 돼요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이오!\""], ["cried Miüsov, beside himself.", "미우소프가 제정신이 아닌 채 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, if it is impossible for Pyotr Alexandrovitch, it is impossible for me, and I won’t stop.", "\"그렇다면, 표트르 알렉산드로비치에게 있을 수 없는 일이라면 나에게도 있을 수 없는 일이오, 나도 머물지 않겠소."], ["That is why I came.", "바로 그러려고 내가 온 거니까."], ["I will keep with Pyotr Alexandrovitch everywhere now.", "이제 어디를 가든 표트르 알렉산드로비치와 함께 붙어 다니겠소."], ["If you will go away, Pyotr Alexandrovitch, I will go away too, if you remain, I will remain.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신이 가면 나도 가고, 당신이 머물면 나도 머물겠소."], ["You stung him by what you said about family harmony, Father Superior, he does not admit he is my relation.", "수도원장님, 당신이 가족의 화목 어쩌고 한 그 말로 이 사람을 콕 찔러 놓으셨소이다. 이 사람은 자기가 나의 친척이라는 걸 인정하지 않거든요."], ["That’s right, isn’t it, von Sohn?", "그렇지 않소, 폰 존?"], ["Here’s von Sohn.", "자, 여기 폰 존이 계시오."], ["How are you, von Sohn?”", "안녕하시오, 폰 존?\""]], [["“Do you mean me?”", "\"나 말씀이오?\""], ["muttered Maximov, puzzled.", "막시모프가 어리둥절하여 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Of course I mean you,”", "\"물론 당신 말이지.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "표도르 파블로비치가 외쳤다."], ["“Who else?", "\"누구겠소?"], ["The Father Superior could not be von Sohn.”", "수도원장님이 폰 존일 리는 없잖소.\""]], [["“But I am not von Sohn either.", "\"하지만 나는 폰 존이 아니외다."], ["I am Maximov.”", "나는 막시모프요.\""]], [["“No, you are von Sohn.", "\"아니, 당신은 폰 존이오."], ["Your reverence, do you know who von Sohn was?", "신부님, 폰 존이 누구였는지 아십니까?"], ["It was a famous murder case.", "그것은 유명한 살인 사건이었지요."], ["He was killed in a house of harlotry—I believe that is what such places are called among you—he was killed and robbed, and in spite of his venerable age, he was nailed up in a box and sent from Petersburg to Moscow in the luggage van, and while they were nailing him up, the harlots sang songs and played the harp, that is to say, the piano.", "그자는 어떤 색주가에서—여러분들 사이에서는 그런 데를 그렇게 부르는 줄로 압니다만—살해당했지요, 살해당하고 강탈당한 데다, 그 점잖으신 나이에도 불구하고 궤짝에 못 박혀 넣어진 채로 페테르부르크에서 모스크바로 화물칸에 실려 갔답니다. 게다가 그자를 궤짝에 못 박는 동안, 갈보들이 노래를 부르고 하프를, 그러니까 피아노를 쳤다는 거요."], ["So this is that very von Sohn.", "자, 이분이 바로 그 폰 존이외다."], ["He has risen from the dead, hasn’t he, von Sohn?”", "죽었다가 다시 살아나신 거지요, 안 그렇소, 폰 존?\""]], [["“What is happening?", "\"무슨 일이오?"], ["What’s this?”", "이게 다 뭐란 말이오?\""], ["voices were heard in the group of monks.", "수도사들 무리에서 웅성거리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Let us go,”", "\"갑시다.\""], ["cried Miüsov, addressing Kalganov.", "미우소프가 칼가노프를 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“No, excuse me,”", "\"안 되오, 잠깐만.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch broke in shrilly, taking another step into the room.", "표도르 파블로비치가 방 안으로 또 한 걸음 들어서며 날카로운 목소리로 가로막았다."], ["“Allow me to finish.", "\"내 말을 끝내게 해 주시오."], ["There in the cell you blamed me for behaving disrespectfully just because I spoke of eating gudgeon, Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치, 당신은 저기 암자에서 내가 모샘치를 먹는 얘기를 했다는 것만으로 나더러 불경스럽게 군다고 나무랐소."], ["Miüsov, my relation, prefers to have plus de noblesse que de sincérité in his words, but I prefer in mine plus de sincérité que de noblesse, and—damn the noblesse!", "나의 친척 미우소프는 자기 말에 진실보다 고상함을 더 담으려는 사람이지만(plus de noblesse que de sincérité), 나는 내 말에 고상함보다 진실을 더 담는 걸 좋아하오(plus de sincérité que de noblesse), 그러니—고상함 따위는 빌어먹으라지!"], ["That’s right, isn’t it, von Sohn?", "그렇지 않소, 폰 존?"], ["Allow me, Father Superior, though I am a buffoon and play the buffoon, yet I am the soul of honor, and I want to speak my mind.", "수도원장님, 잠깐만 허락하시오, 내가 비록 어릿광대이고 어릿광대 노릇을 한다지만, 그래도 나는 명예의 화신이외다, 그러니 내 속마음을 털어놓고 싶소."], ["Yes, I am the soul of honor, while in Pyotr Alexandrovitch there is wounded vanity and nothing else.", "그렇소, 나는 명예의 화신인데, 표트르 알렉산드로비치한테는 상한 허영심밖에는 아무것도 없소."], ["I came here perhaps to have a look and speak my mind.", "내가 여기 온 것은 어쩌면 한번 둘러보고 내 속마음을 털어놓으려고 그런 것인지도 모르겠소."], ["My son, Alexey, is here, being saved.", "내 아들 알렉세이가 여기 있소, 구원을 받고 있다지요."], ["I am his father; I care for his welfare, and it is my duty to care.", "나는 그 아이의 아비요. 나는 그 아이의 안녕을 염려하고, 또 염려하는 것이 내 도리요."], ["While I’ve been playing the fool, I have been listening and having a look on the sly; and now I want to give you the last act of the performance.", "내가 광대짓을 하는 동안에도 나는 가만히 귀 기울여 듣고 슬그머니 살펴보고 있었소이다. 그러니 이제 그 공연의 마지막 막을 여러분께 보여 드리겠소."], ["You know how things are with us?", "우리네 형편이 어떤지 아시오?"], ["As a thing falls, so it lies.", "무언가 한번 쓰러지면, 그대로 누워 있는 거요."], ["As a thing once has fallen, so it must lie for ever.", "한번 쓰러진 것은, 영영 그대로 누워 있어야만 하는 거요."], ["Not a bit of it!", "천만에!"], ["I want to get up again.", "나는 다시 일어나고 싶소."], ["Holy Father, I am indignant with you.", "거룩하신 신부님, 나는 당신들에게 분개하고 있소이다."], ["Confession is a great sacrament, before which I am ready to bow down reverently; but there in the cell, they all kneel down and confess aloud.", "고해는 위대한 성사라, 그 앞에서라면 나도 기꺼이 경건하게 머리를 조아릴 준비가 되어 있소. 그런데 저기 암자에서는 다들 무릎을 꿇고 큰 소리로 고해를 하더이다."], ["Can it be right to confess aloud?", "큰 소리로 고해를 한다는 게 과연 옳은 일이오?"], ["It was ordained by the holy Fathers to confess in secret: then only your confession will be a mystery, and so it was of old.", "거룩한 교부들께서는 은밀히 고해하도록 정하셨소. 그래야만 비로소 당신의 고해가 비밀이 되는 거고, 예로부터 그러했단 말이오."], ["But how can I explain to him before every one that I did this and that ...", "그런데 모든 사람 앞에서 어떻게 내가 이런 짓 저런 짓을 했노라고…"], ["well, you understand what—sometimes it would not be proper to talk about it—so it is really a scandal!", "그러니까, 무슨 말인지 아시잖소—더러는 입에 담기조차 마땅찮은 일을 어떻게 털어놓겠소—그러니 이건 정말 망측한 일이오!"], ["No, Fathers, one might be carried along with you to the Flagellants, I dare say ...", "안 되오, 신부님들, 이러다간 자칫 여러분 따라 채찍 고행파한테까지 끌려갈 판이외다…"], ["at the first opportunity I shall write to the Synod, and I shall take my son, Alexey, home.”", "기회가 닿는 대로 나는 종무원에 진정서를 써 보내고, 내 아들 알렉세이를 집으로 데려가겠소.\""]], [["We must note here that Fyodor Pavlovitch knew where to look for the weak spot.", "여기서 짚어 둘 것은, 표도르 파블로비치가 약점이 어디 있는지 정확히 알고 있었다는 점이다."], ["There had been at one time malicious rumors which had even reached the Archbishop (not only regarding our monastery, but in others where the institution of elders existed) that too much respect was paid to the elders, even to the detriment of the authority of the Superior, that the elders abused the sacrament of confession and so on and so on—absurd charges which had died away of themselves everywhere.", "한때 악의에 찬 소문이 돌아 대주교의 귀에까지 들어간 적이 있었으니(비단 우리 수도원뿐 아니라 장로 제도가 있는 다른 수도원들에 대해서도), 장로들을 지나치게 떠받들어 수도원장의 권위마저 손상될 지경이라느니, 장로들이 고해 성사를 악용한다느니 어쩌니 하는 따위의 터무니없는 비난이었는데, 이런 것들은 어디에서나 저절로 사그라들고 말았다."], ["But the spirit of folly, which had caught up Fyodor Pavlovitch, and was bearing him on the current of his own nerves into lower and lower depths of ignominy, prompted him with this old slander.", "그러나 표도르 파블로비치를 사로잡아 제 신경의 물살에 실어 점점 더 깊은 치욕의 나락으로 떠밀고 있던 그 어리석음의 망령이, 이 케케묵은 비방을 그에게 속삭여 준 것이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch did not understand a word of it, and he could not even put it sensibly, for on this occasion no one had been kneeling and confessing aloud in the elder’s cell, so that he could not have seen anything of the kind.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그 내용을 한마디도 제대로 이해하지 못했고, 조리 있게 늘어놓지도 못했으니, 이번 경우에는 장로의 암자에서 누구도 무릎을 꿇고 큰 소리로 고해한 일이 없었던지라, 그가 그런 따위의 광경을 목격했을 리가 없었기 때문이다."], ["He was only speaking from confused memory of old slanders.", "그는 그저 케케묵은 비방의 어렴풋한 기억을 더듬어 지껄여 댄 것이었다."], ["But as soon as he had uttered his foolish tirade, he felt he had been talking absurd nonsense, and at once longed to prove to his audience, and above all to himself, that he had not been talking nonsense.", "그러나 그 어리석은 일장 연설을 내뱉기가 무섭게 자기가 터무니없는 헛소리를 지껄였음을 깨닫고, 곧장 청중에게, 무엇보다도 자기 자신에게, 자기가 헛소리를 한 게 아니라는 것을 증명하고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["And, though he knew perfectly well that with each word he would be adding more and more absurdity, he could not restrain himself, and plunged forward blindly.", "그리고 한 마디 한 마디 내뱉을수록 점점 더 터무니없는 소리만 보탤 뿐임을 빤히 알면서도, 그는 자제하지 못하고 무작정 앞으로 곤두박질쳤다."]], [["“How disgraceful!”", "\"이 무슨 망신스러운 짓이오!\""], ["cried Pyotr Alexandrovitch.", "표트르 알렉산드로비치가 외쳤다."]], [["“Pardon me!”", "\"용서하시오!\""], ["said the Father Superior.", "수도원장이 말했다."], ["“It was said of old, ‘Many have begun to speak against me and have uttered evil sayings about me.", "\"예로부터 이런 말씀이 있었지요. '많은 이들이 나를 거슬러 말하기 시작하였고 나에 대하여 악한 말을 내뱉었도다."], ["And hearing it I have said to myself: it is the correction of the Lord and He has sent it to heal my vain soul.’ And so we humbly thank you, honored guest!”", "그것을 듣고 나는 스스로에게 일렀노라, 이는 주님의 징계이시며 나의 헛된 영혼을 고치시려고 주님께서 보내신 것이로다.' 그러므로 우리는 겸손히 당신께 감사드립니다, 귀하신 손님이시여!\""], ["and he made Fyodor Pavlovitch a low bow.", "그러고는 표도르 파블로비치에게 깊이 머리를 숙였다."]], [["“Tut—tut—tut—sanctimoniousness and stock phrases!", "\"쳇, 쳇, 쳇—위선이요, 판에 박은 문구로구먼!"], ["Old phrases and old gestures.", "케케묵은 문구에 케케묵은 몸짓이오."], ["The old lies and formal prostrations.", "묵은 거짓말에 형식뿐인 큰절이로다."], ["We know all about them.", "그런 건 우리도 다 아오."], ["A kiss on the lips and a dagger in the heart, as in Schiller’s Robbers.", "실러의 '군도'에 나오듯, 입술에는 입맞춤을, 가슴에는 비수를 꽂는 격이외다."], ["I don’t like falsehood, Fathers, I want the truth.", "나는 거짓을 좋아하지 않소, 신부님들, 나는 진실을 원하오."], ["But the truth is not to be found in eating gudgeon and that I proclaim aloud!", "그런데 진실은 모샘치를 먹는 데 있는 것이 아니라는 걸, 나는 큰 소리로 외치는 바이오!"], ["Father monks, why do you fast?", "수도사 신부님들, 당신들은 어찌하여 금식을 하시오?"], ["Why do you expect reward in heaven for that?", "어찌하여 그 대가로 천국에서 상을 바라시오?"], ["Why, for reward like that I will come and fast too!", "아니, 그런 상이라면야 나도 와서 금식을 하겠소이다!"], ["No, saintly monk, you try being virtuous in the world, do good to society, without shutting yourself up in a monastery at other people’s expense, and without expecting a reward up aloft for it—you’ll find that a bit harder.", "안 되오, 거룩하신 수도사 양반, 당신들 한번 세상 속에서 덕을 베풀어 보시오, 남의 돈으로 수도원에 틀어박히지 않고서, 또 그 대가로 저 위에서 상을 바라지도 않고서, 사회를 위해 좋은 일을 해 보란 말이오—그게 좀 더 힘들다는 걸 알게 될 거요."], ["I can talk sense, too, Father Superior.", "나도 사리에 맞는 소리쯤은 할 줄 안단 말이오, 수도원장님."], ["What have they got here?”", "여기 차려 놓은 게 다 뭐요?\""], ["He went up to the table.", "그는 식탁으로 다가갔다."], ["“Old port wine, mead brewed by the Eliseyev Brothers.", "\"묵은 포트와인에, 옐리세예프 형제 상회에서 빚은 봉밀주라."], ["Fie, fie, fathers!", "저런, 저런, 신부님들!"], ["That is something beyond gudgeon.", "이건 모샘치 따위와는 차원이 다르구먼."], ["Look at the bottles the fathers have brought out, he he he!", "신부님들이 내놓은 이 술병들 좀 보시오, 헤헤헤!"], ["And who has provided it all?", "그런데 이걸 다 누가 댄 거요?"], ["The Russian peasant, the laborer, brings here the farthing earned by his horny hand, wringing it from his family and the tax-gatherer!", "러시아의 농부, 노동자가, 거친 손으로 벌어들인 한 푼을, 제 식구와 세금쟁이한테서 쥐어짜 가며 여기다 갖다 바친 거란 말이오!"], ["You bleed the people, you know, holy fathers.”", "당신들은 백성의 피를 빨고 있는 거요, 알겠소, 거룩하신 신부님들.\""]], [["“This is too disgraceful!”", "\"이건 너무도 망측하오!\""], ["said Father Iosif.", "이오시프 신부가 말했다."]], [["Father Païssy kept obstinately silent.", "파이시 신부는 완강하게 침묵을 지켰다."], ["Miüsov rushed from the room, and Kalganov after him.", "미우소프는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 칼가노프가 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Well, Father, I will follow Pyotr Alexandrovitch!", "\"좋소, 신부님, 나도 표트르 알렉산드로비치를 따라가겠소!"], ["I am not coming to see you again.", "다시는 당신을 보러 오지 않겠소이다."], ["You may beg me on your knees, I shan’t come.", "무릎 꿇고 빌어도 오지 않을 거요."], ["I sent you a thousand roubles, so you have begun to keep your eye on me.", "내가 당신네한테 천 루블을 보냈더니, 그때부터 나를 눈여겨보기 시작했겠다."], ["He he he!", "헤헤헤!"], ["No, I’ll say no more.", "아니, 더는 말하지 않겠소."], ["I am taking my revenge for my youth, for all the humiliation I endured.”", "나는 내 젊은 시절에 대한, 내가 당한 그 온갖 모욕에 대한 앙갚음을 하고 있는 거요.\""], ["He thumped the table with his fist in a paroxysm of simulated feeling.", "그는 꾸며 낸 격정에 사로잡혀 주먹으로 식탁을 쾅 내리쳤다. \"이 수도원은 내 인생에서 큰 노릇을 했단 말이오!"], ["“This monastery has played a great part in my life!", "이 때문에 나는 쓰디쓴 눈물을 얼마나 흘렸는지 모르오."], ["It has cost me many bitter tears. You used to set my wife, the crazy one, against me.", "당신들은 내 마누라를, 그 미친 여편네를 부추겨 나한테 등을 돌리게 만들었소."], ["You cursed me with bell and book, you spread stories about me all over the place.", "종을 울리고 성서를 펴 들고 나를 저주했으며, 사방에 나에 관한 소문을 퍼뜨렸소."], ["Enough, fathers!", "그만하면 됐소, 신부님들!"], ["This is the age of Liberalism, the age of steamers and railways.", "지금은 자유주의의 시대요, 증기선과 철도의 시대란 말이오."], ["Neither a thousand, nor a hundred roubles, no, nor a hundred farthings will you get out of me!”", "천 루블은커녕, 백 루블도, 아니, 백 푼도 나한테서는 한 푼도 받아 내지 못할 거요!\""]], [["It must be noted again that our monastery never had played any great part in his life, and he never had shed a bitter tear owing to it.", "여기서 다시 짚어 둘 것은, 우리 수도원이 그의 인생에서 무슨 큰 노릇을 한 적이라곤 결코 없었으며, 그가 그 때문에 쓰디쓴 눈물을 흘린 적도 결코 없었다는 점이다."], ["But he was so carried away by his simulated emotion, that he was for one moment almost believing it himself.", "그러나 그는 자기가 꾸며 낸 감정에 어찌나 휩쓸렸던지, 한순간 거의 자기 자신마저 그것을 믿을 지경이었다."], ["He was so touched he was almost weeping.", "그는 어찌나 감격했던지 거의 울 뻔했다."], ["But at that very instant, he felt that it was time to draw back.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 이제 물러설 때라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["The Father Superior bowed his head at his malicious lie, and again spoke impressively:", "수도원장은 그의 악의에 찬 거짓말에 고개를 숙이고, 다시금 묵직하게 말했다."]], [["“It is written again, ‘Bear circumspectly and gladly dishonor that cometh upon thee by no act of thine own, be not confounded and hate not him who hath dishonored thee.’ And so will we.”", "\"또한 이렇게 기록되어 있습니다. '네 행위로 말미암지 아니한 치욕이 네게 닥치거든 신중하고 기쁘게 견디라, 당황하지 말며 너를 욕되게 한 자를 미워하지 말라.' 그러므로 우리도 그리하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Tut, tut, tut!", "\"쳇, 쳇, 쳇!"], ["Bethinking thyself and the rest of the rigmarole.", "자기 자신을 돌이켜보라느니 어쩌니 하는 그 나머지 잠꼬대까지 다 늘어놓는구먼."], ["Bethink yourselves, Fathers, I will go.", "당신네나 정신 차리시오, 신부님들, 나는 가겠소."], ["But I will take my son, Alexey, away from here for ever, on my parental authority.", "다만 내 아들 알렉세이만은, 부모 된 권리로 영영 이곳에서 데려가겠소."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch, my most dutiful son, permit me to order you to follow me.", "이반 표도로비치, 더없이 효성스러운 내 아들아, 나를 따라오라고 명하는 것을 허락해 다오."], ["Von Sohn, what have you to stay for?", "폰 존, 당신은 여기 남아 봤자 뭘 하겠소?"], ["Come and see me now in the town.", "지금 나를 따라 읍내로 갑시다."], ["It is fun there.", "거기가 재미있단 말이오."], ["It is only one short verst; instead of lenten oil, I will give you sucking-pig and kasha.", "겨우 한 베르스타밖에 안 되오. 정진하는 기름 대신 새끼돼지 구이에 카샤를 대접하리다."], ["We will have dinner with some brandy and liqueur to it....", "브랜디에 리큐어까지 곁들여 한 상 차려 먹읍시다…"], ["I’ve cloudberry wine.", "나한테는 진들딸기주도 있소."], ["Hey, von Sohn, don’t lose your chance.”", "자, 폰 존, 이 기회를 놓치지 마시오.\""], ["He went out, shouting and gesticulating.", "그는 고래고래 소리치고 손을 휘저으며 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["It was at that moment Rakitin saw him and pointed him out to Alyosha.", "바로 그 순간 라키틴이 그를 보고 알료샤에게 손가락질해 보였다."]], [["“Alexey!”", "\"알렉세이!\""], ["his father shouted, from far off, catching sight of him.", "그의 아비가 멀찍이서 그를 알아보고 외쳤다."], ["“You come home to me to-day, for good, and bring your pillow and mattress, and leave no trace behind.”", "\"오늘 나한테 집으로 돌아오너라, 아주 영영, 그러고 베개와 요도 챙겨 오고, 아무 흔적도 남기지 말거라.\""]], [["Alyosha stood rooted to the spot, watching the scene in silence.", "알료샤는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서서 말없이 그 광경을 지켜보았다."], ["Meanwhile, Fyodor Pavlovitch had got into the carriage, and Ivan was about to follow him in grim silence without even turning to say good-by to Alyosha.", "그사이 표도르 파블로비치는 마차에 올라탔고, 이반도 알료샤에게 작별 인사조차 건네려 돌아보지 않은 채 침울한 침묵 속에 그 뒤를 따라 오르려던 참이었다."], ["But at this point another almost incredible scene of grotesque buffoonery gave the finishing touch to the episode.", "그런데 이때 또 한 차례 거의 믿기지 않을 만큼 괴이한 광대극이 벌어져 이 사건에 마지막 마무리를 지었다."], ["Maximov suddenly appeared by the side of the carriage.", "막시모프가 느닷없이 마차 옆에 나타난 것이다."], ["He ran up, panting, afraid of being too late.", "그는 너무 늦을세라 헐떡이며 달려왔다."], ["Rakitin and Alyosha saw him running.", "라키틴과 알료샤는 그가 달려오는 것을 보았다."], ["He was in such a hurry that in his impatience he put his foot on the step on which Ivan’s left foot was still resting, and clutching the carriage he kept trying to jump in.", "그는 어찌나 다급했던지, 그 조바심에 그만 이반의 왼발이 아직 얹혀 있는 발판에 제 발을 올려놓고는, 마차를 부여잡은 채 자꾸만 뛰어오르려 했다."], ["“I am going with you!”", "\"나도 함께 가겠소이다!\""], ["he kept shouting, laughing a thin mirthful laugh with a look of reckless glee in his face.", "그는 얼굴에 분별없는 환희를 띤 채 가느다랗고 신명 난 웃음을 흘리며 연신 외쳤다."], ["“Take me, too.”", "\"나도 데려가 주시오.\""]], [["“There!”", "\"바로 그거요!\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, delighted.", "표도르 파블로비치가 기뻐서 외쳤다."], ["“Did I not say he was von Sohn.", "\"내가 이 사람이 폰 존이라고 하지 않았소."], ["It is von Sohn himself, risen from the dead.", "죽었다가 다시 살아난 바로 그 폰 존이외다."], ["Why, how did you tear yourself away?", "아니, 어떻게 빠져나왔소?"], ["What did you vonsohn there?", "거기서 무슨 폰존질을 하셨소?"], ["And how could you get away from the dinner?", "게다가 만찬에서는 어떻게 빠져나온 거요?"], ["You must be a brazen-faced fellow!", "당신도 어지간히 뻔뻔스러운 위인이로구먼!"], ["I am that myself, but I am surprised at you, brother!", "나도 그렇긴 하다만, 당신한테는 정말 놀랐소이다, 이 양반아!"], ["Jump in, jump in!", "어서 올라타시오, 어서!"], ["Let him pass, Ivan.", "이반, 이 사람을 들이게."], ["It will be fun.", "이거 재미있겠는걸."], ["He can lie somewhere at our feet.", "우리 발치 아무 데나 누워 가면 되지."], ["Will you lie at our feet, von Sohn?", "우리 발치에 누워 가겠소, 폰 존?"], ["Or perch on the box with the coachman.", "아니면 마부 옆 마부석에 올라앉든가."], ["Skip on to the box, von Sohn!”", "마부석으로 폴짝 뛰어오르시오, 폰 존!\""]], [["But Ivan, who had by now taken his seat, without a word gave Maximov a violent punch in the breast and sent him flying.", "그러나 이미 자리에 앉아 있던 이반은 말 한마디 없이 막시모프의 가슴팍을 우악스레 한 대 쥐어박아 그를 나가떨어지게 했다."], ["It was quite by chance he did not fall.", "그가 넘어지지 않은 것은 순전히 운이었다."]], [["“Drive on!”", "\"가자!\""], ["Ivan shouted angrily to the coachman.", "이반이 마부에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, what are you doing, what are you about?", "\"아니, 자네 무슨 짓인가, 대체 뭘 하는 거야?"], ["Why did you do that?”", "어쩌자고 그런 짓을 한 게야?\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch protested.", "표도르 파블로비치가 항의했다."]], [["But the carriage had already driven away.", "그러나 마차는 이미 달려 나간 뒤였다."], ["Ivan made no reply.", "이반은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Well, you are a fellow,”", "\"허, 자네도 어지간한 사람일세그려.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch said again.", "표도르 파블로비치가 다시 말했다."]], [["After a pause of two minutes, looking askance at his son, “Why, it was you got up all this monastery business.", "이 분쯤 잠자코 있다가, 아들을 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"아니, 이 수도원 일을 죄다 꾸민 게 바로 자네 아닌가."], ["You urged it, you approved of it.", "자네가 부추겼고, 자네가 찬성했잖나."], ["Why are you angry now?”", "그런데 이제 와서 왜 화를 내는 게야?\""]], [["“You’ve talked rot enough.", "\"아버지는 헛소리를 충분히 하셨습니다."], ["You might rest a bit now,”", "이제 좀 쉬셔도 되겠지요.\""], ["Ivan snapped sullenly.", "이반이 시무룩하게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch was silent again for two minutes.", "표도르 파블로비치는 다시 이 분쯤 입을 다물었다."]], [["“A drop of brandy would be nice now,”", "\"브랜디나 한 모금 했으면 좋겠구먼.\""], ["he observed sententiously, but Ivan made no response.", "그가 잠언이라도 읊듯 한마디 했으나, 이반은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“You shall have some, too, when we get home.”", "\"집에 가면 자네도 한잔하게나.\""]], [["Ivan was still silent.", "이반은 여전히 잠자코 있었다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch waited another two minutes.", "표도르 파블로비치는 또 이 분쯤 기다렸다."]], [["“But I shall take Alyosha away from the monastery, though you will dislike it so much, most honored Karl von Moor.”", "\"그래도 나는 알료샤를 수도원에서 데려올 걸세, 자네가 아무리 싫어한다 해도 말이야, 더없이 존경스러운 카를 폰 모어 나리.\""]], [["Ivan shrugged his shoulders contemptuously, and turning away stared at the road.", "이반은 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 고개를 돌려 길을 멀거니 바라보았다."], ["And they did not speak again all the way home.", "그리고 그들은 집에 이를 때까지 다시는 한마디도 나누지 않았다."]], [["# Book III. The Sensualists Chapter I. In The Servants’ Quarters", "# Book III. The Sensualists Chapter I. In The Servants’ Quarters"]], [["The Karamazovs’ house was far from being in the center of the town, but it was not quite outside it.", "카라마조프 집안의 저택은 읍내 한복판이라고 하기에는 거리가 멀었으나, 그렇다고 완전히 변두리에 떨어진 것도 아니었다."], ["It was a pleasant-looking old house of two stories, painted gray, with a red iron roof.", "회색으로 칠하고 붉은 철판 지붕을 얹은, 보기에 정겨운 이층짜리 낡은 집이었다."], ["It was roomy and snug, and might still last many years.", "넓고 아늑하여 앞으로도 여러 해는 너끈히 버틸 만했다."], ["There were all sorts of unexpected little cupboards and closets and staircases.", "온갖 예기치 못한 자그마한 벽장이며 골방, 계단들이 곳곳에 숨어 있었다."], ["There were rats in it, but Fyodor Pavlovitch did not altogether dislike them.", "쥐도 들끓었으나 표도르 파블로비치는 그것을 그리 싫어하지는 않았다."], ["“One doesn’t feel so solitary when one’s left alone in the evening,” he used to say.", "\"저녁에 홀로 남겨졌을 때 그래도 덜 적적한 법이지.\""], ["It was his habit to send the servants away to the lodge for the night and to lock himself up alone.", "그는 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그는 밤이면 하인들을 별채로 내보내고 혼자 문을 잠가 걸어 두는 버릇이 있었다."], ["The lodge was a roomy and solid building in the yard.", "별채는 마당에 세워진 넓고 튼튼한 건물이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch used to have the cooking done there, although there was a kitchen in the house; he did not like the smell of cooking, and, winter and summer alike, the dishes were carried in across the courtyard.", "집 안에 부엌이 따로 있었음에도 표도르 파블로비치는 음식을 별채에서 짓게 했으니, 그는 음식 냄새를 싫어하여 겨울이든 여름이든 한결같이 음식을 마당 너머로 날라 오게 했다."], ["The house was built for a large family; there was room for five times as many, with their servants.", "이 집은 대가족을 위해 지어졌으므로, 하인들까지 합쳐 지금의 다섯 배에 달하는 식구가 들어와 살 만한 공간이 있었다."], ["But at the time of our story there was no one living in the house but Fyodor Pavlovitch and his son Ivan.", "그러나 우리 이야기가 펼쳐지는 무렵에는 이 집에 표도르 파블로비치와 그의 아들 이반 말고는 아무도 살지 않았다."], ["And in the lodge there were only three servants: old Grigory, and his old wife Marfa, and a young man called Smerdyakov.", "그리고 별채에는 하인이 셋뿐이었으니, 늙은 그리고리와 그의 늙은 아내 마르파, 그리고 스메르댜코프라 불리는 젊은이였다."], ["Of these three we must say a few words.", "이 세 사람에 관해서는 몇 마디 해 두어야겠다."], ["Of old Grigory we have said something already.", "늙은 그리고리에 대해서는 이미 얼마간 이야기한 바 있다."], ["He was firm and determined and went blindly and obstinately for his object, if once he had been brought by any reasons (and they were often very illogical ones) to believe that it was immutably right.", "그는 굳세고 단호한 인물로서, 일단 어떤 이유로(그것은 흔히 매우 비논리적인 이유였다) 그 일이 영원토록 옳다고 믿게 되면, 앞뒤 가리지 않고 고집스럽게 그 목적을 향해 밀고 나가는 사내였다."], ["He was honest and incorruptible.", "그는 정직하고 매수당할 줄 모르는 자였다."], ["His wife, Marfa Ignatyevna, had obeyed her husband’s will implicitly all her life, yet she had pestered him terribly after the emancipation of the serfs.", "그의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나는 평생토록 남편의 뜻에 고분고분 따랐으나, 농노 해방이 있은 뒤로는 그를 지독하게 들볶았다."], ["She was set on leaving Fyodor Pavlovitch and opening a little shop in Moscow with their small savings.", "그녀는 표도르 파블로비치를 떠나 모아 둔 푼돈으로 모스크바에 자그마한 가게라도 열고 싶어 했던 것이다."], ["But Grigory decided then, once for all, that “the woman’s talking nonsense, for every woman is dishonest,”", "그러나 그리고리는 그때 단번에 못 박아 결정하기를, \"여편네가 헛소리를 하는 게야, 계집이란 죄다 못 미더운 법이니\""], ["and that they ought not to leave their old master, whatever he might be, for “that was now their duty.”", "하면서, 주인이 어떤 인간이든 간에 늙은 주인을 떠나서는 안 된다고 했으니, \"이제 그것이 우리의 도리이기 때문\"이었다."]], [["“Do you understand what duty is?”", "\"자네는 도리가 뭔지 알기나 해?\""], ["he asked Marfa Ignatyevna.", "그가 마르파 이그나티예브나에게 물었다."]], [["“I understand what duty means, Grigory Vassilyevitch, but why it’s our duty to stay here I never shall understand,”", "\"도리가 무슨 뜻인지야 알지요, 그리고리 바실리예비치. 하지만 어째서 여기 머무는 게 우리 도리인지는 죽어도 모르겠어요.\""], ["Marfa answered firmly.", "마르파가 단호하게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Well, don’t understand then.", "\"그럼 모르고 살아."], ["But so it shall be.", "어쨌든 그렇게 될 테니."], ["And you hold your tongue.”", "그러니 입 다물고 있어.\""]], [["And so it was.", "그리하여 그대로 되었다."], ["They did not go away, and Fyodor Pavlovitch promised them a small sum for wages, and paid it regularly.", "두 사람은 떠나지 않았고, 표도르 파블로비치는 그들에게 적은 액수나마 품삯을 약속하고 꼬박꼬박 지불했다."], ["Grigory knew, too, that he had an indisputable influence over his master.", "그리고리는 또한 자신이 주인에게 거역할 수 없는 영향력을 지니고 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["It was true, and he was aware of it.", "그것은 사실이었고, 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch was an obstinate and cunning buffoon, yet, though his will was strong enough “in some of the affairs of life,” as he expressed it, he found himself, to his surprise, extremely feeble in facing certain other emergencies.", "표도르 파블로비치는 고집 세고 교활한 어릿광대였으나, 그 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 \"인생의 어떤 일들\"에서는 의지가 제법 강했음에도 불구하고, 어떤 다른 위급한 상황 앞에서는 스스로도 놀라울 만큼 지극히 무력해지는 것을 깨닫곤 했다."], ["He knew his weaknesses and was afraid of them.", "그는 제 약점을 알았고 그것을 두려워했다."], ["There are positions in which one has to keep a sharp look out.", "사람이 눈을 부릅뜨고 단단히 경계해야 하는 처지가 있는 법이다."], ["And that’s not easy without a trustworthy man, and Grigory was a most trustworthy man.", "그런데 그것은 믿을 만한 사람 없이는 쉬운 노릇이 아니었고, 그리고리야말로 더없이 믿음직한 사람이었다."], ["Many times in the course of his life Fyodor Pavlovitch had only just escaped a sound thrashing through Grigory’s intervention, and on each occasion the old servant gave him a good lecture.", "표도르 파블로비치는 평생토록 여러 차례 그리고리가 끼어든 덕분에 가까스로 흠씬 두들겨 맞는 봉변을 면했는데, 그럴 때마다 늙은 하인은 그에게 따끔하게 설교를 늘어놓았다."], ["But it wasn’t only thrashings that Fyodor Pavlovitch was afraid of.", "그러나 표도르 파블로비치가 두려워한 것은 매질만이 아니었다."], ["There were graver occasions, and very subtle and complicated ones, when Fyodor Pavlovitch could not have explained the extraordinary craving for some one faithful and devoted, which sometimes unaccountably came upon him all in a moment.", "더 심각한, 그리고 매우 미묘하고 복잡한 경우들이 있었으니, 표도르 파블로비치는 때때로 까닭 모르게 한순간에 자기를 사로잡는, 충직하고 헌신적인 누군가를 향한 그 유별난 갈망을 스스로도 설명할 수 없었다."], ["It was almost a morbid condition.", "그것은 거의 병적인 상태였다."], ["Corrupt and often cruel in his lust, like some noxious insect, Fyodor Pavlovitch was sometimes, in moments of drunkenness, overcome by superstitious terror and a moral convulsion which took an almost physical form.", "정욕에 있어 타락하고 흔히 잔인하기가 마치 해로운 벌레와도 같던 표도르 파블로비치는, 술에 취한 순간이면 이따금 미신적인 공포와 도덕적 발작에 사로잡혔는데, 그것은 거의 육체적인 형태를 띠었다."], ["“My soul’s simply quaking in my throat at those times,” he used to say.", "\"그런 때면 내 영혼이 목구멍에서 부들부들 떨리는 것만 같아.\""], ["At such moments he liked to feel that there was near at hand, in the lodge if not in the room, a strong, faithful man, virtuous and unlike himself, who had seen all his debauchery and knew all his secrets, but was ready in his devotion to overlook all that, not to oppose him, above all, not to reproach him or threaten him with anything, either in this world or in the next, and, in case of need, to defend him—from whom?", "그는 곧잘 이렇게 말했다. 그런 순간이면 그는 가까운 곳에, 방 안은 아니어도 별채에라도, 강인하고 충직한, 덕성스럽고 자기와는 딴판인 사내가 있음을 느끼고 싶어 했으니, 그자는 그의 온갖 방탕을 다 보았고 그의 모든 비밀을 알면서도 충성된 마음으로 그 모두를 눈감아 줄 채비가 되어 있고, 그를 거스르지 않으며, 무엇보다도 이 세상에서든 저 세상에서든 그를 책망하거나 무엇으로 위협하지 않으며, 필요할 때면 그를 지켜 줄—누구로부터?"], ["From somebody unknown, but terrible and dangerous.", "알 수 없으나 무섭고 위험한 누군가로부터—그런 사내였다."], ["What he needed was to feel that there was another man, an old and tried friend, that he might call him in his sick moments merely to look at his face, or, perhaps, exchange some quite irrelevant words with him.", "그에게 필요했던 것은, 또 다른 한 사람이, 오래되고 시험을 거친 벗이 곁에 있어, 병적인 순간이면 그저 그 얼굴이나 들여다보려고, 혹은 아무 상관도 없는 몇 마디나 주고받으려고 그를 불러들일 수 있다고 느끼는 것이었다."], ["And if the old servant were not angry, he felt comforted, and if he were angry, he was more dejected.", "그리고 그 늙은 하인이 성을 내지 않으면 그는 위로를 받았고, 성을 내면 더욱 풀이 죽었다."], ["It happened even (very rarely however) that Fyodor Pavlovitch went at night to the lodge to wake Grigory and fetch him for a moment.", "더러는(그러나 아주 드물게) 표도르 파블로비치가 밤중에 별채로 가서 그리고리를 깨워 잠시 데려오는 일도 있었다."], ["When the old man came, Fyodor Pavlovitch would begin talking about the most trivial matters, and would soon let him go again, sometimes even with a jest.", "늙은이가 오면 표도르 파블로비치는 더없이 사소한 일들을 두고 이야기를 늘어놓다가 이내 그를 다시 돌려보냈는데, 때로는 농담까지 곁들였다."], ["And after he had gone, Fyodor Pavlovitch would get into bed with a curse and sleep the sleep of the just.", "그러고는 늙은이가 가고 나면 표도르 파블로비치는 욕설을 내뱉으며 잠자리에 들어 의인의 잠을 잤다."], ["Something of the same sort had happened to Fyodor Pavlovitch on Alyosha’s arrival.", "알료샤가 찾아왔을 때에도 표도르 파블로비치에게 이와 비슷한 무언가가 일어났다."], ["Alyosha “pierced his heart” by “living with him, seeing everything and blaming nothing.”", "알료샤는 \"그와 함께 살며, 모든 것을 보면서도 아무것도 탓하지 않음\"으로써 \"그의 마음을 꿰뚫었다.\""], ["Moreover, Alyosha brought with him something his father had never known before: a complete absence of contempt for him and an invariable kindness, a perfectly natural unaffected devotion to the old man who deserved it so little.", "게다가 알료샤는 아버지가 그때까지 한 번도 알지 못했던 무언가를 가져다주었으니, 그것은 곧 그를 향한 경멸의 완전한 부재였고, 변함없는 다정함이었으며, 그토록 그것을 받을 자격이 없는 늙은이를 향한 더없이 자연스럽고 꾸밈없는 헌신이었다."], ["All this was a complete surprise to the old profligate, who had dropped all family ties.", "이 모든 것은 온갖 가족의 인연을 죄다 끊어 버렸던 늙은 난봉꾼에게는 완전히 뜻밖의 일이었다."], ["It was a new and surprising experience for him, who had till then loved nothing but “evil.”", "그때까지 \"악\" 말고는 아무것도 사랑해 본 적 없던 그에게는 새롭고 놀라운 경험이었다."], ["When Alyosha had left him, he confessed to himself that he had learnt something he had not till then been willing to learn.", "알료샤가 그를 떠났을 때, 그는 그때까지 결코 배우려 들지 않았던 무언가를 배웠노라고 스스로 고백했다."]], [["I have mentioned already that Grigory had detested Adelaïda Ivanovna, the first wife of Fyodor Pavlovitch and the mother of Dmitri, and that he had, on the contrary, protected Sofya Ivanovna, the poor “crazy woman,”", "앞서 이미 말한 바 있거니와, 그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치의 첫 아내이자 드미트리의 어머니인 아델라이다 이바노브나를 몹시 싫어했고, 반대로 가엾은 \"정신 나간 여자\""], ["against his master and any one who chanced to speak ill or lightly of her.", "소피야 이바노브나는 주인을 비롯해 그녀를 함부로 헐뜯거나 가볍게 입에 올리는 자가 있으면 누구든 막아서며 감싸 주었다."], ["His sympathy for the unhappy wife had become something sacred to him, so that even now, twenty years after, he could not bear a slighting allusion to her from any one, and would at once check the offender.", "이 불행한 아내를 향한 그의 연민은 그에게 거의 신성한 무언가가 되어 버려서, 이십 년이 지난 지금까지도 누구든 그녀를 두고 업신여기는 말을 흘리는 것을 견디지 못하고 당장 그 무례한 자를 제지하곤 했다."], ["Externally, Grigory was cold, dignified and taciturn, and spoke, weighing his words, without frivolity.", "겉으로 보면 그리고리는 차갑고 위엄 있고 말수가 적었으며, 말을 할 때면 경솔함 없이 한 마디 한 마디를 저울질하듯 신중하게 했다."], ["It was impossible to tell at first sight whether he loved his meek, obedient wife; but he really did love her, and she knew it.", "첫눈에는 그가 그 온순하고 고분고분한 아내를 사랑하는지 어떤지 도무지 알 수 없었으나, 그는 진정으로 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀 또한 그것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Marfa Ignatyevna was by no means foolish; she was probably, indeed, cleverer than her husband, or, at least, more prudent than he in worldly affairs, and yet she had given in to him in everything without question or complaint ever since her marriage, and respected him for his spiritual superiority.", "마르파 이그나티예브나는 결코 어리석은 여자가 아니었으니, 어쩌면 남편보다 영리했을지도 모르고, 적어도 세상사에 있어서만큼은 그보다 더 신중했다. 그럼에도 그녀는 시집온 이래로 의문이나 불평 한마디 없이 모든 일에서 남편에게 양보해 왔으며, 그의 정신적 우월함을 존경했다."], ["It was remarkable how little they spoke to one another in the course of their lives, and only of the most necessary daily affairs.", "두 사람이 평생을 살아오며 서로 주고받은 말이라고는 놀라우리만치 적었고, 그것도 오직 일상의 가장 긴요한 일들에 관한 것뿐이었으니, 참으로 눈여겨볼 만한 노릇이었다."], ["The grave and dignified Grigory thought over all his cares and duties alone, so that Marfa Ignatyevna had long grown used to knowing that he did not need her advice.", "근엄하고 위엄 있는 그리고리는 자신의 온갖 근심과 책무를 홀로 곱씹었으므로, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 그가 자기 조언 따위를 필요로 하지 않는다는 것을 진작부터 알아 익숙해져 있었다."], ["She felt that her husband respected her silence, and took it as a sign of her good sense.", "그녀는 남편이 자신의 침묵을 존중하고 그것을 그녀의 분별 있음의 표시로 받아들인다고 느꼈다."], ["He had never beaten her but once, and then only slightly.", "그는 단 한 번을 빼고는 그녀를 때린 적이 없었고, 그 한 번도 가볍게 손을 댄 것에 지나지 않았다."], ["Once during the year after Fyodor Pavlovitch’s marriage with Adelaïda Ivanovna, the village girls and women—at that time serfs—were called together before the house to sing and dance. They were beginning “In the Green Meadows,”", "표도르 파블로비치가 아델라이다 이바노브나와 결혼하고 난 그 이듬해 어느 날, 마을의 처녀들과 아낙네들이—그때는 다들 농노였다—노래하고 춤추라는 부름을 받고 저택 앞으로 불려 나온 일이 있었다."], ["when Marfa, at that time a young woman, skipped forward and danced “the Russian Dance,” not in the village fashion, but as she had danced it when she was a servant in the service of the rich Miüsov family, in their private theater, where the actors were taught to dance by a dancing master from Moscow.", "한창 \"푸른 풀밭에서\"를 부르기 시작할 무렵, 그때만 해도 젊은 여자였던 마르파가 앞으로 깡충 뛰어나와 \"러시아 춤\"을 추었는데, 그것이 마을식이 아니라 그녀가 부유한 미우소프 집안의 하녀로 일하던 시절에, 모스크바에서 온 무용 선생이 배우들에게 춤을 가르치던 그 집안 전용 극장에서 추던 식이었다."], ["Grigory saw how his wife danced, and, an hour later, at home in their cottage he gave her a lesson, pulling her hair a little.", "그리고리는 제 아내가 춤추는 꼴을 보았고, 한 시간 뒤 집 안 오두막에서 그녀의 머리채를 살짝 잡아끌며 따끔하게 본때를 보여 주었다."], ["But there it ended: the beating was never repeated, and Marfa Ignatyevna gave up dancing.", "그러나 그것으로 끝이었다. 매질은 두 번 다시 되풀이되지 않았고, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 춤을 끊었다."]], [["God had not blessed them with children.", "하느님은 그들에게 자식을 점지해 주지 않았다."], ["One child was born but it died.", "아이 하나가 태어났으나 곧 죽고 말았다."], ["Grigory was fond of children, and was not ashamed of showing it.", "그리고리는 아이들을 좋아했고, 그것을 드러내는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["When Adelaïda Ivanovna had run away, Grigory took Dmitri, then a child of three years old, combed his hair and washed him in a tub with his own hands, and looked after him for almost a year.", "아델라이다 이바노브나가 달아났을 때, 그리고리는 그때 세 살배기 어린아이였던 드미트리를 맡아, 제 손으로 머리를 빗기고 함지박에 넣어 씻기며 거의 일 년 동안이나 돌보았다."], ["Afterwards he had looked after Ivan and Alyosha, for which the general’s widow had rewarded him with a slap in the face; but I have already related all that.", "그 뒤에는 이반과 알료샤를 돌보았는데, 그 일로 장군 부인은 그의 따귀를 갈겨 보답했으나, 이에 관해서는 이미 다 이야기한 바 있다."], ["The only happiness his own child had brought him had been in the anticipation of its birth.", "그가 제 자식에게서 얻은 유일한 행복이라고는 그 아이의 탄생을 기다리던 그 기대뿐이었다."], ["When it was born, he was overwhelmed with grief and horror.", "막상 아이가 태어났을 때 그는 슬픔과 공포에 짓눌렸다."], ["The baby had six fingers.", "갓난아이에게는 손가락이 여섯이었던 것이다."], ["Grigory was so crushed by this, that he was not only silent till the day of the christening, but kept away in the garden.", "그리고리는 이 일에 어찌나 짓눌렸던지 세례식 날까지 입을 다물었을 뿐 아니라 정원에 틀어박혀 지냈다."], ["It was spring, and he spent three days digging the kitchen garden.", "때는 봄이었고, 그는 사흘 동안 채마밭을 파헤쳤다."], ["The third day was fixed for christening the baby: mean-time Grigory had reached a conclusion.", "셋째 날이 아이의 세례식으로 정해져 있었는데, 그동안 그리고리는 하나의 결론에 다다라 있었다."], ["Going into the cottage where the clergy were assembled and the visitors had arrived, including Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was to stand god-father, he suddenly announced that the baby “ought not to be christened at all.”", "성직자들이 모여 있고 대부 노릇을 맡기로 한 표도르 파블로비치를 비롯한 손님들이 와 있던 오두막으로 들어선 그는, 느닷없이 이 아이를 \"도무지 세례받게 해서는 안 된다\"고 선언했다."], ["He announced this quietly, briefly, forcing out his words, and gazing with dull intentness at the priest.", "그는 조용히, 간결하게, 한 마디 한 마디를 억지로 짜내듯 내뱉으며 무딘 눈초리로 사제를 뚫어지게 응시했다."]], [["“Why not?”", "\"어째서 안 된단 말이오?\""], ["asked the priest with good-humored surprise.", "사제가 사람 좋게 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“Because it’s a dragon,”", "\"이놈은 용이니까요.\""], ["muttered Grigory.", "그리고리가 웅얼거렸다."]], [["“A dragon?", "\"용이라니?"], ["What dragon?”", "무슨 용 말이오?\""]], [["Grigory did not speak for some time.", "그리고리는 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["“It’s a confusion of nature,”", "\"자연의 어그러짐이지요.\""], ["he muttered vaguely, but firmly, and obviously unwilling to say more.", "그는 막연하게, 그러나 단호하게, 그리고 더는 말하기를 명백히 꺼리는 기색으로 웅얼거렸다."]], [["They laughed, and of course christened the poor baby.", "사람들은 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 물론 그 가엾은 아이에게 세례를 베풀었다."], ["Grigory prayed earnestly at the font, but his opinion of the new-born child remained unchanged.", "그리고리는 세례반 앞에서 간절히 기도했으나, 그 갓난아이에 대한 그의 생각은 변함이 없었다."], ["Yet he did not interfere in any way.", "그러면서도 그는 어떤 식으로도 끼어들지 않았다."], ["As long as the sickly infant lived he scarcely looked at it, tried indeed not to notice it, and for the most part kept out of the cottage.", "병약한 아이가 살아 있는 동안 그는 좀처럼 그 아이를 들여다보지 않았고, 아예 눈길조차 주지 않으려 했으며, 대개는 오두막을 멀리한 채 지냈다."], ["But when, at the end of a fortnight, the baby died of thrush, he himself laid the child in its little coffin, looked at it in profound grief, and when they were filling up the shallow little grave he fell on his knees and bowed down to the earth.", "그러나 보름 만에 아이가 아구창으로 죽고 말았을 때, 그는 손수 아이를 그 작은 관에 뉘었고, 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 아이를 바라보았으며, 사람들이 그 얕은 작은 무덤을 메우고 있을 때 무릎을 꿇고 땅에 엎드려 절했다."], ["He did not for years afterwards mention his child, nor did Marfa speak of the baby before him, and, even if Grigory were not present, she never spoke of it above a whisper. Marfa observed that, from the day of the burial, he devoted himself to “religion,”", "그 뒤로 그는 여러 해 동안 제 자식을 입에 올리지 않았고, 마르파 또한 그 앞에서 아이 이야기를 꺼내지 않았으며, 설령 그리고리가 없는 자리라 해도 결코 속삭이는 소리보다 크게는 그 이야기를 하지 않았다."], ["and took to reading the Lives of the Saints, for the most part sitting alone and in silence, and always putting on his big, round, silver-rimmed spectacles.", "마르파의 눈에 띈 것은, 그가 그 매장의 날부터 \"신앙\"에 몰두하여 성인전을 읽기 시작했다는 것이었으니, 그는 대개 홀로 말없이 앉아, 늘 크고 둥근 은테 안경을 쓰고 그것을 읽었다."], ["He rarely read aloud, only perhaps in Lent.", "소리 내어 읽는 일은 드물었고, 그래봤자 사순절에나 그러했다."], ["He was fond of the Book of Job, and had somehow got hold of a copy of the sayings and sermons of “the God-fearing Father Isaac the Syrian,” which he read persistently for years together, understanding very little of it, but perhaps prizing and loving it the more for that.", "그는 욥기를 즐겨 읽었고, 또 어쩌다가 \"하느님을 경외하는 시리아 사람 이사크 신부\"의 어록과 설교집을 한 권 손에 넣어 여러 해를 두고 끈질기게 읽었는데, 그 내용을 거의 이해하지 못했으나 어쩌면 바로 그 까닭에 그것을 더욱 귀히 여기고 사랑했는지도 모른다."], ["Of late he had begun to listen to the doctrines of the sect of Flagellants settled in the neighborhood.", "근래에 그는 인근에 자리 잡은 채찍고행파 교단의 교리에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."], ["He was evidently shaken by them, but judged it unfitting to go over to the new faith.", "그는 분명 그것에 마음이 흔들렸으나, 새 신앙으로 넘어가는 것은 합당치 않다고 판단했다."], ["His habit of theological reading gave him an expression of still greater gravity.", "신학서적을 읽는 그 버릇은 그에게 한층 더 근엄한 표정을 안겨 주었다."]], [["He was perhaps predisposed to mysticism.", "그는 어쩌면 신비주의에 끌리기 쉬운 천성이었는지도 모른다."], ["And the birth of his deformed child, and its death, had, as though by special design, been accompanied by another strange and marvelous event, which, as he said later, had left a “stamp” upon his soul.", "그리고 그의 기형아의 탄생과 그 죽음에는, 마치 특별한 섭리에 의한 것인 양, 또 하나의 기이하고 놀라운 사건이 따라붙었으니, 그것은 훗날 그 자신의 말마따나 그의 영혼에 \"도장\"을 찍어 놓았다."], ["It happened that, on the very night after the burial of his child, Marfa was awakened by the wail of a new-born baby.", "자식을 묻은 바로 그날 밤, 마르파는 갓난아이의 울음소리에 잠을 깼다. 그녀는 겁에 질려 남편을 깨웠다."], ["She was frightened and waked her husband. He listened and said he thought it was more like some one groaning, “it might be a woman.”", "그는 귀를 기울이더니 그것이 아이 울음이라기보다는 누군가 신음하는 소리에 더 가까운 것 같다고, \"여자일지도 모르겠다\"고 말했다."], ["He got up and dressed.", "그는 일어나 옷을 입었다."], ["It was a rather warm night in May.", "제법 따스한 오월의 밤이었다."], ["As he went down the steps, he distinctly heard groans coming from the garden.", "그가 계단을 내려가는데, 정원 쪽에서 신음 소리가 또렷이 들려왔다."], ["But the gate from the yard into the garden was locked at night, and there was no other way of entering it, for it was enclosed all round by a strong, high fence.", "그러나 마당에서 정원으로 통하는 문은 밤이면 잠가 두었고, 정원은 사방이 튼튼하고 높은 울타리로 둘러쳐져 있어 그 밖에는 달리 들어갈 길이 없었다."], ["Going back into the house, Grigory lighted a lantern, took the garden key, and taking no notice of the hysterical fears of his wife, who was still persuaded that she heard a child crying, and that it was her own baby crying and calling for her, went into the garden in silence.", "그리고리는 집으로 되돌아가 등불을 켜고 정원 열쇠를 챙겨 들었는데, 여전히 아이 울음소리를 들었다고, 그것이 제 갓난아이가 울며 자기를 부르는 소리라고 믿고 있는 아내의 발작적인 두려움 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않고 말없이 정원으로 들어섰다."], ["There he heard at once that the groans came from the bath-house that stood near the garden gate, and that they were the groans of a woman.", "그곳에서 그는 그 신음 소리가 정원 문 가까이에 서 있는 목욕탕에서 나오는 것이며, 그것이 한 여자의 신음이라는 것을 단박에 알아챘다."], ["Opening the door of the bath-house, he saw a sight which petrified him.", "목욕탕 문을 열어젖힌 그는 그 자리에서 굳어 버릴 광경을 마주했다."], ["An idiot girl, who wandered about the streets and was known to the whole town by the nickname of Lizaveta Smerdyastchaya (Stinking Lizaveta), had got into the bath-house and had just given birth to a child.", "거리를 떠돌아다니며 온 읍내에 '냄새나는 리자베타'(리자베타 스메르댜시차야)라는 별명으로 알려진 백치 처녀가 목욕탕 안으로 기어들어 와 막 아이를 낳은 것이었다. 그녀는 아이를 곁에 둔 채 죽어 가며 누워 있었다."], ["She lay dying with the baby beside her. She said nothing, for she had never been able to speak.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 평생 말이라고는 할 줄 몰랐기 때문이다."], ["But her story needs a chapter to itself.", "그러나 그녀의 사연은 따로 한 장을 필요로 한다."]], [["# Chapter II. Lizaveta", "# Chapter II. Lizaveta"]], [["There was one circumstance which struck Grigory particularly, and confirmed a very unpleasant and revolting suspicion.", "그리고리를 유난히 충격에 빠뜨리고, 매우 불쾌하고 역겨운 의심을 굳혀 준 한 가지 정황이 있었다."], ["This Lizaveta was a dwarfish creature, “not five foot within a wee bit,” as many of the pious old women said pathetically about her, after her death.", "이 리자베타는 난쟁이 같은 존재로, 그녀가 죽은 뒤 경건한 노파들이 가엾이 여기며 말하곤 했듯이 \"키가 다섯 자에서 손가락 한 마디쯤 모자랐다.\""], ["Her broad, healthy, red face had a look of blank idiocy and the fixed stare in her eyes was unpleasant, in spite of their meek expression.", "넓적하고 건강하며 불그레한 얼굴에는 텅 빈 백치의 표정이 어려 있었고, 두 눈에 박힌 멈춘 듯한 시선은 온순한 빛을 띠었음에도 불쾌하기 짝이 없었다."], ["She wandered about, summer and winter alike, barefooted, wearing nothing but a hempen smock.", "그녀는 여름이건 겨울이건 가리지 않고 맨발로, 삼베 적삼 한 벌만 걸친 채 떠돌아다녔다."], ["Her coarse, almost black hair curled like lamb’s wool, and formed a sort of huge cap on her head.", "거칠고 거의 검다시피 한 머리카락은 양털처럼 곱슬거리며 머리 위에 일종의 커다란 모자 같은 형상을 이루었다."], ["It was always crusted with mud, and had leaves, bits of stick, and shavings clinging to it, as she always slept on the ground and in the dirt.", "그것은 늘 진흙으로 딱딱하게 엉겨 있었고, 나뭇잎과 잔가지 조각, 대팻밥 따위가 들러붙어 있었으니, 그녀가 언제나 맨땅 위, 흙먼지 속에서 잠들었기 때문이다."], ["Her father, a homeless, sickly drunkard, called Ilya, had lost everything and lived many years as a workman with some well-to-do tradespeople.", "그녀의 아버지는 집도 없이 병들어 술에 절어 사는 사내로 일리야라는 이름이었는데, 가진 것을 모조리 잃고 여러 해 동안 어느 살림 넉넉한 상인 집에서 막일꾼으로 지냈다."], ["Her mother had long been dead.", "어머니는 이미 오래전에 죽고 없었다."], ["Spiteful and diseased, Ilya used to beat Lizaveta inhumanly whenever she returned to him.", "심술궂고 병든 일리야는 리자베타가 제게 돌아올 때마다 사람답지 않게 두들겨 패곤 했다."], ["But she rarely did so, for every one in the town was ready to look after her as being an idiot, and so specially dear to God.", "그러나 그녀가 그에게 돌아오는 일은 드물었으니, 온 고을 사람이 그녀를 백치라 하여, 그래서 하느님께 특별히 사랑받는 자라 하여 기꺼이 거두어 보살피려 했기 때문이다."], ["Ilya’s employers, and many others in the town, especially of the tradespeople, tried to clothe her better, and always rigged her out with high boots and sheepskin coat for the winter.", "일리야의 고용주들과 고을의 많은 사람, 특히 상인들은 그녀에게 옷이라도 변변히 입혀 주려 애썼고, 겨울이면 으레 높은 장화와 양가죽 외투로 그녀를 그럴듯하게 차려입혔다."], ["But, although she allowed them to dress her up without resisting, she usually went away, preferably to the cathedral porch, and taking off all that had been given her—kerchief, sheepskin, skirt or boots—she left them there and walked away barefoot in her smock as before.", "그러나 그녀는 사람들이 입혀 주는 대로 저항 없이 받아 입기는 했으나, 으레 어디론가 가 버렸는데, 주로 대성당 현관 쪽으로 가서는 받은 것을 모두—머릿수건이며 양가죽 외투며 치마며 장화며—벗어 그 자리에 남겨 두고, 전처럼 적삼 차림으로 맨발이 되어 걸어가 버리곤 했다."], ["It happened on one occasion that a new governor of the province, making a tour of inspection in our town, saw Lizaveta, and was wounded in his tenderest susceptibilities.", "한번은 이런 일이 있었으니, 새로 부임한 도지사가 우리 고을을 순시하다가 리자베타를 보고는 가장 여린 감수성에 상처를 입었다."], ["And though he was told she was an idiot, he pronounced that for a young woman of twenty to wander about in nothing but a smock was a breach of the proprieties, and must not occur again.", "그녀가 백치라는 말을 들었음에도 그는, 스무 살 된 젊은 여자가 적삼 한 벌만 걸치고 떠돌아다니는 것은 풍기를 어지럽히는 일이니 다시는 이런 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 선언했다."], ["But the governor went his way, and Lizaveta was left as she was.", "그러나 도지사는 제 갈 길로 가 버렸고, 리자베타는 여전히 그대로 남았다."], ["At last her father died, which made her even more acceptable in the eyes of the religious persons of the town, as an orphan.", "마침내 그녀의 아버지가 죽자, 그녀는 고아가 되어 고을의 신앙심 깊은 사람들 눈에 한층 더 가엾고 받아들일 만한 존재가 되었다."], ["In fact, every one seemed to like her; even the boys did not tease her, and the boys of our town, especially the schoolboys, are a mischievous set.", "사실 모두가 그녀를 좋아하는 듯했고, 사내아이들조차 그녀를 놀리지 않았는데, 우리 고을의 사내아이들, 특히 학생들이 짓궂기로는 이만저만이 아니었음을 생각하면 놀라운 일이었다."], ["She would walk into strange houses, and no one drove her away.", "그녀는 낯선 집에 거리낌 없이 들어가곤 했으나 아무도 그녀를 내쫓지 않았다."], ["Every one was kind to her and gave her something.", "모두가 그녀에게 친절했고 무언가를 쥐여 주었다."], ["If she were given a copper, she would take it, and at once drop it in the alms-jug of the church or prison.", "동전 한 닢을 받으면 그녀는 그것을 받아 들고는 곧장 교회나 감옥의 헌금 단지에 넣어 버렸다."], ["If she were given a roll or bun in the market, she would hand it to the first child she met.", "장터에서 둥근 빵이나 호빵을 받으면 그녀는 맨 처음 마주친 아이에게 건네주었다."], ["Sometimes she would stop one of the richest ladies in the town and give it to her, and the lady would be pleased to take it.", "더러는 고을에서 가장 부유한 부인들 중 하나를 붙들어 세워 그것을 주기도 했는데, 그 부인은 기꺼이 그것을 받았다."], ["She herself never tasted anything but black bread and water.", "정작 그녀 자신은 검은 빵과 물 외에는 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다."], ["If she went into an expensive shop, where there were costly goods or money lying about, no one kept watch on her, for they knew that if she saw thousands of roubles overlooked by them, she would not have touched a farthing.", "값비싼 물건이나 돈이 아무렇게나 놓여 있는 비싼 가게에 들어가더라도 아무도 그녀를 감시하지 않았으니, 그녀라면 사람들이 무심코 놓아둔 수천 루블을 보더라도 단돈 한 푼 손대지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She scarcely ever went to church.", "그녀는 교회에 거의 나가지 않았다."], ["She slept either in the church porch or climbed over a hurdle (there are many hurdles instead of fences to this day in our town) into a kitchen garden.", "그녀는 교회 현관에서 잠을 자거나, 울짱(우리 고을에는 지금도 울타리 대신 울짱이 많다)을 넘어 채마밭으로 기어들어 가 잠을 잤다."], ["She used at least once a week to turn up “at home,” that is at the house of her father’s former employers, and in the winter went there every night, and slept either in the passage or the cowhouse.", "그녀는 적어도 일주일에 한 번은 \"집에\", 그러니까 아버지의 옛 고용주 집에 모습을 드러내곤 했고, 겨울이면 밤마다 그곳으로 가서 행랑이나 외양간에서 잠을 잤다."], ["People were amazed that she could stand such a life, but she was accustomed to it, and, although she was so tiny, she was of a robust constitution.", "사람들은 그녀가 이런 생활을 어떻게 견뎌 내는지 놀라워했으나, 그녀는 그것에 익숙해져 있었고, 비록 그토록 작은 몸집이었으나 체질만은 튼튼했다."], ["Some of the townspeople declared that she did all this only from pride, but that is hardly credible.", "고을 사람 중에는 그녀가 이 모든 일을 오로지 자존심 때문에 한다고 단언하는 이도 있었으나, 그것은 도무지 믿기 어려운 말이었다."], ["She could hardly speak, and only from time to time uttered an inarticulate grunt.", "그녀는 말이라고는 거의 못 했고, 다만 이따금 알아들을 수 없는 끙끙 소리를 낼 따름이었다."], ["How could she have been proud?", "그런 그녀에게 자존심이라니 가당키나 한 말인가?"]], [["It happened one clear, warm, moonlight night in September (many years ago) five or six drunken revelers were returning from the club at a very late hour, according to our provincial notions.", "(여러 해 전) 구월의 어느 맑고 따뜻한 달밤에 이런 일이 있었다. 술에 취한 대여섯 명의 한량들이 우리 지방의 관념으로 보아 몹시 늦은 시각에 클럽에서 돌아오는 길이었다."], ["They passed through the “back-way,” which led between the back gardens of the houses, with hurdles on either side.", "그들은 집들의 뒤뜰 사이로 양쪽에 울짱이 늘어선 \"뒷길\"을 지났다."], ["This way leads out on to the bridge over the long, stinking pool which we were accustomed to call a river.", "이 길은 우리가 강이라 부르곤 하던 길고 악취 풍기는 웅덩이 위에 놓인 다리로 통했다."], ["Among the nettles and burdocks under the hurdle our revelers saw Lizaveta asleep.", "한량들은 울짱 아래 쐐기풀과 우엉 더미 사이에서 리자베타가 잠들어 있는 것을 보았다."], ["They stopped to look at her, laughing, and began jesting with unbridled licentiousness.", "그들은 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 들여다보며 웃어 댔고, 거리낌 없는 음탕한 농지거리를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["It occurred to one young gentleman to make the whimsical inquiry whether any one could possibly look upon such an animal as a woman, and so forth....", "그 가운데 한 젊은 신사가 짓궂은 호기심을 발동해, 과연 누가 이런 짐승 같은 것을 여자로 볼 수 있겠느냐는 둥 시답잖은 물음을 던졌다……"], ["They all pronounced with lofty repugnance that it was impossible.", "모두가 고고한 혐오감을 드러내며 그것은 불가능한 일이라고 단언했다."], ["But Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was among them, sprang forward and declared that it was by no means impossible, and that, indeed, there was a certain piquancy about it, and so on....", "그러나 그 무리에 끼어 있던 표도르 파블로비치가 불쑥 앞으로 나서더니, 결코 불가능하지 않을뿐더러 오히려 거기엔 묘한 짜릿함이 있다는 둥 떠들어 댔다……"], ["It is true that at that time he was overdoing his part as a buffoon.", "사실 그 무렵 그는 어릿광대 노릇을 도가 지나치게 하고 있었다."], ["He liked to put himself forward and entertain the company, ostensibly on equal terms, of course, though in reality he was on a servile footing with them.", "그는 앞에 나서서 좌중을 즐겁게 하는 것을 좋아했는데, 겉으로는 물론 대등한 처지인 척했으나 실상은 그들 앞에서 비굴한 신세였다."], ["It was just at the time when he had received the news of his first wife’s death in Petersburg, and, with crape upon his hat, was drinking and behaving so shamelessly that even the most reckless among us were shocked at the sight of him.", "마침 그것은 그가 페테르부르크에서 첫 아내가 죽었다는 소식을 받은 직후였고, 모자에 상장을 두른 채 술을 마시며 어찌나 파렴치하게 굴던지 우리 가운데 가장 막돼먹은 자들조차 그 꼴을 보고 충격을 받을 지경이었다."], ["The revelers, of course, laughed at this unexpected opinion; and one of them even began challenging him to act upon it.", "한량들은 물론 이 뜻밖의 의견에 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 그 가운데 하나는 심지어 그더러 어디 그 말대로 해 보라고 부추기기까지 했다."], ["The others repelled the idea even more emphatically, although still with the utmost hilarity, and at last they went on their way.", "다른 자들은 여전히 더없이 흥겨운 가운데서도 그 생각을 한층 더 단호하게 물리쳤고, 마침내 그들은 제 갈 길로 떠났다."], ["Later on, Fyodor Pavlovitch swore that he had gone with them, and perhaps it was so, no one knows for certain, and no one ever knew.", "훗날 표도르 파블로비치는 자기도 그들과 함께 갔노라고 맹세했는데, 어쩌면 정말 그랬을지도 모르나, 아무도 확실히 알지 못했고, 또 끝내 아무도 알 수 없었다."], ["But five or six months later, all the town was talking, with intense and sincere indignation, of Lizaveta’s condition, and trying to find out who was the miscreant who had wronged her.", "그러나 대여섯 달이 지나자 온 고을이 격렬하고도 진실한 분노에 차서 리자베타의 처지를 입에 올리며, 그녀를 욕보인 그 파렴치한 놈이 대체 누구인지 알아내려 들었다."], ["Then suddenly a terrible rumor was all over the town that this miscreant was no other than Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그러다 별안간 온 고을에 무서운 소문이 퍼졌으니, 그 파렴치한 놈이 다름 아닌 표도르 파블로비치라는 것이었다."], ["Who set the rumor going?", "누가 이 소문을 퍼뜨렸는가?"], ["Of that drunken band five had left the town and the only one still among us was an elderly and much respected civil councilor, the father of grown-up daughters, who could hardly have spread the tale, even if there had been any foundation for it.", "그 술 취한 무리 가운데 다섯은 고을을 떠났고, 우리 가운데 아직 남아 있는 자라곤 다 큰 딸들을 둔 나이 지긋하고 매우 존경받는 문관 참사관 하나뿐이었는데, 설령 무슨 근거가 있었다 한들 그가 이런 이야기를 퍼뜨렸을 리는 없었다."], ["But rumor pointed straight at Fyodor Pavlovitch, and persisted in pointing at him.", "그러나 소문은 곧장 표도르 파블로비치를 가리켰고, 끈질기게 그를 가리키기를 멈추지 않았다."], ["Of course this was no great grievance to him: he would not have troubled to contradict a set of tradespeople.", "물론 이것은 그에게 그리 대수로운 일도 아니었으니, 그는 한낱 상인 무리를 상대로 굳이 반박하느라 수고할 위인이 아니었다."], ["In those days he was proud, and did not condescend to talk except in his own circle of the officials and nobles, whom he entertained so well.", "그 시절 그는 거만하여, 자기가 그토록 융숭히 대접하던 관리와 귀족들로 이루어진 자기 모임 밖에서는 입을 여는 것조차 탐탁잖게 여겼다."]], [["At the time, Grigory stood up for his master vigorously.", "당시 그리고리는 자기 주인을 위해 힘껏 두둔하고 나섰다."], ["He provoked quarrels and altercations in defense of him and succeeded in bringing some people round to his side.", "그는 주인을 변호하느라 싸움과 언쟁을 마다하지 않았고, 마침내 몇몇 사람을 제 편으로 끌어들이는 데 성공했다."], ["“It’s the wench’s own fault,”", "\"그건 그 계집년 잘못이오.\""], ["he asserted, and the culprit was Karp, a dangerous convict, who had escaped from prison and whose name was well known to us, as he had hidden in our town.", "그는 이렇게 단언했고, 죄를 지은 자는 카르프라는, 감옥에서 탈출하여 우리 고을에 숨어 지냈던 까닭에 그 이름이 우리에게 익히 알려진 위험한 죄수라는 것이었다."], ["This conjecture sounded plausible, for it was remembered that Karp had been in the neighborhood just at that time in the autumn, and had robbed three people.", "이 추측은 그럴듯하게 들렸으니, 카르프가 마침 그해 가을 그 무렵에 인근에 있었고 세 사람의 재물을 털었다는 것이 사람들의 기억에 남아 있었기 때문이다."], ["But this affair and all the talk about it did not estrange popular sympathy from the poor idiot.", "그러나 이 사건과 그것을 둘러싼 온갖 입방아도 가엾은 백치 여자를 향한 사람들의 동정을 거두어 가지는 못했다."], ["She was better looked after than ever.", "그녀는 그 어느 때보다 더 극진히 보살핌을 받았다."], ["A well-to-do merchant’s widow named Kondratyev arranged to take her into her house at the end of April, meaning not to let her go out until after the confinement.", "콘드라티예바라는 살림 넉넉한 상인의 미망인이 사월 말께 그녀를 자기 집에 들이기로 하고, 해산할 때까지는 밖으로 내보내지 않으려 했다."], ["They kept a constant watch over her, but in spite of their vigilance she escaped on the very last day, and made her way into Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden.", "사람들은 그녀를 줄곧 지켜보았으나, 그 경계에도 불구하고 그녀는 바로 마지막 날 빠져나가, 표도르 파블로비치의 정원으로 들어갔다."], ["How, in her condition, she managed to climb over the high, strong fence remained a mystery.", "그 몸으로 어떻게 높고 견고한 울타리를 넘었는지는 끝내 수수께끼로 남았다."], ["Some maintained that she must have been lifted over by somebody; others hinted at something more uncanny.", "누군가가 그녀를 들어 올려 넘겨 주었을 것이라고 주장하는 이들도 있었고, 그보다 더 으스스한 무엇을 넌지시 암시하는 이들도 있었다."], ["The most likely explanation is that it happened naturally—that Lizaveta, accustomed to clambering over hurdles to sleep in gardens, had somehow managed to climb this fence, in spite of her condition, and had leapt down, injuring herself.", "가장 그럴듯한 설명은 그것이 자연스레 일어난 일이라는 것이니—정원에서 잠을 자려 울짱을 넘어 다니는 데 익숙했던 리자베타가 그 몸으로도 어찌어찌 이 울타리를 기어올라, 뛰어내리다가 몸을 다쳤으리라는 것이다."]], [["Grigory rushed to Marfa and sent her to Lizaveta, while he ran to fetch an old midwife who lived close by.", "그리고리는 마르파에게 달려가 그녀를 리자베타에게 보내고, 자기는 바로 가까이에 사는 늙은 산파를 데리러 달려갔다."], ["They saved the baby, but Lizaveta died at dawn.", "그들은 아기는 살려 냈으나, 리자베타는 새벽녘에 숨을 거두었다."], ["Grigory took the baby, brought it home, and making his wife sit down, put it on her lap.", "그리고리는 아기를 받아 안고 집으로 데려와, 아내를 자리에 앉히고는 그 무릎 위에 아기를 올려놓았다."], ["“A child of God—an orphan is akin to all,”", "\"하느님의 아이요—고아는 모두의 핏줄이거니와,\""], ["he said, “and to us above others.", "그가 말했다, \"우리에겐 그 누구보다도 그러하지."], ["Our little lost one has sent us this, who has come from the devil’s son and a holy innocent.", "우리의 잃어버린 어린것이 이 아이를 우리에게 보내 준 것이니, 악마의 아들과 거룩한 백치 사이에서 나온 아이라네."], ["Nurse him and weep no more.”", "이 아이를 거두어 키우고, 더는 울지 말게나.\""]], [["So Marfa brought up the child.", "그리하여 마르파가 아이를 길렀다."], ["He was christened Pavel, to which people were not slow in adding Fyodorovitch (son of Fyodor).", "아이는 파벨이라는 세례명을 받았는데, 사람들은 곧 거기에 표도로비치(표도르의 아들)를 갖다 붙이기를 주저하지 않았다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch did not object to any of this, and thought it amusing, though he persisted vigorously in denying his responsibility.", "표도르 파블로비치는 이 모든 일에 아무 이의도 달지 않았고, 오히려 재미있어했으나, 자기 책임만은 여전히 한사코 부인했다."], ["The townspeople were pleased at his adopting the foundling.", "고을 사람들은 그가 그 업둥이를 거두어들인 것을 흐뭇해했다."], ["Later on, Fyodor Pavlovitch invented a surname for the child, calling him Smerdyakov, after his mother’s nickname.", "훗날 표도르 파블로비치는 그 아이에게 성(姓)을 하나 지어 붙였으니, 어미의 별명을 따서 스메르댜코프라 불렀다."]], [["So this Smerdyakov became Fyodor Pavlovitch’s second servant, and was living in the lodge with Grigory and Marfa at the time our story begins.", "이렇게 하여 이 스메르댜코프는 표도르 파블로비치의 두 번째 하인이 되었고, 우리 이야기가 시작되는 무렵에는 그리고리와 마르파와 함께 별채에서 살고 있었다."], ["He was employed as cook.", "그는 요리사로 부려지고 있었다."], ["I ought to say something of this Smerdyakov, but I am ashamed of keeping my readers’ attention so long occupied with these common menials, and I will go back to my story, hoping to say more of Smerdyakov in the course of it.", "마땅히 이 스메르댜코프에 관해서도 무언가 이야기해야 하겠으나, 독자의 주의를 이런 천한 하인들에게 이토록 오래 붙들어 두는 것이 면구스러우니, 이야기를 풀어 가는 가운데 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 더 이야기할 수 있기를 바라며 본래의 이야기로 돌아가겠다."]], [["# Chapter III. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Verse", "# Chapter III. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Verse"]], [["Alyosha remained for some time irresolute after hearing the command his father shouted to him from the carriage.", "알료샤는 아버지가 마차에서 그에게 소리쳐 던진 명령을 듣고 한동안 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["But in spite of his uneasiness he did not stand still.", "그러나 마음이 편치 않았음에도 그는 가만히 서 있지는 않았다."], ["That was not his way.", "그것은 그의 방식이 아니었다."], ["He went at once to the kitchen to find out what his father had been doing above.", "그는 아버지가 위에서 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 알아보려고 곧장 부엌으로 갔다."], ["Then he set off, trusting that on the way he would find some answer to the doubt tormenting him.", "그러고는 길을 가는 동안 자기를 괴롭히는 의혹에 대한 답을 찾을 수 있으리라 믿으면서 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["I hasten to add that his father’s shouts, commanding him to return home “with his mattress and pillow”", "미리 덧붙여 말해 두자면, \"요와 베개를 가지고\" 집으로 돌아오라는 아버지의 고함은 조금도 그를 겁먹게 하지 못했다."], ["did not frighten him in the least. He understood perfectly that those peremptory shouts were merely “a flourish”", "그는 그 위압적인 고함이 효과를 노린 \"허세\"에 지나지 않음을 완벽하게 이해하고 있었다."], ["to produce an effect. In the same way a tradesman in our town who was celebrating his name-day with a party of friends, getting angry at being refused more vodka, smashed up his own crockery and furniture and tore his own and his wife’s clothes, and finally broke his windows, all for the sake of effect.", "마찬가지로 우리 고장의 한 장사꾼은 친구들을 불러 자기 명명일을 축하하다가 보드카를 더 달라는 청을 거절당하자 화가 치밀어 자기 그릇과 가구를 박살 내고 제 옷과 아내의 옷을 찢고 끝내는 제 집 창문까지 깨뜨렸는데, 이 모든 것이 오로지 효과를 노린 짓이었다."], ["Next day, of course, when he was sober, he regretted the broken cups and saucers.", "물론 이튿날 술이 깨자 그는 깨진 잔과 받침을 두고 후회했다."], ["Alyosha knew that his father would let him go back to the monastery next day, possibly even that evening.", "알료샤는 아버지가 이튿날, 어쩌면 그날 저녁에라도 자기를 수도원으로 돌려보내 주리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Moreover, he was fully persuaded that his father might hurt any one else, but would not hurt him.", "더구나 그는 아버지가 다른 누구에게는 상처를 줄지언정 자기에게만은 그러지 않으리라고 확신하고 있었다."], ["Alyosha was certain that no one in the whole world ever would want to hurt him, and, what is more, he knew that no one could hurt him.", "알료샤는 온 세상 누구도 결코 자기에게 상처를 주려 하지 않으리라고, 게다가 그 누구도 자기에게 상처를 줄 수 없으리라고 확신하고 있었다."], ["This was for him an axiom, assumed once for all without question, and he went his way without hesitation, relying on it.", "이것은 그에게 하나의 공리로서, 한번 받아들인 뒤로는 두 번 다시 의심하지 않는 진리였으며, 그는 그것에 의지해 망설임 없이 길을 갔다."]], [["But at that moment an anxiety of a different sort disturbed him, and worried him the more because he could not formulate it.", "그러나 바로 그때 그는 또 다른 종류의 불안에 시달리고 있었는데, 그것을 또렷이 표현할 수 없었던 까닭에 더욱더 마음이 쓰였다."], ["It was the fear of a woman, of Katerina Ivanovna, who had so urgently entreated him in the note handed to him by Madame Hohlakov to come and see her about something.", "그것은 한 여자에 대한, 곧 카테리나 이바노브나에 대한 두려움이었으니, 그녀는 호흘라코바 부인이 전해 준 쪽지를 통해 무슨 일로든 자기를 찾아와 달라고 그토록 절박하게 간청했던 것이다."], ["This request and the necessity of going had at once aroused an uneasy feeling in his heart, and this feeling had grown more and more painful all the morning in spite of the scenes at the hermitage and at the Father Superior’s.", "이 청과, 또 가야만 한다는 사실은 곧장 그의 마음에 불안한 감정을 불러일으켰고, 이 감정은 암자에서와 수도원장 처소에서 벌어진 소동에도 불구하고 오전 내내 점점 더 고통스럽게 자라났다."], ["He was not uneasy because he did not know what she would speak of and what he must answer.", "그가 불안했던 것은 그녀가 무슨 이야기를 할지, 자기가 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 알지 못해서가 아니었다."], ["And he was not afraid of her simply as a woman.", "또한 그가 그녀를 그저 여자라는 이유로 두려워한 것도 아니었다."], ["Though he knew little of women, he had spent his life, from early childhood till he entered the monastery, entirely with women.", "비록 그가 여자에 대해 아는 것은 적었으나, 그는 어린 시절부터 수도원에 들어가기까지 평생을 오직 여자들 틈에서 보냈다."], ["He was afraid of that woman, Katerina Ivanovna.", "그가 두려워한 것은 바로 그 여자, 카테리나 이바노브나였다."], ["He had been afraid of her from the first time he saw her.", "그는 그녀를 처음 본 순간부터 두려워했다."], ["He had only seen her two or three times, and had only chanced to say a few words to her.", "그는 그녀를 두세 번 보았을 뿐이고, 어쩌다 몇 마디 말을 건넸을 뿐이었다."], ["He thought of her as a beautiful, proud, imperious girl.", "그는 그녀를 아름답고 오만하며 위압적인 처녀로 여기고 있었다."], ["It was not her beauty which troubled him, but something else.", "그를 괴롭힌 것은 그녀의 아름다움이 아니라 무언가 다른 것이었다."], ["And the vagueness of his apprehension increased the apprehension itself.", "그리고 그 두려움의 막연함이 두려움 자체를 더 키웠다."], ["The girl’s aims were of the noblest, he knew that.", "그 처녀의 목적이 더없이 고결하다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["She was trying to save his brother Dmitri simply through generosity, though he had already behaved badly to her.", "그녀는 형 드미트리가 이미 자기에게 못되게 굴었음에도 오로지 너그러운 마음으로 그를 구하려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Yet, although Alyosha recognized and did justice to all these fine and generous sentiments, a shiver began to run down his back as soon as he drew near her house.", "그러나 알료샤가 이 모든 곱고 너그러운 마음을 알아보고 정당하게 평가하면서도, 그녀의 집에 가까이 다가가자마자 등줄기에 오싹한 한기가 흐르기 시작했다."]], [["He reflected that he would not find Ivan, who was so intimate a friend, with her, for Ivan was certainly now with his father.", "그는 그토록 가까운 벗인 이반이 그녀와 함께 있지는 않으리라고 생각했으니, 이반은 분명 지금 아버지와 함께 있을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["Dmitri he was even more certain not to find there, and he had a foreboding of the reason.", "드미트리는 더더욱 거기 없으리라고 확신했으며, 그 까닭을 어렴풋이 예감하고 있었다."], ["And so his conversation would be with her alone.", "그리하여 대화는 그녀와 단둘이서만 나누게 될 터였다."], ["He had a great longing to run and see his brother Dmitri before that fateful interview.", "그는 그 운명적인 만남에 앞서 형 드미트리에게 달려가 만나 보고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."], ["Without showing him the letter, he could talk to him about it.", "편지를 보여 주지는 않더라도 그것에 관해 형과 이야기를 나눌 수는 있을 터였다."], ["But Dmitri lived a long way off, and he was sure to be away from home too.", "그러나 드미트리는 멀리 떨어진 곳에 살았고, 게다가 분명 집에 없을 것이 뻔했다."], ["Standing still for a minute, he reached a final decision.", "잠시 멈춰 선 끝에 그는 마침내 결심을 굳혔다."], ["Crossing himself with a rapid and accustomed gesture, and at once smiling, he turned resolutely in the direction of his terrible lady.", "빠르고 익숙한 동작으로 성호를 긋고는 곧 미소를 지으며, 그는 단호하게 그 무서운 부인이 있는 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["He knew her house.", "그는 그녀의 집을 알고 있었다."], ["If he went by the High Street and then across the market-place, it was a long way round.", "큰길을 지나 시장 광장을 가로질러 가면 한참을 돌아가야 했다."], ["Though our town is small, it is scattered, and the houses are far apart.", "우리 고장은 작기는 해도 흩어져 있어 집들이 서로 멀리 떨어져 있었다."], ["And meanwhile his father was expecting him, and perhaps had not yet forgotten his command.", "그러는 동안 아버지가 그를 기다리고 있을 테고, 어쩌면 아직 그 명령을 잊지 않았을지도 몰랐다."], ["He might be unreasonable, and so he had to make haste to get there and back.", "아버지가 억지를 부릴 수도 있으니, 그는 서둘러 다녀와야만 했다."], ["So he decided to take a short cut by the back-way, for he knew every inch of the ground.", "그래서 그는 뒷길로 질러가기로 했으니, 그 일대의 땅이라면 한 치도 빠짐없이 훤히 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["This meant skirting fences, climbing over hurdles, and crossing other people’s back-yards, where every one he met knew him and greeted him.", "그러자면 울타리를 따라 돌고, 낮은 담을 넘고, 남의 집 뒤뜰을 가로질러야 했는데, 거기서 마주치는 사람마다 그를 알아보고 인사를 건넸다."], ["In this way he could reach the High Street in half the time.", "이렇게 가면 절반의 시간으로 큰길에 닿을 수 있었다."]], [["He had to pass the garden adjoining his father’s, and belonging to a little tumbledown house with four windows.", "그는 아버지네 정원에 잇닿아 있는, 창문이 네 개 달린 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 집의 정원을 지나야 했다."], ["The owner of this house, as Alyosha knew, was a bedridden old woman, living with her daughter, who had been a genteel maid-servant in generals’ families in Petersburg.", "알료샤가 아는 바로, 이 집의 주인은 자리보전을 한 늙은 여자로, 페테르부르크의 장군 댁들에서 양갓집 하녀로 일하던 딸과 함께 살고 있었다."], ["Now she had been at home a year, looking after her sick mother.", "이제 그 딸은 병든 어머니를 돌보느라 일 년째 집에 머물고 있었다."], ["She always dressed up in fine clothes, though her old mother and she had sunk into such poverty that they went every day to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s kitchen for soup and bread, which Marfa gave readily.", "그녀는 늘 고운 옷으로 차려입었는데, 늙은 어머니와 그녀가 어찌나 가난에 빠져 있던지 날마다 표도르 파블로비치네 부엌으로 수프와 빵을 얻으러 갔고, 마르파가 선뜻 내주었다."], ["Yet, though the young woman came up for soup, she had never sold any of her dresses, and one of these even had a long train—a fact which Alyosha had learned from Rakitin, who always knew everything that was going on in the town.", "그러면서도 이 젊은 여자는 수프를 얻으러 다니면서도 옷가지는 단 한 벌도 팔지 않았으니, 그중에는 긴 자락이 달린 옷까지 있었다는 것이다. 이 사실을 알료샤는 라키틴에게서 들었는데, 그는 고을 안에서 벌어지는 일이라면 무엇이든 늘 꿰고 있는 사내였다."], ["He had forgotten it as soon as he heard it, but now, on reaching the garden, he remembered the dress with the train, raised his head, which had been bowed in thought, and came upon something quite unexpected.", "그는 듣자마자 그 이야기를 잊어버렸으나, 이제 정원에 다다르자 그 자락 달린 옷이 떠올라, 생각에 잠겨 숙이고 있던 고개를 들었고, 그러다 전혀 뜻밖의 광경과 마주쳤다."]], [["Over the hurdle in the garden, Dmitri, mounted on something, was leaning forward, gesticulating violently, beckoning to him, obviously afraid to utter a word for fear of being overheard.", "정원의 낮은 담 너머에서 드미트리가 무언가 위에 올라선 채 몸을 앞으로 기울이고 격하게 손짓하며 그를 향해 손짓해 부르고 있었는데, 누가 엿들을까 두려워 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 못하는 기색이 역력했다."], ["Alyosha ran up to the hurdle.", "알료샤는 담 쪽으로 달려갔다."]], [["“It’s a good thing you looked up.", "\"고개를 들어 주길 잘했다."], ["I was nearly shouting to you,”", "하마터면 너한테 소리를 지를 뻔했지 뭐냐.\""], ["Mitya said in a joyful, hurried whisper.", "미탸가 기쁘고 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["“Climb in here quickly!", "\"어서 이리로 넘어와!"], ["How splendid that you’ve come!", "네가 와 줘서 정말 잘됐다!"], ["I was just thinking of you!”", "마침 네 생각을 하고 있던 참이거든!\""]], [["Alyosha was delighted too, but he did not know how to get over the hurdle.", "알료샤도 반가웠으나, 어떻게 담을 넘어야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Mitya put his powerful hand under his elbow to help him jump.", "미탸가 그 억센 손으로 그의 팔꿈치 밑을 받쳐 뛰어오르는 것을 도와주었다."], ["Tucking up his cassock, Alyosha leapt over the hurdle with the agility of a bare-legged street urchin.", "알료샤는 사제복 자락을 걷어 올리고는 맨다리로 거리를 누비는 부랑아 같은 날렵함으로 담을 뛰어넘었다."]], [["“Well done!", "\"잘했다!"], ["Now come along,”", "자, 이리 와.\""], ["said Mitya in an enthusiastic whisper.", "미탸가 열에 들뜬 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디로요?\""], ["whispered Alyosha, looking about him and finding himself in a deserted garden with no one near but themselves.", "알료샤가 주위를 둘러보며 속삭였는데, 그러고 보니 자기들 말고는 아무도 없는 한적한 정원 안에 있었다."], ["The garden was small, but the house was at least fifty paces away.", "정원은 작았지만 집은 적어도 쉰 걸음은 떨어져 있었다."]], [["“There’s no one here.", "\"여긴 아무도 없잖아요."], ["Why do you whisper?”", "왜 속삭이세요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Why do I whisper?", "\"내가 왜 속삭이냐고?"], ["Deuce take it!”", "빌어먹을!\""], ["cried Dmitri at the top of his voice.", "드미트리가 목청껏 소리쳤다."], ["“You see what silly tricks nature plays one.", "\"이것 봐라, 자연이 사람한테 얼마나 어리석은 장난을 치는지."], ["I am here in secret, and on the watch.", "난 지금 여기 몰래 숨어서 망을 보고 있다."], ["I’ll explain later on, but, knowing it’s a secret, I began whispering like a fool, when there’s no need.", "나중에 설명해 주마. 그런데 비밀이라는 걸 알다 보니 그럴 필요도 없는데 바보처럼 속삭이기 시작한 거야."], ["Let us go.", "가자."], ["Over there.", "저쪽으로."], ["Till then be quiet.", "그때까진 조용히 있어."], ["I want to kiss you.", "너한테 입 맞추고 싶구나."]], [["Glory to God in the world, Glory to God in me ...", "세상 가운데 계신 하느님께 영광을, 내 안에 계신 하느님께 영광을……"]], [["I was just repeating that, sitting here, before you came.”", "네가 오기 전에 여기 앉아서 바로 그 구절을 되뇌고 있었단다.\""]], [["The garden was about three acres in extent, and planted with trees only along the fence at the four sides.", "정원은 넓이가 삼 에이커쯤 되었고, 나무는 네 변의 울타리를 따라서만 심겨 있었다."], ["There were apple-trees, maples, limes and birch-trees.", "사과나무와 단풍나무, 보리수와 자작나무가 있었다."], ["The middle of the garden was an empty grass space, from which several hundredweight of hay was carried in the summer.", "정원 한가운데는 텅 빈 풀밭으로, 여름이면 거기서 건초가 수백 푸드씩 실려 나갔다."], ["The garden was let out for a few roubles for the summer.", "정원은 여름 한철 몇 루블에 세를 놓았다."], ["There were also plantations of raspberries and currants and gooseberries laid out along the sides; a kitchen garden had been planted lately near the house.", "또 가장자리를 따라 산딸기와 까치밥나무, 구스베리가 심긴 밭들이 펼쳐져 있었고, 집 가까이에는 얼마 전에 채소밭이 들어서 있었다."]], [["Dmitri led his brother to the most secluded corner of the garden.", "드미트리는 동생을 정원에서 가장 외진 구석으로 데려갔다."], ["There, in a thicket of lime-trees and old bushes of black currant, elder, snowball-tree, and lilac, there stood a tumble-down green summer-house, blackened with age.", "그곳에는 보리수 덤불과 묵은 검은까치밥나무, 딱총나무, 불두화나무, 라일락 떨기 속에 세월에 검게 그을린, 다 쓰러져 가는 초록빛 정자가 한 채 서 있었다."], ["Its walls were of lattice-work, but there was still a roof which could give shelter.", "벽은 격자세공으로 되어 있었으나 비를 가릴 만한 지붕만은 아직 남아 있었다."], ["God knows when this summer-house was built.", "이 정자가 언제 세워졌는지는 아무도 모른다."], ["There was a tradition that it had been put up some fifty years before by a retired colonel called von Schmidt, who owned the house at that time.", "한 오십 년 전쯤 당시 그 집을 소유했던 폰 슈미트라는 퇴역 대령이 지었다는 이야기가 전해 내려왔다."], ["It was all in decay, the floor was rotting, the planks were loose, the woodwork smelled musty.", "모든 것이 허물어져 가고 있었으니, 바닥은 썩어 가고, 널빤지는 헐거워졌으며, 목재에서는 곰팡내가 풍겼다."], ["In the summer-house there was a green wooden table fixed in the ground, and round it were some green benches upon which it was still possible to sit.", "정자 안에는 땅에 붙박인 초록빛 나무 탁자가 있었고 그 둘레로 아직은 앉을 만한 초록 의자 몇 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["Alyosha had at once observed his brother’s exhilarated condition, and on entering the arbor he saw half a bottle of brandy and a wineglass on the table.", "알료샤는 형이 들뜬 상태에 있음을 곧바로 알아챘는데, 정자에 들어서면서 탁자 위에 반쯤 남은 브랜디 병과 포도주잔이 놓여 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“That’s brandy,”", "\"이건 브랜디다.\" 미탸가 웃었다."], ["Mitya laughed.", "\"네 눈빛이 보이는구나. '형이 또 마시는구나!'"], ["“I see your look: ‘He’s drinking again!’ Distrust the apparition.", "하는. 환영을 믿지 마라."]], [["Distrust the worthless, lying crowd, And lay aside thy doubts.", "쓸모없고 거짓된 무리를 믿지 말고 네 의혹일랑 내려놓아라."]], [["I’m not drinking, I’m only ‘indulging,’ as that pig, your Rakitin, says.", "난 마시는 게 아니라, 그저 네 그 돼지 같은 라키틴 말마따나 '한잔 즐기는' 것뿐이다."], ["He’ll be a civil councilor one day, but he’ll always talk about ‘indulging.’ Sit down.", "그자는 언젠가 칠등관쯤은 될 텐데도 늘 '한잔 즐긴다'는 소리나 늘어놓을 거야. 앉아라."], ["I could take you in my arms, Alyosha, and press you to my bosom till I crush you, for in the whole world—in reality—in re-al-i-ty—(can you take it in?) I love no one but you!”", "알료샤, 난 너를 두 팔로 끌어안고 으스러지도록 가슴에 꼭 껴안고 싶구나. 온 세상에서—정말로—저-엉-말로—(알아듣겠냐?) 내가 사랑하는 건 너밖에 없으니까!\""]], [["He uttered the last words in a sort of exaltation.", "그는 마지막 말을 일종의 황홀경에 잠겨 내뱉었다."]], [["“No one but you and one ‘jade’ I have fallen in love with, to my ruin.", "\"너 말고는, 내 신세를 망쳐 놓은 한 '계집'밖에 없지."], ["But being in love doesn’t mean loving.", "사랑에 빠지는 것과 사랑하는 것은 별개의 일이야."], ["You may be in love with a woman and yet hate her.", "한 여자에게 사랑에 빠지면서도 그 여자를 미워할 수 있는 법이거든."], ["Remember that!", "그걸 명심해라!"], ["I can talk about it gayly still.", "아직은 명랑하게 이야기할 수 있구나."], ["Sit down here by the table and I’ll sit beside you and look at you, and go on talking.", "여기 탁자 옆에 앉아라. 난 네 곁에 앉아서 너를 바라보며 계속 이야기하마."], ["You shall keep quiet and I’ll go on talking, for the time has come.", "너는 잠자코 있어. 내가 계속 이야기할 테니까, 이제 그럴 때가 됐거든."], ["But on reflection, you know, I’d better speak quietly, for here—here—you can never tell what ears are listening.", "그런데 생각해 보니, 알겠냐, 조용히 말하는 게 낫겠다. 여기서는—여기서는—어떤 귀가 엿듣고 있을지 도무지 알 수가 없으니까."], ["I will explain everything; as they say, ‘the story will be continued.’ Why have I been longing for you?", "다 설명해 주마. 흔히들 말하듯이, '이야기는 다음으로 이어진다'고 말이야."], ["Why have I been thirsting for you all these days, and just now?", "내가 왜 너를 그토록 그리워했겠냐? 왜 요 며칠 내내, 또 바로 지금 너를 그토록 목말라했겠냐?"], ["(It’s five days since I’ve cast anchor here.) Because it’s only to you I can tell everything; because I must, because I need you, because to-morrow I shall fly from the clouds, because to-morrow life is ending and beginning.", "(내가 여기 닻을 내린 지 닷새째다.) 너한테만은 모든 걸 털어놓을 수 있으니까, 그래야만 하니까, 네가 필요하니까, 내일이면 내가 구름에서 떨어질 테니까, 내일이면 인생이 끝나면서 또 시작될 테니까."], ["Have you ever felt, have you ever dreamt of falling down a precipice into a pit?", "너는 한 번이라도 절벽에서 구덩이로 곤두박질치는 걸 느껴 본 적 있냐, 꿈에서라도 꾸어 본 적 있냐?"], ["That’s just how I’m falling, but not in a dream.", "내가 바로 그렇게 떨어지고 있는 거다, 다만 꿈속에서가 아니지."], ["And I’m not afraid, and don’t you be afraid.", "그런데 난 두렵지 않아. 너도 두려워하지 마라."], ["At least, I am afraid, but I enjoy it.", "사실 두렵긴 하지만, 그래도 난 그게 즐겁구나."], ["It’s not enjoyment though, but ecstasy.", "아니, 즐거운 게 아니라 황홀한 거야."], ["Damn it all, whatever it is!", "빌어먹을, 그게 뭐가 됐든!"], ["A strong spirit, a weak spirit, a womanish spirit—whatever it is!", "강한 영혼이든, 약한 영혼이든, 계집 같은 영혼이든—그게 뭐가 됐든! 자연을 찬미하자."], ["Let us praise nature: you see what sunshine, how clear the sky is, the leaves are all green, it’s still summer; four o’clock in the afternoon and the stillness!", "보아라, 저 햇살을, 저 맑은 하늘을, 이파리들은 죄다 푸르고, 아직 여름이로구나. 오후 네 시에 이 고요함이라니!"], ["Where were you going?”", "너 어디 가는 길이었냐?\""]], [["“I was going to father’s, but I meant to go to Katerina Ivanovna’s first.”", "\"아버지께 가는 길이었는데, 먼저 카테리나 이바노브나한테 들렀다 가려고 했어요.\""]], [["“To her, and to father! Oo!", "\"그 여자한테, 그리고 아버지한테!"], ["what a coincidence!", "오오! 이 무슨 우연이람!"], ["Why was I waiting for you?", "내가 왜 너를 기다렸겠냐?"], ["Hungering and thirsting for you in every cranny of my soul and even in my ribs?", "영혼의 구석구석, 심지어 내 갈빗대 속까지 너를 굶주리고 목말라하며 기다린 까닭이?"], ["Why, to send you to father and to her, Katerina Ivanovna, so as to have done with her and with father.", "그야 너를 아버지한테, 또 그 여자, 카테리나 이바노브나한테 보내서, 그 여자와도 아버지와도 끝장을 내기 위해서지."], ["To send an angel.", "천사를 보내려는 거다."], ["I might have sent any one, but I wanted to send an angel.", "아무나 보낼 수도 있었지만, 난 천사를 보내고 싶었어."], ["And here you are on your way to see father and her.”", "그런데 마침 네가 아버지와 그 여자를 만나러 가는 길이라니.\""]], [["“Did you really mean to send me?”", "\"형은 정말로 저를 보내려고 하셨어요?\""], ["cried Alyosha with a distressed expression.", "알료샤가 괴로운 표정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Stay!", "\"가만!"], ["You knew it!", "너도 알고 있었구나!"], ["And I see you understand it all at once.", "게다가 보아하니 단박에 다 알아챘구나."], ["But be quiet, be quiet for a time.", "하지만 조용히, 잠깐만 조용히 있어."], ["Don’t be sorry, and don’t cry.”", "슬퍼하지도 말고, 울지도 마라.\""]], [["Dmitri stood up, thought a moment, and put his finger to his forehead.", "드미트리는 일어서서 잠시 생각하더니 손가락을 이마에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“She’s asked you, written to you a letter or something, that’s why you’re going to her?", "\"그 여자가 너한테 청했지, 편지인지 뭔지를 써 보냈지, 그래서 그 여자한테 가는 거지?"], ["You wouldn’t be going except for that?”", "그게 아니면 갈 일이 없었을 테니까?\""]], [["“Here is her note.”", "\"여기 그분 쪽지가 있어요.\""], ["Alyosha took it out of his pocket.", "알료샤가 호주머니에서 그것을 꺼냈다."], ["Mitya looked through it quickly.", "미탸는 그것을 재빨리 훑어보았다."]], [["“And you were going the back-way!", "\"게다가 너는 뒷길로 오는 길이었구나!"], ["Oh, gods, I thank you for sending him by the back-way, and he came to me like the golden fish to the silly old fishermen in the fable!", "오, 신들이시여, 그를 뒷길로 보내 주셔서 감사합니다. 마치 우화에 나오는 어리석은 늙은 어부에게 황금 물고기가 찾아오듯이 그가 나한테 와 주었으니!"], ["Listen, Alyosha, listen, brother!", "들어 봐라, 알료샤, 들어 봐라, 동생아!"], ["Now I mean to tell you everything, for I must tell some one.", "이제 너한테 모든 걸 이야기하마. 누군가에게는 털어놓아야만 하니까."], ["An angel in heaven I’ve told already; but I want to tell an angel on earth.", "하늘에 있는 천사한테는 이미 이야기했지만, 땅 위의 천사한테도 이야기하고 싶구나."], ["You are an angel on earth.", "네가 바로 땅 위의 천사다."], ["You will hear and judge and forgive.", "너는 듣고 판단하고 용서해 줄 테지."], ["And that’s what I need, that some one above me should forgive.", "내가 필요한 게 바로 그거야, 누군가 나보다 위에 있는 이가 나를 용서해 주는 것."], ["Listen!", "들어 봐라!"], ["If two people break away from everything on earth and fly off into the unknown, or at least one of them, and before flying off or going to ruin he comes to some one else and says, ‘Do this for me’—some favor never asked before that could only be asked on one’s deathbed—would that other refuse, if he were a friend or a brother?”", "만약 두 사람이 이 땅의 모든 것을 떨치고 미지의 세계로 날아가 버린다면, 적어도 둘 중 하나가 그렇게 떠나거나 파멸하기 전에 다른 누군가에게 찾아가 '날 위해 이걸 해 다오'라고 청한다면—임종의 자리에서나 청할 법한, 여태껏 한 번도 청해 본 적 없는 그런 부탁을—그 다른 사람이, 만약 그가 벗이거나 형제라면, 그걸 거절하겠냐?\""]], [["“I will do it, but tell me what it is, and make haste,”", "\"제가 하겠어요. 그러니 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주세요, 어서요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Make haste!", "\"어서라!"], ["H’m!...", "흠!……"], ["Don’t be in a hurry, Alyosha, you hurry and worry yourself.", "서두르지 마라, 알료샤, 너는 서두르고 안달복달하는구나."], ["There’s no need to hurry now.", "지금은 서두를 필요가 없다."], ["Now the world has taken a new turning.", "이제 세상이 새로운 국면으로 접어들었거든."], ["Ah, Alyosha, what a pity you can’t understand ecstasy.", "아, 알료샤, 네가 황홀경을 이해하지 못한다니 참 안타깝구나."], ["But what am I saying to him?", "그런데 내가 너한테 무슨 소리를 하고 있는 거냐?"], ["As though you didn’t understand it.", "마치 네가 그걸 모르기라도 하는 것처럼."], ["What an ass I am!", "내가 얼마나 어리석은지!"], ["What am I saying?", "내가 무슨 소리를 하는 거지?"], ["‘Be noble, O man!’—who says that?”", "'인간이여, 고결하라!'—이건 누가 한 말이더라?\""]], [["Alyosha made up his mind to wait.", "알료샤는 기다리기로 마음먹었다."], ["He felt that, perhaps, indeed, his work lay here.", "어쩌면 정말로 자기가 할 일이 여기 있을지도 모른다고 느꼈다."], ["Mitya sank into thought for a moment, with his elbow on the table and his head in his hand.", "미탸는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 받친 채 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Both were silent.", "둘 다 말이 없었다."]], [["“Alyosha,”", "\"알료샤,\""], ["said Mitya, “you’re the only one who won’t laugh.", "미탸가 말했다. \"비웃지 않을 사람은 너 하나뿐이야."], ["I should like to begin—my confession—with Schiller’s Hymn to Joy, An die Freude!", "내 고백을 실러의 『환희의 송가』, 『안 디 프로이데』로 시작하고 싶구나!"], ["I don’t know German, I only know it’s called that.", "난 독일어는 모른다. 그저 제목이 그렇다는 것만 알 뿐이야."], ["Don’t think I’m talking nonsense because I’m drunk.", "내가 취해서 헛소리를 하는 거라고 생각하지 마라."], ["I’m not a bit drunk.", "난 조금도 취하지 않았다."], ["Brandy’s all very well, but I need two bottles to make me drunk:", "브랜디야 다 좋지만, 날 취하게 하려면 두 병은 있어야 하거든."]], [["Silenus with his rosy phiz Upon his stumbling ass.", "비틀거리는 나귀 위에 올라탄, 발그레한 낯짝의 실레노스."]], [["But I’ve not drunk a quarter of a bottle, and I’m not Silenus.", "하지만 난 사분의 일 병도 마시지 않았으니, 난 실레노스가 아니다."], ["I’m not Silenus, though I am strong,[1] for I’ve made a decision once for all.", "난 실레노스가 아니야, 비록 강한[1] 사람이긴 해도. 한번 결심을 굳혔으니까."], ["Forgive me the pun; you’ll have to forgive me a lot more than puns to-day.", "말장난은 용서해 다오. 오늘은 말장난보다 훨씬 더 많은 걸 용서해 줘야 할 테니까."], ["Don’t be uneasy.", "불안해하지 마라."], ["I’m not spinning it out.", "시간을 질질 끄는 게 아니다."], ["I’m talking sense, and I’ll come to the point in a minute.", "난 말이 되는 소리를 하는 거고, 곧 본론으로 들어가마."], ["I won’t keep you in suspense.", "너를 마냥 애태우게 하진 않을 거야."], ["Stay, how does it go?”", "가만, 그게 어떻게 시작하더라?\""]], [["He raised his head, thought a minute, and began with enthusiasm:", "그는 고개를 들어 잠시 생각하더니 열정에 차서 읊기 시작했다."]], [["“Wild and fearful in his cavern Hid the naked troglodyte, And the homeless nomad wandered Laying waste the fertile plain.", "\"사납고 두려움에 떨며 동굴 속에 벌거벗은 혈거인이 몸을 숨기고, 집 없는 유랑민이 떠돌아다니며 기름진 들판을 황폐하게 만들었네."], ["Menacing with spear and arrow In the woods the hunter strayed....", "창과 화살로 위협하며 숲속을 사냥꾼이 헤매었으니……"], ["Woe to all poor wretches stranded On those cruel and hostile shores!", "저 잔혹하고 적대적인 기슭에 표류한 가엾은 자들에게 화 있을진저!"]], [["“From the peak of high Olympus Came the mother Ceres down, Seeking in those savage regions Her lost daughter Proserpine.", "높은 올림포스 봉우리에서 어머니 케레스가 내려왔으니, 저 야만의 땅을 헤매며 잃어버린 딸 프로세르피네를 찾았네."], ["But the Goddess found no refuge, Found no kindly welcome there, And no temple bearing witness To the worship of the gods.", "허나 여신은 안식처를 찾지 못하고, 따뜻한 환대도 찾지 못했으며, 신들을 향한 경배를 증언하는 신전 하나 없었네."]], [["“From the fields and from the vineyards Came no fruits to deck the feasts, Only flesh of bloodstained victims Smoldered on the altar-fires, And where’er the grieving goddess Turns her melancholy gaze, Sunk in vilest degradation Man his loathsomeness displays.”", "들판에서도, 포도밭에서도 잔치를 꾸밀 과실은 오지 않고, 오직 피로 물든 제물의 살점만이 제단의 불 위에서 타올랐으며, 비탄에 잠긴 여신이 우울한 눈길을 돌리는 곳마다, 더없이 천한 타락에 빠진 인간이 그 추악함을 드러냈네.\""]], [["Mitya broke into sobs and seized Alyosha’s hand.", "미탸는 흐느껴 울기 시작하더니 알료샤의 손을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“My dear, my dear, in degradation, in degradation now, too.", "\"얘야, 얘야, 타락 속에 있다, 지금 이 순간도 타락 속에 있어."], ["There’s a terrible amount of suffering for man on earth, a terrible lot of trouble.", "이 땅에서 인간이 겪어야 할 고통이 어마어마하구나, 견뎌야 할 시련이 산더미야."], ["Don’t think I’m only a brute in an officer’s uniform, wallowing in dirt and drink.", "내가 그저 진창과 술독에 뒹구는 장교복 입은 짐승일 뿐이라고는 생각하지 마라."], ["I hardly think of anything but of that degraded man—if only I’m not lying.", "난 거의 오로지 그 타락한 인간만을 생각한다—내가 거짓말을 하는 게 아니라면 말이야."], ["I pray God I’m not lying and showing off.", "부디 내가 거짓말로 잘난 척하는 게 아니기를 하느님께 빈다."], ["I think about that man because I am that man myself.", "내가 그 인간을 두고 생각하는 건, 내가 바로 그 인간이기 때문이야."]], [["Would he purge his soul from vileness And attain to light and worth, He must turn and cling for ever To his ancient Mother Earth.", "추악함에서 영혼을 씻어 내고 빛과 가치에 이르고자 한다면, 인간은 돌이켜 영원토록 옛 어머니 대지에 매달려야 하리."]], [["But the difficulty is how am I to cling for ever to Mother Earth.", "그런데 문제는, 내가 어떻게 영원토록 어머니 대지에 매달릴 수 있느냐는 거다."], ["I don’t kiss her. I don’t cleave to her bosom.", "난 그 대지에 입 맞추지 못하고, 그 품에 안기지도 못해."], ["Am I to become a peasant or a shepherd?", "내가 농부나 양치기라도 되어야 한단 말이냐?"], ["I go on and I don’t know whether I’m going to shame or to light and joy.", "난 계속 나아가지만, 내가 수치를 향해 가는지 빛과 기쁨을 향해 가는지 알지 못한다."], ["That’s the trouble, for everything in the world is a riddle!", "그게 바로 문제야, 세상 모든 것이 수수께끼니까!"], ["And whenever I’ve happened to sink into the vilest degradation (and it’s always been happening) I always read that poem about Ceres and man.", "그리고 내가 더없이 천한 타락에 빠질 때마다(그건 늘 일어나는 일이었지) 난 언제나 그 케레스와 인간에 관한 시를 읽곤 했다."], ["Has it reformed me?", "그게 날 바로잡아 주었느냐?"], ["Never!", "천만에!"], ["For I’m a Karamazov.", "난 카라마조프니까."], ["For when I do leap into the pit, I go headlong with my heels up, and am pleased to be falling in that degrading attitude, and pride myself upon it.", "난 구덩이에 뛰어들 때도 머리부터 곤두박질치며 발꿈치를 위로 쳐들고, 그렇게 치욕스러운 꼴로 떨어지는 걸 즐기고, 게다가 그걸 자랑스러워하지."], ["And in the very depths of that degradation I begin a hymn of praise.", "그리고 바로 그 타락의 밑바닥에서 찬미의 송가를 부르기 시작한다."], ["Let me be accursed.", "내가 저주받아도 좋다."], ["Let me be vile and base, only let me kiss the hem of the veil in which my God is shrouded.", "내가 천하고 비루해도 좋아, 다만 내 하느님을 감싼 그 베일 자락에 입 맞추게만 해 다오."], ["Though I may be following the devil, I am Thy son, O Lord, and I love Thee, and I feel the joy without which the world cannot stand.", "비록 내가 악마를 따른다 해도, 주여, 저는 당신의 아들이고, 저는 당신을 사랑하며, 그것 없이는 세상이 설 수 없는 그 기쁨을 느낍니다."]], [["Joy everlasting fostereth The soul of all creation, It is her secret ferment fires The cup of life with flame.", "영원한 환희는 길러 내네 만물의 영혼을, 그것은 은밀한 효모가 되어 생명의 잔을 불꽃으로 채우네."], ["’Tis at her beck the grass hath turned Each blade towards the light And solar systems have evolved From chaos and dark night, Filling the realms of boundless space Beyond the sage’s sight.", "그 부름에 풀잎은 저마다 빛을 향해 고개 돌렸고, 태양계들도 진화하여 나왔네 혼돈과 어두운 밤으로부터, 현자의 눈길도 미치지 못하는 가없는 공간의 영역을 채우며."], ["At bounteous Nature’s kindly breast, All things that breathe drink Joy, And birds and beasts and creeping things All follow where She leads.", "너그러운 자연의 자애로운 품에서 숨 쉬는 모든 것이 환희를 마시고, 새도 짐승도 기어 다니는 것들도 모두 그녀가 이끄는 곳을 따르네."], ["Her gifts to man are friends in need, The wreath, the foaming must, To angels—vision of God’s throne, To insects—sensual lust.", "인간에게 베푸는 그녀의 선물은 곤경 속의 벗, 화관과 거품 이는 새 술, 천사에게는—하느님 옥좌의 환영, 벌레에게는—육욕."]], [["But enough poetry!", "하지만 시는 이만하면 됐다!"], ["I am in tears; let me cry.", "난 눈물을 흘리고 있구나."], ["It may be foolishness that every one would laugh at.", "울게 내버려 다오. 다들 비웃을 어리석은 짓일지도 모르지."], ["But you won’t laugh.", "하지만 넌 비웃지 않겠지."], ["Your eyes are shining, too.", "네 눈에도 빛이 어리는구나."], ["Enough poetry.", "시는 이만하면 됐어."], ["I want to tell you now about the insects to whom God gave “sensual lust.”", "이제 너한테 하느님이 '육욕'을 주신 그 벌레들에 관해 이야기하고 싶구나."]], [["To insects—sensual lust.", "벌레에게는—육욕."]], [["I am that insect, brother, and it is said of me specially.", "내가 바로 그 벌레다, 동생아, 그건 특히 나를 두고 하는 말이야."], ["All we Karamazovs are such insects, and, angel as you are, that insect lives in you, too, and will stir up a tempest in your blood.", "우리 카라마조프 집안 사람들은 죄다 그런 벌레라서, 천사인 너한테도 그 벌레가 깃들어 있어, 언젠가 네 핏속에 폭풍을 일으킬 거다."], ["Tempests, because sensual lust is a tempest—worse than a tempest!", "폭풍이라고 한 건, 육욕이란 폭풍이기 때문이야—폭풍보다 더 사나운 것이지!"], ["Beauty is a terrible and awful thing!", "아름다움이란 무섭고 끔찍한 것이다!"], ["It is terrible because it has not been fathomed and never can be fathomed, for God sets us nothing but riddles.", "그것이 무서운 까닭은 헤아릴 수 없는 데다 결코 헤아려질 수도 없기 때문이야, 하느님은 우리에게 수수께끼만 던져 주시니까."], ["Here the boundaries meet and all contradictions exist side by side.", "여기서 경계선들이 맞닿고 온갖 모순이 나란히 공존하지."], ["I am not a cultivated man, brother, but I’ve thought a lot about this.", "난 교양 있는 사람은 아니다, 동생아, 그래도 이것만은 많이 생각해 봤어."], ["It’s terrible what mysteries there are!", "세상에 얼마나 많은 신비가 있는지 무서울 지경이야!"], ["Too many riddles weigh men down on earth.", "너무도 많은 수수께끼가 이 땅의 인간을 짓누른다."], ["We must solve them as we can, and try to keep a dry skin in the water.", "우리는 할 수 있는 데까지 그것들을 풀어 보면서, 물속에서도 살갗을 적시지 않으려 애써야지."], ["Beauty!", "아름다움!"], ["I can’t endure the thought that a man of lofty mind and heart begins with the ideal of the Madonna and ends with the ideal of Sodom.", "난 고결한 정신과 마음을 지닌 인간이 마돈나의 이상에서 시작해 소돔의 이상으로 끝나 버린다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없다."], ["What’s still more awful is that a man with the ideal of Sodom in his soul does not renounce the ideal of the Madonna, and his heart may be on fire with that ideal, genuinely on fire, just as in his days of youth and innocence.", "더 끔찍한 건, 영혼 속에 소돔의 이상을 품은 인간이 마돈나의 이상을 버리지 않는다는 거고, 그의 가슴이 그 이상으로 불타오를 수도 있다는 거야, 진실로 불타오를 수도 있다는 거지, 마치 순결했던 젊은 날과 다름없이."], ["Yes, man is broad, too broad, indeed.", "그래, 인간이란 넓다, 너무도 넓어, 정말이지."], ["I’d have him narrower.", "차라리 더 좁았으면 좋겠다."], ["The devil only knows what to make of it!", "도무지 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 악마나 알겠지!"], ["What to the mind is shameful is beauty and nothing else to the heart.", "머리에는 수치스러운 것이 가슴에는 아름다움 그 자체로 보이거든."], ["Is there beauty in Sodom?", "소돔에 아름다움이 있을까?"], ["Believe me, that for the immense mass of mankind beauty is found in Sodom.", "내 말을 믿어라, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 인간에게 아름다움은 바로 소돔 속에 있단다."], ["Did you know that secret?", "너 그 비밀을 알고 있었냐?"], ["The awful thing is that beauty is mysterious as well as terrible.", "무서운 건, 아름다움이 끔찍할 뿐 아니라 신비롭기까지 하다는 거야. 거기서 하느님과 악마가 싸우고 있고, 그 싸움터가 바로 인간의 마음이지."], ["God and the devil are fighting there and the battlefield is the heart of man.", "하지만 사람이란 늘 제 아픔만을 떠벌리는 법. 자, 이제 사실을 이야기할 차례구나.\""], ["But a man always talks of his own ache. Listen, now to come to facts.”", "[1] 미탸의 본명 드미트리는 그리스어로 '대지의 여신에게 바쳐진 자'를 뜻하나, 여기서는 발음의 유사함을 빗댄 말장난이다."]], [["# Chapter IV. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Anecdote", "# Chapter IV. The Confession Of A Passionate Heart—In Anecdote"]], [["“I was leading a wild life then.", "\"그 무렵 나는 거친 생활을 하고 있었지."], ["Father said just now that I spent several thousand roubles in seducing young girls. That’s a swinish invention, and there was nothing of the sort.", "아버지가 방금 내가 처녀들을 꾀느라 수천 루블을 썼다고 하시던데, 그건 돼지 같은 날조이고, 그런 일은 전혀 없었어."], ["And if there was, I didn’t need money simply for that.", "설령 있었다 한들, 단지 그것을 위해서 돈이 필요했던 건 아니야."], ["With me money is an accessory, the overflow of my heart, the framework.", "나한테 돈이란 부속물이고, 마음의 넘쳐흐름이고, 뼈대 같은 거야."], ["To-day she would be my lady, to-morrow a wench out of the streets in her place.", "오늘은 귀부인이 내 여자였다가, 내일은 그 자리에 길거리의 계집이 들어서기도 했지."], ["I entertained them both.", "나는 그 둘 모두를 대접했어."], ["I threw away money by the handful on music, rioting, and gypsies.", "음악이며 흥청거림이며 집시들한테 돈을 한 줌씩 내던졌지."], ["Sometimes I gave it to the ladies, too, for they’ll take it greedily, that must be admitted, and be pleased and thankful for it.", "더러는 귀부인들한테도 주었어, 그네들도 탐욕스럽게 받았으니까 말이야, 그건 인정해야지, 그러고는 좋아하고 고마워했단 말이야."], ["Ladies used to be fond of me: not all of them, but it happened, it happened.", "귀부인들은 나를 좋아하곤 했어. 다 그런 건 아니지만, 그런 일이 있었어, 있었다니까."], ["But I always liked side-paths, little dark back-alleys behind the main road—there one finds adventures and surprises, and precious metal in the dirt.", "하지만 나는 언제나 샛길이 좋았어, 큰길 뒤편의 어둡고 자그마한 뒷골목 말이야—거기서 모험과 뜻밖의 일을 만나고, 진창 속에서 귀한 금속을 발견하지."], ["I am speaking figuratively, brother.", "비유로 하는 말이야, 동생."], ["In the town I was in, there were no such back-alleys in the literal sense, but morally there were.", "내가 있던 그 고을엔 글자 그대로의 그런 뒷골목은 없었지만, 도덕적으로는 있었어."], ["If you were like me, you’d know what that means.", "네가 나 같은 사람이라면 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알 텐데."], ["I loved vice, I loved the ignominy of vice.", "나는 악덕을 사랑했고, 악덕의 치욕을 사랑했어."], ["I loved cruelty; am I not a bug, am I not a noxious insect?", "나는 잔혹함을 사랑했지. 내가 벌레가 아니냐, 해로운 곤충이 아니냐?"], ["In fact a Karamazov!", "한마디로 카라마조프 아니냐!"], ["Once we went, a whole lot of us, for a picnic, in seven sledges.", "한번은 우리 한 무리가 썰매 일곱 대에 나눠 타고 소풍을 갔지."], ["It was dark, it was winter, and I began squeezing a girl’s hand, and forced her to kiss me.", "어둡고, 겨울이었는데, 나는 한 처녀의 손을 잡고 짓누르기 시작했고, 억지로 입을 맞추게 했어."], ["She was the daughter of an official, a sweet, gentle, submissive creature.", "그 애는 어느 관리의 딸로, 다정하고 온순하고 고분고분한 아이였지."], ["She allowed me, she allowed me much in the dark.", "그 애는 내가 하는 대로 두었어, 어둠 속에서 많은 걸 허락했어."], ["She thought, poor thing, that I should come next day to make her an offer (I was looked upon as a good match, too).", "가엾게도 그 애는 내가 이튿날 청혼하러 올 거라고 생각했지(나도 꽤 괜찮은 혼처로 여겨지고 있었거든)."], ["But I didn’t say a word to her for five months.", "그런데 나는 다섯 달 동안 그 애한테 한마디도 하지 않았어."], ["I used to see her in a corner at dances (we were always having dances), her eyes watching me.", "무도회에서(우리는 늘 무도회를 열었거든) 한구석에 있는 그 애가, 그 눈이 나를 지켜보는 걸 보곤 했지."], ["I saw how they glowed with fire—a fire of gentle indignation.", "그 눈이 불꽃으로 타오르는 걸 봤어—온순한 분노의 불꽃 말이야."], ["This game only tickled that insect lust I cherished in my soul.", "이 놀음은 다만 내 영혼 속에 품고 있던 그 곤충 같은 욕정을 간질였을 뿐이지."], ["Five months later she married an official and left the town, still angry, and still, perhaps, in love with me.", "다섯 달 뒤에 그 애는 어느 관리와 결혼해서 그 고을을 떠났어, 여전히 화가 난 채로, 그러면서도 어쩌면 나를 사랑한 채로 말이야."], ["Now they live happily.", "지금은 행복하게 살고 있어."], ["Observe that I told no one.", "잘 봐 두렴, 나는 아무한테도 말하지 않았어."], ["I didn’t boast of it.", "그걸 자랑하지도 않았어."], ["Though I’m full of low desires, and love what’s low, I’m not dishonorable.", "내가 비천한 욕망으로 가득 차 있고 비천한 걸 사랑한다고 해도, 나는 비열한 인간은 아니야."], ["You’re blushing; your eyes flashed.", "너 얼굴을 붉히는구나, 눈이 번득였어."], ["Enough of this filth with you.", "너한테는 이런 더러운 얘기는 이쯤이면 됐다."], ["And all this was nothing much—wayside blossoms à la Paul de Kock—though the cruel insect had already grown strong in my soul.", "게다가 이런 건 별것도 아니야—폴 드 코크 풍의 길가에 핀 꽃이었지—이미 잔혹한 곤충이 내 영혼 속에서 무럭무럭 자라고 있긴 했지만. 나한테는 완벽한 추억의 앨범이 있어, 동생."], ["I’ve a perfect album of reminiscences, brother.", "그 애들에게 신의 축복이 있기를, 가여운 것들."], ["God bless them, the darlings.", "나는 다투지 않고 관계를 끊으려고 애썼어."], ["I tried to break it off without quarreling.", "그리고 그 애들을 한 번도 팔아넘긴 적이 없어."], ["And I never gave them away. I never bragged of one of them.", "그 애들 중 누구도 자랑거리로 삼은 적이 없지."], ["But that’s enough.", "하지만 이쯤 하자."], ["You can’t suppose I brought you here simply to talk of such nonsense.", "내가 그저 이런 시시한 얘기나 하려고 너를 여기 데려왔다고 생각하진 않겠지."], ["No, I’m going to tell you something more curious; and don’t be surprised that I’m glad to tell you, instead of being ashamed.”", "아니야, 더 묘한 얘기를 들려줄 거야. 그리고 내가 부끄러워하기는커녕 들려주게 되어 기뻐한다고 놀라지 마라.\""]], [["“You say that because I blushed,”", "\"형이 그렇게 말하는 건 내가 얼굴을 붉혔기 때문이지.\""], ["Alyosha said suddenly.", "알료샤가 갑자기 말했다."], ["“I wasn’t blushing at what you were saying or at what you’ve done.", "\"나는 형이 한 말이나 형이 한 짓 때문에 얼굴을 붉힌 게 아니야."], ["I blushed because I am the same as you are.”", "나도 형과 똑같은 인간이라서 붉힌 거야.\""]], [["“You?", "\"네가?"], ["Come, that’s going a little too far!”", "거참, 그건 좀 지나친데!\""]], [["“No, it’s not too far,”", "\"아니, 지나치지 않아.\""], ["said Alyosha warmly (obviously the idea was not a new one).", "알료샤가 열을 띠며 말했다(분명 그것은 새삼스러운 생각이 아니었다)."], ["“The ladder’s the same.", "\"사다리는 똑같아."], ["I’m at the bottom step, and you’re above, somewhere about the thirteenth.", "나는 맨 아래 칸에 있고, 형은 위쪽, 한 열세 번째쯤 어딘가에 있지."], ["That’s how I see it.", "나는 그렇게 봐."], ["But it’s all the same.", "하지만 다 마찬가지야."], ["Absolutely the same in kind.", "종류로 치면 완전히 똑같아."], ["Any one on the bottom step is bound to go up to the top one.”", "맨 아래 칸에 있는 사람은 누구든 결국 맨 꼭대기까지 올라가게 마련이거든.\""]], [["“Then one ought not to step on at all.”", "\"그렇다면 아예 첫 발을 디디지 말아야겠군.\""]], [["“Any one who can help it had better not.”", "\"피할 수 있는 사람이라면 디디지 않는 편이 낫지.\""]], [["“But can you?” “I think not.”", "\"그런데 너는 피할 수 있어?\""]], [["“Hush, Alyosha, hush, darling!", "\"못 할 것 같아.\" \"쉿, 알료샤, 쉿, 사랑스러운 녀석!"], ["I could kiss your hand, you touch me so.", "네 손에 입이라도 맞추고 싶구나, 네가 이렇게 내 마음을 흔들어 놓으니."], ["That rogue Grushenka has an eye for men.", "그 깍쟁이 그루셴카는 남자 보는 눈이 있어."], ["She told me once that she’d devour you one day.", "언젠가 나한테 그러더군, 어느 날인가 너를 잡아먹어 버리겠다고."], ["There, there, I won’t!", "알았어, 알았어, 안 그럴게!"], ["From this field of corruption fouled by flies, let’s pass to my tragedy, also befouled by flies, that is by every sort of vileness.", "파리가 들끓는 이 부패의 들판에서, 이제 나의 비극으로 넘어가자, 역시 파리가 들끓는 비극, 그러니까 온갖 종류의 비열함이 들끓는 비극으로 말이야."], ["Although the old man told lies about my seducing innocence, there really was something of the sort in my tragedy, though it was only once, and then it did not come off.", "영감이 내가 순결을 짓밟았다고 거짓말을 늘어놓긴 했지만, 사실 내 비극 속에 그 비슷한 일이 정말로 있긴 했어, 다만 단 한 번뿐이었고, 그나마도 실행에 옮기진 못했지."], ["The old man who has reproached me with what never happened does not even know of this fact; I never told any one about it.", "일어나지도 않은 일로 나를 책망하는 그 영감은 이 사실조차 몰라. 나는 이 얘기를 아무한테도 한 적이 없으니까."], ["You’re the first, except Ivan, of course—Ivan knows everything.", "네가 처음이야, 물론 이반만 빼고—이반은 다 알지."], ["He knew about it long before you.", "너보다 한참 전부터 알고 있었어."], ["But Ivan’s a tomb.”", "하지만 이반은 무덤이야.\""]], [["“Ivan’s a tomb?”", "\"이반이 무덤이라고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["Alyosha listened with great attention.", "알료샤는 잔뜩 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“I was lieutenant in a line regiment, but still I was under supervision, like a kind of convict.", "\"나는 보병 연대의 중위였지만, 그래도 일종의 죄수처럼 감시를 받고 있었어."], ["Yet I was awfully well received in the little town.", "그래도 그 작은 고을에서는 엄청난 환대를 받았지."], ["I spent money right and left.", "나는 돈을 좌우로 펑펑 썼어."], ["I was thought to be rich; I thought so myself.", "사람들은 나를 부자로 여겼고, 나 자신도 그렇게 여겼어."], ["But I must have pleased them in other ways as well.", "하지만 다른 점에서도 그들의 마음에 들었던 게 분명해."], ["Although they shook their heads over me, they liked me.", "다들 나를 두고 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서도 나를 좋아했거든."], ["My colonel, who was an old man, took a sudden dislike to me.", "노인인 우리 대령은 갑자기 나를 싫어하게 됐어."], ["He was always down upon me, but I had powerful friends, and, moreover, all the town was on my side, so he couldn’t do me much harm.", "늘 나를 못살게 굴었지만, 나한테는 든든한 친구들이 있었고, 게다가 온 고을이 내 편이었으니, 그도 나를 어쩌지 못했지."], ["I was in fault myself for refusing to treat him with proper respect.", "그를 마땅한 예우로 대하기를 거부한 건 내 잘못이었어. 나는 거만했거든."], ["I was proud. This obstinate old fellow, who was really a very good sort, kind-hearted and hospitable, had had two wives, both dead.", "이 고집스러운 영감은 사실 아주 좋은 사람으로, 마음이 따뜻하고 손님을 잘 대접했는데, 두 번 결혼해서 두 아내를 다 잃었어."], ["His first wife, who was of a humble family, left a daughter as unpretentious as herself.", "첫 아내는 미천한 집안 출신이었는데, 자기처럼 꾸밈없는 딸 하나를 남겼지."], ["She was a young woman of four and twenty when I was there, and was living with her father and an aunt, her mother’s sister.", "내가 거기 있을 때 그 처녀는 스물네 살이었고, 아버지와, 어머니의 여동생인 이모와 함께 살고 있었어."], ["The aunt was simple and illiterate; the niece was simple but lively.", "이모는 순박하고 글을 모르는 사람이었고, 조카는 순박하면서도 활달했지."], ["I like to say nice things about people.", "나는 사람들에 대해 좋게 말하는 걸 좋아해."], ["I never knew a woman of more charming character than Agafya—fancy, her name was Agafya Ivanovna!", "아가피야보다 더 매력적인 성품을 지닌 여자는 만나 본 적이 없어—생각해 보렴, 이름이 아가피야 이바노브나였단다!"], ["And she wasn’t bad-looking either, in the Russian style: tall, stout, with a full figure, and beautiful eyes, though a rather coarse face.", "게다가 외모도 나쁘지 않았어, 러시아 식으로 말하자면 키가 크고 통통하고 풍만한 몸매에 아름다운 눈을 가졌지, 얼굴은 좀 투박했지만."], ["She had not married, although she had had two suitors.", "청혼하는 사람이 둘이나 있었는데도 그 애는 결혼하지 않았어."], ["She refused them, but was as cheerful as ever.", "그들을 거절하고도 여전히 명랑했지."], ["I was intimate with her, not in ‘that’ way, it was pure friendship.", "나는 그 애와 친하게 지냈어, '그런' 식이 아니라, 순수한 우정이었지."], ["I have often been friendly with women quite innocently.", "나는 여자들과 아주 순수하게 친구로 지낸 적이 많아."], ["I used to talk to her with shocking frankness, and she only laughed.", "나는 그 애한테 충격적일 만큼 솔직하게 얘기하곤 했는데, 그 애는 그저 웃기만 했어."], ["Many women like such freedom, and she was a girl too, which made it very amusing.", "많은 여자가 그런 격의 없음을 좋아하지, 게다가 그 애는 처녀였으니 그게 아주 재미있었어."], ["Another thing, one could never think of her as a young lady.", "또 한 가지, 그 애를 두고 아가씨라고는 도무지 생각할 수가 없었지."], ["She and her aunt lived in her father’s house with a sort of voluntary humility, not putting themselves on an equality with other people.", "그 애와 이모는 아버지 집에서 일종의 자발적인 겸손으로 살았어, 다른 사람들과 동등한 위치에 서려 하지 않으면서 말이야."], ["She was a general favorite, and of use to every one, for she was a clever dressmaker.", "그 애는 누구에게나 사랑받았고, 누구에게나 쓸모가 있었어, 솜씨 좋은 침모였거든."], ["She had a talent for it.", "그 일에 재주가 있었지."], ["She gave her services freely without asking for payment, but if any one offered her payment, she didn’t refuse.", "보수를 요구하지 않고 기꺼이 일을 해 주었지만, 누가 보수를 내밀면 거절하지도 않았어."], ["The colonel, of course, was a very different matter.", "물론 대령은 사정이 아주 달랐지."], ["He was one of the chief personages in the district.", "그는 그 지방의 주요 인사 중 하나였어."], ["He kept open house, entertained the whole town, gave suppers and dances.", "집 문을 활짝 열어 놓고 온 고을 사람들을 대접하며 만찬과 무도회를 열었지."], ["At the time I arrived and joined the battalion, all the town was talking of the expected return of the colonel’s second daughter, a great beauty, who had just left a fashionable school in the capital.", "내가 도착해서 대대에 합류했을 무렵, 온 고을이 대령의 둘째 딸이 돌아온다는 소식으로 떠들썩했어, 대단한 미인인 그 딸이 막 수도의 어느 명문 학교를 졸업한 참이었거든."], ["This second daughter is Katerina Ivanovna, and she was the child of the second wife, who belonged to a distinguished general’s family; although, as I learnt on good authority, she too brought the colonel no money.", "이 둘째 딸이 바로 카테리나 이바노브나야, 그 애는 둘째 아내의 자식인데, 그 아내는 어느 명망 있는 장군 가문 출신이었지. 그렇긴 해도 내가 믿을 만한 소식통으로부터 알아낸 바로는, 그 아내 역시 대령에게 돈을 한 푼도 가져다주지 못했어."], ["She had connections, and that was all.", "연줄이 있었을 뿐, 그게 다였지."], ["There may have been expectations, but they had come to nothing.", "어쩌면 무슨 유산에 대한 기대가 있었을지도 모르지만, 결국 아무것도 안 됐어."]], [["“Yet, when the young lady came from boarding-school on a visit, the whole town revived.", "\"그래도 그 아가씨가 기숙학교에서 다니러 왔을 때, 온 고을이 되살아났어."], ["Our most distinguished ladies—two ‘Excellencies’ and a colonel’s wife—and all the rest following their lead, at once took her up and gave entertainments in her honor.", "우리 고을에서 가장 지체 높은 부인들—'각하' 부인 두 분과 대령 부인 한 분—이며, 그 뒤를 따르는 나머지 사람들이 모두 단숨에 그 애를 떠받들며 그 애를 위해 연회를 열었지."], ["She was the belle of the balls and picnics, and they got up tableaux vivants in aid of distressed governesses.", "그 애는 무도회와 소풍의 꽃이었고, 사람들은 곤궁한 가정교사들을 돕는답시고 활인화까지 꾸몄어."], ["I took no notice, I went on as wildly as before, and one of my exploits at the time set all the town talking.", "나는 거들떠보지도 않고 여전히 전처럼 거칠게 지냈는데, 그 무렵 내 무용담 하나가 온 고을을 떠들썩하게 했지."], ["I saw her eyes taking my measure one evening at the battery commander’s, but I didn’t go up to her, as though I disdained her acquaintance.", "어느 날 저녁 포병 중대장 집에서 그 애의 눈이 나를 가늠하는 걸 봤지만, 나는 그 애와 알고 지내기를 경멸하기라도 하듯 다가가지 않았어."], ["I did go up and speak to her at an evening party not long after.", "얼마 안 가 어느 저녁 모임에서는 다가가서 말을 걸긴 했지."], ["She scarcely looked at me, and compressed her lips scornfully.", "그 애는 나를 거들떠보지도 않고 비웃듯이 입술을 꼭 다물더군."], ["‘Wait a bit.", "'두고 보자."], ["I’ll have my revenge,’ thought I.", "복수해 주마.' 나는 그렇게 생각했어."], ["I behaved like an awful fool on many occasions at that time, and I was conscious of it myself.", "그 무렵 나는 여러 번 지독한 바보짓을 했고, 나 자신도 그걸 의식하고 있었지."], ["What made it worse was that I felt that ‘Katenka’ was not an innocent boarding-school miss, but a person of character, proud and really high-principled; above all, she had education and intellect, and I had neither.", "더 고약했던 건, '카첸카'가 순진한 기숙학교 아가씨가 아니라 성품을 갖춘 사람, 거만하고 정말로 고결한 원칙을 지닌 사람이라는 걸 느꼈다는 거야. 무엇보다도 그 애는 교양과 지성을 갖추었는데, 나는 그 어느 것도 없었거든."], ["You think I meant to make her an offer?", "너는 내가 청혼하려 했다고 생각하니?"], ["No, I simply wanted to revenge myself, because I was such a hero and she didn’t seem to feel it.", "아니야, 나는 그저 복수하고 싶었을 뿐이야, 나는 이렇게나 대단한 영웅인데 그 애는 그걸 느끼지도 못하는 것 같았으니까."]], [["“Meanwhile, I spent my time in drink and riot, till the lieutenant-colonel put me under arrest for three days.", "\"그러는 사이에 나는 술과 흥청거림으로 세월을 보냈고, 마침내 중령이 나를 사흘간 영창에 가두었지."], ["Just at that time father sent me six thousand roubles in return for my sending him a deed giving up all claims upon him—settling our accounts, so to speak, and saying that I wouldn’t expect anything more.", "바로 그 무렵 아버지가 나한테 육천 루블을 보냈는데, 내가 아버지에게 일체의 청구권을 포기한다는 증서를 보낸 데 대한 답이었어—말하자면 우리 사이의 셈을 매듭짓고, 더는 아무것도 바라지 않겠다는 거였지."], ["I didn’t understand a word of it at the time.", "그때 나는 그 일을 한마디도 이해하지 못했어."], ["Until I came here, Alyosha, till the last few days, indeed, perhaps even now, I haven’t been able to make head or tail of my money affairs with father.", "여기 오기 전까지, 알료샤, 정말로 바로 며칠 전까지, 어쩌면 지금까지도, 나는 아버지와의 돈 문제를 도무지 갈피를 못 잡고 있어."], ["But never mind that, we’ll talk of it later.", "하지만 그건 됐고, 그 얘긴 나중에 하자."]], [["“Just as I received the money, I got a letter from a friend telling me something that interested me immensely.", "\"바로 그 돈을 받았을 때, 한 친구한테서 편지를 받았는데 나한테 굉장히 흥미로운 소식을 알려 주었지."], ["The authorities, I learnt, were dissatisfied with our lieutenant-colonel.", "알고 보니 당국이 우리 중령에게 불만을 품고 있었던 거야."], ["He was suspected of irregularities; in fact, his enemies were preparing a surprise for him.", "그가 부정을 저질렀다는 혐의를 받고 있었고, 사실 그의 적들이 그에게 한 방 먹일 준비를 하고 있었어."], ["And then the commander of the division arrived, and kicked up the devil of a shindy.", "그러더니 사단장이 와서 한바탕 난리법석을 피웠지."], ["Shortly afterwards he was ordered to retire.", "얼마 안 가 그에게 퇴역 명령이 떨어졌어."], ["I won’t tell you how it all happened.", "그 일이 다 어떻게 됐는지는 얘기하지 않으마."], ["He had enemies certainly.", "분명 그에게는 적들이 있었지."], ["Suddenly there was a marked coolness in the town towards him and all his family.", "갑자기 고을 사람들이 그와 그의 온 가족에게 두드러지게 냉담해졌어."], ["His friends all turned their backs on him.", "친구들이 다 그에게 등을 돌렸지."], ["Then I took my first step.", "그때 나는 첫 발을 디뎠어."], ["I met Agafya Ivanovna, with whom I’d always kept up a friendship, and said, ‘Do you know there’s a deficit of 4,500 roubles of government money in your father’s accounts?’", "나는 줄곧 친하게 지내 온 아가피야 이바노브나를 만나서 이렇게 말했어. '당신 아버지 장부에서 국고 돈이 사천오백 루블이나 모자란다는 걸 아시오?'"]], [["“‘What do you mean?", "\"'무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["What makes you say so?", "왜 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요?"], ["The general was here not long ago, and everything was all right.’", "얼마 전에 장군이 다녀가셨는데 다 괜찮았다고요.'"]], [["“‘Then it was, but now it isn’t.’", "\"'그때는 그랬지만, 지금은 그렇지 않소.'"]], [["“She was terribly scared.", "\"그 애는 몹시 겁에 질렸어."]], [["“‘Don’t frighten me!’ she said.", "\"'저를 겁주지 마세요!' 그 애가 말했어."], ["‘Who told you so?’", "'누가 그런 말을 했나요?'"]], [["“‘Don’t be uneasy,’ I said, ‘I won’t tell any one.", "\"'불안해할 것 없소.' 내가 말했지."], ["You know I’m as silent as the tomb.", "'나는 아무한테도 말하지 않을 테니."], ["I only wanted, in view of “possibilities,”", "알다시피 나는 무덤처럼 입이 무거운 사람이오."], ["to add, that when they demand that 4,500 roubles from your father, and he can’t produce it, he’ll be tried, and made to serve as a common soldier in his old age, unless you like to send me your young lady secretly.", "다만 「만일의 경우」를 생각해서 한마디 덧붙이고 싶은데, 그들이 당신 아버지한테 그 사천오백 루블을 내놓으라고 요구하고 그가 그걸 마련하지 못하면, 그는 재판을 받고 늘그막에 일개 졸병으로 복무하게 될 거요, 당신이 그 아가씨를 나한테 몰래 보내 줄 생각이 없다면 말이오."], ["I’ve just had money paid me.", "마침 나한테 돈이 들어왔거든."], ["I’ll give her four thousand, if you like, and keep the secret religiously.’", "당신이 원한다면 그 애한테 사천 루블을 주고, 비밀은 신성하게 지켜 주겠소.'"]], [["“‘Ah, you scoundrel!’—that’s what she said.", "\"'아, 이 악당!'—그 애가 한 말이 그거였어."], ["‘You wicked scoundrel!", "'이 사악한 악당!"], ["How dare you!’", "어찌 감히!'"]], [["“She went away furiously indignant, while I shouted after her once more that the secret should be kept sacred.", "\"그 애는 분노에 사로잡혀 가 버렸고, 나는 그 뒤에다 대고 다시 한번 비밀은 신성하게 지키겠다고 외쳤지."], ["Those two simple creatures, Agafya and her aunt, I may as well say at once, behaved like perfect angels all through this business.", "미리 말해 두는데, 그 두 순박한 사람, 아가피야와 그 이모는 이 일이 진행되는 내내 완벽한 천사처럼 처신했어."], ["They genuinely adored their ‘Katya,’ thought her far above them, and waited on her, hand and foot.", "그네들은 진심으로 자기네 '카탸'를 떠받들었고, 그 애가 자기네보다 한참 위라고 여기며 손발이 되어 시중을 들었지."], ["But Agafya told her of our conversation.", "그런데 아가피야가 우리가 나눈 대화를 그 애한테 말한 거야."], ["I found that out afterwards.", "그건 나중에 알았어."], ["She didn’t keep it back, and of course that was all I wanted.", "그 애가 그걸 숨기지 않았고, 물론 그게 바로 내가 바라던 거였지."]], [["“Suddenly the new major arrived to take command of the battalion.", "\"갑자기 새 소령이 부임해서 대대의 지휘를 맡게 됐어."], ["The old lieutenant-colonel was taken ill at once, couldn’t leave his room for two days, and didn’t hand over the government money.", "늙은 중령은 즉시 병이 나서 이틀 동안 방에서 나오지도 못했고, 국고 돈을 인계하지도 못했지."], ["Dr. Kravchenko declared that he really was ill.", "크라프첸코 의사는 그가 정말로 병이 났다고 단언했어."], ["But I knew for a fact, and had known for a long time, that for the last four years the money had never been in his hands except when the Commander made his visits of inspection.", "하지만 나는 사실을 알고 있었고, 오래전부터 알고 있었지, 지난 사년간 그 돈이 사령관의 시찰 때를 빼고는 한 번도 그의 손에 있어 본 적이 없다는 걸 말이야."], ["He used to lend it to a trustworthy person, a merchant of our town called Trifonov, an old widower, with a big beard and gold-rimmed spectacles.", "그는 그 돈을 믿을 만한 사람한테 빌려주곤 했는데, 우리 고을의 트리포노프라는 상인으로, 늙은 홀아비에 수염이 덥수룩하고 금테 안경을 쓴 자였어."], ["He used to go to the fair, do a profitable business with the money, and return the whole sum to the colonel, bringing with it a present from the fair, as well as interest on the loan.", "그는 시장에 가서 그 돈으로 짭짤한 장사를 하고는 전액을 대령에게 돌려주었지, 시장에서 사 온 선물과 함께 대부에 대한 이자까지 얹어서 말이야."], ["But this time (I heard all about it quite by chance from Trifonov’s son and heir, a driveling youth and one of the most vicious in the world)—this time, I say, Trifonov brought nothing back from the fair.", "그런데 이번에는(나는 이 모든 걸 트리포노프의 아들이자 상속자한테서 순전히 우연히 들었는데, 그놈은 침이나 질질 흘리는 애송이로 세상에서 가장 못된 부류 중 하나였지)—이번에는, 그러니까, 트리포노프가 시장에서 아무것도 가져오지 않았던 거야."], ["The lieutenant-colonel flew to him.", "중령이 그에게 달려갔지."], ["‘I’ve never received any money from you, and couldn’t possibly have received any.’ That was all the answer he got.", "'나는 당신한테서 돈을 받은 적이 없고, 받을 수도 없었소.' 그가 들은 대답이라곤 그게 전부였어."], ["So now our lieutenant-colonel is confined to the house, with a towel round his head, while they’re all three busy putting ice on it.", "그래서 이제 우리 중령은 머리에 수건을 두른 채 집에 틀어박혀 있고, 셋이서 그 위에 얼음을 얹느라 부산을 떨었지."], ["All at once an orderly arrives on the scene with the book and the order to ‘hand over the battalion money immediately, within two hours.’ He signed the book (I saw the signature in the book afterwards), stood up, saying he would put on his uniform, ran to his bedroom, loaded his double-barreled gun with a service bullet, took the boot off his right foot, fixed the gun against his chest, and began feeling for the trigger with his foot.", "그러던 차에 당번병이 장부를 들고 나타나서는 '대대 자금을 즉시, 두 시간 내에 인계하라'는 명령을 전했어. 그는 장부에 서명하고(나중에 그 장부에서 그 서명을 봤어), 일어서서 군복을 입겠다고 하고는 침실로 달려가, 쌍열 총에 실탄을 장전하고, 오른발의 군화를 벗고, 총을 가슴에 대고는, 발로 방아쇠를 더듬기 시작했어."], ["But Agafya, remembering what I had told her, had her suspicions.", "그런데 내가 한 말을 기억하고 있던 아가피야가 의심을 품고 있었지."], ["She stole up and peeped into the room just in time.", "그 애는 몰래 다가가 마침맞게 방 안을 들여다봤어."], ["She rushed in, flung herself upon him from behind, threw her arms round him, and the gun went off, hit the ceiling, but hurt no one.", "그 애는 달려들어 뒤에서 그에게 몸을 던지며 두 팔로 그를 끌어안았고, 총이 발사돼 천장을 맞혔지만 아무도 다치지 않았어."], ["The others ran in, took away the gun, and held him by the arms.", "다른 사람들이 뛰어들어 총을 빼앗고 그의 팔을 붙들었지."], ["I heard all about this afterwards.", "나는 이 모든 걸 나중에 들었어."], ["I was at home, it was getting dusk, and I was just preparing to go out.", "나는 집에 있었는데, 땅거미가 지고 있었고, 나는 막 외출할 채비를 하던 참이었어."], ["I had dressed, brushed my hair, scented my handkerchief, and taken up my cap, when suddenly the door opened, and facing me in the room stood Katerina Ivanovna.", "옷을 차려입고, 머리를 빗고, 손수건에 향수를 뿌리고, 모자를 집어 들었는데, 갑자기 문이 열리더니, 방 안에서 카테리나 이바노브나가 나를 마주 보고 서 있는 거야."]], [["“It’s strange how things happen sometimes.", "\"이따금 일이 어떻게 벌어지는지 참 묘하단 말이야."], ["No one had seen her in the street, so that no one knew of it in the town.", "거리에서 아무도 그 애를 본 사람이 없어서, 고을에서 아무도 그 일을 몰랐어."], ["I lodged with two decrepit old ladies, who looked after me.", "나는 나를 돌봐 주는 늙고 쇠약한 두 노파와 함께 묵고 있었는데."], ["They were most obliging old things, ready to do anything for me, and at my request were as silent afterwards as two cast-iron posts.", "그네들은 무엇이든 들어주는 더없이 친절한 늙은이들이라 나를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 되어 있었고, 내 부탁에 나중에는 두 개의 무쇠 기둥처럼 입을 다물었지."], ["Of course I grasped the position at once.", "물론 나는 사정을 단번에 파악했어."], ["She walked in and looked straight at me, her dark eyes determined, even defiant, but on her lips and round her mouth I saw uncertainty.", "그 애는 걸어 들어와 나를 똑바로 바라봤는데, 그 검은 눈은 단호하다 못해 도전적이기까지 했지만, 입술에는, 입가에는 망설임이 보였어."]], [["“‘My sister told me,’ she began, ‘that you would give me 4,500 roubles if I came to you for it—myself.", "\"'언니가 그러더군요.' 그 애가 입을 열었어."], ["I have come ...", "'제가 직접 받으러 오면 사천오백 루블을 주시겠다고요."], ["give me the money!’", "그래서 왔어요…… 돈을 주세요!'"]], [["“She couldn’t keep it up.", "\"그 애는 그걸 버티지 못했어."], ["She was breathless, frightened, her voice failed her, and the corners of her mouth and the lines round it quivered.", "숨이 가쁘고, 겁에 질려, 목소리가 나오지 않았고, 입꼬리와 그 둘레의 주름이 떨렸지."], ["Alyosha, are you listening, or are you asleep?”", "알료샤, 듣고 있니, 아니면 잠든 거니?\""]], [["“Mitya, I know you will tell the whole truth,”", "\"미탸, 형이 모든 진실을 다 말하리라는 걸 알아.\""], ["said Alyosha in agitation.", "알료샤가 흥분해서 말했다."]], [["“I am telling it.", "\"말하고 있어."], ["If I tell the whole truth just as it happened I shan’t spare myself.", "일어난 그대로 모든 진실을 다 말한다면, 나 자신도 봐주지 않을 거야."], ["My first idea was a—Karamazov one.", "내 첫 생각은—카라마조프다운 거였어."], ["Once I was bitten by a centipede, brother, and laid up a fortnight with fever from it.", "한번은 지네한테 물려서, 동생, 그 열병으로 보름이나 앓아누운 적이 있었지."], ["Well, I felt a centipede biting at my heart then—a noxious insect, you understand?", "그런데 그때 지네 한 마리가 내 심장을 물어뜯는 게 느껴졌어—해로운 곤충 말이야, 알겠니?"], ["I looked her up and down.", "나는 그 애를 위아래로 훑어봤어."], ["You’ve seen her?", "너 그 애를 봤지?"], ["She’s a beauty.", "미인이야."], ["But she was beautiful in another way then.", "하지만 그때는 다른 식으로 아름다웠어."], ["At that moment she was beautiful because she was noble, and I was a scoundrel; she in all the grandeur of her generosity and sacrifice for her father, and I—a bug!", "그 순간 그 애가 아름다웠던 건 그 애가 고결했기 때문이고, 나는 악당이었기 때문이지. 그 애는 아버지를 위한 너그러움과 희생의 온갖 위엄을 두르고 있었고, 나는—한 마리 벌레였어!"], ["And, scoundrel as I was, she was altogether at my mercy, body and soul.", "그리고 악당이긴 했지만 그 애는 몸도 마음도 완전히 내 손안에 있었어."], ["She was hemmed in.", "그 애는 옴짝달싹할 수 없었지."], ["I tell you frankly, that thought, that venomous thought, so possessed my heart that it almost swooned with suspense.", "솔직히 말하는데, 그 생각이, 그 독기 어린 생각이 내 마음을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 긴장으로 거의 까무러칠 지경이었어."], ["It seemed as if there could be no resisting it; as though I should act like a bug, like a venomous spider, without a spark of pity.", "도무지 거역할 수 없을 것만 같았어. 마치 내가 벌레처럼, 독거미처럼, 한 점의 연민도 없이 굴어야 할 것만 같았지."], ["I could scarcely breathe.", "나는 숨도 제대로 쉴 수 없었어."], ["Understand, I should have gone next day to ask for her hand, so that it might end honorably, so to speak, and that nobody would or could know.", "알아 두렴, 나는 이튿날 그 애에게 청혼하러 가서, 말하자면 일이 명예롭게 끝나도록, 아무도 모르거나 알 수 없도록 했어야 마땅했어."], ["For though I’m a man of base desires, I’m honest.", "비록 내가 비천한 욕망을 지닌 인간이긴 해도, 나는 정직하거든."], ["And at that very second some voice seemed to whisper in my ear, ‘But when you come to-morrow to make your proposal, that girl won’t even see you; she’ll order her coachman to kick you out of the yard.", "그런데 바로 그 순간 어떤 목소리가 내 귓가에 속삭이는 것 같았어. '하지만 네가 내일 청혼하러 가면, 저 처녀는 너를 쳐다보지도 않을걸."], ["“Publish it through all the town,”", "마부를 시켜 너를 마당에서 걷어차 내쫓으라고 하겠지."], ["she would say, “I’m not afraid of you.”’ I looked at the young lady, my voice had not deceived me.", "「온 고을에 떠벌려라」고 그 애는 말할 거야, 「나는 네가 두렵지 않아」.' 나는 그 아가씨를 바라봤어, 그 목소리가 나를 속인 게 아니었지."], ["That is how it would be, not a doubt of it.", "틀림없이 그렇게 됐을 거야, 의심할 여지가 없어."], ["I could see from her face now that I should be turned out of the house.", "이제 그 애의 얼굴에서 내가 집 밖으로 쫓겨나리라는 걸 알 수 있었어."], ["My spite was roused.", "내 악의가 끓어올랐지."], ["I longed to play her the nastiest swinish cad’s trick: to look at her with a sneer, and on the spot where she stood before me to stun her with a tone of voice that only a shopman could use.", "나는 그 애한테 가장 야비한 돼지 같은 상놈의 수작을 부리고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었어. 비웃음을 띤 채 그 애를 바라보며, 내 앞에 서 있는 그 자리에서, 장사치나 쓸 법한 말투로 그 애를 멍하게 만들고 싶었던 거야."]], [["“‘Four thousand!", "\"'사천 루블이라니!"], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 말씀이오?"], ["I was joking.", "농담이었소."], ["You’ve been counting your chickens too easily, madam.", "셈을 너무 쉽게 헤아렸구려, 아가씨."], ["Two hundred, if you like, with all my heart.", "이백 루블이라면, 원한다면야 기꺼이 드리리다."], ["But four thousand is not a sum to throw away on such frivolity.", "하지만 사천 루블은 그런 변덕에 내던질 액수가 아니오."], ["You’ve put yourself out to no purpose.’", "헛수고를 하셨군요.'"]], [["“I should have lost the game, of course.", "\"물론 나는 그 놀음에서 졌을 거야."], ["She’d have run away.", "그 애는 달아나 버렸겠지."], ["But it would have been an infernal revenge.", "하지만 그건 지독한 복수였을 거야."], ["It would have been worth it all.", "그럴 만한 값어치가 있었을 거고."], ["I’d have howled with regret all the rest of my life, only to have played that trick.", "나는 그 수작을 부린 것만으로도 남은 평생을 후회로 울부짖었겠지."], ["Would you believe it, it has never happened to me with any other woman, not one, to look at her at such a moment with hatred.", "믿어지니, 그 어떤 다른 여자한테도, 단 한 명한테도, 그런 순간에 증오를 품고 바라본 적은 한 번도 없었어."], ["But, on my oath, I looked at her for three seconds, or five perhaps, with fearful hatred—that hate which is only a hair’s-breadth from love, from the maddest love!", "하지만 맹세코, 나는 삼 초, 어쩌면 오 초쯤 끔찍한 증오를 품고 그 애를 바라봤어—사랑과는, 가장 미쳐 버린 사랑과는 머리카락 한 올 차이밖에 안 되는 그 증오 말이야!"]], [["“I went to the window, put my forehead against the frozen pane, and I remember the ice burnt my forehead like fire.", "\"나는 창가로 가서 얼어붙은 유리창에 이마를 댔는데, 지금도 기억하지, 그 얼음이 불처럼 내 이마를 태우던 걸."], ["I did not keep her long, don’t be afraid.", "그 애를 오래 붙들어 두진 않았어, 걱정 마."], ["I turned round, went up to the table, opened the drawer and took out a banknote for five thousand roubles (it was lying in a French dictionary).", "나는 몸을 돌려 탁자로 가서, 서랍을 열고 오천 루블짜리 지폐를 한 장 꺼냈어(프랑스어 사전 속에 끼워져 있었지)."], ["Then I showed it her in silence, folded it, handed it to her, opened the door into the passage, and, stepping back, made her a deep bow, a most respectful, a most impressive bow, believe me!", "그러고는 말없이 그걸 그 애한테 보여 주고, 접어서, 손에 쥐여 주고, 복도로 통하는 문을 열고는, 뒤로 물러서며 그 애한테 깊숙이 고개를 숙였어, 더없이 공손하고, 더없이 인상적인 절을, 정말이야!"], ["She shuddered all over, gazed at me for a second, turned horribly pale—white as a sheet, in fact—and all at once, not impetuously but softly, gently, bowed down to my feet—not a boarding-school curtsey, but a Russian bow, with her forehead to the floor.", "그 애는 온몸을 부르르 떨더니, 잠시 나를 응시하다가, 무섭게 창백해져서—사실 시트처럼 새하얘져서—그러더니 갑자기, 격렬하게가 아니라 부드럽게, 다정하게, 내 발치에 머리를 조아렸어—기숙학교식 인사가 아니라, 이마를 바닥에 대는 러시아식 절을 말이야."], ["She jumped up and ran away.", "그 애는 벌떡 일어나 달아나 버렸어."], ["I was wearing my sword.", "나는 칼을 차고 있었지."], ["I drew it and nearly stabbed myself with it on the spot; why, I don’t know.", "나는 그 칼을 뽑아 그 자리에서 하마터면 내 몸을 찌를 뻔했어. 왜인지는 나도 몰라."], ["It would have been frightfully stupid, of course.", "물론 그건 끔찍하게 어리석은 짓이었겠지."], ["I suppose it was from delight.", "아마 기쁨에서였던 것 같아."], ["Can you understand that one might kill oneself from delight?", "사람이 기쁨에서 스스로 목숨을 끊을 수도 있다는 걸 이해할 수 있겠니?"], ["But I didn’t stab myself.", "하지만 나는 내 몸을 찌르진 않았어."], ["I only kissed my sword and put it back in the scabbard—which there was no need to have told you, by the way.", "그저 칼에 입을 맞추고 칼집에 도로 꽂았을 뿐이지—하긴 그건 너한테 굳이 말할 필요가 없는 일이지만."], ["And I fancy that in telling you about my inner conflict I have laid it on rather thick to glorify myself.", "그리고 내 내면의 갈등을 너한테 얘기하면서 나 자신을 미화하려고 좀 과장해서 칠한 것 같기도 해."], ["But let it pass, and to hell with all who pry into the human heart!", "하지만 그냥 넘어가자, 인간의 마음을 캐고 드는 자들은 다 지옥에나 가라지!"], ["Well, so much for that ‘adventure’ with Katerina Ivanovna.", "자, 카테리나 이바노브나와의 그 '모험'은 이쯤 해 두자."], ["So now Ivan knows of it, and you—no one else.”", "그러니까 이제 이반이 그 일을 알고, 너도—그 밖엔 아무도 몰라.\""]], [["Dmitri got up, took a step or two in his excitement, pulled out his handkerchief and mopped his forehead, then sat down again, not in the same place as before, but on the opposite side, so that Alyosha had to turn quite round to face him.", "드미트리는 일어나서, 흥분한 채 한두 걸음 떼고는, 손수건을 꺼내 이마를 닦더니, 다시 앉았는데, 아까와 같은 자리가 아니라 맞은편에 앉아서, 알료샤가 그를 마주 보려면 완전히 돌아앉아야 했다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said Alyosha, “I understand the first half.”", "알료샤가 말했다. \"전반부는 알겠어요.\""]], [["“You understand the first half.", "\"전반부는 알겠다고."], ["That half is a drama, and it was played out there.", "그 절반은 한 편의 드라마였고, 거기 그곳에서 다 끝나 버렸지."], ["The second half is a tragedy, and it is being acted here.”", "후반부는 비극인데, 그건 지금 여기서 벌어지고 있는 거다.\""]], [["“And I understand nothing of that second half so far,”", "\"그런데 그 후반부는 아직 하나도 모르겠어요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“And I?", "\"그럼 나는?"], ["Do you suppose I understand it?”", "내가 그걸 안다고 생각하느냐?\""]], [["“Stop, Dmitri.", "\"잠깐만요, 드미트리 형."], ["There’s one important question.", "한 가지 중요한 질문이 있어요."], ["Tell me, you were betrothed, you are betrothed still?”", "말해 봐요, 형은 약혼한 사이였고, 지금도 약혼한 사이인가요?\""]], [["“We weren’t betrothed at once, not for three months after that adventure.", "\"우리가 곧바로 약혼한 건 아니었다. 그 사건이 있고 나서 석 달이 지나서였지."], ["The next day I told myself that the incident was closed, concluded, that there would be no sequel.", "그 이튿날 나는 그 일이 다 끝나 버렸다고, 마무리됐다고, 뒷이야기는 없을 거라고 스스로에게 말했어."], ["It seemed to me caddish to make her an offer.", "그녀에게 청혼하는 건 비열한 짓 같았거든."], ["On her side she gave no sign of life for the six weeks that she remained in the town; except, indeed, for one action.", "그녀 쪽에서도 읍내에 머문 여섯 주 동안 아무런 기척이 없었다. 단 한 가지 일만 빼면 말이지."], ["The day after her visit the maid-servant slipped round with an envelope addressed to me.", "그녀가 다녀간 다음 날, 하녀가 슬그머니 내게 봉투 하나를 갖다주더구나."], ["I tore it open: it contained the change out of the banknote.", "뜯어 보니, 그 안에는 지폐를 헐어 거슬러 준 돈이 들어 있었다."], ["Only four thousand five hundred roubles was needed, but there was a discount of about two hundred on changing it.", "필요한 건 사천오백 루블뿐이었지만, 환전에 이백 루블쯤 수수료가 붙었지. 그녀가 보낸 건 이백육십 루블쯤이었다."], ["She only sent me about two hundred and sixty.", "정확하진 않지만, 편지 한 장도, 설명 한마디도 없었어."], ["I don’t remember exactly, but not a note, not a word of explanation.", "연필 자국이라도 있나 하고 봉투를 샅샅이 뒤졌지만, 아-무것도 없더구나!"], ["I searched the packet for a pencil mark—n-nothing! Well, I spent the rest of the money on such an orgy that the new major was obliged to reprimand me.", "뭐, 그래서 나는 남은 돈을 어찌나 흥청망청 술판에 다 써 버렸던지, 새로 온 소령이 나를 호되게 나무라지 않을 수 없었지."]], [["“Well, the lieutenant-colonel produced the battalion money, to the astonishment of every one, for nobody believed that he had the money untouched.", "자, 그런데 그 중령이 대대 공금을 내놓더란 말이다. 모두가 깜짝 놀랐지."], ["He’d no sooner paid it than he fell ill, took to his bed, and, three weeks later, softening of the brain set in, and he died five days afterwards.", "그가 그 돈에 손도 안 댔으리라고는 아무도 믿지 않았으니까. 그런데 그 돈을 갚자마자 그가 병으로 쓰러져 자리에 눕더니, 석 주 뒤에 뇌가 물러지는 병이 와서 닷새 만에 죽고 말았다."], ["He was buried with military honors, for he had not had time to receive his discharge.", "군장(軍葬)으로 묻혔지. 전역 처리를 받을 새도 없었으니까."], ["Ten days after his funeral, Katerina Ivanovna, with her aunt and sister, went to Moscow.", "장례가 끝나고 열흘 뒤에 카테리나 이바노브나는 고모와 언니를 데리고 모스크바로 갔어. 그런데 어떻게 됐는지 아느냐, 바로 그들이 떠나던 그날(나는 그녀를 보지도 못했고, 배웅도 작별 인사도 못 했다) 나는 자그마한 쪽지 하나를 받았다."], ["And, behold, on the very day they went away (I hadn’t seen them, didn’t see them off or take leave) I received a tiny note, a sheet of thin blue paper, and on it only one line in pencil: ‘I will write to you.", "얇은 푸른 종이 한 장에 연필로 단 한 줄만 적혀 있더구나. '편지 드리겠어요."], ["Wait.", "기다리세요."], ["K.’ And that was all.", "K.' 그게 다였다."]], [["“I’ll explain the rest now, in two words.", "이제 나머지는 두어 마디로 설명하마."], ["In Moscow their fortunes changed with the swiftness of lightning and the unexpectedness of an Arabian fairy-tale.", "모스크바에서 그들의 운명은 번개 같은 속도로, 아라비아 동화 같은 뜻밖의 방식으로 뒤바뀌었다."], ["That general’s widow, their nearest relation, suddenly lost the two nieces who were her heiresses and next-of-kin—both died in the same week of small-pox.", "그들의 가장 가까운 친척인 그 장군 부인이 갑자기 상속녀이자 가장 가까운 핏줄인 두 조카딸을 잃었거든. 둘 다 같은 주에 천연두로 죽었지."], ["The old lady, prostrated with grief, welcomed Katya as a daughter, as her one hope, clutched at her, altered her will in Katya’s favor.", "슬픔에 짓눌린 노부인은 카탸를 딸처럼, 자기 유일한 희망처럼 맞아들여 그녀에게 매달리고는, 유언장을 카탸에게 유리하게 고쳤어."], ["But that concerned the future.", "하지만 그건 장래의 일이었지."], ["Meanwhile she gave her, for present use, eighty thousand roubles, as a marriage portion, to do what she liked with.", "그러는 한편 당장 쓰라고 결혼 지참금으로 팔만 루블을 주어 마음대로 하게 했다."], ["She was an hysterical woman.", "그 노부인은 신경질적인 여자였어."], ["I saw something of her in Moscow, later.", "나중에 모스크바에서 그녀를 좀 봤지."]], [["“Well, suddenly I received by post four thousand five hundred roubles.", "자, 그런데 갑자기 나는 우편으로 사천오백 루블을 받았다."], ["I was speechless with surprise, as you may suppose.", "너도 짐작하겠지만, 너무 놀라 말문이 막혔지. 사흘 뒤에 약속했던 편지가 왔어."], ["Three days later came the promised letter.", "지금도 내 품에 지니고 있다. 너도 읽어 봐야 해."], ["I have it with me now. You must read it.", "그녀는 내 아내가 되겠다고, 자신을 내게 바치겠다고 했어."], ["She offers to be my wife, offers herself to me.", "'미치도록 당신을 사랑합니다.' 그녀는 이렇게 썼지."], ["‘I love you madly,’ she says, ‘even if you don’t love me, never mind.", "'당신이 나를 사랑하지 않아도 상관없어요. 내 남편이 되어 주세요."], ["Be my husband.", "두려워 마세요."], ["Don’t be afraid. I won’t hamper you in any way.", "무슨 일로든 당신을 얽매지 않겠어요."], ["I will be your chattel.", "당신의 소유물이 되겠어요."], ["I will be the carpet under your feet.", "당신 발밑의 양탄자가 되겠어요."], ["I want to love you for ever.", "영원히 당신을 사랑하고 싶어요."], ["I want to save you from yourself.’ Alyosha, I am not worthy to repeat those lines in my vulgar words and in my vulgar tone, my everlastingly vulgar tone, that I can never cure myself of.", "당신을 당신 자신에게서 구해 내고 싶어요.' 알료샤, 나는 이 천박한 말투로, 이 천박한 어조로, 평생 고칠 수 없는 이 영원히 천박한 어조로 그 구절을 되읊을 자격이 없는 놈이다."], ["That letter stabs me even now.", "그 편지는 지금도 나를 찌른다."], ["Do you think I don’t mind—that I don’t mind still?", "내가 아무렇지 않을 것 같으냐? 지금도 아무렇지 않을 것 같아?"], ["I wrote her an answer at once, as it was impossible for me to go to Moscow.", "나는 곧장 답장을 썼다. 모스크바로 갈 수가 없었으니까."], ["I wrote to her with tears.", "눈물을 흘리며 그녀에게 썼지."], ["One thing I shall be ashamed of for ever.", "한 가지만은 영원히 부끄러워할 일이 있어."], ["I referred to her being rich and having a dowry while I was only a stuck-up beggar!", "나는 그녀가 부유하고 지참금이 있는데 나는 거만 떠는 거지에 불과하다는 얘길 꺼냈다!"], ["I mentioned money!", "돈 얘길 입에 올린 거야!"], ["I ought to have borne it in silence, but it slipped from my pen.", "말없이 견뎠어야 했는데, 펜에서 그만 새어 나오고 말았어."], ["Then I wrote at once to Ivan, and told him all I could about it in a letter of six pages, and sent him to her.", "그러고는 곧장 이반에게 편지를 써서, 여섯 장에 걸쳐 그 일에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말을 다 적어, 그를 그녀에게 보냈다."], ["Why do you look like that?", "왜 그런 얼굴을 하느냐?"], ["Why are you staring at me?", "왜 나를 빤히 쳐다봐?"], ["Yes, Ivan fell in love with her; he’s in love with her still.", "그래, 이반은 그녀를 사랑하게 됐다. 지금도 그녀를 사랑하고 있어."], ["I know that.", "나도 안다."], ["I did a stupid thing, in the world’s opinion; but perhaps that one stupid thing may be the saving of us all now.", "세상 사람들 눈으로 보면 나는 어리석은 짓을 한 거지. 하지만 어쩌면 그 어리석은 짓 하나가 지금 우리 모두를 구원할지도 몰라."], ["Oo!", "오!"], ["Don’t you see what a lot she thinks of Ivan, how she respects him?", "그녀가 이반을 얼마나 높이 평가하는지, 얼마나 존경하는지 모르겠느냐?"], ["When she compares us, do you suppose she can love a man like me, especially after all that has happened here?”", "그녀가 우리 둘을 견주어 보면, 나 같은 사내를, 더구나 여기서 일어난 그 모든 일을 겪은 뒤에 나 같은 사내를 사랑할 수 있을 것 같으냐?\""]], [["“But I am convinced that she does love a man like you, and not a man like him.”", "\"하지만 나는 그녀가 형 같은 사람을 사랑하는 거지, 이반 형 같은 사람을 사랑하는 게 아니라고 확신해요.\""]], [["“She loves her own virtue, not me.”", "\"그녀는 자기 자신의 미덕을 사랑하는 거지, 나를 사랑하는 게 아니다.\""], ["The words broke involuntarily, and almost malignantly, from Dmitri.", "그 말이 드미트리의 입에서 저도 모르게, 거의 독기를 품은 채 튀어나왔다."], ["He laughed, but a minute later his eyes gleamed, he flushed crimson and struck the table violently with his fist.", "그는 웃었지만, 잠시 후 두 눈이 번득이더니 시뻘겋게 달아올라 주먹으로 탁자를 사납게 내리쳤다."]], [["“I swear, Alyosha,”", "\"맹세하건대, 알료샤,\""], ["he cried, with intense and genuine anger at himself; “you may not believe me, but as God is holy, and as Christ is God, I swear that though I smiled at her lofty sentiments just now, I know that I am a million times baser in soul than she, and that these lofty sentiments of hers are as sincere as a heavenly angel’s.", "그는 자기 자신을 향한 격렬하고도 진실한 분노로 부르짖었다. \"넌 나를 안 믿을지 몰라도, 하느님이 거룩하시고 그리스도가 하느님이심을 두고 맹세하건대, 방금 내가 그녀의 고결한 감정을 비웃기는 했다만, 나는 영혼으로 따지면 그녀보다 백만 배는 더 비천한 놈이고, 그녀의 그 고결한 감정은 천상의 천사처럼 진실하다는 걸 안다."], ["That’s the tragedy of it—that I know that for certain.", "그게 바로 이 일의 비극이야. 내가 그걸 확실히 안다는 게 말이다."], ["What if any one does show off a bit?", "누군가 좀 잘난 척한다고 한들 어떠냐?"], ["Don’t I do it myself?", "나도 그러지 않느냐?"], ["And yet I’m sincere, I’m sincere.", "그래도 나는 진심이다, 진심이야."], ["As for Ivan, I can understand how he must be cursing nature now—with his intellect, too!", "이반으로 말하면, 그가 지금 자연을 얼마나 저주하고 있을지 나는 이해할 수 있어. 그만한 지성을 지니고서도 말이지!"], ["To see the preference given—to whom, to what?", "누구에게, 무엇에게 그녀가 마음을 더 주는 꼴을 봐야 하느냐?"], ["To a monster who, though he is betrothed and all eyes are fixed on him, can’t restrain his debaucheries—and before the very eyes of his betrothed!", "약혼한 몸이고 모두의 시선이 쏠려 있는데도 방탕을 절제하지 못하는 괴물에게, 그것도 제 약혼녀가 빤히 보는 앞에서 그러는 괴물에게!"], ["And a man like me is preferred, while he is rejected.", "그런데 나 같은 사내가 더 사랑받고, 그는 거부당하는 거야."], ["And why?", "그게 왜냐고?"], ["Because a girl wants to sacrifice her life and destiny out of gratitude.", "한 처녀가 감사하는 마음에서 제 인생과 운명을 희생하려 들기 때문이지."], ["It’s ridiculous!", "우스꽝스럽지 않으냐!"], ["I’ve never said a word of this to Ivan, and Ivan of course has never dropped a hint of the sort to me.", "나는 이반에게 이런 얘길 한마디도 한 적이 없고, 이반도 물론 내게 그런 기색을 비친 적이 없다. 하지만 운명은 이루어질 테고, 더 나은 사내는 제자리를 지킬 것이며, 자격 없는 놈은 제 뒷골목으로 영영 사라져 버릴 거다."], ["But destiny will be accomplished, and the best man will hold his ground while the undeserving one will vanish into his back-alley for ever—his filthy back-alley, his beloved back-alley, where he is at home and where he will sink in filth and stench at his own free will and with enjoyment.", "그 더러운 뒷골목으로, 그 사랑하는 뒷골목으로 말이지. 거기가 바로 제 집이고, 거기서 그놈은 제 자유 의지로, 그것도 즐기면서 오물과 악취 속에 잠겨 버릴 거야."], ["I’ve been talking foolishly.", "내가 어리석은 소리를 지껄였구나."], ["I’ve no words left.", "이제 할 말도 없다."], ["I use them at random, but it will be as I have said.", "닥치는 대로 말을 내뱉지만, 결국 내가 말한 대로 될 거다."], ["I shall drown in the back-alley, and she will marry Ivan.”", "나는 뒷골목에 빠져 죽을 테고, 그녀는 이반과 결혼하겠지.\""]], [["“Stop, Dmitri,”", "\"잠깐만요, 드미트리 형.\""], ["Alyosha interrupted again with great anxiety.", "알료샤가 다시 몹시 불안해하며 말을 가로막았다."], ["“There’s one thing you haven’t made clear yet: you are still betrothed all the same, aren’t you?", "\"아직 분명히 안 밝힌 게 하나 있어요. 형은 그래도 여전히 약혼한 사이잖아요, 그렇죠?"], ["How can you break off the engagement if she, your betrothed, doesn’t want to?”", "형의 약혼녀가, 그녀가 원하지 않는다면, 어떻게 형이 약혼을 깰 수 있어요?\""]], [["“Yes, formally and solemnly betrothed.", "\"그래, 격식을 갖춰 엄숙하게 약혼했지. 내가 모스크바에 도착하자마자 다 치러졌다."], ["It was all done on my arrival in Moscow, with great ceremony, with ikons, all in fine style.", "성대한 의식에, 성상(聖像)까지 갖추고, 죄다 격식대로 했어. 장군 부인이 우리를 축복하고는, 너 믿겠느냐?"], ["The general’s wife blessed us, and—would you believe it?—congratulated Katya.", "카탸를 축하해 줬다. '잘 골랐다.'"], ["‘You’ve made a good choice,’ she said, ‘I see right through him.’ And—would you believe it?—she didn’t like Ivan, and hardly greeted him.", "부인이 그러더구나. '내가 저 사람 속을 훤히 들여다본다.'"], ["I had a lot of talk with Katya in Moscow.", "그리고 또, 너 믿겠느냐? 부인은 이반은 마음에 안 들어 해서 인사도 거의 안 했어."], ["I told her about myself—sincerely, honorably.", "나는 모스크바에서 카탸와 얘길 많이 나눴다. 나 자신에 대해 그녀에게 털어놓았지."], ["She listened to everything.", "진실하게, 정직하게. 그녀는 다 들어 주었어."]], [["There was sweet confusion, There were tender words.", "달콤한 혼란이 있었고, 다정한 말들이 있었네."]], [["Though there were proud words, too.", "자랑스러운 말들도 있긴 했다만."], ["She wrung out of me a mighty promise to reform.", "그녀는 내게서 새사람이 되겠다는 굳은 약속을 짜냈다."], ["I gave my promise, and here—”", "나는 약속했지, 그리고 여기—\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""]], [["“Why, I called to you and brought you out here to-day, this very day—remember it—to send you—this very day again—to Katerina Ivanovna, and—”", "\"왜, 그래서 내가 오늘, 바로 오늘 너를 불러 여기로 데려온 거다. 기억해 둬라, 너를 보내려고 말이지. 바로 오늘 또다시, 카테리나 이바노브나에게로, 그리고—\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""]], [["“To tell her that I shall never come to see her again.", "\"그녀에게 내가 다시는 그녀를 보러 가지 않겠다고 전하라는 거다."], ["Say, ‘He sends you his compliments.’”", "'그가 안부를 전한다더라'고 말해 줘.\""]], [["“But is that possible?”", "\"하지만 그게 가능한 일이에요?\""]], [["“That’s just the reason I’m sending you, in my place, because it’s impossible.", "\"바로 그게 불가능한 일이니까 너를, 나 대신 보내는 거다."], ["And, how could I tell her myself?”", "게다가 내가 어떻게 그 말을 직접 그녀에게 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“And where are you going?”", "\"그럼 형은 어디로 갈 건데요?\""]], [["“To the back-alley.”", "\"뒷골목으로.\""]], [["“To Grushenka, then!”", "\"그럼 그루셴카에게로!\""], ["Alyosha exclaimed mournfully, clasping his hands.", "알료샤가 두 손을 맞잡으며 비통하게 외쳤다."], ["“Can Rakitin really have told the truth?", "\"라키틴이 정말 사실을 말한 거였군요?"], ["I thought that you had just visited her, and that was all.”", "나는 형이 그저 그녀를 한 번 찾아갔을 뿐, 그게 다인 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["“Can a betrothed man pay such visits?", "\"약혼한 사내가 그런 식으로 찾아다닐 수 있느냔 말이다."], ["Is such a thing possible and with such a betrothed, and before the eyes of all the world?", "그게 가당키나 한 일이냐, 그것도 그런 약혼녀를 두고, 온 세상의 눈앞에서?"], ["Confound it, I have some honor!", "빌어먹을, 나도 명예란 게 있어!"], ["As soon as I began visiting Grushenka, I ceased to be betrothed, and to be an honest man.", "그루셴카를 드나들기 시작한 순간부터 나는 약혼한 몸이기를, 정직한 사내이기를 그만둔 거다."], ["I understand that.", "나도 그건 안다."], ["Why do you look at me?", "왜 나를 그렇게 보느냐?"], ["You see, I went in the first place to beat her.", "봐라, 나는 애초에 그녀를 두들겨 패러 갔던 거다."], ["I had heard, and I know for a fact now, that that captain, father’s agent, had given Grushenka an I.O.U.", "그 대위, 아버지의 앞잡이 노릇 하는 그놈이 내 차용증을 그루셴카에게 넘겨서, 그녀를 시켜 나를 상대로 빚 독촉 소송을 걸게 해 나를 끝장내려 한다는 얘길 들었거든."], ["of mine for her to sue me for payment, so as to put an end to me.", "이제는 그게 사실인 줄 확실히 안다."], ["They wanted to scare me.", "그놈들은 나를 겁주려 했던 거야."], ["I went to beat her.", "나는 그녀를 패러 갔지."], ["I had had a glimpse of her before.", "전에 그녀를 흘끗 본 적은 있었다."], ["She doesn’t strike one at first sight.", "첫눈에 사람을 사로잡는 여자는 아니야."], ["I knew about her old merchant, who’s lying ill now, paralyzed; but he’s leaving her a decent little sum.", "나는 그녀의 그 늙은 상인 정부(情夫) 얘긴 알고 있었다. 지금은 중풍으로 병석에 누워 있는데, 그래도 그녀에게 제법 짭짤한 돈을 남겨 줄 작자지."], ["I knew, too, that she was fond of money, that she hoarded it, and lent it at a wicked rate of interest, that she’s a merciless cheat and swindler.", "그녀가 돈을 밝히고, 돈을 모아 두었다가 악랄한 이자로 빌려 주며, 무자비한 사기꾼에 협잡꾼이라는 것도 알고 있었어."], ["I went to beat her, and I stayed.", "나는 그녀를 패러 갔다가, 눌러앉고 말았다."], ["The storm broke—it struck me down like the plague.", "폭풍이 몰아닥쳤지. 그게 역병처럼 나를 쓰러뜨렸어."], ["I’m plague-stricken still, and I know that everything is over, that there will never be anything more for me.", "나는 지금도 역병에 걸린 채야. 그리고 모든 게 끝났다는 걸, 내게는 다시는 아무것도 없으리라는 걸 안다."], ["The cycle of the ages is accomplished.", "세월의 한 주기가 다 차 버린 거지."], ["That’s my position.", "그게 내 처지다."], ["And though I’m a beggar, as fate would have it, I had three thousand just then in my pocket.", "그리고 비록 나는 운명의 장난으로 거지 신세지만, 그때만은 마침 주머니에 삼천 루블이 있었어."], ["I drove with Grushenka to Mokroe, a place twenty-five versts from here.", "나는 그루셴카를 데리고 모크로예로 마차를 몰고 갔다. 여기서 이십오 베르스타쯤 떨어진 곳이지."], ["I got gypsies there and champagne and made all the peasants there drunk on it, and all the women and girls.", "거기서 집시들을 불러 모으고 샴페인을 들이고, 거기 농부들을 죄다 술에 취하게 했어. 여자들과 처녀들도 몽땅 말이다."], ["I sent the thousands flying.", "그렇게 수천 루블을 날려 버렸지."], ["In three days’ time I was stripped bare, but a hero.", "사흘 만에 나는 알거지가 됐지만, 영웅이 됐어."], ["Do you suppose the hero had gained his end?", "그래, 그 영웅이 제 뜻을 이뤘을 것 같으냐?"], ["Not a sign of it from her.", "그녀 쪽에선 그런 기미라곤 손톱만큼도 없었다."], ["I tell you that rogue, Grushenka, has a supple curve all over her body.", "내 말하건대, 그 요부 그루셴카는 온몸에 나긋나긋한 곡선이 흐르는 여자야. 그 자그마한 발에서도, 새끼발가락에서도 그게 보인다."], ["You can see it in her little foot, even in her little toe.", "나는 그걸 봤고, 입을 맞췄지. 하지만 그게 다였어, 맹세코!"], ["I saw it, and kissed it, but that was all, I swear!", "'원한다면 당신과 결혼해 드리죠.' 그녀가 그러더구나."], ["‘I’ll marry you if you like,’ she said, ‘you’re a beggar, you know.", "'당신, 거지잖아요, 알죠. 나를 때리지 않겠다고, 내가 하고 싶은 대로 뭐든 하게 해 주겠다고 약속해요."], ["Say that you won’t beat me, and will let me do anything I choose, and perhaps I will marry you.’ She laughed, and she’s laughing still!”", "그럼 어쩌면 결혼해 드릴지도 몰라요.' 그녀는 웃었다, 지금도 웃고 있고!\""]], [["Dmitri leapt up with a sort of fury.", "드미트리는 일종의 광기에 사로잡혀 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["He seemed all at once as though he were drunk.", "그는 별안간 술에 취한 사람 같았다."], ["His eyes became suddenly bloodshot.", "두 눈이 갑자기 시뻘겋게 충혈됐다."]], [["“And do you really mean to marry her?”", "\"그래서 형은 정말 그녀와 결혼할 작정이에요?\""]], [["“At once, if she will.", "\"그녀만 원한다면 당장에라도."], ["And if she won’t, I shall stay all the same.", "그녀가 원치 않는다 해도, 나는 그대로 머물 거다."], ["I’ll be the porter at her gate.", "그녀 집 문지기가 되어 줄 테야."], ["Alyosha!”", "알료샤!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["He stopped short before him, and taking him by the shoulders began shaking him violently.", "그는 알료샤 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 두 어깨를 붙잡고 사납게 흔들어 대기 시작했다."], ["“Do you know, you innocent boy, that this is all delirium, senseless delirium, for there’s a tragedy here.", "\"너 아느냐, 이 순진한 녀석아, 이게 다 헛소리란 걸, 정신 나간 헛소리란 걸 말이다. 여기엔 비극이 도사리고 있으니까."], ["Let me tell you, Alexey, that I may be a low man, with low and degraded passions, but a thief and a pickpocket Dmitri Karamazov never can be.", "알렉세이, 내 말 들어라, 나는 비천한 인간일지 모른다, 천박하고 타락한 정욕에 사로잡힌 인간일지 몰라. 하지만 도둑이나 소매치기, 드미트리 카라마조프는 결코 그런 놈은 될 수 없다."], ["Well, then; let me tell you that I am a thief and a pickpocket.", "자, 그런데 말이다, 내가 바로 그 도둑이요 소매치기라면 어쩌겠느냐."], ["That very morning, just before I went to beat Grushenka, Katerina Ivanovna sent for me, and in strict secrecy (why I don’t know, I suppose she had some reason) asked me to go to the chief town of the province and to post three thousand roubles to Agafya Ivanovna in Moscow, so that nothing should be known of it in the town here.", "바로 그날 아침, 내가 그루셴카를 패러 가기 직전에, 카테리나 이바노브나가 나를 불러서는 엄중히 비밀을 지키라며(왜 그랬는지는 모른다, 무슨 이유가 있었겠지) 도청 소재지로 가서 모스크바의 아가피야 이바노브나에게 삼천 루블을 부쳐 달라고, 여기 읍내에서는 아무도 모르게 해 달라고 부탁했어. 그래서 그루셴카를 보러 갈 때 나는 그 삼천 루블을 주머니에 지니고 있었고, 모크로예에서 우리가 흥청거리며 쓴 게 바로 그 돈이다."], ["So I had that three thousand roubles in my pocket when I went to see Grushenka, and it was that money we spent at Mokroe.", "그러고 나서 나는 도청에 다녀온 척했지만, 그녀에게 우체국 영수증은 보여 주지 않았어. 돈을 부쳤다고, 영수증을 가져오겠다고 말해 놓고는 여태 안 가져왔지."], ["Afterwards I pretended I had been to the town, but did not show her the post office receipt.", "잊어버린 척한 거야. 자, 이제 네가 오늘 그녀에게 가서 뭐라고 말할 것 같으냐?"], ["I said I had sent the money and would bring the receipt, and so far I haven’t brought it.", "'그가 안부를 전한다더라.' 그러면 그녀가 묻겠지."], ["I’ve forgotten it.", "'돈은 어떻게 됐나요?'"], ["Now what do you think you’re going to her to-day to say?", "그래도 너는 이렇게는 말할 수 있었을 거다. '그는 타락한 호색한이고, 절제 못 하는 정욕에 사로잡힌 비천한 작자입니다."], ["‘He sends his compliments,’ and she’ll ask you, ‘What about the money?’ You might still have said to her, ‘He’s a degraded sensualist, and a low creature, with uncontrolled passions.", "그때 당신 돈을 부치지 않고 탕진해 버렸어요. 천한 짐승처럼 자제하지 못했기 때문이죠.'"], ["He didn’t send your money then, but wasted it, because, like a low brute, he couldn’t control himself.’ But still you might have added, ‘He isn’t a thief though.", "그래도 이렇게 덧붙일 수는 있었겠지. '하지만 도둑은 아닙니다."], ["Here is your three thousand; he sends it back.", "여기 당신의 삼천 루블이 있어요. 그가 돌려보냅니다."], ["Send it yourself to Agafya Ivanovna.", "아가피야 이바노브나에게는 당신이 직접 부치세요. 그리고 그가 「안부를 전한다」고 말하라더군요.'"], ["But he told me to say “he sends his compliments.”’ But, as it is, she will ask, ‘But where is the money?’”", "하지만 사정이 이러하니, 그녀는 묻겠지. '그런데 그 돈은 어디 있나요?'\""]], [["“Mitya, you are unhappy, yes!", "\"미탸 형, 형은 불행해요, 그래요!"], ["But not as unhappy as you think.", "하지만 형이 생각하는 만큼 불행하진 않아요."], ["Don’t worry yourself to death with despair.”", "절망에 빠져 스스로를 죽도록 괴롭히지 말아요.\""]], [["“What, do you suppose I’d shoot myself because I can’t get three thousand to pay back?", "\"뭐냐, 갚을 삼천 루블을 못 구한다고 내가 권총으로 자살이라도 할 것 같으냐?"], ["That’s just it.", "바로 그게 문제야."], ["I shan’t shoot myself.", "나는 자살하지 않을 거다."], ["I haven’t the strength now.", "지금은 그럴 기력이 없어."], ["Afterwards, perhaps.", "나중에는 모르지, 어쩌면."], ["But now I’m going to Grushenka.", "하지만 지금은 그루셴카에게로 갈 거다."], ["I don’t care what happens.”", "무슨 일이 벌어지든 상관없어.\""]], [["“And what then?”", "\"그럼 그다음은요?\""]], [["“I’ll be her husband if she deigns to have me, and when lovers come, I’ll go into the next room.", "\"그녀가 나 따위를 받아 준다면 그녀의 남편이 되어 줄 테고, 정부들이 찾아오면 나는 옆방으로 물러가 주지."], ["I’ll clean her friends’ goloshes, blow up their samovar, run their errands.”", "그녀 정부들의 덧신을 닦아 주고, 사모바르에 불을 지펴 주고, 심부름을 다녀 주마.\""]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna will understand it all,”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나는 다 이해할 거예요.\""], ["Alyosha said solemnly.", "알료샤가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["“She’ll understand how great this trouble is and will forgive.", "\"이 고통이 얼마나 큰지 알아주고 용서할 거예요."], ["She has a lofty mind, and no one could be more unhappy than you.", "그녀는 고결한 마음을 지녔고, 형보다 더 불행한 사람은 없으니까요."], ["She’ll see that for herself.”", "그녀 스스로 그걸 알아볼 거예요.\""]], [["“She won’t forgive everything,”", "\"그녀가 다 용서하진 못할 거다.\""], ["said Dmitri, with a grin.", "드미트리가 히죽 웃으며 말했다."], ["“There’s something in it, brother, that no woman could forgive.", "\"여기엔 말이다, 동생아, 어떤 여자도 용서 못 할 무언가가 있어."], ["Do you know what would be the best thing to do?”", "가장 좋은 방법이 뭔지 아느냐?\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭔데요?\""]], [["“Pay back the three thousand.”", "\"삼천 루블을 갚는 거지.\""]], [["“Where can we get it from?", "\"그 돈을 어디서 구해요?"], ["I say, I have two thousand.", "저기, 나한테 이천 루블이 있어요."], ["Ivan will give you another thousand—that makes three.", "이반 형이 천 루블을 더 줄 거예요. 그럼 삼천이 되잖아요."], ["Take it and pay it back.”", "그걸 받아서 갚으세요.\""]], [["“And when would you get it, your three thousand?", "\"그 삼천 루블을 네가 언제 마련하겠느냐?"], ["You’re not of age, besides, and you must—you absolutely must—take my farewell to her to-day, with the money or without it, for I can’t drag on any longer, things have come to such a pass.", "게다가 넌 아직 미성년이고, 그리고 너는 오늘, 반드시, 무슨 일이 있어도 오늘 그녀에게 내 작별 인사를 전해야 한다. 돈이 있든 없든 말이다."], ["To-morrow is too late.", "나는 더는 질질 끌 수가 없어, 일이 이 지경에 이르렀으니까."], ["I shall send you to father.”", "내일은 너무 늦어. 너를 아버지에게 보내야겠다.\""]], [["“To father?”", "\"아버지에게요?\""]], [["“Yes, to father first.", "\"그래, 우선 아버지에게."], ["Ask him for three thousand.”", "삼천 루블을 달라고 해 봐.\""]], [["“But, Mitya, he won’t give it.”", "\"하지만 미탸 형, 아버지는 안 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“As though he would!", "\"줄 리가 있나!"], ["I know he won’t.", "안 줄 거라는 거 안다."], ["Do you know the meaning of despair, Alexey?”", "알렉세이, 너 절망이 뭔지 아느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알아요.\""]], [["“Listen.", "\"들어 봐라."], ["Legally he owes me nothing.", "법적으로 그는 내게 빚진 게 없다."], ["I’ve had it all from him, I know that.", "나는 받을 걸 그에게서 다 받았어, 나도 안다."], ["But morally he owes me something, doesn’t he?", "하지만 도의적으로는 그가 내게 빚진 게 있지 않으냐?"], ["You know he started with twenty-eight thousand of my mother’s money and made a hundred thousand with it.", "알다시피 그는 내 어머니의 돈 이만팔천 루블로 시작해서 그걸로 십만 루블을 벌었어."], ["Let him give me back only three out of the twenty-eight thousand, and he’ll draw my soul out of hell, and it will atone for many of his sins.", "그 이만팔천 가운데 단 삼천만 내게 돌려준다면, 그는 내 영혼을 지옥에서 끌어내 주는 셈이고, 그건 그가 지은 숱한 죄를 속죄해 줄 거다."], ["For that three thousand—I give you my solemn word—I’ll make an end of everything, and he shall hear nothing more of me.", "그 삼천 루블만 준다면, 내 엄숙히 약속하건대, 나는 모든 걸 끝내고 그는 다시는 내 소식을 듣지 못할 거야."], ["For the last time I give him the chance to be a father.", "마지막으로 그에게 아버지 노릇 할 기회를 주는 거다."], ["Tell him God Himself sends him this chance.”", "하느님 그분께서 친히 이 기회를 그에게 보내신 거라고 전해 줘.\""]], [["“Mitya, he won’t give it for anything.”", "\"미탸 형, 아버지는 무슨 일이 있어도 안 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“I know he won’t.", "\"안 줄 거란 거 안다."], ["I know it perfectly well.", "더없이 잘 알아."], ["Now, especially.", "지금은 특히 더."], ["That’s not all.", "그게 다가 아니다."], ["I know something more.", "나는 더 알고 있는 게 있어."], ["Now, only a few days ago, perhaps only yesterday he found out for the first time in earnest (underline in earnest) that Grushenka is really perhaps not joking, and really means to marry me.", "자, 바로 며칠 전에, 어쩌면 바로 어제, 아버지는 처음으로 진지하게(진지하게라는 말에 밑줄을 그어라) 그루셴카가 정말 농담하는 게 아닐지도 모른다는 걸, 정말로 나와 결혼할 마음이라는 걸 알아챘다."], ["He knows her nature; he knows the cat.", "그는 그녀의 본성을 알아, 그 암고양이를 안단 말이다."], ["And do you suppose he’s going to give me money to help to bring that about when he’s crazy about her himself?", "그런데 자기가 그녀에게 미쳐 있으면서, 그 일이 이뤄지도록 도우라고 내게 돈을 내줄 것 같으냐?"], ["And that’s not all, either.", "게다가 그게 또 다가 아니야."], ["I can tell you more than that.", "그보다 더한 것도 말해 줄 수 있다."], ["I know that for the last five days he has had three thousand drawn out of the bank, changed into notes of a hundred roubles, packed into a large envelope, sealed with five seals, and tied across with red tape.", "나는 안다, 지난 닷새 동안 그가 은행에서 삼천 루블을 찾아다가, 백 루블짜리 지폐로 바꿔, 커다란 봉투에 넣고, 봉인을 다섯 개나 찍고, 붉은 끈으로 가위표를 쳐서 묶어 두었다는 걸 말이야. 봐라, 내가 얼마나 죄다 환히 알고 있는지!"], ["You see how well I know all about it!", "봉투에는 이렇게 적혀 있다. '내 천사 그루셴카에게, 그녀가 내게 와 줄 때.'"], ["On the envelope is written: ‘To my angel, Grushenka, when she will come to me.’ He scrawled it himself in silence and in secret, and no one knows that the money’s there except the valet, Smerdyakov, whom he trusts like himself.", "그가 말없이, 몰래 직접 휘갈겨 쓴 거지. 그리고 그 돈이 거기 있다는 걸 아는 자라곤, 자기 분신처럼 믿는 하인 스메르댜코프밖에 없어."], ["So now he has been expecting Grushenka for the last three or four days; he hopes she’ll come for the money.", "그래서 지금 그는 지난 사나흘 동안 그루셴카를 기다려 온 거다. 그녀가 그 돈을 받으러 오기를 바라는 거지."], ["He has sent her word of it, and she has sent him word that perhaps she’ll come.", "그가 그녀에게 그렇게 기별을 넣었고, 그녀는 어쩌면 가겠다고 답을 보냈어."], ["And if she does go to the old man, can I marry her after that?", "그런데 만일 그녀가 그 늙은이에게 가 버린다면, 그러고 나서 내가 어떻게 그녀와 결혼하겠느냐?"], ["You understand now why I’m here in secret and what I’m on the watch for.”", "이제 알겠지, 내가 왜 여기 숨어 있는지, 내가 무얼 지켜보고 있는지 말이다.\""]], [["“For her?”", "\"그녀를요?\""]], [["“Yes, for her.", "\"그래, 그녀를."], ["Foma has a room in the house of these sluts here.", "포마가 이 여기 갈보들 집에 방 하나를 얻어 산다."], ["Foma comes from our parts; he was a soldier in our regiment.", "포마는 우리 고향 사람이야. 우리 연대에서 군인이었지."], ["He does jobs for them.", "그 여자들의 잔심부름을 해 주고 있어."], ["He’s watchman at night and goes grouse-shooting in the day-time; and that’s how he lives.", "밤에는 야경꾼 노릇을 하고, 낮에는 들꿩을 잡으러 다니지. 그렇게 먹고사는 거다."], ["I’ve established myself in his room.", "나는 그놈 방에 자리를 잡았어."], ["Neither he nor the women of the house know the secret—that is, that I am on the watch here.”", "그놈도, 이 집 여자들도 비밀은 모른다. 그러니까 내가 여기서 망을 보고 있다는 걸 말이지.\""]], [["“No one but Smerdyakov knows, then?”", "\"그럼 스메르댜코프 말고는 아무도 모르는 거예요?\""]], [["“No one else.", "\"아무도 몰라."], ["He will let me know if she goes to the old man.”", "그녀가 그 늙은이에게 가면 그놈이 내게 알려 줄 거다.\""]], [["“It was he told you about the money, then?”", "\"그럼 형한테 그 돈 얘길 해 준 게 그놈이었어요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["It’s a dead secret.", "그건 절대 비밀이야."], ["Even Ivan doesn’t know about the money, or anything.", "이반조차도 그 돈에 대해선, 아무것도 몰라."], ["The old man is sending Ivan to Tchermashnya on a two or three days’ journey.", "그 늙은이는 이반을 체르마시냐로 이삼일 일정으로 보내려 하고 있어."], ["A purchaser has turned up for the copse: he’ll give eight thousand for the timber.", "잡목림을 사겠다는 사람이 나타났거든. 목재 값으로 팔천 루블을 주겠다는 거야."], ["So the old man keeps asking Ivan to help him by going to arrange it.", "그래서 늙은이가 이반에게 그 일을 처리하러 가서 좀 도와 달라고 자꾸 부탁하는 거다."], ["It will take him two or three days.", "이삼일은 걸릴 거야."], ["That’s what the old man wants, so that Grushenka can come while he’s away.”", "늙은이가 바라는 게 바로 그거지. 자기가 없는 사이에 그루셴카가 올 수 있도록 말이다.\""]], [["“Then he’s expecting Grushenka to-day?”", "\"그럼 아버지는 오늘 그루셴카를 기다리는 거예요?\""]], [["“No, she won’t come to-day; there are signs.", "\"아니, 오늘은 안 올 거다. 그런 낌새가 있어."], ["She’s certain not to come,”", "그녀는 분명 오지 않을 거야.\""], ["cried Mitya suddenly.", "미탸가 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“Smerdyakov thinks so, too.", "\"스메르댜코프도 그렇게 생각해."], ["Father’s drinking now.", "아버지는 지금 술을 마시고 있다."], ["He’s sitting at table with Ivan.", "이반과 함께 식탁에 앉아 있지."], ["Go to him, Alyosha, and ask for the three thousand.”", "그에게 가 봐라, 알료샤, 가서 삼천 루블을 달라고 해.\""]], [["“Mitya, dear, what’s the matter with you?”", "\"미탸 형, 대체 왜 이래요?\""], ["cried Alyosha, jumping up from his place, and looking keenly at his brother’s frenzied face.", "알료샤가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 형의 광기 어린 얼굴을 날카롭게 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["For one moment the thought struck him that Dmitri was mad.", "한순간 드미트리가 미친 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 그를 스쳤다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"왜 그러느냐?"], ["I’m not insane,”", "나는 미치지 않았다.\""], ["said Dmitri, looking intently and earnestly at him.", "드미트리가 진지하게 그를 골똘히 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“No fear.", "\"걱정 마."], ["I am sending you to father, and I know what I’m saying.", "나는 너를 아버지에게 보내는 거고, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 안다."], ["I believe in miracles.”", "나는 기적을 믿어.\""]], [["“In miracles?”", "\"기적을요?\""]], [["“In a miracle of Divine Providence.", "\"신의 섭리의 기적을 말이다."], ["God knows my heart.", "하느님은 내 마음을 아셔."], ["He sees my despair.", "내 절망을 보고 계셔."], ["He sees the whole picture.", "그분은 이 모든 그림을 보고 계셔."], ["Surely He won’t let something awful happen.", "설마 그분께서 끔찍한 일이 벌어지도록 내버려 두시겠느냐."], ["Alyosha, I believe in miracles.", "알료샤, 나는 기적을 믿어."], ["Go!”", "가 봐라!\""]], [["“I am going.", "\"갈게요."], ["Tell me, will you wait for me here?”", "말해 봐요, 형은 여기서 나를 기다릴 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I know it will take some time.", "시간이 좀 걸릴 거라는 거 안다."], ["You can’t go at him point blank.", "그에게 다짜고짜 들이댈 수는 없어. 지금 그는 취해 있으니까."], ["He’s drunk now.", "세 시간이든, 네 시간이든, 다섯, 여섯, 일곱 시간이든 기다리마."], ["I’ll wait three hours—four, five, six, seven.", "다만 기억해라, 너는 오늘 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가야 한다. 한밤중이 되더라도 말이야, 돈이 있든 없든, 가서 '그가 당신에게 안부를 전한다더라'고 말해야 해."], ["Only remember you must go to Katerina Ivanovna to-day, if it has to be at midnight, with the money or without the money, and say, ‘He sends his compliments to you.’ I want you to say that verse to her: ‘He sends his compliments to you.’”", "나는 네가 그 구절을 그녀에게 전해 줬으면 한다. '그가 당신에게 안부를 전한다더라.'\""]], [["“Mitya!", "\"미탸 형!"], ["And what if Grushenka comes to-day—if not to-day, to-morrow, or the next day?”", "그런데 만일 그루셴카가 오늘 오면 어쩌죠? 오늘이 아니면 내일, 아니면 모레라도?\""]], [["“Grushenka?", "\"그루셴카가?"], ["I shall see her.", "내가 그녀를 볼 거다."], ["I shall rush out and prevent it.”", "뛰쳐나가서 막을 거야.\""]], [["“And if—”", "\"그런데 만일—\""]], [["“If there’s an if, it will be murder.", "\"만일이라는 게 있다면, 그건 살인이 될 거다."], ["I couldn’t endure it.”", "나는 그걸 견딜 수 없을 거야.\""]], [["“Who will be murdered?”", "\"누가 죽는데요?\""]], [["“The old man.", "\"그 늙은이."], ["I shan’t kill her.”", "나는 그녀를 죽이진 않을 거다.\""]], [["“Brother, what are you saying?”", "\"형, 무슨 소릴 하는 거예요?\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t know....", "\"아, 나도 모르겠다……"], ["I don’t know.", "모르겠어."], ["Perhaps I shan’t kill, and perhaps I shall.", "어쩌면 안 죽일지도, 어쩌면 죽일지도."], ["I’m afraid that he will suddenly become so loathsome to me with his face at that moment.", "두려운 건, 그 순간 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 내게 너무도 역겹게 느껴질지 모른다는 거다."], ["I hate his ugly throat, his nose, his eyes, his shameless snigger.", "나는 그의 그 추한 목구멍이, 그 코가, 그 두 눈이, 그 뻔뻔한 히죽거림이 증오스러워."], ["I feel a physical repulsion.", "육체적인 혐오감이 느껴져."], ["That’s what I’m afraid of.", "그게 바로 내가 두려워하는 거다."], ["That’s what may be too much for me.”", "그게 바로 나로서는 감당 못 할 일일지도 몰라.\""]], [["“I’ll go, Mitya.", "\"갈게요, 미탸 형."], ["I believe that God will order things for the best, that nothing awful may happen.”", "나는 하느님이 가장 좋은 쪽으로 일을 마련해 주셔서, 끔찍한 일이 하나도 안 일어나도록 해 주시리라 믿어요.\""]], [["“And I will sit and wait for the miracle.", "\"그럼 나는 앉아서 그 기적을 기다리마."], ["And if it doesn’t come to pass—”", "그런데 만일 그게 이루어지지 않는다면—\""]], [["Alyosha went thoughtfully towards his father’s house.", "알료샤는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 아버지의 집을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["# Chapter VI. Smerdyakov", "# Chapter VI. Smerdyakov"]], [["He did in fact find his father still at table.", "그는 과연 아버지가 아직 식탁에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Though there was a dining-room in the house, the table was laid as usual in the drawing-room, which was the largest room, and furnished with old-fashioned ostentation.", "집에는 식당이 따로 있었으나 식탁은 여느 때처럼 응접실에 차려져 있었는데, 그곳이 집에서 가장 큰 방으로 옛날식의 과시적인 취향으로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["The furniture was white and very old, upholstered in old, red, silky material.", "가구는 흰색이었고 몹시 낡았으며, 낡고 붉은 비단 천으로 씌워져 있었다."], ["In the spaces between the windows there were mirrors in elaborate white and gilt frames, of old-fashioned carving.", "창문과 창문 사이에는 흰색과 금색으로 정교하게 짠 옛날식 조각 틀에 끼운 거울들이 걸려 있었다."], ["On the walls, covered with white paper, which was torn in many places, there hung two large portraits—one of some prince who had been governor of the district thirty years before, and the other of some bishop, also long since dead.", "흰 벽지를 발랐으나 군데군데 찢어진 벽에는 커다란 초상화 두 점이 걸려 있었는데, 하나는 삼십 년 전 이 지역의 지사를 지낸 어느 공작의 초상이었고, 다른 하나는 역시 오래전에 죽은 어느 주교의 초상이었다."], ["In the corner opposite the door there were several ikons, before which a lamp was lighted at nightfall ...", "문 맞은편 구석에는 성상 몇 점이 놓여 있었고, 그 앞에는 해가 지면 등불이 밝혀졌는데…"], ["not so much for devotional purposes as to light the room.", "그것은 신앙심에서라기보다 방을 밝히기 위함이었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch used to go to bed very late, at three or four o’clock in the morning, and would wander about the room at night or sit in an arm-chair, thinking.", "표도르 파블로비치는 늘 아주 늦게, 새벽 세 시나 네 시쯤에야 잠자리에 들었고, 밤이면 방 안을 거닐거나 안락의자에 앉아 생각에 잠기곤 했다."], ["This had become a habit with him.", "이것은 그의 버릇이 되어 있었다."], ["He often slept quite alone in the house, sending his servants to the lodge; but usually Smerdyakov remained, sleeping on a bench in the hall.", "그는 하인들을 별채로 보내고 집 안에서 혼자 자는 일이 잦았으나, 대개 스메르쟈코프만은 남아 현관의 긴 의자에서 잠을 잤다."]], [["When Alyosha came in, dinner was over, but coffee and preserves had been served.", "알료샤가 들어섰을 때 저녁 식사는 끝나 있었으나 커피와 설탕에 절인 과일은 나와 있었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch liked sweet things with brandy after dinner.", "표도르 파블로비치는 식후에 브랜디와 더불어 단것을 즐겼다."], ["Ivan was also at table, sipping coffee.", "이반 역시 식탁에 앉아 커피를 홀짝이고 있었다."], ["The servants, Grigory and Smerdyakov, were standing by.", "하인인 그리고리와 스메르쟈코프는 그 곁에 서 있었다."], ["Both the gentlemen and the servants seemed in singularly good spirits.", "주인이건 하인이건 다들 유난히 기분이 좋아 보였다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch was roaring with laughter.", "표도르 파블로비치는 너털웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다."], ["Before he entered the room, Alyosha heard the shrill laugh he knew so well, and could tell from the sound of it that his father had only reached the good-humored stage, and was far from being completely drunk.", "방에 들어서기 전부터 알료샤는 너무도 익숙한 그 새된 웃음소리를 들었고, 그 소리만으로도 아버지가 이제 막 거나하게 기분 좋아지는 단계에 이르렀을 뿐 아직 완전히 취하기에는 한참 멀었다는 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Here he is!", "\"여기 왔구나!"], ["Here he is!”", "여기 왔어!\""], ["yelled Fyodor Pavlovitch, highly delighted at seeing Alyosha.", "표도르 파블로비치가 알료샤를 보고 몹시 반가워하며 소리쳤다."], ["“Join us. Sit down.", "\"이리 와 앉아라."], ["Coffee is a lenten dish, but it’s hot and good.", "커피는 정진 음식이긴 하다만, 뜨겁고 맛이 좋단다."], ["I don’t offer you brandy, you’re keeping the fast.", "너한테 브랜디는 권하지 않으마, 단식을 지키는 중이니까."], ["But would you like some?", "그래도 한 잔 하겠느냐?"], ["No; I’d better give you some of our famous liqueur.", "아니지, 차라리 우리 집 그 유명한 리큐어를 좀 주는 게 낫겠구나."], ["Smerdyakov, go to the cupboard, the second shelf on the right.", "스메르쟈코프, 찬장으로 가서 오른쪽 두 번째 선반을 열어라."], ["Here are the keys.", "여기 열쇠다."], ["Look sharp!”", "얼른얼른 움직여!\""]], [["Alyosha began refusing the liqueur.", "알료샤는 리큐어를 사양하기 시작했다."]], [["“Never mind.", "\"괜찮아."], ["If you won’t have it, we will,”", "네가 안 마시겠다면 우리가 마시지.\""], ["said Fyodor Pavlovitch, beaming.", "표도르 파블로비치가 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“But stay—have you dined?”", "\"그런데 가만있자, 저녁은 먹었느냐?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Alyosha, who had in truth only eaten a piece of bread and drunk a glass of kvas in the Father Superior’s kitchen.", "알료샤가 대답했으나, 사실은 수도원장의 부엌에서 빵 한 조각을 먹고 크바스 한 잔을 마신 게 전부였다."], ["“Though I should be pleased to have some hot coffee.”", "\"그래도 뜨거운 커피라면 기꺼이 마시겠어요.\""]], [["“Bravo, my darling!", "\"옳거니, 내 사랑하는 녀석!"], ["He’ll have some coffee.", "커피를 마시겠다는구나."], ["Does it want warming?", "데워야 하나?"], ["No, it’s boiling.", "아니, 펄펄 끓고 있어."], ["It’s capital coffee: Smerdyakov’s making.", "일품 커피지. 스메르쟈코프가 끓인 거다."], ["My Smerdyakov’s an artist at coffee and at fish patties, and at fish soup, too.", "우리 스메르쟈코프는 커피 끓이는 데도, 생선 파이 만드는 데도, 생선 수프 끓이는 데도 예술가야."], ["You must come one day and have some fish soup.", "언제 한번 와서 생선 수프를 좀 먹어 봐라."], ["Let me know beforehand....", "미리 알려만 다오…"], ["But, stay; didn’t I tell you this morning to come home with your mattress and pillow and all?", "그런데 가만, 오늘 아침에 내가 너더러 요와 베개랑 다 챙겨서 집으로 들어오라 하지 않았느냐?"], ["Have you brought your mattress?", "요는 가져왔어?"], ["He he he!”", "헤헤헤!\""]], [["“No, I haven’t,”", "\"아니요, 안 가져왔어요.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling, too.", "알료샤도 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, but you were frightened, you were frightened this morning, weren’t you?", "\"아하, 그래도 너 겁이 났던 게지, 오늘 아침에 겁이 났던 거야, 안 그러냐?"], ["There, my darling, I couldn’t do anything to vex you.", "자, 내 사랑하는 녀석아, 나는 너를 언짢게 할 짓은 도무지 못 하겠구나."], ["Do you know, Ivan, I can’t resist the way he looks one straight in the face and laughs?", "이반, 알겠느냐, 나는 저 녀석이 사람 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 웃는 그 표정을 당해 낼 재간이 없단다."], ["It makes me laugh all over.", "그걸 보면 온몸으로 웃음이 나."], ["I’m so fond of him.", "나는 저 녀석이 그렇게 좋아."], ["Alyosha, let me give you my blessing—a father’s blessing.”", "알료샤, 내 너에게 축복을 내려 주마—아비의 축복을 말이다.\""]], [["Alyosha rose, but Fyodor Pavlovitch had already changed his mind.", "알료샤가 일어섰으나, 표도르 파블로비치는 벌써 마음을 바꾼 뒤였다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’ll just make the sign of the cross over you, for now.", "\"지금은 그냥 네게 성호나 그어 주마. 가만히 앉아 있어라."], ["Sit still. Now we’ve a treat for you, in your own line, too.", "자, 이제 너한테 딱 어울리는, 네 취향에 맞는 즐길 거리가 하나 있다."], ["It’ll make you laugh.", "너도 웃음이 날 게야."], ["Balaam’s ass has begun talking to us here—and how he talks!", "발람의 나귀가 여기서 우리한테 말을 하기 시작했단 말이지—게다가 얼마나 잘 지껄이는지!"], ["How he talks!”", "얼마나 잘 지껄이는지 몰라!\""]], [["Balaam’s ass, it appeared, was the valet, Smerdyakov.", "발람의 나귀란 알고 보니 하인 스메르쟈코프였다."], ["He was a young man of about four and twenty, remarkably unsociable and taciturn.", "그는 스물네 살가량의 젊은이로, 놀라우리만치 비사교적이고 과묵했다."], ["Not that he was shy or bashful.", "그렇다고 수줍거나 부끄럼을 타는 것은 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, he was conceited and seemed to despise everybody.", "오히려 그 반대로, 거만하고 누구든 다 깔보는 듯했다."]], [["But we must pause to say a few words about him now.", "그러나 여기서 잠시 그에 관해 몇 마디 해 두어야겠다."], ["He was brought up by Grigory and Marfa, but the boy grew up “with no sense of gratitude,”", "그는 그리고리와 마르파의 손에서 자랐으나, 그리고리의 말마따나 \"고마움이라고는 도무지 모르는\""], ["as Grigory expressed it; he was an unfriendly boy, and seemed to look at the world mistrustfully.", "아이로 자라났으니, 정이 없는 아이였고 세상을 불신의 눈으로 바라보는 듯했다."], ["In his childhood he was very fond of hanging cats, and burying them with great ceremony.", "어릴 적에 그는 고양이를 목매다는 것을 무척 좋아했고, 그것을 거창한 의식을 갖춰 묻어 주곤 했다."], ["He used to dress up in a sheet as though it were a surplice, and sang, and waved some object over the dead cat as though it were a censer.", "그는 시트를 몸에 둘러 마치 제의처럼 차려입고는 노래를 불렀으며, 죽은 고양이 위로 어떤 물건을 향로마냥 휘둘러 댔다."], ["All this he did on the sly, with the greatest secrecy.", "이 모든 짓을 그는 남몰래, 더없이 은밀하게 했다."], ["Grigory caught him once at this diversion and gave him a sound beating.", "한번은 그리고리가 이 장난질을 하던 그를 붙잡아 호되게 매질했다."], ["He shrank into a corner and sulked there for a week.", "그러자 그는 구석으로 움츠러들어 일주일이나 골을 부리고 있었다."], ["“He doesn’t care for you or me, the monster,”", "\"저 괴물 같은 녀석은 너도 나도 안중에 없어.\""], ["Grigory used to say to Marfa, “and he doesn’t care for any one.", "그리고리는 마르파에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"어느 누구도 안중에 없단 말이야."], ["Are you a human being?”", "너는 사람이냐?\""], ["he said, addressing the boy directly.", "그는 아이를 향해 대놓고 물었다."], ["“You’re not a human being.", "\"너는 사람도 아니야."], ["You grew from the mildew in the bath-house.[2] That’s what you are.”", "너는 목욕탕 곰팡이에서 생겨난 놈이야. 네가 그런 놈이라고.\""], ["Smerdyakov, it appeared afterwards, could never forgive him those words.", "나중에 드러난 바로는, 스메르쟈코프는 그 말만은 결코 용서할 수 없었다고 한다."], ["Grigory taught him to read and write, and when he was twelve years old, began teaching him the Scriptures.", "그리고리는 그에게 읽고 쓰는 법을 가르쳤고, 그가 열두 살이 되자 성경을 가르치기 시작했다."], ["But this teaching came to nothing.", "그러나 이 가르침은 아무런 결실도 보지 못했다."], ["At the second or third lesson the boy suddenly grinned.", "두 번째인가 세 번째 수업에서 그 아이가 별안간 히죽 웃었다."]], [["“What’s that for?”", "\"그건 또 무슨 짓이냐?\""], ["asked Grigory, looking at him threateningly from under his spectacles.", "그리고리가 안경 너머로 위협하듯 그를 노려보며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing.", "\"아니, 별거 아녜요."], ["God created light on the first day, and the sun, moon, and stars on the fourth day.", "하느님은 첫째 날에 빛을 지으셨고, 해와 달과 별은 넷째 날에 지으셨다잖아요."], ["Where did the light come from on the first day?”", "그럼 첫째 날의 빛은 어디서 나온 거예요?\""]], [["Grigory was thunderstruck.", "그리고리는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했다."], ["The boy looked sarcastically at his teacher.", "아이는 빈정대는 눈으로 제 선생을 바라보았다."], ["There was something positively condescending in his expression.", "그 표정에는 분명 사람을 내려다보는 듯한 기색이 있었다."], ["Grigory could not restrain himself.", "그리고리는 참을 수가 없었다."], ["“I’ll show you where!”", "\"어디서 나왔는지 보여 주마!\""], ["he cried, and gave the boy a violent slap on the cheek.", "그는 이렇게 소리치며 아이의 뺨을 세차게 후려쳤다."], ["The boy took the slap without a word, but withdrew into his corner again for some days.", "아이는 말없이 그 따귀를 맞았으나, 또다시 며칠 동안 제 구석으로 움츠러들었다."], ["A week later he had his first attack of the disease to which he was subject all the rest of his life—epilepsy.", "일주일 뒤 그는 평생토록 그를 따라다니게 될 병, 곧 간질의 첫 발작을 일으켰다."], ["When Fyodor Pavlovitch heard of it, his attitude to the boy seemed changed at once.", "표도르 파블로비치가 이 일을 전해 듣자, 아이를 대하는 그의 태도가 단번에 달라진 듯했다."], ["Till then he had taken no notice of him, though he never scolded him, and always gave him a copeck when he met him.", "그때까지만 해도 그는 아이를 야단친 적은 없었고 마주칠 때마다 늘 코페이카 한 닢을 쥐여 주기는 했으나, 별다른 관심을 두지는 않았다."], ["Sometimes, when he was in good humor, he would send the boy something sweet from his table.", "이따금 기분이 좋을 때면 자기 식탁에서 단것을 아이에게 보내 주기도 했다."], ["But as soon as he heard of his illness, he showed an active interest in him, sent for a doctor, and tried remedies, but the disease turned out to be incurable.", "그러나 그의 병 이야기를 듣자마자 그는 아이에게 적극적인 관심을 보여 의사를 부르고 갖은 약을 써 보았으나, 그 병은 고칠 수 없는 것으로 드러났다."], ["The fits occurred, on an average, once a month, but at various intervals.", "발작은 평균하여 한 달에 한 번꼴로 일어났으되 그 간격은 들쭉날쭉했다."], ["The fits varied too, in violence: some were light and some were very severe.", "발작의 정도도 제각각이어서 어떤 것은 가벼웠고 어떤 것은 몹시 심했다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch strictly forbade Grigory to use corporal punishment to the boy, and began allowing him to come upstairs to him.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그리고리에게 아이에게 손찌검하는 것을 엄히 금했고, 아이가 위층 자기 방으로 올라오는 것을 허락하기 시작했다."], ["He forbade him to be taught anything whatever for a time, too.", "한동안은 그에게 무엇이든 가르치는 것조차 금했다."], ["One day when the boy was about fifteen, Fyodor Pavlovitch noticed him lingering by the bookcase, and reading the titles through the glass.", "어느 날 아이가 열다섯 살쯤 되었을 무렵, 표도르 파블로비치는 그가 책장 곁에서 서성이며 유리 너머로 책 제목들을 읽고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch had a fair number of books—over a hundred—but no one ever saw him reading.", "표도르 파블로비치에게는 제법 많은 책이—백 권이 넘게—있었으나, 그가 책을 읽는 모습을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["He at once gave Smerdyakov the key of the bookcase.", "그는 곧장 스메르쟈코프에게 책장 열쇠를 내주었다."], ["“Come, read.", "\"자, 읽어 봐."], ["You shall be my librarian.", "너를 내 사서로 삼으마."], ["You’ll be better sitting reading than hanging about the courtyard.", "마당이나 빈둥거리며 돌아다니느니 앉아서 책이나 읽는 편이 나을 게다."], ["Come, read this,”", "자, 이걸 읽어 봐라.\""], ["and Fyodor Pavlovitch gave him Evenings in a Cottage near Dikanka.", "그러면서 표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 『디칸카 근교 마을의 야회』를 건네주었다."]], [["He read a little but didn’t like it.", "그는 조금 읽었으나 좋아하지 않았다."], ["He did not once smile, and ended by frowning.", "한 번도 미소 짓지 않았고, 결국에는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 말았다."]], [["“Why?", "\"왜?"], ["Isn’t it funny?”", "우습지 않으냐?\""], ["asked Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "표도르 파블로비치가 물었다."]], [["Smerdyakov did not speak.", "스메르쟈코프는 말이 없었다."]], [["“Answer, stupid!”", "\"대답을 해, 멍청한 놈아!\""]], [["“It’s all untrue,”", "\"죄다 거짓말이잖아요.\""], ["mumbled the boy, with a grin.", "아이는 히죽거리며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Then go to the devil!", "\"그럼 악마한테나 가 버려라!"], ["You have the soul of a lackey.", "너는 하인 근성을 타고났구나."], ["Stay, here’s Smaragdov’s Universal History.", "가만있자, 여기 스마라그도프의 『세계사』가 있다."], ["That’s all true.", "이건 죄다 사실이야."], ["Read that.”", "이걸 읽어.\""]], [["But Smerdyakov did not get through ten pages of Smaragdov.", "그러나 스메르쟈코프는 스마라그도프도 열 페이지를 채 넘기지 못했다."], ["He thought it dull.", "그는 그것이 따분하다고 여겼다."], ["So the bookcase was closed again.", "그리하여 책장은 다시 닫히고 말았다."]], [["Shortly afterwards Marfa and Grigory reported to Fyodor Pavlovitch that Smerdyakov was gradually beginning to show an extraordinary fastidiousness.", "얼마 지나지 않아 마르파와 그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치에게 스메르쟈코프가 차츰 유별난 결벽증을 보이기 시작했다고 일러바쳤다."], ["He would sit before his soup, take up his spoon and look into the soup, bend over it, examine it, take a spoonful and hold it to the light.", "그는 수프 그릇 앞에 앉으면 숟가락을 집어 들고 수프 속을 들여다보았고, 몸을 숙여 자세히 살피더니, 한 술 떠서는 불빛에 비추어 보았다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"그게 뭐냐?"], ["A beetle?”", "딱정벌레라도 있어?\""], ["Grigory would ask.", "그리고리가 묻곤 했다."]], [["“A fly, perhaps,”", "\"파리인지도 모르죠.\""], ["observed Marfa.", "마르파가 한마디 했다."]], [["The squeamish youth never answered, but he did the same with his bread, his meat, and everything he ate.", "이 까다로운 젊은이는 결코 대답하는 법이 없었으나, 빵이든 고기든 무엇을 먹든 똑같이 그러했다."], ["He would hold a piece on his fork to the light, scrutinize it microscopically, and only after long deliberation decide to put it in his mouth.", "한 조각을 포크에 꽂아 불빛에 비추어 현미경으로 들여다보듯 면밀히 살핀 다음, 한참을 곰곰이 따져 본 뒤에야 비로소 입에 넣기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Ach!", "\"아이고!"], ["What fine gentlemen’s airs!”", "어디서 저런 귀공자 티를 다 내는지!\""], ["Grigory muttered, looking at him.", "그리고리는 그를 바라보며 투덜거렸다."]], [["When Fyodor Pavlovitch heard of this development in Smerdyakov he determined to make him his cook, and sent him to Moscow to be trained.", "표도르 파블로비치는 스메르쟈코프의 이런 변화를 전해 듣자 그를 요리사로 삼기로 작정하고 그를 모스크바로 보내 수련을 시켰다."], ["He spent some years there and came back remarkably changed in appearance.", "그는 그곳에서 몇 해를 보낸 뒤 외모가 놀랄 만큼 달라져서 돌아왔다."], ["He looked extraordinarily old for his age.", "나이에 비해 유난히 늙어 보였다."], ["His face had grown wrinkled, yellow, and strangely emasculate.", "얼굴에는 주름이 잡혔고, 누렇게 떴으며, 어딘지 거세된 사람처럼 묘하게 변해 있었다."], ["In character he seemed almost exactly the same as before he went away.", "성품으로 말하자면 떠나기 전과 거의 똑같아 보였다."], ["He was just as unsociable, and showed not the slightest inclination for any companionship.", "여전히 비사교적이었고, 누구와도 어울리려는 기색이라고는 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["In Moscow, too, as we heard afterwards, he had always been silent.", "나중에 들은 바로는 모스크바에서도 그는 늘 말이 없었다고 한다."], ["Moscow itself had little interest for him; he saw very little there, and took scarcely any notice of anything.", "모스크바 자체도 그의 흥미를 거의 끌지 못했으니, 그는 그곳에서 본 것도 거의 없었고 무엇에든 좀처럼 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["He went once to the theater, but returned silent and displeased with it.", "그는 한 번 극장에 갔으나 못마땅한 얼굴로 말없이 돌아왔다."], ["On the other hand, he came back to us from Moscow well dressed, in a clean coat and clean linen.", "그래도 한편으로 그는 모스크바에서 옷차림을 말끔히 하고, 깨끗한 외투에 깨끗한 속옷을 입고 우리에게로 돌아왔다."], ["He brushed his clothes most scrupulously twice a day invariably, and was very fond of cleaning his smart calf boots with a special English polish, so that they shone like mirrors.", "그는 하루도 빠짐없이 하루 두 번씩 더없이 꼼꼼하게 옷을 솔질했고, 영국제 특수 광택제로 자기 멋들어진 송아지 가죽 장화를 거울처럼 번쩍이도록 닦는 것을 무척 좋아했다."], ["He turned out a first-rate cook.", "그는 일류 요리사가 되었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch paid him a salary, almost the whole of which Smerdyakov spent on clothes, pomade, perfumes, and such things.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그에게 급료를 주었는데, 스메르쟈코프는 그 돈의 거의 전부를 옷이며 포마드며 향수 따위에 써 버렸다."], ["But he seemed to have as much contempt for the female sex as for men; he was discreet, almost unapproachable, with them.", "그러나 그는 남자에게 그러하듯 여자에게도 그만한 경멸을 품은 듯했으니, 여자들에게는 조심스럽고 거의 다가갈 수 없을 만큼 쌀쌀했다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch began to regard him rather differently.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그를 다소 다른 눈으로 보기 시작했다."], ["His fits were becoming more frequent, and on the days he was ill Marfa cooked, which did not suit Fyodor Pavlovitch at all.", "그의 발작은 점점 잦아졌고, 그가 앓아눕는 날이면 마르파가 요리를 했는데, 이것이 표도르 파블로비치에게는 영 입에 맞지 않았다."]], [["“Why are your fits getting worse?”", "\"어째서 발작이 점점 더 심해지는 게냐?\""], ["asked Fyodor Pavlovitch, looking askance at his new cook.", "표도르 파블로비치가 새 요리사를 흘겨보며 물었다."], ["“Would you like to get married?", "\"장가라도 들고 싶으냐?"], ["Shall I find you a wife?”", "내가 색싯감이라도 하나 찾아 줄까?\""]], [["But Smerdyakov turned pale with anger, and made no reply.", "그러나 스메르쟈코프는 화가 나서 새파랗게 질린 채 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch left him with an impatient gesture.", "표도르 파블로비치는 짜증스러운 손짓을 하며 그를 내버려 두었다."], ["The great thing was that he had absolute confidence in his honesty.", "중요한 것은 그가 스메르쟈코프의 정직성만큼은 절대적으로 믿었다는 점이다."], ["It happened once, when Fyodor Pavlovitch was drunk, that he dropped in the muddy courtyard three hundred-rouble notes which he had only just received.", "한번은 표도르 파블로비치가 술에 취해 방금 받은 백 루블짜리 지폐 석 장을 진창이 된 마당에 떨어뜨린 일이 있었다."], ["He only missed them next day, and was just hastening to search his pockets when he saw the notes lying on the table.", "그는 이튿날에야 그것이 없어진 것을 알아채고는 부랴부랴 주머니를 뒤지려는 참에 그 지폐들이 식탁 위에 놓여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Where had they come from?", "그것들이 어디서 나왔겠는가?"], ["Smerdyakov had picked them up and brought them in the day before.", "스메르쟈코프가 전날 그것을 주워 들여놓았던 것이다."]], [["“Well, my lad, I’ve never met any one like you,”", "\"이보게, 자네 같은 사람은 내 평생 처음일세.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch said shortly, and gave him ten roubles.", "표도르 파블로비치는 간단히 이렇게 말하며 그에게 십 루블을 주었다."], ["We may add that he not only believed in his honesty, but had, for some reason, a liking for him, although the young man looked as morosely at him as at every one and was always silent. He rarely spoke.", "한마디 덧붙여 두자면, 그는 그의 정직성을 믿었을 뿐 아니라 어쩐 영문인지 그를 마음에 들어 했는데, 정작 그 젊은이는 다른 모든 사람을 대할 때처럼 침울한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았고 늘 말이 없었다."], ["If it had occurred to any one to wonder at the time what the young man was interested in, and what was in his mind, it would have been impossible to tell by looking at him.", "그는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았다. 만일 그 무렵 누군가가 이 젊은이는 무엇에 관심을 두며 그의 머릿속에는 무엇이 들어 있을까 궁금해했다 한들, 그를 들여다본다고 해서 알 길은 없었으리라."], ["Yet he used sometimes to stop suddenly in the house, or even in the yard or street, and would stand still for ten minutes, lost in thought.", "그러면서도 그는 이따금 집 안에서, 더러는 마당이나 거리에서까지 별안간 걸음을 멈추고 십 분이나 우두커니 선 채 생각에 잠겨 있곤 했다."], ["A physiognomist studying his face would have said that there was no thought in it, no reflection, but only a sort of contemplation.", "그의 얼굴을 살피는 관상가라면 거기에는 아무런 사색도, 성찰도 없고 오로지 일종의 응시만이 있다고 말했을 것이다."], ["There is a remarkable picture by the painter Kramskoy, called “Contemplation.”", "화가 크람스코이가 그린 「응시」라는 주목할 만한 그림이 있다."], ["There is a forest in winter, and on a roadway through the forest, in absolute solitude, stands a peasant in a torn kaftan and bark shoes.", "거기에는 겨울 숲이 있고, 그 숲을 가로지르는 길 위에 더할 나위 없는 고적함 속에서 한 농부가 찢어진 카프탄에 나무껍질 신을 신고 서 있다."], ["He stands, as it were, lost in thought.", "그는 흡사 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있다."], ["Yet he is not thinking; he is “contemplating.”", "그러나 그는 생각하고 있는 것이 아니라 \"응시하고\""], ["If any one touched him he would start and look at one as though awakening and bewildered.", "있는 것이다. 만일 누군가가 그를 건드린다면 그는 화들짝 놀라며 잠에서 깨어난 듯 어리둥절한 눈으로 상대를 바라볼 것이다."], ["It’s true he would come to himself immediately; but if he were asked what he had been thinking about, he would remember nothing.", "사실 그는 곧바로 정신을 차리기는 하겠으나, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었느냐고 묻는다면 아무것도 기억해 내지 못할 것이다."], ["Yet probably he has, hidden within himself, the impression which had dominated him during the period of contemplation.", "그러나 십중팔구 그는 응시의 시간 동안 그를 사로잡았던 그 인상을 제 안에 깊이 감추고 있을 것이다."], ["Those impressions are dear to him and no doubt he hoards them imperceptibly, and even unconsciously.", "그 인상들은 그에게 소중한 것이어서, 그는 분명 그것들을 알게 모르게, 심지어 무의식적으로 차곡차곡 쌓아 둘 것이다."], ["How and why, of course, he does not know either.", "어떻게 그리고 왜 그러는지는 물론 그 자신도 알지 못한다."], ["He may suddenly, after hoarding impressions for many years, abandon everything and go off to Jerusalem on a pilgrimage for his soul’s salvation, or perhaps he will suddenly set fire to his native village, and perhaps do both.", "그는 여러 해 동안 인상을 쌓아 두다가 별안간 모든 것을 버리고 영혼의 구원을 위해 예루살렘으로 순례를 떠날지도 모르고, 어쩌면 별안간 자기 고향 마을에 불을 지를지도 모르며, 어쩌면 그 둘을 다 저지를지도 모른다. 농민들 가운데는 이런 \"응시자\"가 적지 않다."], ["There are a good many “contemplatives” among the peasantry.", "그런데 스메르쟈코프도 십중팔구 그런 부류 가운데 하나였고, 그 역시 무슨 까닭인지도 잘 모른 채 탐욕스럽게 자신의 인상을 쌓아 두고 있었던 것이리라."], ["Well, Smerdyakov was probably one of them, and he probably was greedily hoarding up his impressions, hardly knowing why.", "목욕탕 곰팡이: 러시아 민간 속설에서 사생아나 정체불명의 출생을 모욕적으로 이르던 표현으로, 목욕탕(바냐)은 부정한 기운이 깃든 장소로 여겨졌다."]], [["# Chapter VII. The Controversy", "# Chapter VII. The Controversy"]], [["But Balaam’s ass had suddenly spoken.", "그러나 발람의 나귀가 별안간 입을 열었다."], ["The subject was a strange one.", "그 주제가 묘한 것이었다."], ["Grigory had gone in the morning to make purchases, and had heard from the shopkeeper Lukyanov the story of a Russian soldier which had appeared in the newspaper of that day.", "그리고리는 아침에 물건을 사러 나갔다가, 가게 주인 루키야노프에게서 그날 신문에 실린 어느 러시아 병사의 이야기를 전해 들었던 것이다."], ["This soldier had been taken prisoner in some remote part of Asia, and was threatened with an immediate agonizing death if he did not renounce Christianity and follow Islam.", "이 병사는 아시아의 어느 외진 곳에서 포로로 잡혔는데, 기독교를 버리고 이슬람을 따르지 않으면 당장 끔찍한 죽임을 당하리라는 위협을 받았다."], ["He refused to deny his faith, and was tortured, flayed alive, and died, praising and glorifying Christ.", "그는 신앙을 부인하기를 거부했고, 그리하여 고문을 당하고 산 채로 살가죽이 벗겨진 끝에 그리스도를 찬양하고 영광 돌리며 죽었다."], ["Grigory had related the story at table.", "그리고리는 식탁에서 이 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch always liked, over the dessert after dinner, to laugh and talk, if only with Grigory.", "표도르 파블로비치는 저녁 식사 후 후식을 들 때면 언제나, 비록 상대가 그리고리뿐이라 할지라도, 웃고 떠드는 것을 좋아했다."], ["This afternoon he was in a particularly good-humored and expansive mood.", "그날 오후 그는 유달리 기분이 좋고 흥이 올라 있었다."], ["Sipping his brandy and listening to the story, he observed that they ought to make a saint of a soldier like that, and to take his skin to some monastery.", "브랜디를 홀짝이며 그 이야기를 듣더니, 그는 그런 병사라면 마땅히 성인으로 떠받들고 그 살가죽을 어느 수도원에 갖다 두어야 한다고 한마디 했다."], ["“That would make the people flock, and bring the money in.”", "\"그러면 사람들이 떼 지어 몰려들 테고, 돈도 굴러들 게 아닌가.\""]], [["Grigory frowned, seeing that Fyodor Pavlovitch was by no means touched, but, as usual, was beginning to scoff.", "그리고리는 표도르 파블로비치가 조금도 감동하기는커녕 여느 때처럼 비웃기 시작하는 것을 보고 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["At that moment Smerdyakov, who was standing by the door, smiled.", "바로 그때 문가에 서 있던 스메르댜코프가 빙긋 웃었다."], ["Smerdyakov often waited at table towards the end of dinner, and since Ivan’s arrival in our town he had done so every day.", "스메르댜코프는 식사가 끝나갈 무렵이면 곧잘 시중을 들었는데, 이반이 우리 고장에 온 뒤로는 날마다 그렇게 하고 있었다."]], [["“What are you grinning at?”", "\"뭘 그렇게 히죽거리느냐?\""], ["asked Fyodor Pavlovitch, catching the smile instantly, and knowing that it referred to Grigory.", "표도르 파블로비치가 그 웃음을 대뜸 알아채고 그것이 그리고리를 두고 한 것임을 짐작하며 물었다."]], [["“Well, my opinion is,”", "\"제 생각에는 말입니다,\""], ["Smerdyakov began suddenly and unexpectedly in a loud voice, “that if that laudable soldier’s exploit was so very great there would have been, to my thinking, no sin in it if he had on such an emergency renounced, so to speak, the name of Christ and his own christening, to save by that same his life, for good deeds, by which, in the course of years to expiate his cowardice.”", "스메르댜코프가 갑작스럽고도 뜻밖에 큰 소리로 입을 열었다, \"그 갸륵한 병사의 공적이 정말 그렇게 위대한 것이었다면, 제 소견으로는, 그가 그런 위급한 처지에서, 말하자면 그리스도의 이름과 제 세례를 부인하여 바로 그것으로 제 목숨을 건졌다 한들, 그래서 여러 해에 걸쳐 선행을 쌓아 제 비겁함을 속죄하려 했다 한들, 거기에 아무 죄도 없었을 것입니다.\""]], [["“How could it not be a sin?", "\"어찌 죄가 안 된단 말이냐?"], ["You’re talking nonsense.", "헛소리를 지껄이는구나."], ["For that you’ll go straight to hell and be roasted there like mutton,”", "그딴 짓을 하면 곧장 지옥에 떨어져 양고기처럼 구워질 게다.\""], ["put in Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "표도르 파블로비치가 끼어들었다."]], [["It was at this point that Alyosha came in, and Fyodor Pavlovitch, as we have seen, was highly delighted at his appearance.", "바로 이 대목에서 알료샤가 들어왔고, 표도르 파블로비치는 앞서 보았듯이 그의 등장에 몹시 기뻐했다."]], [["“We’re on your subject, your subject,”", "\"마침 네 이야기를 하던 참이다, 네 이야기 말이다.\""], ["he chuckled gleefully, making Alyosha sit down to listen.", "그는 흐뭇하게 낄낄거리며 알료샤를 자리에 앉혀 듣게 했다."]], [["“As for mutton, that’s not so, and there’ll be nothing there for this, and there shouldn’t be either, if it’s according to justice,”", "\"양고기로 말씀드리자면, 그건 그렇지가 않습니다. 거기엔 그런 벌이 없을 것이고, 정의에 따른다면 또한 마땅히 없어야 할 것입니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov maintained stoutly.", "스메르댜코프가 꿋꿋이 주장했다."]], [["“How do you mean ‘according to justice’?”", "\"'정의에 따른다면'이라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch cried still more gayly, nudging Alyosha with his knee.", "표도르 파블로비치가 한층 더 신이 나서 소리치며, 무릎으로 알료샤를 슬쩍 찔렀다."]], [["“He’s a rascal, that’s what he is!”", "\"저놈은 악당입니다, 그게 저놈의 정체예요!\""], ["burst from Grigory.", "그리고리가 버럭 내뱉었다."], ["He looked Smerdyakov wrathfully in the face.", "그는 분노에 차서 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 노려보았다."]], [["“As for being a rascal, wait a little, Grigory Vassilyevitch,”", "\"제가 악당이라는 건 잠시 미뤄 두십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치.\" 스메르댜코프가 더없이 침착하게 대꾸했다."], ["answered Smerdyakov with perfect composure.", "\"당신 스스로 한번 헤아려 보시는 편이 나을 겁니다."], ["“You’d better consider yourself that, once I am taken prisoner by the enemies of the Christian race, and they demand from me to curse the name of God and to renounce my holy christening, I am fully entitled to act by my own reason, since there would be no sin in it.”", "가령 제가 기독교 민족의 적들에게 포로로 잡혀, 그자들이 제게 하느님의 이름을 저주하고 거룩한 세례를 부인하라고 요구한다면, 저는 제 이성에 따라 행동할 온전한 권리가 있습니다. 거기엔 아무 죄도 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“But you’ve said that before.", "\"그건 아까도 한 소리다."], ["Don’t waste words.", "말을 허비하지 마라."], ["Prove it,”", "증명을 해 보아라.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "표도르 파블로비치가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Soup-maker!”", "\"수프나 끓이는 놈이!\""], ["muttered Grigory contemptuously.", "그리고리가 경멸조로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“As for being a soup-maker, wait a bit, too, and consider for yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch, without abusing me.", "\"수프나 끓이는 놈이라는 것도 잠시 미뤄 두시고, 저를 욕하지 마시고 직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치."], ["For as soon as I say to those enemies, ‘No, I’m not a Christian, and I curse my true God,’ then at once, by God’s high judgment, I become immediately and specially anathema accursed, and am cut off from the Holy Church, exactly as though I were a heathen, so that at that very instant, not only when I say it aloud, but when I think of saying it, before a quarter of a second has passed, I am cut off.", "제가 그 적들에게 '아니다, 나는 기독교인이 아니다, 나는 나의 참된 하느님을 저주한다'고 말하는 순간, 곧바로 하느님의 지엄한 심판에 따라 저는 즉시 특별히 파문당해 저주받은 자가 되고, 거룩한 교회로부터 끊겨 나가, 흡사 이교도와 다름없게 됩니다. 그러니 그 바로 그 찰나에, 그것을 소리 내어 말할 때뿐만 아니라 그것을 말하려고 생각만 해도, 사분의 일 초가 지나기도 전에 저는 끊겨 나가는 것입니다."], ["Is that so or not, Grigory Vassilyevitch?”", "그렇습니까, 안 그렇습니까, 그리고리 바실리예비치?\""]], [["He addressed Grigory with obvious satisfaction, though he was really answering Fyodor Pavlovitch’s questions, and was well aware of it, and intentionally pretending that Grigory had asked the questions.", "그는 사실 표도르 파블로비치의 물음에 답하고 있으면서, 그 사실을 잘 알면서도, 짐짓 그리고리가 물어본 것인 양 꾸미며 그리고리를 향해 만족스러운 빛을 띠고 말했다."]], [["“Ivan,”", "\"이반,\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch suddenly, “stoop down for me to whisper.", "표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 외쳤다, \"귓속말을 하게 좀 숙여 보아라."], ["He’s got this all up for your benefit.", "저놈이 이걸 죄다 네 비위를 맞추려고 꾸민 게다."], ["He wants you to praise him.", "네가 칭찬해 주기를 바라는 게야."], ["Praise him.”", "칭찬을 해 주려무나.\""]], [["Ivan listened with perfect seriousness to his father’s excited whisper.", "이반은 아버지의 들뜬 귓속말을 더없이 진지하게 들었다."]], [["“Stay, Smerdyakov, be quiet a minute,”", "\"잠깐, 스메르댜코프, 잠시 가만히 있어 보아라.\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch once more.", "표도르 파블로비치가 다시 한번 외쳤다."], ["“Ivan, your ear again.”", "\"이반, 다시 귀 좀 대 보아라.\""]], [["Ivan bent down again with a perfectly grave face.", "이반은 또다시 자못 엄숙한 얼굴로 몸을 숙였다."]], [["“I love you as I do Alyosha.", "\"나는 너를 알료샤만큼이나 사랑한단다."], ["Don’t think I don’t love you.", "내가 너를 사랑하지 않는다고 여기지 마라."], ["Some brandy?”", "브랜디 한잔 하겠느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.—But you’re rather drunk yourself,”", "\"네. — 그런데 아버지가 좀 취하셨군요.\""], ["thought Ivan, looking steadily at his father.", "이반은 아버지를 가만히 바라보며 속으로 생각했다."]], [["He was watching Smerdyakov with great curiosity.", "그는 큰 호기심을 품고 스메르댜코프를 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“You’re anathema accursed, as it is,”", "\"너는 이미 파문당해 저주받은 놈이야.\""], ["Grigory suddenly burst out, “and how dare you argue, you rascal, after that, if—”", "그리고리가 별안간 터뜨렸다. \"그런 주제에 어찌 감히 따지고 든단 말이냐, 이 악당아, 만약—\""]], [["“Don’t scold him, Grigory, don’t scold him,”", "\"그를 나무라지 마라, 그리고리, 나무라지 말라니까.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch cut him short.", "표도르 파블로비치가 그의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“You should wait, Grigory Vassilyevitch, if only a short time, and listen, for I haven’t finished all I had to say.", "\"잠깐만 기다려 주십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 잠시만이라도요. 그리고 들어 보십시오."], ["For at the very moment I become accursed, at that same highest moment, I become exactly like a heathen, and my christening is taken off me and becomes of no avail.", "아직 할 말을 다 마치지 못했으니까요. 제가 저주받은 그 바로 그 순간, 바로 그 지엄한 순간에 저는 영락없이 이교도와 똑같이 되고, 제 세례는 벗겨져 아무 효력도 없게 됩니다."], ["Isn’t that so?”", "그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Make haste and finish, my boy,”", "\"어서 마저 끝내거라, 이놈아.\""], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch urged him, sipping from his wine-glass with relish.", "표도르 파블로비치가 포도주 잔을 흐뭇하게 홀짝이며 그를 재촉했다."]], [["“And if I’ve ceased to be a Christian, then I told no lie to the enemy when they asked whether I was a Christian or not a Christian, seeing I had already been relieved by God Himself of my Christianity by reason of the thought alone, before I had time to utter a word to the enemy.", "\"그리고 제가 이미 기독교인이기를 그쳤다면, 적들이 제게 기독교인이냐 아니냐를 물었을 때 저는 적들에게 거짓말을 한 것이 아닙니다. 왜냐하면 적에게 한마디 내뱉기도 전에, 오로지 그 생각만으로, 저는 이미 하느님 친히 제 기독교 신앙을 거두어 가셨기 때문입니다."], ["And if I have already been discharged, in what manner and with what sort of justice can I be held responsible as a Christian in the other world for having denied Christ, when, through the very thought alone, before denying Him I had been relieved from my christening?", "그리고 제가 이미 면제받았다면, 저세상에서 무슨 도리로, 어떤 정의에 비추어, 제가 그리스도를 부인했다 하여 기독교인으로서 책임을 져야 한단 말입니까? 그분을 부인하기에 앞서, 오로지 그 생각 하나만으로 저는 이미 세례에서 풀려난 처지인데요."], ["If I’m no longer a Christian, then I can’t renounce Christ, for I’ve nothing then to renounce.", "제가 더는 기독교인이 아니라면, 저는 그리스도를 부인할 수도 없습니다. 그때는 부인할 것이 아무것도 없으니까요."], ["Who will hold an unclean Tatar responsible, Grigory Vassilyevitch, even in heaven, for not having been born a Christian?", "그리고리 바실리예비치, 부정한 타타르인을 누가, 천국에서라 한들, 기독교인으로 태어나지 못했다 하여 책임을 묻겠습니까?"], ["And who would punish him for that, considering that you can’t take two skins off one ox?", "또 누가 그를 그 일로 벌하겠습니까, 황소 한 마리에서 가죽을 두 장이나 벗길 수 없는 노릇인데 말입니다."], ["For God Almighty Himself, even if He did make the Tatar responsible, when he dies would give him the smallest possible punishment, I imagine (since he must be punished), judging that he is not to blame if he has come into the world an unclean heathen, from heathen parents.", "전능하신 하느님 친히, 설령 그 타타르인에게 책임을 물으신다 하더라도, 그가 죽으면 제 짐작으로는 가능한 한 가장 가벼운 벌을 내리실 겁니다(어쨌든 벌은 받아야 할 테니까요). 그가 이교도 부모에게서 부정한 이교도로 세상에 태어난 것은 그의 잘못이 아니라고 헤아리실 테니 말입니다."], ["The Lord God can’t surely take a Tatar and say he was a Christian?", "주 하느님께서 설마 타타르인을 붙들고 그가 기독교인이었다고 말씀하시겠습니까?"], ["That would mean that the Almighty would tell a real untruth.", "그렇게 되면 전능하신 분께서 진짜 거짓말을 하시는 셈이 됩니다."], ["And can the Lord of Heaven and earth tell a lie, even in one word?”", "한데 하늘과 땅의 주님께서 단 한마디라도 거짓을 말씀하실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["Grigory was thunderstruck and looked at the orator, his eyes nearly starting out of his head.", "그리고리는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯이 눈알이 거의 튀어나올 지경으로 그 웅변가를 바라보았다."], ["Though he did not clearly understand what was said, he had caught something in this rigmarole, and stood, looking like a man who has just hit his head against a wall.", "그는 무슨 말인지 또렷이 알아듣지는 못했으나, 그 횡설수설 속에서 뭔가를 붙잡았는지, 방금 벽에 머리를 들이받은 사람 같은 꼴로 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch emptied his glass and went off into his shrill laugh.", "표도르 파블로비치는 잔을 비우고는 날카로운 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Alyosha!", "\"알료샤!"], ["Alyosha!", "알료샤!"], ["What do you say to that!", "너는 저 말을 어떻게 보느냐!"], ["Ah, you casuist!", "아, 이 궤변가 같으니!"], ["He must have been with the Jesuits, somewhere, Ivan.", "저놈은 분명 어디선가 예수회 놈들과 어울려 지냈을 게다, 이반."], ["Oh, you stinking Jesuit, who taught you?", "오, 이 구린내 나는 예수회 놈아, 누가 너를 가르쳤느냐?"], ["But you’re talking nonsense, you casuist, nonsense, nonsense, nonsense.", "하지만 너는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있는 게야, 이 궤변가야, 헛소리, 헛소리, 헛소리란 말이다."], ["Don’t cry, Grigory, we’ll reduce him to smoke and ashes in a moment.", "울지 마라, 그리고리, 우리가 곧 저놈을 연기와 재로 만들어 버릴 테니."], ["Tell me this, O ass; you may be right before your enemies, but you have renounced your faith all the same in your own heart, and you say yourself that in that very hour you became anathema accursed.", "자, 이놈, 나귀야, 이 말에 대답해 보아라. 네가 적들 앞에서는 옳을지 몰라도, 어쨌든 너는 네 마음속에서 신앙을 부인한 것이고, 게다가 네 입으로 바로 그 순간에 파문당해 저주받은 자가 되었다고 했잖느냐."], ["And if once you’re anathema they won’t pat you on the head for it in hell.", "그리고 일단 파문당하고 나면 지옥에서 그 일로 너의 머리를 쓰다듬어 주지는 않을 게다."], ["What do you say to that, my fine Jesuit?”", "그 말에는 뭐라 답하겠느냐, 이 멋진 예수회 놈아?\""]], [["“There is no doubt that I have renounced it in my own heart, but there was no special sin in that.", "\"제가 마음속에서 그것을 부인했다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 하지만 거기에 특별한 죄는 없었습니다."], ["Or if there was sin, it was the most ordinary.”", "설령 죄가 있었다 한들, 그건 더없이 평범한 죄였습니다.\""]], [["“How’s that the most ordinary?”", "\"그게 어째서 더없이 평범한 죄란 말이냐?\""]], [["“You lie, accursed one!”", "\"거짓말 마라, 이 저주받은 놈아!\""], ["hissed Grigory.", "그리고리가 쉭쉭거리며 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“Consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch,”", "\"직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치.\""], ["Smerdyakov went on, staid and unruffled, conscious of his triumph, but, as it were, generous to the vanquished foe.", "스메르댜코프는 제 승리를 의식하면서도 패한 적에게 짐짓 너그러운 태도로, 차분하고 흔들림 없이 말을 이었다."], ["“Consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch; it is said in the Scripture that if you have faith, even as a mustard seed, and bid a mountain move into the sea, it will move without the least delay at your bidding.", "\"직접 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치. 성경에 이르기를, 겨자씨만 한 믿음이라도 있어 산더러 바다로 옮겨 가라 명하면 그 산이 당신의 명에 따라 조금도 지체 없이 옮겨 가리라 하였습니다."], ["Well, Grigory Vassilyevitch, if I’m without faith and you have so great a faith that you are continually swearing at me, you try yourself telling this mountain, not to move into the sea for that’s a long way off, but even to our stinking little river which runs at the bottom of the garden.", "자, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 저는 믿음이 없고 당신은 줄곧 저를 욕하실 만큼 그토록 큰 믿음을 지니셨으니, 당신께서 친히 이 산더러 명해 보시지요. 바다는 너무 머니 거기까지 갈 것도 없고, 그저 우리 정원 아래로 흐르는 저 구린내 나는 작은 개울로라도 옮겨 가라고 해 보십시오."], ["You’ll see for yourself that it won’t budge, but will remain just where it is however much you shout at it, and that shows, Grigory Vassilyevitch, that you haven’t faith in the proper manner, and only abuse others about it.", "당신은 산이 꿈쩍도 하지 않고, 당신이 아무리 소리쳐도 바로 그 자리에 그대로 있는 것을 직접 보시게 될 겁니다. 그것은, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 당신께 제대로 된 믿음이 없으며, 그저 그 일로 남을 욕하기만 한다는 것을 보여 줍니다."], ["Again, taking into consideration that no one in our day, not only you, but actually no one, from the highest person to the lowest peasant, can shove mountains into the sea—except perhaps some one man in the world, or, at most, two, and they most likely are saving their souls in secret somewhere in the Egyptian desert, so you wouldn’t find them—if so it be, if all the rest have no faith, will God curse all the rest?", "게다가 오늘날 그 누구도, 당신뿐만 아니라 실로 그 누구도, 가장 높은 분에서부터 가장 미천한 농부에 이르기까지, 산을 바다로 떠밀어 넣을 수는 없다는 점을 헤아려 보십시오. 어쩌면 세상에 단 한 사람, 기껏해야 두 사람쯤은 그럴 수 있을지 모르나, 그들마저도 십중팔구 이집트 사막 어딘가에서 남몰래 제 영혼을 구하고 있을 테니 당신은 그들을 찾지도 못할 겁니다."], ["that is, the population of the whole earth, except about two hermits in the desert, and in His well-known mercy will He not forgive one of them?", "만일 그렇다면, 만일 나머지 모두가 믿음이 없다면, 하느님께서 나머지 모두를 저주하시겠습니까? 다시 말해, 사막의 은수자 두 사람쯤을 제외한 온 땅의 모든 백성을 저주하시고, 그 널리 알려진 자비로도 그중 단 한 사람조차 용서치 않으시겠습니까?"], ["And so I’m persuaded that though I may once have doubted I shall be forgiven if I shed tears of repentance.”", "그러니 저는 확신합니다. 제가 한때 의심한 적이 있다 하더라도, 회개의 눈물을 흘린다면 용서받으리라고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Stay!”", "\"잠깐!\""], ["cried Fyodor Pavlovitch, in a transport of delight.", "표도르 파블로비치가 환희에 도취되어 외쳤다."], ["“So you do suppose there are two who can move mountains?", "\"그러니까 너는 산을 옮길 수 있는 자가 둘은 있다고 본단 말이지?"], ["Ivan, make a note of it, write it down.", "이반, 그걸 적어 두어라, 받아 적으라니까."], ["There you have the Russian all over!”", "거기에 러시아인의 본색이 고스란히 드러나 있어!\""]], [["“You’re quite right in saying it’s characteristic of the people’s faith,”", "\"그것이 민중 신앙의 특징이라 하시는 건 지당하신 말씀입니다.\""], ["Ivan assented, with an approving smile.", "이반이 흡족한 미소를 띠며 동의했다."]], [["“You agree.", "\"네가 동의하는구나."], ["Then it must be so, if you agree.", "네가 동의한다면 정녕 그런 게 틀림없지."], ["It’s true, isn’t it, Alyosha?", "그렇지 않으냐, 알료샤?"], ["That’s the Russian faith all over, isn’t it?”", "저게 러시아 신앙 그대로지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“No, Smerdyakov has not the Russian faith at all,”", "\"아닙니다, 스메르댜코프는 결코 러시아 신앙을 지니고 있지 않습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha firmly and gravely.", "알료샤가 단호하고 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“I’m not talking about his faith.", "\"나는 저놈의 신앙을 두고 하는 말이 아니다."], ["I mean those two in the desert, only that idea.", "사막에 있는 그 두 사람, 그 발상 하나만을 두고 하는 말이지."], ["Surely that’s Russian, isn’t it?”", "그건 분명 러시아적이지 않으냐?\""]], [["“Yes, that’s purely Russian,”", "\"네, 그건 순전히 러시아적입니다.\""], ["said Alyosha smiling.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Your words are worth a gold piece, O ass, and I’ll give it to you to-day.", "\"네 말은 금화 한 닢의 값어치가 있구나, 이 나귀야. 오늘 그걸 너에게 주마."], ["But as to the rest you talk nonsense, nonsense, nonsense.", "하지만 나머지로 말하면 너는 헛소리를 지껄이고 있어, 헛소리, 헛소리, 헛소리란 말이다. 이 멍청한 놈아, 내가 한마디 일러 주마."], ["Let me tell you, stupid, that we here are all of little faith, only from carelessness, because we haven’t time; things are too much for us, and, in the second place, the Lord God has given us so little time, only twenty-four hours in the day, so that one hasn’t even time to get sleep enough, much less to repent of one’s sins.", "우리는 여기 모두 믿음이 변변찮은데, 그건 그저 무심해서 그런 게야, 우리에게 시간이 없으니까. 일이 우리를 너무 짓누르고, 게다가 둘째로는, 주 하느님께서 우리에게 시간을 너무 적게, 하루에 고작 스물네 시간밖에 주지 않으셨으니, 잠도 실컷 잘 짬이 없는 판에 죄를 회개할 겨를이야 더 말할 것도 없지."], ["While you have denied your faith to your enemies when you’d nothing else to think about but to show your faith!", "한데 너로 말하면, 오직 네 신앙을 드러내는 것 말고는 달리 생각할 거리도 없는 마당에 적들에게 신앙을 부인했단 말이다!"], ["So I consider, brother, that it constitutes a sin.”", "그러니 내 보기에는, 이 사람아, 그게 죄가 되는 게야.\""]], [["“Constitute a sin it may, but consider yourself, Grigory Vassilyevitch, that it only extenuates it, if it does constitute.", "\"죄가 될지는 모릅니다만, 헤아려 보십시오, 그리고리 바실리예비치, 그것이 설령 죄가 된다 하더라도 그저 죄를 덜어 줄 뿐입니다."], ["If I had believed then in very truth, as I ought to have believed, then it really would have been sinful if I had not faced tortures for my faith, and had gone over to the pagan Mohammedan faith.", "만일 제가 그때 마땅히 믿었어야 할 만큼 참으로 믿고 있었다면, 그렇다면 정말이지 제 신앙을 위해 고문을 마주하지 않고 이교도인 마호메트교로 넘어갔다는 것은 죄가 되었을 겁니다. 하지만 물론 그때는 고문에 이르지도 않았을 겁니다."], ["But, of course, it wouldn’t have come to torture then, because I should only have had to say at that instant to the mountain, ‘Move and crush the tormentor,’ and it would have moved and at the very instant have crushed him like a black-beetle, and I should have walked away as though nothing had happened, praising and glorifying God.", "저는 그 찰나에 산더러 '옮겨 가 저 고문자를 짓눌러라' 하고 말하기만 하면 되었을 테고, 그러면 산은 옮겨 가 바로 그 찰나에 그자를 검은 풍뎅이처럼 짓이겨 버렸을 테니까요."], ["But, suppose at that very moment I had tried all that, and cried to that mountain, ‘Crush these tormentors,’ and it hadn’t crushed them, how could I have helped doubting, pray, at such a time, and at such a dread hour of mortal terror?", "그리고 저는 아무 일도 없었던 듯 하느님을 찬양하고 영광 돌리며 걸어 나왔을 겁니다. 한데 가령 제가 바로 그 순간에 그 모든 것을 시험해 보아, 그 산더러 '이 고문자들을 짓눌러라'"], ["And apart from that, I should know already that I could not attain to the fullness of the Kingdom of Heaven (for since the mountain had not moved at my word, they could not think very much of my faith up aloft, and there could be no very great reward awaiting me in the world to come).", "하고 외쳤는데도 산이 그들을 짓누르지 않았다면, 그런 때에, 그런 끔찍한 죽음의 공포의 순간에, 대체 제가 어찌 의심하지 않을 수 있었겠습니까? 그뿐 아니라 저는 이미, 제가 천국의 충만함에 이를 수 없다는 것을 알았을 겁니다(제 말 한마디에 산이 옮겨 가지 않았으니, 저 위에서는 제 믿음을 대단찮게 여겼을 테고, 저세상에서 저를 기다리는 큰 상도 없었을 테니까요)."], ["So why should I let them flay the skin off me as well, and to no good purpose?", "그러니 무엇 하러 제가, 아무런 좋은 보람도 없이, 제 살가죽까지 벗기게 두겠습니까?"], ["For, even though they had flayed my skin half off my back, even then the mountain would not have moved at my word or at my cry.", "설령 그들이 제 등에서 살가죽을 절반쯤 벗겨 냈다 한들, 그래도 산은 제 말이나 외침에 옮겨 가지 않았을 테니까요."], ["And at such a moment not only doubt might come over one but one might lose one’s reason from fear, so that one would not be able to think at all.", "게다가 그런 순간에는 의심이 덮쳐들 뿐만 아니라 두려움 때문에 정신을 잃어, 아무 생각도 못 하게 될 수 있습니다."], ["And, therefore, how should I be particularly to blame if not seeing my advantage or reward there or here, I should, at least, save my skin.", "그러니 저세상에서든 이승에서든 제 이득도 상도 보이지 않는 마당에, 적어도 제 살가죽만이라도 건진다 한들, 어찌 제게 유달리 잘못이 있다 하겠습니까?"], ["And so trusting fully in the grace of the Lord I should cherish the hope that I might be altogether forgiven.”", "그래서 저는 주님의 은총을 온전히 믿고, 제가 죄다 용서받으리라는 희망을 품을 것입니다.\""]], [["# Chapter VIII. Over The Brandy", "# Chapter VIII. Over The Brandy"]], [["The controversy was over.", "논쟁은 끝났다."], ["But, strange to say, Fyodor Pavlovitch, who had been so gay, suddenly began frowning.", "그러나 이상하게도, 그토록 명랑하던 표도르 파블로비치가 별안간 얼굴을 찌푸리기 시작했다."], ["He frowned and gulped brandy, and it was already a glass too much.", "그는 인상을 쓰며 브랜디를 들이켰는데, 이미 한 잔이 지나친 양이었다."]], [["“Get along with you, Jesuits!”", "\"꺼져라, 이 예수회 놈들아!\""], ["he cried to the servants.", "그가 하인들에게 소리쳤다."], ["“Go away, Smerdyakov.", "\"물러가라, 스메르댜코프."], ["I’ll send you the gold piece I promised you to-day, but be off!", "오늘 약속한 금화는 보내 줄 테니 어서 꺼져!"], ["Don’t cry, Grigory.", "울지 마, 그리고리."], ["Go to Marfa.", "마르파한테 가거라."], ["She’ll comfort you and put you to bed.", "그 사람이 너를 달래서 재워 줄 게다."], ["The rascals won’t let us sit in peace after dinner,”", "이 망나니 같은 놈들이 점심 먹고 나서도 우리를 편히 앉아 있게 내버려 두질 않는단 말이야.\""], ["he snapped peevishly, as the servants promptly withdrew at his word.", "그가 짜증스럽게 내뱉자, 하인들은 그 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 물러갔다."]], [["“Smerdyakov always pokes himself in now, after dinner.", "\"스메르댜코프 저 녀석은 요즘 들어 점심 먹고 나면 꼭 끼어든단 말이야."], ["It’s you he’s so interested in.", "저 녀석이 그토록 흥미를 갖는 게 바로 너야."], ["What have you done to fascinate him?”", "도대체 무슨 짓을 했길래 저 녀석을 그렇게 사로잡았느냐?\""], ["he added to Ivan.", "그가 이반에게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Nothing whatever,”", "\"아무것도 한 게 없습니다.\""], ["answered Ivan.", "이반이 대답했다."], ["“He’s pleased to have a high opinion of me; he’s a lackey and a mean soul.", "\"저 녀석은 멋대로 저를 높이 평가하는 거지요. 하인이고 비루한 영혼이니까요."], ["Raw material for revolution, however, when the time comes.”", "그래도 때가 오면 혁명의 원료로는 쓰일 만하지요.\""]], [["“For revolution?”", "\"혁명이라고?\""]], [["“There will be others and better ones.", "\"다른 자들이, 더 나은 자들이 나올 겁니다."], ["But there will be some like him as well.", "하지만 저 녀석 같은 자들도 더러 있겠지요."], ["His kind will come first, and better ones after.”", "저런 부류가 먼저 나오고, 더 나은 자들이 그다음에 나오는 거지요.\""]], [["“And when will the time come?”", "\"그 때라는 게 언제 오는데?\""]], [["“The rocket will go off and fizzle out, perhaps.", "\"로켓이 한번 솟구쳤다가 흐지부지 꺼져 버릴지도 모르지요."], ["The peasants are not very fond of listening to these soup-makers, so far.”", "농민들은 아직까지 이런 멀건 죽이나 끓이는 자들의 말에 귀 기울이는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, brother, but a Balaam’s ass like that thinks and thinks, and the devil knows where he gets to.”", "\"아, 이 사람아, 그래도 저런 발람의 나귀 같은 놈은 생각하고 또 생각하다 보면, 어디까지 가닿을지 악마나 알 노릇이지.\""]], [["“He’s storing up ideas,”", "\"녀석은 머릿속에 생각을 쌓아 두고 있지요.\""], ["said Ivan, smiling.", "이반이 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“You see, I know he can’t bear me, nor any one else, even you, though you fancy that he has a high opinion of you.", "\"이봐, 나는 저 녀석이 나를 견디지 못한다는 걸 알아. 누구든 다 못 견뎌 하지, 너까지도 말이야."], ["Worse still with Alyosha, he despises Alyosha.", "네가 자기를 높이 평가한다고 멋대로 상상하고 있겠지만 말이지. 알료샤한테는 더 심해서, 저 녀석은 알료샤를 경멸하거든."], ["But he doesn’t steal, that’s one thing, and he’s not a gossip, he holds his tongue, and doesn’t wash our dirty linen in public.", "하지만 도둑질은 안 한다, 그건 하나 인정할 만하지. 게다가 입이 가볍지도 않아서 입을 꾹 다물고, 우리 집 더러운 속사정을 밖에 떠벌리지도 않거든."], ["He makes capital fish pasties too.", "생선 파이도 기막히게 잘 만들고 말이야."], ["But, damn him, is he worth talking about so much?”", "그래도 빌어먹을, 저 녀석이 이렇게나 떠들 만한 가치가 있는 놈이냔 말이지?\""]], [["“Of course he isn’t.”", "\"물론 없지요.\""]], [["“And as for the ideas he may be hatching, the Russian peasant, generally speaking, needs thrashing.", "\"그리고 저 녀석이 머릿속에 부화시키고 있을 생각들로 말하자면, 러시아 농민이란 대체로 두들겨 패야 마땅한 족속이야."], ["That I’ve always maintained.", "나는 늘 그렇게 주장해 왔어."], ["Our peasants are swindlers, and don’t deserve to be pitied, and it’s a good thing they’re still flogged sometimes.", "우리 농민들은 사기꾼이라 동정받을 자격이 없고, 가끔이라도 매를 맞는 건 잘된 일이지."], ["Russia is rich in birches.", "러시아엔 자작나무가 풍부하잖느냐."], ["If they destroyed the forests, it would be the ruin of Russia.", "만약 숲을 다 없애 버린다면 그거야말로 러시아의 파멸일 게다."], ["I stand up for the clever people.", "나는 영리한 사람들 편이야."], ["We’ve left off thrashing the peasants, we’ve grown so clever, but they go on thrashing themselves.", "우리가 하도 영리해진 나머지 농민들을 패는 일은 그만뒀지만, 그놈들은 저희끼리 알아서 패고 있단 말이지."], ["And a good thing too.", "그것도 잘된 일이야."], ["‘For with what measure ye mete it shall be measured to you again,’ or how does it go?", "'너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라', 뭐 그렇게 가는 거 아니냐? 아무튼 헤아림은 받게 돼 있어."], ["Anyhow, it will be measured.", "하지만 러시아란 죄다 돼지 같은 더러움뿐이야."], ["But Russia’s all swinishness.", "이봐, 내가 러시아를 얼마나 미워하는지 네가 안다면……"], ["My dear, if you only knew how I hate Russia....", "아니, 러시아가 아니라 이 모든 악덕을 말이야! 하지만 어쩌면 러시아를 두고 하는 말일지도 모르지."], ["That is, not Russia, but all this vice!", "그 모든 게 다 돼지짓이야……. 내가 뭘 좋아하는지 아느냐?"], ["But maybe I mean Russia.", "나는 재치를 좋아한다.\""], ["Tout cela c’est de la cochonnerie....", "* 멀건 죽이나 끓이는 자들: 원문 '수프 만드는 자들(soup-makers)'."], ["Do you know what I like? I like wit.”", "사회주의·혁명 선동가들을 빈정대어 이르는 표현."]], [["“You’ve had another glass.", "\"또 한 잔 드셨군요."], ["That’s enough.”", "그만하면 충분합니다.\""]], [["“Wait a bit.", "\"잠깐만."], ["I’ll have one more, and then another, and then I’ll stop.", "한 잔만 더 하고, 또 한 잔 하고, 그러고 나서 그만두마."], ["No, stay, you interrupted me.", "아니, 가만있어, 네가 내 말을 끊었잖느냐."], ["At Mokroe I was talking to an old man, and he told me: ‘There’s nothing we like so much as sentencing girls to be thrashed, and we always give the lads the job of thrashing them.", "모크로예에서 어떤 늙은이랑 이야기를 나눴는데, 그자가 나한테 이러더구나. '우리한테 계집애들을 매질하라고 선고하는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없는데, 매질하는 일은 늘 사내놈들한테 맡깁죠."], ["And the girl he has thrashed to-day, the young man will ask in marriage to-morrow.", "그래서 오늘 매질당한 계집애한테 그 젊은 놈이 내일이면 청혼을 한답니다."], ["So it quite suits the girls, too,’ he said.", "그러니 계집애들한테도 아주 잘 맞아떨어지는 셈이지요.'"], ["There’s a set of de Sades for you!", "자, 이거야말로 사드 후작들의 무리 아니냐!"], ["But it’s clever, anyway.", "어쨌든 영리하긴 하지."], ["Shall we go over and have a look at it, eh?", "우리 한번 가서 구경이라도 할까, 응?"], ["Alyosha, are you blushing?", "알료샤, 너 얼굴이 붉어졌느냐?"], ["Don’t be bashful, child.", "부끄러워 말거라, 얘야."], ["I’m sorry I didn’t stay to dinner at the Superior’s and tell the monks about the girls at Mokroe.", "내가 수도원장 식탁에 남아서 그 수도사들한테 모크로예 계집애들 얘기를 들려주지 못한 게 아쉽구나."], ["Alyosha, don’t be angry that I offended your Superior this morning.", "알료샤, 내가 오늘 아침 네 수도원장을 모욕했다고 화내지 마라."], ["I lost my temper.", "그만 성질을 못 참았던 게야."], ["If there is a God, if He exists, then, of course, I’m to blame, and I shall have to answer for it.", "만약 신이 있다면, 정말로 존재한다면, 물론 그건 내 잘못이고 그 죄값을 치러야겠지."], ["But if there isn’t a God at all, what do they deserve, your fathers?", "하지만 신이라곤 아예 없다면, 네 그 신부 양반들이 무슨 대접을 받아 마땅하겠느냐?"], ["It’s not enough to cut their heads off, for they keep back progress.", "머리를 잘라 내는 걸로도 모자라, 그자들은 진보를 가로막고 있단 말이야."], ["Would you believe it, Ivan, that that lacerates my sentiments?", "이반, 이게 내 감정을 갈가리 찢어 놓는다는 걸 너는 믿겠느냐?"], ["No, you don’t believe it as I see from your eyes.", "아니, 넌 안 믿는구나, 네 눈을 보니 알겠다."], ["You believe what people say, that I’m nothing but a buffoon.", "너는 사람들이 하는 말, 내가 어릿광대에 지나지 않는다는 그 말을 믿고 있는 게지."], ["Alyosha, do you believe that I’m nothing but a buffoon?”", "알료샤, 너는 내가 어릿광대에 지나지 않는다고 믿느냐?\""]], [["“No, I don’t believe it.”", "\"아뇨, 그렇게 믿지 않습니다.\""]], [["“And I believe you don’t, and that you speak the truth.", "\"그래, 네가 안 믿는다는 것도, 또 네가 진실을 말한다는 것도 나는 믿는다."], ["You look sincere and you speak sincerely.", "너는 진실해 보이고 진실하게 말하지."], ["But not Ivan.", "하지만 이반은 아니야."], ["Ivan’s supercilious....", "이반은 거만하단 말이지……."], ["I’d make an end of your monks, though, all the same.", "그래도 어쨌든 나라면 네 수도사들을 끝장내 버리겠다."], ["I’d take all that mystic stuff and suppress it, once for all, all over Russia, so as to bring all the fools to reason.", "그 신비주의 같은 잡것들을 죄다 거둬다가 러시아 전역에서 단번에 영원히 짓눌러 버려서, 그 바보들을 다 정신 차리게 하겠어."], ["And the gold and the silver that would flow into the mint!”", "그러면 조폐국으로 흘러들 그 금이며 은이 또 얼마겠느냐!\""]], [["“But why suppress it?”", "\"그런데 왜 그걸 짓눌러야 하는데요?\""], ["asked Ivan.", "이반이 물었다."]], [["“That Truth may prevail.", "\"진리가 널리 퍼지도록 하기 위해서지."], ["That’s why.”", "그게 이유야.\""]], [["“Well, if Truth were to prevail, you know, you’d be the first to be robbed and suppressed.”", "\"그런데, 만약 진리가 널리 퍼지게 된다면 말이지요, 맨 먼저 약탈당하고 짓눌릴 사람은 바로 아버지일 텐데요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I dare say you’re right.", "네 말이 옳을지도 모르겠다."], ["Ah, I’m an ass!”", "아, 나는 멍청이로구나!\""], ["burst out Fyodor Pavlovitch, striking himself lightly on the forehead.", "표도르 파블로비치가 자기 이마를 가볍게 탁 치며 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["“Well, your monastery may stand then, Alyosha, if that’s how it is.", "\"그래, 사정이 그렇다면 네 수도원도 그냥 서 있게 둬야겠구나, 알료샤."], ["And we clever people will sit snug and enjoy our brandy.", "그리고 우리 영리한 사람들은 편히 앉아 브랜디나 즐기면 되겠지."], ["You know, Ivan, it must have been so ordained by the Almighty Himself.", "이봐, 이반, 이건 분명 전능하신 분이 친히 그렇게 정해 놓으신 게 틀림없어."], ["Ivan, speak, is there a God or not?", "이반, 말해 봐라, 신이 있느냐 없느냐?"], ["Stay, speak the truth, speak seriously.", "가만, 진실을 말해, 진지하게 말하란 말이다."], ["Why are you laughing again?”", "너 왜 또 웃느냐?\""]], [["“I’m laughing that you should have made a clever remark just now about Smerdyakov’s belief in the existence of two saints who could move mountains.”", "\"방금 아버지가 스메르댜코프의 믿음에 대해 영리한 말씀을 하셨던 게 떠올라 웃은 겁니다. 산을 옮길 수 있는 두 성인이 존재한다는 그 믿음 말이지요.\""]], [["“Why, am I like him now, then?”", "\"그럼 뭐냐, 내가 지금 저 녀석을 닮았다는 게냐?\""]], [["“Very much.”", "\"아주 많이요.\""]], [["“Well, that shows I’m a Russian, too, and I have a Russian characteristic.", "\"그래, 그건 나도 러시아인이라는 걸, 나한테도 러시아인의 특성이 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거지."], ["And you may be caught in the same way, though you are a philosopher.", "그리고 너도 철학자라지만 똑같은 식으로 걸려들 수 있어."], ["Shall I catch you?", "내가 너를 한번 걸려들게 해 볼까?"], ["What do you bet that I’ll catch you to-morrow.", "내일 너를 걸려들게 할 테니 뭘 걸겠느냐?"], ["Speak, all the same, is there a God, or not?", "그건 그렇고 어쨌든 말해 봐, 신이 있느냐 없느냐?"], ["Only, be serious.", "단, 진지하게 말이다."], ["I want you to be serious now.”", "지금만큼은 네가 진지했으면 좋겠다.\""]], [["“No, there is no God.”", "\"아뇨, 신은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Alyosha, is there a God?”", "\"알료샤, 신이 있느냐?\""]], [["“There is.”", "\"있습니다.\""]], [["“Ivan, and is there immortality of some sort, just a little, just a tiny bit?”", "\"이반, 그럼 불멸이란 게, 어떤 종류라도, 아주 조금이라도, 정말 손톱만큼이라도 있느냐?\""]], [["“There is no immortality either.”", "\"불멸도 없습니다.\""]], [["“None at all?”", "\"전혀 없어?\""]], [["“None at all.”", "\"전혀 없습니다.\""]], [["“There’s absolute nothingness then.", "\"그렇다면 절대적인 무(無)뿐이로구나."], ["Perhaps there is just something?", "어쩌면 뭐라도 조금은 있지 않을까?"], ["Anything is better than nothing!”", "무엇이든 아무것도 없는 것보단 나으니 말이야!\""]], [["“Absolute nothingness.”", "\"절대적인 무뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Alyosha, is there immortality?”", "\"알료샤, 불멸이 있느냐?\""]], [["“There is.”", "\"있습니다.\""]], [["“God and immortality?”", "\"신도 불멸도?\""]], [["“God and immortality.", "\"신도 불멸도요."], ["In God is immortality.”", "신 안에 불멸이 있습니다.\""]], [["“H’m!", "\"흠!"], ["It’s more likely Ivan’s right.", "아무래도 이반 말이 옳을 성싶구나."], ["Good Lord!", "맙소사!"], ["to think what faith, what force of all kinds, man has lavished for nothing, on that dream, and for how many thousand years.", "인간이 그 꿈 하나에 온갖 종류의 믿음과 힘을 그렇게나 헛되이 쏟아부어 왔다니, 그것도 몇천 년 동안이나 말이야."], ["Who is it laughing at man?", "대체 누가 인간을 비웃고 있는 거냐?"], ["Ivan!", "이반!"], ["For the last time, once for all, is there a God or not?", "마지막으로, 단번에 영원히 묻겠다, 신이 있느냐 없느냐?"], ["I ask for the last time!”", "마지막으로 묻는 게야!\""]], [["“And for the last time there is not.”", "\"그리고 마지막으로 답하지만, 없습니다.\""]], [["“Who is laughing at mankind, Ivan?”", "\"인류를 비웃고 있는 게 누구냐, 이반?\""]], [["“It must be the devil,”", "\"악마겠지요.\""], ["said Ivan, smiling.", "이반이 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“And the devil?", "\"그럼 악마는?"], ["Does he exist?”", "그놈은 존재하느냐?\""]], [["“No, there’s no devil either.”", "\"아뇨, 악마도 없습니다.\""]], [["“It’s a pity.", "\"그것 참 안됐구나."], ["Damn it all, what wouldn’t I do to the man who first invented God!", "빌어먹을, 신이란 걸 맨 처음 지어낸 놈한테 내가 무슨 짓인들 못 하겠느냐!"], ["Hanging on a bitter aspen tree would be too good for him.”", "쓰디쓴 사시나무에 매달아 죽이는 것으로도 그놈한테는 과분하지.\""]], [["“There would have been no civilization if they hadn’t invented God.”", "\"신을 지어내지 않았더라면 문명이라는 것도 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Wouldn’t there have been?", "\"없었을 거라고?"], ["Without God?”", "신이 없었더라면?\""]], [["“No. And there would have been no brandy either.", "\"없었을 겁니다."], ["But I must take your brandy away from you, anyway.”", "그리고 브랜디라는 것도 없었을 거고요. 하지만 어쨌든 이 브랜디는 아버지한테서 거둬 가야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Stop, stop, stop, dear boy, one more little glass.", "\"잠깐, 잠깐, 잠깐만, 얘야, 딱 한 잔만 더."], ["I’ve hurt Alyosha’s feelings.", "내가 알료샤의 마음을 상하게 했구나."], ["You’re not angry with me, Alyosha?", "너 나한테 화난 건 아니지, 알료샤?"], ["My dear little Alexey!”", "내 사랑하는 어린 알렉세이야!\""]], [["“No, I am not angry.", "\"아뇨, 화 안 났어요."], ["I know your thoughts.", "아버지 마음속 생각을 저는 알아요."], ["Your heart is better than your head.”", "아버지는 머리보다 마음이 더 나은 분이세요.\""]], [["“My heart better than my head, is it?", "\"내 머리보다 마음이 더 낫다고? 오, 주여!"], ["Oh, Lord! And that from you.", "그것도 네 입에서 나온 말이라니."], ["Ivan, do you love Alyosha?”", "이반, 너는 알료샤를 사랑하느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You must love him”", "\"넌 저 애를 사랑해야 해.\""], ["(Fyodor Pavlovitch was by this time very drunk). “Listen, Alyosha, I was rude to your elder this morning.", "(이 무렵 표도르 파블로비치는 잔뜩 취해 있었다.) \"들어 봐라, 알료샤, 내가 오늘 아침 네 장로한테 무례하게 굴었지."], ["But I was excited.", "하지만 그땐 내가 흥분했던 거야."], ["But there’s wit in that elder, don’t you think, Ivan?”", "그래도 그 장로한테는 재치가 있어, 그렇게 생각 안 하느냐, 이반?\""]], [["“Very likely.”", "\"그럴 법도 하지요.\""]], [["“There is, there is.", "\"있고말고, 있어."], ["Il y a du Piron là-dedans.", "그 안에는 피롱 같은 데가 있단 말이야."], ["He’s a Jesuit, a Russian one, that is.", "그자는 예수회원이야, 러시아식 예수회원이지."], ["As he’s an honorable person there’s a hidden indignation boiling within him at having to pretend and affect holiness.”", "그래도 명예로운 인물인지라, 거룩한 척 위선을 떨고 신성한 체해야 하는 데 대한 숨은 분노가 그 속에서 부글부글 끓고 있는 거야.\""]], [["“But, of course, he believes in God.”", "\"하지만 물론 그분은 신을 믿겠지요.\""]], [["“Not a bit of it.", "\"천만에. 너 몰랐느냐?"], ["Didn’t you know? Why, he tells every one so, himself.", "아니, 그 사람이 만나는 사람마다 자기 입으로 그렇게 말하고 다니는걸."], ["That is, not every one, but all the clever people who come to him.", "그러니까 만나는 사람마다는 아니고, 자기를 찾아오는 영리한 사람들 모두한테 말이지. 얼마 전엔 슐츠 지사한테 대놓고 이러더라는구나."], ["He said straight out to Governor Schultz not long ago: ‘Credo, but I don’t know in what.’”", "'나는 믿습니다. 하지만 무엇을 믿는지는 모릅니다.'\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말로요?\""]], [["“He really did.", "\"정말로 그랬다니까."], ["But I respect him.", "하지만 나는 그 사람을 존경해."], ["There’s something of Mephistopheles about him, or rather of ‘The hero of our time’ ...", "그 사람한테는 메피스토펠레스 같은 데가 있어, 아니 그보다 '우리 시대의 영웅'에 나오는……"], ["Arbenin, or what’s his name?...", "아르베닌이던가, 아니 그자 이름이 뭐더라?……"], ["You see, he’s a sensualist.", "알겠느냐, 그 사람은 호색한이야."], ["He’s such a sensualist that I should be afraid for my daughter or my wife if she went to confess to him.", "어찌나 지독한 호색한인지, 내 딸이나 아내가 그자한테 고해하러 간다면 나는 걱정이 돼서 못 견딜 게야. 글쎄, 그 사람이 옛날이야기를 시작하면 말이지……."], ["You know, when he begins telling stories....", "재작년엔 우리를 차 마시러 초대했는데, 술이 든 차였지(부인네들이 그 사람한테 술을 보내거든)."], ["The year before last he invited us to tea, tea with liqueur (the ladies send him liqueur), and began telling us about old times till we nearly split our sides....", "그러더니 옛 시절 이야기를 시작하는데 우리가 배꼽이 빠지도록 웃을 지경이었다니까……. 특히 한번은 그자가 중풍 걸린 여자를 고쳐 줬다는 얘긴데."], ["Especially how he once cured a paralyzed woman.", "'내 다리만 성하다면 당신들한테 춤을 한바탕 춰 보일 텐데' 하더라니까."], ["‘If my legs were not bad I know a dance I could dance you,’ he said.", "그런 말 어떻게 생각하느냐? '내가 한창때엔 별의별 재주를 다 부렸지요'"], ["What do you say to that?", "하더란 말이야. 데르니도프라는 상인한테서 육만 루블을 가로채기도 했고.\""], ["‘I’ve plenty of tricks in my time,’ said he.", "* 피롱: 알렉시 피롱(1689~1773). 재치와 신랄한 풍자로 이름난 프랑스 시인."], ["He did Dernidov, the merchant, out of sixty thousand.”", "* '우리 시대의 영웅': 미하일 레르몬토프의 소설. 표도르 파블로비치는 작중 인물 페초린과 다른 작품 인물 아르베닌을 혼동하고 있다."]], [["“What, he stole it?”", "\"뭐라고요, 그분이 그 돈을 훔쳤다고요?\""]], [["“He brought him the money as a man he could trust, saying, ‘Take care of it for me, friend, there’ll be a police search at my place to-morrow.’ And he kept it.", "\"그자가 믿을 만한 사람한테 맡기는 척 그 돈을 가져다주면서 이랬다는 거야. '여보게, 이걸 나 대신 좀 맡아 주게."], ["‘You have given it to the Church,’ he declared.", "내일 우리 집에 경찰 수색이 있을 거라네.' 그러고는 그걸 꿀꺽한 거지."], ["I said to him: ‘You’re a scoundrel,’ I said.", "'당신은 그 돈을 교회에 바친 거요'라고 그자가 딱 잘라 말하더란 거야. 내가 그자한테 그랬지."], ["‘No,’ said he, ‘I’m not a scoundrel, but I’m broad-minded.’ But that wasn’t he, that was some one else.", "'당신은 악당이오'라고 말이야. 그랬더니 그자가 '아니오, 나는 악당이 아니라 도량이 넓은 거요'라고 하더라는 거야."], ["I’ve muddled him with some one else ...", "아니, 그건 그자가 아니라 다른 사람이었구나."], ["without noticing it.", "내가 그자를 누구랑……"], ["Come, another glass and that’s enough.", "나도 모르게 헷갈렸어. 자, 한 잔만 더 하면 그걸로 충분하다."], ["Take away the bottle, Ivan.", "이반, 그 병을 치워라."], ["I’ve been telling lies.", "내가 거짓말을 늘어놓고 있었구나."], ["Why didn’t you stop me, Ivan, and tell me I was lying?”", "이반, 너는 왜 나를 말리지 않고, 내가 거짓말을 하고 있다고 일러 주지 않았느냐?\""]], [["“I knew you’d stop of yourself.”", "\"아버지가 알아서 멈추실 줄 알았으니까요.\""]], [["“That’s a lie.", "\"그건 거짓말이야."], ["You did it from spite, from simple spite against me.", "너는 악의로 그런 거야, 나에 대한 순전한 악의로 말이지."], ["You despise me.", "너는 나를 경멸해."], ["You have come to me and despised me in my own house.”", "너는 나를 찾아와서는 내 집에서 나를 경멸하고 있는 거야.\""]], [["“Well, I’m going away.", "\"그럼 저는 가 보겠습니다."], ["You’ve had too much brandy.”", "브랜디를 너무 많이 드셨어요.\""]], [["“I’ve begged you for Christ’s sake to go to Tchermashnya for a day or two, and you don’t go.”", "\"내가 그리스도를 봐서라도 하루 이틀만 체르마시냐에 다녀와 달라고 빌었는데, 너는 가질 않는구나.\""]], [["“I’ll go to-morrow if you’re so set upon it.”", "\"그렇게 고집하신다면 내일 다녀오지요.\""]], [["“You won’t go.", "\"넌 안 갈 거다."], ["You want to keep an eye on me.", "너는 나를 감시하고 싶은 거야."], ["That’s what you want, spiteful fellow.", "네가 원하는 게 바로 그거지, 이 심술궂은 녀석."], ["That’s why you won’t go.”", "그래서 안 가려는 거야.\""]], [["The old man persisted.", "노인은 끈질기게 물고 늘어졌다."], ["He had reached that state of drunkenness when the drunkard who has till then been inoffensive tries to pick a quarrel and to assert himself.", "그는 그때까지 얌전하던 술꾼이 별안간 시비를 걸고 제 고집을 부리려 드는, 바로 그런 취기의 단계에 이르러 있었다."]], [["“Why are you looking at me?", "\"왜 나를 그렇게 쳐다보느냐?"], ["Why do you look like that?", "왜 그런 눈으로 보느냔 말이다?"], ["Your eyes look at me and say, ‘You ugly drunkard!’ Your eyes are mistrustful.", "네 눈은 나를 보면서 '이 추악한 주정뱅이!' 하고 말하고 있어."], ["They’re contemptuous....", "네 눈은 못 믿겠다는 눈빛이야."], ["You’ve come here with some design.", "경멸하는 눈빛이고……. 너는 무슨 꿍꿍이속을 품고 여기 온 거야."], ["Alyosha, here, looks at me and his eyes shine.", "여기 이 알료샤를 봐라, 나를 보는 눈이 빛나고 있잖느냐."], ["Alyosha doesn’t despise me.", "알료샤는 나를 경멸하지 않아."], ["Alexey, you mustn’t love Ivan.”", "알렉세이야, 너는 이반을 사랑해선 안 된다.\""]], [["“Don’t be ill-tempered with my brother.", "\"제 형한테 그렇게 심술궂게 굴지 마세요."], ["Leave off attacking him,”", "형을 공격하는 건 그만하세요.\""], ["Alyosha said emphatically.", "알료샤가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, all right.", "\"오, 알았다, 알았어."], ["Ugh, my head aches.", "윽, 머리가 아프구나."], ["Take away the brandy, Ivan.", "브랜디를 치워라, 이반."], ["It’s the third time I’ve told you.”", "벌써 세 번째 말하는 거다.\""]], [["He mused, and suddenly a slow, cunning grin spread over his face.", "그는 잠시 생각에 잠기더니, 별안간 느리고 교활한 웃음이 그의 얼굴 위로 번져 갔다."]], [["“Don’t be angry with a feeble old man, Ivan.", "\"기력 없는 늙은이한테 화내지 마라, 이반."], ["I know you don’t love me, but don’t be angry all the same.", "네가 나를 사랑하지 않는다는 건 안다만, 그래도 화는 내지 마."], ["You’ve nothing to love me for.", "너로선 나를 사랑할 이유가 없으니까."], ["You go to Tchermashnya.", "너 체르마시냐에 다녀오너라."], ["I’ll come to you myself and bring you a present.", "내가 직접 너한테 가서 선물을 가져다주마."], ["I’ll show you a little wench there.", "거기서 어린 계집애 하나를 보여 주지."], ["I’ve had my eye on her a long time.", "오래전부터 눈여겨봐 둔 애야."], ["She’s still running about bare-foot.", "아직 맨발로 뛰어다니는 애지."], ["Don’t be afraid of bare-footed wenches—don’t despise them—they’re pearls!”", "맨발 계집애들을 무서워 말거라, 깔보지도 말고 말이야. 그런 애들이야말로 진주거든!\""]], [["And he kissed his hand with a smack.", "그러고는 자기 손에 쪽 소리 나게 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“To my thinking,”", "\"내 생각에는 말이야,\""], ["he revived at once, seeming to grow sober the instant he touched on his favorite topic.", "그는 좋아하는 화제를 건드리자마자 단번에 술이 깨는 듯 곧장 생기를 되찾았다."], ["“To my thinking ...", "\"내 생각에는……"], ["Ah, you boys!", "아, 이 애송이들!"], ["You children, little sucking-pigs, to my thinking ...", "이 어린것들, 젖먹이 새끼돼지들 같으니, 내 생각에는……"], ["I never thought a woman ugly in my life—that’s been my rule!", "나는 평생 단 한 번도 여자를 못생겼다고 여겨 본 적이 없어. 그게 내 신조였지!"], ["Can you understand that?", "너희가 그걸 이해할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["How could you understand it?", "어떻게 이해하겠어?"], ["You’ve milk in your veins, not blood.", "너희 핏줄엔 피가 아니라 젖이 흐르고 있잖느냐."], ["You’re not out of your shells yet.", "아직 알 껍데기도 못 벗은 것들이."], ["My rule has been that you can always find something devilishly interesting in every woman that you wouldn’t find in any other.", "내 신조인즉, 어떤 여자한테서든 다른 여자한테선 결코 찾을 수 없는, 무언가 지독히도 흥미로운 점을 언제든 찾아낼 수 있다는 거야."], ["Only, one must know how to find it, that’s the point!", "다만 그걸 찾아낼 줄 알아야 한다는 게 핵심이지!"], ["That’s a talent!", "그게 바로 재능이거든!"], ["To my mind there are no ugly women.", "내가 보기엔 못생긴 여자란 없어."], ["The very fact that she is a woman is half the battle ...", "여자라는 사실 그 자체가 이미 절반은 먹고 들어가는 거니까……."], ["but how could you understand that?", "하지만 너희가 그걸 어떻게 이해하겠느냐?"], ["Even in vieilles filles, even in them you may discover something that makes you simply wonder that men have been such fools as to let them grow old without noticing them.", "노처녀들한테서도, 심지어 그런 여자들한테서도, 사내놈들이 오죽 바보 같았으면 저런 여자를 못 알아보고 늙도록 내버려 뒀을까 싶어 그저 놀라게 되는 무언가를 발견할 수 있단 말이지."], ["Bare-footed girls or unattractive ones, you must take by surprise.", "맨발 계집애들이건 매력 없는 여자들이건, 너는 불시에 들이쳐서 사로잡아야 해."], ["Didn’t you know that?", "너 그것도 몰랐느냐?"], ["You must astound them till they’re fascinated, upset, ashamed that such a gentleman should fall in love with such a little slut.", "그런 여자들이 넋을 잃을 때까지, 어쩔 줄 몰라 할 때까지, 그런 보잘것없는 잡것을 신사 양반이 사랑한다는 게 부끄러워질 때까지 깜짝 놀라게 만들어야 하는 거야."], ["It’s a jolly good thing that there always are and will be masters and slaves in the world, so there always will be a little maid-of-all-work and her master, and you know, that’s all that’s needed for happiness.", "세상에 주인과 노예가 늘 있어 왔고 또 앞으로도 늘 있으리란 건 참으로 다행한 일이지. 그러니 잡일을 도맡아 하는 하녀 계집애와 그 주인이 언제나 있을 게고, 너도 알다시피 행복이라는 데 필요한 건 그게 전부거든."], ["Stay ...", "가만……"], ["listen, Alyosha, I always used to surprise your mother, but in a different way.", "들어 봐라, 알료샤, 나는 늘 네 어미를 깜짝 놀라게 했지, 하지만 다른 방식으로 말이야."], ["I paid no attention to her at all, but all at once, when the minute came, I’d be all devotion to her, crawl on my knees, kiss her feet, and I always, always—I remember it as though it were to-day—reduced her to that tinkling, quiet, nervous, queer little laugh.", "평소엔 그 사람한테 아무 관심도 안 두다가, 별안간 때가 무르익으면 나는 그 사람한테 온통 헌신적으로 굴면서 무릎으로 기어가 발에 입을 맞추곤 했어. 그러면 나는 늘, 언제나—마치 오늘 일처럼 생생하구나—그 사람한테서 그 딸랑거리는, 조용하면서도 신경질적인, 묘하고 자그마한 웃음을 끌어내곤 했지."], ["It was peculiar to her.", "그건 그 사람한테만 있는 독특한 웃음이었어."], ["I knew her attacks always used to begin like that.", "나는 그 사람의 발작이 늘 그렇게 시작된다는 걸 알고 있었거든."], ["The next day she would begin shrieking hysterically, and this little laugh was not a sign of delight, though it made a very good counterfeit.", "다음 날이면 그 사람은 히스테리를 부리며 비명을 질러 대기 시작했고, 그 자그마한 웃음은 기쁨의 표시가 아니었던 거야, 비록 기쁨인 척 감쪽같이 흉내를 내긴 했지만 말이지."], ["That’s the great thing, to know how to take every one.", "사람마다 어떻게 다뤄야 하는지를 아는 것, 그게 바로 가장 중요한 일이야."], ["Once Belyavsky—he was a handsome fellow, and rich—used to like to come here and hang about her—suddenly gave me a slap in the face in her presence.", "한번은 벨랴프스키라는 자가—그자는 잘생긴 데다 부자였는데, 여기 와서 그 사람 주위를 어슬렁거리길 좋아했지—난데없이 그 사람 앞에서 내 따귀를 한 대 갈겼어."], ["And she—such a mild sheep—why, I thought she would have knocked me down for that blow.", "그런데 그 사람이—그렇게 순한 양 같던 사람이—그 따귀 한 대 때문에 나를 때려눕히기라도 할 줄 알았다니까."], ["How she set on me!", "어찌나 나한테 달려들던지!"], ["‘You’re beaten, beaten now,’ she said.", "'당신은 맞았어요, 이제 맞은 거예요.' 그 사람이 그랬어."], ["‘You’ve taken a blow from him.", "'당신은 저 사람한테 따귀를 얻어맞았어요."], ["You have been trying to sell me to him,’ she said....", "당신은 나를 저 사람한테 팔아넘기려 했어요.' 그 사람이 그러더라니까……."], ["‘And how dared he strike you in my presence!", "'그런데 저자가 어떻게 감히 내 앞에서 당신을 때린단 말이에요!"], ["Don’t dare come near me again, never, never!", "다시는 내 곁에 얼씬도 말아요, 절대로, 절대로!"], ["Run at once, challenge him to a duel!’...", "당장 달려가서 저자한테 결투를 신청해요!' ……"], ["I took her to the monastery then to bring her to her senses.", "그래서 나는 그때 그 사람을 제정신 차리게 하려고 수도원으로 데려갔지."], ["The holy Fathers prayed her back to reason.", "거룩한 신부님들이 기도를 올려 그 사람을 정신 차리게 했어."], ["But I swear, by God, Alyosha, I never insulted the poor crazy girl!", "하지만 맹세코, 신께 맹세코, 알료샤, 나는 그 가엾고 미친 여자를 단 한 번도 모욕한 적이 없다!"], ["Only once, perhaps, in the first year; then she was very fond of praying.", "어쩌면 단 한 번, 첫해에 그랬을지 모르지. 그때 그 사람은 기도를 무척 좋아했어."], ["She used to keep the feasts of Our Lady particularly and used to turn me out of her room then.", "특히 성모님 축일들을 꼬박꼬박 지키면서, 그럴 때면 나를 자기 방에서 내쫓곤 했지."], ["I’ll knock that mysticism out of her, thought I!", "그래서 내가 저 신비주의를 두들겨 쫓아내 버려야겠다고 생각했어! '자,'"], ["‘Here,’ said I, ‘you see your holy image.", "내가 말했지. '당신의 거룩한 성상을 보시오."], ["Here it is.", "여기 있군."], ["Here I take it down.", "내가 이걸 끌어 내리겠소."], ["You believe it’s miraculous, but here, I’ll spit on it directly and nothing will happen to me for it!’...", "당신은 이게 기적을 일으킨다고 믿겠지만, 보시오, 내가 지금 당장 여기에 침을 뱉어도 나한텐 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 거요!' ……"], ["When she saw it, good Lord!", "그 사람이 그걸 보고는, 맙소사!"], ["I thought she would kill me.", "나는 그 사람이 나를 죽이는 줄 알았어."], ["But she only jumped up, wrung her hands, then suddenly hid her face in them, began trembling all over and fell on the floor ...", "그런데 그 사람은 그저 벌떡 일어나서 두 손을 비틀어 쥐더니, 별안간 그 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 온몸을 부들부들 떨다가 바닥에 쓰러져 버렸어……."], ["fell all of a heap.", "그대로 푹 고꾸라진 거지."], ["Alyosha, Alyosha, what’s the matter?”", "알료샤, 알료샤, 왜 그러느냐?\""]], [["The old man jumped up in alarm.", "노인이 깜짝 놀라 벌떡 일어났다."], ["From the time he had begun speaking about his mother, a change had gradually come over Alyosha’s face.", "그가 알료샤의 어머니 이야기를 꺼내기 시작한 순간부터 알료샤의 얼굴에 차츰 변화가 일고 있었던 것이다."], ["He flushed crimson, his eyes glowed, his lips quivered.", "그의 얼굴은 새빨갛게 달아오르고 두 눈은 이글거렸으며 입술이 떨렸다."], ["The old sot had gone spluttering on, noticing nothing, till the moment when something very strange happened to Alyosha.", "늙은 주정뱅이는 아무것도 알아채지 못한 채 침을 튀기며 계속 지껄여 댔는데, 마침내 알료샤에게 아주 이상한 일이 벌어지는 그 순간이 닥쳤다."], ["Precisely what he was describing in the crazy woman was suddenly repeated with Alyosha.", "그가 미친 여자에 대해 묘사하고 있던 바로 그 일이 별안간 알료샤한테서 똑같이 되풀이된 것이다."], ["He jumped up from his seat exactly as his mother was said to have done, wrung his hands, hid his face in them, and fell back in his chair, shaking all over in an hysterical paroxysm of sudden violent, silent weeping.", "알료샤는 제 어머니가 그랬다고 전해지던 것과 똑같이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 비틀어 쥐고 그 손에 얼굴을 파묻더니, 의자에 도로 쓰러져 갑작스레 격렬하고 소리 없는 흐느낌의 히스테리 발작에 휩싸여 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다."], ["His extraordinary resemblance to his mother particularly impressed the old man.", "그가 제 어머니와 유난히도 닮았다는 사실이 노인에게 특히 충격을 주었다."]], [["“Ivan, Ivan!", "\"이반, 이반!"], ["Water, quickly!", "물이다, 어서 물!"], ["It’s like her, exactly as she used to be then, his mother.", "똑 닮았어, 그때 저 애 어미 모습 그대로야."], ["Spurt some water on him from your mouth, that’s what I used to do to her.", "입에 물을 머금어서 저 애한테 뿜어 줘라, 내가 그 사람한테 해 주던 그대로 말이야."], ["He’s upset about his mother, his mother,”", "저 애가 제 어미 때문에, 제 어미 때문에 저렇게 상심한 거야.\""], ["he muttered to Ivan.", "그가 이반에게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“But she was my mother, too, I believe, his mother.", "\"하지만 그분은 제 어머니이기도 했던 것 같은데요, 저 애 어머니가 말입니다."], ["Was she not?”", "아니었습니까?\""], ["said Ivan, with uncontrolled anger and contempt.", "이반이 억누를 수 없는 분노와 경멸을 담아 말했다."], ["The old man shrank before his flashing eyes.", "노인은 번뜩이는 그의 눈빛 앞에서 움찔 움츠러들었다."], ["But something very strange had happened, though only for a second; it seemed really to have escaped the old man’s mind that Alyosha’s mother actually was the mother of Ivan too.", "그런데 아주 이상한 일이 벌어졌으니, 비록 한순간에 지나지 않았으되, 알료샤의 어머니가 실은 이반의 어머니이기도 하다는 사실이 정말로 노인의 머릿속에서 빠져나가 버린 듯했던 것이다."]], [["“Your mother?”", "\"네 어미라고?\""], ["he muttered, not understanding.", "그가 영문을 모르겠다는 듯 중얼거렸다."], ["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 소리냐?"], ["What mother are you talking about?", "무슨 어미를 두고 하는 말이야?"], ["Was she?...", "그 사람이?……"], ["Why, damn it!", "아니, 빌어먹을!"], ["of course she was yours too!", "물론 그 사람은 네 어미이기도 했지!"], ["Damn it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["My mind has never been so darkened before.", "내 정신이 이렇게까지 흐려진 적은 한 번도 없었는데."], ["Excuse me, why, I was thinking, Ivan....", "미안하구나, 글쎄 나는 생각하기를, 이반……"], ["He he he!”", "헤헤헤!\""], ["He stopped.", "그가 말을 멈췄다."], ["A broad, drunken, half-senseless grin overspread his face.", "넓고 술 취한, 반쯤 넋 나간 듯한 웃음이 그의 얼굴 가득 번졌다."]], [["At that moment a fearful noise and clamor was heard in the hall, there were violent shouts, the door was flung open, and Dmitri burst into the room.", "바로 그 순간 현관에서 무시무시한 소음과 아우성이 들려오더니, 거친 고함 소리가 일고 문이 활짝 젖혀지며 드미트리가 방 안으로 뛰어들었다."], ["The old man rushed to Ivan in terror.", "노인은 공포에 질려 이반에게 달려들었다."]], [["“He’ll kill me!", "\"저놈이 나를 죽일 거야!"], ["He’ll kill me!", "저놈이 나를 죽인다고!"], ["Don’t let him get at me!”", "저놈이 나한테 못 덤비게 해 다오!\""], ["he screamed, clinging to the skirt of Ivan’s coat.", "그가 이반의 외투 자락에 매달리며 비명을 질렀다."]], [["# Chapter IX. The Sensualists", "# Chapter IX. The Sensualists"]], [["Grigory and Smerdyakov ran into the room after Dmitri.", "그리고리와 스메르쟈코프가 드미트리의 뒤를 따라 방 안으로 뛰어 들어왔다."], ["They had been struggling with him in the passage, refusing to admit him, acting on instructions given them by Fyodor Pavlovitch some days before.", "두 사람은 복도에서 그와 몸싸움을 벌이며 그를 들이지 않으려 했으니, 이는 며칠 전 표도르 파블로비치에게서 받은 지시에 따른 것이었다."], ["Taking advantage of the fact that Dmitri stopped a moment on entering the room to look about him, Grigory ran round the table, closed the double doors on the opposite side of the room leading to the inner apartments, and stood before the closed doors, stretching wide his arms, prepared to defend the entrance, so to speak, with the last drop of his blood.", "드미트리가 방에 들어서며 잠시 멈추어 주위를 둘러보는 틈을 타, 그리고리는 탁자를 돌아 안채로 통하는 맞은편의 이중문을 닫고는, 두 팔을 한껏 벌린 채 닫힌 문 앞에 버티고 섰으니, 말하자면 마지막 핏방울 한 점까지 다해 그 입구를 지킬 태세였다."], ["Seeing this, Dmitri uttered a scream rather than a shout and rushed at Grigory.", "이 광경을 보자 드미트리는 외침이라기보다 비명에 가까운 소리를 지르며 그리고리에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Then she’s there! She’s hidden there!", "\"그렇다면 그 여자가 거기 있구나!"], ["Out of the way, scoundrel!”", "거기 숨어 있어! 비켜, 이 악당아!\""]], [["He tried to pull Grigory away, but the old servant pushed him back.", "그는 그리고리를 잡아끌어 떼어 내려 했으나, 늙은 하인이 그를 도로 밀쳐 냈다."], ["Beside himself with fury, Dmitri struck out, and hit Grigory with all his might.", "분노에 정신을 잃은 드미트리는 주먹을 휘둘러 온 힘을 다해 그리고리를 후려쳤다."], ["The old man fell like a log, and Dmitri, leaping over him, broke in the door.", "노인은 통나무처럼 쓰러졌고, 드미트리는 그를 뛰어넘어 문을 부수고 들어갔다."], ["Smerdyakov remained pale and trembling at the other end of the room, huddling close to Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "스메르쟈코프는 방 저쪽 끝에서 창백하게 떨며 표도르 파블로비치에게 바싹 붙어 있었다."]], [["“She’s here!”", "\"여기 있어!\""], ["shouted Dmitri.", "드미트리가 소리쳤다."], ["“I saw her turn towards the house just now, but I couldn’t catch her.", "\"방금 그 여자가 이 집 쪽으로 돌아서는 걸 봤단 말이야. 그런데 따라잡지 못했어."], ["Where is she?", "어디 있어?"], ["Where is she?”", "어디 있느냐고?\""]], [["That shout, “She’s here!”", "\"여기 있어!\""], ["produced an indescribable effect on Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "하는 그 외침은 표도르 파블로비치에게 형언할 수 없는 영향을 미쳤다."], ["All his terror left him.", "그를 사로잡고 있던 공포가 죄다 사라져 버린 것이다."]], [["“Hold him!", "\"붙잡아라!"], ["Hold him!”", "붙잡아!\""], ["he cried, and dashed after Dmitri.", "그는 소리치며 드미트리를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."], ["Meanwhile Grigory had got up from the floor, but still seemed stunned.", "그사이 그리고리는 바닥에서 일어나기는 했으나 아직도 정신이 멍한 듯했다."], ["Ivan and Alyosha ran after their father.", "이반과 알료샤가 아버지를 뒤따라 달려갔다."], ["In the third room something was heard to fall on the floor with a ringing crash: it was a large glass vase—not an expensive one—on a marble pedestal which Dmitri had upset as he ran past it.", "세 번째 방에서 무언가가 쨍그랑 요란한 소리를 내며 바닥에 떨어지는 소리가 들렸으니, 그것은 대리석 받침대 위에 놓인 큼직한 유리 화병이었는데—값나가는 것은 아니었다—드미트리가 그 곁을 지나치며 넘어뜨린 것이었다."]], [["“At him!”", "\"저놈을 잡아라!\""], ["shouted the old man.", "노인이 소리쳤다."], ["“Help!”", "\"사람 살려!\""]], [["Ivan and Alyosha caught the old man and were forcibly bringing him back.", "이반과 알료샤가 노인을 붙들어 억지로 도로 데려왔다."]], [["“Why do you run after him?", "\"왜 저 사람을 쫓아가세요?"], ["He’ll murder you outright,”", "단번에 아버지를 죽여 버릴 거란 말입니다.\""], ["Ivan cried wrathfully at his father.", "이반이 아버지를 향해 노기 띤 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Ivan!", "\"이반!"], ["Alyosha!", "알료샤!"], ["She must be here.", "그 여자가 틀림없이 여기 있어."], ["Grushenka’s here.", "그루셴카가 여기 있어."], ["He said he saw her himself, running.”", "저놈이 제 눈으로 그 여자가 뛰어가는 걸 봤다고 했잖느냐.\""]], [["He was choking.", "그는 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["He was not expecting Grushenka at the time, and the sudden news that she was here made him beside himself.", "그는 그때 그루셴카를 기다리고 있지 않았으므로, 그녀가 여기 있다는 난데없는 소식에 제정신을 잃고 말았다."], ["He was trembling all over.", "그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었다."], ["He seemed frantic.", "미친 사람 같았다."]], [["“But you’ve seen for yourself that she hasn’t come,”", "\"하지만 아버지도 직접 보셨잖습니까, 그 여자는 오지 않았어요.\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."]], [["“But she may have come by that other entrance.”", "\"하지만 저 다른 입구로 들어왔는지도 모르지.\""]], [["“You know that entrance is locked, and you have the key.”", "\"그 입구는 잠겨 있고, 열쇠는 아버지가 가지고 계시잖습니까.\""]], [["Dmitri suddenly reappeared in the drawing-room.", "드미트리가 갑자기 응접실에 다시 나타났다."], ["He had, of course, found the other entrance locked, and the key actually was in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s pocket.", "물론 그는 다른 입구가 잠겨 있는 것을 알아냈고, 열쇠는 실제로 표도르 파블로비치의 호주머니에 들어 있었던 것이다."], ["The windows of all the rooms were also closed, so Grushenka could not have come in anywhere nor have run out anywhere.", "모든 방의 창문도 죄다 닫혀 있었으니, 그루셴카가 어디로도 들어왔을 리 없었고 어디로도 빠져나갔을 리 없었다."]], [["“Hold him!”", "\"저놈을 붙잡아라!\""], ["shrieked Fyodor Pavlovitch, as soon as he saw him again.", "그를 다시 보자마자 표도르 파블로비치가 새된 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“He’s been stealing money in my bedroom.”", "\"저놈이 내 침실에서 돈을 훔쳤다.\""], ["And tearing himself from Ivan he rushed again at Dmitri.", "그러고는 이반에게서 몸을 뿌리치며 다시 드미트리에게 달려들었다."], ["But Dmitri threw up both hands and suddenly clutched the old man by the two tufts of hair that remained on his temples, tugged at them, and flung him with a crash on the floor.", "그러나 드미트리는 두 손을 번쩍 쳐들더니 노인의 양쪽 관자놀이에 남아 있던 두 줌의 머리털을 와락 움켜쥐고는 잡아당겨 쾅 소리가 나도록 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["He kicked him two or three times with his heel in the face.", "그는 발뒤꿈치로 노인의 얼굴을 두세 차례 걷어찼다."], ["The old man moaned shrilly.", "노인은 날카롭게 신음했다."], ["Ivan, though not so strong as Dmitri, threw his arms round him, and with all his might pulled him away.", "이반은 드미트리만큼 힘이 세지는 않았으나, 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 온 힘을 다해 떼어 냈다."], ["Alyosha helped him with his slender strength, holding Dmitri in front.", "알료샤도 그 가냘픈 힘이나마 보태어 드미트리를 앞에서 붙들었다."]], [["“Madman!", "\"미친놈아!"], ["You’ve killed him!”", "아버지를 죽였잖아!\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."]], [["“Serve him right!”", "\"그래도 싸지!\""], ["shouted Dmitri breathlessly.", "드미트리가 숨을 헐떡이며 소리쳤다."], ["“If I haven’t killed him, I’ll come again and kill him.", "\"이번에 죽이지 못했다면, 다시 와서 죽이고 말 테다."], ["You can’t protect him!”", "너희가 저 사람을 지켜낼 순 없어!\""]], [["“Dmitri!", "\"드미트리 형님!"], ["Go away at once!”", "당장 나가요!\""], ["cried Alyosha commandingly.", "알료샤가 명령하듯 외쳤다."]], [["“Alexey!", "\"알렉세이!"], ["You tell me.", "네가 말해 다오."], ["It’s only you I can believe; was she here just now, or not?", "내가 믿을 수 있는 건 너밖에 없으니까. 방금 그 여자가 여기 있었느냐, 없었느냐?"], ["I saw her myself creeping this way by the fence from the lane.", "내 눈으로 그 여자가 골목길에서 울타리를 따라 이쪽으로 슬그머니 오는 걸 봤단 말이다."], ["I shouted, she ran away.”", "내가 소리치니까 도망쳤어.\""]], [["“I swear she’s not been here, and no one expected her.”", "\"맹세코 그 여자는 여기 온 적 없고, 아무도 그 여자를 기다리지 않았어요.\""]], [["“But I saw her....", "\"하지만 내가 봤다니까……"], ["So she must ...", "그러니 틀림없이……"], ["I’ll find out at once where she is....", "당장 그 여자가 어디 있는지 알아내고 말겠다……"], ["Good-by, Alexey!", "잘 있어라, 알렉세이!"], ["Not a word to Æsop about the money now.", "이젠 저 이솝한테 돈 얘긴 한마디도 하지 마라."], ["But go to Katerina Ivanovna at once and be sure to say, ‘He sends his compliments to you!’ Compliments, his compliments!", "하지만 카체리나 이바노브나한테는 당장 가서, 꼭 이렇게 전해라. '그가 당신에게 안부를 전합니다!'"], ["Just compliments and farewell!", "안부, 안부를 전한다고! 그저 안부와 작별 인사뿐이다!"], ["Describe the scene to her.”", "그 여자한테 이 광경을 그대로 일러 주거라.\""]], [["Meanwhile Ivan and Grigory had raised the old man and seated him in an arm-chair.", "그사이 이반과 그리고리는 노인을 부축해 일으켜 안락의자에 앉혔다."], ["His face was covered with blood, but he was conscious and listened greedily to Dmitri’s cries.", "그의 얼굴은 피로 뒤덮여 있었으나, 그는 의식이 있었고 드미트리의 외침에 게걸스레 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["He was still fancying that Grushenka really was somewhere in the house.", "그는 여전히 그루셴카가 정말로 집 어딘가에 있다고 상상하고 있었다."], ["Dmitri looked at him with hatred as he went out.", "드미트리는 방을 나서면서 그를 증오에 찬 눈으로 노려보았다."]], [["“I don’t repent shedding your blood!”", "\"네 피를 흘리게 한 걸 후회하지 않는다!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Beware, old man, beware of your dream, for I have my dream, too.", "\"조심해라, 늙은이여, 네 꿈을 조심해라. 나에게도 내 꿈이 있으니까."], ["I curse you, and disown you altogether.”", "나는 너를 저주하며, 너를 아주 의절하겠다.\""]], [["He ran out of the room.", "그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“She’s here.", "\"그 여자가 여기 있어."], ["She must be here.", "틀림없이 여기 있어."], ["Smerdyakov!", "스메르쟈코프!"], ["Smerdyakov!”", "스메르쟈코프!\""], ["the old man wheezed, scarcely audibly, beckoning to him with his finger.", "노인이 가까스로 들릴락 말락 한 소리로 쌕쌕거리며 손가락으로 그를 불렀다."]], [["“No, she’s not here, you old lunatic!”", "\"아니에요, 여기 없어요, 이 노망난 늙은이야!\""], ["Ivan shouted at him angrily.", "이반이 그에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Here, he’s fainting!", "\"아니, 기절하시는데!"], ["Water!", "물!"], ["A towel!", "수건!"], ["Make haste, Smerdyakov!”", "어서, 스메르쟈코프!\""]], [["Smerdyakov ran for water.", "스메르쟈코프가 물을 가지러 달려갔다."], ["At last they got the old man undressed, and put him to bed.", "마침내 그들은 노인의 옷을 벗겨 침대에 눕혔다."], ["They wrapped a wet towel round his head.", "그들은 그의 머리에 젖은 수건을 둘러 감았다."], ["Exhausted by the brandy, by his violent emotion, and the blows he had received, he shut his eyes and fell asleep as soon as his head touched the pillow.", "브랜디에, 격렬한 흥분에, 거기에 얻어맞은 매질까지 겹쳐 기진맥진한 그는 눈을 감았고, 머리가 베개에 닿자마자 잠들어 버렸다."], ["Ivan and Alyosha went back to the drawing-room.", "이반과 알료샤는 응접실로 돌아갔다."], ["Smerdyakov removed the fragments of the broken vase, while Grigory stood by the table looking gloomily at the floor.", "스메르쟈코프는 깨진 화병 조각을 치웠고, 그리고리는 탁자 곁에 서서 음울한 눈으로 바닥을 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["“Shouldn’t you put a wet bandage on your head and go to bed, too?”", "\"당신도 머리에 젖은 붕대를 감고 누우셔야 하지 않겠어요?\""], ["Alyosha said to him.", "알료샤가 그에게 말했다."], ["“We’ll look after him.", "\"아버지는 우리가 돌볼게요."], ["My brother gave you a terrible blow—on the head.”", "형님이 당신을—머리를 호되게 때렸잖아요.\""]], [["“He’s insulted me!”", "\"그분이 저를 모욕했습니다!\""], ["Grigory articulated gloomily and distinctly.", "그리고리가 음울하면서도 또렷하게 내뱉었다."]], [["“He’s ‘insulted’ his father, not only you,”", "\"그가 '모욕'한 건 자네뿐 아니라 제 아버지일세.\""], ["observed Ivan with a forced smile.", "이반이 억지웃음을 지으며 한마디 했다."]], [["“I used to wash him in his tub.", "\"제가 그분을 목욕통에 넣고 씻겨 드리곤 했지요."], ["He’s insulted me,”", "그런 그분이 저를 모욕했습니다.\""], ["repeated Grigory.", "그리고리가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Damn it all, if I hadn’t pulled him away perhaps he’d have murdered him.", "\"빌어먹을, 내가 떼어 놓지 않았더라면 어쩌면 그놈이 아버지를 죽여 버렸을지도 몰라."], ["It wouldn’t take much to do for Æsop, would it?”", "이솝 하나 해치우는 데야 그리 힘들 것도 없지 않겠나?\""], ["whispered Ivan to Alyosha.", "이반이 알료샤에게 속삭였다."]], [["“God forbid!”", "\"하느님 맙소사!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“Why should He forbid?”", "\"하느님이 왜 막으셔야 하는데?\""], ["Ivan went on in the same whisper, with a malignant grimace.", "이반이 여전히 속삭이는 목소리로, 악의에 찬 표정을 지으며 말을 이었다."], ["“One reptile will devour the other.", "\"한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는 거지."], ["And serve them both right, too.”", "그래야 둘 다 마땅한 벌을 받는 거고.\""]], [["Alyosha shuddered.", "알료샤는 몸서리를 쳤다."]], [["“Of course I won’t let him be murdered as I didn’t just now.", "\"물론 방금처럼 나도 아버지가 살해당하도록 내버려 두진 않을 거다."], ["Stay here, Alyosha, I’ll go for a turn in the yard.", "여기 있어라, 알료샤, 난 마당에 좀 나가 거닐어야겠다."], ["My head’s begun to ache.”", "머리가 아프기 시작했어.\""]], [["Alyosha went to his father’s bedroom and sat by his bedside behind the screen for about an hour.", "알료샤는 아버지의 침실로 가서 칸막이 뒤 침대맡에 한 시간쯤 앉아 있었다."], ["The old man suddenly opened his eyes and gazed for a long while at Alyosha, evidently remembering and meditating.", "노인이 갑자기 눈을 뜨더니 알료샤를 한참이나 바라보았는데, 분명 무언가를 기억해 내며 곰곰이 생각하는 듯했다."], ["All at once his face betrayed extraordinary excitement.", "별안간 그의 얼굴에 예사롭지 않은 흥분이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Alyosha,”", "\"알료샤,\""], ["he whispered apprehensively, “where’s Ivan?”", "그가 불안스레 속삭였다. \"이반은 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["“In the yard.", "\"마당에요."], ["He’s got a headache.", "머리가 아프대요."], ["He’s on the watch.”", "망을 보고 있어요.\""]], [["“Give me that looking-glass.", "\"저 거울을 다오."], ["It stands over there.", "저기 놓여 있는 거 말이다."], ["Give it me.”", "그걸 이리 다오.\""]], [["Alyosha gave him a little round folding looking-glass which stood on the chest of drawers.", "알료샤는 서랍장 위에 놓인 작고 둥근 접이식 거울을 그에게 건네주었다."], ["The old man looked at himself in it; his nose was considerably swollen, and on the left side of his forehead there was a rather large crimson bruise.", "노인은 그 속에 비친 제 모습을 들여다보았다. 코가 상당히 부어 있었고, 이마 왼편에는 제법 큼직한 새빨간 멍이 들어 있었다."]], [["“What does Ivan say?", "\"이반이 뭐라고 하더냐?"], ["Alyosha, my dear, my only son, I’m afraid of Ivan.", "알료샤, 내 사랑하는, 내 하나뿐인 아들아, 나는 이반이 무섭구나."], ["I’m more afraid of Ivan than the other.", "다른 놈보다 이반이 더 무서워."], ["You’re the only one I’m not afraid of....”", "내가 무서워하지 않는 건 너 하나뿐이야……\""]], [["“Don’t be afraid of Ivan either.", "\"이반도 무서워하지 마세요."], ["He is angry, but he’ll defend you.”", "화는 났지만, 그래도 아버지를 지켜 드릴 거예요.\""]], [["“Alyosha, and what of the other?", "\"알료샤, 그럼 그 다른 놈은?"], ["He’s run to Grushenka.", "그놈이 그루셴카한테 달려갔어."], ["My angel, tell me the truth, was she here just now or not?”", "내 천사야, 진실을 말해 다오, 그 여자가 방금 여기 있었느냐 없었느냐?\""]], [["“No one has seen her.", "\"아무도 그 여자를 보지 못했어요."], ["It was a mistake.", "착각이었어요."], ["She has not been here.”", "그 여자는 여기 온 적 없어요.\""]], [["“You know Mitya wants to marry her, to marry her.”", "\"미탸가 그 여자랑 결혼하려는 거 알지, 그 여자랑 결혼하려고.\""]], [["“She won’t marry him.”", "\"그 여자는 형님과 결혼하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“She won’t.", "\"안 하지."], ["She won’t.", "안 하고말고."], ["She won’t.", "안 하지."], ["She won’t on any account!”", "무슨 일이 있어도 안 할 거야!\""]], [["The old man fairly fluttered with joy, as though nothing more comforting could have been said to him.", "노인은 마치 그보다 더 위안이 되는 말은 없으리라는 듯 기뻐 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["In his delight he seized Alyosha’s hand and pressed it warmly to his heart.", "그는 환희에 차서 알료샤의 손을 붙잡아 따뜻하게 제 가슴에 꼭 눌렀다."], ["Tears positively glittered in his eyes.", "두 눈에는 정말로 눈물이 반짝였다."]], [["“That image of the Mother of God of which I was telling you just now,”", "\"방금 너한테 얘기했던 그 성모 마리아의 성상 말이다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Take it home and keep it for yourself.", "\"그걸 집으로 가져가서 네가 간직하거라."], ["And I’ll let you go back to the monastery....", "그리고 수도원으로 돌아가도록 해 주마……"], ["I was joking this morning, don’t be angry with me.", "오늘 아침엔 농담을 한 거니, 나한테 화내지 마라."], ["My head aches, Alyosha....", "머리가 아프구나, 알료샤……"], ["Alyosha, comfort my heart.", "알료샤, 내 마음을 위로해 다오."], ["Be an angel and tell me the truth!”", "천사가 되어 진실을 말해 다오!\""]], [["“You’re still asking whether she has been here or not?”", "\"아직도 그 여자가 여기 있었는지 없었는지를 물으시는 거예요?\""], ["Alyosha said sorrowfully.", "알료샤가 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“No, no, no.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니다. 너를 믿어."], ["I believe you.", "내가 뭘 바라는지 말해 주마. 네가 직접 그루셴카한테 가거라, 아니면 어떻게든 그 여자를 만나거라."], ["I’ll tell you what it is: you go to Grushenka yourself, or see her somehow; make haste and ask her; see for yourself, which she means to choose, him or me.", "어서 서둘러 물어봐 다오. 네 눈으로 직접 확인해라, 그 여자가 그놈과 나 둘 중에 누굴 택할 작정인지."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["What?", "어떠냐?"], ["Can you?”", "그래 줄 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“If I see her I’ll ask her,”", "\"제가 그 여자를 만나면 물어보겠어요.\""], ["Alyosha muttered, embarrassed.", "알료샤가 당황한 듯 우물거렸다."]], [["“No, she won’t tell you,”", "\"아니, 그 여잔 너한테 말 안 할 거다.\""], ["the old man interrupted, “she’s a rogue.", "노인이 말을 가로막았다. \"그 여잔 교활한 계집이야."], ["She’ll begin kissing you and say that it’s you she wants.", "너한테 입을 맞추기 시작하면서 자기가 원하는 건 너라고 할 거다."], ["She’s a deceitful, shameless hussy.", "그 여잔 거짓되고 뻔뻔스러운 화냥년이야."], ["You mustn’t go to her, you mustn’t!”", "넌 그 여자한테 가면 안 돼, 가면 안 된다!\""]], [["“No, father, and it wouldn’t be suitable, it wouldn’t be right at all.”", "\"안 가요, 아버지, 그건 적절하지도 않고, 도무지 옳은 일이 아니에요.\""]], [["“Where was he sending you just now?", "\"방금 그놈이 너를 어디로 보내려던 거냐?"], ["He shouted ‘Go’ as he ran away.”", "달아나면서 '가라'고 소리치던데.\""]], [["“To Katerina Ivanovna.”", "\"카체리나 이바노브나한테요.\""]], [["“For money?", "\"돈 때문에?"], ["To ask her for money?”", "그 여자한테 돈을 달라고 하려고?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아뇨."], ["Not for money.”", "돈 때문은 아니에요.\""]], [["“He’s no money; not a farthing.", "\"그놈은 돈이 없어. 한 푼도 없지."], ["I’ll settle down for the night, and think things over, and you can go.", "난 자리에 누워 밤을 보내며 이런저런 일을 곰곰이 따져 봐야겠다. 너는 가도 좋다."], ["Perhaps you’ll meet her....", "어쩌면 그 여자를 만나게 될지도 모르지……"], ["Only be sure to come to me to-morrow in the morning.", "다만 내일 아침에는 꼭 나한테 와다오."], ["Be sure to.", "꼭 와야 한다."], ["I have a word to say to you to-morrow.", "내일 너한테 할 말이 있어."], ["Will you come?”", "와 주겠지?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“When you come, pretend you’ve come of your own accord to ask after me.", "\"올 때는, 네가 제 발로 내 안부를 물으러 온 척해라."], ["Don’t tell any one I told you to.", "내가 너한테 오라고 했다는 말은 아무한테도 하지 마라."], ["Don’t say a word to Ivan.”", "이반한테는 한마디도 하지 말고.\""]], [["“Very well.”", "\"알았어요.\""]], [["“Good-by, my angel.", "\"잘 가거라, 내 천사야."], ["You stood up for me, just now.", "너는 방금 나를 두둔해 줬지."], ["I shall never forget it.", "결코 잊지 않으마."], ["I’ve a word to say to you to-morrow—but I must think about it.”", "내일 너한테 할 말이 있다만—그건 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠다.\""]], [["“And how do you feel now?”", "\"지금 기분은 좀 어떠세요?\""]], [["“I shall get up to-morrow and go out, perfectly well, perfectly well!”", "\"내일이면 일어나서 나갈 거다, 멀쩡하게, 아주 멀쩡하게 말이야!\""]], [["Crossing the yard Alyosha found Ivan sitting on the bench at the gateway.", "마당을 가로지르던 알료샤는 대문께 벤치에 앉아 있는 이반을 발견했다."], ["He was sitting writing something in pencil in his note-book.", "그는 수첩에 연필로 무언가를 적고 있었다."], ["Alyosha told Ivan that their father had waked up, was conscious, and had let him go back to sleep at the monastery.", "알료샤는 이반에게 아버지가 깨어나 의식을 차렸고, 자기에게 수도원으로 돌아가 자도 좋다고 허락했다고 전했다."]], [["“Alyosha, I should be very glad to meet you to-morrow morning,”", "\"알료샤, 내일 아침에 너를 만나면 정말 기쁘겠구나.\""], ["said Ivan cordially, standing up.", "이반이 일어서며 다정하게 말했다."], ["His cordiality was a complete surprise to Alyosha.", "그 다정함은 알료샤에게 전혀 뜻밖이었다."]], [["“I shall be at the Hohlakovs’ to-morrow,”", "\"내일은 호흘라코바 부인 댁에 갈 거예요.\""], ["answered Alyosha, “I may be at Katerina Ivanovna’s, too, if I don’t find her now.”", "알료샤가 대답했다. \"지금 카체리나 이바노브나를 만나지 못하면, 그분 댁에도 갈지 몰라요.\""]], [["“But you’re going to her now, anyway?", "\"하지만 어쨌든 지금 그분한테 가는 거잖아?"], ["For that ‘compliments and farewell,’”", "그 '안부와 작별 인사' 때문에 말이야.\""], ["said Ivan smiling.", "이반이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Alyosha was disconcerted.", "알료샤는 당황했다."]], [["“I think I quite understand his exclamations just now, and part of what went before.", "\"방금 그가 외쳐 대던 말도, 그 전에 있었던 일의 일부도 내가 충분히 이해할 것 같다."], ["Dmitri has asked you to go to her and say that he—well, in fact—takes his leave of her?”", "드미트리가 너한테 그 여자한테 가서 자기가—그러니까 결국—그 여자에게 작별을 고한다고 전하라고 부탁한 거지?\""]], [["“Brother, how will all this horror end between father and Dmitri?”", "\"형님, 아버지하고 드미트리 형님 사이의 이 끔찍한 일이 대체 어떻게 끝날까요?\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 부르짖었다."]], [["“One can’t tell for certain.", "\"확실히는 알 수 없지."], ["Perhaps in nothing: it may all fizzle out.", "어쩌면 아무 일도 없을지 몰라. 모두 흐지부지될 수도 있고."], ["That woman is a beast.", "그 여자는 짐승 같은 계집이야."], ["In any case we must keep the old man indoors and not let Dmitri in the house.”", "어쨌든 우린 아버지를 집 안에 붙들어 두고 드미트리를 집에 들이지 말아야 해.\""]], [["“Brother, let me ask one thing more: has any man a right to look at other men and decide which is worthy to live?”", "\"형님, 한 가지만 더 묻겠어요. 어떤 사람이 다른 사람들을 바라보면서 누가 살 만한 가치가 있는지 결정할 권리가 있을까요?\""]], [["“Why bring in the question of worth?", "\"왜 가치 문제를 끌어들이지?"], ["The matter is most often decided in men’s hearts on other grounds much more natural.", "그런 일은 대개 사람들 마음속에서 훨씬 더 자연스러운 다른 근거로 결정되는 법이야."], ["And as for rights—who has not the right to wish?”", "권리로 말하자면—바랄 권리가 없는 사람이 어디 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“Not for another man’s death?”", "\"다른 사람의 죽음을 바랄 권리도요?\""]], [["“What even if for another man’s death?", "\"다른 사람의 죽음을 바란들 또 어떠냐?"], ["Why lie to oneself since all men live so and perhaps cannot help living so.", "모든 사람이 다 그렇게 살고, 어쩌면 그렇게 살 수밖에 없는데 굳이 자신을 속일 까닭이 뭐야?"], ["Are you referring to what I said just now—that one reptile will devour the other?", "너 방금 내가 한 말을 두고 그러는 거냐—한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는다고 한 말 말이다?"], ["In that case let me ask you, do you think me like Dmitri capable of shedding Æsop’s blood, murdering him, eh?”", "그렇다면 너한테 묻겠다, 너는 내가 드미트리처럼 이솝의 피를 흘리게 하고, 그를 죽일 수 있는 인간이라고 생각하느냐, 응?\""]], [["“What are you saying, Ivan?", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 이반 형님?"], ["Such an idea never crossed my mind.", "그런 생각은 한 번도 머리에 떠오른 적 없어요."], ["I don’t think Dmitri is capable of it, either.”", "드미트리 형님도 그럴 수 있는 사람이라고 생각하지 않아요.\""]], [["“Thanks, if only for that,”", "\"고맙구나, 그것만으로도.\""], ["smiled Ivan.", "이반이 미소 지었다."], ["“Be sure, I should always defend him.", "\"분명히 말하지만, 난 언제든 그를 두둔할 거다."], ["But in my wishes I reserve myself full latitude in this case.", "하지만 내가 무엇을 바라느냐 하는 점에서는, 이 경우 나 자신에게 완전한 여지를 남겨 두겠다."], ["Good-by till to-morrow.", "내일까지 잘 있어라."], ["Don’t condemn me, and don’t look on me as a villain,”", "나를 단죄하지 말고, 나를 악당으로 보지도 마라.\""], ["he added with a smile.", "그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["They shook hands warmly as they had never done before.", "두 사람은 일찍이 없던 일로 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다."], ["Alyosha felt that his brother had taken the first step towards him, and that he had certainly done this with some definite motive.", "알료샤는 형이 자기를 향해 첫걸음을 내디뎠음을, 그리고 그가 분명 어떤 뚜렷한 동기를 가지고 그렇게 했음을 느꼈다."]], [["# Chapter X. Both Together", "# Chapter X. Both Together"]], [["Alyosha left his father’s house feeling even more exhausted and dejected in spirit than when he had entered it.", "알료샤는 아버지의 집을 나설 때, 들어설 때보다 한층 더 지치고 마음마저 침울해진 상태였다."], ["His mind too seemed shattered and unhinged, while he felt that he was afraid to put together the disjointed fragments and form a general idea from all the agonizing and conflicting experiences of the day.", "그의 머릿속도 산산이 부서져 갈피를 잡지 못하는 것 같았고, 그날 겪은 온갖 괴롭고 모순된 경험의 흩어진 조각들을 한데 모아 어떤 전체적인 그림을 그려 보는 것조차 두렵게 느껴졌다."], ["He felt something bordering upon despair, which he had never known till then.", "그는 그때까지 한 번도 알지 못했던, 절망에 가까운 무언가를 느꼈다."], ["Towering like a mountain above all the rest stood the fatal, insoluble question: How would things end between his father and his brother Dmitri with this terrible woman?", "그 모든 것 위로 산처럼 우뚝 솟아 있는 것은 풀리지 않는 운명의 물음이었다. 아버지와 형 드미트리 사이의 일이 그 무서운 여자를 둘러싸고 대체 어떻게 끝날 것인가?"], ["Now he had himself been a witness of it, he had been present and seen them face to face.", "이제 그는 직접 그 광경의 증인이 되었고, 그 자리에 있었으며, 두 사람이 마주 보고 서 있는 것을 제 눈으로 보았다."], ["Yet only his brother Dmitri could be made unhappy, terribly, completely unhappy: there was trouble awaiting him.", "그러나 불행해질 수 있는 사람은, 무섭게, 완전히 불행해질 수 있는 사람은 오직 형 드미트리뿐이었다."], ["It appeared too that there were other people concerned, far more so than Alyosha could have supposed before.", "그에게는 닥쳐올 재앙이 기다리고 있었다. 게다가 알료샤가 미처 짐작하지 못했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 사람들이 이 일에 얽혀 있는 듯했다."], ["There was something positively mysterious in it, too.", "그 속에는 무언가 분명히 수수께끼 같은 것도 있었다."], ["Ivan had made a step towards him, which was what Alyosha had been long desiring.", "이반이 그를 향해 한 발짝 다가서 주었는데, 그것은 알료샤가 오랫동안 바라 마지않던 일이었다."], ["Yet now he felt for some reason that he was frightened at it.", "그런데도 지금 그는 무슨 까닭에선지 그 일이 두렵게 느껴졌다."], ["And these women?", "그리고 이 여자들은?"], ["Strange to say, that morning he had set out for Katerina Ivanovna’s in the greatest embarrassment; now he felt nothing of the kind.", "이상한 말이지만, 그날 아침 그는 더없이 큰 거북함을 안고 카테리나 이바노브나의 집으로 향했었다. 그런데 지금은 그런 마음이 전혀 들지 않았다."], ["On the contrary, he was hastening there as though expecting to find guidance from her.", "오히려 그는 마치 그녀에게서 어떤 인도를 받기를 기대하기라도 하듯 그곳으로 서둘러 가고 있었다."], ["Yet to give her this message was obviously more difficult than before.", "그러나 그녀에게 이 전갈을 전하는 일은 분명 이전보다 더 어려웠다."], ["The matter of the three thousand was decided irrevocably, and Dmitri, feeling himself dishonored and losing his last hope, might sink to any depth.", "삼천 루블 문제는 돌이킬 수 없이 결정되어 버렸고, 명예를 잃었다고 느끼며 마지막 희망마저 잃은 드미트리는 어떤 나락으로든 떨어질 수 있었다."], ["He had, moreover, told him to describe to Katerina Ivanovna the scene which had just taken place with his father.", "게다가 그는 알료샤에게 방금 아버지와 벌어진 그 장면을 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그대로 전하라고 일러 두기까지 했다."]], [["It was by now seven o’clock, and it was getting dark as Alyosha entered the very spacious and convenient house in the High Street occupied by Katerina Ivanovna.", "어느덧 일곱 시였고, 알료샤가 카테리나 이바노브나가 사는, 큰길가에 자리한 매우 널찍하고 편리한 집으로 들어설 무렵에는 날이 어두워지고 있었다."], ["Alyosha knew that she lived with two aunts.", "알료샤는 그녀가 두 고모와 함께 산다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["One of them, a woman of little education, was that aunt of her half-sister Agafya Ivanovna who had looked after her in her father’s house when she came from boarding-school.", "그중 한 사람은 별로 배우지 못한 여자로, 그녀의 이복 언니 아가피야 이바노브나의 고모였는데, 그녀가 기숙학교를 마치고 돌아왔을 때 친정에서 그녀를 돌봐 준 사람이었다."], ["The other aunt was a Moscow lady of style and consequence, though in straitened circumstances.", "또 한 사람은 모스크바 출신의 격식 있고 명망 있는 부인이었으나 형편은 쪼들리는 처지였다."], ["It was said that they both gave way in everything to Katerina Ivanovna, and that she only kept them with her as chaperons.", "들리는 말로는 두 고모가 매사에 카테리나 이바노브나의 뜻을 따랐으며, 그녀가 다만 보호자 격으로 두 사람을 곁에 두고 있을 뿐이라고 했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna herself gave way to no one but her benefactress, the general’s widow, who had been kept by illness in Moscow, and to whom she was obliged to write twice a week a full account of all her doings.", "카테리나 이바노브나 자신은 그 누구의 뜻에도 굽히지 않았으되, 오직 자신의 후원자인 장군 부인에게만은 예외였으니, 그 부인은 병으로 모스크바에 발이 묶여 있었고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 일주일에 두 번씩 자신의 모든 행적을 빠짐없이 적어 편지로 보내야 했다."]], [["When Alyosha entered the hall and asked the maid who opened the door to him to take his name up, it was evident that they were already aware of his arrival.", "알료샤가 현관으로 들어서서 문을 열어 준 하녀에게 자기 이름을 위층에 전해 달라고 청했을 때, 사람들이 이미 그의 도착을 알고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["Possibly he had been noticed from the window.", "어쩌면 창문으로 그를 발견했는지도 몰랐다."], ["At least, Alyosha heard a noise, caught the sound of flying footsteps and rustling skirts.", "적어도 알료샤는 무언가 부산스러운 소리를, 황급히 내닫는 발소리와 치맛자락 스치는 소리를 들었다."], ["Two or three women, perhaps, had run out of the room.", "어쩌면 두세 명의 여자가 방에서 뛰쳐나갔는지도 몰랐다."]], [["Alyosha thought it strange that his arrival should cause such excitement.", "알료샤는 자신의 도착이 이런 소동을 일으킨다는 것이 이상하다고 생각했다."], ["He was conducted however to the drawing-room at once.", "그러나 그는 곧장 응접실로 안내되었다."], ["It was a large room, elegantly and amply furnished, not at all in provincial style.", "그곳은 넓은 방으로, 우아하고 풍성하게 꾸며져 있어 도무지 지방 도시풍이라고는 할 수 없었다."], ["There were many sofas, lounges, settees, big and little tables.", "소파며 안락의자며 긴 의자가 여러 개 있었고, 크고 작은 탁자들이 놓여 있었다."], ["There were pictures on the walls, vases and lamps on the tables, masses of flowers, and even an aquarium in the window.", "벽에는 그림이 걸려 있었고, 탁자 위에는 꽃병과 등잔이 있었으며, 꽃이 무더기로 꽂혀 있었고, 창가에는 수족관까지 있었다."], ["It was twilight and rather dark.", "황혼 무렵이라 방 안은 제법 어두웠다."], ["Alyosha made out a silk mantle thrown down on the sofa, where people had evidently just been sitting; and on a table in front of the sofa were two unfinished cups of chocolate, cakes, a glass saucer with blue raisins, and another with sweetmeats.", "알료샤는 소파 위에 던져진 비단 망토를 알아보았는데, 분명 방금까지 사람들이 거기 앉아 있었던 모양이었다. 그리고 소파 앞 탁자 위에는 다 마시지 않은 초콜릿 잔 두 개와 과자, 푸른 건포도가 담긴 유리 접시, 그리고 사탕 과자가 담긴 또 하나의 접시가 놓여 있었다."], ["Alyosha saw that he had interrupted visitors, and frowned.", "알료샤는 자기가 손님이 와 있던 자리를 방해했음을 깨닫고 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."], ["But at that instant the portière was raised, and with rapid, hurrying footsteps Katerina Ivanovna came in, holding out both hands to Alyosha with a radiant smile of delight.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 휘장이 걷히더니, 빠르고 다급한 발걸음으로 카테리나 이바노브나가 들어와, 기쁨으로 환하게 빛나는 미소를 띤 채 알료샤를 향해 두 손을 내밀었다."], ["At the same instant a servant brought in two lighted candles and set them on the table.", "그와 동시에 하인이 불 켜진 양초 두 개를 가져와 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["“Thank God!", "\"오, 다행이에요!"], ["At last you have come too!", "마침내 당신도 와 주셨군요!"], ["I’ve been simply praying for you all day!", "온종일 당신을 위해 기도하다시피 했답니다!"], ["Sit down.”", "앉으세요.\""]], [["Alyosha had been struck by Katerina Ivanovna’s beauty when, three weeks before, Dmitri had first brought him, at Katerina Ivanovna’s special request, to be introduced to her.", "알료샤는 세 주 전, 드미트리가 카테리나 이바노브나의 특별한 청에 따라 그를 처음으로 데리고 와 그녀에게 소개했을 때, 그녀의 아름다움에 충격을 받았었다."], ["There had been no conversation between them at that interview, however.", "그러나 그 첫 만남에서 두 사람 사이에 이렇다 할 대화는 없었다."], ["Supposing Alyosha to be very shy, Katerina Ivanovna had talked all the time to Dmitri to spare him.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 알료샤를 몹시 수줍어하는 사람으로 여기고는, 그가 어색하지 않도록 줄곧 드미트리하고만 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Alyosha had been silent, but he had seen a great deal very clearly.", "알료샤는 말이 없었으나, 아주 많은 것을 또렷이 보아 두었다."], ["He was struck by the imperiousness, proud ease, and self-confidence of the haughty girl.", "그는 그 도도한 처녀의 위압적인 태도와 거만한 여유로움, 그리고 자신감에 충격을 받았다."], ["And all that was certain, Alyosha felt that he was not exaggerating it.", "그리고 그 모든 것은 분명한 사실이었으니, 알료샤는 자기가 그것을 과장하고 있는 것이 아니라고 느꼈다."], ["He thought her great glowing black eyes were very fine, especially with her pale, even rather sallow, longish face.", "그는 그녀의 크고 이글거리는 검은 두 눈이 매우 아름답다고 여겼는데, 특히 창백하다 못해 다소 누르스름하기까지 한 갸름한 얼굴과 어우러져 더욱 그러했다."], ["But in those eyes and in the lines of her exquisite lips there was something with which his brother might well be passionately in love, but which perhaps could not be loved for long.", "그러나 그 두 눈에도, 또 그 우아한 입술의 윤곽에도, 형이 충분히 열렬하게 사랑할 만하면서도 어쩌면 오래도록 사랑하기는 어려울 듯한 무언가가 깃들어 있었다."], ["He expressed this thought almost plainly to Dmitri when, after the visit, his brother besought and insisted that he should not conceal his impressions on seeing his betrothed.", "그날 방문을 마친 뒤, 형이 약혼녀를 본 인상을 숨기지 말라고 애원하며 다그쳤을 때, 알료샤는 이 생각을 거의 그대로 드미트리에게 털어놓았다."]], [["“You’ll be happy with her, but perhaps—not tranquilly happy.”", "\"형은 그녀와 행복할 거야. 하지만 어쩌면 마음 편한 행복은 아닐지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Quite so, brother.", "\"바로 그렇다, 아우야."], ["Such people remain always the same.", "그런 사람들은 늘 한결같지."], ["They don’t yield to fate.", "운명에 굴복하는 법이 없어."], ["So you think I shan’t love her for ever.”", "그러니까 너는 내가 그녀를 영원히 사랑하지는 못할 거라고 생각하는구나.\""]], [["“No; perhaps you will love her for ever.", "\"아니. 형은 어쩌면 그녀를 영원히 사랑할지도 몰라."], ["But perhaps you won’t always be happy with her.”", "하지만 어쩌면 그녀와 늘 행복하지는 못할 거야.\""]], [["Alyosha had given his opinion at the time, blushing, and angry with himself for having yielded to his brother’s entreaties and put such “foolish”", "알료샤는 그때 얼굴을 붉히며 자기 의견을 말했고, 형의 간청에 못 이겨 그런 \"어리석은\" 생각을 입 밖에 낸 자신에게 화가 났다."], ["ideas into words. For his opinion had struck him as awfully foolish immediately after he had uttered it.", "그도 그럴 것이, 그 말을 내뱉은 직후 자신의 의견이 끔찍하게 어리석게 느껴졌기 때문이다."], ["He felt ashamed too of having given so confident an opinion about a woman.", "또한 여자에 대해 그토록 자신만만한 평가를 내린 것이 부끄럽기도 했다."], ["It was with the more amazement that he felt now, at the first glance at Katerina Ivanovna as she ran in to him, that he had perhaps been utterly mistaken.", "그랬기에 지금 그는 한결 더 놀라며 느꼈으니, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 달려 들어오는 것을 처음 본 순간, 자기가 어쩌면 완전히 잘못 보았는지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["This time her face was beaming with spontaneous good-natured kindliness, and direct warm-hearted sincerity.", "이번에 그녀의 얼굴은 꾸밈없이 우러나는 따뜻한 다정함과, 거짓 없이 정겨운 진실함으로 빛나고 있었다."], ["The “pride and haughtiness,” which had struck Alyosha so much before, was only betrayed now in a frank, generous energy and a sort of bright, strong faith in herself.", "전에 그토록 알료샤를 놀라게 했던 그 \"긍지와 거만함\"은 이제 다만 솔직하고 너그러운 활기와, 어떤 밝고 굳센 자기 확신으로만 드러날 따름이었다."], ["Alyosha realized at the first glance, at the first word, that all the tragedy of her position in relation to the man she loved so dearly was no secret to her; that she perhaps already knew everything, positively everything.", "알료샤는 첫눈에, 첫마디에 깨달았으니, 그녀가 그토록 애틋이 사랑하는 사내와 얽힌 자기 처지의 그 모든 비극이 그녀에게 결코 비밀이 아니라는 것을, 어쩌면 그녀가 이미 모든 것을, 정말 모든 것을 알고 있으리라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["And yet, in spite of that, there was such brightness in her face, such faith in the future.", "그런데도 불구하고 그녀의 얼굴에는 그토록 환한 빛이, 미래에 대한 그토록 굳은 믿음이 있었다."], ["Alyosha felt at once that he had gravely wronged her in his thoughts.", "알료샤는 곧 자기가 마음속으로 그녀를 몹시 그릇되게 판단했음을 느꼈다."], ["He was conquered and captivated immediately.", "그는 곧장 사로잡히고 매혹되었다."], ["Besides all this, he noticed at her first words that she was in great excitement, an excitement perhaps quite exceptional and almost approaching ecstasy.", "그 모든 것에 더하여, 그는 그녀의 첫마디에서 그녀가 크나큰 흥분에 빠져 있음을, 어쩌면 아주 예사롭지 않은, 거의 환희에 가까운 흥분에 빠져 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“I was so eager to see you, because I can learn from you the whole truth—from you and no one else.”", "\"당신을 무척이나 만나고 싶었어요. 왜냐하면 당신에게서 모든 진실을 알 수 있으니까요. 당신에게서, 다른 누구도 아닌 당신에게서요.\""]], [["“I have come,”", "\"제가 온 것은,\""], ["muttered Alyosha confusedly, “I—he sent me.”", "알료샤가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 중얼거렸다. \"저는, 그 사람이 저를 보냈습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, he sent you!", "\"아, 그 사람이 당신을 보냈군요!"], ["I foresaw that.", "저는 그럴 줄 예감했어요."], ["Now I know everything—everything!”", "이제 저는 모든 것을 알아요, 모든 것을!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, her eyes flashing.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 두 눈을 번득이며 외쳤다."], ["“Wait a moment, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I’ll tell you why I’ve been so longing to see you.", "\"잠깐만요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 왜 그토록 당신을 보고 싶어 했는지 말씀드릴게요."], ["You see, I know perhaps far more than you do yourself, and there’s no need for you to tell me anything.", "보세요, 저는 어쩌면 당신 자신보다도 훨씬 많은 것을 알고 있으니, 당신이 제게 무얼 말씀해 주실 필요는 없어요."], ["I’ll tell you what I want from you.", "제가 당신께 바라는 것을 말씀드리죠."], ["I want to know your own last impression of him.", "저는 당신이 그 사람에게서 받은 마지막 인상을 알고 싶어요."], ["I want you to tell me most directly, plainly, coarsely even (oh, as coarsely as you like!), what you thought of him just now and of his position after your meeting with him to-day.", "저는 당신이 가장 솔직하게, 가감 없이, 거칠게라도(오, 얼마든지 거칠게요!) 말씀해 주시기를 바라요. 오늘 그 사람을 만난 뒤, 당신이 그 사람을, 그리고 그 사람의 처지를 어떻게 보았는지를 말이에요."], ["That will perhaps be better than if I had a personal explanation with him, as he does not want to come to me.", "그게 어쩌면 제가 그 사람과 직접 담판을 짓는 것보다 나을 거예요. 그 사람은 제게 오려 하지 않으니까요."], ["Do you understand what I want from you?", "제가 당신께 무얼 바라는지 아시겠어요?"], ["Now, tell me simply, tell me every word of the message he sent you with (I knew he would send you).”", "자, 그저 단순하게 말씀해 주세요. 그 사람이 당신 편에 보낸 전갈을 한 마디도 빠짐없이 말씀해 주세요(저는 그 사람이 당신을 보내리라는 걸 알고 있었어요).\""]], [["“He told me to give you his compliments—and to say that he would never come again—but to give you his compliments.”", "\"그분은 당신에게 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨습니다. 그리고 다시는 오지 않겠노라고 말해 달라고요. 다만 안부를 전해 달라고 하셨습니다.\""]], [["“His compliments?", "\"안부를요?"], ["Was that what he said—his own expression?”", "그 사람이 그렇게 말했나요, 그 사람 자신의 말로?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Accidentally perhaps he made a mistake in the word, perhaps he did not use the right word?”", "\"혹시 우연히 말을 잘못 골랐던 게 아닐까요, 어쩌면 맞는 말을 쓰지 않았던 게 아닐까요?\""]], [["“No; he told me precisely to repeat that word.", "\"아닙니다. 그분은 제게 그 말을 그대로 전하라고 분명히 일렀습니다."], ["He begged me two or three times not to forget to say so.”", "잊지 말고 꼭 그렇게 전해 달라고 두세 번이나 당부하셨습니다.\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna flushed hotly.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다."]], [["“Help me now, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "\"이제 저를 도와주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["Now I really need your help.", "지금 저는 정말로 당신의 도움이 필요해요."], ["I’ll tell you what I think, and you must simply say whether it’s right or not.", "제 생각을 말씀드릴 테니, 당신은 그저 그게 맞는지 아닌지만 말씀해 주세요. 들어 보세요!"], ["Listen! If he had sent me his compliments in passing, without insisting on your repeating the words, without emphasizing them, that would be the end of everything!", "만일 그 사람이 지나가는 말로 제게 안부를 전한 거라면, 당신더러 그 말을 그대로 옮기라고 고집하지도 않고, 그 말을 강조하지도 않았다면, 그건 모든 것의 끝이었을 거예요!"], ["But if he particularly insisted on those words, if he particularly told you not to forget to repeat them to me, then perhaps he was in excitement, beside himself.", "하지만 만일 그 사람이 그 말을 특별히 강조했다면, 그 말을 제게 그대로 전하는 것을 잊지 말라고 특별히 당부했다면, 그렇다면 어쩌면 그 사람은 흥분해 있었던 거예요, 제정신이 아니었던 거예요."], ["He had made his decision and was frightened at it.", "그 사람은 결심을 내려 놓고도 그 결심에 스스로 겁을 먹은 거예요."], ["He wasn’t walking away from me with a resolute step, but leaping headlong.", "그 사람은 결연한 발걸음으로 제게서 걸어 나간 게 아니라, 무작정 곤두박질치듯 뛰어나간 거예요."], ["The emphasis on that phrase may have been simply bravado.”", "그 말에 힘을 준 건 어쩌면 그저 허세였을 뿐일 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"그래요, 그래요!\""], ["cried Alyosha warmly.", "알료샤가 열을 띠며 외쳤다."], ["“I believe that is it.”", "\"제 생각에도 그런 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“And, if so, he’s not altogether lost.", "\"그렇다면, 그렇다면 그 사람은 아주 끝장난 게 아니에요."], ["I can still save him.", "저는 아직 그 사람을 구할 수 있어요."], ["Stay!", "가만!"], ["Did he not tell you anything about money—about three thousand roubles?”", "그 사람이 돈 이야기는, 삼천 루블 이야기는 하지 않던가요?\""]], [["“He did speak about it, and it’s that more than anything that’s crushing him.", "\"그 이야기도 하셨습니다. 그리고 무엇보다도 바로 그 일이 그분을 짓누르고 있습니다."], ["He said he had lost his honor and that nothing matters now,”", "그분은 자기가 명예를 잃었으며 이제 아무것도 중요하지 않다고 하셨습니다.\""], ["Alyosha answered warmly, feeling a rush of hope in his heart and believing that there really might be a way of escape and salvation for his brother.", "알료샤가 열을 띠며 대답했는데, 그는 가슴속에 희망이 솟구치는 것을 느끼며, 정말로 형에게 벗어날 길이, 구원의 길이 있을지도 모른다고 믿었다."], ["“But do you know about the money?”", "\"그런데 당신은 그 돈에 대해 알고 계십니까?\""], ["he added, and suddenly broke off.", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이다가 갑자기 말을 끊었다."]], [["“I’ve known of it a long time; I telegraphed to Moscow to inquire, and heard long ago that the money had not arrived.", "\"저는 오래전부터 그 일을 알고 있었어요. 모스크바로 전보를 쳐서 알아보았고, 돈이 도착하지 않았다는 걸 벌써 오래전에 들었어요."], ["He hadn’t sent the money, but I said nothing.", "그 사람은 돈을 부치지 않았지만, 저는 아무 말도 하지 않았어요."], ["Last week I learnt that he was still in need of money.", "지난주에 저는 그 사람이 아직도 돈에 쪼들리고 있다는 걸 알게 됐어요."], ["My only object in all this was that he should know to whom to turn, and who was his true friend.", "이 모든 일에서 제 단 하나의 바람은, 그 사람이 누구에게 의지해야 할지, 누가 자신의 진정한 벗인지를 깨닫게 하는 것이었어요."], ["No, he won’t recognize that I am his truest friend; he won’t know me, and looks on me merely as a woman.", "아니에요, 그 사람은 제가 자신의 가장 진실한 벗임을 인정하려 들지 않아요. 그 사람은 저를 알아보려 하지 않고, 그저 저를 한낱 여자로만 여겨요."], ["I’ve been tormented all the week, trying to think how to prevent him from being ashamed to face me because he spent that three thousand.", "저는 한 주 내내 괴로워했어요. 그 사람이 그 삼천 루블을 써 버렸다고 해서 제 얼굴을 보기 부끄러워하지 않도록 하려면 어떻게 해야 할지 골몰하면서요."], ["Let him feel ashamed of himself, let him be ashamed of other people’s knowing, but not of my knowing.", "그 사람더러 자기 자신에게 부끄러워하라고 해요, 다른 사람들이 아는 것을 부끄러워하라고 해요, 하지만 제가 아는 것은 부끄러워하지 말아요."], ["He can tell God everything without shame.", "그 사람은 부끄러움 없이 하느님께는 모든 것을 다 말할 수 있잖아요."], ["Why is it he still does not understand how much I am ready to bear for his sake?", "그 사람은 어째서 아직도 모르는 걸까요, 제가 그 사람을 위해서라면 얼마나 견딜 각오가 되어 있는지를?"], ["Why, why doesn’t he know me?", "어째서, 어째서 그 사람은 저를 모르는 걸까요?"], ["How dare he not know me after all that has happened?", "그 모든 일이 다 벌어진 마당에 어떻게 감히 저를 모를 수 있단 말인가요?"], ["I want to save him for ever.", "저는 그 사람을 영원히 구하고 싶어요."], ["Let him forget me as his betrothed.", "그 사람더러 저를 약혼녀로는 잊으라고 해요."], ["And here he fears that he is dishonored in my eyes.", "그런데도 그 사람은 제 눈에 자기가 명예를 잃은 것으로 비칠까 봐 두려워하고 있어요."], ["Why, he wasn’t afraid to be open with you, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "아니, 그 사람은 당신에게는 솔직하기를 두려워하지 않았잖아요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["How is it that I don’t deserve the same?”", "어째서 저는 그만한 대접도 받을 자격이 없다는 건가요?\""]], [["The last words she uttered in tears.", "마지막 말을 그녀는 눈물 속에서 토해 냈다."], ["Tears gushed from her eyes.", "두 눈에서 눈물이 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["“I must tell you,”", "\"한 가지 말씀드려야겠습니다,\""], ["Alyosha began, his voice trembling too, “what happened just now between him and my father.”", "알료샤가 입을 열었는데, 그의 목소리 역시 떨리고 있었다. \"방금 그분과 제 아버지 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지를요.\""]], [["And he described the whole scene, how Dmitri had sent him to get the money, how he had broken in, knocked his father down, and after that had again specially and emphatically begged him to take his compliments and farewell.", "그리고 그는 그 모든 장면을 들려주었으니, 드미트리가 돈을 받아 오라고 자기를 보낸 일이며, 그가 어떻게 뛰어들어 아버지를 때려눕혔는지, 그러고 나서 다시금 각별히, 힘주어 자기더러 안부와 작별 인사를 전해 달라고 부탁한 일을 이야기했다."], ["“He went to that woman,”", "\"그분은 그 여자에게 갔습니다.\""], ["Alyosha added softly.", "알료샤가 나직이 덧붙였다."]], [["“And do you suppose that I can’t put up with that woman?", "\"그래서 당신은 제가 그 여자를 견뎌 내지 못할 거라고 생각하시나요?"], ["Does he think I can’t?", "그 사람은 제가 견디지 못하리라고 생각하나요?"], ["But he won’t marry her,”", "하지만 그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못할 거예요.\""], ["she suddenly laughed nervously.", "그녀가 갑자기 신경질적으로 웃었다."], ["“Could such a passion last for ever in a Karamazov?", "\"카라마조프 집안 사람에게 그런 열정이 영원히 갈 수 있을까요?"], ["It’s passion, not love.", "그건 사랑이 아니라 욕정이에요."], ["He won’t marry her because she won’t marry him.”", "그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못해요, 그 여자가 그 사람과 결혼하려 하지 않을 테니까요.\""], ["Again Katerina Ivanovna laughed strangely.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 다시 이상하게 웃었다."]], [["“He may marry her,”", "\"그분은 어쩌면 그 여자와 결혼할지도 모릅니다.\""], ["said Alyosha mournfully, looking down.", "알료샤가 시선을 떨군 채 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“He won’t marry her, I tell you. That girl is an angel.", "\"그 사람은 그 여자와 결혼하지 못한다니까요."], ["Do you know that?", "그 처녀는 천사예요."], ["Do you know that?”", "당신은 그걸 아세요?"], ["Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed suddenly with extraordinary warmth.", "그걸 아세요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 별안간 유난한 열기를 띠며 외쳤다."], ["“She is one of the most fantastic of fantastic creatures.", "\"그 여자는 환상적인 존재들 가운데서도 가장 환상적인 사람이에요."], ["I know how bewitching she is, but I know too that she is kind, firm and noble.", "저는 그 여자가 얼마나 사람을 홀리는지 알지만, 동시에 그 여자가 얼마나 다정하고 굳세고 고결한지도 알아요."], ["Why do you look at me like that, Alexey Fyodorovitch?", "어째서 저를 그렇게 보시나요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?"], ["Perhaps you are wondering at my words, perhaps you don’t believe me?", "어쩌면 제 말에 놀라셨나 보군요. 어쩌면 제 말을 믿지 못하시는 거예요?"], ["Agrafena Alexandrovna, my angel!”", "아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 내 천사!\""], ["she cried suddenly to some one, peeping into the next room, “come in to us.", "그녀가 별안간 옆방을 들여다보며 누군가를 향해 소리쳤다. \"이리로 와요."], ["This is a friend.", "이분은 친구예요."], ["This is Alyosha.", "이분은 알료샤예요."], ["He knows all about our affairs.", "이분은 우리 일을 다 알고 계셔요."], ["Show yourself to him.”", "어서 이분께 나와 봐요.\""]], [["“I’ve only been waiting behind the curtain for you to call me,”", "\"부르시기만을 휘장 뒤에서 기다리고 있었답니다.\""], ["said a soft, one might even say sugary, feminine voice.", "부드러운, 어쩌면 달짝지근하다고까지 할 만한 여자의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["The portière was raised and Grushenka herself, smiling and beaming, came up to the table.", "휘장이 걷히고, 그루셴카가 미소를 머금고 환하게 빛나며 탁자 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["A violent revulsion passed over Alyosha.", "알료샤의 마음속에 격렬한 반발감이 일었다."], ["He fixed his eyes on her and could not take them off.", "그는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 시선을 고정했다."], ["Here she was, that awful woman, the “beast,” as Ivan had called her half an hour before.", "바로 그 여자가 거기 있었으니, 반 시간 전에 이반이 \"짐승\"이라 부른 그 무서운 여자였다."], ["And yet one would have thought the creature standing before him most simple and ordinary, a good-natured, kind woman, handsome certainly, but so like other handsome ordinary women!", "그런데도 그의 앞에 서 있는 그 사람은 더없이 단순하고 평범하며, 마음씨 좋고 다정한 여자처럼 보였으니, 분명 아름답기는 했으나 다른 여느 아름답고 평범한 여자들과 너무도 닮아 있었다!"], ["It is true she was very, very good-looking with that Russian beauty so passionately loved by many men.", "사실 그녀는 매우, 매우 아름다웠으니, 수많은 사내들이 그토록 열렬히 사랑하는 그 러시아적인 미모를 지니고 있었다."], ["She was a rather tall woman, though a little shorter than Katerina Ivanovna, who was exceptionally tall.", "그녀는 키가 제법 큰 여자였으되, 유난히 키가 큰 카테리나 이바노브나보다는 조금 작았다."], ["She had a full figure, with soft, as it were, noiseless, movements, softened to a peculiar over-sweetness, like her voice.", "그녀는 풍만한 몸매를 지녔고, 그 동작은 부드러워서, 말하자면 소리가 나지 않았으며, 그 목소리처럼 어떤 유별난 과도한 달콤함으로 누그러져 있었다."], ["She moved, not like Katerina Ivanovna, with a vigorous, bold step, but noiselessly.", "그녀는 카테리나 이바노브나처럼 힘차고 대담한 걸음걸이가 아니라, 소리 없이 움직였다."], ["Her feet made absolutely no sound on the floor.", "그녀의 발은 마룻바닥에서 전혀 소리를 내지 않았다."], ["She sank softly into a low chair, softly rustling her sumptuous black silk dress, and delicately nestling her milk-white neck and broad shoulders in a costly cashmere shawl.", "그녀는 낮은 의자에 사뿐히 몸을 묻으며, 화려한 검은 비단 드레스를 부드럽게 살랑거렸고, 우윳빛 목덜미와 넓은 어깨를 값비싼 캐시미어 숄에 곱게 파묻었다."], ["She was twenty-two years old, and her face looked exactly that age.", "그녀는 스물두 살이었고, 얼굴도 꼭 그 나이로 보였다."], ["She was very white in the face, with a pale pink tint on her cheeks.", "얼굴빛은 매우 희었고, 두 뺨에는 옅은 분홍빛이 어려 있었다."], ["The modeling of her face might be said to be too broad, and the lower jaw was set a trifle forward.", "얼굴의 윤곽은 다소 지나치게 넓다고 할 만했고, 아래턱이 약간 앞으로 내밀려 있었다."], ["Her upper lip was thin, but the slightly prominent lower lip was at least twice as full, and looked pouting.", "윗입술은 얇았으나, 살짝 도드라진 아랫입술은 적어도 그 두 배는 도톰하여 뾰로통해 보였다."], ["But her magnificent, abundant dark brown hair, her sable-colored eyebrows and charming gray-blue eyes with their long lashes would have made the most indifferent person, meeting her casually in a crowd in the street, stop at the sight of her face and remember it long after.", "그러나 그 풍성하고 멋진 짙은 갈색 머리칼과, 검은담비 빛 눈썹, 그리고 긴 속눈썹에 둘러싸인 매혹적인 회청색 눈동자는, 거리의 인파 속에서 우연히 그녀와 마주친 더없이 무심한 사람조차도 그 얼굴을 보고는 걸음을 멈추게 하고 오래도록 기억에 남게 했을 것이다."], ["What struck Alyosha most in that face was its expression of childlike good nature.", "그 얼굴에서 알료샤를 가장 강하게 친 것은 어린아이 같은 천진함의 표정이었다."], ["There was a childlike look in her eyes, a look of childish delight.", "그녀의 눈 속에는 어린아이 같은 눈빛이, 어린아이 같은 기쁨의 빛이 있었다."], ["She came up to the table, beaming with delight and seeming to expect something with childish, impatient, and confiding curiosity.", "그녀는 기쁨에 빛나며 탁자 쪽으로 다가왔는데, 마치 어린아이처럼 조바심 어린, 그러면서도 사람을 믿는 호기심으로 무언가를 기대하는 듯이 보였다."], ["The light in her eyes gladdened the soul—Alyosha felt that.", "그녀의 눈 속에 깃든 빛은 영혼을 기쁘게 했으니, 알료샤는 그것을 느꼈다."], ["There was something else in her which he could not understand, or would not have been able to define, and which yet perhaps unconsciously affected him.", "그녀에게는 그가 이해할 수 없는, 아니 도저히 규정할 수 없을 또 다른 무언가가 있었는데, 그것이 어쩌면 무의식중에 그에게 영향을 미치고 있었다."], ["It was that softness, that voluptuousness of her bodily movements, that catlike noiselessness.", "그것은 바로 그 부드러움, 그 육체적 동작의 관능, 그 고양이 같은 소리 없음이었다."], ["Yet it was a vigorous, ample body.", "그러면서도 그것은 힘차고 풍만한 육체였다."], ["Under the shawl could be seen full broad shoulders, a high, still quite girlish bosom.", "숄 아래로는 둥글고 넓은 어깨와, 높이 솟은, 아직은 자못 처녀다운 가슴이 비쳤다."], ["Her figure suggested the lines of the Venus of Milo, though already in somewhat exaggerated proportions.", "그녀의 몸매는 밀로의 비너스를 연상시키는 선을 지녔으되, 그 비례는 이미 다소 과장되어 있었다."], ["That could be divined.", "그것을 미루어 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["Connoisseurs of Russian beauty could have foretold with certainty that this fresh, still youthful beauty would lose its harmony by the age of thirty, would “spread”; that the face would become puffy, and that wrinkles would very soon appear upon her forehead and round the eyes; the complexion would grow coarse and red perhaps—in fact, that it was the beauty of the moment, the fleeting beauty which is so often met with in Russian women.", "러시아적 미모를 아는 감식가라면 틀림없이 예언할 수 있었을 것이니, 이 싱싱하고 아직 젊은 미모가 서른 무렵이면 그 조화를 잃고 \"퍼져 버릴\" 것이며, 얼굴은 부풀어 오르고, 이마와 눈가에는 곧 주름이 생기며, 안색은 어쩌면 거칠고 불그레해질 것이라는 것을, 요컨대 그것이 한순간의 미모, 러시아 여인들에게서 그토록 흔히 마주치는 덧없는 미모라는 것을 말이다."], ["Alyosha, of course, did not think of this; but though he was fascinated, yet he wondered with an unpleasant sensation, and as it were regretfully, why she drawled in that way and could not speak naturally.", "알료샤가 물론 이런 것을 생각한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 그는 매혹되면서도, 한편으로는 불쾌한 느낌을 품고서, 말하자면 아쉬운 듯이 의아해했으니, 어째서 그녀는 저런 식으로 말을 늘어뜨리며 자연스럽게 말하지 못할까 하는 것이었다."], ["She did so evidently feeling there was a charm in the exaggerated, honeyed modulation of the syllables.", "그녀가 그렇게 하는 것은 분명, 음절을 과장되게 꿀처럼 굴리는 데 어떤 매력이 있다고 느끼기 때문이었다."], ["It was, of course, only a bad, underbred habit that showed bad education and a false idea of good manners.", "그것은 물론 좋지 못한, 천박한 버릇에 지나지 않았으니, 형편없는 교육과 예의범절에 대한 그릇된 관념을 드러낼 뿐이었다."], ["And yet this intonation and manner of speaking impressed Alyosha as almost incredibly incongruous with the childishly simple and happy expression of her face, the soft, babyish joy in her eyes.", "그러면서도 이 억양과 말투는 알료샤에게, 어린아이처럼 단순하고 행복한 그녀의 얼굴 표정, 그 눈 속의 부드럽고 천진한 기쁨과는 거의 믿기 어려울 만큼 어긋나는 것으로 느껴졌다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna at once made her sit down in an arm-chair facing Alyosha, and ecstatically kissed her several times on her smiling lips.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 곧 그녀를 알료샤를 마주 보는 안락의자에 앉히고는, 미소 짓는 그 입술에 황홀한 듯 몇 번이나 입을 맞추었다."], ["She seemed quite in love with her.", "그녀는 그녀에게 흠뻑 빠진 듯이 보였다."]], [["“This is the first time we’ve met, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"우리가 만나는 건 이번이 처음이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["she said rapturously.", "그녀가 황홀한 듯 말했다."], ["“I wanted to know her, to see her.", "\"저는 이 사람을 알고 싶었어요, 이 사람을 보고 싶었어요."], ["I wanted to go to her, but I’d no sooner expressed the wish than she came to me.", "제가 이 사람을 찾아가려 했는데, 그 바람을 입에 올리기가 무섭게 이 사람이 제게 와 주었어요."], ["I knew we should settle everything together—everything.", "저는 우리가 함께라면 모든 것을, 모든 것을 해결하리라는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["My heart told me so—I was begged not to take the step, but I foresaw it would be a way out of the difficulty, and I was not mistaken.", "제 마음이 제게 그렇게 일러 주었어요. 사람들은 제게 그러지 말라고 말렸지만, 저는 이것이 이 어려움에서 벗어날 길이 되리라는 걸 예감했고, 제 예감은 틀리지 않았어요."], ["Grushenka has explained everything to me, told me all she means to do.", "그루셴카가 제게 모든 것을 설명해 주었고, 자기가 어떻게 할 작정인지 다 말해 주었어요."], ["She flew here like an angel of goodness and brought us peace and joy.”", "이 사람은 선의 천사처럼 이리로 날아와 우리에게 평화와 기쁨을 가져다주었어요.\""]], [["“You did not disdain me, sweet, excellent young lady,”", "\"아가씨께서 저를 업신여기지 않으셨군요, 곱고 훌륭하신 아가씨.\""], ["drawled Grushenka in her sing-song voice, still with the same charming smile of delight.", "그루셴카가 여전히 똑같은 매혹적인 기쁨의 미소를 머금은 채, 그 노래하는 듯한 목소리로 말을 늘어뜨렸다."]], [["“Don’t dare to speak to me like that, you sorceress, you witch!", "\"감히 내게 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요, 이 마녀, 이 요녀 같으니!"], ["Disdain you!", "내가 당신을 업신여기다니요!"], ["Here, I must kiss your lower lip once more.", "자, 당신 아랫입술에 한 번 더 입을 맞춰야겠어요."], ["It looks as though it were swollen, and now it will be more so, and more and more.", "부어오른 것 같은데, 이러다 더 부어오를 거예요, 점점 더, 점점 더요."], ["Look how she laughs, Alexey Fyodorovitch!", "저 사람이 어떻게 웃는지 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치!"], ["It does one’s heart good to see the angel.”", "이 천사를 보고 있으면 마음이 흐뭇해진다니까요.\""]], [["Alyosha flushed, and faint, imperceptible shivers kept running down him.", "알료샤는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 희미한, 알아차릴 수 없는 떨림이 자꾸만 그의 온몸을 타고 흘렀다."]], [["“You make so much of me, dear young lady, and perhaps I am not at all worthy of your kindness.”", "\"저를 이토록 떠받들어 주시지만, 다정한 아가씨, 저는 어쩌면 당신의 친절을 받을 자격이 조금도 없을지 몰라요.\""]], [["“Not worthy!", "\"자격이 없다니요!"], ["She’s not worthy of it!”", "이 사람이 자격이 없다니요!\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna cried again with the same warmth.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 또다시 똑같은 열기를 띠고 외쳤다."], ["“You know, Alexey Fyodorovitch, we’re fanciful, we’re self-willed, but proudest of the proud in our little heart.", "\"있잖아요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리는 변덕스럽고, 우리는 제멋대로이지만, 그 조그만 마음속에서는 자존심 강한 사람들 가운데서도 가장 자존심이 강하답니다."], ["We’re noble, we’re generous, Alexey Fyodorovitch, let me tell you.", "우리는 고결하고, 우리는 너그러워요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 말씀드리지만요."], ["We have only been unfortunate.", "우리는 다만 불운했을 뿐이에요."], ["We were too ready to make every sacrifice for an unworthy, perhaps, or fickle man.", "우리는 어쩌면 자격도 없거나 변덕스러운 사내를 위해 너무도 선뜻 온갖 희생을 다 바치려 했어요."], ["There was one man—one, an officer too, we loved him, we sacrificed everything to him.", "한 사내가 있었어요, 단 한 사람, 그 역시 장교였는데, 우리는 그를 사랑했고, 그를 위해 모든 것을 희생했어요."], ["That was long ago, five years ago, and he has forgotten us, he has married.", "그건 오래전, 오 년 전 일이고, 그는 우리를 잊었어요, 그는 결혼했어요."], ["Now he is a widower, he has written, he is coming here, and, do you know, we’ve loved him, none but him, all this time, and we’ve loved him all our life!", "지금 그는 홀아비가 되어 편지를 보냈고, 이리로 오고 있어요. 그리고 있잖아요, 우리는 이 모든 세월 동안 그를, 오직 그 사람만을 사랑했어요, 우리는 평생토록 그를 사랑했어요!"], ["He will come, and Grushenka will be happy again.", "그가 올 거예요, 그러면 그루셴카는 다시 행복해질 거예요."], ["For the last five years she’s been wretched.", "지난 오 년 동안 이 사람은 비참하게 지냈어요."], ["But who can reproach her, who can boast of her favor?", "하지만 누가 이 사람을 나무랄 수 있겠어요, 누가 이 사람의 사랑을 받았노라 자랑할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Only that bedridden old merchant, but he is more like her father, her friend, her protector.", "다만 그 병상에 누운 늙은 상인뿐인데, 그 사람은 이 사람에게 차라리 아버지 같고, 벗 같고, 보호자 같은 사람이에요."], ["He found her then in despair, in agony, deserted by the man she loved.", "그는 그때 이 사람이 절망에 빠져, 고통 속에서, 사랑하던 사내에게 버림받은 것을 발견했어요."], ["She was ready to drown herself then, but the old merchant saved her—saved her!”", "이 사람은 그때 물에 몸을 던지려 했지만, 그 늙은 상인이 이 사람을 구해 주었어요, 구해 주었어요!\""]], [["“You defend me very kindly, dear young lady.", "\"아가씨께서 저를 참 다정히도 변호해 주시는군요, 다정한 아가씨."], ["You are in a great hurry about everything,”", "당신은 모든 일에 너무 서두르시는 분이에요.\""], ["Grushenka drawled again.", "그루셴카가 또다시 말을 늘어뜨렸다."]], [["“Defend you!", "\"당신을 변호한다고요!"], ["Is it for me to defend you?", "내가 당신을 변호한다고요?"], ["Should I dare to defend you?", "내가 감히 당신을 변호할 자격이라도 된단 말인가요?"], ["Grushenka, angel, give me your hand.", "그루셴카, 천사, 내게 손을 줘요."], ["Look at that charming soft little hand, Alexey Fyodorovitch!", "이 곱고 부드러운 작은 손을 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치!"], ["Look at it!", "이 손을 보세요!"], ["It has brought me happiness and has lifted me up, and I’m going to kiss it, outside and inside, here, here, here!”", "이 손이 제게 행복을 가져다주었고 저를 일으켜 세워 주었어요. 그래서 저는 이 손에 입을 맞추겠어요, 겉에도, 안에도, 여기, 여기, 여기에요!\""]], [["And three times she kissed the certainly charming, though rather fat, hand of Grushenka in a sort of rapture.", "그러고는 그녀가 일종의 황홀경에 빠져, 분명 곱기는 하나 다소 통통한 그루셴카의 손에 세 번이나 입을 맞추었다."], ["She held out her hand with a charming musical, nervous little laugh, watched the “sweet young lady,” and obviously liked having her hand kissed.", "그루셴카는 매혹적이고 음악 같은, 신경질적인 작은 웃음소리를 내며 손을 내밀고는 그 \"고운 아가씨\"를 지켜보았는데, 손에 입맞춤을 받는 것이 분명 흐뭇한 모양이었다."]], [["“Perhaps there’s rather too much rapture,”", "'어쩌면 너무 지나친 황홀이 아닌가.'"], ["thought Alyosha.", "알료샤는 생각했다."], ["He blushed.", "그는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["He felt a peculiar uneasiness at heart the whole time.", "그는 내내 마음속에 어떤 야릇한 거북함을 느꼈다."]], [["“You won’t make me blush, dear young lady, kissing my hand like this before Alexey Fyodorovitch.”", "\"저를 부끄럽게 하지 말아 주세요, 다정한 아가씨, 알렉세이 표도로비치 앞에서 이렇게 제 손에 입을 맞추시다니요.\""]], [["“Do you think I meant to make you blush?”", "\"제가 당신을 부끄럽게 하려 한 줄 아세요?\""], ["said Katerina Ivanovna, somewhat surprised.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 다소 놀라며 말했다."], ["“Ah, my dear, how little you understand me!”", "\"아, 그루셴카, 당신은 어쩜 저를 이렇게도 몰라 줄까요!\""]], [["“Yes, and you too perhaps quite misunderstand me, dear young lady.", "\"네, 그리고 당신도 어쩌면 저를 영 잘못 아시는지 몰라요, 다정한 아가씨."], ["Maybe I’m not so good as I seem to you.", "어쩌면 저는 당신이 보시는 것만큼 그렇게 좋은 사람이 아닐지도 몰라요."], ["I’ve a bad heart; I will have my own way.", "저는 못된 마음을 가졌고, 제멋대로 굴 거예요."], ["I fascinated poor Dmitri Fyodorovitch that day simply for fun.”", "저는 그날 가엾은 드미트리 표도로비치를 한낱 장난삼아 홀려 버렸답니다.\""]], [["“But now you’ll save him.", "\"하지만 이제 당신은 그분을 구해 주실 거예요."], ["You’ve given me your word.", "당신은 제게 약속했잖아요."], ["You’ll explain it all to him.", "당신은 그분께 모든 것을 설명해 주실 거예요."], ["You’ll break to him that you have long loved another man, who is now offering you his hand.”", "당신은 그분께, 자신이 오래전부터 다른 사내를 사랑해 왔으며 그 사내가 지금 자신에게 청혼하고 있다는 것을 밝혀 주실 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아니에요!"], ["I didn’t give you my word to do that.", "저는 그렇게 하겠다고 당신께 약속한 적 없어요."], ["It was you kept talking about that.", "그건 당신이 줄곧 말씀하신 거예요."], ["I didn’t give you my word.”", "저는 당신께 약속한 게 없어요.\""]], [["“Then I didn’t quite understand you,”", "\"그렇다면 제가 당신을 잘못 이해한 거로군요.\""], ["said Katerina Ivanovna slowly, turning a little pale.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 약간 창백해지며 천천히 말했다."], ["“You promised—”", "\"당신은 약속했어요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, angel lady, I’ve promised nothing,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 천사 같은 아가씨, 저는 아무것도 약속하지 않았어요.\""], ["Grushenka interrupted softly and evenly, still with the same gay and simple expression.", "그루셴카가 여전히 똑같은 명랑하고 천진한 표정으로, 부드럽고 차분하게 말을 가로막았다."], ["“You see at once, dear young lady, what a willful wretch I am compared with you.", "\"당신은 곧 아실 거예요, 다정한 아가씨, 제가 당신에 비하면 얼마나 제멋대로인 못된 계집인지를요."], ["If I want to do a thing I do it.", "저는 무얼 하고 싶으면 그냥 해 버려요."], ["I may have made you some promise just now.", "어쩌면 방금 당신께 무슨 약속을 했을지도 모르죠."], ["But now again I’m thinking: I may take to Mitya again.", "하지만 지금 다시 이런 생각이 들어요. 저는 어쩌면 미탸에게 다시 끌릴지도 몰라요."], ["I liked him very much once—liked him for almost a whole hour.", "저는 한때 그이를 무척 좋아했거든요, 거의 한 시간 내내 좋아했어요."], ["Now maybe I shall go and tell him to stay with me from this day forward.", "어쩌면 지금이라도 가서 그이더러 오늘부터 영영 제 곁에 있어 달라고 할지도 몰라요."], ["You see, I’m so changeable.”", "보세요, 저는 이렇게 변덕스럽답니다.\""]], [["“Just now you said—something quite different,”", "\"방금은 당신이, 전혀 다른 말을 했잖아요.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna whispered faintly.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 가냘프게 속삭였다."]], [["“Ah, just now!", "\"아, 방금은요!"], ["But, you know. I’m such a soft-hearted, silly creature.", "하지만 있잖아요, 저는 이렇게 마음 여리고 어리석은 계집이랍니다."], ["Only think what he’s gone through on my account!", "그이가 저 때문에 어떤 일을 겪었는지 한번 생각해 보세요!"], ["What if when I go home I feel sorry for him?", "만일 제가 집에 돌아갔을 때 그이가 가엾어진다면 어쩌죠?"], ["What then?”", "그러면 어떻게 되는 건가요?\""]], [["“I never expected—”", "\"저는 전혀 생각지도 못했어요.\""]], [["“Ah, young lady, how good and generous you are compared with me!", "\"아, 아가씨, 당신은 저에 비하면 어쩜 그리도 착하고 너그러우실까요!"], ["Now perhaps you won’t care for a silly creature like me, now you know my character.", "이제 어쩌면 당신은 저 같은 어리석은 계집은 좋아하지 않으실 거예요, 이제 제 됨됨이를 아셨으니까요."], ["Give me your sweet little hand, angelic lady,”", "그 고운 작은 손을 제게 주세요, 천사 같은 아가씨.\""], ["she said tenderly, and with a sort of reverence took Katerina Ivanovna’s hand.", "그녀가 다정하게 말하며, 일종의 경의를 담아 카테리나 이바노브나의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Here, dear young lady, I’ll take your hand and kiss it as you did mine.", "\"자, 다정한 아가씨, 당신이 제게 하셨던 것처럼 저도 당신의 손을 잡고 입을 맞추겠어요."], ["You kissed mine three times, but I ought to kiss yours three hundred times to be even with you.", "당신은 제 손에 세 번 입을 맞추셨지만, 저는 당신과 셈을 맞추려면 당신 손에 삼백 번은 입을 맞춰야 마땅하겠죠."], ["Well, but let that pass.", "자, 하지만 그건 그렇다 치고요."], ["And then it shall be as God wills.", "그다음 일은 하느님 뜻대로 될 거예요."], ["Perhaps I shall be your slave entirely and want to do your bidding like a slave.", "어쩌면 저는 온전히 당신의 노예가 되어, 노예처럼 당신이 시키는 대로 하고 싶어질지도 몰라요."], ["Let it be as God wills, without any agreements and promises.", "어떤 약조도 약속도 없이, 그저 하느님 뜻대로 되게 해요."], ["What a sweet hand—what a sweet hand you have!", "어쩜 이리도 고운 손일까요, 어쩜 이리도 고운 손을 가지셨을까요!"], ["You sweet young lady, you incredible beauty!”", "당신은 고운 아가씨예요, 당신은 믿기지 않을 만큼 아름다운 분이에요!\""]], [["She slowly raised the hands to her lips, with the strange object indeed of “being even”", "그녀는 천천히 그 손을 입술로 들어 올렸는데, 그 야릇한 속셈은 정말로 입맞춤으로 그녀와 \"셈을 맞추려는\""], ["with her in kisses.", "것이었다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna did not take her hand away.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 손을 빼지 않았다."], ["She listened with timid hope to the last words, though Grushenka’s promise to do her bidding like a slave was very strangely expressed.", "그녀는 그 마지막 말들을 수줍은 희망을 품고 들었는데, 비록 노예처럼 자기 뜻을 따르겠다는 그루셴카의 약속이 매우 이상하게 표현되기는 했어도 그러했다."], ["She looked intently into her eyes; she still saw in those eyes the same simple-hearted, confiding expression, the same bright gayety.", "그녀는 그루셴카의 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다. 그녀는 그 눈 속에서 여전히 똑같은 순박하고 사람을 믿는 표정을, 여전히 똑같은 환한 명랑함을 보았다."]], [["“She’s perhaps too naïve,”", "'어쩌면 이 사람은 너무 천진한 거야.'"], ["thought Katerina Ivanovna, with a gleam of hope.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 한 줄기 희망의 빛을 품고 생각했다."]], [["Grushenka meanwhile seemed enthusiastic over the “sweet hand.”", "한편 그루셴카는 그 \"고운 손\"에 황홀해하는 듯이 보였다."], ["She raised it deliberately to her lips.", "그녀는 그 손을 천천히 입술로 들어 올렸다."], ["But she held it for two or three minutes near her lips, as though reconsidering something.", "그러나 그녀는 무언가를 다시 곰곰이 생각하기라도 하듯, 그 손을 두세 시간이나, 아니 두세 분이나 입술 가까이에 든 채로 멈춰 있었다."]], [["“Do you know, angel lady,”", "\"있잖아요, 천사 같은 아가씨,\""], ["she suddenly drawled in an even more soft and sugary voice, “do you know, after all, I think I won’t kiss your hand?”", "그녀가 별안간 한층 더 부드럽고 달짝지근한 목소리로 말을 늘어뜨렸다. \"있잖아요, 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 저는 아무래도 당신 손에 입을 맞추지 않는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["And she laughed a little merry laugh.", "그러고는 즐거운 듯 작은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“As you please.", "\"마음대로 하세요."], ["What’s the matter with you?”", "무슨 일이세요?\""], ["said Katerina Ivanovna, starting suddenly.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 갑자기 흠칫하며 말했다."]], [["“So that you may be left to remember that you kissed my hand, but I didn’t kiss yours.”", "\"그래야 당신은 자기가 내 손에는 입을 맞추었지만, 나는 당신 손에 입을 맞추지 않았다는 걸 기억하실 테니까요.\""]], [["There was a sudden gleam in her eyes.", "그녀의 눈 속에 별안간 어떤 번득임이 일었다."], ["She looked with awful intentness at Katerina Ivanovna.", "그녀는 무시무시한 골똘함으로 카테리나 이바노브나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Insolent creature!”", "\"이 뻔뻔한 것!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, as though suddenly grasping something.", "카테리나 이바노브나가, 마치 별안간 무언가를 깨닫기라도 한 듯 외쳤다."], ["She flushed all over and leapt up from her seat.", "그녀는 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["Grushenka too got up, but without haste.", "그루셴카도 일어섰으나, 서두르는 기색은 없었다."]], [["“So I shall tell Mitya how you kissed my hand, but I didn’t kiss yours at all.", "\"그러니까 저는 미탸에게 가서 일러 줄 거예요, 당신은 내 손에 입을 맞추었지만 나는 당신 손에는 도무지 입을 맞추지 않았다고요."], ["And how he will laugh!”", "그이가 얼마나 웃어 댈까요!\""]], [["“Vile slut!", "\"이 더러운 갈보 같으니!"], ["Go away!”", "썩 나가요!\""]], [["“Ah, for shame, young lady!", "\"아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요, 아가씨!"], ["Ah, for shame!", "아, 부끄러운 줄 아세요!"], ["That’s unbecoming for you, dear young lady, a word like that.”", "그런 말은 당신께 어울리지 않아요, 다정한 아가씨, 그런 말은요.\""]], [["“Go away!", "\"나가요!"], ["You’re a creature for sale!”", "당신은 몸을 파는 계집이에요!\""], ["screamed Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 비명을 질렀다."], ["Every feature was working in her utterly distorted face.", "완전히 일그러진 그녀의 얼굴에서는 이목구비 하나하나가 씰룩거리고 있었다."]], [["“For sale indeed!", "\"몸을 파는 계집이라고요!"], ["You used to visit gentlemen in the dusk for money once; you brought your beauty for sale.", "당신도 한때 돈을 받고 어스름녘에 신사들을 찾아다녔잖아요. 당신도 당신의 미모를 팔러 다녔어요."], ["You see, I know.”", "보세요, 저는 다 알아요.\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna shrieked, and would have rushed at her, but Alyosha held her with all his strength.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 날카로운 비명을 지르며 그녀에게 덤벼들려 했으나, 알료샤가 온 힘을 다해 그녀를 붙들었다."]], [["“Not a step, not a word!", "\"한 발짝도, 한 마디도 안 됩니다!"], ["Don’t speak, don’t answer her.", "말하지 마세요, 저 여자에게 대꾸하지 마세요."], ["She’ll go away—she’ll go at once.”", "저 여자는 가 버릴 겁니다, 곧 가 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["At that instant Katerina Ivanovna’s two aunts ran in at her cry, and with them a maid-servant.", "바로 그 순간, 카테리나 이바노브나의 비명 소리에 두 고모가 뛰어 들어왔고, 그들과 함께 하녀도 들어왔다."], ["All hurried to her.", "모두가 그녀에게로 달려들었다."]], [["“I will go away,”", "\"저는 가겠어요.\""], ["said Grushenka, taking up her mantle from the sofa.", "그루셴카가 소파에서 망토를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["“Alyosha, darling, see me home!”", "\"알료샤, 자기, 집까지 바래다줘요!\""]], [["“Go away—go away, make haste!”", "\"가요, 어서 가요, 서둘러서요!\""], ["cried Alyosha, clasping his hands imploringly.", "알료샤가 두 손을 모아 애원하듯 외쳤다."]], [["“Dear little Alyosha, see me home!", "\"귀여운 알료샤, 집까지 바래다줘요!"], ["I’ve got a pretty little story to tell you on the way.", "가는 길에 들려줄 재미난 이야기가 있어요."], ["I got up this scene for your benefit, Alyosha.", "저는 이 소동을 당신을 위해서 벌인 거예요, 알료샤."], ["See me home, dear, you’ll be glad of it afterwards.”", "집까지 바래다줘요, 자기, 나중에 그러길 잘했다 싶을 거예요.\""]], [["Alyosha turned away, wringing his hands.", "알료샤는 두 손을 비틀며 외면했다."], ["Grushenka ran out of the house, laughing musically.", "그루셴카는 음악 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며 집 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna went into a fit of hysterics.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 발작을 일으켰다."], ["She sobbed, and was shaken with convulsions.", "그녀는 흐느꼈고, 경련으로 몸을 떨었다."], ["Every one fussed round her.", "모두가 그녀를 둘러싸고 부산을 떨었다."]], [["“I warned you,”", "\"내가 경고했잖니.\""], ["said the elder of her aunts.", "나이 든 고모가 말했다."], ["“I tried to prevent your doing this.", "\"내가 이러지 말라고 말렸잖니."], ["You’re too impulsive.", "너는 너무 충동적이야."], ["How could you do such a thing?", "어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수 있었니?"], ["You don’t know these creatures, and they say she’s worse than any of them.", "너는 그런 계집들을 몰라, 게다가 저 여자는 그중에서도 누구보다 고약하다고들 하더구나."], ["You are too self-willed.”", "너는 너무 제멋대로야.\""]], [["“She’s a tigress!”", "\"저 여자는 암호랑이예요!\""], ["yelled Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 악을 썼다."], ["“Why did you hold me, Alexey Fyodorovitch?", "\"어째서 저를 붙드셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?"], ["I’d have beaten her—beaten her!”", "그러지 않았으면 저 여자를 때려 줬을 텐데, 흠씬 때려 줬을 텐데!\""]], [["She could not control herself before Alyosha; perhaps she did not care to, indeed.", "그녀는 알료샤 앞에서 자제하지 못했다. 어쩌면 그녀는 정말로 자제할 마음조차 없었던 것이리라."]], [["“She ought to be flogged in public on a scaffold!”", "\"저런 여자는 처형대에 세워 놓고 사람들이 보는 앞에서 매질을 해야 마땅해요!\""]], [["Alyosha withdrew towards the door.", "알료샤는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."]], [["“But, my God!”", "\"하지만, 오, 하느님!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, clasping her hands.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그 사람이!"], ["“He! He!", "그 사람이!"], ["He could be so dishonorable, so inhuman!", "그 사람이 그토록 비열할 수가, 그토록 인정머리 없을 수가!"], ["Why, he told that creature what happened on that fatal, accursed day!", "아니, 그 사람이 그 운명의, 저주받은 날에 있었던 일을 저 계집에게 다 말해 버렸어요!"], ["‘You brought your beauty for sale, dear young lady.’ She knows it!", "'당신은 당신의 미모를 팔러 다녔어요, 다정한 아가씨.' 저 여자가 그걸 알고 있어요!"], ["Your brother’s a scoundrel, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”", "당신 형은 악당이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""]], [["Alyosha wanted to say something, but he couldn’t find a word.", "알료샤는 무슨 말이든 하고 싶었으나, 한 마디도 찾아낼 수가 없었다."], ["His heart ached.", "그의 가슴이 아렸다."]], [["“Go away, Alexey Fyodorovitch!", "\"가세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치!"], ["It’s shameful, it’s awful for me!", "부끄러워요, 제게는 끔찍한 일이에요!"], ["To-morrow, I beg you on my knees, come to-morrow.", "내일, 무릎 꿇고 부탁드릴게요, 내일 와 주세요."], ["Don’t condemn me.", "저를 나무라지 마세요."], ["Forgive me.", "저를 용서해 주세요."], ["I don’t know what I shall do with myself now!”", "저는 이제 제 자신을 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요!\""]], [["Alyosha walked out into the street reeling.", "알료샤는 비틀거리며 거리로 걸어 나왔다."], ["He could have wept as she did.", "그는 그녀가 그랬듯이 울 수도 있을 것 같았다."], ["Suddenly he was overtaken by the maid.", "별안간 하녀가 그를 뒤쫓아 왔다."]], [["“The young lady forgot to give you this letter from Madame Hohlakov; it’s been left with us since dinner-time.”", "\"아가씨께서 이 편지를 드리는 걸 깜빡하셨어요. 호흘라코바 부인께서 보내신 편지예요. 점심나절부터 저희가 맡아 두고 있었답니다.\""]], [["Alyosha took the little pink envelope mechanically and put it, almost unconsciously, into his pocket.", "알료샤는 그 작은 분홍빛 봉투를 기계적으로 받아 들고는, 거의 무의식적으로 호주머니에 집어넣었다."]], [["# Chapter XI. Another Reputation Ruined", "# Chapter XI. Another Reputation Ruined"]], [["It was not much more than three-quarters of a mile from the town to the monastery.", "수도원은 읍내에서 채 사 분의 삼 마일도 안 되는 거리에 있었다."], ["Alyosha walked quickly along the road, at that hour deserted.", "알료샤는 그 시각이면 인적이 끊긴 길을 따라 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["It was almost night, and too dark to see anything clearly at thirty paces ahead.", "거의 밤이나 다름없어, 서른 걸음 앞도 또렷이 분간하기 어려울 만큼 어두웠다."], ["There were cross-roads half-way.", "길 중간쯤에 갈림길이 있었다."], ["A figure came into sight under a solitary willow at the cross-roads.", "갈림길에 외따로 선 버드나무 아래로 한 사람의 그림자가 눈에 들어왔다."], ["As soon as Alyosha reached the cross-roads the figure moved out and rushed at him, shouting savagely:", "알료샤가 갈림길에 다다르자마자 그 그림자가 불쑥 튀어나와 그에게 덤벼들며 사납게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Your money or your life!”", "\"돈을 내놔라, 아니면 목숨을 내놔라!\""]], [["“So it’s you, Mitya,”", "\"아니, 형이었어, 미탸.\""], ["cried Alyosha, in surprise, violently startled however.", "알료샤가 놀라서 외쳤으나, 그래도 적잖이 가슴이 철렁한 터였다."]], [["“Ha ha ha!", "\"하하하!"], ["You didn’t expect me?", "내가 나타날 줄은 몰랐지?"], ["I wondered where to wait for you.", "어디서 너를 기다릴까 궁리했단다."], ["By her house?", "그 여자 집 앞에서?"], ["There are three ways from it, and I might have missed you.", "거기서 길이 셋으로 갈리니, 너를 놓칠 수도 있었겠지."], ["At last I thought of waiting here, for you had to pass here, there’s no other way to the monastery.", "결국 여기서 기다리기로 했어, 네가 꼭 여기를 지나야 하니 말이야. 수도원으로 가는 길은 이것밖에 없거든."], ["Come, tell me the truth.", "자, 어서 사실대로 말해 봐."], ["Crush me like a beetle.", "나를 딱정벌레처럼 짓밟아 다오."], ["But what’s the matter?”", "그런데 왜 그래?\""]], [["“Nothing, brother—it’s the fright you gave me.", "\"아무것도 아니야, 형—형이 깜짝 놀라게 해서 그래."], ["Oh, Dmitri!", "아아, 드미트리!"], ["Father’s blood just now.”", "조금 전엔 아버지의 피가.\" (알료샤는 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["(Alyosha began to cry, he had been on the verge of tears for a long time, and now something seemed to snap in his soul.) “You almost killed him—cursed him—and now—here—you’re making jokes—‘Your money or your life!’”", "그는 오래전부터 눈물이 그렁그렁한 상태였는데, 이제 마음속에서 무언가가 툭 끊어진 것만 같았다.) \"형은 아버지를 거의 죽일 뻔했고—저주를 퍼부었고—그런데 지금—여기서—농담을 하고 있잖아—'돈을 내놔라, 아니면 목숨을 내놔라!' 하면서.\""]], [["“Well, what of that?", "\"흠, 그게 어쨌단 말이냐?"], ["It’s not seemly—is that it?", "점잖지 못하다—그 말이지?"], ["Not suitable in my position?”", "내 처지에 어울리지 않는다는 거지?\""]], [["“No—I only—”", "\"아니—난 그저—\""]], [["“Stay.", "\"가만있어 봐."], ["Look at the night.", "이 밤을 좀 봐."], ["You see what a dark night, what clouds, what a wind has risen.", "얼마나 어두운 밤인지, 구름은 또 어떤지, 바람이 어떻게 일었는지 보이지?"], ["I hid here under the willow waiting for you.", "나는 여기 버드나무 아래 숨어서 너를 기다렸다."], ["And as God’s above, I suddenly thought, why go on in misery any longer, what is there to wait for?", "그런데, 하늘에 계신 하느님께 맹세코, 문득 이런 생각이 들지 뭐냐, 왜 이런 비참함을 더 끌고 가야 하나, 무얼 더 기다린단 말인가?"], ["Here I have a willow, a handkerchief, a shirt, I can twist them into a rope in a minute, and braces besides, and why go on burdening the earth, dishonoring it with my vile presence?", "여기 버드나무가 있고, 손수건이 있고, 셔츠가 있으니, 단숨에 그것들을 비비 꼬아 밧줄을 만들 수 있고, 게다가 멜빵까지 있으니, 어찌하여 이 더러운 존재로 땅을 짓누르며, 땅을 욕보이면서 계속 살아간단 말인가?"], ["And then I heard you coming—Heavens, it was as though something flew down to me suddenly.", "그런데 그때 네가 오는 소리가 들렸어—맙소사, 마치 무언가가 갑자기 내게로 날아 내려온 것만 같았다. 그러니까, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 하나는 있는 거야."], ["So there is a man, then, whom I love.", "바로 여기 그 사람이, 세상 누구보다도 사랑하는, 세상에서 유일하게 사랑하는 나의 사랑하는 동생이 있는 거야."], ["Here he is, that man, my dear little brother, whom I love more than any one in the world, the only one I love in the world.", "그 순간 나는 너를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 어찌나 사랑했던지, '당장 저 녀석 목을 끌어안아야지' 하고 생각했단다."], ["And I loved you so much, so much at that moment that I thought, ‘I’ll fall on his neck at once.’ Then a stupid idea struck me, to have a joke with you and scare you.", "그러다가 어리석은 생각이 떠올랐어, 너한테 장난을 쳐서 깜짝 놀라게 해 주자고 말이야. 그래서 바보같이 '돈을 내놔라!'"], ["I shouted, like a fool, ‘Your money!’ Forgive my foolery—it was only nonsense, and there’s nothing unseemly in my soul....", "하고 소리친 거야. 내 어리석은 짓을 용서해 다오—그저 헛소리였을 뿐, 내 영혼에는 아무런 점잖지 못한 구석도 없으니까……"], ["Damn it all, tell me what’s happened.", "에라, 빌어먹을, 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 봐."], ["What did she say?", "그 여자가 뭐라고 하더냐?"], ["Strike me, crush me, don’t spare me!", "나를 후려쳐라, 짓밟아라, 봐주지 마라!"], ["Was she furious?”", "그 여자가 노발대발하더냐?\""]], [["“No, not that....", "\"아니, 그런 건 아니야……"], ["There was nothing like that, Mitya.", "그런 일은 전혀 없었어, 미탸."], ["There—I found them both there.”", "그게—난 거기서 두 사람을 다 만났어.\""]], [["“Both?", "\"둘 다?"], ["Whom?”", "누구를?\""]], [["“Grushenka at Katerina Ivanovna’s.”", "\"그루셴카가 카테리나 이바노브나 집에 와 있었어.\""]], [["Dmitri was struck dumb.", "드미트리는 할 말을 잃었다."]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"그럴 리가!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“You’re raving!", "\"헛소리하는 거지!"], ["Grushenka with her?”", "그루셴카가 그 여자랑 같이?\""]], [["Alyosha described all that had happened from the moment he went in to Katerina Ivanovna’s.", "알료샤는 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에 들어선 순간부터 일어난 모든 일을 묘사했다."], ["He was ten minutes telling his story.", "이야기를 하는 데 십 분이 걸렸다."], ["He can’t be said to have told it fluently and consecutively, but he seemed to make it clear, not omitting any word or action of significance, and vividly describing, often in one word, his own sensations.", "그가 유창하고 조리 있게 이야기했다고는 할 수 없으나, 의미 있는 말이나 행동은 하나도 빠뜨리지 않고, 자기 자신의 감정까지 종종 한마디로 생생하게 그려 내며, 사정을 분명하게 전한 듯했다."], ["Dmitri listened in silence, gazing at him with a terrible fixed stare, but it was clear to Alyosha that he understood it all, and had grasped every point.", "드미트리는 무서울 만큼 한곳에 못 박힌 눈길로 그를 응시한 채 잠자코 듣고 있었으나, 그가 모든 것을 이해했고 요점을 빠짐없이 파악했다는 것이 알료샤에게는 분명히 보였다."], ["But as the story went on, his face became not merely gloomy, but menacing.", "그러나 이야기가 진행될수록 그의 얼굴은 단지 음울해지는 데 그치지 않고 위협적으로 변해 갔다."], ["He scowled, he clenched his teeth, and his fixed stare became still more rigid, more concentrated, more terrible, when suddenly, with incredible rapidity, his wrathful, savage face changed, his tightly compressed lips parted, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch broke into uncontrolled, spontaneous laughter.", "그는 인상을 찌푸리고 이를 악물었으며, 못 박힌 눈길은 한층 더 굳어지고 한층 더 집중되고 한층 더 무서워졌는데, 그러다 문득, 믿기지 않을 만큼 빠르게, 분노에 찬 그 사나운 얼굴이 싹 바뀌더니, 꽉 다물려 있던 입술이 벌어지며 드미트리 표도로비치가 걷잡을 수 없이 절로 터져 나오는 웃음을 폭발시켰다."], ["He literally shook with laughter.", "그는 말 그대로 몸을 들썩이며 웃어 댔다."], ["For a long time he could not speak.", "한참 동안 그는 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["“So she wouldn’t kiss her hand!", "\"그래, 그 여자가 손에 입을 맞추려 하지 않았다는 거지!"], ["So she didn’t kiss it; so she ran away!”", "입을 맞추지 않고, 달아나 버렸다는 거지!\""], ["he kept exclaiming with hysterical delight; insolent delight it might have been called, if it had not been so spontaneous.", "그는 히스테릭한 환희에 차서 거듭 외쳐 댔는데, 그것이 그토록 절로 우러난 것이 아니었다면 뻔뻔스러운 환희라 불러도 좋았을 것이다."], ["“So the other one called her tigress!", "\"그래서 그쪽이 저 여자를 암호랑이라고 불렀다는 거지!"], ["And a tigress she is!", "암호랑이가 맞고말고!"], ["So she ought to be flogged on a scaffold?", "그러니 형틀에 묶어 매질을 당해야 한다고?"], ["Yes, yes, so she ought.", "그래, 그래, 그래야지."], ["That’s just what I think; she ought to have been long ago.", "내 생각도 바로 그래. 진작에 그랬어야지."], ["It’s like this, brother, let her be punished, but I must get better first.", "이런 거야, 동생아, 그 여자를 벌하되, 나는 먼저 나아져야 해."], ["I understand the queen of impudence.", "나는 그 뻔뻔함의 여왕을 잘 알아."], ["That’s her all over!", "그게 바로 그 여자다워!"], ["You saw her all over in that hand-kissing, the she-devil!", "너도 그 손에 입맞춤 사건에서 그 여자의 진면목을 송두리째 봤겠지, 그 악녀 말이다!"], ["She’s magnificent in her own line!", "그 여자는 제 분야에선 정말 대단하다니까!"], ["So she ran home?", "그래서 집으로 달아났다고?"], ["I’ll go—ah—I’ll run to her!", "가야지—아—그 여자한테 달려가야지!"], ["Alyosha, don’t blame me, I agree that hanging is too good for her.”", "알료샤, 나를 탓하지 마라, 그런 여자한테는 목을 매다는 것도 과분하다는 데 나도 동의하니까.\""]], [["“But Katerina Ivanovna!”", "\"하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는!\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha sorrowfully.", "알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다."]], [["“I see her, too!", "\"그 여자도 보인다, 나는!"], ["I see right through her, as I’ve never done before!", "이제껏 한 번도 못 했던 것처럼 그 여자 속까지 훤히 꿰뚫어 보인다고!"], ["It’s a regular discovery of the four continents of the world, that is, of the five!", "이건 세계 사 대륙을 발견한 거나 마찬가지야, 아니, 오 대륙을 발견한 셈이지!"], ["What a thing to do!", "그런 짓을 하다니!"], ["That’s just like Katya, who was not afraid to face a coarse, unmannerly officer and risk a deadly insult on a generous impulse to save her father!", "그게 바로 카탸다워, 너그러운 충동에 사로잡혀 아버지를 구하겠다고 거칠고 무례한 장교 앞에 마주 서서 치명적인 모욕을 무릅쓰기를 두려워하지 않던 그 카탸 말이야!"], ["But the pride, the recklessness, the defiance of fate, the unbounded defiance!", "하지만 그 오만함, 그 무모함, 운명에 대한 그 도전, 끝 간 데 없는 도전이라니!"], ["You say that aunt tried to stop her?", "이모가 말리려 들었다고 했지?"], ["That aunt, you know, is overbearing, herself.", "그 이모란 사람도, 알다시피, 위세 부리기 좋아하는 여자야."], ["She’s the sister of the general’s widow in Moscow, and even more stuck-up than she.", "모스크바에 있는 장군 미망인의 동생인데, 그 언니보다도 더 콧대가 높지."], ["But her husband was caught stealing government money.", "그런데 남편이 관청 돈을 빼돌리다 들켰단다."], ["He lost everything, his estate and all, and the proud wife had to lower her colors, and hasn’t raised them since.", "영지를 비롯해 전 재산을 죄다 날렸고, 그 오만한 부인도 깃발을 내려야 했고, 그 후로 다시는 올리지 못했지."], ["So she tried to prevent Katya, but she wouldn’t listen to her!", "그래서 카탸를 말리려 들었지만, 카탸가 들은 척이나 했겠어!"], ["She thinks she can overcome everything, that everything will give way to her.", "그 여자는 자기가 무엇이든 이겨 낼 수 있다고, 만사가 자기 앞에 굴복할 거라고 믿거든."], ["She thought she could bewitch Grushenka if she liked, and she believed it herself: she plays a part to herself, and whose fault is it?", "마음만 먹으면 그루셴카쯤 홀려 버릴 수 있다고 생각했고, 자기도 그렇게 믿었던 거야. 그 여자는 자기 자신을 향해 연극을 하는 거다, 그게 누구 탓이겠어?"], ["Do you think she kissed Grushenka’s hand first, on purpose, with a motive?", "그 여자가 일부러, 어떤 속셈을 품고 먼저 그루셴카의 손에 입을 맞췄다고 생각하느냐?"], ["No, she really was fascinated by Grushenka, that’s to say, not by Grushenka, but by her own dream, her own delusion—because it was her dream, her delusion!", "아니야, 그 여자는 정말로 그루셴카에게 매혹됐던 거다, 그러니까 그루셴카가 아니라 자기 자신의 꿈에, 자기 자신의 환상에 말이야—그게 그 여자 자신의 꿈이고, 그 여자 자신의 환상이었으니까!"], ["Alyosha, darling, how did you escape from them, those women?", "알료샤, 얘야, 너는 그 여자들한테서 어떻게 빠져나왔니?"], ["Did you pick up your cassock and run?", "수도복 자락을 걷어 올리고 냅다 달아났냐?"], ["Ha ha ha!”", "하하하!\""]], [["“Brother, you don’t seem to have noticed how you’ve insulted Katerina Ivanovna by telling Grushenka about that day.", "\"형, 형은 그루셴카에게 그날 일을 떠벌려서 카테리나 이바노브나에게 얼마나 큰 모욕을 주었는지 알아채지도 못한 것 같네."], ["And she flung it in her face just now that she had gone to gentlemen in secret to sell her beauty!", "그루셴카는 조금 전 카테리나 이바노브나의 면전에다 대고, 그 여자가 자기 미모를 팔려고 몰래 신사들을 찾아다녔다고 쏘아붙였다고!"], ["Brother, what could be worse than that insult?”", "형, 그보다 더한 모욕이 어디 있겠어?\""]], [["What worried Alyosha more than anything was that, incredible as it seemed, his brother appeared pleased at Katerina Ivanovna’s humiliation.", "알료샤를 무엇보다 괴롭힌 것은, 믿기지 않는 일이지만, 형이 카테리나 이바노브나의 굴욕을 도리어 기뻐하는 것처럼 보인다는 점이었다."]], [["“Bah!”", "\"이런!\""], ["Dmitri frowned fiercely, and struck his forehead with his hand.", "드미트리가 사납게 얼굴을 찌푸리고는 손으로 제 이마를 쳤다."], ["He only now realized it, though Alyosha had just told him of the insult, and Katerina Ivanovna’s cry: “Your brother is a scoundrel!”", "알료샤가 방금 그 모욕에 대해, 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나가 \"당신 형은 비열한 인간이에요!\" 하고 외친 일까지 말해 주었건만, 그는 이제야 그것을 깨달은 것이다."]], [["“Yes, perhaps, I really did tell Grushenka about that ‘fatal day,’ as Katya calls it.", "\"그래, 어쩌면, 정말로 나는 그루셴카에게 그 '운명의 날' 이야기를 했어, 카탸가 부르는 대로 하자면 말이야."], ["Yes, I did tell her, I remember!", "그래, 내가 말했지, 기억나!"], ["It was that time at Mokroe.", "그게 모크로예에서였어."], ["I was drunk, the gypsies were singing....", "나는 취해 있었고, 집시들이 노래를 부르고 있었지……"], ["But I was sobbing.", "하지만 나는 흐느끼고 있었어."], ["I was sobbing then, kneeling and praying to Katya’s image, and Grushenka understood it.", "그때 나는 흐느끼면서, 무릎을 꿇고 카탸의 초상을 향해 기도하고 있었는데, 그루셴카가 그걸 알아챈 거야."], ["She understood it all then.", "그 여자는 그때 모든 걸 이해했어."], ["I remember, she cried herself....", "기억나, 그 여자도 울었지…… 빌어먹을!"], ["Damn it all! But it’s bound to be so now....", "하지만 이제는 이렇게 될 수밖에 없는 거야……"], ["Then she cried, but now ‘the dagger in the heart’!", "그때는 울었지만, 이제는 '가슴에 비수를 꽂는다'는 거지!"], ["That’s how women are.”", "여자란 다 그래.\""]], [["He looked down and sank into thought.", "그는 고개를 떨구고 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Yes, I am a scoundrel, a thorough scoundrel!”", "\"그래, 나는 비열한 놈이야, 철두철미한 비열한 놈이야!\""], ["he said suddenly, in a gloomy voice.", "그가 문득 음울한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It doesn’t matter whether I cried or not, I’m a scoundrel!", "\"내가 울었든 안 울었든 그건 중요하지 않아, 나는 비열한 놈이야!"], ["Tell her I accept the name, if that’s any comfort.", "그 여자한테 전해 다오, 그 이름을 받아들이겠다고, 그게 조금이라도 위안이 된다면."], ["Come, that’s enough.", "자, 이제 됐다."], ["Good-by.", "잘 가라."], ["It’s no use talking!", "더 말해 봐야 소용없어!"], ["It’s not amusing.", "즐거운 일도 아니고."], ["You go your way and I mine.", "너는 네 갈 길로, 나는 내 갈 길로 가는 거다."], ["And I don’t want to see you again except as a last resource.", "그리고 나는 너를, 최후의 수단이 아니고서는 다시 보고 싶지 않다."], ["Good-by, Alexey!”", "잘 가라, 알렉세이!\""]], [["He warmly pressed Alyosha’s hand, and still looking down, without raising his head, as though tearing himself away, turned rapidly towards the town.", "그는 알료샤의 손을 뜨겁게 움켜쥐고는, 여전히 고개를 떨군 채 한 번도 들지 않고, 마치 자신을 억지로 떼어 내듯이 빠르게 읍내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Alyosha looked after him, unable to believe he would go away so abruptly.", "알료샤는, 형이 그토록 갑작스레 가 버리리라고는 믿기지 않아 그 뒷모습을 바라보았다."]], [["“Stay, Alexey, one more confession to you alone!”", "\"가만, 알렉세이, 너한테만 한 가지 더 고백할 게 있다!\""], ["cried Dmitri, suddenly turning back.", "드미트리가 문득 돌아서며 외쳤다."], ["“Look at me.", "\"나를 봐."], ["Look at me well.", "나를 잘 봐."], ["You see here, here—there’s terrible disgrace in store for me.” (As he said “here,”", "여기, 여기—나한테는 끔찍한 치욕이 기다리고 있어.\""], ["Dmitri struck his chest with his fist with a strange air, as though the dishonor lay precisely on his chest, in some spot, in a pocket, perhaps, or hanging round his neck.) “You know me now, a scoundrel, an avowed scoundrel, but let me tell you that I’ve never done anything before and never shall again, anything that can compare in baseness with the dishonor which I bear now at this very minute on my breast, here, here, which will come to pass, though I’m perfectly free to stop it.", "(그는 \"여기\"라고 말하면서 묘한 표정으로 주먹을 쥐어 제 가슴을 쳤는데, 마치 그 치욕이 바로 그 가슴 어딘가, 어떤 자리, 이를테면 주머니 속에 들어 있거나 목에 걸려 있기라도 한 것 같았다.) \"이제 너는 나를 알지, 비열한 놈, 자타가 공인하는 비열한 놈이라고 말이야. 하지만 한 가지 말해 두마, 나는 이제껏 단 한 번도, 그리고 앞으로도 결코, 바로 이 순간 내 가슴에, 여기, 여기에 지고 있는 이 치욕에 비길 만큼 비열한 짓은 한 적도 없고 하지도 않을 거다."], ["I can stop it or carry it through, note that.", "이 치욕은 기어이 일어나고 말 거야, 내가 얼마든지 멈출 수 있는데도 말이지."], ["Well, let me tell you, I shall carry it through.", "나는 그것을 멈출 수도 있고 끝까지 밀고 나갈 수도 있어, 명심해 둬. 그래, 내가 말해 두마, 나는 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 거다."], ["I shan’t stop it.", "멈추지 않을 거야."], ["I told you everything just now, but I didn’t tell you this, because even I had not brass enough for it.", "조금 전에 너한테 다 말했지만, 이것만은 말하지 않았어, 나조차도 그걸 입에 올릴 만큼 뻔뻔하지 못했으니까."], ["I can still pull up; if I do, I can give back the full half of my lost honor to-morrow.", "나는 아직도 멈출 수 있어. 멈추기만 하면 내일이라도 잃어버린 내 명예의 절반을 고스란히 되찾을 수 있지."], ["But I shan’t pull up.", "하지만 나는 멈추지 않을 거다."], ["I shall carry out my base plan, and you can bear witness that I told you so beforehand.", "나는 내 비열한 계획을 끝까지 해치울 거야, 그리고 너는 내가 미리 그렇게 말했다는 걸 증언할 수 있는 거다."], ["Darkness and destruction!", "어둠과 파멸이라니!"], ["No need to explain.", "설명할 필요 없어."], ["You’ll find out in due time.", "때가 되면 너도 알게 될 테니까."], ["The filthy back-alley and the she-devil.", "그 더러운 뒷골목과 그 악녀 말이야."], ["Good-by.", "잘 가라."], ["Don’t pray for me, I’m not worth it.", "나를 위해 기도하지 마라, 나는 그럴 가치가 없어."], ["And there’s no need, no need at all....", "그리고 그럴 필요도 없고, 전혀 필요 없어……"], ["I don’t need it!", "나한테는 그게 필요 없어!"], ["Away!”", "저리 가!\""]], [["And he suddenly retreated, this time finally.", "그러고는 그가 문득 물러갔는데, 이번에는 정말로 마지막이었다."], ["Alyosha went towards the monastery.", "알료샤는 수도원 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["I shall never see him again!", "다시는 형을 못 본다고?"], ["What is he saying?”", "형은 대체 무슨 소리를 하는 거지?\""], ["he wondered wildly.", "그는 어지러운 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“Why, I shall certainly see him to-morrow.", "\"아니야, 내일이면 분명히 형을 보게 될 거야. 내가 형을 찾아갈 거야."], ["I shall look him up.", "꼭 그러고 말 거야."], ["I shall make a point of it. What does he mean?”", "형은 대체 무슨 뜻으로 그런 말을 한 걸까?\""]], [["He went round the monastery, and crossed the pine-wood to the hermitage.", "그는 수도원을 빙 돌아 솔숲을 가로질러 암자로 갔다."], ["The door was opened to him, though no one was admitted at that hour.", "그 시각이면 아무도 들이지 않는데도, 그에게는 문이 열렸다."], ["There was a tremor in his heart as he went into Father Zossima’s cell.", "그가 조시마 장로의 암실로 들어설 때 가슴이 떨려 왔다."]], [["“Why, why, had he gone forth?", "\"어찌하여, 어찌하여 장로님은 나를 밖으로 내보내셨던가?"], ["Why had he sent him into the world?", "어찌하여 나를 세상으로 보내셨던가?"], ["Here was peace.", "여기에는 평화가 있다."], ["Here was holiness.", "여기에는 거룩함이 있다."], ["But there was confusion, there was darkness in which one lost one’s way and went astray at once....”", "그러나 저기에는 혼란이 있고, 자칫 길을 잃고 단번에 헤매게 되는 어둠이 있다…….\""]], [["In the cell he found the novice Porfiry and Father Païssy, who came every hour to inquire after Father Zossima.", "암실에서 그는 수련 수사 포르피리와 파이시 신부를 만났는데, 파이시 신부는 한 시간에 한 번씩 조시마 장로의 안부를 살피러 오고 있었다."], ["Alyosha learnt with alarm that he was getting worse and worse.", "알료샤는 장로의 병세가 점점 더 나빠지고 있다는 것을 알고 가슴이 철렁했다."], ["Even his usual discourse with the brothers could not take place that day.", "그날은 수사들과 늘 나누던 담화조차 열릴 수 없었다."], ["As a rule every evening after service the monks flocked into Father Zossima’s cell, and all confessed aloud their sins of the day, their sinful thoughts and temptations; even their disputes, if there had been any.", "평소 매일 저녁 예배가 끝나면 수사들은 조시마 장로의 암실로 모여들어, 그날 지은 죄와 죄스러운 생각과 유혹을, 다툼이 있었다면 그 다툼까지도 모두 소리 내어 고백하곤 했다."], ["Some confessed kneeling.", "더러는 무릎을 꿇고 고백하기도 했다."], ["The elder absolved, reconciled, exhorted, imposed penance, blessed, and dismissed them. It was against this general “confession”", "장로는 죄를 사하고, 화해시키고, 타이르고, 보속을 명하고, 축복한 뒤 그들을 돌려보냈다."], ["that the opponents of “elders” protested, maintaining that it was a profanation of the sacrament of confession, almost a sacrilege, though this was quite a different thing.", "\"장로 제도\"의 반대자들이 항의한 것이 바로 이 일반적인 \"고백\"이었으니, 그들은 그것이 고해성사를 모독하는 것이며, 비록 실은 전혀 다른 것이긴 했으나, 거의 신성모독에 가깝다고 주장했다."], ["They even represented to the diocesan authorities that such confessions attained no good object, but actually to a large extent led to sin and temptation.", "그들은 심지어 교구 당국에 이런 고백이 아무런 선한 목적도 이루지 못할뿐더러 실제로는 도리어 죄와 유혹으로 크게 이끈다고까지 진정했다."], ["Many of the brothers disliked going to the elder, and went against their own will because every one went, and for fear they should be accused of pride and rebellious ideas.", "적잖은 수사들이 장로에게 가기를 꺼렸으나, 모두가 가는 데다 교만하다거나 반항적인 생각을 품었다는 비난을 받을까 두려워, 제 뜻과는 달리 마지못해 갔다."], ["People said that some of the monks agreed beforehand, saying, “I’ll confess I lost my temper with you this morning, and you confirm it,”", "사람들 말로는, 더러 수사들끼리 미리 입을 맞추어 \"내가 오늘 아침에 너한테 화를 냈다고 고백할 테니, 너는 그걸 맞장구쳐 다오\""], ["simply in order to have something to say. Alyosha knew that this actually happened sometimes.", "하고 약조했다는데, 그저 무슨 할 말이라도 있어야 했기 때문이었다."], ["He knew, too, that there were among the monks some who deeply resented the fact that letters from relations were habitually taken to the elder, to be opened and read by him before those to whom they were addressed.", "알료샤는 실제로 가끔 그런 일이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 또한 그는 친지들에게서 온 편지를 으레 장로에게 가져가, 수신인이 읽기 전에 장로가 먼저 뜯어 읽는다는 사실에 깊이 분개하는 수사들이 더러 있다는 것도 알고 있었다."]], [["It was assumed, of course, that all this was done freely, and in good faith, by way of voluntary submission and salutary guidance.", "물론 이 모든 것은 자유롭게, 선의로, 자발적인 복종과 유익한 인도의 차원에서 이루어지는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["But, in fact, there was sometimes no little insincerity, and much that was false and strained in this practice.", "그러나 실상 이 관습에는 때로 적잖은 위선이 있었고, 거짓되고 억지스러운 구석도 많았다. 그래도 더 나이 들고 노련한 수사들은 자기네 의견을 고수하며 이렇게 주장했다."], ["Yet the older and more experienced of the monks adhered to their opinion, arguing that “for those who have come within these walls sincerely seeking salvation, such obedience and sacrifice will certainly be salutary and of great benefit; those, on the other hand, who find it irksome, and repine, are no true monks, and have made a mistake in entering the monastery—their proper place is in the world.", "\"진심으로 구원을 찾아 이 담장 안으로 들어온 이들에게는 그러한 순종과 희생이 반드시 유익하고 큰 도움이 될 것이다. 반면에 그것을 성가셔하고 투덜대는 이들은 참된 수도사가 아니며 수도원에 들어온 것이 잘못이니, 그런 자들이 있을 곳은 바로 세속이다."], ["Even in the temple one cannot be safe from sin and the devil.", "성전 안에 있더라도 죄와 악마로부터 안전할 수는 없는 법이다."], ["So it was no good taking it too much into account.”", "그러니 그 일을 지나치게 마음에 두어 봐야 소용없다.\""]], [["“He is weaker, a drowsiness has come over him,”", "\"장로님은 더 쇠약해지셨고, 졸음에 빠져드셨네.\""], ["Father Païssy whispered to Alyosha, as he blessed him.", "파이시 신부가 알료샤를 축복하며 그에게 속삭였다."], ["“It’s difficult to rouse him.", "\"깨우기가 어렵네."], ["And he must not be roused.", "그리고 깨워서도 안 되네."], ["He waked up for five minutes, sent his blessing to the brothers, and begged their prayers for him at night.", "오 분 동안 잠을 깨셨다가, 수사들에게 축복을 보내시고 밤에 자기를 위해 기도해 달라 청하셨네."], ["He intends to take the sacrament again in the morning.", "아침에 다시 성체를 받으실 생각이라네."], ["He remembered you, Alexey.", "자네를 기억하셨어, 알렉세이."], ["He asked whether you had gone away, and was told that you were in the town.", "자네가 떠났느냐고 물으시기에 읍내에 가 있다고 여쭈었지."], ["‘I blessed him for that work,’ he said, ‘his place is there, not here, for awhile.’ Those were his words about you.", "'나는 그 일을 위해 그를 축복해 보냈으니, 한동안 그가 있을 곳은 여기가 아니라 거기다' 하고 말씀하셨네."], ["He remembered you lovingly, with anxiety; do you understand how he honored you?", "자네에 관한 말씀이 그러했네. 자네를 다정하게, 걱정스러운 마음으로 기억하셨어."], ["But how is it that he has decided that you shall spend some time in the world?", "장로님이 자네를 얼마나 귀히 여기셨는지 알겠는가? 그런데 어찌하여 자네가 한동안 세속에서 지내야 한다고 결정하신 걸까?"], ["He must have foreseen something in your destiny!", "필시 자네의 운명에서 무언가를 미리 내다보신 게야!"], ["Understand, Alexey, that if you return to the world, it must be to do the duty laid upon you by your elder, and not for frivolous vanity and worldly pleasures.”", "알아 두게, 알렉세이, 자네가 세속으로 돌아간다면, 그것은 자네 장로께서 맡기신 의무를 다하기 위해서이지 경박한 허영이나 세속의 쾌락을 위해서가 아니라네.\""]], [["Father Païssy went out.", "파이시 신부가 나갔다."], ["Alyosha had no doubt that Father Zossima was dying, though he might live another day or two.", "알료샤는 조시마 장로가 비록 하루 이틀쯤 더 사실 수는 있을지라도 임종을 앞두고 있다는 것을 의심치 않았다."], ["Alyosha firmly and ardently resolved that in spite of his promises to his father, the Hohlakovs, and Katerina Ivanovna, he would not leave the monastery next day, but would remain with his elder to the end.", "알료샤는, 아버지와 호흘라코바 모녀와 카테리나 이바노브나에게 한 약속에도 불구하고, 이튿날 수도원을 떠나지 않고 마지막까지 장로 곁에 남으리라 굳고도 뜨겁게 결심했다."], ["His heart glowed with love, and he reproached himself bitterly for having been able for one instant to forget him whom he had left in the monastery on his deathbed, and whom he honored above every one in the world.", "그의 가슴은 사랑으로 타올랐고, 임종의 자리에서 수도원에 남겨 두고 온, 세상 누구보다도 귀히 여기는 그분을 단 한순간이나마 잊을 수 있었다는 사실로 그는 자신을 쓰라리게 책망했다."], ["He went into Father Zossima’s bedroom, knelt down, and bowed to the ground before the elder, who slept quietly without stirring, with regular, hardly audible breathing and a peaceful face.", "그는 조시마 장로의 침실로 들어가 무릎을 꿇고 장로 앞에 엎드려 땅에 이마를 대었는데, 장로는 미동도 없이 조용히, 고르게, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 숨을 쉬며 평온한 얼굴로 잠들어 있었다."]], [["Alyosha returned to the other room, where Father Zossima had received his guests in the morning.", "알료샤는 아침에 조시마 장로가 손님들을 맞이했던 다른 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["Taking off his boots, he lay down on the hard, narrow, leathern sofa, which he had long used as a bed, bringing nothing but a pillow.", "그는 장화를 벗고, 오래전부터 침대로 써 온 딱딱하고 비좁은 가죽 소파에 누웠으니, 베개 하나 말고는 아무것도 가져다 두지 않은 자리였다."], ["The mattress, about which his father had shouted to him that morning, he had long forgotten to lie on.", "그날 아침 아버지가 그에게 소리치며 운운하던 그 매트리스는, 그가 그 위에 눕는 것을 잊은 지 이미 오래였다."], ["He took off his cassock, which he used as a covering.", "그는 덮개로 쓰는 수도복을 벗었다."], ["But before going to bed, he fell on his knees and prayed a long time.", "그러나 잠자리에 들기 전에 그는 무릎을 꿇고 오랫동안 기도했다."], ["In his fervent prayer he did not beseech God to lighten his darkness but only thirsted for the joyous emotion, which always visited his soul after the praise and adoration, of which his evening prayer usually consisted.", "그 간절한 기도 속에서 그는 자신의 어둠을 밝혀 달라고 하느님께 빌지 않았고, 다만 그의 저녁 기도가 으레 그렇듯 찬미와 경배 끝에 언제나 그의 영혼을 찾아오던 그 기쁨에 찬 감격을 갈망할 뿐이었다."], ["That joy always brought him light untroubled sleep.", "그 기쁨은 늘 그에게 밝고 평온한 잠을 가져다주었다."], ["As he was praying, he suddenly felt in his pocket the little pink note the servant had handed him as he left Katerina Ivanovna’s.", "기도를 드리던 중, 그는 문득 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 나설 때 하인이 건네준 작은 분홍빛 쪽지가 주머니에 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He was disturbed, but finished his prayer.", "그는 마음이 어수선해졌으나 기도를 마쳤다."], ["Then, after some hesitation, he opened the envelope.", "그러고는 잠시 망설인 끝에 봉투를 뜯었다."], ["In it was a letter to him, signed by Lise, the young daughter of Madame Hohlakov, who had laughed at him before the elder in the morning.", "그 안에는 그에게 보내는 편지가 있었는데, 그날 아침 장로 앞에서 그를 비웃었던 호흘라코바 부인의 어린 딸 리즈가 서명한 것이었다."]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["she wrote, “I am writing to you without any one’s knowledge, even mamma’s, and I know how wrong it is.", "그녀는 이렇게 썼다. \"저는 아무도, 엄마조차도 모르게 당신께 이 글을 씁니다, 이것이 얼마나 잘못된 일인지 알면서도요."], ["But I cannot live without telling you the feeling that has sprung up in my heart, and this no one but us two must know for a time.", "하지만 저는 제 마음속에 솟아난 이 감정을 당신께 말하지 않고는 살 수가 없어요, 그리고 이건 한동안 우리 둘 말고는 누구도 알아서는 안 돼요."], ["But how am I to say what I want so much to tell you?", "그런데 그토록 말하고 싶은 것을 어떻게 말해야 할까요?"], ["Paper, they say, does not blush, but I assure you it’s not true and that it’s blushing just as I am now, all over.", "종이는 얼굴을 붉히지 않는다고들 하지만, 단언컨대 그건 사실이 아니에요, 지금 이 종이도 꼭 지금의 저처럼 온통 새빨개져 있어요."], ["Dear Alyosha, I love you, I’ve loved you from my childhood, since our Moscow days, when you were very different from what you are now, and I shall love you all my life.", "사랑하는 알료샤, 저는 당신을 사랑해요, 어린 시절부터, 당신이 지금과는 아주 달랐던 그 모스크바 시절부터 당신을 사랑해 왔어요, 그리고 평생토록 당신을 사랑할 거예요."], ["My heart has chosen you, to unite our lives, and pass them together till our old age.", "제 마음은 당신을 골랐어요, 우리 두 사람의 삶을 하나로 합쳐 늙어 갈 때까지 함께 보내려고요."], ["Of course, on condition that you will leave the monastery.", "물론 당신이 수도원을 떠난다는 조건으로요."], ["As for our age we will wait for the time fixed by the law.", "나이로 말하자면 법이 정한 때까지 기다리면 되고요."], ["By that time I shall certainly be quite strong, I shall be walking and dancing.", "그때쯤이면 저는 분명히 아주 튼튼해져서, 걷고 춤도 출 거예요."], ["There can be no doubt of that.", "그건 의심할 여지가 없어요."]], [["“You see how I’ve thought of everything.", "저 보세요, 제가 모든 걸 다 생각해 두었지요."], ["There’s only one thing I can’t imagine: what you’ll think of me when you read this.", "다만 한 가지만은 도무지 그려지지 않아요. 당신이 이걸 읽고 저를 어떻게 생각하실까 하는 거예요."], ["I’m always laughing and being naughty.", "저는 늘 웃고 까불기만 하잖아요."], ["I made you angry this morning, but I assure you before I took up my pen, I prayed before the Image of the Mother of God, and now I’m praying, and almost crying.", "오늘 아침엔 당신을 화나게 했지만, 단언컨대 저는 펜을 들기 전에 성모님의 성상 앞에서 기도했고, 지금도 기도하면서 거의 울고 있어요."]], [["“My secret is in your hands.", "제 비밀은 당신 손안에 있어요."], ["When you come to-morrow, I don’t know how I shall look at you.", "내일 당신이 오면 제가 당신을 어떻게 바라볼 수 있을지 모르겠어요."], ["Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, what if I can’t restrain myself like a silly and laugh when I look at you as I did to-day.", "아아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 만일 제가 바보처럼 자제하지 못하고 오늘처럼 당신을 보며 웃어 버리면 어쩌죠."], ["You’ll think I’m a nasty girl making fun of you, and you won’t believe my letter.", "그럼 당신은 제가 당신을 놀리는 못된 계집애라고 생각하고, 제 편지를 믿지 않으실 거예요."], ["And so I beg you, dear one, if you’ve any pity for me, when you come to-morrow, don’t look me straight in the face, for if I meet your eyes, it will be sure to make me laugh, especially as you’ll be in that long gown.", "그러니 부탁드려요, 사랑하는 분, 저를 조금이라도 가엾이 여기신다면, 내일 오실 때 제 얼굴을 똑바로 보지 마세요, 만일 당신과 눈이 마주치면 틀림없이 웃음이 터질 테니까요, 더구나 당신이 그 긴 옷을 입고 있을 테니 더욱 그럴 거예요."], ["I feel cold all over when I think of it, so when you come, don’t look at me at all for a time, look at mamma or at the window....", "그 생각만 해도 온몸이 오싹해지니, 오시거든 한동안 저를 아예 보지 마시고 엄마나 창문 쪽을 보세요……"]], [["“Here I’ve written you a love-letter.", "이렇게 저는 당신께 연애편지를 쓰고 말았네요."], ["Oh, dear, what have I done?", "어머나, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요?"], ["Alyosha, don’t despise me, and if I’ve done something very horrid and wounded you, forgive me.", "알료샤, 저를 경멸하지 마세요, 그리고 제가 아주 끔찍한 짓을 해서 당신께 상처를 입혔다면 용서해 주세요."], ["Now the secret of my reputation, ruined perhaps for ever, is in your hands.", "이제 어쩌면 영영 망가져 버렸을지도 모를 제 평판의 비밀이 당신 손안에 있어요."]], [["“I shall certainly cry to-day.", "저는 오늘 틀림없이 울 거예요."], ["Good-by till our meeting, our awful meeting.—LISE.", "우리가 만날 때까지, 그 무시무시한 만남까지, 안녕히.—리즈."]], [["“P.S.—Alyosha!", "추신.—알료샤!"], ["You must, must, must come!—LISE.”", "당신은 꼭, 꼭, 꼭 와야 해요!—리즈.\""]], [["Alyosha read the note in amazement, read it through twice, thought a little, and suddenly laughed a soft, sweet laugh.", "알료샤는 어리둥절해하며 그 쪽지를 읽었는데, 두 번을 거듭 읽고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 문득 부드럽고 다정한 웃음을 지었다."], ["He started.", "그는 흠칫했다."], ["That laugh seemed to him sinful.", "그 웃음이 그에게는 죄스럽게 느껴졌던 것이다."], ["But a minute later he laughed again just as softly and happily.", "그러나 잠시 후 그는 또다시 그처럼 부드럽고 행복하게 웃었다."], ["He slowly replaced the note in the envelope, crossed himself and lay down.", "그는 천천히 쪽지를 봉투에 도로 넣고, 성호를 그은 뒤 자리에 누웠다."], ["The agitation in his heart passed at once.", "가슴속의 동요는 단번에 가라앉았다."], ["“God, have mercy upon all of them, have all these unhappy and turbulent souls in Thy keeping, and set them in the right path.", "\"하느님, 그들 모두에게 자비를 베푸시고, 이 불행하고 소란스러운 영혼들을 모두 당신의 보호 아래 거두시어 바른길로 인도하소서."], ["All ways are Thine.", "모든 길은 당신의 것이옵니다."], ["Save them according to Thy wisdom.", "당신의 지혜로 그들을 구원하소서."], ["Thou art love.", "당신은 사랑이옵니다."], ["Thou wilt send joy to all!”", "당신께서 모두에게 기쁨을 보내 주실 것이옵니다!\""], ["Alyosha murmured, crossing himself, and falling into peaceful sleep.", "알료샤는 성호를 그으며 이렇게 중얼거리고는, 평온한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["# PART II Book IV. Lacerations Chapter I. Father Ferapont", "# PART II Book IV. Lacerations Chapter I. Father Ferapont"]], [["Alyosha was roused early, before daybreak.", "알료샤는 동트기 전 이른 시각에 깨어났다."], ["Father Zossima woke up feeling very weak, though he wanted to get out of bed and sit up in a chair.", "조시마 장로는 잠에서 깨어 몹시 쇠약함을 느끼면서도 침대에서 일어나 안락의자에 앉기를 원했다."], ["His mind was quite clear; his face looked very tired, yet bright and almost joyful.", "정신만은 아주 또렷했고, 얼굴은 무척 지쳐 보였으나 그래도 환하고 거의 기쁨에 차 있었다."], ["It wore an expression of gayety, kindness and cordiality.", "그 얼굴에는 명랑함과 자애로움, 다정함이 어려 있었다."], ["“Maybe I shall not live through the coming day,”", "\"어쩌면 다가오는 하루를 넘기지 못할지도 모르겠구나.\""], ["he said to Alyosha.", "그가 알료샤에게 말했다."], ["Then he desired to confess and take the sacrament at once.", "그러고는 곧바로 고해를 하고 성체를 받기를 원했다."], ["He always confessed to Father Païssy.", "그는 늘 파이시 신부에게 고해했다."], ["After taking the communion, the service of extreme unction followed.", "영성체를 받은 뒤에는 종부성사가 이어졌다."], ["The monks assembled and the cell was gradually filled up by the inmates of the hermitage.", "수도사들이 모여들었고, 암자는 차츰 은수처에 거하는 이들로 가득 찼다."], ["Meantime it was daylight.", "그러는 사이 날이 밝았다."], ["People began coming from the monastery.", "사람들이 수도원에서 찾아오기 시작했다."], ["After the service was over the elder desired to kiss and take leave of every one.", "예배가 끝나자 장로는 한 사람 한 사람에게 입맞추고 작별을 고하기를 원했다."], ["As the cell was so small the earlier visitors withdrew to make room for others.", "암자가 워낙 비좁은 탓에 먼저 온 방문객들은 자리를 비워 주려고 물러났다."], ["Alyosha stood beside the elder, who was seated again in his arm-chair.", "알료샤는 다시 안락의자에 앉은 장로의 곁에 서 있었다."], ["He talked as much as he could.", "장로는 할 수 있는 한 많은 말을 했다."], ["Though his voice was weak, it was fairly steady.", "목소리는 약했으나 제법 또렷했다."]], [["“I’ve been teaching you so many years, and therefore I’ve been talking aloud so many years, that I’ve got into the habit of talking, and so much so that it’s almost more difficult for me to hold my tongue than to talk, even now, in spite of my weakness, dear Fathers and brothers,”", "\"내가 그토록 여러 해 그대들을 가르쳐 왔고, 그렇기에 그토록 여러 해 소리 내어 말해 왔으니, 이제는 말하는 것이 습관이 되어 버려, 지금 이 쇠약한 처지에서도 입을 다물고 있기가 말하기보다 도리어 더 어려울 지경이라오, 사랑하는 신부님들과 형제들이여.\""], ["he jested, looking with emotion at the group round him.", "그는 둘러선 무리를 감격에 젖은 눈으로 바라보며 농을 던졌다."]], [["Alyosha remembered afterwards something of what he said to them.", "알료샤는 훗날 장로가 그들에게 한 말의 일부를 기억해 냈다."], ["But though he spoke out distinctly and his voice was fairly steady, his speech was somewhat disconnected.", "그러나 장로가 또렷하게 말했고 그 목소리도 제법 한결같았건만, 그 말은 어딘가 두서가 없었다."], ["He spoke of many things, he seemed anxious before the moment of death to say everything he had not said in his life, and not simply for the sake of instructing them, but as though thirsting to share with all men and all creation his joy and ecstasy, and once more in his life to open his whole heart.", "그는 여러 가지를 이야기했는데, 죽음의 순간을 앞두고 살아오는 동안 미처 하지 못한 말을 죄다 쏟아 내고 싶어 안달하는 듯했으니, 다만 그들을 가르치려는 뜻에서만이 아니라, 자신의 기쁨과 환희를 만인과 만물과 더불어 나누고, 일생에 한 번 더 자기 가슴 전부를 열어 보이고 싶어 목말라하는 듯했다."]], [["“Love one another, Fathers,”", "\"서로 사랑하시오, 신부님들.\""], ["said Father Zossima, as far as Alyosha could remember afterwards.", "알료샤가 훗날 기억해 낼 수 있는 한, 조시마 장로는 이렇게 말했다."], ["“Love God’s people.", "\"하느님의 백성을 사랑하시오."], ["Because we have come here and shut ourselves within these walls, we are no holier than those that are outside, but on the contrary, from the very fact of coming here, each of us has confessed to himself that he is worse than others, than all men on earth....", "우리가 이곳에 와서 이 담장 안에 스스로를 가두었다 해서, 우리가 바깥에 있는 이들보다 더 거룩한 것은 아니며, 도리어 이곳에 왔다는 바로 그 사실에서, 우리 한 사람 한 사람은 스스로 다른 이들보다, 이 땅의 모든 사람보다 더 못한 자임을 자신에게 고백한 것이라오……."], ["And the longer the monk lives in his seclusion, the more keenly he must recognize that.", "그리고 수도사가 은둔 가운데 오래 살수록, 그것을 더욱 절실히 깨달아야만 하오."], ["Else he would have had no reason to come here.", "그렇지 않다면 그가 이곳에 올 까닭이 없었을 테니."], ["When he realizes that he is not only worse than others, but that he is responsible to all men for all and everything, for all human sins, national and individual, only then the aim of our seclusion is attained.", "그가 다른 이들보다 못할 뿐 아니라, 만인 앞에서 모든 것에 대해, 인류의 모든 죄에 대해, 민족의 죄든 개인의 죄든 그 모든 것에 책임이 있음을 깨달을 때, 비로소 우리 은둔의 목적이 이루어지는 것이라오."], ["For know, dear ones, that every one of us is undoubtedly responsible for all men and everything on earth, not merely through the general sinfulness of creation, but each one personally for all mankind and every individual man.", "사랑하는 이들이여, 알아 두시오. 우리 한 사람 한 사람은 의심할 바 없이 이 땅의 모든 사람과 모든 것에 대해 책임이 있으니, 그저 만물에 깃든 보편적 죄성을 통해서만이 아니라, 각자가 몸소 온 인류와 한 사람 한 사람에 대해 책임이 있는 것이라오."], ["This knowledge is the crown of life for the monk and for every man.", "이 깨달음이야말로 수도사에게나 모든 사람에게나 생명의 면류관이오."], ["For monks are not a special sort of men, but only what all men ought to be.", "수도사란 특별한 부류의 인간이 아니라, 다만 모든 사람이 마땅히 되어야 할 그것일 따름이기 때문이오."], ["Only through that knowledge, our heart grows soft with infinite, universal, inexhaustible love.", "오직 그 깨달음을 통해서만 우리 마음이 무한하고 보편적이며 다함이 없는 사랑으로 부드러워지는 것이오."], ["Then every one of you will have the power to win over the whole world by love and to wash away the sins of the world with your tears....", "그리하면 그대들 한 사람 한 사람이 사랑으로써 온 세상을 얻고, 그대들의 눈물로써 세상의 죄를 씻어 낼 힘을 갖게 되리니……."], ["Each of you keep watch over your heart and confess your sins to yourself unceasingly.", "그대들 각자는 자신의 마음을 지켜보며, 끊임없이 자신에게 죄를 고백하시오."], ["Be not afraid of your sins, even when perceiving them, if only there be penitence, but make no conditions with God.", "자신의 죄를 두려워하지 마시오, 그것을 깨달았을 때라도, 다만 뉘우침만 있다면, 그러나 하느님과 흥정을 하지는 마시오."], ["Again I say, Be not proud.", "거듭 말하노니, 교만하지 마시오."], ["Be proud neither to the little nor to the great.", "작은 자에게도 큰 자에게도 교만하지 마시오."], ["Hate not those who reject you, who insult you, who abuse and slander you.", "그대들을 물리치고, 모욕하고, 욕하고, 헐뜯는 자들을 미워하지 마시오. 무신론자들을, 악을 가르치는 자들을, 유물론자들을 미워하지 마시오."], ["Hate not the atheists, the teachers of evil, the materialists—and I mean not only the good ones—for there are many good ones among them, especially in our day—hate not even the wicked ones.", "그 가운데 선한 자들만이 아니라, 그들 중에는 선한 자가 많으니, 특히 오늘날에는 더욱 그러하오. 악한 자들조차 미워하지 마시오."], ["Remember them in your prayers thus: Save, O Lord, all those who have none to pray for them, save too all those who will not pray.", "기도할 때 그들을 이렇게 기억하시오. 주여, 그들을 위해 기도해 줄 이가 아무도 없는 자들을 모두 구원하소서, 기도하려 들지 않는 자들 또한 모두 구원하소서."], ["And add: it is not in pride that I make this prayer, O Lord, for I am lower than all men....", "그리고 이렇게 덧붙이시오. 주여, 이 기도를 드림은 교만에서가 아니오니, 저야말로 모든 사람보다 못한 자이옵니다……."], ["Love God’s people, let not strangers draw away the flock, for if you slumber in your slothfulness and disdainful pride, or worse still, in covetousness, they will come from all sides and draw away your flock.", "하느님의 백성을 사랑하시오, 낯선 자들이 양 떼를 빼앗아 가지 못하게 하시오. 그대들이 게으름과 거만한 자존심에 빠져 잠들거나, 더 나쁘게는 탐욕에 빠져 잠든다면, 그자들이 사방에서 몰려와 그대들의 양 떼를 빼앗아 갈 것이니."], ["Expound the Gospel to the people unceasingly ...", "끊임없이 사람들에게 복음을 전하시오……."], ["be not extortionate....", "갈취하지 마시오……."], ["Do not love gold and silver, do not hoard them....", "금과 은을 사랑하지 말고, 그것을 쌓아 두지 마시오……."], ["Have faith.", "믿음을 가지시오."], ["Cling to the banner and raise it on high.”", "깃발을 붙들고 그것을 높이 치켜드시오.\""]], [["But the elder spoke more disconnectedly than Alyosha reported his words afterwards.", "그러나 장로의 말은 알료샤가 훗날 전한 것보다 훨씬 더 두서가 없었다."], ["Sometimes he broke off altogether, as though to take breath, and recover his strength, but he was in a sort of ecstasy.", "이따금 그는 숨을 고르며 기운을 되찾으려는 듯 아예 말을 끊기도 했으나, 일종의 환희에 잠겨 있었다."], ["They heard him with emotion, though many wondered at his words and found them obscure....", "사람들은 감격에 젖어 그의 말을 들었으되, 많은 이들은 그 말에 의아해하며 알아듣기 어렵다 여겼다……."], ["Afterwards all remembered those words.", "훗날 모두가 그 말들을 기억해 냈다."]], [["When Alyosha happened for a moment to leave the cell, he was struck by the general excitement and suspense in the monks who were crowding about it.", "알료샤가 잠시 암자를 나섰을 때, 그는 암자 주위에 몰려든 수도사들 사이에 감도는 전반적인 흥분과 긴장에 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["This anticipation showed itself in some by anxiety, in others by devout solemnity.", "이 기대는 어떤 이들에게서는 불안으로, 어떤 이들에게서는 경건한 엄숙함으로 드러났다."], ["All were expecting that some marvel would happen immediately after the elder’s death.", "모두가 장로의 죽음 직후에 어떤 기적이 일어나리라 기다리고 있었다."], ["Their suspense was, from one point of view, almost frivolous, but even the most austere of the monks were affected by it.", "그들의 긴장은 어느 한편에서 보자면 거의 경박하다 할 만한 것이었으나, 가장 엄격한 수도사들조차 그것에 물들어 있었다."], ["Father Païssy’s face looked the gravest of all.", "파이시 신부의 얼굴이 그 가운데서도 가장 근엄해 보였다."]], [["Alyosha was mysteriously summoned by a monk to see Rakitin, who had arrived from town with a singular letter for him from Madame Hohlakov.", "알료샤는 한 수도사로부터 은밀히 부름을 받아 라키틴을 만나러 갔는데, 그는 호흘라코바 부인이 알료샤에게 보낸 기묘한 편지를 들고 읍내에서 막 도착한 참이었다."], ["In it she informed Alyosha of a strange and very opportune incident.", "그 편지에서 부인은 알료샤에게 어떤 이상하고도 무척 시의적절한 사건을 알려 왔다."], ["It appeared that among the women who had come on the previous day to receive Father Zossima’s blessing, there had been an old woman from the town, a sergeant’s widow, called Prohorovna.", "사연인즉, 전날 조시마 신부의 축복을 받으러 온 여인들 가운데 읍내에서 온 노파가 하나 있었으니, 프로호로브나라는 어느 하사관의 미망인이었다."], ["She had inquired whether she might pray for the rest of the soul of her son, Vassenka, who had gone to Irkutsk, and had sent her no news for over a year.", "그녀는 이르쿠츠크로 떠나 일 년 넘도록 아무런 소식도 보내오지 않는 아들 바센카의 영혼의 안식을 위해 기도해도 좋겠느냐고 물었다."], ["To which Father Zossima had answered sternly, forbidding her to do so, and saying that to pray for the living as though they were dead was a kind of sorcery.", "이에 조시마 신부는 엄하게 답하여 그러지 말라고 금하면서, 산 사람을 죽은 사람인 양 위해 기도하는 것은 일종의 주술이라고 말했다."], ["He afterwards forgave her on account of her ignorance, and added, “as though reading the book of the future” (this was Madame Hohlakov’s expression), words of comfort: “that her son Vassya was certainly alive and he would either come himself very shortly or send a letter, and that she was to go home and expect him.”", "그 뒤 그는 그녀의 무지를 헤아려 용서하고는, 마치 \"미래의 책을 읽는 듯이\"(이것은 호흘라코바 부인의 표현이었다) 위로의 말을 덧붙였으니, \"그녀의 아들 바샤는 분명 살아 있으며, 머지않아 직접 돌아오거나 편지를 보낼 터이니, 집으로 돌아가 그를 기다리라\"는 것이었다."], ["And “Would you believe it?”", "그리고 \"이걸 믿으시겠어요?\""], ["exclaimed Madame Hohlakov enthusiastically, “the prophecy has been fulfilled literally indeed, and more than that.”", "호흘라코바 부인은 열에 들떠 외쳤다. \"그 예언이 그야말로 글자 그대로, 아니 그 이상으로 이루어졌답니다.\""], ["Scarcely had the old woman reached home when they gave her a letter from Siberia which had been awaiting her.", "노파가 집에 채 닿기도 전에, 사람들이 그녀에게 시베리아에서 온, 그녀를 기다리고 있던 편지를 건네주었다는 것이다."], ["But that was not all; in the letter written on the road from Ekaterinenburg, Vassya informed his mother that he was returning to Russia with an official, and that three weeks after her receiving the letter he hoped “to embrace his mother.”", "그러나 그뿐만이 아니었으니, 예카테린부르크에서 오는 길에 쓴 그 편지에서 바샤는 어머니에게, 자기가 어느 관리와 함께 러시아로 돌아가는 중이며, 어머니가 편지를 받은 지 삼 주 뒤면 \"어머니를 품에 안기를\" 바란다고 알려 왔던 것이다."]], [["Madame Hohlakov warmly entreated Alyosha to report this new “miracle of prediction” to the Superior and all the brotherhood.", "호흘라코바 부인은 알료샤에게 이 새로운 \"예언의 기적\"을 원장 신부와 모든 형제에게 전해 달라고 간곡히 청했다."], ["“All, all, ought to know of it!” she concluded.", "\"모두, 모두가 이것을 알아야만 해요!\""], ["The letter had been written in haste, the excitement of the writer was apparent in every line of it.", "그녀는 이렇게 끝맺었다. 편지는 황급히 쓰인 것으로, 쓴 이의 흥분이 줄줄이 배어 있었다."], ["But Alyosha had no need to tell the monks, for all knew of it already.", "그러나 알료샤가 수도사들에게 알릴 필요는 없었으니, 이미 모두가 그 일을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Rakitin had commissioned the monk who brought his message “to inform most respectfully his reverence Father Païssy, that he, Rakitin, has a matter to speak of with him, of such gravity that he dare not defer it for a moment, and humbly begs forgiveness for his presumption.”", "라키틴은 자기 전갈을 가져온 수도사에게 \"더없이 공손히 파이시 신부님께 아뢰어, 자신, 곧 라키틴이 한순간도 미룰 수 없을 만큼 중대한 일을 그분과 의논하고자 하니, 외람됨을 삼가 용서해 주시기를 청한다\"고 일러두었던 것이다."], ["As the monk had given the message to Father Païssy before that to Alyosha, the latter found after reading the letter, there was nothing left for him to do but to hand it to Father Païssy in confirmation of the story.", "그 수도사가 알료샤보다 먼저 파이시 신부에게 전갈을 전했던 터라, 알료샤는 편지를 읽고 나서 그 이야기를 뒷받침할 수 있도록 파이시 신부에게 편지를 건네는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없음을 알게 되었다."]], [["And even that austere and cautious man, though he frowned as he read the news of the “miracle,” could not completely restrain some inner emotion.", "그리고 그 엄격하고 신중한 사람조차, \"기적\"의 소식을 읽으며 눈살을 찌푸리기는 했으나, 어떤 내면의 동요를 온전히 억누르지는 못했다."], ["His eyes gleamed, and a grave and solemn smile came into his lips.", "그의 눈이 반짝였고, 입가에는 진지하고도 엄숙한 미소가 떠올랐다."]], [["“We shall see greater things!”", "\"우리는 더 큰 일들을 보게 될 것이오!\""], ["broke from him.", "그의 입에서 이런 말이 새어 나왔다."]], [["“We shall see greater things, greater things yet!”", "\"우리는 더 큰 일들을, 그보다 더 큰 일들을 보게 될 것이오!\""], ["the monks around repeated.", "둘러선 수도사들이 따라 외쳤다."]], [["But Father Païssy, frowning again, begged all of them, at least for a time, not to speak of the matter “till it be more fully confirmed, seeing there is so much credulity among those of this world, and indeed this might well have chanced naturally,” he added, prudently, as it were to satisfy his conscience, though scarcely believing his own disavowal, a fact his listeners very clearly perceived.", "그러나 파이시 신부는 다시 눈살을 찌푸리며 그들 모두에게, 적어도 한동안은 그 일을 입에 올리지 말기를 청했으니, \"더 충분히 확증될 때까지는, 이 세상 사람들 사이에는 쉬이 믿어 버리는 풍조가 워낙 많고, 또 실로 이런 일은 자연스럽게 일어났을 수도 있는 것\"이라고, 그는 마치 양심을 달래기라도 하려는 듯 신중하게 덧붙였으나, 정작 자기 자신의 부인을 거의 믿지 않고 있다는 것은 듣는 이들이 아주 또렷이 알아차린 바였다."]], [["Within the hour the “miracle” was of course known to the whole monastery, and many visitors who had come for the mass.", "한 시간이 채 지나기도 전에 그 \"기적\"은 물론 온 수도원에, 그리고 미사를 드리러 온 많은 방문객들에게까지 알려졌다."], ["No one seemed more impressed by it than the monk who had come the day before from St.", "그 누구도, 전날 머나먼 북쪽 옵도르스크의 작은 수도원, 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 온 수도사만큼 그 일에 감명받은 이는 없어 보였다."], ["Sylvester, from the little monastery of Obdorsk in the far North.", "전날 호흘라코바 부인 곁에 서서, 부인의 딸이 \"낫는\""], ["It was he who had been standing near Madame Hohlakov the previous day and had asked Father Zossima earnestly, referring to the “healing” of the lady’s daughter, “How can you presume to do such things?”", "것을 두고 조시마 신부에게 \"어찌 감히 이런 일을 하실 수 있습니까?\"라고 진지하게 물었던 바로 그 사람이었다."]], [["He was now somewhat puzzled and did not know whom to believe.", "그는 이제 어쩐지 어리둥절하여 누구를 믿어야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["The evening before he had visited Father Ferapont in his cell apart, behind the apiary, and had been greatly impressed and overawed by the visit.", "전날 저녁 그는 양봉장 너머 외따로 떨어진 암자에 페라폰트 신부를 찾아갔는데, 그 방문에 크게 감명받고 압도되었던 것이다."], ["This Father Ferapont was that aged monk so devout in fasting and observing silence who has been mentioned already, as antagonistic to Father Zossima and the whole institution of “elders,”", "이 페라폰트 신부야말로 단식과 묵언 수행에 그토록 독실한, 앞서 이미 언급된 적이 있는 그 노수도사로서, 조시마 신부와 \"장로\""], ["which he regarded as a pernicious and frivolous innovation.", "제도 전체에 적대적인 인물이었으니, 그는 그것을 해롭고도 경박한 새 풍습으로 여겼다."], ["He was a very formidable opponent, although from his practice of silence he scarcely spoke a word to any one.", "그는 묵언 수행 탓에 누구에게도 거의 한 마디도 하지 않았건만, 그래도 만만찮은 적수였다."], ["What made him formidable was that a number of monks fully shared his feeling, and many of the visitors looked upon him as a great saint and ascetic, although they had no doubt that he was crazy.", "그를 만만찮게 만든 것은, 적잖은 수도사들이 그의 감정에 온전히 공감했고, 또 많은 방문객들이 그를 위대한 성자요 고행자로 우러러보았기 때문이니, 비록 그가 미쳤다는 것을 의심하지는 않으면서도 그러했다."], ["But it was just his craziness attracted them.", "그러나 사람들을 끌어당긴 것은 바로 그 광기였다."]], [["Father Ferapont never went to see the elder.", "페라폰트 신부는 결코 장로를 보러 가지 않았다."], ["Though he lived in the hermitage they did not worry him to keep its regulations, and this too because he behaved as though he were crazy.", "그는 은수처에 살면서도 그곳의 규율을 지키라는 성가신 요구를 받지 않았으니, 이 또한 그가 미친 사람처럼 행동했기 때문이다."], ["He was seventy-five or more, and he lived in a corner beyond the apiary in an old decaying wooden cell which had been built long ago for another great ascetic, Father Iona, who had lived to be a hundred and five, and of whose saintly doings many curious stories were still extant in the monastery and the neighborhood.", "그는 일흔다섯 살이 넘었고, 양봉장 너머 한 모퉁이에, 오래전 또 다른 위대한 고행자 이오나 신부를 위해 지어진 낡고 허물어져 가는 목조 암자에서 살았는데, 이오나 신부는 백다섯 살까지 살았으며 그 거룩한 행적에 관한 기이한 이야기들이 수도원과 인근에 아직도 숱하게 전해 내려오고 있었다."]], [["Father Ferapont had succeeded in getting himself installed in this same solitary cell seven years previously.", "페라폰트 신부는 칠 년 전 이 외딴 암자에 스스로 자리 잡는 데 성공했다."], ["It was simply a peasant’s hut, though it looked like a chapel, for it contained an extraordinary number of ikons with lamps perpetually burning before them—which men brought to the monastery as offerings to God.", "그것은 그저 농부의 오두막에 지나지 않았으나, 예배당처럼 보이기도 했으니, 그 안에는 엄청나게 많은 성상이 모셔져 있고 그 앞에는 등불이 끊임없이 타올랐기 때문이다. 그 성상들은 사람들이 하느님께 바치는 봉헌물로 수도원에 가져온 것이었다."], ["Father Ferapont had been appointed to look after them and keep the lamps burning.", "페라폰트 신부는 그것들을 돌보며 등불을 꺼뜨리지 않도록 임명되어 있었다."], ["It was said (and indeed it was true) that he ate only two pounds of bread in three days.", "사람들 말로는 (그리고 실제로 사실이었거니와) 그는 사흘에 빵 두 파운드밖에 먹지 않는다고 했다."], ["The beekeeper, who lived close by the apiary, used to bring him the bread every three days, and even to this man who waited upon him, Father Ferapont rarely uttered a word.", "양봉장 가까이 사는 양봉지기가 사흘마다 빵을 갖다주었는데, 자기를 시중드는 이 사람에게조차 페라폰트 신부는 좀처럼 한 마디도 건네지 않았다."], ["The four pounds of bread, together with the sacrament bread, regularly sent him on Sundays after the late mass by the Father Superior, made up his weekly rations.", "빵 네 파운드와, 원장 신부가 주일마다 늦은 미사 뒤에 정기적으로 보내 주는 성체용 빵을 합한 것이 그의 한 주치 양식이었다. 항아리의 물은 날마다 갈아 주었다."], ["The water in his jug was changed every day.", "그는 좀처럼 미사에 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["He rarely appeared at mass. Visitors who came to do him homage saw him sometimes kneeling all day long at prayer without looking round.", "그에게 경의를 표하러 온 방문객들은 더러 그가 하루 종일 주위도 돌아보지 않고 무릎 꿇어 기도하는 모습을 보았다."], ["If he addressed them, he was brief, abrupt, strange, and almost always rude.", "그가 그들에게 말을 건넨다 해도, 짧고 퉁명스럽고 기이했으며, 거의 언제나 무례했다."], ["On very rare occasions, however, he would talk to visitors, but for the most part he would utter some one strange saying which was a complete riddle, and no entreaties would induce him to pronounce a word in explanation.", "그러나 아주 드물게는 방문객들과 이야기를 나누기도 했는데, 대개는 도무지 풀 수 없는 수수께끼 같은 기이한 말 한 마디를 던질 뿐, 어떤 간청으로도 그에게 설명의 말을 한 마디 끌어낼 수는 없었다."], ["He was not a priest, but a simple monk.", "그는 사제가 아니라 평범한 수도사였다."], ["There was a strange belief, chiefly however among the most ignorant, that Father Ferapont had communication with heavenly spirits and would only converse with them, and so was silent with men.", "주로 가장 무지한 이들 사이에 떠도는 기이한 믿음이 하나 있었으니, 페라폰트 신부가 천상의 영들과 교통하며 오직 그들과만 대화를 나누기에 사람들에게는 입을 다문다는 것이었다."]], [["The monk from Obdorsk, having been directed to the apiary by the beekeeper, who was also a very silent and surly monk, went to the corner where Father Ferapont’s cell stood.", "옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는, 역시 무척 과묵하고 무뚝뚝한 수도사인 양봉지기로부터 양봉장으로 가는 길을 안내받아, 페라폰트 신부의 암자가 서 있는 모퉁이로 갔다."], ["“Maybe he will speak as you are a stranger and maybe you’ll get nothing out of him,”", "\"당신이 낯선 사람이니 어쩌면 말을 할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 한 마디도 못 들을지도 모르오.\""], ["the beekeeper had warned him.", "양봉지기는 그에게 이렇게 일러두었다."], ["The monk, as he related afterwards, approached in the utmost apprehension.", "그 수도사는 훗날 이야기하기를, 더없는 두려움을 안고 다가갔다고 했다."], ["It was rather late in the evening.", "저녁도 제법 깊은 무렵이었다."], ["Father Ferapont was sitting at the door of his cell on a low bench.", "페라폰트 신부는 암자 문가의 낮은 걸상에 앉아 있었다."], ["A huge old elm was lightly rustling overhead.", "머리 위로는 거대한 고목 느릅나무가 가볍게 살랑거렸다."], ["There was an evening freshness in the air.", "공기에는 저녁의 서늘함이 감돌았다."], ["The monk from Obdorsk bowed down before the saint and asked his blessing.", "옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는 그 성자 앞에 엎드려 축복을 청했다."]], [["“Do you want me to bow down to you, monk?”", "\"수도사여, 내가 그대에게 절이라도 하기를 바라는가?\""], ["said Father Ferapont.", "페라폰트 신부가 말했다."], ["“Get up!”", "\"일어나게!\""]], [["The monk got up.", "수도사는 일어났다."]], [["“Blessing, be blessed!", "\"축복을, 복 받으시게!"], ["Sit beside me.", "내 곁에 앉게."], ["Where have you come from?”", "그대는 어디서 왔는가?\""]], [["What most struck the poor monk was the fact that in spite of his strict fasting and great age, Father Ferapont still looked a vigorous old man.", "이 가엾은 수도사가 무엇보다 놀란 것은, 그토록 엄격한 단식과 고령에도 불구하고 페라폰트 신부가 여전히 정정한 노인으로 보인다는 사실이었다."], ["He was tall, held himself erect, and had a thin, but fresh and healthy face.", "그는 키가 컸고, 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠으며, 야위었으되 싱싱하고 건강한 얼굴이었다."], ["There was no doubt he still had considerable strength.", "의심할 바 없이 그에게는 아직도 상당한 힘이 남아 있었다."], ["He was of athletic build.", "그는 다부진 체격이었다."], ["In spite of his great age he was not even quite gray, and still had very thick hair and a full beard, both of which had once been black.", "그 고령에도 머리가 아주 세지조차 않았고, 한때 모두 검었던 머리카락과 수염은 여전히 무척 숱이 많았다."], ["His eyes were gray, large and luminous, but strikingly prominent.", "두 눈은 잿빛에 크고 형형했으나, 놀랍도록 툭 불거져 있었다."], ["He spoke with a broad accent.", "그는 사투리가 짙은 말씨로 이야기했다."], ["He was dressed in a peasant’s long reddish coat of coarse convict cloth (as it used to be called) and had a stout rope round his waist.", "그는 (예전에 일컫던 대로) 거친 죄수복 천으로 지은 농부의 불그스름하고 긴 외투를 걸쳤고, 허리에는 굵은 밧줄을 둘렀다."], ["His throat and chest were bare.", "목과 가슴은 드러나 있었다."], ["Beneath his coat, his shirt of the coarsest linen showed almost black with dirt, not having been changed for months.", "외투 안으로 보이는, 더없이 거친 삼베로 지은 셔츠는 몇 달째 갈아입지 않아 때에 절어 거의 시커멨다."], ["They said that he wore irons weighing thirty pounds under his coat.", "사람들 말로는 그가 외투 안에 서른 파운드짜리 쇠사슬을 두르고 있다고 했다."], ["His stockingless feet were thrust in old slippers almost dropping to pieces.", "양말도 신지 않은 발은 거의 다 해져 떨어질 지경인 낡은 슬리퍼에 꿰여 있었다."]], [["“From the little Obdorsk monastery, from St. Sylvester,”", "\"옵도르스크의 작은 수도원, 성 실베스테르 수도원에서 왔습니다.\""], ["the monk answered humbly, whilst his keen and inquisitive, but rather frightened little eyes kept watch on the hermit.", "수도사가 겸손히 답하는 동안, 그의 예리하고 캐묻기 좋아하면서도 자못 겁먹은 작은 눈은 줄곧 은수자를 지켜보았다."]], [["“I have been at your Sylvester’s.", "\"그대의 실베스테르에 가 본 적이 있네."], ["I used to stay there.", "거기 머물곤 했지."], ["Is Sylvester well?”", "실베스테르는 잘 있는가?\""]], [["The monk hesitated.", "수도사는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“You are a senseless lot!", "\"그대들은 분별없는 무리로다!"], ["How do you keep the fasts?”", "단식은 어떻게 지키는가?\""]], [["“Our dietary is according to the ancient conventual rules.", "\"저희 식단은 옛 수도원 규칙을 따릅니다."], ["During Lent there are no meals provided for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.", "사순절 동안은 월요일과 수요일, 금요일에는 끼니가 마련되지 않습니다."], ["For Tuesday and Thursday we have white bread, stewed fruit with honey, wild berries, or salt cabbage and wholemeal stirabout.", "화요일과 목요일에는 흰 빵에 꿀을 넣어 졸인 과일, 산딸기, 또는 소금에 절인 양배추와 통밀죽을 먹습니다."], ["On Saturday white cabbage soup, noodles with peas, kasha, all with hemp oil.", "토요일에는 흰 양배춧국, 완두콩을 넣은 국수, 카샤를 먹는데, 죄다 삼씨 기름을 칩니다."], ["On weekdays we have dried fish and kasha with the cabbage soup.", "평일에는 말린 생선과 카샤를 양배춧국과 곁들여 먹습니다."], ["From Monday till Saturday evening, six whole days in Holy Week, nothing is cooked, and we have only bread and water, and that sparingly; if possible not taking food every day, just the same as is ordered for first week in Lent.", "성주간에는 월요일부터 토요일 저녁까지 꼬박 엿새 동안 아무것도 익히지 않고, 빵과 물만, 그것도 아껴 먹으며, 가능하면 날마다 먹지 않으니, 사순절 첫 주에 정해진 것과 똑같이 합니다."], ["On Good Friday nothing is eaten.", "성금요일에는 아무것도 먹지 않습니다."], ["In the same way on the Saturday we have to fast till three o’clock, and then take a little bread and water and drink a single cup of wine.", "마찬가지로 토요일에도 세 시까지 단식한 다음, 약간의 빵과 물을 들고 포도주를 단 한 잔 마십니다."], ["On Holy Thursday we drink wine and have something cooked without oil or not cooked at all, inasmuch as the Laodicean council lays down for Holy Thursday: ‘It is unseemly by remitting the fast on the Holy Thursday to dishonor the whole of Lent!’ This is how we keep the fast.", "성목요일에는 포도주를 마시고 기름을 치지 않은 음식을 익히거나 아예 익히지 않은 것을 먹으니, 라오디케이아 공의회가 성목요일에 관하여 '성목요일에 단식을 풀어 사순절 전체를 욕되게 함은 마땅치 않다!'고 정한 까닭입니다. 저희는 이렇게 단식을 지킵니다."], ["But what is that compared with you, holy Father,”", "그러나 거룩하신 신부님, 그것이 신부님에 비하면 무엇이겠습니까.\""], ["added the monk, growing more confident, “for all the year round, even at Easter, you take nothing but bread and water, and what we should eat in two days lasts you full seven.", "수도사는 더욱 자신감을 얻어 덧붙였다. \"신부님께서는 일 년 내내, 부활절에조차 빵과 물밖에 들지 않으시고, 저희가 이틀에 먹을 것을 신부님께서는 꼬박 이레를 버티시니 말입니다."], ["It’s truly marvelous—your great abstinence.”", "참으로 놀랍습니다, 신부님의 그 위대한 절식이.\""]], [["“And mushrooms?”", "\"버섯은?\""], ["asked Father Ferapont, suddenly.", "페라폰트 신부가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Mushrooms?”", "\"버섯이라고요?\""], ["repeated the surprised monk.", "어리둥절해진 수도사가 되물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I can give up their bread, not needing it at all, and go away into the forest and live there on the mushrooms or the berries, but they can’t give up their bread here, wherefore they are in bondage to the devil.", "나는 그들의 빵을 끊을 수 있으니, 빵 따위는 전혀 필요치 않아, 숲으로 들어가 거기서 버섯이나 산딸기로 살아갈 수 있네. 허나 그자들은 이곳의 빵을 끊지 못하니, 그래서 악마에게 매여 있는 게야."], ["Nowadays the unclean deny that there is need of such fasting.", "요즘 들어 부정한 무리는 그런 단식이 필요하다는 것을 부인하지."], ["Haughty and unclean is their judgment.”", "그들의 판단은 교만하고 부정하도다.\""]], [["“Och, true,”", "\"오, 옳으신 말씀입니다.\""], ["sighed the monk.", "수도사는 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“And have you seen devils among them?”", "\"그래, 그자들 가운데서 악마를 본 적이 있는가?\""], ["asked Ferapont.", "페라폰트가 물었다."]], [["“Among them?", "\"그자들 가운데서요?"], ["Among whom?”", "누구 가운데서 말입니까?\""], ["asked the monk, timidly.", "수도사가 머뭇머뭇 물었다."]], [["“I went to the Father Superior on Trinity Sunday last year, I haven’t been since.", "\"작년 삼위일체 주일에 원장 신부에게 갔었는데, 그 뒤로는 가지 않았네. 한 사람의 가슴팍에 악마가 앉아서 수단 자락 밑에 숨어 있는 것을 보았지, 뿔만 삐죽 내밀고서."], ["I saw a devil sitting on one man’s chest hiding under his cassock, only his horns poked out; another had one peeping out of his pocket with such sharp eyes, he was afraid of me; another settled in the unclean belly of one, another was hanging round a man’s neck, and so he was carrying him about without seeing him.”", "또 한 사람은 호주머니에서 악마가 어찌나 날카로운 눈으로 빠끔히 내다보던지, 그놈이 나를 두려워하더군. 또 한 놈은 한 사람의 부정한 뱃속에 들어앉았고, 또 한 놈은 한 사람의 목에 매달려 있어서, 그 사람은 그놈을 알아보지도 못한 채 끌고 다니더란 말이야.\""]], [["“You—can see spirits?”", "\"신부님께서는, 영들을 보실 수 있습니까?\""], ["the monk inquired.", "수도사가 물었다."]], [["“I tell you I can see, I can see through them.", "\"내 말하지 않았는가, 나는 볼 수 있어, 그것들을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있지."], ["When I was coming out from the Superior’s I saw one hiding from me behind the door, and a big one, a yard and a half or more high, with a thick long gray tail, and the tip of his tail was in the crack of the door and I was quick and slammed the door, pinching his tail in it.", "원장에게서 나오는데, 한 놈이 문 뒤에 숨어 나를 피하는 것을 보았네, 그것도 큰 놈이, 한 야드 반은 족히 넘는 키에, 굵고 긴 잿빛 꼬리가 달린 놈이었지. 그놈의 꼬리 끝이 문틈에 끼어 있기에, 내가 잽싸게 문을 쾅 닫아 거기에 꼬리를 짓눌렀단 말이야."], ["He squealed and began to struggle, and I made the sign of the cross over him three times.", "그놈이 꽥꽥대며 발버둥치기에, 나는 그 위에 성호를 세 번 그었지."], ["And he died on the spot like a crushed spider.", "그러자 짓밟힌 거미처럼 그 자리에서 죽고 말았네."], ["He must have rotted there in the corner and be stinking, but they don’t see, they don’t smell it.", "그놈은 분명 그 모퉁이에서 썩어 악취를 풍기고 있을 텐데도, 그자들은 보지도 냄새 맡지도 못해."], ["It’s a year since I have been there.", "거기 가지 않은 지 일 년이 됐지."], ["I reveal it to you, as you are a stranger.”", "그대가 낯선 사람이기에 이걸 일러 주는 게야.\""]], [["“Your words are terrible!", "\"신부님 말씀은 무섭습니다!"], ["But, holy and blessed Father,”", "그러나 거룩하고 복되신 신부님,\""], ["said the monk, growing bolder and bolder, “is it true, as they noise abroad even to distant lands about you, that you are in continual communication with the Holy Ghost?”", "수도사는 점점 더 대담해지며 말했다. \"먼 땅에까지 신부님에 관하여 떠도는 소문대로, 신부님께서 성령과 끊임없이 교통하신다는 것이 참말입니까?\""]], [["“He does fly down at times.”", "\"이따금 내려오시지.\""]], [["“How does he fly down?", "\"어떻게 내려오십니까?"], ["In what form?”", "어떤 모습으로요?\""]], [["“As a bird.”", "\"새의 모습으로.\""]], [["“The Holy Ghost in the form of a dove?”", "\"비둘기의 모습으로 오시는 성령 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“There’s the Holy Ghost and there’s the Holy Spirit.", "\"성령(聖靈)이 따로 있고 거룩한 영(靈)이 따로 있네."], ["The Holy Spirit can appear as other birds—sometimes as a swallow, sometimes a goldfinch and sometimes as a blue-tit.”", "거룩한 영은 다른 새들의 모습으로도 나타날 수 있어. 어떤 때는 제비로, 어떤 때는 황금방울새로, 또 어떤 때는 푸른박새로 말이야.\""]], [["“How do you know him from an ordinary tit?”", "\"그것을 보통 박새와 어떻게 구별하십니까?\""]], [["“He speaks.”", "\"말을 하거든.\""]], [["“How does he speak, in what language?”", "\"어떻게 말합니까, 어떤 언어로요?\""]], [["“Human language.”", "\"사람의 언어로.\""]], [["“And what does he tell you?”", "\"신부님께 무어라 이릅니까?\""]], [["“Why, to-day he told me that a fool would visit me and would ask me unseemly questions.", "\"이를테면 오늘은 나에게, 어리석은 자가 나를 찾아와 마땅찮은 질문들을 물어 댈 것이라고 일러 주더군."], ["You want to know too much, monk.”", "수도사여, 그대는 너무 많은 것을 알려 드는구나.\""]], [["“Terrible are your words, most holy and blessed Father,”", "\"신부님 말씀은 무섭습니다, 더없이 거룩하고 복되신 신부님.\""], ["the monk shook his head.", "수도사는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."], ["But there was a doubtful look in his frightened little eyes.", "그러나 그 겁먹은 작은 눈에는 미심쩍어하는 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Do you see this tree?”", "\"이 나무가 보이는가?\""], ["asked Father Ferapont, after a pause.", "잠시 뜸을 들였다가 페라폰트 신부가 물었다."]], [["“I do, blessed Father.”", "\"보입니다, 복되신 신부님.\""]], [["“You think it’s an elm, but for me it has another shape.”", "\"그대는 저것을 느릅나무로 여기겠지만, 나에게는 다른 형상으로 보이네.\""]], [["“What sort of shape?”", "\"어떤 형상으로요?\""], ["inquired the monk, after a pause of vain expectation.", "부질없이 기다리던 끝에 수도사가 물었다."]], [["“It happens at night.", "\"밤이면 그리 되지."], ["You see those two branches?", "저 두 가지가 보이는가?"], ["In the night it is Christ holding out His arms to me and seeking me with those arms, I see it clearly and tremble.", "밤이면 저것이 그리스도께서 나에게 두 팔을 뻗으시어 그 팔로 나를 찾으시는 모습이라, 나는 그것을 똑똑히 보며 떠노라."], ["It’s terrible, terrible!”", "무섭도다, 무섭도다!\""]], [["“What is there terrible if it’s Christ Himself?”", "\"그리스도 친히 오신 것이라면 무엇이 무섭습니까?\""]], [["“Why, He’ll snatch me up and carry me away.”", "\"나를 낚아채어 데려가실 테니 말이야.\""]], [["“Alive?”", "\"산 채로요?\""]], [["“In the spirit and glory of Elijah, haven’t you heard?", "\"엘리야의 영광과 영으로 말일세, 듣지 못했는가?"], ["He will take me in His arms and bear me away.”", "그분께서 나를 두 팔에 안아 데려가시리라.\""]], [["Though the monk returned to the cell he was sharing with one of the brothers, in considerable perplexity of mind, he still cherished at heart a greater reverence for Father Ferapont than for Father Zossima.", "옵도르스크에서 온 수도사는 한 형제와 함께 쓰는 암자로 돌아오면서 마음이 자못 어수선했으나, 그래도 마음속으로는 조시마 신부보다 페라폰트 신부를 더 크게 우러르고 있었다."], ["He was strongly in favor of fasting, and it was not strange that one who kept so rigid a fast as Father Ferapont should “see marvels.”", "그는 단식을 열렬히 지지하는 사람이었으니, 페라폰트 신부처럼 그토록 엄격한 단식을 지키는 이가 \"기적을 본다\"는 것도 이상할 게 없었다."], ["His words seemed certainly queer, but God only could tell what was hidden in those words, and were not worse words and acts commonly seen in those who have sacrificed their intellects for the glory of God?", "그의 말이 분명 괴이쩍게 들리기는 했으나, 그 말 속에 무엇이 감추어져 있는지는 오직 하느님만이 아실 일이요, 하느님의 영광을 위해 제 지성을 바친 이들에게서 더 심한 말과 행동이 흔히 보이지 않던가?"], ["The pinching of the devil’s tail he was ready and eager to believe, and not only in the figurative sense.", "악마의 꼬리를 짓눌렀다는 이야기를, 그는 그것도 비유적인 뜻으로만이 아니라 글자 그대로 기꺼이, 또 간절히 믿을 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["Besides he had, before visiting the monastery, a strong prejudice against the institution of “elders,”", "게다가 그는 수도원을 찾아오기 전부터 \"장로\""], ["which he only knew of by hearsay and believed to be a pernicious innovation.", "제도에 대해 강한 편견을 품고 있었으니, 그것에 관해 그저 풍문으로만 알았고 해로운 새 풍습이라 믿어 왔다."], ["Before he had been long at the monastery, he had detected the secret murmurings of some shallow brothers who disliked the institution.", "그는 수도원에 온 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 그 제도를 못마땅해하는 몇몇 천박한 형제들의 은밀한 수군거림을 알아챈 터였다."], ["He was, besides, a meddlesome, inquisitive man, who poked his nose into everything.", "게다가 그는 매사에 코를 들이미는, 오지랖 넓고 캐묻기 좋아하는 사람이었다."], ["This was why the news of the fresh “miracle” performed by Father Zossima reduced him to extreme perplexity.", "그렇기에 조시마 신부가 행한 새로운 \"기적\"의 소식은 그를 극도의 혼란에 빠뜨렸다."], ["Alyosha remembered afterwards how their inquisitive guest from Obdorsk had been continually flitting to and fro from one group to another, listening and asking questions among the monks that were crowding within and without the elder’s cell.", "알료샤는 훗날, 옵도르스크에서 온 그 캐묻기 좋아하는 손님이 장로의 암자 안팎에 몰려든 수도사들 사이를 끊임없이 이 무리 저 무리로 옮겨 다니며 귀를 기울이고 질문을 던지던 것을 기억해 냈다."], ["But he did not pay much attention to him at the time, and only recollected it afterwards.", "그러나 그 당시에는 그에게 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 다만 훗날 그것을 떠올렸을 따름이다."]], [["He had no thought to spare for it indeed, for when Father Zossima, feeling tired again, had gone back to bed, he thought of Alyosha as he was closing his eyes, and sent for him.", "실로 그는 그런 데 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었으니, 조시마 신부가 다시 피로를 느껴 침상으로 돌아간 뒤, 눈을 감으면서 알료샤를 떠올리고는 그를 불러오게 했기 때문이다."], ["Alyosha ran at once.", "알료샤는 곧장 달려왔다."], ["There was no one else in the cell but Father Païssy, Father Iosif, and the novice Porfiry.", "암자에는 파이시 신부와 이오시프 신부, 그리고 수련 수사 포르피리 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["The elder, opening his weary eyes and looking intently at Alyosha, asked him suddenly:", "장로는 지친 눈을 뜨고 알료샤를 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Are your people expecting you, my son?”", "\"네 집안 사람들이 너를 기다리고 있느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["Alyosha hesitated.", "알료샤는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Haven’t they need of you?", "\"그들이 너를 필요로 하지 않느냐?"], ["Didn’t you promise some one yesterday to see them to-day?”", "어제 누군가에게 오늘 만나러 가겠다고 약속하지 않았느냐?\""]], [["“I did promise—to my father—my brothers—others too.”", "\"약속했습니다, 아버지께, 형들에게, 다른 이들에게도요.\""]], [["“You see, you must go.", "\"보아라, 너는 가야 한다."], ["Don’t grieve.", "슬퍼하지 마라."], ["Be sure I shall not die without your being by to hear my last word.", "분명히 일러두거니와, 네가 곁에서 내 마지막 말을 듣지 못한 채로는 나는 죽지 않을 게다."], ["To you I will say that word, my son, it will be my last gift to you.", "너에게 그 말을 해 주마, 얘야, 그것이 너에게 주는 나의 마지막 선물이 될 게다."], ["To you, dear son, because you love me.", "너에게, 사랑하는 아들아, 네가 나를 사랑하기에 그러하다."], ["But now go to keep your promise.”", "허나 이제 가서 네 약속을 지키거라.\""]], [["Alyosha immediately obeyed, though it was hard to go.", "알료샤는 떠나기가 괴로웠으나 곧바로 따랐다."], ["But the promise that he should hear his last word on earth, that it should be the last gift to him, Alyosha, sent a thrill of rapture through his soul.", "그러나 그가 장로의 이 땅에서의 마지막 말을 들으리라는 약속, 그것이 알료샤 자신에게 주는 마지막 선물이 되리라는 약속은 그의 영혼에 환희의 전율을 일으켰다."], ["He made haste that he might finish what he had to do in the town and return quickly.", "그는 읍내에서 해야 할 일을 마치고 어서 돌아오려고 서둘렀다."], ["Father Païssy, too, uttered some words of exhortation which moved and surprised him greatly.", "파이시 신부 또한 그를 크게 감동시키고 놀라게 한 권면의 말을 몇 마디 건넸다."], ["He spoke as they left the cell together.", "그들이 함께 암자를 나서는데 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Remember, young man, unceasingly,”", "\"젊은이여, 끊임없이 기억하시오.\""], ["Father Païssy began, without preface, “that the science of this world, which has become a great power, has, especially in the last century, analyzed everything divine handed down to us in the holy books.", "파이시 신부는 서두도 없이 말문을 열었다. \"하나의 거대한 힘이 되어 버린 이 세상의 학문이, 특히 지난 한 세기 동안, 거룩한 책들을 통해 우리에게 전해 내려온 신성한 모든 것을 분석해 왔다는 사실을."], ["After this cruel analysis the learned of this world have nothing left of all that was sacred of old.", "이 잔혹한 분석을 거치고 나니, 이 세상의 학자들에게는 예부터 거룩하던 그 모든 것 가운데 남은 것이 하나도 없게 되었소."], ["But they have only analyzed the parts and overlooked the whole, and indeed their blindness is marvelous.", "그러나 그들은 부분들만 분석했을 뿐 전체를 간과했으니, 실로 그들의 눈멂은 놀라울 지경이오."], ["Yet the whole still stands steadfast before their eyes, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.", "그럼에도 그 전체는 여전히 그들의 눈앞에 굳건히 서 있으며, 지옥의 문이 그것을 이기지 못할 것이오."], ["Has it not lasted nineteen centuries, is it not still a living, a moving power in the individual soul and in the masses of people?", "그것이 십구 세기를 버텨 오지 않았소? 그것이 여전히 개개인의 영혼 속에서도, 무수한 백성들 속에서도 살아 움직이는 힘이 아니오?"], ["It is still as strong and living even in the souls of atheists, who have destroyed everything!", "그것은 모든 것을 파괴한 무신론자들의 영혼 속에서조차 여전히 그토록 강하고 살아 있소!"], ["For even those who have renounced Christianity and attack it, in their inmost being still follow the Christian ideal, for hitherto neither their subtlety nor the ardor of their hearts has been able to create a higher ideal of man and of virtue than the ideal given by Christ of old.", "그리스도교를 저버리고 그것을 공격하는 이들조차, 그 가장 깊은 본성에서는 여전히 그리스도교의 이상을 따르고 있으니, 지금껏 그들의 영리함도, 그들 가슴의 열정도, 옛적 그리스도께서 주신 인간과 덕의 이상보다 더 높은 이상을 빚어내지 못했기 때문이오."], ["When it has been attempted, the result has been only grotesque.", "그것을 시도했을 때 그 결과란 그저 기괴한 것일 따름이었소."], ["Remember this especially, young man, since you are being sent into the world by your departing elder.", "젊은이여, 이것을 특별히 기억하시오, 그대는 떠나가는 그대의 장로에 의해 세상으로 보내지는 처지이니 말이오."], ["Maybe, remembering this great day, you will not forget my words, uttered from the heart for your guidance, seeing you are young, and the temptations of the world are great and beyond your strength to endure.", "어쩌면 이 위대한 날을 기억하노라면, 그대는 진심에서 그대의 인도를 위해 들려준 내 말을 잊지 않을 것이오, 그대가 젊고, 세상의 유혹은 크며 그대의 힘으로 견뎌 내기에는 벅찬 까닭이오."], ["Well, now go, my orphan.”", "자, 이제 가시오, 나의 고아여.\""]], [["With these words Father Païssy blessed him.", "이 말과 더불어 파이시 신부는 그를 축복했다."], ["As Alyosha left the monastery and thought them over, he suddenly realized that he had met a new and unexpected friend, a warmly loving teacher, in this austere monk who had hitherto treated him sternly.", "알료샤는 수도원을 나서며 그 말을 곱씹다가, 지금껏 자기를 엄하게만 대해 오던 이 근엄한 수도사 안에서, 새롭고도 뜻밖의 벗이자 따스한 사랑으로 가득한 스승을 만났음을 문득 깨달았다."], ["It was as though Father Zossima had bequeathed him to him at his death, and “perhaps that’s just what had passed between them,”", "마치 조시마 신부가 임종에 이르러 그를 파이시 신부에게 물려준 것만 같았으니, \"어쩌면 두 사람 사이에 오간 것이 바로 그것이었는지도 몰라.\""], ["Alyosha thought suddenly.", "하고 알료샤는 문득 생각했다."], ["The philosophic reflections he had just heard so unexpectedly testified to the warmth of Father Païssy’s heart.", "방금 그토록 뜻밖에 들은 철학적 사색은 파이시 신부 마음의 따스함을 증언하는 것이었다."], ["He was in haste to arm the boy’s mind for conflict with temptation and to guard the young soul left in his charge with the strongest defense he could imagine.", "그는 소년의 마음을 유혹과의 싸움에 대비해 서둘러 무장시키고, 자신에게 맡겨진 그 젊은 영혼을 자기가 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 굳센 방벽으로 지켜 주려 했던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter II. At His Father’s", "# Chapter II. At His Father’s"]], [["First of all, Alyosha went to his father.", "무엇보다 먼저 알료샤는 아버지에게로 갔다."], ["On the way he remembered that his father had insisted the day before that he should come without his brother Ivan seeing him.", "가는 길에 그는 어제 아버지가 형 이반의 눈에 띄지 않게 오라고 신신당부했던 것을 떠올렸다. \"어째서일까?\""], ["“Why so?” Alyosha wondered suddenly.", "알료샤는 불현듯 의아해졌다."], ["“Even if my father has something to say to me alone, why should I go in unseen?", "\"아버지가 나한테만 따로 할 말이 있다손 치더라도, 어째서 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 들어가야 한다는 것일까?"], ["Most likely in his excitement yesterday he meant to say something different,” he decided.", "어제 흥분한 나머지 무언가 다른 말을 하려다 그리 된 것이겠지.\""], ["Yet he was very glad when Marfa Ignatyevna, who opened the garden gate to him (Grigory, it appeared, was ill in bed in the lodge), told him in answer to his question that Ivan Fyodorovitch had gone out two hours ago.", "그는 이렇게 결론지었다. 그래도 정원 문을 열어 준 마르파 이그나티예브나가(그리고리는 별채에 몸져누워 있다는 것이었다) 그의 물음에 답하여 이반 표도로비치가 두 시간 전에 외출했다고 일러 주었을 때, 그는 무척 반가웠다."]], [["“And my father?”", "\"그럼 아버지는요?\""]], [["“He is up, taking his coffee,”", "\"일어나서 커피를 드시는 중이지요.\""], ["Marfa answered somewhat dryly.", "마르파가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["Alyosha went in.", "알료샤는 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The old man was sitting alone at the table wearing slippers and a little old overcoat.", "노인은 실내화에 낡은 외투를 걸치고 식탁에 홀로 앉아 있었다."], ["He was amusing himself by looking through some accounts, rather inattentively however.", "그는 무슨 장부 따위를 들여다보며 시간을 보내고 있었으나, 그다지 마음을 쏟지는 않았다."], ["He was quite alone in the house, for Smerdyakov too had gone out marketing.", "스메르쟈코프마저 장을 보러 나가, 그는 집 안에 완전히 홀로 있었다."], ["Though he had got up early and was trying to put a bold face on it, he looked tired and weak.", "일찍 일어나 짐짓 대범한 척하려 들었으나, 그는 지치고 약해 보였다."], ["His forehead, upon which huge purple bruises had come out during the night, was bandaged with a red handkerchief; his nose too had swollen terribly in the night, and some smaller bruises covered it in patches, giving his whole face a peculiarly spiteful and irritable look.", "간밤에 시퍼런 멍이 큼지막하게 솟아오른 이마에는 붉은 손수건이 동여매여 있었고, 코 역시 밤사이 끔찍하게 부어올라 자잘한 멍이 여기저기 얼룩져 온 얼굴에 유난히 심술궂고 짜증스러운 기색을 더하고 있었다."], ["The old man was aware of this, and turned a hostile glance on Alyosha as he came in.", "노인도 그것을 알고 있던 터라, 알료샤가 들어서자 적의에 찬 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“The coffee is cold,”", "\"커피가 식었구나.\""], ["he cried harshly; “I won’t offer you any.", "그가 거칠게 소리쳤다. \"너한테는 권하지 않으마."], ["I’ve ordered nothing but a Lenten fish soup to-day, and I don’t invite any one to share it.", "오늘은 사순절 생선 수프밖에 시키지 않았으니, 누구와도 나눠 먹을 생각이 없다."], ["Why have you come?”", "무슨 일로 왔느냐?\""]], [["“To find out how you are,”", "\"안부를 여쭈러 왔습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Besides, I told you to come yesterday.", "게다가 어제 내가 오라고 일러 두었지."], ["It’s all of no consequence.", "다 부질없는 일이다."], ["You need not have troubled.", "굳이 수고할 것 없었느니."], ["But I knew you’d come poking in directly.”", "그래도 네가 곧장 코를 들이밀고 나타날 줄은 알고 있었다.\""]], [["He said this with almost hostile feeling.", "그는 이 말을 거의 적의에 차서 내뱉었다."], ["At the same time he got up and looked anxiously in the looking-glass (perhaps for the fortieth time that morning) at his nose. He began, too, binding his red handkerchief more becomingly on his forehead.", "그러면서 동시에 자리에서 일어나 거울 속의 제 코를 근심스레 들여다보았는데(그날 아침 들어 마흔 번째쯤이었으리라), 이마에 동여맨 붉은 손수건도 좀 더 보기 좋게 고쳐 매기 시작했다."]], [["“Red’s better.", "\"빨간 게 낫지."], ["It’s just like the hospital in a white one,”", "하얀 걸 매면 영락없이 병원 신세 같단 말이야.\""], ["he observed sententiously.", "그는 자못 점잖은 투로 한마디 했다."], ["“Well, how are things over there?", "\"그래, 그쪽 형편은 어떠냐?"], ["How is your elder?”", "네 장로는 좀 어떠시냐?\""]], [["“He is very bad; he may die to-day,”", "\"몹시 위중하십니다. 오늘이라도 돌아가실지 모릅니다.\""], ["answered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 대답했다."], ["But his father had not listened, and had forgotten his own question at once.", "그러나 아버지는 듣고 있지 않았으며, 제가 던진 물음조차 곧장 잊어버렸다."]], [["“Ivan’s gone out,”", "\"이반이 나갔다.\""], ["he said suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“He is doing his utmost to carry off Mitya’s betrothed.", "\"그놈은 미탸의 약혼녀를 가로채려고 안간힘을 쓰고 있어."], ["That’s what he is staying here for,”", "그게 바로 그놈이 여기 눌러앉아 있는 까닭이지.\""], ["he added maliciously, and, twisting his mouth, looked at Alyosha.", "그가 짓궂게 덧붙이고는 입을 비죽이며 알료샤를 바라보았다."]], [["“Surely he did not tell you so?”", "\"설마 형이 아버지께 그렇게 말했을 리가요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, he did, long ago.", "\"그래, 했다, 벌써 오래전에."], ["Would you believe it, he told me three weeks ago?", "믿어지느냐, 삼 주 전에 그렇게 말했다니까?"], ["You don’t suppose he too came to murder me, do you?", "설마 그놈도 나를 죽이러 온 거라고는 생각지 않겠지?"], ["He must have had some object in coming.”", "그놈도 무슨 속셈이 있어서 온 게 분명해.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["Why do you say such things?”", "어째서 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\""], ["said Alyosha, troubled.", "알료샤가 괴로운 듯 물었다."]], [["“He doesn’t ask for money, it’s true, but yet he won’t get a farthing from me.", "\"돈을 달라는 건 아니지, 그건 사실이다. 하지만 그래도 내게서는 한 푼도 못 얻어 갈 게다."], ["I intend living as long as possible, you may as well know, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, and so I need every farthing, and the longer I live, the more I shall need it,”", "내 똑똑히 일러두마, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 되도록 오래오래 살 작정이고, 그러니 한 푼 한 푼이 다 필요하다. 그리고 오래 살면 살수록 그만큼 더 필요해질 게야.\""], ["he continued, pacing from one corner of the room to the other, keeping his hands in the pockets of his loose greasy overcoat made of yellow cotton material.", "그는 누런 무명천으로 지은 헐렁하고 기름때 전 외투 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 방 이 구석에서 저 구석으로 서성거리며 말을 이었다."], ["“I can still pass for a man at five and fifty, but I want to pass for one for another twenty years.", "\"쉰다섯이라도 아직 사내 구실은 할 만하지. 하지만 앞으로 이십 년은 더 사내 구실을 하고 싶단 말이다."], ["As I get older, you know, I shan’t be a pretty object. The wenches won’t come to me of their own accord, so I shall want my money.", "알다시피 늙어 가면 꼴이 볼품없어질 테고, 계집들이 제 발로 찾아오지는 않을 테니, 그때는 돈이 있어야 하지 않겠느냐."], ["So I am saving up more and more, simply for myself, my dear son Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "그래서 나는 점점 더 모으는 게야, 오로지 나 자신을 위해서, 사랑하는 아들 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["You may as well know.", "너도 알아 두는 게 좋을 게다."], ["For I mean to go on in my sins to the end, let me tell you.", "똑똑히 말해 두마, 나는 끝까지 죄 속에서 살 작정이다. 죄란 달콤한 것이니까."], ["For sin is sweet; all abuse it, but all men live in it, only others do it on the sly, and I openly.", "다들 죄를 욕하지만 다들 죄 속에서 살아간다. 다만 남들은 몰래 하고 나는 드러내 놓고 할 뿐이지."], ["And so all the other sinners fall upon me for being so simple.", "그래서 다른 죄인 놈들이 다 나한테 달려드는 거야, 내가 그렇게 솔직하다고 말이다."], ["And your paradise, Alexey Fyodorovitch, is not to my taste, let me tell you that; and it’s not the proper place for a gentleman, your paradise, even if it exists.", "그리고 네 천국 말이다, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 똑똑히 말해 두지만 내 취향에는 도무지 맞지 않아. 설령 천국이라는 게 있다 한들, 그건 점잖은 사내가 갈 만한 데가 못 돼."], ["I believe that I fall asleep and don’t wake up again, and that’s all.", "내 생각엔 잠들면 그뿐, 다시 깨어나지 않으면 그만인 게야, 그게 전부지."], ["You can pray for my soul if you like.", "네가 원한다면 내 영혼을 위해 기도해도 좋다."], ["And if you don’t want to, don’t, damn you!", "싫으면 말고, 빌어먹을! 그게 내 철학이다."], ["That’s my philosophy. Ivan talked well here yesterday, though we were all drunk.", "어제 이반이 여기서 제법 그럴듯하게 떠들어 댔지, 우리가 다들 취해 있긴 했지만 말이다."], ["Ivan is a conceited coxcomb, but he has no particular learning ...", "이반은 잘난 체하는 멋쟁이지만 별다른 학식은 없어……"], ["nor education either.", "교양도 없고."], ["He sits silent and smiles at one without speaking—that’s what pulls him through.”", "그저 잠자코 앉아 말없이 사람을 보고 빙긋이 웃을 뿐이지. 그걸로 그럭저럭 헤쳐 나가는 게야.\""]], [["Alyosha listened to him in silence.", "알료샤는 잠자코 그의 말을 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Why won’t he talk to me?", "\"왜 그놈은 나하고 말을 섞지 않는 거냐?"], ["If he does speak, he gives himself airs.", "어쩌다 입을 열어도 거드름만 피우지."], ["Your Ivan is a scoundrel!", "네 이반은 비열한 놈이야!"], ["And I’ll marry Grushenka in a minute if I want to.", "나는 마음만 먹으면 당장에라도 그루셴카와 결혼할 수 있다."], ["For if you’ve money, Alexey Fyodorovitch, you have only to want a thing and you can have it.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 돈만 있으면 무얼 원하기만 하면 그걸 손에 넣을 수 있는 법이거든. 바로 그걸 이반이 두려워하는 게야."], ["That’s what Ivan is afraid of, he is on the watch to prevent me getting married and that’s why he is egging on Mitya to marry Grushenka himself.", "그놈은 내가 결혼하는 걸 막으려고 망을 보고 있어. 그래서 미탸를 부추겨서 제가 직접 그루셴카와 결혼하게 만들려는 거지."], ["He hopes to keep me from Grushenka by that (as though I should leave him my money if I don’t marry her!).", "그렇게 해서 나를 그루셴카한테서 떼어 놓으려는 속셈이야(내가 그 여자와 결혼하지 않으면 그놈한테 돈을 남길 줄 알고!)."], ["Besides if Mitya marries Grushenka, Ivan will carry off his rich betrothed, that’s what he’s reckoning on!", "게다가 미탸가 그루셴카와 결혼해 버리면 이반은 그 부자 약혼녀를 가로챌 테고, 바로 그걸 노리고 있는 게야!"], ["He is a scoundrel, your Ivan!”", "비열한 놈이라니까, 네 이반은!\""]], [["“How cross you are!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 심술이 잔뜩 나셨어요!"], ["It’s because of yesterday; you had better lie down,”", "다 어제 일 때문이에요. 좀 누우시는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“There!", "\"옳거니!"], ["you say that,”", "네가 그런 말을 하는구나.\""], ["the old man observed suddenly, as though it had struck him for the first time, “and I am not angry with you.", "노인이 마치 처음으로 그 생각이 떠오른 듯 불쑥 말했다. \"그런데도 나는 너한테는 화가 나지 않아."], ["But if Ivan said it, I should be angry with him.", "만약 이반이 그런 말을 했다면 나는 그놈한테 화를 냈을 게다."], ["It is only with you I have good moments, else you know I am an ill-natured man.”", "너하고 있을 때만은 내게도 좋은 순간이 있단 말이지, 그렇지 않으면 너도 알다시피 나는 심보 고약한 인간이거든.\""]], [["“You are not ill-natured, but distorted,”", "\"심보가 고약하신 게 아니라 비뚤어지신 거예요.\""], ["said Alyosha with a smile.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Listen.", "\"들어 보아라."], ["I meant this morning to get that ruffian Mitya locked up and I don’t know now what I shall decide about it.", "나는 오늘 아침 그 망나니 미탸 놈을 잡아 가두게 할 작정이었다만, 이제는 어찌 결판을 낼지 나도 모르겠구나."], ["Of course in these fashionable days fathers and mothers are looked upon as a prejudice, but even now the law does not allow you to drag your old father about by the hair, to kick him in the face in his own house, and brag of murdering him outright—all in the presence of witnesses.", "물론 요즘 같은 시류에는 아비 어미를 한낱 케케묵은 편견쯤으로 여기지만, 그래도 법은 제 늙은 아비의 머리채를 끌고 다니거나, 제 집 안에서 아비 얼굴을 걷어차거나, 보란 듯이 아주 죽여 버리겠다고 떠벌리는 짓은 허락하지 않아—그것도 죄다 증인들이 보는 앞에서 말이다."], ["If I liked, I could crush him and could have him locked up at once for what he did yesterday.”", "내가 마음만 먹으면 그놈을 짓뭉개 버릴 수도 있고, 어제 저지른 짓 때문에 당장 잡아 가두게 할 수도 있어.\""]], [["“Then you don’t mean to take proceedings?”", "\"그럼 고소하실 생각은 없으신 거예요?\""]], [["“Ivan has dissuaded me.", "\"이반이 말렸다."], ["I shouldn’t care about Ivan, but there’s another thing.”", "이반 따위야 나는 아랑곳하지 않다만, 다른 사정이 있어서 말이지.\""]], [["And bending down to Alyosha, he went on in a confidential half-whisper.", "그러고는 알료샤에게 몸을 굽혀 은밀한 반쯤 속삭이는 투로 말을 이었다."]], [["“If I send the ruffian to prison, she’ll hear of it and run to see him at once.", "\"내가 그 망나니를 감옥에 처넣으면, 그 여자가 그 소식을 듣고 당장 그놈한테 면회를 가려고 달려갈 게다."], ["But if she hears that he has beaten me, a weak old man, within an inch of my life, she may give him up and come to me....", "하지만 그놈이 이 약하디약한 늙은이를 거의 죽기 직전까지 두들겨 팼다는 소문을 들으면, 그 여자는 그놈을 내치고 내게로 올지도 몰라……"], ["For that’s her way, everything by contraries.", "그게 그 여자 방식이거든, 매사가 거꾸로지."], ["I know her through and through!", "나는 그 여자를 속속들이 알아!"], ["Won’t you have a drop of brandy?", "너 브랜디 한 모금 안 하겠느냐?"], ["Take some cold coffee and I’ll pour a quarter of a glass of brandy into it, it’s delicious, my boy.”", "식은 커피라도 들어라, 거기다 브랜디를 사분의 일 잔쯤 부어 주마. 기막히게 맛있단다, 얘야.\""]], [["“No, thank you.", "\"아니에요, 괜찮습니다."], ["I’ll take that roll with me if I may,”", "괜찮으시다면 저 빵이나 하나 가져갈게요.\""], ["said Alyosha, and taking a halfpenny French roll he put it in the pocket of his cassock.", "알료샤가 이렇게 말하고는 반 페니짜리 프랑스 빵을 집어 수도복 주머니에 넣었다."], ["“And you’d better not have brandy, either,”", "\"그리고 아버지도 브랜디는 안 드시는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["he suggested apprehensively, looking into the old man’s face.", "그가 노인의 얼굴을 들여다보며 근심스레 권했다."]], [["“You are quite right, it irritates my nerves instead of soothing them.", "\"네 말이 옳다. 그건 신경을 가라앉히기는커녕 도리어 곤두서게 하지."], ["Only one little glass.", "딱 한 잔만이다."], ["I’ll get it out of the cupboard.”", "찬장에서 꺼내 오마.\""]], [["He unlocked the cupboard, poured out a glass, drank it, then locked the cupboard and put the key back in his pocket.", "그는 찬장을 열고 한 잔을 따라 마신 다음, 다시 찬장을 잠그고 열쇠를 도로 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["“That’s enough.", "\"이만하면 됐다."], ["One glass won’t kill me.”", "한 잔쯤으로야 죽기야 하겠느냐.\""]], [["“You see you are in a better humor now,”", "\"보세요, 이제 기분이 한결 좋아지셨잖아요.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Um!", "\"흠!"], ["I love you even without the brandy, but with scoundrels I am a scoundrel.", "나는 브랜디가 없어도 너를 사랑한다만, 비열한 놈들한테는 나도 비열한 놈이 되는 게야."], ["Ivan is not going to Tchermashnya—why is that?", "이반은 체르마시냐에 가지 않아—그건 왜겠느냐?"], ["He wants to spy how much I give Grushenka if she comes.", "그놈은 그루셴카가 오면 내가 그 여자한테 얼마나 주는지 염탐하려는 거야."], ["They are all scoundrels!", "다들 비열한 놈들이지!"], ["But I don’t recognize Ivan, I don’t know him at all.", "하지만 나는 이반이라는 놈을 도무지 모르겠어, 그놈을 통 알 수가 없어."], ["Where does he come from?", "대체 어디서 굴러먹다 온 놈인지."], ["He is not one of us in soul.", "영혼부터가 우리 핏줄이 아니야."], ["As though I’d leave him anything!", "내가 그놈한테 뭐라도 남겨 줄 성싶으냐!"], ["I shan’t leave a will at all, you may as well know.", "너도 알아 두는 게 좋겠다, 나는 아예 유언장 따위는 남기지 않을 테다."], ["And I’ll crush Mitya like a beetle.", "그리고 미탸 놈은 딱정벌레처럼 짓뭉개 버릴 게야."], ["I squash black-beetles at night with my slipper; they squelch when you tread on them.", "나는 밤이면 실내화로 검은 딱정벌레를 으깨 버리거든. 밟으면 짓이겨지면서 찍 소리가 나지."], ["And your Mitya will squelch too.", "네 미탸도 그렇게 찍 소리를 낼 게다."], ["Your Mitya, for you love him.", "네 미탸 말이다, 네가 그놈을 사랑하니까."], ["Yes, you love him and I am not afraid of your loving him.", "그래, 너는 그놈을 사랑하지. 하지만 나는 네가 그놈을 사랑하는 건 두렵지 않아."], ["But if Ivan loved him I should be afraid for myself at his loving him.", "그러나 만약 이반이 그놈을 사랑한다면 나는 내 신상이 두려워질 게다, 그놈이 그놈을 사랑한다면 말이다."], ["But Ivan loves nobody.", "하지만 이반은 아무도 사랑하지 않아."], ["Ivan is not one of us.", "이반은 우리 핏줄이 아니거든."], ["People like Ivan are not our sort, my boy.", "이반 같은 인간들은 우리 부류가 아니야, 얘야."], ["They are like a cloud of dust.", "그런 자들은 한 줌 먼지 같은 것이지."], ["When the wind blows, the dust will be gone....", "바람이 불면 그 먼지는 사라지고 마는 게야……"], ["I had a silly idea in my head when I told you to come to-day; I wanted to find out from you about Mitya.", "오늘 너를 오라고 했을 때 내 머릿속에는 어리석은 생각이 하나 들어 있었다. 너한테서 미탸에 관해 좀 알아보려고 했지."], ["If I were to hand him over a thousand or maybe two now, would the beggarly wretch agree to take himself off altogether for five years or, better still, thirty-five, and without Grushenka, and give her up once for all, eh?”", "만일 내가 지금 그놈한테 천 루블, 어쩌면 이천 루블쯤 쥐여 준다면, 그 거지 같은 놈이 오 년, 아니 그보다 더 좋게는 삼십오 년쯤 아주 멀리 사라져 주는 데, 그것도 그루셴카 없이, 그 여자를 영영 단념하는 데 동의하겠느냐, 응?\""]], [["“I—I’ll ask him,”", "\"제가……"], ["muttered Alyosha.", "제가 형한테 물어보겠습니다.\""], ["“If you would give him three thousand, perhaps he—”", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"삼천 루블을 주신다면, 어쩌면 형이……\""]], [["“That’s nonsense!", "\"그건 다 부질없는 소리다!"], ["You needn’t ask him now, no need!", "이제 그놈한테 물어볼 것 없다, 그럴 필요 없어!"], ["I’ve changed my mind.", "내 마음이 바뀌었으니까."], ["It was a nonsensical idea of mine.", "그건 내 어리석은 생각이었어."], ["I won’t give him anything, not a penny, I want my money myself,”", "나는 그놈한테 아무것도 안 줄 테다, 한 푼도, 내 돈은 내가 쓸 테다.\""], ["cried the old man, waving his hand.", "노인이 손을 내저으며 소리쳤다."], ["“I’ll crush him like a beetle without it.", "\"돈 없이도 그놈을 딱정벌레처럼 짓뭉개 버릴 게야."], ["Don’t say anything to him or else he will begin hoping.", "그놈한테는 아무 말도 하지 마라, 안 그러면 또 헛된 기대를 품기 시작할 테니."], ["There’s nothing for you to do here, you needn’t stay.", "너는 여기서 할 일이 없으니, 더 머물 것 없다."], ["Is that betrothed of his, Katerina Ivanovna, whom he has kept so carefully hidden from me all this time, going to marry him or not?", "그 약혼녀라는 카테리나 이바노브나, 그놈이 이제껏 그토록 조심스레 나한테서 숨겨 온 그 여자가, 그놈하고 결혼하겠다더냐 아니더냐?"], ["You went to see her yesterday, I believe?”", "어제 그 여자를 만나러 갔었다지, 아마?\""]], [["“Nothing will induce her to abandon him.”", "\"그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 형을 버리지 않으실 거예요.\""]], [["“There you see how dearly these fine young ladies love a rake and a scoundrel.", "\"옳거니, 그러니 그 잘난 아가씨들이 난봉꾼이며 비열한 놈을 어찌나 끔찍이도 사랑하는지 알 만하구나."], ["They are poor creatures I tell you, those pale young ladies, very different from—Ah, if I had his youth and the looks I had then (for I was better-looking than he at eight and twenty) I’d have been a conquering hero just as he is.", "내 말하건대 그것들은 가엾은 것들이야, 그 핼쑥한 아가씨들 말이다, 도무지 다른 여자들하고는 딴판이지—아, 내게 그놈 같은 젊음이 있고, 한창때 내 인물이 그대로 남아 있었다면(나는 스물여덟에 그놈보다 인물이 더 훤했단 말이다) 나도 그놈 못지않은 정복의 영웅이 되었을 게야."], ["He is a low cad!", "그놈은 천한 상놈이지!"], ["But he shan’t have Grushenka, anyway, he shan’t!", "하지만 어쨌든 그루셴카만은 못 차지하게 할 테다, 못 차지하게 하고말고!"], ["I’ll crush him!”", "내가 그놈을 짓뭉개 버릴 테다!\""]], [["His anger had returned with the last words.", "마지막 말과 함께 그의 노여움이 도로 치솟았다."]], [["“You can go.", "\"이제 가도 좋다."], ["There’s nothing for you to do here to-day,”", "오늘은 여기서 네가 할 일이 없으니.\""], ["he snapped harshly.", "그가 거칠게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["Alyosha went up to say good-by to him, and kissed him on the shoulder.", "알료샤는 작별 인사를 하려고 다가가 그의 어깨에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“What’s that for?”", "\"그건 또 무슨 짓이냐?\""], ["The old man was a little surprised.", "노인이 조금 놀랐다."], ["“We shall see each other again, or do you think we shan’t?”", "\"우리는 또 만날 텐데, 아니면 네 생각엔 다시는 못 만날 성싶으냐?\""]], [["“Not at all, I didn’t mean anything.”", "\"천만에요, 아무 뜻도 없었어요.\""]], [["“Nor did I, I did not mean anything,”", "\"나도 그렇다, 아무 뜻도 없었어.\""], ["said the old man, looking at him.", "노인이 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Listen, listen,”", "\"들어 봐라, 들어 봐.\""], ["he shouted after him, “make haste and come again and I’ll have a fish soup for you, a fine one, not like to-day.", "그가 떠나는 알료샤의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤다. \"어서 또 오너라, 그러면 너한테 생선 수프를 해 주마, 오늘 같은 게 아니라 아주 근사한 걸로 말이다."], ["Be sure to come!", "꼭 와야 한다!"], ["Come to-morrow, do you hear, to-morrow!”", "내일 와라, 알겠느냐, 내일!\""]], [["And as soon as Alyosha had gone out of the door, he went to the cupboard again and poured out another half-glass.", "그리고 알료샤가 문밖으로 나서기가 무섭게 그는 다시 찬장으로 가서 또 반 잔을 따랐다."]], [["“I won’t have more!”", "\"더는 안 마실 테다!\""], ["he muttered, clearing his throat, and again he locked the cupboard and put the key in his pocket.", "그가 목청을 가다듬으며 중얼거리고는, 다시 찬장을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다."], ["Then he went into his bedroom, lay down on the bed, exhausted, and in one minute he was asleep.", "그러고는 침실로 들어가 기진맥진한 채 침대에 드러누웠고, 일 분도 채 못 되어 잠이 들었다."]], [["# Chapter III. A Meeting With The Schoolboys", "# Chapter III. A Meeting With The Schoolboys"]], [["“Thank goodness he did not ask me about Grushenka,”", "「그루셴카 이야기를 묻지 않으셔서 천만다행이지.」"], ["thought Alyosha, as he left his father’s house and turned towards Madame Hohlakov’s, “or I might have to tell him of my meeting with Grushenka yesterday.”", "알료샤는 아버지의 집을 나서서 호흘라코바 부인의 집 쪽으로 발걸음을 옮기며 이렇게 생각했다. 「안 그랬으면 어제 그루셴카와 마주친 일을 털어놓아야 했을 테니까.」"]], [["Alyosha felt painfully that since yesterday both combatants had renewed their energies, and that their hearts had grown hard again.", "알료샤는 어제 이후로 두 싸움꾼이 다시금 기운을 차렸고, 그들의 마음이 또다시 모질어졌다는 것을 가슴 아프게 느끼고 있었다."], ["“Father is spiteful and angry, he’s made some plan and will stick to it.", "「아버지는 악의에 차서 화가 나 있고, 무슨 계획을 세워 놓고는 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나가실 테지."], ["And what of Dmitri?", "그렇다면 드미트리는 어떨까?"], ["He too will be harder than yesterday, he too must be spiteful and angry, and he too, no doubt, has made some plan.", "그도 어제보다 더 모질어졌을 테고, 그도 악의에 차서 화가 나 있을 게 분명하며, 그 역시 틀림없이 무슨 계획을 세워 놓았을 거야."], ["Oh, I must succeed in finding him to-day, whatever happens.”", "아, 오늘은 무슨 일이 있어도 기어이 형을 찾아내야 한다.」"]], [["But Alyosha had not long to meditate.", "그러나 알료샤가 그렇게 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있을 틈은 길지 않았다."], ["An incident occurred on the road, which, though apparently of little consequence, made a great impression on him.", "길 위에서 한 가지 사건이 벌어졌는데, 겉으로는 별것 아닌 듯 보였으나 그에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["Just after he had crossed the square and turned the corner coming out into Mihailovsky Street, which is divided by a small ditch from the High Street (our whole town is intersected by ditches), he saw a group of schoolboys between the ages of nine and twelve, at the bridge.", "광장을 가로질러 모퉁이를 돌아 미하일롭스키 거리로 막 접어들었을 때—이 거리는 작은 도랑 하나를 사이에 두고 큰길과 갈라져 있었으니(우리 고을 전체가 도랑으로 가로질려 있다)—그는 다리 곁에서 아홉 살에서 열두 살 사이의 학생 무리를 보았다."], ["They were going home from school, some with their bags on their shoulders, others with leather satchels slung across them, some in short jackets, others in little overcoats.", "그들은 학교에서 집으로 돌아가는 길이었는데, 더러는 책가방을 어깨에 메고, 더러는 가죽 책가방을 비스듬히 둘러메고 있었으며, 더러는 짧은 윗옷을, 더러는 작은 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["Some even had those high boots with creases round the ankles, such as little boys spoilt by rich fathers love to wear.", "어떤 아이들은 발목 둘레로 주름이 잡힌 그 높은 장화까지 신고 있었는데, 부유한 아버지에게서 응석받이로 자란 사내아이들이 즐겨 신는 것이었다."], ["The whole group was talking eagerly about something, apparently holding a council.", "무리 전체가 무언가를 두고 열을 올리며 이야기를 나누고 있었는데, 보아하니 회의라도 여는 모양이었다."], ["Alyosha had never from his Moscow days been able to pass children without taking notice of them, and although he was particularly fond of children of three or thereabout, he liked schoolboys of ten and eleven too.", "알료샤는 모스크바 시절부터 아이들을 보면 그냥 지나치지 못하는 사람이었으니, 세 살 안팎의 아이들을 유난히 좋아하기는 했으나 열 살, 열한 살짜리 학생들도 좋아했다."], ["And so, anxious as he was to-day, he wanted at once to turn aside to talk to them.", "그리하여 오늘 그토록 마음이 졸이는 와중에도 그는 곧장 옆으로 발길을 돌려 아이들에게 말을 걸고 싶어졌다."], ["He looked into their excited rosy faces, and noticed at once that all the boys had stones in their hands.", "그가 아이들의 상기되고 발그레한 얼굴을 들여다보다가 이내 알아차린 것은, 아이들 모두가 손에 돌멩이를 쥐고 있다는 사실이었다."], ["Behind the ditch some thirty paces away, there was another schoolboy standing by a fence.", "도랑 건너편 서른 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에는 또 다른 학생 하나가 울타리 곁에 서 있었다."], ["He too had a satchel at his side.", "그 아이도 옆구리에 책가방을 차고 있었다."], ["He was about ten years old, pale, delicate-looking and with sparkling black eyes.", "열 살가량 되어 보이는, 창백하고 가냘프게 생긴 아이로, 까만 눈이 반짝거렸다."], ["He kept an attentive and anxious watch on the other six, obviously his schoolfellows with whom he had just come out of school, but with whom he had evidently had a feud.", "그는 다른 여섯 아이를 주의 깊게, 그리고 불안한 눈빛으로 지켜보고 있었는데, 보아하니 그들은 방금 함께 학교에서 나온 동급생들로, 그와 무슨 다툼이 있었던 것이 분명했다."]], [["Alyosha went up and, addressing a fair, curly-headed, rosy boy in a black jacket, observed:", "알료샤는 다가가서, 검은 윗옷을 입은 살결 희고 곱슬머리에 발그레한 사내아이에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“When I used to wear a satchel like yours, I always used to carry it on my left side, so as to have my right hand free, but you’ve got yours on your right side.", "\"내가 너희 같은 책가방을 메고 다닐 때는 늘 왼쪽으로 메곤 했단다, 오른손을 자유롭게 쓰려고 말이지. 그런데 넌 오른쪽에 메고 있구나."], ["So it will be awkward for you to get at it.”", "그러면 가방을 열기가 불편할 텐데.\""]], [["Alyosha had no art or premeditation in beginning with this practical remark.", "알료샤는 이런 실용적인 말로 이야기를 꺼낸 데에 무슨 기교나 미리 꾸민 속셈이 있었던 것은 아니다."], ["But it is the only way for a grown-up person to get at once into confidential relations with a child, or still more with a group of children.", "그러나 어른이 아이와, 더구나 한 무리의 아이들과 단번에 허물없는 사이가 되려면 이것이 유일한 방법이다."], ["One must begin in a serious, businesslike way so as to be on a perfectly equal footing.", "완전히 대등한 처지에 서기 위해서는 진지하고 사무적인 태도로 말문을 열어야 하는 법이다."], ["Alyosha understood it by instinct.", "알료샤는 그것을 본능으로 알고 있었다."]], [["“But he is left-handed,”", "\"하지만 쟤는 왼손잡이인걸요.\""], ["another, a fine healthy-looking boy of eleven, answered promptly.", "곱고 건강해 보이는 열한 살짜리 다른 아이가 냉큼 대답했다."], ["All the others stared at Alyosha.", "나머지 아이들은 모두 알료샤를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“He even throws stones with his left hand,”", "\"쟤는 돌멩이도 왼손으로 던져요.\""], ["observed a third.", "세 번째 아이가 한마디 했다."]], [["At that instant a stone flew into the group, but only just grazed the left-handed boy, though it was well and vigorously thrown by the boy standing the other side of the ditch.", "바로 그 순간 돌멩이 하나가 무리 속으로 날아들었는데, 도랑 건너편에 서 있던 아이가 야무지고 힘차게 던졌는데도 왼손잡이 아이를 살짝 스치고 지나갔을 뿐이었다."]], [["“Give it him, hit him back, Smurov,”", "\"한 방 먹여, 되받아쳐, 스무로프!\""], ["they all shouted.", "아이들이 모두 소리쳤다."], ["But Smurov, the left-handed boy, needed no telling, and at once revenged himself; he threw a stone, but it missed the boy and hit the ground.", "그러나 왼손잡이 아이 스무로프는 말할 것도 없이 곧바로 앙갚음에 나섰으니, 돌멩이를 하나 던졌으나 그 아이를 맞히지 못하고 땅바닥에 떨어지고 말았다."], ["The boy the other side of the ditch, the pocket of whose coat was visibly bulging with stones, flung another stone at the group; this time it flew straight at Alyosha and hit him painfully on the shoulder.", "도랑 건너편의 아이는 외투 주머니가 돌멩이로 불룩하니 눈에 띄게 부풀어 있었는데, 또 한 개의 돌멩이를 무리를 향해 내던졌다. 이번에는 그것이 알료샤를 향해 곧장 날아와 어깨를 호되게 맞혔다."]], [["“He aimed it at you, he meant it for you.", "\"쟤가 아저씨를 노린 거예요, 일부러 아저씨를 겨눈 거라고요."], ["You are Karamazov, Karamazov!”", "아저씨는 카라마조프죠, 카라마조프!\""], ["the boys shouted, laughing.", "아이들이 웃으며 소리쳤다."], ["“Come, all throw at him at once!”", "\"자, 다 함께 한꺼번에 쟤한테 던지자!\""], ["and six stones flew at the boy.", "그러자 여섯 개의 돌멩이가 그 아이를 향해 날아갔다."], ["One struck the boy on the head and he fell down, but at once leapt up and began ferociously returning their fire.", "한 개가 아이의 머리를 맞혀 그가 쓰러졌으나, 곧장 벌떡 일어나서는 사납게 응사하기 시작했다."], ["Both sides threw stones incessantly.", "양편이 쉴 새 없이 돌을 던져 댔다."], ["Many of the group had their pockets full too.", "무리 가운데 많은 아이들도 주머니가 돌멩이로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["“What are you about!", "\"무슨 짓들이야!"], ["Aren’t you ashamed?", "부끄럽지도 않니?"], ["Six against one!", "여섯이서 하나를 상대하다니!"], ["Why, you’ll kill him,”", "이러다 쟤를 죽이겠다.\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["He ran forward and met the flying stones to screen the solitary boy.", "그는 앞으로 달려 나가 날아오는 돌멩이를 막아서며 외톨이 아이를 가려 주었다."], ["Three or four ceased throwing for a minute.", "서너 명이 잠시 던지기를 멈추었다."]], [["“He began first!”", "\"쟤가 먼저 시작했어요!\""], ["cried a boy in a red shirt in an angry childish voice.", "빨간 셔츠를 입은 아이가 화난 어린아이의 목소리로 소리쳤다."], ["“He is a beast, he stabbed Krassotkin in class the other day with a penknife.", "\"쟤는 짐승 같은 놈이에요, 며칠 전엔 교실에서 크라소트킨을 주머니칼로 찔렀다고요."], ["It bled.", "피가 났어요."], ["Krassotkin wouldn’t tell tales, but he must be thrashed.”", "크라소트킨은 일러바치진 않았지만, 저런 놈은 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 해요.\""]], [["“But what for?", "\"그렇지만 무엇 때문에?"], ["I suppose you tease him.”", "너희가 쟤를 놀려 댄 거겠지.\""]], [["“There, he sent a stone in your back again, he knows you,”", "\"저것 봐요, 쟤가 또 아저씨 등에 돌을 날렸어요, 쟤는 아저씨를 알아요.\""], ["cried the children.", "아이들이 소리쳤다."], ["“It’s you he is throwing at now, not us.", "\"이젠 우리가 아니라 아저씨한테 던지는 거예요."], ["Come, all of you, at him again, don’t miss, Smurov!”", "자, 다 같이 또 한 번 쟤한테, 빗맞히지 마, 스무로프!\""], ["and again a fire of stones, and a very vicious one, began.", "그러더니 또다시 돌멩이의 응사가, 그것도 무척 사나운 응사가 시작되었다."], ["The boy the other side of the ditch was hit in the chest; he screamed, began to cry and ran away uphill towards Mihailovsky Street.", "도랑 건너편의 아이는 가슴을 맞고 비명을 지르며 울음을 터뜨리더니 언덕 위 미하일롭스키 거리 쪽으로 달아났다."], ["They all shouted: “Aha, he is funking, he is running away.", "아이들은 모두 소리쳤다. \"아하, 쟤가 겁을 먹었다, 도망친다."], ["Wisp of tow!”", "검불 같은 놈!\""]], [["“You don’t know what a beast he is, Karamazov, killing is too good for him,”", "\"카라마조프 아저씨는 쟤가 얼마나 못된 놈인지 모르세요, 쟤는 죽여도 시원찮을 놈이에요.\""], ["said the boy in the jacket, with flashing eyes.", "윗옷을 입은 아이가 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다."], ["He seemed to be the eldest.", "그 아이가 그중 맏이인 듯했다."]], [["“What’s wrong with him?”", "\"쟤가 무슨 잘못을 했길래?\""], ["asked Alyosha, “is he a tell-tale or what?”", "알료샤가 물었다. \"고자질쟁이냐, 아니면 뭐냐?\""]], [["The boys looked at one another as though derisively.", "아이들은 비웃기라도 하듯 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“Are you going that way, to Mihailovsky?”", "\"아저씨도 그쪽으로, 미하일롭스키 쪽으로 가시는 거예요?\""], ["the same boy went on.", "아까 그 아이가 말을 이었다."], ["“Catch him up....", "\"쟤를 따라잡아 보세요……"], ["You see he’s stopped again, he is waiting and looking at you.”", "보세요, 쟤가 또 멈춰 섰어요, 서서 아저씨를 쳐다보고 있다고요.\""]], [["“He is looking at you,”", "\"쟤가 아저씨를 보고 있어요.\""], ["the other boys chimed in.", "다른 아이들도 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“You ask him, does he like a disheveled wisp of tow.", "\"쟤한테 물어보세요, 헝클어진 검불을 좋아하느냐고요."], ["Do you hear, ask him that!”", "들으셨죠, 그렇게 물어보세요!\""]], [["There was a general burst of laughter.", "와르르 웃음이 터졌다."], ["Alyosha looked at them, and they at him.", "알료샤는 아이들을 바라보았고, 아이들도 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Don’t go near him, he’ll hurt you,”", "\"가까이 가지 마세요, 다쳐요.\""], ["cried Smurov in a warning voice.", "스무로프가 경고하듯 소리쳤다."]], [["“I shan’t ask him about the wisp of tow, for I expect you tease him with that question somehow.", "\"검불 이야기는 묻지 않으마, 너희가 그 질문으로 어떻게든 쟤를 놀려 대는 모양이니까."], ["But I’ll find out from him why you hate him so.”", "하지만 너희가 왜 그렇게 쟤를 미워하는지는 쟤한테 알아봐야겠다.\""]], [["“Find out then, find out,”", "\"그럼 알아보세요, 알아봐요.\""], ["cried the boys, laughing.", "아이들이 웃으며 소리쳤다."]], [["Alyosha crossed the bridge and walked uphill by the fence, straight towards the boy.", "알료샤는 다리를 건너 울타리를 따라 언덕을 올라가 곧장 그 아이에게로 향했다."]], [["“You’d better look out,”", "\"조심하는 게 좋을걸요.\""], ["the boys called after him; “he won’t be afraid of you.", "아이들이 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다. \"쟤는 아저씨를 무서워하지 않을 거예요."], ["He will stab you in a minute, on the sly, as he did Krassotkin.”", "금세 슬그머니 찔러 버릴 거라고요, 크라소트킨한테 했던 것처럼요.\""]], [["The boy waited for him without budging.", "아이는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 그를 기다렸다."], ["Coming up to him, Alyosha saw facing him a child of about nine years old.", "가까이 다가가 보니, 알료샤의 눈앞에 마주 선 것은 아홉 살가량 된 어린아이였다."], ["He was an undersized weakly boy with a thin pale face, with large dark eyes that gazed at him vindictively.", "가냘프고 야윈 데다 창백한 얼굴에, 큼직한 까만 눈으로 그를 원한에 차서 응시하는, 키가 작고 허약한 사내아이였다."], ["He was dressed in a rather shabby old overcoat, which he had monstrously outgrown.", "그는 제법 낡고 해진 헌 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 몸이 자라 도무지 맞지 않을 만큼 작아져 있었다."], ["His bare arms stuck out beyond his sleeves.", "맨살이 드러난 두 팔이 소맷부리 밖으로 비죽 나와 있었다."], ["There was a large patch on the right knee of his trousers, and in his right boot just at the toe there was a big hole in the leather, carefully blackened with ink.", "바지 오른쪽 무릎에는 커다란 헝겊 조각이 덧대어져 있었고, 오른쪽 장화는 발끝 바로 그 자리에 가죽이 크게 구멍 나 있었는데, 잉크로 정성껏 까맣게 칠해 놓은 것이었다."], ["Both the pockets of his great-coat were weighed down with stones.", "그의 외투 양쪽 주머니는 돌멩이로 불룩하니 묵직해져 있었다."], ["Alyosha stopped two steps in front of him, looking inquiringly at him.", "알료샤는 그의 두 걸음 앞에서 멈춰 서서 묻고 싶은 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["The boy, seeing at once from Alyosha’s eyes that he wouldn’t beat him, became less defiant, and addressed him first.", "아이는 알료샤의 눈빛에서 그가 자기를 때리지 않으리라는 것을 단번에 알아채고는 반항기가 다소 누그러져, 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“I am alone, and there are six of them.", "\"난 혼자고, 쟤네는 여섯이야."], ["I’ll beat them all, alone!”", "그래도 나 혼자 쟤네를 다 때려눕힐 거야!\""], ["he said suddenly, with flashing eyes.", "그가 별안간 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다."]], [["“I think one of the stones must have hurt you badly,”", "\"돌멩이 한 개가 너를 심하게 다치게 한 모양이구나.\""], ["observed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 한마디 했다."]], [["“But I hit Smurov on the head!”", "\"하지만 난 스무로프 머리통을 맞혔는걸!\""], ["cried the boy.", "아이가 소리쳤다."]], [["“They told me that you know me, and that you threw a stone at me on purpose,”", "\"쟤네 말로는 네가 나를 알고 있고, 일부러 나한테 돌을 던졌다던데.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["The boy looked darkly at him.", "아이는 음울한 눈으로 그를 쳐다보았다."]], [["“I don’t know you.", "\"난 너를 몰라."], ["Do you know me?”", "넌 나를 아니?\""], ["Alyosha continued.", "알료샤가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Let me alone!”", "\"날 좀 내버려 둬!\""], ["the boy cried irritably; but he did not move, as though he were expecting something, and again there was a vindictive light in his eyes.", "아이가 짜증스럽게 소리쳤다. 그러나 그는 무언가를 기다리기라도 하듯 움직이지 않았고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 원한에 찬 빛이 어렸다."]], [["“Very well, I am going,”", "\"좋아, 난 가마.\""], ["said Alyosha; “only I don’t know you and I don’t tease you.", "알료샤가 말했다. \"다만 난 너를 모르고, 너를 놀리지도 않아."], ["They told me how they tease you, but I don’t want to tease you.", "쟤네가 너를 어떻게 놀리는지 들었지만, 난 너를 놀리고 싶지 않아."], ["Good-by!”", "잘 있어라!\""]], [["“Monk in silk trousers!”", "\"비단 바지를 입은 수도사 놈!\""], ["cried the boy, following Alyosha with the same vindictive and defiant expression, and he threw himself into an attitude of defense, feeling sure that now Alyosha would fall upon him; but Alyosha turned, looked at him, and walked away.", "아이가 여전히 원한에 차서 반항하는 표정으로 알료샤를 뒤쫓듯 소리치고는, 이제 알료샤가 자기에게 덤벼들 것이 분명하다고 여겨 방어 자세를 취했다. 그러나 알료샤는 돌아서서 그를 한 번 바라보고는 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["He had not gone three steps before the biggest stone the boy had in his pocket hit him a painful blow in the back.", "세 걸음도 채 떼기 전에 아이가 주머니에 지니고 있던 가장 큰 돌멩이가 그의 등을 호되게 후려쳤다."]], [["“So you’ll hit a man from behind!", "\"그래, 사람을 등 뒤에서 치는구나!"], ["They tell the truth, then, when they say that you attack on the sly,”", "그렇다면 네가 슬그머니 공격한다는 쟤네 말이 사실이었나 보다.\""], ["said Alyosha, turning round again.", "알료샤가 다시 돌아서며 말했다."], ["This time the boy threw a stone savagely right into Alyosha’s face; but Alyosha just had time to guard himself, and the stone struck him on the elbow.", "이번에는 아이가 돌멩이를 사납게 알료샤의 얼굴을 향해 곧바로 내던졌다. 그러나 알료샤는 가까스로 몸을 막을 틈이 있었고, 돌멩이는 그의 팔꿈치를 맞혔다."]], [["“Aren’t you ashamed?", "\"부끄럽지도 않니?"], ["What have I done to you?”", "내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 했다고?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["The boy waited in silent defiance, certain that now Alyosha would attack him.", "아이는 이제 알료샤가 자기를 덮칠 것이 분명하다고 확신하며 말없이 반항적인 자세로 기다렸다."], ["Seeing that even now he would not, his rage was like a little wild beast’s; he flew at Alyosha himself, and before Alyosha had time to move, the spiteful child had seized his left hand with both of his and bit his middle finger.", "이제도 그가 덤비지 않는 것을 보자, 그의 분노는 작은 들짐승의 것과도 같았다. 그가 직접 알료샤에게 달려들었고, 알료샤가 미처 몸을 움직일 새도 없이 그 악에 받친 아이는 두 손으로 그의 왼손을 움켜잡고 가운뎃손가락을 깨물었다."], ["He fixed his teeth in it and it was ten seconds before he let go.", "그는 거기에 이를 박아 넣고는 십 초가 지나서야 놓아주었다."], ["Alyosha cried out with pain and pulled his finger away with all his might.", "알료샤는 아픔에 비명을 지르며 있는 힘껏 손가락을 빼냈다."], ["The child let go at last and retreated to his former distance.", "아이는 마침내 손을 놓고 처음 있던 자리만큼 물러섰다."], ["Alyosha’s finger had been badly bitten to the bone, close to the nail; it began to bleed.", "알료샤의 손가락은 손톱 가까이까지 뼈에 닿도록 심하게 물려서 피가 흐르기 시작했다."], ["Alyosha took out his handkerchief and bound it tightly round his injured hand.", "알료샤는 손수건을 꺼내어 다친 손에 단단히 동여맸다."], ["He was a full minute bandaging it.", "붕대를 감는 데 꼬박 일 분이 걸렸다."], ["The boy stood waiting all the time.", "아이는 그동안 내내 서서 기다리고 있었다."], ["At last Alyosha raised his gentle eyes and looked at him.", "마침내 알료샤가 온화한 눈을 들어 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["he said, “you see how badly you’ve bitten me.", "그가 말했다. \"네가 나를 얼마나 심하게 물었는지 봐라."], ["That’s enough, isn’t it?", "이만하면 됐지, 안 그래?"], ["Now tell me, what have I done to you?”", "자, 이제 말해 보렴, 내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 했니?\""]], [["The boy stared in amazement.", "아이는 어리둥절해하며 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Though I don’t know you and it’s the first time I’ve seen you,”", "\"내가 너를 모르고, 오늘 처음 너를 본 거지만,\""], ["Alyosha went on with the same serenity, “yet I must have done something to you—you wouldn’t have hurt me like this for nothing.", "알료샤가 여전히 차분하게 말을 이었다. \"그래도 내가 너한테 무슨 짓을 한 게 분명해—아무 까닭도 없이 나를 이렇게 다치게 했을 리는 없으니까."], ["So what have I done?", "그러니 내가 무슨 짓을 했지?"], ["How have I wronged you, tell me?”", "내가 너한테 어떻게 잘못한 거니, 말해 보렴?\""]], [["Instead of answering, the boy broke into a loud tearful wail and ran away.", "아이는 대답하는 대신 큰 소리로 울음을 터뜨리며 달아났다."], ["Alyosha walked slowly after him towards Mihailovsky Street, and for a long time he saw the child running in the distance as fast as ever, not turning his head, and no doubt still keeping up his tearful wail.", "알료샤는 미하일롭스키 거리 쪽으로 그의 뒤를 천천히 따라갔는데, 그 아이가 머리도 돌리지 않고 줄곧 있는 힘껏 멀리 달려가는 모습을, 그리고 틀림없이 여전히 울음을 그치지 않고 있는 모습을 한참 동안 바라보았다."], ["He made up his mind to find him out as soon as he had time, and to solve this mystery.", "그는 짬이 나는 대로 곧 그 아이를 찾아내어 이 수수께끼를 풀어 보리라 마음먹었다."], ["Just now he had not the time.", "다만 지금은 그럴 겨를이 없었다."]], [["# Chapter IV. At The Hohlakovs’", "# Chapter IV. At The Hohlakovs’"]], [["Alyosha soon reached Madame Hohlakov’s house, a handsome stone house of two stories, one of the finest in our town.", "알료샤는 곧 호흘라코바 부인의 집에 다다랐는데, 그것은 이 층짜리 멋들어진 석조 저택으로 우리 고을에서도 손꼽히는 훌륭한 집 가운데 하나였다."], ["Though Madame Hohlakov spent most of her time in another province where she had an estate, or in Moscow, where she had a house of her own, yet she had a house in our town too, inherited from her forefathers.", "호흘라코바 부인은 대개 영지가 있는 다른 현이나 자기 소유의 집이 있는 모스크바에서 시간을 보냈으나, 우리 고을에도 선조 대대로 물려받은 집을 한 채 가지고 있었다."], ["The estate in our district was the largest of her three estates, yet she had been very little in our province before this time.", "우리 군에 있는 영지는 그녀의 세 영지 가운데 가장 큰 것이었으되, 그녀는 이번 일이 있기 전까지는 우리 현에 거의 머문 적이 없었다."], ["She ran out to Alyosha in the hall.", "그녀는 현관까지 달려 나와 알료샤를 맞았다."]], [["“Did you get my letter about the new miracle?”", "\"새로운 기적에 대해 쓴 내 편지는 받으셨어요?\""], ["She spoke rapidly and nervously.", "그녀는 빠르고 초조하게 물었다. \"받았습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “Did you show it to every one?", "\"모든 사람한테 그걸 보여 주셨나요?"], ["He restored the son to his mother!”", "그분이 아들을 그 어머니에게 되돌려 주셨다니!\""]], [["“He is dying to-day,”", "\"그분은 오늘 임종하실 겁니다.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“I have heard, I know, oh, how I long to talk to you, to you or some one, about all this.", "\"들었어요, 알고 있어요, 아아, 이 모든 일에 대해 당신과, 당신이나 누군가와 얼마나 이야기를 나누고 싶었는지 몰라요."], ["No, to you, to you!", "아니, 당신과요, 바로 당신과요!"], ["And how sorry I am I can’t see him!", "그리고 내가 그분을 뵙지 못하는 것이 어찌나 안타까운지요!"], ["The whole town is in excitement, they are all suspense.", "온 고을이 흥분에 들떠 있어요, 다들 마음을 졸이고 있다고요."], ["But now—do you know Katerina Ivanovna is here now?”", "그건 그렇고—저, 카테리나 이바노브나가 지금 여기 와 계신 거 아세요?\""]], [["“Ah, that’s lucky,”", "\"아, 잘됐군요.\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."], ["“Then I shall see her here.", "\"그럼 여기서 그분을 뵐 수 있겠네요."], ["She told me yesterday to be sure to come and see her to-day.”", "어제 오늘 꼭 찾아와 달라고 하셨거든요.\""]], [["“I know, I know all.", "\"알아요, 다 알고 있어요."], ["I’ve heard exactly what happened yesterday—and the atrocious behavior of that—creature.", "어제 무슨 일이 있었는지도 죄다 들었답니다—그리고 그 끔찍한 계집의 망측한 행실까지도요."], ["C’est tragique, and if I’d been in her place I don’t know what I should have done.", "세 트라지크(C'est tragique)예요, 내가 그분 처지였다면 어떻게 했을지 모르겠어요."], ["And your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch, what do you think of him?—my goodness!", "그리고 당신 형 드미트리 표도로비치는, 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하세요?—아이고 맙소사!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, I am forgetting, only fancy; your brother is in there with her, not that dreadful brother who was so shocking yesterday, but the other, Ivan Fyodorovitch, he is sitting with her talking; they are having a serious conversation.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 깜빡했네요, 글쎄 생각 좀 해 보세요. 당신 형이 지금 그분과 함께 저 안에 있어요, 어제 그 무시무시하게 굴던 끔찍한 형이 아니라, 다른 형, 이반 표도로비치 말이에요, 그가 그분과 함께 앉아서 이야기를 나누고 있답니다."], ["If you could only imagine what’s passing between them now—it’s awful, I tell you it’s lacerating, it’s like some incredible tale of horror.", "두 사람이 자못 심각한 대화를 하고 있어요. 지금 그 두 사람 사이에 오가는 일을 상상이나 할 수 있겠어요—끔찍해요, 정말이지 가슴을 찢어 놓는다고요, 무슨 믿을 수 없는 공포담 같다니까요."], ["They are ruining their lives for no reason any one can see.", "두 사람은 누구도 알 수 없는 까닭으로 자기네 인생을 망치고 있어요."], ["They both recognize it and revel in it.", "둘 다 그것을 알면서도 그 안에서 도취되어 있다니까요."], ["I’ve been watching for you!", "나는 당신을 줄곧 기다렸어요!"], ["I’ve been thirsting for you!", "당신을 애타게 그리워했다고요!"], ["It’s too much for me, that’s the worst of it.", "나로서는 도무지 감당이 안 돼요, 그게 무엇보다 견디기 힘들어요."], ["I’ll tell you all about it presently, but now I must speak of something else, the most important thing—I had quite forgotten what’s most important.", "곧 다 이야기해 드리겠지만, 지금은 다른 이야기를 해야겠어요, 가장 중요한 일을요—내가 가장 중요한 것을 깜빡 잊고 있었네요."], ["Tell me, why has Lise been in hysterics?", "말해 보세요, 리즈가 왜 발작을 일으켰을까요?"], ["As soon as she heard you were here, she began to be hysterical!”", "당신이 왔다는 소릴 듣자마자 발작을 시작했지 뭐예요!\""]], [["“Maman, it’s you who are hysterical now, not I,”", "\"마망, 지금 발작을 일으키는 건 어머니지 제가 아니에요.\""], ["Lise’s voice caroled through a tiny crack of the door at the side.", "옆쪽 문틈으로 리즈의 목소리가 노래하듯 새어 나왔다."], ["Her voice sounded as though she wanted to laugh, but was doing her utmost to control it.", "그녀의 목소리는 웃음을 터뜨리고 싶어 안달이 났으면서도 안간힘을 다해 참고 있는 듯이 들렸다."], ["Alyosha at once noticed the crack, and no doubt Lise was peeping through it, but that he could not see.", "알료샤는 그 문틈을 곧바로 알아차렸고, 틀림없이 리즈가 그 틈으로 엿보고 있으련만 그 모습까지는 보이지 않았다."]], [["“And no wonder, Lise, no wonder ...", "\"그것도 무리는 아니지, 리즈, 무리는 아니야……"], ["your caprices will make me hysterical too.", "네 변덕이 나까지 발작을 일으키게 만든다니까."], ["But she is so ill, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she has been so ill all night, feverish and moaning!", "하지만 이 애가 어찌나 아픈지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 밤새 어찌나 앓았는지, 열에 들떠 신음을 했답니다!"], ["I could hardly wait for the morning and for Herzenstube to come.", "아침이 오기를, 헤르첸슈투베가 오기를 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지 몰라요."], ["He says that he can make nothing of it, that we must wait.", "그런데 그이는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없다, 기다려 보는 수밖에 없다고만 하지 뭐예요."], ["Herzenstube always comes and says that he can make nothing of it.", "헤르첸슈투베는 올 때마다 영문을 알 수 없다는 소리만 한다니까요."], ["As soon as you approached the house, she screamed, fell into hysterics, and insisted on being wheeled back into this room here.”", "당신이 이 집에 가까이 다가오자마자 이 애가 비명을 지르더니 발작을 일으켰고, 도로 이 방으로 들여보내 달라고 막무가내였답니다.\""]], [["“Mamma, I didn’t know he had come.", "\"엄마, 난 그가 온 줄도 몰랐어요."], ["It wasn’t on his account I wanted to be wheeled into this room.”", "그 사람 때문에 이 방으로 들어가고 싶어 한 게 아니라고요.\""]], [["“That’s not true, Lise, Yulia ran to tell you that Alexey Fyodorovitch was coming.", "\"그건 사실이 아니야, 리즈, 율리야가 너한테 알렉세이 표도로비치가 오고 있다고 알리러 달려갔잖니."], ["She was on the look-out for you.”", "그 애가 너를 살펴 주고 있었어.\""]], [["“My darling mamma, it’s not at all clever of you.", "\"사랑하는 엄마, 그건 조금도 영리한 말씀이 못 돼요."], ["But if you want to make up for it and say something very clever, dear mamma, you’d better tell our honored visitor, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that he has shown his want of wit by venturing to us after what happened yesterday and although every one is laughing at him.”", "하지만 그걸 만회하시고 아주 영리한 말씀을 하고 싶으시면, 사랑하는 엄마, 우리의 귀하신 손님 알렉세이 표도로비치께 차라리 이렇게 말씀드리세요. 어제 그런 일이 있었고 다들 그분을 비웃고 있는데도 굳이 우리를 찾아왔으니 분별이 모자란 분이라고 말이에요.\""]], [["“Lise, you go too far.", "\"리즈, 너 너무 지나치다."], ["I declare I shall have to be severe.", "정말이지 단단히 야단을 쳐야겠구나."], ["Who laughs at him?", "누가 그분을 비웃는다고 그러니?"], ["I am so glad he has come, I need him, I can’t do without him.", "나는 그분이 와 주셔서 어찌나 기쁜지 몰라, 나는 그분이 필요해, 그분 없이는 안 된다고."], ["Oh, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I am exceedingly unhappy!”", "아아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 더없이 불행하답니다!\""]], [["“But what’s the matter with you, mamma, darling?”", "\"그런데 무슨 일이 있으신 거예요, 사랑하는 엄마?\""]], [["“Ah, your caprices, Lise, your fidgetiness, your illness, that awful night of fever, that awful everlasting Herzenstube, everlasting, everlasting, that’s the worst of it!", "\"아아, 네 변덕 말이다, 리즈, 네 안절부절못함, 네 병, 그 끔찍한 열병의 밤, 그 지긋지긋한 헤르첸슈투베, 끝도 없이, 끝도 없이 되풀이되는 그 사람, 그게 무엇보다 견디기 힘들어!"], ["Everything, in fact, everything....", "모든 게, 사실 모든 게 다……"], ["Even that miracle, too!", "그 기적까지도 그래!"], ["Oh, how it has upset me, how it has shattered me, that miracle, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch!", "아아, 그 기적이 나를 얼마나 뒤흔들어 놓았는지, 얼마나 산산조각 냈는지 몰라요, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치!"], ["And that tragedy in the drawing-room, it’s more than I can bear, I warn you.", "그리고 응접실에서 벌어지는 그 비극이라니, 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요, 미리 말해 두지만요."], ["I can’t bear it.", "견딜 수가 없다고요."], ["A comedy, perhaps, not a tragedy.", "어쩌면 그건 비극이 아니라 희극인지도 모르겠네요."], ["Tell me, will Father Zossima live till to-morrow, will he?", "말해 보세요, 조시마 장로님이 내일까지 살아 계실까요, 그러실까요?"], ["Oh, my God!", "아아, 하느님!"], ["What is happening to me?", "내가 어떻게 되어 가는 걸까요?"], ["Every minute I close my eyes and see that it’s all nonsense, all nonsense.”", "시시각각 눈을 감으면 모든 게 다 부질없다, 죄다 부질없다는 생각만 들어요.\""]], [["“I should be very grateful,”", "\"한 가지 부탁드려도 될지요.\""], ["Alyosha interrupted suddenly, “if you could give me a clean rag to bind up my finger with.", "알료샤가 불쑥 말을 가로막았다. \"손가락을 동여맬 깨끗한 헝겊 조각을 좀 주신다면 정말 고맙겠습니다."], ["I have hurt it, and it’s very painful.”", "손가락을 다쳤는데 몹시 아프거든요.\""]], [["Alyosha unbound his bitten finger.", "알료샤는 물어뜯긴 손가락을 풀어 보였다."], ["The handkerchief was soaked with blood.", "손수건은 피로 흥건히 젖어 있었다."], ["Madame Hohlakov screamed and shut her eyes.", "호흘라코바 부인은 비명을 지르며 눈을 감았다."]], [["“Good heavens, what a wound, how awful!”", "\"아이고 맙소사, 이게 무슨 상처람, 어쩜 이리 끔찍할까!\""]], [["But as soon as Lise saw Alyosha’s finger through the crack, she flung the door wide open.", "그러나 리즈는 문틈으로 알료샤의 손가락을 보자마자 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다."]], [["“Come, come here,”", "\"이리 오세요, 이리로요.\""], ["she cried, imperiously.", "그녀가 위엄 있게 외쳤다."], ["“No nonsense now!", "\"이젠 허튼소리 마시고요!"], ["Good heavens, why did you stand there saying nothing about it all this time?", "아이고 세상에, 어쩌면 여태 거기 서서 그 얘긴 한마디도 안 하고 계셨대요?"], ["He might have bled to death, mamma!", "피가 다 빠져 죽을 수도 있었잖아요, 엄마!"], ["How did you do it?", "어쩌다 이렇게 됐어요?"], ["Water, water!", "물, 물요!"], ["You must wash it first of all, simply hold it in cold water to stop the pain, and keep it there, keep it there....", "무엇보다 먼저 씻어야 해요, 그냥 찬물에 담그고 있으면 아픈 게 가라앉아요, 그대로 담가 두세요, 담가 두시라니까요……"], ["Make haste, mamma, some water in a slop-basin.", "어서요, 엄마, 세숫대야에 물 좀요."], ["But do make haste,”", "어서 서두르세요.\""], ["she finished nervously.", "그녀가 초조하게 말을 맺었다."], ["She was quite frightened at the sight of Alyosha’s wound.", "알료샤의 상처를 보고 그녀는 적잖이 겁에 질려 있었다."]], [["“Shouldn’t we send for Herzenstube?”", "\"헤르첸슈투베를 부르러 보내야 하지 않겠니?\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov.", "호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Mamma, you’ll be the death of me.", "\"엄마, 나를 죽일 작정이세요."], ["Your Herzenstube will come and say that he can make nothing of it!", "엄마의 그 헤르첸슈투베가 와서는 또 영문을 알 수 없다고 할 거잖아요!"], ["Water, water!", "물, 물요!"], ["Mamma, for goodness’ sake go yourself and hurry Yulia, she is such a slowcoach and never can come quickly!", "엄마, 제발 직접 가서 율리야 좀 재촉해 주세요, 그 애는 어찌나 굼뜬지 빨리 오는 법이 없다니까요!"], ["Make haste, mamma, or I shall die.”", "어서요, 엄마, 안 그러면 나 죽을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Why, it’s nothing much,”", "\"아니, 별것 아닌데요.\""], ["cried Alyosha, frightened at this alarm.", "이 호들갑에 도리어 겁이 난 알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["Yulia ran in with water and Alyosha put his finger in it.", "율리야가 물을 가지고 달려 들어오자 알료샤는 손가락을 그 안에 담갔다."]], [["“Some lint, mamma, for mercy’s sake, bring some lint and that muddy caustic lotion for wounds, what’s it called?", "\"무명실 좀요, 엄마, 제발 부탁이에요, 무명실하고 그 상처에 바르는 거무튀튀한 부식성 물약도요, 그게 뭐더라?"], ["We’ve got some.", "우리 집에 있잖아요."], ["You know where the bottle is, mamma; it’s in your bedroom in the right-hand cupboard, there’s a big bottle of it there with the lint.”", "그 약병이 어디 있는지 아시죠, 엄마. 엄마 침실 오른쪽 찬장에 있어요, 거기 무명실하고 함께 큰 병이 하나 있다고요.\""]], [["“I’ll bring everything in a minute, Lise, only don’t scream and don’t fuss.", "\"금방 다 가져오마, 리즈, 그러니 소리 좀 지르지 말고 부산 떨지 마라."], ["You see how bravely Alexey Fyodorovitch bears it.", "봐라, 알렉세이 표도로비치가 얼마나 의젓하게 견디고 계신지."], ["Where did you get such a dreadful wound, Alexey Fyodorovitch?”", "어쩌다 이렇게 끔찍한 상처를 입으셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?\""]], [["Madame Hohlakov hastened away.", "호흘라코바 부인은 서둘러 자리를 떴다."], ["This was all Lise was waiting for.", "리즈가 기다리던 것이 바로 이것이었다."]], [["“First of all, answer the question, where did you get hurt like this?”", "\"우선 질문에 대답부터 하세요, 어쩌다 이렇게 다치셨어요?\""], ["she asked Alyosha, quickly.", "그녀가 알료샤에게 다급히 물었다."], ["“And then I’ll talk to you about something quite different.", "\"그러고 나서 전혀 다른 이야기를 할 거예요."], ["Well?”", "자요?\""]], [["Instinctively feeling that the time of her mother’s absence was precious for her, Alyosha hastened to tell her of his enigmatic meeting with the schoolboys in the fewest words possible.", "어머니가 자리를 비운 그 시간이 그녀에게 소중하다는 것을 본능적으로 느낀 알료샤는, 학생들과의 그 알 수 없는 마주침을 되도록 짧은 말로 서둘러 들려주었다."], ["Lise clasped her hands at his story.", "리즈는 그의 이야기에 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“How can you, and in that dress too, associate with schoolboys?”", "\"아니 어떻게, 그것도 그런 옷차림을 하고 학생들과 어울릴 수가 있어요?\""], ["she cried angrily, as though she had a right to control him.", "그녀는 마치 그를 다스릴 권리라도 있는 양 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“You are nothing but a boy yourself if you can do that, a perfect boy!", "\"그런 짓을 하시다니 당신도 한낱 어린애일 뿐이에요, 영락없는 어린애라고요!"], ["But you must find out for me about that horrid boy and tell me all about it, for there’s some mystery in it.", "하지만 그 고약한 아이에 대해 알아내서 죄다 말해 주셔야 해요, 거기엔 뭔가 수수께끼가 있으니까요."], ["Now for the second thing, but first a question: does the pain prevent you talking about utterly unimportant things, but talking sensibly?”", "자, 이제 두 번째 일인데, 그 전에 질문 하나요. 그 아픔 때문에 전혀 대수롭지 않은 이야기를, 그렇지만 진지한 이야기를 나누는 데 지장이 있나요?\""]], [["“Of course not, and I don’t feel much pain now.”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요, 게다가 지금은 별로 아프지도 않은걸요.\""]], [["“That’s because your finger is in the water.", "\"그건 손가락을 물에 담그고 있어서예요."], ["It must be changed directly, for it will get warm in a minute.", "곧바로 갈아 줘야 해요, 금방 미지근해질 테니까요."], ["Yulia, bring some ice from the cellar and another basin of water.", "율리야, 지하실에서 얼음 좀 가져오고 물도 한 대야 더 가져와."], ["Now she is gone, I can speak; will you give me the letter I sent you yesterday, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch—be quick, for mamma will be back in a minute and I don’t want—”", "자, 그 애가 갔으니 이제 말할 수 있겠네요. 어제 제가 보낸 편지를 돌려주시겠어요, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치—어서요, 엄마가 곧 돌아올 텐데 저는 싫거든요—\""]], [["“I haven’t got the letter.”", "\"편지를 가지고 있지 않은데요.\""]], [["“That’s not true, you have.", "\"그건 사실이 아니에요, 가지고 계시잖아요."], ["I knew you would say that.", "그렇게 말씀하실 줄 알았어요."], ["You’ve got it in that pocket.", "그 주머니에 넣어 두셨죠."], ["I’ve been regretting that joke all night.", "밤새도록 그 장난을 후회했어요."], ["Give me back the letter at once, give it me.”", "당장 편지를 돌려주세요, 어서 주시라니까요.\""]], [["“I’ve left it at home.”", "\"집에 두고 왔습니다.\""]], [["“But you can’t consider me as a child, a little girl, after that silly joke!", "\"하지만 그 어리석은 장난을 했다고 해서 저를 어린애 취급, 계집애 취급 하시면 안 돼요!"], ["I beg your pardon for that silliness, but you must bring me the letter, if you really haven’t got it—bring it to-day, you must, you must.”", "그 어리석은 짓에 대해서는 용서를 빌어요, 하지만 편지는 꼭 가져다주셔야 해요, 정말로 가지고 계시지 않다면요—오늘 가져오세요, 꼭, 꼭이요.\""]], [["“To-day I can’t possibly, for I am going back to the monastery and I shan’t come and see you for the next two days—three or four perhaps—for Father Zossima—”", "\"오늘은 도저히 안 돼요, 수도원으로 돌아가야 하거든요, 그래서 앞으로 이틀은—어쩌면 사나흘쯤—당신을 찾아뵙지 못할 거예요, 조시마 장로님 때문에—\""]], [["“Four days, what nonsense! Listen.", "\"나흘이라니, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소릴!"], ["Did you laugh at me very much?”", "들어 보세요. 저를 많이 비웃으셨죠?\""]], [["“I didn’t laugh at all.”", "\"전혀 비웃지 않았는데요.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because I believed all you said.”", "\"당신이 쓴 말을 다 믿었으니까요.\""]], [["“You are insulting me!”", "\"저를 모욕하시는군요!\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에요."], ["As soon as I read it, I thought that all that would come to pass, for as soon as Father Zossima dies, I am to leave the monastery.", "그걸 읽자마자 그 모든 일이 다 이루어지리라고 생각했어요, 조시마 장로님이 돌아가시면 저는 수도원을 떠나야 하니까요."], ["Then I shall go back and finish my studies, and when you reach the legal age we will be married.", "그러면 돌아가서 학업을 마칠 거고, 당신이 혼인할 나이가 되면 우리는 결혼하게 되겠죠. 저는 당신을 사랑하게 될 거예요."], ["I shall love you. Though I haven’t had time to think about it, I believe I couldn’t find a better wife than you, and Father Zossima tells me I must marry.”", "아직 그 일을 곰곰이 생각해 볼 겨를은 없었지만, 당신보다 더 나은 아내를 찾을 수는 없으리라 믿어요, 그리고 조시마 장로님도 제게 결혼을 하라고 하셨거든요.\""]], [["“But I am a cripple, wheeled about in a chair,”", "\"하지만 저는 의자에 실려 다니는 불구의 몸인걸요.\""], ["laughed Lise, flushing crimson.", "리즈가 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다."]], [["“I’ll wheel you about myself, but I’m sure you’ll get well by then.”", "\"제가 직접 의자를 밀어 드릴게요, 하지만 그때쯤이면 당신은 분명히 나을 거예요.\""]], [["“But you are mad,”", "\"하지만 당신은 미쳤어요.\""], ["said Lise, nervously, “to make all this nonsense out of a joke!", "리즈가 초조하게 말했다. \"장난 하나로 이런 터무니없는 소릴 다 늘어놓다니요!"], ["Here’s mamma, very à propos, perhaps.", "어쩌면 마침 잘 오셨네요, 엄마."], ["Mamma, how slow you always are, how can you be so long!", "엄마, 어쩜 그렇게 늘 굼뜨세요, 어떻게 이리도 오래 걸리실 수가 있어요!"], ["And here’s Yulia with the ice!”", "율리야도 얼음을 가지고 왔네요!\""]], [["“Oh, Lise, don’t scream, above all things don’t scream.", "\"아아, 리즈, 소리 좀 지르지 마라, 무엇보다 소리 지르지 말라니까."], ["That scream drives me ...", "네가 그렇게 소리를 지르면 내가 정신이 다……"], ["How can I help it when you put the lint in another place?", "무명실을 딴 데 갖다 두니 내가 어쩌겠니?"], ["I’ve been hunting and hunting—I do believe you did it on purpose.”", "한참을 찾고 또 찾았다—아무래도 네가 일부러 그런 것 같구나.\""]], [["“But I couldn’t tell that he would come with a bad finger, or else perhaps I might have done it on purpose.", "\"하지만 그가 다친 손가락을 하고 올 줄을 제가 어떻게 알았겠어요, 그걸 알았다면 어쩌면 일부러 그랬을지도 모르죠."], ["My darling mamma, you begin to say really witty things.”", "사랑하는 엄마, 이제 정말로 재치 있는 말씀을 하기 시작하시네요.\""]], [["“Never mind my being witty, but I must say you show nice feeling for Alexey Fyodorovitch’s sufferings!", "\"내 재치 따윈 아무래도 좋다만, 너야말로 알렉세이 표도로비치의 고통에 참 갸륵한 마음씨를 보이는구나!"], ["Oh, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, what’s killing me is no one thing in particular, not Herzenstube, but everything together, that’s what is too much for me.”", "아아, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나를 죽이는 건 어느 한 가지가 아니에요, 헤르첸슈투베도 아니고, 죄다 한꺼번에 몰아닥치는 거예요, 그게 바로 제가 감당 못 하는 거랍니다.\""]], [["“That’s enough, mamma, enough about Herzenstube,”", "\"이제 그만하세요, 엄마, 헤르첸슈투베 얘긴 그만이에요.\""], ["Lise laughed gayly.", "리즈가 명랑하게 웃었다."], ["“Make haste with the lint and the lotion, mamma.", "\"어서 무명실하고 물약을 발라 주세요, 엄마."], ["That’s simply Goulard’s water, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I remember the name now, but it’s a splendid lotion.", "이건 그냥 굴라르 물약이에요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이제 이름이 생각나네요, 그래도 아주 훌륭한 물약이랍니다."], ["Would you believe it, mamma, on the way here he had a fight with the boys in the street, and it was a boy bit his finger, isn’t he a child, a child himself?", "글쎄 믿으시겠어요, 엄마, 이분이 오는 길에 거리에서 사내애들하고 싸움을 했고, 어떤 아이가 손가락을 물어뜯었다지 뭐예요, 이분도 어린애지요, 그 자신이 어린애 아니에요?"], ["Is he fit to be married after that?", "그러고도 결혼할 자격이 있나요?"], ["For only fancy, he wants to be married, mamma.", "글쎄 생각 좀 해 보세요, 이분이 결혼을 하고 싶어 한대요, 엄마."], ["Just think of him married, wouldn’t it be funny, wouldn’t it be awful?”", "이분이 결혼한 모습을 한번 상상해 보세요, 우습지 않겠어요, 끔찍하지 않겠어요?\""]], [["And Lise kept laughing her thin hysterical giggle, looking slyly at Alyosha.", "리즈는 알료샤를 짓궂게 흘끔거리며 가냘프고 신경질적인 키득거림을 멈추지 않았다."]], [["“But why married, Lise?", "\"하지만 결혼이라니 무슨 소리니, 리즈?"], ["What makes you talk of such a thing?", "어쩌다 그런 얘길 다 꺼낸 거야?"], ["It’s quite out of place—and perhaps the boy was rabid.”", "도무지 격에 맞지 않는 소리로구나—게다가 어쩌면 그 아이가 광견병에 걸렸을지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Why, mamma!", "\"어머나, 엄마!"], ["As though there were rabid boys!”", "광견병에 걸린 아이가 어디 있어요!\""]], [["“Why not, Lise, as though I had said something stupid!", "\"왜 없겠니, 리즈, 마치 내가 무슨 어리석은 소리라도 한 양 그러는구나!"], ["Your boy might have been bitten by a mad dog and he would become mad and bite any one near him.", "그 아이가 미친개한테 물렸을지도 모르잖니, 그러면 그 애도 미쳐서 가까이 있는 사람을 닥치는 대로 물어뜯을 거 아니냐."], ["How well she has bandaged it, Alexey Fyodorovitch!", "정말 솜씨 좋게 잘도 동여맸구나, 알렉세이 표도로비치!"], ["I couldn’t have done it.", "나라면 이렇게는 못 했을 거예요."], ["Do you still feel the pain?”", "아직도 아프세요?\""]], [["“It’s nothing much now.”", "\"이젠 별로 아프지 않습니다.\""]], [["“You don’t feel afraid of water?”", "\"물이 무섭진 않으세요?\""], ["asked Lise.", "리즈가 물었다."]], [["“Come, that’s enough, Lise, perhaps I really was rather too quick talking of the boy being rabid, and you pounced upon it at once Katerina Ivanovna has only just heard that you are here, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she simply rushed at me, she’s dying to see you, dying!”", "\"자, 그만하렴, 리즈, 어쩌면 내가 정말 그 아이가 광견병에 걸렸다는 말을 너무 성급하게 했나 보다, 너는 곧장 그 말꼬리를 물고 늘어지는구나. 카테리나 이바노브나가 당신이 여기 와 있다는 걸 방금 막 들었어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 부랴부랴 내게 달려왔다니까요, 당신을 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 안달이 나서요!\""]], [["“Ach, mamma, go to them yourself.", "\"아이, 엄마, 직접 그분들한테 가 보세요."], ["He can’t go just now, he is in too much pain.”", "이분은 지금 갈 수가 없어요, 너무 아프시니까요.\""]], [["“Not at all, I can go quite well,”", "\"천만에요, 얼마든지 갈 수 있습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["You are going away?", "가시겠다고요?"], ["Is that what you say?”", "정말 그렇게 말씀하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“Well, when I’ve seen them, I’ll come back here and we can talk as much as you like.", "\"그분들을 뵙고 나서 다시 이리로 돌아올게요, 그러면 당신이 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 이야기를 나눌 수 있어요."], ["But I should like to see Katerina Ivanovna at once, for I am very anxious to be back at the monastery as soon as I can.”", "하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 지금 곧 뵙고 싶어요, 되도록 빨리 수도원으로 돌아가야 해서 마음이 몹시 급하거든요.\""]], [["“Mamma, take him away quickly.", "\"엄마, 어서 이분을 데려가 주세요."], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, don’t trouble to come and see me afterwards, but go straight back to your monastery and a good riddance.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 그러고 나서 굳이 저를 찾아오느라 애쓸 것 없이 곧장 수도원으로 돌아가시는 게 차라리 잘된 일이에요."], ["I want to sleep, I didn’t sleep all night.”", "저는 자고 싶어요, 밤새 한숨도 못 잤거든요.\""]], [["“Ah, Lise, you are only making fun, but how I wish you would sleep!”", "\"아아, 리즈, 또 장난을 치는구나, 하지만 정말이지 네가 좀 자 주었으면 얼마나 좋을까!\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov.", "호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“I don’t know what I’ve done....", "\"제가 무슨 잘못을 했는지 모르겠네요……"], ["I’ll stay another three minutes, five if you like,”", "삼 분만, 원하신다면 오 분만 더 있겠습니다.\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 우물거렸다."]], [["“Even five!", "\"오 분씩이나요!"], ["Do take him away quickly, mamma, he is a monster.”", "어서 이분을 데려가 주세요, 엄마, 정말 무서운 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Lise, you are crazy.", "\"리즈, 너 미쳤구나."], ["Let us go, Alexey Fyodorovitch, she is too capricious to-day.", "자, 가시죠, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이 애가 오늘은 너무 변덕스러워서요."], ["I am afraid to cross her.", "비위를 거스를까 봐 겁이 나네요."], ["Oh, the trouble one has with nervous girls!", "아아, 신경이 예민한 여자애를 데리고 사느라 겪는 고생이라니!"], ["Perhaps she really will be able to sleep after seeing you.", "어쩌면 이 애가 당신을 보고 나서 정말로 잠들 수 있을지도 모르겠어요."], ["How quickly you have made her sleepy, and how fortunate it is!”", "어쩜 이렇게 빨리 이 애를 졸리게 만드셨는지, 정말 다행스러운 일이네요!\""]], [["“Ah, mamma, how sweetly you talk!", "\"아아, 엄마, 어쩜 그렇게 다정하게 말씀하세요!"], ["I must kiss you for it, mamma.”", "그 말씀에 입을 맞춰 드려야겠어요, 엄마.\""]], [["“And I kiss you too, Lise.", "\"나도 네게 입을 맞추마, 리즈."], ["Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov began mysteriously and importantly, speaking in a rapid whisper.", "호흘라코바 부인이 자못 비밀스럽고 엄숙하게, 빠른 속삭임으로 입을 열었다."], ["“I don’t want to suggest anything, I don’t want to lift the veil, you will see for yourself what’s going on.", "\"저는 무언가를 넌지시 일러 주려는 것도, 베일을 들춰 보이려는 것도 아니에요, 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지는 당신 눈으로 직접 보시게 될 거예요."], ["It’s appalling.", "정말이지 소름이 끼친답니다."], ["It’s the most fantastic farce.", "더없이 황당무계한 익살극이라니까요."], ["She loves your brother, Ivan, and she is doing her utmost to persuade herself she loves your brother, Dmitri.", "그 애는 당신 형 이반을 사랑하면서도, 당신 형 드미트리를 사랑한다고 자기 자신을 설득하려 갖은 애를 쓰고 있어요."], ["It’s appalling!", "정말 소름 끼치는 일이죠!"], ["I’ll go in with you, and if they don’t turn me out, I’ll stay to the end.”", "저도 당신과 함께 들어갈 거예요, 그리고 저들이 저를 내쫓지만 않는다면 끝까지 남아 있겠어요.\""]], [["# Chapter V. A Laceration In The Drawing-Room", "# Chapter V. A Laceration In The Drawing-Room"]], [["But in the drawing-room the conversation was already over.", "그러나 응접실에서는 이미 이야기가 끝나 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna was greatly excited, though she looked resolute.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 단호한 표정이었으되 몹시 흥분해 있었다."], ["At the moment Alyosha and Madame Hohlakov entered, Ivan Fyodorovitch stood up to take leave.", "알료샤와 호흘라코바 부인이 들어선 순간, 이반 표도로비치는 작별을 고하려고 자리에서 일어선 참이었다."], ["His face was rather pale, and Alyosha looked at him anxiously.", "그의 얼굴은 다소 창백했고, 알료샤는 근심 어린 눈길로 그를 바라보았다."], ["For this moment was to solve a doubt, a harassing enigma which had for some time haunted Alyosha.", "바로 이 순간이, 얼마 전부터 알료샤를 줄곧 괴롭혀 온 의혹, 그 성가신 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 것이기 때문이었다."], ["During the preceding month it had been several times suggested to him that his brother Ivan was in love with Katerina Ivanovna, and, what was more, that he meant “to carry her off”", "지난 한 달 동안 그에게는 형 이반이 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하고 있으며, 더구나 그녀를 드미트리에게서 \"빼앗으려\" 한다는 암시가 여러 차례 던져졌다."], ["from Dmitri. Until quite lately the idea seemed to Alyosha monstrous, though it worried him extremely.", "바로 얼마 전까지만 해도 알료샤는 그런 생각이 어처구니없게 여겨졌으나, 그래도 그것이 그를 몹시 불안하게 했다."], ["He loved both his brothers, and dreaded such rivalry between them.", "그는 두 형을 다 사랑했고, 그들 사이에 그런 경쟁이 벌어지는 것을 두려워했다."], ["Meantime, Dmitri had said outright on the previous day that he was glad that Ivan was his rival, and that it was a great assistance to him, Dmitri.", "그런데 드미트리는 바로 전날, 이반이 자기의 연적이 되어서 기쁘며 그것이 자기, 곧 드미트리에게 크나큰 도움이 된다고 대놓고 말했던 것이다. 그것이 어떻게 그에게 도움이 된다는 말인가?"], ["In what way did it assist him?", "그루셴카와 결혼하기 위해서?"], ["To marry Grushenka? But that Alyosha considered the worst thing possible.", "그러나 그것이야말로 알료샤가 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 나쁜 일로 여기는 것이었다."], ["Besides all this, Alyosha had till the evening before implicitly believed that Katerina Ivanovna had a steadfast and passionate love for Dmitri; but he had only believed it till the evening before.", "이 모든 것 외에도, 알료샤는 바로 어제저녁까지만 해도 카테리나 이바노브나가 드미트리를 향해 변함없고 열정적인 사랑을 품고 있다고 굳게 믿어 왔다. 그러나 그것을 믿은 것도 어제저녁까지였다."], ["He had fancied, too, that she was incapable of loving a man like Ivan, and that she did love Dmitri, and loved him just as he was, in spite of all the strangeness of such a passion.", "그는 또한 그녀가 이반 같은 사내를 사랑할 수는 없으며, 그녀가 사랑하는 것은 드미트리이고, 그런 열정의 온갖 기이함에도 불구하고 그녀가 드미트리를 있는 그대로 사랑한다고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["But during yesterday’s scene with Grushenka another idea had struck him. The word “lacerating,”", "그러나 어제 그루셴카와의 그 장면이 벌어지는 동안 또 다른 생각이 그의 머리를 쳤다."], ["which Madame Hohlakov had just uttered, almost made him start, because half waking up towards daybreak that night he had cried out “Laceration, laceration,” probably applying it to his dream.", "호흘라코바 부인이 방금 입에 올린 \"찢어 발기는\"이라는 말에 그는 거의 흠칫 놀랄 뻔했으니, 그날 밤 동틀 무렵 반쯤 잠에서 깨면서 그가 \"찢어 발기는 고통, 찢어 발기는 고통\"이라고 외쳤기 때문인데, 아마 자기 꿈에 빗대어 한 말이었으리라."], ["He had been dreaming all night of the previous day’s scene at Katerina Ivanovna’s.", "그는 밤새도록 전날 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 벌어진 장면을 꿈꾸었던 것이다."], ["Now Alyosha was impressed by Madame Hohlakov’s blunt and persistent assertion that Katerina Ivanovna was in love with Ivan, and only deceived herself through some sort of pose, from “self-laceration,” and tortured herself by her pretended love for Dmitri from some fancied duty of gratitude.", "그런데 카테리나 이바노브나가 실은 이반을 사랑하고 있으며, 다만 일종의 가식 때문에, \"자기 학대\"에서 스스로를 속이고 있을 뿐이고, 무슨 상상 속의 보은의 의무 때문에 드미트리를 사랑하는 척 자신을 괴롭히고 있을 뿐이라는 호흘라코바 부인의 무뚝뚝하고 집요한 단언이 이제 알료샤에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["he thought, “perhaps the whole truth lies in those words.”", "하고 그는 생각했다. \"어쩌면 모든 진실이 그 말 속에 들어 있는지도 모른다.\""], ["But in that case what was Ivan’s position?", "그러나 그렇다면 이반의 처지는 어떻게 되는가?"], ["Alyosha felt instinctively that a character like Katerina Ivanovna’s must dominate, and she could only dominate some one like Dmitri, and never a man like Ivan.", "알료샤는 본능적으로 느꼈다. 카테리나 이바노브나 같은 성격은 반드시 상대를 지배해야 하며, 그녀가 지배할 수 있는 것은 오직 드미트리 같은 사내뿐이고 이반 같은 사내는 결코 아니라는 것을."], ["For Dmitri might at last submit to her domination “to his own happiness”", "드미트리는 끝내 그녀의 지배에 \"자기 자신의 행복을 위해\""], ["(which was what Alyosha would have desired), but Ivan—no, Ivan could not submit to her, and such submission would not give him happiness.", "굴복할 수도 있겠지만(그것이야말로 알료샤가 바라 마지않는 바였다), 이반은—아니, 이반은 그녀에게 굴복할 수 없으며, 그런 굴복은 그에게 행복을 주지도 못할 것이었다."], ["Alyosha could not help believing that of Ivan.", "알료샤는 이반에 대해 그렇게 믿지 않을 수 없었다."], ["And now all these doubts and reflections flitted through his mind as he entered the drawing-room.", "그리고 이제 이 모든 의혹과 상념이 그가 응접실로 들어서는 동안 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["Another idea, too, forced itself upon him: “What if she loved neither of them—neither Ivan nor Dmitri?”", "또 하나의 생각도 그에게 밀려들었다. \"만약 그녀가 둘 중 누구도—이반도 드미트리도—사랑하지 않는다면 어쩐단 말인가?\""]], [["It must be noted that Alyosha felt as it were ashamed of his own thoughts and blamed himself when they kept recurring to him during the last month.", "여기서 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 지난 한 달 동안 그런 생각이 자꾸만 떠오를 때마다 알료샤가 자기 자신의 생각을 어쩐지 부끄러워하며 스스로를 책망했다는 점이다."], ["“What do I know about love and women and how can I decide such questions?”", "\"사랑이며 여자에 대해 내가 무얼 안다고, 어떻게 그런 문제를 결정할 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["he thought reproachfully, after such doubts and surmises.", "그런 의혹과 추측 끝에 그는 자책하듯 이렇게 생각하곤 했다."], ["And yet it was impossible not to think about it.", "그러면서도 그것을 생각하지 않기란 불가능했다."], ["He felt instinctively that this rivalry was of immense importance in his brothers’ lives and that a great deal depended upon it.", "그는 이 경쟁이 형들의 삶에서 엄청나게 중대한 일이며, 거기에 많은 것이 달려 있음을 본능적으로 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["“One reptile will devour the other,”", "\"한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹을 거다.\""], ["Ivan had pronounced the day before, speaking in anger of his father and Dmitri.", "이반은 바로 전날 자기 아버지와 드미트리를 두고 화가 나서 이렇게 단언했다."], ["So Ivan looked upon Dmitri as a reptile, and perhaps had long done so.", "그러니까 이반은 드미트리를 한 마리 독사로 보고 있었고, 어쩌면 오래전부터 그래 왔는지도 몰랐다."], ["Was it perhaps since he had known Katerina Ivanovna?", "그것이 혹 카테리나 이바노브나를 알게 된 뒤부터였을까?"], ["That phrase had, of course, escaped Ivan unawares yesterday, but that only made it more important.", "물론 그 말은 어제 이반이 무심코 흘린 것이었으되, 그렇기에 오히려 더 중대한 것이었다."], ["If he felt like that, what chance was there of peace?", "그가 그런 심정이라면, 평화의 가망이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["Were there not, on the contrary, new grounds for hatred and hostility in their family?", "오히려 그들 가문 안에 증오와 적대의 새로운 빌미가 생긴 것은 아닌가?"], ["And with which of them was Alyosha to sympathize?", "그리고 알료샤는 그들 가운데 누구의 편을 들어야 하는가?"], ["And what was he to wish for each of them?", "또 그들 각자를 위해 무엇을 바라야 한단 말인가?"], ["He loved them both, but what could he desire for each in the midst of these conflicting interests?", "그는 둘 다 사랑했으나, 이렇게 서로 충돌하는 이해관계 한복판에서 각자를 위해 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["He might go quite astray in this maze, and Alyosha’s heart could not endure uncertainty, because his love was always of an active character.", "그는 이 미궁 속에서 영영 길을 잃을 수도 있었고, 게다가 알료샤의 마음은 불확실함을 견뎌 내지 못했으니, 그의 사랑은 언제나 능동적인 성격의 것이었기 때문이다."], ["He was incapable of passive love.", "그는 수동적인 사랑이라고는 할 줄 모르는 사람이었다."], ["If he loved any one, he set to work at once to help him.", "누군가를 사랑하면 그는 곧장 그를 도우려고 나섰다."], ["And to do so he must know what he was aiming at; he must know for certain what was best for each, and having ascertained this it was natural for him to help them both.", "그러려면 자기가 무엇을 겨누고 있는지를 알아야 했고, 각자에게 무엇이 최선인지를 확실히 알아야 했으며, 그것을 확인한 뒤에야 둘 다 돕는 것이 그에게는 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["But instead of a definite aim, he found nothing but uncertainty and perplexity on all sides.", "그러나 뚜렷한 목표 대신 그가 발견한 것은 사방에 깔린 불확실함과 당혹뿐이었다."], ["“It was lacerating,” as was said just now.", "방금 말한 대로 \"그것은 찢어 발기는 고통\"이었다."], ["But what could he understand even in this “laceration”?", "그러나 그는 이 \"찢어 발기는 고통\"이라는 것에서조차 무엇을 이해할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He did not understand the first word in this perplexing maze.", "그는 이 당혹스러운 미궁 속에서 첫마디조차 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["Seeing Alyosha, Katerina Ivanovna said quickly and joyfully to Ivan, who had already got up to go, “A minute!", "알료샤를 본 카테리나 이바노브나는, 벌써 가려고 일어선 이반을 향해 재빠르고 기쁜 목소리로 말했다. \"잠깐만요!"], ["Stay another minute!", "잠깐만 더 계세요!"], ["I want to hear the opinion of this person here whom I trust absolutely.", "제가 절대적으로 믿는 이분의 의견을 듣고 싶어요."], ["Don’t go away,”", "가지 마세요.\""], ["she added, addressing Madame Hohlakov.", "그러고는 호흘라코바 부인을 향해 덧붙였다."], ["She made Alyosha sit down beside her, and Madame Hohlakov sat opposite, by Ivan.", "그녀는 알료샤를 자기 곁에 앉히고, 호흘라코바 부인은 맞은편 이반 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“You are all my friends here, all I have in the world, my dear friends,”", "\"여러분은 모두 저의 벗이에요, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 전부예요, 소중한 벗들이에요.\""], ["she began warmly, in a voice which quivered with genuine tears of suffering, and Alyosha’s heart warmed to her at once.", "그녀는 고통의 진솔한 눈물로 떨리는 목소리로 따스하게 입을 열었고, 알료샤의 마음은 단번에 그녀에게 누그러졌다."], ["“You, Alexey Fyodorovitch, were witness yesterday of that abominable scene, and saw what I did.", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 어제 그 추악한 장면을 목격하셨고, 제가 무슨 짓을 했는지 보셨어요."], ["You did not see it, Ivan Fyodorovitch, he did.", "이반 표도로비치, 당신은 못 보셨지만 이분은 보셨답니다."], ["What he thought of me yesterday I don’t know.", "어제 그가 저를 어떻게 생각했는지는 모르겠어요."], ["I only know one thing, that if it were repeated to-day, this minute, I should express the same feelings again as yesterday—the same feelings, the same words, the same actions.", "다만 한 가지는 알아요. 만약 그 일이 오늘, 바로 이 순간 되풀이된다 해도, 저는 어제와 똑같은 감정을 다시 드러낼 거예요—똑같은 감정, 똑같은 말, 똑같은 행동을요."], ["You remember my actions, Alexey Fyodorovitch; you checked me in one of them” ...", "제 행동을 기억하시죠, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신이 그중 하나를 막아 주셨잖아요…….\""], ["(as she said that, she flushed and her eyes shone). “I must tell you that I can’t get over it.", "(그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔고 두 눈이 반짝였다.) \"솔직히 말해 저는 그 일을 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요."], ["Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["I don’t even know whether I still love him.", "저는 제가 아직도 그를 사랑하는지조차 모르겠어요."], ["I feel pity for him, and that is a poor sign of love.", "저는 그가 가엾어요. 그런데 그건 사랑의 보잘것없는 증표지요."], ["If I loved him, if I still loved him, perhaps I shouldn’t be sorry for him now, but should hate him.”", "만약 제가 그를 사랑했다면, 아직도 사랑한다면, 어쩌면 지금 그를 가엾어하지 않고 미워했을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["Her voice quivered, and tears glittered on her eyelashes.", "그녀의 목소리가 떨렸고 눈물이 속눈썹 위에서 반짝였다."], ["Alyosha shuddered inwardly.", "알료샤는 속으로 몸서리쳤다."], ["“That girl is truthful and sincere,”", "\"저 아가씨는 진실하고 진솔하다.\""], ["he thought, “and she does not love Dmitri any more.”", "그는 생각했다. \"그리고 더는 드미트리를 사랑하지 않는다.\""]], [["“That’s true, that’s true,”", "\"맞아요, 맞아요.\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov.", "호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Wait, dear.", "\"잠깐만요, 부인."], ["I haven’t told you the chief, the final decision I came to during the night.", "아직 제가 가장 중요한 것, 간밤에 다다른 마지막 결심을 말씀드리지 않았어요."], ["I feel that perhaps my decision is a terrible one—for me, but I foresee that nothing will induce me to change it—nothing.", "어쩌면 제 결심이 끔찍한 것일지도 몰라요—저 자신에게는요. 하지만 저는 그 무엇으로도 제 마음을 돌릴 수 없으리라는 걸 예감하고 있어요—그 무엇으로도요."], ["It will be so all my life.", "평생 이대로일 거예요."], ["My dear, kind, ever-faithful and generous adviser, the one friend I have in the world, Ivan Fyodorovitch, with his deep insight into the heart, approves and commends my decision.", "제 소중하고 다정하며, 늘 충실하고 너그러운 조언자, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 단 하나의 벗, 마음속을 깊이 꿰뚫어 보시는 이반 표도로비치께서 제 결심을 인정하고 칭찬해 주셨어요."], ["He knows it.”", "이분은 알고 계세요.\""]], [["“Yes, I approve of it,”", "\"네, 저는 그것을 인정합니다.\""], ["Ivan assented, in a subdued but firm voice.", "이반이 낮으나 단호한 목소리로 동의했다."]], [["“But I should like Alyosha, too (Ah!", "\"하지만 저는 알료샤에게도(아!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, forgive my calling you simply Alyosha), I should like Alexey Fyodorovitch, too, to tell me before my two friends whether I am right.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 그냥 알료샤라고 부르는 걸 용서하세요), 알렉세이 표도로비치께도 제 두 벗 앞에서 제가 옳은지 말씀해 주십사 부탁드리고 싶어요. 저는 본능적으로 느껴요."], ["I feel instinctively that you, Alyosha, my dear brother (for you are a dear brother to me),”", "당신이, 알료샤, 제 소중한 동생이(당신은 제게 소중한 동생이니까요),\" 그녀는 다시 황홀하게 말하며 자기의 뜨거운 손으로 그의 차가운 손을 잡았다."], ["she said again ecstatically, taking his cold hand in her hot one, “I foresee that your decision, your approval, will bring me peace, in spite of all my sufferings, for, after your words, I shall be calm and submit—I feel that.”", "\"당신의 결정, 당신의 인정이 제 온갖 고통에도 불구하고 제게 평화를 가져다주리라는 걸 예감해요. 당신의 말씀을 듣고 나면 저는 차분해지고 순순히 따를 거예요—그렇게 느껴져요.\""]], [["“I don’t know what you are asking me,”", "\"저는 당신이 제게 무엇을 묻고 계신지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha, flushing.", "알료샤가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I only know that I love you and at this moment wish for your happiness more than my own!...", "\"다만 제가 당신을 사랑하며, 이 순간 제 행복보다 당신의 행복을 더 바라고 있다는 것만은 압니다!……"], ["But I know nothing about such affairs,”", "하지만 저는 그런 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다.\""], ["something impelled him to add hurriedly.", "무언가에 떠밀린 듯 그가 황급히 덧붙였다."]], [["“In such affairs, Alexey Fyodorovitch, in such affairs, the chief thing is honor and duty and something higher—I don’t know what—but higher perhaps even than duty.", "\"그런 일에서는, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그런 일에서 가장 중요한 것은 명예와 의무, 그리고 무언가 더 높은 것이에요—그게 무언지는 모르겠지만—어쩌면 의무보다도 더 높은 것이지요."], ["I am conscious of this irresistible feeling in my heart, and it compels me irresistibly.", "저는 제 마음속에서 이 거역할 수 없는 감정을 느끼고, 그것이 저를 거역할 수 없이 떠밀어요."], ["But it may all be put in two words.", "하지만 그건 다 한두 마디로 말할 수 있어요."], ["I’ve already decided, even if he marries that—creature,”", "저는 이미 결심했어요. 그가 그—여자와 결혼한다 해도,\""], ["she began solemnly, “whom I never, never can forgive, even then I will not abandon him.", "그녀는 엄숙하게 입을 열었다. \"제가 결코, 결코 용서할 수 없는 그 여자와 결혼한다 해도, 그래도 저는 그를 버리지 않겠어요."], ["Henceforward I will never, never abandon him!”", "이제부터 저는 결코, 결코 그를 버리지 않겠어요!\""], ["she cried, breaking into a sort of pale, hysterical ecstasy.", "그녀는 일종의 창백하고 발작적인 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["“Not that I would run after him continually, get in his way and worry him. Oh, no!", "\"그렇다고 제가 줄곧 그를 쫓아다니거나, 그의 앞길을 가로막거나 괴롭히겠다는 게 아니에요."], ["I will go away to another town—where you like—but I will watch over him all my life—I will watch over him all my life unceasingly.", "오, 아니에요! 저는 다른 도시로—당신이 원하시는 곳으로 떠나겠어요—하지만 평생토록 그를 지켜보겠어요—한평생 끊임없이 그를 지켜보겠어요."], ["When he becomes unhappy with that woman, and that is bound to happen quite soon, let him come to me and he will find a friend, a sister....", "그가 그 여자와 불행해지면, 그건 머지않아 반드시 일어날 일이지만, 그가 제게 오면 그는 한 사람의 벗을, 누이를 발견하게 될 거예요……"], ["Only a sister, of course, and so for ever; but he will learn at least that that sister is really his sister, who loves him and has sacrificed all her life to him.", "물론 그저 누이일 뿐이지요, 그리고 영원히 그럴 거예요. 하지만 적어도 그는 그 누이가 진정으로 자기의 누이이며, 그를 사랑하고 그에게 일평생을 바쳤다는 것을 알게 될 거예요."], ["I will gain my point.", "저는 제 뜻을 이루고야 말겠어요."], ["I will insist on his knowing me and confiding entirely in me, without reserve,”", "그가 저를 알아주고, 거리낌 없이 온전히 제게 모든 걸 털어놓도록 기어이 만들겠어요.\""], ["she cried, in a sort of frenzy.", "그녀는 일종의 광란에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["“I will be a god to whom he can pray—and that, at least, he owes me for his treachery and for what I suffered yesterday through him.", "\"저는 그가 기도드릴 수 있는 신이 되겠어요—그리고 적어도 그것은 그가 저를 배신한 대가로, 어제 그 때문에 제가 겪은 고통의 대가로 제게 갚아야 할 빚이에요. 그리고 그가 보게 하겠어요."], ["And let him see that all my life I will be true to him and the promise I gave him, in spite of his being untrue and betraying me.", "그가 거짓되고 저를 배신했음에도, 제가 평생토록 그에게, 그리고 그에게 한 약속에 충실하리라는 것을요. 저는—저는 그의 행복을 위한 한낱 수단이, 아니면—뭐라 해야 할까요?—그의 행복을 위한 도구, 기계가 되겠어요."], ["I will—I will become nothing but a means for his happiness, or—how shall I say?—an instrument, a machine for his happiness, and that for my whole life, my whole life, and that he may see that all his life!", "그것도 제 평생, 제 한평생을요. 그리고 그가 그것을 평생 보게 하겠어요!"], ["That’s my decision.", "그게 제 결심이에요."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch fully approves me.”", "이반 표도로비치께서도 저를 전적으로 인정해 주셨어요.\""]], [["She was breathless.", "그녀는 숨이 가빴다."], ["She had perhaps intended to express her idea with more dignity, art and naturalness, but her speech was too hurried and crude.", "어쩌면 그녀는 자기 생각을 좀 더 품위 있고 정교하고 자연스럽게 표현하려 했을지 모르나, 그 말은 너무 황급하고 거칠었다."], ["It was full of youthful impulsiveness, it betrayed that she was still smarting from yesterday’s insult, and that her pride craved satisfaction.", "거기에는 젊은이다운 충동이 가득 차 있었고, 그녀가 아직 어제의 모욕에 쓰라려하고 있으며 그녀의 자존심이 만족을 갈망하고 있음이 드러났다."], ["She felt this herself.", "그녀 자신도 그것을 느꼈다."], ["Her face suddenly darkened, an unpleasant look came into her eyes.", "그녀의 얼굴이 별안간 어두워졌고, 눈에 불쾌한 기색이 떠올랐다."], ["Alyosha at once saw it and felt a pang of sympathy.", "알료샤는 곧장 그것을 알아채고 연민의 통증을 느꼈다."], ["His brother Ivan made it worse by adding:", "그의 형 이반이 이렇게 덧붙여 사태를 더 악화시켰다."]], [["“I’ve only expressed my own view,”", "\"저는 다만 제 견해를 말했을 뿐입니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“From any one else, this would have been affected and overstrained, but from you—no.", "\"다른 누가 그랬다면 이건 꾸민 것이고 과장된 것이겠지요. 하지만 당신이 그러니—그렇지 않습니다."], ["Any other woman would have been wrong, but you are right.", "다른 어떤 여자라면 틀렸을 테지만, 당신은 옳습니다."], ["I don’t know how to explain it, but I see that you are absolutely genuine and, therefore, you are right.”", "어떻게 설명해야 할지 모르겠으나, 저는 당신이 더없이 진실하다는 것을, 그러므로 옳다는 것을 봅니다.\""]], [["“But that’s only for the moment.", "\"하지만 그건 그저 순간일 뿐이에요."], ["And what does this moment stand for?", "그리고 이 순간이라는 게 대체 무엇을 의미하나요?"], ["Nothing but yesterday’s insult.”", "어제의 모욕 말고는 아무것도 아니지요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov obviously had not intended to interfere, but she could not refrain from this very just comment.", "호흘라코바 부인은 분명 끼어들 생각이 없었으나, 이 더없이 옳은 한마디만은 참지 못했다."]], [["“Quite so, quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 바로 그래요.\""], ["cried Ivan, with peculiar eagerness, obviously annoyed at being interrupted, “in any one else this moment would be only due to yesterday’s impression and would be only a moment.", "이반이 유난한 열의로 외쳤는데, 분명 말을 가로막힌 데에 짜증이 난 듯했다. \"다른 누구였다면 이 순간은 어제의 인상에서 비롯된 것에 불과했을 테고, 그저 한순간으로 끝났을 겁니다."], ["But with Katerina Ivanovna’s character, that moment will last all her life.", "하지만 카테리나 이바노브나의 성격으로 보면 그 순간은 평생토록 지속될 겁니다."], ["What for any one else would be only a promise is for her an everlasting burdensome, grim perhaps, but unflagging duty.", "다른 누구에게라면 한낱 약속에 지나지 않을 것이, 그녀에게는 영원토록 무겁고, 어쩌면 음울하지만 결코 시들지 않는 의무가 됩니다."], ["And she will be sustained by the feeling of this duty being fulfilled.", "그리고 그녀는 그 의무를 다하고 있다는 느낌으로 지탱될 겁니다."], ["Your life, Katerina Ivanovna, will henceforth be spent in painful brooding over your own feelings, your own heroism, and your own suffering; but in the end that suffering will be softened and will pass into sweet contemplation of the fulfillment of a bold and proud design.", "카테리나 이바노브나, 당신의 삶은 이제부터 당신 자신의 감정, 당신 자신의 영웅적 행위, 당신 자신의 고통을 괴롭게 곱씹는 데 바쳐질 겁니다. 하지만 끝내 그 고통은 누그러져, 대담하고 자랑스러운 결심을 완수했다는 달콤한 관조 속으로 옮아갈 겁니다."], ["Yes, proud it certainly is, and desperate in any case, but a triumph for you.", "그래요, 그것은 분명 자랑스러운 것이고, 아무튼 절망적인 것이지만, 당신에게는 하나의 승리입니다."], ["And the consciousness of it will at last be a source of complete satisfaction and will make you resigned to everything else.”", "그리고 그것을 자각하는 것이 마침내 완전한 만족의 원천이 되어, 다른 모든 것을 체념하게 만들 겁니다.\""]], [["This was unmistakably said with some malice and obviously with intention; even perhaps with no desire to conceal that he spoke ironically and with intention.", "이것은 틀림없이 얼마간의 악의를 담아, 그리고 분명 의도적으로 한 말이었다. 어쩌면 자기가 비꼬듯이, 의도적으로 말하고 있음을 굳이 감추려 들지 않은 것이기도 했다."]], [["“Oh, dear, how mistaken it all is!”", "\"아아, 이게 다 얼마나 잘못된 건지!\""], ["Madame Hohlakov cried again.", "호흘라코바 부인이 다시 외쳤다."]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch, you speak.", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 말씀해 주세요."], ["I want dreadfully to know what you will say!”", "당신이 무슨 말씀을 하실지 너무나 알고 싶어요!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, and burst into tears.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외치더니 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Alyosha got up from the sofa.", "알료샤는 소파에서 일어났다."]], [["“It’s nothing, nothing!”", "\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도!\""], ["she went on through her tears.", "그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 말을 이었다."], ["“I’m upset, I didn’t sleep last night.", "\"제가 마음이 어지러워요, 간밤에 잠을 못 잤거든요."], ["But by the side of two such friends as you and your brother I still feel strong—for I know—you two will never desert me.”", "하지만 당신과 당신 형님 같은 두 벗이 곁에 있으니 저는 그래도 강해진 걸 느껴요—두 분이 결코 저를 버리지 않으리라는 걸 아니까요.\""]], [["“Unluckily I am obliged to return to Moscow—perhaps to-morrow—and to leave you for a long time—And, unluckily, it’s unavoidable,”", "\"공교롭게도 저는 모스크바로 돌아가야 합니다—어쩌면 내일이라도—그래서 오랫동안 당신 곁을 떠나야 합니다—그리고 공교롭게도, 그건 피할 수 없는 일입니다.\""], ["Ivan said suddenly.", "이반이 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“To-morrow—to Moscow!”", "\"내일—모스크바로요!\""], ["her face was suddenly contorted; “but—but, dear me, how fortunate!”", "그녀의 얼굴이 별안간 일그러졌다. \"하지만—하지만, 어머나, 정말 잘됐네요!\""], ["she cried in a voice suddenly changed.", "그녀는 별안간 달라진 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["In one instant there was no trace left of her tears.", "한순간에 그녀의 눈물은 흔적도 없이 사라졌다."], ["She underwent an instantaneous transformation, which amazed Alyosha.", "그녀는 순식간에 돌변했고, 그것이 알료샤를 놀라게 했다."], ["Instead of a poor, insulted girl, weeping in a sort of “laceration,”", "일종의 \"찢어 발기는 고통\""], ["he saw a woman completely self-possessed and even exceedingly pleased, as though something agreeable had just happened.", "속에서 우는, 가엾고 모욕당한 처녀 대신, 그는 무언가 흐뭇한 일이 막 벌어지기라도 한 듯 완전히 침착하고 심지어 더없이 기뻐하는 한 여자를 보았다."]], [["“Oh, not fortunate that I am losing you, of course not,”", "\"오, 물론 당신을 잃는 게 잘됐다는 건 아니에요.\""], ["she corrected herself suddenly, with a charming society smile.", "그녀는 매혹적인 사교계의 미소를 띠며 불쑥 말을 고쳤다."], ["“Such a friend as you are could not suppose that.", "\"당신 같은 벗이 그렇게 짐작하실 리 없지요."], ["I am only too unhappy at losing you.”", "저는 당신을 잃는 게 너무나 슬프답니다.\""], ["She rushed impulsively at Ivan, and seizing both his hands, pressed them warmly.", "그녀는 충동적으로 이반에게 달려들어 그의 두 손을 잡고 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐었다."], ["“But what is fortunate is that you will be able in Moscow to see auntie and Agafya and to tell them all the horror of my present position.", "\"하지만 잘된 건, 당신이 모스크바에서 아주머니와 아가피야를 뵙고 제 지금 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 다 말씀드릴 수 있으리라는 거예요."], ["You can speak with complete openness to Agafya, but spare dear auntie.", "아가피야에게는 아주 솔직하게 말씀하셔도 되지만, 소중한 아주머니께는 좀 봐주셔야 해요."], ["You will know how to do that.", "당신은 그렇게 하실 줄 아시죠."], ["You can’t think how wretched I was yesterday and this morning, wondering how I could write them that dreadful letter—for one can never tell such things in a letter....", "어제와 오늘 아침 제가 그 끔찍한 편지를 어떻게 써야 하나 고민하느라 얼마나 비참했는지 당신은 모르실 거예요—그런 일은 편지로는 도저히 전할 수가 없으니까요……"], ["Now it will be easy for me to write, for you will see them and explain everything.", "이제 저는 편지 쓰기가 쉬워졌어요. 당신이 그분들을 만나 다 설명해 주실 테니까요."], ["Oh, how glad I am!", "오, 정말 기뻐요!"], ["But I am only glad of that, believe me.", "하지만 저는 그것만으로 기쁜 거예요, 믿어 주세요."], ["Of course, no one can take your place....", "물론 그 누구도 당신을 대신할 수는 없어요……"], ["I will run at once to write the letter,”", "곧장 가서 편지를 쓰겠어요.\""], ["she finished suddenly, and took a step as though to go out of the room.", "그녀는 불쑥 말을 맺고는 방을 나가려는 듯 한 걸음을 내디뎠다."]], [["“And what about Alyosha and his opinion, which you were so desperately anxious to hear?”", "\"그런데 그토록 애타게 듣고 싶어 하시던 알료샤의 의견은 어떻게 되나요?\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov.", "호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다."], ["There was a sarcastic, angry note in her voice.", "그녀의 목소리에는 비꼬는, 성난 가락이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I had not forgotten that,”", "\"그걸 잊은 게 아니에요.\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, coming to a sudden standstill, “and why are you so antagonistic at such a moment?”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 별안간 우뚝 멈추어 서며 외쳤다. \"그런데 부인은 어째서 이런 순간에 그토록 적대적이세요?\""], ["she added, with warm and bitter reproachfulness.", "그녀는 뜨겁고도 쓰라린 책망을 담아 덧붙였다."], ["“What I said, I repeat.", "\"제가 한 말은 다시 되풀이하겠어요."], ["I must have his opinion.", "저는 그분의 의견을 들어야 해요."], ["More than that, I must have his decision!", "그뿐 아니라 그분의 결정이 필요해요!"], ["As he says, so it shall be.", "그가 말하는 대로 될 거예요."], ["You see how anxious I am for your words, Alexey Fyodorovitch....", "보세요, 제가 당신 말씀을 얼마나 애타게 기다리는지, 알렉세이 표도로비치……"], ["But what’s the matter?”", "그런데 무슨 일이세요?\""]], [["“I couldn’t have believed it.", "\"저는 도무지 믿을 수가 없습니다."], ["I can’t understand it!”", "이해할 수가 없어요!\""], ["Alyosha cried suddenly in distress.", "알료샤가 별안간 괴로운 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["What?”", "뭐가요?\""]], [["“He is going to Moscow, and you cry out that you are glad.", "\"그가 모스크바로 간다는데, 당신은 잘됐다고 외치셨어요."], ["You said that on purpose!", "일부러 그렇게 말씀하셨어요!"], ["And you begin explaining that you are not glad of that but sorry to be—losing a friend.", "그러고는 그게 잘된 게 아니라 벗을—벗을 잃게 되어 슬프다고 설명하기 시작하셨지요."], ["But that was acting, too—you were playing a part—as in a theater!”", "하지만 그것 역시 연기였어요—당신은 한 배역을 연기하고 계셨어요—마치 극장에서처럼요!\""]], [["“In a theater?", "\"극장에서요?"], ["What?", "뭐라고요?"], ["What do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["exclaimed Katerina Ivanovna, profoundly astonished, flushing crimson, and frowning.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤는데, 깊이 경악하여 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오르며 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Though you assure him you are sorry to lose a friend in him, you persist in telling him to his face that it’s fortunate he is going,”", "\"당신은 그에게서 벗을 잃게 되어 슬프다고 다짐하면서도, 그가 떠나서 잘됐다고 그의 면전에 대고 끈질기게 말씀하시잖아요.\""], ["said Alyosha breathlessly.", "알료샤가 숨 가쁘게 말했다."], ["He was standing at the table and did not sit down.", "그는 탁자 곁에 선 채 앉지 않았다."]], [["“What are you talking about?", "\"무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?"], ["I don’t understand.”", "저는 이해할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“I don’t understand myself....", "\"저 자신도 이해가 안 됩니다……"], ["I seemed to see in a flash ...", "어쩐지 섬광처럼 본 것 같아요……"], ["I know I am not saying it properly, but I’ll say it all the same,”", "제대로 말하지 못한다는 걸 알지만, 그래도 다 말하겠습니다.\""], ["Alyosha went on in the same shaking and broken voice.", "알료샤는 여전히 떨리고 끊기는 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["“What I see is that perhaps you don’t love Dmitri at all ...", "\"제가 보기에는, 어쩌면 당신은 드미트리를 전혀 사랑하지 않으시고……"], ["and never have, from the beginning....", "처음부터, 애초부터 사랑하신 적이 없는 것 같아요……"], ["And Dmitri, too, has never loved you ...", "그리고 드미트리도 당신을 결코 사랑한 적이 없고……"], ["and only esteems you....", "다만 당신을 존경할 뿐이에요……"], ["I really don’t know how I dare to say all this, but somebody must tell the truth ...", "제가 어떻게 감히 이 모든 걸 말하는지 정말 모르겠지만, 누군가는 진실을 말해야 하니까요……"], ["for nobody here will tell the truth.”", "여기서는 아무도 진실을 말하지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“What truth?”", "\"무슨 진실 말이에요?\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, and there was an hysterical ring in her voice.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤는데, 그 목소리에는 발작적인 울림이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I’ll tell you,”", "\"말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["Alyosha went on with desperate haste, as though he were jumping from the top of a house.", "알료샤는 마치 집 꼭대기에서 뛰어내리기라도 하듯 필사적으로 황급히 말을 이었다. \"드미트리를 부르세요."], ["“Call Dmitri; I will fetch him—and let him come here and take your hand and take Ivan’s and join your hands.", "제가 데려오겠습니다—그를 이리 오게 해서 당신의 손을 잡고 이반의 손도 잡아 두 분의 손을 맞잡게 하세요."], ["For you’re torturing Ivan, simply because you love him—and torturing him, because you love Dmitri through ‘self-laceration’—with an unreal love—because you’ve persuaded yourself.”", "당신은 이반을 괴롭히고 계세요, 다름 아니라 당신이 그를 사랑하기 때문이에요—그리고 그를 괴롭히는 건, 당신이 '자기 학대'로 드미트리를 사랑하기 때문이고요—거짓된 사랑으로요—당신이 스스로를 그렇게 믿게 만들었으니까요.\""]], [["Alyosha broke off and was silent.", "알료샤는 말을 끊고 입을 다물었다."]], [["“You ...", "\"당신……"], ["you ...", "당신……"], ["you are a little religious idiot—that’s what you are!”", "당신은 신앙에 미친 조그만 바보로군요—그게 바로 당신이에요!\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna snapped.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 쏘아붙였다."], ["Her face was white and her lips were moving with anger.", "그녀의 얼굴은 새하얗고 입술은 분노로 떨리고 있었다."]], [["Ivan suddenly laughed and got up.", "이반이 별안간 웃음을 터뜨리며 일어섰다."], ["His hat was in his hand.", "손에는 모자가 들려 있었다."]], [["“You are mistaken, my good Alyosha,”", "\"네 생각이 틀렸다, 나의 착한 알료샤.\""], ["he said, with an expression Alyosha had never seen in his face before—an expression of youthful sincerity and strong, irresistibly frank feeling.", "그는 알료샤가 그의 얼굴에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정을 지으며 말했다—젊은이다운 진솔함과, 강렬하고 거역할 수 없이 솔직한 감정의 표정이었다."], ["“Katerina Ivanovna has never cared for me!", "\"카테리나 이바노브나는 한 번도 나를 마음에 둔 적이 없어!"], ["She has known all the time that I cared for her—though I never said a word of my love to her—she knew, but she didn’t care for me.", "그녀는 줄곧 내가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 걸 알고 있었지—나는 내 사랑에 대해 그녀에게 한마디도 한 적이 없지만—그녀는 알면서도 나를 마음에 두지 않았어."], ["I have never been her friend either, not for one moment; she is too proud to need my friendship.", "나는 그녀의 벗인 적도 없었어, 단 한순간도. 그녀는 너무 자존심이 강해서 내 우정 따위는 필요 없으니까."], ["She kept me at her side as a means of revenge.", "그녀는 복수의 수단으로 나를 곁에 두었던 거야."], ["She revenged with me and on me all the insults which she has been continually receiving from Dmitri ever since their first meeting.", "그녀는 나를 통해, 그리고 나에게, 첫 만남 이래로 드미트리에게서 줄곧 받아 온 온갖 모욕을 되갚은 거지. 그 첫 만남조차 그녀의 마음속에 모욕으로 사무쳐 있으니까—그녀의 마음이란 게 바로 그래!"], ["For even that first meeting has rankled in her heart as an insult—that’s what her heart is like!", "그녀는 내게 그를 향한 자기 사랑 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않았어. 나는 이제 가겠소."], ["She has talked to me of nothing but her love for him.", "하지만 믿어요, 카테리나 이바노브나, 당신은 정말로 그를 사랑하고 있소."], ["I am going now; but, believe me, Katerina Ivanovna, you really love him.", "그가 당신을 모욕하면 할수록 당신은 더욱 그를 사랑하지—그게 당신의 '찢어 발기는 고통'이오."], ["And the more he insults you, the more you love him—that’s your ‘laceration.’ You love him just as he is; you love him for insulting you.", "당신은 그를 있는 그대로 사랑하고 있소. 그가 당신을 모욕하기에 그를 사랑하는 거요."], ["If he reformed, you’d give him up at once and cease to love him.", "만약 그가 새사람이 된다면 당신은 곧장 그를 버리고 사랑하기를 그칠 거요."], ["But you need him so as to contemplate continually your heroic fidelity and to reproach him for infidelity.", "하지만 당신은 자신의 영웅적 정절을 끊임없이 관조하고, 그의 부정을 책망하기 위해 그가 필요한 거요."], ["And it all comes from your pride.", "그리고 그건 다 당신의 자존심에서 비롯된 거요."], ["Oh, there’s a great deal of humiliation and self-abasement about it, but it all comes from pride....", "오, 거기에는 굴욕과 자기 비하가 잔뜩 들어 있지만, 그것도 다 자존심에서 나온 거요……"], ["I am too young and I’ve loved you too much.", "나는 너무 젊고, 당신을 너무 많이 사랑했소."], ["I know that I ought not to say this, that it would be more dignified on my part simply to leave you, and it would be less offensive for you.", "이런 말을 해서는 안 된다는 걸, 그냥 당신을 떠나는 편이 내게 더 품위 있는 일이고 당신에게도 덜 모욕적이리라는 걸 나도 알고 있소."], ["But I am going far away, and shall never come back....", "하지만 나는 멀리 떠나서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 거요……"], ["It is for ever.", "영원히 말이오."], ["I don’t want to sit beside a ‘laceration.’...", "나는 '찢어 발기는 고통' 곁에 앉아 있고 싶지 않소……"], ["But I don’t know how to speak now.", "그런데 이젠 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠군. 할 말은 다 했소……"], ["I’ve said everything....", "잘 있어요, 카테리나 이바노브나."], ["Good-by, Katerina Ivanovna; you can’t be angry with me, for I am a hundred times more severely punished than you, if only by the fact that I shall never see you again.", "당신은 나에게 화낼 수 없을 거요. 나는 당신보다 백 배는 더 혹독하게 벌을 받고 있으니까, 다시는 당신을 보지 못하리라는 사실 하나만으로도."], ["Good-by!", "잘 있어요!"], ["I don’t want your hand.", "당신 손은 필요 없소."], ["You have tortured me too deliberately for me to be able to forgive you at this moment.", "당신은 너무도 작정하고 나를 괴롭혔기에, 나는 이 순간 당신을 용서할 수가 없소."], ["I shall forgive you later, but now I don’t want your hand.", "나중에는 용서하겠지만, 지금은 당신 손이 필요 없소. '덴 단크, 다메, 베게어 이히 니히트.'\""], ["‘Den Dank, Dame, begehr ich nicht,’”", "그는 억지웃음을 지으며 덧붙였는데, 그러나 그것은 그가 실러를 읽었으며, 그것도 외울 만큼 읽었음을 드러내는 것이었으니—알료샤로서는 도저히 믿지 못했을 일이었다."], ["he added, with a forced smile, showing, however, that he could read Schiller, and read him till he knew him by heart—which Alyosha would never have believed.", "그는 안주인인 호흘라코바 부인에게조차 작별 인사를 하지 않고 방을 나갔다. 알료샤는 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["He went out of the room without saying good-by even to his hostess, Madame Hohlakov. Alyosha clasped his hands.", "* 덴 단크, 다메, 베게어 이히 니히트(Den Dank, Dame, begehr ich nicht): \"감사라면, 부인, 나는 바라지 않소\"라는 뜻의 독일어로, 실러의 발라드 「장갑」의 한 구절이다."]], [["“Ivan!”", "\"이반 형!\""], ["he cried desperately after him.", "그는 절망적으로 형의 뒤를 향해 외쳤다."], ["“Come back, Ivan!", "\"돌아와요, 이반 형!"], ["No, nothing will induce him to come back now!”", "아니, 이젠 무슨 일이 있어도 형이 돌아오지 않을 거야!\""], ["he cried again, regretfully realizing it; “but it’s my fault, my fault.", "그는 후회 어린 심정으로 그것을 깨달으며 다시 외쳤다. \"하지만 내 잘못이야, 내 잘못이야."], ["I began it!", "내가 시작한 거야!"], ["Ivan spoke angrily, wrongly.", "이반 형은 화가 나서, 잘못 말한 거야."], ["Unjustly and angrily.", "부당하게, 화가 나서."], ["He must come back here, come back,”", "형은 이리로 돌아와야 해, 돌아와야 해.\""], ["Alyosha kept exclaiming frantically.", "알료샤는 미친 듯이 거듭 외쳐 댔다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna went suddenly into the next room.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 별안간 옆방으로 들어가 버렸다."]], [["“You have done no harm.", "\"당신은 아무런 해도 끼치지 않았어요."], ["You behaved beautifully, like an angel,”", "천사처럼 훌륭하게 처신하셨어요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov whispered rapidly and ecstatically to Alyosha.", "호흘라코바 부인이 알료샤에게 재빠르고 황홀하게 속삭였다."], ["“I will do my utmost to prevent Ivan Fyodorovitch from going.”", "\"제가 이반 표도로비치가 떠나지 못하게 온 힘을 다하겠어요.\""]], [["Her face beamed with delight, to the great distress of Alyosha, but Katerina Ivanovna suddenly returned.", "그녀의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 환히 빛나 알료샤를 몹시 괴롭게 했는데, 바로 그때 카테리나 이바노브나가 불쑥 되돌아왔다."], ["She had two hundred-rouble notes in her hand.", "그녀의 손에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 두 장이 들려 있었다."]], [["“I have a great favor to ask of you, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"당신께 크게 부탁드릴 일이 있어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["she began, addressing Alyosha with an apparently calm and even voice, as though nothing had happened.", "그녀는 마치 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 겉으로는 차분하고 고른 목소리로 알료샤에게 말을 건넸다."], ["“A week—yes, I think it was a week ago—Dmitri Fyodorovitch was guilty of a hasty and unjust action—a very ugly action.", "\"일주일—그래요, 일주일쯤 전인 것 같아요—드미트리 표도로비치가 성급하고 부당한 짓을—아주 추악한 짓을 저질렀어요."], ["There is a low tavern here, and in it he met that discharged officer, that captain, whom your father used to employ in some business.", "여기 천한 선술집이 하나 있는데, 거기서 그가 퇴역 장교 하나, 당신 아버님이 무슨 일에 부리던 그 대위를 만났어요."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch somehow lost his temper with this captain, seized him by the beard and dragged him out into the street and for some distance along it, in that insulting fashion.", "드미트리 표도로비치가 어쩌다 그 대위에게 화가 나서, 그의 수염을 움켜쥐고 거리로 끌어내어 그 모욕적인 꼴로 길을 따라 한참이나 끌고 다녔다는 거예요."], ["And I am told that his son, a boy, quite a child, who is at the school here, saw it and ran beside them crying and begging for his father, appealing to every one to defend him, while every one laughed.", "그런데 들으니, 여기 학교에 다니는 그의 아들, 아직 어린아이인 사내애가 그걸 보고 울면서 옆에 따라 뛰며 아버지를 살려 달라고, 모든 사람에게 아버지를 지켜 달라고 애원했는데, 다들 웃기만 했다지 뭐예요."], ["You must forgive me, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I cannot think without indignation of that disgraceful action of his ...", "용서하세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 그가 저지른 그 수치스러운 짓을 떠올릴 때마다 치미는 분노를 억누를 수가 없어요……"], ["one of those actions of which only Dmitri Fyodorovitch would be capable in his anger ...", "그건 드미트리 표도로비치가 화가 났을 때만…… 격정에 휩싸였을 때만 저지를 수 있는 그런 짓들 가운데 하나예요!"], ["and in his passions! I can’t describe it even....", "차마 묘사조차 할 수 없어요……"], ["I can’t find my words.", "무슨 말로 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I’ve made inquiries about his victim, and find he is quite a poor man.", "제가 그 피해자에 대해 알아보았더니, 그는 아주 가난한 사람이더군요."], ["His name is Snegiryov.", "이름은 스네기료프예요."], ["He did something wrong in the army and was discharged. I can’t tell you what.", "군에서 무슨 잘못을 저질러 퇴역당했다는데, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어요."], ["And now he has sunk into terrible destitution, with his family—an unhappy family of sick children, and, I believe, an insane wife.", "그러고는 지금은 가족과 함께 끔찍한 궁핍 속에 빠져 있어요—병든 아이들에, 제가 알기로는 미친 아내까지 딸린 불행한 가족이지요."], ["He has been living here a long time; he used to work as a copying clerk, but now he is getting nothing.", "그는 여기 오래 살았고, 한때는 필경사로 일했지만 지금은 한 푼도 못 벌고 있어요."], ["I thought if you ...", "저는 만약 당신이……"], ["that is I thought ...", "그러니까 제 생각엔……"], ["I don’t know.", "모르겠어요."], ["I am so confused.", "제가 너무 어지러워서요."], ["You see, I wanted to ask you, my dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, to go to him, to find some excuse to go to them—I mean to that captain—oh, goodness, how badly I explain it!—and delicately, carefully, as only you know how to”", "보세요, 제가 당신께 부탁드리고 싶은 건, 소중한 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그에게 가셔서, 그들을 찾아갈 무슨 구실을 만드셔서—그러니까 그 대위 말이에요—오, 세상에, 제가 설명을 너무 못하네요!—그리고 섬세하게, 조심스럽게, 오직 당신만이 할 수 있는 그런 방식으로\""], ["(Alyosha blushed), “manage to give him this assistance, these two hundred roubles.", "(알료샤는 얼굴을 붉혔다) \"그에게 이 도움을, 이 이백 루블을 어떻게든 건네주시는 거예요."], ["He will be sure to take it....", "그는 틀림없이 받을 거예요……"], ["I mean, persuade him to take it....", "그러니까, 받도록 설득해 주세요……"], ["Or, rather, what do I mean?", "아니, 제가 무슨 말을 하는 거죠?"], ["You see it’s not by way of compensation to prevent him from taking proceedings (for I believe he meant to), but simply a token of sympathy, of a desire to assist him from me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s betrothed, not from himself....", "보세요, 이건 그가 소송을 걸지 못하게 하려는 보상금 같은 게 아니에요(그가 그럴 작정이었던 것 같으니까요). 그저 동정의 표시, 그를 돕고 싶다는 표시예요."], ["But you know.... I would go myself, but you’ll know how to do it ever so much better.", "저, 드미트리 표도로비치의 약혼녀로서, 그 사람 본인이 아니라요……"], ["He lives in Lake Street, in the house of a woman called Kalmikov....", "하지만 당신은 아실 거예요…… 제가 직접 갈 수도 있지만, 당신이 훨씬 더 잘하실 테니까요."], ["For God’s sake, Alexey Fyodorovitch, do it for me, and now ...", "그는 호수 거리, 칼미코바라는 여자의 집에 살아요…… 부디, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저를 위해 그렇게 해 주세요, 그리고 이제……"], ["now I am rather ...", "이제 저는 좀…… 지쳤어요."], ["tired. Good-by!”", "안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["She turned and disappeared behind the portière so quickly that Alyosha had not time to utter a word, though he wanted to speak.", "그녀는 돌아서서 어찌나 재빨리 휘장 뒤로 사라졌던지, 알료샤는 말을 하고 싶었으나 한마디도 꺼낼 틈이 없었다."], ["He longed to beg her pardon, to blame himself, to say something, for his heart was full and he could not bear to go out of the room without it.", "그는 그녀에게 용서를 빌고, 자신을 책망하고, 무슨 말이든 하고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었으니, 그의 마음이 가득 차 있어 그러지 않고는 방을 나설 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["But Madame Hohlakov took him by the hand and drew him along with her.", "그러나 호흘라코바 부인이 그의 손을 잡고 자기와 함께 그를 끌어냈다."], ["In the hall she stopped him again as before.", "현관에서 그녀는 아까처럼 다시 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“She is proud, she is struggling with herself; but kind, charming, generous,”", "\"저 애는 자존심이 강해서 자기 자신과 싸우고 있어요. 하지만 착하고, 매력적이고, 너그럽지요.\""], ["she exclaimed, in a half-whisper.", "그녀가 반쯤 속삭이며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, how I love her, especially sometimes, and how glad I am again of everything!", "\"오, 제가 저 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 특히 가끔씩은요, 그리고 모든 게 다시 얼마나 기쁜지요! 소중한 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 모르셨겠지만 제가 꼭 말씀드려야겠어요."], ["Dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, you didn’t know, but I must tell you, that we all, all—both her aunts, I and all of us, Lise, even—have been hoping and praying for nothing for the last month but that she may give up your favorite Dmitri, who takes no notice of her and does not care for her, and may marry Ivan Fyodorovitch—such an excellent and cultivated young man, who loves her more than anything in the world.", "우리 모두가, 모두가—저 애의 두 이모와 저, 그리고 우리 모두가, 심지어 리즈까지도—지난 한 달 동안 오직 한 가지만을 바라고 기도해 왔어요. 바로 저 애가, 자기를 거들떠보지도 않고 마음에 두지도 않는 당신의 그 사랑하는 드미트리를 포기하고, 이반 표도로비치와 결혼하는 거예요—그분은 그토록 훌륭하고 교양 있는 청년이고, 이 세상 그 무엇보다도 저 애를 사랑하시니까요."], ["We are in a regular plot to bring it about, and I am even staying on here perhaps on that account.”", "우리는 그 일을 성사시키려고 아주 작당을 하고 있고, 어쩌면 저도 그 때문에 여기 머물고 있는 거랍니다.\""]], [["“But she has been crying—she has been wounded again,”", "\"하지만 저분은 울었어요—다시 상처를 받았어요.\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“Never trust a woman’s tears, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "\"여자의 눈물은 절대 믿지 마세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["I am never for the women in such cases.", "저는 이런 경우에 결코 여자 편을 들지 않아요."], ["I am always on the side of the men.”", "저는 언제나 남자 편이랍니다.\""]], [["“Mamma, you are spoiling him,”", "\"엄마, 그분을 버릇없게 만들고 계세요.\""], ["Lise’s little voice cried from behind the door.", "문 뒤에서 리즈의 가느다란 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“No, it was all my fault.", "\"아니에요, 다 제 잘못이었어요."], ["I am horribly to blame,”", "제가 무섭도록 잘못했어요.\""], ["Alyosha repeated unconsoled, hiding his face in his hands in an agony of remorse for his indiscretion.", "알료샤는 위로받지 못한 채, 자신의 경솔함을 뉘우치는 괴로움에 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Quite the contrary; you behaved like an angel, like an angel.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요. 당신은 천사처럼, 천사처럼 처신하셨어요."], ["I am ready to say so a thousand times over.”", "저는 천 번이라도 그렇게 말할 준비가 되어 있어요.\""]], [["“Mamma, how has he behaved like an angel?”", "\"엄마, 그분이 어떻게 천사처럼 처신했다는 거예요?\""], ["Lise’s voice was heard again.", "다시 리즈의 목소리가 들렸다."]], [["“I somehow fancied all at once,”", "\"저는 어쩐지 별안간 이런 생각이 들었어요.\""], ["Alyosha went on as though he had not heard Lise, “that she loved Ivan, and so I said that stupid thing....", "알료샤는 리즈의 말을 듣지 못한 듯 말을 이었다. \"저분이 이반 형을 사랑한다고요, 그래서 그런 어리석은 말을 한 거예요……"], ["What will happen now?”", "이제 어떻게 될까요?\""]], [["“To whom, to whom?”", "\"누가요, 누가요?\""], ["cried Lise.", "리즈가 외쳤다."], ["“Mamma, you really want to be the death of me.", "\"엄마, 정말 저를 죽이실 작정이세요."], ["I ask you and you don’t answer.”", "제가 묻는데 대답을 안 하시잖아요.\""]], [["At the moment the maid ran in.", "바로 그때 하녀가 뛰어 들어왔다."]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna is ill....", "\"카테리나 이바노브나 아씨께서 편찮으세요……"], ["She is crying, struggling ...", "울면서, 몸부림치며……"], ["hysterics.”", "발작을 일으키셨어요.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""], ["cried Lise, in a tone of real anxiety.", "리즈가 진정으로 걱정스러운 어조로 외쳤다."], ["“Mamma, I shall be having hysterics, and not she!”", "\"엄마, 발작은 그 사람이 아니라 제가 일으키겠어요!\""]], [["“Lise, for mercy’s sake, don’t scream, don’t persecute me.", "\"리즈, 제발 소리치지 마라, 나를 들볶지 말고."], ["At your age one can’t know everything that grown-up people know.", "네 나이에는 어른들이 아는 걸 다 알 수가 없는 거야."], ["I’ll come and tell you everything you ought to know.", "가서 네가 알아야 할 건 다 말해 줄 테니까."], ["Oh, mercy on us!", "오, 세상에!"], ["I am coming, I am coming....", "가요, 가요……"], ["Hysterics is a good sign, Alexey Fyodorovitch; it’s an excellent thing that she is hysterical.", "발작은 좋은 징조예요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 저 애가 발작을 일으키는 건 아주 잘된 일이에요."], ["That’s just as it ought to be.", "바로 그래야 마땅한 거예요."], ["In such cases I am always against the woman, against all these feminine tears and hysterics.", "이런 경우에 저는 늘 여자 편이 아니에요, 이런 온갖 여성스러운 눈물이며 발작에 반대랍니다."], ["Run and say, Yulia, that I’ll fly to her.", "율리야, 어서 가서 내가 곧 날아가겠다고 전해라."], ["As for Ivan Fyodorovitch’s going away like that, it’s her own fault.", "이반 표도로비치가 그런 식으로 떠나는 건 다 저 애 잘못이에요. 하지만 그분은 떠나지 않을 거예요."], ["But he won’t go away.", "리즈, 제발 소리치지 마!"], ["Lise, for mercy’s sake, don’t scream!", "아, 그래, 너는 소리치고 있지 않구나."], ["Oh, yes; you are not screaming.", "소리치는 건 나로구나."], ["It’s I am screaming.", "엄마를 용서해 다오."], ["Forgive your mamma; but I am delighted, delighted, delighted!", "하지만 나는 기뻐, 기뻐, 기뻐 죽겠구나!"], ["Did you notice, Alexey Fyodorovitch, how young, how young Ivan Fyodorovitch was just now when he went out, when he said all that and went out?", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 보셨어요, 아까 이반 표도로비치가 나가실 때, 그 모든 말을 하고 나가실 때 얼마나 젊었는지, 얼마나 젊었는지를요?"], ["I thought he was so learned, such a savant, and all of a sudden he behaved so warmly, openly, and youthfully, with such youthful inexperience, and it was all so fine, like you....", "저는 그분이 그토록 학식 높고 그토록 대단한 학자인 줄 알았는데, 별안간 그렇게 따뜻하고, 솔직하고, 젊은이답게, 그토록 젊은이다운 미숙함으로 처신하시니, 그 모든 게 어찌나 멋지던지요, 꼭 당신처럼요……"], ["And the way he repeated that German verse, it was just like you!", "그리고 그 독일어 시구를 읊으시던 모습이라니, 꼭 당신 같았어요!"], ["But I must fly, I must fly!", "하지만 저는 가야 해요, 가야 해요!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, make haste to carry out her commission, and then make haste back.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 어서 저 애의 부탁을 처리하시고, 그런 다음 어서 돌아오세요."], ["Lise, do you want anything now?", "리즈, 지금 뭐 필요한 거 있니?"], ["For mercy’s sake, don’t keep Alexey Fyodorovitch a minute.", "제발 알렉세이 표도로비치를 잠시도 붙들지 마라."], ["He will come back to you at once.”", "곧 네게 돌아올 테니까.\""]], [["Madame Hohlakov at last ran off.", "호흘라코바 부인은 마침내 뛰어나갔다."], ["Before leaving, Alyosha would have opened the door to see Lise.", "떠나기 전에 알료샤는 리즈를 보려고 문을 열려 했다."]], [["“On no account,”", "\"절대로 안 돼요.\""], ["cried Lise.", "리즈가 외쳤다."], ["“On no account now.", "\"지금은 절대로 안 돼요."], ["Speak through the door.", "문 너머로 말씀하세요."], ["How have you come to be an angel?", "어쩌다 천사가 되신 거예요?"], ["That’s the only thing I want to know.”", "제가 알고 싶은 건 오직 그것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“For an awful piece of stupidity, Lise!", "\"끔찍한 어리석음을 저질러서요, 리즈!"], ["Good-by!”", "안녕히!\""]], [["“Don’t dare to go away like that!”", "\"감히 그런 식으로 가시면 안 돼요!\""], ["Lise was beginning.", "리즈가 입을 열기 시작했다."]], [["“Lise, I have a real sorrow!", "\"리즈, 저는 정말 슬픈 일이 있어요!"], ["I’ll be back directly, but I have a great, great sorrow!”", "곧 돌아올게요. 하지만 저는 크나큰, 크나큰 슬픔이 있어요!\""]], [["And he ran out of the room.", "그러고는 그는 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["# Chapter VI. A Laceration In The Cottage", "# Chapter VI. A Laceration In The Cottage"]], [["He certainly was really grieved in a way he had seldom been before.", "그는 분명 이전에는 좀처럼 겪어 본 적 없는 깊은 비탄에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He had rushed in like a fool, and meddled in what?", "바보처럼 뛰어들어, 대체 무슨 일에 끼어든 것인가? 연애 사건에."], ["In a love-affair. “But what do I know about it?", "\"하지만 내가 그런 일에 대해 무엇을 안단 말인가?"], ["What can I tell about such things?”", "이런 일을 두고 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["he repeated to himself for the hundredth time, flushing crimson.", "그는 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 백 번째로 같은 말을 되뇌었다."], ["“Oh, being ashamed would be nothing; shame is only the punishment I deserve.", "\"오, 부끄러운 것쯤은 아무것도 아니다. 수치란 내가 마땅히 받아야 할 벌일 뿐이니."], ["The trouble is I shall certainly have caused more unhappiness....", "문제는 내가 틀림없이 더 큰 불행을 불러일으키고 말았다는 것이다……"], ["And Father Zossima sent me to reconcile and bring them together.", "게다가 조시마 장로께서는 나를 보내 두 사람을 화해시키고 한데 맺어 주라 하셨는데."], ["Is this the way to bring them together?”", "이것이 두 사람을 맺어 주는 방식이란 말인가?\""], ["Then he suddenly remembered how he had tried to join their hands, and he felt fearfully ashamed again.", "그러다 문득 그는 자신이 두 사람의 손을 맞잡아 주려 했던 일을 떠올리고는, 또다시 끔찍한 부끄러움에 휩싸였다."], ["“Though I acted quite sincerely, I must be more sensible in the future,”", "\"내 행동이 진심에서 우러난 것이었다 해도, 앞으로는 좀 더 분별 있게 처신해야겠다.\""], ["he concluded suddenly, and did not even smile at his conclusion.", "그는 불쑥 이렇게 결론을 내렸으나, 그 결론에 미소조차 짓지 못했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna’s commission took him to Lake Street, and his brother Dmitri lived close by, in a turning out of Lake Street.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 맡긴 심부름 때문에 그는 호수 거리로 가야 했는데, 형 드미트리는 그 부근, 호수 거리에서 갈라진 골목 안에 살고 있었다."], ["Alyosha decided to go to him in any case before going to the captain, though he had a presentiment that he would not find his brother.", "알료샤는 그 대위를 찾아가기 전에 어떻게든 먼저 형에게 들러야겠다고 마음먹었으나, 형을 만나지 못하리라는 예감이 들었다."], ["He suspected that he would intentionally keep out of his way now, but he must find him anyhow.", "형이 지금 일부러 자기를 피하려 들 것이라고 짐작했지만, 어쨌든 반드시 형을 찾아내야 했다."], ["Time was passing: the thought of his dying elder had not left Alyosha for one minute from the time he set off from the monastery.", "시간이 흐르고 있었다. 죽어 가는 장로에 대한 생각은 수도원을 나선 순간부터 단 한 순간도 알료샤의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다."]], [["There was one point which interested him particularly about Katerina Ivanovna’s commission; when she had mentioned the captain’s son, the little schoolboy who had run beside his father crying, the idea had at once struck Alyosha that this must be the schoolboy who had bitten his finger when he, Alyosha, asked him what he had done to hurt him.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 맡긴 심부름 중에서 그가 특히 마음에 걸리는 한 가지가 있었다. 그녀가 그 대위의 아들, 곧 아버지 곁에서 울며 뛰어가던 어린 학생 이야기를 꺼냈을 때, 알료샤는 곧바로 그 아이가 바로 자기 손가락을 깨문 그 학생이 틀림없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["Now Alyosha felt practically certain of this, though he could not have said why.", "자기가, 곧 알료샤가 그에게 무슨 잘못을 했느냐고 물었을 때 손가락을 깨물었던 바로 그 아이 말이다."], ["Thinking of another subject was a relief, and he resolved to think no more about the “mischief”", "이제 알료샤는 왜 그런지 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없었으나 거의 확신하고 있었다."], ["he had done, and not to torture himself with remorse, but to do what he had to do, let come what would. At that thought he was completely comforted.", "다른 일을 떠올리는 것은 마음의 위안이 되었고, 그리하여 그는 자기가 저질러 놓은 \"실수\"에 대해서는 더 이상 생각하지 말고, 후회로 스스로를 괴롭히지도 말며, 무슨 일이 벌어지든 해야 할 일을 하기로 결심했다."], ["Turning to the street where Dmitri lodged, he felt hungry, and taking out of his pocket the roll he had brought from his father’s, he ate it.", "그렇게 생각하니 마음이 완전히 가벼워졌다. 드미트리가 묵고 있는 거리로 접어들면서 그는 시장기를 느꼈고, 주머니에서 아버지 집에서 가져온 빵 한 덩이를 꺼내 먹었다."], ["It made him feel stronger.", "그러자 기운이 좀 났다."]], [["Dmitri was not at home.", "드미트리는 집에 없었다."], ["The people of the house, an old cabinet-maker, his son, and his old wife, looked with positive suspicion at Alyosha.", "그 집 식구들, 곧 늙은 가구장이와 그의 아들, 늙은 아내는 알료샤를 노골적으로 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보았다."], ["“He hasn’t slept here for the last three nights.", "\"사흘째 밤이나 여기서 안 잤습니다."], ["Maybe he has gone away,”", "어디 가 버렸는지도 모르지요.\""], ["the old man said in answer to Alyosha’s persistent inquiries.", "알료샤의 끈질긴 물음에 노인이 이렇게 대답했다."], ["Alyosha saw that he was answering in accordance with instructions.", "알료샤는 그가 누군가의 지시대로 대답하고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["When he asked whether he were not at Grushenka’s or in hiding at Foma’s (Alyosha spoke so freely on purpose), all three looked at him in alarm.", "그가 형이 그루셴카네 집에 있는 것은 아닌지, 아니면 포마네 집에 숨어 있는 것은 아닌지 물었을 때(알료샤는 일부러 그렇게 거리낌 없이 말했다) 세 사람 모두 그를 놀란 눈으로 쳐다보았다."], ["“They are fond of him, they are doing their best for him,”", "\"이 사람들은 형을 아끼고, 형을 위해 최선을 다하고 있구나.\""], ["thought Alyosha.", "알료샤는 생각했다."], ["“That’s good.”", "\"그거 잘된 일이다.\""]], [["At last he found the house in Lake Street.", "마침내 그는 호수 거리에서 그 집을 찾아냈다."], ["It was a decrepit little house, sunk on one side, with three windows looking into the street, and with a muddy yard, in the middle of which stood a solitary cow.", "한쪽으로 폭삭 주저앉은 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 집으로, 거리 쪽으로 창문 셋이 나 있고, 진흙투성이 마당 한복판에는 소 한 마리가 외따로 서 있었다."], ["He crossed the yard and found the door opening into the passage.", "그는 마당을 가로질러, 복도로 통하는 문을 찾아냈다."], ["On the left of the passage lived the old woman of the house with her old daughter.", "복도 왼편에는 늙은 안주인이 역시 늙은 딸과 함께 살고 있었다."], ["Both seemed to be deaf.", "둘 다 귀가 먼 듯했다."], ["In answer to his repeated inquiry for the captain, one of them at last understood that he was asking for their lodgers, and pointed to a door across the passage.", "대위를 찾는다는 그의 거듭된 물음에 마침내 둘 중 하나가 그가 세든 사람을 찾는다는 것을 알아듣고는, 복도 건너편의 한 문을 가리켰다."], ["The captain’s lodging turned out to be a simple cottage room.", "대위의 거처는 알고 보니 평범한 시골집 방 한 칸이었다."], ["Alyosha had his hand on the iron latch to open the door, when he was struck by the strange hush within.", "알료샤가 문을 열려고 쇠 빗장에 손을 얹었을 때, 그는 안에서 풍기는 묘한 정적에 멈칫했다."], ["Yet he knew from Katerina Ivanovna’s words that the man had a family.", "그러나 카테리나 이바노브나의 말로 미루어 그 사내에게 가족이 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["“Either they are all asleep or perhaps they have heard me coming and are waiting for me to open the door.", "\"다들 잠들었거나, 아니면 어쩌면 내가 오는 소리를 듣고 내가 문 열기를 기다리고 있는 것이리라."], ["I’d better knock first,”", "먼저 노크하는 편이 낫겠다.\""], ["and he knocked.", "그러고는 문을 두드렸다."], ["An answer came, but not at once, after an interval of perhaps ten seconds.", "대답이 들려왔으나, 곧바로는 아니었고, 아마 십 초쯤 사이를 둔 뒤였다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "\"누구요?\""], ["shouted some one in a loud and very angry voice.", "누군가 크고 몹시 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["Then Alyosha opened the door and crossed the threshold.", "그러자 알료샤는 문을 열고 문지방을 넘었다."], ["He found himself in a regular peasant’s room.", "그는 영락없는 농가의 방 안에 들어와 있었다."], ["Though it was large, it was cumbered up with domestic belongings of all sorts, and there were several people in it.", "방은 컸지만 온갖 살림살이로 발 디딜 틈 없이 어수선했고, 그 안에는 몇 사람이 있었다."], ["On the left was a large Russian stove.", "왼편에는 커다란 러시아식 페치카가 있었다."], ["From the stove to the window on the left was a string running across the room, and on it there were rags hanging.", "페치카에서 왼쪽 창문까지 방을 가로질러 끈 하나가 걸려 있었고, 그 위에는 누더기들이 널려 있었다."], ["There was a bedstead against the wall on each side, right and left, covered with knitted quilts.", "좌우 양쪽 벽에는 각각 침대가 하나씩 붙어 있었는데, 뜨개질한 누비이불이 덮여 있었다."], ["On the one on the left was a pyramid of four print-covered pillows, each smaller than the one beneath.", "왼쪽 침대 위에는 무명천을 씌운 베개 넷이 아래에서 위로 갈수록 작아지며 피라미드 모양으로 쌓여 있었다."], ["On the other there was only one very small pillow.", "다른 쪽 침대에는 아주 작은 베개 하나만 놓여 있었다."], ["The opposite corner was screened off by a curtain or a sheet hung on a string.", "맞은편 구석은 끈에 걸친 커튼인지 홑이불인지로 가려져 있었다."], ["Behind this curtain could be seen a bed made up on a bench and a chair.", "그 휘장 너머로는 긴 의자와 의자를 붙여 만든 잠자리가 보였다."], ["The rough square table of plain wood had been moved into the middle window.", "거칠게 짠 네모난 통나무 식탁이 가운데 창문 쪽으로 옮겨져 있었다."], ["The three windows, which consisted each of four tiny greenish mildewy panes, gave little light, and were close shut, so that the room was not very light and rather stuffy.", "창문 셋은 저마다 곰팡이 핀 푸르스름한 작은 유리 네 장으로 되어 있어 빛이 거의 들지 않았고, 게다가 꼭 닫혀 있어서 방 안은 그리 밝지 않고 다소 후텁지근했다."], ["On the table was a frying-pan with the remains of some fried eggs, a half-eaten piece of bread, and a small bottle with a few drops of vodka.", "식탁 위에는 달걀부침 찌꺼기가 남은 프라이팬과 반쯤 먹다 만 빵 한 조각, 그리고 보드카가 몇 방울 남은 작은 병이 놓여 있었다."]], [["A woman of genteel appearance, wearing a cotton gown, was sitting on a chair by the bed on the left.", "무명 가운을 걸친, 점잖아 보이는 여인이 왼쪽 침대 곁의 의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["Her face was thin and yellow, and her sunken cheeks betrayed at the first glance that she was ill.", "얼굴은 야위고 누렇게 떴으며, 움푹 꺼진 두 뺨은 한눈에 그녀가 병들었음을 드러냈다."], ["But what struck Alyosha most was the expression in the poor woman’s eyes—a look of surprised inquiry and yet of haughty pride.", "그러나 알료샤의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 것은 그 가엾은 여인의 눈빛이었다. 놀라움이 깃든 캐묻는 듯한 표정이면서도 동시에 오만한 자존심이 서린 눈빛이었다."], ["And while he was talking to her husband, her big brown eyes moved from one speaker to the other with the same haughty and questioning expression.", "그가 그녀의 남편과 이야기를 나누는 동안에도, 그녀의 큼직한 갈색 눈은 똑같이 오만하고 캐묻는 듯한 표정으로 이 사람에게서 저 사람에게로 옮겨 다녔다."], ["Beside her at the window stood a young girl, rather plain, with scanty reddish hair, poorly but very neatly dressed.", "창가의 그녀 곁에는 젊은 처녀가 서 있었는데, 인물은 그리 곱지 못하고 불그스름한 머리숱이 적었으며, 초라하지만 아주 단정하게 차려입고 있었다."], ["She looked disdainfully at Alyosha as he came in.", "그녀는 알료샤가 들어서자 경멸하는 눈초리로 그를 쳐다보았다."], ["Beside the other bed was sitting another female figure.", "다른 침대 곁에는 또 다른 여인의 모습이 앉아 있었다."], ["She was a very sad sight, a young girl of about twenty, but hunchback and crippled “with withered legs,” as Alyosha was told afterwards.", "그것은 몹시 애처로운 광경이었으니, 스무 살가량의 젊은 처녀였으나 곱사등이에 절름발이였고, 나중에 알료샤가 듣기로는 \"두 다리가 말라비틀어진\""], ["Her crutches stood in the corner close by.", "처녀였다. 그녀의 목발은 바로 곁 구석에 세워져 있었다."], ["The strikingly beautiful and gentle eyes of this poor girl looked with mild serenity at Alyosha.", "이 가엾은 처녀의 눈은 놀랍도록 아름답고 부드러웠는데, 그 눈으로 온화하고 고요하게 알료샤를 바라보았다."], ["A man of forty-five was sitting at the table, finishing the fried eggs.", "마흔다섯쯤 된 사내가 식탁에 앉아 달걀부침을 마저 먹고 있었다."], ["He was spare, small and weakly built.", "그는 깡마르고, 키가 작으며, 허약한 체구였다."], ["He had reddish hair and a scanty light-colored beard, very much like a wisp of tow (this comparison and the phrase “a wisp of tow” flashed at once into Alyosha’s mind for some reason, he remembered it afterwards).", "불그스름한 머리에 옅은 빛깔의 성긴 턱수염을 길렀는데, 영락없이 한 줌의 삼 부스러기 같았다(이 비유와 \"한 줌의 삼 부스러기\"라는 말이 무슨 까닭에선지 그 순간 대뜸 알료샤의 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그는 나중까지 그것을 기억했다)."], ["It was obviously this gentleman who had shouted to him, as there was no other man in the room.", "방 안에 다른 사내라곤 없었으니, 그에게 소리친 것은 분명 이 양반이었다."], ["But when Alyosha went in, he leapt up from the bench on which he was sitting, and, hastily wiping his mouth with a ragged napkin, darted up to Alyosha.", "그러나 알료샤가 들어서자 그는 앉아 있던 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 누더기 같은 냅킨으로 황급히 입을 닦으며 알료샤에게로 쏜살같이 달려들었다."]], [["“It’s a monk come to beg for the monastery.", "\"수도원에서 시주를 청하러 온 수도사로군."], ["A nice place to come to!”", "찾아올 데가 따로 있지!\""], ["the girl standing in the left corner said aloud.", "왼쪽 구석에 서 있던 처녀가 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["The man spun round instantly towards her and answered her in an excited and breaking voice:", "사내는 즉시 그녀 쪽으로 홱 돌아서더니, 흥분으로 갈라지는 목소리로 대꾸했다."]], [["“No, Varvara, you are wrong.", "\"아니다, 바르바라, 네가 틀렸어."], ["Allow me to ask,”", "실례지만 묻겠습니다.\""], ["he turned again to Alyosha, “what has brought you to—our retreat?”", "그는 다시 알료샤를 돌아보았다. \"무슨 일로 이런—저희 누추한 거처까지 찾아오셨는지요?\""]], [["Alyosha looked attentively at him.", "알료샤는 그를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["It was the first time he had seen him.", "그를 보는 것은 처음이었다."], ["There was something angular, flurried and irritable about him.", "그에게는 어딘가 모나고, 허둥대며, 짜증스러운 데가 있었다."], ["Though he had obviously just been drinking, he was not drunk.", "방금 술을 마신 것이 분명했으나 취하지는 않았다."], ["There was extraordinary impudence in his expression, and yet, strange to say, at the same time there was fear.", "표정에는 유별난 뻔뻔함이 서려 있으면서도, 이상하게도 그와 동시에 두려움이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He looked like a man who had long been kept in subjection and had submitted to it, and now had suddenly turned and was trying to assert himself.", "그는 오랫동안 짓눌려 거기에 순종해 오다가 이제 별안간 돌아서서 자기를 내세워 보려고 애쓰는 사람 같았다."], ["Or, better still, like a man who wants dreadfully to hit you but is horribly afraid you will hit him.", "아니, 더 정확히 말하자면, 끔찍이도 누군가를 한 대 치고 싶어 하면서도 그 상대가 자기를 칠까 봐 무섭도록 겁내는 사람 같았다."], ["In his words and in the intonation of his shrill voice there was a sort of crazy humor, at times spiteful and at times cringing, and continually shifting from one tone to another.", "그의 말과 날카로운 목소리의 억양에는 일종의 광기 어린 익살이 배어 있었는데, 때로는 악의에 차고 때로는 비굴하며, 끊임없이 이 어조에서 저 어조로 옮겨 다녔다."], ["The question about “our retreat” he had asked as it were quivering all over, rolling his eyes, and skipping up so close to Alyosha that he instinctively drew back a step.", "\"저희 누추한 거처\"에 관한 물음을 그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨다시피 하며, 눈알을 굴리고, 알료샤에게 바짝 다가서며 던졌으므로, 알료샤는 저도 모르게 한 걸음 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["He was dressed in a very shabby dark cotton coat, patched and spotted.", "그는 몹시 낡고 거무튀튀한 무명 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 군데군데 기우고 얼룩이 져 있었다."], ["He wore checked trousers of an extremely light color, long out of fashion, and of very thin material.", "바지는 격자무늬에 빛깔이 지극히 옅고, 유행에 한참 뒤떨어진 데다, 천이 아주 얇았다."], ["They were so crumpled and so short that he looked as though he had grown out of them like a boy.", "어찌나 구깃구깃하고 짧았던지, 마치 사내아이가 자라 옷이 작아진 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“I am Alexey Karamazov,”", "\"저는 알렉세이 카라마조프입니다.\""], ["Alyosha began in reply.", "알료샤가 대답을 시작했다."]], [["“I quite understand that, sir,”", "\"그건 익히 알고 있습지요, 선생.\""], ["the gentleman snapped out at once to assure him that he knew who he was already.", "그 양반은 이미 그가 누군지 알고 있노라 못 박으려는 듯 대뜸 말을 잘랐다."], ["“I am Captain Snegiryov, sir, but I am still desirous to know precisely what has led you—”", "\"저는 스네기료프 대위올시다, 선생. 하지만 그래도 정확히 무엇이 선생을 이리로—\""]], [["“Oh, I’ve come for nothing special.", "\"오, 별 대단한 일로 온 건 아닙니다."], ["I wanted to have a word with you—if only you allow me.”", "그저 한 말씀 나누고 싶어서요—허락해 주신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“In that case, here is a chair, sir; kindly be seated.", "\"그렇다면 여기 의자가 있습니다, 선생. 부디 앉으시지요."], ["That’s what they used to say in the old comedies, ‘kindly be seated,’”", "옛날 희극에서 늘 하던 대사 아닙니까, '부디 앉으시지요'라고.\""], ["and with a rapid gesture he seized an empty chair (it was a rough wooden chair, not upholstered) and set it for him almost in the middle of the room; then, taking another similar chair for himself, he sat down facing Alyosha, so close to him that their knees almost touched.", "그러고는 잽싼 동작으로 빈 의자 하나를 움켜쥐더니(쿠션도 없는 거친 나무 의자였다) 거의 방 한복판에 그것을 놓아 주고는, 자기도 그와 비슷한 의자를 하나 끌어다 알료샤를 마주 보고 앉았는데, 어찌나 바짝 붙어 앉았던지 두 사람의 무릎이 거의 맞닿을 지경이었다."]], [["“Nikolay Ilyitch Snegiryov, sir, formerly a captain in the Russian infantry, put to shame for his vices, but still a captain. Though I might not be one now for the way I talk; for the last half of my life I’ve learnt to say ‘sir.’ It’s a word you use when you’ve come down in the world.”", "\"니콜라이 일리치 스네기료프올시다, 선생. 한때 러시아 보병 대위였으나, 제 악행으로 망신을 당했지요. 그래도 어쨌든 대위입니다. 하기야 이렇게 말하는 꼴을 보면 지금은 대위가 아닐지도 모르지요. 후반생 내내 저는 '선생' 소리를 입에 달고 사는 법을 배웠으니까요. 그건 세상에서 영락한 사람이나 쓰는 말입지요.\""]], [["“That’s very true,”", "\"지당하신 말씀입니다.\""], ["smiled Alyosha.", "알료샤가 미소 지었다."], ["“But is it used involuntarily or on purpose?”", "\"그런데 그건 저절로 나오는 겁니까, 아니면 일부러 그러시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“As God’s above, it’s involuntary, and I usen’t to use it!", "\"하느님께 맹세코, 저절로 나오는 거지요. 예전엔 쓰지도 않았습니다!"], ["I didn’t use the word ‘sir’ all my life, but as soon as I sank into low water I began to say ‘sir.’ It’s the work of a higher power.", "평생 '선생'이란 말을 입에 담은 적이 없는데, 신세가 바닥으로 가라앉자마자 '선생' 소리를 하기 시작했단 말입니다."], ["I see you are interested in contemporary questions, but how can I have excited your curiosity, living as I do in surroundings impossible for the exercise of hospitality?”", "이건 더 높은 힘이 하는 일이지요. 보아하니 선생께서는 요즘 세태에 관심이 있으신 모양인데, 손님 대접이라곤 할 수 없는 이런 형편에서 사는 제가 어찌 선생의 호기심을 자극할 수 있었겠습니까?\""]], [["“I’ve come—about that business.”", "\"제가 온 건—그 일 때문입니다.\""]], [["“About what business?”", "\"그 일이라니, 무슨 일 말입니까?\""], ["the captain interrupted impatiently.", "대위가 안달하며 말을 끊었다."]], [["“About your meeting with my brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"제 형 드미트리 표도로비치와의 그 일 때문입니다.\""], ["Alyosha blurted out awkwardly.", "알료샤가 어색하게 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“What meeting, sir?", "\"무슨 일 말입니까, 선생?"], ["You don’t mean that meeting?", "설마 그 일을 말씀하시는 건 아니겠지요?"], ["About my ‘wisp of tow,’ then?”", "그럼 제 '한 줌의 삼 부스러기'"], ["He moved closer so that his knees positively knocked against Alyosha.", "얘깁니까?\" 그는 더 가까이 다가앉아, 무릎이 정말로 알료샤의 무릎을 부딪쳤다."], ["His lips were strangely compressed like a thread.", "그의 입술은 이상하게도 실처럼 앙다물려 있었다."]], [["“What wisp of tow?”", "\"한 줌의 삼 부스러기라니요?\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“He is come to complain of me, father!”", "\"저 형이 절 일러바치러 온 거예요, 아버지!\""], ["cried a voice familiar to Alyosha—the voice of the schoolboy—from behind the curtain.", "알료샤에게 낯익은 목소리가—그 학생의 목소리가—휘장 너머에서 외쳤다."], ["“I bit his finger just now.”", "\"제가 방금 저 사람 손가락을 깨물었거든요.\""], ["The curtain was pulled, and Alyosha saw his assailant lying on a little bed made up on the bench and the chair in the corner under the ikons.", "휘장이 젖혀지자, 알료샤는 자기를 습격했던 그 아이가 구석의 성상 아래, 긴 의자와 의자를 붙여 만든 작은 잠자리에 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The boy lay covered by his coat and an old wadded quilt.", "아이는 외투와 낡은 솜이불을 덮고 누워 있었다."], ["He was evidently unwell, and, judging by his glittering eyes, he was in a fever.", "보아하니 몸이 성치 않았고, 번들거리는 눈으로 미루어 열이 있는 듯했다."], ["He looked at Alyosha without fear, as though he felt he was at home and could not be touched.", "그는 마치 제집에 있어서 아무도 자기를 건드리지 못한다고 여기는 듯, 두려움 없이 알료샤를 바라보았다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Did he bite your finger?”", "저 애가 네 손가락을 깨물었다고?\""], ["The captain jumped up from his chair.", "대위가 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다."], ["“Was it your finger he bit?”", "\"저 애가 깨문 게 네 손가락이었어?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["He was throwing stones with other schoolboys.", "저 애가 다른 학생들과 돌을 던지고 있었습니다."], ["There were six of them against him alone.", "여섯이서 저 애 혼자를 상대로요."], ["I went up to him, and he threw a stone at me and then another at my head.", "제가 다가가니까 저 애가 제게 돌을 던지고, 또 한 번은 제 머리를 겨눠 던졌습니다."], ["I asked him what I had done to him.", "제가 무슨 잘못을 했느냐고 물었지요."], ["And then he rushed at me and bit my finger badly, I don’t know why.”", "그러자 저 애가 제게 달려들어 제 손가락을 사정없이 깨물었습니다. 왜 그랬는지는 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“I’ll thrash him, sir, at once—this minute!”", "\"제가 저놈을 매질하겠습니다, 선생, 당장에—이 자리에서!\""], ["The captain jumped up from his seat.", "대위가 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어났다."]], [["“But I am not complaining at all, I am simply telling you ...", "\"하지만 저는 결코 일러바치려는 게 아닙니다. 그저 말씀드리는 것뿐이고……"], ["I don’t want him to be thrashed.", "저는 저 애가 매를 맞는 걸 원치 않습니다."], ["Besides, he seems to be ill.”", "게다가 보아하니 몸도 성치 않은 듯하고요.\""]], [["“And do you suppose I’d thrash him?", "\"그럼 제가 저놈을 매질하리라고 생각하십니까?"], ["That I’d take my Ilusha and thrash him before you for your satisfaction?", "제 일류샤를 끌어다 선생 분풀이나 시켜 드리려고 선생 앞에서 매질하리라고요?"], ["Would you like it done at once, sir?”", "당장 그래 드릴까요, 선생?\""], ["said the captain, suddenly turning to Alyosha, as though he were going to attack him.", "대위가 별안간 알료샤를 향해, 마치 그에게 달려들 기세로 돌아섰다."], ["“I am sorry about your finger, sir; but instead of thrashing Ilusha, would you like me to chop off my four fingers with this knife here before your eyes to satisfy your just wrath?", "\"선생 손가락은 안됐습니다만, 일류샤를 매질하는 대신, 선생의 정당한 분노를 풀어 드리려 여기 이 칼로 선생 눈앞에서 제 손가락 넷을 잘라 버리는 게 어떻겠습니까?"], ["I should think four fingers would be enough to satisfy your thirst for vengeance.", "손가락 넷이면 선생의 복수심을 채우기에 족하리라 봅니다만."], ["You won’t ask for the fifth one too?”", "설마 다섯째 손가락까지 달라고 하시진 않겠지요?\""], ["He stopped short with a catch in his throat.", "그는 목이 메어 말을 뚝 그쳤다."], ["Every feature in his face was twitching and working; he looked extremely defiant.", "얼굴의 근육이란 근육은 죄다 씰룩거리며 일그러졌고, 그는 극도로 도전적인 표정이었다."], ["He was in a sort of frenzy.", "일종의 광란 상태에 빠져 있었다."]], [["“I think I understand it all now,”", "\"이제 다 알 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha gently and sorrowfully, still keeping his seat.", "알료샤가 여전히 자리를 지킨 채 부드럽고 슬픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["“So your boy is a good boy, he loves his father, and he attacked me as the brother of your assailant....", "\"그러니까 댁의 아드님은 착한 아이고, 제 아버지를 사랑하기에, 자기 아버지를 습격한 사람의 동생인 저를 공격한 것이군요…… 이제 알겠습니다.\""], ["Now I understand it,”", "그는 생각에 잠겨 거듭 말했다."], ["he repeated thoughtfully.", "\"하지만 제 형 드미트리 표도로비치는 자기 행동을 뉘우치고 있습니다."], ["“But my brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch regrets his action, I know that, and if only it is possible for him to come to you, or better still, to meet you in that same place, he will ask your forgiveness before every one—if you wish it.”", "저는 그걸 알고 있습니다. 그리고 만약 형이 댁으로 찾아올 수 있다면, 아니 그보다 바로 그 자리에서 댁을 만날 수만 있다면, 형은 여러 사람 앞에서 용서를 빌 겁니다—댁이 원하신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“After pulling out my beard, you mean, he will ask my forgiveness?", "\"그러니까 제 수염을 뽑아 놓고 나서 용서를 빌겠다는 말씀이지요?"], ["And he thinks that will be a satisfactory finish, doesn’t he?”", "그러면 그것으로 깔끔하게 마무리되리라 여기는 게로군요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아닙니다!"], ["On the contrary, he will do anything you like and in any way you like.”", "오히려 형은 댁이 원하시는 것은 무엇이든, 댁이 원하시는 방식으로 다 할 겁니다.\""]], [["“So if I were to ask his highness to go down on his knees before me in that very tavern—‘The Metropolis’ it’s called—or in the market-place, he would do it?”", "\"그러면 제가 그 나리께 바로 그 술집에서—'수도'라는 이름의 술집이지요—아니면 장터에서 제 앞에 무릎을 꿇으라고 청하면, 그렇게 하리란 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, he would even go down on his knees.”", "\"네, 형은 무릎까지도 꿇을 겁니다.\""]], [["“You’ve pierced me to the heart, sir.", "\"선생은 제 가슴을 깊이 찌르셨습니다, 선생."], ["Touched me to tears and pierced me to the heart!", "저를 눈물 나도록 감동시키고 제 가슴을 깊이 찌르셨어요!"], ["I am only too sensible of your brother’s generosity.", "저는 댁의 형님의 너그러움을 너무도 잘 헤아리고 있습니다."], ["Allow me to introduce my family, my two daughters and my son—my litter.", "제 식구를 소개하게 해 주십시오. 제 두 딸과 아들, 곧 제 새끼들 말입니다."], ["If I die, who will care for them, and while I live who but they will care for a wretch like me?", "제가 죽으면 누가 이 애들을 돌봐 주겠으며, 제가 살아 있은들 이 애들 말고 누가 저 같은 가련한 인간을 보살펴 주겠습니까?"], ["That’s a great thing the Lord has ordained for every man of my sort, sir.", "그건 주님께서 저 같은 부류의 인간마다에게 마련해 주신 크나큰 은총이지요, 선생."], ["For there must be some one able to love even a man like me.”", "저 같은 인간조차 사랑해 줄 수 있는 누군가는 반드시 있어야 하는 법이니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, that’s perfectly true!”", "\"아, 정말 지당한 말씀입니다!\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Oh, do leave off playing the fool!", "\"오, 제발 어릿광대짓 좀 그만둬요!"], ["Some idiot comes in, and you put us to shame!”", "무슨 바보 같은 작자가 들어왔다고 우리를 망신시키는 거예요!\""], ["cried the girl by the window, suddenly turning to her father with a disdainful and contemptuous air.", "창가의 처녀가 별안간 경멸하고 멸시하는 태도로 아버지를 돌아보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Wait a little, Varvara!”", "\"잠깐만 기다려라, 바르바라!\""], ["cried her father, speaking peremptorily but looking at her quite approvingly.", "아버지가 단호한 어조로 외쳤으나, 정작 그녀를 바라보는 눈길은 자못 흐뭇해하는 빛이었다."], ["“That’s her character,”", "\"저 애의 성미가 저렇답니다.\""], ["he said, addressing Alyosha again.", "그가 다시 알료샤를 향해 말했다."]], [["“And in all nature there was naught That could find favor in his eyes—", "\"그리고 온 누리에 그 무엇도\n그의 눈에 드는 것은 없었더라—"]], [["or rather in the feminine: that could find favor in her eyes.", "아니, 여성형으로 하자면, 그녀의 눈에 드는 것은 없었더라, 이 말입지요."], ["But now let me present you to my wife, Arina Petrovna.", "자, 그럼 이제 제 아내 아리나 페트로브나를 소개하겠습니다."], ["She is crippled, she is forty-three; she can move, but very little.", "다리를 못 쓰고, 마흔셋이며, 움직이긴 하나 아주 조금밖에 못 움직입니다."], ["She is of humble origin.", "천한 집안 태생이지요."], ["Arina Petrovna, compose your countenance.", "아리나 페트로브나, 표정 좀 가다듬으시오."], ["This is Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov.", "이분은 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프올시다."], ["Get up, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”", "일어나시오, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["He took him by the hand and with unexpected force pulled him up.", "그는 알료샤의 손을 잡더니 뜻밖의 힘으로 그를 끌어 일으켰다."], ["“You must stand up to be introduced to a lady.", "\"부인께 소개받으려면 일어서셔야지요."], ["It’s not the Karamazov, mamma, who ...", "그 카라마조프가 아니라오, 여보, 그……"], ["h’m ...", "흠……"], ["etcetera, but his brother, radiant with modest virtues.", "운운하는 그 양반 말고, 그 사람의 동생인데, 겸손한 미덕으로 빛나는 분이라오."], ["Come, Arina Petrovna, come, mamma, first your hand to be kissed.”", "자, 아리나 페트로브나, 자, 여보, 먼저 손에 입을 맞추시도록.\""]], [["And he kissed his wife’s hand respectfully and even tenderly.", "그러고는 아내의 손에 정중하게, 심지어 다정하게 입을 맞추었다."], ["The girl at the window turned her back indignantly on the scene; an expression of extraordinary cordiality came over the haughtily inquiring face of the woman.", "창가의 처녀는 그 광경에 분개하여 등을 돌렸다. 오만하게 캐묻던 여인의 얼굴에 유난스러운 다정함이 어렸다."]], [["“Good morning!", "\"안녕하세요!"], ["Sit down, Mr. Tchernomazov,”", "앉으세요, 체르노마조프 씨.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Karamazov, mamma, Karamazov.", "\"카라마조프요, 여보, 카라마조프."], ["We are of humble origin,”", "우린 천한 집안 태생이지요.\""], ["he whispered again.", "그가 다시 속삭였다."]], [["“Well, Karamazov, or whatever it is, but I always think of Tchernomazov....", "\"그래요, 카라마조프든 뭐든, 난 늘 체르노마조프로 생각된단 말이지요……"], ["Sit down.", "앉으세요."], ["Why has he pulled you up?", "저이는 왜 당신을 일으켜 세웠담?"], ["He calls me crippled, but I am not, only my legs are swollen like barrels, and I am shriveled up myself.", "저이는 날 보고 다리를 못 쓴다고 하지만, 못 쓰는 게 아니라 그저 다리가 술통처럼 퉁퉁 부었을 뿐이고, 나 자신은 비쩍 쪼그라들었답니다."], ["Once I used to be so fat, but now it’s as though I had swallowed a needle.”", "한때는 그렇게 살이 쪘었는데, 지금은 꼭 바늘이라도 삼킨 것 같지 뭐예요.\""]], [["“We are of humble origin,”", "\"우린 천한 집안 태생이지요.\""], ["the captain muttered again.", "대위가 다시 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, father, father!”", "\"오, 아버지, 아버지!\""], ["the hunchback girl, who had till then been silent on her chair, said suddenly, and she hid her eyes in her handkerchief.", "그때까지 의자에 앉아 잠자코 있던 곱사등이 처녀가 별안간 입을 열더니, 손수건에 두 눈을 묻었다."]], [["“Buffoon!”", "\"어릿광대 같으니!\""], ["blurted out the girl at the window.", "창가의 처녀가 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“Have you heard our news?” said the mother, pointing at her daughters. “It’s like clouds coming over; the clouds pass and we have music again. When we were with the army, we used to have many such guests. I don’t mean to make any comparisons; every one to their taste. The deacon’s wife used to come then and say, ‘Alexandr Alexandrovitch is a man of the noblest heart, but Nastasya Petrovna,’ she would say, ‘is of the brood of hell.’ ‘Well,’ I said, ‘that’s a matter of taste; but you are a little spitfire.’ ‘And you want keeping in your place,’ says she. ‘You black sword,’ said I, ‘who asked you to teach me?’ ‘But my breath,’ says she, ‘is clean, and yours is unclean.’ ‘You ask all the officers whether my breath is unclean.’ And ever since then I had it in my mind. Not long ago I was sitting here as I am now, when I saw that very general come in who came here for Easter, and I asked him: ‘Your Excellency,’ said I, ‘can a lady’s breath be unpleasant?’ ‘Yes,’ he answered; ‘you ought to open a window-pane or open the door, for the air is not fresh here.’ And they all go on like that! And what is my breath to them? The dead smell worse still! ‘I won’t spoil the air,’ said I, ‘I’ll order some slippers and go away.’ My darlings, don’t blame your own mother! Nikolay Ilyitch, how is it I can’t please you? There’s only Ilusha who comes home from school and loves me. Yesterday he brought me an apple. Forgive your own mother—forgive a poor lonely creature! Why has my breath become unpleasant to you?”", "\"우리 집 소식을 들으셨나요?\" 어머니가 두 딸을 가리키며 말했다. \"꼭 먹구름이 몰려오는 것 같답니다. 구름이 지나가면 또 음악이 울리지요. 우리가 군대에 있던 시절엔 이런 손님이 참 많이 드나들었답니다. 비교하려는 건 아니에요. 사람마다 취향이 다른 법이니까요. 그 시절엔 부제의 아내가 찾아와선 이러더군요. '알렉산드르 알렉산드로비치는 더없이 고귀한 마음을 지닌 분이지만, 나스타시야 페트로브나는 지옥의 종자라니까요.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '글쎄요, 그건 취향 문제고, 댁이야말로 성깔 사나운 여편네구려.' 그러니까 저쪽이 그래요. '당신은 분수를 좀 알아야겠소.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '이 시커먼 칼 같은 여편네야, 누가 당신더러 날 가르쳐 달라 했소?' 그러니까 이러는 거예요. '하지만 내 숨결은 깨끗하고, 당신 숨결은 더럽다오.' 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '내 숨결이 더러운지 어떤지 장교들한테 다 물어보구려.' 그날 이후로 그 말이 줄곧 마음에 걸렸답니다. 얼마 전에도 내가 지금처럼 여기 앉아 있는데, 부활절 때 여길 들렀던 바로 그 장군이 들어오지 뭐예요. 그래서 내가 물었지요. '각하,' 하고 말이에요, '귀부인의 숨결이 불쾌할 수도 있는 건가요?' 그랬더니 이러는 거예요. '그렇소. 창문이나 문을 좀 열어 두는 게 좋겠소. 여긴 공기가 신선하질 않으니.' 다들 그 모양이라니까요! 하지만 내 숨결이 그 사람들과 무슨 상관이람? 죽은 사람한테선 더 고약한 냄새가 나는데! 그래서 내가 그랬지요. '난 공기를 더럽히지 않겠어요. 슬리퍼나 한 켤레 맞춰서 떠나 버릴 테니.' 얘들아, 너희 어미를 탓하지 말아 다오! 니콜라이 일리치, 어쩌면 난 당신을 이렇게도 기쁘게 해 드리지 못한답니까? 학교에서 돌아와 날 사랑해 주는 건 일류샤뿐이에요. 어제는 사과를 하나 갖다 주더군요. 너희 어미를 용서해 다오—가련하고 외로운 인간을 용서해 다오! 어쩌다 내 숨결이 당신한테 불쾌한 것이 되어 버렸을까요?\""]], [["And the poor mad woman broke into sobs, and tears streamed down her cheeks.", "그러더니 그 가엾고 정신이 온전치 못한 여인은 흐느껴 울기 시작했고, 두 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다."], ["The captain rushed up to her.", "대위가 그녀에게로 달려갔다."]], [["“Mamma, mamma, my dear, give over!", "\"여보, 여보, 진정해요!"], ["You are not lonely.", "당신은 외롭지 않아요."], ["Every one loves you, every one adores you.”", "다들 당신을 사랑하고, 다들 당신을 떠받든다오.\""], ["He began kissing both her hands again and tenderly stroking her face; taking the dinner-napkin, he began wiping away her tears.", "그는 다시 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추기 시작하더니 다정하게 그녀의 얼굴을 어루만졌고, 식탁의 냅킨을 집어 그녀의 눈물을 닦아 주었다."], ["Alyosha fancied that he too had tears in his eyes.", "알료샤가 보기에는 그의 눈에도 눈물이 고인 듯했다."], ["“There, you see, you hear?”", "\"자, 보셨지요, 들으셨지요?\""], ["he turned with a sort of fury to Alyosha, pointing to the poor imbecile.", "그는 그 가엾은 백치를 가리키며 일종의 격분에 휩싸여 알료샤를 돌아보았다."]], [["“I see and hear,”", "\"보았고 들었습니다.\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Father, father, how can you—with him! Let him alone!”", "\"아버지, 아버지, 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요—저런 작자 앞에서!"], ["cried the boy, sitting up in his bed and gazing at his father with glowing eyes.", "저 사람 좀 내버려 둬요!\" 아이가 침대에서 일어나 앉아 이글거리는 눈으로 아버지를 노려보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Do give over fooling, showing off your silly antics which never lead to anything!”", "\"제발 그 어릿광대짓 좀 그만둬요, 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 그 바보 같은 익살을 떨어 대면서!\""], ["shouted Varvara, stamping her foot with passion.", "바르바라가 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 격하게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Your anger is quite just this time, Varvara, and I’ll make haste to satisfy you.", "\"이번만은 네 분노가 지당하구나, 바르바라. 어서 서둘러 네 비위를 맞춰 주마."], ["Come, put on your cap, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and I’ll put on mine.", "자, 모자를 쓰시지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 저도 제 모자를 쓰겠습니다."], ["We will go out.", "우리 밖으로 나갑시다."], ["I have a word to say to you in earnest, but not within these walls.", "진지하게 드릴 말씀이 있는데, 이 벽 안에서는 안 됩니다."], ["This girl sitting here is my daughter Nina; I forgot to introduce her to you.", "여기 앉아 있는 이 아이는 제 딸 니나입니다. 소개하는 걸 깜빡했군요."], ["She is a heavenly angel incarnate ...", "이 애는 하늘에서 내려온 천사의 화신이지요……"], ["who has flown down to us mortals,...", "우리 같은 인간들 사이로 날아 내려온……"], ["if you can understand.”", "이해하실 수 있다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“There he is shaking all over, as though he is in convulsions!”", "\"저것 봐, 마치 경련이라도 일으킨 듯 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있잖아!\""], ["Varvara went on indignantly.", "바르바라가 분개하여 말을 이었다."]], [["“And she there stamping her foot at me and calling me a fool just now, she is a heavenly angel incarnate too, and she has good reason to call me so.", "\"그리고 방금 저기서 내게 발을 구르며 날 보고 바보라 한 저 애도, 역시 하늘에서 내려온 천사의 화신이랍니다. 그리고 저 애가 날 그렇게 부르는 데는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있지요."], ["Come along, Alexey Fyodorovitch, we must make an end.”", "갑시다, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리 이제 끝장을 봐야 합니다.\""]], [["And, snatching Alyosha’s hand, he drew him out of the room into the street.", "그러고는 알료샤의 손을 와락 붙잡더니, 그를 방에서 거리로 끌어냈다."]], [["# Chapter VII. And In The Open Air", "# Chapter VII. And In The Open Air"]], [["“The air is fresh, but in my apartment it is not so in any sense of the word.", "\"공기야 신선하지만, 우리 집 안은 그 어떤 의미로도 그렇지가 못합니다."], ["Let us walk slowly, sir.", "천천히 걸으시지요, 나리."], ["I should be glad of your kind interest.”", "이렇게 너그럽게 관심을 가져 주시니 기쁠 따름입니다.\""]], [["“I too have something important to say to you,”", "\"저도 당신께 드릴 중요한 말씀이 있습니다.\""], ["observed Alyosha, “only I don’t know how to begin.”", "알료샤가 입을 열었다. \"다만 어떻게 시작해야 할지 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“To be sure you must have business with me.", "\"분명 저한테 무슨 용건이 있으셔서 오셨겠지요."], ["You would never have looked in upon me without some object.", "아무 목적도 없이 이런 사람을 찾아오실 리가 없으니까요."], ["Unless you come simply to complain of the boy, and that’s hardly likely.", "그저 그 애 일로 불평하러 오신 것이 아니라면 말입니다만, 그건 좀처럼 있을 법한 일이 아니지요."], ["And, by the way, about the boy: I could not explain to you in there, but here I will describe that scene to you.", "그건 그렇고, 그 애 이야기인데, 안에서는 도무지 설명드릴 수가 없었습니다만, 여기서라면 그 장면을 말씀드릴 수 있겠습니다. 일주일 전만 해도 제 수세미가 좀 더 무성했지요."], ["My tow was thicker a week ago—I mean my beard.", "제 수염 말씀입니다. 학생 녀석들이 무엇보다도 제 수염을 그렇게 별명으로 부른답니다."], ["That’s the nickname they give to my beard, the schoolboys most of all.", "그런데 말입니다, 당신 형님 드미트리 표도로비치께서 제 수염을 잡아끌었지요. 저는 아무 짓도 안 했는데, 그분이 노발대발하시던 차에 마침 저와 마주친 겁니다."], ["Well, your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch was pulling me by my beard, I’d done nothing, he was in a towering rage and happened to come upon me.", "그분은 저를 술집에서 광장으로 끌어내셨는데, 바로 그때 학교에서 아이들이 나오던 참이었고, 그 속에 일류샤도 있었습니다. 그 애는 제가 그런 꼴을 하고 있는 걸 보자마자 저한테 달려왔지요."], ["He dragged me out of the tavern into the market-place; at that moment the boys were coming out of school, and with them Ilusha.", "'아빠.' 그 애가 외쳤습니다."], ["As soon as he saw me in such a state he rushed up to me.", "'아빠!' 그 애는 저를 붙들고 끌어안고는 떼어 놓으려 애쓰면서, 저를 붙든 사람한테 소리쳤습니다."], ["‘Father,’ he cried, ‘father!’ He caught hold of me, hugged me, tried to pull me away, crying to my assailant, ‘Let go, let go, it’s my father, forgive him!’—yes, he actually cried ‘forgive him.’ He clutched at that hand, that very hand, in his little hands and kissed it....", "'놓아 줘요, 놓아 줘요, 우리 아빠예요, 용서해 줘요!' 그렇습니다, 그 애가 정말로 '용서해 줘요'라고 외쳤지요."], ["I remember his little face at that moment, I haven’t forgotten it and I never shall!”", "그 애는 그 손을, 바로 그 손을 제 조그만 손으로 움켜쥐고 입을 맞췄습니다…… 저는 그 순간 그 애의 조그만 얼굴을 기억합니다, 잊지 않았고 앞으로도 영영 잊지 못할 겁니다!\""]], [["“I swear,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["cried Alyosha, “that my brother will express his most deep and sincere regret, even if he has to go down on his knees in that same market-place....", "알료샤가 외쳤다. \"제 형님은 같은 그 광장에서 무릎을 꿇는 한이 있더라도 가장 깊고 진실한 뉘우침을 표할 겁니다……"], ["I’ll make him or he is no brother of mine!”", "제가 그렇게 시키겠습니다, 그러지 않는다면 제 형이 아니지요!\""]], [["“Aha, then it’s only a suggestion!", "\"아하, 그러니까 그건 그저 제안에 불과한 거로군요!"], ["And it does not come from him but simply from the generosity of your own warm heart.", "그것도 그분에게서 나온 게 아니라 그저 당신의 따뜻한 마음씨의 너그러움에서 나온 거고요."], ["You should have said so.", "진작 그렇게 말씀하셨어야지요."], ["No, in that case allow me to tell you of your brother’s highly chivalrous soldierly generosity, for he did give expression to it at the time.", "아니지요, 그렇다면 당신 형님이 보여 주신 더없이 기사도적이고 군인다운 너그러움에 대해 제가 말씀드리도록 해 주십시오."], ["He left off dragging me by my beard and released me: ‘You are an officer,’ he said, ‘and I am an officer, if you can find a decent man to be your second send me your challenge.", "그분은 그때 정말로 그것을 드러내 보이셨으니까요."], ["I will give satisfaction, though you are a scoundrel.’ That’s what he said.", "그분은 제 수염을 잡아끌던 것을 그만두고 저를 놓아주셨습니다. '너도 장교고,'"], ["A chivalrous spirit indeed!", "그분이 말씀하셨지요. '나도 장교다."], ["I retired with Ilusha, and that scene is a family record imprinted for ever on Ilusha’s soul.", "만약 입회인이 되어 줄 점잖은 사람을 구할 수 있거든 나한테 결투장을 보내라."], ["No, it’s not for us to claim the privileges of noblemen.", "네놈이 비록 악당이지만 결투에 응해 주마.' 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Judge for yourself.", "과연 기사도적인 정신이지요!"], ["You’ve just been in our mansion, what did you see there?", "저는 일류샤를 데리고 물러났습니다만, 그 장면은 일류샤의 영혼에 영영 각인된 우리 집안의 기록이 되었습니다. 아니지요, 우리 같은 사람이 귀족의 특권을 주장할 처지는 못 됩니다."], ["Three ladies, one a cripple and weak-minded, another a cripple and hunchback and the third not crippled but far too clever.", "직접 헤아려 보십시오. 방금 우리 저택에 들어와 보셨으니, 거기서 무엇을 보셨습니까?"], ["She is a student, dying to get back to Petersburg, to work for the emancipation of the Russian woman on the banks of the Neva.", "여자 셋인데, 하나는 절름발이에 정신이 약하고, 또 하나는 절름발이에 곱사등이고, 셋째는 절름발이는 아니지만 지나치게 영리하지요. 그 애는 여학생인데, 페테르부르크로 돌아가서 네바 강변에서 러시아 여성 해방을 위해 일하고 싶어 안달이 나 있답니다."], ["I won’t speak of Ilusha, he is only nine.", "일류샤 이야기는 하지 않겠습니다, 그 애는 겨우 아홉 살이니까요."], ["I am alone in the world, and if I die, what will become of all of them?", "저는 이 세상에 혼자고, 제가 죽으면 저 사람들 모두는 어떻게 되겠습니까?"], ["I simply ask you that.", "저는 그저 그것을 여쭙는 겁니다."], ["And if I challenge him and he kills me on the spot, what then?", "제가 그분에게 결투를 신청하고 그분이 그 자리에서 저를 죽인다면, 그땐 어떻게 됩니까?"], ["What will become of them?", "저 사람들은 어떻게 되느냔 말입니다."], ["And worse still, if he doesn’t kill me but only cripples me: I couldn’t work, but I should still be a mouth to feed.", "그보다 더 나쁘게는, 그분이 저를 죽이지 않고 그저 불구로만 만든다면요. 일도 못 하면서 여전히 먹여 살려야 할 입은 하나가 될 테지요."], ["Who would feed it and who would feed them all?", "누가 그 입을 먹이고, 누가 저들 모두를 먹입니까?"], ["Must I take Ilusha from school and send him to beg in the streets?", "일류샤를 학교에서 빼내 길거리로 구걸을 보내야 하겠습니까?"], ["That’s what it means for me to challenge him to a duel.", "제가 그분에게 결투를 신청한다는 건 저로서는 그런 의미입니다."], ["It’s silly talk and nothing else.”", "그건 어리석은 소리에 지나지 않지요.\""]], [["“He will beg your forgiveness, he will bow down at your feet in the middle of the market-place,”", "\"그분은 당신의 용서를 빌 겁니다, 광장 한복판에서 당신의 발치에 엎드려 절을 할 겁니다.\""], ["cried Alyosha again, with glowing eyes.", "알료샤가 다시 두 눈을 빛내며 외쳤다."]], [["“I did think of prosecuting him,”", "\"고소를 해 볼까도 생각했습니다.\" 이등 대위가 말을 이었다."], ["the captain went on, “but look in our code, could I get much compensation for a personal injury?", "\"하지만 우리 법전을 들여다보십시오, 제가 신체상의 상해로 무슨 보상을 많이 받을 수 있겠습니까? 게다가 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 저를 불러서는 소리를 질러 댔지요."], ["And then Agrafena Alexandrovna[3] sent for me and shouted at me: ‘Don’t dare to dream of it!", "'그런 꿈은 꾸지도 마! 그 사람을 걸고넘어졌다간, 네가 부정직해서 그가 너를 때렸다고 온 세상에 떠벌릴 테니까, 그럼 너야말로 고소를 당할 거다.'"], ["If you proceed against him, I’ll publish it to all the world that he beat you for your dishonesty, and then you will be prosecuted.’ I call God to witness whose was the dishonesty and by whose commands I acted, wasn’t it by her own and Fyodor Pavlovitch’s?", "그 부정직이 누구의 것이었으며 누구의 명령으로 제가 그 짓을 했는지, 하느님을 증인으로 세워 묻고 싶습니다. 바로 그 여자와 표도르 파블로비치의 명령이 아니었습니까?"], ["‘And what’s more,’ she went on, ‘I’ll dismiss you for good and you’ll never earn another penny from me.", "'한 가지 더,' 그 여자가 말을 이었지요."], ["I’ll speak to my merchant too’ (that’s what she calls her old man) ‘and he will dismiss you!’ And if he dismisses me, what can I earn then from any one?", "'아주 영영 너를 내쫓을 테니 다시는 나한테서 한 푼도 못 벌 줄 알아라. 내 상인한테도 말해 둘 거다.'"], ["Those two are all I have to look to, for your Fyodor Pavlovitch has not only given over employing me, for another reason, but he means to make use of papers I’ve signed to go to law against me.", "(그 여자는 자기 영감을 그렇게 부른답니다.) '그럼 그이도 너를 내쫓을 테니까!' 만약 그 영감이 저를 내쫓으면, 그땐 제가 누구한테서 무엇을 벌 수 있겠습니까?"], ["And so I kept quiet, and you have seen our retreat.", "제가 기댈 데라곤 그 두 사람뿐입니다. 당신네 표도르 파블로비치께서는 다른 이유로 저를 부리는 일을 그만두셨을 뿐 아니라, 제가 서명한 서류를 가지고 저를 상대로 소송을 걸 작정이시니까요."], ["But now let me ask you: did Ilusha hurt your finger much?", "그래서 저는 잠자코 있었던 겁니다, 그리고 우리 피난처를 보신 거고요. 그런데 이제 제가 여쭙겠습니다."], ["I didn’t like to go into it in our mansion before him.”", "일류샤가 당신 손가락을 많이 다치게 했습니까? 그 애 앞에서는 우리 저택에서 그 이야기를 꺼내기가 싫었습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, very much, and he was in a great fury.", "\"네, 아주 심하게요, 그리고 그 애는 굉장히 격분해 있었습니다."], ["He was avenging you on me as a Karamazov, I see that now.", "그 애는 저를 카라마조프로 여기고 당신의 원수를 갚은 거지요, 이제 알겠습니다."], ["But if only you had seen how he was throwing stones at his school-fellows!", "하지만 그 애가 학교 친구들한테 돌을 던지는 걸 당신이 보셨더라면!"], ["It’s very dangerous.", "굉장히 위험합니다."], ["They might kill him.", "그 애를 죽일 수도 있어요."], ["They are children and stupid.", "다들 어린아이고 어리석으니까요."], ["A stone may be thrown and break somebody’s head.”", "돌이 날아가서 누군가의 머리를 깨뜨릴 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“That’s just what has happened.", "\"바로 그런 일이 벌어졌지요."], ["He has been bruised by a stone to-day.", "오늘 그 애가 돌에 맞아 멍이 들었습니다."], ["Not on the head but on the chest, just above the heart.", "머리는 아니고 가슴에, 심장 바로 위에요."], ["He came home crying and groaning and now he is ill.”", "울고 신음하며 집으로 돌아왔고, 지금은 앓아누워 있습니다.\""]], [["“And you know he attacks them first.", "\"그리고 아시겠지만 그 애가 먼저 공격합니다."], ["He is bitter against them on your account.", "당신 때문에 그 애가 그 애들한테 독이 올라 있는 거지요."], ["They say he stabbed a boy called Krassotkin with a pen-knife not long ago.”", "얼마 전에는 크라소트킨이라는 아이를 주머니칼로 찔렀다고들 하더군요.\""]], [["“I’ve heard about that too, it’s dangerous.", "\"그 이야기도 들었습니다, 위험한 일이지요."], ["Krassotkin is an official here, we may hear more about it.”", "크라소트킨은 여기 관리니까, 그 일로 무슨 말이 더 나올지도 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“I would advise you,”", "\"제가 권해 드리고 싶은 건,\""], ["Alyosha went on warmly, “not to send him to school at all for a time till he is calmer ...", "알료샤가 따뜻하게 말을 이었다. \"그 애가 좀 더 진정되고……"], ["and his anger is passed.”", "분노가 가라앉을 때까지 당분간은 아예 학교에 보내지 않으시는 게…….\""]], [["“Anger!”", "\"분노라!\""], ["the captain repeated, “that’s just what it is.", "이등 대위가 되뇌었다. \"바로 그겁니다."], ["He is a little creature, but it’s a mighty anger.", "그 애는 조그만 녀석이지만 그 분노만은 어마어마하지요. 나리, 당신은 모든 걸 알지 못하십니다."], ["You don’t know all, sir.", "더 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Let me tell you more.", "그 사건 이래로 아이들이 모두 '수세미'"], ["Since that incident all the boys have been teasing him about the ‘wisp of tow.’ Schoolboys are a merciless race, individually they are angels, but together, especially in schools, they are often merciless.", "운운하며 그 애를 놀려 댔습니다. 학생 녀석들이란 무자비한 족속이라, 하나하나 따로 보면 천사 같지만 한데 모이면, 특히 학교에서는 흔히 무자비해지지요."], ["Their teasing has stirred up a gallant spirit in Ilusha.", "그 녀석들의 놀림이 일류샤 안의 용맹한 정신을 들쑤셔 놓았습니다."], ["An ordinary boy, a weak son, would have submitted, have felt ashamed of his father, sir, but he stood up for his father against them all.", "보통 아이라면, 약한 아들이라면 굴복하고 제 아비를 부끄러워했겠지요, 나리. 하지만 그 애는 그 모든 녀석들에 맞서 제 아비를 위해 일어섰습니다."], ["For his father and for truth and justice.", "제 아비를 위해, 또 진실과 정의를 위해서요."], ["For what he suffered when he kissed your brother’s hand and cried to him ‘Forgive father, forgive him,’—that only God knows—and I, his father.", "그 애가 당신 형님의 손에 입 맞추며 '아빠를 용서해 줘요, 용서해 줘요'라고 외쳤을 때 그 애가 겪은 고통이 어떠했는지, 그것은 오직 하느님만이 아십니다."], ["For our children—not your children, but ours—the children of the poor gentlemen looked down upon by every one—know what justice means, sir, even at nine years old.", "그리고 그 애의 아비인 저만이 알지요. 우리 아이들은, 당신네 아이들이 아니라 우리 아이들, 모든 사람에게 멸시받는 가난한 신사들의 아이들은, 나리, 아홉 살 나이에도 정의가 무엇을 뜻하는지 안답니다."], ["How should the rich know?", "부자들이 어찌 그걸 알겠습니까?"], ["They don’t explore such depths once in their lives.", "그들은 평생 단 한 번도 그런 깊은 데를 들여다보지 않지요."], ["But at that moment in the square when he kissed his hand, at that moment my Ilusha had grasped all that justice means.", "하지만 광장에서 그 손에 입을 맞추던 그 순간, 그 순간 제 일류샤는 정의가 무엇을 뜻하는지를 죄다 깨우쳤습니다."], ["That truth entered into him and crushed him for ever, sir,”", "그 진실이 그 애 속으로 파고들어 그 애를 영영 짓눌러 버렸지요, 나리.\""], ["the captain said hotly again with a sort of frenzy, and he struck his right fist against his left palm as though he wanted to show how “the truth” crushed Ilusha.", "이등 대위는 다시 일종의 광란에 휩싸여 뜨겁게 말하며, 마치 '진실'이 어떻게 일류샤를 짓눌렀는지를 보여 주려는 듯 오른 주먹으로 왼 손바닥을 내리쳤다."], ["“That very day, sir, he fell ill with fever and was delirious all night.", "\"바로 그날, 나리, 그 애는 열병으로 앓아누워 밤새 헛소리를 했습니다."], ["All that day he hardly said a word to me, but I noticed he kept watching me from the corner, though he turned to the window and pretended to be learning his lessons.", "그날 온종일 저한테는 거의 한마디도 하지 않았습니다만, 그 애가 구석에서 줄곧 저를 지켜보고 있는 걸 알아챘지요. 창문 쪽으로 돌아앉아 공부를 하는 척했지만 말입니다."], ["But I could see his mind was not on his lessons.", "하지만 그 애의 마음이 공부에 있지 않다는 걸 저는 알 수 있었습니다."], ["Next day I got drunk to forget my troubles, sinful man as I am, and I don’t remember much.", "이튿날 저는 제 시름을 잊으려고 술에 취했지요, 저야 죄 많은 인간이니까요, 그래서 별로 기억나는 게 없습니다. 엄마도 울기 시작했고요."], ["Mamma began crying, too—I am very fond of mamma—well, I spent my last penny drowning my troubles.", "저는 엄마를 무척 아낍니다. 어쨌든 저는 제 시름을 술에 빠뜨리느라 마지막 한 푼까지 다 썼습니다."], ["Don’t despise me for that, sir, in Russia men who drink are the best.", "그 일로 저를 멸시하지는 마십시오, 나리, 러시아에서는 술 마시는 사람들이 가장 좋은 사람들이지요."], ["The best men amongst us are the greatest drunkards.", "우리네 가운데 가장 좋은 사람들이 가장 지독한 술꾼들이랍니다."], ["I lay down and I don’t remember about Ilusha, though all that day the boys had been jeering at him at school.", "저는 드러누웠고, 일류샤에 대해서는 아무것도 기억나지 않습니다, 그날 온종일 아이들이 학교에서 그 애를 비웃었는데도 말입니다. '수세미야,'"], ["‘Wisp of tow,’ they shouted, ‘your father was pulled out of the tavern by his wisp of tow, you ran by and begged forgiveness.’”", "그 녀석들이 소리쳤지요. '네 아비가 수세미를 잡혀 술집에서 끌려 나왔지, 그래서 너는 달려가서 용서를 빌었고.'\""]], [["“On the third day when he came back from school, I saw he looked pale and wretched. ‘What is it?’ I asked. He wouldn’t answer. Well, there’s no talking in our mansion without mamma and the girls taking part in it. What’s more, the girls had heard about it the very first day. Varvara had begun snarling. ‘You fools and buffoons, can you ever do anything rational?’ ‘Quite so,’ I said, ‘can we ever do anything rational?’ For the time I turned it off like that. So in the evening I took the boy out for a walk, for you must know we go for a walk every evening, always the same way, along which we are going now—from our gate to that great stone which lies alone in the road under the hurdle, which marks the beginning of the town pasture. A beautiful and lonely spot, sir. Ilusha and I walked along hand in hand as usual. He has a little hand, his fingers are thin and cold—he suffers with his chest, you know. ‘Father,’ said he, ‘father!’ ‘Well?’ said I. I saw his eyes flashing. ‘Father, how he treated you then!’ ‘It can’t be helped, Ilusha,’ I said. ‘Don’t forgive him, father, don’t forgive him! At school they say that he has paid you ten roubles for it.’ ‘No, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘I would not take money from him for anything.’ Then he began trembling all over, took my hand in both his and kissed it again. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘father, challenge him to a duel, at school they say you are a coward and won’t challenge him, and that you’ll accept ten roubles from him.’ ‘I can’t challenge him to a duel, Ilusha,’ I answered. And I told briefly what I’ve just told you. He listened. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘anyway don’t forgive it. When I grow up I’ll call him out myself and kill him.’ His eyes shone and glowed. And of course I am his father, and I had to put in a word: ‘It’s a sin to kill,’ I said, ‘even in a duel.’ ‘Father,’ he said, ‘when I grow up, I’ll knock him down, knock the sword out of his hand, I’ll fall on him, wave my sword over him and say: “I could kill you, but I forgive you, so there!”’ You see what the workings of his little mind have been during these two days; he must have been planning that vengeance all day, and raving about it at night.", "\"사흘째 되던 날 그 애가 학교에서 돌아왔을 때, 그 애의 얼굴이 창백하고 처참해 보이는 걸 알았습니다. '무슨 일이냐?' 제가 물었지요. 그 애는 대답하려 들지 않았습니다. 그런데 우리 저택에서는 엄마와 딸들이 끼어들지 않고서는 무슨 이야기도 할 수가 없지요. 게다가 딸들은 바로 그 첫날 그 일을 들어 알고 있었습니다. 바르바라가 으르렁대기 시작했지요. '바보 같고 어릿광대 같은 인간들, 도대체 이치에 맞는 짓이라곤 한 가지도 못 하는 거예요?' '그래, 그렇구나,' 제가 말했지요. '우리가 도대체 이치에 맞는 짓을 한 가지라도 할 수 있겠느냐?' 그때는 그렇게 얼버무리고 넘어갔습니다. 그래서 저녁이 되자 저는 그 애를 데리고 산책을 나갔지요, 아시다시피 우리는 저녁마다 산책을 하니까요, 늘 똑같은 길로, 지금 우리가 걷고 있는 이 길로요. 우리 집 대문에서 저기 길가 울타리 밑에 홀로 놓여 있는 저 커다란 돌까지인데, 그 돌이 마을 목초지가 시작되는 데를 표시하지요. 아름답고 한적한 곳입니다, 나리. 일류샤와 저는 여느 때처럼 손을 맞잡고 걸었습니다. 그 애 손은 조그맣고 손가락은 가늘고 차갑지요. 아시다시피 가슴앓이를 하니까요. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '아빠!' '왜 그러냐?' 제가 말했지요. 그 애의 두 눈이 번득이는 게 보였습니다. '아빠, 그 사람이 그때 아빠한테 어떻게 했어요!' '어쩔 수 없는 일이란다, 일류샤.' 제가 말했지요. '아빠, 그 사람을 용서하지 마요, 용서하지 마요! 학교에서 그러는데, 그 사람이 그 대가로 아빠한테 십 루블을 줬다던데요.' '아니다, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '나는 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테서 돈을 받지 않는다.' 그러자 그 애는 온몸을 떨기 시작하더니 제 손을 제 두 손으로 잡고 다시 입을 맞췄습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '아빠, 그 사람한테 결투를 신청해요, 학교에서 그러는데 아빠가 겁쟁이라 결투를 신청하지 못하고, 그 사람한테서 십 루블을 받을 거라던데요.' '나는 그 사람한테 결투를 신청할 수 없단다, 일류샤.' 제가 대답했지요. 그러고는 방금 당신께 말씀드린 것을 간단히 들려줬습니다. 그 애는 듣고 있었지요. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '그래도 어쨌든 그걸 용서하지는 마요. 제가 크면 직접 그 사람을 불러내서 죽여 버릴 거예요.' 그 애의 두 눈이 빛나며 이글거렸습니다. 물론 저는 그 애의 아비이니, 한마디 끼워 넣어야 했지요. '사람을 죽이는 건 죄란다,' 제가 말했지요. '결투에서라도 말이다.' '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '제가 크면 그 사람을 때려눕히고, 그 사람 손에서 칼을 쳐서 떨어뜨리고, 그 사람한테 덤벼들어 그 사람 위로 칼을 휘두르며 말할 거예요. \"내가 너를 죽일 수도 있지만 용서해 주마, 자, 봐라!\"' 그 애의 조그만 머릿속에서 이 이틀 동안 어떤 일들이 벌어졌는지 아시겠지요. 그 애는 온종일 그 복수를 궁리하고, 밤이면 그 일로 헛소리를 했던 게 틀림없습니다."]], [["“But he began to come home from school badly beaten, I found out about it the day before yesterday, and you are right, I won’t send him to that school any more. I heard that he was standing up against all the class alone and defying them all, that his heart was full of resentment, of bitterness—I was alarmed about him. We went for another walk. ‘Father,’ he asked, ‘are the rich people stronger than any one else on earth?’ ‘Yes, Ilusha,’ I said, ‘there are no people on earth stronger than the rich.’ ‘Father,’ he said, ‘I will get rich, I will become an officer and conquer everybody. The Tsar will reward me, I will come back here and then no one will dare—’ Then he was silent and his lips still kept trembling. ‘Father,’ he said, ‘what a horrid town this is.’ ‘Yes, Ilusha,’ I said, ‘it isn’t a very nice town.’ ‘Father, let us move into another town, a nice one,’ he said, ‘where people don’t know about us.’ ‘We will move, we will, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘only I must save up for it.’ I was glad to be able to turn his mind from painful thoughts, and we began to dream of how we would move to another town, how we would buy a horse and cart. ‘We will put mamma and your sisters inside, we will cover them up and we’ll walk, you shall have a lift now and then, and I’ll walk beside, for we must take care of our horse, we can’t all ride. That’s how we’ll go.’ He was enchanted at that, most of all at the thought of having a horse and driving him. For of course a Russian boy is born among horses. We chattered a long while. Thank God, I thought, I have diverted his mind and comforted him.", "\"그런데 그 애가 학교에서 호되게 얻어맞고 돌아오기 시작했습니다, 그제야 그 사실을 알았지요, 그래서 당신 말씀이 옳습니다, 다시는 그 애를 그 학교에 보내지 않겠습니다. 들어 보니 그 애가 온 학급에 홀로 맞서 그 녀석들 모두에게 대들었고, 그 애 가슴이 원한과 쓰라림으로 가득 차 있다는 거였습니다. 저는 그 애가 걱정스러웠지요. 우리는 또 산책을 나갔습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 물었지요. '부자들이 세상 누구보다도 더 힘이 세요?' '그렇단다, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '세상에 부자보다 더 힘센 사람은 없단다.' '아빠,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '저는 부자가 될 거예요, 장교가 되어서 모두를 정복할 거예요. 황제께서 저한테 상을 내리실 거고, 저는 여기로 돌아올 거예요, 그러면 아무도 감히…….' 그러고는 입을 다물었는데, 그 애 입술은 여전히 떨리고 있었습니다. '아빠,' 그 애가 말했지요. '이 동네는 정말 끔찍한 동네예요.' '그래, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '그리 좋은 동네는 못 되지.' '아빠, 우리 다른 동네로, 좋은 동네로 이사 가요,' 그 애가 말했습니다. '사람들이 우리를 모르는 데로요.' '이사 가자, 가고말고, 일류샤,' 제가 말했지요. '다만 그러려면 돈을 모아야 한단다.' 저는 그 애의 마음을 괴로운 생각에서 돌려놓을 수 있게 되어 기뻤고, 우리는 어떻게 다른 동네로 이사 갈지, 어떻게 말과 수레를 살지 꿈꾸기 시작했습니다. '엄마랑 네 누나들을 안에 태우고, 덮어 주고, 우리는 걷는 거다, 너는 가끔씩 태워 주고, 나는 옆에서 걷고 말이다, 우리 말을 아껴야 하니까 다 같이 탈 수는 없거든. 그렇게 가는 거란다.' 그 애는 거기에 푹 빠졌지요, 무엇보다도 말을 갖고 그걸 몬다는 생각에 말입니다. 물론 러시아 아이라면 말들 속에서 태어나는 법이니까요. 우리는 한참 동안 재잘거렸습니다. 하느님, 감사합니다, 저는 생각했지요, 제가 그 애의 마음을 돌려놓고 위로해 주었구나, 하고요."]], [["“That was the day before yesterday, in the evening, but last night everything was changed.", "\"그게 그제 저녁의 일이었습니다만, 어젯밤 모든 게 달라졌습니다."], ["He had gone to school in the morning, he came back depressed, terribly depressed.", "그 애는 아침에 학교에 갔다가 풀이 죽어서, 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어서 돌아왔지요. 저녁에 저는 그 애 손을 잡고 산책을 나갔습니다."], ["In the evening I took him by the hand and we went for a walk; he would not talk.", "그 애는 말을 하려 들지 않았지요. 바람이 불고 해는 없었고, 가을 기운이 느껴졌습니다."], ["There was a wind blowing and no sun, and a feeling of autumn; twilight was coming on.", "땅거미가 내리고 있었지요. 우리는 둘 다 풀이 죽은 채 걸었습니다."], ["We walked along, both of us depressed.", "'얘야,' 제가 말했지요."], ["‘Well, my boy,’ said I, ‘how about our setting off on our travels?’ I thought I might bring him back to our talk of the day before.", "'우리 여행을 떠나는 건 어떠냐?' 그 애를 어제 했던 이야기로 다시 끌어들일 수 있을까 싶어서요."], ["He didn’t answer, but I felt his fingers trembling in my hand.", "그 애는 대답하지 않았지만, 제 손 안에서 그 애의 손가락이 떨리는 게 느껴졌습니다."], ["Ah, I thought, it’s a bad job; there’s something fresh.", "아, 이거 큰일이구나, 무언가 새로 생긴 게 있구나, 저는 생각했지요."], ["We had reached the stone where we are now.", "우리는 지금 우리가 있는 이 돌까지 다다랐습니다."], ["I sat down on the stone.", "저는 돌 위에 앉았지요."], ["And in the air there were lots of kites flapping and whirling.", "그리고 하늘에는 펄럭이며 빙빙 도는 연이 잔뜩 떠 있었습니다."], ["There were as many as thirty in sight.", "눈에 보이는 것만 서른 개는 됐지요."], ["Of course, it’s just the season for the kites.", "물론 마침 연 날리는 철이니까요. '봐라, 일류샤,'"], ["‘Look, Ilusha,’ said I, ‘it’s time we got out our last year’s kite again.", "제가 말했지요. '우리도 작년 연을 다시 꺼낼 때가 됐구나."], ["I’ll mend it, where have you put it away?’ My boy made no answer.", "내가 고쳐 줄 테니, 어디다 치워 뒀느냐?' 그 애는 아무 대답이 없었습니다."], ["He looked away and turned sideways to me.", "그 애는 눈길을 돌리고 제게서 옆으로 비스듬히 돌아앉았지요."], ["And then a gust of wind blew up the sand.", "그러더니 한 줄기 바람이 모래를 불어 올렸습니다."], ["He suddenly fell on me, threw both his little arms round my neck and held me tight.", "그 애는 별안간 제게 쓰러지듯 안기며 두 팔로 제 목을 감고는 저를 꽉 붙들었습니다."], ["You know, when children are silent and proud, and try to keep back their tears when they are in great trouble and suddenly break down, their tears fall in streams.", "아시다시피, 아이들이 말없이 자존심을 부리며 큰 고통 속에서도 눈물을 억누르려 애쓰다가 갑자기 무너질 때, 그 눈물은 줄기를 이루어 흘러내리지요. 그 따뜻한 눈물 줄기로 그 애는 별안간 제 얼굴을 적셨습니다."], ["With those warm streams of tears, he suddenly wetted my face.", "그 애는 흐느끼며 마치 발작이라도 일어난 듯 떨었고, 돌 위에 앉은 제게 바짝 매달렸지요. '아빠,'"], ["He sobbed and shook as though he were in convulsions, and squeezed up against me as I sat on the stone.", "그 애는 계속 외쳤습니다. '아빠, 그 사람이 아빠를 얼마나 모욕했어요!'"], ["‘Father,’ he kept crying, ‘dear father, how he insulted you!’ And I sobbed too.", "그래서 저도 흐느꼈지요. 우리는 서로의 팔에 안겨 떨면서 앉아 있었습니다."], ["We sat shaking in each other’s arms.", "'일류샤,' 제가 그 애한테 말했지요."], ["‘Ilusha,’ I said to him, ‘Ilusha darling.’ No one saw us then.", "'일류샤, 내 사랑.' 그때 아무도 우리를 보지 못했습니다."], ["God alone saw us, I hope He will record it to my credit.", "오직 하느님만이 우리를 보셨지요, 하느님께서 그것을 제 공으로 적어 두시기를 바랍니다."], ["You must thank your brother, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "당신은 당신 형님께 감사드려야 합니다, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["No, sir, I won’t thrash my boy for your satisfaction.”", "아니지요, 나리, 저는 당신을 만족시켜 드리려고 제 아들을 매질하지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["He had gone back to his original tone of resentful buffoonery.", "그는 처음의 원한 어린 어릿광대 같은 어조로 되돌아가 있었다."], ["Alyosha felt though that he trusted him, and that if there had been some one else in his, Alyosha’s place, the man would not have spoken so openly and would not have told what he had just told.", "그래도 알료샤는 그가 자신을 믿고 있으며, 만약 자신, 곧 알료샤의 자리에 다른 누군가가 있었더라면 이 사내가 그토록 솔직하게 말하지도, 방금 말한 것을 들려주지도 않았으리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["This encouraged Alyosha, whose heart was trembling on the verge of tears.", "이것이 알료샤에게 용기를 주었으니, 그의 가슴은 눈물이 솟구치기 직전의 벼랑에서 떨리고 있었다."]], [["“Ah, how I would like to make friends with your boy!”", "\"아, 제가 당신 아드님과 어떻게든 친해지고 싶습니다!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“If you could arrange it—”", "\"그렇게 주선해 주실 수 있다면…….\""]], [["“Certainly, sir,”", "\"그야 물론이지요, 나리.\""], ["muttered the captain.", "이등 대위가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“But now listen to something quite different!”", "\"그런데 이제 전혀 다른 이야기를 들어 주십시오!\""], ["Alyosha went on.", "알료샤가 말을 이었다."], ["“I have a message for you.", "\"당신께 전할 말이 있습니다."], ["That same brother of mine, Dmitri, has insulted his betrothed, too, a noble-hearted girl of whom you have probably heard.", "바로 그 제 형 드미트리가 자기 약혼녀까지 모욕했습니다, 당신도 아마 들어 보셨을 고결한 마음씨를 지닌 아가씨지요. 저는 그분이 입은 부당함을 당신께 말씀드릴 권리가 있습니다, 아니 사실은 그래야 마땅하지요."], ["I have a right to tell you of her wrong; I ought to do so, in fact, for hearing of the insult done to you and learning all about your unfortunate position, she commissioned me at once—just now—to bring you this help from her—but only from her alone, not from Dmitri, who has abandoned her.", "당신이 당한 모욕에 대해 듣고 당신의 불행한 처지를 죄다 알게 되자, 그분은 당장, 바로 방금 저더러 자기가 보내는 이 도움을 당신께 전해 달라 부탁하셨으니까요. 다만 오직 그분 한 분에게서지, 그분을 저버린 드미트리에게서가 아닙니다."], ["Nor from me, his brother, nor from any one else, but from her, only from her!", "그분의 형제인 저에게서도, 그 누구에게서도 아니고, 오직 그분, 오직 그분에게서만입니다!"], ["She entreats you to accept her help....", "그분은 당신께 자기 도움을 받아 달라고 간청하고 계십니다……"], ["You have both been insulted by the same man.", "두 분은 같은 사람에게 모욕을 당하셨지요."], ["She thought of you only when she had just received a similar insult from him—similar in its cruelty, I mean.", "그분은 막 그 사람한테서 비슷한 모욕을, 잔혹함에서 비슷한 모욕을 당하고 나서야 비로소 당신을 떠올리셨습니다."], ["She comes like a sister to help a brother in misfortune....", "그분은 불행에 빠진 형제를 도우러 오는 누이처럼 오신 겁니다……"], ["She told me to persuade you to take these two hundred roubles from her, as from a sister, knowing that you are in such need.", "그분은 저더러 당신을 설득해, 누이에게서 받듯 이 이백 루블을 받아 달라 하셨습니다, 당신이 그토록 궁핍하다는 걸 아시니까요."], ["No one will know of it, it can give rise to no unjust slander.", "아무도 그 일을 알지 못할 테니, 부당한 비방이 생겨날 여지도 없습니다."], ["There are the two hundred roubles, and I swear you must take them unless—unless all men are to be enemies on earth!", "여기 이백 루블이 있습니다, 그리고 맹세컨대 당신은 이걸 받으셔야 합니다, 만약 그러지 않으신다면, 이 세상 모든 사람이 서로 적이 되어야 한다는 것이 아니라면 말입니다!"], ["But there are brothers even on earth....", "그러나 이 세상에도 형제는 있지요……"], ["You have a generous heart ...", "당신은 너그러운 마음을 지니셨습니다……"], ["you must see that, you must,”", "당신은 그걸 아셔야 합니다, 꼭 아셔야 해요.\""], ["and Alyosha held out two new rainbow-colored hundred-rouble notes.", "그러면서 알료샤는 무지갯빛 새 백 루블짜리 지폐 두 장을 내밀었다."]], [["They were both standing at the time by the great stone close to the fence, and there was no one near.", "그때 두 사람은 울타리 가까이 그 커다란 돌 옆에 서 있었고, 근처에는 아무도 없었다."], ["The notes seemed to produce a tremendous impression on the captain.", "그 지폐는 이등 대위에게 어마어마한 인상을 주는 듯했다."], ["He started, but at first only from astonishment.", "그는 흠칫했으나, 처음에는 그저 놀라움 때문이었다."], ["Such an outcome of their conversation was the last thing he expected.", "두 사람의 대화가 이런 결말에 이르리라고는 그가 가장 예상하지 못한 일이었다."], ["Nothing could have been farther from his dreams than help from any one—and such a sum!", "누군가에게서 도움을 받는다는 것은, 그것도 그런 액수를 받는다는 것은, 그의 꿈에서 더없이 멀리 떨어진 일이었던 것이다."]], [["He took the notes, and for a minute he was almost unable to answer, quite a new expression came into his face.", "그는 지폐를 받아 들었고, 한동안 거의 아무 대답도 하지 못했으니, 그의 얼굴에는 전혀 새로운 표정이 떠올랐다."]], [["“That for me?", "\"저한테 이걸요?"], ["So much money—two hundred roubles!", "이렇게 많은 돈을, 이백 루블이나!"], ["Good heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["Why, I haven’t seen so much money for the last four years!", "아니, 지난 사 년 동안 이렇게 많은 돈은 구경도 못 했는데!"], ["Mercy on us!", "우리에게 자비를 베푸시는군요!"], ["And she says she is a sister....", "그런데 그분이 누이라 하셨다고요……"], ["And is that the truth?”", "그게 사실입니까?\""]], [["“I swear that all I told you is the truth,”", "\"제가 말씀드린 건 죄다 사실이라고 맹세합니다.\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["The captain flushed red.", "이등 대위의 얼굴이 빨갛게 달아올랐다."]], [["“Listen, my dear, listen.", "\"들어 보십시오, 친애하는 분, 들어 보십시오."], ["If I take it, I shan’t be behaving like a scoundrel?", "제가 이걸 받으면, 악당처럼 구는 게 아니겠지요?"], ["In your eyes, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I shan’t be a scoundrel?", "당신 눈에, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 악당으로 보이지는 않겠지요?"], ["No, Alexey Fyodorovitch, listen, listen,”", "아니지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 들어 보십시오, 들어 보세요.\""], ["he hurried, touching Alyosha with both his hands.", "그는 두 손으로 알료샤를 만지며 서둘러 말했다."], ["“You are persuading me to take it, saying that it’s a sister sends it, but inwardly, in your heart won’t you feel contempt for me if I take it, eh?”", "\"당신은 저더러 이걸 받으라고 설득하면서 누이가 보내는 것이라 하시지만, 속으로는, 당신 마음속으로는 제가 이걸 받으면 저를 경멸하시지 않겠습니까, 예?\""]], [["“No, no, on my salvation I swear I shan’t!", "\"아니요, 아닙니다, 제 구원을 걸고 맹세컨대 그러지 않겠습니다!"], ["And no one will ever know but me—I, you and she, and one other lady, her great friend.”", "그리고 저 말고는 아무도 알지 못할 겁니다. 저와 당신과 그분, 그리고 그분의 절친한 벗인 다른 한 부인뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Never mind the lady!", "\"그 부인이야 아무래도 좋습니다!"], ["Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch, at a moment like this you must listen, for you can’t understand what these two hundred roubles mean to me now.”", "들어 보십시오, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 이런 순간에는 들어 주셔야 합니다, 당신은 이 이백 루블이 지금 저한테 어떤 의미인지 이해하실 수 없으니까요.\""], ["The poor fellow went on rising gradually into a sort of incoherent, almost wild enthusiasm.", "이 가엾은 사내는 차츰 일종의 두서없는, 거의 광적인 흥분으로 빠져들었다."], ["He was thrown off his balance and talked extremely fast, as though afraid he would not be allowed to say all he had to say.", "그는 평정을 잃고, 마치 하고 싶은 말을 다 하도록 허락받지 못할까 두려운 듯 몹시 빠르게 지껄였다."]], [["“Besides its being honestly acquired from a ‘sister,’ so highly respected and revered, do you know that now I can look after mamma and Nina, my hunchback angel daughter?", "\"그토록 존경받고 우러름받는 '누이'에게서 정직하게 받은 것이라는 점은 둘째 치고, 이제 제가 엄마와, 곱사등이 천사 같은 제 딸 니나를 돌볼 수 있다는 걸 아십니까?"], ["Doctor Herzenstube came to me in the kindness of his heart and was examining them both for a whole hour.", "헤르첸슈투베 박사가 친절한 마음에서 저를 찾아와 둘을 꼬박 한 시간이나 진찰해 주셨지요."], ["‘I can make nothing of it,’ said he, but he prescribed a mineral water which is kept at a chemist’s here.", "'도무지 알 수가 없군요.' 그분이 말씀하셨지만, 여기 약방에서 파는 광천수를 처방해 주셨습니다."], ["He said it would be sure to do her good, and he ordered baths, too, with some medicine in them.", "그게 틀림없이 그 애한테 좋을 거라 하시며, 무슨 약을 탄 목욕도 처방해 주셨고요."], ["The mineral water costs thirty copecks, and she’d need to drink forty bottles perhaps; so I took the prescription and laid it on the shelf under the ikons, and there it lies.", "광천수는 삼십 코페이카인데, 아마 마흔 병은 마셔야 할 겁니다. 그래서 저는 그 처방전을 받아다가 성상 아래 선반에 얹어 두었고, 거기 그대로 놓여 있지요."], ["And he ordered hot baths for Nina with something dissolved in them, morning and evening.", "그리고 니나에게는 무언가 녹인 물로 아침저녁 더운 목욕을 하라 하셨습니다."], ["But how can we carry out such a cure in our mansion, without servants, without help, without a bath, and without water?", "하지만 하인도 없고, 도와줄 사람도 없고, 욕조도 없고, 물도 없는 우리 저택에서 어떻게 그런 치료를 한단 말입니까?"], ["Nina is rheumatic all over, I don’t think I told you that.", "니나는 온몸이 류머티즘이지요, 그 이야긴 안 드린 것 같군요."], ["All her right side aches at night, she is in agony, and, would you believe it, the angel bears it without groaning for fear of waking us.", "밤이면 오른쪽 옆구리가 죄다 쑤셔서 그 애는 괴로워 몸부림칩니다, 그런데 믿어지십니까, 그 천사는 우리를 깨울까 봐 신음 소리 한 번 내지 않고 그걸 견딘답니다."], ["We eat what we can get, and she’ll only take the leavings, what you’d scarcely give to a dog.", "우리는 구할 수 있는 걸 먹는데, 그 애는 남은 찌꺼기만, 개한테도 좀처럼 주지 않을 만한 것만 먹으려 들지요."], ["‘I am not worth it, I am taking it from you, I am a burden on you,’ that’s what her angel eyes try to express.", "'저는 그럴 자격이 없어요, 저는 식구들 것을 뺏어 먹는 거예요, 저는 짐이에요.' 그 애의 천사 같은 눈은 그렇게 말하려고 합니다."], ["We wait on her, but she doesn’t like it.", "우리가 그 애 시중을 들면 그 애는 그걸 싫어하지요."], ["‘I am a useless cripple, no good to any one.’ As though she were not worth it, when she is the saving of all of us with her angelic sweetness.", "'저는 쓸모없는 불구라, 누구한테도 도움이 안 돼요.' 마치 그럴 자격이 없는 것처럼 말입니다, 그 애야말로 그 천사 같은 다정함으로 우리 모두를 구원하고 있는데도요."], ["Without her, without her gentle word it would be hell among us!", "그 애가 없다면, 그 애의 부드러운 말 한마디가 없다면 우리 사이는 지옥이 될 겁니다!"], ["She softens even Varvara.", "그 애는 바르바라마저 누그러뜨립니다."], ["And don’t judge Varvara harshly either, she is an angel too, she, too, has suffered wrong.", "그리고 바르바라도 모질게 보지는 말아 주십시오, 그 애도 천사니까요, 그 애 역시 부당한 일을 겪었지요."], ["She came to us for the summer, and she brought sixteen roubles she had earned by lessons and saved up, to go back with to Petersburg in September, that is now.", "그 애는 여름을 나러 우리한테 왔는데, 과외로 벌어 모은 십육 루블을 가지고 와서는, 구월에, 그러니까 지금쯤 그걸 가지고 페테르부르크로 돌아가려 했지요."], ["But we took her money and lived on it, so now she has nothing to go back with.", "그런데 우리가 그 애 돈을 받아 그걸로 먹고살았으니, 이제 그 애는 돌아갈 밑천이 없는 겁니다."], ["Though indeed she couldn’t go back, for she has to work for us like a slave.", "하기야 사실 그 애는 돌아갈 수도 없지요, 우리를 위해 노예처럼 일해야 하니까요."], ["She is like an overdriven horse with all of us on her back.", "그 애는 우리 모두를 등에 짊어진, 혹사당하는 말 같은 신세입니다."], ["She waits on us all, mends and washes, sweeps the floor, puts mamma to bed.", "그 애는 우리 모두 시중을 들고, 깁고 빨래하고, 마루를 쓸고, 엄마를 잠자리에 뉘지요."], ["And mamma is capricious and tearful and insane!", "그리고 엄마는 변덕스럽고 눈물 많고 제정신이 아니랍니다!"], ["And now I can get a servant with this money, you understand, Alexey Fyodorovitch, I can get medicines for the dear creatures, I can send my student to Petersburg, I can buy beef, I can feed them properly.", "그런데 이제 이 돈으로 하인을 둘 수 있고, 아시겠습니까, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그 사랑스러운 식구들에게 약을 구해 줄 수 있고, 제 여학생을 페테르부르크로 보낼 수 있고, 쇠고기를 살 수 있고, 그 애들을 제대로 먹일 수 있는 겁니다."], ["Good Lord, but it’s a dream!”", "맙소사, 이건 꿈만 같군요!\""]], [["Alyosha was delighted that he had brought him such happiness and that the poor fellow had consented to be made happy.", "알료샤는 자신이 그에게 이런 행복을 가져다주었다는 것과, 이 가엾은 사내가 행복해지기로 마음먹어 주었다는 것이 기뻤다."]], [["“Stay, Alexey Fyodorovitch, stay,”", "\"잠깐만요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 잠깐만요.\""], ["the captain began to talk with frenzied rapidity, carried away by a new day-dream.", "이등 대위는 새로운 백일몽에 휩쓸려 미친 듯이 빠른 속도로 지껄이기 시작했다."], ["“Do you know that Ilusha and I will perhaps really carry out our dream.", "\"아십니까, 일류샤와 저는 어쩌면 정말로 우리 꿈을 이룰지도 모릅니다."], ["We will buy a horse and cart, a black horse, he insists on its being black, and we will set off as we pretended the other day.", "우리는 말과 수레를, 검은 말을 살 겁니다, 그 애는 검은 말이어야 한다고 우기지요, 그리고 며칠 전에 흉내만 냈던 그대로 떠나는 겁니다."], ["I have an old friend, a lawyer in K. province, and I heard through a trustworthy man that if I were to go he’d give me a place as clerk in his office, so, who knows, maybe he would.", "저한테 오랜 벗이 하나 있는데, K현의 변호사지요, 그리고 믿을 만한 사람을 통해 들었는데, 제가 가기만 하면 자기 사무실에 서기 자리를 하나 주겠다더군요, 그러니 누가 압니까, 어쩌면 정말 그럴지도 모르지요."], ["So I’d just put mamma and Nina in the cart, and Ilusha could drive, and I’d walk, I’d walk....", "그러면 엄마와 니나를 수레에 태우고, 일류샤가 몰면, 저는 걷는 겁니다, 걷는 거지요……"], ["Why, if I only succeed in getting one debt paid that’s owing me, I should have perhaps enough for that too!”", "아니, 저한테 빚진 돈 한 가지만 받아 낼 수 있다면, 어쩌면 그것까지 할 만큼 돈이 모일지도 모릅니다!\""]], [["“There would be enough!”", "\"그것만으로도 충분할 겁니다!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."], ["“Katerina Ivanovna will send you as much more as you need, and you know, I have money too, take what you want, as you would from a brother, from a friend, you can give it back later....", "\"카테리나 이바노브나가 당신께 필요한 만큼 더 보내 드릴 테고, 그리고 아시다시피 저한테도 돈이 있으니, 필요한 만큼 가져가십시오, 형제에게서 받듯, 벗에게서 받듯이요, 나중에 갚으셔도 됩니다……"], ["(You’ll get rich, you’ll get rich!) And you know you couldn’t have a better idea than to move to another province!", "(당신은 부자가 될 겁니다, 부자가 될 거예요!) 그리고 아시다시피 다른 현으로 이사 가는 것보다 더 나은 생각은 없을 겁니다!"], ["It would be the saving of you, especially of your boy—and you ought to go quickly, before the winter, before the cold.", "그게 당신을, 특히 당신 아드님을 구하는 길일 테니까요. 그리고 서둘러 가셔야 합니다, 겨울이 오기 전에, 추위가 닥치기 전에요."], ["You must write to us when you are there, and we will always be brothers....", "거기 도착하시거든 우리한테 편지를 보내 주세요, 그러면 우리는 언제까지나 형제일 겁니다……"], ["No, it’s not a dream!”", "아니, 이건 꿈이 아니에요!\""]], [["Alyosha could have hugged him, he was so pleased.", "알료샤는 어찌나 기쁜지 그를 끌어안고 싶을 지경이었다."], ["But glancing at him he stopped short.", "그러나 그를 흘낏 보고는 멈칫했다."], ["The man was standing with his neck outstretched and his lips protruding, with a pale and frenzied face.", "사내는 목을 길게 빼고 입술을 쑥 내민 채, 창백하고 광기 어린 얼굴로 서 있었다."], ["His lips were moving as though trying to articulate something; no sound came, but still his lips moved.", "그의 입술은 무언가를 발음하려는 듯 움직이고 있었으나 아무 소리도 나지 않았고, 그래도 입술은 여전히 움직였다."], ["It was uncanny.", "섬뜩한 광경이었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""], ["asked Alyosha, startled.", "알료샤가 흠칫하며 물었다."]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch ...", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치…… 저는……"], ["I ... you,”", "당신을.\""], ["muttered the captain, faltering, looking at him with a strange, wild, fixed stare, and an air of desperate resolution.", "이등 대위가 더듬거리며 중얼거렸는데, 기이하고 사납고 못 박힌 듯한 눈길로, 절망적인 결의를 띤 기색으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["At the same time there was a sort of grin on his lips. “I ...", "그와 동시에 그의 입술에는 일종의 비웃음 같은 것이 어렸다."], ["you, sir ...", "\"저는……"], ["wouldn’t you like me to show you a little trick I know?”", "당신께, 나리…… 제가 아는 자그마한 재주를 하나 보여 드릴까요?\""], ["he murmured, suddenly, in a firm rapid whisper, his voice no longer faltering.", "그가 별안간, 더는 더듬지 않는 단호하고 빠른 속삭임으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“What trick?”", "\"무슨 재주요?\""]], [["“A pretty trick,”", "\"근사한 재주지요.\""], ["whispered the captain.", "이등 대위가 속삭였다."], ["His mouth was twisted on the left side, his left eye was screwed up.", "그의 입은 왼쪽으로 비뚤어졌고, 왼쪽 눈은 가늘게 찡그려졌다."], ["He still stared at Alyosha.", "그는 여전히 알료샤를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["“What is the matter?", "\"무슨 일입니까?"], ["What trick?”", "무슨 재주요?\""], ["Alyosha cried, now thoroughly alarmed.", "이제 완전히 겁에 질린 알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, look,”", "\"자, 보십시오.\""], ["squealed the captain suddenly, and showing him the two notes which he had been holding by one corner between his thumb and forefinger during the conversation, he crumpled them up savagely and squeezed them tight in his right hand.", "이등 대위가 별안간 새된 소리를 지르더니, 대화 내내 엄지와 검지로 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡고 있던 그 두 장의 지폐를 그에게 내보이고는, 사납게 구겨서 오른손에 꽉 움켜쥐었다."], ["“Do you see, do you see?”", "\"보이지요, 보이지요?\""], ["he shrieked, pale and infuriated.", "그가 창백하게 격분하여 비명을 질렀다."], ["And suddenly flinging up his hand, he threw the crumpled notes on the sand.", "그러더니 별안간 손을 치켜들어 그 구겨진 지폐를 모래밭에 내던졌다."], ["“Do you see?”", "\"보이지요?\""], ["he shrieked again, pointing to them.", "그가 그것을 가리키며 다시 비명을 질렀다."], ["“Look there!”", "\"저기 보세요!\""]], [["And with wild fury he began trampling them under his heel, gasping and exclaiming as he did so:", "그러고는 사나운 광기에 휩싸여 그것을 발뒤꿈치로 짓밟기 시작했고, 그러면서 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳐 댔다."]], [["“So much for your money!", "\"네 돈은 이런 거다!"], ["So much for your money!", "네 돈은 이런 거야!"], ["So much for your money!", "네 돈은 이런 거다!"], ["So much for your money!”", "네 돈은 이런 거야!\""]], [["Suddenly he darted back and drew himself up before Alyosha, and his whole figure expressed unutterable pride.", "별안간 그는 펄쩍 뒤로 물러나 알료샤 앞에 꼿꼿이 섰는데, 그의 온몸이 이루 말할 수 없는 자존심을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Tell those who sent you that the wisp of tow does not sell his honor,”", "\"당신을 보낸 사람들한테 전하시오, 수세미는 제 명예를 팔지 않는다고!\""], ["he cried, raising his arm in the air.", "그가 한 팔을 허공에 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["Then he turned quickly and began to run; but he had not run five steps before he turned completely round and kissed his hand to Alyosha.", "그러고는 휙 돌아서서 달리기 시작했으나, 다섯 걸음도 채 못 가서 완전히 몸을 돌려 알료샤를 향해 제 손에 입을 맞춰 보였다."], ["He ran another five paces and then turned round for the last time.", "그는 다시 다섯 걸음을 달리더니 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아섰다."], ["This time his face was not contorted with laughter, but quivering all over with tears.", "이번에 그의 얼굴은 웃음으로 일그러진 것이 아니라, 온통 눈물로 떨고 있었다."], ["In a tearful, faltering, sobbing voice he cried:", "그는 눈물에 젖어 더듬거리며 흐느끼는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“What should I say to my boy if I took money from you for our shame?”", "\"우리가 당한 치욕의 대가로 당신한테서 돈을 받는다면, 제 아들한테 뭐라고 말하란 말이오?\""]], [["And then he ran on without turning.", "그러고는 그는 돌아보지 않고 달려가 버렸다."], ["Alyosha looked after him, inexpressibly grieved.", "알료샤는 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔에 잠겨 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."], ["Oh, he saw that till the very last moment the man had not known he would crumple up and fling away the notes.", "아, 그는 보았다, 이 사내가 마지막 순간까지도 자신이 그 지폐를 구겨서 내던지리라는 것을 알지 못했음을."], ["He did not turn back.", "그는 돌아보지 않았다."], ["Alyosha knew he would not. He would not follow him and call him back, he knew why.", "알료샤는 그가 돌아보지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["When he was out of sight, Alyosha picked up the two notes.", "그는 그를 뒤쫓아 가서 불러 세우지 않을 작정이었으니, 왜 그런지도 알고 있었다."], ["They were very much crushed and crumpled, and had been pressed into the sand, but were uninjured and even rustled like new ones when Alyosha unfolded them and smoothed them out.", "그가 시야에서 사라지자, 알료샤는 그 두 장의 지폐를 주워 들었다. 지폐는 몹시 짓이겨지고 구겨진 데다 모래 속에 짓눌려 있었으나, 상한 데는 없었고 알료샤가 펴서 매만지니 새것처럼 바스락 소리까지 났다."], ["After smoothing them out, he folded them up, put them in his pocket and went to Katerina Ivanovna to report on the success of her commission.", "그는 그것을 다 펴고 나서 접어 주머니에 넣고는, 그분의 심부름을 잘 해냈는지 보고하러 카테리나 이바노브나를 찾아갔다."]], [["# Book V. Pro And Contra Chapter I. The Engagement", "# Book V. Pro And Contra Chapter I. The Engagement"]], [["Madame Hohlakov was again the first to meet Alyosha.", "호흘라코바 부인이 또다시 알료샤를 가장 먼저 맞이했다."], ["She was flustered; something important had happened.", "그녀는 안절부절못하고 있었으니, 무언가 중대한 일이 벌어진 모양이었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna’s hysterics had ended in a fainting fit, and then “a terrible, awful weakness had followed, she lay with her eyes turned up and was delirious.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 발작은 까무러침으로 끝이 났고, 그러더니 \"끔찍하고 무서운 탈진 상태가 뒤따라, 두 눈을 위로 치켜뜬 채 누워 헛소리를 했다."], ["Now she was in a fever.", "지금은 열이 펄펄 끓고 있다."], ["They had sent for Herzenstube; they had sent for the aunts.", "게르첸슈투베를 부르러 사람을 보냈고, 고모들도 부르러 사람을 보냈다."], ["The aunts were already here, but Herzenstube had not yet come.", "고모들은 벌써 와 있었지만 게르첸슈투베는 아직 오지 않았다."], ["They were all sitting in her room, waiting.", "다들 그녀의 방에 둘러앉아 기다리고 있다."], ["She was unconscious now, and what if it turned to brain fever!”", "지금은 의식이 없는데, 혹시 뇌염으로 번지기라도 한다면!\""]], [["Madame Hohlakov looked gravely alarmed.", "호흘라코바 부인은 자못 심각하게 겁에 질린 표정이었다."], ["“This is serious, serious,”", "\"이건 보통 일이 아니에요, 보통 일이 아니라고요.\""], ["she added at every word, as though nothing that had happened to her before had been serious.", "그녀는 마치 그전에 자기에게 일어난 어떤 일도 결코 보통 일이 아니었던 적이 없다는 듯, 말끝마다 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["Alyosha listened with distress, and was beginning to describe his adventures, but she interrupted him at the first words.", "알료샤는 괴로운 마음으로 귀를 기울였고, 자기가 겪은 일을 이야기하려 했으나, 그녀는 첫마디부터 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["She had not time to listen.", "그녀에게는 들을 겨를이 없었다."], ["She begged him to sit with Lise and wait for her there.", "그녀는 그에게 리즈 곁에 앉아 자기를 기다려 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["“Lise,”", "\"리즈가요.\""], ["she whispered almost in his ear, “Lise has greatly surprised me just now, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "그녀는 거의 그의 귀에 대고 속삭였다. \"리즈가 방금 저를 깜짝 놀라게 했어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["She touched me, too, and so my heart forgives her everything.", "그 애가 제 마음을 움직여서, 제 마음은 그 애의 모든 걸 용서하고 있답니다."], ["Only fancy, as soon as you had gone, she began to be truly remorseful for having laughed at you to-day and yesterday, though she was not laughing at you, but only joking.", "글쎄, 당신이 가시자마자 그 애가 오늘이랑 어제 당신을 비웃은 걸 두고 진심으로 뉘우치기 시작했지 뭐예요, 사실 비웃은 게 아니라 그저 농담을 한 것뿐인데도요."], ["But she was seriously sorry for it, almost ready to cry, so that I was quite surprised.", "그런데 그걸 진지하게 미안해하면서 금방이라도 울 것처럼 굴어서 저는 정말 놀랐답니다."], ["She has never been really sorry for laughing at me, but has only made a joke of it.", "그 애는 저를 비웃은 일로는 단 한 번도 진심으로 미안해한 적이 없고, 그저 농으로 넘겼거든요."], ["And you know she is laughing at me every minute.", "게다가 그 애는 시도 때도 없이 저를 비웃는답니다."], ["But this time she was in earnest.", "그런데 이번에는 진심이었어요."], ["She thinks a great deal of your opinion, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and don’t take offense or be wounded by her if you can help it.", "그 애는 당신 의견을 무척 소중히 여기니까, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 되도록이면 그 애한테 마음 상하거나 기분 다치지 말아 주세요."], ["I am never hard upon her, for she’s such a clever little thing.", "저는 그 애한테 결코 엄하게 굴지 못한답니다, 어찌나 영리한 아인지요."], ["Would you believe it?", "글쎄 믿으시겠어요?"], ["She said just now that you were a friend of her childhood, ‘the greatest friend of her childhood’—just think of that—‘greatest friend’—and what about me?", "그 애가 방금 당신을 두고 자기 어린 시절의 친구라고, '어린 시절 가장 소중한 친구'라고 하지 뭐예요—생각해 보세요—'가장 소중한 친구'라니—그럼 저는 뭐가 되나요?"], ["She has very strong feelings and memories, and, what’s more, she uses these phrases, most unexpected words, which come out all of a sudden when you least expect them.", "그 애는 감정도 추억도 아주 강한 데다, 게다가 이렇게 뜻밖의 말들을, 가장 예상치 못한 순간에 불쑥 튀어나오는 그런 표현들을 쓴답니다."], ["She spoke lately about a pine-tree, for instance: there used to be a pine-tree standing in our garden in her early childhood.", "얼마 전엔 가령 소나무 이야기를 했어요. 그 애가 아주 어렸을 적 우리 정원에 소나무 한 그루가 서 있었거든요."], ["Very likely it’s standing there still; so there’s no need to speak in the past tense.", "아마 지금도 거기 서 있을 거예요. 그러니 과거형으로 말할 필요는 없겠지요."], ["Pine-trees are not like people, Alexey Fyodorovitch, they don’t change quickly.", "소나무는 사람과 달라서요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 빨리 변하질 않으니까요. '엄마,'"], ["‘Mamma,’ she said, ‘I remember this pine-tree as in a dream,’ only she said something so original about it that I can’t repeat it.", "그 애가 그러더군요, '나는 이 소나무를 꿈속처럼 기억해.' 그러면서 어찌나 기발한 말을 덧붙였는지 저는 그대로 옮길 수가 없네요."], ["Besides, I’ve forgotten it.", "게다가 잊어버리기도 했고요."], ["Well, good-by!", "자, 그럼 안녕히!"], ["I am so worried I feel I shall go out of my mind.", "저는 너무 걱정이 되어서 제정신이 나갈 것만 같아요."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, I’ve been out of my mind twice in my life.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 평생에 두 번이나 정신이 나간 적이 있답니다."], ["Go to Lise, cheer her up, as you always can so charmingly.", "리즈한테 가서 그 애 기운을 북돋아 주세요, 당신은 늘 그렇게 사랑스럽게 해 주시잖아요."], ["Lise,”", "리즈.\""], ["she cried, going to her door, “here I’ve brought you Alexey Fyodorovitch, whom you insulted so.", "그녀는 딸의 방문으로 다가가며 소리쳤다. \"네가 그렇게 모욕했던 알렉세이 표도로비치를 데려왔단다."], ["He is not at all angry, I assure you; on the contrary, he is surprised that you could suppose so.”", "이분은 조금도 화가 나지 않으셨어, 정말이야. 오히려 네가 그렇게 생각할 수 있다는 데 놀라고 계신단다.\""]], [["“Merci, maman.", "\"메르시, 마망."], ["Come in, Alexey Fyodorovitch.”", "들어오세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""]], [["Alyosha went in.", "알료샤가 들어갔다."], ["Lise looked rather embarrassed, and at once flushed crimson.", "리즈는 다소 당황한 기색이더니 이내 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["She was evidently ashamed of something, and, as people always do in such cases, she began immediately talking of other things, as though they were of absorbing interest to her at the moment.", "무언가가 부끄러웠던 게 분명했고, 그런 경우에 사람들이 으레 그러듯, 그녀는 곧장 다른 이야기를, 마치 지금 이 순간 자기에게 더없이 흥미진진한 일인 양 떠벌리기 시작했다."]], [["“Mamma has just told me all about the two hundred roubles, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and your taking them to that poor officer ...", "\"엄마가 방금 이백 루블 이야기를 다 해 주셨어요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신이 그 가엾은 장교한테 그 돈을 가져갔다는 거랑요……"], ["and she told me all the awful story of how he had been insulted ...", "그리고 그 사람이 어떻게 모욕을 당했는지 그 끔찍한 사연도 다 말씀해 주셨고요……"], ["and you know, although mamma muddles things ...", "그런데 있잖아요, 엄마가 이야기를 좀 뒤죽박죽 섞으시긴 해도……"], ["she always rushes from one thing to another ...", "이것저것 마구 건너뛰시거든요……"], ["I cried when I heard.", "저는 듣다가 울었어요."], ["Well, did you give him the money and how is that poor man getting on?”", "그래서, 그 사람한테 돈을 주셨어요? 그 가엾은 사람은 어떻게 지내나요?\""]], [["“The fact is I didn’t give it to him, and it’s a long story,”", "\"실은 그 사람한테 돈을 주지 못했어요, 그게 긴 이야기랍니다.\""], ["answered Alyosha, as though he, too, could think of nothing but his regret at having failed, yet Lise saw perfectly well that he, too, looked away, and that he, too, was trying to talk of other things.", "알료샤는 마치 자기 역시 실패했다는 후회 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수 없다는 듯 대답했으나, 리즈는 그 역시 시선을 돌리고 있다는 것, 그 역시 다른 이야기를 하려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다."]], [["Alyosha sat down to the table and began to tell his story, but at the first words he lost his embarrassment and gained the whole of Lise’s attention as well.", "알료샤는 탁자 앞에 앉아 이야기를 시작했는데, 첫마디를 떼자마자 당혹감을 떨쳐 버리고 리즈의 온 주의마저 사로잡았다."], ["He spoke with deep feeling, under the influence of the strong impression he had just received, and he succeeded in telling his story well and circumstantially.", "그는 방금 받은 강렬한 인상에 사로잡혀 깊은 감정을 담아 이야기했고, 자기 이야기를 잘, 그리고 자초지종을 빠짐없이 들려주는 데 성공했다."], ["In old days in Moscow he had been fond of coming to Lise and describing to her what had just happened to him, what he had read, or what he remembered of his childhood.", "모스크바 시절 그는 리즈를 찾아와 자기에게 방금 일어난 일이며 읽은 것이며 어린 시절의 기억을 들려주기를 즐겼다."], ["Sometimes they had made day-dreams and woven whole romances together—generally cheerful and amusing ones.", "때로는 둘이서 함께 공상에 잠겨 온갖 이야기를 엮어 내기도 했는데, 대개는 밝고 즐거운 것들이었다."], ["Now they both felt suddenly transported to the old days in Moscow, two years before.", "이제 두 사람은 문득 이 년 전 모스크바 시절로 되돌아간 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["Lise was extremely touched by his story.", "리즈는 그의 이야기에 몹시 감동했다."], ["Alyosha described Ilusha with warm feeling.", "알료샤는 일류샤를 따뜻한 마음으로 묘사했다."], ["When he finished describing how the luckless man trampled on the money, Lise could not help clasping her hands and crying out:", "그 불운한 사내가 돈을 짓밟던 대목까지 다 이야기하자, 리즈는 두 손을 마주 잡고 외치지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“So you didn’t give him the money!", "\"그래서 그 사람한테 돈을 안 주신 거예요!"], ["So you let him run away!", "그래서 그 사람을 그냥 달아나게 두셨다고요!"], ["Oh, dear, you ought to have run after him!”", "아, 저런, 그 사람을 뒤쫓아 갔어야죠!\""]], [["“No, Lise; it’s better I didn’t run after him,”", "\"아니에요, 리즈. 뒤쫓아 가지 않은 게 더 나아요.\""], ["said Alyosha, getting up from his chair and walking thoughtfully across the room.", "알료샤는 의자에서 일어나 생각에 잠긴 채 방을 가로질러 거닐며 말했다."]], [["“How so?", "\"어째서요?"], ["How is it better?", "어째서 더 낫다는 거예요?"], ["Now they are without food and their case is hopeless?”", "이제 그 사람들은 먹을 것도 없고 처지가 절망적인데요?\""]], [["“Not hopeless, for the two hundred roubles will still come to them.", "\"절망적이지 않아요, 이백 루블은 결국 그 사람들 손에 들어갈 테니까요."], ["He’ll take the money to-morrow.", "내일이면 그 사람이 그 돈을 받을 거예요."], ["To-morrow he will be sure to take it,”", "내일이면 틀림없이 받을 겁니다.\""], ["said Alyosha, pacing up and down, pondering.", "알료샤는 생각에 잠겨 이리저리 거닐며 말했다."], ["“You see, Lise,”", "\"보세요, 리즈.\""], ["he went on, stopping suddenly before her, “I made one blunder, but that, even that, is all for the best.”", "그는 갑자기 그녀 앞에 멈춰 서며 말을 이었다. \"내가 실수를 하나 저질렀어요, 그런데 그 실수마저도, 그 실수마저도 다 잘된 일이랍니다.\""]], [["“What blunder, and why is it for the best?”", "\"무슨 실수인데요, 그리고 왜 잘된 일이라는 거죠?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you.", "\"말씀드릴게요."], ["He is a man of weak and timorous character; he has suffered so much and is very good-natured.", "그 사람은 마음이 약하고 겁이 많은 사람이에요. 그렇게 많은 고생을 했으면서도 무척 선량하답니다."], ["I keep wondering why he took offense so suddenly, for I assure you, up to the last minute, he did not know that he was going to trample on the notes.", "나는 그 사람이 왜 그렇게 갑작스레 모욕감을 느꼈는지 자꾸만 생각하게 돼요, 정말이지 마지막 순간까지도 그 사람은 자기가 그 지폐를 짓밟으리라고는 몰랐거든요."], ["And I think now that there was a great deal to offend him ...", "그리고 지금 생각해 보니 그 사람을 상하게 할 만한 일이 한두 가지가 아니었어요……"], ["and it could not have been otherwise in his position....", "그 사람 처지에서는 달리 될 수도 없었고요……"], ["To begin with, he was sore at having been so glad of the money in my presence and not having concealed it from me.", "우선 그 사람은 내 앞에서 그 돈을 그토록 기뻐하면서 그 기쁨을 나한테 감추지 못했다는 게 마음에 걸렸던 거예요."], ["If he had been pleased, but not so much; if he had not shown it; if he had begun affecting scruples and difficulties, as other people do when they take money, he might still endure to take it.", "기뻐는 하되 그렇게까지는 아니었더라면, 그걸 내색하지 않았더라면, 다른 사람들이 돈을 받을 때 으레 그러듯 짐짓 사양하는 척, 곤란한 척이라도 했더라면, 그래도 그 돈을 받아 챙길 수 있었을지 몰라요."], ["But he was too genuinely delighted, and that was mortifying.", "그런데 그 사람은 너무도 진심으로 기뻐했고, 바로 그게 굴욕스러웠던 거죠."], ["Ah, Lise, he is a good and truthful man—that’s the worst of the whole business.", "아, 리즈, 그 사람은 선하고 진실한 사람이에요—그게 바로 이 모든 일에서 가장 딱한 점이랍니다."], ["All the while he talked, his voice was so weak, so broken, he talked so fast, so fast, he kept laughing such a laugh, or perhaps he was crying—yes, I am sure he was crying, he was so delighted—and he talked about his daughters—and about the situation he could get in another town....", "이야기하는 내내 그 사람 목소리는 어찌나 가냘프고 갈라지던지, 말도 어찌나 빠르고 빠르던지, 그렇게 줄곧 웃어 댔는데, 어쩌면 우는 것이었는지도—그래요, 그 사람은 분명 울고 있었어요, 그토록 기뻐서요—그리고 자기 딸들 이야기며—다른 고장에서 얻을 수 있을 일자리 이야기까지 했답니다……"], ["And when he had poured out his heart, he felt ashamed at having shown me his inmost soul like that.", "그렇게 마음을 다 쏟아 내고 나니, 나한테 자기 속마음을 그렇게 다 드러내 보인 게 부끄러워진 거예요."], ["So he began to hate me at once.", "그래서 단번에 나를 미워하기 시작한 거죠."], ["He is one of those awfully sensitive poor people.", "그 사람은 끔찍이도 예민한 가난한 사람들 가운데 하나예요."], ["What had made him feel most ashamed was that he had given in too soon and accepted me as a friend, you see.", "그 사람을 가장 부끄럽게 만든 건, 너무 빨리 마음을 내주고 나를 친구로 받아들였다는 거였어요, 아시겠어요."], ["At first he almost flew at me and tried to intimidate me, but as soon as he saw the money he had begun embracing me; he kept touching me with his hands.", "처음에 그 사람은 나한테 거의 덤벼들 듯 위협하려 들었는데, 돈을 보자마자 나를 끌어안기 시작했어요. 자꾸만 두 손으로 나를 만졌죠."], ["This must have been how he came to feel it all so humiliating, and then I made that blunder, a very important one.", "바로 그래서 이 모든 게 그토록 굴욕스럽게 느껴졌던 게 틀림없어요, 그러다 내가 그 실수를, 아주 중대한 실수를 저질렀어요."], ["I suddenly said to him that if he had not money enough to move to another town, we would give it to him, and, indeed, I myself would give him as much as he wanted out of my own money.", "만약 다른 고장으로 옮길 만큼 돈이 넉넉지 않으면 우리가 마련해 주겠노라고, 게다가 나 자신이 내 돈에서 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 주겠노라고 불쑥 말해 버렸거든요. 그 말이 단번에 그 사람을 찔렀어요."], ["That struck him all at once. Why, he thought, did I put myself forward to help him?", "어째서, 하고 그 사람은 생각했겠죠, 내가 나서서 자기를 도우려 드는 건가 하고요."], ["You know, Lise, it’s awfully hard for a man who has been injured, when other people look at him as though they were his benefactors....", "아세요, 리즈, 상처 입은 사람한테는, 다른 사람들이 자기를 마치 은인이라도 되는 양 바라보는 게 끔찍이도 힘든 일이거든요……"], ["I’ve heard that; Father Zossima told me so.", "저는 그런 말을 들은 적이 있어요, 조시마 장로님이 그렇게 말씀해 주셨죠."], ["I don’t know how to put it, but I have often seen it myself.", "어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠지만, 저 자신도 그런 경우를 자주 봐 왔어요."], ["And I feel like that myself, too.", "그리고 저 역시 그런 기분이 들고요."], ["And the worst of it was that though he did not know, up to the very last minute, that he would trample on the notes, he had a kind of presentiment of it, I am sure of that.", "그런데 무엇보다 딱한 건, 그 사람이 마지막 순간까지도 자기가 지폐를 짓밟으리라는 걸 몰랐으면서도 어쩐지 그걸 예감하고 있었다는 거예요, 저는 확신해요."], ["That’s just what made him so ecstatic, that he had that presentiment....", "바로 그 예감이 그 사람을 그토록 황홀하게 만든 거죠……"], ["And though it’s so dreadful, it’s all for the best.", "그리고 비록 이건 끔찍한 일이지만, 다 잘된 일이랍니다."], ["In fact, I believe nothing better could have happened.”", "사실 저는 이보다 더 나은 결말은 없었으리라 믿어요.\""]], [["“Why, why could nothing better have happened?”", "\"왜요, 왜 이보다 더 나은 결말이 없었다는 거죠?\""], ["cried Lise, looking with great surprise at Alyosha.", "리즈가 알료샤를 몹시 놀란 눈으로 바라보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Because if he had taken the money, in an hour after getting home, he would be crying with mortification, that’s just what would have happened.", "\"왜냐하면 만약 그 사람이 그 돈을 받았더라면, 집에 돌아가 한 시간도 안 되어 굴욕감에 울고 있었을 테니까요, 꼭 그렇게 됐을 거예요."], ["And most likely he would have come to me early to-morrow, and perhaps have flung the notes at me and trampled upon them as he did just now.", "그리고 십중팔구 내일 일찍 나한테 찾아와서, 어쩌면 방금처럼 지폐를 내 얼굴에 내던지고 짓밟았겠죠."], ["But now he has gone home awfully proud and triumphant, though he knows he has ‘ruined himself.’ So now nothing could be easier than to make him accept the two hundred roubles by to-morrow, for he has already vindicated his honor, tossed away the money, and trampled it under foot....", "그런데 지금은 그 사람이 비록 자기가 '제 신세를 망쳐 버렸다'는 걸 알면서도, 끔찍이도 자랑스럽고 의기양양하게 집으로 돌아갔어요. 그러니 이제 내일이면 그 사람한테 이백 루블을 받아들이게 하는 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없을 거예요, 그 사람은 이미 제 명예를 지켜 냈고, 돈을 내팽개쳤고, 발밑에 짓밟아 버렸으니까요……"], ["He couldn’t know when he did it that I should bring it to him again to-morrow, and yet he is in terrible need of that money.", "그 사람은 그러면서도 내일 내가 그 돈을 다시 가져가리라는 건 몰랐을 테고, 그러면서도 그 돈이 절실히 필요하답니다."], ["Though he is proud of himself now, yet even to-day he’ll be thinking what a help he has lost.", "지금은 비록 자기 자신이 자랑스럽겠지만, 오늘 당장에도 자기가 얼마나 큰 도움을 잃었는지 생각하게 될 거예요."], ["He will think of it more than ever at night, will dream of it, and by to-morrow morning he may be ready to run to me to ask forgiveness.", "밤이 되면 그 어느 때보다도 그 생각이 더하겠죠, 꿈에서까지 그 생각을 할 테고요, 그러다 내일 아침이면 나한테 달려와 용서를 빌 채비가 되어 있을지도 몰라요. 바로 그때 내가 나타나는 거죠."], ["It’s just then that I’ll appear.", "'자, 당신은 자존심 강한 사람이오.' 하고 내가 말하는 거예요."], ["‘Here, you are a proud man,’ I shall say: ‘you have shown it; but now take the money and forgive us!’ And then he will take it!”", "'그걸 보여 줬으니, 이제는 이 돈을 받고 우리를 용서해 주시오!' 그러면 그 사람은 받을 거예요!\""]], [["Alyosha was carried away with joy as he uttered his last words, “And then he will take it!”", "마지막 말을 내뱉을 때 알료샤는 기쁨에 도취되어 있었다. \"그러면 그 사람은 받을 거예요!\""], ["Lise clapped her hands.", "리즈가 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["“Ah, that’s true!", "\"아, 정말 그래요!"], ["I understand that perfectly now.", "이제 완전히 이해했어요."], ["Ah, Alyosha, how do you know all this?", "아, 알료샤, 어떻게 이런 걸 다 아세요?"], ["So young and yet he knows what’s in the heart....", "이렇게 젊은데도 사람 마음속에 뭐가 들었는지 다 아시네요……"], ["I should never have worked it out.”", "저라면 결코 거기까지 헤아리지 못했을 거예요.\""]], [["“The great thing now is to persuade him that he is on an equal footing with us, in spite of his taking money from us,”", "\"이제 무엇보다 중요한 건, 우리한테서 돈을 받더라도 그 사람이 우리와 똑같은 처지에 있다는 걸 납득시키는 거예요.\""], ["Alyosha went on in his excitement, “and not only on an equal, but even on a higher footing.”", "알료샤는 흥분하여 말을 이었다. \"그저 똑같은 처지일 뿐 아니라, 오히려 더 높은 처지에 있다는 걸요.\""]], [["“‘On a higher footing’ is charming, Alexey Fyodorovitch; but go on, go on!”", "\"'더 높은 처지'라니, 참 멋진 말이네요, 알렉세이 표도로비치. 계속하세요, 계속요!\""]], [["“You mean there isn’t such an expression as ‘on a higher footing’; but that doesn’t matter because—”", "\"'더 높은 처지'라는 표현 같은 건 없다는 뜻이군요. 하지만 그건 상관없어요, 왜냐하면—\""]], [["“Oh, no, of course it doesn’t matter.", "\"아, 아니에요, 물론 상관없죠."], ["Forgive me, Alyosha, dear....", "용서하세요, 알료샤……"], ["You know, I scarcely respected you till now—that is I respected you but on an equal footing; but now I shall begin to respect you on a higher footing.", "있잖아요, 저는 지금까지 당신을 거의 존경하지 않았어요—그러니까 존경하긴 했지만 똑같은 처지에서 존경했던 거예요. 그런데 이제부터는 더 높은 처지에서 당신을 존경하기 시작할 거예요."], ["Don’t be angry, dear, at my joking,”", "화내지 마세요, 제가 농담한다고요.\""], ["she put in at once, with strong feeling.", "그녀는 곧 강한 감정을 담아 덧붙였다."], ["“I am absurd and small, but you, you!", "\"저는 우습고 하찮은 사람인데, 당신은, 당신은요!"], ["Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["Isn’t there in all our analysis—I mean your analysis ...", "우리의 이 모든 분석에는—그러니까 당신의 분석에는……"], ["no, better call it ours—aren’t we showing contempt for him, for that poor man—in analyzing his soul like this, as it were, from above, eh?", "아니, 차라리 우리의 것이라고 하는 게 낫겠네요—우리가 그 사람을, 그 가엾은 사람을 경멸하고 있는 건 아닐까요—그 사람의 영혼을 이렇게, 말하자면 위에서 내려다보며 분석하고 있으니 말이에요, 네?"], ["In deciding so certainly that he will take the money?”", "그 사람이 돈을 받으리라고 그토록 확신에 차서 단정해 버리는 게요?\""]], [["“No, Lise, it’s not contempt,”", "\"아니에요, 리즈, 그건 경멸이 아니에요.\""], ["Alyosha answered, as though he had prepared himself for the question.", "알료샤는 마치 그 물음에 미리 대비라도 한 듯 대답했다."], ["“I was thinking of that on the way here.", "\"저도 여기 오는 길에 그 생각을 했어요."], ["How can it be contempt when we are all like him, when we are all just the same as he is?", "우리가 다 그 사람과 같은데, 우리가 다 그 사람과 똑같은데, 어떻게 그게 경멸일 수 있겠어요?"], ["For you know we are just the same, no better.", "우린 정말로 똑같으니까요, 조금도 더 낫지 않아요."], ["If we are better, we should have been just the same in his place....", "설령 더 낫다 해도, 그 사람의 처지에 놓였더라면 우리도 똑같았을 거예요……"], ["I don’t know about you, Lise, but I consider that I have a sordid soul in many ways, and his soul is not sordid; on the contrary, full of fine feeling....", "리즈, 당신은 어떤지 모르겠지만, 저는 제가 여러모로 비루한 영혼을 지녔다고 여겨요, 그런데 그 사람의 영혼은 비루하지 않아요. 오히려 고운 감정으로 가득 차 있죠……"], ["No, Lise, I have no contempt for him.", "아니에요, 리즈, 저는 그 사람을 경멸하지 않아요."], ["Do you know, Lise, my elder told me once to care for most people exactly as one would for children, and for some of them as one would for the sick in hospitals.”", "있잖아요, 리즈, 장로님께서 언젠가 제게 말씀하시기를, 대부분의 사람들은 꼭 아이를 돌보듯 보살피고, 더러는 병원의 환자를 돌보듯 보살피라고 하셨어요.\""]], [["“Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, dear, let us care for people as we would for the sick!”", "\"아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 우리도 환자를 돌보듯 사람들을 보살펴요!\""]], [["“Let us, Lise; I am ready.", "\"그렇게 해요, 리즈. 저도 그럴 마음이 있어요."], ["Though I am not altogether ready in myself.", "비록 제 자신부터가 온전히 준비된 건 아니지만요."], ["I am sometimes very impatient and at other times I don’t see things.", "저는 어떤 땐 몹시 조바심을 내고, 또 어떤 땐 일을 제대로 보지 못해요."], ["It’s different with you.”", "당신은 다르겠지만요.\""]], [["“Ah, I don’t believe it!", "\"아, 그렇지 않아요!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, how happy I am!”", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 저는 정말 행복해요!\""]], [["“I am so glad you say so, Lise.”", "\"그렇게 말해 주시니 정말 기뻐요, 리즈.\""]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch, you are wonderfully good, but you are sometimes sort of formal....", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 놀랍도록 선한 분이지만, 가끔은 어딘가 딱딱하세요……"], ["And yet you are not a bit formal really.", "그런데 사실은 조금도 딱딱하지 않으시고요."], ["Go to the door, open it gently, and see whether mamma is listening,”", "문가로 가서 살그머니 문을 열고, 엄마가 엿듣고 있는지 좀 봐 주세요.\""], ["said Lise, in a nervous, hurried whisper.", "리즈가 초조하고 다급한 목소리로 속삭였다."]], [["Alyosha went, opened the door, and reported that no one was listening.", "알료샤는 가서 문을 열어 보고는 아무도 엿듣고 있지 않다고 알렸다."]], [["“Come here, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"이리 오세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["Lise went on, flushing redder and redder.", "리즈가 점점 더 빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 이었다."], ["“Give me your hand—that’s right.", "\"손을 주세요—그래요, 됐어요."], ["I have to make a great confession, I didn’t write to you yesterday in joke, but in earnest,”", "저, 큰 고백을 해야겠어요, 어제 당신한테 쓴 편지는 농담이 아니라 진심이었어요.\""], ["and she hid her eyes with her hand.", "그러고는 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다."], ["It was evident that she was greatly ashamed of the confession.", "그 고백이 몹시도 부끄러운 게 분명했다."]], [["Suddenly she snatched his hand and impulsively kissed it three times.", "별안간 그녀는 그의 손을 와락 붙잡더니 충동적으로 세 번이나 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Ah, Lise, what a good thing!”", "\"아, 리즈, 정말 잘됐어요!\""], ["cried Alyosha joyfully.", "알료샤가 기쁘게 외쳤다."], ["“You know, I was perfectly sure you were in earnest.”", "\"있잖아요, 저는 당신이 진심이라는 걸 진작부터 확신하고 있었거든요.\""]], [["“Sure? Upon my word!”", "\"확신했다고요?"], ["She put aside his hand, but did not leave go of it, blushing hotly, and laughing a little happy laugh.", "어쩜!\" 그녀는 그의 손을 옆으로 밀어냈으나 놓지는 않은 채, 화끈하게 얼굴을 붉히며 행복한 웃음을 조금 흘렸다."], ["“I kiss his hand and he says, ‘What a good thing!’”", "\"내가 손에 입을 맞췄더니 '정말 잘됐어요'라고 하시다니.\""]], [["But her reproach was undeserved.", "그러나 그 책망은 부당한 것이었다."], ["Alyosha, too, was greatly overcome.", "알료샤 역시 가슴이 벅차올랐던 것이다."]], [["“I should like to please you always, Lise, but I don’t know how to do it,”", "\"저는 늘 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶은데, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""], ["he muttered, blushing too.", "그도 얼굴을 붉히며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Alyosha, dear, you are cold and rude.", "\"알료샤, 당신은 차갑고 무뚝뚝해요."], ["Do you see?", "보세요?"], ["He has chosen me as his wife and is quite settled about it.", "이분은 저를 아내로 골라 놓고는 그걸 아주 당연한 일로 여기고 계세요."], ["He is sure I was in earnest.", "제가 진심이었다고 확신하셨다네요."], ["What a thing to say!", "그런 말이 어디 있어요!"], ["Why, that’s impertinence—that’s what it is.”", "아니, 그건 무례한 거예요—바로 그거라고요.\""]], [["“Why, was it wrong of me to feel sure?”", "\"아니, 제가 확신한 게 잘못이었나요?\""], ["Alyosha asked, laughing suddenly.", "알료샤가 별안간 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“Ah, Alyosha, on the contrary, it was delightfully right,”", "\"아, 알료샤, 오히려 정반대로, 더없이 옳았어요.\""], ["cried Lise, looking tenderly and happily at him.", "리즈가 다정하고 행복한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 외쳤다."]], [["Alyosha stood still, holding her hand in his.", "알료샤는 그녀의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채 가만히 서 있었다."], ["Suddenly he stooped down and kissed her on her lips.", "별안간 그는 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Oh, what are you doing?”", "\"어머, 뭐 하시는 거예요?\""], ["cried Lise.", "리즈가 외쳤다."], ["Alyosha was terribly abashed.", "알료샤는 몹시 당황했다."]], [["“Oh, forgive me if I shouldn’t....", "\"아, 제가 그래선 안 됐다면 용서하세요……"], ["Perhaps I’m awfully stupid....", "아마 제가 끔찍이 어리석은가 봐요……"], ["You said I was cold, so I kissed you....", "당신이 제가 차갑다고 하셔서, 그래서 입을 맞춘 건데……"], ["But I see it was stupid.”", "어리석은 짓이었군요.\""]], [["Lise laughed, and hid her face in her hands.", "리즈는 웃음을 터뜨리며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["“And in that dress!”", "\"게다가 그런 옷차림으로!\""], ["she ejaculated in the midst of her mirth.", "그녀가 웃음 속에서 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["But she suddenly ceased laughing and became serious, almost stern.", "그러나 별안간 웃음을 그치더니 진지해졌고, 거의 엄숙해졌다."]], [["“Alyosha, we must put off kissing.", "\"알료샤, 우리 입맞춤은 미뤄야 해요."], ["We are not ready for that yet, and we shall have a long time to wait,”", "우린 아직 그럴 준비가 안 됐고, 한참을 더 기다려야 할 거예요.\""], ["she ended suddenly.", "그녀가 갑자기 말을 맺었다."], ["“Tell me rather why you who are so clever, so intellectual, so observant, choose a little idiot, an invalid like me?", "\"그보다 말해 주세요, 당신처럼 영리하고, 지성 있고, 통찰력 있는 분이 어째서 저 같은 못난 바보, 환자를 고르시는 거죠?"], ["Ah, Alyosha, I am awfully happy, for I don’t deserve you a bit.”", "아, 알료샤, 저는 끔찍이도 행복해요, 저는 당신을 조금도 받을 자격이 없으니까요.\""]], [["“You do, Lise.", "\"당신은 자격이 있어요, 리즈."], ["I shall be leaving the monastery altogether in a few days.", "저는 며칠 안으로 수도원을 아주 떠날 거예요."], ["If I go into the world, I must marry.", "세상으로 나간다면 결혼해야 해요."], ["I know that.", "저는 그걸 알아요."], ["He told me to marry, too.", "그분도 결혼하라고 하셨고요."], ["Whom could I marry better than you—and who would have me except you?", "당신보다 더 나은 사람과 어떻게 결혼할 수 있겠어요—그리고 당신 말고 누가 저를 받아 주겠어요?"], ["I have been thinking it over.", "저는 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요."], ["In the first place, you’ve known me from a child and you’ve a great many qualities I haven’t.", "우선 당신은 어릴 적부터 저를 알았고, 제게 없는 좋은 점을 아주 많이 지녔어요."], ["You are more light-hearted than I am; above all, you are more innocent than I am.", "당신은 저보다 마음이 밝고, 무엇보다 저보다 순수해요."], ["I have been brought into contact with many, many things already....", "저는 벌써 너무도, 너무도 많은 일과 부딪쳐 왔거든요……"], ["Ah, you don’t know, but I, too, am a Karamazov.", "아, 당신은 모르겠지만, 저 역시 카라마조프랍니다."], ["What does it matter if you do laugh and make jokes, and at me, too?", "당신이 웃고 농담을 한들, 그것도 저를 두고 한들, 그게 무슨 상관이겠어요?"], ["Go on laughing.", "계속 웃으세요."], ["I am so glad you do.", "저는 당신이 웃어서 무척 기쁜걸요."], ["You laugh like a little child, but you think like a martyr.”", "당신은 어린아이처럼 웃지만, 순교자처럼 생각하니까요.\""]], [["“Like a martyr?", "\"순교자처럼요?"], ["How?”", "어떻게요?\""]], [["“Yes, Lise, your question just now: whether we weren’t showing contempt for that poor man by dissecting his soul—that was the question of a sufferer....", "\"네, 리즈, 방금 당신이 던진 물음 말이에요. 우리가 그 가엾은 사람의 영혼을 해부하면서 그 사람을 경멸하고 있는 건 아니냐던 그 물음—그건 고통받아 본 사람의 물음이에요……"], ["You see, I don’t know how to express it, but any one who thinks of such questions is capable of suffering.", "있잖아요, 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠지만, 그런 물음을 떠올리는 사람은 누구든 고통을 겪을 줄 아는 사람이에요."], ["Sitting in your invalid chair you must have thought over many things already.”", "환자용 의자에 앉아 있는 동안 당신은 이미 많은 것을 깊이 생각해 봤을 거예요.\""]], [["“Alyosha, give me your hand.", "\"알료샤, 손을 주세요."], ["Why are you taking it away?”", "왜 손을 빼시는 거예요?\""], ["murmured Lise in a failing voice, weak with happiness.", "리즈가 행복에 겨워 힘없이 잦아드는 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["“Listen, Alyosha.", "\"들어 보세요, 알료샤."], ["What will you wear when you come out of the monastery?", "수도원에서 나오면 무얼 입으실 거예요? 어떤 양복을요?"], ["What sort of suit? Don’t laugh, don’t be angry, it’s very, very important to me.”", "웃지 마세요, 화내지도 마시고요, 이건 저한테 정말, 정말 중요한 일이거든요.\""]], [["“I haven’t thought about the suit, Lise; but I’ll wear whatever you like.”", "\"양복은 생각해 보지 않았어요, 리즈. 하지만 당신이 좋아하는 걸로 뭐든 입을게요.\""]], [["“I should like you to have a dark blue velvet coat, a white piqué waistcoat, and a soft gray felt hat....", "\"저는 당신이 짙은 청색 벨벳 코트에, 하얀 피케 조끼에, 부드러운 회색 펠트 모자를 쓰면 좋겠어요……"], ["Tell me, did you believe that I didn’t care for you when I said I didn’t mean what I wrote?”", "말해 주세요, 제가 편지에 쓴 게 진심이 아니라고 했을 때, 정말로 제가 당신을 마음에 두지 않았다고 믿으셨어요?\""]], [["“No, I didn’t believe it.”", "\"아니요, 믿지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Oh, you insupportable person, you are incorrigible.”", "\"어머, 정말 못 말리는 분이네요, 구제불능이세요.\""]], [["“You see, I knew that you—seemed to care for me, but I pretended to believe that you didn’t care for me to make it—easier for you.”", "\"있잖아요, 저는 당신이—저를 마음에 두고 있는 것 같다는 걸 알고 있었지만, 일부러 당신이 저를 마음에 두지 않는다고 믿는 척한 거예요—당신 마음이 한결 편해지도록요.\""]], [["“That makes it worse!", "\"그건 더 나빠요!"], ["Worse and better than all!", "그 무엇보다 더 나쁘고 또 더 좋아요!"], ["Alyosha, I am awfully fond of you.", "알료샤, 저는 당신이 끔찍이 좋아요."], ["Just before you came this morning, I tried my fortune.", "오늘 아침 당신이 오기 직전에 저는 제 운을 점쳐 봤어요."], ["I decided I would ask you for my letter, and if you brought it out calmly and gave it to me (as might have been expected from you) it would mean that you did not love me at all, that you felt nothing, and were simply a stupid boy, good for nothing, and that I am ruined.", "당신한테 제 편지를 돌려 달라고 청하기로 마음먹었는데, 만약 당신이 (당신답게) 태연히 그걸 꺼내 제게 건네준다면, 그건 당신이 저를 조금도 사랑하지 않는다는 뜻, 아무 감정도 없고 그저 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 어리석은 소년에 지나지 않는다는 뜻이고, 저는 끝장이라는 뜻이었어요."], ["But you left the letter at home and that cheered me.", "그런데 당신이 편지를 집에 두고 오신 덕에 저는 기운이 났답니다."], ["You left it behind on purpose, so as not to give it back, because you knew I would ask for it?", "일부러 두고 오신 거죠, 돌려주지 않으려고, 제가 돌려 달라고 청할 줄 아셨으니까요?"], ["That was it, wasn’t it?”", "그런 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Ah, Lise, it was not so a bit.", "\"아, 리즈, 그런 게 조금도 아니에요."], ["The letter is with me now, and it was this morning, in this pocket.", "편지는 지금 제게 있어요, 오늘 아침에도 바로 이 주머니에 있었고요."], ["Here it is.”", "자, 여기 있어요.\""]], [["Alyosha pulled the letter out laughing, and showed it her at a distance.", "알료샤는 웃으며 편지를 꺼내 멀찍이서 그녀에게 보여 주었다."]], [["“But I am not going to give it to you.", "\"하지만 돌려주지는 않을 거예요."], ["Look at it from here.”", "여기서 보기만 하세요.\""]], [["“Why, then you told a lie?", "\"아니, 그럼 거짓말을 하신 거네요?"], ["You, a monk, told a lie!”", "수도사인 당신이 거짓말을 하다니!\""]], [["“I told a lie if you like,”", "\"정 그렇게 말씀하신다면, 거짓말을 한 거죠.\""], ["Alyosha laughed, too.", "알료샤도 웃었다."], ["“I told a lie so as not to give you back the letter.", "\"당신한테 편지를 돌려주지 않으려고 거짓말을 한 거예요."], ["It’s very precious to me,”", "이건 제게 무척 소중하거든요.\""], ["he added suddenly, with strong feeling, and again he flushed.", "그가 강한 감정을 담아 별안간 덧붙이며 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“It always will be, and I won’t give it up to any one!”", "\"앞으로도 늘 그럴 거고, 그 누구한테도 내주지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["Lise looked at him joyfully.", "리즈는 기쁜 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["“Alyosha,”", "\"알료샤.\""], ["she murmured again, “look at the door.", "그녀가 다시 중얼거렸다. \"문 좀 봐 주세요."], ["Isn’t mamma listening?”", "엄마가 엿듣고 있지 않나요?\""]], [["“Very well, Lise, I’ll look; but wouldn’t it be better not to look?", "\"좋아요, 리즈, 봐 드릴게요. 하지만 안 보는 게 낫지 않을까요?"], ["Why suspect your mother of such meanness?”", "어머님을 그런 비열한 짓이나 할 분이라고 의심할 게 뭐예요?\""]], [["“What meanness?", "\"비열한 짓이라뇨?"], ["As for her spying on her daughter, it’s her right, it’s not meanness!”", "딸을 몰래 엿보는 건 엄마의 권리예요, 비열한 짓이 아니라고요!\""], ["cried Lise, firing up.", "리즈가 발끈하며 외쳤다."], ["“You may be sure, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that when I am a mother, if I have a daughter like myself I shall certainly spy on her!”", "\"분명히 말씀드리는데요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 제가 엄마가 되어서 저 같은 딸을 두게 된다면, 저는 틀림없이 그 애를 몰래 엿볼 거예요!\""]], [["“Really, Lise?", "\"정말이에요, 리즈?"], ["That’s not right.”", "그건 옳지 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh, my goodness!", "\"어머, 세상에!"], ["What has meanness to do with it?", "비열한 짓이 거기서 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["If she were listening to some ordinary worldly conversation, it would be meanness, but when her own daughter is shut up with a young man....", "만약 엄마가 무슨 흔해 빠진 세속의 잡담이나 엿듣는다면 그건 비열한 짓이겠지만, 자기 딸이 젊은 남자와 단둘이 방에 틀어박혀 있는데……"], ["Listen, Alyosha, do you know I shall spy upon you as soon as we are married, and let me tell you I shall open all your letters and read them, so you may as well be prepared.”", "들어 보세요, 알료샤, 있잖아요, 저는 우리가 결혼하자마자 당신을 몰래 엿볼 거예요, 그리고 분명히 말해 두는데 당신 편지를 죄다 뜯어서 읽어 볼 거예요, 그러니 미리 단단히 각오해 두세요.\""]], [["“Yes, of course, if so—”", "\"네, 물론, 정 그러시다면—\""], ["muttered Alyosha, “only it’s not right.”", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다. \"다만 그건 옳지 않아요.\""]], [["“Ah, how contemptuous!", "\"아, 어쩜 그렇게 사람을 깔보세요!"], ["Alyosha, dear, we won’t quarrel the very first day.", "알료샤, 우리 결혼 첫날부터 다투진 말아요."], ["I’d better tell you the whole truth.", "차라리 사실대로 다 말씀드릴게요."], ["Of course, it’s very wrong to spy on people, and, of course, I am not right and you are, only I shall spy on you all the same.”", "물론 남을 몰래 엿보는 건 아주 나쁜 짓이고, 물론 제가 틀렸고 당신이 옳지만, 그래도 저는 당신을 몰래 엿볼 거예요.\""]], [["“Do, then; you won’t find out anything,”", "\"그럼 그렇게 하세요, 아무것도 알아내지 못할 테니까요.\""], ["laughed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 웃었다."]], [["“And, Alyosha, will you give in to me?", "\"그리고 알료샤, 제 말에 양보해 주실 거예요?"], ["We must decide that too.”", "그것도 정해 둬야 해요.\""]], [["“I shall be delighted to, Lise, and certain to, only not in the most important things.", "\"기꺼이 그럴 거예요, 리즈, 틀림없이요, 다만 가장 중요한 일에서는 아니에요."], ["Even if you don’t agree with me, I shall do my duty in the most important things.”", "설령 당신이 제 뜻에 동의하지 않더라도, 저는 가장 중요한 일에서는 제 본분을 다할 거예요.\""]], [["“That’s right; but let me tell you I am ready to give in to you not only in the most important matters, but in everything.", "\"그게 옳아요. 하지만 분명히 말씀드리는데 저는 가장 중요한 일에서뿐 아니라, 모든 일에서 당신한테 양보할 마음이 있어요."], ["And I am ready to vow to do so now—in everything, and for all my life!”", "그리고 지금 당장 그러기로 맹세할 수도 있어요—모든 일에서, 제 평생토록요!\""], ["cried Lise fervently, “and I’ll do it gladly, gladly!", "리즈가 열렬히 외쳤다. \"그것도 기꺼이, 기꺼이 할 거예요!"], ["What’s more, I’ll swear never to spy on you, never once, never to read one of your letters.", "게다가 저는 결코 당신을 몰래 엿보지 않겠다고, 단 한 번도, 당신 편지를 단 한 통도 읽지 않겠다고 맹세하겠어요."], ["For you are right and I am not.", "당신이 옳고 제가 틀렸으니까요."], ["And though I shall be awfully tempted to spy, I know that I won’t do it since you consider it dishonorable.", "그리고 비록 몰래 엿보고 싶은 유혹에 끔찍이 시달리겠지만, 당신이 그걸 떳떳지 못한 일로 여기시니 저는 그러지 않으리라는 걸 알아요."], ["You are my conscience now....", "이제 당신은 제 양심이니까요……"], ["Listen, Alexey Fyodorovitch, why have you been so sad lately—both yesterday and to-day?", "들어 보세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신은 요즘 왜 그렇게 슬퍼 보이세요—어제도 오늘도요?"], ["I know you have a lot of anxiety and trouble, but I see you have some special grief besides, some secret one, perhaps?”", "당신한테 걱정거리와 골칫거리가 많다는 건 저도 알지만, 그것 말고도 무슨 특별한 슬픔이, 어쩌면 무슨 남모를 슬픔이 있는 것 같아 보여요.\""]], [["“Yes, Lise, I have a secret one, too,”", "\"네, 리즈, 저한테도 남모를 슬픔이 있어요.\""], ["answered Alyosha mournfully.", "알료샤가 침울하게 대답했다."], ["“I see you love me, since you guessed that.”", "\"당신이 그걸 알아챈 걸 보니, 저를 사랑하시는군요.\""]], [["“What grief?", "\"무슨 슬픔인데요?"], ["What about?", "무엇에 관한 거예요?"], ["Can you tell me?”", "제게 말해 주실 수 있어요?\""], ["asked Lise with timid entreaty.", "리즈가 머뭇거리며 애원하듯 물었다."]], [["“I’ll tell you later, Lise—afterwards,”", "\"나중에 말씀드릴게요, 리즈—나중에요.\""], ["said Alyosha, confused.", "알료샤가 멋쩍어하며 말했다."], ["“Now you wouldn’t understand it perhaps—and perhaps I couldn’t explain it.”", "\"지금은 당신이 이해하지 못할지도 몰라요—그리고 어쩌면 저도 그걸 설명하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“I know your brothers and your father are worrying you, too.”", "\"당신 형님들이랑 아버님도 당신을 괴롭히고 있다는 걸 알아요.\""]], [["“Yes, my brothers too,”", "\"네, 형님들도요.\""], ["murmured Alyosha, pondering.", "알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I don’t like your brother Ivan, Alyosha,”", "\"저는 당신 형님 이반이 싫어요, 알료샤.\""], ["said Lise suddenly.", "리즈가 별안간 말했다."]], [["He noticed this remark with some surprise, but did not answer it.", "그는 다소 놀라며 이 말에 주목했으나, 대꾸하지는 않았다."]], [["“My brothers are destroying themselves,”", "\"제 형님들은 스스로를 파멸시키고 있어요.\""], ["he went on, “my father, too.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"아버지도 그렇고요."], ["And they are destroying others with them.", "그러면서 다른 사람들까지 함께 파멸시키고 있죠."], ["It’s ‘the primitive force of the Karamazovs,’ as Father Païssy said the other day, a crude, unbridled, earthly force.", "그건 며칠 전 파이시 신부님이 말씀하셨듯이 '카라마조프 가의 원초적인 힘'이에요, 거칠고, 고삐 풀린, 땅에 속한 힘이죠."], ["Does the spirit of God move above that force?", "하느님의 영이 그 힘 위에서 움직이실까요?"], ["Even that I don’t know.", "그조차 저는 모르겠어요."], ["I only know that I, too, am a Karamazov....", "저는 다만 저 역시 카라마조프라는 것만 알 뿐이에요……"], ["Me a monk, a monk!", "제가 수도사라니, 수도사라니!"], ["Am I a monk, Lise?", "제가 수도사인가요, 리즈?"], ["You said just now that I was.”", "방금 당신이 그랬잖아요, 제가 수도사라고요.\""]], [["“Yes, I did.”", "\"네, 그랬어요.\""]], [["“And perhaps I don’t even believe in God.”", "\"그런데 어쩌면 저는 하느님조차 믿지 않는지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“You don’t believe?", "\"믿지 않으신다고요?"], ["What is the matter?”", "무슨 일이세요?\""], ["said Lise quietly and gently.", "리즈가 조용하고 부드럽게 말했다."], ["But Alyosha did not answer.", "그러나 알료샤는 대답하지 않았다."], ["There was something too mysterious, too subjective in these last words of his, perhaps obscure to himself, but yet torturing him.", "이 마지막 말 속에는 너무도 신비롭고, 너무도 주관적인 무언가가 있었으니, 어쩌면 자기 자신에게도 모호하면서도 그를 괴롭히는 것이었다."]], [["“And now on the top of it all, my friend, the best man in the world, is going, is leaving the earth!", "\"그런데 이제 그 모든 일에 더하여, 제 벗이자 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분이 떠나려 하세요, 이 땅을 떠나려 하신다고요!"], ["If you knew, Lise, how bound up in soul I am with him!", "리즈, 제 영혼이 그분과 얼마나 깊이 맺어져 있는지 당신이 안다면!"], ["And then I shall be left alone....", "그러면 저는 홀로 남겨질 거예요……"], ["I shall come to you, Lise....", "저는 당신한테 올게요, 리즈……"], ["For the future we will be together.”", "앞으로 우리는 함께할 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, together, together!", "\"네, 함께해요, 함께!"], ["Henceforward we shall be always together, all our lives!", "앞으로 우리는 늘 함께할 거예요, 평생토록요!"], ["Listen, kiss me, I allow you.”", "들어 보세요, 입을 맞춰 주세요, 제가 허락할게요.\""]], [["Alyosha kissed her.", "알료샤는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Come, now go.", "\"자, 이제 가 보세요."], ["Christ be with you!”", "그리스도께서 함께하시길!\""], ["and she made the sign of the cross over him.", "그러고는 그를 향해 성호를 그어 주었다."], ["“Make haste back to him while he is alive.", "\"그분이 살아 계실 동안 어서 그분께 돌아가세요."], ["I see I’ve kept you cruelly.", "제가 당신을 너무 모질게 붙들었네요."], ["I’ll pray to-day for him and you.", "오늘 그분과 당신을 위해 기도할게요."], ["Alyosha, we shall be happy!", "알료샤, 우리는 행복해질 거예요!"], ["Shall we be happy, shall we?”", "우리 행복해질까요, 그렇겠죠?\""]], [["“I believe we shall, Lise.”", "\"그러리라 믿어요, 리즈.\""]], [["Alyosha thought it better not to go in to Madame Hohlakov and was going out of the house without saying good-by to her.", "알료샤는 호흘라코바 부인에게는 들어가지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 그녀에게 작별 인사도 하지 않고 집을 나서려 했다."], ["But no sooner had he opened the door than he found Madame Hohlakov standing before him.", "그러나 문을 열기가 무섭게 그는 자기 앞에 호흘라코바 부인이 서 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["From the first word Alyosha guessed that she had been waiting on purpose to meet him.", "알료샤는 첫마디부터 그녀가 일부러 자기를 만나려고 기다리고 있었음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch, this is awful.", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치, 이건 끔찍한 일이에요."], ["This is all childish nonsense and ridiculous.", "이건 죄다 어린애 같은 헛소리고 우스꽝스러운 짓이에요."], ["I trust you won’t dream—It’s foolishness, nothing but foolishness!”", "부디 당신은 꿈에도 생각하지—이건 어리석은 짓이에요, 어리석은 짓일 뿐이라고요!\""], ["she said, attacking him at once.", "그녀는 대뜸 그를 몰아붙였다."]], [["“Only don’t tell her that,”", "\"다만 그 애한테는 그런 말씀을 하지 마세요.\""], ["said Alyosha, “or she will be upset, and that’s bad for her now.”", "알료샤가 말했다. \"안 그러면 그 애가 속상해할 텐데, 지금 그 애한테는 그게 안 좋으니까요.\""]], [["“Sensible advice from a sensible young man.", "\"분별 있는 젊은이답게 분별 있는 충고로군요."], ["Am I to understand that you only agreed with her from compassion for her invalid state, because you didn’t want to irritate her by contradiction?”", "그렇다면 당신은 그저 그 애의 병약한 처지를 가엾이 여겨서, 반박해서 그 애를 자극하고 싶지 않아서 맞장구를 쳐 준 것이라고 이해하면 되나요?\""]], [["“Oh, no, not at all.", "\"아, 아니에요, 전혀요."], ["I was quite serious in what I said,”", "저는 제가 한 말에 아주 진심이었어요.\""], ["Alyosha declared stoutly.", "알료샤가 단호하게 잘라 말했다."]], [["“To be serious about it is impossible, unthinkable, and in the first place I shall never be at home to you again, and I shall take her away, you may be sure of that.”", "\"그 일을 진지하게 여긴다는 건 불가능해요, 어림도 없는 일이에요, 그리고 무엇보다 저는 두 번 다시 당신을 집에 들이지 않을 거고, 그 애를 멀리 데려가 버릴 거예요, 그건 분명히 알아 두세요.\""]], [["“But why?”", "\"하지만 어째서요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."], ["“It’s all so far off.", "\"그건 다 한참 먼 이야기예요."], ["We may have to wait another year and a half.”", "우린 어쩌면 일 년 반은 더 기다려야 할지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Ah, Alexey Fyodorovitch, that’s true, of course, and you’ll have time to quarrel and separate a thousand times in a year and a half.", "\"아, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 그건 물론 맞는 말이에요, 그러면 일 년 반 동안 당신들은 천 번도 더 다투고 헤어질 시간이 있겠군요."], ["But I am so unhappy!", "하지만 저는 너무도 불행해요!"], ["Though it’s such nonsense, it’s a great blow to me.", "비록 이게 다 헛소리지만, 저한테는 크나큰 타격이라고요."], ["I feel like Famusov in the last scene of Sorrow from Wit.", "저는 마치 〈지혜의 슬픔〉 마지막 장면의 파무소프가 된 기분이에요."], ["You are Tchatsky and she is Sofya, and, only fancy, I’ve run down to meet you on the stairs, and in the play the fatal scene takes place on the staircase.", "당신은 차츠키고, 그 애는 소피야예요, 그리고 글쎄, 저는 당신을 맞으려고 계단으로 달려 내려왔는데, 그 연극에서도 운명적인 장면이 계단에서 벌어지거든요."], ["I heard it all; I almost dropped.", "저는 다 들었어요, 하마터면 쓰러질 뻔했답니다."], ["So this is the explanation of her dreadful night and her hysterics of late!", "그래서 요즘 그 애의 그 끔찍한 밤이며 발작이 다 이렇게 설명되는 거였어요!"], ["It means love to the daughter but death to the mother.", "그건 딸한테는 사랑이지만 엄마한테는 죽음이에요."], ["I might as well be in my grave at once.", "차라리 저는 당장 무덤에 들어가는 게 낫겠어요."], ["And a more serious matter still, what is this letter she has written?", "그리고 그보다 더 중대한 문제가 있는데, 그 애가 쓴 이 편지는 대체 뭐예요?"], ["Show it me at once, at once!”", "당장 보여 주세요, 당장요!\""]], [["“No, there’s no need.", "\"아니요, 그러실 필요 없어요."], ["Tell me, how is Katerina Ivanovna now?", "말씀해 주세요, 카테리나 이바노브나는 지금 어떤가요?"], ["I must know.”", "저는 꼭 알아야 해요.\""]], [["“She still lies in delirium; she has not regained consciousness.", "\"그 애는 아직도 헛소리를 하며 누워 있어요, 의식을 되찾지 못했답니다."], ["Her aunts are here; but they do nothing but sigh and give themselves airs.", "고모들이 와 있긴 한데, 한숨이나 내쉬고 거드름이나 피울 뿐 아무것도 하지 않아요."], ["Herzenstube came, and he was so alarmed that I didn’t know what to do for him.", "게르첸슈투베가 왔는데, 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지 저는 그 사람을 어떻게 해야 할지 몰랐다니까요."], ["I nearly sent for a doctor to look after him.", "하마터면 그 사람을 돌봐 줄 의사를 부를 뻔했어요."], ["He was driven home in my carriage.", "결국 제 마차로 그 사람을 집까지 태워 보냈죠."], ["And on the top of it all, you and this letter!", "그런 마당에 당신까지, 게다가 이 편지까지!"], ["It’s true nothing can happen for a year and a half.", "정말이지 일 년 반 동안은 아무 일도 일어날 수 없겠죠."], ["In the name of all that’s holy, in the name of your dying elder, show me that letter, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "신성한 모든 것의 이름으로, 당신의 임종을 앞둔 장로님의 이름으로 청하니, 그 편지를 제게 보여 주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["I’m her mother.", "저는 그 애의 어미예요."], ["Hold it in your hand, if you like, and I will read it so.”", "정 그러시면 손에 쥐고 계세요, 그러면 제가 그렇게 읽을게요.\""]], [["“No, I won’t show it to you.", "\"아니요, 보여 드리지 않을 거예요."], ["Even if she sanctioned it, I wouldn’t.", "설령 그 애가 허락한다 해도 그러지 않을 거예요."], ["I am coming to-morrow, and if you like, we can talk over many things, but now good-by!”", "내일 다시 올 테니, 원하신다면 그때 여러 가지를 함께 의논해요, 하지만 지금은 안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["And Alyosha ran downstairs and into the street.", "그러고는 알료샤는 계단을 뛰어 내려가 거리로 나섰다."]], [["# Chapter II. Smerdyakov With A Guitar", "# Chapter II. Smerdyakov With A Guitar"]], [["He had no time to lose indeed.", "그는 정말로 한시도 지체할 수 없었다."], ["Even while he was saying good-by to Lise, the thought had struck him that he must attempt some stratagem to find his brother Dmitri, who was evidently keeping out of his way.", "리즈에게 작별 인사를 건네는 동안에도, 형 드미트리를 찾아내려면 무슨 계책이든 써 봐야겠다는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤으니, 형이 분명 자기를 피해 다니고 있었기 때문이다."], ["It was getting late, nearly three o’clock.", "시간은 늦어져 거의 세 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다."], ["Alyosha’s whole soul turned to the monastery, to his dying saint, but the necessity of seeing Dmitri outweighed everything.", "알료샤의 온 영혼은 수도원으로, 죽어 가는 그의 성자에게로 향해 있었으나, 드미트리를 만나야 한다는 절박함이 모든 것을 압도했다."], ["The conviction that a great inevitable catastrophe was about to happen grew stronger in Alyosha’s mind with every hour.", "어떤 거대하고 피할 수 없는 파국이 닥쳐오리라는 확신이 시간이 갈수록 알료샤의 마음속에서 더 강해졌다."], ["What that catastrophe was, and what he would say at that moment to his brother, he could perhaps not have said definitely.", "그 파국이 무엇인지, 그리고 그 순간 형에게 무어라 말할 것인지는, 어쩌면 그 자신도 분명히 말할 수 없었으리라."], ["“Even if my benefactor must die without me, anyway I won’t have to reproach myself all my life with the thought that I might have saved something and did not, but passed by and hastened home.", "\"설령 나의 은인께서 내가 없는 사이에 돌아가시게 된다 해도, 적어도 나는 무언가를 구할 수 있었는데 그러지 않고 지나쳐 집으로 서둘러 가 버렸다는 생각으로 평생 나 자신을 책망하지는 않게 되리라."], ["If I do as I intend, I shall be following his great precept.”", "내가 마음먹은 대로 한다면, 나는 그분의 위대한 가르침을 따르는 것이다.\""]], [["His plan was to catch his brother Dmitri unawares, to climb over the fence, as he had the day before, get into the garden and sit in the summer-house.", "그의 계획은 형 드미트리의 허를 찌르는 것이었으니, 곧 어제처럼 울타리를 넘어 정원에 들어가 정자에 앉아 있는 것이었다."], ["If Dmitri were not there, thought Alyosha, he would not announce himself to Foma or the women of the house, but would remain hidden in the summer-house, even if he had to wait there till evening.", "알료샤는 생각하기를, 만약 드미트리가 그곳에 없거든 포마에게나 집안의 여인들에게 자기 존재를 알리지 않고, 저녁까지 기다려야 하는 한이 있더라도 정자에 숨어 있겠다는 것이었다."], ["If, as before, Dmitri were lying in wait for Grushenka to come, he would be very likely to come to the summer-house.", "만약 어제처럼 드미트리가 그루셴카가 오기를 숨어서 기다리고 있다면, 그도 십중팔구 정자로 올 터였다."], ["Alyosha did not, however, give much thought to the details of his plan, but resolved to act upon it, even if it meant not getting back to the monastery that day.", "그러나 알료샤는 계획의 세세한 부분까지는 별로 생각하지 않은 채, 설령 그날 수도원으로 돌아가지 못하는 한이 있더라도 그대로 실행하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Everything happened without hindrance, he climbed over the hurdle almost in the same spot as the day before, and stole into the summer-house unseen.", "모든 일이 거침없이 풀려, 그는 어제와 거의 같은 자리에서 울타리를 넘어 아무에게도 들키지 않고 정자로 숨어들었다."], ["He did not want to be noticed.", "그는 눈에 띄고 싶지 않았다."], ["The woman of the house and Foma too, if he were here, might be loyal to his brother and obey his instructions, and so refuse to let Alyosha come into the garden, or might warn Dmitri that he was being sought and inquired for.", "집안의 여인도, 혹시 포마가 여기 있다면 그 역시도, 형에게 충성스러워 형의 지시를 따를 터이니, 알료샤가 정원에 들어오는 것을 막거나, 혹은 누가 그를 찾으며 캐묻고 있다고 드미트리에게 일러바칠지도 몰랐다."]], [["There was no one in the summer-house.", "정자에는 아무도 없었다."], ["Alyosha sat down and began to wait.", "알료샤는 앉아서 기다리기 시작했다."], ["He looked round the summer-house, which somehow struck him as a great deal more ancient than before.", "그는 정자를 둘러보았는데, 어쩐 일인지 그것이 전보다 훨씬 더 낡아 보였다."], ["Though the day was just as fine as yesterday, it seemed a wretched little place this time.", "날씨는 어제와 다름없이 화창했건만, 이번에는 초라한 작은 자리로만 보였다."], ["There was a circle on the table, left no doubt from the glass of brandy having been spilt the day before.", "탁자 위에는 둥근 자국이 남아 있었으니, 어제 브랜디 잔을 엎질러 생긴 것임에 틀림없었다."], ["Foolish and irrelevant ideas strayed about his mind, as they always do in a time of tedious waiting.", "따분하게 기다리는 시간에 으레 그렇듯, 어리석고 부질없는 생각들이 그의 머릿속을 떠돌았다."], ["He wondered, for instance, why he had sat down precisely in the same place as before, why not in the other seat.", "가령 그는 어째서 다른 자리가 아니라 하필 어제와 똑같은 자리에 앉았을까 하는 생각을 했다."], ["At last he felt very depressed—depressed by suspense and uncertainty.", "마침내 그는 몹시 우울해졌다—초조함과 불확실함에 짓눌려 우울해진 것이었다."], ["But he had not sat there more than a quarter of an hour, when he suddenly heard the thrum of a guitar somewhere quite close.", "그러나 그가 그곳에 앉은 지 십오 분도 채 못 되어, 문득 아주 가까운 어딘가에서 기타를 퉁기는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["People were sitting, or had only just sat down, somewhere in the bushes not more than twenty paces away.", "누군가가, 스무 걸음도 채 떨어지지 않은 덤불 어딘가에 앉아 있거나 이제 막 앉은 참이었다."], ["Alyosha suddenly recollected that on coming out of the summer-house the day before, he had caught a glimpse of an old green low garden-seat among the bushes on the left, by the fence.", "알료샤는 문득 어제 정자에서 나오던 길에, 왼편 울타리 가까운 덤불 사이로 낡은 초록빛 낮은 정원용 의자를 언뜻 본 것이 떠올랐다."], ["The people must be sitting on it now.", "사람들은 분명 지금 거기에 앉아 있을 터였다."], ["Who were they?", "누구일까?"]], [["A man’s voice suddenly began singing in a sugary falsetto, accompanying himself on the guitar:", "한 남자의 목소리가 문득 달콤한 가성으로 노래를 부르기 시작했는데, 스스로 기타로 반주를 곁들였다. 거역할 수 없는 힘으로"]], [["With invincible force I am bound to my dear. O Lord, have mercy On her and on me!", "나는 내 임에게 묶였네."], ["On her and on me! On her and on me!", "오 주여, 자비를 베푸소서"]], [["The voice ceased. It was a lackey’s tenor and a lackey’s song.", "그녀와 나에게!"], ["Another voice, a woman’s, suddenly asked insinuatingly and bashfully, though with mincing affectation:", "그녀와 나에게!"]], [["“Why haven’t you been to see us for so long, Pavel Fyodorovitch?", "그녀와 나에게! 목소리가 그쳤다."], ["Why do you always look down upon us?”", "그것은 하인의 테너였고 하인의 노래였다. 또 다른 목소리, 여자의 목소리가 문득 알랑거리듯 수줍게, 그러면서도 새치름한 교태를 부리며 물었다."]], [["“Not at all,” answered a man’s voice politely, but with emphatic dignity.", "\"파벨 표도로비치, 어째서 그토록 오래 우리를 보러 오시지 않으셨어요?"], ["It was clear that the man had the best of the position, and that the woman was making advances.", "어째서 늘 우리를 깔보시는 거예요?\" \"천만에.\""], ["“I believe the man must be Smerdyakov,”", "남자의 목소리가 정중하게, 그러나 도도한 위엄을 담아 대답했다. 남자가 우위를 점하고 있고, 여자가 추파를 던지고 있음이 분명했다."], ["thought Alyosha, “from his voice.", "\"목소리로 보아 저 남자는 분명 스메르댜코프일 거야.\""], ["And the lady must be the daughter of the house here, who has come from Moscow, the one who wears the dress with a tail and goes to Marfa for soup.”", "알료샤는 생각했다. \"그리고 저 아가씨는 이 집 딸, 모스크바에서 왔다는 그 여자, 자락이 달린 드레스를 입고 마르파에게 수프를 얻어먹으러 가는 여자일 테고.\""]], [["“I am awfully fond of verses of all kinds, if they rhyme,”", "\"운만 맞으면 어떤 시구든 무척 좋아해요.\""], ["the woman’s voice continued.", "여자의 목소리가 이어졌다."], ["“Why don’t you go on?”", "\"왜 계속 안 부르세요?\""]], [["The man sang again:", "남자가 다시 노래했다."]], [["What do I care for royal wealth If but my dear one be in health? Lord have mercy On her and on me! On her and on me! On her and on me!", "내 임이 무사하기만 하다면"]], [["“It was even better last time,” observed the woman’s voice. “You sang ‘If my darling be in health’; it sounded more tender. I suppose you’ve forgotten to-day.”", "제왕의 부귀인들 무슨 상관이랴?"]], [["“Poetry is rubbish!” said Smerdyakov curtly.", "주여, 자비를 베푸소서\n그녀와 나에게!"]], [["“Oh, no!", "그녀와 나에게!"], ["I am very fond of poetry.”", "그녀와 나에게!"]], [["“So far as it’s poetry, it’s essential rubbish.", "\"지난번이 훨씬 더 좋았어요.\" 여자의 목소리가 말했다."], ["Consider yourself, who ever talks in rhyme?", "\"그때는 '내 사랑이 무사하기만 하다면'이라고 부르셨잖아요."], ["And if we were all to talk in rhyme, even though it were decreed by government, we shouldn’t say much, should we?", "그게 더 다정하게 들렸어요. 오늘은 잊어버리셨나 봐요.\""], ["Poetry is no good, Marya Kondratyevna.”", "\"시 따위는 시시한 거요!\" 스메르댜코프가 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["“How clever you are!", "\"어머, 아니에요! 저는 시를 무척 좋아하는걸요.\""], ["How is it you’ve gone so deep into everything?”", "\"시인 한에서야, 그건 본질적으로 시시한 거요. 생각해 보시오, 도대체 누가 운을 맞춰서 말을 한답디까?"], ["The woman’s voice was more and more insinuating.", "우리 모두가 운을 맞춰서 말하라고, 설령 그게 정부의 명령이라 한들, 그리 많은 말을 하지는 못할 거 아니오? 시는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 거요, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\""]], [["“I could have done better than that.", "\"어쩜 그렇게 영리하세요! 어떻게 모든 일에 그토록 깊이 통달하셨어요?\""], ["I could have known more than that, if it had not been for my destiny from my childhood up.", "여자의 목소리는 점점 더 알랑거렸다. \"그보다 더한 것도 할 수 있었을 거요."], ["I would have shot a man in a duel if he called me names because I am descended from a filthy beggar and have no father.", "그보다 더 많은 것을 알 수도 있었을 거요, 어릴 적부터의 내 운명만 아니었더라면. 누가 나더러 더러운 거지의 자식으로 태어나 아비도 없는 놈이라고 욕했다면, 나는 결투를 벌여 그자를 쏘아 죽였을 거요."], ["And they used to throw it in my teeth in Moscow.", "모스크바에서도 사람들이 그걸 내 얼굴에다 대고 들먹이곤 했지."], ["It had reached them from here, thanks to Grigory Vassilyevitch.", "그게 여기서 거기까지 흘러간 건 그리고리 바실리예비치 덕분이오."], ["Grigory Vassilyevitch blames me for rebelling against my birth, but I would have sanctioned their killing me before I was born that I might not have come into the world at all.", "그리고리 바실리예비치는 내가 내 출생에 반항한다고 나를 나무라지만, 나는 차라리 태어나기 전에 그들이 나를 죽이는 걸 허락했을 거요, 그래서 아예 이 세상에 나오지나 않았으면 했단 말이오."], ["They used to say in the market, and your mamma too, with great lack of delicacy, set off telling me that her hair was like a mat on her head, and that she was short of five foot by a wee bit.", "장터에서도 그런 말들을 하곤 했고, 당신네 어머니까지도 어찌나 무례하던지 내 어미의 머리털이 머리 위에 깔린 멍석 같았느니, 키가 다섯 자에서 쪼끔 모자랐느니 하고 나한테 떠벌리기 시작합디다."], ["Why talk of a wee bit while she might have said ‘a little bit,’ like every one else?", "어째서 '쪼끔'이라고 하는 거요, 남들처럼 '조금'이라고 말할 수도 있었을 텐데?"], ["She wanted to make it touching, a regular peasant’s feeling.", "그 여자는 그걸 가슴 뭉클하게 만들고 싶었던 거요, 영락없는 촌놈의 감상이지."], ["Can a Russian peasant be said to feel, in comparison with an educated man?", "러시아 촌놈한테 배운 사람에 견줄 만한 감정이 있다고 할 수 있겠소?"], ["He can’t be said to have feeling at all, in his ignorance.", "그 무지로 보아 감정이라고는 도무지 없다고 해야지."], ["From my childhood up when I hear ‘a wee bit,’ I am ready to burst with rage.", "어릴 적부터 나는 '쪼끔'이라는 말만 들으면 분통이 터질 지경이오."], ["I hate all Russia, Marya Kondratyevna.”", "나는 러시아 전체가 다 싫소, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\""]], [["“If you’d been a cadet in the army, or a young hussar, you wouldn’t have talked like that, but would have drawn your saber to defend all Russia.”", "\"당신이 사관 후보생이나 젊은 경기병이었다면 그렇게 말하지 않고 칼을 빼 들어 러시아 전체를 지키려 했을 거예요.\""]], [["“I don’t want to be a hussar, Marya Kondratyevna, and, what’s more, I should like to abolish all soldiers.”", "\"나는 경기병 따위가 되고 싶지 않소, 마리야 콘드라티예브나, 게다가 나라면 군인이란 군인은 죄다 없애 버리고 싶소.\""]], [["“And when an enemy comes, who is going to defend us?”", "\"그러다 적이 쳐들어오면 누가 우리를 지켜 줘요?\""]], [["“There’s no need of defense.", "\"지켜 줄 필요 따위 없소."], ["In 1812 there was a great invasion of Russia by Napoleon, first Emperor of the French, father of the present one, and it would have been a good thing if they had conquered us.", "1812년에 프랑스의 초대 황제, 지금 황제의 아비인 나폴레옹이 러시아를 대대적으로 침공했는데, 그때 그들이 우리를 정복했더라면 좋았을 거요."], ["A clever nation would have conquered a very stupid one and annexed it.", "영리한 민족이 아주 어리석은 민족을 정복해서 병합했을 테니까."], ["We should have had quite different institutions.”", "그랬으면 우리도 사뭇 다른 제도를 갖게 됐을 거요.\""]], [["“Are they so much better in their own country than we are?", "\"그쪽 나라가 우리보다 그렇게나 더 나은가요?"], ["I wouldn’t change a dandy I know of for three young Englishmen,”", "나라면 내가 아는 멋쟁이 하나를 젊은 영국인 셋과도 안 바꾸겠어요.\""], ["observed Marya Kondratyevna tenderly, doubtless accompanying her words with a most languishing glance.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 다정하게 말했는데, 그 말끝에 분명 더없이 애교 어린 눈길을 곁들였을 터였다."]], [["“That’s as one prefers.”", "\"그건 사람마다 취향 나름이지.\""]], [["“But you are just like a foreigner—just like a most gentlemanly foreigner.", "\"하지만 당신은 영락없는 외국인 같아요—영락없이 더없이 신사다운 외국인 같다고요."], ["I tell you that, though it makes me bashful.”", "이런 말 하려니 부끄럽지만, 정말 그렇다고요.\""]], [["“If you care to know, the folks there and ours here are just alike in their vice.", "\"굳이 알고 싶다면 말이오만, 거기 사람들이나 여기 우리 사람들이나 악덕으로 치면 다 똑같소."], ["They are swindlers, only there the scoundrel wears polished boots and here he grovels in filth and sees no harm in it.", "다들 사기꾼이지, 다만 거기서는 악당이 반들반들 광낸 부츠를 신고, 여기서는 진창 속에 뒹굴면서도 그게 나쁜 줄을 모른다는 차이뿐이오."], ["The Russian people want thrashing, as Fyodor Pavlovitch said very truly yesterday, though he is mad, and all his children.”", "러시아 백성한테는 매질이 필요하오, 어제 표도르 파블로비치가 아주 옳은 말을 했지, 비록 그 양반이 미치광이고 그 자식들도 다 그렇긴 하지만 말이오.\""]], [["“You said yourself you had such a respect for Ivan Fyodorovitch.”", "\"이반 표도로비치는 그토록 존경한다고 당신 입으로 말했잖아요.\""]], [["“But he said I was a stinking lackey.", "\"그런데 그 양반은 나더러 구린내 나는 하인이라고 합디다."], ["He thinks that I might be unruly.", "내가 분수를 모르고 까불지도 모른다고 여기는 거요."], ["He is mistaken there.", "그건 그 양반이 잘못 본 거요."], ["If I had a certain sum in my pocket, I would have left here long ago.", "내 주머니에 얼마간의 돈만 있었다면 나는 진작 여길 떠났을 거요."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch is lower than any lackey in his behavior, in his mind, and in his poverty.", "드미트리 표도로비치는 그 행실로 보나, 그 머리로 보나, 그 가난으로 보나 어느 하인보다도 못한 위인이오."], ["He doesn’t know how to do anything, and yet he is respected by every one.", "할 줄 아는 게 아무것도 없는데도 다들 그를 떠받든단 말이오."], ["I may be only a soup-maker, but with luck I could open a café restaurant in Petrovka, in Moscow, for my cookery is something special, and there’s no one in Moscow, except the foreigners, whose cookery is anything special.", "나야 한낱 수프나 끓이는 놈일지 모르지만, 운만 따라 준다면 모스크바 페트로프카에 카페 식당이라도 차릴 수 있소, 내 요리 솜씨가 보통이 아니거든, 모스크바에서 요리 솜씨가 변변한 자라고는 외국인들 말고는 아무도 없으니까."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch is a beggar, but if he were to challenge the son of the first count in the country, he’d fight him.", "드미트리 표도로비치는 거지나 다름없는데도, 이 나라 으뜸가는 백작의 아들에게 결투를 신청한다면 정말로 싸울 위인이오."], ["Though in what way is he better than I am?", "그렇지만 그가 나보다 나은 게 도대체 뭐가 있소?"], ["For he is ever so much stupider than I am.", "나보다 훨씬 더 멍청한걸."], ["Look at the money he has wasted without any need!”", "아무 까닭도 없이 돈을 얼마나 펑펑 날려 버렸는지 좀 보시오!\""]], [["“It must be lovely, a duel,”", "\"결투란 건 분명 멋있을 거예요.\""], ["Marya Kondratyevna observed suddenly.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 문득 말했다."]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서 그렇소?\""]], [["“It must be so dreadful and so brave, especially when young officers with pistols in their hands pop at one another for the sake of some lady.", "\"틀림없이 끔찍하면서도 용감할 거예요, 특히 젊은 장교들이 어떤 귀부인을 위해 손에 권총을 들고 서로를 향해 탕 쏠 때 말이에요."], ["A perfect picture!", "영락없는 한 폭의 그림이죠!"], ["Ah, if only girls were allowed to look on, I’d give anything to see one!”", "아, 아가씨들한테도 구경하는 게 허락된다면, 한 번 보려고 무엇이든 다 내놓겠어요!\""]], [["“It’s all very well when you are firing at some one, but when he is firing straight in your mug, you must feel pretty silly.", "\"당신이 누군가를 향해 쏠 때야 다 괜찮지만, 그자가 당신 상판대기를 정통으로 겨누고 쏠 때는 꽤나 멍청한 기분이 들 거요."], ["You’d be glad to run away, Marya Kondratyevna.”", "줄행랑이라도 치고 싶어질걸, 마리야 콘드라티예브나.\""]], [["“You don’t mean you would run away?”", "\"설마 당신이 줄행랑을 치겠다는 건 아니죠?\""], ["But Smerdyakov did not deign to reply.", "그러나 스메르댜코프는 굳이 대답하려 들지 않았다."], ["After a moment’s silence the guitar tinkled again, and he sang again in the same falsetto:", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 기타가 다시 딸랑거렸고, 그는 또다시 같은 가성으로 노래했다. 당신이 무어라 하건"]], [["Whatever you may say, I shall go far away. Life will be bright and gay In the city far away.", "나는 멀리 떠나가리."], ["I shall not grieve, I shall not grieve at all, I don’t intend to grieve at all.", "저 멀리 도시에서는"]], [["Then something unexpected happened. Alyosha suddenly sneezed. They were silent. Alyosha got up and walked towards them. He found Smerdyakov dressed up and wearing polished boots, his hair pomaded, and perhaps curled. The guitar lay on the garden-seat. His companion was the daughter of the house, wearing a light-blue dress with a train two yards long. She was young and would not have been bad-looking, but that her face was so round and terribly freckled.", "삶이 밝고 즐거우리. 나는 슬퍼하지 않으리,"]], [["“Will my brother Dmitri soon be back?” asked Alyosha with as much composure as he could.", "나는 결코 슬퍼하지 않으리,\n나는 도무지 슬퍼할 마음이 없네."]], [["Smerdyakov got up slowly; Marya Kondratyevna rose too.", "그때 뜻밖의 일이 벌어졌다. 알료샤가 갑자기 재채기를 한 것이다."], ["“How am I to know about Dmitri Fyodorovitch?", "그들은 입을 다물었다. 알료샤는 일어나 그들 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["It’s not as if I were his keeper,”", "가서 보니 스메르댜코프는 말쑥하게 차려입고 반들반들 광낸 부츠를 신었으며, 머리에는 포마드를 발라 어쩌면 곱슬곱슬하게 지졌을지도 몰랐다. 기타는 정원용 의자 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["answered Smerdyakov quietly, distinctly, and superciliously.", "그와 함께 있던 이는 이 집 딸로, 자락이 두 자나 되는 연한 하늘빛 드레스를 입고 있었다. 그녀는 젊었고 못생겼다고 할 수는 없었으나, 다만 얼굴이 어찌나 둥글고 주근깨가 끔찍이도 많은지 그게 흠이었다."]], [["“But I simply asked whether you do know?”", "\"형 드미트리는 곧 돌아오나요?\""], ["Alyosha explained.", "알료샤는 애써 침착하게 물었다."]], [["“I know nothing of his whereabouts and don’t want to.”", "스메르댜코프는 느릿느릿 일어섰고, 마리야 콘드라티예브나도 따라 일어났다."]], [["“But my brother told me that you let him know all that goes on in the house, and promised to let him know when Agrafena Alexandrovna comes.”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치에 대해 제가 어찌 알겠습니까? 제가 그분 보호자도 아니고 말입니다.\" 스메르댜코프가 조용히, 또렷하게, 그리고 거만하게 대답했다. \"그저 혹시 알고 있는지 물었을 뿐이에요.\" 알료샤가 설명했다."]], [["Smerdyakov turned a deliberate, unmoved glance upon him.", "\"그분이 어디 계신지 저는 아무것도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않습니다.\""]], [["“And how did you get in this time, since the gate was bolted an hour ago?” he asked, looking at Alyosha.", "\"하지만 형이 말하길, 자네가 이 집에서 일어나는 일을 다 알려 주고, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오면 알려 주기로 약속했다던데요.\""]], [["“I came in from the back-alley, over the fence, and went straight to the summer-house.", "스메르댜코프는 느릿하고 미동도 없는 눈길을 그에게로 돌렸다."], ["I hope you’ll forgive me,” he added, addressing Marya Kondratyevna.", "\"그건 그렇고, 한 시간 전에 대문 빗장을 걸어 두었는데 이번엔 어떻게 들어오셨습니까?\""], ["“I was in a hurry to find my brother.”", "그가 알료샤를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“Ach, as though we could take it amiss in you!”", "\"뒷골목에서 울타리를 넘어 곧장 정자로 왔어요. 부디 용서해 주시길.\""], ["drawled Marya Kondratyevna, flattered by Alyosha’s apology.", "그는 마리야 콘드라티예브나를 향해 덧붙였다. \"형을 급히 찾아야 했거든요.\""], ["“For Dmitri Fyodorovitch often goes to the summer-house in that way.", "\"어머, 저희가 그걸 가지고 언짢아할 리가 있나요!\" 알료샤의 사과에 우쭐해진 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 길게 빼는 말투로 말했다."], ["We don’t know he is here and he is sitting in the summer-house.”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치도 곧잘 그렇게 정자로 오시는걸요. 저희는 그분이 와 계신 줄도 모르는데 정자에 앉아 계시곤 하지요.\""]], [["“I am very anxious to find him, or to learn from you where he is now.", "\"그분을 꼭 찾고 싶어요, 아니면 지금 어디 계신지 두 분께 알아내고 싶어요."], ["Believe me, it’s on business of great importance to him.”", "정말이지 그분한테 아주 중대한 일 때문이에요.\""]], [["“He never tells us,”", "\"그분은 저희한테 도통 말씀을 안 하셔요.\""], ["lisped Marya Kondratyevna.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 혀 짧은 소리로 말했다."]], [["“Though I used to come here as a friend,”", "\"제가 비록 친구로서 여길 드나들긴 했습니다만,\""], ["Smerdyakov began again, “Dmitri Fyodorovitch has pestered me in a merciless way even here by his incessant questions about the master.", "스메르댜코프가 다시 말문을 열었다, \"드미트리 표도로비치는 여기서까지 주인어른에 대해 끊임없이 캐물으며 사정없이 저를 들볶았습니다."], ["‘What news?’ he’ll ask.", "'무슨 소식 없느냐?'고 묻지요."], ["‘What’s going on in there now?", "'지금 저 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지느냐?"], ["Who’s coming and going?’ and can’t I tell him something more.", "누가 오가느냐?' 그러면서 좀 더 말해 줄 게 없냐고 채근하는 겁니다."], ["Twice already he’s threatened me with death.”", "벌써 두 번이나 저를 죽이겠다고 으름장을 놨습죠.\""]], [["“With death?”", "\"죽이겠다고요?\""], ["Alyosha exclaimed in surprise.", "알료샤가 놀라 소리쳤다."]], [["“Do you suppose he’d think much of that, with his temper, which you had a chance of observing yourself yesterday?", "\"그 성미로 보아 그쯤이야 대수롭게 여길 분이겠습니까, 어제 도련님께서도 직접 보실 기회가 있었지 않습니까?"], ["He says if I let Agrafena Alexandrovna in and she passes the night there, I’ll be the first to suffer for it.", "제가 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 들이고 그녀가 거기서 하룻밤 묵게 되면 제가 제일 먼저 화를 당할 거랍니다."], ["I am terribly afraid of him, and if I were not even more afraid of doing so, I ought to let the police know.", "저는 그분이 끔찍이 무섭습니다, 그러는 게 더 무섭지만 않았다면 경찰에 알려야 마땅할 지경입죠."], ["God only knows what he might not do!”", "그분이 무슨 짓을 저지를지는 하느님만 아실 일입니다!\""]], [["“His honor said to him the other day, ‘I’ll pound you in a mortar!’”", "\"나리께서도 며칠 전에 그분더러 '절구에 넣고 짓찧어 버리겠다!'고 하셨어요.\""], ["added Marya Kondratyevna.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, if it’s pounding in a mortar, it may be only talk,”", "\"아, 절구에 넣고 짓찧겠다는 거라면 그냥 해 보는 말일 수도 있겠죠.\""], ["observed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."], ["“If I could meet him, I might speak to him about that too.”", "\"그분을 만날 수 있다면 그 일에 대해서도 말해 볼게요.\""]], [["“Well, the only thing I can tell you is this,”", "\"음, 제가 도련님께 말씀드릴 수 있는 건 이것뿐입니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, as though thinking better of it; “I am here as an old friend and neighbor, and it would be odd if I didn’t come.", "스메르댜코프가 마음을 고쳐먹은 듯 말했다. \"저야 오랜 친구이자 이웃으로서 여기 있는 거니, 제가 오지 않는다면 그게 오히려 이상한 일이겠지요."], ["On the other hand, Ivan Fyodorovitch sent me first thing this morning to your brother’s lodging in Lake Street, without a letter, but with a message to Dmitri Fyodorovitch to go to dine with him at the restaurant here, in the market-place.", "한편으로, 이반 표도로비치께서 오늘 아침 맨 먼저 저를 도련님 형님이 묵으시는 오제르나야 거리 숙소로 보내셨습니다, 편지는 없이 그저 드미트리 표도로비치께 여기 장터에 있는 식당으로 와서 같이 식사하자는 전갈만 들려서요."], ["I went, but didn’t find Dmitri Fyodorovitch at home, though it was eight o’clock.", "가 보았지만 드미트리 표도로비치는 댁에 안 계셨습니다, 여덟 시였는데도 말입니다."], ["‘He’s been here, but he is quite gone,’ those were the very words of his landlady.", "'여기 다녀가시긴 했는데 아주 떠나 버리셨다'고, 그 댁 안주인이 한 말이 바로 그것이었습니다."], ["It’s as though there was an understanding between them.", "마치 두 분 사이에 무슨 짠 게 있는 것 같았지요."], ["Perhaps at this moment he is in the restaurant with Ivan Fyodorovitch, for Ivan Fyodorovitch has not been home to dinner and Fyodor Pavlovitch dined alone an hour ago, and is gone to lie down.", "어쩌면 지금쯤 그분은 이반 표도로비치와 함께 식당에 계실지도 모릅니다, 이반 표도로비치께서도 식사하러 집에 안 들어오셨고, 표도르 파블로비치께서는 한 시간 전에 혼자 식사를 마치시고 누우러 가셨으니까요."], ["But I beg you most particularly not to speak of me and of what I have told you, for he’d kill me for nothing at all.”", "그렇지만 제발 부탁드립니다, 저에 대해서나 제가 말씀드린 것에 대해서는 입도 뻥끗 마십시오, 그분은 아무것도 아닌 일로도 저를 죽일 사람이니까요.\""]], [["“Brother Ivan invited Dmitri to the restaurant to-day?”", "\"이반 형이 오늘 드미트리 형을 식당으로 초대했다고요?\""], ["repeated Alyosha quickly.", "알료샤가 다급히 되물었다."]], [["“That’s so.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“The Metropolis tavern in the market-place?”", "\"장터에 있는 메트로폴리스 선술집 말인가요?\""]], [["“The very same.”", "\"바로 그곳입니다.\""]], [["“That’s quite likely,”", "\"그럴 만하군요.\""], ["cried Alyosha, much excited.", "알료샤가 몹시 흥분하여 외쳤다."], ["“Thank you, Smerdyakov; that’s important.", "\"고마워요, 스메르댜코프, 이건 중요한 일이에요."], ["I’ll go there at once.”", "당장 거기로 가 봐야겠어요.\""]], [["“Don’t betray me,”", "\"저를 팔지 마십시오.\""], ["Smerdyakov called after him.", "스메르댜코프가 그의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다."]], [["“Oh, no, I’ll go to the tavern as though by chance.", "\"아, 그럼요, 우연히 들른 것처럼 선술집에 갈게요."], ["Don’t be anxious.”", "걱정 말아요.\""]], [["“But wait a minute, I’ll open the gate to you,”", "\"하지만 잠깐만요, 제가 대문을 열어 드릴게요.\""], ["cried Marya Kondratyevna.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 외쳤다."]], [["“No; it’s a short cut, I’ll get over the fence again.”", "\"아니에요, 이쪽이 지름길이라서요, 다시 울타리를 넘을게요.\""]], [["What he had heard threw Alyosha into great agitation.", "들은 이야기는 알료샤를 큰 동요에 빠뜨렸다."], ["He ran to the tavern.", "그는 선술집으로 달려갔다."], ["It was impossible for him to go into the tavern in his monastic dress, but he could inquire at the entrance for his brothers and call them down.", "수도복 차림으로는 선술집 안으로 들어갈 수 없었으나, 입구에서 형들을 찾아 불러내 달라고 청할 수는 있었다."], ["But just as he reached the tavern, a window was flung open, and his brother Ivan called down to him from it.", "그런데 마침 선술집에 다다랐을 때 창문 하나가 활짝 열리더니, 형 이반이 그 창에서 그를 내려다보며 불렀다."]], [["“Alyosha, can’t you come up here to me?", "\"알료샤, 이리로 올라올 수 없겠니?"], ["I shall be awfully grateful.”", "그래 주면 정말 고맙겠다.\""]], [["“To be sure I can, only I don’t quite know whether in this dress—”", "\"그럼 올라가야죠, 다만 이런 차림으로 괜찮을지 잘 모르겠는데요—\""]], [["“But I am in a room apart.", "\"하지만 난 따로 떨어진 방에 있어."], ["Come up the steps; I’ll run down to meet you.”", "계단으로 올라와, 내가 마중하러 내려갈게.\""]], [["A minute later Alyosha was sitting beside his brother.", "잠시 후 알료샤는 형 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["Ivan was alone dining.", "이반은 혼자서 식사를 하던 참이었다."]], [["# Chapter III. The Brothers Make Friends", "# Chapter III. The Brothers Make Friends"]], [["Ivan was not, however, in a separate room, but only in a place shut off by a screen, so that it was unseen by other people in the room.", "그러나 이반은 따로 마련된 방에 있는 것이 아니라 단지 칸막이로 가려진 자리에 있어서, 같은 방 안의 다른 사람들 눈에는 띄지 않았다."], ["It was the first room from the entrance with a buffet along the wall.", "그곳은 입구에서 첫 번째 방으로, 벽을 따라 식탁이 놓여 있었다."], ["Waiters were continually darting to and fro in it.", "종업원들이 끊임없이 그 안을 분주히 오갔다."], ["The only customer in the room was an old retired military man drinking tea in a corner.", "방 안의 손님이라고는 구석에서 차를 마시는, 퇴역한 늙은 군인 하나뿐이었다."], ["But there was the usual bustle going on in the other rooms of the tavern; there were shouts for the waiters, the sound of popping corks, the click of billiard balls, the drone of the organ.", "그러나 술집의 다른 방들에서는 여느 때와 다름없는 소란이 일고 있었으니, 종업원을 부르는 고함 소리, 병마개가 펑 터지는 소리, 당구공이 딱딱 부딪치는 소리, 손풍금이 웅웅대는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Alyosha knew that Ivan did not usually visit this tavern and disliked taverns in general.", "알료샤는 이반이 평소 이 술집에 드나들지 않으며, 대체로 술집이라는 곳을 좋아하지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["So he must have come here, he reflected, simply to meet Dmitri by arrangement.", "그러니 형은 단지 약속에 따라 드미트리를 만나러 이곳에 온 것이리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["Yet Dmitri was not there.", "그런데도 드미트리는 보이지 않았다."]], [["“Shall I order you fish, soup or anything.", "\"생선이나 수프, 뭐 좀 시켜 줄까?"], ["You don’t live on tea alone, I suppose,”", "설마 차만 마시고 사는 건 아닐 테지.\""], ["cried Ivan, apparently delighted at having got hold of Alyosha.", "이반이 외쳤는데, 알료샤를 붙잡은 것이 자못 반가운 기색이었다."], ["He had finished dinner and was drinking tea.", "그는 식사를 마치고 차를 마시던 참이었다."]], [["“Let me have soup, and tea afterwards, I am hungry,”", "\"수프를 먹고 나서 차를 마시겠어요, 배가 고파요.\""], ["said Alyosha gayly.", "알료샤가 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["“And cherry jam?", "\"버찌 잼은 어때?"], ["They have it here.", "여기 그게 있어."], ["You remember how you used to love cherry jam when you were little?”", "어릴 적에 버찌 잼을 그렇게 좋아하던 거 기억나니?\""]], [["“You remember that?", "\"형이 그걸 다 기억해요?"], ["Let me have jam too, I like it still.”", "잼도 주세요, 지금도 좋아하거든요.\""]], [["Ivan rang for the waiter and ordered soup, jam and tea.", "이반은 종을 울려 종업원을 부르더니 수프와 잼과 차를 주문했다."]], [["“I remember everything, Alyosha, I remember you till you were eleven, I was nearly fifteen.", "\"난 다 기억한다, 알료샤. 네가 열한 살이던 때까지 기억하지, 그때 난 열다섯이 다 되어 있었거든."], ["There’s such a difference between fifteen and eleven that brothers are never companions at those ages.", "열다섯하고 열하나 사이에는 그토록 차이가 커서, 그 나이의 형제는 결코 친구가 될 수 없는 법이야."], ["I don’t know whether I was fond of you even.", "내가 너를 좋아하기나 했는지도 모르겠다."], ["When I went away to Moscow for the first few years I never thought of you at all.", "처음 모스크바로 떠나 몇 해 동안은 네 생각이라곤 아예 하지도 않았어."], ["Then, when you came to Moscow yourself, we only met once somewhere, I believe.", "그러다 네가 직접 모스크바에 왔을 때도, 어디선가 딱 한 번 만났던 것 같구나."], ["And now I’ve been here more than three months, and so far we have scarcely said a word to each other.", "그리고 이제 내가 여기 온 지 석 달이 넘었는데, 여태껏 우리는 거의 한마디도 나누지 못했지."], ["To-morrow I am going away, and I was just thinking as I sat here how I could see you to say good-by and just then you passed.”", "내일이면 난 떠나는데, 여기 앉아 있으면서 어떻게 하면 너를 만나 작별 인사를 할 수 있을까 곰곰이 생각하던 차에, 마침 네가 지나가더구나.\""]], [["“Were you very anxious to see me, then?”", "\"그럼 형은 나를 그렇게 만나고 싶었던 거예요?\""]], [["“Very.", "\"무척이나."], ["I want to get to know you once for all, and I want you to know me.", "너라는 사람을 단번에 제대로 알아 두고 싶고, 또 너도 나를 알아 주었으면 했어."], ["And then to say good-by.", "그러고 나서 작별하려고."], ["I believe it’s always best to get to know people just before leaving them.", "사람을 알게 되기에는 헤어지기 직전이 언제나 가장 좋은 법이라고 나는 믿는다."], ["I’ve noticed how you’ve been looking at me these three months.", "이 석 달 동안 네가 나를 어떻게 바라보았는지 나는 다 보았어."], ["There has been a continual look of expectation in your eyes, and I can’t endure that.", "네 눈에는 늘 어떤 기대의 빛이 어려 있었는데, 나는 그게 견딜 수가 없었지."], ["That’s how it is I’ve kept away from you.", "그래서 너를 피해 다녔던 거야."], ["But in the end I have learned to respect you.", "하지만 결국에는 너를 존경하게 되었다."], ["The little man stands firm, I thought.", "이 조그만 녀석이 제법 야무지게 서 있구나, 하고 생각했지."], ["Though I am laughing, I am serious.", "웃고는 있지만 진심으로 하는 말이야."], ["You do stand firm, don’t you?", "너는 정말 야무지게 서 있어, 그렇지 않니?"], ["I like people who are firm like that whatever it is they stand by, even if they are such little fellows as you.", "나는 무엇을 신념으로 삼든 그렇게 야무지게 서 있는 사람이 좋아, 비록 너처럼 조그만 녀석이라 해도 말이야."], ["Your expectant eyes ceased to annoy me, I grew fond of them in the end, those expectant eyes.", "너의 그 기대에 찬 눈도 더 이상 거슬리지 않게 되었고, 끝내는 그 기대에 찬 눈을 사랑하게 되었어."], ["You seem to love me for some reason, Alyosha?”", "너는 어쩐 일인지 나를 사랑하는 것 같구나, 알료샤?\""]], [["“I do love you, Ivan.", "\"나는 형을 사랑해요, 이반."], ["Dmitri says of you—Ivan is a tomb!", "드미트리 형은 형을 두고 이반은 무덤이라고 해요."], ["I say of you, Ivan is a riddle.", "나는 형을 두고 이반은 수수께끼라고 해요."], ["You are a riddle to me even now.", "형은 지금도 내게 수수께끼예요."], ["But I understand something in you, and I did not understand it till this morning.”", "하지만 형 안의 무언가를 나는 이해해요, 오늘 아침까지만 해도 이해하지 못했던 것을요.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭐냐?\""], ["laughed Ivan.", "이반이 웃었다."]], [["“You won’t be angry?”", "\"화내지 않을 거죠?\""], ["Alyosha laughed too.", "알료샤도 웃었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래?\""]], [["“That you are just as young as other young men of three and twenty, that you are just a young and fresh and nice boy, green in fact!", "\"형이 다른 스물세 살 청년들과 똑같이 젊다는 것, 그저 젊고 싱그럽고 착한 소년이라는 것, 사실은 풋내기라는 것 말이에요!"], ["Now, have I insulted you dreadfully?”", "자, 내가 형을 끔찍하게 모욕한 건가요?\""]], [["“On the contrary, I am struck by a coincidence,”", "\"천만에, 오히려 그게 우연의 일치라 놀랍구나.\""], ["cried Ivan, warmly and good-humoredly.", "이반이 다정하고 유쾌하게 외쳤다."], ["“Would you believe it that ever since that scene with her, I have thought of nothing else but my youthful greenness, and just as though you guessed that, you begin about it.", "\"믿을 수 있겠니, 그 여자와 그 장면을 겪은 뒤로 나는 다른 건 아무것도 생각하지 않고 오직 내 젊은 풋내기 같은 면만 생각하고 있었거든, 그런데 마치 네가 그걸 알아챈 것처럼 바로 그 얘기를 꺼내는구나."], ["Do you know I’ve been sitting here thinking to myself: that if I didn’t believe in life, if I lost faith in the woman I love, lost faith in the order of things, were convinced in fact that everything is a disorderly, damnable, and perhaps devil-ridden chaos, if I were struck by every horror of man’s disillusionment—still I should want to live and, having once tasted of the cup, I would not turn away from it till I had drained it!", "알겠니, 나는 여기 앉아서 혼자 이런 생각을 하고 있었어. 만약 내가 삶을 믿지 않게 된다면, 내가 사랑하는 여자에 대한 믿음을 잃고, 사물의 질서에 대한 믿음을 잃고, 정녕 모든 것이 무질서하고 저주받은, 어쩌면 악마에 들린 혼돈에 지나지 않는다고 확신하게 된다면, 인간의 환멸이 자아내는 온갖 공포에 짓눌린다 해도—그래도 나는 살고 싶을 것이고, 일단 그 잔에 입을 댄 이상, 그것을 다 비우기 전에는 결코 외면하지 않을 거라고!"], ["At thirty, though, I shall be sure to leave the cup, even if I’ve not emptied it, and turn away—where I don’t know.", "그렇지만 서른이 되면, 비록 잔을 다 비우지 못했다 해도 분명 그 잔을 내려놓고 돌아설 거야—어디로 갈지는 나도 모르겠지만."], ["But till I am thirty, I know that my youth will triumph over everything—every disillusionment, every disgust with life.", "하지만 서른이 되기 전까지는, 내 젊음이 모든 것을—온갖 환멸을, 삶에 대한 온갖 혐오를 이겨 내리라는 걸 나는 알아."], ["I’ve asked myself many times whether there is in the world any despair that would overcome this frantic and perhaps unseemly thirst for life in me, and I’ve come to the conclusion that there isn’t, that is till I am thirty, and then I shall lose it of myself, I fancy.", "내 안에 있는 이 미친 듯한, 어쩌면 꼴사나운 삶에 대한 갈망을 압도할 만한 절망이 세상에 있는지 나는 수없이 자문해 봤지만, 결국 그런 건 없다는 결론에 이르렀지, 적어도 서른이 되기 전까지는 말이야. 그러고 나면 나는 저절로 그걸 잃게 되겠지, 그럴 것 같아."], ["Some driveling consumptive moralists—and poets especially—often call that thirst for life base.", "침이나 질질 흘리는 폐병쟁이 도덕가들이—특히 시인들이—그 삶에 대한 갈망을 흔히 비루하다고 부르지."], ["It’s a feature of the Karamazovs, it’s true, that thirst for life regardless of everything; you have it no doubt too, but why is it base?", "모든 것을 아랑곳 않는 그 삶에 대한 갈망, 그게 카라마조프 집안의 특징인 것은 사실이고, 너에게도 틀림없이 있을 테지만, 어째서 그게 비루하다는 거냐?"], ["The centripetal force on our planet is still fearfully strong, Alyosha.", "우리 행성의 구심력은 아직도 무섭도록 강해, 알료샤."], ["I have a longing for life, and I go on living in spite of logic.", "나는 삶을 갈망하고, 논리에 어긋나면서도 계속 살아가고 있어."], ["Though I may not believe in the order of the universe, yet I love the sticky little leaves as they open in spring.", "비록 우주의 질서를 믿지 않는다 해도, 봄철에 돋아나는 끈적끈적한 어린 잎사귀를 나는 사랑해."], ["I love the blue sky, I love some people, whom one loves you know sometimes without knowing why.", "푸른 하늘을 사랑하고, 어떤 사람들을 사랑하지, 그게 누군가를 영문도 모른 채 사랑하는 그런 거 있잖니."], ["I love some great deeds done by men, though I’ve long ceased perhaps to have faith in them, yet from old habit one’s heart prizes them.", "인간들이 이룬 어떤 위대한 행적들을 사랑해, 비록 오래전에 그것들에 대한 믿음을 잃어버렸을지도 모르지만, 그래도 오랜 습관 때문에 마음이 그것들을 소중히 여기지."], ["Here they have brought the soup for you, eat it, it will do you good.", "자, 네 수프가 나왔다, 먹어라, 몸에 좋을 거야."], ["It’s first-rate soup, they know how to make it here.", "일품 수프지, 여긴 이걸 잘 끓이거든."], ["I want to travel in Europe, Alyosha, I shall set off from here.", "나는 유럽을 여행하고 싶어, 알료샤, 여기서 출발할 거야."], ["And yet I know that I am only going to a graveyard, but it’s a most precious graveyard, that’s what it is!", "그러면서도 나는 내가 그저 묘지로 가고 있을 뿐이라는 걸 알아, 하지만 그건 더없이 소중한 묘지지, 바로 그런 거야!"], ["Precious are the dead that lie there, every stone over them speaks of such burning life in the past, of such passionate faith in their work, their truth, their struggle and their science, that I know I shall fall on the ground and kiss those stones and weep over them; though I’m convinced in my heart that it’s long been nothing but a graveyard.", "거기 누워 있는 죽은 자들은 소중해, 그들 위에 놓인 비석 하나하나가 지난날의 그토록 불타는 삶을, 자기 일과 자기 진리와 자기 투쟁과 자기 학문에 대한 그토록 열렬한 신념을 증언하고 있어서, 나는 땅에 엎드려 그 비석들에 입 맞추고 그 위에서 눈물을 흘릴 거라는 걸 알아. 비록 마음속으로는 그곳이 오래전부터 묘지에 지나지 않는다고 확신하면서도 말이야."], ["And I shall not weep from despair, but simply because I shall be happy in my tears, I shall steep my soul in my emotion.", "그리고 나는 절망 때문에 우는 게 아니라, 단지 내 눈물 속에서 행복할 것이기에 울 거야, 내 감정 속에 내 영혼을 푹 담글 거야."], ["I love the sticky leaves in spring, the blue sky—that’s all it is.", "봄철의 끈적끈적한 잎사귀를, 푸른 하늘을 나는 사랑해—그게 전부야."], ["It’s not a matter of intellect or logic, it’s loving with one’s inside, with one’s stomach.", "그건 지성이나 논리의 문제가 아니라, 제 속으로, 제 배로 사랑하는 거지."], ["One loves the first strength of one’s youth.", "사람은 제 젊음의 첫 힘을 사랑하는 법이야."], ["Do you understand anything of my tirade, Alyosha?”", "내 장광설 가운데 뭐라도 알아듣겠니, 알료샤?\""], ["Ivan laughed suddenly.", "이반이 별안간 웃었다."]], [["“I understand too well, Ivan.", "\"너무도 잘 알아듣겠어요, 이반."], ["One longs to love with one’s inside, with one’s stomach.", "제 속으로, 제 배로 사랑하기를 갈망한다는 말씀."], ["You said that so well and I am awfully glad that you have such a longing for life,”", "그 말을 어찌나 멋지게 하시던지, 형이 그토록 삶을 갈망한다니 나는 정말이지 기뻐요.\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."], ["“I think every one should love life above everything in the world.”", "\"사람은 누구나 세상 그 무엇보다도 삶을 사랑해야 한다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Love life more than the meaning of it?”", "\"삶의 의미보다도 삶을 더 사랑하라고?\""]], [["“Certainly, love it, regardless of logic as you say, it must be regardless of logic, and it’s only then one will understand the meaning of it.", "\"그럼요, 삶을 사랑하세요, 형 말처럼 논리를 아랑곳하지 말고요, 논리를 아랑곳하지 말아야 해요, 그래야 비로소 삶의 의미를 이해하게 될 테니까요."], ["I have thought so a long time.", "나는 오래전부터 그렇게 생각해 왔어요."], ["Half your work is done, Ivan, you love life, now you’ve only to try to do the second half and you are saved.”", "형 일의 절반은 이미 이루어진 셈이에요, 이반, 형은 삶을 사랑하니까요, 이제 나머지 절반만 해내려고 애쓰면 형은 구원받는 거예요.\""]], [["“You are trying to save me, but perhaps I am not lost!", "\"넌 나를 구원하려 드는구나, 하지만 어쩌면 난 길을 잃은 게 아닐지도 몰라!"], ["And what does your second half mean?”", "그런데 네가 말하는 그 나머지 절반이라는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["“Why, one has to raise up your dead, who perhaps have not died after all.", "\"그건, 형의 죽은 자들을 일으켜 세워야 한다는 거예요, 어쩌면 그들은 결국 죽지 않았는지도 모르니까요."], ["Come, let me have tea.", "자, 차를 좀 마실게요."], ["I am so glad of our talk, Ivan.”", "우리가 이렇게 이야기를 나누니 나는 너무 기뻐요, 이반.\""]], [["“I see you are feeling inspired.", "\"네가 영감에 찬 기분인 게 보이는구나."], ["I am awfully fond of such professions de foi from such—novices.", "나는 너 같은—수련사한테서 이런 신앙 고백을 듣는 게 무척 좋아."], ["You are a steadfast person, Alexey.", "너는 흔들림 없는 사람이야, 알렉세이."], ["Is it true that you mean to leave the monastery?”", "정말 수도원을 떠날 작정이라는 게 사실이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, my elder sends me out into the world.”", "\"네, 장로님이 나를 세상으로 내보내신답니다.\""]], [["“We shall see each other then in the world.", "\"그럼 우리는 세상에서 다시 만나겠구나."], ["We shall meet before I am thirty, when I shall begin to turn aside from the cup.", "내가 서른이 되어 잔에서 돌아서기 시작하기 전에 우리는 만나게 될 거야."], ["Father doesn’t want to turn aside from his cup till he is seventy, he dreams of hanging on to eighty in fact, so he says.", "아버지는 일흔이 되도록 자기 잔에서 돌아설 마음이 없으시지, 실은 여든까지 매달려 버티는 게 꿈이라고 직접 말씀하시거든."], ["He means it only too seriously, though he is a buffoon.", "어릿광대이긴 해도 그 말씀만은 지나칠 만큼 진지하셔."], ["He stands on a firm rock, too, he stands on his sensuality—though after we are thirty, indeed, there may be nothing else to stand on....", "아버지도 단단한 바위 위에 서 계신데, 그게 바로 당신의 육욕이지—하기야 우리가 서른을 넘기고 나면, 정말이지 그것 말고는 발붙일 데가 아무것도 없을지도 모르지……"], ["But to hang on to seventy is nasty, better only to thirty; one might retain ‘a shadow of nobility’ by deceiving oneself.", "하지만 일흔까지 매달리는 건 추접스러운 짓이야, 차라리 서른까지가 낫지. 그래야 자기 자신을 속여 가면서나마 '한 가닥 고결함의 그림자'쯤은 간직할 수 있거든."], ["Have you seen Dmitri to-day?”", "오늘 드미트리를 봤니?\""]], [["“No, but I saw Smerdyakov,”", "\"아뇨, 하지만 스메르댜코프는 봤어요.\""], ["and Alyosha rapidly, though minutely, described his meeting with Smerdyakov.", "그러면서 알료샤는 스메르댜코프와의 만남을 빠르면서도 세세하게 들려주었다."], ["Ivan began listening anxiously and questioned him.", "이반은 불안한 기색으로 귀를 기울이기 시작하더니 이것저것 캐물었다."]], [["“But he begged me not to tell Dmitri that he had told me about him,”", "\"하지만 그가 자기한테서 형 얘기를 들었다고 드미트리 형에게는 말하지 말아 달라고 신신당부했어요.\""], ["added Alyosha.", "알료샤가 덧붙였다."], ["Ivan frowned and pondered.", "이반은 눈살을 찌푸리며 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Are you frowning on Smerdyakov’s account?”", "\"스메르댜코프 때문에 눈살을 찌푸리는 거예요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, on his account.", "\"그래, 그 녀석 때문이지."], ["Damn him, I certainly did want to see Dmitri, but now there’s no need,”", "빌어먹을, 나는 분명 드미트리를 만나고 싶었지만, 이제는 그럴 필요가 없어졌어.\""], ["said Ivan reluctantly.", "이반이 마지못해 말했다."]], [["“But are you really going so soon, brother?”", "\"하지만 형, 정말로 그렇게 빨리 떠나는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“What of Dmitri and father?", "\"드미트리 형과 아버지는 어쩌고요?"], ["how will it end?”", "그 일이 어떻게 끝나려고요?\""], ["asked Alyosha anxiously.", "알료샤가 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“You are always harping upon it!", "\"넌 늘 그 타령이구나!"], ["What have I to do with it?", "그게 나하고 무슨 상관이야?"], ["Am I my brother Dmitri’s keeper?”", "내가 내 동생 드미트리를 지키는 자라도 된단 말이냐?\""], ["Ivan snapped irritably, but then he suddenly smiled bitterly.", "이반이 짜증스럽게 쏘아붙였으나, 곧 별안간 씁쓸하게 웃었다."], ["“Cain’s answer about his murdered brother, wasn’t it?", "\"살해당한 동생을 두고 카인이 했던 대답이지, 안 그러냐?"], ["Perhaps that’s what you’re thinking at this moment?", "어쩌면 지금 이 순간 네가 그걸 떠올리고 있는지도 모르겠구나?"], ["Well, damn it all, I can’t stay here to be their keeper, can I?", "그래, 빌어먹을, 내가 그들을 지키겠다고 여기 머물 수는 없잖니, 안 그래?"], ["I’ve finished what I had to do, and I am going.", "나는 내가 해야 할 일을 끝냈고, 이제 떠나는 거야."], ["Do you imagine I am jealous of Dmitri, that I’ve been trying to steal his beautiful Katerina Ivanovna for the last three months?", "너는 내가 드미트리를 질투한다고, 지난 석 달 동안 그의 아름다운 카테리나 이바노브나를 가로채려 했다고 생각하는 거냐?"], ["Nonsense, I had business of my own.", "말도 안 돼, 나는 내 나름의 볼일이 있었어."], ["I finished it.", "그걸 끝냈고."], ["I am going.", "이제 떠나는 거야."], ["I finished it just now, you were witness.”", "방금 그 일을 끝냈고, 네가 그 증인이었지.\""]], [["“At Katerina Ivanovna’s?”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나 댁에서요?\""]], [["“Yes, and I’ve released myself once for all.", "\"그래, 그래서 나는 단번에 나를 풀어 준 거야."], ["And after all, what have I to do with Dmitri?", "그리고 따지고 보면, 드미트리가 나하고 무슨 상관이야?"], ["Dmitri doesn’t come in.", "드미트리는 끼어들 자리가 없어."], ["I had my own business to settle with Katerina Ivanovna.", "나는 카테리나 이바노브나와 처리할 내 나름의 볼일이 있었던 거야."], ["You know, on the contrary, that Dmitri behaved as though there was an understanding between us.", "너도 알다시피, 오히려 드미트리는 우리 둘 사이에 무슨 양해라도 있는 양 굴었어."], ["I didn’t ask him to do it, but he solemnly handed her over to me and gave us his blessing.", "내가 그러라고 부탁한 적도 없는데, 그는 엄숙하게 그녀를 내게 넘겨주고 우리에게 축복까지 내렸지."], ["It’s all too funny.", "정말이지 우스워 죽겠어."], ["Ah, Alyosha, if you only knew how light my heart is now!", "아, 알료샤, 지금 내 마음이 얼마나 가벼운지 네가 알기만 한다면!"], ["Would you believe, it, I sat here eating my dinner and was nearly ordering champagne to celebrate my first hour of freedom.", "믿을 수 있겠니, 나는 여기 앉아 밥을 먹으면서 내 자유의 첫 시간을 자축하려고 샴페인을 시킬 뻔했다니까."], ["Tfoo!", "체!"], ["It’s been going on nearly six months, and all at once I’ve thrown it off.", "거의 여섯 달이나 끌어 온 일을, 단번에 훌훌 털어 버린 거야."], ["I could never have guessed even yesterday, how easy it would be to put an end to it if I wanted.”", "어제만 해도 나는 마음만 먹으면 이 일을 끝내는 게 이토록 쉬우리라고는 짐작도 못 했어.\""]], [["“You are speaking of your love, Ivan?”", "\"형의 사랑을 두고 하는 말이에요, 이반?\""]], [["“Of my love, if you like.", "\"내 사랑이라고 해도 좋아."], ["I fell in love with the young lady, I worried myself over her and she worried me.", "나는 그 아가씨와 사랑에 빠졌고, 그녀 때문에 속을 끓였고 그녀도 나 때문에 속을 끓였지."], ["I sat watching over her ...", "나는 앉아서 그녀를 지켜보고 있었는데……"], ["and all at once it’s collapsed!", "그러던 게 별안간 와르르 무너져 버린 거야!"], ["I spoke this morning with inspiration, but I went away and roared with laughter.", "오늘 아침에는 영감에 사로잡혀 떠들었지만, 그 자리를 나오면서 나는 껄껄 웃어 댔어."], ["Would you believe it?", "믿을 수 있겠니?"], ["Yes, it’s the literal truth.”", "그래, 글자 그대로 사실이야.\""]], [["“You seem very merry about it now,”", "\"지금은 그 일을 두고 무척 즐거워 보이네요.\""], ["observed Alyosha, looking into his face, which had suddenly grown brighter.", "알료샤가 별안간 환해진 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“But how could I tell that I didn’t care for her a bit!", "\"하지만 내가 그녀를 손톱만큼도 사랑하지 않았다는 걸 어떻게 알 수 있었겠니!"], ["Ha ha!", "하하!"], ["It appears after all I didn’t.", "알고 보니 결국 사랑하지 않았던 거야."], ["And yet how she attracted me!", "그런데도 그녀가 나를 어찌나 끌어당기던지!"], ["How attractive she was just now when I made my speech!", "방금 내가 연설을 늘어놓을 때 그녀가 어찌나 매력적이던지!"], ["And do you know she attracts me awfully even now, yet how easy it is to leave her.", "그리고 알겠니, 그녀는 지금도 나를 끔찍하게 끌어당겨, 그런데도 그녀를 떠나기란 어찌나 쉬운지."], ["Do you think I am boasting?”", "내가 허풍을 떤다고 생각하니?\""]], [["“No, only perhaps it wasn’t love.”", "\"아뇨, 다만 어쩌면 그건 사랑이 아니었나 봐요.\""]], [["“Alyosha,”", "\"알료샤,\""], ["laughed Ivan, “don’t make reflections about love, it’s unseemly for you.", "이반이 웃었다, \"사랑을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵하지 마라, 너한테는 어울리지 않아. 오늘 아침에 너 그 논쟁에 어찌나 불쑥 뛰어들던지!"], ["How you rushed into the discussion this morning!", "그것 때문에 너한테 입맞춰 주는 걸 깜빡했구나……"], ["I’ve forgotten to kiss you for it....", "그건 그렇고 그녀가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히던지!"], ["But how she tormented me!", "그건 정말이지 '생채기'"], ["It certainly was sitting by a ‘laceration.’ Ah, she knew how I loved her!", "옆에 앉아 있는 거나 마찬가지였어. 아, 그녀는 내가 자기를 얼마나 사랑하는지 알고 있었어!"], ["She loved me and not Dmitri,”", "그녀가 사랑한 건 드미트리가 아니라 나였어.\""], ["Ivan insisted gayly.", "이반이 명랑하게 우겼다."], ["“Her feeling for Dmitri was simply a self-laceration.", "\"드미트리에 대한 그녀의 감정은 그저 자기 학대였을 뿐이야."], ["All I told her just now was perfectly true, but the worst of it is, it may take her fifteen or twenty years to find out that she doesn’t care for Dmitri, and loves me whom she torments, and perhaps she may never find it out at all, in spite of her lesson to-day.", "방금 내가 그녀에게 한 말은 죄다 더없이 옳은 말이었지만, 가장 고약한 건 그녀가 자기는 드미트리를 사랑하지 않으며 오히려 자기가 괴롭히는 나를 사랑한다는 걸 깨닫는 데 십오 년이나 이십 년이 걸릴지도 모른다는 점이야, 어쩌면 오늘 같은 교훈을 얻고도 끝내 깨닫지 못할지도 모르지."], ["Well, it’s better so; I can simply go away for good.", "뭐, 그게 차라리 나아. 나는 그저 영영 떠나 버리면 그만이니까."], ["By the way, how is she now?", "그건 그렇고, 그녀는 지금 어떠냐?"], ["What happened after I departed?”", "내가 떠난 뒤에 무슨 일이 있었어?\""]], [["Alyosha told him she had been hysterical, and that she was now, he heard, unconscious and delirious.", "알료샤는 그녀가 발작을 일으켰으며, 듣자 하니 지금은 의식을 잃고 헛소리를 하고 있다고 형에게 말해 주었다."]], [["“Isn’t Madame Hohlakov laying it on?”", "\"호흘라코바 부인이 일을 부풀리는 거 아니냐?\""]], [["“I think not.”", "\"아닌 것 같아요.\""]], [["“I must find out.", "\"알아봐야겠다."], ["Nobody dies of hysterics, though.", "하기야 발작으로 죽는 사람은 없지."], ["They don’t matter.", "그런 건 아무것도 아니야."], ["God gave woman hysterics as a relief.", "하느님이 여자에게 발작을 주신 건 그게 위안이 되라고 그러신 거야."], ["I won’t go to her at all.", "나는 그녀에게 아예 가지 않겠어."], ["Why push myself forward again?”", "무엇하러 또 나서겠니?\""]], [["“But you told her that she had never cared for you.”", "\"하지만 형은 그녀가 형을 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없다고 말했잖아요.\""]], [["“I did that on purpose.", "\"일부러 그런 거야."], ["Alyosha, shall I call for some champagne?", "알료샤, 샴페인 좀 시킬까?"], ["Let us drink to my freedom.", "내 자유를 위해 건배하자."], ["Ah, if only you knew how glad I am!”", "아, 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 네가 알기만 한다면!\""]], [["“No, brother, we had better not drink,”", "\"아뇨, 형, 술은 마시지 않는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Alyosha suddenly.", "알료샤가 별안간 말했다."], ["“Besides I feel somehow depressed.”", "\"게다가 나는 어쩐지 마음이 울적해요.\""]], [["“Yes, you’ve been depressed a long time, I’ve noticed it.”", "\"그래, 너는 오래전부터 울적해 있었지, 나도 눈치챘어.\""]], [["“Have you settled to go to-morrow morning, then?”", "\"그럼 형은 내일 아침에 떠나기로 정한 거예요?\""]], [["“Morning?", "\"아침에?"], ["I didn’t say I should go in the morning....", "아침에 떠난다고는 말하지 않았는데……"], ["But perhaps it may be the morning.", "하지만 어쩌면 아침이 될지도 모르지."], ["Would you believe it, I dined here to-day only to avoid dining with the old man, I loathe him so.", "믿을 수 있겠니, 오늘 여기서 밥을 먹은 것도 그저 그 영감하고 같이 먹기 싫어서였어, 어찌나 그가 역겨운지."], ["I should have left long ago, so far as he is concerned.", "그 영감만 아니었다면 나는 진작에 떠났을 거야."], ["But why are you so worried about my going away?", "그런데 너는 어째서 내가 떠나는 일을 그토록 걱정하는 거냐?"], ["We’ve plenty of time before I go, an eternity!”", "내가 떠나기 전까지 우리한테는 시간이 넉넉해, 영원이 있다고!\""]], [["“If you are going away to-morrow, what do you mean by an eternity?”", "\"내일 떠난다면서 영원이라니 그게 무슨 말이에요?\""]], [["“But what does it matter to us?”", "\"하지만 그게 우리한테 무슨 상관이야?\""], ["laughed Ivan.", "이반이 웃었다."], ["“We’ve time enough for our talk, for what brought us here.", "\"우리가 이야기를 나누기에는, 우리를 여기로 데려온 그 일을 두고 이야기하기에는 시간이 넉넉하다는 거지."], ["Why do you look so surprised?", "왜 그렇게 놀란 얼굴을 하니?"], ["Answer: why have we met here?", "대답해 봐, 우리가 왜 여기서 만났겠니?"], ["To talk of my love for Katerina Ivanovna, of the old man and Dmitri?", "내가 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑한 이야기를 하려고? 영감과 드미트리 이야기를 하려고?"], ["of foreign travel?", "외국 여행 이야기를?"], ["of the fatal position of Russia?", "러시아의 운명적 처지를? 나폴레옹 황제를?"], ["Of the Emperor Napoleon? Is that it?”", "그런 걸 두고 만난 거냐?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아뇨.\""]], [["“Then you know what for.", "\"그럼 너도 무엇 때문인지 알겠지."], ["It’s different for other people; but we in our green youth have to settle the eternal questions first of all.", "다른 사람들이야 사정이 다르지만, 우리처럼 풋내기 젊은이들은 무엇보다도 먼저 영원한 문제들을 해결해야 해."], ["That’s what we care about.", "우리가 마음 쓰는 게 바로 그거지."], ["Young Russia is talking about nothing but the eternal questions now.", "요즘 젊은 러시아는 온통 영원한 문제들 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않아."], ["Just when the old folks are all taken up with practical questions.", "정작 늙은이들은 죄다 실제적인 문제에 매달려 있을 때 말이야."], ["Why have you been looking at me in expectation for the last three months?", "너는 어째서 지난 석 달 동안 기대에 찬 눈으로 나를 바라보았겠니?"], ["To ask me, ‘What do you believe, or don’t you believe at all?’ That’s what your eyes have been meaning for these three months, haven’t they?”", "나한테 '형은 무엇을 믿어요, 아니면 아예 아무것도 믿지 않아요?'라고 물으려고 그런 거잖아. 이 석 달 동안 네 눈이 뜻하던 게 바로 그거 아니었니?\""]], [["“Perhaps so,”", "\"어쩌면 그럴지도요.\""], ["smiled Alyosha.", "알료샤가 미소 지었다."], ["“You are not laughing at me, now, Ivan?”", "\"지금 나를 비웃는 거 아니죠, 이반?\""]], [["“Me laughing!", "\"내가 비웃는다고!"], ["I don’t want to wound my little brother who has been watching me with such expectation for three months.", "석 달 동안이나 그토록 기대에 차서 나를 지켜본 내 동생에게 상처를 주고 싶지는 않아."], ["Alyosha, look straight at me!", "알료샤, 나를 똑바로 봐라!"], ["Of course I am just such a little boy as you are, only not a novice.", "물론 나도 너와 똑같은 조그만 소년이야, 다만 수련사가 아닐 뿐이지."], ["And what have Russian boys been doing up till now, some of them, I mean?", "그런데 러시아 소년들은 지금껏 무얼 해 왔지, 그러니까 그중 일부 말이야?"], ["In this stinking tavern, for instance, here, they meet and sit down in a corner.", "가령 여기, 이 고약한 냄새 나는 술집 같은 데서, 그들은 만나 구석 자리에 앉아."], ["They’ve never met in their lives before and, when they go out of the tavern, they won’t meet again for forty years.", "평생 한 번도 만난 적 없는 사이고, 이 술집을 나가면 사십 년이 지나도 다시는 못 만날 사이지."], ["And what do they talk about in that momentary halt in the tavern?", "그런데 그 잠깐의 술집 휴식 동안 그들이 무슨 이야기를 하겠니?"], ["Of the eternal questions, of the existence of God and immortality.", "영원한 문제들, 하느님의 존재와 영혼 불멸에 관한 이야기지."], ["And those who do not believe in God talk of socialism or anarchism, of the transformation of all humanity on a new pattern, so that it all comes to the same, they’re the same questions turned inside out.", "그리고 하느님을 믿지 않는 자들은 사회주의나 무정부주의 이야기를, 인류 전체를 새로운 틀로 개조하는 이야기를 하지, 그러니 결국 다 같은 거야, 그건 안팎을 뒤집어 놓은 똑같은 문제들이거든."], ["And masses, masses of the most original Russian boys do nothing but talk of the eternal questions!", "그리고 무수히, 무수히 많은 더없이 독창적인 러시아 소년들이 영원한 문제들 말고는 아무것도 이야기하지 않아!"], ["Isn’t it so?”", "그렇지 않니?\""]], [["“Yes, for real Russians the questions of God’s existence and of immortality, or, as you say, the same questions turned inside out, come first and foremost, of course, and so they should,”", "\"네, 진정한 러시아인에게는 하느님의 존재와 영혼 불멸의 문제가, 형 말마따나 안팎을 뒤집어 놓은 똑같은 문제가, 물론 무엇보다도 먼저고, 또 마땅히 그래야 하지요.\""], ["said Alyosha, still watching his brother with the same gentle and inquiring smile.", "알료샤가 여전히 똑같은 부드럽고 캐묻는 듯한 미소로 형을 지켜보며 말했다."]], [["“Well, Alyosha, it’s sometimes very unwise to be a Russian at all, but anything stupider than the way Russian boys spend their time one can hardly imagine.", "\"그런데 말이다, 알료샤, 도대체 러시아인으로 산다는 게 때로는 더없이 어리석은 일이지만, 러시아 소년들이 시간을 보내는 방식보다 더 어리석은 건 도무지 상상하기 어려워."], ["But there’s one Russian boy called Alyosha I am awfully fond of.”", "하지만 그중에 알료샤라는 러시아 소년이 하나 있는데, 나는 그 녀석을 끔찍이 좋아하지.\""]], [["“How nicely you put that in!”", "\"그 말을 어쩜 그렇게 멋지게 끼워 넣어요!\""], ["Alyosha laughed suddenly.", "알료샤가 별안간 웃었다."]], [["“Well, tell me where to begin, give your orders.", "\"자, 어디서부터 시작할지 말해 봐, 명령을 내려라."], ["The existence of God, eh?”", "하느님의 존재부터, 어때?\""]], [["“Begin where you like.", "\"아무 데서나 시작하세요."], ["You declared yesterday at father’s that there was no God.”", "형은 어제 아버지 댁에서 하느님은 없다고 선언했잖아요.\""], ["Alyosha looked searchingly at his brother.", "알료샤가 형을 살피듯 바라보았다."]], [["“I said that yesterday at dinner on purpose to tease you and I saw your eyes glow.", "\"어제 식사 자리에서 그렇게 말한 건 너를 약 올리려는 거였고, 네 눈이 이글거리는 걸 봤지."], ["But now I’ve no objection to discussing with you, and I say so very seriously.", "하지만 지금은 너와 그 문제를 논하는 데 이의가 없어, 진심으로 하는 말이야."], ["I want to be friends with you, Alyosha, for I have no friends and want to try it.", "나는 너와 친구가 되고 싶다, 알료샤, 나한테는 친구가 없거든, 그래서 한번 해 보고 싶어."], ["Well, only fancy, perhaps I too accept God,”", "그런데, 한번 상상해 봐, 어쩌면 나도 하느님을 받아들이는지도 몰라.\""], ["laughed Ivan; “that’s a surprise for you, isn’t it?”", "이반이 웃었다. \"너한테는 뜻밖이지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“Yes, of course, if you are not joking now.”", "\"네, 물론이죠, 지금 형이 농담하는 게 아니라면요.\""]], [["“Joking?", "\"농담이라고?"], ["I was told at the elder’s yesterday that I was joking.", "어제 장로한테서도 내가 농담을 한다는 말을 들었지."], ["You know, dear boy, there was an old sinner in the eighteenth century who declared that, if there were no God, he would have to be invented.", "그런데 말이다, 얘야, 십팔 세기에 어떤 늙은 죄인이 있었는데, 그가 선언하기를, 만약 하느님이 없다면 하느님을 발명해야 할 거라고 했어."], ["S’il n’existait pas Dieu, il faudrait l’inventer.", "'하느님이 존재하지 않는다면, 그분을 발명해야 하리라.'"], ["And man has actually invented God.", "그리고 인간은 실제로 하느님을 발명해 냈지."], ["And what’s strange, what would be marvelous, is not that God should really exist; the marvel is that such an idea, the idea of the necessity of God, could enter the head of such a savage, vicious beast as man.", "그런데 이상한 건, 정말 놀라운 건, 하느님이 정녕 존재한다는 게 아니라, 그런 관념이, 곧 하느님의 필요성이라는 관념이 인간처럼 야만스럽고 사악한 짐승의 머릿속에 떠오를 수 있었다는 점이야."], ["So holy it is, so touching, so wise and so great a credit it does to man.", "그건 그토록 거룩하고, 그토록 가슴 뭉클하고, 그토록 지혜로워서 인간에게 그토록 큰 명예가 되는 일이지."], ["As for me, I’ve long resolved not to think whether man created God or God man.", "나로 말하자면, 인간이 하느님을 창조했는지 하느님이 인간을 창조했는지는 오래전부터 생각하지 않기로 작정했어."], ["And I won’t go through all the axioms laid down by Russian boys on that subject, all derived from European hypotheses; for what’s a hypothesis there, is an axiom with the Russian boy, and not only with the boys but with their teachers too, for our Russian professors are often just the same boys themselves.", "그리고 그 주제를 두고 러시아 소년들이 정해 놓은 온갖 공리(公理)들을 일일이 짚어 보지는 않겠다, 죄다 유럽의 가설에서 끌어온 것들이거든. 거기서는 가설인 것이 러시아 소년에게는 공리고, 소년들뿐 아니라 그들을 가르치는 선생들에게도 그래, 우리 러시아 교수들도 흔히 그 소년들과 똑같으니까."], ["And so I omit all the hypotheses.", "그래서 나는 모든 가설을 건너뛰겠어."], ["For what are we aiming at now?", "지금 우리가 노리는 게 뭐냐?"], ["I am trying to explain as quickly as possible my essential nature, that is what manner of man I am, what I believe in, and for what I hope, that’s it, isn’t it?", "나는 되도록 빨리 내 본질을, 곧 내가 어떤 부류의 인간인지, 무엇을 믿는지, 무엇을 바라는지를 설명하려는 거야, 바로 그거지, 안 그러냐?"], ["And therefore I tell you that I accept God simply.", "그러니 너한테 이렇게 말하겠다, 나는 하느님을 그냥 받아들인다고."], ["But you must note this: if God exists and if He really did create the world, then, as we all know, He created it according to the geometry of Euclid and the human mind with the conception of only three dimensions in space.", "하지만 이건 새겨들어야 해. 만약 하느님이 존재하고 정녕 그분이 세계를 창조하셨다면, 우리 모두 알다시피 그분은 유클리드 기하학에 따라, 또 공간을 오직 삼차원으로만 파악하는 인간의 정신에 따라 세계를 창조하셨지."], ["Yet there have been and still are geometricians and philosophers, and even some of the most distinguished, who doubt whether the whole universe, or to speak more widely the whole of being, was only created in Euclid’s geometry; they even dare to dream that two parallel lines, which according to Euclid can never meet on earth, may meet somewhere in infinity.", "그런데 예나 지금이나 기하학자와 철학자들이, 심지어 그중 가장 저명한 이들 중에도, 과연 온 우주가, 더 넓게 말해 존재 전체가 유클리드 기하학 안에서만 창조되었는지 의심하는 자들이 있었어. 그들은 심지어 유클리드에 따르면 지상에서는 결코 만날 수 없는 두 평행선이 무한 속 어딘가에서는 만날 수도 있다고 감히 꿈꾸기까지 하지."], ["I have come to the conclusion that, since I can’t understand even that, I can’t expect to understand about God.", "나는 이런 결론에 이르렀어, 그것조차 이해하지 못하는 내가 하느님에 관해 이해하기를 바랄 수는 없다고 말이야."], ["I acknowledge humbly that I have no faculty for settling such questions, I have a Euclidian earthly mind, and how could I solve problems that are not of this world?", "나는 그런 문제들을 해결할 능력이 내게 없음을 겸허히 인정해, 나는 유클리드적인 지상의 정신을 지녔으니, 어떻게 이 세상의 것이 아닌 문제들을 풀 수 있겠니?"], ["And I advise you never to think about it either, my dear Alyosha, especially about God, whether He exists or not.", "그러니 너에게도 충고하건대, 그런 건 절대로 생각하지 마라, 사랑하는 알료샤, 특히 하느님에 관해서는, 그분이 존재하는지 안 하는지 말이야."], ["All such questions are utterly inappropriate for a mind created with an idea of only three dimensions.", "그런 문제들은 죄다 오직 삼차원의 관념만 지니고 창조된 정신에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것들이거든."], ["And so I accept God and am glad to, and what’s more, I accept His wisdom, His purpose—which are utterly beyond our ken; I believe in the underlying order and the meaning of life; I believe in the eternal harmony in which they say we shall one day be blended.", "그래서 나는 하느님을 받아들이고, 또 기꺼이 받아들이며, 게다가 그분의 지혜와 그분의 목적까지—우리의 이해를 완전히 넘어서는 것들이지만—받아들여. 나는 삶의 밑바닥에 깔린 질서와 의미를 믿어, 우리가 언젠가 그 속에 녹아들리라고들 말하는 그 영원한 조화를 믿어."], ["I believe in the Word to Which the universe is striving, and Which Itself was ‘with God,’ and Which Itself is God and so on, and so on, to infinity.", "나는 온 우주가 그것을 향해 나아가는 그 말씀을 믿어, 그 자체로 '하느님과 함께' 있었고, 그 자체로 곧 하느님인 그 말씀을 말이야, 등등, 등등, 무한히."], ["There are all sorts of phrases for it.", "그것을 가리키는 온갖 표현이 다 있지."], ["I seem to be on the right path, don’t I?", "나는 올바른 길을 가고 있는 것 같지, 안 그래?"], ["Yet would you believe it, in the final result I don’t accept this world of God’s, and, although I know it exists, I don’t accept it at all.", "그런데 믿을 수 있겠니, 결국 나는 이 하느님의 세계를 받아들이지 않아, 그것이 존재한다는 걸 알면서도 도무지 받아들이지 못한다는 거야."], ["It’s not that I don’t accept God, you must understand, it’s the world created by Him I don’t and cannot accept.", "내가 하느님을 받아들이지 않는다는 게 아니야, 이걸 알아 둬야 해, 내가 받아들이지 못하고 받아들일 수 없는 건 그분이 창조하신 세계라는 거지."], ["Let me make it plain.", "분명하게 말해 줄게."], ["I believe like a child that suffering will be healed and made up for, that all the humiliating absurdity of human contradictions will vanish like a pitiful mirage, like the despicable fabrication of the impotent and infinitely small Euclidian mind of man, that in the world’s finale, at the moment of eternal harmony, something so precious will come to pass that it will suffice for all hearts, for the comforting of all resentments, for the atonement of all the crimes of humanity, of all the blood they’ve shed; that it will make it not only possible to forgive but to justify all that has happened with men—but though all that may come to pass, I don’t accept it.", "나는 어린아이처럼 믿어, 고통은 치유되고 보상받으리라고, 인간의 모순이 빚어내는 그 모든 굴욕적인 부조리가 가련한 신기루처럼, 무력하고 한없이 미소한 인간의 유클리드적 정신이 지어낸 비루한 날조처럼 사라져 버리리라고, 세계의 종막에 이르러 영원한 조화의 순간이 오면 그토록 소중한 무언가가 일어나서 그것이 모든 가슴을 채우고, 모든 원한을 달래고, 인류의 모든 죄악을, 그들이 흘린 모든 피를 속죄하기에 충분하리라고, 그리하여 인간들 사이에 일어난 모든 일을 용서할 수 있게 될 뿐 아니라 정당화할 수 있게까지 되리라고 말이야—하지만 그 모든 일이 일어난다 해도, 나는 받아들이지 않아."], ["I won’t accept it.", "받아들이지 않을 거야."], ["Even if parallel lines do meet and I see it myself, I shall see it and say that they’ve met, but still I won’t accept it.", "설령 평행선이 정말 만나고 내 눈으로 그걸 직접 본다 해도, 나는 그걸 보고 그것들이 만났다고 말은 하겠지만, 그래도 받아들이지는 않을 거야."], ["That’s what’s at the root of me, Alyosha; that’s my creed.", "바로 그게 내 밑바닥에 깔려 있는 거야, 알료샤. 그게 내 신조지."], ["I am in earnest in what I say.", "나는 진지하게 하는 말이야."], ["I began our talk as stupidly as I could on purpose, but I’ve led up to my confession, for that’s all you want.", "우리 이야기를 일부러 되도록 어리석게 시작했지만, 결국 내 고백으로 이끌어 온 거야, 네가 원하는 게 바로 그거였으니까."], ["You didn’t want to hear about God, but only to know what the brother you love lives by.", "너는 하느님에 관한 이야기를 듣고 싶었던 게 아니라, 단지 네가 사랑하는 형이 무얼 의지하며 살아가는지를 알고 싶었던 거지."], ["And so I’ve told you.”", "그래서 너한테 말해 준 거야.\""]], [["Ivan concluded his long tirade with marked and unexpected feeling.", "이반은 그 긴 장광설을 뜻밖에도 도드라진 감정으로 맺었다."]], [["“And why did you begin ‘as stupidly as you could’?”", "\"그런데 형은 왜 '되도록 어리석게' 시작했어요?\""], ["asked Alyosha, looking dreamily at him.", "알료샤가 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“To begin with, for the sake of being Russian.", "\"우선은 러시아인이라는 이유에서지."], ["Russian conversations on such subjects are always carried on inconceivably stupidly.", "그런 주제를 두고 벌이는 러시아인의 대화는 언제나 상상도 못 할 만큼 어리석게 진행되거든."], ["And secondly, the stupider one is, the closer one is to reality.", "그리고 둘째로, 어리석을수록 현실에 더 가까운 법이야."], ["The stupider one is, the clearer one is.", "어리석을수록 더 명료하지."], ["Stupidity is brief and artless, while intelligence wriggles and hides itself.", "어리석음은 간결하고 꾸밈이 없는데, 영리함은 몸을 비틀어 자기를 숨기거든."], ["Intelligence is a knave, but stupidity is honest and straightforward.", "영리함은 악당이지만, 어리석음은 정직하고 솔직해."], ["I’ve led the conversation to my despair, and the more stupidly I have presented it, the better for me.”", "나는 대화를 내 절망으로 이끌어 왔고, 그것을 어리석게 제시할수록 나한테는 더 나았던 거야.\""]], [["“You will explain why you don’t accept the world?”", "\"형이 왜 세계를 받아들이지 않는지 설명해 줄 거죠?\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“To be sure I will, it’s not a secret, that’s what I’ve been leading up to.", "\"물론 설명하고말고, 그건 비밀이 아니야, 바로 그 이야기로 이끌어 온 거니까."], ["Dear little brother, I don’t want to corrupt you or to turn you from your stronghold, perhaps I want to be healed by you.”", "사랑하는 동생아, 나는 너를 타락시키거나 네 굳건한 보루에서 끌어내고 싶은 게 아니야, 어쩌면 나는 너한테서 치유받고 싶은 건지도 몰라.\""], ["Ivan smiled suddenly quite like a little gentle child.", "이반이 별안간, 영락없이 어리고 온순한 아이처럼 미소 지었다."], ["Alyosha had never seen such a smile on his face before.", "알료샤는 형의 얼굴에서 그런 미소를 그전에는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."]], [["# Chapter IV. Rebellion", "# Chapter IV. Rebellion"]], [["“I must make you one confession,”", "\"내가 너한테 한 가지 고백할 게 있다.\""], ["Ivan began.", "이반이 입을 열었다."], ["“I could never understand how one can love one’s neighbors.", "\"나는 어떻게 사람이 제 이웃을 사랑할 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["It’s just one’s neighbors, to my mind, that one can’t love, though one might love those at a distance.", "내 생각으로는 멀리 떨어져 있는 사람이라면 또 몰라도, 바로 그 이웃이라는 자들이야말로 사랑할 수 없는 존재다."], ["I once read somewhere of John the Merciful, a saint, that when a hungry, frozen beggar came to him, he took him into his bed, held him in his arms, and began breathing into his mouth, which was putrid and loathsome from some awful disease.", "언젠가 어디선가 자비로운 자 요한이라는 성인에 대해 읽은 적이 있는데, 굶주리고 얼어붙은 거지가 그를 찾아왔을 때 그는 거지를 제 잠자리에 받아들여 품에 안고, 무슨 끔찍한 병으로 썩어 문드러진 역겨운 입에 숨을 불어넣기 시작했다는 거다."], ["I am convinced that he did that from ‘self-laceration,’ from the self-laceration of falsity, for the sake of the charity imposed by duty, as a penance laid on him.", "나는 확신한다, 그가 그렇게 한 것은 '자기 학대'에서, 거짓으로 자기를 괴롭히는 데서, 의무가 강제하는 자선을 위하여, 스스로에게 내린 속죄로서 그리한 것이라고."], ["For any one to love a man, he must be hidden, for as soon as he shows his face, love is gone.”", "누군가가 한 사람을 사랑하려면 그자는 모습을 감추고 있어야 한다, 왜냐하면 그가 얼굴을 드러내는 순간 사랑은 사라져 버리니까.\""]], [["“Father Zossima has talked of that more than once,”", "\"조시마 장로께서도 그 이야기를 여러 번 하셨어요.\""], ["observed Alyosha; “he, too, said that the face of a man often hinders many people not practiced in love, from loving him.", "알료샤가 말했다. \"장로님도 사랑에 익숙하지 못한 많은 사람들에게는 한 인간의 얼굴이 오히려 그를 사랑하는 데 방해가 되는 경우가 많다고 하셨거든요."], ["But yet there’s a great deal of love in mankind, and almost Christ-like love.", "하지만 그래도 인류 안에는 사랑이 많이 있어요, 거의 그리스도와 같은 사랑이요."], ["I know that myself, Ivan.”", "나는 그걸 알아요, 이반 형.\""]], [["“Well, I know nothing of it so far, and can’t understand it, and the innumerable mass of mankind are with me there.", "\"흠, 나는 아직 그런 건 전혀 모르겠고, 이해할 수도 없는데, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 인류가 그 점에서는 나와 한편이다."], ["The question is, whether that’s due to men’s bad qualities or whether it’s inherent in their nature.", "문제는, 그게 인간의 나쁜 자질 때문인지 아니면 그들의 본성에 본래부터 박혀 있는 것인지 하는 거지."], ["To my thinking, Christ-like love for men is a miracle impossible on earth.", "내 생각으로는, 인간을 향한 그리스도와 같은 사랑이란 이 땅에서는 불가능한 기적이다."], ["He was God.", "그분은 신이셨다."], ["But we are not gods.", "하지만 우리는 신이 아니지."], ["Suppose I, for instance, suffer intensely.", "가령 내가 격심하게 고통받는다고 해 보자."], ["Another can never know how much I suffer, because he is another and not I.", "다른 사람은 내가 얼마나 고통받는지 결코 알 수 없다, 왜냐하면 그는 다른 사람이지 내가 아니니까."], ["And what’s more, a man is rarely ready to admit another’s suffering (as though it were a distinction).", "게다가 더한 것은, 인간이란 좀처럼 남의 고통을 인정하려 들지 않는다는 거다(마치 그것이 무슨 영예라도 되는 양 말이지)."], ["Why won’t he admit it, do you think?", "그가 왜 그걸 인정하지 않으려 하는지, 너는 짐작이 가니?"], ["Because I smell unpleasant, because I have a stupid face, because I once trod on his foot.", "내게서 불쾌한 냄새가 나니까, 내 얼굴이 멍청하게 생겼으니까, 내가 언젠가 그의 발을 밟았으니까 그러는 거다. 게다가 고통에도 여러 종류가 있다."], ["Besides, there is suffering and suffering; degrading, humiliating suffering such as humbles me—hunger, for instance—my benefactor will perhaps allow me; but when you come to higher suffering—for an idea, for instance—he will very rarely admit that, perhaps because my face strikes him as not at all what he fancies a man should have who suffers for an idea.", "나를 비천하게 만드는 모멸적이고 굴욕적인 고통—이를테면 굶주림 같은 것—이라면 나의 은인은 어쩌면 인정해 줄지도 모른다. 그러나 더 높은 고통—가령 어떤 이념을 위한 고통—에 이르면, 그는 그것을 좀처럼 인정하려 들지 않는다, 어쩌면 내 얼굴이, 이념을 위해 고통받는 사람이라면 마땅히 이러이러해야 한다고 그가 멋대로 상상하는 모습과는 전혀 다르게 보이기 때문이겠지."], ["And so he deprives me instantly of his favor, and not at all from badness of heart.", "그래서 그는 당장에 내게서 호의를 거두어 버린다, 그것도 결코 마음이 나빠서가 아니다."], ["Beggars, especially genteel beggars, ought never to show themselves, but to ask for charity through the newspapers.", "거지들은, 특히 점잖은 거지들은, 결코 제 모습을 드러내서는 안 되고, 신문을 통해서나 자선을 청해야 한다."], ["One can love one’s neighbors in the abstract, or even at a distance, but at close quarters it’s almost impossible.", "사람은 추상적으로, 혹은 멀리 떨어져서라면 제 이웃을 사랑할 수 있지만, 가까이서는 그것이 거의 불가능하다."], ["If it were as on the stage, in the ballet, where if beggars come in, they wear silken rags and tattered lace and beg for alms dancing gracefully, then one might like looking at them.", "만약 무대 위에서처럼, 발레에서처럼, 거지들이 비단 누더기와 해진 레이스를 걸치고 우아하게 춤추며 동냥을 청한다면, 그때야 사람은 그들을 바라보기를 좋아할지도 모른다."], ["But even then we should not love them. But enough of that.", "하지만 그렇다 해도 우리는 그들을 사랑하지는 않을 거다."], ["I simply wanted to show you my point of view.", "아무튼 그 얘기는 이쯤 해 두자."], ["I meant to speak of the suffering of mankind generally, but we had better confine ourselves to the sufferings of the children.", "나는 그저 내 관점을 너한테 보여 주고 싶었을 뿐이니까. 나는 인류 전체의 고통에 대해 말하려 했지만, 차라리 아이들의 고통에 한정하는 편이 낫겠다."], ["That reduces the scope of my argument to a tenth of what it would be.", "그러면 내 논지의 범위가 본래의 십분의 일로 줄어들지."], ["Still we’d better keep to the children, though it does weaken my case.", "그래도 아이들에게 국한하는 편이 낫다, 비록 그게 내 주장을 약화시키긴 하지만."], ["But, in the first place, children can be loved even at close quarters, even when they are dirty, even when they are ugly (I fancy, though, children never are ugly).", "그러나 첫째로, 아이들은 가까이서도 사랑할 수 있다, 더럽혀져 있을 때조차, 못생겼을 때조차(하긴 아이들이 못생긴 경우란 없는 것 같지만)."], ["The second reason why I won’t speak of grown-up people is that, besides being disgusting and unworthy of love, they have a compensation—they’ve eaten the apple and know good and evil, and they have become ‘like gods.’ They go on eating it still.", "내가 어른들에 대해 말하지 않으려는 두 번째 이유는, 그들은 역겹고 사랑받을 자격이 없는 데다가, 보상이라는 것을 가지고 있기 때문이다—그들은 사과를 먹었고 선과 악을 알게 되어, '신처럼' 되어 버렸으니까."], ["But the children haven’t eaten anything, and are so far innocent.", "그들은 지금도 여전히 그 사과를 먹고 있지. 하지만 아이들은 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 그래서 아직은 무죄하다."], ["Are you fond of children, Alyosha?", "너는 아이들을 좋아하니, 알료샤?"], ["I know you are, and you will understand why I prefer to speak of them.", "네가 좋아한다는 걸 안다, 그러니 너는 내가 왜 아이들에 대해 말하기를 더 좋아하는지 이해할 거다."], ["If they, too, suffer horribly on earth, they must suffer for their fathers’ sins, they must be punished for their fathers, who have eaten the apple; but that reasoning is of the other world and is incomprehensible for the heart of man here on earth.", "만약 아이들마저 이 땅에서 끔찍하게 고통받는다면, 그것은 틀림없이 제 아비들의 죄 때문에 고통받는 것이고, 사과를 먹은 제 아비들을 대신해 벌받는 것이다. 그러나 그런 논리는 저세상의 것이며, 이 땅에 사는 인간의 마음으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 것이다."], ["The innocent must not suffer for another’s sins, and especially such innocents!", "무죄한 자가 남의 죄 때문에 고통받아서는 안 된다, 더구나 그렇게 무죄한 자들이라면 더욱!"], ["You may be surprised at me, Alyosha, but I am awfully fond of children, too.", "너는 나를 보고 놀랄지도 모르겠다, 알료샤, 그러나 나 역시 아이들을 끔찍이 좋아한다."], ["And observe, cruel people, the violent, the rapacious, the Karamazovs are sometimes very fond of children.", "그리고 잘 봐 둬라, 잔인한 사람들, 난폭한 자들, 탐욕스러운 자들, 카라마조프 집안 사람들도 더러는 아이들을 무척 좋아한단다."], ["Children while they are quite little—up to seven, for instance—are so remote from grown-up people; they are different creatures, as it were, of a different species.", "아이들은 아주 어릴 적에는—가령 일곱 살까지는—어른들과 그토록 동떨어진 존재라서, 말하자면 다른 종에 속한 다른 피조물이다."], ["I knew a criminal in prison who had, in the course of his career as a burglar, murdered whole families, including several children.", "나는 감옥에서 한 범죄자를 알았는데, 그자는 강도질을 하는 동안 여러 아이들까지 포함해서 온 가족을 통째로 살해한 자였다."], ["But when he was in prison, he had a strange affection for them.", "그런데 막상 감옥에 들어와서는 아이들에게 묘한 애착을 보였다."], ["He spent all his time at his window, watching the children playing in the prison yard.", "그는 온종일 창가에 붙어서서 감옥 마당에서 노는 아이들을 지켜보았다."], ["He trained one little boy to come up to his window and made great friends with him....", "그는 한 어린 사내아이를 길들여 제 창가로 오게 만들고는 그 아이와 둘도 없는 친구가 되었지……"], ["You don’t know why I am telling you all this, Alyosha?", "내가 왜 너한테 이런 얘기를 다 늘어놓는지 모르겠지, 알료샤?"], ["My head aches and I am sad.”", "머리가 아프고 마음이 울적하구나.\""]], [["“You speak with a strange air,”", "\"형 말투가 좀 이상해요.\""], ["observed Alyosha uneasily, “as though you were not quite yourself.”", "알료샤가 불안해하며 말했다. \"마치 형이 형 자신이 아닌 것 같아요.\""]], [["“By the way, a Bulgarian I met lately in Moscow,”", "\"그건 그렇고, 얼마 전 모스크바에서 만난 어느 불가리아 사람이,\""], ["Ivan went on, seeming not to hear his brother’s words, “told me about the crimes committed by Turks and Circassians in all parts of Bulgaria through fear of a general rising of the Slavs.", "이반은 동생의 말을 듣지 못한 듯이 말을 이었다, \"슬라브인들이 전면적으로 봉기할까 두려워한 터키인과 체르케스인들이 불가리아 전역에서 저지른 범죄에 대해 내게 이야기해 주었다."], ["They burn villages, murder, outrage women and children, they nail their prisoners by the ears to the fences, leave them so till morning, and in the morning they hang them—all sorts of things you can’t imagine.", "그들은 마을을 불태우고, 사람을 죽이고, 여자와 아이들을 능욕하고, 포로들의 귀를 울타리에 못 박아 아침까지 그대로 두었다가 아침이 되면 목매달아 죽인다—네가 상상도 못 할 온갖 짓들을 다 한다."], ["People talk sometimes of bestial cruelty, but that’s a great injustice and insult to the beasts; a beast can never be so cruel as a man, so artistically cruel.", "사람들은 더러 짐승 같은 잔인성이라고 말하지만, 그건 짐승들에 대한 크나큰 부당함이자 모욕이다. 짐승은 결코 인간만큼 잔인할 수가 없다, 그렇게 예술적으로 잔인할 수는 없다."], ["The tiger only tears and gnaws, that’s all he can do.", "호랑이는 그저 찢고 물어뜯을 뿐, 그게 그놈이 할 수 있는 전부다."], ["He would never think of nailing people by the ears, even if he were able to do it.", "호랑이는 설령 할 수 있다 한들 사람의 귀를 못 박을 생각 따위는 결코 하지 못할 거다."], ["These Turks took a pleasure in torturing children, too; cutting the unborn child from the mother’s womb, and tossing babies up in the air and catching them on the points of their bayonets before their mothers’ eyes.", "이 터키인들은 아이들을 고문하는 데서도 쾌감을 느꼈다. 어머니의 자궁에서 아직 태어나지도 않은 아이를 잘라 내고, 갓난아기들을 공중으로 던져 올렸다가 그 어미들의 눈앞에서 총검 끝으로 받아 냈다."], ["Doing it before the mothers’ eyes was what gave zest to the amusement.", "어미들의 눈앞에서 그 짓을 한다는 것, 바로 그게 그 유희에 묘미를 더해 주었던 거지."], ["Here is another scene that I thought very interesting.", "여기 내가 무척 흥미롭게 여긴 또 다른 장면이 있다."], ["Imagine a trembling mother with her baby in her arms, a circle of invading Turks around her.", "한번 떠올려 봐라, 품에 아기를 안고 벌벌 떠는 어머니와, 그녀를 에워싼 침략자 터키인들의 무리를."], ["They’ve planned a diversion: they pet the baby, laugh to make it laugh.", "그들은 한 가지 여흥을 꾸민다. 아기를 어르고, 아기를 웃기려고 까르르 웃어 보인다."], ["They succeed, the baby laughs.", "마침내 성공해서 아기가 웃는다."], ["At that moment a Turk points a pistol four inches from the baby’s face.", "바로 그 순간 한 터키인이 아기 얼굴에서 네 치쯤 되는 거리에 권총을 겨눈다."], ["The baby laughs with glee, holds out its little hands to the pistol, and he pulls the trigger in the baby’s face and blows out its brains.", "아기는 신이 나서 까르르 웃으며 권총을 향해 조그만 두 손을 뻗고, 그자는 아기 얼굴에 대고 방아쇠를 당겨 그 머리통을 날려 버린다."], ["Artistic, wasn’t it?", "예술적이지 않으냐?"], ["By the way, Turks are particularly fond of sweet things, they say.”", "그건 그렇고, 터키인들은 단것을 유난히 좋아한다더라.\""]], [["“Brother, what are you driving at?”", "\"형, 대체 무슨 말을 하려는 거예요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“I think if the devil doesn’t exist, but man has created him, he has created him in his own image and likeness.”", "\"내 생각엔, 만약 악마가 존재하지 않고 인간이 악마를 창조해 낸 거라면, 인간은 제 형상과 모습을 본떠 악마를 창조한 거다.\""]], [["“Just as he did God, then?”", "\"그럼 신을 창조했을 때와 똑같이요?\""], ["observed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“‘It’s wonderful how you can turn words,’ as Polonius says in Hamlet,”", "\"'어쩌면 그렇게 말을 잘도 비틀어 대는지', 『햄릿』에서 폴로니우스가 하는 말이지.\""], ["laughed Ivan.", "이반이 웃었다."], ["“You turn my words against me.", "\"너는 내 말을 거꾸로 돌려 나를 찌르는구나."], ["Well, I am glad.", "흠, 좋다, 기쁘다."], ["Yours must be a fine God, if man created Him in his image and likeness.", "인간이 제 형상과 모습대로 신을 창조했다면, 네 신은 틀림없이 훌륭한 신일 거다."], ["You asked just now what I was driving at.", "너는 방금 내가 무슨 말을 하려는 거냐고 물었지."], ["You see, I am fond of collecting certain facts, and, would you believe, I even copy anecdotes of a certain sort from newspapers and books, and I’ve already got a fine collection.", "알다시피 나는 어떤 종류의 사실들을 수집하기를 좋아한다, 그리고 믿어지겠니, 신문이며 책에서 어떤 종류의 일화들을 베껴 두기까지 하는데, 벌써 제법 그럴듯한 수집품이 모였단다."], ["The Turks, of course, have gone into it, but they are foreigners.", "물론 터키인들 얘기도 거기 들어갔지만, 그들은 외국인이지."], ["I have specimens from home that are even better than the Turks.", "내겐 본국에서 나온, 터키인들보다 한층 더 나은 표본들이 있다."], ["You know we prefer beating—rods and scourges—that’s our national institution.", "너도 알다시피 우리는 매질을 더 즐긴다—회초리와 채찍 말이다—그게 우리 민족의 제도지."], ["Nailing ears is unthinkable for us, for we are, after all, Europeans.", "귀를 못 박는 짓은 우리한테는 생각도 할 수 없는 일이다, 어쨌든 우리는 유럽인이니까."], ["But the rod and the scourge we have always with us and they cannot be taken from us.", "하지만 회초리와 채찍은 우리가 늘 곁에 지니고 있고, 그건 우리한테서 빼앗아 갈 수도 없는 거다."], ["Abroad now they scarcely do any beating.", "요즘 외국에서는 매질을 거의 하지 않지."], ["Manners are more humane, or laws have been passed, so that they don’t dare to flog men now.", "풍습이 더 인도적이 되었거나, 아니면 법이 제정되어 이제는 감히 사람을 매질하지 못하게 되었으니까."], ["But they make up for it in another way just as national as ours.", "하지만 그들은 우리 못지않게 민족적인 또 다른 방식으로 그걸 벌충한다."], ["And so national that it would be practically impossible among us, though I believe we are being inoculated with it, since the religious movement began in our aristocracy.", "그것도 어찌나 민족적인지 우리한테서는 거의 일어날 수 없는 일이지, 비록 우리 귀족 사회에 종교 운동이 시작된 이래로 우리도 거기에 물들어 가고 있는 것 같긴 하지만."], ["I have a charming pamphlet, translated from the French, describing how, quite recently, five years ago, a murderer, Richard, was executed—a young man, I believe, of three and twenty, who repented and was converted to the Christian faith at the very scaffold.", "내겐 프랑스어에서 번역된 매혹적인 소책자가 한 권 있는데, 그 안에는 바로 얼마 전, 오 년 전에, 리샤르라는 살인자가 처형된 이야기가 적혀 있다—내 생각에 스물세 살쯤 된 젊은이로, 바로 그 단두대 위에서 회개하고 기독교 신앙으로 개종한 자였다."], ["This Richard was an illegitimate child who was given as a child of six by his parents to some shepherds on the Swiss mountains.", "이 리샤르는 사생아로서, 여섯 살 적에 부모에 의해 스위스 산속의 어느 목자들에게 넘겨진 아이였다."], ["They brought him up to work for them.", "목자들은 자기들을 위해 일을 시키려고 그를 길렀다."], ["He grew up like a little wild beast among them.", "그는 그들 틈에서 한 마리 작은 들짐승처럼 자라났다."], ["The shepherds taught him nothing, and scarcely fed or clothed him, but sent him out at seven to herd the flock in cold and wet, and no one hesitated or scrupled to treat him so.", "목자들은 그에게 아무것도 가르치지 않았고, 먹을 것도 입을 것도 거의 주지 않으면서, 일곱 살이 되자 추위와 빗속으로 내몰아 가축을 치게 했다. 누구 하나 그를 그렇게 대하는 것을 망설이거나 거리끼지 않았다."], ["Quite the contrary, they thought they had every right, for Richard had been given to them as a chattel, and they did not even see the necessity of feeding him.", "오히려 그 반대로, 그들은 자기들에게 모든 권리가 있다고 여겼으니, 리샤르가 한낱 가축처럼 그들에게 넘겨진 물건이었던 까닭이며, 그들은 그를 먹여야 할 필요조차 느끼지 못했다."], ["Richard himself describes how in those years, like the Prodigal Son in the Gospel, he longed to eat of the mash given to the pigs, which were fattened for sale.", "리샤르 자신이 어떻게 그 시절을 보냈는지 이야기하는데, 복음서에 나오는 탕자처럼 그는 팔려고 살찌우는 돼지들에게 주는 죽이라도 먹고 싶어 애를 태웠다. 하지만 그들은 그것조차 주려 하지 않았고, 그가 돼지의 먹이를 훔쳐 먹으면 매질을 했다."], ["But they wouldn’t even give him that, and beat him when he stole from the pigs.", "그렇게 그는 어린 시절과 젊은 시절을 다 보냈고, 마침내 자라나 달아나 도둑이 될 만큼 기운이 세지기에 이르렀다."], ["And that was how he spent all his childhood and his youth, till he grew up and was strong enough to go away and be a thief.", "이 야만인은 제네바에서 날품팔이로 생계를 잇기 시작했다. 그는 벌어들인 것을 술로 탕진하고, 짐승처럼 살다가, 끝내 한 노인을 죽이고 강도질을 하기에 이르렀다."], ["The savage began to earn his living as a day laborer in Geneva.", "그는 붙잡혀 재판을 받고 사형을 선고받았다. 그곳 사람들은 감상주의자가 아니거든."], ["He drank what he earned, he lived like a brute, and finished by killing and robbing an old man.", "그러자 감옥에서 그는 곧장 목사들, 기독교 형제단의 회원들, 자선을 베푸는 부인들, 그밖에 그런 부류의 사람들에게 둘러싸였다. 그들은 감옥에서 그에게 읽기와 쓰기를 가르치고 복음서를 풀이해 주었다."], ["He was caught, tried, and condemned to death. They are not sentimentalists there.", "그들은 그를 권면하고, 그에게 공을 들이고, 끊임없이 그를 두드려 대더니, 마침내 그가 자기 죄를 엄숙하게 고백하기에 이르렀다."], ["And in prison he was immediately surrounded by pastors, members of Christian brotherhoods, philanthropic ladies, and the like.", "그는 개종했다. 그는 자기가 괴물이지만 마침내 신께서 자기에게 빛을 베푸시고 은총을 보여 주셨노라고 손수 법정에 편지를 써 보냈다."], ["They taught him to read and write in prison, and expounded the Gospel to him.", "제네바 전체가 그를 두고 흥분에 휩싸였다—자선과 신앙으로 가득 찬 제네바 전체가 말이다."], ["They exhorted him, worked upon him, drummed at him incessantly, till at last he solemnly confessed his crime.", "그 도시의 온갖 귀족과 교양 있는 사람들이 앞다투어 감옥으로 달려가 리샤르에게 입맞추고 그를 끌어안았다. '당신은 우리 형제요, 당신은 은총을 발견했소.'"], ["He was converted.", "그리고 리샤르는 감격에 겨워 울기만 할 뿐이다."], ["He wrote to the court himself that he was a monster, but that in the end God had vouchsafed him light and shown grace.", "'그렇소, 나는 은총을 발견했소! 내 어린 시절과 젊은 시절 내내 나는 돼지의 먹이라도 기꺼이 먹으려 했는데, 이제는 나 같은 자조차 은총을 발견했소."], ["All Geneva was in excitement about him—all philanthropic and religious Geneva.", "나는 주 안에서 죽소.' '그렇소, 리샤르, 주 안에서 죽으시오."], ["All the aristocratic and well-bred society of the town rushed to the prison, kissed Richard and embraced him; ‘You are our brother, you have found grace.’ And Richard does nothing but weep with emotion, ‘Yes, I’ve found grace!", "당신은 피를 흘렸으니 마땅히 죽어야 하오. 비록 당신이 돼지의 먹이를 탐하고 그걸 훔쳤다 해서 매를 맞으며(훔치는 것은 금지된 일이니 그건 당신의 큰 잘못이었소) 주님을 알지 못한 것이 당신 잘못은 아니었다 해도, 어쨌든 당신은 피를 흘렸으니 죽어야 하오.'"], ["All my youth and childhood I was glad of pigs’ food, but now even I have found grace.", "그리고 마지막 날, 리샤르는 완전히 맥이 풀려서, 그저 울기만 하며 매 순간 되뇌었다."], ["I am dying in the Lord.’ ‘Yes, Richard, die in the Lord; you have shed blood and must die.", "'오늘은 내 생애에서 가장 행복한 날이오. 나는 주님께로 가오.'"], ["Though it’s not your fault that you knew not the Lord, when you coveted the pigs’ food and were beaten for stealing it (which was very wrong of you, for stealing is forbidden); but you’ve shed blood and you must die.’ And on the last day, Richard, perfectly limp, did nothing but cry and repeat every minute: ‘This is my happiest day.", "'그렇소,' 목사들과 판사들과 자선을 베푸는 부인들이 외친다."], ["I am going to the Lord.’ ‘Yes,’ cry the pastors and the judges and philanthropic ladies.", "'당신 생애에서 가장 행복한 날이오, 당신은 주님께로 가니 말이오!'"], ["‘This is the happiest day of your life, for you are going to the Lord!’ They all walk or drive to the scaffold in procession behind the prison van.", "그들은 모두 죄수 호송 마차 뒤를 따라 행렬을 이루어 걷거나 마차를 타고 단두대로 향한다. 단두대에 이르자 그들은 리샤르를 향해 부르짖는다."], ["At the scaffold they call to Richard: ‘Die, brother, die in the Lord, for even thou hast found grace!’ And so, covered with his brothers’ kisses, Richard is dragged on to the scaffold, and led to the guillotine.", "'죽으시오, 형제여, 주 안에서 죽으시오, 당신마저 은총을 발견했으니!' 그리하여 형제들의 입맞춤에 뒤덮인 채 리샤르는 단두대 위로 끌려 올라가 기요틴 앞으로 인도된다."], ["And they chopped off his head in brotherly fashion, because he had found grace.", "그리고 그들은 형제다운 방식으로 그의 머리를 잘라 버렸다, 그가 은총을 발견했기 때문에 말이다."], ["Yes, that’s characteristic.", "그래, 그게 바로 특징적인 거다."], ["That pamphlet is translated into Russian by some Russian philanthropists of aristocratic rank and evangelical aspirations, and has been distributed gratis for the enlightenment of the people.", "그 소책자는 귀족 신분이면서 복음주의적 열망을 품은 어느 러시아 자선가들에 의해 러시아어로 번역되어, 민중의 계몽을 위해 무료로 배포되었다."], ["The case of Richard is interesting because it’s national.", "리샤르의 사례가 흥미로운 까닭은 그것이 민족적이기 때문이다."], ["Though to us it’s absurd to cut off a man’s head, because he has become our brother and has found grace, yet we have our own speciality, which is all but worse.", "우리에게는 한 인간이 우리 형제가 되었고 은총을 발견했다 해서 그 목을 잘라 버린다는 게 터무니없는 일이지만, 그래도 우리에게는 우리만의 특기가 있으니, 그건 거의 그보다 더 나쁜 거다."], ["Our historical pastime is the direct satisfaction of inflicting pain.", "우리의 역사적 소일거리는 고통을 가하는 데서 직접적인 만족을 얻는 거다."], ["There are lines in Nekrassov describing how a peasant lashes a horse on the eyes, ‘on its meek eyes,’ every one must have seen it.", "네크라소프의 시 가운데 한 농부가 말의 눈을, '그 온순한 눈을' 채찍질하는 광경을 묘사한 구절이 있는데, 누구든 그런 장면을 본 적이 있을 거다."], ["It’s peculiarly Russian.", "그건 유난히 러시아적이지."], ["He describes how a feeble little nag has foundered under too heavy a load and cannot move.", "그는 묘사한다, 어떻게 비실비실한 작은 말 한 마리가 너무 무거운 짐에 짓눌려 주저앉아 옴짝달싹 못 하게 되었는지를. 농부는 그놈을 때린다, 사납게 때린다, 마침내 잔인성에 도취되어 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모른 채 때리고, 인정사정없이 몇 번이고 되풀이해 후려친다."], ["The peasant beats it, beats it savagely, beats it at last not knowing what he is doing in the intoxication of cruelty, thrashes it mercilessly over and over again.", "'네가 아무리 약해 빠졌어도 끌어야 한다, 죽는 한이 있어도.' 말은 안간힘을 쓰고, 그러자 그는 그 가엾고 무력한 짐승의 울고 있는 눈을, 그 '온순한 눈을'"], ["‘However weak you are, you must pull, if you die for it.’ The nag strains, and then he begins lashing the poor defenseless creature on its weeping, on its ‘meek eyes.’ The frantic beast tugs and draws the load, trembling all over, gasping for breath, moving sideways, with a sort of unnatural spasmodic action—it’s awful in Nekrassov.", "채찍질하기 시작한다. 미친 듯한 짐승은 온몸을 부들부들 떨고, 숨을 헐떡이며, 부자연스럽고 발작적인 동작으로 옆걸음을 치면서 짐을 끌어당긴다—네크라소프의 그 대목은 끔찍하다."], ["But that’s only a horse, and God has given horses to be beaten.", "하지만 그건 한낱 말일 뿐이고, 신은 말을 매 맞으라고 주셨지."], ["So the Tatars have taught us, and they left us the knout as a remembrance of it.", "타타르인들이 우리에게 그렇게 가르쳤고, 그 기념으로 채찍을 남겨 주었으니까."], ["But men, too, can be beaten.", "하지만 사람도 매질당할 수 있다."], ["A well-educated, cultured gentleman and his wife beat their own child with a birch-rod, a girl of seven.", "잘 배우고 교양 있는 한 신사와 그의 아내가 자기들의 친자식을, 일곱 살 난 계집아이를 자작나무 회초리로 때렸다."], ["I have an exact account of it.", "나는 그 일의 정확한 기록을 가지고 있다."], ["The papa was glad that the birch was covered with twigs.", "그 아비는 회초리에 잔가지가 붙어 있는 걸 좋아했다."], ["‘It stings more,’ said he, and so he began stinging his daughter.", "'이게 더 따끔하지', 하고 그는 말하고는, 제 딸을 따끔하게 만들기 시작했다."], ["I know for a fact there are people who at every blow are worked up to sensuality, to literal sensuality, which increases progressively at every blow they inflict.", "나는 사실로 알고 있다, 매질을 한 대씩 할 때마다 관능으로, 글자 그대로의 관능으로 달아오르는 사람들이 있고, 그 관능은 매를 한 대씩 가할 때마다 점점 더 커져 간다는 것을."], ["They beat for a minute, for five minutes, for ten minutes, more often and more savagely.", "그들은 일 분간, 오 분간, 십 분간 때린다, 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 사납게."], ["The child screams.", "아이가 비명을 지른다."], ["At last the child cannot scream, it gasps, ‘Daddy!", "마침내 아이는 비명조차 지르지 못하고 헐떡이며 외친다, '아빠!"], ["daddy!’ By some diabolical unseemly chance the case was brought into court.", "아빠!' 무슨 악마 같은 점잖지 못한 우연으로 그 사건이 법정에까지 오게 되었다."], ["A counsel is engaged.", "변호인이 선임된다."], ["The Russian people have long called a barrister ‘a conscience for hire.’ The counsel protests in his client’s defense.", "러시아 민중은 오래전부터 변호사를 '돈으로 사는 양심'이라고 불러 왔다. 변호인은 자기 의뢰인을 변호하며 항변한다."], ["‘It’s such a simple thing,’ he says, ‘an everyday domestic event.", "'이건 아주 단순한 일입니다', 그는 말한다, '지극히 일상적인 가정사일 뿐입니다."], ["A father corrects his child.", "아버지가 제 자식을 바로잡아 준 것이지요."], ["To our shame be it said, it is brought into court.’ The jury, convinced by him, give a favorable verdict.", "부끄럽게도 이런 일이 법정에까지 끌려 나왔습니다.' 그에게 설득당한 배심원단은 무죄 평결을 내린다."], ["The public roars with delight that the torturer is acquitted.", "방청객은 그 고문자가 방면된 것에 환호하며 떠나갈 듯이 들끓는다."], ["Ah, pity I wasn’t there!", "아, 그 자리에 내가 없었던 게 분하구나!"], ["I would have proposed to raise a subscription in his honor!", "나라면 그자를 기리는 모금이라도 하자고 제안했을 텐데!"], ["Charming pictures.", "매혹적인 그림이지."]], [["“But I’ve still better things about children.", "\"하지만 나한테는 아이들에 관한 더 좋은 얘깃거리가 있다."], ["I’ve collected a great, great deal about Russian children, Alyosha.", "나는 러시아 아이들에 관해 아주아주 많은 것을 수집해 두었다, 알료샤."], ["There was a little girl of five who was hated by her father and mother, ‘most worthy and respectable people, of good education and breeding.’ You see, I must repeat again, it is a peculiar characteristic of many people, this love of torturing children, and children only.", "다섯 살 난 한 계집아이가 있었는데, 제 아비와 어미한테 미움을 받았다, '더없이 훌륭하고 존경받을 만한, 잘 배우고 교양 있는 사람들'한테 말이지. 알다시피, 나는 다시 한 번 되풀이해야겠다, 많은 사람들에게는 한 가지 독특한 특성이 있으니, 그게 바로 아이들을, 오직 아이들만을 고문하기를 즐기는 거다."], ["To all other types of humanity these torturers behave mildly and benevolently, like cultivated and humane Europeans; but they are very fond of tormenting children, even fond of children themselves in that sense.", "다른 모든 부류의 인간들에게는 이 고문자들이 교양 있고 인도적인 유럽인답게 온화하고 자애롭게 행동한다. 그러나 그들은 아이들을 괴롭히기를 무척 좋아하고, 그런 의미에서는 아이들 자체를 좋아하기까지 한다."], ["It’s just their defenselessness that tempts the tormentor, just the angelic confidence of the child who has no refuge and no appeal, that sets his vile blood on fire.", "고문자를 유혹하는 것은 바로 그 아이들의 무방비함이고, 피할 곳도 호소할 데도 없는 아이의 그 천사 같은 신뢰야말로 그 비열한 피를 불타오르게 하는 거다."], ["In every man, of course, a demon lies hidden—the demon of rage, the demon of lustful heat at the screams of the tortured victim, the demon of lawlessness let off the chain, the demon of diseases that follow on vice, gout, kidney disease, and so on.", "물론 모든 인간 속에는 악마가 숨어 있다—분노의 악마, 고문당하는 희생자의 비명에 음탕하게 달아오르는 악마, 사슬에서 풀려난 무법의 악마, 악덕에 뒤따르는 온갖 질병의 악마, 통풍이며 신장병 따위의 악마가 말이지."]], [["“This poor child of five was subjected to every possible torture by those cultivated parents.", "\"이 가엾은 다섯 살배기 아이는 그 교양 있는 부모한테 온갖 고문이란 고문은 다 당했다."], ["They beat her, thrashed her, kicked her for no reason till her body was one bruise.", "그들은 아무 까닭도 없이 아이를 때리고, 후려치고, 발길질을 해서 마침내 그 몸뚱이가 온통 멍투성이가 되게 만들었다."], ["Then, they went to greater refinements of cruelty—shut her up all night in the cold and frost in a privy, and because she didn’t ask to be taken up at night (as though a child of five sleeping its angelic, sound sleep could be trained to wake and ask), they smeared her face and filled her mouth with excrement, and it was her mother, her mother did this.", "그러다가 그들은 한층 더 정교한 잔인성으로 나아갔다—추위와 서리 속에 밤새도록 아이를 변소에 가두어 두었는데, 아이가 밤중에 일으켜 달라고 청하지 않았다는 이유에서였다(마치 천사처럼 곤히 자는 다섯 살배기 아이가 깨어나서 청하도록 훈련될 수 있기라도 한 것처럼 말이지), 그러고는 아이의 얼굴에 똥을 처바르고 입에 똥을 가득 채웠다, 그것도 그 어미가, 그 어미가 이 짓을 했다."], ["And that mother could sleep, hearing the poor child’s groans!", "그리고 그 어미는 가엾은 아이의 신음 소리를 들으면서도 잠을 잘 수 있었다!"], ["Can you understand why a little creature, who can’t even understand what’s done to her, should beat her little aching heart with her tiny fist in the dark and the cold, and weep her meek unresentful tears to dear, kind God to protect her?", "너는 이해할 수 있겠니, 자기한테 무슨 짓이 가해지는지조차 이해하지 못하는 그 작은 피조물이, 어둠과 추위 속에서 조그만 주먹으로 제 아프고 작은 가슴을 두드리며, 자기를 지켜 달라고 다정하고 인자한 신께 그 온순하고 원망 없는 눈물을 흘렸다는 것을?"], ["Do you understand that, friend and brother, you pious and humble novice?", "너는 그걸 이해할 수 있겠니, 벗이여 형제여, 경건하고 겸손한 수도 견습생이여?"], ["Do you understand why this infamy must be and is permitted?", "너는 이 끔찍한 일이 왜 일어나야 하고 왜 용납되고 있는지 이해할 수 있겠니?"], ["Without it, I am told, man could not have existed on earth, for he could not have known good and evil.", "사람들 말로는, 그게 없었더라면 인간은 이 땅에 존재할 수 없었을 거라더라, 그게 없었더라면 인간이 선과 악을 알 수 없었을 테니까."], ["Why should he know that diabolical good and evil when it costs so much?", "그렇게 비싼 대가를 치러야 하는 그 악마 같은 선과 악을 인간이 대체 왜 알아야 한단 말이냐?"], ["Why, the whole world of knowledge is not worth that child’s prayer to ‘dear, kind God’!", "아니, 그 모든 지식의 세계를 다 합쳐도 그 아이가 '다정하고 인자한 신'께 드린 기도 하나만 한 값어치도 없다!"], ["I say nothing of the sufferings of grown-up people, they have eaten the apple, damn them, and the devil take them all!", "어른들의 고통에 대해서는 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다, 그들은 사과를 먹었으니까, 빌어먹을 놈들, 죄다 악마한테나 잡혀가라지!"], ["But these little ones!", "하지만 이 어린것들은!"], ["I am making you suffer, Alyosha, you are not yourself.", "내가 너를 괴롭히고 있구나, 알료샤, 너는 네 정신이 아니다."], ["I’ll leave off if you like.”", "네가 원하면 그만두마.\""]], [["“Never mind.", "\"괜찮아요."], ["I want to suffer too,”", "나도 함께 괴로워하고 싶어요.\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“One picture, only one more, because it’s so curious, so characteristic, and I have only just read it in some collection of Russian antiquities.", "\"한 장면만 더, 딱 한 장면만 더 얘기하마, 어찌나 기이하고 어찌나 특징적인지, 게다가 나도 방금에야 어떤 러시아 고문헌집에서 읽은 거니까."], ["I’ve forgotten the name.", "제목은 잊어버렸다."], ["I must look it up.", "다시 찾아봐야겠어."], ["It was in the darkest days of serfdom at the beginning of the century, and long live the Liberator of the People!", "그건 금세기 초, 농노제가 가장 암울하던 시절의 일이었다, 인민 해방자께 만세를!"], ["There was in those days a general of aristocratic connections, the owner of great estates, one of those men—somewhat exceptional, I believe, even then—who, retiring from the service into a life of leisure, are convinced that they’ve earned absolute power over the lives of their subjects.", "그 시절에 귀족 가문에 연줄이 닿고 광대한 영지를 거느린 한 장군이 있었는데, 그자는—내 생각엔 그 무렵에도 다소 예외적인 부류였지만—군 복무에서 물러나 한가로운 삶으로 접어들면서, 자기가 신민들의 목숨에 대해 절대적인 권력을 얻었다고 확신하는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나였다."], ["There were such men then.", "그 시절엔 그런 사람들이 있었지."], ["So our general, settled on his property of two thousand souls, lives in pomp, and domineers over his poor neighbors as though they were dependents and buffoons.", "그래서 우리의 이 장군은 이천 명의 영혼을 거느린 영지에 자리를 잡고, 호화롭게 살면서, 마치 가난한 이웃들이 제 식객이나 어릿광대라도 되는 양 그들 위에 군림했다."], ["He has kennels of hundreds of hounds and nearly a hundred dog-boys—all mounted, and in uniform.", "그에게는 수백 마리의 사냥개를 기르는 개집과, 거의 백 명에 이르는 개지기 소년들이 있었는데—다들 말을 타고 제복을 갖춰 입었다."], ["One day a serf-boy, a little child of eight, threw a stone in play and hurt the paw of the general’s favorite hound.", "어느 날 한 농노 소년, 여덟 살 난 어린아이가 장난삼아 돌을 던졌는데 그게 장군이 아끼는 사냥개의 발을 다치게 했다."], ["‘Why is my favorite dog lame?’ He is told that the boy threw a stone that hurt the dog’s paw.", "'왜 내가 아끼는 개가 절뚝거리느냐?' 누군가가 소년이 던진 돌이 개의 발을 다치게 했다고 그에게 아뢴다."], ["‘So you did it.’ The general looked the child up and down.", "'그래, 네놈이 했단 말이지.' 장군은 아이를 위아래로 훑어보았다."], ["‘Take him.’ He was taken—taken from his mother and kept shut up all night.", "'저놈을 끌고 가라.' 아이는 끌려갔다—제 어미한테서 끌려가 밤새도록 갇혀 있었다."], ["Early that morning the general comes out on horseback, with the hounds, his dependents, dog-boys, and huntsmen, all mounted around him in full hunting parade.", "이튿날 이른 아침에 장군은 사냥개들과 식객들, 개지기 소년들, 사냥꾼들을 거느리고 말을 타고 나오는데, 다들 그를 에워싸고 말에 올라 완벽한 사냥 행렬을 이루고 있다."], ["The servants are summoned for their edification, and in front of them all stands the mother of the child.", "하인들은 교화를 받으라고 소집되고, 그들 모두의 앞에 그 아이의 어미가 서 있다."], ["The child is brought from the lock-up.", "아이가 유치장에서 끌려 나온다."], ["It’s a gloomy, cold, foggy autumn day, a capital day for hunting.", "음울하고 춥고 안개 낀 가을날, 사냥하기에는 더없이 좋은 날이다."], ["The general orders the child to be undressed; the child is stripped naked.", "장군은 아이의 옷을 벗기라고 명령한다. 아이는 발가벗겨진다."], ["He shivers, numb with terror, not daring to cry....", "아이는 공포로 몸이 굳은 채 부들부들 떨며, 감히 울지도 못한다……"], ["‘Make him run,’ commands the general.", "'저놈을 뛰게 하라', 장군이 명령한다. '뛰어라!"], ["‘Run!", "뛰어!'"], ["run!’ shout the dog-boys.", "개지기 소년들이 소리친다."], ["The boy runs....", "아이가 뛴다……"], ["‘At him!’ yells the general, and he sets the whole pack of hounds on the child.", "'저놈을 물어라!' 장군이 고함을 지르며, 사냥개 떼를 통째로 그 아이에게 풀어 놓는다."], ["The hounds catch him, and tear him to pieces before his mother’s eyes!...", "사냥개들이 아이를 붙잡아, 제 어미가 보는 앞에서 갈가리 찢어 버린다!……"], ["I believe the general was afterwards declared incapable of administering his estates.", "내 생각엔 그 장군은 나중에 영지를 관리할 능력이 없다는 판정을 받았던 것 같다."], ["Well—what did he deserve? To be shot?", "자—그자가 마땅히 받아야 할 게 뭐였을까?"], ["To be shot for the satisfaction of our moral feelings?", "총살? 우리의 도덕적 감정을 만족시키기 위해 총살시켜야 할까?"], ["Speak, Alyosha!”", "말해 봐라, 알료샤!\""]], [["“To be shot,”", "\"총살이요.\""], ["murmured Alyosha, lifting his eyes to Ivan with a pale, twisted smile.", "알료샤가 창백하게 일그러진 미소를 띤 채 이반에게로 눈을 들어 올리며 나직이 말했다."]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"브라보!\""], ["cried Ivan, delighted.", "이반이 기뻐하며 외쳤다."], ["“If even you say so....", "\"심지어 너까지 그렇게 말한다면……"], ["You’re a pretty monk!", "너도 어지간한 수도사로구나!"], ["So there is a little devil sitting in your heart, Alyosha Karamazov!”", "그러니까 네 마음속에도 작은 악마가 한 마리 앉아 있는 거다, 알료샤 카라마조프!\""]], [["“What I said was absurd, but—”", "\"내가 한 말은 터무니없는 거였어요, 하지만—\""]], [["“That’s just the point, that ‘but’!”", "\"바로 그 점이다, 그 '하지만'이!\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."], ["“Let me tell you, novice, that the absurd is only too necessary on earth.", "\"들어 봐라, 견습생아, 이 땅에는 터무니없는 것이 너무도 필요하다는 걸 말이다."], ["The world stands on absurdities, and perhaps nothing would have come to pass in it without them.", "세상은 터무니없는 것들 위에 서 있고, 어쩌면 그게 없었더라면 세상에서는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았을 거다."], ["We know what we know!”", "우리는 우리가 아는 것을 안다!\""]], [["“What do you know?”", "\"형이 뭘 아는데요?\""]], [["“I understand nothing,”", "\"나는 아무것도 이해 못 한다.\""], ["Ivan went on, as though in delirium.", "이반은 헛소리라도 하듯 말을 이었다."], ["“I don’t want to understand anything now.", "\"나는 이제 아무것도 이해하고 싶지 않다."], ["I want to stick to the fact.", "나는 사실에 매달리고 싶다."], ["I made up my mind long ago not to understand.", "나는 오래전에 이해하지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["If I try to understand anything, I shall be false to the fact, and I have determined to stick to the fact.”", "내가 무엇이든 이해하려 든다면 나는 그 사실을 배반하게 될 거고, 그래서 나는 그 사실에 매달리기로 작정했다.\""]], [["“Why are you trying me?”", "\"왜 자꾸 나를 시험하는 거예요?\""], ["Alyosha cried, with sudden distress.", "알료샤가 갑작스러운 괴로움에 사로잡혀 외쳤다."], ["“Will you say what you mean at last?”", "\"이제 형이 무슨 말을 하려는지 좀 말해 줄래요?\""]], [["“Of course, I will; that’s what I’ve been leading up to.", "\"물론 말하고말고. 그게 바로 내가 여태 끌어 온 이야기다."], ["You are dear to me, I don’t want to let you go, and I won’t give you up to your Zossima.”", "너는 나한테 소중한 사람이라, 나는 너를 놓아주고 싶지 않다, 너를 네 조시마한테 넘겨주지 않겠다.\""]], [["Ivan for a minute was silent, his face became all at once very sad.", "이반은 잠시 입을 다물었고, 그의 얼굴은 별안간 몹시 슬퍼졌다."]], [["“Listen!", "\"들어 봐라!"], ["I took the case of children only to make my case clearer.", "나는 내 주장을 더 분명히 하려고 아이들의 경우만을 들었던 거다."], ["Of the other tears of humanity with which the earth is soaked from its crust to its center, I will say nothing.", "이 땅이 그 껍질에서 핵심에 이르기까지 흠뻑 젖어 있는 인류의 그 밖의 눈물에 대해서는 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다."], ["I have narrowed my subject on purpose.", "나는 일부러 내 주제를 좁혔다."], ["I am a bug, and I recognize in all humility that I cannot understand why the world is arranged as it is.", "나는 한 마리 벌레이고, 세상이 왜 이런 식으로 짜여 있는지 내가 이해할 수 없다는 걸 더없이 겸허하게 인정한다."], ["Men are themselves to blame, I suppose; they were given paradise, they wanted freedom, and stole fire from heaven, though they knew they would become unhappy, so there is no need to pity them.", "인간 자신에게 잘못이 있는 거겠지, 아마. 그들에게는 낙원이 주어졌건만 그들은 자유를 원했고, 자기들이 불행해지리라는 걸 알면서도 하늘에서 불을 훔쳤다."], ["With my pitiful, earthly, Euclidian understanding, all I know is that there is suffering and that there are none guilty; that cause follows effect, simply and directly; that everything flows and finds its level—but that’s only Euclidian nonsense, I know that, and I can’t consent to live by it!", "그러니 그들을 가엾이 여길 필요는 없다. 나의 가련하고 지상적이고 유클리드적인 이해력으로 내가 아는 거라곤, 고통이 존재하지만 죄지은 자는 아무도 없다는 것, 원인 뒤에 결과가 단순하고 직접적으로 뒤따른다는 것, 모든 것이 흘러가며 제 수준을 찾는다는 것뿐이다—하지만 그건 그저 유클리드적인 헛소리일 뿐이고, 나도 그걸 알지만, 나는 그걸로 살아가는 데 동의할 수 없다!"], ["What comfort is it to me that there are none guilty and that cause follows effect simply and directly, and that I know it?—I must have justice, or I will destroy myself.", "죄지은 자가 아무도 없고 원인 뒤에 결과가 단순하고 직접적으로 뒤따른다는 것, 게다가 내가 그걸 안다는 것이 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠느냐?—나는 정의를 얻어야 한다, 아니면 나 자신을 파멸시키겠다."], ["And not justice in some remote infinite time and space, but here on earth, and that I could see myself.", "그것도 어딘가 아득한 무한의 시간과 공간 속에서의 정의가 아니라, 바로 여기 이 땅 위에서, 그것도 내 눈으로 직접 볼 수 있는 정의를 말이다."], ["I have believed in it.", "나는 그것을 믿어 왔다."], ["I want to see it, and if I am dead by then, let me rise again, for if it all happens without me, it will be too unfair.", "나는 그것을 보고 싶고, 만약 그때 내가 죽어 있다면 다시 살아나게라도 해 다오, 왜냐하면 그 모든 일이 나 없이 일어난다면 그건 너무도 불공평할 테니까."], ["Surely I haven’t suffered, simply that I, my crimes and my sufferings, may manure the soil of the future harmony for somebody else.", "설마 내가 고통받은 게, 그저 나라는 인간이, 내 죄와 내 고통이, 다른 누군가를 위한 미래의 조화의 토양에 거름이나 되라고 그런 건 아니겠지."], ["I want to see with my own eyes the hind lie down with the lion and the victim rise up and embrace his murderer.", "나는 내 눈으로 직접 보고 싶다, 암사슴이 사자 곁에 눕고 살해당한 자가 일어나 제 살해자를 끌어안는 것을."], ["I want to be there when every one suddenly understands what it has all been for.", "나는 모두가 별안간 그 모든 것이 무엇을 위한 것이었는지 깨닫는 그 자리에 있고 싶다."], ["All the religions of the world are built on this longing, and I am a believer.", "세상의 모든 종교가 이 갈망 위에 세워져 있고, 나는 믿는 자다."], ["But then there are the children, and what am I to do about them?", "하지만 그렇다면 아이들이 있는데, 그 아이들에 대해서는 나는 어떻게 해야 한단 말이냐?"], ["That’s a question I can’t answer.", "그건 내가 대답할 수 없는 물음이다."], ["For the hundredth time I repeat, there are numbers of questions, but I’ve only taken the children, because in their case what I mean is so unanswerably clear.", "백 번째로 되풀이하지만, 물음은 수없이 많다, 그러나 나는 오직 아이들의 경우만을 들었다, 그 경우에는 내가 말하려는 바가 반박할 길 없이 분명하니까."], ["Listen!", "들어 봐라!"], ["If all must suffer to pay for the eternal harmony, what have children to do with it, tell me, please?", "만약 모두가 영원한 조화의 값을 치르려고 고통받아야 한다면, 아이들이 거기에 무슨 상관이란 말이냐, 어디 말 좀 해 봐라?"], ["It’s beyond all comprehension why they should suffer, and why they should pay for the harmony.", "아이들이 왜 고통받아야 하는지, 왜 그 조화의 값을 치러야 하는지, 그건 도무지 이해할 수 없는 일이다."], ["Why should they, too, furnish material to enrich the soil for the harmony of the future?", "그 아이들마저 왜 미래의 조화를 위해 토양을 기름지게 할 재료를 대 주어야 하느냐?"], ["I understand solidarity in sin among men.", "나는 인간들 사이의 죄에 있어서의 연대성은 이해한다."], ["I understand solidarity in retribution, too; but there can be no such solidarity with children.", "응보에 있어서의 연대성도 이해한다. 하지만 아이들과는 그런 연대성이 있을 수 없다."], ["And if it is really true that they must share responsibility for all their fathers’ crimes, such a truth is not of this world and is beyond my comprehension.", "그리고 만약 아이들이 정말로 제 아비들의 모든 죄에 대한 책임을 나누어 져야 한다는 게 사실이라면, 그런 진리는 이 세상의 것이 아니며 내 이해력을 넘어서는 거다."], ["Some jester will say, perhaps, that the child would have grown up and have sinned, but you see he didn’t grow up, he was torn to pieces by the dogs, at eight years old.", "어떤 익살꾼은 어쩌면 이렇게 말할지도 모르지, 그 아이가 자라났다면 죄를 지었을 거라고. 하지만 알다시피 그 아이는 자라나지 못했고, 여덟 살의 나이에 개들한테 갈가리 찢기지 않았느냐."], ["Oh, Alyosha, I am not blaspheming!", "오, 알료샤, 나는 신성을 모독하는 게 아니다!"], ["I understand, of course, what an upheaval of the universe it will be, when everything in heaven and earth blends in one hymn of praise and everything that lives and has lived cries aloud: ‘Thou art just, O Lord, for Thy ways are revealed.’ When the mother embraces the fiend who threw her child to the dogs, and all three cry aloud with tears, ‘Thou art just, O Lord!’ then, of course, the crown of knowledge will be reached and all will be made clear.", "나도 물론 이해한다, 하늘과 땅의 모든 것이 하나의 찬미의 노래로 어우러지고, 살아 있는 모든 것과 살았던 모든 것이 큰 소리로 부르짖을 때—'주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다, 당신의 길이 드러났음이니이다'—그것이 얼마나 우주를 뒤흔드는 일이 될지를. 자기 아이를 개들한테 던져 준 그 악귀를 어머니가 끌어안고, 셋이 다 함께 눈물을 흘리며 큰 소리로 부르짖을 때, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다!'라고—그때야 물론 지식의 면류관에 다다를 것이고 모든 것이 분명해질 거다."], ["But what pulls me up here is that I can’t accept that harmony.", "하지만 여기서 나를 멈춰 세우는 건, 내가 그 조화를 받아들일 수 없다는 거다."], ["And while I am on earth, I make haste to take my own measures.", "그래서 내가 이 땅 위에 있는 동안 나는 서둘러 내 나름의 대책을 강구하는 거다."], ["You see, Alyosha, perhaps it really may happen that if I live to that moment, or rise again to see it, I, too, perhaps, may cry aloud with the rest, looking at the mother embracing the child’s torturer, ‘Thou art just, O Lord!’ but I don’t want to cry aloud then.", "알다시피, 알료샤, 어쩌면 정말로 그런 일이 일어날지도 모르지, 만약 내가 그 순간까지 살아 있거나 다시 살아나 그것을 보게 된다면, 나 역시 어쩌면 다른 사람들과 함께 큰 소리로 부르짖을지도 모른다, 아이의 고문자를 끌어안고 있는 어머니를 바라보며, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다!'라고. 하지만 나는 그때 가서 부르짖고 싶지 않다."], ["While there is still time, I hasten to protect myself, and so I renounce the higher harmony altogether.", "아직 시간이 있는 동안에 나는 서둘러 나 자신을 지키려는 거고, 그래서 나는 그 더 높은 조화를 통째로 거부한다."], ["It’s not worth the tears of that one tortured child who beat itself on the breast with its little fist and prayed in its stinking outhouse, with its unexpiated tears to ‘dear, kind God’!", "그건 똥내 나는 변소에서 조그만 주먹으로 제 가슴을 두드리며 '다정하고 인자한 신'께 그 속죄받지 못한 눈물로 기도한, 그 고문당한 아이 하나의 눈물만 한 값어치도 없다!"], ["It’s not worth it, because those tears are unatoned for.", "그건 그만한 값어치가 없다, 그 눈물이 속죄되지 않은 채로 남아 있기 때문이다."], ["They must be atoned for, or there can be no harmony. But how?", "그 눈물은 반드시 속죄되어야 한다, 그렇지 않으면 어떤 조화도 있을 수 없다."], ["How are you going to atone for them?", "그런데 어떻게? 너는 그 눈물을 어떻게 속죄하려느냐?"], ["Is it possible?", "그게 가능키나 하냐?"], ["By their being avenged?", "그들의 원수를 갚음으로써?"], ["But what do I care for avenging them?", "하지만 내가 그들의 원수를 갚는 일에 무슨 관심이 있겠느냐?"], ["What do I care for a hell for oppressors?", "압제자들을 위한 지옥이 나한테 무슨 상관이냐?"], ["What good can hell do, since those children have already been tortured?", "그 아이들이 이미 고문을 당해 버린 마당에, 지옥이 무슨 소용이 있단 말이냐?"], ["And what becomes of harmony, if there is hell?", "그리고 지옥이 있다면, 조화는 대체 어떻게 되는 거냐?"], ["I want to forgive.", "나는 용서하고 싶다."], ["I want to embrace.", "나는 끌어안고 싶다."], ["I don’t want more suffering.", "나는 더 이상의 고통을 원하지 않는다."], ["And if the sufferings of children go to swell the sum of sufferings which was necessary to pay for truth, then I protest that the truth is not worth such a price.", "그리고 만약 아이들의 고통이 진리의 값을 치르는 데 필요했던 고통의 총량을 불리는 데 보태진다면, 나는 단언하건대 그 진리는 그만한 대가를 치를 값어치가 없다."], ["I don’t want the mother to embrace the oppressor who threw her son to the dogs!", "나는 어머니가 제 아들을 개들한테 던져 준 압제자를 끌어안기를 원치 않는다!"], ["She dare not forgive him!", "그녀는 감히 그자를 용서해서는 안 된다!"], ["Let her forgive him for herself, if she will, let her forgive the torturer for the immeasurable suffering of her mother’s heart.", "정 그러고 싶다면 자기 자신을 위해서라면 그자를 용서하라지, 어미의 가슴이 겪은 그 헤아릴 수 없는 고통에 대해서라면 그 고문자를 용서하라지."], ["But the sufferings of her tortured child she has no right to forgive; she dare not forgive the torturer, even if the child were to forgive him!", "그러나 고문당한 제 아이의 고통만큼은 그녀에게 용서할 권리가 없다. 설령 그 아이가 그자를 용서한다 해도, 그녀는 감히 그 고문자를 용서해서는 안 된다!"], ["And if that is so, if they dare not forgive, what becomes of harmony?", "그리고 만약 그렇다면, 그들이 감히 용서할 수 없다면, 조화는 대체 어떻게 되는 거냐?"], ["Is there in the whole world a being who would have the right to forgive and could forgive?", "온 세상에 용서할 권리가 있고 또 용서할 수 있는 존재가 단 하나라도 있겠느냐?"], ["I don’t want harmony.", "나는 조화를 원하지 않는다."], ["From love for humanity I don’t want it.", "인류에 대한 사랑에서 나는 그것을 원하지 않는다."], ["I would rather be left with the unavenged suffering.", "차라리 나는 원수 갚지 못한 고통과 함께 남겠다."], ["I would rather remain with my unavenged suffering and unsatisfied indignation, even if I were wrong.", "차라리 나는, 설령 내가 틀렸다 할지라도, 원수 갚지 못한 내 고통과 채워지지 못한 분노와 함께 남겠다."], ["Besides, too high a price is asked for harmony; it’s beyond our means to pay so much to enter on it.", "게다가 조화를 위해서는 너무도 비싼 값이 요구된다. 그 안으로 들어가려고 그토록 많은 값을 치르는 건 우리 분수에 넘치는 일이다."], ["And so I hasten to give back my entrance ticket, and if I am an honest man I am bound to give it back as soon as possible.", "그래서 나는 서둘러 내 입장권을 돌려준다, 그리고 내가 정직한 사람이라면 나는 되도록 빨리 그것을 돌려줄 의무가 있다."], ["And that I am doing.", "그리고 나는 그렇게 하고 있는 거다."], ["It’s not God that I don’t accept, Alyosha, only I most respectfully return Him the ticket.”", "내가 받아들이지 않는 건 신이 아니다, 알료샤, 다만 나는 더없이 정중하게 그분께 입장권을 돌려드릴 뿐이다.\""]], [["“That’s rebellion,”", "\"그건 반역이에요.\""], ["murmured Alyosha, looking down.", "알료샤가 눈을 내리깐 채 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Rebellion?", "\"반역이라고?"], ["I am sorry you call it that,”", "네가 그걸 그렇게 부르다니 유감이구나.\""], ["said Ivan earnestly.", "이반이 진지하게 말했다."], ["“One can hardly live in rebellion, and I want to live.", "\"반역 속에서는 누구도 살아가기 어렵다, 그런데 나는 살고 싶거든."], ["Tell me yourself, I challenge you—answer.", "어디 너 자신이 말해 봐라, 내가 너한테 던지는 도전이다—대답해 봐라."], ["Imagine that you are creating a fabric of human destiny with the object of making men happy in the end, giving them peace and rest at last, but that it was essential and inevitable to torture to death only one tiny creature—that baby beating its breast with its fist, for instance—and to found that edifice on its unavenged tears, would you consent to be the architect on those conditions?", "네가 인간의 운명이라는 직물을 짜고 있다고 상상해 봐라, 결국 인간들을 행복하게 만들어 마침내 그들에게 평화와 안식을 주는 것이 목적인데, 다만 그러려면 단 하나의 작디작은 피조물을—이를테면 제 주먹으로 가슴을 두드리던 그 갓난아이를—고문해 죽이는 것이 꼭 필요하고 피할 수 없는 일이고, 그 속죄받지 못한 눈물 위에 그 건축물을 세워야만 한다고 해 보자, 그런 조건이라면 너는 그 건축가가 되기를 승낙하겠느냐?"], ["Tell me, and tell the truth.”", "말해 봐라, 그리고 진실을 말해라.\""]], [["“No, I wouldn’t consent,”", "\"아니요, 나는 승낙하지 않겠어요.\""], ["said Alyosha softly.", "알료샤가 나직이 말했다."]], [["“And can you admit the idea that men for whom you are building it would agree to accept their happiness on the foundation of the unexpiated blood of a little victim?", "\"그리고 너는 이런 생각을 받아들일 수 있겠느냐, 네가 그걸 지어 주는 그 인간들이, 한 작은 희생자의 속죄받지 못한 피를 토대 삼아 제 행복을 받아들이는 데 동의하리라는 생각을?"], ["And accepting it would remain happy for ever?”", "그리고 그걸 받아들이고서도 영원토록 행복하게 남아 있으리라는 생각을?\""]], [["“No, I can’t admit it. Brother,”", "\"아니요, 나는 그걸 받아들일 수 없어요."], ["said Alyosha suddenly, with flashing eyes, “you said just now, is there a being in the whole world who would have the right to forgive and could forgive?", "형,\" 알료샤가 별안간 눈을 번득이며 말했다, \"형은 방금 온 세상에 용서할 권리가 있고 또 용서할 수 있는 존재가 있겠느냐고 했죠?"], ["But there is a Being and He can forgive everything, all and for all, because He gave His innocent blood for all and everything.", "하지만 그런 존재가 계세요, 그분은 모든 것을, 모든 이를, 모든 이를 위하여 용서하실 수 있어요, 왜냐하면 그분은 모든 이와 모든 것을 위하여 당신의 무죄한 피를 내어 주셨으니까요."], ["You have forgotten Him, and on Him is built the edifice, and it is to Him they cry aloud, ‘Thou art just, O Lord, for Thy ways are revealed!’”", "형은 그분을 잊어버렸어요, 그리고 그 건축물은 바로 그분 위에 세워져 있고, 사람들이 큰 소리로 부르짖는 것도 바로 그분을 향해서예요, '주여, 당신은 의로우시나이다, 당신의 길이 드러났음이니이다!'라고요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["the One without sin and His blood!", "죄 없으신 그분과 그분의 피란 말이지!"], ["No, I have not forgotten Him; on the contrary I’ve been wondering all the time how it was you did not bring Him in before, for usually all arguments on your side put Him in the foreground.", "아니, 나는 그분을 잊지 않았다. 오히려 나는 줄곧 네가 어째서 그분을 더 일찍 끌어들이지 않는지 궁금해하고 있었다, 보통 네 쪽 주장은 죄다 그분을 맨 앞에 내세우니까 말이다."], ["Do you know, Alyosha—don’t laugh!", "그런데 알료샤—웃지 마라!"], ["I made a poem about a year ago.", "나는 한 일 년쯤 전에 서사시를 한 편 지었단다."], ["If you can waste another ten minutes on me, I’ll tell it to you.”", "만약 네가 나한테 십 분만 더 허비할 수 있다면, 그걸 너한테 들려주마.\""]], [["“You wrote a poem?”", "\"형이 서사시를 썼다고요?\""]], [["“Oh, no, I didn’t write it,”", "\"오, 아니다, 쓴 건 아니다,\""], ["laughed Ivan, “and I’ve never written two lines of poetry in my life.", "이반이 웃었다, \"나는 평생 시라곤 두 줄도 써 본 적이 없다."], ["But I made up this poem in prose and I remembered it.", "하지만 이건 산문으로 지어 낸 서사시인데, 내가 그걸 기억하고 있단다."], ["I was carried away when I made it up.", "그걸 지어 낼 때 나는 푹 빠져 있었지."], ["You will be my first reader—that is listener.", "너는 내 첫 독자가—아니 청자가 되는 거다."], ["Why should an author forego even one listener?”", "작가가 단 한 명의 청자인들 마다할 까닭이 어디 있겠느냐?\""], ["smiled Ivan.", "이반이 미소 지었다."], ["“Shall I tell it to you?”", "\"어디 너한테 들려줄까?\""]], [["“I am all attention,”", "\"나는 온통 귀를 기울이고 있어요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“My poem is called ‘The Grand Inquisitor’; it’s a ridiculous thing, but I want to tell it to you.”", "\"내 서사시의 제목은 '대심문관'이다. 우스꽝스러운 물건이지만, 그래도 나는 그걸 너한테 들려주고 싶구나.\""]], [["# Chapter V. The Grand Inquisitor", "# Chapter V. The Grand Inquisitor"]], [["“Even this must have a preface—that is, a literary preface,”", "“이것 역시 무슨 머리말이 있어야겠지—그러니까 문학적인 머리말 말이야.” 이반이 웃으며 말했다."], ["laughed Ivan, “and I am a poor hand at making one.", "“하긴 나는 그런 걸 짓는 데는 영 서툰 사람이지만."], ["You see, my action takes place in the sixteenth century, and at that time, as you probably learnt at school, it was customary in poetry to bring down heavenly powers on earth.", "알다시피 내 이야기는 16세기를 배경으로 하는데, 그 무렵에는—아마 너도 학교에서 배웠겠지만—시 속에서 천상의 권능들을 지상으로 끌어내리는 것이 관례였어."], ["Not to speak of Dante, in France, clerks, as well as the monks in the monasteries, used to give regular performances in which the Madonna, the saints, the angels, Christ, and God himself were brought on the stage.", "단테는 말할 것도 없거니와, 프랑스에서는 수도원의 수사들뿐 아니라 성직 서기들까지도 정식 공연을 벌이곤 했는데, 거기엔 성모와 성인들, 천사들, 그리스도, 그리고 하느님까지도 무대에 올랐어."], ["In those days it was done in all simplicity.", "그 시절엔 이 모든 것이 더없이 소박하게 행해졌지."], ["In Victor Hugo’s Notre Dame de Paris an edifying and gratuitous spectacle was provided for the people in the Hôtel de Ville of Paris in the reign of Louis XI. in honor of the birth of the dauphin.", "빅토르 위고의 『노트르담 드 파리』를 보면, 루이 11세 치세에 파리 시청에서 황태자의 탄생을 기념하여 백성들에게 교훈적이고 무료한 구경거리가 마련되는 대목이 나오잖아."], ["It was called Le bon jugement de la très sainte et gracieuse Vierge Marie, and she appears herself on the stage and pronounces her bon jugement.", "그건 『지극히 거룩하고 자비로우신 동정녀 마리아의 선한 심판』이라고 불렸는데, 거기서 마리아가 친히 무대에 등장해 자신의 ‘선한 심판’을 선포하지."], ["Similar plays, chiefly from the Old Testament, were occasionally performed in Moscow too, up to the times of Peter the Great.", "이와 비슷한 극들은, 주로 구약을 소재로 삼은 것이긴 했으되, 모스크바에서도 표트르 대제 시대에 이르기까지 이따금 상연되었어."], ["But besides plays there were all sorts of legends and ballads scattered about the world, in which the saints and angels and all the powers of Heaven took part when required.", "그러나 이런 극들 말고도 온갖 전설과 민요가 세상에 흩어져 있었는데, 거기엔 필요에 따라 성인들과 천사들, 천상의 모든 권능이 등장했지."], ["In our monasteries the monks busied themselves in translating, copying, and even composing such poems—and even under the Tatars.", "우리 수도원들에서도 수사들이 그런 시들을 번역하고 베끼고, 심지어 직접 짓는 일에 매달렸는데—타타르족 치하에서까지도 그랬어."], ["There is, for instance, one such poem (of course, from the Greek), The Wanderings of Our Lady through Hell, with descriptions as bold as Dante’s.", "가령 그런 시가 하나 있는데(물론 그리스어에서 옮긴 것이지) 『성모의 지옥 순례기』라는 것으로, 단테 못지않게 대담한 묘사가 담겨 있어."], ["Our Lady visits hell, and the Archangel Michael leads her through the torments.", "성모께서 지옥을 찾아가시는데, 대천사 미카엘이 그분을 인도하며 온갖 형벌의 광경을 보여 드리지."], ["She sees the sinners and their punishment.", "성모는 죄인들과 그들의 형벌을 목격하셔."], ["There she sees among others one noteworthy set of sinners in a burning lake; some of them sink to the bottom of the lake so that they can’t swim out, and ‘these God forgets’—an expression of extraordinary depth and force.", "그곳에서 다른 무리들 가운데서도 한 무리의 주목할 만한 죄인들이 불타는 호수 속에 있는 것을 보시는데, 그중 어떤 자들은 호수 바닥으로 가라앉아 다시는 헤엄쳐 나오지 못하고, ‘이들은 하느님께서 잊으신 자들’이라는 거야—실로 비범한 깊이와 힘을 지닌 표현이지."], ["And so Our Lady, shocked and weeping, falls before the throne of God and begs for mercy for all in hell—for all she has seen there, indiscriminately.", "그리하여 성모는 충격을 받고 눈물을 흘리시며 하느님의 보좌 앞에 엎드려, 지옥에 있는 모든 자들을 위해—그곳에서 보신 모든 자들을 가리지 않고—자비를 베풀어 달라고 비셔."], ["Her conversation with God is immensely interesting.", "그분과 하느님의 대화는 더없이 흥미롭지."], ["She beseeches Him, she will not desist, and when God points to the hands and feet of her Son, nailed to the Cross, and asks, ‘How can I forgive His tormentors?’ she bids all the saints, all the martyrs, all the angels and archangels to fall down with her and pray for mercy on all without distinction.", "성모께서는 하느님께 간청하시며 결코 물러서려 하지 않으시는데, 하느님께서 십자가에 못 박힌 당신 아드님의 손과 발을 가리키시며 ‘내 어찌 그를 괴롭힌 자들을 용서하겠느냐?’ 하고 물으시자, 성모는 모든 성인과 모든 순교자, 모든 천사와 대천사들에게 자신과 함께 엎드려 차별 없이 모든 자를 위해 자비를 빌라고 명하셔."], ["It ends by her winning from God a respite of suffering every year from Good Friday till Trinity Day, and the sinners at once raise a cry of thankfulness from hell, chanting, ‘Thou art just, O Lord, in this judgment.’ Well, my poem would have been of that kind if it had appeared at that time.", "마침내 성모는 하느님으로부터 해마다 성금요일부터 삼위일체 주일까지 형벌을 멈추어 주는 유예를 얻어 내시고, 그러자 죄인들은 곧장 지옥에서 감사의 함성을 올리며 ‘주여, 이 심판은 의로우십니다’ 하고 노래한다는 거야. 자, 내 시가 그 시절에 나왔더라면 바로 이런 부류였을 거야."], ["He comes on the scene in my poem, but He says nothing, only appears and passes on.", "내 시에서도 그분이 무대에 등장하시는데, 다만 아무 말씀도 하지 않으시고 그저 나타났다가 지나가실 뿐이야."], ["Fifteen centuries have passed since He promised to come in His glory, fifteen centuries since His prophet wrote, ‘Behold, I come quickly’; ‘Of that day and that hour knoweth no man, neither the Son, but the Father,’ as He Himself predicted on earth.", "그분이 영광 가운데 다시 오시겠다고 약속하신 지 열다섯 세기가 흘렀고, 그분의 예언자가 ‘보라, 내가 속히 오리라’고 쓴 지도 열다섯 세기가 지났지. ‘그날과 그때는 아무도 모르나니, 아들도 모르고 오직 아버지만 아시느니라’ 하고 그분 친히 지상에서 예언하셨던 그대로 말이야."], ["But humanity awaits him with the same faith and with the same love.", "그러나 인류는 여전히 같은 믿음과 같은 사랑으로 그분을 기다리고 있어."], ["Oh, with greater faith, for it is fifteen centuries since man has ceased to see signs from heaven.", "아니, 더 큰 믿음으로 기다리고 있지. 인간이 하늘로부터 오는 징표를 보지 못하게 된 지 어느덧 열다섯 세기가 지났으니까."]], [["No signs from heaven come to-day To add to what the heart doth say.", "오늘날 하늘에서는 아무런 징표도 내려오지 않으니 마음이 일러 주는 것에 무엇 하나 더할 길이 없도다."]], [["There was nothing left but faith in what the heart doth say.", "남은 것이라곤 오직 마음이 일러 주는 것에 대한 믿음뿐이었어."], ["It is true there were many miracles in those days.", "하긴 그 시절에는 기적도 많았던 것이 사실이야."], ["There were saints who performed miraculous cures; some holy people, according to their biographies, were visited by the Queen of Heaven herself.", "기적 같은 치유를 행한 성인들도 있었고, 어떤 거룩한 이들은 그들의 전기에 따르면 하늘의 여왕께서 친히 찾아오신 일도 있었지."], ["But the devil did not slumber, and doubts were already arising among men of the truth of these miracles.", "그러나 악마도 잠들지 않았기에, 사람들 사이에서는 이미 그런 기적들의 진위를 의심하는 마음이 싹트고 있었어."], ["And just then there appeared in the north of Germany a terrible new heresy.", "그리고 바로 그 무렵 독일 북부에 무시무시한 새 이단이 나타났지."], ["“A huge star like to a torch” (that is, to a church) “fell on the sources of the waters and they became bitter.”", "‘횃불 같은(즉 교회 같은) 큰 별 하나가 물의 근원들 위에 떨어지매 그 물들이 쓰게 되더라.’ 이 이단자들은 신성을 모독하며 기적을 부정하기 시작했어."], ["These heretics began blasphemously denying miracles.", "그러나 믿음을 지킨 자들은 그럴수록 더욱 뜨겁게 믿었지."], ["But those who remained faithful were all the more ardent in their faith. The tears of humanity rose up to Him as before, awaited His coming, loved Him, hoped for Him, yearned to suffer and die for Him as before.", "인류의 눈물은 예전처럼 그분께로 솟아올랐고, 그분의 오심을 기다리며 그분을 사랑하고 그분께 희망을 걸었으며, 예전처럼 그분을 위해 고난받고 죽기를 갈망했어."], ["And so many ages mankind had prayed with faith and fervor, “O Lord our God, hasten Thy coming,” so many ages called upon Him, that in His infinite mercy He deigned to come down to His servants.", "그렇게 여러 세대에 걸쳐 인류는 믿음과 열정으로 ‘주 우리 하느님이시여, 어서 오소서’ 하고 기도했고, 그토록 오랜 세월 그분을 불렀기에, 마침내 그분께서는 그 무한한 자비로 당신의 종들에게로 내려오기를 허락하셨어."], ["Before that day He had come down, He had visited some holy men, martyrs and hermits, as is written in their lives.", "그날 이전에도 그분은 내려오신 적이 있어, 어떤 거룩한 이들과 순교자들과 은수자들을 찾아오셨던 것이, 그들의 행전에 적혀 있는 그대로지."], ["Among us, Tyutchev, with absolute faith in the truth of his words, bore witness that", "우리네 사람으로는 튜체프가, 자기 말의 진실성을 절대적으로 믿으며 이렇게 증언했어."]], [["Bearing the Cross, in slavish dress, Weary and worn, the Heavenly King Our mother, Russia, came to bless, And through our land went wandering.", "십자가를 지고 종의 모습으로 지치고 야위신 하늘의 임금님이 우리 어머니 러시아를 축복하시며 온 땅을 두루 헤매며 다니셨네."]], [["And that certainly was so, I assure you.", "그리고 그것은 분명히 그러했어, 내가 장담하지."]], [["“And behold, He deigned to appear for a moment to the people, to the tortured, suffering people, sunk in iniquity, but loving Him like children. My story is laid in Spain, in Seville, in the most terrible time of the Inquisition, when fires were lighted every day to the glory of God, and ‘in the splendid auto da fé the wicked heretics were burnt.’ Oh, of course, this was not the coming in which He will appear according to His promise at the end of time in all His heavenly glory, and which will be sudden ‘as lightning flashing from east to west.’ No, He visited His children only for a moment, and there where the flames were crackling round the heretics. In His infinite mercy He came once more among men in that human shape in which He walked among men for three years fifteen centuries ago. He came down to the ‘hot pavements’ of the southern town in which on the day before almost a hundred heretics had, ad majorem gloriam Dei, been burnt by the cardinal, the Grand Inquisitor, in a magnificent auto da fé, in the presence of the king, the court, the knights, the cardinals, the most charming ladies of the court, and the whole population of Seville.", "“그리하여 보라, 그분께서는 잠시 동안 백성들에게, 곧 죄악에 빠져 있으되 어린아이처럼 그분을 사랑하는, 고통받고 시달리는 백성들에게 나타나기를 허락하셨어. 내 이야기의 무대는 에스파냐, 세비야야. 종교 재판이 가장 끔찍했던 시절, 날마다 하느님의 영광을 위해 불길이 타올랐고 ‘화려한 화형식 속에서 사악한 이단자들이 불태워지던’ 그 시절이지. 오, 물론 이것은 약속하신 대로 시대의 종말에 그분이 온 천상의 영광 가운데 ‘번개가 동에서 서로 번쩍이는 것같이’ 홀연히 나타나실 그 오심은 아니야. 아니야, 그분은 다만 잠시 동안, 그것도 이단자들 주위로 불길이 탁탁 타오르는 바로 그곳으로 당신의 자녀들을 찾아오셨을 뿐이야. 그분은 무한한 자비로, 열다섯 세기 전 삼 년 동안 사람들 사이를 거니실 때 지니셨던 바로 그 인간의 모습으로 다시 한번 사람들 가운데 오셨어. 그분은 남쪽 도시의 ‘뜨거운 포석’ 위로 내려오셨는데, 바로 그 전날 그곳에서는 추기경이자 종교 재판소장이 ‘하느님의 더 큰 영광을 위하여’ 거의 백 명에 이르는 이단자들을, 임금과 궁정과 기사들과 추기경들과 더없이 아리따운 궁정 귀부인들과 세비야의 온 백성이 지켜보는 가운데, 장엄한 화형식을 거행하여 불태운 터였어. * 화형식: 종교 재판소가 이단자에게 판결을 선고하고 처형하던 공개 의식. 원어로는 ‘아우토 다 페(auto da fé)’, 곧 ‘신앙의 행위’라는 뜻."]], [["“He came softly, unobserved, and yet, strange to say, every one recognized Him.", "“그분은 가만히, 아무도 모르게 오셨지. 그런데도 이상한 일이지만, 모든 사람이 그분을 알아보았어."], ["That might be one of the best passages in the poem.", "이것이 어쩌면 이 시에서 가장 빼어난 대목 가운데 하나일지도 몰라."], ["I mean, why they recognized Him.", "그러니까, 사람들이 어째서 그분을 알아보았는가 하는 것 말이야."], ["The people are irresistibly drawn to Him, they surround Him, they flock about Him, follow Him.", "백성들은 거역할 수 없는 힘에 이끌려 그분께로 다가가고, 그분을 둘러싸고, 그분 주위로 몰려들며 그분을 따르지."], ["He moves silently in their midst with a gentle smile of infinite compassion.", "그분은 끝없는 연민이 어린 부드러운 미소를 띠고 그들 한가운데를 말없이 거니셔."], ["The sun of love burns in His heart, light and power shine from His eyes, and their radiance, shed on the people, stirs their hearts with responsive love.", "그분의 가슴에서는 사랑의 태양이 타오르고, 두 눈에서는 빛과 권능이 비쳐 나오며, 그 광채가 백성들에게 쏟아져 그들의 가슴에 화답하는 사랑을 불러일으켜."], ["He holds out His hands to them, blesses them, and a healing virtue comes from contact with Him, even with His garments.", "그분이 그들에게 손을 내밀어 축복하시니, 그분과의 접촉에서, 심지어 그분 옷자락과의 접촉에서도 병을 고치는 힘이 흘러나오지."], ["An old man in the crowd, blind from childhood, cries out, ‘O Lord, heal me and I shall see Thee!’ and, as it were, scales fall from his eyes and the blind man sees Him.", "군중 속에서 어려서부터 눈먼 한 노인이 ‘오 주여, 저를 고쳐 주소서, 그러면 당신을 뵐 수 있겠나이다!’ 하고 외치니, 마치 그의 눈에서 비늘이 떨어지듯 눈먼 자가 그분을 보게 돼."], ["The crowd weeps and kisses the earth under His feet.", "군중은 흐느끼며 그분 발밑의 땅에 입을 맞춰."], ["Children throw flowers before Him, sing, and cry hosannah.", "아이들은 그분 앞에 꽃을 뿌리며 노래하고 호산나를 외쳐."], ["‘It is He—it is He!’ all repeat.", "‘저분이야—저분이셔!’ 모두가 입을 모아 되뇌어."], ["‘It must be He, it can be no one but Him!’ He stops at the steps of the Seville cathedral at the moment when the weeping mourners are bringing in a little open white coffin.", "‘틀림없이 저분이셔, 저분 말고 다른 분일 리 없어!’ 그분은 세비야 대성당 계단 앞에서 걸음을 멈추시는데, 마침 흐느끼는 조문객들이 작고 뚜껑 없는 흰 관 하나를 들여오던 참이야."], ["In it lies a child of seven, the only daughter of a prominent citizen.", "그 안에는 어느 이름난 시민의 외동딸인 일곱 살배기 소녀가 누워 있어."], ["The dead child lies hidden in flowers.", "죽은 아이는 꽃에 파묻혀 보이지 않을 지경이야."], ["‘He will raise your child,’ the crowd shouts to the weeping mother.", "‘저분이 당신 아이를 살려 주실 거예요!’ 군중이 흐느끼는 어머니에게 외쳐."], ["The priest, coming to meet the coffin, looks perplexed, and frowns, but the mother of the dead child throws herself at His feet with a wail.", "관을 맞으러 나온 사제는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 눈살을 찌푸리지만, 죽은 아이의 어머니는 통곡하며 그분 발밑에 몸을 던져."], ["‘If it is Thou, raise my child!’ she cries, holding out her hands to Him.", "‘당신이 정녕 그분이시거든, 제 아이를 살려 주소서!’ 그녀가 그분께 두 손을 내밀며 부르짖어."], ["The procession halts, the coffin is laid on the steps at His feet.", "행렬이 멈추고, 관이 그분 발밑 계단 위에 놓여."], ["He looks with compassion, and His lips once more softly pronounce, ‘Maiden, arise!’ and the maiden arises.", "그분은 연민 어린 눈길로 바라보시고, 그 입술이 다시 한번 부드럽게 ‘소녀야, 일어나라!’ 하고 말씀하시니, 소녀가 일어나."], ["The little girl sits up in the coffin and looks round, smiling with wide-open wondering eyes, holding a bunch of white roses they had put in her hand.", "어린 소녀는 관 속에서 몸을 일으켜 두리번거리며, 손에 쥐여 준 흰 장미 한 다발을 든 채, 놀라 동그래진 눈으로 미소를 짓는 거야."]], [["“There are cries, sobs, confusion among the people, and at that moment the cardinal himself, the Grand Inquisitor, passes by the cathedral.", "“백성들 사이에서 외침과 흐느낌과 혼란이 일어. 바로 그 순간 추기경이자 종교 재판소장이 친히 대성당 곁을 지나가지."], ["He is an old man, almost ninety, tall and erect, with a withered face and sunken eyes, in which there is still a gleam of light.", "그는 노인이야, 거의 아흔에 가깝고, 키가 크고 꼿꼿하며, 메마른 얼굴에 움푹 꺼진 두 눈에는 아직도 한 줄기 빛이 남아 있어."], ["He is not dressed in his gorgeous cardinal’s robes, as he was the day before, when he was burning the enemies of the Roman Church—at this moment he is wearing his coarse, old, monk’s cassock.", "그는 어제, 로마 교회의 적들을 불태우던 그날처럼 화려한 추기경 예복을 걸치고 있지 않아—이 순간 그는 거칠고 낡은 수사의 법의를 입고 있어."], ["At a distance behind him come his gloomy assistants and slaves and the ‘holy guard.’ He stops at the sight of the crowd and watches it from a distance.", "그 뒤로는 멀찍이서 음울한 시종들과 노예들, 그리고 ‘성스러운 호위대’가 따라오지. 그는 군중을 보고 걸음을 멈추어 멀리서 그 광경을 지켜봐."], ["He sees everything; he sees them set the coffin down at His feet, sees the child rise up, and his face darkens.", "그는 모든 것을 보지. 사람들이 그분 발밑에 관을 내려놓는 것을 보고, 아이가 일어나는 것을 보자, 그의 얼굴이 어두워져."], ["He knits his thick gray brows and his eyes gleam with a sinister fire.", "그는 짙은 잿빛 눈썹을 찌푸리고, 두 눈에서는 불길한 불꽃이 번뜩여."], ["He holds out his finger and bids the guards take Him.", "그는 손가락을 들어 호위병들에게 그분을 붙잡으라고 명해."], ["And such is his power, so completely are the people cowed into submission and trembling obedience to him, that the crowd immediately makes way for the guards, and in the midst of deathlike silence they lay hands on Him and lead Him away.", "그의 권세가 어찌나 대단한지, 백성들이 어찌나 철저히 겁에 질려 그에게 떨며 복종하는지, 군중은 즉시 호위병들에게 길을 터 주고, 죽음 같은 정적 속에서 그들이 그분께 손을 대어 끌고 가는 거야."], ["The crowd instantly bows down to the earth, like one man, before the old Inquisitor.", "군중은 그 순간 한 사람처럼 일제히 늙은 종교 재판소장 앞에 땅에 엎드려."], ["He blesses the people in silence and passes on.", "그는 말없이 백성을 축복하고 지나가지."], ["The guards lead their prisoner to the close, gloomy vaulted prison in the ancient palace of the Holy Inquisition and shut Him in it.", "호위병들은 그 죄수를 종교 재판소의 옛 궁전에 있는 비좁고 음침한 둥근 천장의 감옥으로 끌고 가 가두어."], ["The day passes and is followed by the dark, burning, ‘breathless’ night of Seville.", "하루가 지나고, 어둡고 후텁지근한 세비야의 ‘숨 막히는’ 밤이 뒤따라."], ["The air is ‘fragrant with laurel and lemon.’ In the pitch darkness the iron door of the prison is suddenly opened and the Grand Inquisitor himself comes in with a light in his hand.", "공기에는 ‘월계수와 레몬 향기가 그윽해.’ 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 별안간 감옥의 철문이 열리고, 종교 재판소장이 친히 손에 등불을 든 채 들어와."], ["He is alone; the door is closed at once behind him.", "그는 혼자야. 그의 등 뒤로 문이 곧 닫혀."], ["He stands in the doorway and for a minute or two gazes into His face.", "그는 문간에 서서 일이 분 동안 그분의 얼굴을 응시해."], ["At last he goes up slowly, sets the light on the table and speaks.", "마침내 그는 천천히 다가가 등불을 탁자 위에 내려놓고는 입을 열어."]], [["“‘Is it Thou?", "“‘당신인가?"], ["Thou?’ but receiving no answer, he adds at once, ‘Don’t answer, be silent.", "당신이오?’ 그러나 아무 대답이 없자, 그는 곧 이렇게 덧붙여. ‘대답하지 마시오, 잠자코 계시오."], ["What canst Thou say, indeed?", "정말이지 당신이 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠소?"], ["I know too well what Thou wouldst say.", "당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있소."], ["And Thou hast no right to add anything to what Thou hadst said of old.", "게다가 당신은 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙일 권리가 없소."], ["Why, then, art Thou come to hinder us?", "그런데 어찌하여 우리를 방해하러 온 것이오?"], ["For Thou hast come to hinder us, and Thou knowest that.", "당신은 우리를 방해하러 온 것이고, 당신 자신도 그것을 알고 있소."], ["But dost Thou know what will be to-morrow?", "한데 내일 무슨 일이 벌어질지 아시오?"], ["I know not who Thou art and care not to know whether it is Thou or only a semblance of Him, but to-morrow I shall condemn Thee and burn Thee at the stake as the worst of heretics.", "나는 당신이 누구인지 모르고, 또 그것이 정말 당신인지 아니면 그저 그분의 허상인지 알고 싶지도 않으나, 내일 나는 당신을 가장 악한 이단자로 정죄하여 화형대에서 불태울 것이오."], ["And the very people who have to-day kissed Thy feet, to-morrow at the faintest sign from me will rush to heap up the embers of Thy fire.", "그리고 오늘 당신의 발에 입을 맞춘 바로 그 백성들이, 내일 내가 가장 희미한 손짓 하나만 보내도 당신을 태우는 불에 숯을 쌓으려고 앞다투어 달려들 것이오."], ["Knowest Thou that?", "그것을 아시오?"], ["Yes, maybe Thou knowest it,’ he added with thoughtful penetration, never for a moment taking his eyes off the Prisoner.”", "그래, 어쩌면 당신은 알고 있겠지.’ 그는 한순간도 죄수에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 깊이 꿰뚫는 듯한 사색에 잠겨 이렇게 덧붙여.”"]], [["“I don’t quite understand, Ivan.", "“잘 모르겠어, 이반 형."], ["What does it mean?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이야?”"], ["Alyosha, who had been listening in silence, said with a smile.", "줄곧 말없이 듣고 있던 알료샤가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Is it simply a wild fantasy, or a mistake on the part of the old man—some impossible quiproquo?”", "“그저 황당한 환상이라는 거야, 아니면 그 노인 쪽의 착각, 그러니까 있을 수 없는 사람 혼동이라는 거야?”"]], [["“Take it as the last,” said Ivan, laughing, “if you are so corrupted by modern realism and can’t stand anything fantastic.", "“네가 현대의 사실주의에 그토록 물들어 환상적인 것이라곤 도무지 견디지 못하겠거든, 마지막 것으로 받아들이렴.”"], ["If you like it to be a case of mistaken identity, let it be so.", "이반이 웃으며 말했다. “그게 사람을 잘못 본 경우이길 바란다면, 그렇게 해도 좋아."], ["It is true,”", "하긴 사실이지.”"], ["he went on, laughing, “the old man was ninety, and he might well be crazy over his set idea.", "그는 여전히 웃으며 말을 이었다. “그 노인은 아흔이었으니, 자기 고정관념에 사로잡혀 미쳐 있었다 해도 이상할 게 없어."], ["He might have been struck by the appearance of the Prisoner.", "죄수의 모습에 충격을 받았을 수도 있고."], ["It might, in fact, be simply his ravings, the delusion of an old man of ninety, over-excited by the auto da fé of a hundred heretics the day before.", "사실 그건 그저 그의 헛소리, 바로 전날 백 명의 이단자를 화형시키느라 지나치게 흥분한 아흔 살 노인의 망상에 지나지 않을 수도 있어."], ["But does it matter to us after all whether it was a mistake of identity or a wild fantasy?", "그런데 그게 사람을 잘못 본 것이었든 황당한 환상이었든, 결국 우리에게 무슨 상관이 있겠어?"], ["All that matters is that the old man should speak out, should speak openly of what he has thought in silence for ninety years.”", "중요한 건 오직, 그 노인이 마침내 입을 연다는 것, 아흔 해 동안 말없이 품어 온 것을 드러내 놓고 말한다는 것뿐이야.”"]], [["“And the Prisoner too is silent?", "“그러면 죄수 역시 잠자코 있어?"], ["Does He look at him and not say a word?”", "그분은 노인을 바라보기만 하고 한마디도 하지 않는 거야?”"]], [["“That’s inevitable in any case,”", "“어쨌든 그건 불가피한 일이지.”"], ["Ivan laughed again.", "이반이 다시 웃었다."], ["“The old man has told Him He hasn’t the right to add anything to what He has said of old.", "“노인이 그분께, 당신은 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙일 권리가 없다고 못 박았잖아."], ["One may say it is the most fundamental feature of Roman Catholicism, in my opinion at least.", "적어도 내 생각으로는, 그것이 로마 가톨릭교의 가장 근본적인 특징이라고 말할 수 있어."], ["‘All has been given by Thee to the Pope,’ they say, ‘and all, therefore, is still in the Pope’s hands, and there is no need for Thee to come now at all.", "‘모든 것은 당신에 의해 교황에게 넘겨졌으니, 따라서 모든 것은 여전히 교황의 손안에 있고, 이제 당신은 굳이 다시 올 필요가 전혀 없다."], ["Thou must not meddle for the time, at least.’ That’s how they speak and write too—the Jesuits, at any rate.", "적어도 지금은 끼어들지 말라.’ 그들은 그렇게 말하고, 또 그렇게 쓰기까지 해—적어도 예수회 사람들은 그래."], ["I have read it myself in the works of their theologians.", "나는 그들 신학자들의 저작에서 직접 읽었어."], ["‘Hast Thou the right to reveal to us one of the mysteries of that world from which Thou hast come?’ my old man asks Him, and answers the question for Him.", "‘당신은 우리에게, 당신이 떠나온 저 세계의 신비 가운데 하나를 드러낼 권리가 있는가?’ 하고 내 노인은 그분께 물은 뒤, 스스로 그 물음에 답해."], ["‘No, Thou hast not; that Thou mayest not add to what has been said of old, and mayest not take from men the freedom which Thou didst exalt when Thou wast on earth.", "‘아니, 당신에게는 그럴 권리가 없다. 옛날에 이미 말한 것에 무엇 하나 덧붙여서도 안 되고, 당신이 지상에 계실 때 그토록 떠받들었던 그 자유를 사람들에게서 빼앗아서도 안 된다."], ["Whatsoever Thou revealest anew will encroach on men’s freedom of faith; for it will be manifest as a miracle, and the freedom of their faith was dearer to Thee than anything in those days fifteen hundred years ago.", "당신이 새로이 드러내는 것은 무엇이든 사람들의 신앙의 자유를 침해할 것이니, 그것이 기적으로 드러날 것이기 때문이다. 한데 천오백 년 전 그 시절, 당신에게는 그들 신앙의 자유보다 더 소중한 것이 없었다."], ["Didst Thou not often say then, “I will make you free”?", "그때 당신은 「내가 너희를 자유롭게 하리라」고 자주 말하지 않았는가?"], ["But now Thou hast seen these “free”", "그러나 이제 당신은 그 「자유로운」"], ["men,’ the old man adds suddenly, with a pensive smile.", "자들을 보았다.’ 노인은 사색에 잠긴 미소를 띠고 별안간 이렇게 덧붙여."], ["‘Yes, we’ve paid dearly for it,’ he goes on, looking sternly at Him, ‘but at last we have completed that work in Thy name.", "‘그래, 우리는 그 대가를 톡톡히 치렀다.’ 그는 그분을 준엄하게 바라보며 말을 이어. ‘그러나 우리는 마침내 당신의 이름으로 그 일을 완수했다."], ["For fifteen centuries we have been wrestling with Thy freedom, but now it is ended and over for good.", "열다섯 세기 동안 우리는 당신의 자유와 씨름해 왔으나, 이제 그것은 끝났고 영영 마무리되었다."], ["Dost Thou not believe that it’s over for good?", "그것이 영영 끝났다는 것을 믿지 못하겠는가?"], ["Thou lookest meekly at me and deignest not even to be wroth with me.", "당신은 온순하게 나를 바라볼 뿐, 나에게 노여워하는 것조차 마다하는구나."], ["But let me tell Thee that now, to-day, people are more persuaded than ever that they have perfect freedom, yet they have brought their freedom to us and laid it humbly at our feet.", "그러나 내 말해 두겠는데, 바로 지금, 오늘날 사람들은 그 어느 때보다도 자기들이 완전한 자유를 누리고 있다고 굳게 믿고 있으나, 정작 그들은 제 자유를 우리에게 가져와 우리 발밑에 고분고분 내려놓았다."], ["But that has been our doing.", "그러나 그것은 우리가 이룬 일이다."], ["Was this what Thou didst?", "이것이 당신이 한 일이던가?"], ["Was this Thy freedom?’”", "이것이 당신의 자유였던가?’”"]], [["“I don’t understand again,”", "“또 모르겠어.”"], ["Alyosha broke in.", "알료샤가 끼어들었다."], ["“Is he ironical, is he jesting?”", "“노인이 빈정대는 거야, 농을 하는 거야?”"]], [["“Not a bit of it!", "“천만의 말씀!"], ["He claims it as a merit for himself and his Church that at last they have vanquished freedom and have done so to make men happy.", "노인은 마침내 자기와 자기 교회가 자유를 정복했다는 것을, 그것도 사람들을 행복하게 해 주려고 그렇게 했다는 것을 자기 공로로 내세우는 거야."], ["‘For now’ (he is speaking of the Inquisition, of course) ‘for the first time it has become possible to think of the happiness of men.", "‘이제야’(물론 종교 재판을 두고 하는 말이지) ‘비로소 사람들의 행복을 생각할 수 있게 되었다."], ["Man was created a rebel; and how can rebels be happy?", "인간은 반항자로 창조되었으니, 반항자들이 어찌 행복할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Thou wast warned,’ he says to Him.", "당신은 경고를 받았다.’ 노인이 그분께 말해."], ["‘Thou hast had no lack of admonitions and warnings, but Thou didst not listen to those warnings; Thou didst reject the only way by which men might be made happy.", "‘당신에게는 훈계와 경고가 부족하지 않았으나, 당신은 그 경고에 귀 기울이지 않았다. 당신은 사람들을 행복하게 할 수 있는 유일한 길을 물리쳤다."], ["But, fortunately, departing Thou didst hand on the work to us.", "그러나 다행히도, 떠나면서 당신은 그 일을 우리에게 맡겼다."], ["Thou hast promised, Thou hast established by Thy word, Thou hast given to us the right to bind and to unbind, and now, of course, Thou canst not think of taking it away.", "당신은 약속했고, 당신의 말로 정해 두었으며, 우리에게 매고 푸는 권리를 주었으니, 이제 와서 물론 그것을 도로 거두어 갈 수는 없다."], ["Why, then, hast Thou come to hinder us?’”", "그런데 어찌하여 우리를 방해하러 온 것인가?’”"]], [["“And what’s the meaning of ‘no lack of admonitions and warnings’?”", "“그런데 ‘훈계와 경고가 부족하지 않았다’는 건 무슨 뜻이야?”"], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Why, that’s the chief part of what the old man must say.", "“아, 그것이야말로 노인이 해야 할 말의 핵심이지."]], [["“‘The wise and dread spirit, the spirit of self-destruction and non- existence,’ the old man goes on, ‘the great spirit talked with Thee in the wilderness, and we are told in the books that he “tempted”", "“‘저 슬기롭고 무시무시한 영, 자기 파멸과 비존재의 영이.’ 노인이 말을 이어. ‘그 위대한 영이 광야에서 당신과 이야기를 나누었고, 성경은 그가 당신을 「시험했다」고 전한다."], ["Thee. Is that so?", "그런가?"], ["And could anything truer be said than what he revealed to Thee in three questions and what Thou didst reject, and what in the books is called “the temptation”?", "그가 세 가지 물음으로 당신께 드러낸 것, 당신이 물리친 것, 그리고 성경에서 「시험」이라 불리는 그것보다 더 참된 말을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["And yet if there has ever been on earth a real stupendous miracle, it took place on that day, on the day of the three temptations.", "만일 이 땅에 일찍이 진정 어마어마한 기적이 있었다면, 그것은 바로 그날, 그 세 가지 시험의 날에 일어난 것이다. 그 세 물음을 던졌다는 것 자체가 기적이었다."], ["The statement of those three questions was itself the miracle.", "단지 논의를 위해 이렇게 한번 상상해 보자."], ["If it were possible to imagine simply for the sake of argument that those three questions of the dread spirit had perished utterly from the books, and that we had to restore them and to invent them anew, and to do so had gathered together all the wise men of the earth—rulers, chief priests, learned men, philosophers, poets—and had set them the task to invent three questions, such as would not only fit the occasion, but express in three words, three human phrases, the whole future history of the world and of humanity—dost Thou believe that all the wisdom of the earth united could have invented anything in depth and force equal to the three questions which were actually put to Thee then by the wise and mighty spirit in the wilderness?", "그 무시무시한 영의 세 물음이 성경에서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버려, 우리가 그것을 복원하고 새로 지어내야만 하게 되었으며, 그러기 위해 이 땅의 모든 지혜로운 자들—통치자들, 대제사장들, 학자들, 철학자들, 시인들—을 한자리에 불러 모아, 단지 그 상황에 들어맞을 뿐 아니라 세 마디 말, 세 마디 인간의 문구로 세계와 인류의 미래 역사 전체를 표현해 낼 세 가지 물음을 지어내라는 과제를 그들에게 맡겼다고 치자. 그렇다면 당신은 정말로 믿는가, 이 땅의 모든 지혜를 한데 모은들, 그때 광야에서 슬기롭고 강대한 영이 실제로 당신께 던졌던 세 물음에 견줄 만큼 깊고 힘찬 무언가를 지어낼 수 있었으리라고?"], ["From those questions alone, from the miracle of their statement, we can see that we have here to do not with the fleeting human intelligence, but with the absolute and eternal.", "오직 그 물음들만으로도, 그것이 던져졌다는 기적만으로도, 우리는 여기서 다루는 것이 덧없는 인간의 지성이 아니라 절대적이고 영원한 것임을 알 수 있다."], ["For in those three questions the whole subsequent history of mankind is, as it were, brought together into one whole, and foretold, and in them are united all the unsolved historical contradictions of human nature.", "그 세 물음 속에는 인류의 그 후의 역사 전체가 말하자면 하나로 응축되어 예언되어 있고, 그 안에는 인간 본성의 풀리지 않은 역사적 모순들이 죄다 한데 엮여 있기 때문이다."], ["At the time it could not be so clear, since the future was unknown; but now that fifteen hundred years have passed, we see that everything in those three questions was so justly divined and foretold, and has been so truly fulfilled, that nothing can be added to them or taken from them.", "그 당시에는 미래가 알려지지 않았으니 그토록 분명할 수 없었으나, 이제 천오백 년이 지난 지금, 우리는 그 세 물음 속의 모든 것이 그토록 정확히 헤아려지고 예언되었으며 그토록 진실되게 이루어졌기에, 거기에 무엇 하나 더할 수도 뺄 수도 없음을 본다."]], [["“‘Judge Thyself who was right—Thou or he who questioned Thee then?", "“‘누가 옳았는지 당신 스스로 판단해 보라—당신인가, 아니면 그때 당신께 물음을 던진 자인가? 첫 번째 물음을 떠올려 보라."], ["Remember the first question; its meaning, in other words, was this: “Thou wouldst go into the world, and art going with empty hands, with some promise of freedom which men in their simplicity and their natural unruliness cannot even understand, which they fear and dread—for nothing has ever been more insupportable for a man and a human society than freedom.", "다른 말로 하자면 그 뜻은 이러했다. 「너는 세상으로 나가려 하면서 빈손으로, 무슨 자유의 약속 따위를 들고 가는데, 그것은 사람들이 그 단순함과 타고난 무질서함으로 인해 도무지 이해조차 못 하는 것이요, 두려워하고 무서워하는 것이다—인간에게도 인간 사회에게도 자유보다 더 견디기 힘든 것은 일찍이 없었기 때문이다."], ["But seest Thou these stones in this parched and barren wilderness?", "그러나 이 메마르고 황량한 광야의 저 돌들이 보이느냐?"], ["Turn them into bread, and mankind will run after Thee like a flock of sheep, grateful and obedient, though for ever trembling, lest Thou withdraw Thy hand and deny them Thy bread.”", "저것들을 빵으로 바꾸어라, 그러면 인류는 양 떼처럼 네 뒤를 따라 달려올 것이니, 감사하고 순종하면서, 다만 네가 손을 거두어 빵을 주지 않을까 늘 떨면서 그리할 것이다.」"], ["But Thou wouldst not deprive man of freedom and didst reject the offer, thinking, what is that freedom worth, if obedience is bought with bread?", "그러나 당신은 인간에게서 자유를 빼앗으려 하지 않았기에 그 제안을 물리쳤다. 순종을 빵으로 산다면 그런 자유가 무슨 값어치가 있겠느냐고 생각하면서 말이다."], ["Thou didst reply that man lives not by bread alone.", "당신은 사람이 빵으로만 사는 것이 아니라고 대답했다."], ["But dost Thou know that for the sake of that earthly bread the spirit of the earth will rise up against Thee and will strive with Thee and overcome Thee, and all will follow him, crying, “Who can compare with this beast?", "그러나 아는가, 바로 그 지상의 빵을 빌미로 땅의 영이 당신을 거슬러 일어나 당신과 다투어 당신을 이길 것이며, 모든 자들이 그를 따르며 「누가 이 짐승에 견줄 수 있으랴? 그가 우리에게 하늘에서 불을 내려 주었도다!」"], ["He has given us fire from heaven!”", "하고 외치게 되리라는 것을?"], ["Dost Thou know that the ages will pass, and humanity will proclaim by the lips of their sages that there is no crime, and therefore no sin; there is only hunger?", "아는가, 세월이 흘러 인류가 제 현자들의 입을 빌려 죄악이란 없고 따라서 죄도 없으며 오직 굶주림만 있을 뿐이라고 선포하게 되리라는 것을?"], ["“Feed men, and then ask of them virtue!”", "「사람들을 먹인 다음에 그들에게 덕을 요구하라!」"], ["that’s what they’ll write on the banner, which they will raise against Thee, and with which they will destroy Thy temple.", "그들이 당신을 거슬러 치켜들 깃발에, 그것으로 당신의 성전을 무너뜨릴 그 깃발에 그렇게 쓸 것이다. 당신의 성전이 섰던 자리에 새 건물이 일어설 것이니, 그 무시무시한 바벨탑이 다시 세워질 것이다."], ["Where Thy temple stood will rise a new building; the terrible tower of Babel will be built again, and though, like the one of old, it will not be finished, yet Thou mightest have prevented that new tower and have cut short the sufferings of men for a thousand years; for they will come back to us after a thousand years of agony with their tower.", "그것 역시 옛것처럼 끝내 완성되지는 못하겠으나, 그래도 당신은 그 새 탑을 막아 천 년 동안 사람들의 고통을 줄일 수도 있었을 것이다. 그들은 천 년에 걸친 고뇌 끝에 자기네 탑을 들고 우리에게 되돌아올 것이기 때문이다."], ["They will seek us again, hidden underground in the catacombs, for we shall be again persecuted and tortured.", "그들은 다시 우리를 찾을 것이니, 땅속 지하 묘지에 숨은 우리를 찾을 것이다. 우리는 또다시 박해받고 고문당할 것이기 때문이다."], ["They will find us and cry to us, “Feed us, for those who have promised us fire from heaven haven’t given it!”", "그들은 우리를 찾아내어 우리에게 부르짖으리라. 「우리를 먹여 주시오, 하늘에서 불을 주겠다고 약속한 자들이 그것을 주지 않았으니!」"], ["And then we shall finish building their tower, for he finishes the building who feeds them.", "그리하여 우리가 그들의 탑을 마저 짓게 되리니, 탑을 완성하는 자는 곧 그들을 먹이는 자이기 때문이다."], ["And we alone shall feed them in Thy name, declaring falsely that it is in Thy name.", "그리고 오직 우리만이 당신의 이름으로 그들을 먹이되, 당신의 이름이라 거짓을 말하며 먹일 것이다."], ["Oh, never, never can they feed themselves without us!", "오, 결코, 결코 그들은 우리 없이는 제 힘으로 먹지 못한다!"], ["No science will give them bread so long as they remain free.", "그들이 자유로운 한, 어떤 과학도 그들에게 빵을 주지 못할 것이다."], ["In the end they will lay their freedom at our feet, and say to us, “Make us your slaves, but feed us.”", "결국 그들은 제 자유를 우리 발밑에 내려놓고 우리에게 이렇게 말할 것이다. 「우리를 당신들의 노예로 삼으시오, 그 대신 먹여만 주시오.」"], ["They will understand themselves, at last, that freedom and bread enough for all are inconceivable together, for never, never will they be able to share between them!", "그들도 마침내 깨닫게 되리라, 자유와 모두에게 넉넉한 빵이 한데 양립할 수 없음을, 그들끼리는 결코, 결코 그것을 나누어 가질 수 없으리라는 것을!"], ["They will be convinced, too, that they can never be free, for they are weak, vicious, worthless and rebellious.", "또한 그들은 자기들이 결코 자유로울 수 없음을 확신하게 되리니, 그들은 약하고 비루하며 쓸모없고 반항적이기 때문이다."], ["Thou didst promise them the bread of Heaven, but, I repeat again, can it compare with earthly bread in the eyes of the weak, ever sinful and ignoble race of man?", "당신은 그들에게 하늘의 빵을 약속했으나, 거듭 말하거니와, 약하고 늘 죄짓고 비천한 인간 족속의 눈에 그것이 지상의 빵에 견줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["And if for the sake of the bread of Heaven thousands shall follow Thee, what is to become of the millions and tens of thousands of millions of creatures who will not have the strength to forego the earthly bread for the sake of the heavenly?", "그리고 하늘의 빵을 위해 수천 명이 당신을 따른다 한들, 하늘의 빵을 위해 지상의 빵을 포기할 힘이 없는 수백만, 수만 곱절의 수백만 피조물들은 어찌 되겠는가?"], ["Or dost Thou care only for the tens of thousands of the great and strong, while the millions, numerous as the sands of the sea, who are weak but love Thee, must exist only for the sake of the great and strong?", "아니면 당신은 오직 그 위대하고 강한 자 수만 명만을 아끼고, 바다의 모래알처럼 수많은, 약하지만 당신을 사랑하는 그 수백만은 오직 위대하고 강한 자들을 위해서만 존재해야 한단 말인가?"], ["No, we care for the weak too.", "아니, 우리는 약한 자들도 아낀다."], ["They are sinful and rebellious, but in the end they too will become obedient.", "그들은 죄 많고 반항적이나, 결국은 그들 역시 순종하게 되리라."], ["They will marvel at us and look on us as gods, because we are ready to endure the freedom which they have found so dreadful and to rule over them—so awful it will seem to them to be free.", "그들은 우리를 경탄하며 신처럼 우러러볼 것이니, 그들이 그토록 무서워하던 그 자유를 우리가 기꺼이 떠맡아 그들을 다스리려 하기 때문이다—자유롭다는 것이 그들에게는 그토록 끔찍하게 여겨질 것이다."], ["But we shall tell them that we are Thy servants and rule them in Thy name.", "그러나 우리는 그들에게, 우리가 당신의 종이며 당신의 이름으로 그들을 다스린다고 말하리라."], ["We shall deceive them again, for we will not let Thee come to us again.", "우리는 또다시 그들을 속일 것이니, 당신이 다시 우리에게 오도록 두지 않을 것이기 때문이다."], ["That deception will be our suffering, for we shall be forced to lie.", "그 기만이 곧 우리의 고난이 되리니, 우리는 거짓말을 하지 않을 수 없을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["“‘This is the significance of the first question in the wilderness, and this is what Thou hast rejected for the sake of that freedom which Thou hast exalted above everything.", "“‘이것이 광야에서의 첫 번째 물음의 의미이며, 이것이 바로 당신이 무엇보다도 떠받든 그 자유를 위해 물리친 것이다."], ["Yet in this question lies hid the great secret of this world.", "그러나 이 물음 속에 이 세상의 위대한 비밀이 숨어 있다."], ["Choosing “bread,” Thou wouldst have satisfied the universal and everlasting craving of humanity—to find some one to worship.", "「빵」을 택했더라면, 당신은 인류의 보편적이고 영원한 갈망을—곧 경배할 대상을 찾으려는 갈망을 채워 주었을 것이다."], ["So long as man remains free he strives for nothing so incessantly and so painfully as to find some one to worship.", "인간은 자유로운 한, 경배할 대상을 찾는 일만큼 끊임없이, 그토록 고통스럽게 애쓰는 일이 달리 없다."], ["But man seeks to worship what is established beyond dispute, so that all men would agree at once to worship it.", "그러나 인간은 의심할 여지 없이 확립된 것을 경배하려 드니, 모든 사람이 즉시 한뜻으로 그것을 경배하도록 하기 위함이다."], ["For these pitiful creatures are concerned not only to find what one or the other can worship, but to find something that all would believe in and worship; what is essential is that all may be together in it.", "이 가련한 피조물들은 단지 이 사람이나 저 사람이 경배할 수 있는 것을 찾는 데 그치지 않고, 모두가 믿고 경배할 만한 것을 찾는 데 골몰하기 때문이다. 무엇보다 중요한 것은 모두가 그 안에서 함께할 수 있어야 한다는 것이다."], ["This craving for community of worship is the chief misery of every man individually and of all humanity from the beginning of time.", "이 경배의 공동체를 향한 갈망이야말로 태초부터 개개인의, 그리고 온 인류의 가장 큰 비참이다."], ["For the sake of common worship they’ve slain each other with the sword.", "그 공통의 경배를 위해 그들은 칼로 서로를 죽여 왔다."], ["They have set up gods and challenged one another, “Put away your gods and come and worship ours, or we will kill you and your gods!”", "그들은 신들을 세워 놓고 서로 도전하며 외쳤다. 「너희 신들을 치워 버리고 와서 우리 신을 경배하라, 그러지 않으면 너희와 너희 신들을 죽이리라!」"], ["And so it will be to the end of the world, even when gods disappear from the earth; they will fall down before idols just the same.", "그리고 세상 끝날까지 그러하리니, 땅에서 신들이 사라진다 해도 그들은 마찬가지로 우상 앞에 엎드릴 것이다. 당신은 인간 본성의 이 근본적인 비밀을 알고 있었다, 모를 수가 없었을 것이다."], ["Thou didst know, Thou couldst not but have known, this fundamental secret of human nature, but Thou didst reject the one infallible banner which was offered Thee to make all men bow down to Thee alone—the banner of earthly bread; and Thou hast rejected it for the sake of freedom and the bread of Heaven.", "그런데도 당신은 모든 사람이 오직 당신 한 분께 엎드리게 할 그 틀림없는 깃발을—지상의 빵이라는 깃발을 거절했다. 그리고 자유와 하늘의 빵을 위해 그것을 물리쳤다."], ["Behold what Thou didst further.", "보라, 당신이 그다음에 한 일을."], ["And all again in the name of freedom!", "그리고 그 또한 죄다 자유의 이름으로!"], ["I tell Thee that man is tormented by no greater anxiety than to find some one quickly to whom he can hand over that gift of freedom with which the ill-fated creature is born.", "내 당신께 이르노니, 인간을 괴롭히는 것 가운데 그 불운한 피조물이 타고난 자유라는 선물을 어서 넘겨줄 누군가를 찾는 일보다 더 큰 근심은 없다."], ["But only one who can appease their conscience can take over their freedom.", "그러나 그들의 양심을 달래 줄 수 있는 자만이 그들의 자유를 떠맡을 수 있다."], ["In bread there was offered Thee an invincible banner; give bread, and man will worship thee, for nothing is more certain than bread.", "빵 속에는 당신께 무적의 깃발이 주어졌으니, 빵을 주라, 그러면 인간은 당신을 경배하리니, 빵보다 더 확실한 것은 없기 때문이다."], ["But if some one else gains possession of his conscience—oh!", "그러나 만일 다른 누군가가 그의 양심을 손에 넣는다면—오!"], ["then he will cast away Thy bread and follow after him who has ensnared his conscience.", "그때 그는 당신의 빵을 내던지고, 그의 양심을 사로잡은 자를 따라가리라."], ["In that Thou wast right.", "그 점에서 당신은 옳았다."], ["For the secret of man’s being is not only to live but to have something to live for.", "인간 존재의 비밀은 단지 사는 데 있는 것이 아니라 무언가 살아갈 까닭을 갖는 데 있기 때문이다."], ["Without a stable conception of the object of life, man would not consent to go on living, and would rather destroy himself than remain on earth, though he had bread in abundance.", "삶의 목적에 대한 확고한 관념이 없다면 인간은 계속 살기를 거부할 것이며, 빵이 넘쳐난다 해도 이 땅에 머무느니 차라리 제 목숨을 끊으려 할 것이다. 그것은 사실이다."], ["That is true. But what happened?", "그러나 무슨 일이 일어났는가?"], ["Instead of taking men’s freedom from them, Thou didst make it greater than ever!", "사람들에게서 자유를 빼앗기는커녕, 당신은 그것을 그 어느 때보다 더 크게 만들어 놓았다!"], ["Didst Thou forget that man prefers peace, and even death, to freedom of choice in the knowledge of good and evil?", "인간이 선악을 아는 가운데 선택하는 자유보다 평안을, 심지어 죽음을 더 좋아한다는 것을 당신은 잊었는가?"], ["Nothing is more seductive for man than his freedom of conscience, but nothing is a greater cause of suffering.", "인간에게 양심의 자유보다 더 유혹적인 것은 없으나, 동시에 그보다 더 큰 고통의 원천도 없다."], ["And behold, instead of giving a firm foundation for setting the conscience of man at rest for ever, Thou didst choose all that is exceptional, vague and enigmatic; Thou didst choose what was utterly beyond the strength of men, acting as though Thou didst not love them at all—Thou who didst come to give Thy life for them!", "그런데 보라, 인간의 양심을 영영 평안케 할 굳건한 토대를 주기는커녕, 당신은 온갖 예외적이고 막연하며 수수께끼 같은 것을 택했고, 인간의 힘에 도무지 부치는 것을 택했으니, 마치 그들을 조금도 사랑하지 않는 것처럼 행한 것이다—그들을 위해 목숨을 바치러 온 당신이!"], ["Instead of taking possession of men’s freedom, Thou didst increase it, and burdened the spiritual kingdom of mankind with its sufferings for ever.", "사람들의 자유를 차지하기는커녕, 당신은 그것을 늘려 놓았고, 인류의 정신 왕국을 그 고통으로 영영 짓눌러 놓았다."], ["Thou didst desire man’s free love, that he should follow Thee freely, enticed and taken captive by Thee.", "당신은 인간의 자유로운 사랑을 갈망했으니, 그가 당신께 매혹되고 사로잡혀 자유로이 당신을 따르기를 바랐다."], ["In place of the rigid ancient law, man must hereafter with free heart decide for himself what is good and what is evil, having only Thy image before him as his guide.", "엄격한 옛 율법 대신, 인간은 이제부터 오직 당신의 형상만을 길잡이로 삼고서 자유로운 마음으로 무엇이 선이고 무엇이 악인지 스스로 결정해야 한다."], ["But didst Thou not know that he would at last reject even Thy image and Thy truth, if he is weighed down with the fearful burden of free choice?", "그러나 당신은 몰랐는가, 자유로운 선택이라는 무시무시한 짐에 짓눌리면 인간은 결국 당신의 형상마저, 당신의 진리마저 물리치게 되리라는 것을?"], ["They will cry aloud at last that the truth is not in Thee, for they could not have been left in greater confusion and suffering than Thou hast caused, laying upon them so many cares and unanswerable problems.", "그들은 마침내 진리가 당신 안에 있지 않다고 소리 높여 부르짖으리니, 당신이 그토록 많은 근심과 풀리지 않는 문제들을 그들에게 지운 것보다 더 큰 혼란과 고통 속에 그들을 버려둘 수는 없었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["“‘So that, in truth, Thou didst Thyself lay the foundation for the destruction of Thy kingdom, and no one is more to blame for it.", "“‘그리하여 참으로, 당신은 친히 당신 왕국이 무너질 토대를 놓은 것이며, 그 누구도 당신보다 더 책임이 크지 않다."], ["Yet what was offered Thee?", "그런데 당신께 주어진 것은 무엇이었던가?"], ["There are three powers, three powers alone, able to conquer and to hold captive for ever the conscience of these impotent rebels for their happiness—those forces are miracle, mystery and authority.", "세 가지 힘이 있다, 오직 세 가지 힘만이 이 무력한 반항자들의 양심을 그들의 행복을 위해 영영 정복하여 사로잡아 둘 수 있으니—그 힘이란 기적과 신비와 권위다."], ["Thou hast rejected all three and hast set the example for doing so.", "당신은 그 셋을 모두 물리쳤고, 그렇게 함으로써 본을 보였다."], ["When the wise and dread spirit set Thee on the pinnacle of the temple and said to Thee, “If Thou wouldst know whether Thou art the Son of God then cast Thyself down, for it is written: the angels shall hold him up lest he fall and bruise himself, and Thou shalt know then whether Thou art the Son of God and shalt prove then how great is Thy faith in Thy Father.”", "슬기롭고 무시무시한 영이 당신을 성전 꼭대기에 세우고 당신께 말했다. 「네가 하느님의 아들인지 알고 싶거든 뛰어내려라, 기록되었으되, 천사들이 그를 떠받들어 그가 떨어져 다치지 않게 하리라 하였으니, 그러면 너는 네가 하느님의 아들인지 알게 될 것이요, 네 아버지에 대한 네 믿음이 얼마나 큰지 증명하게 되리라.」"], ["But Thou didst refuse and wouldst not cast Thyself down.", "그러나 당신은 거절하고 뛰어내리려 하지 않았다."], ["Oh, of course, Thou didst proudly and well, like God; but the weak, unruly race of men, are they gods?", "오, 물론 당신은 신답게, 자랑스럽고 훌륭하게 처신했다. 그러나 약하고 무질서한 인간 족속이, 그들이 신인가?"], ["Oh, Thou didst know then that in taking one step, in making one movement to cast Thyself down, Thou wouldst be tempting God and have lost all Thy faith in Him, and wouldst have been dashed to pieces against that earth which Thou didst come to save.", "오, 당신은 알았다, 한 걸음 내딛는 것만으로도, 뛰어내리려는 몸짓 하나만으로도 당신은 하느님을 시험하는 것이 되어 그분에 대한 믿음을 모조리 잃을 것이며, 당신이 구하러 온 바로 그 땅에 부딪쳐 산산이 부서지리라는 것을."], ["And the wise spirit that tempted Thee would have rejoiced.", "그리고 당신을 시험한 슬기로운 영은 기뻐했을 것이다."], ["But I ask again, are there many like Thee?", "그러나 다시 묻노니, 당신 같은 자가 그리 많겠는가?"], ["And couldst Thou believe for one moment that men, too, could face such a temptation?", "그리고 당신은 한순간이라도 믿을 수 있었는가, 인간들 역시 그런 시험을 견뎌 낼 수 있으리라고?"], ["Is the nature of men such, that they can reject miracle, and at the great moments of their life, the moments of their deepest, most agonizing spiritual difficulties, cling only to the free verdict of the heart?", "인간의 본성이 그러한가, 기적을 물리치고, 그들 삶의 위대한 순간에, 가장 깊고 가장 괴로운 영적 고난의 순간에 오직 마음의 자유로운 판결에만 매달릴 수 있을 만큼?"], ["Oh, Thou didst know that Thy deed would be recorded in books, would be handed down to remote times and the utmost ends of the earth, and Thou didst hope that man, following Thee, would cling to God and not ask for a miracle.", "오, 당신은 알았다, 당신의 행적이 책에 기록되어 머나먼 시대와 땅끝까지 전해지리라는 것을. 그리고 당신은 바랐다, 인간이 당신을 따라 하느님께 매달리고 기적을 구하지 않기를."], ["But Thou didst not know that when man rejects miracle he rejects God too; for man seeks not so much God as the miraculous.", "그러나 당신은 몰랐다, 인간이 기적을 물리치면 하느님마저 물리친다는 것을. 인간은 하느님보다도 기적을 더 찾기 때문이다."], ["And as man cannot bear to be without the miraculous, he will create new miracles of his own for himself, and will worship deeds of sorcery and witchcraft, though he might be a hundred times over a rebel, heretic and infidel.", "그리고 인간은 기적 없이는 견디지 못하므로, 제 손으로 새 기적을 만들어 내어 요술과 마법의 행위를 경배하리니, 그가 백 번을 거듭 반항자요 이단자요 불신자라 하더라도 그러할 것이다."], ["Thou didst not come down from the Cross when they shouted to Thee, mocking and reviling Thee, “Come down from the cross and we will believe that Thou art He.”", "그들이 당신을 조롱하고 욕하며 「십자가에서 내려오라, 그러면 네가 그분인 줄 믿겠다」고 외쳤을 때, 당신은 십자가에서 내려오지 않았다."], ["Thou didst not come down, for again Thou wouldst not enslave man by a miracle, and didst crave faith given freely, not based on miracle.", "당신은 내려오지 않았으니, 또다시 기적으로 인간을 노예 삼으려 하지 않았고, 기적에 근거하지 않은, 자유로이 바쳐진 믿음을 갈망했기 때문이다."], ["Thou didst crave for free love and not the base raptures of the slave before the might that has overawed him for ever.", "당신은 자유로운 사랑을 갈망했지, 자신을 영영 압도해 버린 권능 앞에서 노예가 느끼는 비천한 황홀을 갈망하지 않았다."], ["But Thou didst think too highly of men therein, for they are slaves, of course, though rebellious by nature.", "그러나 그 점에서 당신은 인간을 너무 높이 평가했으니, 물론 그들은 본성이 반항적이긴 해도 결국 노예이기 때문이다."], ["Look round and judge; fifteen centuries have passed, look upon them.", "둘러보고 판단해 보라. 열다섯 세기가 흘렀으니, 그들을 바라보라."], ["Whom hast Thou raised up to Thyself?", "당신은 그들 가운데 누구를 당신께로 끌어올렸는가?"], ["I swear, man is weaker and baser by nature than Thou hast believed him!", "내 맹세컨대, 인간은 당신이 믿었던 것보다 본성이 더 약하고 더 비천하다!"], ["Can he, can he do what Thou didst?", "그가, 그가 당신이 한 일을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["By showing him so much respect, Thou didst, as it were, cease to feel for him, for Thou didst ask far too much from him—Thou who hast loved him more than Thyself!", "그를 그토록 존중함으로써 당신은 말하자면 그를 더는 가엾이 여기지 않게 된 것이니, 당신이 그에게 너무도 많은 것을 요구했기 때문이다—당신 자신보다 그를 더 사랑한 당신이!"], ["Respecting him less, Thou wouldst have asked less of him.", "그를 덜 존중했더라면 그에게 덜 요구했을 것이다."], ["That would have been more like love, for his burden would have been lighter.", "그편이 오히려 사랑에 더 가까웠을 것이니, 그의 짐이 더 가벼웠을 것이기 때문이다."], ["He is weak and vile.", "그는 약하고 비루하다."], ["What though he is everywhere now rebelling against our power, and proud of his rebellion?", "지금 그가 곳곳에서 우리의 권력에 반항하며 제 반항을 자랑한들 어떠랴?"], ["It is the pride of a child and a schoolboy.", "그것은 어린아이와 학생의 자만에 지나지 않는다."], ["They are little children rioting and barring out the teacher at school.", "그들은 학교에서 소동을 부리며 선생을 내쫓는 어린아이들이다."], ["But their childish delight will end; it will cost them dear.", "그러나 그들의 유치한 환희는 끝날 것이고, 그들은 그 대가를 톡톡히 치를 것이다."], ["They will cast down temples and drench the earth with blood.", "그들은 성전을 무너뜨리고 피로 땅을 적실 것이다."], ["But they will see at last, the foolish children, that, though they are rebels, they are impotent rebels, unable to keep up their own rebellion.", "그러나 그 어리석은 아이들도 마침내 알게 되리니, 자기들이 반항자이긴 해도 무력한 반항자, 제 반항조차 끌고 나가지 못하는 자들임을 깨달을 것이다."], ["Bathed in their foolish tears, they will recognize at last that He who created them rebels must have meant to mock at them.", "어리석은 눈물에 젖어, 그들은 마침내 자기들을 반항자로 창조한 그분이 필시 자기들을 조롱하려 하셨던 것이라고 깨달으리라."], ["They will say this in despair, and their utterance will be a blasphemy which will make them more unhappy still, for man’s nature cannot bear blasphemy, and in the end always avenges it on itself.", "그들은 절망 속에서 이렇게 말할 것이며, 그 말은 신성 모독이 되어 그들을 한층 더 불행하게 만들리니, 인간의 본성은 신성 모독을 견디지 못하여 결국 언제나 제 자신에게 그것을 되갚기 때문이다."], ["And so unrest, confusion and unhappiness—that is the present lot of man after Thou didst bear so much for their freedom!", "그리하여 불안과 혼란과 불행이—이것이 당신이 그들의 자유를 위해 그토록 많은 것을 견뎌 낸 끝에 인간에게 남겨진 오늘의 몫이다!"], ["The great prophet tells in vision and in image, that he saw all those who took part in the first resurrection and that there were of each tribe twelve thousand.", "그 위대한 예언자는 환상과 형상으로 이르기를, 첫 번째 부활에 참여한 모든 자를 보았으며 각 지파에서 만 이천씩이 있었다고 했다."], ["But if there were so many of them, they must have been not men but gods.", "그러나 그들이 그토록 많았다면 그들은 인간이 아니라 신이었을 것이다."], ["They had borne Thy cross, they had endured scores of years in the barren, hungry wilderness, living upon locusts and roots—and Thou mayest indeed point with pride at those children of freedom, of free love, of free and splendid sacrifice for Thy name.", "그들은 당신의 십자가를 졌고, 메마르고 굶주린 광야에서 메뚜기와 풀뿌리로 연명하며 수십 년을 견뎌 냈다—그러니 당신은 참으로 그 자유의 자녀들, 자유로운 사랑의 자녀들, 당신 이름을 위해 자유로이 빛나는 희생을 바친 자녀들을 자랑스레 가리킬 수도 있으리라."], ["But remember that they were only some thousands; and what of the rest?", "그러나 잊지 말라, 그들은 고작 수천에 지나지 않았음을. 그렇다면 나머지는 어찌 되는가?"], ["And how are the other weak ones to blame, because they could not endure what the strong have endured?", "그리고 강한 자들이 견뎌 낸 것을 견뎌 낼 수 없었다 하여, 다른 약한 자들에게 무슨 잘못이 있는가?"], ["How is the weak soul to blame that it is unable to receive such terrible gifts?", "그토록 무시무시한 선물을 받아들이지 못한다 하여 약한 영혼에게 무슨 잘못이 있는가?"], ["Canst Thou have simply come to the elect and for the elect?", "당신은 그저 선택된 자들에게, 선택된 자들을 위해서만 온 것인가?"], ["But if so, it is a mystery and we cannot understand it.", "그렇다면 그것은 신비이니 우리로서는 이해할 수 없다."], ["And if it is a mystery, we too have a right to preach a mystery, and to teach them that it’s not the free judgment of their hearts, not love that matters, but a mystery which they must follow blindly, even against their conscience. So we have done.", "그리고 그것이 신비라면, 우리에게도 신비를 설교할 권리가 있으며, 그들에게 중요한 것은 그들 마음의 자유로운 판단도, 사랑도 아니라 그들이 제 양심을 거슬러서라도 맹목적으로 따라야 할 신비라고 가르칠 권리가 있다."], ["We have corrected Thy work and have founded it upon miracle, mystery and authority.", "그리하여 우리는 그렇게 했다. 우리는 당신의 일을 바로잡아, 그것을 기적과 신비와 권위 위에 세웠다."], ["And men rejoiced that they were again led like sheep, and that the terrible gift that had brought them such suffering was, at last, lifted from their hearts.", "그러자 사람들은 자기들이 또다시 양 떼처럼 인도받는 것을, 그토록 큰 고통을 안겨 주었던 그 무시무시한 선물이 마침내 제 가슴에서 들리는 것을 기뻐했다."], ["Were we right teaching them this? Speak!", "우리가 그들에게 이렇게 가르친 것이 옳았는가?"], ["Did we not love mankind, so meekly acknowledging their feebleness, lovingly lightening their burden, and permitting their weak nature even sin with our sanction?", "말해 보라! 우리는 그토록 온순하게 그들의 연약함을 인정하고, 사랑으로 그들의 짐을 덜어 주며, 그들의 연약한 본성이 우리의 승인 아래 죄짓는 것마저 허락했으니, 인류를 사랑하지 않은 것이던가?"], ["Why hast Thou come now to hinder us?", "그런데 어찌하여 이제 와서 우리를 방해하러 온 것인가?"], ["And why dost Thou look silently and searchingly at me with Thy mild eyes?", "그리고 어찌하여 그 온화한 눈으로 말없이 나를 꿰뚫어 보는가? 노여워하라."], ["Be angry. I don’t want Thy love, for I love Thee not.", "나는 당신의 사랑을 원치 않으니, 나 또한 당신을 사랑하지 않기 때문이다."], ["And what use is it for me to hide anything from Thee?", "게다가 내가 당신께 무엇을 숨긴들 무슨 소용이겠는가?"], ["Don’t I know to Whom I am speaking?", "내가 누구에게 말하고 있는지 내가 모를 줄 아는가?"], ["All that I can say is known to Thee already.", "내가 할 수 있는 모든 말은 이미 당신께 알려져 있다."], ["And is it for me to conceal from Thee our mystery?", "그러니 내가 우리의 비밀을 당신께 숨길 까닭이 있겠는가?"], ["Perhaps it is Thy will to hear it from my lips.", "어쩌면 내 입으로 그것을 듣는 것이 당신의 뜻인지도 모르겠다."], ["Listen, then.", "그렇다면 들어 보라."], ["We are not working with Thee, but with him—that is our mystery.", "우리는 당신과 함께 일하는 것이 아니라 그와 함께 일하고 있다—그것이 우리의 비밀이다."], ["It’s long—eight centuries—since we have been on his side and not on Thine.", "우리가 당신 편이 아니라 그의 편에 선 지도 오래되었으니, 어느덧 여덟 세기다."], ["Just eight centuries ago, we took from him what Thou didst reject with scorn, that last gift he offered Thee, showing Thee all the kingdoms of the earth.", "꼭 여덟 세기 전, 당신이 멸시하며 물리친 것, 그가 당신께 이 땅의 모든 나라를 보여 주며 내놓았던 그 마지막 선물을 우리는 그에게서 받았다."], ["We took from him Rome and the sword of Cæsar, and proclaimed ourselves sole rulers of the earth, though hitherto we have not been able to complete our work.", "우리는 그에게서 로마와 카이사르의 칼을 받아, 우리야말로 이 땅의 유일한 통치자라고 선포했다. 비록 지금껏 우리 일을 완성하지는 못했으나 말이다."], ["But whose fault is that?", "그러나 그것이 누구의 잘못인가?"], ["Oh, the work is only beginning, but it has begun.", "오, 일은 이제 겨우 시작되었으되, 그래도 시작되었다."], ["It has long to await completion and the earth has yet much to suffer, but we shall triumph and shall be Cæsars, and then we shall plan the universal happiness of man.", "완성되기까지는 아직 멀었고 이 땅은 아직 많은 고통을 겪어야 하겠으나, 우리는 끝내 승리하여 카이사르가 될 것이며, 그때 비로소 인류의 보편적 행복을 설계하리라."], ["But Thou mightest have taken even then the sword of Cæsar.", "그러나 당신은 그때라도 카이사르의 칼을 받을 수 있었다."], ["Why didst Thou reject that last gift?", "어찌하여 그 마지막 선물을 물리쳤는가?"], ["Hadst Thou accepted that last counsel of the mighty spirit, Thou wouldst have accomplished all that man seeks on earth—that is, some one to worship, some one to keep his conscience, and some means of uniting all in one unanimous and harmonious ant-heap, for the craving for universal unity is the third and last anguish of men.", "당신이 그 강대한 영의 마지막 조언을 받아들였더라면, 인간이 이 땅에서 찾는 모든 것을 이루어 주었을 것이다—곧 경배할 대상, 양심을 맡아 줄 자, 그리고 모두를 하나의 한뜻 되고 조화로운 개미집으로 묶어 줄 방편을. 보편적 통합을 향한 갈망이야말로 인간의 세 번째이자 마지막 고뇌이기 때문이다."], ["Mankind as a whole has always striven to organize a universal state.", "인류는 전체로서 언제나 보편적 국가를 세우려 애써 왔다."], ["There have been many great nations with great histories, but the more highly they were developed the more unhappy they were, for they felt more acutely than other people the craving for world-wide union.", "위대한 역사를 지닌 위대한 민족이 많았으나, 더 높이 발전할수록 그들은 더 불행했으니, 다른 어떤 민족보다도 세계적 통합을 향한 갈망을 더 절절히 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["The great conquerors, Timours and Ghenghis-Khans, whirled like hurricanes over the face of the earth striving to subdue its people, and they too were but the unconscious expression of the same craving for universal unity.", "위대한 정복자들, 티무르와 칭기즈 칸은 이 땅의 백성을 굴복시키려 애쓰며 허리케인처럼 지표면을 휩쓸었는데, 그들 역시 다름 아닌 이 보편적 통합을 향한 갈망의 무의식적 표현이었다."], ["Hadst Thou taken the world and Cæsar’s purple, Thou wouldst have founded the universal state and have given universal peace.", "당신이 세계와 카이사르의 자줏빛 옷을 받았더라면, 보편적 국가를 세우고 보편적 평화를 주었을 것이다."], ["For who can rule men if not he who holds their conscience and their bread in his hands?", "인간의 양심과 빵을 손에 쥔 자가 아니라면 누가 인간을 다스릴 수 있겠는가?"], ["We have taken the sword of Cæsar, and in taking it, of course, have rejected Thee and followed him.", "우리는 카이사르의 칼을 받았으며, 그것을 받음으로써 물론 당신을 물리치고 그를 따랐다."], ["Oh, ages are yet to come of the confusion of free thought, of their science and cannibalism.", "오, 자유로운 사상의 혼란과 그들의 과학과 식인의 시대가 아직 닥쳐올 것이다."], ["For having begun to build their tower of Babel without us, they will end, of course, with cannibalism.", "그들은 우리 없이 제 바벨탑을 짓기 시작했으니, 물론 결국엔 식인으로 끝맺을 것이기 때문이다."], ["But then the beast will crawl to us and lick our feet and spatter them with tears of blood.", "그러나 그때 그 짐승이 우리에게 기어와 우리 발을 핥으며 피눈물을 뿌릴 것이다."], ["And we shall sit upon the beast and raise the cup, and on it will be written, “Mystery.”", "그리고 우리는 그 짐승 위에 올라앉아 잔을 치켜들 것이니, 그 위에는 「신비」라고 쓰여 있으리라."], ["But then, and only then, the reign of peace and happiness will come for men.", "그러나 그때, 오직 그때에야 비로소 인간에게 평화와 행복의 시대가 도래하리라."], ["Thou art proud of Thine elect, but Thou hast only the elect, while we give rest to all.", "당신은 당신의 선택된 자들을 자랑하나, 당신에게는 오직 선택된 자들뿐인 반면, 우리는 모든 자에게 안식을 준다."], ["And besides, how many of those elect, those mighty ones who could become elect, have grown weary waiting for Thee, and have transferred and will transfer the powers of their spirit and the warmth of their heart to the other camp, and end by raising their free banner against Thee.", "게다가 그 선택된 자들, 선택된 자가 될 수도 있었던 그 강한 자들 가운데서도 얼마나 많은 이가 당신을 기다리다 지쳐, 제 정신의 힘과 제 가슴의 온기를 다른 진영으로 옮겨 가고 또 옮겨 갈 것이며, 끝내 당신을 거슬러 제 「자유로운」 깃발을 치켜들 것인가."], ["Thou didst Thyself lift up that banner.", "당신이 친히 그 깃발을 들어 올린 것이다."], ["But with us all will be happy and will no more rebel nor destroy one another as under Thy freedom.", "그러나 우리와 함께라면 모두가 행복할 것이며, 당신의 자유 아래서처럼 더는 반항하지도 서로를 죽이지도 않으리라."], ["Oh, we shall persuade them that they will only become free when they renounce their freedom to us and submit to us.", "오, 우리는 그들을 설득하리라, 자기 자유를 우리에게 내려놓고 우리에게 복종할 때에야 비로소 자유로워지리라고."], ["And shall we be right or shall we be lying?", "그러면 우리가 옳은 것인가, 거짓을 말하는 것인가?"], ["They will be convinced that we are right, for they will remember the horrors of slavery and confusion to which Thy freedom brought them.", "그들은 우리가 옳다고 확신하리니, 당신의 자유가 그들을 몰아넣은 노예 상태와 혼란의 끔찍함을 기억할 것이기 때문이다. 자유와 자유로운 사상과 과학은 그들을 그토록 곤경으로 몰아넣고, 그토록 기이한 것들과 풀리지 않는 신비들 앞에 그들을 세워 놓을 것이니, 그들 가운데 사납고 반항적인 자들은 제 목숨을 끊을 것이요, 반항적이되 약한 자들은 서로를 죽일 것이며, 그 나머지 약하고 불행한 자들은 우리 발밑으로 알랑거리며 기어와 우리에게 칭얼대리라."], ["Freedom, free thought and science, will lead them into such straits and will bring them face to face with such marvels and insoluble mysteries, that some of them, the fierce and rebellious, will destroy themselves, others, rebellious but weak, will destroy one another, while the rest, weak and unhappy, will crawl fawning to our feet and whine to us: “Yes, you were right, you alone possess His mystery, and we come back to you, save us from ourselves!”", "「그래요, 당신들이 옳았소, 오직 당신들만이 그분의 신비를 지니고 있소. 우리가 당신들께 돌아왔으니, 우리 자신에게서 우리를 구해 주시오!」"]], [["“‘Receiving bread from us, they will see clearly that we take the bread made by their hands from them, to give it to them, without any miracle.", "“‘우리에게서 빵을 받으면서, 그들은 우리가 아무 기적도 없이 제 손으로 빚은 빵을 그들에게서 거두어 다시 그들에게 나누어 줄 뿐임을 똑똑히 보게 될 것이다."], ["They will see that we do not change the stones to bread, but in truth they will be more thankful for taking it from our hands than for the bread itself!", "그들은 우리가 돌을 빵으로 바꾸는 것이 아님을 보게 되겠으나, 정녕 그들은 그 빵 자체보다도 우리 손에서 그것을 받는 것에 더 감사하리라!"], ["For they will remember only too well that in old days, without our help, even the bread they made turned to stones in their hands, while since they have come back to us, the very stones have turned to bread in their hands.", "옛날에는 우리의 도움 없이 제 손으로 빚은 빵조차 그들 손안에서 돌로 변했던 것을, 그러나 우리에게 되돌아온 뒤로는 바로 그 돌이 그들 손안에서 빵으로 변했던 것을 그들은 너무도 잘 기억할 것이기 때문이다."], ["Too, too well will they know the value of complete submission!", "완전한 복종의 값어치를 그들은 너무도, 너무도 잘 알게 되리라!"], ["And until men know that, they will be unhappy.", "그리고 인간이 그것을 알기 전까지 그들은 불행하리라."], ["Who is most to blame for their not knowing it?—speak!", "그들이 그것을 알지 못하는 데에 누구의 책임이 가장 큰가?—말해 보라!"], ["Who scattered the flock and sent it astray on unknown paths?", "누가 양 떼를 흩어 놓고 알 수 없는 길로 헤매게 했는가?"], ["But the flock will come together again and will submit once more, and then it will be once for all.", "그러나 양 떼는 다시 모여들어 또 한 번 복종할 것이며, 그때는 영영 그러하리라."], ["Then we shall give them the quiet humble happiness of weak creatures such as they are by nature.", "그때 우리는 그들에게, 그들이 본성상 그러한 약한 피조물에게 어울리는 조용하고 겸허한 행복을 주리라."], ["Oh, we shall persuade them at last not to be proud, for Thou didst lift them up and thereby taught them to be proud.", "오, 우리는 마침내 그들을 설득하여 자만하지 않게 하리니, 당신이 그들을 치켜올려 자만하도록 가르쳤기 때문이다."], ["We shall show them that they are weak, that they are only pitiful children, but that childlike happiness is the sweetest of all.", "우리는 그들에게 보여 주리라, 그들이 약하다는 것, 그저 가련한 어린아이일 뿐이라는 것, 그러나 어린아이의 행복이야말로 그 무엇보다 달콤하다는 것을."], ["They will become timid and will look to us and huddle close to us in fear, as chicks to the hen.", "그들은 겁먹은 채 우리를 우러러보며, 병아리가 어미 닭에게 하듯 두려움에 떨며 우리에게 바싹 붙어들 것이다."], ["They will marvel at us and will be awe-stricken before us, and will be proud at our being so powerful and clever, that we have been able to subdue such a turbulent flock of thousands of millions.", "그들은 우리를 경탄하며 우리 앞에서 경외에 사로잡힐 것이고, 우리가 그토록 강하고 영리하여 수천만에 이르는 그 사나운 양 떼를 굴복시킬 수 있었다는 데에 자랑스러워하리라."], ["They will tremble impotently before our wrath, their minds will grow fearful, they will be quick to shed tears like women and children, but they will be just as ready at a sign from us to pass to laughter and rejoicing, to happy mirth and childish song.", "그들은 우리의 노여움 앞에 무력하게 떨 것이며, 그들의 마음은 겁에 질리고, 여자와 아이들처럼 쉬이 눈물을 흘릴 것이나, 우리의 손짓 하나에 그만큼이나 쉬이 웃음과 환희로, 행복한 즐거움과 어린아이의 노래로 옮아갈 것이다."], ["Yes, we shall set them to work, but in their leisure hours we shall make their life like a child’s game, with children’s songs and innocent dance.", "그렇다, 우리는 그들에게 일을 시키겠으나, 한가한 시간에는 그들의 삶을 어린아이의 놀이처럼, 어린아이의 노래와 천진한 춤이 어우러진 것으로 만들어 주리라."], ["Oh, we shall allow them even sin, they are weak and helpless, and they will love us like children because we allow them to sin.", "오, 우리는 그들에게 죄짓는 것마저 허락하리니, 그들은 약하고 무력하며, 우리가 죄를 허락해 주기에 어린아이처럼 우리를 사랑할 것이다."], ["We shall tell them that every sin will be expiated, if it is done with our permission, that we allow them to sin because we love them, and the punishment for these sins we take upon ourselves.", "우리는 그들에게 이르리라, 우리의 허락 아래 저질러진 죄라면 어떤 죄든 사함을 받을 것이며, 우리가 그들을 사랑하기에 죄짓는 것을 허락하는 것이요, 그 죄에 대한 벌은 우리가 떠맡으리라고."], ["And we shall take it upon ourselves, and they will adore us as their saviors who have taken on themselves their sins before God.", "그리하여 우리는 그것을 떠맡을 것이며, 그들은 하느님 앞에서 제 죄를 대신 짊어진 구원자로 우리를 흠모하리라."], ["And they will have no secrets from us.", "그리고 그들은 우리에게 아무 비밀도 두지 않으리라."], ["We shall allow or forbid them to live with their wives and mistresses, to have or not to have children—according to whether they have been obedient or disobedient—and they will submit to us gladly and cheerfully.", "그들이 아내와 정부와 동침하는 것을, 자식을 두거나 두지 않는 것을—그들이 순종했는가 거역했는가에 따라—우리가 허락하거나 금하리니, 그들은 기꺼이 즐거이 우리에게 복종할 것이다."], ["The most painful secrets of their conscience, all, all they will bring to us, and we shall have an answer for all.", "그들 양심의 가장 괴로운 비밀들을, 모조리, 모조리 그들은 우리에게 가져올 것이며, 우리는 그 모든 것에 대한 답을 갖고 있으리라."], ["And they will be glad to believe our answer, for it will save them from the great anxiety and terrible agony they endure at present in making a free decision for themselves.", "그러면 그들은 기꺼이 우리의 답을 믿으리니, 그것이 지금 그들이 스스로 자유로운 결정을 내리느라 겪는 큰 근심과 무시무시한 고뇌에서 그들을 구해 줄 것이기 때문이다."], ["And all will be happy, all the millions of creatures except the hundred thousand who rule over them.", "그리하여 모두가 행복하리라, 그들을 다스리는 십만 명을 빼고는 그 수백만 피조물 모두가."], ["For only we, we who guard the mystery, shall be unhappy.", "오직 우리, 신비를 지키는 우리만이 불행할 것이기 때문이다."], ["There will be thousands of millions of happy babes, and a hundred thousand sufferers who have taken upon themselves the curse of the knowledge of good and evil.", "행복한 갓난아이들이 수천만이요, 선악을 아는 저주를 제 몸에 떠맡은 고난받는 자가 십만이리라."], ["Peacefully they will die, peacefully they will expire in Thy name, and beyond the grave they will find nothing but death.", "그들은 평화로이 죽을 것이며, 평화로이 당신의 이름으로 숨을 거두겠으나, 무덤 너머에서 죽음 외에는 아무것도 찾지 못하리라."], ["But we shall keep the secret, and for their happiness we shall allure them with the reward of heaven and eternity.", "그러나 우리는 그 비밀을 지킬 것이며, 그들의 행복을 위해 천국과 영생의 상을 미끼로 그들을 꾈 것이다."], ["Though if there were anything in the other world, it certainly would not be for such as they.", "비록 저세상에 무언가 있다 한들, 그것은 분명 그들 같은 자들을 위한 것은 아니겠으나 말이다."], ["It is prophesied that Thou wilt come again in victory, Thou wilt come with Thy chosen, the proud and strong, but we will say that they have only saved themselves, but we have saved all.", "당신이 승리 가운데 다시 오시리라, 당신의 선택받은 자들, 그 자랑스럽고 강한 자들과 함께 오시리라 예언되어 있으나, 우리는 말하리라, 그들은 오직 자기 자신만을 구했을 뿐, 우리는 모든 자를 구했노라고."], ["We are told that the harlot who sits upon the beast, and holds in her hands the mystery, shall be put to shame, that the weak will rise up again, and will rend her royal purple and will strip naked her loathsome body.", "짐승 위에 앉아 손에 그 「신비」를 쥔 음녀가 수치를 당하리라고, 약한 자들이 다시 일어나 그녀의 왕다운 자줏빛 옷을 찢고 그 가증스러운 몸을 발가벗기리라고 우리는 듣는다."], ["But then I will stand up and point out to Thee the thousand millions of happy children who have known no sin.", "그러나 그때 나는 일어서서, 죄라고는 모르고 살아온 그 수천만 행복한 어린아이들을 당신께 가리켜 보이리라."], ["And we who have taken their sins upon us for their happiness will stand up before Thee and say: “Judge us if Thou canst and darest.”", "그리고 그들의 행복을 위해 그들의 죄를 떠맡은 우리는 당신 앞에 일어서서 말하리라. 「할 수 있고 또 감히 그러하거든, 우리를 심판하라.」"], ["Know that I fear Thee not.", "알아 두라, 나는 당신이 두렵지 않다."], ["Know that I too have been in the wilderness, I too have lived on roots and locusts, I too prized the freedom with which Thou hast blessed men, and I too was striving to stand among Thy elect, among the strong and powerful, thirsting “to make up the number.”", "알아 두라, 나 또한 광야에 있었고, 나 또한 풀뿌리와 메뚜기로 연명했으며, 나 또한 당신이 사람들에게 복으로 내린 그 자유를 소중히 여겼고, 나 또한 「그 수를 채우려」 갈망하며 당신의 선택받은 자들, 그 강하고 권능 있는 자들 가운데 서려고 애썼다."], ["But I awakened and would not serve madness.", "그러나 나는 깨어났고, 광기를 섬기려 하지 않았다."], ["I turned back and joined the ranks of those who have corrected Thy work.", "나는 발길을 돌려, 당신의 일을 바로잡은 자들의 대열에 합류했다. 나는 자랑스러운 자들을 떠나 겸허한 자들에게로 돌아왔으니, 겸허한 자들의 행복을 위해서였다."], ["I left the proud and went back to the humble, for the happiness of the humble.", "내가 당신께 하는 말은 그대로 이루어질 것이며, 우리의 지배는 세워질 것이다."], ["What I say to Thee will come to pass, and our dominion will be built up.", "거듭 말하노니, 내일 당신은 보게 되리라, 내 손짓 하나에 당신을 태울 장작더미 둘레로 뜨거운 잿불을 앞다투어 쌓아 올릴 그 순종하는 양 떼를."], ["I repeat, to-morrow Thou shalt see that obedient flock who at a sign from me will hasten to heap up the hot cinders about the pile on which I shall burn Thee for coming to hinder us.", "우리를 방해하러 왔다는 이유로 내가 당신을 불태울 그 장작더미 말이다. 일찍이 우리의 불에 처해 마땅한 자가 있었다면, 그것은 바로 당신이기 때문이다."], ["For if any one has ever deserved our fires, it is Thou.", "내일 나는 당신을 불태우리라. 내 할 말은 끝났다.’”"], ["To-morrow I shall burn Thee. Dixi.’”", "* 디시(Dixi): 라틴어로 ‘내가 말했다’, 곧 ‘내 할 말은 다 했다’는 뜻의 변론 종결 상투어."]], [["Ivan stopped.", "이반은 말을 멈추었다."], ["He was carried away as he talked, and spoke with excitement; when he had finished, he suddenly smiled.", "그는 이야기하는 동안 격해져서 흥분하여 말했는데, 다 끝내고 나자 별안간 미소를 지었다."]], [["Alyosha had listened in silence; towards the end he was greatly moved and seemed several times on the point of interrupting, but restrained himself.", "알료샤는 말없이 듣고 있었다. 끝 무렵에 그는 크게 마음이 동요하여 몇 번이나 끼어들려는 듯하다가도 가까스로 자제했다."], ["Now his words came with a rush.", "이제 그의 말이 봇물 터지듯 쏟아져 나왔다."]], [["“But ...", "“하지만……"], ["that’s absurd!”", "그건 터무니없어!”"], ["he cried, flushing.", "그가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“Your poem is in praise of Jesus, not in blame of Him—as you meant it to be.", "“형의 시는 형이 의도한 것과 달리 예수를 비난하는 게 아니라 오히려 찬미하고 있어."], ["And who will believe you about freedom?", "게다가 자유에 관한 형 말을 누가 믿겠어?"], ["Is that the way to understand it?", "그걸 그렇게 이해해야 한단 말이야?"], ["That’s not the idea of it in the Orthodox Church....", "정교회에서는 그런 식으로 생각하지 않아……"], ["That’s Rome, and not even the whole of Rome, it’s false—those are the worst of the Catholics, the Inquisitors, the Jesuits!...", "그건 로마야, 그것도 로마 전체도 아니고, 그건 거짓이야—그건 가톨릭교도 중에서도 가장 악질인 자들, 종교 재판관들, 예수회 사람들이라고!……"], ["And there could not be such a fantastic creature as your Inquisitor.", "그리고 형이 그린 그런 환상적인 인물은 있을 수가 없어."], ["What are these sins of mankind they take on themselves?", "그들이 떠맡는다는 인류의 죄라는 게 대체 뭐야?"], ["Who are these keepers of the mystery who have taken some curse upon themselves for the happiness of mankind?", "인류의 행복을 위해 무슨 저주를 제 몸에 떠맡았다는, 그 신비의 수호자들은 또 누구야?"], ["When have they been seen?", "그런 자들을 언제 본 적이나 있어?"], ["We know the Jesuits, they are spoken ill of, but surely they are not what you describe?", "우리도 예수회를 알아, 사람들이 그들을 욕하긴 하지만, 그들이 정말 형이 묘사한 그런 자들이겠어?"], ["They are not that at all, not at all....", "그들은 전혀 그렇지 않아, 결코 그렇지 않다고……"], ["They are simply the Romish army for the earthly sovereignty of the world in the future, with the Pontiff of Rome for Emperor ...", "그들은 그저 장차 이 세계를 지상에서 다스리려는 로마의 군대일 뿐이고, 로마 교황을 황제로 떠받드는……"], ["that’s their ideal, but there’s no sort of mystery or lofty melancholy about it....", "그게 그들의 이상이지만, 거기엔 무슨 신비도 숭고한 우수도 없어……"], ["It’s simple lust of power, of filthy earthly gain, of domination—something like a universal serfdom with them as masters—that’s all they stand for.", "그저 권력욕, 더러운 지상의 이득에 대한 욕심, 지배욕일 뿐이야—저들을 주인으로 둔 일종의 보편적 농노제 같은 것 말이야. 그게 저들이 추구하는 전부야."], ["They don’t even believe in God perhaps.", "어쩌면 하느님조차 믿지 않을 거야."], ["Your suffering Inquisitor is a mere fantasy.”", "형의 그 고뇌하는 종교 재판관은 한낱 환상일 뿐이야.”"]], [["“Stay, stay,”", "“가만, 가만.”"], ["laughed Ivan, “how hot you are!", "이반이 웃었다. “어쩜 그리 흥분하는지!"], ["A fantasy you say, let it be so!", "환상이라고? 그래, 그렇다고 치자!"], ["Of course it’s a fantasy.", "물론 환상이지."], ["But allow me to say: do you really think that the Roman Catholic movement of the last centuries is actually nothing but the lust of power, of filthy earthly gain?", "하지만 한 가지만 말하게 해 다오. 너는 정말로 지난 몇 세기 동안의 로마 가톨릭 운동이 실제로 그저 권력욕, 더러운 지상의 이득에 대한 욕심에 지나지 않는다고 생각해?"], ["Is that Father Païssy’s teaching?”", "그게 파이시 신부님의 가르침이야?”"]], [["“No, no, on the contrary, Father Païssy did once say something rather the same as you ...", "“아니, 아니, 오히려 그 반대야. 파이시 신부님도 언젠가 형이랑 좀 비슷한 말씀을 하시긴 했지만……"], ["but of course it’s not the same, not a bit the same,”", "물론 그건 같은 게 아니야, 조금도 같지 않아.”"], ["Alyosha hastily corrected himself.", "알료샤가 황급히 정정했다."]], [["“A precious admission, in spite of your ‘not a bit the same.’ I ask you why your Jesuits and Inquisitors have united simply for vile material gain?", "“네가 ‘조금도 같지 않다’고 하긴 해도, 참으로 값진 인정이로구나. 나는 너에게 묻겠어, 어째서 너의 예수회와 종교 재판관들이 그저 비열한 물질적 이득을 위해 뭉쳤다는 거지?"], ["Why can there not be among them one martyr oppressed by great sorrow and loving humanity?", "어째서 그들 가운데 큰 슬픔에 짓눌리고 인류를 사랑하는 순교자 한 사람쯤 있을 수 없다는 거야?"], ["You see, only suppose that there was one such man among all those who desire nothing but filthy material gain—if there’s only one like my old Inquisitor, who had himself eaten roots in the desert and made frenzied efforts to subdue his flesh to make himself free and perfect.", "보렴, 더러운 물질적 이득밖에 바라는 것이 없는 그 모든 자들 가운데 단 한 사람이라도 그런 인물이 있었다고 가정해 보자—내 늙은 종교 재판관처럼, 자기 자신을 자유롭고 완전하게 만들려고 광야에서 풀뿌리를 먹으며 제 육신을 굴복시키려 미친 듯이 애쓴, 그런 사람이 단 한 명이라도 있었다고 말이야. 그런데도 그는 평생 인류를 사랑했어."], ["But yet all his life he loved humanity, and suddenly his eyes were opened, and he saw that it is no great moral blessedness to attain perfection and freedom, if at the same time one gains the conviction that millions of God’s creatures have been created as a mockery, that they will never be capable of using their freedom, that these poor rebels can never turn into giants to complete the tower, that it was not for such geese that the great idealist dreamt his dream of harmony.", "그러다 별안간 그의 눈이 뜨여, 완전함과 자유에 이르는 것이 그리 대단한 도덕적 축복이 아님을 깨달은 거야. 만일 그와 동시에, 하느님의 피조물 수백만이 조롱거리로 창조되어 결코 제 자유를 쓸 줄 모르게 되었다는 것, 그 가련한 반항자들이 결코 탑을 완성할 거인으로 자라날 수 없다는 것, 그 위대한 이상주의자가 조화의 꿈을 꾼 것은 그런 거위들을 위해서가 아니었다는 것을 확신하게 된다면 말이야."], ["Seeing all that he turned back and joined—the clever people.", "그 모든 것을 본 그는 발길을 돌려—영리한 자들에게 합류한 거지."], ["Surely that could have happened?”", "분명 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있지 않았겠어?”"]], [["“Joined whom, what clever people?”", "“누구에게 합류했다는 거야, 무슨 영리한 자들 말이야?”"], ["cried Alyosha, completely carried away.", "완전히 휩쓸려 든 알료샤가 외쳤다."], ["“They have no such great cleverness and no mysteries and secrets....", "“그들에게는 그런 대단한 영리함도 없고, 무슨 신비도 비밀도 없어……"], ["Perhaps nothing but Atheism, that’s all their secret.", "어쩌면 무신론밖에 없을 거야, 그게 그들 비밀의 전부지."], ["Your Inquisitor does not believe in God, that’s his secret!”", "형의 종교 재판관은 하느님을 믿지 않아, 그게 그의 비밀이야!”"]], [["“What if it is so!", "“설령 그렇다 한들 어떠랴!"], ["At last you have guessed it.", "마침내 네가 알아맞혔구나."], ["It’s perfectly true, it’s true that that’s the whole secret, but isn’t that suffering, at least for a man like that, who has wasted his whole life in the desert and yet could not shake off his incurable love of humanity?", "그건 완벽한 사실이야, 그게 비밀의 전부라는 건 정말이지. 하지만 그건 고통이 아니겠어, 적어도 그런 사람에게는 말이야, 평생을 광야에서 허비하고도 인류에 대한 그 고질적인 사랑을 끝내 떨쳐 버리지 못한 사람에게는?"], ["In his old age he reached the clear conviction that nothing but the advice of the great dread spirit could build up any tolerable sort of life for the feeble, unruly, ‘incomplete, empirical creatures created in jest.’ And so, convinced of this, he sees that he must follow the counsel of the wise spirit, the dread spirit of death and destruction, and therefore accept lying and deception, and lead men consciously to death and destruction, and yet deceive them all the way so that they may not notice where they are being led, that the poor blind creatures may at least on the way think themselves happy.", "노년에 이르러 그는 분명한 확신에 다다랐어, 오직 그 위대하고 무시무시한 영의 조언만이 그 연약하고 무질서한, ‘농담 삼아 창조된 미완의 경험적 피조물들’에게 그나마 견딜 만한 삶을 세워 줄 수 있다는 확신에 말이야. 그리하여 이를 확신한 그는 깨닫지, 자신은 그 슬기로운 영, 죽음과 파멸의 무시무시한 영의 조언을 따라야 하며, 따라서 거짓과 기만을 받아들여 사람들을 의식적으로 죽음과 파멸로 이끌되, 그러면서도 그들이 어디로 끌려가는지 알아채지 못하도록 내내 그들을 속여, 그 가련한 눈먼 피조물들이 적어도 가는 길에서나마 스스로 행복하다고 여기게 해야 한다는 것을."], ["And note, the deception is in the name of Him in Whose ideal the old man had so fervently believed all his life long.", "그리고 명심해, 그 기만은 바로 그 노인이 평생토록 그토록 뜨겁게 믿어 온 이상의 주인이신 그분의 이름으로 행해지는 거야."], ["Is not that tragic?", "이것이 비극이 아니란 말이야?"], ["And if only one such stood at the head of the whole army ‘filled with the lust of power only for the sake of filthy gain’—would not one such be enough to make a tragedy?", "그리고 만일 ‘오직 더러운 이득을 위해 권력욕에 가득 찬’ 그 온 군대의 수장에 그런 사람이 단 하나만 서 있어도—그런 사람 하나만으로도 비극을 빚어내기에 충분하지 않겠어?"], ["More than that, one such standing at the head is enough to create the actual leading idea of the Roman Church with all its armies and Jesuits, its highest idea.", "그뿐 아니라, 그런 사람 하나가 수장에 서 있는 것만으로도 그 모든 군대와 예수회를 거느린 로마 교회의 실질적인 지도 이념, 그 최고의 이념을 만들어 내기에 충분해."], ["I tell you frankly that I firmly believe that there has always been such a man among those who stood at the head of the movement.", "솔직히 말하지만, 나는 그 운동의 수장에 섰던 자들 가운데 언제나 그런 사람이 있었다고 굳게 믿어."], ["Who knows, there may have been some such even among the Roman Popes.", "누가 알겠어, 로마 교황들 가운데에도 그런 자가 더러 있었을지."], ["Who knows, perhaps the spirit of that accursed old man who loves mankind so obstinately in his own way, is to be found even now in a whole multitude of such old men, existing not by chance but by agreement, as a secret league formed long ago for the guarding of the mystery, to guard it from the weak and the unhappy, so as to make them happy.", "누가 알겠어, 제 나름의 방식으로 그토록 끈질기게 인류를 사랑하는 그 저주받은 노인의 정신이, 어쩌면 지금도 그런 노인들의 무리 전체 속에 깃들어 있을지를. 우연이 아니라 합의로 존재하는, 신비를 지키기 위해 오래전에 결성된 비밀 동맹으로서, 약하고 불행한 자들을 행복하게 해 주려고 그들에게서 그 신비를 지키는 노인들의 무리 속에 말이야."], ["No doubt it is so, and so it must be indeed.", "틀림없이 그러할 것이고, 또 정녕 그래야만 하지."], ["I fancy that even among the Masons there’s something of the same mystery at the bottom, and that that’s why the Catholics so detest the Masons as their rivals breaking up the unity of the idea, while it is so essential that there should be one flock and one shepherd....", "내 생각엔 프리메이슨들 사이에도 밑바닥에는 그와 똑같은 신비 비슷한 것이 있는 것 같아. 그래서 가톨릭교도들이 프리메이슨을 그토록 미워하는 거야, 이념의 통일을 깨뜨리는 경쟁자로 여겨서지."], ["But from the way I defend my idea I might be an author impatient of your criticism.", "한 무리, 한 목자가 있어야 한다는 것이 그토록 긴요한 마당에 말이야…… 하지만 내가 내 생각을 변호하는 꼴을 보아하니, 마치 자기 작품에 대한 비평을 못 견뎌 하는 작가 같구나."], ["Enough of it.”", "이쯤 해 두자.”"]], [["“You are perhaps a Mason yourself!”", "“형은 어쩌면 형 자신이 프리메이슨인지도 몰라!”"], ["broke suddenly from Alyosha.", "알료샤의 입에서 별안간 이 말이 튀어나왔다."], ["“You don’t believe in God,”", "“형은 하느님을 믿지 않아.”"], ["he added, speaking this time very sorrowfully.", "그가 이번에는 무척 서글픈 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["He fancied besides that his brother was looking at him ironically.", "게다가 그는 형이 자기를 빈정대는 눈으로 바라보는 것만 같았다."], ["“How does your poem end?”", "“형의 시는 어떻게 끝나?”"], ["he asked, suddenly looking down.", "그가 별안간 고개를 떨구며 물었다."], ["“Or was it the end?”", "“아니면 그게 끝이었어?”"]], [["“I meant to end it like this.", "“이렇게 끝맺을 작정이었어."], ["When the Inquisitor ceased speaking he waited some time for his Prisoner to answer him.", "종교 재판관이 말을 마치고는 한동안 죄수가 자기에게 대답해 주기를 기다려."], ["His silence weighed down upon him.", "그분의 침묵이 그를 짓눌러."], ["He saw that the Prisoner had listened intently all the time, looking gently in his face and evidently not wishing to reply.", "그는 죄수가 내내 온화하게 자기 얼굴을 바라보며 골똘히 귀 기울였을 뿐, 분명 대꾸하고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것을 알아차려."], ["The old man longed for Him to say something, however bitter and terrible.", "노인은 그분이 무슨 말이든, 아무리 쓰디쓰고 무시무시한 말이라도 해 주기를 간절히 바라."], ["But He suddenly approached the old man in silence and softly kissed him on his bloodless aged lips.", "그런데 별안간 그분이 말없이 노인에게 다가가, 그 핏기 없는 늙은 입술에 부드럽게 입을 맞춰."], ["That was all His answer.", "그것이 그분의 대답 전부였어."], ["The old man shuddered.", "노인은 몸을 떨어."], ["His lips moved.", "그의 입술이 움찔거려."], ["He went to the door, opened it, and said to Him: ‘Go, and come no more ...", "그는 문으로 가 문을 열고는 그분에게 말해. ‘가시오, 그리고 다시는 오지 마시오……"], ["come not at all, never, never!’ And he let Him out into the dark alleys of the town.", "다시는 절대 오지 마시오, 절대로, 절대로!’ 그러고는 그분을 도시의 어두운 골목길로 내보내."], ["The Prisoner went away.”", "죄수는 떠나가.”"]], [["“And the old man?”", "“그럼 노인은?”"]], [["“The kiss glows in his heart, but the old man adheres to his idea.”", "“그 입맞춤이 그의 가슴속에서 타오르지만, 노인은 자기 이념을 고수해.”"]], [["“And you with him, you too?”", "“그리고 형도 그와 함께, 형도 그러는 거야?”"], ["cried Alyosha, mournfully.", "알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다."]], [["Ivan laughed.", "이반이 웃었다."]], [["“Why, it’s all nonsense, Alyosha.", "“이런, 다 부질없는 소리야, 알료샤."], ["It’s only a senseless poem of a senseless student, who could never write two lines of verse.", "그건 그저 변변히 시 두 줄도 써 본 적 없는 한 어리석은 대학생이 지은 어리석은 시일 뿐이야."], ["Why do you take it so seriously?", "그걸 왜 그리 진지하게 받아들이지?"], ["Surely you don’t suppose I am going straight off to the Jesuits, to join the men who are correcting His work?", "설마 내가 곧장 예수회로 달려가, 그분의 일을 바로잡는 자들의 무리에 끼리라고 생각하는 건 아니겠지?"], ["Good Lord, it’s no business of mine.", "맙소사, 그건 내 알 바 아니야."], ["I told you, all I want is to live on to thirty, and then ...", "내가 말했잖아, 내가 바라는 거라곤 서른까지 살아남는 것뿐이고, 그다음엔……"], ["dash the cup to the ground!”", "잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐 버리는 거라고!”"]], [["“But the little sticky leaves, and the precious tombs, and the blue sky, and the woman you love!", "“하지만 그 끈적끈적한 어린잎들과, 그 소중한 무덤들과, 푸른 하늘과, 형이 사랑하는 여인은 어쩌고!"], ["How will you live, how will you love them?”", "형은 어떻게 살아갈 거야, 어떻게 그것들을 사랑할 거야?”"], ["Alyosha cried sorrowfully.", "알료샤가 서글프게 외쳤다."], ["“With such a hell in your heart and your head, how can you?", "“가슴속에도 머릿속에도 그런 지옥을 품고서 어떻게 그럴 수 있어?"], ["No, that’s just what you are going away for, to join them ...", "아니, 형이 떠나려는 건 바로 그것 때문이야, 그들 무리에 끼려고……"], ["if not, you will kill yourself, you can’t endure it!”", "그러지 않으면 형은 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거야, 견디지 못할 테니까!”"]], [["“There is a strength to endure everything,”", "“무엇이든 견뎌 낼 힘이 있어.”"], ["Ivan said with a cold smile.", "이반이 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“What strength?”", "“무슨 힘?”"]], [["“The strength of the Karamazovs—the strength of the Karamazov baseness.”", "“카라마조프의 힘—카라마조프적 비열함의 힘이지.”"]], [["“To sink into debauchery, to stifle your soul with corruption, yes?”", "“방탕에 빠져들고, 타락으로 영혼을 질식시키는 것, 그거야?”"]], [["“Possibly even that ...", "“어쩌면 그것마저도……"], ["only perhaps till I am thirty I shall escape it, and then—”", "다만 서른까지는 어쩌면 그걸 피할 수 있겠지, 그다음엔—”"]], [["“How will you escape it?", "“어떻게 그걸 피한다는 거야?"], ["By what will you escape it?", "무엇으로 피할 건데?"], ["That’s impossible with your ideas.”", "형의 그런 생각으로는 불가능해.”"]], [["“In the Karamazov way, again.”", "“다시 카라마조프식으로지.”"]], [["“‘Everything is lawful,’ you mean?", "“‘모든 것이 허용된다’, 그 말이야?"], ["Everything is lawful, is that it?”", "모든 것이 허용된다, 그거지?”"]], [["Ivan scowled, and all at once turned strangely pale.", "이반이 눈살을 찌푸리더니, 별안간 이상하리만치 창백해졌다."]], [["“Ah, you’ve caught up yesterday’s phrase, which so offended Miüsov—and which Dmitri pounced upon so naïvely, and paraphrased!”", "“아, 어제 미우소프를 그토록 발끈하게 만든 그 말을 너도 받아 옮겼구나—드미트리가 그토록 천진하게 덥석 물어 풀어 말한 그 말 말이야!”"], ["he smiled queerly.", "그가 묘하게 미소 지었다."], ["“Yes, if you like, ‘everything is lawful’ since the word has been said.", "“그래, 네가 원한다면, 이미 그 말이 나온 이상 ‘모든 것이 허용된다’고 해 두지."], ["I won’t deny it.", "부정하지 않겠어."], ["And Mitya’s version isn’t bad.”", "게다가 미탸의 해석도 나쁘지 않고.”"]], [["Alyosha looked at him in silence.", "알료샤는 말없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I thought that going away from here I have you at least,”", "“나는 여기를 떠나도 적어도 너만은 있다고 생각했는데.”"], ["Ivan said suddenly, with unexpected feeling; “but now I see that there is no place for me even in your heart, my dear hermit.", "이반이 별안간 뜻밖의 감정을 실어 말했다. “하지만 이제 보니, 내 사랑하는 은수자야, 네 가슴속에조차 나를 위한 자리는 없구나."], ["The formula, ‘all is lawful,’ I won’t renounce—will you renounce me for that, yes?”", "‘모든 것이 허용된다’는 그 명제를 나는 포기하지 않겠어—그렇다고 너는 그 때문에 나를 저버릴 거니, 응?”"]], [["Alyosha got up, went to him and softly kissed him on the lips.", "알료샤는 일어나 그에게 다가가서는 부드럽게 그의 입술에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“That’s plagiarism,”", "“그건 표절이야.”"], ["cried Ivan, highly delighted.", "이반이 몹시 기뻐하며 외쳤다."], ["“You stole that from my poem.", "“내 시에서 훔쳤구나."], ["Thank you though.", "그래도 고맙다."], ["Get up, Alyosha, it’s time we were going, both of us.”", "일어나, 알료샤, 우리 둘 다 이제 갈 시간이야.”"]], [["They went out, but stopped when they reached the entrance of the restaurant.", "그들은 밖으로 나왔으나, 음식점 입구에 이르렀을 때 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Listen, Alyosha,”", "“들어 봐, 알료샤.”"], ["Ivan began in a resolute voice, “if I am really able to care for the sticky little leaves I shall only love them, remembering you.", "이반이 단호한 목소리로 입을 열었다. “만일 내가 정말로 그 끈적끈적한 어린잎들을 아낄 수 있다면, 그건 오직 너를 떠올리며 그것들을 사랑하기 때문일 거야."], ["It’s enough for me that you are somewhere here, and I shan’t lose my desire for life yet.", "네가 여기 어딘가에 있다는 것만으로 나에겐 충분하고, 그러면 아직은 삶에 대한 욕망을 잃지 않을 거야."], ["Is that enough for you?", "이만하면 너에게도 충분하지?"], ["Take it as a declaration of love if you like.", "원한다면 사랑의 고백으로 받아들여도 좋아."], ["And now you go to the right and I to the left.", "자, 너는 오른쪽으로 가고 나는 왼쪽으로 갈게."], ["And it’s enough, do you hear, enough.", "그리고 이걸로 충분해, 알겠지, 충분하다고."], ["I mean even if I don’t go away to-morrow (I think I certainly shall go) and we meet again, don’t say a word more on these subjects.", "그러니까 설령 내일 내가 떠나지 않더라도(아무래도 떠날 것 같긴 하지만), 또 우리가 다시 만나더라도, 이런 문제들에 대해선 더는 한마디도 하지 마."], ["I beg that particularly.", "그것만은 특별히 부탁할게."], ["And about Dmitri too, I ask you specially, never speak to me again,”", "그리고 드미트리에 관해서도, 이건 각별히 부탁하는데, 다시는 나에게 말하지 마.”"], ["he added, with sudden irritation; “it’s all exhausted, it has all been said over and over again, hasn’t it?", "그가 별안간 짜증을 내며 덧붙였다. “그건 다 동났어, 죄다 몇 번이고 되풀이해 말했잖아, 안 그래?"], ["And I’ll make you one promise in return for it.", "그 대신 내가 너에게 약속 하나를 하지."], ["When at thirty, I want to ‘dash the cup to the ground,’ wherever I may be I’ll come to have one more talk with you, even though it were from America, you may be sure of that.", "서른이 되어 ‘잔을 땅바닥에 내동댕이치고’ 싶어지면, 내가 어디에 있든 너와 한 번 더 이야기하러 올게, 설령 아메리카에 있더라도 말이야, 그건 믿어도 좋아."], ["I’ll come on purpose.", "일부러 찾아오겠어."], ["It will be very interesting to have a look at you, to see what you’ll be by that time.", "그때 너를 한번 보는 것, 네가 그 무렵 어떤 사람이 되어 있을지 보는 것도 무척 흥미로울 거야."], ["It’s rather a solemn promise, you see.", "보다시피 제법 엄숙한 약속이지."], ["And we really may be parting for seven years or ten.", "그리고 우리는 정말 칠 년이나 십 년쯤 헤어지게 될지도 몰라."], ["Come, go now to your Pater Seraphicus, he is dying.", "자, 이제 네 파테르 세라피쿠스에게 가 봐, 그분은 임종을 앞두고 계셔."], ["If he dies without you, you will be angry with me for having kept you.", "네가 없는 사이에 돌아가시기라도 하면, 너는 내가 너를 붙들었다고 나를 원망할 테니까. 잘 가, 한 번 더 입맞춰 줘."], ["Good-by, kiss me once more; that’s right, now go.”", "옳지, 그래, 이제 가.\" * 파테르 세라피쿠스(Pater Seraphicus): 라틴어로 ‘천사 같은 아버지’라는 뜻으로, 본디 아시시의 성 프란체스코에게 붙던 칭호."]], [["Ivan turned suddenly and went his way without looking back.", "이반은 별안간 몸을 돌려, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["It was just as Dmitri had left Alyosha the day before, though the parting had been very different.", "그것은 바로 전날 드미트리가 알료샤를 두고 떠났던 모습 그대로였다, 비록 그 작별은 사뭇 달랐지만."], ["The strange resemblance flashed like an arrow through Alyosha’s mind in the distress and dejection of that moment.", "그 야릇한 닮음이 그 괴로움과 낙담의 순간에 화살처럼 알료샤의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["He waited a little, looking after his brother.", "그는 형의 뒷모습을 바라보며 잠시 서 있었다."], ["He suddenly noticed that Ivan swayed as he walked and that his right shoulder looked lower than his left.", "그는 문득 이반이 걸으면서 몸을 휘청거리는 것을, 그리고 오른쪽 어깨가 왼쪽보다 처져 보이는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He had never noticed it before.", "전에는 한 번도 눈여겨본 적이 없는 것이었다."], ["But all at once he turned too, and almost ran to the monastery.", "그러나 별안간 그 역시 몸을 돌려, 거의 뛰다시피 수도원으로 향했다."], ["It was nearly dark, and he felt almost frightened; something new was growing up in him for which he could not account.", "날은 거의 어두워져 있었고, 그는 어쩐지 두려운 느낌마저 들었다. 무언가 새로운 것이 그의 안에서 자라나고 있었는데, 그것이 무엇인지 그는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["The wind had risen again as on the previous evening, and the ancient pines murmured gloomily about him when he entered the hermitage copse.", "전날 저녁처럼 다시 바람이 일었고, 그가 암자 숲으로 들어서자 늙은 소나무들이 그의 주위에서 음울하게 술렁거렸다. 그는 거의 뛰다시피 했다."], ["He almost ran. “Pater Seraphicus—he got that name from somewhere—where from?”", "“파테르 세라피쿠스라—형은 어디선가 그 이름을 따왔는데, 대체 어디서일까?”"], ["Alyosha wondered.", "알료샤는 의아해했다."], ["“Ivan, poor Ivan, and when shall I see you again?...", "“이반, 가엾은 이반, 그러면 언제 다시 형을 보게 될까?……"], ["Here is the hermitage.", "여기가 암자로구나."], ["Yes, yes, that he is, Pater Seraphicus, he will save me—from him and for ever!”", "그래, 그래, 바로 그분이셔, 파테르 세라피쿠스, 그분이 나를 구해 주실 거야—형에게서, 그리고 영원히!”"]], [["Several times afterwards he wondered how he could on leaving Ivan so completely forget his brother Dmitri, though he had that morning, only a few hours before, so firmly resolved to find him and not to give up doing so, even should he be unable to return to the monastery that night.", "그 뒤로 여러 번 그는, 이반과 헤어지면서 어떻게 형 드미트리를 그토록 까맣게 잊어버릴 수 있었는지 의아해하곤 했다. 바로 그날 아침, 불과 몇 시간 전만 해도 그는 형을 찾아내겠다고, 설령 그날 밤 수도원으로 돌아오지 못하는 한이 있더라도 결코 그 일을 포기하지 않겠다고 그토록 굳게 다짐했던 터였다."]], [["# Chapter VI. For Awhile A Very Obscure One", "# Chapter VI. For Awhile A Very Obscure One"]], [["And Ivan, on parting from Alyosha, went home to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house.", "이반은 알료샤와 헤어진 뒤 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["But, strange to say, he was overcome by insufferable depression, which grew greater at every step he took towards the house.", "그러나 이상하게도 그는 견디기 힘든 우울에 사로잡혔는데, 그 우울은 집으로 향하는 걸음마다 점점 더 깊어졌다."], ["There was nothing strange in his being depressed; what was strange was that Ivan could not have said what was the cause of it.", "그가 우울해한다는 것 자체에는 이상할 것이 없었으나, 정작 이상한 것은 그 우울의 원인이 무엇인지 이반 자신도 말할 수 없었다는 점이다."], ["He had often been depressed before, and there was nothing surprising at his feeling so at such a moment, when he had broken off with everything that had brought him here, and was preparing that day to make a new start and enter upon a new, unknown future.", "그는 전에도 자주 우울에 잠긴 적이 있었고, 자기를 이곳으로 끌어들인 모든 것과 인연을 끊고서 바로 그날 새 출발을 하여 미지의 새로운 미래로 들어서려는 그런 순간에 우울을 느낀다는 것에는 놀랄 일이 없었다."], ["He would again be as solitary as ever, and though he had great hopes, and great—too great—expectations from life, he could not have given any definite account of his hopes, his expectations, or even his desires.", "그는 또다시 여느 때처럼 외톨이가 될 터였으나, 비록 인생에 큰 희망과 크나큰—지나치게 큰—기대를 품고 있었음에도, 그 희망이며 기대며 심지어 자신의 소망에 대해서조차 그는 무엇 하나 또렷하게 설명할 수 없었다."]], [["Yet at that moment, though the apprehension of the new and unknown certainly found place in his heart, what was worrying him was something quite different.", "그러나 그 순간 새롭고 미지의 것에 대한 불안이 그의 가슴 한구석에 분명히 자리 잡고 있었음에도, 그를 괴롭히는 것은 전혀 다른 무엇이었다."], ["“Is it loathing for my father’s house?”", "\"아버지 집에 대한 혐오인가?\" 그는 의아해했다."], ["he wondered.", "\"그럴 법도 하지."], ["“Quite likely; I am so sick of it; and though it’s the last time I shall cross its hateful threshold, still I loathe it....", "그 집이라면 이제 지긋지긋하니까. 비록 그 가증스러운 문턱을 넘는 것도 오늘이 마지막이긴 하지만, 그래도 나는 그 집이 역겨워……"], ["No, it’s not that either.", "아니, 그것도 아니야."], ["Is it the parting with Alyosha and the conversation I had with him?", "알료샤와 헤어진 일, 그와 나눈 대화 때문인가?"], ["For so many years I’ve been silent with the whole world and not deigned to speak, and all of a sudden I reel off a rigmarole like that.”", "그토록 오랜 세월 온 세상을 향해 입을 다물고 한마디도 할 가치를 못 느꼈던 내가, 느닷없이 그런 장광설을 줄줄이 늘어놓다니.\""], ["It certainly might have been the youthful vexation of youthful inexperience and vanity—vexation at having failed to express himself, especially with such a being as Alyosha, on whom his heart had certainly been reckoning.", "그것은 분명 젊은 미숙함과 허영심에서 비롯된 젊은이다운 분함—자기 마음을 제대로 표현하지 못한 데 대한 분함이었을 수도 있는데, 더구나 그가 진심으로 믿고 마음을 기대어 온 알료샤 같은 존재 앞에서였으니 더욱 그러했다."], ["No doubt that came in, that vexation, it must have done indeed; but yet that was not it, that was not it either.", "그런 분함도 끼어 있었음에 틀림없고, 그래야 마땅했다. 그러나 그것도 아니었다, 그것도 아니었다."], ["“I feel sick with depression and yet I can’t tell what I want.", "\"우울로 속이 메스껍건만, 정작 내가 무엇을 바라는지 알 수가 없군."], ["Better not think, perhaps.”", "차라리 아무 생각도 하지 않는 편이 나으려나.\""]], [["Ivan tried “not to think,”", "이반은 \"아무 생각도 하지 않으려\""], ["but that, too, was no use.", "애써 보았으나, 그것 또한 소용이 없었다."], ["What made his depression so vexatious and irritating was that it had a kind of casual, external character—he felt that.", "그의 우울이 그토록 짜증스럽고 거슬렸던 것은, 그것이 어딘가 우발적이고 외부에서 비롯된 성격을 띠고 있었기 때문이다—그는 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["Some person or thing seemed to be standing out somewhere, just as something will sometimes obtrude itself upon the eye, and though one may be so busy with work or conversation that for a long time one does not notice it, yet it irritates and almost torments one till at last one realizes, and removes the offending object, often quite a trifling and ridiculous one—some article left about in the wrong place, a handkerchief on the floor, a book not replaced on the shelf, and so on.", "어떤 사람인지 사물인지가 어딘가에 불쑥 튀어나와 있는 듯했는데, 마치 무언가가 이따금 눈앞에 거슬리게 끼어들 때처럼, 일이나 대화에 골몰한 나머지 한참 동안 그것을 알아차리지 못하면서도 끝내 짜증이 나고 거의 괴로워지다가, 마침내 그 거슬리는 물건—흔히 아주 사소하고 우스꽝스러운 것, 즉 엉뚱한 자리에 놓인 어떤 물건이라든가 바닥에 떨어진 손수건이라든가 선반에 도로 꽂아 두지 않은 책 따위—을 깨닫고 치워 버리게 되는 것과 같았다."]], [["At last, feeling very cross and ill-humored, Ivan arrived home, and suddenly, about fifteen paces from the garden gate, he guessed what was fretting and worrying him.", "마침내 잔뜩 신경이 곤두서고 심기가 사나워진 채 이반은 집에 다다랐는데, 정원 문에서 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 문득 자신을 안달하게 하고 괴롭히던 것이 무엇인지를 깨달았다."]], [["On a bench in the gateway the valet Smerdyakov was sitting enjoying the coolness of the evening, and at the first glance at him Ivan knew that the valet Smerdyakov was on his mind, and that it was this man that his soul loathed.", "대문 옆 벤치에는 하인 스메르댜코프가 앉아 저녁의 서늘함을 즐기고 있었는데, 그를 처음 본 순간 이반은 자기 마음에 걸린 것이 바로 그 하인 스메르댜코프이며, 자기 영혼이 역겨워하는 것이 바로 이 사내임을 알아차렸다."], ["It all dawned upon him suddenly and became clear.", "그 모든 것이 별안간 머릿속에 떠오르며 분명해졌다."], ["Just before, when Alyosha had been telling him of his meeting with Smerdyakov, he had felt a sudden twinge of gloom and loathing, which had immediately stirred responsive anger in his heart.", "바로 조금 전, 알료샤가 스메르댜코프와 마주친 이야기를 들려주었을 때 그는 문득 음울함과 혐오가 찌릿하게 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 그 즉시 가슴속에 분노가 맞받아 일었던 것이다."], ["Afterwards, as he talked, Smerdyakov had been forgotten for the time; but still he had been in his mind, and as soon as Ivan parted with Alyosha and was walking home, the forgotten sensation began to obtrude itself again.", "그 뒤 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 스메르댜코프를 잠시 잊고 있었으나, 그래도 그는 줄곧 마음 한구석에 남아 있었고, 이반이 알료샤와 헤어져 집으로 걸어오자마자 그 잊고 있던 감각이 다시금 비집고 나오기 시작했다."], ["“Is it possible that a miserable, contemptible creature like that can worry me so much?”", "\"저런 비참하고 하찮은 작자가 나를 이토록 괴롭히다니, 이게 말이 되는가?\""], ["he wondered, with insufferable irritation.", "그는 견딜 수 없는 짜증과 함께 의아해했다."]], [["It was true that Ivan had come of late to feel an intense dislike for the man, especially during the last few days.", "사실 이반은 요사이, 특히 지난 며칠 사이에 그 사내에게 격심한 혐오감을 느끼게 되었던 것이다."], ["He had even begun to notice in himself a growing feeling that was almost of hatred for the creature.", "그는 심지어 그 작자를 향해 거의 증오에 가까운 감정이 자기 안에서 자라나고 있음을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["Perhaps this hatred was accentuated by the fact that when Ivan first came to the neighborhood he had felt quite differently.", "어쩌면 이 증오는 이반이 처음 이 고장에 왔을 때 전혀 다른 감정을 느꼈다는 사실 때문에 더욱 도드라졌는지도 모른다."], ["Then he had taken a marked interest in Smerdyakov, and had even thought him very original.", "그때만 해도 그는 스메르댜코프에게 뚜렷한 흥미를 보였고, 심지어 그를 아주 독창적인 인간이라고까지 여겼다."], ["He had encouraged him to talk to him, although he had always wondered at a certain incoherence, or rather restlessness, in his mind, and could not understand what it was that so continually and insistently worked upon the brain of “the contemplative.”", "그는 스메르댜코프에게 말을 걸어 보라고 부추기곤 했는데, 그러면서도 그의 정신에 어딘가 두서없음, 아니 그보다는 안절부절못함 같은 것이 있다는 점에 늘 의아해했고, 이 \"사색가\"의 머릿속을 그토록 끊임없이 집요하게 짓누르는 것이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수가 없었다."], ["They discussed philosophical questions and even how there could have been light on the first day when the sun, moon, and stars were only created on the fourth day, and how that was to be understood.", "두 사람은 철학적인 문제들을, 심지어 해와 달과 별이 나흘째 날에야 비로소 창조되었는데 어떻게 첫째 날에 빛이 있을 수 있었는지, 그것을 어떻게 이해해야 하는지 따위를 토론했다."], ["But Ivan soon saw that, though the sun, moon, and stars might be an interesting subject, yet that it was quite secondary to Smerdyakov, and that he was looking for something altogether different.", "그러나 이반은 곧, 해와 달과 별이 흥미로운 주제일 수는 있어도 스메르댜코프에게는 그것이 한참 부차적인 것이며, 그가 노리는 것은 전혀 다른 무엇임을 알아차렸다."], ["In one way and another, he began to betray a boundless vanity, and a wounded vanity, too, and that Ivan disliked.", "이런저런 방식으로 그는 끝 모를 허영심, 그것도 상처 입은 허영심을 드러내기 시작했고, 이반은 그것이 못마땅했다."], ["It had first given rise to his aversion.", "그것이 바로 그의 반감을 처음으로 불러일으킨 것이었다."], ["Later on, there had been trouble in the house.", "그 뒤 집 안에 풍파가 일었다."], ["Grushenka had come on the scene, and there had been the scandals with his brother Dmitri—they discussed that, too.", "그루셴카가 등장했고 그의 형 드미트리와 얽힌 추문들이 벌어졌으니—그들은 그 일도 토론했다."], ["But though Smerdyakov always talked of that with great excitement, it was impossible to discover what he desired to come of it.", "그러나 스메르댜코프는 늘 그 이야기를 굉장한 흥분에 차서 했음에도, 그가 그 일이 어떻게 결말나기를 바라는지는 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었다."], ["There was, in fact, something surprising in the illogicality and incoherence of some of his desires, accidentally betrayed and always vaguely expressed.", "사실 무심코 드러나면서 늘 모호하게 표현되는 그의 어떤 욕망들에는 비논리성과 두서없음이 깃들어 있어 놀라울 정도였다."], ["Smerdyakov was always inquiring, putting certain indirect but obviously premeditated questions, but what his object was he did not explain, and usually at the most important moment he would break off and relapse into silence or pass to another subject.", "스메르댜코프는 늘 캐물었고, 에둘러 가면서도 명백히 미리 궁리해 둔 어떤 질문들을 던졌으나, 그 목적이 무엇인지는 설명하지 않았고, 대개 가장 중요한 대목에 이르면 말을 뚝 끊고 침묵에 잠기거나 다른 화제로 넘어가 버리곤 했다."], ["But what finally irritated Ivan most and confirmed his dislike for him was the peculiar, revolting familiarity which Smerdyakov began to show more and more markedly.", "그러나 끝내 이반을 가장 짜증스럽게 하고 그에 대한 혐오를 굳히게 만든 것은, 스메르댜코프가 점점 더 노골적으로 드러내기 시작한 그 묘하고도 역겨운 허물없음이었다."], ["Not that he forgot himself and was rude; on the contrary, he always spoke very respectfully, yet he had obviously begun to consider—goodness knows why!—that there was some sort of understanding between him and Ivan Fyodorovitch.", "그가 분수를 잊고 무례하게 굴었다는 것은 아니다. 오히려 그는 언제나 아주 공손하게 말했지만, 어찌 된 영문인지—신만이 알 일이지만!—자기와 이반 표도로비치 사이에 무슨 묵계 같은 것이 있다고 여기기 시작한 것이 분명했다."], ["He always spoke in a tone that suggested that those two had some kind of compact, some secret between them, that had at some time been expressed on both sides, only known to them and beyond the comprehension of those around them.", "그는 늘, 두 사람 사이에 어떤 약조가, 어떤 비밀이 있으며 그것이 언젠가 양쪽에서 표명된 바 있어 오직 둘만이 알고 주변 사람들은 도저히 헤아릴 수 없는 것이라는 듯한 어조로 말했다."], ["But for a long while Ivan did not recognize the real cause of his growing dislike and he had only lately realized what was at the root of it.", "그러나 이반은 오랫동안 자라나는 혐오의 진짜 원인을 알아차리지 못했고, 그 뿌리에 무엇이 있는지는 최근에야 비로소 깨달은 터였다."]], [["With a feeling of disgust and irritation he tried to pass in at the gate without speaking or looking at Smerdyakov.", "그는 역겨움과 짜증을 느끼며 스메르댜코프에게 말도 걸지 않고 쳐다보지도 않은 채 문 안으로 들어가려 했다."], ["But Smerdyakov rose from the bench, and from that action alone, Ivan knew instantly that he wanted particularly to talk to him.", "그러나 스메르댜코프가 벤치에서 일어섰고, 이반은 그 동작만으로도 그가 유독 자기에게 할 말이 있다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["Ivan looked at him and stopped, and the fact that he did stop, instead of passing by, as he meant to the minute before, drove him to fury.", "이반은 그를 쳐다보며 걸음을 멈추었는데, 조금 전까지만 해도 그냥 지나치려 했던 자신이 멈춰 섰다는 사실이 그를 격분케 했다."], ["With anger and repulsion he looked at Smerdyakov’s emasculate, sickly face, with the little curls combed forward on his forehead.", "그는 분노와 혐오에 차서, 이마 위로 빗어 내린 자잘한 곱슬머리에 거세된 듯 병약한 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["His left eye winked and he grinned as if to say, “Where are you going?", "사내는 왼쪽 눈을 찡긋하더니, 마치 이렇게 말하기라도 하듯 히죽 웃었다. \"어디 가십니까?"], ["You won’t pass by; you see that we two clever people have something to say to each other.”", "그냥은 못 지나가실 텐데요. 우리처럼 영리한 두 사람끼린 서로 나눌 이야기가 있다는 걸 잘 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["Ivan shook. “Get away, miserable idiot.", "이반은 부르르 떨었다."], ["What have I to do with you?”", "\"꺼져, 이 한심한 멍청아."], ["was on the tip of his tongue, but to his profound astonishment he heard himself say, “Is my father still asleep, or has he waked?”", "내가 너 따위와 무슨 상관이냐\"라는 말이 혀끝까지 차올랐으나, 그는 자기 입에서 \"아버지는 아직 주무시느냐, 아니면 깨셨느냐?\" 하는 말이 흘러나오는 것을 듣고 소스라치게 놀랐다."]], [["He asked the question softly and meekly, to his own surprise, and at once, again to his own surprise, sat down on the bench.", "그는 자기도 놀랄 만큼 나직하고 온순하게 그 질문을 던졌고, 곧이어 역시 자기도 놀랍게도 벤치에 가서 앉았다."], ["For an instant he felt almost frightened; he remembered it afterwards.", "한순간 그는 거의 두려움마저 느꼈는데, 그것을 그는 훗날까지 기억했다."], ["Smerdyakov stood facing him, his hands behind his back, looking at him with assurance and almost severity.", "스메르댜코프는 두 손을 뒷짐 진 채 그를 마주 보고 서서, 자신만만하고 거의 준엄하다 싶은 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“His honor is still asleep,”", "\"나리께선 아직 주무십니다.\""], ["he articulated deliberately (“You were the first to speak, not I,” he seemed to say).", "그는 한 마디 한 마디 또박또박 말했다(\"먼저 말을 건 건 제가 아니라 나리이십니다.\""], ["“I am surprised at you, sir,”", "그는 이렇게 말하는 듯했다)."], ["he added, after a pause, dropping his eyes affectedly, setting his right foot forward, and playing with the tip of his polished boot.", "\"전 나리께서 의아하기 짝이 없습니다.\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 짐짓 눈을 내리깔고 오른발을 앞으로 내밀어 반들반들 닦은 장화 코를 까닥거리며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Why are you surprised at me?”", "\"내가 왜 의아하다는 게냐?\""], ["Ivan asked abruptly and sullenly, doing his utmost to restrain himself, and suddenly realizing, with disgust, that he was feeling intense curiosity and would not, on any account, have gone away without satisfying it.", "이반은 안간힘을 다해 자신을 억누르며 퉁명스럽고 시무룩하게 물었는데, 문득 역겨움과 함께 자신이 격렬한 호기심을 느끼고 있으며 무슨 일이 있어도 그것을 채우지 않고는 자리를 뜨지 못하리라는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["“Why don’t you go to Tchermashnya, sir?”", "\"나리께선 왜 체르마쉬냐로 가지 않으십니까?\""], ["Smerdyakov suddenly raised his eyes and smiled familiarly.", "스메르댜코프가 별안간 눈을 들며 허물없이 웃어 보였다."], ["“Why I smile you must understand of yourself, if you are a clever man,”", "\"제가 왜 웃는지는, 영리하신 분이라면 나리께서 스스로 아셔야지요.\""], ["his screwed-up left eye seemed to say.", "가늘게 뜬 그의 왼쪽 눈이 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“Why should I go to Tchermashnya?”", "\"내가 왜 체르마쉬냐로 가야 하느냐?\""], ["Ivan asked in surprise.", "이반이 놀라며 물었다."]], [["Smerdyakov was silent again.", "스메르댜코프는 다시 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“Fyodor Pavlovitch himself has so begged you to,”", "\"표도르 파블로비치께서 친히 그토록 가시라고 청하지 않으셨습니까.\""], ["he said at last, slowly and apparently attaching no significance to his answer.", "그는 마침내 천천히, 그러면서도 제 대답에 별 의미를 두지 않는 듯이 말했다."], ["“I put you off with a secondary reason,”", "\"그저 무슨 말이라도 하려고 부차적인 핑계를 들어 둘러댄 것뿐입니다.\""], ["he seemed to suggest, “simply to say something.”", "그는 이렇게 넌지시 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“Damn you!", "\"빌어먹을!"], ["Speak out what you want!”", "원하는 게 뭔지 똑바로 말해!\""], ["Ivan cried angrily at last, passing from meekness to violence.", "이반은 마침내 온순함을 떨치고 사납게 소리쳤다."]], [["Smerdyakov drew his right foot up to his left, pulled himself up, but still looked at him with the same serenity and the same little smile.", "스메르댜코프는 오른발을 왼발 쪽으로 끌어당기고 몸을 곧추세웠으나, 여전히 똑같은 태연함과 똑같은 옅은 미소를 띤 채 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Substantially nothing—but just by way of conversation.”", "\"별다른 건 없습니다—그저 이야깃거리 삼아 해 본 말씀이지요.\""]], [["Another silence followed.", "또다시 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["They did not speak for nearly a minute.", "두 사람은 거의 일 분가량 입을 열지 않았다."], ["Ivan knew that he ought to get up and show anger, and Smerdyakov stood before him and seemed to be waiting as though to see whether he would be angry or not.", "이반은 자신이 일어나 화를 내야 마땅하다는 것을 알았고, 스메르댜코프는 그의 앞에 서서 그가 화를 낼지 말지를 지켜보기라도 하듯 기다리는 것 같았다."], ["So at least it seemed to Ivan.", "적어도 이반에게는 그렇게 보였다."], ["At last he moved to get up.", "마침내 그가 일어서려고 몸을 움직였다."], ["Smerdyakov seemed to seize the moment.", "스메르댜코프는 그 틈을 놓치지 않으려는 듯했다."]], [["“I’m in an awful position, Ivan Fyodorovitch.", "\"전 끔찍한 처지에 놓여 있습니다, 이반 표도로비치."], ["I don’t know how to help myself,”", "도무지 어찌해야 할지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["he said resolutely and distinctly, and at his last word he sighed.", "그는 단호하고 또렷하게 말했고, 마지막 말에 이르러 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch sat down again.", "이반 표도로비치는 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“They are both utterly crazy, they are no better than little children,”", "\"두 분 다 완전히 제정신이 아니십니다, 어린애나 다름없으십니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov went on.", "스메르댜코프가 말을 이었다."], ["“I am speaking of your parent and your brother Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"나리이신 아버님과 형님이신 드미트리 표도로비치 말씀입니다."], ["Here Fyodor Pavlovitch will get up directly and begin worrying me every minute, ‘Has she come?", "곧 표도르 파블로비치께서 일어나셔서는 시도 때도 없이 절 들볶으실 겁니다. '그 여자가 왔느냐?"], ["Why hasn’t she come?’ and so on up till midnight and even after midnight.", "왜 안 오는 게냐?' 하면서 자정까지, 아니 자정이 넘도록 그러시겠지요."], ["And if Agrafena Alexandrovna doesn’t come (for very likely she does not mean to come at all) then he will be at me again to-morrow morning, ‘Why hasn’t she come?", "그리고 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오지 않으면(아마 아예 올 생각이 없으실 테지만) 내일 아침에 또 절 붙들고 '왜 안 왔느냐? 언제 오는 게냐?'"], ["When will she come?’—as though I were to blame for it.", "하실 텐데—마치 그게 제 탓이라도 되는 양 말입니다."], ["On the other side it’s no better.", "다른 쪽도 나을 게 없습니다."], ["As soon as it gets dark, or even before, your brother will appear with his gun in his hands: ‘Look out, you rogue, you soup-maker.", "날이 어두워지자마자, 아니 그 전부터 형님께서 총을 손에 들고 나타나실 겁니다. '잘 봐 둬라, 이 악당아, 요리나 끓이는 놈아."], ["If you miss her and don’t let me know she’s been—I’ll kill you before any one.’ When the night’s over, in the morning, he, too, like Fyodor Pavlovitch, begins worrying me to death.", "그 여자를 놓치고 다녀간 걸 내게 알리지 않으면—누구보다 먼저 너부터 죽여 버리겠다.' 밤이 지나고 아침이 오면 그분도 표도르 파블로비치처럼 절 죽도록 들볶기 시작하시지요."], ["‘Why hasn’t she come?", "'왜 안 왔느냐?"], ["Will she come soon?’ And he, too, thinks me to blame because his lady hasn’t come.", "곧 오겠느냐?' 그분도 자기 여자가 오지 않는 걸 제 탓으로 여기십니다."], ["And every day and every hour they get angrier and angrier, so that I sometimes think I shall kill myself in a fright.", "그렇게 날이 갈수록 시간이 갈수록 두 분 다 점점 더 화를 내시니, 전 가끔 겁에 질린 나머지 차라리 죽어 버리는 게 낫겠다 싶을 정도입니다."], ["I can’t depend upon them, sir.”", "전 그분들을 믿을 수가 없습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“And why have you meddled?", "\"한데 넌 왜 끼어든 게냐?"], ["Why did you begin to spy for Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”", "왜 드미트리 표도로비치를 위해 염탐 노릇을 시작했어?\""], ["said Ivan irritably.", "이반이 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“How could I help meddling?", "\"제가 어떻게 안 끼어들 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["Though, indeed, I haven’t meddled at all, if you want to know the truth of the matter.", "하긴, 정말 사실대로 말씀드리자면, 전 끼어든 적이 전혀 없습니다."], ["I kept quiet from the very beginning, not daring to answer; but he pitched on me to be his servant.", "처음부터 감히 대꾸도 못 하고 잠자코만 있었는데, 그분이 절 자기 종으로 점찍어 버리신 거지요. 그 뒤로 그분이 하시는 말씀이라곤 한 가지뿐이었습니다."], ["He has had only one thing to say since: ‘I’ll kill you, you scoundrel, if you miss her,’ I feel certain, sir, that I shall have a long fit to-morrow.”", "'그 여자를 놓치면, 이 악당아, 널 죽여 버리겠다.' 나리, 전 내일 긴 발작을 일으킬 게 분명합니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘a long fit’?”", "\"'긴 발작'이라니, 그게 무슨 소리냐?\""]], [["“A long fit, lasting a long time—several hours, or perhaps a day or two.", "\"긴 발작이란 오래 지속되는 발작입니다—몇 시간, 아니 하루이틀씩 가기도 하지요."], ["Once it went on for three days.", "언젠가는 사흘이나 계속된 적도 있습니다."], ["I fell from the garret that time.", "그때 전 다락에서 떨어졌지요."], ["The struggling ceased and then began again, and for three days I couldn’t come back to my senses.", "경련이 멎었다가 다시 시작되었고, 사흘 동안 전 정신을 차리지 못했습니다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch sent for Herzenstube, the doctor here, and he put ice on my head and tried another remedy, too....", "표도르 파블로비치께서 여기 의사 헤르첸슈투베를 부르셨고, 그분이 제 머리에 얼음을 얹고 또 다른 약도 써 보셨지요……"], ["I might have died.”", "하마터면 죽을 뻔했습니다.\""]], [["“But they say one can’t tell with epilepsy when a fit is coming.", "\"하지만 간질이란 발작이 언제 올지 알 수 없다고들 하던데."], ["What makes you say you will have one to-morrow?”", "어째서 내일 발작을 일으킨다고 말하는 게냐?\""], ["Ivan inquired, with a peculiar, irritable curiosity.", "이반은 묘하고 짜증스러운 호기심에 차서 물었다."]], [["“That’s just so.", "\"바로 그렇습니다."], ["You can’t tell beforehand.”", "미리 알 수는 없지요.\""]], [["“Besides, you fell from the garret then.”", "\"게다가 그때 넌 다락에서 떨어진 거잖느냐.\""]], [["“I climb up to the garret every day.", "\"전 매일 다락에 올라갑니다."], ["I might fall from the garret again to-morrow.", "내일도 다락에서 떨어질 수 있지요."], ["And, if not, I might fall down the cellar steps.", "그게 아니라도 지하실 계단에서 굴러떨어질 수도 있고요."], ["I have to go into the cellar every day, too.”", "전 지하실에도 매일 들어가야 하니까요.\""]], [["Ivan took a long look at him.", "이반은 그를 한참 바라보았다."]], [["“You are talking nonsense, I see, and I don’t quite understand you,”", "\"내가 보기에 넌 지금 허튼소리를 하고 있고, 난 네 말을 도무지 못 알아듣겠다.\""], ["he said softly, but with a sort of menace.", "그는 나직하게, 그러나 어딘가 위협조로 말했다."], ["“Do you mean to pretend to be ill to-morrow for three days, eh?”", "\"그러니까 넌 내일 사흘 동안 앓는 척하겠다는 게냐, 응?\""]], [["Smerdyakov, who was looking at the ground again, and playing with the toe of his right foot, set the foot down, moved the left one forward, and, grinning, articulated:", "다시 땅바닥을 내려다보며 오른발 발끝을 까닥거리던 스메르댜코프는 그 발을 내려놓고 왼발을 앞으로 옮긴 뒤, 히죽 웃으며 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“If I were able to play such a trick, that is, pretend to have a fit—and it would not be difficult for a man accustomed to them—I should have a perfect right to use such a means to save myself from death.", "\"제가 만일 그런 술수를 부릴 수 있다면, 그러니까 발작을 일으킨 척할 수 있다면—발작에 이골이 난 사람에게야 어려운 일도 아니지요—제 목숨을 죽음에서 건지기 위해 그런 수단을 쓸 권리쯤은 충분히 있을 겁니다."], ["For even if Agrafena Alexandrovna comes to see his father while I am ill, his honor can’t blame a sick man for not telling him.", "제가 앓아누워 있는 동안 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 아버님을 찾아오신다 해도, 나리께서 병든 사람더러 어째서 알리지 않았느냐고 탓하실 수는 없을 테니까요."], ["He’d be ashamed to.”", "그러기엔 면구스러우실 테지요.\""]], [["“Hang it all!”", "\"빌어먹을 노릇이군!\""], ["Ivan cried, his face working with anger, “why are you always in such a funk for your life?", "이반은 분노로 얼굴을 일그러뜨리며 외쳤다. \"넌 어째서 늘 그렇게 제 목숨에 벌벌 떠는 게냐?"], ["All my brother Dmitri’s threats are only hasty words and mean nothing.", "드미트리 형님의 협박이라야 다 성마른 말일 뿐 아무 의미도 없어."], ["He won’t kill you; it’s not you he’ll kill!”", "그자는 널 죽이지 않아. 그자가 죽일 상대는 네가 아니야!\""]], [["“He’d kill me first of all, like a fly.", "\"무엇보다 먼저 절 파리 잡듯 죽이실 겁니다."], ["But even more than that, I am afraid I shall be taken for an accomplice of his when he does something crazy to his father.”", "하지만 그보다 더 두려운 건, 그분이 아버님께 무슨 미친 짓을 저질렀을 때 제가 그 공범으로 몰리는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Why should you be taken for an accomplice?”", "\"네가 왜 공범으로 몰린다는 거냐?\""]], [["“They’ll think I am an accomplice, because I let him know the signals as a great secret.”", "\"제가 그분께 큰 비밀로 그 신호를 알려 드렸으니, 사람들은 절 공범이라 여길 겁니다.\""]], [["“What signals?", "\"무슨 신호 말이냐?"], ["Whom did you tell?", "누구한테 말했어?"], ["Confound you, speak more plainly.”", "빌어먹을, 더 분명히 말해 봐.\""]], [["“I’m bound to admit the fact,”", "\"솔직히 인정할 수밖에 없는 사실이 있습니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov drawled with pedantic composure, “that I have a secret with Fyodor Pavlovitch in this business.", "스메르댜코프는 꼼꼼하고 침착하게 느릿느릿 말했다. \"이 일에 관해 제가 표도르 파블로비치와 한 가지 비밀을 나누고 있다는 것 말입니다."], ["As you know yourself (if only you do know it) he has for several days past locked himself in as soon as night or even evening comes on.", "나리께서도 친히 아시다시피(혹시 아신다면 말입니다만), 그분은 며칠째 밤이, 아니 저녁만 되어도 곧장 문을 걸어 잠그십니다."], ["Of late you’ve been going upstairs to your room early every evening, and yesterday you did not come down at all, and so perhaps you don’t know how carefully he has begun to lock himself in at night, and even if Grigory Vassilyevitch comes to the door he won’t open to him till he hears his voice.", "요즈음 나리께선 저녁마다 일찍 위층 방으로 올라가셨고, 어제는 아예 내려오지도 않으셨으니, 그분이 밤마다 얼마나 조심스레 문을 걸어 잠그기 시작하셨는지 아마 모르실 겁니다. 그리고리 바실리예비치가 문 앞에 와도 그분은 목소리를 듣기 전에는 절대 열어 주지 않으십니다."], ["But Grigory Vassilyevitch does not come, because I wait upon him alone in his room now.", "한데 그리고리 바실리예비치는 오지 않습니다, 이제 그분 방의 시중은 저 혼자 들고 있으니까요."], ["That’s the arrangement he made himself ever since this to-do with Agrafena Alexandrovna began.", "아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 둘러싼 이 소동이 시작된 뒤로 그분이 친히 그렇게 정하셨습니다."], ["But at night, by his orders, I go away to the lodge so that I don’t get to sleep till midnight, but am on the watch, getting up and walking about the yard, waiting for Agrafena Alexandrovna to come.", "한데 밤이 되면 그분의 분부에 따라 저는 별채로 물러가는데, 그래서 자정까지 잠들지 못하고 망을 보며 일어나 마당을 거닐면서 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오기를 기다립니다."], ["For the last few days he’s been perfectly frantic expecting her.", "요 며칠 그분은 그 여자를 기다리며 완전히 넋이 나가 계십니다."], ["What he argues is, she is afraid of him, Dmitri Fyodorovitch (Mitya, as he calls him), ‘and so,’ says he, ‘she’ll come the back-way, late at night, to me.", "그분 말씀인즉, 그 여자가 드미트리 표도로비치(그분은 미탸라고 부르시지요)를 무서워하니, '그래서 그 여자가 밤늦게 뒷길로 나를 찾아올 게다. 너는'"], ["You look out for her,’ says he, ‘till midnight and later; and if she does come, you run up and knock at my door or at the window from the garden.", "하고 그분이 말씀하십니다, '자정까지, 아니 그 뒤까지도 그 여자를 살펴라. 만약 정말 오거든 달려 올라와 내 문을, 아니면 정원 쪽 창문을 두드려라."], ["Knock at first twice, rather gently, and then three times more quickly, then,’ says he, ‘I shall understand at once that she has come, and will open the door to you quietly.’ Another signal he gave me in case anything unexpected happens.", "처음엔 두 번, 좀 살그머니 두드리고, 그다음엔 세 번을 좀 더 빠르게 두드려라. 그러면'"], ["At first, two knocks, and then, after an interval, another much louder.", "하고 그분이 말씀하시길, '나는 그 여자가 왔다는 걸 단번에 알아채고 너에게 조용히 문을 열어 주마.'"], ["Then he will understand that something has happened suddenly and that I must see him, and he will open to me so that I can go and speak to him.", "또 한 가지 신호는 무슨 뜻밖의 일이 생겼을 때를 위해 정해 주셨습니다. 처음엔 두 번 두드리고, 그다음 잠시 사이를 두었다가 한 번 훨씬 크게 두드리는 겁니다."], ["That’s all in case Agrafena Alexandrovna can’t come herself, but sends a message.", "그러면 그분은 갑자기 무슨 일이 벌어졌고 제가 꼭 뵈어야 한다는 걸 알아채시고, 제가 들어가 말씀드릴 수 있게 문을 열어 주십니다."], ["Besides, Dmitri Fyodorovitch might come, too, so I must let him know he is near.", "이건 다 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 직접은 못 오고 전갈만 보낼 경우를 위한 겁니다."], ["His honor is awfully afraid of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, so that even if Agrafena Alexandrovna had come and were locked in with him, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch were to turn up anywhere near at the time, I should be bound to let him know at once, knocking three times.", "게다가 드미트리 표도로비치가 들이닥칠 수도 있으니, 그분이 가까이 와 있다는 것도 제가 알려 드려야 하지요. 나리께선 드미트리 표도로비치를 끔찍이 두려워하셔서, 설령 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 와서 그분과 함께 안에 잠겨 있는데 그때 마침 드미트리 표도로비치가 어디 근처에라도 나타난다면, 저는 세 번을 두드려 그분께 즉시 알려 드려야만 합니다."], ["So that the first signal of five knocks means Agrafena Alexandrovna has come, while the second signal of three knocks means ‘something important to tell you.’ His honor has shown me them several times and explained them.", "그러니까 다섯 번 두드리는 첫 번째 신호는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 왔다는 뜻이고, 세 번 두드리는 두 번째 신호는 '긴히 드릴 말씀이 있다'는 뜻입니다. 나리께선 제게 그 신호를 여러 번 보여 주시고 설명해 주셨지요."], ["And as in the whole universe no one knows of these signals but myself and his honor, so he’d open the door without the slightest hesitation and without calling out (he is awfully afraid of calling out aloud).", "그리고 온 세상에서 그 신호를 아는 사람은 저와 나리뿐이니, 그분은 조금도 망설이지 않고, 소리쳐 부르는 일도 없이(그분은 큰 소리로 부르짖는 걸 끔찍이 두려워하십니다) 문을 열어 주실 겁니다."], ["Well, those signals are known to Dmitri Fyodorovitch too, now.”", "한데 이제 그 신호를 드미트리 표도로비치도 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“How are they known?", "\"어떻게 알았어?"], ["Did you tell him?", "네가 말한 게냐?"], ["How dared you tell him?”", "어떻게 감히 그자에게 말했어?\""]], [["“It was through fright I did it.", "\"겁에 질려서 그랬습니다."], ["How could I dare to keep it back from him?", "제가 어떻게 감히 그분께 숨길 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch kept persisting every day, ‘You are deceiving me, you are hiding something from me!", "드미트리 표도로비치는 날마다 끈질기게 다그치셨습니다. '넌 날 속이고 있어, 뭔가 감추고 있지!"], ["I’ll break both your legs for you.’ So I told him those secret signals that he might see my slavish devotion, and might be satisfied that I was not deceiving him, but was telling him all I could.”", "두 다리를 분질러 놓겠다.' 그래서 전 제가 종처럼 충성을 다하고 있다는 걸 보여 드리고, 제가 그분을 속이는 게 아니라 아는 걸 죄다 말씀드리고 있다는 데 만족하시도록, 그 비밀 신호를 알려 드린 겁니다.\""]], [["“If you think that he’ll make use of those signals and try to get in, don’t let him in.”", "\"만약 그자가 그 신호를 이용해 안으로 들어오려 한다고 생각된다면, 그자를 들여보내지 마라.\""]], [["“But if I should be laid up with a fit, how can I prevent him coming in then, even if I dared prevent him, knowing how desperate he is?”", "\"하지만 제가 발작으로 드러눕는다면 그때는 어떻게 그분이 들어오시는 걸 막겠습니까. 설령 감히 막으려 한들, 그분이 얼마나 무모하신 분인지 잘 아는 처지에 말입니다.\""]], [["“Hang it!", "\"빌어먹을!"], ["How can you be so sure you are going to have a fit, confound you?", "네가 어떻게 발작을 일으킬 거라고 그토록 확신하느냐, 이 망할 놈아?"], ["Are you laughing at me?”", "날 비웃는 게냐?\""]], [["“How could I dare laugh at you?", "\"제가 어떻게 감히 나리를 비웃겠습니까?"], ["I am in no laughing humor with this fear on me.", "이렇게 공포에 짓눌려 있는데 웃을 기분이 어디 있겠습니까."], ["I feel I am going to have a fit.", "전 발작이 올 것만 같습니다."], ["I have a presentiment.", "예감이 듭니다."], ["Fright alone will bring it on.”", "공포만으로도 발작이 일어날 겁니다.\""]], [["“Confound it!", "\"빌어먹을!"], ["If you are laid up, Grigory will be on the watch.", "네가 드러눕는다면 그리고리가 망을 볼 게다."], ["Let Grigory know beforehand; he will be sure not to let him in.”", "그리고리에게 미리 알려 둬라. 그러면 그자는 분명 그를 들여보내지 않을 테지.\""]], [["“I should never dare to tell Grigory Vassilyevitch about the signals without orders from my master.", "\"저는 주인 나리의 분부 없이는 그리고리 바실리예비치에게 그 신호 이야기를 감히 꺼낼 수 없습니다."], ["And as for Grigory Vassilyevitch hearing him and not admitting him, he has been ill ever since yesterday, and Marfa Ignatyevna intends to give him medicine to-morrow.", "게다가 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그분의 소리를 듣고 들여보내지 않는 일로 말하자면, 그분은 어제부터 줄곧 앓아누워 계시고, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 내일 약을 드리려고 합니다."], ["They’ve just arranged it.", "방금 그렇게 정했지요."], ["It’s a very strange remedy of hers.", "그 약이라는 게 참 묘한 처방입니다."], ["Marfa Ignatyevna knows of a preparation and always keeps it.", "마르파 이그나티예브나는 어떤 약을 조제할 줄 알고 늘 그걸 지니고 있습니다."], ["It’s a strong thing made from some herb.", "무슨 약초로 만든 독한 것이지요."], ["She has the secret of it, and she always gives it to Grigory Vassilyevitch three times a year when his lumbago’s so bad he is almost paralyzed by it.", "그 비방을 그분이 알고 있어서, 그리고리 바실리예비치가 요통이 너무 심해 거의 몸을 못 가눌 지경이 되면 일 년에 세 번씩 늘 그걸 먹입니다."], ["Then she takes a towel, wets it with the stuff, and rubs his whole back for half an hour till it’s quite red and swollen, and what’s left in the bottle she gives him to drink with a special prayer; but not quite all, for on such occasions she leaves some for herself, and drinks it herself.", "그러면 그분은 수건을 가져다 그 약을 적셔서는, 등이 온통 벌겋게 부어오를 때까지 반 시간이나 그분의 등 전체를 문지르고, 병에 남은 것은 특별한 기도와 함께 마시게 합니다. 하지만 죄다 마시게 하진 않습니다, 그런 때면 그분은 자기 몫을 좀 남겨 두었다가 자기도 마시거든요."], ["And as they never take strong drink, I assure you they both drop asleep at once and sleep sound a very long time.", "그리고 두 사람은 독한 술이라곤 입에도 안 대는 터라, 단언컨대 둘 다 그 자리에서 곯아떨어져 아주 오랫동안 깊이 잠듭니다."], ["And when Grigory Vassilyevitch wakes up he is perfectly well after it, but Marfa Ignatyevna always has a headache from it.", "그리고리 바실리예비치는 깨어나면 씻은 듯이 멀쩡해지지만, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 언제나 그 약 때문에 두통을 앓지요."], ["So, if Marfa Ignatyevna carries out her intention to-morrow, they won’t hear anything and hinder Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그러니 마르파 이그나티예브나가 내일 작정대로 한다면, 두 사람은 아무것도 못 듣고 드미트리 표도로비치를 막지 못할 겁니다."], ["They’ll be asleep.”", "잠들어 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“What a rigmarole!", "\"무슨 그런 장광설이 다 있느냐!"], ["And it all seems to happen at once, as though it were planned.", "게다가 그 모든 게 마치 짜기라도 한 듯 한꺼번에 벌어지는군."], ["You’ll have a fit and they’ll both be unconscious,”", "넌 발작을 일으키고 두 사람은 의식을 잃고.\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."], ["“But aren’t you trying to arrange it so?”", "\"한데 혹시 네가 일부러 그렇게 꾸미려는 건 아니냐?\""], ["broke from him suddenly, and he frowned threateningly.", "하는 말이 불쑥 그의 입에서 튀어나왔고, 그는 위협하듯 이맛살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“How could I?...", "\"제가 어찌 그러겠습니까?……"], ["And why should I, when it all depends on Dmitri Fyodorovitch and his plans?...", "그리고 모든 게 드미트리 표도로비치와 그분의 계획에 달려 있는데, 제가 무엇 때문에 그러겠습니까?……"], ["If he means to do anything, he’ll do it; but if not, I shan’t be thrusting him upon his father.”", "그분이 무슨 짓을 할 작정이라면 하실 테고, 그럴 마음이 없으면 제가 굳이 그분을 아버님께 떠밀어 보내지는 않겠지요.\""]], [["“And why should he go to father, especially on the sly, if, as you say yourself, Agrafena Alexandrovna won’t come at all?”", "\"한데 네 말마따나 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 아예 오지도 않을 거라면, 그자가 무엇 때문에 아버지를 찾아가겠느냐, 그것도 몰래 말이야?\""], ["Ivan went on, turning white with anger.", "이반은 분노로 새파랗게 질려 가며 말을 이었다."], ["“You say that yourself, and all the while I’ve been here, I’ve felt sure it was all the old man’s fancy, and the creature won’t come to him.", "\"그건 네가 직접 한 말이야. 내가 여기 있는 내내 그게 다 늙은이의 망상일 뿐이고 그 계집은 그에게 오지 않으리라고 확신했단 말이다."], ["Why should Dmitri break in on him if she doesn’t come?", "여자가 오지 않는데 드미트리가 무엇 때문에 그에게 쳐들어가겠어?"], ["Speak, I want to know what you are thinking!”", "말해 봐, 네가 무슨 생각을 하는지 알고 싶다!\""]], [["“You know yourself why he’ll come.", "\"그분이 왜 오실지는 나리께서도 친히 아시잖습니까."], ["What’s the use of what I think?", "제 생각이 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?"], ["His honor will come simply because he is in a rage or suspicious on account of my illness perhaps, and he’ll dash in, as he did yesterday through impatience to search the rooms, to see whether she hasn’t escaped him on the sly.", "그분은 그저 화가 나셨거나, 어쩌면 제 병 때문에 의심이 동하셔서 오실 겁니다. 그리고 어제처럼 안달이 나서, 그 여자가 자기 몰래 빠져나간 건 아닌지 살피려고 방들을 뒤지러 들이닥치실 테지요."], ["He is perfectly well aware, too, that Fyodor Pavlovitch has a big envelope with three thousand roubles in it, tied up with ribbon and sealed with three seals.", "게다가 그분은 표도르 파블로비치께서 삼천 루블이 든 큰 봉투를 가지고 계시다는 것도 똑똑히 알고 있습니다. 리본으로 묶고 봉인을 세 개나 찍은 봉투지요."], ["On it is written in his own hand, ‘To my angel Grushenka, if she will come,’ to which he added three days later, ‘for my little chicken.’ There’s no knowing what that might do.”", "거기엔 그분의 친필로 '나의 천사 그루셴카에게, 그녀가 와 준다면'이라고 적혀 있고, 사흘 뒤엔 '나의 병아리에게'라고 덧붙여 두셨습니다. 그게 무슨 일을 부를지 누가 알겠습니까.\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"허튼소리!\""], ["cried Ivan, almost beside himself.", "이반은 거의 제정신이 아닌 채 외쳤다."], ["“Dmitri won’t come to steal money and kill my father to do it.", "\"드미트리는 돈을 훔치려고, 그것 때문에 아버지를 죽이러 오지는 않아."], ["He might have killed him yesterday on account of Grushenka, like the frantic, savage fool he is, but he won’t steal.”", "어제는 그루셴카 때문에 그 미친 듯 사나운 바보처럼 그를 죽였을지 몰라도, 도둑질은 하지 않아.\""]], [["“He is in very great need of money now—the greatest need, Ivan Fyodorovitch.", "\"그분은 지금 돈이 몹시 궁하십니다—더없이 궁하시지요, 이반 표도로비치."], ["You don’t know in what need he is,”", "그분이 얼마나 궁한 처지인지 나리께선 모르십니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov explained, with perfect composure and remarkable distinctness.", "스메르댜코프가 더없이 침착하고 놀라우리만치 또렷하게 설명했다."], ["“He looks on that three thousand as his own, too.", "\"그분은 그 삼천 루블도 자기 것이라 여기십니다."], ["He said so to me himself.", "제게 직접 그렇게 말씀하셨지요."], ["‘My father still owes me just three thousand,’ he said.", "'아버지는 아직도 내게 꼭 삼천 루블을 빚지고 있다'고 말입니다."], ["And besides that, consider, Ivan Fyodorovitch, there is something else perfectly true.", "게다가 이반 표도로비치, 또 한 가지 틀림없는 사실을 헤아려 보십시오."], ["It’s as good as certain, so to say, that Agrafena Alexandrovna will force him, if only she cares to, to marry her—the master himself, I mean, Fyodor Pavlovitch—if only she cares to, and of course she may care to.", "말하자면 거의 확실한 일인데, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 마음만 먹는다면 그분을—주인 나리, 그러니까 표도르 파블로비치 말씀입니다—자기와 결혼하게끔 몰아갈 수 있다는 겁니다. 마음만 먹는다면 말이지요, 물론 그럴 마음이 들 수도 있고요."], ["All I’ve said is that she won’t come, but maybe she’s looking for more than that—I mean to be mistress here.", "제가 한 말은 그저 그 여자가 오지 않으리라는 것뿐이었지만, 어쩌면 그 여자는 그 이상을 노리고 있는지도 모릅니다—그러니까 이 집의 안주인이 되는 것 말입니다."], ["I know myself that Samsonov, her merchant, was laughing with her about it, telling her quite openly that it would not be at all a stupid thing to do.", "저도 알다시피 그 여자의 상인인 삼소노프가 그걸 두고 그 여자와 웃으며, 그건 결코 어리석은 짓이 아닐 거라고 아주 대놓고 말했답니다. 게다가 그 여자는 머리가 비상하지요."], ["And she’s got plenty of sense.", "드미트리 표도로비치 같은 거지에게 시집갈 리가 없습니다."], ["She wouldn’t marry a beggar like Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그러니 이반 표도로비치, 그 점을 헤아려 보십시오."], ["So, taking that into consideration, Ivan Fyodorovitch, reflect that then neither Dmitri Fyodorovitch nor yourself and your brother, Alexey Fyodorovitch, would have anything after the master’s death, not a rouble, for Agrafena Alexandrovna would marry him simply to get hold of the whole, all the money there is.", "그렇게 되면 주인 나리께서 돌아가신 뒤에 드미트리 표도로비치도, 나리도, 동생분이신 알렉세이 표도로비치도 한 푼 손에 넣지 못할 겁니다. 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 오로지 전 재산을, 있는 돈 전부를 거머쥐려고 그분과 결혼할 테니까요."], ["But if your father were to die now, there’d be some forty thousand for sure, even for Dmitri Fyodorovitch whom he hates so, for he’s made no will....", "하지만 아버님께서 지금 돌아가신다면, 적어도 사만 루블쯤은 확실히 남을 겁니다, 그분이 그토록 미워하시는 드미트리 표도로비치에게도 말입니다. 유언장을 안 만들어 두셨으니까요……"], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch knows all that very well.”", "드미트리 표도로비치는 그걸 죄다 훤히 알고 계십니다.\""]], [["A sort of shudder passed over Ivan’s face.", "이반의 얼굴 위로 일종의 전율이 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["He suddenly flushed.", "그는 별안간 얼굴이 붉어졌다."]], [["“Then why on earth,”", "\"그렇다면 도대체 무엇 때문에,\""], ["he suddenly interrupted Smerdyakov, “do you advise me to go to Tchermashnya?", "그는 갑자기 스메르댜코프의 말을 가로막았다. \"내게 체르마쉬냐로 가라고 권하는 거냐?"], ["What did you mean by that?", "그 말은 또 무슨 뜻이야?"], ["If I go away, you see what will happen here.”", "내가 떠나 버리면 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어질지 너도 알 거 아니냐.\""], ["Ivan drew his breath with difficulty.", "이반은 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬었다."]], [["“Precisely so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, softly and reasonably, watching Ivan intently, however.", "스메르댜코프가 나직하고 조리 있게 말했는데, 그러면서도 이반을 뚫어지게 살피고 있었다."]], [["“What do you mean by ‘precisely so’?”", "\"'바로 그렇다'니, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\""], ["Ivan questioned him, with a menacing light in his eyes, restraining himself with difficulty.", "이반은 안간힘으로 자신을 억누르며, 눈에 위협의 빛을 띤 채 그에게 따져 물었다."]], [["“I spoke because I felt sorry for you.", "\"나리가 딱하게 여겨져서 드린 말씀입니다."], ["If I were in your place I should simply throw it all up ...", "제가 나리 처지라면 그저 죄다 내팽개쳐 버리고 말겠습니다……"], ["rather than stay on in such a position,”", "이런 처지에 남아 있느니 말입니다.\""], ["answered Smerdyakov, with the most candid air looking at Ivan’s flashing eyes.", "스메르댜코프는 이반의 번득이는 눈을 바라보며 더없이 솔직한 태도로 대답했다."], ["They were both silent.", "두 사람은 입을 다물었다."]], [["“You seem to be a perfect idiot, and what’s more ...", "\"넌 아무래도 완전히 멍청이인 데다, 게다가……"], ["an awful scoundrel, too.”", "지독한 악당이기도 하구나.\""], ["Ivan rose suddenly from the bench.", "이반이 별안간 벤치에서 일어났다."], ["He was about to pass straight through the gate, but he stopped short and turned to Smerdyakov.", "그는 곧장 문을 지나려 했으나, 우뚝 멈춰 서서 스메르댜코프를 돌아보았다."], ["Something strange followed.", "그러자 묘한 일이 벌어졌다."], ["Ivan, in a sudden paroxysm, bit his lip, clenched his fists, and, in another minute, would have flung himself on Smerdyakov.", "이반은 별안간 발작하듯 입술을 깨물고 주먹을 불끈 쥐었으니, 한순간만 더 있었다면 스메르댜코프에게 달려들었을 것이다."], ["The latter, anyway, noticed it at the same moment, started, and shrank back.", "어쨌든 스메르댜코프도 그 순간 그것을 알아채고는 흠칫 놀라 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["But the moment passed without mischief to Smerdyakov, and Ivan turned in silence, as it seemed in perplexity, to the gate.", "그러나 그 순간은 스메르댜코프에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않은 채 지나갔고, 이반은 영문 모를 당혹감에 잠긴 듯 잠자코 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“I am going away to Moscow to-morrow, if you care to know—early to-morrow morning.", "\"네가 알고 싶다면 말해 두마, 난 내일 모스크바로 떠난다—내일 이른 아침에."], ["That’s all!”", "그게 다야!\""], ["he suddenly said aloud angrily, and wondered himself afterwards what need there was to say this then to Smerdyakov.", "그는 별안간 화가 나서 소리 내어 말했고, 훗날 그때 무엇 때문에 스메르댜코프에게 굳이 그런 말을 했는지 스스로도 의아해했다."]], [["“That’s the best thing you can do,”", "\"그게 나리께서 하실 수 있는 가장 좋은 일이지요.\""], ["he responded, as though he had expected to hear it; “except that you can always be telegraphed for from Moscow, if anything should happen here.”", "그는 그 말을 미리 예상하고 있었다는 듯 응수했다. \"다만 여기서 무슨 일이 생기면 모스크바로도 언제든 전보를 칠 수 있다는 것만 빼면 말입니다.\""]], [["Ivan stopped again, and again turned quickly to Smerdyakov.", "이반은 다시 걸음을 멈추고, 또다시 휙 스메르댜코프를 돌아보았다."], ["But a change had passed over him, too.", "그러나 그에게도 변화가 일어나 있었다."], ["All his familiarity and carelessness had completely disappeared.", "그 모든 허물없음과 무심함이 씻은 듯이 사라져 버린 것이다."], ["His face expressed attention and expectation, intent but timid and cringing.", "그의 얼굴은 주의와 기대를, 그러면서도 골똘하면서도 겁먹은 듯 비굴한 빛을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Haven’t you something more to say—something to add?”", "\"뭔가 더 하실 말씀이—뭔가 덧붙이실 말씀이 없으십니까?\""], ["could be read in the intent gaze he fixed on Ivan.", "그가 이반에게 고정한 골똘한 눈길에서는 이런 물음이 읽혔다."]], [["“And couldn’t I be sent for from Tchermashnya, too—in case anything happened?”", "\"그럼 체르마쉬냐로도 사람을 보내 날 부를 수 있겠느냐—무슨 일이 생기면 말이다?\""], ["Ivan shouted suddenly, for some unknown reason raising his voice.", "이반은 영문 모를 까닭에 목소리를 높이며 별안간 소리쳤다."]], [["“From Tchermashnya, too ...", "\"체르마쉬냐로도……"], ["you could be sent for,”", "사람을 보내 부르실 수 있지요.\""], ["Smerdyakov muttered, almost in a whisper, looking disconcerted, but gazing intently into Ivan’s eyes.", "스메르댜코프가 거의 속삭이듯 중얼거렸는데, 당황한 기색이면서도 이반의 눈을 골똘히 들여다보았다."]], [["“Only Moscow is farther and Tchermashnya is nearer.", "\"다만 모스크바는 더 멀고 체르마쉬냐는 더 가깝다는 것뿐이지."], ["Is it to save my spending money on the fare, or to save my going so far out of my way, that you insist on Tchermashnya?”", "네가 체르마쉬냐를 고집하는 건 내가 찻삯을 아끼게 하려는 거냐, 아니면 내가 그렇게 멀리 돌아가지 않게 하려는 거냐?\""]], [["“Precisely so ...”", "\"바로 그렇습니다……\""], ["muttered Smerdyakov, with a breaking voice.", "스메르댜코프가 갈라지는 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["He looked at Ivan with a revolting smile, and again made ready to draw back.", "그는 역겨운 미소를 띤 채 이반을 바라보더니, 다시 뒷걸음질 칠 채비를 했다."], ["But to his astonishment Ivan broke into a laugh, and went through the gate still laughing.", "그러나 놀랍게도 이반은 웃음을 터뜨리며 여전히 웃은 채로 문을 지나갔다."], ["Any one who had seen his face at that moment would have known that he was not laughing from lightness of heart, and he could not have explained himself what he was feeling at that instant.", "그 순간 그의 얼굴을 본 사람이라면 누구나 그가 마음이 가벼워 웃는 것이 아님을 알았을 터이고, 그 자신도 그 순간 자기가 무엇을 느끼고 있는지 설명할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["He moved and walked as though in a nervous frenzy.", "그는 마치 신경의 광란에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 움직이고 걸었다."]], [["# Chapter VII. “It’s Always Worth While Speaking To A Clever Man”", "# Chapter VII. “It’s Always Worth While Speaking To A Clever Man”"]], [["And in the same nervous frenzy, too, he spoke.", "그리고 그는 바로 그 신경질적인 광기에 휩싸인 채 말을 했다."], ["Meeting Fyodor Pavlovitch in the drawing-room directly he went in, he shouted to him, waving his hands, “I am going upstairs to my room, not in to you.", "안으로 들어서자마자 응접실에서 표도르 파블로비치와 마주치자, 그는 두 손을 내저으며 소리쳤다. \"위층 제 방으로 가는 거지, 아버지한테 가는 게 아닙니다."], ["Good-by!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""], ["and passed by, trying not even to look at his father.", "그러고는 아버지를 쳐다보지조차 않으려 애쓰며 지나쳐 갔다."], ["Very possibly the old man was too hateful to him at that moment; but such an unceremonious display of hostility was a surprise even to Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그 순간 노인이 그에게 너무도 가증스러웠던 것일 수도 있다. 그러나 이토록 거리낌 없는 적의의 표출은 표도르 파블로비치에게마저 뜻밖이었다."], ["And the old man evidently wanted to tell him something at once and had come to meet him in the drawing-room on purpose.", "게다가 노인은 분명 그에게 무언가 곧장 말하고 싶은 것이 있어서 일부러 응접실로 마중을 나온 참이었다."], ["Receiving this amiable greeting, he stood still in silence and with an ironical air watched his son going upstairs, till he passed out of sight.", "이런 살가운 인사를 받고서, 노인은 잠자코 선 채 빈정거리는 기색으로 아들이 위층으로 올라가는 것을, 그 모습이 보이지 않게 될 때까지 지켜보았다."]], [["“What’s the matter with him?”", "\"저놈이 왜 저러나?\""], ["he promptly asked Smerdyakov, who had followed Ivan.", "노인은 이반을 뒤따라온 스메르댜코프에게 즉시 물었다."]], [["“Angry about something.", "\"뭔가 화가 나신 모양입죠."], ["Who can tell?”", "누가 알겠습니까?\""], ["the valet muttered evasively.", "하인은 얼버무리듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Confound him!", "\"빌어먹을 놈!"], ["Let him be angry then.", "그럼 화내라고 해라."], ["Bring in the samovar, and get along with you.", "사모바르나 들여놓고 어서 꺼져. 동작 좀 빨리 해!"], ["Look sharp! No news?”", "무슨 소식 없냐?\""]], [["Then followed a series of questions such as Smerdyakov had just complained of to Ivan, all relating to his expected visitor, and these questions we will omit.", "그러고는 스메르댜코프가 방금 이반에게 하소연한 그런 질문들이 줄줄이 이어졌으니, 죄다 그가 기다리는 손님에 관한 것들이었거니와, 이런 질문들은 여기서 생략하기로 한다."], ["Half an hour later the house was locked, and the crazy old man was wandering along through the rooms in excited expectation of hearing every minute the five knocks agreed upon.", "반 시간쯤 뒤에는 집이 잠겼고, 미친 노인은 약속된 다섯 번의 노크 소리를 매 순간 들을 양으로 들뜬 기대에 차서 이 방 저 방을 헤매고 다녔다."], ["Now and then he peered out into the darkness, seeing nothing.", "이따금 그는 어둠 속을 내다보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["It was very late, but Ivan was still awake and reflecting.", "밤이 무척 깊었지만 이반은 여전히 잠들지 못한 채 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He sat up late that night, till two o’clock.", "그날 밤 그는 두 시까지 늦도록 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["But we will not give an account of his thoughts, and this is not the place to look into that soul—its turn will come.", "그러나 우리는 그의 생각을 여기서 일일이 풀어놓지 않을 것이며, 그 영혼을 들여다볼 자리도 지금은 아니다—그 차례는 다가올 것이다."], ["And even if one tried, it would be very hard to give an account of them, for there were no thoughts in his brain, but something very vague, and, above all, intense excitement.", "그리고 설령 그러려고 애쓴다 한들 그것을 풀어놓기란 매우 어려운 일이니, 그의 머릿속에는 사실 아무 생각도 없었고, 다만 무언가 몹시 어렴풋한 것과, 무엇보다도 격렬한 흥분이 있었을 따름이다."], ["He felt himself that he had lost his bearings.", "그는 자신이 방향을 잃었음을 스스로 느꼈다."], ["He was fretted, too, by all sorts of strange and almost surprising desires; for instance, after midnight he suddenly had an intense irresistible inclination to go down, open the door, go to the lodge and beat Smerdyakov.", "또한 그는 온갖 기이하고 거의 놀랍기까지 한 욕망들에 시달렸으니, 이를테면 자정이 지나자 그는 별안간 아래층으로 내려가 문을 열고 별채로 가서 스메르댜코프를 두들겨 패고 싶다는, 격렬하고 거역할 수 없는 충동에 사로잡혔다."], ["But if he had been asked why, he could not have given any exact reason, except perhaps that he loathed the valet as one who had insulted him more gravely than any one in the world.", "그러나 누가 그에게 왜냐고 물었다면, 그는 그 어떤 정확한 이유도 댈 수 없었으리니, 다만 그 하인이 세상 누구보다도 자기를 더 심하게 모욕한 자라서 그를 혐오한다는 것 정도가 고작이었으리라."], ["On the other hand, he was more than once that night overcome by a sort of inexplicable humiliating terror, which he felt positively paralyzed his physical powers.", "한편으로 그날 밤 그는 한두 번이 아니게 무어라 설명할 수 없는 굴욕적인 공포에 사로잡혔으니, 그 공포가 자신의 육체적 기력을 그야말로 마비시키는 것을 느꼈다."], ["His head ached and he was giddy.", "머리가 아프고 어지러웠다."], ["A feeling of hatred was rankling in his heart, as though he meant to avenge himself on some one.", "마치 누군가에게 복수라도 하려는 듯, 증오의 감정이 가슴속에서 쓰라리게 곪아 갔다."], ["He even hated Alyosha, recalling the conversation he had just had with him.", "그는 방금 알료샤와 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 그마저 미워했다."], ["At moments he hated himself intensely.", "어떤 순간에는 자기 자신을 격렬하게 증오하기도 했다."], ["Of Katerina Ivanovna he almost forgot to think, and wondered greatly at this afterwards, especially as he remembered perfectly that when he had protested so valiantly to Katerina Ivanovna that he would go away next day to Moscow, something had whispered in his heart, “That’s nonsense, you are not going, and it won’t be so easy to tear yourself away as you are boasting now.”", "카테리나 이바노브나에 대해서는 거의 생각하는 것조차 잊었는데, 나중에 그는 이 사실에 크게 놀랐으니, 더욱이 자신이 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그토록 호기롭게 이튿날 모스크바로 떠나겠다고 다짐했을 때 가슴속에서 무언가가 이렇게 속삭였던 것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있었기 때문이다. \"그건 헛소리야, 너는 떠나지 않아, 그리고 지금 큰소리치는 만큼 그렇게 쉽게 떨치고 가지도 못할걸.\""]], [["Remembering that night long afterwards, Ivan recalled with peculiar repulsion how he had suddenly got up from the sofa and had stealthily, as though he were afraid of being watched, opened the door, gone out on the staircase and listened to Fyodor Pavlovitch stirring down below, had listened a long while—some five minutes—with a sort of strange curiosity, holding his breath while his heart throbbed.", "오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 그날 밤을 회상할 때면, 이반은 자신이 어떻게 별안간 소파에서 일어나, 마치 누가 지켜볼까 봐 두려워하기라도 하듯 살그머니 문을 열고 계단으로 나가, 아래층에서 부스럭거리는 표도르 파블로비치의 기척에 귀를 기울였던가를 유난한 혐오감과 함께 떠올렸으니, 그는 그렇게 한참을—오 분쯤을—기이한 호기심 같은 것에 사로잡혀, 심장이 두근거리는 동안 숨을 죽인 채 엿들었던 것이다."], ["And why he had done all this, why he was listening, he could not have said. That “action”", "그리고 어째서 그 모든 짓을 했는지, 어째서 엿듣고 있었는지, 그는 끝내 말할 수 없었으리라."], ["all his life afterwards he called “infamous,” and at the bottom of his heart, he thought of it as the basest action of his life.", "그 \"행동\"을 그는 평생토록 \"수치스러운 짓\"이라 불렀고, 마음 밑바닥에서는 그것을 제 생애에서 가장 비열한 짓으로 여겼다."], ["For Fyodor Pavlovitch himself he felt no hatred at that moment, but was simply intensely curious to know how he was walking down there below and what he must be doing now.", "그 순간 표도르 파블로비치 자신에 대해서는 아무런 증오도 느끼지 않았고, 다만 그가 저 아래서 어떻게 걸어 다니는지, 지금 무엇을 하고 있을지를 알고 싶은 격렬한 호기심에 사로잡혀 있었을 뿐이다."], ["He wondered and imagined how he must be peeping out of the dark windows and stopping in the middle of the room, listening, listening—for some one to knock.", "그는 노인이 어두운 창밖을 어떻게 내다보고, 방 한가운데 멈춰 서서 누군가 노크하기를 듣고, 또 듣고 있을지를 궁금해하며 머릿속에 그려 보았다."], ["Ivan went out on to the stairs twice to listen like this.", "이렇게 엿듣기 위해 이반은 두 번이나 계단으로 나갔다."]], [["About two o’clock when everything was quiet, and even Fyodor Pavlovitch had gone to bed, Ivan had got into bed, firmly resolved to fall asleep at once, as he felt fearfully exhausted.", "두 시쯤 모든 것이 잠잠해지고 표도르 파블로비치마저 잠자리에 들었을 때, 이반도 곧장 잠들기로 단단히 마음먹고 침대에 들었으니, 끔찍하게 지친 것을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["And he did fall asleep at once, and slept soundly without dreams, but waked early, at seven o’clock, when it was broad daylight.", "그리고 그는 정말로 곧바로 잠들었고, 꿈도 꾸지 않고 깊이 잠들었으나, 일곱 시에 일찍, 날이 환히 밝았을 때 잠에서 깼다."], ["Opening his eyes, he was surprised to feel himself extraordinarily vigorous.", "눈을 뜬 그는 자신이 유난히 기운차게 느껴지는 데 놀랐다."], ["He jumped up at once and dressed quickly; then dragged out his trunk and began packing immediately.", "그는 곧장 벌떡 일어나 부랴부랴 옷을 입었고, 그러고는 트렁크를 끌어내어 당장 짐을 꾸리기 시작했다."], ["His linen had come back from the laundress the previous morning.", "그의 속옷가지는 전날 아침 세탁부에게서 돌아와 있었다."], ["Ivan positively smiled at the thought that everything was helping his sudden departure.", "모든 일이 자신의 돌연한 출발을 거들어 주고 있다는 생각에 이반은 정말로 빙긋 웃었다."], ["And his departure certainly was sudden.", "그리고 그의 출발은 분명 돌연한 것이었다."], ["Though Ivan had said the day before (to Katerina Ivanovna, Alyosha, and Smerdyakov) that he was leaving next day, yet he remembered that he had no thought of departure when he went to bed, or, at least, had not dreamed that his first act in the morning would be to pack his trunk.", "이반이 전날 (카테리나 이바노브나와 알료샤와 스메르댜코프에게) 이튿날 떠나겠다고 말하기는 했으나, 그는 잠자리에 들 때만 해도 떠날 생각이라곤 없었으며, 적어도 아침에 일어나 가장 먼저 할 일이 트렁크를 꾸리는 것이리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했음을 떠올렸다."], ["At last his trunk and bag were ready.", "마침내 트렁크와 가방이 준비되었다."], ["It was about nine o’clock when Marfa Ignatyevna came in with her usual inquiry, “Where will your honor take your tea, in your own room or downstairs?”", "마르파 이그나티예브나가 늘 하던 대로 \"나리, 차는 어디서 드시겠습니까, 방에서요, 아래층에서요?\" 하고 물으러 들어온 것은 아홉 시쯤이었다."], ["He looked almost cheerful, but there was about him, about his words and gestures, something hurried and scattered.", "그는 거의 명랑해 보였으나, 그의 태도에도, 말과 몸짓에도 무언가 서두르고 산만한 데가 있었다."], ["Greeting his father affably, and even inquiring specially after his health, though he did not wait to hear his answer to the end, he announced that he was starting off in an hour to return to Moscow for good, and begged him to send for the horses.", "아버지에게 살갑게 인사하고 심지어 건강이 어떠냐고 특별히 안부까지 물었으되 그 대답을 끝까지 듣지도 않은 채, 그는 한 시간 뒤에 모스크바로 아주 돌아가기 위해 떠나겠노라 알리며 말을 준비시켜 달라고 청했다."], ["His father heard this announcement with no sign of surprise, and forgot in an unmannerly way to show regret at losing him.", "그의 아버지는 이 통고를 놀라는 기색 하나 없이 들었고, 무례하게도 아들을 잃는 데 대한 아쉬움을 내비치는 것마저 잊었다."], ["Instead of doing so, he flew into a great flutter at the recollection of some important business of his own.", "그러기는커녕 그는 무언가 제 중요한 용무가 떠올라 크게 부산을 떨기 시작했다."]], [["“What a fellow you are!", "\"이런 녀석을 봤나!"], ["Not to tell me yesterday!", "어째서 어제 말하지 않았어!"], ["Never mind; we’ll manage it all the same.", "괜찮다, 어쨌든 다 어떻게든 될 테니."], ["Do me a great service, my dear boy.", "얘야, 나한테 큰 일 하나만 해 다오."], ["Go to Tchermashnya on the way.", "가는 길에 체르마시냐에 좀 들러라."], ["It’s only to turn to the left from the station at Volovya, only another twelve versts and you come to Tchermashnya.”", "볼로뱌 역에서 왼쪽으로 꺾기만 하면 되는데, 거기서 겨우 십이 베르스타만 더 가면 체르마시냐가 나오거든.\""]], [["“I’m sorry, I can’t.", "\"죄송하지만, 못 합니다."], ["It’s eighty versts to the railway and the train starts for Moscow at seven o’clock to-night.", "철도까지가 팔십 베르스타인 데다, 모스크바행 기차가 오늘 밤 일곱 시에 떠나거든요."], ["I can only just catch it.”", "겨우 시간 맞춰 탈 수 있을까 말까예요.\""]], [["“You’ll catch it to-morrow or the day after, but to-day turn off to Tchermashnya.", "\"기차야 내일이나 모레 타면 되고, 오늘은 체르마시냐로 꺾어 들어가라."], ["It won’t put you out much to humor your father!", "아비 비위 좀 맞춰 준다고 그리 손해 볼 것도 없잖느냐!"], ["If I hadn’t had something to keep me here, I would have run over myself long ago, for I’ve some business there in a hurry.", "여기 발목 잡는 일만 없었어도 진작 내가 직접 다녀왔을 게다, 거기 급한 용무가 좀 있어서 말이다."], ["But here I ...", "한데 지금 나는…"], ["it’s not the time for me to go now....", "지금은 도무지 갈 형편이 못 돼서…."], ["You see, I’ve two pieces of copse land there.", "저, 거기 내 잡목림이 두 자리 있단다."], ["The Maslovs, an old merchant and his son, will give eight thousand for the timber.", "마슬로프네, 그 늙은 상인과 그 아들놈이 목재 값으로 팔천을 주겠다는 게야."], ["But last year I just missed a purchaser who would have given twelve.", "한데 작년에는 만이천을 주겠다던 사람을 놓쳐 버렸지 뭐냐."], ["There’s no getting any one about here to buy it.", "이 근방에서는 도무지 사 줄 사람을 구할 수가 없어."], ["The Maslovs have it all their own way.", "마슬로프네가 죄다 제멋대로 주물러 대거든."], ["One has to take what they’ll give, for no one here dare bid against them.", "그놈들이 주는 대로 받을 수밖에 없으니, 여기서는 아무도 감히 그놈들과 맞붙어 값을 부르질 못한단 말이다."], ["The priest at Ilyinskoe wrote to me last Thursday that a merchant called Gorstkin, a man I know, had turned up.", "일린스코예 신부가 지난 목요일에 편지를 보냈는데, 내가 아는 고르스트킨이라는 상인이 나타났다는 게야."], ["What makes him valuable is that he is not from these parts, so he is not afraid of the Maslovs.", "그자가 쓸모 있는 건 이 고장 사람이 아니라서 마슬로프네를 두려워하지 않는다는 점이지."], ["He says he will give me eleven thousand for the copse.", "그자가 잡목림 값으로 만천을 주겠다는구나."], ["Do you hear?", "듣고 있느냐?"], ["But he’ll only be here, the priest writes, for a week altogether, so you must go at once and make a bargain with him.”", "한데 신부 말로는 그자가 통틀어 일주일밖에 여기 머물지 않는다니, 네가 당장 가서 그자와 흥정을 매듭지어야 한다는 게야.\""]], [["“Well, you write to the priest; he’ll make the bargain.”", "\"그럼 신부님께 편지를 쓰세요, 신부님이 흥정을 매듭지으시면 되잖아요.\""]], [["“He can’t do it.", "\"그 사람은 못 한다."], ["He has no eye for business.", "그 사람은 장사에 영 눈이 없어."], ["He is a perfect treasure, I’d give him twenty thousand to take care of for me without a receipt; but he has no eye for business, he is a perfect child, a crow could deceive him.", "그야 둘도 없는 보배 같은 인물이지, 영수증 한 장 없이도 이만을 맡겨 둘 만한 사람이야. 한데 장사에는 도통 눈이 없어, 영락없는 어린애라니까, 까마귀한테도 속아 넘어갈 위인이야."], ["And yet he is a learned man, would you believe it?", "그런데도 학식은 또 있는 사람이거든, 믿어지느냐?"], ["This Gorstkin looks like a peasant, he wears a blue kaftan, but he is a regular rogue.", "이 고르스트킨이란 자는 겉보기엔 농사꾼 같고 파란 카프탄을 걸치고 다니지만, 천생 사기꾼이지."], ["That’s the common complaint.", "그게 또 다들 골치 썩는 점이고."], ["He is a liar.", "그자는 거짓말쟁이야."], ["Sometimes he tells such lies that you wonder why he is doing it.", "어떨 땐 어찌나 새빨간 거짓말을 늘어놓는지 대체 왜 저러나 싶을 정도란다."], ["He told me the year before last that his wife was dead and that he had married another, and would you believe it, there was not a word of truth in it?", "재작년에는 나한테 마누라가 죽어서 새장가를 들었다고 하더구나, 한데 그게 한마디도 사실이 아니었으니 믿어지느냐?"], ["His wife has never died at all, she is alive to this day and gives him a beating twice a week.", "마누라는 죽기는커녕 지금까지도 멀쩡히 살아서 일주일에 두 번씩 그자를 두들겨 팬다지 뭐냐."], ["So what you have to find out is whether he is lying or speaking the truth, when he says he wants to buy it and would give eleven thousand.”", "그러니 네가 알아내야 할 건, 그자가 잡목림을 사겠다며 만천을 주겠다고 할 때, 그게 거짓말인지 참말인지 하는 거다.\""]], [["“I shall be no use in such a business.", "\"그런 일에는 제가 아무 쓸모도 없을 겁니다."], ["I have no eye either.”", "저도 그런 눈이 없는걸요.\""]], [["“Stay, wait a bit!", "\"가만, 잠깐 기다려 봐라!"], ["You will be of use, for I will tell you the signs by which you can judge about Gorstkin.", "너는 쓸모가 있고말고, 고르스트킨을 판가름할 표시를 일러 줄 테니. 그자와 거래한 지 오래되었거든."], ["I’ve done business with him a long time.", "그게 말이다, 너는 그자의 턱수염을 잘 봐야 한다."], ["You see, you must watch his beard; he has a nasty, thin, red beard.", "그자한텐 더럽고 가늘고 불그스름한 턱수염이 있거든."], ["If his beard shakes when he talks and he gets cross, it’s all right, he is saying what he means, he wants to do business.", "그자가 말할 때 턱수염이 떨리고 화를 내면, 그건 됐다, 진심을 말하는 거고 거래를 하고 싶다는 게야."], ["But if he strokes his beard with his left hand and grins—he is trying to cheat you.", "한데 왼손으로 턱수염을 쓰다듬으면서 히죽거리면, 그건 너를 속여먹으려는 거지."], ["Don’t watch his eyes, you won’t find out anything from his eyes, he is a deep one, a rogue—but watch his beard!", "눈은 보지 마라, 그자 눈에서는 아무것도 알아낼 수 없으니, 그자는 속을 알 수 없는 깊은 위인, 사기꾼이거든—하지만 턱수염은 봐야 한다!"], ["I’ll give you a note and you show it to him.", "내가 쪽지를 한 장 써 줄 테니 그자한테 보여 줘라."], ["He’s called Gorstkin, though his real name is Lyagavy;[4] but don’t call him so, he will be offended.", "그자는 고르스트킨이라 불리지만 본명은 랴가비야. 그래도 그렇게 부르진 마라, 화를 낼 테니."], ["If you come to an understanding with him, and see it’s all right, write here at once.", "그자와 말이 통해서 다 됐다 싶거든 여기로 당장 편지를 써라. 그저 '거짓말이 아닙니다'라고만 쓰면 된다."], ["You need only write: ‘He’s not lying.’ Stand out for eleven thousand; one thousand you can knock off, but not more.", "만천에서 버텨라. 천쯤은 깎아 줘도 좋지만 그 이상은 안 된다."], ["Just think!", "생각해 봐라!"], ["there’s a difference between eight thousand and eleven thousand.", "팔천하고 만천은 차이가 크지 않으냐. 삼천을 그냥 줍는 거나 다름없어."], ["It’s as good as picking up three thousand; it’s not so easy to find a purchaser, and I’m in desperate need of money.", "사 줄 사람을 구하기도 쉽지 않은 데다, 나는 지금 돈이 절박하게 필요하단 말이다."], ["Only let me know it’s serious, and I’ll run over and fix it up.", "그저 진짜다 싶거든 알려만 다오, 내가 직접 달려가서 매듭지을 테니."], ["I’ll snatch the time somehow.", "어떻게든 짬을 내 보마."], ["But what’s the good of my galloping over, if it’s all a notion of the priest’s?", "한데 그게 다 신부 혼자만의 짐작이라면, 내가 달려간들 무슨 소용이겠느냐?"], ["Come, will you go?”", "자, 가 주겠느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, I can’t spare the time.", "\"아, 저는 그럴 시간이 없습니다."], ["You must excuse me.”", "양해해 주세요.\""]], [["“Come, you might oblige your father.", "\"이봐라, 아비 청 한번 들어줘도 되잖느냐."], ["I shan’t forget it.", "내 잊지 않으마."], ["You’ve no heart, any of you—that’s what it is?", "너희들은 죄다 인정머리가 없어—바로 그게 문제다?"], ["What’s a day or two to you?", "너한테 하루 이틀이 뭐 대수냐?"], ["Where are you going now—to Venice?", "지금 어디로 가는 거냐—베네치아냐?"], ["Your Venice will keep another two days.", "네 베네치아야 이틀쯤 더 기다려 줄 게다."], ["I would have sent Alyosha, but what use is Alyosha in a thing like that?", "알료샤를 보낼까도 했다만, 이런 일에 알료샤가 무슨 소용이겠느냐?"], ["I send you just because you are a clever fellow.", "너를 보내는 건 다름 아니라 네가 영리한 녀석이라서다."], ["Do you suppose I don’t see that?", "내가 그걸 모를 성싶으냐?"], ["You know nothing about timber, but you’ve got an eye.", "너는 목재에 대해선 아무것도 모르지만, 보는 눈이 있어."], ["All that is wanted is to see whether the man is in earnest.", "필요한 건 그저 그자가 진심인지 아닌지를 알아내는 것뿐이야."], ["I tell you, watch his beard—if his beard shakes you know he is in earnest.”", "그러니까 말이다, 턱수염을 봐라—턱수염이 떨리면 진심인 줄 알면 된다.\""]], [["“You force me to go to that damned Tchermashnya yourself, then?”", "\"그럼 아버지가 직접 저를 그 빌어먹을 체르마시냐로 떠밀어 보내시는 거군요?\""], ["cried Ivan, with a malignant smile.", "이반이 음험한 미소를 지으며 외쳤다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch did not catch, or would not catch, the malignancy, but he caught the smile.", "표도르 파블로비치는 그 음험함을 알아채지 못했거나, 아니면 알아채려 들지 않았으나, 미소만은 알아챘다."]], [["“Then you’ll go, you’ll go?", "\"그럼 가는 게냐, 가는 거야?"], ["I’ll scribble the note for you at once.”", "내 당장 쪽지를 끄적여 주마.\""]], [["“I don’t know whether I shall go.", "\"갈지 안 갈지 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t know.", "모르겠다고요."], ["I’ll decide on the way.”", "가는 길에 정하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"무슨 소리!"], ["Decide at once.", "당장 정해라."], ["My dear fellow, decide!", "얘야, 정하라니까!"], ["If you settle the matter, write me a line; give it to the priest and he’ll send it on to me at once.", "일을 매듭짓거든 나한테 몇 자 적어 보내라. 신부한테 건네주면 그 사람이 곧장 내게 부쳐 줄 게다."], ["And I won’t delay you more than that.", "그것 말고는 너를 더 붙들지 않으마."], ["You can go to Venice.", "베네치아엔 가도 좋아."], ["The priest will give you horses back to Volovya station.”", "신부가 너를 볼로뱌 역까지 도로 데려다줄 말을 내줄 게다.\""]], [["The old man was quite delighted.", "노인은 무척 흡족해했다."], ["He wrote the note, and sent for the horses.", "그는 쪽지를 써서 말을 부르러 보냈다."], ["A light lunch was brought in, with brandy.", "가벼운 점심이 브랜디와 함께 들어왔다."], ["When Fyodor Pavlovitch was pleased, he usually became expansive, but to-day he seemed to restrain himself.", "표도르 파블로비치는 기분이 좋으면 으레 수다스러워졌으나, 오늘은 자제하는 듯했다."], ["Of Dmitri, for instance, he did not say a word.", "가령 드미트리에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He was quite unmoved by the parting, and seemed, in fact, at a loss for something to say.", "그는 이별에 조금도 동요하지 않았고, 사실 무슨 말을 해야 할지 막막해하는 눈치였다."], ["Ivan noticed this particularly.", "이반은 이 점을 유독 눈여겨보았다."], ["“He must be bored with me,”", "'내가 지겨워진 게로군.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["Only when accompanying his son out on to the steps, the old man began to fuss about.", "다만 아들을 현관 계단까지 배웅하러 나가서야 노인은 부산을 떨기 시작했다."], ["He would have kissed him, but Ivan made haste to hold out his hand, obviously avoiding the kiss.", "그는 아들에게 입맞추려 했으나, 이반이 서둘러 손을 내밀어 보란 듯이 입맞춤을 피했다."], ["His father saw it at once, and instantly pulled himself up.", "그의 아버지는 그것을 대번에 알아채고 즉시 자세를 가다듬었다."]], [["“Well, good luck to you, good luck to you!”", "\"자, 잘 가거라, 잘 가!\""], ["he repeated from the steps.", "노인은 계단에서 거듭 말했다."], ["“You’ll come again some time or other?", "\"언제든 또 오겠지?"], ["Mind you do come.", "꼭 와야 한다."], ["I shall always be glad to see you.", "나는 언제든 너를 보면 반가울 게다."], ["Well, Christ be with you!”", "그럼, 그리스도께서 너와 함께하시기를!\""]], [["Ivan got into the carriage.", "이반은 마차에 올라탔다."]], [["“Good-by, Ivan!", "\"잘 가거라, 이반!"], ["Don’t be too hard on me!”", "이 아비를 너무 모질게 대하지 말아 다오!\""], ["the father called for the last time.", "아버지가 마지막으로 외쳤다."]], [["The whole household came out to take leave—Smerdyakov, Marfa and Grigory.", "온 집안 식구가 작별 인사를 하러 나왔으니—스메르댜코프와 마르파와 그리고리였다."], ["Ivan gave them ten roubles each.", "이반은 그들에게 십 루블씩 나눠 주었다."], ["When he had seated himself in the carriage, Smerdyakov jumped up to arrange the rug.", "그가 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉자, 스메르댜코프가 펄쩍 뛰어올라 무릎 덮개를 매만져 주었다."]], [["“You see ...", "\"이봐…"], ["I am going to Tchermashnya,”", "나는 체르마시냐로 가네.\""], ["broke suddenly from Ivan.", "이반의 입에서 별안간 이런 말이 튀어나왔다."], ["Again, as the day before, the words seemed to drop of themselves, and he laughed, too, a peculiar, nervous laugh.", "전날과 마찬가지로 그 말은 저절로 흘러나오는 듯했고, 그는 또 기이하고 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["He remembered it long after.", "그는 오래도록 그것을 기억했다."]], [["“It’s a true saying then, that ‘it’s always worth while speaking to a clever man,’”", "\"그러니까 '영리한 사람한테는 언제든 말을 붙여 볼 만하다'는 말이 참말입죠.\""], ["answered Smerdyakov firmly, looking significantly at Ivan.", "스메르댜코프가 이반을 의미심장하게 바라보며 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["The carriage rolled away.", "마차가 굴러갔다."], ["Nothing was clear in Ivan’s soul, but he looked eagerly around him at the fields, at the hills, at the trees, at a flock of geese flying high overhead in the bright sky.", "이반의 영혼 속에는 무엇 하나 분명한 것이 없었으나, 그는 들판을, 언덕을, 나무들을, 환한 하늘 높이 날아가는 기러기 떼를 열심히 둘러보았다."], ["And all of a sudden he felt very happy.", "그러다 별안간 그는 무척 행복해졌다."], ["He tried to talk to the driver, and he felt intensely interested in an answer the peasant made him; but a minute later he realized that he was not catching anything, and that he had not really even taken in the peasant’s answer.", "그는 마부에게 말을 붙여 보려 했고, 그 농사꾼이 한 대답에 격렬하게 흥미를 느꼈다. 그러나 잠시 뒤 그는 자기가 아무것도 알아듣지 못하고 있으며, 사실은 농사꾼의 대답을 제대로 새겨듣지도 않았음을 깨달았다."], ["He was silent, and it was pleasant even so.", "그는 입을 다물었고, 그래도 마음은 흐뭇했다."], ["The air was fresh, pure and cool, the sky bright.", "공기는 신선하고 맑고 서늘했으며, 하늘은 환했다."], ["The images of Alyosha and Katerina Ivanovna floated into his mind.", "알료샤와 카테리나 이바노브나의 모습이 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["But he softly smiled, blew softly on the friendly phantoms, and they flew away.", "그러나 그는 부드럽게 미소 지으며 그 다정한 환영들을 향해 살그머니 입김을 불었고, 그러자 그것들은 날아가 버렸다."], ["“There’s plenty of time for them,”", "'저들을 위한 시간은 얼마든지 있어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["They reached the station quickly, changed horses, and galloped to Volovya.", "그들은 이내 역에 닿아 말을 갈아매고 볼로뱌를 향해 내달렸다."], ["“Why is it worth while speaking to a clever man?", "'어째서 영리한 사람한테는 말을 붙여 볼 만하다는 거지?"], ["What did he mean by that?”", "그자가 그 말로 무슨 뜻을 비친 걸까?'"], ["The thought seemed suddenly to clutch at his breathing.", "그 생각이 별안간 그의 숨통을 옥죄어 오는 듯했다."], ["“And why did I tell him I was going to Tchermashnya?”", "'그리고 나는 왜 그자한테 체르마시냐로 간다고 말했을까?'"], ["They reached Volovya station.", "그들은 볼로뱌 역에 닿았다."], ["Ivan got out of the carriage, and the drivers stood round him bargaining over the journey of twelve versts to Tchermashnya.", "이반은 마차에서 내렸고, 마부들이 그를 둘러싸고 체르마시냐까지 십이 베르스타 길의 삯을 두고 흥정을 벌였다."], ["He told them to harness the horses.", "그는 말을 매라고 일렀다."], ["He went into the station house, looked round, glanced at the overseer’s wife, and suddenly went back to the entrance.", "그는 역사로 들어가 둘레를 살펴보고, 역장의 아내를 흘끗 보더니, 별안간 입구로 되돌아 나왔다."]], [["“I won’t go to Tchermashnya.", "\"체르마시냐엔 안 가겠소."], ["Am I too late to reach the railway by seven, brothers?”", "이보게들, 일곱 시까지 철도에 닿기엔 너무 늦었나?\""]], [["“We shall just do it.", "\"꼭 맞춰 갈 수 있습니다."], ["Shall we get the carriage out?”", "마차를 끌어낼깝쇼?\""]], [["“At once.", "\"당장."], ["Will any one of you be going to the town to-morrow?”", "자네들 중에 내일 읍내로 갈 사람 있나?\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"있고말고요."], ["Mitri here will.”", "여기 미트리가 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["“Can you do me a service, Mitri?", "\"미트리, 내 부탁 하나 들어주겠나?"], ["Go to my father’s, to Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, and tell him I haven’t gone to Tchermashnya.", "우리 아버지, 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 댁에 가서, 내가 체르마시냐엔 가지 않았다고 전해 주게."], ["Can you?”", "해 주겠나?\""]], [["“Of course I can.", "\"여부가 있겠습니까."], ["I’ve known Fyodor Pavlovitch a long time.”", "표도르 파블로비치는 오래전부터 알고 지냈는걸요.\""]], [["“And here’s something for you, for I dare say he won’t give you anything,”", "\"그리고 이건 자네한테 주는 걸세, 아무래도 그 양반은 자네한테 한 푼도 안 줄 것 같으니 말이야.\""], ["said Ivan, laughing gayly.", "이반이 유쾌하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“You may depend on it he won’t.”", "\"그야 틀림없이 안 주실 겁니다.\""], ["Mitya laughed too.", "미탸도 웃었다."], ["“Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리."], ["I’ll be sure to do it.”", "꼭 전해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["At seven o’clock Ivan got into the train and set off to Moscow “Away with the past.", "일곱 시에 이반은 기차에 올라 모스크바로 떠났다. '지나간 것이여, 사라져라."], ["I’ve done with the old world for ever, and may I have no news, no echo, from it.", "나는 옛 세계와 영영 끝장을 보았으니, 거기서 그 어떤 소식도, 그 어떤 메아리도 들려오지 않기를."], ["To a new life, new places and no looking back!”", "새로운 삶으로, 새로운 곳으로, 뒤돌아봄 없이!'"], ["But instead of delight his soul was filled with such gloom, and his heart ached with such anguish, as he had never known in his life before.", "그러나 환희 대신 그의 영혼은 일찍이 평생 겪어 본 적 없는 그런 어둠으로 가득 찼고, 그의 가슴은 그런 비통으로 쓰라렸다."], ["He was thinking all the night.", "그는 밤새도록 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The train flew on, and only at daybreak, when he was approaching Moscow, he suddenly roused himself from his meditation.", "기차는 날 듯이 달렸고, 동틀 녘 모스크바에 가까워질 무렵에야 그는 별안간 명상에서 깨어났다."]], [["“I am a scoundrel,”", "\"나는 비열한 놈이다.\""], ["he whispered to himself.", "그는 혼잣말로 속삭였다."]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch remained well satisfied at having seen his son off.", "표도르 파블로비치는 아들을 떠나보내고 나서 흡족해했다."], ["For two hours afterwards he felt almost happy, and sat drinking brandy.", "그 뒤 두 시간 동안 그는 거의 행복하다시피 한 기분으로 앉아 브랜디를 마셨다."], ["But suddenly something happened which was very annoying and unpleasant for every one in the house, and completely upset Fyodor Pavlovitch’s equanimity at once.", "그러나 별안간 집 안의 모두에게 몹시 성가시고 언짢은 일이 벌어졌으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 평정이 단번에 완전히 무너지고 말았다."], ["Smerdyakov went to the cellar for something and fell down from the top of the steps.", "스메르댜코프가 무언가를 가지러 지하실에 내려갔다가 계단 꼭대기에서 굴러떨어진 것이다."], ["Fortunately, Marfa Ignatyevna was in the yard and heard him in time.", "다행히 마르파 이그나티예브나가 마당에 있다가 때맞춰 그 소리를 들었다."], ["She did not see the fall, but heard his scream—the strange, peculiar scream, long familiar to her—the scream of the epileptic falling in a fit.", "그녀는 떨어지는 것은 못 보았으나 그의 비명을—그 기이하고 독특한, 그녀에게 오래도록 익숙한 비명을—발작으로 쓰러지는 간질병자의 비명을 들었다."], ["They could not tell whether the fit had come on him at the moment he was descending the steps, so that he must have fallen unconscious, or whether it was the fall and the shock that had caused the fit in Smerdyakov, who was known to be liable to them.", "발작이 계단을 내려가던 순간에 덮쳐 그가 정신을 잃고 굴러떨어진 것인지, 아니면 굴러떨어진 충격이 발작을 자아낸 것인지는 아무도 알 수 없었으니, 스메르댜코프가 평소 발작을 일으키기 쉬운 자라는 것은 익히 알려진 터였다."], ["They found him at the bottom of the cellar steps, writhing in convulsions and foaming at the mouth.", "그들은 그가 지하실 계단 밑바닥에서 경련으로 몸부림치며 입에 거품을 무는 것을 발견했다."], ["It was thought at first that he must have broken something—an arm or a leg—and hurt himself, but “God had preserved him,”", "처음에는 어딘가—팔이나 다리를—부러뜨리고 다친 게 분명하리라 여겼으나, 마르파 이그나티예브나의 말마따나 \"하느님이 그를 지켜 주셔서\""], ["as Marfa Ignatyevna expressed it—nothing of the kind had happened.", "그런 일은 전혀 일어나지 않았다."], ["But it was difficult to get him out of the cellar.", "그러나 그를 지하실에서 끌어내기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["They asked the neighbors to help and managed it somehow.", "그들은 이웃에게 도움을 청해 어떻게든 일을 해냈다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch himself was present at the whole ceremony.", "표도르 파블로비치 자신도 이 야단법석에 시종 자리를 지켰다."], ["He helped, evidently alarmed and upset.", "그는 거들었으니, 보아하니 놀라고 심란한 기색이었다."], ["The sick man did not regain consciousness; the convulsions ceased for a time, but then began again, and every one concluded that the same thing would happen, as had happened a year before, when he accidentally fell from the garret.", "병자는 정신을 차리지 못했고, 경련은 한동안 멎었다가 다시 시작되었으니, 모두가 일 년 전 그가 어쩌다 다락에서 떨어졌을 때와 똑같은 일이 벌어지리라 단정했다."], ["They remembered that ice had been put on his head then.", "그들은 그때 그의 머리에 얼음을 얹었던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["There was still ice in the cellar, and Marfa Ignatyevna had some brought up.", "지하실에는 아직 얼음이 남아 있어, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 얼마쯤 가져오게 했다."], ["In the evening, Fyodor Pavlovitch sent for Doctor Herzenstube, who arrived at once.", "저녁에 표도르 파블로비치는 헤르첸슈투베 의사를 부르러 보냈고, 의사는 즉시 도착했다."], ["He was a most estimable old man, and the most careful and conscientious doctor in the province.", "그는 더없이 존경받을 만한 노인으로, 이 지방에서 가장 세심하고 양심적인 의사였다."], ["After careful examination, he concluded that the fit was a very violent one and might have serious consequences; that meanwhile he, Herzenstube, did not fully understand it, but that by to-morrow morning, if the present remedies were unavailing, he would venture to try something else.", "그는 면밀히 진찰한 끝에, 발작이 매우 격심한 것이어서 중대한 결과를 빚을 수도 있다는 것, 다만 자기, 곧 헤르첸슈투베로서도 아직 그것을 온전히 이해하지는 못한다는 것, 그러나 내일 아침까지 지금 쓰는 처방이 듣지 않으면 다른 것을 한번 시도해 보겠다는 것을 결론지었다."], ["The invalid was taken to the lodge, to a room next to Grigory’s and Marfa Ignatyevna’s.", "병자는 별채로, 그리고리와 마르파 이그나티예브나의 방 옆방으로 옮겨졌다."]], [["Then Fyodor Pavlovitch had one misfortune after another to put up with that day.", "그러고 나서 표도르 파블로비치는 그날 하나둘 잇따른 불운을 견뎌야 했다."], ["Marfa Ignatyevna cooked the dinner, and the soup, compared with Smerdyakov’s, was “no better than dish-water,” and the fowl was so dried up that it was impossible to masticate it.", "마르파 이그나티예브나가 저녁을 지었는데, 그 수프는 스메르댜코프 것에 비하면 \"설거지 물이나 진배없었고\", 닭고기는 어찌나 바싹 말랐던지 도무지 씹을 수가 없었다."], ["To her master’s bitter, though deserved, reproaches, Marfa Ignatyevna replied that the fowl was a very old one to begin with, and that she had never been trained as a cook.", "주인의 쓰라린, 그래도 받아 마땅한 책망에, 마르파 이그나티예브나는 그 닭이 애당초 아주 늙은 놈이었던 데다 자기는 요리사로 배운 적도 없노라고 대꾸했다."], ["In the evening there was another trouble in store for Fyodor Pavlovitch; he was informed that Grigory, who had not been well for the last three days, was completely laid up by his lumbago.", "저녁에는 표도르 파블로비치에게 또 다른 골칫거리가 기다리고 있었으니, 지난 사흘간 몸이 성치 않던 그리고리가 요통으로 아주 드러눕고 말았다는 전갈이 온 것이다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch finished his tea as early as possible and locked himself up alone in the house.", "표도르 파블로비치는 되도록 일찍 차를 마저 마시고는 집 안에 홀로 틀어박혔다."], ["He was in terrible excitement and suspense.", "그는 끔찍한 흥분과 초조에 휩싸여 있었다."], ["That evening he reckoned on Grushenka’s coming almost as a certainty.", "그날 저녁 그는 그루셴카가 오리라는 것을 거의 확실한 일로 점치고 있었다."], ["He had received from Smerdyakov that morning an assurance “that she had promised to come without fail.”", "그날 아침 그는 스메르댜코프에게서 \"그 여자가 틀림없이 오겠다고 약속했다\"는 다짐을 받아 둔 터였다."], ["The incorrigible old man’s heart throbbed with excitement; he paced up and down his empty rooms listening.", "이 구제 불능의 노인은 흥분으로 심장이 두근거렸다. 그는 텅 빈 방들을 오르내리며 귀를 기울였다."], ["He had to be on the alert.", "잔뜩 경계 태세를 갖추어야 했다."], ["Dmitri might be on the watch for her somewhere, and when she knocked on the window (Smerdyakov had informed him two days before that he had told her where and how to knock) the door must be opened at once.", "드미트리가 어딘가에서 그 여자를 노리고 지키고 있을지도 모르니, 그녀가 창을 두드리면 (스메르댜코프가 이틀 전에 어디를 어떻게 두드리라고 일러 두었다고 알려 온 터였다) 즉시 문을 열어 주어야 했다. 그녀가 통로에 한순간이라도 머물러서는 안 되었으니, 행여—그런 일이 있어서는 안 되겠지만!—그녀가 겁을 먹고 달아날까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["She must not be a second in the passage, for fear—which God forbid!—that she should be frightened and run away.", "표도르 파블로비치는 궁리할 것이 많았으나, 그의 심장이 이토록 음탕한 희망에 흠뻑 젖어 본 적은 일찍이 없었다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch had much to think of, but never had his heart been steeped in such voluptuous hopes.", "이번에야말로 그는 그녀가 오리라고 거의 확실하게 말할 수 있었던 것이다!"], ["This time he could say almost certainly that she would come!", "* 베르스타: 옛 러시아의 거리 단위로, 약 1.067킬로미터에 해당한다."]], [["# Book VI. The Russian Monk Chapter I. Father Zossima And His Visitors", "# Book VI. The Russian Monk Chapter I. Father Zossima And His Visitors"]], [["When with an anxious and aching heart Alyosha went into his elder’s cell, he stood still almost astonished.", "불안과 아릿한 아픔을 안은 채 알료샤가 장로의 암자로 들어섰을 때, 그는 거의 놀라움에 사로잡혀 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Instead of a sick man at his last gasp, perhaps unconscious, as he had feared to find him, he saw him sitting up in his chair and, though weak and exhausted, his face was bright and cheerful, he was surrounded by visitors and engaged in a quiet and joyful conversation.", "마지막 숨을 헐떡이며, 어쩌면 정신마저 잃었으리라 두려워했던 병자 대신, 그는 장로가 의자에 일어나 앉아 있는 모습을 보았는데, 비록 쇠약하고 기진해 있었으나 그 얼굴은 밝고 명랑했으며, 문병객들에 둘러싸여 조용하고도 기쁨에 찬 대화를 나누고 있었다."], ["But he had only got up from his bed a quarter of an hour before Alyosha’s arrival; his visitors had gathered together in his cell earlier, waiting for him to wake, having received a most confident assurance from Father Païssy that “the teacher would get up, and as he had himself promised in the morning, converse once more with those dear to his heart.”", "그러나 그가 자리에서 일어난 것은 알료샤가 도착하기 겨우 십오 분 전이었다. 문병객들은 그보다 일찍 암자에 모여들어 그가 깨어나기를 기다리고 있었으니, 파이시 신부에게서 \"스승께서는 일어나실 것이며, 아침에 친히 약속하신 대로 마음으로 아끼는 이들과 다시 한 번 이야기를 나누실 것\"이라는 더없이 확고한 보증을 받았던 것이다."], ["This promise and indeed every word of the dying elder Father Païssy put implicit trust in.", "이 약속을, 아니 죽어 가는 장로의 한마디 한마디를 파이시 신부는 절대적으로 믿었다."], ["If he had seen him unconscious, if he had seen him breathe his last, and yet had his promise that he would rise up and say good-by to him, he would not have believed perhaps even in death, but would still have expected the dead man to recover and fulfill his promise.", "설령 그가 의식을 잃은 장로를 보았더라도, 마지막 숨을 거두는 모습을 목격했더라도, 그러면서도 장로에게서 다시 일어나 작별을 고하겠노라는 약속을 받았더라면, 그는 어쩌면 죽음조차 믿지 않고 도리어 죽은 이가 되살아나 그 약속을 지키리라 기대했을 것이다."], ["In the morning as he lay down to sleep, Father Zossima had told him positively: “I shall not die without the delight of another conversation with you, beloved of my heart.", "아침에 잠자리에 들면서 조시마 장로는 그에게 분명히 일러두었다. \"내 마음의 사랑하는 이여, 나는 그대와 한 번 더 이야기를 나누는 기쁨 없이는 죽지 않을 것이오."], ["I shall look once more on your dear face and pour out my heart to you once again.”", "그대의 그리운 얼굴을 한 번 더 바라보고, 그대에게 또 한 번 내 마음을 쏟아 놓겠소.\""], ["The monks, who had gathered for this probably last conversation with Father Zossima, had all been his devoted friends for many years.", "조시마 장로와 아마도 마지막이 될 이 대화를 위해 모여든 수도사들은 모두 여러 해 동안 그의 헌신적인 벗이었다."], ["There were four of them: Father Iosif and Father Païssy, Father Mihaïl, the warden of the hermitage, a man not very old and far from being learned.", "그들은 모두 넷이었으니, 이오시프 신부와 파이시 신부, 그리고 암자의 관리인인 미하일 신부였는데, 미하일 신부는 그리 늙지도 않았고 학식과도 거리가 먼 사람이었다."], ["He was of humble origin, of strong will and steadfast faith, of austere appearance, but of deep tenderness, though he obviously concealed it as though he were almost ashamed of it.", "그는 미천한 가문에서 태어나 의지가 굳고 신앙이 흔들림 없었으며, 겉모습은 준엄했으나 속으로는 깊은 다정함을 품고 있었는데, 다만 그것이 거의 부끄럽기라도 한 듯 분명히 감추려 들었다."], ["The fourth, Father Anfim, was a very old and humble little monk of the poorest peasant class.", "넷째인 안핌 신부는 더없이 가난한 농민 출신의, 아주 늙고 보잘것없는 작은 수도사였다."], ["He was almost illiterate, and very quiet, scarcely speaking to any one.", "그는 거의 글을 읽지 못했고, 무척 말이 없어 누구와도 좀처럼 이야기를 나누지 않았다."], ["He was the humblest of the humble, and looked as though he had been frightened by something great and awful beyond the scope of his intelligence.", "그는 겸손한 이들 가운데서도 가장 겸손했으며, 자기 지력의 한계를 넘어서는 무언가 위대하고 두려운 것에 겁먹은 듯한 모습이었다."], ["Father Zossima had a great affection for this timorous man, and always treated him with marked respect, though perhaps there was no one he had known to whom he had said less, in spite of the fact that he had spent years wandering about holy Russia with him.", "조시마 장로는 이 소심한 사람에게 깊은 애정을 품고 언제나 각별한 존경으로 대했는데, 비록 그와 함께 여러 해 동안 거룩한 러시아 땅을 떠돌아다녔음에도, 어쩌면 그가 알던 누구에게보다도 이 사람에게 가장 적은 말을 건넸을 것이다."], ["That was very long ago, forty years before, when Father Zossima first began his life as a monk in a poor and little monastery at Kostroma, and when, shortly after, he had accompanied Father Anfim on his pilgrimage to collect alms for their poor monastery.", "그것은 아주 오래전, 사십 년 전의 일이었으니, 조시마 장로가 코스트로마의 가난하고 작은 수도원에서 수도사로서의 삶을 처음 시작했을 무렵, 그리고 그 직후 가난한 수도원을 위해 희사금을 모으러 떠나는 안핌 신부의 순례길에 동행했을 무렵의 일이었다."]], [["The whole party were in the bedroom which, as we mentioned before, was very small, so that there was scarcely room for the four of them (in addition to Porfiry, the novice, who stood) to sit round Father Zossima on chairs brought from the sitting-room.", "일행 모두는 침실에 있었는데, 앞서 말한 대로 침실은 무척 비좁아 거실에서 가져온 의자에 앉은 네 사람이(거기에 서 있는 견습 수도사 포르피리까지 더하면) 조시마 장로를 둘러싸기에도 자리가 모자랄 지경이었다."], ["It was already beginning to get dark, the room was lighted up by the lamps and the candles before the ikons.", "이미 날이 어둑해지기 시작했고, 방 안은 등불과 성상 앞의 촛불로 밝혀져 있었다."]], [["Seeing Alyosha standing embarrassed in the doorway, Father Zossima smiled at him joyfully and held out his hand.", "알료샤가 어쩔 줄 몰라 문간에 서 있는 것을 보고, 조시마 장로는 기쁜 듯 미소 지으며 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Welcome, my quiet one, welcome, my dear, here you are too.", "\"어서 오너라, 나의 조용한 아이야, 어서 오너라, 사랑하는 이여, 너도 왔구나."], ["I knew you would come.”", "네가 올 줄 알았다.\""]], [["Alyosha went up to him, bowed down before him to the ground and wept.", "알료샤가 그에게 다가가 땅에 닿도록 절을 올리고 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Something surged up from his heart, his soul was quivering, he wanted to sob.", "무언가가 가슴에서 북받쳐 올랐고, 영혼이 떨렸으며, 흐느껴 울고 싶었다."]], [["“Come, don’t weep over me yet,”", "\"자, 아직은 나를 두고 울지 마라.\""], ["Father Zossima smiled, laying his right hand on his head.", "조시마 장로가 오른손을 그의 머리에 얹으며 미소 지었다. \"보다시피 나는 일어나 앉아 이야기를 하고 있지 않느냐."], ["“You see I am sitting up talking; maybe I shall live another twenty years yet, as that dear good woman from Vishegorye, with her little Lizaveta in her arms, wished me yesterday.", "어쩌면 나는 앞으로 이십 년은 더 살지도 모른다. 어제 비셰고리예에서 온 그 착하고 마음씨 고운 여인이, 어린 리자베타를 품에 안고 내게 빌어 준 그대로 말이다."], ["God bless the mother and the little girl Lizaveta,”", "하느님께서 그 어미와 어린 딸 리자베타에게 복을 내리시기를.\""], ["he crossed himself.", "그는 성호를 그었다."], ["“Porfiry, did you take her offering where I told you?”", "\"포르피리야, 그 여인의 헌금은 내가 일러둔 곳에 가져다주었느냐?\""]], [["He meant the sixty copecks brought him the day before by the good-humored woman to be given “to some one poorer than me.”", "그가 말한 것은, 전날 그 마음씨 좋은 여인이 \"나보다 더 가난한 누군가에게\" 주라며 가져온 육십 코페이카였다."], ["Such offerings, always of money gained by personal toil, are made by way of penance voluntarily undertaken.", "이런 헌금은 언제나 손수 땀 흘려 번 돈으로, 스스로 자청한 속죄의 한 방편으로 바쳐지는 것이었다."], ["The elder had sent Porfiry the evening before to a widow, whose house had been burnt down lately, and who after the fire had gone with her children begging alms.", "장로는 전날 저녁 포르피리를 한 과부에게 보냈는데, 그 과부는 얼마 전 집이 불타 버린 뒤 아이들을 데리고 동냥을 다니던 처지였다."], ["Porfiry hastened to reply that he had given the money, as he had been instructed, “from an unknown benefactress.”", "포르피리는 일러 준 대로 \"어느 알 수 없는 자선가가 보냈다\"며 돈을 전했노라고 서둘러 대답했다."]], [["“Get up, my dear boy,”", "\"일어나거라, 사랑하는 아이야.\""], ["the elder went on to Alyosha.", "장로가 알료샤에게 말을 이었다."], ["“Let me look at you.", "\"너를 좀 보자꾸나."], ["Have you been home and seen your brother?”", "집에 다녀와 네 형을 만나 보았느냐?\""], ["It seemed strange to Alyosha that he asked so confidently and precisely, about one of his brothers only—but which one?", "그가 형들 가운데 오직 한 사람에 대해서만 그토록 확신에 차고 정확하게 묻는 것이 알료샤에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["Then perhaps he had sent him out both yesterday and to-day for the sake of that brother.", "그런데 그 형이 누구란 말인가? 그렇다면 어쩌면 그는 어제도 오늘도 바로 그 형을 위해 자기를 내보낸 것이리라."]], [["“I have seen one of my brothers,”", "\"형 하나를 만났습니다.\""], ["answered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 대답했다."]], [["“I mean the elder one, to whom I bowed down.”", "\"내가 절을 올린 그 맏형 말이다.\""]], [["“I only saw him yesterday and could not find him to-day,”", "\"어제는 만났지만 오늘은 찾을 수가 없었습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Make haste to find him, go again to-morrow and make haste, leave everything and make haste.", "\"서둘러 그를 찾거라. 내일 다시 가서 서둘러라."], ["Perhaps you may still have time to prevent something terrible.", "모든 일을 제쳐 두고 서둘러라. 어쩌면 너는 아직 무언가 끔찍한 일을 막을 시간이 있을지도 모른다."], ["I bowed down yesterday to the great suffering in store for him.”", "나는 어제 그에게 닥쳐올 크나큰 고난 앞에 절을 올렸느니라.\""]], [["He was suddenly silent and seemed to be pondering.", "그는 갑자기 입을 다물고 무언가를 곰곰이 헤아리는 듯했다."], ["The words were strange.", "그 말은 이상했다."], ["Father Iosif, who had witnessed the scene yesterday, exchanged glances with Father Païssy.", "어제 그 장면을 목격했던 이오시프 신부가 파이시 신부와 눈길을 주고받았다."], ["Alyosha could not resist asking:", "알료샤는 묻지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Father and teacher,”", "\"신부님, 스승님.\""], ["he began with extreme emotion, “your words are too obscure....", "그는 북받치는 격정을 안고 입을 열었다. \"말씀이 너무 모호하옵니다……."], ["What is this suffering in store for him?”", "형에게 닥쳐올 그 고난이란 무엇이옵니까?\""]], [["“Don’t inquire.", "\"묻지 마라."], ["I seemed to see something terrible yesterday ...", "나는 어제 무언가 끔찍한 것을 본 듯하구나……."], ["as though his whole future were expressed in his eyes.", "마치 그의 온 미래가 그의 두 눈에 드러나 있는 것 같았다."], ["A look came into his eyes—so that I was instantly horror-stricken at what that man is preparing for himself.", "그의 눈빛에 어떤 기색이 어렸는데—그 사람이 스스로를 위해 무엇을 준비하고 있는지를 보고 나는 순식간에 공포에 사로잡혔다."], ["Once or twice in my life I’ve seen such a look in a man’s face ...", "내 평생에 한두 번, 어느 사람의 얼굴에서 그런 눈빛을 본 적이 있었으니……."], ["reflecting as it were his future fate, and that fate, alas, came to pass.", "말하자면 그 사람의 미래의 운명을 비추는 눈빛이었고, 슬프게도 그 운명은 그대로 이루어졌다."], ["I sent you to him, Alexey, for I thought your brotherly face would help him.", "나는 너를 그에게 보냈다, 알렉세이야, 네 형제다운 얼굴이 그를 도와주리라 여겼기 때문이다."], ["But everything and all our fates are from the Lord.", "그러나 모든 것이, 우리 모두의 운명이 주님께로부터 오는 것이다."], ["‘Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone; but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit.’ Remember that.", "'한 알의 밀이 땅에 떨어져 죽지 아니하면 한 알 그대로 있고, 죽으면 많은 열매를 맺느니라.' 그것을 명심하거라."], ["You, Alexey, I’ve many times silently blessed for your face, know that,”", "알렉세이야, 나는 네 얼굴 때문에 여러 번 묵묵히 너를 축복했으니, 그것을 알아 두거라.\""], ["added the elder with a gentle smile.", "장로가 부드러운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."], ["“This is what I think of you, you will go forth from these walls, but will live like a monk in the world.", "\"이것이 너에 대한 나의 생각이다. 너는 이 담장 밖으로 나가게 되겠지만, 세상 속에서 수도사처럼 살게 될 것이다."], ["You will have many enemies, but even your foes will love you.", "너에게는 적이 많겠으나, 네 원수들조차 너를 사랑할 것이다."], ["Life will bring you many misfortunes, but you will find your happiness in them, and will bless life and will make others bless it—which is what matters most.", "삶은 너에게 많은 불행을 가져다주겠으나, 너는 바로 그 안에서 너의 행복을 찾을 것이며, 삶을 축복하고 또 남들로 하여금 삶을 축복하게 만들 것이니—그것이야말로 무엇보다 중요한 일이다."], ["Well, that is your character.", "자, 이것이 너의 천성이다."], ["Fathers and teachers,”", "신부들이여, 스승들이여.\""], ["he addressed his friends with a tender smile, “I have never till to-day told even him why the face of this youth is so dear to me.", "그는 다정한 미소를 띠며 벗들에게 말을 건넸다. \"나는 오늘에 이르도록 이 젊은이의 얼굴이 어찌하여 내게 그토록 소중한지 이 아이에게조차 한 번도 말한 적이 없소."], ["Now I will tell you.", "이제 그대들에게 말하겠소."], ["His face has been as it were a remembrance and a prophecy for me.", "그의 얼굴은 내게 말하자면 하나의 추억이자 하나의 예언이었소."], ["At the dawn of my life when I was a child I had an elder brother who died before my eyes at seventeen.", "내 삶의 여명기, 내가 아이였을 때 내게는 열일곱 나이에 내 눈앞에서 죽은 형이 있었소."], ["And later on in the course of my life I gradually became convinced that that brother had been for a guidance and a sign from on high for me.", "그리고 훗날 살아가는 동안 나는 차츰 확신하게 되었으니, 그 형이 나를 위해 높은 곳에서 내려온 인도이자 표징이었다는 것이오."], ["For had he not come into my life, I should never perhaps, so I fancy at least, have become a monk and entered on this precious path.", "만일 그가 내 삶에 나타나지 않았더라면, 적어도 내 짐작으로는, 나는 아마 결코 수도사가 되어 이 귀한 길에 들어서지 못했을 것이오."], ["He appeared first to me in my childhood, and here, at the end of my pilgrimage, he seems to have come to me over again.", "그는 내 어린 시절에 처음 내게 나타났는데, 이제 내 순례의 끝자락에 이르러 다시금 내게 찾아온 듯하오."], ["It is marvelous, fathers and teachers, that Alexey, who has some, though not a great, resemblance in face, seems to me so like him spiritually, that many times I have taken him for that young man, my brother, mysteriously come back to me at the end of my pilgrimage, as a reminder and an inspiration.", "신부들이여, 스승들이여, 참으로 놀라운 일은, 얼굴이 그다지 닮은 것도 아닌 이 알렉세이가 내게는 영적으로 그토록 그를 닮은 듯이 여겨져, 나는 여러 번 이 아이를 그 젊은이, 곧 내 순례의 끝자락에 하나의 일깨움이자 영감으로서 신비로이 내게 되돌아온 내 형으로 착각했다는 것이오."], ["So that I positively wondered at so strange a dream in myself.", "그리하여 나는 내 안의 이토록 이상한 꿈에 정녕 놀라곤 했소."], ["Do you hear this, Porfiry?”", "포르피리야, 이 말을 듣고 있느냐?\""], ["he turned to the novice who waited on him.", "그는 자기 시중을 드는 견습 수도사에게 몸을 돌렸다."], ["“Many times I’ve seen in your face as it were a look of mortification that I love Alexey more than you.", "\"여러 번 나는 네 얼굴에서, 내가 너보다 알렉세이를 더 사랑한다는 데 대한 서운함 같은 기색을 보았다."], ["Now you know why that was so, but I love you too, know that, and many times I grieved at your mortification.", "이제 너는 그것이 어찌하여 그러했는지 알았을 것이다. 그러나 나는 너 또한 사랑한다, 그것을 알아 두거라, 그리고 여러 번 나는 너의 서운함에 마음 아파했다."], ["I should like to tell you, dear friends, of that youth, my brother, for there has been no presence in my life more precious, more significant and touching.", "사랑하는 벗들이여, 나는 그대들에게 그 젊은이, 내 형에 대해 이야기하고 싶소, 내 삶에서 그보다 더 소중하고, 더 뜻깊고, 더 가슴 저미는 존재는 없었기 때문이오."], ["My heart is full of tenderness, and I look at my whole life at this moment as though living through it again.”", "내 마음은 다정함으로 가득하고, 나는 지금 이 순간 내 온 생애를 마치 다시 한 번 살아 내는 것처럼 바라보고 있소.\""]], [["Here I must observe that this last conversation of Father Zossima with the friends who visited him on the last day of his life has been partly preserved in writing.", "여기서 나는 이 점을 짚어 두어야겠다. 조시마 장로가 생애 마지막 날 자기를 찾아온 벗들과 나눈 이 마지막 대화는 일부가 글로 남아 보존되어 있다."], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov wrote it down from memory, some time after his elder’s death.", "알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 스승이 죽은 뒤 얼마간 지나 기억을 더듬어 적어 둔 것이다."], ["But whether this was only the conversation that took place then, or whether he added to it his notes of parts of former conversations with his teacher, I cannot determine.", "그러나 이것이 그때 오간 대화만을 옮긴 것인지, 아니면 그가 스승과 나눈 예전 대화들의 일부에 대한 자기 기록을 거기에 보탠 것인지, 나로서는 단정할 수 없다."], ["In his account, Father Zossima’s talk goes on without interruption, as though he told his life to his friends in the form of a story, though there is no doubt, from other accounts of it, that the conversation that evening was general.", "그의 기록에서는 조시마 장로의 이야기가 끊김 없이 이어져, 마치 그가 자기 생애를 한 편의 이야기 형식으로 벗들에게 들려준 것처럼 되어 있으나, 다른 증언들로 미루어 보건대 그날 저녁의 대화가 여럿이 함께 나눈 것이었음에는 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["Though the guests did not interrupt Father Zossima much, yet they too talked, perhaps even told something themselves.", "손님들이 조시마 장로의 말을 자주 끊지는 않았다 해도, 그들 역시 이야기를 했고 어쩌면 스스로 무언가를 들려주기까지 했을 것이다."], ["Besides, Father Zossima could not have carried on an uninterrupted narrative, for he was sometimes gasping for breath, his voice failed him, and he even lay down to rest on his bed, though he did not fall asleep and his visitors did not leave their seats.", "게다가 조시마 장로가 끊김 없는 이야기를 이어 갈 수는 없었을 것이니, 그는 때로 숨을 헐떡였고 목소리가 잦아들었으며, 심지어 침상에 누워 쉬기까지 했기 때문이다. 다만 잠이 든 것은 아니었고 문병객들도 자리를 떠나지 않았다."], ["Once or twice the conversation was interrupted by Father Païssy’s reading the Gospel.", "한두 번은 파이시 신부가 복음서를 읽음으로써 대화가 중단되었다."], ["It is worthy of note, too, that no one of them supposed that he would die that night, for on that evening of his life after his deep sleep in the day he seemed suddenly to have found new strength, which kept him up through this long conversation.", "또한 주목할 만한 점은, 그들 가운데 누구도 그가 그날 밤에 죽으리라고는 생각하지 않았다는 것이니, 그의 생애의 그 저녁, 낮에 깊이 잠든 뒤 그는 돌연 새로운 기력을 얻은 듯했고, 그 기력이 이 긴 대화 내내 그를 지탱해 주었기 때문이다. 그것은 마치 그에게 놀라운 활력을 안겨 준 사랑의 마지막 안간힘과도 같았다."], ["It was like a last effort of love which gave him marvelous energy; only for a little time, however, for his life was cut short immediately....", "그러나 그것은 잠시뿐이었으니, 그의 생명은 곧바로 끊기고 말았던 것이다……. 그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하자."], ["But of that later. I will only add now that I have preferred to confine myself to the account given by Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov.", "지금은 다만, 내가 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 남긴 기록에만 의지하기로 했다는 것만 덧붙여 두겠다."], ["It will be shorter and not so fatiguing, though of course, as I must repeat, Alyosha took a great deal from previous conversations and added them to it.", "그편이 더 짧고 또 그리 지루하지 않을 것이다. 물론 거듭 말하지만, 알료샤가 예전 대화들에서 적잖은 것을 가져와 거기에 보탰음은 분명하지만 말이다."]], [["Notes of the Life of the deceased Priest and Monk, the Elder Zossima, taken from his own words by Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov.", "고인이 된 사제이자 수도사인 조시마 장로의 생애에 대한 기록, 그 자신의 말에서 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프가 받아 적다."]], [["(a) Father Zossima’s Brother", "전기적 기록\n(가) 조시마 장로의 형"]], [["Beloved fathers and teachers, I was born in a distant province in the north, in the town of V.", "사랑하는 신부들이여, 스승들이여, 나는 북쪽 어느 먼 지방의 도시 V에서 태어났소."], ["My father was a gentleman by birth, but of no great consequence or position.", "내 아버지는 가문으로는 귀족이었으나 그리 대단하거나 높은 지위에 있지는 않았소."], ["He died when I was only two years old, and I don’t remember him at all.", "그는 내가 겨우 두 살 때 세상을 떠나서, 나는 그를 전혀 기억하지 못하오."], ["He left my mother a small house built of wood, and a fortune, not large, but sufficient to keep her and her children in comfort.", "그는 내 어머니에게 나무로 지은 작은 집 한 채와, 크지는 않으나 어머니와 자식들이 그럭저럭 편히 지내기에는 넉넉한 재산을 남겨 주었소."], ["There were two of us, my elder brother Markel and I.", "우리는 둘이었으니, 나보다 여덟 살 위인 형 마르켈과 나였소."], ["He was eight years older than I was, of hasty irritable temperament, but kind-hearted and never ironical.", "형은 성미가 급하고 곧잘 짜증을 냈으나 마음씨는 따뜻했고 결코 빈정대는 법이 없었소."], ["He was remarkably silent, especially at home with me, his mother, and the servants.", "그는 놀라우리만치 말이 없었는데, 특히 집에서 나와 어머니와 하인들 앞에서 그러했소."], ["He did well at school, but did not get on with his schoolfellows, though he never quarreled, at least so my mother has told me.", "학교에서는 성적이 좋았으나 동무들과는 잘 어울리지 못했소. 그래도 다투는 일은 없었다고, 적어도 어머니가 내게 그렇게 일러 주었소."], ["Six months before his death, when he was seventeen, he made friends with a political exile who had been banished from Moscow to our town for freethinking, and led a solitary existence there.", "죽기 여섯 달 전, 열일곱 살이던 그는 자유사상 때문에 모스크바에서 우리 도시로 유배되어 그곳에서 외톨이로 지내던 한 정치적 유배자와 가까워졌소."], ["He was a good scholar who had gained distinction in philosophy in the university.", "그는 대학에서 철학으로 두각을 나타낸 훌륭한 학자였소."], ["Something made him take a fancy to Markel, and he used to ask him to see him.", "무슨 까닭에선지 그가 마르켈을 마음에 들어 해서, 형에게 자기를 보러 오라고 청하곤 했소."], ["The young man would spend whole evenings with him during that winter, till the exile was summoned to Petersburg to take up his post again at his own request, as he had powerful friends.", "형은 그해 겨울 내내 저녁마다 그와 함께 시간을 보냈는데, 마침내 그 유배자가 권세 있는 벗들을 둔 덕에 자기 청원으로 페테르부르크에 불려 가 다시 관직에 오르게 될 때까지 그러했소."]], [["It was the beginning of Lent, and Markel would not fast, he was rude and laughed at it.", "마침 사순절의 시작이었는데, 마르켈은 금식을 하려 들지 않았소. 그는 무례하게 그것을 비웃었소."], ["“That’s all silly twaddle, and there is no God,”", "\"그딴 건 죄다 어리석은 헛소리야, 하느님 따위는 없어.\""], ["he said, horrifying my mother, the servants, and me too.", "그가 이렇게 말해 어머니와 하인들을, 그리고 나까지 소스라치게 했소."], ["For though I was only nine, I too was aghast at hearing such words.", "나는 겨우 아홉 살이었으나, 그런 말을 듣고 나 역시 아연실색했기 때문이오."], ["We had four servants, all serfs.", "우리 집에는 하인이 넷 있었는데, 모두 농노였소."], ["I remember my mother selling one of the four, the cook Afimya, who was lame and elderly, for sixty paper roubles, and hiring a free servant to take her place.", "나는 어머니가 그 넷 중 하나인, 다리를 절고 나이 든 요리사 아피미야를 종이돈 육십 루블에 팔고 그 자리에 자유로운 하인을 들이던 일을 기억하오."]], [["In the sixth week in Lent, my brother, who was never strong and had a tendency to consumption, was taken ill.", "사순절 여섯째 주에, 본래 몸이 튼튼하지 못하고 폐병 기가 있던 형이 병으로 자리에 누웠소."], ["He was tall but thin and delicate-looking, and of very pleasing countenance.", "그는 키가 컸으나 야위고 가냘퍼 보였으며, 더없이 호감 가는 얼굴이었소."], ["I suppose he caught cold, anyway the doctor, who came, soon whispered to my mother that it was galloping consumption, that he would not live through the spring.", "짐작건대 그는 감기에 걸렸던 듯한데, 어쨌든 왕진을 온 의사는 곧 어머니에게 그것이 급성 폐병이며 봄을 넘기지 못할 것이라고 속삭였소."], ["My mother began weeping, and, careful not to alarm my brother, she entreated him to go to church, to confess and take the sacrament, as he was still able to move about.", "어머니는 울기 시작했으나, 형이 놀랄까 조심하며 그에게 아직 몸을 움직일 수 있을 때 성당에 가서 고해하고 성체를 받으라고 간곡히 청했소."], ["This made him angry, and he said something profane about the church.", "이 말에 형은 화를 내며 성당에 대해 불경한 말을 내뱉었소."], ["He grew thoughtful, however; he guessed at once that he was seriously ill, and that that was why his mother was begging him to confess and take the sacrament.", "그러나 그는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼소. 그는 자기가 중병에 걸렸으며, 그래서 어머니가 고해하고 성체를 받으라 애원하는 것임을 단번에 알아차렸던 것이오."], ["He had been aware, indeed, for a long time past, that he was far from well, and had a year before coolly observed at dinner to our mother and me, “My life won’t be long among you, I may not live another year,”", "사실 그는 오래전부터 자기 몸이 영 좋지 못하다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 일 년 전에는 식탁에서 어머니와 내게 태연히 이런 말을 한 적이 있었소. \"내 목숨은 당신들 곁에 오래 머물지 못할 거예요, 한 해도 더 못 살지 모르죠.\""], ["which seemed now like a prophecy.", "그것이 이제는 하나의 예언처럼 여겨졌소."]], [["Three days passed and Holy Week had come.", "사흘이 지나 성주간이 다가왔소."], ["And on Tuesday morning my brother began going to church.", "그리고 화요일 아침, 형이 성당에 다니기 시작했소."], ["“I am doing this simply for your sake, mother, to please and comfort you,”", "\"이건 오로지 어머니를 위해서, 어머니를 기쁘게 하고 위로해 드리려고 하는 거예요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했소."], ["My mother wept with joy and grief.", "어머니는 기쁨과 슬픔으로 울었소."], ["“His end must be near,” she thought, “if there’s such a change in him.”", "'저 애에게 저런 변화가 일었다면, 그 끝이 가까운 게 틀림없어'"], ["But he was not able to go to church long, he took to his bed, so he had to confess and take the sacrament at home.", "하고 어머니는 생각했소. 그러나 그는 오래 성당에 다니지 못하고 자리에 눕고 말아서, 결국 집에서 고해하고 성체를 받아야 했소."]], [["It was a late Easter, and the days were bright, fine, and full of fragrance.", "그해는 부활절이 늦었는데, 날들은 밝고 화창했으며 향기로 가득했소."], ["I remember he used to cough all night and sleep badly, but in the morning he dressed and tried to sit up in an arm-chair.", "나는 형이 밤새 기침을 하며 잠을 설치곤 했던 일을 기억하오. 그러나 아침이면 그는 옷을 차려입고 안락의자에 일어나 앉으려 애썼소."], ["That’s how I remember him sitting, sweet and gentle, smiling, his face bright and joyous, in spite of his illness.", "내가 그를 기억하는 모습이 바로 그러하니, 그는 병중인데도 다정하고 부드럽게 미소 지으며, 얼굴은 밝고 기쁨에 차서 앉아 있었소."], ["A marvelous change passed over him, his spirit seemed transformed.", "그에게 놀라운 변화가 찾아왔으니, 그 정신이 마치 새로이 거듭난 듯했소."], ["The old nurse would come in and say, “Let me light the lamp before the holy image, my dear.”", "늙은 유모가 들어와 말하곤 했소. \"거룩한 성상 앞에 등불을 켜게 해 다오, 우리 도련님.\""], ["And once he would not have allowed it and would have blown it out.", "예전 같으면 그는 그것을 허락하지 않고 불을 꺼 버렸을 것이오."]], [["“Light it, light it, dear, I was a wretch to have prevented you doing it.", "\"켜요, 켜요, 할멈, 내가 그걸 못 하게 했던 건 못된 짓이었어요."], ["You are praying when you light the lamp, and I am praying when I rejoice seeing you.", "할멈은 등불을 켜며 기도하고, 나는 할멈을 보는 기쁨으로 기도하니까요."], ["So we are praying to the same God.”", "그러니 우리는 같은 하느님께 기도하는 거예요.\""]], [["Those words seemed strange to us, and mother would go to her room and weep, but when she went in to him she wiped her eyes and looked cheerful.", "그 말들이 우리에게는 이상하게 들려서, 어머니는 제 방으로 가 울곤 했소. 그러나 형에게 들어갈 때면 눈물을 닦고 밝은 얼굴을 지었소."], ["“Mother, don’t weep, darling,”", "\"어머니, 울지 마세요, 사랑하는 어머니.\" 형이 말하곤 했소."], ["he would say, “I’ve long to live yet, long to rejoice with you, and life is glad and joyful.”", "\"나는 아직 오래 살 거예요, 어머니와 함께 오래 기뻐할 거예요. 삶이란 즐겁고 기쁜 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, dear boy, how can you talk of joy when you lie feverish at night, coughing as though you would tear yourself to pieces.”", "\"아, 사랑하는 아들아, 밤이면 열에 들떠 제 몸을 갈가리 찢기라도 할 듯 기침을 해 대면서 어떻게 기쁨을 이야기하느냐.\""]], [["“Don’t cry, mother,”", "\"울지 마세요, 어머니.\" 그가 대답하곤 했소."], ["he would answer, “life is paradise, and we are all in paradise, but we won’t see it, if we would, we should have heaven on earth the next day.”", "\"삶은 낙원이에요, 우리는 모두 낙원에 있어요, 다만 우리가 그걸 보려 들지 않을 뿐이죠. 우리가 보려고만 하면 바로 다음 날에 이 땅 위에 천국이 펼쳐질 거예요.\""]], [["Every one wondered at his words, he spoke so strangely and positively; we were all touched and wept.", "다들 그의 말에 놀랐으니, 그가 어찌나 이상하고 단호하게 말하던지 우리 모두는 마음이 움직여 울었소."], ["Friends came to see us.", "벗들이 우리를 보러 왔소."], ["“Dear ones,”", "\"사랑하는 이들이여.\""], ["he would say to them, “what have I done that you should love me so, how can you love any one like me, and how was it I did not know, I did not appreciate it before?”", "그가 그들에게 말하곤 했소. \"내가 무엇을 했기에 당신들이 나를 이토록 사랑해 주는 거죠, 어떻게 나 같은 사람을 사랑할 수 있죠, 그리고 어찌하여 나는 전에 그걸 알지 못했죠, 그걸 귀하게 여기지 못했죠?\""]], [["When the servants came in to him he would say continually, “Dear, kind people, why are you doing so much for me, do I deserve to be waited on?", "하인들이 그에게 들어오면 그는 끊임없이 말하곤 했소. \"사랑하고 친절한 이들이여, 어찌하여 당신들은 나를 위해 이토록 애쓰는 거죠, 내가 시중받을 자격이 있나요?"], ["If it were God’s will for me to live, I would wait on you, for all men should wait on one another.”", "하느님의 뜻으로 내가 살게 된다면, 나는 당신들의 시중을 들 거예요. 사람은 모두 서로의 시중을 들어야 하니까요.\""]], [["Mother shook her head as she listened.", "어머니는 그 말을 들으며 고개를 저었소."], ["“My darling, it’s your illness makes you talk like that.”", "\"사랑하는 아들아, 그건 네 병 때문에 그런 말을 하는 거란다.\""]], [["“Mother, darling,”", "\"어머니, 사랑하는 어머니.\""], ["he would say, “there must be servants and masters, but if so I will be the servant of my servants, the same as they are to me.", "그가 말하곤 했소. \"하인과 주인은 있어야 해요, 하지만 그렇다면 나는 내 하인들의 하인이 되겠어요, 그들이 내게 하듯 똑같이요."], ["And another thing, mother, every one of us has sinned against all men, and I more than any.”", "그리고 또 한 가지, 어머니, 우리 하나하나가 모든 사람에게 죄를 지었어요, 그중에서도 내가 누구보다 더 그래요.\""]], [["Mother positively smiled at that, smiled through her tears.", "어머니는 그 말에 정말로 미소를 지었으니, 눈물 속에서 미소 지었소."], ["“Why, how could you have sinned against all men, more than all?", "\"아니, 어떻게 네가 모든 사람에게, 그것도 누구보다 더 많이 죄를 지었단 말이냐?"], ["Robbers and murderers have done that, but what sin have you committed yet, that you hold yourself more guilty than all?”", "강도나 살인자라면 그렇겠지만, 네가 무슨 죄를 지었기에 스스로를 누구보다 더 죄 많다고 여기느냐?\""]], [["“Mother, little heart of mine,”", "\"어머니, 내 작은 심장 같은 어머니.\""], ["he said (he had begun using such strange caressing words at that time), “little heart of mine, my joy, believe me, every one is really responsible to all men for all men and for everything.", "그가 말했소(그는 그 무렵부터 이런 이상하고 다정스러운 말을 쓰기 시작했소). \"내 작은 심장 같은 어머니, 나의 기쁨이여, 내 말을 믿어 주세요, 우리 하나하나가 참으로 모든 사람에게, 모든 일에 대해 책임이 있어요."], ["I don’t know how to explain it to you, but I feel it is so, painfully even.", "어떻게 그걸 어머니께 설명해야 할지 모르겠지만, 나는 그것이 그러하다고 느껴요, 아프도록 절절히요."], ["And how is it we went on then living, getting angry and not knowing?”", "그런데 우리는 어찌하여 그토록 화를 내며, 그것을 알지도 못한 채 살아왔을까요?\""]], [["So he would get up every day, more and more sweet and joyous and full of love.", "이렇게 그는 날마다 일어나, 더욱더 다정하고 기쁘며 사랑으로 충만해졌소."], ["When the doctor, an old German called Eisenschmidt, came:", "아이젠슈미트라는 늙은 독일인 의사가 왕진을 오면 형이 농담조로 물었소."]], [["“Well, doctor, have I another day in this world?” he would ask, joking.", "\"그래, 의사 선생, 나에게 이 세상의 하루가 더 남아 있나요?\""]], [["“You’ll live many days yet,”", "\"앞으로 여러 날을 더 사실 겁니다.\""], ["the doctor would answer, “and months and years too.”", "의사가 대답하곤 했소. \"여러 달, 여러 해도요.\""]], [["“Months and years!”", "\"여러 달, 여러 해라니요!\""], ["he would exclaim.", "형은 외치곤 했소."], ["“Why reckon the days?", "\"어찌하여 날수를 헤아리죠?"], ["One day is enough for a man to know all happiness.", "사람이 온갖 행복을 깨닫는 데는 하루로 족해요."], ["My dear ones, why do we quarrel, try to outshine each other and keep grudges against each other?", "사랑하는 이들이여, 어찌하여 우리는 다투고, 서로를 누르려 하며, 서로에게 앙심을 품을까요?"], ["Let’s go straight into the garden, walk and play there, love, appreciate, and kiss each other, and glorify life.”", "곧장 정원으로 나가요, 그곳을 거닐고 노닐며, 사랑하고, 서로를 귀히 여기고, 입 맞추며, 삶을 찬미해요.\""]], [["“Your son cannot last long,”", "\"댁의 아드님은 오래가지 못할 겁니다.\""], ["the doctor told my mother, as she accompanied him to the door.", "의사는 문까지 배웅하는 어머니에게 말했소."], ["“The disease is affecting his brain.”", "\"병이 뇌까지 침범하고 있어요.\""]], [["The windows of his room looked out into the garden, and our garden was a shady one, with old trees in it which were coming into bud.", "그의 방 창문은 정원으로 나 있었는데, 우리 정원은 그늘이 짙고, 막 움이 트기 시작한 오래된 나무들이 서 있었소. 봄의 첫 새들이 가지 사이를 옮겨 다니며 짹짹거리고 창가에서 노래했소."], ["The first birds of spring were flitting in the branches, chirruping and singing at the windows.", "그것들을 바라보며 감탄하던 형은 돌연 새들에게마저 용서를 빌기 시작했소. \"하늘의 새들이여, 행복한 새들이여, 나를 용서해 다오, 나는 너희에게도 죄를 지었으니.\""], ["And looking at them and admiring them, he began suddenly begging their forgiveness too: “Birds of heaven, happy birds, forgive me, for I have sinned against you too.”", "그때 우리 가운데 누구도 그 말을 이해하지 못했으나, 형은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸소. \"그래.\""], ["None of us could understand that at the time, but he shed tears of joy.", "그가 말했소. \"내 둘레에는 하느님의 영광이 이토록 가득했어."], ["“Yes,”", "새들, 나무들, 풀밭, 하늘이."], ["he said, “there was such a glory of God all about me: birds, trees, meadows, sky; only I lived in shame and dishonored it all and did not notice the beauty and glory.”", "그런데 오직 나만이 수치 속에 살며 그 모든 것을 더럽혔고, 그 아름다움과 영광을 알아차리지 못했어.\""]], [["“You take too many sins on yourself,”", "\"너는 너무 많은 죄를 스스로 짊어지는구나.\""], ["mother used to say, weeping.", "어머니는 울며 말하곤 했소."]], [["“Mother, darling, it’s for joy, not for grief I am crying.", "\"어머니, 사랑하는 어머니, 나는 슬퍼서가 아니라 기뻐서 우는 거예요."], ["Though I can’t explain it to you, I like to humble myself before them, for I don’t know how to love them enough.", "어머니께 설명할 수는 없지만, 나는 그들 앞에 나를 낮추는 게 좋아요, 그들을 어떻게 충분히 사랑해야 할지 모르겠기 때문이에요."], ["If I have sinned against every one, yet all forgive me, too, and that’s heaven.", "내가 모든 이에게 죄를 지었어도, 그래도 모두가 나를 용서해 주니, 그것이 바로 천국이에요."], ["Am I not in heaven now?”", "나는 지금 천국에 있는 게 아닐까요?\""]], [["And there was a great deal more I don’t remember.", "그 밖에도 내가 기억하지 못하는 많은 말이 있었소."], ["I remember I went once into his room when there was no one else there.", "한번은 아무도 없을 때 내가 그의 방에 들어갔던 일을 기억하오."], ["It was a bright evening, the sun was setting, and the whole room was lighted up.", "밝은 저녁이었고, 해가 지고 있어 온 방이 환히 물들어 있었소."], ["He beckoned me, and I went up to him.", "그가 나를 손짓해 불러 나는 그에게 다가갔소."], ["He put his hands on my shoulders and looked into my face tenderly, lovingly; he said nothing for a minute, only looked at me like that.", "그는 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 다정하고 사랑스러운 눈길로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았소. 그는 한동안 아무 말 없이 그저 그렇게 나를 바라보기만 했소."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자.\""], ["he said, “run and play now, enjoy life for me too.”", "그가 말했소. \"이제 나가서 놀거라, 나를 위해서도 삶을 누리거라.\""]], [["I went out then and ran to play.", "그래서 나는 밖으로 나가 놀러 달려갔소."], ["And many times in my life afterwards I remembered even with tears how he told me to enjoy life for him too.", "그리고 그 뒤로 살아가는 동안 여러 번, 그가 나를 위해서도 삶을 누리라고 일러 준 일을 눈물마저 흘리며 떠올렸소."], ["There were many other marvelous and beautiful sayings of his, though we did not understand them at the time.", "그가 남긴 놀랍고 아름다운 말은 그 밖에도 많았으나, 그때 우리는 그것을 이해하지 못했소."], ["He died the third week after Easter.", "그는 부활절 셋째 주에 죽었소."], ["He was fully conscious though he could not talk; up to his last hour he did not change.", "그는 말을 할 수는 없었으나 의식만은 또렷했고, 마지막 순간까지 변함이 없었소."], ["He looked happy, his eyes beamed and sought us, he smiled at us, beckoned us.", "그는 행복해 보였고, 두 눈은 빛을 내며 우리를 찾았으며, 우리에게 미소 짓고 우리를 손짓해 불렀소."], ["There was a great deal of talk even in the town about his death.", "그의 죽음에 대해서는 온 도시에서도 적잖은 이야기가 오갔소."], ["I was impressed by all this at the time, but not too much so, though I cried a good deal at his funeral.", "나는 그때 이 모든 일에 깊은 인상을 받았으나, 그렇게까지 깊지는 않았소. 그래도 그의 장례에서 나는 많이 울었소."], ["I was young then, a child, but a lasting impression, a hidden feeling of it all, remained in my heart, ready to rise up and respond when the time came.", "나는 그때 어렸으니, 어린아이였으나, 이 모든 일에 대한 오래도록 남는 인상, 숨겨진 감정이 내 마음에 머물러, 때가 오면 솟아올라 응답할 채비를 갖추고 있었소."], ["So indeed it happened.", "그리고 정녕 그렇게 되었소."]], [["(b) Of the Holy Scriptures in the Life of Father Zossima", "(나) 조시마 장로의 생애에서 성경에 대하여"]], [["I was left alone with my mother.", "나는 어머니와 단둘이 남게 되었소."], ["Her friends began advising her to send me to Petersburg as other parents did.", "어머니의 벗들이 다른 부모들처럼 나를 페테르부르크로 보내라고 권하기 시작했소."], ["“You have only one son now,”", "\"이제 아드님이 하나뿐이고,\""], ["they said, “and have a fair income, and you will be depriving him perhaps of a brilliant career if you keep him here.”", "그들이 말했소. \"수입도 그만하니, 이 아이를 여기 붙들어 두면 어쩌면 빛나는 앞길을 빼앗는 셈이 될 거예요.\""], ["They suggested I should be sent to Petersburg to the Cadet Corps, that I might afterwards enter the Imperial Guard.", "그들은 내가 페테르부르크의 유년사관학교로 보내져, 나중에 황실 근위대에 들어가게 하라고 일렀소."], ["My mother hesitated for a long time, it was awful to part with her only child, but she made up her mind to it at last, though not without many tears, believing she was acting for my happiness.", "어머니는 오래도록 망설였으니, 외아들과 헤어지는 일은 끔찍했기 때문이오. 그러나 마침내 어머니는 그것을 결심했소, 비록 숱한 눈물을 흘리기는 했으나, 그것이 내 행복을 위한 일이라 믿으면서 말이오."], ["She brought me to Petersburg and put me into the Cadet Corps, and I never saw her again.", "어머니는 나를 페테르부르크로 데려가 유년사관학교에 넣었고, 나는 다시는 어머니를 보지 못했소."], ["For she too died three years afterwards.", "어머니 또한 삼 년 뒤에 세상을 떠났기 때문이오."], ["She spent those three years mourning and grieving for both of us.", "어머니는 그 삼 년을 우리 둘을 위해 슬퍼하고 비통해하며 보냈소."]], [["From the house of my childhood I have brought nothing but precious memories, for there are no memories more precious than those of early childhood in one’s first home.", "내 어린 시절의 집에서 나는 소중한 추억밖에는 아무것도 가져오지 못했으니, 한 사람의 첫 보금자리에서 보낸 어린 시절의 추억보다 더 소중한 추억은 없기 때문이오."], ["And that is almost always so if there is any love and harmony in the family at all.", "그리고 가정에 조금이나마 사랑과 화목이 있다면 거의 언제나 그러하오."], ["Indeed, precious memories may remain even of a bad home, if only the heart knows how to find what is precious.", "참으로, 소중한 추억은 좋지 못한 가정에서도 남을 수 있으니, 다만 마음이 그 소중한 것을 찾아낼 줄 알기만 한다면 말이오."], ["With my memories of home I count, too, my memories of the Bible, which, child as I was, I was very eager to read at home.", "내 집에 대한 추억과 더불어 나는 성경에 대한 추억도 헤아리오. 어린아이였던 나는 집에서 그것을 무척이나 읽고 싶어 했소."], ["I had a book of Scripture history then with excellent pictures, called A Hundred and Four Stories from the Old and New Testament, and I learned to read from it.", "그때 내게는 빼어난 그림이 곁들여진 성경 역사책이 있었는데, 『구약과 신약의 백네 가지 이야기』라는 책으로, 나는 그것으로 글 읽기를 배웠소."], ["I have it lying on my shelf now, I keep it as a precious relic of the past.", "나는 지금도 그 책을 내 서가에 두고, 지난날의 소중한 유물로 간직하고 있소."], ["But even before I learned to read, I remember first being moved to devotional feeling at eight years old.", "그러나 글을 깨치기도 전, 나는 여덟 살 적에 처음으로 신심에 마음이 움직였던 일을 기억하오."], ["My mother took me alone to mass (I don’t remember where my brother was at the time) on the Monday before Easter.", "부활절 전 월요일에 어머니가 나만 데리고 미사에 갔소(그때 형이 어디 있었는지는 기억나지 않소)."], ["It was a fine day, and I remember to-day, as though I saw it now, how the incense rose from the censer and softly floated upwards and, overhead in the cupola, mingled in rising waves with the sunlight that streamed in at the little window.", "화창한 날이었고, 나는 지금도, 마치 눈앞에 보이는 듯이, 향로에서 향연이 피어올라 위로 부드럽게 떠다니다가 머리 위 둥근 천장에서 작은 창으로 흘러드는 햇살과 솟구치는 물결처럼 뒤섞이던 모습을 기억하오."], ["I was stirred by the sight, and for the first time in my life I consciously received the seed of God’s word in my heart.", "그 광경에 내 마음이 흔들렸고, 나는 평생 처음으로 하느님 말씀의 씨앗을 의식하며 내 마음에 받아들였소."], ["A youth came out into the middle of the church carrying a big book, so large that at the time I fancied he could scarcely carry it.", "한 젊은이가 커다란 책을 들고 성당 한가운데로 나왔는데, 책이 어찌나 컸던지 그때 나는 그가 그것을 들고 있기조차 버거우리라 여겼소."], ["He laid it on the reading desk, opened it, and began reading, and suddenly for the first time I understood something read in the church of God.", "그는 그것을 독서대에 올려놓고 펼쳐 읽기 시작했고, 그러자 돌연 나는 난생처음으로 하느님의 성당에서 읽히는 무언가를 알아들었소."], ["In the land of Uz, there lived a man, righteous and God-fearing, and he had great wealth, so many camels, so many sheep and asses, and his children feasted, and he loved them very much and prayed for them.", "우스 땅에 한 사람이 살았는데, 의롭고 하느님을 두려워하는 이로, 큰 재물을 지녀 낙타도 숱하게, 양과 나귀도 숱하게 거느렸으며, 그의 자식들은 잔치를 벌였고, 그는 그들을 무척 사랑하여 그들을 위해 기도했소."], ["“It may be that my sons have sinned in their feasting.”", "\"혹시 내 아들들이 잔치를 벌이며 죄를 지었을지도 모른다.\""], ["Now the devil came before the Lord together with the sons of God, and said to the Lord that he had gone up and down the earth and under the earth.", "그런데 마귀가 하느님의 아들들과 함께 주님 앞에 나아와, 자기가 땅 위와 땅 아래를 두루 오르내렸노라고 주님께 아뢰었소."], ["“And hast thou considered my servant Job?”", "\"네가 내 종 욥을 눈여겨보았느냐?\""], ["God asked of him.", "하느님이 그에게 물으셨소."], ["And God boasted to the devil, pointing to his great and holy servant.", "그리고 하느님은 자신의 위대하고 거룩한 종을 가리키며 마귀에게 자랑하셨소."], ["And the devil laughed at God’s words.", "그러자 마귀는 하느님의 말씀을 비웃었소."], ["“Give him over to me and Thou wilt see that Thy servant will murmur against Thee and curse Thy name.”", "\"그를 제게 넘기소서, 그러면 당신의 종이 당신을 거슬러 원망하고 당신의 이름을 저주하는 것을 보시리이다.\""], ["And God gave up the just man He loved so, to the devil.", "그리하여 하느님은 그토록 사랑하시던 의인을 마귀에게 넘기셨소."], ["And the devil smote his children and his cattle and scattered his wealth, all of a sudden like a thunderbolt from heaven.", "그러자 마귀는 마치 하늘에서 떨어진 벼락처럼 느닷없이 그의 자식들과 가축을 쳐 죽이고 그의 재물을 흩어 버렸소."], ["And Job rent his mantle and fell down upon the ground and cried aloud, “Naked came I out of my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return into the earth; the Lord gave and the Lord has taken away.", "그러자 욥은 자기 겉옷을 찢고 땅에 엎드려 큰 소리로 부르짖었소. \"내가 모태에서 알몸으로 나왔으니 또한 알몸으로 그리로 돌아가리이다."], ["Blessed be the name of the Lord for ever and ever.”", "주신 이도 주님이시요 거두신 이도 주님이시니, 주님의 이름이 영원무궁토록 찬송받으시기를 비나이다.\""]], [["Fathers and teachers, forgive my tears now, for all my childhood rises up again before me, and I breathe now as I breathed then, with the breast of a little child of eight, and I feel as I did then, awe and wonder and gladness.", "신부들이여, 스승들이여, 지금 내 눈물을 용서하시오. 내 온 어린 시절이 다시금 내 앞에 떠올라, 나는 지금 그때 그러했듯 숨 쉬고 있으니, 여덟 살 어린아이의 가슴으로 숨 쉬며, 그때처럼 경외와 경탄과 기쁨을 느끼고 있소."], ["The camels at that time caught my imagination, and Satan, who talked like that with God, and God who gave His servant up to destruction, and His servant crying out: “Blessed be Thy name although Thou dost punish me,”", "그때 낙타들이 내 상상을 사로잡았고, 하느님과 그렇게 이야기를 나누던 사탄이, 그리고 제 종을 파멸로 넘기신 하느님이, 또 \"당신께서 저를 벌하셔도 당신의 이름이 찬송받으시기를\""], ["and then the soft and sweet singing in the church: “Let my prayer rise up before Thee,”", "하고 부르짖던 그분의 종이, 그리고 뒤이어 성당 안의 부드럽고 감미로운 노랫소리가, \"내 기도가 당신 앞에 향연처럼 피어오르게 하소서\""], ["and again incense from the priest’s censer and the kneeling and the prayer.", "하던 그 노래가, 또다시 사제의 향로에서 피어오르는 향연과 무릎 꿇음과 기도가 내 마음을 사로잡았소."], ["Ever since then—only yesterday I took it up—I’ve never been able to read that sacred tale without tears.", "그때 이후로—바로 어제도 나는 그것을 집어 들었소—나는 그 거룩한 이야기를 눈물 없이는 한 번도 읽지 못했소."], ["And how much that is great, mysterious and unfathomable there is in it!", "그리고 그 안에 위대하고 신비로우며 헤아릴 수 없는 것이 얼마나 많은지!"], ["Afterwards I heard the words of mockery and blame, proud words, “How could God give up the most loved of His saints for the diversion of the devil, take from him his children, smite him with sore boils so that he cleansed the corruption from his sores with a pot-sherd—and for no object except to boast to the devil!", "훗날 나는 비웃음과 비난의 말, 오만한 말을 들었소. \"어찌하여 하느님은 마귀의 심심풀이를 위해 가장 사랑하는 성도를 넘기시고, 그에게서 자식들을 앗아 가시며, 그를 모진 종기로 치시어 그가 사금파리로 제 상처의 고름을 긁어내게 하셨단 말인가—그것도 마귀에게 자랑하려는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없이!"], ["‘See what My saint can suffer for My sake.’”", "'보아라, 내 성도가 나를 위해 어떤 고난을 견디는지를.'\""], ["But the greatness of it lies just in the fact that it is a mystery—that the passing earthly show and the eternal verity are brought together in it.", "그러나 그 위대함은 바로 그것이 하나의 신비라는 데 있으니—덧없이 스러지는 지상의 모습과 영원한 진리가 그 안에서 한데 모이는 것이오."], ["In the face of the earthly truth, the eternal truth is accomplished.", "지상의 진리 앞에서 영원한 진리가 이루어지는 것이오."], ["The Creator, just as on the first days of creation He ended each day with praise: “That is good that I have created,”", "창조주께서는, 마치 창조의 첫날들에 날마다 그 끝에 \"내가 지은 것이 좋도다\""], ["looks upon Job and again praises His creation.", "하고 찬미하셨던 그대로, 욥을 굽어보시며 다시금 자신의 피조물을 찬미하시오."], ["And Job, praising the Lord, serves not only Him but all His creation for generations and generations, and for ever and ever, since for that he was ordained.", "그리고 욥은 주님을 찬미함으로써 그분뿐 아니라 그분의 온 피조물을 대대손손, 영원무궁토록 섬기는 것이니, 그것을 위해 그가 정해졌기 때문이오."], ["Good heavens, what a book it is, and what lessons there are in it!", "오, 맙소사, 이 얼마나 위대한 책이며, 그 안에 얼마나 큰 가르침이 담겨 있는지!"], ["What a book the Bible is, what a miracle, what strength is given with it to man!", "성경이란 얼마나 위대한 책이며, 얼마나 큰 기적이며, 그것과 더불어 사람에게 얼마나 큰 힘이 주어지는지!"], ["It is like a mold cast of the world and man and human nature, everything is there, and a law for everything for all the ages.", "그것은 세상과 사람과 인간 본성을 떠 낸 거푸집과도 같아서, 모든 것이 그 안에 있고, 온 시대를 위한 모든 것의 법이 그 안에 있소."], ["And what mysteries are solved and revealed!", "그리고 얼마나 많은 신비가 그 안에서 풀리고 드러나는지!"], ["God raises Job again, gives him wealth again.", "하느님은 욥을 다시 일으키시고 다시 재물을 주시오."], ["Many years pass by, and he has other children and loves them.", "여러 해가 지나 그에게 다른 자식들이 생기고 그는 그들을 사랑하오."], ["But how could he love those new ones when those first children are no more, when he has lost them?", "그러나 처음의 자식들이 더는 없는데, 그들을 잃었는데, 어찌 그가 그 새 자식들을 사랑할 수 있단 말이오?"], ["Remembering them, how could he be fully happy with those new ones, however dear the new ones might be?", "그들을 떠올리면서 어찌 그가 그 새 자식들과 더불어 온전히 행복할 수 있단 말이오, 그 새 자식들이 아무리 사랑스럽다 한들?"], ["But he could, he could.", "그러나 그는 그럴 수 있었소, 그럴 수 있었소."], ["It’s the great mystery of human life that old grief passes gradually into quiet, tender joy.", "묵은 슬픔이 차츰 고요하고 다정한 기쁨으로 옮겨 가는 것, 그것이야말로 인간 삶의 위대한 신비요."], ["The mild serenity of age takes the place of the riotous blood of youth.", "젊음의 들끓는 피를 대신해 노년의 잔잔한 평온이 찾아드는 것이오."], ["I bless the rising sun each day, and, as before, my hearts sings to meet it, but now I love even more its setting, its long slanting rays and the soft, tender, gentle memories that come with them, the dear images from the whole of my long, happy life—and over all the Divine Truth, softening, reconciling, forgiving!", "나는 날마다 떠오르는 해를 축복하고, 예전처럼 내 마음은 그것을 맞으러 노래하오. 그러나 이제는 그것이 저물 때를, 그 길게 비껴드는 빛살과 그것과 함께 찾아오는 부드럽고 다정스럽고 정겨운 추억들을, 내 길고 행복한 생애 전체에서 떠오르는 그리운 모습들을 더욱 사랑하오—그리고 그 모든 것 위로 부드럽게 하고, 화해시키고, 용서하는 하느님의 진리가 깃들어 있소!"], ["My life is ending, I know that well, but every day that is left me I feel how my earthly life is in touch with a new infinite, unknown, that approaching life, the nearness of which sets my soul quivering with rapture, my mind glowing and my heart weeping with joy.", "내 생명이 끝나 가고 있음을, 나는 잘 알고 있소. 그러나 내게 남은 하루하루마다 나는 내 지상의 삶이 어떤 새롭고 무한하며 알 수 없는 삶, 다가오는 그 삶과 맞닿아 있음을 느끼니, 그 삶의 가까움이 내 영혼을 환희로 떨게 하고, 내 정신을 타오르게 하며, 내 마음을 기쁨으로 울게 하오."]], [["Friends and teachers, I have heard more than once, and of late one may hear it more often, that the priests, and above all the village priests, are complaining on all sides of their miserable income and their humiliating lot.", "벗들이여, 스승들이여, 나는 한 번 아니라 여러 번 들었소, 그리고 요즈음에는 더 자주 들을 수 있으니, 사제들이, 그중에서도 특히 시골 사제들이 사방에서 자기들의 비참한 수입과 굴욕적인 처지를 한탄하고 있다는 것이오."], ["They plainly state, even in print—I’ve read it myself—that they are unable to teach the Scriptures to the people because of the smallness of their means, and if Lutherans and heretics come and lead the flock astray, they let them lead them astray because they have so little to live upon.", "그들은 노골적으로, 심지어 활자로—나도 직접 읽었소—이르기를, 가진 것이 적은 탓에 백성에게 성경을 가르칠 수 없으며, 루터파나 이단자가 와서 양 떼를 그릇된 길로 이끌어도 자기들은 먹고살 것이 너무 적어 그들이 미혹하게 내버려 둔다고 하오."], ["May the Lord increase the sustenance that is so precious to them, for their complaint is just, too.", "주님께서 그들에게 그토록 소중한 양식을 늘려 주시기를, 그들의 하소연 또한 정당하기에 그러하오."], ["But of a truth I say, if any one is to blame in the matter, half the fault is ours.", "그러나 참으로 내가 말하노니, 이 일에 누군가 책임이 있다면 그 절반은 우리의 잘못이오."], ["For he may be short of time, he may say truly that he is overwhelmed all the while with work and services, but still it’s not all the time, even he has an hour a week to remember God.", "그에게 시간이 모자랄 수 있고, 줄곧 일과 예배에 짓눌려 있다고 진정 말할 수 있으나, 그래도 늘 그런 것은 아니니, 그에게도 한 주에 한 시간쯤은 하느님을 기억할 짬이 있소."], ["And he does not work the whole year round.", "또 그가 일 년 내내 쉼 없이 일하는 것도 아니오."], ["Let him gather round him once a week, some hour in the evening, if only the children at first—the fathers will hear of it and they too will begin to come.", "한 주에 한 번, 저녁의 어느 한 시간, 처음에는 아이들만이라도 그의 둘레에 모아 보게 하시오—아비들이 그 소식을 듣고 그들 또한 모여들기 시작할 것이오."], ["There’s no need to build halls for this, let him take them into his own cottage.", "이를 위해 따로 회관을 지을 필요도 없으니, 그저 그들을 자기 오두막으로 들이게 하시오."], ["They won’t spoil his cottage, they would only be there one hour.", "그들이 그의 오두막을 망가뜨리지 않을 것이니, 그저 한 시간 거기 있을 뿐이오."], ["Let him open that book and begin reading it without grand words or superciliousness, without condescension to them, but gently and kindly, being glad that he is reading to them and that they are listening with attention, loving the words himself, only stopping from time to time to explain words that are not understood by the peasants.", "그로 하여금 그 책을 펼쳐, 거창한 말이나 거만함 없이, 그들을 깔보는 기색 없이, 부드럽고 다정하게, 그들에게 읽어 주는 것을 기뻐하며, 그들이 귀 기울여 듣는 것을 기뻐하며, 그 자신이 그 말씀을 사랑하면서 읽기 시작하게 하시오. 다만 이따금 멈추어 농민들이 알아듣지 못하는 말들을 풀이해 주면서 말이오."], ["Don’t be anxious, they will understand everything, the orthodox heart will understand all!", "염려치 마시오, 그들은 모든 것을 알아들을 것이니, 정교를 믿는 마음은 모든 것을 알아들을 것이오!"], ["Let him read them about Abraham and Sarah, about Isaac and Rebecca, of how Jacob went to Laban and wrestled with the Lord in his dream and said, “This place is holy”—and he will impress the devout mind of the peasant.", "그로 하여금 아브라함과 사라에 대해, 이사악과 레베카에 대해, 야곱이 어떻게 라반에게로 가서 꿈속에서 주님과 씨름하며 \"이곳은 거룩하도다\" 하고 말했는지를 읽어 주게 하시오—그러면 그는 농민의 경건한 마음에 깊은 자취를 새길 것이오."], ["Let him read, especially to the children, how the brothers sold Joseph, the tender boy, the dreamer and prophet, into bondage, and told their father that a wild beast had devoured him, and showed him his blood-stained clothes.", "특히 아이들에게는, 형들이 어떻게 다정한 소년이자 꿈꾸는 자요 예언자였던 요셉을 종으로 팔아넘기고, 아비에게는 들짐승이 그를 잡아먹었다 이르며 피 묻은 그의 옷을 보였는지를 읽어 주게 하시오."], ["Let him read them how the brothers afterwards journeyed into Egypt for corn, and Joseph, already a great ruler, unrecognized by them, tormented them, accused them, kept his brother Benjamin, and all through love: “I love you, and loving you I torment you.”", "또 그 형들이 어떻게 나중에 곡식을 구하러 이집트로 갔는지, 그리고 이미 큰 통치자가 된 요셉이, 형들이 알아보지 못하는 가운데 그들을 괴롭히고 추궁하며 아우 베냐민을 붙들어 두었는지를, 그것도 오직 사랑에서 그리했음을 읽어 주게 하시오."], ["For he remembered all his life how they had sold him to the merchants in the burning desert by the well, and how, wringing his hands, he had wept and besought his brothers not to sell him as a slave in a strange land.", "\"나는 너희를 사랑하기에, 사랑하기에 너희를 괴롭히노라.\" 그는 형들이 자기를 불타는 사막의 우물가에서 상인들에게 팔아넘기던 일을, 그리고 자기가 두 손을 비틀며 울면서 낯선 땅에 노예로 팔지 말아 달라고 형들에게 애원하던 일을 평생토록 기억했기 때문이오."], ["And how, seeing them again after many years, he loved them beyond measure, but he harassed and tormented them in love.", "그리고 여러 해 만에 다시 그들을 보았을 때 그는 그들을 한없이 사랑했으나, 사랑하기에 그들을 들볶고 괴롭혔소."], ["He left them at last not able to bear the suffering of his heart, flung himself on his bed and wept.", "그는 마침내 제 가슴의 고통을 견디지 못하고 그들 곁을 떠나, 침상에 몸을 던지고 울었소."], ["Then, wiping his tears away, he went out to them joyful and told them, “Brothers, I am your brother Joseph!”", "그러고는 눈물을 닦고 기쁜 얼굴로 그들에게 나아가 일렀소. \"형제들이여, 내가 그대들의 아우 요셉이오!\""], ["Let him read them further how happy old Jacob was on learning that his darling boy was still alive, and how he went to Egypt leaving his own country, and died in a foreign land, bequeathing his great prophecy that had lain mysteriously hidden in his meek and timid heart all his life, that from his offspring, from Judah, will come the great hope of the world, the Messiah and Saviour.", "그로 하여금 더 나아가, 늙은 야곱이 사랑하는 아들이 아직 살아 있음을 알고 얼마나 행복해했는지, 그리고 그가 어떻게 제 고향을 떠나 이집트로 가서 타향에서 죽으며, 평생토록 그 온순하고 겁 많은 마음속에 신비로이 감추어 두었던 위대한 예언을—그의 후손에게서, 유다에게서 세상의 큰 소망인 메시아요 구세주가 나오리라는 예언을—남겼는지를 읽어 주게 하시오."]], [["Fathers and teachers, forgive me and don’t be angry, that like a little child I’ve been babbling of what you know long ago, and can teach me a hundred times more skillfully.", "신부들이여, 스승들이여, 어린아이처럼 그대들이 진작에 알고 있으며 나보다 백 배는 더 능란하게 가르칠 수 있는 것을 두고 내가 주절거린 것을 용서하시고 노여워 마시오."], ["I only speak from rapture, and forgive my tears, for I love the Bible.", "나는 다만 환희에 겨워 말할 뿐이니, 내 눈물을 용서하시오, 나는 성경을 사랑하기 때문이오."], ["Let him too weep, the priest of God, and be sure that the hearts of his listeners will throb in response.", "하느님의 사제 또한 울게 하시오, 그리하면 그 말을 듣는 이들의 마음이 그에 화답하여 두근거릴 것임을 확신하시오."], ["Only a little tiny seed is needed—drop it into the heart of the peasant and it won’t die, it will live in his soul all his life, it will be hidden in the midst of his darkness and sin, like a bright spot, like a great reminder.", "그저 아주 작은 씨앗 하나면 족하니—그것을 농민의 마음에 떨어뜨리면 그것은 죽지 않고 평생토록 그의 영혼 속에 살아, 그의 어둠과 죄의 한가운데에 밝은 점처럼, 위대한 일깨움처럼 숨어 있을 것이오."], ["And there’s no need of much teaching or explanation, he will understand it all simply.", "그리고 많은 가르침이나 풀이가 필요치 않으니, 그는 모든 것을 그저 단순하게 알아들을 것이오."], ["Do you suppose that the peasants don’t understand?", "그대들은 농민들이 알아듣지 못하리라 여기시오?"], ["Try reading them the touching story of the fair Esther and the haughty Vashti; or the miraculous story of Jonah in the whale. Don’t forget either the parables of Our Lord, choose especially from the Gospel of St.", "시험 삼아 아름다운 에스더와 오만한 와스디의 가슴 저미는 이야기를, 혹은 고래 속 요나의 기적 같은 이야기를 읽어 주어 보시오."], ["Luke (that is what I did), and then from the Acts of the Apostles the conversion of St. Paul (that you mustn’t leave out on any account), and from the Lives of the Saints, for instance, the life of Alexey, the man of God and, greatest of all, the happy martyr and the seer of God, Mary of Egypt—and you will penetrate their hearts with these simple tales.", "우리 주님의 비유들 또한 잊지 마시고, 특히 루카 복음에서 골라 읽으시오(내가 그렇게 했소), 그다음에는 사도행전에서 바울로의 회심을(그것은 무슨 일이 있어도 빼놓아서는 아니 되오), 그리고 『성인전』에서는, 이를테면 하느님의 사람 알렉세이의 생애를, 무엇보다도 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 빼어난, 복된 순교자요 하느님을 본 이집트의 마리아의 생애를 읽어 주시오—그러면 그대들은 이 소박한 이야기들로 그들의 마음에 스며들 것이오."], ["Give one hour a week to it in spite of your poverty, only one little hour.", "그대들의 가난에도 불구하고 한 주에 한 시간을, 오직 한 작은 시간을 거기에 바치시오."], ["And you will see for yourselves that our people is gracious and grateful, and will repay you a hundred-fold.", "그러면 그대들 스스로 보게 되리니, 우리 백성은 너그럽고 고마움을 아는지라, 그대들에게 백배로 갚을 것이오."], ["Mindful of the kindness of their priest and the moving words they have heard from him, they will of their own accord help him in his fields and in his house, and will treat him with more respect than before—so that it will even increase his worldly well-being too.", "자기 사제의 친절과 그에게서 들은 감동적인 말을 기억하여, 그들은 제 스스로 그의 밭일과 집안일을 거들 것이며, 전보다 더 큰 존경으로 그를 대할 것이오—그리하여 그것은 그의 세속적인 형편마저 한결 나아지게 할 것이오."], ["The thing is so simple that sometimes one is even afraid to put it into words, for fear of being laughed at, and yet how true it is!", "그 이치가 어찌나 단순한지 때로는 비웃음을 살까 두려워 그것을 말로 옮기기조차 겁이 나오, 그러나 그것이 얼마나 참된지!"], ["One who does not believe in God will not believe in God’s people.", "하느님을 믿지 않는 자는 하느님의 백성도 믿지 않을 것이오."], ["He who believes in God’s people will see His Holiness too, even though he had not believed in it till then.", "하느님의 백성을 믿는 자는 그분의 거룩함 또한 보게 되리니, 설령 그때까지 그것을 믿지 않았다 할지라도 그러하오."], ["Only the people and their future spiritual power will convert our atheists, who have torn themselves away from their native soil.", "오직 백성과 그들의 미래의 영적인 힘만이, 제 고향 땅에서 스스로를 떼어 낸 우리의 무신론자들을 회개시킬 것이오."]], [["And what is the use of Christ’s words, unless we set an example?", "그리고 우리가 본을 보이지 않는다면, 그리스도의 말씀이 무슨 소용이 있겠소?"], ["The people is lost without the Word of God, for its soul is athirst for the Word and for all that is good.", "백성은 하느님의 말씀이 없으면 길을 잃으니, 그 영혼이 말씀을, 그리고 온갖 선한 것을 목말라하기 때문이오."]], [["In my youth, long ago, nearly forty years ago, I traveled all over Russia with Father Anfim, collecting funds for our monastery, and we stayed one night on the bank of a great navigable river with some fishermen.", "내 젊은 시절, 오래전, 거의 사십 년 전에, 나는 안핌 신부와 함께 우리 수도원을 위한 기금을 모으며 러시아 곳곳을 두루 돌아다녔는데, 어느 큰 배가 다닐 만한 강가에서 어부 몇과 더불어 하룻밤을 묵었소."], ["A good-looking peasant lad, about eighteen, joined us; he had to hurry back next morning to pull a merchant’s barge along the bank.", "열여덟 살쯤 되어 보이는 잘생긴 농촌 청년 하나가 우리와 어울렸는데, 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 돌아가 강기슭을 따라 한 상인의 거룻배를 끌어야 하는 처지였소."], ["I noticed him looking straight before him with clear and tender eyes.", "나는 그가 맑고 다정한 눈으로 앞을 똑바로 바라보는 것을 눈여겨보았소."], ["It was a bright, warm, still, July night, a cool mist rose from the broad river, we could hear the plash of a fish, the birds were still, all was hushed and beautiful, everything praying to God.", "밝고 따뜻하며 고요한 칠월의 밤이었고, 너른 강에서 서늘한 안개가 피어올랐으며, 물고기가 첨벙대는 소리가 들리고, 새들은 잠잠했으며, 모든 것이 고요하고 아름다워, 만물이 하느님께 기도하고 있었소."], ["Only we two were not sleeping, the lad and I, and we talked of the beauty of this world of God’s and of the great mystery of it.", "잠들지 않은 것은 오직 우리 둘, 그 청년과 나뿐이어서, 우리는 하느님의 이 세상의 아름다움과 그 위대한 신비에 대해 이야기를 나누었소."], ["Every blade of grass, every insect, ant, and golden bee, all so marvelously know their path, though they have not intelligence, they bear witness to the mystery of God and continually accomplish it themselves.", "풀잎 하나하나, 벌레 하나하나, 개미와 황금빛 꿀벌이, 모두 그토록 놀랍게 제 길을 알고 있으니, 비록 지력이 없을지언정 그것들은 하느님의 신비를 증언하며 끊임없이 스스로 그것을 이루어 가고 있소."], ["I saw the dear lad’s heart was moved.", "나는 그 어여쁜 청년의 마음이 움직이는 것을 보았소."], ["He told me that he loved the forest and the forest birds.", "그는 자기가 숲과 숲의 새들을 사랑한다고 내게 말했소."], ["He was a bird-catcher, knew the note of each of them, could call each bird.", "그는 새잡이여서, 새마다의 울음소리를 알았고, 어느 새든 불러낼 수 있었소."], ["“I know nothing better than to be in the forest,”", "\"나는 숲속에 있는 것보다 더 좋은 게 없어요.\" 그가 말했소."], ["said he, “though all things are good.”", "\"물론 세상 모든 것이 다 좋지만요.\""]], [["“Truly,”", "\"참으로 그렇소.\""], ["I answered him, “all things are good and fair, because all is truth.", "나는 그에게 대답했소. \"세상 모든 것이 좋고 아름다우니, 모든 것이 진리이기 때문이오."], ["Look,”", "보시오.\" 내가 말했소."], ["said I, “at the horse, that great beast that is so near to man; or the lowly, pensive ox, which feeds him and works for him; look at their faces, what meekness, what devotion to man, who often beats them mercilessly.", "\"사람과 그토록 가까운 저 큰 짐승, 말을 보시오. 혹은 사람을 먹여 살리고 사람을 위해 일하는 저 보잘것없고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 소를 보시오."], ["What gentleness, what confidence and what beauty!", "그것들의 얼굴을 보시오, 그 얼마나 온순하며, 흔히 자기들을 무자비하게 매질하는 사람을 향해 그 얼마나 헌신적인지."], ["It’s touching to know that there’s no sin in them, for all, all except man, is sinless, and Christ has been with them before us.”", "그 얼마나 부드럽고, 그 얼마나 믿음에 차 있으며, 그 얼마나 아름다운지! 그것들에게 죄가 없음을 안다는 것은 가슴 저미는 일이오, 사람을 빼고는 모든 것이, 모두가 죄가 없으며, 그리스도께서 우리보다 앞서 그들과 함께 계셨기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"어찌하여,\""], ["asked the boy, “is Christ with them too?”", "청년이 물었소. \"그리스도께서 그들과도 함께 계시나요?\""]], [["“It cannot but be so,”", "\"그러지 않을 수 없소.\""], ["said I, “since the Word is for all.", "나는 말했소. \"말씀은 모두를 위한 것이기 때문이오."], ["All creation and all creatures, every leaf is striving to the Word, singing glory to God, weeping to Christ, unconsciously accomplishing this by the mystery of their sinless life.", "모든 피조물과 모든 생명이, 잎사귀 하나하나가 말씀을 향해 나아가며, 하느님께 영광의 노래를 부르고, 그리스도께 눈물지으며, 자기들의 죄 없는 삶의 신비를 통해 무심결에 이를 이루고 있소."], ["Yonder,”", "저기,\""], ["said I, “in the forest wanders the dreadful bear, fierce and menacing, and yet innocent in it.”", "내가 말했소. \"저 숲속에는 무시무시한 곰이 떠도오, 사납고 위협적이나, 그 안에 또한 죄가 없소.\""], ["And I told him how once a bear came to a great saint who had taken refuge in a tiny cell in the wood.", "그러고 나서 나는, 언젠가 곰 한 마리가 숲속의 자그마한 암자에 몸을 의탁하고 있던 한 위대한 성자에게 찾아온 이야기를 그에게 들려주었소."], ["And the great saint pitied him, went up to him without fear and gave him a piece of bread.", "그 위대한 성자는 곰을 가엾이 여겨, 두려움 없이 그에게 다가가 빵 한 조각을 주었소. \"가거라.\""], ["“Go along,”", "그가 말했소."], ["said he, “Christ be with you,”", "\"그리스도께서 너와 함께하시기를.\""], ["and the savage beast walked away meekly and obediently, doing no harm.", "그러자 그 사나운 짐승은 아무런 해도 끼치지 않고 온순하고 고분고분하게 걸어가 버렸소."], ["And the lad was delighted that the bear had walked away without hurting the saint, and that Christ was with him too.", "청년은 곰이 성자를 해치지 않고 걸어가 버린 것을, 그리고 그리스도께서 곰과도 함께 계셨던 것을 기뻐했소."], ["“Ah,”", "\"아아,\""], ["said he, “how good that is, how good and beautiful is all God’s work!”", "그가 말했소. \"그 얼마나 좋은지요, 하느님의 모든 일이 그 얼마나 좋고 아름다운지요!\""], ["He sat musing softly and sweetly.", "그는 부드럽고 감미롭게 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었소."], ["I saw he understood.", "나는 그가 알아들었음을 보았소."], ["And he slept beside me a light and sinless sleep.", "그리고 그는 내 곁에서 가볍고 죄 없는 잠을 잤소."], ["May God bless youth!", "하느님께서 젊음을 축복하시기를!"], ["And I prayed for him as I went to sleep.", "나는 잠들면서 그를 위해 기도했소."], ["Lord, send peace and light to Thy people!", "주님이시여, 당신의 백성에게 평화와 빛을 내려 주소서!"]], [["# Chapter II. The Duel", "# Chapter II. The Duel"]], [["(c) Recollections of Father Zossima’s Youth before he became a Monk.", "(다) 조시마 장로가 수도자가 되기 전, 청년 시절의 회상."], ["The Duel", "결투"]], [["I spent a long time, almost eight years, in the military cadet school at Petersburg, and in the novelty of my surroundings there, many of my childish impressions grew dimmer, though I forgot nothing.", "나는 페테르부르크의 사관학교에서 거의 팔 년에 이르는 오랜 세월을 보냈는데, 그 새로운 환경의 신기함 속에서 어린 시절의 인상들 가운데 많은 것이 점차 희미해져 갔으나, 그렇다고 무엇 하나 잊어버린 것은 아니었다."], ["I picked up so many new habits and opinions that I was transformed into a cruel, absurd, almost savage creature.", "나는 너무도 많은 새로운 습관과 관념을 익혀서 마침내 잔혹하고 어처구니없으며 거의 야만에 가까운 인간으로 변모하고 말았다."], ["A surface polish of courtesy and society manners I did acquire together with the French language.", "예의범절과 사교계의 처신이라는 겉치레의 윤기만큼은 프랑스어와 더불어 익혀 두긴 했다."]], [["But we all, myself included, looked upon the soldiers in our service as cattle.", "그러나 나를 포함한 우리 모두는 우리 밑에서 복무하는 병사들을 가축으로 여겼다."], ["I was perhaps worse than the rest in that respect, for I was so much more impressionable than my companions.", "그 점에서는 어쩌면 내가 나머지 사람들보다 더 심했을지도 모르니, 나는 동료들보다 훨씬 더 감수성이 예민한 인간이었기 때문이다."], ["By the time we left the school as officers, we were ready to lay down our lives for the honor of the regiment, but no one of us had any knowledge of the real meaning of honor, and if any one had known it, he would have been the first to ridicule it.", "우리가 사관학교를 졸업하고 장교가 되었을 무렵, 우리는 연대의 명예를 위해서라면 목숨을 내던질 각오가 되어 있었으나, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 명예의 참된 의미를 알지 못했고, 설령 누군가가 그것을 알았더라면 그자가 가장 먼저 그것을 비웃었을 것이다."], ["Drunkenness, debauchery and devilry were what we almost prided ourselves on.", "음주와 방탕과 난행, 그것이야말로 우리가 거의 자랑으로 삼던 것이었다."], ["I don’t say that we were bad by nature, all these young men were good fellows, but they behaved badly, and I worst of all.", "우리가 천성적으로 나빴다는 말은 아니니, 이 모든 젊은이들은 다 좋은 친구들이었으나, 행실이 나빴을 따름이고, 그중에서도 내가 가장 나빴다."], ["What made it worse for me was that I had come into my own money, and so I flung myself into a life of pleasure, and plunged headlong into all the recklessness of youth.", "게다가 나에게는 사정을 더욱 악화시키는 일이 있었으니, 내가 마침 내 몫의 재산을 손에 넣게 되었던 것이라, 나는 향락의 생활에 몸을 던졌고 젊음의 온갖 무모함 속으로 곤두박질치고 말았다."]], [["I was fond of reading, yet strange to say, the Bible was the one book I never opened at that time, though I always carried it about with me, and I was never separated from it; in very truth I was keeping that book “for the day and the hour, for the month and the year,”", "나는 책 읽기를 좋아했으나, 이상한 말이지만, 그 무렵 단 한 권 펼쳐 보지 않은 책이 바로 성경이었으니, 그러면서도 나는 늘 그것을 지니고 다녔고 한순간도 그것과 떨어진 적이 없었다. 실로 나는 그 책을 \"그날과 그 시각을 위하여, 그달과 그해를 위하여\""], ["though I knew it not.", "간직하고 있었던 것이나, 정작 나 자신은 그것을 알지 못했다."]], [["After four years of this life, I chanced to be in the town of K. where our regiment was stationed at the time.", "이런 생활을 사 년 동안 보낸 끝에, 나는 우연히도 당시 우리 연대가 주둔하고 있던 K 시에 머물게 되었다."], ["We found the people of the town hospitable, rich and fond of entertainments.", "우리는 그 고을 사람들이 손님 대접을 후하게 하고, 부유하며, 즐기기를 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["I met with a cordial reception everywhere, as I was of a lively temperament and was known to be well off, which always goes a long way in the world.", "나는 어디서나 따뜻한 환대를 받았으니, 천성이 쾌활한 데다 살림이 넉넉하다고 알려져 있었기 때문인데, 이런 평판은 세상에서 언제나 큰 힘을 발휘하는 법이다."], ["And then a circumstance happened which was the beginning of it all.", "그리고 그때 한 가지 사건이 벌어졌으니, 그것이 이 모든 일의 발단이었다."]], [["I formed an attachment to a beautiful and intelligent young girl of noble and lofty character, the daughter of people much respected.", "나는 아름답고 총명하며, 고결하고 드높은 성품을 지닌 한 젊은 처녀에게 마음을 빼앗기게 되었으니, 그녀는 사람들에게 크게 존경받는 집안의 딸이었다."], ["They were well-to-do people of influence and position.", "그들은 살림이 넉넉하고 세력과 지위가 있는 사람들이었다."], ["They always gave me a cordial and friendly reception.", "그들은 늘 나를 따뜻하고 다정하게 맞아 주었다."], ["I fancied that the young lady looked on me with favor and my heart was aflame at such an idea.", "나는 그 아가씨가 나를 호의로 바라본다고 여겼고, 그런 생각에 내 가슴은 불타올랐다."], ["Later on I saw and fully realized that I perhaps was not so passionately in love with her at all, but only recognized the elevation of her mind and character, which I could not indeed have helped doing.", "나중에 가서야 나는 어쩌면 내가 그녀를 그토록 열렬히 사랑했던 것은 결코 아니었으며, 다만 그녀의 드높은 정신과 성품을 인정했을 따름임을 보고 또 온전히 깨달았으니, 사실 그것을 인정하지 않을 도리는 없었다."], ["I was prevented, however, from making her an offer at the time by my selfishness, I was loath to part with the allurements of my free and licentious bachelor life in the heyday of my youth, and with my pockets full of money.", "그러나 그 무렵 나는 나의 이기심 때문에 그녀에게 청혼하기를 미루고 있었으니, 한창 젊은 나이에 주머니 가득 돈을 채운 채 누리던 자유롭고 방종한 독신 생활의 유혹과 작별하기가 못내 싫었던 것이다."], ["I did drop some hint as to my feelings however, though I put off taking any decisive step for a time.", "그래도 내 마음에 관해 넌지시 암시를 흘리기는 했으나, 결정적인 한 걸음을 내딛는 일만큼은 한동안 미루어 두었다."], ["Then, all of a sudden, we were ordered off for two months to another district.", "그러던 차에, 별안간 우리에게 두 달 동안 다른 지방으로 떠나라는 명령이 내려졌다."]], [["On my return two months later, I found the young lady already married to a rich neighboring landowner, a very amiable man, still young though older than I was, connected with the best Petersburg society, which I was not, and of excellent education, which I also was not.", "두 달 뒤 돌아와 보니, 그 아가씨는 이미 이웃 고을의 부유한 지주와 결혼한 뒤였으니, 그는 매우 상냥한 사람으로, 나보다는 연상이었으나 아직 젊었고, 페테르부르크 최상류 사교계와 연줄이 닿아 있었으되 나는 그렇지 못했으며, 훌륭한 교육을 받은 사람이었으되 나는 또한 그렇지 못했다."], ["I was so overwhelmed at this unexpected circumstance that my mind was positively clouded.", "나는 이 뜻밖의 사태에 어찌나 압도되었던지 정신이 그야말로 흐려질 지경이었다."], ["The worst of it all was that, as I learned then, the young landowner had been a long while betrothed to her, and I had met him indeed many times in her house, but blinded by my conceit I had noticed nothing.", "무엇보다 견디기 힘들었던 것은, 그때 알게 된 사실인데, 그 젊은 지주가 오래전부터 그녀와 약혼한 사이였고, 나도 실은 그녀의 집에서 그를 여러 차례 마주쳤건만, 내 자만심에 눈이 멀어 아무것도 알아채지 못했다는 점이었다."], ["And this particularly mortified me; almost everybody had known all about it, while I knew nothing.", "바로 이것이 나를 유난히 분하게 만들었으니, 거의 모든 사람이 그 일을 다 알고 있었는데 나만 아무것도 몰랐던 것이다."], ["I was filled with sudden irrepressible fury.", "나는 별안간 걷잡을 수 없는 분노에 사로잡혔다."], ["With flushed face I began recalling how often I had been on the point of declaring my love to her, and as she had not attempted to stop me or to warn me, she must, I concluded, have been laughing at me all the time.", "얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 나는 내가 그녀에게 사랑을 고백하려던 순간이 얼마나 잦았던가를 떠올리기 시작했고, 그녀가 나를 말리거나 일러 주려 하지 않았으니 결국 그녀가 내내 나를 비웃고 있었던 것이라고 단정 짓고 말았다."], ["Later on, of course, I reflected and remembered that she had been very far from laughing at me; on the contrary, she used to turn off any love-making on my part with a jest and begin talking of other subjects; but at that moment I was incapable of reflecting and was all eagerness for revenge.", "물론 나중에 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그녀가 결코 나를 비웃었던 것이 아니었음이 기억났으며, 도리어 그녀는 내가 사랑을 내비칠라치면 농담으로 슬쩍 받아넘기고는 다른 이야기로 화제를 돌리곤 했던 것이다. 그러나 그 순간에 나는 곰곰이 생각할 능력이 없었고 오로지 복수심으로 들끓고 있었다."], ["I am surprised to remember that my wrath and revengeful feelings were extremely repugnant to my own nature, for being of an easy temper, I found it difficult to be angry with any one for long, and so I had to work myself up artificially and became at last revolting and absurd.", "지금 돌이켜 보면 놀라운 일이지만, 나의 분노와 복수의 감정은 본디 나의 천성에 지극히 거슬리는 것이었으니, 나는 성미가 무던한 사람이라 누군가에게 오래 화를 내기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었고, 그래서 일부러 억지로 감정을 부추겨야 했으며, 마침내는 역겹고 어처구니없는 꼴이 되고 말았다."]], [["I waited for an opportunity and succeeded in insulting my “rival” in the presence of a large company.", "나는 기회를 노린 끝에, 많은 사람이 모인 자리에서 나의 \"연적\"을 모욕하는 데 성공했다."], ["I insulted him on a perfectly extraneous pretext, jeering at his opinion upon an important public event—it was in the year 1826[5]—and my jeer was, so people said, clever and effective.", "나는 전혀 엉뚱한 구실을 잡아 그를 모욕했으니, 어느 중대한 사회적 사건에 대한 그의 견해를 비웃은 것인데—그것은 1826년의 일이었다—사람들 말로는 나의 빈정거림이 제법 기지 넘치고 효과적이었다고 한다."], ["Then I forced him to ask for an explanation, and behaved so rudely that he accepted my challenge in spite of the vast inequality between us, as I was younger, a person of no consequence, and of inferior rank.", "그러고는 나는 그가 해명을 요구하지 않을 수 없게끔 몰아붙였고, 어찌나 무례하게 굴었던지, 우리 둘 사이에 엄청난 격차가 있었음에도—나는 더 어리고, 보잘것없는 인간이며, 신분도 낮았건만—그가 나의 결투 신청을 받아들이고 말았다."], ["I learned afterwards for a fact that it was from a jealous feeling on his side also that my challenge was accepted; he had been rather jealous of me on his wife’s account before their marriage; he fancied now that if he submitted to be insulted by me and refused to accept my challenge, and if she heard of it, she might begin to despise him and waver in her love for him.", "나는 나중에 가서야 한 가지 사실을 알게 되었으니, 그가 나의 신청을 받아들인 데에는 그쪽의 질투심도 한몫했다는 것이다. 그는 결혼 전부터 아내 때문에 나를 적잖이 질투하고 있었던 것이며, 이제 와서 그가 나에게 모욕당하고도 결투 신청을 거절했다는 것을 만약 그녀가 듣게 된다면, 그녀가 그를 경멸하기 시작하여 그에 대한 사랑이 흔들릴지도 모른다고 여겼던 것이다."], ["I soon found a second in a comrade, an ensign of our regiment.", "나는 곧 우리 연대의 한 동료, 한 소위에게서 입회인을 구했다."], ["In those days though duels were severely punished, yet dueling was a kind of fashion among the officers—so strong and deeply rooted will a brutal prejudice sometimes be.", "그 시절에는 결투가 엄히 처벌받았음에도 불구하고, 장교들 사이에서는 결투가 일종의 유행이었으니—야만적인 편견이란 때로 이토록 강하고 뿌리 깊게 박혀 있는 법이다."]], [["It was the end of June, and our meeting was to take place at seven o’clock the next day on the outskirts of the town—and then something happened that in very truth was the turning-point of my life.", "때는 유월 말이었고, 우리의 결투는 이튿날 아침 일곱 시에 고을 변두리에서 벌어지기로 되어 있었다—그런데 그때 한 가지 일이 벌어졌으니, 그것이 실로 내 생애의 전환점이었다."], ["In the evening, returning home in a savage and brutal humor, I flew into a rage with my orderly Afanasy, and gave him two blows in the face with all my might, so that it was covered with blood.", "그날 저녁, 사납고 거친 기분으로 집에 돌아온 나는 내 종졸 아파나시에게 벌컥 화를 내고는 있는 힘껏 그의 얼굴을 두 번 후려쳐서, 그 얼굴이 피투성이가 되도록 만들었다. 그는 나에게 온 지 얼마 되지 않았고, 나는 전에도 그를 때린 적이 있었으나, 그토록 잔인무도하게 친 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["He had not long been in my service and I had struck him before, but never with such ferocious cruelty.", "그리고 믿어 주시오, 비록 사십 년 전의 일이지만, 나는 지금도 그 일을 부끄러움과 아픔 속에서 떠올리고 있소."], ["And, believe me, though it’s forty years ago, I recall it now with shame and pain.", "나는 잠자리에 들어 세 시간쯤 잤는데, 깨어 보니 동이 트고 있었다."], ["I went to bed and slept for about three hours; when I waked up the day was breaking.", "나는 일어났다—더는 자고 싶지 않았다—창가로 다가가—창을 열었더니, 그것은 정원 쪽으로 나 있었다."], ["I got up—I did not want to sleep any more—I went to the window—opened it, it looked out upon the garden; I saw the sun rising; it was warm and beautiful, the birds were singing.", "나는 해가 떠오르는 것을 보았다. 날은 따스하고 아름다웠으며, 새들이 지저귀고 있었다."]], [["“What’s the meaning of it?”", "\"대체 이게 무슨 영문일까?\""], ["I thought.", "나는 생각했다."], ["“I feel in my heart as it were something vile and shameful.", "\"내 마음속에 어쩐지 비열하고 수치스러운 무언가가 느껴진다."], ["Is it because I am going to shed blood?", "내가 사람의 피를 흘리려 하기 때문일까?"], ["No,”", "아니,\""], ["I thought, “I feel it’s not that.", "나는 생각했다. \"그게 아닌 것 같다."], ["Can it be that I am afraid of death, afraid of being killed?", "죽음이 두려운 것일까, 죽임을 당할까 봐 무서운 것일까?"], ["No, that’s not it, that’s not it at all.”...", "아니, 그것도 아니다, 결코 그것이 아니다.\"……"], ["And all at once I knew what it was: it was because I had beaten Afanasy the evening before!", "그러다 별안간 나는 그것이 무엇인지 깨달았으니, 그것은 바로 내가 어제저녁 아파나시를 때렸기 때문이었다!"], ["It all rose before my mind, it all was as it were repeated over again; he stood before me and I was beating him straight on the face and he was holding his arms stiffly down, his head erect, his eyes fixed upon me as though on parade.", "그 모든 것이 내 마음속에 떠올랐고, 모든 것이 그대로 다시 한번 되풀이되는 듯했다. 그가 내 앞에 서 있고, 나는 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 후려치고 있는데, 그는 두 팔을 뻣뻣이 내려뜨린 채 고개를 꼿꼿이 세우고, 마치 사열대에 선 듯 두 눈을 내게 못 박고 있었다."], ["He staggered at every blow and did not even dare to raise his hands to protect himself.", "그는 매번 얻어맞을 때마다 비틀거렸으나, 제 몸을 가리려 손을 들어 올릴 엄두조차 내지 못했다."], ["That is what a man has been brought to, and that was a man beating a fellow creature!", "인간이 이 지경에까지 이르렀구나, 그리고 그것은 인간이 같은 동족을 때리고 있는 꼴이었다!"], ["What a crime!", "이 무슨 죄악인가!"], ["It was as though a sharp dagger had pierced me right through.", "마치 예리한 단검이 내 몸을 그대로 꿰뚫고 지나간 것 같았다."], ["I stood as if I were struck dumb, while the sun was shining, the leaves were rejoicing and the birds were trilling the praise of God....", "나는 벙어리가 된 듯 멍하니 서 있었고, 그동안 해는 빛나고, 나뭇잎은 환희에 차 있고, 새들은 하느님을 찬미하며 지저귀고 있었다.……"], ["I hid my face in my hands, fell on my bed and broke into a storm of tears.", "나는 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻은 채 침대에 쓰러져 폭풍 같은 눈물을 터뜨렸다."], ["And then I remembered my brother Markel and what he said on his death-bed to his servants: “My dear ones, why do you wait on me, why do you love me, am I worth your waiting on me?”", "그리고 그때 나는 형 마르켈을, 그리고 그가 임종의 자리에서 하인들에게 했던 말을 떠올렸다. \"사랑하는 이들이여, 어찌하여 너희가 나의 시중을 드느냐, 어찌하여 너희가 나를 사랑하느냐, 내가 과연 너희의 시중을 받을 만한 사람이더냐?\""]], [["“Yes, am I worth it?”", "\"그래, 내가 과연 그럴 만한 사람인가?\""], ["flashed through my mind.", "하는 생각이 내 마음속을 스쳤다."], ["“After all what am I worth, that another man, a fellow creature, made in the likeness and image of God, should serve me?”", "\"도대체 나라는 인간이 무슨 값어치가 있기에, 다른 사람이, 하느님의 형상과 모양대로 지음받은 같은 동족이 나를 섬겨야 한단 말인가?\""], ["For the first time in my life this question forced itself upon me.", "내 평생 처음으로 이 물음이 나에게 들이닥쳤다. 형은 이렇게 말했었다."], ["He had said, “Mother, my little heart, in truth we are each responsible to all for all, it’s only that men don’t know this.", "\"어머니, 제 작은 심장이시여, 진실로 우리는 저마다 모든 이에 대해 모든 일에 책임이 있는 것입니다, 다만 사람들이 이것을 모를 뿐이지요."], ["If they knew it, the world would be a paradise at once.”", "만약 그들이 그것을 안다면, 세상은 당장 낙원이 될 텐데요.\""]], [["“God, can that too be false?”", "\"하느님, 그것마저도 거짓일 수 있을까?\""], ["I thought as I wept.", "나는 울면서 생각했다."], ["“In truth, perhaps, I am more than all others responsible for all, a greater sinner than all men in the world.”", "\"진실로, 어쩌면 나야말로 모든 사람보다 더 모든 일에 책임이 있는 자, 세상 어느 누구보다도 더 큰 죄인일지도 모른다.\""], ["And all at once the whole truth in its full light appeared to me; what was I going to do?", "그러자 별안간 온전한 진실이 그 충만한 빛 속에서 내게 모습을 드러냈다. 내가 지금 무엇을 하려는 것인가?"], ["I was going to kill a good, clever, noble man, who had done me no wrong, and by depriving his wife of happiness for the rest of her life, I should be torturing and killing her too.", "나는 나에게 아무런 잘못도 저지르지 않은, 선량하고 영리하며 고결한 사람을 죽이려 하고 있으며, 그의 아내에게서 남은 평생의 행복을 앗아 감으로써 그녀까지도 괴롭혀 죽이려는 것이었다."], ["I lay thus in my bed with my face in the pillow, heedless how the time was passing.", "나는 그렇게 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채 침대에 누워, 시간이 어떻게 흐르는지도 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Suddenly my second, the ensign, came in with the pistols to fetch me.", "그때 별안간 나의 입회인인 소위가 권총을 들고 나를 데리러 들어왔다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["said he, “it’s a good thing you are up already, it’s time we were off, come along!”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"벌써 일어나 있으니 다행이군, 떠날 시간이네, 자 가세!\""]], [["I did not know what to do and hurried to and fro undecided; we went out to the carriage, however.", "나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 마음을 정하지 못한 채 이리저리 서성거렸으나, 그래도 마차가 있는 곳으로 나갔다."]], [["“Wait here a minute,” I said to him.", "\"여기서 잠깐 기다려 주게,\""], ["“I’ll be back directly, I have forgotten my purse.”", "나는 그에게 말했다. \"곧 돌아오겠네, 지갑을 두고 왔어.\""]], [["And I ran back alone, to Afanasy’s little room.", "그러고는 나 혼자 아파나시의 작은 방으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Afanasy,”", "\"아파나시,\""], ["I said, “I gave you two blows on the face yesterday, forgive me,”", "나는 말했다. \"내가 어제 네 얼굴을 두 번 때렸으니, 나를 용서해 다오,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["He started as though he were frightened, and looked at me; and I saw that it was not enough, and on the spot, in my full officer’s uniform, I dropped at his feet and bowed my head to the ground.", "그는 깜짝 놀란 듯 흠칫하며 나를 바라보았다. 그래서 나는 그것만으로는 모자란다는 것을 깨닫고, 그 자리에서, 장교 정복을 차려입은 채, 그의 발치에 엎드려 이마가 땅에 닿도록 절을 했다."]], [["“Forgive me,”", "\"용서해 다오,\""], ["I said.", "나는 말했다."]], [["Then he was completely aghast.", "그러자 그는 완전히 넋이 나가고 말았다."]], [["“Your honor ...", "\"나리……"], ["sir, what are you doing?", "나리, 무슨 짓을 하시는 겁니까?"], ["Am I worth it?”", "제가 무슨 값어치가 있다고요?\""]], [["And he burst out crying as I had done before, hid this face in his hands, turned to the window and shook all over with his sobs.", "그러고는 그가, 조금 전 내가 그랬던 것처럼, 와락 울음을 터뜨리며 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 창 쪽으로 돌아서서는 흐느낌으로 온몸을 들썩였다."], ["I flew out to my comrade and jumped into the carriage.", "나는 동료에게로 달려 나가 마차에 뛰어올랐다."]], [["“Ready,”", "\"준비됐네,\""], ["I cried.", "나는 외쳤다."], ["“Have you ever seen a conqueror?”", "\"자네 정복자라는 걸 본 적이 있나?\""], ["I asked him.", "나는 그에게 물었다."], ["“Here is one before you.”", "\"여기 자네 앞에 하나 있네.\""]], [["I was in ecstasy, laughing and talking all the way, I don’t remember what about.", "나는 황홀경에 빠져, 가는 내내 웃고 떠들었는데, 무슨 이야기였는지는 기억나지 않는다."]], [["He looked at me.", "그는 나를 쳐다보았다."], ["“Well, brother, you are a plucky fellow, you’ll keep up the honor of the uniform, I can see.”", "\"이 친구, 자네 참 배짱 두둑한 사내로군, 자네라면 군복의 명예를 지켜 내겠어, 내 눈에는 그게 보이네.\""]], [["So we reached the place and found them there, waiting us.", "그렇게 우리는 그곳에 다다랐고, 그들이 우리를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["We were placed twelve paces apart; he had the first shot.", "우리는 열두 걸음 간격을 두고 마주 섰다. 첫 발은 그의 차례였다."], ["I stood gayly, looking him full in the face; I did not twitch an eyelash, I looked lovingly at him, for I knew what I would do.", "나는 즐거운 기색으로, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 서 있었다. 나는 눈썹 하나 까딱하지 않았고, 다정한 눈길로 그를 바라보았으니, 내가 무엇을 할지 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His shot just grazed my cheek and ear.", "그가 쏜 총알은 내 뺨과 귀를 스쳐 지나갔을 뿐이었다."]], [["“Thank God,”", "\"하느님 감사합니다,\""], ["I cried, “no man has been killed,”", "나는 외쳤다. \"아무도 죽지 않았습니다,\""], ["and I seized my pistol, turned back and flung it far away into the wood.", "그러고는 내 권총을 움켜쥐고 돌아서서 그것을 숲 속으로 멀리 던져 버렸다."], ["“That’s the place for you,”", "\"네가 있을 자리는 거기다,\""], ["I cried.", "나는 외쳤다."]], [["I turned to my adversary.", "나는 나의 상대 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Forgive me, young fool that I am, sir,”", "\"용서해 주십시오, 나야말로 어리석은 풋내기입니다,\""], ["I said, “for my unprovoked insult to you and for forcing you to fire at me.", "나는 말했다. \"까닭도 없이 당신을 모욕하고, 당신으로 하여금 나를 향해 총을 쏘게 만든 것을 용서해 주십시오."], ["I am ten times worse than you and more, maybe.", "나는 당신보다 열 곱절은 더 나쁜 인간이며, 어쩌면 그 이상일 겁니다."], ["Tell that to the person whom you hold dearest in the world.”", "부디 이 말을 당신이 세상에서 가장 소중히 여기는 분에게 전해 주십시오.\""]], [["I had no sooner said this than they all three shouted at me.", "내가 이 말을 마치기가 무섭게 세 사람이 일제히 나에게 소리를 질러 댔다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"이런 말도 안 되는,\""], ["cried my adversary, annoyed, “if you did not want to fight, why did not you let me alone?”", "나의 상대가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"싸울 마음이 없었다면, 어째서 나를 그냥 내버려 두지 않았소?\""]], [["“Yesterday I was a fool, to-day I know better,”", "\"어제의 저는 바보였습니다만, 오늘의 저는 좀 더 분별이 생겼습니다,\""], ["I answered him gayly.", "나는 즐거운 기색으로 그에게 대답했다."]], [["“As to yesterday, I believe you, but as for to-day, it is difficult to agree with your opinion,”", "\"어제의 일이라면 당신 말을 믿겠소만, 오늘의 일이라면 당신 의견에 동의하기 어렵구려,\""], ["said he.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["“Bravo,”", "\"브라보,\""], ["I cried, clapping my hands.", "나는 손뼉을 치며 외쳤다."], ["“I agree with you there too.", "\"그 점에서도 나는 당신 말에 동의합니다."], ["I have deserved it!”", "나는 그런 소리를 들어 마땅합니다!\""]], [["“Will you shoot, sir, or not?”", "\"쏘시겠소, 안 쏘시겠소?\""]], [["“No, I won’t,”", "\"아니요, 쏘지 않겠습니다,\""], ["I said; “if you like, fire at me again, but it would be better for you not to fire.”", "나는 말했다. \"원하신다면 다시 한번 나를 향해 쏘셔도 좋습니다만, 쏘지 않으시는 편이 당신에게 나을 겁니다.\""]], [["The seconds, especially mine, were shouting too: “Can you disgrace the regiment like this, facing your antagonist and begging his forgiveness!", "입회인들도, 특히 내 쪽의 입회인이 소리를 질렀다. \"이렇게 연대를 욕보일 수가 있나, 적수 앞에 서서 용서를 빌다니!"], ["If I’d only known this!”", "이럴 줄 알았더라면!\""]], [["I stood facing them all, not laughing now.", "나는 이제 웃지 않고, 그들 모두를 마주하고 섰다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["I said, “is it really so wonderful in these days to find a man who can repent of his stupidity and publicly confess his wrongdoing?”", "나는 말했다. \"요즘 세상에 자기의 어리석음을 뉘우치고 자기의 잘못을 사람들 앞에서 공공연히 고백할 수 있는 사람을 만나는 것이 그토록 놀라운 일입니까?\""]], [["“But not in a duel,”", "\"하지만 결투에서는 안 될 말이지,\""], ["cried my second again.", "내 쪽 입회인이 다시 외쳤다."]], [["“That’s what’s so strange,” I said. “For I ought to have owned my fault as soon as I got here, before he had fired a shot, before leading him into a great and deadly sin; but we have made our life so grotesque, that to act in that way would have been almost impossible, for only after I have faced his shot at the distance of twelve paces could my words have any significance for him, and if I had spoken before, he would have said, ‘He is a coward, the sight of the pistols has frightened him, no use to listen to him.’ Gentlemen,” I cried suddenly, speaking straight from my heart, “look around you at the gifts of God, the clear sky, the pure air, the tender grass, the birds; nature is beautiful and sinless, and we, only we, are sinful and foolish, and we don’t understand that life is heaven, for we have only to understand that and it will at once be fulfilled in all its beauty, we shall embrace each other and weep.”", "\"바로 그 점이 이상하다는 것입니다,\" 나는 말했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 이곳에 도착하자마자, 그가 한 발 쏘기 전에, 그를 크고도 치명적인 죄악으로 끌어들이기 전에 내 잘못을 인정했어야 마땅했으니까요. 그러나 우리는 우리의 삶을 너무도 기괴하게 만들어 놓았기에, 그런 식으로 행동하기란 거의 불가능한 일이었던 겁니다. 오로지 열두 걸음 거리에서 그의 총탄을 마주한 뒤에야 비로소 내 말이 그에게 어떤 의미를 지닐 수 있었던 것이지, 만약 내가 그 전에 말을 꺼냈더라면 그는 이렇게 말했을 겁니다. '저자는 겁쟁이로군, 권총을 보고는 겁을 집어먹은 거야, 저런 자 말은 들을 것도 없지.' 여러분,\" 나는 별안간, 마음에서 우러나오는 그대로 외쳤다. \"여러분의 둘레에 있는 하느님의 선물들을 둘러보십시오, 저 맑은 하늘, 저 깨끗한 공기, 저 보드라운 풀, 저 새들을. 자연은 아름답고 죄가 없는데, 우리만이, 오직 우리만이 죄 많고 어리석으며, 우리는 삶이 곧 천국임을 깨닫지 못하고 있습니다. 우리가 그것을 깨닫기만 하면, 그 즉시 삶은 그 온갖 아름다움 속에서 이루어질 것이고, 우리는 서로를 끌어안고 눈물을 흘릴 것입니다.\""]], [["I would have said more but I could not; my voice broke with the sweetness and youthful gladness of it, and there was such bliss in my heart as I had never known before in my life.", "나는 더 말하고 싶었으나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 그 달콤함과 젊은 환희에 목이 메어 왔고, 내 평생 한 번도 맛본 적 없는 그런 더없는 행복이 내 가슴에 가득 차올랐던 것이다."]], [["“All this as rational and edifying,”", "\"이 모든 말이 다 이치에 맞고 교훈적이로군요,\""], ["said my antagonist, “and in any case you are an original person.”", "나의 적수가 말했다. \"어쨌든 당신은 별난 사람이오.\""]], [["“You may laugh,”", "\"웃으셔도 좋습니다,\""], ["I said to him, laughing too, “but afterwards you will approve of me.”", "나도 함께 웃으며 그에게 말했다. \"하지만 나중에는 당신도 나를 좋게 여기게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, I am ready to approve of you now,”", "\"오, 지금이라도 당신을 좋게 여길 마음이 있소,\""], ["said he; “will you shake hands?", "그가 말했다. \"악수하지 않겠소?"], ["for I believe you are genuinely sincere.”", "내 보기에 당신은 진심으로 진실한 사람이니 말이오.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["I said, “not now, later on when I have grown worthier and deserve your esteem, then shake hands and you will do well.”", "나는 말했다. \"지금은 아닙니다, 나중에 내가 더 훌륭한 사람이 되어 당신의 존경을 받을 만해지면, 그때 악수하십시오, 그래야 옳을 겁니다.\""]], [["We went home, my second upbraiding me all the way, while I kissed him.", "우리는 집으로 돌아갔는데, 내 쪽 입회인은 가는 내내 나를 나무라댔고, 나는 그에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["All my comrades heard of the affair at once and gathered together to pass judgment on me the same day.", "내 동료들은 모두 그 일을 곧바로 전해 듣고, 바로 그날 한자리에 모여 나에 대한 판결을 내리려 했다."]], [["“He has disgraced the uniform,”", "\"저자가 군복을 욕보였다,\""], ["they said; “let him resign his commission.”", "그들은 말했다. \"장교직을 사임하게 하라.\""]], [["Some stood up for me: “He faced the shot,”", "몇몇은 나를 두둔했다. \"그는 총탄을 마주했어,\""], ["they said.", "그들은 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but he was afraid of his other shot and begged for forgiveness.”", "\"그렇긴 하지만, 그는 두 번째 총탄이 두려워 용서를 빈 거야.\""]], [["“If he had been afraid of being shot, he would have shot his own pistol first before asking forgiveness, while he flung it loaded into the forest.", "\"총에 맞을 게 두려웠다면, 용서를 빌기 전에 먼저 제 권총부터 쐈겠지, 그런데 그는 장전된 권총을 그대로 숲 속에 던져 버렸잖나."], ["No, there’s something else in this, something original.”", "아니, 이 일에는 뭔가 다른 게 있어, 뭔가 별난 데가 있다고.\""]], [["I enjoyed listening and looking at them.", "나는 그들의 말을 듣고 그들을 바라보는 것이 즐거웠다."], ["“My dear friends and comrades,”", "\"친애하는 벗들이여, 동료들이여,\""], ["said I, “don’t worry about my resigning my commission, for I have done so already.", "나는 말했다. \"내가 장교직을 사임하는 일이라면 걱정들 말게, 나는 이미 사임했으니까."], ["I have sent in my papers this morning and as soon as I get my discharge I shall go into a monastery—it’s with that object I am leaving the regiment.”", "나는 오늘 아침 사직서를 제출했고, 제대 허가가 나오는 대로 수도원에 들어갈 작정이라네—바로 그 목적으로 연대를 떠나는 걸세.\""]], [["When I had said this every one of them burst out laughing.", "내가 이 말을 하자, 그들은 하나같이 폭소를 터뜨렸다."]], [["“You should have told us of that first, that explains everything, we can’t judge a monk.”", "\"그 말을 먼저 했어야지, 그러면 모든 게 설명이 되잖나, 우리가 수도자를 판결할 수야 없지.\""]], [["They laughed and could not stop themselves, and not scornfully, but kindly and merrily.", "그들은 웃으며 그칠 줄을 몰랐는데, 그것은 비웃음이 아니라 다정하고 흥겨운 웃음이었다."], ["They all felt friendly to me at once, even those who had been sternest in their censure, and all the following month, before my discharge came, they could not make enough of me.", "그들은 모두 그 즉시 나에게 우정을 느꼈으니, 가장 매섭게 나를 비난하던 자들마저 그러했고, 제대 허가가 나오기 전까지의 한 달 내내 그들은 나를 더없이 떠받들어 주었다. \"이런, 우리 수도자 양반,\""], ["“Ah, you monk,” they would say.", "하고 그들은 말하곤 했다."], ["And every one said something kind to me, they began trying to dissuade me, even to pity me: “What are you doing to yourself?”", "그리고 저마다 나에게 다정한 말을 한마디씩 건넸으며, 나를 만류하려 들고, 심지어 나를 가엾게 여기기까지 했다. \"자네 대체 스스로에게 무슨 짓을 하려는 건가?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니,\""], ["they would say, “he is a brave fellow, he faced fire and could have fired his own pistol too, but he had a dream the night before that he should become a monk, that’s why he did it.”", "그들은 말하곤 했다. \"이 친구는 용감한 사내야, 포화를 마주하고도 제 권총을 쏠 수 있었는데, 그 전날 밤 수도자가 되는 꿈을 꾸었던 거지, 그래서 그렇게 한 거라고.\""]], [["It was the same thing with the society of the town.", "고을의 사교계에서도 사정은 마찬가지였다."], ["Till then I had been kindly received, but had not been the object of special attention, and now all came to know me at once and invited me; they laughed at me, but they loved me.", "그때까지 나는 따뜻한 환대를 받기는 했으나 특별한 주목의 대상은 아니었는데, 이제는 모두가 단번에 나를 알게 되어 나를 초대하기 시작했으니, 그들은 나를 비웃으면서도 나를 사랑했다."], ["I may mention that although everybody talked openly of our duel, the authorities took no notice of it, because my antagonist was a near relation of our general, and as there had been no bloodshed and no serious consequences, and as I resigned my commission, they took it as a joke.", "한 가지 언급해 둘 것은, 비록 모두가 우리의 결투에 대해 공공연히 떠들어 댔으나, 당국은 그것을 문제 삼지 않았다는 사실이다. 나의 적수가 우리 장군의 가까운 친척이었던 데다, 유혈도 없고 심각한 후과도 없었으며, 게다가 내가 장교직을 사임했으므로, 그들은 그것을 한낱 농담쯤으로 받아들였던 것이다."], ["And I began then to speak aloud and fearlessly, regardless of their laughter, for it was always kindly and not spiteful laughter.", "그래서 나는 그때부터 그들의 웃음에 아랑곳하지 않고 거리낌 없이 큰 소리로 이야기하기 시작했으니, 그것은 언제나 다정한 웃음이지 악의에 찬 웃음이 아니었기 때문이다."], ["These conversations mostly took place in the evenings, in the company of ladies; women particularly liked listening to me then and they made the men listen.", "이런 대화는 대개 저녁에, 부인들과 함께한 자리에서 이루어졌으니, 그 무렵 특히 여자들이 내 이야기 듣기를 좋아하여 남자들에게도 듣게끔 만들었다."]], [["“But how can I possibly be responsible for all?”", "\"하지만 제가 어떻게 모든 일에 책임이 있을 수 있단 말이에요?\""], ["every one would laugh in my face.", "저마다 내 면전에서 웃어 댔다."], ["“Can I, for instance, be responsible for you?”", "\"이를테면 제가 당신에 대해서까지 책임이 있다는 건가요?\""]], [["“You may well not know it,”", "\"당신이 그것을 모르는 것도 무리는 아닙니다,\""], ["I would answer, “since the whole world has long been going on a different line, since we consider the veriest lies as truth and demand the same lies from others.", "나는 대답하곤 했다. \"온 세상이 오래전부터 다른 길을 걸어왔고, 우리가 새빨간 거짓을 진실로 여기며 남들에게도 똑같은 거짓을 요구하고 있으니 말입니다."], ["Here I have for once in my life acted sincerely and, well, you all look upon me as a madman.", "자, 나는 내 평생 단 한 번 진심으로 행동했는데, 그랬더니, 보십시오, 당신들 모두 나를 미치광이로 여기고 있지 않습니까."], ["Though you are friendly to me, yet, you see, you all laugh at me.”", "당신들이 나에게 우정을 품고 있으면서도, 보시다시피, 모두 나를 비웃고 있는 거지요.\""]], [["“But how can we help being friendly to you?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 당신에게 우정을 품지 않을 수 있겠어요?\""], ["said my hostess, laughing.", "안주인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["The room was full of people.", "방 안은 사람들로 가득했다."], ["All of a sudden the young lady rose, on whose account the duel had been fought and whom only lately I had intended to be my future wife.", "그때 별안간, 우리가 결투를 벌인 까닭이 되었던, 그리고 얼마 전까지만 해도 내가 장차 아내로 삼으려 했던 그 젊은 부인이 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["I had not noticed her coming into the room.", "나는 그녀가 방에 들어온 것을 알아채지 못하고 있었다."], ["She got up, came to me and held out her hand.", "그녀는 일어나, 나에게 다가와 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Let me tell you,”", "\"제 말 좀 들어 주세요,\""], ["she said, “that I am the first not to laugh at you, but on the contrary I thank you with tears and express my respect for you for your action then.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 당신을 비웃지 않는 첫 번째 사람이에요, 오히려 그 반대로, 눈물을 흘리며 당신에게 감사드리고, 그때 당신이 보여 준 그 행동에 대해 존경을 표하고 싶어요.\""]], [["Her husband, too, came up and then they all approached me and almost kissed me.", "그녀의 남편도 다가왔고, 그러자 모두가 나에게로 몰려와 거의 입을 맞출 듯이 했다."], ["My heart was filled with joy, but my attention was especially caught by a middle-aged man who came up to me with the others.", "내 가슴은 기쁨으로 가득 찼으나, 다른 사람들과 함께 나에게 다가온 어느 중년 남자가 특히 내 주의를 끌었다."], ["I knew him by name already, but had never made his acquaintance nor exchanged a word with him till that evening.", "나는 이미 이름으로는 그를 알고 있었으나, 그날 저녁까지 그와 사귀어 본 적도, 말 한마디 나누어 본 적도 없었다."]], [["(d) The Mysterious Visitor", "(라) 수수께끼의 방문자"]], [["He had long been an official in the town; he was in a prominent position, respected by all, rich and had a reputation for benevolence.", "그는 오래전부터 그 고을의 관리였으니, 두드러진 지위에 있어 모두에게 존경받았고, 부유했으며, 자선가라는 평판이 있었다."], ["He subscribed considerable sums to the almshouse and the orphan asylum; he was very charitable, too, in secret, a fact which only became known after his death.", "그는 양로원과 고아원에 상당한 액수를 기부했고, 남몰래도 매우 자선을 베풀었는데, 이 사실은 그가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 알려졌다."], ["He was a man of about fifty, almost stern in appearance and not much given to conversation.", "그는 쉰 살가량의 사람으로, 거의 근엄해 보이는 풍모에 말수가 많지 않았다."], ["He had been married about ten years and his wife, who was still young, had borne him three children.", "그는 결혼한 지 십 년쯤 되었는데, 아직 젊은 그의 아내가 그에게 세 자녀를 낳아 주었다."], ["Well, I was sitting alone in my room the following evening, when my door suddenly opened and this gentleman walked in.", "그런데 이튿날 저녁 내가 방에 홀로 앉아 있을 때, 별안간 문이 열리며 이 신사가 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["I must mention, by the way, that I was no longer living in my former quarters.", "그건 그렇고 한 가지 말해 둘 것은, 나는 더 이상 예전의 거처에서 살고 있지 않았다는 사실이다."], ["As soon as I resigned my commission, I took rooms with an old lady, the widow of a government clerk.", "장교직을 사임하자마자 나는 어느 노부인의 집에 방을 얻었는데, 그녀는 어느 관청 서기의 미망인이었다."], ["My landlady’s servant waited upon me, for I had moved into her rooms simply because on my return from the duel I had sent Afanasy back to the regiment, as I felt ashamed to look him in the face after my last interview with him.", "집주인 노부인의 하녀가 내 시중을 들었으니, 내가 그녀의 집으로 옮긴 까닭은 다름이 아니라, 결투에서 돌아온 뒤 아파나시를 연대로 돌려보냈기 때문이었다. 마지막으로 그와 마주한 뒤로는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보기가 부끄러웠던 것이다."], ["So prone is the man of the world to be ashamed of any righteous action.", "세상 사람이란 이토록 의로운 행동을 부끄러워하기 쉬운 법이다."]], [["“I have,”", "\"나는,\""], ["said my visitor, “with great interest listened to you speaking in different houses the last few days and I wanted at last to make your personal acquaintance, so as to talk to you more intimately.", "하고 나의 방문자가 말했다. \"지난 며칠 동안 여러 집에서 당신이 이야기하는 것을 깊은 관심을 가지고 들어 왔는데, 마침내 당신과 직접 사귀어 좀 더 허물없이 이야기를 나누고 싶어졌습니다."], ["Can you, dear sir, grant me this favor?”", "선생, 부디 나에게 그런 호의를 베풀어 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I can, with the greatest pleasure, and I shall look upon it as an honor.”", "\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다, 오히려 그것을 영광으로 여기겠습니다.\""], ["I said this, though I felt almost dismayed, so greatly was I impressed from the first moment by the appearance of this man.", "나는 이렇게 말했으나, 거의 당혹스러운 심정이었으니, 첫 순간부터 이 사람의 풍모에 그토록 크게 압도되었던 것이다."], ["For though other people had listened to me with interest and attention, no one had come to me before with such a serious, stern and concentrated expression.", "다른 사람들도 관심과 주의를 기울여 내 이야기를 듣기는 했으나, 그토록 진지하고 근엄하며 골똘한 표정으로 나에게 다가온 사람은 일찍이 없었던 것이다."], ["And now he had come to see me in my own rooms.", "게다가 이제 그가 내 방으로 직접 나를 찾아온 것이었다."], ["He sat down.", "그는 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“You are, I see, a man of great strength of character,”", "\"내가 보기에 당신은 대단히 강인한 성품을 지닌 사람이오,\""], ["he said; “as you have dared to serve the truth, even when by doing so you risked incurring the contempt of all.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 그렇게 함으로써 만인의 멸시를 감수할 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 감히 진실을 섬길 줄 알았으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Your praise is, perhaps, excessive,”", "\"당신의 칭찬은 어쩌면 지나치십니다,\""], ["I replied.", "나는 대답했다."]], [["“No, it’s not excessive,”", "\"아니요, 지나치지 않소,\""], ["he answered; “believe me, such a course of action is far more difficult than you think.", "그가 대답했다. \"내 말을 믿으시오, 그런 식으로 행동하기란 당신이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 어려운 일이오."], ["It is that which has impressed me, and it is only on that account that I have come to you,”", "바로 그것이 나에게 깊은 인상을 주었고, 오로지 그 때문에 내가 당신을 찾아온 것이오,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued. “Tell me, please, that is if you are not annoyed by my perhaps unseemly curiosity, what were your exact sensations, if you can recall them, at the moment when you made up your mind to ask forgiveness at the duel.", "\"한 가지 말씀해 주시오, 혹시 나의, 어쩌면 무례할지도 모를 호기심에 언짢지 않으시다면 말입니다만, 당신이 결투에서 용서를 빌기로 마음을 정한 그 순간, 만약 그것을 기억해 낼 수 있다면, 당신의 정확한 심정이 어떠했는지 말이오."], ["Do not think my question frivolous; on the contrary, I have in asking the question a secret motive of my own, which I will perhaps explain to you later on, if it is God’s will that we should become more intimately acquainted.”", "나의 물음을 경박하게 여기지 마시오. 오히려 나는 이 물음을 던지는 데에 나 나름의 은밀한 동기가 있으며, 만약 우리가 더 가까이 사귀게 되는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라면, 어쩌면 나중에 그것을 당신에게 설명할 수도 있을 것이오.\""]], [["All the while he was speaking, I was looking at him straight into the face and I felt all at once a complete trust in him and great curiosity on my side also, for I felt that there was some strange secret in his soul.", "그가 이야기하는 내내, 나는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 별안간 그에 대한 온전한 신뢰와 더불어 나 자신도 큰 호기심을 느꼈으니, 그의 영혼 속에 무언가 기이한 비밀이 있다는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["“You ask what were my exact sensations at the moment when I asked my opponent’s forgiveness,”", "\"내가 적수에게 용서를 빌던 그 순간의 정확한 심정이 어떠했는지 물으시는군요,\" 나는 대답했다."], ["I answered; “but I had better tell you from the beginning what I have not yet told any one else.”", "\"하지만 그보다 먼저, 내가 아직 그 누구에게도 이야기한 적 없는 것을 처음부터 당신에게 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""], ["And I described all that had passed between Afanasy and me, and how I had bowed down to the ground at his feet.", "그러고는 나는 아파나시와 나 사이에 있었던 모든 일을, 그리고 내가 어떻게 그의 발치에 엎드려 이마가 땅에 닿도록 절했는지를 낱낱이 이야기했다."], ["“From that you can see for yourself,”", "\"그러니 당신도 친히 아실 수 있을 겁니다,\""], ["I concluded, “that at the time of the duel it was easier for me, for I had made a beginning already at home, and when once I had started on that road, to go farther along it was far from being difficult, but became a source of joy and happiness.”", "나는 맺음말을 했다. \"결투할 때에는 오히려 한결 수월했다는 것을 말이지요, 나는 이미 집에서 그 첫걸음을 떼어 두었던 터라, 일단 그 길로 들어선 이상, 그 길을 더 멀리 나아가기란 결코 어렵기는커녕 도리어 기쁨과 행복의 샘이 되었던 것입니다.\""]], [["I liked the way he looked at me as he listened. “All that,”", "그가 내 이야기를 들으며 나를 바라보던 그 눈길이 나는 마음에 들었다."], ["he said, “is exceedingly interesting.", "\"그 모든 것이,\" 그가 말했다."], ["I will come to see you again and again.”", "\"지극히 흥미롭소. 내 다시 또다시 당신을 찾아오겠소.\""]], [["And from that time forth he came to see me nearly every evening.", "그리고 그때부터 그는 거의 매일 저녁 나를 찾아왔다."], ["And we should have become greater friends, if only he had ever talked of himself.", "그리고 만약 그가 자기 자신에 관해 단 한 번이라도 이야기를 했더라면, 우리는 더 가까운 벗이 되었을 것이다."], ["But about himself he scarcely ever said a word, yet continually asked me about myself.", "그러나 자기 자신에 관해서는 그가 좀처럼 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그러면서도 끊임없이 나에 관해 물었다."], ["In spite of that I became very fond of him and spoke with perfect frankness to him about all my feelings; “for,”", "그럼에도 불구하고 나는 그를 무척 좋아하게 되어, 내 모든 감정에 관해 그에게 완전히 솔직하게 털어놓았다. \"왜냐하면,\""], ["thought I, “what need have I to know his secrets, since I can see without that that he is a good man?", "하고 나는 생각했다. \"내가 무엇 때문에 그의 비밀을 알 필요가 있겠는가, 그것 없이도 그가 선량한 사람이라는 걸 알 수 있는데?"], ["Moreover, though he is such a serious man and my senior, he comes to see a youngster like me and treats me as his equal.”", "게다가 그는 그토록 진지한 사람이고 나보다 손위인데도, 나 같은 풋내기를 찾아와 나를 동등한 사람으로 대해 주지 않는가.\""], ["And I learned a great deal that was profitable from him, for he was a man of lofty mind.", "그리고 나는 그에게서 유익한 것을 매우 많이 배웠으니, 그는 드높은 정신을 지닌 사람이었던 것이다."]], [["“That life is heaven,”", "\"삶이 곧 천국이라는 것,\""], ["he said to me suddenly, “that I have long been thinking about”; and all at once he added, “I think of nothing else indeed.”", "하고 그가 별안간 나에게 말했다. \"그것을 나는 오래전부터 생각해 왔소.\""], ["He looked at me and smiled.", "그러고는 별안간 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"실로 나는 그것 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않소.\""], ["“I am more convinced of it than you are, I will tell you later why.”", "그는 나를 바라보며 미소 지었다. \"나는 당신보다 그것을 더 확신하고 있소, 그 까닭은 나중에 말해 주겠소.\""]], [["I listened to him and thought that he evidently wanted to tell me something.", "나는 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그가 분명 나에게 무언가를 말하고 싶어 한다고 생각했다."]], [["“Heaven,”", "\"천국은,\""], ["he went on, “lies hidden within all of us—here it lies hidden in me now, and if I will it, it will be revealed to me to-morrow and for all time.”", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리 모두의 안에 숨겨져 있소—지금 내 안에도 그것이 숨겨져 있으며, 만약 내가 원하기만 하면, 그것은 내일이라도, 그리고 영원토록 나에게 드러날 것이오.\""]], [["I looked at him; he was speaking with great emotion and gazing mysteriously at me, as if he were questioning me.", "나는 그를 바라보았다. 그는 깊은 격정에 차서 이야기하며, 마치 나에게 무언가를 묻기라도 하듯 수수께끼 같은 눈길로 나를 응시하고 있었다."]], [["“And that we are all responsible to all for all, apart from our own sins, you were quite right in thinking that, and it is wonderful how you could comprehend it in all its significance at once.", "\"그리고 우리 모두가, 우리 자신의 죄와는 별개로, 모든 이에 대해 모든 일에 책임이 있다는 것, 당신이 그렇게 생각한 것은 지극히 옳았소, 그리고 당신이 그 온전한 의미를 단번에 깨달을 수 있었다는 것은 놀라운 일이오."], ["And in very truth, so soon as men understand that, the Kingdom of Heaven will be for them not a dream, but a living reality.”", "그리고 진실로, 사람들이 그것을 깨닫는 순간, 천국은 그들에게 한낱 꿈이 아니라 살아 있는 현실이 될 것이오.\""]], [["“And when,”", "\"그렇다면 언제,\""], ["I cried out to him bitterly, “when will that come to pass?", "나는 그에게 비통하게 외쳤다. \"언제 그런 일이 일어날까요?"], ["and will it ever come to pass?", "그리고 그런 일이 과연 일어나기는 할까요?"], ["Is not it simply a dream of ours?”", "그것은 그저 우리의 꿈에 지나지 않는 것이 아닐까요?\""]], [["“What then, you don’t believe it,”", "\"그렇다면, 당신은 그것을 믿지 않는구려,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You preach it and don’t believe it yourself.", "\"당신은 그것을 설교하면서 정작 당신 자신은 믿지 않는 것이오."], ["Believe me, this dream, as you call it, will come to pass without doubt; it will come, but not now, for every process has its law.", "내 말을 믿으시오, 당신이 꿈이라 부르는 이것은 의심할 바 없이 이루어질 것이오. 그것은 오기는 오겠으나, 지금은 아니니, 모든 과정에는 저마다의 법칙이 있기 때문이오."], ["It’s a spiritual, psychological process.", "그것은 영적이고 심리적인 과정이오."], ["To transform the world, to recreate it afresh, men must turn into another path psychologically.", "세상을 변화시키고, 그것을 새로이 다시 빚으려면, 사람들은 심리적으로 다른 길로 돌아서야만 하오."], ["Until you have become really, in actual fact, a brother to every one, brotherhood will not come to pass.", "당신이 정말로, 실제로, 모든 사람에게 형제가 되기 전에는, 형제애란 이루어지지 않을 것이오."], ["No sort of scientific teaching, no kind of common interest, will ever teach men to share property and privileges with equal consideration for all.", "그 어떤 과학적 가르침도, 그 어떤 공동의 이익도 사람들로 하여금 모두를 동등하게 배려하며 재산과 특권을 나누도록 가르치지는 못할 것이오."], ["Every one will think his share too small and they will be always envying, complaining and attacking one another.", "저마다 자기 몫이 너무 적다고 여길 것이며, 그들은 늘 서로를 시기하고, 불평하고, 헐뜯을 것이오."], ["You ask when it will come to pass; it will come to pass, but first we have to go through the period of isolation.”", "당신은 그것이 언제 이루어지겠느냐고 묻는데, 그것은 이루어질 것이나, 다만 그에 앞서 우리는 고립의 시대를 거쳐야만 하오.\""]], [["“What do you mean by isolation?”", "\"고립이라니, 무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀입니까?\""], ["I asked him.", "나는 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Why, the isolation that prevails everywhere, above all in our age—it has not fully developed, it has not reached its limit yet.", "\"다름 아니라, 도처에, 무엇보다도 우리 시대에 만연한 그 고립 말이오—그것은 아직 온전히 무르익지 않았고, 아직 그 한계에 다다르지 않았소."], ["For every one strives to keep his individuality as apart as possible, wishes to secure the greatest possible fullness of life for himself; but meantime all his efforts result not in attaining fullness of life but self-destruction, for instead of self-realization he ends by arriving at complete solitude.", "왜냐하면 저마다 자기의 개성을 되도록 떼어 놓으려 애쓰고, 자기 자신을 위해 되도록 충만한 삶을 누리고자 하기 때문이오. 그러나 그러는 동안 그의 온갖 노력은 삶의 충만함에 이르기는커녕 오히려 자기 파멸로 귀결되니, 자기 실현 대신 결국 완전한 고독에 다다르고 마는 것이오."], ["All mankind in our age have split up into units, they all keep apart, each in his own groove; each one holds aloof, hides himself and hides what he has, from the rest, and he ends by being repelled by others and repelling them.", "우리 시대의 온 인류는 단위들로 산산이 쪼개져, 저마다 따로따로 자기만의 골 속에 틀어박혀 있소. 저마다 남들과 거리를 두고, 자기 자신을 숨기며, 자기가 가진 것을 나머지 사람들에게서 감추다가, 마침내 남들에게 배척당하고 또 남들을 배척하기에 이르오."], ["He heaps up riches by himself and thinks, ‘How strong I am now and how secure,’ and in his madness he does not understand that the more he heaps up, the more he sinks into self-destructive impotence.", "그는 혼자서 재물을 쌓아 올리고는 '이제 나는 얼마나 강하고 얼마나 든든한가' 하고 생각하지만, 그 어리석음 속에서 정작 깨닫지 못하는 것은, 더 많이 쌓아 올릴수록 그만큼 더 자기 파멸의 무력함 속으로 가라앉는다는 사실이오."], ["For he is accustomed to rely upon himself alone and to cut himself off from the whole; he has trained himself not to believe in the help of others, in men and in humanity, and only trembles for fear he should lose his money and the privileges that he has won for himself.", "왜냐하면 그는 오로지 자기 자신만을 의지하고 전체로부터 자기를 떼어 놓는 데 익숙해진 데다, 남들의 도움을, 사람을, 그리고 인류를 믿지 않도록 자기를 길들였으며, 오로지 자기가 손에 넣은 돈과 특권을 잃을까 봐 벌벌 떨 따름이기 때문이오."], ["Everywhere in these days men have, in their mockery, ceased to understand that the true security is to be found in social solidarity rather than in isolated individual effort.", "요즘 세상 도처에서 사람들은, 그 조롱 속에서, 참된 든든함이란 고립된 개인의 노력보다는 사회적 결속 속에 있다는 사실을 이해하기를 그만두어 버렸소."], ["But this terrible individualism must inevitably have an end, and all will suddenly understand how unnaturally they are separated from one another.", "그러나 이 끔찍한 개인주의는 반드시 종말을 맞이할 수밖에 없을 것이며, 그러면 모두가 별안간 자기들이 서로에게서 얼마나 부자연스럽게 갈라져 있었는지를 깨달을 것이오."], ["It will be the spirit of the time, and people will marvel that they have sat so long in darkness without seeing the light.", "그것은 시대의 정신이 될 것이며, 사람들은 자기들이 빛을 보지 못한 채 그토록 오래도록 어둠 속에 앉아 있었다는 사실에 경탄할 것이오."], ["And then the sign of the Son of Man will be seen in the heavens....", "그리고 그때 하늘에 인자(人子)의 표징이 나타나리라.……"], ["But, until then, we must keep the banner flying.", "그러나 그때까지 우리는 깃발을 높이 들고 있어야만 하오."], ["Sometimes even if he has to do it alone, and his conduct seems to be crazy, a man must set an example, and so draw men’s souls out of their solitude, and spur them to some act of brotherly love, that the great idea may not die.”", "때로는, 비록 그 일을 혼자서 해야 하고 그의 행동이 미친 짓처럼 보일지라도, 한 사람이 모범을 보여, 그리하여 사람들의 영혼을 고독으로부터 끌어내고, 그들에게 어떤 형제애의 행위로 박차를 가하여, 그 위대한 이념이 죽어 버리지 않도록 해야만 하는 것이오.\""]], [["Our evenings, one after another, were spent in such stirring and fervent talk.", "우리의 저녁은 그렇게 하루하루, 가슴을 뒤흔드는 열정적인 대화로 채워졌다."], ["I gave up society and visited my neighbors much less frequently.", "나는 사교계를 그만두고, 이웃들을 찾는 일도 훨씬 뜸해졌다."], ["Besides, my vogue was somewhat over.", "게다가 나의 인기도 어느 정도 시들해진 터였다."], ["I say this, not as blame, for they still loved me and treated me good-humoredly, but there’s no denying that fashion is a great power in society.", "이런 말을 하는 것은 그들을 탓해서가 아니니, 그들은 여전히 나를 사랑하고 너그럽게 대해 주었으나, 유행이 사교계에서 큰 힘을 발휘한다는 것만큼은 부정할 수 없는 노릇이다."], ["I began to regard my mysterious visitor with admiration, for besides enjoying his intelligence, I began to perceive that he was brooding over some plan in his heart, and was preparing himself perhaps for a great deed.", "나는 이 수수께끼 같은 방문자를 차츰 감탄의 눈으로 바라보기 시작했으니, 그의 총명함을 즐기는 것에 더해, 그가 마음속에 어떤 계획을 곱씹으며 어쩌면 위대한 행위를 준비하고 있다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["Perhaps he liked my not showing curiosity about his secret, not seeking to discover it by direct question nor by insinuation.", "어쩌면 그는 내가 그의 비밀에 대해 호기심을 드러내지 않고, 직접적인 물음으로도 넌지시 떠보는 것으로도 그것을 캐내려 들지 않는 점을 마음에 들어 했는지도 모른다."], ["But I noticed at last, that he seemed to show signs of wanting to tell me something.", "그러나 나는 마침내, 그가 나에게 무언가를 말하고 싶어 하는 기색을 내비치는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["This had become quite evident, indeed, about a month after he first began to visit me.", "실로 이것은, 그가 나를 처음 찾아오기 시작한 지 한 달쯤 지나자 제법 분명해졌다."]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"당신도 아시오,\""], ["he said to me once, “that people are very inquisitive about us in the town and wonder why I come to see you so often.", "하고 그가 언젠가 나에게 말했다. \"고을 사람들이 우리에 대해 무척 궁금해하며, 내가 어째서 그토록 자주 당신을 찾아오는지 의아해한다는 것을 말이오."], ["But let them wonder, for soon all will be explained.”", "하지만 의아해하라고 내버려 두시오, 머지않아 모든 것이 밝혀질 테니까.\""]], [["Sometimes an extraordinary agitation would come over him, and almost always on such occasions he would get up and go away.", "이따금 그에게 예사롭지 않은 동요가 엄습하곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 거의 언제나 그는 일어나서 가 버렸다."], ["Sometimes he would fix a long piercing look upon me, and I thought, “He will say something directly now.”", "이따금 그는 나를 오래도록 꿰뚫는 눈길로 응시하곤 했고, 나는 '이제 곧 무슨 말인가를 하겠구나' 하고 생각하곤 했다."], ["But he would suddenly begin talking of something ordinary and familiar.", "그러나 그는 별안간 무언가 평범하고 익숙한 이야기를 꺼내곤 했다."], ["He often complained of headache too.", "그는 또 자주 두통을 호소했다."]], [["One day, quite unexpectedly indeed, after he had been talking with great fervor a long time, I saw him suddenly turn pale, and his face worked convulsively, while he stared persistently at me.", "어느 날, 실로 전혀 뜻밖에도, 그가 오랫동안 깊은 열정에 차서 이야기를 하던 끝에, 나는 그가 별안간 창백해지는 것을 보았는데, 그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러지면서, 그는 줄곧 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""], ["I said; “do you feel ill?”—he had just been complaining of headache.", "나는 말했다. \"어디 편찮으신가요?\"—그는 방금 두통을 호소하던 참이었다."]], [["“I ...", "\"나는……"], ["do you know ...", "그러니까……"], ["I murdered some one.”", "나는 사람을 죽였소.\""]], [["He said this and smiled with a face as white as chalk.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는, 백묵처럼 새하얀 얼굴로 미소를 지었다."], ["“Why is it he is smiling?”", "'저 사람은 어째서 웃고 있는 걸까?'"], ["The thought flashed through my mind before I realized anything else.", "다른 무엇을 깨닫기도 전에 이 생각이 내 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["I too turned pale.", "나도 창백해졌다."]], [["“What are you saying?”", "\"무슨 말씀을 하시는 겁니까?\""], ["I cried.", "나는 외쳤다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보다시피,\""], ["he said, with a pale smile, “how much it has cost me to say the first word.", "그가 창백한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그 첫 마디를 입에 올리는 데 내가 얼마나 큰 대가를 치렀는지 말이오."], ["Now I have said it, I feel I’ve taken the first step and shall go on.”", "이제 그 말을 했으니, 나는 첫걸음을 떼었다는 느낌이 들고, 앞으로 계속 나아가게 될 것이오.\""]], [["For a long while I could not believe him, and I did not believe him at that time, but only after he had been to see me three days running and told me all about it.", "나는 한참 동안 그의 말을 믿을 수가 없었고, 그때만 해도 그를 믿지 못했으나, 그가 사흘 연이어 나를 찾아와 그 모든 것을 낱낱이 이야기한 뒤에야 비로소 믿게 되었다."], ["I thought he was mad, but ended by being convinced, to my great grief and amazement.", "나는 그가 미친 것이라고 생각했으나, 마침내, 크나큰 비통함과 경악 속에서, 확신하기에 이르렀다."], ["His crime was a great and terrible one.", "그의 죄는 크고도 끔찍한 것이었다."]], [["Fourteen years before, he had murdered the widow of a landowner, a wealthy and handsome young woman who had a house in our town.", "십사 년 전, 그는 어느 지주의 미망인을 죽였으니, 그녀는 우리 고을에 저택을 한 채 가지고 있던 부유하고 아름다운 젊은 여인이었다."], ["He fell passionately in love with her, declared his feeling and tried to persuade her to marry him.", "그는 그녀에게 열렬히 반하여, 자기 마음을 고백하고 그녀에게 자기와 결혼해 달라고 설득하려 했다."], ["But she had already given her heart to another man, an officer of noble birth and high rank in the service, who was at that time away at the front, though she was expecting him soon to return.", "그러나 그녀는 이미 다른 남자에게 마음을 주고 있었으니, 그는 명문 출신에 군에서 높은 계급에 있는 장교로, 당시에는 전선에 나가 있었으나, 그녀는 그가 머지않아 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She refused his offer and begged him not to come and see her.", "그녀는 그의 청혼을 거절하고 자기를 찾아오지 말아 달라고 간청했다."], ["After he had ceased to visit her, he took advantage of his knowledge of the house to enter at night through the garden by the roof, at great risk of discovery.", "그가 그녀를 찾는 일을 그만둔 뒤, 그는 그 저택에 대해 자기가 알고 있는 바를 이용해, 발각될 위험이 큰데도 밤중에 정원을 통해 지붕으로 잠입했다."], ["But, as often happens, a crime committed with extraordinary audacity is more successful than others.", "그러나 흔히 그렇듯, 유달리 대담하게 저질러진 범행이 오히려 다른 경우보다 더 잘 성공하는 법이다."]], [["Entering the garret through the skylight, he went down the ladder, knowing that the door at the bottom of it was sometimes, through the negligence of the servants, left unlocked.", "천창을 통해 다락방으로 들어선 그는 사다리를 타고 내려갔는데, 그 사다리 아래쪽의 문이 이따금 하인들의 부주의로 잠기지 않은 채 있다는 것을 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["He hoped to find it so, and so it was.", "그는 그러기를 바랐고, 과연 그러했다."], ["He made his way in the dark to her bedroom, where a light was burning.", "그는 어둠 속을 더듬어 그녀의 침실로 들어갔는데, 그곳에는 불이 켜져 있었다."], ["As though on purpose, both her maids had gone off to a birthday-party in the same street, without asking leave.", "마치 일부러 그런 듯, 그녀의 두 하녀는 허락도 받지 않고 같은 거리에서 열린 생일잔치에 가 버린 뒤였다."], ["The other servants slept in the servants’ quarters or in the kitchen on the ground-floor.", "다른 하인들은 일층의 하인 방이나 부엌에서 잠들어 있었다."], ["His passion flamed up at the sight of her asleep, and then vindictive, jealous anger took possession of his heart, and like a drunken man, beside himself, he thrust a knife into her heart, so that she did not even cry out.", "잠든 그녀의 모습을 보자 그의 정욕이 활활 타올랐고, 그러다 앙심에 찬 질투의 분노가 그의 마음을 사로잡아, 마치 술에 취한 사람처럼 제정신이 아닌 채로, 그는 칼을 그녀의 심장에 찔러 넣었으니, 그녀는 비명조차 지르지 못했다."], ["Then with devilish and criminal cunning he contrived that suspicion should fall on the servants.", "그러고는 악마같이 교활한 범죄자의 간계로, 그는 하인들에게 혐의가 쏠리도록 꾸몄다."], ["He was so base as to take her purse, to open her chest with keys from under her pillow, and to take some things from it, doing it all as it might have been done by an ignorant servant, leaving valuable papers and taking only money.", "그는 비열하게도 그녀의 지갑을 챙기고, 베개 밑에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 그녀의 궤를 열어 그 안에서 몇 가지 물건을 꺼냈는데, 무지한 하인이 했음 직하게 이 모든 일을 해냈으니, 값진 서류는 그대로 둔 채 돈만 챙긴 것이다."], ["He took some of the larger gold things, but left smaller articles that were ten times as valuable.", "그는 큼직한 금붙이 몇 가지를 가져갔으나, 그 열 곱절은 더 값진 자잘한 물건들은 그냥 두었다."], ["He took with him, too, some things for himself as remembrances, but of that later.", "그는 또한 자기 자신을 위해 기념이 될 만한 몇 가지 물건도 가지고 갔는데, 그 이야기는 나중에 하겠다."], ["Having done this awful deed, he returned by the way he had come.", "이 끔찍한 일을 저지른 뒤, 그는 왔던 길로 되돌아 나갔다."]], [["Neither the next day, when the alarm was raised, nor at any time after in his life, did any one dream of suspecting that he was the criminal.", "이튿날 소동이 벌어졌을 때에도, 그리고 그 후 그의 평생 어느 때에도, 그가 범인이라고 의심하는 꿈을 꾼 자는 단 한 사람도 없었다."], ["No one indeed knew of his love for her, for he was always reserved and silent and had no friend to whom he would have opened his heart.", "실로 아무도 그가 그녀를 사랑했다는 사실을 알지 못했으니, 그는 늘 말이 없고 과묵하여 자기 마음을 털어놓을 만한 벗조차 없었기 때문이다."], ["He was looked upon simply as an acquaintance, and not a very intimate one, of the murdered woman, as for the previous fortnight he had not even visited her.", "사람들은 그를 그저 살해된 여인의 지인, 그것도 그리 가깝지 않은 지인쯤으로 여겼으니, 지난 보름 동안 그는 그녀를 찾아간 적조차 없었던 것이다."], ["A serf of hers called Pyotr was at once suspected, and every circumstance confirmed the suspicion.", "그녀의 농노인 표트르라는 자가 즉각 의심을 받았고, 모든 정황이 그 의심을 뒷받침했다."], ["The man knew—indeed his mistress did not conceal the fact—that having to send one of her serfs as a recruit she had decided to send him, as he had no relations and his conduct was unsatisfactory.", "그 사내는—실은 그 여주인이 그 사실을 숨기지도 않았으므로—자기 농노들 가운데 하나를 신병으로 보내야 하게 되자 그녀가 바로 그를 보내기로 마음먹었다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그에게 친척도 없고 행실도 변변치 못했기 때문이다."], ["People had heard him angrily threatening to murder her when he was drunk in a tavern.", "사람들은 그가 어느 선술집에서 술에 취해 그녀를 죽여 버리겠다고 성난 목소리로 으름장 놓는 것을 들은 적이 있었다."], ["Two days before her death, he had run away, staying no one knew where in the town.", "그녀가 죽기 이틀 전, 그는 달아나 버려, 고을 어디에 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["The day after the murder, he was found on the road leading out of the town, dead drunk, with a knife in his pocket, and his right hand happened to be stained with blood.", "살인이 벌어진 이튿날, 그는 고을을 빠져나가는 길에서 발견되었는데, 곤드레만드레 취한 채 주머니에 칼을 지니고 있었고, 마침 그의 오른손이 피로 물들어 있었다."], ["He declared that his nose had been bleeding, but no one believed him.", "그는 코피가 났던 것이라고 우겼으나, 아무도 그의 말을 믿지 않았다."], ["The maids confessed that they had gone to a party and that the street-door had been left open till they returned.", "하녀들은 자기들이 잔치에 갔으며 자기들이 돌아올 때까지 한길 쪽 문이 열린 채로 있었다고 자백했다."], ["And a number of similar details came to light, throwing suspicion on the innocent servant.", "그리하여 이와 비슷한 갖가지 사정이 속속 드러나며 무고한 하인에게 혐의를 씌웠다."]], [["They arrested him, and he was tried for the murder; but a week after the arrest, the prisoner fell sick of a fever and died unconscious in the hospital.", "그들은 그를 체포했고, 그는 살인죄로 재판을 받았다. 그러나 체포된 지 일주일 만에, 그 죄수는 열병에 걸려 병원에서 의식을 잃은 채 죽고 말았다."], ["There the matter ended and the judges and the authorities and every one in the town remained convinced that the crime had been committed by no one but the servant who had died in the hospital.", "그것으로 사건은 마무리되었고, 판사들도, 당국도, 고을의 모든 사람도 그 범행은 병원에서 죽은 그 하인이 저지른 것임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다."], ["And after that the punishment began.", "그리고 그 뒤로 형벌이 시작되었다."]], [["My mysterious visitor, now my friend, told me that at first he was not in the least troubled by pangs of conscience.", "이제 나의 벗이 된 그 수수께끼의 방문자가 나에게 말하기를, 처음에는 양심의 가책에 조금도 시달리지 않았다고 했다."], ["He was miserable a long time, but not for that reason; only from regret that he had killed the woman he loved, that she was no more, that in killing her he had killed his love, while the fire of passion was still in his veins.", "그는 오랫동안 비참한 심정이었으나, 그것은 그 때문이 아니었다. 다만 자기가 사랑하던 여인을 죽였다는 것, 그녀가 이제는 더 이상 이 세상에 없다는 것, 정욕의 불길이 아직 핏줄 속에 남아 있는데도 그녀를 죽임으로써 자기 사랑마저 죽여 버렸다는 것에 대한 회한 때문이었다."], ["But of the innocent blood he had shed, of the murder of a fellow creature, he scarcely thought.", "그러나 자기가 흘린 무고한 피에 대해서는, 같은 동족을 죽인 살인에 대해서는, 그는 거의 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["The thought that his victim might have become the wife of another man was insupportable to him, and so, for a long time, he was convinced in his conscience that he could not have acted otherwise.", "자기 희생자가 다른 남자의 아내가 되었을지도 모른다는 생각이 그에게는 견딜 수 없는 것이었으니, 그리하여 그는 오랫동안 자기 양심 속에서, 자기로서는 달리 행동할 수 없었던 것이라고 확신하고 있었다."]], [["At first he was worried at the arrest of the servant, but his illness and death soon set his mind at rest, for the man’s death was apparently (so he reflected at the time) not owing to his arrest or his fright, but a chill he had taken on the day he ran away, when he had lain all night dead drunk on the damp ground.", "처음에 그는 그 하인이 체포된 것을 마음에 걸려 했으나, 그자의 병과 죽음이 곧 그의 마음을 가라앉혀 주었으니, 그자의 죽음은 (그가 당시에 그렇게 헤아린 바로는) 체포나 공포 때문이 아니라, 달아나던 날 밤 곤드레만드레 취해 축축한 땅바닥에 밤새 누워 있다가 걸린 오한 때문인 것이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["The theft of the money and other things troubled him little, for he argued that the theft had not been committed for gain but to avert suspicion.", "돈과 다른 물건들을 훔친 일은 그를 별로 괴롭히지 않았으니, 그 절도는 이득을 보려고 저지른 것이 아니라 혐의를 돌리기 위함이었다고 스스로 따져 댔던 것이다."], ["The sum stolen was small, and he shortly afterwards subscribed the whole of it, and much more, towards the funds for maintaining an almshouse in the town.", "훔친 액수는 얼마 되지 않았고, 그는 그 직후 그 전액을, 그것도 훨씬 더 많은 액수를 보태어 고을의 양로원 유지 기금으로 기부했다."], ["He did this on purpose to set his conscience at rest about the theft, and it’s a remarkable fact that for a long time he really was at peace—he told me this himself.", "그는 이 절도에 대해 자기 양심을 안심시키려고 일부러 그렇게 한 것이었는데, 한 가지 주목할 만한 사실은, 그가 오랫동안 정말로 마음의 평안을 누렸다는 점이다—이것은 그가 직접 나에게 한 말이다."], ["He entered then upon a career of great activity in the service, volunteered for a difficult and laborious duty, which occupied him two years, and being a man of strong will almost forgot the past.", "그러고 나서 그는 관직에서 대단히 활발한 경력을 쌓아 나갔으니, 어렵고 고된 임무를 자청하여 이 년 동안 그 일에 매달렸으며, 의지가 강한 사람이었던지라 거의 과거를 잊다시피 했다."], ["Whenever he recalled it, he tried not to think of it at all.", "그것이 떠오를 때면 그는 그것에 대해 아예 생각하지 않으려 애썼다."], ["He became active in philanthropy too, founded and helped to maintain many institutions in the town, did a good deal in the two capitals, and in both Moscow and Petersburg was elected a member of philanthropic societies.", "그는 또한 자선 사업에도 적극적으로 나서, 고을에 여러 시설을 세우고 그 유지를 도왔으며, 두 수도(首都)에서도 많은 일을 하여 모스크바와 페테르부르크 양쪽에서 자선 단체의 회원으로 선출되었다."]], [["At last, however, he began brooding over the past, and the strain of it was too much for him.", "그러나 마침내 그는 과거를 곱씹기 시작했으니, 그 부담이 그에게는 너무도 버거운 것이었다."], ["Then he was attracted by a fine and intelligent girl and soon after married her, hoping that marriage would dispel his lonely depression, and that by entering on a new life and scrupulously doing his duty to his wife and children, he would escape from old memories altogether.", "그때 그는 어느 곱고 총명한 처녀에게 마음이 끌려 머지않아 그녀와 결혼했으니, 결혼이 자기의 외로운 우울을 떨쳐 주리라고, 그리고 새로운 삶에 들어서서 아내와 자식들에 대한 자기 도리를 빈틈없이 다함으로써 옛 기억으로부터 아주 벗어날 수 있으리라고 기대했던 것이다."], ["But the very opposite of what he expected happened.", "그러나 그가 기대했던 것과는 정반대의 일이 벌어지고 말았다."], ["He began, even in the first month of his marriage, to be continually fretted by the thought, “My wife loves me—but what if she knew?”", "그는 결혼한 첫 달부터 벌써 '내 아내가 나를 사랑하지만—만약 그녀가 알게 된다면?' 하는 생각에 끊임없이 시달리기 시작했다."], ["When she first told him that she would soon bear him a child, he was troubled.", "그녀가 머지않아 그에게 아이를 낳아 주겠다고 처음 말했을 때, 그는 괴로웠다."], ["“I am giving life, but I have taken life.”", "'나는 생명을 주고 있다, 그러나 나는 생명을 앗은 자다.'"], ["Children came.", "아이들이 태어났다."], ["“How dare I love them, teach and educate them, how can I talk to them of virtue?", "'내가 어찌 감히 이 아이들을 사랑하고, 가르치고, 길러 낸단 말인가, 내가 어찌 이 아이들에게 미덕에 대해 이야기할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["I have shed blood.”", "나는 피를 흘린 자인데.'"], ["They were splendid children, he longed to caress them; “and I can’t look at their innocent candid faces, I am unworthy.”", "그 아이들은 더없이 사랑스러운 아이들이라, 그는 아이들을 어루만지고 싶어 못 견딜 지경이었다. '그런데도 나는 이 아이들의 순진하고 맑은 얼굴을 차마 바라볼 수가 없으니, 나는 그럴 자격이 없는 인간이다.'"]], [["At last he began to be bitterly and ominously haunted by the blood of his murdered victim, by the young life he had destroyed, by the blood that cried out for vengeance.", "마침내 그는 자기가 살해한 희생자의 피에, 자기가 짓밟아 버린 그 젊은 생명에, 복수를 부르짖는 그 피에 비통하고도 불길하게 시달리기 시작했다."], ["He had begun to have awful dreams.", "그는 끔찍한 꿈에 시달리게 되었다."], ["But, being a man of fortitude, he bore his suffering a long time, thinking: “I shall expiate everything by this secret agony.”", "그러나 그는 굳센 사람이었던지라, 오랫동안 그 고통을 견디며 이렇게 생각했다. '나는 이 은밀한 고뇌로써 모든 것을 속죄하리라.'"], ["But that hope, too, was vain; the longer it went on, the more intense was his suffering.", "그러나 그 희망 또한 부질없는 것이었으니, 세월이 흐를수록 그의 고통은 그만큼 더 격심해질 따름이었다."]], [["He was respected in society for his active benevolence, though every one was overawed by his stern and gloomy character.", "그는 활발한 자선 활동으로 사교계에서 존경을 받았으나, 모두가 그의 근엄하고 음울한 성품에 위압을 느꼈다."], ["But the more he was respected, the more intolerable it was for him.", "그러나 존경을 받으면 받을수록, 그것은 그에게 더욱 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["He confessed to me that he had thoughts of killing himself.", "그는 나에게, 자살을 생각한 적도 있었다고 고백했다."], ["But he began to be haunted by another idea—an idea which he had at first regarded as impossible and unthinkable, though at last it got such a hold on his heart that he could not shake it off.", "그러나 그는 또 다른 생각에 시달리기 시작했으니—처음에는 불가능하고 생각조차 할 수 없는 것으로 여겼으나, 마침내 그 생각이 그의 마음을 어찌나 단단히 사로잡았던지 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["He dreamed of rising up, going out and confessing in the face of all men that he had committed murder.", "그는 떨치고 일어나, 밖으로 나가, 만인 앞에서 자기가 살인을 저질렀노라고 고백하는 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["For three years this dream had pursued him, haunting him in different forms.", "삼 년 동안 이 꿈은 갖가지 형태로 그를 사로잡으며 따라다녔다."], ["At last he believed with his whole heart that if he confessed his crime, he would heal his soul and would be at peace for ever.", "마침내 그는 진심으로, 만약 자기 죄를 고백한다면 자기 영혼이 치유되고 영원히 평안해지리라고 믿게 되었다."], ["But this belief filled his heart with terror, for how could he carry it out?", "그러나 이 믿음은 그의 마음을 공포로 가득 채웠으니, 어떻게 그것을 실행한단 말인가?"], ["And then came what happened at my duel.", "그리고 그때 나의 결투에서 벌어진 그 일이 일어났던 것이다."]], [["“Looking at you, I have made up my mind.”", "\"당신을 보고서, 나는 마음을 굳혔소.\""]], [["I looked at him.", "나는 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"그럴 수가 있습니까,\""], ["I cried, clasping my hands, “that such a trivial incident could give rise to such a resolution in you?”", "나는 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"그토록 사소한 사건이 당신에게 그런 결심을 불러일으킬 수 있다는 것이?\""]], [["“My resolution has been growing for the last three years,”", "\"내 결심은 지난 삼 년 동안 자라 온 것이오,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he answered, “and your story only gave the last touch to it.", "\"당신의 이야기가 거기에 마지막 한 획을 더했을 뿐이오."], ["Looking at you, I reproached myself and envied you.”", "당신을 보면서, 나는 나 자신을 책망하고 또 당신을 부러워했소.\""], ["He said this to me almost sullenly.", "그는 거의 침울하다 싶을 만큼 이 말을 나에게 했다."]], [["“But you won’t be believed,”", "\"하지만 사람들이 당신을 믿지 않을 겁니다,\""], ["I observed; “it’s fourteen years ago.”", "나는 의견을 내놓았다. \"십사 년 전의 일이지 않습니까.\""]], [["“I have proofs, great proofs.", "\"내게는 증거가 있소, 확실한 증거가."], ["I shall show them.”", "그것을 내보일 것이오.\""]], [["Then I cried and kissed him.", "그러자 나는 눈물을 흘리며 그에게 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Tell me one thing, one thing,”", "\"한 가지만, 한 가지만 말해 주시오,\""], ["he said (as though it all depended upon me), “my wife, my children!", "그가 (마치 그 모든 것이 나에게 달려 있기라도 하다는 듯) 말했다."], ["My wife may die of grief, and though my children won’t lose their rank and property, they’ll be a convict’s children and for ever!", "\"내 아내, 내 자식들 말이오! 내 아내는 슬픔으로 죽을지도 모르고, 내 자식들은 비록 신분과 재산을 잃지는 않겠으나, 죄수의 자식으로, 그것도 영원토록 살아가야 하지 않겠소!"], ["And what a memory, what a memory of me I shall leave in their hearts!”", "그리고 나에 대한 어떤 기억을, 어떤 기억을 그 어린 가슴들에 남기게 될 것이오!\""]], [["I said nothing.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“And to part from them, to leave them for ever?", "\"그리고 그들과 헤어지는 것, 그들을 영원히 떠나는 것이라니?"], ["It’s for ever, you know, for ever!”", "그것은 영원이오, 알겠소, 영원이란 말이오!\""]], [["I sat still and repeated a silent prayer.", "나는 가만히 앉아 소리 없는 기도를 거듭 올렸다."], ["I got up at last, I felt afraid.", "마침내 나는 일어섰으니, 두려움을 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떻소?\""], ["He looked at me.", "그가 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Go!”", "\"가십시오!\""], ["said I, “confess.", "나는 말했다. \"고백하십시오."], ["Everything passes, only the truth remains.", "모든 것은 지나가고, 오직 진실만이 남습니다."], ["Your children will understand, when they grow up, the nobility of your resolution.”", "당신의 자식들도 자라나면, 당신이 내린 그 결심의 고결함을 이해하게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["He left me that time as though he had made up his mind.", "그때 그는 마치 마음을 굳힌 듯이 나에게서 떠나갔다."], ["Yet for more than a fortnight afterwards, he came to me every evening, still preparing himself, still unable to bring himself to the point.", "그러나 그 뒤로도 보름이 넘도록, 그는 매일 저녁 나를 찾아와, 여전히 마음의 채비를 하면서도, 여전히 그 결단의 순간으로 자신을 몰아가지 못했다."], ["He made my heart ache.", "그는 나의 가슴을 아프게 했다."], ["One day he would come determined and say fervently:", "어느 날은 단단히 작정한 채 찾아와 열정에 차서 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["“I know it will be heaven for me, heaven, the moment I confess.", "\"고백하는 그 순간, 내게는 천국이 펼쳐지리라는 것을, 천국이 펼쳐지리라는 것을 나는 알고 있소."], ["Fourteen years I’ve been in hell.", "십사 년 동안 나는 지옥에 있었소."], ["I want to suffer.", "나는 고통받고 싶소."], ["I will take my punishment and begin to live.", "나는 내 형벌을 받아들이고 새로이 살기 시작할 것이오."], ["You can pass through the world doing wrong, but there’s no turning back.", "사람이 잘못을 저지르며 세상을 살아갈 수는 있어도, 되돌릴 길은 없는 법이오."], ["Now I dare not love my neighbor nor even my own children.", "지금 나는 내 이웃은커녕 내 자식조차도 감히 사랑할 수가 없소."], ["Good God, my children will understand, perhaps, what my punishment has cost me and will not condemn me!", "오, 하느님, 내 자식들은 어쩌면, 이 형벌이 나에게 어떤 대가를 치르게 했는지 이해하고 나를 단죄하지 않을 것이오!"], ["God is not in strength but in truth.”", "하느님은 힘 안에 계신 것이 아니라 진실 안에 계시는 것이오.\""]], [["“All will understand your sacrifice,”", "\"모두가 당신의 희생을 이해하게 될 겁니다,\" 나는 그에게 말했다."], ["I said to him, “if not at once, they will understand later; for you have served truth, the higher truth, not of the earth.”", "\"당장은 아니더라도, 나중에는 이해하게 될 겁니다. 왜냐하면 당신은 진실을, 그것도 땅의 것이 아닌 더 높은 진실을 섬긴 것이니까요.\""]], [["And he would go away seeming comforted, but next day he would come again, bitter, pale, sarcastic.", "그러면 그는 위안을 얻은 듯이 떠나갔으나, 이튿날이면 다시 찾아오곤 했으니, 비통하고, 창백하고, 비아냥대는 모습으로였다."]], [["“Every time I come to you, you look at me so inquisitively as though to say, ‘He has still not confessed!’ Wait a bit, don’t despise me too much.", "\"내가 당신을 찾아올 때마다, 당신은 마치 '이 사람이 아직도 고백을 하지 않았군!' 하고 말하기라도 하듯 그렇게 캐묻는 눈길로 나를 바라보는구려."], ["It’s not such an easy thing to do, as you would think.", "잠깐 기다려 주시오, 나를 너무 멸시하지는 마시오. 그것은 당신이 생각하는 것처럼 그리 쉬운 일이 아니오."], ["Perhaps I shall not do it at all.", "어쩌면 나는 끝내 그 일을 하지 않을지도 모르오."], ["You won’t go and inform against me then, will you?”", "그러면 당신은 가서 나를 고발하거나 하지는 않겠지요, 그렇소?\""]], [["And far from looking at him with indiscreet curiosity, I was afraid to look at him at all.", "나는 무례한 호기심으로 그를 바라보기는커녕, 차마 그를 바라보는 것조차 두려웠다."], ["I was quite ill from anxiety, and my heart was full of tears.", "나는 불안에 시달려 거의 병이 날 지경이었고, 내 가슴은 눈물로 가득 차 있었다."], ["I could not sleep at night.", "나는 밤에 잠을 이룰 수가 없었다."]], [["“I have just come from my wife,”", "\"나는 방금 아내에게서 오는 길이오,\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“Do you understand what the word ‘wife’ means?", "\"'아내'라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 당신이 이해하겠소?"], ["When I went out, the children called to me, ‘Good-by, father, make haste back to read The Children’s Magazine with us.’ No, you don’t understand that!", "내가 나서는데, 아이들이 나를 부르며 이러는 것이오. '아빠, 안녕히 다녀오세요, 어서 돌아오셔서 우리랑 같이 어린이 잡지를 읽어요.'"], ["No one is wise from another man’s woe.”", "아니, 당신은 그것을 이해하지 못하오! 남의 슬픔으로 지혜로워지는 사람은 없는 법이니까.\""]], [["His eyes were glittering, his lips were twitching.", "그의 두 눈은 번득이고 있었고, 그의 입술은 실룩거리고 있었다."], ["Suddenly he struck the table with his fist so that everything on it danced—it was the first time he had done such a thing, he was such a mild man.", "별안간 그가 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳐서 그 위의 모든 것이 들썩거렸는데—그가 그런 짓을 한 것은 처음이었으니, 그는 그토록 온화한 사람이었던 것이다."]], [["“But need I?”", "\"하지만 내가 꼭 그래야 하오?\""], ["he exclaimed, “must I?", "그가 부르짖었다."], ["No one has been condemned, no one has been sent to Siberia in my place, the man died of fever.", "\"내가 반드시 그래야만 하오? 아무도 유죄 선고를 받지 않았고, 아무도 나 대신 시베리아로 보내지지 않았으며, 그자는 열병으로 죽었소."], ["And I’ve been punished by my sufferings for the blood I shed.", "그리고 나는 내가 흘린 피에 대해 내 고통으로 형벌을 받아 왔소."], ["And I shan’t be believed, they won’t believe my proofs.", "게다가 사람들은 나를 믿지 않을 것이고, 내 증거도 믿지 않을 것이오."], ["Need I confess, need I?", "내가 고백을 해야 하오, 내가 꼭 그래야만 하오?"], ["I am ready to go on suffering all my life for the blood I have shed, if only my wife and children may be spared.", "오로지 내 아내와 자식들만 화를 면할 수 있다면, 나는 내가 흘린 피에 대해 평생토록 고통받을 각오가 되어 있소."], ["Will it be just to ruin them with me?", "그들을 나와 함께 파멸시키는 것이 과연 옳은 일이겠소?"], ["Aren’t we making a mistake?", "우리가 잘못 생각하고 있는 것은 아니오?"], ["What is right in this case?", "이 경우에 무엇이 옳은 것이오?"], ["And will people recognize it, will they appreciate it, will they respect it?”", "그리고 사람들이 그것을 알아주기나 할 것이오, 그것을 인정해 주기나 할 것이오, 그것을 존중해 주기나 할 것이오?\""]], [["“Good Lord!”", "'맙소사,'"], ["I thought to myself, “he is thinking of other people’s respect at such a moment!”", "나는 속으로 생각했다. '이런 순간에도 이 사람은 남들의 존경을 생각하고 있구나!'"], ["And I felt so sorry for him then, that I believe I would have shared his fate if it could have comforted him.", "그러고는 그때 그가 어찌나 가엾던지, 만약 그것이 그에게 위안이 될 수 있다면 나는 그의 운명을 함께 나눠 졌으리라고 생각한다."], ["I saw he was beside himself.", "나는 그가 제정신이 아니라는 것을 알았다."], ["I was aghast, realizing with my heart as well as my mind what such a resolution meant.", "나는, 그런 결심이 무엇을 뜻하는지를 머리로뿐 아니라 가슴으로도 깨달으며, 아연실색하고 말았다."]], [["“Decide my fate!”", "\"내 운명을 결정해 주시오!\""], ["he exclaimed again.", "그가 다시 부르짖었다."]], [["“Go and confess,”", "\"가서 고백하십시오,\""], ["I whispered to him.", "나는 그에게 속삭였다."], ["My voice failed me, but I whispered it firmly. I took up the New Testament from the table, the Russian translation, and showed him the Gospel of St.", "목소리가 제대로 나오지 않았으나, 나는 단호하게 그것을 속삭였다."], ["John, chapter xii. verse 24:", "나는 탁자에서 신약 성경을, 그 러시아어 번역본을 집어 들어, 그에게 요한복음 십이 장 이십사 절을 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["“Verily, verily, I say unto you, except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone: but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit.”", "\"진실로 진실로 너희에게 이르노니, 한 알의 밀이 땅에 떨어져 죽지 아니하면 한 알 그대로 있고, 죽으면 많은 열매를 맺느니라.\""]], [["I had just been reading that verse when he came in.", "그가 들어오기 직전에 나는 마침 그 구절을 읽고 있던 참이었다."], ["He read it.", "그는 그것을 읽었다."]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그것은 진실이오,\""], ["he said, but he smiled bitterly.", "그가 말했으나, 비통하게 미소 지었다."], ["“It’s terrible the things you find in those books,”", "\"그런 책들 속에서 찾아내는 것들이란 참 무서운 것들이오,\""], ["he said, after a pause.", "그가 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다."], ["“It’s easy enough to thrust them upon one.", "\"그런 것들을 남에게 들이밀기란 아주 쉬운 일이지요."], ["And who wrote them?", "그런데 그것을 쓴 자는 누구요?"], ["Can they have been written by men?”", "그것이 과연 사람들의 손으로 쓰일 수 있는 것이겠소?\""]], [["“The Holy Spirit wrote them,”", "\"성령께서 그것을 쓰셨습니다,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했다."]], [["“It’s easy for you to prate,”", "\"당신이야 떠벌리기 쉽겠지요,\""], ["he smiled again, this time almost with hatred.", "그가 다시 미소 지었는데, 이번에는 거의 증오에 차서였다."]], [["I took the book again, opened it in another place and showed him the Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter x.", "나는 다시 그 책을 집어 들어, 다른 곳을 펼쳐 그에게 히브리서 십 장 삼십일 절을 보여 주었다."], ["verse 31. He read:", "그는 읽었다."]], [["“It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.”", "\"살아 계신 하느님의 손에 빠져 들어가는 것이 무서울진저.\""]], [["He read it and simply flung down the book.", "그는 그것을 읽고는 그대로 책을 내던졌다."], ["He was trembling all over.", "그는 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있었다."]], [["“An awful text,”", "\"끔찍한 구절이오,\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“There’s no denying you’ve picked out fitting ones.”", "\"당신이 딱 들어맞는 것들만 골라낸 것은 부정할 수 없구려.\""], ["He rose from the chair.", "그는 의자에서 일어섰다."], ["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["he said, “good-by, perhaps I shan’t come again ...", "그가 말했다. \"그럼 잘 있으시오, 어쩌면 나는 다시 오지 못할 거요……"], ["we shall meet in heaven.", "우리는 천국에서 만나게 될 것이오."], ["So I have been for fourteen years ‘in the hands of the living God,’ that’s how one must think of those fourteen years.", "그러니까 나는 십사 년 동안 '살아 계신 하느님의 손에' 있었던 셈이오, 그 십사 년을 그렇게 생각해야 하는 것이지요."], ["To-morrow I will beseech those hands to let me go.”", "내일 나는 그 손에 나를 놓아 달라고 간구하겠소.\""]], [["I wanted to take him in my arms and kiss him, but I did not dare—his face was contorted and somber.", "나는 그를 품에 안고 입 맞추고 싶었으나, 차마 그럴 수가 없었으니—그의 얼굴은 일그러지고 침울했던 것이다."], ["He went away.", "그는 떠나갔다."]], [["“Good God,”", "'맙소사,'"], ["I thought, “what has he gone to face!”", "나는 생각했다. '이 사람이 무엇을 마주하러 간 것인가!'"], ["I fell on my knees before the ikon and wept for him before the Holy Mother of God, our swift defender and helper.", "나는 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 우리의 날랜 보호자이자 조력자이신 거룩하신 하느님의 어머니 앞에서 그를 위해 울었다."], ["I was half an hour praying in tears, and it was late, about midnight.", "나는 반 시간 동안 눈물을 흘리며 기도했는데, 시각은 늦은 자정 무렵이었다."], ["Suddenly I saw the door open and he came in again.", "그때 별안간 나는 문이 열리는 것을 보았고, 그가 다시 들어왔다."], ["I was surprised.", "나는 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["“Where have you been?”", "\"어디를 다녀오셨습니까?\""], ["I asked him.", "나는 그에게 물었다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"내가 아무래도,\""], ["he said, “I’ve forgotten something ...", "그가 말했다. \"무언가를 두고 간 것 같소……"], ["my handkerchief, I think....", "손수건을, 그런 것 같소.……"], ["Well, even if I’ve not forgotten anything, let me stay a little.”", "뭐, 설령 두고 간 것이 없더라도, 잠깐 머무르게 해 주시오.\""]], [["He sat down.", "그는 자리에 앉았다."], ["I stood over him.", "나는 그의 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["“You sit down, too,”", "\"당신도 앉으시오,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["I sat down.", "나는 앉았다."], ["We sat still for two minutes; he looked intently at me and suddenly smiled—I remembered that—then he got up, embraced me warmly and kissed me.", "우리는 두어 시간 동안 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 그는 나를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 별안간 미소를 지었다—나는 그것을 기억하고 있다—그러더니 그는 일어나, 나를 따뜻하게 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Remember,”", "\"기억해 두시오,\""], ["he said, “how I came to you a second time.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 두 번째로 당신을 찾아왔다는 것을."], ["Do you hear, remember it!” And he went out.", "듣고 있소, 그것을 기억해 두시오!\""]], [["“To-morrow,” I thought.", "그러고는 그는 나가 버렸다."]], [["And so it was.", "'내일이로구나,' 나는 생각했다."], ["I did not know that evening that the next day was his birthday.", "그리고 과연 그러했다. 그날 저녁 나는 이튿날이 그의 생일이라는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["I had not been out for the last few days, so I had no chance of hearing it from any one.", "지난 며칠 동안 나는 외출을 하지 않았던 터라, 누구에게서도 그것을 전해 들을 길이 없었던 것이다."], ["On that day he always had a great gathering, every one in the town went to it.", "그날이면 그는 늘 큰 모임을 열었고, 고을의 모든 사람이 그 자리에 갔다."], ["It was the same this time.", "이번에도 마찬가지였다."], ["After dinner he walked into the middle of the room, with a paper in his hand—a formal declaration to the chief of his department who was present.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤, 그는 손에 종이 한 장을 든 채 방 한가운데로 걸어 나갔으니—그것은 그 자리에 와 있던 자기 부서의 장(長) 앞으로 쓴 정식 진술서였다."], ["This declaration he read aloud to the whole assembly.", "그는 이 진술서를 모인 사람들 모두에게 큰 소리로 낭독했다."], ["It contained a full account of the crime, in every detail.", "그것에는 그 범행에 대한 온전한 전모가, 낱낱의 세부에 이르기까지 담겨 있었다."]], [["“I cut myself off from men as a monster.", "\"나는 괴물로서 사람들로부터 나 자신을 끊어 냈소."], ["God has visited me,”", "하느님께서 나를 찾아오셨소,\""], ["he said in conclusion.", "그가 끝맺으며 말했다."], ["“I want to suffer for my sin!”", "\"나는 내 죄로 인해 고통받기를 원하오!\""]], [["Then he brought out and laid on the table all the things he had been keeping for fourteen years, that he thought would prove his crime, the jewels belonging to the murdered woman which he had stolen to divert suspicion, a cross and a locket taken from her neck with a portrait of her betrothed in the locket, her notebook and two letters; one from her betrothed, telling her that he would soon be with her, and her unfinished answer left on the table to be sent off next day.", "그러고 나서 그는, 자기가 십사 년 동안 간직해 온, 자기 범행을 입증하리라고 여긴 모든 물건을 꺼내어 탁자 위에 늘어놓았으니, 혐의를 돌리려고 훔쳤던 살해당한 여인의 보석들, 그녀의 목에서 떼어 낸 십자가와 로켓—그 로켓 안에는 그녀의 약혼자의 초상이 들어 있었다—그녀의 수첩과 편지 두 통이었다. 그 가운데 한 통은 그녀의 약혼자가 보낸 것으로, 머지않아 그녀 곁으로 가겠다는 내용이었고, 다른 한 통은 그녀가 채 끝맺지 못한 답장으로, 이튿날 부치려고 탁자 위에 남겨 두었던 것이었다."], ["He carried off these two letters—what for?", "그는 이 두 통의 편지를 가져갔던 것이다—무엇 때문에?"], ["Why had he kept them for fourteen years afterwards instead of destroying them as evidence against him?", "어째서 그는 그것을 자기에게 불리한 증거로 없애 버리지 않고, 그 후로 십사 년 동안이나 간직하고 있었던 것일까?"]], [["And this is what happened: every one was amazed and horrified, every one refused to believe it and thought that he was deranged, though all listened with intense curiosity.", "그리고 벌어진 일은 이러했다. 모두가 경악하고 소스라쳤으며, 모두가 그것을 믿기를 거부하고 그가 미친 것이라고 여기면서도, 다들 강렬한 호기심을 가지고 귀를 기울였다."], ["A few days later it was fully decided and agreed in every house that the unhappy man was mad.", "며칠 뒤, 그 불행한 사람이 미쳤다는 것이 집집마다 완전히 단정되고 합의되었다."], ["The legal authorities could not refuse to take the case up, but they too dropped it.", "사법 당국은 그 사건을 맡지 않을 수 없었으나, 그들 역시 그것을 흐지부지 덮어 버렸다."], ["Though the trinkets and letters made them ponder, they decided that even if they did turn out to be authentic, no charge could be based on those alone.", "비록 그 장신구들과 편지들이 그들을 골똘히 생각하게 만들기는 했으나, 그들은 설령 그것들이 진짜로 판명된다 하더라도 그것만으로는 어떤 혐의도 성립시킬 수 없다고 결론 내렸다."], ["Besides, she might have given him those things as a friend, or asked him to take care of them for her.", "게다가 그녀가 벗으로서 그에게 그 물건들을 주었을 수도 있고, 자기 대신 보관해 달라고 부탁했을 수도 있다는 것이었다."], ["I heard afterwards, however, that the genuineness of the things was proved by the friends and relations of the murdered woman, and that there was no doubt about them.", "그러나 나는 나중에, 그 물건들의 진위가 살해된 여인의 벗들과 친척들에 의해 입증되었으며, 그것에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없었다는 것을 전해 들었다."], ["Yet nothing was destined to come of it, after all.", "그러나 결국 그 일에서는 아무것도 비롯되지 않을 운명이었다."]], [["Five days later, all had heard that he was ill and that his life was in danger.", "닷새 뒤, 그가 병들었으며 목숨이 위태롭다는 소식을 모두가 들었다."], ["The nature of his illness I can’t explain, they said it was an affection of the heart.", "그의 병의 본질이 무엇인지 나로서는 설명할 수가 없는데, 사람들은 그것이 심장의 질환이라고들 했다."], ["But it became known that the doctors had been induced by his wife to investigate his mental condition also, and had come to the conclusion that it was a case of insanity.", "그러나 의사들이 그의 아내의 권유로 그의 정신 상태까지 진찰하여 그것이 정신 착란의 경우라는 결론에 이르렀다는 사실이 알려졌다."], ["I betrayed nothing, though people ran to question me.", "나는 사람들이 나에게 달려와 캐물어도 아무것도 발설하지 않았다."], ["But when I wanted to visit him, I was for a long while forbidden to do so, above all by his wife.", "그러나 내가 그를 찾아가려 했을 때, 나는 오랫동안, 무엇보다도 그의 아내에 의해 그러는 것을 금지당했다."]], [["“It’s you who have caused his illness,”", "\"바로 당신이 그이의 병을 불러온 거예요,\""], ["she said to me; “he was always gloomy, but for the last year people noticed that he was peculiarly excited and did strange things, and now you have been the ruin of him.", "그녀가 나에게 말했다. \"그이는 늘 음울하긴 했지만, 지난 일 년 동안 사람들은 그이가 유난히 흥분해 이상한 짓을 한다는 것을 눈치챘는데, 이제 당신이 그이를 망쳐 놓은 거예요."], ["Your preaching has brought him to this; for the last month he was always with you.”", "당신의 설교가 그이를 이 지경으로 몰고 온 거라고요. 지난 한 달 동안 그이는 늘 당신과 함께 있었으니까요.\""]], [["Indeed, not only his wife but the whole town were down upon me and blamed me.", "실로 그의 아내뿐만 아니라 온 고을이 나를 들들 볶으며 나를 탓했다."], ["“It’s all your doing,”", "\"다 당신이 한 짓이오,\""], ["they said.", "그들은 말했다."], ["I was silent and indeed rejoiced at heart, for I saw plainly God’s mercy to the man who had turned against himself and punished himself.", "나는 잠자코 있었으며, 실은 마음속으로 기뻐했으니, 자기 자신에게 맞서 자기 자신을 벌한 그 사람에게 하느님께서 베푸신 자비를 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다."], ["I could not believe in his insanity.", "나는 그가 미쳤다는 것을 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["They let me see him at last, he insisted upon saying good-by to me.", "마침내 그들이 나에게 그를 만나게 해 주었으니, 그가 나에게 작별 인사를 하겠다고 고집했던 것이다."], ["I went in to him and saw at once, that not only his days, but his hours were numbered.", "나는 그에게로 들어가, 그에게 남은 것이 이제 며칠이 아니라 몇 시간뿐임을 단번에 알아보았다."], ["He was weak, yellow, his hands trembled, he gasped for breath, but his face was full of tender and happy feeling.", "그는 쇠약하고, 누렇게 떴으며, 두 손이 떨리고, 숨을 헐떡거렸으나, 그의 얼굴은 다정하고 행복한 감정으로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["“It is done!”", "\"끝났소!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’ve long been yearning to see you, why didn’t you come?”", "\"나는 오랫동안 당신을 보고 싶어 애태웠는데, 어째서 오지 않았소?\""]], [["I did not tell him that they would not let me see him.", "나는 그들이 나에게 그를 만나게 해 주지 않았다는 말은 하지 않았다."]], [["“God has had pity on me and is calling me to Himself.", "\"하느님께서 나를 가엾이 여기시어 나를 당신 곁으로 부르고 계시오."], ["I know I am dying, but I feel joy and peace for the first time after so many years.", "나는 내가 죽어 가고 있음을 알지만, 그토록 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 처음으로 기쁨과 평안을 느끼고 있소."], ["There was heaven in my heart from the moment I had done what I had to do.", "내가 해야 할 일을 해낸 그 순간부터, 내 마음속에는 천국이 깃들었소."], ["Now I dare to love my children and to kiss them.", "이제 나는 감히 내 자식들을 사랑하고 그 아이들에게 입 맞출 수 있게 되었소."], ["Neither my wife nor the judges, nor any one has believed it.", "내 아내도, 판사들도, 그 누구도 그것을 믿지 않았소."], ["My children will never believe it either.", "내 자식들도 결코 그것을 믿지 않을 것이오."], ["I see in that God’s mercy to them.", "나는 거기에서 그 아이들에게 베푸신 하느님의 자비를 보오."], ["I shall die, and my name will be without a stain for them.", "나는 죽겠지만, 내 이름은 그 아이들에게 한 점 오점 없이 남을 것이오."], ["And now I feel God near, my heart rejoices as in Heaven ...", "그리고 이제 나는 하느님이 가까이 계심을 느끼며, 내 마음은 천국에 있는 것처럼 기뻐하오……"], ["I have done my duty.”", "나는 내 도리를 다했소.\""]], [["He could not speak, he gasped for breath, he pressed my hand warmly, looking fervently at me.", "그는 말을 잇지 못하고 숨을 헐떡였으며, 나의 손을 따뜻하게 움켜쥔 채 열렬한 눈길로 나를 바라보았다."], ["We did not talk for long, his wife kept peeping in at us.", "우리는 오래 이야기를 나누지는 못했으니, 그의 아내가 줄곧 우리 쪽을 엿보고 있었던 것이다."], ["But he had time to whisper to me:", "그러나 그는 나에게 이렇게 속삭일 짬은 있었다."]], [["“Do you remember how I came back to you that second time, at midnight?", "\"내가 그날 자정에 두 번째로 당신을 찾아갔던 것을 기억하시오?"], ["I told you to remember it.", "나는 당신에게 그것을 기억해 두라고 했소."], ["You know what I came back for?", "내가 무엇 때문에 다시 찾아갔는지 아시오?"], ["I came to kill you!”", "나는 당신을 죽이러 갔던 것이오!\""]], [["I started.", "나는 흠칫했다."]], [["“I went out from you then into the darkness, I wandered about the streets, struggling with myself.", "\"나는 그때 당신에게서 나와 어둠 속으로 나섰고, 나 자신과 싸우며 거리를 헤매고 다녔소."], ["And suddenly I hated you so that I could hardly bear it.", "그러다 별안간 나는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 당신이 미워졌소. '이제,'"], ["Now, I thought, he is all that binds me, and he is my judge.", "하고 나는 생각했소. '나를 옭아매는 것은 오직 저자뿐이고, 저자가 나의 심판자다."], ["I can’t refuse to face my punishment to-morrow, for he knows all.", "나는 내일 내 형벌을 마주하기를 거부할 수가 없으니, 저자가 모든 것을 알고 있기 때문이다.'"], ["It was not that I was afraid you would betray me (I never even thought of that), but I thought, ‘How can I look him in the face if I don’t confess?’ And if you had been at the other end of the earth, but alive, it would have been all the same, the thought was unendurable that you were alive knowing everything and condemning me.", "그것은 당신이 나를 밀고할까 봐 두려워서가 아니었소 (나는 그런 것은 생각조차 하지 않았소). 다만 나는 이렇게 생각했소."], ["I hated you as though you were the cause, as though you were to blame for everything.", "'내가 고백하지 않는다면, 어떻게 저자의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라볼 수 있단 말인가?' 그리고 설령 당신이 땅끝에 가 있더라도, 살아 있기만 하다면, 마찬가지였을 것이오."], ["I came back to you then, remembering that you had a dagger lying on your table.", "당신이 모든 것을 알면서 살아서 나를 단죄하고 있다는 그 생각이 견딜 수 없었던 것이오."], ["I sat down and asked you to sit down, and for a whole minute I pondered.", "나는 마치 당신이 그 모든 일의 원인이기라도 하듯, 마치 당신에게 그 모든 책임이 있기라도 하듯 당신을 미워했소."], ["If I had killed you, I should have been ruined by that murder even if I had not confessed the other.", "나는 그때 당신의 탁자 위에 단검이 놓여 있던 것을 떠올리며 당신에게 되돌아갔던 것이오. 나는 자리에 앉아 당신에게도 앉으라고 청한 뒤, 꼬박 일 분 동안 곰곰이 생각했소."], ["But I didn’t think about that at all, and I didn’t want to think of it at that moment.", "만약 내가 당신을 죽였더라면, 비록 그 다른 죄는 고백하지 않았더라도 나는 그 살인으로 파멸하고 말았을 것이오. 그러나 나는 그런 것은 전혀 생각하지 않았고, 그 순간 그런 것을 생각하고 싶지도 않았소."], ["I only hated you and longed to revenge myself on you for everything.", "나는 그저 당신을 미워했고, 그 모든 것에 대해 당신에게 복수하기를 갈망했을 뿐이오."], ["The Lord vanquished the devil in my heart.", "주님께서 내 마음속의 악마를 물리치셨소."], ["But let me tell you, you were never nearer death.”", "그러나 한 가지 말해 두는데, 당신은 그때 일찍이 죽음에 그토록 가까이 다가간 적이 없었소.\""]], [["A week later he died.", "일주일 뒤 그는 죽었다."], ["The whole town followed him to the grave.", "온 고을이 그를 무덤까지 따라갔다."], ["The chief priest made a speech full of feeling.", "수석 사제가 감동에 찬 추도사를 했다."], ["All lamented the terrible illness that had cut short his days.", "모두가 그의 생을 앗아 간 그 끔찍한 병을 애도했다."], ["But all the town was up in arms against me after the funeral, and people even refused to see me.", "그러나 장례식이 끝난 뒤, 온 고을이 나에게 들고일어났으며, 사람들은 심지어 나를 만나기조차 거부했다."], ["Some, at first a few and afterwards more, began indeed to believe in the truth of his story, and they visited me and questioned me with great interest and eagerness, for man loves to see the downfall and disgrace of the righteous.", "더러는, 처음에는 몇 사람이, 나중에는 더 많은 사람이 실제로 그의 이야기의 진실을 믿기 시작하여, 나를 찾아와 크나큰 관심과 열의를 가지고 캐물었으니, 사람이란 의로운 자의 몰락과 치욕을 보기를 좋아하는 법이기 때문이다."], ["But I held my tongue, and very shortly after, I left the town, and five months later by God’s grace I entered upon the safe and blessed path, praising the unseen finger which had guided me so clearly to it.", "그러나 나는 입을 다물고 있었고, 그 직후 곧 그 고을을 떠나, 다섯 달 뒤 하느님의 은총으로 안전하고 축복받은 길에 들어서서, 나를 그토록 또렷이 그 길로 인도해 주신 보이지 않는 손가락을 찬미했다."], ["But I remember in my prayer to this day, the servant of God, Mihail, who suffered so greatly.", "그러나 나는 오늘날까지도 내 기도 속에서, 그토록 크나큰 고통을 겪은 하느님의 종 미하일을 기억하고 있다."]], [["(e) The Russian Monk and his possible Significance\nFathers and teachers, what is the monk?", "신부님들과 스승님들이여, 수도승이란 무엇입니까?"], ["In the cultivated world the word is nowadays pronounced by some people with a jeer, and by others it is used as a term of abuse, and this contempt for the monk is growing.", "교양 있는 세상에서는 요즈음 이 말을 어떤 이들은 비웃음을 담아 입에 올리고, 또 어떤 이들은 욕설로 쓰며, 수도승을 향한 이 경멸은 점점 더 커지고 있습니다."], ["It is true, alas, it is true, that there are many sluggards, gluttons, profligates and insolent beggars among monks.", "슬프게도, 참으로 슬프게도, 수도승들 가운데 게으름뱅이며 식충이며 방탕한 자며 뻔뻔한 거지가 많은 것은 사실입니다."], ["Educated people point to these: “You are idlers, useless members of society, you live on the labor of others, you are shameless beggars.”", "배운 사람들은 이런 자들을 가리키며 말합니다. \"너희는 게으름뱅이며, 사회에 쓸모없는 존재이며, 남의 노동에 기대어 살아가는, 부끄러움을 모르는 거지들이다.\""], ["And yet how many meek and humble monks there are, yearning for solitude and fervent prayer in peace!", "그러나 온유하고 겸손한 수도승, 고독과 평화 속에서 뜨거운 기도를 갈망하는 수도승은 또 얼마나 많습니까!"], ["These are less noticed, or passed over in silence.", "이런 이들은 덜 주목받거나, 침묵 속에 묻혀 버립니다."], ["And how surprised men would be if I were to say that from these meek monks, who yearn for solitary prayer, the salvation of Russia will come perhaps once more!", "그러니 만일 내가, 고독한 기도를 갈망하는 이 온유한 수도승들로부터 러시아의 구원이 어쩌면 다시 한 번 비롯되리라고 말한다면 사람들이 얼마나 놀라겠습니까!"], ["For they are in truth made ready in peace and quiet “for the day and the hour, the month and the year.”", "참으로 이들은 평화와 고요 속에서 \"그날과 그 시각, 그달과 그해\"를 위하여 채비를 갖추고 있는 것입니다."], ["Meanwhile, in their solitude, they keep the image of Christ fair and undefiled, in the purity of God’s truth, from the times of the Fathers of old, the Apostles and the martyrs.", "그러는 동안 그들은 고독 속에서, 옛 교부들과 사도들과 순교자들의 시대로부터 이어 온 하느님 진리의 순결함 가운데, 그리스도의 형상을 아름답고 더럽혀지지 않은 채 지켜 나갑니다."], ["And when the time comes they will show it to the tottering creeds of the world.", "그리고 때가 이르면 그들은 그 형상을 흔들리는 세상의 신앙들 앞에 내보일 것입니다."], ["That is a great thought.", "이는 위대한 사상입니다."], ["That star will rise out of the East.", "그 별은 동방에서 떠오를 것입니다."]], [["That is my view of the monk, and is it false?", "이것이 수도승에 관한 나의 견해인데, 그것이 거짓입니까?"], ["is it too proud?", "그것이 너무 교만합니까?"], ["Look at the worldly and all who set themselves up above the people of God, has not God’s image and His truth been distorted in them?", "세속의 인간들과, 하느님의 백성 위에 스스로를 높이 세운 모든 자들을 보십시오. 그들 안에서 하느님의 형상과 그분의 진리가 일그러지지 않았습니까?"], ["They have science; but in science there is nothing but what is the object of sense.", "그들에게는 학문이 있습니다. 그러나 학문에는 감각의 대상이 되는 것밖에 아무것도 없습니다."], ["The spiritual world, the higher part of man’s being is rejected altogether, dismissed with a sort of triumph, even with hatred.", "영적인 세계, 인간 존재의 더 높은 부분은 송두리째 거부당하고, 일종의 의기양양함으로, 심지어 증오로 내쳐집니다."], ["The world has proclaimed the reign of freedom, especially of late, but what do we see in this freedom of theirs?", "세상은, 특히 최근 들어 자유의 통치를 선포했으나, 그들의 그 자유 속에서 우리가 보는 것은 무엇입니까?"], ["Nothing but slavery and self-destruction!", "오직 노예 상태와 자기 파멸뿐입니다!"], ["For the world says:", "세상은 이렇게 말하기 때문입니다."]], [["“You have desires and so satisfy them, for you have the same rights as the most rich and powerful.", "\"너에게 욕망이 있으니 그것을 채워라, 너는 가장 부유하고 권세 있는 자들과 똑같은 권리를 지녔으니."], ["Don’t be afraid of satisfying them and even multiply your desires.”", "욕망을 채우기를 두려워 말고 도리어 네 욕망을 늘려라.\""], ["That is the modern doctrine of the world.", "이것이 세상의 현대적 교리입니다."], ["In that they see freedom.", "그들은 거기서 자유를 봅니다."], ["And what follows from this right of multiplication of desires?", "그런데 이 욕망 증식의 권리에서 무엇이 따라 나옵니까?"], ["In the rich, isolation and spiritual suicide; in the poor, envy and murder; for they have been given rights, but have not been shown the means of satisfying their wants.", "부자에게는 고립과 영적 자살이, 가난한 자에게는 질투와 살인이 따라 나옵니다. 권리는 주어졌으되, 그 욕구를 채울 수단은 보여 주지 않았기 때문입니다."], ["They maintain that the world is getting more and more united, more and more bound together in brotherly community, as it overcomes distance and sets thoughts flying through the air.", "그들은 세상이 거리를 정복하고 생각을 공중으로 날려 보내는 만큼, 점점 더 하나로 결합하고 점점 더 형제적 공동체로 묶여 가고 있다고 주장합니다."]], [["Alas, put no faith in such a bond of union.", "슬프게도, 그러한 결속의 유대를 믿지 마십시오."], ["Interpreting freedom as the multiplication and rapid satisfaction of desires, men distort their own nature, for many senseless and foolish desires and habits and ridiculous fancies are fostered in them.", "자유를 욕망의 증식과 신속한 충족으로 해석함으로써 사람들은 자기 자신의 본성을 일그러뜨리는데, 무분별하고 어리석은 숱한 욕망과 습관과 가소로운 공상이 그들 안에서 길러지기 때문입니다."], ["They live only for mutual envy, for luxury and ostentation.", "그들은 오직 서로를 시기하기 위하여, 사치와 허영을 위하여 살아갑니다."], ["To have dinners, visits, carriages, rank and slaves to wait on one is looked upon as a necessity, for which life, honor and human feeling are sacrificed, and men even commit suicide if they are unable to satisfy it.", "만찬을 베풀고, 방문을 다니고, 마차를 굴리고, 관등을 누리고, 시중들 노예를 거느리는 일이 하나의 필수로 여겨지며, 그것을 위하여 생명과 명예와 인간적 정감마저 희생되고, 그것을 채우지 못하면 사람들은 자살까지 합니다."], ["We see the same thing among those who are not rich, while the poor drown their unsatisfied need and their envy in drunkenness.", "부유하지 않은 자들 사이에서도 우리는 똑같은 일을 보거니와, 가난한 자들은 채워지지 않은 욕구와 그 질투를 술에 빠져 잠재웁니다."], ["But soon they will drink blood instead of wine, they are being led on to it. I ask you is such a man free?", "그러나 머지않아 그들은 포도주 대신 피를 마시게 될 것이니, 그들은 그리로 이끌려 가고 있습니다."], ["I knew one “champion of freedom”", "묻건대, 그런 인간이 자유롭습니까?"], ["who told me himself that, when he was deprived of tobacco in prison, he was so wretched at the privation that he almost went and betrayed his cause for the sake of getting tobacco again!", "나는 어느 \"자유의 투사\"를 알고 있었는데, 그가 직접 내게 털어놓기를, 감옥에서 담배를 빼앗겼을 때 그 박탈이 어찌나 비참하던지 다시 담배를 얻기 위하여 하마터면 제 대의를 배신할 뻔했다는 것이었습니다!"], ["And such a man says, “I am fighting for the cause of humanity.”", "그러면서도 그런 인간이 \"나는 인류의 대의를 위하여 싸운다\"고 말합니다."]], [["How can such a one fight? what is he fit for?", "그런 자가 어찌 싸울 수 있겠습니까?"], ["He is capable perhaps of some action quickly over, but he cannot hold out long.", "그가 무엇에 쓸모가 있겠습니까? 그는 어쩌면 금세 끝나 버리는 어떤 행동은 할 수 있을지 몰라도, 오래 견뎌 내지는 못합니다."], ["And it’s no wonder that instead of gaining freedom they have sunk into slavery, and instead of serving the cause of brotherly love and the union of humanity have fallen, on the contrary, into dissension and isolation, as my mysterious visitor and teacher said to me in my youth.", "그러니 그들이 자유를 얻기는커녕 노예 상태로 가라앉고, 형제애와 인류 결합의 대의에 봉사하기는커녕 도리어 불화와 고립에 빠져 버린 것도 이상할 게 없으니, 이는 내 젊은 시절 나의 신비로운 방문자이자 스승이 내게 일러 준 그대로입니다."], ["And therefore the idea of the service of humanity, of brotherly love and the solidarity of mankind, is more and more dying out in the world, and indeed this idea is sometimes treated with derision.", "그러므로 인류에 대한 봉사, 형제애, 인류의 연대라는 사상은 세상에서 점점 더 사그라들어 가고, 실로 이 사상은 때로 조롱거리로 취급되기까지 합니다."], ["For how can a man shake off his habits?", "인간이 어찌 자기 습관을 떨쳐 낼 수 있겠습니까?"], ["What can become of him if he is in such bondage to the habit of satisfying the innumerable desires he has created for himself?", "스스로 만들어 낸 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 욕망을 채우는 습관에 그토록 얽매여 있다면 그가 어찌 되겠습니까?"], ["He is isolated, and what concern has he with the rest of humanity?", "그는 고립되어 있으니, 나머지 인류와 무슨 상관이 있겠습니까?"], ["They have succeeded in accumulating a greater mass of objects, but the joy in the world has grown less.", "그들은 더 많은 물건을 쌓아 올리는 데는 성공했으나, 세상의 기쁨은 도리어 줄어들고 말았습니다."]], [["The monastic way is very different.", "수도원의 길은 이와는 아주 다릅니다."], ["Obedience, fasting and prayer are laughed at, yet only through them lies the way to real, true freedom.", "순종과 단식과 기도는 비웃음을 받지만, 오직 그것들을 통해서만 진정하고 참된 자유에 이르는 길이 열립니다."], ["I cut off my superfluous and unnecessary desires, I subdue my proud and wanton will and chastise it with obedience, and with God’s help I attain freedom of spirit and with it spiritual joy.", "나는 군더더기 같고 불필요한 욕망을 잘라 내고, 교만하고 방종한 내 의지를 다스리며 순종으로 그것을 매질하고, 하느님의 도우심으로 영혼의 자유와 그에 따른 영적 기쁨에 이릅니다."], ["Which is most capable of conceiving a great idea and serving it—the rich man in his isolation or the man who has freed himself from the tyranny of material things and habits?", "위대한 사상을 품고 거기 봉사할 수 있는 이는 어느 쪽이겠습니까—고립 속의 부자입니까, 아니면 물질과 습관의 폭정에서 스스로를 해방한 사람입니까?"], ["The monk is reproached for his solitude, “You have secluded yourself within the walls of the monastery for your own salvation, and have forgotten the brotherly service of humanity!”", "수도승은 자기 고독으로 인하여 비난받습니다. \"너는 네 자신의 구원을 위하여 수도원 담장 안에 스스로를 가두고, 인류를 향한 형제적 봉사는 잊어버렸다!\""], ["But we shall see which will be most zealous in the cause of brotherly love.", "그러나 형제애의 대의에 누가 더 열심일지는 두고 보아야 할 것입니다."], ["For it is not we, but they, who are in isolation, though they don’t see that.", "고립되어 있는 것은 우리가 아니라 그들이건만, 그들은 그것을 보지 못하기 때문입니다."], ["Of old, leaders of the people came from among us, and why should they not again?", "옛날에는 백성의 지도자들이 우리 가운데서 나왔거늘, 어찌 다시 그러지 못하겠습니까?"], ["The same meek and humble ascetics will rise up and go out to work for the great cause.", "똑같이 온유하고 겸손한 고행자들이 일어나 저 위대한 대의를 위하여 나아갈 것입니다."], ["The salvation of Russia comes from the people.", "러시아의 구원은 백성에게서 옵니다."], ["And the Russian monk has always been on the side of the people.", "그리고 러시아의 수도승은 언제나 백성의 편에 서 있었습니다."], ["We are isolated only if the people are isolated.", "우리가 고립되는 것은 오직 백성이 고립될 때뿐입니다."], ["The people believe as we do, and an unbelieving reformer will never do anything in Russia, even if he is sincere in heart and a genius.", "백성은 우리와 같이 믿으니, 믿음 없는 개혁가는 비록 마음이 진실하고 천재라 할지라도 러시아에서는 결코 아무 일도 이루지 못할 것입니다."], ["Remember that!", "이것을 명심하십시오!"], ["The people will meet the atheist and overcome him, and Russia will be one and orthodox.", "백성은 무신론자와 마주쳐 그를 이겨 낼 것이며, 러시아는 하나가 되어 정교를 받들 것입니다."], ["Take care of the peasant and guard his heart.", "농민을 보살피고 그의 마음을 지키십시오."], ["Go on educating him quietly.", "조용히 그를 가르쳐 나가십시오."], ["That’s your duty as monks, for the peasant has God in his heart. (f) Of Masters and Servants, and of whether it is possible for them to be Brothers in the Spirit", "그것이 수도승으로서 그대들의 의무이니, 농민의 마음속에는 하느님이 계시기 때문입니다."]], [["Of course, I don’t deny that there is sin in the peasants too.", "물론 나는 농민들 안에도 죄가 있음을 부정하지 않습니다."], ["And the fire of corruption is spreading visibly, hourly, working from above downwards.", "그리고 타락의 불길은 눈에 보이게, 시시각각, 위에서 아래로 번져 내려가고 있습니다."], ["The spirit of isolation is coming upon the people too.", "고립의 정신이 백성에게도 덮쳐 오고 있습니다."], ["Money-lenders and devourers of the commune are rising up.", "고리대금업자와 공동체를 집어삼키는 자들이 일어나고 있습니다."], ["Already the merchant grows more and more eager for rank, and strives to show himself cultured though he has not a trace of culture, and to this end meanly despises his old traditions, and is even ashamed of the faith of his fathers.", "이미 상인은 점점 더 관등을 탐내고, 교양이라곤 한 자락도 없으면서 자기를 교양 있는 사람으로 내보이려 애쓰며, 그 목적을 위하여 비루하게도 제 오랜 전통을 멸시하고, 심지어 조상들의 신앙마저 부끄러워합니다."], ["He visits princes, though he is only a peasant corrupted.", "그는 그저 타락한 농민일 뿐이면서도 공작들을 찾아다닙니다."], ["The peasants are rotting in drunkenness and cannot shake off the habit.", "농민들은 술에 절어 썩어 가면서도 그 습관을 떨쳐 내지 못합니다."], ["And what cruelty to their wives, to their children even!", "그리고 제 아내에게, 심지어 제 자식에게 베푸는 그 잔혹함이라니!"], ["All from drunkenness!", "모두가 술 때문입니다!"], ["I’ve seen in the factories children of nine years old, frail, rickety, bent and already depraved.", "나는 공장에서 아홉 살배기 아이들을 보았는데, 허약하고 구루병에 걸려 허리가 굽고 벌써 타락해 있었습니다."], ["The stuffy workshop, the din of machinery, work all day long, the vile language and the drink, the drink—is that what a little child’s heart needs?", "숨 막히는 작업장, 기계의 굉음, 온종일 이어지는 노동, 입에 담기 더러운 욕설과 술, 또 술—그것이 어린아이의 마음에 필요한 것입니까?"], ["He needs sunshine, childish play, good examples all about him, and at least a little love.", "아이에게는 햇빛이, 어린아이다운 놀이가, 사방에 둘러싼 좋은 본보기가, 그리고 적어도 약간의 사랑이 필요합니다."], ["There must be no more of this, monks, no more torturing of children, rise up and preach that, make haste, make haste!", "더는 이래서는 안 됩니다, 수도승들이여, 더는 아이들을 괴롭혀서는 안 됩니다. 일어나 그것을 설교하십시오, 서두르십시오, 서두르십시오!"]], [["But God will save Russia, for though the peasants are corrupted and cannot renounce their filthy sin, yet they know it is cursed by God and that they do wrong in sinning.", "그러나 하느님께서 러시아를 구원하실 것이니, 농민들이 타락하여 그 더러운 죄를 버리지 못한다 할지라도, 그래도 그들은 그것이 하느님께 저주받은 일이며 죄를 지음으로써 자기가 그릇된 짓을 저지른다는 것을 알고 있기 때문입니다."], ["So that our people still believe in righteousness, have faith in God and weep tears of devotion.", "그러니 우리 백성은 여전히 의로움을 믿고, 하느님을 신뢰하며, 경건의 눈물을 흘립니다."]], [["It is different with the upper classes.", "상류 계급은 다릅니다."], ["They, following science, want to base justice on reason alone, but not with Christ, as before, and they have already proclaimed that there is no crime, that there is no sin.", "그들은 학문을 좇으며 이전처럼 그리스도와 더불어서가 아니라 오직 이성에만 정의를 세우려 하고, 이미 범죄란 없으며 죄란 없다고 선포했습니다."], ["And that’s consistent, for if you have no God what is the meaning of crime?", "그것은 일관된 논리이니, 하느님이 없다면 범죄가 무슨 의미가 있겠습니까?"], ["In Europe the people are already rising up against the rich with violence, and the leaders of the people are everywhere leading them to bloodshed, and teaching them that their wrath is righteous.", "유럽에서는 백성이 이미 폭력으로 부자들에게 맞서 일어나고 있으며, 백성의 지도자들은 곳곳에서 그들을 유혈로 이끌고, 그들의 분노가 의롭다고 가르치고 있습니다."], ["But their “wrath is accursed, for it is cruel.”", "그러나 그들의 \"분노는 저주받았으니, 그것이 잔혹하기 때문\"입니다."], ["But God will save Russia as He has saved her many times.", "그러나 하느님께서는 여러 번 그러하셨듯이 러시아를 구원하실 것입니다."], ["Salvation will come from the people, from their faith and their meekness.", "구원은 백성에게서, 그들의 신앙과 그들의 온유함에서 올 것입니다."]], [["Fathers and teachers, watch over the people’s faith and this will not be a dream.", "신부님들과 스승님들이여, 백성의 신앙을 지켜보십시오, 그리하면 이것은 한낱 꿈이 아닐 것입니다."], ["I’ve been struck all my life in our great people by their dignity, their true and seemly dignity.", "나는 평생토록 우리 위대한 백성에게서 그들의 위엄, 그들의 참되고 단정한 위엄에 깊이 감동해 왔습니다."], ["I’ve seen it myself, I can testify to it, I’ve seen it and marveled at it, I’ve seen it in spite of the degraded sins and poverty-stricken appearance of our peasantry.", "나는 그것을 직접 보았고, 그것을 증언할 수 있으며, 그것을 보고 경탄했으니, 우리 농민의 타락한 죄와 빈궁에 찌든 행색에도 불구하고 나는 그것을 보았습니다."], ["They are not servile, and even after two centuries of serfdom they are free in manner and bearing, yet without insolence, and not revengeful and not envious.", "그들은 비굴하지 않으며, 두 세기에 걸친 농노 생활 뒤에도 그 몸가짐과 태도가 자유로우면서도 무례하지 않고, 앙심을 품지도 시기하지도 않습니다."], ["“You are rich and noble, you are clever and talented, well, be so, God bless you.", "\"당신은 부유하고 고귀하며, 영리하고 재능이 있으니, 그래, 그러시오, 하느님의 축복이 함께하기를."], ["I respect you, but I know that I too am a man.", "나는 당신을 존경하오, 그러나 나 또한 한 인간임을 압니다."], ["By the very fact that I respect you without envy I prove my dignity as a man.”", "시기하지 않고 당신을 존경한다는 바로 그 사실로써 나는 인간으로서 내 위엄을 증명하는 것이오.\""]], [["In truth if they don’t say this (for they don’t know how to say this yet), that is how they act.", "참으로 그들이 이렇게 말하지는 않더라도(아직 이렇게 말할 줄 모르기 때문입니다), 그들은 그렇게 처신합니다."], ["I have seen it myself, I have known it myself, and, would you believe it, the poorer our Russian peasant is, the more noticeable is that serene goodness, for the rich among them are for the most part corrupted already, and much of that is due to our carelessness and indifference.", "나는 그것을 직접 보았고, 직접 겪어 알거니와, 믿어지겠습니까, 우리 러시아 농민은 가난할수록 그 잔잔한 선량함이 더욱 두드러집니다. 그들 가운데 부유한 자들은 대부분 이미 타락해 있으며, 그 많은 부분이 우리의 무관심과 무신경 탓이기 때문입니다."], ["But God will save His people, for Russia is great in her humility.", "그러나 하느님께서는 당신의 백성을 구원하실 것이니, 러시아는 그 겸손함으로 위대하기 때문입니다."], ["I dream of seeing, and seem to see clearly already, our future.", "나는 우리의 미래를 보기를 꿈꾸며, 이미 그것을 또렷이 보는 듯합니다."], ["It will come to pass, that even the most corrupt of our rich will end by being ashamed of his riches before the poor, and the poor, seeing his humility, will understand and give way before him, will respond joyfully and kindly to his honorable shame.", "마침내 우리 부자들 가운데 가장 타락한 자조차 가난한 자들 앞에서 자기 부를 부끄러워하기에 이르고, 가난한 자들은 그의 겸손함을 보고 그것을 이해하여 그에게 길을 내주며, 그의 명예로운 부끄러움에 기쁘고 다정하게 응답하는 일이 이루어질 것입니다."], ["Believe me that it will end in that; things are moving to that.", "결국 그렇게 끝나리라는 것을 믿으십시오. 모든 것이 그리로 움직여 가고 있습니다."], ["Equality is to be found only in the spiritual dignity of man, and that will only be understood among us.", "평등은 오직 인간의 영적 위엄 안에서만 찾을 수 있으며, 그것은 우리 사이에서만 이해될 것입니다."], ["If we were brothers, there would be fraternity, but before that, they will never agree about the division of wealth.", "우리가 형제라면 형제애가 있을 것이나, 그 전에는 결코 부의 분배를 두고 합의하지 못할 것입니다."], ["We preserve the image of Christ, and it will shine forth like a precious diamond to the whole world.", "우리는 그리스도의 형상을 간직하고 있으며, 그것은 귀한 금강석처럼 온 세상에 빛을 발할 것입니다."], ["So may it be, so may it be!", "부디 그렇게 되기를, 부디 그렇게 되기를!"]], [["Fathers and teachers, a touching incident befell me once.", "신부님들과 스승님들이여, 언젠가 내게 가슴 뭉클한 일이 있었습니다."], ["In my wanderings I met in the town of K. my old orderly, Afanasy.", "떠돌이 길에 나는 K시에서 옛 종졸이었던 아파나시를 만났습니다."], ["It was eight years since I had parted from him.", "그와 헤어진 지 여덟 해가 되던 때였습니다."], ["He chanced to see me in the market-place, recognized me, ran up to me, and how delighted he was!", "그는 우연히 장터에서 나를 보고는 알아보고 달려왔는데, 얼마나 기뻐하던지요! 그는 그야말로 내게 와락 달려들었습니다."], ["He simply pounced on me: “Master dear, is it you?", "\"나리, 나리이십니까? 정말 제가 뵙는 분이 나리이십니까?\""], ["Is it really you I see?” He took me home with him.", "그러더니 그는 나를 자기 집으로 데려갔습니다."]], [["He was no longer in the army, he was married and already had two little children.", "그는 더 이상 군에 있지 않았고, 결혼하여 벌써 어린 자식이 둘 있었습니다."], ["He and his wife earned their living as costermongers in the market-place.", "그와 그의 아내는 장터에서 행상으로 생계를 꾸리고 있었습니다."], ["His room was poor, but bright and clean.", "그의 방은 가난했으나 밝고 깨끗했습니다."], ["He made me sit down, set the samovar, sent for his wife, as though my appearance were a festival for them.", "그는 나를 앉히고 사모바르를 올리고 아내를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 내가 찾아온 것이 그들에게 마치 축제라도 되는 듯했습니다."], ["He brought me his children: “Bless them, Father.”", "그는 자기 아이들을 데려왔습니다. \"이 아이들을 축복해 주십시오, 신부님.\""]], [["“Is it for me to bless them?", "\"내가 이 아이들을 축복할 자격이 있겠는가?"], ["I am only a humble monk.", "나는 한낱 보잘것없는 수도승일 뿐일세."], ["I will pray for them.", "이 아이들을 위해 기도하겠네."], ["And for you, Afanasy Pavlovitch, I have prayed every day since that day, for it all came from you,”", "그리고 아파나시 파블로비치, 그날 이후로 나는 날마다 자네를 위해 기도해 왔다네, 모든 것이 자네로부터 비롯되었으니 말일세.\" 나는 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["said I. And I explained that to him as well as I could.", "그리고 그것을 할 수 있는 데까지 그에게 설명해 주었습니다."], ["And what do you think?", "그런데 어찌 되었는지 아십니까?"], ["The man kept gazing at me and could not believe that I, his former master, an officer, was now before him in such a guise and position; it made him shed tears.", "그는 줄곧 나를 바라보면서, 한때 자기 주인이었고 장교였던 내가 이제 이런 행색과 처지로 자기 앞에 서 있다는 것을 믿지 못했습니다. 그것이 그로 하여금 눈물을 흘리게 했습니다."]], [["“Why are you weeping?”", "\"어찌하여 우는가?\""], ["said I, “better rejoice over me, dear friend, whom I can never forget, for my path is a glad and joyful one.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"차라리 나를 두고 기뻐하게나, 결코 잊을 수 없는 정다운 벗이여, 나의 길은 즐겁고 기쁜 길이니 말일세.\""]], [["He did not say much, but kept sighing and shaking his head over me tenderly.", "그는 말은 많이 하지 않았으나, 줄곧 한숨을 쉬며 다정하게 나를 향해 고개를 가로저었습니다."]], [["“What has became of your fortune?”", "\"나리의 재산은 어찌 되었습니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었습니다."]], [["“I gave it to the monastery,”", "\"수도원에 내놓았다네.\" 내가 대답했습니다."], ["I answered; “we live in common.”", "\"우리는 모든 것을 함께 나누며 산다네.\""]], [["After tea I began saying good-by, and suddenly he brought out half a rouble as an offering to the monastery, and another half-rouble I saw him thrusting hurriedly into my hand: “That’s for you in your wanderings, it may be of use to you, Father.”", "차를 마신 뒤 나는 작별 인사를 하기 시작했는데, 갑자기 그가 수도원에 바치는 헌금으로 반 루블을 내놓았고, 또 다른 반 루블을 황급히 내 손에 쥐여 주는 것을 나는 보았습니다. \"이것은 떠돌이 길에 나리께 드리는 것입니다, 쓸모가 있을 겁니다, 신부님.\""]], [["I took his half-rouble, bowed to him and his wife, and went out rejoicing.", "나는 그의 반 루블을 받아 그와 그의 아내에게 절하고는 기쁜 마음으로 나왔습니다."], ["And on my way I thought: “Here we are both now, he at home and I on the road, sighing and shaking our heads, no doubt, and yet smiling joyfully in the gladness of our hearts, remembering how God brought about our meeting.”", "그리고 길을 가며 나는 생각했습니다. \"이제 우리 둘은, 그는 집에 있고 나는 길 위에 있으면서, 분명 한숨을 쉬고 고개를 가로젓고 있겠지만, 그래도 하느님께서 우리의 만남을 어떻게 이루어 주셨는가를 떠올리며 마음의 기쁨 속에 즐거이 미소 짓고 있으리라.\""]], [["I have never seen him again since then.", "그 뒤로 나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했습니다."], ["I had been his master and he my servant, but now when we exchanged a loving kiss with softened hearts, there was a great human bond between us.", "나는 그의 주인이었고 그는 나의 종이었으나, 이제 우리가 누그러진 마음으로 정다운 입맞춤을 나누었을 때 우리 사이에는 위대한 인간적 유대가 있었습니다."], ["I have thought a great deal about that, and now what I think is this: Is it so inconceivable that that grand and simple-hearted unity might in due time become universal among the Russian people?", "나는 그것에 관하여 많은 것을 생각해 왔으며, 이제 내가 생각하는 바는 이러합니다. 그 장엄하고 소박한 마음의 하나됨이 때가 이르러 러시아 백성 가운데 보편적인 것이 된다는 것이 그토록 상상도 못 할 일이겠습니까?"], ["I believe that it will come to pass and that the time is at hand.", "나는 그것이 이루어지리라고, 그리고 그때가 가까이 왔다고 믿습니다."]], [["And of servants I will add this: In old days when I was young I was often angry with servants; “the cook had served something too hot, the orderly had not brushed my clothes.” But what taught me better then was a thought of my dear brother’s, which I had heard from him in childhood: “Am I worth it, that another should serve me and be ordered about by me in his poverty and ignorance?” And I wondered at the time that such simple and self-evident ideas should be so slow to occur to our minds.", "그리고 종들에 관하여 나는 이것을 덧붙이겠습니다. 옛날 내가 젊었을 때 나는 종들에게 자주 화를 냈습니다. \"요리사가 너무 뜨거운 것을 내왔다느니, 종졸이 내 옷을 솔질해 두지 않았다느니\" 하면서 말입니다. 그러나 그때 나를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 준 것은 어린 시절 내 사랑하는 형에게서 들었던 한 생각이었습니다. \"다른 사람이 그 가난과 무지 속에서 나를 섬기고 내게 부림을 받는다는 것이, 내가 과연 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 자인가?\" 그리고 나는 그토록 단순하고 자명한 생각이 어찌하여 우리 마음에 그처럼 더디게 떠오르는가를 그 무렵 의아하게 여겼습니다."]], [["It is impossible that there should be no servants in the world, but act so that your servant may be freer in spirit than if he were not a servant.", "세상에 종이 없을 수는 없는 일입니다. 그러나 그대의 종이 종이 아닐 때보다 영혼에 있어 더 자유롭도록 처신하십시오."], ["And why cannot I be a servant to my servant and even let him see it, and that without any pride on my part or any mistrust on his?", "그리고 어찌하여 내가 내 종의 종이 되지 못하고, 게다가 그것을 그가 보도록 하지 못하겠습니까, 그것도 내 쪽에 아무런 교만도 없고 그의 쪽에 아무런 불신도 없이 말입니다?"], ["Why should not my servant be like my own kindred, so that I may take him into my family and rejoice in doing so?", "어찌하여 내 종이 내 친족과 같아져서, 내가 그를 내 가족으로 받아들이고 그렇게 함을 기뻐하지 못하겠습니까?"], ["Even now this can be done, but it will lead to the grand unity of men in the future, when a man will not seek servants for himself, or desire to turn his fellow creatures into servants as he does now, but on the contrary, will long with his whole heart to be the servant of all, as the Gospel teaches.", "지금도 이것은 이루어질 수 있으나, 이것은 장차 인류의 장엄한 하나됨으로 이끌어 갈 것이니, 그때에는 사람이 지금처럼 제 종을 구하거나 제 이웃을 종으로 만들고자 하지 않고, 도리어 복음이 가르치는 대로 온 마음으로 모든 이의 종이 되기를 갈망할 것입니다."]], [["And can it be a dream, that in the end man will find his joy only in deeds of light and mercy, and not in cruel pleasures as now, in gluttony, fornication, ostentation, boasting and envious rivalry of one with the other?", "그리고 마침내 인간이 지금처럼 잔혹한 쾌락, 곧 탐식과 음행과 허영과 자랑과 서로를 향한 시기 어린 경쟁에서가 아니라, 오직 빛과 자비의 행위에서만 자기 기쁨을 찾게 되리라는 것이 한낱 꿈일 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I firmly believe that it is not and that the time is at hand.", "나는 그것이 꿈이 아니며 그때가 가까이 왔음을 굳게 믿습니다."], ["People laugh and ask: “When will that time come and does it look like coming?”", "사람들은 비웃으며 묻습니다. \"그때가 언제 오겠으며, 그것이 올 듯이나 보이는가?\""], ["I believe that with Christ’s help we shall accomplish this great thing.", "나는 그리스도의 도우심으로 우리가 이 위대한 일을 이루어 내리라고 믿습니다."], ["And how many ideas there have been on earth in the history of man which were unthinkable ten years before they appeared!", "그리고 인간의 역사에서, 나타나기 십 년 전만 해도 생각조차 할 수 없었던 사상이 이 땅에 얼마나 많았습니까!"], ["Yet when their destined hour had come, they came forth and spread over the whole earth.", "그러나 정해진 때가 이르자 그것들은 나타나 온 땅에 퍼져 나갔습니다."], ["So it will be with us, and our people will shine forth in the world, and all men will say: “The stone which the builders rejected has become the corner-stone of the building.”", "우리도 그러할 것이며, 우리 백성은 세상에 빛을 발할 것이고, 모든 사람이 이렇게 말할 것입니다. \"건축자들이 버린 돌이 집의 모퉁잇돌이 되었도다.\""]], [["And we may ask the scornful themselves: If our hope is a dream, when will you build up your edifice and order things justly by your intellect alone, without Christ?", "그리고 우리는 비웃는 자들 자신에게도 물을 수 있습니다. 만일 우리의 희망이 한낱 꿈이라면, 그대들은 그리스도 없이 오직 그대들의 지성만으로 언제 그대들의 건물을 세우고 세상사를 의롭게 질서 지을 것입니까?"], ["If they declare that it is they who are advancing towards unity, only the most simple-hearted among them believe it, so that one may positively marvel at such simplicity.", "만일 그들이, 하나됨을 향해 나아가는 것은 바로 자기들이라고 단언한다면, 그들 가운데 오직 가장 순진한 자들만이 그것을 믿을 따름이니, 그 단순함에는 그야말로 경탄할 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Of a truth, they have more fantastic dreams than we.", "참으로 그들은 우리보다 더 허황된 꿈을 품고 있습니다."], ["They aim at justice, but, denying Christ, they will end by flooding the earth with blood, for blood cries out for blood, and he that taketh up the sword shall perish by the sword.", "그들은 정의를 겨냥하나, 그리스도를 부정하므로 마침내 온 땅을 피로 적시고 말 것이니, 피는 피를 부르고, 칼을 잡는 자는 칼로 망할 것이기 때문입니다."], ["And if it were not for Christ’s covenant, they would slaughter one another down to the last two men on earth.", "그리고 그리스도의 언약이 아니었던들, 그들은 이 땅에 마지막 두 사람이 남을 때까지 서로를 도륙했을 것입니다."], ["And those two last men would not be able to restrain each other in their pride, and the one would slay the other and then himself.", "그리고 그 마지막 두 사람조차 제 교만 속에서 서로를 억제하지 못하여, 하나가 다른 하나를 죽이고 그런 다음 제 자신마저 죽였을 것입니다."], ["And that would come to pass, were it not for the promise of Christ that for the sake of the humble and meek the days shall be shortened.", "그리고 온유하고 겸손한 자들을 위하여 그날들을 줄이시리라는 그리스도의 약속이 아니었던들, 그런 일이 일어났을 것입니다."]], [["While I was still wearing an officer’s uniform after my duel, I talked about servants in general society, and I remember every one was amazed at me.", "결투 후 아직 장교의 군복을 입고 있을 무렵, 나는 사교 모임에서 종들에 관하여 이야기했는데, 모두가 나를 보고 놀라워하던 것이 기억납니다."], ["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["they asked, “are we to make our servants sit down on the sofa and offer them tea?”", "그들이 물었습니다. \"우리더러 종들을 소파에 앉히고 차를 대접하란 말입니까?\""], ["And I answered them: “Why not, sometimes at least?”", "그래서 나는 그들에게 대답했습니다. \"어찌 안 되겠습니까, 적어도 가끔은 말입니다.\""], ["Every one laughed.", "모두가 웃었습니다."], ["Their question was frivolous and my answer was not clear; but the thought in it was to some extent right.", "그들의 물음은 경박했고 내 대답도 명료하지 못했으나, 그 안에 담긴 생각은 어느 정도 옳았습니다."]], [["(g) Of Prayer, of Love, and of Contact with other Worlds\nYoung man, be not forgetful of prayer.", "젊은이여, 기도를 잊지 마십시오."], ["Every time you pray, if your prayer is sincere, there will be new feeling and new meaning in it, which will give you fresh courage, and you will understand that prayer is an education.", "그대가 기도할 때마다, 그 기도가 진실하다면, 거기에는 새로운 느낌과 새로운 의미가 깃들 것이며, 그것이 그대에게 새 용기를 줄 것이고, 그대는 기도가 하나의 교육임을 깨닫게 될 것입니다."], ["Remember, too, every day, and whenever you can, repeat to yourself, “Lord, have mercy on all who appear before Thee to-day.”", "또한 날마다, 그리고 할 수 있을 때마다 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌십시오. \"주여, 오늘 당신 앞에 나아오는 모든 이에게 자비를 베푸소서.\""], ["For every hour and every moment thousands of men leave life on this earth, and their souls appear before God.", "매시간 매 순간 수천 명의 사람이 이 땅에서 생을 떠나며, 그 영혼들이 하느님 앞에 나아가기 때문입니다."], ["And how many of them depart in solitude, unknown, sad, dejected that no one mourns for them or even knows whether they have lived or not!", "그리고 그들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 고독 속에, 알아주는 이도 없이, 슬프게, 아무도 자기를 애도해 주지 않고 자기가 살았는지조차 알지 못하는 채로 낙담하여 떠나갑니까!"], ["And behold, from the other end of the earth perhaps, your prayer for their rest will rise up to God though you knew them not nor they you.", "그런데 보십시오, 어쩌면 땅의 저편 끝에서, 그대가 그들을 알지 못하고 그들도 그대를 알지 못했건만, 그들의 안식을 비는 그대의 기도가 하느님께로 올라갈 것입니다."], ["How touching it must be to a soul standing in dread before the Lord to feel at that instant that, for him too, there is one to pray, that there is a fellow creature left on earth to love him too!", "주님 앞에 두려움에 떨며 서 있는 한 영혼이, 바로 그 순간 자기를 위해서도 기도해 주는 이가 있음을, 이 땅에 자기를 사랑해 주는 한 동족이 남아 있음을 느낀다는 것이 얼마나 가슴 뭉클한 일이겠습니까!"], ["And God will look on you both more graciously, for if you have had so much pity on him, how much will He have pity Who is infinitely more loving and merciful than you!", "그리고 하느님께서는 그대들 둘을 더 자비로이 굽어보실 것이니, 그대가 그를 그토록 가엾이 여겼다면, 그대보다 한없이 더 사랑이 많고 자비로우신 그분께서는 얼마나 더 가엾이 여기시겠습니까!"], ["And He will forgive him for your sake.", "그리고 그분께서는 그대를 보아서 그를 용서하실 것입니다."]], [["Brothers, have no fear of men’s sin.", "형제들이여, 사람의 죄를 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["Love a man even in his sin, for that is the semblance of Divine Love and is the highest love on earth.", "사람을 그 죄 가운데서도 사랑하십시오. 그것이 신적 사랑의 모상이며 이 땅에서 가장 높은 사랑이기 때문입니다."], ["Love all God’s creation, the whole and every grain of sand in it.", "하느님의 모든 피조물을, 그 전체와 그 안의 모래 한 알까지 사랑하십시오."], ["Love every leaf, every ray of God’s light.", "잎사귀 하나하나를, 하느님 빛의 빛줄기 하나하나를 사랑하십시오."], ["Love the animals, love the plants, love everything.", "짐승들을 사랑하고, 식물들을 사랑하고, 모든 것을 사랑하십시오."], ["If you love everything, you will perceive the divine mystery in things.", "그대가 모든 것을 사랑하면, 사물들 속에 깃든 신적 신비를 깨닫게 될 것입니다."], ["Once you perceive it, you will begin to comprehend it better every day.", "일단 그것을 깨닫고 나면, 날마다 그것을 더 잘 이해하기 시작할 것입니다."], ["And you will come at last to love the whole world with an all-embracing love.", "그리고 마침내 그대는 온 세상을 끌어안는 사랑으로 사랑하기에 이를 것입니다."], ["Love the animals: God has given them the rudiments of thought and joy untroubled.", "짐승들을 사랑하십시오. 하느님께서는 그들에게 사유의 단초와 흐트러짐 없는 기쁨을 주셨습니다."], ["Do not trouble it, don’t harass them, don’t deprive them of their happiness, don’t work against God’s intent.", "그것을 어지럽히지 말고, 그들을 들볶지 말고, 그들의 행복을 앗아 가지 말고, 하느님의 뜻을 거슬러 일하지 마십시오. 인간이여, 짐승보다 우월하다고 우쭐대지 마십시오."], ["Man, do not pride yourself on superiority to the animals; they are without sin, and you, with your greatness, defile the earth by your appearance on it, and leave the traces of your foulness after you—alas, it is true of almost every one of us!", "그들은 죄가 없으나, 그대는 그 위대함을 지니고서도 이 땅에 나타남으로써 땅을 더럽히고, 그대가 지나간 뒤에 그대의 추악한 자취를 남기니—슬프게도, 이것은 우리 거의 모두에게 사실입니다! 특히 아이들을 사랑하십시오."], ["Love children especially, for they too are sinless like the angels; they live to soften and purify our hearts and as it were to guide us.", "그들 또한 천사들처럼 죄가 없기 때문입니다. 그들은 우리 마음을 누그러뜨리고 정화하기 위하여, 말하자면 우리를 이끌기 위하여 살아갑니다."], ["Woe to him who offends a child!", "아이를 노엽게 하는 자에게 화가 있을진저!"], ["Father Anfim taught me to love children.", "안핌 신부는 내게 아이들을 사랑하라고 가르쳐 주었습니다."], ["The kind, silent man used often on our wanderings to spend the farthings given us on sweets and cakes for the children.", "그 친절하고 과묵한 사람은 우리의 떠돌이 길에서 사람들이 우리에게 준 동전들을 자주 아이들에게 줄 사탕이며 과자에 썼습니다."], ["He could not pass by a child without emotion.", "그는 아이를 보면 마음이 동하지 않고서는 그냥 지나치지 못했습니다."], ["That’s the nature of the man.", "그것이 그 사람의 천성이었습니다."]], [["At some thoughts one stands perplexed, especially at the sight of men’s sin, and wonders whether one should use force or humble love.", "어떤 생각들 앞에서는 사람이 어찌할 바를 몰라 망설이게 되니, 특히 사람의 죄를 목격할 때, 힘을 써야 할지 겸손한 사랑을 써야 할지 의아해집니다."], ["Always decide to use humble love.", "언제나 겸손한 사랑을 쓰기로 결단하십시오."], ["If you resolve on that once for all, you may subdue the whole world.", "그것을 단번에 영영 결단한다면, 그대는 온 세상을 굴복시킬 수 있습니다."], ["Loving humility is marvelously strong, the strongest of all things, and there is nothing else like it.", "사랑이 깃든 겸손은 경이로울 만큼 강하며, 모든 것 가운데 가장 강하니, 그것에 견줄 만한 것은 달리 없습니다."]], [["Every day and every hour, every minute, walk round yourself and watch yourself, and see that your image is a seemly one.", "날마다 매시간 매분, 그대 자신을 돌아보며 그대 자신을 살피고, 그대의 형상이 단정한지 보십시오."], ["You pass by a little child, you pass by, spiteful, with ugly words, with wrathful heart; you may not have noticed the child, but he has seen you, and your image, unseemly and ignoble, may remain in his defenseless heart.", "그대가 어린아이 곁을 지나는데, 악의를 품고, 추한 말을 내뱉으며, 분노한 마음으로 지나친다 합시다. 그대는 그 아이를 알아채지 못했을지 몰라도 그 아이는 그대를 보았으며, 단정치 못하고 비루한 그대의 형상이 그 무방비한 마음에 남을지도 모릅니다."], ["You don’t know it, but you may have sown an evil seed in him and it may grow, and all because you were not careful before the child, because you did not foster in yourself a careful, actively benevolent love.", "그대는 그것을 알지 못하지만, 어쩌면 그대는 그 아이 안에 악의 씨앗을 뿌렸을지 모르며, 그것이 자라날 수도 있으니, 모두가 그대가 아이 앞에서 조심하지 않았기 때문이요, 그대 안에 조심스럽고 능동적으로 자애로운 사랑을 길러 두지 않았기 때문입니다."], ["Brothers, love is a teacher; but one must know how to acquire it, for it is hard to acquire, it is dearly bought, it is won slowly by long labor.", "형제들이여, 사랑은 스승이지만, 그것을 어떻게 얻는지 알아야 합니다. 그것은 얻기 어렵고, 비싼 값을 치러야 하며, 오랜 수고로 더디게 쟁취되기 때문입니다."], ["For we must love not only occasionally, for a moment, but for ever.", "우리는 어쩌다 한 번, 한순간만 사랑할 것이 아니라 영원토록 사랑해야 하기 때문입니다."], ["Every one can love occasionally, even the wicked can.", "어쩌다 한 번 사랑하는 것이라면 누구나 할 수 있고, 악한 자조차 할 수 있습니다."]], [["My brother asked the birds to forgive him; that sounds senseless, but it is right; for all is like an ocean, all is flowing and blending; a touch in one place sets up movement at the other end of the earth.", "내 형은 새들에게 자기를 용서해 달라고 빌었습니다. 그것은 분별없는 소리로 들리지만 옳은 일입니다."], ["It may be senseless to beg forgiveness of the birds, but birds would be happier at your side—a little happier, anyway—and children and all animals, if you were nobler than you are now.", "만물은 바다와 같아서 모든 것이 흐르며 섞이고, 한곳을 건드리면 땅의 저편 끝에서 움직임이 일어나기 때문입니다. 새들에게 용서를 빈다는 것이 분별없는 일일지 모르나, 그대가 지금보다 더 고결하다면 새들도 그대 곁에서 더 행복할 것이요—어쨌든 조금은 더 행복할 것이며—아이들도, 모든 짐승도 그러할 것입니다."], ["It’s all like an ocean, I tell you.", "거듭 말하건대, 만물은 바다와 같습니다."], ["Then you would pray to the birds too, consumed by an all-embracing love, in a sort of transport, and pray that they too will forgive you your sin.", "그러면 그대 또한 모든 것을 끌어안는 사랑에 사로잡혀, 일종의 황홀경 속에서 새들에게도 기도하며, 그들 또한 그대의 죄를 용서해 주기를 빌게 될 것입니다."], ["Treasure this ecstasy, however senseless it may seem to men.", "사람들에게는 그것이 아무리 분별없어 보일지라도, 그 환희를 소중히 간직하십시오."]], [["My friends, pray to God for gladness.", "벗들이여, 기쁨을 얻고자 하느님께 기도하십시오."], ["Be glad as children, as the birds of heaven.", "어린아이처럼, 하늘의 새들처럼 기뻐하십시오."], ["And let not the sin of men confound you in your doings.", "그리고 그대들이 하는 일에서 사람의 죄가 그대들을 어지럽히지 못하게 하십시오."], ["Fear not that it will wear away your work and hinder its being accomplished.", "그것이 그대들의 일을 닳아 없애고 그 성취를 가로막으리라고 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["Do not say, “Sin is mighty, wickedness is mighty, evil environment is mighty, and we are lonely and helpless, and evil environment is wearing us away and hindering our good work from being done.”", "\"죄는 강력하고, 사악함은 강력하며, 악한 환경은 강력하건만, 우리는 외롭고 무력하니, 악한 환경이 우리를 닳아 없애며 우리의 선한 일이 이루어지는 것을 가로막는다\"고 말하지 마십시오."], ["Fly from that dejection, children!", "그 낙담에서 달아나십시오, 아이들이여!"], ["There is only one means of salvation, then take yourself and make yourself responsible for all men’s sins, that is the truth, you know, friends, for as soon as you sincerely make yourself responsible for everything and for all men, you will see at once that it is really so, and that you are to blame for every one and for all things.", "구원의 길은 오직 하나뿐이니, 그대 자신을 붙들어 모든 사람의 죄에 대하여 스스로 책임을 지도록 하십시오. 그것이 진리입니다, 벗들이여."], ["But throwing your own indolence and impotence on others you will end by sharing the pride of Satan and murmuring against God.", "그대가 진실로 모든 것에 대하여, 모든 사람에 대하여 스스로 책임을 지자마자, 그대는 그것이 정말로 그러하며 그대가 모든 이에게, 모든 일에 대하여 죄가 있음을 단번에 알게 될 것이기 때문입니다. 그러나 그대 자신의 나태와 무력을 남에게 떠넘기다 보면, 그대는 결국 사탄의 교만에 가담하여 하느님께 불평을 늘어놓기에 이를 것입니다."]], [["Of the pride of Satan what I think is this: it is hard for us on earth to comprehend it, and therefore it is so easy to fall into error and to share it, even imagining that we are doing something grand and fine.", "사탄의 교만에 관하여 내가 생각하는 바는 이러합니다. 그것은 이 땅의 우리로서는 헤아리기 어려운 것이므로, 거기에 빠져 그것에 가담하기가 그토록 쉬우며, 심지어 우리가 무언가 장엄하고 훌륭한 일을 하고 있다고 착각하기까지 합니다."], ["Indeed, many of the strongest feelings and movements of our nature we cannot comprehend on earth.", "실로 우리 본성의 가장 강한 감정과 충동 가운데 많은 것을 우리는 이 땅에서 헤아릴 수 없습니다."], ["Let not that be a stumbling-block, and think not that it may serve as a justification to you for anything.", "그것이 걸림돌이 되게 하지 말며, 그것이 그대에게 무엇에 대한 변명으로 쓰일 수 있다고 생각하지 마십시오."], ["For the Eternal Judge asks of you what you can comprehend and not what you cannot.", "영원하신 심판자께서는 그대에게 헤아릴 수 없는 것이 아니라 헤아릴 수 있는 것을 물으시기 때문입니다."], ["You will know that yourself hereafter, for you will behold all things truly then and will not dispute them.", "그대는 훗날 그것을 스스로 알게 될 것이니, 그때에는 모든 것을 참되이 보게 되어 그것들에 시비를 걸지 않을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["On earth, indeed, we are as it were astray, and if it were not for the precious image of Christ before us, we should be undone and altogether lost, as was the human race before the flood.", "참으로 이 땅에서 우리는 말하자면 길 잃은 자와 같으며, 우리 앞에 그리스도의 귀한 형상이 없었던들 우리는 홍수 이전의 인류가 그러했듯이 파멸하여 송두리째 길을 잃었을 것입니다."], ["Much on earth is hidden from us, but to make up for that we have been given a precious mystic sense of our living bond with the other world, with the higher heavenly world, and the roots of our thoughts and feelings are not here but in other worlds.", "이 땅에서는 많은 것이 우리에게 감추어져 있으나, 그것을 메우고자 우리에게는 다른 세계, 더 높은 천상의 세계와 우리가 살아 있는 채로 맺은 유대에 대한 귀한 신비적 감각이 주어졌으며, 우리 생각과 느낌의 뿌리는 여기가 아니라 다른 세계들에 있습니다."], ["That is why the philosophers say that we cannot apprehend the reality of things on earth.", "철학자들이 우리가 이 땅에서 사물의 실재를 파악할 수 없다고 말하는 것은 바로 그 때문입니다."]], [["God took seeds from different worlds and sowed them on this earth, and His garden grew up and everything came up that could come up, but what grows lives and is alive only through the feeling of its contact with other mysterious worlds.", "하느님께서는 여러 세계로부터 씨앗들을 가져다 이 땅에 뿌리셨고, 그분의 정원이 자라나 자라날 수 있는 모든 것이 돋아났으나, 자라나는 것은 다른 신비로운 세계들과 맞닿아 있다는 느낌을 통해서만 살아 있고 또 살아갑니다."], ["If that feeling grows weak or is destroyed in you, the heavenly growth will die away in you.", "만일 그대 안에서 그 느낌이 약해지거나 사라진다면, 그대 안의 천상적 성장도 시들어 버릴 것입니다."], ["Then you will be indifferent to life and even grow to hate it.", "그러면 그대는 삶에 무관심해지고, 심지어 삶을 미워하기에 이를 것입니다."], ["That’s what I think.", "그것이 내 생각입니다."]], [["(h) Can a Man judge his Fellow Creatures?", "그대가 누구도 심판할 수 없음을 특히 명심하십시오."], ["Faith to the End\nRemember particularly that you cannot be a judge of any one. For no one can judge a criminal, until he recognizes that he is just such a criminal as the man standing before him, and that he perhaps is more than all men to blame for that crime.", "자기가 자기 앞에 서 있는 사람과 똑같은 죄인이며, 어쩌면 그 죄에 대하여 자기야말로 모든 사람보다 더 책임이 있음을 깨닫기 전까지는, 그 누구도 죄인을 심판할 수 없기 때문입니다."], ["When he understands that, he will be able to be a judge.", "그것을 이해할 때에야 그는 심판자가 될 수 있을 것입니다."], ["Though that sounds absurd, it is true.", "그것이 터무니없게 들릴지라도 그것은 참입니다."], ["If I had been righteous myself, perhaps there would have been no criminal standing before me.", "만일 나 자신이 의로웠던들, 어쩌면 내 앞에 서 있는 죄인은 없었을 것입니다."], ["If you can take upon yourself the crime of the criminal your heart is judging, take it at once, suffer for him yourself, and let him go without reproach.", "만일 그대 마음이 심판하고 있는 그 죄인의 죄를 그대가 떠맡을 수 있다면, 당장 그것을 떠맡아 그를 위하여 그대 자신이 고통받고, 그를 책망 없이 보내 주십시오."], ["And even if the law itself makes you his judge, act in the same spirit so far as possible, for he will go away and condemn himself more bitterly than you have done.", "그리고 설령 법 자체가 그대를 그의 심판자로 세운다 할지라도, 할 수 있는 한 같은 정신으로 행하십시오. 그는 떠나가 그대가 한 것보다 더 쓰라리게 자기 자신을 단죄할 것이기 때문입니다."], ["If, after your kiss, he goes away untouched, mocking at you, do not let that be a stumbling-block to you.", "만일 그대의 입맞춤에도 그가 마음 동하지 않은 채 그대를 비웃으며 떠나간다 해도, 그것이 그대에게 걸림돌이 되게 하지 마십시오."], ["It shows his time has not yet come, but it will come in due course.", "그것은 그의 때가 아직 이르지 않았음을 보여 줄 뿐이며, 그 때는 머지않아 올 것입니다."], ["And if it come not, no matter; if not he, then another in his place will understand and suffer, and judge and condemn himself, and the truth will be fulfilled.", "그리고 설령 그 때가 오지 않더라도 상관없으니, 그가 아니면 그를 대신할 또 다른 이가 이해하고 고통받으며, 자기 자신을 심판하고 단죄할 것이요, 진리는 이루어질 것입니다."], ["Believe that, believe it without doubt; for in that lies all the hope and faith of the saints.", "그것을 믿으십시오, 의심 없이 믿으십시오. 성인들의 모든 희망과 신앙이 바로 그 안에 있기 때문입니다."]], [["Work without ceasing.", "쉼 없이 일하십시오."], ["If you remember in the night as you go to sleep, “I have not done what I ought to have done,” rise up at once and do it.", "밤에 잠자리에 들며 \"나는 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하지 않았다\"고 떠오른다면, 당장 일어나 그것을 하십시오."], ["If the people around you are spiteful and callous and will not hear you, fall down before them and beg their forgiveness; for in truth you are to blame for their not wanting to hear you.", "그대 주위의 사람들이 악의에 차고 냉담하여 그대의 말을 들으려 하지 않거든, 그들 앞에 엎드려 용서를 비십시오. 참으로 그들이 그대의 말을 들으려 하지 않는 것은 그대에게 책임이 있기 때문입니다."], ["And if you cannot speak to them in their bitterness, serve them in silence and in humility, never losing hope.", "그리고 그들의 쓰라림 속에서 그들에게 말을 건넬 수 없거든, 침묵과 겸손 가운데 그들을 섬기되 결코 희망을 잃지 마십시오."], ["If all men abandon you and even drive you away by force, then when you are left alone fall on the earth and kiss it, water it with your tears and it will bring forth fruit even though no one has seen or heard you in your solitude.", "만일 모든 사람이 그대를 저버리고 심지어 힘으로 그대를 내쫓는다 해도, 그때 홀로 남거든 땅에 엎드려 그것에 입 맞추고 그대의 눈물로 그것을 적시십시오, 그리하면 비록 그대의 고독을 본 이도 들은 이도 없을지라도 땅은 열매를 맺을 것입니다."], ["Believe to the end, even if all men went astray and you were left the only one faithful; bring your offering even then and praise God in your loneliness.", "끝까지 믿으십시오, 설령 모든 사람이 길을 잃고 그대만이 신실한 자로 남았다 할지라도. 그때에도 그대의 제물을 바치고 그대의 고독 속에서 하느님을 찬양하십시오."], ["And if two of you are gathered together—then there is a whole world, a world of living love.", "그리고 그대들 둘이 함께 모인다면—그곳에는 온전한 하나의 세계가, 살아 있는 사랑의 세계가 있습니다."], ["Embrace each other tenderly and praise God, for if only in you two His truth has been fulfilled.", "서로를 다정히 끌어안고 하느님을 찬양하십시오. 비록 그대들 둘 안에서만이라도 그분의 진리가 이루어졌기 때문입니다."]], [["If you sin yourself and grieve even unto death for your sins or for your sudden sin, then rejoice for others, rejoice for the righteous man, rejoice that if you have sinned, he is righteous and has not sinned.", "만일 그대 자신이 죄를 짓고 그대의 죄로 인하여, 또는 그대의 갑작스러운 죄로 인하여 죽을 듯이 슬퍼한다면, 그때에는 다른 이들을 위하여 기뻐하고, 의로운 사람을 위하여 기뻐하며, 그대가 죄를 지었어도 그는 의로워 죄를 짓지 않았음을 기뻐하십시오."]], [["If the evil-doing of men moves you to indignation and overwhelming distress, even to a desire for vengeance on the evil-doers, shun above all things that feeling.", "만일 사람들의 악행이 그대를 분노와 압도적인 비탄으로, 심지어 악행자들에 대한 복수의 욕망으로까지 몰아간다면, 무엇보다도 그 감정을 피하십시오."], ["Go at once and seek suffering for yourself, as though you were yourself guilty of that wrong.", "당장 가서, 마치 그대 자신이 그 악행의 죄를 지은 것인 양 그대 자신을 위한 고통을 구하십시오."], ["Accept that suffering and bear it and your heart will find comfort, and you will understand that you too are guilty, for you might have been a light to the evil-doers, even as the one man sinless, and you were not a light to them.", "그 고통을 받아들이고 견디면 그대의 마음은 위안을 얻을 것이며, 그대 또한 죄가 있음을 깨닫게 될 것이니, 그대가 죄 없는 한 사람으로서 악행자들에게 빛이 되어 줄 수도 있었건만 그들에게 빛이 되어 주지 못했기 때문입니다."], ["If you had been a light, you would have lightened the path for others too, and the evil-doer might perhaps have been saved by your light from his sin.", "만일 그대가 빛이었던들, 다른 이들을 위해서도 길을 밝혀 주었을 것이요, 그 악행자도 어쩌면 그대의 빛으로 인하여 자기 죄에서 구원받았을지 모릅니다."], ["And even though your light was shining, yet you see men were not saved by it, hold firm and doubt not the power of the heavenly light.", "그리고 비록 그대의 빛이 빛나고 있었음에도 사람들이 그것으로 구원받지 못한 것을 그대가 본다 할지라도, 굳건히 버티며 천상의 빛의 힘을 의심하지 마십시오."], ["Believe that if they were not saved, they will be saved hereafter.", "만일 그들이 구원받지 못했다면 훗날에 구원받으리라고 믿으십시오."], ["And if they are not saved hereafter, then their sons will be saved, for your light will not die even when you are dead.", "그리고 만일 그들이 훗날에도 구원받지 못한다면 그들의 자손들이 구원받을 것이니, 그대의 빛은 그대가 죽은 뒤에도 사라지지 않을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["The righteous man departs, but his light remains.", "의로운 사람은 떠나가도 그의 빛은 남습니다."], ["Men are always saved after the death of the deliverer.", "사람들은 언제나 구원자가 죽은 뒤에 구원을 받습니다."], ["Men reject their prophets and slay them, but they love their martyrs and honor those whom they have slain.", "사람들은 자기 예언자들을 거부하고 그들을 죽이지만, 자기 순교자들은 사랑하며 자기가 죽인 이들을 떠받듭니다."], ["You are working for the whole, you are acting for the future.", "그대는 전체를 위하여 일하고 있으며, 미래를 위하여 행하고 있는 것입니다."], ["Seek no reward, for great is your reward on this earth: the spiritual joy which is only vouchsafed to the righteous man.", "보상을 구하지 마십시오. 이 땅에서 그대의 보상은 크기 때문이니, 곧 의로운 사람에게만 허락되는 영적 기쁨입니다."], ["Fear not the great nor the mighty, but be wise and ever serene.", "위대한 자도 권세 있는 자도 두려워하지 말고, 지혜로우며 늘 평온하십시오."], ["Know the measure, know the times, study that.", "분수를 알고, 때를 알며, 그것을 익히십시오."], ["When you are left alone, pray.", "홀로 남거든 기도하십시오."], ["Love to throw yourself on the earth and kiss it.", "땅에 몸을 던져 그것에 입 맞추기를 즐기십시오."], ["Kiss the earth and love it with an unceasing, consuming love.", "땅에 입 맞추고 쉼 없이 타오르는 사랑으로 그것을 사랑하십시오."], ["Love all men, love everything.", "모든 사람을 사랑하고, 모든 것을 사랑하십시오."], ["Seek that rapture and ecstasy.", "그 환희와 황홀경을 구하십시오."], ["Water the earth with the tears of your joy and love those tears.", "그대의 기쁨의 눈물로 땅을 적시고 그 눈물을 사랑하십시오."], ["Don’t be ashamed of that ecstasy, prize it, for it is a gift of God and a great one; it is not given to many but only to the elect.", "그 황홀경을 부끄러워하지 말고 소중히 여기십시오. 그것은 하느님의 선물이며 위대한 선물이기 때문이니, 그것은 많은 이에게가 아니라 오직 택함받은 자에게만 주어집니다."]], [["(i) Of Hell and Hell Fire, a Mystic Reflection\nFathers and teachers, I ponder, “What is hell?”", "신부님들과 스승님들이여, 나는 \"지옥이란 무엇인가?\"를 곰곰이 생각합니다."], ["I maintain that it is the suffering of being unable to love.", "나는 그것이 사랑할 수 없음의 고통이라고 단언합니다."], ["Once in infinite existence, immeasurable in time and space, a spiritual creature was given on his coming to earth, the power of saying, “I am and I love.”", "시간과 공간으로 헤아릴 수 없는 무한한 존재 가운데, 어느 영적 피조물이 이 땅에 나아올 때 \"나는 존재하며 나는 사랑한다\"고 말할 능력을 한 번 부여받았습니다."], ["Once, only once, there was given him a moment of active living love, and for that was earthly life given him, and with it times and seasons.", "한 번, 오직 한 번, 그에게는 능동적으로 살아 있는 사랑의 한순간이 주어졌고, 그것을 위하여 그에게 지상의 삶이 주어졌으며, 그와 더불어 때와 시절이 주어졌습니다."], ["And that happy creature rejected the priceless gift, prized it and loved it not, scorned it and remained callous.", "그런데 그 행복한 피조물은 그 값을 매길 수 없는 선물을 거부하고, 그것을 소중히 여기지도 사랑하지도 않으며, 그것을 멸시하고 냉담한 채로 남았습니다."], ["Such a one, having left the earth, sees Abraham’s bosom and talks with Abraham as we are told in the parable of the rich man and Lazarus, and beholds heaven and can go up to the Lord.", "그런 자는 이 땅을 떠난 뒤, 부자와 나사로의 비유에서 우리에게 일러 주는 대로, 아브라함의 품을 보고 아브라함과 이야기를 나누며, 천국을 바라보고 주님께로 올라갈 수도 있습니다."], ["But that is just his torment, to rise up to the Lord without ever having loved, to be brought close to those who have loved when he has despised their love.", "그러나 바로 그것이 그의 고통이니, 한 번도 사랑해 본 적 없이 주님께로 올라가는 것이요, 그들의 사랑을 멸시해 놓고서 사랑했던 이들 가까이로 이끌려 가는 것입니다. 그는 똑똑히 보면서 스스로에게 말하기 때문입니다."], ["For he sees clearly and says to himself, “Now I have understanding, and though I now thirst to love, there will be nothing great, no sacrifice in my love, for my earthly life is over, and Abraham will not come even with a drop of living water (that is the gift of earthly active life) to cool the fiery thirst of spiritual love which burns in me now, though I despised it on earth; there is no more life for me and will be no more time!", "\"이제 나는 깨달았으나, 비록 내가 지금 사랑하기를 갈망한들, 내 사랑에는 아무런 위대함도, 아무런 희생도 없을 것이니, 나의 지상의 삶이 끝났고, 아브라함은 지금 내 안에 타오르는 영적 사랑의 불 같은 갈증을 식혀 줄 살아 있는 물 한 방울조차(그것이 곧 지상의 능동적 삶이라는 선물이다) 들고 오지 않을 것이기 때문이다. 비록 내가 이 땅에서 그것을 멸시했을지라도 말이다."], ["Even though I would gladly give my life for others, it can never be, for that life is passed which can be sacrificed for love, and now there is a gulf fixed between that life and this existence.”", "내게는 더 이상 삶이 없으며 더 이상 때도 없으리라! 비록 내가 기꺼이 다른 이들을 위하여 내 생명을 바치고자 한들 그것은 결코 이루어질 수 없으니, 사랑을 위하여 희생될 수 있었던 그 삶은 이미 지나갔고, 이제 그 삶과 이 존재 사이에는 건널 수 없는 심연이 가로놓여 있기 때문이다.\""]], [["They talk of hell fire in the material sense.", "사람들은 물질적인 의미에서의 지옥불을 이야기합니다."], ["I don’t go into that mystery and I shun it.", "나는 그 신비 속으로 들어가지 않으며 그것을 피합니다."], ["But I think if there were fire in material sense, they would be glad of it, for I imagine that in material agony, their still greater spiritual agony would be forgotten for a moment.", "그러나 내 생각에, 만일 물질적인 의미의 불이 있다면 그들은 도리어 그것을 반길 것이니, 짐작건대 물질적 고통 속에서라면 그들의 더욱더 큰 영적 고통이 한순간이나마 잊힐 것이기 때문입니다."], ["Moreover, that spiritual agony cannot be taken from them, for that suffering is not external but within them.", "더욱이 그 영적 고통은 그들에게서 거두어 갈 수 없으니, 그 고통은 외부의 것이 아니라 그들 안에 있는 것이기 때문입니다."], ["And if it could be taken from them, I think it would be bitterer still for the unhappy creatures.", "그리고 설령 그것을 그들에게서 거두어 갈 수 있다 해도, 내 생각에 그것은 그 불행한 피조물들에게 더욱더 쓰라린 일이 될 것입니다."], ["For even if the righteous in Paradise forgave them, beholding their torments, and called them up to heaven in their infinite love, they would only multiply their torments, for they would arouse in them still more keenly a flaming thirst for responsive, active and grateful love which is now impossible.", "비록 낙원의 의로운 자들이 그들의 고통을 굽어보며 그들을 용서하고, 그 무한한 사랑으로 그들을 천국으로 불러올린다 해도, 그것은 그들의 고통을 곱절로 늘릴 따름이기 때문이니, 이제는 불가능해진, 응답하고 능동적이며 감사할 줄 아는 사랑을 향한 타오르는 갈증을 그들 안에 더욱 날카롭게 불러일으킬 것이기 때문입니다."], ["In the timidity of my heart I imagine, however, that the very recognition of this impossibility would serve at last to console them.", "그러나 마음의 소심함 속에서나마 나는, 바로 이 불가능함을 인정하는 것이 마침내 그들에게 위안이 되어 주리라고 상상합니다."], ["For accepting the love of the righteous together with the impossibility of repaying it, by this submissiveness and the effect of this humility, they will attain at last, as it were, to a certain semblance of that active love which they scorned in life, to something like its outward expression....", "의로운 자들의 사랑을 그것을 갚을 수 없다는 불가능함과 더불어 받아들임으로써, 이 순종과 이 겸손함의 작용으로, 그들은 마침내, 말하자면 그들이 생전에 멸시했던 그 능동적 사랑의 어떤 모상에, 그 사랑의 외적 표현 같은 무언가에 이르게 될 것이기 때문입니다……"], ["I am sorry, friends and brothers, that I cannot express this clearly.", "벗들이여, 형제들이여, 내가 이것을 명료하게 표현하지 못하는 것이 미안합니다."], ["But woe to those who have slain themselves on earth, woe to the suicides!", "그러나 이 땅에서 스스로 목숨을 끊은 자들에게 화가 있을진저, 자살한 자들에게 화가 있을진저!"], ["I believe that there can be none more miserable than they.", "나는 그들보다 더 비참한 이는 있을 수 없다고 믿습니다."], ["They tell us that it is a sin to pray for them and outwardly the Church, as it were, renounces them, but in my secret heart I believe that we may pray even for them.", "사람들은 우리에게 그들을 위하여 기도하는 것이 죄라고 말하며, 겉으로 교회는 말하자면 그들을 내치는 듯하지만, 나는 내 은밀한 마음속으로 우리가 그들을 위해서도 기도할 수 있다고 믿습니다."], ["Love can never be an offense to Christ.", "사랑이 그리스도께 거스르는 일이 될 수는 결코 없습니다."], ["For such as those I have prayed inwardly all my life, I confess it, fathers and teachers, and even now I pray for them every day.", "그런 이들을 위하여 나는 평생토록 마음속으로 기도해 왔음을, 신부님들과 스승님들이여, 나는 고백하며, 지금도 날마다 그들을 위하여 기도합니다."]], [["Oh, there are some who remain proud and fierce even in hell, in spite of their certain knowledge and contemplation of the absolute truth; there are some fearful ones who have given themselves over to Satan and his proud spirit entirely.", "오, 지옥에서조차, 절대적 진리를 분명히 알고 바라보면서도 여전히 교만하고 사나운 채로 남아 있는 자들이 있으며, 사탄과 그의 교만한 정신에 자기를 송두리째 내맡긴 무서운 자들이 있습니다."], ["For such, hell is voluntary and ever consuming; they are tortured by their own choice.", "그런 자들에게 지옥은 자발적이며 끝없이 타오르는 것이니, 그들은 자기 자신의 선택으로 고통받습니다."], ["For they have cursed themselves, cursing God and life.", "그들은 하느님과 삶을 저주하며 자기 자신을 저주했기 때문입니다."], ["They live upon their vindictive pride like a starving man in the desert sucking blood out of his own body.", "그들은 마치 사막에서 굶주린 자가 제 몸에서 피를 빨아 먹듯이 자기의 앙심에 찬 교만을 양식 삼아 살아갑니다."], ["But they are never satisfied, and they refuse forgiveness, they curse God Who calls them.", "그러나 그들은 결코 만족하지 못하며 용서를 거부하고, 자기를 부르시는 하느님을 저주합니다."], ["They cannot behold the living God without hatred, and they cry out that the God of life should be annihilated, that God should destroy Himself and His own creation.", "그들은 살아 계신 하느님을 증오 없이는 바라보지 못하며, 생명의 하느님이 소멸되어야 한다고, 하느님께서 당신 자신과 당신 자신의 피조물을 멸하셔야 한다고 부르짖습니다."], ["And they will burn in the fire of their own wrath for ever and yearn for death and annihilation.", "그리고 그들은 영원토록 자기 자신의 분노의 불 속에서 타오르며 죽음과 소멸을 갈망할 것입니다."], ["But they will not attain to death....", "그러나 그들은 죽음에 이르지 못할 것입니다……"]], [["Here Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov’s manuscript ends.", "여기에서 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프의 수기가 끝난다."], ["I repeat, it is incomplete and fragmentary.", "거듭 말하거니와, 그것은 미완이며 단편적이다."], ["Biographical details, for instance, cover only Father Zossima’s earliest youth.", "이를테면 전기적 세부 사항은 조시마 신부의 가장 이른 젊은 시절만을 다루고 있다."], ["Of his teaching and opinions we find brought together sayings evidently uttered on very different occasions.", "그의 가르침과 견해에 관해서는 분명 서로 매우 다른 자리에서 발설된 말들이 한데 묶여 있는 것을 보게 된다."], ["His utterances during the last few hours have not been kept separate from the rest, but their general character can be gathered from what we have in Alexey Fyodorovitch’s manuscript.", "마지막 몇 시간 동안의 그의 발언들은 나머지와 따로 구분되어 있지 않으나, 그 전반적 성격은 알렉세이 표도로비치의 수기에 담긴 내용에서 미루어 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["The elder’s death came in the end quite unexpectedly.", "장로의 죽음은 결국 전혀 예기치 못하게 찾아왔다."], ["For although those who were gathered about him that last evening realized that his death was approaching, yet it was difficult to imagine that it would come so suddenly.", "그 마지막 저녁 그의 곁에 모여 있던 이들이 그의 죽음이 다가오고 있음을 알아차리기는 했으나, 그래도 그것이 그토록 갑작스레 닥치리라고는 상상하기 어려웠던 것이다."], ["On the contrary, his friends, as I observed already, seeing him that night apparently so cheerful and talkative, were convinced that there was at least a temporary change for the better in his condition.", "도리어 그의 벗들은, 내가 앞서 말한 대로, 그날 밤 그가 보아하니 그토록 쾌활하고 말이 많은 것을 보고는, 적어도 그의 상태에 일시적인 호전이 있는 것이라고 확신했다."], ["Even five minutes before his death, they said afterwards wonderingly, it was impossible to foresee it.", "그가 죽기 오 분 전까지도 그것을 내다보기란 불가능했다고 그들은 나중에 의아해하며 말했다."], ["He seemed suddenly to feel an acute pain in his chest, he turned pale and pressed his hands to his heart.", "그는 갑자기 가슴에 날카로운 통증을 느끼는 듯하더니, 얼굴이 창백해지며 두 손을 심장에 갖다 댔다."], ["All rose from their seats and hastened to him.", "모두가 자리에서 일어나 그에게로 달려갔다."], ["But though suffering, he still looked at them with a smile, sank slowly from his chair on to his knees, then bowed his face to the ground, stretched out his arms and as though in joyful ecstasy, praying and kissing the ground, quietly and joyfully gave up his soul to God.", "그러나 고통스러워하면서도 그는 여전히 미소를 띤 채 그들을 바라보았고, 의자에서 천천히 미끄러져 무릎을 꿇더니, 얼굴을 땅에 숙이고 두 팔을 뻗어, 마치 기쁨에 찬 황홀경에 잠긴 듯 기도하며 땅에 입 맞추고는, 조용하고 기쁘게 자기 영혼을 하느님께 바쳤다."]], [["The news of his death spread at once through the hermitage and reached the monastery.", "그의 죽음에 관한 소식은 곧장 암자 전체로 퍼져 수도원에까지 이르렀다."], ["The nearest friends of the deceased and those whose duty it was from their position began to lay out the corpse according to the ancient ritual, and all the monks gathered together in the church.", "고인의 가장 가까운 벗들과 그 지위로 보아 그것이 임무인 이들이 옛 의식에 따라 시신을 안치하기 시작했고, 모든 수도승이 성당에 한데 모였다."], ["And before dawn the news of the death reached the town.", "그리고 동이 트기 전에 그 죽음의 소식이 읍내에 다다랐다."], ["By the morning all the town was talking of the event, and crowds were flocking from the town to the monastery.", "아침이 되자 온 읍내가 그 일을 이야기했고, 사람들이 읍내에서 수도원으로 떼 지어 몰려들었다."], ["But this subject will be treated in the next book; I will only add here that before a day had passed something happened so unexpected, so strange, upsetting, and bewildering in its effect on the monks and the townspeople, that after all these years, that day of general suspense is still vividly remembered in the town.", "그러나 이 화제는 다음 권에서 다루기로 하겠다. 여기서는 다만 하루가 채 지나기도 전에, 수도승들과 읍내 사람들에게 미친 영향에 있어 너무도 예기치 못하고, 너무도 기이하며, 동요를 일으키고 어리둥절하게 만드는 어떤 일이 벌어졌으며, 그래서 이 모든 세월이 지난 지금까지도 그 온통 긴장에 휩싸였던 날이 읍내에서 여전히 생생히 기억되고 있다는 것만을 덧붙여 두겠다."]], [["# PART III Book VII. Alyosha Chapter I. The Breath Of Corruption", "# PART III Book VII. Alyosha Chapter I. The Breath Of Corruption"]], [["The body of Father Zossima was prepared for burial according to the established ritual.", "조시마 장로의 유해는 정해진 의식에 따라 매장 준비가 이루어졌다."], ["As is well known, the bodies of dead monks and hermits are not washed.", "널리 알려진 대로 죽은 수도사와 은수자의 시신은 씻기지 않는다."], ["In the words of the Church Ritual: “If any one of the monks depart in the Lord, the monk designated (that is, whose office it is) shall wipe the body with warm water, making first the sign of the cross with a sponge on the forehead of the deceased, on the breast, on the hands and feet and on the knees, and that is enough.”", "교회 의식서의 말을 빌리면 이러하다. \"수도사들 가운데 누구든 주 안에서 잠들거든, 지명을 받은 수도사가(곧 그 일을 맡은 자가) 따뜻한 물로 시신을 닦되, 먼저 해면으로 고인의 이마와 가슴과 두 손과 두 발과 무릎에 십자 표시를 그으면 그것으로 족하니라.\""], ["All this was done by Father Païssy, who then clothed the deceased in his monastic garb and wrapped him in his cloak, which was, according to custom, somewhat slit to allow of its being folded about him in the form of a cross.", "이 모든 일을 파이시 신부가 손수 행했고, 그런 다음 고인에게 수도복을 입히고 망토로 감쌌는데, 그 망토는 관습에 따라 십자 모양으로 시신을 감싸 접을 수 있도록 일부가 트여 있었다."], ["On his head he put a hood with an eight-cornered cross.", "머리에는 여덟 모 십자가가 달린 두건을 씌웠다."], ["The hood was left open and the dead man’s face was covered with black gauze.", "두건은 열어 둔 채, 죽은 이의 얼굴은 검은 망사로 덮었다."], ["In his hands was put an ikon of the Saviour.", "두 손에는 구세주의 이콘을 쥐어 주었다."], ["Towards morning he was put in the coffin which had been made ready long before.", "새벽녘에 이르러 고인은 오래전에 이미 마련해 둔 관 속에 안치되었다."], ["It was decided to leave the coffin all day in the cell, in the larger room in which the elder used to receive his visitors and fellow monks.", "관은 하루 종일 암자에, 곧 장로가 평소 방문객들과 동료 수도사들을 맞이하던 더 큰 방에 그대로 두기로 결정되었다."], ["As the deceased was a priest and monk of the strictest rule, the Gospel, not the Psalter, had to be read over his body by monks in holy orders.", "고인이 가장 엄격한 계율을 지키는 사제이자 수도사였으므로, 그 시신 위에서는 시편이 아니라 복음서를 사제 서품을 받은 수도사들이 읽어야만 했다."], ["The reading was begun by Father Iosif immediately after the requiem service.", "낭독은 위령미사가 끝난 직후 이오시프 신부가 시작했다."], ["Father Païssy desired later on to read the Gospel all day and night over his dead friend, but for the present he, as well as the Father Superintendent of the Hermitage, was very busy and occupied, for something extraordinary, an unheard-of, even “unseemly”", "파이시 신부는 나중에 죽은 벗의 시신 위에서 밤낮으로 복음서를 읽고 싶어 했으나, 당장은 그도 은수처의 원장 신부와 마찬가지로 몹시 바쁘고 분주했으니, 무언가 심상치 않은, 전에 없던, 심지어 \"온당치 못한\""], ["excitement and impatient expectation began to be apparent in the monks, and the visitors from the monastery hostels, and the crowds of people flocking from the town.", "흥분과 안달 난 기대가 수도사들 사이에서, 또 수도원 숙소에서 온 방문객들과 읍내에서 떼 지어 몰려든 군중 사이에서 드러나기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["And as time went on, this grew more and more marked.", "그리고 시간이 흐를수록 이런 분위기는 점점 더 뚜렷해졌다."], ["Both the Superintendent and Father Païssy did their utmost to calm the general bustle and agitation.", "원장도 파이시 신부도 이 전반적인 소란과 동요를 가라앉히려고 온 힘을 다했다."]], [["When it was fully daylight, some people began bringing their sick, in most cases children, with them from the town—as though they had been waiting expressly for this moment to do so, evidently persuaded that the dead elder’s remains had a power of healing, which would be immediately made manifest in accordance with their faith.", "날이 완전히 밝자, 어떤 이들은 읍내에서 병자들을, 대개는 아이들을 데리고 오기 시작했으니—마치 일부러 이 순간을 기다리기라도 한 듯했고, 죽은 장로의 유해에 치유의 힘이 깃들어 있어 자기들의 믿음에 따라 즉시 그 힘이 나타나리라고 분명히 확신하는 모양이었다."], ["It was only then apparent how unquestionably every one in our town had accepted Father Zossima during his lifetime as a great saint.", "우리 고장 사람들 모두가 생전의 조시마 장로를 의심할 바 없이 위대한 성자로 받아들이고 있었다는 사실이 그제야 비로소 분명히 드러난 것이다."], ["And those who came were far from being all of the humbler classes.", "그리고 찾아온 이들이 결코 하나같이 미천한 계층만은 아니었다."]], [["This intense expectation on the part of believers displayed with such haste, such openness, even with impatience and almost insistence, impressed Father Païssy as unseemly.", "이토록 서둘러, 이토록 노골적으로, 심지어 안달과 거의 강요에 가까운 태도로 드러나는 신자들의 이 강렬한 기대가 파이시 신부에게는 온당치 못하게만 비쳤다."], ["Though he had long foreseen something of the sort, the actual manifestation of the feeling was beyond anything he had looked for.", "그는 이런 일이 있으리라고 진작부터 어느 정도 내다보고는 있었으나, 막상 그 감정이 실제로 표출되는 양상은 그가 예상했던 것을 훨씬 넘어서는 것이었다."], ["When he came across any of the monks who displayed this excitement, Father Païssy began to reprove them.", "이런 흥분을 드러내는 수도사를 마주칠 때마다 파이시 신부는 그들을 나무라기 시작했다."], ["“Such immediate expectation of something extraordinary,”", "\"무언가 비범한 일을 그렇게 곧장 기대하는 것은,\""], ["he said, “shows a levity, possible to worldly people but unseemly in us.”", "그가 말했다, \"세속 사람들에게나 있을 법한 경박함이지 우리에게는 온당치 못한 짓이오.\""]], [["But little attention was paid him and Father Païssy noticed it uneasily.", "그러나 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 이는 거의 없었고, 파이시 신부는 그것을 불안하게 알아차렸다."], ["Yet he himself (if the whole truth must be told), secretly at the bottom of his heart, cherished almost the same hopes and could not but be aware of it, though he was indignant at the too impatient expectation around him, and saw in it light-mindedness and vanity.", "그러면서도 정작 그 자신은(모든 진실을 털어놓아야 한다면) 남몰래 마음 깊은 곳에서 거의 같은 희망을 품고 있었고, 자기 주위의 너무도 안달 난 기대에 분개하며 그 안에서 경박함과 허영을 보면서도, 자기 안의 그 희망을 의식하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Nevertheless, it was particularly unpleasant to him to meet certain persons, whose presence aroused in him great misgivings.", "그럼에도 그에게 특히 불쾌했던 것은 어떤 사람들과 마주치는 일이었으니, 그들의 존재는 그의 마음속에 커다란 의구심을 불러일으켰다."], ["In the crowd in the dead man’s cell he noticed with inward aversion (for which he immediately reproached himself) the presence of Rakitin and of the monk from Obdorsk, who was still staying in the monastery.", "죽은 이의 암자에 모여든 군중 가운데서 그는 내심 거부감을 느끼며(그러고는 곧장 스스로를 책망하며) 라키틴과, 아직 수도원에 머물고 있는 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사가 와 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Of both of them Father Païssy felt for some reason suddenly suspicious—though, indeed, he might well have felt the same about others.", "어쩐 일인지 파이시 신부는 그 두 사람에 대해 갑자기 의심을 품게 되었는데—하기야 다른 이들에게도 똑같은 의심을 품을 만했을 것이다."]], [["The monk from Obdorsk was conspicuous as the most fussy in the excited crowd.", "오브도르스크에서 온 수도사는 흥분한 군중 가운데서도 가장 부산스러운 자로 눈에 띄었다."], ["He was to be seen everywhere; everywhere he was asking questions, everywhere he was listening, on all sides he was whispering with a peculiar, mysterious air.", "그는 어디서나 모습을 드러냈고, 어디서나 이것저것 캐물었으며, 어디서나 귀를 기울였고, 사방에서 묘하고 비밀스러운 기색으로 속닥거렸다."], ["His expression showed the greatest impatience and even a sort of irritation.", "그의 얼굴에는 더없는 안달과 심지어 일종의 짜증마저 떠올라 있었다."]], [["As for Rakitin, he, as appeared later, had come so early to the hermitage at the special request of Madame Hohlakov.", "라키틴으로 말하자면, 나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 그가 그렇게 일찍 은수처에 온 것은 호흘라코바 부인의 특별한 부탁 때문이었다."], ["As soon as that good-hearted but weak-minded woman, who could not herself have been admitted to the hermitage, waked and heard of the death of Father Zossima, she was overtaken with such intense curiosity that she promptly dispatched Rakitin to the hermitage, to keep a careful look out and report to her by letter every half-hour or so “everything that takes place.”", "마음씨는 착하나 머리가 모자란 이 부인은—자기 몸으로는 은수처에 들어올 수도 없었거니와—잠에서 깨어 조시마 장로의 죽음을 전해 듣자마자 어찌나 강렬한 호기심에 사로잡혔던지, 즉시 라키틴을 은수처로 보내어 주의 깊게 동정을 살피고 반 시간마다 한 번씩 \"일어나는 일을 모조리\" 편지로 보고하게 했다."], ["She regarded Rakitin as a most religious and devout young man.", "부인은 라키틴을 더없이 신앙심 깊고 독실한 청년으로 여기고 있었다."], ["He was particularly clever in getting round people and assuming whatever part he thought most to their taste, if he detected the slightest advantage to himself from doing so.", "그는 사람들의 비위를 맞추고, 거기서 조금이라도 자기에게 득 될 것을 알아채면 그들 입맛에 가장 맞으리라 여겨지는 배역이라면 무엇이든 능청스레 연기하는 데 유난히 능했다."]], [["It was a bright, clear day, and many of the visitors were thronging about the tombs, which were particularly numerous round the church and scattered here and there about the hermitage.", "화창하고 맑은 날이었고, 방문객들 가운데 많은 이들이 무덤들 주위로 몰려들었는데, 그 무덤들은 특히 교회 둘레에 빽빽이 모여 있었고 은수처 여기저기에도 흩어져 있었다."], ["As he walked round the hermitage, Father Païssy remembered Alyosha and that he had not seen him for some time, not since the night.", "파이시 신부는 은수처를 한 바퀴 돌다가 알료샤를 떠올렸고, 그를 못 본 지 한참 되었다는 것을, 그날 밤 이후로 보지 못했다는 것을 생각해 냈다."], ["And he had no sooner thought of him than he at once noticed him in the farthest corner of the hermitage garden, sitting on the tombstone of a monk who had been famous long ago for his saintliness.", "그런데 그를 떠올리기가 무섭게 곧 은수처 정원의 가장 외진 구석에서, 오래전 성덕으로 이름났던 어느 수도사의 묘비 위에 앉아 있는 그를 알아보았다."], ["He sat with his back to the hermitage and his face to the wall, and seemed to be hiding behind the tombstone.", "알료샤는 은수처를 등지고 담벼락을 향해 앉아 있어서, 묘비 뒤에 숨어 있는 듯이 보였다."], ["Going up to him, Father Païssy saw that he was weeping quietly but bitterly, with his face hidden in his hands, and that his whole frame was shaking with sobs.", "가까이 다가간 파이시 신부는 그가 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 없이, 그러나 비통하게 울고 있으며 온몸이 흐느낌으로 떨리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Father Païssy stood over him for a little.", "파이시 신부는 잠시 그를 굽어보며 서 있었다."]], [["“Enough, dear son, enough, dear,”", "\"됐다, 얘야, 됐어, 이 사람아.\""], ["he pronounced with feeling at last.", "마침내 그가 정에 겨운 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Why do you weep?", "\"왜 우느냐?"], ["Rejoice and weep not.", "기뻐할 일이지 울 일이 아니다."], ["Don’t you know that this is the greatest of his days?", "오늘이 그분에게는 가장 큰 날이라는 것을 모르느냐?"], ["Think only where he is now, at this moment!”", "지금 이 순간 그분이 어디에 계신지를 생각해 보아라!\""]], [["Alyosha glanced at him, uncovering his face, which was swollen with crying like a child’s, but turned away at once without uttering a word and hid his face in his hands again.", "알료샤는 그를 흘긋 쳐다보며 얼굴을 드러냈는데, 아이처럼 울어서 퉁퉁 부어 있는 그 얼굴을 보이더니, 곧 한마디도 하지 못한 채 다시 고개를 돌리고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["“Maybe it is well,”", "\"어쩌면 그것도 좋은 일이겠지.\""], ["said Father Païssy thoughtfully; “weep if you must, Christ has sent you those tears.”", "파이시 신부가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"울어야겠거든 울려무나, 그리스도께서 너에게 그 눈물을 보내 주신 것이니라.\""]], [["“Your touching tears are but a relief to your spirit and will serve to gladden your dear heart,”", "\"네 가슴 저린 눈물은 네 영혼을 달래 주고 네 갸륵한 마음을 기쁘게 해 줄 따름이다.\""], ["he added to himself, walking away from Alyosha, and thinking lovingly of him.", "그는 알료샤에게서 물러나며 속으로 이렇게 덧붙였고, 그를 애틋하게 생각했다."], ["He moved away quickly, however, for he felt that he too might weep looking at him.", "그러나 그는 빠르게 자리를 떴으니, 그를 바라보다가는 자기마저 울고 말 것 같았기 때문이다."]], [["Meanwhile the time was passing; the monastery services and the requiems for the dead followed in their due course.", "그러는 동안 시간은 흘러갔다. 수도원의 예배와 고인을 위한 위령미사가 순서에 따라 차례로 이어졌다."], ["Father Païssy again took Father Iosif’s place by the coffin and began reading the Gospel.", "파이시 신부는 다시 이오시프 신부를 대신해 관 곁에 자리를 잡고 복음서를 읽기 시작했다."], ["But before three o’clock in the afternoon that something took place to which I alluded at the end of the last book, something so unexpected by all of us and so contrary to the general hope, that, I repeat, this trivial incident has been minutely remembered to this day in our town and all the surrounding neighborhood.", "그러나 오후 세 시도 되기 전에, 내가 앞 권 말미에서 넌지시 비친 그 일이 벌어졌으니, 우리 모두에게는 너무도 뜻밖이었고 모두의 기대와는 너무도 어긋나는 것이어서, 거듭 말하거니와 이 사소한 사건은 오늘날까지도 우리 읍내와 인근 온 동네에서 시시콜콜하게 기억되고 있다."], ["I may add here, for myself personally, that I feel it almost repulsive to recall that event which caused such frivolous agitation and was such a stumbling-block to many, though in reality it was the most natural and trivial matter.", "여기서 나 개인으로서 덧붙여 두자면, 그토록 경박한 동요를 일으키고 많은 이들에게 그토록 큰 걸림돌이 된 그 사건을 떠올리는 것이 거의 역겹게까지 느껴지거니와, 실상 그것은 더없이 자연스럽고 사소한 일이었을 뿐이다."], ["I should, of course, have omitted all mention of it in my story, if it had not exerted a very strong influence on the heart and soul of the chief, though future, hero of my story, Alyosha, forming a crisis and turning-point in his spiritual development, giving a shock to his intellect, which finally strengthened it for the rest of his life and gave it a definite aim.", "물론 나는 이 이야기에서 그 일을 일절 언급하지 않고 넘어갔을 것이나, 그것이 내 이야기의 주인공이자—비록 장차의 일이지만—주인공인 알료샤의 가슴과 영혼에 매우 강한 영향을 끼쳐, 그의 정신적 성장에 한 위기이자 전환점을 이루었고 그의 지성에 충격을 가하여, 그 충격이 마침내 그의 남은 평생토록 지성을 단단하게 다지고 그것에 분명한 목표를 부여했기 때문이다."]], [["And so, to return to our story.", "자, 그러면 우리 이야기로 돌아가 보자."], ["When before dawn they laid Father Zossima’s body in the coffin and brought it into the front room, the question of opening the windows was raised among those who were around the coffin.", "동트기 전에 조시마 장로의 유해를 관에 누이고 그것을 앞방으로 옮겨 왔을 때, 관 주위에 있던 이들 사이에서 창문을 열어야 하지 않겠느냐는 문제가 제기되었다."], ["But this suggestion made casually by some one was unanswered and almost unnoticed.", "그러나 누군가가 무심코 던진 이 제안에는 아무도 대답하지 않았고 거의 주목하지도 않았다."], ["Some of those present may perhaps have inwardly noticed it, only to reflect that the anticipation of decay and corruption from the body of such a saint was an actual absurdity, calling for compassion (if not a smile) for the lack of faith and the frivolity it implied.", "그 자리에 있던 이들 가운데 몇몇은 어쩌면 속으로 그 말을 새겼을지도 모르나, 그저 이만한 성자의 시신에서 부패와 썩는 냄새를 예상한다는 것은 그야말로 터무니없는 일이며, 그것이 내비치는 믿음의 부족과 경박함에 대해 측은함을(웃음까지는 아니더라도) 자아낼 만하다고 마음속으로 생각했을 따름이다."], ["For they expected something quite different.", "사람들이 기대한 것은 전혀 다른 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["And, behold, soon after midday there were signs of something, at first only observed in silence by those who came in and out and were evidently each afraid to communicate the thought in his mind.", "그런데 보라, 정오가 조금 지나자 무언가의 징후가 나타났으니, 처음에는 드나들던 이들이 말없이 알아차리기만 할 뿐, 저마다 제 머릿속의 생각을 입 밖에 내기를 분명히 두려워하는 눈치였다."], ["But by three o’clock those signs had become so clear and unmistakable, that the news swiftly reached all the monks and visitors in the hermitage, promptly penetrated to the monastery, throwing all the monks into amazement, and finally, in the shortest possible time, spread to the town, exciting every one in it, believers and unbelievers alike.", "그러나 세 시에 이르자 그 징후들이 너무도 또렷하고 의심할 여지가 없게 되어, 그 소식은 삽시간에 은수처의 모든 수도사와 방문객에게 퍼졌고, 곧장 수도원까지 파고들어 모든 수도사를 경악에 빠뜨렸으며, 마침내 그야말로 눈 깜짝할 사이에 읍내로 번져, 신자건 불신자건 그곳의 모든 사람을 들끓게 했다."], ["The unbelievers rejoiced, and as for the believers some of them rejoiced even more than the unbelievers, for “men love the downfall and disgrace of the righteous,”", "불신자들은 기뻐했고, 신자들로 말하자면 그 가운데 어떤 이들은 불신자보다 더 기뻐하기까지 했으니, 죽은 장로가 어느 설교에서 말했듯이 \"사람들은 의인의 몰락과 치욕을 사랑하기\""], ["as the deceased elder had said in one of his exhortations.", "때문이다."]], [["The fact is that a smell of decomposition began to come from the coffin, growing gradually more marked, and by three o’clock it was quite unmistakable.", "사실인즉, 관에서 썩는 냄새가 풍기기 시작했고 그 냄새가 차츰 짙어지더니 세 시쯤에는 도무지 의심할 수 없을 지경이 되었던 것이다."], ["In all the past history of our monastery, no such scandal could be recalled, and in no other circumstances could such a scandal have been possible, as showed itself in unseemly disorder immediately after this discovery among the very monks themselves.", "우리 수도원의 지난 역사를 통틀어 이런 추문은 떠올릴 수조차 없었고, 다른 어떤 상황에서도 이런 추문은 일어날 수 없었을 터인데, 이 발견이 있은 직후 다름 아닌 수도사들 자신 사이에서 온당치 못한 무질서로 그것이 모습을 드러냈던 것이다."], ["Afterwards, even many years afterwards, some sensible monks were amazed and horrified, when they recalled that day, that the scandal could have reached such proportions.", "훗날, 여러 해가 지난 뒤에까지도, 분별 있는 몇몇 수도사들은 그날을 돌이켜 볼 때마다 추문이 어찌 그토록 큰 규모에 이를 수 있었는지 놀라고 또 몸서리쳤다."], ["For in the past, monks of very holy life had died, God-fearing old men, whose saintliness was acknowledged by all, yet from their humble coffins, too, the breath of corruption had come, naturally, as from all dead bodies, but that had caused no scandal nor even the slightest excitement.", "지난날에도 더없이 거룩한 삶을 산 수도사들이, 모두가 성덕을 인정하던 경건한 노인들이 죽어 갔고, 그들의 소박한 관에서도 여느 시신과 마찬가지로 자연스레 썩는 냄새가 풍겼지만, 그것이 추문은커녕 조금의 동요조차 일으킨 적이 없었다."], ["Of course there had been, in former times, saints in the monastery whose memory was carefully preserved and whose relics, according to tradition, showed no signs of corruption.", "물론 지난날에도 수도원에는, 그 기억이 정성껏 보존되고 전승에 따르면 그 유골에 부패의 흔적이 전혀 없었다는 성자들이 있었다."], ["This fact was regarded by the monks as touching and mysterious, and the tradition of it was cherished as something blessed and miraculous, and as a promise, by God’s grace, of still greater glory from their tombs in the future.", "수도사들은 이 사실을 가슴 뭉클하고 신비로운 일로 여겼고, 그 전승을 무언가 복되고 기적적인 것으로, 또 하느님의 은총으로 그 무덤들에서 장차 더욱 큰 영광이 비롯되리라는 약속으로 소중히 간직했다."]], [["One such, whose memory was particularly cherished, was an old monk, Job, who had died seventy years before at the age of a hundred and five.", "그런 이들 가운데 특히 기억이 소중히 간직된 한 사람은 욥이라는 노수도사로, 칠십 년 전에 백다섯 살의 나이로 세상을 떠난 인물이었다."], ["He had been a celebrated ascetic, rigid in fasting and silence, and his tomb was pointed out to all visitors on their arrival with peculiar respect and mysterious hints of great hopes connected with it. (That was the very tomb on which Father Païssy had found Alyosha sitting in the morning.) Another memory cherished in the monastery was that of the famous Father Varsonofy, who was only recently dead and had preceded Father Zossima in the eldership.", "그는 단식과 침묵에 엄격하기로 이름난 고행자였고, 그의 무덤은 새로 도착한 모든 방문객에게 각별한 경의와 함께, 그것과 결부된 큰 희망에 대한 신비로운 암시와 함께 가리켜 보여지곤 했다.(바로 그 무덤이, 아침에 파이시 신부가 알료샤가 앉아 있는 것을 발견한 그 무덤이었다.) 수도원에서 소중히 간직된 또 하나의 기억은 이름난 바르소노피 신부에 관한 것이었으니, 그는 비교적 최근에 세상을 떠나 조시마 장로보다 앞서 장로직을 맡았던 인물이었다."], ["He was reverenced during his lifetime as a crazy saint by all the pilgrims to the monastery.", "그는 생전에 수도원을 찾는 모든 순례자들로부터 광인 같은 성자로 추앙받았다."], ["There was a tradition that both of these had lain in their coffins as though alive, that they had shown no signs of decomposition when they were buried and that there had been a holy light in their faces.", "전승에 따르면 이 두 사람은 마치 살아 있는 듯이 관 속에 누워 있었고, 묻힐 때에도 부패의 흔적이 전혀 없었으며 그 얼굴에는 거룩한 빛이 어려 있었다고 했다."], ["And some people even insisted that a sweet fragrance came from their bodies.", "더러는 그 시신에서 향긋한 내음마저 풍겼다고 우기는 이들까지 있었다."]], [["Yet, in spite of these edifying memories, it would be difficult to explain the frivolity, absurdity and malice that were manifested beside the coffin of Father Zossima.", "그러나 이렇듯 교훈적인 기억들이 있었음에도, 조시마 장로의 관 곁에서 드러난 경박함과 부조리와 악의를 설명하기란 쉽지 않을 것이다."], ["It is my private opinion that several different causes were simultaneously at work, one of which was the deeply-rooted hostility to the institution of elders as a pernicious innovation, an antipathy hidden deep in the hearts of many of the monks.", "나 개인의 생각으로는 서로 다른 여러 원인이 동시에 작용하고 있었으니, 그 가운데 하나는 장로 제도를 해로운 폐단으로 여기는 뿌리 깊은 반감, 곧 수많은 수도사들의 가슴 깊숙이 숨어 있던 적의였다."], ["Even more powerful was jealousy of the dead man’s saintliness, so firmly established during his lifetime that it was almost a forbidden thing to question it.", "그보다 더 강력했던 것은 죽은 이의 성덕에 대한 시샘이었으니, 그 성덕이 생전에 너무도 확고히 자리 잡은 나머지 그것을 의심한다는 것이 거의 금기에 가까운 일이 되어 버렸기 때문이다."], ["For though the late elder had won over many hearts, more by love than by miracles, and had gathered round him a mass of loving adherents, none the less, in fact, rather the more on that account he had awakened jealousy and so had come to have bitter enemies, secret and open, not only in the monastery but in the world outside it.", "죽은 장로가 기적보다는 사랑으로 수많은 이들의 마음을 사로잡았고 자기 둘레에 그를 따르는 무리를 가득 모았다고는 하나, 그럼에도, 아니 오히려 바로 그 까닭에 그는 시샘을 불러일으켰고, 그리하여 수도원 안에서뿐 아니라 바깥세상에서도 은밀한, 또 드러난 모진 원수들을 두게 되었던 것이다."], ["He did no one any harm, but “Why do they think him so saintly?”", "그는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않았건만, \"어째서 저자를 그토록 거룩하다고 여기는 거지?\""], ["And that question alone, gradually repeated, gave rise at last to an intense, insatiable hatred of him.", "하는 것이었다. 그리고 차츰 되풀이된 그 물음 하나가 마침내 그에 대한 격렬하고 채울 길 없는 증오를 낳았다."], ["That, I believe, was why many people were extremely delighted at the smell of decomposition which came so quickly, for not a day had passed since his death.", "내가 믿기로는, 그토록 빠르게 풍긴 썩는 냄새에—그가 죽은 지 하루도 채 지나지 않았으니—수많은 사람들이 더없이 기뻐한 까닭도 바로 거기에 있었다."], ["At the same time there were some among those who had been hitherto reverently devoted to the elder, who were almost mortified and personally affronted by this incident.", "한편으로는 그때까지 장로를 경건히 따르던 이들 가운데서도, 이 사건에 거의 굴욕을 느끼고 자기가 직접 모욕이라도 당한 듯이 여기는 자들도 있었다."], ["This was how the thing happened.", "그 일은 이렇게 벌어졌다."]], [["As soon as signs of decomposition had begun to appear, the whole aspect of the monks betrayed their secret motives in entering the cell.", "부패의 징후가 나타나기 시작하자마자, 수도사들의 행색은 그들이 암자에 들어가는 은밀한 속셈을 죄다 드러내 보였다."], ["They went in, stayed a little while and hastened out to confirm the news to the crowd of other monks waiting outside.", "그들은 안으로 들어가 잠시 머물다가는, 밖에서 기다리고 있는 다른 수도사 무리에게 소식을 확인해 주려고 서둘러 빠져나왔다."], ["Some of the latter shook their heads mournfully, but others did not even care to conceal the delight which gleamed unmistakably in their malignant eyes.", "그 가운데 어떤 이들은 침울하게 고개를 내저었으나, 또 어떤 이들은 심술궂은 눈에 또렷이 번득이는 기쁨을 감추려 들지조차 않았다."], ["And now no one reproached them for it, no one raised his voice in protest, which was strange, for the majority of the monks had been devoted to the dead elder.", "그런데도 이제 아무도 그들을 나무라지 않았고, 아무도 항의의 목소리를 높이지 않았으니, 이는 기이한 일이었다. 대다수 수도사들은 죽은 장로를 따르던 이들이었기 때문이다."], ["But it seemed as though God had in this case let the minority get the upper hand for a time.", "그러나 이 경우에는 하느님께서 한동안 소수가 우위를 차지하도록 내버려 두신 듯했다."]], [["Visitors from outside, particularly of the educated class, soon went into the cell, too, with the same spying intent.", "바깥에서 온 방문객들, 특히 교양 있는 계층의 사람들도 곧 똑같이 염탐하려는 속셈으로 암자 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Of the peasantry few went into the cell, though there were crowds of them at the gates of the hermitage.", "농민들 가운데서는 암자 안으로 들어간 이가 거의 없었으나, 은수처 문간에는 그들이 떼 지어 몰려 있었다."], ["After three o’clock the rush of worldly visitors was greatly increased and this was no doubt owing to the shocking news.", "세 시가 지나자 세속 방문객들의 발길이 부쩍 늘었는데, 이는 두말할 것 없이 그 충격적인 소식 때문이었다."], ["People were attracted who would not otherwise have come on that day and had not intended to come, and among them were some personages of high standing.", "그렇지 않았다면 그날 오지 않았을, 올 생각조차 없었던 사람들이 이끌려 왔고, 그 가운데에는 지체 높은 인사들도 더러 있었다."], ["But external decorum was still preserved and Father Païssy, with a stern face, continued firmly and distinctly reading aloud the Gospel, apparently not noticing what was taking place around him, though he had, in fact, observed something unusual long before.", "그러나 겉으로의 예의범절은 그래도 지켜지고 있었고, 파이시 신부는 엄숙한 얼굴로 자기 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 알아차리지 못하는 양 굳건하고 또렷하게 복음서를 소리 내어 계속 읽어 나갔는데, 실은 진작부터 무언가 심상치 않은 낌새를 알아챈 터였다."], ["But at last the murmurs, first subdued but gradually louder and more confident, reached even him.", "그러나 마침내 처음에는 죽인 듯 낮았다가 차츰 커지고 대담해진 수군거림이 그의 귀에까지 닿았다."], ["“It shows God’s judgment is not as man’s,” Father Païssy heard suddenly.", "\"이것은 하느님의 심판이 사람의 심판과 같지 않음을 보여 주는 거요.\""], ["The first to give utterance to this sentiment was a layman, an elderly official from the town, known to be a man of great piety.", "파이시 신부는 문득 이런 말을 들었다. 이 말을 처음 입 밖에 낸 사람은 평신도, 곧 읍내에서 온 나이 지긋한 관리였는데 신앙심이 매우 깊기로 알려진 인물이었다."], ["But he only repeated aloud what the monks had long been whispering.", "그러나 그는 수도사들이 진작부터 수군거려 온 말을 소리 내어 되풀이했을 뿐이다."], ["They had long before formulated this damning conclusion, and the worst of it was that a sort of triumphant satisfaction at that conclusion became more and more apparent every moment.", "그들은 이미 오래전에 이 단죄의 결론을 정해 두었고, 무엇보다 고약한 것은 그 결론에 대한 일종의 의기양양한 만족감이 순간순간 점점 더 또렷이 드러나고 있었다는 점이다."], ["Soon they began to lay aside even external decorum and almost seemed to feel they had a sort of right to discard it.", "머지않아 그들은 겉으로의 예의범절마저 내팽개치기 시작했고, 거의 그것을 내던질 무슨 권리라도 있는 듯이 느끼는 것 같았다."]], [["“And for what reason can this have happened,”", "\"대체 무슨 까닭으로 이런 일이 생긴 걸까.\""], ["some of the monks said, at first with a show of regret; “he had a small frame and his flesh was dried up on his bones, what was there to decay?”", "몇몇 수도사들이 처음에는 안타까운 기색을 꾸미며 말했다. \"그분은 몸집도 자그마했고 살이라곤 뼈에 말라붙어 있었는데, 썩을 거리가 뭐가 있다고?\""]], [["“It must be a sign from heaven,”", "\"이건 하늘이 내린 징조임에 틀림없소.\""], ["others hastened to add, and their opinion was adopted at once without protest.", "다른 이들이 서둘러 덧붙였고, 그들의 의견은 아무런 반대 없이 곧장 받아들여졌다."], ["For it was pointed out, too, that if the decomposition had been natural, as in the case of every dead sinner, it would have been apparent later, after a lapse of at least twenty-four hours, but this premature corruption “was in excess of nature,”", "그도 그럴 것이, 만일 부패가 죄 지은 모든 죽은 자의 경우처럼 자연스러운 것이었다면 적어도 스물네 시간은 지난 뒤에야 나타났을 텐데, 이렇게 너무 이른 부패는 \"자연의 도를 넘어선\" 것이므로 거기에는 하느님의 손길이 분명하다는 지적까지 나왔던 것이다."], ["and so the finger of God was evident.", "그것은 하나의 징조로 내린 것이었다."], ["It was meant for a sign. This conclusion seemed irresistible.", "이 결론은 거역할 수 없는 것으로 보였다."]], [["Gentle Father Iosif, the librarian, a great favorite of the dead man’s, tried to reply to some of the evil speakers that “this is not held everywhere alike,” and that the incorruptibility of the bodies of the just was not a dogma of the Orthodox Church, but only an opinion, and that even in the most Orthodox regions, at Athos for instance, they were not greatly confounded by the smell of corruption, and there the chief sign of the glorification of the saved was not bodily incorruptibility, but the color of the bones when the bodies have lain many years in the earth and have decayed in it.", "죽은 이가 몹시 아끼던 온화한 사서 이오시프 신부가 험담을 일삼는 자들 가운데 몇몇에게 대꾸하려 애쓰며, \"그건 어디서나 한결같이 받아들여지는 것이 아니오\"라고, 또 의인의 시신이 썩지 않는다는 것은 정교회의 교리가 아니라 다만 하나의 견해일 뿐이며, 가장 정교다운 지방에서도, 이를테면 아토스에서도 부패의 냄새에 그리 당황하지 않으며, 그곳에서는 구원받은 자의 영광을 드러내는 으뜸가는 징표가 육신이 썩지 않는 데 있는 것이 아니라 시신이 여러 해 땅속에 누워 썩은 뒤 그 유골의 빛깔에 있다고 했다."], ["“And if the bones are yellow as wax, that is the great sign that the Lord has glorified the dead saint, if they are not yellow but black, it shows that God has not deemed him worthy of such glory—that is the belief in Athos, a great place, where the Orthodox doctrine has been preserved from of old, unbroken and in its greatest purity,”", "\"그래서 유골이 밀랍처럼 누렇다면, 그것은 주께서 죽은 성자에게 영광을 내리셨다는 위대한 징표이고, 누렇지 않고 검다면, 그것은 하느님께서 그를 그런 영광에 합당하다고 여기지 않으셨음을 보여 주는 것이오—이것이 아토스의 믿음으로, 그곳은 위대한 곳이며 정교의 교리가 예로부터 끊김 없이 가장 순수하게 보존되어 온 곳이오.\""], ["said Father Iosif in conclusion.", "이오시프 신부가 끝으로 말했다."]], [["But the meek Father’s words had little effect and even provoked a mocking retort.", "그러나 이 온순한 신부의 말은 별다른 효력이 없었고 도리어 비웃는 대꾸만 자아냈다."], ["“That’s all pedantry and innovation, no use listening to it,”", "\"그건 죄다 현학이고 새로 들여온 헛소리니 귀담아들을 게 못 되오.\""], ["the monks decided.", "수도사들은 이렇게 단정 지었다."], ["“We stick to the old doctrine, there are all sorts of innovations nowadays, are we to follow them all?”", "\"우리는 옛 교리를 지킬 뿐이오, 요즘에는 별의별 새로운 것들이 다 나도는데, 그걸 죄다 따라가야 한단 말이오?\""], ["added others.", "다른 이들이 덧붙였다."]], [["“We have had as many holy fathers as they had.", "\"우리에게도 그들 못지않게 거룩한 신부님들이 계셨소."], ["There they are among the Turks, they have forgotten everything.", "저들은 투르크인들 틈에 끼여 모든 걸 잊어버렸소."], ["Their doctrine has long been impure and they have no bells even,”", "저들의 교리는 오래전부터 불순해졌고 종조차 없지 않소.\""], ["the most sneering added.", "가장 비아냥대는 자들이 덧붙였다."]], [["Father Iosif walked away, grieving the more since he had put forward his own opinion with little confidence as though scarcely believing in it himself.", "이오시프 신부는 자리를 떴는데, 자기 의견을 그토록 자신 없이, 자기 스스로조차 거의 믿지 않는 듯이 내세웠던 만큼 더욱 마음이 아팠다."], ["He foresaw with distress that something very unseemly was beginning and that there were positive signs of disobedience.", "그는 무언가 매우 온당치 못한 일이 시작되고 있으며 분명한 불복종의 징후들이 보인다는 것을 괴롭게 내다보았다."], ["Little by little, all the sensible monks were reduced to silence like Father Iosif.", "차츰차츰 분별 있는 수도사들은 모두 이오시프 신부처럼 입을 다물게 되었다."], ["And so it came to pass that all who loved the elder and had accepted with devout obedience the institution of the eldership were all at once terribly cast down and glanced timidly in one another’s faces, when they met.", "그리하여 결국, 장로를 사랑하고 경건한 순종으로 장로 제도를 받아들였던 이들은 모두 한순간에 무섭게 풀이 죽어, 서로 마주칠 때면 겁먹은 눈으로 슬며시 상대의 얼굴을 살피게 되었다."], ["Those who were hostile to the institution of elders, as a novelty, held up their heads proudly.", "장로 제도를 새것이라 하여 적대하던 자들은 거만하게 고개를 쳐들었다."], ["“There was no smell of corruption from the late elder Varsonofy, but a sweet fragrance,”", "\"돌아가신 바르소노피 장로한테서는 썩는 냄새가 아니라 향긋한 내음이 풍겼지.\""], ["they recalled malignantly.", "그들은 심술궂게 떠올렸다."], ["“But he gained that glory not because he was an elder, but because he was a holy man.”", "\"하지만 그분이 그 영광을 얻은 건 장로여서가 아니라 거룩한 분이어서였어.\""]], [["And this was followed by a shower of criticism and even blame of Father Zossima.", "그리고 뒤이어 조시마 장로에 대한 비난, 심지어 책망이 쏟아졌다."], ["“His teaching was false; he taught that life is a great joy and not a vale of tears,”", "\"그분의 가르침은 그릇된 것이었어. 삶이란 눈물의 골짜기가 아니라 큰 기쁨이라고 가르치셨거든.\""], ["said some of the more unreasonable.", "좀 더 사리에 어두운 자들이 이렇게 말했다."], ["“He followed the fashionable belief, he did not recognize material fire in hell,”", "\"유행하는 믿음을 따르며 지옥에 실제로 불이 있다는 걸 인정하지 않으셨지.\""], ["others, still more unreasonable, added.", "한층 더 사리에 어두운 다른 이들이 덧붙였다."], ["“He was not strict in fasting, allowed himself sweet things, ate cherry jam with his tea, ladies used to send it to him.", "\"단식에도 엄격하지 못해서 단것을 입에 대셨고, 차에 버찌잼을 타 드셨지. 부인네들이 그걸 보내 드리곤 했거든."], ["Is it for a monk of strict rule to drink tea?”", "엄격한 계율을 지키는 수도사가 차를 마실 일이오?\""], ["could be heard among some of the envious.", "시샘하는 자들 사이에서 이런 말이 들렸다."], ["“He sat in pride,”", "\"그분은 거만하게 앉아 계셨어.\""], ["the most malignant declared vindictively; “he considered himself a saint and he took it as his due when people knelt before him.”", "가장 심술궂은 자들이 앙심을 품고 단언했다. \"스스로를 성자로 여기고, 사람들이 무릎 꿇는 걸 당연한 일로 받아들이셨지.\""], ["“He abused the sacrament of confession,”", "\"그분은 고해성사를 함부로 다루셨어.\""], ["the fiercest opponents of the institution of elders added in a malicious whisper.", "장로 제도를 가장 격렬히 반대하는 자들이 심술궂은 속삭임으로 덧붙였다."], ["And among these were some of the oldest monks, strictest in their devotion, genuine ascetics, who had kept silent during the life of the deceased elder, but now suddenly unsealed their lips.", "그리고 이들 가운데에는 신앙에 가장 엄격한 가장 늙은 수도사들, 진짜 고행자들도 끼여 있었으니, 그들은 죽은 장로가 살아 있는 동안에는 입을 다물고 있다가 이제 와서 느닷없이 입을 연 것이었다."], ["And this was terrible, for their words had great influence on young monks who were not yet firm in their convictions.", "그리고 이것은 무서운 일이었으니, 그들의 말은 아직 신념이 굳지 못한 젊은 수도사들에게 큰 영향을 끼쳤기 때문이다."], ["The monk from Obdorsk heard all this attentively, heaving deep sighs and nodding his head.", "오브도르스크에서 온 수도사는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고 고개를 끄덕이며 이 모든 이야기를 주의 깊게 들었다."], ["“Yes, clearly Father Ferapont was right in his judgment yesterday,”", "\"그래, 어제 페라폰트 신부님의 판단이 옳았던 게 분명해.\""], ["and at that moment Father Ferapont himself made his appearance, as though on purpose to increase the confusion.", "바로 그때 페라폰트 신부 자신이, 마치 일부러 혼란을 키우려는 듯이 모습을 나타냈다."]], [["I have mentioned already that he rarely left his wooden cell by the apiary.", "앞서 말했듯이 그는 양봉장 옆 통나무 암자를 좀처럼 떠나지 않았다."], ["He was seldom even seen at church and they overlooked this neglect on the ground of his craziness, and did not keep him to the rules binding on all the rest.", "그는 교회에서조차 좀처럼 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 사람들은 그의 광기를 핑계로 이 태만을 눈감아 주었고, 나머지 모두에게 매인 규칙을 그에게는 적용하지 않았다."], ["But if the whole truth is to be told, they hardly had a choice about it.", "그러나 모든 진실을 다 말하자면, 그들에게는 달리 선택의 여지가 거의 없었다."], ["For it would have been discreditable to insist on burdening with the common regulations so great an ascetic, who prayed day and night (he even dropped asleep on his knees).", "밤낮으로 기도하는(그는 무릎 꿇은 채로 잠이 들기까지 했다) 이만한 고행자에게 공동의 규율을 굳이 짊어지라고 우긴다는 것은 체면 깎이는 일이었을 터이기 때문이다."], ["If they had insisted, the monks would have said, “He is holier than all of us and he follows a rule harder than ours.", "만일 우겼다면 수도사들은 이렇게 말했을 것이다. \"저분은 우리 모두보다 거룩하시고, 우리보다 더 혹독한 계율을 따르신다."], ["And if he does not go to church, it’s because he knows when he ought to; he has his own rule.”", "교회에 안 가시는 것도 언제 가야 할지를 스스로 아시기 때문이지. 저분께는 저분만의 계율이 있는 게야.\""], ["It was to avoid the chance of these sinful murmurs that Father Ferapont was left in peace.", "바로 이런 죄스러운 수군거림이 일어날 빌미를 피하려고 사람들은 페라폰트 신부를 가만히 내버려 두었던 것이다."]], [["As every one was aware, Father Ferapont particularly disliked Father Zossima.", "누구나 알다시피 페라폰트 신부는 조시마 장로를 유난히 싫어했다."], ["And now the news had reached him in his hut that “God’s judgment is not the same as man’s,”", "그런데 이제 그의 오두막에까지 \"하느님의 심판은 사람의 심판과 같지 않다\"는, 그리고 \"자연의 도를 넘어선\""], ["and that something had happened which was “in excess of nature.”", "무슨 일이 벌어졌다는 소식이 닿았던 것이다."], ["It may well be supposed that among the first to run to him with the news was the monk from Obdorsk, who had visited him the evening before and left his cell terror-stricken.", "그 소식을 들고 가장 먼저 그에게 달려간 이들 가운데 하나가 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사였으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하니, 그는 전날 저녁 그를 찾아갔다가 겁에 질려 그 암자를 떠났던 인물이다."]], [["I have mentioned above, that though Father Païssy, standing firm and immovable reading the Gospel over the coffin, could not hear nor see what was passing outside the cell, he gauged most of it correctly in his heart, for he knew the men surrounding him, well.", "앞서 말했듯이, 파이시 신부는 관 위에서 흔들림 없이 굳건하게 복음서를 읽고 있어 암자 밖에서 벌어지는 일을 듣지도 보지도 못했으나, 그 대부분을 마음속으로 정확히 헤아리고 있었으니, 자기를 둘러싼 사람들을 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He was not shaken by it, but awaited what would come next without fear, watching with penetration and insight for the outcome of the general excitement.", "그는 그 일에 흔들리지 않았고, 두려움 없이 다음에 무슨 일이 닥칠지를 기다리며 이 전반적인 흥분의 결말을 꿰뚫는 통찰로 지켜보았다."]], [["Suddenly an extraordinary uproar in the passage in open defiance of decorum burst on his ears.", "그런데 별안간 예법을 노골적으로 무시하는 어마어마한 소란이 복도에서 터져 나와 그의 귀를 때렸다."], ["The door was flung open and Father Ferapont appeared in the doorway.", "문이 벌컥 열리더니 문간에 페라폰트 신부가 나타났다."], ["Behind him there could be seen accompanying him a crowd of monks, together with many people from the town.", "그의 뒤로는 그를 따르는 수도사 무리가, 거기에 읍내에서 온 많은 사람들과 함께 보였다."], ["They did not, however, enter the cell, but stood at the bottom of the steps, waiting to see what Father Ferapont would say or do.", "그러나 그들은 암자 안으로 들어오지는 않고 계단 아래에 서서 페라폰트 신부가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 할지 지켜보았다."], ["For they felt with a certain awe, in spite of their audacity, that he had not come for nothing.", "그도 그럴 것이 그들은 그 대담함에도 불구하고, 그가 까닭 없이 온 것이 아님을 어떤 외경심과 함께 느끼고 있었던 것이다."], ["Standing in the doorway, Father Ferapont raised his arms, and under his right arm the keen inquisitive little eyes of the monk from Obdorsk peeped in.", "문간에 선 페라폰트 신부가 두 팔을 치켜들자, 그의 오른팔 아래로 오브도르스크에서 온 수도사의 날카롭고 캐기 좋아하는 작은 눈이 안을 들여다보았다."], ["He alone, in his intense curiosity, could not resist running up the steps after Father Ferapont.", "그 강렬한 호기심에 그 혼자만은 페라폰트 신부를 뒤따라 계단을 뛰어 올라가지 않고는 배길 수 없었던 것이다."], ["The others, on the contrary, pressed farther back in sudden alarm when the door was noisily flung open.", "반대로 다른 이들은 문이 요란하게 벌컥 열리자 화들짝 놀라 더 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["Holding his hands aloft, Father Ferapont suddenly roared:", "페라폰트 신부는 두 손을 높이 쳐든 채 별안간 우렁차게 외쳤다."]], [["“Casting out I cast out!”", "\"쫓아내고 또 쫓아내노라!\""], ["and, turning in all directions, he began at once making the sign of the cross at each of the four walls and four corners of the cell in succession.", "그러고는 사방으로 몸을 돌려, 암자의 네 벽과 네 귀퉁이에 차례차례 곧장 십자 표시를 긋기 시작했다."], ["All who accompanied Father Ferapont immediately understood his action.", "페라폰트 신부를 따라온 이들은 모두 그 행동을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["For they knew he always did this wherever he went, and that he would not sit down or say a word, till he had driven out the evil spirits.", "그가 어디를 가든 늘 이렇게 했고, 악령을 쫓아내기 전에는 자리에 앉지도, 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Satan, go hence!", "\"사탄아, 물러가라!"], ["Satan, go hence!”", "사탄아, 물러가라!\""], ["he repeated at each sign of the cross.", "그는 십자 표시를 그을 때마다 이렇게 되뇌었다."], ["“Casting out I cast out,”", "\"쫓아내고 또 쫓아내노라.\""], ["he roared again.", "그가 다시 우렁차게 외쳤다."]], [["He was wearing his coarse gown girt with a rope.", "그는 밧줄로 동여맨 거친 수도복을 걸치고 있었다."], ["His bare chest, covered with gray hair, could be seen under his hempen shirt.", "삼베 셔츠 아래로 잿빛 털이 덮인 맨가슴이 드러나 보였다."], ["His feet were bare.", "두 발은 맨발이었다."], ["As soon as he began waving his arms, the cruel irons he wore under his gown could be heard clanking.", "그가 팔을 휘젓기 시작하자마자, 수도복 아래에 차고 있는 잔혹한 쇠사슬이 절그렁거리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["Father Païssy paused in his reading, stepped forward and stood before him waiting.", "파이시 신부는 낭독을 멈추고 앞으로 나서서 그 앞에 서서 기다렸다."]], [["“What have you come for, worthy Father?", "\"무슨 일로 오셨소, 존경하는 신부님?"], ["Why do you offend against good order?", "어찌하여 좋은 질서를 어지럽히시오?"], ["Why do you disturb the peace of the flock?”", "어찌하여 양 떼의 평화를 어지럽히시오?\""], ["he said at last, looking sternly at him.", "마침내 그가 엄하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했다."]], [["“What have I come for?", "\"내가 무슨 일로 왔느냐?"], ["You ask why? What is your faith?”", "어찌하여 왔느냐고 묻는 게냐?"], ["shouted Father Ferapont crazily.", "네 믿음이 어떤 것이냐?\""], ["“I’ve come here to drive out your visitors, the unclean devils.", "페라폰트 신부가 실성한 듯 소리쳤다. \"나는 네 손님들, 그 부정한 마귀들을 쫓아내러 여기 왔다."], ["I’ve come to see how many have gathered here while I have been away.", "내가 없는 동안 여기 얼마나 많이 모여들었는지 보러 왔지."], ["I want to sweep them out with a birch broom.”", "자작나무 빗자루로 싹 쓸어내려고 말이다.\""]], [["“You cast out the evil spirit, but perhaps you are serving him yourself,”", "\"신부님은 악령을 쫓아낸다고 하시나, 어쩌면 신부님 자신이 그를 섬기고 계신지도 모르오.\""], ["Father Païssy went on fearlessly.", "파이시 신부가 두려움 없이 말을 이었다."], ["“And who can say of himself ‘I am holy’?", "\"그리고 누가 제 입으로 '나는 거룩하다'고 말할 수 있겠소?"], ["Can you, Father?”", "신부님은 그러실 수 있소?\""]], [["“I am unclean, not holy.", "\"나는 부정한 자이지 거룩한 자가 아니다."], ["I would not sit in an arm-chair and would not have them bow down to me as an idol,”", "나라면 안락의자에 앉지도 않을 것이며, 사람들이 나를 우상처럼 떠받들게 하지도 않을 것이다.\""], ["thundered Father Ferapont.", "페라폰트 신부가 천둥같이 호통쳤다."], ["“Nowadays folk destroy the true faith.", "\"요즘 사람들은 참된 믿음을 망가뜨리고 있어."], ["The dead man, your saint,”", "저 죽은 자, 너희의 성자는,\""], ["he turned to the crowd, pointing with his finger to the coffin, “did not believe in devils.", "그가 군중을 향해 손가락으로 관을 가리키며 말했다, \"마귀를 믿지 않았어. 마귀를 막는답시고 약을 줬지."], ["He gave medicine to keep off the devils.", "그러니 마귀들이 구석의 거미들만큼이나 흔해진 게야."], ["And so they have become as common as spiders in the corners.", "그리고 이제는 제놈이 직접 썩는 냄새를 풍기기 시작했어."], ["And now he has begun to stink himself. In that we see a great sign from God.”", "거기서 우리는 하느님께서 내리신 위대한 징조를 보는 게야.\""]], [["The incident he referred to was this.", "그가 가리킨 사건은 이러했다."], ["One of the monks was haunted in his dreams and, later on, in waking moments, by visions of evil spirits.", "어느 수도사가 꿈에서, 또 나중에는 깨어 있는 순간에까지 악령의 환영에 시달렸다."], ["When in the utmost terror he confided this to Father Zossima, the elder had advised continual prayer and rigid fasting.", "더없는 공포에 사로잡혀 그가 이를 조시마 장로에게 털어놓자, 장로는 끊임없는 기도와 엄격한 단식을 권했다."], ["But when that was of no use, he advised him, while persisting in prayer and fasting, to take a special medicine.", "그러나 그것이 아무 소용이 없자, 장로는 기도와 단식을 계속하되 특별한 약을 한 가지 쓰라고 일렀다."], ["Many persons were shocked at the time and wagged their heads as they talked over it—and most of all Father Ferapont, to whom some of the censorious had hastened to report this “extraordinary”", "그 무렵 많은 사람들이 충격을 받고 이 일을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵 떠들며 고개를 내저었거니와—그 가운데서도 페라폰트 신부가 가장 심했으니, 험담 좋아하는 자들 몇몇이 장로의 이 \"기상천외한\""], ["counsel on the part of the elder.", "충고를 부랴부랴 그에게 일러바쳤던 것이다."]], [["“Go away, Father!”", "\"가시오, 신부님!\""], ["said Father Païssy, in a commanding voice, “it’s not for man to judge but for God.", "파이시 신부가 명령조로 말했다. \"심판하는 것은 사람의 몫이 아니라 하느님의 몫이오."], ["Perhaps we see here a ‘sign’ which neither you, nor I, nor any one of us is able to comprehend.", "어쩌면 우리는 여기서 신부님도, 나도, 우리 가운데 그 누구도 헤아릴 수 없는 '징조'를 보고 있는 것인지도 모르오."], ["Go, Father, and do not trouble the flock!”", "가시오, 신부님, 양 떼를 어지럽히지 마시오!\""], ["he repeated impressively.", "그가 힘주어 거듭 말했다."]], [["“He did not keep the fasts according to the rule and therefore the sign has come.", "\"그자는 계율대로 단식을 지키지 않았고, 그래서 징조가 내린 게야."], ["That is clear and it’s a sin to hide it,”", "그건 분명한 일이고, 그걸 숨기는 건 죄야.\""], ["the fanatic, carried away by a zeal that outstripped his reason, would not be quieted.", "이성을 앞지른 열광에 휩쓸린 광신자는 좀처럼 가라앉으려 하지 않았다."], ["“He was seduced by sweetmeats, ladies brought them to him in their pockets, he sipped tea, he worshiped his belly, filling it with sweet things and his mind with haughty thoughts....", "\"그자는 단것에 빠져 부인네들이 그걸 주머니에 넣어 갖다 바쳤고, 차를 홀짝거렸으며, 제 배때기를 단것으로 채우고 제 머릿속은 오만한 생각으로 채우며 그 배때기를 떠받들었어……."], ["And for this he is put to shame....”", "그래서 이렇게 치욕을 당하는 게야…….\""]], [["“You speak lightly, Father.”", "\"신부님은 함부로 말씀하시오.\""], ["Father Païssy, too, raised his voice.", "파이시 신부도 목소리를 높였다."], ["“I admire your fasting and severities, but you speak lightly like some frivolous youth, fickle and childish.", "\"나는 신부님의 단식과 고행은 우러러보지만, 신부님은 변덕스럽고 철없는 경박한 젊은이처럼 함부로 말씀하시오."], ["Go away, Father, I command you!”", "가시오, 신부님, 내가 명하오!\""], ["Father Païssy thundered in conclusion.", "파이시 신부가 끝으로 천둥같이 호통쳤다."]], [["“I will go,”", "\"가지.\""], ["said Ferapont, seeming somewhat taken aback, but still as bitter.", "페라폰트가 다소 주춤하는 기색이었으나 여전히 모질게 말했다."], ["“You learned men!", "\"너희 배운 자들!"], ["You are so clever you look down upon my humbleness.", "너희는 어찌나 영리한지 내 비천함을 깔보는구나."], ["I came hither with little learning and here I have forgotten what I did know, God Himself has preserved me in my weakness from your subtlety.”", "나는 가진 배움이 변변찮은 채로 여기 왔다가, 알던 것마저 잊어버렸지. 하느님 친히 나를 내 약함 가운데 두시어 너희의 잔꾀로부터 지켜 주셨다.\""]], [["Father Païssy stood over him, waiting resolutely.", "파이시 신부는 단호히 기다리며 그를 굽어보고 서 있었다."], ["Father Ferapont paused and, suddenly leaning his cheek on his hand despondently, pronounced in a sing-song voice, looking at the coffin of the dead elder:", "페라폰트 신부는 잠시 입을 다물더니, 별안간 풀 죽은 듯 한 손에 뺨을 괴고는 죽은 장로의 관을 바라보며 가락을 붙인 목소리로 읊조렸다."]], [["“To-morrow they will sing over him ‘Our Helper and Defender’—a splendid anthem—and over me when I die all they’ll sing will be ‘What earthly joy’—a little canticle,”[6] he added with tearful regret.", "\"내일이면 저자를 두고는 '우리의 도움이요 방패시여'를—그 거룩한 성가를 부르겠지—그런데 내가 죽으면 사람들이 부를 거라곤 고작 '무슨 세상의 기쁨이'라는 짤막한 송가뿐일 테지.\" 그가 눈물 어린 회한과 함께 덧붙였다."], ["“You are proud and puffed up, this is a vain place!”", "\"너희는 거만하고 우쭐대는구나, 여기는 헛된 곳이야!\""], ["he shouted suddenly like a madman, and with a wave of his hand he turned quickly and quickly descended the steps.", "그가 별안간 미치광이처럼 소리치더니, 손을 한 번 내젓고는 휙 돌아서서 빠르게 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The crowd awaiting him below wavered; some followed him at once and some lingered, for the cell was still open, and Father Païssy, following Father Ferapont on to the steps, stood watching him.", "아래에서 그를 기다리던 군중이 술렁였다. 더러는 곧장 그를 뒤따랐고 더러는 머뭇거렸으니, 암자가 아직 열려 있었고 파이시 신부가 페라폰트 신부를 따라 계단까지 나와 그를 지켜보고 서 있었기 때문이다."], ["But the excited old fanatic was not completely silenced.", "그러나 흥분한 늙은 광신자는 아직 완전히 잠잠해진 것이 아니었다."], ["Walking twenty steps away, he suddenly turned towards the setting sun, raised both his arms and, as though some one had cut him down, fell to the ground with a loud scream.", "스무 걸음쯤 걸어가더니, 그는 별안간 지는 해를 향해 돌아서서 두 팔을 치켜들고는, 마치 누군가가 그를 베어 쓰러뜨리기라도 한 듯이 큰 비명을 지르며 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["“My God has conquered!", "\"내 하느님께서 이기셨다!"], ["Christ has conquered the setting sun!”", "그리스도께서 지는 해를 이기셨다!\""], ["he shouted frantically, stretching up his hands to the sun, and falling face downwards on the ground, he sobbed like a little child, shaken by his tears and spreading out his arms on the ground.", "그가 미친 듯이 소리치며 해를 향해 두 손을 뻗었고, 땅바닥에 엎어져 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈으니, 눈물에 온몸을 떨며 두 팔을 땅 위에 활짝 벌렸다. 그러자 모두가 그에게 달려갔다."], ["Then all rushed up to him; there were exclamations and sympathetic sobs ...", "여기저기서 탄성이 터지고 동정 어린 흐느낌이 일었으니……"], ["a kind of frenzy seemed to take possession of them all.", "일종의 광란이 그들 모두를 사로잡은 듯했다."]], [["“This is the one who is a saint!", "\"이분이야말로 성자다!"], ["This is the one who is a holy man!”", "이분이야말로 거룩한 분이다!\""], ["some cried aloud, losing their fear.", "두려움을 잊은 몇몇이 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“This is he who should be an elder,”", "\"이분이야말로 장로가 되셔야 할 분이다.\""], ["others added malignantly.", "다른 이들이 심술궂게 덧붙였다."]], [["“He wouldn’t be an elder ...", "\"저분은 장로가 안 되실 거야……."], ["he would refuse ...", "마다하실 테지……."], ["he wouldn’t serve a cursed innovation ...", "저주받은 새 제도를 받들지 않으실 거야……."], ["he wouldn’t imitate their foolery,”", "저들의 어릿광대짓을 흉내 내지 않으실 거야.\""], ["other voices chimed in at once.", "또 다른 목소리들이 곧장 맞장구쳤다."], ["And it is hard to say how far they might have gone, but at that moment the bell rang summoning them to service.", "그들이 어디까지 갔을지는 말하기 어려우나, 바로 그때 예배를 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["All began crossing themselves at once.", "모두가 일제히 성호를 긋기 시작했다."], ["Father Ferapont, too, got up and crossing himself went back to his cell without looking round, still uttering exclamations which were utterly incoherent.", "페라폰트 신부도 일어나 성호를 긋고는 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 자기 암자로 돌아갔는데, 여전히 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 탄성을 내뱉고 있었다."], ["A few followed him, but the greater number dispersed, hastening to service.", "몇몇이 그를 따랐으나, 대다수는 흩어져 예배를 향해 서둘러 갔다."], ["Father Païssy let Father Iosif read in his place and went down.", "파이시 신부는 이오시프 신부에게 자기 대신 낭독을 맡기고 아래로 내려갔다."], ["The frantic outcries of bigots could not shake him, but his heart was suddenly filled with melancholy for some special reason and he felt that.", "광신자들의 광란에 찬 외침도 그를 흔들지 못했으나, 그의 마음은 어떤 특별한 까닭으로 별안간 우수에 잠겼고 그는 그것을 느꼈다."], ["He stood still and suddenly wondered, “Why am I sad even to dejection?”", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 문득 의아해했다. \"어찌하여 나는 의기소침할 만큼 슬픈가?\""], ["and immediately grasped with surprise that his sudden sadness was due to a very small and special cause.", "그러고는 그 갑작스러운 슬픔이 매우 사소하고 특별한 까닭에서 비롯되었음을 놀라움과 함께 곧 깨달았다."], ["In the crowd thronging at the entrance to the cell, he had noticed Alyosha and he remembered that he had felt at once a pang at heart on seeing him.", "암자 입구에 몰려든 군중 가운데서 그는 알료샤를 보았고, 그를 본 순간 가슴에 통증을 느꼈음을 떠올렸던 것이다."], ["“Can that boy mean so much to my heart now?”", "\"저 아이가 지금 내 마음에 그토록 큰 자리를 차지하고 있단 말인가?\""], ["he asked himself, wondering.", "그는 의아해하며 자문했다."]], [["At that moment Alyosha passed him, hurrying away, but not in the direction of the church. Their eyes met.", "바로 그 순간 알료샤가 그의 곁을 서둘러 지나갔는데, 교회 쪽이 아니었다."], ["Alyosha quickly turned away his eyes and dropped them to the ground, and from the boy’s look alone, Father Païssy guessed what a great change was taking place in him at that moment.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 알료샤는 얼른 눈길을 돌려 땅바닥으로 떨구었고, 그 눈빛 하나만으로도 파이시 신부는 지금 이 순간 그의 안에서 얼마나 큰 변화가 일어나고 있는지를 짐작했다."]], [["“Have you, too, fallen into temptation?”", "\"너마저 유혹에 빠진 게냐?\""], ["cried Father Païssy.", "파이시 신부가 외쳤다."], ["“Can you be with those of little faith?”", "\"너마저 믿음이 약한 자들 틈에 끼려는 게냐?\""], ["he added mournfully.", "그가 침울하게 덧붙였다."]], [["Alyosha stood still and gazed vaguely at Father Païssy, but quickly turned his eyes away again and again looked on the ground.", "알료샤는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 파이시 신부를 멍하니 바라보았으나, 곧 다시 눈길을 돌려 또 땅바닥을 내려다보았다."], ["He stood sideways and did not turn his face to Father Païssy, who watched him attentively.", "그는 옆으로 비스듬히 서서 파이시 신부에게 얼굴을 돌리지 않았는데, 신부는 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보았다."]], [["“Where are you hastening?", "\"어디로 그리 서두르느냐?"], ["The bell calls to service,”", "종이 예배를 알리고 있다.\""], ["he asked again, but again Alyosha gave no answer.", "그가 다시 물었으나, 알료샤는 또다시 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Are you leaving the hermitage?", "\"은수처를 떠나려는 게냐?"], ["What, without asking leave, without asking a blessing?”", "무어라, 허락도 구하지 않고, 축복도 청하지 않고?\""]], [["Alyosha suddenly gave a wry smile, cast a strange, very strange, look at the Father to whom his former guide, the former sovereign of his heart and mind, his beloved elder, had confided him as he lay dying.", "알료샤는 별안간 일그러진 미소를 짓더니, 죽어 가며 자신의 옛 인도자, 자신의 마음과 정신의 옛 군주, 사랑하는 장로가 그를 맡긴 그 신부에게 기이한, 너무도 기이한 눈길을 던졌다. 그러고는 별안간 여전히 한마디 말도 없이, 예의를 차릴 마음조차 없는 듯 손을 한 번 내젓고는, 빠른 걸음으로 은수처를 벗어나 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["And suddenly, still without speaking, waved his hand, as though not caring even to be respectful, and with rapid steps walked towards the gates away from the hermitage.", "\"너는 다시 돌아올 게다!\" 파이시 신부가 슬픈 놀라움과 함께 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You will come back again!” murmured Father Païssy, looking after him with sorrowful surprise.", "각주: 여기서 '무슨 세상의 기쁨이'는 정교회 장례식에서 평수도사를 위해 부르는 짧은 송가의 첫 구절이고, '우리의 도움이요 방패시여'는 장로나 사제처럼 높은 위격의 고인을 위해 부르는 장중한 성가다."]], [["# Chapter II. A Critical Moment", "# Chapter II. A Critical Moment"]], [["Father Païssy, of course, was not wrong when he decided that his “dear boy” would come back again.", "파이시 신부가 자기의 \"사랑하는 아이\"가 다시 돌아오리라고 판단한 것은 물론 틀린 일이 아니었다."], ["Perhaps indeed, to some extent, he penetrated with insight into the true meaning of Alyosha’s spiritual condition.", "어쩌면 실로 그는 어느 정도까지 알료샤의 영적 상태가 지닌 참된 의미를 통찰의 눈으로 꿰뚫어 보았을 것이다."], ["Yet I must frankly own that it would be very difficult for me to give a clear account of that strange, vague moment in the life of the young hero I love so much.", "그러나 솔직히 고백하건대, 내가 그토록 사랑하는 이 젊은 주인공의 생애에서 벌어진 그 기이하고도 막연한 순간을 명료하게 풀어내기란 나로서는 무척이나 어려운 일이다."], ["To Father Païssy’s sorrowful question, “Are you too with those of little faith?”", "파이시 신부의 슬픔 어린 물음, \"그대 또한 믿음이 얕은 자들 가운데 끼어 있는가?\"에 대해 나는 물론 알료샤를 대신해 자신 있게 대답할 수 있다."], ["I could of course confidently answer for Alyosha, “No, he is not with those of little faith.", "\"아니다, 그는 믿음이 얕은 자들 가운데 있지 않다."], ["Quite the contrary.”", "오히려 정반대다.\""], ["Indeed, all his trouble came from the fact that he was of great faith.", "사실 그의 모든 괴로움은 그가 큰 믿음을 지녔다는 바로 그 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["But still the trouble was there and was so agonizing that even long afterwards Alyosha thought of that sorrowful day as one of the bitterest and most fatal days of his life.", "그렇지만 괴로움은 엄연히 거기 있었고, 그것이 어찌나 사무치는 것이었던지 알료샤는 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에도 그 슬픔의 날을 자기 생애에서 가장 쓰라리고 가장 운명적이었던 날의 하나로 떠올렸다."], ["If the question is asked: “Could all his grief and disturbance have been only due to the fact that his elder’s body had shown signs of premature decomposition instead of at once performing miracles?”", "만약 누군가 이렇게 묻는다면, \"그의 모든 비탄과 동요가 그저 그의 장로의 시신이 곧장 기적을 행하기는커녕 때 이른 부패의 징후를 드러냈다는 사실 하나 때문이었단 말인가?\"라고 묻는다면, 나는 변죽을 울리지 않고 곧바로 답해야 하리라."], ["I must answer without beating about the bush, “Yes, it certainly was.”", "\"그렇다, 분명 그러했다.\""], ["I would only beg the reader not to be in too great a hurry to laugh at my young hero’s pure heart.", "다만 나는 독자가 내 젊은 주인공의 순결한 마음을 비웃는 일에 너무 서두르지 말기를 청할 뿐이다."], ["I am far from intending to apologize for him or to justify his innocent faith on the ground of his youth, or the little progress he had made in his studies, or any such reason.", "나는 그를 변명하려거나, 그의 천진한 믿음을 그의 어린 나이나 학업에서 거둔 보잘것없는 진척이나 그 밖의 어떤 이유로도 정당화하려는 의도가 전혀 없다."], ["I must declare, on the contrary, that I have genuine respect for the qualities of his heart.", "오히려 나는 선언해야 하리니, 나는 그의 마음이 지닌 품성에 진정한 경의를 품고 있다."], ["No doubt a youth who received impressions cautiously, whose love was lukewarm, and whose mind was too prudent for his age and so of little value, such a young man might, I admit, have avoided what happened to my hero.", "의심할 바 없이, 인상을 조심스럽게만 받아들이고, 그 사랑이 미적지근하며, 그 정신이 제 나이에 견주어 지나치게 신중하여 그만큼 값어치가 적은 그런 젊은이라면, 인정하건대, 내 주인공에게 일어난 일을 피할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But in some cases it is really more creditable to be carried away by an emotion, however unreasonable, which springs from a great love, than to be unmoved.", "그러나 어떤 경우에는, 비록 분별없을지언정 큰 사랑에서 솟아오른 감정에 사로잡히는 편이 도리어 무감각한 것보다 더 떳떳한 법이다."], ["And this is even truer in youth, for a young man who is always sensible is to be suspected and is of little worth—that’s my opinion!", "그리고 이는 젊은 시절에는 더욱 그러하니, 언제나 분별 있기만 한 젊은이란 의심스러운 자이며 값어치가 적은 자이기 때문이다. 이것이 내 견해다!"]], [["“But,”", "\"그렇지만,\""], ["reasonable people will exclaim perhaps, “every young man cannot believe in such a superstition and your hero is no model for others.”", "분별 있는 사람들은 어쩌면 이렇게 외치리라. \"젊은이라고 누구나 다 그런 미신을 믿을 수 있는 건 아니며, 당신의 주인공은 다른 이들의 본보기가 못 되오.\""]], [["To this I reply again, “Yes!", "이에 나는 다시 답하겠다. \"그렇다!"], ["my hero had faith, a faith holy and steadfast, but still I am not going to apologize for him.”", "내 주인공은 믿음을, 거룩하고 굳건한 믿음을 지니고 있었다. 그렇지만 그래도 나는 그를 변명하지는 않으련다.\""]], [["Though I declared above, and perhaps too hastily, that I should not explain or justify my hero, I see that some explanation is necessary for the understanding of the rest of my story.", "위에서 나는, 어쩌면 너무 성급하게, 내 주인공을 설명하거나 정당화하지 않겠노라 선언했으나, 내 이야기의 나머지를 이해하기 위해서는 얼마간의 설명이 필요하다는 것을 깨닫는다."], ["Let me say then, it was not a question of miracles.", "그러면 이렇게 말해 두자, 그것은 기적의 문제가 아니었다."], ["There was no frivolous and impatient expectation of miracles in his mind.", "그의 마음속에 기적을 향한 경박하고 조급한 기대 따위는 없었다."], ["And Alyosha needed no miracles at the time, for the triumph of some preconceived idea—oh, no, not at all—what he saw before all was one figure—the figure of his beloved elder, the figure of that holy man whom he revered with such adoration.", "그리고 알료샤에게는 그때 어떤 선입견을 굳히기 위한 기적 따위가 필요치 않았으니—오, 아니다, 결코 아니다—그가 무엇보다 먼저 본 것은 한 인물의 모습, 자기가 사랑하는 장로의 모습, 그가 그토록 흠모하며 우러르던 그 거룩한 사람의 모습이었다."], ["The fact is that all the love that lay concealed in his pure young heart for every one and everything had, for the past year, been concentrated—and perhaps wrongly so—on one being, his beloved elder.", "사실인즉, 그의 순결하고 젊은 가슴속에 만인과 만물을 향해 감추어져 있던 그 모든 사랑이, 지난 한 해 동안 한 존재, 곧 그가 사랑하는 장로에게로 집중되어 있었으며—어쩌면 그것은 잘못된 일이었으리라."], ["It is true that being had for so long been accepted by him as his ideal, that all his young strength and energy could not but turn towards that ideal, even to the forgetting at the moment “of every one and everything.”", "그 존재가 그토록 오래도록 그에게 자신의 이상으로 받아들여져 온 것은 사실이니, 그리하여 그의 모든 젊은 힘과 정력이 그 이상을 향하지 않을 수 없었고, 마침내는 그 순간 \"만인과 만물을\" 잊을 정도에까지 이르렀던 것이다."], ["He remembered afterwards how, on that terrible day, he had entirely forgotten his brother Dmitri, about whom he had been so anxious and troubled the day before; he had forgotten, too, to take the two hundred roubles to Ilusha’s father, though he had so warmly intended to do so the preceding evening.", "그는 훗날 떠올렸다, 그 끔찍한 날에 자기가 형 드미트리를 까맣게 잊고 있었음을, 바로 전날만 해도 그토록 마음 졸이며 애를 태우던 그 형을 말이다. 또 그는 일류샤의 아버지에게 이백 루블을 가져다주는 일도 잊고 있었으니, 전날 저녁 그토록 간절히 그러기로 마음먹었건만 그러했다."], ["But again it was not miracles he needed but only “the higher justice” which had been in his belief outraged by the blow that had so suddenly and cruelly wounded his heart.", "그러나 거듭 말하지만, 그가 필요로 한 것은 기적이 아니라 오로지 \"더 높은 정의\"였으며, 그토록 갑작스럽고 잔인하게 그의 가슴을 후려친 일격으로 인해 그 정의가 짓밟혔다고 그는 믿었던 것이다."], ["And what does it signify that this “justice” looked for by Alyosha inevitably took the shape of miracles to be wrought immediately by the ashes of his adored teacher?", "그리고 알료샤가 찾던 이 \"정의\"가 필연적으로, 흠모하던 스승의 유해로 말미암아 즉각 일어날 기적의 형태를 띠었다 한들 그것이 무슨 대수랴?"], ["Why, every one in the monastery cherished the same thought and the same hope, even those whose intellects Alyosha revered, Father Païssy himself, for instance.", "어차피 수도원의 모든 사람이 똑같은 생각과 똑같은 희망을 품고 있었으니, 알료샤가 그 지성을 우러르던 이들조차, 이를테면 파이시 신부 자신마저도 그러하지 않았던가."], ["And so Alyosha, untroubled by doubts, clothed his dreams too in the same form as all the rest.", "그리하여 알료샤도 아무런 의심에 흔들림 없이 자기의 꿈을 나머지 모든 사람과 똑같은 형태로 옷 입혔던 것이다."], ["And a whole year of life in the monastery had formed the habit of this expectation in his heart.", "그리고 수도원에서 보낸 한 해의 세월이 그의 가슴속에 이런 기대의 습관을 빚어 놓았던 것이다."], ["But it was justice, justice, he thirsted for, not simply miracles.", "그러나 그가 목말라했던 것은 정의, 정의였지, 단순한 기적이 아니었다."]], [["And now the man who should, he believed, have been exalted above every one in the whole world, that man, instead of receiving the glory that was his due, was suddenly degraded and dishonored!", "그런데 그가 믿기로는 마땅히 온 세상 누구보다도 높이 떠받들어졌어야 할 그 사람이, 그 사람이 응당 받아야 할 영광을 받기는커녕 별안간 격하되고 욕을 당하다니! 무엇 때문에?"], ["What for? Who had judged him?", "누가 그를 심판했단 말인가?"], ["Who could have decreed this?", "누가 이런 일을 명할 수 있었단 말인가?"], ["Those were the questions that wrung his inexperienced and virginal heart.", "이것이야말로 그의 미숙하고 순결한 가슴을 쥐어짜는 물음이었다."], ["He could not endure without mortification, without resentment even, that the holiest of holy men should have been exposed to the jeering and spiteful mockery of the frivolous crowd so inferior to him.", "거룩한 자들 가운데서도 가장 거룩한 분이, 그분보다 그토록 못한 경박한 무리의 비웃음과 악의 어린 조롱에 내던져졌다는 사실을, 그는 분함 없이, 아니 원망조차 없이는 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Even had there been no miracles, had there been nothing marvelous to justify his hopes, why this indignity, why this humiliation, why this premature decay, “in excess of nature,”", "설령 아무 기적도 없었다 한들, 그의 희망을 정당화할 만한 그 어떤 경이도 없었다 한들, 어찌하여 이런 모욕인가, 어찌하여 이런 굴욕인가, 어찌하여 악의 어린 수도사들의 말마따나 \"자연의 도를 넘은\""], ["as the spiteful monks said?", "이런 때 이른 부패란 말인가?"], ["Why this “sign from heaven,” which they so triumphantly acclaimed in company with Father Ferapont, and why did they believe they had gained the right to acclaim it?", "어찌하여 그들이 페라폰트 신부와 더불어 그토록 의기양양하게 환호하던 이 \"하늘의 징표\"란 말인가, 또 어찌하여 그들은 그것을 환호할 권리를 얻었노라 믿었단 말인가?"], ["Where is the finger of Providence?", "섭리의 손가락은 어디에 있는가?"], ["Why did Providence hide its face “at the most critical moment” (so Alyosha thought it), as though voluntarily submitting to the blind, dumb, pitiless laws of nature?", "어찌하여 섭리는 마치 눈멀고 말 없고 무자비한 자연의 법칙에 스스로 굴복하기라도 하듯이 \"가장 위태로운 순간에\"(알료샤는 그렇게 여겼다) 그 얼굴을 가렸단 말인가?"]], [["That was why Alyosha’s heart was bleeding, and, of course, as I have said already, the sting of it all was that the man he loved above everything on earth should be put to shame and humiliated!", "그것이 바로 알료샤의 가슴이 피 흘린 까닭이었으니, 물론 이미 말했듯이, 그 모든 일의 가시는 그가 이 세상 무엇보다도 사랑하는 사람이 수치와 굴욕을 당해야 했다는 데 있었던 것이다!"], ["This murmuring may have been shallow and unreasonable in my hero, but I repeat again for the third time—and am prepared to admit that it might be difficult to defend my feeling—I am glad that my hero showed himself not too reasonable at that moment, for any man of sense will always come back to reason in time, but, if love does not gain the upper hand in a boy’s heart at such an exceptional moment, when will it?", "내 주인공의 이런 원망이 얕고 분별없는 것이었는지는 모르나, 나는 세 번째로 거듭 말하거니와—그리고 내 이 감정을 옹호하기란 어려운 일임을 인정할 준비가 되어 있거니와—나는 내 주인공이 그 순간 그다지 분별 있는 모습을 보이지 않았다는 것이 기쁘다. 분별 있는 사람이라면 때가 되면 언제든 분별로 되돌아오게 마련이지만, 만약 그런 예외적인 순간에 소년의 가슴속에서 사랑이 우위를 차지하지 못한다면 대체 언제 그러겠는가?"], ["I will not, however, omit to mention something strange, which came for a time to the surface of Alyosha’s mind at this fatal and obscure moment.", "그러나 나는 이 운명적이고 어두운 순간에 한동안 알료샤의 마음 표면으로 떠오른 어떤 기이한 것을 빼놓지 않고 언급해 두겠다."], ["This new something was the harassing impression left by the conversation with Ivan, which now persistently haunted Alyosha’s mind.", "이 새로운 무언가란 이반과 나눈 대화가 남긴 괴로운 인상으로, 그것이 이제 끈질기게 알료샤의 마음을 따라다녔다."], ["At this moment it haunted him.", "바로 이 순간 그것이 그를 따라다녔다."], ["Oh, it was not that something of the fundamental, elemental, so to speak, faith of his soul had been shaken.", "오, 그의 영혼이 지닌 이른바 근본적이고 본질적인 믿음이 흔들렸다는 것은 아니었다."], ["He loved his God and believed in Him steadfastly, though he was suddenly murmuring against Him.", "그는 자기의 하느님을 사랑했고 굳건히 그분을 믿었으니, 비록 별안간 그분을 원망하고 있기는 했어도 그러했다."], ["Yet a vague but tormenting and evil impression left by his conversation with Ivan the day before, suddenly revived again now in his soul and seemed forcing its way to the surface of his consciousness.", "그렇지만 전날 이반과 나눈 대화가 남긴 막연하면서도 괴롭고 사악한 그 인상이 이제 그의 영혼 속에서 별안간 다시 되살아나 그의 의식 표면으로 비집고 올라오려 하는 듯했다."]], [["It had begun to get dusk when Rakitin, crossing the pine copse from the hermitage to the monastery, suddenly noticed Alyosha, lying face downwards on the ground under a tree, not moving and apparently asleep.", "라키친이 암자에서 수도원으로 가는 솔숲을 가로질러 갈 무렵 날이 어슴푸레 저물기 시작했는데, 그는 별안간 알료샤가 나무 아래 땅바닥에 얼굴을 묻고 엎드린 채 미동도 없이, 보아하니 잠든 듯이 누워 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He went up and called him by his name.", "그는 다가가 그의 이름을 불렀다."]], [["“You here, Alexey?", "\"여기 있었군, 알렉세이?"], ["Can you have—”", "자네 설마—\""], ["he began wondering but broke off.", "그는 의아해하며 말을 꺼냈다가 뚝 그쳤다."], ["He had meant to say, “Can you have come to this?”", "그가 하려던 말은 \"자네 설마 이 지경에까지 이른 건가?\"였다."]], [["Alyosha did not look at him, but from a slight movement Rakitin at once saw that he heard and understood him.", "알료샤는 그를 쳐다보지 않았으나, 라키친은 그 미미한 움직임 하나로 그가 자기 말을 듣고 알아들었음을 단번에 알아챘다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["he went on; but the surprise in his face gradually passed into a smile that became more and more ironical.", "그는 말을 이었으나, 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 놀라움은 차차 미소로 바뀌더니 점점 더 빈정대는 빛을 띠어 갔다."]], [["“I say, I’ve been looking for you for the last two hours.", "\"이봐, 내가 자넬 두 시간이나 찾아다녔단 말일세."], ["You suddenly disappeared.", "자네가 별안간 사라져 버렸으니."], ["What are you about?", "대체 뭘 하고 있는 건가?"], ["What foolery is this?", "이게 무슨 어리광이야?"], ["You might just look at me...”", "나 좀 쳐다보기라도 하지 그래…….\""]], [["Alyosha raised his head, sat up and leaned his back against the tree.", "알료샤는 고개를 들고 일어나 앉더니 나무에 등을 기댔다."], ["He was not crying, but there was a look of suffering and irritability in his face.", "울고 있지는 않았으나 그의 얼굴에는 괴로움과 짜증의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["He did not look at Rakitin, however, but looked away to one side of him.", "그렇지만 그는 라키친을 쳐다보지 않고 그의 한쪽 옆으로 시선을 돌렸다."]], [["“Do you know your face is quite changed?", "\"자네 얼굴이 아주 딴판이 된 거 아나?"], ["There’s none of your famous mildness to be seen in it.", "그 유명한 온화함이라곤 눈을 씻고 봐도 없네그려."], ["Are you angry with some one?", "누구한테 화라도 났나?"], ["Have they been ill-treating you?”", "누가 자넬 못살게 굴기라도 했어?\""]], [["“Let me alone,”", "\"날 좀 내버려 두게.\""], ["said Alyosha suddenly, with a weary gesture of his hand, still looking away from him.", "알료샤가 별안간 말하며 지친 듯 한 손을 내저었으나, 여전히 그에게서 시선을 돌린 채였다."]], [["“Oho!", "\"오호!"], ["So that’s how we are feeling!", "그래, 우리 기분이 그렇단 말이지!"], ["So you can shout at people like other mortals.", "자네도 다른 인간들처럼 남한테 소리를 지를 줄 아는군."], ["That is a come-down from the angels.", "천사 신분에서 한 단계 추락한 셈이야."], ["I say, Alyosha, you have surprised me, do you hear?", "이봐, 알료샤, 자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군, 듣고 있나?"], ["I mean it.", "진심으로 하는 말일세."], ["It’s long since I’ve been surprised at anything here.", "내가 이곳에서 무슨 일에든 놀라 본 게 참 오랜만이야."], ["I always took you for an educated man....”", "난 늘 자넬 교양 있는 사람으로 여겨 왔는데…….\""]], [["Alyosha at last looked at him, but vaguely, as though scarcely understanding what he said.", "알료샤가 마침내 그를 쳐다보았으나, 그 눈길은 그가 하는 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하는 양 멍한 것이었다."]], [["“Can you really be so upset simply because your old man has begun to stink?", "\"자네 정말 그저 자네의 그 늙은이가 썩는 냄새를 풍기기 시작했다는 것 하나 때문에 이렇게 속이 뒤집힐 수 있단 말인가?"], ["You don’t mean to say you seriously believed that he was going to work miracles?”", "설마 그분이 기적을 행하시리라고 진심으로 믿었다는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["exclaimed Rakitin, genuinely surprised again.", "라키친이 다시 진심으로 놀라워하며 외쳤다."]], [["“I believed, I believe, I want to believe, and I will believe, what more do you want?”", "\"난 믿었고, 믿고 있고, 믿고 싶고, 또 믿을 걸세, 더 무얼 바라는가?\""], ["cried Alyosha irritably.", "알료샤가 짜증스레 소리쳤다."]], [["“Nothing at all, my boy.", "\"아무것도 바라지 않아, 이 사람아."], ["Damn it all!", "빌어먹을!"], ["why, no schoolboy of thirteen believes in that now.", "아니, 요즘은 열세 살짜리 학생도 그런 걸 믿지 않아."], ["But there....", "하지만 그래……."], ["So now you are in a temper with your God, you are rebelling against Him; He hasn’t given promotion, He hasn’t bestowed the order of merit!", "그래서 지금 자네는 자네 하느님께 부아가 났고, 그분께 반항하고 있다 이거지. 그분이 승진을 안 시켜 주셨다, 훈장을 안 내려 주셨다 이거야!"], ["Eh, you are a set!”", "허, 자네들 참 별종들이야!\""]], [["Alyosha gazed a long while with his eyes half closed at Rakitin, and there was a sudden gleam in his eyes ...", "알료샤는 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 한참 동안 라키친을 응시했는데, 그 눈에서 별안간 어떤 빛이 번뜩였다……"], ["but not of anger with Rakitin.", "그러나 라키친을 향한 분노의 빛은 아니었다."]], [["“I am not rebelling against my God; I simply ‘don’t accept His world.’”", "\"난 내 하느님께 반항하는 게 아닐세. 다만 '그분의 세계를 받아들이지 않을'"], ["Alyosha suddenly smiled a forced smile.", "뿐이지.\" 알료샤가 별안간 억지웃음을 지어 보였다."]], [["“How do you mean, you don’t accept the world?”", "\"세계를 받아들이지 않는다니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\""], ["Rakitin thought a moment over his answer.", "라키친은 그 대답을 두고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["“What idiocy is this?”", "\"이게 무슨 얼빠진 소리야?\""]], [["Alyosha did not answer.", "알료샤는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Come, enough nonsense, now to business.", "\"자, 헛소린 그만하고, 이제 본론으로 들어가지."], ["Have you had anything to eat to-day?”", "오늘 뭐라도 먹긴 했나?\""]], [["“I don’t remember....", "\"기억이 안 나는군……"], ["I think I have.”", "먹은 것 같기도 하고.\""]], [["“You need keeping up, to judge by your face.", "\"자네 얼굴을 보아하니 기운을 좀 차려야겠어."], ["It makes one sorry to look at you.", "보고 있자니 딱하구먼."], ["You didn’t sleep all night either, I hear, you had a meeting in there.", "듣자 하니 밤새 한숨도 못 잤다며, 거기서 회합이 있었다지."], ["And then all this bobbery afterwards.", "게다가 그 뒤로 이 야단법석까지."], ["Most likely you’ve had nothing to eat but a mouthful of holy bread.", "십중팔구 성찬 빵 한 입밖에 못 먹었겠지."], ["I’ve got some sausage in my pocket; I’ve brought it from the town in case of need, only you won’t eat sausage....”", "내 주머니에 소시지가 좀 있네. 만약을 대비해 읍내에서 가져온 건데, 자넨 소시지를 안 먹을 테고…….\""]], [["“Give me some.”", "\"좀 주게.\""]], [["“I say!", "\"아니 이런!"], ["You are going it!", "자네 갈 데까지 가는군!"], ["Why, it’s a regular mutiny, with barricades!", "아니, 이거야말로 바리케이드까지 친 진짜 반란일세!"], ["Well, my boy, we must make the most of it.", "그래, 이 사람아, 기왕 이렇게 된 거 최대한 누려 봐야지."], ["Come to my place....", "우리 집으로 가세……"], ["I shouldn’t mind a drop of vodka myself, I am tired to death.", "나도 보드카 한 모금쯤은 마다하지 않겠어, 죽도록 피곤하니까."], ["Vodka is going too far for you, I suppose ...", "보드카는 자네한텐 너무 지나친 일이겠지……"], ["or would you like some?”", "아니면 자네도 좀 마시려나?\""]], [["“Give me some vodka too.”", "\"보드카도 좀 주게.\""]], [["“Hullo!", "\"이런!"], ["You surprise me, brother!”", "자네 정말 날 놀라게 하는군, 이 사람아!\""], ["Rakitin looked at him in amazement.", "라키친이 어안이 벙벙해서 그를 바라보았다."], ["“Well, one way or another, vodka or sausage, this is a jolly fine chance and mustn’t be missed.", "\"그래, 어찌 됐든, 보드카든 소시지든, 이거야말로 둘도 없는 좋은 기회인데 놓쳐선 안 되지."], ["Come along.”", "자, 가세.\""]], [["Alyosha got up in silence and followed Rakitin.", "알료샤는 말없이 일어나 라키친을 뒤따랐다."]], [["“If your little brother Ivan could see this—wouldn’t he be surprised!", "\"자네 동생 이반이 이 꼴을 봤다면—얼마나 놀라겠나!"], ["By the way, your brother Ivan set off to Moscow this morning, did you know?”", "그건 그렇고, 자네 형 이반이 오늘 아침 모스크바로 떠났는데, 알고 있었나?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응.\""], ["answered Alyosha listlessly, and suddenly the image of his brother Dmitri rose before his mind.", "알료샤가 맥없이 대답하는데, 별안간 그의 형 드미트리의 모습이 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["But only for a minute, and though it reminded him of something that must not be put off for a moment, some duty, some terrible obligation, even that reminder made no impression on him, did not reach his heart and instantly faded out of his mind and was forgotten.", "그러나 그것은 단 일 분뿐이었으니, 그 모습이 단 한순간도 미룰 수 없는 무언가를, 어떤 의무를, 어떤 끔찍한 책무를 그에게 일깨워 주었음에도, 그 일깨움조차 그에게 아무런 인상도 남기지 못한 채, 그의 가슴에 가닿지 못한 채 곧장 그의 머릿속에서 흐릿하게 사라져 잊히고 말았다."], ["But, a long while afterwards, Alyosha remembered this.", "그러나 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에 알료샤는 이 일을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Your brother Ivan declared once that I was a ‘liberal booby with no talents whatsoever.’ Once you, too, could not resist letting me know I was ‘dishonorable.’ Well!", "\"자네 형 이반은 언젠가 내가 '아무런 재능도 없는 자유주의 얼간이'라고 단언한 적이 있지. 자네도 언젠가 내가 '명예를 모르는 자'라고 기어이 내게 일러 주지 않고는 못 배기더군."], ["I should like to see what your talents and sense of honor will do for you now.”", "그래! 이제 자네의 그 재능과 명예심이 자네한테 무슨 도움이 되는지 어디 한번 보고 싶구먼.\""], ["This phrase Rakitin finished to himself in a whisper.", "이 마지막 말을 라키친은 속으로 나직이 중얼거리며 끝맺었다."]], [["“Listen!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["he said aloud, “let’s go by the path beyond the monastery straight to the town.", "그가 소리 내어 말했다. \"수도원 너머 오솔길로 해서 곧장 읍내로 가세."], ["Hm!", "흠!"], ["I ought to go to Madame Hohlakov’s by the way.", "가는 길에 호흘라코바 부인 댁에 들러야 하는데."], ["Only fancy, I’ve written to tell her everything that happened, and would you believe it, she answered me instantly in pencil (the lady has a passion for writing notes) that ‘she would never have expected such conduct from a man of such a reverend character as Father Zossima.’ That was her very word: ‘conduct.’ She is angry too.", "글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보게, 내가 그분께 일어난 일을 죄다 적어 보냈더니, 자네 믿겠나, 그분이 당장에 연필로 답장을 보내왔지 뭔가(그 부인은 쪽지 쓰는 데 사족을 못 쓰거든). '조시마 신부처럼 그토록 존경받는 인품을 지닌 분에게서 그런 처신을 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다'는 거야."], ["Eh, you are a set!", "그분이 쓴 말 그대로일세, '처신'이라니."], ["Stay!”", "그분도 화가 난 거지."], ["he cried suddenly again.", "허, 자네들 참 별종들이야! 가만!\""], ["He suddenly stopped and taking Alyosha by the shoulder made him stop too.", "그가 별안간 다시 외쳤다. 그는 느닷없이 걸음을 멈추더니 알료샤의 어깨를 붙들어 그도 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Do you know, Alyosha,”", "\"이봐 알료샤, 자네 아나,\""], ["he peeped inquisitively into his eyes, absorbed in a sudden new thought which had dawned on him, and though he was laughing outwardly he was evidently afraid to utter that new idea aloud, so difficult he still found it to believe in the strange and unexpected mood in which he now saw Alyosha.", "그는 자기에게 막 떠오른 어떤 새로운 생각에 골똘히 빠져들어 알료샤의 눈을 캐묻듯 들여다보았는데, 겉으로는 웃고 있었지만 그 새로운 생각을 소리 내어 입에 담기는 분명 두려운 모양이었으니, 그가 지금 알료샤에게서 보고 있는 이 기이하고 뜻밖의 기분을 도무지 믿기 어려웠던 까닭이다."], ["“Alyosha, do you know where we had better go?”", "\"알료샤, 자네 우리가 어디로 가는 게 제일 좋을지 아나?\""], ["he brought out at last timidly, and insinuatingly.", "그가 마침내 머뭇거리며, 슬며시 구슬리듯 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I don’t care ...", "\"아무래도 좋아……"], ["where you like.”", "자네 좋을 대로 하게.\""]], [["“Let’s go to Grushenka, eh?", "\"그루셴카한테 가세, 어때?"], ["Will you come?”", "같이 가겠나?\""], ["pronounced Rakitin at last, trembling with timid suspense.", "라키친이 마침내 머뭇거리는 마음으로 가슴을 졸이며 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Let’s go to Grushenka,”", "\"그루셴카한테 가세.\""], ["Alyosha answered calmly, at once, and this prompt and calm agreement was such a surprise to Rakitin that he almost started back.", "알료샤가 차분하게, 단번에 대답했는데, 이 선뜻하고 차분한 동의가 라키친에게는 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지 그는 하마터면 흠칫 뒤로 물러설 뻔했다."]], [["“Well!", "\"이런!"], ["I say!”", "어럽쇼!\""], ["he cried in amazement, but seizing Alyosha firmly by the arm he led him along the path, still dreading that he would change his mind.", "그가 놀라 외쳤으나, 알료샤의 팔을 단단히 움켜쥐고는 그가 마음을 바꿀까 여전히 두려워하며 오솔길을 따라 그를 이끌었다."]], [["They walked along in silence, Rakitin was positively afraid to talk.", "그들은 말없이 걸어갔으니, 라키친은 입을 떼는 것조차 정녕 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["“And how glad she will be, how delighted!”", "\"그 여자가 얼마나 기뻐할까, 얼마나 좋아 날뛸까!\""], ["he muttered, but lapsed into silence again.", "그가 중얼거렸으나 이내 다시 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["And indeed it was not to please Grushenka he was taking Alyosha to her.", "사실 그가 알료샤를 그녀에게 데려가는 것은 그루셴카를 기쁘게 해 주려는 것이 아니었다."], ["He was a practical person and never undertook anything without a prospect of gain for himself.", "그는 실속을 차리는 인간이라 자기에게 이득이 될 전망이 없으면 무슨 일에도 결코 손을 대지 않았다."], ["His object in this case was twofold, first a revengeful desire to see “the downfall of the righteous,”", "이 경우 그의 목적은 두 가지였으니, 첫째는 \"의로운 자의 몰락\"과 알료샤가 \"성자의 자리에서 죄인의 자리로\""], ["and Alyosha’s fall “from the saints to the sinners,” over which he was already gloating in his imagination, and in the second place he had in view a certain material gain for himself, of which more will be said later.", "떨어지는 꼴을 보고 싶은 앙심 품은 욕망으로, 그는 벌써부터 그 광경을 상상하며 회심의 미소를 짓고 있었고, 둘째로는 자기에게 돌아올 어떤 물질적 이득을 염두에 두고 있었는데, 그에 관해서는 나중에 더 이야기하기로 하겠다."]], [["“So the critical moment has come,”", "\"드디어 결정적인 순간이 온 거야,\""], ["he thought to himself with spiteful glee, “and we shall catch it on the hop, for it’s just what we want.”", "그는 앙큼한 희열에 잠겨 속으로 생각했다. \"이참에 단숨에 낚아채 버리는 거지, 바로 우리가 바라던 바이니 말이야.\""]], [["# Chapter III. An Onion", "# Chapter III. An Onion"]], [["Grushenka lived in the busiest part of the town, near the cathedral square, in a small wooden lodge in the courtyard belonging to the house of the widow Morozov.", "그루셴카는 시내에서 가장 번잡한 구역, 대성당 광장 가까이에 살았으니, 모로조바 미망인 소유의 저택 안뜰에 딸린 자그마한 목조 별채가 그녀의 거처였다."], ["The house was a large stone building of two stories, old and very ugly.", "본채는 이층짜리 커다란 석조 건물로, 낡은 데다 보기에도 흉했다."], ["The widow led a secluded life with her two unmarried nieces, who were also elderly women.", "미망인은 역시 나이 든 처녀인 결혼하지 않은 두 조카딸과 더불어 은둔하다시피 살았다."], ["She had no need to let her lodge, but every one knew that she had taken in Grushenka as a lodger, four years before, solely to please her kinsman, the merchant Samsonov, who was known to be the girl’s protector.", "별채를 세놓을 필요가 전혀 없는 처지였으나, 그녀가 사 년 전 그루셴카를 세입자로 들인 것은 오로지 친척인 상인 삼소노프의 비위를 맞추기 위해서였음을 누구나 알고 있었으니, 삼소노프가 이 처녀의 후견인이라는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이었다."], ["It was said that the jealous old man’s object in placing his “favorite” with the widow Morozov was that the old woman should keep a sharp eye on her new lodger’s conduct.", "질투심 많은 이 노인이 자기의 \"총애하는 여자\"를 모로조바 미망인의 곁에 둔 까닭은, 늙은 여인으로 하여금 새 세입자의 품행을 매서운 눈으로 감시하게 하려 함이었다는 말이 있었다."], ["But this sharp eye soon proved to be unnecessary, and in the end the widow Morozov seldom met Grushenka and did not worry her by looking after her in any way.", "그러나 이 매서운 눈은 이내 쓸모없는 것으로 드러났고, 결국 모로조바 미망인은 그루셴카와 마주치는 일조차 드물었으며 어떤 식으로도 그녀를 살피거나 귀찮게 굴지 않았다."], ["It is true that four years had passed since the old man had brought the slim, delicate, shy, timid, dreamy, and sad girl of eighteen from the chief town of the province, and much had happened since then.", "노인이 호리호리하고 가냘프며 수줍고 겁 많고 몽상에 잠긴 듯 슬픈 열여덟 살 처녀를 도(道)의 중심 도시에서 데려온 지 사 년이 지났고, 그 사이에 적잖은 일이 벌어진 것은 사실이었다."], ["Little was known of the girl’s history in the town and that little was vague.", "이 처녀의 내력에 관하여 시내에 알려진 것은 거의 없었으며, 그나마 알려진 것도 어렴풋했다."], ["Nothing more had been learnt during the last four years, even after many persons had become interested in the beautiful young woman into whom Agrafena Alexandrovna had meanwhile developed.", "그 사이 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 자라나 빚어낸 아름다운 젊은 여인에게 많은 이들이 관심을 가지게 된 뒤에도, 지난 사 년 동안 더 알아낸 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["There were rumors that she had been at seventeen betrayed by some one, some sort of officer, and immediately afterwards abandoned by him.", "소문에 따르면 그녀는 열일곱 살에 누군가에게, 무슨 장교라는 자에게 농락당했고, 그 직후 그에게 버림받았다고 했다."], ["The officer had gone away and afterwards married, while Grushenka had been left in poverty and disgrace.", "그 장교는 떠나 버린 뒤 결혼했고, 그루셴카는 가난과 치욕 속에 남겨졌다는 것이었다."], ["It was said, however, that though Grushenka had been raised from destitution by the old man, Samsonov, she came of a respectable family belonging to the clerical class, that she was the daughter of a deacon or something of the sort.", "그러나 비록 그루셴카가 노인 삼소노프 덕분에 궁핍에서 벗어나기는 했으나, 그녀가 성직자 계급에 속한 점잖은 가문 출신으로, 부제(副祭)인지 그 비슷한 무엇인지 하는 사람의 딸이라는 말도 있었다."]], [["And now after four years the sensitive, injured and pathetic little orphan had become a plump, rosy beauty of the Russian type, a woman of bold and determined character, proud and insolent.", "그리고 이제 사 년이 지나, 상처 입고 가련하던 예민한 어린 고아는 통통하고 발그레한 러시아풍 미인으로, 대담하고 단호한 성격에 거만하고 오만한 여인으로 변해 있었다."], ["She had a good head for business, was acquisitive, saving and careful, and by fair means or foul had succeeded, it was said, in amassing a little fortune.", "그녀는 장사에 밝은 머리를 지녔고, 욕심이 많으며 알뜰하고 빈틈이 없었으니, 수단이 옳건 그르건 가리지 않고 제법 재산을 모으는 데 성공했다는 말이 돌았다."], ["There was only one point on which all were agreed.", "다만 한 가지 점에 대해서만큼은 모두의 의견이 일치했다."], ["Grushenka was not easily to be approached and except her aged protector there had not been one man who could boast of her favors during those four years.", "그루셴카에게는 쉬이 다가갈 수 없었고, 늙은 후견인을 제외하면 지난 사 년 동안 그녀의 총애를 받았노라 자랑할 수 있는 사내는 단 한 명도 없었다는 점이었다."], ["It was a positive fact, for there had been a good many, especially during the last two years, who had attempted to obtain those favors.", "그것은 엄연한 사실이었으니, 특히 지난 이 년 동안 그 총애를 얻으려 시도한 자가 적지 않았기 때문이다."], ["But all their efforts had been in vain and some of these suitors had been forced to beat an undignified and even comic retreat, owing to the firm and ironical resistance they met from the strong-willed young person.", "그러나 그들의 온갖 노력은 헛수고로 돌아갔고, 이 구애자들 중 더러는 의지가 굳센 이 젊은 여인에게서 단호하고 빈정대는 거절을 당한 끝에 위신을 잃은, 심지어 우스꽝스럽기까지 한 퇴각을 할 수밖에 없었다."], ["It was known, too, that the young person had, especially of late, been given to what is called “speculation,” and that she had shown marked abilities in that direction, so that many people began to say that she was no better than a Jew.", "또한 알려진 바로는, 이 젊은 여인이 특히 근래 들어 이른바 \"투기\"라는 것에 손을 댔고, 그 방면에서 두드러진 재능을 보였기에, 적잖은 사람들이 그녀가 유대인과 다를 바 없다고 입에 올리기 시작했다는 것이다."], ["It was not that she lent money on interest, but it was known, for instance, that she had for some time past, in partnership with old Karamazov, actually invested in the purchase of bad debts for a trifle, a tenth of their nominal value, and afterwards had made out of them ten times their value.", "그녀가 이자를 받고 돈을 빌려준 것은 아니었으나, 이를테면 얼마 전부터 늙은 카라마조프와 손을 잡고 부실 채권을 명목 가치의 십분의 일에 불과한 헐값으로 사들였다가, 나중에 그것으로 본래 가치의 열 배를 벌어들였다는 것은 익히 알려진 사실이었다."]], [["The old widower Samsonov, a man of large fortune, was stingy and merciless.", "홀아비 노인 삼소노프는 막대한 재산을 지닌 사람으로, 인색하고 무자비했다."], ["He tyrannized over his grown-up sons, but, for the last year during which he had been ill and lost the use of his swollen legs, he had fallen greatly under the influence of his protégée, whom he had at first kept strictly and in humble surroundings, “on Lenten fare,”", "그는 장성한 아들들을 폭군처럼 휘둘렀으나, 지난 일 년 동안 병으로 퉁퉁 부은 다리를 쓰지 못하게 되면서부터는 자기가 보살피던 여자의 영향력에 크게 휘둘리게 되었으니, 처음에는 그녀를 엄히 단속하며 변변찮은 처지에, 당시 익살꾼들이 일컫던 대로 \"사순절 음식이나 먹는\""], ["as the wits said at the time.", "형편에 묶어 두었던 터였다."], ["But Grushenka had succeeded in emancipating herself, while she established in him a boundless belief in her fidelity.", "그러나 그루셴카는 스스로를 해방시키는 데 성공했고, 동시에 노인에게는 자기 정절에 대한 끝없는 믿음을 심어 놓았다."], ["The old man, now long since dead, had had a large business in his day and was also a noteworthy character, miserly and hard as flint.", "이제는 죽은 지 오래된 그 노인은 한 시절 큰 사업을 했고, 또한 인색하고 부싯돌처럼 단단한, 주목할 만한 인물이기도 했다."], ["Though Grushenka’s hold upon him was so strong that he could not live without her (it had been so especially for the last two years), he did not settle any considerable fortune on her and would not have been moved to do so, if she had threatened to leave him.", "그루셴카가 그를 사로잡은 힘이 어찌나 강했던지 그는 그녀 없이는 살 수 없을 지경이었으나(특히 지난 이 년간은 더욱 그러했다), 그렇다고 그녀에게 상당한 재산을 떼어 주지는 않았고, 설령 그녀가 떠나겠노라 위협했더라도 마음이 흔들리지 않았을 것이다."], ["But he had presented her with a small sum, and even that was a surprise to every one when it became known.", "다만 그는 그녀에게 적은 액수를 선물했는데, 그것조차 알려졌을 때 모두를 놀라게 했다."]], [["“You are a wench with brains,”", "\"너는 머리가 잘 돌아가는 계집이야.\" 노인은 그녀에게 팔천 루블을 주면서 말했다."], ["he said to her, when he gave her eight thousand roubles, “and you must look after yourself, but let me tell you that except your yearly allowance as before, you’ll get nothing more from me to the day of my death, and I’ll leave you nothing in my will either.”", "\"그러니 네 앞가림은 네가 알아서 하고, 다만 한 가지 일러두자면, 전처럼 해마다 받는 생활비를 빼고는 내가 죽는 날까지 나한테서 더 받을 건 아무것도 없을 게다. 그리고 내 유언에도 너한테 남길 건 하나 없을 게야.\""]], [["And he kept his word; he died and left everything to his sons, whom, with their wives and children, he had treated all his life as servants.", "그리고 그는 그 말을 지켰다. 그는 죽으면서 모든 것을 아들들에게 남겼는데, 평생 그 아들들을 아내와 자식들까지 싸잡아 종 부리듯 다루어 왔던 터였다."], ["Grushenka was not even mentioned in his will.", "그루셴카는 그의 유언에 이름조차 언급되지 않았다."], ["All this became known afterwards.", "이 모든 사실은 나중에야 알려졌다."], ["He helped Grushenka with his advice to increase her capital and put business in her way.", "그는 그루셴카에게 자본을 불릴 조언을 해 주고 장사할 길을 터 주는 것으로 도움을 주었다."]], [["When Fyodor Pavlovitch, who first came into contact with Grushenka over a piece of speculation, ended to his own surprise by falling madly in love with her, old Samsonov, gravely ill as he was, was immensely amused.", "표도르 파블로비치가 처음 어느 투기 거래를 두고 그루셴카와 인연을 맺었다가 스스로도 놀랍게 그녀에게 미친 듯이 빠져드는 것으로 끝나고 말았을 때, 늙은 삼소노프는 중병을 앓는 처지였으면서도 더없이 재미있어했다."], ["It is remarkable that throughout their whole acquaintance Grushenka was absolutely and spontaneously open with the old man, and he seems to have been the only person in the world with whom she was so.", "주목할 만한 점은, 둘이 알고 지내는 내내 그루셴카가 노인에게만큼은 완전히 또 거리낌 없이 속을 터놓았다는 사실이며, 노인은 그녀가 그토록 마음을 연 세상에 단 하나뿐인 사람이었던 듯하다."], ["Of late, when Dmitri too had come on the scene with his love, the old man left off laughing.", "근래에 드미트리마저 자기 사랑을 품고 무대에 등장하자, 노인은 웃음을 거두었다."], ["On the contrary, he once gave Grushenka a stern and earnest piece of advice.", "도리어 한번은 그루셴카에게 엄하고도 진지한 충고를 한마디 던졌다."]], [["“If you have to choose between the two, father or son, you’d better choose the old man, if only you make sure the old scoundrel will marry you and settle some fortune on you beforehand.", "\"아비냐 아들이냐, 둘 중에 골라야 한다면 늙은 쪽을 고르는 게 낫지. 다만 그 늙은 악당이 너랑 결혼하고 미리 재산도 좀 떼어 줄 게 확실해야 한다."], ["But don’t keep on with the captain, you’ll get no good out of that.”", "하지만 그 대위 놈하고는 계속 어울리지 마라, 그래 봤자 좋을 게 하나도 없으니까.\""]], [["These were the very words of the old profligate, who felt already that his death was not far off and who actually died five months later.", "이것이 바로, 이미 제 죽음이 멀지 않았음을 느끼던, 그리고 실제로 다섯 달 뒤에 죽은 그 늙은 난봉꾼의 말이었다."]], [["I will note, too, in passing, that although many in our town knew of the grotesque and monstrous rivalry of the Karamazovs, father and son, the object of which was Grushenka, scarcely any one understood what really underlay her attitude to both of them.", "또 한 가지 지나가는 김에 일러두겠는데, 우리 시내의 많은 사람들이 그루셴카를 두고 벌어진 카라마조프 부자(父子)의 기괴하고 흉측한 경쟁을 알고 있었으나, 그녀가 두 사람을 대하는 태도의 밑바닥에 정작 무엇이 깔려 있는지를 이해하는 자는 거의 없었다."], ["Even Grushenka’s two servants (after the catastrophe of which we will speak later) testified in court that she received Dmitri Fyodorovitch simply from fear because “he threatened to murder her.”", "그루셴카의 두 하녀조차(나중에 이야기할 그 참극이 벌어진 뒤) 법정에서 증언하기를, 여주인이 드미트리 표도로비치를 받아들인 것은 순전히 두려움 때문이었다고 했으니, \"그가 자기를 죽이겠다고 협박했기\""], ["These servants were an old cook, invalidish and almost deaf, who came from Grushenka’s old home, and her granddaughter, a smart young girl of twenty, who performed the duties of a maid.", "때문이라는 것이었다. 이 하녀들이란 그루셴카의 옛집에서 따라온, 병약하고 귀가 거의 먹은 늙은 식모와, 하녀 일을 맡아 보는 스무 살의 영리한 젊은 처녀인 그 손녀였다."], ["Grushenka lived very economically and her surroundings were anything but luxurious.", "그루셴카는 매우 검소하게 살았고, 그녀의 살림살이는 결코 호화롭다고 할 수 없었다."], ["Her lodge consisted of three rooms furnished with mahogany furniture in the fashion of 1820, belonging to her landlady.", "그녀의 별채는 방 세 칸으로 이루어졌고, 집주인 소유의, 1820년대 양식을 따른 마호가니 가구가 놓여 있었다."]], [["It was quite dark when Rakitin and Alyosha entered her rooms, yet they were not lighted up.", "라키틴과 알료샤가 그녀의 방에 들어섰을 때는 이미 사위가 캄캄했는데도 불을 켜 두지 않은 상태였다."], ["Grushenka was lying down in her drawing-room on the big, hard, clumsy sofa, with a mahogany back.", "그루셴카는 거실의, 마호가니 등받이가 달린 크고 딱딱하며 투박한 소파에 누워 있었다."], ["The sofa was covered with shabby and ragged leather.", "소파에는 닳고 해진 가죽이 씌워져 있었다."], ["Under her head she had two white down pillows taken from her bed.", "머리 밑에는 침대에서 가져온 하얀 솜털 베개 두 개를 받쳐 두었다."], ["She was lying stretched out motionless on her back with her hands behind her head.", "그녀는 두 손을 머리 뒤로 한 채 똑바로 누워 꼼짝도 않고 몸을 쭉 뻗고 있었다."], ["She was dressed as though expecting some one, in a black silk dress, with a dainty lace fichu on her head, which was very becoming.", "마치 누군가를 기다리기라도 하는 듯한 차림새였으니, 검은 비단 드레스를 입고 머리에는 매우 잘 어울리는 앙증맞은 레이스 두건을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Over her shoulders was thrown a lace shawl pinned with a massive gold brooch.", "어깨에는 묵직한 금 브로치로 여민 레이스 숄을 걸쳤다."], ["She certainly was expecting some one.", "그녀는 분명 누군가를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She lay as though impatient and weary, her face rather pale and her lips and eyes hot, restlessly tapping the arm of the sofa with the tip of her right foot.", "그녀는 애가 타고 지친 듯이 누워 있었는데, 얼굴은 다소 핼쑥했으나 입술과 눈은 달아올랐고, 오른발 끝으로 소파 팔걸이를 안절부절못하며 톡톡 두드리고 있었다."], ["The appearance of Rakitin and Alyosha caused a slight excitement.", "라키틴과 알료샤가 나타나자 그녀는 약간 동요했다."], ["From the hall they could hear Grushenka leap up from the sofa and cry out in a frightened voice, “Who’s there?”", "현관에서 두 사람은 그루셴카가 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 겁먹은 목소리로 외치는 소리를 들었다. \"누구세요?\""], ["But the maid met the visitors and at once called back to her mistress.", "그러나 하녀가 손님들을 맞이하고는 곧장 여주인에게 되받아 소리쳤다."]], [["“It’s not he, it’s nothing, only other visitors.”", "\"그분이 아니에요, 아무것도 아니에요, 그냥 다른 손님들이세요.\""]], [["“What can be the matter?”", "\"대체 무슨 일이람?\""], ["muttered Rakitin, leading Alyosha into the drawing-room.", "라키틴은 중얼거리며 알료샤를 데리고 거실로 들어섰다."]], [["Grushenka was standing by the sofa as though still alarmed.", "그루셴카는 여전히 놀란 듯이 소파 곁에 서 있었다."], ["A thick coil of her dark brown hair escaped from its lace covering and fell on her right shoulder, but she did not notice it and did not put it back till she had gazed at her visitors and recognized them.", "짙은 갈색 머리채 한 가닥이 레이스 두건에서 풀려나와 오른쪽 어깨로 흘러내렸으나, 그녀는 그것을 알아차리지도, 손님들을 찬찬히 들여다보고 누구인지 알아볼 때까지는 다시 매만지지도 않았다."]], [["“Ah, it’s you, Rakitin?", "\"아, 너였구나, 라키틴?"], ["You quite frightened me.", "깜짝 놀랐잖아."], ["Whom have you brought?", "누구를 데려온 거야?"], ["Who is this with you?", "같이 온 이 사람은 누구지?"], ["Good heavens, you have brought him!”", "오, 세상에, 그분을 데려왔구나!\""], ["she exclaimed, recognizing Alyosha.", "그녀는 알료샤를 알아보고는 소리쳤다."]], [["“Do send for candles!”", "\"촛불 좀 가져오라고 해요!\""], ["said Rakitin, with the free-and-easy air of a most intimate friend, who is privileged to give orders in the house.", "라키틴은 이 집에서 명령을 내릴 자격이 있는 더없이 막역한 친구다운, 거리낌 없고 스스럼없는 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Candles ...", "\"촛불……"], ["of course, candles....", "그래, 촛불……"], ["Fenya, fetch him a candle....", "페냐, 촛불 좀 가져다드려라……"], ["Well, you have chosen a moment to bring him!”", "거참, 하필 이런 때를 골라 이분을 데려오다니!\""], ["she exclaimed again, nodding towards Alyosha, and turning to the looking-glass she began quickly fastening up her hair with both hands.", "그녀는 알료샤 쪽으로 고갯짓을 하며 다시 소리치더니, 거울 쪽으로 돌아서서 두 손으로 부랴부랴 머리를 매만지기 시작했다."], ["She seemed displeased.", "못마땅한 기색이었다."]], [["“Haven’t I managed to please you?”", "\"내가 영 마음에 안 들게 했나 보군요?\""], ["asked Rakitin, instantly almost offended.", "라키틴은 대뜸 거의 화가 난 듯이 물었다."]], [["“You frightened me, Rakitin, that’s what it is.”", "\"날 놀라게 했잖아, 라키틴, 그 말이야.\""], ["Grushenka turned with a smile to Alyosha.", "그루셴카는 미소를 지으며 알료샤에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“Don’t be afraid of me, my dear Alyosha, you cannot think how glad I am to see you, my unexpected visitor.", "\"날 무서워 마요, 사랑스러운 알료샤, 뜻밖에 찾아와 준 당신을 보니 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 당신은 모를 거예요."], ["But you frightened me, Rakitin, I thought it was Mitya breaking in.", "하지만 넌 날 놀라게 했어, 라키틴, 미탸가 쳐들어오는 줄 알았다고."], ["You see, I deceived him just now, I made him promise to believe me and I told him a lie.", "알겠지만 내가 방금 그를 속였거든, 날 믿겠다는 약속을 받아 내고는 거짓말을 한 거야."], ["I told him that I was going to spend the evening with my old man, Kuzma Kuzmitch, and should be there till late counting up his money.", "오늘 저녁은 나의 노인장, 쿠지마 쿠지미치하고 함께 보내며 늦게까지 그분 돈 계산을 도와드릴 거라고 했지."], ["I always spend one whole evening a week with him making up his accounts.", "난 일주일에 한 번은 꼬박 저녁 내내 그분과 함께 장부를 정리해."], ["We lock ourselves in and he counts on the reckoning beads while I sit and put things down in the book.", "우린 문을 걸어 잠그고, 그분이 주판알을 튕기면 나는 앉아서 장부에 적어 넣는 거야."], ["I am the only person he trusts.", "그분이 믿는 사람은 나밖에 없거든."], ["Mitya believes that I am there, but I came back and have been sitting locked in here, expecting some news.", "미탸는 내가 거기 있는 줄 알고 있지만, 사실 나는 돌아와서 여기 문을 걸어 잠그고 앉아 무슨 소식을 기다리고 있던 참이야."], ["How was it Fenya let you in?", "페냐가 어쩌다 너희를 들여보냈을까?"], ["Fenya, Fenya, run out to the gate, open it and look about whether the captain is to be seen!", "페냐, 페냐, 어서 문간으로 달려가서 문을 열고 그 대위가 보이지 않나 살펴봐라!"], ["Perhaps he is hiding and spying, I am dreadfully frightened.”", "어쩌면 어딘가 숨어서 엿보고 있을지도 몰라, 난 무서워 죽겠어.\""]], [["“There’s no one there, Agrafena Alexandrovna, I’ve just looked out, I keep running to peep through the crack, I am in fear and trembling myself.”", "\"거기 아무도 없어요, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 방금 내다봤어요, 저도 자꾸만 틈으로 달려가 엿보고 있는걸요, 저 역시 무서워서 벌벌 떨고 있답니다.\""]], [["“Are the shutters fastened, Fenya?", "\"덧문은 잠갔니, 페냐?"], ["And we must draw the curtains—that’s better!”", "그리고 커튼도 쳐야겠다—그게 낫겠어!\""], ["She drew the heavy curtains herself.", "그녀는 손수 묵직한 커튼을 쳤다."], ["“He’d rush in at once if he saw a light.", "\"불빛을 보면 당장 뛰어들 테니까."], ["I am afraid of your brother Mitya to-day, Alyosha.”", "오늘은 당신 형 미탸가 무서워, 알료샤.\""]], [["Grushenka spoke aloud, and, though she was alarmed, she seemed very happy about something.", "그루셴카는 큰 소리로 말했는데, 비록 겁에 질려 있긴 했으나 무언가에 대해 무척 행복해하는 듯했다."]], [["“Why are you so afraid of Mitya to-day?”", "\"오늘따라 왜 그렇게 미탸를 무서워하는 거요?\""], ["inquired Rakitin.", "라키틴이 물었다."], ["“I should have thought you were not timid with him, you’d twist him round your little finger.”", "\"당신은 그자 앞에서 겁낼 사람이 아닌 줄 알았는데, 손가락 하나로 그자를 쥐락펴락하잖소.\""]], [["“I tell you, I am expecting news, priceless news, so I don’t want Mitya at all.", "\"분명히 말하지만, 난 소식을 기다리고 있어, 더없이 귀한 소식을, 그러니 미탸는 조금도 곁에 두고 싶지 않다고."], ["And he didn’t believe, I feel he didn’t, that I should stay at Kuzma Kuzmitch’s.", "게다가 그는 믿지 않았어, 느낌으로 알아, 내가 쿠지마 쿠지미치네 머물러 있을 거라는 걸 믿지 않았다고."], ["He must be in his ambush now, behind Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, in the garden, watching for me.", "지금쯤 그는 분명 표도르 파블로비치네 뒷마당 정원에 매복해서 날 노리고 있을 거야."], ["And if he’s there, he won’t come here, so much the better!", "거기 있다면 여기로는 오지 않을 테니, 그럴수록 더 잘된 일이지!"], ["But I really have been to Kuzma Kuzmitch’s, Mitya escorted me there.", "하지만 난 정말로 쿠지마 쿠지미치네 다녀왔어, 미탸가 거기까지 날 바래다줬거든."], ["I told him I should stay there till midnight, and I asked him to be sure to come at midnight to fetch me home.", "자정까지 거기 있겠노라 일러두고, 자정에 꼭 데리러 와 달라고 부탁했지."], ["He went away and I sat ten minutes with Kuzma Kuzmitch and came back here again.", "그가 떠나고 나서 나는 쿠지마 쿠지미치와 십 분쯤 앉아 있다가 여기로 다시 돌아온 거야."], ["Ugh, I was afraid, I ran for fear of meeting him.”", "어휴, 무서워서, 그를 마주칠까 봐 부랴부랴 달려왔다고.\""]], [["“And why are you so dressed up?", "\"그런데 왜 그렇게 차려입은 거요?"], ["What a curious cap you’ve got on!”", "머리에 쓴 그 두건은 또 뭐가 그리 유별나오!\""]], [["“How curious you are yourself, Rakitin!", "\"넌 너대로 어찌나 유난을 떠는지, 라키틴!"], ["I tell you, I am expecting a message.", "말했잖아, 난 전갈을 기다리고 있다고."], ["If the message comes, I shall fly, I shall gallop away and you will see no more of me.", "전갈이 오면 난 날아갈 거야, 말을 달려 떠나 버릴 테니 너희는 다시는 내 모습을 못 볼걸."], ["That’s why I am dressed up, so as to be ready.”", "그래서 채비를 갖추려고 이렇게 차려입은 거라고.\""]], [["“And where are you flying to?”", "\"그래, 어디로 날아간다는 거요?\""]], [["“If you know too much, you’ll get old too soon.”", "\"너무 많이 알면 그만큼 일찍 늙는 법이야.\""]], [["“Upon my word!", "\"이거 참!"], ["You are highly delighted ...", "무척이나 들떠 있군……"], ["I’ve never seen you like this before.", "당신이 이런 모습은 처음 보오."], ["You are dressed up as if you were going to a ball.”", "무도회에라도 가는 것처럼 차려입었구려.\""], ["Rakitin looked her up and down.", "라키틴은 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다."]], [["“Much you know about balls.”", "\"무도회에 대해 아주 많이도 아는구나.\""]], [["“And do you know much about them?”", "\"그럼 당신은 무도회를 많이 아오?\""]], [["“I have seen a ball.", "\"무도회를 본 적이 있어."], ["The year before last, Kuzma Kuzmitch’s son was married and I looked on from the gallery.", "재작년에 쿠지마 쿠지미치 아들이 결혼했을 때 회랑에서 구경했지."], ["Do you suppose I want to be talking to you, Rakitin, while a prince like this is standing here.", "라키틴, 이런 왕자님 같은 분이 여기 서 계신데 내가 너 따위와 떠들고 있고 싶겠니. 이런 손님이 오셨는데!"], ["Such a visitor! Alyosha, my dear boy, I gaze at you and can’t believe my eyes.", "알료샤, 사랑스러운 분, 당신을 보고 있어도 내 눈을 믿을 수가 없어요."], ["Good heavens, can you have come here to see me!", "오, 세상에, 정말 날 보러 여기 와 주신 거예요!"], ["To tell you the truth, I never had a thought of seeing you and I didn’t think that you would ever come and see me.", "솔직히 말하면, 난 당신을 만나리라곤 한 번도 생각지 못했고, 당신이 날 보러 와 줄 거라고도 생각지 못했어요."], ["Though this is not the moment now, I am awfully glad to see you.", "지금이 그럴 때는 아니지만, 그래도 당신을 보니 정말이지 너무 기뻐요."], ["Sit down on the sofa, here, that’s right, my bright young moon.", "소파에 앉아요, 여기, 그래요, 나의 환히 빛나는 어린 달님."], ["I really can’t take it in even now....", "지금도 도무지 믿기지가 않네요……"], ["Eh, Rakitin, if only you had brought him yesterday or the day before!", "아, 라키틴, 네가 어제나 그제 이분을 데려왔더라면!"], ["But I am glad as it is!", "하지만 이대로도 기뻐!"], ["Perhaps it’s better he has come now, at such a moment, and not the day before yesterday.”", "어쩌면 그제가 아니라 지금, 바로 이런 순간에 오신 게 더 잘된 일인지도 몰라.\""]], [["She gayly sat down beside Alyosha on the sofa, looking at him with positive delight.", "그녀는 더없이 즐거운 듯이 알료샤 곁 소파에 앉아 진심 어린 기쁨으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["And she really was glad, she was not lying when she said so.", "그녀는 정말로 기뻤고, 기쁘다고 말한 것은 거짓이 아니었다."], ["Her eyes glowed, her lips laughed, but it was a good-hearted merry laugh.", "그녀의 눈은 빛났고 입술은 웃고 있었으나, 그것은 선량하고 명랑한 웃음이었다."], ["Alyosha had not expected to see such a kind expression in her face....", "알료샤는 그녀의 얼굴에서 그토록 다정한 표정을 보게 되리라고는 미처 예상치 못했었다……"], ["He had hardly met her till the day before, he had formed an alarming idea of her, and had been horribly distressed the day before by the spiteful and treacherous trick she had played on Katerina Ivanovna.", "그는 어제 처음 그녀를 만났다시피 했고, 그녀에 대해 무시무시한 인상을 품고 있었으며, 어제는 그녀가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 부린 악랄하고 음흉한 농간 때문에 끔찍이도 괴로워했던 터였다."], ["He was greatly surprised to find her now altogether different from what he had expected.", "이제 그녀가 자기가 예상했던 것과는 전혀 딴판인 모습임을 알고 그는 몹시 놀랐다."], ["And, crushed as he was by his own sorrow, his eyes involuntarily rested on her with attention.", "그리고 자신의 슬픔에 짓눌린 처지였으면서도, 그의 눈은 저도 모르게 주의를 기울여 그녀에게 머물렀다."], ["Her whole manner seemed changed for the better since yesterday, there was scarcely any trace of that mawkish sweetness in her speech, of that voluptuous softness in her movements.", "어제 이후 그녀의 태도는 통째로 더 나은 쪽으로 바뀐 듯했으니, 그녀의 말투에서는 그 느끼하리만치 달콤하던 기색이, 몸짓에서는 그 관능적인 나긋함이 거의 자취를 감추고 없었다."], ["Everything was simple and good-natured, her gestures were rapid, direct, confiding, but she was greatly excited.", "모든 것이 소박하고 선량했으며, 그녀의 몸짓은 재고 직선적이고 거리낌이 없었으나, 다만 그녀는 몹시 흥분해 있었다."]], [["“Dear me, how everything comes together to-day!”", "\"어머나, 오늘은 어쩜 이렇게 모든 일이 한꺼번에 겹치는지!\""], ["she chattered on again.", "그녀는 다시 재잘거리기 시작했다."], ["“And why I am so glad to see you, Alyosha, I couldn’t say myself!", "\"그리고 알료샤, 당신을 보는 게 왜 이리도 기쁜지, 나도 알 수가 없어요!"], ["If you ask me, I couldn’t tell you.”", "누가 물어도 대답할 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Come, don’t you know why you’re glad?”", "\"이런, 당신은 자기가 왜 기쁜지도 모르오?\""], ["said Rakitin, grinning.", "라키틴이 이를 드러내고 히죽거리며 말했다."], ["“You used to be always pestering me to bring him, you’d some object, I suppose.”", "\"당신은 늘 나를 들볶으며 이 사람을 데려오라고 하지 않았소, 무슨 속셈이 있었던 게지.\""]], [["“I had a different object once, but now that’s over, this is not the moment.", "\"한때는 다른 속셈이 있었지만, 이제 그건 다 끝났어, 지금은 그럴 때가 아니야."], ["I say, I want you to have something nice. I am so good-natured now.", "저기, 내가 너희한테 뭐 좋은 걸 좀 대접하고 싶어."], ["You sit down, too, Rakitin; why are you standing?", "난 지금 마음이 이렇게 너그러워졌거든."], ["You’ve sat down already?", "너도 앉아, 라키틴, 왜 서 있어?"], ["There’s no fear of Rakitin’s forgetting to look after himself.", "벌써 앉았네? 라키틴이 제 앞가림하는 걸 잊을까 봐 걱정할 필요는 없지."], ["Look, Alyosha, he’s sitting there opposite us, so offended that I didn’t ask him to sit down before you.", "봐요, 알료샤, 저기 우리 맞은편에 앉아서는, 내가 당신보다 먼저 앉으라고 권하지 않았다고 어찌나 토라졌는지."], ["Ugh, Rakitin is such a one to take offense!”", "어휴, 라키틴은 정말이지 토라지기 선수라니까!\""], ["laughed Grushenka.", "그루셴카는 웃었다."], ["“Don’t be angry, Rakitin, I’m kind to-day.", "\"화내지 마, 라키틴, 난 오늘 마음이 너그러우니까."], ["Why are you so depressed, Alyosha?", "알료샤, 당신은 왜 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있어요?"], ["Are you afraid of me?”", "내가 무서워요?\""], ["She peeped into his eyes with merry mockery.", "그녀는 명랑한 장난기를 담아 그의 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["“He’s sad.", "\"이 사람은 슬픈 거요."], ["The promotion has not been given,”", "승진이 안 됐거든.\""], ["boomed Rakitin.", "라키틴이 굵은 소리로 말했다."]], [["“What promotion?”", "\"무슨 승진?\""]], [["“His elder stinks.”", "\"이 사람 장로가 썩어 가고 있잖소.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["You are talking some nonsense, you want to say something nasty.", "무슨 말도 안 되는 소릴 하는 거야, 무슨 고약한 소리를 하고 싶은 게지."], ["Be quiet, you stupid!", "닥쳐, 이 멍청아!"], ["Let me sit on your knee, Alyosha, like this.”", "당신 무릎에 앉게 해 줘요, 알료샤, 이렇게.\""], ["She suddenly skipped forward and jumped, laughing, on his knee, like a nestling kitten, with her right arm about his neck.", "그녀는 갑자기 깡충 다가와 웃으면서, 둥지 속 새끼 고양이처럼 그의 무릎 위로 뛰어올라 오른팔로 그의 목을 감았다."], ["“I’ll cheer you up, my pious boy.", "\"당신 기운을 북돋아 줄게요, 나의 경건한 분."], ["Yes, really, will you let me sit on your knee?", "그래요, 정말로, 당신 무릎에 앉아도 될까요?"], ["You won’t be angry?", "화나지 않을 거예요?"], ["If you tell me, I’ll get off?”", "화나면 말해요, 내려갈 테니까?\""]], [["Alyosha did not speak.", "알료샤는 입을 열지 않았다."], ["He sat afraid to move, he heard her words, “If you tell me, I’ll get off,” but he did not answer.", "그는 꼼짝하기조차 두려운 듯이 앉아 \"화나면 말해요, 내려갈게요\"라는 그녀의 말을 들었으나 대답하지 않았다."], ["But there was nothing in his heart such as Rakitin, for instance, watching him malignantly from his corner, might have expected or fancied.", "그러나 그의 마음속에는, 이를테면 한구석에서 그를 음흉하게 지켜보던 라키틴이 예상하거나 짐작했을 법한 그 어떤 것도 없었다."], ["The great grief in his heart swallowed up every sensation that might have been aroused, and, if only he could have thought clearly at that moment, he would have realized that he had now the strongest armor to protect him from every lust and temptation.", "그의 마음속에 자리한 크나큰 슬픔이 일어날 법한 온갖 욕정을 죄다 집어삼켜 버렸으니, 만약 그 순간 그가 또렷이 생각할 수 있었다면, 자신이 이제 온갖 정욕과 유혹으로부터 자기를 지켜 줄 가장 든든한 갑옷을 갖추었음을 깨달았으리라."], ["Yet in spite of the vague irresponsiveness of his spiritual condition and the sorrow that overwhelmed him, he could not help wondering at a new and strange sensation in his heart.", "그러나 그의 영적 상태가 어렴풋이 무감각한 데다 그를 짓누르는 슬픔에도 불구하고, 그는 자기 마음속에 일어난 새롭고 야릇한 감정에 놀라지 않을 수 없었다."], ["This woman, this “dreadful”", "이 여자, 이 \"무시무시한\""], ["woman, had no terror for him now, none of that terror that had stirred in his soul at any passing thought of woman.", "여자가 이제는 그에게 아무런 두려움도 일으키지 않았으니, 여자라는 것을 잠깐 떠올리기만 해도 그의 영혼 속에 일렁이던 그 두려움이 조금도 없었다."], ["On the contrary, this woman, dreaded above all women, sitting now on his knee, holding him in her arms, aroused in him now a quite different, unexpected, peculiar feeling, a feeling of the intensest and purest interest without a trace of fear, of his former terror.", "도리어 모든 여자 가운데 가장 두려워하던 이 여자가, 지금 그의 무릎에 앉아 그를 두 팔로 안고 있는 이 여자가, 그의 안에 지금껏과는 사뭇 다른, 뜻밖의, 묘한 감정을 일으켰으니, 그것은 두려움이라고는, 예전의 그 공포라고는 흔적조차 없는, 더없이 강렬하고 더없이 순수한 관심의 감정이었다."], ["That was what instinctively surprised him.", "바로 그 점이 본능적으로 그를 놀라게 했다."]], [["“You’ve talked nonsense enough,”", "\"허튼소리는 그쯤 했으면 됐소.\""], ["cried Rakitin, “you’d much better give us some champagne.", "라키틴이 외쳤다. \"그보다 우리한테 샴페인이나 좀 내오시구려."], ["You owe it me, you know you do!”", "당신이 내게 진 빚이잖소, 잘 알면서!\""]], [["“Yes, I really do.", "\"그래, 정말 그렇지."], ["Do you know, Alyosha, I promised him champagne on the top of everything, if he’d bring you?", "알료샤, 알아요? 내가 당신을 데려와 주면 다른 것에 더해 샴페인까지 주겠다고 이 사람한테 약속했거든요."], ["I’ll have some too!", "나도 좀 마실래!"], ["Fenya, Fenya, bring us the bottle Mitya left!", "페냐, 페냐, 미탸가 두고 간 그 병을 가져와!"], ["Look sharp!", "어서 서둘러!"], ["Though I am so stingy, I’ll stand a bottle, not for you, Rakitin, you’re a toadstool, but he is a falcon!", "내가 이렇게 인색하긴 해도 한 병 정도는 내겠어, 너 때문이 아니라, 라키틴, 넌 독버섯이지만 이분은 매니까!"], ["And though my heart is full of something very different, so be it, I’ll drink with you.", "그리고 내 마음은 전혀 딴것으로 가득 차 있지만, 그래, 그러라지, 너희와 함께 마시겠어."], ["I long for some dissipation.”", "흥청거리며 한바탕 놀고 싶어서 못 견디겠으니.\""]], [["“But what is the matter with you?", "\"한데 대체 무슨 일이오?"], ["And what is this message, may I ask, or is it a secret?”", "그리고 그 전갈이라는 게 뭐요, 물어봐도 될까, 아니면 비밀인가?\""], ["Rakitin put in inquisitively, doing his best to pretend not to notice the snubs that were being continually aimed at him.", "라키틴은 자기를 향해 끊임없이 날아드는 면박을 못 본 척하려 애쓰며 캐묻듯 끼어들었다."]], [["“Ech, it’s not a secret, and you know it, too,”", "\"에이, 비밀도 아니고, 너도 다 알잖아.\""], ["Grushenka said, in a voice suddenly anxious, turning her head towards Rakitin, and drawing a little away from Alyosha, though she still sat on his knee with her arm round his neck.", "그루셴카는 갑자기 불안해진 목소리로, 라키틴 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며, 여전히 팔로 알료샤의 목을 감은 채 무릎에 앉아 있으면서도 그에게서 약간 물러나 앉으며 말했다."], ["“My officer is coming, Rakitin, my officer is coming.”", "\"내 장교가 오고 있어, 라키틴, 내 장교가 오고 있단 말이야.\""]], [["“I heard he was coming, but is he so near?”", "\"그자가 온다는 말은 들었지만, 그렇게 가까이 와 있소?\""]], [["“He is at Mokroe now; he’ll send a messenger from there, so he wrote; I got a letter from him to-day.", "\"지금 모크로예에 있어. 거기서 사람을 보내겠다고, 그렇게 편지에 썼더라고."], ["I am expecting the messenger every minute.”", "오늘 그에게서 편지를 받았어. 시시각각 그 심부름꾼을 기다리고 있는 거야.\""]], [["“You don’t say so!", "\"설마!"], ["Why at Mokroe?”", "왜 하필 모크로예에 있다는 거요?\""]], [["“That’s a long story, I’ve told you enough.”", "\"그건 긴 이야기야, 너한테 할 만큼 했어.\""]], [["“Mitya’ll be up to something now—I say!", "\"미탸가 이제 무슨 일을 저지를 텐데—거참!"], ["Does he know or doesn’t he?”", "그자가 아는 거요, 모르는 거요?\""]], [["“He know! Of course he doesn’t.", "\"그가 안다고!"], ["If he knew, there would be murder.", "당연히 몰라. 알았다면 살인이 났겠지."], ["But I am not afraid of that now, I am not afraid of his knife.", "하지만 이제 난 그게 두렵지 않아, 그의 칼도 두렵지 않다고."], ["Be quiet, Rakitin, don’t remind me of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, he has bruised my heart.", "조용히 해, 라키틴, 내게 드미트리 표도로비치 이야기는 꺼내지 마, 그는 내 가슴에 멍이 들도록 했으니까."], ["And I don’t want to think of that at this moment.", "그리고 난 지금 이 순간 그 생각은 하고 싶지 않아."], ["I can think of Alyosha here, I can look at Alyosha ...", "여기 알료샤를 생각할 수 있어, 알료샤를 바라볼 수 있다고……"], ["smile at me, dear, cheer up, smile at my foolishness, at my pleasure....", "웃어 봐요, 사랑스러운 분, 기운 내요, 내 어리석음을, 내 기쁨을 보고 웃어 줘요……"], ["Ah, he’s smiling, he’s smiling!", "아, 웃는다, 웃어요!"], ["How kindly he looks at me!", "어쩜 저렇게 다정하게 날 봐 주는지!"], ["And you know, Alyosha, I’ve been thinking all this time you were angry with me, because of the day before yesterday, because of that young lady.", "그리고 있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 그제 일 때문에, 그 아가씨 일 때문에 당신이 줄곧 내게 화가 나 있을 거라고 생각했어요."], ["I was a cur, that’s the truth....", "난 못된 년이었어, 그게 사실이지요……"], ["But it’s a good thing it happened so.", "하지만 그렇게 된 게 차라리 잘된 일이에요."], ["It was a horrid thing, but a good thing too.”", "끔찍한 일이었지만, 좋은 일이기도 했어요.\""], ["Grushenka smiled dreamily and a little cruel line showed in her smile.", "그루셴카는 꿈꾸듯 미소 지었는데, 그 미소에 약간 잔인한 주름이 비쳤다."], ["“Mitya told me that she screamed out that I ‘ought to be flogged.’ I did insult her dreadfully.", "\"미탸가 그러는데, 그 아가씨가 내가 '매를 맞아야 한다'고 비명을 질렀다더군요. 내가 그녀를 끔찍하게 모욕하긴 했지요."], ["She sent for me, she wanted to make a conquest of me, to win me over with her chocolate....", "그녀가 날 부른 건, 나를 자기편으로 만들려고, 초콜릿으로 날 구슬려 자기 사람으로 만들려는 거였어요……"], ["No, it’s a good thing it did end like that.”", "아니, 그렇게 끝나 버린 게 잘된 일이에요.\""], ["She smiled again.", "그녀는 다시 미소 지었다."], ["“But I am still afraid of your being angry.”", "\"하지만 난 여전히 당신이 화가 났을까 봐 두려워요.\""]], [["“Yes, that’s really true,”", "\"맞아, 정말 그렇구려.\""], ["Rakitin put in suddenly with genuine surprise.", "라키틴이 갑자기 진심으로 놀라며 끼어들었다."], ["“Alyosha, she is really afraid of a chicken like you.”", "\"알료샤, 이 여자가 정말로 당신 같은 햇병아리를 무서워하다니.\""]], [["“He is a chicken to you, Rakitin ...", "\"이분이 너한테나 햇병아리지, 라키틴……"], ["because you’ve no conscience, that’s what it is!", "그건 네게 양심이라곤 없기 때문이야, 바로 그거라고!"], ["You see, I love him with all my soul, that’s how it is!", "봐, 난 이분을 온 영혼을 다해 사랑해, 그게 진실이라고!"], ["Alyosha, do you believe I love you with all my soul?”", "알료샤, 내가 당신을 온 영혼을 다해 사랑한다는 게 믿겨요?\""]], [["“Ah, you shameless woman!", "\"이런, 부끄러움도 모르는 여자 같으니!"], ["She is making you a declaration, Alexey!”", "당신한테 사랑 고백을 하고 있구려, 알렉세이!\""]], [["“Well, what of it, I love him!”", "\"그래, 그게 어때서, 난 이분을 사랑해!\""]], [["“And what about your officer?", "\"그럼 당신 장교는 어쩌고?"], ["And the priceless message from Mokroe?”", "그리고 모크로예에서 온다는 그 더없이 귀한 전갈은?\""]], [["“That is quite different.”", "\"그건 전혀 다른 거야.\""]], [["“That’s a woman’s way of looking at it!”", "\"그게 바로 여자의 사고방식이지!\""]], [["“Don’t you make me angry, Rakitin.”", "\"날 화나게 하지 마, 라키틴.\""], ["Grushenka caught him up hotly.", "그루셴카가 발끈하며 그의 말을 가로챘다."], ["“This is quite different.", "\"이건 전혀 달라."], ["I love Alyosha in a different way.", "난 알료샤를 다른 식으로 사랑한다고."], ["It’s true, Alyosha, I had sly designs on you before.", "사실이에요, 알료샤, 난 전에 당신에게 음흉한 속셈을 품었어요."], ["For I am a horrid, violent creature.", "난 끔찍하고 사나운 인간이니까요."], ["But at other times I’ve looked upon you, Alyosha, as my conscience.", "하지만 다른 때에는, 알료샤, 난 당신을 내 양심처럼 여겨 왔어요."], ["I’ve kept thinking ‘how any one like that must despise a nasty thing like me.’ I thought that the day before yesterday, as I ran home from the young lady’s.", "'저런 사람이라면 나처럼 더러운 것을 얼마나 경멸할까' 하고 줄곧 생각했지요."], ["I have thought of you a long time in that way, Alyosha, and Mitya knows, I’ve talked to him about it.", "그제 그 아가씨네서 집으로 달려오면서도 그런 생각을 했어요. 알료샤, 난 오랫동안 당신을 그렇게 생각해 왔고, 미탸도 알아요, 그에게 그 이야기를 했거든요."], ["Mitya understands.", "미탸는 이해해요."], ["Would you believe it, I sometimes look at you and feel ashamed, utterly ashamed of myself....", "믿어지나요, 난 가끔 당신을 바라보면 부끄러워져요, 나 자신이 한없이 부끄러워진다고요……"], ["And how, and since when, I began to think about you like that, I can’t say, I don’t remember....”", "그리고 어쩌다가, 언제부터 당신을 그렇게 생각하게 됐는지는 말할 수가 없어요, 기억이 안 나요……\""]], [["Fenya came in and put a tray with an uncorked bottle and three glasses of champagne on the table.", "페냐가 들어와 마개를 딴 병 하나와 샴페인이 담긴 잔 세 개를 쟁반에 받쳐 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["“Here’s the champagne!”", "\"샴페인이 왔구려!\""], ["cried Rakitin.", "라키틴이 외쳤다."], ["“You’re excited, Agrafena Alexandrovna, and not yourself.", "\"당신은 흥분해서, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 제정신이 아니구려."], ["When you’ve had a glass of champagne, you’ll be ready to dance.", "샴페인 한 잔 들이켜고 나면 당장 춤이라도 출 기세요."], ["Eh, they can’t even do that properly,”", "에잇, 이런 것조차 제대로 할 줄 모르다니.\""], ["he added, looking at the bottle.", "그는 병을 보며 덧붙였다."], ["“The old woman’s poured it out in the kitchen and the bottle’s been brought in warm and without a cork.", "\"노파가 부엌에서 따라 놓는 바람에 병이 미지근해진 데다 마개도 없이 들어왔구려."], ["Well, let me have some, anyway.”", "뭐, 그래도 어쨌든 좀 마셔 봅시다.\""]], [["He went up to the table, took a glass, emptied it at one gulp and poured himself out another.", "그는 탁자로 다가가 잔 하나를 들고 단숨에 비운 뒤 자기 잔에 다시 따랐다."]], [["“One doesn’t often stumble upon champagne,”", "\"샴페인이란 게 흔히 마주칠 수 있는 게 아니지.\""], ["he said, licking his lips.", "그는 입맛을 다시며 말했다."], ["“Now, Alyosha, take a glass, show what you can do!", "\"자, 알료샤, 잔을 들어요, 당신 솜씨 좀 보여 주시오!"], ["What shall we drink to?", "무엇을 위해 마실까?"], ["The gates of paradise?", "천국의 문을 위해?"], ["Take a glass, Grushenka, you drink to the gates of paradise, too.”", "잔을 들어요, 그루셴카, 당신도 천국의 문을 위해 마시구려.\""]], [["“What gates of paradise?”", "\"무슨 천국의 문?\""]], [["She took a glass, Alyosha took his, tasted it and put it back.", "그녀가 잔을 들었고, 알료샤도 자기 잔을 들어 한 모금 맛보고는 도로 내려놓았다."]], [["“No, I’d better not,”", "\"아니, 안 마시는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["he smiled gently.", "그는 부드럽게 미소 지었다."]], [["“And you bragged!”", "\"그렇게 큰소리를 쳐 놓고!\""], ["cried Rakitin.", "라키틴이 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, if so, I won’t either,”", "\"그래, 그렇다면 나도 안 마실래.\""], ["chimed in Grushenka, “I really don’t want any.", "그루셴카가 맞장구쳤다. \"사실 난 조금도 마시고 싶지 않아."], ["You can drink the whole bottle alone, Rakitin.", "라키틴, 너 혼자 병을 다 비워도 돼."], ["If Alyosha has some, I will.”", "알료샤가 마시면 나도 마시겠어.\""]], [["“What touching sentimentality!”", "\"이 무슨 가슴 뭉클한 감상이람!\""], ["said Rakitin tauntingly; “and she’s sitting on his knee, too!", "라키틴이 빈정대며 말했다. \"게다가 저 사람 무릎에까지 앉아 있고!"], ["He’s got something to grieve over, but what’s the matter with you?", "이 사람이야 슬퍼할 일이 있다지만, 당신은 대체 무슨 일이오?"], ["He is rebelling against his God and ready to eat sausage....”", "이 사람은 제 하느님께 반항하고 소시지라도 먹어 치울 판인데……\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“His elder died to-day, Father Zossima, the saint.”", "\"이 사람 장로가 오늘 죽었거든, 조시마 신부, 그 성자 말이오.\""]], [["“So Father Zossima is dead,”", "\"그럼 조시마 신부님이 돌아가셨다는 거야.\""], ["cried Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 외쳤다."], ["“Good God, I did not know!”", "\"오, 하느님, 난 몰랐어!\""], ["She crossed herself devoutly.", "그녀는 경건하게 성호를 그었다."], ["“Goodness, what have I been doing, sitting on his knee like this at such a moment!”", "\"세상에, 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거람, 이런 순간에 이렇게 이분 무릎에 앉아 있다니!\""], ["She started up as though in dismay, instantly slipped off his knee and sat down on the sofa.", "그녀는 소스라치게 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어나, 곧장 그의 무릎에서 미끄러져 내려와 소파에 앉았다."]], [["Alyosha bent a long wondering look upon her and a light seemed to dawn in his face.", "알료샤는 의아한 눈길로 한참 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴에 무언가 빛이 동트는 듯했다."]], [["“Rakitin,”", "\"라키틴.\""], ["he said suddenly, in a firm and loud voice; “don’t taunt me with having rebelled against God.", "그는 갑자기 단호하고 큰 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 하느님께 반항했다고 빈정대지 마."], ["I don’t want to feel angry with you, so you must be kinder, too, I’ve lost a treasure such as you have never had, and you cannot judge me now.", "나는 너에게 화를 내고 싶지 않으니, 너도 좀 더 너그러워져야 해. 나는 너 같은 사람은 가져 본 적도 없는 보물을 잃었으니, 너는 지금 나를 판단할 수 없어."], ["You had much better look at her—do you see how she has pity on me?", "차라리 이분을 봐—이분이 나를 얼마나 가엾이 여기는지 보이지 않아?"], ["I came here to find a wicked soul—I felt drawn to evil because I was base and evil myself, and I’ve found a true sister, I have found a treasure—a loving heart.", "나는 사악한 영혼을 찾아 여기 왔어—나 자신이 비루하고 사악했기에 악에 이끌렸던 거야, 그런데 진정한 누이를 찾았어, 보물을 찾았다고—사랑하는 마음을."], ["She had pity on me just now....", "이분은 방금 나를 가엾이 여겨 주었어……"], ["Agrafena Alexandrovna, I am speaking of you.", "아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 당신을 두고 하는 말입니다."], ["You’ve raised my soul from the depths.”", "당신이 내 영혼을 깊은 나락에서 들어 올려 주었어요.\""]], [["Alyosha’s lips were quivering and he caught his breath.", "알료샤의 입술이 떨렸고, 그는 숨을 삼켰다."]], [["“She has saved you, it seems,”", "\"이 여자가 당신을 구원했다는 말씀이로군.\""], ["laughed Rakitin spitefully.", "라키틴이 악의에 차서 웃었다."], ["“And she meant to get you in her clutches, do you realize that?”", "\"이 여자가 당신을 제 손아귀에 넣으려 했다는 걸 당신은 알기나 하오?\""]], [["“Stay, Rakitin.”", "\"그만해, 라키틴.\""], ["Grushenka jumped up.", "그루셴카가 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["“Hush, both of you.", "\"둘 다 조용히 해."], ["Now I’ll tell you all about it.", "이제 내가 다 이야기할게."], ["Hush, Alyosha, your words make me ashamed, for I am bad and not good—that’s what I am.", "조용히 해요, 알료샤, 당신 말은 나를 부끄럽게 해요, 난 나쁘지 좋은 사람이 아니거든요—내가 바로 그런 사람이에요."], ["And you hush, Rakitin, because you are telling lies.", "그리고 너도 조용히 해, 라키틴, 넌 거짓말을 하고 있으니까."], ["I had the low idea of trying to get him in my clutches, but now you are lying, now it’s all different.", "그를 내 손아귀에 넣으려는 비열한 생각을 품었던 건 맞지만, 지금 넌 거짓말을 하고 있어, 이제는 모든 게 달라졌으니까."], ["And don’t let me hear anything more from you, Rakitin.”", "그리고 라키틴, 네 입에서 더는 아무 소리도 듣고 싶지 않아.\""]], [["All this Grushenka said with extreme emotion.", "이 모든 말을 그루셴카는 극도로 격앙된 채 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“They are both crazy,”", "\"둘 다 미쳤군.\""], ["said Rakitin, looking at them with amazement.", "라키틴은 어이없다는 듯 두 사람을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I feel as though I were in a madhouse.", "\"꼭 정신병원에 와 있는 기분이오."], ["They’re both getting so feeble they’ll begin crying in a minute.”", "둘 다 어찌나 마음이 약해졌는지 곧 울음이라도 터뜨릴 판이군.\""]], [["“I shall begin to cry, I shall,”", "\"울 거야, 정말 울 테야.\""], ["repeated Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 거듭 말했다."], ["“He called me his sister and I shall never forget that.", "\"이분이 날 누이라고 불렀어, 난 그걸 결코 잊지 못할 거야."], ["Only let me tell you, Rakitin, though I am bad, I did give away an onion.”", "다만 너한테 한마디 해 두지, 라키틴, 내가 나쁜 인간이긴 해도, 양파 한 알은 베푼 적이 있어.\""]], [["“An onion?", "\"양파라고?"], ["Hang it all, you really are crazy.”", "빌어먹을, 당신 정말 미쳤군.\""]], [["Rakitin wondered at their enthusiasm.", "라키틴은 두 사람의 열띤 모습에 어리둥절했다."], ["He was aggrieved and annoyed, though he might have reflected that each of them was just passing through a spiritual crisis such as does not come often in a lifetime.", "그는 모욕감을 느끼고 짜증이 났는데, 사실 두 사람이 저마다 평생에 흔히 찾아오지 않는 영적 위기를 지나고 있는 중임을 헤아렸어야 마땅했다."], ["But though Rakitin was very sensitive about everything that concerned himself, he was very obtuse as regards the feelings and sensations of others—partly from his youth and inexperience, partly from his intense egoism.", "그러나 라키틴은 자기 자신에 관한 일에는 매우 예민했으되, 타인의 감정과 느낌에 대해서는 무척이나 둔감했으니—그것은 한편으로는 그의 젊음과 미숙함 때문이었고, 한편으로는 그의 극심한 이기심 때문이었다."]], [["“You see, Alyosha,”", "\"있잖아요, 알료샤.\""], ["Grushenka turned to him with a nervous laugh.", "그루셴카는 신경질적인 웃음을 지으며 그에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“I was boasting when I told Rakitin I had given away an onion, but it’s not to boast I tell you about it.", "\"내가 양파 한 알을 베풀었다고 라키틴한테 말한 건 으스대려고 한 거지만, 당신한테 이 이야기를 하는 건 으스대려는 게 아니에요."], ["It’s only a story, but it’s a nice story.", "그냥 한 이야기일 뿐인데, 좋은 이야기예요."], ["I used to hear it when I was a child from Matryona, my cook, who is still with me.", "내가 어릴 적, 지금도 나와 함께 사는 식모 마트료나한테서 들었던 거예요."], ["It’s like this.", "이런 이야기랍니다."], ["Once upon a time there was a peasant woman and a very wicked woman she was.", "옛날 옛적에 한 농사꾼 아낙이 있었는데, 어찌나 악독한 여자였는지 몰라요. 그 여자가 죽었는데, 평생 선행이라곤 단 하나도 남기지 못했어요."], ["And she died and did not leave a single good deed behind.", "악마들이 그 여자를 붙잡아 불바다 호수에 처넣었지요. 그래서 그 여자의 수호천사가 서서, 하느님께 아뢸 그 여자의 선행을 무엇이든 떠올려 보려 애를 썼어요."], ["The devils caught her and plunged her into the lake of fire.", "'저 여자가 한번은 제 텃밭에서 양파 한 알을 뽑아서' 하고 천사가 말했어요, '거지 여인에게 준 적이 있습니다.'"], ["So her guardian angel stood and wondered what good deed of hers he could remember to tell to God; ‘She once pulled up an onion in her garden,’ said he, ‘and gave it to a beggar woman.’ And God answered: ‘You take that onion then, hold it out to her in the lake, and let her take hold and be pulled out.", "그러자 하느님이 대답하셨지요. '그러면 그 양파를 가져다가 호수 속의 저 여자에게 내밀어 붙잡게 하여 끌어내 보아라."], ["And if you can pull her out of the lake, let her come to Paradise, but if the onion breaks, then the woman must stay where she is.’ The angel ran to the woman and held out the onion to her.", "네가 그 여자를 호수에서 끌어낼 수 있거든 천국으로 오게 하고, 만일 양파가 끊어지거든 저 여자는 있던 자리에 그대로 두어라.' 천사가 그 여자에게 달려가 양파를 내밀었어요."], ["‘Come,’ said he, ‘catch hold and I’ll pull you out.’ And he began cautiously pulling her out.", "'자.' 천사가 말했지요, '이걸 붙잡아라, 내가 끌어내 주겠다.'"], ["He had just pulled her right out, when the other sinners in the lake, seeing how she was being drawn out, began catching hold of her so as to be pulled out with her.", "그러고는 조심조심 그 여자를 끌어올리기 시작했어요. 막 그 여자를 다 끌어낸 참인데, 호수에 있던 다른 죄인들이 그 여자가 끌려 나오는 걸 보고는 자기들도 함께 끌려 나가려고 그 여자에게 매달리기 시작했어요."], ["But she was a very wicked woman and she began kicking them.", "그런데 그 여자는 어찌나 악독한지 그들을 발로 차 대기 시작했어요."], ["‘I’m to be pulled out, not you.", "'끌려 나가는 건 나지 너희가 아니야."], ["It’s my onion, not yours.’ As soon as she said that, the onion broke.", "이건 내 양파야, 너희 게 아니라고.' 그 여자가 그 말을 하자마자 양파가 뚝 끊어져 버렸어요."], ["And the woman fell into the lake and she is burning there to this day.", "그래서 그 여자는 호수로 떨어졌고, 지금까지도 거기서 타고 있답니다. 그래서 천사는 울면서 떠나갔지요."], ["So the angel wept and went away.", "자, 이게 그 이야기예요, 알료샤."], ["So that’s the story, Alyosha; I know it by heart, for I am that wicked woman myself.", "난 이걸 외우고 있어요, 내가 바로 그 악독한 여자거든요. 라키틴한테는 양파 한 알을 베풀었다고 으스댔지만, 당신한테는 이렇게 말하겠어요."], ["I boasted to Rakitin that I had given away an onion, but to you I’ll say: ‘I’ve done nothing but give away one onion all my life, that’s the only good deed I’ve done.’ So don’t praise me, Alyosha, don’t think me good, I am bad, I am a wicked woman and you make me ashamed if you praise me.", "'난 평생 양파 한 알 베푼 것 말고는 한 일이 아무것도 없어요, 그게 내가 한 단 하나의 선행이에요.' 그러니 날 칭찬하지 말아요, 알료샤, 날 좋은 사람이라 여기지 말아요, 난 나쁜 인간이에요, 난 악독한 여자라고요, 당신이 날 칭찬하면 난 부끄러워져요."], ["Eh, I must confess everything.", "에잇, 모든 걸 다 털어놔야겠어요."], ["Listen, Alyosha.", "들어 봐요, 알료샤."], ["I was so anxious to get hold of you that I promised Rakitin twenty-five roubles if he would bring you to me.", "난 당신을 손에 넣고 싶어서 안달이 난 나머지, 라키틴한테 당신을 데려와 주면 이십오 루블을 주겠다고 약속했어요."], ["Stay, Rakitin, wait!”", "가만있어, 라키틴, 기다려!\""]], [["She went with rapid steps to the table, opened a drawer, pulled out a purse and took from it a twenty-five rouble note.", "그녀는 빠른 걸음으로 탁자로 가서 서랍을 열고 지갑을 꺼내더니 이십오 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼냈다."]], [["“What nonsense!", "\"무슨 헛소리요!"], ["What nonsense!”", "무슨 헛소리야!\""], ["cried Rakitin, disconcerted.", "라키틴이 당황하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Take it. Rakitin, I owe it you, there’s no fear of your refusing it, you asked for it yourself.”", "\"받아, 라키틴, 내가 너한테 진 빚이니 거절할 까닭이야 없겠지, 네가 직접 달라고 한 거잖아.\""], ["And she threw the note to him.", "그러고는 지폐를 그에게 던졌다."]], [["“Likely I should refuse it,”", "\"내가 거절할 리가 있나.\""], ["boomed Rakitin, obviously abashed, but carrying off his confusion with a swagger.", "라키틴은 굵은 소리로 말했는데, 분명 멋쩍어하면서도 그 거북함을 허세로 얼버무렸다."], ["“That will come in very handy; fools are made for wise men’s profit.”", "\"이거 아주 요긴하게 쓰겠군. 어리석은 자들이란 영리한 자들 잇속 채우라고 생기는 법이니까.\""]], [["“And now hold your tongue, Rakitin, what I am going to say now is not for your ears.", "\"이제 입 다물어, 라키틴, 지금 내가 할 이야기는 네 귀에 들으라는 게 아니야."], ["Sit down in that corner and keep quiet.", "저 구석에 앉아서 조용히 있어."], ["You don’t like us, so hold your tongue.”", "넌 우리를 좋아하지도 않으니 입을 다물고 있으라고.\""]], [["“What should I like you for?”", "\"내가 당신들을 좋아할 까닭이 어디 있다고?\""], ["Rakitin snarled, not concealing his ill-humor.", "라키틴은 못마땅한 심기를 감추지 않고 으르렁댔다."], ["He put the twenty-five rouble note in his pocket and he felt ashamed at Alyosha’s seeing it.", "그는 이십오 루블짜리 지폐를 호주머니에 넣었는데, 알료샤가 그걸 본 것이 부끄러웠다."], ["He had reckoned on receiving his payment later, without Alyosha’s knowing of it, and now, feeling ashamed, he lost his temper.", "그는 알료샤 모르게 나중에 돈을 받을 작정이었던 터라, 이제 부끄러운 나머지 화가 치밀었다."], ["Till that moment he had thought it discreet not to contradict Grushenka too flatly in spite of her snubbing, since he had something to get out of her.", "그 순간까지 그는, 그루셴카에게서 뜯어낼 것이 있었으므로 그녀가 면박을 주어도 너무 노골적으로 맞받아치지 않는 편이 분별 있다고 여겼다."], ["But now he, too, was angry:", "그러나 이제는 그 역시 화가 났다."]], [["“One loves people for some reason, but what have either of you done for me?”", "\"사람이란 무슨 까닭이 있어서 사랑하는 법인데, 당신들 둘 중 누가 나한테 해 준 게 뭐가 있다고?\""]], [["“You should love people without a reason, as Alyosha does.”", "\"사람은 까닭 없이 사랑해야 하는 거야, 알료샤가 그러는 것처럼.\""]], [["“How does he love you?", "\"이 사람이 당신을 어떻게 사랑한다고?"], ["How has he shown it, that you make such a fuss about it?”", "당신이 그렇게 호들갑을 떨 만큼 이 사람이 뭘 어떻게 보여 줬소?\""]], [["Grushenka was standing in the middle of the room; she spoke with heat and there were hysterical notes in her voice.", "그루셴카는 방 한가운데 서 있었다. 그녀는 격앙되어 말했고, 그 목소리에는 신경질적인 가락이 서려 있었다."]], [["“Hush, Rakitin, you know nothing about us!", "\"입 다물어, 라키틴, 넌 우리에 대해 아무것도 몰라!"], ["And don’t dare to speak to me like that again.", "그리고 다시는 내게 감히 그런 식으로 지껄이지 마."], ["How dare you be so familiar!", "어떻게 그렇게 함부로 굴 수가 있어!"], ["Sit in that corner and be quiet, as though you were my footman!", "저 구석에 앉아서 내 하인이라도 된 양 조용히 있으라고!"], ["And now, Alyosha, I’ll tell you the whole truth, that you may see what a wretch I am!", "그리고 이제, 알료샤, 당신한테 모든 진실을 다 말할게요, 내가 얼마나 못된 인간인지 알 수 있도록 말이에요!"], ["I am not talking to Rakitin, but to you.", "라키틴한테 하는 말이 아니라, 당신한테 하는 말이에요."], ["I wanted to ruin you, Alyosha, that’s the holy truth; I quite meant to.", "난 당신을 파멸시키고 싶었어요, 알료샤, 그게 거룩한 진실이에요. 정말 그러려고 했어요."], ["I wanted to so much, that I bribed Rakitin to bring you.", "어찌나 그러고 싶었던지 라키틴한테 뇌물을 줘서 당신을 데려오게 한 거예요."], ["And why did I want to do such a thing?", "그런데 내가 왜 그런 짓을 하고 싶었을까요?"], ["You knew nothing about it, Alyosha, you turned away from me; if you passed me, you dropped your eyes.", "알료샤, 당신은 그런 걸 전혀 몰랐어요, 당신은 나한테서 고개를 돌렸고, 나를 지나칠 때면 눈을 내리깔았지요. 그런데 난 오늘 이전에도 당신을 백 번은 바라봤어요."], ["And I’ve looked at you a hundred times before to-day; I began asking every one about you.", "사람들한테 당신에 대해 묻기 시작했지요. 당신 얼굴이 내 가슴을 떠나지 않았어요."], ["Your face haunted my heart.", "'저 사람은 날 경멸해.' 난 그렇게 생각했어요."], ["‘He despises me,’ I thought; ‘he won’t even look at me.’ And I felt it so much at last that I wondered at myself for being so frightened of a boy.", "'나를 쳐다보지도 않아.' 그리고 마침내 그게 어찌나 사무치던지, 사내아이 하나를 그토록 무서워하는 나 자신이 의아할 정도였어요."], ["I’ll get him in my clutches and laugh at him.", "저 사람을 내 손아귀에 넣어서 비웃어 주겠어."], ["I was full of spite and anger.", "난 악의와 분노로 가득 차 있었어요."], ["Would you believe it, nobody here dares talk or think of coming to Agrafena Alexandrovna with any evil purpose.", "믿어지나요, 여기서는 누구도 감히 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 무슨 흉계를 품고 다가올 엄두를 못 내요. 여기서 내가 상대하는 남자라곤 늙은 쿠지마뿐이에요."], ["Old Kuzma is the only man I have anything to do with here; I was bound and sold to him; Satan brought us together, but there has been no one else.", "난 그에게 매여 팔린 몸이니까요. 사탄이 우리를 맺어 준 거지요, 하지만 그 밖에는 아무도 없었어요."], ["But looking at you, I thought, I’ll get him in my clutches and laugh at him.", "그런데 당신을 보면서, 저 사람을 내 손아귀에 넣어 비웃어 주겠다고 생각했어요."], ["You see what a spiteful cur I am, and you called me your sister!", "보다시피 난 이렇게 악독한 년이에요, 그런데도 당신은 날 누이라고 불렀지요!"], ["And now that man who wronged me has come; I sit here waiting for a message from him.", "그리고 이제 나를 농락했던 그 남자가 돌아왔어요. 난 여기 앉아 그에게서 올 전갈을 기다리는 중이에요."], ["And do you know what that man has been to me?", "그런데 그 남자가 나한테 어떤 존재였는지 알아요?"], ["Five years ago, when Kuzma brought me here, I used to shut myself up, that no one might have sight or sound of me.", "오 년 전, 쿠지마가 날 여기 데려왔을 때, 난 누구도 내 모습을 보거나 내 목소리를 듣지 못하도록 문을 걸어 잠그고 틀어박혀 지냈어요. 난 어리석은 풋내기 계집애였어요."], ["I was a silly slip of a girl; I used to sit here sobbing; I used to lie awake all night, thinking: ‘Where is he now, the man who wronged me?", "여기 앉아 흐느껴 울곤 했지요. 밤새 잠 못 이루며 생각했어요."], ["He is laughing at me with another woman, most likely.", "'날 농락한 그 남자는 지금 어디 있을까? 십중팔구 다른 여자랑 날 비웃고 있겠지."], ["If only I could see him, if I could meet him again, I’d pay him out, I’d pay him out!’ At night I used to lie sobbing into my pillow in the dark, and I used to brood over it; I used to tear my heart on purpose and gloat over my anger.", "그를 한 번이라도 볼 수 있다면, 다시 만날 수만 있다면, 본때를 보여 줄 텐데, 본때를 보여 줄 텐데!' 밤이면 어둠 속에서 베개에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼며 그 일을 곱씹곤 했어요."], ["‘I’ll pay him out, I’ll pay him out!’ That’s what I used to cry out in the dark.", "일부러 내 가슴을 쥐어뜯으며 내 분노를 고소해하며 음미했지요. '본때를 보여 줄 테야, 본때를 보여 줄 테야!'"], ["And when I suddenly thought that I should really do nothing to him, and that he was laughing at me then, or perhaps had utterly forgotten me, I would fling myself on the floor, melt into helpless tears, and lie there shaking till dawn.", "어둠 속에서 그렇게 외쳐 댔어요. 그러다 문득, 사실 난 그에게 아무 짓도 못 할 거라는 생각, 그는 그때 날 비웃고 있거나 어쩌면 날 까맣게 잊었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들면, 난 바닥에 몸을 던져 하염없이 눈물에 잠긴 채 새벽이 올 때까지 거기 누워 떨곤 했어요."], ["In the morning I would get up more spiteful than a dog, ready to tear the whole world to pieces.", "아침이면 개보다 더 사나운 꼴로 일어나, 온 세상을 갈가리 찢어발길 기세였지요."], ["And then what do you think?", "그런데 그다음 어찌 됐는지 알아요?"], ["I began saving money, I became hard-hearted, grew stout—grew wiser, would you say?", "난 돈을 모으기 시작했고, 마음이 모질어졌고, 살이 올랐어요—더 영리해졌다고 할까요?"], ["No, no one in the whole world sees it, no one knows it, but when night comes on, I sometimes lie as I did five years ago, when I was a silly girl, clenching my teeth and crying all night, thinking, ‘I’ll pay him out, I’ll pay him out!’ Do you hear?", "아니, 온 세상 누구도 그걸 알지 못해요, 아무도 모르지만, 밤이 찾아오면 난 가끔 오 년 전 어리석은 계집애였을 때처럼 누워, 이를 악물고 밤새 울며 생각해요. '본때를 보여 줄 테야, 본때를 보여 줄 테야!'"], ["Well then, now you understand me.", "들려요? 자, 이제 당신은 나를 이해할 거예요."], ["A month ago a letter came to me—he was coming, he was a widower, he wanted to see me.", "한 달 전 내게 편지 한 통이 왔어요—그가 온다고, 그는 홀아비가 됐고, 나를 보고 싶어 한다고요. 난 숨이 멎는 것 같았어요."], ["It took my breath away; then I suddenly thought: ‘If he comes and whistles to call me, I shall creep back to him like a beaten dog.’ I couldn’t believe myself.", "그러다 문득 이런 생각이 들었지요. '그가 와서 휘파람을 불어 날 부르면, 난 매 맞은 개처럼 그에게 기어 돌아가겠지.'"], ["Am I so abject?", "나 자신을 믿을 수가 없었어요."], ["Shall I run to him or not?", "내가 이토록 비굴한가?"], ["And I’ve been in such a rage with myself all this month that I am worse than I was five years ago.", "그에게 달려갈 것인가 말 것인가? 그래서 난 이 한 달 내내 나 자신에게 어찌나 화가 났던지 오 년 전보다 더 못한 꼴이 됐어요."], ["Do you see now, Alyosha, what a violent, vindictive creature I am?", "이제 알겠어요, 알료샤, 내가 얼마나 난폭하고 앙심 깊은 인간인지?"], ["I have shown you the whole truth!", "당신한테 모든 진실을 다 보여 줬어요!"], ["I played with Mitya to keep me from running to that other.", "난 그 다른 남자에게 달려가지 않으려고 미탸를 가지고 놀았던 거예요."], ["Hush, Rakitin, it’s not for you to judge me, I am not speaking to you.", "입 다물어, 라키틴, 나를 판단하는 건 네가 할 일이 아니야, 너한테 하는 말이 아니라고."], ["Before you came in, I was lying here waiting, brooding, deciding my whole future life, and you can never know what was in my heart.", "너희가 들어오기 전에 난 여기 누워 기다리며, 골똘히 생각하며, 내 앞날 전부를 정하고 있었어, 그러니 너희는 내 가슴속에 무엇이 있었는지 결코 알 수 없을 거야."], ["Yes, Alyosha, tell your young lady not to be angry with me for what happened the day before yesterday....", "그래요, 알료샤, 당신 아가씨한테 전해 줘요, 그제 일어난 일로 나한테 화내지 말라고요……"], ["Nobody in the whole world knows what I am going through now, and no one ever can know....", "온 세상 누구도 내가 지금 어떤 일을 겪고 있는지 모르고, 또 결코 알 수도 없어요……"], ["For perhaps I shall take a knife with me to-day, I can’t make up my mind ...”", "어쩌면 난 오늘 칼을 품고 갈지도 몰라요, 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없으니……\""]], [["And at this “tragic”", "그리고 이 \"비극적인\""], ["phrase Grushenka broke down, hid her face in her hands, flung herself on the sofa pillows, and sobbed like a little child.", "말끝에 그루셴카는 무너지듯 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 소파 베개에 몸을 던지며 어린아이처럼 흐느껴 울었다."]], [["Alyosha got up and went to Rakitin.", "알료샤는 일어나 라키틴에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Misha,”", "\"미샤.\""], ["he said, “don’t be angry.", "그가 말했다. \"화내지 마."], ["She wounded you, but don’t be angry.", "이 여자가 너한테 상처를 주긴 했지만, 화내지 마."], ["You heard what she said just now?", "방금 이 여자가 한 말을 들었잖아?"], ["You mustn’t ask too much of human endurance, one must be merciful.”", "인간의 인내심에 너무 많은 걸 요구해선 안 돼, 자비로워야 하는 거야.\""]], [["Alyosha said this at the instinctive prompting of his heart.", "알료샤는 자기 마음이 본능적으로 시키는 대로 이렇게 말했다."], ["He felt obliged to speak and he turned to Rakitin.", "그는 무언가 말하지 않을 수 없어 라키틴에게로 돌아섰다."], ["If Rakitin had not been there, he would have spoken to the air.", "만일 라키틴이 거기 없었더라면, 그는 허공에 대고라도 말했으리라."], ["But Rakitin looked at him ironically and Alyosha stopped short.", "그러나 라키틴이 그를 빈정대듯 바라보자 알료샤는 말을 뚝 멈추었다."]], [["“You were so primed up with your elder’s teaching last night that now you have to let it off on me, Alexey, man of God!”", "\"어젯밤 네 장로의 가르침을 잔뜩 채워 넣고 와서는, 이제 그걸 나한테 쏟아 내야 직성이 풀리는 모양이군, 하느님의 사람 알렉세이!\""], ["said Rakitin, with a smile of hatred.", "라키틴은 증오에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t laugh, Rakitin, don’t smile, don’t talk of the dead—he was better than any one in the world!”", "\"웃지 마, 라키틴, 비웃지 마, 죽은 분에 대해 함부로 말하지 마—그분은 세상 누구보다 훌륭한 분이었어!\" 알료샤가 목멘 소리로 외쳤다."], ["cried Alyosha, with tears in his voice.", "\"난 심판자로서가 아니라 심판받는 자들 가운데 가장 낮은 자로서 너한테 말한 거야."], ["“I didn’t speak to you as a judge but as the lowest of the judged.", "이 여자에 비하면 내가 다 뭐란 말이야? 난 내 파멸을 찾아 여기 왔고, 비겁하게도 속으로 '그게 무슨 대수랴?'"], ["What am I beside her?", "하고 되뇌었어."], ["I came here seeking my ruin, and said to myself, ‘What does it matter?’ in my cowardliness, but she, after five years in torment, as soon as any one says a word from the heart to her—it makes her forget everything, forgive everything, in her tears!", "그런데 이 여자는, 오 년을 고통 속에 보낸 끝에, 누군가 가슴에서 우러난 한마디만 건네도—그 한마디에 모든 걸 잊고, 모든 걸 용서하며 눈물짓는 거야!"], ["The man who has wronged her has come back, he sends for her and she forgives him everything, and hastens joyfully to meet him and she won’t take a knife with her.", "자기를 농락했던 남자가 돌아와 사람을 보내 부르니, 이 여자는 그에게 모든 걸 용서하고 기쁘게 그를 맞으러 서둘러 가면서도 칼은 품고 가지 않겠다는 거야."], ["She won’t!", "안 품을 거라고!"], ["No, I am not like that.", "아니, 난 그렇지 못해."], ["I don’t know whether you are, Misha, but I am not like that.", "너는 어떤지 모르겠다, 미샤, 하지만 난 그렇지 못해. 이건 내게 하나의 가르침이야……"], ["It’s a lesson to me.... She is more loving than we....", "이 여자는 우리보다 더 사랑할 줄 아는 사람이야……"], ["Have you heard her speak before of what she has just told us?", "너는 이 여자가 방금 우리한테 들려준 이야기를 전에도 들은 적이 있어?"], ["No, you haven’t; if you had, you’d have understood her long ago ...", "아니, 없을 거야. 들었더라면 진작 이 여자를 이해했을 테니까……"], ["and the person insulted the day before yesterday must forgive her, too!", "그리고 그제 모욕당한 그 사람도 이 여자를 용서해야 해!"], ["She will, when she knows ...", "알게 되면 용서할 거야……"], ["and she shall know....", "그리고 그녀는 알게 될 거야……"], ["This soul is not yet at peace with itself, one must be tender with it ...", "이 영혼은 아직 스스로와 화해하지 못했어, 다정하게 대해 줘야 해……"], ["there may be a treasure in that soul....”", "그 영혼 속에 보물이 있을지도 몰라……\""]], [["Alyosha stopped, because he caught his breath.", "알료샤는 숨이 가빠 말을 멈추었다."], ["In spite of his ill-humor Rakitin looked at him with astonishment.", "못마땅한 심기에도 불구하고 라키틴은 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["He had never expected such a tirade from the gentle Alyosha.", "그는 온순한 알료샤에게서 그런 일장 연설이 나오리라곤 미처 예상치 못했던 것이다."]], [["“She’s found some one to plead her cause!", "\"이 여자가 자기 변호를 해 줄 사람을 하나 찾았군!"], ["Why, are you in love with her?", "아니, 당신 이 여자한테 반한 거요?"], ["Agrafena Alexandrovna, our monk’s really in love with you, you’ve made a conquest!”", "아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 우리 수도사가 정말로 당신한테 반했소, 당신이 그를 정복한 거요!\""], ["he cried, with a coarse laugh.", "그는 천박하게 웃으며 외쳤다."]], [["Grushenka lifted her head from the pillow and looked at Alyosha with a tender smile shining on her tear-stained face.", "그루셴카는 베개에서 고개를 들고, 눈물에 젖은 얼굴에 다정한 미소를 빛내며 알료샤를 바라보았다."]], [["“Let him alone, Alyosha, my cherub; you see what he is, he is not a person for you to speak to.", "\"내버려 둬요, 알료샤, 나의 천사여. 보다시피 저런 인간이니, 당신이 말 섞을 상대가 못 돼요."], ["Mihail Osipovitch,”", "미하일 오시포비치.\""], ["she turned to Rakitin, “I meant to beg your pardon for being rude to you, but now I don’t want to.", "그녀는 라키틴에게로 돌아섰다. \"너한테 무례하게 군 걸 사과하려고 했는데, 이제는 그러고 싶지 않아."], ["Alyosha, come to me, sit down here.”", "알료샤, 이리 와요, 여기 앉아요.\""], ["She beckoned to him with a happy smile.", "그녀는 행복한 미소를 지으며 그를 손짓해 불렀다."], ["“That’s right, sit here.", "\"그래요, 여기 앉아요."], ["Tell me,”", "말해 줘요.\""], ["she shook him by the hand and peeped into his face, smiling, “tell me, do I love that man or not?", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 미소 띤 얼굴로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. \"말해 줘요, 내가 그 남자를 사랑하는 거예요, 아닌 거예요?"], ["the man who wronged me, do I love him or not?", "나를 농락한 그 남자를, 내가 사랑하는 거예요, 아니에요?"], ["Before you came, I lay here in the dark, asking my heart whether I loved him.", "당신이 오기 전에 난 여기 어둠 속에 누워, 내 마음에게 그를 사랑하는지 묻고 있었어요."], ["Decide for me, Alyosha, the time has come, it shall be as you say.", "날 위해 결정해 줘요, 알료샤, 때가 왔어요, 당신이 말하는 대로 따를게요."], ["Am I to forgive him or not?”", "그를 용서해야 하나요, 말아야 하나요?\""]], [["“But you have forgiven him already,”", "\"하지만 당신은 이미 그를 용서했잖아요.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I really have forgiven him,”", "\"그래요, 난 정말로 그를 용서했어요.\""], ["Grushenka murmured thoughtfully.", "그루셴카가 생각에 잠겨 중얼거렸다."], ["“What an abject heart! To my abject heart!”", "\"어쩌면 이렇게 비굴한 마음일까!"], ["She snatched up a glass from the table, emptied it at a gulp, lifted it in the air and flung it on the floor.", "이 비굴한 내 마음을 위하여!\" 그녀는 탁자에서 잔을 낚아채 단숨에 비우더니, 허공으로 치켜들었다가 바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."], ["The glass broke with a crash.", "잔은 요란한 소리를 내며 깨졌다."], ["A little cruel line came into her smile.", "약간 잔인한 주름이 그녀의 미소에 어렸다."]], [["“Perhaps I haven’t forgiven him, though,”", "\"하긴 어쩌면 난 그를 용서하지 않은 건지도 몰라.\""], ["she said, with a sort of menace in her voice, and she dropped her eyes to the ground as though she were talking to herself.", "그녀는 목소리에 어떤 위협 같은 것을 담아 말하더니, 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 눈을 바닥으로 떨구었다."], ["“Perhaps my heart is only getting ready to forgive.", "\"어쩌면 내 마음은 이제 막 용서할 채비를 하고 있을 뿐인지도 몰라."], ["I shall struggle with my heart.", "난 내 마음과 싸울 거예요."], ["You see, Alyosha, I’ve grown to love my tears in these five years....", "있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 이 오 년 동안 내 눈물을 사랑하게 됐어요……"], ["Perhaps I only love my resentment, not him ...”", "어쩌면 난 그가 아니라 내 원한을 사랑하는 건지도 몰라요……\""]], [["“Well, I shouldn’t care to be in his shoes,”", "\"흥, 난 그자 처지가 되고 싶진 않군.\""], ["hissed Rakitin.", "라키틴이 쉭쉭거리듯 내뱉었다."]], [["“Well, you won’t be, Rakitin, you’ll never be in his shoes.", "\"그래, 넌 그렇게 안 될 거야, 라키틴, 넌 결코 그자 처지가 못 돼."], ["You shall black my shoes, Rakitin, that’s the place you are fit for.", "넌 내 구두나 닦으면 돼, 라키틴, 그게 너한테 어울리는 자리지."], ["You’ll never get a woman like me ...", "넌 나 같은 여자는 결코 얻지 못할 거야……"], ["and he won’t either, perhaps ...”", "그리고 어쩌면 그도 못 얻겠지……\""]], [["“Won’t he?", "\"못 얻는다고?"], ["Then why are you dressed up like that?”", "그럼 왜 그렇게 차려입었소?\""], ["said Rakitin, with a venomous sneer.", "라키틴이 독기 어린 비웃음을 띠며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t taunt me with dressing up, Rakitin, you don’t know all that is in my heart!", "\"차려입었다고 날 비웃지 마, 라키틴, 넌 내 가슴속에 든 걸 다 알지 못해! 내가 이 단장한 차림을 벗어 던지기로 마음먹으면, 지금 당장, 이 순간이라도 벗어 던질 거야.\""], ["If I choose to tear off my finery, I’ll tear it off at once, this minute,”", "그녀는 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"이 단장이 무엇을 위한 건지 넌 모를 거야, 라키틴!"], ["she cried in a resonant voice.", "어쩌면 난 그를 만나 이렇게 말할지도 몰라."], ["“You don’t know what that finery is for, Rakitin!", "'당신은 전에 내가 이런 모습인 걸 본 적 있어요?'"], ["Perhaps I shall see him and say: ‘Have you ever seen me look like this before?’ He left me a thin, consumptive cry-baby of seventeen.", "그는 비쩍 마르고 폐병이나 앓는, 울보 같은 열일곱 살짜리 계집애를 버리고 떠났지. 난 그의 곁에 앉아 그를 홀리고 애를 태워 줄 거야."], ["I’ll sit by him, fascinate him and work him up.", "'지금 내 모습이 어때 보여요?' 그에게 이렇게 말할 테야."], ["‘Do you see what I am like now?’ I’ll say to him; ‘well, and that’s enough for you, my dear sir, there’s many a slip twixt the cup and the lip!’ That may be what the finery is for, Rakitin.”", "'자, 그걸로 충분하겠지요, 그런데 친애하는 양반, 잔과 입술 사이에서도 미끄러지는 일은 허다하답니다!' 어쩌면 이 단장은 그걸 위한 건지도 몰라, 라키틴.\""], ["Grushenka finished with a malicious laugh.", "그루셴카는 악의에 찬 웃음으로 말을 맺었다."], ["“I’m violent and resentful, Alyosha, I’ll tear off my finery, I’ll destroy my beauty, I’ll scorch my face, slash it with a knife, and turn beggar.", "\"난 난폭하고 앙심이 깊어요, 알료샤, 난 이 단장을 벗어 던지고, 내 아름다움을 망가뜨리고, 내 얼굴을 지지고, 칼로 그어 거지가 될 거예요."], ["If I choose, I won’t go anywhere now to see any one.", "마음만 먹으면 이제 누구를 보러 어디로도 가지 않을 거예요."], ["If I choose, I’ll send Kuzma back all he has ever given me, to-morrow, and all his money and I’ll go out charing for the rest of my life.", "마음만 먹으면 쿠지마가 내게 준 모든 걸 그에게 돌려보낼 거예요, 내일이라도, 그의 돈도 죄다 돌려주고, 남은 평생 남의 집 허드렛일이나 하러 다닐 거라고요."], ["You think I wouldn’t do it, Rakitin, that I would not dare to do it?", "내가 그러지 못할 거라고 생각하지, 라키틴, 내가 그럴 엄두도 못 낼 거라고?"], ["I would, I would, I could do it directly, only don’t exasperate me ...", "난 할 거야, 할 거라고, 당장이라도 할 수 있어, 다만 날 더 약 올리지나 마……"], ["and I’ll send him about his business, I’ll snap my fingers in his face, he shall never see me again!”", "그러고는 그를 쫓아내 버릴 거야, 그의 면전에 손가락을 튕겨 줄 테니, 그는 다시는 날 보지 못할 거야!\""]], [["She uttered the last words in an hysterical scream, but broke down again, hid her face in her hands, buried it in the pillow and shook with sobs.", "그녀는 마지막 말을 신경질적인 비명처럼 내질렀으나, 다시 무너지듯 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 베개에 파묻은 채 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었다."]], [["Rakitin got up.", "라키틴이 일어섰다."]], [["“It’s time we were off,”", "\"이제 갈 시간이오.\""], ["he said, “it’s late, we shall be shut out of the monastery.”", "그가 말했다. \"늦었소, 수도원 문이 닫혀 우린 못 들어가게 될 거요.\""]], [["Grushenka leapt up from her place.", "그루셴카는 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Surely you don’t want to go, Alyosha!”", "\"설마 가려는 건 아니지요, 알료샤!\""], ["she cried, in mournful surprise.", "그녀가 슬픔에 찬 놀라움으로 외쳤다."], ["“What are you doing to me?", "\"나한테 무슨 짓을 하는 거예요?"], ["You’ve stirred up my feeling, tortured me, and now you’ll leave me to face this night alone!”", "내 마음을 휘저어 놓고, 날 괴롭혀 놓고, 이제 이 밤을 혼자 견디라고 날 두고 가려는 거예요!\""]], [["“He can hardly spend the night with you!", "\"이 사람이 당신과 함께 밤을 보낼 수야 없지!"], ["Though if he wants to, let him!", "하긴 그러고 싶다면야, 그렇게 하라지!"], ["I’ll go alone,”", "난 혼자 가겠소.\""], ["Rakitin scoffed jeeringly.", "라키틴이 비아냥대듯 조롱했다."]], [["“Hush, evil tongue!”", "\"닥쳐, 못된 혓바닥아!\""], ["Grushenka cried angrily at him; “you never said such words to me as he has come to say.”", "그루셴카가 그에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"넌 이분이 와서 해 준 그런 말을 내게 한마디도 해 준 적이 없어.\""]], [["“What has he said to you so special?”", "\"이 사람이 당신한테 무슨 그리 특별한 말을 했다는 거요?\""], ["asked Rakitin irritably.", "라키틴이 짜증스레 물었다."]], [["“I can’t say, I don’t know.", "\"말할 수 없어, 나도 몰라."], ["I don’t know what he said to me, it went straight to my heart; he has wrung my heart....", "이분이 내게 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠지만, 그건 곧장 내 가슴으로 파고들었어. 이분이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰단 말이야……"], ["He is the first, the only one who has pitied me, that’s what it is.", "이분은 나를 가엾이 여겨 준 첫 번째, 단 한 사람이야, 바로 그거라고."], ["Why did you not come before, you angel?”", "왜 진작 오지 않았어요, 천사 같은 분?\""], ["She fell on her knees before him as though in a sudden frenzy.", "그녀는 갑자기 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["“I’ve been waiting all my life for some one like you, I knew that some one like you would come and forgive me.", "\"난 평생 당신 같은 사람을 기다려 왔어요, 당신 같은 사람이 와서 날 용서해 주리라는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["I believed that, nasty as I am, some one would really love me, not only with a shameful love!”", "난 믿었어요, 내가 이토록 못된 인간이라도, 누군가 정말로 날 사랑해 주리라는 걸, 부끄러운 사랑만이 아닌 사랑으로요!\""]], [["“What have I done to you?”", "\"내가 당신한테 무얼 해 줬다고요?\""], ["answered Alyosha, bending over her with a tender smile, and gently taking her by the hands; “I only gave you an onion, nothing but a tiny little onion, that was all!”", "알료샤가 다정한 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 몸을 숙이고 가만히 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 대답했다. \"난 그저 당신한테 양파 한 알을 주었을 뿐이에요, 자그마한 양파 한 알, 그게 다예요!\""]], [["He was moved to tears himself as he said it.", "이렇게 말하면서 그 자신도 눈물이 솟구쳤다."], ["At that moment there was a sudden noise in the passage, some one came into the hall.", "바로 그 순간 복도에서 갑자기 소란한 소리가 나며 누군가 현관으로 들어섰다."], ["Grushenka jumped up, seeming greatly alarmed.", "그루셴카는 몹시 놀란 듯 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Fenya ran noisily into the room, crying out:", "페냐가 요란하게 방으로 뛰어들며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Mistress, mistress darling, a messenger has galloped up,”", "\"아씨, 사랑하는 아씨, 심부름꾼이 말을 달려 왔어요.\""], ["she cried, breathless and joyful.", "그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며 기쁨에 차서 외쳤다."], ["“A carriage from Mokroe for you, Timofey the driver, with three horses, they are just putting in fresh horses....", "\"모크로예에서 아씨한테 마차가 왔어요, 마부 티모페이가 말 세 필을 끌고요, 지금 막 새 말로 갈아매고 있어요……"], ["A letter, here’s the letter, mistress.”", "편지예요, 여기 편지가 왔어요, 아씨.\""]], [["A letter was in her hand and she waved it in the air all the while she talked.", "손에 편지가 들려 있었고, 그녀는 이야기하는 내내 그것을 허공에 흔들어 댔다."], ["Grushenka snatched the letter from her and carried it to the candle.", "그루셴카는 그녀에게서 편지를 낚아채 촛불 곁으로 가져갔다."], ["It was only a note, a few lines.", "그것은 그저 몇 줄짜리 짧은 쪽지였다."], ["She read it in one instant.", "그녀는 단숨에 그것을 읽었다."]], [["“He has sent for me,”", "\"그가 날 부르고 있어.\""], ["she cried, her face white and distorted, with a wan smile; “he whistles!", "그녀는 핼쑥하게 일그러진 얼굴에 파리한 미소를 띠며 외쳤다. \"휘파람을 부는구나!"], ["Crawl back, little dog!”", "기어 돌아가라, 강아지야!\""]], [["But only for one instant she stood as though hesitating; suddenly the blood rushed to her head and sent a glow to her cheeks.", "그러나 그녀는 단 한순간 망설이듯 서 있었을 뿐이었다. 별안간 피가 머리로 솟구쳐 두 뺨에 홍조가 번졌다."]], [["“I will go,”", "\"갈 거야.\""], ["she cried; “five years of my life!", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"내 인생 오 년!"], ["Good-by!", "잘 있어요!"], ["Good-by, Alyosha, my fate is sealed.", "잘 있어요, 알료샤, 내 운명은 정해졌어요."], ["Go, go, leave me all of you, don’t let me see you again!", "가요, 가, 다들 날 두고 떠나요, 다시는 내 모습을 보이지 않을게요!"], ["Grushenka is flying to a new life....", "그루셴카는 새로운 삶을 향해 날아가요……"], ["Don’t you remember evil against me either, Rakitin.", "너도 내게 악감정 품지 마, 라키틴."], ["I may be going to my death! Ugh!", "난 어쩌면 죽으러 가는 건지도 몰라!"], ["I feel as though I were drunk!”", "어휴! 꼭 취한 것만 같아!\""]], [["She suddenly left them and ran into her bedroom.", "그녀는 별안간 그들을 두고 자기 침실로 달려 들어갔다."]], [["“Well, she has no thoughts for us now!”", "\"이런, 이제 우리 따위는 안중에도 없군!\""], ["grumbled Rakitin.", "라키틴이 투덜거렸다."], ["“Let’s go, or we may hear that feminine shriek again.", "\"갑시다, 안 그러면 저 여자의 비명을 또 듣게 될지도 모르니."], ["I am sick of all these tears and cries.”", "이놈의 눈물이며 울음소리에 진절머리가 나는군.\""]], [["Alyosha mechanically let himself be led out.", "알료샤는 기계적으로 이끌려 밖으로 나갔다."], ["In the yard stood a covered cart.", "마당에는 포장마차 한 대가 서 있었다."], ["Horses were being taken out of the shafts, men were running to and fro with a lantern.", "사람들이 말들을 채에서 풀어내고 있었고, 등불을 든 자들이 이리저리 분주히 오갔다."], ["Three fresh horses were being led in at the open gate.", "열린 문으로 새 말 세 필이 끌려 들어오고 있었다."], ["But when Alyosha and Rakitin reached the bottom of the steps, Grushenka’s bedroom window was suddenly opened and she called in a ringing voice after Alyosha:", "그러나 알료샤와 라키틴이 층계 아래에 다다랐을 때, 그루셴카의 침실 창문이 갑자기 열리더니 그녀가 낭랑한 목소리로 알료샤를 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“Alyosha, give my greetings to your brother Mitya and tell him not to remember evil against me, though I have brought him misery.", "\"알료샤, 당신 형 미탸한테 내 인사를 전해 주고, 내가 그에게 비참함을 안겨 주긴 했어도 날 나쁘게 기억하진 말라고 전해 줘요. 그리고 또 내 말이라며 이렇게도 전해 줘요."], ["And tell him, too, in my words: ‘Grushenka has fallen to a scoundrel, and not to you, noble heart.’ And add, too, that Grushenka loved him only one hour, only one short hour she loved him—so let him remember that hour all his life—say, ‘Grushenka tells you to!’”", "'그루셴카는 한낱 악당의 차지가 됐을 뿐, 고결한 마음을 지닌 당신의 차지가 되지는 못했어요.' 그리고 이것도 덧붙여 줘요, 그루셴카는 그를 단 한 시간 사랑했노라고, 단 한 시간, 그 짧은 한 시간을 사랑했으니, 그 한 시간을 평생토록 기억하라고요—'그루셴카가 당신에게 그러라고 했다'고 말해 줘요!\""]], [["She ended in a voice full of sobs.", "그녀는 흐느낌으로 가득 찬 목소리로 말을 맺었다."], ["The window was shut with a slam.", "창문이 쾅 하고 닫혔다."]], [["“H’m, h’m!”", "\"흠, 흠!\""], ["growled Rakitin, laughing, “she murders your brother Mitya and then tells him to remember it all his life!", "라키틴이 웃으며 으르렁댔다. \"당신 형 미탸를 죽여 놓고는 그걸 평생 기억하라니!"], ["What ferocity!”", "어쩌면 저리도 표독스러울까!\""]], [["Alyosha made no reply, he seemed not to have heard.", "알료샤는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 마치 그 말을 듣지 못한 듯했다."], ["He walked fast beside Rakitin as though in a terrible hurry.", "그는 끔찍이도 다급한 사람처럼 라키틴 곁에서 빠르게 걸었다."], ["He was lost in thought and moved mechanically.", "그는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 기계적으로 움직였다."], ["Rakitin felt a sudden twinge as though he had been touched on an open wound.", "라키틴은 갑자기 벌어진 상처를 건드리기라도 한 듯한 따끔한 통증을 느꼈다."], ["He had expected something quite different by bringing Grushenka and Alyosha together.", "그는 그루셴카와 알료샤를 맺어 줌으로써 전혀 다른 무언가를 기대했었다."], ["Something very different from what he had hoped for had happened.", "그가 바라던 것과는 사뭇 다른 일이 벌어진 것이다."]], [["“He is a Pole, that officer of hers,”", "\"저 여자 장교라는 자는 폴란드인이오.\""], ["he began again, restraining himself; “and indeed he is not an officer at all now.", "그는 자제하며 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"게다가 이제는 사실 장교도 뭣도 아니지."], ["He served in the customs in Siberia, somewhere on the Chinese frontier, some puny little beggar of a Pole, I expect.", "시베리아 어딘가 중국 국경 근처에서 세관 일을 했다는데, 짐작건대 비쩍 마른 보잘것없는 폴란드인 거지나 다름없는 자일 거요."], ["Lost his job, they say.", "일자리도 잃었다더군."], ["He’s heard now that Grushenka’s saved a little money, so he’s turned up again—that’s the explanation of the mystery.”", "이제 그루셴카가 돈푼깨나 모았다는 소문을 듣고 다시 나타난 거지—그게 그 수수께끼의 진상이오.\""]], [["Again Alyosha seemed not to hear.", "이번에도 알료샤는 듣지 못한 듯했다."], ["Rakitin could not control himself.", "라키틴은 자신을 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["“Well, so you’ve saved the sinner?”", "\"흥, 그래, 죄인을 구원하셨나?\""], ["he laughed spitefully.", "그가 악의에 차서 웃었다."], ["“Have you turned the Magdalene into the true path?", "\"막달라 마리아를 참된 길로 돌려놓으셨나?"], ["Driven out the seven devils, eh?", "일곱 마귀를 쫓아냈나, 어?"], ["So you see the miracles you were looking out for just now have come to pass!”", "자, 보시오, 방금 당신이 학수고대하던 그 기적이 일어났구려!\""]], [["“Hush, Rakitin,”", "\"그만해, 라키틴.\""], ["Alyosha answered with an aching heart.", "알료샤가 아픈 마음으로 대답했다."]], [["“So you despise me now for those twenty-five roubles?", "\"그래, 그 이십오 루블 때문에 이제 나를 경멸하는 건가?"], ["I’ve sold my friend, you think.", "친구를 팔아넘겼다, 그 말이지."], ["But you are not Christ, you know, and I am not Judas.”", "하지만 알다시피 당신은 그리스도가 아니고, 나도 유다가 아니야.\""]], [["“Oh, Rakitin, I assure you I’d forgotten about it,”", "\"아, 라키틴, 정말이지 난 그 일을 잊고 있었어.\""], ["cried Alyosha, “you remind me of it yourself....”", "알료샤가 외쳤다. \"네가 직접 그 일을 일깨워 주는구나……\""]], [["But this was the last straw for Rakitin.", "그러나 이것이 라키틴에게는 마지막 한 방이 되고 말았다."]], [["“Damnation take you all and each of you!”", "\"네놈들 모두, 하나하나 다 뒈져 버려라!\""], ["he cried suddenly, “why the devil did I take you up?", "그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"내가 무슨 빌어먹을 마음으로 너 따위와 어울렸을까?"], ["I don’t want to know you from this time forward.", "이제부터 너 같은 건 알고 싶지도 않다."], ["Go alone, there’s your road!”", "혼자 가라, 저기가 네 갈 길이다!\""]], [["And he turned abruptly into another street, leaving Alyosha alone in the dark.", "그러고는 홱 다른 골목으로 꺾어 들며, 알료샤를 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨 둔 채 떠나 버렸다."], ["Alyosha came out of the town and walked across the fields to the monastery.", "알료샤는 시내를 벗어나 들판을 가로질러 수도원을 향해 걸었다."]], [["# Chapter IV. Cana Of Galilee", "# Chapter IV. Cana Of Galilee"]], [["It was very late, according to the monastery ideas, when Alyosha returned to the hermitage; the door-keeper let him in by a special entrance.", "수도원의 관습으로 따지자면 알료샤가 암자로 돌아온 것은 무척 늦은 시각이었으니, 문지기는 특별한 출입구로 그를 들여보내 주었다."], ["It had struck nine o’clock—the hour of rest and repose after a day of such agitation for all.", "시계가 아홉 시를 친 뒤였는데—모두에게 그토록 격동의 하루가 지나간 끝의 휴식과 안식의 시각이었다."], ["Alyosha timidly opened the door and went into the elder’s cell where his coffin was now standing.", "알료샤는 조심스레 문을 열고 장로의 암실로 들어섰으니, 그곳에는 이제 그의 관이 놓여 있었다."], ["There was no one in the cell but Father Païssy, reading the Gospel in solitude over the coffin, and the young novice Porfiry, who, exhausted by the previous night’s conversation and the disturbing incidents of the day, was sleeping the deep sound sleep of youth on the floor of the other room.", "암실에는 관 곁에서 홀로 복음서를 읽고 있는 파이시 신부와, 어린 수련자 포르피리 외에는 아무도 없었는데, 포르피리는 전날 밤의 담화와 그날의 어수선한 사건들에 지친 나머지 옆방 마룻바닥에서 젊음의 깊고 곤한 잠에 빠져 있었다."], ["Though Father Païssy heard Alyosha come in, he did not even look in his direction.", "파이시 신부는 알료샤가 들어오는 소리를 들었으면서도 그쪽으로 눈길조차 돌리지 않았다."], ["Alyosha turned to the right from the door to the corner, fell on his knees and began to pray.", "알료샤는 문에서 오른쪽 구석으로 돌아가 무릎을 꿇고 기도하기 시작했다."]], [["His soul was overflowing but with mingled feelings; no single sensation stood out distinctly; on the contrary, one drove out another in a slow, continual rotation.", "그의 영혼은 차고 넘쳤으나 그것은 뒤섞인 감정들이었으니, 단 하나의 감각도 뚜렷하게 두드러지지 않았고, 오히려 느리고 끊임없는 순환 속에서 하나가 다른 하나를 밀어내고 있었다."], ["But there was a sweetness in his heart and, strange to say, Alyosha was not surprised at it.", "그러나 그의 마음에는 어떤 감미로움이 깃들어 있었으며, 이상하게도 알료샤는 그것을 두고 놀라지 않았다."], ["Again he saw that coffin before him, the hidden dead figure so precious to him, but the weeping and poignant grief of the morning was no longer aching in his soul.", "그는 또다시 자기 앞의 그 관을, 그토록 소중한 망자의 가려진 형상을 바라보았으나, 아침나절의 그 울음과 사무치는 슬픔은 더 이상 그의 영혼을 쑤시지 않았다."], ["As soon as he came in, he fell down before the coffin as before a holy shrine, but joy, joy was glowing in his mind and in his heart.", "들어서자마자 그는 성스러운 성소 앞에 엎드리듯 관 앞에 엎드렸으나, 기쁨이, 기쁨이 그의 마음과 가슴 속에서 타오르고 있었다."], ["The one window of the cell was open, the air was fresh and cool.", "암실의 단 하나뿐인 창문이 열려 있어 공기는 신선하고 서늘했다."], ["“So the smell must have become stronger, if they opened the window,”", "\"창문을 열어 둔 걸 보면 냄새가 더 심해진 모양이군.\""], ["thought Alyosha.", "하고 알료샤는 생각했다."], ["But even this thought of the smell of corruption, which had seemed to him so awful and humiliating a few hours before, no longer made him feel miserable or indignant.", "그러나 몇 시간 전만 해도 그토록 끔찍하고 모욕적으로 여겨졌던 그 부패의 냄새에 대한 이 생각조차도, 이제는 그를 비참하게 만들거나 분개하게 만들지 않았다."], ["He began quietly praying, but he soon felt that he was praying almost mechanically.", "그는 조용히 기도하기 시작했으나, 곧 자신이 거의 기계적으로 기도하고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Fragments of thought floated through his soul, flashed like stars and went out again at once, to be succeeded by others.", "단편적인 생각들이 그의 영혼을 떠돌며 별처럼 반짝이다가 이내 다시 꺼지고는, 또 다른 생각들에 자리를 내주었다."], ["But yet there was reigning in his soul a sense of the wholeness of things—something steadfast and comforting—and he was aware of it himself.", "그러나 그래도 그의 영혼에는 만물의 온전함에 대한 어떤 감각이—굳건하고 위안을 주는 무언가가—군림하고 있었으며, 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Sometimes he began praying ardently, he longed to pour out his thankfulness and love....", "때때로 그는 열렬히 기도하기 시작했고, 자신의 감사와 사랑을 쏟아 내고 싶어 했다……"]], [["But when he had begun to pray, he passed suddenly to something else, and sank into thought, forgetting both the prayer and what had interrupted it.", "그러나 기도를 시작하자마자 그는 문득 다른 무언가로 옮겨 가, 기도도 그것을 가로막은 것도 다 잊은 채 상념에 잠겨 들었다."], ["He began listening to what Father Païssy was reading, but worn out with exhaustion he gradually began to doze.", "그는 파이시 신부가 읽는 것에 귀를 기울이기 시작했으나, 기진맥진한 탓에 차츰 졸기 시작했다."]], [["“And the third day there was a marriage in Cana of Galilee;”", "\"사흘째 되던 날에 갈릴리 가나에 혼인이 있었으니,\""], ["read Father Païssy.", "하고 파이시 신부가 읽었다."], ["“And the mother of Jesus was there; And both Jesus was called, and his disciples, to the marriage.”", "\"예수의 어머니도 거기 계시고, 예수와 그 제자들도 그 혼인에 청함을 받았더라.\""]], [["“Marriage?", "\"혼인?"], ["What’s that?...", "그게 무엇이지?……"], ["A marriage!”", "혼인이라!\""], ["floated whirling through Alyosha’s mind.", "하는 생각이 알료샤의 머릿속을 빙글빙글 떠돌았다."], ["“There is happiness for her, too....", "\"그녀에게도 행복이 있구나……"], ["She has gone to the feast....", "그녀는 잔치에 갔어……"], ["No, she has not taken the knife....", "아니, 그녀는 칼을 들지 않았어……"], ["That was only a tragic phrase....", "그건 그저 비극적인 말이었을 뿐……"], ["Well ...", "그래……"], ["tragic phrases should be forgiven, they must be.", "비극적인 말은 용서받아야 해, 그래야만 해."], ["Tragic phrases comfort the heart....", "비극적인 말은 마음을 위로해 주니까……"], ["Without them, sorrow would be too heavy for men to bear.", "그것이 없다면 슬픔은 사람이 견디기에 너무 무거울 거야."], ["Rakitin has gone off to the back alley.", "라키틴은 뒷골목으로 가 버렸지."], ["As long as Rakitin broods over his wrongs, he will always go off to the back alley....", "라키틴이 제 억울함을 곱씹는 한, 그는 언제나 뒷골목으로 가 버릴 거야……"], ["But the high road ...", "하지만 큰길은……"], ["The road is wide and straight and bright as crystal, and the sun is at the end of it....", "그 길은 넓고 곧으며 수정처럼 밝고, 그 끝에는 태양이 있어…… 아!……"], ["Ah!... What’s being read?”...", "지금 뭘 읽고 있는 거지?\"……"]], [["“And when they wanted wine, the mother of Jesus saith unto him, They have no wine”", "\"포도주가 떨어진지라 예수의 어머니가 예수에게 이르되, 저들에게 포도주가 없다 하니\"……"], ["... Alyosha heard.", "하고 알료샤는 들었다."]], [["“Ah, yes, I was missing that, and I didn’t want to miss it, I love that passage: it’s Cana of Galilee, the first miracle....", "\"아, 그래, 이 대목을 놓치고 있었는데, 놓치고 싶지 않았어, 이 구절을 나는 사랑하니까. 갈릴리 가나, 첫 번째 기적이야……"], ["Ah, that miracle!", "아, 그 기적!"], ["Ah, that sweet miracle!", "아, 그 감미로운 기적!"], ["It was not men’s grief, but their joy Christ visited, He worked His first miracle to help men’s gladness....", "그리스도께서 찾아오신 것은 사람들의 슬픔이 아니라 그들의 기쁨이었으니, 그분은 사람들의 즐거움을 도우시려고 첫 기적을 행하셨어……"], ["‘He who loves men loves their gladness, too’ ...", "'사람을 사랑하는 자는 그들의 즐거움도 사랑한다'……"], ["He was always repeating that, it was one of his leading ideas....", "그분은 늘 그 말씀을 되풀이하셨고, 그것은 그분의 핵심 사상 가운데 하나였지……"], ["‘There’s no living without joy,’ Mitya says....", "'기쁨 없이는 살아갈 수 없다'고 미탸는 말하지……"], ["Yes, Mitya....", "그래, 미탸……"], ["‘Everything that is true and good is always full of forgiveness,’ he used to say that, too” ...", "'참되고 선한 모든 것은 언제나 용서로 가득 차 있다'고 그분은 그 말씀도 하시곤 했어\"……"]], [["“Jesus saith unto her, Woman, what has it to do with thee or me? Mine hour is not yet come.", "\"예수께서 이르시되, 여자여, 나와 당신이 무슨 상관이 있나이까, 내 때가 아직 이르지 아니하였나이다."]], [["“His mother saith unto the servants, Whatsoever he saith unto you, do it” ...", "그 어머니가 하인들에게 이르되, 그가 너희에게 무슨 말을 하든지 그대로 하라 하니라\"……"]], [["“Do it....", "\"그대로 하라……"], ["Gladness, the gladness of some poor, very poor, people....", "즐거움, 어느 가난한, 무척이나 가난한 사람들의 즐거움이여……"], ["Of course they were poor, since they hadn’t wine enough even at a wedding....", "물론 그들은 가난했지, 혼인 잔치에서조차 포도주가 넉넉하지 못했으니……"], ["The historians write that, in those days, the people living about the Lake of Gennesaret were the poorest that can possibly be imagined ...", "역사가들은 그 시절 게네사렛 호숫가에 살던 사람들이 도무지 상상할 수 없을 만큼 가난한 자들이었다고 쓰고 있어……"], ["and another great heart, that other great being, His Mother, knew that He had come not only to make His great terrible sacrifice.", "그리고 또 하나의 위대한 마음이, 저 또 다른 위대한 존재인 그분의 어머니께서는, 그분이 단지 자신의 위대하고 두려운 희생을 치르려고 오신 것만은 아님을 알고 계셨어."], ["She knew that His heart was open even to the simple, artless merrymaking of some obscure and unlearned people, who had warmly bidden Him to their poor wedding.", "그분의 마음이 그분을 가난한 혼인 잔치에 따뜻이 청한 어느 이름 없고 무지한 사람들의, 그 소박하고 꾸밈없는 흥겨움에까지도 열려 있음을 그분의 어머니는 알고 계셨던 거야. '내 때가 아직 이르지 아니하였나이다.'"], ["‘Mine hour is not yet come,’ He said, with a soft smile (He must have smiled gently to her).", "그분은 부드러운 미소와 함께 그렇게 말씀하셨지(그분은 어머니께 다정하게 미소 지으셨을 거야)."], ["And, indeed, was it to make wine abundant at poor weddings He had come down to earth?", "그리고 정말이지, 가난한 혼인 잔치에 포도주를 넉넉히 하시려고 그분이 이 땅에 내려오셨던가?"], ["And yet He went and did as she asked Him....", "그런데도 그분은 가셔서 어머니가 청하신 대로 행하셨어……"], ["Ah, he is reading again”....", "아, 또 읽고 있구나\"……"]], [["“Jesus saith unto them, Fill the waterpots with water. And they filled them up to the brim.", "\"예수께서 그들에게 이르시되, 항아리에 물을 채우라 하시니, 그들이 아귀까지 채우니라."]], [["“And he saith unto them, Draw out now and bear unto the governor of the feast. And they bare it.", "이에 이르시되, 이제는 떠서 잔치를 맡은 자에게 갖다 주라 하시매, 그들이 갖다 주었더니."]], [["“When the ruler of the feast had tasted the water that was made wine, and knew not whence it was; (but the servants which drew the water knew;) the governor of the feast called the bridegroom,", "잔치를 맡은 자가 포도주가 된 물을 맛보고도 어디서 났는지 알지 못하되(물 떠 온 하인들은 알더라), 잔치를 맡은 자가 신랑을 불러,"]], [["“And saith unto him, Every man at the beginning doth set forth good wine; and when men have well drunk, that which is worse; but thou hast kept the good wine until now.”", "말하되, 사람마다 먼저 좋은 포도주를 내고 취한 후에 낮은 것을 내거늘, 그대는 지금까지 좋은 포도주를 두었도다 하니라.\""]], [["“But what’s this, what’s this?", "\"그런데 이게 뭐지, 이게 뭐야?"], ["Why is the room growing wider?...", "어째서 방이 점점 넓어지는 거지?……"], ["Ah, yes ...", "아, 그래……"], ["It’s the marriage, the wedding ...", "혼인이야, 혼인 잔치……"], ["yes, of course.", "그래, 물론이지."], ["Here are the guests, here are the young couple sitting, and the merry crowd and ...", "여기 손님들이 있고, 여기 젊은 부부가 앉아 있고, 흥겨운 무리들이 있고, 그리고……"], ["Where is the wise governor of the feast?", "잔치를 맡은 지혜로운 자는 어디 있지?"], ["But who is this? Who?", "그런데 이분은 누구지?"], ["Again the walls are receding....", "누구지? 또 벽들이 물러나는구나……"], ["Who is getting up there from the great table?", "저기 큰 식탁에서 일어나는 이는 누구지? 뭐라고!……"], ["What!... He here, too?", "저분도 여기 계신단 말인가?"], ["But he’s in the coffin ...", "하지만 저분은 관 속에 계신데……"], ["but he’s here, too.", "그런데도 여기 계셔."], ["He has stood up, he sees me, he is coming here....", "일어나셨어, 나를 보고 계셔, 이리로 오시는구나……"], ["God!”...", "하느님!\"……"]], [["Yes, he came up to him, to him, he, the little, thin old man, with tiny wrinkles on his face, joyful and laughing softly.", "그렇다, 그분이 그에게로, 바로 그에게로 다가오셨으니, 얼굴에 자잘한 주름이 잡힌 그 작고 여윈 노인이, 기쁨에 젖어 부드럽게 웃으면서 다가오셨다."], ["There was no coffin now, and he was in the same dress as he had worn yesterday sitting with them, when the visitors had gathered about him.", "이제 관은 없었고, 그분은 어제 사람들이 둘러 모였을 때 그들과 함께 앉아 있던 그 옷차림 그대로였다."], ["His face was uncovered, his eyes were shining.", "그분의 얼굴은 가려져 있지 않았고, 눈은 빛나고 있었다."], ["How was this, then?", "그렇다면 이것은 어찌 된 일인가?"], ["He, too, had been called to the feast.", "그분도 잔치에 청함을 받았던 것이다."], ["He, too, at the marriage of Cana in Galilee....", "그분도 갈릴리 가나의 혼인 잔치에……"]], [["“Yes, my dear, I am called, too, called and bidden,”", "\"그렇다, 얘야, 나도 청함을 받았단다, 청함을 받고 부름을 받았어.\""], ["he heard a soft voice saying over him.", "그는 자기 머리 위에서 들려오는 부드러운 목소리를 들었다."], ["“Why have you hidden yourself here, out of sight?", "\"어찌하여 여기 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 숨어 있느냐?"], ["You come and join us too.”", "너도 와서 우리와 함께하려무나.\""]], [["It was his voice, the voice of Father Zossima.", "그것은 그분의 목소리, 조시마 장로의 목소리였다."], ["And it must be he, since he called him!", "그분이 그를 불렀으니, 분명 그분이 틀림없었다!"]], [["The elder raised Alyosha by the hand and he rose from his knees.", "장로가 알료샤의 손을 잡아 일으키니 그는 무릎에서 일어섰다."]], [["“We are rejoicing,”", "\"우리는 기뻐하고 있단다.\""], ["the little, thin old man went on.", "그 작고 여윈 노인이 말을 이었다."], ["“We are drinking the new wine, the wine of new, great gladness; do you see how many guests?", "\"우리는 새 포도주를, 새롭고 위대한 기쁨의 포도주를 마시고 있어. 손님이 얼마나 많은지 보이느냐?"], ["Here are the bride and bridegroom, here is the wise governor of the feast, he is tasting the new wine.", "여기 신부와 신랑이 있고, 여기 잔치를 맡은 지혜로운 자가 있어, 그가 새 포도주를 맛보고 있구나."], ["Why do you wonder at me?", "어찌하여 나를 보고 놀라느냐?"], ["I gave an onion to a beggar, so I, too, am here.", "나는 거지에게 파 한 뿌리를 주었기에, 그래서 나도 여기 있는 거란다."], ["And many here have given only an onion each—only one little onion....", "그리고 여기 있는 많은 이들이 저마다 파 한 뿌리씩만을—오직 하나의 자그마한 파 한 뿌리만을 주었을 뿐이야……"], ["What are all our deeds?", "우리의 행적이란 다 무엇이겠느냐?"], ["And you, my gentle one, you, my kind boy, you too have known how to give a famished woman an onion to-day.", "그리고 너, 나의 온순한 아이야, 너, 나의 착한 아이야, 너도 오늘 굶주린 여인에게 파 한 뿌리를 줄 줄 알았구나."], ["Begin your work, dear one, begin it, gentle one!...", "네 일을 시작하여라, 사랑하는 아이야, 그것을 시작하여라, 온순한 아이야!……"], ["Do you see our Sun, do you see Him?”", "우리의 태양이 보이느냐, 그분이 보이느냐?\""]], [["“I am afraid ...", "\"저는 두렵습니다……"], ["I dare not look,”", "감히 바라보지 못하겠습니다.\""], ["whispered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 속삭였다."]], [["“Do not fear Him.", "\"그분을 두려워하지 말아라."], ["He is terrible in His greatness, awful in His sublimity, but infinitely merciful.", "그분은 그 위대하심으로 두렵고, 그 숭고하심으로 경외로우시나, 한없이 자비로우시단다."], ["He has made Himself like unto us from love and rejoices with us.", "그분은 사랑으로 우리와 같이 되셨고 우리와 함께 기뻐하신단다."], ["He is changing the water into wine that the gladness of the guests may not be cut short.", "그분은 손님들의 즐거움이 끊기지 않도록 물을 포도주로 바꾸고 계셔."], ["He is expecting new guests, He is calling new ones unceasingly for ever and ever....", "그분은 새 손님들을 기다리시며, 새 손님들을 영원무궁토록 끊임없이 부르고 계셔……"], ["There they are bringing new wine.", "저기 그들이 새 포도주를 가져오는구나."], ["Do you see they are bringing the vessels....”", "그들이 그릇들을 가져오는 것이 보이느냐……\""]], [["Something glowed in Alyosha’s heart, something filled it till it ached, tears of rapture rose from his soul....", "알료샤의 가슴 속에서 무언가가 타올랐고, 무언가가 그것을 아릴 만큼 가득 채웠으며, 환희의 눈물이 그의 영혼에서 솟구쳤다……"], ["He stretched out his hands, uttered a cry and waked up.", "그는 두 손을 뻗으며 외마디 소리를 지르고는 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["Again the coffin, the open window, and the soft, solemn, distinct reading of the Gospel.", "다시 관이, 열린 창문이, 그리고 부드럽고 엄숙하며 또렷한 복음서 낭독 소리가 거기 있었다."], ["But Alyosha did not listen to the reading.", "그러나 알료샤는 그 낭독에 귀를 기울이지 않았다."], ["It was strange, he had fallen asleep on his knees, but now he was on his feet, and suddenly, as though thrown forward, with three firm rapid steps he went right up to the coffin.", "이상한 일이었으니, 그는 무릎을 꿇은 채 잠들었으나 이제는 일어서 있었고, 갑자기 앞으로 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 세 걸음의 굳건하고 빠른 발걸음으로 곧장 관으로 다가갔다."], ["His shoulder brushed against Father Païssy without his noticing it.", "그의 어깨가 파이시 신부를 스쳤으나 그는 알아차리지도 못했다."], ["Father Païssy raised his eyes for an instant from his book, but looked away again at once, seeing that something strange was happening to the boy.", "파이시 신부는 책에서 잠시 눈을 들었으나, 이 소년에게 무언가 이상한 일이 벌어지고 있음을 보고는 곧 다시 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["Alyosha gazed for half a minute at the coffin, at the covered, motionless dead man that lay in the coffin, with the ikon on his breast and the peaked cap with the octangular cross, on his head.", "알료샤는 반 분쯤 관을, 관 속에 누운 그 가려진, 미동도 없는 망자를 바라보았으니, 그 망자의 가슴에는 성상이 놓여 있고 머리에는 팔각 십자가가 새겨진 뾰족한 모자가 씌워져 있었다."], ["He had only just been hearing his voice, and that voice was still ringing in his ears.", "그는 방금 전까지 그분의 목소리를 듣고 있었고, 그 목소리는 여전히 그의 귓전에 울리고 있었다."], ["He was listening, still expecting other words, but suddenly he turned sharply and went out of the cell.", "그는 또 다른 말씀을 기대하며 귀를 기울이고 있었으나, 갑자기 홱 돌아서서 암실 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["He did not stop on the steps either, but went quickly down; his soul, overflowing with rapture, yearned for freedom, space, openness.", "그는 층계에서도 멈추지 않고 빠르게 내려갔으니, 환희로 차고 넘치는 그의 영혼은 자유를, 공간을, 탁 트인 것을 갈망하고 있었다."], ["The vault of heaven, full of soft, shining stars, stretched vast and fathomless above him.", "부드럽게 빛나는 별들로 가득한 하늘의 궁륭이 그의 머리 위로 광대하고도 가늠할 길 없이 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["The Milky Way ran in two pale streams from the zenith to the horizon.", "은하수가 천정에서 지평선까지 두 줄기 창백한 흐름으로 흘러내렸다."], ["The fresh, motionless, still night enfolded the earth.", "신선하고 미동도 없는 고요한 밤이 대지를 감싸고 있었다."], ["The white towers and golden domes of the cathedral gleamed out against the sapphire sky.", "대성당의 흰 탑들과 황금빛 둥근 지붕들이 사파이어빛 하늘을 배경으로 반짝였다."], ["The gorgeous autumn flowers, in the beds round the house, were slumbering till morning.", "집 둘레의 화단에 핀 화사한 가을꽃들이 아침까지 잠들어 있었다."], ["The silence of earth seemed to melt into the silence of the heavens.", "대지의 침묵이 하늘의 침묵 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다."], ["The mystery of earth was one with the mystery of the stars....", "대지의 신비가 별들의 신비와 하나였다……"]], [["Alyosha stood, gazed, and suddenly threw himself down on the earth.", "알료샤는 서서 바라보다가, 갑자기 땅 위로 몸을 던졌다."], ["He did not know why he embraced it.", "그는 왜 그것을 끌어안는지 알지 못했다."], ["He could not have told why he longed so irresistibly to kiss it, to kiss it all.", "그는 어째서 그토록 억누를 수 없이 땅에 입 맞추고 싶은지, 온 땅에 입 맞추고 싶은지 말로 설명할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["But he kissed it weeping, sobbing and watering it with his tears, and vowed passionately to love it, to love it for ever and ever.", "그러나 그는 울면서, 흐느끼면서, 자신의 눈물로 그것을 적시며 입을 맞추었고, 그것을 사랑하리라고, 영원무궁토록 그것을 사랑하리라고 열렬히 맹세했다."], ["“Water the earth with the tears of your joy and love those tears,”", "\"네 기쁨의 눈물로 땅을 적시고 그 눈물을 사랑하여라.\""], ["echoed in his soul.", "하는 말이 그의 영혼에서 메아리쳤다."]], [["What was he weeping over?", "그는 무엇을 두고 울고 있었던가?"]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["in his rapture he was weeping even over those stars, which were shining to him from the abyss of space, and “he was not ashamed of that ecstasy.”", "그는 환희에 젖어 저 별들을 두고도 울고 있었으니, 그 별들은 공간의 심연으로부터 그에게 빛을 비추고 있었고, \"그는 그 황홀경을 부끄러워하지 않았다.\""], ["There seemed to be threads from all those innumerable worlds of God, linking his soul to them, and it was trembling all over “in contact with other worlds.”", "하느님의 저 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 세계들로부터 뻗어 나온 실들이 그의 영혼을 그 세계들과 잇고 있는 듯했으며, 그의 영혼은 \"다른 세계들과 닿아\" 온통 떨고 있었다."], ["He longed to forgive every one and for everything, and to beg forgiveness.", "그는 모든 이를, 모든 것을 용서하고 싶었고, 또 용서를 빌고 싶었다."], ["Oh, not for himself, but for all men, for all and for everything.", "오, 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니라 모든 사람을 위해, 모두를 위하고 모든 것을 위해서였다."], ["“And others are praying for me too,”", "\"다른 이들도 나를 위해 기도하고 있어.\""], ["echoed again in his soul.", "하는 말이 그의 영혼에서 또다시 메아리쳤다."], ["But with every instant he felt clearly and, as it were, tangibly, that something firm and unshakable as that vault of heaven had entered into his soul.", "그러나 매 순간 그는, 저 하늘의 궁륭처럼 굳건하고 흔들림 없는 무언가가 자신의 영혼 속으로 들어왔음을 분명히, 말하자면 손에 잡힐 듯 느꼈다."], ["It was as though some idea had seized the sovereignty of his mind—and it was for all his life and for ever and ever.", "마치 어떤 사상이 그의 마음의 주권을 사로잡은 듯했으니—그것은 그의 평생을 위한 것이요 영원무궁토록 이어질 것이었다."], ["He had fallen on the earth a weak boy, but he rose up a resolute champion, and he knew and felt it suddenly at the very moment of his ecstasy.", "그는 연약한 소년으로 땅에 엎드렸으나, 결연한 투사로 일어섰으니, 그는 바로 그 황홀경의 순간에 그것을 문득 알고 또 느꼈다."], ["And never, never, all his life long, could Alyosha forget that minute.", "그리고 알료샤는 평생토록 결코, 결코 그 순간을 잊을 수 없었다."]], [["“Some one visited my soul in that hour,”", "\"그 시각에 누군가 내 영혼을 찾아왔었지.\""], ["he used to say afterwards, with implicit faith in his words.", "하고 그는 훗날 자기 말에 절대적인 믿음을 담아 말하곤 했다."]], [["Within three days he left the monastery in accordance with the words of his elder, who had bidden him “sojourn in the world.”", "사흘이 채 지나지 않아 그는 \"세상에 머물러 살라\"고 명하신 장로의 말씀에 따라 수도원을 떠났다."]], [["# Book VIII. Mitya Chapter I. Kuzma Samsonov", "# Book VIII. Mitya Chapter I. Kuzma Samsonov"]], [["But Dmitri, to whom Grushenka, flying away to a new life, had left her last greetings, bidding him remember the hour of her love for ever, knew nothing of what had happened to her, and was at that moment in a condition of feverish agitation and activity.", "그러나 새 삶을 향해 날아가며 그루셴카가 마지막 인사를 남기고, 자신을 사랑했던 그 시간을 영원히 기억해 달라 당부했던 그 상대 드미트리는, 그녀에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 아무것도 알지 못했고, 바로 그 순간 열병에 들뜬 듯한 흥분과 분주함 속에 빠져 있었다."], ["For the last two days he had been in such an inconceivable state of mind that he might easily have fallen ill with brain fever, as he said himself afterwards.", "지난 이틀 동안 그는 도무지 상상할 수 없을 만큼 심란한 상태였으니, 뒷날 그 자신이 말했듯이, 하마터면 뇌염으로 드러눕고 말았을 정도였다."], ["Alyosha had not been able to find him the morning before, and Ivan had not succeeded in meeting him at the tavern on the same day.", "알료샤는 그 전날 아침 그를 찾아내지 못했고, 이반 역시 같은 날 선술집에서 그를 만나는 데 성공하지 못했다."], ["The people at his lodgings, by his orders, concealed his movements.", "그의 하숙집 사람들은 그의 지시에 따라 그의 행적을 숨겼다."]], [["He had spent those two days literally rushing in all directions, “struggling with his destiny and trying to save himself,”", "그는 그 이틀을 글자 그대로 사방으로 내달리며 보냈으니, 뒷날 그 자신이 표현한 대로 \"운명과 씨름하며 자기 자신을 구하려 애쓰며\""], ["as he expressed it himself afterwards, and for some hours he even made a dash out of the town on urgent business, terrible as it was to him to lose sight of Grushenka for a moment.", "지냈고, 그루셴카에게서 한순간이라도 눈을 떼는 것이 그토록 끔찍한 일이었음에도, 몇 시간 동안은 화급한 용무로 읍내 밖까지 달려가기도 했다."], ["All this was explained afterwards in detail, and confirmed by documentary evidence; but for the present we will only note the most essential incidents of those two terrible days immediately preceding the awful catastrophe, that broke so suddenly upon him.", "이 모든 것은 뒷날 상세히 밝혀졌고 문서 증거로도 확인되었으나, 지금으로서는 다만, 그토록 갑작스레 그를 덮친 끔찍한 파국 직전의 그 무시무시한 이틀 동안 벌어진 가장 본질적인 사건들만을 짚어 두기로 하겠다."]], [["Though Grushenka had, it is true, loved him for an hour, genuinely and sincerely, yet she tortured him sometimes cruelly and mercilessly.", "그루셴카가 한 시간만큼은 진정으로, 또 진심으로 그를 사랑한 것은 사실이었으나, 그녀는 때로 잔인하고 무자비하게 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["The worst of it was that he could never tell what she meant to do.", "무엇보다 견디기 힘든 것은, 그녀가 무엇을 하려는 것인지 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 점이었다."], ["To prevail upon her by force or kindness was also impossible: she would yield to nothing.", "힘으로든 다정함으로든 그녀를 꺾는 것 또한 불가능했으니, 그녀는 어떤 것에도 굴하지 않았다."], ["She would only have become angry and turned away from him altogether, he knew that well already.", "그저 화를 내고 그에게서 아주 등을 돌려 버릴 뿐이라는 것을, 그는 이미 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He suspected, quite correctly, that she, too, was passing through an inward struggle, and was in a state of extraordinary indecision, that she was making up her mind to something, and unable to determine upon it.", "그는 아주 정확하게도, 그녀 역시 내면의 갈등을 겪고 있으며 유달리 우유부단한 상태에 빠져, 무언가를 마음먹으려 하면서도 끝내 결단을 내리지 못하고 있다고 짐작했다."], ["And so, not without good reason, he divined, with a sinking heart, that at moments she must simply hate him and his passion.", "그리하여 그는, 까닭이 없지 않게, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 심정으로, 순간순간 그녀가 자기와 자기의 열정을 그저 미워할 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["And so, perhaps, it was, but what was distressing Grushenka he did not understand.", "어쩌면 정말 그러했을지도 모른다. 그러나 무엇이 그루셴카를 괴롭히고 있는지 그는 이해하지 못했다."], ["For him the whole tormenting question lay between him and Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그에게 이 모든 괴로운 문제는 자신과 표도르 파블로비치 사이에 놓여 있었다."]], [["Here, we must note, by the way, one certain fact: he was firmly persuaded that Fyodor Pavlovitch would offer, or perhaps had offered, Grushenka lawful wedlock, and did not for a moment believe that the old voluptuary hoped to gain his object for three thousand roubles.", "여기서 우리는, 지나가는 김에, 한 가지 분명한 사실을 짚어 두어야 한다. 그는 표도르 파블로비치가 그루셴카에게 정식 결혼을 제안하리라고, 어쩌면 이미 제안했으리라고 굳게 믿었으며, 그 늙은 호색한이 삼천 루블로 제 목적을 이룰 수 있으리라 기대하고 있다고는 한순간도 믿지 않았다."], ["Mitya had reached this conclusion from his knowledge of Grushenka and her character.", "미탸가 이런 결론에 이른 것은 그루셴카와 그녀의 성격을 알기 때문이었다."], ["That was how it was that he could believe at times that all Grushenka’s uneasiness rose from not knowing which of them to choose, which was most to her advantage.", "그래서 그는, 그루셴카의 온갖 불안이 둘 가운데 누구를 택해야 할지, 누가 자기에게 가장 이로운지 알지 못하는 데서 비롯된 것이라고 때때로 믿을 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["Strange to say, during those days it never occurred to him to think of the approaching return of the “officer,” that is, of the man who had been such a fatal influence in Grushenka’s life, and whose arrival she was expecting with such emotion and dread.", "이상하게도, 그 며칠 동안 그는 다가오는 \"장교\"의 귀환, 다시 말해 그루셴카의 삶에 그토록 치명적인 영향을 끼쳤고, 그녀가 그토록 동요와 두려움 속에서 그 도착을 기다리고 있던 그 사내에 관해서는 단 한 번도 생각이 미치지 않았다."], ["It is true that of late Grushenka had been very silent about it.", "근래 들어 그루셴카가 그 일에 관해 매우 입을 다물어 온 것은 사실이었다."], ["Yet he was perfectly aware of a letter she had received a month ago from her seducer, and had heard of it from her own lips.", "그렇지만 그는 한 달 전 그녀가 자신을 유혹했던 그 사내로부터 편지 한 통을 받았다는 사실을 똑똑히 알고 있었고, 그것을 그녀의 입으로 직접 들었다."], ["He partly knew, too, what the letter contained.", "그 편지에 무엇이 적혀 있었는지도 그는 어느 정도 알았다."], ["In a moment of spite Grushenka had shown him that letter, but to her astonishment he attached hardly any consequence to it.", "그루셴카가 홧김에 그 편지를 그에게 보여 준 적이 있었으나, 그녀가 놀랍게도 그는 그것을 거의 대수롭지 않게 여겼다."], ["It would be hard to say why this was.", "어째서 그러했는지는 말하기 어렵다."], ["Perhaps, weighed down by all the hideous horror of his struggle with his own father for this woman, he was incapable of imagining any danger more terrible, at any rate for the time.", "어쩌면 이 여자를 두고 친아버지와 벌이는 흉측하고 끔찍한 싸움에 짓눌려, 그로서는 적어도 당분간은 그보다 더 무서운 위험이라곤 상상할 수조차 없었던 것이리라."], ["He simply did not believe in a suitor who suddenly turned up again after five years’ disappearance, still less in his speedy arrival. Moreover, in the “officer’s”", "그는 오 년 동안이나 종적을 감추었다가 느닷없이 다시 나타난 구혼자라는 것을 도무지 믿지 않았고, 그가 곧 도착하리라는 것은 더더욱 믿지 않았다."], ["first letter which had been shown to Mitya, the possibility of his new rival’s visit was very vaguely suggested.", "게다가 미탸에게 보여 준 그 \"장교\"의 첫 편지에서는 새 연적이 찾아올 가능성이 매우 모호하게만 암시되어 있었다."], ["The letter was very indefinite, high-flown, and full of sentimentality.", "그 편지는 더없이 막연하고, 거창하며, 감상으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["It must be noted that Grushenka had concealed from him the last lines of the letter, in which his return was alluded to more definitely.", "한 가지 짚어 둘 것은, 그루셴카가 그에게 편지의 마지막 줄들을 감추었다는 점인데, 그 부분에서는 그의 귀환이 한층 분명하게 언급되어 있었다."], ["He had, besides, noticed at that moment, he remembered afterwards, a certain involuntary proud contempt for this missive from Siberia on Grushenka’s face.", "그뿐 아니라 그는 그 순간—뒷날 기억해 낸 바로는—그루셴카의 얼굴에 시베리아에서 온 그 편지를 향한 어떤 무심결의 오만한 경멸이 떠올라 있는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Grushenka told him nothing of what had passed later between her and this rival; so that by degrees he had completely forgotten the officer’s existence.", "그루셴카는 그 후 자기와 이 연적 사이에 오간 일에 관해서는 그에게 아무것도 말하지 않았다. 그리하여 그는 차츰 그 장교의 존재 자체를 까맣게 잊어버리고 말았다."]], [["He felt that whatever might come later, whatever turn things might take, his final conflict with Fyodor Pavlovitch was close upon him, and must be decided before anything else.", "그는 뒷날 무슨 일이 닥치든, 일이 어떻게 굴러가든, 표도르 파블로비치와의 마지막 대결이 코앞에 닥쳐 있으며, 그것이 무엇보다 먼저 결판나야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["With a sinking heart he was expecting every moment Grushenka’s decision, always believing that it would come suddenly, on the impulse of the moment.", "가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 심정으로 그는 그루셴카의 결단을 시시각각 기다렸으니, 그것이 어느 순간의 충동에 따라 느닷없이 찾아오리라고 줄곧 믿었다."], ["All of a sudden she would say to him: “Take me, I’m yours for ever,”", "별안간 그녀가 그에게 이렇게 말하리라. \"저를 데려가세요, 저는 영원히 당신 거예요.\""], ["and it would all be over.", "그러면 모든 것이 끝나는 것이다."], ["He would seize her and bear her away at once to the ends of the earth.", "그는 당장 그녀를 붙들어 땅끝까지라도 데리고 달아나리라."], ["Oh, then he would bear her away at once, as far, far away as possible; to the farthest end of Russia, if not of the earth, then he would marry her, and settle down with her incognito, so that no one would know anything about them, there, here, or anywhere.", "오, 그때는 곧장 그녀를 데리고 멀리, 멀리, 될 수 있는 한 멀리 떠나리라. 땅끝까지는 아닐지라도 러시아의 가장 먼 변두리까지라도 가서, 그녀와 혼인하고 아무도 모르게 자리 잡으리니, 그리하여 그곳에서든 이곳에서든 그 어디에서든 아무도 그들에 관해 알지 못하리라."], ["Then, oh, then, a new life would begin at once!", "그러면, 오, 그때는, 새 삶이 당장 시작되리라!"]], [["Of this different, reformed and “virtuous”", "이 다르고, 개과천선한, \"덕스러운\""], ["life (“it must, it must be virtuous”) he dreamed feverishly at every moment.", "삶을(\"그것은 반드시, 반드시 덕스러워야 한다\") 그는 매 순간 열에 들뜬 듯 꿈꾸었다."], ["He thirsted for that reformation and renewal.", "그는 그 개심과 갱생을 목말라했다."], ["The filthy morass, in which he had sunk of his own free will, was too revolting to him, and, like very many men in such cases, he put faith above all in change of place.", "제 발로 빠져든 그 더러운 수렁이 너무도 역겨웠고, 이런 처지에 놓인 숱한 사람들이 그러하듯, 그는 무엇보다 장소를 바꾸는 데 믿음을 걸었다."], ["If only it were not for these people, if only it were not for these circumstances, if only he could fly away from this accursed place—he would be altogether regenerated, would enter on a new path.", "이 사람들만 아니라면, 이 정황만 아니라면, 이 저주받은 곳에서 날아가 버릴 수만 있다면—그는 완전히 거듭나, 새로운 길로 들어서리라."], ["That was what he believed in, and what he was yearning for.", "바로 그것이 그가 믿는 바였고, 그가 갈망하는 바였다."]], [["But all this could only be on condition of the first, the happy solution of the question.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 오로지 그 첫 번째, 그 행복한 해결이라는 조건에서만 가능한 일이었다."], ["There was another possibility, a different and awful ending.", "또 다른 가능성, 그와는 다른 끔찍한 결말이 있었다."], ["Suddenly she might say to him: “Go away.", "별안간 그녀가 그에게 이렇게 말할지도 몰랐다."], ["I have just come to terms with Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "\"가 버리세요. 저는 방금 표도르 파블로비치와 합의를 보았어요."], ["I am going to marry him and don’t want you”—and then ...", "저는 그분과 결혼할 거고 당신은 필요 없어요.\" 그러면 그땐…"], ["but then....", "그러나 그땐…."], ["But Mitya did not know what would happen then.", "그러나 미탸는 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알지 못했다."], ["Up to the last hour he didn’t know.", "마지막 순간까지도 그는 알지 못했다."], ["That must be said to his credit.", "이 점만은 그를 위해 말해 두어야 한다."], ["He had no definite intentions, had planned no crime.", "그에게는 뚜렷한 의도가 없었고, 어떤 범죄도 계획하지 않았다."], ["He was simply watching and spying in agony, while he prepared himself for the first, happy solution of his destiny.", "그는 그저 고통 속에서 지켜보고 염탐하며, 제 운명의 첫 번째, 행복한 해결을 맞을 채비를 하고 있었을 뿐이다."], ["He drove away any other idea, in fact.", "사실 그는 그 밖의 다른 생각은 모조리 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["But for that ending a quite different anxiety arose, a new, incidental, but yet fatal and insoluble difficulty presented itself.", "그러나 그 결말을 위해 전혀 다른 종류의 근심이 생겨났으니, 새롭고, 부수적이면서도, 그럼에도 치명적이고 풀 길 없는 난관이 그 앞에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["If she were to say to him: “I’m yours; take me away,” how could he take her away?", "만일 그녀가 그에게 \"저는 당신 거예요, 저를 데려가세요\"라고 말한다면, 그가 어떻게 그녀를 데려간단 말인가?"], ["Where had he the means, the money to do it?", "그럴 방편이, 그럴 돈이 그에게 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["It was just at this time that all sources of revenue from Fyodor Pavlovitch, doles which had gone on without interruption for so many years, ceased.", "그런데 바로 이때에, 그토록 오랜 세월 끊임없이 이어져 오던 표도르 파블로비치의 적선, 그 온갖 수입원이 죄다 끊겨 버렸다."], ["Grushenka had money, of course, but with regard to this Mitya suddenly evinced extraordinary pride; he wanted to carry her away and begin the new life with her himself, at his own expense, not at hers.", "물론 그루셴카에게는 돈이 있었으나, 이에 관해서는 미탸가 별안간 유달리 큰 자존심을 드러냈으니, 그는 그녀를 데리고 가서 그녀의 돈이 아니라 제 손으로, 제 비용으로 그녀와 함께 새 삶을 시작하고 싶었다."], ["He could not conceive of taking her money, and the very idea caused him a pang of intense repulsion.", "그녀의 돈을 받는다는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없었고, 그런 생각만으로도 격렬한 혐오의 통증이 그를 찔렀다."], ["I won’t enlarge on this fact or analyze it here, but confine myself to remarking that this was his attitude at the moment.", "나는 여기서 이 사실을 늘어놓거나 분석하지는 않겠고, 다만 그 순간 그의 태도가 이러했다는 것만 짚어 두겠다."], ["All this may have arisen indirectly and unconsciously from the secret stings of his conscience for the money of Katerina Ivanovna that he had dishonestly appropriated.", "이 모든 것은 어쩌면 그가 부정하게 가로챈 카테리나 이바노브나의 돈을 두고 양심이 은밀히 찔러 대는 데서 간접적으로, 또 무의식적으로 비롯된 것이었으리라."], ["“I’ve been a scoundrel to one of them, and I shall be a scoundrel again to the other directly,”", "\"나는 그 여자 하나에게 비열한 놈이었는데, 곧 또 다른 여자에게도 비열한 놈이 되겠구나.\""], ["was his feeling then, as he explained after: “and when Grushenka knows, she won’t care for such a scoundrel.”", "뒷날 그가 설명한 대로, 그때 그의 심정은 이러했다. \"그리고 그루셴카가 그걸 알면, 그런 비열한 놈 따위는 거들떠보지도 않을 거야.\""]], [["Where then was he to get the means, where was he to get the fateful money?", "그렇다면 그는 그 방편을 어디서 구한단 말인가, 그 운명의 돈을 어디서 구한단 말인가?"], ["Without it, all would be lost and nothing could be done, “and only because I hadn’t the money.", "그것이 없으면 모든 것이 허사가 되고 아무것도 이룰 수 없으리라. \"그것도 다 내게 돈이 없다는 이유 하나 때문이라니."], ["Oh, the shame of it!”", "오, 이 수치스러움이라니!\""]], [["To anticipate things: he did, perhaps, know where to get the money, knew, perhaps, where it lay at that moment.", "미리 말해 두자면, 그는 어쩌면 그 돈을 어디서 구해야 할지 알고 있었을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 그 순간 그것이 어디에 놓여 있는지도 알고 있었을지 모른다."], ["I will say no more of this here, as it will all be clear later.", "이에 관해서는 모든 것이 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 터이므로 여기서는 더 말하지 않겠다."], ["But his chief trouble, I must explain however obscurely, lay in the fact that to have that sum he knew of, to have the right to take it, he must first restore Katerina Ivanovna’s three thousand—if not, “I’m a common pickpocket, I’m a scoundrel, and I don’t want to begin a new life as a scoundrel,”", "그러나 그의 가장 큰 고민은—아무리 어렴풋하게라도 설명해 두어야겠는데—그가 아는 그 금액을 손에 넣으려면, 그것을 취할 권리를 가지려면, 무엇보다 먼저 카테리나 이바노브나의 삼천 루블부터 갚아야 한다는 사실에 있었다. 그러지 않으면, \"나는 한낱 소매치기요, 비열한 놈이니, 비열한 놈인 채로 새 삶을 시작하고 싶지는 않다\"라고 미탸는 작정했다."], ["Mitya decided. And so he made up his mind to move heaven and earth to return Katerina Ivanovna that three thousand, and that first of all.", "그리하여 그는 무슨 수를 써서라도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그 삼천 루블을 돌려주기로, 그것도 무엇보다 먼저 그러기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["The final stage of this decision, so to say, had been reached only during the last hours, that is, after his last interview with Alyosha, two days before, on the high-road, on the evening when Grushenka had insulted Katerina Ivanovna, and Mitya, after hearing Alyosha’s account of it, had admitted that he was a scoundrel, and told him to tell Katerina Ivanovna so, if it could be any comfort to her.", "말하자면 이 결심의 마지막 단계에 그가 이른 것은 불과 지난 몇 시간 동안의 일이었으니, 곧 이틀 전 큰길에서 알료샤와 마지막으로 만난 뒤, 그루셴카가 카테리나 이바노브나를 모욕한 그날 저녁, 미탸가 알료샤에게서 그 이야기를 듣고 자신이 비열한 놈이라고 인정하며, 그것이 그녀에게 조금이라도 위로가 된다면 카테리나 이바노브나에게 그렇게 전하라고 일렀던 그 일이 있은 뒤였다."], ["After parting from his brother on that night, he had felt in his frenzy that it would be better “to murder and rob some one than fail to pay my debt to Katya.", "그날 밤 동생과 헤어진 뒤, 그는 광기에 사로잡힌 채 \"카탸에게 진 빚을 갚지 못할 바에야 차라리 누군가를 죽이고 털어 버리는 편이 낫다\"고 느꼈다."], ["I’d rather every one thought me a robber and a murderer, I’d rather go to Siberia than that Katya should have the right to say that I deceived her and stole her money, and used her money to run away with Grushenka and begin a new life!", "\"남들이 모두 나를 강도요 살인자로 여기는 편이 낫겠다, 카탸가 내가 자기를 속이고 그 돈을 훔쳤으며 그 돈으로 그루셴카와 달아나 새 삶을 시작했다고 말할 권리를 갖느니 차라리 시베리아로 가는 편이 낫겠다!"], ["That I can’t do!”", "그것만은 할 수 없다!\""], ["So Mitya decided, grinding his teeth, and he might well fancy at times that his brain would give way.", "미탸는 이를 갈며 이렇게 작정했고, 때때로 정말이지 머리가 돌아 버릴 것만 같았다."], ["But meanwhile he went on struggling....", "그러나 그러면서도 그는 계속 발버둥쳤다…."]], [["Strange to say, though one would have supposed there was nothing left for him but despair—for what chance had he, with nothing in the world, to raise such a sum?—yet to the very end he persisted in hoping that he would get that three thousand, that the money would somehow come to him of itself, as though it might drop from heaven.", "이상하게도, 그에게 남은 것이라곤 절망밖에 없으리라 여겨질 만했는데—세상에 아무것도 가진 것 없는 그가 그만한 큰돈을 마련할 가망이 무엇이 있었겠는가?—그럼에도 그는 끝까지, 그 삼천 루블을 손에 넣게 되리라고, 그 돈이 마치 하늘에서 떨어지기라도 하듯 어떻게든 제 발로 굴러들어 오리라고 끈질기게 믿었다."], ["That is just how it is with people who, like Dmitri, have never had anything to do with money, except to squander what has come to them by inheritance without any effort of their own, and have no notion how money is obtained.", "드미트리처럼, 돈이란 것을 제 노력 하나 들이지 않고 상속받은 것을 흥청망청 써 버리는 일 말고는 도무지 다뤄 본 적이 없고, 돈이 어떻게 마련되는지도 전혀 모르는 사람들이 바로 그러한 법이다."], ["A whirl of the most fantastic notions took possession of his brain immediately after he had parted with Alyosha two days before, and threw his thoughts into a tangle of confusion.", "이틀 전 알료샤와 헤어진 직후부터 더없이 황당무계한 망상들이 회오리치며 그의 머릿속을 사로잡아, 그의 생각을 온통 뒤엉킨 혼란에 빠뜨렸다."], ["This is how it was he pitched first on a perfectly wild enterprise.", "그래서 그는 맨 먼저 완전히 터무니없는 계획에 매달리게 된 것이다."], ["And perhaps to men of that kind in such circumstances the most impossible, fantastic schemes occur first, and seem most practical.", "그리고 어쩌면 그런 처지에 놓인 그런 부류의 사람들에게는, 가장 불가능하고 환상적인 계획이 맨 먼저 떠올라 가장 현실적으로 보이는 법인지도 모른다."]], [["He suddenly determined to go to Samsonov, the merchant who was Grushenka’s protector, and to propose a “scheme” to him, and by means of it to obtain from him at once the whole of the sum required.", "그는 별안간 그루셴카의 후견인인 상인 삼소노프를 찾아가 그에게 \"사업안\"을 제안하고, 그것을 통해 당장 그에게서 필요한 돈 전부를 얻어 내기로 작정했다."], ["Of the commercial value of his scheme he had no doubt, not the slightest, and was only uncertain how Samsonov would look upon his freak, supposing he were to consider it from any but the commercial point of view.", "자기 사업안의 상업적 가치에 관해서는 그는 추호도, 털끝만큼도 의심하지 않았고, 다만 삼소노프가 이를 순전히 상업적인 관점이 아닌 다른 시각에서 본다면 자기의 이 엉뚱한 발상을 어떻게 받아들일지가 미덥지 않을 따름이었다."], ["Though Mitya knew the merchant by sight, he was not acquainted with him and had never spoken a word to him.", "미탸는 그 상인을 얼굴로는 알았으나, 인사를 나눈 사이도 아니었고 단 한마디도 말을 섞어 본 적이 없었다."], ["But for some unknown reason he had long entertained the conviction that the old reprobate, who was lying at death’s door, would perhaps not at all object now to Grushenka’s securing a respectable position, and marrying a man “to be depended upon.”", "그러나 무슨 까닭에선지 그는 오래전부터, 죽음의 문턱에 누운 그 늙은 난봉꾼이라면 이제 그루셴카가 번듯한 처지를 얻어 \"믿을 만한\" 사내와 혼인하는 것에 전혀 반대하지 않으리라는 확신을 품어 왔다."], ["And he believed not only that he would not object, but that this was what he desired, and, if opportunity arose, that he would be ready to help.", "그리고 그는 그가 반대하지 않을 뿐 아니라 바로 그것을 바라고 있으며, 기회만 닿는다면 기꺼이 거들어 주리라고까지 믿었다."], ["From some rumor, or perhaps from some stray word of Grushenka’s, he had gathered further that the old man would perhaps prefer him to Fyodor Pavlovitch for Grushenka.", "어떤 소문에서, 아니면 어쩌면 그루셴카가 무심코 흘린 한마디에서, 그는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그 노인이 그루셴카의 짝으로 표도르 파블로비치보다 자기를 더 마음에 들어 할지도 모른다는 것까지 짐작해 냈다."]], [["Possibly many of the readers of my novel will feel that in reckoning on such assistance, and being ready to take his bride, so to speak, from the hands of her protector, Dmitri showed great coarseness and want of delicacy.", "내 소설을 읽는 독자들 가운데 많은 이들은, 그런 도움을 기대하고 말하자면 신부를 그 후견인의 손에서 넘겨받을 채비를 했다는 점에서, 드미트리가 대단한 조야함과 섬세함의 결여를 드러냈다고 느낄 것이다."], ["I will only observe that Mitya looked upon Grushenka’s past as something completely over.", "나는 다만, 미탸가 그루셴카의 과거를 완전히 끝나 버린 무엇으로 여겼다는 것만 짚어 두겠다."], ["He looked on that past with infinite pity and resolved with all the fervor of his passion that when once Grushenka told him she loved him and would marry him, it would mean the beginning of a new Grushenka and a new Dmitri, free from every vice.", "그는 그 과거를 끝없는 연민으로 바라보았고, 일단 그루셴카가 자기를 사랑하며 자기와 혼인하겠다고 말하기만 하면, 그것은 곧 온갖 악덕에서 벗어난 새로운 그루셴카와 새로운 드미트리의 시작을 뜻하리라고 제 열정의 온 열기를 다해 굳게 마음먹었다."], ["They would forgive one another and would begin their lives afresh.", "두 사람은 서로를 용서하고 삶을 처음부터 새로 시작하리라."], ["As for Kuzma Samsonov, Dmitri looked upon him as a man who had exercised a fateful influence in that remote past of Grushenka’s, though she had never loved him, and who was now himself a thing of the past, completely done with, and, so to say, non-existent.", "쿠지마 삼소노프로 말하자면, 드미트리는 그를, 비록 그루셴카가 한 번도 사랑한 적은 없으나 그녀의 그 머나먼 과거에 운명적인 영향을 끼쳤던 사내이자, 이제는 그 자신이 과거의 일이 되어 완전히 끝나 버린, 말하자면 존재하지 않는 사람으로 여겼다."], ["Besides, Mitya hardly looked upon him as a man at all, for it was known to every one in the town that he was only a shattered wreck, whose relations with Grushenka had changed their character and were now simply paternal, and that this had been so for a long time.", "게다가 미탸는 그를 사람으로조차 거의 여기지 않았으니, 그가 한낱 부서져 버린 폐물에 지나지 않으며, 그루셴카와의 관계도 그 성격이 바뀌어 이제는 단지 부녀간의 정과 같은 것이 된 지 오래라는 사실을 읍내 사람이라면 누구나 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["In any case there was much simplicity on Mitya’s part in all this, for in spite of all his vices, he was a very simple-hearted man.", "어쨌든 이 모든 일에는 미탸 쪽의 순박함이 적잖이 깃들어 있었으니, 그는 온갖 악덕에도 불구하고 매우 순박한 마음을 지닌 사내였기 때문이다."], ["It was an instance of this simplicity that Mitya was seriously persuaded that, being on the eve of his departure for the next world, old Kuzma must sincerely repent of his past relations with Grushenka, and that she had no more devoted friend and protector in the world than this now harmless old man.", "미탸가, 저세상으로 떠날 날을 앞둔 늙은 쿠지마라면 그루셴카와의 지난 관계를 진심으로 뉘우치고 있으리라고, 그리고 이제 무해해진 이 노인보다 더 헌신적인 벗이자 보호자를 그녀가 세상에 둘 수 없으리라고 진지하게 믿었다는 것이야말로 그 순박함의 한 본보기였다."]], [["After his conversation with Alyosha, at the cross-roads, he hardly slept all night, and at ten o’clock next morning, he was at the house of Samsonov and telling the servant to announce him.", "네거리에서 알료샤와 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 밤새 거의 잠을 이루지 못했고, 이튿날 아침 열 시에 삼소노프의 집에 이르러 하인에게 자기가 왔음을 알리라고 일렀다."], ["It was a very large and gloomy old house of two stories, with a lodge and outhouses.", "그것은 별채와 헛간들이 딸린, 매우 크고 음울한 이 층짜리 낡은 저택이었다."], ["In the lower story lived Samsonov’s two married sons with their families, his old sister, and his unmarried daughter.", "아래층에는 삼소노프의 결혼한 두 아들이 그 가족과 함께, 그리고 그의 늙은 누이와 시집 안 간 딸이 살았다."], ["In the lodge lived two of his clerks, one of whom also had a large family.", "별채에는 그의 점원 둘이 살았는데, 그중 하나도 대가족을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Both the lodge and the lower story were overcrowded, but the old man kept the upper floor to himself, and would not even let the daughter live there with him, though she waited upon him, and in spite of her asthma was obliged at certain fixed hours, and at any time he might call her, to run upstairs to him from below.", "별채도 아래층도 사람들로 빽빽했으나, 노인은 위층을 제 혼자 차지하고는, 딸이 시중을 들고 천식이 있는 몸으로 정해진 시각마다, 또 부르기만 하면 아무 때나 아래층에서 그에게로 부랴부랴 뛰어 올라가야 했음에도, 딸조차 거기서 함께 살지 못하게 했다."]], [["This upper floor contained a number of large rooms kept purely for show, furnished in the old-fashioned merchant style, with long monotonous rows of clumsy mahogany chairs along the walls, with glass chandeliers under shades, and gloomy mirrors on the walls.", "이 위층에는 순전히 보여 주기 위한 큰 방들이 여럿 있었는데, 옛 상인풍으로 꾸며져 벽을 따라 투박한 마호가니 의자들이 길고 단조롭게 줄지어 늘어서 있었고, 갓을 씌운 유리 샹들리에들과 벽에 걸린 음울한 거울들이 있었다."], ["All these rooms were entirely empty and unused, for the old man kept to one room, a small, remote bedroom, where he was waited upon by an old servant with a kerchief on her head, and by a lad, who used to sit on the locker in the passage.", "이 방들은 죄다 텅 비어 쓰이지 않았으니, 노인은 한 방에만, 외딴 작은 침실에만 틀어박혀 머리에 수건을 두른 늙은 하녀와 복도의 궤짝에 걸터앉아 있곤 하는 사내아이의 시중을 받았다."], ["Owing to his swollen legs, the old man could hardly walk at all, and was only rarely lifted from his leather arm-chair, when the old woman supporting him led him up and down the room once or twice.", "다리가 퉁퉁 부어 노인은 도무지 걷지 못했고, 늙은 하녀가 부축해 방 안을 한두 번 오르내리게 할 때나 어쩌다 한 번씩 가죽 안락의자에서 일으켜질 따름이었다."], ["He was morose and taciturn even with this old woman.", "그는 이 늙은 하녀에게마저 시무룩하고 말이 없었다."]], [["When he was informed of the arrival of the “captain,”", "\"대위\"가 왔다는 전갈을 받자 그는 대뜸 만나기를 거절했다."], ["he at once refused to see him. But Mitya persisted and sent his name up again.", "그러나 미탸는 물러서지 않고 다시 제 이름을 올려 보냈다."], ["Samsonov questioned the lad minutely: What he looked like?", "삼소노프는 사내아이에게 꼬치꼬치 캐물었다. 그 사람 행색이 어떠냐?"], ["Whether he was drunk?", "술에 취했더냐?"], ["Was he going to make a row?", "소란을 피울 것 같더냐?"], ["The answer he received was: that he was sober, but wouldn’t go away.", "돌아온 대답은, 그가 멀쩡한데 도무지 가려 들지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["The old man again refused to see him.", "노인은 또다시 만나기를 거절했다."], ["Then Mitya, who had foreseen this, and purposely brought pencil and paper with him, wrote clearly on the piece of paper the words: “On most important business closely concerning Agrafena Alexandrovna,”", "그러자 이를 미리 내다보고 일부러 연필과 종이를 가지고 온 미탸는, 그 종이에 또박또박 이렇게 적었다. \"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에 관한 더없이 중대한 용무로.\""], ["and sent it up to the old man.", "그러고는 그것을 노인에게 올려 보냈다."]], [["After thinking a little Samsonov told the lad to take the visitor to the drawing-room, and sent the old woman downstairs with a summons to his younger son to come upstairs to him at once.", "잠시 생각한 끝에 삼소노프는 사내아이에게 손님을 응접실로 안내하라 이르고, 늙은 하녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 작은아들에게 당장 위층으로 올라오라는 전갈을 전하게 했다."], ["This younger son, a man over six foot and of exceptional physical strength, who was closely-shaven and dressed in the European style, though his father still wore a kaftan and a beard, came at once without a comment.", "이 작은아들은 키가 육 척이 넘고 남달리 힘이 센 사내로, 아비는 아직도 카프탄을 입고 수염을 기르고 있었으나 그는 말끔히 면도하고 유럽식으로 차려입은 사람이었는데, 군말 없이 곧장 올라왔다."], ["All the family trembled before the father.", "온 식구가 아비 앞에서 벌벌 떨었다."], ["The old man had sent for this giant, not because he was afraid of the “captain” (he was by no means of a timorous temper), but in order to have a witness in case of any emergency.", "노인이 이 거구를 부른 것은 \"대위\"가 두려워서가 아니라(그는 결코 겁 많은 성미가 아니었다), 무슨 일이 벌어질 경우에 대비해 증인을 두기 위함이었다."], ["Supported by his son and the servant-lad, he waddled at last into the drawing-room.", "아들과 하인 아이의 부축을 받아 그는 마침내 뒤뚱뒤뚱 응접실로 들어섰다."], ["It may be assumed that he felt considerable curiosity.", "짐작건대 그는 적잖은 호기심을 느꼈으리라."], ["The drawing-room in which Mitya was awaiting him was a vast, dreary room that laid a weight of depression on the heart.", "미탸가 그를 기다리고 있던 응접실은 가슴에 짓누르는 듯한 우울을 안기는, 휑하고 음산한 방이었다."], ["It had a double row of windows, a gallery, marbled walls, and three immense chandeliers with glass lusters covered with shades.", "거기엔 이중으로 늘어선 창들과 회랑, 대리석을 바른 벽, 그리고 갓을 씌운 유리 장식이 매달린 거대한 샹들리에 셋이 있었다."]], [["Mitya was sitting on a little chair at the entrance, awaiting his fate with nervous impatience.", "미탸는 입구의 작은 의자에 앉아, 초조한 조바심 속에서 제 운명을 기다리고 있었다."], ["When the old man appeared at the opposite door, seventy feet away, Mitya jumped up at once, and with his long, military stride walked to meet him.", "일흔 자나 떨어진 맞은편 문에 노인이 모습을 드러내자, 미탸는 대뜸 벌떡 일어나 길고 군인다운 걸음걸이로 그를 맞으러 걸어갔다."], ["Mitya was well dressed, in a frock-coat, buttoned up, with a round hat and black gloves in his hands, just as he had been three days before at the elder’s, at the family meeting with his father and brothers.", "미탸는 단정히 차려입고 있었으니, 단추를 채운 프록코트 차림에 둥근 모자와 검은 장갑을 손에 든 모양새가, 사흘 전 장로의 처소에서 아버지며 형제들과 함께한 가족 모임 때 그대로였다."], ["The old man waited for him, standing dignified and unbending, and Mitya felt at once that he had looked him through and through as he advanced.", "노인은 위엄 있게, 꼿꼿이 서서 그를 기다렸고, 미탸는 자기가 다가가는 동안 그가 자신을 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보았음을 단번에 느꼈다."], ["Mitya was greatly impressed, too, with Samsonov’s immensely swollen face.", "미탸는 또한 삼소노프의 엄청나게 부어오른 얼굴에 크게 압도되었다."], ["His lower lip, which had always been thick, hung down now, looking like a bun.", "늘 두툼하던 아랫입술이 이제는 빵 덩어리처럼 축 늘어져 있었다."], ["He bowed to his guest in dignified silence, motioned him to a low chair by the sofa, and, leaning on his son’s arm he began lowering himself on to the sofa opposite, groaning painfully, so that Mitya, seeing his painful exertions, immediately felt remorseful and sensitively conscious of his insignificance in the presence of the dignified person he had ventured to disturb.", "노인은 위엄 있게 잠자코 손님에게 고개를 숙여 보이고는, 소파 옆의 낮은 의자를 손짓으로 가리킨 뒤, 아들의 팔에 기대어 맞은편 소파에 몸을 낮추기 시작하며 괴롭게 신음했으니, 그 고통스러운 안간힘을 본 미탸는 그 즉시 가책을 느끼며, 감히 폐를 끼친 이 위엄 있는 인물 앞에서 제 하찮음을 예민하게 의식했다."]], [["“What is it you want of me, sir?”", "\"무슨 일로 나를 찾았소, 선생?\""], ["said the old man, deliberately, distinctly, severely, but courteously, when he was at last seated.", "마침내 자리에 앉자 노인이 천천히, 또박또박, 엄하게, 그러나 정중하게 말했다."]], [["Mitya started, leapt up, but sat down again.", "미탸는 흠칫하며 벌떡 일어섰다가 다시 앉았다."], ["Then he began at once speaking with loud, nervous haste, gesticulating, and in a positive frenzy.", "그러더니 곧장 큰 소리로, 신경질적으로 다급하게, 손짓을 섞어 가며, 그야말로 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 말하기 시작했다."], ["He was unmistakably a man driven into a corner, on the brink of ruin, catching at the last straw, ready to sink if he failed.", "그는 누가 봐도 궁지에 몰려, 파멸의 벼랑 끝에서 마지막 지푸라기를 붙드는, 실패하면 그대로 가라앉을 채비가 된 사내였다."], ["Old Samsonov probably grasped all this in an instant, though his face remained cold and immovable as a statue’s.", "늙은 삼소노프는 얼굴은 조각상처럼 차갑고 미동도 없었으나, 십중팔구 이 모든 것을 순식간에 알아챘으리라."]], [["“Most honored sir, Kuzma Kuzmitch, you have no doubt heard more than once of my disputes with my father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, who robbed me of my inheritance from my mother ...", "\"더없이 존경하옵는 선생님, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 선생님께서도 제가 아버지 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프와 다투고 있다는 것을 분명 한두 번은 들으셨을 줄 압니다. 그자가 어머니에게서 물려받은 제 유산을 가로챘는데…"], ["seeing the whole town is gossiping about it ...", "온 읍내가 그 일을 입에 올리고 있으니 말입니다…"], ["for here every one’s gossiping of what they shouldn’t ...", "여기서는 다들 입에 담아서는 안 될 것들을 떠벌리니까요…"], ["and besides, it might have reached you through Grushenka ...", "게다가 그루셴카를 통해 그 이야기가 선생님 귀에 들어갔을 수도 있겠지요…"], ["I beg your pardon, through Agrafena Alexandrovna ...", "죄송합니다, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 통해서 말입니다…"], ["Agrafena Alexandrovna, the lady for whom I have the highest respect and esteem ...”", "제가 더없이 깊이 존경하고 우러르는 분, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 통해서요…\""]], [["So Mitya began, and broke down at the first sentence.", "이렇게 미탸는 입을 떼었으나 첫 문장에서부터 말이 막히고 말았다."], ["We will not reproduce his speech word for word, but will only summarize the gist of it.", "우리는 그의 말을 한 마디 한 마디 옮기지는 않고, 다만 그 요지만을 간추리기로 하겠다."], ["Three months ago, he said, he had of express intention (Mitya purposely used these words instead of “intentionally”) consulted a lawyer in the chief town of the province, “a distinguished lawyer, Kuzma Kuzmitch, Pavel Pavlovitch Korneplodov.", "그는 석 달 전에 모종의 작정을 품고(미탸는 \"의도적으로\"라는 말 대신 일부러 이런 표현을 썼다) 도의 중심 도시에서 한 변호사와 상의했다고 했다. \"쿠지마 쿠지미치, 명망 높은 변호사인데, 파벨 파블로비치 코르네플로도프라는 사람입니다."], ["You have perhaps heard of him?", "혹시 들어 보셨는지요?"], ["A man of vast intellect, the mind of a statesman ...", "어마어마한 지력에, 정치가의 두뇌를 지닌 사람으로…"], ["he knows you, too ...", "그가 선생님도 알더군요…"], ["spoke of you in the highest terms ...”", "선생님을 더없이 칭송하던데요…\""], ["Mitya broke down again.", "미탸는 또다시 말이 막혔다."], ["But these breaks did not deter him.", "그러나 이런 끊김들이 그를 멈추게 하지는 못했다."], ["He leapt instantly over the gaps, and struggled on and on.", "그는 그 틈새들을 단숨에 뛰어넘어 끊임없이 발버둥치며 말을 이어 나갔다."]], [["This Korneplodov, after questioning him minutely, and inspecting the documents he was able to bring him (Mitya alluded somewhat vaguely to these documents, and slurred over the subject with special haste), reported that they certainly might take proceedings concerning the village of Tchermashnya, which ought, he said, to have come to him, Mitya, from his mother, and so checkmate the old villain, his father ...", "이 코르네플로도프라는 자는 그에게 꼬치꼬치 캐묻고 그가 가져갈 수 있었던 서류들을 살펴본 끝에(미탸는 이 서류들에 관해서는 다소 모호하게 둘러대며 유달리 서둘러 그 대목을 얼버무렸다), 어머니에게서 자기, 곧 미탸에게로 마땅히 넘어왔어야 할 체르마시냐 마을에 관해 소송을 걸어 그 늙은 악당인 아비에게 한 방 먹일 수 있다고 알려 주었다."], ["“because every door was not closed and justice might still find a loophole.”", "\"왜냐하면 모든 문이 닫힌 것은 아니어서 정의가 아직 빠져나갈 구멍을 찾을 수 있기 때문\"이라는 것이었다."], ["In fact, he might reckon on an additional sum of six or even seven thousand roubles from Fyodor Pavlovitch, as Tchermashnya was worth, at least, twenty-five thousand, he might say twenty-eight thousand, in fact, “thirty, thirty, Kuzma Kuzmitch, and would you believe it, I didn’t get seventeen from that heartless man!”", "사실 그는 표도르 파블로비치에게서 육천, 아니 칠천 루블까지도 더 받아 낼 셈을 칠 수 있었으니, 체르마시냐가 적어도 이만오천 루블, 아니 이만팔천 루블, 사실은 \"삼만, 삼만은 나가는데, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 그런데 믿어지십니까, 저는 그 매정한 자한테서 만 칠천도 못 받았답니다!\""], ["So he, Mitya, had thrown the business up, for the time, knowing nothing about the law, but on coming here was struck dumb by a cross-claim made upon him (here Mitya went adrift again and again took a flying leap forward), “so will not you, excellent and honored Kuzma Kuzmitch, be willing to take up all my claims against that unnatural monster, and pay me a sum down of only three thousand?...", "그래서 그, 미탸는 법에 관해 아는 것이 없는 터라 그 일을 당분간 접어 두었는데, 막상 이리로 와 보니 자기에게 걸린 맞소송에 그만 말문이 막혀 버렸다는 것이었다(여기서 미탸는 또다시 갈피를 잃고 다시 한 번 앞으로 훌쩍 건너뛰었다). \"그러니 훌륭하고 존경하옵는 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 그 천륜을 저버린 괴물을 상대로 한 제 모든 청구권을 떠맡으시고, 제게 단돈 삼천만 한꺼번에 내주시지 않겠습니까?…"], ["You see, you cannot, in any case, lose over it.", "보십시오, 선생님은 어떤 경우에도 손해 보실 일이 없습니다."], ["On my honor, my honor, I swear that.", "제 명예를 걸고, 제 명예를 걸고 맹세합니다."], ["Quite the contrary, you may make six or seven thousand instead of three.”", "외려 그 반대로, 삼천 대신 육천이나 칠천을 버시게 될 겁니다.\""], ["Above all, he wanted this concluded that very day.", "무엇보다 그는 이 일을 바로 그날 안에 매듭짓고 싶어 했다."]], [["“I’ll do the business with you at a notary’s, or whatever it is ...", "\"공증인 앞에서든 뭐든 선생님과 일을 마무리짓겠습니다…"], ["in fact, I’m ready to do anything....", "사실 저는 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 되어 있습니다…."], ["I’ll hand over all the deeds ...", "모든 증서를 넘겨드리고…"], ["whatever you want, sign anything ...", "무엇이든 원하시는 대로, 뭐든 서명하고…"], ["and we could draw up the agreement at once ...", "계약서도 당장 작성할 수 있습니다…"], ["and if it were possible, if it were only possible, that very morning....", "그리고 가능하다면, 정말 가능하기만 하다면, 바로 오늘 아침에라도…. 선생님께서 제게 그 삼천을 내주실 수 있으십니다."], ["You could pay me that three thousand, for there isn’t a capitalist in this town to compare with you, and so would save me from ...", "이 읍내에 선생님께 견줄 자본가는 없으니까요. 그러면 저를 구해 주시는 겁니다…"], ["would save me, in fact ...", "사실 저를 구해 주시는 거지요…"], ["for a good, I might say an honorable action....", "선하고, 명예롭다 할 만한 일을 위해서 말입니다…."], ["For I cherish the most honorable feelings for a certain person, whom you know well, and care for as a father.", "왜냐하면 저는 선생님께서도 잘 아시고 아버지처럼 아끼시는 어떤 분을 향해 더없이 명예로운 감정을 품고 있기 때문입니다."], ["I would not have come, indeed, if it had not been as a father.", "사실 아버지 같은 분이 아니었다면 저는 찾아오지도 않았을 겁니다."], ["And, indeed, it’s a struggle of three in this business, for it’s fate—that’s a fearful thing, Kuzma Kuzmitch!", "게다가 이건 셋이 얽힌 싸움이거든요, 운명이란 말입니다—그게 무서운 거예요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치!"], ["A tragedy, Kuzma Kuzmitch, a tragedy!", "비극입니다, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 비극이라고요!"], ["And as you’ve dropped out long ago, it’s a tug-of-war between two.", "그런데 선생님은 오래전에 발을 빼셨으니, 이건 둘 사이의 줄다리기지요."], ["I’m expressing it awkwardly, perhaps, but I’m not a literary man.", "어쩌면 제가 서툴게 말하고 있는지도 모르겠습니다만, 저는 문필가가 아니라서요."], ["You see, I’m on the one side, and that monster on the other.", "보십시오, 한쪽엔 제가 있고, 다른 한쪽엔 그 괴물이 있습니다."], ["So you must choose.", "그러니 선생님께서 택하셔야 합니다."], ["It’s either I or the monster.", "저냐, 그 괴물이냐예요."], ["It all lies in your hands—the fate of three lives, and the happiness of two....", "이 모든 게 선생님 손에 달렸습니다—세 목숨의 운명과 두 사람의 행복이…."], ["Excuse me, I’m making a mess of it, but you understand ...", "죄송합니다, 제가 횡설수설하고 있습니다만, 선생님은 아실 겁니다…"], ["I see from your venerable eyes that you understand ...", "선생님의 존엄한 눈빛을 보니 아시는 게 분명합니다…"], ["and if you don’t understand, I’m done for ...", "그런데 만일 모르신다면, 저는 끝장입니다…"], ["so you see!”", "자, 그러니까요!\""]], [["Mitya broke off his clumsy speech with that, “so you see!” and jumping up from his seat, awaited the answer to his foolish proposal.", "미탸는 이 \"자, 그러니까요!\"라는 말로 그 어설픈 연설을 뚝 그치고는, 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 자신의 어리석은 제안에 대한 답을 기다렸다."], ["At the last phrase he had suddenly become hopelessly aware that it had all fallen flat, above all, that he had been talking utter nonsense.", "마지막 구절에 이르러 그는 별안간, 모든 것이 빗나가 버렸다는 것을, 무엇보다도 자기가 순전히 헛소리를 지껄여 댔다는 것을 절망적으로 깨달았다."]], [["“How strange it is!", "\"참 이상도 하구나!"], ["On the way here it seemed all right, and now it’s nothing but nonsense.”", "여기 오는 길엔 멀쩡해 보였는데, 막상 와 보니 죄다 헛소리뿐이라니.\""], ["The idea suddenly dawned on his despairing mind.", "그 절망에 빠진 머릿속에 별안간 이런 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["All the while he had been talking, the old man sat motionless, watching him with an icy expression in his eyes.", "그가 말하는 내내 노인은 미동도 없이 앉아, 얼음장 같은 표정을 눈에 담은 채 그를 지켜보았다."], ["After keeping him for a moment in suspense, Kuzma Kuzmitch pronounced at last in the most positive and chilling tone:", "잠시 그를 애태우게 내버려 둔 끝에, 쿠지마 쿠지미치는 마침내 더없이 단호하고 싸늘한 어조로 이렇게 못 박았다."]], [["“Excuse me, we don’t undertake such business.”", "\"미안하오만, 우리는 그런 일은 맡지 않소.\""]], [["Mitya suddenly felt his legs growing weak under him.", "미탸는 별안간 다리에 힘이 풀리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“What am I to do now, Kuzma Kuzmitch?”", "\"이제 저는 어쩌면 좋습니까, 쿠지마 쿠지미치?\""], ["he muttered, with a pale smile.", "그가 창백한 미소를 띠며 중얼거렸다."], ["“I suppose it’s all up with me—what do you think?”", "\"이제 다 끝장난 것 같은데—어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Excuse me....”", "\"미안하오만….\""]], [["Mitya remained standing, staring motionless.", "미탸는 선 채로, 꼼짝 않고 멍하니 쳐다보았다."], ["He suddenly noticed a movement in the old man’s face.", "문득 그는 노인의 얼굴에 무언가 변화가 이는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He started.", "그는 흠칫했다."]], [["“You see, sir, business of that sort’s not in our line,”", "\"보시오, 선생, 그런 종류의 일은 우리 분야가 아니오.\""], ["said the old man slowly.", "노인이 느릿느릿 말했다."], ["“There’s the court, and the lawyers—it’s a perfect misery.", "\"법정이며 변호사들이 있긴 하지만—그건 그야말로 고역이오."], ["But if you like, there is a man here you might apply to.”", "하지만 원한다면, 여기 찾아가 볼 만한 사람이 하나 있긴 하오.\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Who is it?", "누구입니까?"], ["You’re my salvation, Kuzma Kuzmitch,”", "선생님이 저를 구원하시는군요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치.\""], ["faltered Mitya.", "미탸가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“He doesn’t live here, and he’s not here just now.", "\"그는 여기 살지 않고, 지금 여기 있지도 않소."], ["He is a peasant, he does business in timber.", "농민인데, 목재 일을 하는 자요."], ["His name is Lyagavy.", "이름은 랴가비라고 하오."], ["He’s been haggling with Fyodor Pavlovitch for the last year, over your copse at Tchermashnya.", "지난 한 해 동안 표도르 파블로비치와 체르마시냐에 있는 당신네 숲을 두고 흥정을 벌이고 있소."], ["They can’t agree on the price, maybe you’ve heard?", "값이 안 맞아 합의를 못 보는 모양인데, 혹시 들어 봤소?"], ["Now he’s come back again and is staying with the priest at Ilyinskoe, about twelve versts from the Volovya station.", "지금 그가 다시 돌아와서 일린스코예의 사제 집에 묵고 있소, 볼로뱌 역에서 십이 베르스타쯤 되는 곳이오."], ["He wrote to me, too, about the business of the copse, asking my advice.", "그 숲 일로 내게도 편지를 보내 조언을 청했소."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch means to go and see him himself.", "표도르 파블로비치가 직접 그를 찾아가 만날 셈이라고 하더이다."], ["So if you were to be beforehand with Fyodor Pavlovitch and to make Lyagavy the offer you’ve made me, he might possibly—”", "그러니 만일 당신이 표도르 파블로비치보다 한발 앞서, 내게 한 그 제안을 랴가비에게 한다면, 어쩌면 그가—\""]], [["“A brilliant idea!”", "\"기막힌 생각이군요!\""], ["Mitya interrupted ecstatically.", "미탸가 황홀해하며 말을 가로챘다."], ["“He’s the very man, it would just suit him.", "\"그 사람이야말로 딱 맞는 사람입니다, 그에겐 그게 안성맞춤이지요."], ["He’s haggling with him for it, being asked too much, and here he would have all the documents entitling him to the property itself.", "값을 너무 부른다고 흥정을 벌이고 있는데, 여기 그 재산 자체에 대한 권리를 입증하는 서류가 죄다 그의 손에 들어오는 거니까요."], ["Ha ha ha!”", "하하하!\""]], [["And Mitya suddenly went off into his short, wooden laugh, startling Samsonov.", "그러고는 미탸가 별안간 짧고 메마른 웃음을 터뜨려 삼소노프를 흠칫하게 했다."]], [["“How can I thank you, Kuzma Kuzmitch?”", "\"이 은혜를 어떻게 갚아야 할지요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치?\""], ["cried Mitya effusively.", "미탸가 넘치는 감정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Don’t mention it,”", "\"별말씀을.\""], ["said Samsonov, inclining his head.", "삼소노프가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“But you don’t know, you’ve saved me.", "\"하지만 선생님은 모르십니다, 선생님이 저를 구하신 겁니다."], ["Oh, it was a true presentiment brought me to you....", "오, 저를 선생님께 데려온 건 정말이지 참된 예감이었습니다…."], ["So now to this priest!”", "그럼 이제 그 사제에게로!\""]], [["“No need of thanks.”", "\"고마워할 것 없소.\""]], [["“I’ll make haste and fly there.", "\"서둘러 그리로 날아가겠습니다."], ["I’m afraid I’ve overtaxed your strength.", "선생님 기력을 너무 축내 드린 건 아닌지 모르겠습니다."], ["I shall never forget it.", "결코 잊지 않겠습니다."], ["It’s a Russian says that, Kuzma Kuzmitch, a R-r-russian!”", "이렇게 말하는 건 러시아 사람이랍니다, 쿠지마 쿠지미치, 러-러-러시아 사람이요!\""]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"암, 그렇고말고!\""]], [["Mitya seized his hand to press it, but there was a malignant gleam in the old man’s eye.", "미탸는 노인의 손을 잡아 쥐려 했으나, 그 눈에 악의에 찬 번득임이 어렸다."], ["Mitya drew back his hand, but at once blamed himself for his mistrustfulness.", "미탸는 손을 도로 거두었지만, 이내 제 의심 많음을 스스로 나무랐다."]], [["“It’s because he’s tired,”", "\"노인장이 지치셨기 때문이지.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“For her sake!", "\"그분을 위해서입니다!"], ["For her sake, Kuzma Kuzmitch!", "그분을 위해서예요, 쿠지마 쿠지미치!"], ["You understand that it’s for her,”", "이게 그분을 위한 일이라는 걸 아시겠지요.\""], ["he cried, his voice ringing through the room.", "그가 외치자, 그 목소리가 방 안에 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸다."], ["He bowed, turned sharply round, and with the same long stride walked to the door without looking back.", "그는 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 홱 돌아서서, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 똑같은 그 긴 걸음걸이로 문을 향해 걸어갔다."], ["He was trembling with delight.", "그는 환희에 떨고 있었다."]], [["“Everything was on the verge of ruin and my guardian angel saved me,”", "\"모든 것이 파멸의 벼랑에 놓였었는데 내 수호천사가 나를 구해 주셨구나.\""], ["was the thought in his mind.", "그의 머릿속에 이런 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["And if such a business man as Samsonov (a most worthy old man, and what dignity!) had suggested this course, then ...", "그리고 삼소노프 같은 사업가(더없이 훌륭한 노인장, 게다가 그 위엄이라니!)가 이런 방도를 일러 주었다면, 그렇다면…"], ["then success was assured.", "그렇다면 성공은 보장된 것이나 다름없었다."], ["He would fly off immediately.", "그는 당장 날아가리라."], ["“I will be back before night, I shall be back at night and the thing is done.", "\"밤이 되기 전에 돌아오마, 밤에는 돌아올 거고, 그러면 일은 다 된 거다."], ["Could the old man have been laughing at me?”", "노인장이 나를 놀리고 있었던 건 아닐까?\""], ["exclaimed Mitya, as he strode towards his lodging.", "미탸는 제 하숙을 향해 성큼성큼 걸으며 소리쳤다."], ["He could, of course, imagine nothing, but that the advice was practical “from such a business man”", "그로서는 물론, 그 충고가 \"그런 사업가에게서\""], ["with an understanding of the business, with an understanding of this Lyagavy (curious surname!).", "나온, 그 일의 내막을 훤히 아는, 그 랴가비라는 자(참 묘한 성씨로군!)를 잘 아는 사람의 실속 있는 조언이라는 것 말고는 달리 아무것도 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["Or—the old man was laughing at him.", "아니면—노인장이 자기를 놀리고 있었거나."]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["The second alternative was the correct one.", "두 번째 쪽이 맞는 것이었다."], ["Long afterwards, when the catastrophe had happened, old Samsonov himself confessed, laughing, that he had made a fool of the “captain.” He was a cold, spiteful and sarcastic man, liable to violent antipathies.", "한참 뒤, 파국이 벌어지고 난 다음, 늙은 삼소노프 자신이 껄껄 웃으며, 자기가 그 \"대위\"를 바보로 만들었노라 털어놓았다."], ["Whether it was the “captain’s” excited face, or the foolish conviction of the “rake and spendthrift,”", "그는 차갑고 심술궂으며 빈정대기 좋아하는 사람으로, 격렬한 반감에 곧잘 사로잡히는 위인이었다."], ["that he, Samsonov, could be taken in by such a cock-and-bull story as his scheme, or his jealousy of Grushenka, in whose name this “scapegrace” had rushed in on him with such a tale to get money which worked on the old man, I can’t tell.", "그 \"대위\"의 흥분한 얼굴이었는지, 아니면 그 \"난봉꾼이자 탕아\"가 자기, 곧 삼소노프 같은 사람이 그따위 황당무계한 계획에 넘어가리라 어리석게 굳게 믿은 탓이었는지, 아니면 이 \"망나니\"가 돈을 우려내겠다고 그런 이야기를 들고 제게 뛰어들면서 들먹인 그루셴카에 대한 질투 탓이었는지, 그것이 그 노인의 마음을 건드린 것인지는 나로서도 알 수 없다."], ["But at the instant when Mitya stood before him, feeling his legs grow weak under him, and frantically exclaiming that he was ruined, at that moment the old man looked at him with intense spite, and resolved to make a laughing-stock of him.", "그러나 미탸가 그 앞에 서서 다리에 힘이 풀리는 것을 느끼며 자기는 끝장났다고 미친 듯이 외쳐 대던 바로 그 순간, 노인은 격렬한 악의를 품고 그를 바라보며 그를 웃음거리로 만들기로 작정했던 것이다."], ["When Mitya had gone, Kuzma Kuzmitch, white with rage, turned to his son and bade him see to it that that beggar be never seen again, and never admitted even into the yard, or else he’d—", "미탸가 떠나고 나자, 쿠지마 쿠지미치는 분노로 새파랗게 질려 아들을 돌아보고는, 그 거지가 다시는 얼씬도 못 하게, 마당에조차 들이지 말도록 단단히 단속하라, 그러지 않으면 가만두지—"]], [["He did not utter his threat.", "그는 그 위협을 끝내 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["But even his son, who often saw him enraged, trembled with fear.", "그러나 아비가 격노하는 모습을 자주 보아 온 아들조차 두려움에 떨었다."], ["For a whole hour afterwards, the old man was shaking with anger, and by evening he was worse, and sent for the doctor.", "그 후로도 노인은 꼬박 한 시간 동안 분노로 부들부들 떨었고, 저녁이 되자 더 나빠져 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다."]], [["# Chapter II. Lyagavy", "# Chapter II. Lyagavy"]], [["So he must drive at full speed, and he had not the money for horses.", "그리하여 그는 전속력으로 달려가야만 했으나, 말을 빌릴 돈이 없었다."], ["He had forty kopecks, and that was all, all that was left after so many years of prosperity!", "그에게 남은 것은 사십 코페이카가 전부였으니, 그토록 오랜 세월 누려 온 풍족함 끝에 남은 것이라곤 고작 그것뿐이었다!"], ["But he had at home an old silver watch which had long ceased to go.", "그러나 그에게는 집에 오래전부터 멈춰 버린 낡은 은시계가 하나 있었다."], ["He snatched it up and carried it to a Jewish watchmaker who had a shop in the market-place.", "그는 그것을 낚아채어 시장 광장에 가게를 차린 유대인 시계공에게 들고 갔다."], ["The Jew gave him six roubles for it.", "유대인은 그것을 받고 육 루블을 내주었다."]], [["“And I didn’t expect that,”", "\"그건 나도 기대 못 했던 거야.\""], ["cried Mitya, ecstatically.", "미탸가 황홀에 겨워 소리쳤다."], ["(He was still in a state of ecstasy.) He seized his six roubles and ran home.", "(그는 여전히 황홀경에 빠져 있는 상태였다.) 그는 육 루블을 거머쥐고 집으로 달려갔다."], ["At home he borrowed three roubles from the people of the house, who loved him so much that they were pleased to give it him, though it was all they had.", "집에서는 셋집 사람들에게서 삼 루블을 빌렸는데, 그들은 그를 어찌나 좋아했던지 가진 돈 전부였음에도 기꺼이 내주었다."], ["Mitya in his excitement told them on the spot that his fate would be decided that day, and he described, in desperate haste, the whole scheme he had put before Samsonov, the latter’s decision, his own hopes for the future, and so on.", "미탸는 흥분한 나머지 그 자리에서, 오늘 자기 운명이 결판날 것이라고 그들에게 일러 주었고, 다급하기 짝이 없는 어조로 삼소노프에게 내놓았던 계획 전부와 그 노인의 결정, 자신이 품은 앞날의 희망 따위를 죄다 늘어놓았다."], ["These people had been told many of their lodger’s secrets before, and so looked upon him as a gentleman who was not at all proud, and almost one of themselves.", "이 사람들은 전부터 셋방살이하는 그의 비밀을 적잖이 들어 온 터라, 그를 조금도 거만하지 않은, 거의 자기네 식구나 다름없는 신사로 여기고 있었다."], ["Having thus collected nine roubles Mitya sent for posting-horses to take him to the Volovya station.", "그렇게 해서 구 루블을 그러모은 미탸는 볼로뱌 역까지 데려다줄 역마를 부르러 사람을 보냈다."], ["This was how the fact came to be remembered and established that “at midday, on the day before the event, Mitya had not a farthing, and that he had sold his watch to get money and had borrowed three roubles from his landlord, all in the presence of witnesses.”", "바로 이런 연유로 \"사건이 벌어지기 전날 한낮에 미탸는 한 푼도 없었으며, 돈을 마련하려고 시계를 팔았고 집주인에게서 삼 루블을 빌렸는데, 이 모든 일이 증인들이 보는 앞에서 벌어졌다\"는 사실이 사람들 기억에 남고 또 확증되기에 이르렀다."]], [["I note this fact, later on it will be apparent why I do so.", "내가 이 사실을 적어 두는 까닭은, 뒤에 가서 분명해질 것이다."]], [["Though he was radiant with the joyful anticipation that he would at last solve all his difficulties, yet, as he drew near Volovya station, he trembled at the thought of what Grushenka might be doing in his absence.", "마침내 자신의 모든 난관을 풀어 줄 것이라는 기쁜 기대로 그는 환하게 빛나고 있었으나, 막상 볼로뱌 역에 가까워지자 자기가 없는 동안 그루센카가 무슨 짓을 하고 있을지 생각하니 몸이 떨려 왔다."], ["What if she made up her mind to-day to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch?", "만일 그녀가 오늘 표도르 파블로비치에게로 가기로 마음을 굳힌다면 어쩐단 말인가?"], ["This was why he had gone off without telling her and why he left orders with his landlady not to let out where he had gone, if any one came to inquire for him.", "바로 이 때문에 그는 그녀에게 알리지도 않고 떠나왔으며, 누구든 자기를 찾아 묻거든 어디로 갔는지 발설하지 말라고 하숙집 안주인에게 일러두었던 것이다."]], [["“I must, I must get back to-night,”", "\"오늘 밤에는, 오늘 밤에는 반드시 돌아가야 해.\""], ["he repeated, as he was jolted along in the cart, “and I dare say I shall have to bring this Lyagavy back here ...", "그는 마차에 실려 덜컹덜컹 흔들리며 거듭 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 아마도 이 라가비를 여기로 데려와야 할 테지…"], ["to draw up the deed.”", "증서를 작성하자면 말이야.\""], ["So mused Mitya, with a throbbing heart, but alas!", "미탸는 가슴을 두근거리며 이렇게 골똘히 생각했으나, 아아!"], ["his dreams were not fated to be carried out.", "그의 꿈은 이루어질 운명이 못 되었다."]], [["To begin with, he was late, taking a short cut from Volovya station which turned out to be eighteen versts instead of twelve.", "우선 그는 늦고 말았으니, 볼로뱌 역에서 지름길로 들어섰던 것이 십이 베르스타가 아니라 십팔 베르스타로 드러났던 것이다."], ["Secondly, he did not find the priest at home at Ilyinskoe; he had gone off to a neighboring village.", "둘째로 그는 일리인스코예에서 사제를 집에서 만나지 못했으니, 사제는 이웃 마을로 떠나 버린 뒤였다."], ["While Mitya, setting off there with the same exhausted horses, was looking for him, it was almost dark.", "미탸가 그 지친 말들을 이끌고 그리로 길을 잡아 사제를 찾아 헤매는 동안 날은 거의 어두워져 있었다."]], [["The priest, a shy and amiable looking little man, informed him at once that though Lyagavy had been staying with him at first, he was now at Suhoy Possyolok, that he was staying the night in the forester’s cottage, as he was buying timber there too.", "수줍고 상냥해 보이는 자그마한 사내인 사제는, 라가비가 처음에는 자기 집에 묵었으나 지금은 수호이 포숄로크에 가 있으며, 그곳에서도 목재를 사들이느라 산지기 오두막에서 하룻밤을 묵고 있다고 곧바로 일러 주었다."], ["At Mitya’s urgent request that he would take him to Lyagavy at once, and by so doing “save him, so to speak,” the priest agreed, after some demur, to conduct him to Suhoy Possyolok; his curiosity was obviously aroused.", "자기를 당장 라가비에게 데려다주어 \"말하자면 자기를 구해 달라\"는 미탸의 절박한 청에, 사제는 얼마간 망설인 끝에 수호이 포숄로크까지 안내하기로 응낙했으니, 그의 호기심이 동했음이 분명했다."], ["But, unluckily, he advised their going on foot, as it would not be “much over” a verst.", "그러나 공교롭게도 사제는 걸어가자고 권했는데, 거리가 한 베르스타에서 \"그리 멀지 않다\"는 것이었다."], ["Mitya, of course, agreed, and marched off with his yard-long strides, so that the poor priest almost ran after him.", "미탸는 물론 동의하고 한 발에 한 마장씩 성큼성큼 내디뎠으니, 가엾은 사제는 그를 뒤쫓아 거의 뛰다시피 했다."], ["He was a very cautious man, though not old.", "그는 늙지는 않았으나 무척 조심스러운 사람이었다."]], [["Mitya at once began talking to him, too, of his plans, nervously and excitedly asking advice in regard to Lyagavy, and talking all the way.", "미탸는 그에게도 곧장 자기 계획을 이야기하기 시작했고, 라가비에 관해 안절부절못하며 흥분한 어조로 조언을 구하면서 가는 내내 떠들어 댔다."], ["The priest listened attentively, but gave little advice.", "사제는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였으나 조언이라곤 별로 해 주지 못했다."], ["He turned off Mitya’s questions with: “I don’t know.", "그는 미탸의 물음을 \"글쎄요, 잘 모르겠습니다."], ["Ah, I can’t say.", "뭐라 말할 수가 없군요."], ["How can I tell?” and so on.", "제가 어찌 알겠습니까?\""], ["When Mitya began to speak of his quarrel with his father over his inheritance, the priest was positively alarmed, as he was in some way dependent on Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "하는 식으로 받아넘기곤 했다. 미탸가 유산 문제로 아버지와 벌인 다툼을 이야기하기 시작하자, 사제는 어떤 식으로든 표도르 파블로비치에게 신세를 지고 있던 터라 대놓고 경계하는 빛을 보였다."], ["He inquired, however, with surprise, why he called the peasant-trader Gorstkin, Lyagavy, and obligingly explained to Mitya that, though the man’s name really was Lyagavy, he was never called so, as he would be grievously offended at the name, and that he must be sure to call him Gorstkin, “or you’ll do nothing with him; he won’t even listen to you,”", "그래도 그는 의아하다는 듯, 어째서 고르스트킨이라는 농부 장사꾼을 라가비라고 부르느냐고 물었고, 친절하게도 미탸에게 설명하기를, 그 사내의 이름이 실제로 라가비이긴 하나 누구도 그렇게 부르지 않으니, 그 이름을 들으면 몹시 기분이 상하기 때문이며, 반드시 고르스트킨이라고 불러야 한다고 했다. \"안 그러면 그자와는 아무 일도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["said the priest in conclusion.", "당신 말은 들으려고도 안 할 테니까요.\" 사제가 마무리 삼아 이렇게 말했다."]], [["Mitya was somewhat surprised for a moment, and explained that that was what Samsonov had called him.", "미탸는 잠깐 다소 어리둥절했다가, 삼소노프가 그렇게 불렀던 것이라고 설명했다."], ["On hearing this fact, the priest dropped the subject, though he would have done well to put into words his doubt whether, if Samsonov had sent him to that peasant, calling him Lyagavy, there was not something wrong about it and he was turning him into ridicule.", "이 사실을 듣고 사제는 그 화제를 더는 입에 올리지 않았으나, 만일 삼소노프가 그 농부에게 그를 보내면서 라가비라고 불렀다면 거기에 뭔가 석연찮은 데가 있는 것이며 그를 조롱거리로 삼은 것은 아닌지, 그런 의심을 말로 꺼냈더라면 좋았을 것이다."], ["But Mitya had no time to pause over such trifles.", "그러나 미탸로서는 그런 사소한 일에 잠시 머무를 겨를이 없었다."], ["He hurried, striding along, and only when he reached Suhoy Possyolok did he realize that they had come not one verst, nor one and a half, but at least three.", "그는 성큼성큼 걸음을 재촉했고, 수호이 포숄로크에 다다라서야 비로소 자기들이 한 베르스타도, 한 베르스타 반도 아니고 적어도 삼 베르스타는 걸어왔음을 깨달았다."], ["This annoyed him, but he controlled himself.", "이 일이 짜증스러웠으나 그는 꾹 참았다."]], [["They went into the hut.", "그들은 오두막 안으로 들어섰다."], ["The forester lived in one half of the hut, and Gorstkin was lodging in the other, the better room the other side of the passage.", "산지기는 오두막 한쪽에 살고 있었고, 고르스트킨은 다른 쪽, 통로 건너편의 더 나은 방에 묵고 있었다."], ["They went into that room and lighted a tallow candle.", "그들은 그 방으로 들어가 수지로 만든 양초에 불을 붙였다."], ["The hut was extremely overheated.", "오두막 안은 지나치게 후끈했다."], ["On the table there was a samovar that had gone out, a tray with cups, an empty rum bottle, a bottle of vodka partly full, and some half-eaten crusts of wheaten bread.", "탁자 위에는 불이 꺼진 사모바르 하나와 찻잔이 놓인 쟁반, 빈 럼주 병 하나, 절반쯤 든 보드카 병 하나, 그리고 반쯤 베어 먹다 만 밀빵 조각 몇 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["The visitor himself lay stretched at full length on the bench, with his coat crushed up under his head for a pillow, snoring heavily.", "손님 본인은 외투를 둘둘 말아 머리 밑에 베개 삼아 받친 채 긴 의자 위에 길게 뻗고 누워 요란하게 코를 골고 있었다."], ["Mitya stood in perplexity.", "미탸는 어찌할 바를 몰라 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Of course I must wake him.", "\"당연히 깨워야지."], ["My business is too important.", "내 용건이 너무도 중대하니까."], ["I’ve come in such haste.", "이렇게 다급하게 달려왔잖아."], ["I’m in a hurry to get back to-day,”", "오늘 안으로 돌아가야 한단 말이야.\""], ["he said in great agitation.", "그가 몹시 동요하며 말했다."], ["But the priest and the forester stood in silence, not giving their opinion.", "그러나 사제와 산지기는 아무 의견도 내놓지 않고 잠자코 서 있었다."], ["Mitya went up and began trying to wake him himself; he tried vigorously, but the sleeper did not wake.", "미탸는 다가가 직접 그를 깨워 보려 했고, 힘껏 흔들어 댔으나 잠든 사내는 깨어나지 않았다."]], [["“He’s drunk,”", "\"취했군.\""], ["Mitya decided.", "미탸는 단정했다."], ["“Good Lord!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What am I to do?", "어떻게 해야 하나?"], ["What am I to do?”", "어떻게 해야 한단 말인가?\""], ["And, terribly impatient, he began pulling him by the arms, by the legs, shaking his head, lifting him up and making him sit on the bench.", "그러고는 참다못해 그의 팔을, 다리를 잡아당기고 머리를 흔들어 대며 그를 들어 올려 긴 의자에 앉히려 했다."], ["Yet, after prolonged exertions, he could only succeed in getting the drunken man to utter absurd grunts, and violent, but inarticulate oaths.", "그러나 한참을 용을 쓴 끝에 얻어 낸 것이라곤, 술 취한 사내가 터무니없는 끙끙 소리와 사납지만 알아들을 수 없는 욕설을 내뱉게 한 것뿐이었다."]], [["“No, you’d better wait a little,”", "\"안 되겠습니다, 조금 기다리시는 편이 낫겠어요.\""], ["the priest pronounced at last, “for he’s obviously not in a fit state.”", "마침내 사제가 입을 열었다. \"보아하니 도무지 제정신이 아닌 상태이니 말입니다.\""]], [["“He’s been drinking the whole day,”", "\"하루 종일 마셔 댔거든요.\""], ["the forester chimed in.", "산지기가 거들었다."]], [["“Good heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“If only you knew how important it is to me and how desperate I am!”", "\"이게 나한테 얼마나 중대한 일인지, 또 내가 얼마나 절박한지 두 분이 아신다면!\""]], [["“No, you’d better wait till morning,”", "\"안 됩니다, 아침까지 기다리시는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["the priest repeated.", "사제가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Till morning?", "\"아침까지요?"], ["Mercy! that’s impossible!”", "원, 그건 말도 안 됩니다!\""]], [["And in his despair he was on the point of attacking the sleeping man again, but stopped short at once, realizing the uselessness of his efforts.", "그리고 절망에 빠진 그는 또다시 잠든 사내에게 달려들려다가, 그런 짓이 부질없음을 깨닫고 곧장 멈춰 섰다."], ["The priest said nothing, the sleepy forester looked gloomy.", "사제는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 졸음에 겨운 산지기는 침울한 얼굴이었다."]], [["“What terrible tragedies real life contrives for people,”", "\"현실이라는 게 사람한테 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 비극을 꾸며 내는지.\""], ["said Mitya, in complete despair.", "미탸가 완전히 절망에 잠겨 말했다."], ["The perspiration was streaming down his face.", "땀이 그의 얼굴을 타고 줄줄 흘러내렸다."], ["The priest seized the moment to put before him, very reasonably, that, even if he succeeded in wakening the man, he would still be drunk and incapable of conversation.", "사제는 이 틈을 놓치지 않고, 설령 그를 깨우는 데 성공한다 해도 여전히 취해 있어 대화를 나눌 형편이 못 될 것이라고 지극히 사리에 맞게 일러 주었다."], ["“And your business is important,”", "\"게다가 당신 용건이 중대하니,\""], ["he said, “so you’d certainly better put it off till morning.”", "그가 말했다. \"필시 아침까지 미루시는 편이 나을 겁니다.\""], ["With a gesture of despair Mitya agreed.", "미탸는 절망의 몸짓을 하며 그 말에 동의했다."]], [["“Father, I will stay here with a light, and seize the favorable moment.", "\"신부님, 저는 여기 불을 켜 놓고 남아서 좋은 기회를 잡겠습니다."], ["As soon as he wakes I’ll begin.", "저자가 깨어나는 즉시 시작할 겁니다."], ["I’ll pay you for the light,”", "불값은 치르겠습니다.\" 그가 산지기에게 말했다."], ["he said to the forester, “for the night’s lodging, too; you’ll remember Dmitri Karamazov.", "\"하룻밤 묵은 값도요. 드미트리 카라마조프를 기억해 두십시오."], ["Only, Father, I don’t know what we’re to do with you.", "다만 신부님, 신부님은 어떻게 해 드려야 할지 모르겠군요."], ["Where will you sleep?”", "어디서 주무시겠습니까?\""]], [["“No, I’m going home.", "\"아니요, 저는 집으로 돌아가겠습니다."], ["I’ll take his horse and get home,”", "저 사람 말을 빌려 집으로 가지요.\""], ["he said, indicating the forester.", "그가 산지기를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“And now I’ll say good-by.", "\"그럼 이만 작별 인사를 드리겠습니다."], ["I wish you all success.”", "모든 일이 잘되기를 빕니다.\""]], [["So it was settled.", "그렇게 해서 매듭이 지어졌다."], ["The priest rode off on the forester’s horse, delighted to escape, though he shook his head uneasily, wondering whether he ought not next day to inform his benefactor Fyodor Pavlovitch of this curious incident, “or he may in an unlucky hour hear of it, be angry, and withdraw his favor.”", "사제는 산지기의 말을 타고 떠났으니, 빠져나가게 된 것을 기뻐하면서도 불안한 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, 이튿날 자기 은인인 표도르 파블로비치에게 이 기묘한 사건을 알려야 하는 것은 아닐까 하고 생각했다. \"안 그러면 그분이 재수 없게 어디서 듣고는 화를 내며 베풀던 호의를 거두어 버릴지도 모르니까.\""]], [["The forester, scratching himself, went back to his room without a word, and Mitya sat on the bench to “catch the favorable moment,”", "산지기는 몸을 긁적이며 한마디 말도 없이 자기 방으로 돌아갔고, 미탸는 본인 표현대로 \"좋은 기회를 잡으려고\""], ["as he expressed it.", "긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["Profound dejection clung about his soul like a heavy mist.", "깊은 낙담이 짙은 안개처럼 그의 영혼에 들러붙었다."], ["A profound, intense dejection!", "깊고 사무치는 낙담이!"], ["He sat thinking, but could reach no conclusion.", "그는 앉아서 골똘히 생각했으나 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못했다."], ["The candle burnt dimly, a cricket chirped; it became insufferably close in the overheated room.", "촛불은 침침하게 타올랐고 귀뚜라미가 울었다. 후끈하게 달궈진 방 안은 견딜 수 없을 만큼 숨이 막혔다."], ["He suddenly pictured the garden, the path behind the garden, the door of his father’s house mysteriously opening and Grushenka running in.", "문득 그의 머릿속에 정원이, 정원 뒤편의 오솔길이, 아버지 집의 문이 은밀히 열리고 그루센카가 뛰어들어 가는 모습이 떠올랐다."], ["He leapt up from the bench.", "그는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“It’s a tragedy!”", "\"이건 비극이야!\""], ["he said, grinding his teeth.", "그는 이를 갈며 말했다."], ["Mechanically he went up to the sleeping man and looked in his face.", "무심결에 그는 잠든 사내에게 다가가 그 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["He was a lean, middle-aged peasant, with a very long face, flaxen curls, and a long, thin, reddish beard, wearing a blue cotton shirt and a black waistcoat, from the pocket of which peeped the chain of a silver watch.", "그는 깡마른 중년 농부로, 얼굴이 무척 길쭉하고 머리는 아마 빛 곱슬이었으며 길고 가느다란 불그스레한 수염을 길렀고, 파란 무명 셔츠에 검은 조끼를 걸쳤는데 그 조끼 주머니에서는 은시계 줄이 삐죽이 내다보였다."], ["Mitya looked at his face with intense hatred, and for some unknown reason his curly hair particularly irritated him.", "미탸는 격렬한 증오를 품고 그의 얼굴을 노려보았는데, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그 곱슬머리가 유난히 그의 신경을 거슬렀다."]], [["What was insufferably humiliating was, that after leaving things of such importance and making such sacrifices, he, Mitya, utterly worn out, should with business of such urgency be standing over this dolt on whom his whole fate depended, while he snored as though there were nothing the matter, as though he’d dropped from another planet.", "견딜 수 없이 굴욕스러운 것은, 그토록 중대한 일들을 내팽개치고 그토록 큰 희생을 치른 끝에, 이 미탸가 기진맥진한 몸으로 이토록 다급한 용건을 안고서 자기 운명 전부가 걸린 이 멍청이 위에 우두커니 서 있다는 사실이었으니, 정작 그자는 아무 일도 없다는 듯, 마치 다른 별에서 뚝 떨어지기라도 한 듯 코를 골고 있는 것이었다."]], [["“Oh, the irony of fate!”", "\"오, 운명의 농간이여!\""], ["cried Mitya, and, quite losing his head, he fell again to rousing the tipsy peasant.", "미탸가 소리쳤고, 완전히 이성을 잃은 채 다시금 술 취한 농부를 깨우는 데 달려들었다."], ["He roused him with a sort of ferocity, pulled at him, pushed him, even beat him; but after five minutes of vain exertions, he returned to his bench in helpless despair, and sat down.", "그는 일종의 광포함을 띠고 그를 흔들어 깨웠으니, 잡아당기고 떠밀고 심지어 두들겨 패기까지 했다. 그러나 오 분 동안 헛되이 용을 쓴 끝에, 그는 힘없는 절망에 잠겨 자기 긴 의자로 돌아와 주저앉았다."]], [["“Stupid!", "\"멍청한 놈!"], ["Stupid!”", "멍청한 놈!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“And how dishonorable it all is!”", "\"게다가 이게 다 얼마나 떳떳치 못한 짓이냐!\""], ["something made him add.", "무언가가 그로 하여금 이 말을 덧붙이게 했다."], ["His head began to ache horribly.", "머리가 끔찍하게 아프기 시작했다."], ["“Should he fling it up and go away altogether?”", "\"이 일을 내던지고 아주 떠나 버릴까?\""], ["he wondered.", "그는 자문했다."], ["“No, wait till to-morrow now.", "\"아니, 이제 내일까지 기다려야지."], ["I’ll stay on purpose.", "일부러라도 남아 있겠어."], ["What else did I come for?", "안 그러면 뭐 하러 왔단 말인가?"], ["Besides, I’ve no means of going.", "게다가 떠날 방도도 없잖아."], ["How am I to get away from here now?", "이제 와서 어떻게 여기를 벗어난단 말인가?"], ["Oh, the idiocy of it!”", "아, 이 멍청한 짓거리라니!\""]], [["But his head ached more and more.", "그러나 머리는 점점 더 아파 왔다."], ["He sat without moving, and unconsciously dozed off and fell asleep as he sat.", "그는 꼼짝도 않고 앉아 있다가 자기도 모르게 꾸벅꾸벅 졸더니 앉은 채로 잠이 들고 말았다."], ["He seemed to have slept for two hours or more.", "그렇게 두 시간 남짓 잔 듯했다."], ["He was waked up by his head aching so unbearably that he could have screamed.", "그는 머리가 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 아픈지 소리라도 지르고 싶을 지경이 되어 잠에서 깨어났다."], ["There was a hammering in his temples, and the top of his head ached.", "관자놀이가 쿵쾅거리고 정수리가 욱신거렸다."], ["It was a long time before he could wake up fully and understand what had happened to him.", "한참이 지나서야 그는 비로소 완전히 정신을 차리고 자기에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 깨달을 수 있었다."]], [["At last he realized that the room was full of charcoal fumes from the stove, and that he might die of suffocation.", "마침내 그는 방 안이 난로에서 새어 나온 숯 가스로 가득 차서 자칫 질식해 죽을 수도 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["And the drunken peasant still lay snoring.", "그리고 술 취한 농부는 여전히 코를 골며 누워 있었다."], ["The candle guttered and was about to go out.", "촛불은 가물거리며 막 꺼지려 하고 있었다."], ["Mitya cried out, and ran staggering across the passage into the forester’s room.", "미탸는 소리를 지르며 비틀비틀 통로를 가로질러 산지기 방으로 뛰어 들어갔다."], ["The forester waked up at once, but hearing that the other room was full of fumes, to Mitya’s surprise and annoyance, accepted the fact with strange unconcern, though he did go to see to it.", "산지기는 곧 잠에서 깨어났으나, 옆방이 가스로 가득 찼다는 말을 듣고도 미탸가 놀라고 짜증스럽게도 묘하게 무덤덤하게 그 사실을 받아들였으니, 그래도 살펴보러 가기는 했다."]], [["“But he’s dead, he’s dead!", "\"하지만 저 사람이 죽었다고요, 죽었어요!"], ["and ...", "그러면…"], ["what am I to do then?”", "난 대체 어떻게 하란 말입니까?\""], ["cried Mitya frantically.", "미탸가 미친 듯이 소리쳤다."]], [["They threw open the doors, opened a window and the chimney.", "그들은 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 창과 굴뚝을 텄다."], ["Mitya brought a pail of water from the passage.", "미탸는 통로에서 물 한 통을 길어 왔다."], ["First he wetted his own head, then, finding a rag of some sort, dipped it into the water, and put it on Lyagavy’s head.", "먼저 자기 머리를 적신 다음, 헝겊 조각 같은 것을 찾아내 물에 적셔서는 라가비의 머리에 얹었다."], ["The forester still treated the matter contemptuously, and when he opened the window said grumpily:", "산지기는 여전히 이 일을 대수롭잖게 여겼고, 창문을 열면서 퉁명스레 말했다."]], [["“It’ll be all right, now.”", "\"이제 괜찮을 겁니다.\""]], [["He went back to sleep, leaving Mitya a lighted lantern.", "그는 미탸에게 불 켜진 등을 남겨 두고 다시 자러 갔다."], ["Mitya fussed about the drunken peasant for half an hour, wetting his head, and gravely resolved not to sleep all night.", "미탸는 술 취한 농부를 두고 반 시간 동안 부산을 떨며 그의 머리를 적셨고, 밤새도록 자지 않으리라 굳게 마음먹었다."], ["But he was so worn out that when he sat down for a moment to take breath, he closed his eyes, unconsciously stretched himself full length on the bench and slept like the dead.", "그러나 그는 너무도 기진맥진한 터라, 잠깐 숨을 돌리려고 앉은 순간 두 눈이 감겼고, 자기도 모르게 긴 의자에 길게 몸을 뻗고 죽은 듯이 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["It was dreadfully late when he waked.", "그가 깨어났을 때는 끔찍이도 늦은 시각이었다."], ["It was somewhere about nine o’clock.", "아홉 시쯤이었다."], ["The sun was shining brightly in the two little windows of the hut.", "오두막의 자그마한 두 창문으로 햇살이 환하게 비쳐 들고 있었다."], ["The curly-headed peasant was sitting on the bench and had his coat on.", "곱슬머리 농부는 긴 의자에 앉아 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["He had another samovar and another bottle in front of him.", "그 앞에는 또 다른 사모바르와 또 다른 병이 놓여 있었다."], ["Yesterday’s bottle had already been finished, and the new one was more than half empty.", "어제의 병은 이미 비워졌고, 새 병도 절반 넘게 줄어 있었다."], ["Mitya jumped up and saw at once that the cursed peasant was drunk again, hopelessly and incurably.", "미탸는 벌떡 일어나, 그 빌어먹을 농부가 다시, 도무지 어찌해 볼 도리 없이 곤드레만드레 취해 있음을 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["He stared at him for a moment with wide opened eyes.", "그는 잠시 두 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜨고 그를 노려보았다."], ["The peasant was silently and slyly watching him, with insulting composure, and even a sort of contemptuous condescension, so Mitya fancied.", "농부는 모욕적인 침착함을 띠고, 미탸가 보기에는 심지어 일종의 경멸 어린 거드름까지 피우며 잠자코 능청스레 그를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["He rushed up to him.", "미탸는 그에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Excuse me, you see ...", "\"이보시오, 실은…"], ["I ...", "나는…"], ["you’ve most likely heard from the forester here in the hut.", "당신도 여기 오두막의 산지기한테서 들었겠지만."], ["I’m Lieutenant Dmitri Karamazov, the son of the old Karamazov whose copse you are buying.”", "나는 드미트리 카라마조프 중위요, 당신이 숲을 사들이고 있는 그 카라마조프 영감의 아들이란 말이오.\""]], [["“That’s a lie!”", "\"거짓말이군.\""], ["said the peasant, calmly and confidently.", "농부가 침착하고 자신만만하게 말했다."]], [["“A lie?", "\"거짓말이라니?"], ["You know Fyodor Pavlovitch?”", "당신 표도르 파블로비치를 알잖소?\""]], [["“I don’t know any of your Fyodor Pavlovitches,”", "\"당신네 표도르 파블로비치 따위는 난 모르오.\""], ["said the peasant, speaking thickly.", "농부가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다."]], [["“You’re bargaining with him for the copse, for the copse.", "\"당신은 그 사람하고 숲을, 그 숲을 두고 흥정하고 있잖소."], ["Do wake up, and collect yourself.", "좀 깨어나서 정신을 차려 보시오."], ["Father Pavel of Ilyinskoe brought me here.", "일리인스코예의 파벨 신부님이 나를 여기로 데려왔단 말이오."], ["You wrote to Samsonov, and he has sent me to you,”", "당신이 삼소노프한테 편지를 썼고, 그가 나를 당신한테 보냈잖소.\""], ["Mitya gasped breathlessly.", "미탸가 숨을 헐떡이며 다급하게 말했다."]], [["“You’re l-lying!”", "\"거, 거짓말이야!\""], ["Lyagavy blurted out again.", "라가비가 또다시 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["Mitya’s legs went cold.", "미탸는 다리가 싸늘해졌다."]], [["“For mercy’s sake!", "\"제발 좀!"], ["It isn’t a joke!", "이건 농담이 아니란 말이오!"], ["You’re drunk, perhaps.", "당신 취했나 본데."], ["Yet you can speak and understand ...", "그래도 말은 하고 알아듣잖소… 아니면…"], ["or else ... I understand nothing!”", "난 도무지 영문을 모르겠군!\""]], [["“You’re a painter!”", "\"당신은 환쟁이야!\""]], [["“For mercy’s sake!", "\"제발 좀!"], ["I’m Karamazov, Dmitri Karamazov.", "나는 카라마조프요, 드미트리 카라마조프란 말이오."], ["I have an offer to make you, an advantageous offer ...", "당신한테 제안할 게 있소, 솔깃한 제안이지…"], ["very advantageous offer, concerning the copse!”", "아주 솔깃한 제안이오, 그 숲에 관한!\""]], [["The peasant stroked his beard importantly.", "농부는 거드름을 피우며 수염을 쓰다듬었다."]], [["“No, you’ve contracted for the job and turned out a scamp.", "\"아니지, 당신은 일을 맡아 놓고 사기꾼으로 드러난 거야."], ["You’re a scoundrel!”", "당신은 악당이라고!\""]], [["“I assure you you’re mistaken,”", "\"맹세컨대 당신이 잘못 안 거요.\""], ["cried Mitya, wringing his hands in despair.", "미탸가 절망에 빠져 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다."], ["The peasant still stroked his beard, and suddenly screwed up his eyes cunningly.", "농부는 여전히 수염을 쓰다듬다가, 갑자기 교활하게 두 눈을 가늘게 떴다."]], [["“No, you show me this: you tell me the law that allows roguery.", "\"아니지, 그럼 어디 이걸 내게 보여 봐. 사기를 쳐도 된다는 법이 어디 있는지 말해 보란 말이야."], ["D’you hear?", "알아들어?"], ["You’re a scoundrel!", "당신은 악당이야!"], ["Do you understand that?”", "그걸 알아듣겠어?\""]], [["Mitya stepped back gloomily, and suddenly “something seemed to hit him on the head,” as he said afterwards.", "미탸는 침울하게 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 그 순간 그가 나중에 한 말마따나 \"무언가가 머리를 후려치는 것 같았다.\""], ["In an instant a light seemed to dawn in his mind, “a light was kindled and I grasped it all.”", "한순간에 그의 머릿속에 한 줄기 빛이 동트는 듯했으니, \"빛이 켜지면서 나는 모든 것을 깨달았다\"는 것이다."], ["He stood, stupefied, wondering how he, after all a man of intelligence, could have yielded to such folly, have been led into such an adventure, and have kept it up for almost twenty-four hours, fussing round this Lyagavy, wetting his head.", "그는 멍하니 선 채로, 명색이 분별 있는 인간이라는 자기가 어떻게 이런 어리석음에 굴복하고, 어떻게 이런 모험에 끌려들어, 거의 스물네 시간 동안이나 이 라가비를 둘러싸고 부산을 떨며 그 머리를 적셔 가면서까지 이 짓을 이어 왔는지 의아해했다."]], [["“Why, the man’s drunk, dead drunk, and he’ll go on drinking now for a week; what’s the use of waiting here?", "\"이런, 저 작자는 취했어, 곤죽이 되도록 취했고, 이제 일주일은 줄곧 마셔 댈 텐데. 여기서 기다린들 무슨 소용이람?"], ["And what if Samsonov sent me here on purpose?", "그리고 만일 삼소노프가 일부러 나를 여기로 보낸 거라면?"], ["What if she—?", "만일 그녀가…?"], ["Oh, God, what have I done?”", "오, 하느님, 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지?\""]], [["The peasant sat watching him and grinning.", "농부는 앉아서 그를 지켜보며 히죽거렸다."], ["Another time Mitya might have killed the fool in a fury, but now he felt as weak as a child.", "다른 때 같았으면 미탸는 격분하여 그 멍청이를 죽여 버렸을지도 모르나, 지금은 어린아이처럼 무력하게 느껴졌다."], ["He went quietly to the bench, took up his overcoat, put it on without a word, and went out of the hut.", "그는 조용히 긴 의자로 가서 외투를 집어 들고 한마디 말도 없이 걸쳐 입고는 오두막을 나섰다."], ["He did not find the forester in the next room; there was no one there.", "옆방에서 산지기는 보이지 않았으니, 거기엔 아무도 없었다."], ["He took fifty kopecks in small change out of his pocket and put them on the table for his night’s lodging, the candle, and the trouble he had given.", "그는 주머니에서 잔돈으로 오십 코페이카를 꺼내, 하룻밤 묵은 값과 촛값과 끼친 폐에 대한 값으로 탁자 위에 놓아두었다."], ["Coming out of the hut he saw nothing but forest all round.", "오두막을 나서자 사방에 온통 숲뿐이었다."], ["He walked at hazard, not knowing which way to turn out of the hut, to the right or to the left.", "그는 오두막에서 오른쪽으로 가야 할지 왼쪽으로 가야 할지도 모른 채 무작정 걸었다."], ["Hurrying there the evening before with the priest, he had not noticed the road.", "전날 저녁 사제와 함께 그리로 서둘러 오느라 길을 눈여겨보지 못했던 것이다."], ["He had no revengeful feeling for anybody, even for Samsonov, in his heart.", "그의 마음속에는 누구에 대해서도, 심지어 삼소노프에 대해서도 복수심이라곤 없었다."], ["He strode along a narrow forest path, aimless, dazed, without heeding where he was going.", "그는 좁다란 숲길을 따라 정처 없이, 얼떨떨한 채로, 어디로 가는지도 아랑곳없이 성큼성큼 걸었다."], ["A child could have knocked him down, so weak was he in body and soul.", "어린아이라도 그를 쓰러뜨릴 수 있었을 만큼, 그는 몸도 마음도 쇠약해져 있었다."], ["He got out of the forest somehow, however, and a vista of fields, bare after the harvest, stretched as far as the eye could see.", "그래도 그는 어찌어찌 숲을 빠져나왔고, 추수가 끝나 휑하게 드러난 들판이 눈길 닿는 데까지 멀리 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["“What despair!", "\"이 무슨 절망인가!"], ["What death all round!”", "사방이 온통 죽음뿐이로구나!\""], ["he repeated, striding on and on.", "그는 걷고 또 걸으며 거듭 중얼거렸다."]], [["He was saved by meeting an old merchant who was being driven across country in a hired trap.", "그는 세를 낸 마차를 타고 들판을 가로질러 가던 한 늙은 상인을 만난 덕분에 구원을 받았다."], ["When he overtook him, Mitya asked the way, and it turned out that the old merchant, too, was going to Volovya.", "그를 따라잡은 미탸가 길을 묻자, 그 늙은 상인 또한 볼로뱌로 가는 길임이 드러났다."], ["After some discussion Mitya got into the trap.", "얼마간 이야기를 주고받은 끝에 미탸는 그 마차에 올라탔다."], ["Three hours later they arrived.", "세 시간 뒤 그들은 도착했다."], ["At Volovya, Mitya at once ordered posting-horses to drive to the town, and suddenly realized that he was appallingly hungry.", "볼로뱌에서 미탸는 곧장 읍내로 갈 역마를 청해 두고는, 문득 자기가 무시무시하게 배고프다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["While the horses were being harnessed, an omelette was prepared for him.", "말에 마구를 채우는 동안 그를 위해 오믈렛이 마련되었다."], ["He ate it all in an instant, ate a huge hunk of bread, ate a sausage, and swallowed three glasses of vodka.", "그는 그것을 눈 깜짝할 새에 먹어 치웠고, 커다란 빵 한 덩이를 먹고 소시지를 먹고 보드카 석 잔을 들이켰다."], ["After eating, his spirits and his heart grew lighter.", "먹고 나자 기분이 한결 가벼워지고 마음도 누그러졌다."], ["He flew towards the town, urged on the driver, and suddenly made a new and “unalterable” plan to procure that “accursed money”", "그는 읍내를 향해 날 듯이 달리며 마부를 재촉했고, 문득 저녁이 되기 전에 그 \"빌어먹을 돈\"을 마련하겠다는 새롭고도 \"결코 번복할 수 없는\""], ["before evening.", "계획을 세웠다."], ["“And to think, only to think that a man’s life should be ruined for the sake of that paltry three thousand!”", "\"생각해 보면, 글쎄 생각해 보면 사람의 인생이 그까짓 하찮은 삼천 루블 때문에 망가져야 한다니!\""], ["he cried, contemptuously.", "그가 경멸조로 외쳤다."], ["“I’ll settle it to-day.”", "\"오늘 안에 결판을 내겠어.\""], ["And if it had not been for the thought of Grushenka and of what might have happened to her, which never left him, he would perhaps have become quite cheerful again....", "그리고 한순간도 그를 떠나지 않는 그루센카 생각과 그녀에게 무슨 일이 일어났을지도 모른다는 생각만 아니었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 다시 제법 명랑해졌을지도 모른다…"], ["But the thought of her was stabbing him to the heart every moment, like a sharp knife.", "그러나 그녀 생각은 시시각각 날카로운 칼처럼 그의 가슴을 찔러 댔다."]], [["At last they arrived, and Mitya at once ran to Grushenka.", "마침내 그들은 도착했고, 미탸는 곧장 그루센카에게로 달려갔다."]], [["# Chapter III. Gold-Mines", "# Chapter III. Gold-Mines"]], [["This was the visit of Mitya of which Grushenka had spoken to Rakitin with such horror.", "그것은 그루셴카가 라키친에게 그토록 진저리를 치며 이야기했던 미탸의 방문이었다."], ["She was just then expecting the “message,” and was much relieved that Mitya had not been to see her that day or the day before.", "그녀는 마침 그 \"전갈\"을 기다리고 있던 참이라, 미탸가 그날도 그 전날도 자기를 찾아오지 않은 것을 무척 다행으로 여기던 터였다."], ["She hoped that “please God he won’t come till I’m gone away,”", "그녀는 \"제발 내가 떠날 때까지 그 사람이 오지 않게 해 주십사\""], ["and he suddenly burst in on her. The rest we know already.", "하고 빌었건만, 그가 느닷없이 그녀에게 들이닥친 것이었다."], ["To get him off her hands she suggested at once that he should walk with her to Samsonov’s, where she said she absolutely must go “to settle his accounts,”", "그다음의 일은 우리도 이미 아는 바와 같다. 그를 떼어 놓으려고 그녀는 곧장 자기와 함께 삼소노프의 집까지 걸어가자고 제안했는데, 그곳에 가서 \"그의 셈을 정리해야\""], ["and when Mitya accompanied her at once, she said good-by to him at the gate, making him promise to come at twelve o’clock to take her home again.", "한다고 했고, 반드시 가야만 한다고 말했다. 미탸가 곧장 그녀를 따라나서자, 그녀는 대문 앞에서 작별을 고하며 열두 시에 다시 와서 자기를 집으로 데려다 달라고 약속을 받아 두었다."], ["Mitya, too, was delighted at this arrangement.", "미탸 또한 이 약속에 흡족해했다."], ["If she was sitting at Samsonov’s she could not be going to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, “if only she’s not lying,”", "그녀가 삼소노프의 집에 앉아 있다면 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 갈 수는 없을 테니 말이다. \"그저 그 여자가 거짓말만 하지 않는다면야.\""], ["he added at once.", "그는 곧바로 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["But he thought she was not lying from what he saw.", "그러나 자기가 본 바로는 그녀가 거짓말을 하는 것 같지 않다고 생각했다."]], [["He was that sort of jealous man who, in the absence of the beloved woman, at once invents all sorts of awful fancies of what may be happening to her, and how she may be betraying him, but, when shaken, heartbroken, convinced of her faithlessness, he runs back to her; at the first glance at her face, her gay, laughing, affectionate face, he revives at once, lays aside all suspicion and with joyful shame abuses himself for his jealousy.", "그는 사랑하는 여자가 곁에 없을 때면 당장에 그녀에게 무슨 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있을지, 그녀가 어떻게 자기를 배신하고 있을지 온갖 무서운 공상을 지어내면서도, 막상 마음이 흔들리고 가슴이 찢어지며 그녀의 부정을 확신하게 되면 다시 그녀에게로 달려가는 그런 부류의 질투하는 사내였다. 그녀의 얼굴을 처음 보는 순간, 그 명랑하고 웃음 띤 다정한 얼굴을 보는 순간 그는 단숨에 되살아나 모든 의심을 내려놓고는 기쁘면서도 부끄러운 마음으로 제 질투를 스스로 꾸짖는 것이었다."]], [["After leaving Grushenka at the gate he rushed home.", "그루셴카를 대문 앞에 남겨 두고 그는 집으로 달려갔다."], ["Oh, he had so much still to do that day!", "아아, 그날 그에게는 아직도 해야 할 일이 그토록 많았던 것이다!"], ["But a load had been lifted from his heart, anyway.", "그래도 어쨌든 가슴에서 짐 하나는 내려간 셈이었다."]], [["“Now I must only make haste and find out from Smerdyakov whether anything happened there last night, whether, by any chance, she went to Fyodor Pavlovitch; ough!”", "\"이제 서둘러서 스메르댜코프에게 어젯밤 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 혹시라도 그녀가 표도르 파블로비치한테 갔던 것은 아닌지 알아보기만 하면 된다, 어휴!\""], ["floated through his mind.", "이런 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."]], [["Before he had time to reach his lodging, jealousy had surged up again in his restless heart.", "그러나 그가 미처 하숙집에 다다르기도 전에 그 불안한 가슴속에는 또다시 질투가 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["Jealousy!", "질투라니!"], ["“Othello was not jealous, he was trustful,”", "\"오셀로는 질투하지 않았다, 그는 사람을 믿었던 것이다.\""], ["observed Pushkin.", "푸시킨은 이렇게 말했다."], ["And that remark alone is enough to show the deep insight of our great poet.", "그 한마디만으로도 우리의 위대한 시인이 지닌 깊은 통찰을 넉넉히 엿볼 수 있다."], ["Othello’s soul was shattered and his whole outlook clouded simply because his ideal was destroyed.", "오셀로의 영혼이 산산이 부서지고 그의 온 세계관이 흐려진 것은 다만 그의 이상이 파괴되었기 때문이었다."], ["But Othello did not begin hiding, spying, peeping.", "그러나 오셀로는 숨거나 엿보거나 몰래 살피기 시작하지는 않았다."], ["He was trustful, on the contrary.", "오히려 그는 사람을 믿었다."], ["He had to be led up, pushed on, excited with great difficulty before he could entertain the idea of deceit.", "그는 누가 이끌어 주고 떠밀어 주고 갖은 애를 써서 부추겨 준 뒤에야 비로소 배신이라는 생각을 품을 수 있었다."], ["The truly jealous man is not like that.", "진정으로 질투하는 사내는 그렇지 않다."], ["It is impossible to picture to oneself the shame and moral degradation to which the jealous man can descend without a qualm of conscience.", "질투하는 사내가 양심의 거리낌 하나 없이 빠져들 수 있는 수치와 도덕적 타락을 누구도 상상할 수 없을 것이다."], ["And yet it’s not as though the jealous were all vulgar and base souls.", "그렇다고 질투하는 자들이 모두 천박하고 비루한 영혼인 것도 아니다."], ["On the contrary, a man of lofty feelings, whose love is pure and full of self-sacrifice, may yet hide under tables, bribe the vilest people, and be familiar with the lowest ignominy of spying and eavesdropping.", "오히려 고귀한 감정을 지닌 사람, 순결하고 자기희생으로 가득 찬 사랑을 품은 사람도 식탁 밑에 숨고, 가장 더러운 자들을 매수하고, 염탐과 도청이라는 가장 천한 치욕에 익숙해질 수 있는 법이다."]], [["Othello was incapable of making up his mind to faithlessness—not incapable of forgiving it, but of making up his mind to it—though his soul was as innocent and free from malice as a babe’s.", "오셀로는 결코 부정을 받아들이기로 마음먹지 못했으니—용서하지 못한 것이 아니라 그것을 받아들이기로 마음먹지 못한 것이다—그의 영혼이 갓난아이처럼 순결하고 악의가 없었기 때문이다."], ["It is not so with the really jealous man.", "그러나 진정으로 질투하는 사내는 그렇지 않다."], ["It is hard to imagine what some jealous men can make up their mind to and overlook, and what they can forgive!", "어떤 질투하는 사내들이 무엇을 받아들이고 무엇을 못 본 체할 수 있으며, 또 무엇을 용서할 수 있는지는 상상조차 하기 어렵다!"], ["The jealous are the readiest of all to forgive, and all women know it.", "질투하는 자들이야말로 누구보다 선뜻 용서하는 자들이며, 이것은 모든 여자가 다 아는 사실이다."], ["The jealous man can forgive extraordinarily quickly (though, of course, after a violent scene), and he is able to forgive infidelity almost conclusively proved, the very kisses and embraces he has seen, if only he can somehow be convinced that it has all been “for the last time,” and that his rival will vanish from that day forward, will depart to the ends of the earth, or that he himself will carry her away somewhere, where that dreaded rival will not get near her.", "질투하는 사내는 (물론 한바탕 격렬한 소동을 치른 뒤이긴 하지만) 놀라우리만치 빨리 용서할 수 있으며, 거의 결정적으로 입증된 부정마저, 제 눈으로 본 입맞춤과 포옹마저 용서할 수 있으니, 다만 그 모든 것이 \"마지막\"이었으며 그날 이후로 연적이 사라져 땅끝으로 떠나 버리리라고, 혹은 자기 자신이 그녀를 어딘가 그 두려운 연적이 가까이 다가오지 못할 곳으로 데려가 버리리라고 어떻게든 확신할 수만 있다면 그렇게 한다."], ["Of course the reconciliation is only for an hour.", "물론 그 화해란 고작 한 시간짜리다."], ["For, even if the rival did disappear next day, he would invent another one and would be jealous of him.", "설령 연적이 다음 날 사라진다 해도 그는 또 다른 연적을 지어내어 그를 질투할 테니 말이다."], ["And one might wonder what there was in a love that had to be so watched over, what a love could be worth that needed such strenuous guarding.", "그렇게까지 감시해야 하는 사랑이라면 대체 그 안에 무엇이 들어 있는 것인지, 그토록 안간힘을 써서 지켜야 하는 사랑이 대체 무슨 값어치가 있는 것인지 의아할 법도 하다."], ["But that the jealous will never understand.", "그러나 그것을 질투하는 자는 결코 이해하지 못한다."], ["And yet among them are men of noble hearts.", "그런데도 그들 가운데는 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사내들이 있다."], ["It is remarkable, too, that those very men of noble hearts, standing hidden in some cupboard, listening and spying, never feel the stings of conscience at that moment, anyway, though they understand clearly enough with their “noble hearts” the shameful depths to which they have voluntarily sunk.", "게다가 주목할 만한 것은, 바로 그 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사내들이 어느 벽장 속에 숨어 귀를 기울이고 엿보면서도, 적어도 그 순간만은 양심의 가책을 조금도 느끼지 않는다는 점이니, 그들의 \"고귀한 마음\"으로도 자신이 스스로 가라앉은 그 수치스러운 나락이 어느 정도인지를 똑똑히 헤아리고 있으면서도 그러한 것이다."]], [["At the sight of Grushenka, Mitya’s jealousy vanished, and, for an instant he became trustful and generous, and positively despised himself for his evil feelings.", "그루셴카를 보는 순간 미탸의 질투는 사라졌고, 한순간 그는 사람을 믿는 너그러운 사내가 되어 자신의 사악한 감정을 진정으로 경멸했다."], ["But it only proved that, in his love for the woman, there was an element of something far higher than he himself imagined, that it was not only a sensual passion, not only the “curve of her body,” of which he had talked to Alyosha.", "그러나 그것은 다만, 그 여자를 향한 그의 사랑 속에 그 자신이 상상하던 것보다 훨씬 더 높은 무언가가 깃들어 있음을, 그것이 그저 관능적인 정념만이, 알료샤에게 늘어놓았던 그 \"몸의 곡선\"만이 아님을 증명할 따름이었다."], ["But, as soon as Grushenka had gone, Mitya began to suspect her of all the low cunning of faithlessness, and he felt no sting of conscience at it.", "그러나 그루셴카가 떠나자마자 미탸는 그녀가 부정한 여자의 온갖 저열한 술수를 부리고 있을 것이라 의심하기 시작했고, 그러면서도 양심의 가책이라곤 조금도 느끼지 않았다."]], [["And so jealousy surged up in him again.", "그리하여 그의 안에서 또다시 질투가 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["He had, in any case, to make haste.", "어쨌거나 그는 서둘러야만 했다."], ["The first thing to be done was to get hold of at least a small, temporary loan of money.", "먼저 해야 할 일은 적어도 얼마간이라도 임시로 돈을 빌리는 일이었다."], ["The nine roubles had almost all gone on his expedition.", "그 구 루블은 이번 여정에 거의 다 써 버린 터였다."], ["And, as we all know, one can’t take a step without money.", "그리고 누구나 알다시피, 돈 없이는 한 발짝도 움직일 수 없는 법이다."], ["But he had thought over in the cart where he could get a loan.", "그러나 그는 마차 안에서 어디서 돈을 빌릴 수 있을지 곰곰이 생각해 둔 바가 있었다."], ["He had a brace of fine dueling pistols in a case, which he had not pawned till then because he prized them above all his possessions.", "그에게는 상자에 든 훌륭한 결투용 권총 한 쌍이 있었는데, 그것은 자기 소유물 중에서 무엇보다 아끼는 물건이었기에 그때까지 전당포에 잡히지 않고 있던 것이었다."]], [["In the “Metropolis”", "그는 얼마 전 \"메트로폴리스\""], ["tavern he had some time since made acquaintance with a young official and had learnt that this very opulent bachelor was passionately fond of weapons.", "선술집에서 한 젊은 관리와 알게 되었고, 이 매우 부유한 독신 사내가 무기를 열렬히 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He used to buy pistols, revolvers, daggers, hang them on his wall and show them to acquaintances.", "그는 권총이며 회전식 연발총이며 단검을 사들여 벽에 걸어 두고 지인들에게 보여 주곤 했다."], ["He prided himself on them, and was quite a specialist on the mechanism of the revolver.", "그는 그것들을 자랑으로 여겼고, 연발총의 구조에 관해서는 어엿한 전문가였다."], ["Mitya, without stopping to think, went straight to him, and offered to pawn his pistols to him for ten roubles.", "미탸는 더 생각해 볼 것도 없이 곧장 그를 찾아가 권총을 십 루블에 잡혀 달라고 제안했다."], ["The official, delighted, began trying to persuade him to sell them outright.", "관리는 기뻐하며 아예 통째로 팔라고 설득하려 들었다."], ["But Mitya would not consent, so the young man gave him ten roubles, protesting that nothing would induce him to take interest.", "그러나 미탸가 응하지 않자, 젊은 사내는 십 루블을 내주면서 이자 따위는 결코 받지 않겠노라고 우겼다."], ["They parted friends.", "두 사람은 친구처럼 헤어졌다."]], [["Mitya was in haste; he rushed towards Fyodor Pavlovitch’s by the back way, to his arbor, to get hold of Smerdyakov as soon as possible.", "미탸는 서둘렀다. 그는 되도록 빨리 스메르댜코프를 붙들려고 표도르 파블로비치의 집 뒷길을 통해 그 정자 쪽으로 달려갔다."], ["In this way the fact was established that three or four hours before a certain event, of which I shall speak later on, Mitya had not a farthing, and pawned for ten roubles a possession he valued, though, three hours later, he was in possession of thousands....", "이리하여, 이제 곧 이야기할 어떤 사건이 일어나기 서너 시간 전만 해도 미탸에게는 한 푼도 없었으며, 자기가 아끼던 소유물을 십 루블에 잡혔다는 사실이 확인된 셈이다. 그러나 세 시간 뒤에 그는 수천 루블을 손에 쥐고 있었다……"], ["But I am anticipating.", "그러나 내가 너무 앞서 가고 있다."], ["From Marya Kondratyevna (the woman living near Fyodor Pavlovitch’s) he learned the very disturbing fact of Smerdyakov’s illness.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나(표도르 파블로비치의 집 근처에 사는 여자)에게서 그는 스메르댜코프가 병이 났다는 매우 걱정스러운 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["He heard the story of his fall in the cellar, his fit, the doctor’s visit, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s anxiety; he heard with interest, too, that his brother Ivan had set off that morning for Moscow.", "그가 지하실에서 굴러떨어진 이야기, 발작을 일으킨 일, 의사가 다녀간 일, 표도르 파블로비치가 노심초사하던 일을 들었으며, 또한 형 이반이 그날 아침 모스크바로 떠났다는 소식도 흥미롭게 들었다."]], [["“Then he must have driven through Volovya before me,”", "\"그렇다면 형이 나보다 먼저 볼로뱌를 지나갔겠군.\""], ["thought Dmitri, but he was terribly distressed about Smerdyakov.", "드미트리는 이렇게 생각했으나, 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 몹시 마음이 어지러웠다."], ["“What will happen now?", "\"이제 어떻게 되는 거지?"], ["Who’ll keep watch for me?", "누가 나를 위해 망을 봐 준단 말인가?"], ["Who’ll bring me word?” he thought.", "누가 내게 소식을 전해 준단 말인가?\""], ["He began greedily questioning the women whether they had seen anything the evening before.", "그는 이렇게 생각했다. 그는 전날 저녁에 무언가 본 것이 없느냐고 여자들에게 다급히 캐묻기 시작했다."], ["They quite understood what he was trying to find out, and completely reassured him.", "그들은 그가 무엇을 알아내려 하는지 충분히 알아차렸고, 그를 완전히 안심시켜 주었다."], ["No one had been there.", "아무도 거기 오지 않았다는 것이었다."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch had been there the night; everything had been perfectly as usual.", "이반 표도로비치가 밤에 거기 있었고, 모든 것이 평소와 다름없이 지나갔다고 했다."], ["Mitya grew thoughtful.", "미탸는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["He would certainly have to keep watch to-day, but where?", "그는 오늘 틀림없이 망을 봐야 할 텐데, 어디서 봐야 한단 말인가? 여기서?"], ["Here or at Samsonov’s gate?", "아니면 삼소노프의 대문 앞에서?"], ["He decided that he must be on the look out both here and there, and meanwhile ...", "그는 여기든 저기든 양쪽을 다 살피기로 마음먹었으나, 그러는 동안에……"], ["meanwhile....", "그러는 동안에……"], ["The difficulty was that he had to carry out the new plan that he had made on the journey back.", "문제는 돌아오는 길에 세운 새로운 계획을 실행에 옮겨야 한다는 것이었다."], ["He was sure of its success, but he must not delay acting upon it.", "그는 그 성공을 확신했으나, 지체 없이 행동에 나서야만 했다."], ["Mitya resolved to sacrifice an hour to it: “In an hour I shall know everything, I shall settle everything, and then, then, first of all to Samsonov’s.", "미탸는 거기에 한 시간을 바치기로 결심했다. \"한 시간이면 모든 것을 알게 되고 모든 것을 매듭지을 수 있을 테니, 그런 다음에, 그런 다음에 무엇보다 먼저 삼소노프의 집으로 가는 거다."], ["I’ll inquire whether Grushenka’s there and instantly be back here again, stay till eleven, and then to Samsonov’s again to bring her home.”", "그루셴카가 거기 있는지 알아보고는 곧장 다시 여기로 돌아와 열한 시까지 머물다가, 그런 뒤에 다시 삼소노프의 집으로 가서 그녀를 집으로 데려오는 거다.\""], ["This was what he decided.", "이것이 그가 내린 결정이었다."]], [["He flew home, washed, combed his hair, brushed his clothes, dressed, and went to Madame Hohlakov’s.", "그는 집으로 날아가 몸을 씻고 머리를 빗고 옷에 솔질을 한 다음, 옷을 차려입고 호흘라코바 부인의 집으로 갔다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["he had built his hopes on her.", "그는 그 부인에게 희망을 걸어 두었던 것이다."], ["He had resolved to borrow three thousand from that lady.", "그는 그 부인에게서 삼천 루블을 빌리기로 결심했다."], ["And what was more, he felt suddenly convinced that she would not refuse to lend it to him.", "게다가 그는 그녀가 결코 빌려 주기를 거절하지 않으리라는 확신이 문득 들었다."], ["It may be wondered why, if he felt so certain, he had not gone to her at first, one of his own sort, so to speak, instead of to Samsonov, a man he did not know, who was not of his own class, and to whom he hardly knew how to speak.", "그가 그토록 확신했다면, 어째서 처음부터 그녀, 말하자면 자기와 같은 부류의 사람에게 가지 않고, 알지도 못하는 데다 자기와 같은 계층도 아니어서 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지조차 막막한 삼소노프에게 갔던 것인지 의아할 법도 하다."]], [["But the fact was that he had never known Madame Hohlakov well, and had seen nothing of her for the last month, and that he knew she could not endure him.", "그러나 사실인즉 그는 호흘라코바 부인을 한 번도 제대로 알았던 적이 없었고, 지난 한 달 동안 그녀를 통 본 적도 없었으며, 게다가 그녀가 자기를 못 견뎌 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She had detested him from the first because he was engaged to Katerina Ivanovna, while she had, for some reason, suddenly conceived the desire that Katerina Ivanovna should throw him over, and marry the “charming, chivalrously refined Ivan, who had such excellent manners.”", "그녀는 처음부터 그를 몹시 싫어했는데, 그가 카테리나 이바노브나와 약혼한 사이였기 때문이었다. 그런데 그녀는 무슨 까닭에선지 문득, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그를 버리고 \"매력적이고 기사답게 세련된 데다 그토록 흠잡을 데 없는 예의범절을 갖춘 이반\"과 결혼했으면 하는 바람을 품게 된 것이었다."], ["Mitya’s manners she detested.", "미탸의 예의범절을 그녀는 몹시 싫어했다."], ["Mitya positively laughed at her, and had once said about her that she was just as lively and at her ease as she was uncultivated.", "미탸 또한 대놓고 그녀를 비웃었고, 한번은 그녀를 두고, 교양이 없는 만큼이나 활달하고 격의가 없는 여자라고 말한 적도 있었다."], ["But that morning in the cart a brilliant idea had struck him: “If she is so anxious I should not marry Katerina Ivanovna”", "그러나 그날 아침 마차 안에서 그에게 한 가지 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐던 것이다. \"그 여자가 내가 카테리나 이바노브나와 결혼하지 않기를 그토록 바란다면,\""], ["(and he knew she was positively hysterical upon the subject) “why should she refuse me now that three thousand, just to enable me to leave Katya and get away from her for ever.", "(그는 그녀가 이 문제에 관해서는 거의 신경질적일 정도임을 알고 있었다) \"내가 카탸를 떠나 영영 그녀에게서 벗어날 수 있도록 그 삼천 루블을 거절할 까닭이 어디 있겠는가."], ["These spoilt fine ladies, if they set their hearts on anything, will spare no expense to satisfy their caprice.", "응석받이로 자란 이런 귀부인들은 일단 무엇엔가 마음을 쏟으면 제 변덕을 채우기 위해 비용을 아끼지 않는 법이지. 게다가 그 여자는 어찌나 부유한가.\""], ["Besides, she’s so rich,” Mitya argued.", "미탸는 이렇게 따져 보았다."]], [["As for his “plan” it was just the same as before; it consisted of the offer of his rights to Tchermashnya—but not with a commercial object, as it had been with Samsonov, not trying to allure the lady with the possibility of making a profit of six or seven thousand—but simply as a security for the debt.", "그의 \"계획\"으로 말하자면 그것은 전과 똑같은 것이었으니, 곧 체르마쉬냐에 대한 자기 권리를 내놓겠다는 것이었다."], ["As he worked out this new idea, Mitya was enchanted with it, but so it always was with him in all his undertakings, in all his sudden decisions.", "다만 삼소노프에게 했던 것처럼 장삿속으로, 즉 육칠천 루블의 이익을 볼 가능성으로 부인을 꾀려는 것이 아니라, 그저 빚에 대한 담보로 내놓겠다는 것이었다. 이 새로운 착상을 다듬어 가면서 미탸는 그것에 매료되었으나, 그가 벌이는 모든 일, 그가 문득 내리는 모든 결정에서 늘 그러했듯이 이번에도 마찬가지였다."], ["He gave himself up to every new idea with passionate enthusiasm.", "그는 새로운 착상이 떠오를 때마다 그것에 열렬한 열정으로 빠져들었다."], ["Yet, when he mounted the steps of Madame Hohlakov’s house he felt a shiver of fear run down his spine.", "그러나 호흘라코바 부인의 집 계단을 올라설 때 그는 등줄기를 타고 두려움의 전율이 흘러내리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["At that moment he saw fully, as a mathematical certainty, that this was his last hope, that if this broke down, nothing else was left him in the world, but to “rob and murder some one for the three thousand.”", "그 순간 그는, 이것이 마지막 희망이며, 이것마저 무너지면 이 세상에 자기에게 남은 것이라곤 \"그 삼천 루블을 위해 누군가를 털고 죽이는\" 일밖에 없으리라는 것을, 수학적 확실성처럼 분명히 깨달았던 것이다."], ["It was half-past seven when he rang at the bell.", "그가 초인종을 울렸을 때는 일곱 시 반이었다."]], [["At first fortune seemed to smile upon him.", "처음에는 운이 그에게 미소를 짓는 듯했다."], ["As soon as he was announced he was received with extraordinary rapidity.", "그가 왔다는 전갈이 들어가자마자 그는 놀라우리만치 빠르게 맞아들여졌다."], ["“As though she were waiting for me,”", "\"마치 나를 기다리고 있었던 것 같군.\""], ["thought Mitya, and as soon as he had been led to the drawing-room, the lady of the house herself ran in, and declared at once that she was expecting him.", "미탸는 이렇게 생각했고, 응접실로 안내되자마자 안주인이 몸소 달려 나와 자기가 그를 기다리고 있었노라고 대뜸 선언했다."]], [["“I was expecting you!", "\"기다리고 있었어요!"], ["I was expecting you!", "기다리고 있었답니다!"], ["Though I’d no reason to suppose you would come to see me, as you will admit yourself.", "당신이 나를 찾아오리라 짐작할 만한 이유라곤 없었는데도 말이에요, 그건 당신 자신도 인정하실 테죠."], ["Yet, I did expect you.", "그런데도 나는 당신을 기다렸어요."], ["You may marvel at my instinct, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but I was convinced all the morning that you would come.”", "내 직감에 놀라셔도 좋아요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 하지만 나는 아침 내내 당신이 오리라고 확신하고 있었답니다.\""]], [["“That is certainly wonderful, madam,”", "\"그것 참 놀라운 일이로군요, 부인.\""], ["observed Mitya, sitting down limply, “but I have come to you on a matter of great importance....", "미탸는 맥없이 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"그러나 저는 대단히 중대한 일로 부인을 찾아왔습니다……"], ["On a matter of supreme importance for me, that is, madam ...", "제게는 더없이 중대한 일이지요, 부인…… 오직 저 혼자만의 일입니다……"], ["for me alone ... and I hasten—”", "그래서 서둘러—\""]], [["“I know you’ve come on most important business, Dmitri Fyodorovitch; it’s not a case of presentiment, no reactionary harking back to the miraculous (have you heard about Father Zossima?). This is a case of mathematics: you couldn’t help coming, after all that has passed with Katerina Ivanovna; you couldn’t, you couldn’t, that’s a mathematical certainty.”", "\"당신이 무척 중대한 용건으로 오셨다는 건 알아요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 이건 예감 같은 게 아니에요, 기적 따위로 되돌아가는 반동적인 일도 아니고요(조시마 장로 이야기는 들으셨나요?). 이건 수학의 문제예요. 카테리나 이바노브나와 그 모든 일이 있은 뒤이니, 당신은 오지 않을 수가 없었던 거예요. 오지 않을 수가, 오지 않을 수가 없었어요, 그건 수학적 확실성이랍니다.\""]], [["“The realism of actual life, madam, that’s what it is.", "\"실제 삶의 현실주의지요, 부인, 바로 그겁니다."], ["But allow me to explain—”", "그러나 제 말씀을 좀 들려 드리도록 해 주십시오—\""]], [["“Realism indeed, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"현실주의라, 그래요, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["I’m all for realism now.", "나는 이제 온통 현실주의 편이에요."], ["I’ve seen too much of miracles.", "기적이라면 지긋지긋하게 봐 왔으니까요."], ["You’ve heard that Father Zossima is dead?”", "조시마 장로께서 돌아가셨다는 소식은 들으셨나요?\""]], [["“No, madam, it’s the first time I’ve heard of it.”", "\"아니요, 부인. 처음 듣는 이야깁니다.\""], ["Mitya was a little surprised.", "미탸는 조금 놀랐다."], ["The image of Alyosha rose to his mind.", "알료샤의 모습이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Last night, and only imagine—”", "\"어젯밤이었어요, 그런데 글쎄 상상해 보세요—\""]], [["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["said Mitya, “I can imagine nothing except that I’m in a desperate position, and that if you don’t help me, everything will come to grief, and I first of all.", "미탸가 말했다. \"제가 절망적인 처지에 놓여 있다는 것, 그리고 부인께서 저를 도와주지 않으시면 모든 것이 파국에 이르고 무엇보다 저 자신이 먼저 그리되리라는 것 말고는 아무것도 상상할 수가 없습니다."], ["Excuse me for the triviality of the expression, but I’m in a fever—”", "표현이 변변찮은 것을 용서해 주십시오, 그러나 저는 지금 열병에 들떠 있어서—\""]], [["“I know, I know that you’re in a fever.", "\"알아요, 당신이 열병에 들떠 있다는 거 다 알아요."], ["You could hardly fail to be, and whatever you may say to me, I know beforehand.", "그러지 않을 수가 없겠지요. 당신이 내게 무슨 말씀을 하시든 나는 미리 다 알고 있어요."], ["I have long been thinking over your destiny, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I am watching over it and studying it....", "나는 오래전부터 당신의 운명을 곰곰이 생각해 왔답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 나는 그것을 지켜보고 연구하고 있어요……"], ["Oh, believe me, I’m an experienced doctor of the soul, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "오, 믿어 주세요, 나는 영혼의 노련한 의사랍니다, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""]], [["“Madam, if you are an experienced doctor, I’m certainly an experienced patient,”", "\"부인, 부인께서 노련한 의사시라면 저야말로 노련한 환자임에 틀림없지요.\" 미탸는 애써 정중하게 말했다."], ["said Mitya, with an effort to be polite, “and I feel that if you are watching over my destiny in this way, you will come to my help in my ruin, and so allow me, at least to explain to you the plan with which I have ventured to come to you ...", "\"그리고 부인께서 그처럼 제 운명을 지켜봐 주신다면, 제가 파멸하는 마당에 저를 도와주시리라 믿습니다, 그러니 적어도 제가 부인을 감히 찾아오게 만든 그 계획만이라도…… 그리고 부인께 바라는 것이 무엇인지만이라도 설명드릴 수 있게 해 주십시오……"], ["and what I am hoping of you.... I have come, madam—”", "저는 찾아왔습니다, 부인—\""]], [["“Don’t explain it.", "\"설명하실 것 없어요."], ["It’s of secondary importance.", "그건 그다지 중요한 일이 아니니까요."], ["But as for help, you’re not the first I have helped, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그런데 돕는 것으로 말하자면, 당신이 내가 도운 첫 번째 사람도 아니랍니다, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["You have most likely heard of my cousin, Madame Belmesov.", "내 사촌 벨메소바 부인 이야기는 아마 들으셨겠지요."], ["Her husband was ruined, ‘had come to grief,’ as you characteristically express it, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그 남편이 파산해서, 당신이 그 특유의 표현으로 말씀하셨듯이 '파국에 이르렀더랬어요', 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["I recommended him to take to horse-breeding, and now he’s doing well.", "내가 그 사람에게 말 사육을 해 보라고 권했더니, 지금은 아주 잘 지내고 있답니다."], ["Have you any idea of horse-breeding, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”", "당신은 말 사육에 대해 좀 아시나요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\""]], [["“Not the faintest, madam; ah, madam, not the faintest!”", "\"전혀, 조금도 모릅니다, 부인. 아, 부인, 정말이지 조금도 모릅니다!\""], ["cried Mitya, in nervous impatience, positively starting from his seat.", "미탸는 초조한 조바심에 사로잡혀 외쳤고, 실제로 자리에서 벌떡 일어날 뻔했다."], ["“I simply implore you, madam, to listen to me.", "\"그저 간청드리오니, 부인, 제 말씀을 좀 들어 주십시오."], ["Only give me two minutes of free speech that I may just explain to you everything, the whole plan with which I have come.", "그저 제가 모든 것을, 제가 가지고 온 계획 전부를 설명드릴 수 있도록 자유롭게 말할 이 분만 주십시오."], ["Besides, I am short of time.", "게다가 저는 시간이 없습니다."], ["I’m in a fearful hurry,”", "저는 무섭게 서두르고 있어요.\""], ["Mitya cried hysterically, feeling that she was just going to begin talking again, and hoping to cut her short.", "미탸는 그녀가 또다시 말을 시작하려는 것을 느끼고는 말을 끊어 보려는 심산으로 신경질적으로 외쳤다."], ["“I have come in despair ...", "\"저는 절망에 빠져 찾아왔습니다……"], ["in the last gasp of despair, to beg you to lend me the sum of three thousand, a loan, but on safe, most safe security, madam, with the most trustworthy guarantees!", "절망의 마지막 숨결에 매달려, 삼천 루블이라는 돈을 꾸어 주십사 간청드리러 온 것입니다, 빌리는 것입니다, 그러나 확실하고도 더없이 확실한 담보로, 부인, 가장 믿을 만한 보증을 걸고서 말입니다!"], ["Only let me explain—”", "그저 설명드리게만—\""]], [["“You must tell me all that afterwards, afterwards!”", "\"그건 다 나중에, 나중에 말씀하셔야 해요!\" 호흘라코바 부인은 이번에는 자기 차례라는 듯 손짓으로 침묵을 요구했다."], ["Madame Hohlakov with a gesture demanded silence in her turn, “and whatever you may tell me, I know it all beforehand; I’ve told you so already.", "\"당신이 무슨 말씀을 하시든 나는 미리 다 알고 있어요. 아까 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요."], ["You ask for a certain sum, for three thousand, but I can give you more, immeasurably more, I will save you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but you must listen to me.”", "당신은 일정한 금액을, 삼천 루블을 청하시지만, 나는 그보다 더, 헤아릴 수 없이 더 많은 것을 드릴 수 있어요. 내가 당신을 구해 드리겠어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그러나 당신은 내 말을 들으셔야 해요.\""]], [["Mitya started from his seat again.", "미탸는 또다시 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Madam, will you really be so good!”", "\"부인, 정말로 그토록 친절을 베풀어 주시는 겁니까!\""], ["he cried, with strong feeling.", "그는 북받치는 감정으로 외쳤다."], ["“Good God, you’ve saved me!", "\"맙소사, 부인께서 저를 구하셨군요!"], ["You have saved a man from a violent death, from a bullet....", "부인께서는 한 사내를 비참한 죽음에서, 총탄에서 구해 주신 겁니다……"], ["My eternal gratitude—”", "영원토록 감사드리겠—\""]], [["“I will give you more, infinitely more than three thousand!”", "\"삼천 루블보다 더, 한없이 더 많은 것을 드리겠어요!\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov, looking with a radiant smile at Mitya’s ecstasy.", "호흘라코바 부인은 미탸의 환희를 환한 미소로 바라보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Infinitely?", "\"한없이요?"], ["But I don’t need so much.", "하지만 저는 그렇게 많이는 필요 없습니다."], ["I only need that fatal three thousand, and on my part I can give security for that sum with infinite gratitude, and I propose a plan which—”", "저는 그저 그 운명적인 삼천 루블만 있으면 됩니다. 그리고 저로서는 한없는 감사와 함께 그 금액에 대한 담보를 드릴 수 있으며, 한 가지 계획을 제안드리는데 그것은—\""]], [["“Enough, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, it’s said and done.”", "\"그만하면 됐어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 말은 이미 끝났고 일은 다 된 거예요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov cut him short, with the modest triumph of beneficence: “I have promised to save you, and I will save you.", "호흘라코바 부인은 선행을 베푸는 자의 겸손한 승리감에 젖어 그의 말을 잘랐다. \"내가 당신을 구해 드리겠노라고 약속했으니, 구해 드리겠어요."], ["I will save you as I did Belmesov.", "벨메소프에게 해 준 것처럼 당신을 구해 드릴 거예요."], ["What do you think of the gold-mines, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”", "금광에 대해서는 어떻게 생각하세요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\""]], [["“Of the gold-mines, madam?", "\"금광 말입니까, 부인?"], ["I have never thought anything about them.”", "그런 건 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“But I have thought of them for you.", "\"하지만 나는 당신을 위해 그걸 생각해 왔어요."], ["Thought of them over and over again.", "몇 번이고 거듭 생각했답니다."], ["I have been watching you for the last month.", "나는 지난 한 달 동안 당신을 지켜봐 왔어요."], ["I’ve watched you a hundred times as you’ve walked past, saying to myself: that’s a man of energy who ought to be at the gold-mines.", "당신이 지나가는 모습을 백 번도 더 보면서 혼자 이렇게 되뇌었지요. 저 사람은 정력적인 사람이니 금광에 가 있어야 마땅하다고요."], ["I’ve studied your gait and come to the conclusion: that’s a man who would find gold.”", "나는 당신의 걸음걸이를 연구한 끝에 이런 결론에 이르렀답니다. 저 사람은 금을 찾아낼 사람이라고요.\""]], [["“From my gait, madam?”", "\"제 걸음걸이로 말입니까, 부인?\""], ["said Mitya, smiling.", "미탸는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, from your gait.", "\"그래요, 당신의 걸음걸이로요."], ["You surely don’t deny that character can be told from the gait, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?", "걸음걸이를 보면 사람의 성품을 알 수 있다는 걸 설마 부정하시진 않겠지요, 드미트리 표도로비치?"], ["Science supports the idea.", "과학이 그 견해를 뒷받침하고 있어요."], ["I’m all for science and realism now.", "나는 이제 온통 과학과 현실주의 편이랍니다."], ["After all this business with Father Zossima, which has so upset me, from this very day I’m a realist and I want to devote myself to practical usefulness.", "나를 그토록 뒤흔들어 놓은 조시마 장로의 그 모든 일을 겪고 나서, 바로 오늘부터 나는 현실주의자가 되어 실질적인 유익에 헌신하고 싶어요. 나는 다 나았답니다."], ["I’m cured. ‘Enough!’ as Turgenev says.”", "투르게네프의 말마따나 '이제 그만!'이지요.\""]], [["“But, madam, the three thousand you so generously promised to lend me—”", "\"하지만 부인, 부인께서 그토록 너그러이 꾸어 주마 약속하신 그 삼천 루블은—\""]], [["“It is yours, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"그건 당신 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov cut in at once.", "호흘라코바 부인은 곧장 그의 말을 잘랐다."], ["“The money is as good as in your pocket, not three thousand, but three million, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, in less than no time.", "\"그 돈은 이미 당신 호주머니에 든 거나 다름없어요, 삼천 루블이 아니라 삼백만 루블이, 그것도 눈 깜짝할 사이에 말이에요."], ["I’ll make you a present of the idea: you shall find gold-mines, make millions, return and become a leading man, and wake us up and lead us to better things.", "내가 당신께 그 착상을 선물로 드리지요. 당신은 금광을 찾아내어 수백만 루블을 벌고 돌아와 지도적인 인물이 되어, 우리를 일깨우고 더 나은 길로 이끌게 될 거예요."], ["Are we to leave it all to the Jews?", "우리가 그 모든 걸 유대인들 손에 내맡겨야 하겠어요?"], ["You will found institutions and enterprises of all sorts.", "당신은 온갖 기관과 사업을 세우게 될 거예요."], ["You will help the poor, and they will bless you.", "당신은 가난한 이들을 도울 것이고, 그들은 당신을 축복하겠지요."], ["This is the age of railways, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "지금은 철도의 시대예요, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["You’ll become famous and indispensable to the Department of Finance, which is so badly off at present.", "당신은 유명해질 것이고, 지금 그토록 형편이 어려운 재무부에 없어서는 안 될 사람이 될 거예요."], ["The depreciation of the rouble keeps me awake at night, Dmitri Fyodorovitch; people don’t know that side of me—”", "루블화의 가치 하락 때문에 나는 밤잠을 못 이룬답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치. 사람들은 나의 그런 면을 모르지요—\""]], [["“Madam, madam!”", "\"부인, 부인!\""], ["Dmitri interrupted with an uneasy presentiment.", "드미트리가 불안한 예감에 사로잡혀 말을 가로막았다."], ["“I shall indeed, perhaps, follow your advice, your wise advice, madam....", "\"정말이지 저는 부인의 충고를, 그 현명한 충고를 따를지도 모릅니다, 부인……"], ["I shall perhaps set off ...", "어쩌면 저는 떠날지도 모르지요……"], ["to the gold-mines....", "금광으로 말입니다……"], ["I’ll come and see you again about it ...", "그 일에 관해서는 다시 부인을 찾아뵙겠습니다……"], ["many times, indeed ...", "정말이지 여러 번이라도요……"], ["but now, that three thousand you so generously ...", "그러나 지금은, 부인께서 그토록 너그러이 약속하신 그 삼천 루블이……"], ["oh, that would set me free, and if you could to-day ...", "오, 그것이 저를 자유롭게 해 줄 텐데요, 오늘 안에 부인께서 해 주실 수만 있다면……"], ["you see, I haven’t a minute, a minute to lose to-day—”", "보십시오, 저는 오늘 일 분도, 일 분도 허비할 틈이 없습니다—\""]], [["“Enough, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, enough!”", "\"그만하면 됐어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그만하세요!\""], ["Madame Hohlakov interrupted emphatically.", "호흘라코바 부인이 단호하게 말을 가로막았다."], ["“The question is, will you go to the gold-mines or not; have you quite made up your mind?", "\"문제는, 당신이 금광에 가시겠느냐 안 가시겠느냐예요. 완전히 마음을 정하셨나요?"], ["Answer yes or no.”", "예, 아니요로 답해 주세요.\""]], [["“I will go, madam, afterwards....", "\"가겠습니다, 부인, 나중에요……"], ["I’ll go where you like ...", "부인께서 원하시는 곳이라면 어디든 가겠습니다……"], ["but now—”", "그러나 지금은—\""]], [["“Wait!”", "\"기다려요!\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov.", "호흘라코바 부인이 외쳤다."], ["And jumping up and running to a handsome bureau with numerous little drawers, she began pulling out one drawer after another, looking for something with desperate haste.", "그러고는 벌떡 일어나 작은 서랍이 잔뜩 달린 멋들어진 책상으로 달려가더니, 서랍을 하나씩 차례로 잡아 빼면서 무언가를 미친 듯이 허둥지둥 찾기 시작했다."]], [["“The three thousand,”", "\"삼천 루블이다.\""], ["thought Mitya, his heart almost stopping, “and at the instant ...", "미탸는 심장이 멎을 것만 같은 심정으로 생각했다. \"게다가 당장……"], ["without any papers or formalities ...", "무슨 서류나 절차도 없이……"], ["that’s doing things in gentlemanly style!", "이거야말로 신사답게 일을 처리하는 방식이로군!"], ["She’s a splendid woman, if only she didn’t talk so much!”", "저 부인은 훌륭한 여자야, 그저 말만 그렇게 많이 하지 않는다면 말이지!\""]], [["“Here!”", "\"여기 있어요!\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov, running back joyfully to Mitya, “here is what I was looking for!”", "호흘라코바 부인이 미탸에게 기쁘게 달려오며 외쳤다. \"내가 찾던 게 여기 있네요!\""]], [["It was a tiny silver ikon on a cord, such as is sometimes worn next the skin with a cross.", "그것은 끈에 매단 자그마한 은제 성상으로, 때로는 십자가와 함께 살갗에 닿게 걸치곤 하는 그런 종류의 것이었다."]], [["“This is from Kiev, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"이건 키예프에서 가져온 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["she went on reverently, “from the relics of the Holy Martyr, Varvara.", "그녀는 경건하게 말을 이었다. \"거룩한 순교자 바르바라의 성유물에서 나온 것이랍니다."], ["Let me put it on your neck myself, and with it dedicate you to a new life, to a new career.”", "내가 손수 당신 목에 걸어 드리지요, 그리고 이것과 더불어 당신을 새로운 삶에, 새로운 길에 봉헌하겠어요.\""]], [["And she actually put the cord round his neck, and began arranging it.", "그러고는 실제로 그 끈을 그의 목에 둘러 매무새를 다듬기 시작했다."], ["In extreme embarrassment, Mitya bent down and helped her, and at last he got it under his neck-tie and collar through his shirt to his chest.", "미탸는 몹시 당혹스러워하며 몸을 숙여 그녀를 도왔고, 마침내 그것을 넥타이와 옷깃 밑으로, 셔츠를 지나 가슴께까지 집어넣었다."]], [["“Now you can set off,”", "\"이제 떠나셔도 돼요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov pronounced, sitting down triumphantly in her place again.", "호흘라코바 부인이 다시 의기양양하게 제자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Madam, I am so touched.", "\"부인, 저는 정말 감동했습니다."], ["I don’t know how to thank you, indeed ...", "어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 모르겠습니다, 정말이지……"], ["for such kindness, but ...", "이런 친절을 베풀어 주시니, 하지만……"], ["If only you knew how precious time is to me....", "제게 시간이 얼마나 소중한지 부인께서 아신다면……"], ["That sum of money, for which I shall be indebted to your generosity....", "부인의 너그러움에 신세를 지게 될 그 돈 말입니다……"], ["Oh, madam, since you are so kind, so touchingly generous to me,”", "오, 부인, 부인께서 제게 이토록 친절하시고 이토록 가슴 뭉클하게 너그러우시니,\""], ["Mitya exclaimed impulsively, “then let me reveal to you ...", "미탸는 충동적으로 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 부인께 털어놓겠습니다……"], ["though, of course, you’ve known it a long time ...", "물론 부인께서는 오래전부터 알고 계셨겠지만……"], ["that I love somebody here....", "제가 이 고장의 어떤 여인을 사랑하고 있다는 사실을요……"], ["I have been false to Katya ...", "저는 카탸를 배신했습니다……"], ["Katerina Ivanovna I should say....", "카테리나 이바노브나라고 해야겠군요……"], ["Oh, I’ve behaved inhumanly, dishonorably to her, but I fell in love here with another woman ...", "오, 저는 그녀에게 비인간적으로, 명예롭지 못하게 굴었습니다, 그러나 저는 이곳에서 다른 여인을 사랑하게 되었습니다……"], ["a woman whom you, madam, perhaps, despise, for you know everything already, but whom I cannot leave on any account, and therefore that three thousand now—”", "부인께서는, 부인, 어쩌면 경멸하실지도 모를 여인을, 부인께서는 이미 모든 것을 다 알고 계시니까요, 그러나 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 그 여인을 떠날 수 없는 까닭에, 그 삼천 루블이 지금—\""]], [["“Leave everything, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"모든 걸 떠나세요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov interrupted in the most decisive tone.", "호흘라코바 부인이 더없이 단호한 어조로 말을 가로막았다."], ["“Leave everything, especially women.", "\"모든 걸 떠나세요, 특히 여자들을요."], ["Gold-mines are your goal, and there’s no place for women there.", "금광이 당신의 목표이고, 거기에 여자가 들어설 자리는 없어요."], ["Afterwards, when you come back rich and famous, you will find the girl of your heart in the highest society.", "나중에 부유하고 유명해져서 돌아오면, 당신은 가장 높은 사교계에서 당신 마음에 드는 처녀를 찾게 될 거예요."], ["That will be a modern girl, a girl of education and advanced ideas.", "그 처녀는 현대적인 처녀, 교양과 진보적인 사상을 갖춘 처녀일 거예요."], ["By that time the dawning woman question will have gained ground, and the new woman will have appeared.”", "그때쯤이면 이제 막 동트기 시작한 여성 문제가 자리를 잡았을 테고, 새로운 여성이 나타나 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Madam, that’s not the point, not at all....”", "\"부인, 그게 핵심이 아닙니다, 전혀 아니에요…….\""], ["Mitya clasped his hands in entreaty.", "미탸는 간청하듯 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Yes, it is, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, just what you need; the very thing you’re yearning for, though you don’t realize it yourself.", "\"아니에요, 바로 그거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 바로 당신께 필요한 거예요. 당신 자신은 깨닫지 못하고 있지만, 바로 당신이 갈망하고 있는 그거란 말이에요."], ["I am not at all opposed to the present woman movement, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "나는 오늘날의 여성 운동에 결코 반대하지 않아요, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["The development of woman, and even the political emancipation of woman in the near future—that’s my ideal.", "여성의 발전, 그리고 머지않은 장래에 이루어질 여성의 정치적 해방까지—그것이 내 이상이랍니다. 나에게도 딸이 하나 있어요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 사람들은 나의 그런 면을 모르지요."], ["I’ve a daughter myself, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, people don’t know that side of me.", "나는 그 문제에 관해 작가 시체드린에게 편지를 한 통 보낸 적이 있어요."], ["I wrote a letter to the author, Shtchedrin, on that subject.", "그분은 여성의 사명에 관해 내게 참 많은 것을, 정말 많은 것을 가르쳐 주셨거든요."], ["He has taught me so much, so much about the vocation of woman.", "그래서 작년에 나는 그분께 두 줄짜리 익명의 편지를 보냈답니다."], ["So last year I sent him an anonymous letter of two lines: ‘I kiss and embrace you, my teacher, for the modern woman.", "'현대 여성을 위하여, 스승이신 당신께 입맞춤과 포옹을 보냅니다. 부디 굽히지 마소서.'"], ["Persevere.’ And I signed myself, ‘A Mother.’ I thought of signing myself ‘A contemporary Mother,’ and hesitated, but I stuck to the simple ‘Mother’; there’s more moral beauty in that, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그러고는 '한 어머니로부터'라고 서명했지요. '한 동시대의 어머니로부터'라고 서명할까 망설이다가, 결국 그냥 '어머니'로 했어요."], ["And the word ‘contemporary’ might have reminded him of ‘The Contemporary’—a painful recollection owing to the censorship....", "그편이 도덕적으로 더 아름답거든요, 드미트리 표도로비치. 게다가 '동시대'라는 말은 그분께 '동시대인'을—검열 때문에 떠올리기 괴로운 기억을—떠올리게 했을지도 모르니까요……"], ["Good Heavens, what is the matter!”", "맙소사, 대체 무슨 일이세요!\""]], [["“Madam!”", "\"부인!\""], ["cried Mitya, jumping up at last, clasping his hands before her in helpless entreaty.", "미탸가 마침내 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 그녀 앞에서 어쩔 줄 모르고 두 손을 맞잡아 간청했다."], ["“You will make me weep if you delay what you have so generously—”", "\"부인께서 그토록 너그러이 약속하신 것을 미루신다면 저는 울고 말 겁니다—\""]], [["“Oh, do weep, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, do weep!", "\"오, 우세요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 어서 우세요!"], ["That’s a noble feeling ...", "그건 고귀한 감정이에요……"], ["such a path lies open before you!", "당신 앞에는 그토록 활짝 열린 길이 있는걸요!"], ["Tears will ease your heart, and later on you will return rejoicing.", "눈물은 당신의 마음을 편하게 해 줄 거예요, 그리고 나중에 당신은 기뻐하며 돌아오겠지요."], ["You will hasten to me from Siberia on purpose to share your joy with me—”", "당신은 그 기쁨을 나와 나누려고 일부러 시베리아에서부터 내게로 달려올 거예요—\""]], [["“But allow me, too!” Mitya cried suddenly.", "\"하지만 저에게도 말할 기회를 좀 주십시오!\""], ["“For the last time I entreat you, tell me, can I have the sum you promised me to-day, if not, when may I come for it?”", "미탸가 느닷없이 외쳤다. \"마지막으로 간청드립니다, 말씀해 주십시오, 부인께서 약속하신 그 돈을 제가 오늘 받을 수 있습니까, 그게 안 된다면 언제 받으러 와야 합니까?\""]], [["“What sum, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?”", "\"무슨 돈 말인가요, 드미트리 표도로비치?\""]], [["“The three thousand you promised me ...", "\"부인께서 약속하신 그 삼천 루블 말입니다……"], ["that you so generously—”", "그토록 너그러이—\""]], [["“Three thousand?", "\"삼천 루블이요?"], ["Roubles?", "루블 말씀이세요?"], ["Oh, no, I haven’t got three thousand,”", "오, 아니에요, 나에게는 삼천 루블이 없는걸요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov announced with serene amazement.", "호흘라코바 부인은 태연하게 놀라워하며 선언했다."], ["Mitya was stupefied.", "미탸는 얼이 빠지고 말았다."]], [["“Why, you said just now ...", "\"아니, 방금 말씀하시기를……"], ["you said ...", "말씀하시기를……"], ["you said it was as good as in my hands—”", "그것이 제 손에 든 거나 다름없다고 하셨잖습니까—\""]], [["“Oh, no, you misunderstood me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"오, 아니에요, 당신이 나를 오해하신 거예요, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["In that case you misunderstood me.", "그렇다면 당신이 나를 오해하신 거죠."], ["I was talking of the gold-mines.", "나는 금광 이야기를 하고 있었던 거예요."], ["It’s true I promised you more, infinitely more than three thousand, I remember it all now, but I was referring to the gold-mines.”", "당신께 그보다 더, 삼천 루블보다 한없이 더 많은 것을 약속한 건 사실이에요, 이제 다 기억이 나는군요, 하지만 나는 금광을 두고 한 말이었답니다.\""]], [["“But the money?", "\"하지만 돈은요?"], ["The three thousand?”", "그 삼천 루블은요?\""], ["Mitya exclaimed, awkwardly.", "미탸가 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, if you meant money, I haven’t any.", "\"오, 돈을 말씀하시는 거라면, 나에게는 한 푼도 없어요."], ["I haven’t a penny, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "나에게는 땡전 한 푼 없답니다, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["I’m quarreling with my steward about it, and I’ve just borrowed five hundred roubles from Miüsov, myself.", "그 일로 지금 집사와 다투고 있는 중이고, 나 자신도 방금 미우소프에게서 오백 루블을 빌렸는걸요."], ["No, no, I’ve no money.", "아니, 아니에요, 나에게는 돈이 없어요."], ["And, do you know, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, if I had, I wouldn’t give it to you.", "그리고 말이지요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 설령 내게 돈이 있다 한들 당신께는 드리지 않겠어요."], ["In the first place I never lend money.", "우선 첫째로 나는 결코 돈을 빌려 주지 않아요."], ["Lending money means losing friends.", "돈을 빌려 준다는 건 친구를 잃는다는 뜻이니까요."], ["And I wouldn’t give it to you particularly.", "게다가 당신께는 더더욱 드리지 않을 거예요."], ["I wouldn’t give it you, because I like you and want to save you, for all you need is the gold-mines, the gold-mines, the gold-mines!”", "당신을 좋아하고 당신을 구하고 싶어서 드리지 않겠다는 거예요, 당신께 필요한 건 오직 금광, 금광, 금광뿐이니까요!\""]], [["“Oh, the devil!”", "\"오, 빌어먹을!\""], ["roared Mitya, and with all his might brought his fist down on the table.", "미탸는 고함을 지르며 있는 힘껏 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳤다."]], [["“Aie! Aie!”", "\"아이고!"], ["cried Madame Hohlakov, alarmed, and she flew to the other end of the drawing-room.", "아이고!\" 호흘라코바 부인이 기겁하여 외치며 응접실 저쪽 끝으로 달아났다."]], [["Mitya spat on the ground, and strode rapidly out of the room, out of the house, into the street, into the darkness!", "미탸는 바닥에 침을 뱉고는 성큼성큼 방을 빠져나가 집을 나서서, 거리로, 어둠 속으로 나섰다!"], ["He walked like one possessed, and beating himself on the breast, on the spot where he had struck himself two days previously, before Alyosha, the last time he saw him in the dark, on the road.", "그는 무언가에 홀린 사람처럼 걸으며 가슴을, 이틀 전 알료샤 앞에서, 마지막으로 그를 보았던 그 어두운 길 위에서 스스로 내리쳤던 바로 그 자리를 주먹으로 쳤다."], ["What those blows upon his breast signified, on that spot, and what he meant by it—that was, for the time, a secret which was known to no one in the world, and had not been told even to Alyosha.", "가슴팍 그 자리를 친 그 주먹질이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그가 그것으로 무엇을 말하려 했는지는 그때까지만 해도 이 세상 누구도 알지 못하는 비밀이었으니, 알료샤에게조차 털어놓지 않은 것이었다."], ["But that secret meant for him more than disgrace; it meant ruin, suicide.", "그러나 그 비밀은 그에게 치욕 이상의 것을, 곧 파멸과 자살을 뜻했다."], ["So he had determined, if he did not get hold of the three thousand that would pay his debt to Katerina Ivanovna, and so remove from his breast, from that spot on his breast, the shame he carried upon it, that weighed on his conscience.", "카테리나 이바노브나에게 진 빚을 갚아 줄 그 삼천 루블을 손에 넣지 못한다면, 그리하여 자기 가슴에서, 가슴팍 그 자리에서, 양심을 짓누르는 그 짊어진 수치를 떨쳐 내지 못한다면, 그렇게 하기로 그는 작정해 두었던 것이다."], ["All this will be fully explained to the reader later on, but now that his last hope had vanished, this man, so strong in appearance, burst out crying like a little child a few steps from the Hohlakovs’ house.", "이 모든 것은 나중에 독자에게 충분히 설명될 테지만, 지금 마지막 희망마저 사라지자, 겉보기에 그토록 건장하던 이 사내는 호흘라코프 댁에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다."], ["He walked on, and not knowing what he was doing, wiped away his tears with his fist.", "그는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지도 모른 채 걸으며 주먹으로 눈물을 훔쳤다."], ["In this way he reached the square, and suddenly became aware that he had stumbled against something.", "그렇게 하여 그는 광장에 이르렀고, 문득 무언가에 부딪혔다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["He heard a piercing wail from an old woman whom he had almost knocked down.", "그가 하마터면 쓰러뜨릴 뻔한 어느 노파의 째지는 듯한 비명이 들려왔다."]], [["“Good Lord, you’ve nearly killed me!", "\"이런 맙소사, 사람 잡을 뻔했네!"], ["Why don’t you look where you’re going, scapegrace?”", "어디 눈을 어디다 두고 다니는 거야, 이 망나니야?\""]], [["“Why, it’s you!”", "\"아니, 당신이군!\""], ["cried Mitya, recognizing the old woman in the dark.", "미탸가 어둠 속에서 그 노파를 알아보고 외쳤다."], ["It was the old servant who waited on Samsonov, whom Mitya had particularly noticed the day before.", "그녀는 삼소노프의 시중을 드는 그 늙은 하녀로, 미탸가 전날 유심히 눈여겨봐 둔 여자였다."]], [["“And who are you, my good sir?”", "\"그런데 댁은 뉘시오, 나리?\""], ["said the old woman, in quite a different voice.", "노파가 사뭇 다른 목소리로 물었다."], ["“I don’t know you in the dark.”", "\"어두워서 댁을 못 알아보겠구려.\""]], [["“You live at Kuzma Kuzmitch’s.", "\"당신은 쿠즈마 쿠즈미치 댁에 살지요."], ["You’re the servant there?”", "거기 하녀 아니오?\""]], [["“Just so, sir, I was only running out to Prohoritch’s....", "\"그렇습죠, 나리, 잠깐 프로호리치 댁에 다녀오려던 참이었어요……"], ["But I don’t know you now.”", "그래도 영 댁을 못 알아보겠네요.\""]], [["“Tell me, my good woman, is Agrafena Alexandrovna there now?”", "\"여보시오, 말 좀 해 주시오, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 지금 거기 있소?\""], ["said Mitya, beside himself with suspense.", "미탸가 초조함에 제정신이 아닌 채로 물었다."], ["“I saw her to the house some time ago.”", "\"내가 좀 전에 그 댁까지 바래다주었는데 말이오.\""]], [["“She has been there, sir.", "\"거기 와 계셨더랬죠, 나리."], ["She stayed a little while, and went off again.”", "잠깐 머무시더니 또 가셨어요.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Went away?”", "가 버렸다고?\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“When did she go?”", "\"언제 갔소?\""]], [["“Why, as soon as she came.", "\"아니, 오시자마자요."], ["She only stayed a minute.", "그저 잠깐 계셨을 뿐인걸요."], ["She only told Kuzma Kuzmitch a tale that made him laugh, and then she ran away.”", "쿠즈마 쿠즈미치께 우스운 이야기 하나를 들려 드려서 그분을 웃기시고는, 곧장 달아나듯 가 버리셨어요.\""]], [["“You’re lying, damn you!”", "\"거짓말 마, 이 빌어먹을 할멈아!\""], ["roared Mitya.", "미탸가 고함쳤다."]], [["“Aie!", "\"아이고!"], ["Aie!”", "아이고!\""], ["shrieked the old woman, but Mitya had vanished.", "노파가 비명을 질렀으나, 미탸는 이미 사라지고 없었다."]], [["He ran with all his might to the house where Grushenka lived.", "그는 그루셴카가 사는 집으로 있는 힘껏 달려갔다."], ["At the moment he reached it, Grushenka was on her way to Mokroe.", "그가 그곳에 다다른 바로 그 순간, 그루셴카는 모크로예로 가는 길이었다."], ["It was not more than a quarter of an hour after her departure.", "그녀가 떠난 지 채 십오 분도 지나지 않은 때였다."]], [["Fenya was sitting with her grandmother, the old cook, Matryona, in the kitchen when “the captain” ran in.", "페냐는 부엌에서 자기 할머니인 늙은 식모 마트료나와 함께 앉아 있었는데, 그때 \"그 대위\"가 뛰어 들어왔다."], ["Fenya uttered a piercing shriek on seeing him.", "페냐는 그를 보고 째지는 듯한 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“You scream?”", "\"비명을 지르다니?\""], ["roared Mitya, “where is she?”", "미탸가 고함쳤다. \"그 여자는 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["But without giving the terror-stricken Fenya time to utter a word, he fell all of a heap at her feet.", "그러나 공포에 질린 페냐가 한마디 내뱉을 틈도 주지 않고 그는 그녀의 발치에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Fenya, for Christ’s sake, tell me, where is she?”", "\"페냐, 제발 부탁이다, 말해 다오, 그 여자는 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"몰라요."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch, my dear, I don’t know.", "드미트리 표도로비치, 나리, 정말 몰라요."], ["You may kill me but I can’t tell you.”", "절 죽이셔도 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\""], ["Fenya swore and protested.", "페냐는 맹세하며 항변했다."], ["“You went out with her yourself not long ago—”", "\"조금 전에 나리께서 직접 그분이랑 같이 나가셨잖아요—\""]], [["“She came back!”", "\"그 여자가 돌아왔어!\""]], [["“Indeed she didn’t.", "\"정말 안 돌아오셨어요."], ["By God I swear she didn’t come back.”", "하느님께 맹세코 안 돌아오셨다니까요.\""]], [["“You’re lying!”", "\"거짓말 마!\""], ["shouted Mitya.", "미탸가 고함쳤다."], ["“From your terror I know where she is.”", "\"네가 무서워하는 걸 보니 그 여자가 어디 있는지 알겠다.\""]], [["He rushed away.", "그는 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다."], ["Fenya in her fright was glad she had got off so easily.", "페냐는 겁에 질린 채로 이렇게 쉽게 벗어난 것을 다행으로 여겼다."], ["But she knew very well that it was only that he was in such haste, or she might not have fared so well.", "그러나 그녀는, 그것이 다만 그가 그토록 서둘렀기 때문일 뿐, 그러지 않았더라면 자기가 이렇게 무사하지 못했을지도 모른다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["But as he ran, he surprised both Fenya and old Matryona by an unexpected action.", "그런데 그는 달려 나가면서 뜻밖의 행동으로 페냐와 늙은 마트료나를 둘 다 놀라게 했다."], ["On the table stood a brass mortar, with a pestle in it, a small brass pestle, not much more than six inches long.", "탁자 위에는 절굿공이가 꽂힌 놋쇠 절구가 놓여 있었는데, 그것은 길이가 여섯 치 남짓밖에 안 되는 자그마한 놋쇠 절굿공이였다."], ["Mitya already had opened the door with one hand when, with the other, he snatched up the pestle, and thrust it in his side-pocket.", "미탸는 한 손으로 이미 문을 연 채로, 다른 한 손으로 그 절굿공이를 낚아채어 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."]], [["“Oh, Lord!", "\"맙소사!"], ["He’s going to murder some one!”", "저 양반이 누굴 죽이려나 봐!\""], ["cried Fenya, flinging up her hands.", "페냐가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 외쳤다."]], [["# Chapter IV. In The Dark", "# Chapter IV. In The Dark"]], [["Where was he running?", "그는 어디로 달려가고 있었던가?"], ["“Where could she be except at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s?", "\"그녀가 표도르 파블로비치의 집 말고 또 어디에 있겠는가?"], ["She must have run straight to him from Samsonov’s, that was clear now.", "그녀는 삼소노프의 집에서 곧장 그에게로 달려간 것이 틀림없다, 이제는 그것이 분명해졌다."], ["The whole intrigue, the whole deceit was evident.”", "이 음모 전체가, 이 기만 전체가 환히 드러난 것이다.\" ..."], ["... It all rushed whirling through his mind.", "이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속에서 소용돌이치며 휘몰아쳤다."], ["He did not run to Marya Kondratyevna’s.", "그는 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 집으로 달려가지 않았다."], ["“There was no need to go there ...", "\"그곳에 갈 필요는 없다 ..."], ["not the slightest need ...", "조금도 그럴 필요가 없다 ..."], ["he must raise no alarm ...", "소란을 피워서는 안 된다 ..."], ["they would run and tell directly....", "그러면 그들이 즉시 달려가 일러바칠 것이다...."], ["Marya Kondratyevna was clearly in the plot, Smerdyakov too, he too, all had been bought over!”", "마리야 콘드라티예브나도 이 음모에 가담한 것이 분명하고, 스메르댜코프도, 그놈도, 모두가 매수당한 것이다!\""]], [["He formed another plan of action: he ran a long way round Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house, crossing the lane, running down Dmitrovsky Street, then over the little bridge, and so came straight to the deserted alley at the back, which was empty and uninhabited, with, on one side the hurdle fence of a neighbor’s kitchen-garden, on the other the strong high fence, that ran all round Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden.", "그는 다른 행동 계획을 세웠다. 표도르 파블로비치의 집을 멀찍이 빙 돌아, 골목을 가로질러 드미트롭스키 거리를 따라 내려간 다음 작은 다리를 건너, 그렇게 해서 집 뒤편의 인적 없는 좁은 길로 곧장 나왔는데, 그 길은 텅 비어 사람이 살지 않았으며, 한쪽에는 이웃집 채마밭의 섶나무 울타리가, 다른 한쪽에는 표도르 파블로비치의 정원을 빙 둘러 친 튼튼하고 높은 울타리가 서 있었다."], ["Here he chose a spot, apparently the very place, where according to the tradition, he knew Lizaveta had once climbed over it: “If she could climb over it,”", "여기서 그는 한 자리를 골랐는데, 보아하니 바로 그 자리, 전해 내려오는 이야기에 따르면 리자베타가 언젠가 그곳을 타넘었다고 그가 알고 있던 바로 그 지점이었다. \"그 여자가 타넘을 수 있었다면,\""], ["the thought, God knows why, occurred to him, “surely I can.”", "어찌 된 영문인지 그런 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다, \"나라고 못 할 리 없지.\""], ["He did in fact jump up, and instantly contrived to catch hold of the top of the fence.", "과연 그는 펄쩍 뛰어올라 단숨에 울타리 꼭대기를 붙잡는 데 성공했다."], ["Then he vigorously pulled himself up and sat astride on it.", "그러고는 힘차게 몸을 끌어올려 그 위에 걸터앉았다."], ["Close by, in the garden stood the bath-house, but from the fence he could see the lighted windows of the house too.", "바로 가까이, 정원 안에는 목욕간이 서 있었으나, 울타리 위에서는 집의 불 켜진 창문들도 보였다."]], [["“Yes, the old man’s bedroom is lighted up.", "\"그래, 노인의 침실에 불이 켜져 있다."], ["She’s there!”", "그 여자가 거기 있다!\""], ["and he leapt from the fence into the garden.", "그러고는 울타리에서 정원 안으로 뛰어내렸다."], ["Though he knew Grigory was ill and very likely Smerdyakov, too, and that there was no one to hear him, he instinctively hid himself, stood still, and began to listen.", "그리고리가 앓고 있고 스메르댜코프 역시 십중팔구 그러하며, 자기 소리를 들을 사람이 아무도 없다는 것을 알면서도 그는 본능적으로 몸을 숨겨 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."], ["But there was dead silence on all sides and, as though of design, complete stillness, not the slightest breath of wind.", "그러나 사방은 죽은 듯이 고요했고, 마치 일부러 그런 듯이 완전한 정적이었으며, 한 점의 바람기도 없었다."]], [["“And naught but the whispering silence,”", "\"속삭이는 듯한 정적밖에는 아무것도 없네.\""], ["the line for some reason rose to his mind.", "어쩐 일인지 그 시구가 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["“If only no one heard me jump over the fence! I think not.”", "\"내가 울타리를 넘는 소리를 아무도 못 들었어야 할 텐데!"], ["Standing still for a minute, he walked softly over the grass in the garden, avoiding the trees and shrubs.", "못 들었을 것이다.\" 잠시 가만히 서 있다가 그는 나무와 덤불을 피해 가며 정원 풀밭 위를 살그머니 걸었다."], ["He walked slowly, creeping stealthily at every step, listening to his own footsteps.", "그는 천천히 걸으며 한 걸음 한 걸음 살금살금 기듯이 나아갔고, 자기 발소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["It took him five minutes to reach the lighted window.", "불 켜진 창문에 이르기까지 오 분이 걸렸다."], ["He remembered that just under the window there were several thick and high bushes of elder and whitebeam.", "그는 그 창문 바로 아래에 딱총나무와 마가목의 무성하고 키 큰 덤불 몇 그루가 있었다는 것을 기억해 냈다."], ["The door from the house into the garden on the left-hand side, was shut; he had carefully looked on purpose to see, in passing.", "집에서 정원으로 통하는 왼편의 문은 닫혀 있었으니, 그는 지나가면서 일부러 주의 깊게 살펴 두었던 것이다."], ["At last he reached the bushes and hid behind them.", "마침내 그는 덤불에 이르러 그 뒤로 몸을 숨겼다."], ["He held his breath.", "그는 숨을 죽였다."], ["“I must wait now,”", "\"이제 기다려야 한다.\""], ["he thought, “to reassure them, in case they heard my footsteps and are listening ...", "그는 생각했다. \"그들을 안심시키기 위해서, 혹여 그들이 내 발소리를 듣고 귀를 기울이고 있다면 말이다 ..."], ["if only I don’t cough or sneeze.”", "기침이나 재채기만 하지 않으면 된다.\""]], [["He waited two minutes.", "그는 이 분을 기다렸다."], ["His heart was beating violently, and, at moments, he could scarcely breathe.", "심장이 격렬하게 뛰어, 순간순간 거의 숨을 쉴 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["“No, this throbbing at my heart won’t stop,”", "\"안 되겠다, 이 심장의 두근거림은 멎질 않는다.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“I can’t wait any longer.”", "\"더는 기다릴 수 없다.\""], ["He was standing behind a bush in the shadow.", "그는 그늘 속 덤불 뒤에 서 있었다."], ["The light of the window fell on the front part of the bush.", "창문의 불빛이 덤불의 앞부분에 떨어졌다."]], [["“How red the whitebeam berries are!”", "\"마가목 열매가 어쩌면 저리도 붉을까!\""], ["he murmured, not knowing why.", "그는 까닭도 모른 채 중얼거렸다."], ["Softly and noiselessly, step by step, he approached the window, and raised himself on tiptoe.", "소리도 없이 살그머니, 한 걸음 한 걸음 그는 창문에 다가가 발끝으로 몸을 일으켜 세웠다."], ["All Fyodor Pavlovitch’s bedroom lay open before him.", "표도르 파블로비치의 침실 전체가 그의 눈앞에 활짝 드러났다."], ["It was not a large room, and was divided in two parts by a red screen, “Chinese,”", "그리 큰 방은 아니었고, 표도르 파블로비치가 늘 \"중국식\"이라고 부르던 붉은 칸막이로 둘로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["as Fyodor Pavlovitch used to call it. The word “Chinese”", "\"중국식\"이라는 말이 미탸의 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다."], ["flashed into Mitya’s mind, “and behind the screen, is Grushenka,”", "\"그리고 저 칸막이 뒤에 그루셴카가 있다.\""], ["thought Mitya.", "미탸는 생각했다."], ["He began watching Fyodor Pavlovitch, who was wearing his new striped-silk dressing-gown, which Mitya had never seen, and a silk cord with tassels round the waist.", "그는 표도르 파블로비치를 살펴보기 시작했는데, 노인은 미탸가 한 번도 본 적 없는 새 줄무늬 비단 실내복을 입고, 허리에는 술 달린 비단 끈을 두르고 있었다."], ["A clean, dandified shirt of fine linen with gold studs peeped out under the collar of the dressing-gown.", "깨끗하고 멋스러운 고운 아마포 셔츠가 금단추를 단 채 실내복 깃 아래로 살짝 내비쳤다."], ["On his head Fyodor Pavlovitch had the same red bandage which Alyosha had seen.", "표도르 파블로비치는 머리에 알료샤가 보았던 것과 똑같은 붉은 띠를 두르고 있었다."]], [["“He has got himself up,”", "\"잔뜩 차려입었군.\""], ["thought Mitya.", "미탸는 생각했다."]], [["His father was standing near the window, apparently lost in thought.", "그의 아버지는 창가에 서서 보아하니 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Suddenly he jerked up his head, listened a moment, and hearing nothing went up to the table, poured out half a glass of brandy from a decanter and drank it off.", "별안간 그는 고개를 번쩍 쳐들고 잠시 귀를 기울이더니, 아무 소리도 들리지 않자 탁자로 가서 유리병에서 브랜디를 반 잔쯤 따라 단숨에 들이켰다."], ["Then he uttered a deep sigh, again stood still a moment, walked carelessly up to the looking-glass on the wall, with his right hand raised the red bandage on his forehead a little, and began examining his bruises and scars, which had not yet disappeared.", "그러고는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬며 다시 잠시 가만히 서 있다가, 무심한 걸음으로 벽에 걸린 거울 앞으로 가더니 오른손으로 이마의 붉은 띠를 조금 들어 올리고는, 아직 사라지지 않은 멍과 상처를 살펴보기 시작했다."]], [["“He’s alone,”", "\"혼자다.\""], ["thought Mitya, “in all probability he’s alone.”", "미탸는 생각했다. \"십중팔구 혼자다.\""]], [["Fyodor Pavlovitch moved away from the looking-glass, turned suddenly to the window and looked out.", "표도르 파블로비치는 거울에서 물러나 별안간 창문 쪽으로 돌아서서 밖을 내다보았다."], ["Mitya instantly slipped away into the shadow.", "미탸는 순식간에 그늘 속으로 몸을 숨겼다."]], [["“She may be there behind the screen.", "\"그 여자가 어쩌면 저 칸막이 뒤에 있을지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps she’s asleep by now,”", "어쩌면 벌써 잠들었는지도.\""], ["he thought, with a pang at his heart.", "그는 가슴이 찔리는 듯한 아픔을 느끼며 생각했다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch moved away from the window.", "표도르 파블로비치가 창가에서 물러났다."], ["“He’s looking for her out of the window, so she’s not there.", "\"저자가 창밖으로 그 여자를 찾고 있다, 그러니 여기에는 없는 것이다."], ["Why should he stare out into the dark?", "왜 어둠 속을 저렇게 노려보겠는가?"], ["He’s wild with impatience.” ...", "초조함에 미칠 지경인 게다.\""], ["Mitya slipped back at once, and fell to gazing in at the window again.", "... 미탸는 곧바로 다시 다가가 창 안을 들여다보기 시작했다."], ["The old man was sitting down at the table, apparently disappointed.", "노인은 보아하니 실망한 듯 탁자에 앉아 있었다."], ["At last he put his elbow on the table, and laid his right cheek against his hand.", "마침내 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 오른쪽 뺨을 손에 갖다 댔다."], ["Mitya watched him eagerly.", "미탸는 그를 열심히 지켜보았다."]], [["“He’s alone, he’s alone!”", "\"혼자다, 혼자야!\""], ["he repeated again.", "그는 다시 되뇌었다."], ["“If she were here, his face would be different.”", "\"그 여자가 여기 있다면 저자의 얼굴이 다를 것이다.\""]], [["Strange to say, a queer, irrational vexation rose up in his heart that she was not here.", "이상한 말이지만, 그녀가 여기 없다는 사실에 묘하고도 까닭 없는 분함이 그의 가슴속에서 솟구쳤다."], ["“It’s not that she’s not here,”", "\"그 여자가 여기 없다는 게 문제가 아니다.\""], ["he explained to himself, immediately, “but that I can’t tell for certain whether she is or not.”", "그는 곧장 스스로에게 변명했다. \"그 여자가 있는지 없는지 확실히 알 수 없다는 게 문제다.\""], ["Mitya remembered afterwards that his mind was at that moment exceptionally clear, that he took in everything to the slightest detail, and missed no point.", "미탸는 훗날 기억하기를, 그 순간 자기 정신이 유난히 또렷했으며, 사소한 세부까지 죄다 받아들여 한 가지도 놓치지 않았다고 했다."], ["But a feeling of misery, the misery of uncertainty and indecision, was growing in his heart with every instant. “Is she here or not?”", "그러나 비참한 감정이, 곧 불확실함과 망설임의 비참함이 매 순간 그의 가슴속에서 자라나고 있었다."], ["The angry doubt filled his heart, and suddenly, making up his mind, he put out his hand and softly knocked on the window frame.", "\"그 여자가 여기 있는가, 없는가?\" 성난 의혹이 그의 가슴을 채웠고, 별안간 마음을 정하자 그는 손을 내밀어 살그머니 창틀을 두드렸다."], ["He knocked the signal the old man had agreed upon with Smerdyakov, twice slowly and then three times more quickly, the signal that meant “Grushenka is here!”", "그는 노인이 스메르댜코프와 약속해 둔 신호를, 곧 천천히 두 번 두드린 다음 조금 더 빠르게 세 번 더 두드리는, \"그루셴카가 여기 왔다!\"는 뜻의 신호를 두드렸다."]], [["The old man started, jerked up his head, and, jumping up quickly, ran to the window.", "노인은 흠칫 놀라 고개를 번쩍 쳐들더니, 재빨리 벌떡 일어나 창문으로 달려왔다."], ["Mitya slipped away into the shadow.", "미탸는 그늘 속으로 몸을 숨겼다."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch opened the window and thrust his whole head out.", "표도르 파블로비치는 창문을 열고 머리를 통째로 밖으로 내밀었다."]], [["“Grushenka, is it you?", "\"그루셴카, 너냐?"], ["Is it you?”", "너야?\""], ["he said, in a sort of trembling half-whisper.", "그는 떨리는 듯한 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Where are you, my angel, where are you?”", "\"어디 있느냐, 내 천사야, 어디 있어?\""], ["He was fearfully agitated and breathless.", "그는 무섭도록 흥분해 숨을 헐떡였다."]], [["“He’s alone.”", "\"혼자다.\""], ["Mitya decided.", "미탸는 단정했다."]], [["“Where are you?”", "\"어디 있느냐?\""], ["cried the old man again; and he thrust his head out farther, thrust it out to the shoulders, gazing in all directions, right and left.", "노인이 다시 외쳤다. 그러고는 머리를 더 멀리, 어깨까지 내밀고 좌우 사방을 두리번거렸다."], ["“Come here, I’ve a little present for you.", "\"이리 오너라, 너에게 줄 작은 선물이 있다."], ["Come, I’ll show you....”", "오너라, 보여 주마....\""]], [["“He means the three thousand,”", "\"삼천 루블을 말하는 게로군.\""], ["thought Mitya.", "미탸는 생각했다."]], [["“But where are you?", "\"한데 어디 있느냐?"], ["Are you at the door?", "문 앞에 있는 게냐?"], ["I’ll open it directly.”", "곧 열어 주마.\""]], [["And the old man almost climbed out of the window, peering out to the right, where there was a door into the garden, trying to see into the darkness.", "그러고는 노인은 정원으로 통하는 문이 있는 오른쪽을 내다보며 어둠 속을 살피려 들면서 거의 창밖으로 기어 나오다시피 했다."], ["In another second he would certainly have run out to open the door without waiting for Grushenka’s answer.", "한 순간만 더 지났더라면 그는 분명 그루셴카의 대답도 기다리지 않고 문을 열러 뛰쳐나갔을 것이다."]], [["Mitya looked at him from the side without stirring.", "미탸는 꼼짝도 않고 옆에서 그를 바라보았다."], ["The old man’s profile that he loathed so, his pendent Adam’s apple, his hooked nose, his lips that smiled in greedy expectation, were all brightly lighted up by the slanting lamplight falling on the left from the room.", "그토록 진저리 치던 노인의 옆모습, 축 늘어진 울대뼈, 갈고리 같은 코, 탐욕스러운 기대에 미소 짓는 입술이, 방 안에서 왼쪽으로 비스듬히 떨어지는 등불에 밝게 비쳐 있었다."], ["A horrible fury of hatred suddenly surged up in Mitya’s heart: “There he was, his rival, the man who had tormented him, had ruined his life!”", "무시무시한 증오의 격분이 별안간 미탸의 가슴속에서 치솟았다. \"저기 그가 있다, 자기의 연적, 자기를 괴롭히고 자기 인생을 망쳐 놓은 사내가!\""], ["It was a rush of that sudden, furious, revengeful anger of which he had spoken, as though foreseeing it, to Alyosha, four days ago in the arbor, when, in answer to Alyosha’s question, “How can you say you’ll kill our father?”", "그것은 나흘 전 정자에서 알료샤에게, 마치 이를 예감한 듯이 말했던 그 갑작스럽고 격렬한 복수의 분노가 솟구치는 것이었으니, 그때 알료샤가 \"어떻게 형이 아버지를 죽이겠다는 말을 할 수 있어요?\"라고 묻자 그는 이렇게 말했었다."], ["“I don’t know, I don’t know,”", "\"나도 모른다, 나도 몰라.\""], ["he had said then.", "그는 그때 그렇게 말했다."], ["“Perhaps I shall not kill him, perhaps I shall.", "\"어쩌면 안 죽일지도 모르고, 어쩌면 죽일지도 모른다."], ["I’m afraid he’ll suddenly be so loathsome to me at that moment.", "두려운 건 그 순간 그자가 갑자기 너무도 역겹게 느껴질까 봐 하는 거야."], ["I hate his double chin, his nose, his eyes, his shameless grin.", "나는 그자의 이중 턱이, 코가, 눈이, 그 뻔뻔스러운 히죽거림이 싫다."], ["I feel a personal repulsion.", "나는 개인적인 혐오를 느낀다."], ["That’s what I’m afraid of, that’s what may be too much for me.”", "그게 두렵다, 그것이 나를 어쩌지 못하게 만들까 봐 두렵다.\" ..."], ["... This personal repulsion was growing unendurable.", "이 개인적인 혐오가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 자라나고 있었다."], ["Mitya was beside himself, he suddenly pulled the brass pestle out of his pocket.", "미탸는 제정신이 아니었고, 별안간 호주머니에서 놋쇠 절굿공이를 끄집어냈다."]], [["“God was watching over me then,”", "\"그때 하느님이 나를 지켜보고 계셨다.\""], ["Mitya himself said afterwards.", "미탸 자신이 훗날 말했다."], ["At that very moment Grigory waked up on his bed of sickness.", "바로 그 순간 그리고리가 병석에서 잠이 깼다."], ["Earlier in the evening he had undergone the treatment which Smerdyakov had described to Ivan.", "그날 저녁 일찍이 그는 스메르댜코프가 이반에게 묘사했던 그 치료를 받았던 것이다."], ["He had rubbed himself all over with vodka mixed with a secret, very strong decoction, had drunk what was left of the mixture while his wife repeated a “certain prayer” over him, after which he had gone to bed.", "그는 어떤 비방의 아주 독한 달인 물을 섞은 보드카로 온몸을 문질렀고, 아내가 그의 머리맡에서 \"어떤 기도\"를 되뇌는 동안 남은 혼합액을 마신 다음 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Marfa Ignatyevna had tasted the stuff, too, and, being unused to strong drink, slept like the dead beside her husband.", "마르파 이그나티예브나도 그 물을 맛보았는데, 독한 술에 익숙지 않은 터라 남편 곁에서 죽은 듯이 잠들었다."]], [["But Grigory waked up in the night, quite suddenly, and, after a moment’s reflection, though he immediately felt a sharp pain in his back, he sat up in bed.", "그러나 그리고리는 한밤중에 아주 갑작스레 잠이 깼고, 잠시 생각에 잠긴 끝에, 곧바로 등에 날카로운 통증을 느꼈음에도 침대에 일어나 앉았다."], ["Then he deliberated again, got up and dressed hurriedly.", "그러고는 다시 한 번 곰곰이 따져 본 뒤 일어나 허둥지둥 옷을 입었다."], ["Perhaps his conscience was uneasy at the thought of sleeping while the house was unguarded “in such perilous times.”", "어쩌면 \"이런 위태로운 시절에\" 집을 지키는 이 없이 잠을 잤다는 생각에 양심이 편치 않았는지도 모른다."], ["Smerdyakov, exhausted by his fit, lay motionless in the next room.", "발작에 지친 스메르댜코프는 옆방에 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다."], ["Marfa Ignatyevna did not stir.", "마르파 이그나티예브나도 미동도 없었다."], ["“The stuff’s been too much for the woman,”", "\"그 물이 마누라에게는 너무 셌던 게로군.\""], ["Grigory thought, glancing at her, and groaning, he went out on the steps.", "그리고리는 아내를 흘긋 보며 생각했고, 신음 소리를 내며 현관 층계로 나갔다."], ["No doubt he only intended to look out from the steps, for he was hardly able to walk, the pain in his back and his right leg was intolerable.", "분명 그는 층계에서 밖을 내다볼 작정이었을 뿐이었으니, 등과 오른쪽 다리의 통증이 참을 수 없어 거의 걸을 수조차 없었기 때문이다."], ["But he suddenly remembered that he had not locked the little gate into the garden that evening.", "그러나 그는 그날 저녁 정원으로 통하는 작은 쪽문을 잠그지 않았다는 것이 별안간 생각났다."], ["He was the most punctual and precise of men, a man who adhered to an unchangeable routine, and habits that lasted for years.", "그는 더없이 시간을 잘 지키고 빈틈없는 사람이었으며, 변치 않는 일과와 여러 해 이어 온 습관을 고수하는 사람이었다."], ["Limping and writhing with pain he went down the steps and towards the garden.", "다리를 절며 통증에 몸을 뒤틀면서 그는 층계를 내려가 정원 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Yes, the gate stood wide open.", "과연 쪽문은 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["Mechanically he stepped into the garden.", "그는 기계적으로 정원 안으로 발을 들여놓았다."], ["Perhaps he fancied something, perhaps caught some sound, and, glancing to the left he saw his master’s window open.", "어쩌면 무언가가 눈에 어른거렸는지도, 어쩌면 무슨 소리를 들었는지도 모른다, 그리고 왼쪽을 흘긋 보니 주인의 창문이 열려 있는 것이 보였다."], ["No one was looking out of it then.", "그때 그 창문 밖을 내다보는 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["“What’s it open for?", "\"저 창문이 왜 열려 있지?"], ["It’s not summer now,”", "지금이 여름도 아닌데.\""], ["thought Grigory, and suddenly, at that very instant he caught a glimpse of something extraordinary before him in the garden.", "그리고리는 생각했는데, 별안간 바로 그 순간 정원 저 앞에서 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 그의 눈에 언뜻 들어왔다."], ["Forty paces in front of him a man seemed to be running in the dark, a sort of shadow was moving very fast.", "그의 앞 마흔 걸음쯤 되는 곳에서 한 사내가 어둠 속을 달려가는 것 같았고, 어떤 그림자가 아주 빠르게 움직이고 있었다."]], [["“Good Lord!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Grigory beside himself, and forgetting the pain in his back, he hurried to intercept the running figure.", "그리고리는 제정신이 아닌 채로 외쳤고, 등의 통증도 잊은 채 달려가는 그림자를 가로막으려고 서둘렀다."], ["He took a short cut, evidently he knew the garden better; the flying figure went towards the bath-house, ran behind it and rushed to the garden fence.", "그는 지름길을 택했는데, 보아하니 정원을 더 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 달아나는 그림자는 목욕간 쪽으로 가서 그 뒤로 돌아 정원 울타리로 내달았다."], ["Grigory followed, not losing sight of him, and ran, forgetting everything.", "그리고리는 그를 시야에서 놓치지 않고 뒤쫓으며, 모든 것을 잊은 채 달렸다."], ["He reached the fence at the very moment the man was climbing over it.", "그는 사내가 울타리를 막 타넘던 바로 그 순간 울타리에 다다랐다."], ["Grigory cried out, beside himself, pounced on him, and clutched his leg in his two hands.", "그리고리는 제정신이 아닌 채로 소리치며 그에게 덤벼들어 두 손으로 그의 다리를 움켜잡았다."]], [["Yes, his foreboding had not deceived him.", "과연 그의 예감은 그를 속이지 않았다."], ["He recognized him, it was he, the “monster,” the “parricide.”", "그는 그를 알아보았으니, 바로 그였다, 그 \"괴물\", 그 \"아비를 죽인 놈\"이었다."]], [["“Parricide!”", "\"아비를 죽인 놈!\""], ["the old man shouted so that the whole neighborhood could hear, but he had not time to shout more, he fell at once, as though struck by lightning.", "노인은 온 이웃이 들을 만큼 큰 소리로 외쳤으나, 더 외칠 겨를도 없이 마치 벼락에 맞은 듯 그 자리에서 쓰러지고 말았다."]], [["Mitya jumped back into the garden and bent over the fallen man.", "미탸는 정원 안으로 도로 뛰어내려 쓰러진 사내 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["In Mitya’s hands was a brass pestle, and he flung it mechanically in the grass.", "미탸의 손에는 놋쇠 절굿공이가 들려 있었는데, 그는 그것을 기계적으로 풀밭에 내던졌다."], ["The pestle fell two paces from Grigory, not in the grass but on the path, in a most conspicuous place.", "절굿공이는 그리고리에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 풀밭이 아니라 길 위, 더없이 눈에 띄는 자리에 떨어졌다."], ["For some seconds he examined the prostrate figure before him.", "그는 몇 초 동안 자기 앞에 엎어진 형체를 살펴보았다."], ["The old man’s head was covered with blood.", "노인의 머리는 피로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["Mitya put out his hand and began feeling it.", "미탸는 손을 내밀어 그것을 만져 보기 시작했다."], ["He remembered afterwards clearly, that he had been awfully anxious to make sure whether he had broken the old man’s skull, or simply stunned him with the pestle.", "그는 훗날 또렷이 기억하기를, 자기가 노인의 두개골을 깨뜨린 것인지 아니면 그저 절굿공이로 기절시킨 것인지 무섭도록 확인하고 싶어 했다고 했다."], ["But the blood was flowing horribly; and in a moment Mitya’s fingers were drenched with the hot stream.", "그러나 피가 끔찍하게 흘러나왔고, 한순간 미탸의 손가락은 그 뜨거운 핏줄기에 흠뻑 젖었다."], ["He remembered taking out of his pocket the clean white handkerchief with which he had provided himself for his visit to Madame Hohlakov, and putting it to the old man’s head, senselessly trying to wipe the blood from his face and temples.", "그는 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아갈 때 챙겨 두었던 깨끗하고 흰 손수건을 호주머니에서 꺼내 노인의 머리에 갖다 대고는, 분별없이 그의 얼굴과 관자놀이에서 피를 닦아 내려 했던 것을 기억했다."], ["But the handkerchief was instantly soaked with blood.", "그러나 손수건은 순식간에 피에 흠뻑 젖었다."]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what am I doing it for?”", "내가 무엇 때문에 이 짓을 하고 있나?\""], ["thought Mitya, suddenly pulling himself together.", "미탸는 별안간 정신을 차리며 생각했다."], ["“If I have broken his skull, how can I find out now?", "\"내가 그의 두개골을 깨뜨렸다 한들, 지금 그걸 어떻게 알아낸단 말인가?"], ["And what difference does it make now?”", "게다가 이제 와서 무슨 차이가 있단 말인가?\""], ["he added, hopelessly.", "그는 절망스럽게 덧붙였다."], ["“If I’ve killed him, I’ve killed him....", "\"내가 그를 죽였다면, 죽인 것이다...."], ["You’ve come to grief, old man, so there you must lie!” he said aloud.", "영감, 당신은 화를 자초했으니, 거기 그렇게 누워 있어야지!\""], ["And suddenly turning to the fence, he vaulted over it into the lane and fell to running—the handkerchief soaked with blood he held, crushed up in his right fist, and as he ran he thrust it into the back pocket of his coat.", "그는 소리 내어 말했다. 그러고는 별안간 울타리 쪽으로 돌아서 그것을 뛰어넘어 좁은 길로 내려서더니 달리기 시작했다 — 피에 젖은 손수건을 오른손 주먹에 꽉 움켜쥔 채로, 그리고 달리면서 그것을 외투 뒷주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["He ran headlong, and the few passers-by who met him in the dark, in the streets, remembered afterwards that they had met a man running that night.", "그는 정신없이 달렸고, 어둠 속 거리에서 그와 마주친 몇 안 되는 행인들은 훗날 그날 밤 한 사내가 달려가는 것을 보았다고 기억했다."], ["He flew back again to the widow Morozov’s house.", "그는 다시 모로조바 미망인의 집으로 날듯이 되돌아갔다."]], [["Immediately after he had left it that evening, Fenya had rushed to the chief porter, Nazar Ivanovitch, and besought him, for Christ’s sake, “not to let the captain in again to-day or to-morrow.”", "그날 저녁 그가 그 집을 떠나자마자, 페냐는 수석 문지기 나자르 이바노비치에게 달려가, 제발 그리스도를 봐서라도 \"오늘이든 내일이든 그 대위를 다시 들여보내지 말아 달라\"고 간청했다."], ["Nazar Ivanovitch promised, but went upstairs to his mistress who had suddenly sent for him, and meeting his nephew, a boy of twenty, who had recently come from the country, on the way up told him to take his place, but forgot to mention “the captain.”", "나자르 이바노비치는 약속했으나, 별안간 그를 부른 여주인에게 가려고 위층으로 올라가다가, 도중에 최근에 시골에서 올라온 스무 살짜리 조카를 만나 자기 자리를 대신 맡으라고 일렀는데, \"그 대위\"에 관해 일러두는 것은 깜박 잊고 말았다."], ["Mitya, running up to the gate, knocked.", "미탸가 대문으로 달려와 두드렸다."], ["The lad instantly recognized him, for Mitya had more than once tipped him.", "그 젊은이는 단번에 그를 알아보았으니, 미탸가 한두 번 그에게 행하를 주었기 때문이다."], ["Opening the gate at once, he let him in, and hastened to inform him with a good-humored smile that “Agrafena Alexandrovna is not at home now, you know.”", "그는 곧장 대문을 열어 그를 들여보내고는, 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 \"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나는 지금 댁에 안 계세요, 아시잖아요\" 하고 서둘러 알려 주었다."]], [["“Where is she then, Prohor?”", "\"그럼 그 여자는 어디 있느냐, 프로호르?\""], ["asked Mitya, stopping short.", "미탸는 우뚝 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["“She set off this evening, some two hours ago, with Timofey, to Mokroe.”", "\"오늘 저녁에 길을 떠나셨어요, 두 시간쯤 전에 티모페이와 함께 모크로예로요.\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"무엇 때문에?\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["“That I can’t say.", "\"그건 저도 모르겠습니다."], ["To see some officer.", "어떤 장교를 만나러요."], ["Some one invited her and horses were sent to fetch her.”", "누군가가 그분을 초대했고 모시러 말까지 보내왔거든요.\""]], [["Mitya left him, and ran like a madman to Fenya.", "미탸는 그를 두고 미친 사람처럼 페냐에게로 달려갔다."]], [["# Chapter V. A Sudden Resolution", "# Chapter V. A Sudden Resolution"]], [["She was sitting in the kitchen with her grandmother; they were both just going to bed.", "그녀는 부엌에서 할머니와 함께 앉아 있었는데, 두 사람 다 막 잠자리에 들려던 참이었다."], ["Relying on Nazar Ivanovitch, they had not locked themselves in.", "나자르 이바노비치를 믿고서 그들은 문을 잠가 두지 않았다."], ["Mitya ran in, pounced on Fenya and seized her by the throat.", "미탸가 뛰어 들어와 페냐에게 달려들더니 그녀의 목을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Speak at once!", "\"당장 말해!"], ["Where is she?", "그 여자 어디 있어?"], ["With whom is she now, at Mokroe?”", "지금 누구하고 있어, 모크로예에서?\""], ["he roared furiously.", "그는 미친 듯이 울부짖었다."]], [["Both the women squealed.", "두 여자가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“Aie!", "\"아이고!"], ["I’ll tell you. Aie!", "말씀드릴게요."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch, darling, I’ll tell you everything directly, I won’t hide anything,”", "아이고! 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 다 말씀드릴게요, 하나도 안 숨길게요.\""], ["gabbled Fenya, frightened to death; “she’s gone to Mokroe, to her officer.”", "페냐가 죽도록 겁에 질려 횡설수설했다. \"아씨는 모크로예로, 그 장교한테 가셨어요.\""]], [["“What officer?”", "\"무슨 장교야?\""], ["roared Mitya.", "미탸가 고함을 질렀다."]], [["“To her officer, the same one she used to know, the one who threw her over five years ago,”", "\"그 장교요, 예전에 알던 바로 그 사람, 오 년 전에 아씨를 버린 그 사람이요.\""], ["cackled Fenya, as fast as she could speak.", "페냐는 입에서 나오는 대로 빠르게 종알거렸다."]], [["Mitya withdrew the hands with which he was squeezing her throat.", "미탸는 그녀의 목을 조르던 두 손을 거두었다."], ["He stood facing her, pale as death, unable to utter a word, but his eyes showed that he realized it all, all, from the first word, and guessed the whole position.", "그는 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로 그녀를 마주 보고 서서 한마디도 내뱉지 못했으나, 그의 눈은 첫마디에서부터 모든 것을, 모든 것을 깨닫고 사태의 전모를 짐작했음을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Poor Fenya was not in a condition at that moment to observe whether he understood or not.", "가엾은 페냐는 그 순간 그가 알아들었는지 어떤지 살필 형편이 아니었다."], ["She remained sitting on the trunk as she had been when he ran into the room, trembling all over, holding her hands out before her as though trying to defend herself.", "그녀는 그가 방으로 뛰어 들어왔을 때 그대로 궤짝 위에 앉은 채로 온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 마치 제 몸을 막으려는 듯이 두 손을 앞으로 내밀고 있었다."], ["She seemed to have grown rigid in that position.", "그녀는 그 자세 그대로 굳어 버린 것 같았다."], ["Her wide-opened, scared eyes were fixed immovably upon him.", "휘둥그레지고 겁에 질린 두 눈은 그에게 못 박힌 듯 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["And to make matters worse, both his hands were smeared with blood.", "게다가 사태를 더 끔찍하게 만든 것은, 그의 두 손이 온통 피로 얼룩져 있었다는 점이다."], ["On the way, as he ran, he must have touched his forehead with them, wiping off the perspiration, so that on his forehead and his right cheek were blood-stained patches.", "달려오는 길에 그는 땀을 닦느라 그 손으로 이마를 만진 모양이어서, 이마와 오른쪽 뺨에 피로 물든 자국이 묻어 있었다."], ["Fenya was on the verge of hysterics.", "페냐는 막 발작을 일으킬 지경이었다."], ["The old cook had jumped up and was staring at him like a mad woman, almost unconscious with terror.", "늙은 식모는 벌떡 일어나 미친 여자처럼 그를 노려보았는데, 공포로 거의 정신을 잃을 지경이었다."]], [["Mitya stood for a moment, then mechanically sank on to a chair next to Fenya.", "미탸는 한순간 서 있다가, 기계적으로 페냐 옆 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."], ["He sat, not reflecting but, as it were, terror-stricken, benumbed.", "그는 무엇을 곰곰이 생각하는 것이 아니라, 말하자면 공포에 사로잡혀 마비된 채로 앉아 있었다."], ["Yet everything was clear as day: that officer, he knew about him, he knew everything perfectly, he had known it from Grushenka herself, had known that a letter had come from him a month before.", "그러면서도 모든 것이 대낮처럼 분명했다. 그 장교, 그는 그자에 관해 알고 있었고, 죄다 완벽하게 알고 있었으며, 그루셴카 자신에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 한 달 전에 그에게서 편지가 왔다는 것까지 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["So that for a month, for a whole month, this had been going on, a secret from him, till the very arrival of this new man, and he had never thought of him!", "그러니까 한 달 동안, 꼬박 한 달 동안 이 일이 그에게는 비밀로 진행되어 왔던 것이다, 이 새로운 사내가 들이닥치는 바로 그 순간까지. 그런데도 그는 그자를 한 번도 떠올린 적이 없었다!"], ["But how could he, how could he not have thought of him?", "그러나 어떻게, 어떻게 그자를 떠올리지 못할 수 있었단 말인가?"], ["Why was it he had forgotten this officer, like that, forgotten him as soon as he heard of him?", "어찌하여 그는 이 장교를 그렇게, 그자에 대해 듣자마자 그렇게 까맣게 잊어버렸던 것인가?"], ["That was the question that faced him like some monstrous thing.", "그것이 무슨 괴물처럼 그의 앞에 들이닥친 물음이었다."], ["And he looked at this monstrous thing with horror, growing cold with horror.", "그리고 그는 그 괴물 같은 것을 공포에 사로잡혀 바라보았고, 공포로 온몸이 싸늘하게 식어 갔다."]], [["But suddenly, as gently and mildly as a gentle and affectionate child, he began speaking to Fenya as though he had utterly forgotten how he had scared and hurt her just now.", "그러나 별안간, 온순하고 다정한 어린아이처럼 부드럽고 상냥하게 그는 페냐에게 말을 걸기 시작했는데, 마치 방금 자신이 그녀를 겁주고 아프게 했던 것을 깡그리 잊은 듯했다."], ["He fell to questioning Fenya with an extreme preciseness, astonishing in his position, and though the girl looked wildly at his blood-stained hands, she, too, with wonderful readiness and rapidity, answered every question as though eager to put the whole truth and nothing but the truth before him.", "그는 그의 처지로서는 놀라우리만치 극도로 정확하게 페냐에게 캐묻기 시작했고, 처녀는 그의 피투성이 손을 겁에 질려 바라보면서도 역시 놀랄 만큼 선뜻하고 빠르게 모든 물음에 대답했는데, 마치 진실을, 오로지 진실만을 그의 앞에 죄다 펼쳐 보이고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["Little by little, even with a sort of enjoyment, she began explaining every detail, not wanting to torment him, but, as it were, eager to be of the utmost service to him.", "차츰차츰 그녀는 어떤 즐거움마저 느끼며 시시콜콜한 사정을 하나하나 설명하기 시작했으니, 그를 괴롭히려는 것이 아니라 말하자면 그에게 최대한 도움이 되고 싶어 안달하는 것 같았다."], ["She described the whole of that day, in great detail, the visit of Rakitin and Alyosha, how she, Fenya, had stood on the watch, how the mistress had set off, and how she had called out of the window to Alyosha to give him, Mitya, her greetings, and to tell him “to remember for ever how she had loved him for an hour.”", "그녀는 그날 하루의 일을 아주 자세히, 라키틴과 알료샤가 찾아온 일, 자기 페냐가 어떻게 망을 보고 있었는지, 아씨가 어떻게 길을 떠났는지, 그리고 아씨가 어떻게 창밖으로 알료샤를 불러 미탸에게 안부를 전해 달라고, \"한 시간 동안 그를 사랑했던 것을 영원히 기억하라\"고 전해 달라 했는지를 늘어놓았다."]], [["Hearing of the message, Mitya suddenly smiled, and there was a flush of color on his pale cheeks.", "그 전갈을 듣고 미탸는 별안간 빙긋 웃었고, 그의 창백한 뺨에 붉은 기운이 번졌다."], ["At the same moment Fenya said to him, not a bit afraid now to be inquisitive:", "바로 그 순간 페냐는 이제 전혀 두려워하지 않고 호기심에 차서 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Look at your hands, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"손을 보세요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리."], ["They’re all over blood!”", "온통 피투성이예요!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["answered Mitya mechanically.", "미탸는 기계적으로 대답했다."], ["He looked carelessly at his hands and at once forgot them and Fenya’s question.", "그는 무심히 자기 손을 들여다보다가 곧 그 손도, 페냐의 물음도 잊어버렸다."]], [["He sank into silence again.", "그는 다시 침묵 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["Twenty minutes had passed since he had run in.", "그가 뛰어 들어온 지 이십 분이 지나 있었다."], ["His first horror was over, but evidently some new fixed determination had taken possession of him.", "처음의 공포는 가셨으나, 분명 무슨 새로운 굳은 결심이 그를 사로잡고 있었다."], ["He suddenly stood up, smiling dreamily.", "그는 별안간 일어서서 꿈꾸듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“What has happened to you, sir?”", "\"무슨 일이 있으셨어요, 나리?\""], ["said Fenya, pointing to his hands again.", "페냐가 다시 그의 손을 가리키며 말했다."], ["She spoke compassionately, as though she felt very near to him now in his grief.", "그녀는 가엾어하는 투로 말했는데, 마치 슬픔에 잠긴 그가 이제는 아주 가깝게 느껴지는 듯했다."], ["Mitya looked at his hands again.", "미탸는 다시 자기 손을 들여다보았다."]], [["“That’s blood, Fenya,”", "\"이건 피야, 페냐.\""], ["he said, looking at her with a strange expression.", "그는 야릇한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“That’s human blood, and my God!", "\"이건 사람의 피인데, 아아, 하느님!"], ["why was it shed?", "어쩌다 이게 흘려졌단 말인가?"], ["But ...", "하지만……"], ["Fenya ...", "페냐……"], ["there’s a fence here”", "여기 울타리가 하나 있는데\""], ["(he looked at her as though setting her a riddle), “a high fence, and terrible to look at.", "(그는 그녀에게 수수께끼라도 내듯 그녀를 바라보았다) \"높다란 울타리라, 보기에도 무시무시한 거야."], ["But at dawn to-morrow, when the sun rises, Mitya will leap over that fence....", "그렇지만 내일 새벽, 해가 떠오르면 미탸는 그 울타리를 뛰어넘을 거야……"], ["You don’t understand what fence, Fenya, and, never mind....", "무슨 울타리인지 너는 모를 거야, 페냐, 하지만 괜찮아……"], ["You’ll hear to-morrow and understand ...", "내일이면 듣고 알게 될 테니……"], ["and now, good-by.", "그럼 이제 잘 있어."], ["I won’t stand in her way.", "나는 그 여자의 길을 막지 않겠어."], ["I’ll step aside, I know how to step aside.", "나는 옆으로 비켜설 거야, 나는 비켜설 줄 안다고."], ["Live, my joy....", "잘 살아라, 내 기쁨이여……"], ["You loved me for an hour, remember Mityenka Karamazov so for ever....", "너는 한 시간 동안 나를 사랑했으니, 미텐카 카라마조프를 그렇게 영원히 기억해 줘……"], ["She always used to call me Mityenka, do you remember?”", "그 여자는 늘 나를 미텐카라고 불렀지, 기억나니?\""]], [["And with those words he went suddenly out of the kitchen.", "그렇게 말하고 그는 별안간 부엌을 나가 버렸다."], ["Fenya was almost more frightened at this sudden departure than she had been when he ran in and attacked her.", "페냐는 그가 뛰어 들어와 자기에게 달려들었을 때보다도 이 느닷없는 떠남에 거의 더 겁을 먹었다."]], [["Just ten minutes later Dmitri went in to Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, the young official with whom he had pawned his pistols.", "꼭 십 분 뒤에 드미트리는 표트르 일리치 페르호틴에게로 들어갔으니, 그는 미탸가 권총을 저당 잡혔던 그 젊은 관리였다."], ["It was by now half-past eight, and Pyotr Ilyitch had finished his evening tea, and had just put his coat on again to go to the “Metropolis” to play billiards.", "어느덧 여덟 시 반이 되어 있었고, 표트르 일리치는 저녁 차를 마치고 막 다시 외투를 걸치고서 \"메트로폴리스\"로 당구를 치러 가려던 참이었다."], ["Mitya caught him coming out.", "미탸는 막 나오는 그를 붙들었다."]], [["Seeing him with his face all smeared with blood, the young man uttered a cry of surprise.", "얼굴이 온통 피로 얼룩진 그를 보고 젊은 관리는 놀라 소리쳤다."]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"이게 무슨 일이오!"], ["What is the matter?”", "어찌 된 거요?\""]], [["“I’ve come for my pistols,”", "\"내 권총을 찾으러 왔소.\""], ["said Mitya, “and brought you the money.", "미탸가 말했다. \"그리고 돈을 가져왔소."], ["And thanks very much.", "정말 고맙소."], ["I’m in a hurry, Pyotr Ilyitch, please make haste.”", "내가 좀 급하오, 표트르 일리치, 부디 서둘러 주시오.\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch grew more and more surprised; he suddenly caught sight of a bundle of bank-notes in Mitya’s hand, and what was more, he had walked in holding the notes as no one walks in and no one carries money: he had them in his right hand, and held them outstretched as if to show them.", "표트르 일리치는 점점 더 놀랐다. 그는 문득 미탸의 손에 한 뭉치의 지폐가 쥐어져 있는 것을 알아챘고, 더구나 그는 아무도 그렇게 들어오지 않고 아무도 그렇게 돈을 들고 다니지 않는 모양새로 지폐를 쥐고 걸어 들어왔던 것이다."], ["Perhotin’s servant-boy, who met Mitya in the passage, said afterwards that he walked into the passage in the same way, with the money outstretched in his hand, so he must have been carrying them like that even in the streets.", "그는 오른손에 지폐를 들고서, 마치 보여 주려는 듯 앞으로 쭉 내민 채였다. 통로에서 미탸를 맞은 페르호틴의 사환 아이는 나중에 말하기를, 그가 통로로 들어올 때도 그런 식으로, 돈을 손에 쭉 내민 채 걸어 들어왔다고 했으니, 그렇다면 그는 거리에서조차 그렇게 들고 다녔던 것이 분명하다."], ["They were all rainbow-colored hundred-rouble notes, and the fingers holding them were covered with blood.", "죄다 무지갯빛이 도는 백 루블짜리 지폐였고, 그것을 쥔 손가락은 피로 뒤덮여 있었다."]], [["When Pyotr Ilyitch was questioned later on as to the sum of money, he said that it was difficult to judge at a glance, but that it might have been two thousand, or perhaps three, but it was a big, “fat”", "표트르 일리치는 나중에 그 액수에 관해 질문을 받았을 때, 한눈에 가늠하기는 어려웠지만 이천 루블쯤, 혹은 어쩌면 삼천 루블쯤 되었을 것이라고, 어쨌든 큼지막한 \"두툼한\" 뭉치였다고 말했다."], ["bundle.", "\"드미트리 표도로비치는\""], ["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "하고 그는 나중에 증언했다."], ["so he testified afterwards, “seemed unlike himself, too; not drunk, but, as it were, exalted, lost to everything, but at the same time, as it were, absorbed, as though pondering and searching for something and unable to come to a decision.", "\"어딘지 평소의 그 사람 같지 않았습니다. 취한 것은 아니었으나 말하자면 들떠 있었고, 모든 것을 잊은 듯하면서도 동시에 무언가에 골똘히 빠진 듯, 마치 무언가를 곰곰 생각하며 찾고 있는데 결단을 내리지 못하는 것 같았습니다."], ["He was in great haste, answered abruptly and very strangely, and at moments seemed not at all dejected but quite cheerful.”", "그는 몹시 서둘렀고, 무뚝뚝하면서도 아주 야릇하게 대답했으며, 어떤 순간에는 전혀 풀이 죽은 기색이 아니라 도리어 아주 쾌활해 보였습니다.\""]], [["“But what is the matter with you?", "\"대체 무슨 일이오? 무슨 일이 난 거요?\""], ["What’s wrong?” cried Pyotr Ilyitch, looking wildly at his guest.", "표트르 일리치가 손님을 놀란 눈으로 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["“How is it that you’re all covered with blood?", "\"어쩌다 온통 피투성이가 됐소?"], ["Have you had a fall?", "넘어지기라도 했소?"], ["Look at yourself!”", "자기 꼴 좀 보시오!\""]], [["He took him by the elbow and led him to the glass.", "그는 미탸의 팔꿈치를 잡아 거울 앞으로 이끌었다."]], [["Seeing his blood-stained face, Mitya started and scowled wrathfully.", "피로 얼룩진 자기 얼굴을 보고 미탸는 흠칫하더니 노여운 듯 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Damnation!", "\"제기랄!"], ["That’s the last straw,”", "이런 일까지.\""], ["he muttered angrily, hurriedly changing the notes from his right hand to the left, and impulsively jerked the handkerchief out of his pocket.", "그는 화가 나서 중얼거리며 오른손의 지폐를 황급히 왼손으로 옮기고는, 충동적으로 주머니에서 손수건을 홱 꺼냈다."], ["But the handkerchief turned out to be soaked with blood, too (it was the handkerchief he had used to wipe Grigory’s face). There was scarcely a white spot on it, and it had not merely begun to dry, but had stiffened into a crumpled ball and could not be pulled apart.", "그러나 손수건도 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었으니 (그것은 그가 그리고리의 얼굴을 닦는 데 쓴 손수건이었다) 흰 자국이라곤 거의 남아 있지 않았고, 마르기 시작했을 뿐 아니라 구깃구깃한 덩어리로 빳빳하게 굳어 펼칠 수조차 없었다."], ["Mitya threw it angrily on the floor.", "미탸는 화가 나서 그것을 바닥에 내던졌다."]], [["“Oh, damn it!” he said.", "\"아, 빌어먹을!\""], ["“Haven’t you a rag of some sort ...", "그가 말했다. \"헝겊 쪼가리라도 없소……"], ["to wipe my face?”", "얼굴 좀 닦게?\""]], [["“So you’re only stained, not wounded?", "\"그러니까 다친 게 아니라 그냥 묻은 거로군."], ["You’d better wash,”", "씻는 게 낫겠소.\""], ["said Pyotr Ilyitch.", "표트르 일리치가 말했다."], ["“Here’s a wash-stand.", "\"여기 세면대가 있소."], ["I’ll pour you out some water.”", "내가 물을 부어 주리다.\""]], [["“A wash-stand?", "\"세면대?"], ["That’s all right ...", "그거 좋군……"], ["but where am I to put this?”", "그런데 이건 어디다 두지?\""]], [["With the strangest perplexity he indicated his bundle of hundred-rouble notes, looking inquiringly at Pyotr Ilyitch as though it were for him to decide what he, Mitya, was to do with his own money.", "그는 더없이 야릇하게 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 가리켰고, 마치 자기, 미탸가 자기 돈을 어떻게 해야 할지 표트르 일리치더러 정해 달라는 듯 그를 미심쩍게 바라보았다."]], [["“In your pocket, or on the table here.", "\"주머니에 넣든지, 아니면 여기 탁자 위에 두시오."], ["They won’t be lost.”", "없어지지 않을 테니.\""]], [["“In my pocket?", "\"주머니에?"], ["Yes, in my pocket.", "그래, 주머니에."], ["All right....", "좋아……"], ["But, I say, that’s all nonsense,”", "그런데 말이오, 이건 다 부질없는 짓이지.\""], ["he cried, as though suddenly coming out of his absorption.", "그는 별안간 골똘한 상념에서 깨어난 듯 외쳤다."], ["“Look here, let’s first settle that business of the pistols.", "\"이봐요, 먼저 그 권총 일부터 끝냅시다."], ["Give them back to me.", "그걸 내게 돌려주시오."], ["Here’s your money ...", "여기 당신 돈이오……"], ["because I am in great need of them ...", "내가 그게 몹시 필요하거든……"], ["and I haven’t a minute, a minute to spare.”", "게다가 나는 잠시도, 단 한순간도 지체할 틈이 없소.\""]], [["And taking the topmost note from the bundle he held it out to Pyotr Ilyitch.", "그리고 뭉치에서 맨 위의 지폐를 빼서 표트르 일리치에게 내밀었다."]], [["“But I shan’t have change enough.", "\"하지만 내겐 거스를 잔돈이 모자랄 거요."], ["Haven’t you less?”", "더 작은 건 없소?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"없소.\""], ["said Mitya, looking again at the bundle, and as though not trusting his own words he turned over two or three of the topmost ones.", "미탸가 다시 뭉치를 들여다보며 말했고, 마치 제 말이 미덥지 않은 듯 맨 위의 두세 장을 들춰 보았다."]], [["“No, they’re all alike,”", "\"없군, 다 똑같은 거요.\""], ["he added, and again he looked inquiringly at Pyotr Ilyitch.", "그가 덧붙이며 다시 표트르 일리치를 미심쩍게 바라보았다."]], [["“How have you grown so rich?”", "\"어쩌다 이렇게 부자가 됐소?\""], ["the latter asked.", "상대가 물었다."], ["“Wait, I’ll send my boy to Plotnikov’s, they close late—to see if they won’t change it.", "\"잠깐, 우리 집 아이를 플로트니코프 가게로 보내리다. 거긴 늦게까지 문을 여니까."], ["Here, Misha!”", "거스를 수 있는지 보지요."], ["he called into the passage.", "미샤, 이리 와!\" 그가 통로 쪽으로 불렀다."]], [["“To Plotnikov’s shop—first-rate!”", "\"플로트니코프 가게로! 그거 아주 좋군!\""], ["cried Mitya, as though struck by an idea.", "미탸가 무슨 생각에 퍼뜩 떠오른 듯 외쳤다."], ["“Misha,”", "\"미샤,\""], ["he turned to the boy as he came in, “look here, run to Plotnikov’s and tell them that Dmitri Fyodorovitch sends his greetings, and will be there directly....", "그는 들어오는 아이에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이봐, 플로트니코프 가게로 달려가서, 드미트리 표도로비치가 안부를 전한다고, 곧 갈 거라고 일러라……"], ["But listen, listen, tell them to have champagne, three dozen bottles, ready before I come, and packed as it was to take to Mokroe.", "그런데 잘 들어, 잘 들어, 샴페인을 준비해 두라고 해, 세 다스를, 내가 가기 전에 마련해서, 모크로예로 가져갈 때처럼 꾸려 놓으라고 해."], ["I took four dozen with me then,”", "그때 나는 네 다스를 가져갔지.\""], ["he added (suddenly addressing Pyotr Ilyitch); “they know all about it, don’t you trouble, Misha,”", "그가 (별안간 표트르 일리치를 향해) 덧붙였다. \"그쪽이 다 알고 있으니, 너는 걱정 말아라, 미샤.\""], ["he turned again to the boy.", "그는 다시 아이에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“Stay, listen; tell them to put in cheese, Strasburg pies, smoked fish, ham, caviare, and everything, everything they’ve got, up to a hundred roubles, or a hundred and twenty as before....", "\"가만, 들어 봐. 치즈하고, 스트라스부르 파이하고, 훈제 생선하고, 햄하고, 캐비아하고, 죄다, 가게에 있는 건 죄다 넣으라고 해, 백 루블어치, 아니 예전처럼 백이십 루블어치까지……"], ["But wait: don’t let them forget dessert, sweets, pears, water-melons, two or three or four—no, one melon’s enough, and chocolate, candy, toffee, fondants; in fact, everything I took to Mokroe before, three hundred roubles’ worth with the champagne ...", "가만, 후식도 잊지 말라고 해, 사탕이며 배며 수박이며, 두세 개, 아니 네 개—아니, 수박은 하나면 충분해, 그리고 초콜릿, 캔디, 토피, 봉봉, 하여간 예전에 모크로예로 가져간 건 죄다, 샴페인 합쳐서 삼백 루블어치로……"], ["let it be just the same again.", "꼭 그때와 똑같이 하라고 해."], ["And remember, Misha, if you are called Misha—His name is Misha, isn’t it?”", "그리고 미샤, 기억해 둬, 네 이름이 미샤라면 말이지—이 애 이름이 미샤 맞소?\""], ["He turned to Pyotr Ilyitch again.", "그는 다시 표트르 일리치에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Wait a minute,”", "\"잠깐만.\""], ["Protr Ilyitch intervened, listening and watching him uneasily, “you’d better go yourself and tell them.", "표트르 일리치가 불안하게 그를 듣고 지켜보다가 끼어들었다. \"당신이 직접 가서 일러 주는 게 낫겠소."], ["He’ll muddle it.”", "이 애는 다 헷갈리게 할 테니.\""]], [["“He will, I see he will!", "\"그러겠지, 그럴 거란 걸 알아!"], ["Eh, Misha!", "어이, 미샤!"], ["Why, I was going to kiss you for the commission....", "아니, 심부름을 시키려고 너한테 입이라도 맞추려던 참인데……"], ["If you don’t make a mistake, there’s ten roubles for you, run along, make haste....", "틀리지만 않으면, 너한테 십 루블을 주마, 어서 달려가, 서둘러……"], ["Champagne’s the chief thing, let them bring up champagne.", "샴페인이 제일 중요해, 샴페인을 올려 보내라고 해."], ["And brandy, too, and red and white wine, and all I had then....", "그리고 브랜디도, 적포도주와 백포도주도, 그때 내가 가져간 건 죄다……"], ["They know what I had then.”", "그쪽이 그때 뭘 가져갔는지 다 알고 있어.\""]], [["“But listen!”", "\"하지만 들어 보시오!\""], ["Pyotr Ilyitch interrupted with some impatience.", "표트르 일리치가 약간 짜증스레 말을 끊었다."], ["“I say, let him simply run and change the money and tell them not to close, and you go and tell them....", "\"그러니까, 그냥 이 애더러 달려가서 돈을 거슬러 오고, 문 닫지 말라고만 이르게 합시다. 그리고 당신이 가서 일러 주시오……"], ["Give him your note.", "이 애한테 지폐를 주시오."], ["Be off, Misha!", "가거라, 미샤!"], ["Put your best leg forward!”", "어서 발 빠르게!\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch seemed to hurry Misha off on purpose, because the boy remained standing with his mouth and eyes wide open, apparently understanding little of Mitya’s orders, gazing up with amazement and terror at his blood-stained face and the trembling bloodstained fingers that held the notes.", "표트르 일리치는 일부러 미샤를 서둘러 내보내려는 듯했으니, 아이가 입과 눈을 휘둥그렇게 벌린 채 멍하니 서서 미탸의 분부를 도통 알아듣지 못하는 듯, 그의 피투성이 얼굴과 지폐를 쥔 피투성이의 떨리는 손가락을 놀라움과 공포에 차서 올려다보고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Well, now come and wash,”", "\"자, 이제 와서 씻으시오.\""], ["said Pyotr Ilyitch sternly.", "표트르 일리치가 엄하게 말했다."], ["“Put the money on the table or else in your pocket....", "\"돈은 탁자 위에 두든지 주머니에 넣든지 하고……"], ["That’s right, come along.", "그렇지, 이리 오시오."], ["But take off your coat.”", "그런데 외투를 벗으시오.\""]], [["And beginning to help him off with his coat, he cried out again:", "그리고 그의 외투 벗는 것을 거들기 시작하다가 그는 또 외쳤다."]], [["“Look, your coat’s covered with blood, too!”", "\"이것 보시오, 외투에도 온통 피가 묻었잖소!\""]], [["“That ...", "\"그건……"], ["it’s not the coat.", "외투가 아니오."], ["It’s only a little here on the sleeve....", "그건 그냥 여기 소매에 조금……"], ["And that’s only here where the handkerchief lay.", "그리고 그것도 손수건이 닿았던 데뿐이오."], ["It must have soaked through.", "배어 나온 게 틀림없소."], ["I must have sat on the handkerchief at Fenya’s, and the blood’s come through,”", "페냐네 집에서 손수건 위에 앉았던 모양이라, 피가 배어 나온 거요.\""], ["Mitya explained at once with a childlike unconsciousness that was astounding.", "미탸는 놀라우리만치 어린아이 같은 천진함으로 곧장 설명했다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch listened, frowning.", "표트르 일리치는 눈살을 찌푸리며 들었다."]], [["“Well, you must have been up to something; you must have been fighting with some one,”", "\"그러니까 무슨 일을 저지른 게 분명하군. 누군가와 싸운 게 틀림없소.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["They began to wash.", "두 사람은 씻기 시작했다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch held the jug and poured out the water.", "표트르 일리치가 물주전자를 들고 물을 부었다."], ["Mitya, in desperate haste, scarcely soaped his hands (they were trembling, and Pyotr Ilyitch remembered it afterwards).", "미탸는 죽도록 서두르며 손에 비누칠도 제대로 하지 않았다 (손이 떨리고 있었는데, 표트르 일리치는 나중에 그것을 기억해 냈다)."], ["But the young official insisted on his soaping them thoroughly and rubbing them more.", "그러나 젊은 관리는 손에 비누칠을 꼼꼼히 하고 더 문지르라고 우겼다."], ["He seemed to exercise more and more sway over Mitya, as time went on.", "시간이 갈수록 그는 미탸에게 점점 더 영향력을 행사하는 듯했다."], ["It may be noted in passing that he was a young man of sturdy character.", "그러고 보면 그는 다부진 성격의 젊은이였다는 점을 덧붙여 둘 만하다."]], [["“Look, you haven’t got your nails clean.", "\"이것 보시오, 손톱이 깨끗하지 않잖소."], ["Now rub your face; here, on your temples, by your ear....", "이제 얼굴을 문지르시오. 자, 여기 관자놀이, 귀 옆에다……"], ["Will you go in that shirt?", "그 셔츠를 입고 갈 거요?"], ["Where are you going?", "어디로 가는 거요?"], ["Look, all the cuff of your right sleeve is covered with blood.”", "보시오, 오른쪽 소맷부리가 온통 피투성이잖소.\""]], [["“Yes, it’s all bloody,”", "\"그래, 다 피투성이로군.\""], ["observed Mitya, looking at the cuff of his shirt.", "미탸가 셔츠 소맷부리를 들여다보며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Then change your shirt.”", "\"그럼 셔츠를 갈아입으시오.\""]], [["“I haven’t time.", "\"그럴 시간이 없소."], ["You see I’ll ...”", "그러니까 나는……\""], ["Mitya went on with the same confiding ingenuousness, drying his face and hands on the towel, and putting on his coat.", "미탸는 여전히 그 미더운 천진함으로 말을 이으며 수건으로 얼굴과 손을 닦고 외투를 걸쳤다."], ["“I’ll turn it up at the wrist.", "\"소맷부리를 걷어 올리면 돼요."], ["It won’t be seen under the coat....", "외투 밑에 가려서 안 보일 테니……"], ["You see!”", "봐요!\""]], [["“Tell me now, what game have you been up to?", "\"그러지 말고 이제 말해 보시오, 무슨 짓을 벌인 거요?"], ["Have you been fighting with some one?", "누군가와 싸웠소? 또 전처럼 술집에서?"], ["In the tavern again, as before?", "또 그 대위를 두들겨 팼소?\""], ["Have you been beating that captain again?”", "표트르 일리치가 나무라듯 물었다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch asked him reproachfully.", "\"이번엔 누구를 두들겨 팬 거요……"], ["“Whom have you been beating now ... or killing, perhaps?”", "아니, 어쩌면 죽인 거요?\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"헛소리!\""], ["said Mitya.", "미탸가 말했다."]], [["“Why ‘nonsense’?”", "\"어째서 '헛소리'요?\""]], [["“Don’t worry,”", "\"걱정 마시오.\""], ["said Mitya, and he suddenly laughed.", "미탸가 말하고는 별안간 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“I smashed an old woman in the market-place just now.”", "\"내가 방금 시장 바닥에서 노파 하나를 박살 냈거든.\""]], [["“Smashed?", "\"박살을?"], ["An old woman?”", "노파를?\""]], [["“An old man!”", "\"노인을!\""], ["cried Mitya, looking Pyotr Ilyitch straight in the face, laughing, and shouting at him as though he were deaf.", "미탸가 표트르 일리치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며, 웃으면서, 마치 그가 귀머거리라도 되는 양 그에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Confound it!", "\"빌어먹을!"], ["An old woman, an old man....", "노파다, 노인이다……"], ["Have you killed some one?”", "누군가를 죽인 거요?\""]], [["“We made it up.", "\"우린 화해했소."], ["We had a row—and made it up.", "우린 다투었고—화해했소."], ["In a place I know of.", "내가 아는 어떤 데서."], ["We parted friends.", "우린 친구로 헤어졌소."], ["A fool....", "바보 같은 자였지……"], ["He’s forgiven me....", "그자는 날 용서했소……"], ["He’s sure to have forgiven me by now ...", "지금쯤은 틀림없이 날 용서했을 거요……"], ["if he had got up, he wouldn’t have forgiven me”—Mitya suddenly winked—“only damn him, you know, I say, Pyotr Ilyitch, damn him!", "일어났다면 날 용서하지 않았겠지만\"—미탸가 별안간 눈을 찡긋했다—\"하지만 빌어먹을, 알겠소, 이봐요, 표트르 일리치, 그자 따위는 집어치우시오!"], ["Don’t worry about him!", "그자 걱정은 마시오!"], ["I don’t want to just now!”", "지금은 그러고 싶지 않으니까!\""], ["Mitya snapped out, resolutely.", "미탸가 단호하게 잘라 말했다."]], [["“Whatever do you want to go picking quarrels with every one for?", "\"대체 무엇하러 아무하고나 시비를 거는 거요?……"], ["... Just as you did with that captain over some nonsense....", "꼭 그 대위하고 그랬듯이, 별것도 아닌 일로 말이오……"], ["You’ve been fighting and now you’re rushing off on the spree—that’s you all over!", "싸움질을 해 놓고 이제 또 술판을 벌이러 내달리다니—영락없는 당신답군!"], ["Three dozen champagne—what do you want all that for?”", "샴페인 세 다스라니—그 많은 게 다 무슨 소용이오?\""]], [["“Bravo!", "\"좋았어!"], ["Now give me the pistols.", "자, 이제 권총을 주시오."], ["Upon my honor I’ve no time now.", "맹세코 지금은 시간이 없소."], ["I should like to have a chat with you, my dear boy, but I haven’t the time.", "자네하고 좀 이야기를 나누고 싶지만, 친구, 시간이 없소."], ["And there’s no need, it’s too late for talking.", "게다가 그럴 필요도 없지, 이야기하기엔 너무 늦었으니."], ["Where’s my money?", "내 돈이 어디 있지?"], ["Where have I put it?”", "어디다 뒀더라?\""], ["he cried, thrusting his hands into his pockets.", "그가 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣으며 외쳤다."]], [["“You put it on the table ...", "\"당신이 탁자 위에 뒀잖소……"], ["yourself....", "직접……"], ["Here it is.", "여기 있소."], ["Had you forgotten?", "잊었소?"], ["Money’s like dirt or water to you, it seems.", "당신한텐 돈이 흙이나 물 같은 모양이군."], ["Here are your pistols.", "자, 당신 권총이오."], ["It’s an odd thing, at six o’clock you pledged them for ten roubles, and now you’ve got thousands.", "참 묘한 일이군, 여섯 시엔 이걸 십 루블에 저당 잡히더니, 지금은 수천 루블이 생겼으니."], ["Two or three I should say.”", "이삼천 루블은 되겠소.\""]], [["“Three, you bet,”", "\"삼천이지, 틀림없이.\""], ["laughed Mitya, stuffing the notes into the side-pocket of his trousers.", "미탸가 웃으며 지폐를 바지 옆주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."]], [["“You’ll lose it like that.", "\"그러다 잃어버리겠소."], ["Have you found a gold-mine?”", "금광이라도 찾은 거요?\""]], [["“The mines?", "\"금광?"], ["The gold-mines?”", "금광 말이오?\""], ["Mitya shouted at the top of his voice and went off into a roar of laughter.", "미탸가 목청껏 소리치더니 한바탕 큰 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Would you like to go to the mines, Perhotin?", "\"금광에 가고 싶소, 페르호틴?"], ["There’s a lady here who’ll stump up three thousand for you, if only you’ll go.", "여기 어떤 부인이 있는데, 당신이 가겠다고만 하면 당신한테 삼천을 척 내놓을 거요."], ["She did it for me, she’s so awfully fond of gold-mines.", "그 부인이 나한테 그랬거든, 금광을 어찌나 좋아하는지."], ["Do you know Madame Hohlakov?”", "호흘라코바 부인을 아시오?\""]], [["“I don’t know her, but I’ve heard of her and seen her.", "\"알지는 못하지만, 소문은 들었고 본 적도 있소."], ["Did she really give you three thousand?", "정말로 당신한테 삼천을 줬소? 정말로?\""], ["Did she really?” said Pyotr Ilyitch, eyeing him dubiously.", "표트르 일리치가 미심쩍게 그를 훑어보며 말했다."]], [["“As soon as the sun rises to-morrow, as soon as Phœbus, ever young, flies upwards, praising and glorifying God, you go to her, this Madame Hohlakov, and ask her whether she did stump up that three thousand or not.", "\"내일 해가 떠오르면, 언제나 젊은 포이보스가 하느님을 찬미하고 영광 돌리며 솟아오르거든, 그때 그 부인한테, 그 호흘라코바 부인한테 가서 정말로 그 삼천을 척 내놓았는지 아닌지 물어보시오."], ["Try and find out.”", "한번 알아보란 말이오.\""]], [["“I don’t know on what terms you are ...", "\"당신네가 어떤 사이인지 나야 모르오만……"], ["since you say it so positively, I suppose she did give it to you.", "당신이 그렇게 딱 잘라 말하니, 정말로 줬나 보군."], ["You’ve got the money in your hand, but instead of going to Siberia you’re spending it all....", "돈을 손에 쥐었으면서 시베리아로 갈 생각은 않고 죄다 흥청망청 쓰려 하다니……"], ["Where are you really off to now, eh?”", "지금 정말로 어디로 가는 거요, 응?\""]], [["“To Mokroe.”", "\"모크로예로.\""]], [["“To Mokroe?", "\"모크로예로?"], ["But it’s night!”", "한밤중에?\""]], [["“Once the lad had all, now the lad has naught,”", "\"한때 사내는 다 가졌으나, 이제 사내는 빈털터리로다.\""], ["cried Mitya suddenly.", "미탸가 별안간 외쳤다."]], [["“How ‘naught’?", "\"어째서 '빈털터리'요?"], ["You say that with all those thousands!”", "그 수천 루블을 쥐고서 그런 소리를!\""]], [["“I’m not talking about thousands.", "\"수천 루블 얘기가 아니오."], ["Damn thousands!", "수천 루블 따위 집어치워!"], ["I’m talking of the female character.", "나는 여자의 마음을 말하는 거요."]], [["Fickle is the heart of woman Treacherous and full of vice;", "변덕스럽기는 여인의 마음, 믿을 수 없고 악덕으로 가득하니."]], [["I agree with Ulysses.", "나는 율리시스에게 동감이오."], ["That’s what he says.”", "바로 그가 한 말이지.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you!”", "\"무슨 소린지 모르겠소!\""]], [["“Am I drunk?”", "\"내가 취했소?\""]], [["“Not drunk, but worse.”", "\"취하진 않았지만, 그보다 더하오.\""]], [["“I’m drunk in spirit, Pyotr Ilyitch, drunk in spirit!", "\"나는 마음이 취했소, 표트르 일리치, 마음이 취했지!"], ["But that’s enough!”", "하지만 그쯤 하면 됐고!\""]], [["“What are you doing, loading the pistol?”", "\"뭘 하는 거요, 권총에 총알을 재는 거요?\""]], [["“I’m loading the pistol.”", "\"권총에 총알을 재고 있소.\""]], [["Unfastening the pistol-case, Mitya actually opened the powder horn, and carefully sprinkled and rammed in the charge.", "권총집을 끄르고서 미탸는 정말로 화약통을 열어 조심스레 화약을 뿌려 넣고 다져 넣었다."], ["Then he took the bullet and, before inserting it, held it in two fingers in front of the candle.", "그런 다음 총알을 집어, 끼워 넣기 전에 두 손가락으로 촛불 앞에 들어 보였다."]], [["“Why are you looking at the bullet?”", "\"왜 총알을 들여다보는 거요?\""], ["asked Pyotr Ilyitch, watching him with uneasy curiosity.", "표트르 일리치가 불안한 호기심으로 그를 지켜보며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, a fancy.", "\"아, 그냥 변덕이오."], ["Why, if you meant to put that bullet in your brain, would you look at it or not?”", "그게, 이 총알을 당신 머릿속에 박아 넣을 작정이라면, 들여다보겠소, 안 보겠소?\""]], [["“Why look at it?”", "\"왜 들여다본단 말이오?\""]], [["“It’s going into my brain, so it’s interesting to look and see what it’s like.", "\"이게 내 머릿속으로 들어갈 거니까, 어떻게 생겼는지 들여다보고 알아 두는 게 흥미롭지 않겠소."], ["But that’s foolishness, a moment’s foolishness.", "하지만 이런 건 바보짓이지, 한순간의 바보짓이오."], ["Now that’s done,”", "자, 이제 됐군.\""], ["he added, putting in the bullet and driving it home with the ramrod.", "그가 총알을 끼워 넣고 꽂을대로 다져 넣으며 덧붙였다."], ["“Pyotr Ilyitch, my dear fellow, that’s nonsense, all nonsense, and if only you knew what nonsense!", "\"표트르 일리치, 이보게, 이건 부질없는 짓이오, 죄다 부질없지, 얼마나 부질없는지 당신이 알기만 한다면!"], ["Give me a little piece of paper now.”", "자, 이제 종이 한 장만 주시오.\""]], [["“Here’s some paper.”", "\"여기 종이가 있소.\""]], [["“No, a clean new piece, writing-paper.", "\"아니, 깨끗한 새것, 편지지로."], ["That’s right.”", "그래, 그거요.\""]], [["And taking a pen from the table, Mitya rapidly wrote two lines, folded the paper in four, and thrust it in his waistcoat pocket.", "그리고 탁자에서 펜을 집어 미탸는 재빨리 두 줄을 적고, 종이를 네 겹으로 접어 조끼 주머니에 찔러 넣었다."], ["He put the pistols in the case, locked it up, and kept it in his hand.", "그는 권총을 권총집에 넣고 잠근 다음, 그것을 손에 쥐었다."], ["Then he looked at Pyotr Ilyitch with a slow, thoughtful smile.", "그러고는 느릿하고 사색에 잠긴 미소로 표트르 일리치를 바라보았다."]], [["“Now, let’s go.”", "\"자, 갑시다.\""]], [["“Where are we going?", "\"어디로 가는 거요?"], ["No, wait a minute....", "아니, 잠깐만……"], ["Are you thinking of putting that bullet in your brain, perhaps?”", "혹시 그 총알을 당신 머릿속에 박을 생각인 거요?\""], ["Pyotr Ilyitch asked uneasily.", "표트르 일리치가 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“I was fooling about the bullet!", "\"총알 얘기는 그냥 장난이었소!"], ["I want to live.", "나는 살고 싶소."], ["I love life!", "나는 삶을 사랑하오!"], ["You may be sure of that.", "그건 믿어도 좋소."], ["I love golden-haired Phœbus and his warm light....", "나는 금발의 포이보스와 그 따스한 빛을 사랑하지……"], ["Dear Pyotr Ilyitch, do you know how to step aside?”", "이보게, 표트르 일리치, 당신은 옆으로 비켜설 줄 아오?\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘stepping aside’?”", "\"'옆으로 비켜선다'는 게 무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“Making way.", "\"길을 내주는 거요."], ["Making way for a dear creature, and for one I hate.", "어떤 사랑스러운 존재를 위해, 그리고 내가 미워하는 자를 위해 길을 내주는 거지."], ["And to let the one I hate become dear—that’s what making way means!", "그리고 내가 미워하는 자가 사랑받게 만드는 것—그게 길을 내준다는 뜻이오! 그리고 그들에게 이렇게 말하는 거지."], ["And to say to them: God bless you, go your way, pass on, while I—”", "하느님이 당신들을 축복하시기를, 당신들 길을 가시오, 지나가시오, 그동안 나는—\""]], [["“While you—?”", "\"그동안 당신은—?\""]], [["“That’s enough, let’s go.”", "\"그쯤이면 됐소, 갑시다.\""]], [["“Upon my word. I’ll tell some one to prevent your going there,”", "\"맹세코, 누군가한테 일러서 당신이 거기 가는 걸 막게 하겠소.\""], ["said Pyotr Ilyitch, looking at him.", "표트르 일리치가 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“What are you going to Mokroe for, now?”", "\"지금 모크로예엔 무엇하러 가는 거요?\""]], [["“There’s a woman there, a woman.", "\"거기 한 여자가 있소, 한 여자가."], ["That’s enough for you.", "당신한텐 그거면 됐소."], ["You shut up.”", "입 다무시오.\""]], [["“Listen, though you’re such a savage I’ve always liked you....", "\"이보시오, 당신이 이런 야만인이긴 해도 나는 늘 당신을 좋아했소……"], ["I feel anxious.”", "마음이 놓이질 않는구려.\""]], [["“Thanks, old fellow.", "\"고맙소, 친구."], ["I’m a savage you say.", "내가 야만인이라고 했지."], ["Savages, savages!", "야만인, 야만인이라!"], ["That’s what I am always saying.", "그게 바로 내가 늘 하는 말이오."], ["Savages!", "야만인!"], ["Why, here’s Misha!", "아니, 미샤가 왔군!"], ["I was forgetting him.”", "깜빡 잊고 있었네.\""]], [["Misha ran in, post-haste, with a handful of notes in change, and reported that every one was in a bustle at the Plotnikovs’; “They’re carrying down the bottles, and the fish, and the tea; it will all be ready directly.”", "미샤가 잔돈으로 바꾼 지폐를 한 움큼 쥐고 부랴부랴 뛰어 들어와, 플로트니코프네에선 다들 부산을 떨고 있다고 알렸다. \"병이며 생선이며 차를 들고 내려오고 있어요."], ["Mitya seized ten roubles and handed it to Pyotr Ilyitch, then tossed another ten-rouble note to Misha.", "곧 다 준비될 거래요.\" 미탸는 십 루블을 집어 표트르 일리치에게 건네고, 또 다른 십 루블짜리 지폐를 미샤에게 던져 주었다."]], [["“Don’t dare to do such a thing!”", "\"그런 짓은 하지 마시오!\""], ["cried Pyotr Ilyitch.", "표트르 일리치가 외쳤다."], ["“I won’t have it in my house, it’s a bad, demoralizing habit.", "\"내 집에선 그런 짓 못 보겠소, 그건 나쁜, 못된 버릇을 들이는 짓이오."], ["Put your money away.", "돈을 거두시오."], ["Here, put it here, why waste it?", "자, 여기 두시오, 뭣하러 낭비하는 거요?"], ["It would come in handy to-morrow, and I dare say you’ll be coming to me to borrow ten roubles again.", "내일이면 요긴할 텐데, 또 내게 와서 십 루블만 빌려 달라고 할 게 뻔하지 않소."], ["Why do you keep putting the notes in your side-pocket?", "어째서 자꾸 지폐를 옆주머니에 넣는 거요?"], ["Ah, you’ll lose them!”", "아, 그러다 잃어버리겠소!\""]], [["“I say, my dear fellow, let’s go to Mokroe together.”", "\"이보게, 친구, 함께 모크로예로 갑시다.\""]], [["“What should I go for?”", "\"내가 뭣하러 가겠소?\""]], [["“I say, let’s open a bottle at once, and drink to life!", "\"이봐요, 당장 한 병 따서 삶을 위해 건배합시다!"], ["I want to drink, and especially to drink with you.", "나는 마시고 싶소, 특히 당신하고 마시고 싶소."], ["I’ve never drunk with you, have I?”", "당신하고는 한 번도 마셔 본 적이 없잖소, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“Very well, we can go to the ‘Metropolis.’ I was just going there.”", "\"좋소, '메트로폴리스'로 가면 되겠군. 마침 거기 가려던 참이었으니.\""]], [["“I haven’t time for that.", "\"그럴 시간은 없소."], ["Let’s drink at the Plotnikovs’, in the back room.", "플로트니코프네 뒷방에서 마십시다."], ["Shall I ask you a riddle?”", "수수께끼 하나 내볼까?\""]], [["“Ask away.”", "\"내보시오.\""]], [["Mitya took the piece of paper out of his waistcoat pocket, unfolded it and showed it.", "미탸는 조끼 주머니에서 종이쪽지를 꺼내, 펼쳐서 보여 주었다."], ["In a large, distinct hand was written: “I punish myself for my whole life, my whole life I punish!”", "큼지막하고 또렷한 필체로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"나는 내 평생을 두고 나 자신을 벌하노라, 평생토록 나를 벌하노라!\""]], [["“I will certainly speak to some one, I’ll go at once,”", "\"내 반드시 누군가한테 일러두겠소, 지금 당장 가겠소.\""], ["said Pyotr Ilyitch, after reading the paper.", "표트르 일리치가 종이를 읽고 나서 말했다."]], [["“You won’t have time, dear boy, come and have a drink.", "\"그럴 시간 없소, 친구, 와서 한잔합시다."], ["March!”", "행진!\""]], [["Plotnikov’s shop was at the corner of the street, next door but one to Pyotr Ilyitch’s.", "플로트니코프 가게는 거리 모퉁이에, 표트르 일리치의 집에서 한 집 건너에 있었다."], ["It was the largest grocery shop in our town, and by no means a bad one, belonging to some rich merchants.", "그것은 우리 고장에서 제일 큰 식료품 가게로, 결코 변변찮은 가게가 아니었고, 어느 부유한 상인들의 소유였다."], ["They kept everything that could be got in a Petersburg shop, grocery of all sort, wines “bottled by the brothers Eliseyev,”", "그들은 페테르부르크 가게에서 구할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 갖춰 두었으니, 온갖 종류의 식료품, \"옐리세예프 형제가 병에 담은\""], ["fruits, cigars, tea, coffee, sugar, and so on.", "포도주, 과일, 시가, 차, 커피, 설탕 따위였다."], ["There were three shop-assistants and two errand boys always employed.", "점원이 셋, 심부름꾼 아이가 둘 늘 일하고 있었다."], ["Though our part of the country had grown poorer, the landowners had gone away, and trade had got worse, yet the grocery stores flourished as before, every year with increasing prosperity; there were plenty of purchasers for their goods.", "우리 고장이 한결 가난해지고 지주들이 떠나가고 장사가 더 나빠지긴 했으나, 식료품 가게만은 예전처럼 번창하여 해마다 형편이 나아졌으니, 그들의 물건을 사려는 손님이 넘쳐났던 것이다."]], [["They were awaiting Mitya with impatience in the shop.", "가게에서는 미탸를 애타게 기다리고 있었다."], ["They had vivid recollections of how he had bought, three or four weeks ago, wine and goods of all sorts to the value of several hundred roubles, paid for in cash (they would never have let him have anything on credit, of course).", "그들은 그가 서너 주 전에 포도주며 온갖 물건을 수백 루블어치나 사서 현금으로 치렀던 일을 생생히 기억하고 있었다 (물론 그들이 그에게 외상으로 물건을 줄 리는 결코 없었다)."], ["They remembered that then, as now, he had had a bundle of hundred-rouble notes in his hand, and had scattered them at random, without bargaining, without reflecting, or caring to reflect what use so much wine and provisions would be to him.", "그들은 그때도 지금처럼 그가 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고서, 흥정도 하지 않고, 곰곰 따져 보지도, 그렇게 많은 포도주며 식료품이 자기에게 무슨 소용이 있을지 따져 볼 마음도 없이 마구 뿌려 댔던 것을 기억하고 있었다."], ["The story was told all over the town that, driving off then with Grushenka to Mokroe, he had “spent three thousand in one night and the following day, and had come back from the spree without a penny.”", "온 고을에 이런 이야기가 퍼져 있었으니, 그때 그루셴카와 함께 모크로예로 마차를 몰고 가서 \"하룻밤과 이튿날에 삼천 루블을 써 버리고, 술판에서 한 푼도 없이 돌아왔다\"는 것이었다."], ["He had picked up a whole troop of gypsies (encamped in our neighborhood at the time), who for two days got money without stint out of him while he was drunk, and drank expensive wine without stint.", "그는 (그 무렵 우리 동네에 진을 치고 있던) 집시 한 떼를 통째로 불러들였는데, 그들은 그가 술에 취해 있는 이틀 동안 그에게서 아낌없이 돈을 뜯어내고 값비싼 포도주를 실컷 들이켰다."], ["People used to tell, laughing at Mitya, how he had given champagne to grimy-handed peasants, and feasted the village women and girls on sweets and Strasburg pies.", "사람들은 미탸를 비웃으며 이야기하곤 했으니, 그가 손이 새까만 농부들에게 샴페인을 먹이고 마을 아낙들과 처녀들에게 사탕이며 스트라스부르 파이로 잔치를 베풀었다는 것이었다."], ["Though to laugh at Mitya to his face was rather a risky proceeding, there was much laughter behind his back, especially in the tavern, at his own ingenuous public avowal that all he had got out of Grushenka by this “escapade” was “permission to kiss her foot, and that was the utmost she had allowed him.”", "미탸의 면전에서 그를 비웃는 것은 다소 위험한 일이긴 했으나, 등 뒤에서는, 특히 술집에서는 그를 두고 적잖은 웃음거리가 오갔으니, 이 \"탈선\"으로 그루셴카에게서 얻어 낸 것이라곤 \"그녀의 발에 입 맞추는 허락뿐이었고, 그것이 그녀가 허락한 전부였다\"고 그가 스스로 천진하게 사람들 앞에서 털어놓았기 때문이다."]], [["By the time Mitya and Pyotr Ilyitch reached the shop, they found a cart with three horses harnessed abreast with bells, and with Andrey, the driver, ready waiting for Mitya at the entrance.", "미탸와 표트르 일리치가 가게에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 방울을 단 채 세 마리 말이 나란히 매인 짐마차가, 마부 안드레이와 함께 입구에서 미탸를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["In the shop they had almost entirely finished packing one box of provisions, and were only waiting for Mitya’s arrival to nail it down and put it in the cart.", "가게에서는 식료품 한 상자의 꾸리기를 거의 다 끝내고, 못질을 해서 짐마차에 실으려고 미탸가 오기만을 기다리고 있던 참이었다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch was astounded.", "표트르 일리치는 어안이 벙벙했다."]], [["“Where did this cart come from in such a hurry?”", "\"이 짐마차는 이렇게 급히 어디서 난 거요?\""], ["he asked Mitya.", "그가 미탸에게 물었다."]], [["“I met Andrey as I ran to you, and told him to drive straight here to the shop.", "\"당신한테 달려오다가 안드레이를 만나서, 곧장 여기 가게로 몰고 오라고 일렀소."], ["There’s no time to lose.", "지체할 시간이 없으니까."], ["Last time I drove with Timofey, but Timofey now has gone on before me with the witch.", "지난번엔 티모페이하고 갔는데, 티모페이는 지금 그 마녀하고 나보다 먼저 떠나 버렸소."], ["Shall we be very late, Andrey?”", "우리가 많이 늦을까, 안드레이?\""]], [["“They’ll only get there an hour at most before us, not even that maybe.", "\"기껏해야 한 시간, 그것도 안 될지 모릅죠. 저쪽보다 한 시간 먼저 닿을까 말까 합니다."], ["I got Timofey ready to start.", "제가 티모페이를 채비시켜 출발시켰는뎁쇼."], ["I know how he’ll go.", "그자가 어떻게 갈지 제가 압죠."], ["Their pace won’t be ours, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그 사람들 속도가 우리만 하겠습니까요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리."], ["How could it be?", "어떻게 그러겠어요?"], ["They won’t get there an hour earlier!”", "한 시간 먼저는 못 닿을 겁니다요!\""], ["Andrey, a lanky, red-haired, middle-aged driver, wearing a full-skirted coat, and with a kaftan on his arm, replied warmly.", "안드레이는 키 크고 빨간 머리에 중년인 마부로, 자락이 긴 외투를 입고 팔에는 카프탄을 걸친 채 신이 나서 대답했다."]], [["“Fifty roubles for vodka if we’re only an hour behind them.”", "\"저쪽보다 한 시간만 뒤처진다면 보드카 값으로 오십 루블이다.\""]], [["“I warrant the time, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"제가 시간을 책임집죠, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리."], ["Ech, they won’t be half an hour before us, let alone an hour.”", "에헤, 한 시간은커녕 반 시간도 저쪽이 우리보다 앞서진 못할 겁니다요.\""]], [["Though Mitya bustled about seeing after things, he gave his orders strangely, as it were disconnectedly, and inconsecutively.", "미탸는 부산을 떨며 일들을 챙기긴 했으나, 분부를 야릇하게, 말하자면 두서없이 앞뒤도 맞지 않게 내렸다."], ["He began a sentence and forgot the end of it.", "그는 한 문장을 시작해 놓고 끝을 잊어버렸다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch found himself obliged to come to the rescue.", "표트르 일리치는 거들고 나서지 않을 수 없게 되었다."]], [["“Four hundred roubles’ worth, not less than four hundred roubles’ worth, just as it was then,”", "\"사백 루블어치다, 사백 루블어치보다 적어선 안 돼, 꼭 그때처럼.\""], ["commanded Mitya.", "미탸가 명령했다."], ["“Four dozen champagne, not a bottle less.”", "\"샴페인 네 다스, 한 병도 모자라선 안 돼.\""]], [["“What do you want with so much?", "\"그 많은 게 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["What’s it for?", "뭣에 쓰려고?"], ["Stay!”", "잠깐!\""], ["cried Pyotr Ilyitch.", "표트르 일리치가 외쳤다."], ["“What’s this box?", "\"이 상자는 뭐요?"], ["What’s in it?", "안에 뭐가 들었소?"], ["Surely there isn’t four hundred roubles’ worth here?”", "설마 여기 사백 루블어치가 들어 있는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["The officious shopmen began explaining with oily politeness that the first box contained only half a dozen bottles of champagne, and only “the most indispensable articles,” such as savories, sweets, toffee, etc. But the main part of the goods ordered would be packed and sent off, as on the previous occasion, in a special cart also with three horses traveling at full speed, so that it would arrive not more than an hour later than Dmitri Fyodorovitch himself.", "설치기 좋아하는 점원들이 느물느물한 공손함으로 설명하기 시작하기를, 첫 번째 상자에는 샴페인 여섯 병과 안주며 사탕이며 토피 따위 \"꼭 있어야 할 물건들\"만 들어 있고, 주문하신 물건의 대부분은 지난번처럼 역시 세 마리 말이 끄는 특별한 짐마차에 꾸려서 전속력으로 보낼 터이니, 드미트리 표도로비치 본인보다 한 시간 이상 늦지 않게 도착할 거라고 했다."]], [["“Not more than an hour!", "\"한 시간 이상 늦으면 안 돼!"], ["Not more than an hour!", "한 시간 이상은 안 돼!"], ["And put in more toffee and fondants.", "그리고 토피와 봉봉을 더 넣어."], ["The girls there are so fond of it,”", "거기 처녀들이 그걸 어찌나 좋아하는지.\""], ["Mitya insisted hotly.", "미탸가 열을 올려 우겼다."]], [["“The fondants are all right.", "\"봉봉이야 좋소."], ["But what do you want with four dozen of champagne?", "하지만 샴페인 네 다스는 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["One would be enough,”", "한 다스면 충분할 텐데.\""], ["said Pyotr Ilyitch, almost angry.", "표트르 일리치가 거의 화를 내며 말했다."], ["He began bargaining, asking for a bill of the goods, and refused to be satisfied.", "그는 흥정을 시작해 물건 명세서를 청하고는 좀처럼 수긍하려 들지 않았다."], ["But he only succeeded in saving a hundred roubles.", "그러나 그가 절약한 것은 고작 백 루블뿐이었다."], ["In the end it was agreed that only three hundred roubles’ worth should be sent.", "결국 삼백 루블어치만 보내기로 합의가 되었다."]], [["“Well, you may go to the devil!”", "\"에이, 빌어먹을, 당신 마음대로 하시오!\""], ["cried Pyotr Ilyitch, on second thoughts.", "표트르 일리치가 다시 생각해 보고 외쳤다."], ["“What’s it to do with me?", "\"내가 알 바요?"], ["Throw away your money, since it’s cost you nothing.”", "당신 돈을 내버리든 말든, 어차피 거저 생긴 거니까.\""]], [["“This way, my economist, this way, don’t be angry.”", "\"이리 오시오, 우리 절약가 양반, 이리, 화내지 마시오.\""], ["Mitya drew him into a room at the back of the shop.", "미탸가 그를 가게 뒤편 방으로 끌어들였다."], ["“They’ll give us a bottle here directly.", "\"여기서 곧 한 병 내줄 거요."], ["We’ll taste it.", "우리 맛 좀 봅시다."], ["Ech, Pyotr Ilyitch, come along with me, for you’re a nice fellow, the sort I like.”", "에헤, 표트르 일리치, 나하고 같이 갑시다, 당신은 좋은 사람이거든, 내가 좋아하는 그런 부류요.\""]], [["Mitya sat down on a wicker chair, before a little table, covered with a dirty dinner-napkin.", "미탸는 더러운 식탁보가 덮인 작은 탁자 앞 등나무 의자에 앉았다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch sat down opposite, and the champagne soon appeared, and oysters were suggested to the gentlemen.", "표트르 일리치는 맞은편에 앉았고, 이내 샴페인이 나왔으며, 신사분들께 굴이 어떠시냐는 권유가 따랐다."], ["“First-class oysters, the last lot in.”", "\"최상품 굴입니다, 마지막으로 들어온 거예요.\""]], [["“Hang the oysters.", "\"굴은 집어치워."], ["I don’t eat them.", "나는 그런 거 안 먹소."], ["And we don’t need anything,”", "그리고 우린 아무것도 필요 없소.\""], ["cried Pyotr Ilyitch, almost angrily.", "표트르 일리치가 거의 화를 내며 외쳤다."]], [["“There’s no time for oysters,”", "\"굴 따위에 쓸 시간 없소.\""], ["said Mitya.", "미탸가 말했다."], ["“And I’m not hungry.", "\"게다가 나는 배고프지 않소."], ["Do you know, friend,”", "이보게, 친구,\""], ["he said suddenly, with feeling, “I never have liked all this disorder.”", "그가 별안간 감정을 실어 말했다. \"나는 이 모든 무질서가 한 번도 마음에 든 적이 없소.\""]], [["“Who does like it?", "\"누군들 그게 마음에 들겠소?"], ["Three dozen of champagne for peasants, upon my word, that’s enough to make any one angry!”", "농부들한테 샴페인 세 다스라니, 정말이지 누구라도 화가 날 노릇이오!\""]], [["“That’s not what I mean.", "\"그런 뜻이 아니오."], ["I’m talking of a higher order.", "나는 더 높은 질서를 말하는 거요."], ["There’s no order in me, no higher order. But ...", "내 안엔 질서가 없소, 더 높은 질서가 없지."], ["that’s all over.", "하지만……"], ["There’s no need to grieve about it.", "그것도 다 끝났소. 슬퍼할 것 없지."], ["It’s too late, damn it!", "너무 늦었소, 빌어먹을!"], ["My whole life has been disorder, and one must set it in order.", "내 평생이 무질서였으니, 이제 그걸 질서 잡아야지."], ["Is that a pun, eh?”", "이게 말장난이오, 응?\""]], [["“You’re raving, not making puns!”", "\"말장난이 아니라 헛소리를 지껄이는 거요!\""]], [["“Glory be to God in Heaven, Glory be to God in me....", "\"하늘에 계신 하느님께 영광을, 내 안에 계신 하느님께 영광을……"]], [["“That verse came from my heart once, it’s not a verse, but a tear....", "이 시구는 언젠가 내 가슴에서 우러나온 거요, 시가 아니라 눈물이지……"], ["I made it myself ...", "내가 직접 지었소……"], ["not while I was pulling the captain’s beard, though....”", "대위의 수염을 잡아 뜯던 때는 아니었지만……\""]], [["“Why do you bring him in all of a sudden?”", "\"왜 그자를 이렇게 느닷없이 끌어들이는 거요?\""]], [["“Why do I bring him in? Foolery!", "\"내가 왜 그자를 끌어들이냐고?"], ["All things come to an end; all things are made equal.", "부질없는 짓이지! 만물은 끝이 있고, 만물은 똑같아지는 법이오."], ["That’s the long and short of it.”", "그게 다요, 한마디로.\""]], [["“You know, I keep thinking of your pistols.”", "\"이보시오, 나는 자꾸 당신 권총이 마음에 걸리오.\""]], [["“That’s all foolery, too!", "\"그것도 다 부질없는 짓이오!"], ["Drink, and don’t be fanciful.", "마시기나 하시오, 공상에 빠지지 말고."], ["I love life.", "나는 삶을 사랑하오."], ["I’ve loved life too much, shamefully much. Enough!", "나는 삶을 너무 사랑했지, 부끄러우리만치 많이."], ["Let’s drink to life, dear boy, I propose the toast.", "그만하면 됐소! 삶을 위해 마십시다, 친구, 내가 건배를 제의하리다."], ["Why am I pleased with myself?", "내가 왜 나 자신이 흡족하냐고?"], ["I’m a scoundrel, but I’m satisfied with myself.", "나는 비열한 놈이지만, 나 자신에게 만족하오."], ["And yet I’m tortured by the thought that I’m a scoundrel, but satisfied with myself.", "그러면서도 내가 비열한 놈이라는 생각에, 그런데도 나 자신에게 만족한다는 생각에 괴로워하지."], ["I bless the creation.", "나는 이 창조를 축복하오."], ["I’m ready to bless God and His creation directly, but ...", "나는 당장이라도 하느님과 그분의 창조를 축복할 준비가 되어 있소, 하지만……"], ["I must kill one noxious insect for fear it should crawl and spoil life for others....", "나는 해로운 벌레 한 마리를 죽여야만 하오, 그게 기어다니며 남들의 삶을 망칠까 봐서……"], ["Let us drink to life, dear brother.", "삶을 위해 마십시다, 친애하는 형제여."], ["What can be more precious than life?", "삶보다 더 귀한 것이 어디 있겠소?"], ["Nothing!", "아무것도 없지!"], ["To life, and to one queen of queens!”", "삶을 위해, 그리고 여왕 중의 여왕을 위해!\""]], [["“Let’s drink to life and to your queen, too, if you like.”", "\"삶을 위해, 그리고 당신이 원한다면 당신의 여왕을 위해서도 마십시다.\""]], [["They drank a glass each.", "두 사람은 각자 한 잔씩 마셨다."], ["Although Mitya was excited and expansive, yet he was melancholy, too.", "미탸는 흥분하여 들떠 있었으나, 한편으로 우울하기도 했다."], ["It was as though some heavy, overwhelming anxiety were weighing upon him.", "마치 무슨 무겁고 짓누르는 듯한 불안이 그를 짓누르고 있는 듯했다."]], [["“Misha ...", "\"미샤……"], ["here’s your Misha come!", "너희 미샤가 왔구나!"], ["Misha, come here, my boy, drink this glass to Phœbus, the golden-haired, of to-morrow morn....”", "미샤, 이리 오너라, 얘야, 내일 아침의 금발 포이보스를 위해 이 잔을 마셔라……\""]], [["“What are you giving it him for?”", "\"왜 그 애한테 그걸 주는 거요?\""], ["cried Pyotr Ilyitch, irritably.", "표트르 일리치가 짜증스레 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, yes, yes, let me!", "\"괜찮아요, 괜찮아, 괜찮으니 그냥 두시오!"], ["I want to!”", "내가 그러고 싶소!\""]], [["“E—ech!”", "\"에—이!\""]], [["Misha emptied the glass, bowed, and ran out.", "미샤는 잔을 비우고 절을 하더니 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“He’ll remember it afterwards,”", "\"이 애는 나중에 이걸 기억할 거요.\""], ["Mitya remarked.", "미탸가 말했다."], ["“Woman, I love woman!", "\"여자, 나는 여자를 사랑하오!"], ["What is woman?", "여자란 무엇이오?"], ["The queen of creation!", "창조의 여왕이지!"], ["My heart is sad, my heart is sad, Pyotr Ilyitch.", "내 마음은 슬프오, 내 마음은 슬프오, 표트르 일리치."], ["Do you remember Hamlet?", "햄릿을 기억하시오?"], ["‘I am very sorry, good Horatio!", "'나는 참으로 가슴이 아프다, 착한 호레이쇼여!"], ["Alas, poor Yorick!’ Perhaps that’s me, Yorick?", "아아, 가엾은 요릭!' 어쩌면 그게 나, 요릭이 아닐까?"], ["Yes, I’m Yorick now, and a skull afterwards.”", "그래, 나는 지금은 요릭이고, 나중엔 해골이 되겠지.\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch listened in silence.", "표트르 일리치는 잠자코 들었다."], ["Mitya, too, was silent for a while.", "미탸도 한동안 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“What dog’s that you’ve got here?”", "\"여기 있는 저 개는 무슨 개요?\""], ["he asked the shopman, casually, noticing a pretty little lap-dog with dark eyes, sitting in the corner.", "그가 구석에 앉아 있는, 눈이 까만 예쁘장한 작은 발바리를 알아채고 점원에게 무심히 물었다."]], [["“It belongs to Varvara Alexyevna, the mistress,”", "\"바르바라 알렉세예브나 마님 거예요.\""], ["answered the clerk.", "점원이 대답했다."], ["“She brought it and forgot it here.", "\"그분이 데려왔다가 여기 두고 가셨어요."], ["It must be taken back to her.”", "마님께 도로 갖다 드려야지요.\""]], [["“I saw one like it ...", "\"군대에서……"], ["in the regiment ...”", "저런 걸 한 마리 봤지……\""], ["murmured Mitya dreamily, “only that one had its hind leg broken....", "미탸가 꿈꾸듯 중얼거렸다. \"그놈은 뒷다리가 부러져 있었지만……"], ["By the way, Pyotr Ilyitch, I wanted to ask you: have you ever stolen anything in your life?”", "그건 그렇고, 표트르 일리치, 당신한테 물어보고 싶은 게 있소. 당신은 평생 무언가를 훔쳐 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["“What a question!”", "\"별 질문을 다 하는군!\""]], [["“Oh, I didn’t mean anything.", "\"아, 뭐 별 뜻은 없소."], ["From somebody’s pocket, you know.", "누군가의 주머니에서 말이오."], ["I don’t mean government money, every one steals that, and no doubt you do, too....”", "관청 돈을 말하는 게 아니오, 그건 다들 훔치는 거고, 당신도 분명 그러겠지……\""]], [["“You go to the devil.”", "\"꺼지시오.\""]], [["“I’m talking of other people’s money.", "\"나는 남의 돈을 말하는 거요."], ["Stealing straight out of a pocket?", "주머니에서 곧장 훔치는 것 말이오."], ["Out of a purse, eh?”", "지갑에서, 응?\""]], [["“I stole twenty copecks from my mother when I was nine years old.", "\"아홉 살 때 어머니한테서 이십 코페이카를 훔친 적이 있소."], ["I took it off the table on the sly, and held it tight in my hand.”", "몰래 탁자에서 집어 손에 꽉 쥐었지.\""]], [["“Well, and what happened?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 됐소?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing.", "\"아, 아무것도."], ["I kept it three days, then I felt ashamed, confessed, and gave it back.”", "사흘 동안 가지고 있다가, 부끄러워져서 고백하고 돌려줬소.\""]], [["“And what then?”", "\"그래서 그다음엔?\""]], [["“Naturally I was whipped.", "\"당연히 매를 맞았지."], ["But why do you ask?", "그런데 왜 묻는 거요?"], ["Have you stolen something?”", "당신이 뭘 훔쳤소?\""]], [["“I have,”", "\"훔쳤지.\""], ["said Mitya, winking slyly.", "미탸가 능청스레 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."]], [["“What have you stolen?”", "\"뭘 훔쳤소?\""], ["inquired Pyotr Ilyitch curiously.", "표트르 일리치가 궁금해서 물었다."]], [["“I stole twenty copecks from my mother when I was nine years old, and gave it back three days after.”", "\"아홉 살 때 어머니한테서 이십 코페이카를 훔쳤다가, 사흘 뒤에 돌려줬지.\""]], [["As he said this, Mitya suddenly got up.", "이렇게 말하며 미탸는 별안간 일어섰다."]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, won’t you come now?”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 이제 안 오십니까요?\""], ["called Andrey from the door of the shop.", "안드레이가 가게 문간에서 불렀다."]], [["“Are you ready?", "\"준비됐나?"], ["We’ll come!”", "가자!\""], ["Mitya started.", "미탸가 흠칫했다."], ["“A few more last words and—Andrey, a glass of vodka at starting.", "\"마지막으로 몇 마디만 더 하고—안드레이, 출발하기 전에 보드카 한 잔."], ["Give him some brandy as well!", "브랜디도 좀 줘라!"], ["That box”", "저 상자는\""], ["(the one with the pistols) “put under my seat.", "(권총이 든 상자였다) \"내 자리 밑에 넣어 둬."], ["Good-by, Pyotr Ilyitch, don’t remember evil against me.”", "잘 있으시오, 표트르 일리치, 나를 나쁘게 기억하지 마시오.\""]], [["“But you’re coming back to-morrow?”", "\"하지만 내일은 돌아오는 거요?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론이오.\""]], [["“Will you settle the little bill now?”", "\"이 자그마한 계산은 지금 치러 주시겠습니까?\""], ["cried the clerk, springing forward.", "점원이 앞으로 튀어나오며 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, yes, the bill.", "\"아, 그래, 계산."], ["Of course.”", "물론이지.\""]], [["He pulled the bundle of notes out of his pocket again, picked out three hundred roubles, threw them on the counter, and ran hurriedly out of the shop.", "그는 다시 주머니에서 지폐 뭉치를 꺼내 삼백 루블을 골라내 계산대 위에 던지고는 허둥지둥 가게를 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Every one followed him out, bowing and wishing him good luck.", "모두가 머리를 조아리고 행운을 빌며 그를 뒤따라 나왔다."], ["Andrey, coughing from the brandy he had just swallowed, jumped up on the box.", "안드레이는 방금 들이켠 브랜디에 기침을 하며 마부석에 뛰어 올라탔다."], ["But Mitya was only just taking his seat when suddenly to his surprise he saw Fenya before him.", "그러나 미탸가 막 자리에 앉으려는 순간, 뜻밖에도 그는 자기 앞에 페냐가 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She ran up panting, clasped her hands before him with a cry, and plumped down at his feet.", "그녀는 숨을 헐떡이며 달려와, 그의 앞에서 두 손을 맞잡고 부르짖더니, 그의 발치에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear good Dmitri Fyodorovitch, don’t harm my mistress.", "\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 친절하신 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 제발 우리 아씨를 해치지 마세요."], ["And it was I told you all about it....", "그리고 그걸 다 일러바친 건 저예요……"], ["And don’t murder him, he came first, he’s hers!", "그리고 그분을 죽이지 마세요, 그분이 먼저였고, 아씨는 그분 거예요!"], ["He’ll marry Agrafena Alexandrovna now.", "그분은 이제 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 결혼할 거예요."], ["That’s why he’s come back from Siberia.", "그래서 시베리아에서 돌아온 거라고요."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear, don’t take a fellow creature’s life!”", "드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 제발, 사람의 목숨을 빼앗지 마세요!\""]], [["“Tut—tut—tut!", "\"쯧—쯧—쯧!"], ["That’s it, is it?", "그렇게 된 거로군, 그래?"], ["So you’re off there to make trouble!”", "그러니까 거기로 사달을 내러 가는 거였군!\""], ["muttered Pyotr Ilyitch.", "표트르 일리치가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Now, it’s all clear, as clear as daylight.", "\"이제 다 분명해졌소, 대낮처럼 분명하군."], ["Dmitri Fyodorovitch, give me your pistols at once if you mean to behave like a man,”", "드미트리 표도로비치, 사내답게 처신할 생각이라면 당장 그 권총을 내게 주시오.\""], ["he shouted aloud to Mitya.", "그가 미탸에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Do you hear, Dmitri?”", "\"들리오, 드미트리?\""]], [["“The pistols?", "\"권총?"], ["Wait a bit, brother, I’ll throw them into the pool on the road,”", "잠깐만, 친구, 가는 길에 웅덩이에 던져 버릴 거요.\""], ["answered Mitya.", "미탸가 대답했다."], ["“Fenya, get up, don’t kneel to me.", "\"페냐, 일어나라, 내게 무릎 꿇지 마라."], ["Mitya won’t hurt any one, the silly fool won’t hurt any one again.", "미탸는 아무도 해치지 않아, 이 어리석은 바보는 다시는 아무도 해치지 않을 거다."], ["But I say, Fenya,”", "하지만 이봐, 페냐,\""], ["he shouted, after having taken his seat.", "그가 자리에 앉고 나서 외쳤다."], ["“I hurt you just now, so forgive me and have pity on me, forgive a scoundrel....", "\"내가 방금 너를 아프게 했으니, 나를 용서하고 가엾이 여겨 다오, 비열한 놈을 용서해 다오……"], ["But it doesn’t matter if you don’t.", "하지만 용서하지 않아도 괜찮아."], ["It’s all the same now.", "이제 다 마찬가지니까."], ["Now then, Andrey, look alive, fly along full speed!”", "자 그럼, 안드레이, 정신 차리고, 전속력으로 날려라!\""]], [["Andrey whipped up the horses, and the bells began ringing.", "안드레이가 말을 채찍질하자, 방울이 울리기 시작했다."]], [["“Good-by, Pyotr Ilyitch!", "\"잘 있으시오, 표트르 일리치!"], ["My last tear is for you!...”", "내 마지막 눈물은 당신을 위한 거요!……\""]], [["“He’s not drunk, but he keeps babbling like a lunatic,”", "\"취하진 않았는데, 미친놈처럼 자꾸 횡설수설하는군.\""], ["Pyotr Ilyitch thought as he watched him go.", "표트르 일리치는 그가 떠나는 것을 지켜보며 생각했다."], ["He had half a mind to stay and see the cart packed with the remaining wines and provisions, knowing that they would deceive and defraud Mitya.", "그는 남아서 남은 포도주며 식료품을 짐마차에 꾸리는 것을 지켜볼까 하는 생각이 반쯤 들었으니, 그들이 미탸를 속여 등쳐 먹으리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But, suddenly feeling vexed with himself, he turned away with a curse and went to the tavern to play billiards.", "그러나 별안간 자기 자신에게 짜증이 나서, 그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 돌아서서 당구를 치러 술집으로 갔다."]], [["“He’s a fool, though he’s a good fellow,”", "\"좋은 녀석이긴 하지만 바보야.\""], ["he muttered as he went.", "그는 걸어가며 중얼거렸다."], ["“I’ve heard of that officer, Grushenka’s former flame.", "\"그 장교, 그루셴카의 옛 정인 얘기는 들은 적이 있지."], ["Well, if he has turned up....", "그래, 그자가 정말로 나타났다면……"], ["Ech, those pistols!", "에이, 그 권총!"], ["Damn it all!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I’m not his nurse!", "내가 그자의 보모도 아니고!"], ["Let them do what they like!", "멋대로 하라지!"], ["Besides, it’ll all come to nothing.", "게다가 다 아무 일 없이 끝날 거야."], ["They’re a set of brawlers, that’s all.", "그자들은 그저 한 무리의 싸움꾼들일 뿐이지."], ["They’ll drink and fight, fight and make friends again.", "마시고 싸우고, 싸우고 또 화해하겠지."], ["They are not men who do anything real.", "진짜로 무슨 일을 저지를 위인들이 아니야."], ["What does he mean by ‘I’m stepping aside, I’m punishing myself?’ It’ll come to nothing!", "'나는 비켜서겠소, 나 자신을 벌하겠소'라니 그게 무슨 소리야? 다 아무 일 없이 끝날 거라고!"], ["He’s shouted such phrases a thousand times, drunk, in the taverns.", "그런 문구야 술집에서 취해서 천 번도 더 외쳐 댄 거지."], ["But now he’s not drunk.", "하지만 지금은 취하지도 않았어."], ["‘Drunk in spirit’—they’re fond of fine phrases, the villains.", "'마음이 취했다'—그자들은 그럴싸한 문구를 좋아하지, 그 악당들은."], ["Am I his nurse?", "내가 그자의 보모야?"], ["He must have been fighting, his face was all over blood.", "분명 싸움질을 한 거야, 얼굴이 온통 피투성이였으니."], ["With whom?", "누구하고?"], ["I shall find out at the ‘Metropolis.’ And his handkerchief was soaked in blood....", "'메트로폴리스'에서 알아봐야지. 그리고 그자의 손수건은 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었어……"], ["It’s still lying on my floor....", "아직도 내 집 바닥에 떨어져 있겠군……"], ["Hang it!”", "에이, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["He reached the tavern in a bad humor and at once made up a game.", "그는 언짢은 기분으로 술집에 다다라 곧 한판 어울렸다."], ["The game cheered him.", "게임은 그의 기분을 풀어 주었다."], ["He played a second game, and suddenly began telling one of his partners that Dmitri Karamazov had come in for some cash again—something like three thousand roubles, and had gone to Mokroe again to spend it with Grushenka....", "그는 두 번째 판을 치다가, 별안간 같이 치는 사람 가운데 하나에게 드미트리 카라마조프가 또 큰돈이—삼천 루블쯤 되는 돈이 생겨서, 그루셴카와 함께 그것을 쓰러 또 모크로예로 갔다고 이야기하기 시작했다……"], ["This news roused singular interest in his listeners.", "이 소식은 듣는 이들에게 유난한 흥미를 불러일으켰다."], ["They all spoke of it, not laughing, but with a strange gravity.", "그들은 모두 그 이야기를 했는데, 웃지도 않고 야릇하게 진지했다."], ["They left off playing.", "그들은 게임을 멈추었다."]], [["“Three thousand?", "\"삼천이라고?"], ["But where can he have got three thousand?”", "한데 그자가 어디서 삼천을 구했단 말이야?\""]], [["Questions were asked.", "질문이 쏟아졌다."], ["The story of Madame Hohlakov’s present was received with skepticism.", "호흘라코바 부인이 선물했다는 이야기는 미심쩍게 받아들여졌다."]], [["“Hasn’t he robbed his old father?—that’s the question.”", "\"제 늙은 아비를 털어먹은 거 아냐?—그게 문제지.\""]], [["“Three thousand!", "\"삼천이라!"], ["There’s something odd about it.”", "뭔가 수상쩍은데.\""]], [["“He boasted aloud that he would kill his father; we all heard him, here.", "\"그자는 제 아비를 죽이겠다고 큰소리쳤지. 우리 모두 들었잖아, 여기서."], ["And it was three thousand he talked about ...”", "그리고 그자가 말한 게 삼천이었어……\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch listened.", "표트르 일리치는 듣고 있었다."], ["All at once he became short and dry in his answers.", "그는 별안간 대답이 짤막하고 무뚝뚝해졌다."], ["He said not a word about the blood on Mitya’s face and hands, though he had meant to speak of it at first.", "그는 미탸의 얼굴과 손에 묻은 피에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 처음엔 그 이야기를 할 작정이었지만 말이다."]], [["They began a third game, and by degrees the talk about Mitya died away.", "그들은 세 번째 판을 시작했고, 미탸에 관한 이야기는 차츰 잦아들었다."], ["But by the end of the third game, Pyotr Ilyitch felt no more desire for billiards; he laid down the cue, and without having supper as he had intended, he walked out of the tavern.", "그러나 세 번째 판이 끝날 무렵, 표트르 일리치는 더는 당구 칠 마음이 없었다. 그는 큐를 내려놓고, 작정했던 저녁 식사도 하지 않은 채 술집을 걸어 나왔다."], ["When he reached the market-place he stood still in perplexity, wondering at himself.", "시장에 다다랐을 때 그는 어쩔 줄 몰라 멈춰 서서, 스스로 의아해했다."], ["He realized that what he wanted was to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s and find out if anything had happened there.", "그는 자기가 원하는 것이 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 가서 거기서 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알아보는 것임을 깨달았다."], ["“On account of some stupid nonsense—as it’s sure to turn out—am I going to wake up the household and make a scandal?", "\"어차피 뻔히 그렇게 드러날 무슨 어리석은 헛소리 때문에—내가 온 집안을 깨워서 소동을 벌인단 말인가?"], ["Fooh!", "흥!"], ["damn it, is it my business to look after them?”", "빌어먹을, 그자들을 돌보는 게 내 일이야?\""]], [["In a very bad humor he went straight home, and suddenly remembered Fenya.", "몹시 언짢은 기분으로 그는 곧장 집으로 향하다가, 별안간 페냐를 떠올렸다."], ["“Damn it all!", "\"에잇, 빌어먹을!"], ["I ought to have questioned her just now,”", "아까 그 여자한테 캐물었어야 했는데.\""], ["he thought with vexation, “I should have heard everything.”", "그는 분이 나서 생각했다. \"그랬으면 다 들었을 텐데.\""], ["And the desire to speak to her, and so find out, became so pressing and importunate that when he was half-way home he turned abruptly and went towards the house where Grushenka lodged.", "그리고 그녀에게 말을 걸어 알아보고 싶은 욕구가 어찌나 절박하고 끈질기게 밀려오는지, 집으로 가는 길 절반쯤에서 그는 홱 돌아서서 그루셴카가 묵고 있는 집 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Going up to the gate he knocked.", "대문 앞으로 다가가 그는 문을 두드렸다."], ["The sound of the knock in the silence of the night sobered him and made him feel annoyed.", "밤의 정적 속에 울리는 그 노크 소리에 그는 정신이 들면서 짜증이 났다."], ["And no one answered him; every one in the house was asleep.", "그리고 아무도 대답하지 않았으니, 집안 사람들이 모두 잠들어 있었다."]], [["“And I shall be making a fuss!”", "\"내가 또 야단법석을 떠는구나!\""], ["he thought, with a feeling of positive discomfort.", "그는 영락없이 거북한 기분으로 생각했다."], ["But instead of going away altogether, he fell to knocking again with all his might, filling the street with clamor.", "그러나 아주 가 버리는 대신, 그는 다시 온 힘을 다해 문을 두드리기 시작해, 거리를 떠들썩하게 메웠다."]], [["“Not coming?", "\"안 나와?"], ["Well, I will knock them up, I will!”", "좋아, 내가 깨워 주지, 깨워 주고말고!\""], ["he muttered at each knock, fuming at himself, but at the same time he redoubled his knocks on the gate.", "그는 두드릴 때마다 자기 자신에게 화를 내며 중얼거렸으나, 그러면서도 대문을 두드리는 손길을 더욱 거세게 했다."]], [["# Chapter VI. “I Am Coming, Too!”", "# Chapter VI. “I Am Coming, Too!”"]], [["But Dmitri Fyodorovitch was speeding along the road.", "그러나 드미트리 표도로비치는 길 위를 쏜살같이 달리고 있었다."], ["It was a little more than twenty versts to Mokroe, but Andrey’s three horses galloped at such a pace that the distance might be covered in an hour and a quarter.", "모크로예까지는 이십 베르스타 남짓이었으나, 안드레이의 세 마리 말은 워낙 빠른 속도로 내달려 그 거리는 한 시간 십오 분이면 닿을 수 있었다."], ["The swift motion revived Mitya.", "빠른 움직임이 미탸를 되살아나게 했다."], ["The air was fresh and cool, there were big stars shining in the sky.", "공기는 신선하고 서늘했으며, 하늘에는 커다란 별들이 빛나고 있었다."], ["It was the very night, and perhaps the very hour, in which Alyosha fell on the earth, and rapturously swore to love it for ever and ever.", "바로 그날 밤, 어쩌면 바로 그 시각이었으니, 알료샤가 땅 위로 쓰러져 황홀경 속에서 영원토록 이 땅을 사랑하리라 맹세하던 그때였다."]], [["All was confusion, confusion, in Mitya’s soul, but although many things were goading his heart, at that moment his whole being was yearning for her, his queen, to whom he was flying to look on her for the last time.", "* 베르스타(верста): 제정 러시아의 거리 단위로 약 1.067킬로미터. 미탸의 영혼은 온통 혼란, 또 혼란이었으나, 비록 많은 것들이 그의 가슴을 찌르고 있었어도, 그 순간 그의 온 존재는 그녀를, 자신의 여왕을 향해 사무치게 갈망하고 있었으니, 마지막으로 한 번 그녀를 보려고 그곳으로 날아가고 있는 것이었다."], ["One thing I can say for certain; his heart did not waver for one instant.", "한 가지만은 단언할 수 있다. 그의 가슴은 한순간도 흔들리지 않았다."], ["I shall perhaps not be believed when I say that this jealous lover felt not the slightest jealousy of this new rival, who seemed to have sprung out of the earth.", "어쩌면 사람들은 믿지 않을지도 모르나, 이 질투 많은 연인은 땅에서 솟아난 듯한 이 새로운 연적에게 추호의 질투도 느끼지 않았던 것이다."], ["If any other had appeared on the scene, he would have been jealous at once, and would perhaps have stained his fierce hands with blood again.", "만약 다른 누군가가 무대 위에 나타났더라면 그는 당장 질투에 사로잡혔을 것이고, 어쩌면 그 사나운 두 손을 또다시 피로 물들였을지도 모른다."], ["But as he flew through the night, he felt no envy, no hostility even, for the man who had been her first lover....", "그러나 밤을 가르며 날아가는 동안, 그는 그녀의 첫사랑이었던 그 사내에게 선망도, 적의조차도 느끼지 않았다……"], ["It is true he had not yet seen him.", "하기야 그는 아직 그자를 본 적이 없기는 했다."]], [["“Here there was no room for dispute: it was her right and his; this was her first love which, after five years, she had not forgotten; so she had loved him only for those five years, and I, how do I come in?", "\"여기에는 다툴 여지가 없다. 그것은 그녀의 권리요, 그의 권리다."], ["What right have I?", "이것은 그녀의 첫사랑이고, 오 년이 지나도록 그녀가 잊지 못한 사랑이다."], ["Step aside, Mitya, and make way!", "그러니 그녀는 그 오 년 동안 오직 그자만을 사랑한 것이고, 나는, 나는 대체 무슨 자격으로 끼어든단 말인가? 무슨 권리가 있단 말인가?"], ["What am I now?", "비켜라, 미탸, 길을 내주어라! 이제 나는 무엇인가?"], ["Now everything is over apart from the officer—even if he had not appeared, everything would be over ...”", "이제 저 장교만 제외하면 모든 것이 끝났다. 설령 그자가 나타나지 않았더라도 모든 것은 끝났을 것이다……\""]], [["These words would roughly have expressed his feelings, if he had been capable of reasoning.", "이 말들은 만약 그가 이치를 따질 줄 아는 상태였더라면 그의 심정을 대강 표현했을 것이다."], ["But he could not reason at that moment.", "그러나 그는 그 순간 이치를 따질 수 없었다."], ["His present plan of action had arisen without reasoning.", "지금 그가 하려는 행동의 계획은 따짐 없이 솟아난 것이었다."], ["At Fenya’s first words, it had sprung from feeling, and been adopted in a flash, with all its consequences.", "페냐의 첫마디에서 그것은 감정으로부터 솟구쳐 나와, 그 모든 결과와 함께 순식간에 채택되었다."], ["And yet, in spite of his resolution, there was confusion in his soul, an agonizing confusion: his resolution did not give him peace.", "그러나 그 결심에도 불구하고 그의 영혼 안에는 혼란이, 괴로움에 찬 혼란이 있었으니, 그 결심은 그에게 평안을 주지 못했다."], ["There was so much behind that tortured him.", "그를 괴롭히는 것이 그 뒤에 너무도 많이 도사리고 있었다."], ["And it seemed strange to him, at moments, to think that he had written his own sentence of death with pen and paper: “I punish myself,”", "그리고 펜과 종이로 자신의 사형 선고문을 직접 써 두었다는 생각이 이따금 그에게 기이하게 다가왔다."], ["and the paper was lying there in his pocket, ready; the pistol was loaded; he had already resolved how, next morning, he would meet the first warm ray of “golden-haired Phœbus.”", "\"나는 나 자신을 벌하노라\"라고 쓴 그 종이는 그의 주머니 속에 준비된 채 들어 있었고, 권총은 장전되어 있었으며, 그는 이미 다음 날 아침 \"황금빛 머리칼의 포이보스\"의 첫 따스한 빛살을 어떻게 맞이할 것인지 결심해 두고 있었다."]], [["And yet he could not be quit of the past, of all that he had left behind and that tortured him.", "* 포이보스(Phœbus): 그리스 신화에서 태양신 아폴론을 일컫는 별칭."], ["He felt that miserably, and the thought of it sank into his heart with despair.", "그럼에도 그는 과거에서, 자신이 뒤에 남겨 두고 온 모든 것, 자신을 괴롭히는 모든 것에서 벗어날 수 없었다."], ["There was one moment when he felt an impulse to stop Andrey, to jump out of the cart, to pull out his loaded pistol, and to make an end of everything without waiting for the dawn.", "그는 그것을 비참하게 느꼈고, 그 생각은 절망과 함께 그의 가슴속으로 가라앉았다. 한순간 그는 안드레이를 멈춰 세우고 마차에서 뛰어내려, 장전된 권총을 뽑아 들고 새벽을 기다릴 것도 없이 모든 것을 끝장내고 싶은 충동을 느꼈다."], ["But that moment flew by like a spark.", "그러나 그 순간은 불꽃처럼 스쳐 날아가 버렸다."], ["The horses galloped on, “devouring space,”", "말들은 \"공간을 집어삼키며\""], ["and as he drew near his goal, again the thought of her, of her alone, took more and more complete possession of his soul, chasing away the fearful images that had been haunting it.", "줄곧 내달렸고, 목적지에 가까워질수록 그녀에 대한 생각이, 오직 그녀만에 대한 생각이 점점 더 완전하게 그의 영혼을 사로잡아, 그것을 떠나지 않고 맴돌던 무시무시한 형상들을 쫓아내었다."], ["Oh, how he longed to look upon her, if only for a moment, if only from a distance!", "오, 그는 단 한순간만이라도, 단지 멀리서나마 그녀를 바라보기를 얼마나 갈망했던가!"]], [["“She’s now with him,”", "\"그녀는 지금 그자와 함께 있다.\""], ["he thought, “now I shall see what she looks like with him, her first love, and that’s all I want.”", "그는 생각했다. \"이제 나는 그녀가 그자와, 그 첫사랑과 함께 있을 때 어떤 모습인지 보게 될 것이다."], ["Never had this woman, who was such a fateful influence in his life, aroused such love in his breast, such new and unknown feeling, surprising even to himself, a feeling tender to devoutness, to self-effacement before her!", "내가 바라는 건 그것뿐이다.\" 그의 생애에서 그토록 운명적인 영향을 끼친 이 여자가 그의 가슴속에 이런 사랑을, 이토록 새롭고 알 수 없는 감정을, 그 자신에게조차 놀라운, 경건에 이르도록 다정하고 그녀 앞에서 스스로를 지워 버리고 싶을 만큼 헌신적인 감정을 불러일으킨 적은 일찍이 없었다."], ["“I will efface myself!”", "\"나는 나를 지워 버리겠다!\""], ["he said, in a rush of almost hysterical ecstasy.", "그는 거의 발작에 가까운 환희에 휩싸여 말했다."]], [["They had been galloping nearly an hour.", "그들은 거의 한 시간을 내달렸다."], ["Mitya was silent, and though Andrey was, as a rule, a talkative peasant, he did not utter a word, either.", "미탸는 말이 없었고, 안드레이는 본래 수다스러운 농사꾼이었으나 그 역시 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["He seemed afraid to talk, he only whipped up smartly his three lean, but mettlesome, bay horses.", "그는 말하기를 두려워하는 듯, 다만 비쩍 마르긴 했어도 혈기 넘치는 세 마리 적갈색 말에게 야무지게 채찍질만 가했다."], ["Suddenly Mitya cried out in horrible anxiety:", "그러다 별안간 미탸가 끔찍한 불안에 사로잡혀 소리쳤다."]], [["“Andrey!", "\"안드레이!"], ["What if they’re asleep?”", "만약 그들이 잠들어 있으면 어쩌지?\""]], [["This thought fell upon him like a blow.", "이 생각이 한 대 얻어맞은 것처럼 그에게 떨어졌다."], ["It had not occurred to him before.", "그전에는 미처 떠오르지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“It may well be that they’re gone to bed, by now, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "\"지금쯤이면 잠자리에 들었을 법도 합니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리.\""]], [["Mitya frowned as though in pain.", "미탸는 고통스러운 듯 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["Yes, indeed ...", "그렇다, 정말이지……"], ["he was rushing there ... with such feelings ...", "자신은 이런 심정으로 이렇게 그곳으로 달려가고 있는데……"], ["while they were asleep ...", "그들은 잠들어 있다니……"], ["she was asleep, perhaps, there too....", "어쩌면 그녀도 그곳에서 잠들어 있을지 모른다……"], ["An angry feeling surged up in his heart.", "분노의 감정이 그의 가슴속에서 치솟았다."]], [["“Drive on, Andrey!", "\"몰아라, 안드레이!"], ["Whip them up!", "채찍질해!"], ["Look alive!”", "정신 차려!\""], ["he cried, beside himself.", "그는 제정신이 아닌 채 소리쳤다."]], [["“But maybe they’re not in bed!”", "\"하지만 어쩌면 잠자리에 안 들었을지도 모르지요!\""], ["Andrey went on after a pause.", "안드레이가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말을 이었다."], ["“Timofey said they were a lot of them there—”", "\"티모페이 말로는 거기 사람들이 한 무리 와 있다고 하던뎁쇼—\""]], [["“At the station?”", "\"역참에?\""]], [["“Not at the posting-station, but at Plastunov’s, at the inn, where they let out horses, too.”", "\"역참이 아니라, 플라스투노프네 여인숙에 말입니다요, 거기서 말도 빌려주는뎁쇼.\""]], [["“I know.", "\"안다."], ["So you say there are a lot of them?", "그러니까 사람들이 많다는 거냐?"], ["How’s that?", "어찌 된 일이야?"], ["Who are they?”", "누구누구냐?\""], ["cried Mitya, greatly dismayed at this unexpected news.", "미탸가 이 뜻밖의 소식에 몹시 당황하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, Timofey was saying they’re all gentlefolk.", "\"글쎄요, 티모페이 말로는 죄다 신사 양반들이라던뎁쇼."], ["Two from our town—who they are I can’t say—and there are two others, strangers, maybe more besides.", "우리 고장 사람 둘은—누군지는 저도 모르겠습니다요—그리고 다른 둘은 외지 사람이고, 어쩌면 더 있을지도 모르지요."], ["I didn’t ask particularly.", "자세히는 안 물어봤습니다요."], ["They’ve set to playing cards, so Timofey said.”", "카드놀이를 시작했다고 티모페이가 그러더군요.\""]], [["“Cards?”", "\"카드놀이?\""]], [["“So, maybe they’re not in bed if they’re at cards.", "\"그러니 카드놀이를 하고 있다면야 잠자리에 안 들었을 겁니다요."], ["It’s most likely not more than eleven.”", "아직 열한 시도 안 됐을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Quicker, Andrey!", "\"빨리, 안드레이!"], ["Quicker!”", "더 빨리!\""], ["Mitya cried again, nervously.", "미탸가 또다시 신경질적으로 외쳤다."]], [["“May I ask you something, sir?”", "\"한 가지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까요, 나리?\""], ["said Andrey, after a pause.", "안드레이가 잠시 뒤에 말했다."], ["“Only I’m afraid of angering you, sir.”", "\"다만 나리를 노엽게 할까 봐 겁이 납니다만요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇이냐?\""]], [["“Why, Fenya threw herself at your feet just now, and begged you not to harm her mistress, and some one else, too ...", "\"아니, 페냐가 아까 나리 발치에 몸을 던지며 자기 여주인을 해치지 말아 달라고, 또 다른 누군가도 해치지 말아 달라고 빌지 않았습니까요……"], ["so you see, sir— It’s I am taking you there ...", "그러니 보십쇼, 나리— 그곳으로 나리를 모시고 가는 건 다름 아닌 접니다요……"], ["forgive me, sir, it’s my conscience ...", "용서하십쇼, 나리, 양심에 걸려서요……"], ["maybe it’s stupid of me to speak of it—”", "이런 말씀을 드리는 게 어리석은 짓일지도 모릅니다만—\""]], [["Mitya suddenly seized him by the shoulders from behind.", "미탸가 별안간 뒤에서 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Are you a driver?”", "\"네가 마부냐?\""], ["he asked frantically.", "그가 미친 듯이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["“Then you know that one has to make way.", "\"그렇다면 길을 비켜 줘야 한다는 걸 알 테지."], ["What would you say to a driver who wouldn’t make way for any one, but would just drive on and crush people?", "누구에게도 길을 비켜 주지 않고 그저 마차를 몰아 사람을 깔아뭉개는 마부를 두고 뭐라 하겠느냐?"], ["No, a driver mustn’t run over people.", "안 되지, 마부가 사람을 치고 지나가서는 안 되는 법이다."], ["One can’t run over a man.", "사람을 깔아뭉개서는 안 돼."], ["One can’t spoil people’s lives.", "사람의 인생을 망쳐서는 안 된다."], ["And if you have spoilt a life—punish yourself....", "그리고 만약 네가 한 인생을 망쳤다면—네 자신을 벌하거라……"], ["If only you’ve spoilt, if only you’ve ruined any one’s life—punish yourself and go away.”", "만약 네가 누군가의 인생을 망쳤다면, 파멸시켰다면—네 자신을 벌하고 떠나가거라.\""]], [["These phrases burst from Mitya almost hysterically.", "이 말들이 거의 발작하듯 미탸에게서 터져 나왔다."], ["Though Andrey was surprised at him, he kept up the conversation.", "안드레이는 그에게 놀랐으면서도 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["“That’s right, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, you’re quite right, one mustn’t crush or torment a man, or any kind of creature, for every creature is created by God.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 지당하신 말씀입니다요, 사람을 깔아뭉개거나 괴롭혀서는 안 되지요, 어떤 생물이든 마찬가집니다요, 모든 생물은 하느님이 지으신 것이니까요."], ["Take a horse, for instance, for some folks, even among us drivers, drive anyhow.", "말을 예로 들어도 그렇습니다요, 어떤 사람들은, 우리 마부들 중에서도, 막무가내로 몰아댄단 말입니다요."], ["Nothing will restrain them, they just force it along.”", "무엇으로도 막을 수가 없지요, 그냥 억지로 몰고 가니까요.\""]], [["“To hell?”", "\"지옥으로 말이냐?\""], ["Mitya interrupted, and went off into his abrupt, short laugh.", "미탸가 말을 가로채더니 특유의 짧고 퉁명스러운 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Andrey, simple soul,”", "\"안드레이, 순박한 사람아.\""], ["he seized him by the shoulders again, “tell me, will Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov go to hell, or not, what do you think?”", "그는 다시 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다. \"말해 보아라, 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프는 지옥에 갈까, 안 갈까, 네 생각은 어떠냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know, darling, it depends on you, for you are ...", "\"저야 모르지요, 나리, 그건 나리께 달린 거니까요, 나리께서는……"], ["you see, sir, when the Son of God was nailed on the Cross and died, He went straight down to hell from the Cross, and set free all sinners that were in agony.", "보십쇼, 나리, 하느님의 아드님이 십자가에 못 박혀 돌아가셨을 때, 그분은 십자가에서 곧장 지옥으로 내려가시어 거기서 고통받고 있던 죄인들을 모두 풀어 주셨답니다요."], ["And the devil groaned, because he thought that he would get no more sinners in hell.", "그러자 악마가 신음했지요, 이제 지옥에는 죄인을 더 받지 못하겠다고 생각했으니까요."], ["And God said to him, then, ‘Don’t groan, for you shall have all the mighty of the earth, the rulers, the chief judges, and the rich men, and shall be filled up as you have been in all the ages till I come again.’ Those were His very words ...”", "그래서 하느님이 그자에게 말씀하셨답니다요, '신음하지 말라, 너는 땅 위의 권세 있는 자들을, 통치자들과 으뜸 재판관들과 부자들을 모조리 갖게 될 것이며, 내가 다시 올 때까지 그 모든 세월 동안 그래 왔듯이 가득 채워질 것이다.' 그것이 바로 그분이 하신 말씀이랍니다요……\""]], [["“A peasant legend!", "\"농사꾼의 전설이로구나!"], ["Capital!", "기막히다!"], ["Whip up the left, Andrey!”", "왼쪽 말을 채찍질해라, 안드레이!\""]], [["“So you see, sir, who it is hell’s for,”", "\"그러니 보십쇼, 나리, 지옥이 누구를 위한 것인지 말입니다요.\""], ["said Andrey, whipping up the left horse, “but you’re like a little child ...", "안드레이가 왼쪽 말에 채찍질하며 말했다. \"한데 나리께서는 어린아이 같으십니다요……"], ["that’s how we look on you ...", "우리는 나리를 그렇게 봅죠……"], ["and though you’re hasty-tempered, sir, yet God will forgive you for your kind heart.”", "그리고 비록 성미는 급하셔도, 나리, 하느님께서는 그 어진 마음씨를 봐서 나리를 용서하실 겁니다요.\""]], [["“And you, do you forgive me, Andrey?”", "\"그럼 너는, 너는 나를 용서하느냐, 안드레이?\""]], [["“What should I forgive you for, sir?", "\"제가 나리를 무얼 용서한단 말입니까요, 나리?"], ["You’ve never done me any harm.”", "나리께서는 제게 해를 끼치신 적이 한 번도 없는뎁쇼.\""]], [["“No, for every one, for every one, you here alone, on the road, will you forgive me for every one?", "\"아니다, 모든 사람을 위해서, 모든 사람을 위해서다, 여기 길 위에서 너 혼자라도, 모든 사람을 대신해 나를 용서해 주겠느냐?"], ["Speak, simple peasant heart!”", "말해 보아라, 순박한 농사꾼의 마음아!\""]], [["“Oh, sir!", "\"오, 나리!"], ["I feel afraid of driving you, your talk is so strange.”", "나리를 모시고 가는 게 겁이 납니다요, 나리 말씀이 어찌나 이상한지요.\""]], [["But Mitya did not hear.", "그러나 미탸는 듣고 있지 않았다."], ["He was frantically praying and muttering to himself.", "그는 미친 듯이 기도하며 혼자 중얼거리고 있었다."]], [["“Lord, receive me, with all my lawlessness, and do not condemn me.", "\"주여, 이 모든 무법한 죄와 함께 저를 거두시고, 저를 정죄하지 마옵소서."], ["Let me pass by Thy judgment ...", "당신의 심판을 그냥 지나치게 하옵소서……"], ["do not condemn me, for I have condemned myself, do not condemn me, for I love Thee, O Lord.", "저를 정죄하지 마옵소서, 제가 이미 스스로를 정죄하였나이다, 저를 정죄하지 마옵소서, 제가 당신을 사랑하나이다, 오 주여."], ["I am a wretch, but I love Thee.", "저는 비루한 자이오나 당신을 사랑하나이다."], ["If Thou sendest me to hell, I shall love Thee there, and from there I shall cry out that I love Thee for ever and ever....", "당신께서 저를 지옥으로 보내신다 해도, 저는 거기서도 당신을 사랑하겠나이다, 그리고 그곳에서 영원토록 당신을 사랑한다고 부르짖겠나이다……"], ["But let me love to the end....", "그러나 끝까지 사랑하게 하옵소서……"], ["Here and now for just five hours ...", "지금 여기서 단지 다섯 시간만……"], ["till the first light of Thy day ...", "당신의 날의 첫 빛이 비칠 때까지만……"], ["for I love the queen of my soul ...", "제가 제 영혼의 여왕을 사랑하기 때문이옵니다……"], ["I love her and I cannot help loving her.", "저는 그녀를 사랑하옵고, 사랑하지 않을 수 없나이다."], ["Thou seest my whole heart....", "당신께서는 저의 온 마음을 보고 계시나이다……"], ["I shall gallop up, I shall fall before her and say, ‘You are right to pass on and leave me.", "저는 말을 몰아 달려가, 그녀 앞에 엎드려 말하겠나이다, '당신이 그대로 떠나 저를 버리는 것이 옳습니다."], ["Farewell and forget your victim ...", "잘 가시오, 그리고 당신의 희생물을 잊으시오……"], ["never fret yourself about me!’”", "결코 나 때문에 마음 쓰지 마시오!'\""]], [["“Mokroe!”", "\"모크로예입니다!\""], ["cried Andrey, pointing ahead with his whip.", "안드레이가 채찍으로 앞쪽을 가리키며 외쳤다."]], [["Through the pale darkness of the night loomed a solid black mass of buildings, flung down, as it were, in the vast plain.", "밤의 희뿌연 어둠 사이로 건물들의 검고 단단한 덩어리가, 마치 광막한 평원 위에 내던져진 듯이 어렴풋이 떠올랐다."], ["The village of Mokroe numbered two thousand inhabitants, but at that hour all were asleep, and only here and there a few lights still twinkled.", "모크로예 마을은 주민이 이천 명이었으나, 그 시각에는 모두 잠들어 있었고, 다만 여기저기에서 몇 점의 불빛만이 깜박이고 있었다."]], [["“Drive on, Andrey, I come!”", "\"몰아라, 안드레이, 내가 왔다!\""], ["Mitya exclaimed, feverishly.", "미탸가 열에 들떠 소리쳤다."]], [["“They’re not asleep,”", "\"안 잤군요.\""], ["said Andrey again, pointing with his whip to the Plastunovs’ inn, which was at the entrance to the village.", "안드레이가 또다시 마을 어귀에 있는 플라스투노프네 여인숙을 채찍으로 가리키며 말했다."], ["The six windows, looking on the street, were all brightly lighted up.", "거리 쪽으로 난 여섯 개의 창문이 모두 환하게 불을 밝히고 있었다."]], [["“They’re not asleep,”", "\"안 잤어.\""], ["Mitya repeated joyously.", "미탸가 기쁘게 되뇌었다."], ["“Quicker, Andrey!", "\"더 빨리, 안드레이!"], ["Gallop!", "내달려라!"], ["Drive up with a dash!", "한달음에 들이닥쳐라!"], ["Set the bells ringing!", "방울을 울려라!"], ["Let all know that I have come.", "내가 왔다는 걸 모두에게 알려라."], ["I’m coming!", "내가 간다!"], ["I’m coming, too!”", "나도 간다!\""]], [["Andrey lashed his exhausted team into a gallop, drove with a dash and pulled up his steaming, panting horses at the high flight of steps.", "안드레이는 지친 말떼를 채찍질해 질주시켰고, 한달음에 내달아 김이 무럭무럭 나며 헐떡이는 말들을 높은 층계 앞에 세웠다."]], [["Mitya jumped out of the cart just as the innkeeper, on his way to bed, peeped out from the steps curious to see who had arrived.", "미탸가 마차에서 뛰어내리는 바로 그때, 여인숙 주인이 잠자리에 들려다가 누가 왔는지 궁금하여 층계에서 빼꼼히 내다보았다."]], [["“Trifon Borissovitch, is that you?”", "\"트리폰 보리소비치, 자넨가?\""]], [["The innkeeper bent down, looked intently, ran down the steps, and rushed up to the guest with obsequious delight.", "여인숙 주인은 몸을 굽혀 유심히 들여다보더니, 층계를 뛰어 내려와 굽실거리는 환희에 차서 손님에게로 달려들었다."]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, your honor!", "\"드미트리 표도로비치, 나리!"], ["Do I see you again?”", "또 뵙게 되다니요!\""]], [["Trifon Borissovitch was a thick-set, healthy peasant, of middle height, with a rather fat face.", "트리폰 보리소비치는 다부지고 건장한 농사꾼으로, 중키에 다소 살진 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["His expression was severe and uncompromising, especially with the peasants of Mokroe, but he had the power of assuming the most obsequious countenance, when he had an inkling that it was to his interest.", "그 표정은 엄하고 가차 없었는데, 특히 모크로예의 농사꾼들을 대할 때 그러했으나, 자기 잇속에 보탬이 되겠다 싶은 낌새를 채면 더없이 굽실거리는 낯빛을 꾸며낼 줄도 알았다."], ["He dressed in Russian style, with a shirt buttoning down on one side, and a full-skirted coat.", "그는 러시아식으로, 한쪽으로 단추를 채우는 셔츠에 자락이 넓은 외투를 걸치고 다녔다."], ["He had saved a good sum of money, but was for ever dreaming of improving his position.", "그는 꽤 많은 돈을 모아 두었으면서도 형편을 더 끌어올릴 궁리만 줄곧 하고 있었다."], ["More than half the peasants were in his clutches, every one in the neighborhood was in debt to him.", "농사꾼들의 절반 이상이 그의 손아귀에 잡혀 있었고, 인근의 누구나 그에게 빚을 지고 있었다."], ["From the neighboring landowners he bought and rented lands which were worked by the peasants, in payment of debts which they could never shake off.", "인근의 지주들에게서 그는 땅을 사들이거나 빌려, 그것을 농사꾼들이 부치게 하고는, 그들이 결코 벗어날 수 없는 빚 대신 그 일을 시켰다."], ["He was a widower, with four grown-up daughters.", "그는 홀아비였고 장성한 딸 넷을 두었다."], ["One of them was already a widow and lived in the inn with her two children, his grandchildren, and worked for him like a charwoman.", "그중 하나는 이미 과부가 되어 두 아이, 곧 그의 손주들과 함께 여인숙에서 살며 그를 위해 허드렛일을 하는 여자처럼 일했다."], ["Another of his daughters was married to a petty official, and in one of the rooms of the inn, on the wall could be seen, among the family photographs, a miniature photograph of this official in uniform and official epaulettes.", "또 다른 딸은 하급 관리에게 시집을 갔는데, 여인숙의 어느 방 벽에는 가족사진들 사이에, 제복에 관등 견장을 단 이 관리의 자그마한 사진 한 장이 걸려 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["The two younger daughters used to wear fashionable blue or green dresses, fitting tight at the back, and with trains a yard long, on Church holidays or when they went to pay visits.", "아래 두 딸은 교회 축일이나 누군가를 방문하러 갈 때면 등 쪽이 꼭 끼고 한 야드나 되는 긴 자락이 달린, 유행하는 파랑이나 초록 드레스를 입곤 했다."], ["But next morning they would get up at dawn, as usual, sweep out the rooms with a birch-broom, empty the slops, and clean up after lodgers.", "그러나 다음 날 아침이면 여느 때처럼 동틀 녘에 일어나 자작나무 빗자루로 방을 쓸고, 구정물을 비우고, 투숙객이 머물다 간 자리를 치웠다."]], [["In spite of the thousands of roubles he had saved, Trifon Borissovitch was very fond of emptying the pockets of a drunken guest, and remembering that not a month ago he had, in twenty-four hours, made two if not three hundred roubles out of Dmitri, when he had come on his escapade with Grushenka, he met him now with eager welcome, scenting his prey the moment Mitya drove up to the steps.", "수천 루블을 모아 두었음에도 트리폰 보리소비치는 술 취한 손님의 주머니를 털어내는 것을 무척 즐겼다. 한 달도 채 안 된 전날, 드미트리가 그루셴카와 함께 한바탕 흥청거리러 왔을 때 그에게서 스물네 시간 만에 이삼백 루블은 아니어도 이백 루블은 우려냈던 것을 떠올리며, 그는 미탸가 층계 앞으로 마차를 몰고 다가오는 순간 먹잇감 냄새를 맡고서 이제 그를 살갑게 맞이했다."]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch, dear sir, we see you once more!”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치, 나리, 또 뵙게 되다니요!\""]], [["“Stay, Trifon Borissovitch,”", "\"가만, 트리폰 보리소비치.\""], ["began Mitya, “first and foremost, where is she?”", "미탸가 입을 열었다. \"무엇보다 먼저, 그녀는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Agrafena Alexandrovna?”", "\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나 말씀입니까요?\""], ["The inn-keeper understood at once, looking sharply into Mitya’s face.", "여인숙 주인은 미탸의 얼굴을 날카롭게 들여다보며 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["“She’s here, too ...”", "\"그분도 여기 계십니다요……\""]], [["“With whom?", "\"누구와 함께?"], ["With whom?”", "누구와 함께?\""]], [["“Some strangers.", "\"외지 사람들과 함께지요."], ["One is an official gentleman, a Pole, to judge from his speech.", "하나는 관리 양반인데, 말투로 보아 폴란드 사람입니다요."], ["He sent the horses for her from here; and there’s another with him, a friend of his, or a fellow traveler, there’s no telling.", "그 사람이 여기서 그분을 모시러 말을 보냈지요. 그리고 그와 함께 또 한 사람이 있는데, 그 친구인지 길동무인지는 알 수 없습니다요."], ["They’re dressed like civilians.”", "둘 다 평복 차림입죠.\""]], [["“Well, are they feasting?", "\"그래, 그들은 잔치를 벌이고 있나?"], ["Have they money?”", "돈이 있던가?\""]], [["“Poor sort of a feast!", "\"잔치라고 할 것도 없습죠!"], ["Nothing to boast of, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "내세울 것 하나 없습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리.\""]], [["“Nothing to boast of?", "\"내세울 게 없다고?"], ["And who are the others?”", "그럼 나머지 사람들은 누구냐?\""]], [["“They’re two gentlemen from the town....", "\"고장에서 온 신사 두 분입니다요……"], ["They’ve come back from Tcherny, and are putting up here.", "체르니에서 돌아오는 길에 여기 묵고 계십죠."], ["One’s quite a young gentleman, a relative of Mr. Miüsov, he must be, but I’ve forgotten his name ...", "하나는 아주 젊은 양반인데, 미우소프 씨의 친척인 듯합니다요, 한데 이름은 잊어버렸군요……"], ["and I expect you know the other, too, a gentleman called Maximov.", "그리고 다른 한 분은 나리께서도 아실 듯합니다요, 막시모프라는 양반입니다요."], ["He’s been on a pilgrimage, so he says, to the monastery in the town.", "자기 말로는 고장에 있는 수도원으로 순례를 다녀오는 길이라더군요."], ["He’s traveling with this young relation of Mr. Miüsov.”", "미우소프 씨의 그 젊은 친척과 함께 다닌답니다요.\""]], [["“Is that all?”", "\"그게 전부인가?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Stay, listen, Trifon Borissovitch.", "\"가만, 들어 보게, 트리폰 보리소비치."], ["Tell me the chief thing: What of her?", "가장 중요한 걸 말해 주게. 그녀는 어떤가?"], ["How is she?”", "그녀의 상태는 어떠냐 말일세?\""]], [["“Oh, she’s only just come.", "\"오, 그분은 이제 막 오셨습니다요."], ["She’s sitting with them.”", "그들과 함께 앉아 계십죠.\""]], [["“Is she cheerful?", "\"명랑한가?"], ["Is she laughing?”", "웃고 있나?\""]], [["“No, I think she’s not laughing much.", "\"아니요, 별로 웃지 않으시는 것 같습니다요."], ["She’s sitting quite dull.", "아주 시무룩하게 앉아 계십죠."], ["She’s combing the young gentleman’s hair.”", "그 젊은 양반의 머리칼을 빗겨 주고 계십니다요.\""]], [["“The Pole—the officer?”", "\"폴란드 사람—그 장교 말인가?\""]], [["“He’s not young, and he’s not an officer, either.", "\"그 사람은 젊지도 않고, 장교도 아닙니다요."], ["Not him, sir.", "그 사람이 아니라요, 나리."], ["It’s the young gentleman that’s Mr. Miüsov’s relation ...", "미우소프 씨의 친척인 그 젊은 양반입니다요……"], ["I’ve forgotten his name.”", "이름을 잊어버렸습니다만.\""]], [["“Kalganov.”", "\"칼가노프로군.\""]], [["“That’s it, Kalganov!”", "\"맞습니다요, 칼가노프!\""]], [["“All right.", "\"좋아."], ["I’ll see for myself.", "내가 직접 보지."], ["Are they playing cards?”", "그들은 카드놀이를 하고 있나?\""]], [["“They have been playing, but they’ve left off.", "\"하고 있다가 그만뒀습니다요."], ["They’ve been drinking tea, the official gentleman asked for liqueurs.”", "차를 마셨고, 그 관리 양반이 리큐어를 청했습죠.\""]], [["“Stay, Trifon Borissovitch, stay, my good soul, I’ll see for myself.", "\"가만, 트리폰 보리소비치, 가만, 이 사람아, 내가 직접 보겠네."], ["Now answer one more question: are the gypsies here?”", "자, 한 가지만 더 대답해 주게. 집시들이 여기 와 있나?\""]], [["“You can’t have the gypsies now, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"지금은 집시를 부르실 수 없습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리."], ["The authorities have sent them away.", "당국에서 그들을 쫓아냈거든요."], ["But we’ve Jews that play the cymbals and the fiddle in the village, so one might send for them.", "하지만 마을에 심벌즈하고 바이올린을 켜는 유대인들이 있으니, 그들을 부르면 됩니다요."], ["They’d come.”", "그들이라면 올 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Send for them.", "\"불러 오게."], ["Certainly send for them!”", "꼭 불러 와!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“And you can get the girls together as you did then, Marya especially, Stepanida, too, and Arina.", "\"그리고 그때처럼 처녀들도 모아 올 수 있겠지, 특히 마리야, 스테파니다도, 그리고 아리나도."], ["Two hundred roubles for a chorus!”", "합창에 이백 루블이다!\""]], [["“Oh, for a sum like that I can get all the village together, though by now they’re asleep.", "\"오, 그만한 돈이라면 온 마을 사람들을 다 모아 올 수 있습죠, 비록 지금쯤은 다들 잠들어 있겠지만요."], ["Are the peasants here worth such kindness, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, or the girls either?", "한데 여기 농사꾼들이, 드미트리 표도로비치 나리, 그만한 후의를 받을 만한 자들입니까요, 처녀들인들 그렇겠습니까요?"], ["To spend a sum like that on such coarseness and rudeness!", "그런 거칠고 무례한 것들에게 그만한 돈을 쓰시다니요!"], ["What’s the good of giving a peasant a cigar to smoke, the stinking ruffian!", "농사꾼에게 시가를 물려 피우게 한들 무슨 소용입니까요, 그 고약한 냄새 나는 무뢰한들에게 말입니다요!"], ["And the girls are all lousy.", "처녀들이래야 죄다 이투성이고요."], ["Besides, I’ll get my daughters up for nothing, let alone a sum like that.", "게다가 제 딸들이라면 그만한 돈은커녕 거저라도 일으켜 세우겠습니다요."], ["They’ve only just gone to bed, I’ll give them a kick and set them singing for you.", "이제 막 잠자리에 들었으니, 한번 걷어차서 나리를 위해 노래를 부르게 하겠습니다요."], ["You gave the peasants champagne to drink the other day, e—ech!”", "나리께서는 지난번에 농사꾼들에게 샴페인까지 먹이지 않았습니까요, 에—이!\""]], [["For all his pretended compassion for Mitya, Trifon Borissovitch had hidden half a dozen bottles of champagne on that last occasion, and had picked up a hundred-rouble note under the table, and it had remained in his clutches.", "미탸를 동정하는 척하면서도, 트리폰 보리소비치는 그 지난번에 샴페인 여섯 병을 몰래 빼돌렸고, 식탁 밑에서 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 주워 자기 손아귀에 넣어 두었던 것이다."]], [["“Trifon Borissovitch, I sent more than one thousand flying last time I was here.", "\"트리폰 보리소비치, 지난번에 내가 여기 왔을 때 천 루블도 넘게 날려 버렸지."], ["Do you remember?”", "기억하나?\""]], [["“You did send it flying.", "\"날려 버리셨고말굽쇼."], ["I may well remember.", "똑똑히 기억하고 있습니다요."], ["You must have left three thousand behind you.”", "삼천 루블은 뿌리고 가셨을 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Well, I’ve come to do the same again, do you see?”", "\"그래, 이번에도 똑같이 하러 왔지, 알겠나?\""]], [["And he pulled out his roll of notes, and held them up before the innkeeper’s nose.", "그러고는 그가 지폐 뭉치를 꺼내 여인숙 주인의 코앞에 치켜들었다."]], [["“Now, listen and remember.", "\"자, 들어 두고 기억하게."], ["In an hour’s time the wine will arrive, savories, pies, and sweets—bring them all up at once.", "한 시간 뒤에 술이 도착할 걸세, 안주에 파이에 사탕까지—그것들을 죄다 한꺼번에 올려 주게."], ["That box Andrey has got is to be brought up at once, too.", "안드레이가 가지고 있는 저 상자도 당장 올려 와야 해."], ["Open it, and hand champagne immediately.", "그걸 열어서 샴페인을 즉시 내오게."], ["And the girls, we must have the girls, Marya especially.”", "그리고 처녀들, 처녀들이 있어야 하네, 특히 마리야 말일세.\""]], [["He turned to the cart and pulled out the box of pistols.", "그가 마차로 돌아서서 권총 상자를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Here, Andrey, let’s settle.", "\"자, 안드레이, 셈을 치르자."], ["Here’s fifteen roubles for the drive, and fifty for vodka ...", "여기 마차삯으로 십오 루블, 보드카 값으로 오십 루블이다……"], ["for your readiness, for your love....", "네 정성에 대해, 네 충정에 대해서지……"], ["Remember Karamazov!”", "카라마조프를 잊지 말거라!\""]], [["“I’m afraid, sir,”", "\"무섭습니다요, 나리.\""], ["faltered Andrey.", "안드레이가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“Give me five roubles extra, but more I won’t take.", "\"오 루블만 더 얹어 주십쇼, 그 이상은 받지 않겠습니다요."], ["Trifon Borissovitch, bear witness.", "트리폰 보리소비치, 증인이 되어 주시오."], ["Forgive my foolish words ...”", "제 어리석은 말들을 용서하십쇼……\""]], [["“What are you afraid of?”", "\"뭐가 무섭다는 게냐?\""], ["asked Mitya, scanning him.", "미탸가 그를 훑어보며 물었다."], ["“Well, go to the devil, if that’s it!”", "\"그래, 정 그렇다면 악마에게나 가거라!\""], ["he cried, flinging him five roubles.", "그가 오 루블을 던져 주며 외쳤다."], ["“Now, Trifon Borissovitch, take me up quietly and let me first get a look at them, so that they don’t see me.", "\"자, 트리폰 보리소비치, 조용히 나를 데려가 주게, 그들이 나를 보지 못하게 먼저 살펴볼 수 있도록 말일세."], ["Where are they?", "그들이 어디 있나?"], ["In the blue room?”", "파란 방에 있나?\""]], [["Trifon Borissovitch looked apprehensively at Mitya, but at once obediently did his bidding.", "트리폰 보리소비치는 불안한 듯 미탸를 쳐다보았으나, 곧바로 순순히 그가 시키는 대로 했다."], ["Leading him into the passage, he went himself into the first large room, adjoining that in which the visitors were sitting, and took the light away.", "그를 복도로 안내한 뒤, 자신이 먼저 손님들이 앉아 있는 방에 잇닿은 첫 번째 큰 방으로 들어가 불을 치웠다."], ["Then he stealthily led Mitya in, and put him in a corner in the dark, whence he could freely watch the company without being seen.", "그러고는 살그머니 미탸를 들여보내, 들키지 않고도 그 무리를 마음껏 살펴볼 수 있도록 어두운 구석에 세워 두었다."], ["But Mitya did not look long, and, indeed, he could not see them, he saw her, his heart throbbed violently, and all was dark before his eyes.", "그러나 미탸는 오래 보지 않았다, 아니, 실은 그들을 볼 수가 없었으니, 그는 그녀를 보았고, 그의 심장이 격렬하게 두근거렸으며, 눈앞이 온통 캄캄해졌다."]], [["She was sitting sideways to the table in a low chair, and beside her, on the sofa, was the pretty youth, Kalganov.", "그녀는 식탁 옆으로 비스듬히 낮은 의자에 앉아 있었고, 그 곁의 소파에는 잘생긴 젊은이 칼가노프가 있었다."], ["She was holding his hand and seemed to be laughing, while he, seeming vexed and not looking at her, was saying something in a loud voice to Maximov, who sat the other side of the table, facing Grushenka.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 웃고 있는 듯했는데, 그는 짜증이 난 듯 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않은 채, 식탁 맞은편에서 그루셴카를 마주 보고 앉은 막시모프에게 큰 소리로 무언가 말하고 있었다."], ["Maximov was laughing violently at something.", "막시모프는 무언가에 격하게 웃고 있었다."], ["On the sofa sat he, and on a chair by the sofa there was another stranger.", "소파에는 바로 그자가 앉아 있었고, 소파 옆 의자에는 또 다른 외지 사람이 있었다."], ["The one on the sofa was lolling backwards, smoking a pipe, and Mitya had an impression of a stoutish, broad-faced, short little man, who was apparently angry about something.", "소파에 앉은 자는 등을 뒤로 젖힌 채 파이프를 빨고 있었는데, 미탸는 다소 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 넓적한, 키 작은 사내라는 인상을 받았으며, 그자는 무언가에 화가 나 있는 듯했다."], ["His friend, the other stranger, struck Mitya as extraordinarily tall, but he could make out nothing more.", "그의 친구인 또 다른 외지 사람은 미탸에게 유달리 키가 크다는 느낌을 주었으나, 그 이상은 분간할 수 없었다."], ["He caught his breath.", "그는 숨이 막혔다."], ["He could not bear it for a minute, he put the pistol-case on a chest, and with a throbbing heart he walked, feeling cold all over, straight into the blue room to face the company.", "한순간도 더는 견딜 수 없어, 그는 권총 상자를 궤짝 위에 올려 두고, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 온몸에 한기를 느끼며, 곧장 파란 방으로 걸어 들어가 그 무리와 마주했다."]], [["“Aie!”", "\"아이쿠!\""], ["shrieked Grushenka, the first to notice him.", "그를 맨 먼저 알아본 그루셴카가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["# Chapter VII. The First And Rightful Lover", "# Chapter VII. The First And Rightful Lover"]], [["With his long, rapid strides, Mitya walked straight up to the table.", "미탸는 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 곧장 탁자 앞으로 다가갔다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["he said in a loud voice, almost shouting, yet stammering at every word, “I ...", "그는 거의 고함에 가까운 큰 소리로, 그러면서도 말마디마다 더듬거리며 말했다."], ["I’m all right!", "\"나, 나는 괜찮습니다!"], ["Don’t be afraid!”", "두려워 마세요!\""], ["he exclaimed, “I—there’s nothing the matter,”", "그가 외쳤다. \"나는, 아무 일도 없습니다.\""], ["he turned suddenly to Grushenka, who had shrunk back in her chair towards Kalganov, and clasped his hand tightly.", "그는 의자에 앉은 채 칼가노프 쪽으로 몸을 움츠리고 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 있던 그루센카를 향해 불쑥 돌아섰다."], ["“I ... I’m coming, too.", "\"나, 나도 함께 있겠소."], ["I’m here till morning.", "아침까지 여기 있겠소."], ["Gentlemen, may I stay with you till morning?", "여러분, 아침까지 여러분과 함께 있어도 되겠습니까?"], ["Only till morning, for the last time, in this same room?”", "그저 아침까지만, 마지막으로, 바로 이 방에서?\""]], [["So he finished, turning to the fat little man, with the pipe, sitting on the sofa.", "그렇게 말을 맺으며 그는 파이프를 문 채 소파에 앉아 있는 작고 뚱뚱한 사내를 향했다."], ["The latter removed his pipe from his lips with dignity and observed severely:", "그 사내는 위엄을 갖추어 입에서 파이프를 떼더니 엄하게 한마디 했다."]], [["“Panie, we’re here in private.", "\"여보시오, 우리는 여기서 사사로이 모여 있는 거요."], ["There are other rooms.”", "다른 방들이 있잖소.\""]], [["“Why, it’s you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch!", "\"아니, 당신이군요, 드미트리 표도로비치!"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["answered Kalganov suddenly.", "칼가노프가 불쑥 대답했다."], ["“Sit down with us.", "\"우리와 함께 앉으세요."], ["How are you?”", "안녕하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Delighted to see you, dear ...", "\"만나서 반갑소, 친애하는…"], ["and precious fellow, I always thought a lot of you.”", "소중한 친구여, 나는 늘 당신을 높이 여겨 왔소.\""], ["Mitya responded, joyfully and eagerly, at once holding out his hand across the table.", "미탸가 기쁨에 들떠 다급하게 응수하며, 곧장 탁자 너머로 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Aie!", "\"아얏!"], ["How tight you squeeze!", "어찌 그리 꽉 쥐시오!"], ["You’ve quite broken my fingers,”", "손가락이 다 부러지겠소.\""], ["laughed Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 웃었다."]], [["“He always squeezes like that, always,”", "\"이이는 늘 저렇게 꽉 쥔답니다, 늘 그래요.\""], ["Grushenka put in gayly, with a timid smile, seeming suddenly convinced from Mitya’s face that he was not going to make a scene.", "그루센카가 수줍은 미소를 띠며 명랑하게 끼어들었으니, 미탸의 얼굴을 보고 그가 소동을 부리지는 않으리라는 것을 문득 확신한 듯했다."], ["She was watching him with intense curiosity and still some uneasiness.", "그녀는 강한 호기심을, 그러면서도 여전히 약간의 불안을 품고 그를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["She was impressed by something about him, and indeed the last thing she expected of him was that he would come in and speak like this at such a moment.", "그에게서 무언가가 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았으니, 실로 그녀가 그에게서 가장 예상치 못한 것은 그가 이런 순간에 들어와 이런 식으로 말하는 것이었다."]], [["“Good evening,”", "\"안녕하시오.\""], ["Maximov ventured blandly on the left.", "막시모프가 왼편에서 부드럽게 인사를 건넸다."], ["Mitya rushed up to him, too.", "미탸는 그에게도 달려갔다."]], [["“Good evening.", "\"안녕하시오."], ["You’re here, too!", "당신도 여기 계셨군요!"], ["How glad I am to find you here, too!", "당신까지 여기서 만나다니 얼마나 기쁜지요!"], ["Gentlemen, gentlemen, I—”", "여러분, 여러분, 나는—\""], ["(He addressed the Polish gentleman with the pipe again, evidently taking him for the most important person present.) “I flew here....", "(그는 다시 파이프를 문 폴란드 신사에게 말을 건넸으니, 분명 그를 그 자리에서 가장 중요한 인물로 여기는 모양이었다.) \"나는 여기로 날아왔소…."], ["I wanted to spend my last day, my last hour in this room, in this very room ...", "나는 내 마지막 날을, 내 마지막 시간을 이 방에서, 바로 이 방에서…"], ["where I, too, adored ...", "나도 흠모했던…"], ["my queen....", "내 여왕을…."], ["Forgive me, panie,”", "용서하시오, 여보시오.\""], ["he cried wildly, “I flew here and vowed— Oh, don’t be afraid, it’s my last night!", "그가 격하게 외쳤다. \"나는 여기로 날아와 맹세했소— 오, 두려워 마시오, 이건 내 마지막 밤이오!"], ["Let’s drink to our good understanding.", "우리의 화목을 위해 한잔합시다."], ["They’ll bring the wine at once....", "곧 술을 가져올 거요…."], ["I brought this with me.”", "이걸 가지고 왔소.\""], ["(Something made him pull out his bundle of notes.) “Allow me, panie!", "(무언가에 떠밀린 듯 그는 지폐 뭉치를 꺼냈다.) \"허락해 주시오, 여보시오!"], ["I want to have music, singing, a revel, as we had before.", "나는 음악을, 노래를, 흥청거리는 잔치를 벌이고 싶소, 예전처럼 말이오."], ["But the worm, the unnecessary worm, will crawl away, and there’ll be no more of him.", "하지만 그 벌레는, 쓸데없는 벌레는 기어 사라질 것이고, 다시는 그런 놈이 없을 거요."], ["I will commemorate my day of joy on my last night.”", "나는 내 마지막 밤에 내 기쁨의 날을 기리겠소.\""]], [["He was almost choking.", "그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["There was so much, so much he wanted to say, but strange exclamations were all that came from his lips.", "말하고 싶은 것이 그토록 많고도 많았건만, 입에서 나오는 것이라곤 기이한 외침뿐이었다."], ["The Pole gazed fixedly at him, at the bundle of notes in his hand; looked at Grushenka, and was in evident perplexity.", "폴란드인은 그를, 그의 손에 든 지폐 뭉치를 뚫어지게 바라보더니 그루센카를 쳐다보며 역력한 당혹감에 빠져 있었다."]], [["“If my suverin lady is permitting—”", "\"내 군주이신 마님께서 허락하신다면—\""], ["he was beginning.", "그가 입을 떼기 시작했다."]], [["“What does ‘suverin’ mean?", "\"'군주'가 무슨 뜻이에요?"], ["‘Sovereign,’ I suppose?”", "'주권자' 말씀인가요?\""], ["interrupted Grushenka.", "그루센카가 말을 가로챘다."], ["“I can’t help laughing at you, the way you talk.", "\"당신 말투를 들으면 웃음이 나서 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["Sit down, Mitya, what are you talking about?", "앉아요, 미탸, 무슨 소리를 하는 거예요?"], ["Don’t frighten us, please.", "제발 우리를 놀라게 하지 말아요."], ["You won’t frighten us, will you?", "우리를 놀라게 하진 않을 거죠?"], ["If you won’t, I am glad to see you ...”", "그러지만 않는다면 당신을 보게 돼서 기뻐요….\""]], [["“Me, me frighten you?”", "\"내가, 내가 당신을 놀라게 한다고?\""], ["cried Mitya, flinging up his hands.", "미탸가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, pass me by, go your way, I won’t hinder you!...”", "\"오, 나를 지나쳐 가시오, 당신 갈 길을 가시오, 나는 방해하지 않겠소!…\""]], [["And suddenly he surprised them all, and no doubt himself as well, by flinging himself on a chair, and bursting into tears, turning his head away to the opposite wall, while his arms clasped the back of the chair tight, as though embracing it.", "그러더니 그는 별안간 의자에 몸을 던지고 울음을 터뜨려, 머리를 맞은편 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 두 팔로 의자 등받이를 끌어안듯 꽉 부둥켜안아, 모두를, 그리고 틀림없이 자기 자신마저 놀라게 만들었다."]], [["“Come, come, what a fellow you are!”", "\"이런, 이런, 당신은 정말 어쩔 수 없는 사람이군요!\""], ["cried Grushenka reproachfully.", "그루센카가 나무라듯 외쳤다."], ["“That’s just how he comes to see me—he begins talking, and I can’t make out what he means.", "\"꼭 저런 식으로 나를 보러 온다니까요—말을 시작하는데 무슨 소린지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없어요."], ["He cried like that once before, and now he’s crying again!", "전에도 한 번 저렇게 울더니, 이제 또 우는군요!"], ["It’s shameful!", "부끄럽지도 않아요?"], ["Why are you crying?", "왜 우는 거예요?"], ["As though you had anything to cry for!”", "마치 울 일이라도 있는 것처럼!\""], ["she added enigmatically, emphasizing each word with some irritability.", "그녀는 짜증 섞인 어조로 말마디마다 힘을 주며 수수께끼 같은 말을 덧붙였다."]], [["“I ... I’m not crying....", "\"나, 나는 울지 않소…."], ["Well, good evening!”", "자, 안녕하시오!\""], ["He instantly turned round in his chair, and suddenly laughed, not his abrupt wooden laugh, but a long, quivering, inaudible nervous laugh.", "그는 곧장 의자에서 몸을 돌리더니 별안간 웃었는데, 그 퉁명스럽고 나무토막 같은 웃음이 아니라 길게 떨리는, 소리 없는 신경질적인 웃음이었다."]], [["“Well, there you are again....", "\"자, 또 시작이네요…."], ["Come, cheer up, cheer up!”", "자, 기운 내요, 기운 내요!\""], ["Grushenka said to him persuasively.", "그루센카가 그를 달래듯 말했다."], ["“I’m very glad you’ve come, very glad, Mitya, do you hear, I’m very glad!", "\"당신이 와 줘서 정말 기뻐요, 정말로요, 미탸, 듣고 있어요, 정말 기쁘다니까요!"], ["I want him to stay here with us,”", "이이가 여기 우리와 함께 있었으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said peremptorily, addressing the whole company, though her words were obviously meant for the man sitting on the sofa.", "그녀는 좌중 전체를 향해 단호하게 말했지만, 그 말이 분명 소파에 앉은 사내를 겨냥한 것임은 누가 봐도 알 수 있었다."], ["“I wish it, I wish it!", "\"그게 내 바람이에요, 내 바람이라고요!"], ["And if he goes away I shall go, too!”", "그리고 만약 이이가 가 버린다면 나도 가겠어요!\""], ["she added with flashing eyes.", "그녀는 눈을 번득이며 덧붙였다."]], [["“What my queen commands is law!”", "\"내 여왕께서 분부하시는 것이 곧 법이외다!\""], ["pronounced the Pole, gallantly kissing Grushenka’s hand.", "폴란드인이 그루센카의 손에 정중히 입을 맞추며 선언했다."], ["“I beg you, panie, to join our company,”", "\"여보시오, 우리 자리에 함께해 주시기를 청합니다.\""], ["he added politely, addressing Mitya.", "그가 미탸를 향해 정중하게 덧붙였다."]], [["Mitya was jumping up with the obvious intention of delivering another tirade, but the words did not come.", "미탸는 또 한바탕 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 작정으로 벌떡 일어났지만, 말이 나오지 않았다."]], [["“Let’s drink, panie,”", "\"한잔합시다, 여보시오.\""], ["he blurted out instead of making a speech.", "그는 연설을 늘어놓는 대신 이렇게 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["Every one laughed.", "모두가 웃었다."]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["I thought he was going to begin again!”", "또 시작하는 줄 알았네요!\""], ["Grushenka exclaimed nervously.", "그루센카가 신경질적으로 외쳤다."], ["“Do you hear, Mitya,”", "\"들어요, 미탸.\""], ["she went on insistently, “don’t prance about, but it’s nice you’ve brought the champagne.", "그녀가 끈질기게 말을 이었다. \"껑충거리며 설치지 말아요."], ["I want some myself, and I can’t bear liqueurs.", "그래도 샴페인을 가져온 건 잘했어요."], ["And best of all, you’ve come yourself.", "나도 좀 마시고 싶었는데, 리큐어는 질색이거든요."], ["We were fearfully dull here....", "그리고 무엇보다도, 당신이 직접 와 줬으니까요."], ["You’ve come for a spree again, I suppose?", "여기 정말 지루해 죽을 지경이었거든요…. 또 흥청거리려고 온 거죠?"], ["But put your money in your pocket.", "그건 그렇고 돈은 주머니에 넣어 둬요."], ["Where did you get such a lot?”", "그렇게 많은 돈을 어디서 났어요?\""]], [["Mitya had been, all this time, holding in his hand the crumpled bundle of notes on which the eyes of all, especially of the Poles, were fixed.", "미탸는 그동안 줄곧 구겨진 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 모두의, 특히 폴란드인들의 시선이 거기에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["In confusion he thrust them hurriedly into his pocket.", "그는 당황하여 황급히 그것을 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["He flushed.", "얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["At that moment the innkeeper brought in an uncorked bottle of champagne, and glasses on a tray.", "바로 그때 여관 주인이 마개를 딴 샴페인 한 병과 쟁반에 받친 잔들을 들고 들어왔다."], ["Mitya snatched up the bottle, but he was so bewildered that he did not know what to do with it.", "미탸가 병을 낚아챘지만 너무도 얼떨떨한 나머지 그것을 어찌해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["Kalganov took it from him and poured out the champagne.", "칼가노프가 그에게서 병을 받아 샴페인을 따랐다."]], [["“Another! Another bottle!”", "\"한 병 더!"], ["Mitya cried to the innkeeper, and, forgetting to clink glasses with the Pole whom he had so solemnly invited to drink to their good understanding, he drank off his glass without waiting for any one else.", "한 병 더 가져오시오!\" 미탸가 여관 주인에게 외치고는, 그토록 엄숙하게 화목을 위해 건배하자고 청했던 폴란드인과 잔을 부딪치는 것도 잊은 채, 누구도 기다리지 않고 제 잔을 단숨에 비웠다."], ["His whole countenance suddenly changed.", "그의 표정이 별안간 온통 달라졌다."], ["The solemn and tragic expression with which he had entered vanished completely, and a look of something childlike came into his face.", "들어올 때 띠고 있던 엄숙하고 비극적인 표정은 완전히 사라지고, 무언가 어린아이 같은 기색이 얼굴에 떠올랐다."], ["He seemed to have become suddenly gentle and subdued.", "그는 별안간 온순하고 잠잠해진 듯했다."], ["He looked shyly and happily at every one, with a continual nervous little laugh, and the blissful expression of a dog who has done wrong, been punished, and forgiven.", "그는 수줍고도 행복한 표정으로 모두를 바라보며 끊임없이 신경질적인 작은 웃음을 흘렸으니, 잘못을 저질러 벌을 받았다가 용서받은 개의 더없이 행복한 표정 같았다."], ["He seemed to have forgotten everything, and was looking round at every one with a childlike smile of delight.", "그는 모든 것을 잊어버린 듯, 어린아이처럼 기쁨에 찬 미소를 띠고 모두를 둘러보았다."], ["He looked at Grushenka, laughing continually, and bringing his chair close up to her.", "그는 그루센카를 바라보며 끊임없이 웃었고, 의자를 그녀 가까이로 바짝 끌어당겼다."], ["By degrees he had gained some idea of the two Poles, though he had formed no definite conception of them yet.", "차차 그는 두 폴란드인에 대해 어느 정도 감을 잡게 되었으나, 아직 그들에 대한 명확한 인상을 갖추지는 못했다."]], [["The Pole on the sofa struck him by his dignified demeanor and his Polish accent; and, above all, by his pipe.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인은 그 위엄 있는 태도와 폴란드 억양으로, 그리고 무엇보다 그 파이프로 미탸에게 강한 인상을 주었다."], ["“Well, what of it?", "\"뭐, 그게 어떻다는 거지?"], ["It’s a good thing he’s smoking a pipe,”", "파이프를 피우는 거야 좋은 일이지.\""], ["he reflected.", "그는 이렇게 생각했다."], ["The Pole’s puffy, middle-aged face, with its tiny nose and two very thin, pointed, dyed and impudent-looking mustaches, had not so far roused the faintest doubts in Mitya.", "그 폴란드인의 부석부석하고 중년에 접어든 얼굴은 자그마한 코와, 가늘고 뾰족하게 다듬어 염색한 두 가닥의 뻔뻔스러워 보이는 콧수염을 달고 있었지만, 아직 미탸의 마음속에 일말의 의심도 불러일으키지 못했다."], ["He was not even particularly struck by the Pole’s absurd wig made in Siberia, with love-locks foolishly combed forward over the temples. “I suppose it’s all right since he wears a wig,”", "그는 그 폴란드인이 시베리아에서 만든 우스꽝스러운 가발을 쓰고, 관자놀이 위로 어리석게 빗어 내린 곱슬머리 타래를 늘어뜨린 것조차 별로 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["he went on, musing blissfully.", "\"가발을 쓰고 있으니 뭐 괜찮겠지.\""], ["The other, younger Pole, who was staring insolently and defiantly at the company and listening to the conversation with silent contempt, still only impressed Mitya by his great height, which was in striking contrast to the Pole on the sofa.", "그는 황홀하게 상념에 잠긴 채 생각을 이어 갔다. 좌중을 뻔뻔스럽고 도전적으로 노려보며 침묵 속의 경멸을 담아 대화를 듣고 있던 또 한 사람, 더 젊은 폴란드인은 그의 엄청난 키로만 미탸에게 인상을 주었으니, 그것은 소파에 앉은 폴란드인과는 두드러진 대조를 이루었다."], ["“If he stood up he’d be six foot three.”", "\"저자가 일어선다면 여섯 자 석 치는 되겠군.\""], ["The thought flitted through Mitya’s mind.", "이런 생각이 미탸의 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."], ["It occurred to him, too, that this Pole must be the friend of the other, as it were, a “bodyguard,” and no doubt the big Pole was at the disposal of the little Pole with the pipe.", "또한 그는 이 폴란드인이 다른 자의 친구, 말하자면 \"경호원\"이며, 이 큰 폴란드인은 틀림없이 파이프를 문 작은 폴란드인의 처분에 맡겨진 신세이리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["But this all seemed to Mitya perfectly right and not to be questioned.", "그러나 이 모든 것이 미탸에게는 더없이 당연하고 의심할 여지가 없어 보였다."], ["In his mood of doglike submissiveness all feeling of rivalry had died away.", "개처럼 순종하는 그의 기분 속에서 경쟁심이라는 감정은 죄다 사그라들어 있었다."]], [["Grushenka’s mood and the enigmatic tone of some of her words he completely failed to grasp.", "그루센카의 기분과 그녀가 내뱉은 몇몇 말의 수수께끼 같은 어조를 그는 전혀 알아채지 못했다."], ["All he understood, with thrilling heart, was that she was kind to him, that she had forgiven him, and made him sit by her.", "그가 가슴 떨리는 마음으로 이해한 것이라곤 오로지, 그녀가 자기에게 다정하다는 것, 자기를 용서하고 자기 곁에 앉게 해 주었다는 것뿐이었다."], ["He was beside himself with delight, watching her sip her glass of champagne.", "그는 그녀가 샴페인 잔을 홀짝이는 모습을 지켜보며 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 몰랐다."], ["The silence of the company seemed somehow to strike him, however, and he looked round at every one with expectant eyes.", "그러나 좌중의 침묵이 어쩐지 그의 마음에 걸린 듯, 그는 기대에 찬 눈으로 모두를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Why are we sitting here though, gentlemen?", "\"그런데 여러분, 우리는 왜 이렇게 앉아만 있는 거요?"], ["Why don’t you begin doing something?”", "왜 뭔가 시작하지 않는 거요?\""], ["his smiling eyes seemed to ask.", "그의 웃음 띤 눈이 이렇게 묻는 듯했다."]], [["“He keeps talking nonsense, and we were all laughing,”", "\"이 사람이 자꾸 허튼소리를 늘어놓아서 다들 웃고 있었답니다.\""], ["Kalganov began suddenly, as though divining his thought, and pointing to Maximov.", "칼가노프가 그의 생각을 알아챈 듯 별안간 입을 열며 막시모프를 가리켰다."]], [["Mitya immediately stared at Kalganov and then at Maximov.", "미탸는 곧장 칼가노프를, 그러고 나서 막시모프를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“He’s talking nonsense?”", "\"이분이 허튼소리를 한다고?\""], ["he laughed, his short, wooden laugh, seeming suddenly delighted at something—“ha ha!”", "그가 짧고 나무토막 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며, 무언가에 별안간 즐거워진 듯 웃었다. \"하하!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇다니까요."], ["Would you believe it, he will have it that all our cavalry officers in the twenties married Polish women.", "글쎄, 믿어지시겠어요, 이 사람은 20년대에 우리 기병 장교들이 죄다 폴란드 여자와 결혼했다고 우긴다니까요."], ["That’s awful rot, isn’t it?”", "어처구니없는 허풍 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Polish women?”", "\"폴란드 여자라고?\""], ["repeated Mitya, perfectly ecstatic.", "미탸가 완전히 황홀경에 빠져 되뇌었다."]], [["Kalganov was well aware of Mitya’s attitude to Grushenka, and he guessed about the Pole, too, but that did not so much interest him, perhaps did not interest him at all; what he was interested in was Maximov.", "칼가노프는 미탸가 그루센카에게 품은 마음을 잘 알고 있었고, 폴란드인에 대해서도 짐작하고 있었지만, 그런 것은 그다지 그의 관심을 끌지 못했으니, 어쩌면 전혀 관심이 없었는지도 몰랐다."], ["He had come here with Maximov by chance, and he met the Poles here at the inn for the first time in his life.", "그의 관심을 끄는 것은 막시모프였다. 그는 우연히 막시모프와 함께 이곳에 왔고, 폴란드인들은 이 여관에서 난생처음 만났다."], ["Grushenka he knew before, and had once been with some one to see her; but she had not taken to him.", "그루센카는 전부터 알고 지냈으며, 한번은 누군가와 함께 그녀를 보러 온 적도 있었지만, 그녀는 그를 마음에 들어 하지 않았다."], ["But here she looked at him very affectionately: before Mitya’s arrival, she had been making much of him, but he seemed somehow to be unmoved by it.", "그러나 여기서 그녀는 그를 무척 다정하게 바라보았으니, 미탸가 도착하기 전까지 그녀는 그를 한껏 추어올렸으나, 그는 어쩐지 거기에 마음이 동하지 않는 듯했다."], ["He was a boy, not over twenty, dressed like a dandy, with a very charming fair-skinned face, and splendid thick, fair hair.", "그는 스무 살이 채 안 된 소년으로, 멋쟁이처럼 차려입었고, 살결이 희고 무척 매력적인 얼굴에 숱 많고 멋진 금발을 지니고 있었다."], ["From his fair face looked out beautiful pale blue eyes, with an intelligent and sometimes even deep expression, beyond his age indeed, although the young man sometimes looked and talked quite like a child, and was not at all ashamed of it, even when he was aware of it himself.", "그 흰 얼굴에서는 아름다운 옅은 푸른 눈이 내다보았으니, 그 눈에는 영리하고 때로는 심지어 깊이까지 어린 표정이, 실로 그 나이를 넘어서는 표정이 깃들어 있었다. 비록 이 젊은이는 이따금 어린아이처럼 보이고 어린아이처럼 말했으며, 스스로 그것을 깨달았을 때조차 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았지만 말이다."], ["As a rule he was very willful, even capricious, though always friendly.", "대체로 그는 무척 제멋대로이고 심지어 변덕스러웠으나, 늘 다정했다."], ["Sometimes there was something fixed and obstinate in his expression.", "때로는 그의 표정에 무언가 고정되고 완고한 데가 있었다."], ["He would look at you and listen, seeming all the while to be persistently dreaming over something else.", "그는 당신을 바라보고 귀를 기울이면서도, 그러는 내내 무언가 다른 것을 끈질기게 꿈꾸는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Often he was listless and lazy, at other times he would grow excited, sometimes, apparently, over the most trivial matters.", "흔히 그는 무기력하고 나른했으나, 때로는 흥분하기도 했으니, 그것도 더러는 보기에 더없이 하찮은 일에 그러했다."]], [["“Only imagine, I’ve been taking him about with me for the last four days,”", "\"글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보세요, 내가 나흘째 이 사람을 데리고 다니는 중이랍니다.\""], ["he went on, indolently drawling his words, quite naturally though, without the slightest affectation.", "그는 나른하게 말을 끌면서, 그러나 조금도 꾸밈없이 아주 자연스럽게 말을 이었다."], ["“Ever since your brother, do you remember, shoved him off the carriage and sent him flying.", "\"당신 형님이, 기억하시죠, 이 사람을 마차에서 밀쳐 내동댕이친 그날 이후로 말입니다."], ["That made me take an interest in him at the time, and I took him into the country, but he keeps talking such rot I’m ashamed to be with him.", "그 일로 그때 이 사람한테 관심이 생겨서 시골로 데려왔는데, 어찌나 허튼소리를 늘어놓는지 함께 다니기가 창피할 지경이에요."], ["I’m taking him back.”", "그래서 다시 데려다주는 길이랍니다.\""]], [["“The gentleman has not seen Polish ladies, and says what is impossible,”", "\"이 양반은 폴란드 부인들을 본 적이 없으니 있을 수 없는 소리를 하는 거외다.\""], ["the Pole with the pipe observed to Maximov.", "파이프를 문 폴란드인이 막시모프를 향해 말했다."]], [["He spoke Russian fairly well, much better, anyway, than he pretended.", "그는 러시아어를 제법 잘했으니, 아무튼 짐짓 꾸며 보이는 것보다는 훨씬 잘했다."], ["If he used Russian words, he always distorted them into a Polish form.", "러시아어 단어를 쓸 때면 그는 늘 그것을 폴란드식으로 비틀어 댔다."]], [["“But I was married to a Polish lady myself,”", "\"하지만 나도 폴란드 부인과 결혼했었는걸요.\""], ["tittered Maximov.", "막시모프가 킬킬거렸다."]], [["“But did you serve in the cavalry?", "\"그렇지만 기병으로 복무하긴 했소?"], ["You were talking about the cavalry.", "당신은 기병 이야기를 하고 있었잖소."], ["Were you a cavalry officer?”", "기병 장교였소?\""], ["put in Kalganov at once.", "칼가노프가 곧장 끼어들었다."]], [["“Was he a cavalry officer indeed?", "\"정말로 기병 장교였답니까?"], ["Ha ha!”", "하하!\""], ["cried Mitya, listening eagerly, and turning his inquiring eyes to each as he spoke, as though there were no knowing what he might hear from each.", "미탸가 열심히 귀를 기울이며 외쳤으니, 말하는 동안 그는 캐묻는 눈길을 한 사람 한 사람에게로 돌렸으며, 마치 각자에게서 무슨 말을 듣게 될지 알 수 없다는 투였다."]], [["“No, you see,” Maximov turned to him.", "\"아니요, 그게 말이죠,\""], ["“What I mean is that those pretty Polish ladies ...", "막시모프가 그를 향해 돌아섰다. \"내 말은, 그 어여쁜 폴란드 부인들이…"], ["when they danced the mazurka with our Uhlans ...", "우리 창기병들과 마주르카를 출 때면…"], ["when one of them dances a mazurka with a Uhlan she jumps on his knee like a kitten ...", "그중 하나가 창기병과 마주르카를 추다가 새끼고양이처럼 그 무릎 위로 폴짝 뛰어오른답니다…"], ["a little white one ...", "자그마한 흰 고양이처럼요…"], ["and the pan-father and pan-mother look on and allow it....", "그러면 그 댁 아버님이며 어머님이 보고도 그냥 내버려 두지요…."], ["They allow it ...", "내버려 둔답니다…"], ["and next day the Uhlan comes and offers her his hand....", "그러고는 이튿날 창기병이 와서 그녀에게 청혼을 하는 겁니다…."], ["That’s how it is ...", "바로 그런 식이지요…"], ["offers her his hand, he he!”", "청혼을 한다니까요, 헤헤!\""], ["Maximov ended, tittering.", "막시모프가 킬킬거리며 말을 맺었다."]], [["“The pan is a lajdak!”", "\"이 양반은 비열한 놈이오!\""], ["the tall Pole on the chair growled suddenly and crossed one leg over the other.", "의자에 앉은 키 큰 폴란드인이 별안간 으르렁대며 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 꼬아 얹었다."], ["Mitya’s eye was caught by his huge greased boot, with its thick, dirty sole.", "미탸의 눈에 두껍고 더러운 밑창이 달린 그의 커다란 기름투성이 장화가 들어왔다."], ["The dress of both the Poles looked rather greasy.", "두 폴란드인의 옷차림은 어딘지 기름때가 낀 듯했다."]], [["“Well, now it’s lajdak!", "\"이젠 비열한 놈이라고요!"], ["What’s he scolding about?”", "대체 무얼 가지고 욕설을 퍼붓는 거예요?\""], ["said Grushenka, suddenly vexed.", "그루센카가 별안간 짜증을 내며 말했다."]], [["“Pani Agrippina, what the gentleman saw in Poland were servant girls, and not ladies of good birth,”", "\"아그리피나 마님, 이 양반이 폴란드에서 본 건 하녀들이지 양가의 부인들이 아니었던 거외다.\""], ["the Pole with the pipe observed to Grushenka.", "파이프를 문 폴란드인이 그루센카를 향해 말했다."]], [["“You can reckon on that,”", "\"그건 장담할 수 있소.\""], ["the tall Pole snapped contemptuously.", "키 큰 폴란드인이 경멸하듯 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“What next!", "\"별꼴이야!"], ["Let him talk!", "이야기하게 놔둬요!"], ["People talk, why hinder them?", "사람들이 이야기하는데 왜 막는 거예요?"], ["It makes it cheerful,”", "그래야 흥겹잖아요.\""], ["Grushenka said crossly.", "그루센카가 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“I’m not hindering them, pani,”", "\"막는 게 아니외다, 마님.\""], ["said the Pole in the wig, with a long look at Grushenka, and relapsing into dignified silence he sucked his pipe again.", "가발을 쓴 폴란드인이 그루센카를 길게 바라보며 말하고는, 다시 위엄 있는 침묵 속으로 가라앉아 파이프를 빨았다."]], [["“No, no.", "\"아니, 아니."], ["The Polish gentleman spoke the truth.”", "폴란드 양반 말이 옳아요.\""], ["Kalganov got excited again, as though it were a question of vast import.", "칼가노프가 마치 무슨 대단히 중요한 문제라도 되는 양 다시 흥분했다."], ["“He’s never been in Poland, so how can he talk about it?", "\"이 사람은 폴란드에 가 본 적이 없는데 어떻게 폴란드 이야기를 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["I suppose you weren’t married in Poland, were you?”", "당신은 폴란드에서 결혼한 게 아니었죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“No, in the Province of Smolensk.", "\"아니요, 스몰렌스크 현(縣)에서였어요."], ["Only, a Uhlan had brought her to Russia before that, my future wife, with her mamma and her aunt, and another female relation with a grown-up son.", "다만 그 전에 어느 창기병이 그녀를, 내 장차 아내가 될 사람을 어머니와 이모, 그리고 또 다 큰 아들을 둔 다른 여자 친척과 함께 러시아로 데려온 거지요."], ["He brought her straight from Poland and gave her up to me.", "그가 그녀를 폴란드에서 곧장 데려와 나한테 넘겨준 거예요."], ["He was a lieutenant in our regiment, a very nice young man.", "그는 우리 연대의 중위였는데, 아주 좋은 청년이었지요."], ["At first he meant to marry her himself.", "처음엔 자기가 그녀와 결혼할 작정이었어요."], ["But he didn’t marry her, because she turned out to be lame.”", "하지만 그녀가 절름발이로 드러나는 바람에 결혼하지 않았답니다.\""]], [["“So you married a lame woman?”", "\"그래서 절름발이 여자랑 결혼했단 말이에요?\""], ["cried Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇답니다."], ["They both deceived me a little bit at the time, and concealed it.", "그때 두 사람이 나를 조금 속이고 그걸 감췄던 거지요."], ["I thought she was hopping; she kept hopping....", "나는 그녀가 깡충거리는 줄로만 알았어요. 자꾸 깡충거리길래…."], ["I thought it was for fun.”", "그저 장난삼아 그러는 줄 알았답니다.\""]], [["“So pleased she was going to marry you!”", "\"당신과 결혼하게 돼서 그렇게나 좋았던 거군요!\""], ["yelled Kalganov, in a ringing, childish voice.", "칼가노프가 어린아이처럼 낭랑한 목소리로 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes, so pleased.", "\"그래요, 그렇게나 좋았던 거지요."], ["But it turned out to be quite a different cause.", "그런데 알고 보니 영 다른 까닭이었던 겁니다."], ["Afterwards, when we were married, after the wedding, that very evening, she confessed, and very touchingly asked forgiveness.", "나중에 우리가 결혼하고 나서, 식을 올린 바로 그날 저녁에 그녀가 고백을 하면서 무척이나 애처롭게 용서를 빌더군요."], ["‘I once jumped over a puddle when I was a child,’ she said, ‘and injured my leg.’ He he!”", "'제가 어렸을 적에 한번은 웅덩이를 뛰어넘다가 다리를 다쳤어요'라고 말이지요. 헤헤!\""]], [["Kalganov went off into the most childish laughter, almost falling on the sofa.", "칼가노프가 더없이 어린아이 같은 웃음을 터뜨리며 거의 소파에 쓰러질 지경이었다."], ["Grushenka, too, laughed.", "그루센카도 웃었다."], ["Mitya was at the pinnacle of happiness.", "미탸는 행복의 절정에 올라 있었다."]], [["“Do you know, that’s the truth, he’s not lying now,”", "\"있잖아요, 그건 사실이에요, 이번엔 거짓말이 아니라고요.\""], ["exclaimed Kalganov, turning to Mitya; “and do you know, he’s been married twice; it’s his first wife he’s talking about.", "칼가노프가 미탸를 향해 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"그리고 말이죠, 이 사람은 두 번 결혼했는데, 지금 말하는 건 첫 아내 이야기예요."], ["But his second wife, do you know, ran away, and is alive now.”", "그런데 두 번째 아내는요, 글쎄, 달아나 버려서 지금도 살아 있답니다.\""]], [["“Is it possible?”", "\"그게 가능한 일이오?\""], ["said Mitya, turning quickly to Maximov with an expression of the utmost astonishment.", "미탸가 더없는 경악의 표정을 띠고 막시모프를 향해 휙 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["She did run away.", "정말로 달아나 버렸지요."], ["I’ve had that unpleasant experience,”", "나는 그런 불쾌한 일을 겪었답니다.\""], ["Maximov modestly assented, “with a monsieur.", "막시모프가 겸손하게 인정했다. \"어떤 양반과 말이지요."], ["And what was worse, she’d had all my little property transferred to her beforehand.", "게다가 더 나빴던 건, 그 여자가 미리 내 자그마한 재산을 죄다 자기 앞으로 돌려놓았던 겁니다. '당신은 배운 사람이잖아요'라고 그 여자가 나한테 말하더군요."], ["‘You’re an educated man,’ she said to me.", "'언제든 벌어먹고 살 수 있잖아요.'"], ["‘You can always get your living.’ She settled my business with that.", "그렇게 한마디로 내 일을 끝장내 버린 거지요. 어느 존경할 만한 주교님이 언젠가 나한테 이런 말씀을 하셨답니다."], ["A venerable bishop once said to me: ‘One of your wives was lame, but the other was too light-footed.’ He he!”", "'자네 아내 하나는 절름발이였지만, 다른 하나는 발이 너무 가벼웠구먼.' 헤헤!\""]], [["“Listen, listen!”", "\"들어 봐요, 들어 봐요!\""], ["cried Kalganov, bubbling over, “if he’s telling lies—and he often is—he’s only doing it to amuse us all.", "칼가노프가 들떠서 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다. \"만약 이 사람이 거짓말을 하는 거라면—실은 종종 그러지만—그건 그저 우리 모두를 즐겁게 해 주려는 거예요."], ["There’s no harm in that, is there?", "그게 뭐 나쁠 거 있나요, 안 그래요?"], ["You know, I sometimes like him.", "있잖아요, 난 가끔 이 사람이 좋아져요."], ["He’s awfully low, but it’s natural to him, eh?", "형편없이 비루한 사람이지만, 그게 타고난 천성이거든요, 안 그래요?"], ["Don’t you think so?", "그렇게 생각 안 하세요?"], ["Some people are low from self-interest, but he’s simply so, from nature.", "어떤 사람들은 잇속 때문에 비루해지지만, 이 사람은 그저 천성이 그런 거예요."], ["Only fancy, he claims (he was arguing about it all the way yesterday) that Gogol wrote Dead Souls about him.", "글쎄, 생각 좀 해 보세요, 이 사람은 고골이 자기를 두고 《죽은 혼》을 썼다고 우긴다니까요(어제 내내 그걸 두고 옥신각신했지요)."], ["Do you remember, there’s a landowner called Maximov in it, whom Nozdryov thrashed.", "기억하시죠, 거기 막시모프라는 지주가 나오는데, 노즈드료프한테 흠씬 두들겨 맞잖아요."], ["He was charged, do you remember, ‘for inflicting bodily injury with rods on the landowner Maximov in a drunken condition.’ Would you believe it, he claims that he was that Maximov and that he was beaten!", "그 사람이 기소된 게, 기억나세요, '술에 취한 상태에서 지주 막시모프에게 회초리로 신체적 상해를 가한 죄'였지요. 그런데 글쎄, 이 사람이 자기가 바로 그 막시모프이고 자기가 매를 맞은 거라고 우긴다니까요!"], ["Now can it be so?", "자, 그게 말이 됩니까?"], ["Tchitchikov made his journey, at the very latest, at the beginning of the twenties, so that the dates don’t fit.", "치치코프가 여행을 한 게 아무리 늦어도 20년대 초니까 시기가 도무지 안 맞잖아요."], ["He couldn’t have been thrashed then, he couldn’t, could he?”", "그때 이 사람이 두들겨 맞았을 리가 없어요, 그럴 리가요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["It was difficult to imagine what Kalganov was excited about, but his excitement was genuine.", "칼가노프가 무엇 때문에 흥분하는 것인지 헤아리기는 어려웠으나, 그 흥분만큼은 진심이었다."], ["Mitya followed his lead without protest.", "미탸는 군말 없이 그의 말장단을 따라갔다."]], [["“Well, but if they did thrash him!”", "\"뭐, 하지만 정말로 두들겨 팼다면 어떻소!\""], ["he cried, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 외쳤다."]], [["“It’s not that they thrashed me exactly, but what I mean is—”", "\"날 두들겨 팼다기보다는, 내 말은 그게—\""], ["put in Maximov.", "막시모프가 끼어들었다."]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말이오?"], ["Either they thrashed you or they didn’t.”", "두들겨 맞았든지 안 맞았든지 둘 중 하나 아니오.\""]], [["“What o’clock is it, panie?”", "\"몇 시나 됐소, 여보시오?\""], ["the Pole, with the pipe, asked his tall friend, with a bored expression.", "파이프를 문 폴란드인이 따분한 표정으로 키 큰 친구에게 물었다."], ["The other shrugged his shoulders in reply.", "상대는 대답 대신 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["Neither of them had a watch.", "둘 다 시계가 없었던 것이다."]], [["“Why not talk?", "\"이야기 좀 하면 어때서?"], ["Let other people talk.", "남들이 이야기하게 놔두라고요."], ["Mustn’t other people talk because you’re bored?”", "당신이 따분하다고 남들도 이야기하면 안 된단 말이에요?\""], ["Grushenka flew at him with evident intention of finding fault.", "그루센카가 트집을 잡을 작정이 역력한 채 그에게 달려들었다."], ["Something seemed for the first time to flash upon Mitya’s mind.", "미탸의 머릿속에 처음으로 무언가가 번뜩이는 듯했다."], ["This time the Pole answered with unmistakable irritability.", "이번에 폴란드인은 누가 봐도 분명한 짜증을 내며 대꾸했다."]], [["“Pani, I didn’t oppose it.", "\"마님, 나는 막지 않았소."], ["I didn’t say anything.”", "아무 말도 안 했소이다.\""]], [["“All right then.", "\"그럼 됐어요."], ["Come, tell us your story,”", "자, 당신 이야기를 들려줘요.\""], ["Grushenka cried to Maximov.", "그루센카가 막시모프에게 외쳤다."], ["“Why are you all silent?”", "\"왜들 죄다 입을 다물고 있는 거예요?\""]], [["“There’s nothing to tell, it’s all so foolish,”", "\"들려드릴 게 없는걸요, 죄다 어리석은 이야기뿐이라서요.\""], ["answered Maximov at once, with evident satisfaction, mincing a little.", "막시모프가 곧장 대답했는데, 역력히 만족스러운 기색으로 약간 점잔을 뺐다."], ["“Besides, all that’s by way of allegory in Gogol, for he’s made all the names have a meaning.", "\"게다가 그게 다 고골이 빗대어 쓴 거라서요, 이름마다 죄다 무슨 뜻이 있게 만들어 놓았거든요."], ["Nozdryov was really called Nosov, and Kuvshinikov had quite a different name, he was called Shkvornev.", "노즈드료프는 실은 노소프라는 이름이었고, 쿠브시니코프는 영 다른 이름, 시크보르네프였답니다."], ["Fenardi really was called Fenardi, only he wasn’t an Italian but a Russian, and Mamsel Fenardi was a pretty girl with her pretty little legs in tights, and she had a little short skirt with spangles, and she kept turning round and round, only not for four hours but for four minutes only, and she bewitched every one...”", "페나르디는 정말로 페나르디였지만, 이탈리아인이 아니라 러시아인이었고, 마드무아젤 페나르디는 살색 타이츠 속에 예쁘장한 작은 다리를 드러낸 어여쁜 처녀로, 반짝이 장식을 단 짤막한 치마를 입고 빙글빙글 돌아 댔지요, 다만 네 시간이 아니라 딱 사 분 동안만요, 그래도 모두를 홀려 버렸답니다….\""]], [["“But what were you beaten for?”", "\"그런데 당신은 뭣 때문에 두들겨 맞은 거예요?\""], ["cried Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 외쳤다."]], [["“For Piron!”", "\"피롱 때문이지요!\""], ["answered Maximov.", "막시모프가 대답했다."]], [["“What Piron?”", "\"피롱이 누구요?\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["“The famous French writer, Piron.", "\"그 유명한 프랑스 작가, 피롱 말입니다."], ["We were all drinking then, a big party of us, in a tavern at that very fair.", "그때 우리가 다들 술을 마시고 있었어요, 한 무리가 잔뜩 모여서, 바로 그 장터 어느 선술집에서요."], ["They’d invited me, and first of all I began quoting epigrams.", "사람들이 날 불러서, 나는 우선 풍자시 몇 구절을 읊기 시작했지요."], ["‘Is that you, Boileau?", "'거기 자네 부알로 아닌가?"], ["What a funny get-up!’ and Boileau answers that he’s going to a masquerade, that is to the baths, he he!", "무슨 우스꽝스러운 차림이람!' 그러자 부알로가 자기는 가장무도회에 가는 길이라고, 그러니까 목욕탕에 간다고 대답하는 거지요, 헤헤!"], ["And they took it to themselves, so I made haste to repeat another, very sarcastic, well known to all educated people:", "그런데 사람들이 그걸 자기들한테 한 소리로 받아들이는 바람에, 나는 부랴부랴 또 다른 시를, 아주 신랄하고 배운 사람이라면 누구나 다 아는 시를 읊었답니다."]], [["Yes, Sappho and Phaon are we!", "그렇소, 우리는 사포와 파온이라오!"], ["But one grief is weighing on me.", "허나 한 가지 시름이 나를 짓누른다오."], ["You don’t know your way to the sea!", "그대는 바다로 가는 길을 모르나니!"]], [["They were still more offended and began abusing me in the most unseemly way for it.", "그러자 사람들이 더더욱 화를 내며 더없이 망측한 말로 날 욕해 대기 시작하더군요."], ["And as ill-luck would have it, to set things right, I began telling a very cultivated anecdote about Piron, how he was not accepted into the French Academy, and to revenge himself wrote his own epitaph:", "그런데 운수 사납게도, 일을 바로잡으려고 나는 피롱에 관한 아주 교양 있는 일화를 들려주기 시작했지요, 그가 프랑스 학술원에 받아들여지지 못해서, 그 앙갚음으로 자기 묘비명을 직접 지었다는 이야기 말입니다. 여기 아무것도 아니었던 피롱이 잠들다, 학술원 회원조차 못 되었던 자가."]], [["Ci-gît Piron qui ne fut rien, Pas même académicien.", "그랬더니 사람들이 날 붙잡아 흠씬 두들겨 패는 거예요.\""]], [["They seized me and thrashed me.”", "\"그런데 뭣 때문에요? 뭣 때문에?\""]], [["“But what for? What for?”", "\"내 교양 때문이지요."], ["“For my education. People can thrash a man for anything,”", "사람이란 무슨 이유로든 남을 두들겨 팰 수 있는 법이랍니다.\""], ["Maximov concluded, briefly and sententiously.", "막시모프가 짤막하게, 격언조로 말을 맺었다."]], [["“Eh, that’s enough!", "\"에, 그만하면 됐어요!"], ["That’s all stupid, I don’t want to listen.", "죄다 어리석은 소리뿐이라 더는 듣고 싶지 않아요."], ["I thought it would be amusing,”", "재미있을 줄 알았더니만.\""], ["Grushenka cut them short, suddenly.", "그루센카가 별안간 그들의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["Mitya started, and at once left off laughing.", "미탸가 움찔하더니 곧장 웃음을 거두었다."], ["The tall Pole rose upon his feet, and with the haughty air of a man, bored and out of his element, began pacing from corner to corner of the room, his hands behind his back.", "키 큰 폴란드인이 일어서더니, 따분하고 자기 자리가 아닌 데 끼인 듯한 사내 특유의 오만한 태도로 뒷짐을 진 채 방 이 구석 저 구석을 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["“Ah, he can’t sit still,”", "\"아, 저이는 가만히 앉아 있질 못하네요.\""], ["said Grushenka, looking at him contemptuously.", "그루센카가 그를 경멸하듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["Mitya began to feel anxious.", "미탸는 불안을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He noticed besides, that the Pole on the sofa was looking at him with an irritable expression.", "게다가 그는 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 짜증스러운 표정으로 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Panie!”", "\"여보시오!\""], ["cried Mitya, “let’s drink!", "미탸가 외쳤다. \"한잔합시다!"], ["and the other pan, too!", "저쪽 양반도 함께!"], ["Let us drink.”", "한잔합시다.\""]], [["In a flash he had pulled three glasses towards him, and filled them with champagne.", "그는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 잔 세 개를 끌어당겨 샴페인으로 가득 채웠다."]], [["“To Poland, panovie, I drink to your Poland!”", "\"폴란드를 위하여, 여러분, 나는 당신네 폴란드를 위해 마시겠소!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["“I shall be delighted, panie,”", "\"기꺼이 받겠소, 여보시오.\""], ["said the Pole on the sofa, with dignity and affable condescension, and he took his glass.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 위엄과 상냥한 겸양을 갖추어 말하며 잔을 들었다."]], [["“And the other pan, what’s his name?", "\"그리고 저쪽 양반, 성함이 뭐였더라?"], ["Drink, most illustrious, take your glass!”", "드시오, 더없이 고명하신 분, 잔을 드시오!\""], ["Mitya urged.", "미탸가 재촉했다."]], [["“Pan Vrublevsky,”", "\"브루블레프스키 씨요.\""], ["put in the Pole on the sofa.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 일러 주었다."]], [["Pan Vrublevsky came up to the table, swaying as he walked.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 휘청거리는 걸음으로 탁자로 다가왔다."]], [["“To Poland, panovie!”", "\"폴란드를 위하여, 여러분!\""], ["cried Mitya, raising his glass.", "미탸가 잔을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["“Hurrah!”", "\"만세!\""]], [["All three drank.", "셋이 다 마셨다."], ["Mitya seized the bottle and again poured out three glasses.", "미탸가 병을 움켜쥐고 다시 잔 세 개를 채웠다."]], [["“Now to Russia, panovie, and let us be brothers!”", "\"이번엔 러시아를 위하여, 여러분, 그리고 우리 형제가 됩시다!\""]], [["“Pour out some for us,”", "\"우리한테도 좀 따라 줘요.\""], ["said Grushenka; “I’ll drink to Russia, too!”", "그루센카가 말했다. \"나도 러시아를 위해 마시겠어요!\""]], [["“So will I,”", "\"나도요.\""], ["said Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 말했다."]], [["“And I would, too ...", "\"나도 마시지요…"], ["to Russia, the old grandmother!”", "러시아를 위하여, 늙은 할머니를 위하여!\""], ["tittered Maximov.", "막시모프가 킬킬거렸다."]], [["“All!", "\"모두!"], ["All!”", "모두!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“Trifon Borissovitch, some more bottles!”", "\"트리폰 보리소비치, 술 좀 더 가져오시오!\""]], [["The other three bottles Mitya had brought with him were put on the table.", "미탸가 가지고 온 나머지 세 병이 탁자 위에 놓였다."], ["Mitya filled the glasses.", "미탸가 잔을 채웠다."]], [["“To Russia!", "\"러시아를 위하여!"], ["Hurrah!”", "만세!\""], ["he shouted again.", "그가 다시 소리쳤다."], ["All drank the toast except the Poles, and Grushenka tossed off her whole glass at once.", "폴란드인들을 빼고는 모두가 건배를 들이켰고, 그루센카는 제 잔을 단숨에 쭉 비웠다."], ["The Poles did not touch theirs.", "폴란드인들은 자기들 잔에 손도 대지 않았다."]], [["“How’s this, panovie?”", "\"이게 어찌 된 일이오, 여러분?\""], ["cried Mitya, “won’t you drink it?”", "미탸가 외쳤다. \"마시지 않겠소?\""]], [["Pan Vrublevsky took the glass, raised it and said with a resonant voice:", "브루블레프스키 씨가 잔을 들어 치켜올리더니 우렁찬 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“To Russia as she was before 1772.”", "\"1772년 이전의 러시아를 위하여.\""]], [["“Come, that’s better!”", "\"옳거니, 그게 낫구려!\""], ["cried the other Pole, and they both emptied their glasses at once.", "다른 폴란드인이 외쳤고, 두 사람이 단숨에 잔을 비웠다."]], [["“You’re fools, you panovie,”", "\"당신네는 바보들이오, 여러분.\""], ["broke suddenly from Mitya.", "미탸의 입에서 별안간 이런 말이 튀어나왔다."]], [["“Panie!”", "\"여보시오!\""], ["shouted both the Poles, menacingly, setting on Mitya like a couple of cocks.", "두 폴란드인이 한 쌍의 수탉처럼 미탸에게 달려들 듯 위협적으로 외쳤다."], ["Pan Vrublevsky was specially furious.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 특히 길길이 날뛰었다."]], [["“Can one help loving one’s own country?”", "\"제 조국을 사랑하는 게 어찌 잘못이란 말이오?\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Be silent!", "\"닥쳐요!"], ["Don’t quarrel!", "다투지 말아요!"], ["I won’t have any quarreling!”", "다투는 건 못 봐줘요!\""], ["cried Grushenka imperiously, and she stamped her foot on the floor.", "그루센카가 위압적으로 외치며 발로 바닥을 쾅 굴렀다."], ["Her face glowed, her eyes were shining.", "그녀의 얼굴이 달아오르고 눈이 빛났다."], ["The effects of the glass she had just drunk were apparent.", "방금 마신 술기운이 역력했다."], ["Mitya was terribly alarmed.", "미탸는 끔찍하게 당황했다."]], [["“Panovie, forgive me!", "\"여러분, 용서하시오!"], ["It was my fault, I’m sorry.", "내 잘못이었소, 미안하오."], ["Vrublevsky, panie Vrublevsky, I’m sorry.”", "브루블레프스키, 브루블레프스키 씨, 미안하오.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, you, anyway!", "\"당신부터 입 좀 다물어요, 아무튼!"], ["Sit down, you stupid!”", "앉아요, 이 멍청한 양반아!\""], ["Grushenka scolded with angry annoyance.", "그루센카가 성난 짜증을 내며 나무랐다."]], [["Every one sat down, all were silent, looking at one another.", "모두가 자리에 앉았고, 다들 입을 다문 채 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“Gentlemen, I was the cause of it all,”", "\"여러분, 이게 다 내 탓이었소.\""], ["Mitya began again, unable to make anything of Grushenka’s words.", "미탸가 그루센카의 말뜻을 도무지 헤아리지 못한 채 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“Come, why are we sitting here?", "\"자, 우리가 왜 이렇게 앉아만 있는 거요?"], ["What shall we do ...", "무얼 하면…"], ["to amuse ourselves again?”", "우리 다시 즐겨 볼까요?\""]], [["“Ach, it’s certainly anything but amusing!”", "\"아흐, 정말이지 도무지 즐겁지가 않네요!\""], ["Kalganov mumbled lazily.", "칼가노프가 나른하게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Let’s play faro again, as we did just now,”", "\"아까처럼 다시 파로 게임을 합시다.\""], ["Maximov tittered suddenly.", "막시모프가 별안간 킬킬거렸다."]], [["“Faro?", "\"파로?"], ["Splendid!”", "좋소!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“If only the panovie—”", "\"여러분만 좋다면야—\""]], [["“It’s lite, panovie,”", "\"늦었소이다, 여러분.\""], ["the Pole on the sofa responded, as it were unwillingly.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 마지못한 듯 응수했다."]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞는 말이오.\""], ["assented Pan Vrublevsky.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 동의했다."]], [["“Lite?", "\"늦었다고요?"], ["What do you mean by ‘lite’?”", "'늦었다'니 무슨 말이에요?\""], ["asked Grushenka.", "그루센카가 물었다."]], [["“Late, pani! ‘a late hour’ I mean,”", "\"늦은 시각이라는 말이외다, 마님!"], ["the Pole on the sofa explained.", "'깊은 밤'이라는 뜻이지요.\" 소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 설명했다."]], [["“It’s always late with them.", "\"이 사람들은 늘 늦었다지 뭐예요."], ["They can never do anything!”", "도무지 아무것도 못 한다니까요!\""], ["Grushenka almost shrieked in her anger.", "그루센카가 성이 나서 거의 비명을 지르듯 했다."], ["“They’re dull themselves, so they want others to be dull.", "\"자기들이 따분하니까 남들도 따분하길 바라는 거예요."], ["Before you came, Mitya, they were just as silent and kept turning up their noses at me.”", "당신이 오기 전에도, 미탸, 이 사람들은 꼭 저렇게 입을 다물고 나한테 콧대만 세우고 있었다니까요.\""]], [["“My goddess!”", "\"내 여신이여!\""], ["cried the Pole on the sofa, “I see you’re not well-disposed to me, that’s why I’m gloomy.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 외쳤다. \"당신이 나를 탐탁잖게 여기는 걸 알겠소이다, 그래서 내가 우울한 거외다."], ["I’m ready, panie,”", "나는 준비됐소, 여보시오.\""], ["added he, addressing Mitya.", "그가 미탸를 향해 덧붙였다."]], [["“Begin, panie,”", "\"시작합시다, 여보시오.\""], ["Mitya assented, pulling his notes out of his pocket, and laying two hundred-rouble notes on the table.", "미탸가 동의하며 주머니에서 지폐를 꺼내 백 루블짜리 두 장을 탁자 위에 내려놓았다."], ["“I want to lose a lot to you.", "\"나는 당신에게 잔뜩 잃고 싶소."], ["Take your cards.", "패를 잡으시오."], ["Make the bank.”", "물주를 맡으시오.\""]], [["“We’ll have cards from the landlord, panie,”", "\"여관 주인한테서 패를 가져옵시다, 여보시오.\""], ["said the little Pole, gravely and emphatically.", "작은 폴란드인이 진지하고 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“That’s much the best way,”", "\"그게 훨씬 좋은 방법이외다.\""], ["chimed in Pan Vrublevsky.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“From the landlord?", "\"여관 주인한테서?"], ["Very good, I understand, let’s get them from him.", "좋소, 알겠소, 그한테서 가져옵시다."], ["Cards!”", "카드!\""], ["Mitya shouted to the landlord.", "미탸가 여관 주인에게 소리쳤다."]], [["The landlord brought in a new, unopened pack, and informed Mitya that the girls were getting ready, and that the Jews with the cymbals would most likely be here soon; but the cart with the provisions had not yet arrived.", "여관 주인이 새것 그대로 봉하지 않은 패 한 벌을 들고 들어와, 처녀들이 채비를 차리고 있고 심벌즈를 든 유대인들도 곧 당도할 듯하다고 미탸에게 일러 주었으나, 먹거리를 실은 짐수레는 아직 도착하지 않았다고 했다."], ["Mitya jumped up from the table and ran into the next room to give orders, but only three girls had arrived, and Marya was not there yet.", "미탸가 탁자에서 벌떡 일어나 분부를 내리러 옆방으로 달려갔지만, 처녀는 셋만 와 있을 뿐 마리야는 아직 없었다."], ["And he did not know himself what orders to give and why he had run out.", "게다가 그는 무슨 분부를 내려야 할지, 무엇 때문에 달려 나왔는지 자기도 알지 못했다."], ["He only told them to take out of the box the presents for the girls, the sweets, the toffee and the fondants.", "그는 다만 상자에서 처녀들에게 줄 선물들, 사탕과 토피와 봉봉을 꺼내라고만 일렀다. \"그리고 안드레이한테 보드카를, 안드레이한테 보드카를!\""], ["“And vodka for Andrey, vodka for Andrey!”", "그가 다급하게 외쳤다."], ["he cried in haste. “I was rude to Andrey!”", "\"내가 안드레이한테 무례하게 굴었지!\""]], [["Suddenly Maximov, who had followed him out, touched him on the shoulder.", "별안간 그를 따라 나온 막시모프가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다."]], [["“Give me five roubles,”", "\"오 루블만 주세요.\""], ["he whispered to Mitya.", "그가 미탸에게 속삭였다."], ["“I’ll stake something at faro, too, he he!”", "\"나도 파로에 좀 걸어 보게요, 헤헤!\""]], [["“Capital!", "\"좋고말고요!"], ["Splendid!", "훌륭하오!"], ["Take ten, here!”", "십 루블 받으시오, 여기!\""]], [["Again he took all the notes out of his pocket and picked out one for ten roubles.", "그는 다시 주머니에서 지폐를 죄다 꺼내 십 루블짜리 한 장을 골랐다."], ["“And if you lose that, come again, come again.”", "\"그걸 잃거든 또 오시오, 또 와요.\""]], [["“Very good,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["Maximov whispered joyfully, and he ran back again.", "막시모프가 기쁘게 속삭이고는 다시 안으로 달려 들어갔다."], ["Mitya, too, returned, apologizing for having kept them waiting.", "미탸도 그들을 기다리게 해서 미안하다고 사과하며 돌아왔다."], ["The Poles had already sat down, and opened the pack.", "폴란드인들은 이미 자리에 앉아 패를 펼쳐 놓고 있었다."], ["They looked much more amiable, almost cordial.", "그들은 한결 상냥해진, 거의 다정한 기색이었다."], ["The Pole on the sofa had lighted another pipe and was preparing to throw.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인은 또 다른 파이프에 불을 붙이고 패를 던질 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["He wore an air of solemnity.", "그는 엄숙한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다."]], [["“To your places, gentlemen,”", "\"자리에들 앉으시오, 여러분.\""], ["cried Pan Vrublevsky.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["“No, I’m not going to play any more,”", "\"아니요, 나는 더 안 할 겁니다.\""], ["observed Kalganov, “I’ve lost fifty roubles to them just now.”", "칼가노프가 한마디 했다. \"방금 이 사람들한테 오십 루블을 잃었거든요.\""]], [["“The pan had no luck, perhaps he’ll be lucky this time,”", "\"이 양반은 운이 없었던 거외다, 어쩌면 이번엔 운이 트일지도 모르지요.\""], ["the Pole on the sofa observed in his direction.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 그쪽을 향해 한마디 했다."]], [["“How much in the bank?", "\"물주 판돈은 얼마요?"], ["To correspond?”", "거기에 맞추겠소.\""], ["asked Mitya.", "미탸가 물었다."]], [["“That’s according, panie, maybe a hundred, maybe two hundred, as much as you will stake.”", "\"형편 닿는 대로지요, 여보시오, 백이 될 수도, 이백이 될 수도 있소, 당신이 거는 만큼 말이오.\""]], [["“A million!”", "\"백만!\""], ["laughed Mitya.", "미탸가 웃었다."]], [["“The Pan Captain has heard of Pan Podvysotsky, perhaps?”", "\"대위 양반은 어쩌면 포드비소츠키 씨에 대해 들어 보셨을지 모르겠소?\""]], [["“What Podvysotsky?”", "\"포드비소츠키가 누구요?\""]], [["“In Warsaw there was a bank and any one comes and stakes against it.", "\"바르샤바에 도박판이 하나 있었는데, 누구든 와서 물주를 상대로 돈을 거는 거외다. 포드비소츠키가 와서 금화 천 닢을 보고는 물주를 상대로 걸었지요."], ["Podvysotsky comes, sees a thousand gold pieces, stakes against the bank.", "물주가 말하기를, '포드비소츠키 씨, 금화를 내려놓으시려오, 아니면 우리가 당신 명예를 믿어야 하오?' '내 명예를 믿으시오, 여보시오'"], ["The banker says, ‘Panie Podvysotsky, are you laying down the gold, or must we trust to your honor?’ ‘To my honor, panie,’ says Podvysotsky.", "하고 포드비소츠키가 말했소. '그게 더 좋소.'"], ["‘So much the better.’ The banker throws the dice.", "물주가 주사위를 던졌소. 포드비소츠키가 이겼지요."], ["Podvysotsky wins.", "'가져가시오, 여보시오'"], ["‘Take it, panie,’ says the banker, and pulling out the drawer he gives him a million.", "하고 물주가 말하며 서랍을 빼서 그에게 백만을 내주었소. '가져가시오, 여보시오, 이게 당신이 딴 거외다.'"], ["‘Take it, panie, this is your gain.’ There was a million in the bank.", "도박판에 백만이 걸려 있었던 거지요. '난 그런 줄 몰랐소'"], ["‘I didn’t know that,’ says Podvysotsky.", "하고 포드비소츠키가 말했소. '포드비소츠키 씨,'"], ["‘Panie Podvysotsky,’ said the banker, ‘you pledged your honor and we pledged ours.’ Podvysotsky took the million.”", "물주가 말하기를, '당신은 명예를 걸었고 우리도 명예를 걸었소이다.' 포드비소츠키가 백만을 가져갔지요.\""]], [["“That’s not true,”", "\"그건 사실이 아니오.\""], ["said Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 말했다."]], [["“Panie Kalganov, in gentlemanly society one doesn’t say such things.”", "\"칼가노프 씨, 신사들 사이에서는 그런 말을 하는 게 아니외다.\""]], [["“As if a Polish gambler would give away a million!”", "\"폴란드 노름꾼이 백만을 거저 내줬다니, 어림도 없는 소리지!\""], ["cried Mitya, but checked himself at once.", "미탸가 외쳤다가 곧장 스스로를 다잡았다."], ["“Forgive me, panie, it’s my fault again, he would, he would give away a million, for honor, for Polish honor.", "\"용서하시오, 여보시오, 또 내 잘못이오, 그렇소, 그는 내줬을 거요, 명예를 위해, 폴란드의 명예를 위해서라면 백만을 내줬을 거요."], ["You see how I talk Polish, ha ha!", "보시오, 내가 이렇게 폴란드 말을 한다오, 하하!"], ["Here, I stake ten roubles, the knave leads.”", "자, 나는 십 루블을 걸겠소, 잭을 내겠소.\""]], [["“And I put a rouble on the queen, the queen of hearts, the pretty little panienotchka, he he!”", "\"그러면 나는 퀸에다 일 루블을 걸겠어요, 하트 퀸에다, 그 어여쁜 아가씨한테요, 헤헤!\""], ["laughed Maximov, pulling out his queen, and, as though trying to conceal it from every one, he moved right up and crossed himself hurriedly under the table.", "막시모프가 자기 퀸을 꺼내며 웃었고, 마치 모두에게 그것을 감추려는 듯 바짝 다가앉아 탁자 밑에서 황급히 성호를 그었다."], ["Mitya won.", "미탸가 이겼다."], ["The rouble won, too.", "일 루블도 이겼다."]], [["“A corner!”", "\"코너!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["“I’ll bet another rouble, a ‘single’ stake,”", "\"나는 일 루블 더 걸겠소, '단일' 판돈으로요.\""], ["Maximov muttered gleefully, hugely delighted at having won a rouble.", "막시모프가 일 루블을 딴 것에 잔뜩 신이 나서 기쁘게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Lost!”", "\"잃었소!\""], ["shouted Mitya.", "미탸가 소리쳤다."], ["“A ‘double’ on the seven!”", "\"칠에다 '더블'!\""]], [["The seven too was trumped.", "칠도 잡아먹혔다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"그만!\""], ["cried Kalganov suddenly.", "칼가노프가 별안간 외쳤다."]], [["“Double!", "\"더블!"], ["Double!”", "더블!\""], ["Mitya doubled his stakes, and each time he doubled the stake, the card he doubled was trumped by the Poles.", "미탸가 판돈을 곱절로 올렸고, 곱절로 올릴 때마다 그가 곱절을 건 패는 폴란드인들에게 잡아먹혔다."], ["The rouble stakes kept winning.", "일 루블짜리 판돈은 계속 이겼다."]], [["“On the double!”", "\"곱절로!\""], ["shouted Mitya furiously.", "미탸가 길길이 날뛰며 소리쳤다."]], [["“You’ve lost two hundred, panie.", "\"이백을 잃으셨소, 여보시오."], ["Will you stake another hundred?”", "백을 더 거시겠소?\""], ["the Pole on the sofa inquired.", "소파에 앉은 폴란드인이 물었다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐요?"], ["Lost two hundred already?", "벌써 이백을 잃었다고?"], ["Then another two hundred!", "그럼 또 이백!"], ["All doubles!”", "죄다 곱절로!\""]], [["And pulling his money out of his pocket, Mitya was about to fling two hundred roubles on the queen, but Kalganov covered it with his hand.", "그러고서 미탸가 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 이백 루블을 퀸에다 내던지려는데, 칼가노프가 손으로 그것을 덮었다."]], [["“That’s enough!”", "\"그만하면 됐어요!\""], ["he shouted in his ringing voice.", "그가 낭랑한 목소리로 소리쳤다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["Mitya stared at him.", "미탸가 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“That’s enough!", "\"그만하면 됐어요!"], ["I don’t want you to play any more.", "더는 노름 안 했으면 좋겠어요."], ["Don’t!”", "하지 말아요!\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because I don’t.", "\"내가 싫으니까요."], ["Hang it, come away.", "젠장, 그만 일어나요."], ["That’s why.", "그래서예요."], ["I won’t let you go on playing.”", "더는 노름하게 놔두지 않겠어요.\""]], [["Mitya gazed at him in astonishment.", "미탸가 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Give it up, Mitya.", "\"그만둬요, 미탸."], ["He may be right. You’ve lost a lot as it is,”", "이 사람 말이 옳을지도 몰라요."], ["said Grushenka, with a curious note in her voice.", "이미 많이 잃었잖아요.\" 그루센카가 묘한 어조를 담아 말했다."], ["Both the Poles rose from their seats with a deeply offended air.", "폴란드인 둘이 몹시 모욕당한 기색으로 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“Are you joking, panie?”", "\"농담이오, 여보시오?\""], ["said the short man, looking severely at Kalganov.", "작은 사내가 칼가노프를 엄하게 노려보며 말했다."]], [["“How dare you!”", "\"네놈이 감히!\""], ["Pan Vrublevsky, too, growled at Kalganov.", "브루블레프스키 씨도 칼가노프에게 으르렁댔다."]], [["“Don’t dare to shout like that,”", "\"감히 그렇게 소리 지르지 말아요.\""], ["cried Grushenka.", "그루센카가 외쳤다."], ["“Ah, you turkey-cocks!”", "\"아휴, 이 칠면조 수컷들 같으니!\""]], [["Mitya looked at each of them in turn.", "미탸가 그들을 차례로 둘러보았다."], ["But something in Grushenka’s face suddenly struck him, and at the same instant something new flashed into his mind—a strange new thought!", "그러나 그루센카의 얼굴에 어린 무언가가 별안간 그의 마음을 쳤고, 바로 그 순간 무언가 새로운 것이 그의 머릿속에 번뜩였으니—기이하고도 새로운 생각이었다!"]], [["“Pani Agrippina,”", "\"아그리피나 마님,\""], ["the little Pole was beginning, crimson with anger, when Mitya suddenly went up to him and slapped him on the shoulder.", "작은 폴란드인이 분노로 시뻘게진 얼굴로 입을 떼는데, 미탸가 별안간 그에게 다가가 어깨를 툭 쳤다."]], [["“Most illustrious, two words with you.”", "\"더없이 고명하신 분, 당신과 두 마디만 합시다.\""]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무얼 원하시오?\""]], [["“In the next room, I’ve two words to say to you, something pleasant, very pleasant.", "\"옆방에서, 당신과 두 마디 나눌 게 있소, 무언가 흐뭇한, 아주 흐뭇한 일이오."], ["You’ll be glad to hear it.”", "듣고 나면 기뻐할 거요.\""]], [["The little pan was taken aback and looked apprehensively at Mitya.", "작은 양반이 흠칫 놀라 미탸를 미심쩍게 바라보았다."], ["He agreed at once, however, on condition that Pan Vrublevsky went with them.", "그래도 그는 브루블레프스키 씨가 함께 간다는 조건으로 곧 응했다."]], [["“The bodyguard?", "\"경호원 말이오?"], ["Let him come, and I want him, too.", "같이 옵시다, 나도 그가 함께 갔으면 하오."], ["I must have him!”", "그가 꼭 있어야 하오!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“March, panovie!”", "\"갑시다, 여러분!\""]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가는 거예요?\""], ["asked Grushenka, anxiously.", "그루센카가 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“We’ll be back in one moment,”", "\"금방 돌아올 거요.\""], ["answered Mitya.", "미탸가 대답했다."]], [["There was a sort of boldness, a sudden confidence shining in his eyes.", "그의 눈에는 무언가 대담함이, 별안간 솟은 자신감이 빛나고 있었다."], ["His face had looked very different when he entered the room an hour before.", "한 시간 전 이 방에 들어섰을 때 그의 얼굴은 사뭇 달라 보였더랬다."]], [["He led the Poles, not into the large room where the chorus of girls was assembling and the table was being laid, but into the bedroom on the right, where the trunks and packages were kept, and there were two large beds, with pyramids of cotton pillows on each.", "그는 폴란드인들을 처녀들의 합창대가 모여들고 식탁이 차려지고 있는 큰 방이 아니라, 오른편 침실로 데려갔으니, 그곳에는 트렁크와 짐 꾸러미가 놓여 있고, 큼직한 침대 두 개에 솜 베개가 저마다 피라미드처럼 쌓여 있었다."], ["There was a lighted candle on a small deal table in the corner.", "구석의 자그마한 전나무 탁자 위에는 불 켜진 양초가 놓여 있었다."], ["The small man and Mitya sat down to this table, facing each other, while the huge Vrublevsky stood beside them, his hands behind his back.", "작은 사내와 미탸가 이 탁자에 마주 보고 앉았고, 거구의 브루블레프스키는 뒷짐을 진 채 그들 곁에 섰다."], ["The Poles looked severe but were evidently inquisitive.", "폴란드인들은 엄한 표정이었으나 역력히 궁금해하는 기색이었다."]], [["“What can I do for you, panie?”", "\"무슨 일이오, 여보시오?\""], ["lisped the little Pole.", "작은 폴란드인이 혀짤배기소리로 물었다."]], [["“Well, look here, panie, I won’t keep you long.", "\"자, 이보시오, 여보시오, 길게 붙들지 않겠소."], ["There’s money for you,”", "당신한테 줄 돈이 있소.\""], ["he pulled out his notes.", "그가 지폐를 꺼냈다."], ["“Would you like three thousand?", "\"삼천 루블, 어떻소?"], ["Take it and go your way.”", "그걸 받고 당신 갈 길을 가시오.\""]], [["The Pole gazed open-eyed at Mitya, with a searching look.", "폴란드인이 캐묻는 눈초리로 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 미탸를 바라보았다."]], [["“Three thousand, panie?”", "\"삼천 루블이라, 여보시오?\""], ["He exchanged glances with Vrublevsky.", "그가 브루블레프스키와 눈빛을 주고받았다."]], [["“Three, panovie, three!", "\"삼천이오, 여러분, 삼천!"], ["Listen, panie, I see you’re a sensible man.", "들어 보시오, 여보시오, 당신이 분별 있는 사람인 걸 알겠소."], ["Take three thousand and go to the devil, and Vrublevsky with you—d’you hear?", "삼천을 받고 악마한테로 꺼지시오, 브루블레프스키도 함께—알겠소?"], ["But, at once, this very minute, and for ever.", "다만, 당장, 바로 이 순간, 그리고 영원히 말이오."], ["You understand that, panie, for ever.", "알아들었소, 여보시오, 영원히."], ["Here’s the door, you go out of it.", "여기 문이 있소, 그리로 나가면 되오."], ["What have you got there, a great-coat, a fur coat?", "거기 무얼 가졌소, 외투, 모피 코트?"], ["I’ll bring it out to you.", "내가 가져다주겠소."], ["They’ll get the horses out directly, and then—good-by, panie!”", "곧 말을 끌어낼 테니, 그러고는—잘 가시오, 여보시오!\""]], [["Mitya awaited an answer with assurance.", "미탸가 자신만만하게 대답을 기다렸다."], ["He had no doubts.", "그는 일말의 의심도 없었다."], ["An expression of extraordinary resolution passed over the Pole’s face.", "폴란드인의 얼굴에 비상한 결연함의 빛이 스쳐 갔다."]], [["“And the money, panie?”", "\"그래서 돈은, 여보시오?\""]], [["“The money, panie?", "\"돈 말이오, 여보시오?"], ["Five hundred roubles I’ll give you this moment for the journey, and as a first installment, and two thousand five hundred to-morrow, in the town—I swear on my honor, I’ll get it, I’ll get it at any cost!”", "노자로, 그리고 첫 몫으로 오백 루블을 이 순간 당신에게 주겠소, 그리고 나머지 이천오백은 내일, 읍내에서—내 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 무슨 수를 써서라도 마련하겠소, 마련하고말고!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["The Poles exchanged glances again.", "폴란드인들이 다시 눈빛을 주고받았다."], ["The short man’s face looked more forbidding.", "작은 사내의 얼굴이 한층 험상궂어 보였다."]], [["“Seven hundred, seven hundred, not five hundred, at once, this minute, cash down!”", "\"칠백, 칠백이오, 오백이 아니라, 당장, 이 순간, 현찰로!\""], ["Mitya added, feeling something wrong.", "미탸가 무언가 잘못되어 가는 것을 느끼며 덧붙였다."], ["“What’s the matter, panie?", "\"무슨 일이오, 여보시오?"], ["Don’t you trust me?", "나를 못 믿겠소?"], ["I can’t give you the whole three thousand straight off.", "삼천을 한꺼번에 다 줄 수는 없소."], ["If I give it, you may come back to her to-morrow....", "그걸 다 주면 당신이 내일 그녀한테 되돌아올지도 모르잖소…."], ["Besides, I haven’t the three thousand with me.", "게다가 지금 수중에 삼천이 없소."], ["I’ve got it at home in the town,”", "읍내 집에 두고 왔소.\""], ["faltered Mitya, his spirit sinking at every word he uttered.", "미탸가 자기 입에서 말이 한 마디씩 나올 때마다 기가 꺾인 채 더듬거렸다."], ["“Upon my word, the money’s there, hidden.”", "\"맹세코, 돈은 거기 있소, 감춰 두었소.\""]], [["In an instant an extraordinary sense of personal dignity showed itself in the little man’s face.", "순식간에 비상한 자존감이 작은 사내의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."]], [["“What next?”", "\"그래서 어쩌라는 거요?\""], ["he asked ironically.", "그가 비꼬듯 물었다."], ["“For shame!”", "\"수치스러운 줄 아시오!\""], ["and he spat on the floor.", "그러고는 바닥에 침을 뱉었다."], ["Pan Vrublevsky spat too.", "브루블레프스키 씨도 침을 뱉었다."]], [["“You do that, panie,”", "\"당신이 그렇게 구는 건, 여보시오,\""], ["said Mitya, recognizing with despair that all was over, “because you hope to make more out of Grushenka?", "미탸가 절망 속에 모든 것이 끝났음을 깨달으며 말했다. \"그루센카한테서 더 많이 우려낼 속셈이기 때문이지?"], ["You’re a couple of capons, that’s what you are!”", "당신네는 한 쌍의 거세된 수탉들이오, 그게 바로 당신네 꼴이오!\""]], [["“This is a mortal insult!”", "\"이건 죽도록 모욕적인 일이오!\""], ["The little Pole turned as red as a crab, and he went out of the room, briskly, as though unwilling to hear another word.", "작은 폴란드인이 게처럼 시뻘게져서는, 한 마디도 더 들을 수 없다는 듯 잰걸음으로 방을 나갔다."], ["Vrublevsky swung out after him, and Mitya followed, confused and crestfallen.", "브루블레프스키가 그 뒤를 따라 휭하니 나갔고, 미탸도 당황하여 풀이 죽은 채 따라 나섰다."], ["He was afraid of Grushenka, afraid that the pan would at once raise an outcry.", "그는 그루센카가 두려웠고, 폴란드 양반이 당장 고함을 질러 댈까 봐 두려웠다."], ["And so indeed he did.", "과연 그가 그렇게 했다."], ["The Pole walked into the room and threw himself in a theatrical attitude before Grushenka.", "폴란드인이 방 안으로 걸어 들어와 그루센카 앞에서 연극조의 자세를 취했다."]], [["“Pani Agrippina, I have received a mortal insult!”", "\"아그리피나 마님, 나는 죽도록 모욕당했소!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["But Grushenka suddenly lost all patience, as though they had wounded her in the tenderest spot.", "그러나 그루센카는 별안간 인내심을 잃었으니, 마치 그들이 그녀의 가장 여린 데를 찔러 상처라도 입힌 듯했다."]], [["“Speak Russian!", "\"러시아 말로 해요!"], ["Speak Russian!”", "러시아 말로 하라고요!\""], ["she cried, “not another word of Polish!", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"폴란드 말은 한 마디도 더 하지 말아요!"], ["You used to talk Russian.", "전에는 러시아 말을 했잖아요."], ["You can’t have forgotten it in five years.”", "오 년 사이에 잊어버렸을 리가 없잖아요.\""]], [["She was red with passion.", "그녀가 격정으로 시뻘게져 있었다."]], [["“Pani Agrippina—”", "\"아그리피나 마님—\""]], [["“My name’s Agrafena, Grushenka, speak Russian or I won’t listen!”", "\"내 이름은 아그라페나, 그루센카예요, 러시아 말로 하든지 아니면 듣지 않겠어요!\""]], [["The Pole gasped with offended dignity, and quickly and pompously delivered himself in broken Russian:", "폴란드인이 모욕당한 자존심에 숨을 헐떡이더니, 서툰 러시아어로 빠르고 거드름스럽게 떠벌렸다."]], [["“Pani Agrafena, I came here to forget the past and forgive it, to forget all that has happened till to-day—”", "\"아그라페나 마님, 나는 지난날을 잊고 그것을 용서하려고, 오늘까지 일어난 모든 일을 잊으려고 여기로 온 것이오—\""]], [["“Forgive?", "\"용서한다고요?"], ["Came here to forgive me?”", "나를 용서하러 여기 왔다고요?\""], ["Grushenka cut him short, jumping up from her seat.", "그루센카가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 그의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“Just so, pani, I’m not pusillanimous, I’m magnanimous.", "\"바로 그렇소, 마님, 나는 옹졸한 사람이 아니라 도량이 넓은 사람이오."], ["But I was astounded when I saw your lovers.", "허나 당신의 정부(情夫)들을 보고 나는 아연실색했소."], ["Pan Mitya offered me three thousand, in the other room to depart.", "미탸 양반이 옆방에서 내게 삼천 루블을 줄 테니 떠나라고 하더이다."], ["I spat in the pan’s face.”", "나는 그 양반 면상에 침을 뱉었소.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["He offered you money for me?”", "이이가 나 때문에 당신한테 돈을 줬다고요?\""], ["cried Grushenka, hysterically.", "그루센카가 히스테릭하게 외쳤다. \"사실이에요, 미탸?"], ["“Is it true, Mitya?", "어떻게 감히 그래요?"], ["How dare you? Am I for sale?”", "내가 팔아 치울 물건이라도 돼요?\""]], [["“Panie, panie!”", "\"여보시오, 여보시오!\""], ["yelled Mitya, “she’s pure and shining, and I have never been her lover!", "미탸가 소리쳤다. \"그녀는 순결하고 빛나는 사람이오, 나는 한 번도 그녀의 정부였던 적이 없소!"], ["That’s a lie....”", "그건 거짓말이오….\""]], [["“How dare you defend me to him?”", "\"어떻게 감히 저자 앞에서 날 두둔하는 거예요?\""], ["shrieked Grushenka.", "그루센카가 비명을 질렀다."], ["“It wasn’t virtue kept me pure, and it wasn’t that I was afraid of Kuzma, but that I might hold up my head when I met him, and tell him he’s a scoundrel.", "\"내가 순결을 지킨 건 미덕 때문도 아니었고, 쿠지마가 두려워서도 아니었어요, 그저 저자를 만났을 때 고개를 빳빳이 들고 저자더러 비열한 놈이라고 말해 주고 싶어서였다고요."], ["And he did actually refuse the money?”", "그래, 저자가 정말로 그 돈을 거절했다고요?\""]], [["“He took it!", "\"받았소!"], ["He took it!”", "받았다고요!\""], ["cried Mitya; “only he wanted to get the whole three thousand at once, and I could only give him seven hundred straight off.”", "미탸가 외쳤다. \"다만 삼천을 한꺼번에 다 받으려 했을 뿐이오, 그런데 나는 당장은 칠백밖에 줄 수가 없었던 거요.\""]], [["“I see: he heard I had money, and came here to marry me!”", "\"알겠어요. 저자가 나한테 돈이 있다는 소릴 듣고, 나랑 결혼하려고 여기 온 거였군요!\""]], [["“Pani Agrippina!”", "\"아그리피나 마님!\""], ["cried the little Pole.", "작은 폴란드인이 외쳤다."], ["“I’m—a knight, I’m—a nobleman, and not a lajdak.", "\"나는—기사요, 나는—귀족이지 비열한 놈이 아니오."], ["I came here to make you my wife and I find you a different woman, perverse and shameless.”", "나는 당신을 아내로 삼으려고 여기 왔건만, 보니 당신은 영 딴사람이 되어 있구려, 비뚤어지고 부끄러움을 모르는 여자로 말이오.\""]], [["“Oh, go back where you came from!", "\"오, 당신 온 데로 도로 돌아가요!"], ["I’ll tell them to turn you out and you’ll be turned out,”", "사람을 시켜 당신을 쫓아내라고 할 테니, 쫓겨나게 될 거예요.\""], ["cried Grushenka, furious.", "그루센카가 길길이 날뛰며 외쳤다."], ["“I’ve been a fool, a fool, to have been miserable these five years!", "\"내가 바보였어요, 바보였다고요, 오 년 동안이나 비참해하다니!"], ["And it wasn’t for his sake, it was my anger made me miserable.", "그것도 저자 때문이 아니라, 내 분노 때문에 비참했던 거예요."], ["And this isn’t he at all!", "그런데 이건 저 사람이 전혀 아니에요!"], ["Was he like this?", "저 사람이 이런 모습이었던가요?"], ["It might be his father!", "저 사람 아버지뻘이나 되겠네!"], ["Where did you get your wig from?", "그 가발은 어디서 구해 왔어요?"], ["He was a falcon, but this is a gander.", "그 사람은 매였는데, 이건 수거위잖아요."], ["He used to laugh and sing to me....", "그 사람은 나한테 웃고 노래를 불러 줬는데…."], ["And I’ve been crying for five years, damned fool, abject, shameless I was!”", "그런데 나는 오 년 동안이나 울었으니, 빌어먹을 바보 같으니, 비굴하고 부끄러움도 모르는 계집이었어!\""]], [["She sank back in her low chair and hid her face in her hands.", "그녀는 나지막한 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["At that instant the chorus of Mokroe began singing in the room on the left—a rollicking dance song.", "바로 그 순간, 왼편 방에서 모크로예의 합창대가 노래를 부르기 시작했으니—흥겹게 들썩이는 춤 노래였다."]], [["“A regular Sodom!”", "\"완전히 소돔이로구먼!\""], ["Vrublevsky roared suddenly.", "브루블레프스키가 별안간 고함쳤다."], ["“Landlord, send the shameless hussies away!”", "\"주인장, 저 부끄러움도 모르는 계집들을 내보내시오!\""]], [["The landlord, who had been for some time past inquisitively peeping in at the door, hearing shouts and guessing that his guests were quarreling, at once entered the room.", "여관 주인은 한동안 문틈으로 궁금한 듯 안을 엿보고 있었는데, 고함 소리를 듣고 손님들이 다투고 있음을 짐작하고는 곧장 방 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“What are you shouting for?", "\"뭣 때문에 고래고래 소리를 지르는 거요?"], ["D’you want to split your throat?”", "목구멍이라도 찢어 놓을 작정이오?\""], ["he said, addressing Vrublevsky, with surprising rudeness.", "그가 놀라우리만치 무례하게 브루블레프스키를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Animal!”", "\"짐승 같은 놈!\""], ["bellowed Pan Vrublevsky.", "브루블레프스키 씨가 으르렁댔다."]], [["“Animal? And what sort of cards were you playing with just now?", "\"짐승 같은 놈이라고?"], ["I gave you a pack and you hid it.", "그러는 당신은 방금 무슨 패로 노름을 한 거요?"], ["You played with marked cards!", "내가 새 패 한 벌을 줬는데 당신이 그걸 감췄잖소."], ["I could send you to Siberia for playing with false cards, d’you know that, for it’s just the same as false banknotes....”", "표시해 둔 패로 노름을 한 거요! 가짜 패로 노름한 죄로 당신을 시베리아로 보낼 수도 있소, 그거 아시오, 그건 가짜 지폐랑 똑같은 거니까….\""]], [["And going up to the sofa he thrust his fingers between the sofa back and the cushion, and pulled out an unopened pack of cards.", "그러고는 소파로 다가가 등받이와 방석 사이에 손가락을 찔러 넣어 봉하지 않은 패 한 벌을 끄집어냈다."]], [["“Here’s my pack unopened!”", "\"여기 내 패가 봉한 그대로 있소!\""]], [["He held it up and showed it to all in the room.", "그가 그것을 치켜들어 방 안의 모두에게 내보였다."], ["“From where I stood I saw him slip my pack away, and put his in place of it—you’re a cheat and not a gentleman!”", "\"내가 서 있던 데서, 저자가 내 패를 슬쩍 빼돌리고 그 자리에 제 패를 끼워 넣는 걸 봤단 말이오—당신은 사기꾼이지 신사가 아니오!\""]], [["“And I twice saw the pan change a card!”", "\"나도 이 양반이 패를 바꿔치는 걸 두 번이나 봤어요!\""], ["cried Kalganov.", "칼가노프가 외쳤다."]], [["“How shameful!", "\"부끄러워라!"], ["How shameful!”", "부끄러워라!\""], ["exclaimed Grushenka, clasping her hands, and blushing for genuine shame.", "그루센카가 두 손을 마주 잡고 진정 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“Good Lord, he’s come to that!”", "\"맙소사, 저 사람이 저런 지경까지 됐다니!\""]], [["“I thought so, too!”", "\"나도 그렇게 생각했소!\""], ["said Mitya.", "미탸가 말했다."], ["But before he had uttered the words, Vrublevsky, with a confused and infuriated face, shook his fist at Grushenka, shouting:", "그러나 그가 그 말을 채 내뱉기도 전에, 브루블레프스키가 당황하고 격분한 얼굴로 그루센카에게 주먹을 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다."]], [["“You low harlot!”", "\"이 천한 갈보 같으니!\""]], [["Mitya flew at him at once, clutched him in both hands, lifted him in the air, and in one instant had carried him into the room on the right, from which they had just come.", "미탸가 곧장 그에게 달려들어 두 손으로 그를 움켜쥐고는 번쩍 들어 올려, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 방금 둘이 나왔던 오른편 방으로 그를 옮겨다 놓았다."]], [["“I’ve laid him on the floor, there,”", "\"저자를 저쪽 바닥에 눕혀 놨소.\""], ["he announced, returning at once, gasping with excitement.", "그가 흥분에 헐떡이며 곧장 돌아와 알렸다."], ["“He’s struggling, the scoundrel!", "\"버둥거리고 있소, 저 비열한 놈이!"], ["But he won’t come back, no fear of that!...”", "하지만 다시 나오진 못할 거요, 그건 염려 마시오!…\""]], [["He closed one half of the folding doors, and holding the other ajar called out to the little Pole:", "그는 여닫이문의 한쪽을 닫고, 다른 한쪽을 빠끔히 연 채 작은 폴란드인을 향해 소리쳤다."]], [["“Most illustrious, will you be pleased to retire as well?”", "\"더없이 고명하신 분, 당신도 물러가 주시겠소?\""]], [["“My dear Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치 나리,\""], ["said Trifon Borissovitch, “make them give you back the money you lost.", "트리폰 보리소비치가 말했다. \"잃으신 돈을 도로 받아 내십시오."], ["It’s as good as stolen from you.”", "그건 나리한테서 훔쳐 간 거나 다름없습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t want my fifty roubles back,”", "\"내 오십 루블은 도로 받고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["Kalganov declared suddenly.", "칼가노프가 별안간 선언했다."]], [["“I don’t want my two hundred, either,”", "\"내 이백도 받고 싶지 않소.\""], ["cried Mitya, “I wouldn’t take it for anything!", "미탸가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 받지 않겠소!"], ["Let him keep it as a consolation.”", "위로 삼아 그자가 갖게 놔둡시다.\""]], [["“Bravo, Mitya!", "\"잘했어요, 미탸!"], ["You’re a trump, Mitya!”", "당신은 정말 멋진 사람이에요, 미탸!\""], ["cried Grushenka, and there was a note of fierce anger in the exclamation.", "그루센카가 외쳤는데, 그 외침에는 사나운 분노의 가락이 서려 있었다."]], [["The little pan, crimson with fury but still mindful of his dignity, was making for the door, but he stopped short and said suddenly, addressing Grushenka:", "작은 양반은 분노로 시뻘게졌으면서도 여전히 제 위엄을 잊지 않은 채 문 쪽으로 향하다가, 별안간 멈춰 서더니 그루센카를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Pani, if you want to come with me, come.", "\"마님, 나와 함께 가고 싶으면 갑시다."], ["If not, good-by.”", "아니라면, 잘 있으시오.\""]], [["And swelling with indignation and importance he went to the door.", "그러고서 그는 분개와 거드름으로 잔뜩 부풀어 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["This was a man of character: he had so good an opinion of himself that after all that had passed, he still expected that she would marry him.", "이자는 줏대 있는 인간이었다. 그는 자기 자신을 어찌나 높이 여겼던지, 그 모든 일이 있고 난 뒤에도 여전히 그녀가 자기와 결혼하리라 기대하고 있었던 것이다."], ["Mitya slammed the door after him.", "미탸가 그의 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다."]], [["“Lock it,”", "\"잠가요.\" 칼가노프가 말했다."], ["said Kalganov.", "그러나 열쇠가 저쪽에서 찰칵 돌아갔으니, 그들이 안에서 문을 잠가 버린 것이었다. \"잘됐네요!\""], ["But the key clicked on the other side, they had locked it from within.", "그루센카가 매정하게 외쳤다. \"그래도 싸지!\""]], [["“That’s capital!” exclaimed Grushenka relentlessly.", "* 1772년: 제1차 폴란드 분할이 일어난 해로, 이후 폴란드는 러시아·프로이센·오스트리아에 의해 영토가 잠식되어 끝내 소멸했다."], ["“Serve them right!”", "브루블레프스키의 건배는 분할 이전의 온전한 폴란드를 기리는 것으로, 러시아에 대한 폴란드 민족주의자의 적개심을 드러낸다."]], [["# Chapter VIII. Delirium", "# Chapter VIII. Delirium"]], [["What followed was almost an orgy, a feast to which all were welcome.", "그 뒤에 이어진 것은 거의 광란의 잔치였으니, 누구든 환영받는 향연이었다."], ["Grushenka was the first to call for wine.", "술을 가장 먼저 청한 사람은 그루셴카였다."]], [["“I want to drink.", "\"마시고 싶어요."], ["I want to be quite drunk, as we were before.", "예전처럼 흠뻑 취하고 싶어요."], ["Do you remember, Mitya, do you remember how we made friends here last time!”", "기억나요, 미탸, 우리가 지난번에 여기서 얼마나 의기투합했는지 기억나요!\""]], [["Mitya himself was almost delirious, feeling that his happiness was at hand.", "미탸 자신도 거의 정신이 나가 있었으니, 자신의 행복이 손에 잡힐 듯 가까이 있음을 느끼고 있었다."], ["But Grushenka was continually sending him away from her.", "그러나 그루셴카는 끊임없이 그를 제 곁에서 멀리 내쫓았다."]], [["“Go and enjoy yourself.", "\"가서 즐겨요."], ["Tell them to dance, to make merry, ‘let the stove and cottage dance’; as we had it last time,”", "사람들한테 춤추라고, 흥겹게 놀라고 일러요, '난로도 오두막도 다 춤추게' 하라고요."], ["she kept exclaiming.", "지난번에 그랬던 것처럼요.\""], ["She was tremendously excited.", "그녀는 연신 이렇게 외쳐 댔다."], ["And Mitya hastened to obey her.", "그녀는 어마어마하게 들떠 있었다."], ["The chorus were in the next room.", "미탸는 서둘러 그녀의 말에 따랐다."], ["The room in which they had been sitting till that moment was too small, and was divided in two by cotton curtains, behind which was a huge bed with a puffy feather mattress and a pyramid of cotton pillows.", "합창대는 옆방에 있었다. 그때까지 그들이 앉아 있던 방은 너무 좁았는데, 무명천 커튼으로 둘로 나뉘어 있었고, 그 뒤에는 푹신한 깃털 매트리스와 무명 베개를 피라미드처럼 쌓아 올린 거대한 침대가 놓여 있었다."], ["In the four rooms for visitors there were beds.", "손님용으로 마련된 네 개의 방에는 침대가 갖춰져 있었다."], ["Grushenka settled herself just at the door.", "그루셴카는 바로 문가에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Mitya set an easy chair for her.", "미탸가 그녀에게 안락의자를 가져다주었다."], ["She had sat in the same place to watch the dancing and singing “the time before,” when they had made merry there.", "그녀는 \"지난번\"에 그들이 여기서 흥겹게 놀았을 때에도 춤과 노래를 구경하느라 바로 그 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["All the girls who had come had been there then; the Jewish band with fiddles and zithers had come, too, and at last the long expected cart had arrived with the wines and provisions.", "그때 왔던 처녀들이 모두 그 자리에 있었고, 바이올린과 치터를 든 유대인 악단도 와 있었으며, 마침내 그토록 고대하던, 술과 음식을 실은 짐수레가 당도했던 것이다."]], [["Mitya bustled about.", "미탸는 부산스레 돌아다녔다."], ["All sorts of people began coming into the room to look on, peasants and their women, who had been roused from sleep and attracted by the hopes of another marvelous entertainment such as they had enjoyed a month before.", "온갖 부류의 사람들이 구경하러 방 안으로 몰려들기 시작했으니, 잠에서 깨어난 농부들과 그 아낙들이었는데, 한 달 전에 누렸던 것 같은 또 한 번의 기막힌 잔치를 바라는 마음에 이끌려 온 것이었다."], ["Mitya remembered their faces, greeting and embracing every one he knew.", "미탸는 그들의 얼굴을 알아보고, 아는 사람마다 일일이 인사를 건네고 끌어안았다."], ["He uncorked bottles and poured out wine for every one who presented himself.", "그는 병마개를 따서 자기 앞에 나서는 사람마다 술을 따라 주었다."], ["Only the girls were very eager for the champagne.", "다만 샴페인을 몹시 탐낸 것은 처녀들뿐이었다."], ["The men preferred rum, brandy, and, above all, hot punch.", "사내들은 럼주와 브랜디, 무엇보다도 뜨거운 펀치를 더 좋아했다."], ["Mitya had chocolate made for all the girls, and ordered that three samovars should be kept boiling all night to provide tea and punch for everyone to help himself.", "미탸는 처녀들 모두에게 초콜릿을 만들어 주게 했고, 사모바르 세 개를 밤새도록 끓여 두어 누구든 차와 펀치를 마음껏 마실 수 있게 하라고 일렀다."]], [["An absurd chaotic confusion followed, but Mitya was in his natural element, and the more foolish it became, the more his spirits rose.", "터무니없고 어수선한 혼란이 뒤따랐으나, 미탸는 제 본바탕의 물 만난 격이었으니, 그 꼴이 어리석어질수록 그의 기분은 더욱 들떠 올랐다."], ["If the peasants had asked him for money at that moment, he would have pulled out his notes and given them away right and left.", "만약 그 순간 농부들이 그에게 돈을 달라고 했다면, 그는 지폐를 꺼내어 좌우로 마구 뿌려 주었을 것이다."], ["This was probably why the landlord, Trifon Borissovitch, kept hovering about Mitya to protect him.", "아마 그래서였을 것이니, 여관 주인 트리폰 보리소비치는 미탸를 지키느라 그의 주위를 줄곧 맴돌았다."], ["He seemed to have given up all idea of going to bed that night; but he drank little, only one glass of punch, and kept a sharp look-out on Mitya’s interests after his own fashion.", "그는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들 생각을 아예 접은 듯했으나, 정작 술은 거의 마시지 않아 펀치 한 잔만 들었을 뿐이고, 제 나름의 방식으로 미탸의 잇속에 날카롭게 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["He intervened in the nick of time, civilly and obsequiously persuading Mitya not to give away “cigars and Rhine wine,” and, above all, money to the peasants as he had done before.", "그는 적절한 때에 끼어들어, 공손하고 굽실거리는 태도로 미탸에게 예전처럼 \"시가와 라인산 포도주\"를, 무엇보다도 돈을 농부들에게 뿌리지 말라고 타일렀다."], ["He was very indignant, too, at the peasant girls drinking liqueur, and eating sweets.", "그는 또한 농가 처녀들이 리큐어를 마시고 사탕을 먹는 것에도 몹시 분개했다."]], [["“They’re a lousy lot, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"저것들은 하찮은 무리입니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’d give them a kick, every one of them, and they’d take it as an honor—that’s all they’re worth!”", "\"저런 것들은 죄다 발길로 한 번 걷어차 주면, 오히려 그걸 영광으로 여길 겁니다요. 딱 그만한 값어치밖에 안 되는 것들이죠!\""]], [["Mitya remembered Andrey again, and ordered punch to be sent out to him.", "미탸는 다시금 안드레이를 떠올리고, 그에게 펀치를 내보내라고 일렀다."], ["“I was rude to him just now,”", "\"방금 그자한테 무례했어.\""], ["he repeated with a sinking, softened voice.", "그는 가라앉고 누그러진 목소리로 거듭 중얼거렸다."], ["Kalganov did not want to drink, and at first did not care for the girls’ singing; but after he had drunk a couple of glasses of champagne he became extraordinarily lively, strolling about the room, laughing and praising the music and the songs, admiring every one and everything.", "칼가노프는 술을 마시고 싶어 하지 않았고, 처음에는 처녀들의 노래에도 관심이 없었으나, 샴페인을 두어 잔 들이켜고 나자 유난히 활기가 돌아, 방 안을 거닐며 웃고 음악과 노래를 칭찬하고 모든 사람과 모든 것을 감탄하며 보았다."], ["Maximov, blissfully drunk, never left his side.", "더할 나위 없이 행복하게 취한 막시모프는 그의 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았다."], ["Grushenka, too, was beginning to get drunk.", "그루셴카 역시 취기가 오르기 시작했다."], ["Pointing to Kalganov, she said to Mitya:", "그녀는 칼가노프를 가리키며 미탸에게 말했다."]], [["“What a dear, charming boy he is!”", "\"저 애 참 귀엽고 사랑스러운 도련님이지요!\""]], [["And Mitya, delighted, ran to kiss Kalganov and Maximov.", "그러자 미탸는 기뻐하며 달려가 칼가노프와 막시모프에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Oh, great were his hopes!", "오, 그의 희망은 크기도 했다!"], ["She had said nothing yet, and seemed, indeed, purposely to refrain from speaking.", "그녀는 아직 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 실로 일부러 입을 다물고 있는 듯했다."], ["But she looked at him from time to time with caressing and passionate eyes.", "그러나 이따금 그녀는 어루만지는 듯한, 정열에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["At last she suddenly gripped his hand and drew him vigorously to her.", "마침내 그녀는 별안간 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 힘껏 제 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."], ["She was sitting at the moment in the low chair by the door.", "그 순간 그녀는 문가의 낮은 의자에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“How was it you came just now, eh?", "\"방금 어떻게 들어왔어요, 응?"], ["Have you walked in!...", "걸어 들어왔어요!…"], ["I was frightened.", "난 무서웠어요."], ["So you wanted to give me up to him, did you?", "그래서 날 저 사람한테 넘겨주려고 했던 거예요, 그랬어요?"], ["Did you really want to?”", "정말로 그러고 싶었어요?\""]], [["“I didn’t want to spoil your happiness!”", "\"당신의 행복을 망치고 싶지 않았어!\""], ["Mitya faltered blissfully.", "미탸는 행복에 겨워 더듬더듬 말했다."], ["But she did not need his answer.", "그러나 그녀에게 그의 대답 따위는 필요치 않았다."]], [["“Well, go and enjoy yourself ...”", "\"자, 가서 즐겨요…\""], ["she sent him away once more.", "그녀는 다시 한번 그를 내쫓았다."], ["“Don’t cry, I’ll call you back again.”", "\"울지 말아요, 다시 불러 줄 테니까.\""]], [["He would run away, and she listened to the singing and looked at the dancing, though her eyes followed him wherever he went.", "그는 달려가곤 했고, 그녀는 노래에 귀를 기울이고 춤을 바라보았으나, 그녀의 눈은 그가 어디로 가든 그를 좇았다."], ["But in another quarter of an hour she would call him once more and again he would run back to her.", "그러나 다시 십오 분쯤 지나면 그녀는 또 한 번 그를 불렀고, 그러면 그는 또다시 그녀에게 달려가곤 했다."]], [["“Come, sit beside me, tell me, how did you hear about me, and my coming here yesterday?", "\"이리 와요, 내 곁에 앉아요. 말해 봐요, 어떻게 내 얘기를 들었어요, 어제 내가 여기 온 걸 말이에요?"], ["From whom did you first hear it?”", "누구한테서 처음 들었어요?\""]], [["And Mitya began telling her all about it, disconnectedly, incoherently, feverishly.", "미탸는 두서없이, 종잡을 수 없이, 열에 들뜬 채 그 모든 이야기를 그녀에게 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["He spoke strangely, often frowning, and stopping abruptly.", "그는 이상하게 이야기했으니, 자주 이맛살을 찌푸리고 불쑥불쑥 말을 끊었다."]], [["“What are you frowning at?”", "\"뭘 그렇게 찡그려요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Nothing....", "\"아무것도 아니야…"], ["I left a man ill there.", "거기 한 사람을 앓아눕게 하고 왔어."], ["I’d give ten years of my life for him to get well, to know he was all right!”", "그자가 다 나아서 무사하다는 걸 알 수만 있다면 내 수명을 십 년이라도 내놓겠어!\""]], [["“Well, never mind him, if he’s ill.", "\"그자가 앓건 말건 신경 쓰지 말아요."], ["So you meant to shoot yourself to-morrow!", "그러니까 당신은 내일 자기를 쏘려고 했단 말이지요!"], ["What a silly boy! What for?", "어쩌면 그렇게 어리석은 도련님일까!"], ["I like such reckless fellows as you,”", "무엇 때문에요? 난 당신처럼 막무가내인 사람이 좋아요.\""], ["she lisped, with a rather halting tongue.", "그녀는 다소 꼬부라진 혀로 혀짤배기소리를 했다."], ["“So you would go any length for me, eh?", "\"그러니까 나를 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 하겠다는 거지요, 응?"], ["Did you really mean to shoot yourself to-morrow, you stupid?", "정말로 내일 자기를 쏘려고 했어요, 이 바보 같은 사람아?"], ["No, wait a little.", "아니, 잠깐만 기다려요."], ["To-morrow I may have something to say to you....", "내일은 당신한테 할 말이 있을지도 몰라요…"], ["I won’t say it to-day, but to-morrow.", "오늘은 안 하고 내일 할 거예요."], ["You’d like it to be to-day?", "당신은 오늘 듣고 싶지요?"], ["No, I don’t want to to-day.", "아니에요, 난 오늘은 싫어요."], ["Come, go along now, go and amuse yourself.”", "자, 이제 가요, 가서 즐겨요.\""]], [["Once, however, she called him, as it were, puzzled and uneasy.", "그러나 한번은 그녀가 그를 불렀는데, 어쩐지 어리둥절하고 불안해 보였다."]], [["“Why are you sad?", "\"왜 슬퍼해요?"], ["I see you’re sad....", "슬퍼 보여요…"], ["Yes, I see it,”", "그래요, 보여요.\""], ["she added, looking intently into his eyes.", "그녀는 그의 눈을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 덧붙였다."], ["“Though you keep kissing the peasants and shouting, I see something.", "\"당신이 자꾸 농부들한테 입을 맞추고 소리를 질러 대지만, 난 뭔가가 보여요."], ["No, be merry.", "아니, 즐거워해요."], ["I’m merry; you be merry, too....", "난 즐거우니까, 당신도 즐거워해야 해요…"], ["I love somebody here.", "난 여기서 어떤 사람을 사랑해요."], ["Guess who it is.", "그게 누군지 알아맞혀 봐요."], ["Ah, look, my boy has fallen asleep, poor dear, he’s drunk.”", "아, 봐요, 우리 도련님이 잠들었네, 가엾기도 하지, 취했어요.\""]], [["She meant Kalganov.", "그녀가 가리킨 것은 칼가노프였다."], ["He was, in fact, drunk, and had dropped asleep for a moment, sitting on the sofa.", "그는 실제로 취해 소파에 앉은 채 잠시 잠이 들었던 것이다."], ["But he was not merely drowsy from drink; he felt suddenly dejected, or, as he said, “bored.”", "그러나 그는 단지 술기운에 졸린 것만이 아니었으니, 갑자기 의기소침해진, 그 자신의 말로는 \"따분한\""], ["He was intensely depressed by the girls’ songs, which, as the drinking went on, gradually became coarse and more reckless.", "기분이 들었던 것이다. 그는 처녀들의 노래에 몹시 우울해졌는데, 그 노래는 술판이 무르익을수록 점점 더 천박하고 무분별해졌다."], ["And the dances were as bad.", "춤도 그에 못지않게 형편없었다."], ["Two girls dressed up as bears, and a lively girl, called Stepanida, with a stick in her hand, acted the part of keeper, and began to “show them.”", "처녀 둘이 곰으로 분장하고, 스테파니다라는 활달한 처녀가 손에 막대기를 들고 곰 부리는 사람 역을 맡아 \"곰들을 부리기\" 시작했다."]], [["“Look alive, Marya, or you’ll get the stick!”", "\"기운차게 굴어, 마리야, 안 그러면 막대기로 한 대 맞을 줄 알아!\""]], [["The bears rolled on the ground at last in the most unseemly fashion, amid roars of laughter from the closely-packed crowd of men and women.", "마침내 곰들은 빽빽이 들어찬 남녀 무리의 폭소 속에서 더없이 꼴사나운 모양으로 바닥을 뒹굴었다."]], [["“Well, let them! Let them!”", "\"자, 내버려 둬요!"], ["said Grushenka sententiously, with an ecstatic expression on her face.", "내버려 두라니까!\" 그루셴카는 황홀한 표정을 얼굴에 담은 채 격언이라도 읊듯 말했다."], ["“When they do get a day to enjoy themselves, why shouldn’t folks be happy?”", "\"저들도 하루쯤 즐길 날을 얻었는데, 사람들이 행복해하면 안 될 게 뭐예요?\""]], [["Kalganov looked as though he had been besmirched with dirt.", "칼가노프는 마치 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["“It’s swinish, all this peasant foolery,”", "\"이런 농부들의 짓거리는 죄다 돼지 같아.\""], ["he murmured, moving away; “it’s the game they play when it’s light all night in summer.”", "그는 자리를 옮기며 중얼거렸다. \"여름에 밤새도록 환할 때나 하는 놀음이지.\""]], [["He particularly disliked one “new” song to a jaunty dance-tune.", "그가 특히 싫어한 것은 경쾌한 춤곡에 맞춘 어느 \"새\""], ["It described how a gentleman came and tried his luck with the girls, to see whether they would love him:", "노래였다. 그 노래는 어떤 나리가 와서 처녀들이 자기를 사랑할지 어떨지 운을 떼어 보는 내용이었다."]], [["The master came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?", "나리가 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?"]], [["But the girls could not love the master:", "그러나 처녀들은 나리를 사랑할 수 없었으니."]], [["He would beat me cruelly And such love won’t do for me.", "저이는 날 모질게 때릴 테니 그런 사랑은 내겐 싫어라."]], [["Then a gypsy comes along and he, too, tries:", "그다음에는 집시가 와서 그 역시 떠보았다."]], [["The gypsy came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?", "집시가 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?"]], [["But they couldn’t love the gypsy either:", "그러나 처녀들은 집시도 사랑할 수 없었으니."]], [["He would be a thief, I fear, And would cause me many a tear.", "저이는 도둑이 될까 두려워 내게 눈물깨나 흘리게 할 테지."]], [["And many more men come to try their luck, among them a soldier:", "그리고 더 많은 사내들이 운을 떼어 보러 왔는데, 그중에는 군인도 있었다."]], [["The soldier came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?", "군인이 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?"]], [["But the soldier is rejected with contempt, in two indecent lines, sung with absolute frankness and producing a furore in the audience.", "그러나 군인은 외설스러운 두 행의 노랫말로 경멸 속에 퇴짜를 맞았는데, 그 노랫말은 거리낌 없이 적나라하게 불려 청중을 열광시켰다."], ["The song ends with a merchant:", "노래는 상인으로 끝을 맺는다."]], [["The merchant came to try the girls: Would they love him, would they not?", "상인이 와서 처녀들을 떠보네. 나를 사랑할까, 안 할까?"]], [["And it appears that he wins their love because:", "그리고 그가 처녀들의 사랑을 얻는 것으로 드러나는데, 그 까닭인즉."]], [["The merchant will make gold for me And his queen I’ll gladly be.", "상인은 내게 금을 벌어다 주리니 나는 기꺼이 그의 여왕이 되리라."]], [["Kalganov was positively indignant.", "칼가노프는 단단히 분개했다."]], [["“That’s just a song of yesterday,”", "\"저런 건 그저 어제 만든 노래일 뿐이야.\""], ["he said aloud.", "그가 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“Who writes such things for them?", "\"누가 저들을 위해 저런 걸 짓는담?"], ["They might just as well have had a railwayman or a Jew come to try his luck with the girls; they’d have carried all before them.”", "철도 인부나 유대인을 불러내어 처녀들에게 운을 떼게 했어도 매한가지였을걸. 그자들이 죄다 휩쓸어 갔겠지.\""]], [["And, almost as though it were a personal affront, he declared, on the spot, that he was bored, sat down on the sofa and immediately fell asleep.", "그러고는 마치 개인적인 모욕이라도 당한 듯, 그는 그 자리에서 따분하다고 선언하더니, 소파에 앉아 이내 잠들어 버렸다."], ["His pretty little face looked rather pale, as it fell back on the sofa cushion.", "그의 예쁘장한 작은 얼굴은 소파 쿠션에 젖혀지자 다소 핼쑥해 보였다."]], [["“Look how pretty he is,”", "\"이것 봐요, 얼마나 예쁜지.\""], ["said Grushenka, taking Mitya up to him.", "그루셴카가 미탸를 그에게 데려가며 말했다."], ["“I was combing his hair just now; his hair’s like flax, and so thick....”", "\"방금 내가 이 애 머리를 빗겨 줬는데, 머리칼이 아마 실 같고 어찌나 숱이 많은지…\""]], [["And, bending over him tenderly, she kissed his forehead.", "그러고는 다정하게 그에게 몸을 굽혀 그의 이마에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Kalganov instantly opened his eyes, looked at her, stood up, and with the most anxious air inquired where was Maximov?", "칼가노프는 그 즉시 눈을 뜨고 그녀를 바라보더니, 일어나 더없이 근심스러운 표정으로 막시모프가 어디 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“So that’s who it is you want.”", "\"그러니까 당신이 찾는 게 바로 그 사람이로군요.\""], ["Grushenka laughed.", "그루셴카가 웃었다."], ["“Stay with me a minute.", "\"잠깐만 내 곁에 있어요."], ["Mitya, run and find his Maximov.”", "미탸, 가서 이 애의 막시모프를 찾아 줘요.\""]], [["Maximov, it appeared, could not tear himself away from the girls, only running away from time to time to pour himself out a glass of liqueur.", "알고 보니 막시모프는 처녀들 곁을 도무지 떠나지 못하고, 이따금씩만 빠져나와 리큐어를 한 잔씩 따라 마실 뿐이었다."], ["He had drunk two cups of chocolate.", "그는 초콜릿을 두 잔이나 마셨다."], ["His face was red, and his nose was crimson; his eyes were moist and mawkishly sweet.", "그의 얼굴은 붉었고, 코는 진홍빛이었으며, 눈은 축축하니 느끼하리만치 달콤했다."], ["He ran up and announced that he was going to dance the “sabotière.”", "그는 달려와 자기가 \"사부티에르\"를 추겠노라고 알렸다."]], [["“They taught me all those well-bred, aristocratic dances when I was little....”", "\"어렸을 때 그 점잖고 귀족적인 춤들을 죄다 배웠지요…\""]], [["“Go, go with him, Mitya, and I’ll watch from here how he dances,”", "\"가요, 저이랑 같이 가요, 미탸, 난 여기서 저이가 춤추는 걸 구경할 테니.\""], ["said Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 말했다."]], [["“No, no, I’m coming to look on, too,”", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 나도 구경하러 갈래요.\""], ["exclaimed Kalganov, brushing aside in the most naïve way Grushenka’s offer to sit with him.", "칼가노프가 곁에 앉아 있으라는 그루셴카의 권유를 더없이 천진하게 뿌리치며 외쳤다."], ["They all went to look on.", "모두들 구경하러 갔다."], ["Maximov danced his dance.", "막시모프는 자기 춤을 추었다."], ["But it roused no great admiration in any one but Mitya.", "그러나 그것은 미탸 외에는 아무에게서도 큰 감탄을 자아내지 못했다."], ["It consisted of nothing but skipping and hopping, kicking up the feet, and at every skip Maximov slapped the upturned sole of his foot.", "그것은 그저 깡충깡충 뛰고 발을 차올리는 것이 전부였으니, 막시모프는 한 번씩 뛸 때마다 위로 젖힌 발바닥을 철썩철썩 손바닥으로 쳤다."], ["Kalganov did not like it at all, but Mitya kissed the dancer.", "칼가노프는 그것을 전혀 마음에 들어 하지 않았으나, 미탸는 그 춤꾼에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Thanks.", "\"고맙소."], ["You’re tired perhaps?", "혹시 지치셨소?"], ["What are you looking for here?", "여기서 뭘 찾고 있소?"], ["Would you like some sweets?", "사탕이라도 드릴까?"], ["A cigar, perhaps?”", "시가는 어떻소?\""]], [["“A cigarette.”", "\"담배 한 대 주시오.\""]], [["“Don’t you want a drink?”", "\"술은 안 드시겠소?\""]], [["“I’ll just have a liqueur....", "\"리큐어나 한 잔 하지요…"], ["Have you any chocolates?”", "초콜릿은 좀 있소?\""]], [["“Yes, there’s a heap of them on the table there.", "\"그럼, 저기 탁자 위에 잔뜩 쌓여 있소."], ["Choose one, my dear soul!”", "하나 고르시오, 이 친구!\""]], [["“I like one with vanilla ...", "\"난 바닐라가 든 게 좋소…"], ["for old people.", "노인네들한테 어울리지."], ["He he!”", "헤헤!\""]], [["“No, brother, we’ve none of that special sort.”", "\"안 됐네, 이 사람아, 그런 특별한 건 없는데.\""]], [["“I say,”", "\"이보시오.\""], ["the old man bent down to whisper in Mitya’s ear.", "노인이 미탸의 귀에 대고 속삭이려고 몸을 굽혔다."], ["“That girl there, little Marya, he he!", "\"저기 저 처녀, 어린 마리야 말이오, 헤헤!"], ["How would it be if you were to help me make friends with her?”", "당신이 나하고 저 애를 친해지게 좀 주선해 주면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“So that’s what you’re after!", "\"그게 바로 당신 속셈이었구려!"], ["No, brother, that won’t do!”", "안 되오, 이 사람아, 그건 안 될 말이지!\""]], [["“I’d do no harm to any one,”", "\"난 누구한테도 해를 끼치지 않아.\""], ["Maximov muttered disconsolately.", "막시모프가 풀이 죽어 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, all right, all right.", "\"아, 좋소, 좋아."], ["They only come here to dance and sing, you know, brother.", "저 애들은 그저 춤추고 노래하러 여기 온 거요, 알겠소, 이 사람아."], ["But damn it all, wait a bit!...", "하지만 에라, 잠깐만 기다리시오!…"], ["Eat and drink and be merry, meanwhile.", "그동안 먹고 마시고 즐기시오."], ["Don’t you want money?”", "돈이 필요하진 않소?\""]], [["“Later on, perhaps,”", "\"나중에라면 몰라도.\""], ["smiled Maximov.", "막시모프가 미소 지었다."]], [["“All right, all right....”", "\"좋소, 좋아…\""]], [["Mitya’s head was burning.", "미탸의 머리는 불덩이 같았다."], ["He went outside to the wooden balcony which ran round the whole building on the inner side, overlooking the courtyard.", "그는 건물 안쪽 둘레를 따라 안마당을 굽어보며 빙 둘러쳐진 나무 발코니로 나갔다."], ["The fresh air revived him.", "신선한 공기가 그를 되살려 놓았다."], ["He stood alone in a dark corner, and suddenly clutched his head in both hands.", "그는 어두운 구석에 홀로 서 있다가, 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다."], ["His scattered thoughts came together; his sensations blended into a whole and threw a sudden light into his mind.", "흩어졌던 생각들이 한데 모이고, 갖가지 감각이 하나로 어우러져 그의 마음속에 갑작스러운 빛을 던졌다."], ["A fearful and terrible light!", "무섭고도 끔찍한 빛이었다!"], ["“If I’m to shoot myself, why not now?”", "\"기왕 자결할 거라면, 지금이라고 안 될 게 뭐냐?\""], ["passed through his mind.", "하는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다."], ["“Why not go for the pistols, bring them here, and here, in this dark dirty corner, make an end?”", "\"권총을 가지러 가서 이리로 가져와, 바로 여기, 이 어둡고 더러운 구석에서 끝장내 버리는 게 어떤가?\""], ["Almost a minute he stood, undecided.", "거의 일 분 동안 그는 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다."], ["A few hours earlier, when he had been dashing here, he was pursued by disgrace, by the theft he had committed, and that blood, that blood!...", "몇 시간 전, 이곳으로 달려올 때만 해도 그는 치욕에, 자기가 저지른 도둑질에, 그리고 그 피, 그 피에 쫓기고 있었다!…"], ["But yet it was easier for him then.", "그러나 그래도 그때는 그에게 더 수월했다."], ["Then everything was over: he had lost her, given her up.", "그때는 모든 것이 끝나 있었으니, 그는 그녀를 잃었고 그녀를 포기했던 것이다."], ["She was gone, for him—oh, then his death sentence had been easier for him; at least it had seemed necessary, inevitable, for what had he to stay on earth for?", "그녀는 그에게서 떠나 버렸으니—오, 그때는 그의 사형 선고가 그에게 더 수월했고, 적어도 그것이 필연적이고 불가피한 일로 여겨졌으니, 그가 이 땅에 더 머무를 이유가 무엇이었겠는가?"]], [["But now?", "그러나 지금은?"], ["Was it the same as then?", "지금이 그때와 같은가?"], ["Now one phantom, one terror at least was at an end: that first, rightful lover, that fateful figure had vanished, leaving no trace.", "지금은 적어도 하나의 망령, 하나의 공포만은 끝장이 났으니, 그 첫 번째 정당한 연인, 그 숙명적인 인물이 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸던 것이다."], ["The terrible phantom had turned into something so small, so comic; it had been carried into the bedroom and locked in.", "그 무서운 망령은 너무도 보잘것없고 너무도 우스꽝스러운 무언가로 변하여, 침실로 옮겨져 자물쇠가 채워졌다. 그것은 다시는 돌아오지 않을 것이다."], ["It would never return. She was ashamed, and from her eyes he could see now whom she loved.", "그녀는 부끄러워했고, 그녀의 눈에서 그는 이제 그녀가 누구를 사랑하는지 알 수 있었다."], ["Now he had everything to make life happy ...", "이제 그는 인생을 행복하게 할 모든 것을 갖추었건만…"], ["but he could not go on living, he could not; oh, damnation!", "그러나 그는 계속 살아갈 수 없었다, 그럴 수 없었다. 오, 빌어먹을!"], ["“O God!", "\"오, 하느님!"], ["restore to life the man I knocked down at the fence!", "제가 울타리에서 때려눕힌 그자를 되살려 주소서!"], ["Let this fearful cup pass from me!", "이 무서운 잔을 제게서 거두어 주소서!"], ["Lord, thou hast wrought miracles for such sinners as me!", "주여, 당신은 저 같은 죄인들을 위해서도 기적을 행하셨나이다!"], ["But what, what if the old man’s alive?", "하지만 만약, 만약 그 노인이 살아 있다면?"], ["Oh, then the shame of the other disgrace I would wipe away.", "오, 그렇다면 저 다른 치욕의 부끄러움은 제가 씻어 내겠나이다."], ["I would restore the stolen money.", "훔친 돈은 돌려놓겠나이다."], ["I’d give it back; I’d get it somehow....", "다시 갚겠나이다. 어떻게든 마련해서……"], ["No trace of that shame will remain except in my heart for ever!", "그 치욕의 흔적이라곤 영원토록 제 가슴속에만 남으리라!"], ["But no, no; oh, impossible cowardly dreams!", "하지만 아니, 아니야. 오, 가당찮고 비겁한 꿈이로다!"], ["Oh, damnation!”", "오, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["Yet there was a ray of light and hope in his darkness.", "그래도 그의 어둠 속에는 한 줄기 빛과 희망이 있었다."], ["He jumped up and ran back to the room—to her, to her, his queen for ever!", "그는 벌떡 일어나 방으로—그녀에게로, 그녀에게로, 영원한 그의 여왕에게로 달려 돌아갔다!"], ["Was not one moment of her love worth all the rest of life, even in the agonies of disgrace?", "그녀의 사랑이라면 단 한순간이라도, 비록 치욕의 고뇌 속에서일망정 남은 평생을 다 바칠 만한 값어치가 있지 않은가?"], ["This wild question clutched at his heart.", "이 거친 물음이 그의 가슴을 움켜쥐었다."], ["“To her, to her alone, to see her, to hear her, to think of nothing, to forget everything, if only for that night, for an hour, for a moment!”", "\"그녀에게로, 오직 그녀에게로, 그녀를 보고, 그녀의 목소리를 듣고, 아무것도 생각하지 않고, 모든 것을 잊는 거다, 단 하룻밤만이라도, 한 시간만이라도, 한순간만이라도!\""], ["Just as he turned from the balcony into the passage, he came upon the landlord, Trifon Borissovitch.", "발코니에서 통로로 막 돌아서던 참에, 그는 여관 주인 트리폰 보리소비치와 마주쳤다."], ["He thought he looked gloomy and worried, and fancied he had come to find him.", "미탸가 보기에 그는 침울하고 근심스러워 보였는데, 어쩐지 자기를 찾아온 듯싶었다."]], [["“What is it, Trifon Borissovitch?", "\"무슨 일이오, 트리폰 보리소비치?"], ["are you looking for me?”", "날 찾고 있었소?\""]], [["“No, sir.”", "\"아닙니다요, 나리.\""], ["The landlord seemed disconcerted.", "여관 주인은 당황한 듯했다."], ["“Why should I be looking for you?", "\"제가 왜 나리를 찾겠습니까요?"], ["Where have you been?”", "어디 계셨습니까요?\""]], [["“Why do you look so glum?", "\"왜 그렇게 시무룩하오?"], ["You’re not angry, are you?", "화난 건 아니겠지, 응?"], ["Wait a bit, you shall soon get to bed....", "잠깐만 기다리시오, 곧 잠자리에 들게 해 주겠소……"], ["What’s the time?”", "지금 몇 시요?\""]], [["“It’ll be three o’clock.", "\"세 시쯤 됐을 겁니다요."], ["Past three, it must be.”", "세 시는 넘었을 겁니다요.\""]], [["“We’ll leave off soon.", "\"우리도 곧 끝낼 거요."], ["We’ll leave off.”", "곧 끝내겠소.\""]], [["“Don’t mention it; it doesn’t matter.", "\"천만의 말씀입니다요. 괜찮습니다요."], ["Keep it up as long as you like....”", "마음껏 즐기십시오……\""]], [["“What’s the matter with him?”", "\"저 사람이 왜 저러지?\""], ["Mitya wondered for an instant, and he ran back to the room where the girls were dancing.", "미탸는 한순간 의아해하다가, 처녀들이 춤추고 있는 방으로 달려 돌아갔다."], ["But she was not there.", "그러나 그녀는 거기 없었다."], ["She was not in the blue room either; there was no one but Kalganov asleep on the sofa.", "푸른 방에도 그녀는 없었으니, 소파에 잠든 칼가노프 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["Mitya peeped behind the curtain—she was there.", "미탸는 커튼 뒤를 들여다보았다—그녀는 거기 있었다."], ["She was sitting in the corner, on a trunk.", "그녀는 구석에 놓인 궤짝 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["Bent forward, with her head and arms on the bed close by, she was crying bitterly, doing her utmost to stifle her sobs that she might not be heard.", "앞으로 몸을 숙이고 머리와 두 팔을 바로 옆 침대에 기댄 채, 그녀는 흐느낌 소리가 새어 나가 들키지 않도록 안간힘을 다해 억누르면서 서럽게 울고 있었다."], ["Seeing Mitya, she beckoned him to her, and when he ran to her, she grasped his hand tightly.", "미탸를 보자 그녀는 손짓해 그를 불렀고, 그가 그녀에게 달려가자 그녀는 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Mitya, Mitya, I loved him, you know.", "\"미탸, 미탸, 난 그이를 사랑했어요, 알지요."], ["How I have loved him these five years, all that time!", "이 다섯 해 동안, 그 세월 내내 내가 그이를 얼마나 사랑했는지!"], ["Did I love him or only my own anger? No, him, him!", "내가 그이를 사랑한 거였을까요, 아니면 그저 내 분노만 사랑한 걸까요?"], ["It’s a lie that it was my anger I loved and not him.", "아니, 그이를, 그이를 사랑했어요!"], ["Mitya, I was only seventeen then; he was so kind to me, so merry; he used to sing to me....", "내가 사랑한 게 그이가 아니라 내 분노였다는 건 거짓말이에요. 미탸, 그때 난 겨우 열일곱이었어요."], ["Or so it seemed to a silly girl like me....", "그이는 내게 그렇게 다정하고 그렇게 명랑했어요. 내게 노래를 불러 주곤 했어요……"], ["And now, O Lord, it’s not the same man.", "아니, 나처럼 어리석은 계집애한테 그렇게 보였던 것뿐이겠지요……"], ["Even his face is not the same; he’s different altogether.", "그런데 이제, 오, 주여, 그이는 그때 그 사람이 아니에요. 얼굴마저 그때 그 얼굴이 아니에요."], ["I shouldn’t have known him.", "아주 딴사람이 됐어요."], ["I drove here with Timofey, and all the way I was thinking how I should meet him, what I should say to him, how we should look at one another.", "알아보지도 못할 뻔했어요. 난 티모페이의 마차를 타고 여기로 오면서, 오는 내내 그이를 어떻게 만날지, 그이한테 무슨 말을 할지, 우리가 서로 어떻게 바라볼지 생각했어요."], ["My soul was faint, and all of a sudden it was just as though he had emptied a pail of dirty water over me.", "내 영혼은 까무러칠 듯했는데, 갑자기 그이가 더러운 물 한 통을 내게 끼얹은 것만 같았어요."], ["He talked to me like a schoolmaster, all so grave and learned; he met me so solemnly that I was struck dumb.", "그이는 마치 학교 선생처럼 내게 말했어요, 어찌나 근엄하고 학자연하던지요. 어찌나 거드름을 피우며 날 맞던지 난 입을 다물어 버렸어요."], ["I couldn’t get a word in.", "한마디도 끼워 넣을 수가 없었어요."], ["At first I thought he was ashamed to talk before his great big Pole.", "처음엔 그 덩치 큰 폴란드 사람 앞이라 말하기가 부끄러운가 보다 생각했어요."], ["I sat staring at him and wondering why I couldn’t say a word to him now.", "난 멍하니 그이를 바라보며 어째서 이제는 그이한테 한마디도 못 하는 걸까 의아해했어요."], ["It must have been his wife that ruined him; you know he threw me up to get married.", "분명 그이를 망쳐 놓은 건 그이 아내일 거예요. 알지요, 그이는 결혼하려고 날 버렸잖아요."], ["She must have changed him like that.", "그 여자가 그이를 저렇게 바꿔 놓은 게 틀림없어요."], ["Mitya, how shameful it is!", "미탸, 어쩌면 이렇게 부끄러울까요!"], ["Oh, Mitya, I’m ashamed, I’m ashamed for all my life.", "오, 미탸, 부끄러워요, 평생토록 부끄러워요."], ["Curse it, curse it, curse those five years!”", "빌어먹을, 빌어먹을, 그 다섯 해가 저주스러워요!\""]], [["And again she burst into tears, but clung tight to Mitya’s hand and did not let it go.", "그러고는 그녀는 다시 울음을 터뜨렸으나, 미탸의 손을 꼭 붙든 채 놓지 않았다."]], [["“Mitya, darling, stay, don’t go away.", "\"미탸, 사랑하는 사람, 가지 말고 있어요."], ["I want to say one word to you,”", "당신한테 한마디 하고 싶어요.\""], ["she whispered, and suddenly raised her face to him.", "그녀가 속삭이더니, 갑자기 그를 향해 얼굴을 들었다."], ["“Listen, tell me who it is I love?", "\"들어요, 말해 봐요, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 누구지요?"], ["I love one man here.", "난 여기서 한 사람을 사랑해요."], ["Who is that man?", "그 사람이 누구지요?"], ["That’s what you must tell me.”", "그걸 당신이 말해 줘야 해요.\""]], [["A smile lighted up her face that was swollen with weeping, and her eyes shone in the half darkness.", "울어서 부어오른 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 환하게 피어났고, 그녀의 눈은 어스름 속에서 빛났다."]], [["“A falcon flew in, and my heart sank.", "\"매 한 마리가 날아들자 내 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았어요."], ["‘Fool!", "'바보야!"], ["that’s the man you love!’ That was what my heart whispered to me at once.", "저 사람이 네가 사랑하는 사람이야!' 그게 그 순간 내 가슴이 내게 속삭인 말이었어요."], ["You came in and all grew bright.", "당신이 들어오자 온통 환해졌어요."], ["What’s he afraid of? I wondered.", "저이는 뭘 두려워하는 걸까, 난 의아했어요."], ["For you were frightened; you couldn’t speak.", "당신이 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 말도 못 하고 있었으니까요."], ["It’s not them he’s afraid of—could you be frightened of any one?", "저이가 두려워하는 건 저들이 아니야—당신이 누군가를 두려워할 리가 있어요?"], ["It’s me he’s afraid of, I thought, only me.", "저이가 두려워하는 건 나야, 오직 나뿐이야, 난 그렇게 생각했어요."], ["So Fenya told you, you little stupid, how I called to Alyosha out of the window that I’d loved Mityenka for one hour, and that I was going now to love ...", "그래, 페냐가 당신한테 일러바쳤군요, 이 바보 같은 양반아, 내가 창밖으로 알료샤한테 한 시간 동안 미텐카를 사랑했지만 이제 다른 사람을 사랑하러 간다고 외친 걸 말이에요."], ["another. Mitya, Mitya, how could I be such a fool as to think I could love any one after you?", "미탸, 미탸, 어떻게 내가 당신 다음으로 누군가를 사랑할 수 있다고 생각할 만큼 어리석을 수 있었을까요?"], ["Do you forgive me, Mitya?", "날 용서해 주겠어요, 미탸?"], ["Do you forgive me or not?", "날 용서해요, 안 해요?"], ["Do you love me?", "날 사랑해요?"], ["Do you love me?”", "날 사랑해요?\""], ["She jumped up and held him with both hands on his shoulders.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 두 손으로 그의 두 어깨를 붙들었다."], ["Mitya, dumb with rapture, gazed into her eyes, at her face, at her smile, and suddenly clasped her tightly in his arms and kissed her passionately.", "미탸는 환희에 차 말을 잃은 채 그녀의 눈을, 그녀의 얼굴을, 그녀의 미소를 응시하다가, 별안간 그녀를 두 팔로 꼭 끌어안고 정열적으로 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“You will forgive me for having tormented you?", "\"내가 당신을 괴롭힌 걸 용서해 주겠지요? 난 심술이 나서 당신들을 다 괴롭혔어요."], ["It was through spite I tormented you all.", "심술이 나서 그 노인을 미치게 만들었어요……"], ["It was for spite I drove the old man out of his mind....", "기억해요, 언젠가 당신이 우리 집에서 술을 마시다가 술잔을 깬 거?"], ["Do you remember how you drank at my house one day and broke the wine-glass?", "난 그걸 기억해 뒀다가 오늘 잔을 깨고 '내 더러운 마음을 위하여' 마셨어요."], ["I remembered that and I broke a glass to-day and drank ‘to my vile heart.’ Mitya, my falcon, why don’t you kiss me?", "미탸, 내 매여, 왜 내게 입을 맞추지 않아요? 한 번 입을 맞추더니, 이제는 물러나서 바라보기만 하고 귀만 기울이네요."], ["He kissed me once, and now he draws back and looks and listens. Why listen to me?", "왜 내 말에 귀를 기울여요?"], ["Kiss me, kiss me hard, that’s right.", "입을 맞춰요, 세게 입을 맞춰요, 그래요."], ["If you love, well, then, love!", "사랑한다면, 그럼, 사랑해요!"], ["I’ll be your slave now, your slave for the rest of my life.", "이제 난 당신의 노예가 될 거예요, 평생토록 당신의 노예가 될 거예요."], ["It’s sweet to be a slave. Kiss me!", "노예가 되는 건 달콤한 일이에요."], ["Beat me, ill-treat me, do what you will with me....", "입을 맞춰요! 날 때려요, 날 학대해요, 나한테 마음대로 해요……"], ["And I do deserve to suffer.", "난 정말 고통받아 마땅해요."], ["Stay, wait, afterwards, I won’t have that....”", "잠깐, 기다려요, 나중에, 난 그건 싫어요……\""], ["she suddenly thrust him away.", "그녀는 별안간 그를 밀쳐 냈다."], ["“Go along, Mitya, I’ll come and have some wine, I want to be drunk, I’m going to get drunk and dance; I must, I must!”", "\"가요, 미탸, 난 가서 술을 마실래요, 취하고 싶어요, 취해서 춤출 거예요. 그래야 해요, 그래야 한다고요!\""], ["She tore herself away from him and disappeared behind the curtain.", "그녀는 그에게서 몸을 뿌리치고 커튼 뒤로 사라졌다."], ["Mitya followed like a drunken man.", "미탸는 술 취한 사람처럼 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Yes, come what may—whatever may happen now, for one minute I’d give the whole world,” he thought.", "\"그래, 어떻게 되든—이제 무슨 일이 일어나든, 단 일 분을 위해서라면 온 세상이라도 내놓겠어.\""], ["Grushenka did, in fact, toss off a whole glass of champagne at one gulp, and became at once very tipsy.", "그는 생각했다. 그루셴카는 실제로 샴페인 한 잔을 단숨에 들이켜고는 곧장 몹시 취해 버렸다."], ["She sat down in the same chair as before, with a blissful smile on her face.", "그녀는 행복에 겨운 미소를 얼굴에 담은 채 아까와 같은 의자에 앉았다."], ["Her cheeks were glowing, her lips were burning, her flashing eyes were moist; there was passionate appeal in her eyes.", "그녀의 두 뺨은 달아올랐고, 입술은 타는 듯했으며, 번득이는 눈은 촉촉했으니, 그 눈에는 정열적인 호소가 담겨 있었다."], ["Even Kalganov felt a stir at the heart and went up to her.", "칼가노프조차 가슴이 술렁여 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Did you feel how I kissed you when you were asleep just now?”", "\"방금 당신이 잠들었을 때 내가 입 맞춘 거 느꼈어요?\""], ["she said thickly.", "그녀가 혀 꼬부라진 소리로 말했다."], ["“I’m drunk now, that’s what it is....", "\"난 지금 취했어요, 그게 문제예요……"], ["And aren’t you drunk?", "당신은 안 취했어요?"], ["And why isn’t Mitya drinking?", "그런데 미탸는 왜 술을 안 마셔요?"], ["Why don’t you drink, Mitya?", "왜 안 마셔요, 미탸?"], ["I’m drunk, and you don’t drink....”", "난 취했는데, 당신은 안 마시네요……\""]], [["“I am drunk!", "\"난 취했어!"], ["I’m drunk as it is ...", "이미 취했다고……"], ["drunk with you ...", "당신한테 취했어……"], ["and now I’ll be drunk with wine, too.”", "이제 술에도 취할 거야.\""]], [["He drank off another glass, and—he thought it strange himself—that glass made him completely drunk.", "그는 또 한 잔을 들이켰는데—스스로도 이상하게 여겼지만—그 한 잔이 그를 완전히 취하게 만들었다."], ["He was suddenly drunk, although till that moment he had been quite sober, he remembered that.", "그 순간까지만 해도 그는 멀쩡히 맨정신이었음을 그는 기억하고 있었건만, 그는 갑자기 취해 버렸다."], ["From that moment everything whirled about him, as though he were delirious.", "그 순간부터 모든 것이 그의 주위에서 빙빙 돌았으니, 마치 그가 헛것을 보는 것만 같았다."], ["He walked, laughed, talked to everybody, without knowing what he was doing.", "그는 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 걷고, 웃고, 누구에게나 말을 걸었다."], ["Only one persistent burning sensation made itself felt continually, “like a red-hot coal in his heart,”", "다만 한 가지 집요하게 타오르는 감각만이 끊임없이 느껴졌으니, 훗날 그의 말로는 \"가슴속의 시뻘건 숯덩이 같은\""], ["he said afterwards.", "것이었다."], ["He went up to her, sat beside her, gazed at her, listened to her....", "그는 그녀에게 다가가 곁에 앉아 그녀를 응시하고 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였다……"], ["She became very talkative, kept calling every one to her, and beckoned to different girls out of the chorus.", "그녀는 몹시 수다스러워져서, 누구든 자기에게 불러들이고, 합창대에서 이런저런 처녀들에게 손짓했다."], ["When the girl came up, she either kissed her, or made the sign of the cross over her.", "처녀가 다가오면 그녀는 그 처녀에게 입을 맞추거나 성호를 그어 주었다."], ["In another minute she might have cried.", "다시 일 분쯤 지나면 울음을 터뜨릴 것만 같았다."], ["She was greatly amused by the “little old man,” as she called Maximov.", "그녀는 자기가 \"꼬마 노인네\"라고 부르는 막시모프를 보고 무척 즐거워했다."], ["He ran up every minute to kiss her hands, “each little finger,”", "그는 끊임없이 달려와 그녀의 두 손에, \"손가락 하나하나에\""], ["and finally he danced another dance to an old song, which he sang himself.", "입을 맞추었고, 마침내 자기가 직접 부르는 옛 노래에 맞춰 또 한 곡 춤을 추었다."], ["He danced with special vigor to the refrain:", "그는 후렴 대목에서 특히 힘차게 춤추었다."]], [["The little pig says—umph! umph!", "새끼 돼지는 꿀꿀, 꿀꿀, 꿀꿀!"], ["umph! The little calf says—moo, moo, moo, The little duck says—quack, quack, quack, The little goose says—ga, ga, ga.", "송아지는 음매, 음매, 음매, 새끼 오리는 꽥꽥, 꽥꽥, 꽥꽥, 새끼 거위는 가가, 가가, 가가."], ["The hen goes strutting through the porch; Troo-roo-roo-roo-roo, she’ll say, Troo-roo-roo-roo-roo, she’ll say!", "암탉은 현관을 으스대며 거닐다가 꼬꼬댁 꼬꼬, 노래하리니, 꼬꼬댁 꼬꼬, 노래하리라!"]], [["“Give him something, Mitya,”", "\"저이한테 뭐라도 줘요, 미탸.\""], ["said Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 말했다."], ["“Give him a present, he’s poor, you know.", "\"저이한테 선물을 줘요, 가난한 사람이잖아요."], ["Ah, the poor, the insulted!...", "아, 가난한 사람들, 모욕받는 사람들!……"], ["Do you know, Mitya, I shall go into a nunnery.", "알아요, 미탸, 난 수녀원에 들어갈 거예요."], ["No, I really shall one day, Alyosha said something to me to-day that I shall remember all my life....", "아니, 정말로 언젠가 그럴 거예요, 알료샤가 오늘 내게 평생 잊지 못할 말을 했거든요……"], ["Yes....", "그래요……"], ["But to-day let us dance.", "하지만 오늘은 춤을 춰요."], ["To-morrow to the nunnery, but to-day we’ll dance.", "내일은 수녀원으로, 하지만 오늘은 춤을 추는 거예요."], ["I want to play to-day, good people, and what of it?", "난 오늘 놀고 싶어요, 선량한 분들이여, 그게 뭐 어때서요?"], ["God will forgive us.", "하느님이 우릴 용서해 주실 거예요."], ["If I were God, I’d forgive every one: ‘My dear sinners, from this day forth I forgive you.’ I’m going to beg forgiveness: ‘Forgive me, good people, a silly wench.’ I’m a beast, that’s what I am.", "내가 하느님이라면 모두를 용서할 거예요. '내 사랑하는 죄인들아, 오늘부터 너희를 용서하노라.'"], ["But I want to pray.", "난 용서를 빌 거예요."], ["I gave a little onion.", "'용서해 주세요, 선량한 분들이여, 어리석은 계집을.'"], ["Wicked as I’ve been, I want to pray.", "난 짐승이에요, 그래요. 하지만 난 기도하고 싶어요."], ["Mitya, let them dance, don’t stop them.", "난 양파 한 뿌리를 줬어요. 내가 아무리 못된 짓을 했어도, 난 기도하고 싶어요."], ["Every one in the world is good.", "미탸, 사람들이 춤추게 둬요, 막지 말아요."], ["Every one—even the worst of them.", "세상 사람은 누구나 다 선해요."], ["The world’s a nice place.", "누구나 다—그중 가장 못된 사람조차도요."], ["Though we’re bad the world’s all right.", "세상은 좋은 곳이에요. 우리가 나쁘다 해도 세상은 괜찮아요."], ["We’re good and bad, good and bad....", "우리는 선하기도 하고 악하기도 해요, 선하기도 하고 악하기도……"], ["Come, tell me, I’ve something to ask you: come here every one, and I’ll ask you: Why am I so good?", "자, 말해 봐요, 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어요. 다들 이리 와요, 물어볼 게 있으니까."], ["You know I am good.", "난 왜 이렇게 착할까요?"], ["I’m very good....", "내가 착한 거 알지요."], ["Come, why am I so good?”", "난 아주 착해요…… 자, 난 왜 이렇게 착하지요?\""]], [["So Grushenka babbled on, getting more and more drunk.", "이렇게 그루셴카는 점점 더 취해 가며 횡설수설 지껄였다."], ["At last she announced that she was going to dance, too.", "마침내 그녀는 자기도 춤을 추겠노라고 선언했다."], ["She got up from her chair, staggering.", "그녀는 비틀거리며 의자에서 일어났다."], ["“Mitya, don’t give me any more wine—if I ask you, don’t give it to me.", "\"미탸, 이젠 술을 더 주지 말아요—내가 달라고 해도 주지 말아요."], ["Wine doesn’t give peace.", "술은 평안을 주지 않아요."], ["Everything’s going round, the stove, and everything.", "모든 게 빙빙 돌아요, 난로도, 다른 것도 죄다."], ["I want to dance.", "난 춤추고 싶어요."], ["Let every one see how I dance ...", "다들 내가 춤추는 걸 보게 둬요……"], ["let them see how beautifully I dance....”", "내가 얼마나 아름답게 춤추는지 보게 둬요……\""]], [["She really meant it.", "그녀는 진심이었다."], ["She pulled a white cambric handkerchief out of her pocket, and took it by one corner in her right hand, to wave it in the dance.", "그녀는 주머니에서 흰 무명 손수건을 꺼내어, 춤추며 흔들려고 오른손으로 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡았다."], ["Mitya ran to and fro, the girls were quiet, and got ready to break into a dancing song at the first signal.", "미탸는 이리저리 뛰어다녔고, 처녀들은 조용해진 채 신호가 떨어지면 곧장 춤곡을 부르려고 채비했다."], ["Maximov, hearing that Grushenka wanted to dance, squealed with delight, and ran skipping about in front of her, humming:", "막시모프는 그루셴카가 춤추고 싶어 한다는 말을 듣자 기뻐서 꽥 소리를 지르며 그녀 앞에서 깡충깡충 뛰어다니며 흥얼거렸다."]], [["With legs so slim and sides so trim And its little tail curled tight.", "다리는 가늘고 옆구리는 날씬한 데다 조그만 꼬리는 야무지게 말려 있네."]], [["But Grushenka waved her handkerchief at him and drove him away.", "그러나 그루셴카는 그에게 손수건을 휘둘러 쫓아 버렸다."]], [["“Sh-h!", "\"쉬잇!"], ["Mitya, why don’t they come?", "미탸, 저 사람들은 왜 안 와요?"], ["Let every one come ...", "다들 오게 해요……"], ["to look on.", "구경하라고요."], ["Call them in, too, that were locked in....", "갇혀 있는 사람들도 불러들여요……"], ["Why did you lock them in?", "왜 그 사람들을 가둬 놨어요?"], ["Tell them I’m going to dance.", "내가 춤출 거라고 일러요."], ["Let them look on, too....”", "그 사람들도 구경하게 둬요……\""]], [["Mitya walked with a drunken swagger to the locked door, and began knocking to the Poles with his fist.", "미탸는 술 취해 거들먹거리는 걸음걸이로 잠긴 문으로 걸어가, 주먹으로 폴란드 사람들을 향해 문을 두드리기 시작했다."]], [["“Hi, you ...", "\"여보시오, 당신들……"], ["Podvysotskys!", "포드비소츠키 양반들!"], ["Come, she’s going to dance.", "자, 그녀가 춤을 출 거요."], ["She calls you.”", "당신들을 부르고 있소.\""]], [["“Lajdak!”", "\"라이다크(lajdak)!\""], ["one of the Poles shouted in reply.", "폴란드 사람 하나가 대꾸하며 소리쳤다."]], [["“You’re a lajdak yourself!", "\"당신이야말로 라이다크요!"], ["You’re a little scoundrel, that’s what you are.”", "당신이야말로 하찮은 악당이지, 바로 그거요.\""]], [["“Leave off laughing at Poland,”", "\"폴란드를 비웃는 건 그만둬요.\""], ["said Kalganov sententiously.", "칼가노프가 격언이라도 읊듯 말했다."], ["He too was drunk.", "그 역시 취해 있었다."]], [["“Be quiet, boy!", "\"조용히 해, 도련님!"], ["If I call him a scoundrel, it doesn’t mean that I called all Poland so.", "내가 저자를 악당이라 부른다고 해서, 폴란드 전체를 악당이라 부른 건 아니야."], ["One lajdak doesn’t make a Poland.", "라이다크 하나가 폴란드를 이루는 건 아니니까."], ["Be quiet, my pretty boy, eat a sweetmeat.”", "조용히 해, 내 예쁜 도련님, 사탕이나 하나 먹어.\""]], [["“Ach, what fellows!", "\"아, 어쩌면 저런 작자들이!"], ["As though they were not men.", "마치 사람도 아닌 것 같아요."], ["Why won’t they make friends?”", "왜 화해하려 들지 않을까요?\""], ["said Grushenka, and went forward to dance.", "그루셴카는 이렇게 말하며 춤추러 앞으로 나섰다."], ["The chorus broke into “Ah, my porch, my new porch!”", "합창대가 \"아, 나의 현관, 나의 새 현관!\"을 부르기 시작했다."], ["Grushenka flung back her head, half opened her lips, smiled, waved her handkerchief, and suddenly, with a violent lurch, stood still in the middle of the room, looking bewildered.", "그루셴카는 고개를 뒤로 젖히고, 입술을 반쯤 벌리고, 미소 지으며, 손수건을 흔들다가, 별안간 휘청 심하게 비틀거리더니 방 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 어리둥절한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“I’m weak....”", "\"기운이 없어요……\""], ["she said in an exhausted voice.", "그녀가 지친 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Forgive me....", "\"용서해요……"], ["I’m weak, I can’t....", "기운이 없어요, 못 추겠어요……"], ["I’m sorry.”", "미안해요.\""]], [["She bowed to the chorus, and then began bowing in all directions.", "그녀는 합창대를 향해 머리를 숙이더니, 이어 사방을 향해 머리를 숙이기 시작했다."]], [["“I’m sorry....", "\"미안해요……"], ["Forgive me....”", "용서해요……\""]], [["“The lady’s been drinking.", "\"아씨가 술을 드셨군."], ["The pretty lady has been drinking,”", "예쁜 아씨가 술을 드셨어.\""], ["voices were heard saying.", "사람들의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“The lady’s drunk too much,”", "\"아씨가 너무 많이 드셨어.\""], ["Maximov explained to the girls, giggling.", "막시모프가 킥킥거리며 처녀들에게 설명했다."]], [["“Mitya, lead me away ...", "\"미탸, 날 데려가요……"], ["take me,”", "날 데려가요.\""], ["said Grushenka helplessly.", "그루셴카가 힘없이 말했다."], ["Mitya pounced on her, snatched her up in his arms, and carried the precious burden through the curtains.", "미탸는 그녀에게 달려들어 두 팔로 와락 안아 들고는, 그 소중한 짐을 안고 커튼을 지나갔다."]], [["“Well, now I’ll go,”", "\"자, 이제 난 가야겠군.\""], ["thought Kalganov, and walking out of the blue room, he closed the two halves of the door after him.", "칼가노프는 이렇게 생각하고, 푸른 방에서 나가며 두 짝 문을 뒤로 닫았다."], ["But the orgy in the larger room went on and grew louder and louder.", "그러나 더 큰 방에서 벌어진 광란의 잔치는 계속되었고, 점점 더 시끌벅적해졌다."], ["Mitya laid Grushenka on the bed and kissed her on the lips.", "미탸는 그루셴카를 침대에 눕히고 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Don’t touch me....”", "\"날 건드리지 말아요……\""], ["she faltered, in an imploring voice.", "그녀가 애원하는 목소리로 더듬더듬 말했다."], ["“Don’t touch me, till I’m yours....", "\"내가 당신 것이 되기 전에는 날 건드리지 말아요……"], ["I’ve told you I’m yours, but don’t touch me ...", "난 당신 것이라고 말했지만, 그래도 날 건드리지 말아요……"], ["spare me....", "날 봐줘요……"], ["With them here, with them close, you mustn’t.", "저들이 여기 있는데, 저들이 가까이 있는데 그러면 안 돼요."], ["He’s here.", "그이가 여기 있어요."], ["It’s nasty here....”", "여긴 추잡해요……\""]], [["“I’ll obey you!", "\"당신 말대로 하겠어!"], ["I won’t think of it ...", "그런 생각은 하지 않겠어……"], ["I worship you!”", "난 당신을 떠받드는 거야!\""], ["muttered Mitya.", "미탸가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Yes, it’s nasty here, it’s abominable.”", "\"그래, 여긴 추잡해, 끔찍해.\""]], [["And still holding her in his arms, he sank on his knees by the bedside.", "그러고는 여전히 그녀를 두 팔로 안은 채, 그는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다."]], [["“I know, though you’re a brute, you’re generous,”", "\"알아요, 당신은 짐승이긴 해도 너그러워요.\""], ["Grushenka articulated with difficulty.", "그루셴카가 힘겹게 말을 이었다."], ["“It must be honorable ...", "\"이건 떳떳해야 해요……"], ["it shall be honorable for the future ...", "앞으로는 떳떳할 거예요……"], ["and let us be honest, let us be good, not brutes, but good ...", "우리 정직하게 살아요, 우리 선하게 살아요, 짐승이 아니라 선한 사람으로……"], ["take me away, take me far away, do you hear?", "날 데려가요, 멀리 데려가요, 알겠어요?"], ["I don’t want it to be here, but far, far away....”", "여기선 싫어요, 멀리, 멀리 떨어진 곳에서……\""]], [["“Oh, yes, yes, it must be!”", "\"오, 그래, 그래, 꼭 그래야지!\""], ["said Mitya, pressing her in his arms.", "미탸가 그녀를 두 팔로 꽉 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“I’ll take you and we’ll fly away....", "\"당신을 데리고 날아가 버릴 거야……"], ["Oh, I’d give my whole life for one year only to know about that blood!”", "오, 그 피에 관해 알 수만 있다면 단 일 년을 위해서라도 내 평생을 다 내놓겠어!\""]], [["“What blood?”", "\"무슨 피요?\""], ["asked Grushenka, bewildered.", "그루셴카가 어리둥절해서 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니야.\""], ["muttered Mitya, through his teeth.", "미탸가 이를 악문 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“Grusha, you wanted to be honest, but I’m a thief.", "\"그루샤, 당신은 정직하게 살고 싶다지만, 난 도둑이야."], ["But I’ve stolen money from Katya....", "난 카탸한테서 돈을 훔쳤어……"], ["Disgrace, a disgrace!”", "치욕이야, 치욕이라고!\""]], [["“From Katya, from that young lady?", "\"카탸한테서, 그 아가씨한테서요?"], ["No, you didn’t steal it.", "아니, 당신은 그걸 훔친 게 아니에요."], ["Give it her back, take it from me....", "그 여자한테 돌려줘요, 내게서 가져다가……"], ["Why make a fuss?", "뭘 그렇게 야단이에요?"], ["Now everything of mine is yours.", "이제 내 것은 다 당신 거예요."], ["What does money matter?", "돈이 무슨 대수예요?"], ["We shall waste it anyway....", "어차피 우린 다 써 버릴 텐데……"], ["Folks like us are bound to waste money.", "우리 같은 사람들은 돈을 헤프게 쓰게 마련이에요."], ["But we’d better go and work the land.", "하지만 우린 차라리 땅을 일구러 가는 게 좋겠어요."], ["I want to dig the earth with my own hands.", "난 내 손으로 흙을 파고 싶어요."], ["We must work, do you hear?", "우린 일해야 해요, 알겠어요?"], ["Alyosha said so.", "알료샤가 그렇게 말했어요."], ["I won’t be your mistress, I’ll be faithful to you, I’ll be your slave, I’ll work for you.", "난 당신 정부 노릇은 안 할 거예요, 난 당신한테 충실할 거예요, 당신의 노예가 될 거예요, 당신을 위해 일할 거예요."], ["We’ll go to the young lady and bow down to her together, so that she may forgive us, and then we’ll go away.", "우리 그 아가씨한테 함께 가서 같이 머리를 숙여요, 그 여자가 우릴 용서하게요, 그러고 나서 떠나요."], ["And if she won’t forgive us, we’ll go, anyway.", "그 여자가 용서해 주지 않는다 해도 우린 어쨌든 떠날 거예요."], ["Take her her money and love me....", "그 여자한테 돈을 가져다주고 날 사랑해요……"], ["Don’t love her....", "그 여자는 사랑하지 말아요……"], ["Don’t love her any more.", "이제 더는 그 여자를 사랑하지 말아요."], ["If you love her, I shall strangle her....", "당신이 그 여자를 사랑하면 난 그 여자 목을 졸라 버릴 거예요……"], ["I’ll put out both her eyes with a needle....”", "바늘로 두 눈을 다 찔러 버릴 거예요……\""]], [["“I love you.", "\"난 당신을 사랑해."], ["I love only you.", "난 오직 당신만 사랑해."], ["I’ll love you in Siberia....”", "시베리아에서도 당신을 사랑할 거야……\""]], [["“Why Siberia?", "\"왜 시베리아예요?"], ["Never mind, Siberia, if you like.", "괜찮아요, 시베리아라도 좋아요, 당신만 원한다면."], ["I don’t care ...", "난 상관없어요……"], ["we’ll work ...", "우린 일할 거예요……"], ["there’s snow in Siberia....", "시베리아에는 눈이 있어요……"], ["I love driving in the snow ...", "난 눈 속에서 마차 모는 게 좋아요……"], ["and must have bells....", "그리고 방울이 있어야 해요……"], ["Do you hear, there’s a bell ringing?", "들려요, 방울이 울리고 있어요?"], ["Where is that bell ringing?", "저 방울은 어디서 울리는 거지요?"], ["There are people coming....", "사람들이 오고 있어요……"], ["Now it’s stopped.”", "이제 멎었네요.\""]], [["She closed her eyes, exhausted, and suddenly fell asleep for an instant.", "그녀는 기진해 눈을 감더니, 갑자기 한순간 잠들어 버렸다."], ["There had certainly been the sound of a bell in the distance, but the ringing had ceased.", "멀리서 방울 소리가 들려온 것은 분명했으나, 그 울림은 이미 멎어 있었다."], ["Mitya let his head sink on her breast.", "미탸는 그녀의 가슴에 머리를 떨구었다."], ["He did not notice that the bell had ceased ringing, nor did he notice that the songs had ceased, and that instead of singing and drunken clamor there was absolute stillness in the house.", "그는 방울 소리가 멎은 것을 알아차리지 못했고, 노래가 그친 것도, 노래와 술 취한 떠들썩함 대신 집 안에 완전한 정적이 깃든 것도 알아차리지 못했다."], ["Grushenka opened her eyes.", "그루셴카가 눈을 떴다."]], [["“What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일이지요?"], ["Was I asleep?", "내가 잠들었어요?"], ["Yes ...", "그래요……"], ["a bell ...", "방울……"], ["I’ve been asleep and dreamt I was driving over the snow with bells, and I dozed.", "잠이 들어 꿈을 꿨어요, 방울을 단 채 눈밭 위를 마차로 달리는 꿈을요, 그러다 깜빡 졸았어요."], ["I was with some one I loved, with you. And far, far away.", "난 내가 사랑하는 누군가와, 당신과 함께 있었어요."], ["I was holding you and kissing you, nestling close to you.", "그리고 멀리, 멀리 떨어진 곳에 있었어요."], ["I was cold, and the snow glistened....", "난 당신을 끌어안고 입 맞추며, 당신한테 바싹 파고들었어요."], ["You know how the snow glistens at night when the moon shines.", "난 추웠고, 눈은 반짝였어요…… 알지요, 달빛이 비칠 때 밤에 눈이 어떻게 반짝이는지."], ["It was as though I was not on earth.", "마치 내가 이 땅에 있지 않은 것 같았어요."], ["I woke up, and my dear one is close to me.", "깨어 보니 내 사랑하는 사람이 곁에 있네요."], ["How sweet that is!...”", "어쩌면 이렇게 달콤할까요!……\""]], [["“Close to you,”", "\"당신 곁에.\""], ["murmured Mitya, kissing her dress, her bosom, her hands.", "미탸가 그녀의 옷에, 그녀의 가슴에, 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추며 중얼거렸다."], ["And suddenly he had a strange fancy: it seemed to him that she was looking straight before her, not at him, not into his face, but over his head, with an intent, almost uncanny fixity.", "그런데 갑자기 그에게 이상한 느낌이 들었으니, 그녀가 자기 앞을 똑바로 바라보고 있는 것 같았는데, 자기를 보는 것도, 자기 얼굴을 보는 것도 아니라, 그의 머리 너머를, 골똘하게, 거의 섬뜩하리만치 고정된 시선으로 바라보고 있는 듯했다."], ["An expression of wonder, almost of alarm, came suddenly into her face.", "별안간 그녀의 얼굴에 놀라움, 거의 경악에 가까운 표정이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Mitya, who is that looking at us?”", "\"미탸, 우릴 보고 있는 저게 누구지요?\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."]], [["Mitya turned, and saw that some one had, in fact, parted the curtains and seemed to be watching them.", "미탸가 돌아보니, 과연 누군가가 커튼을 젖히고 그들을 지켜보고 있는 듯했다."], ["And not one person alone, it seemed.", "그것도 한 사람만이 아닌 듯했다."]], [["He jumped up and walked quickly to the intruder.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 그 침입자에게 빠른 걸음으로 다가갔다."]], [["“Here, come to us, come here,”", "\"이리 와요, 우리 쪽으로, 이리.\""], ["said a voice, speaking not loudly, but firmly and peremptorily.", "한 목소리가 크지는 않으나 단호하고 위압적으로 말했다."]], [["Mitya passed to the other side of the curtain and stood stock still.", "미탸는 커튼 저편으로 건너가더니 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["The room was filled with people, but not those who had been there before.", "방은 사람들로 가득했으나, 아까 거기 있던 사람들이 아니었다."], ["An instantaneous shiver ran down his back, and he shuddered.", "순간 그의 등줄기로 오한이 훑고 지나가, 그는 몸을 부르르 떨었다."], ["He recognized all those people instantly.", "그는 그 사람들을 모두 단번에 알아보았다."], ["That tall, stout old man in the overcoat and forage-cap with a cockade—was the police captain, Mihail Makarovitch. And that “consumptive-looking”", "외투를 걸치고 모표가 달린 정모를 쓴 저 키 크고 살집 있는 노인은—경찰서장 미하일 마카로비치였다."], ["trim dandy, “who always has such polished boots”—that was the deputy prosecutor.", "그리고 \"늘 그렇게 반들반들한 장화를 신고 다니는\", \"폐병 환자 같은\" 말쑥한 멋쟁이는—검사보였다."], ["“He has a chronometer worth four hundred roubles; he showed it to me.”", "\"저 사람은 사백 루블짜리 정밀 회중시계를 갖고 있지, 내게 보여 준 적이 있어.\""], ["And that small young man in spectacles....", "그리고 안경을 쓴 저 자그마한 젊은이는……"], ["Mitya forgot his surname though he knew him, had seen him: he was the “investigating lawyer,” from the “school of jurisprudence,”", "미탸는 그를 알고 있었고, 본 적도 있건만 그 성은 잊어버렸으니, 그는 최근에야 읍내에 부임한, \"법학교\""], ["who had only lately come to the town.", "출신의 \"예심 판사\"였다."], ["And this man—the inspector of police, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, a man he knew well.", "그리고 이 사람은—경찰 감독관 마브리키 마브리키예비치로, 그가 잘 아는 사람이었다."], ["And those fellows with the brass plates on, why are they here?", "그리고 가슴에 놋쇠 휘장을 단 저 친구들은, 어째서 여기 있는 걸까?"], ["And those other two ...", "그리고 저 다른 두 사람은……"], ["peasants....", "농부들이로군……"], ["And there at the door Kalganov with Trifon Borissovitch....", "그리고 저쪽 문가에는 칼가노프와 트리폰 보리소비치가……"]], [["“Gentlemen!", "\"여러분!"], ["What’s this for, gentlemen?”", "이게 무슨 일입니까, 여러분?\""], ["began Mitya, but suddenly, as though beside himself, not knowing what he was doing, he cried aloud, at the top of his voice:", "미탸가 입을 열었으나, 별안간 정신이 나간 듯, 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채, 목청껏 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“I un—der—stand!”", "\"알—겠—습니다!\""]], [["The young man in spectacles moved forward suddenly, and stepping up to Mitya, began with dignity, though hurriedly:", "안경 쓴 젊은이가 갑자기 앞으로 나서서 미탸에게 다가서더니, 서두르면서도 위엄을 갖추어 입을 열었다."]], [["“We have to make ...", "\"우리는……"], ["in brief, I beg you to come this way, this way to the sofa....", "간단히 말씀드리자면, 이쪽으로, 이쪽 소파로 와 주시기를 청합니다……"], ["It is absolutely imperative that you should give an explanation.”", "당신은 반드시 진술을 하셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“The old man!”", "\"노인이!\""], ["cried Mitya frantically.", "미탸가 미친 듯이 외쳤다."], ["“The old man and his blood!...", "\"노인과 그 피가!……"], ["I understand.”", "알겠습니다.\""]], [["And he sank, almost fell, on a chair close by, as though he had been mown down by a scythe.", "그러고는 그는 낫에 베어 쓰러지기라도 한 듯, 바로 곁의 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 거의 쓰러지다시피 했다."]], [["“You understand?", "\"알겠다고?"], ["He understands it!", "저자가 알겠다고 하는군!"], ["Monster and parricide!", "괴물, 친부 살해자!"], ["Your father’s blood cries out against you!”", "네 아비의 피가 너를 향해 울부짖고 있다!\""], ["the old captain of police roared suddenly, stepping up to Mitya.", "늙은 경찰서장이 별안간 미탸에게 다가서며 고함을 질렀다."]], [["He was beside himself, crimson in the face and quivering all over.", "그는 제정신이 아니었으니, 얼굴이 시뻘게진 채 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 있었다."]], [["“This is impossible!”", "\"이러시면 안 됩니다!\""], ["cried the small young man.", "자그마한 젊은이가 외쳤다."], ["“Mihail Makarovitch, Mihail Makarovitch, this won’t do!...", "\"미하일 마카로비치, 미하일 마카로비치, 이러시면 안 됩니다!……"], ["I beg you’ll allow me to speak.", "부디 제가 말하도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["I should never have expected such behavior from you....”", "서장님께 이런 처사는 결코 기대하지 않았습니다……\""]], [["“This is delirium, gentlemen, raving delirium,”", "\"이건 헛소리요, 여러분, 미친 헛소리야.\" 경찰서장이 외쳤다."], ["cried the captain of police; “look at him: drunk, at this time of night, in the company of a disreputable woman, with the blood of his father on his hands....", "\"저자를 보시오. 술에 취해서, 이 야밤에, 행실 더러운 계집과 어울려, 제 아비의 피를 손에 묻힌 채로……"], ["It’s delirium!...”", "이건 헛소리야!……\""]], [["“I beg you most earnestly, dear Mihail Makarovitch, to restrain your feelings,”", "\"간곡히 부탁드립니다, 친애하는 미하일 마카로비치, 감정을 자제해 주십시오.\""], ["the prosecutor said in a rapid whisper to the old police captain, “or I shall be forced to resort to—”", "검사가 늙은 경찰서장에게 빠른 말로 속삭였다. \"안 그러시면 저로서는 부득이……\""]], [["But the little lawyer did not allow him to finish.", "그러나 자그마한 판사는 그의 말을 끝까지 듣지 않았다."], ["He turned to Mitya, and delivered himself in a loud, firm, dignified voice:", "그는 미탸에게 돌아서서, 크고 단호하며 위엄 있는 목소리로 말을 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“Ex-Lieutenant Karamazov, it is my duty to inform you that you are charged with the murder of your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, perpetrated this night....”", "\"전직 중위 카라마조프, 본관은 당신에게 알리는 바이오. 당신은 오늘 밤 자행된 당신의 아버지 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 살해 혐의로 기소되었소……\""]], [["He said something more, and the prosecutor, too, put in something, but though Mitya heard them he did not understand them.", "그는 무슨 말인가를 더 했고, 검사 역시 무슨 말인가를 보탰으나, 미탸는 그 말을 듣기는 했지만 알아듣지는 못했다."], ["He stared at them all with wild eyes.", "그는 사나운 눈으로 그들 모두를 멍하니 바라보았다."]], [["Book IX. The Preliminary Investigation Chapter I.", "우리가 모로조바 미망인의 집, 굳게 잠긴 튼튼한 대문을 두드리던 자리에 남겨 두고 온 표트르 일리치 페르호틴은 물론 마침내 인기척을 알리는 데 성공했다."], ["The Beginning Of Perhotin’s Official Career\nPyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, whom we left knocking at the strong locked gates of the widow Morozov’s house, ended, of course, by making himself heard. Fenya, who was still excited by the fright she had had two hours before, and too much “upset”", "두 시간 전에 겪은 공포로 아직도 마음이 들떠 있던 페냐는, 어찌나 \"심란\"한지 잠자리에 들지도 못한 채 있다가, 대문을 사납게 두드리는 소리를 듣고는 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 만큼 겁에 질렸다."], ["to go to bed, was almost frightened into hysterics on hearing the furious knocking at the gate. Though she had herself seen him drive away, she fancied that it must be Dmitri Fyodorovitch knocking again, no one else could knock so savagely.", "그가 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 제 눈으로 보고서도, 그녀는 그것이 또다시 드미트리 표도로비치가 두드리는 것이라고 여겼으니, 그토록 거칠게 두드릴 수 있는 사람은 달리 없으리라 싶었던 것이다."], ["She ran to the house-porter, who had already waked up and gone out to the gate, and began imploring him not to open it.", "그녀는 이미 잠에서 깨어 대문 쪽으로 나가 있던 문지기에게로 달려가, 제발 문을 열지 말라고 사정했다."], ["But having questioned Pyotr Ilyitch, and learned that he wanted to see Fenya on very “important business,” the man made up his mind at last to open.", "그러나 표트르 일리치를 캐물어 그가 페냐를 매우 \"중대한 용건\"으로 만나고자 한다는 것을 알게 되자, 문지기는 마침내 문을 열어 주기로 마음먹었다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch was admitted into Fenya’s kitchen, but the girl begged him to allow the house-porter to be present, “because of her misgivings.”", "표트르 일리치는 페냐의 부엌으로 들여보내졌으나, 처녀는 \"마음이 놓이지 않으니\" 문지기를 곁에 두게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He began questioning her and at once learnt the most vital fact, that is, that when Dmitri Fyodorovitch had run out to look for Grushenka, he had snatched up a pestle from the mortar, and that when he returned, the pestle was not with him and his hands were smeared with blood.", "그는 그녀를 캐묻기 시작했고, 곧장 가장 중요한 사실을 알아냈으니, 다름 아니라 드미트리 표도로비치가 그루셴카를 찾으러 뛰쳐나갈 때 절구에서 절굿공이를 집어 들었다는 것, 그리고 돌아왔을 때 절굿공이는 그의 손에 없었고 두 손이 피로 얼룩져 있었다는 것이었다."]], [["“And the blood was simply flowing, dripping from him, dripping!”", "\"게다가 피가 그냥 줄줄, 뚝뚝 떨어지고 있었어요, 뚝뚝!\""], ["Fenya kept exclaiming.", "페냐는 거듭 외쳐 댔다."], ["This horrible detail was simply the product of her disordered imagination.", "이 끔찍한 세부는 그저 어지러워진 그녀의 상상이 빚어낸 것일 뿐이었다."], ["But although not “dripping,” Pyotr Ilyitch had himself seen those hands stained with blood, and had helped to wash them.", "그러나 \"뚝뚝 떨어지지\"는 않았다 해도, 표트르 일리치는 피로 물든 그 두 손을 제 눈으로 보았고, 그것을 씻는 일까지 거들었다."], ["Moreover, the question he had to decide was not how soon the blood had dried, but where Dmitri Fyodorovitch had run with the pestle, or rather, whether it really was to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, and how he could satisfactorily ascertain.", "더구나 그가 판가름해야 할 문제는 피가 얼마나 빨리 말랐느냐가 아니라, 드미트리 표도로비치가 절굿공이를 들고 어디로 달려갔느냐, 아니 그보다 그것이 정말로 표도르 파블로비치의 집이었느냐, 그리고 그것을 어떻게 흡족하게 확인할 수 있느냐 하는 것이었다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch persisted in returning to this point, and though he found out nothing conclusive, yet he carried away a conviction that Dmitri Fyodorovitch could have gone nowhere but to his father’s house, and that therefore something must have happened there.", "표트르 일리치는 끈질기게 이 점으로 되돌아갔으나, 결정적인 것은 아무것도 알아내지 못했음에도, 드미트리 표도로비치가 제 아버지의 집 말고는 어디로도 갈 수 없었으리라는 것, 따라서 그곳에서 무슨 일인가가 틀림없이 벌어졌으리라는 확신을 안고 떠났다."]], [["“And when he came back,”", "\"그런데 그분이 돌아왔을 때,\""], ["Fenya added with excitement, “I told him the whole story, and then I began asking him, ‘Why have you got blood on your hands, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?’ and he answered that that was human blood, and that he had just killed some one.", "페냐가 흥분해서 덧붙였다, \"제가 그분께 자초지종을 다 말했어요, 그러고는 묻기 시작했죠, '드미트리 표도로비치, 손에 어째서 피가 묻어 있어요?' 하고요, 그러니까 그분은 그게 사람의 피라고, 방금 누군가를 죽였다고 대답했어요."], ["He confessed it all to me, and suddenly ran off like a madman.", "저한테 다 털어놓고는 갑자기 미친 사람처럼 뛰쳐나갔어요."], ["I sat down and began thinking, where’s he run off to now like a madman?", "저는 주저앉아서 생각하기 시작했죠, 지금 저 양반이 미친 사람처럼 어디로 달려간 걸까?"], ["He’ll go to Mokroe, I thought, and kill my mistress there.", "모크로예로 가겠지, 하고 생각했어요, 거기 가서 우리 아씨를 죽이려는 거구나."], ["I ran out to beg him not to kill her.", "저는 아씨를 죽이지 말라고 빌려고 뛰쳐나갔어요."], ["I was running to his lodgings, but I looked at Plotnikov’s shop, and saw him just setting off, and there was no blood on his hands then.”", "그분 숙소로 달려가던 참인데, 플로트니코프 가게 쪽을 보니까 그분이 막 떠나려는 게 보였어요, 그때는 손에 피가 없었어요.\""], ["(Fenya had noticed this and remembered it.) Fenya’s old grandmother confirmed her evidence as far as she was capable.", "(페냐는 이것을 알아채고 기억해 두었다.) 페냐의 늙은 할머니도 제 능력껏 그녀의 진술을 거들어 확인해 주었다."], ["After asking some further questions, Pyotr Ilyitch left the house, even more upset and uneasy than he had been when he entered it.", "몇 가지를 더 캐물은 뒤, 표트르 일리치는 들어왔을 때보다 한층 더 심란하고 불안한 마음으로 그 집을 나섰다."]], [["The most direct and the easiest thing for him to do would have been to go straight to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s, to find out whether anything had happened there, and if so, what; and only to go to the police captain, as Pyotr Ilyitch firmly intended doing, when he had satisfied himself of the fact.", "그로서 가장 직접적이고 가장 손쉬운 길은 곧장 표도르 파블로비치의 집으로 가서 거기서 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 일어났다면 무엇인지 알아보고, 그 사실을 확인한 뒤에야—표트르 일리치가 굳게 작정한 대로—경찰서장에게 가는 것이었으리라."], ["But the night was dark, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s gates were strong, and he would have to knock again.", "그러나 밤은 어두웠고, 표도르 파블로비치의 대문은 튼튼했으며, 그는 또다시 두드려야 할 판이었다."], ["His acquaintance with Fyodor Pavlovitch was of the slightest, and what if, after he had been knocking, they opened to him, and nothing had happened?", "표도르 파블로비치와의 친분이라고는 지극히 얕은 터에, 만약 한참 두드린 끝에 사람들이 문을 열어 주었는데 아무 일도 없었다면 어쩔 것인가?"], ["Then Fyodor Pavlovitch in his jeering way would go telling the story all over the town, how a stranger, called Perhotin, had broken in upon him at midnight to ask if any one had killed him.", "그러면 표도르 파블로비치는 그 빈정대는 투로 온 고을을 돌아다니며 이 이야기를 떠벌릴 것이니, 페르호틴이라는 낯선 사내가 한밤중에 자기 집에 쳐들어와 누가 자기를 죽였느냐고 물었다고 할 게 아닌가."], ["It would make a scandal.", "그것은 추문거리가 될 것이었다."], ["And scandal was what Pyotr Ilyitch dreaded more than anything in the world.", "그리고 추문이야말로 표트르 일리치가 이 세상 무엇보다도 두려워하는 것이었다."]], [["Yet the feeling that possessed him was so strong, that though he stamped his foot angrily and swore at himself, he set off again, not to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s but to Madame Hohlakov’s.", "그러나 그를 사로잡은 감정이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그는 화가 나서 발을 구르고 제 자신에게 욕설을 퍼부으면서도, 다시 길을 나섰으니, 표도르 파블로비치의 집이 아니라 호흘라코바 부인의 집으로였다."], ["He decided that if she denied having just given Dmitri Fyodorovitch three thousand roubles, he would go straight to the police captain, but if she admitted having given him the money, he would go home and let the matter rest till next morning.", "그는 마음먹기를, 만약 그녀가 방금 드미트리 표도로비치에게 삼천 루블을 주었다는 것을 부인한다면 곧장 경찰서장에게로 가겠고, 만약 그 돈을 주었다고 인정한다면 집으로 돌아가 이 일을 이튿날 아침까지 덮어 두겠다고 했다."]], [["It is, of course, perfectly evident that there was even more likelihood of causing scandal by going at eleven o’clock at night to a fashionable lady, a complete stranger, and perhaps rousing her from her bed to ask her an amazing question, than by going to Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "물론, 한밤 열한 시에 생면부지의 낯선 상류층 부인을 찾아가, 어쩌면 잠자리에서 깨워 가며 어처구니없는 질문을 던지는 편이, 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 가는 것보다 추문을 일으킬 공산이 한층 더 크다는 것은 더없이 자명한 일이었다."], ["But that is just how it is, sometimes, especially in cases like the present one, with the decisions of the most precise and phlegmatic people.", "그러나 더없이 빈틈없고 냉정한 사람들의 결정이라는 것도, 때로는, 특히 이번과 같은 경우에는, 바로 그런 식으로 흘러가는 법이다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch was by no means phlegmatic at that moment.", "표트르 일리치는 그 순간 결코 냉정하지 못했다."], ["He remembered all his life how a haunting uneasiness gradually gained possession of him, growing more and more painful and driving him on, against his will.", "그는 평생토록 기억했으니, 어떻게 끈질긴 불안이 차츰차츰 그를 사로잡아, 점점 더 괴로워지며 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 그를 내몰았던가를."], ["Yet he kept cursing himself, of course, all the way for going to this lady, but “I will get to the bottom of it, I will!”", "그래도 그는 물론 이 부인에게 가는 내내 제 자신을 저주했으나, \"내 끝까지 파헤치고야 말겠다, 끝까지!\""], ["he repeated for the tenth time, grinding his teeth, and he carried out his intention.", "하고 이를 갈며 열 번째로 되뇌고는, 제 작정을 실행에 옮겼다."]], [["It was exactly eleven o’clock when he entered Madame Hohlakov’s house.", "그가 호흘라코바 부인의 집에 들어선 것은 정확히 열한 시였다."], ["He was admitted into the yard pretty quickly, but, in response to his inquiry whether the lady was still up, the porter could give no answer, except that she was usually in bed by that time.", "그는 제법 빨리 안마당으로 들여보내졌으나, 부인이 아직 깨어 있느냐는 그의 물음에 문지기는 대답해 줄 도리가 없었고, 다만 보통 그 시각이면 잠자리에 든다고만 했다."]], [["“Ask at the top of the stairs.", "\"위층 계단에서 물어보시오."], ["If the lady wants to receive you, she’ll receive you. If she won’t, she won’t.”", "마님이 손님을 맞고 싶으시면 맞으실 거고, 안 그러시면 안 맞으실 거요.\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch went up, but did not find things so easy here.", "표트르 일리치는 위로 올라갔으나, 여기서는 일이 그리 수월하지 않았다."], ["The footman was unwilling to take in his name, but finally called a maid.", "하인은 그의 이름을 안에 전하기를 꺼렸으나, 마침내 하녀를 불렀다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch politely but insistently begged her to inform her lady that an official, living in the town, called Perhotin, had called on particular business, and that if it were not of the greatest importance he would not have ventured to come.", "표트르 일리치는 정중하지만 집요하게, 이 고을에 사는 페르호틴이라는 관리가 특별한 용건으로 찾아왔으며, 그것이 더없이 중대한 일이 아니었다면 감히 오지 않았으리라고 마님께 전해 달라고 그녀에게 청했다."], ["“Tell her in those words, in those words exactly,”", "\"그 말 그대로 전해 주시오, 그 말 그대로 정확히요.\""], ["he asked the girl.", "그가 처녀에게 부탁했다."]], [["She went away.", "그녀가 물러갔다."], ["He remained waiting in the entry.", "그는 현관에서 기다렸다."], ["Madame Hohlakov herself was already in her bedroom, though not yet asleep.", "호흘라코바 부인은 이미 침실에 있었으나, 아직 잠들지는 않았다."], ["She had felt upset ever since Mitya’s visit, and had a presentiment that she would not get through the night without the sick headache which always, with her, followed such excitement.", "그녀는 미탸가 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 심란해하고 있었고, 늘 그러한 흥분 끝에 따라오는 그 지긋지긋한 두통 없이는 이 밤을 넘기지 못하리라는 예감에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["She was surprised on hearing the announcement from the maid.", "그녀는 하녀의 전갈을 듣고 놀랐다."], ["She irritably declined to see him, however, though the unexpected visit at such an hour, of an “official living in the town,” who was a total stranger, roused her feminine curiosity intensely.", "그러나 그녀는 짜증스럽게 만나기를 거절했으니, 비록 이런 시각에 생면부지의 \"이 고을에 사는 관리\"가 뜻밖에 찾아온 것이 그녀의 여성다운 호기심을 한껏 부추겼음에도 그러했다."], ["But this time Pyotr Ilyitch was as obstinate as a mule.", "그러나 이번만은 표트르 일리치가 노새처럼 고집스러웠다."], ["He begged the maid most earnestly to take another message in these very words:", "그는 하녀에게 더없이 간곡히, 바로 이 말 그대로 다시 한번 전해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“That he had come on business of the greatest importance, and that Madame Hohlakov might have cause to regret it later, if she refused to see him now.”", "\"그가 더없이 중대한 용건으로 찾아왔으며, 만약 지금 그를 만나기를 거절하신다면 호흘라코바 부인께서 나중에 후회하실 까닭이 생길지도 모른다고요.\""]], [["“I plunged headlong,”", "\"나는 무작정 뛰어들었습니다.\""], ["he described it afterwards.", "그는 나중에 그렇게 묘사했다."]], [["The maid, gazing at him in amazement, went to take his message again.", "하녀는 놀라움에 차서 그를 바라보더니, 다시 한번 그의 전갈을 전하러 갔다."], ["Madame Hohlakov was impressed.", "호흘라코바 부인은 마음이 움직였다."], ["She thought a little, asked what he looked like, and learned that he was “very well dressed, young and so polite.”", "그녀는 잠시 생각하더니, 그가 어떻게 생겼느냐고 물었고, 그가 \"옷차림이 아주 단정하고 젊으며 무척 정중하다\"는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["We may note, parenthetically, that Pyotr Ilyitch was a rather good-looking young man, and well aware of the fact.", "여담으로 적어 두자면, 표트르 일리치는 제법 풍채 좋은 청년이었고, 그 자신도 그 사실을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Madame Hohlakov made up her mind to see him.", "호흘라코바 부인은 그를 만나기로 마음먹었다."], ["She was in her dressing-gown and slippers, but she flung a black shawl over her shoulders. “The official”", "그녀는 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림이었으나, 어깨에 검은 숄을 걸쳤다."], ["was asked to walk into the drawing-room, the very room in which Mitya had been received shortly before.", "\"관리\"는 응접실로 들도록 청해졌으니, 바로 얼마 전 미탸를 맞이했던 그 방이었다."], ["The lady came to meet her visitor, with a sternly inquiring countenance, and, without asking him to sit down, began at once with the question:", "부인은 엄하게 캐묻는 낯빛으로 손님을 맞으러 나왔고, 앉으라는 말도 없이 대뜸 질문부터 시작했다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 용건이시죠?\""]], [["“I have ventured to disturb you, madam, on a matter concerning our common acquaintance, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov,”", "\"부인, 우리 두 사람의 공통된 지인인 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프에 관한 일로 감히 폐를 끼치게 되었습니다.\""], ["Perhotin began.", "페르호틴이 입을 열었다."]], [["But he had hardly uttered the name, when the lady’s face showed signs of acute irritation.", "그러나 그가 그 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 부인의 얼굴에는 격심한 짜증의 기색이 떠올랐다."], ["She almost shrieked, and interrupted him in a fury:", "그녀는 거의 비명을 지르다시피 하며 분에 차서 그의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“How much longer am I to be worried by that awful man?”", "\"내가 도대체 언제까지 그 끔찍한 사내 때문에 시달려야 한단 말이오?\""], ["she cried hysterically.", "그녀가 발작하듯 외쳤다."], ["“How dare you, sir, how could you venture to disturb a lady who is a stranger to you, in her own house at such an hour!...", "\"당신, 어찌 감히, 어떻게 생면부지의 부인을, 그것도 제 집에서, 이런 시각에 감히 방해할 수 있단 말이오!…"], ["And to force yourself upon her to talk of a man who came here, to this very drawing-room, only three hours ago, to murder me, and went stamping out of the room, as no one would go out of a decent house.", "게다가, 불과 세 시간 전에 바로 이 응접실로 나를 죽이러 찾아와서는, 멀쩡한 집에서라면 누구도 그렇게 나가지 않을 만큼 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 방을 나가 버린 그 사내 이야기를 하겠다고 억지로 들이밀다니."], ["Let me tell you, sir, that I shall lodge a complaint against you, that I will not let it pass.", "분명히 말해 두겠는데, 나는 당신을 고소하겠소, 이 일을 그냥 넘기지 않겠어요. 당장 나가 주시오…."], ["Kindly leave me at once....", "나는 어미 된 사람이오…."], ["I am a mother....", "나는…"], ["I ... I—”", "나는—\""]], [["“Murder!", "\"죽이다니!"], ["then he tried to murder you, too?”", "그러면 그자가 부인까지 죽이려 했단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Why, has he killed somebody else?”", "\"아니, 그가 누구 또 다른 사람을 죽였단 말이오?\""], ["Madame Hohlakov asked impulsively.", "호흘라코바 부인이 충동적으로 물었다."]], [["“If you would kindly listen, madam, for half a moment, I’ll explain it all in a couple of words,”", "\"부인, 부디 잠깐만, 반 순간만 들어 주신다면, 제가 두어 마디로 모두 설명해 드리겠습니다.\""], ["answered Perhotin, firmly.", "페르호틴이 단호하게 대답했다."], ["“At five o’clock this afternoon Dmitri Fyodorovitch borrowed ten roubles from me, and I know for a fact he had no money.", "\"오늘 오후 다섯 시에 드미트리 표도로비치가 제게서 십 루블을 빌렸는데, 저는 그에게 돈이 한 푼도 없었다는 것을 분명히 알고 있습니다."], ["Yet at nine o’clock, he came to see me with a bundle of hundred-rouble notes in his hand, about two or three thousand roubles.", "그런데 아홉 시에 그가 백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 손에 쥐고 저를 찾아왔으니, 어림잡아 이삼천 루블쯤 되었습니다."], ["His hands and face were all covered with blood, and he looked like a madman.", "그의 두 손과 얼굴은 온통 피투성이였고, 미친 사람 같은 몰골이었습니다."], ["When I asked him where he had got so much money, he answered that he had just received it from you, that you had given him a sum of three thousand to go to the gold-mines....”", "제가 그 많은 돈을 어디서 났느냐고 물었더니, 그는 방금 부인께 받았다고, 부인이 금광에 가라고 그에게 삼천 루블을 주셨다고 대답하더군요….\""]], [["Madame Hohlakov’s face assumed an expression of intense and painful excitement.", "호흘라코바 부인의 얼굴에 격심하고도 고통스러운 흥분의 기색이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["He must have killed his old father!”", "그가 제 늙은 아비를 죽인 게 틀림없어요!\""], ["she cried, clasping her hands.", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“I have never given him money, never!", "\"나는 그에게 돈을 준 적이 없어요, 단 한 번도!"], ["Oh, run, run!...", "오, 어서 가세요, 가요!…"], ["Don’t say another word!", "더는 한마디도 마세요!"], ["Save the old man ...", "그 노인을 구하세요…"], ["run to his father ...", "그의 아버지에게로 달려가세요…"], ["run!”", "어서요!\""]], [["“Excuse me, madam, then you did not give him money?", "\"실례지만, 부인, 그러면 그에게 돈을 주지 않으셨다는 거군요?"], ["You remember for a fact that you did not give him any money?”", "그에게 한 푼도 주지 않으셨다는 것을 분명히 기억하신단 말씀이지요?\""]], [["“No, I didn’t, I didn’t!", "\"네, 안 줬어요, 안 줬다고요!"], ["I refused to give it him, for he could not appreciate it.", "나는 주기를 거절했어요, 그가 그것을 알아볼 위인이 못 되니까요."], ["He ran out in a fury, stamping.", "그는 분에 차서 발을 쾅쾅 구르며 뛰쳐나갔어요."], ["He rushed at me, but I slipped away....", "나한테 달려들었지만, 내가 슬쩍 비켜 빠져나왔지요…."], ["And let me tell you, as I wish to hide nothing from you now, that he positively spat at me.", "그리고 이제 당신에게 아무것도 숨기고 싶지 않으니 말해 두겠는데, 그가 정말로 내게 침을 뱉었어요."], ["Can you fancy that!", "상상이 가시오!"], ["But why are we standing?", "한데 우리는 왜 서 있는 거죠?"], ["Ah, sit down.”", "아, 앉으세요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, I....”", "\"실례합니다, 저는….\""]], [["“Or better run, run, you must run and save the poor old man from an awful death!”", "\"아니, 그보다 어서 가세요, 가요, 가서 그 가엾은 노인을 끔찍한 죽음에서 구해 내야 해요!\""]], [["“But if he has killed him already?”", "\"하지만 만약 그가 이미 죽였다면요?\""]], [["“Ah, good heavens, yes!", "\"아, 세상에, 그래요!"], ["Then what are we to do now?", "그러면 우리가 이제 어떻게 해야 하지요?"], ["What do you think we must do now?”", "우리가 지금 무엇을 해야 한다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["Meantime she had made Pyotr Ilyitch sit down and sat down herself, facing him.", "그러는 사이에 그녀는 표트르 일리치를 앉히고 자신도 그를 마주 보고 앉았다."], ["Briefly, but fairly clearly, Pyotr Ilyitch told her the history of the affair, that part of it at least which he had himself witnessed.", "표트르 일리치는 간결하지만 제법 또렷하게, 적어도 자기가 직접 목격한 부분만큼은, 그 사건의 전말을 그녀에게 들려주었다."], ["He described, too, his visit to Fenya, and told her about the pestle.", "그는 페냐를 찾아갔던 일도 묘사하고, 절굿공이에 대해서도 이야기했다."], ["All these details produced an overwhelming effect on the distracted lady, who kept uttering shrieks, and covering her face with her hands....", "이 모든 세부는 넋이 나간 부인에게 압도적인 충격을 안겨 주었으니, 그녀는 연신 비명을 질러 대고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리곤 했다…."]], [["“Would you believe it, I foresaw all this!", "\"믿으시겠어요, 나는 이 모든 걸 예감하고 있었어요!"], ["I have that special faculty, whatever I imagine comes to pass.", "나한테는 그런 특별한 능력이 있어서, 내가 상상하는 것은 무엇이든 그대로 이루어진답니다."], ["And how often I’ve looked at that awful man and always thought, that man will end by murdering me.", "그리고 내가 그 끔찍한 사내를 볼 때마다 얼마나 자주 생각했는지, 저 사내는 결국 나를 죽이고 말 거라고요."], ["And now it’s happened ...", "그런데 이제 그 일이 벌어진 거예요…"], ["that is, if he hasn’t murdered me, but only his own father, it’s only because the finger of God preserved me, and what’s more, he was ashamed to murder me because, in this very place, I put the holy ikon from the relics of the holy martyr, Saint Varvara, on his neck....", "다시 말해, 그가 나를 죽이지 않고 제 아비만 죽인 거라면, 그건 오로지 하느님의 손가락이 나를 지켜 주신 덕분이고, 더구나 그가 나를 죽이기를 부끄러워한 건, 바로 이 자리에서 내가 거룩한 순교자 성녀 바르바라의 성유물에서 나온 거룩한 성상을 그의 목에 걸어 주었기 때문이에요…."], ["And to think how near I was to death at that minute, I went close up to him and he stretched out his neck to me!...", "그리고 그 순간 내가 죽음에 얼마나 가까이 다가가 있었는지 생각해 보세요, 나는 그에게 바짝 다가갔고 그는 내게 목을 쭉 내밀었답니다!…"], ["Do you know, Pyotr Ilyitch (I think you said your name was Pyotr Ilyitch), I don’t believe in miracles, but that ikon and this unmistakable miracle with me now—that shakes me, and I’m ready to believe in anything you like.", "아세요, 표트르 일리치 씨(이름이 표트르 일리치라고 하셨던 것 같은데), 나는 기적 같은 건 믿지 않아요, 하지만 그 성상과 지금 나에게 일어난 이 틀림없는 기적은—그게 나를 뒤흔들어서, 이제 무엇이든 당신이 바라는 대로 믿을 채비가 되어 있어요."], ["Have you heard about Father Zossima?...", "조시마 장로에 대해 들어 보셨어요?…"], ["But I don’t know what I’m saying ...", "한데 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 모르겠네요…"], ["and only fancy, with the ikon on his neck he spat at me....", "그리고 생각해 보세요, 성상을 목에 건 채로 그가 내게 침을 뱉었다니까요…."], ["He only spat, it’s true, he didn’t murder me and ...", "침만 뱉은 건 사실이에요, 나를 죽이지는 않았고… 그러고는 쏜살같이 달아났어요!"], ["he dashed away! But what shall we do, what must we do now?", "한데 우리가 무엇을 해야 하죠, 이제 무엇을 해야 할까요?"], ["What do you think?”", "어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch got up, and announced that he was going straight to the police captain, to tell him all about it, and leave him to do what he thought fit.", "표트르 일리치는 일어서며, 곧장 경찰서장에게 가서 이 일을 전부 알리고, 그가 옳다고 여기는 대로 처리하도록 맡기겠다고 알렸다."]], [["“Oh, he’s an excellent man, excellent!", "\"오, 그분은 훌륭한 분이에요, 훌륭하고말고요!"], ["Mihail Makarovitch, I know him.", "미하일 마카로비치, 나도 그분을 알아요."], ["Of course, he’s the person to go to.", "물론 그분이야말로 찾아갈 만한 분이지요."], ["How practical you are, Pyotr Ilyitch!", "표트르 일리치 씨, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 빈틈이 없으세요!"], ["How well you’ve thought of everything!", "어쩌면 그렇게 모든 걸 잘 생각해 내셨어요!"], ["I should never have thought of it in your place!”", "나라면 당신 처지에서 결코 그런 생각을 못 했을 거예요!\""]], [["“Especially as I know the police captain very well, too,”", "\"더구나 저는 경찰서장도 아주 잘 알고 있습니다.\""], ["observed Pyotr Ilyitch, who still continued to stand, and was obviously anxious to escape as quickly as possible from the impulsive lady, who would not let him say good-by and go away.", "표트르 일리치가 여전히 선 채로 말했으니, 그는 작별 인사를 하고 떠나도록 내버려 두지 않는 이 충동적인 부인에게서 되도록 빨리 벗어나고 싶어 안달이 난 기색이 역력했다."]], [["“And be sure, be sure,”", "\"그리고 꼭, 꼭,\""], ["she prattled on, “to come back and tell me what you see there, and what you find out ...", "그녀가 재잘재잘 늘어놓았다, \"다시 와서 거기서 무엇을 보셨는지, 무엇을 알아내셨는지 내게 말씀해 주세요…"], ["what comes to light ...", "무엇이 밝혀지는지…"], ["how they’ll try him ...", "그를 어떻게 재판하는지…"], ["and what he’s condemned to....", "그가 어떤 형을 선고받는지를요…."], ["Tell me, we have no capital punishment, have we?", "말씀해 보세요, 우리나라엔 사형 제도가 없지요, 그렇죠?"], ["But be sure to come, even if it’s at three o’clock at night, at four, at half-past four....", "하지만 꼭 와 주세요, 설령 그게 새벽 세 시든, 네 시든, 네 시 반이든…."], ["Tell them to wake me, to wake me, to shake me, if I don’t get up....", "하인들에게 일러서 나를 깨우게, 깨우게, 내가 일어나지 않으면 흔들어서라도 깨우게 하세요…."], ["But, good heavens, I shan’t sleep!", "하지만, 세상에, 나는 잠들지 못할 거예요!"], ["But wait, hadn’t I better come with you?”", "한데 잠깐만요, 차라리 내가 당신과 함께 가는 게 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["“N—no.", "\"아—아니요."], ["But if you would write three lines with your own hand, stating that you did not give Dmitri Fyodorovitch money, it might, perhaps, be of use ...", "하지만 부인께서 친필로 세 줄만, 드미트리 표도로비치에게 돈을 주지 않으셨다고 적어 주신다면, 어쩌면 쓸모가 있을지도 모릅니다…"], ["in case it’s needed....”", "필요할 경우에 대비해서요….\""]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"그렇고말고요!\""], ["Madame Hohlakov skipped, delighted, to her bureau.", "호흘라코바 부인은 기뻐서 깡충 뛰며 책상으로 갔다."], ["“And you know I’m simply struck, amazed at your resourcefulness, your good sense in such affairs.", "\"그리고 있잖아요, 나는 이런 일에서 당신이 보여 주는 그 임기응변, 그 분별력에 그저 놀랐답니다, 경탄했어요."], ["Are you in the service here?", "이곳에서 관직에 계신가요?"], ["I’m delighted to think that you’re in the service here!”", "당신이 이곳에서 관직에 계신다니 정말 기뻐요!\""]], [["And still speaking, she scribbled on half a sheet of notepaper the following lines:", "그러면서도 줄곧 말을 멈추지 않고, 그녀는 편지지 반 장에 다음과 같은 글귀를 갈겨썼다."]], [["I’ve never in my life lent to that unhappy man, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov (for, in spite of all, he is unhappy), three thousand roubles to-day. I’ve never given him money, never: That I swear by all that’s holy!", "저는 오늘 그 불행한 사내 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프에게(이러나저러나, 그는 불행한 사람이니까요) 삼천 루블을 빌려준 적이 평생 단 한 번도 없습니다. 저는 그에게 돈을 준 적이 결코 없어요, 단 한 번도. 거룩한 모든 것에 두고 맹세합니다! 카. 호흘라코바."]], [["# K. HOHLAKOV.", "# K. HOHLAKOV."]], [["“Here’s the note!”", "\"여기 쪽지예요!\""], ["she turned quickly to Pyotr Ilyitch.", "그녀가 표트르 일리치 쪽으로 휙 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“Go, save him.", "\"가세요, 그를 구하세요."], ["It’s a noble deed on your part!”", "당신 입장에서 보면 참으로 고귀한 일이에요!\""]], [["And she made the sign of the cross three times over him.", "그러고는 그를 향해 세 번 성호를 그었다."], ["She ran out to accompany him to the passage.", "그녀는 그를 복도까지 배웅하려고 뛰쳐나왔다."]], [["“How grateful I am to you!", "\"당신께 얼마나 감사한지!"], ["You can’t think how grateful I am to you for having come to me, first.", "당신이 제일 먼저 나를 찾아와 주신 게 얼마나 고마운지 당신은 모르실 거예요."], ["How is it I haven’t met you before?", "어쩌면 내가 전에 당신을 만나 뵙지 못했을까요?"], ["I shall feel flattered at seeing you at my house in the future.", "앞으로 우리 집에서 당신을 뵙게 된다면 영광으로 알겠어요."], ["How delightful it is that you are living here!...", "당신이 이곳에 사신다니 얼마나 기쁜지요!…"], ["Such precision!", "그토록 빈틈없으시다니!"], ["Such practical ability!...", "그토록 실무에 밝으시다니!…"], ["They must appreciate you, they must understand you.", "사람들이 당신을 알아봐 드려야 해요, 당신을 알아드려야 해요."], ["If there’s anything I can do, believe me ...", "내가 도울 수 있는 일이 있다면, 믿어 주세요…"], ["oh, I love young people!", "오, 나는 젊은이들을 사랑해요!"], ["I’m in love with young people!", "젊은이들에게 반해 있답니다!"], ["The younger generation are the one prop of our suffering country.", "젊은 세대야말로 고통받는 우리 조국의 단 하나뿐인 버팀목이에요."], ["Her one hope....", "그 유일한 희망이고요…."], ["Oh, go, go!...”", "오, 가세요, 가요!…\""]], [["But Pyotr Ilyitch had already run away or she would not have let him go so soon.", "그러나 표트르 일리치는 이미 달아나 버린 뒤였으니, 그러지 않았더라면 그녀가 그를 그리 일찍 보내 주지 않았을 것이다."], ["Yet Madame Hohlakov had made a rather agreeable impression on him, which had somewhat softened his anxiety at being drawn into such an unpleasant affair.", "그렇지만 호흘라코바 부인은 그에게 제법 흐뭇한 인상을 남겼고, 그것이 이런 불쾌한 사건에 휘말려 든 그의 불안을 얼마간 누그러뜨려 주었다."], ["Tastes differ, as we all know. “She’s by no means so elderly,”", "사람마다 취향이 다른 법이라는 것은 우리 모두 아는 바이다."], ["he thought, feeling pleased, “on the contrary I should have taken her for her daughter.”", "\"저 부인이 그리 늙은 것도 아니로군.\" 그는 흐뭇한 마음으로 생각했다, \"오히려 제 딸뻘이라고 봤을 정도야.\""]], [["As for Madame Hohlakov, she was simply enchanted by the young man.", "호흘라코바 부인으로 말하자면, 그녀는 그 청년에게 그저 홀딱 반해 버렸다."], ["“Such sense!", "\"그런 분별력이라니!"], ["such exactness!", "그런 빈틈없음이라니!"], ["in so young a man!", "저렇게 젊은 사람이!"], ["in our day!", "요즘 세상에!"], ["and all that with such manners and appearance!", "게다가 그 모든 게 저런 매너와 풍채까지 갖추고서!"], ["People say the young people of to-day are no good for anything, but here’s an example!”", "사람들은 요즘 젊은이들이 아무짝에도 쓸모없다고 하지만, 여기 그렇지 않다는 본보기가 있지 않은가!\""], ["etc.", "운운."], ["So she simply forgot this “dreadful affair,” and it was only as she was getting into bed, that, suddenly recalling “how near death she had been,”", "그리하여 그녀는 그 \"끔찍한 사건\"을 까맣게 잊어버렸고, 잠자리에 들면서야 비로소 문득 \"내가 죽음에 얼마나 가까이 갔던가\"를 떠올리고는 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["she exclaimed: “Ah, it is awful, awful!”", "\"아, 끔찍해, 끔찍하기도 하지!\""], ["But she fell at once into a sound, sweet sleep.", "그러나 그녀는 곧장 깊고 달콤한 잠에 빠져들었다."]], [["I would not, however, have dwelt on such trivial and irrelevant details, if this eccentric meeting of the young official with the by no means elderly widow had not subsequently turned out to be the foundation of the whole career of that practical and precise young man.", "그러나 만약 이 젊은 관리와 결코 늙었다고 할 수 없는 미망인의 기이한 만남이, 훗날 그 실무에 밝고 빈틈없는 청년의 출세 전체의 토대가 되었던 것이 아니었다면, 나는 이런 사소하고 본 줄거리와 무관한 세부에 이토록 머무르지는 않았으리라."], ["His story is remembered to this day with amazement in our town, and I shall perhaps have something to say about it, when I have finished my long history of the Brothers Karamazov.", "그의 이야기는 오늘날까지도 우리 고을에서 경탄과 함께 회자되거니와, 카라마조프가의 형제들에 관한 이 기나긴 이야기를 끝마치고 나면 나는 어쩌면 그에 관해 한마디 할 거리가 생길지도 모른다."]], [["# Chapter II. The Alarm", "# Chapter II. The Alarm"]], [["Our police captain, Mihail Makarovitch Makarov, a retired lieutenant-colonel, was a widower and an excellent man.", "우리 고장의 경찰서장 미하일 마카로비치 마카로프는 퇴역한 중령으로, 홀아비였고 더없이 훌륭한 인물이었다."], ["He had only come to us three years previously, but had won general esteem, chiefly because he “knew how to keep society together.”", "그가 우리 고장에 온 것은 삼 년 전의 일에 지나지 않았으나, 그는 이미 두루 존경을 받고 있었으니, 무엇보다도 \"사람들을 한데 어울리게 하는 재주\"를 지녔기 때문이었다."], ["He was never without visitors, and could not have got on without them.", "그의 집에는 손님이 끊일 날이 없었고, 그 자신도 손님 없이는 도무지 지낼 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["Some one or other was always dining with him; he never sat down to table without guests.", "늘 누군가가 그와 함께 식사를 했으며, 그는 손님 없이 식탁에 앉는 법이 없었다."], ["He gave regular dinners, too, on all sorts of occasions, sometimes most surprising ones.", "게다가 그는 온갖 구실을 붙여, 때로는 더없이 뜻밖의 구실로 정기적인 만찬을 베풀었다."], ["Though the fare was not recherché, it was abundant.", "음식이 썩 세련된 것은 아니었으나 양만은 푸짐했다."], ["The fish-pies were excellent, and the wine made up in quantity for what it lacked in quality.", "생선 파이는 일품이었고, 포도주는 질에서 모자란 것을 양으로 메웠다."]], [["The first room his guests entered was a well-fitted billiard-room, with pictures of English race-horses, in black frames on the walls, an essential decoration, as we all know, for a bachelor’s billiard-room.", "손님들이 처음 들어서는 방은 잘 꾸며진 당구실로, 벽에는 검은 액자에 끼운 영국 경주마 그림들이 걸려 있었으니, 다들 알다시피 독신자의 당구실에 빠질 수 없는 장식이었다."], ["There was card-playing every evening at his house, if only at one table.", "그의 집에서는 매일 저녁 카드놀이가 벌어졌으며, 비록 탁자 하나뿐일 때도 있었다."], ["But at frequent intervals, all the society of our town, with the mammas and young ladies, assembled at his house to dance.", "그러나 빈번하게 우리 고을의 온 사교계가 어머니들과 젊은 처녀들을 거느리고 그의 집에 모여 춤을 추곤 했다."], ["Though Mihail Makarovitch was a widower, he did not live alone.", "미하일 마카로비치는 홀아비였으나 혼자 사는 것은 아니었다."], ["His widowed daughter lived with him, with her two unmarried daughters, grown-up girls, who had finished their education.", "그에게는 역시 과부가 된 딸이 함께 살고 있었는데, 그 딸에게는 결혼하지 않은 두 딸, 곧 학업을 마친 다 자란 처녀들이 있었다."], ["They were of agreeable appearance and lively character, and though every one knew they would have no dowry, they attracted all the young men of fashion to their grandfather’s house.", "이들은 보기에 마음에 들고 성격도 발랄하여, 지참금이 한 푼도 없으리라는 것을 누구나 알면서도 외할아버지의 집으로 멋쟁이 젊은이들을 죄다 끌어모았다."]], [["Mihail Makarovitch was by no means very efficient in his work, though he performed his duties no worse than many others.", "미하일 마카로비치는 일에 있어 결코 썩 유능한 편은 아니었으나, 직무를 수행하는 데는 다른 많은 이들에 비해 못할 것이 없었다."], ["To speak plainly, he was a man of rather narrow education.", "솔직히 말하자면 그는 교양이 다소 좁은 인물이었다."], ["His understanding of the limits of his administrative power could not always be relied upon.", "자기 행정 권한의 한계를 헤아리는 그의 분별력은 늘 믿을 만한 것이 못 되었다."], ["It was not so much that he failed to grasp certain reforms enacted during the present reign, as that he made conspicuous blunders in his interpretation of them.", "그것은 그가 당대의 치세에 제정된 어떤 개혁들을 이해하지 못했다기보다, 그것들을 해석하는 데서 두드러진 실수를 저질렀다는 쪽이었다."], ["This was not from any special lack of intelligence, but from carelessness, for he was always in too great a hurry to go into the subject.", "이는 특별히 지능이 모자라서가 아니라 부주의에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그는 언제나 너무 서둘러 매사를 그저 건너뛰려 했기 때문이다."]], [["“I have the heart of a soldier rather than of a civilian,” he used to say of himself.", "\"나는 문관이라기보다는 군인의 심장을 지닌 사람이지.\""], ["He had not even formed a definite idea of the fundamental principles of the reforms connected with the emancipation of the serfs, and only picked it up, so to speak, from year to year, involuntarily increasing his knowledge by practice.", "그는 자기 자신을 두고 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그는 농노 해방과 관련된 개혁의 근본 원리에 대해 뚜렷한 관념조차 세우지 못한 채, 말하자면 해가 갈수록 어깨너머로 주워들으며 실무를 통해 본의 아니게 지식을 늘려 가는 형편이었다."], ["And yet he was himself a landowner.", "그러면서도 그 자신이 지주였던 것이다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch knew for certain that he would meet some of Mihail Makarovitch’s visitors there that evening, but he didn’t know which.", "표트르 일리치는 그날 저녁 그곳에서 미하일 마카로비치의 손님들 가운데 몇몇과 마주치게 되리라는 것을 확실히 알았으나, 누구일지는 알지 못했다."], ["As it happened, at that moment the prosecutor, and Varvinsky, our district doctor, a young man, who had only just come to us from Petersburg after taking a brilliant degree at the Academy of Medicine, were playing whist at the police captain’s.", "마침 그 순간에는 검사와, 우리 고을의 군의관 바르빈스키가 경찰서장과 휘스트를 치고 있었는데, 바르빈스키는 의학 아카데미에서 뛰어난 성적으로 학위를 따고 페테르부르크에서 막 우리 고장으로 온 젊은이였다."], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch, the prosecutor (he was really the deputy prosecutor, but we always called him the prosecutor), was rather a peculiar man, of about five and thirty, inclined to be consumptive, and married to a fat and childless woman.", "검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치는(실은 검사보였으나 우리는 늘 그를 검사라고 불렀다) 서른다섯 살가량의 다소 별난 사람으로, 폐병 기운이 있었고, 뚱뚱하고 자식이 없는 여자를 아내로 두고 있었다."], ["He was vain and irritable, though he had a good intellect, and even a kind heart.", "그는 허영심이 많고 성마른 사람이었으나, 머리가 좋았고 마음씨조차 따뜻한 편이었다."], ["It seemed that all that was wrong with him was that he had a better opinion of himself than his ability warranted.", "그에게 흠이 있다면 오로지, 제 능력이 보증하는 것 이상으로 자기 자신을 높이 평가한다는 점뿐인 듯싶었다."], ["And that made him seem constantly uneasy.", "그래서 그는 늘 안절부절못하는 사람처럼 보였다."], ["He had, moreover, certain higher, even artistic, leanings, towards psychology, for instance, a special study of the human heart, a special knowledge of the criminal and his crime.", "게다가 그는 어떤 한층 높은, 심지어 예술적이라 할 만한 취향을 지니고 있었으니, 이를테면 심리학이라든가, 인간의 마음에 대한 각별한 연구라든가, 범죄자와 그 범죄에 대한 각별한 식견 같은 것이었다."], ["He cherished a grievance on this ground, considering that he had been passed over in the service, and being firmly persuaded that in higher spheres he had not been properly appreciated, and had enemies.", "그는 이 점에서 한 가닥 원한을 품고 있었으니, 자신이 관직에서 부당하게 외면당했다고 여겼으며, 윗자리에서 자기를 제대로 인정해 주지 않았고 적들까지 있다고 굳게 믿고 있었다."], ["In gloomy moments he even threatened to give up his post, and practice as a barrister in criminal cases.", "울적할 때면 그는 직위를 내던지고 형사 사건 변호사로 개업하겠노라고 으르대기까지 했다."], ["The unexpected Karamazov case agitated him profoundly: “It was a case that might well be talked about all over Russia.”", "뜻밖에 터진 카라마조프 사건은 그를 깊이 뒤흔들어 놓았으니, \"이것이야말로 온 러시아에 두루 회자될 만한 사건이었다.\""], ["But I am anticipating.", "그러나 이는 앞질러 가는 이야기다."]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch Nelyudov, the young investigating lawyer, who had only come from Petersburg two months before, was sitting in the next room with the young ladies.", "두 달 전에야 페테르부르크에서 온 젊은 예심판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치 넬류도프는 옆방에서 젊은 처녀들과 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["People talked about it afterwards and wondered that all the gentlemen should, as though intentionally, on the evening of “the crime” have been gathered together at the house of the executive authority.", "사람들은 나중에 이 일을 두고 입방아를 찧으며, 마치 일부러 짠 것처럼 \"그 범행\"이 있던 저녁에 이 신사들이 죄다 행정 당국자의 집에 모여 있었다는 것을 의아해했다."], ["Yet it was perfectly simple and happened quite naturally.", "그러나 그것은 더없이 단순한 일이었고 지극히 자연스럽게 벌어진 일이었다."]], [["Ippolit Kirillovitch’s wife had had toothache for the last two days, and he was obliged to go out to escape from her groans.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치의 아내는 지난 이틀 동안 치통을 앓고 있었으므로, 그는 아내의 신음 소리를 피해 집을 나설 수밖에 없었다."], ["The doctor, from the very nature of his being, could not spend an evening except at cards.", "의사는 그 천성으로 보아 카드놀이 없이는 저녁을 보낼 수 없는 위인이었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch Nelyudov had been intending for three days past to drop in that evening at Mihail Makarovitch’s, so to speak casually, so as slyly to startle the eldest granddaughter, Olga Mihailovna, by showing that he knew her secret, that he knew it was her birthday, and that she was trying to conceal it on purpose, so as not to be obliged to give a dance.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치 넬류도프는 사흘 전부터 그날 저녁 미하일 마카로비치의 집에 말하자면 우연인 척 들를 작정이었으니, 맏손녀 올가 미하일로브나가 감추려 드는 비밀, 곧 그날이 그녀의 생일이며, 무도회를 열지 않으려고 일부러 그것을 숨기고 있다는 사실을 자기가 알고 있음을 넌지시 드러내어 짓궂게 그녀를 놀래 주려는 속셈이었다."], ["He anticipated a great deal of merriment, many playful jests about her age, and her being afraid to reveal it, about his knowing her secret and telling everybody, and so on.", "그는 한바탕 큰 웃음판이 벌어지리라고 기대했으니, 그녀의 나이를 두고, 그리고 그것을 밝히기를 겁내는 것을 두고, 또 자기가 그 비밀을 알아서 모두에게 떠벌리겠다는 것을 두고 갖은 익살을 부릴 작정이었다."], ["The charming young man was a great adept at such teasing; the ladies had christened him “the naughty man,” and he seemed to be delighted at the name.", "이 매력적인 젊은이는 그런 놀림에 능수능란한 사람이었으니, 부인들은 그를 \"심술꾸러기\"라고 별명 붙였는데, 그는 그 별명을 도리어 흐뭇해하는 듯했다."], ["He was extremely well-bred, however, of good family, education and feelings, and, though leading a life of pleasure, his sallies were always innocent and in good taste.", "그렇지만 그는 더없이 점잖은 사람으로, 좋은 가문에 교양과 감수성을 갖추었으며, 향락의 삶을 살면서도 그의 재담은 언제나 순수하고 품위가 있었다."], ["He was short, and delicate-looking.", "그는 키가 작고 가냘파 보였다."], ["On his white, slender, little fingers he always wore a number of big, glittering rings.", "희고 가느다란 작은 손가락마다 그는 늘 번쩍이는 큼직한 반지를 여러 개 끼고 다녔다."], ["When he was engaged in his official duties, he always became extraordinarily grave, as though realizing his position and the sanctity of the obligations laid upon him.", "공무에 임할 때면 그는 자기 위치와 자기에게 지워진 책무의 신성함을 깨닫기라도 한 듯 유난히 엄숙해졌다."], ["He had a special gift for mystifying murderers and other criminals of the peasant class during interrogation, and if he did not win their respect, he certainly succeeded in arousing their wonder.", "그는 심문 도중 살인범이나 농민 계층의 다른 범죄자들을 어리둥절하게 만드는 데 각별한 재주가 있었으니, 그들의 존경까지 얻지는 못할지언정 그들의 경탄을 자아내는 데는 분명 성공하곤 했다."]], [["Pyotr Ilyitch was simply dumbfounded when he went into the police captain’s.", "표트르 일리치는 경찰서장의 집에 들어섰을 때 그저 어안이 벙벙해질 따름이었다."], ["He saw instantly that every one knew.", "그는 모두가 이미 알고 있음을 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["They had positively thrown down their cards, all were standing up and talking.", "그들은 카드를 내팽개친 채 죄다 일어서서 떠들어 대고 있었다."], ["Even Nikolay Parfenovitch had left the young ladies and run in, looking strenuous and ready for action.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치마저 젊은 처녀들을 버려두고 달려 나와, 잔뜩 긴장한 채 당장이라도 행동에 나설 태세였다."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch was met with the astounding news that old Fyodor Pavlovitch really had been murdered that evening in his own house, murdered and robbed.", "표트르 일리치는, 늙은 표도르 파블로비치가 정말로 그날 저녁 제 집에서 살해되었다는, 살해되고 강도까지 당했다는 놀라운 소식을 전해 들었다."], ["The news had only just reached them in the following manner.", "그 소식이 그들에게 전해진 경위는 다음과 같았다."]], [["Marfa Ignatyevna, the wife of old Grigory, who had been knocked senseless near the fence, was sleeping soundly in her bed and might well have slept till morning after the draught she had taken.", "울타리 곁에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌던 늙은 그리고리의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나는 자기 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 그녀가 마신 탕약을 생각하면 능히 아침까지도 내처 잘 만했다."], ["But, all of a sudden she waked up, no doubt roused by a fearful epileptic scream from Smerdyakov, who was lying in the next room unconscious.", "그러나 별안간 그녀는 잠에서 깼는데, 옆방에 의식을 잃고 누워 있던 스메르댜코프가 내지른 무시무시한 간질 발작의 비명에 놀라 깬 것이 틀림없었다."], ["That scream always preceded his fits, and always terrified and upset Marfa Ignatyevna.", "그 비명은 언제나 그의 발작에 앞서 터져 나왔고, 언제나 마르파 이그나티예브나를 소스라치게 하고 마음을 뒤흔들어 놓았다."], ["She could never get accustomed to it.", "그녀는 결코 그 소리에 익숙해질 수 없었다."], ["She jumped up and ran half-awake to Smerdyakov’s room.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 잠이 덜 깬 채로 스메르댜코프의 방으로 달려갔다."], ["But it was dark there, and she could only hear the invalid beginning to gasp and struggle.", "그러나 그곳은 캄캄했고, 다만 그 병자가 헐떡이며 몸부림치기 시작하는 소리만 들릴 뿐이었다."], ["Then Marfa Ignatyevna herself screamed out and was going to call her husband, but suddenly realized that when she had got up, he was not beside her in bed.", "그러자 마르파 이그나티예브나 자신도 비명을 지르며 남편을 부르려 했으나, 문득 자기가 일어났을 때 그가 침대에서 곁에 없었다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["She ran back to the bedstead and began groping with her hands, but the bed was really empty.", "그녀는 침대로 되달려 가 손으로 더듬어 보았으나, 침상은 정말로 비어 있었다."], ["Then he must have gone out—where? She ran to the steps and timidly called him.", "그렇다면 그는 밖으로 나간 것이 틀림없는데—대체 어디로 갔단 말인가?"], ["She got no answer, of course, but she caught the sound of groans far away in the garden in the darkness.", "그녀는 현관 층계로 달려 나가 겁먹은 목소리로 그를 불렀다. 물론 아무 대답도 없었으나, 어둠 속 정원 저 멀리서 신음 소리가 들려오는 것을 그녀는 알아챘다."], ["She listened.", "그녀는 귀를 기울였다."], ["The groans were repeated, and it was evident they came from the garden.", "신음 소리가 거듭 들려왔고, 그것이 정원에서 나는 소리임이 분명했다."]], [["“Good Lord!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Just as it was with Lizaveta Smerdyastchaya!”", "꼭 리자베타 스메르댜시차야 때하고 똑같구먼!\""], ["she thought distractedly.", "그녀는 정신없이 그렇게 생각했다."], ["She went timidly down the steps and saw that the gate into the garden was open.", "그녀는 겁먹은 채로 층계를 내려갔고, 정원으로 통하는 쪽문이 열려 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“He must be out there, poor dear,” she thought.", "\"저 양반이 저기 나가 있는 게로구나, 가엾어라.\""], ["She went up to the gate and all at once she distinctly heard Grigory calling her by name, “Marfa!", "그녀는 그렇게 생각했다. 그녀가 쪽문으로 다가가는데, 별안간 그리고리가 자기 이름을 부르는 소리가 또렷이 들렸으니, \"마르파!"], ["Marfa!”", "마르파!\""], ["in a weak, moaning, dreadful voice.", "하고 힘없이 신음하는 듯한 끔찍한 목소리였다."]], [["“Lord, preserve us from harm!”", "\"주여, 우리를 화로부터 지켜 주소서!\""], ["Marfa Ignatyevna murmured, and ran towards the voice, and that was how she found Grigory.", "마르파 이그나티예브나는 중얼거리며 그 목소리를 향해 달려갔고, 그렇게 해서 그리고리를 찾아냈다."], ["But she found him not by the fence where he had been knocked down, but about twenty paces off.", "그러나 그녀가 그를 찾아낸 곳은 그가 얻어맞고 쓰러졌던 울타리 곁이 아니라, 거기서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 자리였다."], ["It appeared later, that he had crawled away on coming to himself, and probably had been a long time getting so far, losing consciousness several times.", "나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 그가 정신을 차리고서 기어서 그곳까지 갔으며, 거기까지 오는 데 아마도 오랜 시간이 걸렸고 도중에 몇 번이나 의식을 잃었던 모양이었다."], ["She noticed at once that he was covered with blood, and screamed at the top of her voice.", "그녀는 그가 온통 피투성이가 된 것을 단번에 알아채고 목청껏 비명을 질렀다."], ["Grigory was muttering incoherently:", "그리고리는 두서없이 중얼거리고 있었다."]], [["“He has murdered ...", "\"그놈이 죽였어…"], ["his father murdered....", "제 아비를 죽였어…"], ["Why scream, silly ... run ...", "비명은 왜 질러, 이 멍청아…"], ["fetch some one....”", "어서 달려가… 누구든 데려와…\""]], [["But Marfa continued screaming, and seeing that her master’s window was open and that there was a candle alight in the window, she ran there and began calling Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그러나 마르파는 계속 비명을 질러 댔고, 주인의 창문이 열려 있고 창가에 촛불이 켜져 있는 것을 보고는 그쪽으로 달려가 표도르 파블로비치를 부르기 시작했다."], ["But peeping in at the window, she saw a fearful sight.", "그러나 창 안을 들여다보다가 그녀는 무시무시한 광경을 보았다."], ["Her master was lying on his back, motionless, on the floor.", "그녀의 주인이 바닥에 반듯이 누워 꼼짝도 하지 않고 있었던 것이다."], ["His light-colored dressing-gown and white shirt were soaked with blood.", "그의 옅은 빛깔 실내복과 흰 셔츠는 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다."], ["The candle on the table brightly lighted up the blood and the motionless dead face of Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "탁자 위의 촛불이 그 피와, 표도르 파블로비치의 꼼짝 않는 죽은 얼굴을 환히 비추고 있었다."], ["Terror-stricken, Marfa rushed away from the window, ran out of the garden, drew the bolt of the big gate and ran headlong by the back way to the neighbor, Marya Kondratyevna.", "공포에 사로잡힌 마르파는 창가에서 뛰쳐나와 정원 밖으로 달려 나갔고, 큰 대문의 빗장을 빼고는 뒷길로 곤두박질치듯 이웃집 마리야 콘드라티예브나에게로 내달렸다."], ["Both mother and daughter were asleep, but they waked up at Marfa’s desperate and persistent screaming and knocking at the shutter.", "어머니와 딸은 둘 다 잠들어 있었으나, 마르파가 덧문을 두드리며 필사적으로 끈질기게 비명을 질러 대는 통에 잠에서 깼다."], ["Marfa, shrieking and screaming incoherently, managed to tell them the main fact, and to beg for assistance.", "마르파는 째질 듯한 소리로 두서없이 외쳐 대면서도 가까스로 핵심만은 일러 주고 도움을 청했다."], ["It happened that Foma had come back from his wanderings and was staying the night with them.", "마침 떠돌이 생활을 하던 포마가 돌아와 그 집에서 밤을 보내고 있던 참이었다."], ["They got him up immediately and all three ran to the scene of the crime.", "그들은 곧장 그를 깨웠고, 셋이서 범행 현장으로 달려갔다."], ["On the way, Marya Kondratyevna remembered that at about eight o’clock she heard a dreadful scream from their garden, and this was no doubt Grigory’s scream, “Parricide!” uttered when he caught hold of Mitya’s leg.", "가는 길에 마리야 콘드라티예브나는, 여덟 시쯤 자기네 정원에서 끔찍한 비명이 들렸던 것을 떠올렸으니, 이는 그리고리가 미탸의 다리를 붙들었을 때 내질렀던 \"아비를 죽인 놈!\"이라는 비명이 틀림없었다."]], [["“Some one person screamed out and then was silent,”", "\"누군가 한 사람이 비명을 지르고는 곧 잠잠해졌어요.\""], ["Marya Kondratyevna explained as she ran.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 달려가면서 설명했다."], ["Running to the place where Grigory lay, the two women with the help of Foma carried him to the lodge.", "그리고리가 누워 있는 곳으로 달려간 두 여인은 포마의 도움을 받아 그를 별채로 옮겼다."], ["They lighted a candle and saw that Smerdyakov was no better, that he was writhing in convulsions, his eyes fixed in a squint, and that foam was flowing from his lips.", "그들이 촛불을 켜고 보니 스메르댜코프는 조금도 나아지지 않은 채 경련으로 몸을 뒤틀고 있었고, 두 눈은 사팔뜨기처럼 한곳에 굳어 있었으며, 입술에서는 거품이 흘러내리고 있었다."], ["They moistened Grigory’s forehead with water mixed with vinegar, and the water revived him at once.", "그들은 식초를 탄 물로 그리고리의 이마를 적셨고, 그 물에 그는 이내 정신을 차렸다."], ["He asked immediately:", "그는 즉시 물었다."]], [["“Is the master murdered?”", "\"주인님이 살해당하셨소?\""]], [["Then Foma and both the women ran to the house and saw this time that not only the window, but also the door into the garden was wide open, though Fyodor Pavlovitch had for the last week locked himself in every night and did not allow even Grigory to come in on any pretext.", "그러자 포마와 두 여인은 본채로 달려갔고, 이번에는 창문뿐 아니라 정원으로 통하는 문까지 활짝 열려 있는 것을 보았으니, 표도르 파블로비치는 지난 한 주 내내 밤마다 문을 안에서 걸어 잠그고 어떤 핑계로도 그리고리조차 들이지 않던 터였다."], ["Seeing that door open, they were afraid to go in to Fyodor Pavlovitch “for fear anything should happen afterwards.”", "그 문이 열려 있는 것을 본 그들은 \"나중에 무슨 일이 생길까 두려워\" 표도르 파블로비치에게 들어가기를 꺼렸다."], ["And when they returned to Grigory, the old man told them to go straight to the police captain.", "그들이 그리고리에게 돌아오자, 노인은 곧장 경찰서장에게 가라고 일렀다."], ["Marya Kondratyevna ran there and gave the alarm to the whole party at the police captain’s.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 그리로 달려가 경찰서장 집에 모인 사람들 전체에게 변고를 알렸다."], ["She arrived only five minutes before Pyotr Ilyitch, so that his story came, not as his own surmise and theory, but as the direct confirmation, by a witness, of the theory held by all, as to the identity of the criminal (a theory he had in the bottom of his heart refused to believe till that moment).", "그녀는 표트르 일리치보다 단 오 분 앞서 당도했으니, 그리하여 그의 이야기는 그 자신의 추측이나 견해가 아니라, 범인의 신원에 관해 모두가 품고 있던 견해를(그가 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 그 순간까지도 믿기를 거부해 온 견해를) 한 목격자가 직접 확증해 주는 것으로 받아들여졌다."]], [["It was resolved to act with energy.", "힘차게 움직이기로 결정이 났다."], ["The deputy police inspector of the town was commissioned to take four witnesses, to enter Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house and there to open an inquiry on the spot, according to the regular forms, which I will not go into here.", "고을의 경찰부서장이 증인 네 사람을 데리고 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 들어가 그 자리에서 정식 절차에 따라 조사를 개시하라는 임무를 맡았으니, 그 절차에 관해서는 여기서 시시콜콜 늘어놓지 않겠다."], ["The district doctor, a zealous man, new to his work, almost insisted on accompanying the police captain, the prosecutor, and the investigating lawyer.", "군의관은 열성적인 사람이자 일이 손에 익지 않은 신참이었던 터라, 경찰서장과 검사와 예심판사를 따라가겠다고 거의 우기다시피 했다."]], [["I will note briefly that Fyodor Pavlovitch was found to be quite dead, with his skull battered in. But with what?", "간략히 적어 두자면, 표도르 파블로비치는 두개골이 박살난 채 완전히 숨이 끊어진 것으로 밝혀졌다."], ["Most likely with the same weapon with which Grigory had been attacked.", "그런데 무엇으로 그랬단 말인가? 십중팔구 그리고리를 내리쳤던 바로 그 흉기였다."], ["And immediately that weapon was found, Grigory, to whom all possible medical assistance was at once given, described in a weak and breaking voice how he had been knocked down.", "그리고 곧 그 흉기가 발견되었으니, 곧바로 가능한 모든 의료 처치를 받은 그리고리가 힘없이 끊어지는 목소리로 자기가 어떻게 얻어맞고 쓰러졌는지를 진술한 덕분이었다."], ["They began looking with a lantern by the fence and found the brass pestle dropped in a most conspicuous place on the garden path.", "그들이 등불을 들고 울타리 곁을 살피기 시작하자, 정원 오솔길의 더없이 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 떨어져 있는 놋쇠 절굿공이가 발견되었다."], ["There were no signs of disturbance in the room where Fyodor Pavlovitch was lying.", "표도르 파블로비치가 누워 있던 방에는 다투거나 흐트러진 흔적이 없었다."], ["But by the bed, behind the screen, they picked up from the floor a big and thick envelope with the inscription: “A present of three thousand roubles for my angel Grushenka, if she is willing to come.”", "그러나 침대 곁, 가리개 뒤편에서 그들은 바닥에 떨어진 크고 두툼한 봉투 하나를 주워 들었는데, 거기에는 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"나의 천사 그루셴카가 와 주겠다면, 그녀에게 줄 삼천 루블 선물.\""], ["And below had been added by Fyodor Pavlovitch, “For my little chicken.”", "그리고 그 아래에는 표도르 파블로비치가 덧붙여 쓴 글이 있었으니, \"나의 귀여운 병아리에게\"라고 적혀 있었다."], ["There were three seals of red sealing-wax on the envelope, but it had been torn open and was empty: the money had been removed.", "봉투에는 붉은 봉랍으로 찍은 봉인이 세 개 있었으나, 이미 뜯겨 텅 비어 있었으니, 돈은 빼내어진 뒤였다."], ["They found also on the floor a piece of narrow pink ribbon, with which the envelope had been tied up.", "그들은 또한 봉투를 묶었던 가느다란 분홍 리본 한 조각을 바닥에서 발견했다."]], [["One piece of Pyotr Ilyitch’s evidence made a great impression on the prosecutor and the investigating magistrate, namely, his idea that Dmitri Fyodorovitch would shoot himself before daybreak, that he had resolved to do so, had spoken of it to Ilyitch, had taken the pistols, loaded them before him, written a letter, put it in his pocket, etc.", "표트르 일리치의 진술 가운데 한 대목이 검사와 예심판사에게 큰 인상을 남겼으니, 곧 드미트리 표도로비치가 동트기 전에 스스로 목숨을 끊으리라는 그의 생각이었다. 미탸가 그렇게 하기로 작정했으며, 그것을 일리치에게 말했고, 권총을 챙겨 그가 보는 앞에서 장전했으며, 편지를 써서 호주머니에 넣었다는 등의 이야기였다."], ["When Pyotr Ilyitch, though still unwilling to believe in it, threatened to tell some one so as to prevent the suicide, Mitya had answered grinning: “You’ll be too late.”", "표트르 일리치가, 그것을 믿고 싶지 않으면서도, 자살을 막으려고 누군가에게 알리겠다고 으르대자, 미탸는 히죽 웃으며 이렇게 대답했다는 것이다. \"너무 늦을걸.\""], ["So they must make haste to Mokroe to find the criminal, before he really did shoot himself.", "그러니 그가 정말로 스스로를 쏘아 버리기 전에 서둘러 모크로예로 가서 범인을 찾아야만 했다."]], [["“That’s clear, that’s clear!”", "\"분명하군, 분명해!\""], ["repeated the prosecutor in great excitement.", "검사는 잔뜩 흥분하여 거듭 외쳤다. \"그런 미치광이 같은 자들이 꼭 그렇지."], ["“That’s just the way with mad fellows like that: ‘I shall kill myself to-morrow, so I’ll make merry till I die!’”", "'내일 자살할 테니, 죽기 전에 한바탕 흥청거려 보자!' 하는 식이거든.\""]], [["The story of how he had bought the wine and provisions excited the prosecutor more than ever.", "그가 어떻게 포도주와 음식을 사들였는가 하는 이야기는 검사를 그 어느 때보다 흥분시켰다."]], [["“Do you remember the fellow that murdered a merchant called Olsufyev, gentlemen?", "\"여러분, 올수피예프라는 상인을 죽인 그 작자를 기억하시오?"], ["He stole fifteen hundred, went at once to have his hair curled, and then, without even hiding the money, carrying it almost in his hand in the same way, he went off to the girls.”", "그놈은 천오백 루블을 훔치고는 곧장 이발소로 가서 머리를 지졌고, 그러고는 돈을 숨기지도 않은 채, 그것을 거의 손에 든 그대로 들고서 계집들한테로 가 버렸지요.\""]], [["All were delayed, however, by the inquiry, the search, and the formalities, etc., in the house of Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "그러나 표도르 파블로비치의 집에서 진행된 조사와 수색, 갖가지 절차 따위 때문에 모두가 발이 묶였다."], ["It all took time and so, two hours before starting, they sent on ahead to Mokroe the officer of the rural police, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch Schmertsov, who had arrived in the town the morning before to get his pay.", "그 일에는 시간이 걸렸으므로, 출발하기 두 시간 전에 그들은 시골 경찰관 마브리키 마브리키예비치 시메르초프를 모크로예로 먼저 보냈는데, 그는 봉급을 타려고 그 전날 아침 고을에 와 있던 참이었다."], ["He was instructed to avoid raising the alarm when he reached Mokroe, but to keep constant watch over the “criminal” till the arrival of the proper authorities, to procure also witnesses for the arrest, police constables, and so on.", "그는 모크로예에 닿거든 소동을 일으키지 말고, 정식 당국자들이 도착할 때까지 \"범인\"을 끊임없이 감시하며, 아울러 체포에 필요한 증인과 순경 따위를 확보해 두라는 지시를 받았다."], ["Mavriky Mavrikyevitch did as he was told, preserving his incognito, and giving no one but his old acquaintance, Trifon Borissovitch, the slightest hint of his secret business.", "마브리키 마브리키예비치는 지시받은 대로, 신분을 감춘 채 오랜 지기인 트리폰 보리소비치 외에는 아무에게도 자기 비밀 임무에 대해 손톱만큼의 낌새도 내비치지 않았다."], ["He had spoken to him just before Mitya met the landlord in the balcony, looking for him in the dark, and noticed at once a change in Trifon Borissovitch’s face and voice.", "그가 그에게 말을 건넨 것은, 미탸가 어둠 속에서 그를 찾다가 발코니에서 그 주인과 마주치기 바로 직전이었으니, 미탸는 그 즉시 트리폰 보리소비치의 얼굴빛과 목소리가 달라진 것을 알아챘다."], ["So neither Mitya nor any one else knew that he was being watched.", "그러나 미탸도, 그 누구도 자기가 감시당하고 있다는 것은 알지 못했다."], ["The box with the pistols had been carried off by Trifon Borissovitch and put in a suitable place.", "권총이 든 상자는 트리폰 보리소비치가 가져가 적당한 곳에 치워 두었다."], ["Only after four o’clock, almost at sunrise, all the officials, the police captain, the prosecutor, the investigating lawyer, drove up in two carriages, each drawn by three horses.", "네 시가 지나서야, 거의 해 뜰 무렵에야 모든 관리들, 곧 경찰서장과 검사와 예심판사가 각각 세 마리 말이 끄는 두 대의 마차에 나누어 타고 당도했다."], ["The doctor remained at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s to make a post-mortem next day on the body.", "군의관은 이튿날 시신을 부검하기 위해 표도르 파블로비치의 집에 남았다."], ["But he was particularly interested in the condition of the servant, Smerdyakov.", "그러나 그가 각별히 흥미를 느낀 것은 하인 스메르댜코프의 상태였다."]], [["“Such violent and protracted epileptic fits, recurring continually for twenty-four hours, are rarely to be met with, and are of interest to science,”", "\"이토록 격렬하고 오래 끄는 간질 발작이 스물네 시간 동안 끊임없이 되풀이되는 경우는 좀처럼 보기 드물어서, 학문적으로 흥미로운 사례입니다.\""], ["he declared enthusiastically to his companions, and as they left they laughingly congratulated him on his find.", "그는 동행들에게 열을 올려 단언했고, 그들은 떠나면서 그의 진귀한 발견을 두고 웃으며 축하해 주었다."], ["The prosecutor and the investigating lawyer distinctly remembered the doctor’s saying that Smerdyakov could not outlive the night.", "검사와 예심판사는, 스메르댜코프가 이 밤을 넘기지 못하리라던 군의관의 말을 또렷이 기억하고 있었다."]], [["After these long, but I think necessary explanations, we will return to that moment of our tale at which we broke off.", "이처럼 길었으나 내 생각으로는 꼭 필요했던 설명을 마쳤으니, 이제 우리는 이야기를 끊었던 그 대목으로 되돌아가기로 하자."]], [["# Chapter III. The Sufferings Of A Soul, The First Ordeal", "# Chapter III. The Sufferings Of A Soul, The First Ordeal"]], [["And so Mitya sat looking wildly at the people round him, not understanding what was said to him.", "그리하여 미탸는 주위 사람들을 멍하니 바라보며 앉아 있었고, 자기에게 무슨 말을 하는지조차 알아듣지 못했다."], ["Suddenly he got up, flung up his hands, and shouted aloud:", "갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 치켜들고 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“I’m not guilty!", "\"나는 결백하오!"], ["I’m not guilty of that blood!", "그 피에는 죄가 없소!"], ["I’m not guilty of my father’s blood....", "아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없단 말이오……. 죽이려고는 했소."], ["I meant to kill him.", "하지만 나는 결백하오."], ["But I’m not guilty. Not I.”", "내가 한 짓이 아니오.\""]], [["But he had hardly said this, before Grushenka rushed from behind the curtain and flung herself at the police captain’s feet.", "그러나 그가 미처 이 말을 끝맺기도 전에 그루셴카가 휘장 뒤에서 뛰쳐나와 경찰서장의 발치에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["“It was my fault!", "\"이건 제 탓이에요!"], ["Mine!", "제 탓!"], ["My wickedness!”", "제가 사악해서예요!\""], ["she cried, in a heartrending voice, bathed in tears, stretching out her clasped hands towards them.", "그녀는 눈물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 가슴이 미어지는 목소리로 외치며, 맞잡은 두 손을 그들에게 내밀었다."], ["“He did it through me.", "\"이이는 저 때문에 그런 거예요."], ["I tortured him and drove him to it.", "제가 이이를 들볶아서 이 지경으로 몰아넣었어요."], ["I tortured that poor old man that’s dead, too, in my wickedness, and brought him to this!", "제 사악함 때문에 저 가엾은 노인까지, 이제는 죽고 만 그 양반까지 들볶아서 이 꼴로 만든 거예요!"], ["It’s my fault, mine first, mine most, my fault!”", "제 탓이에요, 무엇보다 제 탓, 가장 큰 제 탓, 제 죄예요!\""]], [["“Yes, it’s your fault!", "\"그래, 네 탓이지!"], ["You’re the chief criminal!", "네가 주범이야! 이 독부 같은 것!"], ["You fury! You harlot!", "이 음탕한 계집!"], ["You’re the most to blame!”", "죄가 가장 큰 건 너야!\""], ["shouted the police captain, threatening her with his hand.", "경찰서장이 손을 들어 그녀를 위협하며 소리쳤다."], ["But he was quickly and resolutely suppressed.", "그러나 그는 곧장 단호하게 제지당했다."], ["The prosecutor positively seized hold of him.", "검사가 그를 다짜고짜 붙들어 버린 것이다."]], [["“This is absolutely irregular, Mihail Makarovitch!”", "\"이건 전혀 규정에 어긋납니다, 미하일 마카로비치!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“You are positively hindering the inquiry....", "\"당신은 지금 심리를 방해하고 있습니다……."], ["You’re ruining the case....”", "사건을 망치고 있단 말이오…….\""], ["he almost gasped.", "그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."]], [["“Follow the regular course!", "\"규정대로 합시다!"], ["Follow the regular course!”", "규정대로!\""], ["cried Nikolay Parfenovitch, fearfully excited too, “otherwise it’s absolutely impossible!...”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치도 몹시 흥분하여 소리쳤다. \"그러지 않으면 전혀 진행이 불가능하단 말입니다!…….\""]], [["“Judge us together!”", "\"우리를 함께 심판하세요!\""], ["Grushenka cried frantically, still kneeling.", "그루셴카가 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채 미친 듯이 외쳤다."], ["“Punish us together.", "\"우리를 함께 벌하세요."], ["I will go with him now, if it’s to death!”", "지금 당장 이이와 함께 가겠어요, 그게 죽음이라 해도!\""]], [["“Grusha, my life, my blood, my holy one!”", "\"그루샤, 내 목숨, 내 피, 내 성스러운 사람!\""], ["Mitya fell on his knees beside her and held her tight in his arms.", "미탸가 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇으며 두 팔로 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["“Don’t believe her,”", "\"이 사람 말을 믿지 마시오.\""], ["he cried, “she’s not guilty of anything, of any blood, of anything!”", "그가 외쳤다. \"이 사람은 아무 죄도 없소, 어떤 피에 대해서도, 그 무엇에 대해서도!\""]], [["He remembered afterwards that he was forcibly dragged away from her by several men, and that she was led out, and that when he recovered himself he was sitting at the table.", "그는 나중에야 자기가 몇몇 사내들의 손에 힘으로 그녀에게서 떼어내어졌다는 것과, 그녀가 끌려 나갔다는 것과, 정신을 차리고 보니 자기가 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었다는 것을 기억해냈다."], ["Beside him and behind him stood the men with metal plates.", "그의 곁과 등 뒤에는 금속 표찰을 단 사내들이 서 있었다."], ["Facing him on the other side of the table sat Nikolay Parfenovitch, the investigating lawyer.", "탁자 맞은편에는 예심판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 앉아 있었다."], ["He kept persuading him to drink a little water out of a glass that stood on the table.", "그는 미탸에게 탁자 위에 놓인 잔의 물을 좀 마시라고 거듭 권했다."]], [["“That will refresh you, that will calm you.", "\"이걸 마시면 기운이 날 거고, 마음이 가라앉을 겁니다."], ["Be calm, don’t be frightened,”", "진정하세요, 겁먹지 마시고.\""], ["he added, extremely politely.", "그가 더없이 정중하게 덧붙였다."], ["Mitya (he remembered it afterwards) became suddenly intensely interested in his big rings, one with an amethyst, and another with a transparent bright yellow stone, of great brilliance.", "미탸는(이것도 나중에 기억해냈다) 갑자기 그의 커다란 반지들에 무서울 만큼 정신이 팔렸으니, 하나에는 자수정이, 다른 하나에는 투명하고 밝은 노란 보석이 박혀 눈부시게 빛나고 있었다."], ["And long afterwards he remembered with wonder how those rings had riveted his attention through all those terrible hours of interrogation, so that he was utterly unable to tear himself away from them and dismiss them, as things that had nothing to do with his position.", "그리고 훗날 그는 그 반지들이 그 끔찍한 심문의 시간 내내 어쩌면 그렇게도 자기 주의를 사로잡았는지, 그래서 자기 처지와는 아무 상관도 없는 물건이건만 도무지 거기서 시선을 떼어 떨쳐낼 수 없었던 것을 의아한 마음으로 회상하곤 했다."], ["On Mitya’s left side, in the place where Maximov had been sitting at the beginning of the evening, the prosecutor was now seated, and on Mitya’s right hand, where Grushenka had been, was a rosy-cheeked young man in a sort of shabby hunting-jacket, with ink and paper before him.", "미탸의 왼쪽, 그날 저녁 처음에 막시모프가 앉아 있던 자리에는 이제 검사가 앉아 있었고, 미탸의 오른쪽, 그루셴카가 앉아 있던 자리에는 허름한 사냥 재킷 같은 것을 걸친 발그레한 뺨의 젊은이가 앞에 잉크와 종이를 놓고 앉아 있었다."], ["This was the secretary of the investigating lawyer, who had brought him with him.", "이 사람은 예심판사가 데려온 서기였다."], ["The police captain was now standing by the window at the other end of the room, beside Kalganov, who was sitting there.", "경찰서장은 이제 방 반대편 끝 창가에 서 있었고, 그 옆에는 칼가노프가 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Drink some water,”", "\"물을 좀 드세요.\""], ["said the investigating lawyer softly, for the tenth time.", "예심판사가 벌써 열 번째로 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“I have drunk it, gentlemen, I have ...", "\"마셨소, 여러분, 마셨다니까……."], ["but ... come, gentlemen, crush me, punish me, decide my fate!”", "그러나……, 자, 여러분, 나를 짓밟으시오, 벌하시오, 내 운명을 결정하시오!\""], ["cried Mitya, staring with terribly fixed wide-open eyes at the investigating lawyer.", "미탸가 무섭게 고정된 채 크게 뜬 눈으로 예심판사를 노려보며 외쳤다."]], [["“So you positively declare that you are not guilty of the death of your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch?”", "\"그러니까 당신은 부친 표도르 파블로비치의 죽음에 대해 결백하다고 단호히 선언하는 거지요?\""], ["asked the investigating lawyer, softly but insistently.", "예심판사가 부드럽지만 집요하게 물었다."]], [["“I am not guilty.", "\"나는 결백하오."], ["I am guilty of the blood of another old man but not of my father’s.", "나는 다른 노인의 피에 대해서는 죄가 있으나 아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없소."], ["And I weep for it!", "그리고 그 일로 통곡하고 있소!"], ["I killed, I killed the old man and knocked him down....", "내가 죽였소, 내가 그 노인을 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸소……."], ["But it’s hard to have to answer for that murder with another, a terrible murder of which I am not guilty....", "하지만 그 살인을 또 다른 살인, 내가 결백한 그 끔찍한 살인까지 뒤집어쓰고 대답해야 한다니 이건 견디기 힘든 일이오……."], ["It’s a terrible accusation, gentlemen, a knock-down blow.", "끔찍한 혐의요, 여러분, 사람을 때려눕히는 일격이오."], ["But who has killed my father, who has killed him?", "그런데 누가 내 아버지를 죽였단 말이오, 대체 누가?"], ["Who can have killed him if I didn’t?", "내가 아니라면 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소?"], ["It’s marvelous, extraordinary, impossible.”", "이건 기이하고, 터무니없고, 있을 수 없는 일이오.\""]], [["“Yes, who can have killed him?”", "\"그렇소, 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소?\""], ["the investigating lawyer was beginning, but Ippolit Kirillovitch, the prosecutor, glancing at him, addressed Mitya.", "예심판사가 입을 떼려 했으나, 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 그를 흘긋 보더니 미탸에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“You need not worry yourself about the old servant, Grigory Vassilyevitch.", "\"늙은 하인 그리고리 바실리예비치에 대해서는 걱정할 것 없습니다."], ["He is alive, he has recovered, and in spite of the terrible blows inflicted, according to his own and your evidence, by you, there seems no doubt that he will live, so the doctor says, at least.”", "그는 살아 있고, 회복했으며, 자신과 당신의 진술에 따르면 당신이 가한 끔찍한 타격에도 불구하고 살아날 것이 거의 틀림없어 보입니다, 적어도 의사 말로는 그렇소.\""]], [["“Alive? He’s alive?”", "\"살아 있다고?"], ["cried Mitya, flinging up his hands.", "그가 살아 있어?\" 미탸가 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤다."], ["His face beamed.", "그의 얼굴이 환하게 빛났다."], ["“Lord, I thank Thee for the miracle Thou has wrought for me, a sinner and evildoer.", "\"주여, 죄인이요 악행을 저지른 저를 위해 베푸신 기적에 감사드리나이다."], ["That’s an answer to my prayer.", "이것은 제 기도에 대한 응답입니다."], ["I’ve been praying all night.”", "저는 밤새도록 기도했습니다.\""], ["And he crossed himself three times.", "그러고는 세 번 성호를 그었다."], ["He was almost breathless.", "그는 거의 숨이 차오를 지경이었다."]], [["“So from this Grigory we have received such important evidence concerning you, that—”", "\"그래서 이 그리고리한테서 우리는 당신에 관한 매우 중요한 증언을 받았는데, 그것은—\""], ["The prosecutor would have continued, but Mitya suddenly jumped up from his chair.", "검사가 말을 이으려 했으나, 미탸가 갑자기 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“One minute, gentlemen, for God’s sake, one minute; I will run to her—”", "\"잠깐만, 여러분, 제발, 잠깐만; 그녀에게 달려가게 해주시오—\""]], [["“Excuse me, at this moment it’s quite impossible,”", "\"미안하지만, 지금 이 순간엔 전혀 불가능합니다.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch almost shrieked.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 거의 비명을 지르듯 말했다."], ["He, too, leapt to his feet.", "그도 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Mitya was seized by the men with the metal plates, but he sat down of his own accord....", "미탸는 금속 표찰을 단 사내들에게 붙들렸으나, 스스로 자리에 앉았다……."]], [["“Gentlemen, what a pity!", "\"여러분, 참으로 안타깝구려!"], ["I wanted to see her for one minute only; I wanted to tell her that it has been washed away, it has gone, that blood that was weighing on my heart all night, and that I am not a murderer now!", "단 일 분만 그녀를 보고 싶었는데; 내 가슴을 밤새도록 짓누르던 그 피가 씻겨 내려갔다고, 사라졌다고, 그래서 이제 나는 살인자가 아니라고 그녀에게 말해주고 싶었소!"], ["Gentlemen, she is my betrothed!”", "여러분, 그녀는 내 약혼녀요!\""], ["he said ecstatically and reverently, looking round at them all.", "그는 그들 모두를 둘러보며 황홀하고도 경건하게 말했다."], ["“Oh, thank you, gentlemen!", "\"아, 고맙소, 여러분!"], ["Oh, in one minute you have given me new life, new heart!...", "아, 단 일 분 만에 여러분은 내게 새 생명을, 새 가슴을 주셨소!……."], ["That old man used to carry me in his arms, gentlemen.", "그 노인은 나를 두 팔로 안아주곤 했소, 여러분."], ["He used to wash me in the tub when I was a baby three years old, abandoned by every one, he was like a father to me!...”", "내가 세 살배기 아기였을 때, 모두에게 버림받았을 때 그는 함지에 나를 씻겨주곤 했소, 그는 내게 아버지나 다름없었소!…….\""]], [["“And so you—”", "\"그래서 당신은—\""], ["the investigating lawyer began.", "예심판사가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Allow me, gentlemen, allow me one minute more,”", "\"잠깐만요, 여러분, 일 분만 더 주시오.\""], ["interposed Mitya, putting his elbows on the table and covering his face with his hands.", "미탸가 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 끼어들었다."], ["“Let me have a moment to think, let me breathe, gentlemen.", "\"잠시 생각할 틈을 주시오, 숨 좀 쉬게 해주시오, 여러분."], ["All this is horribly upsetting, horribly.", "이 모든 게 끔찍하게 사람을 뒤흔들어 놓는구려, 끔찍하게도."], ["A man is not a drum, gentlemen!”", "사람은 북이 아니란 말이오, 여러분!\""]], [["“Drink a little more water,”", "\"물을 좀 더 드세요.\""], ["murmured Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Mitya took his hands from his face and laughed.", "미탸는 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼고 웃었다."], ["His eyes were confident.", "그의 눈에는 자신감이 어려 있었다."], ["He seemed completely transformed in a moment.", "그는 한순간에 완전히 딴사람이 된 듯했다."], ["His whole bearing was changed; he was once more the equal of these men, with all of whom he was acquainted, as though they had all met the day before, when nothing had happened, at some social gathering.", "태도가 송두리째 달라졌으니, 그는 다시 한번 이 사람들과 대등한 사람이 되었던바, 그들 모두와 안면이 있었으며, 마치 아무 일도 없던 어제 어느 사교 모임에서 다 함께 만났던 것만 같았다."], ["We may note in passing that, on his first arrival, Mitya had been made very welcome at the police captain’s, but later, during the last month especially, Mitya had hardly called at all, and when the police captain met him, in the street, for instance, Mitya noticed that he frowned and only bowed out of politeness.", "지나가는 김에 짚어두자면, 미탸가 처음 이 고장에 왔을 때는 경찰서장 댁에서 무척 환대를 받았으나, 나중에, 특히 지난 한 달 동안은 거의 발길을 끊다시피 했고, 경찰서장이 이를테면 길에서 그를 마주칠 때면 미탸는 그가 눈살을 찌푸리며 예의상으로만 인사하는 것을 알아챘다."], ["His acquaintance with the prosecutor was less intimate, though he sometimes paid his wife, a nervous and fanciful lady, visits of politeness, without quite knowing why, and she always received him graciously and had, for some reason, taken an interest in him up to the last.", "검사와의 친분은 그보다 못했으나, 미탸는 가끔 그의 아내를, 신경질적이고 공상이 많은 부인을 까닭도 잘 모른 채 예의상 찾아가곤 했고, 그녀는 언제나 그를 정중히 맞아주었으며 무슨 까닭에선지 마지막까지 그에게 관심을 두고 있었다."], ["He had not had time to get to know the investigating lawyer, though he had met him and talked to him twice, each time about the fair sex.", "예심판사와는 친해질 겨를이 없었으나, 두 번 만나 이야기를 나눈 적이 있었고, 그때마다 화제는 여인들에 관한 것이었다."]], [["“You’re a most skillful lawyer, I see, Nikolay Parfenovitch,”", "\"보아하니 당신은 대단히 노련한 법관이시군요, 니콜라이 파르페노비치.\""], ["cried Mitya, laughing gayly, “but I can help you now.", "미탸가 유쾌하게 웃으며 외쳤다. \"하지만 이제 내가 당신을 도울 수 있소."], ["Oh, gentlemen, I feel like a new man, and don’t be offended at my addressing you so simply and directly.", "아, 여러분, 나는 새사람이 된 기분이오, 내가 이렇게 허물없이 직설적으로 말을 거는 걸 언짢게 여기지 마시오."], ["I’m rather drunk, too, I’ll tell you that frankly.", "게다가 나는 좀 취했소, 솔직히 말해두겠소."], ["I believe I’ve had the honor and pleasure of meeting you, Nikolay Parfenovitch, at my kinsman Miüsov’s.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치, 당신을 내 친척 미우소프의 집에서 뵙는 영광과 기쁨을 누린 적이 있는 것 같소."], ["Gentlemen, gentlemen, I don’t pretend to be on equal terms with you.", "여러분, 여러분, 나는 당신들과 대등하다고 자처하지는 않소."], ["I understand, of course, in what character I am sitting before you.", "물론 내가 어떤 처지로 당신들 앞에 앉아 있는지 잘 알고 있소."], ["Oh, of course, there’s a horrible suspicion ... hanging over me ...", "아, 물론, 끔찍한 혐의가……, 내게 씌워져 있소……, 그리고리가 증언을 했다면 말이오……."], ["if Grigory has given evidence....", "끔찍한 혐의요!"], ["A horrible suspicion! It’s awful, awful, I understand that!", "무섭소, 무서워, 나도 그건 잘 알고 있소!"], ["But to business, gentlemen, I am ready, and we will make an end of it in one moment; for, listen, listen, gentlemen!", "하지만 본론으로 들어갑시다, 여러분, 나는 준비가 되어 있고, 우리는 단숨에 이걸 끝낼 수 있소; 들어보시오, 들어보시오, 여러분!"], ["Since I know I’m innocent, we can put an end to it in a minute.", "내가 결백하다는 걸 알고 있는 이상, 우리는 단번에 끝낼 수 있소."], ["Can’t we?", "안 그렇소?"], ["Can’t we?”", "안 그렇소?\""]], [["Mitya spoke much and quickly, nervously and effusively, as though he positively took his listeners to be his best friends.", "미탸는 많은 말을 빠르게, 신경질적이면서도 격정적으로 쏟아냈으니, 마치 듣는 사람들을 자기의 둘도 없는 벗으로 단단히 여기는 듯했다."]], [["“So, for the present, we will write that you absolutely deny the charge brought against you,”", "\"그럼 우선, 당신은 자신에게 씌워진 혐의를 단호히 부인한다고 적겠습니다.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, impressively, and bending down to the secretary he dictated to him in an undertone what to write.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 무게 있게 말하더니, 서기 쪽으로 몸을 숙여 무엇을 쓸지 낮은 목소리로 받아쓰게 했다."]], [["“Write it down?", "\"적는다고?"], ["You want to write that down?", "그걸 적겠다는 거요?"], ["Well, write it; I consent, I give my full consent, gentlemen, only ...", "좋소, 적으시오; 동의하오, 전적으로 동의하오, 여러분, 다만……, 알겠소?……."], ["do you see?... Stay, stay, write this.", "가만, 가만, 이것도 적으시오."], ["Of disorderly conduct I am guilty, of violence on a poor old man I am guilty.", "난동을 부린 죄라면 나는 유죄요, 가엾은 노인에게 폭력을 휘두른 죄라면 나는 유죄요."], ["And there is something else at the bottom of my heart, of which I am guilty, too—but that you need not write down” (he turned suddenly to the secretary); “that’s my personal life, gentlemen, that doesn’t concern you, the bottom of my heart, that’s to say....", "그리고 내 가슴 밑바닥에 또 다른, 내가 죄지은 무언가가 있소—하지만 그건 적을 필요 없소\"(그는 갑자기 서기 쪽으로 돌아섰다); \"그건 내 사적인 삶이오, 여러분, 당신들과는 상관없는 일이오, 그러니까 내 가슴 밑바닥의 일이란 말이오……."], ["But of the murder of my old father I’m not guilty.", "하지만 내 늙은 아버지를 죽인 죄에 대해서는 나는 결백하오."], ["That’s a wild idea.", "그건 터무니없는 생각이오."], ["It’s quite a wild idea!...", "정말이지 터무니없는 생각이오!……."], ["I will prove you that and you’ll be convinced directly....", "내 그걸 당신들에게 증명해 보이겠소, 그럼 곧장 납득하게 될 거요……."], ["You will laugh, gentlemen.", "당신들은 웃을 거요, 여러분."], ["You’ll laugh yourselves at your suspicion!...”", "당신들 자신이 품은 혐의를 두고 스스로 웃게 될 거요!…….\""]], [["“Be calm, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"진정하세요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["said the investigating lawyer evidently trying to allay Mitya’s excitement by his own composure.", "예심판사가 자신의 침착함으로 미탸의 흥분을 가라앉히려는 듯이 말했다."], ["“Before we go on with our inquiry, I should like, if you will consent to answer, to hear you confirm the statement that you disliked your father, Fyodor Pavlovitch, that you were involved in continual disputes with him.", "\"심리를 계속하기 전에, 만약 대답해주신다면, 한 가지 진술을 당신 입으로 확인해 듣고 싶소, 곧 당신이 부친 표도르 파블로비치를 싫어했고, 그와 끊임없는 다툼에 휘말려 있었다는 것 말이오."], ["Here at least, a quarter of an hour ago, you exclaimed that you wanted to kill him: ‘I didn’t kill him,’ you said, ‘but I wanted to kill him.’”", "적어도 여기서, 십오 분 전에, 당신은 그를 죽이고 싶었다고 외쳤소. '내가 그를 죽이지는 않았다'고 당신은 말했소, '그러나 죽이고 싶었다'고 말이오.\""]], [["“Did I exclaim that?", "\"내가 그렇게 외쳤소?"], ["Ach, that may be so, gentlemen!", "아아, 그랬을지도 모르겠소, 여러분!"], ["Yes, unhappily, I did want to kill him ...", "그렇소, 불행히도 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소……."], ["many times I wanted to ... unhappily, unhappily!”", "여러 번이나 죽이고 싶었소……, 불행하게도, 불행하게도!\""]], [["“You wanted to.", "\"죽이고 싶었다고요."], ["Would you consent to explain what motives precisely led you to such a sentiment of hatred for your parent?”", "정확히 어떤 동기에서 그렇게 친부에 대한 증오의 감정에 이르게 되었는지 설명해주시겠소?\""]], [["“What is there to explain, gentlemen?”", "\"설명할 게 뭐 있겠소, 여러분?\""], ["Mitya shrugged his shoulders sullenly, looking down.", "미탸가 시무룩하게 어깨를 으쓱하며 눈을 내리깔았다."], ["“I have never concealed my feelings.", "\"나는 한 번도 내 감정을 숨긴 적이 없소."], ["All the town knows about it—every one knows in the tavern.", "온 고을이 그걸 알고 있고—술집에서는 누구나 다 알고 있소."], ["Only lately I declared them in Father Zossima’s cell....", "바로 얼마 전에도 나는 조시마 장로의 암자에서 그 감정을 털어놨소……."], ["And the very same day, in the evening I beat my father.", "그리고 바로 그날, 저녁에 나는 아버지를 두들겨 팼소."], ["I nearly killed him, and I swore I’d come again and kill him, before witnesses....", "죽일 뻔했고, 다시 와서 죽이겠다고 증인들 앞에서 맹세까지 했소……."], ["Oh, a thousand witnesses!", "아, 천 명의 증인이 있소!"], ["I’ve been shouting it aloud for the last month, any one can tell you that!...", "나는 지난 한 달 내내 그걸 큰 소리로 떠들고 다녔소, 누구든 당신들에게 말해줄 거요!……."], ["The fact stares you in the face, it speaks for itself, it cries aloud, but feelings, gentlemen, feelings are another matter.", "사실은 당신들 눈앞에 빤히 드러나 있고, 그 자체로 말하고 있으며, 크게 외쳐대고 있소, 하지만 감정은, 여러분, 감정은 별개의 문제요."], ["You see, gentlemen”—Mitya frowned—“it seems to me that about feelings you’ve no right to question me.", "보시오, 여러분\"—미탸는 눈살을 찌푸렸다—\"감정에 관해서는 당신들에게 나를 심문할 권리가 없는 것 같소."], ["I know that you are bound by your office, I quite understand that, but that’s my affair, my private, intimate affair, yet ... since I haven’t concealed my feelings in the past ...", "당신들이 직무에 매여 있다는 건 나도 아오, 그건 충분히 이해하오, 하지만 그건 내 일이오, 내 사적이고 내밀한 일이오, 그렇긴 하나……, 과거에 나는 내 감정을 숨기지 않았으니……, 이를테면 술집에서 누구에게나 다 떠들어댔으니까, 그러니……, 그러니 지금도 그걸 비밀로 하지는 않겠소."], ["in the tavern, for instance, I’ve talked to every one, so ... so I won’t make a secret of it now.", "보시오, 여러분, 나도 이 사건에서 나한테 불리한 끔찍한 사실들이 있다는 걸 이해하오."], ["You see, I understand, gentlemen, that there are terrible facts against me in this business.", "나는 그를 죽이겠다고 누구에게나 말하고 다녔고, 이제 별안간 그가 살해됐소."], ["I told every one that I’d kill him, and now, all of a sudden, he’s been killed. So it must have been me!", "그러니 내가 한 짓임에 틀림없다는 거지!"], ["Ha ha!", "하하!"], ["I can make allowances for you, gentlemen, I can quite make allowances.", "나는 당신들을 이해할 수 있소, 여러분, 충분히 이해할 수 있단 말이오."], ["I’m struck all of a heap myself, for who can have murdered him, if not I?", "나 자신도 망연자실하오, 내가 아니라면 대체 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠소?"], ["That’s what it comes to, isn’t it?", "결국 그렇게 되는 거 아니오?"], ["If not I, who can it be, who?", "내가 아니라면 누구일 수 있겠소, 누구?"], ["Gentlemen, I want to know, I insist on knowing!”", "여러분, 나도 알고 싶소, 꼭 알아야겠소!\""], ["he exclaimed suddenly.", "그가 별안간 외쳤다."], ["“Where was he murdered?", "\"그는 어디서 살해됐소?"], ["How was he murdered?", "어떻게 살해됐소?"], ["How, and with what?", "어떻게, 무엇으로?"], ["Tell me,”", "말해주시오.\""], ["he asked quickly, looking at the two lawyers.", "그가 두 법관을 바라보며 다급하게 물었다."]], [["“We found him in his study, lying on his back on the floor, with his head battered in,”", "\"우리는 그를 서재에서 발견했는데, 머리가 박살난 채 바닥에 반듯이 누워 있었소.\""], ["said the prosecutor.", "검사가 말했다."]], [["“That’s horrible!”", "\"끔찍하구려!\""], ["Mitya shuddered and, putting his elbows on the table, hid his face in his right hand.", "미탸가 몸서리를 치며 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 오른손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“We will continue,”", "\"계속합시다.\""], ["interposed Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다."], ["“So what was it that impelled you to this sentiment of hatred?", "\"그래서 무엇이 당신을 그런 증오의 감정으로 몰아갔소?"], ["You have asserted in public, I believe, that it was based upon jealousy?”", "당신은 그것이 질투에 근거한 것이라고 공공연히 주장했다고 알고 있는데요?\""]], [["“Well, yes, jealousy.", "\"그렇소, 질투요."], ["And not only jealousy.”", "그리고 질투만은 아니오.\""]], [["“Disputes about money?”", "\"돈을 둘러싼 다툼이었소?\""]], [["“Yes, about money, too.”", "\"그렇소, 돈에 관한 것이기도 했소.\""]], [["“There was a dispute about three thousand roubles, I think, which you claimed as part of your inheritance?”", "\"제 생각엔 삼천 루블을 둘러싼 다툼이 있었던 것 같은데, 당신이 그걸 상속분의 일부라고 주장했지요?\""]], [["“Three thousand!", "\"삼천이라!"], ["More, more,”", "더, 더 되오.\""], ["cried Mitya hotly; “more than six thousand, more than ten, perhaps.", "미탸가 격하게 외쳤다. \"육천도 넘고, 어쩌면 만 루블도 넘소."], ["I told every one so, shouted it at them.", "누구에게나 그렇게 말했고, 그들에게 소리쳐댔소."], ["But I made up my mind to let it go at three thousand.", "하지만 나는 삼천 루블로 끝내기로 마음먹었소."], ["I was desperately in need of that three thousand ... so the bundle of notes for three thousand that I knew he kept under his pillow, ready for Grushenka, I considered as simply stolen from me.", "그 삼천 루블이 절박하게 필요했소……, 그래서 그가 그루셴카를 위해 베개 밑에 넣어두고 있던 그 삼천 루블 지폐 뭉치를 나는 그냥 나한테서 도둑맞은 것으로 여겼소."], ["Yes, gentlemen, I looked upon it as mine, as my own property....”", "그렇소, 여러분, 나는 그걸 내 것으로, 내 재산으로 여겼소…….\""]], [["The prosecutor looked significantly at the investigating lawyer, and had time to wink at him on the sly.", "검사는 의미심장하게 예심판사를 바라보았고, 슬쩍 그에게 눈짓을 보낼 틈도 있었다."]], [["“We will return to that subject later,”", "\"그 문제는 나중에 다시 다루겠소.\""], ["said the lawyer promptly.", "예심판사가 곧바로 말했다."], ["“You will allow us to note that point and write it down; that you looked upon that money as your own property?”", "\"그 점을 적어두는 걸 허락해주시겠소; 곧 당신이 그 돈을 자기 재산으로 여겼다는 점 말이오?\""]], [["“Write it down, by all means.", "\"얼마든지 적으시오."], ["I know that’s another fact that tells against me, but I’m not afraid of facts and I tell them against myself.", "그것 역시 나한테 불리한 또 하나의 사실이라는 걸 알지만, 나는 사실을 두려워하지 않고 나 스스로 나에게 불리한 사실을 말하는 거요."], ["Do you hear?", "듣고 있소?"], ["Do you know, gentlemen, you take me for a different sort of man from what I am,”", "알겠소, 여러분, 당신들은 나를 실제의 나와는 다른 부류의 인간으로 여기고 있소.\""], ["he added, suddenly gloomy and dejected.", "그가 갑자기 침울하고 풀이 죽어 덧붙였다."], ["“You have to deal with a man of honor, a man of the highest honor; above all—don’t lose sight of it—a man who’s done a lot of nasty things, but has always been, and still is, honorable at bottom, in his inner being.", "\"당신들이 상대하는 건 명예를 아는 사람, 더없이 명예를 아는 사람이오; 무엇보다도—이걸 놓치지 마시오—더러운 짓을 많이 저질렀으나 그 밑바닥, 그 내면에서는 언제나 명예로웠고 지금도 명예로운 사람이란 말이오."], ["I don’t know how to express it.", "어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠소."], ["That’s just what’s made me wretched all my life, that I yearned to be honorable, that I was, so to say, a martyr to a sense of honor, seeking for it with a lantern, with the lantern of Diogenes, and yet all my life I’ve been doing filthy things like all of us, gentlemen ... that is like me alone.", "바로 그게 내 평생을 비참하게 만든 거요, 내가 명예롭게 되기를 갈망했다는 것, 말하자면 명예 의식의 순교자였다는 것, 등불을 들고, 디오게네스의 등불을 들고 그걸 찾아 헤맸다는 것이오, 그러면서도 평생 우리 모두처럼 더러운 짓을 저질러왔다는 것이오, 여러분……, 그러니까 나 혼자만 그랬다는 거요."], ["That was a mistake, like me alone, me alone!...", "그게 잘못이었소, 나 혼자만, 나 혼자만!……."], ["Gentlemen, my head aches ...”", "여러분, 머리가 아프구려…….\""], ["His brows contracted with pain.", "그의 눈썹이 고통으로 찡그러졌다."], ["“You see, gentlemen, I couldn’t bear the look of him, there was something in him ignoble, impudent, trampling on everything sacred, something sneering and irreverent, loathsome, loathsome.", "\"보시오, 여러분, 나는 그자의 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없었소, 그자에게는 무언가 비천하고, 뻔뻔하고, 온갖 신성한 것을 짓밟는, 무언가 비웃는 듯하고 불경한, 역겨운, 역겨운 데가 있었소."], ["But now that he’s dead, I feel differently.”", "하지만 이제 그가 죽고 나니, 느낌이 다르구려.\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“I don’t feel differently, but I wish I hadn’t hated him so.”", "\"느낌이 다르다기보다는, 그를 그렇게 미워하지 않았더라면 좋았을 거라는 거요.\""]], [["“You feel penitent?”", "\"뉘우치는 거요?\""]], [["“No, not penitent, don’t write that.", "\"아니, 뉘우치는 건 아니오, 그건 적지 마시오."], ["I’m not much good myself, I’m not very beautiful, so I had no right to consider him repulsive.", "나 자신도 별로 잘난 게 없고, 그리 아름다운 인간도 못 되니, 그자를 역겹다고 여길 권리가 없었다는 거요."], ["That’s what I mean.", "내 말은 그거요."], ["Write that down, if you like.”", "정 그렇다면 그걸 적으시오.\""]], [["Saying this Mitya became very mournful.", "이렇게 말하면서 미탸는 무척 침울해졌다."], ["He had grown more and more gloomy as the inquiry continued.", "심리가 이어질수록 그는 점점 더 우울해졌다."]], [["At that moment another unexpected scene followed.", "바로 그때 또 한 차례 뜻밖의 장면이 벌어졌다."], ["Though Grushenka had been removed, she had not been taken far away, only into the room next but one from the blue room, in which the examination was proceeding.", "그루셴카는 끌려 나가긴 했으나 멀리 데려가지는 않았고, 심문이 진행 중인 파란 방에서 한 칸 건넛방에 있었다."], ["It was a little room with one window, next beyond the large room in which they had danced and feasted so lavishly.", "그곳은 창이 하나 달린 작은 방으로, 그들이 그토록 흥청거리며 춤추고 잔치를 벌인 큰 방 바로 옆이었다."], ["She was sitting there with no one by her but Maximov, who was terribly depressed, terribly scared, and clung to her side, as though for security.", "그녀는 그곳에 앉아 있었는데 곁에는 막시모프밖에 없었으니, 그는 몹시 의기소침하고 무섭게 겁에 질려, 마치 안전을 구하듯 그녀 옆에 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["At their door stood one of the peasants with a metal plate on his breast.", "그들의 문가에는 가슴에 금속 표찰을 단 농부 하나가 서 있었다."], ["Grushenka was crying, and suddenly her grief was too much for her, she jumped up, flung up her arms and, with a loud wail of sorrow, rushed out of the room to him, to her Mitya, and so unexpectedly that they had not time to stop her.", "그루셴카는 울고 있었는데, 갑자기 슬픔을 주체하지 못하고 벌떡 일어나 두 팔을 치켜들더니, 비통하게 큰 소리로 울부짖으며 방에서 뛰쳐나가 그에게로, 자기의 미탸에게로 달려갔으니, 어찌나 갑작스러웠던지 그들이 그녀를 막을 틈도 없었다."], ["Mitya, hearing her cry, trembled, jumped up, and with a yell rushed impetuously to meet her, not knowing what he was doing.", "미탸는 그녀의 울음소리를 듣자 부르르 떨더니 벌떡 일어나, 비명을 지르며 자기가 무얼 하는지도 모른 채 맹렬히 그녀를 맞으러 달려갔다."], ["But they were not allowed to come together, though they saw one another.", "그러나 두 사람은 서로를 보기는 했어도 가까이 가는 것은 허락되지 않았다."], ["He was seized by the arms.", "그는 양팔이 붙들렸다."], ["He struggled, and tried to tear himself away.", "그는 몸부림치며 떨쳐내려 했다."], ["It took three or four men to hold him.", "그를 붙드는 데 서너 명이 달라붙어야 했다."], ["She was seized too, and he saw her stretching out her arms to him, crying aloud as they carried her away.", "그녀도 붙들렸는데, 그는 그녀가 자기를 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 끌려가는 동안 큰 소리로 울부짖는 모습을 보았다."], ["When the scene was over, he came to himself again, sitting in the same place as before, opposite the investigating lawyer, and crying out to them:", "그 소동이 끝나자 그는 다시 정신을 차렸으니, 이전과 같은 자리에, 예심판사 맞은편에 앉은 채 그들을 향해 외치고 있었다."]], [["“What do you want with her?", "\"그녀를 어쩌겠다는 거요?"], ["Why do you torment her?", "왜 그녀를 괴롭히는 거요?"], ["She’s done nothing, nothing!...”", "그녀는 아무 짓도 하지 않았소, 아무 짓도!…….\""]], [["The lawyers tried to soothe him.", "법관들은 그를 달래려 했다."], ["About ten minutes passed like this.", "그렇게 십 분쯤 지났다."], ["At last Mihail Makarovitch, who had been absent, came hurriedly into the room, and said in a loud and excited voice to the prosecutor:", "마침내 자리를 비웠던 미하일 마카로비치가 황급히 방으로 들어와, 크고 흥분된 목소리로 검사에게 말했다."]], [["“She’s been removed, she’s downstairs.", "\"그녀는 옮겨놨소, 아래층에 있소."], ["Will you allow me to say one word to this unhappy man, gentlemen?", "여러분, 이 가엾은 사람에게 한마디만 하게 해주시겠소?"], ["In your presence, gentlemen, in your presence.”", "당신들이 보는 앞에서 말이오, 여러분, 당신들이 보는 앞에서.\""]], [["“By all means, Mihail Makarovitch,”", "\"얼마든지요, 미하일 마카로비치.\""], ["answered the investigating lawyer.", "예심판사가 대답했다."], ["“In the present case we have nothing against it.”", "\"지금 경우에는 그것을 반대할 이유가 없소.\""]], [["“Listen, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, my dear fellow,”", "\"이보게, 드미트리 표도로비치, 여보게.\""], ["began the police captain, and there was a look of warm, almost fatherly, feeling for the luckless prisoner on his excited face.", "경찰서장이 입을 열었는데, 흥분한 그의 얼굴에는 이 불운한 피고를 향한 따뜻한, 거의 부정(父情)에 가까운 감정이 어려 있었다."], ["“I took your Agrafena Alexandrovna downstairs myself, and confided her to the care of the landlord’s daughters, and that old fellow Maximov is with her all the time.", "\"내가 직접 자네의 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 아래층으로 데려가서 주인집 딸들에게 보살피라고 맡겨두었고, 그 막시모프라는 노인이 줄곧 그녀 곁에 붙어 있네."], ["And I soothed her, do you hear?", "그리고 내가 그녀를 달랬네, 알겠나?"], ["I soothed and calmed her.", "달래고 진정시켜 주었어."], ["I impressed on her that you have to clear yourself, so she mustn’t hinder you, must not depress you, or you may lose your head and say the wrong thing in your evidence.", "자네가 결백을 밝혀야 하니 그녀가 자네를 방해해선 안 되고, 자네 기를 꺾어선 안 된다고, 안 그러면 자네가 정신을 잃고 증언에서 엉뚱한 말을 할 수도 있다고 단단히 일러두었네."], ["In fact, I talked to her and she understood.", "사실 그녀와 이야기를 나눴더니 그녀가 알아듣더군."], ["She’s a sensible girl, my boy, a good-hearted girl, she would have kissed my old hands, begging help for you.", "그녀는 분별 있는 처자야, 여보게, 마음씨 고운 처자야, 자네를 도와달라며 이 늙은 손에 입이라도 맞출 기세였다네."], ["She sent me herself, to tell you not to worry about her.", "그녀가 직접 나를 보냈네, 자기 걱정은 말라고 자네에게 전해달라고 말일세."], ["And I must go, my dear fellow, I must go and tell her that you are calm and comforted about her.", "그러니 나는 가봐야 하네, 여보게, 가서 자네가 그녀 때문에 마음 놓고 진정해 있다고 전해줘야겠네."], ["And so you must be calm, do you understand?", "그러니 자네도 진정해야 하네, 알겠나?"], ["I was unfair to her; she is a Christian soul, gentlemen, yes, I tell you, she’s a gentle soul, and not to blame for anything.", "내가 그녀에게 부당하게 굴었어; 그녀는 그리스도교도의 영혼을 지녔어, 여러분, 그렇소, 분명히 말하지만, 그녀는 온순한 영혼이고 아무 잘못도 없소."], ["So what am I to tell her, Dmitri Fyodorovitch?", "그래, 그녀에게 뭐라고 전할까, 드미트리 표도로비치?"], ["Will you sit quiet or not?”", "가만히 앉아 있겠나, 안 그러겠나?\""]], [["The good-natured police captain said a great deal that was irregular, but Grushenka’s suffering, a fellow creature’s suffering, touched his good-natured heart, and tears stood in his eyes.", "이 마음씨 좋은 경찰서장은 규정에 어긋나는 말을 잔뜩 했지만, 그루셴카의 고통이, 한 동포의 고통이 그의 마음씨 고운 가슴을 울렸고, 그의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."], ["Mitya jumped up and rushed towards him.", "미탸는 벌떡 일어나 그에게 달려갔다."]], [["“Forgive me, gentlemen, oh, allow me, allow me!”", "\"용서하시오, 여러분, 아, 허락해주시오, 허락해주시오!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“You’ve the heart of an angel, an angel, Mihail Makarovitch, I thank you for her.", "\"당신은 천사의 마음을 지녔소, 천사 말이오, 미하일 마카로비치, 그녀를 위해 감사드리오."], ["I will, I will be calm, cheerful, in fact.", "진정하겠소, 정말로 진정하고 명랑해지겠소."], ["Tell her, in the kindness of your heart, that I am cheerful, quite cheerful, that I shall be laughing in a minute, knowing that she has a guardian angel like you.", "부디 그 다정한 마음으로 그녀에게 전해주시오, 내가 명랑하다고, 아주 명랑하다고, 그녀에게 당신 같은 수호천사가 있다는 걸 알았으니 곧 웃게 되리라고."], ["I shall have done with all this directly, and as soon as I’m free, I’ll be with her, she’ll see, let her wait.", "나는 이걸 곧 끝낼 거고, 풀려나는 대로 그녀에게 갈 거요, 두고 보면 알 거요, 기다리라고 하시오."], ["Gentlemen,”", "여러분.\""], ["he said, turning to the two lawyers, “now I’ll open my whole soul to you; I’ll pour out everything.", "그가 두 법관 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이제 당신들에게 내 영혼을 송두리째 열어 보이겠소; 모든 걸 쏟아내겠소."], ["We’ll finish this off directly, finish it off gayly.", "우리는 이걸 곧장 끝낼 거요, 명랑하게 끝낼 거요."], ["We shall laugh at it in the end, shan’t we?", "결국 우리는 이걸 두고 웃게 될 거요, 안 그렇소?"], ["But, gentlemen, that woman is the queen of my heart.", "그러나 여러분, 저 여인은 내 마음의 여왕이오."], ["Oh, let me tell you that.", "아, 이 말만은 하게 해주시오."], ["That one thing I’ll tell you now....", "지금 이 한 가지만은 말해두겠소……."], ["I see I’m with honorable men.", "보아하니 나는 명예로운 분들과 함께 있구려."], ["She is my light, she is my holy one, and if only you knew!", "그녀는 내 빛이고, 내 성스러운 사람이오, 당신들이 알기만 한다면!"], ["Did you hear her cry, ‘I’ll go to death with you’?", "그녀가 '당신과 함께 죽음으로 가겠다'고 외치는 걸 들었소?"], ["And what have I, a penniless beggar, done for her?", "그런데 한 푼 없는 거지인 내가 그녀를 위해 무얼 해주었단 말이오?"], ["Why such love for me?", "어째서 나를 그토록 사랑하는 거요?"], ["How can a clumsy, ugly brute like me, with my ugly face, deserve such love, that she is ready to go to exile with me?", "나처럼 굼뜨고 못생긴 짐승 같은 인간이, 이 추한 얼굴을 하고서 어떻게 그런 사랑을 받을 자격이 있어, 그녀가 나와 함께 유형길에 오를 각오까지 한단 말이오?"], ["And how she fell down at your feet for my sake, just now!...", "그리고 조금 전에 나를 위해 어떻게 당신들 발치에 엎드렸는지!……."], ["and yet she’s proud and has done nothing!", "그런데도 그녀는 자존심이 강하고 아무 잘못도 없소!"], ["How can I help adoring her, how can I help crying out and rushing to her as I did just now?", "내가 어찌 그녀를 흠모하지 않을 수 있겠소, 어찌 외치며 조금 전처럼 그녀에게 달려가지 않을 수 있겠소?"], ["Gentlemen, forgive me!", "여러분, 용서하시오!"], ["But now, now I am comforted.”", "그러나 이제, 이제 나는 위안을 얻었소.\""]], [["And he sank back in his chair and, covering his face with his hands, burst into tears.", "그러고는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["But they were happy tears.", "그러나 그것은 행복한 눈물이었다."], ["He recovered himself instantly.", "그는 곧장 정신을 차렸다."], ["The old police captain seemed much pleased, and the lawyers also.", "늙은 경찰서장은 무척 흐뭇해 보였고, 법관들도 그러했다."], ["They felt that the examination was passing into a new phase.", "그들은 심문이 새로운 국면으로 접어들고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["When the police captain went out, Mitya was positively gay.", "경찰서장이 나가자 미탸는 정말이지 명랑해졌다."]], [["“Now, gentlemen, I am at your disposal, entirely at your disposal.", "\"자, 여러분, 이제 나는 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소, 전적으로 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소."], ["And if it were not for all these trivial details, we should understand one another in a minute.", "그리고 이 사소한 곁가지들만 없다면, 우리는 단번에 서로를 이해할 수 있을 거요. 또 그 곁가지 타령이군."], ["I’m at those details again. I’m at your disposal, gentlemen, but I declare that we must have mutual confidence, you in me and I in you, or there’ll be no end to it.", "나는 당신들 처분에 맡기겠소, 여러분, 다만 우리는 서로 믿어야 한다는 걸 분명히 해두겠소, 당신들이 나를, 내가 당신들을 믿어야 한다는 말이오, 안 그러면 이건 끝이 없을 거요."], ["I speak in your interests.", "나는 당신들을 위해 하는 말이오."], ["To business, gentlemen, to business, and don’t rummage in my soul; don’t tease me with trifles, but only ask me about facts and what matters, and I will satisfy you at once.", "본론으로 갑시다, 여러분, 본론으로, 그리고 내 영혼을 함부로 뒤지지 마시오; 사소한 것들로 나를 들볶지 말고, 다만 사실과 중요한 것만 물으시오, 그러면 당장 흡족하게 해드리겠소."], ["And damn the details!”", "곁가지 따위는 집어치웁시다!\""]], [["So spoke Mitya.", "미탸는 이렇게 말했다."], ["The interrogation began again.", "심문이 다시 시작되었다."]], [["# Chapter IV. The Second Ordeal", "# Chapter IV. The Second Ordeal"]], [["“You don’t know how you encourage us, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, by your readiness to answer,”", "\"드미트리 표도로비치, 당신이 이렇게 선뜻 대답해 주시니 우리가 얼마나 힘을 얻는지 모르실 겁니다.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, with an animated air, and obvious satisfaction beaming in his very prominent, short-sighted, light gray eyes, from which he had removed his spectacles a moment before.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 활기 띤 태도로 말했는데, 조금 전에 안경을 벗은 그 유난히 불거진, 근시의, 연회색 눈에는 만족감이 또렷이 빛나고 있었다."], ["“And you have made a very just remark about the mutual confidence, without which it is sometimes positively impossible to get on in cases of such importance, if the suspected party really hopes and desires to defend himself and is in a position to do so.", "\"그리고 당신은 상호 신뢰에 관해 아주 옳은 지적을 하셨습니다. 이렇게 중대한 사건에서는, 혐의를 받는 자가 진심으로 자기를 변호하기를 바라고 또 그럴 처지에 있다면, 그 신뢰 없이는 일을 진척시키기가 때로 도저히 불가능하지요."], ["We, on our side, will do everything in our power, and you can see for yourself how we are conducting the case.", "우리 쪽에서도 할 수 있는 일은 무엇이든 다 할 것이며, 우리가 이 사건을 어떻게 다루는지는 당신 스스로도 보실 수 있습니다."], ["You approve, Ippolit Kirillovitch?”", "동의하십니까, 이폴리트 키릴로비치?\""], ["He turned to the prosecutor.", "그는 검사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Oh, undoubtedly,”", "\"오, 두말할 나위가 없지요.\""], ["replied the prosecutor.", "검사가 대답했다."], ["His tone was somewhat cold, compared with Nikolay Parfenovitch’s impulsiveness.", "그의 어조는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 충동적인 태도에 견주면 다소 차가운 데가 있었다."]], [["I will note once for all that Nikolay Parfenovitch, who had but lately arrived among us, had from the first felt marked respect for Ippolit Kirillovitch, our prosecutor, and had become almost his bosom friend.", "여기서 한 번에 일러 두어야 할 것이 있다. 얼마 전에야 우리 고장으로 부임해 온 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 처음부터 우리 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치에게 각별한 존경심을 품었고, 거의 그의 막역한 벗이 되어 있었다."], ["He was almost the only person who put implicit faith in Ippolit Kirillovitch’s extraordinary talents as a psychologist and orator and in the justice of his grievance.", "그는 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 심리학자이자 웅변가로서의 비범한 재능과, 그가 품은 불만의 정당함을 전적으로 믿어 의심치 않은 거의 유일한 사람이었다. 그는 페테르부르크에 있을 적에 이미 그의 소문을 들었던 것이다."], ["He had heard of him in Petersburg.", "한편 우리의 이 \"인정받지 못한\""], ["On the other hand, young Nikolay Parfenovitch was the only person in the whole world whom our “unappreciated” prosecutor genuinely liked.", "검사가 온 세상에서 진정으로 좋아한 단 한 사람이 바로 이 젊은 니콜라이 파르페노비치였다."], ["On their way to Mokroe they had time to come to an understanding about the present case.", "모크로예로 오는 길에 두 사람은 이번 사건에 관해 의견을 맞출 시간이 있었다."], ["And now as they sat at the table, the sharp-witted junior caught and interpreted every indication on his senior colleague’s face—half a word, a glance, or a wink.", "그래서 지금 탁자에 마주 앉은 자리에서, 명민한 후배는 선배 동료의 얼굴에 떠오르는 온갖 기색을—반쯤 흘린 말이며, 눈짓이며, 윙크 하나하나까지—놓치지 않고 알아채어 그 뜻을 헤아렸다."]], [["“Gentlemen, only let me tell my own story and don’t interrupt me with trivial questions and I’ll tell you everything in a moment,”", "\"여러분, 제 이야기를 제 식대로 하게만 해 주시고 시시한 질문으로 끊지만 마십시오. 그러면 당장 모든 걸 다 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["said Mitya excitedly.", "미탸가 흥분하여 말했다."]], [["“Excellent!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["Thank you.", "감사합니다."], ["But before we proceed to listen to your communication, will you allow me to inquire as to another little fact of great interest to us?", "그런데 당신의 진술을 듣기에 앞서, 우리에게 무척 흥미로운 또 다른 자그마한 사실 하나를 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?"], ["I mean the ten roubles you borrowed yesterday at about five o’clock on the security of your pistols, from your friend, Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin.”", "다름이 아니라 어제 다섯 시 무렵 당신이 친구인 표트르 일리치 페르호틴에게서 권총을 담보로 십 루블을 빌리셨다는 일 말입니다.\""]], [["“I pledged them, gentlemen.", "\"전당을 잡혔지요, 여러분."], ["I pledged them for ten roubles.", "십 루블에 전당을 잡혔습니다."], ["What more? That’s all about it.", "더 무엇이 있겠습니까?"], ["As soon as I got back to town I pledged them.”", "그게 전부입니다. 시내로 돌아오자마자 전당을 잡혔지요.\""]], [["“You got back to town?", "\"시내로 돌아오셨다고요?"], ["Then you had been out of town?”", "그럼 시외로 나가 계셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I went a journey of forty versts into the country.", "\"그렇습니다. 사십 베르스타나 떨어진 시골로 갔다 왔지요."], ["Didn’t you know?”", "모르셨습니까?\""]], [["The prosecutor and Nikolay Parfenovitch exchanged glances.", "검사와 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 서로 눈짓을 주고받았다."]], [["“Well, how would it be if you began your story with a systematic description of all you did yesterday, from the morning onwards?", "\"그럼 어제 아침부터 차례차례, 당신이 한 일을 빠짐없이 조리 있게 묘사하는 것으로 이야기를 시작하시는 게 어떻겠습니까?"], ["Allow us, for instance, to inquire why you were absent from the town, and just when you left and when you came back—all those facts.”", "가령 당신이 어째서 시내를 비웠는지, 또 언제 떠났다가 언제 돌아왔는지—그런 사실들을 우리가 여쭙도록 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["“You should have asked me like that from the beginning,”", "\"처음부터 그렇게 물으셨어야지요.\" 미탸가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["cried Mitya, laughing aloud, “and, if you like, we won’t begin from yesterday, but from the morning of the day before; then you’ll understand how, why, and where I went.", "\"정 원하신다면 어제부터가 아니라 그저께 아침부터 시작합시다. 그러면 내가 어떻게, 왜, 어디로 갔는지를 아시게 될 겁니다."], ["I went the day before yesterday, gentlemen, to a merchant of the town, called Samsonov, to borrow three thousand roubles from him on safe security.", "여러분, 나는 그저께 이 고을의 삼소노프라는 상인에게 갔습니다. 확실한 담보를 잡히고 그에게서 삼천 루블을 빌리려 했지요."], ["It was a pressing matter, gentlemen, it was a sudden necessity.”", "급한 일이었습니다, 여러분. 별안간 닥친 필요였지요.\""]], [["“Allow me to interrupt you,”", "\"잠깐 끊겠습니다.\""], ["the prosecutor put in politely.", "검사가 정중하게 끼어들었다."], ["“Why were you in such pressing need for just that sum, three thousand?”", "\"어째서 하필 그 액수, 삼천 루블이 그렇게 급하게 필요하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, gentlemen, you needn’t go into details, how, when and why, and why just so much money, and not so much, and all that rigmarole.", "\"오, 여러분, 어떻게, 언제, 왜, 어째서 하필 그만큼의 돈이고 그보다 적지도 많지도 않은가, 그런 잡다한 사정까지 들추실 건 없습니다."], ["Why, it’ll run to three volumes, and then you’ll want an epilogue!”", "그러다간 세 권짜리 책이 될 테고, 그러고도 후기까지 붙여야 할 겁니다!\""]], [["Mitya said all this with the good-natured but impatient familiarity of a man who is anxious to tell the whole truth and is full of the best intentions.", "미탸는 이 모든 말을, 온전한 진실을 다 털어놓으려는 마음에 더없이 선의로 가득 찬 사람 특유의 사람 좋으면서도 조바심 어린 허물없는 투로 했다."]], [["“Gentlemen!”—he corrected himself hurriedly—“don’t be vexed with me for my restiveness, I beg you again.", "\"여러분!\" 그가 황급히 자신을 고쳐 잡으며 말했다."], ["Believe me once more, I feel the greatest respect for you and understand the true position of affairs.", "\"내가 가만있지 못하는 걸 두고 노여워 마시기를 다시 한번 청합니다. 거듭 믿어 주십시오, 나는 여러분에게 더없는 존경을 품고 있으며 사태의 참된 형편을 이해하고 있습니다."], ["Don’t think I’m drunk.", "내가 취했다고는 여기지 마십시오."], ["I’m quite sober now.", "지금은 아주 말짱합니다."], ["And, besides, being drunk would be no hindrance.", "게다가 취했다 한들 거리낄 것도 없지요."], ["It’s with me, you know, like the saying: ‘When he is sober, he is a fool; when he is drunk, he is a wise man.’ Ha ha!", "나로 말하자면, 아시다시피 그 속담 그대로입니다. '맨정신일 땐 바보요, 취하면 현자라.'"], ["But I see, gentlemen, it’s not the proper thing to make jokes to you, till we’ve had our explanation, I mean.", "하하! 그런데 여러분, 보아하니 우리의 해명이 끝나기 전엔, 그러니까 그 전엔 여러분께 농담을 늘어놓는 게 마땅한 일이 아니로군요."], ["And I’ve my own dignity to keep up, too.", "또 나도 지켜야 할 체면이라는 게 있고요."], ["I quite understand the difference for the moment.", "지금 이 순간의 처지가 다르다는 건 잘 압니다."], ["I am, after all, in the position of a criminal, and so, far from being on equal terms with you.", "어쨌거나 나는 죄인의 처지에 놓여 있고, 그러니 여러분과 대등한 처지와는 거리가 멉니다."], ["And it’s your business to watch me.", "그리고 나를 감시하는 것이 여러분의 직무지요."], ["I can’t expect you to pat me on the head for what I did to Grigory, for one can’t break old men’s heads with impunity.", "내가 그리고리에게 한 짓을 두고 머리를 쓰다듬어 주길 바랄 수야 없는 노릇입니다. 노인의 머리통을 무사히 깨뜨릴 수는 없는 법이니까요."], ["I suppose you’ll put me away for him for six months, or a year perhaps, in a house of correction.", "짐작컨대 그 일로 나를 반년쯤, 아니면 한 일 년쯤 교화소에 처넣으시겠지요."], ["I don’t know what the punishment is—but it will be without loss of the rights of my rank, without loss of my rank, won’t it?", "형벌이 어떻게 되는지는 모르겠습니다만—그래도 신분상의 권리를 잃거나, 내 신분을 잃지는 않겠지요?"], ["So you see, gentlemen, I understand the distinction between us....", "그러니 보십시오, 여러분, 나는 우리 사이의 차이를 잘 알고 있습니다……"], ["But you must see that you could puzzle God Himself with such questions.", "하지만 그런 질문들로는 하느님조차 쩔쩔매게 만들 수 있다는 걸 여러분도 아셔야 합니다."], ["‘How did you step?", "'어떻게 발을 디뎠나?"], ["Where did you step?", "어디에 발을 디뎠나?"], ["When did you step?", "언제 발을 디뎠나?"], ["And on what did you step?’ I shall get mixed up, if you go on like this, and you will put it all down against me.", "무엇을 밟았나?' 이런 식으로 계속하시면 나는 뒤죽박죽이 될 테고, 여러분은 그걸 모조리 나에게 불리하게 적어 두시겠지요."], ["And what will that lead to?", "그러면 그게 무엇으로 이어지겠습니까?"], ["To nothing!", "아무것도 아닙니다!"], ["And even if it’s nonsense I’m talking now, let me finish, and you, gentlemen, being men of honor and refinement, will forgive me!", "그리고 설령 지금 내가 떠드는 게 헛소리라 해도, 끝까지 말하게 해 주십시오. 그러면 여러분은, 명예롭고 교양 있는 분들이시니, 나를 용서해 주실 겁니다!"], ["I’ll finish by asking you, gentlemen, to drop that conventional method of questioning.", "끝으로 여러분께 한 가지 청하건대, 그 틀에 박힌 심문 방식은 좀 버려 주십시오."], ["I mean, beginning from some miserable trifle, how I got up, what I had for breakfast, how I spat, and where I spat, and so distracting the attention of the criminal, suddenly stun him with an overwhelming question, ‘Whom did you murder?", "그러니까 무슨 보잘것없는 사소한 것에서부터 시작해서, 내가 어떻게 일어났는지, 아침으로 무엇을 먹었는지, 어떻게 침을 뱉었는지, 어디에 침을 뱉었는지, 그렇게 죄인의 주의를 흐트러뜨려 놓고는 느닷없이 압도적인 질문으로 머리를 후려치는 거 말입니다. '누구를 죽였느냐?"], ["Whom did you rob?’ Ha ha!", "누구를 털었느냐?' 하하!"], ["That’s your regulation method, that’s where all your cunning comes in.", "그게 여러분의 규정된 방식이고, 거기에 여러분의 온갖 술수가 들어 있지요."], ["You can put peasants off their guard like that, but not me.", "그런 식으로 농사꾼들의 경계를 풀어 놓을 수야 있겠지만, 나한테는 안 통합니다."], ["I know the tricks.", "나도 그 수법을 다 압니다."], ["I’ve been in the service, too.", "나도 관직에 있어 봤거든요."], ["Ha ha ha!", "하하하!"], ["You’re not angry, gentlemen?", "노여워하지 않으시지요, 여러분?"], ["You forgive my impertinence?”", "내 무례를 용서하시겠지요?\""], ["he cried, looking at them with a good-nature that was almost surprising.", "그는 거의 놀라울 정도의 사람 좋은 낯빛으로 그들을 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["“It’s only Mitya Karamazov, you know, so you can overlook it.", "\"그저 미탸 카라마조프니까요, 아시다시피, 그러니 너그러이 봐주시면 됩니다."], ["It would be inexcusable in a sensible man; but you can forgive it in Mitya.", "분별 있는 사람이라면 변명의 여지가 없겠지만, 미탸에게는 용서가 되는 거지요."], ["Ha ha!”", "하하!\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch listened, and laughed too.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 귀를 기울이며 함께 웃었다."], ["Though the prosecutor did not laugh, he kept his eyes fixed keenly on Mitya, as though anxious not to miss the least syllable, the slightest movement, the smallest twitch of any feature of his face.", "검사는 웃지 않았으나, 한 음절도, 아주 미세한 움직임도, 그의 얼굴 어느 한구석의 가장 작은 경련 하나도 놓치지 않으려는 듯 미탸에게서 날카로운 시선을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“That’s how we have treated you from the beginning,”", "\"우리는 처음부터 당신을 그렇게 대해 왔습니다.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, still laughing.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 여전히 웃으면서 말했다."], ["“We haven’t tried to put you out by asking how you got up in the morning and what you had for breakfast.", "\"아침에 어떻게 일어났는지, 아침으로 무얼 먹었는지 따위를 물어 당신을 곤혹스럽게 만들려 들지 않았지요."], ["We began, indeed, with questions of the greatest importance.”", "우리는 오히려 가장 중대한 질문들로 시작하지 않았습니까.\""]], [["“I understand.", "\"이해합니다."], ["I saw it and appreciated it, and I appreciate still more your present kindness to me, an unprecedented kindness, worthy of your noble hearts.", "그것을 보았고 또 고맙게 여겼지요. 그리고 지금 나에게 베푸시는 친절은 더더욱 고맙게 여깁니다."], ["We three here are gentlemen, and let everything be on the footing of mutual confidence between educated, well-bred people, who have the common bond of noble birth and honor.", "그건 여러분의 고결한 마음에 어울리는, 유례없는 친절입니다. 여기 우리 셋은 다 신사이니, 모든 것을 교양 있고 잘 자란 사람들 사이의 상호 신뢰의 바탕 위에 놓읍시다."], ["In any case, allow me to look upon you as my best friends at this moment of my life, at this moment when my honor is assailed.", "고귀한 혈통과 명예라는 공통의 끈으로 맺어진 사람들 말입니다. 어쨌든 이 순간만큼은, 내 명예가 공격받고 있는 내 생애의 이 순간만큼은, 여러분을 나의 가장 좋은 벗으로 여기게 해 주십시오."], ["That’s no offense to you, gentlemen, is it?”", "그렇다고 여러분께 무례가 되는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“On the contrary.", "\"천만에요."], ["You’ve expressed all that so well, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "모든 걸 참으로 훌륭하게 표현하셨습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch answered with dignified approbation.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 위엄 있는 동의를 담아 대답했다."]], [["“And enough of those trivial questions, gentlemen, all those tricky questions!”", "\"그러니 그 시시한 질문들은 이제 그만, 여러분, 그 까다로운 질문들은 다 그만둡시다!\""], ["cried Mitya enthusiastically.", "미탸가 신이 나서 외쳤다."], ["“Or there’s simply no knowing where we shall get to!", "\"안 그러면 도대체 우리가 어디까지 가게 될지 알 수가 없지요!"], ["Is there?”", "안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“I will follow your sensible advice entirely,”", "\"당신의 현명한 충고를 전적으로 따르겠습니다.\""], ["the prosecutor interposed, addressing Mitya.", "검사가 미탸에게 말하며 끼어들었다."], ["“I don’t withdraw my question, however.", "\"하지만 내 질문을 거두지는 않겠습니다."], ["It is now vitally important for us to know exactly why you needed that sum, I mean precisely three thousand.”", "어째서 당신에게 그 액수가, 그러니까 바로 삼천 루블이 필요했는지를 정확히 아는 것이 지금 우리에게는 무엇보다 중요합니다.\""]], [["“Why I needed it?...", "\"왜 그게 필요했느냐고요?……"], ["Oh, for one thing and another....", "오, 이런저런 일 때문에……"], ["Well, it was to pay a debt.”", "뭐, 빚을 갚으려던 거였습니다.\""]], [["“A debt to whom?”", "\"누구에게 진 빚이지요?\""]], [["“That I absolutely refuse to answer, gentlemen.", "\"그건 절대로 대답하지 않겠습니다, 여러분."], ["Not because I couldn’t, or because I shouldn’t dare, or because it would be damaging, for it’s all a paltry matter and absolutely trifling, but—I won’t, because it’s a matter of principle: that’s my private life, and I won’t allow any intrusion into my private life.", "대답할 수 없어서도, 감히 못 해서도, 또 그게 나에게 해가 되어서도 아닙니다. 그건 다 하찮은 일이고 더없이 사소한 거니까요."], ["That’s my principle.", "다만—말하지 않겠습니다. 그건 원칙의 문제이기 때문이지요."], ["Your question has no bearing on the case, and whatever has nothing to do with the case is my private affair.", "그건 내 사생활이고, 나는 내 사생활에 그 누구도 끼어드는 걸 허락하지 않습니다. 그게 내 원칙입니다."], ["I wanted to pay a debt.", "당신의 질문은 이 사건과 아무 상관이 없고, 사건과 상관없는 것은 무엇이든 내 사사로운 일입니다. 나는 빚을 갚으려 했습니다."], ["I wanted to pay a debt of honor but to whom I won’t say.”", "명예의 빚을 갚으려 했지요. 하지만 누구에게인지는 말하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Allow me to make a note of that,”", "\"그것을 기록해 두는 걸 허락하시지요.\""], ["said the prosecutor.", "검사가 말했다."]], [["“By all means.", "\"얼마든지요."], ["Write down that I won’t say, that I won’t.", "내가 말하지 않겠다고, 말하지 않겠노라고 적으십시오."], ["Write that I should think it dishonorable to say.", "말하는 걸 불명예로 여길 거라고 적으십시오."], ["Ech!", "에잇!"], ["you can write it; you’ve nothing else to do with your time.”", "적으셔도 됩니다. 어차피 시간을 달리 쓸 데도 없으실 테니.\""]], [["“Allow me to caution you, sir, and to remind you once more, if you are unaware of it,”", "\"한 가지 주의를 드리고, 혹 모르고 계실까 하여 다시 한번 일러 드리겠습니다.\""], ["the prosecutor began, with a peculiar and stern impressiveness, “that you have a perfect right not to answer the questions put to you now, and we on our side have no right to extort an answer from you, if you decline to give it for one reason or another.", "검사가 묘하게 준엄한 무게를 실어 입을 열었다. \"지금 당신에게 던지는 질문들에 대답하지 않을 완전한 권리가 당신에게 있으며, 우리 쪽에서는 당신이 이런저런 까닭으로 대답하기를 거절할 경우 그 답을 강요할 권리가 없습니다."], ["That is entirely a matter for your personal decision.", "그것은 전적으로 당신 개인의 판단에 달린 문제입니다."], ["But it is our duty, on the other hand, in such cases as the present, to explain and set before you the degree of injury you will be doing yourself by refusing to give this or that piece of evidence.", "그러나 다른 한편으로, 지금과 같은 경우에 이런저런 증언을 거절함으로써 당신이 스스로에게 얼마만 한 손해를 입히게 되는지를 설명하고 일러 드리는 것이 우리의 의무이지요."], ["After which I will beg you to continue.”", "그러고 나서 다시 말씀을 이어 가시기를 청하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Gentlemen, I’m not angry ... I ...”", "\"여러분, 나는 노여운 게 아닙니다……"], ["Mitya muttered in a rather disconcerted tone.", "나는……\" 미탸가 다소 머쓱해진 어조로 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, gentlemen, you see, that Samsonov to whom I went then ...”", "\"자, 여러분, 그러니까 그때 내가 찾아간 그 삼소노프 말입니다……\""]], [["We will, of course, not reproduce his account of what is known to the reader already.", "물론 우리는 이미 독자가 아는 이야기를 그의 진술로 되풀이하지는 않겠다."], ["Mitya was impatiently anxious not to omit the slightest detail.", "미탸는 조바심을 내며 아주 사소한 것 하나도 빠뜨리지 않으려 애썼다."], ["At the same time he was in a hurry to get it over.", "동시에 그는 그것을 어서 끝내 버리고 싶어 서둘렀다."], ["But as he gave his evidence it was written down, and therefore they had continually to pull him up.", "그러나 그가 증언을 하는 동안 그것이 받아 적혔으므로, 그들은 줄곧 그를 멈춰 세워야 했다."], ["Mitya disliked this, but submitted; got angry, though still good-humoredly.", "미탸는 이것을 싫어했으나 따랐고, 화를 내면서도 여전히 기분은 좋게 유지했다."], ["He did, it is true, exclaim, from time to time, “Gentlemen, that’s enough to make an angel out of patience!” Or, “Gentlemen, it’s no good your irritating me.”", "사실 그는 이따금 \"여러분, 이건 천사라도 인내심을 잃게 만들 노릇이오!\"라거나 \"여러분, 나를 약 올려 봐야 소용없소\"라고 외치기는 했다."]], [["But even though he exclaimed he still preserved for a time his genially expansive mood.", "그러나 그렇게 외치면서도 그는 한동안 너그럽고 활달한 기분을 지켜 갔다."], ["So he told them how Samsonov had made a fool of him two days before.", "그렇게 그는 이틀 전에 삼소노프가 자기를 어떻게 바보로 만들었는지를 그들에게 이야기했다."], ["(He had completely realized by now that he had been fooled.) The sale of his watch for six roubles to obtain money for the journey was something new to the lawyers.", "(이제 그는 자기가 농락당했다는 것을 완전히 깨달은 터였다.) 여비를 마련하려고 시계를 육 루블에 판 일은 법관들에게 처음 듣는 사실이었다."], ["They were at once greatly interested, and even, to Mitya’s intense indignation, thought it necessary to write the fact down as a secondary confirmation of the circumstance that he had hardly a farthing in his pocket at the time.", "그들은 곧 크게 흥미를 보였으며, 심지어—미탸의 격렬한 분노를 자아내게도—그 사실을 당시 그의 주머니에 한 푼도 거의 없었다는 정황을 보강해 주는 부차적 증거로 적어 두어야 한다고 여겼다."], ["Little by little Mitya began to grow surly.", "미탸는 차츰 무뚝뚝해지기 시작했다."], ["Then, after describing his journey to see Lyagavy, the night spent in the stifling hut, and so on, he came to his return to the town.", "그러고는 랴가비를 만나러 간 여정과, 숨 막히는 오두막에서 보낸 밤 따위를 묘사한 뒤, 시내로 돌아온 대목에 이르렀다."], ["Here he began, without being particularly urged, to give a minute account of the agonies of jealousy he endured on Grushenka’s account.", "여기서 그는 별로 다그치지도 않았는데 그루셴카 때문에 겪은 질투의 고통을 시시콜콜 들려주기 시작했다."]], [["He was heard with silent attention.", "사람들은 잠자코 주의를 기울여 그의 말을 들었다."], ["They inquired particularly into the circumstance of his having a place of ambush in Marya Kondratyevna’s house at the back of Fyodor Pavlovitch’s garden to keep watch on Grushenka, and of Smerdyakov’s bringing him information.", "그들은 특히 그가 그루셴카를 감시하려고 표도르 파블로비치의 정원 뒤편 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 집에 잠복할 자리를 마련했던 정황과, 스메르댜코프가 그에게 정보를 가져다주던 정황을 캐물었다."], ["They laid particular stress on this, and noted it down.", "그들은 이 점을 유독 강조하며 받아 적었다."], ["Of his jealousy he spoke warmly and at length, and though inwardly ashamed at exposing his most intimate feelings to “public ignominy,” so to speak, he evidently overcame his shame in order to tell the truth.", "자기 질투에 관해 그는 열을 올려 길게 이야기했는데, 자기의 더없이 은밀한 감정을 이를테면 \"만인의 치욕거리\"로 드러내는 것이 속으로 부끄러웠으나, 진실을 말하기 위해 그 수치심을 분명히 이겨 내려 했다."], ["The frigid severity, with which the investigating lawyer, and still more the prosecutor, stared intently at him as he told his story, disconcerted him at last considerably.", "그가 이야기하는 동안 예심판사가, 그리고 그보다 더욱 검사가 그를 뚫어지게 응시하던 그 싸늘한 준엄함은 마침내 그를 상당히 머쓱하게 만들었다."]], [["“That boy, Nikolay Parfenovitch, to whom I was talking nonsense about women only a few days ago, and that sickly prosecutor are not worth my telling this to,”", "'며칠 전만 해도 내가 여자 얘기로 시답잖은 소리를 늘어놓던 저 애송이 니콜라이 파르페노비치며, 저 비실거리는 검사 따위에게 이런 이야기를 들려줄 만한 가치가 없는데.'"], ["he reflected mournfully.", "그는 우울하게 생각했다."], ["“It’s ignominious.", "'치욕스러운 일이지."], ["‘Be patient, humble, hold thy peace.’”", "참아라, 겸손하라, 입을 다물라.'"], ["He wound up his reflections with that line.", "그는 그 시구로 상념을 맺었다."], ["But he pulled himself together to go on again.", "그러나 그는 다시 마음을 다잡고 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["When he came to telling of his visit to Madame Hohlakov, he regained his spirits and even wished to tell a little anecdote of that lady which had nothing to do with the case.", "호흘라코바 부인을 찾아간 대목에 이르자 그는 다시 기운을 되찾았고, 심지어 사건과는 아무 상관도 없는 그 부인에 관한 자그마한 일화 하나를 들려주고 싶어 했다."], ["But the investigating lawyer stopped him, and civilly suggested that he should pass on to “more essential matters.”", "그러나 예심판사가 그를 막고는, \"좀 더 본질적인 사안\"으로 넘어가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 정중히 권했다."], ["At last, when he described his despair and told them how, when he left Madame Hohlakov’s, he thought that he’d “get three thousand if he had to murder some one to do it,” they stopped him again and noted down that he had “meant to murder some one.”", "마침내 그가 자기 절망을 묘사하며, 호흘라코바 부인의 집을 나섰을 때 \"누군가를 죽여서라도 삼천 루블을 손에 넣겠다\"고 생각했다고 말하자, 그들은 다시 그를 멈춰 세우고 그가 \"누군가를 죽이려 했다\"고 적어 두었다."], ["Mitya let them write it without protest.", "미탸는 항의 없이 그것을 적게 두었다."], ["At last he reached the point in his story when he learned that Grushenka had deceived him and had returned from Samsonov’s as soon as he left her there, though she had said that she would stay there till midnight.", "마침내 그는 이야기에서, 그루셴카가 자기를 속였다는 것과, 자정까지 그곳에 머물겠다고 해 놓고는 자기가 떠나자마자 삼소노프의 집에서 돌아왔다는 것을 알게 된 대목에 이르렀다."]], [["“If I didn’t kill Fenya then, gentlemen, it was only because I hadn’t time,”", "\"여러분, 그때 내가 페냐를 죽이지 않은 건 오로지 그럴 시간이 없었기 때문입니다.\""], ["broke from him suddenly at that point in his story.", "이야기의 그 대목에서 그에게서 불쑥 이런 말이 터져 나왔다."], ["That, too, was carefully written down.", "그것 또한 꼼꼼히 받아 적혔다."], ["Mitya waited gloomily, and was beginning to tell how he ran into his father’s garden when the investigating lawyer suddenly stopped him, and opening the big portfolio that lay on the sofa beside him he brought out the brass pestle.", "미탸가 침울하게 기다리다가 아버지의 정원으로 뛰어든 경위를 막 이야기하려는데, 예심판사가 별안간 그를 멈춰 세우고는, 자기 옆 소파에 놓인 커다란 서류 가방을 열어 놋쇠 절굿공이를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Do you recognize this object?”", "\"이 물건을 알아보시겠습니까?\""], ["he asked, showing it to Mitya.", "그가 그것을 미탸에게 보이며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,” he laughed gloomily.", "\"오, 그럼요.\""], ["“Of course I recognize it.", "그가 침울하게 웃었다."], ["Let me have a look at it....", "\"물론 알아봅니다. 어디 좀 봅시다……"], ["Damn it, never mind!”", "제기랄, 됐어요!\""]], [["“You have forgotten to mention it,”", "\"이것을 언급하는 걸 잊으셨더군요.\""], ["observed the investigating lawyer.", "예심판사가 지적했다."]], [["“Hang it all, I shouldn’t have concealed it from you.", "\"빌어먹을, 여러분께 이걸 숨길 까닭이야 없지요."], ["Do you suppose I could have managed without it?", "내가 이것 없이 일을 치를 수 있었으리라고 보십니까?"], ["It simply escaped my memory.”", "그저 깜빡 잊었을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Be so good as to tell us precisely how you came to arm yourself with it.”", "\"어떻게 해서 이것으로 무장하게 되었는지 정확히 말씀해 주시지요.\""]], [["“Certainly I will be so good, gentlemen.”", "\"그러지요, 기꺼이 그러겠습니다, 여러분.\""]], [["And Mitya described how he took the pestle and ran.", "그리하여 미탸는 자기가 절굿공이를 집어 들고 달려 나간 경위를 묘사했다."]], [["“But what object had you in view in arming yourself with such a weapon?”", "\"하지만 그런 무기로 무장하면서 무슨 목적을 품고 계셨습니까?\""]], [["“What object?", "\"목적이요?"], ["No object.", "아무 목적도 없었습니다."], ["I just picked it up and ran off.”", "그냥 집어 들고 달려 나갔지요.\""]], [["“What for, if you had no object?”", "\"목적이 없었다면 무엇 때문에요?\""]], [["Mitya’s wrath flared up.", "미탸의 분노가 확 타올랐다."], ["He looked intently at “the boy” and smiled gloomily and malignantly.", "그는 \"그 애송이\"를 뚫어지게 바라보며 침울하고 악의에 찬 미소를 지었다."], ["He was feeling more and more ashamed at having told “such people” the story of his jealousy so sincerely and spontaneously.", "그는 \"이런 자들\"에게 자기 질투 이야기를 그토록 진솔하고 거리낌 없이 털어놓은 것이 점점 더 부끄러워지고 있었다."]], [["“Bother the pestle!”", "\"그놈의 절굿공이야 아무래도 좋소!\""], ["broke from him suddenly.", "그에게서 불쑥 이런 말이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“But still—”", "\"그래도—\""]], [["“Oh, to keep off dogs....", "\"오, 개를 쫓으려고……"], ["Oh, because it was dark....", "오, 어두웠으니까……"], ["In case anything turned up.”", "무슨 일이라도 생길까 봐.\""]], [["“But have you ever on previous occasions taken a weapon with you when you went out, since you’re afraid of the dark?”", "\"하지만 어둠이 무섭다면서, 전에 외출하실 때도 무기를 지니고 다닌 적이 있으셨습니까?\""]], [["“Ugh!", "\"으윽!"], ["damn it all, gentlemen!", "빌어먹을, 여러분!"], ["There’s positively no talking to you!”", "당신들하고는 도무지 말이 안 통하는군!\""], ["cried Mitya, exasperated beyond endurance, and turning to the secretary, crimson with anger, he said quickly, with a note of fury in his voice:", "미탸가 더는 참을 수 없을 만큼 약이 올라 외쳤고, 분노로 새빨개진 얼굴로 서기 쪽으로 돌아서서, 격분이 서린 목소리로 빠르게 말했다."]], [["“Write down at once ...", "\"당장 적으시오……"], ["at once ...", "당장……"], ["‘that I snatched up the pestle to go and kill my father ...", "'내가 아버지를……"], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch ...", "표도르 파블로비치를……"], ["by hitting him on the head with it!’ Well, now are you satisfied, gentlemen?", "절굿공이로 머리통을 후려쳐 죽이려고 그걸 움켜쥐었다!'고 말이오. 자, 이제 만족하십니까, 여러분?"], ["Are your minds relieved?”", "마음이 후련해지셨소?\""], ["he said, glaring defiantly at the lawyers.", "그는 도전하듯 법관들을 노려보며 말했다."]], [["“We quite understand that you made that statement just now through exasperation with us and the questions we put to you, which you consider trivial, though they are, in fact, essential,”", "\"당신이 방금 그 진술을 한 것은, 우리와 우리가 던진 질문들에 대한 격분 때문이라는 걸 잘 압니다. 당신은 그 질문들을 시시하다고 여기시지만, 실은 그것이 본질적인 것이지요.\""], ["the prosecutor remarked dryly in reply.", "검사가 메마르게 응수했다."]], [["“Well, upon my word, gentlemen!", "\"원, 참, 여러분!"], ["Yes, I took the pestle....", "그래요, 절굿공이를 집어 들었소……"], ["What does one pick things up for at such moments?", "그런 순간에 사람이 물건을 집어 드는 게 무엇 때문이겠소?"], ["I don’t know what for.", "무엇 때문인지 나도 모르겠소."], ["I snatched it up and ran—that’s all.", "그냥 움켜쥐고 달려 나갔소—그게 다요."], ["For to me, gentlemen, passons, or I declare I won’t tell you any more.”", "나로서는, 여러분, 그건 넘어갑시다. 안 그러면 정말이지 더는 한마디도 안 하겠소.\""]], [["He sat with his elbows on the table and his head in his hand.", "그는 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 짚고 한 손으로 머리를 괴고 앉았다."], ["He sat sideways to them and gazed at the wall, struggling against a feeling of nausea.", "그는 그들에게 옆으로 비스듬히 앉아 벽을 바라보며, 치미는 구역질을 억누르고 있었다."], ["He had, in fact, an awful inclination to get up and declare that he wouldn’t say another word, “not if you hang me for it.”", "사실 그는 일어서서 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠노라고, \"이 일로 나를 목매단다 해도\" 그러겠노라고 선언해 버리고 싶은 끔찍한 충동에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["“You see, gentlemen,”", "\"보십시오, 여러분.\""], ["he said at last, with difficulty controlling himself, “you see.", "그는 가까스로 자신을 다스리며 마침내 입을 열었다. \"보십시오."], ["I listen to you and am haunted by a dream....", "나는 여러분의 말을 듣고 있노라니 어떤 꿈에 사로잡힙니다……"], ["It’s a dream I have sometimes, you know....", "그게 내가 가끔 꾸는 꿈이거든요, 아시겠습니까……"], ["I often dream it—it’s always the same ...", "자주 꾸는데—언제나 똑같습니다…… 누군가가 나를 쫓고 있어요."], ["that some one is hunting me, some one I’m awfully afraid of ...", "내가 무섭도록 두려워하는 누군가가…… 그자가 어둠 속에서, 밤중에 나를 쫓는 겁니다……"], ["that he’s hunting me in the dark, in the night ...", "내 뒤를 밟아 오고, 나는 어딘가에 그자를 피해 숨지요. 문 뒤나 찬장 뒤에, 비굴하게 숨는 겁니다."], ["tracking me, and I hide somewhere from him, behind a door or cupboard, hide in a degrading way, and the worst of it is, he always knows where I am, but he pretends not to know where I am on purpose, to prolong my agony, to enjoy my terror....", "그런데 가장 끔찍한 것은, 그자가 내가 어디 있는지 늘 알고 있으면서도, 일부러 모르는 척한다는 거예요. 내 고통을 늘리고 내 공포를 즐기려고 말입니다……"], ["That’s just what you’re doing now.", "지금 여러분이 하시는 게 바로 그겁니다."], ["It’s just like that!”", "꼭 그것과 똑같아요!\""]], [["“Is that the sort of thing you dream about?”", "\"그런 종류의 꿈을 꾸신다는 말씀이군요?\""], ["inquired the prosecutor.", "검사가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, it is.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["Don’t you want to write it down?”", "그것도 적어 두고 싶지 않으십니까?\""], ["said Mitya, with a distorted smile.", "미탸가 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No; no need to write it down.", "\"아니요, 적을 필요는 없습니다."], ["But still you do have curious dreams.”", "하지만 그래도 참 별난 꿈을 꾸시는군요.\""]], [["“It’s not a question of dreams now, gentlemen—this is realism, this is real life!", "\"지금은 꿈이 문제가 아닙니다, 여러분—이건 현실이고, 이건 실제 삶이오!"], ["I’m a wolf and you’re the hunters.", "나는 늑대고 여러분은 사냥꾼이지요."], ["Well, hunt him down!”", "자, 그놈을 잡아 보시오!\""]], [["“You are wrong to make such comparisons ...”", "\"그런 비유를 드시는 건 잘못입니다……\""], ["began Nikolay Parfenovitch, with extraordinary softness.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 유난히 부드러운 목소리로 입을 열었다."]], [["“No, I’m not wrong, not at all!”", "\"아니요, 잘못이 아니오, 결코 아니지!\""], ["Mitya flared up again, though his outburst of wrath had obviously relieved his heart.", "미탸가 다시 발끈했으나, 분노의 폭발은 분명히 그의 가슴을 후련하게 풀어 준 모양이었다."], ["He grew more good-humored at every word.", "그는 말 한마디 한마디마다 점점 기분이 누그러졌다."], ["“You may not trust a criminal or a man on trial tortured by your questions, but an honorable man, the honorable impulses of the heart (I say that boldly!)—no!", "\"여러분의 질문에 시달리는 죄인이나 피고는 못 믿을 수 있겠지요. 하지만 명예로운 사람은, 마음에서 우러나는 명예로운 충동은(이건 떳떳하게 말합니다!)—안 되지요!"], ["That you must believe you have no right indeed ...", "그건 여러분이 믿어야 합니다. 그러지 않을 권리는 정녕 없으니까요……"], ["but—", "그러나—"]], [["Be silent, heart, Be patient, humble, hold thy peace.", "잠잠하라, 가슴아,\n참아라, 겸손하라, 입을 다물라."]], [["Well, shall I go on?”", "자, 계속할까요?\""], ["he broke off gloomily.", "그가 침울하게 말을 끊었다."]], [["“If you’ll be so kind,”", "\"그래 주신다면 고맙겠습니다.\""], ["answered Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 대답했다."]], [["# Chapter V. The Third Ordeal", "# Chapter V. The Third Ordeal"]], [["Though Mitya spoke sullenly, it was evident that he was trying more than ever not to forget or miss a single detail of his story.", "미탸는 무뚝뚝하게 말하면서도, 자기 이야기의 사소한 대목 하나라도 잊거나 빠뜨리지 않으려고 그 어느 때보다 애쓰고 있는 것이 역력했다."], ["He told them how he had leapt over the fence into his father’s garden; how he had gone up to the window; told them all that had passed under the window.", "그는 어떻게 울타리를 뛰어넘어 아버지의 정원으로 들어갔는지, 어떻게 창가로 다가갔는지 들려주었고, 그 창문 아래에서 벌어진 일을 죄다 이야기했다."], ["Clearly, precisely, distinctly, he described the feelings that troubled him during those moments in the garden when he longed so terribly to know whether Grushenka was with his father or not.", "정원에서 보낸 그 순간들 동안, 그루셴카가 아버지와 함께 있는지 아닌지를 그토록 애타게 알고 싶어 했던 자신을 괴롭히던 감정들을, 그는 또렷하고 정확하고 분명하게 묘사했다."], ["But, strange to say, both the lawyers listened now with a sort of awful reserve, looked coldly at him, asked few questions.", "그러나 이상하게도 두 법관은 이제 무언가 무시무시한 신중함을 띠고 듣고 있었으며, 그를 차갑게 바라보면서 질문은 거의 던지지 않았다."], ["Mitya could gather nothing from their faces.", "미탸는 그들의 얼굴에서 아무것도 읽어 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“They’re angry and offended,”", "\"이자들은 화가 나서 토라진 거야.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Well, bother them!”", "\"흥, 마음대로 하라지!\""]], [["When he described how he made up his mind at last to make the “signal” to his father that Grushenka had come, so that he should open the window, the lawyers paid no attention to the word “signal,”", "마침내 그루셴카가 왔다는 \"신호\"를 아버지에게 보내어 창문을 열게 하기로 결심한 대목을 묘사할 때, 두 법관은 \"신호\"라는 말에 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않았으니, 마치 이런 맥락에서 그 말이 지니는 의미를 도무지 파악하지 못한 듯했다."], ["as though they entirely failed to grasp the meaning of the word in this connection: so much so, that Mitya noticed it.", "미탸가 그것을 눈치챌 정도였다."], ["Coming at last to the moment when, seeing his father peering out of the window, his hatred flared up and he pulled the pestle out of his pocket, he suddenly, as though of design, stopped short.", "마침내 아버지가 창밖을 내다보는 것을 보고 증오가 치밀어 호주머니에서 절굿공이를 꺼내 든 순간에 이르자, 그는 마치 일부러 그런 듯 별안간 말을 뚝 멈추었다."], ["He sat gazing at the wall and was aware that their eyes were fixed upon him.", "그는 벽을 응시하며 앉아 있었으나, 그들의 눈길이 자기에게 못박혀 있다는 것을 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["said the investigating lawyer.", "예심판사가 말했다. \"흉기를 꺼내 들었고……"], ["“You pulled out the weapon and ... and what happened then?”", "그래서 그다음엔 무슨 일이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“Then?", "\"그다음?"], ["Why, then I murdered him ...", "흥, 그다음엔 내가 그를 죽인 거지요……"], ["hit him on the head and cracked his skull....", "머리를 후려쳐서 두개골을 박살냈고요……"], ["I suppose that’s your story.", "그게 당신들 각본인 것 같은데."], ["That’s it!”", "바로 그거지요!\""]], [["His eyes suddenly flashed.", "그의 눈이 별안간 번뜩였다."], ["All his smothered wrath suddenly flamed up with extraordinary violence in his soul.", "짓눌려 있던 모든 분노가 갑자기 그의 영혼 속에서 엄청난 격렬함으로 타올랐다."]], [["“Our story?”", "\"우리 각본이라고요?\""], ["repeated Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 되받았다."], ["“Well—and yours?”", "\"그래서—당신 각본은요?\""]], [["Mitya dropped his eyes and was a long time silent.", "미탸는 눈을 떨어뜨리고 한참 동안 말이 없었다."]], [["“My story, gentlemen?", "\"내 각본 말이오, 여러분?"], ["Well, it was like this,”", "자, 그건 이랬소.\""], ["he began softly.", "그는 나직이 입을 열었다."], ["“Whether it was some one’s tears, or my mother prayed to God, or a good angel kissed me at that instant, I don’t know.", "\"누군가의 눈물 때문이었는지, 어머니가 하느님께 기도를 드린 덕분인지, 아니면 바로 그 순간 선한 천사가 내게 입을 맞춰 준 것인지는 모르겠소."], ["But the devil was conquered.", "하지만 악마가 패배했지요."], ["I rushed from the window and ran to the fence.", "나는 창가에서 뛰쳐나와 울타리 쪽으로 달렸소."], ["My father was alarmed and, for the first time, he saw me then, cried out, and sprang back from the window.", "아버지는 깜짝 놀라, 그제야 처음으로 나를 보더니 소리를 지르며 창에서 펄쩍 물러났소."], ["I remember that very well.", "나는 그걸 아주 똑똑히 기억하오."], ["I ran across the garden to the fence ...", "나는 정원을 가로질러 울타리로 달려갔고……"], ["and there Grigory caught me, when I was sitting on the fence.”", "그러다 거기서, 내가 울타리에 걸터앉아 있을 때 그리고리에게 붙들렸소.\""]], [["At that point he raised his eyes at last and looked at his listeners.", "그 대목에서 그는 마침내 눈을 들어 듣는 이들을 바라보았다."], ["They seemed to be staring at him with perfectly unruffled attention.", "그들은 더없이 흔들림 없는 주의를 기울이며 그를 응시하고 있는 것 같았다."], ["A sort of paroxysm of indignation seized on Mitya’s soul.", "일종의 분노의 발작이 미탸의 영혼을 사로잡았다."]], [["“Why, you’re laughing at me at this moment, gentlemen!”", "\"아니, 당신들은 지금 이 순간에도 날 비웃고 있군, 여러분!\""], ["he broke off suddenly.", "그가 별안간 말을 끊었다."]], [["“What makes you think that?”", "\"무엇 때문에 그렇게 생각하십니까?\""], ["observed Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말했다."]], [["“You don’t believe one word—that’s why!", "\"단 한마디도 안 믿으니까요—바로 그래서지요!"], ["I understand, of course, that I have come to the vital point.", "물론 나도 내가 결정적인 대목에 이르렀다는 건 알고 있소."], ["The old man’s lying there now with his skull broken, while I—after dramatically describing how I wanted to kill him, and how I snatched up the pestle—I suddenly run away from the window.", "노인네는 지금 두개골이 박살난 채 저기 누워 있는데, 나는—그를 죽이고 싶었던 심정이며 절굿공이를 움켜쥔 경위를 극적으로 묘사해 놓고는—별안간 창가에서 도망쳐 버린다. 한 편의 로맨스로군!"], ["A romance!", "시(詩)고!"], ["Poetry! As though one could believe a fellow on his word.", "마치 사내가 제 말 하나로 믿어지기라도 할 것처럼 말이오."], ["Ha ha!", "하하!"], ["You are scoffers, gentlemen!”", "당신들은 비웃는 자들이오, 여러분!\""]], [["And he swung round on his chair so that it creaked.", "그러더니 그는 의자 위에서 홱 몸을 돌려 의자가 삐걱거렸다."]], [["“And did you notice,”", "\"그런데 혹시 알아챘습니까,\" 검사가 미탸의 흥분에는 아랑곳하지 않는 듯 별안간 물었다."], ["asked the prosecutor suddenly, as though not observing Mitya’s excitement, “did you notice when you ran away from the window, whether the door into the garden was open?”", "\"창가에서 도망쳐 나올 때, 정원으로 통하는 문이 열려 있던지 어떤지 알아챘습니까?\" \"아니오, 열려 있지 않았소.\""]], [["“No, it was not open.”", "\"열려 있지 않았다고요?\""]], [["“It was not?”", "\"닫혀 있었소."], ["“It was shut. And who could open it?", "게다가 누가 그걸 열 수 있었겠소?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["the door.", "그 문이라니."], ["Wait a bit!”", "잠깐만!\""], ["he seemed suddenly to bethink himself, and almost with a start:", "그는 별안간 무언가 생각이 미친 듯, 거의 흠칫 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“Why, did you find the door open?”", "\"아니, 당신들은 문이 열려 있는 걸 발견했단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, it was open.”", "\"그렇소, 열려 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Why, who could have opened it if you did not open it yourselves?”", "\"아니, 당신들이 직접 연 게 아니라면 대체 누가 그 문을 열 수 있었단 말이오?\""], ["cried Mitya, greatly astonished.", "미탸가 몹시 놀라 소리쳤다."]], [["“The door stood open, and your father’s murderer undoubtedly went in at that door, and, having accomplished the crime, went out again by the same door,”", "\"문은 열려 있었고, 당신 아버지를 살해한 자는 의심할 바 없이 바로 그 문으로 들어가, 범행을 마친 뒤 같은 문으로 다시 나갔소.\""], ["the prosecutor pronounced deliberately, as though chiseling out each word separately.", "검사가 마치 한마디 한마디를 따로따로 새겨 내듯 신중하게 말했다."], ["“That is perfectly clear.", "\"그건 더없이 명백합니다."], ["The murder was committed in the room and not through the window; that is absolutely certain from the examination that has been made, from the position of the body and everything.", "살인은 방 안에서 저질러졌고 창문을 통해서가 아니오. 그건 실시된 검시로 보나, 시신의 자세며 모든 것으로 보나 절대적으로 확실합니다."], ["There can be no doubt of that circumstance.”", "그 정황에는 의심의 여지가 없소.\""]], [["Mitya was absolutely dumbfounded.", "미탸는 완전히 어안이 벙벙해졌다."]], [["“But that’s utterly impossible!”", "\"하지만 그건 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이오!\""], ["he cried, completely at a loss. “I ...", "그가 완전히 갈피를 못 잡고 소리쳤다."], ["I didn’t go in....", "\"나는……"], ["I tell you positively, definitely, the door was shut the whole time I was in the garden, and when I ran out of the garden.", "나는 안으로 들어가지 않았소…… 단언컨대, 분명히 말하지만, 내가 정원에 있던 내내, 그리고 정원에서 뛰쳐나올 때까지 문은 닫혀 있었소."], ["I only stood at the window and saw him through the window.", "나는 그저 창가에 서서 창문 너머로 그를 보았을 뿐이오."], ["That’s all, that’s all....", "그게 다요, 그게 전부란 말이오……"], ["I remember to the last minute.", "나는 마지막 순간까지 다 기억하고 있소."], ["And if I didn’t remember, it would be just the same.", "설령 기억하지 못한다 해도 마찬가지요. 나는 그걸 아니까."], ["I know it, for no one knew the signals except Smerdyakov, and me, and the dead man.", "그 신호를 아는 사람은 스메르댜코프와 나, 그리고 죽은 그 양반밖에 없었으니 말이오."], ["And he wouldn’t have opened the door to any one in the world without the signals.”", "그리고 그 양반은 신호 없이는 세상 그 누구에게도 문을 열어 주지 않았을 거요.\""]], [["“Signals?", "\"신호라고요?"], ["What signals?”", "무슨 신호 말입니까?\""], ["asked the prosecutor, with greedy, almost hysterical, curiosity.", "검사가 탐욕스럽다 못해 거의 발작적인 호기심으로 물었다."], ["He instantly lost all trace of his reserve and dignity.", "그는 단번에 신중함과 위엄의 흔적을 죄다 잃어버렸다."], ["He asked the question with a sort of cringing timidity.", "그는 일종의 비굴한 소심함으로 그 질문을 던졌다."], ["He scented an important fact of which he had known nothing, and was already filled with dread that Mitya might be unwilling to disclose it.", "그는 자신이 전혀 모르고 있던 중대한 사실의 냄새를 맡았고, 미탸가 그것을 밝히지 않으려 할까 봐 벌써부터 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["“So you didn’t know!”", "\"그러니까 당신들은 몰랐던 거로군!\""], ["Mitya winked at him with a malicious and mocking smile.", "미탸가 심술궂고 비웃는 미소를 지으며 그에게 눈을 찡긋해 보였다."], ["“What if I won’t tell you?", "\"내가 말해 주지 않는다면 어떻게 할 거요?"], ["From whom could you find out?", "누구한테서 알아낼 수 있겠소?"], ["No one knew about the signals except my father, Smerdyakov, and me: that was all.", "그 신호에 관해 아는 사람은 아버지와 스메르댜코프, 그리고 나밖에 없었소. 그게 다요."], ["Heaven knew, too, but it won’t tell you.", "하늘도 알기야 하지만, 하늘이 당신들한테 말해 주진 않을 거요."], ["But it’s an interesting fact.", "하지만 그건 흥미로운 사실이지."], ["There’s no knowing what you might build on it.", "그걸 가지고 무슨 탑을 쌓아 올릴지 누가 알겠소."], ["Ha ha!", "하하!"], ["Take comfort, gentlemen, I’ll reveal it.", "안심하시오, 여러분, 내가 밝혀 주겠소."], ["You’ve some foolish idea in your hearts.", "당신들 마음속엔 무슨 어리석은 생각이 들어 있군."], ["You don’t know the man you have to deal with!", "당신들은 지금 상대하고 있는 사람이 어떤 위인인지 모르고 있어!"], ["You have to do with a prisoner who gives evidence against himself, to his own damage!", "당신들이 상대하고 있는 건 자기에게 불리하게, 제 손해를 무릅쓰고 증언하는 피의자란 말이오!"], ["Yes, for I’m a man of honor and you—are not.”", "그렇소, 나는 명예를 아는 사람이고 당신들은—아니니까.\""]], [["The prosecutor swallowed this without a murmur.", "검사는 군말 없이 이 말을 꿀꺽 삼켰다."], ["He was trembling with impatience to hear the new fact.", "그는 새로운 사실을 듣고 싶어 안달이 나서 몸을 떨고 있었다."], ["Minutely and diffusely Mitya told them everything about the signals invented by Fyodor Pavlovitch for Smerdyakov.", "미탸는 표도르 파블로비치가 스메르댜코프를 위해 고안해 낸 신호에 관한 모든 것을 시시콜콜 장황하게 들려주었다."], ["He told them exactly what every tap on the window meant, tapped the signals on the table, and when Nikolay Parfenovitch said that he supposed he, Mitya, had tapped the signal “Grushenka has come,” when he tapped to his father, he answered precisely that he had tapped that signal, that “Grushenka had come.”", "그는 창문을 두드리는 신호 하나하나가 무엇을 뜻하는지 정확히 일러 주었고, 그 신호를 탁자 위에 두드려 보였으며, 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 그가, 곧 미탸가 아버지에게 신호를 두드릴 때 \"그루셴카가 왔다\"는 신호를 두드렸을 거라고 하자, 그는 바로 그 신호, 곧 \"그루셴카가 왔다\"는 신호를 두드렸다고 정확히 답했다."]], [["“So now you can build up your tower,”", "\"자, 이제 당신들의 탑을 쌓아 올리시구려.\""], ["Mitya broke off, and again turned away from them contemptuously.", "미탸가 말을 뚝 끊고는 다시 그들에게서 경멸하듯 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“So no one knew of the signals but your dead father, you, and the valet Smerdyakov?", "\"그러니까 그 신호를 안 사람은 돌아가신 당신 아버지와 당신, 그리고 하인 스메르댜코프뿐이었다는 거지요?"], ["And no one else?”", "그밖에 다른 사람은 없고요?\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch inquired once more.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 다시 한번 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["The valet Smerdyakov, and Heaven.", "하인 스메르댜코프와, 그리고 하늘이오."], ["Write down about Heaven.", "하늘에 관해서도 적어 두시오."], ["That may be of use.", "그게 쓸모가 있을지도 모르니."], ["Besides, you will need God yourselves.”", "게다가 당신들도 하느님이 필요해질 거요.\""]], [["And they had already, of course, begun writing it down.", "그리고 그들은 물론 벌써 그것을 받아 적기 시작한 터였다."], ["But while they wrote, the prosecutor said suddenly, as though pitching on a new idea:", "그런데 받아 적는 동안 검사가 별안간 새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯 말했다."]], [["“But if Smerdyakov also knew of these signals and you absolutely deny all responsibility for the death of your father, was it not he, perhaps, who knocked the signal agreed upon, induced your father to open to him, and then ...", "\"하지만 만약 스메르댜코프도 이 신호를 알고 있었고, 당신은 부친의 죽음에 대한 모든 책임을 절대적으로 부인한다면, 어쩌면 그가 약속된 신호를 두드려 당신 부친으로 하여금 문을 열게 한 뒤……"], ["committed the crime?”", "범행을 저지른 게 아닐까요?\""]], [["Mitya turned upon him a look of profound irony and intense hatred.", "미탸는 깊은 빈정거림과 격렬한 증오가 어린 시선을 그에게 돌렸다."], ["His silent stare lasted so long that it made the prosecutor blink.", "그의 말없는 응시가 어찌나 길게 이어졌던지 검사가 눈을 깜박일 정도였다."]], [["“You’ve caught the fox again,”", "\"또다시 여우를 잡았군요.\""], ["commented Mitya at last; “you’ve got the beast by the tail.", "마침내 미탸가 말했다. \"당신들은 그 짐승의 꼬리를 붙들었어요."], ["Ha ha!", "하하!"], ["I see through you, Mr. Prosecutor.", "검사 양반, 나는 당신 속내를 다 꿰뚫어 보고 있소."], ["You thought, of course, that I should jump at that, catch at your prompting, and shout with all my might, ‘Aie!", "당신은 물론, 내가 그 미끼에 덥석 달려들어 당신 부추김에 넘어가서는 있는 힘껏 '아이고! 스메르댜코프야, 그놈이 살인자라고'"], ["it’s Smerdyakov; he’s the murderer.’ Confess that’s what you thought.", "하고 소리칠 줄 알았겠지. 그게 당신 속셈이었다고 실토하시구려."], ["Confess, and I’ll go on.”", "실토하면 내가 계속 말해 주겠소.\""]], [["But the prosecutor did not confess.", "그러나 검사는 실토하지 않았다."], ["He held his tongue and waited.", "그는 입을 다물고 기다렸다."]], [["“You’re mistaken.", "\"오산이오."], ["I’m not going to shout ‘It’s Smerdyakov,’”", "나는 '스메르댜코프 짓이다' 하고 소리칠 생각이 없으니까.\""], ["said Mitya.", "미탸가 말했다."]], [["“And you don’t even suspect him?”", "\"그를 의심조차 하지 않는단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Why, do you suspect him?”", "\"흥, 당신들은 그를 의심하오?\""]], [["“He is suspected, too.”", "\"그도 의심받고 있소.\""]], [["Mitya fixed his eyes on the floor.", "미탸는 바닥에 시선을 못박았다."]], [["“Joking apart,”", "\"농담은 접어 두고.\" 그가 침울하게 내뱉었다."], ["he brought out gloomily.", "\"들어 보시오. 맨 처음부터, 그러니까 거의 내가 휘장 뒤에서 당신들한테로 뛰쳐나온 순간부터 내 머릿속엔 스메르댜코프 생각이 박혀 있었소."], ["“Listen.", "여기 이렇게 앉아 내가 결백하다고 소리치면서도 줄곧 '스메르댜코프!'"], ["From the very beginning, almost from the moment when I ran out to you from behind the curtain, I’ve had the thought of Smerdyakov in my mind.", "하고 생각하고 있었던 거요."], ["I’ve been sitting here, shouting that I’m innocent and thinking all the time ‘Smerdyakov!’ I can’t get Smerdyakov out of my head.", "스메르댜코프를 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었소. 사실 나도 방금 스메르댜코프를 떠올렸소."], ["In fact, I, too, thought of Smerdyakov just now; but only for a second.", "하지만 그저 한순간이었지요. 거의 곧바로 '아니야, 스메르댜코프가 아니야'"], ["Almost at once I thought, ‘No, it’s not Smerdyakov.’ It’s not his doing, gentlemen.”", "하고 생각했소. 그건 그의 짓이 아니오, 여러분.\""]], [["“In that case is there anybody else you suspect?”", "\"그렇다면 달리 의심 가는 사람이라도 있습니까?\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch inquired cautiously.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 조심스레 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know any one it could be, whether it’s the hand of Heaven or Satan, but ...", "\"그게 누구일 수 있을지는 모르겠소. 하늘의 손인지 사탄의 손인지도."], ["not Smerdyakov,”", "하지만…… 스메르댜코프는 아니오.\""], ["Mitya jerked out with decision.", "미탸가 단호하게 잘라 말했다."]], [["“But what makes you affirm so confidently and emphatically that it’s not he?”", "\"그런데 무엇 때문에 그토록 자신 있게, 그토록 단호하게 그가 아니라고 단언하는 겁니까?\""]], [["“From my conviction—my impression.", "\"내 확신에서, 내 인상에서 그러는 거요."], ["Because Smerdyakov is a man of the most abject character and a coward.", "스메르댜코프는 더할 나위 없이 비굴한 성품의 인간이고 겁쟁이니까."], ["He’s not a coward, he’s the epitome of all the cowardice in the world walking on two legs.", "겁쟁이 정도가 아니라, 세상의 온갖 비겁함을 죄다 모아 두 다리로 걸어 다니게 한 표본이오."], ["He has the heart of a chicken.", "그놈은 닭의 간을 가진 자요."], ["When he talked to me, he was always trembling for fear I should kill him, though I never raised my hand against him.", "나와 이야기할 때면 늘 내가 자기를 죽일까 봐 벌벌 떨었지요. 내가 그놈한테 손찌검 한 번 한 적 없는데도 말이오."], ["He fell at my feet and blubbered; he has kissed these very boots, literally, beseeching me ‘not to frighten him.’ Do you hear?", "그놈은 내 발치에 엎드려 질질 짜면서, 글자 그대로 바로 이 장화에 입을 맞추며 '제발 겁주지 말아 달라'고 애원했소. 들었소?"], ["‘Not to frighten him.’ What a thing to say!", "'겁주지 말아 달라'고. 그런 말을 다 하다니!"], ["Why, I offered him money.", "흥, 나는 그놈한테 돈까지 줬소."], ["He’s a puling chicken—sickly, epileptic, weak-minded—a child of eight could thrash him.", "그놈은 칭얼대는 병아리요—병약하고, 간질에 걸렸고, 머리가 모자란—여덟 살짜리 아이라도 흠씬 두들겨 팰 수 있는 위인이오."], ["He has no character worth talking about.", "입에 올릴 만한 성품이라고는 없소."], ["It’s not Smerdyakov, gentlemen.", "스메르댜코프가 아니오, 여러분."], ["He doesn’t care for money; he wouldn’t take my presents.", "그놈은 돈에 관심도 없소. 내가 주는 선물도 받지 않았으니까."], ["Besides, what motive had he for murdering the old man?", "게다가 그놈이 노인네를 죽일 무슨 동기가 있었겠소?"], ["Why, he’s very likely his son, you know—his natural son.", "그놈은 어쩌면 그 양반의 아들일 가능성이 아주 높단 말이오, 알겠소—그 양반의 사생아 말이오."], ["Do you know that?”", "그걸 알고 있소?\""]], [["“We have heard that legend.", "\"우리도 그 풍문은 들었소."], ["But you are your father’s son, too, you know; yet you yourself told every one you meant to murder him.”", "하지만 당신도 부친의 아들이긴 마찬가지 아니오. 그런데도 당신 스스로 만나는 사람마다 부친을 죽이겠다고 떠벌렸잖소.\""]], [["“That’s a thrust!", "\"그건 한 방 먹이는 소리로군!"], ["And a nasty, mean one, too!", "게다가 더럽고 비열한 한 방이오!"], ["I’m not afraid!", "나는 두렵지 않소!"], ["Oh, gentlemen, isn’t it too base of you to say that to my face?", "오, 여러분, 그 말을 내 면전에 대고 하다니 너무 비열하지 않소?"], ["It’s base, because I told you that myself.", "비열하지요, 그건 내가 직접 당신들한테 말한 거니까."], ["I not only wanted to murder him, but I might have done it.", "나는 그를 죽이고 싶었을 뿐만 아니라, 죽일 수도 있었소."], ["And, what’s more, I went out of my way to tell you of my own accord that I nearly murdered him.", "게다가 한술 더 떠서, 내가 그를 거의 죽일 뻔했다고 자진해서 당신들한테 일부러 말해 주기까지 했단 말이오."], ["But, you see, I didn’t murder him; you see, my guardian angel saved me—that’s what you’ve not taken into account.", "하지만 보다시피 나는 그를 죽이지 않았소. 보다시피 내 수호천사가 나를 구했지요—바로 그걸 당신들이 셈에 넣지 않은 거요."], ["And that’s why it’s so base of you.", "그래서 그토록 비열하다는 거요."], ["For I didn’t kill him, I didn’t kill him!", "나는 그를 죽이지 않았으니까, 죽이지 않았으니까!"], ["Do you hear, I did not kill him.”", "들었소, 나는 그를 죽이지 않았다고.\""]], [["He was almost choking.", "그는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["He had not been so moved before during the whole interrogation.", "심문 내내 그가 이토록 격앙된 적은 없었다."]], [["“And what has he told you, gentlemen—Smerdyakov, I mean?”", "\"그런데 그놈은 당신들한테 뭐라고 말했소, 여러분—스메르댜코프 말이오.\""], ["he added suddenly, after a pause.", "그가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 별안간 덧붙였다."], ["“May I ask that question?”", "\"그 질문을 해도 되겠소?\""]], [["“You may ask any question,”", "\"무슨 질문이든 하셔도 됩니다.\""], ["the prosecutor replied with frigid severity, “any question relating to the facts of the case, and we are, I repeat, bound to answer every inquiry you make.", "검사가 냉랭하고 엄격하게 대답했다. \"사건의 사실관계에 관한 질문이라면 무엇이든, 그리고 거듭 말하지만 우리는 당신이 하는 모든 질문에 답할 의무가 있소."], ["We found the servant Smerdyakov, concerning whom you inquire, lying unconscious in his bed, in an epileptic fit of extreme severity, that had recurred, possibly, ten times.", "당신이 묻는 그 하인 스메르댜코프를, 우리는 침대에 의식을 잃고 누워 있는 채로 발견했소. 극도로 심한 간질 발작 상태였는데, 어쩌면 열 번쯤 거듭 일어났던 모양이오."], ["The doctor who was with us told us, after seeing him, that he may possibly not outlive the night.”", "우리와 동행한 의사가 그를 진찰한 뒤, 어쩌면 오늘 밤을 넘기지 못할지도 모른다고 우리한테 말했소.\""]], [["“Well, if that’s so, the devil must have killed him,”", "\"흠, 그렇다면 그를 죽인 건 악마가 틀림없겠군.\""], ["broke suddenly from Mitya, as though until that moment he had been asking himself: “Was it Smerdyakov or not?”", "미탸가 별안간 내뱉었으니, 마치 그 순간까지 \"스메르댜코프였을까 아니었을까?\" 하고 스스로에게 묻고 있던 듯했다."]], [["“We will come back to this later,”", "\"그 문제는 나중에 다시 다루기로 하지요.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch decided.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 결정을 내렸다."], ["“Now, wouldn’t you like to continue your statement?”", "\"자, 이제 진술을 계속하시겠습니까?\""]], [["Mitya asked for a rest.", "미탸는 잠시 쉬게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["His request was courteously granted.", "그의 청은 정중하게 받아들여졌다."], ["After resting, he went on with his story.", "쉬고 난 뒤 그는 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["But he was evidently depressed.", "그러나 그는 눈에 띄게 의기소침해 있었다."], ["He was exhausted, mortified and morally shaken.", "그는 기진맥진했고, 굴욕감에 빠졌으며, 정신적으로 흔들리고 있었다."], ["To make things worse the prosecutor exasperated him, as though intentionally, by vexatious interruptions about “trifling points.”", "설상가상으로 검사가 마치 일부러 그러는 듯 \"사소한 대목\"을 두고 성가시게 끼어들어 그를 더욱 약 올렸다."], ["Scarcely had Mitya described how, sitting on the wall, he had struck Grigory on the head with the pestle, while the old man had hold of his left leg, and how he had then jumped down to look at him, when the prosecutor stopped him to ask him to describe exactly how he was sitting on the wall.", "미탸가 노인이 자기 왼쪽 다리를 붙들고 있는 동안 담장에 걸터앉은 채 절굿공이로 그리고리의 머리를 후려쳤고, 그러고는 뛰어내려 그를 들여다본 경위를 채 다 묘사하기도 전에, 검사가 그를 멈춰 세우고 담장에 정확히 어떻게 걸터앉아 있었는지 묘사해 달라고 청했다."], ["Mitya was surprised.", "미탸는 어리둥절했다."]], [["“Oh, I was sitting like this, astride, one leg on one side of the wall and one on the other.”", "\"오, 나는 이렇게, 말 타듯 두 다리를 벌려서 한쪽 다리는 담장 이쪽에, 다른 쪽 다리는 저쪽에 걸치고 앉아 있었소.\""]], [["“And the pestle?”", "\"그러면 절굿공이는요?\""]], [["“The pestle was in my hand.”", "\"절굿공이는 손에 들고 있었소.\""]], [["“Not in your pocket?", "\"호주머니에 들어 있던 게 아니고요?"], ["Do you remember that precisely?", "그걸 정확히 기억합니까?"], ["Was it a violent blow you gave him?”", "당신이 그에게 가한 건 세찬 일격이었소?\""]], [["“It must have been a violent one.", "\"세찬 일격이 틀림없었겠지요."], ["But why do you ask?”", "한데 왜 묻는 거요?\""]], [["“Would you mind sitting on the chair just as you sat on the wall then and showing us just how you moved your arm, and in what direction?”", "\"수고스럽겠지만 그때 담장에 걸터앉았던 것과 똑같이 의자에 앉아서, 팔을 어떻게, 어느 방향으로 휘둘렀는지 우리한테 보여 주시겠소?\""]], [["“You’re making fun of me, aren’t you?”", "\"나를 놀리는 거요, 그렇지 않소?\""], ["asked Mitya, looking haughtily at the speaker; but the latter did not flinch.", "미탸가 거만하게 상대를 노려보며 물었다. 그러나 상대는 움찔하지도 않았다."]], [["Mitya turned abruptly, sat astride on his chair, and swung his arm.", "미탸는 홱 몸을 돌려 의자에 두 다리를 벌리고 걸터앉더니 팔을 휘둘렀다."]], [["“This was how I struck him!", "\"바로 이렇게 그를 후려쳤소!"], ["That’s how I knocked him down!", "이렇게 그를 때려눕혔단 말이오!"], ["What more do you want?”", "더 뭘 바라는 거요?\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고맙소."], ["May I trouble you now to explain why you jumped down, with what object, and what you had in view?”", "그럼 이제 수고스럽지만 왜 뛰어내렸는지, 무슨 목적으로, 무엇을 염두에 두고 그랬는지 설명해 주시겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, hang it!...", "\"오, 빌어먹을!……"], ["I jumped down to look at the man I’d hurt ...", "내가 다치게 한 자를 들여다보려고 뛰어내렸소……"], ["I don’t know what for!”", "뭣 때문이었는지는 나도 모르겠소!\""]], [["“Though you were so excited and were running away?”", "\"그토록 흥분해서 도망치던 와중에도 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, though I was excited and running away.”", "\"그렇소, 흥분해서 도망치던 와중에도 그랬소.\""]], [["“You wanted to help him?”", "\"그를 도와주려고 한 거요?\""]], [["“Help!...", "\"도와주려고!……"], ["Yes, perhaps I did want to help him....", "그렇소, 어쩌면 그를 도와주고 싶었는지도 모르겠소……"], ["I don’t remember.”", "기억이 안 나오.\""]], [["“You don’t remember?", "\"기억이 안 난다고요?"], ["Then you didn’t quite know what you were doing?”", "그럼 당신이 무얼 하고 있는지 제대로 알지도 못했단 말이오?\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않소."], ["I remember everything—every detail.", "나는 모든 걸 기억하고 있소—세세한 대목 하나하나까지."], ["I jumped down to look at him, and wiped his face with my handkerchief.”", "나는 그를 들여다보려고 뛰어내렸고, 손수건으로 그의 얼굴을 닦아 주었소.\""]], [["“We have seen your handkerchief.", "\"우리도 당신 손수건을 봤소."], ["Did you hope to restore him to consciousness?”", "그를 다시 의식이 들게 하려는 바람이었소?\""]], [["“I don’t know whether I hoped it.", "\"그걸 바랐는지 어땠는지는 모르겠소."], ["I simply wanted to make sure whether he was alive or not.”", "나는 그저 그가 살아 있는지 아닌지 확인하고 싶었을 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You wanted to be sure?", "확인하고 싶었다고요?"], ["Well, what then?”", "그래서, 그다음엔요?\""]], [["“I’m not a doctor.", "\"나는 의사가 아니오."], ["I couldn’t decide.", "판단할 수가 없었소."], ["I ran away thinking I’d killed him.", "나는 그를 죽였구나 생각하며 도망쳤소."], ["And now he’s recovered.”", "그런데 지금 보니 그가 살아났더군요.\""]], [["“Excellent,”", "\"훌륭하오.\""], ["commented the prosecutor.", "검사가 말했다. \"고맙소."], ["“Thank you. That’s all I wanted.", "내가 알고 싶었던 건 그게 전부요."], ["Kindly proceed.”", "그럼 계속해 주시오.\""]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["it never entered Mitya’s head to tell them, though he remembered it, that he had jumped back from pity, and standing over the prostrate figure had even uttered some words of regret: “You’ve come to grief, old man—there’s no help for it.", "미탸의 머릿속에는, 비록 그것을 기억하고 있었으면서도, 자기가 연민에서 뛰어내렸으며 쓰러진 사람 위에 서서 후회의 말까지 몇 마디 내뱉었다는 것을 그들에게 말할 생각이 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. \"영감, 당신은 화를 입었구려—어쩔 수가 없소."], ["Well, there you must lie.”", "자, 거기 누워 계셔야겠소.\""]], [["The prosecutor could only draw one conclusion: that the man had jumped back “at such a moment and in such excitement simply with the object of ascertaining whether the only witness of his crime were dead; that he must therefore have been a man of great strength, coolness, decision and foresight even at such a moment,”", "검사가 끌어낼 수 있는 결론은 단 하나뿐이었다. 곧 그자가 \"그런 순간에, 그런 흥분 상태에서, 오로지 자기 범행의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 어떤지를 확인할 목적으로 뛰어내렸으며, 따라서 그런 순간에조차 대단한 완력과 냉정함과 결단력과 선견지명을 갖춘 인간이 틀림없다\"는……"], ["... and so on.", "따위의 결론이었다."], ["The prosecutor was satisfied: “I’ve provoked the nervous fellow by ‘trifles’ and he has said more than he meant to.”", "검사는 흡족했다. \"'사소한 것들'로 이 신경질적인 작자를 자극했더니, 의도한 것보다 더 많은 말을 했군.\""]], [["With painful effort Mitya went on.", "미탸는 고통스러운 노력을 기울이며 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["But this time he was pulled up immediately by Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "그러나 이번에는 곧바로 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게 제지당했다."]], [["“How came you to run to the servant, Fedosya Markovna, with your hands so covered with blood, and, as it appears, your face, too?”", "\"어쩌다가 두 손에 그토록 피를 잔뜩 묻힌 채로, 게다가 보아하니 얼굴에도 피가 묻은 채로 하녀 페도시야 마르코브나한테 달려가게 된 거요?\""]], [["“Why, I didn’t notice the blood at all at the time,”", "\"흥, 나는 그때 피라곤 전혀 알아채지 못했소.\""], ["answered Mitya.", "미탸가 대답했다."]], [["“That’s quite likely.", "\"그건 충분히 있을 법한 일이오."], ["It does happen sometimes.”", "그런 일이 가끔 일어나지요.\""], ["The prosecutor exchanged glances with Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "검사가 니콜라이 파르페노비치와 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["“I simply didn’t notice.", "\"나는 그저 알아채지 못했소."], ["You’re quite right there, prosecutor,”", "그 점은 당신 말이 옳소, 검사 양반.\""], ["Mitya assented suddenly.", "미탸가 별안간 수긍했다."]], [["Next came the account of Mitya’s sudden determination to “step aside”", "다음으로는 미탸가 \"비켜서서\" 두 사람의 행복에 길을 터 주기로 별안간 결심한 사연이 이어졌다."], ["and make way for their happiness. But he could not make up his mind to open his heart to them as before, and tell them about “the queen of his soul.”", "그러나 그는 전처럼 그들에게 자기 속내를 털어놓고 \"제 영혼의 여왕\"에 관해 이야기할 결심이 서지 않았다."], ["He disliked speaking of her before these chilly persons “who were fastening on him like bugs.”", "그는 \"빈대처럼 자기한테 들러붙는\" 이 냉랭한 위인들 앞에서 그녀를 입에 올리기가 싫었다."], ["And so in response to their reiterated questions he answered briefly and abruptly:", "그래서 그들이 거듭 묻는 말에 그는 짧고 퉁명스럽게 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I made up my mind to kill myself.", "\"흥, 나는 스스로 목숨을 끊기로 결심했소."], ["What had I left to live for?", "내게 무슨 살아갈 까닭이 남아 있었겠소?"], ["That question stared me in the face.", "그 물음이 내 면전을 빤히 들여다보고 있었소."], ["Her first rightful lover had come back, the man who wronged her but who’d hurried back to offer his love, after five years, and atone for the wrong with marriage....", "그녀의 첫 정당한 연인이 돌아왔으니, 그녀에게 몹쓸 짓을 했으나 오 년이 지난 뒤 부랴부랴 돌아와 사랑을 바치고, 결혼으로 그 잘못을 속죄하겠다는 사내가 말이오……"], ["So I knew it was all over for me....", "그래서 나는 이제 내겐 모든 게 끝났다는 걸 알았소……"], ["And behind me disgrace, and that blood—Grigory’s....", "게다가 내 등 뒤에는 치욕이, 그리고 그 피—그리고리의 피가 있었지요……"], ["What had I to live for?", "내게 무슨 살아갈 까닭이 있었겠소?"], ["So I went to redeem the pistols I had pledged, to load them and put a bullet in my brain to-morrow.”", "그래서 나는 저당 잡혀 둔 권총을 되찾으러 갔던 거요. 거기에 총알을 재어 내일 내 머리통에 한 발 박아 넣으려고.\""]], [["“And a grand feast the night before?”", "\"그러면서 그 전날 밤엔 성대한 잔치를 벌였다?\""]], [["“Yes, a grand feast the night before.", "\"그렇소, 그 전날 밤엔 성대한 잔치를 벌였지."], ["Damn it all, gentlemen!", "빌어먹을, 여러분!"], ["Do make haste and finish it.", "제발 서둘러서 끝내 주시오."], ["I meant to shoot myself not far from here, beyond the village, and I’d planned to do it at five o’clock in the morning.", "나는 여기서 멀지 않은 곳, 마을 너머에서 총으로 자결할 작정이었고, 새벽 다섯 시에 그 일을 치를 계획이었소."], ["And I had a note in my pocket already.", "그리고 호주머니엔 이미 쪽지가 한 장 들어 있었소."], ["I wrote it at Perhotin’s when I loaded my pistols.", "페르호틴의 집에서 권총에 총알을 잴 때 쓴 거요."], ["Here’s the letter.", "자, 여기 그 편지가 있소."], ["Read it!", "읽어 보시오!"], ["It’s not for you I tell it,”", "당신들 들으라고 하는 말은 아니지만.\""], ["he added contemptuously.", "그가 경멸하듯 덧붙였다."], ["He took it from his waistcoat pocket and flung it on the table.", "그는 그것을 조끼 호주머니에서 꺼내 탁자 위로 내던졌다."], ["The lawyers read it with curiosity, and, as is usual, added it to the papers connected with the case.", "두 법관은 호기심 어린 눈으로 그것을 읽었고, 으레 그렇듯 사건에 관련된 서류에 그것을 끼워 넣었다."]], [["“And you didn’t even think of washing your hands at Perhotin’s?", "\"그런데 페르호틴의 집에서 손을 씻을 생각조차 안 한 거요?"], ["You were not afraid then of arousing suspicion?”", "그때는 의심을 살까 봐 두렵지 않았소?\""]], [["“What suspicion?", "\"무슨 의심 말이오?"], ["Suspicion or not, I should have galloped here just the same, and shot myself at five o’clock, and you wouldn’t have been in time to do anything.", "의심을 사든 안 사든, 나는 어차피 똑같이 이리로 말을 몰아 다섯 시에 자결했을 테고, 당신들은 무슨 일을 하기엔 너무 늦었을 거요."], ["If it hadn’t been for what’s happened to my father, you would have known nothing about it, and wouldn’t have come here.", "만약 아버지한테 그런 일이 벌어지지 않았더라면, 당신들은 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐을 테고 이리로 오지도 않았을 거요."], ["Oh, it’s the devil’s doing.", "오, 이건 악마의 짓이오."], ["It was the devil murdered father, it was through the devil that you found it out so soon.", "아버지를 죽인 건 악마요, 당신들이 그토록 빨리 알아낸 것도 악마를 통해서지."], ["How did you manage to get here so quick?", "대체 어떻게 이렇게 빨리 여기까지 온 거요?"], ["It’s marvelous, a dream!”", "놀랍군, 한 편의 꿈 같소!\""]], [["“Mr. Perhotin informed us that when you came to him, you held in your hands ...", "\"페르호틴 씨의 말로는, 당신이 그를 찾아갔을 때 두 손에……"], ["your blood-stained hands ...", "피 묻은 두 손에…… 돈을……"], ["your money ...", "큰돈을……"], ["a lot of money ... a bundle of hundred-rouble notes, and that his servant-boy saw it too.”", "백 루블짜리 지폐 뭉치를 쥐고 있었고, 그 집 사환 아이도 그걸 봤다고 하더군요.\""]], [["“That’s true, gentlemen.", "\"사실이오, 여러분."], ["I remember it was so.”", "그랬던 기억이 나오.\""]], [["“Now, there’s one little point presents itself. Can you inform us,”", "\"자, 그런데 여기 한 가지 자그마한 의문점이 떠오르오."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch began, with extreme gentleness, “where did you get so much money all of a sudden, when it appears from the facts, from the reckoning of time, that you had not been home?”", "사실관계로 보나, 시간을 따져 보나 당신은 집에 들르지 않았던 것으로 보이는데, 어떻게 별안간 그렇게 많은 돈이 생겼는지 우리한테 알려 줄 수 있겠소?\" 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 더없이 부드럽게 운을 떼었다."]], [["The prosecutor’s brows contracted at the question being asked so plainly, but he did not interrupt Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "이렇게 단도직입으로 질문이 던져지자 검사는 눈썹을 찌푸렸으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 말을 가로막지는 않았다."]], [["“No, I didn’t go home,”", "\"아니, 나는 집에 들르지 않았소.\""], ["answered Mitya, apparently perfectly composed, but looking at the floor.", "미탸가 겉보기엔 완벽하게 차분한 모습으로, 그러나 바닥을 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“Allow me then to repeat my question,” Nikolay Parfenovitch went on as though creeping up to the subject.", "\"그럼 내 질문을 다시 한번 되풀이하도록 허락해 주시오.\""], ["“Where were you able to procure such a sum all at once, when by your own confession, at five o’clock the same day you—”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마치 슬며시 본론으로 다가가듯 말을 이었다. \"당신 스스로 실토했듯이, 같은 날 다섯 시에 당신은—\""]], [["“I was in want of ten roubles and pledged my pistols with Perhotin, and then went to Madame Hohlakov to borrow three thousand which she wouldn’t give me, and so on, and all the rest of it,”", "\"나는 십 루블이 아쉬워서 권총을 페르호틴에게 저당 잡혔고, 그러고는 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아가 삼천 루블을 빌리려 했으나 그녀가 주지 않았고, 운운, 그밖에 죄다 그런 식이었지요.\""], ["Mitya interrupted sharply.", "미탸가 날카롭게 가로막았다."], ["“Yes, gentlemen, I was in want of it, and suddenly thousands turned up, eh?", "\"그렇소, 여러분, 나는 그게 아쉬웠는데, 별안간 수천 루블이 생겨났다, 그렇지요?"], ["Do you know, gentlemen, you’re both afraid now ‘what if he won’t tell us where he got it?’ That’s just how it is.", "당신들 말이오, 여러분, 두 사람 다 지금 '저자가 돈을 어디서 났는지 말하지 않으면 어쩌나' 하고 두려워하고 있군."], ["I’m not going to tell you, gentlemen.", "바로 그렇소. 나는 당신들한테 말하지 않을 거요, 여러분."], ["You’ve guessed right.", "당신들이 옳게 짚었소."], ["You’ll never know,”", "당신들은 영영 알아내지 못할 거요.\""], ["said Mitya, chipping out each word with extraordinary determination.", "미탸가 비상한 결의로 한마디 한마디를 깎아 내듯 말했다."], ["The lawyers were silent for a moment.", "두 법관은 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“You must understand, Mr. Karamazov, that it is of vital importance for us to know,”", "\"이해하셔야 하오, 카라마조프 씨, 우리로서는 그걸 아는 게 사활이 걸린 중대한 일이오.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, softly and suavely.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 부드럽고 상냥하게 말했다."]], [["“I understand; but still I won’t tell you.”", "\"이해하오. 하지만 그래도 말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["The prosecutor, too, intervened, and again reminded the prisoner that he was at liberty to refuse to answer questions, if he thought it to his interest, and so on.", "검사 또한 끼어들어, 피의자가 자기에게 이롭다고 판단되면 질문에 답하기를 거부할 자유가 있다는 점 따위를 다시금 일러 주었다."], ["But in view of the damage he might do himself by his silence, especially in a case of such importance as—", "그러나 침묵으로 인해 그가 스스로에게 끼칠 수 있는 손해를 고려할 때, 특히 이토록 중대한 사건에서는—"]], [["“And so on, gentlemen, and so on.", "\"운운, 여러분, 운운."], ["Enough!", "됐소!"], ["I’ve heard that rigmarole before,”", "그 장광설이라면 전에도 들었소.\""], ["Mitya interrupted again.", "미탸가 또다시 가로막았다."], ["“I can see for myself how important it is, and that this is the vital point, and still I won’t say.”", "\"그게 얼마나 중대한지, 이게 결정적인 대목이라는 것쯤은 나도 직접 알 수 있소. 그래도 말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“What is it to us?", "\"그게 우리한테 무슨 상관이오?"], ["It’s not our business, but yours.", "그건 우리 일이 아니라 당신 일이오."], ["You are doing yourself harm,”", "당신 스스로에게 해를 끼치고 있는 거요.\""], ["observed Nikolay Parfenovitch nervously.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 신경질적으로 말했다."]], [["“You see, gentlemen, joking apart”—Mitya lifted his eyes and looked firmly at them both—“I had an inkling from the first that we should come to loggerheads at this point.", "\"이거 보시오, 여러분, 농담은 접어 두고\"—미탸가 눈을 들어 두 사람을 똑바로 바라보았다—\"나는 맨 처음부터 우리가 바로 이 대목에서 충돌하게 되리라는 예감이 들었소."], ["But at first when I began to give my evidence, it was all still far away and misty; it was all floating, and I was so simple that I began with the supposition of mutual confidence existing between us.", "하지만 처음 증언을 시작할 때만 해도 그건 다 아직 까마득하고 흐릿했소. 다 둥둥 떠다니고 있었고, 나는 어찌나 순진했던지 우리 사이에 서로 간의 신뢰가 존재한다는 가정에서 출발했소."], ["Now I can see for myself that such confidence is out of the question, for in any case we were bound to come to this cursed stumbling-block.", "이제 보니 그런 신뢰란 애당초 말도 안 되는 것임을 나 스스로 알겠소. 어차피 우리는 이 빌어먹을 걸림돌에 부딪히게 되어 있었으니까."], ["And now we’ve come to it!", "그리고 이제 거기에 다다른 거요!"], ["It’s impossible and there’s an end of it!", "그건 불가능하고, 그걸로 끝이오! 하지만 당신들을 탓하지는 않겠소."], ["But I don’t blame you. You can’t believe it all simply on my word.", "당신들은 그저 내 말 하나만으로 그걸 다 믿을 수는 없을 테니까."], ["I understand that, of course.”", "물론 나도 그건 이해하오.\""]], [["He relapsed into gloomy silence.", "그는 다시 침울한 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["“Couldn’t you, without abandoning your resolution to be silent about the chief point, could you not, at the same time, give us some slight hint as to the nature of the motives which are strong enough to induce you to refuse to answer, at a crisis so full of danger to you?”", "\"핵심 대목에 관해 침묵하겠다는 결심은 버리지 않으면서도, 그와 동시에, 이토록 당신에게 위험천만한 고비에서 답변을 거부하게 만들 만큼 강력한 그 동기의 성격에 대해, 우리에게 약간의 실마리라도 줄 수는 없겠소?\""]], [["Mitya smiled mournfully, almost dreamily.", "미탸는 슬픈 듯, 거의 꿈꾸는 듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“I’m much more good-natured than you think, gentlemen.", "\"나는 당신들이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 마음씨가 좋은 사람이오, 여러분."], ["I’ll tell you the reason why and give you that hint, though you don’t deserve it.", "그 까닭을 말해 주고 그 실마리를 주겠소. 당신들은 그럴 자격이 없지만 말이오."], ["I won’t speak of that, gentlemen, because it would be a stain on my honor.", "나는 그것에 관해 말하지 않을 거요, 여러분, 왜냐하면 그건 내 명예에 오점이 될 테니까."], ["The answer to the question where I got the money would expose me to far greater disgrace than the murder and robbing of my father, if I had murdered and robbed him.", "돈을 어디서 났느냐는 물음에 대한 답은, 설령 내가 아버지를 죽이고 그 돈을 털었다 한들 그 살인과 강도질보다도 훨씬 더 큰 치욕에 나를 빠뜨릴 거요."], ["That’s why I can’t tell you.", "그래서 당신들한테 말할 수 없는 거요."], ["I can’t for fear of disgrace.", "치욕이 두려워서 말이오."], ["What, gentlemen, are you going to write that down?”", "어떻소, 여러분, 그걸 받아 적을 작정이오?\""]], [["“Yes, we’ll write it down,”", "\"그렇소, 받아 적겠소.\""], ["lisped Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다."]], [["“You ought not to write that down about ‘disgrace.’ I only told you that in the goodness of my heart.", "\"'치욕'에 관한 그 대목은 적지 말아야 하오. 그건 그저 내 마음이 좋아서 말해 준 거요."], ["I needn’t have told you.", "굳이 말할 필요도 없었지."], ["I made you a present of it, so to speak, and you pounce upon it at once.", "말하자면 내가 그걸 당신들한테 선물로 준 셈인데, 당신들은 곧바로 그걸 덮치는군."], ["Oh, well, write—write what you like,”", "오, 그래, 적으시오—적고 싶은 대로 적으란 말이오.\""], ["he concluded, with scornful disgust.", "그가 경멸 어린 혐오를 띠고 결론지었다."], ["“I’m not afraid of you and I can still hold up my head before you.”", "\"나는 당신들이 두렵지 않고, 당신들 앞에서 아직도 고개를 빳빳이 들 수 있소.\""]], [["“And can’t you tell us the nature of that disgrace?”", "\"그러면 그 치욕의 성격이 어떤 것인지 우리한테 말해 줄 수는 없겠소?\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch hazarded.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 조심스레 떠보았다."]], [["The prosecutor frowned darkly.", "검사가 어둡게 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“No, no, c’est fini, don’t trouble yourselves.", "\"아니, 아니, 다 끝났소, 애쓰지들 마시오."], ["It’s not worth while soiling one’s hands.", "손을 더럽힐 만한 가치도 없는 일이오."], ["I have soiled myself enough through you as it is.", "나는 당신들 때문에 이미 충분히 나를 더럽혔소."], ["You’re not worth it—no one is ...", "당신들은 그럴 가치가 없는 자들이오—그 누구도 마찬가지지만……"], ["Enough, gentlemen.", "됐소, 여러분."], ["I’m not going on.”", "나는 더 이상 말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["This was said too peremptorily.", "이 말은 너무도 단호하게 내뱉어졌다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch did not insist further, but from Ippolit Kirillovitch’s eyes he saw that he had not given up hope.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 더 이상 우기지 않았으나, 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 눈빛에서 그가 아직 희망을 포기하지 않았다는 것을 읽어 냈다."]], [["“Can you not, at least, tell us what sum you had in your hands when you went into Mr. Perhotin’s—how many roubles exactly?”", "\"그렇다면 적어도, 페르호틴 씨의 집에 들어섰을 때 당신 손에 쥐고 있던 돈이 얼마였는지—정확히 몇 루블이었는지는 말해 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“I can’t tell you that.”", "\"그건 말할 수 없소.\""]], [["“You spoke to Mr. Perhotin, I believe, of having received three thousand from Madame Hohlakov.”", "\"당신은 페르호틴 씨에게 호흘라코바 부인한테서 삼천 루블을 받았다고 말한 것으로 알고 있는데요.\""]], [["“Perhaps I did.", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 말했을지도 모르지."], ["Enough, gentlemen.", "됐소, 여러분."], ["I won’t say how much I had.”", "내가 얼마를 가지고 있었는지는 말하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“Will you be so good then as to tell us how you came here and what you have done since you arrived?”", "\"그렇다면 황송하지만 어떻게 이리로 왔는지, 그리고 도착한 뒤로 무슨 일을 했는지는 말해 주시겠소?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you might ask the people here about that.", "그거라면 여기 사람들한테 물어보면 될 거요."], ["But I’ll tell you if you like.”", "하지만 원한다면 내가 말해 주겠소.\""]], [["He proceeded to do so, but we won’t repeat his story.", "그는 그렇게 하기 시작했으나, 그 이야기는 여기서 되풀이하지 않겠다."], ["He told it dryly and curtly.", "그는 무미건조하고 짤막하게 이야기했다."], ["Of the raptures of his love he said nothing, but told them that he abandoned his determination to shoot himself, owing to “new factors in the case.”", "자기 사랑의 황홀경에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 그저 \"사건의 새로운 정황들\" 때문에 자결하려던 결심을 버렸다고만 말했다."], ["He told the story without going into motives or details.", "그는 동기나 세부 사항으로 파고드는 일 없이 그 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["And this time the lawyers did not worry him much.", "그리고 이번에는 두 법관도 그를 그다지 들볶지 않았다."], ["It was obvious that there was no essential point of interest to them here.", "여기엔 그들에게 흥미로운 본질적 대목이 없다는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“We shall verify all that.", "\"그건 다 우리가 확인하겠소."], ["We will come back to it during the examination of the witnesses, which will, of course, take place in your presence,”", "증인 신문 때 그 문제로 다시 돌아오게 될 텐데, 물론 당신이 입회한 자리에서 진행될 거요.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch in conclusion.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마무리 짓듯 말했다."], ["“And now allow me to request you to lay on the table everything in your possession, especially all the money you still have about you.”", "\"그럼 이제 부탁하건대, 당신이 지니고 있는 모든 것, 특히 아직 몸에 지니고 있는 돈을 전부 탁자 위에 올려놓아 주시오.\""]], [["“My money, gentlemen? Certainly.", "\"내 돈 말이오, 여러분?"], ["I understand that that is necessary.", "좋소. 그게 필요하다는 건 이해하오."], ["I’m surprised, indeed, that you haven’t inquired about it before.", "사실 진작 그걸 묻지 않은 게 오히려 놀랍군."], ["It’s true I couldn’t get away anywhere.", "하기야 나야 어디로 달아날 수도 없었지."], ["I’m sitting here where I can be seen.", "이렇게 빤히 보이는 자리에 앉아 있으니."], ["But here’s my money—count it—take it.", "자, 여기 내 돈이 있소—세어 보고—가져가시오."], ["That’s all, I think.”", "이게 다인 것 같소.\""]], [["He turned it all out of his pockets; even the small change—two pieces of twenty copecks—he pulled out of his waistcoat pocket.", "그는 호주머니에서 그것을 죄다 끄집어냈다. 잔돈까지—이십 코페이카짜리 동전 두 닢을—조끼 호주머니에서 끌어냈다."], ["They counted the money, which amounted to eight hundred and thirty-six roubles, and forty copecks.", "그들은 돈을 세었는데, 모두 합쳐 팔백삼십육 루블 사십 코페이카였다."]], [["“And is that all?”", "\"이게 다요?\""], ["asked the investigating lawyer.", "예심판사가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“You stated just now in your evidence that you spent three hundred roubles at Plotnikovs’.", "\"방금 증언에서 당신은 플로트니코프 상점에서 삼백 루블을 썼다고 진술했소."], ["You gave Perhotin ten, your driver twenty, here you lost two hundred, then....”", "페르호틴에게 십 루블을 주었고, 마부에게 이십 루블, 여기서 이백 루블을 잃었고, 그다음에……\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch reckoned it all up.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그 모든 것을 셈해 나갔다."], ["Mitya helped him readily.", "미탸는 선뜻 그를 거들었다."], ["They recollected every farthing and included it in the reckoning.", "그들은 한 푼까지 죄다 떠올려 셈에 넣었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch hurriedly added up the total.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 서둘러 총액을 합산했다."]], [["“With this eight hundred you must have had about fifteen hundred at first?”", "\"이 팔백 루블까지 치면 당신은 처음에 천오백 루블쯤 가지고 있었던 게 틀림없겠군요?\""]], [["“I suppose so,”", "\"그런 것 같소.\""], ["snapped Mitya.", "미탸가 퉁명스레 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“How is it they all assert there was much more?”", "\"한데 어째서 다들 훨씬 더 많았다고 주장하는 걸까요?\""]], [["“Let them assert it.”", "\"주장하라지요.\""]], [["“But you asserted it yourself.”", "\"하지만 당신 스스로도 그렇게 주장했잖소.\""]], [["“Yes, I did, too.”", "\"그렇소, 나도 그랬지요.\""]], [["“We will compare all this with the evidence of other persons not yet examined.", "\"이건 다 아직 신문하지 않은 다른 사람들의 증언과 대조해 보겠소."], ["Don’t be anxious about your money.", "당신 돈에 대해서는 염려 마시오."], ["It will be properly taken care of and be at your disposal at the conclusion of ...", "잘 보관해 두었다가, 이제 막 시작되는…… 이것이……"], ["what is beginning ... if it appears, or, so to speak, is proved that you have undisputed right to it.", "만약 당신에게 그것에 대한 다툼의 여지 없는 권리가 있는 것으로 드러난다면, 아니, 말하자면 입증된다면, 그게 끝날 때 당신 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 될 거요."], ["Well, and now....”", "자, 그럼 이제……\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch suddenly got up, and informed Mitya firmly that it was his duty and obligation to conduct a minute and thorough search “of your clothes and everything else....”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 별안간 일어서더니, 미탸에게 \"당신의 옷과 그밖에 모든 것\"에 대해 세밀하고 철저한 수색을 하는 것이 자기의 직무이자 의무라고 단호히 알렸다."]], [["“By all means, gentlemen.", "\"얼마든지 하시오, 여러분."], ["I’ll turn out all my pockets, if you like.”", "원한다면 호주머니를 죄다 뒤집어 보이겠소.\""]], [["And he did, in fact, begin turning out his pockets.", "그러고는 실제로 호주머니를 뒤집어 까기 시작했다."]], [["“It will be necessary to take off your clothes, too.”", "\"옷도 벗어야 할 거요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Undress?", "옷을 벗으라고?"], ["Ugh!", "윽!"], ["Damn it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["Won’t you search me as I am!", "이대로 나를 수색하면 안 되겠소!"], ["Can’t you?”", "그럴 수 없는 거요?\""]], [["“It’s utterly impossible, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"그건 절대로 불가능하오, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["You must take off your clothes.”", "옷을 벗으셔야 하오.\""]], [["“As you like,”", "\"좋을 대로 하시오.\""], ["Mitya submitted gloomily; “only, please, not here, but behind the curtains.", "미탸가 침울하게 따랐다. \"다만, 제발, 여기서 말고 휘장 뒤에서 했으면 하오."], ["Who will search them?”", "누가 수색하는 거요?\""]], [["“Behind the curtains, of course.”", "\"물론 휘장 뒤에서 하지요.\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch bent his head in assent.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 동의의 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."], ["His small face wore an expression of peculiar solemnity.", "그의 자그마한 얼굴에는 유난히 엄숙한 표정이 어려 있었다."]], [["# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor Catches Mitya", "# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor Catches Mitya"]], [["Something utterly unexpected and amazing to Mitya followed.", "미탸로서는 도무지 예상치 못한, 놀랍기 짝이 없는 일이 뒤따랐다."], ["He could never, even a minute before, have conceived that any one could behave like that to him, Mitya Karamazov.", "그는 단 일 분 전까지만 해도 누군가가 자기에게, 다름 아닌 미탸 카라마조프에게 이런 식으로 굴 수 있으리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없었다."], ["What was worst of all, there was something humiliating in it, and on their side something “supercilious and scornful.”", "무엇보다 견디기 어려웠던 것은, 그 속에 어딘가 모욕적인 무언가가 깃들어 있었고, 그들 쪽에서도 무언가 \"거만하고 경멸하는\" 기색이 있었다는 점이다."], ["It was nothing to take off his coat, but he was asked to undress further, or rather not asked but “commanded,” he quite understood that.", "외투를 벗는 것쯤이야 아무것도 아니었으나, 그는 그 이상으로 옷을 벗으라는 요구를 받았으니, 아니 요구가 아니라 \"명령\"을 받은 것이었고, 그도 그것을 똑똑히 알아차렸다."], ["From pride and contempt he submitted without a word.", "자존심과 경멸에서 그는 한마디 말도 없이 따랐다."], ["Several peasants accompanied the lawyers and remained on the same side of the curtain.", "농부 몇 사람이 검사 일행을 따라와 휘장 안쪽 그 편에 남아 있었다."], ["“To be ready if force is required,”", "\"혹시 완력이 필요할 때를 대비해서겠지,\""], ["thought Mitya, “and perhaps for some other reason, too.”", "미탸는 생각했다, \"어쩌면 또 다른 이유 때문일 수도 있고.\""]], [["“Well, must I take off my shirt, too?”", "\"흠, 셔츠까지 벗어야 하오?\""], ["he asked sharply, but Nikolay Parfenovitch did not answer.", "그가 날카롭게 물었으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 대답하지 않았다."], ["He was busily engaged with the prosecutor in examining the coat, the trousers, the waistcoat and the cap; and it was evident that they were both much interested in the scrutiny.", "그는 검사와 함께 외투며 바지며 조끼며 모자를 살펴보느라 여념이 없었고, 두 사람 모두 그 검사(檢査)에 자못 깊은 흥미를 쏟고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["“They make no bones about it,”", "\"거리낌이라곤 없군,\""], ["thought Mitya, “they don’t keep up the most elementary politeness.”", "미탸는 생각했다, \"가장 기본적인 예의조차 차리지 않는단 말이지.\""]], [["“I ask you for the second time—need I take off my shirt or not?”", "\"두 번째로 묻겠소—셔츠를 벗어야 하오, 말아야 하오?\""], ["he said, still more sharply and irritably.", "그가 더욱 날카롭고 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t trouble yourself.", "\"수고하실 것 없습니다."], ["We will tell you what to do,”", "어찌해야 할지는 저희가 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch said, and his voice was positively peremptory, or so it seemed to Mitya.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말했는데, 그 목소리는 단연 명령조였거나, 적어도 미탸에게는 그렇게 들렸다."]], [["Meantime a consultation was going on in undertones between the lawyers.", "그러는 동안 두 법조인 사이에 낮은 목소리로 의논이 오갔다."], ["There turned out to be on the coat, especially on the left side at the back, a huge patch of blood, dry, and still stiff.", "알고 보니 외투에는, 특히 뒤쪽 왼편에 커다란 핏자국이 말라붙어 아직도 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있었다."], ["There were bloodstains on the trousers, too.", "바지에도 핏자국이 있었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch, moreover, in the presence of the peasant witnesses, passed his fingers along the collar, the cuffs, and all the seams of the coat and trousers, obviously looking for something—money, of course.", "게다가 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 농부 증인들이 보는 앞에서 외투와 바지의 깃이며 소맷부리며 솔기란 솔기를 모조리 손가락으로 더듬어 보았으니, 무언가를—물론 돈을—찾고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["He didn’t even hide from Mitya his suspicion that he was capable of sewing money up in his clothes.", "그는 미탸가 옷 속에 돈을 꿰매어 넣을 만한 위인이라는 의심조차 미탸 앞에서 감추지 않았다."]], [["“He treats me not as an officer but as a thief,”", "\"이자는 나를 장교가 아니라 도둑놈 취급하는군,\""], ["Mitya muttered to himself.", "미탸는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["They communicated their ideas to one another with amazing frankness.", "그들은 서로 제 생각을 놀라우리만치 거리낌 없이 주고받았다."], ["The secretary, for instance, who was also behind the curtain, fussing about and listening, called Nikolay Parfenovitch’s attention to the cap, which they were also fingering.", "이를테면, 역시 휘장 뒤에서 부산을 떨며 엿듣고 있던 서기가 그들이 마찬가지로 만지작거리던 모자를 두고 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 주의를 끌었다."]], [["“You remember Gridyenko, the copying-clerk,”", "\"필사 서기 그리졘코를 기억하시지요,\""], ["observed the secretary.", "서기가 말했다."], ["“Last summer he received the wages of the whole office, and pretended to have lost the money when he was drunk.", "\"지난여름에 사무소 전체 봉급을 받아 들고는, 술에 취했을 때 그 돈을 잃어버린 척했지 않습니까. 한데 그 돈이 어디서 나왔게요?"], ["And where was it found? Why, in just such pipings in his cap.", "글쎄, 바로 이런 모자의 가두리 안에서 나왔답니다."], ["The hundred-rouble notes were screwed up in little rolls and sewed in the piping.”", "백 루블짜리 지폐를 돌돌 말아 가두리에 꿰매 넣었더라고요.\""]], [["Both the lawyers remembered Gridyenko’s case perfectly, and so laid aside Mitya’s cap, and decided that all his clothes must be more thoroughly examined later.", "두 법조인은 그리졘코 사건을 똑똑히 기억해 내고는, 미탸의 모자를 한쪽에 치워 두면서, 그의 모든 옷가지는 나중에 좀 더 철저히 조사해야겠다고 결정했다."]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"실례합니다만,\""], ["cried Nikolay Parfenovitch, suddenly, noticing that the right cuff of Mitya’s shirt was turned in, and covered with blood, “excuse me, what’s that, blood?”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미탸의 셔츠 오른쪽 소맷부리가 안으로 접혀 들어가 피로 물들어 있는 것을 문득 알아채고 외쳤다. \"실례합니다, 이게 뭡니까, 피입니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Mitya jerked out.", "미탸가 툭 내뱉었다."]], [["“That is, what blood? ...", "\"그러니까, 무슨 피냐는 말씀입니다……"], ["and why is the cuff turned in?”", "그리고 어째서 소맷부리가 안으로 접혀 있습니까?\""]], [["Mitya told him how he had got the sleeve stained with blood looking after Grigory, and had turned it inside when he was washing his hands at Perhotin’s.", "미탸는 그리고리를 돌보다가 소매에 피가 묻었으며, 페르호틴의 집에서 손을 씻을 때 그것을 안으로 접어 넣었던 사정을 그에게 이야기했다."]], [["“You must take off your shirt, too.", "\"셔츠도 벗으셔야겠습니다."], ["That’s very important as material evidence.”", "그건 물증으로서 대단히 중요하니까요.\""]], [["Mitya flushed red and flew into a rage.", "미탸는 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오르더니 격노했다."]], [["“What, am I to stay naked?”", "\"뭐요, 발가벗고 있으란 말이오?\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Don’t disturb yourself.", "\"심려치 마십시오."], ["We will arrange something.", "저희가 어떻게든 마련하겠습니다."], ["And meanwhile take off your socks.”", "그리고 그동안 양말도 벗으십시오.\""]], [["“You’re not joking?", "\"농담은 아니겠지요?"], ["Is that really necessary?”", "그게 정말 필요하단 말이오?\""], ["Mitya’s eyes flashed.", "미탸의 두 눈이 번쩍였다."]], [["“We are in no mood for joking,”", "\"저희는 농담할 처지가 못 됩니다.\""], ["answered Nikolay Parfenovitch sternly.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 엄하게 대답했다."]], [["“Well, if I must—”", "\"흠, 정 그래야 한다면—\""], ["muttered Mitya, and sitting down on the bed, he took off his socks.", "미탸는 중얼거리며 침대에 걸터앉아 양말을 벗었다."], ["He felt unbearably awkward.", "그는 견딜 수 없이 어색했다."], ["All were clothed, while he was naked, and strange to say, when he was undressed he felt somehow guilty in their presence, and was almost ready to believe himself that he was inferior to them, and that now they had a perfect right to despise him.", "모두가 옷을 입고 있는데 자기만 발가벗은 꼴이었고, 이상한 노릇이지만 옷을 벗고 나니 그들 앞에서 어쩐지 죄지은 사람이 된 듯한 기분이 들었으며, 자기가 그들보다 못한 인간이고 이제 그들이 자기를 경멸할 완전한 권리를 지녔다고 거의 스스로 믿어 버릴 지경이었다."]], [["“When all are undressed, one is somehow not ashamed, but when one’s the only one undressed and everybody is looking, it’s degrading,”", "\"모두가 옷을 벗고 있으면 어쩐지 부끄럽지 않은데, 혼자만 벗고 있고 모두가 쳐다보고 있으면 그건 굴욕이야,\""], ["he kept repeating to himself, again and again.", "그는 거듭거듭 혼잣말을 되뇌었다."], ["“It’s like a dream, I’ve sometimes dreamed of being in such degrading positions.”", "\"꿈을 꾸는 것 같군, 나는 가끔 이런 굴욕스러운 처지에 빠지는 꿈을 꾸곤 했지.\""], ["It was a misery to him to take off his socks.", "양말을 벗는 것은 그에게 고역이었다."], ["They were very dirty, and so were his underclothes, and now every one could see it.", "양말은 몹시 더러웠고, 속옷도 마찬가지였는데, 이제 누구나 그것을 볼 수 있게 된 것이다."], ["And what was worse, he disliked his feet.", "게다가 더 고약한 것은, 그가 자기 발을 싫어한다는 점이었다."], ["All his life he had thought both his big toes hideous.", "그는 평생토록 두 엄지발가락이 흉측하다고 여겨 왔다."], ["He particularly loathed the coarse, flat, crooked nail on the right one, and now they would all see it.", "특히 오른쪽 발의 거칠고 납작하며 비뚤어진 발톱을 끔찍이도 혐오했는데, 이제 그들 모두가 그것을 보게 될 판이었다."], ["Feeling intolerably ashamed made him, at once and intentionally, rougher.", "참을 수 없이 부끄럽다는 느낌이 그를 단숨에, 또 일부러, 더 거칠게 만들었다."], ["He pulled off his shirt, himself.", "그는 셔츠를 손수 벗어 던졌다."]], [["“Would you like to look anywhere else if you’re not ashamed to?”", "\"부끄럽지 않으시다면 더 살펴보고 싶은 데가 있으십니까?\""]], [["“No, there’s no need to, at present.”", "\"아니요, 지금으로서는 그럴 필요가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Well, am I to stay naked like this?”", "\"흠, 그럼 이렇게 발가벗고 있으란 말이오?\""], ["he added savagely.", "그가 사납게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, that can’t be helped for the time....", "\"네, 당분간은 어쩔 수가 없습니다……"], ["Kindly sit down here for a while.", "잠시 이쪽에 앉아 계시지요."], ["You can wrap yourself in a quilt from the bed, and I ...", "침대의 누비이불을 둘러도 좋습니다. 그리고 저는……"], ["I’ll see to all this.”", "제가 이 모든 걸 처리하겠습니다.\""]], [["All the things were shown to the witnesses.", "모든 물건이 증인들에게 제시되었다."], ["The report of the search was drawn up, and at last Nikolay Parfenovitch went out, and the clothes were carried out after him.", "수색 조서가 작성되었고, 마침내 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 밖으로 나가자, 옷가지도 그 뒤를 따라 들려 나갔다."], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch went out, too.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치도 나갔다."], ["Mitya was left alone with the peasants, who stood in silence, never taking their eyes off him.", "미탸는 농부들과 단둘이 남았는데, 그들은 잠자코 서서 그에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["Mitya wrapped himself up in the quilt.", "미탸는 누비이불을 둘렀다."], ["He felt cold.", "그는 추웠다."], ["His bare feet stuck out, and he couldn’t pull the quilt over so as to cover them.", "맨발이 비죽 삐져나왔으나, 그것을 덮을 만큼 이불을 끌어당길 수가 없었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch seemed to be gone a long time, “an insufferable time.”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 오래도록, \"견딜 수 없으리만치 오래\" 돌아오지 않는 듯했다."], ["“He thinks of me as a puppy,”", "\"저자는 나를 강아지쯤으로 여기는군,\""], ["thought Mitya, gnashing his teeth.", "미탸는 이를 갈며 생각했다."], ["“That rotten prosecutor has gone, too, contemptuous no doubt, it disgusts him to see me naked!”", "\"저 썩어 빠진 검사놈도 가 버렸어, 분명 경멸하는 거지, 내 발가벗은 꼴을 보는 게 역겨운 거야!\""]], [["Mitya imagined, however, that his clothes would be examined and returned to him.", "그래도 미탸는 자기 옷이 조사된 뒤에 도로 돌아오리라 짐작하고 있었다."], ["But what was his indignation when Nikolay Parfenovitch came back with quite different clothes, brought in behind him by a peasant.", "그러나 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 전혀 다른 옷을, 그 뒤를 따라 들어온 농부의 손에 들려서 가지고 돌아왔을 때, 그의 분노가 어떠했겠는가."]], [["“Here are clothes for you,”", "\"여기 입으실 옷입니다.\""], ["he observed airily, seeming well satisfied with the success of his mission.", "그는 자기 임무의 성공에 자못 흡족해하는 기색으로 가뿐하게 말했다."], ["“Mr. Kalganov has kindly provided these for this unusual emergency, as well as a clean shirt.", "\"이 뜻밖의 비상사태에 칼가노프 씨가 친절히 이것들을 마련해 주셨고, 깨끗한 셔츠까지 내주셨습니다."], ["Luckily he had them all in his trunk.", "다행히 그가 그것들을 모두 자기 트렁크에 가지고 있더군요."], ["You can keep your own socks and underclothes.”", "양말과 속옷은 본인 것을 그대로 입으셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["Mitya flew into a passion.", "미탸는 발끈 성을 냈다."]], [["“I won’t have other people’s clothes!”", "\"남의 옷은 입지 않겠소!\""], ["he shouted menacingly, “give me my own!”", "그가 위협하듯 소리쳤다, \"내 옷을 내놓으시오!\""]], [["“It’s impossible!”", "\"그건 불가능합니다!\""]], [["“Give me my own.", "\"내 옷을 내놓으시오."], ["Damn Kalganov and his clothes, too!”", "칼가노프도, 그자의 옷도 빌어먹을!\""]], [["It was a long time before they could persuade him.", "그들이 그를 설득하기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."], ["But they succeeded somehow in quieting him down.", "그래도 그들은 어떻게든 그를 진정시키는 데 성공했다."], ["They impressed upon him that his clothes, being stained with blood, must be “included with the other material evidence,”", "그들은 그의 옷이 피로 물들어 있어 \"다른 물증들과 함께 포함되어야\""], ["and that they “had not even the right to let him have them now ...", "하며, \"사건의 있을 법한 결말을 고려할 때……"], ["taking into consideration the possible outcome of the case.”", "지금 그것을 내드릴 권리조차 없다\"는 점을 그에게 거듭 일깨웠다."], ["Mitya at last understood this.", "미탸도 마침내 이를 납득했다."], ["He subsided into gloomy silence and hurriedly dressed himself.", "그는 침울한 침묵 속으로 가라앉아 서둘러 옷을 입었다."], ["He merely observed, as he put them on, that the clothes were much better than his old ones, and that he disliked “gaining by the change.”", "그는 다만 옷을 입으면서, 이 옷이 자기 헌 옷보다 훨씬 좋다는 것과, 자기가 \"이런 교환으로 득을 보는\" 것이 싫다는 말만 했다."], ["The coat was, besides, “ridiculously tight.", "게다가 외투는 \"우스꽝스러우리만치 꽉 끼었다."], ["Am I to be dressed up like a fool ...", "내가 무슨 바보처럼 차려입어야 하오……"], ["for your amusement?”", "당신네들 구경거리가 되라고?\""]], [["They urged upon him again that he was exaggerating, that Kalganov was only a little taller, so that only the trousers might be a little too long.", "그들은 그가 과장하고 있을 뿐이라고, 칼가노프가 키가 조금 클 따름이라 바지만 약간 길지도 모른다고 다시 그를 다독였다."], ["But the coat turned out to be really tight in the shoulders.", "그러나 외투는 정말로 어깨가 꽉 끼었다."]], [["“Damn it all!", "\"제기랄!"], ["I can hardly button it,”", "단추도 겨우 채우겠군,\""], ["Mitya grumbled.", "미탸가 투덜거렸다."], ["“Be so good as to tell Mr. Kalganov from me that I didn’t ask for his clothes, and it’s not my doing that they’ve dressed me up like a clown.”", "\"내가 칼가노프 씨한테 옷을 달라고 한 적도 없고, 나를 어릿광대처럼 꾸며 놓은 게 내 짓도 아니라고, 부디 그분께 내 말을 전해 주시오.\""]], [["“He understands that, and is sorry ...", "\"그분도 그걸 알고 있고, 미안해하고 있습니다……"], ["I mean, not sorry to lend you his clothes, but sorry about all this business,”", "그러니까, 옷을 빌려준 게 미안하다는 게 아니라, 이 모든 사태가 안타깝다는 거지요.\""], ["mumbled Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 우물거렸다."]], [["“Confound his sorrow!", "\"그자의 동정 따위 집어치우라지!"], ["Well, where now?", "자, 이제 어디로 가오?"], ["Am I to go on sitting here?”", "여기 계속 앉아 있어야 하오?\""]], [["He was asked to go back to the “other room.”", "그는 \"다른 방\"으로 돌아가 달라는 청을 받았다."], ["Mitya went in, scowling with anger, and trying to avoid looking at any one.", "미탸는 분노로 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 누구와도 눈을 마주치지 않으려 애쓰며 들어갔다."], ["Dressed in another man’s clothes he felt himself disgraced, even in the eyes of the peasants, and of Trifon Borissovitch, whose face appeared, for some reason, in the doorway, and vanished immediately.", "남의 옷을 입은 그는 농부들의 눈에도, 또 무슨 까닭인지 문간에 얼굴을 비쳤다가 이내 사라져 버린 트리폰 보리소비치의 눈에도 자기가 망신을 당한 듯 느껴졌다."], ["“He’s come to look at me dressed up,”", "\"이자는 내가 차려입은 꼴을 구경하러 온 거로군,\""], ["thought Mitya.", "미탸는 생각했다."], ["He sat down on the same chair as before.", "그는 아까와 같은 의자에 앉았다."], ["He had an absurd nightmarish feeling, as though he were out of his mind.", "그는 마치 제정신이 아닌 양 터무니없는 악몽 같은 기분에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["“Well, what now?", "\"흠, 이제 어쩌겠소?"], ["Are you going to flog me?", "나를 매질이라도 하겠소?"], ["That’s all that’s left for you,”", "당신들한테 남은 건 그것뿐일 텐데,\""], ["he said, clenching his teeth and addressing the prosecutor.", "그가 이를 악물고 검사에게 말했다."], ["He would not turn to Nikolay Parfenovitch, as though he disdained to speak to him.", "그는 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게는, 그와는 말을 섞기조차 꺼린다는 듯, 몸을 돌리려 하지 않았다."]], [["“He looked too closely at my socks, and turned them inside out on purpose to show every one how dirty they were—the scoundrel!”", "\"저자는 내 양말을 너무 빤히 들여다보더니, 그게 얼마나 더러운지 모두에게 보여 주려고 일부러 뒤집어 놓았어—저 비열한 놈이!\""]], [["“Well, now we must proceed to the examination of witnesses,”", "\"자, 이제 증인 신문으로 넘어가야겠습니다,\""], ["observed Nikolay Parfenovitch, as though in reply to Mitya’s question.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 마치 미탸의 물음에 답하기라도 하듯 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said the prosecutor thoughtfully, as though reflecting on something.", "검사가 무언가를 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 사려 깊게 말했다."]], [["“We’ve done what we could in your interest, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"저희는 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 했습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치,\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch went on, “but having received from you such an uncompromising refusal to explain to us the source from which you obtained the money found upon you, we are, at the present moment—”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 말을 이었다. \"그러나 당신 몸에서 발견된 돈의 출처를 설명하기를 그토록 단호히 거부하셨으니, 저희로서는 지금 이 순간—\""]], [["“What is the stone in your ring?”", "\"당신 반지에 박힌 돌이 뭐요?\""], ["Mitya interrupted suddenly, as though awakening from a reverie.", "미탸가 마치 몽상에서 깨어나기라도 한 듯 불쑥 말을 가로막았다."], ["He pointed to one of the three large rings adorning Nikolay Parfenovitch’s right hand.", "그는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 오른손을 장식한 큼직한 반지 세 개 가운데 하나를 가리켰다."]], [["“Ring?”", "\"반지요?\""], ["repeated Nikolay Parfenovitch with surprise.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀라서 되물었다."]], [["“Yes, that one ...", "\"그렇소, 그거 말이오……"], ["on your middle finger, with the little veins in it, what stone is that?”", "가운뎃손가락에 낀, 자잘한 실금이 들어 있는 거, 그게 무슨 돌이오?\""], ["Mitya persisted, like a peevish child.", "미탸가 짜증 난 어린아이처럼 집요하게 캐물었다."]], [["“That’s a smoky topaz,”", "\"그건 연수정입니다,\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, smiling.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Would you like to look at it?", "\"보고 싶으십니까?"], ["I’ll take it off ...”", "빼 드리지요……\""]], [["“No, don’t take it off,”", "\"아니, 빼지 마시오,\""], ["cried Mitya furiously, suddenly waking up, and angry with himself.", "미탸가 사납게 외치더니, 문득 정신을 차리고 자기 자신에게 화를 냈다."], ["“Don’t take it off ...", "\"빼지 마시오……"], ["there’s no need....", "그럴 필요 없소……"], ["Damn it!...", "제기랄!……"], ["Gentlemen, you’ve sullied my heart!", "여러분, 당신들은 내 마음을 더럽혀 놓았소!"], ["Can you suppose that I would conceal it from you, if I had really killed my father, that I would shuffle, lie, and hide myself?", "내가 정말로 아버지를 죽였다면, 그걸 당신들한테 감추고, 발뺌하고, 거짓말하고, 몸을 숨길 거라고 생각하시오?"], ["No, that’s not like Dmitri Karamazov, that he couldn’t do, and if I were guilty, I swear I shouldn’t have waited for your coming, or for the sunrise as I meant at first, but should have killed myself before this, without waiting for the dawn!", "아니, 그건 드미트리 카라마조프답지 않소, 그런 짓은 그가 할 수 없는 일이오, 만약 내가 죄인이었다면, 맹세컨대 나는 당신들이 오기를, 처음에 작정했던 대로 해가 뜨기를 기다리지 않고, 동이 트기도 전에 진작 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 거요!"], ["I know that about myself now.", "이제 나는 나 자신에 대해 그걸 알고 있소."], ["I couldn’t have learnt so much in twenty years as I’ve found out in this accursed night!...", "나는 이 저주받은 하룻밤에 깨달은 것만큼, 이십 년 동안에도 배우지 못했을 거요!……"], ["And should I have been like this on this night, and at this moment, sitting with you, could I have talked like this, could I have moved like this, could I have looked at you and at the world like this, if I had really been the murderer of my father, when the very thought of having accidentally killed Grigory gave me no peace all night—not from fear—oh, not simply from fear of your punishment!", "그리고 내가 정말로 아버지를 죽인 자였다면, 실수로 그리고리를 죽였을지도 모른다는 생각만으로도 밤새도록 마음이 편치 못했던 내가—그게 두려움 때문이 아니라, 오, 그저 당신들의 형벌이 두려워서가 아니라! 그 치욕 때문이었는데—이 밤에, 이 순간에, 당신들과 마주 앉아, 이렇게 이야기하고, 이렇게 몸을 움직이고, 당신들과 이 세상을 이렇게 바라볼 수 있었겠소?"], ["The disgrace of it! And you expect me to be open with such scoffers as you, who see nothing and believe in nothing, blind moles and scoffers, and to tell you another nasty thing I’ve done, another disgrace, even if that would save me from your accusation!", "그러니 당신들 같은, 아무것도 보지 못하고 아무것도 믿지 않는, 눈먼 두더지에 조롱꾼인 자들에게 내가 속을 털어놓고, 그것이 당신들의 고발에서 나를 구해 준다 할지라도, 내가 저지른 또 다른 추악한 짓, 또 다른 치욕을 당신들에게 이야기하기를 바란단 말이오!"], ["No, better Siberia!", "아니, 차라리 시베리아가 낫소!"], ["The man who opened the door to my father and went in at that door, he killed him, he robbed him.", "내 아버지에게 문을 열어 주고 그 문으로 들어간 자, 그자가 아버지를 죽였고, 아버지의 것을 빼앗았소."], ["Who was he?", "그자가 누구냐고?"], ["I’m racking my brains and can’t think who.", "머리를 쥐어짜 봐도 누구인지 떠오르지 않소."], ["But I can tell you it was not Dmitri Karamazov, and that’s all I can tell you, and that’s enough, enough, leave me alone....", "하지만 그게 드미트리 카라마조프가 아니라는 것만은 당신들한테 말할 수 있고, 그게 내가 당신들한테 말할 수 있는 전부이며, 그것으로 충분하오, 충분해, 나를 좀 내버려 두시오……"], ["Exile me, punish me, but don’t bother me any more.", "나를 유형 보내든 벌하든 마음대로 하시오, 하지만 더는 나를 들볶지 마시오."], ["I’ll say no more.", "더는 아무 말도 하지 않겠소."], ["Call your witnesses!”", "증인들이나 부르시오!\""]], [["Mitya uttered his sudden monologue as though he were determined to be absolutely silent for the future.", "미탸는 앞으로는 절대로 입을 다물기로 작정한 사람처럼 이 느닷없는 독백을 쏟아 냈다."], ["The prosecutor watched him the whole time and only when he had ceased speaking, observed, as though it were the most ordinary thing, with the most frigid and composed air:", "검사는 그동안 줄곧 그를 지켜보다가, 그가 말을 그친 뒤에야, 마치 그것이 더없이 예사로운 일이라는 듯, 더없이 냉랭하고 침착한 태도로 입을 열었다."]], [["“Oh, about the open door of which you spoke just now, we may as well inform you, by the way, now, of a very interesting piece of evidence of the greatest importance both to you and to us, that has been given us by Grigory, the old man you wounded.", "\"오, 방금 말씀하신 그 열린 문에 대해서, 마침 이 자리에서 알려 드려도 좋겠군요, 당신께도 저희에게도 더없이 중대한, 무척 흥미로운 증언 하나를요. 당신이 부상을 입힌 노인, 그리고리가 그것을 진술했습니다."], ["On his recovery, he clearly and emphatically stated, in reply to our questions, that when, on coming out to the steps, and hearing a noise in the garden, he made up his mind to go into it through the little gate which stood open, before he noticed you running, as you have told us already, in the dark from the open window where you saw your father, he, Grigory, glanced to the left, and, while noticing the open window, observed at the same time, much nearer to him, the door, standing wide open—that door which you have stated to have been shut the whole time you were in the garden.", "그는 회복된 뒤 저희 질문에 답하여, 또렷하고 단호하게 진술했습니다. 즉 그가 현관 계단으로 나와 정원에서 나는 소리를 듣고, 열려 있던 쪽문을 통해 정원으로 들어가기로 마음먹었을 때, 당신이 이미 말씀하신 대로 어둠 속에서 당신 아버지를 보았던 그 열린 창문에서 달아나는 것을 알아채기에 앞서, 그가, 그리고리가 왼쪽을 흘끗 보았는데, 그 열린 창문을 알아채는 동시에, 자기 가까이에서 활짝 열린 채 서 있는 그 문을—당신이 정원에 있는 내내 닫혀 있었다고 진술한 바로 그 문을—보았다는 겁니다."], ["I will not conceal from you that Grigory himself confidently affirms and bears witness that you must have run from that door, though, of course, he did not see you do so with his own eyes, since he only noticed you first some distance away in the garden, running towards the fence.”", "당신께 감추지 않겠습니다만, 그리고리 본인은 당신이 그 문에서 달려 나왔음이 틀림없다고 확신에 차 단언하며 증언하고 있습니다. 물론 그가 그 광경을 제 눈으로 직접 본 것은 아닙니다, 그가 당신을 처음 알아챈 것은 이미 정원 저편 멀찍이서, 당신이 울타리를 향해 달려가던 때였으니까요.\""]], [["Mitya had leapt up from his chair half-way through this speech.", "미탸는 이 말이 절반쯤 진행될 무렵 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리!\""], ["he yelled, in a sudden frenzy, “it’s a barefaced lie.", "그가 별안간 광란에 빠져 고래고래 외쳤다, \"뻔뻔스러운 거짓말이오."], ["He couldn’t have seen the door open because it was shut.", "그 문은 닫혀 있었으니 그자가 열린 걸 봤을 리가 없소."], ["He’s lying!”", "그자가 거짓말을 하는 거요!\""]], [["“I consider it my duty to repeat that he is firm in his statement.", "\"그가 그 진술을 굳게 고수하고 있다는 점을 거듭 말씀드리는 게 제 의무라고 봅니다."], ["He does not waver.", "그는 흔들리지 않습니다."], ["He adheres to it.", "그 진술을 고수하고 있어요."], ["We’ve cross-examined him several times.”", "저희가 그를 여러 차례 추궁해 봤습니다.\""]], [["“Precisely.", "\"바로 그렇습니다."], ["I have cross-examined him several times,”", "제가 그를 여러 차례 추궁했지요.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch confirmed warmly.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 열을 띠며 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“It’s false, false!", "\"거짓이오, 거짓!"], ["It’s either an attempt to slander me, or the hallucination of a madman,”", "그건 나를 모함하려는 수작이거나, 미친 자의 헛것이오,\""], ["Mitya still shouted.", "미탸가 여전히 소리쳤다."], ["“He’s simply raving, from loss of blood, from the wound.", "\"그자는 피를 흘려서, 상처 때문에 헛소리를 하는 것뿐이오."], ["He must have fancied it when he came to....", "정신이 들었을 때 헛것을 본 게 틀림없소……"], ["He’s raving.”", "그자는 헛소리를 하는 거요.\""]], [["“Yes, but he noticed the open door, not when he came to after his injuries, but before that, as soon as he went into the garden from the lodge.”", "\"하지만 그는 부상을 입고 정신이 든 뒤가 아니라, 그 전에, 곧 별채에서 정원으로 들어서자마자 열린 문을 알아챘습니다.\""]], [["“But it’s false, it’s false!", "\"하지만 거짓이오, 거짓!"], ["It can’t be so!", "그럴 리가 없소!"], ["He’s slandering me from spite....", "그자는 앙심을 품고 나를 모함하는 거요……"], ["He couldn’t have seen it ...", "그자가 그걸 봤을 리 없소……"], ["I didn’t come from the door,”", "나는 그 문에서 나오지 않았소,\""], ["gasped Mitya.", "미탸가 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["The prosecutor turned to Nikolay Parfenovitch and said to him impressively:", "검사는 니콜라이 파르페노비치 쪽으로 몸을 돌리고 의미심장하게 말했다."]], [["“Confront him with it.”", "\"그것을 들이대 보시오.\""]], [["“Do you recognize this object?”", "\"이 물건을 알아보시겠습니까?\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch laid upon the table a large and thick official envelope, on which three seals still remained intact.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 봉인 세 개가 아직 멀쩡히 남아 있는, 크고 두툼한 공문서용 봉투를 탁자 위에 놓았다."], ["The envelope was empty, and slit open at one end.", "봉투는 비어 있었고, 한쪽 끝이 칼로 베이듯 뜯겨 있었다."], ["Mitya stared at it with open eyes.", "미탸는 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 그것을 응시했다."]], [["“It ...", "\"그건……"], ["it must be that envelope of my father’s, the envelope that contained the three thousand roubles ...", "그건 우리 아버지의 봉투가 틀림없소, 삼천 루블이 들어 있던 그 봉투……"], ["and if there’s inscribed on it, allow me, ‘For my little chicken’ ...", "그리고 거기 적혀 있다면, 어디 봅시다, '내 귀여운 병아리에게'라고……"], ["yes—three thousand!”", "그렇소—삼천 루블!\""], ["he shouted, “do you see, three thousand, do you see?”", "그가 소리쳤다, \"보이시오, 삼천 루블, 보이시오?\""]], [["“Of course, we see.", "\"물론 보입니다."], ["But we didn’t find the money in it.", "하지만 저희는 그 안에서 돈을 찾지 못했습니다."], ["It was empty, and lying on the floor by the bed, behind the screen.”", "그것은 비어 있었고, 칸막이 뒤 침대 곁 바닥에 떨어져 있었습니다.\""]], [["For some seconds Mitya stood as though thunderstruck.", "몇 초 동안 미탸는 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 서 있었다."]], [["“Gentlemen, it’s Smerdyakov!”", "\"여러분, 그건 스메르댜코프요!\""], ["he shouted suddenly, at the top of his voice.", "그가 별안간 목청을 다해 외쳤다."], ["“It’s he who’s murdered him!", "\"그자가 아버지를 죽인 거요!"], ["He’s robbed him!", "그자가 아버지를 턴 거요!"], ["No one else knew where the old man hid the envelope.", "노인이 그 봉투를 어디 숨겼는지 아는 자는 그자 말고 아무도 없었소."], ["It’s Smerdyakov, that’s clear, now!”", "그건 스메르댜코프요, 이제 분명해졌소!\""]], [["“But you, too, knew of the envelope and that it was under the pillow.”", "\"하지만 당신도 그 봉투를 알고 있었고, 그게 베개 밑에 있다는 것도 알고 있었지요.\""]], [["“I never knew it.", "\"나는 결코 그걸 몰랐소."], ["I’ve never seen it.", "한 번도 본 적이 없소."], ["This is the first time I’ve looked at it.", "그걸 들여다본 건 이번이 처음이오."], ["I’d only heard of it from Smerdyakov....", "그저 스메르댜코프한테 들었을 뿐이오……"], ["He was the only one who knew where the old man kept it hidden, I didn’t know ...”", "노인이 그걸 어디 숨겨 두는지 아는 자는 그자뿐이었소, 나는 몰랐단 말이오……\""], ["Mitya was completely breathless.", "미탸는 완전히 숨이 차 헐떡였다."]], [["“But you told us yourself that the envelope was under your deceased father’s pillow.", "\"하지만 당신은 그 봉투가 돌아가신 부친의 베개 밑에 있었다고 직접 말씀하셨습니다."], ["You especially stated that it was under the pillow, so you must have known it.”", "베개 밑에 있었다고 특별히 진술하셨으니, 당신은 그것을 알고 있었던 게 분명합니다.\""]], [["“We’ve got it written down,”", "\"저희가 그걸 기록해 두었습니다.\""], ["confirmed Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 확인해 주었다."]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 되오!"], ["It’s absurd!", "터무니없는 소리요!"], ["I’d no idea it was under the pillow.", "나는 그게 베개 밑에 있는 줄 전혀 몰랐소."], ["And perhaps it wasn’t under the pillow at all....", "어쩌면 애초에 베개 밑에 있지도 않았을지 모르오……"], ["It was just a chance guess that it was under the pillow.", "그게 베개 밑에 있다는 건 그저 우연히 한 짐작이었소."], ["What does Smerdyakov say?", "스메르댜코프는 뭐라고 하오?"], ["Have you asked him where it was?", "그자한테 그게 어디 있었는지 물어봤소?"], ["What does Smerdyakov say? that’s the chief point....", "스메르댜코프가 뭐라고 하느냐, 그게 핵심이오……"], ["And I went out of my way to tell lies against myself....", "그런데 나는 일부러 나서서 내게 불리한 거짓말을 한 셈이오……"], ["I told you without thinking that it was under the pillow, and now you— Oh, you know how one says the wrong thing, without meaning it.", "나는 아무 생각 없이 그게 베개 밑에 있었다고 당신들한테 말한 건데, 이제 당신들은—오, 사람이 어떻게 본의 아니게 엉뚱한 소리를 하는지 당신들도 알 거 아니오."], ["No one knew but Smerdyakov, only Smerdyakov, and no one else....", "스메르댜코프 말고는 아무도 몰랐소, 오직 스메르댜코프뿐, 다른 누구도 아니오……"], ["He didn’t even tell me where it was!", "그자는 그게 어디 있는지 나한테 말해 주지도 않았소!"], ["But it’s his doing, his doing; there’s no doubt about it, he murdered him, that’s as clear as daylight now,”", "하지만 이건 그자의 짓이오, 그자의 짓이오, 의심할 여지가 없소, 그자가 아버지를 죽인 거요, 이제 그건 대낮처럼 분명하오,\""], ["Mitya exclaimed more and more frantically, repeating himself incoherently, and growing more and more exasperated and excited.", "미탸는 갈수록 미친 듯이 외쳐 대며, 두서없이 같은 말을 되풀이했고, 점점 더 격분하고 흥분해 갔다."], ["“You must understand that, and arrest him at once....", "\"당신들은 그걸 깨닫고 당장 그자를 체포해야 하오……"], ["He must have killed him while I was running away and while Grigory was unconscious, that’s clear now....", "내가 달아나는 동안, 그리고리가 정신을 잃은 동안 그자가 아버지를 죽인 게 틀림없소, 이제 그건 분명하오……"], ["He gave the signal and father opened to him ...", "그자가 신호를 보냈고 아버지가 그자에게 문을 열어 준 거요……"], ["for no one but he knew the signal, and without the signal father would never have opened the door....”", "그자 말고는 아무도 그 신호를 몰랐고, 신호가 없으면 아버지는 결코 문을 열어 주지 않았을 테니까……\""]], [["“But you’re again forgetting the circumstance,”", "\"하지만 당신은 또 그 사정을 잊고 계시는군요,\""], ["the prosecutor observed, still speaking with the same restraint, though with a note of triumph, “that there was no need to give the signal if the door already stood open when you were there, while you were in the garden....”", "검사가 여전히 똑같이 절제된 어조로, 그러나 어딘가 승리의 가락을 담아 말했다. \"당신이 거기 있었을 때, 당신이 정원에 있는 동안 그 문이 이미 열려 있었다면 신호를 보낼 필요가 없었다는 사정 말입니다……\""]], [["“The door, the door,”", "\"문, 그 문이……\""], ["muttered Mitya, and he stared speechless at the prosecutor.", "미탸가 중얼거리며, 아무 말도 못 한 채 검사를 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["He sank back helpless in his chair.", "그는 맥없이 의자에 도로 주저앉았다."], ["All were silent.", "모두가 잠잠했다."]], [["“Yes, the door!...", "\"그래, 그 문이!……"], ["It’s a nightmare!", "이건 악몽이야!"], ["God is against me!”", "하느님이 나를 적대하시는구나!\""], ["he exclaimed, staring before him in complete stupefaction.", "그가 완전히 얼이 빠진 채 앞을 멍하니 응시하며 외쳤다."]], [["“Come, you see,”", "\"자, 보십시오,\""], ["the prosecutor went on with dignity, “and you can judge for yourself, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "검사가 위엄 있게 말을 이었다. \"당신 스스로 판단해 보실 수 있습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["On the one hand we have the evidence of the open door from which you ran out, a fact which overwhelms you and us.", "한편으로는 당신이 달려 나온 그 열린 문에 대한 증언이 있는데, 이는 당신도 저희도 압도하는 사실입니다."], ["On the other side your incomprehensible, persistent, and, so to speak, obdurate silence with regard to the source from which you obtained the money which was so suddenly seen in your hands, when only three hours earlier, on your own showing, you pledged your pistols for the sake of ten roubles!", "다른 한편으로는, 불과 세 시간 전만 해도, 당신 자신의 진술대로 십 루블 때문에 권총을 저당 잡혔던 당신의 손에 그토록 느닷없이 나타난 돈, 그 출처에 대한 당신의 납득할 수 없고, 집요하며, 말하자면 완강한 침묵이 있습니다!"], ["In view of all these facts, judge for yourself.", "이 모든 사실에 비추어, 스스로 판단해 보십시오."], ["What are we to believe, and what can we depend upon?", "저희가 무엇을 믿어야 하며, 무엇에 의지할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["And don’t accuse us of being ‘frigid, cynical, scoffing people,’ who are incapable of believing in the generous impulses of your heart....", "그리고 저희를, 당신 마음속의 너그러운 충동을 믿을 줄 모르는 '냉랭하고, 냉소적이고, 조롱이나 일삼는 자들'이라고 탓하지 마십시오……"], ["Try to enter into our position ...”", "저희 처지가 되어 헤아려 보십시오……\""]], [["Mitya was indescribably agitated.", "미탸는 형언할 수 없으리만치 동요했다."], ["He turned pale.", "그는 얼굴이 창백해졌다."]], [["“Very well!”", "\"좋소!\""], ["he exclaimed suddenly.", "그가 별안간 외쳤다."], ["“I will tell you my secret.", "\"내 비밀을 말하겠소."], ["I’ll tell you where I got the money!...", "내가 그 돈을 어디서 손에 넣었는지 말하겠소!……"], ["I’ll reveal my shame, that I may not have to blame myself or you hereafter.”", "내 치욕을 드러내겠소, 그래야 앞으로 나 자신도, 당신들도 탓하지 않게 될 테니.\""]], [["“And believe me, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"믿어 주십시오, 드미트리 표도로비치,\""], ["put in Nikolay Parfenovitch, in a voice of almost pathetic delight, “that every sincere and complete confession on your part at this moment may, later on, have an immense influence in your favor, and may, indeed, moreover—”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 거의 애처로우리만치 기쁨에 찬 목소리로 끼어들었다. \"이 순간 당신이 진실하고 온전한 고백을 하신다면, 그것이 나중에 당신께 더없이 유리하게 작용할 수 있고, 실로, 게다가—\""]], [["But the prosecutor gave him a slight shove under the table, and he checked himself in time.", "그러나 검사가 탁자 밑으로 그를 살짝 떠밀자, 그는 때맞춰 말을 삼켰다."], ["Mitya, it is true, had not heard him.", "사실 미탸는 그의 말을 듣지 못했다."]], [["# Chapter VII. Mitya’s Great Secret. Received With Hisses", "# Chapter VII. Mitya’s Great Secret. Received With Hisses"]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["he began, still in the same agitation, “I want to make a full confession: that money was my own.”", "그는 여전히 같은 흥분에 사로잡힌 채 입을 열었다. \"나는 모든 것을 낱낱이 고백하고 싶소."], ["The lawyers’ faces lengthened.", "그 돈은 내 것이었소.\" 두 법관의 얼굴이 길어졌다."], ["That was not at all what they expected.", "그것은 그들이 예상하던 바와는 전혀 달랐던 것이다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\""], ["faltered Nikolay Parfenovitch, “when at five o’clock on the same day, from your own confession—”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 머뭇거리며 물었다. \"당신 자신의 진술에 따르면 같은 날 다섯 시에—\""]], [["“Damn five o’clock on the same day and my own confession!", "\"같은 날 다섯 시고 내 진술이고 다 집어치우시오!"], ["That’s nothing to do with it now!", "그건 이제 아무 상관도 없소!"], ["That money was my own, my own, that is, stolen by me ...", "그 돈은 내 것이었소, 내 것, 다시 말해 내가 훔친 것 ……"], ["not mine, I mean, but stolen by me, and it was fifteen hundred roubles, and I had it on me all the time, all the time ...”", "내 것은 아니지만, 내가 훔친 것이고, 천오백 루블이었으며, 그걸 줄곧 내 몸에 지니고 있었소, 줄곧 ……\""]], [["“But where did you get it?”", "\"하지만 그 돈을 어디서 얻으셨습니까?\""]], [["“I took it off my neck, gentlemen, off this very neck ...", "\"내 목에서 끄집어냈소, 여러분, 바로 이 목에서 ……"], ["it was here, round my neck, sewn up in a rag, and I’d had it round my neck a long time, it’s a month since I put it round my neck ...", "여기 있었소, 내 목에 두르고, 헝겊에 꿰매 넣은 채로, 오랫동안 목에 걸고 다녔소, 목에 두른 지 한 달이 되었소 ……"], ["to my shame and disgrace!”", "내 수치이자 치욕으로!\""]], [["“And from whom did you ...", "\"그러면 그것을 누구에게서 ……"], ["appropriate it?”", "가져오신 것입니까?\""]], [["“You mean, ‘steal it’?", "\"'훔쳤느냐'는 말씀이오?"], ["Speak out plainly now.", "이제 솔직하게 말하시오."], ["Yes, I consider that I practically stole it, but, if you prefer, I ‘appropriated it.’ I consider I stole it.", "그렇소, 나는 그것을 사실상 훔친 것이나 다름없다고 생각하오만, 정 그러시다면 내가 그것을 '가져왔다'고 해 둡시다. 나는 그것을 훔쳤다고 생각하오."], ["And last night I stole it finally.”", "그리고 어젯밤에 마침내 완전히 훔치고 만 거요.\""]], [["“Last night?", "\"어젯밤이라고요? 하지만 그것을 ……"], ["But you said that it’s a month since you ... obtained it?...”", "손에 넣은 지 한 달이 되었다고 하시지 않았습니까?……\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["But not from my father.", "하지만 아버지에게서 훔친 건 아니오."], ["Not from my father, don’t be uneasy.", "아버지에게서가 아니니 마음 놓으시오."], ["I didn’t steal it from my father, but from her.", "나는 아버지에게서 훔친 게 아니라 그 여자에게서 훔쳤소."], ["Let me tell you without interrupting.", "끼어들지 말고 들어 주시오."], ["It’s hard to do, you know.", "이건 정말 말하기 어려운 거요."], ["You see, a month ago, I was sent for by Katerina Ivanovna, formerly my betrothed.", "보시오, 한 달 전에 나는 카테리나 이바노브나, 한때 내 약혼녀였던 그 여자에게 불려 갔소."], ["Do you know her?”", "그 여자를 아시오?\""]], [["“Yes, of course.”", "\"네, 물론입니다.\""]], [["“I know you know her.", "\"당신들이 그 여자를 안다는 건 나도 아오."], ["She’s a noble creature, noblest of the noble.", "그녀는 고귀한 여인이오, 고귀한 이들 가운데서도 가장 고귀하지."], ["But she has hated me ever so long, oh, ever so long ...", "하지만 그녀는 오래도록 나를 미워해 왔소, 오, 정말 오래도록 ……"], ["and hated me with good reason, good reason!”", "그것도 그럴 만한 이유가 있어 미워했지, 그럴 만한 이유가!\""]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna!”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나라고요!\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch exclaimed with wonder.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀라움을 감추지 못하고 외쳤다."], ["The prosecutor, too, stared.", "검사도 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Oh, don’t take her name in vain!", "\"오, 그녀의 이름을 함부로 입에 올리지 마시오!"], ["I’m a scoundrel to bring her into it.", "그녀를 이 일에 끌어들이다니 나는 비열한 놈이오."], ["Yes, I’ve seen that she hated me ... a long while....", "그렇소, 나는 그녀가 나를 미워한다는 걸 알고 있었소 ……"], ["From the very first, even that evening at my lodging ...", "오래전부터 …… 처음부터, 내 숙소에서의 그날 저녁부터 말이오 ……"], ["but enough, enough.", "하지만 됐소, 됐어."], ["You’re unworthy even to know of that.", "당신들은 그 일을 알 자격조차 없소."], ["No need of that at all....", "그런 건 전혀 필요 없는 일이오 ……"], ["I need only tell you that she sent for me a month ago, gave me three thousand roubles to send off to her sister and another relation in Moscow (as though she couldn’t have sent it off herself!) and I ...", "다만 이것만 말해 두겠소. 그녀가 한 달 전에 나를 불러, 모스크바에 있는 자기 언니와 또 다른 친척에게 부쳐 달라며 삼천 루블을 내게 주었던 것이오(마치 자기가 직접 부치지 못하기라도 하는 것처럼!)."], ["it was just at that fatal moment in my life when I ...", "그런데 나는 …… 그건 바로 내 인생의 그 운명적인 순간이었소, 내가 ……"], ["well, in fact, when I’d just come to love another, her, she’s sitting down below now, Grushenka.", "그러니까, 내가 막 다른 여자를, 그녀를, 지금 아래층에 앉아 있는 그루셴카를 사랑하게 된 바로 그때였소."], ["I carried her off here to Mokroe then, and wasted here in two days half that damned three thousand, but the other half I kept on me.", "나는 그때 그녀를 이곳 모크로예로 데려와 이틀 동안 그 빌어먹을 삼천 루블의 절반을 여기서 탕진했지만, 나머지 절반은 내 몸에 지니고 있었소."], ["Well, I’ve kept that other half, that fifteen hundred, like a locket round my neck, but yesterday I undid it, and spent it.", "그렇소, 나는 그 나머지 절반, 그 천오백 루블을 목에 건 작은 패물처럼 지니고 있다가 어제 그것을 풀어 써 버린 거요."], ["What’s left of it, eight hundred roubles, is in your hands now, Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "그러고 남은 것이, 팔백 루블이 지금 당신 손에 있소, 니콜라이 파르페노비치."], ["That’s the change out of the fifteen hundred I had yesterday.”", "그게 어제 내가 가지고 있던 천오백 루블에서 거슬러 남은 돈이오.\""]], [["“Excuse me. How’s that?", "\"실례합니다만."], ["Why, when you were here a month ago you spent three thousand, not fifteen hundred, everybody knows that.”", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있지요? 아니, 당신이 한 달 전에 여기 왔을 때 천오백이 아니라 삼천을 쓰셨다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실인데요.\""]], [["“Who knows it?", "\"누가 안다는 거요?"], ["Who counted the money?", "누가 그 돈을 세어 봤소?"], ["Did I let any one count it?”", "내가 누구에게 세어 보라고 했소?\""]], [["“Why, you told every one yourself that you’d spent exactly three thousand.”", "\"아니, 당신 스스로 모두에게 정확히 삼천을 썼다고 말씀하셨지요.\""]], [["“It’s true, I did.", "\"맞소, 그랬소."], ["I told the whole town so, and the whole town said so.", "나는 온 고을에 그렇게 말했고, 온 고을이 그렇게 떠들었소."], ["And here, at Mokroe, too, every one reckoned it was three thousand.", "그리고 여기 모크로예에서도 모두가 삼천이라고 계산했지."], ["Yet I didn’t spend three thousand, but fifteen hundred.", "하지만 나는 삼천을 쓴 게 아니라 천오백을 쓴 거요."], ["And the other fifteen hundred I sewed into a little bag.", "그리고 나머지 천오백은 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣었소."], ["That’s how it was, gentlemen.", "그렇게 된 거요, 여러분."], ["That’s where I got that money yesterday....”", "어제 내가 그 돈을 얻은 곳이 바로 거기요 ……\""]], [["“This is almost miraculous,”", "\"이건 거의 기적이나 다름없군요.\""], ["murmured Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Allow me to inquire,”", "\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\" 검사가 마침내 입을 열었다."], ["observed the prosecutor at last, “have you informed any one whatever of this circumstance before, I mean that you had fifteen hundred left about you a month ago?”", "\"당신은 이 정황을, 그러니까 한 달 전에 천오백을 몸에 지니고 있었다는 사실을, 이전에 누구에게든 알린 적이 있습니까?\" \"아무에게도 말하지 않았소.\""]], [["“I told no one.”", "\"그것 참 이상하군요."], ["“That’s strange. Do you mean absolutely no one?”", "정말 단 한 사람에게도 말하지 않았다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Absolutely no one.", "\"단 한 사람에게도."], ["No one and nobody.”", "누구에게도, 아무에게도.\""]], [["“What was your reason for this reticence?", "\"이렇게 입을 다물고 있은 까닭이 무엇입니까?"], ["What was your motive for making such a secret of it?", "무슨 동기로 그것을 그토록 비밀로 삼으셨습니까? 좀 더 정확히 말씀드리지요."], ["To be more precise: You have told us at last your secret, in your words, so ‘disgraceful,’ though in reality—that is, of course, comparatively speaking—this action, that is, the appropriation of three thousand roubles belonging to some one else, and, of course, only for a time is, in my view at least, only an act of the greatest recklessness and not so disgraceful, when one takes into consideration your character....", "당신은 마침내 그 비밀을, 당신 말로는 그토록 '치욕스러운' 비밀을 우리에게 털어놓으셨소만, 실은—물론 어디까지나 상대적으로 말해서—이 행위는, 그러니까 남의 것인 삼천 루블을, 그것도 물론 잠깐 동안 가로챈 행위는, 적어도 내가 보기에는 그저 더없이 무모한 짓일 뿐 그리 치욕스러운 일은 아니오."], ["Even admitting that it was an action in the highest degree discreditable, still, discreditable is not ‘disgraceful.’...", "당신의 성격을 고려해 본다면 말이오 …… 설령 그것이 더없이 떳떳지 못한 행위였음을 인정한다 하더라도, 떳떳지 못한 것이 곧 '치욕스러운'"], ["Many people have already guessed, during this last month, about the three thousand of Katerina Ivanovna’s, that you have spent, and I heard the legend myself, apart from your confession....", "것은 아니오 …… 이미 적잖은 사람들이 지난 한 달 동안 당신이 카테리나 이바노브나의 삼천을 써 버렸다고 짐작하고 있었고, 나 역시 당신의 고백과는 별개로 그 이야기를 들었소 ……"], ["Mihail Makarovitch, for instance, had heard it, too, so that indeed, it was scarcely a legend, but the gossip of the whole town.", "가령 미하일 마카로비치도 그것을 들었으니, 사실 그건 이야깃거리라고 할 것도 없이 온 고을의 입소문이었던 거요."], ["There are indications, too, if I am not mistaken, that you confessed this yourself to some one, I mean that the money was Katerina Ivanovna’s, and so, it’s extremely surprising to me that hitherto, that is, up to the present moment, you have made such an extraordinary secret of the fifteen hundred you say you put by, apparently connecting a feeling of positive horror with that secret....", "게다가 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 당신이 누군가에게 이것을 직접 고백했다는, 즉 그 돈이 카테리나 이바노브나의 것이라고 털어놓았다는 정황도 있소. 그러니 지금까지, 다시 말해 이 순간까지 당신이 따로 떼어 두었다는 그 천오백을 그토록 유별나게 비밀로 삼아 왔다는 것이, 게다가 그 비밀에 거의 공포에 가까운 감정까지 결부시키고 있다는 것이 나로서는 몹시 놀라울 따름이오 ……"], ["It’s not easy to believe that it could cost you such distress to confess such a secret....", "그런 비밀을 고백하는 일이 당신에게 그토록 큰 괴로움이 된다는 것은 도무지 믿기 어려운 일이오 ……"], ["You cried out, just now, that Siberia would be better than confessing it ...”", "당신은 방금 전 그것을 고백하느니 차라리 시베리아가 낫겠다고 외치지 않았소 ……\""]], [["The prosecutor ceased speaking.", "검사는 말을 멈췄다."], ["He was provoked.", "그는 화가 나 있었다."], ["He did not conceal his vexation, which was almost anger, and gave vent to all his accumulated spleen, disconnectedly and incoherently, without choosing words.", "거의 분노에 가까운 짜증을 굳이 감추지 않고, 쌓이고 쌓인 울화를 두서없고 갈피 잡히지 않게, 말을 가려 쓰지도 않은 채 죄다 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“It’s not the fifteen hundred that’s the disgrace, but that I put it apart from the rest of the three thousand,”", "\"치욕스러운 것은 그 천오백이 아니라, 그것을 나머지 삼천에서 따로 떼어 둔 그 행위요.\""], ["said Mitya firmly.", "미탸가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜 그렇지요?\""], ["smiled the prosecutor irritably.", "검사가 신경질적으로 웃었다."], ["“What is there disgraceful, to your thinking, in your having set aside half of the three thousand you had discreditably, if you prefer, ‘disgracefully,’ appropriated?", "\"당신이 떳떳지 못하게, 정 그러시다면 '치욕스럽게' 가로챈 삼천 가운데 절반을 따로 떼어 두었다는 것이, 당신 생각에 대체 무엇이 그리 치욕스럽다는 거요?"], ["Your taking the three thousand is more important than what you did with it.", "당신이 그 삼천을 가져간 것이 그 돈으로 무엇을 했느냐보다 더 중요한 일이오."], ["And by the way, why did you do that—why did you set apart that half, for what purpose, for what object did you do it?", "그건 그렇고, 어째서 당신은 그렇게 했소—어째서 그 절반을 따로 떼어 두었으며, 무슨 까닭으로, 무슨 목적으로 그렇게 했소?"], ["Can you explain that to us?”", "우리에게 설명해 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, gentlemen, the purpose is the whole point!”", "\"오, 여러분, 바로 그 목적이 핵심이오!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“I put it aside because I was vile, that is, because I was calculating, and to be calculating in such a case is vile ...", "\"내가 그것을 따로 떼어 둔 것은 내가 비열했기 때문이오, 다시 말해 내가 잇속을 따졌기 때문이며, 그런 경우에 잇속을 따진다는 것 자체가 비열한 일이오 ……"], ["and that vileness has been going on a whole month.”", "그리고 그 비열함이 꼬박 한 달 동안이나 계속되었던 거요.\""]], [["“It’s incomprehensible.”", "\"도무지 이해할 수가 없군요.\""]], [["“I wonder at you.", "\"당신들이 이상하구려."], ["But I’ll make it clearer.", "하지만 좀 더 분명하게 설명해 드리겠소."], ["Perhaps it really is incomprehensible.", "어쩌면 정말로 이해하기 어려운 일일지도 모르지. 자, 내 말을 잘 들어 보시오."], ["You see, attend to what I say.", "내가 명예를 걸고 맡은 삼천을 가로채어 흥청망청 써 버렸다 칩시다, 죄다 써 버렸다 칩시다."], ["I appropriate three thousand entrusted to my honor, I spend it on a spree, say I spend it all, and next morning I go to her and say, ‘Katya, I’ve done wrong, I’ve squandered your three thousand,’ well, is that right?", "그러고 이튿날 아침 그녀에게 가서 이렇게 말하는 거요. '카탸, 내가 잘못했소, 당신의 삼천을 탕진해 버렸소.'"], ["No, it’s not right—it’s dishonest and cowardly, I’m a beast, with no more self-control than a beast, that’s so, isn’t it?", "자, 이게 옳은 일이오? 아니, 옳지 않소—이건 부정직하고 비겁한 짓이오, 나는 짐승이오, 짐승 정도밖에 자제력이 없는 놈이지, 그렇지 않소?"], ["But still I’m not a thief?", "하지만 그래도 나는 도둑은 아니지 않소?"], ["Not a downright thief, you’ll admit!", "적어도 완전한 도둑은 아니라는 걸 당신들도 인정할 거요!"], ["I squandered it, but I didn’t steal it.", "나는 탕진했지만 훔치지는 않았소. 자, 이번엔 좀 더 나은 두 번째 경우요."], ["Now a second, rather more favorable alternative: follow me carefully, or I may get confused again—my head’s going round—and so, for the second alternative: I spend here only fifteen hundred out of the three thousand, that is, only half.", "잘 따라오시오, 안 그러면 또 헷갈릴지도 모르니—머리가 빙빙 도는구려—그래서, 두 번째 경우는 이렇소. 나는 삼천 가운데 천오백만, 그러니까 절반만 여기서 쓰는 거요."], ["Next day I go and take that half to her: ‘Katya, take this fifteen hundred from me, I’m a low beast, and an untrustworthy scoundrel, for I’ve wasted half the money, and I shall waste this, too, so keep me from temptation!’ Well, what of that alternative?", "이튿날 나는 그 절반을 그녀에게 가져가 이렇게 말하지. '카탸, 내게서 이 천오백을 받아 두시오, 나는 비열한 짐승이고 믿을 수 없는 악당이오, 절반을 탕진해 버렸고 이것마저 탕진할 테니, 부디 나를 유혹에서 지켜 주시오!'"], ["I should be a beast and a scoundrel, and whatever you like; but not a thief, not altogether a thief, or I should not have brought back what was left, but have kept that, too.", "자, 이 경우는 어떻소? 나는 짐승이요 악당이요, 당신들 좋을 대로 무엇이든 되겠지만, 도둑은 아니오, 완전한 도둑은 아니지, 안 그랬다면 나는 남은 것을 돌려주지 않고 그것마저 가지고 있었을 테니 말이오."], ["She would see at once that since I brought back half, I should pay back what I’d spent, that I should never give up trying to, that I should work to get it and pay it back.", "그녀는 곧바로 알아챘을 거요, 내가 절반을 돌려준 이상 쓴 것도 갚을 것이며, 그 노력을 결코 포기하지 않을 것이고, 일을 해서라도 돈을 마련해 갚으리라는 것을."], ["So in that case I should be a scoundrel, but not a thief, you may say what you like, not a thief!”", "그러니 그 경우에 나는 악당일지언정 도둑은 아닌 거요, 당신들이 뭐라고 하든, 도둑은 아니란 말이오!\""]], [["“I admit that there is a certain distinction,”", "\"어느 정도 구분이 있다는 건 인정하겠소.\""], ["said the prosecutor, with a cold smile.", "검사가 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“But it’s strange that you see such a vital difference.”", "\"하지만 당신이 거기서 그토록 본질적인 차이를 본다는 게 이상하군요.\""]], [["“Yes, I see a vital difference!", "\"그렇소, 나는 본질적인 차이를 보오!"], ["Every man may be a scoundrel, and perhaps every man is a scoundrel, but not every one can be a thief, it takes an arch-scoundrel to be that.", "누구든 악당이 될 수 있고, 어쩌면 모든 사람이 악당일지도 모르지만, 누구나 다 도둑이 될 수 있는 건 아니오, 그건 더없이 악랄한 자라야 될 수 있는 거요."], ["Oh, of course, I don’t know how to make these fine distinctions ...", "오, 물론 나는 이런 미묘한 구분을 제대로 할 줄은 모르오 ……"], ["but a thief is lower than a scoundrel, that’s my conviction.", "하지만 도둑은 악당보다 더 비천하다는 것, 그게 내 확신이오."], ["Listen, I carry the money about me a whole month, I may make up my mind to give it back to-morrow, and I’m a scoundrel no longer, but I cannot make up my mind, you see, though I’m making up my mind every day, and every day spurring myself on to do it, and yet for a whole month I can’t bring myself to it, you see.", "들어 보시오, 나는 그 돈을 꼬박 한 달이나 몸에 지니고 다녔소, 당장 내일이라도 돌려주기로 마음을 정할 수도 있었고, 그러면 나는 더 이상 악당이 아니게 되는 거요, 하지만 보다시피 나는 좀처럼 결심을 못 했소, 날마다 결심을 하고, 날마다 스스로를 다그쳐 그렇게 하려 했건만, 그래도 꼬박 한 달 동안 차마 그것을 실행에 옮기지 못했단 말이오."], ["Is that right to your thinking, is that right?”", "당신들 생각에 이게 옳은 일이오, 이게 옳소?\""]], [["“Certainly, that’s not right, that I can quite understand, and that I don’t dispute,”", "\"분명 옳지 않소, 그건 나도 충분히 이해할 수 있고, 그 점은 따지지 않겠소.\""], ["answered the prosecutor with reserve.", "검사가 조심스럽게 대답했다."], ["“And let us give up all discussion of these subtleties and distinctions, and, if you will be so kind, get back to the point.", "\"그러니 이런 미묘한 구분이나 차이에 관한 이야기는 모두 그만두기로 하고, 부디 본론으로 돌아갑시다."], ["And the point is, that you have still not told us, altogether we’ve asked you, why, in the first place, you halved the money, squandering one half and hiding the other?", "그런데 그 본론이란, 당신이 아직도 우리에게 말하지 않은 것이오, 그러니까 우리가 거듭 물었는데도, 첫째로 어째서 그 돈을 절반으로 나누어 한쪽은 탕진하고 다른 한쪽은 감추었는가 하는 것이오."], ["For what purpose exactly did you hide it, what did you mean to do with that fifteen hundred?", "정확히 무슨 목적으로 그것을 감추었으며, 그 천오백으로 무엇을 하려 했소?"], ["I insist upon that question, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "나는 이 질문을 고집하겠소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""]], [["“Yes, of course!”", "\"그렇지, 물론이오!\""], ["cried Mitya, striking himself on the forehead; “forgive me, I’m worrying you, and am not explaining the chief point, or you’d understand in a minute, for it’s just the motive of it that’s the disgrace!", "미탸가 이마를 탁 치며 외쳤다. \"용서하시오, 내가 당신들을 괴롭히면서 정작 핵심은 설명하지 않고 있구려, 그것만 알면 당신들도 당장 이해할 텐데, 바로 그 동기야말로 치욕스러운 거니까!"], ["You see, it was all to do with the old man, my dead father.", "보시오, 이건 다 그 늙은이, 죽은 내 아버지와 관련된 일이오."], ["He was always pestering Agrafena Alexandrovna, and I was jealous; I thought then that she was hesitating between me and him.", "그자는 늘 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 치근덕거렸고, 나는 질투에 사로잡혔소. 그때 나는 그녀가 나와 그자 사이에서 망설이고 있다고 생각했지."], ["So I kept thinking every day, suppose she were to make up her mind all of a sudden, suppose she were to leave off tormenting me, and were suddenly to say to me, ‘I love you, not him; take me to the other end of the world.’ And I’d only forty copecks; how could I take her away, what could I do?", "그래서 날마다 이런 생각이 들었소, 만일 그녀가 별안간 마음을 정한다면, 만일 그녀가 나를 괴롭히는 일을 그만두고 갑자기 내게 이렇게 말한다면, '내가 사랑하는 건 그가 아니라 당신이에요, 나를 세상 저 끝으로 데려가 줘요'라고."], ["Why, I’d be lost.", "그런데 내겐 사십 코페이카밖에 없었소."], ["You see, I didn’t know her then, I didn’t understand her, I thought she wanted money, and that she wouldn’t forgive my poverty.", "그러니 어떻게 그녀를 데려갈 수 있단 말이오, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠소? 그야말로 나는 끝장날 판이었지."], ["And so I fiendishly counted out the half of that three thousand, sewed it up, calculating on it, sewed it up before I was drunk, and after I had sewn it up, I went off to get drunk on the rest.", "보시오, 그때 나는 그녀를 알지 못했고, 그녀를 이해하지 못했소, 나는 그녀가 돈을 바란다고, 내 가난을 용서하지 않으리라고 생각했던 거요. 그래서 나는 악마처럼 그 삼천의 절반을 세어 내어 꿰매 넣었소, 그걸 노리고 계산해서, 술에 취하기 전에 미리 꿰매 두고, 다 꿰매 놓은 다음에야 나머지를 가지고 취하러 갔던 거요."], ["Yes, that was base.", "그렇소, 그건 비열한 짓이었소."], ["Do you understand now?”", "이제 이해가 가시오?\""]], [["Both the lawyers laughed aloud.", "두 법관이 소리 내어 웃었다."]], [["“I should have called it sensible and moral on your part not to have squandered it all,”", "\"그걸 죄다 탕진하지 않았다니, 나라면 오히려 분별 있고 도덕적인 처사라고 했을 거요.\""], ["chuckled Nikolay Parfenovitch, “for after all what does it amount to?”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 빙긋이 웃었다. \"어쨌든 그게 무슨 대단한 일이라고요?\""]], [["“Why, that I stole it, that’s what it amounts to!", "\"아니, 그게 내가 그걸 훔쳤다는 거요, 그게 바로 그거란 말이오!"], ["Oh, God, you horrify me by not understanding!", "오, 맙소사, 당신들이 이해를 못 하니 나는 소름이 끼치는구려!"], ["Every day that I had that fifteen hundred sewn up round my neck, every day and every hour I said to myself, ‘You’re a thief!", "그 천오백을 목에 꿰매어 두르고 있던 날마다, 매일 매시간 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 되뇌었소. '너는 도둑이다!"], ["you’re a thief!’ Yes, that’s why I’ve been so savage all this month, that’s why I fought in the tavern, that’s why I attacked my father, it was because I felt I was a thief.", "너는 도둑이다!' 그렇소, 그래서 이 한 달 내내 내가 그토록 사나웠던 거요, 그래서 술집에서 싸움을 벌였고, 그래서 아버지에게 덤벼들었던 거요, 그건 다 내가 도둑이라고 느꼈기 때문이오."], ["I couldn’t make up my mind, I didn’t dare even to tell Alyosha, my brother, about that fifteen hundred: I felt I was such a scoundrel and such a pickpocket.", "나는 차마 결심을 못 했고, 내 동생 알료샤에게조차 그 천오백 이야기를 감히 꺼내지 못했소. 내가 그토록 악당 같고 소매치기 같은 놈이라고 느꼈던 거요."], ["But, do you know, while I carried it I said to myself at the same time every hour: ‘No, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, you may yet not be a thief.’ Why?", "하지만 들어 보시오, 그것을 지니고 다니는 동안 나는 매시간 동시에 이렇게도 되뇌었소. '아니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 너는 아직 도둑이 아닐 수도 있다'라고."], ["Because I might go next day and pay back that fifteen hundred to Katya.", "왜냐고? 이튿날 당장 가서 그 천오백을 카탸에게 돌려줄 수도 있었으니까."], ["And only yesterday I made up my mind to tear my amulet off my neck, on my way from Fenya’s to Perhotin.", "그런데 바로 어제, 페냐의 집에서 페르호틴에게 가는 길에 나는 그 부적을 목에서 떼어 내기로 마음을 정한 거요."], ["I hadn’t been able till that moment to bring myself to it.", "그 순간까지도 나는 차마 그렇게 하지 못하고 있었던 거지."], ["And it was only when I tore it off that I became a downright thief, a thief and a dishonest man for the rest of my life.", "그리고 그것을 떼어 낸 바로 그 순간에야 나는 완전한 도둑이, 남은 평생토록 도둑이요 부정직한 인간이 되고 만 거요."], ["Why?", "왜냐고?"], ["Because, with that I destroyed, too, my dream of going to Katya and saying, ‘I’m a scoundrel, but not a thief!’ Do you understand now?", "그것으로 나는 카탸에게 찾아가 '나는 악당이지만 도둑은 아니오!'라고 말할 꿈마저 무너뜨려 버렸으니까. 이제 이해가 가시오?"], ["Do you understand?”", "이해가 가시오?\""]], [["“What was it made you decide to do it yesterday?”", "\"무엇 때문에 어제 그렇게 하기로 결심하셨습니까?\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch interrupted.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Why?", "\"왜냐고?"], ["It’s absurd to ask.", "그건 물어볼 것도 없는 일이오."], ["Because I had condemned myself to die at five o’clock this morning, here, at dawn.", "오늘 새벽 다섯 시에, 여기서, 동틀 무렵에 나는 스스로 죽기로 작정했기 때문이오."], ["I thought it made no difference whether I died a thief or a man of honor.", "도둑으로 죽건 명예로운 사람으로 죽건 아무 차이 없다고 생각했소."], ["But I see it’s not so, it turns out that it does make a difference.", "하지만 그게 그렇지가 않더군, 차이가 있다는 게 드러났소."], ["Believe me, gentlemen, what has tortured me most during this night has not been the thought that I’d killed the old servant, and that I was in danger of Siberia just when my love was being rewarded, and Heaven was open to me again.", "믿어 주시오, 여러분, 이 밤 내내 나를 가장 괴롭힌 것은 내가 그 늙은 하인을 죽였다는 생각도, 또 내 사랑이 막 보답을 받고 천국이 다시 내게 열리려는 바로 그때 시베리아로 끌려갈 위기에 처했다는 생각도 아니었소."], ["Oh, that did torture me, but not in the same way: not so much as the damned consciousness that I had torn that damned money off my breast at last and spent it, and had become a downright thief!", "오, 그것도 나를 괴롭히긴 했지만 같은 식은 아니었소. 마침내 그 빌어먹을 돈을 내 가슴에서 떼어 내어 써 버리고 완전한 도둑이 되고 말았다는 그 저주받을 자각만큼은 아니었던 거요!"], ["Oh, gentlemen, I tell you again, with a bleeding heart, I have learnt a great deal this night.", "오, 여러분, 다시 한번 피눈물 흘리는 심정으로 말하겠소, 나는 이 밤에 많은 것을 깨달았소."], ["I have learnt that it’s not only impossible to live a scoundrel, but impossible to die a scoundrel....", "악당으로 살아가는 것이 불가능할 뿐 아니라 악당으로 죽는 것마저 불가능하다는 것을 깨달았소 ……"], ["No, gentlemen, one must die honest....”", "아니, 여러분, 사람은 명예롭게 죽어야 하는 거요 ……\""]], [["Mitya was pale.", "미탸는 창백했다."], ["His face had a haggard and exhausted look, in spite of his being intensely excited.", "그는 극도로 흥분해 있었음에도 그 얼굴에는 초췌하고 기진맥진한 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I am beginning to understand you, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"이제 당신을 이해하기 시작했소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["the prosecutor said slowly, in a soft and almost compassionate tone.", "검사가 부드럽고 거의 연민에 가까운 어조로 천천히 말했다."], ["“But all this, if you’ll excuse my saying so, is a matter of nerves, in my opinion ...", "\"하지만 이 모든 것이, 이런 말을 해서 미안하오만, 내 생각에는 다 신경 탓이오 ……"], ["your overwrought nerves, that’s what it is.", "당신의 과민해진 신경 말이오, 바로 그게 문제요."], ["And why, for instance, should you not have saved yourself such misery for almost a month, by going and returning that fifteen hundred to the lady who had entrusted it to you?", "그건 그렇고, 가령 어째서 당신은 그 천오백을 가지고 가서 그것을 당신에게 맡긴 부인에게 돌려줌으로써, 거의 한 달 동안이나 자신을 그런 고통에서 구해 내지 않았던 거요?"], ["And why could you not have explained things to her, and in view of your position, which you describe as being so awful, why could you not have had recourse to the plan which would so naturally have occurred to one’s mind, that is, after honorably confessing your errors to her, why could you not have asked her to lend you the sum needed for your expenses, which, with her generous heart, she would certainly not have refused you in your distress, especially if it had been with some guarantee, or even on the security you offered to the merchant Samsonov, and to Madame Hohlakov?", "어째서 당신은 그녀에게 사정을 설명할 수 없었던 거요, 그리고 당신이 그토록 끔찍하다고 묘사하는 당신의 처지를 생각하면, 누구라도 자연스레 떠올렸을 그 방법에 의지할 수 있지 않았소, 즉 그녀에게 자신의 과오를 정직하게 고백한 다음, 어째서 당신은 자신의 비용에 필요한 그 액수를 빌려 달라고 그녀에게 부탁할 수 없었던 거요, 그토록 너그러운 마음을 지닌 그녀라면 당신의 곤경을 두고 분명 거절하지 않았을 텐데 말이오, 더구나 어떤 담보라도 있었다면, 아니면 당신이 상인 삼소노프와 호흘라코바 부인에게 제안했던 그 담보라도 있었다면 더욱 그러했을 거요."], ["I suppose you still regard that security as of value?”", "당신은 아직도 그 담보를 값어치 있는 것으로 여기고 있겠지요?\""]], [["Mitya suddenly crimsoned.", "미탸의 얼굴이 별안간 새빨개졌다."]], [["“Surely you don’t think me such an out and out scoundrel as that?", "\"설마 나를 그토록 철저한 악당으로 여기는 건 아니겠지요?"], ["You can’t be speaking in earnest?”", "진심으로 하는 말은 아니겠지요?\""], ["he said, with indignation, looking the prosecutor straight in the face, and seeming unable to believe his ears.", "그는 분개하여, 검사의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며, 제 귀를 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“I assure you I’m in earnest....", "\"분명히 말하지만 나는 진심이오 ……"], ["Why do you imagine I’m not serious?”", "어째서 내가 진심이 아니라고 생각하는 거요?\""], ["It was the prosecutor’s turn to be surprised.", "이번엔 검사가 놀랄 차례였다."]], [["“Oh, how base that would have been!", "\"오, 그게 얼마나 비열한 짓이 되었겠소!"], ["Gentlemen, do you know, you are torturing me!", "여러분, 알겠소, 당신들은 나를 괴롭히고 있소!"], ["Let me tell you everything, so be it.", "다 털어놓겠소, 좋소."], ["I’ll confess all my infernal wickedness, but to put you to shame, and you’ll be surprised yourselves at the depth of ignominy to which a medley of human passions can sink.", "내 지옥 같은 사악함을 죄다 고백하겠소, 다만 당신들을 부끄럽게 하기 위해서요, 그러면 당신들도 인간의 온갖 정념이 뒤엉켰을 때 얼마나 깊은 치욕의 나락으로 가라앉을 수 있는지를 보고 스스로 놀라게 될 거요."], ["You must know that I already had that plan myself, that plan you spoke of, just now, prosecutor!", "알아 두시오, 검사 양반, 방금 당신이 말한 그 방법, 그건 이미 나 자신도 품고 있던 것이오!"], ["Yes, gentlemen, I, too, have had that thought in my mind all this current month, so that I was on the point of deciding to go to Katya—I was mean enough for that.", "그렇소, 여러분, 나 역시 이번 한 달 내내 그 생각을 마음에 품고 있었고, 그래서 카탸에게 찾아가기로 거의 마음을 정할 뻔했소—나는 그만큼 비열한 놈이었던 거요."], ["But to go to her, to tell her of my treachery, and for that very treachery, to carry it out, for the expenses of that treachery, to beg for money from her, Katya (to beg, do you hear, to beg), and go straight from her to run away with the other, the rival, who hated and insulted her—to think of it!", "하지만 그녀에게 찾아가, 내 배신을 그녀에게 털어놓고, 바로 그 배신을 위해, 그 배신을 실행에 옮기기 위해, 그 배신에 드는 비용을 위해, 그녀에게, 카탸에게 돈을 구걸하고(구걸이오, 듣고 있소, 구걸 말이오), 그러고는 그녀에게서 곧장 나와 그 여자를, 그녀를 미워하고 모욕한 연적과 함께 달아난다—그걸 한번 생각해 보시오!"], ["You must be mad, prosecutor!”", "당신 제정신이 아니구려, 검사 양반!\""]], [["“Mad I am not, but I did speak in haste, without thinking ...", "\"제정신이 아닌 건 아니오만, 내가 깊이 생각하지 않고 성급하게 말한 건 사실이오 ……"], ["of that feminine jealousy ...", "그 여자의 질투에 관해서 말이오 ……"], ["if there could be jealousy in this case, as you assert ...", "만일 당신 말대로 이 경우에 질투라는 게 있을 수 있다면 ……"], ["yes, perhaps there is something of the kind,”", "그렇소, 어쩌면 그런 것이 좀 있을지도 모르겠군요.\""], ["said the prosecutor, smiling.", "검사가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“But that would have been so infamous!”", "\"하지만 그건 너무도 파렴치한 짓이 되었을 거요!\""], ["Mitya brought his fist down on the table fiercely.", "미탸가 거칠게 주먹으로 탁자를 내리쳤다."], ["“That would have been filthy beyond everything!", "\"그건 무엇보다도 더없이 추악한 짓이 되었을 거란 말이오!"], ["Yes, do you know that she might have given me that money, yes, and she would have given it, too; she’d have been certain to give it, to be revenged on me, she’d have given it to satisfy her vengeance, to show her contempt for me, for hers is an infernal nature, too, and she’s a woman of great wrath.", "그렇소, 알겠소, 그녀라면 내게 그 돈을 줬을지도 모르오, 그렇소, 정말 주었을 거요, 틀림없이 줬을 거요, 내게 복수하기 위해서, 그녀는 자신의 복수심을 채우려고, 나를 향한 경멸을 드러내려고 그 돈을 주었을 거요, 그녀 역시 지옥 같은 천성을 지녔고 분노가 대단한 여자니까."], ["I’d have taken the money, too, oh, I should have taken it; I should have taken it, and then, for the rest of my life ...", "나도 그 돈을 받았을 거요, 오, 틀림없이 받았겠지, 받고서, 그러고 나면 남은 평생토록 …… 오, 맙소사!"], ["oh, God! Forgive me, gentlemen, I’m making such an outcry because I’ve had that thought in my mind so lately, only the day before yesterday, that night when I was having all that bother with Lyagavy, and afterwards yesterday, all day yesterday, I remember, till that happened ...”", "용서하시오, 여러분, 내가 이렇게 소리를 질러 대는 건 바로 얼마 전에, 겨우 그저께, 랴가비와 그 온갖 실랑이를 벌이던 그날 밤에, 그리고 그다음 어제, 어제 하루 종일, 그 일이 일어나기 전까지 그 생각을 마음에 품고 있었기 때문이오 ……\""]], [["“Till what happened?”", "\"그 일이라니, 무슨 일이 일어나기 전 말입니까?\""], ["put in Nikolay Parfenovitch inquisitively, but Mitya did not hear it.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 궁금한 듯 끼어들었으나, 미탸는 그것을 듣지 못했다."]], [["“I have made you an awful confession,”", "\"나는 당신들에게 끔찍한 고백을 했소.\""], ["Mitya said gloomily in conclusion.", "미탸가 침울하게 끝맺었다."], ["“You must appreciate it, and what’s more, you must respect it, for if not, if that leaves your souls untouched, then you’ve simply no respect for me, gentlemen, I tell you that, and I shall die of shame at having confessed it to men like you!", "\"당신들은 그것을 마땅히 알아주어야 하고, 더 나아가 그것을 존중해 주어야 하오, 만일 그러지 않는다면, 만일 그것이 당신들의 영혼을 조금도 건드리지 못한다면, 그건 당신들이 나를 도무지 존중하지 않는다는 뜻이오, 분명히 말해 두겠소, 그렇다면 나는 당신들 같은 사람들에게 그것을 고백했다는 부끄러움으로 죽고 말 거요!"], ["Oh, I shall shoot myself!", "오, 나는 권총으로 내 머리를 쏘아 버릴 거요!"], ["Yes, I see, I see already that you don’t believe me.", "그렇소, 알겠소, 당신들이 나를 믿지 않는다는 걸 이미 다 알겠소."], ["What, you want to write that down, too?”", "뭐요, 당신들 그것마저 받아 적으려는 거요?\""], ["he cried in dismay.", "그가 경악하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, what you said just now,”", "\"그렇습니다, 방금 하신 말씀 말입니다.\""], ["said Nikolay Parfenovitch, looking at him in surprise, “that is, that up to the last hour you were still contemplating going to Katerina Ivanovna to beg that sum from her....", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 놀란 듯 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"그러니까 당신이 마지막 순간까지도 카테리나 이바노브나에게 찾아가 그 액수를 구걸할 생각을 하고 있었다는 말씀 말입니다 ……"], ["I assure you, that’s a very important piece of evidence for us, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I mean for the whole case ...", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 그건 우리에게 매우 중요한 증거입니다, 드미트리 표도로비치, 그러니까 이 사건 전체에 ……"], ["and particularly for you, particularly important for you.”", "그리고 특히 당신에게, 당신에게 특히 중요한 것입니다.\""]], [["“Have mercy, gentlemen!”", "\"제발 자비를 베푸시오, 여러분!\""], ["Mitya flung up his hands.", "미탸가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들었다."], ["“Don’t write that, anyway; have some shame.", "\"어쨌거나 그것만은 받아 적지 마시오, 부끄러운 줄 좀 아시오."], ["Here I’ve torn my heart asunder before you, and you seize the opportunity and are fingering the wounds in both halves....", "여기 내가 당신들 앞에서 내 심장을 두 쪽으로 찢어 보였더니, 당신들은 그 틈을 타 두 쪽 다 그 상처를 후벼 파고 있구려 ……"], ["Oh, my God!”", "오, 맙소사!\""]], [["In despair he hid his face in his hands.", "절망에 빠져 그는 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["“Don’t worry yourself so, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"그리 괴로워하지 마시오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["observed the prosecutor, “everything that is written down will be read over to you afterwards, and what you don’t agree to we’ll alter as you like.", "검사가 말했다. \"받아 적은 것은 나중에 모두 당신에게 읽어 드릴 거요, 그리고 당신이 동의하지 않는 부분은 당신 뜻대로 고쳐 드리겠소."], ["But now I’ll ask you one little question for the second time.", "다만 지금 한 가지 사소한 질문을 두 번째로 드리겠소."], ["Has no one, absolutely no one, heard from you of that money you sewed up?", "당신이 꿰매 넣었다는 그 돈에 관해, 정말 아무도, 단 한 사람도 당신에게서 들은 적이 없다는 거요?"], ["That, I must tell you, is almost impossible to believe.”", "그건, 분명히 말씀드리지만, 도무지 믿기 어려운 일이라서 말이오.\""]], [["“No one, no one, I told you so before, or you’ve not understood anything!", "\"아무도, 아무도 없소, 아까도 그렇게 말하지 않았소, 아니면 당신들이 아무것도 못 알아들은 거요!"], ["Let me alone!”", "나를 좀 내버려 두시오!\""]], [["“Very well, this matter is bound to be explained, and there’s plenty of time for it, but meantime, consider; we have perhaps a dozen witnesses that you yourself spread it abroad, and even shouted almost everywhere about the three thousand you’d spent here; three thousand, not fifteen hundred.", "\"좋소, 이 문제는 언젠가 밝혀질 게 분명하고 그럴 시간도 충분하오, 하지만 그동안 한번 생각해 보시오. 당신 자신이 여기서 쓴 삼천에 관해 떠들고 다녔으며, 거의 어디서나 그것을 소리쳐 외치다시피 했다는 증인이 우리에게 어쩌면 열두어 명은 될 거요."], ["And now, too, when you got hold of the money you had yesterday, you gave many people to understand that you had brought three thousand with you.”", "천오백이 아니라 삼천 말이오. 그리고 지금도, 어제 그 돈을 손에 넣었을 때 당신은 많은 사람들에게 삼천을 가지고 왔다는 인상을 주었소.\""]], [["“You’ve got not dozens, but hundreds of witnesses, two hundred witnesses, two hundred have heard it, thousands have heard it!”", "\"열두어 명이 아니라 수백 명, 이백 명의 증인이 있을 거요, 이백 명이 들었고, 수천 명이 들었소!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, you see, all bear witness to it.", "\"자, 보시오, 모두가 그것을 증언하는 거요."], ["And the word all means something.”", "그리고 모두라는 말에는 무언가 의미가 있는 법이오.\""]], [["“It means nothing.", "\"아무 의미도 없소."], ["I talked rot, and every one began repeating it.”", "내가 헛소리를 지껄였더니 모두가 그걸 따라 떠들기 시작한 거요.\""]], [["“But what need had you to ‘talk rot,’ as you call it?”", "\"하지만 당신 말마따나 '헛소리를 지껄일' 무슨 필요가 있었소?\""]], [["“The devil knows.", "\"악마나 알겠지."], ["From bravado perhaps ...", "아마 허세였을 거요 ……"], ["at having wasted so much money....", "그렇게 많은 돈을 탕진한 데 대한 허세 ……"], ["To try and forget that money I had sewn up, perhaps ...", "어쩌면 내가 꿰매 넣어 둔 그 돈을 잊어 보려고 그랬는지도 모르오 ……"], ["yes, that was why ...", "그렇소, 바로 그래서였소 ……"], ["damn it ...", "빌어먹을 ……"], ["how often will you ask me that question?", "도대체 그 질문을 몇 번이나 더 할 셈이오?"], ["Well, I told a fib, and that was the end of it, once I’d said it, I didn’t care to correct it.", "자, 나는 거짓말을 했고, 그걸로 끝난 거요, 일단 입 밖에 내고 나니 굳이 바로잡고 싶지 않았던 거요."], ["What does a man tell lies for sometimes?”", "사람이 때때로 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하겠소?\""]], [["“That’s very difficult to decide, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, what makes a man tell lies,”", "\"사람이 무엇 때문에 거짓말을 하는가, 그건 참 판단하기 어려운 일이오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["observed the prosecutor impressively.", "검사가 의미심장하게 말했다."], ["“Tell me, though, was that ‘amulet,’ as you call it, on your neck, a big thing?”", "\"그건 그렇고, 한 가지 묻겠소, 당신 목에 두르고 있었다는 그 '부적'이라는 것은 큰 물건이었소?\""]], [["“No, not big.”", "\"아니, 크지 않았소.\""]], [["“How big, for instance?”", "\"예를 들어 어느 정도였소?\""]], [["“If you fold a hundred-rouble note in half, that would be the size.”", "\"백 루블짜리 지폐를 반으로 접으면 딱 그만한 크기일 거요.\""]], [["“You’d better show us the remains of it.", "\"그 남은 것을 우리에게 보여 주는 게 좋겠소."], ["You must have them somewhere.”", "어딘가에 분명 가지고 있을 테니.\""]], [["“Damnation, what nonsense!", "\"빌어먹을, 무슨 헛소리요!"], ["I don’t know where they are.”", "그게 어디 있는지 나도 모르오.\""]], [["“But excuse me: where and when did you take it off your neck?", "\"하지만 실례지만, 그것을 언제 어디서 목에서 떼어 냈소?"], ["According to your own evidence you didn’t go home.”", "당신 자신의 진술에 따르면 집에는 가지 않았는데 말이오.\""]], [["“When I was going from Fenya’s to Perhotin’s, on the way I tore it off my neck and took out the money.”", "\"페냐의 집에서 페르호틴에게 가던 길에, 도중에 그것을 목에서 떼어 내어 돈을 꺼냈소.\""]], [["“In the dark?”", "\"어둠 속에서 말이오?\""]], [["“What should I want a light for?", "\"불빛이 무슨 필요가 있겠소?"], ["I did it with my fingers in one minute.”", "손가락으로 일 분 만에 해치웠소.\""]], [["“Without scissors, in the street?”", "\"가위도 없이, 길거리에서 말이오?\""]], [["“In the market-place I think it was.", "\"아마 장터였던 것 같소."], ["Why scissors?", "가위는 무슨."], ["It was an old rag.", "낡은 헝겊이었소."], ["It was torn in a minute.”", "순식간에 찢어졌지.\""]], [["“Where did you put it afterwards?”", "\"그걸 그 뒤에 어디다 두었소?\""]], [["“I dropped it there.”", "\"거기 떨어뜨렸소.\""]], [["“Where was it, exactly?”", "\"정확히 어디였소?\""]], [["“In the market-place, in the market-place!", "\"장터였소, 장터!"], ["The devil knows whereabouts.", "어디쯤이었는지는 악마나 알겠지."], ["What do you want to know for?”", "그걸 알아서 뭣 하려는 거요?\""]], [["“That’s extremely important, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"그게 대단히 중요한 일이오, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["It would be material evidence in your favor.", "그건 당신에게 유리한 물증이 될 수도 있단 말이오."], ["How is it you don’t understand that?", "어째서 그걸 이해하지 못하는 거요?"], ["Who helped you to sew it up a month ago?”", "한 달 전에 그걸 꿰매는 일을 누가 도와주었소?\""]], [["“No one helped me.", "\"아무도 도와주지 않았소."], ["I did it myself.”", "내가 직접 했소.\""]], [["“Can you sew?”", "\"바느질을 할 줄 아시오?\""]], [["“A soldier has to know how to sew.", "\"군인이라면 바느질할 줄 알아야 하는 법이오."], ["No knowledge was needed to do that.”", "그런 걸 하는 데 무슨 대단한 솜씨가 필요한 것도 아니고.\""]], [["“Where did you get the material, that is, the rag in which you sewed the money?”", "\"그 재료는, 그러니까 돈을 꿰매 넣은 그 헝겊은 어디서 났소?\""]], [["“Are you laughing at me?”", "\"지금 나를 비웃는 거요?\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에."], ["And we are in no mood for laughing, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "그리고 우리는 지금 웃을 기분이 아니오, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""]], [["“I don’t know where I got the rag from—somewhere, I suppose.”", "\"그 헝겊을 어디서 났는지 모르겠소—어딘가에서 났겠지.\""]], [["“I should have thought you couldn’t have forgotten it?”", "\"설마 그걸 잊어버렸을 리가 없을 텐데요?\""]], [["“Upon my word, I don’t remember.", "\"맹세코, 기억이 안 나오."], ["I might have torn a bit off my linen.”", "어쩌면 내 속옷에서 한 조각 찢어 냈을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“That’s very interesting.", "\"그것 참 흥미롭군요."], ["We might find in your lodgings to-morrow the shirt or whatever it is from which you tore the rag.", "우리가 내일 당신 숙소에서 당신이 헝겊을 찢어 낸 그 셔츠인지 뭔지를 찾아낼 수도 있겠소."], ["What sort of rag was it, cloth or linen?”", "그게 어떤 헝겊이었소, 천이었소 아니면 아마포였소?\""]], [["“Goodness only knows what it was.", "\"그게 무엇이었는지는 하느님만 아실 일이오."], ["Wait a bit....", "잠깐 ……"], ["I believe I didn’t tear it off anything.", "아무 데서도 찢어 낸 게 아닌 것 같소."], ["It was a bit of calico....", "옥양목 조각이었소 ……"], ["I believe I sewed it up in a cap of my landlady’s.”", "내 하숙집 여주인의 보닛에 꿰매 넣었던 것 같소.\""]], [["“In your landlady’s cap?”", "\"하숙집 여주인의 보닛에 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["I took it from her.”", "그 여자한테서 가져왔소.\""]], [["“How did you get it?”", "\"어떻게 손에 넣었소?\""]], [["“You see, I remember once taking a cap for a rag, perhaps to wipe my pen on.", "\"보시오, 언젠가 보닛 하나를 헝겊으로 쓰려고, 아마 펜을 닦으려고 가져온 일이 있소."], ["I took it without asking, because it was a worthless rag.", "보잘것없는 헝겊이라 묻지도 않고 가져왔지."], ["I tore it up, and I took the notes and sewed them up in it.", "그걸 찢어서 지폐를 그 안에 꿰매 넣었소."], ["I believe it was in that very rag I sewed them.", "바로 그 헝겊에 꿰맨 것 같소."], ["An old piece of calico, washed a thousand times.”", "천 번은 빨았을 낡은 옥양목 조각이었소.\""]], [["“And you remember that for certain now?”", "\"이제 그것만은 확실히 기억하시오?\""]], [["“I don’t know whether for certain.", "\"확실한지는 모르겠소."], ["I think it was in the cap.", "보닛이었던 것 같긴 한데."], ["But, hang it, what does it matter?”", "하지만 젠장, 그게 무슨 상관이오?\""]], [["“In that case your landlady will remember that the thing was lost?”", "\"그렇다면 당신 하숙집 여주인이 그 물건이 없어진 걸 기억하고 있겠군요?\""]], [["“No, she won’t, she didn’t miss it.", "\"아니, 기억 못 할 거요, 없어진 줄도 몰랐을 테니."], ["It was an old rag, I tell you, an old rag not worth a farthing.”", "낡은 헝겊이었다지 않소, 한 푼어치도 안 되는 낡은 헝겊 말이오.\""]], [["“And where did you get the needle and thread?”", "\"그러면 바늘과 실은 어디서 났소?\""]], [["“I’ll stop now.", "\"이제 그만하겠소."], ["I won’t say any more.", "더는 말하지 않겠소."], ["Enough of it!”", "이젠 됐소!\""], ["said Mitya, losing his temper at last.", "미탸가 마침내 화를 터뜨리며 말했다."]], [["“It’s strange that you should have so completely forgotten where you threw the pieces in the market-place.”", "\"당신이 장터의 어디쯤에 그 조각들을 던졌는지 그렇게 깡그리 잊어버렸다는 게 이상하군요.\""]], [["“Give orders for the market-place to be swept to-morrow, and perhaps you’ll find it,”", "\"내일 장터를 쓸어 보라고 명령을 내리시오, 그러면 어쩌면 찾아낼지도 모르지.\" 미탸가 비웃으며 말했다."], ["said Mitya, sneering.", "\"됐소, 여러분, 됐소!\""], ["“Enough, gentlemen, enough!”", "그가 기진맥진한 목소리로 단언했다."], ["he decided, in an exhausted voice.", "\"당신들이 나를 믿지 않는다는 걸 알겠소!"], ["“I see you don’t believe me!", "단 한순간도 믿지 않는다는 걸!"], ["Not for a moment! It’s my fault, not yours.", "그건 내 잘못이지 당신들 잘못이 아니오."], ["I ought not to have been so ready.", "내가 그렇게 선뜻 입을 열지 말았어야 했소."], ["Why, why did I degrade myself by confessing my secret to you?", "어째서, 어째서 나는 당신들에게 내 비밀을 고백해 스스로를 욕보였단 말인가?"], ["It’s a joke to you.", "당신들에게야 이건 한낱 우스갯거리지."], ["I see that from your eyes.", "당신들 눈빛을 보면 알겠소."], ["You led me on to it, prosecutor?", "검사 양반, 당신이 나를 거기에 끌어들인 거요?"], ["Sing a hymn of triumph if you can....", "할 수 있다면 어디 승리의 찬가나 불러 보시오 ……"], ["Damn you, you torturers!”", "빌어먹을, 이 고문자들 같으니!\""]], [["He bent his head, and hid his face in his hands.", "그는 고개를 숙이고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다."], ["The lawyers were silent.", "두 법관은 잠자코 있었다."], ["A minute later he raised his head and looked at them almost vacantly.", "잠시 후 그는 고개를 들어 거의 멍한 눈길로 그들을 바라보았다."], ["His face now expressed complete, hopeless despair, and he sat mute and passive as though hardly conscious of what was happening. In the meantime they had to finish what they were about.", "그의 얼굴은 이제 완전한, 가망 없는 절망을 드러내고 있었고, 그는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 거의 의식하지 못하는 듯 말없이 무기력하게 앉아 있었다."], ["They had immediately to begin examining the witnesses.", "그러는 동안 그들은 하던 일을 마저 끝내야만 했다."], ["It was by now eight o’clock in the morning.", "곧바로 증인 신문을 시작해야 했던 것이다."], ["The lights had been extinguished long ago.", "어느덧 아침 여덟 시였다."], ["Mihail Makarovitch and Kalganov, who had been continually in and out of the room all the while the interrogation had been going on, had now both gone out again.", "등불은 진작에 꺼져 있었다. 신문이 진행되는 내내 줄곧 방을 들락거리던 미하일 마카로비치와 칼가노프는 이제 둘 다 다시 밖으로 나가고 없었다."], ["The lawyers, too, looked very tired.", "두 법관도 몹시 지쳐 보였다."], ["It was a wretched morning, the whole sky was overcast, and the rain streamed down in bucketfuls.", "음울한 아침이었으니, 온 하늘이 잔뜩 흐려 있었고 비가 양동이로 퍼붓듯 쏟아져 내렸다."], ["Mitya gazed blankly out of the window.", "미탸는 멍하니 창밖을 내다보았다."]], [["“May I look out of the window?”", "\"창밖을 좀 내다봐도 되겠소?\""], ["he asked Nikolay Parfenovitch, suddenly.", "그가 별안간 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, as much as you like,”", "\"오, 얼마든지 보시오.\""], ["the latter replied.", "상대가 대답했다."]], [["Mitya got up and went to the window....", "미탸는 일어나 창가로 다가갔다 ……"], ["The rain lashed against its little greenish panes.", "비가 자그마한 녹빛 유리창을 후려치고 있었다."], ["He could see the muddy road just below the house, and farther away, in the rain and mist, a row of poor, black, dismal huts, looking even blacker and poorer in the rain.", "그는 바로 집 아래의 진창길과, 그 너머 빗속과 안개 속으로 늘어선 가난하고 거뭇하고 음울한 오두막 한 줄을 볼 수 있었는데, 빗속에서 그것들은 한층 더 거뭇하고 가난해 보였다."], ["Mitya thought of “Phœbus the golden-haired,” and how he had meant to shoot himself at his first ray.", "미탸는 \"황금빛 머리칼의 포이보스\"를 떠올렸고, 그 첫 햇살이 비칠 때 권총으로 자신을 쏘려 했던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["“Perhaps it would be even better on a morning like this,”", "\"어쩌면 이런 아침이 오히려 더 나을지도 모르지.\""], ["he thought with a smile, and suddenly, flinging his hand downwards, he turned to his “torturers.”", "그는 미소를 띤 채 생각하다가, 별안간 손을 아래로 내저으며 자신의 \"고문자들\"을 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["he cried, “I see that I am lost!", "그가 외쳤다. \"내가 끝장났다는 건 알겠소!"], ["But she?", "하지만 그녀는?"], ["Tell me about her, I beseech you.", "제발 부탁이오, 그녀에 관해 말해 주시오."], ["Surely she need not be ruined with me?", "설마 그녀까지 나와 함께 파멸해야 하는 건 아니겠지요?"], ["She’s innocent, you know, she was out of her mind when she cried last night ‘It’s all my fault!’ She’s done nothing, nothing!", "그녀는 죄가 없소, 알겠소, 어젯밤 '다 내 잘못이에요!'라고 외쳤을 때 그녀는 제정신이 아니었던 거요. 그녀는 아무 짓도 하지 않았소, 아무 짓도!"], ["I’ve been grieving over her all night as I sat with you....", "나는 당신들과 함께 앉아 있는 내내 밤새 그녀를 생각하며 가슴 아파했소 ……"], ["Can’t you, won’t you tell me what you are going to do with her now?”", "이제 그녀를 어떻게 하려는지 내게 말해 줄 수는, 말해 줄 마음은 없는 거요?\""]], [["“You can set your mind quite at rest on that score, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"그 점이라면 마음을 완전히 놓으셔도 좋소, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["the prosecutor answered at once, with evident alacrity.", "검사가 눈에 띄게 선뜻 즉각 대답했다."], ["“We have, so far, no grounds for interfering with the lady in whom you are so interested.", "\"지금까지는 당신이 그토록 마음을 쓰는 그 부인에게 간섭할 만한 근거가 우리에게 전혀 없소."], ["I trust that it may be the same in the later development of the case....", "사건이 앞으로 전개되어 가더라도 그러하기를 바라오 ……"], ["On the contrary, we’ll do everything that lies in our power in that matter.", "오히려 우리는 그 문제에 관해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다할 거요."], ["Set your mind completely at rest.”", "부디 완전히 마음을 놓으시오.\""]], [["“Gentlemen, I thank you.", "\"여러분, 고맙소."], ["I knew that you were honest, straight-forward people in spite of everything.", "나는 당신들이 무슨 일이 있어도 정직하고 올곧은 사람들이라는 걸 알고 있었소."], ["You’ve taken a load off my heart....", "당신들이 내 가슴에서 무거운 짐을 덜어 주었구려 ……"], ["Well, what are we to do now?", "자, 이제 우리는 무엇을 해야 하오?"], ["I’m ready.”", "나는 준비됐소.\""]], [["“Well, we ought to make haste.", "\"자, 서둘러야겠소."], ["We must pass to examining the witnesses without delay.", "지체 없이 증인 신문으로 넘어가야 하오."], ["That must be done in your presence and therefore—”", "그건 반드시 당신이 입회한 자리에서 이루어져야 하니, 그러므로—\""]], [["“Shouldn’t we have some tea first?”", "\"우선 차라도 좀 마시는 게 어떻겠습니까?\""], ["interposed Nikolay Parfenovitch, “I think we’ve deserved it!”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 끼어들었다. \"우리도 그만한 자격은 있는 것 같은데요!\""]], [["They decided that if tea were ready downstairs (Mihail Makarovitch had, no doubt, gone down to get some) they would have a glass and then “go on and on,”", "그들은 아래층에 차가 준비되어 있다면(미하일 마카로비치가 틀림없이 차를 가지러 내려갔을 터였다) 한 잔씩 마시고 나서 \"계속, 계속\""], ["putting off their proper breakfast until a more favorable opportunity.", "진행하기로, 정작 제대로 된 아침 식사는 좀 더 적당한 기회로 미루기로 정했다."], ["Tea really was ready below, and was soon brought up.", "차는 정말로 아래층에 준비되어 있었고, 이내 위로 올라왔다."], ["Mitya at first refused the glass that Nikolay Parfenovitch politely offered him, but afterwards he asked for it himself and drank it greedily.", "미탸는 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 정중하게 권한 잔을 처음엔 사양했으나, 나중엔 직접 청해서 게걸스럽게 들이켰다."], ["He looked surprisingly exhausted.", "그는 놀라우리만치 지쳐 보였다."], ["It might have been supposed from his Herculean strength that one night of carousing, even accompanied by the most violent emotions, could have had little effect on him.", "그 헤라클레스 같은 체력으로 보아 하룻밤의 술잔치쯤은, 그것이 아무리 격렬한 감정을 동반했다 하더라도, 그에게 별다른 영향을 미치지 못했으리라 짐작할 수도 있었다."], ["But he felt that he could hardly hold his head up, and from time to time all the objects about him seemed heaving and dancing before his eyes.", "그러나 그는 머리조차 가누기 어려울 지경이었고, 때때로 주위의 온갖 사물이 눈앞에서 출렁이며 춤추는 것만 같았다."], ["“A little more and I shall begin raving,”", "\"조금만 더 있으면 나는 헛소리를 지껄이기 시작할 거야.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["# Chapter VIII. The Evidence Of The Witnesses. The Babe", "# Chapter VIII. The Evidence Of The Witnesses. The Babe"]], [["The examination of the witnesses began.", "증인 신문이 시작되었다."], ["But we will not continue our story in such detail as before.", "그러나 우리는 앞서처럼 그렇게 시시콜콜하게 이야기를 이어 가지는 않겠다."], ["And so we will not dwell on how Nikolay Parfenovitch impressed on every witness called that he must give his evidence in accordance with truth and conscience, and that he would afterwards have to repeat his evidence on oath, how every witness was called upon to sign the protocol of his evidence, and so on.", "그러므로 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 호출된 증인 한 사람 한 사람에게 진실과 양심에 따라 증언해야 하며, 나중에 선서한 후 자신의 증언을 되풀이해야 한다고 일러둔 일이라든가, 증인마다 자기 증언의 조서에 서명하도록 요구받은 일 따위는 더 이상 다루지 않겠다."], ["We will only note that the point principally insisted upon in the examination was the question of the three thousand roubles, that is, was the sum spent here, at Mokroe, by Mitya on the first occasion, a month before, three thousand or fifteen hundred?", "다만 신문에서 주로 집요하게 파고든 것이 삼천 루블에 관한 문제였다는 점만 짚어 두겠다. 즉 한 달 전 처음 이곳 모크로예에서 미탸가 쓴 돈이 삼천 루블이었는가, 아니면 천오백 루블이었는가?"], ["And again had he spent three thousand or fifteen hundred yesterday?", "또한 어제 그가 쓴 돈은 삼천이었는가, 천오백이었는가?"], ["Alas, all the evidence given by every one turned out to be against Mitya.", "아아, 모든 이가 내놓은 증언은 죄다 미탸에게 불리하게 드러나고 말았다."], ["There was not one in his favor, and some witnesses introduced new, almost crushing facts, in contradiction of his, Mitya’s, story.", "그에게 유리한 증언은 단 하나도 없었으며, 어떤 증인들은 그, 미탸의 진술과 어긋나는, 거의 결정적인 새 사실들까지 들고 나왔다."]], [["The first witness examined was Trifon Borissovitch.", "처음 신문을 받은 증인은 트리폰 보리소비치였다."], ["He was not in the least abashed as he stood before the lawyers.", "그는 법관들 앞에 서면서도 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다."], ["He had, on the contrary, an air of stern and severe indignation with the accused, which gave him an appearance of truthfulness and personal dignity.", "오히려 그는 피고에 대해 준엄하고 가혹한 분노의 기색을 띠었으며, 그 때문에 그에게는 진실됨과 인격적 위엄의 풍모가 감돌았다."], ["He spoke little, and with reserve, waited to be questioned, answered precisely and deliberately.", "그는 말수가 적었고 조심스러웠으며, 질문을 기다렸다가 정확하고 신중하게 대답했다."], ["Firmly and unhesitatingly he bore witness that the sum spent a month before could not have been less than three thousand, that all the peasants about here would testify that they had heard the sum of three thousand mentioned by Dmitri Fyodorovitch himself.", "그는 한 달 전에 쓰인 돈이 삼천 루블보다 적었을 리 없다고 단호하고 망설임 없이 증언했으며, 이 근방의 농부들이 모두 드미트리 표도로비치 자신의 입에서 삼천이라는 액수가 언급되는 것을 들었다고 증언할 것이라고 했다."], ["“What a lot of money he flung away on the gypsy girls alone!", "\"집시 계집들한테만도 어찌나 돈을 뿌려 댔는지!"], ["He wasted a thousand, I daresay, on them alone.”", "그것들한테만 천 루블쯤은 날렸을 겝니다.\""]], [["“I don’t believe I gave them five hundred,”", "\"내가 그것들한테 오백 루블이라도 줬을 성싶지 않은데.\""], ["was Mitya’s gloomy comment on this.", "미탸는 이에 대해 침울하게 한마디 했다."], ["“It’s a pity I didn’t count the money at the time, but I was drunk....”", "\"그때 돈을 세어 두지 않은 게 안타깝긴 하군. 하지만 난 취해 있었으니까…….\""]], [["Mitya was sitting sideways with his back to the curtains.", "미탸는 옆으로 비스듬히 앉아 커튼을 등지고 있었다."], ["He listened gloomily, with a melancholy and exhausted air, as though he would say:", "그는 침울하게, 우울하고 진이 빠진 기색으로 귀를 기울이고 있었는데, 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯했다."]], [["“Oh, say what you like.", "\"오, 마음대로들 떠들라지."], ["It makes no difference now.”", "이제 와선 아무래도 상관없으니까.\""]], [["“More than a thousand went on them, Dmitri Fyodorovitch,”", "\"천 루블도 넘게 그것들한테 들어갔습니다요, 드미트리 표도로비치.\""], ["retorted Trifon Borissovitch firmly.", "트리폰 보리소비치가 단호하게 받아쳤다."], ["“You flung it about at random and they picked it up.", "\"되는대로 뿌려 대셨고, 그것들이 그걸 주워 갔지요."], ["They were a rascally, thievish lot, horse-stealers, they’ve been driven away from here, or maybe they’d bear witness themselves how much they got from you.", "천하의 망나니에 도둑놈들, 말 도둑들이었는데, 이젠 여기서 다 쫓겨났습죠. 안 그랬으면 그것들이 직접 당신한테서 얼마를 받았는지 증언했을 텐데요."], ["I saw the sum in your hands, myself—count it I didn’t, you didn’t let me, that’s true enough—but by the look of it I should say it was far more than fifteen hundred ...", "나도 그 돈을 당신 손에서 직접 봤습니다요. 세어 보진 못했지요, 당신이 못 세게 했으니까, 그건 사실입니다만, 그래도 어림잡아 보아도 천오백보다는 훨씬 많았다고 하겠습니다……"], ["fifteen hundred, indeed!", "천오백이라니, 원!"], ["We’ve seen money too.", "우리도 돈을 본 적이 있는 사람들입니다."], ["We can judge of amounts....”", "액수쯤은 가늠할 줄 압죠…….\""]], [["As for the sum spent yesterday he asserted that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had told him, as soon as he arrived, that he had brought three thousand with him.", "어제 쓴 돈에 관해서는, 드미트리 표도로비치가 도착하자마자 삼천 루블을 가져왔다고 자기에게 말했다고 주장했다."]], [["“Come now, is that so, Trifon Borissovitch?”", "\"이봐요, 정말 그랬소, 트리폰 보리소비치?\""], ["replied Mitya.", "미탸가 대꾸했다."], ["“Surely I didn’t declare so positively that I’d brought three thousand?”", "\"내가 정말로 삼천을 가져왔다고 그렇게 딱 잘라 말했단 말이오?\""]], [["“You did say so, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "\"그렇게 말씀하셨습니다, 드미트리 표도로비치."], ["You said it before Andrey.", "안드레이 앞에서 그러셨지요."], ["Andrey himself is still here.", "안드레이가 아직 여기 있습니다."], ["Send for him.", "그를 불러오십쇼."], ["And in the hall, when you were treating the chorus, you shouted straight out that you would leave your sixth thousand here—that is with what you spent before, we must understand.", "그리고 홀에서 합창단에게 한턱내실 때, 여섯째 천을 여기다 두고 가겠다고 대놓고 소리치셨습니다요. 즉 전에 쓰신 것까지 합쳐서 그렇단 얘기로 알아들어야겠지요."], ["Stepan and Semyon heard it, and Pyotr Fomitch Kalganov, too, was standing beside you at the time.", "스테판이랑 세묜이 그걸 들었고, 표트르 포미치 칼가노프도 그때 당신 곁에 서 있었습니다요."], ["Maybe he’d remember it....”", "아마 그분도 기억하실 겝니다…….\""]], [["The evidence as to the “sixth”", "이 \"여섯째\""], ["thousand made an extraordinary impression on the two lawyers.", "천 루블에 관한 증언은 두 법관에게 엄청난 인상을 남겼다."], ["They were delighted with this new mode of reckoning; three and three made six, three thousand then and three now made six, that was clear.", "그들은 이 새로운 계산법에 흡족해했다. 삼에 삼을 더하면 육, 그때의 삼천에 지금의 삼천을 더하면 육천, 그것은 명백한 일이었다."]], [["They questioned all the peasants suggested by Trifon Borissovitch, Stepan and Semyon, the driver Andrey, and Kalganov.", "그들은 트리폰 보리소비치가 지목한 농부들, 즉 스테판과 세묜, 마부 안드레이, 그리고 칼가노프를 모두 신문했다. 농부들과 마부는 망설임 없이 트리폰 보리소비치의 증언을 확인해 주었다."], ["The peasants and the driver unhesitatingly confirmed Trifon Borissovitch’s evidence.", "그들은 안드레이가 길에서 미탸와 나눈 대화에 관한 진술을 특별히 신경 써서 받아 적었다."], ["They noted down, with particular care, Andrey’s account of the conversation he had had with Mitya on the road: “‘Where,’ says he, ‘am I, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, going, to heaven or to hell, and shall I be forgiven in the next world or not?’”", "\"'안드레이, 난 말이다,' 하고 그분이 묻습디다, '나 드미트리 표도로비치는 어디로 가는 거냐, 천국이냐 지옥이냐, 그리고 저세상에서 용서받겠느냐 못 받겠느냐?'\""]], [["The psychological Ippolit Kirillovitch heard this with a subtle smile, and ended by recommending that these remarks as to where Dmitri Fyodorovitch would go should be “included in the case.”", "심리학자 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 이 말을 묘한 미소를 지으며 들었고, 마침내 드미트리 표도로비치가 어디로 가게 될지에 관한 이 발언을 \"사건 기록에 포함\"하도록 권고하는 것으로 마무리했다."]], [["Kalganov, when called, came in reluctantly, frowning and ill-humored, and he spoke to the lawyers as though he had never met them before in his life, though they were acquaintances whom he had been meeting every day for a long time past.", "칼가노프는 호출되자 마지못해, 얼굴을 찌푸리고 언짢은 기색으로 들어왔으며, 두 법관과는 오래전부터 매일같이 만나 온 지인 사이였음에도 마치 평생 처음 보는 사람들에게 말하듯 대했다."], ["He began by saying that “he knew nothing about it and didn’t want to.”", "그는 우선 \"그 일에 대해선 아무것도 모르고, 알고 싶지도 않다\"고 운을 뗐다."], ["But it appeared that he had heard of the “sixth”", "그러나 그가 \"여섯째\""], ["thousand, and he admitted that he had been standing close by at the moment.", "천 루블에 관한 말을 들었다는 것이 드러났고, 그 순간 바로 곁에 서 있었다는 사실도 인정했다."], ["As far as he could see he “didn’t know” how much money Mitya had in his hands.", "그가 본 바로는 미탸의 손에 돈이 얼마나 있었는지 \"모른다\"고 했다."], ["He affirmed that the Poles had cheated at cards.", "그는 폴란드인들이 카드에서 속임수를 썼다고 단언했다."], ["In reply to reiterated questions he stated that, after the Poles had been turned out, Mitya’s position with Agrafena Alexandrovna had certainly improved, and that she had said that she loved him.", "거듭된 질문에 대해, 폴란드인들이 쫓겨난 후 미탸의 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와의 관계가 분명 나아졌으며, 그녀가 그를 사랑한다고 말했다고 진술했다."], ["He spoke of Agrafena Alexandrovna with reserve and respect, as though she had been a lady of the best society, and did not once allow himself to call her Grushenka.", "그는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에 대해 마치 그녀가 최상류 사교계의 귀부인이기라도 한 듯 조심스럽고 정중하게 말했으며, 단 한 번도 그녀를 그루셴카라고 부르려 들지 않았다."], ["In spite of the young man’s obvious repugnance at giving evidence, Ippolit Kirillovitch examined him at great length, and only from him learnt all the details of what made up Mitya’s “romance,”", "그 젊은이가 증언하기를 분명히 꺼리는데도 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 그를 아주 길게 신문했으며, 그날 밤 미탸의 이른바 \"로맨스\"를 이룬 온갖 세부 사정을 오직 그에게서만 알아냈다."], ["so to say, on that night. Mitya did not once pull Kalganov up.", "미탸는 단 한 번도 칼가노프의 말을 막지 않았다."], ["At last they let the young man go, and he left the room with unconcealed indignation.", "마침내 그들은 그 젊은이를 놓아주었고, 그는 감추지 못한 분노를 품은 채 방을 나섰다."]], [["The Poles, too, were examined.", "폴란드인들도 신문을 받았다."], ["Though they had gone to bed in their room, they had not slept all night, and on the arrival of the police officers they hastily dressed and got ready, realizing that they would certainly be sent for.", "그들은 자기들 방에서 자리에 눕긴 했으나 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했고, 경찰관들이 도착하자 분명 호출되리라는 것을 깨닫고는 허둥지둥 옷을 차려입고 채비를 갖추었다."], ["They gave their evidence with dignity, though not without some uneasiness.", "그들은 다소 불안한 기색이 없지는 않았으나 위엄을 갖추고 증언했다."], ["The little Pole turned out to be a retired official of the twelfth class, who had served in Siberia as a veterinary surgeon.", "키 작은 폴란드인은 시베리아에서 수의사로 복무했던 십이등관 퇴직 관리로 드러났다."], ["His name was Mussyalovitch.", "이름은 무샬로비치였다."], ["Pan Vrublevsky turned out to be an uncertificated dentist.", "브루블레프스키 씨는 무자격 치과 의사로 밝혀졌다."], ["Although Nikolay Parfenovitch asked them questions on entering the room they both addressed their answers to Mihail Makarovitch, who was standing on one side, taking him in their ignorance for the most important person and in command, and addressed him at every word as “Pan Colonel.”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 방에 들어서며 그들에게 질문을 던졌건만, 둘은 모두 한쪽에 서 있던 미하일 마카로비치를 향해 답했는데, 무지한 탓에 그를 가장 중요하고 지휘권을 쥔 인물로 여겨 말끝마다 그를 \"대령 나리\"라고 불렀다."], ["Only after several reproofs from Mihail Makarovitch himself, they grasped that they had to address their answers to Nikolay Parfenovitch only.", "미하일 마카로비치 자신에게서 몇 차례 핀잔을 듣고 나서야 둘은 답변을 오직 니콜라이 파르페노비치에게만 해야 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["It turned out that they could speak Russian quite correctly except for their accent in some words.", "그들은 몇몇 단어의 발음을 빼면 러시아어를 꽤 정확하게 구사할 줄 아는 것으로 드러났다."], ["Of his relations with Grushenka, past and present, Pan Mussyalovitch spoke proudly and warmly, so that Mitya was roused at once and declared that he would not allow the “scoundrel” to speak like that in his presence!", "그루셴카와의 과거와 현재의 관계에 대해 무샬로비치 씨는 자랑스럽고 열을 올려 말하는 바람에 미탸가 대번에 발끈하여, 자기가 보는 앞에서 그 \"비열한 놈\"이 그런 식으로 말하도록 내버려 두지 않겠다고 선언했다!"], ["Pan Mussyalovitch at once called attention to the word “scoundrel” and begged that it should be put down in the protocol.", "무샬로비치 씨는 즉시 \"비열한 놈\"이라는 말에 주의를 환기하며 그것을 조서에 기록해 달라고 청했다."], ["Mitya fumed with rage.", "미탸는 노여움에 부르르 떨었다."]], [["“He’s a scoundrel!", "\"저놈은 비열한 놈이야!"], ["A scoundrel!", "비열한 놈이라고!"], ["You can put that down.", "그걸 기록하시오."], ["And put down, too, that, in spite of the protocol I still declare that he’s a scoundrel!”", "그리고 조서에 적든 말든 나는 여전히 저놈이 비열한 놈이라고 선언한다는 것도 적으시오!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["Though Nikolay Parfenovitch did insert this in the protocol, he showed the most praiseworthy tact and management.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 이것을 조서에 적어 넣기는 했으나, 더없이 칭찬할 만한 재치와 수완을 발휘했다."], ["After sternly reprimanding Mitya, he cut short all further inquiry into the romantic aspect of the case, and hastened to pass to what was essential.", "그는 미탸를 준엄하게 나무란 후, 사건의 낭만적인 측면에 관한 추가 심문을 모조리 잘라 버리고 서둘러 본질적인 문제로 넘어갔다."], ["One piece of evidence given by the Poles roused special interest in the lawyers: that was how, in that very room, Mitya had tried to buy off Pan Mussyalovitch, and had offered him three thousand roubles to resign his claims, seven hundred roubles down, and the remaining two thousand three hundred “to be paid next day in the town.”", "폴란드인들이 내놓은 증언 가운데 한 대목이 법관들의 각별한 관심을 불러일으켰는데, 그것은 미탸가 바로 그 방에서 무샬로비치 씨를 매수하려 들었던 일, 즉 그에게 청구권을 포기하는 대가로 삼천 루블을 제안하며 그 자리에서 칠백 루블을 내놓고 나머지 이천삼백 루블은 \"이튿날 읍내에서 지불\"하겠다고 한 일이었다."], ["He had sworn at the time that he had not the whole sum with him at Mokroe, but that his money was in the town.", "그때 그는 모크로예에서 전액을 가지고 있지는 않으나 자기 돈이 읍내에 있다고 맹세했다는 것이다."], ["Mitya observed hotly that he had not said that he would be sure to pay him the remainder next day in the town.", "미탸는 이튿날 읍내에서 나머지를 반드시 갚겠다고 말한 적은 없다고 발끈하여 지적했다."], ["But Pan Vrublevsky confirmed the statement, and Mitya, after thinking for a moment admitted, frowning, that it must have been as the Poles stated, that he had been excited at the time, and might indeed have said so.", "그러나 브루블레프스키 씨가 그 진술을 확인해 주었고, 미탸는 잠시 생각한 후 얼굴을 찌푸리며, 아마 폴란드인들이 진술한 대로일 것이며, 그때 자기가 흥분해 있었으니 정말로 그렇게 말했을 수도 있다고 인정했다."]], [["The prosecutor positively pounced on this piece of evidence.", "검사는 이 증언에 단숨에 달려들었다."], ["It seemed to establish for the prosecution (and they did, in fact, base this deduction on it) that half, or a part of, the three thousand that had come into Mitya’s hands might really have been left somewhere hidden in the town, or even, perhaps, somewhere here, in Mokroe.", "그것은 미탸의 손에 들어온 삼천 루블 가운데 절반, 혹은 일부가 정말로 읍내 어딘가에, 어쩌면 여기 모크로예 어딘가에 감춰진 채 남아 있을지도 모른다는 것을 검찰 측에 입증해 주는 듯했다(실제로 그들은 이 추론을 그것에 근거하여 세웠다)."], ["This would explain the circumstance, so baffling for the prosecution, that only eight hundred roubles were to be found in Mitya’s hands.", "이것은 미탸의 손에서 고작 팔백 루블밖에 발견되지 않았다는, 검찰 측으로서는 그토록 당혹스러운 정황을 설명해 줄 터였다."], ["This circumstance had been the one piece of evidence which, insignificant as it was, had hitherto told, to some extent, in Mitya’s favor.", "이 정황은 비록 사소하기는 했으나, 그때까지 어느 정도는 미탸에게 유리하게 작용해 온 유일한 증거였다."], ["Now this one piece of evidence in his favor had broken down.", "그런데 이제 그에게 유리한 이 단 하나의 증거마저 무너지고 만 것이다."], ["In answer to the prosecutor’s inquiry, where he would have got the remaining two thousand three hundred roubles, since he himself had denied having more than fifteen hundred, Mitya confidently replied that he had meant to offer the “little chap,” not money, but a formal deed of conveyance of his rights to the village of Tchermashnya, those rights which he had already offered to Samsonov and Madame Hohlakov.", "자기 자신이 천오백 이상은 가지고 있지 않았다고 부인해 놓고서 나머지 이천삼백 루블은 어디서 마련하려 했느냐는 검사의 추궁에, 미탸는 그 \"꼬마 친구\"에게 돈이 아니라 체르마시냐 마을에 대한 자기 권리의 정식 양도 증서를 내놓을 작정이었다고 자신만만하게 대답했으니, 그것은 이미 삼소노프와 호흘라코바 부인에게도 제안했던 바로 그 권리였다."], ["The prosecutor positively smiled at the “innocence of this subterfuge.”", "검사는 \"이 발뺌의 천진함\"에 대놓고 미소를 지었다."]], [["“And you imagine he would have accepted such a deed as a substitute for two thousand three hundred roubles in cash?”", "\"그러면 당신은 그 사람이 이천삼백 루블 현금 대신 그런 증서를 받아들였으리라고 생각하는 거요?\""]], [["“He certainly would have accepted it,”", "\"그자라면 틀림없이 받아들였을 거요.\""], ["Mitya declared warmly.", "미탸가 열을 올리며 단언했다."], ["“Why, look here, he might have grabbed not two thousand, but four or six, for it.", "\"이거 보시오, 그자라면 그걸 가지고 이천이 아니라 사천이고 육천이고 거머쥘 수 있었을 테니까."], ["He would have put his lawyers, Poles and Jews, on to the job, and might have got, not three thousand, but the whole property out of the old man.”", "그자는 자기 변호사들, 폴란드인이며 유대인들을 그 일에 붙여서, 삼천이 아니라 그 영감한테서 재산을 통째로 뜯어낼 수도 있었을 거란 말이오.\""]], [["The evidence of Pan Mussyalovitch was, of course, entered in the protocol in the fullest detail.", "무샬로비치 씨의 증언은 물론 더없이 자세하게 조서에 기록되었다."], ["Then they let the Poles go.", "그런 다음 그들은 폴란드인들을 놓아주었다."], ["The incident of the cheating at cards was hardly touched upon.", "카드 속임수 사건은 거의 건드려지지 않았다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch was too well pleased with them, as it was, and did not want to worry them with trifles, moreover, it was nothing but a foolish, drunken quarrel over cards.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그것만으로도 그들에게 충분히 만족했던 터라 시시한 일로 그들을 성가시게 하고 싶지 않았으며, 더구나 그것은 카드를 둘러싼 어리석고 술 취한 다툼에 지나지 않았다."], ["There had been drinking and disorder enough, that night....", "그날 밤은 술판과 소동만으로도 이미 충분했던 것이다……"], ["So the two hundred roubles remained in the pockets of the Poles.", "그리하여 이백 루블은 폴란드인들의 주머니 속에 그대로 남게 되었다."]], [["Then old Maximov was summoned.", "이어서 늙은 막시모프가 불려 왔다."], ["He came in timidly, approached with little steps, looking very disheveled and depressed.", "그는 잔걸음으로 머뭇머뭇 다가왔으며, 몹시 헝클어지고 풀이 죽은 모습이었다."], ["He had, all this time, taken refuge below with Grushenka, sitting dumbly beside her, and “now and then he’d begin blubbering over her and wiping his eyes with a blue check handkerchief,” as Mihail Makarovitch described afterwards.", "그는 그동안 내내 아래층에서 그루셴카 곁에 피신해 말없이 앉아 있었는데, \"이따금 그녀를 붙들고 훌쩍거리며 파란 체크무늬 손수건으로 눈물을 닦곤 했다\"고 미하일 마카로비치가 나중에 묘사했다."], ["So that she herself began trying to pacify and comfort him.", "그래서 오히려 그녀가 그를 달래고 위로하려 들 정도였다."], ["The old man at once confessed that he had done wrong, that he had borrowed “ten roubles in my poverty,”", "늙은이는 대뜸 자기가 잘못을 저질렀다고, \"곤궁한 처지라 십 루블을\""], ["from Dmitri Fyodorovitch, and that he was ready to pay it back.", "드미트리 표도로비치에게서 빌렸으며 그것을 갚을 용의가 있다고 고백했다."], ["To Nikolay Parfenovitch’s direct question, had he noticed how much money Dmitri Fyodorovitch held in his hand, as he must have been able to see the sum better than any one when he took the note from him, Maximov, in the most positive manner, declared that there was twenty thousand.", "드미트리 표도로비치에게서 지폐를 받을 때 누구보다 그 액수를 잘 보았을 터이니 그의 손에 돈이 얼마나 들려 있었는지 보았느냐는 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 직접적인 질문에, 막시모프는 더없이 단호한 태도로 이만 루블이 있었다고 단언했다."]], [["“Have you ever seen so much as twenty thousand before, then?”", "\"그렇다면 당신은 이만 루블이나 되는 돈을 전에 본 적이라도 있단 말이오?\""], ["inquired Nikolay Parfenovitch, with a smile.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["“To be sure I have, not twenty, but seven, when my wife mortgaged my little property.", "\"물론 본 적이 있고말굽쇼, 이만은 아니어도 칠천을요, 우리 마누라가 내 자그마한 영지를 저당 잡혔을 때 말입니다."], ["She’d only let me look at it from a distance, boasting of it to me.", "멀찍이서 구경만 시켜 주고는 나한테 그걸 자랑하더군요."], ["It was a very thick bundle, all rainbow-colored notes.", "아주 두툼한 뭉치였는데, 죄다 무지갯빛 지폐였습죠."], ["And Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s were all rainbow-colored....”", "그런데 드미트리 표도로비치의 것도 죄다 무지갯빛이더란 말입니다…….\""]], [["He was not kept long.", "그는 오래 붙들려 있지 않았다."], ["At last it was Grushenka’s turn.", "마침내 그루셴카의 차례가 되었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch was obviously apprehensive of the effect her appearance might have on Mitya, and he muttered a few words of admonition to him, but Mitya bowed his head in silence, giving him to understand “that he would not make a scene.”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 그녀의 등장이 미탸에게 미칠 영향을 분명히 우려하여 그에게 몇 마디 주의를 중얼거렸으나, 미탸는 말없이 고개를 숙여 \"소란을 피우지 않겠다\"는 뜻을 내비쳤다."], ["Mihail Makarovitch himself led Grushenka in.", "미하일 마카로비치가 친히 그루셴카를 데리고 들어왔다."], ["She entered with a stern and gloomy face, that looked almost composed and sat down quietly on the chair offered her by Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "그녀는 준엄하고 침울한, 거의 평정해 보이는 얼굴로 들어와서는 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 권한 의자에 조용히 앉았다."], ["She was very pale, she seemed to be cold, and wrapped herself closely in her magnificent black shawl.", "그녀는 몹시 창백했고, 추워하는 듯 자신의 화려한 검은 숄로 몸을 단단히 감쌌다."], ["She was suffering from a slight feverish chill—the first symptom of the long illness which followed that night.", "그녀는 가벼운 오한에 시달리고 있었는데, 그것은 그날 밤 뒤에 찾아온 긴 병의 첫 징후였다."], ["Her grave air, her direct earnest look and quiet manner made a very favorable impression on every one.", "그녀의 진중한 태도, 솔직하고 진지한 눈빛, 조용한 거동은 모든 이에게 매우 호의적인 인상을 남겼다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch was even a little bit “fascinated.”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 심지어 다소 \"매료\"되기까지 했다."], ["He admitted himself, when talking about it afterwards, that only then had he seen “how handsome the woman was,”", "그는 나중에 그 일을 이야기할 때, 비록 전에도 그녀를 여러 번 보긴 했으나 늘 그녀를 일종의 \"시골 창부\""], ["for, though he had seen her several times before, he had always looked upon her as something of a “provincial hetaira.”", "정도로 여겨 왔던 터라 그제야 비로소 \"그 여자가 얼마나 아름다운지\" 보았노라고 스스로 인정했다."], ["“She has the manners of the best society,”", "\"그녀에겐 최상류 사교계의 몸가짐이 있더군.\""], ["he said enthusiastically, gossiping about her in a circle of ladies.", "그는 부인들의 모임에서 그녀에 관한 잡담을 늘어놓으며 열을 올려 말했다."], ["But this was received with positive indignation by the ladies, who immediately called him a “naughty man,” to his great satisfaction.", "그러나 이 말은 부인들에게서 노골적인 분노를 샀고, 그들은 대번에 그를 \"엉큼한 양반\"이라고 불렀는데, 그는 그것을 무척 흡족해했다."]], [["As she entered the room, Grushenka only glanced for an instant at Mitya, who looked at her uneasily.", "그루셴카는 방에 들어서며 미탸를 잠깐 한번 흘끗 쳐다보았을 뿐인데, 미탸는 불안한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다."], ["But her face reassured him at once.", "그러나 그녀의 표정은 대번에 그를 안심시켰다."], ["After the first inevitable inquiries and warnings, Nikolay Parfenovitch asked her, hesitating a little, but preserving the most courteous manner, on what terms she was with the retired lieutenant, Dmitri Fyodorovitch Karamazov.", "으레 거쳐야 할 첫 질문과 경고가 끝난 후, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 다소 머뭇거리면서도 더없이 정중한 태도를 잃지 않은 채, 퇴역 중위 드미트리 표도로비치 카라마조프와 어떤 사이냐고 그녀에게 물었다."], ["To this Grushenka firmly and quietly replied:", "이에 그루셴카는 단호하고 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["“He was an acquaintance.", "\"지인이었어요."], ["He came to see me as an acquaintance during the last month.”", "지난 한 달 동안 지인으로서 저를 찾아오곤 했지요.\""], ["To further inquisitive questions she answered plainly and with complete frankness, that, though “at times”", "캐묻는 듯한 추가 질문에 대해 그녀는 거리낌 없이 완전히 솔직하게, 비록 \"때때로\" 그가 매력적이라고 여기긴 했으나 그를 사랑한 적은 없으며, \"고약한 심술에서\""], ["she had thought him attractive, she had not loved him, but had won his heart as well as his old father’s “in my nasty spite,”", "그와 그의 늙은 아버지의 마음을 모두 사로잡았다고, 미탸가 표도르 파블로비치며 그 밖의 모든 이를 몹시 질투하는 것을 보았으나 그것이 그저 재미있었을 뿐이라고 대답했다."], ["that she had seen that Mitya was very jealous of Fyodor Pavlovitch and every one else; but that had only amused her.", "그녀는 표도르 파블로비치에게 갈 마음 따위는 결코 없었으며, 그저 그를 놀려 댔을 뿐이라고 했다."], ["She had never meant to go to Fyodor Pavlovitch, she had simply been laughing at him.", "\"지난 한 달 동안 저는 그 두 사람 어느 쪽에도 마음이 없었어요."], ["“I had no thoughts for either of them all this last month.", "저는 저를 모욕했던 다른 남자를 기다리고 있었으니까요."], ["I was expecting another man who had wronged me.", "하지만 제 생각엔,\""], ["But I think,”", "그녀가 끝맺으며 말했다."], ["she said in conclusion, “that there’s no need for you to inquire about that, nor for me to answer you, for that’s my own affair.”", "\"그 일에 대해서는 당신들이 캐물을 필요도, 제가 답할 필요도 없을 것 같아요. 그건 제 개인적인 일이니까요.\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch immediately acted upon this hint.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 곧바로 이 암시에 따랐다."], ["He again dismissed the “romantic”", "그는 다시 사건의 \"낭만적인\""], ["aspect of the case and passed to the serious one, that is, to the question of most importance, concerning the three thousand roubles.", "측면을 접어 두고 진지한 측면으로, 즉 삼천 루블에 관한 가장 중요한 문제로 넘어갔다."], ["Grushenka confirmed the statement that three thousand roubles had certainly been spent on the first carousal at Mokroe, and, though she had not counted the money herself, she had heard that it was three thousand from Dmitri Fyodorovitch’s own lips.", "그루셴카는 모크로예에서의 첫 번째 술잔치에 삼천 루블이 분명히 쓰였다는 진술을 확인해 주었으며, 비록 자신이 직접 돈을 세어 보지는 않았으나 드미트리 표도로비치 자신의 입에서 삼천이라는 말을 들었다고 했다."]], [["“Did he tell you that alone, or before some one else, or did you only hear him speak of it to others in your presence?”", "\"그가 당신에게만 그렇게 말했소, 아니면 다른 누군가 앞에서 그랬소, 그것도 아니면 그저 당신이 있는 자리에서 그가 남들에게 그 말을 하는 것을 들었을 뿐이오?\""], ["the prosecutor inquired immediately.", "검사가 곧바로 물었다."]], [["To which Grushenka replied that she had heard him say so before other people, and had heard him say so when they were alone.", "이에 대해 그루셴카는, 다른 사람들 앞에서 그가 그렇게 말하는 것도 들었고, 둘만 있을 때 그렇게 말하는 것도 들었다고 답했다."]], [["“Did he say it to you alone once, or several times?”", "\"그가 당신에게만 그렇게 말한 것이 한 번이오, 아니면 여러 번이오?\""], ["inquired the prosecutor, and learned that he had told Grushenka so several times.", "검사가 물었고, 미탸가 그루셴카에게 그렇게 말한 것이 여러 번이었다는 것을 알아냈다."]], [["Ippolit Kirillovitch was very well satisfied with this piece of evidence.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 이 증언에 매우 흡족해했다."], ["Further examination elicited that Grushenka knew, too, where that money had come from, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had got it from Katerina Ivanovna.", "추가 신문을 통해, 그루셴카 역시 그 돈이 어디서 났는지 알고 있었으며, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그것을 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 받았다는 것이 밝혀졌다."]], [["“And did you never, once, hear that the money spent a month ago was not three thousand, but less, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch had saved half that sum for his own use?”", "\"그러면 당신은 한 달 전에 쓰인 돈이 삼천이 아니라 그보다 적었으며, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그 절반을 자기가 쓰려고 따로 챙겨 두었다는 말을 단 한 번도 들은 적이 없소?\""]], [["“No, I never heard that,”", "\"아뇨, 그런 말은 전혀 들은 적이 없어요.\""], ["answered Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 대답했다."]], [["It was explained further that Mitya had, on the contrary, often told her that he hadn’t a farthing.", "미탸가 오히려 그녀에게 자기는 한 푼도 없다고 자주 말했다는 것도 밝혀졌다."]], [["“He was always expecting to get some from his father,”", "\"그이는 늘 아버지한테서 돈을 좀 받게 되기를 기대하고 있었어요.\""], ["said Grushenka in conclusion.", "그루셴카가 끝맺으며 말했다."]], [["“Did he never say before you ...", "\"그가 당신 앞에서 한 번도……"], ["casually, or in a moment of irritation,”", "무심코, 혹은 발끈한 순간에,\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch put in suddenly, “that he intended to make an attempt on his father’s life?”", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 불쑥 끼어들었다. \"자기 아버지의 목숨을 노릴 작정이라고 말한 적이 없소?\""]], [["“Ach, he did say so,”", "\"아흐, 그런 말은 했어요.\""], ["sighed Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Once or several times?”", "\"한 번이오, 아니면 여러 번이오?\""]], [["“He mentioned it several times, always in anger.”", "\"여러 번 그 말을 했어요, 언제나 화가 났을 때였지요.\""]], [["“And did you believe he would do it?”", "\"그러면 당신은 그가 정말 그러리라고 믿었소?\""]], [["“No, I never believed it,”", "\"아뇨, 저는 그런 건 결코 믿지 않았어요.\""], ["she answered firmly.", "그녀가 단호하게 대답했다."], ["“I had faith in his noble heart.”", "\"저는 그이의 고귀한 마음을 믿었으니까요.\""]], [["“Gentlemen, allow me,”", "\"여러분, 허락해 주십시오.\""], ["cried Mitya suddenly, “allow me to say one word to Agrafena Alexandrovna, in your presence.”", "미탸가 불쑥 외쳤다. \"여러분이 보는 앞에서 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나에게 한마디만 하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“You can speak,”", "\"말해도 좋소.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch assented.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 동의했다."]], [["“Agrafena Alexandrovna!”", "\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나!\""], ["Mitya got up from his chair, “have faith in God and in me.", "미탸가 의자에서 일어섰다. \"하느님을 믿고, 또 나를 믿어 주오."], ["I am not guilty of my father’s murder!”", "나는 아버지의 살해에 죄가 없소!\""]], [["Having uttered these words Mitya sat down again on his chair.", "이 말을 내뱉고 미탸는 다시 의자에 앉았다."], ["Grushenka stood up and crossed herself devoutly before the ikon.", "그루셴카는 일어서서 성상 앞에 경건하게 성호를 그었다."], ["“Thanks be to Thee, O Lord,”", "\"주여, 당신께 감사드립니다.\""], ["she said, in a voice thrilled with emotion, and still standing, she turned to Nikolay Parfenovitch and added:", "그녀는 감격에 떨리는 목소리로 말했고, 여전히 선 채로 니콜라이 파르페노비치를 향해 돌아서서 덧붙였다."]], [["“As he has spoken now, believe it! I know him.", "\"방금 저이가 말한 그대로니, 그걸 믿으세요!"], ["He’ll say anything as a joke or from obstinacy, but he’ll never deceive you against his conscience.", "저는 저이를 알아요. 저이는 농담이나 고집으로는 무슨 말이든 하지만, 양심을 거슬러 당신들을 속이는 일은 결코 없어요."], ["He’s telling the whole truth, you may believe it.”", "저이는 모든 진실을 말하고 있으니, 그걸 믿으셔도 돼요.\""]], [["“Thanks, Agrafena Alexandrovna, you’ve given me fresh courage,”", "\"고맙소, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나, 당신이 내게 새 용기를 주었소.\""], ["Mitya responded in a quivering voice.", "미탸가 떨리는 목소리로 화답했다."]], [["As to the money spent the previous day, she declared that she did not know what sum it was, but had heard him tell several people that he had three thousand with him.", "전날 쓴 돈에 관해서는, 그것이 얼마였는지 모르지만 그가 여러 사람에게 삼천을 가지고 있다고 말하는 것을 들었다고 그녀는 진술했다."], ["And to the question where he got the money, she said that he had told her that he had “stolen” it from Katerina Ivanovna, and that she had replied to that that he hadn’t stolen it, and that he must pay the money back next day.", "그리고 그 돈을 어디서 났느냐는 질문에는, 그가 그것을 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 \"훔쳤다\"고 자기에게 말했으며, 자기는 그에게 그건 훔친 게 아니니 이튿날 그 돈을 갚아야 한다고 대꾸했다고 했다."], ["On the prosecutor’s asking her emphatically whether the money he said he had stolen from Katerina Ivanovna was what he had spent yesterday, or what he had squandered here a month ago, she declared that he meant the money spent a month ago, and that that was how she understood him.", "그가 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 훔쳤다고 한 돈이 어제 쓴 돈인지, 아니면 한 달 전 이곳에서 탕진한 돈인지 검사가 힘주어 묻자, 그녀는 그가 한 달 전에 쓴 돈을 두고 한 말이며, 자기는 그렇게 알아들었다고 단언했다."]], [["Grushenka was at last released, and Nikolay Parfenovitch informed her impulsively that she might at once return to the town and that if he could be of any assistance to her, with horses for example, or if she would care for an escort, he ...", "마침내 그루셴카는 풀려났고, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 충동적으로 그녀에게 곧장 읍내로 돌아가도 좋다고, 그리고 자기가 무슨 도움이라도 될 수 있다면, 이를테면 말을 마련해 주는 일이라든가, 혹은 호위가 필요하다면 자기가……"], ["would be—", "기꺼이 하겠노라고 일러 주었다."]], [["“I thank you sincerely,”", "\"진심으로 감사드려요.\" 그루셴카가 그에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다."], ["said Grushenka, bowing to him, “I’m going with this old gentleman, I am driving him back to town with me, and meanwhile, if you’ll allow me, I’ll wait below to hear what you decide about Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "\"저는 이 노신사분과 함께 갈 거예요, 저이를 제 마차에 태워 읍내로 모시고 갈 참이거든요. 그리고 그동안, 허락해 주신다면, 드미트리 표도로비치에 관해 당신들이 무슨 결정을 내리는지 듣기 위해 아래층에서 기다리겠어요.\""]], [["She went out.", "그녀가 나갔다."], ["Mitya was calm, and even looked more cheerful, but only for a moment.", "미탸는 차분했고, 심지어 한결 밝아 보이기까지 했으나, 그것도 잠깐뿐이었다."], ["He felt more and more oppressed by a strange physical weakness.", "그는 점점 더 기이한 육체적 무력감에 짓눌렸다."], ["His eyes were closing with fatigue.", "두 눈은 피로로 감겨 왔다."], ["The examination of the witnesses was, at last, over.", "증인 신문은 마침내 끝났다."], ["They proceeded to a final revision of the protocol.", "그들은 조서의 최종 검토에 들어갔다."], ["Mitya got up, moved from his chair to the corner by the curtain, lay down on a large chest covered with a rug, and instantly fell asleep.", "미탸는 일어나 의자에서 커튼 옆 구석으로 자리를 옮겨, 깔개가 덮인 커다란 궤짝 위에 드러눕더니, 곧바로 잠이 들었다."]], [["He had a strange dream, utterly out of keeping with the place and the time.", "그는 그 장소와 시간에 전혀 어울리지 않는 기이한 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["He was driving somewhere in the steppes, where he had been stationed long ago, and a peasant was driving him in a cart with a pair of horses, through snow and sleet.", "그는 오래전에 주둔했던 어느 초원 지대를 마차로 달리고 있었는데, 한 농부가 두 마리 말이 끄는 짐마차에 그를 태우고 눈과 진눈깨비 속을 헤치고 갔다."], ["He was cold, it was early in November, and the snow was falling in big wet flakes, melting as soon as it touched the earth.", "그는 추웠고, 때는 십일월 초였으며, 눈은 큼직하게 젖은 송이로 내려 땅에 닿자마자 녹아 버렸다."], ["And the peasant drove him smartly, he had a fair, long beard.", "농부는 그를 활기차게 몰아갔는데, 희끗한 긴 수염을 기르고 있었다."], ["He was not an old man, somewhere about fifty, and he had on a gray peasant’s smock.", "늙은이는 아니고 쉰쯤 되어 보였으며, 잿빛 농부 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["Not far off was a village, he could see the black huts, and half the huts were burnt down, there were only the charred beams sticking up.", "멀지 않은 곳에 마을이 있어 검은 오두막들이 보였는데, 오두막 절반은 불에 타 버려 그을린 들보들만 삐죽삐죽 솟아 있었다."], ["And as they drove in, there were peasant women drawn up along the road, a lot of women, a whole row, all thin and wan, with their faces a sort of brownish color, especially one at the edge, a tall, bony woman, who looked forty, but might have been only twenty, with a long thin face.", "그들이 마을로 들어서자, 길을 따라 농가 아낙들이 줄지어 서 있었는데, 한 무리의 여인들이, 죽 늘어선 한 줄이, 모두 야위고 핼쑥했으며, 얼굴은 거뭇한 빛을 띠고 있었고, 특히 맨 끝에 선 한 여인은 키가 크고 뼈가 앙상하여 마흔쯤으로 보였으나 어쩌면 스물밖에 안 되었을지도 모를, 길고 야윈 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["And in her arms was a little baby crying.", "그리고 그녀의 품에는 울고 있는 어린 갓난아기가 안겨 있었다."], ["And her breasts seemed so dried up that there was not a drop of milk in them.", "그녀의 젖가슴은 어찌나 바싹 말라붙었는지 젖이 한 방울도 나지 않을 듯했다."], ["And the child cried and cried, and held out its little bare arms, with its little fists blue from cold.", "아이는 울고 또 울었으며, 추위로 새파래진 작은 주먹을 쥔, 발가벗은 작은 두 팔을 내뻗고 있었다."]], [["“Why are they crying?", "\"왜 저들이 울고 있지?"], ["Why are they crying?”", "왜 저들이 울지?\""], ["Mitya asked, as they dashed gayly by.", "미탸가 그 곁을 경쾌하게 내달리며 물었다."]], [["“It’s the babe,”", "\"애기예요.\""], ["answered the driver, “the babe weeping.”", "마부가 대답했다. \"애기가 우는 겁니다.\""]], [["And Mitya was struck by his saying, in his peasant way, “the babe,” and he liked the peasant’s calling it a “babe.”", "미탸는 마부가 농부답게 \"애기\"라고 말하는 것에 마음이 찔렸고, 그 농부가 그것을 \"애기\"라고 부르는 것이 마음에 들었다."], ["There seemed more pity in it.", "거기에는 더 많은 연민이 담겨 있는 듯했다."]], [["“But why is it weeping?”", "\"한데 왜 우는 거지?\""], ["Mitya persisted stupidly, “why are its little arms bare?", "미탸가 어리석게도 끈질기게 물었다. \"왜 저 작은 팔이 발가벗었지?"], ["Why don’t they wrap it up?”", "왜 감싸 주질 않는 거지?\""]], [["“The babe’s cold, its little clothes are frozen and don’t warm it.”", "\"애기가 춥거든요, 애기 옷이 얼어붙어서 따뜻하질 않은 거지요.\""]], [["“But why is it?", "\"한데 왜 그렇지?"], ["Why?”", "왜?\""], ["foolish Mitya still persisted.", "어리석은 미탸가 여전히 끈질기게 물었다."]], [["“Why, they’re poor people, burnt out. They’ve no bread.", "\"왜라뇨, 가난한 사람들이거든요, 불타 버렸어요."], ["They’re begging because they’ve been burnt out.”", "빵도 없습죠. 불타 버렸으니 구걸을 하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아냐, 아냐.\""], ["Mitya, as it were, still did not understand.", "미탸는 여전히 알아듣지 못하겠다는 듯했다."], ["“Tell me why it is those poor mothers stand there?", "\"왜 저 가난한 어미들이 저기 서 있는 건지 말해 봐."], ["Why are people poor?", "왜 사람들은 가난한 거지?"], ["Why is the babe poor?", "왜 애기는 가난한 거지?"], ["Why is the steppe barren?", "왜 초원은 황량한 거지?"], ["Why don’t they hug each other and kiss?", "왜 저들은 서로 부둥켜안고 입 맞추지 않는 거지?"], ["Why don’t they sing songs of joy?", "왜 저들은 기쁨의 노래를 부르지 않지?"], ["Why are they so dark from black misery?", "왜 저들은 시커먼 비참함으로 저토록 거뭇한 거지?"], ["Why don’t they feed the babe?”", "왜 저들은 애기에게 젖을 먹이지 않지?\""]], [["And he felt that, though his questions were unreasonable and senseless, yet he wanted to ask just that, and he had to ask it just in that way.", "그리고 그는 비록 자신의 물음들이 이치에 닿지 않고 터무니없긴 하나, 그래도 바로 그것을 묻고 싶었고, 또 바로 그런 식으로 물어야만 한다고 느꼈다."], ["And he felt that a passion of pity, such as he had never known before, was rising in his heart, that he wanted to cry, that he wanted to do something for them all, so that the babe should weep no more, so that the dark-faced, dried-up mother should not weep, that no one should shed tears again from that moment, and he wanted to do it at once, at once, regardless of all obstacles, with all the recklessness of the Karamazovs.", "그리고 그는 일찍이 한 번도 알지 못했던 연민의 격정이 자기 가슴속에 솟구치는 것을, 울고 싶고, 저들 모두를 위해 무언가를 하고 싶다는 것을 느꼈으니, 애기가 더는 울지 않도록, 거뭇한 얼굴의 바싹 마른 어미가 울지 않도록, 그 순간부터 다시는 아무도 눈물 흘리지 않도록 하고 싶었고, 또 그것을 지금 당장, 당장에, 온갖 장애물에도 아랑곳없이, 카라마조프 일가다운 그 모든 무모함을 다해 해내고 싶었다."]], [["“And I’m coming with you.", "\"그리고 저도 당신과 함께 가겠어요."], ["I won’t leave you now for the rest of my life, I’m coming with you,”", "이제 평생토록 당신 곁을 떠나지 않겠어요, 저도 당신과 함께 가겠어요.\""], ["he heard close beside him Grushenka’s tender voice, thrilling with emotion.", "그는 바로 곁에서 감격에 떨리는 그루셴카의 다정한 목소리를 들었다."], ["And his heart glowed, and he struggled forward towards the light, and he longed to live, to live, to go on and on, towards the new, beckoning light, and to hasten, hasten, now, at once!", "그러자 그의 가슴은 뜨겁게 타올랐고, 그는 빛을 향해 안간힘을 쓰며 나아갔으며, 살고 싶고, 살고 싶고, 그 새로운, 손짓하는 빛을 향해 자꾸자꾸 나아가고 싶었고, 서두르고, 서두르고 싶었으니, 지금, 당장에!"]], [["“What!", "\"뭐야!"], ["Where?”", "어디지?\""], ["he exclaimed opening his eyes, and sitting up on the chest, as though he had revived from a swoon, smiling brightly.", "그가 두 눈을 뜨고 궤짝 위에 일어나 앉으며 외쳤는데, 마치 졸도에서 깨어난 듯 환하게 미소 짓고 있었다."], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch was standing over him, suggesting that he should hear the protocol read aloud and sign it.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 그를 굽어보고 서서, 조서를 낭독하는 것을 듣고 거기에 서명하라고 권하고 있었다."], ["Mitya guessed that he had been asleep an hour or more, but he did not hear Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "미탸는 자기가 한 시간 남짓 잠들어 있었음을 알아차렸으나, 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 말은 귀에 들어오지 않았다."], ["He was suddenly struck by the fact that there was a pillow under his head, which hadn’t been there when he had leant back, exhausted, on the chest.", "그는 진이 빠진 채 궤짝에 등을 기댔을 때는 없던 베개가 자기 머리 밑에 놓여 있다는 사실에 문득 마음이 찔렸다."]], [["“Who put that pillow under my head?", "\"누가 내 머리 밑에 이 베개를 놓아 주었지?"], ["Who was so kind?”", "누가 이토록 친절을 베풀었지?\""], ["he cried, with a sort of ecstatic gratitude, and tears in his voice, as though some great kindness had been shown him.", "그는 마치 자기에게 무슨 크나큰 친절이 베풀어지기라도 한 듯, 일종의 황홀한 감사에 젖어 목소리에 눈물을 머금고 외쳤다."]], [["He never found out who this kind man was; perhaps one of the peasant witnesses, or Nikolay Parfenovitch’s little secretary, had compassionately thought to put a pillow under his head; but his whole soul was quivering with tears.", "그는 이 친절한 사람이 누구였는지 끝내 알아내지 못했다. 어쩌면 증인으로 나온 농부들 가운데 하나, 혹은 니콜라이 파르페노비치의 어린 서기가 가엾이 여겨 그의 머리 밑에 베개를 놓아 줄 생각을 했는지도 몰랐다."], ["He went to the table and said that he would sign whatever they liked.", "그러나 그의 온 영혼이 눈물로 떨리고 있었다. 그는 탁자로 가서, 그들이 원하는 것이라면 무엇이든 서명하겠다고 말했다."]], [["“I’ve had a good dream, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분, 나는 좋은 꿈을 꾸었소.\""], ["he said in a strange voice, with a new light, as of joy, in his face.", "그는 얼굴에 기쁨인 듯한 새로운 빛을 띤 채 기이한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["# Chapter IX. They Carry Mitya Away", "# Chapter IX. They Carry Mitya Away"]], [["When the protocol had been signed, Nikolay Parfenovitch turned solemnly to the prisoner and read him the “Committal,” setting forth, that in such a year, on such a day, in such a place, the investigating lawyer of such-and-such a district court, having examined so-and-so (to wit, Mitya) accused of this and of that (all the charges were carefully written out) and having considered that the accused, not pleading guilty to the charges made against him, had brought forward nothing in his defense, while the witnesses, so-and-so, and so-and-so, and the circumstances such-and-such testify against him, acting in accordance with such-and-such articles of the Statute Book, and so on, has ruled, that, in order to preclude so-and-so (Mitya) from all means of evading pursuit and judgment he be detained in such-and-such a prison, which he hereby notifies to the accused and communicates a copy of this same “Committal” to the deputy prosecutor, and so on, and so on.", "조서에 서명이 끝나자, 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 엄숙하게 피고를 향해 돌아서서 \"구류 결정서\"를 읽어 내려갔으니, 그 내용인즉 모년 모월 모일 모소에서 모 지방 법원의 예심 판사가 이러저러한 혐의로 기소된 모씨(곧 미탸)를 심문하였고(모든 혐의가 빠짐없이 적혀 있었다), 피고가 자신에게 제기된 혐의에 대해 유죄를 인정하지 않으면서도 변호를 위해 아무것도 제시하지 못한 점, 그리고 증인 모씨와 모씨, 또한 이러저러한 정황이 그에게 불리하게 증언하는 점을 헤아려, 법전의 이러저러한 조항에 따라 모씨(미탸)가 추적과 재판을 피할 모든 수단을 차단하기 위하여 그를 모 감옥에 구류함을 판결하며, 이로써 피고에게 이를 고지하고 동일한 \"구류 결정서\"의 사본 한 부를 검사보에게 전달한다는, 그러한 내용이 이러쿵저러쿵 이어졌다."]], [["In brief, Mitya was informed that he was, from that moment, a prisoner, and that he would be driven at once to the town, and there shut up in a very unpleasant place.", "요컨대 미탸는 그 순간부터 자신이 죄수의 신세이며, 곧장 읍내로 끌려가 거기서 아주 불쾌한 곳에 갇히게 되리라는 통고를 받은 것이었다."], ["Mitya listened attentively, and only shrugged his shoulders.", "미탸는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였고, 다만 어깨를 한번 으쓱했을 뿐이다."]], [["“Well, gentlemen, I don’t blame you.", "\"좋습니다, 여러분, 나는 여러분을 탓하지 않소."], ["I’m ready....", "나는 준비가 되어 있소…."], ["I understand that there’s nothing else for you to do.”", "여러분으로서도 달리 어쩔 도리가 없다는 것을 이해하니까.\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch informed him gently that he would be escorted at once by the rural police officer, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, who happened to be on the spot....", "니콜라이 파르페노비치는 부드러운 어조로, 마침 그 자리에 있던 시골 경찰관 마브리키 마브리키예비치가 곧 그를 호송할 것이라고 알려 주었다…."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐만.\""], ["Mitya interrupted, suddenly, and impelled by uncontrollable feeling he pronounced, addressing all in the room:", "미탸가 갑자기 말을 가로막았고, 억누를 수 없는 격정에 떠밀려 방 안의 모든 사람을 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“Gentlemen, we’re all cruel, we’re all monsters, we all make men weep, and mothers, and babes at the breast, but of all, let it be settled here, now, of all I am the lowest reptile!", "\"여러분, 우리는 모두 잔인한 인간이며, 우리는 모두 괴물이오. 우리는 모두 사람들을 울리고, 어머니들을 울리고, 젖먹이 아기들을 울리지만, 그 모든 사람들 가운데서도, 여기서 지금 결판을 내자면, 그 모두 가운데 내가 가장 비천한 벌레요!"], ["I’ve sworn to amend, and every day I’ve done the same filthy things.", "나는 고치겠노라 맹세하고도 날마다 똑같은 추악한 짓을 거듭해 왔소."], ["I understand now that such men as I need a blow, a blow of destiny to catch them as with a noose, and bind them by a force from without.", "나 같은 인간에게는 한 방의 타격이, 올가미로 옭아매듯 사로잡아 외부의 힘으로 결박해 줄 운명의 타격이 필요하다는 것을 이제야 알겠소."], ["Never, never should I have risen of myself!", "나는 결코, 결코 스스로의 힘으로는 일어서지 못했을 것이오!"], ["But the thunderbolt has fallen.", "그러나 벼락이 떨어졌소."], ["I accept the torture of accusation, and my public shame, I want to suffer and by suffering I shall be purified.", "나는 고발이라는 형벌을, 그리고 만인 앞에서의 치욕을 받아들이겠소. 나는 고통받기를 원하며, 고통을 통해 정화될 것이오."], ["Perhaps I shall be purified, gentlemen?", "어쩌면 나는 정화될지도 모르오, 여러분?"], ["But listen, for the last time, I am not guilty of my father’s blood.", "그러나 들어 주시오, 마지막으로 하는 말이오, 나는 아버지의 피에 대해서는 죄가 없소."], ["I accept my punishment, not because I killed him, but because I meant to kill him, and perhaps I really might have killed him.", "나는 내 벌을 받아들이지만, 그것은 내가 아버지를 죽였기 때문이 아니라, 죽이려는 마음을 품었기 때문이며, 어쩌면 정말로 죽였을지도 모르기 때문이오."], ["Still I mean to fight it out with you.", "그래도 나는 여러분과 끝까지 싸울 작정이오."], ["I warn you of that.", "그것을 미리 일러두겠소."], ["I’ll fight it out with you to the end, and then God will decide.", "나는 여러분과 끝까지 싸울 것이며, 그런 다음에는 하느님께서 판결하실 것이오."], ["Good-by, gentlemen, don’t be vexed with me for having shouted at you during the examination.", "안녕히 계시오, 여러분, 심문하는 동안 여러분에게 고함을 질렀다고 노여워하지 마시오."], ["Oh, I was still such a fool then....", "아, 그때만 해도 나는 여전히 그토록 어리석은 바보였소…."], ["In another minute I shall be a prisoner, but now, for the last time, as a free man, Dmitri Karamazov offers you his hand.", "일 분만 지나면 나는 죄수가 되겠지만, 지금은 마지막으로, 자유로운 인간으로서, 드미트리 카라마조프가 여러분에게 손을 내미는 것이오."], ["Saying good-by to you, I say it to all men.”", "여러분에게 작별을 고하면서, 나는 그것을 모든 사람에게 고하는 것이오.\""]], [["His voice quivered and he stretched out his hand, but Nikolay Parfenovitch, who happened to stand nearest to him, with a sudden, almost nervous movement, hid his hands behind his back.", "그의 목소리는 떨렸고 그는 손을 내밀었으나, 마침 그에게 가장 가까이 서 있던 니콜라이 파르페노비치가 별안간, 거의 신경질적인 동작으로 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추었다."], ["Mitya instantly noticed this, and started.", "미탸는 그것을 즉각 알아채고 흠칫했다."], ["He let his outstretched hand fall at once.", "그는 내밀었던 손을 그 자리에서 떨구었다."]], [["“The preliminary inquiry is not yet over,”", "\"예심은 아직 끝나지 않았습니다.\""], ["Nikolay Parfenovitch faltered, somewhat embarrassed.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치가 다소 멋쩍어하며 더듬더듬 말했다."], ["“We will continue it in the town, and I, for my part, of course, am ready to wish you all success ...", "\"읍내에서 그것을 계속할 것이며, 또 나로서는 물론 당신에게 모든 행운을… 당신의 변호에…"], ["in your defense....", "빌어 마지않습니다…."], ["As a matter of fact, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, I’ve always been disposed to regard you as, so to speak, more unfortunate than guilty.", "사실인즉, 드미트리 표도로비치, 나는 늘 당신을 이를테면 죄인이라기보다는 불행한 사람으로 여기는 편이었습니다."], ["All of us here, if I may make bold to speak for all, we are all ready to recognize that you are, at bottom, a young man of honor, but, alas, one who has been carried away by certain passions to a somewhat excessive degree....”", "여기 있는 우리 모두가, 내가 감히 모두를 대신해 말해도 좋다면, 우리는 모두 당신이 본바탕은 명예를 아는 청년이지만, 아아, 어떤 격정에 다소 지나치게 휩쓸려 버린 사람임을 기꺼이 인정할 마음이 있습니다….\""]], [["Nikolay Parfenovitch’s little figure was positively majestic by the time he had finished speaking.", "니콜라이 파르페노비치의 자그마한 몸집은 말을 끝마칠 무렵에는 그야말로 위엄에 차 있었다."], ["It struck Mitya that in another minute this “boy” would take his arm, lead him to another corner, and renew their conversation about “girls.”", "미탸에게는 문득 이 \"소년\"이 곧 자기 팔을 붙들고 다른 구석으로 데려가, \"처녀들\"에 관한 대화를 다시 시작할 것만 같았다."], ["But many quite irrelevant and inappropriate thoughts sometimes occur even to a prisoner when he is being led out to execution.", "그러나 사형장으로 끌려나가는 죄수에게조차 더러는 영 동떨어지고 엉뚱한 생각들이 떠오르는 법이다."]], [["“Gentlemen, you are good, you are humane, may I see her to say ‘good-by’ for the last time?”", "\"여러분, 여러분은 선량하고, 여러분은 인정이 있으니, 그 여자를 만나 마지막으로 작별 인사를 해도 되겠소?\""], ["asked Mitya.", "미탸가 물었다."]], [["“Certainly, but considering ...", "\"물론입니다, 그러나 여러 가지 사정을 고려하면…"], ["in fact, now it’s impossible except in the presence of—”", "실은, 지금으로서는 다음과 같은 사람의 입회 없이는 불가능합니다만—\""]], [["“Oh, well, if it must be so, it must!”", "\"오, 그래요, 그래야만 한다면 그래야겠지요!\""]], [["Grushenka was brought in, but the farewell was brief, and of few words, and did not at all satisfy Nikolay Parfenovitch.", "그루셴카가 안으로 불려 들어왔으나, 작별은 짧았고, 말수도 적었으며, 니콜라이 파르페노비치를 전혀 만족시키지 못했다."], ["Grushenka made a deep bow to Mitya.", "그루셴카는 미탸에게 깊이 허리를 숙여 절했다."]], [["“I have told you I am yours, and I will be yours.", "\"당신에게 말했잖아요, 나는 당신의 것이고, 앞으로도 당신의 것이 되겠다고."], ["I will follow you for ever, wherever they may send you.", "나는 그들이 당신을 어디로 보내든 영원히 당신을 따라가겠어요. 안녕히 가세요."], ["Farewell; you are guiltless, though you’ve been your own undoing.”", "당신은 죄가 없어요. 비록 당신 스스로 자기 자신을 망쳐 버렸을지언정요.\""]], [["Her lips quivered, tears flowed from her eyes.", "그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Forgive me, Grusha, for my love, for ruining you, too, with my love.”", "\"용서해 줘, 그루샤, 내 사랑을, 내 사랑으로 너까지 망쳐 버린 것을.\""]], [["Mitya would have said something more, but he broke off and went out.", "미탸는 무언가 더 말하려다가 그만 입을 다물고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["He was at once surrounded by men who kept a constant watch on him.", "그는 곧장 끊임없이 자신을 감시하는 사람들에게 에워싸였다."], ["At the bottom of the steps to which he had driven up with such a dash the day before with Andrey’s three horses, two carts stood in readiness.", "어제 안드레이의 세 마리 말을 몰아 그토록 기세 좋게 들이닥쳤던 그 계단 아래에, 마차 두 대가 채비를 갖추고 서 있었다."], ["Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, a sturdy, thick-set man with a wrinkled face, was annoyed about something, some sudden irregularity.", "다부지고 떡 벌어진 체격에 주름투성이 얼굴을 한 마브리키 마브리키예비치는 무언가에, 어떤 갑작스러운 차질에 짜증이 나 있었다."], ["He was shouting angrily.", "그는 성난 목소리로 고함을 지르고 있었다."], ["He asked Mitya to get into the cart with somewhat excessive surliness.", "그는 다소 지나치게 무뚝뚝한 태도로 미탸에게 마차에 오르라고 일렀다."]], [["“When I stood him drinks in the tavern, the man had quite a different face,”", "'주막에서 내가 이 사람에게 술을 사 주었을 때는 얼굴이 영 딴판이었는데.'"], ["thought Mitya, as he got in.", "미탸는 마차에 오르며 이렇게 생각했다."], ["At the gates there was a crowd of people, peasants, women and drivers.", "대문께에는 사람들이, 농부들과 아낙네들과 마부들이 무리 지어 있었다."], ["Trifon Borissovitch came down the steps too.", "트리폰 보리소비치도 계단을 내려왔다."], ["All stared at Mitya.", "모두가 미탸를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Forgive me at parting, good people!”", "\"헤어지는 마당에, 부디 나를 용서해 주시오, 선량한 사람들이여!\""], ["Mitya shouted suddenly from the cart.", "미탸가 갑자기 마차에서 외쳤다."]], [["“Forgive us too!”", "\"우리도 용서해 주시오!\""], ["he heard two or three voices.", "두세 사람의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Good-by to you, too, Trifon Borissovitch!”", "\"당신에게도 작별을 고하오, 트리폰 보리소비치!\""]], [["But Trifon Borissovitch did not even turn round.", "그러나 트리폰 보리소비치는 돌아보지조차 않았다."], ["He was, perhaps, too busy.", "그는 어쩌면 너무 바빴는지도 모른다."], ["He, too, was shouting and fussing about something.", "그 역시 무언가 때문에 고함을 지르고 부산을 떨고 있었다."], ["It appeared that everything was not yet ready in the second cart, in which two constables were to accompany Mavriky Mavrikyevitch.", "알고 보니 마브리키 마브리키예비치를 따라갈 순경 두 사람이 탈 두 번째 마차의 채비가 아직 다 갖추어지지 않았던 것이다."], ["The peasant who had been ordered to drive the second cart was pulling on his smock, stoutly maintaining that it was not his turn to go, but Akim’s.", "두 번째 마차를 몰라는 명을 받은 농부는 작업복을 꿰입으면서, 이번엔 제 차례가 아니라 아킴의 차례라고 한사코 우겨댔다."], ["But Akim was not to be seen.", "그러나 아킴은 코빼기도 보이지 않았다."], ["They ran to look for him.", "사람들이 그를 찾으러 뛰어갔다."], ["The peasant persisted and besought them to wait.", "농부는 고집을 꺾지 않으며 제발 기다려 달라고 사정했다."]], [["“You see what our peasants are, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch.", "\"우리 농부들 꼴이 어떤지 좀 보시오, 마브리키 마브리키예비치."], ["They’ve no shame!”", "부끄러운 줄을 몰라요!\""], ["exclaimed Trifon Borissovitch.", "트리폰 보리소비치가 외쳤다."], ["“Akim gave you twenty-five copecks the day before yesterday.", "\"아킴이 그저께 당신한테 이십오 코페이카를 줬잖소."], ["You’ve drunk it all and now you cry out.", "그걸 죄다 술로 처마시고는 이제 와서 떠들어 대다니."], ["I’m simply surprised at your good-nature, with our low peasants, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, that’s all I can say.”", "우리 천한 농부들을 데리고 그렇게 너그러우신 게, 나로선 그저 놀라울 따름이오, 마브리키 마브리키예비치, 내가 할 말은 그뿐이오.\""]], [["“But what do we want a second cart for?”", "\"한데 우리한테 두 번째 마차가 무슨 소용이오?\""], ["Mitya put in.", "미탸가 끼어들었다."], ["“Let’s start with the one, Mavriky Mavrikyevitch.", "\"한 대로 출발합시다, 마브리키 마브리키예비치."], ["I won’t be unruly, I won’t run away from you, old fellow.", "나는 난동을 부리지 않을 테고, 당신한테서 달아나지도 않을 거요, 영감."], ["What do we want an escort for?”", "호송이 무슨 소용이란 말이오?\""]], [["“I’ll trouble you, sir, to learn how to speak to me if you’ve never been taught.", "\"이보시오, 미안하지만, 나한테 어떻게 말하는 건지 배운 적이 없거든 이참에 좀 배우시지."], ["I’m not ‘old fellow’ to you, and you can keep your advice for another time!”", "나는 당신한테 '영감'이 아니오, 그리고 당신 충고 따위는 다음번을 위해 고이 간직해 두시오!\""], ["Mavriky Mavrikyevitch snapped out savagely, as though glad to vent his wrath.", "마브리키 마브리키예비치는 마치 분풀이라도 하게 되어 기쁘다는 듯 사납게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["Mitya was reduced to silence.", "미탸는 입을 다물 수밖에 없었다."], ["He flushed all over.", "그는 온몸이 붉게 달아올랐다."], ["A moment later he felt suddenly very cold.", "잠시 뒤 그는 문득 몹시 한기를 느꼈다."], ["The rain had ceased, but the dull sky was still overcast with clouds, and a keen wind was blowing straight in his face.", "비는 그쳤으나 잔뜩 흐린 하늘은 여전히 구름으로 뒤덮여 있었고, 매서운 바람이 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 후려쳤다."]], [["“I’ve taken a chill,”", "'몸이 으슬으슬하군.'"], ["thought Mitya, twitching his shoulders.", "미탸는 어깨를 움찔거리며 생각했다."]], [["At last Mavriky Mavrikyevitch, too, got into the cart, sat down heavily, and, as though without noticing it, squeezed Mitya into the corner.", "마침내 마브리키 마브리키예비치도 마차에 올라 무겁게 주저앉으면서, 미처 알아채지도 못했다는 듯 미탸를 구석으로 밀어붙였다."], ["It is true that he was out of humor and greatly disliked the task that had been laid upon him.", "그가 심기가 틀어져 자기에게 떨어진 임무를 몹시 못마땅해하고 있었던 것은 사실이었다."]], [["“Good-by, Trifon Borissovitch!”", "\"잘 있으시오, 트리폰 보리소비치!\""], ["Mitya shouted again, and felt himself, that he had not called out this time from good-nature, but involuntarily, from resentment.", "미탸가 다시 외쳤고, 이번에는 자기도 그것이 선의에서 나온 외침이 아니라, 원망에서 저도 모르게 터져 나온 외침임을 느꼈다."]], [["But Trifon Borissovitch stood proudly, with both hands behind his back, and staring straight at Mitya with a stern and angry face, he made no reply.", "그러나 트리폰 보리소비치는 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌린 채 거만하게 서서, 험상궂고 성난 얼굴로 미탸를 똑바로 노려보며 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Good-by, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, good-by!”", "\"안녕히 가세요, 드미트리 표도로비치, 안녕히 가세요!\""], ["he heard all at once the voice of Kalganov, who had suddenly darted out.", "별안간 칼가노프의 목소리가 들려왔는데, 그가 불쑥 뛰어나온 것이었다."], ["Running up to the cart he held out his hand to Mitya.", "마차로 달려온 그는 미탸에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["He had no cap on.", "그는 모자도 쓰지 않은 차림이었다."]], [["Mitya had time to seize and press his hand.", "미탸는 가까스로 그 손을 붙잡아 꼭 쥘 수 있었다."]], [["“Good-by, dear fellow!", "\"잘 있게, 친애하는 친구!"], ["I shan’t forget your generosity,”", "자네의 너그러움을 잊지 않겠네.\""], ["he cried warmly.", "그가 따뜻하게 외쳤다."]], [["But the cart moved and their hands parted.", "그러나 마차가 움직이며 두 사람의 손은 떨어졌다."], ["The bell began ringing and Mitya was driven off.", "방울 소리가 울리기 시작했고 미탸는 실려 갔다."]], [["Kalganov ran back, sat down in a corner, bent his head, hid his face in his hands, and burst out crying.", "칼가노프는 도로 뛰어가 한구석에 주저앉더니, 고개를 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻은 채 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["For a long while he sat like that, crying as though he were a little boy instead of a young man of twenty.", "그는 오랫동안 그렇게 앉아, 스무 살 청년이 아니라 마치 어린 소년이기라도 한 양 울었다."], ["Oh, he believed almost without doubt in Mitya’s guilt.", "아, 그는 미탸의 유죄를 거의 의심 없이 믿고 있었다."]], [["“What are these people?", "\"이 사람들은 대체 뭐란 말인가?"], ["What can men be after this?”", "이런 일이 있고서야 인간이란 대체 무엇일 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["he exclaimed incoherently, in bitter despondency, almost despair.", "그는 쓰디쓴 낙담 속에서, 거의 절망에 빠져 두서없이 외쳐 댔다."], ["At that moment he had no desire to live.", "그 순간 그에게는 살고 싶은 마음조차 없었다."]], [["“Is it worth it? Is it worth it?”", "\"이게 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가?"], ["exclaimed the boy in his grief.", "이게 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가?\" 슬픔에 잠긴 청년이 외쳤다."]], [["# PART IV Book X. The Boys Chapter I. Kolya Krassotkin", "# PART IV Book X. The Boys Chapter I. Kolya Krassotkin"]], [["It was the beginning of November.", "11월 초였다."], ["There had been a hard frost, eleven degrees Réaumur, without snow, but a little dry snow had fallen on the frozen ground during the night, and a keen dry wind was lifting and blowing it along the dreary streets of our town, especially about the market-place.", "매서운 추위가 몰아쳐 레오뮈르* 영하 11도였으나 눈은 내리지 않았는데, 밤사이 얼어붙은 땅 위에 메마른 눈이 조금 흩날려 쌓였고, 날카롭고 건조한 바람이 그 눈을 들어 올려 우리 고을의 쓸쓸한 거리를 따라, 특히 장터 언저리로 휘몰아 가고 있었다."], ["It was a dull morning, but the snow had ceased.", "흐릿한 아침이었으나 눈은 이미 그쳐 있었다."]], [["Not far from the market-place, close to Plotnikov’s shop, there stood a small house, very clean both without and within.", "* 레오뮈르: 물의 어는점을 0도, 끓는점을 80도로 정한 옛 온도 단위. 영하 11도는 섭씨로 약 영하 14도에 해당한다."], ["It belonged to Madame Krassotkin, the widow of a former provincial secretary, who had been dead for fourteen years.", "장터에서 그리 멀지 않은, 플로트니코프의 가게 가까운 곳에 작은 집 한 채가 서 있었는데, 안팎이 모두 무척 정갈했다."], ["His widow, still a nice-looking woman of thirty-two, was living in her neat little house on her private means.", "그 집은 크라소트킨 부인의 소유로, 그녀는 십사 년 전에 세상을 떠난 전직 도청 서기의 미망인이었다."], ["She lived in respectable seclusion; she was of a soft but fairly cheerful disposition.", "그 미망인은 서른두 살로 아직 제법 고운 여인이었으며, 단정한 그 작은 집에서 자기 재산으로 살아가고 있었다."], ["She was about eighteen at the time of her husband’s death; she had been married only a year and had just borne him a son.", "그녀는 점잖게 세상과 거리를 두고 살았으니, 성품은 부드러우면서도 제법 명랑한 편이었다. 남편이 죽었을 때 그녀는 열여덟 살가량이었고, 결혼한 지 겨우 일 년 만에 아들 하나를 막 낳은 참이었다."], ["From the day of his death she had devoted herself heart and soul to the bringing up of her precious treasure, her boy Kolya.", "남편이 죽은 그날부터 그녀는 자신의 소중한 보물, 아들 콜랴를 키우는 일에 온 마음과 영혼을 바쳤다."], ["Though she had loved him passionately those fourteen years, he had caused her far more suffering than happiness.", "그 십사 년 동안 그녀는 아들을 열렬히 사랑했으나, 아들은 그녀에게 행복보다는 고통을 훨씬 더 많이 안겨 주었다."], ["She had been trembling and fainting with terror almost every day, afraid he would fall ill, would catch cold, do something naughty, climb on a chair and fall off it, and so on and so on.", "그녀는 거의 날마다 공포에 떨며 졸도할 지경이었으니, 아들이 병이 들지나 않을까, 감기에 걸리지나 않을까, 무슨 못된 짓을 하지나 않을까, 의자에 올라갔다가 떨어지지나 않을까, 그리고 이러쿵저러쿵 끊임없이 마음을 졸였다."], ["When Kolya began going to school, the mother devoted herself to studying all the sciences with him so as to help him, and go through his lessons with him.", "콜랴가 학교에 다니기 시작하자, 어머니는 아들을 돕고 함께 공부를 따라가려고 온갖 학문을 그와 더불어 익히는 일에 매달렸다."], ["She hastened to make the acquaintance of the teachers and their wives, even made up to Kolya’s schoolfellows, and fawned upon them in the hope of thus saving Kolya from being teased, laughed at, or beaten by them.", "그녀는 부랴부랴 교사들과 그 아내들과 친분을 텄고, 콜랴의 학교 친구들에게까지 비위를 맞추며 아첨하였으니, 그렇게 함으로써 콜랴가 그들에게 놀림받거나 비웃음당하거나 얻어맞는 일을 막아 보려는 심산이었다."], ["She went so far that the boys actually began to mock at him on her account and taunt him with being a “mother’s darling.”", "그녀가 어찌나 그러했던지, 급기야 아이들은 그녀 때문에 콜랴를 조롱하기 시작하여 \"엄마의 귀염둥이\"라고 놀려 대기에 이르렀다."]], [["But the boy could take his own part. He was a resolute boy, “tremendously strong,”", "그러나 그 소년은 제 앞가림을 할 줄 아는 아이였다."], ["as was rumored in his class, and soon proved to be the fact; he was agile, strong-willed, and of an audacious and enterprising temper.", "그는 의지가 굳은 소년이었고, 그가 속한 반에서 소문이 났듯 \"엄청나게 힘이 센\" 아이였는데, 그 소문이 곧 사실로 드러났으니, 그는 날렵하고 의지가 강하며 대담하고 진취적인 기질을 지니고 있었다."], ["He was good at lessons, and there was a rumor in the school that he could beat the teacher, Dardanelov, at arithmetic and universal history.", "공부도 잘하여, 학교에는 그가 산수와 만국사에서 교사 다르다넬로프를 이길 수 있다는 소문이 돌았다."], ["Though he looked down upon every one, he was a good comrade and not supercilious.", "그는 누구든 깔보기는 했어도 좋은 동무였고 거만하지는 않았다."], ["He accepted his schoolfellows’ respect as his due, but was friendly with them.", "학교 친구들의 존경을 마땅한 것으로 받아들이면서도 그들과는 다정하게 지냈다."], ["Above all, he knew where to draw the line.", "무엇보다도 그는 어디서 선을 그어야 하는지를 알았다."], ["He could restrain himself on occasion, and in his relations with the teachers he never overstepped that last mystic limit beyond which a prank becomes an unpardonable breach of discipline.", "필요한 때에는 스스로를 억제할 줄 알았고, 교사들과의 관계에서도 장난이 용서받지 못할 규율 위반으로 넘어가는 그 마지막 신비로운 한계선만은 결코 넘지 않았다."], ["But he was as fond of mischief on every possible occasion as the smallest boy in the school, and not so much for the sake of mischief as for creating a sensation, inventing something, something effective and conspicuous.", "그러면서도 그는 기회만 있으면 학교에서 가장 어린 아이 못지않게 장난을 좋아했는데, 장난 자체를 위해서라기보다 무언가 화제를 불러일으키고, 무언가를 꾸며 내고, 무언가 효과적이고 눈에 띄는 일을 벌이고 싶어서였다."], ["He was extremely vain.", "그는 허영심이 대단히 강했다."], ["He knew how to make even his mother give way to him; he was almost despotic in his control of her.", "그는 제 어머니마저 자기에게 굴복하게 만들 줄 알았으니, 어머니를 거의 폭군처럼 휘둘렀다."], ["She gave way to him, oh, she had given way to him for years.", "어머니는 그에게 양보했고, 아, 그녀는 몇 해 동안 줄곧 그에게 양보해 온 것이었다."], ["The one thought unendurable to her was that her boy had no great love for her. She was always fancying that Kolya was “unfeeling”", "그녀가 도저히 견딜 수 없었던 단 한 가지 생각은, 제 아들이 자기를 그리 사랑하지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["to her, and at times, dissolving into hysterical tears, she used to reproach him with his coldness.", "그녀는 늘 콜랴가 자기에게 \"무정하다\"고 여겼고, 때때로 히스테릭한 눈물을 쏟으며 그의 냉담함을 나무라곤 했다."], ["The boy disliked this, and the more demonstrations of feeling were demanded of him the more he seemed intentionally to avoid them.", "소년은 이것을 싫어했고, 감정의 표현을 요구받으면 받을수록 일부러 그것을 피하려는 듯 보였다."], ["Yet it was not intentional on his part but instinctive—it was his character.", "그러나 그것은 그의 의도에서 나온 것이 아니라 본능에서 나온 것이었으니—그것이 그의 성격이었다."], ["His mother was mistaken; he was very fond of her.", "어머니는 잘못 알고 있었다. 그는 어머니를 무척 사랑했다."], ["He only disliked “sheepish sentimentality,” as he expressed it in his schoolboy language.", "다만 그가 자기네 학생들 말투로 표현하듯 \"양처럼 칠칠치 못한 감상벽\"을 싫어할 뿐이었다."]], [["There was a bookcase in the house containing a few books that had been his father’s.", "집에는 책장이 하나 있었는데, 그 안에는 그의 아버지가 남긴 책 몇 권이 들어 있었다."], ["Kolya was fond of reading, and had read several of them by himself.", "콜랴는 책 읽기를 좋아하여 그 가운데 몇 권을 혼자 읽어 두었다."], ["His mother did not mind that and only wondered sometimes at seeing the boy stand for hours by the bookcase poring over a book instead of going to play.", "어머니는 그것을 개의치 않았고, 다만 이따금 아들이 놀러 나가는 대신 몇 시간씩 책장 곁에 서서 책에 골몰해 있는 모습을 보고 의아해할 뿐이었다."], ["And in that way Kolya read some things unsuitable for his age.", "그런 식으로 콜랴는 제 나이에 어울리지 않는 책들을 더러 읽기도 했다."]], [["Though the boy, as a rule, knew where to draw the line in his mischief, he had of late begun to play pranks that caused his mother serious alarm.", "소년은 대체로 장난에서 어디까지가 한계인지를 알고 있었으나, 근래에 들어 어머니를 진정으로 놀라게 하는 장난을 치기 시작했다."], ["It is true there was nothing vicious in what he did, but a wild mad recklessness.", "사실 그가 한 짓에 악의가 깃든 것은 없었으나, 거기에는 거칠고 미친 듯한 무모함이 있었다."]], [["It happened that July, during the summer holidays, that the mother and son went to another district, forty-five miles away, to spend a week with a distant relation, whose husband was an official at the railway station (the very station, the nearest one to our town, from which a month later Ivan Fyodorovitch Karamazov set off for Moscow).", "그해 칠월, 여름방학 동안의 일이었다. 어머니와 아들은 사십오 마일 떨어진 다른 군으로 가서, 한 먼 친척네 집에서 일주일을 보냈는데, 그 친척의 남편은 철도역의 관리였다(바로 그 역, 우리 고을에서 가장 가까운 그 역으로, 한 달 뒤에 이반 표도로비치 카라마조프가 그곳에서 모스크바로 떠나게 될 그 역이었다)."], ["There Kolya began by carefully investigating every detail connected with the railways, knowing that he could impress his schoolfellows when he got home with his newly acquired knowledge.", "그곳에서 콜랴는 우선 철도와 관련된 온갖 세부 사항을 꼼꼼히 조사하는 일부터 시작했으니, 새로 익힌 지식으로 집에 돌아가 학교 친구들의 코를 납작하게 해 줄 수 있으리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["But there happened to be some other boys in the place with whom he soon made friends.", "그런데 마침 그곳에는 다른 소년들 몇이 있어서 그는 곧 그들과 친해졌다."], ["Some of them were living at the station, others in the neighborhood; there were six or seven of them, all between twelve and fifteen, and two of them came from our town.", "그 가운데 더러는 역에서 살았고, 더러는 인근에서 살았는데, 모두 여섯이나 일곱쯤 되었고 죄다 열두 살에서 열다섯 살 사이였으며, 그중 둘은 우리 고을 출신이었다."], ["The boys played together, and on the fourth or fifth day of Kolya’s stay at the station, a mad bet was made by the foolish boys.", "소년들은 함께 어울려 놀았는데, 콜랴가 역에 머문 지 나흘인가 닷새째 되던 날, 그 어리석은 소년들 사이에서 미친 내기 하나가 벌어졌다."], ["Kolya, who was almost the youngest of the party and rather looked down upon by the others in consequence, was moved by vanity or by reckless bravado to bet them two roubles that he would lie down between the rails at night when the eleven o’clock train was due, and would lie there without moving while the train rolled over him at full speed.", "콜랴는 그 무리에서 거의 가장 어린 축에 들었고 그런 까닭에 다른 아이들에게 다소 무시당했는데, 허영심에서인지 무모한 객기에서인지 그는 두 루블을 걸고, 밤에 열한 시 기차가 들어올 시각에 철로 사이에 누워, 기차가 전속력으로 제 몸 위를 지나가는 동안 꼼짝 않고 그대로 누워 있겠노라고 내기를 걸었다."], ["It is true they made a preliminary investigation, from which it appeared that it was possible to lie so flat between the rails that the train could pass over without touching, but to lie there was no joke!", "사실 그들은 미리 조사를 해 보았는데, 철로 사이에 몸을 바싹 붙여 누우면 기차가 몸에 닿지 않고 지나갈 수 있다는 것이 밝혀지기는 했으나, 거기에 가만히 누워 있다는 것은 결코 우스운 일이 아니었다!"], ["Kolya maintained stoutly that he would.", "콜랴는 자기는 해내겠다고 단단히 우겼다."], ["At first they laughed at him, called him a little liar, a braggart, but that only egged him on.", "처음에 아이들은 그를 비웃으며 거짓말쟁이니 허풍선이니 하고 불렀으나, 그것은 오히려 그를 부추길 뿐이었다."], ["What piqued him most was that these boys of fifteen turned up their noses at him too superciliously, and were at first disposed to treat him as “a small boy,” not fit to associate with them, and that was an unendurable insult.", "무엇보다 그의 자존심을 건드린 것은, 그 열다섯 살짜리들이 자기를 너무도 거만하게 깔보며 처음에는 자기들과 어울릴 자격이 없는 \"꼬마\"로 취급하려 든 점이었으니, 그것은 도저히 참을 수 없는 모욕이었다."]], [["And so it was resolved to go in the evening, half a mile from the station, so that the train might have time to get up full speed after leaving the station.", "그리하여 저녁에 역에서 반 마일 떨어진 곳으로 가기로 정해졌으니, 그래야 기차가 역을 떠난 뒤 충분히 속력을 낼 여유가 있기 때문이었다."], ["The boys assembled.", "소년들이 모였다."], ["It was a pitch-dark night without a moon.", "달도 없는 칠흑같이 어두운 밤이었다."], ["At the time fixed, Kolya lay down between the rails.", "정해진 시각에 콜랴는 철로 사이에 드러누웠다."], ["The five others who had taken the bet waited among the bushes below the embankment, their hearts beating with suspense, which was followed by alarm and remorse.", "내기를 건 나머지 다섯은 둑 아래 덤불 속에서 기다렸는데, 가슴은 조마조마한 긴장으로 두근거렸고, 이어 그 긴장은 두려움과 후회로 바뀌었다."], ["At last they heard in the distance the rumble of the train leaving the station.", "마침내 그들은 멀리서 기차가 역을 떠나는 우르릉 소리를 들었다."], ["Two red lights gleamed out of the darkness; the monster roared as it approached.", "어둠 속에서 빨간 불빛 두 개가 번득였고, 괴물이 다가오며 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“Run, run away from the rails,”", "\"달아나, 철로에서 비켜!\""], ["the boys cried to Kolya from the bushes, breathless with terror.", "소년들이 덤불 속에서 공포에 숨이 막힌 채 콜랴에게 소리쳤다."], ["But it was too late: the train darted up and flew past.", "그러나 이미 늦었으니, 기차가 쏜살같이 달려와 휙 지나가 버렸다."], ["The boys rushed to Kolya.", "소년들은 콜랴에게로 달려갔다."], ["He lay without moving.", "그는 꼼짝 않고 누워 있었다."], ["They began pulling at him, lifting him up.", "그들은 그를 잡아당기며 일으켜 세우려 했다."], ["He suddenly got up and walked away without a word.", "그는 별안간 일어나더니 한마디 말도 없이 걸어가 버렸다."], ["Then he explained that he had lain there as though he were insensible to frighten them, but the fact was that he really had lost consciousness, as he confessed long after to his mother.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기들을 겁주려고 의식을 잃은 척하고 누워 있었던 것이라고 둘러댔으나, 사실인즉 그는 정말로 의식을 잃었던 것이니, 이는 그가 한참 뒤에 어머니에게 고백한 대로였다."], ["In this way his reputation as “a desperate character,” was established for ever.", "이런 식으로 \"겁 없는 인물\"이라는 그의 명성이 영영 확립되었다."], ["He returned home to the station as white as a sheet.", "그는 종잇장처럼 새하얗게 질려서 역의 친척집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Next day he had a slight attack of nervous fever, but he was in high spirits and well pleased with himself.", "이튿날 그는 가벼운 신경성 열병을 앓았으나, 기분만은 한껏 들떠 있었고 제 자신이 무척 흐뭇했다."], ["The incident did not become known at once, but when they came back to the town it penetrated to the school and even reached the ears of the masters.", "그 사건은 곧바로 알려지지는 않았으나, 그들이 고을로 돌아왔을 때 학교에까지 스며들어 마침내 교사들 귀에까지 들어가고 말았다."], ["But then Kolya’s mother hastened to entreat the masters on her boy’s behalf, and in the end Dardanelov, a respected and influential teacher, exerted himself in his favor, and the affair was ignored.", "그러나 그때 콜랴의 어머니가 부랴부랴 교사들에게 제 아들을 위해 사정사정하며 매달렸고, 끝내는 존경받고 영향력 있는 교사인 다르다넬로프가 그를 위해 힘써 주어, 그 일은 그냥 묻혀 버렸다."]], [["Dardanelov was a middle-aged bachelor, who had been passionately in love with Madame Krassotkin for many years past, and had once already, about a year previously, ventured, trembling with fear and the delicacy of his sentiments, to offer her most respectfully his hand in marriage.", "다르다넬로프는 중년의 독신 남자로, 여러 해 전부터 크라소트킨 부인을 열렬히 사모해 왔으며, 이미 한 번, 일 년쯤 전에 두려움과 자기 감정의 섬세함에 떨면서 그녀에게 더없이 정중하게 청혼을 감행한 적이 있었다."], ["But she refused him resolutely, feeling that to accept him would be an act of treachery to her son, though Dardanelov had, to judge from certain mysterious symptoms, reason for believing that he was not an object of aversion to the charming but too chaste and tender-hearted widow.", "그러나 그녀는 단호히 그를 거절했으니, 그를 받아들이는 것은 제 아들에 대한 배신이라고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 하기야 다르다넬로프는, 어떤 알 수 없는 징후들로 미루어 보건대, 자기가 그 매력적이면서도 너무나 정숙하고 정 많은 미망인에게 혐오의 대상은 아니라고 믿을 만한 근거를 가지고 있었다."], ["Kolya’s mad prank seemed to have broken the ice, and Dardanelov was rewarded for his intercession by a suggestion of hope.", "콜랴의 미친 장난이 그 얼음을 깨뜨린 듯하였으니, 다르다넬로프는 자신이 중재해 준 보답으로 한 가닥 희망의 암시를 얻었다."], ["The suggestion, it is true, was a faint one, but then Dardanelov was such a paragon of purity and delicacy that it was enough for the time being to make him perfectly happy.", "사실 그 암시는 희미한 것이었으나, 다르다넬로프는 워낙 순수함과 섬세함의 본보기 같은 사람이었던 터라, 당장은 그것만으로도 더없이 행복해지기에 충분했다."], ["He was fond of the boy, though he would have felt it beneath him to try and win him over, and was severe and strict with him in class.", "그는 그 소년을 좋아했으나, 그를 구슬려 제 편으로 끌어들이려 드는 것은 자신의 격에 어긋나는 일이라 여겼고, 수업 시간에는 그에게 엄격하고 단호하게 대했다."], ["Kolya, too, kept him at a respectful distance.", "콜랴 역시 그를 정중한 거리에 두었다."], ["He learned his lessons perfectly; he was second in his class, was reserved with Dardanelov, and the whole class firmly believed that Kolya was so good at universal history that he could “beat”", "그는 수업을 완벽하게 익혀 반에서 이등이었고, 다르다넬로프에게는 말수가 적었는데, 반 전체가 굳게 믿기를 콜랴는 만국사에 어찌나 능한지 다르다넬로프조차 \"이길\""], ["even Dardanelov.", "수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["Kolya did indeed ask him the question, “Who founded Troy?” to which Dardanelov had made a very vague reply, referring to the movements and migrations of races, to the remoteness of the period, to the mythical legends.", "콜랴는 실제로 그에게 \"트로이를 세운 자가 누구냐?\"고 물은 적이 있었는데, 다르다넬로프는 이에 매우 막연한 대답을 내놓아, 민족들의 이동과 이주, 그 시대의 아득함, 신화적 전설 따위를 들먹였다."], ["But the question, “Who had founded Troy?” that is, what individuals, he could not answer, and even for some reason regarded the question as idle and frivolous.", "그러나 \"트로이를 세운 자가 누구냐?\", 즉 어떤 개인들이 세웠느냐 하는 물음에는 그가 대답하지 못했고, 게다가 무슨 까닭에선지 그 질문을 한가롭고 시시한 것으로 여겼다."], ["But the boys remained convinced that Dardanelov did not know who founded Troy.", "그러나 소년들은 다르다넬로프가 트로이를 세운 자가 누구인지 모른다고 굳게 믿게 되었다."], ["Kolya had read of the founders of Troy in Smaragdov, whose history was among the books in his father’s bookcase.", "콜랴는 트로이를 세운 자들에 관한 이야기를 스마라그도프에서 읽었는데, 그 역사책은 그의 아버지 책장에 꽂혀 있던 책들 가운데 하나였다."], ["In the end all the boys became interested in the question, who it was that had founded Troy, but Krassotkin would not tell his secret, and his reputation for knowledge remained unshaken.", "결국 모든 소년들이 트로이를 세운 자가 대체 누구냐 하는 문제에 관심을 갖게 되었으나, 크라소트킨은 제 비밀을 일러 주지 않았고, 그리하여 그의 박식하다는 명성은 흔들림 없이 유지되었다."]], [["After the incident on the railway a certain change came over Kolya’s attitude to his mother.", "철도 사건이 있은 뒤로 어머니에 대한 콜랴의 태도에 어떤 변화가 일어났다."], ["When Anna Fyodorovna (Madame Krassotkin) heard of her son’s exploit, she almost went out of her mind with horror.", "아나 표도로브나(크라소트킨 부인)는 아들의 그 무용담을 전해 들었을 때 공포로 거의 정신이 나갈 뻔했다."], ["She had such terrible attacks of hysterics, lasting with intervals for several days, that Kolya, seriously alarmed at last, promised on his honor that such pranks should never be repeated.", "그녀는 며칠 동안 간헐적으로 이어지는 끔찍한 히스테리 발작을 일으켰고, 마침내 콜랴는 정말로 겁을 집어먹고서 다시는 그런 장난을 되풀이하지 않겠다고 명예를 걸고 약속했다."], ["He swore on his knees before the holy image, and swore by the memory of his father, at Madame Krassotkin’s instance, and the “manly”", "크라소트킨 부인의 요구에 따라 그는 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 맹세하였으며 아버지의 영전에 두고도 맹세하였는데, 그 \"사내다운\""], ["Kolya burst into tears like a boy of six.", "콜랴가 여섯 살배기 어린아이처럼 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["And all that day the mother and son were constantly rushing into each other’s arms sobbing.", "그날 하루 종일 어머니와 아들은 끊임없이 서로의 품에 뛰어들어 흐느꼈다."], ["Next day Kolya woke up as “unfeeling”", "이튿날 콜랴는 전과 다름없이 \"무정한\""], ["as before, but he had become more silent, more modest, sterner, and more thoughtful.", "모습으로 잠에서 깨었으나, 한결 말수가 적어지고 한결 얌전해졌으며, 한결 엄격하고 한결 생각이 깊어져 있었다."]], [["Six weeks later, it is true, he got into another scrape, which even brought his name to the ears of our Justice of the Peace, but it was a scrape of quite another kind, amusing, foolish, and he did not, as it turned out, take the leading part in it, but was only implicated in it. But of this later.", "사실 그로부터 여섯 주 뒤에 그는 또 다른 말썽을 일으켜, 그 이름이 우리 고을 치안 판사의 귀에까지 들어가기에 이르렀으나, 그것은 전혀 다른 종류의 말썽으로, 우스꽝스럽고 어리석은 것이었으며, 알고 보니 그가 그 일에서 주도적인 역할을 한 것이 아니라 그저 거기에 연루되었을 뿐이었다."], ["His mother still fretted and trembled, but the more uneasy she became, the greater were the hopes of Dardanelov.", "그러나 이에 관해서는 나중에 이야기하기로 한다. 어머니는 여전히 마음을 졸이며 떨었으나, 그녀가 불안해할수록 다르다넬로프의 희망은 그만큼 더 커져 갔다."], ["It must be noted that Kolya understood and divined what was in Dardanelov’s heart and, of course, despised him profoundly for his “feelings”; he had in the past been so tactless as to show this contempt before his mother, hinting vaguely that he knew what Dardanelov was after.", "짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 콜랴가 다르다넬로프의 마음속에 무엇이 들어 있는지를 알아채고 헤아렸으며, 물론 그의 그 \"감정\" 때문에 그를 속으로 깊이 경멸했다는 점이다."], ["But from the time of the railway incident his behavior in this respect also was changed; he did not allow himself the remotest allusion to the subject and began to speak more respectfully of Dardanelov before his mother, which the sensitive woman at once appreciated with boundless gratitude.", "그는 전에 어머니 앞에서 그 경멸을 드러낼 만큼 눈치 없이 굴어, 다르다넬로프가 무엇을 노리는지 자기가 안다고 어렴풋이 내비치기까지 했었다. 그러나 철도 사건 이후로는 이 점에서도 그의 태도가 바뀌어, 그는 그 일에 대해 아주 먼 암시조차 입에 담지 않게 되었고, 어머니 앞에서 다르다넬로프를 더욱 정중하게 말하기 시작하니, 그 예민한 여인은 곧장 그것을 한없는 감사의 마음으로 알아차렸다."], ["But at the slightest mention of Dardanelov by a visitor in Kolya’s presence, she would flush as pink as a rose.", "그러나 콜랴가 있는 자리에서 손님이 다르다넬로프를 조금이라도 입에 올리면 그녀는 장미처럼 발갛게 얼굴을 붉히곤 했다."], ["At such moments Kolya would either stare out of the window scowling, or would investigate the state of his boots, or would shout angrily for “Perezvon,” the big, shaggy, mangy dog, which he had picked up a month before, brought home, and kept for some reason secretly indoors, not showing him to any of his schoolfellows.", "그런 순간이면 콜랴는 인상을 찌푸리며 창밖을 노려보거나, 제 장화 상태를 살피거나, 아니면 \"페레즈본\"을 성난 목소리로 불러 대곤 했는데, 그것은 그가 한 달 전에 주워다 집으로 데려온 크고 털북숭이에 옴이 오른 개로, 무슨 까닭에선지 집 안에 몰래 가두어 두고 학교 친구들 누구에게도 보여 주지 않았다."], ["He bullied him frightfully, teaching him all sorts of tricks, so that the poor dog howled for him whenever he was absent at school, and when he came in, whined with delight, rushed about as if he were crazy, begged, lay down on the ground pretending to be dead, and so on; in fact, showed all the tricks he had taught him, not at the word of command, but simply from the zeal of his excited and grateful heart.", "그는 그 개를 끔찍하게 들볶으며 온갖 재주를 가르쳤는데, 그래서 가엾은 개는 그가 학교에 가서 자리에 없으면 그를 그리워하며 울부짖었고, 그가 들어오면 기뻐 낑낑대며 미친 듯이 이리저리 뛰어다니고, 앞발을 들어 빌고, 땅에 누워 죽은 시늉을 하는 따위, 요컨대 그가 가르친 온갖 재주를 다 부려 보였는데, 그것은 명령을 받아서가 아니라 그저 흥분하고 고마워하는 제 마음의 열정에서 우러나온 것이었다."]], [["I have forgotten, by the way, to mention that Kolya Krassotkin was the boy stabbed with a penknife by the boy already known to the reader as the son of Captain Snegiryov.", "그런데 말이 나온 김에 깜빡 잊고 빠뜨린 것이 있으니, 콜랴 크라소트킨이 바로, 독자들이 이미 스네기료프 대위의 아들로 알고 있는 그 소년에게 주머니칼로 찔린 그 소년이라는 점이다."], ["Ilusha had been defending his father when the schoolboys jeered at him, shouting the nickname “wisp of tow.”", "일류샤는 학교 아이들이 자기 아버지를 두고 \"수세미 같은 머리\"라는 별명을 외쳐 대며 조롱했을 때 아버지를 두둔하다가 그리한 것이었다."]], [["# Chapter II. Children", "# Chapter II. Children"]], [["And so on that frosty, snowy, and windy day in November, Kolya Krassotkin was sitting at home.", "그리하여 그 살을 에는 듯 춥고 눈보라 휘몰아치는 십일월의 어느 날, 콜랴 크라소트킨은 집에 앉아 있었다."], ["It was Sunday and there was no school.", "그날은 일요일이라 학교가 없었다."], ["It had just struck eleven, and he particularly wanted to go out “on very urgent business,” but he was left alone in charge of the house, for it so happened that all its elder inmates were absent owing to a sudden and singular event.", "마침 열한 시를 친 참이었고, 그는 무슨 \"아주 긴급한 용무\"로 어딘가에 나가고 싶어 안달이었으나, 어쩌다 보니 집안의 손위 식구들이 갑작스럽고 별난 사건 탓에 모두 자리를 비워 그 혼자 집을 지키는 신세가 되고 말았다."], ["Madame Krassotkin had let two little rooms, separated from the rest of the house by a passage, to a doctor’s wife with her two small children.", "크라소트킨 부인은 복도 하나를 사이에 두고 나머지 집과 떨어진 작은 방 두 칸을, 어린아이 둘을 둔 어느 의사의 아내에게 세놓고 있었다."], ["This lady was the same age as Anna Fyodorovna, and a great friend of hers.", "이 부인은 안나 표도로브나와 동갑이었고, 그녀와 막역한 사이였다."], ["Her husband, the doctor, had taken his departure twelve months before, going first to Orenburg and then to Tashkend, and for the last six months she had not heard a word from him.", "의사인 그녀의 남편은 일 년 전에 떠나 처음에는 오렌부르크로, 다음에는 타슈켄트로 가더니, 지난 반년 동안 그녀에게는 그에게서 한마디 소식도 없었다."], ["Had it not been for her friendship with Madame Krassotkin, which was some consolation to the forsaken lady, she would certainly have completely dissolved away in tears.", "버림받은 이 부인에게 그나마 얼마간 위안이 되어 준 크라소트킨 부인과의 우정이 아니었던들, 그녀는 십중팔구 눈물에 녹아 흔적도 없이 사라지고 말았을 것이다."], ["And now, to add to her misfortunes, Katerina, her only servant, was suddenly moved the evening before to announce, to her mistress’s amazement, that she proposed to bring a child into the world before morning.", "그런데 이제 그녀의 불행에 한술 더 떠, 그녀의 단 하나뿐인 하녀 카테리나가 바로 전날 저녁 느닷없이 마음이 동하여, 안주인이 깜짝 놀라게도 동트기 전에 아이 하나를 세상에 내놓을 작정이라고 알리고 나섰다."], ["It seemed almost miraculous to every one that no one had noticed the probability of it before.", "미리 그럴 낌새를 아무도 알아채지 못했다는 것이 누구에게나 거의 기적처럼 여겨졌다."], ["The astounded doctor’s wife decided to move Katerina while there was still time to an establishment in the town kept by a midwife for such emergencies.", "어안이 벙벙해진 의사의 아내는 아직 시간이 있을 때 카테리나를 읍내에서 그런 급한 일을 맡아 주는 산파의 집으로 옮기기로 마음먹었다."], ["As she set great store by her servant, she promptly carried out this plan and remained there looking after her.", "그녀는 하녀를 무척 소중히 여겼기에 곧장 이 계획을 실행에 옮겼고, 그곳에 남아 그녀를 돌보았다."], ["By the morning all Madame Krassotkin’s friendly sympathy and energy were called upon to render assistance and appeal to some one for help in the case.", "아침이 되자 이 일에 거들고 누군가에게 도움을 청하느라 크라소트킨 부인의 정다운 동정과 기력이 죄다 동원되기에 이르렀다."]], [["So both the ladies were absent from home, the Krassotkins’ servant, Agafya, had gone out to the market, and Kolya was thus left for a time to protect and look after “the kids,” that is, the son and daughter of the doctor’s wife, who were left alone.", "그리하여 두 부인은 모두 집을 비웠고, 크라소트킨 집안의 하녀 아가피야는 장을 보러 나간 터라, 콜랴는 잠시 \"꼬맹이들\", 곧 둘만 덩그러니 남겨진 의사 아내의 아들과 딸을 지키고 돌보는 처지가 된 것이었다."], ["Kolya was not afraid of taking care of the house, besides he had Perezvon, who had been told to lie flat, without moving, under the bench in the hall.", "콜랴는 집을 지키는 일이 두렵지 않았고, 게다가 그에게는 페레즈본이 있었으니, 그 개는 현관 의자 밑에 옴짝달싹 말고 납작 엎드려 있으라는 명령을 받은 터였다."], ["Every time Kolya, walking to and fro through the rooms, came into the hall, the dog shook his head and gave two loud and insinuating taps on the floor with his tail, but alas!", "콜랴가 방들을 이리저리 오가다 현관으로 들어설 때마다 개는 고개를 흔들고 꼬리로 마루를 두 번 크게 알랑거리듯 두드렸으나, 아아!"], ["the whistle did not sound to release him.", "그를 풀어 줄 휘파람 소리는 들려오지 않았다."], ["Kolya looked sternly at the luckless dog, who relapsed again into obedient rigidity.", "콜랴는 그 가엾은 개를 매섭게 노려보았고, 개는 다시금 고분고분하게 굳은 자세로 돌아갔다."], ["The one thing that troubled Kolya was “the kids.”", "콜랴를 괴롭히는 단 한 가지는 \"꼬맹이들\"이었다."], ["He looked, of course, with the utmost scorn on Katerina’s unexpected adventure, but he was very fond of the bereaved “kiddies,” and had already taken them a picture-book.", "물론 그는 카테리나의 뜻밖의 모험을 더없는 경멸의 눈초리로 바라보았으나, 그 버림받은 \"꼬맹이들\"만큼은 무척 귀여워해 이미 그림책 한 권을 가져다준 터였다."], ["Nastya, the elder, a girl of eight, could read, and Kostya, the boy, aged seven, was very fond of being read to by her.", "손위인 여덟 살배기 계집아이 나스탸는 글을 읽을 줄 알았고, 일곱 살배기 사내아이 코스탸는 누이가 글을 읽어 주는 것을 무척 좋아했다."], ["Krassotkin could, of course, have provided more diverting entertainment for them.", "크라소트킨은 물론 그들에게 더 신나는 놀잇거리를 마련해 줄 수도 있었다."], ["He could have made them stand side by side and played soldiers with them, or sent them hiding all over the house.", "둘을 나란히 세워 놓고 병정놀이를 하거나, 온 집 안을 뒤져 가며 숨바꼭질을 시킬 수도 있었다."], ["He had done so more than once before and was not above doing it, so much so that a report once spread at school that Krassotkin played horses with the little lodgers at home, prancing with his head on one side like a trace-horse.", "그는 전에도 한두 번 그렇게 한 적이 있었고 또 그 정도쯤 마다하지 않는 터라, 한번은 학교에 크라소트킨이 집에서 어린 셋방 아이들과 말놀이를 하며 곁말처럼 고개를 한쪽으로 갸우뚱한 채 날뛰더라는 소문까지 퍼졌을 정도였다."], ["But Krassotkin haughtily parried this thrust, pointing out that to play horses with boys of one’s own age, boys of thirteen, would certainly be disgraceful “at this date,”", "그러나 크라소트킨은 도도하게 이 빈정거림을 받아넘기며, 제 또래의 사내아이들, 곧 열세 살짜리들과 말놀이를 한다면야 \"이 나이에\""], ["but that he did it for the sake of “the kids” because he liked them, and no one had a right to call him to account for his feelings.", "분명 망신스러운 일이겠지만, 자기는 \"꼬맹이들\"이 좋아서 그들을 위해 그러는 것이며, 자기 감정을 두고 시비를 걸 권리는 누구에게도 없다고 못 박았다."], ["The two “kids” adored him.", "두 \"꼬맹이\"는 그를 무척 따랐다."]], [["But on this occasion he was in no mood for games.", "그러나 이번만큼은 그도 놀이를 할 기분이 아니었다."], ["He had very important business of his own before him, something almost mysterious.", "그의 앞에는 거의 무슨 비밀에 가까운, 제 나름의 대단히 중요한 용무가 놓여 있었던 것이다."], ["Meanwhile time was passing and Agafya, with whom he could have left the children, would not come back from market.", "그러는 사이에도 시간은 자꾸 흘렀고, 아이들을 맡길 수 있을 아가피야는 장에서 돌아오지 않았다."], ["He had several times already crossed the passage, opened the door of the lodgers’ room and looked anxiously at “the kids” who were sitting over the book, as he had bidden them.", "그는 벌써 몇 번이고 복도를 가로질러 셋방 문을 열고는, 일러둔 대로 책을 펴 놓고 앉아 있는 \"꼬맹이들\"을 안절부절못하며 들여다보았다."], ["Every time he opened the door they grinned at him, hoping he would come in and would do something delightful and amusing.", "그가 문을 열 때마다 아이들은 그가 들어와 무언가 즐겁고 재미난 짓을 해 주기를 바라며 그를 보고 히죽 웃었다."], ["But Kolya was bothered and did not go in.", "그러나 콜랴는 마음이 어수선해 들어가지 않았다."]], [["At last it struck eleven and he made up his mind, once for all, that if that “damned”", "마침내 열한 시를 알리는 소리가 울리자 그는 단번에 결심을 굳혔다. 그 \"빌어먹을\""], ["Agafya did not come back within ten minutes he should go out without waiting for her, making “the kids” promise, of course, to be brave when he was away, not to be naughty, not to cry from fright.", "아가피야가 십 분 안에 돌아오지 않는다면 더는 기다리지 않고 나가 버리되, 물론 \"꼬맹이들\"에게는 자기가 없는 동안 용감하게 굴고, 말썽 부리지 말고, 무서워서 울지 말라고 다짐을 받아 두기로 한 것이다."], ["With this idea he put on his wadded winter overcoat with its catskin fur collar, slung his satchel round his shoulder, and, regardless of his mother’s constantly reiterated entreaties that he would always put on goloshes in such cold weather, he looked at them contemptuously as he crossed the hall and went out with only his boots on.", "이런 생각에 그는 고양이 털 깃이 달린 솜 둔 겨울 외투를 걸치고 책가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 뒤, 이렇게 추운 날에는 늘 덧신을 신으라고 어머니가 입이 닳도록 당부하던 것도 아랑곳없이, 현관을 가로지르며 그 덧신을 경멸하듯 한 번 쳐다보고는 장화만 신은 채 밖으로 나섰다."], ["Perezvon, seeing him in his outdoor clothes, began tapping nervously, yet vigorously, on the floor with his tail.", "페레즈본은 그가 외출복 차림인 것을 보더니 안절부절못하면서도 힘차게 꼬리로 마루를 두드리기 시작했다."], ["Twitching all over, he even uttered a plaintive whine.", "온몸을 떨며 심지어 애처로운 낑낑 소리까지 냈다."], ["But Kolya, seeing his dog’s passionate excitement, decided that it was a breach of discipline, kept him for another minute under the bench, and only when he had opened the door into the passage, whistled for him.", "그러나 콜랴는 개가 그토록 흥분해 들뜬 것을 보고 이것이 규율 위반이라 판단해, 다시 일 분 동안 의자 밑에 붙들어 두었다가, 복도로 통하는 문을 열고 나서야 비로소 휘파람을 불어 불러냈다."], ["The dog leapt up like a mad creature and rushed bounding before him rapturously.", "개는 미친 것처럼 펄쩍 뛰어올라 황홀해하며 그의 앞으로 깡충깡충 달려 나갔다."]], [["Kolya opened the door to peep at “the kids.”", "콜랴는 문을 열고 \"꼬맹이들\"을 들여다보았다."], ["They were both sitting as before at the table, not reading but warmly disputing about something.", "둘은 여전히 탁자 앞에 앉아 있었으나, 책을 읽는 것이 아니라 무언가를 두고 열을 올리며 다투고 있었다."], ["The children often argued together about various exciting problems of life, and Nastya, being the elder, always got the best of it.", "아이들은 인생의 갖가지 흥미진진한 문제를 두고 곧잘 함께 입씨름을 벌였는데, 나스탸가 손위였던 까닭에 늘 이겼다."], ["If Kostya did not agree with her, he almost always appealed to Kolya Krassotkin, and his verdict was regarded as infallible by both of them.", "코스탸는 누이와 의견이 맞지 않으면 거의 언제나 콜랴 크라소트킨에게 판가름을 청했고, 그의 판결은 둘 다에게 한 치도 틀림없는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["This time the “kids’” discussion rather interested Krassotkin, and he stood still in the passage to listen.", "이번에 \"꼬맹이들\"의 토론은 크라소트킨의 흥미를 제법 끌었던 터라, 그는 복도에 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["The children saw he was listening and that made them dispute with even greater energy.", "아이들은 그가 엿듣고 있는 것을 알아채자 더더욱 기를 쓰며 다투었다."]], [["“I shall never, never believe,”", "\"난 절대로, 절대로 안 믿어.\""], ["Nastya prattled, “that the old women find babies among the cabbages in the kitchen-garden.", "나스탸가 재잘거렸다. \"할머니들이 텃밭의 양배추 속에서 아기를 찾아낸다는 거 말이야."], ["It’s winter now and there are no cabbages, and so the old woman couldn’t have taken Katerina a daughter.”", "지금은 겨울이라 양배추도 없잖아, 그러니까 할머니가 카테리나한테 딸을 갖다줬을 리가 없어.\""]], [["Kolya whistled to himself.", "콜랴는 혼자 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["“Or perhaps they do bring babies from somewhere, but only to those who are married.”", "\"아니면 어쩌면 어디선가 아기를 데려오기는 하는데, 그게 결혼한 사람들한테만 그러는 거겠지.\""]], [["Kostya stared at Nastya and listened, pondering profoundly.", "코스탸는 나스탸를 빤히 바라보며 듣더니, 깊이 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Nastya, how silly you are!”", "\"나스탸, 너 참 바보구나!\""], ["he said at last, firmly and calmly.", "마침내 그가 단호하고 차분하게 말했다."], ["“How can Katerina have a baby when she isn’t married?”", "\"결혼도 안 한 카테리나가 어떻게 아기를 가질 수가 있어?\""]], [["Nastya was exasperated.", "나스탸는 약이 올랐다."]], [["“You know nothing about it,”", "\"넌 아무것도 모르면서.\""], ["she snapped irritably.", "그녀가 신경질을 부리며 톡 쏘았다."], ["“Perhaps she has a husband, only he is in prison, so now she’s got a baby.”", "\"어쩌면 남편이 있는데, 다만 감옥에 가 있는지도 모르잖아, 그래서 이제 아기를 가진 거지.\""]], [["“But is her husband in prison?”", "\"그런데 그 여자 남편이 감옥에 있어?\""], ["the matter-of-fact Kostya inquired gravely.", "따지기 좋아하는 코스탸가 진지하게 캐물었다."]], [["“Or, I tell you what,”", "\"아니, 내가 말해 줄게.\""], ["Nastya interrupted impulsively, completely rejecting and forgetting her first hypothesis.", "나스탸가 충동적으로 말을 가로채며, 처음에 내세운 가설은 깡그리 버리고 잊은 채 말했다."], ["“She hasn’t a husband, you are right there, but she wants to be married, and so she’s been thinking of getting married, and thinking and thinking of it till now she’s got it, that is, not a husband but a baby.”", "\"그 여잔 남편이 없어, 그건 네 말이 맞아. 그런데 결혼하고 싶어서, 그래서 결혼할 생각을 했는데, 생각하고 또 생각하다 보니까 이제 그걸 갖게 된 거야, 그러니까 남편이 아니라 아기를 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, perhaps so,”", "\"음, 어쩌면 그럴지도 몰라.\""], ["Kostya agreed, entirely vanquished.", "완전히 두 손 든 코스탸가 수긍했다."], ["“But you didn’t say so before.", "\"그런데 넌 아까는 그 말 안 했잖아."], ["So how could I tell?”", "그러니 내가 어떻게 알았겠어?\""]], [["“Come, kiddies,”", "\"자, 꼬맹이들.\""], ["said Kolya, stepping into the room.", "콜랴가 방 안으로 들어서며 말했다."], ["“You’re terrible people, I see.”", "\"보아하니 너희들 대단한 사람들이로구나.\""]], [["“And Perezvon with you!”", "\"페레즈본도 같이 왔다!\""], ["grinned Kostya, and began snapping his fingers and calling Perezvon.", "코스탸가 히죽 웃으며 손가락을 딱딱 튕겨 페레즈본을 부르기 시작했다."]], [["“I am in a difficulty, kids,”", "\"꼬맹이들, 나한테 골치 아픈 일이 생겼는데,\""], ["Krassotkin began solemnly, “and you must help me.", "크라소트킨이 엄숙하게 운을 떼었다. \"너희가 좀 도와줘야겠다."], ["Agafya must have broken her leg, since she has not turned up till now, that’s certain.", "아가피야가 다리라도 부러진 게 분명해, 지금까지 안 돌아오는 걸 보면 말이야, 그건 틀림없어."], ["I must go out.", "난 나가 봐야 해."], ["Will you let me go?”", "나 좀 나가게 해 줄래?\""]], [["The children looked anxiously at one another.", "아이들은 불안한 듯 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Their smiling faces showed signs of uneasiness, but they did not yet fully grasp what was expected of them.", "웃고 있던 그들의 얼굴에 근심의 빛이 떠올랐으나, 자기들에게 무엇이 요구되는지는 아직 온전히 헤아리지 못했다."]], [["“You won’t be naughty while I am gone?", "\"내가 없는 동안 말썽 안 부릴 거지?"], ["You won’t climb on the cupboard and break your legs?", "찬장에 기어올라가 다리 부러뜨리지 않을 거지?"], ["You won’t be frightened alone and cry?”", "혼자 무섭다고 울지 않을 거지?\""]], [["A look of profound despondency came into the children’s faces.", "아이들의 얼굴에 깊은 낙담의 기색이 어렸다."]], [["“And I could show you something as a reward, a little copper cannon which can be fired with real gunpowder.”", "\"그러면 상으로 너희한테 뭔가 보여 줄 수도 있는데, 진짜 화약으로 쏠 수 있는 자그마한 구리 대포 말이야.\""]], [["The children’s faces instantly brightened.", "아이들의 얼굴이 대뜸 환해졌다. \"대포 보여 줘.\""], ["“Show us the cannon,” said Kostya, beaming all over.", "코스탸가 온 얼굴에 함박웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["Krassotkin put his hand in his satchel, and pulling out a little bronze cannon stood it on the table.", "크라소트킨은 책가방에 손을 넣어 자그마한 청동 대포를 끄집어내더니 탁자 위에 세워 놓았다."]], [["“Ah, you are bound to ask that!", "\"아, 그건 물어볼 줄 알았지!"], ["Look, it’s on wheels.”", "봐, 바퀴가 달렸잖아.\""], ["He rolled the toy on along the table.", "그는 그 장난감을 탁자 위로 굴렸다."], ["“And it can be fired off, too.", "\"그리고 쏠 수도 있어."], ["It can be loaded with shot and fired off.”", "산탄을 재워서 쏠 수 있단 말이야.\""]], [["“And it could kill any one?”", "\"그럼 누구든 죽일 수 있어?\""]], [["“It can kill any one; you’ve only got to aim at anybody,”", "\"누구든 죽일 수 있지. 누구한테든 겨누기만 하면 돼.\""], ["and Krassotkin explained where the powder had to be put, where the shot should be rolled in, showing a tiny hole like a touch-hole, and told them that it kicked when it was fired.", "그러고는 크라소트킨이 화약을 어디에 넣고 산탄을 어디에 굴려 넣어야 하는지 설명하며 점화구 같은 자그마한 구멍을 보여 주었고, 쏠 때면 뒤로 튄다고 일러 주었다."]], [["The children listened with intense interest.", "아이들은 무척이나 흥미진진하게 귀를 기울였다."], ["What particularly struck their imagination was that the cannon kicked.", "특히 그들의 상상을 사로잡은 것은 대포가 뒤로 튄다는 점이었다."]], [["“And have you got any powder?”", "\"그럼 화약도 있어?\""], ["Nastya inquired.", "나스탸가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“Show us the powder, too,”", "\"화약도 보여 줘.\""], ["she drawled with a smile of entreaty.", "그녀가 애원하는 미소를 띠며 길게 늘여 말했다."]], [["Krassotkin dived again into his satchel and pulled out a small flask containing a little real gunpowder.", "크라소트킨은 다시 책가방을 뒤져 진짜 화약이 조금 든 작은 약병을 꺼냈다."], ["He had some shot, too, in a screw of paper.", "종이로 비틀어 싼 산탄도 얼마간 있었다."], ["He even uncorked the flask and shook a little powder into the palm of his hand.", "그는 약병 마개까지 뽑아 손바닥에 화약을 조금 털어 보였다."]], [["“One has to be careful there’s no fire about, or it would blow up and kill us all,”", "\"가까이에 불이 없도록 조심해야 해, 안 그러면 터져서 우리 모두 죽고 말 테니까.\""], ["Krassotkin warned them sensationally.", "크라소트킨이 짐짓 으스스하게 그들을 일렀다."]], [["The children gazed at the powder with an awe-stricken alarm that only intensified their enjoyment.", "아이들은 두려움에 사로잡힌 눈빛으로 화약을 바라보았는데, 그 두려움이 도리어 그들의 즐거움을 한층 돋우었다."], ["But Kostya liked the shot better.", "그러나 코스탸는 산탄이 더 마음에 들었다."]], [["“And does the shot burn?”", "\"그럼 산탄도 타?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“No, it doesn’t.”", "\"아니, 그건 안 타.\""]], [["“Give me a little shot,”", "\"나 산탄 조금만 줘.\""], ["he asked in an imploring voice.", "그가 애원하는 목소리로 청했다."]], [["“I’ll give you a little shot; here, take it, but don’t show it to your mother till I come back, or she’ll be sure to think it’s gunpowder, and will die of fright and give you a thrashing.”", "\"산탄 조금 줄게. 자, 받아, 그런데 내가 돌아올 때까지 너희 엄마한테는 보여 주면 안 돼, 안 그러면 분명히 화약인 줄 알고 놀라서 죽을 지경이 돼서 너희를 매질할 테니까.\""]], [["“Mother never does whip us,”", "\"엄마는 우리 한 번도 매질 안 해.\""], ["Nastya observed at once.", "나스탸가 곧바로 한마디 했다."]], [["“I know, I only said it to finish the sentence.", "\"알아, 그냥 말을 마무리하려고 한 거야."], ["And don’t you ever deceive your mother except just this once, until I come back.", "그리고 너흰 절대로 엄마를 속이면 안 돼, 딱 이번 한 번만, 내가 돌아올 때까지만이야."], ["And so, kiddies, can I go out?", "그래서, 꼬맹이들, 나 나가도 될까?"], ["You won’t be frightened and cry when I’m gone?”", "내가 없어도 무서워서 울지 않을 거지?\""]], [["“We sha—all cry,”", "\"우우—울 거야.\""], ["drawled Kostya, on the verge of tears already.", "코스탸가 벌써 울먹울먹하며 길게 늘여 말했다."]], [["“We shall cry, we shall be sure to cry,”", "\"우리 울 거야, 분명히 울 거야.\""], ["Nastya chimed in with timid haste.", "나스탸도 겁먹은 듯 조급하게 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“Oh, children, children, how fraught with peril are your years!", "\"오, 애들아, 애들아, 너희 나이란 어찌 이리도 위태로운지!"], ["There’s no help for it, chickens, I shall have to stay with you I don’t know how long.", "어쩔 수가 없구나, 병아리들아, 내가 얼마나 걸릴지도 모르면서 너희와 함께 있어야겠다."], ["And time is passing, time is passing, oogh!”", "그런데 시간은 자꾸 흐르고, 시간은 자꾸 흐르는데, 어휴!\""]], [["“Tell Perezvon to pretend to be dead!”", "\"페레즈본더러 죽은 척하라고 시켜 줘!\""], ["Kostya begged.", "코스탸가 졸랐다."]], [["“There’s no help for it, we must have recourse to Perezvon.", "\"어쩔 수가 없지, 페레즈본의 힘을 빌려야겠구나."], ["Ici, Perezvon.”", "이시, 페레즈본.\""], ["And Kolya began giving orders to the dog, who performed all his tricks.", "그러고는 콜랴가 개에게 명령을 내리기 시작했고, 개는 온갖 재주를 다 부렸다."]], [["He was a rough-haired dog, of medium size, with a coat of a sort of lilac-gray color.", "페레즈본은 털이 뻣뻣하고 몸집이 중간쯤 되는 개로, 털빛은 일종의 보랏빛 도는 잿빛이었다."], ["He was blind in his right eye, and his left ear was torn.", "오른쪽 눈은 멀었고 왼쪽 귀는 찢어져 있었다."], ["He whined and jumped, stood and walked on his hind legs, lay on his back with his paws in the air, rigid as though he were dead.", "그는 낑낑거리고 펄쩍 뛰고, 일어서서 뒷다리로 걷고, 죽은 듯 뻣뻣하게 발을 허공에 든 채 벌렁 드러누웠다."], ["While this last performance was going on, the door opened and Agafya, Madame Krassotkin’s servant, a stout woman of forty, marked with small-pox, appeared in the doorway.", "이 마지막 재주를 부리고 있을 때 문이 열리고, 크라소트킨 부인의 하녀 아가피야가, 마흔 살에 천연두 자국이 얽은 뚱뚱한 여자가 문간에 나타났다."], ["She had come back from market and had a bag full of provisions in her hand.", "그녀는 장에서 돌아오는 길이라 먹을거리가 가득 든 자루를 손에 들고 있었다."], ["Holding up the bag of provisions in her left hand she stood still to watch the dog.", "왼손에 그 먹을거리 자루를 든 채 그녀는 가만히 멈춰 서서 개를 지켜보았다."], ["Though Kolya had been so anxious for her return, he did not cut short the performance, and after keeping Perezvon dead for the usual time, at last he whistled to him.", "콜랴는 그토록 그녀가 돌아오기를 애타게 기다렸으면서도 그 재주 부리기를 도중에 그치게 하지 않았고, 페레즈본을 으레 그러던 만큼 죽은 채로 붙들어 두었다가 마침내 휘파람을 불었다."], ["The dog jumped up and began bounding about in his joy at having done his duty.", "개는 벌떡 일어나 제 임무를 다한 기쁨에 겨워 깡충깡충 뛰어다니기 시작했다."]], [["“Only think, a dog!”", "\"세상에, 개라는 게 말이야!\""], ["Agafya observed sententiously.", "아가피야가 점잔을 빼며 한마디 했다."]], [["“Why are you late, female?”", "\"왜 이리 늦었어, 여편네?\""], ["asked Krassotkin sternly.", "크라소트킨이 매섭게 물었다."]], [["“Female, indeed!", "\"여편네라니, 무슨!"], ["Go on with you, you brat.” “Brat?”", "저리 가, 이 꼬마 녀석아.\""]], [["“Yes, a brat.", "\"꼬마 녀석?\""], ["What is it to you if I’m late; if I’m late, you may be sure I have good reason,”", "\"그래, 꼬마 녀석. 내가 늦는 게 너랑 무슨 상관이람."], ["muttered Agafya, busying herself about the stove, without a trace of anger or displeasure in her voice.", "내가 늦으면 다 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그런 거지.\" 아가피야가 화덕 주위를 부산스레 오가며, 목소리에는 노여움이나 언짢음의 기색이라곤 한 점도 없이 중얼거렸다."], ["She seemed quite pleased, in fact, to enjoy a skirmish with her merry young master.", "사실 그녀는 짓궂은 젊은 주인과 한바탕 옥신각신하는 것이 자못 즐거운 듯 보였다."]], [["“Listen, you frivolous young woman,”", "\"이봐, 이 경박한 아낙네야,\" 크라소트킨이 소파에서 일어서며 입을 열었다."], ["Krassotkin began, getting up from the sofa, “can you swear by all you hold sacred in the world and something else besides, that you will watch vigilantly over the kids in my absence?", "\"세상에서 네가 신성하게 여기는 모든 것에 걸고, 게다가 그것 말고 또 무엇에든 걸고 맹세할 수 있겠어? 내가 없는 동안 이 꼬맹이들을 한눈팔지 않고 잘 지키겠다고 말이야."], ["I am going out.”", "난 나가 봐야 해.\""]], [["“And what am I going to swear for?”", "\"내가 무엇 때문에 맹세를 한담?\""], ["laughed Agafya.", "아가피야가 웃었다."], ["“I shall look after them without that.”", "\"그런 거 없이도 잘 봐줄 텐데.\""]], [["“No, you must swear on your eternal salvation.", "\"안 돼, 영원한 구원에 걸고 맹세해야 해."], ["Else I shan’t go.”", "안 그러면 난 안 나갈 거야.\""]], [["“Well, don’t then.", "\"그럼 나가지 말려무나."], ["What does it matter to me?", "그게 나랑 무슨 상관이야?"], ["It’s cold out; stay at home.”", "밖은 추우니, 집에나 있어.\""]], [["“Kids,”", "\"꼬맹이들,\""], ["Kolya turned to the children, “this woman will stay with you till I come back or till your mother comes, for she ought to have been back long ago.", "콜랴가 아이들 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 아줌마가 내가 돌아오거나 너희 엄마가 올 때까지 너희랑 있어 줄 거야, 엄마는 진작 돌아왔어야 하는데 말이야."], ["She will give you some lunch, too.", "점심도 차려 줄 거야."], ["You’ll give them something, Agafya, won’t you?”", "얘들한테 뭐 좀 차려 줄 거지, 아가피야, 그렇지?\""]], [["“That I can do.”", "\"그쯤이야 할 수 있지.\""]], [["“Good-by, chickens, I go with my heart at rest.", "\"잘 있어라, 병아리들아, 난 마음 놓고 간다."], ["And you, granny,”", "그리고 할멈은,\""], ["he added gravely, in an undertone, as he passed Agafya, “I hope you’ll spare their tender years and not tell them any of your old woman’s nonsense about Katerina.", "그가 아가피야 곁을 지나며 나직한 소리로 진지하게 덧붙였다. \"이 어린것들을 봐서라도 카테리나에 관해 할멈의 그 노파다운 허튼소리 따위는 한마디도 안 했으면 좋겠어."], ["Ici, Perezvon!”", "이시, 페레즈본!\""]], [["“Get along with you!”", "\"가, 어서 가 버려!\""], ["retorted Agafya, really angry this time.", "아가피야가 이번에는 정말로 화가 나서 쏘아붙였다."], ["“Ridiculous boy!", "\"어처구니없는 녀석 같으니!"], ["You want a whipping for saying such things, that’s what you want!”", "그런 소리를 하다니 매를 좀 맞아야 정신을 차리겠어, 바로 그거라고!\""]], [["# Chapter III. The Schoolboy", "# Chapter III. The Schoolboy"]], [["But Kolya did not hear her. At last he could go out.", "그러나 콜랴는 어머니의 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["As he went out at the gate he looked round him, shrugged up his shoulders, and saying “It is freezing,”", "마침내 그는 밖으로 나설 수 있었다. 대문을 나서며 그는 사방을 둘러보고 어깨를 으쓱하더니, \"꽝꽝 얼겠는걸\""], ["went straight along the street and turned off to the right towards the market-place.", "하고 중얼거리며 곧장 거리를 따라 걷다가 오른쪽으로 꺾어 장터 쪽으로 향했다."], ["When he reached the last house but one before the market-place he stopped at the gate, pulled a whistle out of his pocket, and whistled with all his might as though giving a signal.", "장터 바로 앞에서 두 번째 집에 이르렀을 때 그는 대문 앞에 멈춰 서서 주머니에서 호루라기를 꺼내, 마치 신호라도 보내듯 있는 힘껏 불었다."], ["He had not to wait more than a minute before a rosy-cheeked boy of about eleven, wearing a warm, neat and even stylish coat, darted out to meet him.", "일 분도 채 기다리기 전에 따뜻하고 깔끔한, 그것도 제법 멋스러운 외투를 걸친 열한 살가량의 발그레한 볼을 가진 소년이 그를 맞으러 쏜살같이 달려 나왔다."], ["This was Smurov, a boy in the preparatory class (two classes below Kolya Krassotkin), son of a well-to-do official.", "이 아이는 예비반(콜랴 크라소트킨보다 두 학년 아래였다)의 소년 스무로프로, 형편이 넉넉한 관리의 아들이었다."], ["Apparently he was forbidden by his parents to associate with Krassotkin, who was well known to be a desperately naughty boy, so Smurov was obviously slipping out on the sly.", "보아하니 그의 부모는 소문난 망나니 크라소트킨과 어울리는 것을 금했던 모양이라, 스무로프는 분명 몰래 빠져나온 것이었다."], ["He was—if the reader has not forgotten—one of the group of boys who two months before had thrown stones at Ilusha.", "그는—독자가 잊지 않았다면—두 달 전 일류샤에게 돌을 던졌던 소년 패거리 가운데 하나였다."], ["He was the one who told Alyosha Karamazov about Ilusha.", "일류샤에 관해 알렉세이 카라마조프에게 일러바친 것도 바로 그였다."]], [["“I’ve been waiting for you for the last hour, Krassotkin,”", "\"한 시간이나 기다렸어, 크라소트킨.\""], ["said Smurov stolidly, and the boys strode towards the market-place.", "스무로프가 무덤덤하게 말하자, 두 소년은 장터 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어갔다."]], [["“I am late,”", "\"늦었지.\""], ["answered Krassotkin.", "크라소트킨이 대답했다."], ["“I was detained by circumstances.", "\"사정이 있어 붙들렸어."], ["You won’t be thrashed for coming with me?”", "나하고 같이 다닌다고 두들겨 맞진 않겠지?\""]], [["“Come, I say, I’m never thrashed!", "\"아니, 정말이지 난 한 번도 안 맞아!"], ["And you’ve got Perezvon with you?”", "그런데 페레즈본도 데려왔어?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“You’re taking him, too?”", "\"걔도 데려가?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["if it were only Zhutchka!”", "주치카였으면 좋았을 텐데!\""]], [["“That’s impossible.", "\"그건 불가능해."], ["Zhutchka’s non-existent.", "주치카는 이제 없어."], ["Zhutchka is lost in the mists of obscurity.”", "주치카는 어둠의 안갯속으로 사라져 버렸다고.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["couldn’t we do this?”", "이렇게 하면 안 될까?\""], ["Smurov suddenly stood still.", "스무로프가 갑자기 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["“You see Ilusha says that Zhutchka was a shaggy, grayish, smoky-looking dog like Perezvon.", "\"있잖아, 일류샤가 그러는데 주치카는 페레즈본처럼 털북숭이에 거무스름한 잿빛 개였대."], ["Couldn’t you tell him this is Zhutchka, and he might believe you?”", "이게 주치카라고 하면 어때, 걔가 믿을지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Boy, shun a lie, that’s one thing; even with a good object—that’s another.", "\"이봐, 거짓말은 피하라는 게 첫째고, 설령 좋은 뜻이라 해도 그래선 안 된다는 게 둘째야."], ["Above all, I hope you’ve not told them anything about my coming.”", "무엇보다, 내가 간다는 걸 그쪽에 한마디도 안 했길 바란다.\""]], [["“Heaven forbid!", "\"천만에!"], ["I know what I am about.", "내가 알아서 하지."], ["But you won’t comfort him with Perezvon,”", "그래도 페레즈본으로는 걔를 위로하지 못할 거야.\""], ["said Smurov, with a sigh.", "스무로프가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“You know his father, the captain, ‘the wisp of tow,’ told us that he was going to bring him a real mastiff pup, with a black nose, to-day.", "\"걔 아버지, 그 대위, '삼 부스러기' 말이야, 오늘 코가 까만 진짜 마스티프 새끼를 데려다주겠다고 하셨거든."], ["He thinks that would comfort Ilusha; but I doubt it.”", "그러면 일류샤가 위로받을 거라고 생각하시는데, 글쎄 모르겠어.\""]], [["“And how is Ilusha?”", "\"일류샤는 좀 어때?\""]], [["“Ah, he is bad, very bad!", "\"아, 안 좋아, 아주 안 좋아!"], ["I believe he’s in consumption: he is quite conscious, but his breathing!", "폐병에 걸린 것 같아. 정신은 또렷한데, 숨이!"], ["His breathing’s gone wrong.", "숨 쉬는 게 영 잘못됐어."], ["The other day he asked to have his boots on to be led round the room.", "며칠 전엔 장화를 신겨 달라고 하더니 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 싶어 했어. 걸어 보려고 했지만 서질 못했어."], ["He tried to walk, but he couldn’t stand.", "'아, 아버지, 진작 말했잖아요."], ["‘Ah, I told you before, father,’ he said, ‘that those boots were no good.", "이 장화는 못쓴다고요. 이걸론 도무지 똑바로 걸을 수가 없어요.'"], ["I could never walk properly in them.’ He fancied it was his boots that made him stagger, but it was simply weakness, really.", "이러는 거야. 장화 때문에 비틀거린다고 여겼지만, 사실은 그냥 기운이 없는 거였어."], ["He won’t live another week.", "일주일도 못 넘길 거야."], ["Herzenstube is looking after him.", "게르첸슈투베가 봐주고 있어."], ["Now they are rich again—they’ve got heaps of money.”", "이제 그 집은 다시 부자가 됐어. 돈이 산더미처럼 생겼대.\""]], [["“They are rogues.”", "\"사기꾼들이지.\""]], [["“Who are rogues?”", "\"누가 사기꾼이야?\""]], [["“Doctors and the whole crew of quacks collectively, and also, of course, individually.", "\"의사들, 그 돌팔이 무리 전부 통틀어서 말이야, 물론 하나하나 따져도 그렇고."], ["I don’t believe in medicine.", "난 의학을 안 믿어. 쓸데없는 제도야."], ["It’s a useless institution. I mean to go into all that.", "이 문제는 내가 죄다 파헤쳐 볼 작정이야."], ["But what’s that sentimentality you’ve got up there?", "그런데 너희가 저기서 부리는 그 감상은 다 뭐냐?"], ["The whole class seems to be there every day.”", "매일 반 전체가 거기 가는 모양이던데.\""]], [["“Not the whole class: it’s only ten of our fellows who go to see him every day.", "\"반 전체는 아니야. 우리 애들 가운데 열 명쯤이 매일 걔를 보러 가는 거야."], ["There’s nothing in that.”", "그게 뭐 대수도 아니고.\""]], [["“What I don’t understand in all this is the part that Alexey Karamazov is taking in it.", "\"이 일에서 내가 도무지 이해 못 하는 건 알렉세이 카라마조프가 거기 끼어 있다는 거야."], ["His brother’s going to be tried to-morrow or next day for such a crime, and yet he has so much time to spend on sentimentality with boys.”", "자기 형이 그런 죄로 내일이나 모레면 재판을 받는데, 어린애들하고 감상에 젖을 시간이 어찌 그리도 넉넉한지.\""]], [["“There’s no sentimentality about it.", "\"감상 같은 거 없어."], ["You are going yourself now to make it up with Ilusha.”", "너도 지금 일류샤하고 화해하러 가는 거잖아.\""]], [["“Make it up with him?", "\"화해라고?"], ["What an absurd expression!", "무슨 그런 어이없는 말을!"], ["But I allow no one to analyze my actions.”", "하지만 내 행동을 누가 분석하는 건 용납 못 해.\""]], [["“And how pleased Ilusha will be to see you!", "\"일류샤가 널 보면 얼마나 기뻐하겠어!"], ["He has no idea that you are coming.", "걘 네가 온다는 걸 까맣게 몰라."], ["Why was it, why was it you wouldn’t come all this time?”", "도대체 왜, 왜 넌 이때까지 안 온 거야?\""], ["Smurov cried with sudden warmth.", "스무로프가 갑자기 따뜻한 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“My dear boy, that’s my business, not yours.", "\"이봐, 그건 내 일이지 네 일이 아니야."], ["I am going of myself because I choose to, but you’ve all been hauled there by Alexey Karamazov—there’s a difference, you know.", "난 내가 가고 싶어서 가는 거고, 너희는 죄다 알렉세이 카라마조프한테 끌려간 거야. 거기엔 차이가 있단 말이야, 알겠어?"], ["And how do you know?", "게다가 네가 어떻게 알아?"], ["I may not be going to make it up at all.", "난 화해 같은 건 아예 안 할지도 몰라."], ["It’s a stupid expression.”", "어처구니없는 말이야.\""]], [["“It’s not Karamazov at all; it’s not his doing.", "\"카라마조프 일이 절대 아니야. 그 사람이 시킨 게 아니라고."], ["Our fellows began going there of themselves.", "우리 애들이 스스로 가기 시작한 거야."], ["Of course, they went with Karamazov at first.", "물론 처음엔 카라마조프하고 같이 갔지."], ["And there’s been nothing of that sort—no silliness.", "그래도 그런 거 전혀 없었어. 어리석은 짓 같은 건."], ["First one went, and then another.", "하나가 가고, 또 하나가 가고 그랬어."], ["His father was awfully pleased to see us.", "걔 아버지는 우리를 보고 어찌나 기뻐하시는지."], ["You know he will simply go out of his mind if Ilusha dies.", "너도 알지, 일류샤가 죽으면 그분은 그냥 미쳐 버리실 거야."], ["He sees that Ilusha’s dying.", "그분도 일류샤가 죽어 간다는 걸 알아."], ["And he seems so glad we’ve made it up with Ilusha.", "그래서 우리가 일류샤하고 화해한 걸 무척 기뻐하시는 것 같아."], ["Ilusha asked after you, that was all.", "일류샤가 네 안부를 물었어, 그게 다야."], ["He just asks and says no more.", "그냥 묻기만 하고 더는 말 안 해."], ["His father will go out of his mind or hang himself.", "걔 아버지는 미쳐 버리거나 목을 매실 거야."], ["He behaved like a madman before.", "전에는 정말 미친 사람처럼 굴었어."], ["You know he is a very decent man.", "너도 알다시피 그분은 아주 점잖은 분이야."], ["We made a mistake then.", "그때 우리가 잘못한 거야."], ["It’s all the fault of that murderer who beat him then.”", "그건 죄다 그때 그분을 때린 그 살인자 탓이지.\""]], [["“Karamazov’s a riddle to me all the same.", "\"그래도 카라마조프는 내겐 수수께끼야."], ["I might have made his acquaintance long ago, but I like to have a proper pride in some cases.", "진작 사귀어 둘 수도 있었지만, 난 경우에 따라선 응당한 자존심을 지키는 걸 좋아해."], ["Besides, I have a theory about him which I must work out and verify.”", "게다가 그 사람에 대해선 내가 세워 두고 풀어 가며 검증해야 할 이론이 하나 있거든.\""]], [["Kolya subsided into dignified silence.", "콜랴는 위엄 어린 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["Smurov, too, was silent.", "스무로프 역시 입을 다물었다."], ["Smurov, of course, worshiped Krassotkin and never dreamed of putting himself on a level with him.", "스무로프는 물론 크라소트킨을 떠받들었고, 자기를 그와 같은 반열에 놓는다는 건 꿈도 꾸지 못했다."], ["Now he was tremendously interested at Kolya’s saying that he was “going of himself” to see Ilusha.", "지금 그는 콜랴가 일류샤를 보러 \"내가 가고 싶어서 간다\"고 한 말에 굉장한 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["He felt that there must be some mystery in Kolya’s suddenly taking it into his head to go to him that day.", "콜랴가 갑자기 그날 그를 찾아갈 마음을 먹은 데에는 무언가 비밀이 있으리라 짐작했다."], ["They crossed the market-place, in which at that hour were many loaded wagons from the country and a great number of live fowls.", "그들은 장터를 가로질렀는데, 그 시각에는 시골에서 온 짐수레가 잔뜩 있었고 살아 있는 가금도 수두룩했다."], ["The market women were selling rolls, cottons and threads, etc., in their booths.", "장터 아낙들은 좌판에서 빵이며 무명천, 실 따위를 팔고 있었다."], ["These Sunday markets were naïvely called “fairs” in the town, and there were many such fairs in the year.", "읍내에서는 이런 일요 장을 순박하게도 \"큰장\"이라 불렀고, 한 해에 그런 큰장이 여러 차례 섰다."]], [["Perezvon ran about in the wildest spirits, sniffing about first one side, then the other.", "페레즈본은 더없이 신이 나서, 처음엔 이쪽, 다음엔 저쪽 냄새를 맡으며 쏘다녔다."], ["When he met other dogs they zealously smelt each other over according to the rules of canine etiquette.", "다른 개들을 만나면 개들끼리의 예법에 따라 열심히 서로의 냄새를 맡았다."]], [["“I like to watch such realistic scenes, Smurov,”", "\"난 저런 실감 나는 광경을 보는 게 좋아, 스무로프.\""], ["said Kolya suddenly.", "콜랴가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“Have you noticed how dogs sniff at one another when they meet?", "\"개들이 마주치면 서로 냄새를 맡는 거 봤어?"], ["It seems to be a law of their nature.”", "그건 그놈들 본성의 법칙인 모양이야.\""]], [["“Yes; it’s a funny habit.”", "\"응, 우스운 버릇이지.\""]], [["“No, it’s not funny; you are wrong there.", "\"아니, 우습지 않아. 거기선 네가 틀렸어."], ["There’s nothing funny in nature, however funny it may seem to man with his prejudices.", "자연에는 우스운 게 없어, 편견에 사로잡힌 인간 눈엔 아무리 우스워 보여도 말이야."], ["If dogs could reason and criticize us they’d be sure to find just as much that would be funny to them, if not far more, in the social relations of men, their masters—far more, indeed.", "개들이 이성을 갖고 우릴 비판할 수 있다면, 그놈들 주인인 인간들의 사회적 관계 속에서 분명 그만큼이나, 아니 훨씬 더 우스운 걸 찾아낼 거야. 정말이지 훨씬 더 많이."], ["I repeat that, because I am convinced that there is far more foolishness among us.", "내가 이 말을 거듭하는 건, 우리 사이에 어리석음이 훨씬 더 많다고 확신하기 때문이야."], ["That’s Rakitin’s idea—a remarkable idea.", "그건 라키틴의 생각인데, 탁월한 생각이지."], ["I am a Socialist, Smurov.”", "난 사회주의자야, 스무로프.\""]], [["“And what is a Socialist?”", "\"사회주의자가 뭔데?\""], ["asked Smurov.", "스무로프가 물었다."]], [["“That’s when all are equal and all have property in common, there are no marriages, and every one has any religion and laws he likes best, and all the rest of it.", "\"그건 모두가 평등하고 재산을 다 함께 갖고, 결혼이라는 게 없고, 누구나 제일 마음에 드는 종교와 법을 가질 수 있고, 뭐 그 밖의 모든 게 그렇다는 거야."], ["You are not old enough to understand that yet.", "넌 아직 그걸 이해할 나이가 못 돼."], ["It’s cold, though.”", "그건 그렇고, 춥다.\""]], [["“Yes, twelve degrees of frost.", "\"응, 영하 십이 도래."], ["Father looked at the thermometer just now.”", "아빠가 방금 온도계를 봤어.\""]], [["“Have you noticed, Smurov, that in the middle of winter we don’t feel so cold even when there are fifteen or eighteen degrees of frost as we do now, in the beginning of winter, when there is a sudden frost of twelve degrees, especially when there is not much snow.", "\"넌 눈치챘니, 스무로프, 한겨울엔 영하 십오 도나 십팔 도여도 지금만큼 춥게 느껴지지 않는데, 초겨울에 별안간 영하 십이 도까지 떨어지면, 특히 눈이 많이 안 왔을 때, 이렇게 춥게 느껴진다는 거?"], ["It’s because people are not used to it.", "사람들이 아직 거기 익숙해지지 않아서 그래."], ["Everything is habit with men, everything even in their social and political relations.", "인간한텐 모든 게 습관이야, 사회적이고 정치적인 관계에서까지도 죄다 그렇지."], ["Habit is the great motive-power.", "습관이야말로 위대한 추진력이라고."], ["What a funny-looking peasant!”", "저 농부 꼴 좀 봐, 우습게 생겼네!\""]], [["Kolya pointed to a tall peasant, with a good-natured countenance in a long sheepskin coat, who was standing by his wagon, clapping together his hands, in their shapeless leather gloves, to warm them.", "콜랴는 키 큰 농부 하나를 가리켰는데, 긴 양가죽 외투를 입고 선량한 얼굴을 한 그는 짐수레 옆에 서서 볼품없는 가죽 장갑을 낀 두 손을 마주 쳐 가며 녹이고 있었다."], ["His long fair beard was all white with frost.", "그의 길고 옅은 빛깔의 수염은 서리로 온통 하얗게 덮여 있었다."]], [["“That peasant’s beard’s frozen,”", "\"저 농부 수염이 얼어붙었네.\""], ["Kolya cried in a loud provocative voice as he passed him.", "콜랴는 그를 지나치며 일부러 도발하듯 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Lots of people’s beards are frozen,”", "\"수염 얼어붙은 사람이 한둘인가.\""], ["the peasant replied, calmly and sententiously.", "농부가 침착하고 점잖게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Don’t provoke him,”", "\"건드리지 마.\""], ["observed Smurov.", "스무로프가 한마디 했다."]], [["“It’s all right; he won’t be cross; he’s a nice fellow.", "\"괜찮아, 화 안 낼 거야. 좋은 사람이거든."], ["Good-by, Matvey.”", "잘 가요, 마트베이.\""]], [["“Good-by.”", "\"잘 가게.\""]], [["“Is your name Matvey?”", "\"이름이 마트베이예요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Didn’t you know?”", "어떻게 알았나?\""]], [["“No, I didn’t.", "\"몰랐어요."], ["It was a guess.”", "그냥 찍어 본 거예요.\""]], [["“You don’t say so!", "\"허, 그러게!"], ["You are a schoolboy, I suppose?”", "학생인가 보군?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“You get whipped, I expect?”", "\"매도 좀 맞겠구먼?\""]], [["“Nothing to speak of—sometimes.”", "\"뭐, 별것 아니에요. 가끔요.\""]], [["“Does it hurt?”", "\"아프나?\""]], [["“Well, yes, it does.”", "\"음, 네, 아프죠.\""]], [["“Ech, what a life!”", "\"에휴, 사는 게 다 그렇지!\""], ["The peasant heaved a sigh from the bottom of his heart.", "농부가 가슴속 깊은 데서 한숨을 토해 냈다."]], [["“Good-by, Matvey.”", "\"잘 가요, 마트베이.\""]], [["“Good-by.", "\"잘 가게."], ["You are a nice chap, that you are.”", "자넨 참 괜찮은 녀석이야, 정말이지.\""]], [["The boys went on.", "소년들은 계속 걸어갔다."]], [["“That was a nice peasant,”", "\"괜찮은 농부였어.\""], ["Kolya observed to Smurov.", "콜랴가 스무로프에게 말했다."], ["“I like talking to the peasants, and am always glad to do them justice.”", "\"난 농부들하고 얘기하는 게 좋아. 늘 기꺼이 그들을 공정하게 대접하려고 하지.\""]], [["“Why did you tell a lie, pretending we are thrashed?”", "\"근데 왜 거짓말했어, 우리가 매 맞는 척하면서?\""], ["asked Smurov.", "스무로프가 물었다."]], [["“I had to say that to please him.”", "\"그 사람을 흐뭇하게 해 주려고 그래야 했어.\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["“You know, Smurov, I don’t like being asked the same thing twice.", "\"있잖아, 스무로프, 난 같은 걸 두 번 묻는 게 싫어."], ["I like people to understand at the first word.", "사람들이 첫마디에 알아듣는 게 좋아."], ["Some things can’t be explained.", "어떤 건 설명이 안 돼."], ["According to a peasant’s notions, schoolboys are whipped, and must be whipped.", "농부의 관념대로라면 학생은 매를 맞고, 또 응당 맞아야 하는 거야."], ["What would a schoolboy be if he were not whipped?", "매도 안 맞는 학생이 무슨 학생이겠어?"], ["And if I were to tell him we are not, he’d be disappointed.", "우린 안 맞는다고 했다면 그 사람은 실망했을 거라고."], ["But you don’t understand that.", "하지만 넌 그걸 이해 못 해."], ["One has to know how to talk to the peasants.”", "농부들하곤 어떻게 얘기해야 하는지 알아야 하는 거야.\""]], [["“Only don’t tease them, please, or you’ll get into another scrape as you did about that goose.”", "\"제발 놀려 먹지만 마, 안 그러면 그 거위 때처럼 또 무슨 사달이 날 테니까.\""]], [["“So you’re afraid?”", "\"그래서 겁이 나?\""]], [["“Don’t laugh, Kolya.", "\"웃지 마, 콜랴."], ["Of course I’m afraid.", "당연히 겁나지."], ["My father would be awfully cross.", "우리 아빠가 엄청 화내실 거야."], ["I am strictly forbidden to go out with you.”", "너랑 같이 나다니는 건 엄하게 금지당했단 말이야.\""]], [["“Don’t be uneasy, nothing will happen this time.", "\"걱정 마, 이번엔 아무 일도 없을 거야."], ["Hallo, Natasha!”", "어이, 나타샤!\""], ["he shouted to a market woman in one of the booths.", "그는 좌판에 있던 한 장터 아낙에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Call me Natasha!", "\"누구더러 나타샤래!"], ["What next!", "별꼴이야!"], ["My name is Marya,”", "내 이름은 마리야다.\""], ["the middle-aged market woman shouted at him.", "중년의 장터 아낙이 그에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“I am so glad it’s Marya.", "\"마리야라니 정말 다행이네요."], ["Good-by!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["“Ah, you young rascal!", "\"아이고, 이 망나니 같은 놈아!"], ["A brat like you to carry on so!”", "코흘리개 같은 게 까불기는!\""]], [["“I’m in a hurry.", "\"바빠서요."], ["I can’t stay now.", "지금은 못 있어요."], ["You shall tell me next Sunday.”", "다음 일요일에 얘기해 줘요.\""], ["Kolya waved his hand at her, as though she had attacked him and not he her.", "콜랴는, 마치 자기가 아낙을 건드린 게 아니라 아낙이 자기를 건드리기라도 한 것처럼 그녀에게 손을 흔들었다."]], [["“I’ve nothing to tell you next Sunday.", "\"다음 일요일에 너한테 해 줄 얘기 따위 없다."], ["You set upon me, you impudent young monkey.", "네놈이 시비를 걸었지, 이 뻔뻔한 원숭이 새끼야."], ["I didn’t say anything,”", "난 아무 말도 안 했어.\""], ["bawled Marya.", "마리야가 악을 썼다."], ["“You want a whipping, that’s what you want, you saucy jackanapes!”", "\"넌 매가 필요해, 그게 네놈한테 필요한 거야, 이 건방진 풋내기 녀석!\""]], [["There was a roar of laughter among the other market women round her.", "그녀를 둘러싼 다른 장터 아낙들 사이에서 웃음이 와르르 터졌다."], ["Suddenly a man in a violent rage darted out from the arcade of shops close by.", "그때 별안간 한 사내가 가까운 가게 회랑에서 잔뜩 성이 나서 튀어나왔다."], ["He was a young man, not a native of the town, with dark, curly hair and a long, pale face, marked with smallpox.", "그는 젊은 사내로, 이 읍내 토박이는 아니었으며, 검고 곱슬곱슬한 머리에 길고 창백한 얼굴엔 마맛자국이 있었다."], ["He wore a long blue coat and a peaked cap, and looked like a merchant’s clerk.", "긴 파란 외투를 입고 챙 달린 모자를 썼는데, 상인의 점원처럼 보였다."], ["He was in a state of stupid excitement and brandished his fist at Kolya.", "그는 어리석은 흥분에 휩싸여 콜랴를 향해 주먹을 휘둘렀다."]], [["“I know you!”", "\"네놈을 알아!\""], ["he cried angrily, “I know you!”", "그가 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"네놈을 안다고!\""]], [["Kolya stared at him.", "콜랴는 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["He could not recall when he could have had a row with the man.", "자기가 이 사내와 언제 다툰 적이 있었는지 도무지 떠오르지 않았다."], ["But he had been in so many rows in the street that he could hardly remember them all.", "그러나 거리에서 하도 많은 다툼을 벌인 터라 그걸 다 기억하기란 어려운 노릇이었다."]], [["“Do you?”", "\"안다고요?\""], ["he asked sarcastically.", "그가 비꼬듯 물었다."]], [["“I know you!", "\"네놈을 알아!"], ["I know you!”", "네놈을 안다니까!\""], ["the man repeated idiotically.", "사내가 멍청하게 같은 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“So much the better for you.", "\"그거 잘됐네요."], ["Well, it’s time I was going.", "그럼, 난 이제 가 봐야겠어요."], ["Good-by!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["“You are at your saucy pranks again?”", "\"또 그 까부는 장난질이지?\""], ["cried the man.", "사내가 외쳤다."], ["“You are at your saucy pranks again?", "\"또 그 까부는 장난질이야?"], ["I know, you are at it again!”", "알아, 또 그 짓이지!\""]], [["“It’s not your business, brother, if I am at my saucy pranks again,”", "\"형씨, 내가 또 까부는 장난질을 하건 말건 그게 당신이 상관할 바는 아니죠.\""], ["said Kolya, standing still and scanning him.", "콜랴는 멈춰 서서 그를 찬찬히 훑어보며 말했다."]], [["“Not my business?”", "\"내가 상관할 바가 아니라고?\""]], [["“No; it’s not your business.”", "\"네, 당신이 상관할 바가 아니죠.\""]], [["“Whose then?", "\"그럼 누구 상관이야?"], ["Whose then?", "누구 상관?"], ["Whose then?”", "누구 상관이냐고?\""]], [["“It’s Trifon Nikititch’s business, not yours.”", "\"트리폰 니키티치가 상관할 바지 당신은 아니에요.\""]], [["“What Trifon Nikititch?”", "\"트리폰 니키티치가 누구야?\""], ["asked the youth, staring with loutish amazement at Kolya, but still as angry as ever.", "젊은 사내는 얼간이처럼 어리둥절해 콜랴를 빤히 쳐다보면서도 여전히 성이 나 있었다."]], [["Kolya scanned him gravely.", "콜랴는 진지하게 그를 훑어보았다."]], [["“Have you been to the Church of the Ascension?”", "\"승천 교회엔 가 봤어요?\""], ["he suddenly asked him, with stern emphasis.", "그가 갑자기 엄숙하게 힘주어 물었다."]], [["“What Church of Ascension?", "\"무슨 승천 교회? 뭣 하러?"], ["What for? No, I haven’t,”", "아니, 안 가 봤어.\""], ["said the young man, somewhat taken aback.", "젊은 사내가 좀 당황해서 대꾸했다."]], [["“Do you know Sabaneyev?”", "\"사바네예프는 알아요?\""], ["Kolya went on even more emphatically and even more severely.", "콜랴가 한층 더 힘주어, 한층 더 근엄하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“What Sabaneyev?", "\"무슨 사바네예프?"], ["No, I don’t know him.”", "아니, 난 그런 사람 몰라.\""]], [["“Well then you can go to the devil,”", "\"그럼 당신은 지옥에나 가시오.\""], ["said Kolya, cutting short the conversation; and turning sharply to the right he strode quickly on his way as though he disdained further conversation with a dolt who did not even know Sabaneyev.", "콜랴는 이렇게 대화를 끊어 버리고, 사바네예프조차 모르는 멍청이와 더 말을 섞는 건 자기 격에 안 맞는다는 듯 홱 오른쪽으로 돌아 잰걸음으로 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["“Stop, heigh!", "\"멈춰, 어이!"], ["What Sabaneyev?”", "무슨 사바네예프?\""], ["the young man recovered from his momentary stupefaction and was as excited as before.", "젊은 사내는 잠깐의 멍한 상태에서 깨어나 다시 전처럼 흥분했다."], ["“What did he say?”", "\"저 녀석이 뭐랬어?\""], ["He turned to the market women with a silly stare.", "그는 멍청한 눈초리로 장터 아낙들을 돌아보았다."]], [["The women laughed.", "아낙들이 웃었다."]], [["“You can never tell what he’s after,”", "\"저 애가 뭘 노리는지 누가 알겠어.\""], ["said one of them.", "그중 하나가 말했다."]], [["“What Sabaneyev is it he’s talking about?”", "\"대체 무슨 사바네예프 얘길 하는 거야?\""], ["the young man repeated, still furious and brandishing his right arm.", "젊은 사내는 여전히 길길이 날뛰며 오른팔을 휘둘러 대고 같은 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“It must be a Sabaneyev who worked for the Kuzmitchovs, that’s who it must be,”", "\"쿠즈미초프네 집에서 일하던 사바네예프겠지, 분명 그럴 거야.\""], ["one of the women suggested.", "아낙 하나가 넌지시 일러 주었다."]], [["The young man stared at her wildly.", "젊은 사내가 그녀를 거칠게 노려보았다."]], [["“For the Kuzmitchovs?”", "\"쿠즈미초프네 집?\""], ["repeated another woman.", "다른 아낙이 받았다."], ["“But his name wasn’t Trifon.", "\"근데 그 사람 이름은 트리폰이 아니었어."], ["His name’s Kuzma, not Trifon; but the boy said Trifon Nikititch, so it can’t be the same.”", "이름이 쿠즈마지 트리폰이 아니야. 그런데 저 애는 트리폰 니키티치라고 했으니, 같은 사람일 리가 없지.\""]], [["“His name is not Trifon and not Sabaneyev, it’s Tchizhov,”", "\"그 사람 이름은 트리폰도 사바네예프도 아니라 치조프야.\""], ["put in suddenly a third woman, who had hitherto been silent, listening gravely.", "여태 잠자코 진지하게 듣고만 있던 세 번째 아낙이 불쑥 끼어들었다."], ["“Alexey Ivanitch is his name.", "\"알렉세이 이바니치라는 사람이라고."], ["Tchizhov, Alexey Ivanitch.”", "치조프, 알렉세이 이바니치.\""]], [["“Not a doubt about it, it’s Tchizhov,”", "\"틀림없이 치조프야.\""], ["a fourth woman emphatically confirmed the statement.", "네 번째 아낙이 그 말을 힘주어 거들었다."]], [["The bewildered youth gazed from one to another.", "어리둥절해진 젊은 사내는 이 사람 저 사람을 번갈아 쳐다보았다."]], [["“But what did he ask for, what did he ask for, good people?”", "\"그래도 저 녀석이 뭘 물은 거요, 뭘 물은 거냐고요, 여러분?\""], ["he cried almost in desperation.", "그가 거의 절망에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["“‘Do you know Sabaneyev?’ says he.", "\"'사바네예프를 아느냐'고 했단 말이오."], ["And who the devil’s to know who is Sabaneyev?”", "사바네예프가 누군지 대체 누가 안담?\""]], [["“You’re a senseless fellow.", "\"이런 답답한 양반 같으니."], ["I tell you it’s not Sabaneyev, but Tchizhov, Alexey Ivanitch Tchizhov, that’s who it is!”", "사바네예프가 아니라 치조프, 알렉세이 이바니치 치조프라니까, 바로 그 사람이라고!\""], ["one of the women shouted at him impressively.", "아낙 하나가 그에게 인상 깊게 소리쳤다."]], [["“What Tchizhov?", "\"치조프가 누군데?"], ["Who is he?", "그게 누구냐고?"], ["Tell me, if you know.”", "알면 말해 봐요.\""]], [["“That tall, sniveling fellow who used to sit in the market in the summer.”", "\"여름이면 장터에 앉아 있던, 키 크고 코나 훌쩍거리던 그 사람 말이야.\""]], [["“And what’s your Tchizhov to do with me, good people, eh?”", "\"그래서 그 치조프인지 뭔지가 나하고 무슨 상관이오, 여러분, 엉?\""]], [["“How can I tell what he’s to do with you?” put in another.", "\"걔가 자네하고 무슨 상관인지 내가 어찌 알아?\""], ["“You ought to know yourself what you want with him, if you make such a clamor about him.", "또 다른 아낙이 끼어들었다. \"그 사람한테 뭘 바라는지는 자네가 알아야지, 그렇게 야단법석을 떨면서."], ["He spoke to you, he did not speak to us, you stupid.", "그 애가 자네한테 말한 거지 우리한테 말한 게 아니잖아, 이 멍청아."], ["Don’t you really know him?”", "정말로 그 사람 몰라?\""]], [["“Know whom?”", "\"누굴 몰라요?\""]], [["“Tchizhov.”", "\"치조프 말이야.\""]], [["“The devil take Tchizhov and you with him.", "\"치조프고 너희들이고 다 지옥에나 가라지."], ["I’ll give him a hiding, that I will.", "내가 그놈을 흠씬 패 주겠어, 꼭 그러고 말겠어."], ["He was laughing at me!”", "그놈이 날 비웃었어!\""]], [["“Will give Tchizhov a hiding!", "\"치조프를 흠씬 패 줘?"], ["More likely he will give you one.", "오히려 그 사람이 자넬 패 주겠지."], ["You are a fool, that’s what you are!”", "자넨 바보야, 그게 바로 자네라고!\""]], [["“Not Tchizhov, not Tchizhov, you spiteful, mischievous woman.", "\"치조프가 아니야, 치조프가 아니라고, 이 심술궂고 못된 여편네야."], ["I’ll give the boy a hiding.", "그 애새끼를 흠씬 패 주겠어."], ["Catch him, catch him, he was laughing at me!”", "저놈 잡아라, 잡아, 저놈이 날 비웃었어!\""]], [["The woman guffawed.", "아낙이 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["But Kolya was by now a long way off, marching along with a triumphant air.", "그러나 콜랴는 이미 한참 멀리 가서 의기양양한 태도로 걸어가고 있었다."], ["Smurov walked beside him, looking round at the shouting group far behind.", "스무로프는 그 곁에서 걸으며 저 멀리 뒤편에서 아우성치는 무리를 돌아보았다."], ["He too was in high spirits, though he was still afraid of getting into some scrape in Kolya’s company.", "그 역시 신이 났지만, 콜랴와 어울리다 무슨 사달에 휘말릴까 여전히 겁이 났다."]], [["“What Sabaneyev did you mean?”", "\"무슨 사바네예프 말이었어?\""], ["he asked Kolya, foreseeing what his answer would be.", "그는 어떤 대답이 나올지 짐작하면서 콜랴에게 물었다."]], [["“How do I know?", "\"내가 어찌 알아?"], ["Now there’ll be a hubbub among them all day.", "이제 저들 사이에선 온종일 한바탕 소동이 벌어질 거야."], ["I like to stir up fools in every class of society.", "난 어느 계층에서든 바보들을 들쑤셔 놓는 게 좋아."], ["There’s another blockhead, that peasant there.", "저기 또 다른 멍청이가 있군, 저 농부 말이야."], ["You know, they say ‘there’s no one stupider than a stupid Frenchman,’ but a stupid Russian shows it in his face just as much.", "너도 알지, '멍청한 프랑스인보다 더 멍청한 자는 없다'는 말이 있잖아. 그런데 멍청한 러시아인도 얼굴에 똑같이 다 드러난단 말이야."], ["Can’t you see it all over his face that he is a fool, that peasant, eh?”", "저 농부가 바보라는 게 얼굴에 온통 다 쓰여 있는 거 안 보여, 엉?\""]], [["“Let him alone, Kolya.", "\"내버려 둬, 콜랴."], ["Let’s go on.”", "그냥 가자.\""]], [["“Nothing could stop me, now I am once off.", "\"한번 시작한 이상 아무것도 날 못 말려."], ["Hey, good morning, peasant!”", "어이, 안녕하시오, 농부 양반!\""]], [["A sturdy-looking peasant, with a round, simple face and grizzled beard, who was walking by, raised his head and looked at the boy.", "마침 곁을 지나가던, 둥글고 순박한 얼굴에 희끗희끗한 수염을 기른 건장한 농부가 고개를 들어 소년을 바라보았다."], ["He seemed not quite sober.", "그는 그다지 멀쩡한 정신은 아닌 듯했다."]], [["“Good morning, if you are not laughing at me,”", "\"안녕하신가, 자네가 날 비웃는 게 아니라면 말일세.\""], ["he said deliberately in reply.", "그가 천천히 대꾸했다."]], [["“And if I am?”", "\"비웃는 거라면요?\""], ["laughed Kolya.", "콜랴가 웃었다."]], [["“Well, a joke’s a joke.", "\"뭐, 농담은 농담이니까."], ["Laugh away.", "실컷 웃게."], ["I don’t mind.", "난 상관없어."], ["There’s no harm in a joke.”", "농담에 해될 거야 없지.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, brother, it was a joke.”", "\"미안해요, 형씨, 농담이었어요.\""]], [["“Well, God forgive you!”", "\"뭐, 하느님이 자넬 용서하시길!\""]], [["“Do you forgive me, too?”", "\"당신도 날 용서해 줘요?\""]], [["“I quite forgive you.", "\"기꺼이 용서하지."], ["Go along.”", "어서 가게.\""]], [["“I say, you seem a clever peasant.”", "\"이거 참, 당신 똑똑한 농부 같은데요.\""]], [["“Cleverer than you,”", "\"자네보단 똑똑하지.\""], ["the peasant answered unexpectedly, with the same gravity.", "농부가 뜻밖에도 여전히 진지한 얼굴로 대답했다."]], [["“I doubt it,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["said Kolya, somewhat taken aback.", "콜랴가 좀 당황해서 말했다."]], [["“It’s true, though.”", "\"그래도 사실인걸.\""]], [["“Perhaps it is.”", "\"그럴지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“It is, brother.”", "\"그렇다니까, 형씨.\""]], [["“Good-by, peasant!”", "\"잘 가요, 농부 양반!\""]], [["“Good-by!”", "\"잘 가게!\""]], [["“There are all sorts of peasants,”", "\"농부에도 별의별 농부가 다 있어.\""], ["Kolya observed to Smurov after a brief silence.", "콜랴가 잠깐 입을 다물고 있다가 스무로프에게 말했다."], ["“How could I tell I had hit on a clever one?", "\"내가 하필 똑똑한 농부를 골랐을 줄 어찌 알았겠어?"], ["I am always ready to recognize intelligence in the peasantry.”", "난 언제든 농민층의 지성을 인정할 준비가 돼 있어.\""]], [["In the distance the cathedral clock struck half-past eleven.", "저 멀리서 대성당의 시계가 열한 시 반을 쳤다."], ["The boys made haste and they walked as far as Captain Snegiryov’s lodging, a considerable distance, quickly and almost in silence.", "소년들은 발걸음을 서둘러, 제법 먼 스네기료프 대위의 거처까지 빠르게, 거의 말없이 걸어갔다."], ["Twenty paces from the house Kolya stopped and told Smurov to go on ahead and ask Karamazov to come out to him.", "그 집에서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 콜랴는 멈춰 서더니, 스무로프에게 먼저 가서 카라마조프더러 나와 달라고 청하라고 일렀다."]], [["“One must sniff round a bit first,”", "\"먼저 좀 냄새를 맡아 봐야지.\""], ["he observed to Smurov.", "그가 스무로프에게 한마디 했다."]], [["“Why ask him to come out?”", "\"뭣 하러 나오라고 해?\""], ["Smurov protested.", "스무로프가 항변했다."], ["“You go in; they will be awfully glad to see you.", "\"그냥 들어가. 다들 널 보면 엄청 기뻐할 거야."], ["What’s the sense of making friends in the frost out here?”", "이 추위에 여기서 화해하는 게 무슨 소용이야?\""]], [["“I know why I want to see him out here in the frost,”", "\"이 추위에 여기 밖에서 그 사람을 보려는 데엔 내 나름의 이유가 있어.\""], ["Kolya cut him short in the despotic tone he was fond of adopting with “small boys,” and Smurov ran to do his bidding.", "콜랴는 '꼬맹이들'을 상대할 때 즐겨 쓰던 그 거만한 어조로 그의 말을 잘랐고, 스무로프는 그가 시키는 대로 하려고 달려갔다."]], [["# Chapter IV. The Lost Dog", "# Chapter IV. The Lost Dog"]], [["Kolya leaned against the fence with an air of dignity, waiting for Alyosha to appear.", "콜랴는 위엄을 갖춘 태도로 울타리에 기댄 채 알료샤가 나타나기를 기다렸다."], ["Yes, he had long wanted to meet him.", "그렇다, 그는 오래전부터 그를 만나고 싶었다."], ["He had heard a great deal about him from the boys, but hitherto he had always maintained an appearance of disdainful indifference when he was mentioned, and he had even “criticized” what he heard about Alyosha.", "아이들에게서 그에 관한 이야기를 숱하게 들어 왔으나, 지금껏 그의 이름이 입에 오르면 늘 경멸 어린 무관심을 가장했고, 알료샤에 관해 들은 바를 두고 \"비판\"까지 곁들이곤 했다."], ["But secretly he had a great longing to make his acquaintance; there was something sympathetic and attractive in all he was told about Alyosha.", "그러나 속으로는 그와 알고 지내고 싶은 마음이 간절했으니, 알료샤에 관해 들은 모든 이야기에는 무언가 마음을 끄는, 호감 가는 데가 있었던 것이다. 그러니 지금 이 순간이 중요했다."], ["So the present moment was important: to begin with, he had to show himself at his best, to show his independence, “Or he’ll think of me as thirteen and take me for a boy, like the rest of them.", "우선 그는 자신의 가장 좋은 모습을 보여 주어야 했고, 자신의 독립성을 내보여야 했다. \"안 그러면 저 사람은 나를 열세 살짜리로 여기고, 다른 사람들처럼 어린애 취급을 하겠지."], ["And what are these boys to him?", "게다가 저 애들이 저 사람한테 다 뭐란 말인가?"], ["I shall ask him when I get to know him.", "친해지면 물어봐야겠다."], ["It’s a pity I am so short, though.", "그나저나 내가 이렇게 키가 작은 건 안타깝군."], ["Tuzikov is younger than I am, yet he is half a head taller.", "투지코프는 나보다 어린데도 머리통 반쯤은 더 크지 않은가."], ["But I have a clever face.", "하지만 나는 영리한 얼굴을 가졌어."], ["I am not good-looking.", "잘생기진 않았지."], ["I know I’m hideous, but I’ve a clever face.", "내가 못생긴 줄은 알지만, 영리한 얼굴이야."], ["I mustn’t talk too freely; if I fall into his arms all at once, he may think—Tfoo!", "너무 함부로 떠들어선 안 되겠다. 단번에 그의 품에 와락 안기기라도 하면 저 사람이 생각하기를—체!"], ["how horrible if he should think—!”", "저 사람이 그렇게 생각한다면 얼마나 끔찍한가—!\""]], [["Such were the thoughts that excited Kolya while he was doing his utmost to assume the most independent air.", "그런 생각들이, 더없이 독립적인 태도를 갖추려고 안간힘을 쓰는 콜랴의 마음을 들끓게 했다."], ["What distressed him most was his being so short; he did not mind so much his “hideous”", "그를 가장 괴롭히는 것은 키가 작다는 점이었으니, \"못생긴\" 얼굴은 키가 작은 것만큼 신경 쓰이지 않았다."], ["face, as being so short. On the wall in a corner at home he had the year before made a pencil-mark to show his height, and every two months since he anxiously measured himself against it to see how much he had gained.", "작년에 그는 집 한구석 벽에다 연필로 제 키를 표시해 두었고, 그 뒤로 두 달마다 한 번씩 거기에 자기 키를 대 보며 얼마나 자랐는지 초조하게 재 보곤 했다."], ["But alas!", "그러나 아아!"], ["he grew very slowly, and this sometimes reduced him almost to despair.", "그는 너무도 더디게 자랐고, 그 때문에 더러는 절망에 가까운 심정에 빠지기까지 했다. 그의 얼굴은 실상 결코 \"못생긴\""], ["His face was in reality by no means “hideous”; on the contrary, it was rather attractive, with a fair, pale skin, freckled.", "것이 아니었으니, 오히려 제법 매력적이었다. 희고 창백한 살갗에 주근깨가 박혀 있었다."], ["His small, lively gray eyes had a fearless look, and often glowed with feeling.", "작고 생기 넘치는 잿빛 두 눈은 겁이 없어 보였고, 더러는 감정으로 불타올랐다."], ["He had rather high cheekbones; small, very red, but not very thick, lips; his nose was small and unmistakably turned up.", "광대뼈는 다소 높은 편이었고, 입술은 작고 무척 붉으나 그다지 두텁지는 않았으며, 코는 자그마하면서 영락없이 위로 들려 있었다."], ["“I’ve a regular pug nose, a regular pug nose,”", "\"내 코는 영락없는 들창코야, 영락없는 들창코라니까.\""], ["Kolya used to mutter to himself when he looked in the looking-glass, and he always left it with indignation.", "콜랴는 거울을 들여다볼 때면 이렇게 혼잣말로 중얼거렸고, 늘 분개하며 거울 앞을 떠났다."], ["“But perhaps I haven’t got a clever face?”", "\"그런데 어쩌면 나는 영리한 얼굴마저 없는 게 아닐까?\""], ["he sometimes thought, doubtful even of that.", "그는 때때로 그것마저 의심하며 이렇게 생각하기도 했다."], ["But it must not be supposed that his mind was preoccupied with his face and his height.", "그러나 그의 마음이 제 얼굴과 키에만 사로잡혀 있었다고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["On the contrary, however bitter the moments before the looking-glass were to him, he quickly forgot them, and forgot them for a long time, “abandoning himself entirely to ideas and to real life,”", "오히려 거울 앞에서 보내는 순간들이 그에게 아무리 쓰라렸더라도, 그는 그것을 금세 잊었고, 오래도록 잊었으니, 그 자신이 스스로 표현한 바와 같이 \"온통 사상과 실제 삶에 자신을 내맡겼던\""], ["as he formulated it to himself.", "것이다."]], [["Alyosha came out quickly and hastened up to Kolya.", "알료샤가 빠른 걸음으로 나와 콜랴에게 서둘러 다가왔다."], ["Before he reached him, Kolya could see that he looked delighted.", "그에게 다다르기도 전에, 콜랴는 그가 기뻐하는 얼굴이라는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["“Can he be so glad to see me?”", "\"나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워한단 말인가?\""], ["Kolya wondered, feeling pleased.", "콜랴는 흐뭇한 기분이 들어 의아해했다."], ["We may note here, in passing, that Alyosha’s appearance had undergone a complete change since we saw him last.", "여기서 잠깐 짚고 넘어가자면, 알료샤의 모습은 우리가 마지막으로 그를 보았을 때와 견주어 완전히 달라져 있었다."], ["He had abandoned his cassock and was wearing now a well-cut coat, a soft, round hat, and his hair had been cropped short.", "그는 수도복을 벗고, 이제는 잘 재단된 외투에 부드럽고 둥근 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 머리도 짧게 깎은 차림이었다."], ["All this was very becoming to him, and he looked quite handsome.", "이 모든 것이 그에게 무척 잘 어울려, 그는 제법 잘생겨 보였다."], ["His charming face always had a good-humored expression; but there was a gentleness and serenity in his good-humor.", "그 매력적인 얼굴에는 언제나 쾌활한 표정이 어려 있었으나, 그 쾌활함에는 부드러움과 평온함이 깃들어 있었다."], ["To Kolya’s surprise, Alyosha came out to him just as he was, without an overcoat.", "콜랴가 놀랍게도, 알료샤는 외투조차 걸치지 않은 차림 그대로 그에게 나왔다."], ["He had evidently come in haste.", "보아하니 그는 급히 나온 모양이었다."], ["He held out his hand to Kolya at once.", "그는 곧장 콜랴에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Here you are at last!", "\"드디어 왔구나!"], ["How anxious we’ve been to see you!”", "우리가 얼마나 너를 보고 싶어 했는지 모른다!\""]], [["“There were reasons which you shall know directly.", "\"곧 알게 되실 사정이 좀 있었어요."], ["Anyway, I am glad to make your acquaintance.", "어쨌든 알게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["I’ve long been hoping for an opportunity, and have heard a great deal about you,”", "오래전부터 기회를 바라 왔고, 형님에 관한 이야기도 무척 많이 들었어요.\""], ["Kolya muttered, a little breathless.", "콜랴가 다소 숨을 헐떡이며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“We should have met anyway.", "\"우리야 어차피 만났을 거야."], ["I’ve heard a great deal about you, too; but you’ve been a long time coming here.”", "나도 네 이야기를 많이 들었거든. 그런데 너는 여기 오는 데 시간이 꽤 걸렸구나.\""]], [["“Tell me, how are things going?”", "\"말씀해 주세요, 사정이 어떻게 돌아가고 있나요?\""]], [["“Ilusha is very ill.", "\"일류샤가 무척 위중하단다."], ["He is certainly dying.”", "틀림없이 죽어 가고 있어.\""]], [["“How awful!", "\"끔찍하군요!"], ["You must admit that medicine is a fraud, Karamazov,”", "의학이라는 게 사기라는 걸 형님도 인정하셔야 합니다, 카라마조프.\""], ["cried Kolya warmly.", "콜랴가 격하게 외쳤다."]], [["“Ilusha has mentioned you often, very often, even in his sleep, in delirium, you know.", "\"일류샤가 네 이야기를 자주, 아주 자주 했어. 잠결에도, 헛소리 속에서도 말이야."], ["One can see that you used to be very, very dear to him ...", "네가 그 애한테 아주, 아주 소중한 사람이었다는 걸 알 수 있더구나…"], ["before the incident ...", "그 사건이 있기 전까지는…"], ["with the knife....", "그 칼 사건 말이야…."], ["Then there’s another reason....", "그리고 또 다른 이유도 있고…."], ["Tell me, is that your dog?”", "말해 봐, 저게 네 개냐?\""]], [["“Yes, Perezvon.”", "\"네, 페레즈본이에요.\""]], [["“Not Zhutchka?”", "\"주치카가 아니고?\""], ["Alyosha looked at Kolya with eyes full of pity.", "알료샤가 가엾음이 가득한 눈으로 콜랴를 바라보았다."], ["“Is she lost for ever?”", "\"그 개는 영영 사라진 거니?\""]], [["“I know you would all like it to be Zhutchka.", "\"형님들 모두 그게 주치카이기를 바란다는 거 알아요."], ["I’ve heard all about it.”", "그 이야긴 죄다 들었어요.\""], ["Kolya smiled mysteriously.", "콜랴가 수수께끼처럼 빙긋 웃었다."], ["“Listen, Karamazov, I’ll tell you all about it.", "\"들어 보세요, 카라마조프, 제가 그 일을 죄다 말씀드릴게요."], ["That’s what I came for; that’s what I asked you to come out here for, to explain the whole episode to you before we go in,”", "그러려고 온 거예요. 안으로 들어가기 전에 그 사건 전부를 설명해 드리려고, 형님더러 여기 나와 달라고 한 거예요.\""], ["he began with animation.", "그가 활기를 띠며 말문을 열었다."], ["“You see, Karamazov, Ilusha came into the preparatory class last spring.", "\"그러니까요, 카라마조프, 일류샤는 지난봄에 예비반에 들어왔어요."], ["Well, you know what our preparatory class is—a lot of small boys.", "우리 예비반이 어떤지 아시잖아요—꼬마들 천지죠."], ["They began teasing Ilusha at once.", "애들이 곧장 일류샤를 놀려 대기 시작했어요."], ["I am two classes higher up, and, of course, I only look on at them from a distance.", "저는 두 학년 위라서, 당연히 멀찍이서 그 애들을 지켜보기만 했죠."], ["I saw the boy was weak and small, but he wouldn’t give in to them; he fought with them.", "그 애가 약하고 작은데도 애들한테 굴복하지 않는 걸 봤어요. 맞서 싸우더군요."], ["I saw he was proud, and his eyes were full of fire.", "자존심이 강하고, 눈에는 불꽃이 가득하다는 걸 알아봤죠."], ["I like children like that.", "저는 그런 아이들이 좋거든요."], ["And they teased him all the more.", "그런데 애들은 그럴수록 더 놀려 댔어요."], ["The worst of it was he was horribly dressed at the time, his breeches were too small for him, and there were holes in his boots.", "무엇보다 고약했던 건, 그 애가 그때 형편없는 옷차림이었다는 거예요. 바지는 너무 작아 끼었고, 장화에는 구멍이 나 있었죠."], ["They worried him about it; they jeered at him.", "애들이 그걸 가지고 그 애를 들볶고 비웃었어요."], ["That I can’t stand.", "저는 그런 꼴은 못 참아요."], ["I stood up for him at once, and gave it to them hot.", "곧장 그 애 편을 들어, 애들한테 따끔하게 본때를 보여 줬죠."], ["I beat them, but they adore me, do you know, Karamazov?”", "애들을 때려 줬는데, 그래도 걔들은 저를 떠받든답니다, 아세요, 카라마조프?\""], ["Kolya boasted impulsively; “but I am always fond of children.", "콜랴가 충동적으로 으스댔다. \"저는 늘 아이들을 좋아하거든요."], ["I’ve two chickens in my hands at home now—that’s what detained me to-day.", "지금 집에는 제 손에 병아리 두 마리가 있는데—그것 때문에 오늘 늦은 거예요."], ["So they left off beating Ilusha and I took him under my protection.", "어쨌든 그래서 애들이 일류샤 때리는 걸 그만뒀고, 제가 그 애를 보호 아래 두게 됐죠. 그 애가 자존심이 강하다는 걸 알아봤어요."], ["I saw the boy was proud.", "정말이지 그 애는 자존심이 강했어요."], ["I tell you that, the boy was proud; but in the end he became slavishly devoted to me: he did my slightest bidding, obeyed me as though I were God, tried to copy me.", "그런데도 결국엔 저한테 노예처럼 헌신하게 됐죠. 제 사소한 분부도 다 따랐고, 저를 하느님처럼 떠받들었고, 저를 흉내 내려고 했어요."], ["In the intervals between the classes he used to run to me at once, and I’d go about with him.", "수업 사이사이엔 곧장 제게 달려왔고, 저는 그 애를 데리고 돌아다녔어요."], ["On Sundays, too.", "일요일에도 그랬죠."], ["They always laugh when an older boy makes friends with a younger one like that; but that’s a prejudice.", "윗학년 애가 그렇게 아랫학년 애랑 친구가 되면 다들 비웃지만, 그건 편견이에요."], ["If it’s my fancy, that’s enough.", "제 마음이 그렇다면, 그걸로 충분한 거죠."], ["I am teaching him, developing him.", "저는 그 애를 가르치고, 키워 주고 있어요."], ["Why shouldn’t I develop him if I like him?", "제가 그 애를 좋아하는데 왜 못 키워 줘요?"], ["Here you, Karamazov, have taken up with all these nestlings.", "자, 카라마조프, 형님도 저 어린것들을 전부 떠맡으셨잖아요."], ["I see you want to influence the younger generation—to develop them, to be of use to them, and I assure you this trait in your character, which I knew by hearsay, attracted me more than anything.", "형님이 젊은 세대에 영향을 끼치고 싶어 하는 걸 알겠어요—그들을 키워 주고, 그들에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶으신 거죠. 단언컨대 풍문으로만 알던 형님 성격의 그런 점이, 무엇보다도 저를 끌어당겼어요."], ["Let us get to the point, though.", "그건 그렇고, 본론으로 들어가죠."], ["I noticed that there was a sort of softness and sentimentality coming over the boy, and you know I have a positive hatred of this sheepish sentimentality, and I have had it from a baby.", "그 애한테 일종의 물러 터진 감상벽 같은 게 스며드는 걸 알아챘는데, 아시다시피 저는 그런 양 같은 감상벽을 정말이지 질색해요. 어릴 적부터 그랬죠."], ["There were contradictions in him, too: he was proud, but he was slavishly devoted to me, and yet all at once his eyes would flash and he’d refuse to agree with me; he’d argue, fly into a rage.", "그 애한테는 모순도 있었어요. 자존심이 강하면서도 저한테는 노예처럼 헌신했는데, 그러다가도 별안간 눈을 번뜩이며 제 말에 동의하길 거부하곤 했어요."], ["I used sometimes to propound certain ideas; I could see that it was not so much that he disagreed with the ideas, but that he was simply rebelling against me, because I was cool in responding to his endearments.", "따지고 들고, 버럭 화를 냈죠. 제가 가끔 어떤 사상들을 펼쳐 보이면, 그게 사상에 동의하지 않아서라기보다 그저 저한테 반항하는 거라는 걸 알 수 있었어요."], ["And so, in order to train him properly, the tenderer he was, the colder I became.", "제가 그 애의 살가운 표현에 냉담하게 대했기 때문이죠. 그래서 그 애를 제대로 길들이려고, 그 애가 다정하게 굴수록 저는 더 차갑게 굴었어요."], ["I did it on purpose: that was my idea.", "일부러 그런 거예요. 그게 제 생각이었죠."], ["My object was to form his character, to lick him into shape, to make a man of him ...", "제 목적은 그 애의 성격을 빚어 내고, 다듬어서, 한 사람의 사내로 만드는 거였어요…"], ["and besides ...", "게다가…"], ["no doubt, you understand me at a word.", "한마디면 형님은 분명 제 말을 알아들으시겠죠."], ["Suddenly I noticed for three days in succession he was downcast and dejected, not because of my coldness, but for something else, something more important.", "그러던 어느 날, 사흘 내리 그 애가 풀이 죽어 침울해하는 걸 알아챘어요. 제 냉담함 때문이 아니라 다른 무언가 때문에, 더 중대한 무언가 때문에요."], ["I wondered what the tragedy was.", "무슨 비극이 있나 싶었죠."], ["I have pumped him and found out that he had somehow got to know Smerdyakov, who was footman to your late father—it was before his death, of course—and he taught the little fool a silly trick—that is, a brutal, nasty trick.", "캐물어서 알아냈는데, 그 애가 어쩌다 스메르댜코프와 알게 됐다는 거예요. 형님 돌아가신 아버님 댁 하인 말이에요—물론 그분이 돌아가시기 전 일이죠—그자가 그 어린 바보한테 못된 장난을 가르쳤더라고요."], ["He told him to take a piece of bread, to stick a pin in it, and throw it to one of those hungry dogs who snap up anything without biting it, and then to watch and see what would happen.", "그러니까, 잔인하고 더러운 장난이죠. 빵 한 조각을 가져다 거기 핀을 찔러 넣은 다음, 아무거나 씹지도 않고 덥석 삼키는 굶주린 개한테 던져 주고는, 무슨 일이 벌어지나 지켜보라고 한 거예요."], ["So they prepared a piece of bread like that and threw it to Zhutchka, that shaggy dog there’s been such a fuss about.", "그래서 그렇게 빵 한 조각을 마련해서, 그렇게 한바탕 소동을 일으켰던 그 주치카라는 텁수룩한 개한테 던져 줬어요."], ["The people of the house it belonged to never fed it at all, though it barked all day.", "그 개가 딸린 집 사람들은 종일 짖어 대는데도 통 먹이를 안 줬거든요."], ["(Do you like that stupid barking, Karamazov?", "(형님은 그런 멍청한 짖는 소리가 좋으세요, 카라마조프?"], ["I can’t stand it.) So it rushed at the bread, swallowed it, and began to squeal; it turned round and round and ran away, squealing as it ran out of sight.", "저는 못 참아요.) 그래서 그 개가 빵으로 달려들어 꿀꺽 삼키더니, 깨갱거리기 시작했어요. 빙빙 돌다가, 깨갱거리며 달아나서는 시야에서 사라졌죠."], ["That was Ilusha’s own account of it.", "그게 일류샤가 직접 들려준 이야기예요."], ["He confessed it to me, and cried bitterly.", "그 애가 저한테 고백하면서 서럽게 울었어요."], ["He hugged me, shaking all over.", "온몸을 부들부들 떨며 저를 끌어안았죠."], ["He kept on repeating ‘He ran away squealing’: the sight of that haunted him.", "'그 개가 깨갱거리며 달아났어'라고 자꾸만 되뇌었어요. 그 광경이 그 애를 떠나지 않았던 거예요."], ["He was tormented by remorse, I could see that.", "죄책감에 시달리고 있었어요. 그게 보였죠."], ["I took it seriously.", "저는 그걸 심각하게 받아들였어요."], ["I determined to give him a lesson for other things as well.", "다른 일들까지 곁들여 그 애한테 따끔한 교훈을 주기로 마음먹었죠."], ["So I must confess I wasn’t quite straightforward, and pretended to be more indignant perhaps than I was.", "그래서 솔직히 말하면, 제가 완전히 정직하지는 않았고, 실제보다 더 분개한 척했을지도 몰라요."], ["‘You’ve done a nasty thing,’ I said, ‘you are a scoundrel.", "'넌 못된 짓을 한 거야,' 제가 말했죠, '넌 악당이야."], ["I won’t tell of it, of course, but I shall have nothing more to do with you for a time.", "물론 일러바치진 않겠지만, 한동안은 너랑 더는 상종하지 않겠어."], ["I’ll think it over and let you know through Smurov’—that’s the boy who’s just come with me; he’s always ready to do anything for me—‘whether I will have anything to do with you in the future or whether I give you up for good as a scoundrel.’ He was tremendously upset.", "생각해 보고 스무로프를 통해 알려 줄게'—스무로프는 방금 저랑 같이 온 애인데, 저를 위해서라면 뭐든 할 준비가 돼 있는 녀석이죠—'앞으로 너랑 상종할지, 아니면 악당이라고 영영 너를 버릴지 말이야.' 그 애는 엄청나게 충격을 받았어요."], ["I must own I felt I’d gone too far as I spoke, but there was no help for it.", "솔직히 말하면 제가 너무 심하게 굴었다는 느낌이 들긴 했지만, 어쩔 수 없었어요."], ["I did what I thought best at the time.", "그땐 그게 최선이라고 생각한 대로 한 거예요."], ["A day or two after, I sent Smurov to tell him that I would not speak to him again.", "하루 이틀 뒤에, 스무로프를 보내 다시는 그 애한테 말을 걸지 않겠다고 전했어요. 학교 친구 둘이 더는 서로 상종하지 않기로 할 때 우리가 그렇게 부르거든요."], ["That’s what we call it when two schoolfellows refuse to have anything more to do with one another.", "사실 속으로는 며칠만 그 애를 따돌렸다가, 뉘우치는 기색이 보이면 다시 손을 내밀 작정이었어요. 그게 제 의도였죠."], ["Secretly I only meant to send him to Coventry for a few days and then, if I saw signs of repentance, to hold out my hand to him again.", "그런데 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 아세요? 그 애가 스무로프의 전갈을 듣더니 눈을 번뜩였어요."], ["That was my intention.", "'크라소트킨한테 전해,'"], ["But what do you think happened?", "그 애가 소리쳤죠, '내가 핀 박은 빵을 개란 개한테 다 던져 줄 거라고—전부—전부 다한테!'"], ["He heard Smurov’s message, his eyes flashed.", "'아, 성깔 좀 부리는군. 저걸 좀 빼 줘야겠어.'"], ["‘Tell Krassotkin from me,’ he cried, ‘that I will throw bread with pins to all the dogs—all—all of them!’ ‘So he’s going in for a little temper.", "그래서 저는 그 애를 경멸하듯 대하기 시작했어요. 마주칠 때마다 외면하거나 비꼬듯 웃었죠."], ["We must smoke it out of him.’ And I began to treat him with contempt; whenever I met him I turned away or smiled sarcastically.", "그러던 바로 그 무렵에 그 애 아버지와 관련된 그 일이 벌어졌어요. 기억하시죠?"], ["And just then that affair with his father happened. You remember?", "그 애가 이미 벌어진 일로 끔찍하게 흥분해 있었다는 걸 아셔야 해요."], ["You must realize that he was fearfully worked up by what had happened already.", "애들은 제가 그 애를 버린 걸 보고 달려들어 약을 올렸어요. '수세미 같은 놈, 수세미 같은 놈!'"], ["The boys, seeing I’d given him up, set on him and taunted him, shouting, ‘Wisp of tow, wisp of tow!’ And he had soon regular skirmishes with them, which I am very sorry for.", "하고 외치면서요. 그러다 곧 그 애는 애들과 본격적으로 드잡이질을 벌이게 됐는데, 그게 저는 정말 안타까워요."], ["They seem to have given him one very bad beating.", "애들이 그 애를 한 번 아주 호되게 두들겨 팼던 모양이에요."], ["One day he flew at them all as they were coming out of school.", "어느 날 그 애가 학교에서 나오는 애들 모두한테 달려들었어요."], ["I stood a few yards off, looking on.", "저는 몇 걸음 떨어진 데 서서 지켜보고 있었죠."], ["And, I swear, I don’t remember that I laughed; it was quite the other way, I felt awfully sorry for him, in another minute I would have run up to take his part.", "그리고, 맹세컨대, 제가 웃었던 기억은 없어요. 오히려 정반대로, 그 애가 너무 가여워서, 조금만 더 있었으면 달려가 그 애 편을 들어 줬을 거예요."], ["But he suddenly met my eyes.", "그런데 그 애가 별안간 제 눈과 마주쳤어요."], ["I don’t know what he fancied; but he pulled out a penknife, rushed at me, and struck at my thigh, here in my right leg.", "그 애가 뭘 떠올렸는지 모르겠어요. 어쨌든 주머니칼을 꺼내 저한테 달려들더니, 제 허벅지를, 여기 오른쪽 다리를 찔렀어요."], ["I didn’t move.", "저는 꿈쩍도 안 했어요."], ["I don’t mind owning I am plucky sometimes, Karamazov.", "솔직히 말하면 저도 가끔은 배짱이 두둑하답니다, 카라마조프. 저는 그저 그 애를 경멸하듯 바라봤어요."], ["I simply looked at him contemptuously, as though to say, ‘This is how you repay all my kindness!", "마치 '이게 내 호의에 보답하는 꼴이냐? 하고 싶으면 또 해 봐, 얼마든지 받아 주마'"], ["Do it again, if you like, I’m at your service.’ But he didn’t stab me again; he broke down, he was frightened at what he had done, he threw away the knife, burst out crying, and ran away.", "하는 것처럼요. 그런데 그 애는 다시 찌르지 않았어요."], ["I did not sneak on him, of course, and I made them all keep quiet, so it shouldn’t come to the ears of the masters.", "무너져 내렸죠. 제가 저지른 짓에 겁을 먹고는 칼을 내던지더니 와락 울음을 터뜨리며 달아났어요."], ["I didn’t even tell my mother till it had healed up.", "물론 저는 그 애를 일러바치지 않았고, 선생님들 귀에 들어가지 않게 다들 입을 다물게 했어요."], ["And the wound was a mere scratch.", "어머니한테도 상처가 아물 때까지 말하지 않았죠."], ["And then I heard that the same day he’d been throwing stones and had bitten your finger—but you understand now what a state he was in!", "게다가 그 상처는 그저 긁힌 정도였어요. 그러고 나서 그 애가 같은 날 돌을 던지고 형님 손가락을 물었다는 이야기를 들었는데—이제 형님도 그 애가 어떤 상태였는지 아시겠죠!"], ["Well, it can’t be helped: it was stupid of me not to come and forgive him—that is, to make it up with him—when he was taken ill.", "뭐, 어쩔 수 없죠. 그 애가 앓아누웠을 때 제가 찾아가 용서해 주지 않은 것—그러니까 화해하지 않은 것—은 어리석은 짓이었어요."], ["I am sorry for it now.", "지금은 후회해요."], ["But I had a special reason.", "하지만 저한테는 특별한 이유가 있었어요."], ["So now I’ve told you all about it ...", "자, 이제 다 말씀드렸어요…"], ["but I’m afraid it was stupid of me.”", "그래도 제가 어리석었던 것 같아 걱정이네요.\""]], [["“Oh, what a pity,”", "\"아, 안타깝구나.\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha, with feeling, “that I didn’t know before what terms you were on with him, or I’d have come to you long ago to beg you to go to him with me.", "알료샤가 감정을 담아 외쳤다. \"네가 그 애와 그런 사이였다는 걸 진작 알았더라면, 벌써 너를 찾아가 나와 함께 그 애한테 가 달라고 부탁했을 텐데."], ["Would you believe it, when he was feverish he talked about you in delirium.", "믿어지니, 그 애가 열에 들떠 헛소리 속에서 네 이야기를 했단다."], ["I didn’t know how much you were to him!", "네가 그 애한테 그렇게 소중한 사람인 줄은 몰랐어!"], ["And you’ve really not succeeded in finding that dog?", "그런데 너는 정말 그 개를 못 찾은 거니?"], ["His father and the boys have been hunting all over the town for it.", "그 애 아버지와 아이들이 온 읍내를 뒤지며 찾아다녔는데."], ["Would you believe it, since he’s been ill, I’ve three times heard him repeat with tears, ‘It’s because I killed Zhutchka, father, that I am ill now.", "믿어지니, 그 애가 앓아누운 뒤로 눈물을 흘리며 이렇게 되뇌는 걸 세 번이나 들었어. '아버지, 제가 주치카를 죽여서 지금 이렇게 앓는 거예요."], ["God is punishing me for it.’ He can’t get that idea out of his head.", "하느님이 그 벌을 내리시는 거예요.' 그 생각을 머릿속에서 떨치질 못해."], ["And if the dog were found and proved to be alive, one might almost fancy the joy would cure him.", "그러니 만약 그 개를 찾아내서 살아 있다는 게 밝혀진다면, 어쩌면 그 기쁨이 그 애를 낫게 하지 않을까 싶을 정도야."], ["We have all rested our hopes on you.”", "우리 모두 너한테 희망을 걸어 왔단다.\""]], [["“Tell me, what made you hope that I should be the one to find him?”", "\"말씀해 주세요, 어째서 제가 그 개를 찾아낼 사람이라고 기대하셨어요?\""], ["Kolya asked, with great curiosity.", "콜랴가 큰 호기심을 품고 물었다."], ["“Why did you reckon on me rather than any one else?”", "\"왜 다른 누구도 아닌 저를 믿으셨죠?\""]], [["“There was a report that you were looking for the dog, and that you would bring it when you’d found it.", "\"네가 그 개를 찾고 있고, 찾으면 데려올 거라는 소문이 있었거든."], ["Smurov said something of the sort.", "스무로프가 비슷한 말을 했어."], ["We’ve all been trying to persuade Ilusha that the dog is alive, that it’s been seen.", "우리 모두 일류샤한테 그 개가 살아 있다고, 누가 봤다고 설득하려 애써 왔단다."], ["The boys brought him a live hare; he just looked at it, with a faint smile, and asked them to set it free in the fields.", "아이들이 그 애한테 살아 있는 산토끼를 갖다줬는데, 그 애는 그저 희미하게 웃으며 바라보더니 들판에 풀어 주라고 하더구나."], ["And so we did.", "그래서 그렇게 했지."], ["His father has just this moment come back, bringing him a mastiff pup, hoping to comfort him with that; but I think it only makes it worse.”", "그 애 아버지가 방금 막 돌아왔어. 마스티프 강아지를 데려와서 그걸로 위로해 줄까 했는데, 내 생각엔 외려 더 나빠지기만 하는 것 같아.\""]], [["“Tell me, Karamazov, what sort of man is the father?", "\"말씀해 주세요, 카라마조프, 그 애 아버지는 어떤 사람이에요?"], ["I know him, but what do you make of him—a mountebank, a buffoon?”", "저도 알긴 하지만, 형님이 보시기엔 어떤가요—협잡꾼인가요, 어릿광대인가요?\""]], [["“Oh, no; there are people of deep feeling who have been somehow crushed.", "\"오, 그렇지 않아. 깊은 감정을 지니고도 어쩌다 짓밟혀 버린 사람들이 있단다."], ["Buffoonery in them is a form of resentful irony against those to whom they daren’t speak the truth, from having been for years humiliated and intimidated by them.", "그런 이들의 어릿광대짓이란, 여러 해 모욕당하고 위협당한 끝에 차마 진실을 말하지 못하는 자들을 향한, 원한 어린 빈정거림의 한 형태야."], ["Believe me, Krassotkin, that sort of buffoonery is sometimes tragic in the extreme.", "믿어 다오, 크라소트킨, 그런 어릿광대짓은 때로 더없이 비극적이란다."], ["His whole life now is centered in Ilusha, and if Ilusha dies, he will either go mad with grief or kill himself.", "그 사람의 삶은 이제 온통 일류샤에게 쏠려 있어. 만약 일류샤가 죽으면, 그 사람은 슬픔에 미쳐 버리거나 스스로 목숨을 끊을 거야."], ["I feel almost certain of that when I look at him now.”", "지금 그 사람을 보고 있으면 그게 거의 틀림없다는 느낌이 들어.\""]], [["“I understand you, Karamazov.", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 카라마조프."], ["I see you understand human nature,”", "형님은 인간 본성을 꿰뚫어 보시는군요.\""], ["Kolya added, with feeling.", "콜랴가 감정을 담아 덧붙였다."]], [["“And as soon as I saw you with a dog, I thought it was Zhutchka you were bringing.”", "\"그리고 네가 개를 데리고 있는 걸 보자마자, 나는 네가 주치카를 데려오는 줄 알았어.\""]], [["“Wait a bit, Karamazov, perhaps we shall find it yet; but this is Perezvon.", "\"잠깐만요, 카라마조프, 어쩌면 아직 찾아낼 수도 있어요. 하지만 이건 페레즈본이에요."], ["I’ll let him go in now and perhaps it will amuse Ilusha more than the mastiff pup.", "지금 이 녀석을 안으로 들여보낼게요. 어쩌면 마스티프 강아지보다 일류샤를 더 즐겁게 해 줄지도 몰라요."], ["Wait a bit, Karamazov, you will know something in a minute.", "잠깐만요, 카라마조프, 곧 뭔가 알게 되실 거예요."], ["But, I say, I am keeping you here!”", "그런데, 참, 제가 형님을 여기 붙들어 놓고 있네요!\""], ["Kolya cried suddenly.", "콜랴가 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“You’ve no overcoat on in this bitter cold.", "\"이 매서운 추위에 외투도 안 걸치셨는데."], ["You see what an egoist I am.", "보세요, 제가 얼마나 이기적인지."], ["Oh, we are all egoists, Karamazov!”", "아, 우린 다 이기주의자예요, 카라마조프!\""]], [["“Don’t trouble; it is cold, but I don’t often catch cold.", "\"신경 쓰지 마라. 춥긴 해도 나는 감기에 잘 걸리지 않아."], ["Let us go in, though, and, by the way, what is your name?", "그래도 들어가자. 그런데, 참, 네 이름이 뭐니?"], ["I know you are called Kolya, but what else?”", "콜랴라고 불리는 건 아는데, 그 밖엔?\""]], [["“Nikolay—Nikolay Ivanovitch Krassotkin, or, as they say in official documents, ‘Krassotkin son.’”", "\"니콜라이—니콜라이 이바노비치 크라소트킨이에요. 아니면 공식 문서에서 부르는 대로 하자면 '크라소트킨의 아들'이고요.\""], ["Kolya laughed for some reason, but added suddenly, “Of course I hate my name Nikolay.”", "콜랴가 무슨 까닭에선지 웃더니, 별안간 덧붙였다. \"물론 저는 니콜라이라는 제 이름이 싫어요.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"왜 그러니?\""]], [["“It’s so trivial, so ordinary.”", "\"너무 흔해 빠지고, 너무 평범하잖아요.\""]], [["“You are thirteen?”", "\"열세 살이지?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“No, fourteen—that is, I shall be fourteen very soon, in a fortnight.", "\"아니요, 열네 살이에요—그러니까, 곧, 보름 뒤면 열네 살이 돼요."], ["I’ll confess one weakness of mine, Karamazov, just to you, since it’s our first meeting, so that you may understand my character at once.", "형님한테만 제 약점 하나를 고백할게요, 카라마조프, 이게 우리 첫 만남이니까, 형님이 단번에 제 성격을 알 수 있게요."], ["I hate being asked my age, more than that ...", "저는 나이를 묻는 게 싫어요. 그것보다 더한 건…"], ["and in fact ...", "사실은…"], ["there’s a libelous story going about me, that last week I played robbers with the preparatory boys.", "저에 관해 떠도는 험담이 하나 있는데, 지난주에 제가 예비반 애들이랑 강도놀이를 했다는 거예요."], ["It’s a fact that I did play with them, but it’s a perfect libel to say I did it for my own amusement.", "제가 걔들이랑 논 건 사실이지만, 제 재미로 그랬다는 건 완전히 모함이에요."], ["I have reasons for believing that you’ve heard the story; but I wasn’t playing for my own amusement, it was for the sake of the children, because they couldn’t think of anything to do by themselves.", "형님이 그 이야기를 들으셨으리라고 믿을 만한 이유가 있는데, 저는 제 재미로 논 게 아니라 아이들을 위해서 그런 거예요. 걔들끼리는 뭘 하고 놀지 도무지 생각해 내질 못하니까요."], ["But they’ve always got some silly tale.", "하지만 사람들은 늘 무슨 어처구니없는 이야기를 만들어 내요."], ["This is an awful town for gossip, I can tell you.”", "정말이지 여긴 험담으로 치면 끔찍한 읍내예요.\""]], [["“But what if you had been playing for your own amusement, what’s the harm?”", "\"하지만 만약 네 재미로 놀았다 한들, 그게 무슨 잘못이니?\""]], [["“Come, I say, for my own amusement!", "\"이거 참, 제 재미라뇨!"], ["You don’t play horses, do you?”", "형님은 말타기 놀이 같은 건 안 하시잖아요?\""]], [["“But you must look at it like this,”", "\"하지만 이렇게 봐야 해.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling.", "알료샤가 빙긋 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Grown-up people go to the theater and there the adventures of all sorts of heroes are represented—sometimes there are robbers and battles, too—and isn’t that just the same thing, in a different form, of course?", "\"어른들도 극장에 가는데, 거기서는 온갖 영웅들의 모험이 펼쳐지지—때로는 강도와 전투도 나오고—그것도 결국 형식만 다를 뿐 똑같은 것 아니겠니?"], ["And young people’s games of soldiers or robbers in their playtime are also art in its first stage.", "그리고 젊은이들이 놀이 시간에 하는 병정놀이나 강도놀이도 첫 단계의 예술이란다."], ["You know, they spring from the growing artistic instincts of the young.", "알다시피, 그건 자라나는 젊은이들의 예술적 본능에서 솟아나는 거야."], ["And sometimes these games are much better than performances in the theater, the only difference is that people go there to look at the actors, while in these games the young people are the actors themselves.", "그리고 때로는 이런 놀이가 극장의 공연보다 훨씬 나아. 유일한 차이라면, 극장에는 배우를 보러 가는 거지만, 이런 놀이에서는 젊은이들 자신이 배우라는 점이지."], ["But that’s only natural.”", "하지만 그건 지극히 자연스러운 일이야.\""]], [["“You think so?", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?"], ["Is that your idea?”", "그게 형님 생각이에요?\""], ["Kolya looked at him intently.", "콜랴가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["“Oh, you know, that’s rather an interesting view.", "\"아, 그거 참, 제법 흥미로운 견해네요."], ["When I go home, I’ll think it over.", "집에 가면 곰곰이 생각해 봐야겠어요."], ["I’ll admit I thought I might learn something from you.", "솔직히 형님한테 뭔가 배울 수 있겠다 싶었거든요."], ["I’ve come to learn of you, Karamazov,”", "저는 형님께 배우러 온 거예요, 카라마조프.\""], ["Kolya concluded, in a voice full of spontaneous feeling.", "콜랴가 절로 우러나는 감정이 가득한 목소리로 말을 맺었다."]], [["“And I of you,”", "\"나도 너한테 배우러 온 거란다.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling and pressing his hand.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 그의 손을 꼭 잡았다."]], [["Kolya was much pleased with Alyosha.", "콜랴는 알료샤가 무척 마음에 들었다."], ["What struck him most was that he treated him exactly like an equal and that he talked to him just as if he were “quite grown up.”", "그를 가장 강하게 사로잡은 것은, 알료샤가 그를 꼭 동등한 사람처럼 대하고, 마치 그가 \"다 큰 어른\"이라도 되는 것처럼 그에게 이야기한다는 점이었다."]], [["“I’ll show you something directly, Karamazov; it’s a theatrical performance, too,”", "\"제가 곧 형님께 뭔가 보여 드릴게요, 카라마조프. 이것도 일종의 연극 공연이에요.\""], ["he said, laughing nervously.", "그가 초조하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“That’s why I’ve come.”", "\"그것 때문에 온 거예요.\""]], [["“Let us go first to the people of the house, on the left.", "\"먼저 왼쪽의 집주인네로 가자."], ["All the boys leave their coats in there, because the room is small and hot.”", "아이들이 다들 외투를 거기 벗어 두거든. 방이 좁고 더워서 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh, I’m only coming in for a minute.", "\"아, 저는 잠깐만 들어갈 거예요."], ["I’ll keep on my overcoat.", "외투는 그냥 입고 있을게요."], ["Perezvon will stay here in the passage and be dead.", "페레즈본은 여기 복도에서 죽은 시늉을 하고 있을 거예요. 이시,* 페레즈본, 엎드려 죽은 척해!"], ["Ici, Perezvon, lie down and be dead!", "보세요, 이렇게 죽은 시늉을 하잖아요."], ["You see how he’s dead.", "제가 먼저 들어가서 살펴볼게요."], ["I’ll go in first and explore, then I’ll whistle to him when I think fit, and you’ll see, he’ll dash in like mad.", "그러다 적당하다 싶을 때 이 녀석한테 휘파람을 불 텐데, 그러면 보세요, 미친 듯이 달려 들어올 거예요. 다만 스무로프가 그 순간에 문 여는 걸 잊으면 안 돼요."], ["Only Smurov must not forget to open the door at the moment.", "제가 다 준비해 둘 테니, 형님은 뭔가 구경하시게 될 거예요.\""], ["I’ll arrange it all and you’ll see something.”", "* 이시(Ici): '여기로'라는 뜻의 프랑스어."]], [["# Chapter V. By Ilusha’s Bedside", "# Chapter V. By Ilusha’s Bedside"]], [["The room inhabited by the family of the retired captain Snegiryov is already familiar to the reader.", "퇴역 이등 대위 스네기료프의 가족이 사는 그 방은 독자에게 이미 낯익은 곳이다."], ["It was close and crowded at that moment with a number of visitors.", "그때 그 방은 답답하고 비좁았으며 여러 손님들로 북적이고 있었다."], ["Several boys were sitting with Ilusha, and though all of them, like Smurov, were prepared to deny that it was Alyosha who had brought them and reconciled them with Ilusha, it was really the fact.", "사내아이 몇이 일류샤 곁에 앉아 있었는데, 스무로프를 비롯한 그들 모두가 자기들을 데려와 일류샤와 화해시킨 것이 알료샤라는 사실을 부인하려 들었으나, 실은 분명한 사실이었다. 그가 부린 재간이라곤 \"양 떼처럼 질질 짜는 감상\""], ["All the art he had used had been to take them, one by one, to Ilusha, without “sheepish sentimentality,” appearing to do so casually and without design.", "따위 없이, 마치 우연인 듯 아무런 꿍꿍이도 없는 척하며 아이들을 하나씩 하나씩 일류샤에게 데려간 것뿐이었다."], ["It was a great consolation to Ilusha in his suffering.", "그것은 고통 속에 있던 일류샤에게 크나큰 위안이 되었다."], ["He was greatly touched by seeing the almost tender affection and sympathy shown him by these boys, who had been his enemies.", "한때 자기의 적이었던 이 아이들이 보여 주는 거의 다정하다 할 애정과 동정에 그는 깊이 감동했다."], ["Krassotkin was the only one missing and his absence was a heavy load on Ilusha’s heart.", "오직 크라소트킨 한 사람만이 보이지 않았고, 그의 부재는 일류샤의 가슴을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["Perhaps the bitterest of all his bitter memories was his stabbing Krassotkin, who had been his one friend and protector.", "그의 쓰라린 기억들 가운데서도 가장 쓰라린 것은, 자기의 유일한 친구이자 보호자였던 크라소트킨을 칼로 찌른 일이었을 것이다."], ["Clever little Smurov, who was the first to make it up with Ilusha, thought it was so.", "일류샤와 가장 먼저 화해한 영리한 꼬마 스무로프도 그렇게 생각했다."], ["But when Smurov hinted to Krassotkin that Alyosha wanted to come and see him about something, the latter cut him short, bidding Smurov tell “Karamazov”", "그러나 스무로프가 크라소트킨에게 알료샤가 무슨 일로 너를 보러 오고 싶어 한다고 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 크라소트킨은 그의 말을 잘라 버리며, \"카라마조프\"에게 당장 가서, 자기는 어떻게 해야 할지 누구보다 잘 알고 있고, 누구의 충고도 필요 없으며, 일류샤를 보러 간다면 그건 \"나름의 이유가 있어서\""], ["at once that he knew best what to do, that he wanted no one’s advice, and that, if he went to see Ilusha, he would choose his own time for he had “his own reasons.”", "자기가 정한 때에 갈 것이라고 전하라고 스무로프에게 일렀다."]], [["That was a fortnight before this Sunday.", "그것은 이번 일요일보다 두 주 전의 일이었다."], ["That was why Alyosha had not been to see him, as he had meant to.", "그래서 알료샤는 가려고 마음먹었으면서도 그를 보러 가지 못했던 것이다."], ["But though he waited, he sent Smurov to him twice again.", "그러나 그는 기다리면서도 스무로프를 두 번 더 그에게 보냈다."], ["Both times Krassotkin met him with a curt, impatient refusal, sending Alyosha a message not to bother him any more, that if he came himself, he, Krassotkin, would not go to Ilusha at all.", "두 번 다 크라소트킨은 무뚝뚝하고 짜증스러운 거절로 그를 대했고, 알료샤에게 더는 자기를 귀찮게 하지 말라는, 만일 그가 직접 온다면 자기, 크라소트킨은 일류샤에게 아예 가지 않겠다는 전갈을 보냈다."], ["Up to the very last day, Smurov did not know that Kolya meant to go to Ilusha that morning, and only the evening before, as he parted from Smurov, Kolya abruptly told him to wait at home for him next morning, for he would go with him to the Snegiryovs’, but warned him on no account to say he was coming, as he wanted to drop in casually.", "바로 그 마지막 날까지도 스무로프는 콜랴가 그날 아침 일류샤에게 갈 작정인 줄 몰랐고, 다만 바로 전날 저녁 스무로프와 헤어지면서 콜랴가 불쑥, 다음 날 아침 집에서 자기를 기다리라고, 함께 스네기료프네에 가겠다고 일렀을 뿐이었는데, 다만 자기가 간다는 말은 절대로 하지 말라고, 우연히 들른 것처럼 하고 싶다고 단단히 일러두었다. 스무로프는 그대로 따랐다."], ["Smurov obeyed. Smurov’s fancy that Kolya would bring back the lost dog was based on the words Kolya had dropped that “they must be asses not to find the dog, if it was alive.”", "콜랴가 잃어버린 개를 데려올 것이라는 스무로프의 짐작은, \"그 개가 살아 있는데도 찾아내지 못한다면 그놈들은 멍청이가 틀림없다\"고 콜랴가 흘린 말에 근거한 것이었다."], ["When Smurov, waiting for an opportunity, timidly hinted at his guess about the dog, Krassotkin flew into a violent rage.", "스무로프가 기회를 엿보다가 그 개에 관한 자기 짐작을 머뭇거리며 넌지시 비쳤을 때, 크라소트킨은 불같이 화를 냈다."], ["“I’m not such an ass as to go hunting about the town for other people’s dogs when I’ve got a dog of my own!", "\"나한테도 개가 있는데, 남의 개를 찾겠다고 온 동네를 쏘다닐 만큼 멍청이는 아니란 말이야!"], ["And how can you imagine a dog could be alive after swallowing a pin?", "게다가 핀을 삼킨 개가 살아 있을 거라고 어떻게 상상이나 할 수 있어?"], ["Sheepish sentimentality, that’s what it is!”", "양 떼처럼 질질 짜는 감상, 바로 그게 그거라고!\""]], [["For the last fortnight Ilusha had not left his little bed under the ikons in the corner.", "지난 두 주 동안 일류샤는 구석 성상(聖像) 아래의 자그마한 침대를 떠나지 못했다."], ["He had not been to school since the day he met Alyosha and bit his finger.", "알료샤를 만나 그 손가락을 깨문 그날 이후로 그는 학교에 가지 못했다."], ["He was taken ill the same day, though for a month afterwards he was sometimes able to get up and walk about the room and passage.", "바로 그날부터 그는 앓아누웠는데, 그 뒤로 한 달가량은 이따금 일어나 방 안과 복도를 걸어 다닐 수 있기도 했다."], ["But latterly he had become so weak that he could not move without help from his father.", "그러나 근래에는 너무 쇠약해져서 아버지의 부축 없이는 꼼짝도 할 수 없게 되었다."], ["His father was terribly concerned about him.", "아버지는 그를 두고 무섭도록 애를 태웠다."], ["He even gave up drinking and was almost crazy with terror that his boy would die.", "그는 술까지 끊었고, 제 아들이 죽을지도 모른다는 공포에 거의 미칠 지경이었다."], ["And often, especially after leading him round the room on his arm and putting him back to bed, he would run to a dark corner in the passage and, leaning his head against the wall, he would break into paroxysms of violent weeping, stifling his sobs that they might not be heard by Ilusha.", "그리고 흔히, 특히 아들을 팔에 안다시피 부축해 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 다시 침대에 누인 뒤에는, 복도의 어두운 구석으로 달려가 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 일류샤가 듣지 못하도록 흐느낌을 억누르며 격렬한 통곡의 발작에 몸을 맡기곤 했다."]], [["Returning to the room, he would usually begin doing something to amuse and comfort his precious boy; he would tell him stories, funny anecdotes, or would mimic comic people he had happened to meet, even imitate the howls and cries of animals.", "방으로 돌아오면 그는 으레 그 귀한 아들을 즐겁게 하고 위로하느라 무언가를 하기 시작했다. 옛이야기며 우스운 일화를 들려주거나, 어쩌다 만났던 우스꽝스러운 사람들을 흉내 내고, 심지어 짐승들의 울부짖음과 울음소리까지 흉내 내곤 했다."], ["But Ilusha could not bear to see his father fooling and playing the buffoon.", "그러나 일류샤는 아버지가 어릿광대 짓을 하며 익살을 부리는 꼴을 차마 볼 수 없었다."], ["Though the boy tried not to show how he disliked it, he saw with an aching heart that his father was an object of contempt, and he was continually haunted by the memory of the “wisp of tow” and that “terrible day.”", "아이는 자기가 그것을 싫어한다는 티를 내지 않으려 애썼으나, 아버지가 멸시의 대상이라는 사실을 가슴 저미는 심정으로 알았고, \"삼 부스러기\"와 그 \"끔찍한 날\"의 기억에 끊임없이 시달렸다."]], [["Nina, Ilusha’s gentle, crippled sister, did not like her father’s buffoonery either (Varvara had been gone for some time past to Petersburg to study at the university).", "일류샤의 온순하고 다리를 저는 누이 니나도 아버지의 어릿광대 짓을 좋아하지 않았다(바르바라는 얼마 전부터 대학에서 공부하느라 페테르부르크에 가 있었다)."], ["But the half-imbecile mother was greatly diverted and laughed heartily when her husband began capering about or performing something.", "그러나 반쯤 정신이 나간 어머니는 남편이 깡충거리며 뛰어다니거나 무슨 재주를 부리기 시작하면 무척 즐거워하며 깔깔 웃었다."], ["It was the only way she could be amused; all the rest of the time she was grumbling and complaining that now every one had forgotten her, that no one treated her with respect, that she was slighted, and so on.", "그것이 그녀를 즐겁게 할 수 있는 유일한 방법이었으니, 그 밖의 시간에는 줄곧 투덜대며 이제는 다들 자기를 잊어버렸다느니, 아무도 자기를 존중해 주지 않는다느니, 자기를 업신여긴다느니 하며 불평을 늘어놓았다."], ["But during the last few days she had completely changed.", "그러나 지난 며칠 사이 그녀는 완전히 달라졌다."], ["She began looking constantly at Ilusha’s bed in the corner and seemed lost in thought.", "구석의 일류샤 침대를 줄곧 바라보기 시작했고, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다."], ["She was more silent, quieter, and, if she cried, she cried quietly so as not to be heard.", "그녀는 말수가 줄고 조용해졌으며, 운다 해도 남이 듣지 못하도록 소리 죽여 울었다."], ["The captain noticed the change in her with mournful perplexity.", "대위는 그 변화를 침울한 당혹감 속에서 알아차렸다."], ["The boys’ visits at first only angered her, but later on their merry shouts and stories began to divert her, and at last she liked them so much that, if the boys had given up coming, she would have felt dreary without them.", "아이들의 방문은 처음에는 그녀를 짜증나게 했을 뿐이지만, 나중에는 그들의 명랑한 외침과 이야기가 그녀를 즐겁게 하기 시작했고, 마침내 그녀는 그들을 어찌나 좋아하게 되었던지, 아이들이 오기를 그만두었더라면 그들 없이는 적막했을 것이다."], ["When the children told some story or played a game, she laughed and clapped her hands.", "아이들이 무슨 이야기를 하거나 놀이를 할 때면 그녀는 웃으며 손뼉을 쳤다."], ["She called some of them to her and kissed them.", "몇몇 아이를 곁으로 불러 입을 맞추기도 했다."], ["She was particularly fond of Smurov.", "그녀는 특히 스무로프를 귀여워했다."]], [["As for the captain, the presence in his room of the children, who came to cheer up Ilusha, filled his heart from the first with ecstatic joy.", "대위로 말하자면, 일류샤의 기운을 북돋우러 오는 아이들이 자기 방에 모여 있다는 사실이 처음부터 그의 가슴을 황홀한 기쁨으로 가득 채웠다."], ["He even hoped that Ilusha would now get over his depression, and that that would hasten his recovery.", "그는 일류샤가 이제 그 우울을 떨쳐 버릴 것이고, 그것이 회복을 앞당겨 줄 것이라는 희망까지 품었다."], ["In spite of his alarm about Ilusha, he had not, till lately, felt one minute’s doubt of his boy’s ultimate recovery.", "일류샤에 대한 불안에도 불구하고 그는 근래까지만 해도 아들이 끝내 회복하리라는 것을 단 한순간도 의심하지 않았다."]], [["He met his little visitors with homage, waited upon them hand and foot; he was ready to be their horse and even began letting them ride on his back, but Ilusha did not like the game and it was given up.", "그는 어린 손님들을 깍듯이 맞이하며 그들의 시중을 정성껏 들었다. 그들의 말이 되어 줄 채비까지 되어 있어 등에 올라타게 하기 시작했으나, 일류샤가 그 놀이를 좋아하지 않아 그만두었다."], ["He began buying little things for them, gingerbread and nuts, gave them tea and cut them sandwiches.", "그는 아이들에게 줄 자잘한 것들, 생강과자와 호두를 사들이기 시작했고, 차를 따라 주며 샌드위치를 만들어 주었다."], ["It must be noted that all this time he had plenty of money.", "짚어 둘 것은, 이 무렵 그에게는 돈이 넉넉했다는 점이다."], ["He had taken the two hundred roubles from Katerina Ivanovna just as Alyosha had predicted he would.", "알료샤가 예언했던 그대로 그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 이백 루블을 받았던 것이다."], ["And afterwards Katerina Ivanovna, learning more about their circumstances and Ilusha’s illness, visited them herself, made the acquaintance of the family, and succeeded in fascinating the half-imbecile mother.", "그리고 그 뒤 카테리나 이바노브나는 그들의 형편과 일류샤의 병에 대해 더 자세히 알게 되자 직접 그들을 찾아와 가족과 가까워졌고, 반쯤 정신이 나간 어머니의 마음을 사로잡는 데 성공했다."], ["Since then she had been lavish in helping them, and the captain, terror-stricken at the thought that his boy might be dying, forgot his pride and humbly accepted her assistance.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 아낌없이 그들을 도왔고, 제 아들이 죽어 갈지도 모른다는 생각에 공포에 질린 대위는 자존심을 버리고 그녀의 도움을 겸허히 받아들였다."]], [["All this time Doctor Herzenstube, who was called in by Katerina Ivanovna, came punctually every other day, but little was gained by his visits and he dosed the invalid mercilessly.", "이 무렵 내내, 카테리나 이바노브나가 불러들인 헤르첸슈투베 의사가 하루걸러 한 번씩 어김없이 찾아왔으나, 그의 왕진으로 얻는 것은 별로 없었고, 그는 가차 없이 환자에게 약을 들이부었다."], ["But on that Sunday morning a new doctor was expected, who had come from Moscow, where he had a great reputation.", "그런데 그 일요일 아침에는 모스크바에서 온, 그곳에서 명성이 자자한 새 의사가 오기로 되어 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna had sent for him from Moscow at great expense, not expressly for Ilusha, but for another object of which more will be said in its place hereafter.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 큰 비용을 들여 모스크바에서 그를 불러들였는데, 일류샤를 위해서가 아니라 다른 목적이 있어서였고, 그 일에 관해서는 적절한 대목에서 따로 더 이야기하기로 하겠다."], ["But, as he had come, she had asked him to see Ilusha as well, and the captain had been told to expect him.", "그러나 그가 온 김에 그녀가 일류샤도 봐 달라고 청했고, 대위에게는 그를 기다리라는 말이 전해져 있었다."], ["He hadn’t the slightest idea that Kolya Krassotkin was coming, though he had long wished for a visit from the boy for whom Ilusha was fretting.", "그는 일류샤가 애타게 그리워하던 그 아이의 방문을 오래도록 바라 왔으면서도, 콜랴 크라소트킨이 온다는 것은 까맣게 모르고 있었다."]], [["At the moment when Krassotkin opened the door and came into the room, the captain and all the boys were round Ilusha’s bed, looking at a tiny mastiff pup, which had only been born the day before, though the captain had bespoken it a week ago to comfort and amuse Ilusha, who was still fretting over the lost and probably dead Zhutchka.", "크라소트킨이 문을 열고 방으로 들어선 바로 그 순간, 대위와 아이들은 모두 일류샤의 침대를 둘러싸고서, 바로 전날 태어난 자그마한 마스티프 강아지를 들여다보고 있었다. 대위가 그 강아지를 일주일 전에 미리 부탁해 두었던 것은, 잃어버려 십중팔구 죽었을 주치카를 두고 여전히 애를 태우는 일류샤를 위로하고 즐겁게 해 주기 위해서였다."], ["Ilusha, who had heard three days before that he was to be presented with a puppy, not an ordinary puppy, but a pedigree mastiff (a very important point, of course), tried from delicacy of feeling to pretend that he was pleased.", "사흘 전부터 강아지를, 그것도 보통 강아지가 아니라 혈통 있는 마스티프를(물론 이건 아주 중요한 점이었다) 선물 받게 되리라는 말을 들은 일류샤는, 섬세한 마음씨에서 기쁜 척하려 애썼다."], ["But his father and the boys could not help seeing that the puppy only served to recall to his little heart the thought of the unhappy dog he had killed.", "그러나 아버지와 아이들은, 그 강아지가 자기가 죽인 그 가엾은 개의 기억을 도리어 그 어린 가슴에 불러일으킨다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The puppy lay beside him feebly moving and he, smiling sadly, stroked it with his thin, pale, wasted hand.", "강아지는 그의 곁에 누워 힘없이 꿈틀거렸고, 그는 슬픈 미소를 띠며 여위고 창백하게 메마른 손으로 강아지를 쓰다듬었다."], ["Clearly he liked the puppy, but ...", "그가 그 강아지를 좋아하는 것은 분명했으나…"], ["it wasn’t Zhutchka; if he could have had Zhutchka and the puppy, too, then he would have been completely happy.", "그것은 주치카가 아니었다. 만일 주치카와 그 강아지를 둘 다 가질 수 있었더라면 그는 더없이 행복했을 것이다."]], [["“Krassotkin!”", "\"크라소트킨이다!\""], ["cried one of the boys suddenly.", "한 아이가 갑자기 소리쳤다."], ["He was the first to see him come in.", "그가 들어오는 것을 맨 먼저 본 것이다."]], [["Krassotkin’s entrance made a general sensation; the boys moved away and stood on each side of the bed, so that he could get a full view of Ilusha.", "크라소트킨의 등장은 좌중에 술렁임을 일으켰다. 아이들이 비켜서서 침대 양옆에 늘어서자 그는 일류샤를 똑똑히 볼 수 있게 되었다."], ["The captain ran eagerly to meet Kolya.", "대위가 콜랴를 맞으러 부리나케 달려왔다."]], [["“Please come in ...", "\"어서 들어오게…"], ["you are welcome!”", "잘 왔네!\""], ["he said hurriedly.", "그가 다급하게 말했다."], ["“Ilusha, Mr. Krassotkin has come to see you!”", "\"일류샤, 크라소트킨 군이 너를 보러 왔구나!\""]], [["But Krassotkin, shaking hands with him hurriedly, instantly showed his complete knowledge of the manners of good society.", "그러나 크라소트킨은 그와 황급히 악수를 나누고는, 곧바로 자기가 상류 사회의 예법을 완벽하게 익혔음을 드러냈다."], ["He turned first to the captain’s wife sitting in her arm-chair, who was very ill-humored at the moment, and was grumbling that the boys stood between her and Ilusha’s bed and did not let her see the new puppy.", "그는 먼저, 안락의자에 앉은 채 아이들이 자기와 일류샤의 침대 사이를 가로막아 새 강아지를 못 보게 한다고 그 순간 잔뜩 심술이 나 투덜대던 대위 부인에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["With the greatest courtesy he made her a bow, scraping his foot, and turning to Nina, he made her, as the only other lady present, a similar bow.", "더없이 정중하게 그는 한쪽 발을 끌며 그녀에게 절을 했고, 니나에게로 몸을 돌려, 그 자리에 있는 또 한 사람의 부인에게 하듯 똑같이 절을 했다."], ["This polite behavior made an extremely favorable impression on the deranged lady.", "이 예의 바른 행동은 그 정신 나간 부인에게 더없이 호의적인 인상을 주었다."]], [["“There, you can see at once he is a young man that has been well brought up,”", "\"저것 보세요, 한눈에 알 수 있잖아요, 잘 자란 도련님이라는 걸.\""], ["she commented aloud, throwing up her hands; “but as for our other visitors they come in one on the top of another.”", "그녀가 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 소리 내어 평했다. \"그런데 우리 다른 손님들로 말하자면, 하나가 또 하나 위에 포개 타고 들어온다니까요.\""]], [["“How do you mean, mamma, one on the top of another, how is that?”", "\"하나가 또 하나 위에 포개 타고라니, 그게 무슨 말이오, 여보?"], ["muttered the captain affectionately, though a little anxious on her account.", "그게 어떻게 그렇다는 거요?\" 대위가 다정하게, 그러면서도 그녀를 두고 조금 걱정스러운 듯 웅얼거렸다."]], [["“That’s how they ride in.", "\"바로 그렇게 타고 들어온다니까요."], ["They get on each other’s shoulders in the passage and prance in like that on a respectable family.", "복도에서 서로 어깨에 올라타서는, 그렇게 점잖은 집안에 깡충깡충 뛰어든다고요."], ["Strange sort of visitors!”", "별스러운 손님들이지!\""]], [["“But who’s come in like that, mamma?”", "\"하지만 누가 그렇게 들어왔다는 거요, 여보?\""]], [["“Why, that boy came in riding on that one’s back and this one on that one’s.”", "\"아니, 저 애가 저 애 등에 타고 들어오고, 또 이 애가 저 애 등에 타고 들어왔잖아요.\""]], [["Kolya was already by Ilusha’s bedside.", "콜랴는 어느새 일류샤의 머리맡에 와 있었다."], ["The sick boy turned visibly paler.", "병든 아이는 눈에 띄게 창백해졌다."], ["He raised himself in the bed and looked intently at Kolya.", "그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 콜랴를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Kolya had not seen his little friend for two months, and he was overwhelmed at the sight of him.", "콜랴는 두 달 동안 그 어린 친구를 보지 못했던 터라, 그를 보는 순간 망연자실했다."], ["He had never imagined that he would see such a wasted, yellow face, such enormous, feverishly glowing eyes and such thin little hands.", "그렇게 여위고 누렇게 뜬 얼굴, 그렇게 크고 열에 들떠 번들거리는 눈, 그렇게 가느다란 손을 보게 되리라고는 상상조차 못 했던 것이다."], ["He saw, with grieved surprise, Ilusha’s rapid, hard breathing and dry lips.", "그는 슬픈 놀라움 속에서 일류샤의 가쁘고 거친 숨결과 메마른 입술을 보았다."], ["He stepped close to him, held out his hand, and almost overwhelmed, he said:", "그는 가까이 다가서서 손을 내밀었고, 거의 압도된 채 말했다."]], [["“Well, old man ...", "\"음, 친구…"], ["how are you?”", "좀 어때?\""], ["But his voice failed him, he couldn’t achieve an appearance of ease; his face suddenly twitched and the corners of his mouth quivered.", "그러나 목소리가 잠겨 버려, 그는 태연한 척하지 못했다. 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 씰룩거렸고 입가가 떨렸다."], ["Ilusha smiled a pitiful little smile, still unable to utter a word.", "일류샤는 여전히 한마디도 못 한 채 가엾고 자그마한 미소를 지었다."], ["Something moved Kolya to raise his hand and pass it over Ilusha’s hair.", "무언가에 이끌려 콜랴는 손을 들어 일류샤의 머리카락을 쓸어 주었다."]], [["“Never mind!”", "\"괜찮아!\""], ["he murmured softly to him to cheer him up, or perhaps not knowing why he said it.", "그는 그를 위로하려고, 아니 어쩌면 왜 그런 말을 하는지도 모른 채, 그에게 나직이 속삭였다."], ["For a minute they were silent again.", "잠시 그들은 다시 말이 없었다."]], [["“Hallo, so you’ve got a new puppy?”", "\"어이, 새 강아지를 얻었구나?\""], ["Kolya said suddenly, in a most callous voice.", "콜랴가 더없이 무심한 목소리로 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Ye—es,”", "\"으응.\""], ["answered Ilusha in a long whisper, gasping for breath.", "일류샤가 숨을 헐떡이며 길게 끄는 속삭임으로 대답했다."]], [["“A black nose, that means he’ll be fierce, a good house-dog,”", "\"코가 검은 걸 보니 사납겠는걸, 좋은 집 지키는 개가 되겠어.\""], ["Kolya observed gravely and stolidly, as if the only thing he cared about was the puppy and its black nose.", "콜랴가, 마치 자기가 신경 쓰는 것이라곤 오직 그 강아지와 그 검은 코뿐이라는 듯, 진지하고 무덤덤하게 한마디 했다."], ["But in reality he still had to do his utmost to control his feelings not to burst out crying like a child, and do what he would he could not control it.", "그러나 사실 그는 어린아이처럼 울음을 터뜨리지 않으려고 여전히 안간힘을 다해 감정을 억눌러야 했고, 아무리 애써도 그것을 다스릴 수가 없었다."], ["“When it grows up, you’ll have to keep it on the chain, I’m sure.”", "\"이놈이 다 자라면 사슬에 매어 둬야 할걸, 틀림없이.\""]], [["“He’ll be a huge dog!”", "\"엄청나게 큰 개가 될 거예요!\""], ["cried one of the boys.", "한 아이가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Of course he will,”", "\"당연하지.\" \"마스티프인걸.\""], ["“a mastiff,” “large,”", "\"큼지막하게.\""], ["“like this,”", "\"이만큼.\""], ["“as big as a calf,”", "\"송아지만큼 크게.\""], ["shouted several voices.", "여러 목소리가 외쳐 댔다."]], [["“As big as a calf, as a real calf,”", "\"송아지만큼, 진짜 송아지만큼 크게요.\""], ["chimed in the captain.", "대위가 맞장구를 쳤다."], ["“I got one like that on purpose, one of the fiercest breed, and his parents are huge and very fierce, they stand as high as this from the floor....", "\"내가 일부러 그런 놈을 골랐지요, 가장 사나운 종으로 말입니다. 어미 아비가 다 어마어마하게 크고 무척 사납거든요, 바닥에서 이만큼이나 높이 선다니까요…"], ["Sit down here, on Ilusha’s bed, or here on the bench.", "여기 앉게, 일류샤 침대에 앉든지, 아니면 여기 의자에 앉게."], ["You are welcome, we’ve been hoping to see you a long time....", "잘 왔네, 오래도록 자네가 오기를 바라고 있었지…"], ["You were so kind as to come with Alexey Fyodorovitch?”", "알렉세이 표도로비치와 함께 와 줬구먼, 고맙기도 하지?\""]], [["Krassotkin sat on the edge of the bed, at Ilusha’s feet.", "크라소트킨은 일류샤의 발치, 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다."], ["Though he had perhaps prepared a free-and-easy opening for the conversation on his way, now he completely lost the thread of it.", "오는 길에 어쩌면 대화를 트는 격의 없는 말머리를 준비해 두었을지도 모르나, 이제 그는 그 실마리를 완전히 놓쳐 버렸다."]], [["“No ...", "\"아뇨…"], ["I came with Perezvon.", "저는 페레즈본하고 같이 왔어요."], ["I’ve got a dog now, called Perezvon.", "제게 이제 개가 한 마리 있는데, 페레즈본이라고 불러요."], ["A Slavonic name.", "슬라브식 이름이죠."], ["He’s out there ...", "저 밖에 있어요…"], ["if I whistle, he’ll run in.", "제가 휘파람을 불면 달려 들어올 거예요."], ["I’ve brought a dog, too,”", "저도 개를 한 마리 데려왔거든요.\""], ["he said, addressing Ilusha all at once.", "그가 별안간 일류샤를 향해 말했다."], ["“Do you remember Zhutchka, old man?”", "\"주치카 기억나니, 친구야?\""], ["he suddenly fired the question at him.", "그가 불쑥 그 질문을 그에게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["Ilusha’s little face quivered.", "일류샤의 자그마한 얼굴이 떨렸다."], ["He looked with an agonized expression at Kolya.", "그는 괴로움이 가득한 표정으로 콜랴를 바라보았다."], ["Alyosha, standing at the door, frowned and signed to Kolya not to speak of Zhutchka, but he did not or would not notice.", "문가에 서 있던 알료샤가 눈살을 찌푸리며 주치카 이야기는 하지 말라고 콜랴에게 신호를 보냈으나, 그는 알아차리지 못했거나 알아차리려 하지 않았다."]], [["“Where ...", "\"주치카는…"], ["is Zhutchka?”", "어디 있어?\""], ["Ilusha asked in a broken voice.", "일류샤가 끊어지는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Oh, well, my boy, your Zhutchka’s lost and done for!”", "\"아, 그게 말이지, 친구야, 네 주치카는 없어졌고 끝장나 버렸어!\""]], [["Ilusha did not speak, but he fixed an intent gaze once more on Kolya.", "일류샤는 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 다시 한번 콜랴에게 뚫어질 듯한 눈길을 못 박았다."], ["Alyosha, catching Kolya’s eye, signed to him vigorously again, but he turned away his eyes pretending not to have noticed.", "알료샤가 콜랴의 눈길을 붙잡아 또 한 번 힘주어 신호를 보냈으나, 그는 알아채지 못한 척 눈길을 돌려 버렸다."]], [["“It must have run away and died somewhere.", "\"어딘가로 달아나서 죽었을 거야."], ["It must have died after a meal like that,”", "그런 걸 삼켰으니 죽었겠지.\""], ["Kolya pronounced pitilessly, though he seemed a little breathless.", "콜랴가 가차 없이 단언했는데, 다만 숨이 조금 가빠 보였다."], ["“But I’ve got a dog, Perezvon ...", "\"하지만 나한테는 개가 있어, 페레즈본이라고…"], ["A Slavonic name....", "슬라브식 이름이지…"], ["I’ve brought him to show you.”", "너한테 보여 주려고 데려왔어.\""]], [["“I don’t want him!”", "\"보기 싫어!\""], ["said Ilusha suddenly.", "일류샤가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“No, no, you really must see him ...", "\"아냐, 아냐, 정말로 한번 봐야 해…"], ["it will amuse you.", "재미있을 거야."], ["I brought him on purpose....", "일부러 데려온 거란 말이야…"], ["He’s the same sort of shaggy dog....", "똑같이 털북숭이 개거든…"], ["You allow me to call in my dog, madam?”", "제 개를 불러들여도 될까요, 부인?\""], ["He suddenly addressed Madame Snegiryov, with inexplicable excitement in his manner.", "그가 갑자기, 까닭 모를 흥분이 어린 태도로 스네기료프 부인을 향해 말했다."]], [["“I don’t want him, I don’t want him!”", "\"보기 싫어, 보기 싫단 말이야!\""], ["cried Ilusha, with a mournful break in his voice.", "일류샤가 목소리에 구슬픈 떨림을 담아 외쳤다."], ["There was a reproachful light in his eyes.", "그의 눈에는 원망의 빛이 어렸다."]], [["“You’d better,”", "\"그러는 게 좋겠어.\""], ["the captain started up from the chest by the wall on which he had just sat down, “you’d better ...", "대위가 방금 걸터앉았던 벽가의 궤짝에서 벌떡 일어났다. \"그러는 게 좋겠네…"], ["another time,”", "다음에 하지.\""], ["he muttered, but Kolya could not be restrained.", "그가 웅얼거렸으나, 콜랴는 도무지 말릴 수가 없었다."], ["He hurriedly shouted to Smurov, “Open the door,”", "그는 황급히 스무로프에게 \"문 열어!\""], ["and as soon as it was open, he blew his whistle.", "하고 소리쳤고, 문이 열리자마자 휘파람을 불었다."], ["Perezvon dashed headlong into the room.", "페레즈본이 쏜살같이 방 안으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Jump, Perezvon, beg!", "\"뛰어, 페레즈본, 빌어!"], ["Beg!”", "빌어!\""], ["shouted Kolya, jumping up, and the dog stood erect on its hind-legs by Ilusha’s bedside.", "콜랴가 벌떡 일어서며 소리쳤고, 개는 일류샤의 머리맡에서 뒷다리로 곧추섰다."], ["What followed was a surprise to every one: Ilusha started, lurched violently forward, bent over Perezvon and gazed at him, faint with suspense.", "그다음에 벌어진 일은 모두에게 뜻밖이었다. 일류샤가 흠칫하더니 격렬하게 앞으로 휘청 쏠려 페레즈본 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 숨이 멎을 듯한 긴장 속에서 그를 응시했다."]], [["“It’s ...", "\"이건…"], ["Zhutchka!”", "주치카야!\""], ["he cried suddenly, in a voice breaking with joy and suffering.", "그가 기쁨과 고통으로 갈라지는 목소리로 별안간 소리쳤다."]], [["“And who did you think it was?”", "\"그럼 누구인 줄 알았어?\""], ["Krassotkin shouted with all his might, in a ringing, happy voice, and bending down he seized the dog and lifted him up to Ilusha.", "크라소트킨이 낭랑하고 행복에 겨운 목소리로 있는 힘껏 외치고는, 몸을 굽혀 개를 붙잡아 일류샤에게로 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Look, old man, you see, blind of one eye and the left ear is torn, just the marks you described to me.", "\"봐, 친구야, 봐, 한쪽 눈이 멀었고 왼쪽 귀가 찢어졌지, 네가 나한테 말해 준 그 자국 그대로야."], ["It was by that I found him.", "바로 그걸로 찾아낸 거야."], ["I found him directly.", "단번에 찾아냈지."], ["He did not belong to any one!”", "임자 없는 개였어!\""], ["he explained, turning quickly to the captain, to his wife, to Alyosha and then again to Ilusha.", "그가 대위에게로, 그 부인에게로, 알료샤에게로, 그러고는 다시 일류샤에게로 잽싸게 몸을 돌리며 설명했다."], ["“He used to live in the Fedotovs’ back-yard.", "\"이놈은 페도토프네 뒤뜰에 살고 있었어."], ["Though he made his home there, they did not feed him.", "거기 자리를 잡고 살긴 했어도 먹이를 주진 않더라고."], ["He was a stray dog that had run away from the village ...", "마을에서 도망쳐 나온 들개였던 거야…"], ["I found him....", "내가 찾아냈지…"], ["You see, old man, he couldn’t have swallowed what you gave him.", "봐, 친구야, 네가 준 걸 이놈이 삼켰을 리가 없어."], ["If he had, he must have died, he must have!", "삼켰다면 죽었을 거야, 틀림없이 죽었겠지!"], ["So he must have spat it out, since he is alive.", "그러니 살아 있는 걸 보면 도로 뱉어 낸 게 분명해."], ["You did not see him do it.", "네가 그걸 못 봤을 뿐이지."], ["But the pin pricked his tongue, that is why he squealed.", "핀이 혀를 찔러서 깽깽거린 거고."], ["He ran away squealing and you thought he’d swallowed it.", "깽깽거리며 달아났으니까 네가 삼킨 줄로 알았던 거야."], ["He might well squeal, because the skin of dogs’ mouths is so tender ...", "깽깽거릴 만도 하지, 개 입 속 살갗이 얼마나 여린데…"], ["tenderer than in men, much tenderer!”", "사람보다 여려, 훨씬 더 여리다고!\""], ["Kolya cried impetuously, his face glowing and radiant with delight.", "콜랴가 기쁨으로 얼굴이 환하게 빛나며 격하게 외쳐 댔다."], ["Ilusha could not speak.", "일류샤는 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["White as a sheet, he gazed open-mouthed at Kolya, with his great eyes almost starting out of his head.", "백지장처럼 하얘진 얼굴로, 거의 튀어나올 듯한 커다란 눈을 한 채 입을 벌리고 콜랴를 응시했다."], ["And if Krassotkin, who had no suspicion of it, had known what a disastrous and fatal effect such a moment might have on the sick child’s health, nothing would have induced him to play such a trick on him.", "그리고 만일 그것을 까맣게 모르던 크라소트킨이, 그런 순간이 병든 아이의 건강에 얼마나 파멸적이고 치명적인 결과를 미칠 수 있는지 알았더라면, 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 그런 장난을 치지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But Alyosha was perhaps the only person in the room who realized it.", "그러나 그 방에서 그것을 깨달은 사람은 어쩌면 알료샤 한 사람뿐이었을 것이다."], ["As for the captain he behaved like a small child.", "대위로 말하자면 그는 어린아이처럼 굴었다."]], [["“Zhutchka!", "\"주치카야!"], ["It’s Zhutchka!”", "주치카라고!\""], ["he cried in a blissful voice, “Ilusha, this is Zhutchka, your Zhutchka!", "그가 더없이 행복한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"일류샤, 이게 주치카야, 네 주치카라고!"], ["Mamma, this is Zhutchka!”", "여보, 이게 주치카요!\""], ["He was almost weeping.", "그는 거의 울먹였다."]], [["“And I never guessed!”", "\"난 짐작도 못 했어!\""], ["cried Smurov regretfully.", "스무로프가 분하다는 듯 소리쳤다."], ["“Bravo, Krassotkin!", "\"잘했다, 크라소트킨!"], ["I said he’d find the dog and here he’s found him.”", "내가 그랬잖아, 쟤가 개를 찾아낼 거라고, 그런데 정말 찾아냈어.\""]], [["“Here he’s found him!”", "\"정말 찾아냈어!\""], ["another boy repeated gleefully.", "다른 아이가 신이 나서 따라 외쳤다."]], [["“Krassotkin’s a brick!”", "\"크라소트킨은 멋진 녀석이야!\""], ["cried a third voice.", "또 다른 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“He’s a brick, he’s a brick!”", "\"멋진 녀석이야, 멋진 녀석!\""], ["cried the other boys, and they began clapping.", "다른 아이들도 외치며 손뼉을 치기 시작했다."]], [["“Wait, wait,”", "\"잠깐, 잠깐.\""], ["Krassotkin did his utmost to shout above them all.", "크라소트킨이 그들 모두를 누르고 외치려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["“I’ll tell you how it happened, that’s the whole point.", "\"어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄게, 그게 바로 핵심이야."], ["I found him, I took him home and hid him at once.", "내가 이놈을 찾아내서 집으로 데려와 곧장 숨겨 뒀어."], ["I kept him locked up at home and did not show him to any one till to-day.", "집에 가둬 두고 오늘까지는 아무한테도 안 보여 줬지."], ["Only Smurov has known for the last fortnight, but I assured him this dog was called Perezvon and he did not guess.", "지난 두 주 동안 스무로프만 알고 있었지만, 이 개는 페레즈본이라는 놈이라고 둘러댔더니 짐작도 못 하더라고."], ["And meanwhile I taught the dog all sorts of tricks.", "그러는 사이에 나는 이놈한테 온갖 재주를 가르쳤어."], ["You should only see all the things he can do!", "이놈이 할 줄 아는 걸 죄다 봐야 하는데!"], ["I trained him so as to bring you a well-trained dog, in good condition, old man, so as to be able to say to you, ‘See, old man, what a fine dog your Zhutchka is now!’ Haven’t you a bit of meat?", "잘 길들여진, 상태 좋은 개를 너한테 데려다주려고 훈련시킨 거야, 친구야, '봐, 친구야, 네 주치카가 이제 얼마나 훌륭한 개가 됐는지!' 하고 너한테 말할 수 있게 말이야!"], ["He’ll show you a trick that will make you die with laughing.", "고기 한 점 없어요? 배꼽 빠지게 웃을 재주를 보여 줄 텐데."], ["A piece of meat, haven’t you got any?”", "고기 한 조각, 없어요?\""]], [["The captain ran across the passage to the landlady, where their cooking was done.", "대위가 음식 만드는 곳인 주인 여자네로 복도를 가로질러 달려갔다."], ["Not to lose precious time, Kolya, in desperate haste, shouted to Perezvon, “Dead!”", "귀한 시간을 허비하지 않으려고 콜랴는 다급하기 짝이 없게 페레즈본에게 \"죽어!\""], ["And the dog immediately turned round and lay on his back with its four paws in the air.", "하고 외쳤다. 그러자 개는 즉시 몸을 굴려 네 발을 허공으로 쳐든 채 벌렁 드러누웠다."], ["The boys laughed.", "아이들이 웃었다."], ["Ilusha looked on with the same suffering smile, but the person most delighted with the dog’s performance was “mamma.”", "일류샤는 여전히 고통스러운 그 미소를 띤 채 바라보았는데, 개의 재주에 누구보다 기뻐한 사람은 \"엄마\"였다."], ["She laughed at the dog and began snapping her fingers and calling it, “Perezvon, Perezvon!”", "그녀는 개를 보며 웃다가 손가락을 딱딱 튀기며 \"페레즈본, 페레즈본!\" 하고 부르기 시작했다."]], [["“Nothing will make him get up, nothing!”", "\"무슨 짓을 해도 이놈은 일어나지 않아요, 절대로!\""], ["Kolya cried triumphantly, proud of his success.", "콜랴가 제 성공이 자랑스러워 의기양양하게 외쳤다."], ["“He won’t move for all the shouting in the world, but if I call to him, he’ll jump up in a minute.", "\"세상 누가 아무리 소리쳐도 꿈쩍 안 하지만, 제가 부르면 단박에 벌떡 일어날 거예요."], ["Ici, Perezvon!”", "이리 와, 페레즈본!\""], ["The dog leapt up and bounded about, whining with delight.", "개가 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 기뻐서 낑낑대며 이리저리 뛰어다녔다."], ["The captain ran back with a piece of cooked beef.", "대위가 구운 쇠고기 한 점을 들고 달려 돌아왔다."]], [["“Is it hot?”", "\"뜨거운가요?\""], ["Kolya inquired hurriedly, with a business-like air, taking the meat.", "콜랴가 고기를 받으며, 일을 다루는 듯한 태도로 다급히 물었다."], ["“Dogs don’t like hot things.", "\"개는 뜨거운 걸 싫어하거든요."], ["No, it’s all right.", "아뇨, 괜찮네요."], ["Look, everybody, look, Ilusha, look, old man; why aren’t you looking?", "다들 봐요, 봐, 일류샤, 봐, 친구야. 왜 안 보고 있어?"], ["He does not look at him, now I’ve brought him.”", "모처럼 데려왔는데 얘가 이놈을 안 보네.\""]], [["The new trick consisted in making the dog stand motionless with his nose out and putting a tempting morsel of meat just on his nose.", "새 재주란, 개가 코를 쭉 내민 채 꼼짝 않고 서 있게 하고는 그 코끝에 먹음직스러운 고기 한 조각을 올려놓는 것이었다."], ["The luckless dog had to stand without moving, with the meat on his nose, as long as his master chose to keep him, without a movement, perhaps for half an hour.", "가엾은 개는 코에 고기를 얹은 채 꼼짝도 않고, 주인이 붙잡아 두려는 동안 내내 미동도 없이 서 있어야 했으니, 어쩌면 반 시간이라도 그래야 했다."], ["But he kept Perezvon only for a brief moment.", "그러나 그는 페레즈본을 잠깐만 그렇게 붙잡아 두었다."]], [["“Paid for!”", "\"받아!\""], ["cried Kolya, and the meat passed in a flash from the dog’s nose to his mouth.", "콜랴가 외치자, 고기가 눈 깜짝할 사이에 개의 코에서 입으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["The audience, of course, expressed enthusiasm and surprise.", "좌중은 물론 열광과 감탄을 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“Can you really have put off coming all this time simply to train the dog?”", "\"정말로 그동안 안 온 게 단지 개를 훈련시키느라 그런 거니?\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha, with an involuntary note of reproach in his voice.", "알료샤가 저도 모르게 책망의 가락을 목소리에 담아 소리쳤다."]], [["“Simply for that!”", "\"오직 그것 때문이었죠!\""], ["answered Kolya, with perfect simplicity.", "콜랴가 더없이 천연덕스럽게 대답했다."], ["“I wanted to show him in all his glory.”", "\"이놈을 영광스러운 모습으로 보여 주고 싶었거든요.\""]], [["“Perezvon!", "\"페레즈본!"], ["Perezvon,”", "페레즈본.\""], ["called Ilusha suddenly, snapping his thin fingers and beckoning to the dog.", "일류샤가 가느다란 손가락을 딱딱 튀기며 개를 손짓해 부르며 별안간 불렀다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"왜 그래?"], ["Let him jump up on the bed!", "이놈을 침대 위로 올라오게 해!"], ["Ici, Perezvon!”", "이리 와, 페레즈본!\""], ["Kolya slapped the bed and Perezvon darted up by Ilusha.", "콜랴가 침대를 탁탁 치자 페레즈본이 일류샤 곁으로 쏜살같이 뛰어올랐다."], ["The boy threw both arms round his head and Perezvon instantly licked his cheek.", "아이가 두 팔로 개의 머리를 끌어안자 페레즈본은 즉시 그의 뺨을 핥았다."], ["Ilusha crept close to him, stretched himself out in bed and hid his face in the dog’s shaggy coat.", "일류샤는 개에게 바싹 다가들어 침대에 길게 몸을 뻗고는 개의 털북숭이 몸에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["“Dear, dear!”", "\"아이고, 아이고!\""], ["kept exclaiming the captain.", "대위가 연신 탄성을 질렀다."], ["Kolya sat down again on the edge of the bed.", "콜랴는 다시 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다."]], [["“Ilusha, I can show you another trick.", "\"일류샤, 다른 재주를 하나 더 보여 줄게."], ["I’ve brought you a little cannon.", "너한테 줄 자그마한 대포를 가져왔어."], ["You remember, I told you about it before and you said how much you’d like to see it.", "기억하지, 전에 내가 이 얘기를 했더니 네가 그걸 무척 보고 싶다고 했잖아."], ["Well, here, I’ve brought it to you.”", "자, 여기, 너한테 가져왔어.\""]], [["And Kolya hurriedly pulled out of his satchel the little bronze cannon.", "그러고서 콜랴는 가방에서 황급히 그 자그마한 청동 대포를 꺼냈다."], ["He hurried, because he was happy himself.", "자기 스스로가 행복했기에 서두른 것이다."], ["Another time he would have waited till the sensation made by Perezvon had passed off, now he hurried on regardless of all consideration.", "다른 때 같았으면 페레즈본이 일으킨 흥분이 가라앉을 때까지 기다렸을 테지만, 이제 그는 아무것도 따지지 않고 서둘렀다."], ["“You are all happy now,”", "\"다들 이제 행복하니까,\""], ["he felt, “so here’s something to make you happier!”", "그는 느꼈다, \"자, 여기 더 행복하게 해 줄 게 있다고!\""], ["He was perfectly enchanted himself.", "그 자신이 더없이 황홀해 있었다."]], [["“I’ve been coveting this thing for a long while; it’s for you, old man, it’s for you.", "\"이걸 오래도록 탐내 왔는데, 이건 네 거야, 친구야, 네 거라고."], ["It belonged to Morozov, it was no use to him, he had it from his brother.", "이건 모로조프 거였는데, 걔한테는 쓸모가 없었지, 제 형한테서 받은 거거든."], ["I swopped a book from father’s book-case for it, A Kinsman of Mahomet or Salutary Folly, a scandalous book published in Moscow a hundred years ago, before they had any censorship.", "내가 이걸 얻으려고 아버지 책장에서 책 한 권을 바꿨어. 『마호메트의 친척, 또는 이로운 어리석음』이라고, 백 년 전 모스크바에서, 검열이라는 게 있기도 전에 나온 망측한 책이지."], ["And Morozov has a taste for such things.", "그런데 모로조프는 그런 걸 좋아하거든."], ["He was grateful to me, too....”", "걔도 나한테 고마워했어….\""]], [["Kolya held the cannon in his hand so that all could see and admire it.", "콜랴는 모두가 보고 감탄할 수 있도록 대포를 손에 받쳐 들었다."], ["Ilusha raised himself, and, with his right arm still round the dog, he gazed enchanted at the toy.", "일류샤가 몸을 일으켰고, 여전히 오른팔로는 개를 끌어안은 채, 그 장난감을 황홀하게 바라보았다."], ["The sensation was even greater when Kolya announced that he had gunpowder too, and that it could be fired off at once “if it won’t alarm the ladies.”", "콜랴가 화약도 가져왔다고, 그래서 \"부인들이 놀라지 않으신다면\" 당장 쏘아 볼 수도 있다고 밝히자 흥분은 더욱 커졌다."], ["“Mamma” immediately asked to look at the toy closer and her request was granted.", "\"엄마\"가 곧장 그 장난감을 더 가까이서 보겠다고 청했고, 그 청은 받아들여졌다."], ["She was much pleased with the little bronze cannon on wheels and began rolling it to and fro on her lap.", "그녀는 바퀴 달린 그 자그마한 청동 대포가 무척 마음에 들어, 그것을 제 무릎 위에서 앞뒤로 굴리기 시작했다."], ["She readily gave permission for the cannon to be fired, without any idea of what she had been asked.", "그녀는 자기가 무슨 부탁을 받았는지도 모른 채 선뜻 대포를 쏘아도 좋다고 허락했다."], ["Kolya showed the powder and the shot.", "콜랴가 화약과 탄알을 보여 주었다."], ["The captain, as a military man, undertook to load it, putting in a minute quantity of powder.", "군인이었던 대위가 화약을 아주 조금 넣어 장전하는 일을 맡았다."], ["He asked that the shot might be put off till another time.", "그는 탄알 쏘는 일은 다음으로 미루자고 청했다."], ["The cannon was put on the floor, aiming towards an empty part of the room, three grains of powder were thrust into the touch-hole and a match was put to it.", "대포를 방의 빈 쪽을 겨누어 바닥에 놓고, 화약 세 알을 점화 구멍에 밀어 넣은 다음 거기에 불을 댔다."], ["A magnificent explosion followed.", "굉장한 폭발이 뒤따랐다."], ["Mamma was startled, but at once laughed with delight.", "엄마가 흠칫 놀랐으나 곧 기뻐서 깔깔 웃었다."], ["The boys gazed in speechless triumph.", "아이들은 말없는 환희 속에서 바라보았다."], ["But the captain, looking at Ilusha, was more enchanted than any of them.", "그러나 일류샤를 바라보는 대위가 그들 누구보다 더 황홀해했다."], ["Kolya picked up the cannon and immediately presented it to Ilusha, together with the powder and the shot.", "콜랴는 대포를 집어 들어, 화약과 탄알과 함께 즉시 일류샤에게 선사했다."]], [["“I got it for you, for you!", "\"너 주려고 얻은 거야, 너 주려고!"], ["I’ve been keeping it for you a long time,”", "너 주려고 오래도록 간직해 뒀어.\""], ["he repeated once more in his delight.", "그가 기쁨에 겨워 다시 한번 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Oh, give it to me!", "\"오, 그걸 나 줘!"], ["No, give me the cannon!”", "아니, 그 대포를 나한테 줘!\""], ["mamma began begging like a little child.", "엄마가 어린아이처럼 조르기 시작했다."], ["Her face showed a piteous fear that she would not get it.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 그것을 못 얻을까 봐 애처로운 두려움이 떠올라 있었다."], ["Kolya was disconcerted.", "콜랴는 당황했다."], ["The captain fidgeted uneasily.", "대위는 안절부절못하며 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."]], [["“Mamma, mamma,”", "\"여보, 여보.\" 그가 그녀에게로 달려갔다."], ["he ran to her, “the cannon’s yours, of course, but let Ilusha have it, because it’s a present to him, but it’s just as good as yours.", "\"물론 그 대포는 당신 거요, 하지만 일류샤가 갖게 합시다, 그건 그 애한테 준 선물이니까. 그래도 당신 거나 마찬가지요."], ["Ilusha will always let you play with it; it shall belong to both of you, both of you.”", "일류샤가 언제든 당신이 갖고 놀게 해 줄 테니까. 그건 두 사람 다의 것이 되는 거요, 두 사람 다의.\""]], [["“No, I don’t want it to belong to both of us, I want it to be mine altogether, not Ilusha’s,”", "\"싫어요, 난 두 사람 거가 되는 건 싫어요, 통째로 내 것, 일류샤 게 아니라 내 것이었으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["persisted mamma, on the point of tears.", "엄마가 금세 울음을 터뜨릴 듯이 고집을 부렸다."]], [["“Take it, mother, here, keep it!”", "\"가지세요, 어머니, 자, 가지세요!\""], ["Ilusha cried.", "일류샤가 외쳤다."], ["“Krassotkin, may I give it to my mother?”", "\"크라소트킨, 이걸 어머니께 드려도 될까?\""], ["he turned to Krassotkin with an imploring face, as though he were afraid he might be offended at his giving his present to some one else.", "그는 마치 제가 받은 선물을 다른 사람에게 줘서 그가 기분 상하지나 않을까 두려운 듯, 애원하는 얼굴로 크라소트킨을 돌아보았다."]], [["“Of course you may,”", "\"물론 되고말고.\""], ["Krassotkin assented heartily, and, taking the cannon from Ilusha, he handed it himself to mamma with a polite bow.", "크라소트킨이 흔쾌히 응했고, 일류샤에게서 대포를 받아 정중히 절을 하며 그것을 직접 엄마에게 건넸다."], ["She was so touched that she cried.", "그녀는 어찌나 감동했던지 울었다."]], [["“Ilusha, darling, he’s the one who loves his mamma!”", "\"일류샤, 우리 아가, 이 애야말로 제 엄마를 사랑하는 애구나!\""], ["she said tenderly, and at once began wheeling the cannon to and fro on her lap again.", "그녀가 다정하게 말하고는, 곧 다시 대포를 제 무릎 위에서 앞뒤로 굴리기 시작했다."]], [["“Mamma, let me kiss your hand.”", "\"여보, 당신 손에 입 맞추게 해 주오.\""], ["The captain darted up to her at once and did so.", "대위가 즉시 그녀에게로 달려가 그렇게 했다."]], [["“And I never saw such a charming fellow as this nice boy,”", "\"그리고 이 착한 도련님처럼 사랑스러운 사람은 본 적이 없어요.\""], ["said the grateful lady, pointing to Krassotkin.", "고마움에 찬 부인이 크라소트킨을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“And I’ll bring you as much powder as you like, Ilusha.", "\"그리고 화약도 얼마든지 가져다줄게, 일류샤."], ["We make the powder ourselves now.", "이제 화약은 우리가 직접 만들거든."], ["Borovikov found out how it’s made—twenty-four parts of saltpeter, ten of sulphur and six of birchwood charcoal.", "보로비코프가 만드는 법을 알아냈어. 초석 스물네 몫에 황 열 몫, 그리고 자작나무 숯 여섯 몫."], ["It’s all pounded together, mixed into a paste with water and rubbed through a tammy sieve—that’s how it’s done.”", "그걸 다 함께 빻아서 물에 섞어 반죽으로 만든 다음, 고운 체로 걸러 내는 거야. 그렇게 만드는 거지.\""]], [["“Smurov told me about your powder, only father says it’s not real gunpowder,”", "\"화약 얘기는 스무로프한테 들었어, 그런데 아버지는 그게 진짜 화약이 아니래.\""], ["responded Ilusha.", "일류샤가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Not real?”", "\"진짜가 아니라고?\""], ["Kolya flushed.", "콜랴가 발끈했다."], ["“It burns.", "\"타기는 잘 타는데."], ["I don’t know, of course.”", "뭐, 나도 잘은 모르지만.\""]], [["“No, I didn’t mean that,”", "\"아니, 그런 뜻이 아니었네.\""], ["put in the captain with a guilty face.", "대위가 면구스러운 얼굴로 끼어들었다."], ["“I only said that real powder is not made like that, but that’s nothing, it can be made so.”", "\"다만 진짜 화약은 그렇게 만드는 게 아니라는 말이었지, 하지만 그건 별것 아니야, 그렇게 만들어도 되고말고.\""]], [["“I don’t know, you know best.", "\"잘 모르겠어요, 어른께서 더 잘 아시겠죠."], ["We lighted some in a pomatum pot, it burned splendidly, it all burnt away leaving only a tiny ash.", "우리가 포마드 통에다 좀 불을 붙여 봤더니 멋지게 타더라고요, 아주 자그마한 재만 남기고 다 타 버렸어요."], ["But that was only the paste, and if you rub it through ...", "하지만 그건 반죽 그대로였고, 그걸 체에 걸러 내면…"], ["but of course you know best, I don’t know....", "뭐 어쨌든 어른께서 더 잘 아시겠죠, 저야 모르고요…"], ["And Bulkin’s father thrashed him on account of our powder, did you hear?”", "그리고 불킨은 우리 화약 때문에 아버지한테 두들겨 맞았어, 그거 들었어?\""], ["he turned to Ilusha.", "그가 일류샤를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응.\""], ["answered Ilusha.", "일류샤가 대답했다."], ["He listened to Kolya with immense interest and enjoyment.", "그는 콜랴의 이야기를 엄청난 흥미와 즐거움 속에서 들었다."]], [["“We had prepared a whole bottle of it and he used to keep it under his bed.", "\"우리가 한 병 가득 만들어 놨었는데, 걔가 그걸 침대 밑에 둬 두곤 했거든."], ["His father saw it.", "걔 아버지가 그걸 본 거야."], ["He said it might explode, and thrashed him on the spot.", "그게 터질지도 모른다면서 그 자리에서 두들겨 팼지."], ["He was going to make a complaint against me to the masters.", "나를 선생님들한테 일러바치겠다고 하더라고."], ["He is not allowed to go about with me now, no one is allowed to go about with me now.", "이제 걔는 나하고 어울리는 게 금지됐어, 이제 아무도 나하고 못 어울리게 돼 버렸지."], ["Smurov is not allowed to either, I’ve got a bad name with every one.", "스무로프도 안 되고, 다들 나를 나쁜 놈으로 여기거든."], ["They say I’m a ‘desperate character,’”", "내가 '막돼먹은 녀석'이라는 거야.\""], ["Kolya smiled scornfully.", "콜랴가 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃었다."], ["“It all began from what happened on the railway.”", "\"이게 다 그 철길 사건에서 시작된 거지.\""]], [["“Ah, we’ve heard of that exploit of yours, too,”", "\"아, 우리도 그 무용담을 들었네.\" 대위가 외쳤다."], ["cried the captain. “How could you lie still on the line?", "\"어떻게 선로 위에 가만히 누워 있을 수가 있었나?"], ["Is it possible you weren’t the least afraid, lying there under the train?", "정말 조금도 안 무서웠단 말인가, 기차 밑에 그렇게 누워 있으면서?"], ["Weren’t you frightened?”", "겁나지 않았나?\""]], [["The captain was abject in his flattery of Kolya.", "대위는 콜랴를 추어올리는 데 비굴할 지경이었다."]], [["“N—not particularly,”", "\"그…그렇게까지 무섭진 않았어요.\""], ["answered Kolya carelessly.", "콜랴가 무심하게 대답했다."], ["“What’s blasted my reputation more than anything here was that cursed goose,”", "\"여기서 무엇보다 제 평판을 망쳐 놓은 건 그 빌어먹을 거위 사건이었죠.\""], ["he said, turning again to Ilusha.", "그가 다시 일류샤를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["But though he assumed an unconcerned air as he talked, he still could not control himself and was continually missing the note he tried to keep up.", "그러나 이야기하는 동안 짐짓 무심한 태도를 꾸미면서도 그는 여전히 자제하지 못해, 유지하려던 가락을 끊임없이 놓치곤 했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I heard about the goose!”", "그 거위 얘기는 들었어!\""], ["Ilusha laughed, beaming all over.", "일류샤가 온 얼굴에 환한 웃음을 띠며 웃었다."], ["“They told me, but I didn’t understand.", "\"사람들이 얘기해 줬는데, 난 잘 못 알아들었어."], ["Did they really take you to the court?”", "정말로 너를 법정에까지 데려갔어?\""]], [["“The most stupid, trivial affair, they made a mountain of a molehill as they always do,”", "\"세상에서 제일 멍청하고 하찮은 일을, 그치들이 늘 하듯이 침소봉대한 거지.\""], ["Kolya began carelessly.", "콜랴가 무심하게 운을 뗐다."], ["“I was walking through the market-place here one day, just when they’d driven in the geese.", "\"어느 날 여기 장터를 지나가는데, 마침 거위들을 몰아넣었을 때였어."], ["I stopped and looked at them.", "내가 걸음을 멈추고 거위들을 보고 있었지."], ["All at once a fellow, who is an errand-boy at Plotnikov’s now, looked at me and said, ‘What are you looking at the geese for?’ I looked at him; he was a stupid, moon-faced fellow of twenty.", "그런데 별안간 지금 플로트니코프네 심부름꾼으로 있는 어떤 녀석이 나를 보더니, '뭣 하러 거위를 보고 있냐?' 하더라고."], ["I am always on the side of the peasantry, you know.", "내가 그 녀석을 봤지. 멍청하고 둥근 달덩이 같은 얼굴의 스무 살짜리였어."], ["I like talking to the peasants....", "나는 늘 농민 편이거든, 알다시피."], ["We’ve dropped behind the peasants—that’s an axiom.", "농민들하고 얘기하는 걸 좋아해… 우리는 농민들한테 뒤떨어져 있어, 그건 자명한 이치지."], ["I believe you are laughing, Karamazov?”", "너 웃는 것 같은데, 카라마조프?\""]], [["“No, Heaven forbid, I am listening,”", "\"아냐, 천만에, 듣고 있어.\""], ["said Alyosha with a most good-natured air, and the sensitive Kolya was immediately reassured.", "알료샤가 더없이 선량한 태도로 말했고, 예민한 콜랴는 즉시 안심했다."]], [["“My theory, Karamazov, is clear and simple,”", "\"내 이론은, 카라마조프, 명료하고 단순해.\""], ["he hurried on again, looking pleased.", "그가 흡족한 표정으로 다시 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["“I believe in the people and am always glad to give them their due, but I am not for spoiling them, that is a sine qua non ...", "\"나는 민중을 믿고, 늘 그들에게 마땅한 몫을 주고 싶지만, 그렇다고 그들을 응석받이로 만드는 건 반대야, 그건 절대적인 전제 조건이지…"], ["But I was telling you about the goose.", "그런데 거위 얘기를 하고 있었지."], ["So I turned to the fool and answered, ‘I am wondering what the goose thinks about.’ He looked at me quite stupidly, ‘And what does the goose think about?’ he asked.", "그래서 내가 그 바보한테 몸을 돌리고 대답했어. '저 거위가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 궁금해서.'"], ["‘Do you see that cart full of oats?’ I said.", "그 녀석이 아주 멍청하게 나를 보더니, '거위가 무슨 생각을 한다고요?'"], ["‘The oats are dropping out of the sack, and the goose has put its neck right under the wheel to gobble them up—do you see?’ ‘I see that quite well,’ he said.", "하고 묻더라고. '저기 귀리가 가득 실린 짐수레 보이지?'"], ["‘Well,’ said I, ‘if that cart were to move on a little, would it break the goose’s neck or not?’ ‘It’d be sure to break it,’ and he grinned all over his face, highly delighted.", "하고 내가 말했지. '귀리가 자루에서 떨어지고 있는데, 거위가 그걸 주워 먹으려고 바퀴 바로 밑에다 목을 들이밀고 있잖아, 보여?'"], ["‘Come on, then,’ said I, ‘let’s try.’ ‘Let’s,’ he said.", "'잘 보이는데요.' 하더라고."], ["And it did not take us long to arrange: he stood at the bridle without being noticed, and I stood on one side to direct the goose.", "'그럼,' 하고 내가 말했어, '저 짐수레가 조금만 앞으로 움직이면 거위 목이 부러질까, 안 부러질까?'"], ["And the owner wasn’t looking, he was talking to some one, so I had nothing to do, the goose thrust its head in after the oats of itself, under the cart, just under the wheel.", "'틀림없이 부러지죠.' 하면서 그 녀석이 무척 신이 나서 얼굴 가득 히죽거리는 거야."], ["I winked at the lad, he tugged at the bridle, and crack.", "'그럼 가서,' 하고 내가 말했어, '한번 해 보자.'"], ["The goose’s neck was broken in half.", "'해 봅시다.' 하더라고."], ["And, as luck would have it, all the peasants saw us at that moment and they kicked up a shindy at once.", "그러고는 채비하는 데 오래 걸리지도 않았어. 그 녀석이 눈에 안 띄게 고삐 곁에 서고, 나는 한쪽에 서서 거위를 몰아갔지."], ["‘You did that on purpose!’ ‘No, not on purpose.’ ‘Yes, you did, on purpose!’ Well, they shouted, ‘Take him to the justice of the peace!’ They took me, too.", "그런데 주인은 안 보고 있었어, 누군가하고 얘기를 하고 있었거든, 그러니 나는 할 게 없었어, 거위가 제 발로 귀리를 좇아 짐수레 밑, 바퀴 바로 밑으로 머리를 들이밀더라고. 내가 그 녀석한테 눈짓을 했더니, 녀석이 고삐를 홱 잡아당겼고, 우두둑."], ["‘You were there, too,’ they said, ‘you helped, you’re known all over the market!’ And, for some reason, I really am known all over the market,”", "거위 목이 두 동강 났지. 그런데 운수 사납게도, 바로 그 순간 농민들이 죄다 우리를 봤고 당장 한바탕 난리가 났어."], ["Kolya added conceitedly.", "'일부러 그랬지!' '아뇨, 일부러가 아니에요.'"], ["“We all went off to the justice’s, they brought the goose, too.", "'아냐, 일부러 그랬어!' 그래서 그치들이 외쳐 댔지, '저놈을 치안 판사한테 끌고 가!'"], ["The fellow was crying in a great funk, simply blubbering like a woman.", "나도 끌고 갔어. '너도 거기 있었잖아,'"], ["And the farmer kept shouting that you could kill any number of geese like that.", "하면서, '네가 거들었어, 너야 온 장터가 다 아는 놈이지!' 그런데 무슨 영문인지 나는 정말로 온 장터에 다 알려져 있거든.\""], ["Well, of course, there were witnesses.", "콜랴가 으스대며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 다들 치안 판사한테 갔고, 거위도 가져갔지."], ["The justice of the peace settled it in a minute, that the farmer was to be paid a rouble for the goose, and the fellow to have the goose.", "그 녀석은 잔뜩 겁에 질려 울고 있었어, 그냥 여편네처럼 엉엉 울더라고. 그리고 그 농사꾼은 그런 식으로 거위를 얼마든지 죽일 수 있겠다고 줄곧 소리쳤지."], ["And he was warned not to play such pranks again.", "뭐, 물론 증인들도 있었고. 치안 판사가 단박에 처결했어, 농사꾼한테 거위 값으로 일 루블을 물어 주고, 거위는 그 녀석이 갖는 걸로."], ["And the fellow kept blubbering like a woman.", "그리고 다시는 그런 장난을 치지 말라고 주의를 줬지. 그런데 그 녀석은 여편네처럼 계속 엉엉 우는 거야."], ["‘It wasn’t me,’ he said, ‘it was he egged me on,’ and he pointed to me.", "'제가 한 게 아니에요,' 하면서, '저 애가 부추긴 거예요.'"], ["I answered with the utmost composure that I hadn’t egged him on, that I simply stated the general proposition, had spoken hypothetically.", "하고 나를 가리키더라고. 나는 더없이 침착하게, 내가 부추긴 게 아니라 그저 일반적인 명제를 말했을 뿐이라고, 가정해서 말한 것뿐이라고 대답했어."], ["The justice of the peace smiled and was vexed with himself at once for having smiled.", "치안 판사가 빙긋 웃더니, 웃었다는 게 즉시 분해진 모양이더라고."], ["‘I’ll complain to your masters of you, so that for the future you mayn’t waste your time on such general propositions, instead of sitting at your books and learning your lessons.’ He didn’t complain to the masters, that was a joke, but the matter was noised abroad and came to the ears of the masters.", "'앞으로는 책상 앞에 앉아 책을 보고 공부나 할 것이지, 그런 일반적인 명제 따위에 시간을 허비하지 못하도록 너희 선생님들한테 일러바치겠다.' 선생님들한테 일러바치진 않았어, 그건 농담이었지, 하지만 그 일이 소문나서 선생님들 귀에까지 들어갔어."], ["Their ears are long, you know!", "그분들 귀는 길거든, 알다시피!"], ["The classical master, Kolbasnikov, was particularly shocked about it, but Dardanelov got me off again.", "고전어 선생인 콜바스니코프가 특히 그 일에 펄펄 뛰었는데, 다르다넬로프가 또 나를 구해 줬지."], ["But Kolbasnikov is savage with every one now like a green ass.", "그런데 콜바스니코프는 요즘 어디서 풀이 죽은 당나귀처럼 모두한테 사납게 군다니까."], ["Did you know, Ilusha, he is just married, got a dowry of a thousand roubles, and his bride’s a regular fright of the first rank and the last degree.", "너 알아, 일류샤, 그 사람이 얼마 전에 결혼했는데, 천 루블 지참금을 받았다지만, 신부가 일등급으로 추하고 최하등으로 못생긴 진짜 흉물이래."], ["The third-class fellows wrote an epigram on it:", "삼 학년 애들이 거기에 풍자시를 지었어."]], [["Astounding news has reached the class, Kolbasnikov has been an ass.", "놀라운 소식이 학급에 닿았네,\n콜바스니코프가 당나귀 됐다네."]], [["And so on, awfully funny, I’ll bring it to you later on.", "이런 식으로, 무척 우스워, 나중에 가져다줄게."], ["I say nothing against Dardanelov, he is a learned man, there’s no doubt about it.", "다르다넬로프를 두고는 험담할 게 없어, 그분은 학식 있는 사람이야, 그건 의심할 여지가 없지."], ["I respect men like that and it’s not because he stood up for me.”", "나는 그런 사람들을 존경해, 그분이 나를 변호해 줘서가 아니라.\""]], [["“But you took him down about the founders of Troy!”", "\"하지만 형은 트로이를 세운 사람들 문제로 그 사람을 코 납작하게 만들었잖아!\""], ["Smurov put in suddenly, unmistakably proud of Krassotkin at such a moment.", "스무로프가 별안간 끼어들었는데, 그런 순간 크라소트킨이 자랑스럽기 짝이 없는 기색이 역력했다."], ["He was particularly pleased with the story of the goose.", "그는 거위 이야기가 특히 마음에 들었던 것이다."]], [["“Did you really take him down?”", "\"정말로 그를 코 납작하게 만들었나?\""], ["the captain inquired, in a flattering way.", "대위가 추어올리듯 물었다."], ["“On the question who founded Troy?", "\"누가 트로이를 세웠느냐는 문제로 말이지?"], ["We heard of it, Ilusha told me about it at the time.”", "우리도 그 얘긴 들었네, 그때 일류샤가 내게 얘기해 줬거든.\""]], [["“He knows everything, father, he knows more than any of us!”", "\"이 형은 뭐든지 다 알아요, 아버지, 우리 중 누구보다 많이 안다고요!\""], ["put in Ilusha; “he only pretends to be like that, but really he is top in every subject....”", "일류샤가 끼어들었다. \"겉으로만 저런 척하지, 사실은 무슨 과목이든 일등이에요….\""]], [["Ilusha looked at Kolya with infinite happiness.", "일류샤는 그지없는 행복에 찬 눈길로 콜랴를 바라보았다."]], [["“Oh, that’s all nonsense about Troy, a trivial matter.", "\"오, 트로이 얘기는 다 시시한 거야, 하찮은 일이지."], ["I consider this an unimportant question,”", "나는 그걸 대수롭지 않은 문제로 여겨.\""], ["said Kolya with haughty humility.", "콜랴가 거만한 겸손을 담아 말했다."], ["He had by now completely recovered his dignity, though he was still a little uneasy.", "이제 그는 위엄을 완전히 되찾았으나, 그래도 여전히 조금은 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["He felt that he was greatly excited and that he had talked about the goose, for instance, with too little reserve, while Alyosha had looked serious and had not said a word all the time.", "자기가 무척 흥분해 있다는 것을, 가령 거위 이야기를 너무 거리낌 없이 떠벌렸다는 것을 느꼈는데, 그동안 알료샤는 시종 진지한 얼굴로 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["And the vain boy began by degrees to have a rankling fear that Alyosha was silent because he despised him, and thought he was showing off before him.", "그러자 그 허영심 많은 아이는 차츰, 알료샤가 잠자코 있는 것은 자기를 경멸하기 때문이며, 자기가 그 앞에서 잘난 척한다고 여기는 게 아닐까 하는 쓰라린 두려움을 품기 시작했다."], ["If he dared to think anything like that Kolya would—", "만일 알료샤가 감히 그런 생각을 한다면 콜랴는—"]], [["“I regard the question as quite a trivial one,”", "\"나는 그 문제를 아주 하찮은 거로 본다고.\""], ["he rapped out again, proudly.", "그가 거만하게 다시 한번 딱 잘라 말했다."]], [["“And I know who founded Troy,”", "\"그리고 나는 누가 트로이를 세웠는지 알아.\""], ["a boy, who had not spoken before, said suddenly, to the surprise of every one.", "그때껏 한마디도 하지 않던 한 아이가 모두를 놀라게 하며 별안간 말했다."], ["He was silent and seemed to be shy.", "그는 말이 없었고 수줍어하는 듯 보였다."], ["He was a pretty boy of about eleven, called Kartashov. He was sitting near the door.", "카르타쇼프라는 열한 살가량의 예쁘장한 아이로, 문 가까이에 앉아 있었다."], ["Kolya looked at him with dignified amazement.", "콜랴가 위엄 어린 놀라움으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["The fact was that the identity of the founders of Troy had become a secret for the whole school, a secret which could only be discovered by reading Smaragdov, and no one had Smaragdov but Kolya.", "사실 트로이를 세운 사람들이 누구냐 하는 것은 온 학교에 비밀이 되어 있었으니, 그 비밀은 오로지 스마라그도프를 읽어야만 알아낼 수 있는 것이었고, 콜랴 말고는 아무도 스마라그도프를 갖고 있지 않았다."], ["One day, when Kolya’s back was turned, Kartashov hastily opened Smaragdov, which lay among Kolya’s books, and immediately lighted on the passage relating to the foundation of Troy.", "어느 날 콜랴가 등을 돌리고 있을 때, 카르타쇼프가 콜랴의 책들 틈에 놓인 스마라그도프를 잽싸게 펼쳤고, 곧장 트로이 건국에 관한 대목을 짚어 냈던 것이다."], ["This was a good time ago, but he felt uneasy and could not bring himself to announce publicly that he too knew who had founded Troy, afraid of what might happen and of Krassotkin’s somehow putting him to shame over it.", "이것은 한참 전의 일이었으나, 그는 무슨 일이 벌어질지, 또 크라소트킨이 어떻게든 그 일로 자기를 망신 주지나 않을지 두려워, 자기도 누가 트로이를 세웠는지 안다고 차마 공공연히 밝히지 못한 채 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["But now he couldn’t resist saying it.", "그러나 이제 그는 그 말을 참을 수가 없었다."], ["For weeks he had been longing to.", "몇 주 동안이나 그 말을 하고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다."]], [["“Well, who did found it?”", "\"그래, 누가 세웠는데?\""], ["asked Kolya, turning to him with haughty superciliousness.", "콜랴가 거만한 오만함을 담아 그를 향해 돌아서며 물었다."], ["He saw from his face that he really did know and at once made up his mind how to take it.", "그는 그 아이의 얼굴에서 그가 정말로 알고 있다는 것을 알아채고는 즉시 어떻게 받아넘길지 마음을 정했다."], ["There was, so to speak, a discordant note in the general harmony.", "말하자면 전체의 화음에 불협화음 한 가락이 끼어든 셈이었다."]], [["“Troy was founded by Teucer, Dardanus, Ilius and Tros,”", "\"트로이는 테우케르, 다르다노스, 일로스, 트로스가 세웠어.\""], ["the boy rapped out at once, and in the same instant he blushed, blushed so, that it was painful to look at him.", "그 아이가 단번에 딱 잘라 말하고는, 그 순간 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 어찌나 붉혔던지 보기에 딱할 지경이었다."], ["But the boys stared at him, stared at him for a whole minute, and then all the staring eyes turned at once and were fastened upon Kolya, who was still scanning the audacious boy with disdainful composure.", "그러나 아이들은 그를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 꼬박 일 분 동안 그를 쳐다보다가, 이윽고 그 응시하던 눈들이 일제히 콜랴에게로 쏠려 못 박혔으니, 콜랴는 여전히 그 당돌한 아이를 멸시 어린 침착함으로 훑어보고 있었다."]], [["“In what sense did they found it?”", "\"무슨 뜻으로 그들이 세웠다는 거지?\""], ["he deigned to comment at last.", "마침내 그가 한마디 던지는 영광을 베풀었다."], ["“And what is meant by founding a city or a state?", "\"그리고 도시나 국가를 세운다는 건 무슨 뜻이야?"], ["What do they do?", "그 사람들이 뭘 했다는 건데?"], ["Did they go and each lay a brick, do you suppose?”", "한 사람씩 가서 벽돌을 한 장씩 놓았다, 그렇게 생각하는 거야?\""]], [["There was laughter.", "웃음이 터졌다."], ["The offending boy turned from pink to crimson.", "무안을 당한 아이는 분홍빛에서 진홍빛으로 변했다."], ["He was silent and on the point of tears.", "그는 입을 다문 채 금세 울음을 터뜨릴 듯했다."], ["Kolya held him so for a minute.", "콜랴는 잠시 그를 그대로 붙들어 두었다."]], [["“Before you talk of a historical event like the foundation of a nationality, you must first understand what you mean by it,”", "\"한 민족의 건국 같은 역사적 사건을 입에 올리려면, 우선 그게 무슨 뜻인지부터 알아야 해.\""], ["he admonished him in stern, incisive tones.", "그가 준엄하고 날카로운 어조로 그를 타일렀다."], ["“But I attach no consequence to these old wives’ tales and I don’t think much of universal history in general,”", "\"하지만 나는 이런 노파들의 옛이야기 따위엔 아무 의미도 두지 않고, 세계사라는 것 전반을 대수롭잖게 여겨.\""], ["he added carelessly, addressing the company generally.", "그가 좌중 전체를 향해 무심하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Universal history?”", "\"세계사를요?\""], ["the captain inquired, looking almost scared.", "대위가 거의 겁먹은 표정으로 물었다."]], [["“Yes, universal history!", "\"네, 세계사요!"], ["It’s the study of the successive follies of mankind and nothing more.", "그건 인류가 차례로 저질러 온 어리석은 짓들에 대한 연구일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요."], ["The only subjects I respect are mathematics and natural science,”", "제가 존중하는 학문은 오직 수학과 자연 과학뿐이죠.\""], ["said Kolya.", "콜랴가 말했다."], ["He was showing off and he stole a glance at Alyosha; his was the only opinion he was afraid of there.", "그는 잘난 척하면서 알료샤를 흘끔 곁눈질했다. 그곳에서 그가 두려워하는 의견은 오직 알료샤의 것뿐이었다."], ["But Alyosha was still silent and still serious as before.", "그러나 알료샤는 여전히 잠자코, 여전히 전처럼 진지했다."], ["If Alyosha had said a word it would have stopped him, but Alyosha was silent and “it might be the silence of contempt,”", "알료샤가 한마디만 했더라도 그를 멈추게 했을 텐데, 알료샤는 입을 다물고 있었고 \"그건 경멸의 침묵일지도 몰라\""], ["and that finally irritated Kolya.", "싶었으니, 그것이 끝내 콜랴를 자극했다."]], [["“The classical languages, too ...", "\"고전어들도 그래요…"], ["they are simply madness, nothing more.", "그건 그저 미친 짓일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요."], ["You seem to disagree with me again, Karamazov?”", "또 제 말에 동의하지 않으시는 것 같은데, 카라마조프?\""]], [["“I don’t agree,”", "\"동의하지 않아.\""], ["said Alyosha, with a faint smile.", "알료샤가 엷은 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“The study of the classics, if you ask my opinion, is simply a police measure, that’s simply why it has been introduced into our schools.”", "\"고전어 공부란, 제 의견을 묻는다면, 그저 경찰 행정상의 방책일 뿐이고, 그게 바로 그게 우리 학교에 도입된 까닭이죠.\""], ["By degrees Kolya began to get breathless again.", "차츰 콜랴가 다시 숨이 가빠지기 시작했다."], ["“Latin and Greek were introduced because they are a bore and because they stupefy the intellect.", "\"라틴어와 그리스어가 도입된 건 그것이 따분하고 지력을 마비시키기 때문이에요."], ["It was dull before, so what could they do to make things duller?", "전부터 지루했으니, 더 지루하게 만들려고 무슨 수를 쓸 수 있었겠어요?"], ["It was senseless enough before, so what could they do to make it more senseless?", "전부터 충분히 무의미했으니, 더 무의미하게 만들려고 무슨 수를 쓸 수 있었겠냐고요?"], ["So they thought of Greek and Latin.", "그래서 그리스어와 라틴어를 생각해 낸 거죠."], ["That’s my opinion, I hope I shall never change it,”", "그게 제 의견이에요, 부디 이 생각을 절대 바꾸지 않았으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Kolya finished abruptly.", "콜랴가 불쑥 말을 맺었다."], ["His cheeks were flushed.", "그의 두 뺨은 발갛게 달아올라 있었다."]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞는 말이야.\""], ["assented Smurov suddenly, in a ringing tone of conviction.", "스무로프가 별안간 낭랑하고 확신에 찬 어조로 맞장구쳤다."], ["He had listened attentively.", "그는 주의 깊게 듣고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“And yet he is first in Latin himself,”", "\"그런데 형은 자기가 라틴어에서 일등이잖아.\""], ["cried one of the group of boys suddenly.", "아이들 무리 가운데 하나가 별안간 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, father, he says that and yet he is first in Latin,”", "\"맞아요, 아버지, 형은 그렇게 말하면서도 라틴어에서 일등이라고요.\""], ["echoed Ilusha.", "일류샤가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“What of it?”", "\"그게 어쨌다는 거야?\""], ["Kolya thought fit to defend himself, though the praise was very sweet to him.", "콜랴가 변명해야겠다고 여겼는데, 그 칭찬이 그에게는 무척 달콤하기는 했다."], ["“I am fagging away at Latin because I have to, because I promised my mother to pass my examination, and I think that whatever you do, it’s worth doing it well.", "\"내가 라틴어에 죽어라 매달리는 건 그래야만 하기 때문이야, 어머니한테 시험에 합격하겠다고 약속했거든, 그리고 나는 무슨 일을 하든 제대로 할 만한 값어치가 있다고 생각하니까."], ["But in my soul I have a profound contempt for the classics and all that fraud....", "하지만 마음속으로는 고전어니 그 사기극이니 하는 것들을 깊이 경멸하고 있어…"], ["You don’t agree, Karamazov?”", "동의하지 않아, 카라마조프?\""]], [["“Why ‘fraud’?”", "\"왜 '사기극'이야?\""], ["Alyosha smiled again.", "알료샤가 다시 미소 지었다."]], [["“Well, all the classical authors have been translated into all languages, so it was not for the sake of studying the classics they introduced Latin, but solely as a police measure, to stupefy the intelligence.", "\"아니, 고전 작가들은 죄다 온갖 언어로 번역돼 있잖아, 그러니 고전을 공부하자고 라틴어를 도입한 게 아니라, 오로지 경찰 행정상의 방책으로, 지력을 마비시키려고 도입한 거지."], ["So what can one call it but a fraud?”", "그러니 그걸 사기극 말고 뭐라고 부를 수 있겠어?\""]], [["“Why, who taught you all this?”", "\"아니, 그런 건 다 누가 가르쳐 줬어?\""], ["cried Alyosha, surprised at last.", "알료샤가 끝내 놀라며 소리쳤다."]], [["“In the first place I am capable of thinking for myself without being taught.", "\"첫째로 나는 누가 가르쳐 주지 않아도 스스로 생각할 줄 알아."], ["Besides, what I said just now about the classics being translated our teacher Kolbasnikov has said to the whole of the third class.”", "게다가 방금 고전이 번역돼 있다고 한 말은, 우리 선생 콜바스니코프가 삼 학년 전체한테 한 얘기야.\""]], [["“The doctor has come!”", "\"의사 선생님이 오셨어요!\""], ["cried Nina, who had been silent till then.", "그때껏 잠자코 있던 니나가 외쳤다."]], [["A carriage belonging to Madame Hohlakov drove up to the gate.", "호흘라코바 부인의 마차 한 대가 대문 앞으로 다가왔다."], ["The captain, who had been expecting the doctor all the morning, rushed headlong out to meet him.", "아침 내내 의사를 기다리던 대위가 그를 맞으러 부리나케 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["“Mamma” pulled herself together and assumed a dignified air.", "\"엄마\"는 정신을 가다듬고 위엄 있는 태도를 꾸몄다."], ["Alyosha went up to Ilusha and began setting his pillows straight.", "알료샤는 일류샤에게 다가가 베개를 바로 놓아 주기 시작했다."], ["Nina, from her invalid chair, anxiously watched him putting the bed tidy.", "니나는 환자용 의자에 앉은 채, 그가 침대를 정돈하는 것을 근심스레 지켜보았다."], ["The boys hurriedly took leave.", "아이들은 황급히 작별을 고했다."], ["Some of them promised to come again in the evening.", "그중 몇몇은 저녁에 다시 오겠다고 약속했다."], ["Kolya called Perezvon and the dog jumped off the bed.", "콜랴가 페레즈본을 부르자 개가 침대에서 뛰어내렸다."]], [["“I won’t go away, I won’t go away,”", "\"나는 안 갈게, 안 가.\""], ["Kolya said hastily to Ilusha.", "콜랴가 일류샤에게 다급히 말했다."], ["“I’ll wait in the passage and come back when the doctor’s gone, I’ll come back with Perezvon.”", "\"복도에서 기다리다가 의사 선생님이 가시면 다시 올게, 페레즈본하고 같이 돌아올게.\""]], [["But by now the doctor had entered, an important-looking person with long, dark whiskers and a shiny, shaven chin, wearing a bearskin coat.", "그러나 그사이에 의사가 들어와 있었는데, 길고 검은 구레나룻과 윤이 나도록 면도한 턱을 한, 곰가죽 외투를 걸친 거드름 피우는 인물이었다."], ["As he crossed the threshold he stopped, taken aback; he probably fancied he had come to the wrong place.", "그는 문지방을 넘어서자 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 섰다. 아마도 자기가 엉뚱한 곳에 온 줄로 여긴 모양이었다."], ["“How is this? Where am I?”", "\"이게 어찌 된 일이지?"], ["he muttered, not removing his coat nor his peaked sealskin cap.", "여기가 어디야?\" 그가 외투도 끝이 뾰족한 물개가죽 모자도 벗지 않은 채 웅얼거렸다."], ["The crowd, the poverty of the room, the washing hanging on a line in the corner, puzzled him.", "북적이는 사람들이며, 방의 궁색함이며, 구석에 줄을 매고 널어놓은 빨래가 그를 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["The captain, bent double, was bowing low before him.", "대위는 허리를 한껏 굽힌 채 그 앞에서 굽실거리며 절을 했다."]], [["“It’s here, sir, here, sir,”", "\"여깁니다, 선생님, 여기예요, 선생님.\""], ["he muttered cringingly; “it’s here, you’ve come right, you were coming to us...”", "그가 굽실거리며 웅얼거렸다. \"여기가 맞습니다, 제대로 오셨어요, 저희 집에 오시던 길이니까요….\""]], [["“Sne-gi-ryov?”", "\"스네-기-료프?\""], ["the doctor said loudly and pompously.", "의사가 큰 소리로 거드름을 피우며 말했다."], ["“Mr. Snegiryov—is that you?”", "\"스네기료프 씨, 당신이오?\""]], [["“That’s me, sir!”", "\"접니다, 선생님!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""]], [["The doctor looked round the room with a squeamish air once more and threw off his coat, displaying to all eyes the grand decoration at his neck.", "의사는 비위 상한다는 듯한 기색으로 다시 한번 방을 둘러보고는 외투를 벗어 던졌고, 그러자 목에 걸린 큼지막한 훈장이 모든 이의 눈에 드러났다."], ["The captain caught the fur coat in the air, and the doctor took off his cap.", "대위가 허공에서 그 모피 외투를 받아 들었고, 의사는 모자를 벗었다."]], [["“Where is the patient?”", "\"환자는 어디 있소?\""], ["he asked emphatically.", "그가 힘주어 물었다."]], [["# Chapter VI. Precocity", "# Chapter VI. Precocity"]], [["“What do you think the doctor will say to him?”", "\"의사가 그 애한테 뭐라고 할 것 같으세요?\""], ["Kolya asked quickly.", "콜랴가 다급하게 물었다."], ["“What a repulsive mug, though, hasn’t he?", "\"그건 그렇고, 저 상판대기가 어찌나 역겨운지요?"], ["I can’t endure medicine!”", "저는 의학이라면 도무지 견딜 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“Ilusha is dying.", "\"일류샤는 죽어 가고 있어."], ["I think that’s certain,”", "그건 틀림없는 사실인 것 같아.\""], ["answered Alyosha, mournfully.", "알료샤가 침울하게 대답했다."]], [["“They are rogues!", "\"다들 사기꾼이에요!"], ["Medicine’s a fraud!", "의학은 협잡질이라고요!"], ["I am glad to have made your acquaintance, though, Karamazov.", "그래도 카라마조프, 당신과 알게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["I wanted to know you for a long time.", "오래전부터 당신을 알고 싶었어요."], ["I am only sorry we meet in such sad circumstances.”", "다만 이렇게 슬픈 처지에서 만나게 된 것이 유감스러울 뿐이에요.\""]], [["Kolya had a great inclination to say something even warmer and more demonstrative, but he felt ill at ease.", "콜랴는 그보다 더 따뜻하고 더 마음을 드러내는 무언가를 말하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았지만, 어쩐지 거북했다."], ["Alyosha noticed this, smiled, and pressed his hand.", "알료샤는 그것을 눈치채고 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“I’ve long learned to respect you as a rare person,”", "\"저는 오래전부터 당신을 보기 드문 사람으로 존경해 왔어요.\""], ["Kolya muttered again, faltering and uncertain.", "콜랴가 더듬거리며 머뭇머뭇 다시 중얼거렸다."], ["“I have heard you are a mystic and have been in the monastery.", "\"당신이 신비주의자이고 수도원에 있었다는 말을 들었어요."], ["I know you are a mystic, but ...", "당신이 신비주의자라는 건 알아요. 하지만…"], ["that hasn’t put me off.", "그것 때문에 마음이 식지는 않았어요."], ["Contact with real life will cure you....", "실제 삶과 부딪히다 보면 당신도 그게 고쳐질 거예요…."], ["It’s always so with characters like yours.”", "당신 같은 성격은 늘 그렇거든요.\""]], [["“What do you mean by mystic?", "\"신비주의자라니 무슨 뜻이지?"], ["Cure me of what?”", "무엇이 고쳐진다는 거고?\""], ["Alyosha was rather astonished.", "알료샤는 다소 놀랐다."]], [["“Oh, God and all the rest of it.”", "\"아, 신이라든가 그 밖의 모든 것 말이에요.\""]], [["“What, don’t you believe in God?”", "\"뭐라고, 너는 신을 믿지 않니?\""]], [["“Oh, I’ve nothing against God.", "\"아니요, 저는 신에 대해 반감을 가진 건 아니에요."], ["Of course, God is only a hypothesis, but ...", "물론 신이란 하나의 가설일 뿐이지만…"], ["I admit that He is needed ...", "그래도 신이 필요하다는 건 인정해요…"], ["for the order of the universe and all that ...", "우주의 질서라든가 그런 모든 것을 위해서요…"], ["and that if there were no God He would have to be invented,”", "그리고 만약 신이 없다면 신을 만들어 내야 했을 거예요.\""], ["added Kolya, beginning to blush.", "콜랴가 얼굴을 붉히기 시작하며 덧붙였다."], ["He suddenly fancied that Alyosha might think he was trying to show off his knowledge and to prove that he was “grown up.”", "그는 문득 알료샤가 자기더러 지식을 자랑하려 들고 자기가 \"어른\"임을 증명하려 한다고 여길지 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["“I haven’t the slightest desire to show off my knowledge to him,”", "'나는 그 사람한테 지식을 자랑할 마음 따위는 눈곱만큼도 없는데.'"], ["Kolya thought indignantly.", "콜랴는 분하게 생각했다."], ["And all of a sudden he felt horribly annoyed.", "그러자 갑자기 견딜 수 없이 짜증이 치밀었다."]], [["“I must confess I can’t endure entering on such discussions,”", "\"솔직히 말해서 저는 이런 논의에 끼어드는 걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\""], ["he said with a final air.", "그가 매듭을 짓는 듯한 어조로 말했다."], ["“It’s possible for one who doesn’t believe in God to love mankind, don’t you think so?", "\"신을 믿지 않는 사람도 인류를 사랑할 수 있는 거 아니겠어요?"], ["Voltaire didn’t believe in God and loved mankind?”", "볼테르는 신을 믿지 않았지만 인류를 사랑했잖아요?\""], ["(“I am at it again,”", "('또 시작이군.'"], ["he thought to himself.)", "그는 속으로 생각했다.)"]], [["“Voltaire believed in God, though not very much, I think, and I don’t think he loved mankind very much either,”", "\"볼테르는 신을 믿었어. 그다지 깊이는 아니었던 것 같지만 말이야."], ["said Alyosha quietly, gently, and quite naturally, as though he were talking to some one of his own age, or even older.", "그리고 인류를 그렇게 사랑한 것 같지도 않아.\" 알료샤가 조용하고 부드럽게, 마치 자기 또래나 더 나이 든 누군가와 이야기를 나누듯이 아주 자연스럽게 말했다."], ["Kolya was particularly struck by Alyosha’s apparent diffidence about his opinion of Voltaire.", "콜랴는 볼테르에 관한 자기 견해를 두고 알료샤가 보이는 조심스러운 태도에 유난히 마음이 끌렸다."], ["He seemed to be leaving the question for him, little Kolya, to settle.", "마치 그 문제를 어린 콜랴 자신이 결판내도록 남겨 두는 듯했다."]], [["“Have you read Voltaire?”", "\"너는 볼테르를 읽어 봤니?\""], ["Alyosha finished.", "알료샤가 말을 맺었다."]], [["“No, not to say read....", "\"아니요, 읽었다고 할 정도는…."], ["But I’ve read Candide in the Russian translation ...", "하지만 《캉디드》를 러시아어 번역본으로 읽긴 했어요…"], ["in an absurd, grotesque, old translation ...", "우스꽝스럽고 괴상한, 케케묵은 번역본이었지만요…"], ["(At it again!", "(또 시작이군!"], ["again!)”", "또!)\""]], [["“And did you understand it?”", "\"그래서 이해는 했고?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, everything....", "\"아, 네, 다 이해했어요…."], ["That is ...", "그러니까…"], ["Why do you suppose I shouldn’t understand it?", "어째서 제가 이해 못 했으리라고 생각하세요?"], ["There’s a lot of nastiness in it, of course....", "물론 그 안에 추잡한 게 잔뜩 들어 있긴 하죠…."], ["Of course I can understand that it’s a philosophical novel and written to advocate an idea....”", "물론 그게 철학 소설이고 하나의 사상을 옹호하기 위해 쓰인 거라는 건 이해할 수 있고요….\""], ["Kolya was getting mixed by now.", "콜랴는 이제 횡설수설하기 시작했다."], ["“I am a Socialist, Karamazov, I am an incurable Socialist,”", "\"저는 사회주의자예요, 카라마조프. 저는 구제 불능의 사회주의자라고요.\""], ["he announced suddenly, apropos of nothing.", "그가 느닷없이, 아무 맥락도 없이 선언했다."]], [["“A Socialist?”", "\"사회주의자라고?\""], ["laughed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 웃었다."], ["“But when have you had time to become one?", "\"그런데 대체 언제 사회주의자가 될 틈이 있었지?"], ["Why, I thought you were only thirteen?”", "아니, 나는 네가 겨우 열세 살인 줄 알았는데?\""]], [["Kolya winced.", "콜랴가 움찔했다."]], [["“In the first place I am not thirteen, but fourteen, fourteen in a fortnight,”", "\"우선 저는 열세 살이 아니라 열네 살이에요. 보름만 지나면 열네 살이라고요.\""], ["he flushed angrily, “and in the second place I am at a complete loss to understand what my age has to do with it?", "그가 화가 나서 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"그리고 둘째로, 제 나이가 이 일과 무슨 상관인지 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["The question is what are my convictions, not what is my age, isn’t it?”", "문제는 제 신념이 무엇이냐 하는 거지, 제 나이가 몇이냐 하는 게 아니잖아요. 안 그래요?\""]], [["“When you are older, you’ll understand for yourself the influence of age on convictions.", "\"네가 좀 더 나이가 들면, 나이가 신념에 미치는 영향을 스스로 깨닫게 될 거야."], ["I fancied, too, that you were not expressing your own ideas,”", "그리고 나는 네가 네 자신의 생각을 말하고 있는 게 아니라는 인상도 받았고.\""], ["Alyosha answered serenely and modestly, but Kolya interrupted him hotly:", "알료샤가 차분하고 겸손하게 대답했으나, 콜랴는 발끈하여 그의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Come, you want obedience and mysticism.", "\"이봐요, 당신은 복종과 신비주의를 원하는 거예요."], ["You must admit that the Christian religion, for instance, has only been of use to the rich and the powerful to keep the lower classes in slavery.", "가령 그리스도교라는 종교가 오직 부자와 권력자들이 하층 계급을 노예 상태로 묶어 두는 데에만 쓸모가 있었다는 걸 인정해야 한다고요."], ["That’s so, isn’t it?”", "그렇잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Ah, I know where you read that, and I am sure some one told you so!”", "\"아, 네가 그걸 어디서 읽었는지 알겠다. 분명 누군가 너한테 그렇게 말해 줬을 거야!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 소리쳤다."]], [["“I say, what makes you think I read it?", "\"이봐요, 어째서 제가 그걸 읽었다고 생각하세요?"], ["And certainly no one told me so.", "그리고 분명히 아무도 저한테 그렇게 말해 주지 않았어요."], ["I can think for myself....", "저도 스스로 생각할 줄 안다고요…."], ["I am not opposed to Christ, if you like.", "정 그러시다면, 저는 그리스도를 반대하지 않아요."], ["He was a most humane person, and if He were alive to-day, He would be found in the ranks of the revolutionists, and would perhaps play a conspicuous part....", "그분은 더없이 인간적인 분이었고, 만약 그분이 오늘날 살아 계셨다면 혁명가들의 대열에 끼셨을 테고, 어쩌면 두드러진 역할을 하셨을지도 몰라요…."], ["There’s no doubt about that.”", "거기엔 의심의 여지가 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh, where, where did you get that from?", "\"오, 어디서, 대체 어디서 그런 걸 주워들었니?"], ["What fool have you made friends with?”", "어떤 멍청이하고 친구가 된 거야?\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“Come, the truth will out!", "\"이봐요, 진실은 드러나기 마련이죠!"], ["It has so chanced that I have often talked to Mr.", "어쩌다 보니 라키친 씨하고 자주 이야기를 나눈 건 사실이에요."], ["Rakitin, of course, but ...", "물론 그렇지만…"], ["old Byelinsky said that, too, so they say.”", "옛날에 벨린스키도 그렇게 말했다고들 하던데요.\""]], [["“Byelinsky?", "\"벨린스키가?"], ["I don’t remember.", "기억에 없는데."], ["He hasn’t written that anywhere.”", "그는 그런 걸 어디에도 쓴 적이 없어.\""]], [["“If he didn’t write it, they say he said it.", "\"쓰지는 않았어도 그렇게 말했다고들 해요."], ["I heard that from a ...", "저는 어떤 사람한테서 들었는데…"], ["but never mind.”", "뭐, 아무래도 상관없죠.\""]], [["“And have you read Byelinsky?”", "\"그럼 너는 벨린스키를 읽어 보긴 했니?\""]], [["“Well, no ...", "\"음, 아니요…"], ["I haven’t read all of him, but ...", "그를 다 읽지는 못했어요. 하지만…"], ["I read the passage about Tatyana, why she didn’t go off with Onyegin.”", "타티야나에 관한 대목은 읽었어요. 그녀가 어째서 오네긴과 떠나지 않았는가 하는 부분 말이에요.\""]], [["“Didn’t go off with Onyegin?", "\"오네긴과 떠나지 않았다고?"], ["Surely you don’t ...", "설마 너는…"], ["understand that already?”", "벌써 그런 것까지 이해한단 말이니?\""]], [["“Why, you seem to take me for little Smurov,”", "\"이봐요, 당신은 저를 어린 스무로프쯤으로 여기시는 모양이군요.\""], ["said Kolya, with a grin of irritation.", "콜랴가 짜증 섞인 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“But please don’t suppose I am such a revolutionist.", "\"하지만 제발 제가 그런 혁명가라고 생각하지는 말아 주세요."], ["I often disagree with Mr. Rakitin.", "저는 라키친 씨하고 의견이 안 맞을 때가 많거든요."], ["Though I mention Tatyana, I am not at all for the emancipation of women.", "제가 타티야나를 언급하긴 했지만, 저는 여성 해방 같은 건 결코 찬성하지 않아요."], ["I acknowledge that women are a subject race and must obey.", "저는 여자가 종속된 종족이고 복종해야 한다는 걸 인정해요."], ["Les femmes tricottent, as Napoleon said.”", "나폴레옹이 말했듯이, '여자들은 뜨개질이나 하라지(Les femmes tricottent)'예요.\""], ["Kolya, for some reason, smiled, “And on that question at least I am quite of one mind with that pseudo-great man.", "콜랴가 무슨 까닭에서인지 빙긋 웃었다. \"적어도 그 문제에서만큼은 저도 그 사이비 위인하고 완전히 한마음이에요."], ["I think, too, that to leave one’s own country and fly to America is mean, worse than mean—silly.", "또 저는 제 조국을 버리고 미국으로 달아나는 건 비열한 짓이라고 생각해요. 비열하다 못해 더 나쁜, 어리석은 짓이죠."], ["Why go to America when one may be of great service to humanity here?", "여기서도 인류를 위해 크게 봉사할 수 있는데 무엇하러 미국에 간단 말이에요?"], ["Now especially.", "특히 지금 같은 때에요."], ["There’s a perfect mass of fruitful activity open to us.", "우리에게는 결실 있는 활동거리가 산더미처럼 활짝 열려 있단 말이에요."], ["That’s what I answered.”", "저는 그렇게 대답했어요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 말이지?"], ["Answered whom?", "누구한테 대답했다는 거야?"], ["Has some one suggested your going to America already?”", "누군가 벌써 너한테 미국에 가라고 부추기기라도 했니?\""]], [["“I must own, they’ve been at me to go, but I declined.", "\"사실 저더러 가라고 들볶긴 했지만, 저는 거절했어요."], ["That’s between ourselves, of course, Karamazov; do you hear, not a word to any one.", "물론 이건 우리끼리 얘기예요, 카라마조프. 듣고 있죠, 누구한테도 한마디도 하면 안 돼요."], ["I say this only to you.", "이건 당신한테만 하는 말이에요."], ["I am not at all anxious to fall into the clutches of the secret police and take lessons at the Chain bridge.", "저는 비밀경찰의 손아귀에 떨어져서 '사슬 다리'에서 수업을 받을 마음은 전혀 없거든요."]], [["Long will you remember The house at the Chain bridge.", "오래도록 너는 기억하리라 사슬 다리 옆의 그 집을."]], [["Do you remember?", "기억나세요? 멋지죠."], ["It’s splendid.", "왜 웃으세요?"], ["Why are you laughing? You don’t suppose I am fibbing, do you?”", "설마 제가 허풍을 떤다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\""], ["(“What if he should find out that I’ve only that one number of The Bell in father’s bookcase, and haven’t read any more of it?”", "('만약 내가 아버지 책장에 《종(鐘)》 그 한 호밖에 없고 그 이상은 더 읽어 보지도 못했다는 걸 저 사람이 알아채기라도 하면 어쩌지?'"], ["Kolya thought with a shudder.)", "콜랴는 몸서리치며 생각했다.)"]], [["“Oh, no, I am not laughing and don’t suppose for a moment that you are lying.", "\"오, 아니야, 나는 웃는 게 아니고, 네가 거짓말을 한다고는 한순간도 생각하지 않아."], ["No, indeed, I can’t suppose so, for all this, alas!", "정말이지 그렇게 생각할 수가 없어. 슬프게도 이 모든 게 너무도 사실이거든."], ["is perfectly true. But tell me, have you read Pushkin—Onyegin, for instance?...", "그런데 말해 보렴, 너는 푸시킨을, 가령 《오네긴》을 읽어 봤니?…"], ["You spoke just now of Tatyana.”", "방금 타티야나 이야기를 했잖아.\""]], [["“No, I haven’t read it yet, but I want to read it.", "\"아니요, 아직 안 읽었지만 읽고 싶긴 해요."], ["I have no prejudices, Karamazov; I want to hear both sides.", "저는 편견이 없거든요, 카라마조프. 저는 양쪽 이야기를 다 들어 보고 싶어요."], ["What makes you ask?”", "왜 그런 걸 물으세요?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing.”", "\"아, 아무것도 아니야.\""]], [["“Tell me, Karamazov, have you an awful contempt for me?”", "\"말해 주세요, 카라마조프, 당신은 저를 끔찍이 경멸하시나요?\""], ["Kolya rapped out suddenly and drew himself up before Alyosha, as though he were on drill.", "콜랴가 느닷없이 쏘아붙이듯 묻고는, 마치 훈련이라도 받는 듯이 알료샤 앞에서 몸을 꼿꼿이 폈다."], ["“Be so kind as to tell me, without beating about the bush.”", "\"부디 에둘러 말씀하지 마시고 솔직히 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“I have a contempt for you?”", "\"내가 너를 경멸한다고?\""], ["Alyosha looked at him wondering. “What for?", "알료샤가 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["I am only sad that a charming nature such as yours should be perverted by all this crude nonsense before you have begun life.”", "\"무엇 때문에? 나는 다만 너처럼 매력적인 천성이 인생을 시작하기도 전에 이런 거친 헛소리들로 비뚤어져 버린 것이 슬플 뿐이야.\""]], [["“Don’t be anxious about my nature,”", "\"제 천성에 대해선 염려하지 마세요.\""], ["Kolya interrupted, not without complacency.", "콜랴가 자못 흡족한 기색을 감추지 않으며 말을 가로챘다."], ["“But it’s true that I am stupidly sensitive, crudely sensitive.", "\"하지만 제가 어리석게 민감하다는 건, 거칠게 민감하다는 건 사실이에요."], ["You smiled just now, and I fancied you seemed to—”", "방금 당신이 미소를 지으셨을 때, 저는 당신이 마치—\""]], [["“Oh, my smile meant something quite different.", "\"오, 내 미소는 전혀 다른 뜻이었어. 왜 웃었는지 말해 줄게."], ["I’ll tell you why I smiled.", "얼마 전에 나는 러시아에서 살았던 어느 독일 사람이 오늘날 우리 대학생과 학생들에 대해 쓴 비평을 읽었어. '러시아 학생에게,'"], ["Not long ago I read the criticism made by a German who had lived in Russia, on our students and schoolboys of to-day.", "그는 이렇게 썼어. '아무것도 모르는 별자리 지도를 보여 줘 보라."], ["‘Show a Russian schoolboy,’ he writes, ‘a map of the stars, which he knows nothing about, and he will give you back the map next day with corrections on it.’ No knowledge and unbounded conceit—that’s what the German meant to say about the Russian schoolboy.”", "그러면 다음 날 그 지도에 수정까지 가해서 돌려줄 것이다.' 아무 지식도 없으면서 끝없는 자만심뿐이라는 것—그 독일 사람이 러시아 학생에 대해 하려던 말이 바로 그거였어.\""]], [["“Yes, that’s perfectly right,”", "\"네, 그건 완전히 맞는 말이에요.\" 콜랴가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Kolya laughed suddenly, “exactly so!", "\"정말 그래요! 그 독일 사람, 브라보!"], ["Bravo the German!", "하지만 그는 좋은 면은 못 본 거예요."], ["But he did not see the good side, what do you think?", "어떻게 생각하세요? 자만심이야 있을 수 있죠."], ["Conceit may be, that comes from youth, that will be corrected if need be, but, on the other hand, there is an independent spirit almost from childhood, boldness of thought and conviction, and not the spirit of these sausage makers, groveling before authority....", "그건 젊음에서 나오는 거고, 필요하다면 바로잡힐 테니까요. 하지만 다른 한편으로는, 거의 어린 시절부터 독립적인 정신이 있고, 사상과 신념의 대담함이 있단 말이에요."], ["But the German was right all the same. Bravo the German!", "권위 앞에 굽실거리는 그 소시지 장수들 같은 정신이 아니라요…."], ["But Germans want strangling all the same.", "그래도 그 독일 사람 말은 옳긴 해요. 그 독일 사람, 브라보!"], ["Though they are so good at science and learning they must be strangled.”", "하지만 독일인들은 어쨌든 목 졸라 죽여야 해요. 학문과 학식에는 그렇게 뛰어나다 해도 목 졸라 죽여야 한다고요.\""]], [["“Strangled, what for?”", "\"목 졸라 죽이다니, 무엇 때문에?\""], ["smiled Alyosha.", "알료샤가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Well, perhaps I am talking nonsense, I agree.", "\"음, 어쩌면 제가 허튼소리를 하는 건지도 모르겠어요. 인정해요."], ["I am awfully childish sometimes, and when I am pleased about anything I can’t restrain myself and am ready to talk any stuff.", "저는 가끔 끔찍이 유치해지거든요. 무언가가 기쁘면 저는 자제를 못 하고 무슨 헛소리든 떠벌릴 채비가 돼 있어요."], ["But, I say, we are chattering away here about nothing, and that doctor has been a long time in there.", "그런데 이봐요, 우리가 여기서 쓸데없는 잡담이나 늘어놓고 있는 사이에 저 의사가 안에 들어간 지 너무 오래됐어요."], ["But perhaps he’s examining the mamma and that poor crippled Nina.", "하지만 어쩌면 어머니랑 그 가엾은 불구의 니나를 진찰하고 있는지도 모르겠네요. 저는 그 니나가 마음에 들었어요, 아세요."], ["I liked that Nina, you know.", "제가 나오려는데 그 애가 갑자기 저한테 속삭이더라고요."], ["She whispered to me suddenly as I was coming away, ‘Why didn’t you come before?’ And in such a voice, so reproachfully!", "'왜 진작 안 오셨어요?' 게다가 그런 목소리로, 어찌나 원망하는 투였는지요!"], ["I think she is awfully nice and pathetic.”", "저는 그 애가 끔찍이 착하고 가엾다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes!", "\"그래, 그래!"], ["Well, you’ll be coming often, you will see what she is like.", "그럼 너는 자주 오게 될 테고, 그 애가 어떤 애인지 알게 될 거야."], ["It would do you a great deal of good to know people like that, to learn to value a great deal which you will find out from knowing these people,”", "그런 사람들을 알게 되는 건 너한테 큰 도움이 될 거야. 그 사람들을 알게 됨으로써 비로소 깨닫게 될 많은 것의 가치를 배우게 될 테니까.\""], ["Alyosha observed warmly.", "알료샤가 따뜻하게 말했다."], ["“That would have more effect on you than anything.”", "\"그게 그 무엇보다도 너한테 큰 영향을 줄 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, how I regret and blame myself for not having come sooner!”", "\"오, 진작 오지 못한 것이 어찌나 후회스럽고 제 자신이 원망스러운지요!\""], ["Kolya exclaimed, with bitter feeling.", "콜랴가 쓰라린 심정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, it’s a great pity.", "\"그래, 정말 안타까운 일이야."], ["You saw for yourself how delighted the poor child was to see you.", "너도 직접 봤잖아, 그 가엾은 아이가 너를 보고 얼마나 기뻐했는지."], ["And how he fretted for you to come!”", "그리고 네가 오기를 얼마나 애타게 기다렸는지!\""]], [["“Don’t tell me!", "\"그런 말씀 마세요!"], ["You make it worse!", "더 괴로워지잖아요!"], ["But it serves me right.", "하지만 당해도 싸죠."], ["What kept me from coming was my conceit, my egoistic vanity, and the beastly wilfullness, which I never can get rid of, though I’ve been struggling with it all my life.", "제가 오지 못하게 막은 건 제 자만심이었어요. 제 이기적인 허영심, 그리고 그 빌어먹을 고집—평생 싸워 왔는데도 도무지 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없는 그 고집 말이에요."], ["I see that now.", "이제야 그게 보여요."], ["I am a beast in lots of ways, Karamazov!”", "저는 여러모로 짐승 같은 놈이에요, 카라마조프!\""]], [["“No, you have a charming nature, though it’s been distorted, and I quite understand why you have had such an influence on this generous, morbidly sensitive boy,”", "\"아니야, 너는 매력적인 천성을 지녔어. 비록 비뚤어지긴 했지만 말이야."], ["Alyosha answered warmly.", "그리고 네가 어째서 이 너그럽고 병적으로 민감한 아이한테 그토록 큰 영향을 끼쳤는지 충분히 이해가 돼.\" 알료샤가 따뜻하게 대답했다."]], [["“And you say that to me!”", "\"그런 말씀을 저한테 하시다니요!\""], ["cried Kolya; “and would you believe it, I thought—I’ve thought several times since I’ve been here—that you despised me!", "콜랴가 외쳤다. \"믿어지세요, 저는 생각했었어요—여기 온 뒤로도 몇 번이나 그렇게 생각했어요—당신이 저를 경멸한다고요!"], ["If only you knew how I prize your opinion!”", "제가 당신의 의견을 얼마나 소중히 여기는지 아신다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\""]], [["“But are you really so sensitive?", "\"그런데 너는 정말로 그렇게 민감하니?"], ["At your age!", "그 나이에!"], ["Would you believe it, just now, when you were telling your story, I thought, as I watched you, that you must be very sensitive!”", "믿어지니, 방금 네가 네 이야기를 들려줄 때, 나는 너를 지켜보면서 네가 무척 민감한 아이임이 틀림없다고 생각했어!\""]], [["“You thought so?", "\"그렇게 생각하셨어요?"], ["What an eye you’ve got, I say!", "정말 눈썰미가 대단하시네요!"], ["I bet that was when I was talking about the goose.", "분명 제가 그 거위 이야기를 할 때였을 거예요."], ["That was just when I was fancying you had a great contempt for me for being in such a hurry to show off, and for a moment I quite hated you for it, and began talking like a fool.", "바로 그때 저는 당신이 제가 그렇게 서둘러 잘난 척하려 든다고 저를 잔뜩 경멸한다고 여기고 있었거든요. 그래서 한순간 그것 때문에 당신이 정말 미워졌고, 바보처럼 떠들어 대기 시작했던 거예요."], ["Then I fancied—just now, here—when I said that if there were no God He would have to be invented, that I was in too great a hurry to display my knowledge, especially as I got that phrase out of a book.", "그러고 나서 또—방금, 여기서—제가 신이 없다면 신을 만들어 내야 했을 거라고 말했을 때, 제가 제 지식을 뽐내려고 너무 서두르고 있다고 여겼어요. 특히 그 구절을 책에서 따온 거였으니까요."], ["But I swear I wasn’t showing off out of vanity, though I really don’t know why.", "하지만 맹세코 저는 허영심 때문에 잘난 척한 게 아니에요. 정말이지 왜 그랬는지 모르겠지만요."], ["Because I was so pleased?", "너무 기뻐서였을까요?"], ["Yes, I believe it was because I was so pleased ...", "네, 너무 기뻐서 그랬던 것 같아요…."], ["though it’s perfectly disgraceful for any one to be gushing directly they are pleased, I know that.", "기쁘다고 곧장 호들갑을 떠는 건 누구한테나 더없이 창피한 짓이라는 걸 알면서도요. 하지만 이제 저는 당신이 저를 경멸하지 않는다고 확신해요."], ["But I am convinced now that you don’t despise me; it was all my imagination.", "그건 다 제 망상이었던 거예요. 오, 카라마조프, 저는 뼈저리게 불행해요."], ["Oh, Karamazov, I am profoundly unhappy.", "저는 가끔 별별 상상을 다 한단 말이에요."], ["I sometimes fancy all sorts of things, that every one is laughing at me, the whole world, and then I feel ready to overturn the whole order of things.”", "모두가, 온 세상이 저를 비웃고 있다고요. 그러면 저는 세상의 질서 전체를 뒤엎어 버리고 싶은 마음이 들어요.\""]], [["“And you worry every one about you,”", "\"그러면서 너는 네 주위의 모든 사람을 들볶는구나.\""], ["smiled Alyosha.", "알료샤가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Yes, I worry every one about me, especially my mother.", "\"네, 저는 제 주위의 모두를, 특히 어머니를 들볶아요."], ["Karamazov, tell me, am I very ridiculous now?”", "카라마조프, 말해 주세요, 제가 지금 아주 우스꽝스러운가요?\""]], [["“Don’t think about that, don’t think of it at all!”", "\"그런 건 생각하지 마, 아예 생각도 하지 마!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."], ["“And what does ridiculous mean?", "\"그리고 우스꽝스럽다는 게 대체 뭐지?"], ["Isn’t every one constantly being or seeming ridiculous?", "사람은 누구나 끊임없이 우스꽝스럽거나 우스꽝스러워 보이지 않니?"], ["Besides, nearly all clever people now are fearfully afraid of being ridiculous, and that makes them unhappy.", "게다가 요즘 영리한 사람들은 거의 다 우스꽝스러워 보일까 봐 끔찍이 두려워하고, 그 때문에 불행해진단 말이야."], ["All I am surprised at is that you should be feeling that so early, though I’ve observed it for some time past, and not only in you.", "다만 내가 놀라는 건, 네가 그걸 이렇게 일찍부터 느끼고 있다는 거야. 사실 나는 얼마 전부터 그걸 눈여겨봐 왔어."], ["Nowadays the very children have begun to suffer from it.", "너뿐만이 아니라 말이야. 요즘은 어린아이들까지 그것 때문에 괴로워하기 시작했거든."], ["It’s almost a sort of insanity.", "그건 거의 일종의 광기야."], ["The devil has taken the form of that vanity and entered into the whole generation; it’s simply the devil,”", "악마가 그 허영심의 모습을 띠고 온 세대 안으로 들어와 버린 거지. 그건 그야말로 악마야.\""], ["added Alyosha, without a trace of the smile that Kolya, staring at him, expected to see.", "알료샤가 덧붙였는데, 콜랴가 빤히 바라보며 기대했던 그 미소는 조금도 띠고 있지 않았다."], ["“You are like every one else,”", "\"너도 다른 모든 사람과 똑같아.\""], ["said Alyosha, in conclusion, “that is, like very many others.", "알료샤가 마무리 지어 말했다. \"그러니까, 아주 많은 다른 사람들과 똑같다는 거야."], ["Only you must not be like everybody else, that’s all.”", "다만 너는 다른 모든 사람과 똑같아져서는 안 돼. 그게 다야.\""]], [["“Even if every one is like that?”", "\"모두가 다 그렇다 해도요?\""]], [["“Yes, even if every one is like that.", "\"그래, 모두가 다 그렇다 해도."], ["You be the only one not like it.", "너만은 그렇지 않은 유일한 사람이 되어야 해."], ["You really are not like every one else, here you are not ashamed to confess to something bad and even ridiculous.", "너는 정말로 다른 모든 사람과는 달라. 봐, 너는 무언가 나쁜 것, 심지어 우스꽝스러운 것까지 고백하기를 부끄러워하지 않잖아."], ["And who will admit so much in these days?", "요즘 누가 그만큼 인정하겠어? 아무도 안 해."], ["No one. And people have even ceased to feel the impulse to self-criticism.", "사람들은 자기 자신을 비판하려는 충동조차 느끼지 않게 돼 버렸어."], ["Don’t be like every one else, even if you are the only one.”", "다른 모든 사람과 똑같아지지 마. 설령 네가 유일한 사람이 된다 해도 말이야.\""]], [["“Splendid!", "\"훌륭해요!"], ["I was not mistaken in you.", "제가 당신을 잘못 본 게 아니었어요."], ["You know how to console one.", "당신은 사람을 위로할 줄 아시는군요."], ["Oh, how I have longed to know you, Karamazov!", "오, 제가 당신을 얼마나 알고 싶어 했는지요, 카라마조프!"], ["I’ve long been eager for this meeting.", "저는 오래전부터 이 만남을 간절히 바라 왔어요."], ["Can you really have thought about me, too?", "정말로 당신도 저에 대해 생각하셨던 거예요?"], ["You said just now that you thought of me, too?”", "방금 당신도 저를 생각했다고 하셨잖아요?\""]], [["“Yes, I’d heard of you and had thought of you, too ...", "\"그래, 나는 네 이야기를 들었고 너에 대해 생각도 했어…."], ["and if it’s partly vanity that makes you ask, it doesn’t matter.”", "그리고 설령 그걸 묻는 게 어느 정도는 허영심에서 나온 거라 해도, 상관없어.\""]], [["“Do you know, Karamazov, our talk has been like a declaration of love,”", "\"있잖아요, 카라마조프, 우리 대화는 마치 사랑의 고백 같았어요.\""], ["said Kolya, in a bashful and melting voice.", "콜랴가 수줍고 녹아드는 듯한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“That’s not ridiculous, is it?”", "\"이건 우스꽝스럽지 않죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Not at all ridiculous, and if it were, it wouldn’t matter, because it’s been a good thing.”", "\"전혀 우스꽝스럽지 않아. 설령 그렇다 해도 상관없어."], ["Alyosha smiled brightly.", "좋은 일이었으니까.\" 알료샤가 환하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“But do you know, Karamazov, you must admit that you are a little ashamed yourself, now....", "\"그런데 있잖아요, 카라마조프, 당신도 지금 조금은 부끄러워하고 있다는 걸 인정하셔야 해요…."], ["I see it by your eyes.”", "당신 눈을 보면 알 수 있거든요.\""], ["Kolya smiled with a sort of sly happiness.", "콜랴가 짓궂은 행복감 같은 것을 띠며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Why ashamed?”", "\"왜 부끄러워한다는 거지?\""]], [["“Well, why are you blushing?”", "\"음, 그럼 왜 얼굴을 붉히시는데요?\""]], [["“It was you made me blush,”", "\"네가 나를 붉히게 만든 거잖아.\""], ["laughed Alyosha, and he really did blush.", "알료샤가 웃었는데, 그는 정말로 얼굴을 붉히고 있었다."], ["“Oh, well, I am a little, goodness knows why, I don’t know...”", "\"오, 그래, 조금 그렇긴 한데, 왜 그런지는 정말이지 나도 모르겠다….\""], ["he muttered, almost embarrassed.", "그가 거의 당황한 듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, how I love you and admire you at this moment just because you are rather ashamed!", "\"오, 바로 당신이 조금 부끄러워하고 있다는 그 이유 때문에 지금 이 순간 제가 당신을 얼마나 사랑하고 우러러보는지요!"], ["Because you are just like me,”", "당신이 바로 저와 똑같으니까요!\""], ["cried Kolya, in positive ecstasy.", "콜랴가 그야말로 황홀경에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["His cheeks glowed, his eyes beamed.", "그의 두 뺨이 달아오르고 두 눈이 빛났다."]], [["“You know, Kolya, you will be very unhappy in your life,”", "\"있잖아, 콜랴, 너는 살아가는 동안 무척 불행할 거야.\""], ["something made Alyosha say suddenly.", "무언가가 알료샤로 하여금 불쑥 그렇게 말하게 했다."]], [["“I know, I know.", "\"알아요, 알아요."], ["How you know it all beforehand!”", "어쩌면 그렇게 모든 걸 미리 다 아세요!\""], ["Kolya agreed at once.", "콜랴가 곧바로 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“But you will bless life on the whole, all the same.”", "\"하지만 그래도 너는 인생 전체를 두고 보면 삶을 축복하게 될 거야.\""]], [["“Just so, hurrah!", "\"바로 그거예요, 만세!"], ["You are a prophet.", "당신은 예언자예요."], ["Oh, we shall get on together, Karamazov!", "오, 우리는 잘 지낼 거예요, 카라마조프!"], ["Do you know, what delights me most, is that you treat me quite like an equal.", "있잖아요, 무엇보다 저를 기쁘게 하는 건, 당신이 저를 완전히 대등한 사람처럼 대해 주신다는 거예요."], ["But we are not equals, no, we are not, you are better!", "하지만 우리는 대등하지 않아요, 아니에요, 우리는 대등하지 않아요, 당신이 더 나은 사람이에요!"], ["But we shall get on.", "그래도 우리는 잘 지낼 거예요."], ["Do you know, all this last month, I’ve been saying to myself, ‘Either we shall be friends at once, for ever, or we shall part enemies to the grave!’”", "있잖아요, 지난 한 달 내내 저는 제 자신한테 이렇게 말해 왔어요. '우리는 단번에 영원한 친구가 되거나, 아니면 무덤까지 가는 원수로 갈라서거나, 둘 중 하나다!'라고요.\""]], [["“And saying that, of course, you loved me,”", "\"그리고 그렇게 말했다는 건, 물론 너는 나를 사랑한 거지.\""], ["Alyosha laughed gayly.", "알료샤가 유쾌하게 웃었다."]], [["“I did.", "\"그랬어요."], ["I loved you awfully.", "저는 당신을 끔찍이 사랑했어요."], ["I’ve been loving and dreaming of you.", "저는 줄곧 당신을 사랑하고 당신을 꿈꿔 왔어요."], ["And how do you know it all beforehand?", "그런데 어떻게 그 모든 걸 미리 다 아세요?"], ["Ah, here’s the doctor.", "아, 의사가 나오네요."], ["Goodness!", "맙소사!"], ["What will he tell us?", "그가 우리한테 뭐라고 할까요?"], ["Look at his face!”", "저 얼굴 좀 보세요!\""]], [["# Chapter VII. Ilusha", "# Chapter VII. Ilusha"]], [["The doctor came out of the room again, muffled in his fur coat and with his cap on his head.", "의사가 모피 외투를 단단히 여미고 머리에 모자를 쓴 채 다시 방에서 나왔다."], ["His face looked almost angry and disgusted, as though he were afraid of getting dirty.", "그의 얼굴은 무언가에 더럽혀질까 두려워하는 사람처럼 거의 성이 나고 역겨워하는 표정이었다."], ["He cast a cursory glance round the passage, looking sternly at Alyosha and Kolya as he did so.", "그는 복도를 휙 둘러보았는데, 그러면서 알료샤와 콜랴를 매섭게 쏘아보았다."], ["Alyosha waved from the door to the coachman, and the carriage that had brought the doctor drove up.", "알료샤는 문가에서 마부에게 손짓을 했고, 의사를 태우고 왔던 마차가 다가왔다."], ["The captain darted out after the doctor, and, bowing apologetically, stopped him to get the last word.", "대위가 의사를 뒤쫓아 뛰어나가, 사죄하듯 허리를 굽히며 그를 멈춰 세우고는 마지막 한마디를 들으려 했다."], ["The poor fellow looked utterly crushed; there was a scared look in his eyes.", "가엾은 사내는 완전히 으스러진 듯 보였고, 그 눈에는 겁에 질린 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Your Excellency, your Excellency ...", "\"각하, 각하……"], ["is it possible?”", "그럴 수가 있습니까?\""], ["he began, but could not go on and clasped his hands in despair.", "그는 입을 떼었으나 더는 말을 잇지 못하고 절망에 차 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["Yet he still gazed imploringly at the doctor, as though a word from him might still change the poor boy’s fate.", "그러면서도 그는 여전히 애원하듯 의사를 바라보고 있었으니, 마치 그의 말 한마디가 아직도 이 가엾은 아이의 운명을 바꿔 줄 수 있기라도 하다는 듯이."]], [["“I can’t help it, I am not God!”", "\"나로서는 어쩔 수가 없소, 나는 신이 아니란 말이오!\""], ["the doctor answered offhand, though with the customary impressiveness.", "의사는 무심하게 대꾸했으나, 그래도 늘 그렇듯 위엄을 곁들였다."]], [["“Doctor ...", "\"선생님……"], ["your Excellency ...", "각하……"], ["and will it be soon, soon?”", "그럼 곧, 곧 그렇게 됩니까?\""]], [["“You must be prepared for anything,”", "\"무슨 일이든 각오를 해 두셔야 할 거요.\""], ["said the doctor in emphatic and incisive tones, and dropping his eyes, he was about to step out to the coach.", "의사는 단호하고 날카로운 어조로 말하더니, 눈을 내리깔고는 마차로 발을 내디디려 했다."]], [["“Your Excellency, for Christ’s sake!”", "\"각하, 제발 부탁입니다!\""], ["the terror-stricken captain stopped him again.", "공포에 사로잡힌 대위가 다시 그를 가로막았다. \"각하!"], ["“Your Excellency! but can nothing, absolutely nothing save him now?”", "정말 이제는 아무것도, 정말 아무것도 저 애를 살릴 수 없는 겁니까?\""]], [["“It’s not in my hands now,”", "\"이제 내 손을 떠난 일이오.\""], ["said the doctor impatiently, “but h’m!...”", "의사가 짜증스레 말했다."], ["he stopped suddenly.", "\"하지만 흠!……\""], ["“If you could, for instance ... send ...", "그는 갑자기 말을 멈췄다."], ["your patient ...", "\"만약 가령……"], ["at once, without delay”", "환자를……"], ["(the words “at once, without delay,” the doctor uttered with an almost wrathful sternness that made the captain start) “to Syracuse, the change to the new be-ne-ficial climatic conditions might possibly effect—”", "지금 당장, 한시도 지체 없이\"(이 \"지금 당장, 한시도 지체 없이\"라는 말을 의사는 거의 노여움이 어린 엄격함으로 내뱉어, 대위가 흠칫 놀랐다)\"시라쿠사로 보낼 수 있다면, 새로운 유-익한 기후 조건으로의 변화가 어쩌면 효과를……\""]], [["“To Syracuse!”", "\"시라쿠사로요!\""], ["cried the captain, unable to grasp what was said.", "대위는 들은 말을 미처 알아듣지 못하고 소리쳤다."]], [["“Syracuse is in Sicily,”", "\"시라쿠사는 시칠리아에 있어요.\""], ["Kolya jerked out suddenly in explanation.", "콜랴가 불쑥 끼어들어 설명했다."], ["The doctor looked at him.", "의사가 그를 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Sicily!", "\"시칠리아요!"], ["your Excellency,”", "각하,\""], ["faltered the captain, “but you’ve seen”—he spread out his hands, indicating his surroundings—“mamma and my family?”", "대위는 더듬거렸다. \"하지만 보셨다시피\"—그는 두 손을 펼쳐 주변을 가리켰다—\"안사람이며 제 식구들을……?\""]], [["“N—no, Sicily is not the place for the family, the family should go to Caucasus in the early spring ...", "\"아—니, 시칠리아는 식구들이 갈 곳이 아니오. 가족은 이른 봄에 캅카스로 가야 하오……"], ["your daughter must go to the Caucasus, and your wife ...", "따님은 캅카스로 가야 하고, 부인은……"], ["after a course of the waters in the Caucasus for her rheumatism ...", "류머티즘 때문에 캅카스에서 온천 요법을 받은 다음……"], ["must be sent straight to Paris to the mental specialist Lepelletier; I could give you a note to him, and then ...", "곧장 파리의 정신과 전문의 르펠르티에에게 보내야 하오. 내가 그에게 보내는 소개장을 써 드릴 수도 있소."], ["there might be a change—”", "그렇게 하면…… 차도가 있을지도 모르오……\""]], [["“Doctor, doctor! But you see!”", "\"선생님, 선생님!"], ["The captain flung wide his hands again despairingly, indicating the bare wooden walls of the passage.", "하지만 보십시오!\" 대위는 다시 절망에 차 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 복도의 휑한 나무 벽을 가리켰다."]], [["“Well, that’s not my business,”", "\"흠, 그건 내 일이 아니지요.\""], ["grinned the doctor.", "의사는 히죽 웃었다."], ["“I have only told you the answer of medical science to your question as to possible treatment.", "\"나는 다만 가능한 치료에 관한 당신의 물음에 의학이 내놓는 답을 말해 주었을 뿐이오."], ["As for the rest, to my regret—”", "그 나머지로 말하면, 유감스럽게도……\""]], [["“Don’t be afraid, apothecary, my dog won’t bite you,”", "\"걱정 마쇼, 약장수 양반, 우리 개는 안 물어요.\""], ["Kolya rapped out loudly, noticing the doctor’s rather uneasy glance at Perezvon, who was standing in the doorway.", "콜랴가 큰 소리로 쏘아붙였으니, 문간에 서 있는 페레즈본을 향해 의사가 다소 불안한 눈길을 던지는 것을 알아챘던 것이다."], ["There was a wrathful note in Kolya’s voice.", "콜랴의 목소리에는 노여움이 서려 있었다."], ["He used the word apothecary instead of doctor on purpose, and, as he explained afterwards, used it “to insult him.”", "그는 의사라는 말 대신 일부러 약장수라는 말을 썼고, 나중에 스스로 밝힌 대로 \"그를 모욕하려고\" 그 말을 쓴 것이었다."]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"뭐야, 이게?\""], ["The doctor flung up his head, staring with surprise at Kolya.", "의사는 고개를 번쩍 쳐들고 놀란 눈으로 콜랴를 노려보았다."], ["“Who’s this?”", "\"이건 또 누군가?\""], ["he addressed Alyosha, as though asking him to explain.", "그는 설명을 구하기라도 하듯 알료샤를 향해 물었다."]], [["“It’s Perezvon’s master, don’t worry about me,”", "\"이건 페레즈본의 주인이올시다, 내 걱정은 마쇼.\""], ["Kolya said incisively again.", "콜랴가 다시 날카롭게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Perezvon?”[7] repeated the doctor, perplexed.", "\"페레즈본?\" 의사가 어리둥절해하며 되뇌었다."]], [["“He hears the bell, but where it is he cannot tell.", "\"종소리는 들려도 어디서 나는지는 모른다 이거지."], ["Good-by, we shall meet in Syracuse.”", "잘 가쇼, 시라쿠사에서 만납시다.\""]], [["“Who’s this?", "\"이건 누구야?"], ["Who’s this?”", "누구냐고?\""], ["The doctor flew into a terrible rage.", "의사는 무서운 분노에 휩싸였다."]], [["“He is a schoolboy, doctor, he is a mischievous boy; take no notice of him,”", "\"그저 학생입니다, 선생님, 장난기 많은 애예요, 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""], ["said Alyosha, frowning and speaking quickly.", "알료샤가 눈살을 찌푸리며 빠르게 말했다."], ["“Kolya, hold your tongue!”", "\"콜랴, 입 다물어!\""], ["he cried to Krassotkin.", "그는 크라소트킨에게 소리쳤다."], ["“Take no notice of him, doctor,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마십시오, 선생님.\""], ["he repeated, rather impatiently.", "그는 다소 조바심을 내며 거듭 말했다."]], [["“He wants a thrashing, a good thrashing!”", "\"저놈은 매를 맞아야 해, 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 한다고!\""], ["The doctor stamped in a perfect fury.", "의사가 그야말로 길길이 날뛰며 발을 굴렀다."]], [["“And you know, apothecary, my Perezvon might bite!”", "\"그런데 말이오, 약장수 양반, 우리 페레즈본이 물지도 몰라!\""], ["said Kolya, turning pale, with quivering voice and flashing eyes.", "콜랴는 얼굴이 새파래진 채 목소리를 떨고 눈을 번득이며 말했다."], ["“Ici, Perezvon!”", "\"이리 와, 페레즈본!\""]], [["“Kolya, if you say another word, I’ll have nothing more to do with you,”", "\"콜랴, 한마디만 더 하면 나는 너와 두 번 다시 상종하지 않겠다.\""], ["Alyosha cried peremptorily.", "알료샤가 단호하게 소리쳤다."]], [["“There is only one man in the world who can command Nikolay Krassotkin—this is the man”; Kolya pointed to Alyosha.", "\"이 세상에 니콜라이 크라소트킨에게 명령할 수 있는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이야—바로 이 사람이지.\" 콜랴가 알료샤를 가리켰다."], ["“I obey him, good-by!”", "\"나는 이 사람한테 복종한다. 잘 가쇼!\""]], [["He stepped forward, opened the door, and quickly went into the inner room.", "그는 앞으로 나서서 문을 열고 안쪽 방으로 잰걸음으로 들어갔다."], ["Perezvon flew after him.", "페레즈본이 그 뒤를 쫓아 달려갔다."], ["The doctor stood still for five seconds in amazement, looking at Alyosha; then, with a curse, he went out quickly to the carriage, repeating aloud, “This is ...", "의사는 어이가 없어 오 초쯤 꼼짝 않고 서서 알료샤를 바라보다가, 욕설을 내뱉으며 황급히 마차로 나가서는 큰 소리로 거듭 중얼거렸다. \"이건……"], ["this is ...", "이건……"], ["I don’t know what it is!”", "대체 이게 뭔지 모르겠군!\""], ["The captain darted forward to help him into the carriage.", "대위가 앞으로 달려 나가 그가 마차에 오르는 것을 거들었다."], ["Alyosha followed Kolya into the room.", "알료샤는 콜랴를 따라 방으로 들어갔다."], ["He was already by Ilusha’s bedside.", "콜랴는 이미 일류샤의 머리맡에 가 있었다."], ["The sick boy was holding his hand and calling for his father.", "병든 아이는 그의 손을 잡고 아버지를 부르고 있었다."], ["A minute later the captain, too, came back.", "잠시 후 대위도 돌아왔다."]], [["“Father, father, come ... we ...”", "\"아버지, 아버지, 이리 와요……"], ["Ilusha faltered in violent excitement, but apparently unable to go on, he flung his wasted arms round his father and Kolya, uniting them in one embrace, and hugging them as tightly as he could.", "우리……\" 일류샤는 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 더듬거렸으나, 더는 말을 잇지 못하고 야윈 팔로 아버지와 콜랴를 한꺼번에 끌어안아 하나로 모으고는, 있는 힘껏 그들을 꼭 껴안았다."], ["The captain suddenly began to shake with dumb sobs, and Kolya’s lips and chin twitched.", "대위가 갑자기 소리 없는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨기 시작했고, 콜랴의 입술과 턱도 실룩거렸다."]], [["“Father, father!", "\"아버지, 아버지!"], ["How sorry I am for you!”", "저 때문에 얼마나 마음이 아프세요!\""], ["Ilusha moaned bitterly.", "일류샤가 비통하게 신음했다."]], [["“Ilusha ...", "\"일류샤……"], ["darling ...", "우리 아가……"], ["the doctor said ...", "의사 선생이 말했어……"], ["you would be all right ...", "너는 괜찮을 거라고……"], ["we shall be happy ...", "우리는 행복해질 거야……"], ["the doctor ...”", "의사 선생이……\""], ["the captain began.", "대위가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Ah, father!", "\"아, 아버지!"], ["I know what the new doctor said to you about me.... I saw!”", "새로 온 의사가 저에 대해서 아버지한테 뭐라고 했는지 다 알아요……"], ["cried Ilusha, and again he hugged them both with all his strength, hiding his face on his father’s shoulder.", "봤단 말이에요!\" 일류샤가 소리치며, 다시 온 힘을 다해 두 사람을 끌어안고는 아버지의 어깨에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“Father, don’t cry, and when I die get a good boy, another one ...", "\"아버지, 울지 마세요. 그리고 제가 죽으면 좋은 아이를, 다른 아이를 하나 데려오세요……"], ["choose one of them all, a good one, call him Ilusha and love him instead of me....”", "아이들 중에서 좋은 아이를 골라서, 일류샤라고 부르고 제 대신 그 애를 사랑해 주세요……\""]], [["“Hush, old man, you’ll get well,”", "\"쉿, 이 친구야, 너는 나을 거야.\""], ["Krassotkin cried suddenly, in a voice that sounded angry.", "크라소트킨이 갑자기 성난 듯한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“But don’t ever forget me, father,”", "\"하지만 절 절대 잊지는 마세요, 아버지.\""], ["Ilusha went on, “come to my grave ...", "일류샤가 말을 이었다. \"제 무덤에 와 주세요……"], ["and, father, bury me by our big stone, where we used to go for our walk, and come to me there with Krassotkin in the evening ...", "그리고 아버지, 우리가 산책하러 다니던 그 큰 바위 옆에 저를 묻어 주세요. 저녁이면 크라소트킨이랑 같이 그리로 저를 찾아와 주세요……"], ["and Perezvon ...", "페레즈본도요……"], ["I shall expect you....", "저는 아버지를 기다릴 거예요……"], ["Father, father!”", "아버지, 아버지!\""]], [["His voice broke.", "그의 목소리가 끊겼다."], ["They were all three silent, still embracing.", "세 사람은 여전히 서로를 껴안은 채 모두 말이 없었다."], ["Nina was crying quietly in her chair, and at last seeing them all crying, “mamma,” too, burst into tears.", "니나는 의자에 앉아 조용히 울고 있었는데, 마침내 모두가 우는 것을 보고는 \"엄마\"마저 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Ilusha!", "\"일류샤!"], ["Ilusha!”", "일류샤!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["Krassotkin suddenly released himself from Ilusha’s embrace.", "크라소트킨이 갑자기 일류샤의 품에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["“Good-by, old man, mother expects me back to dinner,”", "\"잘 있어, 이 친구야, 어머니가 저녁때 돌아오라고 하셨거든.\""], ["he said quickly.", "그가 황급히 말했다."], ["“What a pity I did not tell her!", "\"어머니한테 말씀을 못 드리고 나온 게 정말 후회되는걸!"], ["She will be dreadfully anxious....", "무척 걱정하실 거야……"], ["But after dinner I’ll come back to you for the whole day, for the whole evening, and I’ll tell you all sorts of things, all sorts of things.", "하지만 저녁을 먹고 나면 다시 너한테 와서 하루 종일, 저녁 내내 같이 있을게. 그리고 온갖 이야기를, 별의별 이야기를 다 들려줄게."], ["And I’ll bring Perezvon, but now I will take him with me, because he will begin to howl when I am away and bother you.", "페레즈본도 데려올게, 지금은 데리고 갈 거야. 내가 없으면 저놈이 짖어 대기 시작해서 너를 성가시게 할 테니까."], ["Good-by!”", "잘 있어!\""]], [["And he ran out into the passage.", "그러고는 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["He didn’t want to cry, but in the passage he burst into tears.", "울고 싶지 않았으나, 복도에 나서자 그는 그만 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Alyosha found him crying.", "알료샤가 우는 그를 발견했다."]], [["“Kolya, you must be sure to keep your word and come, or he will be terribly disappointed,”", "\"콜랴, 꼭 약속을 지키고 와야 해, 안 그러면 저 애가 몹시 실망할 거야.\""], ["Alyosha said emphatically.", "알료샤가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“I will!", "\"올 거예요!"], ["Oh, how I curse myself for not having come before!”", "아아, 진작 오지 않은 제 자신이 얼마나 원망스러운지!\""], ["muttered Kolya, crying, and no longer ashamed of it.", "콜랴는 울먹이며 중얼거렸고, 이제는 그것을 부끄러워하지도 않았다."]], [["At that moment the captain flew out of the room, and at once closed the door behind him.", "바로 그 순간 대위가 방에서 뛰쳐나오며 곧장 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["His face looked frenzied, his lips were trembling.", "그의 얼굴은 광기에 휩싸인 듯했고, 입술은 떨리고 있었다."], ["He stood before the two and flung up his arms.", "그는 두 사람 앞에 서서 두 팔을 치켜들었다."]], [["“I don’t want a good boy!", "\"나는 좋은 아이 따위 싫어!"], ["I don’t want another boy!”", "다른 아이는 싫단 말이야!\""], ["he muttered in a wild whisper, clenching his teeth.", "그는 이를 악물고 거칠게 속삭이듯 중얼거렸다."], ["“If I forget thee, Jerusalem, may my tongue—”", "\"예루살렘아, 내가 너를 잊을진대, 내 혀가—\""], ["He broke off with a sob and sank on his knees before the wooden bench.", "그는 흐느낌으로 말을 끊고는 나무 의자 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["Pressing his fists against his head, he began sobbing with absurd whimpering cries, doing his utmost that his cries should not be heard in the room.", "두 주먹을 머리에 짓누른 채, 그는 우스꽝스럽게 칭얼대는 듯한 소리로 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 그 울음소리가 방 안까지 들리지 않게 하려고 안간힘을 다했다."]], [["Kolya ran out into the street.", "콜랴는 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“Good-by, Karamazov?", "\"잘 있어요, 카라마조프?"], ["Will you come yourself?”", "형도 직접 올 거죠?\""], ["he cried sharply and angrily to Alyosha.", "그가 알료샤에게 날카롭고 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“I will certainly come in the evening.”", "\"저녁에 꼭 올게.\""]], [["“What was that he said about Jerusalem?...", "\"그런데 아저씨가 예루살렘에 대해서 한 말은 뭐예요?……"], ["What did he mean by that?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이죠?\""]], [["“It’s from the Bible. ‘If I forget thee, Jerusalem,’ that is, if I forget all that is most precious to me, if I let anything take its place, then may—”", "\"성경에 나오는 말이야. '예루살렘아, 내가 너를 잊을진대'라는 건, 곧 내가 나에게 가장 소중한 모든 것을 잊는다면, 그 무엇이든 그 자리를 대신하게 한다면, 그때는—\" \"알았어요, 그만하면 됐어요! 꼭 와요! 이리 와, 페레즈본!\" 그는 개를 향해 그야말로 사납게 소리치고는, 성큼성큼 빠른 걸음으로 집으로 향했다."]], [["“I understand, that’s enough! Mind you come! Ici, Perezvon!” he cried with positive ferocity to the dog, and with rapid strides he went home.", "* 페레즈본(Перезвон): 러시아어로 '여러 종이 어우러져 울리는 소리'를 뜻하며, 콜랴가 의사에게 읊은 \"종소리는 들려도 어디서 나는지는 모른다\"라는 말장난과 연결된다."]], [["# Book XI. Ivan Chapter I. At Grushenka’s", "# Book XI. Ivan Chapter I. At Grushenka’s"]], [["Alyosha went towards the cathedral square to the widow Morozov’s house to see Grushenka, who had sent Fenya to him early in the morning with an urgent message begging him to come.", "알료샤는 대성당 광장 쪽으로, 미망인 모로조바의 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다. 그루셴카를 만나러 가는 길이었으니, 그녀가 이른 아침 페냐를 보내어 부디 와 달라는 절박한 전갈을 전했던 것이다."], ["Questioning Fenya, Alyosha learned that her mistress had been particularly distressed since the previous day.", "페냐에게 이것저것 묻는 동안 알료샤는 자기 여주인이 전날부터 유난히 마음 졸이고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["During the two months that had passed since Mitya’s arrest, Alyosha had called frequently at the widow Morozov’s house, both from his own inclination and to take messages for Mitya.", "미탸가 체포된 후 두 달이 흐르는 사이 알료샤는 모로조바의 집에 자주 들렀으니, 그 자신의 마음에서이기도 했고 미탸의 전갈을 전하기 위해서이기도 했다."], ["Three days after Mitya’s arrest, Grushenka was taken very ill and was ill for nearly five weeks.", "미탸가 체포되고 사흘이 지났을 때 그루셴카는 몹시 앓아누워 거의 다섯 주 동안이나 병석에 있었다."], ["For one whole week she was unconscious.", "그 가운데 꼬박 한 주일은 의식을 잃은 채였다."], ["She was very much changed—thinner and a little sallow, though she had for the past fortnight been well enough to go out.", "그녀는 몰라보게 변해서, 더 야위었고 안색도 다소 누렇게 떴으나, 지난 두 주 동안에는 외출할 수 있을 만큼은 회복되어 있었다."], ["But to Alyosha her face was even more attractive than before, and he liked to meet her eyes when he went in to her.", "그러나 알료샤가 보기에 그녀의 얼굴은 예전보다 오히려 더 매력적이었고, 그녀에게 들어설 때 그 눈과 마주치는 것이 그는 좋았다."], ["A look of firmness and intelligent purpose had developed in her face.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 굳건함과 지혜로운 결의의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["There were signs of a spiritual transformation in her, and a steadfast, fine and humble determination that nothing could shake could be discerned in her.", "그녀에게는 어떤 정신적 변모의 자취가 엿보였고, 무엇으로도 흔들 수 없는 한결같고 고결하며 겸허한 결심이 분간되었다."], ["There was a small vertical line between her brows which gave her charming face a look of concentrated thought, almost austere at the first glance.", "두 눈썹 사이에는 작은 세로 주름이 한 줄 잡혀 있었는데, 그것이 그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 듯한, 첫눈에는 거의 준엄해 보이는 인상을 더했다."], ["There was scarcely a trace of her former frivolity.", "예전의 그 경박함은 거의 자취도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["It seemed strange to Alyosha, too, that in spite of the calamity that had overtaken the poor girl, betrothed to a man who had been arrested for a terrible crime, almost at the instant of their betrothal, in spite of her illness and the almost inevitable sentence hanging over Mitya, Grushenka had not yet lost her youthful cheerfulness.", "알료샤에게는 또 한 가지 이상하게 여겨지는 것이 있었으니, 가엾은 처녀를 덮친 재난에도 불구하고—약혼한 지 거의 그 순간에 약혼자가 끔찍한 죄목으로 체포되었고, 그녀 자신의 병환에 더해 미탸에게 거의 피할 수 없는 선고가 드리워져 있는데도—그루셴카가 아직 젊은이다운 명랑함을 잃지 않았다는 점이었다."], ["There was a soft light in the once proud eyes, though at times they gleamed with the old vindictive fire when she was visited by one disturbing thought stronger than ever in her heart.", "한때 오만하던 그 두 눈에는 이제 부드러운 빛이 어려 있었으나, 마음속에서 그 어느 때보다 강하게 그녀를 괴롭히는 한 가지 생각이 찾아들 때면 더러는 옛날의 그 앙심 어린 불길로 번득이기도 했다."], ["The object of that uneasiness was the same as ever—Katerina Ivanovna, of whom Grushenka had even raved when she lay in delirium.", "그 불안의 대상은 변함없이 똑같았으니, 곧 카테리나 이바노브나였고, 그루셴카는 헛소리를 하며 누워 있을 때조차 그녀의 이름을 부르며 광란했던 것이다."], ["Alyosha knew that she was fearfully jealous of her.", "알료샤는 그녀가 그녀를 끔찍하게 질투하고 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["Yet Katerina Ivanovna had not once visited Mitya in his prison, though she might have done it whenever she liked.", "그런데도 카테리나 이바노브나는 마음만 먹으면 언제든 갈 수 있었음에도, 옥에 갇힌 미탸를 단 한 번도 찾지 않았다."], ["All this made a difficult problem for Alyosha, for he was the only person to whom Grushenka opened her heart and from whom she was continually asking advice.", "이 모든 것이 알료샤에게는 풀기 어려운 문제였으니, 그는 그루셴카가 속마음을 털어놓는 유일한 사람이자 그녀가 끊임없이 조언을 구하는 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["Sometimes he was unable to say anything.", "더러는 그도 아무 말도 해 줄 수 없었다."]], [["Full of anxiety he entered her lodging.", "근심에 가득 차 그는 그녀의 숙소로 들어섰다."], ["She was at home.", "그녀는 집에 있었다."], ["She had returned from seeing Mitya half an hour before, and from the rapid movement with which she leapt up from her chair to meet him he saw that she had been expecting him with great impatience.", "그녀는 반 시간 전에 미탸를 만나고 돌아온 참이었는데, 그를 맞으려 의자에서 벌떡 일어서는 그 재빠른 몸짓을 보고 그는 그녀가 몹시 애타게 자기를 기다리고 있었음을 알아챘다."], ["A pack of cards dealt for a game of “fools” lay on the table.", "탁자 위에는 \"바보 놀이\"를 하려고 돌려 놓은 카드 한 벌이 놓여 있었다."], ["A bed had been made up on the leather sofa on the other side and Maximov lay, half-reclining, on it.", "맞은편 가죽 소파 위에는 잠자리가 마련되어 있었고, 그 위에 막시모프가 반쯤 기대어 누워 있었다."], ["He wore a dressing-gown and a cotton nightcap, and was evidently ill and weak, though he was smiling blissfully.", "그는 실내복에 무명 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 있었으며, 보아하니 병들어 기운이 없었으나, 그래도 더없이 행복한 듯 미소를 짓고 있었다."], ["When the homeless old man returned with Grushenka from Mokroe two months before, he had simply stayed on and was still staying with her.", "두 달 전 그루셴카와 함께 모크로예에서 돌아온 이 의지가지없는 늙은이는, 그저 눌러앉아 지금껏 그녀와 함께 머물고 있는 것이었다."], ["He arrived with her in rain and sleet, sat down on the sofa, drenched and scared, and gazed mutely at her with a timid, appealing smile.", "그는 비와 진눈깨비를 맞으며 그녀와 함께 도착해서는, 흠뻑 젖고 잔뜩 겁먹은 채 소파에 주저앉아, 소심하면서도 애원하는 듯한 미소를 띠고 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Grushenka, who was in terrible grief and in the first stage of fever, almost forgot his existence in all she had to do the first half-hour after her arrival.", "끔찍한 슬픔에 잠긴 데다 열병의 초기 증세까지 겹쳐 있던 그루셴카는, 도착한 뒤 첫 반 시간 동안 처리할 일이 많아 그의 존재를 거의 잊고 있었다."], ["Suddenly she chanced to look at him intently: he laughed a pitiful, helpless little laugh.", "그러다 문득 그를 뚫어지게 바라보게 되었는데, 그는 가엾고 무력한 작은 웃음을 흘렸다."], ["She called Fenya and told her to give him something to eat.", "그녀는 페냐를 불러 그에게 먹을 것을 좀 주라고 일렀다."], ["All that day he sat in the same place, almost without stirring.", "그날 하루 종일 그는 거의 꼼짝도 않고 같은 자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["When it got dark and the shutters were closed, Fenya asked her mistress:", "날이 어두워지고 덧창이 닫혔을 때 페냐가 여주인에게 물었다."]], [["“Is the gentleman going to stay the night, mistress?”", "\"마님, 저 어른은 여기서 밤을 보내실 건가요?\""]], [["“Yes; make him a bed on the sofa,”", "\"그래, 소파에 잠자리를 봐 드려라.\""], ["answered Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 대답했다."]], [["Questioning him more in detail, Grushenka learned from him that he had literally nowhere to go, and that “Mr.", "좀 더 자세히 캐물어, 그루셴카는 그가 그야말로 갈 데가 한 군데도 없다는 것과, \"제 은인이신 칼가노프 나리께서 다시는 저를 거두지 않겠노라 대놓고 말씀하시고는 오 루블을 주셨습죠\""], ["Kalganov, my benefactor, told me straight that he wouldn’t receive me again and gave me five roubles.”", "하는 사정을 알게 되었다."]], [["“Well, God bless you, you’d better stay, then,”", "\"그래요, 그럼 하느님의 가호가 함께하시길, 여기 머무는 게 낫겠네요.\""], ["Grushenka decided in her grief, smiling compassionately at him.", "슬픔에 잠긴 채 그루셴카는 가엾이 여기는 미소를 그에게 지어 보이며 그렇게 결정했다."], ["Her smile wrung the old man’s heart and his lips twitched with grateful tears.", "그녀의 미소는 늙은이의 가슴을 후벼 팠고, 그의 입술은 고마운 눈물로 떨렸다."], ["And so the destitute wanderer had stayed with her ever since.", "그리하여 이 빈털터리 떠돌이는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그녀와 함께 머물게 되었다."], ["He did not leave the house even when she was ill.", "그는 그녀가 앓아누웠을 때조차 그 집을 떠나지 않았다."], ["Fenya and her grandmother, the cook, did not turn him out, but went on serving him meals and making up his bed on the sofa.", "페냐와 그 할머니인 식모도 그를 내쫓지 않고, 줄곧 끼니를 차려 주고 소파에 잠자리를 봐 주었다."], ["Grushenka had grown used to him, and coming back from seeing Mitya (whom she had begun to visit in prison before she was really well) she would sit down and begin talking to “Maximushka” about trifling matters, to keep her from thinking of her sorrow.", "그루셴카도 그에게 익숙해져서, 미탸를 만나고 돌아오면(그녀는 채 몸이 낫기도 전부터 옥에 갇힌 그를 찾기 시작했다) 자리에 앉아 슬픔에 골몰하지 않으려고 \"막시무시카\"와 시시콜콜한 이야기를 나누곤 했다."], ["The old man turned out to be a good story-teller on occasions, so that at last he became necessary to her.", "늙은이는 이따금 제법 그럴듯하게 이야기를 풀어 놓는 재주가 있어, 마침내 그녀에게 없어서는 안 될 사람이 되었다."], ["Grushenka saw scarcely any one else beside Alyosha, who did not come every day and never stayed long.", "그루셴카는 알료샤 말고는 거의 아무도 만나지 않았는데, 알료샤조차 매일 오는 것도 아니었고 오래 머무는 법도 없었다."], ["Her old merchant lay seriously ill at this time, “at his last gasp”", "그녀의 옛 상인은 이 무렵 중병으로 누워 있었으니, 고을 사람들 말마따나 \"마지막 숨을 헐떡이고\""], ["as they said in the town, and he did, in fact, die a week after Mitya’s trial.", "있었고, 실제로 그는 미탸의 재판이 끝난 일주일 뒤에 죽었다."], ["Three weeks before his death, feeling the end approaching, he made his sons, their wives and children, come upstairs to him at last and bade them not leave him again.", "죽기 세 주 전, 임종이 가까워 옴을 느낀 그는 마침내 아들들과 며느리들, 손주들을 위층 자기 방으로 불러올려 다시는 곁을 떠나지 말라고 명했다."], ["From that moment he gave strict orders to his servants not to admit Grushenka and to tell her if she came, “The master wishes you long life and happiness and tells you to forget him.”", "그 순간부터 그는 하인들에게 엄명을 내려 그루셴카를 절대 들이지 말고, 만일 그녀가 오면 이렇게 전하라고 했다. \"주인 어른께서는 부인이 오래 건강히 행복하게 사시기를 바라시며, 자신을 잊어 달라고 하셨습니다.\""], ["But Grushenka sent almost every day to inquire after him.", "그래도 그루셴카는 거의 날마다 사람을 보내 그의 안부를 물었다."]], [["“You’ve come at last!”", "\"드디어 오셨네요!\""], ["she cried, flinging down the cards and joyfully greeting Alyosha, “and Maximushka’s been scaring me that perhaps you wouldn’t come.", "그녀는 카드를 내던지고 알료샤를 기쁘게 맞으며 외쳤다. \"막시무시카가, 어쩌면 안 오실지도 모른다고 자꾸 겁을 주잖겠어요."], ["Ah, how I need you!", "아, 당신이 얼마나 필요한지!"], ["Sit down to the table.", "어서 탁자에 앉으세요."], ["What will you have—coffee?”", "뭘 드시겠어요, 커피?\""]], [["“Yes, please,”", "\"네, 주세요.\""], ["said Alyosha, sitting down at the table.", "알료샤가 탁자에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“I am very hungry.”", "\"몹시 시장하네요.\""]], [["“That’s right.", "\"잘됐네요."], ["Fenya, Fenya, coffee,”", "페냐, 페냐, 커피!\""], ["cried Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 외쳤다."], ["“It’s been made a long time ready for you.", "\"당신 드리려고 진작 끓여 놨어요."], ["And bring some little pies, and mind they are hot.", "그리고 작은 파이도 좀 가져오너라, 꼭 뜨겁게 해서."], ["Do you know, we’ve had a storm over those pies to-day.", "그거 아세요, 오늘 그 파이 때문에 한바탕 난리가 났답니다."], ["I took them to the prison for him, and would you believe it, he threw them back to me: he would not eat them.", "그이한테 주려고 옥으로 가져갔는데, 글쎄 그걸 도로 저한테 내던지지 뭐예요. 먹지 않겠다는 거예요."], ["He flung one of them on the floor and stamped on it.", "그중 하나는 바닥에 내동댕이치고 발로 짓밟더라고요. 그래서 제가 그랬죠."], ["So I said to him: ‘I shall leave them with the warder; if you don’t eat them before evening, it will be that your venomous spite is enough for you!’ With that I went away.", "'간수한테 맡겨 두고 가겠어요. 저녁때까지 안 드시면, 그건 당신 그 독한 심술만으로도 배가 부르다는 거겠죠!'"], ["We quarreled again, would you believe it?", "그러고는 나와 버렸어요. 또 싸웠지 뭐예요, 글쎄."], ["Whenever I go we quarrel.”", "갈 때마다 싸운다니까요.\""]], [["Grushenka said all this in one breath in her agitation.", "그루셴카는 격앙된 나머지 이 모든 것을 단숨에 쏟아냈다."], ["Maximov, feeling nervous, at once smiled and looked on the floor.", "막시모프는 마음이 불안해져서 이내 미소를 지으며 바닥으로 눈을 떨구었다."]], [["“What did you quarrel about this time?”", "\"이번엔 무슨 일로 다투셨는데요?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“I didn’t expect it in the least.", "\"전혀 생각지도 못한 일이었어요."], ["Only fancy, he is jealous of the Pole.", "글쎄, 그이가 그 폴란드 사람을 질투하지 뭐예요."], ["‘Why are you keeping him?’ he said.", "'왜 그자를 거두고 있는 거야?' 그러더라고요."], ["‘So you’ve begun keeping him.’ He is jealous, jealous of me all the time, jealous eating and sleeping!", "'그래, 이제 아주 그자를 데리고 사는군.' 질투, 줄곧 저를 질투해요, 먹는 것 자는 것까지 질투한다니까요!"], ["He even took it into his head to be jealous of Kuzma last week.”", "지난주에는 또 쿠즈마를 질투할 생각까지 다 하더라고요.\""]], [["“But he knew about the Pole before?”", "\"하지만 그분도 그 폴란드 사람에 대해 진작부터 알고 있었잖아요?\""]], [["“Yes, but there it is.", "\"네, 그런데 그게 문제예요."], ["He has known about him from the very beginning, but to-day he suddenly got up and began scolding about him.", "처음부터 다 알고 있었으면서, 오늘은 느닷없이 벌떡 일어나서 그자에 대해 욕을 퍼붓기 시작하는 거예요."], ["I am ashamed to repeat what he said.", "무슨 말을 했는지 입에 담기도 부끄러워요."], ["Silly fellow!", "어리석은 사람!"], ["Rakitin went in as I came out.", "제가 나올 때 라키틴이 들어가더라고요."], ["Perhaps Rakitin is egging him on.", "어쩌면 라키틴이 부추기는지도 몰라요."], ["What do you think?”", "어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["she added carelessly.", "그녀는 아무렇지 않은 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["“He loves you, that’s what it is: he loves you so much.", "\"그분은 당신을 사랑하는 겁니다, 그게 다예요. 그토록 당신을 사랑하는 거죠."], ["And now he is particularly worried.”", "그런데 지금은 유난히 마음이 불안한 거고요.\""]], [["“I should think he might be, with the trial to-morrow.", "\"그야 그럴 만도 하겠죠, 내일이 재판이니까요."], ["And I went to him to say something about to-morrow, for I dread to think what’s going to happen then.", "그래서 제가 내일 일을 두고 뭐라도 좀 말하려고 갔던 거예요. 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각만 해도 두려우니까요."], ["You say that he is worried, but how worried I am!", "당신은 그이가 불안해한다고 하시지만, 제가 얼마나 불안한지 아세요!"], ["And he talks about the Pole!", "그런데 폴란드 사람 이야기나 하고 있다니!"], ["He’s too silly!", "너무 어리석어요!"], ["He is not jealous of Maximushka yet, anyway.”", "그래도 막시무시카는 아직 질투하지 않으니 다행이죠.\""]], [["“My wife was dreadfully jealous over me, too,”", "\"제 아내도 저를 두고 어찌나 질투를 하던지요.\""], ["Maximov put in his word.", "막시모프가 한마디 끼어들었다."]], [["“Jealous of you?”", "\"당신을 질투해요?\""], ["Grushenka laughed in spite of herself.", "그루셴카는 자기도 모르게 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Of whom could she have been jealous?”", "\"대체 누굴 질투할 게 있다고요?\""]], [["“Of the servant girls.”", "\"하녀들을 두고요.\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, Maximushka, I am in no laughing mood now; I feel angry.", "\"입 다물어요, 막시무시카, 지금은 웃을 기분이 아니에요. 화가 나 있다고요."], ["Don’t ogle the pies.", "파이를 그렇게 눈독 들이지 말아요."], ["I shan’t give you any; they are not good for you, and I won’t give you any vodka either.", "안 줄 거예요. 당신 몸에 안 좋아요."], ["I have to look after him, too, just as though I kept an almshouse,”", "보드카도 안 줄 거고요. 나는 이이까지 보살펴야 한다니까요, 마치 양로원이라도 차린 것처럼.\""], ["she laughed.", "그녀가 웃었다."]], [["“I don’t deserve your kindness.", "\"저는 마님의 친절을 받을 자격이 없습니다."], ["I am a worthless creature,”", "저는 쓸모없는 인간이지요.\""], ["said Maximov, with tears in his voice.", "막시모프가 목멘 소리로 말했다."], ["“You would do better to spend your kindness on people of more use than me.”", "\"저 같은 것보다 더 쓸모 있는 사람들에게 친절을 베푸시는 게 나을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Ech, every one is of use, Maximushka, and how can we tell who’s of most use?", "\"에, 누구나 다 쓸모가 있는 거예요, 막시무시카, 게다가 누가 제일 쓸모 있는지 우리가 어찌 알겠어요?"], ["If only that Pole didn’t exist, Alyosha.", "그 폴란드 사람만 없으면 좋으련만, 알료샤."], ["He’s taken it into his head to fall ill, too, to-day.", "그자도 오늘 또 병이 날 생각을 했지 뭐예요."], ["I’ve been to see him also.", "그래서 그자한테도 다녀왔어요."], ["And I shall send him some pies, too, on purpose.", "그자한테도 일부러 파이를 좀 보낼 거예요."], ["I hadn’t sent him any, but Mitya accused me of it, so now I shall send some!", "보낸 적이 없었는데, 미탸가 그렇다고 저를 몰아세웠으니, 그러니 이제 보낼 거예요!"], ["Ah, here’s Fenya with a letter!", "아, 페냐가 편지를 들고 오네요!"], ["Yes, it’s from the Poles—begging again!”", "네, 그 폴란드 사람들한테서 온 거예요. 또 구걸하는 거죠!\""]], [["Pan Mussyalovitch had indeed sent an extremely long and characteristically eloquent letter in which he begged her to lend him three roubles.", "과연 무샬로비치 씨는 더없이 길고도 그답게 화려한 편지를 보내왔는데, 그 안에서 그는 그녀에게 삼 루블만 빌려 달라고 간청하고 있었다."], ["In the letter was enclosed a receipt for the sum, with a promise to repay it within three months, signed by Pan Vrublevsky as well.", "편지에는 그 금액에 대한 차용증이 동봉되어 있었고, 석 달 안에 갚겠다는 약속과 함께 브루블렙스키 씨의 서명까지 들어 있었다."], ["Grushenka had received many such letters, accompanied by such receipts, from her former lover during the fortnight of her convalescence.", "그루셴카는 회복기에 접어든 두 주 동안 옛 정인에게서 이런 차용증을 곁들인 편지를 여러 통 받았다."], ["But she knew that the two Poles had been to ask after her health during her illness.", "그러나 그녀는 그 두 폴란드 사람이 그녀가 앓는 동안 안부를 물으러 왔었다는 것은 알고 있었다."], ["The first letter Grushenka got from them was a long one, written on large notepaper and with a big family crest on the seal.", "그루셴카가 그들에게서 받은 첫 번째 편지는 길고 긴 것으로, 큰 편지지에 쓰여 봉인에는 커다란 가문(家紋)이 찍혀 있었다."], ["It was so obscure and rhetorical that Grushenka put it down before she had read half, unable to make head or tail of it.", "그것이 어찌나 알아먹기 어렵고 미사여구로 가득하던지, 그루셴카는 절반도 읽기 전에 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없어 내려놓고 말았다."], ["She could not attend to letters then.", "그때는 편지 따위에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었던 것이다."], ["The first letter was followed next day by another in which Pan Mussyalovitch begged her for a loan of two thousand roubles for a very short period.", "첫 번째 편지에 이어 이튿날 두 번째 편지가 왔는데, 거기서 무샬로비치 씨는 아주 짧은 기간만 이천 루블을 빌려 달라고 간청했다."], ["Grushenka left that letter, too, unanswered.", "그루셴카는 그 편지에도 답하지 않았다."], ["A whole series of letters had followed—one every day—all as pompous and rhetorical, but the loan asked for, gradually diminishing, dropped to a hundred roubles, then to twenty-five, to ten, and finally Grushenka received a letter in which both the Poles begged her for only one rouble and included a receipt signed by both.", "그 뒤로 일련의 편지가 줄을 이었으니, 날마다 한 통씩 모두 한결같이 거창하고 화려했으나, 빌려 달라는 액수는 차츰 줄어들어 백 루블로, 다음엔 이십오 루블로, 십 루블로 떨어졌고, 마침내 그루셴카는 두 폴란드 사람이 단 일 루블만 간청하면서 둘 다 서명한 차용증을 동봉한 편지를 받게 되었다."]], [["Then Grushenka suddenly felt sorry for them, and at dusk she went round herself to their lodging.", "그러자 그루셴카는 문득 그들이 가엾게 여겨져, 해 질 녘에 손수 그들의 숙소로 찾아갔다."], ["She found the two Poles in great poverty, almost destitution, without food or fuel, without cigarettes, in debt to their landlady.", "가 보니 두 폴란드 사람은 극심한 가난, 거의 알거지나 다름없는 처지에 빠져 있었으니, 먹을 것도 땔감도 담배도 없는 데다 셋집 안주인에게 빚까지 지고 있었다."], ["The two hundred roubles they had carried off from Mitya at Mokroe had soon disappeared.", "모크로예에서 미탸에게서 챙겨 온 이백 루블은 금세 사라져 버린 것이다."], ["But Grushenka was surprised at their meeting her with arrogant dignity and self-assertion, with the greatest punctilio and pompous speeches.", "그러나 그루셴카가 놀란 것은, 그들이 거만한 위엄과 자기 과시를 부리며, 더없이 까다로운 격식과 거창한 언사로 그녀를 맞이했다는 점이었다."], ["Grushenka simply laughed, and gave her former admirer ten roubles.", "그루셴카는 그저 웃으며 옛 흠모자에게 십 루블을 주었다."], ["Then, laughing, she told Mitya of it and he was not in the least jealous.", "그러고는 웃으며 미탸에게 그 이야기를 했더니 그는 조금도 질투하지 않았다."], ["But ever since, the Poles had attached themselves to Grushenka and bombarded her daily with requests for money and she had always sent them small sums.", "그러나 그때부터 폴란드 사람들은 그루셴카에게 들러붙어 날마다 돈 요구로 그녀를 들볶았고, 그녀는 늘 얼마씩 적은 돈을 보내 주었다."], ["And now that day Mitya had taken it into his head to be fearfully jealous.", "그런데 바로 그날 미탸가 끔찍하게 질투를 부릴 생각을 했던 것이다."]], [["“Like a fool, I went round to him just for a minute, on the way to see Mitya, for he is ill, too, my Pole,”", "\"바보처럼, 미탸를 보러 가는 길에 잠깐만 들르려고 그자한테 갔지 뭐예요, 그 사람도 아프거든요, 그 폴란드 사람 말이에요.\""], ["Grushenka began again with nervous haste.", "그루셴카는 다시 안절부절못하며 황급히 말을 이었다."], ["“I was laughing, telling Mitya about it.", "\"그러고는 웃으면서 미탸한테 그 이야기를 했어요."], ["‘Fancy,’ I said, ‘my Pole had the happy thought to sing his old songs to me to the guitar.", "'글쎄,' 그랬죠, '그 폴란드 사람이 좋다고 기타를 치면서 옛날 노래를 불러 주지 뭐예요."], ["He thought I would be touched and marry him!’ Mitya leapt up swearing....", "그러면 내가 감동해서 자기랑 결혼이라도 할 줄 알았던 거예요!'"], ["So, there, I’ll send them the pies!", "그랬더니 미탸가 욕을 퍼부으며 벌떡 일어서더라고요……"], ["Fenya, is it that little girl they’ve sent?", "그러니까, 봐요, 그래서 그들한테 파이를 보낼 거예요!"], ["Here, give her three roubles and pack a dozen pies up in a paper and tell her to take them.", "페냐, 저 애가 그자들이 보낸 그 계집애냐? 자, 얘한테 삼 루블 주고 파이 열두 개를 종이에 싸서 가져가라고 일러라."], ["And you, Alyosha, be sure to tell Mitya that I did send them the pies.”", "그리고 당신, 알료샤, 미탸한테 내가 그들한테 파이를 정말로 보냈다고 꼭 전해 주세요.\""]], [["“I wouldn’t tell him for anything,”", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 그 말은 전하지 않겠습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha, smiling.", "알료샤가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Ech!", "\"에!"], ["You think he is unhappy about it.", "당신은 그이가 그 일로 괴로워한다고 생각하시죠."], ["Why, he’s jealous on purpose.", "천만에요, 일부러 질투하는 거예요."], ["He doesn’t care,”", "아무렇지도 않으면서.\""], ["said Grushenka bitterly.", "그루셴카가 쓰라리게 말했다."]], [["“On purpose?”", "\"일부러요?\""], ["queried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“I tell you you are silly, Alyosha.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 봐요, 당신은 어리석어요, 알료샤."], ["You know nothing about it, with all your cleverness.", "그렇게 똑똑하면서도 이런 일은 도통 모른다니까요."], ["I am not offended that he is jealous of a girl like me.", "나는 그이가 나 같은 계집을 질투한다고 화가 나는 게 아니에요."], ["I would be offended if he were not jealous.", "질투하지 않는다면 오히려 화가 나겠죠."], ["I am like that.", "나는 그런 사람이에요."], ["I am not offended at jealousy.", "질투에는 화가 안 나요."], ["I have a fierce heart, too.", "나도 사나운 마음을 가졌거든요."], ["I can be jealous myself.", "나도 질투할 줄 알아요."], ["Only what offends me is that he doesn’t love me at all.", "다만 내가 화가 나는 건, 그이가 나를 조금도 사랑하지 않는다는 거예요."], ["I tell you he is jealous now on purpose.", "지금 그이가 일부러 질투하고 있다니까요."], ["Am I blind? Don’t I see?", "내가 눈이 멀었나요?"], ["He began talking to me just now of that woman, of Katerina, saying she was this and that, how she had ordered a doctor from Moscow for him, to try and save him; how she had ordered the best counsel, the most learned one, too.", "내가 못 볼 것 같아요? 그이가 방금 나한테 그 여자 이야기를, 카테리나 이야기를 꺼내더라고요, 그 여자가 이렇네 저렇네, 자기를 살려 보겠다고 모스크바에서 의사를 불러왔다느니, 가장 훌륭한, 가장 박식한 변호사까지 구해 줬다느니."], ["So he loves her, if he’ll praise her to my face, more shame to him!", "그러니 그이는 그 여자를 사랑하는 거예요, 내 면전에서 그 여자를 칭찬해 대다니, 그런 뻔뻔한 사람 같으니!"], ["He’s treated me badly himself, so he attacked me, to make out I am in fault first and to throw it all on me.", "자기가 나한테 못되게 굴어 놓고는, 오히려 나를 몰아세우는 거예요, 내가 먼저 잘못한 걸로 꾸며서 죄다 나한테 뒤집어씌우려고요."], ["‘You were with your Pole before me, so I can’t be blamed for Katerina,’ that’s what it amounts to.", "'당신은 나보다 먼저 그 폴란드 사람이랑 같이 있었으니, 카테리나 일로 나를 탓할 수는 없지,' 결국 그런 얘기예요."], ["He wants to throw the whole blame on me.", "모든 죄를 나한테 뒤집어씌우려는 거죠."], ["He attacked me on purpose, on purpose, I tell you, but I’ll—”", "일부러 나를 몰아세운 거예요, 일부러라니까요, 내 말 들어요, 하지만 나는——\""]], [["Grushenka could not finish saying what she would do.", "그루셴카는 자기가 어쩌겠다는 말을 끝맺지 못했다."], ["She hid her eyes in her handkerchief and sobbed violently.", "그녀는 손수건에 눈을 묻고 격하게 흐느꼈다."]], [["“He doesn’t love Katerina Ivanovna,”", "\"그분은 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하지 않아요.\""], ["said Alyosha firmly.", "알료샤가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Well, whether he loves her or not, I’ll soon find out for myself,”", "\"그래, 사랑하든 안 하든, 내가 곧 직접 알아낼 거예요.\""], ["said Grushenka, with a menacing note in her voice, taking the handkerchief from her eyes.", "그루셴카가 위협이 담긴 목소리로 말하며 눈에서 손수건을 떼었다."], ["Her face was distorted.", "그녀의 얼굴은 일그러져 있었다."], ["Alyosha saw sorrowfully that from being mild and serene, it had become sullen and spiteful.", "알료샤는 온화하고 평온하던 그 얼굴이 음울하고 앙심으로 가득해진 것을 슬프게 바라보았다."]], [["“Enough of this foolishness,”", "\"이런 어리석은 얘기는 이제 그만.\""], ["she said suddenly; “it’s not for that I sent for you.", "그녀가 갑자기 말했다. \"그것 때문에 당신을 부른 게 아니에요."], ["Alyosha, darling, to-morrow—what will happen to-morrow?", "알료샤, 그대여, 내일—내일은 무슨 일이 벌어질까요?"], ["That’s what worries me!", "그게 나를 괴롭혀요!"], ["And it’s only me it worries!", "그리고 그걸 괴로워하는 건 나뿐이에요!"], ["I look at every one and no one is thinking of it.", "다들 둘러봐도 아무도 그 생각을 안 해요."], ["No one cares about it.", "아무도 신경 쓰지 않아요."], ["Are you thinking about it even?", "당신은 그 생각이라도 하나요?"], ["To-morrow he’ll be tried, you know.", "내일이면 그이가 재판을 받잖아요."], ["Tell me, how will he be tried?", "말해 봐요, 그이가 어떻게 재판을 받게 될까요?"], ["You know it’s the valet, the valet killed him!", "그 하인이, 그 하인이 죽인 거 알잖아요! 맙소사!"], ["Good heavens! Can they condemn him in place of the valet and will no one stand up for him?", "하인 대신 그이에게 죄를 뒤집어씌울 수 있는 건가요, 아무도 그이 편을 들어 주지 않는 건가요?"], ["They haven’t troubled the valet at all, have they?”", "그 하인은 아예 건드리지도 않은 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“He’s been severely cross-examined,”", "\"그자는 호되게 심문을 받았어요.\""], ["observed Alyosha thoughtfully; “but every one came to the conclusion it was not he.", "알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"하지만 모두가 그자는 아니라는 결론에 이르렀어요."], ["Now he is lying very ill.", "지금 그자는 몹시 앓아누워 있어요."], ["He has been ill ever since that attack.", "그 발작 이후로 줄곧 앓고 있어요."], ["Really ill,”", "정말로 앓고 있어요.\""], ["added Alyosha.", "알료샤가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, dear!", "\"오, 저런!"], ["couldn’t you go to that counsel yourself and tell him the whole thing by yourself?", "당신이 직접 그 변호사한테 가서 모든 걸 손수 다 이야기할 수는 없나요?"], ["He’s been brought from Petersburg for three thousand roubles, they say.”", "그 사람을 페테르부르크에서 삼천 루블에 데려왔다던데요.\""]], [["“We gave these three thousand together—Ivan, Katerina Ivanovna and I—but she paid two thousand for the doctor from Moscow herself.", "\"그 삼천 루블은 우리가 함께 냈어요—이반과 카테리나 이바노브나와 내가요—하지만 모스크바에서 의사를 부르는 데 든 이천 루블은 그녀가 혼자서 냈어요."], ["The counsel Fetyukovitch would have charged more, but the case has become known all over Russia; it’s talked of in all the papers and journals.", "변호사 페튜코비치라면 더 많이 청구했겠지만, 이 사건이 온 러시아에 알려졌잖아요. 모든 신문과 잡지에서 떠들어 대니까요."], ["Fetyukovitch agreed to come more for the glory of the thing, because the case has become so notorious.", "페튜코비치는 명성을 위해서 오겠다고 한 면이 더 커요, 사건이 그토록 유명해졌으니까요."], ["I saw him yesterday.”", "어제 그 사람을 만났어요.\""]], [["“Well?", "\"그래서요?"], ["Did you talk to him?”", "그 사람과 이야기는 나눠 봤나요?\""], ["Grushenka put in eagerly.", "그루셴카가 다급히 끼어들었다."]], [["“He listened and said nothing.", "\"그는 듣기만 하고 아무 말도 하지 않았어요."], ["He told me that he had already formed his opinion.", "자기는 이미 의견을 정했다고 하더군요."], ["But he promised to give my words consideration.”", "그래도 내 말을 참작해 보겠다고는 약속했어요.\""]], [["“Consideration!", "\"참작이라니!"], ["Ah, they are swindlers!", "아, 다들 사기꾼이에요!"], ["They’ll ruin him.", "그들이 그이를 망쳐 놓을 거예요."], ["And why did she send for the doctor?”", "그런데 그 여자는 왜 의사를 불렀대요?\""]], [["“As an expert.", "\"감정인으로요. 그들은 미탸가 미쳤고, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르는 상태에서 살인을 저질렀다는 걸 입증하려는 거예요.\""], ["They want to prove that Mitya’s mad and committed the murder when he didn’t know what he was doing”; Alyosha smiled gently; “but Mitya won’t agree to that.”", "알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다. \"하지만 미탸는 거기에 동의하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes; but that would be the truth if he had killed him!”", "\"그래요, 하지만 그게 만일 그이가 정말 죽였다면 사실이었을 거예요!\""], ["cried Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 외쳤다."], ["“He was mad then, perfectly mad, and that was my fault, wretch that I am!", "\"그때 그이는 미쳐 있었어요, 완전히 미쳐 있었고, 그건 다 내 잘못이에요, 못난 나 같은 것 때문에!"], ["But, of course, he didn’t do it, he didn’t do it!", "하지만 물론 그이는 그러지 않았어요, 그러지 않았다고요!"], ["And they are all against him, the whole town.", "그런데도 다들 그이를 적으로 돌리고 있어요, 온 고을이 다요."], ["Even Fenya’s evidence went to prove he had done it.", "페냐의 증언조차 그이가 저질렀다는 걸 입증하는 쪽으로 갔어요."], ["And the people at the shop, and that official, and at the tavern, too, before, people had heard him say so!", "가게 사람들도, 그 관리도, 술집에서도, 전에 사람들이 그이가 그렇게 말하는 걸 들었다지 뭐예요!"], ["They are all, all against him, all crying out against him.”", "다들, 다들 그이를 적으로 돌리고, 다들 그이를 향해 소리치고 있어요.\""]], [["“Yes, there’s a fearful accumulation of evidence,”", "\"그래요, 증거가 무섭게 쌓여 있어요.\""], ["Alyosha observed grimly.", "알료샤가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“And Grigory—Grigory Vassilyevitch—sticks to his story that the door was open, persists that he saw it—there’s no shaking him.", "\"그리고 그리고리—그리고리 바실리예비치—그 사람은 문이 열려 있었다는 자기 이야기를 끝까지 고집하고, 자기가 직접 봤다고 우겨 대고 있어요—도무지 그 사람을 흔들 수가 없어요."], ["I went and talked to him myself.", "내가 직접 가서 이야기해 봤거든요."], ["He’s rude about it, too.”", "그 일에 대해서는 무례하기까지 하더라고요.\""]], [["“Yes, that’s perhaps the strongest evidence against him,”", "\"그래요, 그게 어쩌면 그분에게 가장 불리한 증거일 거예요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“And as for Mitya’s being mad, he certainly seems like it now,”", "\"그리고 미탸가 미쳤다는 거 말인데요, 정말이지 지금 보면 그래 보이긴 해요.\""], ["Grushenka began with a peculiarly anxious and mysterious air.", "그루셴카가 유난히 불안하고 알 수 없는 기색으로 입을 뗐다."], ["“Do you know, Alyosha, I’ve been wanting to talk to you about it for a long time.", "\"그거 아세요, 알료샤, 이 얘기를 당신한테 하고 싶었던 게 오래됐어요."], ["I go to him every day and simply wonder at him.", "나는 날마다 그이를 찾아가는데, 그저 그이가 의아할 뿐이에요."], ["Tell me, now, what do you suppose he’s always talking about?", "자, 말해 봐요, 그이가 늘 무슨 이야기를 하는 것 같아요?"], ["He talks and talks and I can make nothing of it.", "말하고 또 말하는데, 나는 도무지 갈피를 못 잡겠어요."], ["I fancied he was talking of something intellectual that I couldn’t understand in my foolishness.", "처음엔 내가 어리석어 알아듣지 못하는 무슨 어려운 얘기를 하는가 싶었어요."], ["Only he suddenly began talking to me about a babe—that is, about some child.", "그런데 갑자기 어떤 아기에 대해서, 그러니까 무슨 어린아이에 대해서 말하기 시작하는 거예요."], ["‘Why is the babe poor?’ he said.", "'왜 그 아기는 가난한 거지?' 그러더라고요."], ["‘It’s for that babe I am going to Siberia now.", "'바로 그 아기 때문에 내가 이제 시베리아로 가는 거야."], ["I am not a murderer, but I must go to Siberia!’ What that meant, what babe, I couldn’t tell for the life of me.", "나는 살인자가 아니지만, 시베리아로 가야만 해!' 그게 무슨 뜻인지, 무슨 아기인지, 나는 죽었다 깨어나도 알 수가 없었어요."], ["Only I cried when he said it, because he said it so nicely.", "다만 그이가 그 말을 할 때 나는 울었어요, 어찌나 다정하게 말하던지요."], ["He cried himself, and I cried, too.", "그이도 울고, 나도 울었어요."], ["He suddenly kissed me and made the sign of the cross over me.", "그이는 갑자기 나한테 입을 맞추고 내 위로 성호를 그어 주었어요."], ["What did it mean, Alyosha, tell me?", "그게 무슨 뜻일까요, 알료샤, 말해 봐요?"], ["What is this babe?”", "그 아기가 뭘까요?\""]], [["“It must be Rakitin, who’s been going to see him lately,”", "\"분명 라키틴일 거예요, 요즘 그분을 찾아가고 있으니까요.\""], ["smiled Alyosha, “though ...", "알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"하긴……"], ["that’s not Rakitin’s doing.", "그건 라키틴이 시킨 일은 아니지만요."], ["I didn’t see Mitya yesterday.", "어제는 미탸를 못 봤어요."], ["I’ll see him to-day.”", "오늘 만나 봐야겠어요.\""]], [["“No, it’s not Rakitin; it’s his brother Ivan Fyodorovitch upsetting him.", "\"아니요, 라키틴이 아니에요. 그건 그이 동생인 이반 표도로비치가 그이를 뒤흔들어 놓는 거예요."], ["It’s his going to see him, that’s what it is,”", "그 사람이 그이를 찾아가는 것, 그게 문제예요.\""], ["Grushenka began, and suddenly broke off.", "그루셴카가 입을 떼다가 갑자기 말을 뚝 끊었다."], ["Alyosha gazed at her in amazement.", "알료샤는 놀라서 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Ivan’s going?", "\"이반이 찾아간다고요?"], ["Has he been to see him?", "그가 그분을 만나러 왔었나요?"], ["Mitya told me himself that Ivan hasn’t been once.”", "미탸가 직접 나한테 이반은 한 번도 안 왔다고 했는데요.\""]], [["“There ...", "\"저런……"], ["there!", "저런!"], ["What a girl I am!", "내가 무슨 계집애람!"], ["Blurting things out!”", "함부로 입을 놀리다니!\""], ["exclaimed Grushenka, confused and suddenly blushing.", "그루셴카가 당황해서 갑자기 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“Stay, Alyosha, hush!", "\"잠깐만요, 알료샤, 쉿!"], ["Since I’ve said so much I’ll tell the whole truth—he’s been to see him twice, the first directly he arrived.", "이왕 이만큼 말했으니 진실을 다 말할게요—그 사람은 그이를 두 번 찾아갔어요, 첫 번째는 도착하자마자였죠."], ["He galloped here from Moscow at once, of course, before I was taken ill; and the second time was a week ago.", "물론 모스크바에서 단숨에 말을 달려 이리로 왔어요, 내가 앓아눕기도 전이었죠. 그리고 두 번째는 일주일 전이었어요."], ["He told Mitya not to tell you about it, under any circumstances; and not to tell any one, in fact.", "그 사람은 미탸한테 무슨 일이 있어도 당신한테 이 얘기를 하지 말라고, 사실 아무한테도 말하지 말라고 했대요."], ["He came secretly.”", "그 사람은 몰래 온 거예요.\""]], [["Alyosha sat plunged in thought, considering something.", "알료샤는 생각에 잠겨 무언가를 곰곰이 따져 보며 앉아 있었다."], ["The news evidently impressed him.", "그 소식은 분명 그에게 깊은 인상을 남긴 것이었다."]], [["“Ivan doesn’t talk to me of Mitya’s case,”", "\"이반은 나한테 미탸의 사건 이야기를 하지 않아요.\""], ["he said slowly.", "그가 천천히 말했다."], ["“He’s said very little to me these last two months.", "\"요 두 달 동안 나한테 거의 말이 없었어요."], ["And whenever I go to see him, he seems vexed at my coming, so I’ve not been to him for the last three weeks.", "그리고 내가 찾아갈 때마다 내가 오는 걸 언짢아하는 것 같아서, 지난 세 주 동안은 그를 찾아가지 않았어요."], ["H’m!...", "흠!……"], ["if he was there a week ago ...", "일주일 전에 그가 거기 있었다면……"], ["there certainly has been a change in Mitya this week.”", "정말이지 이번 주에 미탸에게 변화가 있긴 했어요.\""]], [["“There has been a change,”", "\"변화가 있었어요.\""], ["Grushenka assented quickly.", "그루셴카가 재빨리 맞장구쳤다."], ["“They have a secret, they have a secret!", "\"그들에겐 비밀이 있어요, 비밀이 있다고요!"], ["Mitya told me himself there was a secret, and such a secret that Mitya can’t rest.", "미탸가 직접 나한테 비밀이 있다고 말했어요, 그것도 미탸가 도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없을 만큼 큰 비밀이라고요."], ["Before then, he was cheerful—and, indeed, he is cheerful now—but when he shakes his head like that, you know, and strides about the room and keeps pulling at the hair on his right temple with his right hand, I know there is something on his mind worrying him....", "전에는 명랑했어요—사실 지금도 명랑하긴 해요—그런데 그렇게 고개를 절레절레 흔들면서, 알죠, 방 안을 성큼성큼 거닐고 오른손으로 오른쪽 관자놀이 머리카락을 자꾸 잡아당기면, 나는 그이 마음속에 그이를 괴롭히는 무언가가 있다는 걸 알아요……"], ["I know!", "안다고요!"], ["He was cheerful before, though, indeed, he is cheerful to-day.”", "전에는 명랑했는데, 하긴 오늘도 명랑하긴 해요.\""]], [["“But you said he was worried.”", "\"하지만 그분이 불안해한다고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, he is worried and yet cheerful.", "\"네, 불안해하면서도 또 명랑해요."], ["He keeps on being irritable for a minute and then cheerful and then irritable again.", "잠깐 짜증을 내다가 또 명랑해지고 또 짜증을 내요."], ["And you know, Alyosha, I am constantly wondering at him—with this awful thing hanging over him, he sometimes laughs at such trifles as though he were a baby himself.”", "그리고 그거 아세요, 알료샤, 나는 늘 그이가 의아해요—그렇게 끔찍한 일이 그이 머리 위에 드리워져 있는데도, 가끔은 마치 자기가 아기라도 된 양 그런 시시한 일에 깔깔 웃거든요.\""]], [["“And did he really tell you not to tell me about Ivan?", "\"그런데 그분이 정말로 당신한테 이반에 대해 나한테 말하지 말라고 했나요?"], ["Did he say, ‘Don’t tell him’?”", "'그한테 말하지 마'라고 했어요?\""]], [["“Yes, he told me, ‘Don’t tell him.’ It’s you that Mitya’s most afraid of.", "\"네, 그랬어요, '그한테 말하지 마.' 미탸가 가장 두려워하는 게 바로 당신이에요."], ["Because it’s a secret: he said himself it was a secret.", "그게 비밀이니까요. 그이 자신이 비밀이라고 했어요."], ["Alyosha, darling, go to him and find out what their secret is and come and tell me,”", "알료샤, 그대여, 그이한테 가서 그들의 비밀이 뭔지 알아내고 와서 나한테 말해 줘요.\""], ["Grushenka besought him with sudden eagerness.", "그루셴카가 갑자기 간절하게 그에게 애원했다."], ["“Set my mind at rest that I may know the worst that’s in store for me.", "\"나한테 닥칠 최악의 일이 뭔지 알 수 있게 내 마음을 편하게 해 줘요."], ["That’s why I sent for you.”", "그래서 당신을 부른 거예요.\""]], [["“You think it’s something to do with you?", "\"그게 당신과 관련된 일이라고 생각하세요?"], ["If it were, he wouldn’t have told you there was a secret.”", "만일 그렇다면, 그분이 당신한테 비밀이 있다는 말조차 하지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["Perhaps he wants to tell me, but doesn’t dare to. He warns me.", "어쩌면 나한테 말하고 싶은데 차마 못 하는 건지도 모르죠."], ["There is a secret, he tells me, but he won’t tell me what it is.”", "나한테 미리 일러두는 거예요. 비밀이 있다고는 하면서, 그게 뭔지는 말해 주지 않아요.\""]], [["“What do you think yourself?”", "\"당신 생각은 어때요?\""]], [["“What do I think?", "\"내 생각이요?"], ["It’s the end for me, that’s what I think.", "나한테는 끝장이에요, 그게 내 생각이에요."], ["They all three have been plotting my end, for Katerina’s in it.", "그들 셋이서 내 끝장을 꾸미고 있어요, 카테리나가 거기 끼어 있으니까요."], ["It’s all Katerina, it all comes from her.", "모두 카테리나 때문이에요, 모두 그 여자한테서 비롯된 거예요."], ["She is this and that, and that means that I am not.", "그 여자가 이렇네 저렇네 하는 건, 곧 내가 아니라는 뜻이죠."], ["He tells me that beforehand—warns me.", "그이는 그걸 미리 나한테 일러두는 거예요—경고하는 거죠."], ["He is planning to throw me over, that’s the whole secret.", "나를 내팽개칠 작정인 거예요, 그게 비밀의 전부예요."], ["They’ve planned it together, the three of them—Mitya, Katerina, and Ivan Fyodorovitch.", "그들이 함께 꾸민 거예요, 셋이서요—미탸, 카테리나, 그리고 이반 표도로비치가요."], ["Alyosha, I’ve been wanting to ask you a long time.", "알료샤, 오래전부터 당신한테 묻고 싶었어요."], ["A week ago he suddenly told me that Ivan was in love with Katerina, because he often goes to see her.", "일주일 전에 그이가 갑자기 나한테 이반이 카테리나를 사랑한다고 말했어요, 자주 그 여자를 만나러 간다면서요."], ["Did he tell me the truth or not?", "그이가 나한테 사실을 말한 건가요, 아닌가요?"], ["Tell me, on your conscience, tell me the worst.”", "말해 봐요, 양심을 걸고, 최악의 것을 말해 줘요.\""]], [["“I won’t tell you a lie.", "\"거짓말은 하지 않을게요."], ["Ivan is not in love with Katerina Ivanovna, I think.”", "내 생각에 이반은 카테리나 이바노브나를 사랑하지 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s what I thought!", "\"오, 나도 그렇게 생각했어요!"], ["He is lying to me, shameless deceiver, that’s what it is!", "그이가 나한테 거짓말을 한 거예요, 뻔뻔한 사기꾼 같으니, 바로 그거예요!"], ["And he was jealous of me just now, so as to put the blame on me afterwards.", "그리고 방금 나를 질투한 것도, 나중에 죄를 나한테 뒤집어씌우려는 거였어요."], ["He is stupid, he can’t disguise what he is doing; he is so open, you know....", "어리석은 사람이라 자기가 하는 짓을 숨기지도 못해요. 워낙 속이 다 들여다보이는 사람이거든요, 알죠……"], ["But I’ll give it to him, I’ll give it to him!", "하지만 내가 본때를 보여 주겠어요, 본때를 보여 주겠다고요!"], ["‘You believe I did it,’ he said.", "'당신은 내가 그랬다고 믿잖아,' 그러더라고요."], ["He said that to me, to me.", "나한테, 나한테 그런 말을 했어요."], ["He reproached me with that!", "나를 그걸로 나무라다니!"], ["God forgive him!", "하느님이 그이를 용서하시길!"], ["You wait, I’ll make it hot for Katerina at the trial!", "두고 봐요, 재판에서 카테리나를 혼쭐을 내 주겠어요!"], ["I’ll just say a word then ...", "그때 한마디만 하면……"], ["I’ll tell everything then!”", "그때 다 까발릴 거예요!\""]], [["And again she cried bitterly.", "그러고는 그녀가 다시 쓰라리게 울었다."]], [["“This I can tell you for certain, Grushenka,”", "\"이것만은 확실히 말해 줄 수 있어요, 그루셴카.\""], ["Alyosha said, getting up.", "알료샤가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“First, that he loves you, loves you more than any one in the world, and you only, believe me. I know.", "\"첫째, 그분은 당신을 사랑해요, 세상 누구보다도 당신을 사랑하고, 오직 당신만을 사랑해요, 내 말을 믿어요."], ["I do know.", "나는 알아요."], ["The second thing is that I don’t want to worm his secret out of him, but if he’ll tell me of himself to-day, I shall tell him straight out that I have promised to tell you.", "정말로 알아요. 둘째는, 나는 그분에게서 비밀을 캐낼 생각은 없지만, 만일 그분이 오늘 자기 입으로 말해 준다면, 나는 당신한테 전하기로 약속했다고 그분한테 솔직히 말하겠어요."], ["Then I’ll come to you to-day, and tell you.", "그러고 나서 오늘 당신한테 와서 말해 줄게요."], ["Only ...", "다만……"], ["I fancy ...", "내 생각엔……"], ["Katerina Ivanovna has nothing to do with it, and that the secret is about something else.", "카테리나 이바노브나와는 아무 상관이 없고, 그 비밀은 뭔가 다른 일에 관한 거예요."], ["That’s certain.", "그건 분명해요."], ["It isn’t likely it’s about Katerina Ivanovna, it seems to me.", "내가 보기엔 카테리나 이바노브나에 관한 일일 것 같지가 않아요."], ["Good-by for now.”", "그럼 이만 가 볼게요.\""]], [["Alyosha shook hands with her.", "알료샤는 그녀와 악수했다."], ["Grushenka was still crying.", "그루셴카는 여전히 울고 있었다."], ["He saw that she put little faith in his consolation, but she was better for having had her sorrow out, for having spoken of it.", "그는 그녀가 자신의 위로를 거의 믿지 않는다는 것을 알았으나, 그래도 슬픔을 다 털어놓고 그것을 입 밖에 낸 덕에 그녀의 마음은 한결 나아져 있었다."], ["He was sorry to leave her in such a state of mind, but he was in haste.", "그런 심정의 그녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 그는 안타까웠으나, 그는 마음이 급했다."], ["He had a great many things to do still.", "아직 해야 할 일이 산더미처럼 많았던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter II. The Injured Foot", "# Chapter II. The Injured Foot"]], [["The first of these things was at the house of Madame Hohlakov, and he hurried there to get it over as quickly as possible and not be too late for Mitya.", "그 첫 번째 일은 호흘라코바 부인의 집에서 있을 터였고, 그는 미탸에게 너무 늦지 않으려고 가능한 한 빨리 끝내 버릴 작정으로 그리로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Madame Hohlakov had been slightly ailing for the last three weeks: her foot had for some reason swollen up, and though she was not in bed, she lay all day half-reclining on the couch in her boudoir, in a fascinating but decorous déshabillé.", "호흘라코바 부인은 지난 삼 주 동안 가벼운 병을 앓고 있었으니, 무슨 까닭에서인지 발이 부어올라, 자리보전을 하지는 않았으나 종일토록 자기 규방의 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기대어 누운 채로, 매혹적이면서도 단정한 실내복 차림으로 지내고 있었다."], ["Alyosha had once noted with innocent amusement that, in spite of her illness, Madame Hohlakov had begun to be rather dressy—top-knots, ribbons, loose wrappers, had made their appearance, and he had an inkling of the reason, though he dismissed such ideas from his mind as frivolous.", "알료샤는 언젠가 천진한 재미를 느끼며 알아챈 적이 있었는데, 호흘라코바 부인이 병중임에도 불구하고 제법 멋을 부리기 시작했다는 것이니—머리 위로 틀어 올린 장식이며 리본이며 헐렁한 실내 가운 따위가 등장했던 것이라, 그는 그 까닭을 어렴풋이 짐작하면서도 그런 생각들은 경박한 것이라 여겨 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["During the last two months the young official, Perhotin, had become a regular visitor at the house.", "지난 두 달 동안 젊은 관리 페르호틴이 이 집의 단골손님이 되어 있었다."]], [["Alyosha had not called for four days and he was in haste to go straight to Lise, as it was with her he had to speak, for Lise had sent a maid to him the previous day, specially asking him to come to her “about something very important,”", "알료샤는 나흘 동안 발걸음을 하지 않았던 터라 곧장 리즈에게로 가려고 서둘렀으니, 그가 이야기를 나눠야 할 상대는 바로 그녀였기 때문이었다. 리즈가 전날 그에게 하녀를 보내어 특별히 \"아주 중요한 일로\""], ["a request which, for certain reasons, had interest for Alyosha.", "찾아와 달라고 부탁했던 것인데, 그 청은 어떤 이유에서인지 알료샤의 관심을 끌었다."], ["But while the maid went to take his name in to Lise, Madame Hohlakov heard of his arrival from some one, and immediately sent to beg him to come to her “just for one minute.”", "그러나 하녀가 그의 이름을 리즈에게 전하러 간 사이에, 호흘라코바 부인이 누군가에게서 그가 왔다는 소식을 듣고는 즉시 사람을 보내어 \"잠깐만이라도\" 자기에게 들러 달라고 청했다."], ["Alyosha reflected that it was better to accede to the mamma’s request, or else she would be sending down to Lise’s room every minute that he was there.", "알료샤는 어머니 쪽의 청을 들어주는 편이 낫겠다고 판단했으니, 그렇지 않으면 그가 머무는 내내 매 순간 리즈의 방으로 사람을 내려보낼 것이기 때문이었다."], ["Madame Hohlakov was lying on a couch.", "호흘라코바 부인은 긴 의자에 누워 있었다."], ["She was particularly smartly dressed and was evidently in a state of extreme nervous excitement.", "그녀는 유난히 맵시 있게 차려입고 있었고 누가 보아도 극도의 신경 흥분 상태에 빠져 있었다."], ["She greeted Alyosha with cries of rapture.", "그녀는 환희에 찬 외침으로 알료샤를 맞이했다."]], [["“It’s ages, ages, perfect ages since I’ve seen you!", "\"오래, 정말 오래, 한참 만이로군요, 당신을 못 본 지!"], ["It’s a whole week—only think of it!", "꼬박 일주일이에요—생각 좀 해 보세요!"], ["Ah, but you were here only four days ago, on Wednesday.", "아니, 그런데 당신은 나흘 전, 수요일에 여기 오셨던가요."], ["You have come to see Lise.", "리즈를 보러 오신 거지요."], ["I’m sure you meant to slip into her room on tiptoe, without my hearing you.", "분명 내가 못 듣게 발끝으로 살금살금 그 애 방으로 빠져나갈 작정이셨을 거예요."], ["My dear, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, if you only knew how worried I am about her!", "사랑하고 또 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 그 애 때문에 얼마나 애를 태우는지 당신이 알기만 한다면!"], ["But of that later, though that’s the most important thing, of that later.", "하지만 그건 나중에, 그게 제일 중요한 일이긴 해도, 그건 나중에요."], ["Dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, I trust you implicitly with my Lise.", "사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 내 리즈를 당신께 온전히 믿고 맡기겠어요."], ["Since the death of Father Zossima—God rest his soul!” (she crossed herself)—“I look upon you as a monk, though you look charming in your new suit.", "조시마 장로님이 돌아가신 뒤로—그분의 영혼에 평안이 깃들기를!\"(그녀는 성호를 그었다)\"—나는 당신을 수도자처럼 여기고 있어요, 그 새 옷을 입으니 참 매력적이긴 하지만요."], ["Where did you find such a tailor in these parts?", "이런 시골에서 어디서 그런 재단사를 찾으셨어요?"], ["No, no, that’s not the chief thing—of that later.", "아니, 아니에요, 그게 중요한 게 아니지요—그건 나중에요."], ["Forgive me for sometimes calling you Alyosha; an old woman like me may take liberties,”", "내가 가끔 당신을 알료샤라고 부르는 걸 용서해 줘요. 나처럼 늙은 여자야 좀 허물없이 굴어도 되지 않겠어요.\""], ["she smiled coquettishly; “but that will do later, too.", "그녀는 교태 어린 미소를 지었다. \"하지만 그것도 나중에 하기로 해요."], ["The important thing is that I shouldn’t forget what is important.", "중요한 건 내가 중요한 것을 잊지 말아야 한다는 거예요."], ["Please remind me of it yourself.", "부디 당신이 직접 일깨워 줘요."], ["As soon as my tongue runs away with me, you just say ‘the important thing?’ Ach!", "내 혀가 마구 달아나기 시작하거든, 그냥 '중요한 일은요?' 하고 말해 줘요."], ["how do I know now what is of most importance?", "아아! 지금 무엇이 가장 중요한지 내가 어찌 알겠어요?"], ["Ever since Lise took back her promise—her childish promise, Alexey Fyodorovitch—to marry you, you’ve realized, of course, that it was only the playful fancy of a sick child who had been so long confined to her chair—thank God, she can walk now!...", "리즈가 당신과 결혼하겠다던 약속을—그 애다운 약속이었지요, 알렉세이 표도로비치—거두어들인 뒤로, 당신도 물론 깨달으셨겠지만, 그건 그토록 오래 의자에 매여 있던 병든 아이의 장난스러운 공상에 지나지 않았던 거예요—다행히도, 이제 그 애는 걸을 수 있어요!…"], ["that new doctor Katya sent for from Moscow for your unhappy brother, who will to-morrow—But why speak of to-morrow?", "카탸가 당신의 불행한 형님을 위해 모스크바에서 불러온 그 새 의사 말이에요, 내일이면—아니, 내일 얘기를 왜 하겠어요?"], ["I am ready to die at the very thought of to-morrow.", "나는 내일을 생각하기만 해도 죽을 것만 같아요."], ["Ready to die of curiosity....", "궁금해서 죽을 지경이에요…."], ["That doctor was with us yesterday and saw Lise....", "그 의사가 어제 우리 집에 와서 리즈를 봤어요…."], ["I paid him fifty roubles for the visit.", "그 진찰비로 오십 루블을 줬지요."], ["But that’s not the point, that’s not the point again.", "하지만 그게 요점이 아니에요, 또 그게 요점이 아니라니까요."], ["You see, I’m mixing everything up.", "보세요, 나는 모든 걸 뒤죽박죽 섞고 있어요."], ["I am in such a hurry.", "너무 마음이 급해서요."], ["Why am I in a hurry?", "내가 왜 이리 서두를까요?"], ["I don’t understand.", "모르겠어요."], ["It’s awful how I seem growing unable to understand anything.", "무얼 도무지 이해할 수 없게 되어 가는 것 같아서 끔찍해요."], ["Everything seems mixed up in a sort of tangle.", "모든 게 일종의 엉킨 실타래처럼 뒤섞여 버린 것 같아요."], ["I am afraid you are so bored you will jump up and run away, and that will be all I shall see of you.", "당신이 너무 지루해서 벌떡 일어나 달아나 버릴까 봐 겁이 나요, 그러면 내가 당신을 보는 것도 그게 마지막일 테니까요."], ["Goodness!", "어머나!"], ["Why are we sitting here and no coffee?", "우리가 왜 여기 앉아 있는데 커피가 없을까요?"], ["Yulia, Glafira, coffee!”", "율리야, 글라피라, 커피!\""]], [["Alyosha made haste to thank her, and said that he had only just had coffee.", "알료샤는 서둘러 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고는, 방금 막 커피를 마시고 왔다고 말했다."]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서요?\""]], [["“At Agrafena Alexandrovna’s.”", "\"아그라페나 알렉산드로브나 댁에서요.\""]], [["“At ...", "\"그…"], ["at that woman’s?", "그 여자 집에서요?"], ["Ah, it’s she has brought ruin on every one.", "아아, 그 여자가 모든 사람을 파멸로 몰아넣었지요."], ["I know nothing about it though.", "하긴 나는 그 일에 대해선 아무것도 몰라요."], ["They say she has become a saint, though it’s rather late in the day.", "사람들 말로는 그 여자가 성녀가 됐다더군요, 좀 때늦은 감이 있긴 하지만요."], ["She had better have done it before.", "진작에 그랬으면 좋았을걸."], ["What use is it now?", "이제 와서 무슨 소용이겠어요?"], ["Hush, hush, Alexey Fyodorovitch, for I have so much to say to you that I am afraid I shall tell you nothing.", "쉿, 쉿, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 당신에게 할 말이 너무 많아서 결국엔 아무것도 못 할까 봐 겁이 나요."], ["This awful trial ...", "이 끔찍한 재판이…."], ["I shall certainly go, I am making arrangements.", "나는 꼭 갈 거예요, 지금 준비를 하고 있어요."], ["I shall be carried there in my chair; besides I can sit up.", "의자에 실려서라도 그리로 갈 거예요. 게다가 나는 앉아 있을 수도 있어요."], ["I shall have people with me.", "사람들도 데려갈 거고요."], ["And, you know, I am a witness.", "그리고, 아시겠지만, 나는 증인이거든요."], ["How shall I speak, how shall I speak?", "내가 어떻게 말해야 하지요, 어떻게 말해야 하지요?"], ["I don’t know what I shall say.", "무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["One has to take an oath, hasn’t one?”", "선서를 해야 하지요, 그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["“Yes; but I don’t think you will be able to go.”", "\"예. 하지만 부인께서 가실 수 있을 것 같지는 않은데요.\""]], [["“I can sit up.", "\"나는 앉아 있을 수 있어요."], ["Ah, you put me out! Ah!", "아아, 당신이 날 헷갈리게 하잖아요!"], ["this trial, this savage act, and then they are all going to Siberia, some are getting married, and all this so quickly, so quickly, everything’s changing, and at last—nothing.", "아아! 이 재판, 이 야만스러운 짓, 그러고는 모두 시베리아로 가고, 누구는 결혼을 하고, 이 모든 게 이리도 빨리, 이리도 빨리, 모든 게 변해 버리고, 결국엔—아무것도 남지 않아요."], ["All grow old and have death to look forward to.", "다들 늙어 가고 죽음만을 앞두게 되지요."], ["Well, so be it! I am weary.", "뭐, 그렇다면 그러라죠!"], ["This Katya, cette charmante personne, has disappointed all my hopes.", "나는 지쳤어요. 이 카탸, 그 사랑스러운 사람이, 내 모든 희망을 저버렸어요."], ["Now she is going to follow one of your brothers to Siberia, and your other brother is going to follow her, and will live in the nearest town, and they will all torment one another.", "이제 그 애는 당신의 형님들 중 하나를 따라 시베리아로 가려 하고, 또 다른 형님은 그 애를 따라가 가장 가까운 읍내에 살려 한다니, 그러면 다들 서로를 괴롭히게 될 거예요."], ["It drives me out of my mind.", "그 생각만 하면 미칠 것만 같아요."], ["Worst of all—the publicity.", "무엇보다도—그 세상의 입방아 말이에요."], ["The story has been told a million times over in all the papers in Moscow and Petersburg.", "그 이야기가 모스크바와 페테르부르크의 온갖 신문에 백만 번도 더 실렸어요. 아아!"], ["Ah! yes, would you believe it, there’s a paragraph that I was ‘a dear friend’ of your brother’s ——, I can’t repeat the horrid word.", "그래, 당신은 믿을 수 있겠어요, 내가 당신 형님의 '정다운 벗'이었다는 구절이 실렸다니까요——그 끔찍한 단어는 차마 입에 담을 수가 없어요."], ["Just fancy, just fancy!”", "상상이나 해 보세요, 상상이나!\""]], [["“Impossible!", "\"그럴 리가요!"], ["Where was the paragraph?", "그 구절이 어디 실렸지요?"], ["What did it say?”", "뭐라고 쓰여 있었어요?\""]], [["“I’ll show you directly.", "\"곧 보여 드릴게요."], ["I got the paper and read it yesterday.", "어제 그 신문을 구해서 읽었어요."], ["Here, in the Petersburg paper Gossip.", "여기, 페테르부르크 신문 〈소문〉이에요."], ["The paper began coming out this year.", "그 신문이 올해부터 나오기 시작했지요."], ["I am awfully fond of gossip, and I take it in, and now it pays me out—this is what gossip comes to!", "나는 소문이라면 무척 좋아해서 그 신문을 구독하는데, 이제 와서 그것이 내게 앙갚음을 하는군요—소문이란 게 결국 이 꼴이 되는 거예요!"], ["Here it is, here, this passage.", "여기 있어요, 여기, 이 대목이에요."], ["Read it.”", "읽어 보세요.\""]], [["And she handed Alyosha a sheet of newspaper which had been under her pillow.", "그러면서 그녀는 베개 밑에 넣어 두었던 신문 한 장을 알료샤에게 건넸다."]], [["It was not exactly that she was upset, she seemed overwhelmed and perhaps everything really was mixed up in a tangle in her head.", "그녀는 정확히 말해 흥분했다기보다는 압도당한 듯이 보였고, 어쩌면 정말로 머릿속의 모든 것이 한데 뒤엉켜 있는지도 몰랐다."], ["The paragraph was very typical, and must have been a great shock to her, but, fortunately perhaps, she was unable to keep her mind fixed on any one subject at that moment, and so might race off in a minute to something else and quite forget the newspaper.", "그 구절은 아주 전형적인 것이라 그녀에게는 큰 충격이었을 게 분명하지만, 어쩌면 다행스럽게도 그녀는 그 순간 어느 한 가지 주제에 정신을 붙들어 둘 수가 없었으니, 그래서 잠깐 사이에 다른 것으로 달아나 버려 신문 따위는 까맣게 잊어버릴 수도 있는 것이었다."]], [["Alyosha was well aware that the story of the terrible case had spread all over Russia.", "알료샤는 그 끔찍한 사건의 이야기가 온 러시아에 퍼졌다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["And, good heavens!", "그리고, 오 하느님!"], ["what wild rumors about his brother, about the Karamazovs, and about himself he had read in the course of those two months, among other equally credible items!", "자기 형에 관하여, 카라마조프 집안에 관하여, 또 자기 자신에 관하여, 그 두 달 동안 그가 읽은 그 밖의 똑같이 믿을 만한 항목들 가운데 얼마나 터무니없는 소문들이 떠돌았던가!"], ["One paper had even stated that he had gone into a monastery and become a monk, in horror at his brother’s crime.", "어느 신문은 심지어 그가 형의 범죄에 경악한 나머지 수도원에 들어가 수도자가 되었다고까지 했다."], ["Another contradicted this, and stated that he and his elder, Father Zossima, had broken into the monastery chest and “made tracks from the monastery.”", "또 다른 신문은 이를 반박하며, 그와 그의 장로인 조시마 신부가 수도원의 금고를 부수고 들어가 \"수도원에서 줄행랑을 쳤다\"고 했다."], ["The present paragraph in the paper Gossip was under the heading, “The Karamazov Case at Skotoprigonyevsk.” (That, alas!", "〈소문〉지에 실린 이번 구절은 \"스코토프리고니옙스크의 카라마조프 사건\"이라는 표제 아래 실려 있었다.(아아, 그것이 우리 작은 읍의 이름이었다."], ["was the name of our little town. I had hitherto kept it concealed.) It was brief, and Madame Hohlakov was not directly mentioned in it.", "나는 지금껏 그 이름을 숨겨 왔다.) 그것은 짤막했고, 호흘라코바 부인이 직접 언급되지는 않았다."], ["No names appeared, in fact.", "사실 어떤 이름도 등장하지 않았다."], ["It was merely stated that the criminal, whose approaching trial was making such a sensation—retired army captain, an idle swaggerer, and reactionary bully—was continually involved in amorous intrigues, and particularly popular with certain ladies “who were pining in solitude.”", "다만 이렇게만 적혀 있었으니, 곧 임박한 재판으로 그토록 큰 화제를 불러일으키고 있는 그 범인—퇴역한 육군 대위로서, 빈둥거리는 허풍쟁이이자 반동적인 망나니인 그자가—끊임없이 연애 사건에 휘말려 들었고, 특히 \"고독 속에서 시들어 가던\" 어떤 부인들에게 유달리 인기가 있었다는 것이었다."], ["One such lady, a pining widow, who tried to seem young though she had a grown-up daughter, was so fascinated by him that only two hours before the crime she offered him three thousand roubles, on condition that he would elope with her to the gold mines.", "그런 부인들 가운데 하나, 곧 시들어 가던 어느 과부는, 다 자란 딸이 있으면서도 젊어 보이려 애쓰는 여자였는데, 그자에게 어찌나 홀딱 빠졌던지 범행이 있기 불과 두 시간 전에 자기와 함께 금광으로 달아나 준다면 삼천 루블을 주겠다고 그자에게 제안했다는 것이었다."], ["But the criminal, counting on escaping punishment, had preferred to murder his father to get the three thousand rather than go off to Siberia with the middle-aged charms of his pining lady.", "그러나 그 범인은 처벌을 모면할 속셈으로, 시들어 가던 그 부인의 중년의 매력과 함께 시베리아로 떠나느니 차라리 삼천 루블을 손에 넣으려고 제 아비를 죽이는 편을 택했다는 것이었다."], ["This playful paragraph finished, of course, with an outburst of generous indignation at the wickedness of parricide and at the lately abolished institution of serfdom.", "이 장난기 어린 구절은 물론, 친부 살해의 사악함과 근래 폐지된 농노제에 대한 너그러운 분노의 폭발로 마무리되었다."], ["Reading it with curiosity, Alyosha folded up the paper and handed it back to Madame Hohlakov.", "호기심에 차서 그것을 읽고 난 알료샤는 신문을 접어 호흘라코바 부인에게 도로 건넸다."]], [["“Well, that must be me,”", "\"자, 그게 분명 나일 거예요.\""], ["she hurried on again.", "그녀가 다시 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["“Of course I am meant.", "\"물론 나를 가리키는 거예요."], ["Scarcely more than an hour before, I suggested gold mines to him, and here they talk of ‘middle-aged charms’ as though that were my motive!", "한 시간 남짓밖에 안 됐어요, 내가 그이에게 금광 얘기를 꺼낸 게, 그런데 여기서는 그게 마치 내 속셈이었던 것처럼 '중년의 매력'이라고 떠들어 대는군요!"], ["He writes that out of spite!", "그이는 앙심을 품고 그걸 쓴 거예요!"], ["God Almighty forgive him for the middle-aged charms, as I forgive him!", "전능하신 하느님께서 그 중년의 매력 운운한 것을 용서해 주시길, 나도 그이를 용서하듯이!"], ["You know it’s— Do you know who it is?", "당신도 알겠지만, 그게—그게 누군지 아세요?"], ["It’s your friend Rakitin.”", "당신 친구 라키틴이에요.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"아마도요.\""], ["said Alyosha, “though I’ve heard nothing about it.”", "알료샤가 말했다. \"하긴 저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 듣지 못했지만요.\""]], [["“It’s he, it’s he!", "\"그자예요, 그자라니까요!"], ["No ‘perhaps’ about it.", "'아마도'랄 게 없어요."], ["You know I turned him out of the house....", "알다시피 내가 그자를 집에서 쫓아냈잖아요…."], ["You know all that story, don’t you?”", "당신도 그 이야기를 다 알고 있지요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“I know that you asked him not to visit you for the future, but why it was, I haven’t heard ...", "\"부인께서 그에게 앞으로는 찾아오지 말라고 하셨다는 건 알지만, 무슨 까닭에서였는지는 듣지 못했습니다…"], ["from you, at least.”", "적어도 부인에게서는요.\""]], [["“Ah, then you’ve heard it from him!", "\"아, 그렇다면 그자한테서 들었군요!"], ["He abuses me, I suppose, abuses me dreadfully?”", "그자가 나를 헐뜯었겠지요, 끔찍하게 헐뜯었겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes, he does; but then he abuses every one.", "\"예, 그러긴 합니다. 하지만 그는 누구든 다 헐뜯으니까요."], ["But why you’ve given him up I haven’t heard from him either.", "그래도 부인께서 왜 그와 인연을 끊으셨는지는 그에게서도 듣지 못했습니다."], ["I meet him very seldom now, indeed.", "실은 요즘 그를 아주 가끔밖에 만나지 않아요."], ["We are not friends.”", "우리는 친구 사이가 아니거든요.\""]], [["“Well, then, I’ll tell you all about it.", "\"그럼, 좋아요, 내가 다 이야기해 드리지요."], ["There’s no help for it, I’ll confess, for there is one point in which I was perhaps to blame.", "어쩔 수 없는 일이니, 고백할게요, 어쩌면 내가 잘못한 점이 한 가지 있었으니까요."], ["Only a little, little point, so little that perhaps it doesn’t count.", "다만 아주 작은, 작은 점이라, 너무 작아서 어쩌면 셈에도 안 들어갈 정도예요. 보세요, 사랑하는 나의 도련님—\""], ["You see, my dear boy”—Madame Hohlakov suddenly looked arch and a charming, though enigmatic, smile played about her lips—“you see, I suspect ...", "호흘라코바 부인은 갑자기 짓궂은 표정을 짓더니 매혹적이면서도 알 수 없는 미소를 입가에 띄웠다. \"—보세요, 내 생각엔 말이에요…"], ["You must forgive me, Alyosha.", "용서해 줘야 해요, 알료샤."], ["I am like a mother to you....", "나는 당신에게 어머니나 다름없으니까요…."], ["No, no; quite the contrary.", "아니, 아니에요, 정반대예요."], ["I speak to you now as though you were my father—mother’s quite out of place.", "지금 나는 당신을 마치 내 아버지인 양 여기고 말하는 거예요—어머니라는 건 영 어울리지 않아요."], ["Well, it’s as though I were confessing to Father Zossima, that’s just it.", "자, 그러니까 마치 내가 조시마 신부님께 고해를 하는 것 같다고 해 두지요, 바로 그거예요."], ["I called you a monk just now.", "아까 내가 당신을 수도자라고 불렀잖아요."], ["Well, that poor young man, your friend, Rakitin (Mercy on us!", "자, 그 가엾은 젊은이, 당신 친구 라키틴이(저런, 가엾어라!"], ["I can’t be angry with him.", "나는 그자한테 화를 낼 수가 없네요."], ["I feel cross, but not very), that frivolous young man, would you believe it, seems to have taken it into his head to fall in love with me.", "부아가 치밀긴 해도 그리 심하진 않아요), 그 경박한 젊은이가, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 나에게 반해 버릴 생각을 머릿속에 품은 모양이에요."], ["I only noticed it later.", "나중에야 그걸 알아챘지요."], ["At first—a month ago—he only began to come oftener to see me, almost every day; though, of course, we were acquainted before.", "처음에는—한 달 전이었어요—그저 나를 더 자주 보러 오기 시작했어요, 거의 매일같이요. 물론 우리는 전부터 아는 사이였지만요."], ["I knew nothing about it ...", "나는 아무것도 몰랐어요…"], ["and suddenly it dawned upon me, and I began to notice things with surprise.", "그러다 갑자기 깨달았고, 놀라면서 이것저것 눈여겨보기 시작했지요."], ["You know, two months ago, that modest, charming, excellent young man, Pyotr Ilyitch Perhotin, who’s in the service here, began to be a regular visitor at the house.", "알다시피, 두 달 전에 그 점잖고 매력적이고 훌륭한 젊은이, 여기서 관직에 있는 표트르 일리치 페르호틴이 이 집의 단골손님이 되기 시작했어요."], ["You met him here ever so many times yourself.", "당신도 여기서 그를 여러 번 만났잖아요."], ["And he is an excellent, earnest young man, isn’t he?", "그는 훌륭하고 진지한 젊은이지요, 안 그래요?"], ["He comes once every three days, not every day (though I should be glad to see him every day), and always so well dressed.", "그는 사흘에 한 번씩 와요, 매일은 아니고요(나야 매일 봐도 좋겠지만요), 그리고 늘 그렇게 잘 차려입고 오지요."], ["Altogether, I love young people, Alyosha, talented, modest, like you, and he has almost the mind of a statesman, he talks so charmingly, and I shall certainly, certainly try and get promotion for him.", "대체로 나는 젊은 사람들을 좋아해요, 알료샤, 당신처럼 재능 있고 겸손한 사람들 말이에요, 그리고 그는 거의 정치가다운 두뇌를 지녔어요, 어찌나 매력적으로 말을 하는지, 나는 꼭, 반드시 그가 승진하도록 힘써 볼 거예요."], ["He is a future diplomat.", "그는 장래의 외교관이에요."], ["On that awful day he almost saved me from death by coming in the night.", "그 끔찍한 날에 그는 밤중에 찾아와 줘서 나를 죽음에서 거의 구해 준 셈이지요."], ["And your friend Rakitin comes in such boots, and always stretches them out on the carpet....", "그런데 당신 친구 라키틴은 그런 장화를 신고 와서는, 늘 그걸 양탄자 위에 쭉 뻗어 놓는다니까요…."], ["He began hinting at his feelings, in fact, and one day, as he was going, he squeezed my hand terribly hard.", "어쨌든 그자는 자기 속마음을 넌지시 비치기 시작했고, 어느 날 돌아가려다가 내 손을 어찌나 세게 꽉 쥐던지요."], ["My foot began to swell directly after he pressed my hand like that.", "그자가 그렇게 내 손을 누른 직후에 곧바로 내 발이 부어오르기 시작했어요."], ["He had met Pyotr Ilyitch here before, and would you believe it, he is always gibing at him, growling at him, for some reason.", "그자는 전에도 여기서 표트르 일리치를 만난 적이 있었는데, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 무슨 까닭에선지 늘 그를 빈정거리고 으르렁대지 뭐예요."], ["I simply looked at the way they went on together and laughed inwardly.", "나는 그저 그 둘이서 그렇게 하는 꼴을 지켜보면서 속으로 웃었지요."], ["So I was sitting here alone—no, I was laid up then.", "그래서 내가 여기 혼자 앉아 있었는데—아니, 그때는 자리보전을 하고 있었지요."], ["Well, I was lying here alone and suddenly Rakitin comes in, and only fancy!", "자, 내가 여기 혼자 누워 있는데 갑자기 라키틴이 들어와서는, 글쎄 상상해 보세요!"], ["brought me some verses of his own composition—a short poem, on my bad foot: that is, he described my foot in a poem.", "자기가 직접 지은 시를 가져왔지 뭐예요—내 아픈 발에 관한 짤막한 시였어요. 그러니까 내 발을 시로 묘사한 거예요."], ["Wait a minute—how did it go?", "잠깐만요—어떻게 됐더라?"]], [["A captivating little foot.", "매혹적인 작은 발이여."]], [["It began somehow like that.", "그렇게 시작했던 것 같아요."], ["I can never remember poetry.", "나는 도통 시를 외우질 못해요."], ["I’ve got it here.", "여기 가지고 있어요."], ["I’ll show it to you later.", "나중에 보여 드릴게요."], ["But it’s a charming thing—charming; and, you know, it’s not only about the foot, it had a good moral, too, a charming idea, only I’ve forgotten it; in fact, it was just the thing for an album.", "하지만 매력적인 거예요—매력적이지요. 그리고, 알다시피, 발에 관한 것만이 아니라 훌륭한 교훈도 담겨 있었어요, 매력적인 발상이었는데, 다만 내가 잊어버렸어요."], ["So, of course, I thanked him, and he was evidently flattered.", "사실 꼭 앨범에나 어울릴 만한 것이었지요. 그래서 물론 나는 고맙다고 했고, 그자는 보아하니 우쭐해진 모양이었어요."], ["I’d hardly had time to thank him when in comes Pyotr Ilyitch, and Rakitin suddenly looked as black as night.", "내가 미처 고마움을 다 표하기도 전에 표트르 일리치가 들어왔는데, 그 순간 라키틴의 얼굴이 갑자기 밤처럼 시커메지지 뭐예요."], ["I could see that Pyotr Ilyitch was in the way, for Rakitin certainly wanted to say something after giving me the verses.", "표트르 일리치가 거치적거린다는 걸 나는 알 수 있었어요, 라키틴이 시를 건네준 뒤에 분명 무슨 말을 하고 싶어 했으니까요."], ["I had a presentiment of it; but Pyotr Ilyitch came in.", "나는 그걸 예감했지요. 그런데 표트르 일리치가 들어온 거예요."], ["I showed Pyotr Ilyitch the verses and didn’t say who was the author.", "나는 표트르 일리치에게 그 시를 보여 주면서 누가 쓴 것인지는 말하지 않았어요."], ["But I am convinced that he guessed, though he won’t own it to this day, and declares he had no idea.", "하지만 나는 그가 알아챘다고 확신해요, 비록 오늘날까지도 그걸 인정하지 않고, 전혀 짐작도 못 했다고 우기지만요. 하지만 그는 일부러 그러는 거예요."], ["But he says that on purpose.", "표트르 일리치는 곧장 웃기 시작하더니 그것을 헐뜯기 시작했어요."], ["Pyotr Ilyitch began to laugh at once, and fell to criticizing it.", "'형편없는 엉터리 시로군요' 하고 그가 말하더군요, '어느 신학생이 쓴 게 분명해요'"], ["‘Wretched doggerel,’ he said they were, ‘some divinity student must have written them,’ and with such vehemence, such vehemence!", "하면서, 어찌나 격하게, 어찌나 격하게 그러던지요! 그러자 당신 친구는 웃기는커녕 버럭 화를 내며 발끈했지요."], ["Then, instead of laughing, your friend flew into a rage.", "'저런!' 나는 생각했어요, '저러다 서로 달려들겠어.'"], ["‘Good gracious!’ I thought, ‘they’ll fly at each other.’ ‘It was I who wrote them,’ said he.", "'그건 내가 쓴 거요' 하고 그자가 말했어요."], ["‘I wrote them as a joke,’ he said, ‘for I think it degrading to write verses....", "'농담 삼아 쓴 거요' 하고 말이지요, '나는 시 따위 짓는 게 천박하다고 생각하니까…."], ["But they are good poetry.", "하지만 그건 좋은 시요."], ["They want to put a monument to your Pushkin for writing about women’s feet, while I wrote with a moral purpose, and you,’ said he, ‘are an advocate of serfdom.", "사람들이 여자 발에 대해 썼다고 당신네 푸시킨한테는 기념비를 세워 주려 하면서, 나는 교훈적인 목적으로 썼는데도, 당신은' 하고 그자가 말했어요, '농노제 옹호자요."], ["You’ve no humane ideas,’ said he.", "당신에겐 인도주의적인 사상이라곤 없소' 하고 말이에요."], ["‘You have no modern enlightened feelings, you are uninfluenced by progress, you are a mere official,’ he said, ‘and you take bribes.’ Then I began screaming and imploring them.", "'당신에겐 현대의 계몽된 감정이 없고, 진보의 영향도 받지 않았으며, 그저 한낱 관리에 불과하오' 하고 그자가 말했어요, '게다가 당신은 뇌물이나 받아먹지.'"], ["And, you know, Pyotr Ilyitch is anything but a coward.", "그래서 나는 비명을 지르며 두 사람에게 애원하기 시작했지요."], ["He at once took up the most gentlemanly tone, looked at him sarcastically, listened, and apologized.", "그런데, 알다시피, 표트르 일리치는 겁쟁이라곤 도무지 아니에요. 그는 즉시 더없이 신사다운 어조를 취하고는, 그자를 비웃듯 바라보면서, 가만히 듣다가 사과를 했어요."], ["‘I’d no idea,’ said he.", "'몰랐습니다' 하고 그가 말했어요."], ["‘I shouldn’t have said it, if I had known.", "'알았더라면 그런 말을 하지 않았을 겁니다. 칭찬을 했겠지요."], ["I should have praised it.", "시인이란 다들 그렇게 신경이 예민하니까요'"], ["Poets are all so irritable,’ he said.", "하고 말이에요. 한마디로, 그는 더없이 신사다운 어조로 그자를 비웃은 거예요."], ["In short, he laughed at him under cover of the most gentlemanly tone.", "나중에 그는 그게 다 빈정거린 거였다고 내게 설명해 주더군요. 나는 그가 진심인 줄로만 알았는데 말이에요."], ["He explained to me afterwards that it was all sarcastic.", "다만 내가 거기 누워서, 꼭 지금 당신 앞에서처럼, 이런 생각을 했어요, '내 집에서 손님에게 그토록 무례하게 소리를 질러 댄다고 라키틴을 쫓아내는 것이 마땅한 일일까, 아닐까?'"], ["I thought he was in earnest.", "하고요."], ["Only as I lay there, just as before you now, I thought, ‘Would it, or would it not, be the proper thing for me to turn Rakitin out for shouting so rudely at a visitor in my house?’ And, would you believe it, I lay here, shut my eyes, and wondered, would it be the proper thing or not.", "그리고, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 나는 여기 누워서 눈을 감고는, 그게 마땅한 일일까 아닐까 곰곰이 생각했어요. 자꾸자꾸 마음을 졸였고, 심장이 마구 뛰기 시작했는데, 소리를 내질러야 할지 말아야 할지 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었어요."], ["I kept worrying and worrying, and my heart began to beat, and I couldn’t make up my mind whether to make an outcry or not.", "한쪽 목소리는 내게 '말해' 하는 것 같았고, 다른 쪽은 '아니, 말하지 마'"], ["One voice seemed to be telling me, ‘Speak,’ and the other ‘No, don’t speak.’ And no sooner had the second voice said that than I cried out, and fainted.", "하는 것 같았어요. 그런데 그 두 번째 목소리가 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게 나는 비명을 지르고 기절해 버렸지요."], ["Of course, there was a fuss.", "물론 한바탕 소동이 났어요."], ["I got up suddenly and said to Rakitin, ‘It’s painful for me to say it, but I don’t wish to see you in my house again.’ So I turned him out.", "나는 벌떡 일어나서 라키틴에게 말했어요, '이런 말을 하기는 괴롭지만, 다시는 내 집에서 당신을 보고 싶지 않아요.' 그렇게 그자를 쫓아냈지요."], ["Ah!", "아아!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, I know myself I did wrong.", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 내가 잘못했다는 건 나 자신도 알아요."], ["I was putting it on.", "나는 꾸민 거였어요."], ["I wasn’t angry with him at all, really; but I suddenly fancied—that was what did it—that it would be such a fine scene....", "사실은 그자에게 조금도 화가 나지 않았거든요. 하지만 갑자기 그런 생각이 들었어요—바로 그게 일을 그르친 거예요—그게 참 멋진 장면이 될 거라는 생각이요…."], ["And yet, believe me, it was quite natural, for I really shed tears and cried for several days afterwards, and then suddenly, one afternoon, I forgot all about it.", "그런데도, 믿어 주세요, 그건 아주 자연스러운 일이었어요, 정말로 나는 눈물을 흘렸고 그 뒤로 며칠이나 울었으니까요, 그러다 갑자기, 어느 날 오후, 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버렸지요."], ["So it’s a fortnight since he’s been here, and I kept wondering whether he would come again.", "그래서 그자가 여기 오지 않은 지 보름이 됐는데, 나는 그자가 다시 올까 자꾸 궁금해했어요."], ["I wondered even yesterday, then suddenly last night came this Gossip.", "어제도 궁금해했지요, 그런데 갑자기 어젯밤에 이 〈소문〉이 온 거예요."], ["I read it and gasped.", "나는 그걸 읽고 숨이 막혔어요."], ["Who could have written it?", "누가 그걸 썼겠어요?"], ["He must have written it.", "분명 그자가 쓴 거예요."], ["He went home, sat down, wrote it on the spot, sent it, and they put it in.", "집에 가서, 앉아서, 그 자리에서 써서, 보냈고, 신문사에서 그걸 실은 거예요."], ["It was a fortnight ago, you see.", "그게 보름 전이었잖아요, 아시겠어요."], ["But, Alyosha, it’s awful how I keep talking and don’t say what I want to say.", "하지만, 알료샤, 끔찍하게도 나는 자꾸 떠들기만 하고 정작 하고 싶은 말은 하지 못하고 있네요."], ["Ah!", "아아!"], ["the words come of themselves!”", "말들이 제멋대로 쏟아져 나와요!\""]], [["“It’s very important for me to be in time to see my brother to-day,”", "\"오늘은 제때에 형님을 만나는 것이 저로서는 무척 중요합니다.\""], ["Alyosha faltered.", "알료샤가 우물거리며 말했다."]], [["“To be sure, to be sure!", "\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요!"], ["You bring it all back to me.", "당신이 그걸 다시 떠올려 주는군요."], ["Listen, what is an aberration?”", "들어 봐요, 정신착란이라는 게 뭐예요?\""]], [["“What aberration?”", "\"무슨 정신착란 말씀입니까?\""], ["asked Alyosha, wondering.", "알료샤가 의아해하며 물었다."]], [["“In the legal sense.", "\"법률상의 의미로요."], ["An aberration in which everything is pardonable.", "모든 게 용서받을 수 있는 그런 정신착란 말이에요."], ["Whatever you do, you will be acquitted at once.”", "무슨 짓을 했든 즉시 무죄로 풀려나게 되는 거요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you.", "\"내가 이야기해 드리지요."], ["This Katya ...", "이 카탸가… 아아!"], ["Ah! she is a charming, charming creature, only I never can make out who it is she is in love with.", "그 애는 사랑스럽고 또 사랑스러운 사람이에요, 다만 그 애가 대체 누구를 사랑하는지 도무지 알아낼 수가 없어요."], ["She was with me some time ago and I couldn’t get anything out of her.", "얼마 전에 그 애가 나한테 왔었는데 도통 아무것도 캐낼 수가 없었어요."], ["Especially as she won’t talk to me except on the surface now.", "더구나 그 애는 이제 나하고는 겉도는 이야기밖에 안 하니까요."], ["She is always talking about my health and nothing else, and she takes up such a tone with me, too.", "늘 내 건강 얘기만 하고 다른 건 일절 안 해요, 게다가 나한테 그런 어조까지 취한다니까요."], ["I simply said to myself, ‘Well, so be it.", "나는 그저 혼잣말을 했지요, '그래, 그러라지."], ["I don’t care’...", "난 상관 안 해'…"], ["Oh, yes.", "아, 그래요."], ["I was talking of aberration.", "정신착란 얘기를 하고 있었지요."], ["This doctor has come.", "그 의사가 왔어요."], ["You know a doctor has come?", "의사가 왔다는 거 아세요?"], ["Of course, you know it—the one who discovers madmen.", "물론 아시겠지요—미치광이를 가려내는 그 의사 말이에요."], ["You wrote for him.", "당신이 사람을 보내 불렀잖아요."], ["No, it wasn’t you, but Katya.", "아니, 당신이 아니라 카탸였지요."], ["It’s all Katya’s doing.", "다 카탸가 한 일이에요."], ["Well, you see, a man may be sitting perfectly sane and suddenly have an aberration.", "자, 보세요, 사람이 멀쩡하게 제정신으로 앉아 있다가도 갑자기 정신착란을 일으킬 수가 있는 거예요."], ["He may be conscious and know what he is doing and yet be in a state of aberration.", "의식이 또렷하고 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알면서도 정신착란 상태에 빠질 수 있다는 거지요."], ["And there’s no doubt that Dmitri Fyodorovitch was suffering from aberration.", "그리고 드미트리 표도로비치가 정신착란을 앓고 있었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["They found out about aberration as soon as the law courts were reformed.", "사람들이 정신착란이라는 걸 알아낸 건 법원이 개혁되자마자였어요."], ["It’s all the good effect of the reformed law courts.", "다 개혁된 법원의 좋은 효과인 거지요."], ["The doctor has been here and questioned me about that evening, about the gold mines.", "그 의사가 여기 와서 그날 저녁 일을, 금광 얘기를 나한테 캐물었어요."], ["‘How did he seem then?’ he asked me.", "'그때 그가 어떻게 보이던가요?'"], ["He must have been in a state of aberration.", "하고 묻더군요. 그는 분명 정신착란 상태였을 거예요."], ["He came in shouting, ‘Money, money, three thousand!", "그가 들어와서 소리쳤거든요, '돈, 돈, 삼천 루블!"], ["Give me three thousand!’ and then went away and immediately did the murder.", "삼천 루블을 주시오!' 하고요, 그러고는 나가서 곧장 살인을 저질렀지요."], ["‘I don’t want to murder him,’ he said, and he suddenly went and murdered him.", "'나는 그를 죽이고 싶지 않아' 하고 그가 말했는데, 그러고는 갑자기 가서 그를 죽인 거예요."], ["That’s why they’ll acquit him, because he struggled against it and yet he murdered him.”", "그래서 사람들이 그를 무죄로 풀어 줄 거예요, 그가 그것에 맞서 발버둥 쳤는데도 결국 그를 죽였으니까요.\""]], [["“But he didn’t murder him,”", "\"하지만 형님은 아버지를 죽이지 않았습니다.\""], ["Alyosha interrupted rather sharply.", "알료샤가 다소 날카롭게 끼어들었다."], ["He felt more and more sick with anxiety and impatience.", "그는 불안과 초조함으로 점점 더 속이 메스꺼워지고 있었다."]], [["“Yes, I know it was that old man Grigory murdered him.”", "\"그래요, 나도 알아요, 그를 죽인 건 그 늙은이 그리고리였지요.\""]], [["“Grigory?”", "\"그리고리가요?\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, yes; it was Grigory.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그리고리였어요."], ["He lay as Dmitri Fyodorovitch struck him down, and then got up, saw the door open, went in and killed Fyodor Pavlovitch.”", "드미트리 표도로비치가 그를 쳐서 쓰러뜨린 자리에 누워 있다가, 그러고는 일어나, 문이 열려 있는 걸 보고, 안으로 들어가 표도르 파블로비치를 죽인 거예요.\""]], [["“But why, why?”", "\"하지만 왜요, 어째서요?\""]], [["“Suffering from aberration.", "\"정신착란을 앓고 있었으니까요."], ["When he recovered from the blow Dmitri Fyodorovitch gave him on the head, he was suffering from aberration; he went and committed the murder.", "드미트리 표도로비치가 머리를 후려친 그 충격에서 깨어났을 때, 그는 정신착란을 앓고 있었던 거예요. 그래서 가서 살인을 저지른 거지요."], ["As for his saying he didn’t, he very likely doesn’t remember.", "그가 자기는 안 그랬다고 하는 건, 아마 기억을 못 하는 거겠지요."], ["Only, you know, it’ll be better, ever so much better, if Dmitri Fyodorovitch murdered him.", "다만, 알다시피, 드미트리 표도로비치가 그를 죽인 편이 더 나아요, 훨씬 더 나아요."], ["And that’s how it must have been, though I say it was Grigory.", "그리고 그렇게 된 게 분명해요, 비록 내가 그리고리라고 말하긴 하지만요."], ["It certainly was Dmitri Fyodorovitch, and that’s better, ever so much better!", "분명 드미트리 표도로비치였고, 그게 더 나아요, 훨씬 더 나아요! 아아!"], ["Oh! not better that a son should have killed his father, I don’t defend that.", "아들이 제 아비를 죽이는 게 더 낫다는 말은 아니에요, 나는 그걸 두둔하는 게 아니에요."], ["Children ought to honor their parents, and yet it would be better if it were he, as you’d have nothing to cry over then, for he did it when he was unconscious or rather when he was conscious, but did not know what he was doing.", "자식은 마땅히 부모를 공경해야지요, 그래도 그가 그랬다면 더 나을 거예요, 그러면 당신이 슬퍼할 게 없어질 테니까요, 그는 무의식 상태에서, 아니 차라리 의식은 있었으나 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지는 몰랐던 상태에서 그 짓을 한 거니까요."], ["Let them acquit him—that’s so humane, and would show what a blessing reformed law courts are.", "사람들이 그를 무죄로 풀어 주라죠—그건 참으로 인도적인 일이고, 개혁된 법원이 얼마나 큰 축복인지를 보여 줄 거예요."], ["I knew nothing about it, but they say they have been so a long time.", "나는 그런 건 아무것도 몰랐는데, 사람들 말로는 오래전부터 그래 왔다더군요."], ["And when I heard it yesterday, I was so struck by it that I wanted to send for you at once.", "그리고 어제 그 얘기를 들었을 때 나는 어찌나 큰 충격을 받았던지 당장 당신을 부르고 싶었어요."], ["And if he is acquitted, make him come straight from the law courts to dinner with me, and I’ll have a party of friends, and we’ll drink to the reformed law courts.", "그리고 만일 그가 무죄로 풀려나거든, 그를 법원에서 곧장 우리 집으로 데려와 나와 함께 저녁을 들게 해 줘요, 그러면 나는 친구들을 불러 모아 잔치를 벌이고, 우리 다 함께 개혁된 법원을 위해 축배를 들 거예요."], ["I don’t believe he’d be dangerous; besides, I’ll invite a great many friends, so that he could always be led out if he did anything.", "그가 위험할 거라곤 생각하지 않아요. 게다가 나는 아주 많은 친구들을 초대할 테니, 그가 무슨 짓을 하더라도 언제든 끌어낼 수 있을 거예요."], ["And then he might be made a justice of the peace or something in another town, for those who have been in trouble themselves make the best judges.", "그리고 그다음엔 그를 다른 읍의 치안판사 같은 자리에 앉히면 될 거예요, 자기 자신이 곤경을 겪어 본 사람이야말로 가장 훌륭한 재판관이 되는 법이니까요."], ["And, besides, who isn’t suffering from aberration nowadays?—you, I, all of us are in a state of aberration, and there are ever so many examples of it: a man sits singing a song, suddenly something annoys him, he takes a pistol and shoots the first person he comes across, and no one blames him for it.", "게다가, 요즘 정신착란을 앓지 않는 사람이 어디 있겠어요?—당신도, 나도, 우리 모두가 정신착란 상태에 있는 거예요, 그런 예는 얼마든지 있어요. 어떤 사람이 노래를 부르며 앉아 있다가, 갑자기 무언가에 짜증이 나서, 권총을 집어 들고 맨 처음 마주친 사람을 쏘아 버리는데도, 아무도 그를 탓하지 않아요."], ["I read that lately, and all the doctors confirm it.", "나는 최근에 그런 걸 읽었는데, 의사들이 다들 그걸 확인해 줬어요."], ["The doctors are always confirming; they confirm anything.", "의사들은 늘 확인해 주지요. 뭐든 다 확인해 준다니까요."], ["Why, my Lise is in a state of aberration.", "글쎄, 우리 리즈도 정신착란 상태예요."], ["She made me cry again yesterday, and the day before, too, and to-day I suddenly realized that it’s all due to aberration.", "그 애가 어제 또 나를 울렸고, 그저께도 그랬고, 그래서 오늘 나는 문득 그게 다 정신착란 탓이라는 걸 깨달았지요. 아아, 리즈 때문에 내가 얼마나 마음을 졸이는지!"], ["Oh, Lise grieves me so! I believe she’s quite mad.", "그 애는 완전히 미쳐 버린 것 같아요."], ["Why did she send for you?", "그 애가 왜 당신을 불렀을까요?"], ["Did she send for you or did you come of yourself?”", "그 애가 당신을 부른 건가요, 아니면 당신이 제 발로 온 건가요?\""]], [["“Yes, she sent for me, and I am just going to her.”", "\"예, 그 애가 저를 불렀습니다, 그래서 지금 막 그 애에게 가려는 참입니다.\""], ["Alyosha got up resolutely.", "알료샤는 단호하게 일어섰다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, perhaps that’s what’s most important,”", "\"오, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 어쩌면 그게 가장 중요한 일일 거예요.\""], ["Madame Hohlakov cried, suddenly bursting into tears.", "호흘라코바 부인이 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["“God knows I trust Lise to you with all my heart, and it’s no matter her sending for you on the sly, without telling her mother.", "\"하느님께서 아시지만, 나는 온 마음으로 리즈를 당신께 믿고 맡겨요, 그 애가 제 어미한테는 말도 없이 몰래 당신을 부른 거야 아무래도 좋아요."], ["But forgive me, I can’t trust my daughter so easily to your brother Ivan Fyodorovitch, though I still consider him the most chivalrous young man.", "하지만 용서해요, 나는 내 딸을 당신의 형님 이반 표도로비치에게는 그렇게 쉽사리 맡길 수가 없어요, 비록 나는 여전히 그를 더없이 기사도적인 젊은이라고 여기긴 하지만요."], ["But only fancy, he’s been to see Lise and I knew nothing about it!”", "그런데 글쎄 상상해 보세요, 그가 리즈를 보러 왔었는데 나는 아무것도 몰랐다니까요!\""]], [["“How?", "\"어떻게요?"], ["What? When?”", "무슨 말씀이세요?"], ["Alyosha was exceedingly surprised.", "언제요?\" 알료샤는 몹시 놀랐다."], ["He had not sat down again and listened standing.", "그는 다시 앉지 않고 선 채로 듣고 있었다."]], [["“I will tell you; that’s perhaps why I asked you to come, for I don’t know now why I did ask you to come.", "\"내가 이야기해 드리지요. 어쩌면 그래서 내가 당신에게 와 달라고 청한 건지도 몰라요, 사실 이제는 내가 왜 당신더러 와 달라고 했는지도 모르겠으니까요."], ["Well, Ivan Fyodorovitch has been to see me twice, since he came back from Moscow.", "자, 이반 표도로비치는 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 두 번 나를 보러 왔어요."], ["First time he came as a friend to call on me, and the second time Katya was here and he came because he heard she was here.", "처음에는 친구로서 나를 찾아온 거였고, 두 번째는 카탸가 여기 있을 때였는데 그 애가 여기 있다는 걸 듣고 온 거였지요."], ["I didn’t, of course, expect him to come often, knowing what a lot he has to do as it is, vous comprenez, cette affaire et la mort terrible de votre papa.", "물론 나는 그가 자주 오리라곤 기대하지 않았어요, 안 그래도 그가 할 일이 얼마나 많은지 아니까요, 당신도 이해하겠지요, 그 사건이며 당신 아버님의 그 끔찍한 죽음이며 말이에요."], ["But I suddenly heard he’d been here again, not to see me but to see Lise.", "그런데 갑자기 듣자 하니 그가 또 여기 왔었다는 거예요, 나를 보러 온 게 아니라 리즈를 보러요."], ["That’s six days ago now.", "이제 엿새 전 일이에요."], ["He came, stayed five minutes, and went away.", "그가 와서, 오 분쯤 머물다가, 가 버렸어요."], ["And I didn’t hear of it till three days afterwards, from Glafira, so it was a great shock to me.", "그리고 나는 사흘이 지나서야 글라피라한테서 그 얘기를 들었으니, 그게 나한테는 큰 충격이었지요."], ["I sent for Lise directly.", "나는 곧장 리즈를 불렀어요."], ["She laughed.", "그 애는 웃더군요."], ["‘He thought you were asleep,’ she said, ‘and came in to me to ask after your health.’ Of course, that’s how it happened.", "'그분은 어머니가 주무시는 줄 아셨대요' 하고 그 애가 말했어요, '그래서 어머니 안부를 여쭈러 내 방에 들르신 거예요.'"], ["But Lise, Lise, mercy on us, how she distresses me!", "물론 그렇게 된 거겠지요. 하지만 리즈, 리즈가, 저런, 그 애가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히는지!"], ["Would you believe it, one night, four days ago, just after you saw her last time, and had gone away, she suddenly had a fit, screaming, shrieking, hysterics!", "글쎄 믿어지세요, 어느 날 밤, 나흘 전이에요, 당신이 그 애를 마지막으로 보고 떠난 직후에, 그 애가 갑자기 발작을 일으켰어요, 비명을 지르고, 악을 쓰고, 히스테리를 부리고!"], ["Why is it I never have hysterics?", "나는 어째서 한 번도 히스테리를 일으키지 않는 걸까요?"], ["Then, next day another fit, and the same thing on the third, and yesterday too, and then yesterday that aberration.", "그러더니, 다음 날 또 발작, 사흘째에도 마찬가지, 어제도 그랬고, 그러고는 어제 그 정신착란이지요."], ["She suddenly screamed out, ‘I hate Ivan Fyodorovitch.", "그 애가 갑자기 소리를 질렀어요, '나는 이반 표도로비치가 싫어요."], ["I insist on your never letting him come to the house again.’ I was struck dumb at these amazing words, and answered, ‘On what grounds could I refuse to see such an excellent young man, a young man of such learning too, and so unfortunate?’—for all this business is a misfortune, isn’t it?", "다시는 그분을 집에 들이지 말라고 어머니께 당부해요.' 나는 그 놀라운 말에 입이 딱 막혀서 대답했지요, '그렇게 훌륭한 젊은이를, 더구나 그렇게 학식 있고, 또 그렇게 불행한 젊은이를 무슨 명목으로 안 만나겠다고 하겠니?'—이 모든 일이 다 불행이잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["She suddenly burst out laughing at my words, and so rudely, you know.", "그 애는 내 말에 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸어요, 그것도 어찌나 무례하게요."], ["Well, I was pleased; I thought I had amused her and the fits would pass off, especially as I wanted to refuse to see Ivan Fyodorovitch anyway on account of his strange visits without my knowledge, and meant to ask him for an explanation.", "그래도 나는 기뻤지요, 내가 그 애를 즐겁게 해 줘서 발작이 가실 거라고 생각했으니까요, 더구나 나는 그가 나도 모르게 그런 이상한 방문을 한 일 때문에 어차피 이반 표도로비치를 안 만날 작정이었고, 그에게 해명을 요구할 생각이었거든요."], ["But early this morning Lise waked up and flew into a passion with Yulia and, would you believe it, slapped her in the face.", "그런데 오늘 이른 아침에 리즈가 잠에서 깨더니 율리야에게 버럭 화를 내고는, 글쎄 믿어지세요, 그 애의 뺨을 후려쳤지 뭐예요."], ["That’s monstrous; I am always polite to my servants.", "그건 끔찍한 짓이에요. 나는 늘 하인들에게 정중하게 대하거든요."], ["And an hour later she was hugging Yulia’s feet and kissing them.", "그러더니 한 시간 뒤에는 율리야의 발을 끌어안고 입을 맞추더라니까요."], ["She sent a message to me that she wasn’t coming to me at all, and would never come and see me again, and when I dragged myself down to her, she rushed to kiss me, crying, and as she kissed me, she pushed me out of the room without saying a word, so I couldn’t find out what was the matter.", "그 애가 나한테 전갈을 보내왔어요, 나한테는 도무지 오지 않겠다고, 다시는 나를 보러 오지 않겠다고요, 그래서 내가 몸을 끌고 그 애에게 내려갔더니, 그 애는 나한테 달려들어 입을 맞추며 울었고, 그렇게 입을 맞추면서, 한마디 말도 없이 나를 방에서 밀어내는 바람에, 무슨 일인지 알아낼 수가 없었어요."], ["Now, dear Alexey Fyodorovitch, I rest all my hopes on you, and, of course, my whole life is in your hands.", "자, 사랑하는 알렉세이 표도로비치, 나는 모든 희망을 당신에게 걸겠어요, 그리고, 물론, 내 온 삶이 당신 손에 달려 있어요."], ["I simply beg you to go to Lise and find out everything from her, as you alone can, and come back and tell me—me, her mother, for you understand it will be the death of me, simply the death of me, if this goes on, or else I shall run away.", "부디 리즈에게 가서 그 애한테서 모든 걸 알아내 줘요, 당신만이 할 수 있는 일이니까요, 그러고는 돌아와 나한테—그 애 어미인 나한테 이야기해 줘요, 당신도 알겠지만 이대로 가다간 내가 죽고 말 거예요, 그냥 죽어 버릴 거예요, 아니면 도망쳐 버리든가요."], ["I can stand no more.", "나는 더는 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I have patience; but I may lose patience, and then ...", "나도 참을성이 있지만, 그 참을성을 잃어버릴지도 몰라요, 그러면…"], ["then something awful will happen.", "그러면 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지겠지요."], ["Ah, dear me!", "아아, 어머나!"], ["At last, Pyotr Ilyitch!”", "드디어 오셨군요, 표트르 일리치!\""], ["cried Madame Hohlakov, beaming all over as she saw Perhotin enter the room.", "호흘라코바 부인은 페르호틴이 방으로 들어오는 것을 보고는 온 얼굴에 환한 빛을 띠며 외쳤다."], ["“You are late, you are late!", "\"늦으셨어요, 늦으셨어요!"], ["Well, sit down, speak, put us out of suspense.", "자, 앉으세요, 말씀해 보세요, 우리를 이 애타는 마음에서 풀어 주세요."], ["What does the counsel say.", "변호인이 뭐라고 하던가요."], ["Where are you off to, Alexey Fyodorovitch?”", "어디로 가시려고요, 알렉세이 표도로비치?\""]], [["“To Lise.”", "\"리즈에게요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes.", "\"오, 그래요."], ["You won’t forget, you won’t forget what I asked you?", "잊지 않을 거지요, 내가 부탁한 걸 잊지 않을 거지요?"], ["It’s a question of life and death!”", "이건 죽느냐 사느냐의 문제예요!\""]], [["“Of course, I won’t forget, if I can ...", "\"물론 잊지 않겠습니다, 할 수 있다면요…"], ["but I am so late,”", "하지만 제가 너무 늦어서요.\""], ["muttered Alyosha, beating a hasty retreat.", "알료샤가 황급히 물러나며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“No, be sure, be sure to come in; don’t say ‘If you can.’ I shall die if you don’t,”", "\"안 돼요, 꼭, 꼭 들러 줘야 해요. '할 수 있다면'이라고 하지 말아요."], ["Madame Hohlakov called after him, but Alyosha had already left the room.", "당신이 안 오면 나는 죽고 말 거예요.\" 호흘라코바 부인이 그의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤으나, 알료샤는 이미 방을 나선 뒤였다."]], [["# Chapter III. A Little Demon", "# Chapter III. A Little Demon"]], [["Going in to Lise, he found her half reclining in the invalid-chair, in which she had been wheeled when she was unable to walk.", "리즈에게 들어서니, 그녀는 걸을 수 없을 때 누가 밀어 주던 바로 그 환자용 의자에 반쯤 기대어 있었다."], ["She did not move to meet him, but her sharp, keen eyes were simply riveted on his face.", "그를 맞으려고 몸을 움직이지는 않았으나, 날카롭고 매서운 두 눈만은 그저 그의 얼굴에 못 박힌 듯 고정되어 있었다."], ["There was a feverish look in her eyes, her face was pale and yellow.", "그 눈에는 열에 들뜬 빛이 어려 있었고, 얼굴은 창백하고 누르께했다."], ["Alyosha was amazed at the change that had taken place in her in three days.", "알료샤는 사흘 사이에 그녀에게 일어난 변화에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["She was positively thinner.", "그녀는 확연히 야위어 있었다."], ["She did not hold out her hand to him.", "그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀지 않았다."], ["He touched the thin, long fingers which lay motionless on her dress, then he sat down facing her, without a word.", "그는 옷자락 위에 미동도 없이 놓인, 가늘고 긴 손가락을 만져 보고는, 말없이 그녀를 마주하고 앉았다."]], [["“I know you are in a hurry to get to the prison,”", "\"오빠가 교도소에 가려고 서두르고 있는 거 알아요.\" 리즈가 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["Lise said curtly, “and mamma’s kept you there for hours; she’s just been telling you about me and Yulia.”", "\"엄마가 거기서 몇 시간이나 오빠를 붙들어 뒀죠. 방금 나랑 율리야에 관해서 늘어놓았을 테고요.\""]], [["“How do you know?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 알아?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“I’ve been listening.", "\"엿듣고 있었거든요."], ["Why do you stare at me?", "왜 그렇게 빤히 쳐다봐요?"], ["I want to listen and I do listen, there’s no harm in that.", "엿듣고 싶어서 엿들었어요, 그게 무슨 잘못이라고요."], ["I don’t apologize.”", "사과 같은 건 안 해요.\""]], [["“You are upset about something?”", "\"무슨 일로 속이 상한 거니?\""]], [["“On the contrary, I am very happy.", "\"그 반대예요, 난 아주 행복해요."], ["I’ve only just been reflecting for the thirtieth time what a good thing it is I refused you and shall not be your wife.", "오빠를 거절하고 오빠 아내가 되지 않게 된 게 얼마나 잘한 일인지, 방금 서른 번째로 곱씹어 보고 있던 참이라고요."], ["You are not fit to be a husband.", "오빠는 남편감이 못 돼요."], ["If I were to marry you and give you a note to take to the man I loved after you, you’d take it and be sure to give it to him and bring an answer back, too.", "만약 내가 오빠랑 결혼해서, 오빠 다음으로 사랑하게 된 남자한테 전해 달라고 쪽지를 건네면, 오빠는 그걸 받아서 틀림없이 그 사람한테 전해 주고, 답장까지 받아다 줄 거예요."], ["If you were forty, you would still go on taking my love-letters for me.”", "오빠가 마흔 살이 돼도, 여전히 내 연애편지를 날라다 주겠죠.\""]], [["She suddenly laughed.", "그녀는 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“There is something spiteful and yet open-hearted about you,”", "\"너에게는 심술궂으면서도 동시에 솔직한 데가 있구나.\""], ["Alyosha smiled to her.", "알료샤가 그녀에게 미소 지었다."]], [["“The open-heartedness consists in my not being ashamed of myself with you.", "\"솔직하다는 건, 오빠 앞에서는 내 자신이 부끄럽지 않다는 거예요."], ["What’s more, I don’t want to feel ashamed with you, just with you.", "게다가 난 오빠 앞에서는 부끄럽고 싶지도 않아요, 딱 오빠 앞에서만요."], ["Alyosha, why is it I don’t respect you?", "알료샤, 왜 난 오빠를 존경하지 않는 걸까요?"], ["I am very fond of you, but I don’t respect you.", "오빠를 무척 좋아하는데도, 존경은 안 돼요."], ["If I respected you, I shouldn’t talk to you without shame, should I?”", "만약 오빠를 존경한다면, 부끄러움도 없이 이런 얘길 늘어놓진 못하겠죠, 안 그래요?\" \"그렇겠지.\""]], [["“No.” “But do you believe that I am not ashamed with you?”", "\"하지만 내가 오빠 앞에서 부끄럽지 않다는 거, 믿어요?\""]], [["“No, I don’t believe it.”", "\"아니, 안 믿어.\""]], [["Lise laughed nervously again; she spoke rapidly.", "리즈는 또다시 신경질적으로 웃었다. 그녀는 빠르게 말했다."]], [["“I sent your brother, Dmitri Fyodorovitch, some sweets in prison.", "\"오빠 형, 드미트리 표도로비치한테 교도소로 사탕을 좀 보냈어요."], ["Alyosha, you know, you are quite pretty!", "알료샤, 있잖아요, 오빠는 참 예뻐요!"], ["I shall love you awfully for having so quickly allowed me not to love you.”", "오빠를 사랑하지 않아도 되게끔 그렇게 빨리 허락해 준 걸로, 난 오빠를 끔찍이 사랑할 거예요.\""]], [["“Why did you send for me to-day, Lise?”", "\"오늘 왜 나를 부른 거니, 리즈?\""]], [["“I wanted to tell you of a longing I have.", "\"내게 있는 어떤 갈망을 오빠한테 말하고 싶었어요."], ["I should like some one to torture me, marry me and then torture me, deceive me and go away.", "누군가 나를 괴롭혀 줬으면 좋겠어요, 나랑 결혼하고 나서 나를 괴롭히고, 나를 속이고 떠나 버렸으면 해요."], ["I don’t want to be happy.”", "난 행복해지고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["“You are in love with disorder?”", "\"넌 무질서를 사랑하는구나?\""]], [["“Yes, I want disorder.", "\"그래요, 난 무질서를 원해요."], ["I keep wanting to set fire to the house.", "자꾸만 집에 불을 지르고 싶어요."], ["I keep imagining how I’ll creep up and set fire to the house on the sly; it must be on the sly.", "몰래 살금살금 다가가 슬쩍 집에 불을 놓는 장면을 계속 떠올린다고요. 반드시 몰래여야 해요."], ["They’ll try to put it out, but it’ll go on burning.", "다들 불을 끄려 들겠지만, 불은 계속 타오를 거예요."], ["And I shall know and say nothing.", "그리고 난 알면서도 아무 말도 안 하겠죠."], ["Ah, what silliness!", "아, 이 무슨 바보 같은 소린지!"], ["And how bored I am!”", "그리고 난 어찌나 따분한지!\""]], [["She waved her hand with a look of repulsion.", "그녀는 혐오스럽다는 표정으로 손을 내저었다."]], [["“It’s your luxurious life,”", "\"네 사치스러운 생활 탓이야.\""], ["said Alyosha, softly.", "알료샤가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Is it better, then, to be poor?”", "\"그럼 가난한 게 더 낫다는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes, it is better.”", "\"그래, 그게 더 낫지.\""]], [["“That’s what your monk taught you.", "\"그건 오빠네 수도사가 가르쳐 준 거잖아요."], ["That’s not true.", "그건 사실이 아니에요."], ["Let me be rich and all the rest poor, I’ll eat sweets and drink cream and not give any to any one else.", "나만 부자고 다른 사람들은 다 가난뱅이라면, 난 사탕을 먹고 크림을 마시면서 아무한테도 한 입 안 나눠 줄 거예요."], ["Ach, don’t speak, don’t say anything,”", "아, 말하지 마요, 아무 말도 하지 마요.\""], ["she shook her hand at him, though Alyosha had not opened his mouth.", "알료샤가 입도 떼지 않았는데도, 그녀는 그에게 손을 내저었다."], ["“You’ve told me all that before, I know it all by heart.", "\"그런 얘긴 전에 다 해 줬잖아요, 난 죄다 외우고 있어요."], ["It bores me.", "따분하다니까요."], ["If I am ever poor, I shall murder somebody, and even if I am rich, I may murder some one, perhaps—why do nothing!", "만약 내가 언젠가 가난해진다면, 난 누군가를 죽여 버릴 거예요. 부자라 해도 누군가를 죽일지도 몰라요, 어쩌면요."], ["But do you know, I should like to reap, cut the rye?", "왜 아무것도 안 하고 있어야 하죠! 그런데 있잖아요, 난 곡식을 거두고 호밀을 베어 보고 싶어요."], ["I’ll marry you, and you shall become a peasant, a real peasant; we’ll keep a colt, shall we?", "오빠랑 결혼해서, 오빠를 농사꾼으로, 진짜 농사꾼으로 만들 거예요. 망아지도 한 마리 기르는 거예요, 어때요?"], ["Do you know Kalganov?”", "칼가노프 알죠?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알지.\""]], [["“He is always wandering about, dreaming.", "\"그 사람은 늘 이리저리 헤매며 공상에 잠겨 있어요."], ["He says, ‘Why live in real life?", "그가 그러더라고요. '왜 현실 속에서 살아야 하지?"], ["It’s better to dream.", "꿈꾸는 편이 더 낫지."], ["One can dream the most delightful things, but real life is a bore.’ But he’ll be married soon for all that; he’s been making love to me already.", "더없이 황홀한 것들을 꿈꿀 수 있는데, 현실의 삶은 따분하기만 하잖아.' 그래도 그는 곧 결혼할 거예요."], ["Can you spin tops?”", "벌써 나한테 구애했거든요. 오빠는 팽이 돌릴 줄 알아요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"알지.\""]], [["“Well, he’s just like a top: he wants to be wound up and set spinning and then to be lashed, lashed, lashed with a whip.", "\"그럼, 그는 꼭 팽이 같아요. 감아 올려서 돌리고는 채찍으로 후려치고, 후려치고, 또 후려쳐 주기를 바라죠."], ["If I marry him, I’ll keep him spinning all his life.", "내가 그와 결혼하면, 평생토록 그를 빙빙 돌게 만들 거예요."], ["You are not ashamed to be with me?”", "오빠는 나랑 같이 있는 게 부끄럽지 않아요?\" \"부끄럽지 않아.\""]], [["“No.” “You are awfully cross, because I don’t talk about holy things.", "\"오빠는 내가 거룩한 것들에 관해 이야기하지 않으니까 무척이나 못마땅하겠죠."], ["I don’t want to be holy.", "난 거룩해지고 싶지 않아요."], ["What will they do to one in the next world for the greatest sin?", "가장 큰 죄를 지으면 저세상에서 사람한테 무슨 짓을 할까요?"], ["You must know all about that.”", "오빠는 그런 걸 다 알 거 아니에요.\""]], [["“God will censure you.”", "\"하느님이 너를 꾸짖으시겠지.\""], ["Alyosha was watching her steadily.", "알료샤는 그녀를 가만히 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“That’s just what I should like.", "\"내가 바라는 게 바로 그거예요."], ["I would go up and they would censure me, and I would burst out laughing in their faces.", "내가 올라가면 그들이 나를 꾸짖을 테고, 난 그들 면전에서 와락 웃음을 터뜨릴 거예요."], ["I should dreadfully like to set fire to the house, Alyosha, to our house; you still don’t believe me?”", "난 집에 불을 지르고 싶어 죽겠어요, 알료샤, 우리 집에 말이에요. 아직도 내 말을 안 믿죠?\""]], [["“Why?", "\"왜?"], ["There are children of twelve years old, who have a longing to set fire to something and they do set things on fire, too.", "무언가에 불을 지르고 싶은 갈망을 품은 열두 살짜리 아이들도 있고, 실제로 불을 놓기도 해."], ["It’s a sort of disease.”", "그건 일종의 병이야.\""]], [["“That’s not true, that’s not true; there may be children, but that’s not what I mean.”", "\"그건 사실이 아니에요, 사실이 아니라고요. 그런 아이들이야 있겠죠, 하지만 내가 말하는 건 그런 게 아니에요.\""]], [["“You take evil for good; it’s a passing crisis, it’s the result of your illness, perhaps.”", "\"넌 악을 선으로 착각하고 있어. 그건 지나가는 위기일 뿐이야, 어쩌면 네 병에서 비롯된 것이고.\""]], [["“You do despise me, though!", "\"그래도 오빠는 날 경멸하는군요!"], ["It’s simply that I don’t want to do good, I want to do evil, and it has nothing to do with illness.”", "그냥 난 선을 행하고 싶지 않고 악을 행하고 싶을 뿐이에요, 그건 병하고는 아무 상관이 없어요.\""]], [["“Why do evil?”", "\"왜 악을 행하려는 거니?\""]], [["“So that everything might be destroyed.", "\"모든 게 파괴되도록 하기 위해서요."], ["Ah, how nice it would be if everything were destroyed!", "아, 모든 게 파괴된다면 얼마나 좋을까요!"], ["You know, Alyosha, I sometimes think of doing a fearful lot of harm and everything bad, and I should do it for a long while on the sly and suddenly every one would find it out.", "있잖아요, 알료샤, 난 가끔 어마어마하게 해악을 끼치고 온갖 나쁜 짓을 저지르는 걸 상상해요. 그걸 오래도록 몰래 저지르다가, 어느 날 갑자기 모두가 그걸 알아채는 거예요."], ["Every one will stand round and point their fingers at me and I would look at them all.", "다들 빙 둘러서서 나를 손가락질하겠죠, 그러면 난 그들 모두를 바라보는 거예요."], ["That would be awfully nice.", "그건 정말 근사할 거예요."], ["Why would it be so nice, Alyosha?”", "왜 그렇게 근사할까요, 알료샤?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠어."], ["It’s a craving to destroy something good or, as you say, to set fire to something.", "그건 선한 무언가를 파괴하고 싶은, 아니 네 말마따나 무언가에 불을 지르고 싶은 갈망이야."], ["It happens sometimes.”", "가끔 그런 일이 일어나지.\""]], [["“I not only say it, I shall do it.” “I believe you.”", "\"난 말만 하는 게 아니라, 정말로 그렇게 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, how I love you for saying you believe me.", "\"믿어.\" \"아, 날 믿는다고 말해 주다니, 그래서 오빠를 어찌나 사랑하는지."], ["And you are not lying one little bit.", "그리고 오빠는 손톱만큼도 거짓말을 안 해요."], ["But perhaps you think that I am saying all this on purpose to annoy you?”", "하지만 어쩌면 오빠는, 내가 오빠를 약 올리려고 일부러 이런 말들을 다 한다고 생각할지도 몰라요?\""]], [["“No, I don’t think that ...", "\"아니, 그렇게 생각하진 않아……"], ["though perhaps there is a little desire to do that in it, too.”", "하지만 어쩌면 그 안에 그러고 싶은 마음이 조금은 있을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“There is a little.", "\"조금은 있어요."], ["I never can tell lies to you,”", "난 오빠한테는 절대 거짓말을 못 해요.\""], ["she declared, with a strange fire in her eyes.", "그녀가 두 눈에 묘한 불꽃을 담고 단언했다."]], [["What struck Alyosha above everything was her earnestness.", "무엇보다도 알료샤를 놀라게 한 것은 그녀의 진지함이었다. 지금 그녀의 얼굴에는 익살이나 농담의 흔적이라곤 조금도 없었는데, 예전 같으면 가장 \"진지한\""], ["There was not a trace of humor or jesting in her face now, though, in old days, fun and gayety never deserted her even at her most “earnest” moments.", "순간에도 그녀에게서 장난기와 명랑함이 떠난 적이 없었던 것이다."]], [["“There are moments when people love crime,”", "\"사람들이 죄악을 사랑하는 순간들이 있어.\""], ["said Alyosha thoughtfully.", "알료샤가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes!", "\"맞아요, 맞아요!"], ["You have uttered my thought; they love crime, every one loves crime, they love it always, not at some ‘moments.’ You know, it’s as though people have made an agreement to lie about it and have lied about it ever since.", "오빠가 내 생각을 그대로 말했어요. 사람들은 죄악을 사랑해요, 누구나 죄악을 사랑하고, '어떤 순간들'이 아니라 늘 사랑한다고요."], ["They all declare that they hate evil, but secretly they all love it.”", "있잖아요, 그건 마치 사람들이 그것에 관해 거짓말하기로 약속이라도 한 것 같아요, 그래서 그 이래로 줄곧 거짓말을 해 온 거죠. 다들 자기는 악을 미워한다고 떠벌리지만, 속으로는 다들 그걸 사랑한다고요.\""]], [["“And are you still reading nasty books?”", "\"넌 아직도 그 못된 책들을 읽고 있는 거니?\""]], [["“Yes, I am.", "\"네, 읽어요."], ["Mamma reads them and hides them under her pillow and I steal them.”", "엄마가 그것들을 읽고는 베개 밑에 숨겨 두는데, 내가 슬쩍 훔쳐요.\""]], [["“Aren’t you ashamed to destroy yourself?”", "\"네 자신을 망가뜨리는 게 부끄럽지 않니?\""]], [["“I want to destroy myself.", "\"난 내 자신을 망가뜨리고 싶어요."], ["There’s a boy here, who lay down between the railway lines when the train was passing.", "여기 어떤 남자애가, 기차가 지나갈 때 철로 사이에 누워 있었대요."], ["Lucky fellow!", "운 좋은 녀석이죠!"], ["Listen, your brother is being tried now for murdering his father and every one loves his having killed his father.”", "들어 봐요, 지금 오빠 형이 아버지를 죽인 죄로 재판을 받고 있는데, 다들 그가 아버지를 죽인 걸 사랑해요.\""]], [["“Loves his having killed his father?”", "\"그가 아버지를 죽인 걸 사랑한다고?\""]], [["“Yes, loves it; every one loves it!", "\"네, 사랑해요. 다들 그걸 사랑한다고요!"], ["Everybody says it’s so awful, but secretly they simply love it.", "다들 그게 너무 끔찍하다고 말하지만, 속으로는 그냥 그걸 사랑하는 거예요."], ["I for one love it.”", "나부터가 그걸 사랑해요.\""]], [["“There is some truth in what you say about every one,”", "\"누구나 다 그렇다는 네 말에는 어느 정도 진실이 담겨 있어.\""], ["said Alyosha softly.", "알료샤가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Oh, what ideas you have!”", "\"어머, 오빠는 어쩌면 그런 생각을 다 해요!\""], ["Lise shrieked in delight.", "리즈가 기뻐 날카롭게 소리쳤다."], ["“And you a monk, too!", "\"게다가 오빠는 수도사잖아요!"], ["You wouldn’t believe how I respect you, Alyosha, for never telling lies.", "거짓말이라곤 절대 안 하는 오빠를, 내가 얼마나 존경하는지 오빠는 믿지 못할 거예요, 알료샤."], ["Oh, I must tell you a funny dream of mine.", "아, 내 우스운 꿈 얘기를 해 줘야겠어요."], ["I sometimes dream of devils.", "난 가끔 악마들 꿈을 꿔요."], ["It’s night; I am in my room with a candle and suddenly there are devils all over the place, in all the corners, under the table, and they open the doors; there’s a crowd of them behind the doors and they want to come and seize me.", "밤이고, 난 촛불을 켜고 내 방에 있는데, 갑자기 사방에 악마들이 우글거려요, 온갖 구석마다, 탁자 밑에도요, 그러더니 문을 열어요. 문 뒤로 한 떼가 몰려 있는데, 그놈들이 다가와서 나를 붙잡으려고 해요."], ["And they are just coming, just seizing me.", "그놈들이 막 다가와서, 막 나를 붙잡으려는 거예요."], ["But I suddenly cross myself and they all draw back, though they don’t go away altogether, they stand at the doors and in the corners, waiting.", "그런데 내가 갑자기 성호를 그으면 그놈들이 죄다 물러서요, 그렇다고 아주 가 버리는 건 아니고, 문가랑 구석에 서서 기다려요."], ["And suddenly I have a frightful longing to revile God aloud, and so I begin, and then they come crowding back to me, delighted, and seize me again and I cross myself again and they all draw back.", "그러다 갑자기 난 하느님을 소리 내어 욕하고 싶은 무시무시한 갈망에 사로잡혀서 욕을 시작해요, 그러면 그놈들이 신이 나서 다시 우르르 내게 몰려들어 또 나를 붙잡고, 난 또 성호를 그어요, 그러면 그놈들이 죄다 물러서요."], ["It’s awful fun.", "정말 끔찍하게 재미나요."], ["it takes one’s breath away.”", "숨이 다 멎을 지경이라니까요.\""]], [["“I’ve had the same dream, too,”", "\"나도 똑같은 꿈을 꾼 적이 있어.\""], ["said Alyosha suddenly.", "알료샤가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["cried Lise, surprised.", "리즈가 깜짝 놀라 외쳤다."], ["“I say, Alyosha, don’t laugh, that’s awfully important.", "\"있잖아요, 알료샤, 웃지 마요, 이건 정말 중요한 일이에요."], ["Could two different people have the same dream?”", "서로 다른 두 사람이 똑같은 꿈을 꿀 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“It seems they can.”", "\"꿀 수 있나 봐.\""]], [["“Alyosha, I tell you, it’s awfully important,”", "\"알료샤, 정말이지, 이건 정말 중요한 일이에요.\""], ["Lise went on, with really excessive amazement.", "리즈는 정말로 지나치다 싶을 만큼 경탄하며 말을 이었다."], ["“It’s not the dream that’s important, but your having the same dream as me.", "\"중요한 건 그 꿈이 아니라, 오빠가 나랑 똑같은 꿈을 꿨다는 거예요."], ["You never lie to me, don’t lie now: is it true?", "오빠는 나한테 절대 거짓말을 안 하잖아요, 지금도 거짓말하지 마요. 정말이에요?"], ["You are not laughing?”", "웃고 있는 거 아니죠?\""]], [["“It’s true.”", "\"정말이야.\""]], [["Lise seemed extraordinarily impressed and for half a minute she was silent.", "리즈는 어지간히 깊은 인상을 받은 듯, 삼십 초쯤 말이 없었다."]], [["“Alyosha, come and see me, come and see me more often,”", "\"알료샤, 나를 보러 와요, 좀 더 자주 나를 보러 와요.\""], ["she said suddenly, in a supplicating voice.", "그녀가 갑자기 애원하는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I’ll always come to see you, all my life,”", "\"언제든 너를 보러 올게, 평생토록.\""], ["answered Alyosha firmly.", "알료샤가 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["“You are the only person I can talk to, you know,”", "\"오빠는 내가 얘기를 나눌 수 있는 유일한 사람이에요, 알아요?\""], ["Lise began again.", "리즈가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“I talk to no one but myself and you.", "\"난 나 자신하고 오빠 말고는 아무하고도 이야기를 안 해요."], ["Only you in the whole world.", "온 세상에 오빠 하나뿐이라고요."], ["And to you more readily than to myself.", "게다가 나 자신한테보다 오빠한테 더 선뜻 털어놓아요."], ["And I am not a bit ashamed with you, not a bit.", "그리고 난 오빠 앞에서는 조금도 부끄럽지 않아요, 조금도요."], ["Alyosha, why am I not ashamed with you, not a bit?", "알료샤, 난 왜 오빠 앞에서는 부끄럽지 않을까요, 조금도요?"], ["Alyosha, is it true that at Easter the Jews steal a child and kill it?”", "알료샤, 부활절 때 유대인들이 아이를 훔쳐다가 죽인다는 게 정말이에요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"난 몰라.\""]], [["“There’s a book here in which I read about the trial of a Jew, who took a child of four years old and cut off the fingers from both hands, and then crucified him on the wall, hammered nails into him and crucified him, and afterwards, when he was tried, he said that the child died soon, within four hours.", "\"여기 어떤 책에서 한 유대인의 재판에 관해 읽었는데, 그자가 네 살짜리 아이를 데려다가 양손의 손가락을 죄다 잘라 내고는, 벽에 십자가형으로 못 박았대요, 못을 박아 넣어 십자가에 매단 거예요. 그러고 나서 재판을 받을 때, 그자가 말하길 아이가 곧 죽었다고, 네 시간 안에 죽었다고 했대요."], ["That was ‘soon’!", "그게 '곧'이라니!"], ["He said the child moaned, kept on moaning and he stood admiring it.", "그자 말로는, 아이가 신음했다고, 계속 신음하기에 자기는 서서 그걸 흐뭇하게 바라보고 있었다고 했대요. 정말 근사하죠!\""], ["That’s nice!”", "\"근사하다고?\""]], [["“Nice?”", "\"근사해요."], ["“Nice; I sometimes imagine that it was I who crucified him.", "난 가끔 그 아이를 십자가에 매단 게 나라고 상상해요."], ["He would hang there moaning and I would sit opposite him eating pineapple compote.", "그 아이가 거기 매달려 신음하고 있으면, 난 맞은편에 앉아 파인애플 콩포트를 먹는 거예요."], ["I am awfully fond of pineapple compote.", "난 파인애플 콩포트를 끔찍이 좋아하거든요."], ["Do you like it?”", "오빠도 좋아해요?\""]], [["Alyosha looked at her in silence.", "알료샤는 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Her pale, sallow face was suddenly contorted, her eyes burned.", "그녀의 창백하고 누르께한 얼굴이 갑자기 일그러지고, 두 눈이 불타올랐다."]], [["“You know, when I read about that Jew I shook with sobs all night.", "\"있잖아요, 그 유대인 얘기를 읽고 나서 난 밤새 흐느끼며 떨었어요."], ["I kept fancying how the little thing cried and moaned (a child of four years old understands, you know), and all the while the thought of pineapple compote haunted me.", "그 가엾은 것이 어떻게 울부짖고 신음했을지 자꾸만 떠올랐어요(네 살짜리 아이도 다 알잖아요), 그러면서도 내내 파인애플 콩포트 생각이 나를 떠나지 않았어요."], ["In the morning I wrote a letter to a certain person, begging him particularly to come and see me.", "아침이 되자 난 어떤 사람한테 편지를 써서, 꼭 좀 나를 보러 와 달라고 간곡히 부탁했어요."], ["He came and I suddenly told him all about the child and the pineapple compote.", "그가 왔고, 난 갑자기 그 아이랑 파인애플 콩포트 얘기를 죄다 그에게 털어놨어요."], ["All about it, all, and said that it was nice.", "전부 다요, 전부요, 그러고는 그게 근사하다고 말했어요."], ["He laughed and said it really was nice.", "그가 웃으면서, 정말로 근사하다고 했어요."], ["Then he got up and went away.", "그러더니 일어나서 가 버렸어요."], ["He was only here five minutes.", "그는 여기 딱 오 분 있었어요."], ["Did he despise me?", "그가 나를 경멸했을까요?"], ["Did he despise me?", "나를 경멸했을까요?"], ["Tell me, tell me, Alyosha, did he despise me or not?”", "말해 줘요, 말해 줘요, 알료샤, 그가 나를 경멸했을까요, 아닐까요?\""], ["She sat up on the couch, with flashing eyes.", "그녀는 두 눈을 번득이며 긴 의자에서 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“Tell me,”", "\"말해 봐.\""], ["Alyosha asked anxiously, “did you send for that person?”", "알료샤가 근심스레 물었다. \"네가 그 사람을 부른 거니?\""]], [["“Yes, I did.”", "\"네, 그랬어요.\""]], [["“Did you send him a letter?”", "\"그 사람한테 편지를 보냈고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Simply to ask about that, about that child?”", "\"그저 그걸 묻기 위해서, 그 아이 얘기를 하려고?\""]], [["“No, not about that at all.", "\"아니요, 그것 때문은 전혀 아니에요."], ["But when he came, I asked him about that at once.", "하지만 그가 왔을 때, 난 대뜸 그걸 물었어요."], ["He answered, laughed, got up and went away.”", "그가 대답하고는, 웃고, 일어나서 가 버렸어요.\""]], [["“That person behaved honorably,”", "\"그 사람은 점잖게 처신한 거야.\""], ["Alyosha murmured.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And did he despise me?", "\"그가 나를 경멸했을까요?"], ["Did he laugh at me?”", "나를 비웃은 걸까요?\""]], [["“No, for perhaps he believes in the pineapple compote himself.", "\"아니, 어쩌면 그 사람 자신도 파인애플 콩포트를 믿고 있을 테니까."], ["He is very ill now, too, Lise.”", "그 사람도 지금 몹시 아프단다, 리즈.\""]], [["“Yes, he does believe in it,”", "\"네, 그는 그걸 믿어요.\""], ["said Lise, with flashing eyes.", "리즈가 두 눈을 번득이며 말했다."]], [["“He doesn’t despise any one,”", "\"그 사람은 누구도 경멸하지 않아.\""], ["Alyosha went on.", "알료샤가 말을 이었다."], ["“Only he does not believe any one.", "\"다만 누구도 믿지 않을 뿐이야."], ["If he doesn’t believe in people, of course, he does despise them.”", "사람을 믿지 않으니, 당연히 사람을 경멸하는 거지.\""]], [["“Then he despises me, me?”", "\"그럼 그가 나를 경멸하는군요, 나를?\""]], [["“You, too.”", "\"너도.\""]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아요.\""], ["Lise seemed to grind her teeth.", "리즈는 이를 가는 듯했다."], ["“When he went out laughing, I felt that it was nice to be despised.", "\"그가 웃으면서 나가 버렸을 때, 난 경멸당하는 게 근사하다고 느꼈어요."], ["The child with fingers cut off is nice, and to be despised is nice....”", "손가락이 잘린 그 아이도 근사하고, 경멸당하는 것도 근사하고…….\""]], [["And she laughed in Alyosha’s face, a feverish malicious laugh.", "그러고는 알료샤의 면전에 대고 웃었으니, 열에 들뜬 악의에 찬 웃음이었다."]], [["“Do you know, Alyosha, do you know, I should like—Alyosha, save me!”", "\"있잖아요, 알료샤, 있잖아요, 난…… 알료샤, 나를 구해 줘요!\""], ["She suddenly jumped from the couch, rushed to him and seized him with both hands.", "그녀가 갑자기 긴 의자에서 벌떡 뛰어내려 그에게 달려들더니 두 손으로 그를 붙잡았다. \"나를 구해 줘요!\""], ["“Save me!” she almost groaned.", "그녀는 거의 신음하듯 말했다."], ["“Is there any one in the world I could tell what I’ve told you?", "\"내가 오빠한테 한 얘기를 털어놓을 수 있는 사람이 이 세상에 또 누가 있겠어요?"], ["I’ve told you the truth, the truth.", "난 오빠한테 진실을, 진실을 말했어요."], ["I shall kill myself, because I loathe everything!", "난 자살할 거예요, 모든 게 다 역겨우니까요!"], ["I don’t want to live, because I loathe everything!", "살고 싶지 않아요, 모든 게 다 역겨우니까요!"], ["I loathe everything, everything.", "모든 게 다 역겨워요, 전부 다요."], ["Alyosha, why don’t you love me in the least?”", "알료샤, 오빠는 왜 나를 손톱만큼도 사랑하지 않는 거예요?\""], ["she finished in a frenzy.", "그녀가 광기에 사로잡혀 말을 맺었다."]], [["“But I do love you!”", "\"하지만 난 너를 사랑해!\""], ["answered Alyosha warmly.", "알료샤가 따뜻하게 대답했다."]], [["“And will you weep over me, will you?”", "\"그럼 나를 위해 울어 줄 거예요, 그럴 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Not because I won’t be your wife, but simply weep for me?”", "\"내가 오빠 아내가 되지 않아서가 아니라, 그냥 나를 위해 울어 줄 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Thank you!", "\"고마워요!"], ["It’s only your tears I want.", "난 오직 오빠의 눈물만 바라요."], ["Every one else may punish me and trample me under foot, every one, every one, not excepting any one.", "다른 사람들은 다 나를 벌하고 발밑에 짓밟아도 돼요, 누구나, 누구나, 단 한 사람도 빼놓지 않고요."], ["For I don’t love any one.", "난 누구도 사랑하지 않으니까요."], ["Do you hear, not any one!", "들었죠, 누구도요!"], ["On the contrary, I hate him!", "오히려, 난 그를 미워해요!"], ["Go, Alyosha; it’s time you went to your brother”; she tore herself away from him suddenly.", "가요, 알료샤, 오빠 형한테 갈 시간이에요.\" 그녀가 갑자기 그에게서 몸을 떼어 냈다."]], [["“How can I leave you like this?”", "\"이런 너를 두고 어떻게 떠나니?\""], ["said Alyosha, almost in alarm.", "알료샤가 거의 놀라다시피 말했다."]], [["“Go to your brother, the prison will be shut; go, here’s your hat.", "\"오빠 형한테 가요, 교도소 문이 닫힐 거예요. 가요, 여기 오빠 모자요."], ["Give my love to Mitya, go, go!”", "미탸한테 내 사랑을 전해 줘요, 가요, 가요!\""]], [["And she almost forcibly pushed Alyosha out of the door.", "그러고는 거의 떠밀다시피 알료샤를 문밖으로 내보냈다."], ["He looked at her with pained surprise, when he was suddenly aware of a letter in his right hand, a tiny letter folded up tight and sealed.", "그가 고통스러운 놀라움에 잠겨 그녀를 바라보던 그때, 문득 오른손에 편지 한 통이 쥐여 있다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 단단히 접어 봉한 자그마한 편지였다."], ["He glanced at it and instantly read the address, “To Ivan Fyodorovitch Karamazov.”", "그가 그것을 흘끗 보고 그 자리에서 수신인을 읽으니, \"이반 표도로비치 카라마조프에게\"라고 적혀 있었다."], ["He looked quickly at Lise.", "그는 재빨리 리즈를 보았다."], ["Her face had become almost menacing.", "그녀의 얼굴은 거의 위협적으로 변해 있었다."]], [["“Give it to him, you must give it to him!”", "\"그한테 전해 줘요, 반드시 그한테 전해 줘야 해요!\""], ["she ordered him, trembling and beside herself.", "그녀가 부들부들 떨며 제정신이 아닌 채로 그에게 명령했다."], ["“To-day, at once, or I’ll poison myself!", "\"오늘, 당장요, 안 그러면 난 독을 마실 거예요!"], ["That’s why I sent for you.”", "그래서 오빠를 부른 거란 말이에요.\""]], [["And she slammed the door quickly.", "그러고는 재빨리 문을 쾅 닫았다."], ["The bolt clicked.", "빗장이 철컥 채워졌다."], ["Alyosha put the note in his pocket and went straight downstairs, without going back to Madame Hohlakov; forgetting her, in fact.", "알료샤는 쪽지를 주머니에 넣고 호흘라코바 부인에게 들르지도 않은 채 곧장 아래층으로 내려갔으니, 실은 그녀를 까맣게 잊은 것이었다."], ["As soon as Alyosha had gone, Lise unbolted the door, opened it a little, put her finger in the crack and slammed the door with all her might, pinching her finger.", "알료샤가 떠나자마자 리즈는 빗장을 풀고 문을 조금 연 다음, 손가락을 문틈에 끼워 넣고는 있는 힘껏 문을 쾅 닫아 손가락을 짓이겼다."], ["Ten seconds after, releasing her finger, she walked softly, slowly to her chair, sat up straight in it and looked intently at her blackened finger and at the blood that oozed from under the nail.", "십 초쯤 지나 손가락을 빼낸 그녀는, 부드럽고 느릿한 걸음으로 의자로 걸어가 거기에 꼿꼿이 앉아서는, 시커멓게 변한 손가락과 손톱 밑에서 배어 나오는 피를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Her lips were quivering and she kept whispering rapidly to herself:", "그녀의 입술은 바르르 떨렸고, 그녀는 줄곧 빠르게 혼자 속삭였다."]], [["“I am a wretch, wretch, wretch, wretch!”", "\"난 비열한 년이야, 비열한 년, 비열한 년, 비열한 년!\""]], [["# Chapter IV. A Hymn And A Secret", "# Chapter IV. A Hymn And A Secret"]], [["It was quite late (days are short in November) when Alyosha rang at the prison gate.", "알료샤가 감옥 정문에서 초인종을 울렸을 때는 이미 제법 늦은 시각이었다(십일월의 낮은 짧았다)."], ["It was beginning to get dusk.", "어스름이 깔리기 시작하고 있었다."], ["But Alyosha knew that he would be admitted without difficulty.", "그러나 알료샤는 자기가 별다른 어려움 없이 안으로 들어갈 수 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Things were managed in our little town, as everywhere else.", "우리 작은 고장에서도 일은 다른 곳과 마찬가지로 돌아갔다."], ["At first, of course, on the conclusion of the preliminary inquiry, relations and a few other persons could only obtain interviews with Mitya by going through certain inevitable formalities.", "물론 처음에는, 예심이 끝난 직후라 친척이나 그 밖의 몇몇 사람들도 정해진 절차를 어쩔 수 없이 거쳐야만 미탸와 면회할 수 있었다."], ["But later, though the formalities were not relaxed, exceptions were made for some, at least, of Mitya’s visitors.", "그러나 나중에는, 절차 자체가 느슨해진 것은 아니었으되, 적어도 미탸를 찾아오는 방문객 가운데 몇몇에게는 예외가 인정되었다."], ["So much so, that sometimes the interviews with the prisoner in the room set aside for the purpose were practically tête-à-tête.", "어찌나 그러했던지, 면회용으로 따로 마련된 방에서 죄수와 만나는 자리가 사실상 단둘이서 마주 앉는 것이나 다름없을 때도 더러 있었다."]], [["These exceptions, however, were few in number; only Grushenka, Alyosha and Rakitin were treated like this.", "그렇지만 이런 예외는 몇 사람에게만 허락되었으니, 오로지 그루셴카와 알료샤, 그리고 라키틴만이 이런 대접을 받았다."], ["But the captain of the police, Mihail Mihailovitch, was very favorably disposed to Grushenka.", "그런데 경찰서장 미하일 미하일로비치는 그루셴카에게 무척 호의적이었다."], ["His abuse of her at Mokroe weighed on the old man’s conscience, and when he learned the whole story, he completely changed his view of her.", "모크로예에서 그녀에게 퍼부었던 욕설이 노인의 양심을 짓눌렀고, 자초지종을 다 알게 되자 그는 그녀에 대한 생각을 완전히 바꾸었다."], ["And strange to say, though he was firmly persuaded of his guilt, yet after Mitya was once in prison, the old man came to take a more and more lenient view of him.", "그리고 이상하게도, 그는 미탸가 유죄라고 굳게 믿고 있었으면서도 미탸가 일단 감옥에 들어간 뒤로는 점점 더 너그러운 시선으로 그를 바라보게 되었다."], ["“He was a man of good heart, perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면 마음씨 착한 사람이었던 게야.\""], ["he thought, “who had come to grief from drinking and dissipation.”", "그는 생각했다. \"술과 방탕 때문에 신세를 망친 게지.\""], ["His first horror had been succeeded by pity.", "처음에 느꼈던 공포는 어느새 연민으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["As for Alyosha, the police captain was very fond of him and had known him for a long time.", "알료샤로 말하자면, 경찰서장은 그를 무척 아꼈고 오래전부터 알고 지내던 사이였다."], ["Rakitin, who had of late taken to coming very often to see the prisoner, was one of the most intimate acquaintances of the “police captain’s young ladies,” as he called them, and was always hanging about their house.", "라키틴은 요즘 들어 죄수를 매우 자주 보러 오게 되었는데, 그는 자신이 \"경찰서장 댁 아가씨들\"이라 부르던 이들과 가장 가깝게 지내는 사람 가운데 하나로서 늘 그 집 언저리를 맴돌았다."], ["He gave lessons in the house of the prison superintendent, too, who, though scrupulous in the performance of his duties, was a kind-hearted old man.", "그는 감옥 소장의 집에서도 가정교사 노릇을 하고 있었는데, 그 소장은 직무 수행에는 빈틈이 없었으나 마음씨는 따뜻한 노인이었다."], ["Alyosha, again, had an intimate acquaintance of long standing with the superintendent, who was fond of talking to him, generally on sacred subjects.", "알료샤 또한 소장과는 오래도록 가깝게 지낸 사이였고, 소장은 그와 이야기 나누기를, 대개 성스러운 주제에 관해 이야기 나누기를 좋아했다."], ["He respected Ivan Fyodorovitch, and stood in awe of his opinion, though he was a great philosopher himself; “self-taught,”", "그는 이반 표도로비치를 존경했고 그의 견해 앞에서는 경외감을 품었는데, 정작 자기 자신도 대단한 철학자였다. 물론 \"독학\"으로 그렇게 된 것이었다."], ["of course. But Alyosha had an irresistible attraction for him.", "그러나 알료샤에게는 그가 거역할 수 없는 끌림을 느꼈다."], ["During the last year the old man had taken to studying the Apocryphal Gospels, and constantly talked over his impressions with his young friend.", "지난 한 해 동안 노인은 외경(外經) 복음서를 공부하기 시작했고, 자신이 받은 인상을 끊임없이 이 젊은 벗과 더불어 이야기했다."], ["He used to come and see him in the monastery and discussed for hours together with him and with the monks.", "그는 수도원으로 알료샤를 찾아와 그와, 또 수도사들과 더불어 몇 시간씩 토론을 벌이곤 했다."], ["So even if Alyosha were late at the prison, he had only to go to the superintendent and everything was made easy.", "그러니 설령 알료샤가 감옥에 늦게 도착한다 해도 소장에게로 가기만 하면 모든 일이 수월하게 풀렸다."], ["Besides, every one in the prison, down to the humblest warder, had grown used to Alyosha.", "게다가 감옥 안에서는 가장 말단 간수에 이르기까지 모두가 알료샤에게 익숙해져 있었다."], ["The sentry, of course, did not trouble him so long as the authorities were satisfied.", "보초병 또한, 물론, 윗선이 만족하는 한 그를 귀찮게 굴지 않았다."]], [["When Mitya was summoned from his cell, he always went downstairs, to the place set aside for interviews.", "미탸는 감방에서 호출을 받을 때면 언제나 아래층으로, 면회를 위해 따로 마련된 자리로 내려갔다."], ["As Alyosha entered the room he came upon Rakitin, who was just taking leave of Mitya.", "알료샤가 방으로 들어서니 마침 미탸와 작별 인사를 나누고 있던 라키틴과 마주쳤다."], ["They were both talking loudly.", "두 사람 다 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었다."], ["Mitya was laughing heartily as he saw him out, while Rakitin seemed grumbling.", "미탸는 그를 배웅하며 유쾌하게 웃고 있었으나, 라키틴은 무언가 투덜대는 듯했다."], ["Rakitin did not like meeting Alyosha, especially of late.", "라키틴은 알료샤와 마주치는 것을 좋아하지 않았는데, 특히 요즘 들어 더욱 그랬다."], ["He scarcely spoke to him, and bowed to him stiffly.", "그는 알료샤에게 말을 거의 건네지 않았고, 뻣뻣하게 고개만 까딱했다."], ["Seeing Alyosha enter now, he frowned and looked away, as though he were entirely absorbed in buttoning his big, warm, fur-trimmed overcoat.", "알료샤가 들어오는 것을 보자 그는 인상을 찌푸리며 시선을 돌리더니, 모피로 가장자리를 두른 크고 따뜻한 외투의 단추를 채우는 데 온통 정신이 팔린 척했다."], ["Then he began looking at once for his umbrella.", "그러고는 곧 우산을 찾기 시작했다."]], [["“I must mind not to forget my belongings,”", "\"내 물건 잊지 않도록 조심해야지.\""], ["he muttered, simply to say something.", "그는 그저 무슨 말이라도 하려고 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Mind you don’t forget other people’s belongings,”", "\"남의 물건 잊지 않도록 조심하시게.\""], ["said Mitya, as a joke, and laughed at once at his own wit.", "미탸가 농담조로 말하고는 곧 제 재치에 스스로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Rakitin fired up instantly.", "라키틴은 대번에 발끈했다."]], [["“You’d better give that advice to your own family, who’ve always been a slave-driving lot, and not to Rakitin,”", "\"그런 충고라면 라키틴이 아니라 자네 집안사람들한테나 하지그래, 늘 종 부리듯 사람들을 부려먹던 족속들 아닌가.\""], ["he cried, suddenly trembling with anger.", "그는 갑자기 분노로 부들부들 떨며 소리쳤다."]], [["“What’s the matter?", "\"왜 이러나?"], ["I was joking,”", "농담이었네.\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“Damn it all!", "\"에잇, 빌어먹을!"], ["They are all like that,”", "다들 저 모양이라니까.\""], ["he turned to Alyosha, nodding towards Rakitin’s hurriedly retreating figure.", "그는 알료샤를 돌아보며 황급히 물러가는 라키틴의 뒷모습을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“He was sitting here, laughing and cheerful, and all at once he boils up like that.", "\"여기 앉아서 웃고 떠들며 즐거워하더니, 갑자기 저렇게 부글부글 끓어오른단 말이야."], ["He didn’t even nod to you.", "너한테는 고갯짓 한 번 안 하던데."], ["Have you broken with him completely?", "저 친구하고 완전히 갈라선 거냐?"], ["Why are you so late?", "그런데 왜 이렇게 늦었어?"], ["I’ve not been simply waiting, but thirsting for you the whole morning.", "난 그저 너를 기다린 게 아니라, 아침 내내 너를 목말라하며 기다렸다."], ["But never mind.", "하지만 됐다."], ["We’ll make up for it now.”", "이제 그걸 만회하면 되지.\""]], [["“Why does he come here so often?", "\"저 사람은 왜 여기 이렇게 자주 오는 거야?"], ["Surely you are not such great friends?”", "설마 그렇게까지 친한 사이는 아닐 텐데?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."], ["He, too, nodded at the door through which Rakitin had disappeared.", "그 역시 라키틴이 사라진 문 쪽을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Great friends with Rakitin?", "\"라키틴하고 친한 사이냐고?"], ["No, not as much as that.", "아니, 그 정도는 아니야."], ["Is it likely—a pig like that? He considers I am ...", "말이 되냐, 그런 돼지 같은 놈하고?"], ["a blackguard.", "그자는 나를…"], ["They can’t understand a joke either, that’s the worst of such people.", "비열한 놈이라고 여긴다. 농담도 못 알아듣지, 그게 바로 그런 인간들의 가장 못된 점이야."], ["They never understand a joke, and their souls are dry, dry and flat; they remind me of prison walls when I was first brought here.", "농담이라곤 도무지 알아듣지 못하고, 영혼이 메말랐어, 메마르고 납작해빠졌지. 그자를 보면 내가 여기 처음 끌려왔을 때의 감옥 벽이 떠올라."], ["But he is a clever fellow, very clever.", "하지만 똑똑한 놈이야, 아주 똑똑하지."], ["Well, Alexey, it’s all over with me now.”", "그건 그렇고, 알렉세이, 이제 나는 다 끝났다.\""]], [["He sat down on the bench and made Alyosha sit down beside him.", "그는 벤치에 앉으며 알료샤를 제 곁에 앉혔다."]], [["“Yes, the trial’s to-morrow.", "\"그래, 재판이 내일이지."], ["Are you so hopeless, brother?”", "형은 그렇게도 가망이 없는 거야?\""], ["Alyosha said, with an apprehensive feeling.", "알료샤가 불안한 마음으로 말했다."]], [["“What are you talking about?”", "\"무슨 소리를 하는 거냐?\""], ["said Mitya, looking at him rather uncertainly.", "미탸가 다소 멍한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Oh, you mean the trial!", "\"아, 재판 말이로구나!"], ["Damn it all!", "빌어먹을!"], ["Till now we’ve been talking of things that don’t matter, about this trial, but I haven’t said a word to you about the chief thing.", "지금까지 우리는 별것 아닌 것들, 이 재판 얘기만 하고 있었지, 정작 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 너한테 한마디도 안 했다."], ["Yes, the trial is to-morrow; but it wasn’t the trial I meant, when I said it was all over with me.", "그래, 재판은 내일이야. 하지만 내가 다 끝났다고 했을 때 말한 건 재판이 아니었어."], ["Why do you look at me so critically?”", "왜 그렇게 따지는 눈으로 나를 보는 거냐?\""]], [["“What do you mean, Mitya?”", "\"무슨 말이야, 미탸?\""]], [["“Ideas, ideas, that’s all!", "\"관념, 관념, 그게 전부야!"], ["Ethics!", "윤리학 말이다!"], ["What is ethics?”", "윤리학이란 게 뭐냐?\""]], [["“Ethics?”", "\"윤리학?\""], ["asked Alyosha, wondering.", "알료샤가 어리둥절해하며 물었다."]], [["“Yes; is it a science?”", "\"그래, 그게 학문이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, there is such a science ...", "\"그래, 그런 학문이 있긴 한데…"], ["but ...", "하지만…"], ["I confess I can’t explain to you what sort of science it is.”", "솔직히 그게 어떤 학문인지 형한테 설명할 수가 없네.\""]], [["“Rakitin knows.", "\"라키틴은 알아."], ["Rakitin knows a lot, damn him!", "그놈은 아는 게 많지, 제기랄!"], ["He’s not going to be a monk.", "그놈은 수도사가 되지 않을 거야."], ["He means to go to Petersburg.", "페테르부르크로 갈 작정이지."], ["There he’ll go in for criticism of an elevating tendency.", "거기서 고상한 경향의 비평이라는 걸 할 거다."], ["Who knows, he may be of use and make his own career, too.", "누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 쓸모도 있고 제 출셋길도 닦을지."], ["Ough!", "으!"], ["they are first-rate, these people, at making a career!", "정말 일류라니까, 그런 인간들은, 출세하는 데는 말이야."], ["Damn ethics, I am done for, Alexey, I am, you man of God!", "윤리학이고 뭐고 다 집어치워, 난 끝장났어, 알렉세이, 끝장났다고, 너 같은 하느님의 사람아!"], ["I love you more than any one.", "난 그 누구보다 너를 사랑해."], ["It makes my heart yearn to look at you.", "너를 보면 내 가슴이 사무친다."], ["Who was Karl Bernard?”", "그런데 카를 베르나르가 누구였더라?\""]], [["“Karl Bernard?”", "\"카를 베르나르?\""], ["Alyosha was surprised again.", "알료샤가 또다시 놀랐다."]], [["“No, not Karl.", "\"아니, 카를이 아니지."], ["Stay, I made a mistake.", "가만, 내가 틀렸군."], ["Claude Bernard.", "클로드 베르나르 말이야."], ["What was he?", "그 사람이 뭐였더라?"], ["Chemist or what?”", "화학자였나, 뭐였나?\""]], [["“He must be a savant,”", "\"학자임에는 틀림없겠지.\""], ["answered Alyosha; “but I confess I can’t tell you much about him, either.", "알료샤가 대답했다. \"하지만 솔직히 그 사람에 대해서도 내가 형한테 별로 해 줄 말이 없네."], ["I’ve heard of him as a savant, but what sort I don’t know.”", "학자라는 얘기는 들었는데, 어떤 사람인지는 몰라.\""]], [["“Well, damn him, then!", "\"그럼 그자도 빌어먹으라지!"], ["I don’t know either,”", "나도 모르겠다.\""], ["swore Mitya.", "미탸가 욕설을 퍼부었다."], ["“A scoundrel of some sort, most likely.", "\"필시 무슨 악당 같은 놈이겠지."], ["They are all scoundrels.", "그놈들은 죄다 악당이야."], ["And Rakitin will make his way.", "그리고 라키틴은 출세할 거다."], ["Rakitin will get on anywhere; he is another Bernard.", "라키틴은 어디서든 잘나갈 놈이야. 그자도 또 하나의 베르나르지."], ["Ugh, these Bernards!", "으, 이놈의 베르나르들!"], ["They are all over the place.”", "천지에 깔렸어.\""]], [["“But what is the matter?”", "\"그런데 대체 무슨 일이야?\""], ["Alyosha asked insistently.", "알료샤가 끈질기게 물었다."]], [["“He wants to write an article about me, about my case, and so begin his literary career.", "\"그놈이 나에 대해, 내 사건에 대해 글을 써서 그걸로 제 문필 경력을 시작하려는 거야. 그래서 여기 오는 거지."], ["That’s what he comes for; he said so himself.", "자기 입으로 그렇게 말하더라. 무슨 이론인가를 증명하고 싶어 해."], ["He wants to prove some theory.", "'그는 아버지를 죽이지 않을 수 없었다, 환경 탓에 타락한 것이다'"], ["He wants to say ‘he couldn’t help murdering his father, he was corrupted by his environment,’ and so on.", "어쩌고저쩌고하고 싶다는 거지. 나한테 전부 설명하더군."], ["He explained it all to me.", "거기에다 사회주의 색채를 좀 가미하겠다는 거야."], ["He is going to put in a tinge of Socialism, he says.", "하지만 뭐, 그놈은 빌어먹으라지, 색채를 가미하고 싶으면 가미하라지, 난 상관없어. 그놈은 이반을 못 견뎌 해, 이반을 미워하지."], ["But there, damn the fellow, he can put in a tinge if he likes, I don’t care.", "너도 좋아하지 않아. 하지만 난 그놈을 내쫓지 않는다, 똑똑한 놈이니까."], ["He can’t bear Ivan, he hates him.", "그래도 끔찍하게 자만심이 강하지. 방금 그놈한테 이렇게 말해 줬다."], ["He’s not fond of you, either.", "'카라마조프 집안 사람들은 비열한 놈들이 아니라 철학자들이야. 참된 러시아인은 다 철학자거든."], ["But I don’t turn him out, for he is a clever fellow.", "그런데 자네는 공부를 했어도 철학자가 아니야 ― 자네는 천박한 놈이지.' 그놈이 웃더라, 어찌나 악의에 차서 웃던지."], ["Awfully conceited, though.", "그래서 내가 또 이렇게 말해 줬지. '데 이데아부스 논 에스트 디스푸탄둠.'"], ["I said to him just now, ‘The Karamazovs are not blackguards, but philosophers; for all true Russians are philosophers, and though you’ve studied, you are not a philosopher—you are a low fellow.’ He laughed, so maliciously.", "제법 그럴듯하지 않냐? 봐라, 나도 고전학자 행세를 할 수 있다고!\""], ["And I said to him, ‘De ideabus non est disputandum.’ Isn’t that rather good?", "미탸가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다. * 데 이데아부스 논 에스트 디스푸탄둠(De ideabus non est disputandum): \"관념에 관해서는 논쟁할 것이 없다\"라는 뜻의 라틴어."], ["I can set up for being a classic, you see!” Mitya laughed suddenly.", "\"취향에 관해서는 논쟁할 것이 없다(De gustibus non est disputandum)\"라는 격언을 미탸가 익살스럽게 바꾼 것이다."]], [["“Why is it all over with you?", "\"형은 왜 다 끝났다는 거야?"], ["You said so just now,”", "방금 그렇게 말했잖아.\""], ["Alyosha interposed.", "알료샤가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Why is it all over with me?", "\"내가 왜 끝장났느냐고?"], ["H’m!...", "흠!…"], ["The fact of it is ...", "사실인즉슨…"], ["if you take it as a whole, I am sorry to lose God—that’s why it is.”", "통틀어 말하자면, 난 하느님을 잃는 게 안타까워 ― 그래서 그런 거다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘sorry to lose God’?”", "\"'하느님을 잃는 게 안타깝다'니, 그게 무슨 말이야?\""]], [["“Imagine: inside, in the nerves, in the head—that is, these nerves are there in the brain ...", "\"한번 상상해 봐라. 몸 안에, 신경 속에, 머릿속에 ― 그러니까 이 신경이라는 게 뇌 속에 있는데…"], ["(damn them!) there are sort of little tails, the little tails of those nerves, and as soon as they begin quivering ...", "(빌어먹을 것들!) 거기에 무슨 작은 꼬리 같은 게 있어, 그 신경들의 작은 꼬리들 말이야, 그게 떨리기 시작하면…"], ["that is, you see, I look at something with my eyes and then they begin quivering, those little tails ...", "그러니까, 봐라, 내가 무언가를 눈으로 보면 그것들이 떨리기 시작하는 거야, 그 작은 꼬리들이…"], ["and when they quiver, then an image appears ...", "그리고 그것들이 떨리면 어떤 상(像)이 떠오르지…"], ["it doesn’t appear at once, but an instant, a second, passes ...", "곧바로 떠오르는 건 아니고, 한순간, 일 초가 지나야… 그러고 나서 무슨 한 찰나 같은 게 떠올라."], ["and then something like a moment appears; that is, not a moment—devil take the moment!—but an image; that is, an object, or an action, damn it!", "그러니까, 찰나가 아니라 ― 찰나 따위 악마한테나 잡아먹히라지! ― 상이지."], ["That’s why I see and then think, because of those tails, not at all because I’ve got a soul, and that I am some sort of image and likeness.", "그러니까 대상, 아니면 행동, 빌어먹을! 그래서 나는 보고, 그다음에 생각하는 거야, 그 꼬리들 때문에 말이지, 내게 영혼이 있어서가 결코 아니고, 내가 무슨 하느님의 형상이자 모상이라서가 아니란 말이다."], ["All that is nonsense!", "그딴 건 죄다 헛소리야!"], ["Rakitin explained it all to me yesterday, brother, and it simply bowled me over.", "라키틴이 어제 나한테 전부 설명해 줬는데, 형제여, 그게 그냥 나를 나가떨어지게 만들었어."], ["It’s magnificent, Alyosha, this science!", "굉장하더라, 알료샤, 이 과학이라는 게!"], ["A new man’s arising—that I understand....", "새로운 인간이 일어서고 있다 ― 그건 나도 알겠어…"], ["And yet I am sorry to lose God!”", "그런데도 난 하느님을 잃는 게 안타깝단 말이야!\""]], [["“Well, that’s a good thing, anyway,”", "\"뭐, 어쨌든 그건 좋은 일이네.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“That I am sorry to lose God?", "\"내가 하느님을 잃는 걸 안타까워하는 게?"], ["It’s chemistry, brother, chemistry!", "그건 화학이야, 형제여, 화학이라고!"], ["There’s no help for it, your reverence, you must make way for chemistry.", "어쩔 도리가 없지, 신부님, 화학에 자리를 비켜 줘야 한다 이거야."], ["And Rakitin does dislike God. Ough!", "그리고 라키틴은 정말 하느님을 싫어해."], ["doesn’t he dislike Him!", "으! 어찌나 싫어하는지!"], ["That’s the sore point with all of them.", "그게 바로 그놈들 모두의 아픈 데지. 그런데 다들 그걸 감춰."], ["But they conceal it.", "거짓말을 한다고. 시치미를 떼."], ["They tell lies.", "'자네 비평에 그런 걸 설파할 텐가?'"], ["They pretend.", "내가 물었지."], ["‘Will you preach this in your reviews?’ I asked him.", "'오, 글쎄, 대놓고 그러면 통과시켜 주질 않을 텐데.' 하더라."], ["‘Oh, well, if I did it openly, they won’t let it through,’ he said.", "웃으면서 말이야. '그럼 사람들은 어떻게 되는 건가?'"], ["He laughed.", "내가 물었어. '하느님도, 영생도 없이?"], ["‘But what will become of men then?’ I asked him, ‘without God and immortal life?", "그렇다면 모든 것이 허용되는 거 아닌가, 마음대로 해도 되는 거 아니냐고?' '몰랐나?'"], ["All things are lawful then, they can do what they like?’ ‘Didn’t you know?’ he said laughing, ‘a clever man can do what he likes,’ he said.", "그놈이 웃으며 말하더라. '똑똑한 자는 뭐든 마음대로 할 수 있어."], ["‘A clever man knows his way about, but you’ve put your foot in it, committing a murder, and now you are rotting in prison.’ He says that to my face!", "똑똑한 자는 처세에 밝지. 그런데 자네는 살인이나 저지르고 발을 헛디뎌서, 지금 감옥에서 썩고 있는 거 아닌가.'"], ["A regular pig!", "내 면전에 대고 그러는 거야!"], ["I used to kick such people out, but now I listen to them.", "영락없는 돼지지! 예전 같으면 그런 놈들은 발로 차서 내쫓았을 텐데, 지금은 그놈들 말을 들어 준다."], ["He talks a lot of sense, too.", "사리에 맞는 말도 꽤 하긴 해."], ["Writes well.", "글도 잘 쓰고."], ["He began reading me an article last week.", "지난주에 나한테 글 한 편을 읽어 주기 시작하던데."], ["I copied out three lines of it.", "그중 세 줄을 베껴 뒀어."], ["Wait a minute.", "잠깐만."], ["Here it is.”", "여기 있다.\""]], [["Mitya hurriedly pulled out a piece of paper from his pocket and read:", "미탸는 황급히 주머니에서 종이쪽지를 꺼내 읽었다."]], [["“‘In order to determine this question, it is above all essential to put one’s personality in contradiction to one’s reality.’ Do you understand that?”", "\"'이 문제를 규명하기 위해서는 무엇보다도 자신의 인격을 자신의 현실과 모순 관계에 놓는 것이 긴요하다.' 이게 무슨 소린지 알겠냐?\""]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "\"아니, 모르겠는데.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."], ["He looked at Mitya and listened to him with curiosity.", "그는 미탸를 바라보며 호기심 어린 눈으로 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“I don’t understand either.", "\"나도 모르겠어."], ["It’s dark and obscure, but intellectual.", "어둡고 모호하지만, 어쨌든 지적이지."], ["‘Every one writes like that now,’ he says, ‘it’s the effect of their environment.’ They are afraid of the environment.", "'요즘은 다들 이렇게 써.' 그놈이 그러더라."], ["He writes poetry, too, the rascal.", "'환경의 영향이지.' 그놈들은 환경을 두려워해."], ["He’s written in honor of Madame Hohlakov’s foot.", "그놈은 시도 쓴다, 그 악당 같은 놈이. 호흘라코바 부인의 발을 기리는 시를 썼지."], ["Ha ha ha!”", "하하하!\""]], [["“I’ve heard about it,”", "\"그 얘기는 들었어.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Have you?", "\"들었어?"], ["And have you heard the poem?”", "그 시도 들어 봤냐?\" \"아니.\""]], [["“No.” “I’ve got it.", "\"나한테 있어."], ["Here it is.", "여기 있다."], ["I’ll read it to you.", "너한테 읽어 줄게."], ["You don’t know—I haven’t told you—there’s quite a story about it.", "너는 모르지 ― 내가 말 안 했으니까 ― 거기엔 제법 사연이 있어."], ["He’s a rascal!", "그놈은 악당이야!"], ["Three weeks ago he began to tease me.", "삼 주 전에 나를 놀려대기 시작하더라."], ["‘You’ve got yourself into a mess, like a fool, for the sake of three thousand, but I’m going to collar a hundred and fifty thousand.", "'자네는 고작 삼천 루블 때문에 바보같이 곤경에 빠졌지만, 나는 십오만 루블을 거머쥘 거야. 과부와 결혼해서 페테르부르크에 집을 살 거라고.'"], ["I am going to marry a widow and buy a house in Petersburg.’ And he told me he was courting Madame Hohlakov.", "그러고는 자기가 호흘라코바 부인한테 구애하고 있다고 털어놓더군. 그 부인은 젊었을 때도 별로 머리가 없었는데, 이제 마흔이 되니 그나마 있던 것마저 잃어버렸다는 거야."], ["She hadn’t much brains in her youth, and now at forty she has lost what she had.", "'하지만 그 여자는 끔찍하게 감상적이거든.' 그놈이 그러더라."], ["‘But she’s awfully sentimental,’ he says; ‘that’s how I shall get hold of her.", "'그 점을 노려서 사로잡을 거야. 결혼하면 그 여자를 페테르부르크로 데려가서 거기서 신문을 창간할 거다.'"], ["When I marry her, I shall take her to Petersburg and there I shall start a newspaper.’ And his mouth was simply watering, the beast, not for the widow, but for the hundred and fifty thousand.", "그러면서 그놈은 입에 침이 마를 지경이었어, 그 짐승 같은 놈이, 과부가 탐나서가 아니라 십오만 루블이 탐나서 말이야. 그러고는 나도 그 말을 믿게 만들었지."], ["And he made me believe it.", "날마다 나를 보러 왔어."], ["He came to see me every day.", "'여자가 넘어오고 있어.'"], ["‘She is coming round,’ he declared.", "하고 큰소리쳤지. 기쁨에 겨워 환하게 빛나더라."], ["He was beaming with delight.", "그런데 그러다가, 별안간 그 집에서 쫓겨났지 뭐냐."], ["And then, all of a sudden, he was turned out of the house.", "페르호틴이 모든 걸 휩쓸어 버린 거야, 잘했다! 그놈을 집에서 내쫓아 준 그 어리석은 늙은 멍청이한테 입이라도 맞춰 주고 싶다."], ["Perhotin’s carrying everything before him, bravo!", "아무튼 그놈이 이 엉터리 시를 썼던 거야."], ["I could kiss the silly old noodle for turning him out of the house.", "'내가 시를 쓰느라 손을 더럽힌 건 이번이 처음이야.' 그놈이 그러더라."], ["And he had written this doggerel.", "'여자의 마음을 얻으려는 거니까 좋은 명분이지."], ["‘It’s the first time I’ve soiled my hands with writing poetry,’ he said.", "그 어리석은 여자의 재산을 손에 넣으면 사회에 크게 이바지할 수 있을 거다.' 그놈들은 자기네가 저지르는 온갖 더러운 짓마다 이런 사회적 명분을 갖다 붙인다니까!"], ["‘It’s to win her heart, so it’s in a good cause.", "'어쨌든 이건 자네가 떠받드는 푸시킨의 시보다는 낫지.'"], ["When I get hold of the silly woman’s fortune, I can be of great social utility.’ They have this social justification for every nasty thing they do!", "그놈이 그러더라. '난 이 시에서까지 계몽을 옹호하는 데 성공했으니까 말이야.'"], ["‘Anyway it’s better than your Pushkin’s poetry,’ he said, ‘for I’ve managed to advocate enlightenment even in that.’ I understand what he means about Pushkin, I quite see that, if he really was a man of talent and only wrote about women’s feet.", "푸시킨에 대해 그놈이 무슨 말을 하려는 건지는 나도 알아, 충분히 알겠어. 푸시킨이 정말 재능 있는 사람이었으면서도 고작 여자 발 따위나 노래했다면 말이지."], ["But wasn’t Rakitin stuck up about his doggerel!", "그런데 라키틴 그놈, 자기 엉터리 시를 어찌나 우쭐대던지!"], ["The vanity of these fellows!", "그런 놈들의 허영이라니!"], ["‘On the convalescence of the swollen foot of the object of my affections’—he thought of that for a title.", "'내 사랑하는 임의 부어오른 발의 쾌유에 부쳐' ― 그게 그놈이 생각해 낸 제목이야."], ["He’s a waggish fellow.", "익살맞은 놈이지."]], [["A captivating little foot, Though swollen and red and tender!", "매혹적인 작은 발이여, 부어오르고 붉어지고 쓰라릴지언정!"], ["The doctors come and plasters put, But still they cannot mend her.", "의사들이 와서 고약을 붙여 보지만 그래도 그녀를 고치지 못하네."]], [["Yet, ’tis not for her foot I dread— A theme for Pushkin’s muse more fit— It’s not her foot, it is her head: I tremble for her loss of wit!", "허나 내가 두려운 건 그녀의 발이 아니라네 ― 그것은 차라리 푸시킨의 시신(詩神)에 어울릴 주제, 그녀의 발이 아니라 그녀의 머리일세, 나는 그녀가 정신을 잃을까 떠노라!"]], [["For as her foot swells, strange to say, Her intellect is on the wane— Oh, for some remedy I pray That may restore both foot and brain!", "이상하게도 그녀의 발이 부어오를수록 그녀의 지력은 시들어 가니 ― 오, 나는 기도하노라, 무슨 묘약이 있어 발과 두뇌를 함께 되살려 주기를!"]], [["He is a pig, a regular pig, but he’s very arch, the rascal!", "그놈은 돼지야, 영락없는 돼지지, 하지만 아주 능청맞다, 그 악당 같은 놈!"], ["And he really has put in a progressive idea.", "게다가 정말로 진보적인 사상을 집어넣었단 말이야."], ["And wasn’t he angry when she kicked him out!", "그런데 그 부인이 그놈을 쫓아냈을 때 어찌나 화를 내던지!"], ["He was gnashing his teeth!”", "이를 갈더라니까!\""]], [["“He’s taken his revenge already,”", "\"그놈은 벌써 복수를 했어.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."], ["“He’s written a paragraph about Madame Hohlakov.”", "\"호흘라코바 부인에 대해 단평을 한 편 썼지.\""]], [["And Alyosha told him briefly about the paragraph in Gossip.", "그러고 나서 알료샤는 〈소문〉지에 실린 그 단평에 대해 간략하게 이야기해 주었다."]], [["“That’s his doing, that’s his doing!”", "\"그놈 짓이야, 그놈 짓이라고!\""], ["Mitya assented, frowning.", "미탸가 인상을 찌푸리며 맞장구쳤다."], ["“That’s him!", "\"그놈이 한 거지!"], ["These paragraphs ...", "그 단평들이라는 게…"], ["I know ...", "나도 알아…"], ["the insulting things that have been written about Grushenka, for instance....", "이를테면 그루셴카에 대해 쓰인 모욕적인 글들 말이야…"], ["And about Katya, too....", "그리고 카탸에 대해서도…"], ["H’m!”", "흠!\""]], [["He walked across the room with a harassed air.", "그는 시달린 듯한 얼굴로 방을 가로질러 거닐었다."]], [["“Brother, I cannot stay long,”", "\"형, 나 오래 있을 수가 없어.\""], ["Alyosha said, after a pause.", "잠시 침묵하다가 알료샤가 말했다."], ["“To-morrow will be a great and awful day for you, the judgment of God will be accomplished ...", "\"내일은 형에게 크고도 두려운 날이 될 거야, 하느님의 심판이 이루어질 거라고…"], ["I am amazed at you, you walk about here, talking of I don’t know what ...”", "난 형이 놀라워, 이렇게 여기서 거닐며 뭔지 모를 얘기들만 하고 있으니…\""]], [["“No, don’t be amazed at me,”", "\"아니, 나를 놀라워하지 마라.\""], ["Mitya broke in warmly.", "미탸가 따뜻하게 가로막았다."], ["“Am I to talk of that stinking dog?", "\"내가 그 악취 나는 개 같은 놈 얘기를 해야겠냐?"], ["Of the murderer?", "그 살인자 얘기를?"], ["We’ve talked enough of him.", "그놈 얘기는 할 만큼 했어."], ["I don’t want to say more of the stinking son of Stinking Lizaveta!", "그 악취 풍기는 리자베타의 악취 나는 자식 얘기는 더는 하고 싶지 않다고!"], ["God will kill him, you will see.", "하느님이 그놈을 죽이실 거야, 두고 봐라."], ["Hush!”", "쉿!\""]], [["He went up to Alyosha excitedly and kissed him.", "그는 흥분하여 알료샤에게 다가가더니 그에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["His eyes glowed.", "그의 두 눈이 이글거렸다."]], [["“Rakitin wouldn’t understand it,”", "\"라키틴은 이걸 이해하지 못할 거야.\""], ["he began in a sort of exaltation; “but you, you’ll understand it all.", "그는 일종의 황홀경에 빠져 입을 열었다. \"하지만 너는, 너는 이 모든 걸 이해할 거야."], ["That’s why I was thirsting for you.", "그래서 너를 목말라하며 기다린 거다."], ["You see, there’s so much I’ve been wanting to tell you for ever so long, here, within these peeling walls, but I haven’t said a word about what matters most; the moment never seems to have come.", "봐라, 정말 오래전부터 너한테 하고 싶었던 얘기가 어찌나 많았던지, 바로 여기, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 말이야, 그런데도 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 한마디도 못 했어. 도무지 그럴 순간이 오지 않는 것 같았거든."], ["Now I can wait no longer.", "이제는 더 기다릴 수가 없다."], ["I must pour out my heart to you.", "너한테 내 마음을 다 쏟아 내야겠어."], ["Brother, these last two months I’ve found in myself a new man.", "형제여, 지난 두 달 동안 나는 내 안에서 새로운 인간을 발견했다."], ["A new man has risen up in me.", "내 안에서 새로운 인간이 일어선 거야."], ["He was hidden in me, but would never have come to the surface, if it hadn’t been for this blow from heaven.", "그건 내 안에 숨어 있었는데, 하늘에서 내려온 이 일격이 없었다면 결코 표면으로 떠오르지 못했을 거다."], ["I am afraid!", "나는 두려워!"], ["And what do I care if I spend twenty years in the mines, breaking ore with a hammer?", "그런데 내가 광산에서 망치로 광석을 깨며 이십 년을 보낸들 그게 무슨 대수겠냐?"], ["I am not a bit afraid of that—it’s something else I am afraid of now: that that new man may leave me.", "그런 건 조금도 두렵지 않아 ― 지금 내가 두려운 건 다른 거야, 그 새로운 인간이 나를 떠나 버릴까 봐 두려운 거지."], ["Even there, in the mines, under-ground, I may find a human heart in another convict and murderer by my side, and I may make friends with him, for even there one may live and love and suffer.", "거기서도, 광산에서도, 땅속에서도, 내 곁에 있는 또 다른 죄수, 또 다른 살인자 안에서 인간의 마음을 발견할 수 있을 거야, 그리고 그자와 벗이 될 수도 있겠지, 거기서도 사람은 살고 사랑하고 고통받을 수 있으니까."], ["One may thaw and revive a frozen heart in that convict, one may wait upon him for years, and at last bring up from the dark depths a lofty soul, a feeling, suffering creature; one may bring forth an angel, create a hero!", "그 죄수의 얼어붙은 마음을 녹이고 되살릴 수도 있어, 몇 해고 그를 정성껏 돌볼 수도 있고, 마침내는 그 어두운 심연으로부터 고결한 영혼을, 느끼고 고통받는 존재를 끌어올릴 수도 있는 거야. 천사를 길러 낼 수도 있고, 영웅을 만들어 낼 수도 있어!"], ["There are so many of them, hundreds of them, and we are all to blame for them.", "그런 자들이 어찌나 많은지, 수백 명이나 되지, 그리고 우리 모두에게 그들에 대한 책임이 있는 거다."], ["Why was it I dreamed of that ‘babe’ at such a moment?", "어째서 나는 하필 그런 순간에 그 '갓난아이' 꿈을 꾸었을까?"], ["‘Why is the babe so poor?’ That was a sign to me at that moment.", "'어째서 갓난아이는 저토록 가난한가?' 그건 그 순간 나에게 주어진 하나의 징표였어."], ["It’s for the babe I’m going.", "나는 그 갓난아이를 위해 가는 거다."], ["Because we are all responsible for all.", "우리 모두가 모두에 대해 책임이 있으니까."], ["For all the ‘babes,’ for there are big children as well as little children.", "모든 '갓난아이들'에 대해서 말이야, 큰 아이들도 있고 작은 아이들도 있으니까."], ["All are ‘babes.’ I go for all, because some one must go for all.", "모두가 '갓난아이들'이지. 나는 모두를 위해 간다, 누군가는 모두를 위해 가야 하니까."], ["I didn’t kill father, but I’ve got to go.", "나는 아버지를 죽이지 않았지만, 그래도 가야만 해."], ["I accept it.", "나는 그걸 받아들이겠어."], ["It’s all come to me here, here, within these peeling walls.", "이 모든 게 여기서, 바로 여기서, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 나에게 찾아온 거야."], ["There are numbers of them there, hundreds of them underground, with hammers in their hands.", "거기엔 그런 자들이 숱하게 있어, 땅속에 수백 명씩, 손에 망치를 들고 말이지."], ["Oh, yes, we shall be in chains and there will be no freedom, but then, in our great sorrow, we shall rise again to joy, without which man cannot live nor God exist, for God gives joy: it’s His privilege—a grand one.", "오, 그래, 우리는 쇠사슬에 묶일 테고 자유라곤 없겠지, 하지만 그때, 그 크나큰 슬픔 속에서 우리는 다시 기쁨으로 일어설 거야, 그 기쁨 없이는 인간도 살 수 없고 하느님도 존재하실 수 없는 그 기쁨으로 말이야, 하느님이 기쁨을 주시니까. 그건 그분의 특권이지 ― 위대한 특권이야."], ["Ah, man should be dissolved in prayer!", "아, 인간은 기도 속에 녹아들어야 해!"], ["What should I be underground there without God?", "하느님이 없다면 거기 땅속에서 내가 무엇이 되겠냐?"], ["Rakitin’s laughing!", "라키틴은 비웃지!"], ["If they drive God from the earth, we shall shelter Him underground.", "그놈들이 하느님을 이 땅에서 몰아낸다 해도, 우리는 그분을 땅속에 모실 거야."], ["One cannot exist in prison without God; it’s even more impossible than out of prison.", "감옥 안에서는 하느님 없이 존재할 수 없어. 감옥 밖에서보다 더 불가능한 일이지."], ["And then we men underground will sing from the bowels of the earth a glorious hymn to God, with Whom is joy.", "그리고 그때 우리 땅속의 인간들은 대지의 깊은 곳에서 하느님께, 기쁨이 함께하시는 그분께 영광스러운 찬가를 부를 거야."], ["Hail to God and His joy!", "하느님과 그분의 기쁨에 영광 있으라!"], ["I love Him!”", "나는 그분을 사랑한다!\""]], [["Mitya was almost gasping for breath as he uttered his wild speech.", "미탸는 이 격정적인 말을 쏟아 내며 거의 숨을 헐떡일 지경이었다."], ["He turned pale, his lips quivered, and tears rolled down his cheeks.", "그의 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 입술이 떨렸으며, 두 뺨으로 눈물이 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Yes, life is full, there is life even underground,”", "\"그래, 삶은 충만한 거야, 땅속에도 삶은 있어.\""], ["he began again.", "그가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“You wouldn’t believe, Alexey, how I want to live now, what a thirst for existence and consciousness has sprung up in me within these peeling walls.", "\"넌 못 믿을 거다, 알렉세이, 내가 지금 얼마나 살고 싶은지, 이 벗겨진 벽들 안에서 존재와 의식에 대한 갈증이 내 안에 얼마나 솟구쳤는지 말이야."], ["Rakitin doesn’t understand that; all he cares about is building a house and letting flats.", "라키틴은 그걸 이해 못 해. 그놈이 신경 쓰는 거라곤 집을 짓고 셋방을 놓는 것뿐이지."], ["But I’ve been longing for you.", "하지만 나는 너를 그리워해 왔어."], ["And what is suffering?", "그리고 고통이 뭐란 말이냐?"], ["I am not afraid of it, even if it were beyond reckoning.", "난 그게 두렵지 않아, 설령 그게 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 크다 해도 말이지."], ["I am not afraid of it now.", "이제 난 그게 두렵지 않아."], ["I was afraid of it before.", "전에는 두려웠지만."], ["Do you know, perhaps I won’t answer at the trial at all....", "알아 두렴, 어쩌면 나는 재판에서 아예 입을 열지 않을지도 몰라…"], ["And I seem to have such strength in me now, that I think I could stand anything, any suffering, only to be able to say and to repeat to myself every moment, ‘I exist.’ In thousands of agonies—I exist.", "그리고 지금 내 안에는 무슨 힘이 가득 차 있는 것 같아, 어떤 것이든, 어떤 고통이든 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같다고, 다만 매 순간 나 자신에게 이렇게 말하고 또 말할 수만 있다면 말이야. '나는 존재한다.'"], ["I’m tormented on the rack—but I exist!", "수천 가지 고뇌 속에서도 ― 나는 존재한다."], ["Though I sit alone on a pillar—I exist!", "고문대 위에서 괴로워하면서도 ― 그래도 나는 존재한다!"], ["I see the sun, and if I don’t see the sun, I know it’s there.", "기둥 위에 홀로 앉아 있어도 ― 나는 존재한다! 나는 태양을 본다, 설령 태양을 보지 못한다 해도 그것이 거기 있음을 안다."], ["And there’s a whole life in that, in knowing that the sun is there.", "그리고 거기에, 태양이 거기 있음을 안다는 그 사실 안에 온전한 삶 하나가 담겨 있는 거야."], ["Alyosha, my angel, all these philosophies are the death of me.", "알료샤, 내 천사야, 이 모든 철학이 나를 죽이고 있어."], ["Damn them!", "빌어먹을 것들!"], ["Brother Ivan—”", "이반 형은―\""]], [["“What of brother Ivan?”", "\"이반 형이 어쨌다는 거야?\""], ["interrupted Alyosha, but Mitya did not hear.", "알료샤가 끼어들었으나 미탸는 듣지 못했다."]], [["“You see, I never had any of these doubts before, but it was all hidden away in me.", "\"봐라, 전에는 이런 의심을 조금도 품지 않았는데, 사실은 그 모든 게 내 안에 숨어 있었던 거야."], ["It was perhaps just because ideas I did not understand were surging up in me, that I used to drink and fight and rage.", "어쩌면 바로 내가 이해하지 못하는 관념들이 내 안에서 들끓고 있었기 때문에, 내가 술을 마시고 싸우고 미쳐 날뛰곤 했던 건지도 몰라."], ["It was to stifle them in myself, to still them, to smother them.", "그것들을 내 안에서 짓누르고, 잠재우고, 질식시키려고 말이지."], ["Ivan is not Rakitin, there is an idea in him.", "이반은 라키틴 같은 놈이 아니야, 그 안에는 관념이 있어."], ["Ivan is a sphinx and is silent; he is always silent.", "이반은 스핑크스고 입을 다물고 있어. 늘 침묵하지."], ["It’s God that’s worrying me.", "나를 괴롭히는 건 하느님이야."], ["That’s the only thing that’s worrying me.", "오직 그것만이 나를 괴롭히는 거지."], ["What if He doesn’t exist?", "만약 그분이 존재하지 않는다면?"], ["What if Rakitin’s right—that it’s an idea made up by men?", "만약 라키틴 말이 옳다면 ― 그게 인간들이 지어낸 관념일 뿐이라면?"], ["Then if He doesn’t exist, man is the chief of the earth, of the universe.", "그렇다면 그분이 존재하지 않는다면, 인간이 이 땅의, 우주의 으뜸이지."], ["Magnificent!", "굉장하지!"], ["Only how is he going to be good without God?", "다만 하느님 없이 어떻게 선하게 될 수 있을까?"], ["That’s the question.", "그게 문제야."], ["I always come back to that.", "나는 늘 그리로 되돌아온다."], ["For whom is man going to love then?", "그렇다면 인간은 누구를 사랑하게 될까?"], ["To whom will he be thankful?", "누구에게 감사하게 될까?"], ["To whom will he sing the hymn?", "누구에게 찬가를 부르게 될까?"], ["Rakitin laughs.", "라키틴은 비웃지."], ["Rakitin says that one can love humanity without God.", "라키틴은 하느님 없이도 인류를 사랑할 수 있다고 해."], ["Well, only a sniveling idiot can maintain that.", "흥, 그런 소리는 콧물이나 흘리는 멍청이나 늘어놓을 수 있는 거지."], ["I can’t understand it.", "난 도무지 이해할 수가 없어."], ["Life’s easy for Rakitin.", "라키틴한테는 삶이 쉽지."], ["‘You’d better think about the extension of civic rights, or even of keeping down the price of meat.", "'자네는 차라리 시민권 확대를 고민하거나, 아니면 고기값을 잡아 두는 게 낫지. 그게 철학보다 더 단순하고 직접적으로 인류에 대한 사랑을 보여 주는 길이야.'"], ["You will show your love for humanity more simply and directly by that, than by philosophy.’ I answered him, ‘Well, but you, without a God, are more likely to raise the price of meat, if it suits you, and make a rouble on every copeck.’ He lost his temper.", "내가 그놈한테 대꾸했지. '흥, 그렇지만 자네는 하느님이 없으니, 자네한테 유리하다면 오히려 고기값을 올려서, 한 코페이카마다 일 루블씩 남겨 먹기가 더 쉬울걸.'"], ["But after all, what is goodness?", "그놈이 발끈하더라. 그런데 결국, 선이란 게 뭐냐?"], ["Answer me that, Alexey.", "그것 좀 대답해 봐라, 알렉세이."], ["Goodness is one thing with me and another with a Chinaman, so it’s a relative thing.", "선이라는 게 나한테는 이런 것이고 중국인한테는 저런 것이라면, 그건 상대적인 거잖아."], ["Or isn’t it?", "안 그러냐?"], ["Is it not relative?", "상대적인 게 아니야?"], ["A treacherous question!", "음흉한 질문이지!"], ["You won’t laugh if I tell you it’s kept me awake two nights.", "이 질문 때문에 내가 이틀 밤이나 잠을 못 이뤘다고 하면 너는 웃지 않겠지."], ["I only wonder now how people can live and think nothing about it.", "난 그저 어떻게 사람들이 살아가면서 그것에 대해 아무 생각도 안 하는지 그게 궁금할 뿐이야. 다 허영이지!"], ["Vanity! Ivan has no God.", "이반은 하느님이 없어."], ["He has an idea.", "이반한테는 관념이 있지."], ["It’s beyond me.", "그건 내 능력 밖이야."], ["But he is silent.", "그런데 그는 침묵해."], ["I believe he is a free-mason.", "난 그가 프리메이슨이라고 믿는다."], ["I asked him, but he is silent.", "물어봤지만 입을 다물더라."], ["I wanted to drink from the springs of his soul—he was silent.", "나는 그의 영혼의 샘에서 물을 마시고 싶었어 ― 그런데 그는 침묵했어."], ["But once he did drop a word.”", "하지만 딱 한 번 말 한마디를 흘리긴 했지.\""]], [["“What did he say?”", "\"뭐라고 했는데?\""], ["Alyosha took it up quickly.", "알료샤가 얼른 받아 물었다."]], [["“I said to him, ‘Then everything is lawful, if it is so?’ He frowned. ‘Fyodor Pavlovitch, our papa,’ he said, ‘was a pig, but his ideas were right enough.’ That was what he dropped. That was all he said. That was going one better than Rakitin.”", "\"내가 그한테 물었어. '그렇다면 만약 그렇다면, 모든 것이 허용되는 거 아닌가?' 그가 인상을 찌푸리더라. '우리 아버지 표도르 파블로비치는' 하고 그가 말했어. '돼지였지만, 그분의 생각은 충분히 옳았어.' 그가 흘린 게 그거였어. 그가 한 말은 그게 다였지. 라키틴보다 한 수 위였던 거야.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["Alyosha assented bitterly.", "알료샤가 씁쓸하게 동의했다."], ["“When was he with you?”", "\"그 형이 언제 형한테 왔었어?\""]], [["“Of that later; now I must speak of something else.", "\"그건 나중에 얘기하자. 지금은 다른 걸 말해야 해."], ["I have said nothing about Ivan to you before.", "전에 너한테 이반에 대해서는 한마디도 안 했지."], ["I put it off to the last.", "맨 마지막으로 미뤄 뒀어."], ["When my business here is over and the verdict has been given, then I’ll tell you something.", "여기 내 일이 끝나고 판결이 내려지면, 그때 너한테 뭔가를 말해 줄게."], ["I’ll tell you everything.", "전부 말해 줄 거야."], ["We’ve something tremendous on hand....", "우리한테는 엄청난 일이 걸려 있어…"], ["And you shall be my judge in it.", "그리고 그 일에서 네가 나의 심판자가 되어 줘야 해."], ["But don’t begin about that now; be silent.", "하지만 지금은 그 얘기를 꺼내지 마, 잠자코 있어."], ["You talk of to-morrow, of the trial; but, would you believe it, I know nothing about it.”", "너는 내일 얘기를, 재판 얘기를 하지만, 믿어지겠냐, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다.\""]], [["“Have you talked to the counsel?”", "\"변호사하고는 얘기해 봤어?\""]], [["“What’s the use of the counsel?", "\"변호사가 무슨 소용이냐?"], ["I told him all about it.", "그한테 죄다 말했지."], ["He’s a soft, city-bred rogue—a Bernard!", "도시에서 자란 무르고 교활한 놈이야 ― 또 하나의 베르나르지!"], ["But he doesn’t believe me—not a bit of it.", "그런데 그놈은 나를 믿지 않아 ― 눈곱만큼도."], ["Only imagine, he believes I did it.", "한번 상상해 봐라, 그놈은 내가 했다고 믿는다니까. 다 보여."], ["I see it.", "'그렇다면'"], ["‘In that case,’ I asked him, ‘why have you come to defend me?’ Hang them all!", "하고 내가 물었지. '뭣 하러 나를 변호하러 온 거요?'"], ["They’ve got a doctor down, too, want to prove I’m mad.", "그놈들 다 뒈져 버려라! 게다가 의사까지 불러왔어, 내가 미쳤다는 걸 증명하려고 말이야."], ["I won’t have that!", "난 그딴 건 받아들이지 않아!"], ["Katerina Ivanovna wants to do her ‘duty’ to the end, whatever the strain!”", "카테리나 이바노브나는 무슨 일이 있어도 끝까지 자기 '의무'를 다하고 싶어 해, 그게 아무리 무리한 일이라도!\""], ["Mitya smiled bitterly. “The cat!", "미탸가 씁쓸하게 웃었다."], ["Hard-hearted creature!", "\"그 고양이 같은 여자! 매정한 것!"], ["She knows that I said of her at Mokroe that she was a woman of ‘great wrath.’ They repeated it.", "그 여자는 내가 모크로예에서 자기를 두고 '진노가 대단한' 여자라고 말한 걸 알고 있어."], ["Yes, the facts against me have grown numerous as the sands of the sea.", "그놈들이 그 말을 옮긴 거지. 그래, 나한테 불리한 사실들이 바다의 모래알처럼 불어났어."], ["Grigory sticks to his point.", "그리고리는 자기 주장을 굽히지 않아."], ["Grigory’s honest, but a fool.", "그리고리는 정직하지만 바보지."], ["Many people are honest because they are fools: that’s Rakitin’s idea.", "많은 사람들이 바보라서 정직한 거야. 그게 라키틴의 지론이지."], ["Grigory’s my enemy.", "그리고리는 내 적이야."], ["And there are some people who are better as foes than friends.", "그리고 어떤 사람들은 벗보다 적일 때가 더 나아."], ["I mean Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나 말이다."], ["I am afraid, oh, I am afraid she will tell how she bowed to the ground after that four thousand.", "나는 두려워, 오, 두렵다, 그 여자가 그 사천 루블 때문에 나한테 땅에 닿도록 절했던 일을 입에 올릴까 봐."], ["She’ll pay it back to the last farthing.", "그 여자는 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 거야."], ["I don’t want her sacrifice; they’ll put me to shame at the trial.", "난 그 여자의 희생을 원하지 않아. 그놈들은 재판에서 나에게 수치를 안길 거야."], ["I wonder how I can stand it.", "내가 어떻게 그걸 견딜지 모르겠다."], ["Go to her, Alyosha, ask her not to speak of that in the court, can’t you?", "그 여자한테 가 봐라, 알료샤, 법정에서 그 얘기를 하지 말아 달라고 부탁해 줘, 그럴 수 있겠냐?"], ["But damn it all, it doesn’t matter!", "하지만 다 빌어먹으라지, 상관없어!"], ["I shall get through somehow.", "어떻게든 헤쳐 나갈 테니까."], ["I don’t pity her.", "난 그 여자를 동정하지 않아."], ["It’s her own doing.", "그건 자업자득이야."], ["She deserves what she gets.", "받을 만한 대접을 받는 거지."], ["I shall have my own story to tell, Alexey.”", "나한테도 내 나름대로 할 얘기가 있을 테니까, 알렉세이.\""], ["He smiled bitterly again.", "그가 또다시 씁쓸하게 웃었다."], ["“Only ...", "\"다만…"], ["only Grusha, Grusha!", "다만 그루샤, 그루샤가 문제야!"], ["Good Lord!", "맙소사!"], ["Why should she have such suffering to bear?”", "어째서 그 여자가 그런 고통을 짊어져야 하는 거냐?\""], ["he exclaimed suddenly, with tears.", "그가 갑자기 눈물을 흘리며 외쳤다."], ["“Grusha’s killing me; the thought of her’s killing me, killing me.", "\"그루샤가 나를 죽이고 있어. 그 여자 생각이 나를 죽여, 죽인다고."], ["She was with me just now....”", "방금 전까지 그 여자가 나하고 함께 있었어…\""]], [["“She told me she was very much grieved by you to-day.”", "\"오늘 형 때문에 무척 마음이 상했다고 그러던데.\""]], [["“I know.", "\"알아."], ["Confound my temper!", "빌어먹을 내 성질!"], ["It was jealousy.", "질투였어."], ["I was sorry, I kissed her as she was going.", "미안했지, 그래서 떠날 때 입을 맞춰 줬어."], ["I didn’t ask her forgiveness.”", "용서를 빌지는 않았지만.\""]], [["“Why didn’t you?”", "\"왜 안 빌었어?\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["Suddenly Mitya laughed almost mirthfully.", "갑자기 미탸가 거의 즐겁다는 듯 웃었다."]], [["“God preserve you, my dear boy, from ever asking forgiveness for a fault from a woman you love.", "\"하느님이 너를 지켜 주시기를, 얘야, 네가 사랑하는 여자한테 잘못을 저질렀다고 용서를 비는 일이 결코 없도록 말이다."], ["From one you love especially, however greatly you may have been in fault.", "특히 네가 사랑하는 여자한테는, 네가 아무리 큰 잘못을 저질렀다 해도 말이야."], ["For a woman—devil only knows what to make of a woman!", "여자란 ― 여자라는 건 악마나 알까 무슨 수로 알겠냐!"], ["I know something about them, anyway.", "어쨌든 나는 여자에 대해 좀 알지."], ["But try acknowledging you are in fault to a woman.", "한번 여자한테 네 잘못을 인정해 봐라."], ["Say, ‘I am sorry, forgive me,’ and a shower of reproaches will follow!", "'미안해, 용서해 줘.' 하고 말해 봐, 그러면 비난이 소나기처럼 쏟아질걸!"], ["Nothing will make her forgive you simply and directly, she’ll humble you to the dust, bring forward things that have never happened, recall everything, forget nothing, add something of her own, and only then forgive you.", "무슨 수를 써도 여자가 너를 단순하고 직접적으로 용서해 주는 일은 없을 거야, 너를 흙먼지 속까지 짓밟아 놓고, 일어나지도 않은 일들을 끄집어내고, 죄다 들춰내고, 아무것도 잊지 않고, 거기에 제멋대로 뭔가를 보태고, 그러고 나서야 너를 용서할 거다."], ["And even the best, the best of them do it.", "가장 나은 여자, 가장 나은 여자라도 그렇게 한다고."], ["She’ll scrape up all the scrapings and load them on your head.", "부스러기란 부스러기는 죄다 긁어모아서 네 머리 위에 쏟아부을 거야."], ["They are ready to flay you alive, I tell you, every one of them, all these angels without whom we cannot live!", "그 여자들은 너를 산 채로 가죽을 벗길 태세야, 정말이지, 하나같이 다, 그 천사들 없이는 우리가 살 수 없는 바로 그 천사들이 말이지!"], ["I tell you plainly and openly, dear boy, every decent man ought to be under some woman’s thumb.", "분명하고 솔직하게 말하마, 얘야, 점잖은 사내라면 누구나 어떤 여자의 손아귀에 잡혀 있어야 하는 거야."], ["That’s my conviction—not conviction, but feeling.", "그게 내 신념이다 ― 신념이 아니라 느낌이지."], ["A man ought to be magnanimous, and it’s no disgrace to a man!", "사내는 너그러워야 해, 그건 사내한테 수치가 아니야!"], ["No disgrace to a hero, not even a Cæsar!", "영웅한테도, 카이사르한테조차도 수치가 아니지!"], ["But don’t ever beg her pardon all the same for anything.", "하지만 그래도 무슨 일이 있어도 절대로 여자한테 용서를 빌지는 마라."], ["Remember that rule given you by your brother Mitya, who’s come to ruin through women.", "여자들 때문에 신세를 망친 형 미탸가 너한테 일러 주는 그 규칙을 잊지 마."], ["No, I’d better make it up to Grusha somehow, without begging pardon.", "아니, 차라리 용서를 빌지 않고 어떻게든 그루샤한테 만회를 해야겠어."], ["I worship her, Alexey, worship her.", "난 그 여자를 떠받든다, 알렉세이, 떠받들어."], ["Only she doesn’t see it.", "다만 그 여자가 그걸 못 볼 뿐이야."], ["No, she still thinks I don’t love her enough.", "아니, 그 여자는 여전히 내가 자기를 충분히 사랑하지 않는다고 여겨."], ["And she tortures me, tortures me with her love.", "그래서 나를 괴롭혀, 자기 사랑으로 나를 괴롭힌다고."], ["The past was nothing!", "지난날은 아무것도 아니었어!"], ["In the past it was only those infernal curves of hers that tortured me, but now I’ve taken all her soul into my soul and through her I’ve become a man myself.", "지난날에는 그저 그 여자의 그 지옥 같은 몸의 곡선이 나를 괴롭혔지만, 지금은 그 여자의 영혼을 통째로 내 영혼 안에 받아들였고, 그 여자를 통해 나 자신이 비로소 사람이 된 거야."], ["Will they marry us?", "우리를 결혼시켜 줄까?"], ["If they don’t, I shall die of jealousy.", "안 시켜 주면 나는 질투로 죽고 말 거야."], ["I imagine something every day....", "날마다 별생각이 다 든다고…"], ["What did she say to you about me?”", "그 여자가 나에 대해 너한테 뭐라고 하던?\""]], [["Alyosha repeated all Grushenka had said to him that day.", "알료샤는 그날 그루셴카가 자기에게 했던 말을 전부 되풀이해 들려주었다."], ["Mitya listened, made him repeat things, and seemed pleased.", "미탸는 귀를 기울이며 몇몇 대목은 다시 말해 보라고 하더니, 흡족해하는 듯했다."]], [["“Then she is not angry at my being jealous?”", "\"그럼 그 여자는 내가 질투한 걸 화내지 않는 거구나?\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“She is a regular woman!", "\"영락없는 여자라니까!"], ["‘I’ve a fierce heart myself!’ Ah, I love such fierce hearts, though I can’t bear any one’s being jealous of me.", "'나도 마음이 거센 여자예요!' 아, 난 그런 거센 마음을 사랑해, 누가 나를 질투하는 건 못 견디지만 말이야."], ["I can’t endure it.", "그건 도무지 참을 수가 없어."], ["We shall fight.", "우린 싸우게 될 거야."], ["But I shall love her, I shall love her infinitely.", "하지만 난 그 여자를 사랑할 거야, 한없이 사랑할 거다."], ["Will they marry us?", "우리를 결혼시켜 줄까?"], ["Do they let convicts marry?", "죄수들도 결혼을 허락해 주나?"], ["That’s the question.", "그게 문제야."], ["And without her I can’t exist....”", "그 여자 없이는 나는 살 수가 없어…\""]], [["Mitya walked frowning across the room.", "미탸는 인상을 찌푸린 채 방을 가로질러 거닐었다."], ["It was almost dark.", "방 안은 거의 어두워져 있었다."], ["He suddenly seemed terribly worried.", "그는 갑자기 끔찍하게 괴로워하는 듯했다."]], [["“So there’s a secret, she says, a secret?", "\"그래, 비밀이 있다고 그 여자가 그러더라, 비밀이?"], ["We have got up a plot against her, and Katya is mixed up in it, she thinks.", "우리가 자기를 두고 음모를 꾸미고 있고, 거기에 카탸가 한몫 끼어 있다고 여기는 거지."], ["No, my good Grushenka, that’s not it.", "아니야, 사랑스러운 내 그루셴카, 그게 아니란다."], ["You are very wide of the mark, in your foolish feminine way.", "너는 그 어리석은 여자 특유의 방식으로 한참 빗나간 거야."], ["Alyosha, darling, well, here goes!", "알료샤, 얘야, 자, 어디 해 보자!"], ["I’ll tell you our secret!”", "너한테 우리 비밀을 말해 주마!\""]], [["He looked round, went close up quickly to Alyosha, who was standing before him, and whispered to him with an air of mystery, though in reality no one could hear them: the old warder was dozing in the corner, and not a word could reach the ears of the soldiers on guard.", "그는 주위를 둘러보더니 자기 앞에 서 있던 알료샤에게 재빨리 바짝 다가가, 무언가 은밀한 기색으로 속삭였다. 사실은 아무도 그들의 말을 들을 수 없었는데도 말이다. 늙은 간수는 구석에서 졸고 있었고, 보초를 서는 병사들의 귀에는 단 한마디도 닿을 수 없었다."]], [["“I will tell you all our secret,”", "\"우리 비밀을 전부 말해 줄게.\""], ["Mitya whispered hurriedly.", "미탸가 황급히 속삭였다."], ["“I meant to tell you later, for how could I decide on anything without you?", "\"나중에 말하려고 했어, 너 없이 내가 어떻게 무슨 결정을 내릴 수 있겠냐?"], ["You are everything to me.", "너는 내게 모든 것이야."], ["Though I say that Ivan is superior to us, you are my angel.", "내가 이반이 우리보다 한 수 위라고 말하긴 하지만, 너는 나의 천사다."], ["It’s your decision will decide it.", "그건 너의 결정으로 결판날 일이야."], ["Perhaps it’s you that is superior and not Ivan.", "어쩌면 한 수 위인 건 이반이 아니라 너일지도 몰라."], ["You see, it’s a question of conscience, question of the higher conscience—the secret is so important that I can’t settle it myself, and I’ve put it off till I could speak to you.", "봐라, 이건 양심의 문제야, 더 높은 양심의 문제지 ― 그 비밀이 어찌나 중대한지 나 혼자서는 결판을 낼 수가 없어서, 너한테 말할 수 있을 때까지 미뤄 둔 거다."], ["But anyway it’s too early to decide now, for we must wait for the verdict.", "하지만 어쨌든 지금 결정하기엔 너무 일러, 판결을 기다려야 하니까."], ["As soon as the verdict is given, you shall decide my fate.", "판결이 내려지는 즉시 네가 내 운명을 결정해 줘."], ["Don’t decide it now.", "지금은 결정하지 마."], ["I’ll tell you now.", "지금 너한테 말은 해 줄게."], ["You listen, but don’t decide.", "너는 듣기만 하고, 결정은 하지 마."], ["Stand and keep quiet.", "가만히 서서 잠자코 있어."], ["I won’t tell you everything.", "전부 말해 주진 않을 거야."], ["I’ll only tell you the idea, without details, and you keep quiet.", "그저 그 생각만, 세세한 건 빼고 말해 줄 테니, 너는 잠자코 있어."], ["Not a question, not a movement.", "질문도 하지 말고, 꼼짝도 하지 마."], ["You agree?", "알겠지?"], ["But, goodness, what shall I do with your eyes?", "그런데, 맙소사, 네 그 눈은 어쩐다?"], ["I’m afraid your eyes will tell me your decision, even if you don’t speak.", "네가 말은 안 해도 그 눈이 네 결정을 나한테 말해 줄까 봐 두렵구나."], ["Oo!", "으!"], ["I’m afraid!", "무서워!"], ["Alyosha, listen!", "알료샤, 들어라!"], ["Ivan suggests my escaping.", "이반이 나더러 탈주하라고 권한다."], ["I won’t tell you the details: it’s all been thought out: it can all be arranged.", "세세한 건 말 안 할게. 다 짜 놓았어, 다 손쓸 수 있다."], ["Hush, don’t decide.", "쉿, 결정하지 마."], ["I should go to America with Grusha.", "나는 그루샤와 함께 미국으로 가는 거야."], ["You know I can’t live without Grusha!", "알다시피 난 그루샤 없이는 못 살아!"], ["What if they won’t let her follow me to Siberia?", "만약 그놈들이 그루샤가 나를 따라 시베리아로 오는 걸 허락하지 않으면 어떡하냐?"], ["Do they let convicts get married?", "죄수들도 결혼을 허락해 주나?"], ["Ivan thinks not.", "이반은 안 된다고 봐."], ["And without Grusha what should I do there underground with a hammer?", "그런데 그루샤 없이 거기 땅속에서 망치나 들고 내가 뭘 하겠냐?"], ["I should only smash my skull with the hammer!", "그저 망치로 내 머리통이나 박살 내고 말겠지!"], ["But, on the other hand, my conscience?", "하지만, 다른 한편으로, 내 양심은?"], ["I should have run away from suffering.", "나는 고통으로부터 달아난 셈이 되잖아."], ["A sign has come, I reject the sign.", "징표가 내려왔는데, 내가 그 징표를 내치는 거야."], ["I have a way of salvation and I turn my back on it.", "구원의 길이 있는데, 거기에 등을 돌리는 거지."], ["Ivan says that in America, ‘with the good-will,’ I can be of more use than underground.", "이반은 미국에서, '선의를 가지고' 일하면 땅속에서보다 내가 더 쓸모 있을 수 있다고 해."], ["But what becomes of our hymn from underground?", "하지만 우리의 땅속 찬가는 어떻게 되는 거냐?"], ["What’s America?", "미국이 뭐란 말이야?"], ["America is vanity again!", "미국도 또다시 허영일 뿐이지!"], ["And there’s a lot of swindling in America, too, I expect.", "게다가 미국에도 사기가 수두룩할 거다, 내 짐작에는."], ["I should have run away from crucifixion!", "나는 십자가 처형으로부터 달아난 셈이 되는 거야!"], ["I tell you, you know, Alexey, because you are the only person who can understand this.", "너한테 말하는 거다, 알렉세이, 너만이 이걸 이해할 수 있는 유일한 사람이니까."], ["There’s no one else.", "다른 사람은 아무도 없어."], ["It’s folly, madness to others, all I’ve told you of the hymn.", "내가 너한테 찬가에 대해 말한 그 모든 게 다른 사람들한테는 어리석음이고, 미친 소리일 거야."], ["They’ll say I’m out of my mind or a fool.", "그놈들은 내가 정신이 나갔거나 바보라고 하겠지."], ["I am not out of my mind and I am not a fool.", "난 정신이 나가지도 않았고 바보도 아니야."], ["Ivan understands about the hymn, too.", "이반도 찬가에 대해서는 이해해."], ["He understands, only he doesn’t answer—he doesn’t speak.", "이해하긴 하는데, 다만 대답을 안 해 ― 입을 다물 뿐이지."], ["He doesn’t believe in the hymn.", "그는 찬가를 믿지 않아. 말하지 마, 말하지 마."], ["Don’t speak, don’t speak. I see how you look!", "네가 어떤 눈으로 보고 있는지 다 보여!"], ["You have already decided.", "너는 벌써 결정을 내렸어."], ["Don’t decide, spare me!", "결정하지 마, 나를 좀 봐주렴!"], ["I can’t live without Grusha.", "난 그루샤 없이는 살 수 없어."], ["Wait till after the trial!”", "재판이 끝날 때까지 기다려 줘!\""]], [["Mitya ended beside himself.", "미탸는 제정신이 아닌 채로 말을 맺었다."], ["He held Alyosha with both hands on his shoulders, and his yearning, feverish eyes were fixed on his brother’s.", "그는 두 손으로 알료샤의 두 어깨를 붙들었고, 갈망에 찬 열에 들뜬 두 눈을 동생에게 못 박았다."]], [["“They don’t let convicts marry, do they?”", "\"죄수들은 결혼을 허락받지 못하지, 그렇지?\""], ["he repeated for the third time in a supplicating voice.", "그는 애원하는 목소리로 세 번째 같은 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["Alyosha listened with extreme surprise and was deeply moved.", "알료샤는 극도의 놀라움에 휩싸여 듣고 있었고, 깊이 마음이 움직였다."]], [["“Tell me one thing,”", "\"한 가지만 말해 줘.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Is Ivan very keen on it, and whose idea was it?”", "\"이반 형이 그 일에 무척 적극적이야, 그리고 그건 누구 생각이었어?\""]], [["“His, his, and he is very keen on it.", "\"그의 생각이야, 그의 생각, 그리고 그가 무척 적극적이지."], ["He didn’t come to see me at first, then he suddenly came a week ago and he began about it straight away.", "처음엔 나를 보러 오지도 않더니, 일주일 전에 갑자기 와서는 다짜고짜 그 얘기를 꺼내더라."], ["He is awfully keen on it.", "끔찍하게 열을 올려."], ["He doesn’t ask me, but orders me to escape.", "나한테 묻는 게 아니라 탈주하라고 명령하는 거야."], ["He doesn’t doubt of my obeying him, though I showed him all my heart as I have to you, and told him about the hymn, too.", "내가 너한테 하듯 그한테 내 마음을 다 보여 주고, 찬가에 대해서도 말해 줬는데도, 그는 내가 자기 말에 따르리라는 걸 의심치 않아. 자기가 다 손써 주겠다고 하더라."], ["He told me he’d arrange it; he’s found out about everything.", "모든 걸 알아봐 뒀다는 거야. 하지만 그건 나중 얘기다."], ["But of that later. He’s simply set on it.", "그는 그냥 거기에 단단히 마음을 굳혔어."], ["It’s all a matter of money: he’ll pay ten thousand for escape and give me twenty thousand for America.", "다 돈 문제지. 탈주에 만 루블을 내고, 미국 갈 자금으로 이만 루블을 주겠대."], ["And he says we can arrange a magnificent escape for ten thousand.”", "그리고 만 루블이면 근사한 탈주를 꾸밀 수 있다는 거야.\""]], [["“And he told you on no account to tell me?”", "\"그러면서 무슨 일이 있어도 나한테는 말하지 말라고 했어?\""], ["Alyosha asked again.", "알료샤가 다시 물었다."]], [["“To tell no one, and especially not you; on no account to tell you.", "\"아무한테도 말하지 말라고, 특히 너한테는 더더욱. 무슨 일이 있어도 너한테는 말하지 말라고 했어."], ["He is afraid, no doubt, that you’ll stand before me as my conscience.", "틀림없이, 네가 내 양심처럼 내 앞을 가로막고 설까 봐 두려운 거지."], ["Don’t tell him I told you.", "내가 너한테 말했다는 건 그한테 알리지 마."], ["Don’t tell him, for anything.”", "무슨 일이 있어도 그한테는 말하지 마.\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"형 말이 맞아.\""], ["Alyosha pronounced; “it’s impossible to decide anything before the trial is over.", "알료샤가 단언했다. \"재판이 끝나기 전에는 아무것도 결정할 수가 없어."], ["After the trial you’ll decide of yourself.", "재판이 끝나면 형 스스로 결정하게 될 거야."], ["Then you’ll find that new man in yourself and he will decide.”", "그때 형 안에서 그 새로운 인간을 발견하게 될 테고, 그가 결정할 거야.\""]], [["“A new man, or a Bernard who’ll decide à la Bernard, for I believe I’m a contemptible Bernard myself,”", "\"새로운 인간이든, 아니면 베르나르식으로 결정할 베르나르든 간에 말이지, 사실 나 자신도 경멸스러운 베르나르라고 믿으니까.\""], ["said Mitya, with a bitter grin.", "미탸가 씁쓸하게 이를 드러내며 말했다."]], [["“But, brother, have you no hope then of being acquitted?”", "\"그런데 형, 그럼 무죄로 풀려날 가망은 전혀 없는 거야?\""]], [["Mitya shrugged his shoulders nervously and shook his head.", "미탸는 초조하게 어깨를 으쓱하고는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["“Alyosha, darling, it’s time you were going,”", "\"알료샤, 얘야, 이제 너 갈 시간이다.\""], ["he said, with a sudden haste.", "그가 갑자기 서둘러 말했다."], ["“There’s the superintendent shouting in the yard.", "\"마당에서 소장이 소리치는구나. 곧 여기로 올 거야."], ["He’ll be here directly.", "우린 너무 늦었어."], ["We are late; it’s irregular.", "규칙에 어긋나는 거지."], ["Embrace me quickly.", "어서 나를 안아 다오."], ["Kiss me!", "입을 맞춰 줘!"], ["Sign me with the cross, darling, for the cross I have to bear to-morrow.”", "십자 성호를 그어 다오, 얘야, 내일 내가 짊어져야 할 그 십자가를 위해서 말이야.\""]], [["They embraced and kissed.", "그들은 서로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Ivan,”", "\"이반은,\" 미탸가 갑자기 말했다."], ["said Mitya suddenly, “suggests my escaping; but, of course, he believes I did it.”", "\"나더러 탈주하라고 권하지. 하지만, 물론, 그는 내가 했다고 믿어.\""]], [["A mournful smile came on to his lips.", "그의 입가에 서글픈 미소가 떠올랐다."]], [["“Have you asked him whether he believes it?”", "\"형이 했다고 믿느냐고 그한테 물어봤어?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“No, I haven’t.", "\"아니, 못 물어봤어."], ["I wanted to, but I couldn’t.", "묻고 싶었지만, 그럴 수가 없었어."], ["I hadn’t the courage.", "그럴 용기가 없었지."], ["But I saw it from his eyes.", "하지만 그의 눈에서 봤어."], ["Well, good-by!”", "자, 그럼, 잘 가라!\""]], [["Once more they kissed hurriedly, and Alyosha was just going out, when Mitya suddenly called him back.", "그들은 다시 한번 황급히 입을 맞추었고, 알료샤가 막 나가려는 참에 미탸가 갑자기 그를 불러 세웠다."]], [["“Stand facing me!", "\"나를 마주 보고 서 봐라!"], ["That’s right!”", "그래, 그렇지!\""], ["And again he seized Alyosha, putting both hands on his shoulders.", "그러고는 다시 알료샤를 붙들어 두 손을 그의 두 어깨에 얹었다."], ["His face became suddenly quite pale, so that it was dreadfully apparent, even through the gathering darkness.", "그의 얼굴이 별안간 새파랗게 질려, 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서도 그것이 무섭도록 또렷이 드러났다."], ["His lips twitched, his eyes fastened upon Alyosha.", "그의 입술이 실룩거렸고, 두 눈이 알료샤에게 못 박혔다."]], [["“Alyosha, tell me the whole truth, as you would before God.", "\"알료샤, 하느님 앞에서 하듯 나한테 진실을 다 말해 다오."], ["Do you believe I did it?", "너는 내가 했다고 믿느냐?"], ["Do you, do you in yourself, believe it?", "정말, 네 속으로 정말 그걸 믿어?"], ["The whole truth, don’t lie!”", "진실을 다 말해, 거짓말은 하지 말고!\""], ["he cried desperately.", "그가 필사적으로 외쳤다."]], [["Everything seemed heaving before Alyosha, and he felt something like a stab at his heart.", "알료샤에게는 눈앞의 모든 것이 일렁이는 듯했고, 가슴에 무언가 칼에 찔리는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 소리야?\""], ["he faltered helplessly.", "그가 어쩔 줄 몰라하며 더듬거렸다."]], [["“The whole truth, the whole, don’t lie!”", "\"진실을 다, 전부 다, 거짓말은 하지 마!\""], ["repeated Mitya.", "미탸가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“I’ve never for one instant believed that you were the murderer!”", "\"난 단 한순간도 형이 살인자라고 믿은 적이 없어!\""], ["broke in a shaking voice from Alyosha’s breast, and he raised his right hand in the air, as though calling God to witness his words.", "알료샤의 가슴속에서 떨리는 목소리가 터져 나왔고, 그는 마치 하느님을 불러 자기 말의 증인으로 삼으려는 듯 오른손을 허공으로 들어 올렸다."]], [["Mitya’s whole face was lighted up with bliss.", "미탸의 얼굴 전체가 환희로 밝아졌다."]], [["“Thank you!”", "\"고맙다!\""], ["he articulated slowly, as though letting a sigh escape him after fainting.", "그는 마치 기절했다가 한숨을 토해 내듯 천천히 말했다."], ["“Now you have given me new life.", "\"이제 너는 나에게 새 생명을 줬어."], ["Would you believe it, till this moment I’ve been afraid to ask you, you, even you.", "믿어지냐, 이 순간까지 나는 너한테, 너한테조차 물어보기가 두려웠다."], ["Well, go!", "자, 어서 가!"], ["You’ve given me strength for to-morrow.", "너는 나한테 내일을 위한 힘을 주었어. 하느님의 축복이 있기를!"], ["God bless you!", "자, 어서 가렴!"], ["Come, go along!", "이반을 사랑해라!\""], ["Love Ivan!” was Mitya’s last word.", "이것이 미탸의 마지막 말이었다."]], [["Alyosha went out in tears.", "알료샤는 눈물을 흘리며 밖으로 나왔다."], ["Such distrustfulness in Mitya, such lack of confidence even to him, to Alyosha—all this suddenly opened before Alyosha an unsuspected depth of hopeless grief and despair in the soul of his unhappy brother.", "미탸 안에 그런 불신이, 자기에게조차, 알료샤에게조차 그런 못 미더움이 있었다니 ― 이 모든 것이 갑자기 알료샤 앞에, 자기 불행한 형의 영혼 속에 자리한, 미처 짐작도 못 했던 절망과 가망 없는 슬픔의 심연을 열어 보였다."], ["Intense, infinite compassion overwhelmed him instantly.", "강렬하고 한없는 연민이 순식간에 그를 사로잡았다."], ["There was a poignant ache in his torn heart.", "그의 찢긴 가슴에 사무치는 통증이 일었다."], ["“Love Ivan!”—he suddenly recalled Mitya’s words.", "\"이반을 사랑해라!\" ― 그는 문득 미탸의 말을 떠올렸다."], ["And he was going to Ivan.", "그리고 그는 이반에게로 가려는 참이었다."], ["He badly wanted to see Ivan all day.", "하루 종일 이반을 몹시 만나고 싶었다."], ["He was as much worried about Ivan as about Mitya, and more than ever now.", "그는 미탸 못지않게 이반에 대해서도 마음을 졸이고 있었고, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더욱 그랬다."]], [["# Chapter V. Not You, Not You!", "# Chapter V. Not You, Not You!"]], [["On the way to Ivan he had to pass the house where Katerina Ivanovna was living.", "이반에게 가는 길에 그는 카테리나 이바노브나가 사는 집을 지나가야 했다."], ["There was light in the windows.", "창문에 불빛이 새어 나오고 있었다."], ["He suddenly stopped and resolved to go in.", "그는 문득 걸음을 멈추고 들어가 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["He had not seen Katerina Ivanovna for more than a week.", "그는 카테리나 이바노브나를 못 본 지 일주일이 넘었다."], ["But now it struck him that Ivan might be with her, especially on the eve of the terrible day.", "그러나 지금 문득, 이반이 그녀와 함께 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었으니, 더욱이 그 끔찍한 날을 하루 앞둔 밤이었기 때문이다."], ["Ringing, and mounting the staircase, which was dimly lighted by a Chinese lantern, he saw a man coming down, and as they met, he recognized him as his brother.", "초인종을 누르고, 중국식 등롱이 희미하게 비추는 계단을 올라가다가, 그는 한 사내가 내려오는 것을 보았고, 두 사람이 마주치는 순간 그가 자기 형임을 알아보았다."], ["So he was just coming from Katerina Ivanovna.", "그러니까 형은 방금 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 나오는 길이었다."]], [["“Ah, it’s only you,”", "\"아, 너로구나.\""], ["said Ivan dryly.", "이반이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["“Well, good-by!", "\"그럼, 잘 가라!"], ["You are going to her?”", "그녀에게 가는 거냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I don’t advise you to; she’s upset and you’ll upset her more.”", "\"권하고 싶지는 않다. 그녀는 마음이 어지러우니, 너까지 가면 더 어지러워질 게다.\""]], [["A door was instantly flung open above, and a voice cried suddenly:", "순간 위쪽에서 문이 활짝 열리며, 별안간 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["“No, no!", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요!"], ["Alexey Fyodorovitch, have you come from him?”", "알렉세이 표도로비치, 그이한테서 오시는 길인가요?\""]], [["“Yes, I have been with him.”", "\"네, 형님과 함께 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Has he sent me any message?", "\"그이가 제게 무슨 전갈이라도 남겼나요?"], ["Come up, Alyosha, and you, Ivan Fyodorovitch, you must come back, you must.", "올라오세요, 알료샤. 그리고 당신, 이반 표도로비치, 당신은 돌아오셔야 해요, 꼭이요."], ["Do you hear?”", "들리세요?\""]], [["There was such a peremptory note in Katya’s voice that Ivan, after a moment’s hesitation, made up his mind to go back with Alyosha.", "카탸의 목소리에는 어찌나 단호한 기색이 서려 있던지, 이반은 잠시 망설이다가 알료샤와 함께 돌아가기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["“She was listening,”", "\"엿듣고 있었군.\""], ["he murmured angrily to himself, but Alyosha heard it.", "그는 성난 목소리로 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸으나, 알료샤가 그 말을 들었다."]], [["“Excuse my keeping my greatcoat on,”", "\"외투를 입은 채로 있는 걸 양해해 주시오.\""], ["said Ivan, going into the drawing-room.", "이반이 응접실로 들어서며 말했다."], ["“I won’t sit down.", "\"앉지는 않겠소."], ["I won’t stay more than a minute.”", "일 분 이상 머물지도 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Sit down, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"앉으세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["said Katerina Ivanovna, though she remained standing.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 정작 자신은 선 채로 말했다."], ["She had changed very little during this time, but there was an ominous gleam in her dark eyes.", "그동안 그녀는 거의 변하지 않았으나, 검은 눈동자에는 불길한 광채가 어려 있었다."], ["Alyosha remembered afterwards that she had struck him as particularly handsome at that moment.", "알료샤는 훗날, 그 순간 그녀가 유난히 아름답게 보였던 것을 기억해 냈다."]], [["“What did he ask you to tell me?”", "\"그이가 제게 뭘 전하라고 하던가요?\""]], [["“Only one thing,”", "\"오직 한 가지뿐이었습니다.\""], ["said Alyosha, looking her straight in the face, “that you would spare yourself and say nothing at the trial of what”", "알료샤가 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"그게 무엇이냐 하면, 재판에서 부디 자신을 아끼시고, 그러니까……\""], ["(he was a little confused) “...", "그는 조금 당황했다."], ["passed between you ... at the time of your first acquaintance ...", "\"처음 알게 되었을 무렵, 그 도시에서 두 분 사이에 있었던 일을……"], ["in that town.”", "발설하지 마시라는 거였습니다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["that I bowed down to the ground for that money!”", "그 돈 때문에 제가 땅바닥에 엎드려 절했던 일 말이군요!\""], ["She broke into a bitter laugh.", "그녀는 쓰디쓴 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Why, is he afraid for me or for himself?", "\"그래, 그이가 두려워하는 게 저인가요, 자기 자신인가요?"], ["He asks me to spare—whom? Him or myself?", "누구를 아끼라는 거죠—그이를, 아니면 저를?"], ["Tell me, Alexey Fyodorovitch!”", "말씀해 주세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치!\""]], [["Alyosha watched her intently, trying to understand her.", "알료샤는 그녀를 이해하려 애쓰며 골똘히 바라보았다."]], [["“Both yourself and him,”", "\"두 분 다입니다.\""], ["he answered softly.", "그가 나직이 대답했다."]], [["“I am glad to hear it,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군요.\""], ["she snapped out maliciously, and she suddenly blushed.", "그녀는 심술궂게 쏘아붙였고, 갑자기 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“You don’t know me yet, Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"당신은 아직 저를 모르세요, 알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["she said menacingly.", "그녀가 위협하듯 말했다."], ["“And I don’t know myself yet.", "\"저 자신조차도 저를 모르는걸요."], ["Perhaps you’ll want to trample me under foot after my examination to-morrow.”", "내일 제가 심문을 받고 나면, 당신은 어쩌면 저를 발밑에 짓밟고 싶어질지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“You will give your evidence honorably,”", "\"당신은 떳떳하게 증언하실 겁니다.\""], ["said Alyosha; “that’s all that’s wanted.”", "알료샤가 말했다. \"필요한 건 그것뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Women are often dishonorable,” she snarled.", "\"여자란 흔히 떳떳하지 못한 법이에요.\""], ["“Only an hour ago I was thinking I felt afraid to touch that monster ...", "그녀가 으르렁거리듯 내뱉었다. \"바로 한 시간 전만 해도 저는 그 괴물을 만지는 것조차 두렵다고 느꼈어요……"], ["as though he were a reptile ...", "마치 그가 파충류라도 되는 양……"], ["but no, he is still a human being to me!", "하지만 아니에요, 제겐 그이가 여전히 한 인간인걸요!"], ["But did he do it?", "그런데 정말 그이가 그랬을까요?"], ["Is he the murderer?”", "그이가 살인자인가요?\""], ["she cried, all of a sudden, hysterically, turning quickly to Ivan.", "그녀는 별안간 히스테릭하게 소리치며, 이반에게 홱 돌아섰다."], ["Alyosha saw at once that she had asked Ivan that question before, perhaps only a moment before he came in, and not for the first time, but for the hundredth, and that they had ended by quarreling.", "알료샤는 그녀가 이반에게 그 질문을 이미 던졌었다는 것을, 어쩌면 그가 들어오기 바로 직전에, 그것도 처음이 아니라 수백 번째로 던졌고, 두 사람이 끝내 다투고 말았다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다."]], [["“I’ve been to see Smerdyakov....", "\"저는 스메르쟈코프를 만나러 갔었어요……"], ["It was you, you who persuaded me that he murdered his father.", "그이가 아버지를 죽였다고 저를 설득한 사람이 바로 당신이에요."], ["It’s only you I believed!”", "저는 오직 당신만을 믿었다고요!\""], ["she continued, still addressing Ivan.", "그녀는 여전히 이반을 향해 말을 이었다."], ["He gave her a sort of strained smile.", "그는 어딘가 억지스러운 미소를 그녀에게 지어 보였다."], ["Alyosha started at her tone.", "알료샤는 그녀의 어조에 흠칫했다."], ["He had not suspected such familiar intimacy between them.", "두 사람 사이에 그토록 허물없는 친밀함이 있으리라고는 짐작도 못 했던 것이다."]], [["“Well, that’s enough, anyway,”", "\"뭐, 어쨌든 이쯤 하면 충분하오.\""], ["Ivan cut short the conversation.", "이반이 대화를 잘라 버렸다."], ["“I am going.", "\"나는 가겠소."], ["I’ll come to-morrow.”", "내일 다시 오리다.\""], ["And turning at once, he walked out of the room and went straight downstairs.", "그러고는 그 자리에서 곧장 돌아서서, 방을 나가 곧바로 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["With an imperious gesture, Katerina Ivanovna seized Alyosha by both hands.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 명령하듯 단호한 몸짓으로 알료샤의 두 손을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Follow him!", "\"저이를 따라가세요!"], ["Overtake him!", "따라잡으세요!"], ["Don’t leave him alone for a minute!”", "단 일 분도 혼자 두지 마세요!\""], ["she said, in a hurried whisper.", "그녀가 다급히 속삭였다."], ["“He’s mad!", "\"저이는 미쳤어요!"], ["Don’t you know that he’s mad?", "저이가 미쳤다는 걸 모르세요?"], ["He is in a fever, nervous fever.", "저이는 열에 들떠 있어요, 신경성 열병이에요."], ["The doctor told me so.", "의사가 제게 그렇게 말했어요."], ["Go, run after him....”", "어서, 저이를 쫓아가세요……\""]], [["Alyosha jumped up and ran after Ivan, who was not fifty paces ahead of him.", "알료샤는 벌떡 일어나 이반을 뒤쫓아 달려갔는데, 그는 쉰 걸음도 채 앞서가 있지 않았다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 바라는 거냐?\""], ["He turned quickly on Alyosha, seeing that he was running after him.", "그는 알료샤가 자기를 쫓아오는 것을 보고 홱 돌아섰다."], ["“She told you to catch me up, because I’m mad.", "\"내가 미쳤으니 따라잡으라고 그녀가 시킨 거겠지."], ["I know it all by heart,”", "다 외울 만큼 잘 알고 있다.\""], ["he added irritably.", "그가 짜증스럽게 덧붙였다."]], [["“She is mistaken, of course; but she is right that you are ill,”", "\"그분이 잘못 안 거예요, 물론. 하지만 형님이 아프다는 건 옳은 말이에요.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."], ["“I was looking at your face just now.", "\"방금 형님 얼굴을 봤어요."], ["You look very ill, Ivan.”", "몹시 아파 보여요, 형.\""]], [["Ivan walked on without stopping.", "이반은 멈추지 않고 계속 걸었다."], ["Alyosha followed him.", "알료샤가 그를 뒤따랐다."]], [["“And do you know, Alexey Fyodorovitch, how people do go out of their mind?”", "\"그런데 알겠느냐, 알렉세이 표도로비치, 사람이 어떻게 미쳐 가는지를?\""], ["Ivan asked in a voice suddenly quiet, without a trace of irritation, with a note of the simplest curiosity.", "이반이 별안간 차분한 목소리로, 짜증이라곤 흔적도 없이, 더없이 단순한 호기심의 어조로 물었다."]], [["“No, I don’t.", "\"아뇨, 몰라요."], ["I suppose there are all kinds of insanity.”", "미치는 데도 갖가지 종류가 있겠죠.\""]], [["“And can one observe that one’s going mad oneself?”", "\"그러면 자기가 미쳐 가고 있다는 걸 스스로 알아챌 수 있을까?\""]], [["“I imagine one can’t see oneself clearly in such circumstances,”", "\"그런 처지에서 자기 자신을 또렷이 볼 수는 없을 것 같은데요.\""], ["Alyosha answered with surprise.", "알료샤가 놀라며 대답했다."]], [["Ivan paused for half a minute.", "이반은 반 분쯤 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“If you want to talk to me, please change the subject,”", "\"나하고 이야기를 하고 싶거든, 제발 화제를 바꿔라.\""], ["he said suddenly.", "그가 갑자기 말했다."]], [["“Oh, while I think of it, I have a letter for you,”", "\"아, 생각난 김에 말하자면, 형님께 드릴 편지가 있어요.\""], ["said Alyosha timidly, and he took Lise’s note from his pocket and held it out to Ivan.", "알료샤가 머뭇거리며 말하고는, 주머니에서 리즈의 쪽지를 꺼내 이반에게 내밀었다."], ["They were just under a lamp-post.", "두 사람은 마침 가로등 바로 아래에 있었다."], ["Ivan recognized the handwriting at once.", "이반은 단번에 필체를 알아보았다."]], [["“Ah, from that little demon!”", "\"아, 그 작은 악마한테서 온 거로군!\""], ["he laughed maliciously, and, without opening the envelope, he tore it into bits and threw it in the air.", "그는 심술궂게 웃고는, 봉투를 뜯지도 않고 갈기갈기 찢어 공중에 던져 버렸다."], ["The bits were scattered by the wind.", "찢긴 조각들이 바람에 흩날렸다."]], [["“She’s not sixteen yet, I believe, and already offering herself,”", "\"아직 열여섯도 안 됐을 텐데, 벌써 제 몸을 내던지고 있다니.\""], ["he said contemptuously, striding along the street again.", "그가 경멸하듯 말하며, 다시 거리를 성큼성큼 걸어갔다."]], [["“How do you mean, offering herself?”", "\"제 몸을 내던지다니, 그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["“As wanton women offer themselves, to be sure.”", "\"음탕한 여자들이 제 몸을 내던지듯이, 그렇다는 거다.\""]], [["“How can you, Ivan, how can you?”", "\"어떻게 그러실 수 있어요, 형, 어떻게 그러실 수 있어요?\""], ["Alyosha cried warmly, in a grieved voice.", "알료샤가 비통한 목소리로 격하게 부르짖었다."], ["“She is a child; you are insulting a child!", "\"그앤 아이예요. 형님은 지금 아이를 모욕하는 거예요!"], ["She is ill; she is very ill, too.", "그앤 아파요. 게다가 몹시 아파요."], ["She is on the verge of insanity, too, perhaps....", "어쩌면 그애도 광기의 벼랑 끝에 서 있는지도 몰라요……"], ["I had hoped to hear something from you ... that would save her.”", "저는 형님한테서 그애를 구할 무슨 말을 들을 수 있기를 바랐어요.\""]], [["“You’ll hear nothing from me.", "\"내게서 들을 건 아무것도 없다."], ["If she is a child I am not her nurse.", "그애가 아이라면, 나는 그애의 유모가 아니야."], ["Be quiet, Alexey.", "조용히 해라, 알렉세이."], ["Don’t go on about her.", "그애 얘기는 그만둬."], ["I am not even thinking about it.”", "나는 그애에 대해 생각조차 하고 있지 않다.\""]], [["They were silent again for a moment.", "두 사람은 다시 한동안 말이 없었다."]], [["“She will be praying all night now to the Mother of God to show her how to act to-morrow at the trial,”", "\"그녀는 지금 밤새도록 성모님께 기도를 드리겠지, 내일 재판에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알려 달라고 말이야.\""], ["he said sharply and angrily again.", "그가 다시 날카롭고 성난 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You ...", "\"형님은……"], ["you mean Katerina Ivanovna?”", "카테리나 이바노브나 말씀이에요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Whether she’s to save Mitya or ruin him.", "미탸를 구할지 파멸시킬지를 두고 말이야."], ["She’ll pray for light from above.", "그녀는 위로부터 빛이 내려오기를 기도할 거다."], ["She can’t make up her mind for herself, you see.", "알겠느냐, 그녀는 스스로 마음을 정하지 못하거든."], ["She has not had time to decide yet.", "아직 결심할 틈이 없었던 거야."], ["She takes me for her nurse, too.", "그녀는 나까지도 자기 유모로 여기지."], ["She wants me to sing lullabies to her.”", "내가 그녀에게 자장가를 불러 주기를 바란다고.\""]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna loves you, brother,”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나는 형님을 사랑하고 있어요, 형.\""], ["said Alyosha sadly.", "알료샤가 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps; but I am not very keen on her.”", "\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 그녀에게 그다지 끌리지 않는다.\""]], [["“She is suffering.", "\"그분은 괴로워하고 있어요."], ["Why do you ...", "그런데 어째서 형님은……"], ["sometimes say things to her that give her hope?”", "이따금 그분에게 희망을 품게 하는 말을 하시는 거죠?\""], ["Alyosha went on, with timid reproach.", "알료샤가 머뭇거리는 책망조로 말을 이었다."], ["“I know that you’ve given her hope.", "\"형님이 그분에게 희망을 주었다는 걸 저는 알아요."], ["Forgive me for speaking to you like this,”", "이렇게 말씀드리는 걸 용서하세요.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“I can’t behave to her as I ought—break off altogether and tell her so straight out,”", "\"나는 그녀에게 마땅히 해야 할 대로 행동할 수가 없어—완전히 관계를 끊고 그렇다고 그녀에게 곧이곧대로 말해 버리는 일 말이야.\""], ["said Ivan, irritably.", "이반이 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["“I must wait till sentence is passed on the murderer.", "\"나는 그 살인자에게 판결이 내려질 때까지 기다려야 해."], ["If I break off with her now, she will avenge herself on me by ruining that scoundrel to-morrow at the trial, for she hates him and knows she hates him.", "지금 그녀와 관계를 끊어 버리면, 그녀는 내일 재판에서 그 비열한 놈을 파멸시킴으로써 내게 복수할 거야. 그녀는 그를 증오하고, 자기가 그를 증오한다는 걸 알고 있으니까."], ["It’s all a lie—lie upon lie!", "그건 죄다 거짓이야—거짓 위에 거짓이지!"], ["As long as I don’t break off with her, she goes on hoping, and she won’t ruin that monster, knowing how I want to get him out of trouble.", "내가 그녀와 관계를 끊지 않는 한, 그녀는 계속 희망을 품을 테고, 내가 그 괴물을 곤경에서 빼내고 싶어 한다는 걸 알기에 그를 파멸시키지 않을 거야."], ["If only that damned verdict would come!”", "그 빌어먹을 판결이 어서 나기만 한다면!\""]], [["The words “murderer” and “monster” echoed painfully in Alyosha’s heart.", "\"살인자\"와 \"괴물\"이라는 말이 알료샤의 가슴속에 고통스럽게 메아리쳤다."]], [["“But how can she ruin Mitya?”", "\"하지만 그분이 어떻게 미탸를 파멸시킬 수 있다는 거죠?\""], ["he asked, pondering on Ivan’s words.", "그가 이반의 말을 곱씹으며 물었다."], ["“What evidence can she give that would ruin Mitya?”", "\"그분이 미탸를 파멸시킬 무슨 증언을 할 수 있다는 거예요?\""]], [["“You don’t know that yet.", "\"너는 아직 그걸 모르지."], ["She’s got a document in her hands, in Mitya’s own writing, that proves conclusively that he did murder Fyodor Pavlovitch.”", "그녀는 미탸가 직접 쓴 문서를 손에 쥐고 있는데, 그게 미탸가 표도르 파블로비치를 죽였다는 걸 결정적으로 증명한단 말이야.\""]], [["“That’s impossible!”", "\"그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 부르짖었다."]], [["“Why is it impossible?", "\"어째서 있을 수 없는 일이라는 거냐?"], ["I’ve read it myself.”", "내가 직접 읽어 봤는데.\""]], [["“There can’t be such a document!”", "\"그런 문서는 있을 수 없어요!\""], ["Alyosha repeated warmly.", "알료샤가 격하게 거듭 말했다."], ["“There can’t be, because he’s not the murderer.", "\"있을 수 없어요, 형은 살인자가 아니니까요."], ["It’s not he murdered father, not he!”", "아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니에요, 형이 아니라고요!\""]], [["Ivan suddenly stopped.", "이반이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Who is the murderer then, according to you?”", "\"그렇다면 네 생각엔 누가 살인자라는 거냐?\""], ["he asked, with apparent coldness.", "그가 짐짓 냉랭하게 물었다."], ["There was even a supercilious note in his voice.", "그의 목소리에는 거만한 기색마저 서려 있었다."]], [["“You know who,”", "\"누군지 형님도 아시잖아요.\""], ["Alyosha pronounced in a low, penetrating voice.", "알료샤가 낮고도 파고드는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“Who?", "\"누구?"], ["You mean the myth about that crazy idiot, the epileptic, Smerdyakov?”", "그 미치광이 백치, 간질쟁이 스메르쟈코프에 관한 그 신화 말이냐?\""]], [["Alyosha suddenly felt himself trembling all over.", "알료샤는 별안간 온몸이 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“You know who,”", "\"누군지 형님도 아시잖아요.\""], ["broke helplessly from him.", "어쩔 수 없다는 듯 그의 입에서 터져 나왔다."], ["He could scarcely breathe.", "그는 숨조차 쉴 수 없을 지경이었다."]], [["“Who?", "\"누구?"], ["Who?”", "누구냐고?\""], ["Ivan cried almost fiercely.", "이반이 거의 사납게 소리쳤다."], ["All his restraint suddenly vanished.", "그의 모든 자제력이 별안간 사라졌다."]], [["“I only know one thing,”", "\"저는 단 한 가지만 알아요.\""], ["Alyosha went on, still almost in a whisper, “it wasn’t you killed father.”", "알료샤가 여전히 거의 속삭이듯 말을 이었다. \"아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니라는 거예요.\""]], [["“‘Not you’!", "\"'형이 아니라니'!"], ["What do you mean by ‘not you’?”", "'형이 아니라니' 그게 무슨 뜻이냐?\""], ["Ivan was thunderstruck.", "이반은 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 굳어졌다."]], [["“It was not you killed father, not you!”", "\"아버지를 죽인 건 형이 아니에요, 형이 아니라고요!\""], ["Alyosha repeated firmly.", "알료샤가 단호하게 거듭 말했다."]], [["The silence lasted for half a minute.", "침묵이 반 분간 이어졌다."]], [["“I know I didn’t.", "\"내가 죽이지 않은 건 나도 안다."], ["Are you raving?”", "너 헛소리를 하는 거냐?\""], ["said Ivan, with a pale, distorted smile.", "이반이 창백하게 일그러진 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["His eyes were riveted on Alyosha.", "그의 눈은 알료샤에게 못 박혀 있었다."], ["They were standing again under a lamp-post.", "두 사람은 다시 가로등 아래 서 있었다."]], [["“No, Ivan.", "\"아뇨, 이반 형."], ["You’ve told yourself several times that you are the murderer.”", "형님은 자신이 살인자라고 여러 번 스스로에게 말했어요.\""]], [["“When did I say so?", "\"내가 언제 그런 말을 했다는 거냐?"], ["I was in Moscow....", "나는 모스크바에 있었어……"], ["When have I said so?”", "내가 언제 그런 말을 했다고?\""], ["Ivan faltered helplessly.", "이반이 어쩔 줄 몰라 더듬거렸다."]], [["“You’ve said so to yourself many times, when you’ve been alone during these two dreadful months,”", "\"형님은 그 끔찍한 두 달 동안 혼자 있을 때, 스스로에게 그 말을 수도 없이 했어요.\""], ["Alyosha went on softly and distinctly as before.", "알료샤가 여전히 부드럽고 또렷하게 말을 이었다."], ["Yet he was speaking now, as it were, not of himself, not of his own will, but obeying some irresistible command.", "그러나 그는 이제, 말하자면 자기 자신에 대해서가, 자기 자신의 뜻으로가 아니라, 거역할 수 없는 어떤 명령에 따라 말하고 있었다."], ["“You have accused yourself and have confessed to yourself that you are the murderer and no one else.", "\"형님은 자기 자신을 단죄했고, 살인자는 다름 아닌 형님 자신이라고 스스로에게 고백했어요. 하지만 형은 하지 않았어요."], ["But you didn’t do it: you are mistaken: you are not the murderer.", "형은 잘못 알고 있어요. 형은 살인자가 아니에요."], ["Do you hear?", "들리세요?"], ["It was not you!", "그건 형이 아니에요!"], ["God has sent me to tell you so.”", "하느님이 형님께 그 말을 전하라고 저를 보내신 거예요.\""]], [["They were both silent.", "두 사람 다 말이 없었다."], ["The silence lasted a whole long minute.", "침묵은 꼬박 일 분 동안 이어졌다."], ["They were both standing still, gazing into each other’s eyes.", "둘 다 가만히 선 채 서로의 눈을 응시했다."], ["They were both pale.", "둘 다 창백했다."], ["Suddenly Ivan began trembling all over, and clutched Alyosha’s shoulder.", "갑자기 이반이 온몸을 부들부들 떨기 시작하며 알료샤의 어깨를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“You’ve been in my room!”", "\"너, 내 방에 들어왔었구나!\""], ["he whispered hoarsely.", "그가 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다."], ["“You’ve been there at night, when he came....", "\"밤에, 그자가 왔을 때 거기 있었던 거야……"], ["Confess ...", "실토해라……"], ["have you seen him, have you seen him?”", "너 그자를 봤지, 그자를 봤지?\""]], [["“Whom do you mean—Mitya?”", "\"누구 말씀이에요—미탸요?\""], ["Alyosha asked, bewildered.", "알료샤가 어리둥절해하며 물었다."]], [["“Not him, damn the monster!”", "\"그놈 말이 아냐, 그 괴물은 빌어먹을!\""], ["Ivan shouted, in a frenzy.", "이반이 광란하듯 소리쳤다."], ["“Do you know that he visits me?", "\"그자가 나를 찾아온다는 걸 너는 알고 있지?"], ["How did you find out?", "어떻게 알아낸 거냐?"], ["Speak!”", "말해!\""]], [["“Who is he!", "\"그자가 누구예요!"], ["I don’t know whom you are talking about,”", "형님이 누구 얘기를 하는 건지 모르겠어요.\""], ["Alyosha faltered, beginning to be alarmed.", "알료샤가 더듬거리며, 차츰 겁에 질리기 시작했다."]], [["“Yes, you do know ...", "\"아니, 너는 알고 있어……"], ["or how could you—?", "안 그러면 어떻게 네가——?"], ["It’s impossible that you don’t know.”", "네가 모를 리가 없어.\""]], [["Suddenly he seemed to check himself.", "별안간 그는 스스로를 억누르는 듯했다."], ["He stood still and seemed to reflect.", "그는 가만히 서서 무언가를 곰곰이 생각하는 것 같았다."], ["A strange grin contorted his lips.", "야릇한 비웃음이 그의 입술을 일그러뜨렸다."]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형.\""], ["Alyosha began again, in a shaking voice, “I have said this to you, because you’ll believe my word, I know that.", "알료샤가 떨리는 목소리로 다시 입을 열었다. \"제가 형님께 이 말을 한 건, 형님이 제 말을 믿어 주실 줄 알기 때문이에요, 저는 그걸 알아요."], ["I tell you once and for all, it’s not you.", "단번에 영영 말씀드릴게요, 그건 형이 아니에요."], ["You hear, once for all!", "들리세요, 단번에 영영!"], ["God has put it into my heart to say this to you, even though it may make you hate me from this hour.”", "비록 이 순간부터 형님이 저를 미워하게 될지라도, 하느님이 이 말을 형님께 하라고 제 마음속에 넣어 주신 거예요.\""]], [["But by now Ivan had apparently regained his self-control.", "그러나 이제 이반은 분명 자제력을 되찾은 듯했다."]], [["“Alexey Fyodorovitch,”", "\"알렉세이 표도로비치.\""], ["he said, with a cold smile, “I can’t endure prophets and epileptics—messengers from God especially—and you know that only too well.", "그가 차가운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"나는 예언자나 간질쟁이는 견딜 수가 없어—특히 하느님의 사자라는 자들은 더더욱—그건 너도 너무나 잘 알고 있지."], ["I break off all relations with you from this moment and probably for ever.", "이 순간부터 너와는 모든 관계를 끊겠다, 아마 영원히."], ["I beg you to leave me at this turning.", "여기 갈림길에서 나를 놓아주기 바란다."], ["It’s the way to your lodgings, too.", "네 숙소로 가는 길이기도 하지."], ["You’d better be particularly careful not to come to me to-day!", "오늘은 특히 내게 오지 않도록 각별히 조심하는 게 좋을 거다!"], ["Do you hear?”", "들었느냐?\""]], [["He turned and walked on with a firm step, not looking back.", "그는 돌아서서, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 단호한 걸음으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형.\""], ["Alyosha called after him, “if anything happens to you to-day, turn to me before any one!”", "알료샤가 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다. \"오늘 형님께 무슨 일이 생기거든, 그 누구보다 먼저 제게로 오세요!\""]], [["But Ivan made no reply.", "그러나 이반은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Alyosha stood under the lamp-post at the cross roads, till Ivan had vanished into the darkness.", "알료샤는 이반이 어둠 속으로 사라질 때까지 갈림길 가로등 아래 서 있었다."], ["Then he turned and walked slowly homewards.", "그러고 나서 그는 돌아서서 천천히 집을 향해 걸었다."], ["Both Alyosha and Ivan were living in lodgings; neither of them was willing to live in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s empty house.", "알료샤도 이반도 셋방살이를 하고 있었으니, 둘 다 표도르 파블로비치의 텅 빈 집에서 살고 싶어 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["Alyosha had a furnished room in the house of some working people.", "알료샤는 어느 노동자 집에 가구 딸린 방 하나를 얻어 들어 있었다."], ["Ivan lived some distance from him.", "이반은 그에게서 좀 떨어진 곳에 살았다."], ["He had taken a roomy and fairly comfortable lodge attached to a fine house that belonged to a well-to-do lady, the widow of an official.", "그는 어느 부유한 부인, 곧 한 관리의 미망인 소유인 번듯한 집에 딸린, 널찍하고 제법 안락한 별채를 빌려 쓰고 있었다."], ["But his only attendant was a deaf and rheumatic old crone who went to bed at six o’clock every evening and got up at six in the morning.", "그러나 그의 시중을 드는 사람이라고는 귀먹고 류머티즘을 앓는 늙은 노파 한 명뿐이었는데, 그녀는 저녁마다 여섯 시면 잠자리에 들고 아침마다 여섯 시면 일어났다."], ["Ivan had become remarkably indifferent to his comforts of late, and very fond of being alone.", "이반은 근래 들어 자기 안락에 놀라우리만치 무심해졌고, 혼자 있기를 무척 좋아하게 되었다."], ["He did everything for himself in the one room he lived in, and rarely entered any of the other rooms in his abode.", "그는 자기가 사는 단칸방에서 무엇이든 손수 해결했고, 거처의 다른 방에는 좀처럼 발을 들이지 않았다."]], [["He reached the gate of the house and had his hand on the bell, when he suddenly stopped.", "그는 집 대문 앞에 이르러 초인종에 손을 얹었다가, 별안간 멈춰 섰다. 그는 분노로 온몸이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He felt that he was trembling all over with anger.", "갑자기 그는 초인종에서 손을 떼고, 욕설을 내뱉으며 돌아서서, 빠른 걸음으로 반대 방향으로 걸어갔다."], ["Suddenly he let go of the bell, turned back with a curse, and walked with rapid steps in the opposite direction.", "그는 일 베르스타 반쯤 걸어, 작고 비스듬히 기운, 거의 오두막이나 다름없는 목조 가옥에 다다랐는데, 그곳에는 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 세 들어 살고 있었다."], ["He walked a mile and a half to a tiny, slanting, wooden house, almost a hut, where Marya Kondratyevna, the neighbor who used to come to Fyodor Pavlovitch’s kitchen for soup and to whom Smerdyakov had once sung his songs and played on the guitar, was now lodging.", "그녀는 한때 표도르 파블로비치의 부엌으로 수프를 얻으러 드나들던 이웃 여자로, 스메르쟈코프가 언젠가 노래를 불러 주고 기타를 쳐 주었던 바로 그 여인이었다. 그녀는 자기네 작은 집을 팔고, 지금은 어머니와 함께 이곳에서 살고 있었다."], ["She had sold their little house, and was now living here with her mother.", "거의 죽어 가다시피 앓고 있던 스메르쟈코프는 표도르 파블로비치가 죽은 이래로 줄곧 그들과 함께 지내고 있었다."], ["Smerdyakov, who was ill—almost dying—had been with them ever since Fyodor Pavlovitch’s death.", "이반이 지금 갑작스럽고도 거역할 수 없는 충동에 이끌려 찾아가는 사람이 바로 그였다."], ["It was to him Ivan was going now, drawn by a sudden and irresistible prompting.", "* 베르스타(верста): 약 1.067킬로미터에 해당하는 옛 러시아의 거리 단위."]], [["# Chapter VI. The First Interview With Smerdyakov", "# Chapter VI. The First Interview With Smerdyakov"]], [["This was the third time that Ivan had been to see Smerdyakov since his return from Moscow.", "이반이 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 스메르댜코프를 찾아간 것은 이번이 세 번째였다."], ["The first time he had seen him and talked to him was on the first day of his arrival, then he had visited him once more, a fortnight later.", "처음 그를 만나 이야기를 나눈 것은 도착한 첫날이었고, 그 후 다시 한 번, 두 주가 지나서 찾아간 적이 있었다."], ["But his visits had ended with that second one, so that it was now over a month since he had seen him.", "그러나 그의 방문은 그 두 번째를 끝으로 막을 내렸으니, 이제 그를 본 지도 한 달이 넘었다."], ["And he had scarcely heard anything of him.", "그리고 그에 관한 소식이라고는 거의 듣지 못했다."]], [["Ivan had only returned five days after his father’s death, so that he was not present at the funeral, which took place the day before he came back.", "이반은 아버지가 죽고 닷새가 지나서야 돌아왔으므로, 그가 돌아오기 바로 전날 치러진 장례식에는 참석하지 못했다. 그가 늦어진 까닭은 이러했다."], ["The cause of his delay was that Alyosha, not knowing his Moscow address, had to apply to Katerina Ivanovna to telegraph to him, and she, not knowing his address either, telegraphed to her sister and aunt, reckoning on Ivan’s going to see them as soon as he arrived in Moscow.", "알료샤가 그의 모스크바 주소를 몰라 카테리나 이바노브나에게 전보를 쳐 달라고 부탁할 수밖에 없었는데, 그녀 역시 주소를 알지 못해 자기 언니와 고모에게 전보를 쳤고, 이반이 모스크바에 도착하는 대로 그들을 찾아가리라 짐작했던 것이다."], ["But he did not go to them till four days after his arrival.", "그러나 그는 도착하고 나흘이 지나서야 그들을 찾아갔다."], ["When he got the telegram, he had, of course, set off post-haste to our town.", "전보를 받자마자 그는 물론 부랴부랴 우리 고을로 달려왔다."], ["The first to meet him was Alyosha, and Ivan was greatly surprised to find that, in opposition to the general opinion of the town, he refused to entertain a suspicion against Mitya, and spoke openly of Smerdyakov as the murderer.", "가장 먼저 그를 맞은 것은 알료샤였는데, 이반은 알료샤가 온 고을의 일반적인 견해와는 달리 미탸를 향한 의심을 한사코 받아들이지 않으며 스메르댜코프를 살인자라고 거리낌 없이 말하는 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐다."], ["Later on, after seeing the police captain and the prosecutor, and hearing the details of the charge and the arrest, he was still more surprised at Alyosha, and ascribed his opinion only to his exaggerated brotherly feeling and sympathy with Mitya, of whom Alyosha, as Ivan knew, was very fond.", "나중에 경찰서장과 검사를 만나 혐의와 체포에 관한 자세한 내막을 듣고 나서 그는 더욱더 알료샤에게 놀랐고, 그의 견해를 다만 형제로서의 지나친 정과 미탸에 대한 동정 탓으로 돌렸으니, 이반이 알기로 알료샤는 그 형을 무척이나 따랐던 것이다."]], [["By the way, let us say a word or two of Ivan’s feeling to his brother Dmitri.", "여기서 이반이 형 드미트리에게 품은 감정에 대해 한두 마디 해 두기로 하자."], ["He positively disliked him; at most, felt sometimes a compassion for him, and even that was mixed with great contempt, almost repugnance.", "그는 형을 노골적으로 싫어했으니, 기껏해야 이따금 연민을 느낄 뿐이었고, 그나마도 그 연민에는 커다란 경멸이, 거의 혐오에 가까운 것이 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["Mitya’s whole personality, even his appearance, was extremely unattractive to him.", "미탸의 인간됨 전부가, 심지어 그 생김새까지도 이반에게는 더없이 매력 없게 비쳤다."], ["Ivan looked with indignation on Katerina Ivanovna’s love for his brother.", "이반은 형을 향한 카테리나 이바노브나의 사랑을 분개의 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["Yet he went to see Mitya on the first day of his arrival, and that interview, far from shaking Ivan’s belief in his guilt, positively strengthened it.", "그러면서도 그는 도착한 첫날 미탸를 찾아갔는데, 그 면회는 형이 유죄라는 이반의 확신을 흔들기는커녕 오히려 더욱 굳혀 놓았다."], ["He found his brother agitated, nervously excited.", "그가 본 형은 동요하고 신경이 곤두서 흥분해 있었다."], ["Mitya had been talkative, but very absent-minded and incoherent.", "미탸는 말이 많았으나 몹시 산만하고 두서가 없었다."], ["He used violent language, accused Smerdyakov, and was fearfully muddled.", "그는 거친 말을 내뱉으며 스메르댜코프를 비난했고 끔찍하리만치 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["He talked principally about the three thousand roubles, which he said had been “stolen” from him by his father.", "그가 주로 떠든 것은 삼천 루블에 관한 것이었으니, 그것을 아버지가 자기에게서 \"훔쳤다\"는 것이었다."]], [["“The money was mine, it was my money,”", "\"그 돈은 내 것이었어, 내 돈이었단 말이야.\""], ["Mitya kept repeating.", "미탸는 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["“Even if I had stolen it, I should have had the right.”", "\"설령 내가 그걸 훔쳤다 한들, 나에게는 그럴 권리가 있었어.\""]], [["He hardly contested the evidence against him, and if he tried to turn a fact to his advantage, it was in an absurd and incoherent way.", "그는 자기에게 불리한 증거를 거의 반박하지 않았고, 어떤 사실을 자기에게 유리하게 돌려 보려 할 때면 그것도 터무니없고 두서없는 방식이었다."], ["He hardly seemed to wish to defend himself to Ivan or any one else.", "그는 이반에게든 그 누구에게든 자신을 변호하고 싶어 하는 기색조차 거의 없었다."], ["Quite the contrary, he was angry and proudly scornful of the charges against him; he was continually firing up and abusing every one.", "오히려 그 반대로, 그는 자기에게 걸린 혐의에 화를 내며 거만하게 경멸했고, 끊임없이 발끈하여 모두를 욕했다."], ["He only laughed contemptuously at Grigory’s evidence about the open door, and declared that it was “the devil that opened it.”", "열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언에 대해서도 그는 경멸하듯 웃을 뿐, \"문을 연 것은 악마였다\"고 단언했다."], ["But he could not bring forward any coherent explanation of the fact.", "그러나 그 사실에 대해 그는 조리 있는 설명이라곤 단 하나도 내놓지 못했다."], ["He even succeeded in insulting Ivan during their first interview, telling him sharply that it was not for people who declared that “everything was lawful,” to suspect and question him.", "그는 첫 면회 자리에서 이반을 모욕하기까지 했으니, \"모든 것이 합법이다\"라고 떠드는 자들이 자기를 의심하고 추궁할 자격은 없다고 날카롭게 쏘아붙였던 것이다."], ["Altogether he was anything but friendly with Ivan on that occasion.", "어쨌든 그날 그는 이반에게 결코 우호적이지 않았다."], ["Immediately after that interview with Mitya, Ivan went for the first time to see Smerdyakov.", "미탸와의 그 면회를 마치자마자 이반은 처음으로 스메르댜코프를 찾아갔다."]], [["In the railway train on his way from Moscow, he kept thinking of Smerdyakov and of his last conversation with him on the evening before he went away.", "모스크바에서 돌아오는 기차 안에서 그는 줄곧 스메르댜코프와, 떠나기 전날 저녁 그와 나눈 마지막 대화를 곱씹었다."], ["Many things seemed to him puzzling and suspicious.", "여러 가지가 그에게는 알 수 없고 미심쩍게 여겨졌다."], ["But when he gave his evidence to the investigating lawyer Ivan said nothing, for the time, of that conversation.", "그러나 그는 수사 판사에게 진술할 때 그 대화에 관해서는 당분간 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He put that off till he had seen Smerdyakov, who was at that time in the hospital.", "그는 그것을 스메르댜코프를 만난 뒤로 미뤄 두었으니, 그자는 그 무렵 병원에 있었다."]], [["Doctor Herzenstube and Varvinsky, the doctor he met in the hospital, confidently asserted in reply to Ivan’s persistent questions, that Smerdyakov’s epileptic attack was unmistakably genuine, and were surprised indeed at Ivan asking whether he might not have been shamming on the day of the catastrophe.", "헤르첸슈투베 의사와, 병원에서 만난 의사 바르빈스키는 이반의 집요한 물음에 대해, 스메르댜코프의 간질 발작은 의심할 여지 없이 진짜였다고 자신 있게 단언했으며, 참사가 일어난 날 그가 꾀병을 부린 것은 아니냐는 이반의 물음에는 오히려 의아해했다."], ["They gave him to understand that the attack was an exceptional one, the fits persisting and recurring several times, so that the patient’s life was positively in danger, and it was only now, after they had applied remedies, that they could assert with confidence that the patient would survive.", "그들은 그 발작이 예사롭지 않은 것이었으며, 경련이 거듭하여 여러 차례 되풀이되는 통에 환자의 목숨이 실로 위태로웠고, 이제야, 약을 쓰고 난 지금에야 환자가 살아남으리라고 자신 있게 말할 수 있게 되었음을 이해시켰다. \"다만 충분히 있을 수 있는 일이지요.\""], ["“Though it might well be,”", "헤르첸슈투베 의사가 덧붙였다."], ["added Doctor Herzenstube, “that his reason would be impaired for a considerable period, if not permanently.”", "\"그의 이성이 영구히는 아니더라도 상당 기간 손상될 수 있다는 것 말입니다.\""], ["On Ivan’s asking impatiently whether that meant that he was now mad, they told him that this was not yet the case, in the full sense of the word, but that certain abnormalities were perceptible.", "그렇다면 지금 그가 미친 것이냐고 이반이 조바심을 내며 묻자, 그들은 아직 온전한 의미에서 그런 것은 아니지만 어떤 비정상적인 징후는 감지된다고 일러 주었다."], ["Ivan decided to find out for himself what those abnormalities were.", "이반은 그 비정상이라는 것이 무엇인지 직접 알아내기로 마음먹었다."]], [["At the hospital he was at once allowed to see the patient.", "병원에서 그는 곧바로 환자를 만나도록 허락받았다."], ["Smerdyakov was lying on a truckle-bed in a separate ward.", "스메르댜코프는 별도의 병실에 놓인 간이침대에 누워 있었다."], ["There was only one other bed in the room, and in it lay a tradesman of the town, swollen with dropsy, who was obviously almost dying; he could be no hindrance to their conversation.", "그 방에는 침대가 하나 더 있을 뿐이었는데, 거기에는 수종으로 퉁퉁 부어 누가 봐도 거의 죽어 가는 고을의 한 상인이 누워 있었으니, 그가 두 사람의 대화를 방해할 일은 없었다."], ["Smerdyakov grinned uncertainly on seeing Ivan, and for the first instant seemed nervous.", "스메르댜코프는 이반을 보자 미덥지 못하게 씩 웃었고, 처음 한순간은 긴장한 듯 보였다."], ["So at least Ivan fancied.", "적어도 이반에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다."], ["But that was only momentary.", "그러나 그것은 잠깐뿐이었다."], ["For the rest of the time he was struck, on the contrary, by Smerdyakov’s composure.", "그 후로는 오히려 스메르댜코프의 침착함에 그는 충격을 받았다."], ["From the first glance Ivan had no doubt that he was very ill.", "첫눈에 이반은 그가 몹시 아프다는 것을 의심치 않았다."], ["He was very weak; he spoke slowly, seeming to move his tongue with difficulty; he was much thinner and sallower.", "그는 무척 쇠약했고, 혀를 움직이기조차 힘든 듯 천천히 말했으며, 한결 야위고 누렇게 떠 있었다."], ["Throughout the interview, which lasted twenty minutes, he kept complaining of headache and of pain in all his limbs.", "이십 분간 이어진 면회 내내 그는 두통과 온몸의 통증을 끊임없이 호소했다."], ["His thin emasculate face seemed to have become so tiny; his hair was ruffled, and his crest of curls in front stood up in a thin tuft.", "거세된 듯한 그 야윈 얼굴은 어찌나 조그매졌는지, 머리칼은 헝클어졌고 앞이마에 솟은 곱슬머리는 가느다란 한 줌으로 곤두서 있었다."], ["But in the left eye, which was screwed up and seemed to be insinuating something, Smerdyakov showed himself unchanged.", "그러나 무언가를 넌지시 암시하는 듯 가늘게 찌푸린 왼쪽 눈만은, 스메르댜코프가 조금도 변치 않았음을 드러냈다."], ["“It’s always worth while speaking to a clever man.”", "\"영리한 자와 이야기하는 것은 언제나 보람이 있는 법이지요.\""], ["Ivan was reminded of that at once.", "이반은 이내 그 말이 떠올랐다."], ["He sat down on the stool at his feet.", "그는 침대 발치의 걸상에 앉았다."], ["Smerdyakov, with painful effort, shifted his position in bed, but he was not the first to speak.", "스메르댜코프는 고통스레 안간힘을 쓰며 자리에서 몸을 옮겼으나, 먼저 입을 떼지는 않았다."], ["He remained dumb, and did not even look much interested.", "그는 묵묵히 입을 다물었고, 별로 흥미를 느끼는 기색조차 없었다."]], [["“Can you talk to me?”", "\"나와 얘기 좀 할 수 있겠나?\""], ["asked Ivan.", "이반이 물었다."], ["“I won’t tire you much.”", "\"오래 지치게 하지는 않을 테니.\""]], [["“Certainly I can,”", "\"물론 할 수 있습죠.\""], ["mumbled Smerdyakov, in a faint voice.", "스메르댜코프가 가느다란 목소리로 웅얼거렸다."], ["“Has your honor been back long?”", "\"도련님께서는 돌아오신 지 오래되셨나요?\""], ["he added patronizingly, as though encouraging a nervous visitor.", "그는 마치 긴장한 손님을 다독이기라도 하듯 거들먹거리며 덧붙였다."]], [["“I only arrived to-day....", "\"오늘에야 도착했네……."], ["To see the mess you are in here.”", "자네가 여기서 이 꼴이 된 걸 보러 온 거지.\""]], [["Smerdyakov sighed.", "스메르댜코프가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Why do you sigh?", "\"어째서 한숨을 쉬는 건가?"], ["You knew of it all along,”", "자네는 처음부터 다 알고 있지 않았나.\""], ["Ivan blurted out.", "이반이 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["Smerdyakov was stolidly silent for a while.", "스메르댜코프는 한동안 무뚝뚝하게 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["“How could I help knowing?", "\"제가 어찌 모를 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["It was clear beforehand.", "미리부터 뻔한 일이었지요."], ["But how could I tell it would turn out like that?”", "하지만 그게 그렇게 끝날 줄을 제가 어찌 알았겠습니까?\""]], [["“What would turn out?", "\"무엇이 그렇게 끝나다니?"], ["Don’t prevaricate!", "둘러대지 말게!"], ["You’ve foretold you’d have a fit; on the way down to the cellar, you know.", "자네는 발작이 일어날 거라고 예언했어. 지하실로 내려가는 길에 말이야, 알지."], ["You mentioned the very spot.”", "바로 그 자리까지 짚어 말했지.\""]], [["“Have you said so at the examination yet?”", "\"그걸 벌써 심문 때 말씀하셨습니까?\""], ["Smerdyakov queried with composure.", "스메르댜코프가 침착하게 캐물었다."]], [["Ivan felt suddenly angry.", "이반은 갑자기 화가 치밀었다."]], [["“No, I haven’t yet, but I certainly shall.", "\"아니, 아직은 말하지 않았어. 하지만 반드시 말할 거야."], ["You must explain a great deal to me, my man; and let me tell you, I am not going to let you play with me!”", "자네는 나에게 설명해야 할 게 잔뜩 있어, 이 사람아. 그리고 똑똑히 말해 두지, 나는 자네가 나를 가지고 놀게 내버려 두지 않을 테니까!\""]], [["“Why should I play with you, when I put my whole trust in you, as in God Almighty?”", "\"제가 어찌 도련님을 가지고 놀겠습니까, 전능하신 하느님께 그러하듯 도련님께 온 믿음을 걸고 있는 마당에 말입니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with the same composure, only for a moment closing his eyes.", "스메르댜코프는 한순간 눈을 감을 뿐, 여전히 침착하게 말했다."]], [["“In the first place,”", "\"우선 첫째로.\""], ["began Ivan, “I know that epileptic fits can’t be told beforehand.", "이반이 입을 열었다. \"간질 발작이란 미리 예언할 수 없는 거라는 걸 나는 알고 있어."], ["I’ve inquired; don’t try and take me in.", "물어봤거든. 나를 속이려 들지 마."], ["You can’t foretell the day and the hour.", "날짜며 시각을 미리 알 수는 없는 법이야."], ["How was it you told me the day and the hour beforehand, and about the cellar, too?", "그런데 어떻게 자네는 나에게 날짜와 시각을, 게다가 지하실까지 미리 일러 줬던 거지?"], ["How could you tell that you would fall down the cellar stairs in a fit, if you didn’t sham a fit on purpose?”", "일부러 발작을 꾸민 게 아니라면 어떻게 발작이 일어나 지하실 계단에서 굴러떨어질 거라고 알 수 있었겠나?\""]], [["“I had to go to the cellar anyway, several times a day, indeed,”", "\"어차피 저는 지하실에 내려가야 했습니다, 실은 하루에도 몇 번씩 말이지요.\""], ["Smerdyakov drawled deliberately.", "스메르댜코프가 일부러 느릿느릿 말을 끌었다."], ["“I fell from the garret just in the same way a year ago.", "\"꼭 그처럼 일 년 전에도 다락방에서 떨어졌습죠."], ["It’s quite true you can’t tell the day and hour of a fit beforehand, but you can always have a presentiment of it.”", "발작의 날과 시각을 미리 알 수 없다는 건 정말 맞는 말씀입니다만, 예감만은 늘 들 수 있는 법이지요.\""]], [["“But you did foretell the day and the hour!”", "\"그렇지만 자네는 날짜와 시각을 분명히 예언했어!\""]], [["“In regard to my epilepsy, sir, you had much better inquire of the doctors here.", "\"제 간질에 관해서라면, 도련님, 여기 의사 선생들께 여쭤보시는 편이 훨씬 나을 겁니다."], ["You can ask them whether it was a real fit or a sham; it’s no use my saying any more about it.”", "진짜 발작이었는지 꾸민 것이었는지 물어보시면 되지요. 제가 더 이상 무슨 말을 해 봐야 소용없습니다.\""]], [["“And the cellar?", "\"그럼 지하실은?"], ["How could you know beforehand of the cellar?”", "어떻게 지하실 일을 미리 알 수 있었나?\""]], [["“You don’t seem able to get over that cellar!", "\"그놈의 지하실에서 영 헤어나질 못하시는군요!"], ["As I was going down to the cellar, I was in terrible dread and doubt.", "제가 지하실로 내려갈 때 저는 무서운 두려움과 의심에 사로잡혀 있었습니다."], ["What frightened me most was losing you and being left without defense in all the world.", "무엇보다 저를 떨게 한 것은, 도련님을 잃고 이 세상천지에 아무 보호자도 없이 남겨진다는 것이었지요. 그래서 지하실로 내려가면서 이렇게 생각했습니다."], ["So I went down into the cellar thinking, ‘Here, it’ll come on directly, it’ll strike me down directly, shall I fall?’ And it was through this fear that I suddenly felt the spasm that always comes ...", "'자, 이제 곧 닥쳐올 거야, 당장 나를 쓰러뜨릴 거야, 내가 떨어지려나?' 바로 이 두려움 때문에 저는 늘 찾아오는 그 경련을 갑자기 느꼈고……"], ["and so I went flying.", "그렇게 굴러떨어졌습니다."], ["All that and all my previous conversation with you at the gate the evening before, when I told you how frightened I was and spoke of the cellar, I told all that to Doctor Herzenstube and Nikolay Parfenovitch, the investigating lawyer, and it’s all been written down in the protocol.", "그 모든 것을, 그리고 전날 저녁 대문 앞에서 도련님과 나눈 이전의 대화 전부를, 제가 얼마나 두려워했는지, 지하실 얘기를 했던 것까지 다 헤르첸슈투베 의사와 수사 판사 니콜라이 파르페노비치께 말씀드렸고, 그게 전부 조서에 적혔습니다."], ["And the doctor here, Mr. Varvinsky, maintained to all of them that it was just the thought of it brought it on, the apprehension that I might fall.", "그리고 여기 의사 바르빈스키 선생께서도 모두에게, 바로 그 생각이, 떨어질지도 모른다는 그 불안이 발작을 일으킨 거라고 확언하셨지요."], ["It was just then that the fit seized me.", "바로 그 순간 발작이 저를 덮친 겁니다."], ["And so they’ve written it down, that it’s just how it must have happened, simply from my fear.”", "그래서 그들은 그렇게, 틀림없이 그런 식으로 일어났을 거라고, 순전히 제 두려움 때문에 그리 됐을 거라고 적어 두었습니다.\""]], [["As he finished, Smerdyakov drew a deep breath, as though exhausted.", "말을 마치자 스메르댜코프는 마치 기진맥진한 듯 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["“Then you have said all that in your evidence?”", "\"그럼 자네는 그 모든 걸 진술에서 다 말했단 말이지?\""], ["said Ivan, somewhat taken aback.", "이반이 다소 당황하여 말했다."], ["He had meant to frighten him with the threat of repeating their conversation, and it appeared that Smerdyakov had already reported it all himself.", "그는 두 사람의 대화를 그대로 옮기겠다는 위협으로 그를 겁주려 했는데, 알고 보니 스메르댜코프가 이미 그 모두를 스스로 진술해 버린 것이었다."]], [["“What have I to be afraid of?", "\"제가 무얼 두려워해야 합니까?"], ["Let them write down the whole truth,”", "온전한 진실을 죄다 적게 두면 그만이지요.\""], ["Smerdyakov pronounced firmly.", "스메르댜코프가 단호하게 잘라 말했다."]], [["“And have you told them every word of our conversation at the gate?”", "\"그럼 대문 앞에서 나눈 우리 대화를 한마디도 빼놓지 않고 다 말했나?\""]], [["“No, not to say every word.”", "\"아니요, 한마디도 빼놓지 않고는 아닙니다.\""]], [["“And did you tell them that you can sham fits, as you boasted then?”", "\"그리고 그때 자네가 떠벌렸듯이, 발작을 꾸며 낼 수 있다는 것도 그들에게 말했나?\""]], [["“No, I didn’t tell them that either.”", "\"아니요, 그건 말하지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Tell me now, why did you send me then to Tchermashnya?”", "\"그럼 이제 말해 보게, 자네는 어째서 그때 나를 체르마시냐로 보냈지?\""]], [["“I was afraid you’d go away to Moscow; Tchermashnya is nearer, anyway.”", "\"도련님께서 모스크바로 가 버리실까 봐 두려웠습니다. 어쨌든 체르마시냐가 더 가까우니까요.\""]], [["“You are lying; you suggested my going away yourself; you told me to get out of the way of trouble.”", "\"거짓말이야. 자네가 직접 떠나라고 권했어. 화를 피하라고 나에게 말했지.\""]], [["“That was simply out of affection and my sincere devotion to you, foreseeing trouble in the house, to spare you.", "\"그건 그저 도련님을 향한 애정과 진실한 충정에서, 집안에 화가 닥칠 것을 내다보고 도련님을 지켜 드리려는 마음이었습니다."], ["Only I wanted to spare myself even more.", "다만 저는 저 자신을 지키고 싶은 마음이 더 컸지요."], ["That’s why I told you to get out of harm’s way, that you might understand that there would be trouble in the house, and would remain at home to protect your father.”", "그래서 화를 피하시라고 말씀드린 겁니다, 집안에 화가 있으리라는 걸 알아채시고 댁에 남아 아버님을 지켜 드리시라고요.\""]], [["“You might have said it more directly, you blockhead!”", "\"그렇다면 좀 더 직접적으로 말했어야지, 이 멍청아!\""], ["Ivan suddenly fired up.", "이반이 갑자기 발끈했다."]], [["“How could I have said it more directly then?", "\"그때 어떻게 더 직접적으로 말씀드릴 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["It was simply my fear that made me speak, and you might have been angry, too.", "그저 두려움 때문에 그렇게 말한 것이었고, 도련님께서 화를 내셨을지도 모를 일이지요."], ["I might well have been apprehensive that Dmitri Fyodorovitch would make a scene and carry away that money, for he considered it as good as his own; but who could tell that it would end in a murder like this?", "드미트리 표도로비치께서 한바탕 소동을 부리며 그 돈을 가져가실까 봐 저는 충분히 겁이 났습니다, 그분은 그 돈을 거의 자기 것이나 다름없다고 여기셨으니까요. 하지만 그게 이런 살인으로 끝나리라고 누가 알았겠습니까?"], ["I thought that he would only carry off the three thousand that lay under the master’s mattress in the envelope, and you see, he’s murdered him.", "저는 그분이 주인님 침대 요 밑 봉투에 든 삼천 루블만 가져가시리라고 생각했는데, 보십시오, 그분이 그분을 죽여 버린 겁니다."], ["How could you guess it either, sir?”", "도련님인들 그걸 어찌 짐작하셨겠습니까?\""]], [["“But if you say yourself that it couldn’t be guessed, how could I have guessed and stayed at home?", "\"하지만 자네 스스로 짐작할 수 없었다고 말하면서, 어떻게 내가 짐작하고 집에 남아 있을 수 있었겠나?"], ["You contradict yourself!”", "자네는 앞뒤가 안 맞아!\""], ["said Ivan, pondering.", "이반이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."]], [["“You might have guessed from my sending you to Tchermashnya and not to Moscow.”", "\"제가 도련님을 모스크바가 아니라 체르마시냐로 보낸 것에서 짐작하실 수 있었지요.\""]], [["“How could I guess it from that?”", "\"그걸로 어떻게 짐작하란 말인가?\""]], [["Smerdyakov seemed much exhausted, and again he was silent for a minute.", "스메르댜코프는 무척 지친 듯 보였고, 다시 한동안 입을 다물었다."]], [["“You might have guessed from the fact of my asking you not to go to Moscow, but to Tchermashnya, that I wanted to have you nearer, for Moscow’s a long way off, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch, knowing you are not far off, would not be so bold.", "\"제가 도련님께 모스크바로 가지 마시고 체르마시냐로 가시라고 청한 사실에서, 제가 도련님을 더 가까이 두고 싶어 했다는 걸 짐작하실 수 있었지요. 모스크바는 멀고, 드미트리 표도로비치께서도 도련님이 멀리 있지 않다는 걸 아시면 그토록 대담하게는 굴지 못하셨을 테니까요."], ["And if anything had happened, you might have come to protect me, too, for I warned you of Grigory Vassilyevitch’s illness, and that I was afraid of having a fit.", "그리고 무슨 일이 생기면 도련님께서 저까지 지키러 와 주실 수 있었지요. 제가 그리고리 바실리예비치의 병환을 알려 드렸고, 발작이 일어날까 봐 두렵다고도 말씀드렸으니까요."], ["And when I explained those knocks to you, by means of which one could go in to the deceased, and that Dmitri Fyodorovitch knew them all through me, I thought that you would guess yourself that he would be sure to do something, and so wouldn’t go to Tchermashnya even, but would stay.”", "게다가 그 신호에 대해 설명해 드렸을 때, 그걸로 고인께 들어갈 수 있다는 것과 드미트리 표도로비치께서 저를 통해 그걸 죄다 알고 계시다는 것까지 말씀드렸으니, 저는 도련님께서 그분이 틀림없이 무슨 일을 저지르시리라는 걸 스스로 짐작하시고, 체르마시냐로도 가지 않으시고 남아 계시리라 여겼던 겁니다.\""]], [["“He talks very coherently,”", "'녀석은 웅얼거리기는 해도, 무척 조리 있게 말하는군.'"], ["thought Ivan, “though he does mumble; what’s the derangement of his faculties that Herzenstube talked of?”", "이반은 생각했다. '헤르첸슈투베가 말한 정신의 이상이라는 게 대체 뭐란 말이야?'"]], [["“You are cunning with me, damn you!”", "\"자네는 나에게 잔꾀를 부리고 있어, 이 빌어먹을 놈!\""], ["he exclaimed, getting angry.", "그는 화가 치밀어 소리쳤다."]], [["“But I thought at the time that you quite guessed,”", "\"하지만 저는 그때 도련님께서 진작에 다 짐작하셨다고 생각했습니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov parried with the simplest air.", "스메르댜코프가 더없이 천연덕스러운 태도로 응수했다."]], [["“If I’d guessed, I should have stayed,”", "\"내가 짐작했더라면 남아 있었을 거야.\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Why, I thought that it was because you guessed, that you went away in such a hurry, only to get out of trouble, only to run away and save yourself in your fright.”", "\"아니, 저는 도련님께서 짐작하셨기 때문에, 화를 피하시려고, 그저 도망쳐 두려움 속에서 자신을 지키시려고 그토록 급히 떠나신 거라고 생각했지요.\""]], [["“You think that every one is as great a coward as yourself?”", "\"자네는 모든 사람이 자네처럼 그렇게 비겁하다고 여기나?\""]], [["“Forgive me, I thought you were like me.”", "\"용서하십시오, 저는 도련님께서 저와 같은 줄로만 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Of course, I ought to have guessed,”", "\"물론 나는 짐작했어야 했어.\""], ["Ivan said in agitation; “and I did guess there was some mischief brewing on your part ...", "이반이 동요하며 말했다. \"그리고 나는 자네 쪽에서 무슨 흉계가 꾸며지고 있다는 걸 정말로 짐작했지……."], ["only you are lying, you are lying again,”", "다만 자네는 거짓말을 하고 있어, 또 거짓말을 하고 있어.\""], ["he cried, suddenly recollecting.", "그는 문득 떠올라 소리쳤다."], ["“Do you remember how you went up to the carriage and said to me, ‘It’s always worth while speaking to a clever man’?", "\"기억하나, 자네가 마차로 다가와 나에게 '영리한 자와 이야기하는 것은 언제나 보람이 있는 법이지요'라고 말했던 거?"], ["So you were glad I went away, since you praised me?”", "그럼 자네는 나를 칭찬한 걸 보니 내가 떠나서 기뻤던 거로군?\""]], [["Smerdyakov sighed again and again.", "스메르댜코프는 거듭 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["A trace of color came into his face.", "그의 얼굴에 핏기가 한 줄 떠올랐다."]], [["“If I was pleased,”", "\"제가 기뻤다면.\""], ["he articulated rather breathlessly, “it was simply because you agreed not to go to Moscow, but to Tchermashnya.", "그는 다소 숨가쁘게 또박또박 말했다. \"그건 그저 도련님께서 모스크바가 아니라 체르마시냐로 가시기로 하셨기 때문입니다."], ["For it was nearer, anyway.", "어쨌든 거기가 더 가까우니까요."], ["Only when I said these words to you, it was not by way of praise, but of reproach.", "다만 그 말씀을 드린 것은 칭찬으로가 아니라 책망으로 드린 겁니다."], ["You didn’t understand it.”", "도련님께서는 그걸 이해하지 못하셨지요.\""]], [["“What reproach?”", "\"무슨 책망이란 말인가?\""]], [["“Why, that foreseeing such a calamity you deserted your own father, and would not protect us, for I might have been taken up any time for stealing that three thousand.”", "\"아니, 그런 재앙을 내다보시고도 친아버님을 버리시고, 우리를 지켜 주려 하지 않으셨다는 책망이지요. 저는 그 삼천 루블을 훔쳤다는 죄로 언제든 끌려갈 수 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Damn you!” Ivan swore again.", "\"빌어먹을 놈!\""], ["“Stay, did you tell the prosecutor and the investigating lawyer about those knocks?”", "이반이 다시 욕을 내뱉었다. \"잠깐, 자네는 검사와 수사 판사에게 그 신호 얘기를 했나?\""]], [["“I told them everything just as it was.”", "\"있는 그대로 죄다 말했습니다.\""]], [["Ivan wondered inwardly again.", "이반은 또다시 속으로 의아해했다."]], [["“If I thought of anything then,”", "\"그때 내가 무언가를 생각했다면.\""], ["he began again, “it was solely of some wickedness on your part.", "그가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그건 오로지 자네 쪽의 어떤 흉계에 관한 것이었어."], ["Dmitri might kill him, but that he would steal—I did not believe that then....", "드미트리가 그를 죽일 수는 있어도, 그가 도둑질을 하리라고는—그땐 그렇게 믿지 않았지……."], ["But I was prepared for any wickedness from you.", "하지만 자네에게서는 어떤 흉계든 나올 거라고 각오하고 있었어."], ["You told me yourself you could sham a fit.", "자네 스스로 발작을 꾸며 낼 수 있다고 나에게 말했지."], ["What did you say that for?”", "무엇 때문에 그런 말을 한 건가?\""]], [["“It was just through my simplicity, and I never have shammed a fit on purpose in my life.", "\"그건 그저 저의 순박함 때문이었고, 저는 평생 일부러 발작을 꾸민 적이 한 번도 없습니다."], ["And I only said so then to boast to you.", "그저 그때 도련님 앞에서 뻐기느라고 그렇게 말한 것뿐이지요."], ["It was just foolishness.", "한낱 어리석은 짓이었습니다."], ["I liked you so much then, and was open-hearted with you.”", "저는 그때 도련님이 어찌나 좋았던지, 도련님께 마음을 활짝 열었던 겁니다.\""]], [["“My brother directly accuses you of the murder and theft.”", "\"내 형은 대놓고 자네를 살인과 절도로 고발하고 있어.\""]], [["“What else is left for him to do?”", "\"그분에게 달리 무슨 수가 있겠습니까?\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with a bitter grin.", "스메르댜코프가 쓰디쓴 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“And who will believe him with all the proofs against him?", "\"그 많은 증거가 죄다 그분에게 불리한 마당에, 누가 그분의 말을 믿겠습니까?"], ["Grigory Vassilyevitch saw the door open.", "그리고리 바실리예비치께서 문이 열린 걸 보셨다지요."], ["What can he say after that?", "그러니 그분이 그 다음에 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["But never mind him!", "하지만 그분이야 어쩔 수 없지요!"], ["He is trembling to save himself.”", "자기 목숨을 구하려고 벌벌 떨고 계신 겁니다.\""]], [["He slowly ceased speaking; then suddenly, as though on reflection, added:", "그는 천천히 말을 멈추더니, 갑자기 무슨 생각이라도 떠오른 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["“And look here again.", "\"그리고 또 이걸 보십시오."], ["He wants to throw it on me and make out that it is the work of my hands—I’ve heard that already.", "그분은 그걸 저에게 뒤집어씌워 제 손으로 한 짓인 양 꾸미려 하십니다—저도 벌써 그 얘기를 들었지요."], ["But as to my being clever at shamming a fit: should I have told you beforehand that I could sham one, if I really had had such a design against your father?", "하지만 제가 발작을 꾸미는 데 능하다는 것으로 말하자면, 만일 제가 정말로 아버님께 그런 흉계를 품고 있었다면, 제가 발작을 꾸밀 수 있다고 미리 도련님께 말씀드렸겠습니까?"], ["If I had been planning such a murder could I have been such a fool as to give such evidence against myself beforehand?", "만일 제가 그런 살인을 꾸미고 있었다면, 그토록 어리석게 미리 제 입으로 저에게 불리한 증거를 댔겠습니까?"], ["And to his son, too! Upon my word!", "그것도 그분의 아드님께 말입니다!"], ["Is that likely?", "정말이지!"], ["As if that could be, such a thing has never happened.", "그게 있을 법한 일입니까? 그런 일은 일찍이 일어난 적이 없습니다."], ["No one hears this talk of ours now, except Providence itself, and if you were to tell of it to the prosecutor and Nikolay Parfenovitch you might defend me completely by doing so, for who would be likely to be such a criminal, if he is so open-hearted beforehand?", "지금 우리의 이 이야기는 섭리 그 자체 말고는 아무도 듣는 이가 없으니, 만일 도련님께서 이걸 검사와 니콜라이 파르페노비치께 말씀하신다면 그것으로 저를 완전히 변호해 주시는 셈이 됩니다. 미리부터 이토록 마음을 활짝 여는 자가 어떻게 그런 죄인일 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Any one can see that.”", "누구라도 그건 알 수 있는 일이지요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["and Ivan got up to cut short the conversation, struck by Smerdyakov’s last argument.", "이반은 스메르댜코프의 마지막 논리에 충격을 받아, 대화를 끝맺으려고 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["“I don’t suspect you at all, and I think it’s absurd, indeed, to suspect you.", "\"나는 자네를 조금도 의심하지 않네. 실은 자네를 의심한다는 게 터무니없다고 생각해."], ["On the contrary, I am grateful to you for setting my mind at rest.", "오히려 내 마음을 놓이게 해 줘서 고맙네."], ["Now I am going, but I’ll come again.", "이제 가 보겠지만, 다시 오겠네."], ["Meanwhile, good-by.", "그동안 잘 있게."], ["Get well.", "어서 낫고."], ["Is there anything you want?”", "무어 필요한 게 있나?\""]], [["“I am very thankful for everything.", "\"모든 게 그저 고마울 따름입니다."], ["Marfa Ignatyevna does not forget me, and provides me anything I want, according to her kindness.", "마르파 이그나티예브나께서 저를 잊지 않으시고, 그분의 너그러움대로 제가 원하는 건 무엇이든 챙겨 주십니다."], ["Good people visit me every day.”", "착한 분들이 날마다 저를 찾아오시고요.\""]], [["“Good-by.", "\"잘 있게."], ["But I shan’t say anything of your being able to sham a fit, and I don’t advise you to, either,”", "하지만 자네가 발작을 꾸밀 수 있다는 얘기는 내가 하지 않겠네. 자네에게도 그러지 말라고 권하고 싶군.\""], ["something made Ivan say suddenly.", "무언가가 이반으로 하여금 불쑥 그렇게 말하게 했다."]], [["“I quite understand.", "\"잘 알겠습니다."], ["And if you don’t speak of that, I shall say nothing of that conversation of ours at the gate.”", "그리고 도련님께서 그걸 말씀하지 않으신다면, 저도 대문 앞에서 나눈 우리의 그 대화에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["Then it happened that Ivan went out, and only when he had gone a dozen steps along the corridor, he suddenly felt that there was an insulting significance in Smerdyakov’s last words.", "그러고 나서 이반은 밖으로 나갔는데, 복도를 따라 열 걸음 남짓 걸어가서야 그는 문득 스메르댜코프의 마지막 말에 모욕적인 뜻이 담겨 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He was almost on the point of turning back, but it was only a passing impulse, and muttering, “Nonsense!” he went out of the hospital.", "그는 하마터면 돌아설 뻔했으나, 그것은 한순간의 충동에 지나지 않았고, \"터무니없는 소리!\"라고 중얼거리며 그는 병원을 나섰다."]], [["His chief feeling was one of relief at the fact that it was not Smerdyakov, but Mitya, who had committed the murder, though he might have been expected to feel the opposite.", "그의 가장 큰 감정은, 살인을 저지른 것이 스메르댜코프가 아니라 미탸라는 사실에서 오는 안도감이었으니, 마땅히 그 반대의 감정을 느꼈으리라 짐작할 만한데도 그러했다."], ["He did not want to analyze the reason for this feeling, and even felt a positive repugnance at prying into his sensations.", "그는 이 감정의 까닭을 분석하고 싶지 않았고, 자기 감각을 캐어 들여다보는 데 대해 노골적인 혐오마저 느꼈다."], ["He felt as though he wanted to make haste to forget something.", "그는 마치 무언가를 서둘러 잊어버리고 싶은 것 같았다."], ["In the following days he became convinced of Mitya’s guilt, as he got to know all the weight of evidence against him.", "그 후 며칠 동안 그는 미탸에게 걸린 증거의 무게를 모조리 알게 되면서 형의 유죄를 확신하게 되었다."], ["There was evidence of people of no importance, Fenya and her mother, for instance, but the effect of it was almost overpowering.", "보잘것없는 사람들, 가령 페냐와 그녀의 어머니 같은 이들의 증언도 있었으나, 그것이 미치는 영향은 거의 압도적이었다."], ["As to Perhotin, the people at the tavern, and at Plotnikov’s shop, as well as the witnesses at Mokroe, their evidence seemed conclusive.", "페르호틴, 선술집 사람들, 플로트니코프의 가게 사람들, 그리고 모크로예의 증인들로 말하자면 그들의 증언은 결정적으로 보였다."], ["It was the details that were so damning.", "그토록 치명적인 것은 바로 그 자질구레한 정황들이었다."], ["The secret of the knocks impressed the lawyers almost as much as Grigory’s evidence as to the open door.", "그 신호의 비밀은 열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언만큼이나 법조인들에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["Grigory’s wife, Marfa, in answer to Ivan’s questions, declared that Smerdyakov had been lying all night the other side of the partition wall.", "그리고리의 아내 마르파는 이반의 물음에 답하여, 스메르댜코프가 밤새도록 칸막이벽 저편에 누워 있었다고 단언했다."], ["“He was not three paces from our bed,”", "\"우리 침대에서 세 걸음도 떨어지지 않은 곳이었지요.\""], ["and that although she was a sound sleeper she waked several times and heard him moaning, “He was moaning the whole time, moaning continually.”", "그러면서 자기는 잠귀가 어두운 편인데도 몇 번이나 깨어 그가 신음하는 소리를 들었다고 했다. \"그이는 내내 신음했어요, 끊임없이 신음했지요.\""]], [["Talking to Herzenstube, and giving it as his opinion that Smerdyakov was not mad, but only rather weak, Ivan only evoked from the old man a subtle smile.", "헤르첸슈투베와 이야기하면서, 스메르댜코프는 미친 게 아니라 그저 조금 쇠약할 뿐이라는 견해를 내놓자, 이반은 그 노인에게서 미묘한 웃음을 자아냈을 뿐이었다."]], [["“Do you know how he spends his time now?”", "\"그가 요즘 어떻게 시간을 보내는지 아십니까?\""], ["he asked; “learning lists of French words by heart.", "노인이 물었다. \"프랑스어 단어 목록을 외우고 있지요."], ["He has an exercise-book under his pillow with the French words written out in Russian letters for him by some one, he he he!”", "베개 밑에 공책을 두고 있는데, 거기엔 누군가가 그를 위해 러시아 글자로 적어 준 프랑스어 단어들이 쓰여 있더군요, 히히히!\""]], [["Ivan ended by dismissing all doubts.", "이반은 결국 모든 의혹을 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["He could not think of Dmitri without repulsion.", "그는 혐오 없이는 드미트리를 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["Only one thing was strange, however.", "다만 한 가지 이상한 점이 있었다."], ["Alyosha persisted that Dmitri was not the murderer, and that “in all probability”", "알료샤는 드미트리가 살인자가 아니며 \"십중팔구\" 스메르댜코프가 살인자라고 고집했다."], ["Smerdyakov was. Ivan always felt that Alyosha’s opinion meant a great deal to him, and so he was astonished at it now.", "이반은 늘 알료샤의 견해가 자기에게 큰 의미가 있다고 느껴 왔으므로, 이번에는 그 견해에 놀랐다."], ["Another thing that was strange was that Alyosha did not make any attempt to talk about Mitya with Ivan, that he never began on the subject and only answered his questions.", "또 한 가지 이상한 점은, 알료샤가 미탸에 관해 이반과 이야기하려는 시도를 전혀 하지 않으며, 결코 먼저 그 화제를 꺼내지 않고 다만 그의 물음에만 답할 뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["This, too, struck Ivan particularly.", "이 점 역시 이반에게는 유난히 충격적이었다."]], [["But he was very much preoccupied at that time with something quite apart from that.", "그러나 그는 그 무렵 그것과는 전혀 동떨어진 무언가에 무척 골몰해 있었다."], ["On his return from Moscow, he abandoned himself hopelessly to his mad and consuming passion for Katerina Ivanovna.", "모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 그는 카테리나 이바노브나를 향한 미친 듯하고 사람을 집어삼키는 정념에 가망 없이 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["This is not the time to begin to speak of this new passion of Ivan’s, which left its mark on all the rest of his life: this would furnish the subject for another novel, which I may perhaps never write.", "이반의 남은 평생에 흔적을 남긴 이 새로운 정념에 관해 이야기를 시작할 때는 지금이 아니다. 이는 또 다른 소설의 소재가 될 만한 것으로, 어쩌면 나는 끝내 그 소설을 쓰지 못할지도 모른다."], ["But I cannot omit to mention here that when Ivan, on leaving Katerina Ivanovna with Alyosha, as I’ve related already, told him, “I am not keen on her,”", "그러나 여기서 짚어 두지 않을 수 없는 것은, 이반이 앞서 내가 이야기했듯 알료샤를 데리고 카테리나 이바노브나의 곁을 떠나며 그에게 \"나는 그 여자에게 마음이 없어\"라고 말했을 때, 그것은 완전한 거짓말이었다는 점이다."], ["it was an absolute lie: he loved her madly, though at times he hated her so that he might have murdered her.", "그는 그녀를 미치도록 사랑했으니, 때로는 그녀를 죽이고 싶을 만큼 미워하기도 했지만 그러했다."], ["Many causes helped to bring about this feeling.", "여러 가지 원인이 이 감정을 빚어 내는 데 한몫했다."], ["Shattered by what had happened with Mitya, she rushed on Ivan’s return to meet him as her one salvation.", "미탸와의 일로 산산이 부서진 그녀는, 이반이 돌아오자 그를 유일한 구원으로 여겨 그에게 달려들었다."], ["She was hurt, insulted and humiliated in her feelings.", "그녀는 감정에 상처를 입었고, 모욕당했으며, 굴욕을 겪었다."], ["And here the man had come back to her, who had loved her so ardently before (oh!", "그런데 바로 그 사내가 그녀에게 돌아온 것이니, 일찍이 그토록 열렬히 그녀를 사랑했고(오!"], ["she knew that very well), and whose heart and intellect she considered so superior to her own.", "그녀는 그것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다), 그 마음과 지성이 자기보다 훨씬 뛰어나다고 그녀가 여겨 온 사내였다."], ["But the sternly virtuous girl did not abandon herself altogether to the man she loved, in spite of the Karamazov violence of his passions and the great fascination he had for her.", "그러나 엄격하리만치 정숙한 이 처녀는, 카라마조프 특유의 격정과 그가 그녀에게 끼치는 커다란 매혹에도 불구하고 사랑하는 사내에게 자신을 송두리째 내맡기지는 않았다."], ["She was continually tormented at the same time by remorse for having deserted Mitya, and in moments of discord and violent anger (and they were numerous) she told Ivan so plainly.", "그녀는 동시에 미탸를 버렸다는 회한에 끊임없이 시달렸고, 불화와 격한 분노의 순간(그런 순간은 숱하게 많았다)이면 이반에게 그것을 노골적으로 말하곤 했다. 이것이 바로 이반이 알료샤에게 \"거짓말에 거짓말을 쌓는다\"고 일컬은 것이었다."], ["This was what he had called to Alyosha “lies upon lies.”", "거기에는 물론 거짓된 것이 많았고, 그것이 무엇보다도 이반을 화나게 했다……."], ["There was, of course, much that was false in it, and that angered Ivan more than anything.... But of all this later.", "그러나 이 모든 것에 대해서는 나중에 다루기로 하자."]], [["He did, in fact, for a time almost forget Smerdyakov’s existence, and yet, a fortnight after his first visit to him, he began to be haunted by the same strange thoughts as before.", "사실 그는 한동안 스메르댜코프의 존재를 거의 잊다시피 했는데, 그를 처음 찾아간 지 두 주가 지나서부터 그는 전과 똑같은 기이한 생각들에 다시 시달리기 시작했다."], ["It’s enough to say that he was continually asking himself, why was it that on that last night in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s house he had crept out on to the stairs like a thief and listened to hear what his father was doing below?", "그는 자기 자신에게 끊임없이 이렇게 물었다고만 말해 두면 충분할 것이다. 어찌하여 그날 밤 표도르 파블로비치의 집에서, 그는 도둑처럼 계단으로 살금살금 기어 나가 아버지가 아래층에서 무엇을 하는지 엿들었던가?"], ["Why had he recalled that afterwards with repulsion?", "어찌하여 그 일을 나중에 혐오로써 떠올렸던가?"], ["Why next morning, had he been suddenly so depressed on the journey?", "어찌하여 그 이튿날 아침, 그는 길에서 그토록 갑작스레 우울해졌던가?"], ["Why, as he reached Moscow, had he said to himself, “I am a scoundrel”?", "어찌하여 모스크바에 다다랐을 때, 그는 스스로에게 \"나는 비열한 놈이다\"라고 말했던가?"], ["And now he almost fancied that these tormenting thoughts would make him even forget Katerina Ivanovna, so completely did they take possession of him again.", "그리고 이제 그는 이 괴로운 생각들이 다시금 자신을 송두리째 사로잡았으므로, 그것들이 카테리나 이바노브나마저 잊게 만들 것만 같은 기분이 들 지경이었다."], ["It was just after fancying this, that he met Alyosha in the street.", "바로 이런 기분이 든 직후, 그는 길에서 알료샤를 만났다."], ["He stopped him at once, and put a question to him:", "그는 곧장 그를 멈춰 세우고 한 가지 물음을 던졌다."]], [["“Do you remember when Dmitri burst in after dinner and beat father, and afterwards I told you in the yard that I reserved ‘the right to desire’?...", "\"드미트리가 저녁 식사 후에 들이닥쳐 아버지를 때렸을 때, 그리고 그 뒤에 내가 마당에서 너에게 '바랄 권리'를 남겨 둔다고 말했던 거 기억하니?……"], ["Tell me, did you think then that I desired father’s death or not?”", "말해 봐라, 너는 그때 내가 아버지의 죽음을 바랐다고 생각했니, 아니면 아니라고 생각했니?\""]], [["“I did think so,”", "\"그렇게 생각했어요.\""], ["answered Alyosha, softly.", "알료샤가 나직하게 대답했다."]], [["“It was so, too; it was not a matter of guessing.", "\"실제로 그랬어. 짐작의 문제가 아니었지."], ["But didn’t you fancy then that what I wished was just that ‘one reptile should devour another’; that is, just that Dmitri should kill father, and as soon as possible ...", "그런데 너는 그때 내가 바라던 게 바로 '한 마리 독사가 다른 독사를 잡아먹는 것', 그러니까 바로 드미트리가 아버지를 죽이는 것, 그것도 되도록 빨리……"], ["and that I myself was even prepared to help to bring that about?”", "그리고 나 자신이 그렇게 되도록 거들 채비까지 되어 있었다고 여기지는 않았니?\""]], [["Alyosha turned rather pale, and looked silently into his brother’s face.", "알료샤는 다소 창백해지더니, 말없이 형의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Speak!”", "\"말해 봐!\""], ["cried Ivan, “I want above everything to know what you thought then.", "이반이 소리쳤다. \"나는 네가 그때 무얼 생각했는지 무엇보다도 알고 싶어."], ["I want the truth, the truth!”", "나는 진실을 원해, 진실을!\""]], [["He drew a deep breath, looking angrily at Alyosha before his answer came.", "그는 대답이 나오기 전에 알료샤를 노여운 눈으로 바라보며 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["“Forgive me, I did think that, too, at the time,”", "\"용서하세요, 저는 그때 그것도 생각했어요.\""], ["whispered Alyosha, and he did not add one softening phrase.", "알료샤가 속삭였고, 누그러뜨리는 말은 한마디도 덧붙이지 않았다."]], [["“Thanks,”", "\"고맙다.\""], ["snapped Ivan, and, leaving Alyosha, he went quickly on his way.", "이반은 쏘아붙이듯 말하고는, 알료샤를 남겨 둔 채 빠른 걸음으로 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["From that time Alyosha noticed that Ivan began obviously to avoid him and seemed even to have taken a dislike to him, so much so that Alyosha gave up going to see him.", "그때부터 알료샤는 이반이 눈에 띄게 자기를 피하기 시작했고 심지어 자기를 싫어하게 된 것 같다는 것을 알아차렸는데, 어찌나 그러했던지 알료샤는 그를 찾아가는 것을 그만두었다."], ["Immediately after that meeting with him, Ivan had not gone home, but went straight to Smerdyakov again.", "그를 만난 직후 이반은 집으로 돌아가지 않고 곧장 다시 스메르댜코프에게로 갔다."]], [["# Chapter VII. The Second Visit To Smerdyakov", "# Chapter VII. The Second Visit To Smerdyakov"]], [["By that time Smerdyakov had been discharged from the hospital.", "그 무렵 스메르댜코프는 병원에서 퇴원해 있었다."], ["Ivan knew his new lodging, the dilapidated little wooden house, divided in two by a passage on one side of which lived Marya Kondratyevna and her mother, and on the other, Smerdyakov.", "이반은 그의 새 거처를 알고 있었는데, 그것은 다 쓰러져 가는 작은 목조 가옥으로, 복도 하나를 사이에 두고 둘로 나뉘어 한쪽에는 마리야 콘드라티예브나와 그 어머니가 살았고 다른 쪽에는 스메르댜코프가 살았다."], ["No one knew on what terms he lived with them, whether as a friend or as a lodger.", "그가 그들과 어떤 사이로 지내는지는 아무도 몰랐으니, 친구로서인지 세 들어 사는 사람으로서인지조차 알 수 없었다."], ["It was supposed afterwards that he had come to stay with them as Marya Kondratyevna’s betrothed, and was living there for a time without paying for board or lodging.", "나중에 추측하기로는 그가 마리야 콘드라티예브나의 약혼자 자격으로 함께 들어와 살게 되었으며, 한동안은 숙식비를 한 푼도 내지 않고 지냈다는 것이었다."], ["Both mother and daughter had the greatest respect for him and looked upon him as greatly superior to themselves.", "어머니와 딸은 둘 다 그를 더없이 떠받들었고, 자기들보다 한참 윗길의 사람으로 우러러보았다."]], [["Ivan knocked, and, on the door being opened, went straight into the passage.", "이반은 문을 두드렸고, 문이 열리자 곧장 복도 안으로 들어섰다."], ["By Marya Kondratyevna’s directions he went straight to the better room on the left, occupied by Smerdyakov.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 일러 준 대로 그는 왼편에 있는, 스메르댜코프가 쓰는 더 나은 방으로 곧장 갔다."], ["There was a tiled stove in the room and it was extremely hot.", "방에는 타일을 붙인 난로가 있어 몹시 더웠다."], ["The walls were gay with blue paper, which was a good deal used however, and in the cracks under it cockroaches swarmed in amazing numbers, so that there was a continual rustling from them.", "벽은 푸른빛 벽지로 화사하게 발려 있었으나 적잖이 낡았고, 그 아래 갈라진 틈에는 바퀴벌레가 놀랄 만큼 우글거려 끊임없이 사각거리는 소리를 냈다."], ["The furniture was very scanty: two benches against each wall and two chairs by the table.", "가구라곤 별것이 없어서, 벽마다 의자 둘이 붙어 있고 식탁 곁에 의자 둘이 놓여 있을 뿐이었다."], ["The table of plain wood was covered with a cloth with pink patterns on it.", "거친 나무로 짠 식탁에는 분홍빛 무늬가 있는 식탁보가 덮여 있었다."], ["There was a pot of geranium on each of the two little windows.", "자그마한 두 창문에는 저마다 제라늄 화분이 하나씩 놓여 있었다."], ["In the corner there was a case of ikons.", "한쪽 구석에는 성상함이 있었다."], ["On the table stood a little copper samovar with many dents in it, and a tray with two cups.", "식탁 위에는 여기저기 우그러진 자그마한 구리 사모바르가 놓이고, 찻잔 두 개가 얹힌 쟁반이 있었다."], ["But Smerdyakov had finished tea and the samovar was out.", "그러나 스메르댜코프는 차를 다 마셔서 사모바르의 불은 꺼져 있었다."], ["He was sitting at the table on a bench.", "그는 식탁 앞 의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["He was looking at an exercise-book and slowly writing with a pen.", "그는 공책을 들여다보며 펜으로 천천히 무언가를 쓰고 있었다."], ["There was a bottle of ink by him and a flat iron candlestick, but with a composite candle.", "그의 곁에는 잉크병과 납작한 철제 촛대가 있었는데, 촛대에는 짜 맞춘 양초가 꽂혀 있었다."], ["Ivan saw at once from Smerdyakov’s face that he had completely recovered from his illness.", "이반은 스메르댜코프의 얼굴을 보자마자 그가 병에서 완전히 회복되었음을 알아차렸다."], ["His face was fresher, fuller, his hair stood up jauntily in front, and was plastered down at the sides.", "그의 얼굴은 한결 생기 있고 살이 올라 있었으며, 앞머리는 멋들어지게 곤두서 있고 양옆은 매끈하게 빗어 붙여져 있었다."], ["He was sitting in a parti-colored, wadded dressing-gown, rather dirty and frayed, however.", "그는 알록달록한 솜 누비 실내복을 걸치고 있었는데, 다만 제법 때가 묻고 너덜너덜했다."], ["He had spectacles on his nose, which Ivan had never seen him wearing before.", "코에는 안경을 걸치고 있었으니, 이반은 그가 안경 쓴 모습을 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["This trifling circumstance suddenly redoubled Ivan’s anger: “A creature like that and wearing spectacles!”", "이 하찮은 사실이 별안간 이반의 분노를 곱절로 부추겼다. \"저런 따위 위인이 안경을 다 쓰다니!\""]], [["Smerdyakov slowly raised his head and looked intently at his visitor through his spectacles; then he slowly took them off and rose from the bench, but by no means respectfully, almost lazily, doing the least possible required by common civility.", "스메르댜코프는 천천히 고개를 들어 안경 너머로 손님을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러더니 천천히 안경을 벗고 의자에서 일어섰는데, 결코 공손하다고는 할 수 없는, 거의 굼뜬 태도로, 흔한 예의가 요구하는 최소한의 것만을 마지못해 갖추었다."], ["All this struck Ivan instantly; he took it all in and noted it at once—most of all the look in Smerdyakov’s eyes, positively malicious, churlish and haughty.", "이 모든 것이 이반의 눈에 단번에 들어왔다. 그는 그것을 죄다 받아들이고 즉시 새겨 두었으니, 무엇보다도 스메르댜코프의 눈빛이 그러했는데, 그것은 영락없이 악의에 차 있고 무뚝뚝하며 거만했다."], ["“What do you want to intrude for?”", "\"뭐 하러 끼어드는 거요?\""], ["it seemed to say; “we settled everything then; why have you come again?”", "하고 그 눈빛이 말하는 듯했다. \"그때 다 정리하지 않았소."], ["Ivan could scarcely control himself.", "왜 또 온 거요?\" 이반은 좀처럼 자신을 억누를 수가 없었다."]], [["“It’s hot here,”", "\"여기는 덥군.\""], ["he said, still standing, and unbuttoned his overcoat.", "그는 여전히 선 채로 말하며 외투의 단추를 풀었다."]], [["“Take off your coat,”", "\"외투를 벗으시지요.\""], ["Smerdyakov conceded.", "스메르댜코프가 마지못해 허락하듯 말했다."]], [["Ivan took off his coat and threw it on a bench with trembling hands.", "이반은 외투를 벗어 떨리는 손으로 의자 위에 내던졌다."], ["He took a chair, moved it quickly to the table and sat down.", "그는 의자 하나를 잡아 재빨리 식탁 쪽으로 끌어다 놓고 앉았다."], ["Smerdyakov managed to sit down on his bench before him.", "스메르댜코프는 그보다 앞서 제 의자에 가까스로 앉았다."]], [["“To begin with, are we alone?”", "\"우선 묻겠는데, 여기엔 우리 둘뿐인가?\""], ["Ivan asked sternly and impulsively.", "이반이 엄하면서도 충동적으로 물었다."], ["“Can they overhear us in there?”", "\"저 안에서 우리 얘기를 엿들을 수 있나?\""]], [["“No one can hear anything.", "\"아무도 아무것도 들을 수 없습니다."], ["You’ve seen for yourself: there’s a passage.”", "직접 보셨잖습니까. 복도가 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Listen, my good fellow; what was that you babbled, as I was leaving the hospital, that if I said nothing about your faculty of shamming fits, you wouldn’t tell the investigating lawyer all our conversation at the gate?", "\"잘 들어라, 이 사람아. 내가 병원에서 나올 때 자네가 지껄인 그 소리는 대체 무슨 뜻이었나?"], ["What do you mean by all? What could you mean by it?", "내가 자네의 발작 흉내 내는 재주에 관해 입을 다물어 주면, 자네도 대문에서 우리가 나눈 얘기를 전부 예심판사에게 말하지 않겠다고 했잖나."], ["Were you threatening me?", "그 전부라는 게 무슨 뜻이야?"], ["Have I entered into some sort of compact with you?", "그게 도대체 무슨 뜻일 수가 있나? 자네 나를 협박한 건가?"], ["Do you suppose I am afraid of you?”", "내가 자네와 무슨 거래라도 맺었단 말인가? 내가 자네를 두려워하기라도 한다는 건가?\""]], [["Ivan said this in a perfect fury, giving him to understand with obvious intention that he scorned any subterfuge or indirectness and meant to show his cards.", "이반은 완전히 격분한 채로 이렇게 말했는데, 자신은 어떤 둘러대기나 에두름도 경멸하며 패를 다 드러내 보일 작정임을 분명한 의도로 그에게 알리려 했다."], ["Smerdyakov’s eyes gleamed resentfully, his left eye winked, and he at once gave his answer, with his habitual composure and deliberation.", "스메르댜코프의 눈이 원망스럽게 번득였고, 그의 왼쪽 눈이 찡긋했으며, 그는 곧바로 여느 때와 다름없는 침착함과 신중함으로 대답을 내놓았다."], ["“You want to have everything above-board; very well, you shall have it,”", "\"다 까놓고 하길 바라신다면, 좋습니다, 그렇게 해드리지요.\""], ["he seemed to say.", "하고 그가 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“This is what I meant then, and this is why I said that, that you, knowing beforehand of this murder of your own parent, left him to his fate, and that people mightn’t after that conclude any evil about your feelings and perhaps of something else, too—that’s what I promised not to tell the authorities.”", "\"제가 그때 뜻한 바는 이것이고, 또 제가 그 말을 한 까닭도 이것입니다. 도련님께서는 친아버지가 살해되리라는 것을 미리 아셨으면서도 그분을 운명에 내맡겨 두셨고, 그렇기에 사람들이 그 일로 도련님의 심사에 관해, 또 어쩌면 다른 무엇에 관해서도 좋지 못한 결론을 내릴까 봐, 바로 그것을 제가 관헌에 말하지 않겠다고 약속한 겁니다.\""]], [["Though Smerdyakov spoke without haste and obviously controlling himself, yet there was something in his voice, determined and emphatic, resentful and insolently defiant.", "스메르댜코프는 서두르지 않고, 또 분명히 자신을 억누르며 말했지만, 그래도 그 목소리에는 무언가 단호하고 힘준, 원망에 차고 뻔뻔스레 도전적인 것이 담겨 있었다."], ["He stared impudently at Ivan.", "그는 이반을 무엄하게 노려보았다."], ["A mist passed before Ivan’s eyes for the first moment.", "한순간 이반의 눈앞에 안개가 끼었다."]], [["“How? What?", "\"어째서?"], ["Are you out of your mind?”", "뭐라고? 자네 정신이 나갔나?\""]], [["“I’m perfectly in possession of all my faculties.”", "\"제 정신은 멀쩡합니다.\""]], [["“Do you suppose I knew of the murder?”", "\"내가 그 살인을 알고 있었다고 생각한단 말이냐?\""], ["Ivan cried at last, and he brought his fist violently on the table.", "이반이 마침내 소리치며 주먹으로 식탁을 사납게 내리쳤다."], ["“What do you mean by ‘something else, too’?", "\"'다른 무엇에 관해서도'라는 건 또 무슨 소리냐?"], ["Speak, scoundrel!”", "말해, 이 악당아!\""]], [["Smerdyakov was silent and still scanned Ivan with the same insolent stare.", "스메르댜코프는 잠자코, 여전히 똑같이 무엄한 눈초리로 이반을 살펴보았다."]], [["“Speak, you stinking rogue, what is that ‘something else, too’?”", "\"말하라니까, 이 구린내 나는 악당아, 그 '다른 무엇에 관해서도'라는 게 뭐야?\""]], [["“The ‘something else’ I meant was that you probably, too, were very desirous of your parent’s death.”", "\"제가 말한 그 '다른 무엇'이란, 도련님 역시 친아버지가 죽기를 무던히도 바라셨으리라는 겁니다.\""]], [["Ivan jumped up and struck him with all his might on the shoulder, so that he fell back against the wall.", "이반은 벌떡 일어나 있는 힘껏 그의 어깨를 후려쳤고, 그 바람에 그는 벽에 부딪혀 나동그라졌다."], ["In an instant his face was bathed in tears.", "순식간에 그의 얼굴이 눈물로 흠뻑 젖었다."], ["Saying, “It’s a shame, sir, to strike a sick man,”", "\"병자를 때리시다니, 너무하십니다요.\""], ["he dried his eyes with a very dirty blue check handkerchief and sank into quiet weeping.", "하고 말하며 그는 몹시 더러운 푸른 격자무늬 손수건으로 눈물을 닦더니 가만히 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."], ["A minute passed.", "일 분이 흘렀다."]], [["“That’s enough!", "\"그만!"], ["Leave off,”", "됐어.\""], ["Ivan said peremptorily, sitting down again.", "이반이 다시 앉으며 단호하게 말했다."], ["“Don’t put me out of all patience.”", "\"더 이상 내 인내심을 시험하지 마라.\""]], [["Smerdyakov took the rag from his eyes.", "스메르댜코프는 눈에서 손수건을 떼었다."], ["Every line of his puckered face reflected the insult he had just received.", "잔뜩 일그러진 그의 얼굴 구석구석에는 방금 받은 모욕이 어려 있었다."]], [["“So you thought then, you scoundrel, that together with Dmitri I meant to kill my father?”", "\"그래, 자네는 그때 생각한 건가, 이 악당아, 내가 드미트리와 한패가 되어 아버지를 죽이려 한다고?\""]], [["“I didn’t know what thoughts were in your mind then,”", "\"저는 그때 도련님 마음속에 무슨 생각이 들어 있는지 몰랐습니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov resentfully; “and so I stopped you then at the gate to sound you on that very point.”", "스메르댜코프가 원망스레 말했다. \"그래서 그때 대문에서 도련님을 붙들어 바로 그 점을 떠보려 한 겁니다.\""]], [["“To sound what, what?”", "\"무엇을 떠봐, 무엇을?\""]], [["“Why, that very circumstance, whether you wanted your father to be murdered or not.”", "\"아니, 바로 그 사정 말입니다, 도련님께서 아버지가 살해되기를 바라시는지 아닌지를요.\""]], [["What infuriated Ivan more than anything was the aggressive, insolent tone to which Smerdyakov persistently adhered.", "이반을 무엇보다 격노하게 한 것은, 스메르댜코프가 끈질기게 고수하는 그 공격적이고 무엄한 말투였다."]], [["“It was you murdered him?”", "\"네가 아버지를 죽인 거지?\""], ["he cried suddenly.", "그가 별안간 소리쳤다."]], [["Smerdyakov smiled contemptuously.", "스메르댜코프는 경멸하듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“You know of yourself, for a fact, that it wasn’t I murdered him.", "\"제가 그분을 죽인 게 아니라는 건 도련님께서도 사실로 잘 아시잖습니까."], ["And I should have thought that there was no need for a sensible man to speak of it again.”", "그리고 분별 있는 분이라면 그 얘기를 또 입에 올리실 필요가 없으리라 생각했습니다요.\""]], [["“But why, why had you such a suspicion about me at the time?”", "\"하지만 어째서, 어째서 그때 자네는 나를 그렇게 의심했나?\""]], [["“As you know already, it was simply from fear.", "\"이미 아시다시피, 그저 두려움 때문이었습니다."], ["For I was in such a position, shaking with fear, that I suspected every one.", "저는 그런 처지에 놓여, 두려움에 벌벌 떨며 누구든 다 의심했으니까요."], ["I resolved to sound you, too, for I thought if you wanted the same as your brother, then the business was as good as settled and I should be crushed like a fly, too.”", "도련님도 떠보기로 마음먹은 건, 만일 도련님이 형님과 똑같은 것을 바라신다면 그 일은 이미 다 끝난 거나 진배없고, 저도 파리처럼 짓이겨지리라 생각했기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Look here, you didn’t say that a fortnight ago.”", "\"이봐, 자네는 보름 전에는 그런 말을 하지 않았어.\""]], [["“I meant the same when I talked to you in the hospital, only I thought you’d understand without wasting words, and that being such a sensible man you wouldn’t care to talk of it openly.”", "\"병원에서 도련님과 얘기할 때도 똑같은 뜻이었습니다요. 다만 도련님이 말을 허비하지 않아도 알아들으시리라, 또 그토록 분별 있는 분이니 그걸 대놓고 말하길 꺼리시리라 생각했을 따름입니다.\""]], [["“What next!", "\"그래서 그다음은!"], ["Come answer, answer, I insist: what was it ...", "대답해, 대답하라니까, 내가 명한다. 그게 무엇이었나…"], ["what could I have done to put such a degrading suspicion into your mean soul?”", "내가 무엇을 했기에 자네의 그 비열한 영혼 속에 그런 모욕적인 의심을 품게 했단 말이냐?\""]], [["“As for the murder, you couldn’t have done that and didn’t want to, but as for wanting some one else to do it, that was just what you did want.”", "\"살인으로 말하자면, 도련님은 그걸 하실 수도 없었고 바라지도 않으셨지요. 하지만 누군가 다른 사람이 그 일을 해주기를 바라는 것으로 말하면, 그거야말로 도련님이 정말로 바라시던 바였습니다.\""]], [["“And how coolly, how coolly he speaks!", "\"한데 어쩌면 저토록 태연하게, 저토록 태연하게 말하는가!"], ["But why should I have wanted it; what grounds had I for wanting it?”", "그런데 내가 왜 그것을 바랐겠나. 내가 무슨 근거로 그것을 바랐겠어?\""]], [["“What grounds had you?", "\"무슨 근거냐고요?"], ["What about the inheritance?”", "유산은 어쩌고요?\""], ["said Smerdyakov sarcastically, and, as it were, vindictively.", "스메르댜코프가 비아냥대듯, 그리고 흡사 앙심을 품은 듯이 말했다."], ["“Why, after your parent’s death there was at least forty thousand to come to each of you, and very likely more, but if Fyodor Pavlovitch got married then to that lady, Agrafena Alexandrovna, she would have had all his capital made over to her directly after the wedding, for she’s plenty of sense, so that your parent would not have left you two roubles between the three of you.", "\"아니, 아버님이 돌아가시면 도련님들 각자에게 적어도 사만 루블씩은 돌아올 테고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 많겠지요. 하지만 만일 표도르 파블로비치께서 그 마님, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 그때 결혼하셨다면, 그 마님은 머리가 잘 돌아가는 분이니 혼인하자마자 그분 재산을 죄다 제 명의로 옮겨 놓았을 겁니다."], ["And were they far from a wedding, either?", "그랬다면 아버님은 세 분께 통틀어 이 루블도 남기지 않으셨겠지요. 게다가 결혼이 머나먼 일이었던가요?"], ["Not a hair’s-breadth: that lady had only to lift her little finger and he would have run after her to church, with his tongue out.”", "머리카락 한 올만큼도 멀지 않았습니다요. 그 마님이 새끼손가락만 까딱하면 아버님은 혀를 빼물고 교회로 쫓아 달려가셨을 테니까요.\""]], [["Ivan restrained himself with painful effort.", "이반은 괴로운 노력으로 자신을 억눌렀다."]], [["“Very good,”", "\"좋다.\""], ["he commented at last.", "그가 마침내 입을 열었다."], ["“You see, I haven’t jumped up, I haven’t knocked you down, I haven’t killed you.", "\"보다시피 나는 벌떡 일어나지도, 너를 때려눕히지도, 죽이지도 않았다."], ["Speak on.", "계속 말해라."], ["So, according to you, I had fixed on Dmitri to do it; I was reckoning on him?”", "그래, 네 말대로라면 나는 드미트리가 그 일을 하도록 점찍어 두었다, 나는 그를 믿고 셈을 하고 있었다는 거냐?\""]], [["“How could you help reckoning on him?", "\"어찌 그분을 믿고 셈하지 않으셨겠습니까?"], ["If he killed him, then he would lose all the rights of a nobleman, his rank and property, and would go off to exile; so his share of the inheritance would come to you and your brother Alexey Fyodorovitch in equal parts; so you’d each have not forty, but sixty thousand each.", "만일 그분이 아버님을 죽이면 귀족으로서의 모든 권리와 신분과 재산을 잃고 유형을 떠나게 되지요. 그러면 그분 몫의 유산이 도련님과 아우 알렉세이 표도로비치께 똑같이 나뉘어 돌아올 테니, 두 분이 각각 사만이 아니라 육만씩 갖게 되는 겁니다."], ["There’s not a doubt you did reckon on Dmitri Fyodorovitch.”", "도련님이 드미트리 표도로비치를 믿고 셈하셨다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다요.\""]], [["“What I put up with from you!", "\"내가 너한테 별꼴을 다 당하는구나!"], ["Listen, scoundrel, if I had reckoned on any one then, it would have been on you, not on Dmitri, and I swear I did expect some wickedness from you ...", "잘 들어라, 이 악당아, 내가 그때 누군가를 믿고 셈했다면, 그건 드미트리가 아니라 바로 너였을 거다. 그리고 맹세컨대 나는 너에게서 무슨 흉악한 짓을 예감하고 있었어…"], ["at the time....", "그때…"], ["I remember my impression!”", "그 인상이 지금도 기억난다!\""]], [["“I thought, too, for a minute, at the time, that you were reckoning on me as well,”", "\"저 역시 그때 한순간, 도련님이 저까지 믿고 셈하시는 게 아닌가 생각했습니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with a sarcastic grin.", "스메르댜코프가 비아냥대는 웃음을 띠며 말했다."], ["“So that it was just by that more than anything you showed me what was in your mind.", "\"그러니 다른 무엇보다도 바로 그 점으로 도련님은 제게 마음속을 내보이신 겁니다."], ["For if you had a foreboding about me and yet went away, you as good as said to me, ‘You can murder my parent, I won’t hinder you!’”", "저에 대해 무슨 예감을 품으시고도 떠나 버리셨으니, 도련님은 제게 '내 아버지를 죽여도 좋다, 나는 너를 막지 않겠다!' 하고 말씀하신 거나 마찬가지니까요.\""]], [["“You scoundrel!", "\"이 악당아!"], ["So that’s how you understood it!”", "그래, 그런 식으로 알아들었단 말이냐!\""]], [["“It was all that going to Tchermashnya.", "\"죄다 그 체르마시냐로 가신 일 때문입니다."], ["Why!", "아니!"], ["You were meaning to go to Moscow and refused all your father’s entreaties to go to Tchermashnya—and simply at a foolish word from me you consented at once!", "도련님은 모스크바로 가려 하시면서 아버님이 체르마시냐로 가달라고 그토록 간청해도 죄다 마다하시더니, 그저 저의 어리석은 한마디에 당장 응하시지 않았습니까!"], ["What reason had you to consent to Tchermashnya?", "무슨 까닭으로 체르마시냐 행에 응하셨습니까?"], ["Since you went to Tchermashnya with no reason, simply at my word, it shows that you must have expected something from me.”", "아무 까닭도 없이, 그저 저의 한마디에 체르마시냐로 가셨다는 건, 도련님이 저에게서 무언가를 기대하셨다는 걸 보여 주는 겁니다.\""]], [["“No, I swear I didn’t!”", "\"아니다, 맹세코 나는 기대하지 않았어!\""], ["shouted Ivan, grinding his teeth.", "이반이 이를 갈며 외쳤다."]], [["“You didn’t?", "\"기대하지 않으셨다고요?"], ["Then you ought, as your father’s son, to have had me taken to the lock-up and thrashed at once for my words then ...", "그렇다면 아버님의 아들로서, 그때 저의 그런 말 때문에라도 저를 당장 유치장에 처넣고 매질을 시키셨어야지요… 적어도 그 자리에서 제 얼굴을 한 대 후려치셨어야 합니다."], ["or at least, to have given me a punch in the face on the spot, but you were not a bit angry, if you please, and at once in a friendly way acted on my foolish word and went away, which was utterly absurd, for you ought to have stayed to save your parent’s life.", "한데 도련님은, 미안한 말씀이지만, 조금도 화내지 않으시고 곧장 다정하게 저의 어리석은 말을 따라 떠나 버리셨으니, 이건 더없이 어처구니없는 일입니다. 도련님은 아버님의 목숨을 구하려고 남아 계셨어야 마땅하니까요."], ["How could I help drawing my conclusions?”", "그런데 제가 어찌 그런 결론을 내리지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?\""]], [["Ivan sat scowling, both his fists convulsively pressed on his knees.", "이반은 두 주먹을 무릎에 경련하듯 짓누른 채 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Yes, I am sorry I didn’t punch you in the face,”", "\"그래, 네 얼굴을 한 대 후려치지 않은 게 후회스럽다.\""], ["he said with a bitter smile.", "그가 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t have taken you to the lock-up just then.", "\"그때 너를 유치장에 처넣을 수는 없었지."], ["Who would have believed me and what charge could I bring against you?", "누가 내 말을 믿어 주었겠으며, 너에게 무슨 죄목을 씌울 수 있었겠나?"], ["But the punch in the face ...", "하지만 얼굴을 한 대 친다는 건…"], ["oh, I’m sorry I didn’t think of it.", "아, 그 생각을 못 한 게 분하구나."], ["Though blows are forbidden, I should have pounded your ugly face to a jelly.”", "손찌검이 금지돼 있긴 하지만, 네 그 흉한 얼굴을 짓이겨 곤죽을 만들어 놓았어야 했는데.\""]], [["Smerdyakov looked at him almost with relish.", "스메르댜코프는 거의 흐뭇한 듯이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“In the ordinary occasions of life,” he said in the same complacent and sententious tone in which he had taunted Grigory and argued with him about religion at Fyodor Pavlovitch’s table, “in the ordinary occasions of life, blows on the face are forbidden nowadays by law, and people have given them up, but in exceptional occasions of life people still fly to blows, not only among us but all over the world, be it even the fullest Republic of France, just as in the time of Adam and Eve, and they never will leave off, but you, even in an exceptional case, did not dare.”", "\"평범한 일상사에서는,\" 그는 일찍이 표도르 파블로비치의 식탁에서 그리고리를 비웃고 그와 종교를 두고 입씨름하던 때와 똑같이 자족적이고 잘난 체하는 말투로 말했다. \"평범한 일상사에서는 얼굴을 치는 것이 요즘 법으로 금지되어 있어 사람들이 다들 그만두었습지요. 하지만 예외적인 경우에는 사람들이 여전히 주먹질에 의지하는데, 그건 우리나라만 그런 게 아니라 온 세상이 다, 설령 그게 가장 자유로운 프랑스 공화국이라 해도, 아담과 이브의 시절과 똑같이 그러하며, 결코 그만두는 법이 없을 겁니다. 그런데 도련님은 예외적인 경우에도 감히 그렇게 하지 못하셨군요.\""]], [["“What are you learning French words for?”", "\"프랑스어 단어는 뭐 하러 배우는 거냐?\""], ["Ivan nodded towards the exercise-book lying on the table.", "이반이 식탁 위에 놓인 공책 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."]], [["“Why shouldn’t I learn them so as to improve my education, supposing that I may myself chance to go some day to those happy parts of Europe?”", "\"제 교양을 좀 닦자고 배우는 게 어째서 안 됩니까, 언젠가 저 자신이 유럽의 그 행복한 고장에 가게 될 기회가 올지도 모르는데요?\""]], [["“Listen, monster.”", "\"잘 들어라, 이 괴물 같은 놈.\""], ["Ivan’s eyes flashed and he trembled all over.", "이반의 눈이 번득였고 그는 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. \"나는 네 고발 따위 두렵지 않다."], ["“I am not afraid of your accusations; you can say what you like about me, and if I don’t beat you to death, it’s simply because I suspect you of that crime and I’ll drag you to justice.", "나에 대해 무슨 말이든 멋대로 지껄여라. 그리고 내가 너를 때려죽이지 않는 건, 오로지 네가 그 범죄를 저질렀다고 의심하기 때문이며, 나는 너를 법의 심판대로 끌고 갈 것이다."], ["I’ll unmask you.”", "네 정체를 까발리고 말겠다.\""]], [["“To my thinking, you’d better keep quiet, for what can you accuse me of, considering my absolute innocence?", "\"제 생각엔, 도련님은 가만히 계시는 게 좋을 겁니다. 제 결백이 이토록 완전한데 무슨 죄목으로 저를 고발하시겠습니까?"], ["and who would believe you?", "게다가 누가 도련님 말을 믿겠습니까?"], ["Only if you begin, I shall tell everything, too, for I must defend myself.”", "다만 도련님이 시작하시면 저도 죄다 털어놓겠습니다, 제 몸은 제가 지켜야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Do you think I am afraid of you now?”", "\"내가 이제 와서 너를 두려워한다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“If the court doesn’t believe all I’ve said to you just now, the public will, and you will be ashamed.”", "\"법정이 제가 방금 도련님께 한 말을 다 믿지 않더라도, 세상 사람들은 믿을 테고, 그러면 도련님이 부끄러워지실 겁니다.\""]], [["“That’s as much as to say, ‘It’s always worth while speaking to a sensible man,’ eh?”", "\"결국 '분별 있는 사람과는 언제든 얘기할 만하다'는 소리로구나, 응?\""], ["snarled Ivan.", "이반이 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“You hit the mark, indeed.", "\"과연 정곡을 찌르셨습니다."], ["And you’d better be sensible.”", "그러니 도련님도 분별 있게 처신하시는 게 좋을 겁니다.\""]], [["Ivan got up, shaking all over with indignation, put on his coat, and without replying further to Smerdyakov, without even looking at him, walked quickly out of the cottage.", "이반은 분개에 온몸을 떨며 일어나 외투를 걸쳤고, 스메르댜코프에게 더는 대꾸하지 않고, 거들떠보지도 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 오두막을 나섰다."], ["The cool evening air refreshed him.", "서늘한 저녁 공기가 그를 상쾌하게 했다."], ["There was a bright moon in the sky.", "하늘에는 밝은 달이 떠 있었다."], ["A nightmare of ideas and sensations filled his soul.", "갖가지 상념과 감각의 악몽이 그의 영혼을 가득 메웠다."], ["“Shall I go at once and give information against Smerdyakov?", "\"당장 가서 스메르댜코프를 고발할까?"], ["But what information can I give?", "하지만 내가 무슨 고발을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He is not guilty, anyway.", "어쨌든 그는 죄가 없다."], ["On the contrary, he’ll accuse me.", "오히려 그가 나를 고발할 것이다."], ["And in fact, why did I set off for Tchermashnya then?", "그리고 사실, 그때 나는 왜 체르마시냐로 떠났던가?"], ["What for?", "무엇 때문에?"], ["What for?”", "무엇 때문에?\""], ["Ivan asked himself.", "이반은 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“Yes, of course, I was expecting something and he is right....”", "\"그래, 물론, 나는 무언가를 기대하고 있었다, 그리고 그의 말이 옳다….\""], ["And he remembered for the hundredth time how, on the last night in his father’s house, he had listened on the stairs.", "그러고는 그날 아버지 집에서 보낸 마지막 밤에 자신이 계단에서 어떻게 귀를 기울이고 있었는지를 백 번째로 떠올렸다."], ["But he remembered it now with such anguish that he stood still on the spot as though he had been stabbed.", "그러나 이제 그것을 어찌나 괴롭게 떠올렸던지, 그는 마치 칼에 찔리기라도 한 것처럼 그 자리에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["“Yes, I expected it then, that’s true!", "\"그래, 나는 그때 그것을 기대하고 있었어, 그건 사실이다!"], ["I wanted the murder, I did want the murder!", "나는 그 살인을 바랐다, 나는 정말로 그 살인을 바랐어!"], ["Did I want the murder?", "내가 그 살인을 바랐던가?"], ["Did I want it?", "내가 그것을 바랐던가?"], ["I must kill Smerdyakov!", "스메르댜코프를 죽여야 한다!"], ["If I don’t dare kill Smerdyakov now, life is not worth living!”", "지금 스메르댜코프를 죽일 용기조차 없다면, 산다는 것이 다 무슨 소용이란 말인가!\""]], [["Ivan did not go home, but went straight to Katerina Ivanovna and alarmed her by his appearance.", "이반은 집으로 가지 않고 곧장 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 갔으며, 그의 몰골에 그녀는 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["He was like a madman.", "그는 미친 사람 같았다."], ["He repeated all his conversation with Smerdyakov, every syllable of it.", "그는 스메르댜코프와 나눈 대화를 한 마디 한 마디 빠짐없이 죄다 되풀이했다."], ["He couldn’t be calmed, however much she tried to soothe him: he kept walking about the room, speaking strangely, disconnectedly.", "그녀가 아무리 달래 보아도 그는 진정되지 않았으니, 그는 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 기이하고 두서없이 지껄여 댔다."], ["At last he sat down, put his elbows on the table, leaned his head on his hands and pronounced this strange sentence: “If it’s not Dmitri, but Smerdyakov who’s the murderer, I share his guilt, for I put him up to it.", "마침내 그는 자리에 앉아 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고 두 손에 머리를 떨군 채 이런 기이한 말을 내뱉었다. \"만일 드미트리가 아니라 스메르댜코프가 살인자라면, 나는 그의 죄를 함께 진 것이오, 내가 그를 부추겨 그 일을 하게 했으니까."], ["Whether I did, I don’t know yet.", "내가 정말 그랬는지는 아직 나도 모르오."], ["But if he is the murderer, and not Dmitri, then, of course, I am the murderer, too.”", "하지만 만일 드미트리가 아니라 그가 살인자라면, 그렇다면 물론, 나도 살인자인 것이오.\""]], [["When Katerina Ivanovna heard that, she got up from her seat without a word, went to her writing-table, opened a box standing on it, took out a sheet of paper and laid it before Ivan.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 그 말을 듣자 말없이 자리에서 일어나 자신의 책상으로 가서, 그 위에 놓인 상자를 열고 종이 한 장을 꺼내 이반 앞에 내려놓았다."], ["This was the document of which Ivan spoke to Alyosha later on as a “conclusive proof” that Dmitri had killed his father.", "이것이 바로 훗날 이반이 알료샤에게 드미트리가 아버지를 죽였다는 \"결정적 증거\"라고 말한 그 문서였다."], ["It was the letter written by Mitya to Katerina Ivanovna when he was drunk, on the very evening he met Alyosha at the crossroads on the way to the monastery, after the scene at Katerina Ivanovna’s, when Grushenka had insulted her.", "그것은 미탸가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 술에 취해 쓴 편지로, 바로 그날 저녁 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 그루셴카가 그녀를 모욕한 그 소동이 있은 뒤, 수도원으로 가던 길의 갈림길에서 알료샤와 마주쳤던 그날 밤에 쓴 것이었다."], ["Then, parting from Alyosha, Mitya had rushed to Grushenka.", "그때 알료샤와 헤어진 미탸는 그루셴카에게로 달려갔다."], ["I don’t know whether he saw her, but in the evening he was at the “Metropolis,” where he got thoroughly drunk.", "그가 그녀를 만났는지는 모르겠으나, 저녁에 그는 '메트로폴리스'에 있었고 거기서 곤드레만드레 취하도록 마셨다."], ["Then he asked for pen and paper and wrote a document of weighty consequences to himself.", "그러고는 펜과 종이를 청해 제 자신에게 중대한 결과를 가져올 문서 한 장을 썼다."], ["It was a wordy, disconnected, frantic letter, a drunken letter in fact.", "그것은 장황하고 두서없으며 광란에 찬 편지, 한마디로 술 취한 편지였다."], ["It was like the talk of a drunken man, who, on his return home, begins with extraordinary heat telling his wife or one of his household how he has just been insulted, what a rascal had just insulted him, what a fine fellow he is on the other hand, and how he will pay that scoundrel out; and all that at great length, with great excitement and incoherence, with drunken tears and blows on the table.", "그것은 마치 술 취한 사내의 넋두리 같았으니, 집에 돌아온 그가 아내나 식구 가운데 누군가를 붙들고 자기가 방금 어떤 모욕을 당했는지, 어떤 악당이 방금 자기를 모욕했는지, 반면에 자기는 또 얼마나 훌륭한 사내인지, 그리고 자기가 그 악당에게 어떻게 앙갚음할 것인지를 엄청난 열기로 늘어놓기 시작하는 것과 같았다. 그 모든 것을 한없이 길게, 굉장한 흥분과 횡설수설로, 술 취한 눈물과 식탁을 내리치는 주먹질을 곁들여 가며 떠벌리는 것이다."], ["The letter was written on a dirty piece of ordinary paper of the cheapest kind.", "편지는 가장 싸구려 보통 종이의 더러운 한 장에 쓰여 있었다."], ["It had been provided by the tavern and there were figures scrawled on the back of it.", "그것은 술집에서 내준 것으로, 뒷면에는 숫자가 갈겨져 있었다."], ["There was evidently not space enough for his drunken verbosity and Mitya not only filled the margins but had written the last line right across the rest.", "분명 그의 술 취한 장광설을 담기엔 자리가 모자랐던지, 미탸는 여백까지 죄다 채우고도 마지막 줄은 나머지 글 위로 가로질러 써 놓았다."], ["The letter ran as follows:", "편지의 내용은 다음과 같았다."]], [["FATAL KATYA: To-morrow I will get the money and repay your three thousand and farewell, woman of great wrath, but farewell, too, my love!", "운명의 카탸. 내일 나는 돈을 마련해 당신의 삼천 루블을 갚을 것이오, 그러니 잘 있으시오, 진노에 가득 찬 여인이여, 그래도 잘 있으시오, 내 사랑이여!"], ["Let us make an end!", "우리 이제 끝장을 냅시다!"], ["To-morrow I shall try and get it from every one, and if I can’t borrow it, I give you my word of honor I shall go to my father and break his skull and take the money from under the pillow, if only Ivan has gone.", "내일 나는 누구한테서든 그것을 마련해 보려 애쓸 것이고, 만일 빌리지 못하면, 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 나는 아버지에게 가서 그 두개골을 쪼개고 베개 밑에서 돈을 꺼내 가겠소, 이반만 떠나고 없다면 말이오."], ["If I have to go to Siberia for it, I’ll give you back your three thousand.", "그것 때문에 시베리아로 가야 한다 해도, 나는 당신의 삼천 루블을 돌려주겠소."], ["And farewell.", "그리고 잘 있으시오."], ["I bow down to the ground before you, for I’ve been a scoundrel to you.", "나는 당신 앞에 땅바닥에 엎드려 절하오, 내가 당신에게 악당 노릇을 했으니."], ["Forgive me!", "나를 용서해 주오!"], ["No, better not forgive me, you’ll be happier and so shall I!", "아니, 차라리 용서하지 마오, 그래야 당신도 나도 더 행복할 테니!"], ["Better Siberia than your love, for I love another woman and you got to know her too well to-day, so how can you forgive?", "당신의 사랑보다는 차라리 시베리아가 낫소, 나는 다른 여자를 사랑하며, 당신도 오늘 그 여자를 너무 잘 알게 되었으니, 어찌 당신이 용서할 수 있겠소?"], ["I will murder the man who’s robbed me!", "나는 내게서 훔쳐 간 그 사내를 죽일 것이오!"], ["I’ll leave you all and go to the East so as to see no one again.", "나는 당신들 모두를 버리고 동방으로 떠나 다시는 아무도 보지 않으리다."], ["Not her either, for you are not my only tormentress; she is too.", "그 여자도 보지 않겠소, 당신만이 나를 괴롭히는 게 아니라 그 여자도 그러하니."], ["Farewell!", "잘 있으시오!"], ["P.S.—I write my curse, but I adore you!", "추신. 나는 저주를 쓰면서도, 당신을 흠모하오!"], ["I hear it in my heart.", "내 가슴속에서 그것이 들려오오."], ["One string is left, and it vibrates.", "줄 하나가 남아 떨리고 있소."], ["Better tear my heart in two!", "차라리 내 가슴을 둘로 찢어 버리는 게 낫겠소!"], ["I shall kill myself, but first of all that cur.", "나는 스스로 목숨을 끊겠지만, 무엇보다 먼저 그 개 같은 놈부터."], ["I shall tear three thousand from him and fling it to you.", "나는 그놈에게서 삼천을 뜯어내 당신에게 내던지겠소."], ["Though I’ve been a scoundrel to you, I am not a thief!", "내가 당신에게 악당 노릇을 했을망정, 나는 도둑은 아니오!"], ["You can expect three thousand.", "당신은 삼천을 기다려도 좋소."], ["The cur keeps it under his mattress, in pink ribbon.", "그 개 같은 놈은 그걸 자기 요 밑에, 분홍빛 리본에 싸서 간직하고 있소."], ["I am not a thief, but I’ll murder my thief.", "나는 도둑이 아니지만, 내 것을 훔친 도둑은 죽이겠소."], ["Katya, don’t look disdainful.", "카탸, 경멸하는 눈으로 보지 마오."], ["Dmitri is not a thief! but a murderer!", "드미트리는 도둑이 아니라 살인자요!"], ["He has murdered his father and ruined himself to hold his ground, rather than endure your pride.", "그는 당신의 오만을 견디느니 차라리 제 입지를 지키려고 아버지를 죽이고 자신을 망쳤소."], ["And he doesn’t love you.", "그리고 그는 당신을 사랑하지 않소."], ["P.P.S.—I kiss your feet, farewell!", "재추신. 당신의 발에 입 맞추오, 잘 있으시오!"], ["P.P.P.S.—Katya, pray to God that some one’ll give me the money.", "재재추신. 카탸, 누군가 내게 그 돈을 주도록 하느님께 기도해 주오."], ["Then I shall not be steeped in gore, and if no one does—I shall!", "그러면 나는 피에 물들지 않을 테지만, 아무도 주지 않는다면 나는 물들고 말 것이오!"], ["Kill me!", "나를 죽여 주오!"]], [["Your slave and enemy, D. KARAMAZOV.", "당신의 종이자 적, 드미트리 카라마조프."]], [["When Ivan read this “document”", "이반은 이 \"문서\"를 읽고 확신했다."], ["he was convinced. So then it was his brother, not Smerdyakov.", "그러니까 살인을 저지른 것은 스메르댜코프가 아니라 그의 형이었던 것이다."], ["And if not Smerdyakov, then not he, Ivan.", "그리고 스메르댜코프가 아니라면, 그 자신, 이반도 아니었다."], ["This letter at once assumed in his eyes the aspect of a logical proof.", "이 편지는 그의 눈에 단번에 논리적 증거의 모양새를 띠었다."], ["There could be no longer the slightest doubt of Mitya’s guilt.", "미탸의 죄에 관해서는 이제 추호의 의심도 있을 수 없었다."], ["The suspicion never occurred to Ivan, by the way, that Mitya might have committed the murder in conjunction with Smerdyakov, and, indeed, such a theory did not fit in with the facts.", "그런데 미탸가 스메르댜코프와 한패가 되어 살인을 저질렀을지도 모른다는 의혹은 이반의 머릿속에 한 번도 떠오르지 않았으며, 실제로 그런 가설은 여러 정황과 들어맞지도 않았다."], ["Ivan was completely reassured.", "이반은 완전히 안심했다."], ["The next morning he only thought of Smerdyakov and his gibes with contempt.", "이튿날 아침 그는 스메르댜코프와 그의 빈정거림을 그저 경멸하는 마음으로 떠올릴 뿐이었다."], ["A few days later he positively wondered how he could have been so horribly distressed at his suspicions.", "며칠 뒤에는 어떻게 자신이 그런 의혹 때문에 그토록 끔찍하게 괴로워할 수 있었는지 정말로 의아해할 정도였다."], ["He resolved to dismiss him with contempt and forget him.", "그는 그를 경멸로 떨쳐 내고 잊어버리기로 작정했다."], ["So passed a month.", "그렇게 한 달이 지나갔다."], ["He made no further inquiry about Smerdyakov, but twice he happened to hear that he was very ill and out of his mind.", "그는 스메르댜코프에 관해 더는 알아보지 않았으나, 두 차례 우연히 그가 몹시 병들어 정신이 온전치 못하다는 말을 들었다."]], [["“He’ll end in madness,”", "\"저 사람은 미쳐 버리고 말 겁니다.\""], ["the young doctor Varvinsky observed about him, and Ivan remembered this.", "젊은 의사 바르빈스키가 그를 두고 한 말이었는데, 이반은 이 말을 기억해 두었다."], ["During the last week of that month Ivan himself began to feel very ill.", "그달의 마지막 주에 이반 자신도 몹시 몸이 아프기 시작했다."], ["He went to consult the Moscow doctor who had been sent for by Katerina Ivanovna just before the trial.", "그는 재판이 열리기 직전에 카테리나 이바노브나가 불러온 모스크바의 의사를 찾아가 진찰을 받았다."], ["And just at that time his relations with Katerina Ivanovna became acutely strained.", "그리고 바로 그 무렵 카테리나 이바노브나와의 관계가 첨예하게 틀어졌다."], ["They were like two enemies in love with one another.", "두 사람은 마치 서로 사랑하는 두 원수와도 같았다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna’s “returns” to Mitya, that is, her brief but violent revulsions of feeling in his favor, drove Ivan to perfect frenzy.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 미탸에게로 \"되돌아가는 것\", 곧 그에게 유리한 쪽으로 짧지만 격렬하게 감정이 뒤바뀌는 것은 이반을 완전한 광란으로 몰아넣었다."], ["Strange to say, until that last scene described above, when Alyosha came from Mitya to Katerina Ivanovna, Ivan had never once, during that month, heard her express a doubt of Mitya’s guilt, in spite of those “returns” that were so hateful to him.", "기이한 노릇이지만, 앞서 묘사한 그 마지막 장면, 곧 알료샤가 미탸의 부탁으로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 찾아간 그때에 이르기까지, 이반은 그달 내내 그토록 가증스럽던 그 \"되돌아감\"에도 불구하고 그녀가 미탸의 죄를 의심하는 말을 단 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었다."], ["It is remarkable, too, that while he felt that he hated Mitya more and more every day, he realized that it was not on account of Katya’s “returns”", "또 한 가지 주목할 만한 것은, 그가 미탸를 날이 갈수록 점점 더 미워한다고 느끼면서도, 그 미움이 카탸의 \"되돌아감\""], ["that he hated him, but just because he was the murderer of his father.", "때문이 아니라 바로 그가 아버지를 죽인 살인자이기 때문임을 깨달았다는 점이다."], ["He was conscious of this and fully recognized it to himself.", "그는 이를 의식하고 있었고 스스로에게도 그것을 온전히 인정했다."]], [["Nevertheless, he went to see Mitya ten days before the trial and proposed to him a plan of escape—a plan he had obviously thought over a long time.", "그럼에도 그는 재판이 열리기 열흘 전에 미탸를 찾아가 그에게 탈출 계획을 제안했으니, 그것은 분명 그가 오랫동안 곰곰이 궁리해 온 계획이었다."], ["He was partly impelled to do this by a sore place still left in his heart from a phrase of Smerdyakov’s, that it was to his, Ivan’s, advantage that his brother should be convicted, as that would increase his inheritance and Alyosha’s from forty to sixty thousand roubles.", "그가 이렇게 하게 된 데에는, 형이 유죄를 선고받는 것이 이반 자신에게 이로운 일이라던, 그렇게 되면 자기와 알료샤의 유산이 사만에서 육만 루블로 불어난다던 스메르댜코프의 한마디가 여전히 그의 가슴속에 쓰라린 상처로 남아 있던 것이 한몫했다."], ["He determined to sacrifice thirty thousand on arranging Mitya’s escape.", "그는 미탸의 탈출을 주선하는 데 삼만 루블을 내놓기로 작정했다."], ["On his return from seeing him, he was very mournful and dispirited; he suddenly began to feel that he was anxious for Mitya’s escape, not only to heal that sore place by sacrificing thirty thousand, but for another reason.", "그를 만나고 돌아오는 길에 그는 몹시 침울하고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그는 별안간, 자신이 미탸의 탈출을 바라는 것이 삼만을 내놓아 그 쓰라린 상처를 치유하기 위해서일 뿐만 아니라 또 다른 이유 때문임을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["“Is it because I am as much a murderer at heart?”", "\"그건 내 마음속도 그만큼 살인자이기 때문인가?\""], ["he asked himself.", "그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["Something very deep down seemed burning and rankling in his soul.", "무언가 아주 깊은 곳에서 그의 영혼을 태우고 쓰라리게 좀먹는 듯했다."], ["His pride above all suffered cruelly all that month.", "무엇보다도 그의 자존심이 그달 내내 잔혹하게 고통받았다."], ["But of that later....", "그러나 그것은 나중에 이야기하기로 하자…."]], [["When, after his conversation with Alyosha, Ivan suddenly decided with his hand on the bell of his lodging to go to Smerdyakov, he obeyed a sudden and peculiar impulse of indignation.", "알료샤와 대화를 나눈 뒤, 이반이 자기 거처의 초인종에 손을 얹은 채 별안간 스메르댜코프에게 가기로 결심했을 때, 그는 갑작스럽고 기이한 분노의 충동에 따른 것이었다."], ["He suddenly remembered how Katerina Ivanovna had only just cried out to him in Alyosha’s presence: “It was you, you, persuaded me of his”", "그는 카테리나 이바노브나가 방금 알료샤가 보는 앞에서 자기를 향해 이렇게 외쳤던 것이 불현듯 떠올랐다. \"그의(곧 미탸의) 죄를 내게 믿게 한 건 당신이에요, 바로 당신이라고요!\""], ["(that is, Mitya’s) “guilt!” Ivan was thunderstruck when he recalled it.", "이반은 그 말을 떠올리고 벼락을 맞은 듯 멍해졌다."], ["He had never once tried to persuade her that Mitya was the murderer; on the contrary, he had suspected himself in her presence, that time when he came back from Smerdyakov.", "그는 미탸가 살인자라고 그녀를 설득하려 한 적이 단 한 번도 없었다. 오히려 그는 스메르댜코프에게서 돌아왔던 그때, 그녀가 보는 앞에서 자기 자신을 의심하기까지 했었다."], ["It was she, she, who had produced that “document”", "그 \"문서\"를 내놓아 형의 죄를 입증한 것은 바로 그녀, 그녀였다."], ["and proved his brother’s guilt.", "그런데 이제 그녀가 별안간 이렇게 외친 것이다."], ["And now she suddenly exclaimed: “I’ve been at Smerdyakov’s myself!”", "\"나도 스메르댜코프한테 직접 다녀왔다고요!\" 그녀가 언제 거기에 갔단 말인가?"], ["When had she been there?", "이반은 그것을 전혀 모르고 있었다."], ["Ivan had known nothing of it. So she was not at all so sure of Mitya’s guilt!", "그러니 그녀는 미탸의 죄를 결코 그렇게 확신하고 있던 게 아니었던 것이다!"], ["And what could Smerdyakov have told her?", "그리고 스메르댜코프가 대체 그녀에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있었을까?"], ["What, what, had he said to her?", "무슨, 무슨 말을 그녀에게 했단 말인가?"], ["His heart burned with violent anger.", "그의 가슴은 격렬한 분노로 불타올랐다."], ["He could not understand how he could, half an hour before, have let those words pass and not have cried out at the moment.", "그는 자신이 어떻게 반 시간 전에 그 말을 그냥 흘려보내고 그 순간 소리쳐 따지지 않았는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["He let go of the bell and rushed off to Smerdyakov.", "그는 초인종에서 손을 떼고 스메르댜코프에게로 달려갔다."], ["“I shall kill him, perhaps, this time,”", "\"이번에는 어쩌면 그를 죽이게 될지도 모른다.\""], ["he thought on the way.", "그는 가는 길에 이렇게 생각했다."]], [["# Chapter VIII. The Third And Last Interview With Smerdyakov", "# Chapter VIII. The Third And Last Interview With Smerdyakov"]], [["When he was half-way there, the keen dry wind that had been blowing early that morning rose again, and a fine dry snow began falling thickly.", "그가 그곳으로 절반쯤 갔을 무렵, 그날 이른 아침부터 불어 대던 매섭고 건조한 바람이 다시 일더니 가늘고 메마른 눈발이 빽빽하게 쏟아지기 시작했다."], ["It did not lie on the ground, but was whirled about by the wind, and soon there was a regular snowstorm.", "눈은 땅에 쌓이지 않고 바람에 휘날려 이내 본격적인 눈보라로 변했다."], ["There were scarcely any lamp-posts in the part of the town where Smerdyakov lived.", "스메르댜코프가 사는 동네에는 가로등이라고는 거의 없었다."], ["Ivan strode alone in the darkness, unconscious of the storm, instinctively picking out his way.", "이반은 어둠 속을 홀로 성큼성큼 걸으며, 폭풍 따위는 의식하지 못한 채 본능적으로 길을 가려냈다."], ["His head ached and there was a painful throbbing in his temples.", "머리가 욱신거렸고 관자놀이가 고통스럽게 지끈거렸다."], ["He felt that his hands were twitching convulsively.", "두 손이 발작적으로 떨리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["Not far from Marya Kondratyevna’s cottage, Ivan suddenly came upon a solitary drunken little peasant.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나의 오두막에서 멀지 않은 곳에서, 이반은 문득 술에 취한 외톨이 작은 농부와 마주쳤다."], ["He was wearing a coarse and patched coat, and was walking in zigzags, grumbling and swearing to himself.", "그는 거칠고 누덕누덕 기운 외투를 걸친 채 비틀비틀 갈지자걸음을 하며 혼자 투덜대고 욕설을 내뱉고 있었다."], ["Then suddenly he would begin singing in a husky drunken voice:", "그러다 별안간 술에 잠긴 쉰 목소리로 노래를 부르기 시작했다."]], [["“Ach, Vanka’s gone to Petersburg; I won’t wait till he comes back.”", "\"아, 반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났네,\n돌아올 때까지 난 기다리지 않으리.\""]], [["But he broke off every time at the second line and began swearing again; then he would begin the same song again.", "그러나 그는 매번 둘째 줄에 이르러 노래를 끊고는 다시 욕설을 늘어놓았고, 그러고 나면 또 같은 노래를 처음부터 부르기 시작했다."], ["Ivan felt an intense hatred for him before he had thought about him at all.", "이반은 그 사내에 관해 미처 무엇을 생각하기도 전부터 그를 향해 격렬한 증오를 느꼈다."], ["Suddenly he realized his presence and felt an irresistible impulse to knock him down.", "별안간 그는 그 사내의 존재를 의식했고, 그를 쓰러뜨리고 싶다는 거역할 수 없는 충동을 느꼈다."], ["At that moment they met, and the peasant with a violent lurch fell full tilt against Ivan, who pushed him back furiously.", "바로 그 순간 두 사람이 마주쳤고, 농부는 거세게 휘청거리며 이반에게 정면으로 부딪쳐 왔는데, 이반은 그를 사납게 밀쳐 냈다."], ["The peasant went flying backwards and fell like a log on the frozen ground.", "농부는 뒤로 나가떨어지며 통나무처럼 얼어붙은 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그는 애처롭게 \"오—오!\""], ["He uttered one plaintive “O—oh!” and then was silent.", "하고 한마디 신음을 내뱉더니 이내 잠잠해졌다."], ["Ivan stepped up to him.", "이반은 그에게 다가갔다."], ["He was lying on his back, without movement or consciousness.", "사내는 움직임도 의식도 없이 등을 대고 누워 있었다."], ["“He will be frozen,”", "\"이러다 얼어 죽겠군.\""], ["thought Ivan, and he went on his way to Smerdyakov’s.", "이반은 그렇게 생각하며 스메르댜코프의 집으로 가던 길을 계속 갔다."]], [["In the passage, Marya Kondratyevna, who ran out to open the door with a candle in her hand, whispered that Smerdyakov was very ill, “It’s not that he’s laid up, but he seems not himself, and he even told us to take the tea away; he wouldn’t have any.”", "복도에서 촛불을 손에 들고 문을 열려고 뛰어나온 마리야 콘드라티예브나는, 스메르댜코프가 몹시 아프다고 속삭였다. \"자리에 누운 건 아닌데요, 어쩐지 사람이 제정신이 아닌 것 같아요, 차도 가져가라고 했답니다, 한 모금도 안 들겠다고요.\""]], [["“Why, does he make a row?”", "\"왜, 소란이라도 피우던가?\""], ["asked Ivan coarsely.", "이반이 거칠게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, dear, no, quite the contrary, he’s very quiet.", "\"어머, 아니에요, 오히려 정반대예요, 아주 조용하답니다."], ["Only please don’t talk to him too long,”", "그저 너무 오래 말을 붙이진 마세요.\""], ["Marya Kondratyevna begged him.", "마리야 콘드라티예브나가 그에게 부탁했다."], ["Ivan opened the door and stepped into the room.", "이반은 문을 열고 방으로 들어섰다."]], [["It was over-heated as before, but there were changes in the room.", "방 안은 전처럼 지나치게 더웠으나, 방에는 달라진 데가 있었다."], ["One of the benches at the side had been removed, and in its place had been put a large old mahogany leather sofa, on which a bed had been made up, with fairly clean white pillows.", "옆에 놓였던 긴 의자 하나가 치워지고, 그 자리에 마호가니 가죽을 씌운 크고 낡은 소파가 놓였는데, 그 위에는 제법 깨끗한 흰 베개를 갖춘 잠자리가 마련되어 있었다."], ["Smerdyakov was sitting on the sofa, wearing the same dressing-gown.", "스메르댜코프는 전과 같은 실내복 차림으로 그 소파에 앉아 있었다."], ["The table had been brought out in front of the sofa, so that there was hardly room to move.", "탁자가 소파 앞으로 끌어내어져 있어서 몸을 움직일 자리조차 거의 없었다."], ["On the table lay a thick book in yellow cover, but Smerdyakov was not reading it.", "탁자 위에는 노란 표지의 두툼한 책이 놓여 있었으나, 스메르댜코프는 그 책을 읽고 있지 않았다."], ["He seemed to be sitting doing nothing.", "그는 아무것도 하지 않고 그저 앉아 있는 듯했다."], ["He met Ivan with a slow silent gaze, and was apparently not at all surprised at his coming.", "그는 느릿하고 말없는 시선으로 이반을 맞이했는데, 이반이 찾아온 것에 조금도 놀라지 않는 기색이 역력했다."], ["There was a great change in his face; he was much thinner and sallower.", "그의 얼굴에는 큰 변화가 있었으니, 훨씬 야위고 누렇게 떠 있었다."], ["His eyes were sunken and there were blue marks under them.", "두 눈은 움푹 꺼지고 그 밑에는 푸르스름한 자국이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Why, you really are ill?”", "\"아니, 자네 정말로 아픈 게로군?\""], ["Ivan stopped short.", "이반이 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["“I won’t keep you long, I won’t even take off my coat.", "\"오래 붙들진 않겠네, 외투도 벗지 않겠어."], ["Where can one sit down?”", "어디 좀 앉을 데가 없나?\""]], [["He went to the other end of the table, moved up a chair and sat down on it.", "그는 탁자 반대편 끝으로 가서 의자 하나를 끌어다 놓고 거기에 앉았다."]], [["“Why do you look at me without speaking?", "\"왜 말없이 나만 쳐다보고 있나?"], ["I’ve only come with one question, and I swear I won’t go without an answer.", "난 그저 한 가지 물어볼 게 있어서 왔을 뿐이고, 맹세컨대 대답을 듣기 전엔 가지 않겠네."], ["Has the young lady, Katerina Ivanovna, been with you?”", "그 아가씨, 카체리나 이바노브나가 자네를 찾아왔었나?\""]], [["Smerdyakov still remained silent, looking quietly at Ivan as before.", "스메르댜코프는 여전히 잠자코 전처럼 조용히 이반을 바라보고 있었다."], ["Suddenly, with a motion of his hand, he turned his face away.", "별안간 그는 손을 한 번 움직이더니 얼굴을 돌려 버렸다."]], [["“What’s the matter with you?”", "\"자네 대체 왜 그러나?\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도 아닙니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘nothing’?”", "\"'아무것도 아니'라니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“Yes, she has.", "\"네, 다녀갔습니다."], ["It’s no matter to you.", "나리하곤 상관없는 일이지요."], ["Let me alone.”", "그냥 좀 내버려 두십시오.\""]], [["“No, I won’t let you alone.", "\"아니, 그냥 내버려 두지 않겠네."], ["Tell me, when was she here?”", "말해 봐, 그 여자가 언제 여기 왔었나?\""]], [["“Why, I’d quite forgotten about her,”", "\"원, 그 여자에 관한 일은 까맣게 잊고 있었습니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with a scornful smile, and turning his face to Ivan again, he stared at him with a look of frenzied hatred, the same look that he had fixed on him at their last interview, a month before.", "스메르댜코프는 비웃는 듯한 미소를 지으며 그렇게 말하더니, 다시 이반에게로 얼굴을 돌려 광적인 증오의 눈빛으로 그를 응시했는데, 그것은 한 달 전 마지막으로 만났을 때 그에게 박아 넣었던 바로 그 눈빛이었다."]], [["“You seem very ill yourself, your face is sunken; you don’t look like yourself,”", "\"나리야말로 몹시 편찮아 보이는데요, 얼굴이 핼쑥하군요, 도무지 평소의 나리 같지가 않습니다.\""], ["he said to Ivan.", "그가 이반에게 말했다."]], [["“Never mind my health, tell me what I ask you.”", "\"내 건강 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없고, 내가 묻는 거나 대답해.\""]], [["“But why are your eyes so yellow?", "\"한데 어째서 눈이 그렇게 누렇습니까?"], ["The whites are quite yellow.", "흰자위가 온통 노랗군요."], ["Are you so worried?”", "그렇게 근심이 많으신가요?\""], ["He smiled contemptuously and suddenly laughed outright.", "그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓더니 별안간 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Listen; I’ve told you I won’t go away without an answer!”", "\"잘 들어, 대답을 듣기 전엔 가지 않겠다고 말했잖나!\""], ["Ivan cried, intensely irritated.", "이반이 극도로 짜증이 나서 소리쳤다."]], [["“Why do you keep pestering me?", "\"왜 자꾸 절 들볶으십니까?"], ["Why do you torment me?”", "어째서 절 괴롭히시는 거예요?\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with a look of suffering.", "스메르댜코프가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Damn it!", "\"제기랄!"], ["I’ve nothing to do with you.", "난 자네하곤 아무 상관도 없네."], ["Just answer my question and I’ll go away.”", "그저 내 질문에 대답만 하면 곧 가겠어.\""]], [["“I’ve no answer to give you,”", "\"드릴 대답이 없습니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, looking down again.", "스메르댜코프는 다시 눈을 내리깔며 말했다."]], [["“You may be sure I’ll make you answer!”", "\"기어이 대답하게 만들고 말 테니 두고 봐!\""]], [["“Why are you so uneasy?”", "\"왜 그렇게 안절부절못하십니까?\""], ["Smerdyakov stared at him, not simply with contempt, but almost with repulsion.", "스메르댜코프는 단순한 경멸이 아니라 거의 혐오에 가까운 눈으로 그를 응시했다."], ["“Is this because the trial begins to-morrow?", "\"내일 재판이 시작되기 때문입니까?"], ["Nothing will happen to you; can’t you believe that at last?", "나리한테는 아무 일도 없을 겁니다, 그것조차 끝내 못 믿으시겠어요?"], ["Go home, go to bed and sleep in peace, don’t be afraid of anything.”", "집에 가서 자리에 누워 편히 주무세요, 아무것도 두려워하실 것 없습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you....", "\"무슨 소린지 모르겠군……"], ["What have I to be afraid of to-morrow?”", "내가 내일 두려워할 게 뭐가 있단 말인가?\""], ["Ivan articulated in astonishment, and suddenly a chill breath of fear did in fact pass over his soul.", "이반은 어리둥절해서 그렇게 내뱉었는데, 그 순간 별안간 차디찬 공포의 기운이 정말로 그의 영혼을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["Smerdyakov measured him with his eyes.", "스메르댜코프는 눈으로 그를 찬찬히 훑어보았다."]], [["“You don’t understand?”", "\"모르신다고요?\""], ["he drawled reproachfully.", "그가 책망하듯 느릿느릿 말을 끌었다."], ["“It’s a strange thing a sensible man should care to play such a farce!”", "\"분별 있는 분이 이런 익살극을 즐기려 드시다니, 참 별일이군요!\""]], [["Ivan looked at him speechless.", "이반은 말없이 그를 바라보았다."], ["The startling, incredibly supercilious tone of this man who had once been his valet, was extraordinary in itself.", "한때 자기 하인이었던 이 사내가 던지는, 놀라우리만치 믿기 어려울 만큼 거만한 그 말투는 그 자체로 예사롭지 않았다."], ["He had not taken such a tone even at their last interview.", "지난번 마지막으로 만났을 때조차 그는 이런 말투를 쓴 적이 없었다."]], [["“I tell you, you’ve nothing to be afraid of.", "\"말씀드리지만, 나리는 아무것도 두려워하실 게 없습니다."], ["I won’t say anything about you; there’s no proof against you.", "전 나리에 관해선 한마디도 하지 않을 거고, 나리를 옭아맬 증거 따위도 없으니까요."], ["I say, how your hands are trembling!", "한데 어쩌면 그렇게 손을 떠시는지!"], ["Why are your fingers moving like that?", "손가락은 또 왜 그렇게 떠시는 겁니까?"], ["Go home, you did not murder him.”", "집에 가세요, 그분을 죽인 건 나리가 아닙니다.\""]], [["Ivan started.", "이반은 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["He remembered Alyosha.", "그는 알료샤를 떠올렸다."]], [["“I know it was not I,”", "\"내가 아니란 건 나도 알아.\""], ["he faltered.", "그가 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“Do you?”", "\"아신다고요?\""], ["Smerdyakov caught him up again.", "스메르댜코프가 다시 그 말꼬리를 낚아챘다."]], [["Ivan jumped up and seized him by the shoulder.", "이반은 벌떡 일어나 그의 어깨를 움켜잡았다."]], [["“Tell me everything, you viper!", "\"전부 말해, 이 독사 같은 놈아!"], ["Tell me everything!”", "죄다 말하라고!\""]], [["Smerdyakov was not in the least scared.", "스메르댜코프는 조금도 겁먹지 않았다."], ["He only riveted his eyes on Ivan with insane hatred.", "그저 광적인 증오의 눈길을 이반에게 못 박을 뿐이었다."]], [["“Well, it was you who murdered him, if that’s it,”", "\"그러니까, 정 그러시다면, 그분을 죽인 건 바로 나리입니다.\""], ["he whispered furiously.", "그가 사납게 속삭였다."]], [["Ivan sank back on his chair, as though pondering something.", "이반은 무언가를 곰곰이 헤아리듯 의자에 도로 주저앉았다."], ["He laughed malignantly.", "그는 악의에 차서 웃었다."]], [["“You mean my going away.", "\"내가 떠난 일을 말하는 게로군."], ["What you talked about last time?”", "지난번에 자네가 떠벌렸던 그 얘기 말이야?\""]], [["“You stood before me last time and understood it all, and you understand it now.”", "\"나리는 지난번에 제 앞에 서서 죄다 알아들으셨고, 지금도 알아들으시는 겁니다.\""]], [["“All I understand is that you are mad.”", "\"내가 알아듣는 건 자네가 미쳤다는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["“Aren’t you tired of it?", "\"이젠 진력도 안 나십니까?"], ["Here we are face to face; what’s the use of going on keeping up a farce to each other?", "우리 둘이 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 있는데, 무엇 하러 계속 서로 익살극을 부린답니까?"], ["Are you still trying to throw it all on me, to my face?", "아직도 그걸 죄다 제게, 제 면전에 떠넘기려고 애쓰시는 겁니까?"], ["You murdered him; you are the real murderer, I was only your instrument, your faithful servant, and it was following your words I did it.”", "그분을 죽인 건 나리예요, 진짜 살인자는 나리고, 저는 그저 나리의 도구이자 충실한 하인이었을 뿐, 나리 말씀을 따라 그 일을 해치운 것뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Did it?", "\"해치워?"], ["Why, did you murder him?”", "아니, 자네가 그분을 죽였단 말인가?\""], ["Ivan turned cold.", "이반은 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었다."]], [["Something seemed to give way in his brain, and he shuddered all over with a cold shiver.", "뇌리에서 무언가가 툭 끊어지는 듯하더니, 그는 차디찬 전율로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다."], ["Then Smerdyakov himself looked at him wonderingly; probably the genuineness of Ivan’s horror struck him.", "그러자 이번에는 스메르댜코프 쪽이 의아한 듯 그를 바라보았으니, 아마도 이반의 경악이 진짜라는 사실이 그를 놀라게 한 모양이었다."]], [["“You don’t mean to say you really did not know?”", "\"설마 정말로 모르고 계셨다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\""], ["he faltered mistrustfully, looking with a forced smile into his eyes.", "그가 미심쩍은 투로 더듬거리며, 억지웃음을 머금은 채 이반의 눈을 들여다보았다."], ["Ivan still gazed at him, and seemed unable to speak.", "이반은 여전히 그를 응시할 뿐, 말을 잇지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["Ach, Vanka’s gone to Petersburg; I won’t wait till he comes back,", "아, 반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났네,\n돌아올 때까지 난 기다리지 않으리."]], [["suddenly echoed in his head.", "별안간 이 노랫말이 그의 머릿속에서 메아리쳤다."]], [["“Do you know, I am afraid that you are a dream, a phantom sitting before me,”", "\"이보게, 난 자네가 내 앞에 앉은 한낱 꿈이고 허깨비일까 봐 두렵네.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“There’s no phantom here, but only us two and one other.", "\"여기 허깨비 따위는 없습니다, 다만 우리 둘과 또 하나가 있을 뿐이지요."], ["No doubt he is here, that third, between us.”", "틀림없이 그분도 여기 계실 겁니다, 그 셋째 분 말입니다, 우리 사이에요.\""]], [["“Who is he?", "\"그게 누군가?"], ["Who is here?", "누가 여기 있다는 거야?"], ["What third person?”", "무슨 셋째란 말이냐?\""], ["Ivan cried in alarm, looking about him, his eyes hastily searching in every corner.", "이반은 놀라 소리치며 사방을 둘러보았고, 그의 눈은 다급하게 구석구석을 뒤졌다."]], [["“That third is God Himself—Providence.", "\"그 셋째 분이 바로 하느님이십니다—섭리지요."], ["He is the third beside us now.", "그분이 지금 우리 곁에 셋째로 계시는 겁니다."], ["Only don’t look for Him, you won’t find Him.”", "다만 그분을 찾으려 들진 마세요, 찾아내지 못하실 테니까요.\""]], [["“It’s a lie that you killed him!”", "\"자네가 그분을 죽였다는 건 거짓말이야!\""], ["Ivan cried madly.", "이반이 미친 듯이 소리쳤다."], ["“You are mad, or teasing me again!”", "\"자넨 미쳤거나, 아니면 또 날 놀려 먹는 거지!\""]], [["Smerdyakov, as before, watched him curiously, with no sign of fear.", "스메르댜코프는 전처럼 두려운 기색도 없이 호기심에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["He could still scarcely get over his incredulity; he still fancied that Ivan knew everything and was trying to “throw it all on him to his face.”", "그는 좀처럼 의혹을 떨치지 못한 채, 여전히 이반이 죄다 알면서도 \"그걸 죄다 제 면전에 떠넘기려\" 애쓰는 것이라고 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["“Wait a minute,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["he said at last in a weak voice, and suddenly bringing up his left leg from under the table, he began turning up his trouser leg.", "그가 마침내 힘없는 목소리로 말하더니, 별안간 탁자 밑에서 왼쪽 다리를 끌어 올려 바짓단을 걷어 올리기 시작했다."], ["He was wearing long white stockings and slippers.", "그는 흰 긴 양말에 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다."], ["Slowly he took off his garter and fumbled to the bottom of his stocking.", "그는 천천히 양말 대님을 풀더니 양말 속 깊숙이 손을 더듬어 넣었다."], ["Ivan gazed at him, and suddenly shuddered in a paroxysm of terror.", "이반은 그를 바라보다가 별안간 발작적인 공포로 몸을 부르르 떨었다."]], [["“He’s mad!”", "\"미친놈이야!\""], ["he cried, and rapidly jumping up, he drew back, so that he knocked his back against the wall and stood up against it, stiff and straight.", "그가 소리쳤고, 잽싸게 벌떡 일어나 뒷걸음질을 치다가 등을 벽에 쿵 부딪고는, 뻣뻣하고 꼿꼿하게 그 벽에 기대섰다."], ["He looked with insane terror at Smerdyakov, who, entirely unaffected by his terror, continued fumbling in his stocking, as though he were making an effort to get hold of something with his fingers and pull it out.", "그는 광적인 공포의 눈으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보았는데, 스메르댜코프는 그의 공포에는 아랑곳없이 계속 양말 속을 더듬으며, 마치 손가락으로 무언가를 붙잡아 끄집어내려고 애쓰는 듯했다."], ["At last he got hold of it and began pulling it out.", "마침내 그는 그것을 붙잡아 끌어내기 시작했다."], ["Ivan saw that it was a piece of paper, or perhaps a roll of papers.", "이반의 눈에 그것은 종잇조각, 어쩌면 종이를 말아 놓은 뭉치 같은 것으로 보였다."], ["Smerdyakov pulled it out and laid it on the table.", "스메르댜코프는 그것을 끄집어내어 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"여기 있습니다.\""], ["he said quietly.", "그가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""], ["asked Ivan, trembling.", "이반이 떨면서 물었다."]], [["“Kindly look at it,”", "\"좀 보십시오.\""], ["Smerdyakov answered, still in the same low tone.", "스메르댜코프는 여전히 낮은 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["Ivan stepped up to the table, took up the roll of paper and began unfolding it, but suddenly he drew back his fingers, as though from contact with a loathsome reptile.", "이반은 탁자로 다가가 그 종이 뭉치를 집어 들고 펴기 시작했으나, 별안간 무슨 징그러운 파충류라도 만진 듯이 손가락을 움츠렸다."]], [["“Your hands keep twitching,”", "\"손이 자꾸 떨리시는군요.\""], ["observed Smerdyakov, and he deliberately unfolded the bundle himself.", "스메르댜코프는 그렇게 말하고는, 손수 천천히 그 뭉치를 펼쳤다."], ["Under the wrapper were three packets of hundred-rouble notes.", "포장 안에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 세 다발이 들어 있었다."]], [["“They are all here, all the three thousand roubles; you need not count them.", "\"전부 여기 있습니다, 삼천 루블 고스란히요, 세어 보실 것도 없습니다."], ["Take them,”", "가져가세요.\""], ["Smerdyakov suggested to Ivan, nodding at the notes.", "스메르댜코프가 지폐를 향해 고갯짓을 하며 이반에게 권했다."], ["Ivan sank back in his chair.", "이반은 의자에 도로 털썩 주저앉았다."], ["He was as white as a handkerchief.", "그는 손수건처럼 새하얗게 질려 있었다."]], [["“You frightened me ...", "\"날 놀라게 했군……"], ["with your stocking,”", "그 양말로 말이야.\""], ["he said, with a strange grin.", "그가 묘한 쓴웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Can you really not have known till now?”", "\"정말로 지금까지 모르고 계셨다는 말씀인가요?\""], ["Smerdyakov asked once more.", "스메르댜코프가 다시 한번 물었다."]], [["“No, I did not know.", "\"아니, 난 몰랐어."], ["I kept thinking of Dmitri.", "줄곧 드미트리만 생각하고 있었네."], ["Brother, brother!", "형님, 형님!"], ["Ach!”", "아!\""], ["He suddenly clutched his head in both hands.", "그는 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Listen.", "\"이봐."], ["Did you kill him alone?", "자네가 혼자서 죽인 건가?"], ["With my brother’s help or without?”", "우리 형의 도움을 받았나, 아니면 혼자였나?\""]], [["“It was only with you, with your help, I killed him, and Dmitri Fyodorovitch is quite innocent.”", "\"오직 나리와, 나리의 도움으로 제가 그분을 죽인 겁니다, 드미트리 표도로비치는 아무 죄도 없습니다.\""]], [["“All right, all right.", "\"좋아, 좋아."], ["Talk about me later.", "내 얘긴 나중에 하지."], ["Why do I keep on trembling?", "난 왜 자꾸 이렇게 떨리는 거지?"], ["I can’t speak properly.”", "말도 제대로 못 하겠군.\""]], [["“You were bold enough then.", "\"그때만 해도 그렇게 대담하셨는데요."], ["You said ‘everything was lawful,’ and how frightened you are now,”", "'모든 것이 허용된다'고 말씀하시더니, 지금은 어쩌면 그렇게 겁을 내십니까.\""], ["Smerdyakov muttered in surprise.", "스메르댜코프가 놀란 듯 중얼거렸다."], ["“Won’t you have some lemonade?", "\"레모네이드라도 좀 드시겠습니까?"], ["I’ll ask for some at once.", "당장 가져오라고 하지요."], ["It’s very refreshing.", "아주 시원하답니다."], ["Only I must hide this first.”", "다만 이걸 먼저 감춰 둬야겠습니다.\""]], [["And again he motioned at the notes.", "그러고서 그는 다시 지폐를 향해 손짓했다."], ["He was just going to get up and call at the door to Marya Kondratyevna to make some lemonade and bring it them, but, looking for something to cover up the notes that she might not see them, he first took out his handkerchief, and as it turned out to be very dirty, took up the big yellow book that Ivan had noticed at first lying on the table, and put it over the notes.", "그는 막 일어나 문간으로 가서 마리야 콘드라티예브나에게 레모네이드를 만들어 가져오라고 부르려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 보지 못하도록 지폐를 덮을 만한 것을 찾다가, 먼저 손수건을 꺼냈으나 그것이 몹시 더러웠던 터라, 이반이 처음에 탁자 위에 놓인 것을 보았던 그 노란 큰 책을 집어 들어 지폐 위에 덮었다."], ["The book was The Sayings of the Holy Father Isaac the Syrian.", "그 책은 「시리아 사람 성 이사악 성인의 어록」이었다."], ["Ivan read it mechanically.", "이반은 무심코 그 제목을 읽었다."]], [["“I won’t have any lemonade,”", "\"레모네이드는 됐네.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Talk of me later.", "\"내 얘긴 나중에 하고."], ["Sit down and tell me how you did it.", "앉아서 어떻게 그 일을 저질렀는지 말해 봐."], ["Tell me all about it.”", "처음부터 끝까지 다.\""]], [["“You’d better take off your greatcoat, or you’ll be too hot.”", "\"외투를 벗으시는 게 좋겠습니다, 안 그러면 너무 더우실 텐데요.\""], ["Ivan, as though he’d only just thought of it, took off his coat, and, without getting up from his chair, threw it on the bench.", "이반은 그제야 비로소 생각이 났다는 듯 외투를 벗어, 의자에서 일어나지도 않은 채 긴 의자 위로 던져 놓았다."]], [["“Speak, please, speak.”", "\"말해, 어서, 말하라고.\""]], [["He seemed calmer.", "그는 한결 차분해진 듯했다."], ["He waited, feeling sure that Smerdyakov would tell him all about it.", "그는 스메르댜코프가 그 일의 자초지종을 죄다 들려주리라 확신하며 기다렸다."]], [["“How it was done?”", "\"어떻게 저질렀느냐고요?\""], ["sighed Smerdyakov.", "스메르댜코프가 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["“It was done in a most natural way, following your very words.”", "\"더없이 자연스러운 방식으로, 나리께서 하신 바로 그 말씀을 따라서 했지요.\""]], [["“Of my words later,”", "\"내 말 얘긴 나중에 하고.\""], ["Ivan broke in again, apparently with complete self-possession, firmly uttering his words, and not shouting as before.", "이반이 다시 말을 가로막았는데, 보아하니 완전히 평정을 되찾은 듯, 전처럼 고함치는 대신 또박또박 다부지게 말을 내뱉었다."], ["“Only tell me in detail how you did it.", "\"그저 자네가 어떻게 했는지 낱낱이 말해 봐."], ["Everything, as it happened.", "일어난 그대로 전부 다."], ["Don’t forget anything.", "무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 말고."], ["The details, above everything, the details, I beg you.”", "무엇보다도 세세한 것들을, 그 세세한 것들을, 제발 부탁이야.\""]], [["“You’d gone away, then I fell into the cellar.”", "\"나리께서 떠나시고 나서, 전 지하실에 굴러떨어졌습니다.\""]], [["“In a fit or in a sham one?”", "\"발작 때문이었나, 아니면 꾸민 거였나?\""]], [["“A sham one, naturally.", "\"꾸민 거지요, 물론."], ["I shammed it all.", "죄다 꾸며 낸 겁니다."], ["I went quietly down the steps to the very bottom and lay down quietly, and as I lay down I gave a scream, and struggled, till they carried me out.”", "전 조용히 계단을 따라 맨 밑바닥까지 내려가서 조용히 누웠고, 그렇게 누운 채로 비명을 지르며 발버둥을 쳤는데, 결국 사람들이 절 들어내 갈 때까지 그랬지요.\""]], [["“Stay!", "\"잠깐!"], ["And were you shamming all along, afterwards, and in the hospital?”", "그러면 그 뒤로도, 병원에서도 줄곧 꾸민 거였나?\""]], [["“No, not at all.", "\"아닙니다, 천만에요."], ["Next day, in the morning, before they took me to the hospital, I had a real attack and a more violent one than I’ve had for years.", "이튿날 아침, 사람들이 절 병원으로 데려가기 전에, 진짜로 발작이 일어났는데 몇 해 동안 겪어 본 것 중 가장 심한 발작이었습니다."], ["For two days I was quite unconscious.”", "이틀 동안이나 완전히 의식이 없었지요.\""]], [["“All right, all right.", "\"좋아, 좋아."], ["Go on.”", "계속해.\""]], [["“They laid me on the bed.", "\"사람들이 절 침대에 눕혔습니다."], ["I knew I’d be the other side of the partition, for whenever I was ill, Marfa Ignatyevna used to put me there, near them.", "전 칸막이 저편에 눕게 되리란 걸 알고 있었지요, 제가 아플 때면 마르파 이그나티예브나가 늘 거기, 그분들 가까이에 절 뉘었으니까요."], ["She’s always been very kind to me, from my birth up.", "그 여자는 제가 태어났을 때부터 줄곧 제게 무척 잘해 줬습니다."], ["At night I moaned, but quietly.", "밤이 되자 전 신음을 했지만, 나직하게 했지요."], ["I kept expecting Dmitri Fyodorovitch to come.”", "줄곧 드미트리 표도로비치가 오기를 기다리면서요.\""]], [["“Expecting him?", "\"기다렸다고?"], ["To come to you?”", "자네한테 오기를?\""]], [["“Not to me.", "\"제게가 아닙니다."], ["I expected him to come into the house, for I’d no doubt that he’d come that night, for being without me and getting no news, he’d be sure to come and climb over the fence, as he used to, and do something.”", "그분이 집 안으로 들어오시기를 기다린 거지요, 그날 밤 반드시 오시리라 의심치 않았으니까요, 왜냐하면 제가 없어서 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터라, 그분은 틀림없이 와서 늘 그러시던 대로 담을 넘어 들어와 무슨 일이든 저지르실 게 분명했거든요.\""]], [["“And if he hadn’t come?”", "\"그가 오지 않았다면?\""]], [["“Then nothing would have happened.", "\"그러면 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요."], ["I should never have brought myself to it without him.”", "그분 없이는 저 혼자선 도저히 결행하지 못했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“All right, all right ...", "\"좋아, 좋아……"], ["speak more intelligibly, don’t hurry; above all, don’t leave anything out!”", "좀 더 알아듣게 말해, 서두르지 말고, 무엇보다 하나도 빠뜨리지 마!\""]], [["“I expected him to kill Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "\"전 그분이 표도르 파블로비치를 죽이리라 기대했습니다."], ["I thought that was certain, for I had prepared him for it ...", "그건 확실하다고 생각했지요, 제가 그분을 그렇게 몰아세워 뒀으니까요……"], ["during the last few days....", "지난 며칠 동안에요……"], ["He knew about the knocks, that was the chief thing.", "그분은 그 신호 소리를 알고 계셨는데, 그게 무엇보다 중요했습니다."], ["With his suspiciousness and the fury which had been growing in him all those days, he was bound to get into the house by means of those taps.", "그 의심 많은 성미에다 그날들 내내 그분 속에서 부풀어 오른 분노까지 더해졌으니, 그분은 틀림없이 그 신호 소리를 빌미로 집 안에 들어오실 수밖에 없었지요."], ["That was inevitable, so I was expecting him.”", "그건 피할 수 없는 일이었고, 그래서 전 그분을 기다린 겁니다.\""]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["Ivan interrupted; “if he had killed him, he would have taken the money and carried it away; you must have considered that.", "이반이 말을 끊었다. \"만약 그가 그분을 죽였다면, 돈을 챙겨서 가져가 버렸을 텐데, 자네도 그건 생각해 봤을 거 아닌가."], ["What would you have got by it afterwards?", "그러면 그 뒤에 자넨 뭘 얻겠다는 거였지?"], ["I don’t see.”", "도무지 모르겠군.\""]], [["“But he would never have found the money.", "\"하지만 그분은 그 돈을 결코 찾아내지 못했을 겁니다."], ["That was only what I told him, that the money was under the mattress.", "돈이 매트리스 밑에 있다고 한 건 제가 그분께 그렇게 말한 것뿐이지요."], ["But that wasn’t true.", "한데 그건 사실이 아니었습니다."], ["It had been lying in a box.", "돈은 상자 안에 들어 있었거든요."], ["And afterwards I suggested to Fyodor Pavlovitch, as I was the only person he trusted, to hide the envelope with the notes in the corner behind the ikons, for no one would have guessed that place, especially if they came in a hurry.", "그러고 나서 전 표도르 파블로비치께, 그분이 믿는 사람이라곤 저뿐이었으니, 지폐가 든 봉투를 성상들 뒤 구석에 숨겨 두시라고 일러 드렸지요, 아무도 그 자리는 짐작하지 못할 테니까요, 더구나 다급하게 들이닥친다면 말입니다."], ["So that’s where the envelope lay, in the corner behind the ikons.", "그래서 봉투는 거기, 성상들 뒤 구석에 놓여 있었던 겁니다."], ["It would have been absurd to keep it under the mattress; the box, anyway, could be locked.", "매트리스 밑에 둔다는 건 어처구니없는 일이었지요, 어쨌거나 상자는 자물쇠를 채울 수 있으니까요."], ["But all believe it was under the mattress.", "그래도 다들 돈이 매트리스 밑에 있다고 믿었지요."], ["A stupid thing to believe.", "어리석은 믿음이었습니다."], ["So if Dmitri Fyodorovitch had committed the murder, finding nothing, he would either have run away in a hurry, afraid of every sound, as always happens with murderers, or he would have been arrested.", "그러니 드미트리 표도로비치가 살인을 저질렀다 해도, 아무것도 찾지 못한 채 살인자들이 으레 그러듯 작은 소리에도 겁을 먹고 황급히 달아나거나, 아니면 붙잡혔을 겁니다."], ["So I could always have clambered up to the ikons and have taken away the money next morning or even that night, and it would have all been put down to Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "그러면 전 언제든 성상들 위로 기어 올라가 이튿날 아침에, 아니 바로 그날 밤에라도 돈을 빼낼 수 있었을 테고, 죄는 죄다 드미트리 표도로비치에게 뒤집어씌워졌을 겁니다."], ["I could reckon upon that.”", "전 그걸 믿고 계산한 거지요.\""]], [["“But what if he did not kill him, but only knocked him down?”", "\"하지만 만약 그가 그분을 죽이지 않고 그저 때려눕히기만 했다면?\""]], [["“If he did not kill him, of course, I would not have ventured to take the money, and nothing would have happened.", "\"그분이 죽이지 않았다면, 물론 전 감히 돈에 손을 대지 못했을 테고, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요."], ["But I calculated that he would beat him senseless, and I should have time to take it then, and then I’d make out to Fyodor Pavlovitch that it was no one but Dmitri Fyodorovitch who had taken the money after beating him.”", "하지만 전 그분이 그분을 정신을 잃을 만큼 때려 줄 거라고 계산했고, 그러면 그 틈에 돈을 빼낼 시간이 있을 테고, 그런 다음 표도르 파블로비치께는 드미트리 표도로비치가 그분을 때린 뒤 돈을 가져간 거라고, 그 사람 말고는 아무도 아니라고 둘러댈 작정이었지요.\""]], [["“Stop ...", "\"잠깐……"], ["I am getting mixed.", "점점 헷갈리는군."], ["Then it was Dmitri after all who killed him; you only took the money?”", "그러니까 결국 그분을 죽인 건 드미트리고, 자넨 돈만 가져간 거란 말인가?\""]], [["“No, he didn’t kill him.", "\"아니요, 그분이 죽인 게 아닙니다."], ["Well, I might as well have told you now that he was the murderer....", "뭐, 이제 와서 그분이 살인자라고 나리께 말씀드릴 수도 있었겠지요……"], ["But I don’t want to lie to you now, because ...", "하지만 전 지금 나리께 거짓말을 하고 싶지 않습니다, 왜냐하면……"], ["because if you really haven’t understood till now, as I see for myself, and are not pretending, so as to throw your guilt on me to my very face, you are still responsible for it all, since you knew of the murder and charged me to do it, and went away knowing all about it.", "왜냐하면 만약 나리께서 정말로 지금까지 모르고 계셨다면, 제 눈으로 직접 보아하니 그런 것 같고, 또 죄를 제 면전에 떠넘기려 시치미를 떼시는 게 아니라면, 그래도 나리는 이 모든 일에 책임이 있으십니다, 살인을 알면서도 제게 그 일을 맡기고 죄다 아시면서 떠나 버리셨으니까요."], ["And so I want to prove to your face this evening that you are the only real murderer in the whole affair, and I am not the real murderer, though I did kill him.", "그래서 전 오늘 저녁 나리 면전에 증명해 보이고 싶은 겁니다, 이 모든 사건에서 진짜 살인자는 나리 한 분뿐이고, 비록 제가 그분을 죽이긴 했어도 진짜 살인자는 제가 아니란 것을요."], ["You are the rightful murderer.”", "나리야말로 마땅한 살인자이십니다.\""]], [["“Why, why, am I a murderer? Oh, God!”", "\"어째서, 어째서 내가 살인자란 말이냐?"], ["Ivan cried, unable to restrain himself at last, and forgetting that he had put off discussing himself till the end of the conversation.", "오, 하느님!\" 이반은 마침내 자제력을 잃고 소리쳤는데, 자기 얘기는 대화의 맨 끝으로 미뤄 두기로 한 것조차 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["“You still mean that Tchermashnya?", "\"자넨 아직도 그 체르마시냐 얘길 하는 건가?"], ["Stay, tell me, why did you want my consent, if you really took Tchermashnya for consent?", "잠깐, 말해 봐, 자네가 정말로 체르마시냐를 동의의 표시로 받아들였다면, 어째서 굳이 내 동의가 필요했지?"], ["How will you explain that now?”", "그걸 이제 어떻게 설명할 텐가?\""]], [["“Assured of your consent, I should have known that you wouldn’t have made an outcry over those three thousand being lost, even if I’d been suspected, instead of Dmitri Fyodorovitch, or as his accomplice; on the contrary, you would have protected me from others....", "\"나리의 동의를 확신하고 나면, 전 알 수 있었지요, 설령 드미트리 표도로비치 대신, 혹은 그 공범으로 제가 의심을 받는다 해도, 나리께서 그 삼천 루블이 없어진 일로 떠들썩하게 소동을 일으키진 않으시리란 걸요. 오히려 나리는 남들로부터 절 감싸 주셨을 겁니다……"], ["And when you got your inheritance you would have rewarded me when you were able, all the rest of your life.", "그리고 유산을 받으시면 형편이 되는 대로, 평생토록 제게 보답해 주셨겠지요."], ["For you’d have received your inheritance through me, seeing that if he had married Agrafena Alexandrovna, you wouldn’t have had a farthing.”", "왜냐하면 나리는 저를 통해 그 유산을 받으시는 셈이니까요, 만약 그분이 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나와 결혼하셨다면, 나리는 단 한 푼도 손에 넣지 못하셨을 테니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Then you intended to worry me all my life afterwards,”", "그러니까 자넨 그 뒤로 평생토록 날 들볶을 작정이었군.\""], ["snarled Ivan.", "이반이 으르렁거렸다."], ["“And what if I hadn’t gone away then, but had informed against you?”", "\"그럼 만약 내가 그때 떠나지 않고 자넬 밀고했더라면?\""]], [["“What could you have informed?", "\"무얼 밀고하실 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["That I persuaded you to go to Tchermashnya?", "제가 나리께 체르마시냐로 가시라고 권했다는 걸요?"], ["That’s all nonsense.", "그건 죄다 헛소리지요."], ["Besides, after our conversation you would either have gone away or have stayed.", "게다가 우리가 그 얘기를 나눈 뒤로, 나리는 떠나시거나 머무시거나 둘 중 하나였습니다."], ["If you had stayed, nothing would have happened.", "머무셨다면 아무 일도 일어나지 않았겠지요."], ["I should have known that you didn’t want it done, and should have attempted nothing.", "전 나리께서 그 일이 일어나길 원치 않으신다는 걸 알았을 테고, 아무것도 시도하지 않았을 테니까요."], ["As you went away, it meant you assured me that you wouldn’t dare to inform against me at the trial, and that you’d overlook my having the three thousand.", "한데 나리께서 떠나셨다는 건, 곧 재판에서 절 밀고할 엄두를 내지 않으시고 제가 삼천 루블을 가지는 것도 눈감아 주시겠노라 제게 확약하신 셈이지요."], ["And, indeed, you couldn’t have prosecuted me afterwards, because then I should have told it all in the court; that is, not that I had stolen the money or killed him—I shouldn’t have said that—but that you’d put me up to the theft and the murder, though I didn’t consent to it.", "그리고 사실 나리는 그 뒤에 절 고발하실 수도 없었습니다, 그랬다면 전 법정에서 죄다 까발렸을 테니까요. 그러니까 제가 돈을 훔쳤다거나 그분을 죽였다는 게 아니라—그런 얘긴 하지 않았겠지요—나리께서 절 부추겨 절도와 살인을 시키셨다고요, 비록 제가 동의하진 않았지만 말입니다."], ["That’s why I needed your consent, so that you couldn’t have cornered me afterwards, for what proof could you have had?", "그래서 전 나리의 동의가 필요했던 겁니다, 그래야 나리께서 나중에 절 궁지로 몰아넣지 못하실 테니까요, 무슨 증거가 있으셨겠습니까?"], ["I could always have cornered you, revealing your eagerness for your father’s death, and I tell you the public would have believed it all, and you would have been ashamed for the rest of your life.”", "하지만 전 언제든 나리를 궁지로 몰 수 있었지요, 나리께서 부친의 죽음을 얼마나 갈망하셨는지 폭로하면서요, 말씀드리지만 세상 사람들은 그걸 죄다 곧이들었을 테고, 나리는 평생토록 부끄러움 속에 사셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Was I then so eager, was I?”", "\"그래, 내가 그렇게나 갈망했단 말이냐, 정말로?\""], ["Ivan snarled again.", "이반이 다시 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“To be sure you were, and by your consent you silently sanctioned my doing it.”", "\"그럼요, 그러셨고말고요, 그리고 동의하심으로써 제가 그 일을 저지르는 걸 말없이 묵인하신 거지요.\""], ["Smerdyakov looked resolutely at Ivan.", "스메르댜코프는 단호하게 이반을 바라보았다."], ["He was very weak and spoke slowly and wearily, but some hidden inner force urged him on.", "그는 몹시 쇠약해서 느릿하고 힘없이 말했으나, 무언가 숨겨진 내면의 힘이 그를 떠밀고 있었다."], ["He evidently had some design.", "그에게 어떤 속셈이 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["Ivan felt that.", "이반은 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Go on,”", "\"계속해.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Tell me what happened that night.”", "\"그날 밤 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 봐.\""]], [["“What more is there to tell!", "\"더 말씀드릴 게 뭐가 있겠습니까!"], ["I lay there and I thought I heard the master shout.", "전 거기 누워 있었는데, 주인 나리가 소리치시는 게 들리는 것 같았습니다."], ["And before that Grigory Vassilyevitch had suddenly got up and came out, and he suddenly gave a scream, and then all was silence and darkness.", "그보다 앞서 그리고리 바실리예비치가 별안간 일어나 밖으로 나가더니, 갑자기 비명을 지르고, 그러고는 온통 적막과 어둠뿐이었지요."], ["I lay there waiting, my heart beating; I couldn’t bear it.", "전 거기 누운 채 심장을 두근거리며 기다렸지만, 도저히 견딜 수가 없었습니다."], ["I got up at last, went out.", "마침내 일어나 밖으로 나갔지요."], ["I saw the window open on the left into the garden, and I stepped to the left to listen whether he was sitting there alive, and I heard the master moving about, sighing, so I knew he was alive.", "왼편으로 정원 쪽 창문이 열려 있는 게 보여서, 그분이 거기 살아서 앉아 계신지 귀를 기울이려고 왼쪽으로 다가갔는데, 주인 나리가 이리저리 움직이며 한숨을 쉬시는 소리가 들리길래, 그분이 살아 계시다는 걸 알았지요. '에잇!'"], ["‘Ech!’ I thought.", "전 생각했습니다."], ["I went to the window and shouted to the master, ‘It’s I.’ And he shouted to me, ‘He’s been, he’s been; he’s run away.’ He meant Dmitri Fyodorovitch had been.", "창가로 다가가 주인 나리께 소리쳤지요, '접니다.' 그러자 그분이 제게 소리치셨습니다, '왔었다, 왔었어, 달아났다.'"], ["‘He’s killed Grigory!’ ‘Where?’ I whispered.", "드미트리 표도로비치가 다녀갔다는 뜻이었지요."], ["‘There, in the corner,’ he pointed.", "'그놈이 그리고리를 죽였어!' '어디서요?'"], ["He was whispering, too.", "전 속삭였습니다. '저기, 저 구석에서.'"], ["‘Wait a bit,’ I said.", "그분이 손가락으로 가리키셨지요."], ["I went to the corner of the garden to look, and there I came upon Grigory Vassilyevitch lying by the wall, covered with blood, senseless.", "그분도 속삭이고 계셨습니다. '잠깐만요.'"], ["So it’s true that Dmitri Fyodorovitch has been here, was the thought that came into my head, and I determined on the spot to make an end of it, as Grigory Vassilyevitch, even if he were alive, would see nothing of it, as he lay there senseless.", "전 그렇게 말하고 정원 구석으로 가서 살펴봤는데, 거기 그리고리 바실리예비치가 담장 옆에 온통 피투성이가 된 채 정신을 잃고 누워 있더군요. 그래서 정말 드미트리 표도로비치가 다녀갔구나, 하는 생각이 제 머리를 스쳤고, 전 그 자리에서 이 일을 끝장내기로 마음먹었습니다, 그리고리 바실리예비치는 설령 살아 있다 해도 정신을 잃고 누워 있으니 아무것도 보지 못할 테니까요."], ["The only risk was that Marfa Ignatyevna might wake up.", "단 하나 위험한 건 마르파 이그나티예브나가 깨어날지도 모른다는 것이었지요."], ["I felt that at the moment, but the longing to get it done came over me, till I could scarcely breathe.", "전 그 순간 그걸 느꼈지만, 그 일을 해치우고 싶은 갈망이 어찌나 솟구치던지 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없을 지경이었습니다."], ["I went back to the window to the master and said, ‘She’s here, she’s come; Agrafena Alexandrovna has come, wants to be let in.’ And he started like a baby.", "전 창가로 돌아가 주인 나리께 말씀드렸지요, '그 여자가 여기 왔습니다, 왔어요, 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나가 왔는데, 들여보내 달랍니다.' 그러자 그분은 어린아이처럼 흠칫하시더군요."], ["‘Where is she?’ he fairly gasped, but couldn’t believe it.", "'어디 있느냐?' 그분은 숨이 턱 막힌 듯 물으셨지만, 좀처럼 믿지 못하셨습니다."], ["‘She’s standing there,’ said I.", "'저기 서 있습니다.' 전 말했지요."], ["‘Open.’ He looked out of the window at me, half believing and half distrustful, but afraid to open.", "'문 여세요.' 그분은 반쯤 믿고 반쯤 못 미더워하시며 창문 너머로 절 내다보셨는데, 문을 여시기는 두려워하셨습니다."], ["‘Why, he is afraid of me now,’ I thought.", "'아니, 이제 나를 무서워하시는군.' 전 생각했지요."], ["And it was funny.", "그게 우스웠습니다."], ["I bethought me to knock on the window-frame those taps we’d agreed upon as a signal that Grushenka had come, in his presence, before his eyes.", "전 그분 면전에서, 그분 눈앞에서, 그루셴카가 왔다는 신호로 우리가 약속해 둔 그 신호 소리를 창틀에 두드려 보일 생각이 떠올랐습니다."], ["He didn’t seem to believe my word, but as soon as he heard the taps, he ran at once to open the door.", "그분은 제 말은 믿지 않으시는 듯했지만, 그 신호 소리를 듣자마자 곧장 문을 열러 달려가셨지요."], ["He opened it.", "그분은 문을 여셨습니다."], ["I would have gone in, but he stood in the way to prevent me passing.", "전 안으로 들어가려 했지만, 그분이 길을 막고 서서 제가 지나가지 못하게 하시더군요. '어디 있느냐?"], ["‘Where is she?", "어디 있어?'"], ["Where is she?’ He looked at me, all of a tremble.", "그분은 온몸을 떨면서 절 쳐다보셨습니다. '이런,'"], ["‘Well,’ thought I, ‘if he’s so frightened of me as all that, it’s a bad look out!’ And my legs went weak with fright that he wouldn’t let me in or would call out, or Marfa Ignatyevna would run up, or something else might happen.", "전 생각했지요, '이렇게나 날 무서워하시다니, 일이 고약하게 됐군!' 그분이 절 들여보내지 않거나 소리를 지르거나, 마르파 이그나티예브나가 달려오거나, 무슨 다른 일이 벌어질까 봐 겁이 나서 다리가 후들거렸습니다."], ["I don’t remember now, but I must have stood pale, facing him.", "지금은 기억나지 않지만, 전 분명 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 그분 앞에 서 있었을 겁니다."], ["I whispered to him, ‘Why, she’s there, there, under the window; how is it you don’t see her?’ I said.", "전 그분께 속삭였지요, '아니, 저기 있잖습니까, 저기, 창문 아래요, 어째서 안 보이십니까?' 전 말했습니다."], ["‘Bring her then, bring her.’ ‘She’s afraid,’ said I; ‘she was frightened at the noise, she’s hidden in the bushes; go and call to her yourself from the study.’ He ran to the window, put the candle in the window.", "'그럼 데려와라, 데려와.' '겁을 먹었습니다.'"], ["‘Grushenka,’ he cried, ‘Grushenka, are you here?’ Though he cried that, he didn’t want to lean out of the window, he didn’t want to move away from me, for he was panic-stricken; he was so frightened he didn’t dare to turn his back on me.", "전 말했지요, '소리에 놀라서 덤불 속에 숨었습니다, 서재에서 직접 그 여자를 불러 보세요.' 그분은 창가로 달려가 촛불을 창턱에 올려놓으셨습니다."], ["‘Why, here she is,’ said I.", "'그루셴카,'"], ["I went up to the window and leaned right out of it.", "그분이 외치셨지요, '그루셴카, 거기 있느냐?'"], ["‘Here she is; she’s in the bush, laughing at you, don’t you see her?’ He suddenly believed it; he was all of a shake—he was awfully crazy about her—and he leaned right out of the window.", "그렇게 외치시면서도 그분은 창밖으로 몸을 내밀려 하지 않으셨고, 제게서 떨어지려 하지 않으셨지요, 공포에 사로잡혀 계셨으니까요. 너무 무서운 나머지 감히 제게 등을 돌리지 못하셨던 겁니다."], ["I snatched up that iron paper-weight from his table; do you remember, weighing about three pounds?", "'아니, 여기 있잖습니까.' 전 말했지요."], ["I swung it and hit him on the top of the skull with the corner of it.", "전 창가로 다가가 그 밖으로 몸을 쑥 내밀었습니다. '여기 있어요, 덤불 속에서 나리를 비웃고 있잖습니까, 안 보이세요?'"], ["He didn’t even cry out.", "그분은 별안간 그 말을 믿으셨습니다."], ["He only sank down suddenly, and I hit him again and a third time.", "온몸을 부들부들 떠셨지요—그 여자에게 미칠 듯이 빠져 계셨으니까요—그러더니 창밖으로 몸을 쑥 내미셨습니다. 전 그분 책상에서 그 무쇠 문진을 잽싸게 집어 들었지요, 기억하십니까, 삼 파운드쯤 나가던 그것 말입니다?"], ["And the third time I knew I’d broken his skull.", "전 그걸 휘둘러 그 모서리로 그분 정수리를 내리쳤습니다."], ["He suddenly rolled on his back, face upwards, covered with blood.", "그분은 비명조차 지르지 않으셨지요. 그저 별안간 풀썩 주저앉으셨고, 전 다시 한번, 그리고 세 번째로 내리쳤습니다."], ["I looked round. There was no blood on me, not a spot.", "세 번째에 전 그분 두개골이 부서진 걸 알았지요."], ["I wiped the paper-weight, put it back, went up to the ikons, took the money out of the envelope, and flung the envelope on the floor and the pink ribbon beside it.", "그분은 별안간 벌렁 나자빠지셨는데, 얼굴을 위로 한 채 온통 피투성이가 되어 계셨습니다. 전 사방을 둘러봤지요."], ["I went out into the garden all of a tremble, straight to the apple-tree with a hollow in it—you know that hollow.", "제 몸에는 피 한 점 묻지 않았더군요, 단 한 방울도요. 전 문진을 닦아 제자리에 돌려놓고, 성상들 쪽으로 다가가 봉투에서 돈을 꺼낸 뒤, 봉투는 바닥에 내던지고 그 곁에 분홍색 끈도 던져 놨습니다."], ["I’d marked it long before and put a rag and a piece of paper ready in it.", "전 온몸을 떨며 정원으로 나가, 곧장 구멍이 파인 사과나무로 갔지요—그 구멍을 아시죠."], ["I wrapped all the notes in the rag and stuffed it deep down in the hole.", "전 오래전부터 그걸 봐 두고 거기에 헝겊 한 조각과 종이 한 장을 미리 넣어 뒀습니다. 전 지폐를 죄다 헝겊에 싸서 그 구멍 속 깊숙이 쑤셔 넣었지요."], ["And there it stayed for over a fortnight.", "그리고 그건 보름 넘게 거기 그대로 있었습니다."], ["I took it out later, when I came out of the hospital.", "나중에, 제가 병원에서 나왔을 때 그걸 꺼냈지요."], ["I went back to my bed, lay down and thought, ‘If Grigory Vassilyevitch has been killed outright it may be a bad job for me, but if he is not killed and recovers, it will be first-rate, for then he’ll bear witness that Dmitri Fyodorovitch has been here, and so he must have killed him and taken the money.’ Then I began groaning with suspense and impatience, so as to wake Marfa Ignatyevna as soon as possible.", "전 제 침상으로 돌아가 누워서 생각했습니다, '만약 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그 자리에서 죽어 버렸다면 나한테 고약한 일이 되겠지만, 죽지 않고 회복된다면 그야말로 더할 나위 없겠다, 그러면 그가 드미트리 표도로비치가 여기 다녀갔다고 증언할 테고, 그러니 그분이 그분을 죽이고 돈을 가져간 게 될 테니까.' 그러고 나서 전 마르파 이그나티예브나를 되도록 빨리 깨우려고 조바심과 안달 속에 신음을 하기 시작했지요."], ["At last she got up and she rushed to me, but when she saw Grigory Vassilyevitch was not there, she ran out, and I heard her scream in the garden.", "마침내 그 여자가 일어나 제게 달려왔는데, 그리고리 바실리예비치가 없는 걸 보더니 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔고, 전 그 여자가 정원에서 비명을 지르는 소리를 들었습니다."], ["And that set it all going and set my mind at rest.”", "그래서 모든 게 굴러가기 시작했고, 제 마음도 놓였지요.\""]], [["He stopped.", "그는 말을 멈췄다."], ["Ivan had listened all the time in dead silence without stirring or taking his eyes off him.", "이반은 그동안 내내 미동도 없이, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["As he told his story Smerdyakov glanced at him from time to time, but for the most part kept his eyes averted.", "스메르댜코프는 이야기를 하면서 이따금 그를 흘끗거렸으나, 대개는 눈길을 딴 데로 돌리고 있었다."], ["When he had finished he was evidently agitated and was breathing hard.", "이야기를 마쳤을 때 그는 분명 동요한 기색이었고 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬고 있었다."], ["The perspiration stood out on his face.", "얼굴에는 땀이 송골송골 맺혀 있었다."], ["But it was impossible to tell whether it was remorse he was feeling, or what.", "그러나 그가 느끼는 것이 회한인지, 아니면 무엇인지는 도무지 알 길이 없었다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["cried Ivan, pondering.", "이반이 곰곰이 헤아리며 소리쳤다."], ["“What about the door?", "\"그 문은 어떻게 된 거지?"], ["If he only opened the door to you, how could Grigory have seen it open before?", "만약 그가 자네한테만 문을 열어 줬다면, 어떻게 그리고리가 그보다 먼저 문이 열려 있는 걸 봤을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["For Grigory saw it before you went.”", "그리고리는 자네가 가기 전에 그걸 봤다는데.\""]], [["It was remarkable that Ivan spoke quite amicably, in a different tone, not angry as before, so if any one had opened the door at that moment and peeped in at them, he would certainly have concluded that they were talking peaceably about some ordinary, though interesting, subject.", "이반이 사뭇 다정하게, 전과는 달리 화내지 않는 어조로 말하는 것이 눈에 띄었으니, 만약 그 순간 누군가 문을 열고 두 사람을 들여다봤더라면, 그는 분명 두 사람이 흥미롭긴 해도 그저 평범한 어떤 화제를 두고 평화롭게 이야기를 나누고 있다고 단정했을 것이다."]], [["“As for that door and Grigory Vassilyevitch’s having seen it open, that’s only his fancy,”", "\"그 문이며 그리고리 바실리예비치가 그게 열려 있는 걸 봤다는 거며, 그건 죄다 그 사람 망상일 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Smerdyakov, with a wry smile.", "스메르댜코프가 입을 비뚜름히 일그러뜨리며 말했다."], ["“He is not a man, I assure you, but an obstinate mule.", "\"장담컨대 그자는 사람이 아니라 고집불통 노새지요."], ["He didn’t see it, but fancied he had seen it, and there’s no shaking him.", "보지도 않았으면서 봤다고 우기는 거고, 도무지 그 고집을 꺾을 수가 없습니다."], ["It’s just our luck he took that notion into his head, for they can’t fail to convict Dmitri Fyodorovitch after that.”", "그자가 그런 생각을 머릿속에 박아 넣은 게 우리한테는 그야말로 천운이지요, 그 덕에 드미트리 표도로비치는 영락없이 유죄 판결을 받을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Listen ...”", "\"이봐……\""], ["said Ivan, beginning to seem bewildered again and making an effort to grasp something.", "이반이 다시 어리둥절해지는 듯하더니, 무언가를 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“Listen.", "\"이봐."], ["There are a lot of questions I want to ask you, but I forget them ...", "자네한테 물어볼 게 많은데, 자꾸 잊어버리는군……"], ["I keep forgetting and getting mixed up.", "자꾸 잊어버리고 헷갈려. 그래."], ["Yes. Tell me this at least, why did you open the envelope and leave it there on the floor?", "적어도 이것만은 말해 봐, 자넨 어째서 봉투를 뜯어서 그걸 바닥에 내버려 둔 거지?"], ["Why didn’t you simply carry off the envelope?...", "어째서 그냥 봉투째 들고 가지 않았나?……"], ["When you were telling me, I thought you spoke about it as though it were the right thing to do ...", "자네가 얘기할 때 보니, 마치 그게 마땅히 해야 할 일이었다는 듯이 말하더군……"], ["but why, I can’t understand....”", "하지만 어째서인지, 난 도무지 모르겠어……\""]], [["“I did that for a good reason.", "\"전 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서 그렇게 한 겁니다."], ["For if a man had known all about it, as I did for instance, if he’d seen those notes before, and perhaps had put them in that envelope himself, and had seen the envelope sealed up and addressed, with his own eyes, if such a man had done the murder, what should have made him tear open the envelope afterwards, especially in such desperate haste, since he’d know for certain the notes must be in the envelope?", "만약 어떤 자가 그 일을 죄다 알고 있었다면, 이를테면 저처럼요, 그 지폐를 전에 본 적이 있고, 어쩌면 손수 그 봉투에 넣었으며, 봉투가 봉해지고 겉봉이 쓰이는 걸 제 눈으로 직접 봤다면, 그런 자가 살인을 저질렀을 경우, 무엇하러 나중에 봉투를 뜯겠습니까, 더구나 그렇게 다급하기 짝이 없는 와중에 말입니다, 지폐가 봉투 안에 들어 있다는 걸 분명히 알고 있을 텐데요?"], ["No, if the robber had been some one like me, he’d simply have put the envelope straight in his pocket and got away with it as fast as he could.", "그렇지요, 만약 도둑이 저 같은 자였다면, 그냥 봉투를 곧장 주머니에 넣고 되도록 빨리 들고 달아났을 겁니다."], ["But it’d be quite different with Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "하지만 드미트리 표도로비치라면 사정이 전혀 다르지요."], ["He only knew about the envelope by hearsay; he had never seen it, and if he’d found it, for instance, under the mattress, he’d have torn it open as quickly as possible to make sure the notes were in it.", "그분은 봉투에 관해 그저 풍문으로만 알았을 뿐, 한 번도 본 적이 없었으니, 만약 그걸, 이를테면 매트리스 밑에서 찾아냈다면, 지폐가 안에 들었는지 확인하려고 되도록 빨리 그걸 뜯었을 겁니다."], ["And he’d have thrown the envelope down, without having time to think that it would be evidence against him.", "그러고는 그게 자기한테 불리한 증거가 되리란 걸 미처 생각할 겨를도 없이 봉투를 내던졌겠지요."], ["Because he was not an habitual thief and had never directly stolen anything before, for he is a gentleman born, and if he did bring himself to steal, it would not be regular stealing, but simply taking what was his own, for he’d told the whole town he meant to before, and had even bragged aloud before every one that he’d go and take his property from Fyodor Pavlovitch.", "왜냐하면 그분은 상습 도둑이 아닌 데다 전에 직접 무얼 훔쳐 본 적도 없으니까요, 태생이 양반이시니 말입니다. 설령 도둑질을 결심하셨다 해도 그건 제대로 된 도둑질이 아니라 그저 제 것을 되찾는 것일 뿐이었을 테니, 그분은 그러겠다고 전부터 온 고을에 떠벌리셨고, 표도르 파블로비치에게서 제 재산을 찾아오겠노라 모두 앞에서 큰소리로 자랑까지 하셨거든요."], ["I didn’t say that openly to the prosecutor when I was being examined, but quite the contrary, I brought him to it by a hint, as though I didn’t see it myself, and as though he’d thought of it himself and I hadn’t prompted him; so that Mr.", "전 심문을 받을 때 검사에게 그걸 대놓고 말하지는 않았고, 오히려 정반대로, 제 입으로는 그걸 미처 깨닫지 못한 척, 그리고 그분 스스로 그런 생각을 떠올린 척, 제가 부추긴 게 아닌 척하면서 슬쩍 암시를 흘려 검사를 그쪽으로 이끌었지요."], ["Prosecutor’s mouth positively watered at my suggestion.”", "그랬더니 검사 나리는 제 암시에 그야말로 군침을 흘리시더군요.\""]], [["“But can you possibly have thought of all that on the spot?”", "\"하지만 자네가 그 모든 걸 그 자리에서 생각해 냈단 말인가?\""], ["cried Ivan, overcome with astonishment.", "이반은 경악에 사로잡혀 소리쳤다."], ["He looked at Smerdyakov again with alarm.", "그는 다시 놀란 눈으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보았다."]], [["“Mercy on us!", "\"원, 별말씀을!"], ["Could any one think of it all in such a desperate hurry?", "그렇게 다급하기 짝이 없는 와중에 누가 그 모든 걸 생각해 내겠습니까?"], ["It was all thought out beforehand.”", "죄다 미리 궁리해 둔 거지요.\""]], [["“Well ...", "\"그래……"], ["well, it was the devil helped you!”", "그래, 그건 악마가 자넬 도운 거야!\""], ["Ivan cried again.", "이반이 다시 소리쳤다."], ["“No, you are not a fool, you are far cleverer than I thought....”", "\"아니, 자넨 바보가 아니야, 내가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 영리한 놈이군……\""]], [["He got up, obviously intending to walk across the room.", "그는 자리에서 일어났는데, 보아하니 방을 가로질러 거닐 작정인 듯했다."], ["He was in terrible distress.", "그는 끔찍한 고뇌에 빠져 있었다."], ["But as the table blocked his way, and there was hardly room to pass between the table and the wall, he only turned round where he stood and sat down again.", "그러나 탁자가 길을 막고 있는 데다 탁자와 벽 사이로 빠져나갈 틈이 거의 없는 터라, 그는 선 자리에서 그저 몸을 돌려 도로 주저앉고 말았다."], ["Perhaps the impossibility of moving irritated him, as he suddenly cried out almost as furiously as before.", "어쩌면 움직일 수 없다는 사실이 그를 짜증나게 했는지, 그는 별안간 거의 전처럼 사납게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Listen, you miserable, contemptible creature!", "\"잘 들어, 이 비참하고 천하기 짝이 없는 놈아!"], ["Don’t you understand that if I haven’t killed you, it’s simply because I am keeping you to answer to-morrow at the trial.", "내가 널 죽이지 않은 건 그저 내일 재판에서 답하게 하려고 널 살려 두는 것뿐이란 걸 모르겠나?"], ["God sees,”", "하느님이 보고 계신다.\""], ["Ivan raised his hand, “perhaps I, too, was guilty; perhaps I really had a secret desire for my father’s ...", "이반이 손을 들어 올렸다. \"어쩌면 나도 죄가 있을지 몰라, 어쩌면 정말로 내 아버지의……"], ["death, but I swear I was not as guilty as you think, and perhaps I didn’t urge you on at all.", "죽음을 은밀히 바랐을지도 모르지, 하지만 맹세컨대 난 네가 생각하는 만큼 죄가 있진 않아, 어쩌면 난 널 부추긴 적이 전혀 없을지도 몰라."], ["No, no, I didn’t urge you on!", "아냐, 아냐, 난 널 부추기지 않았어!"], ["But no matter, I will give evidence against myself to-morrow at the trial.", "하지만 상관없다, 난 내일 재판에서 나 자신에게 불리한 증언을 하겠어."], ["I’m determined to!", "결심했다고!"], ["I shall tell everything, everything.", "죄다 말하겠어, 죄다 말이야."], ["But we’ll make our appearance together.", "하지만 우린 함께 출두하는 거다."], ["And whatever you may say against me at the trial, whatever evidence you give, I’ll face it; I am not afraid of you.", "그리고 네가 재판에서 나에게 무슨 불리한 말을 하든, 무슨 증언을 하든, 난 맞서겠어. 난 네가 두렵지 않아."], ["I’ll confirm it all myself!", "죄다 내 입으로 확인해 주마!"], ["But you must confess, too!", "하지만 너도 자백해야 해!"], ["You must, you must; we’ll go together.", "해야 한다, 반드시, 우린 함께 가는 거야."], ["That’s how it shall be!”", "그렇게 될 거다!\""]], [["Ivan said this solemnly and resolutely and from his flashing eyes alone it could be seen that it would be so.", "이반은 이 말을 엄숙하고 단호하게 내뱉었고, 번득이는 그 눈빛만 보아도 정말 그렇게 되리란 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“You are ill, I see; you are quite ill.", "\"나리는 편찮으십니다, 보아하니 그래요, 아주 편찮으십니다."], ["Your eyes are yellow,”", "눈이 누렇잖습니까.\""], ["Smerdyakov commented, without the least irony, with apparent sympathy in fact.", "스메르댜코프는 조금의 빈정거림도 없이, 오히려 동정하는 기색을 띠며 말했다."]], [["“We’ll go together,”", "\"우린 함께 간다.\""], ["Ivan repeated.", "이반이 거듭 말했다."], ["“And if you won’t go, no matter, I’ll go alone.”", "\"그리고 네가 가지 않겠다면, 상관없어, 나 혼자 가겠다.\""]], [["Smerdyakov paused as though pondering.", "스메르댜코프는 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“There’ll be nothing of the sort, and you won’t go,”", "\"그런 일은 절대 없을 거고, 나리는 가지 않으실 겁니다.\""], ["he concluded at last positively.", "그가 마침내 단정적으로 결론을 내렸다."]], [["“You don’t understand me,”", "\"넌 날 이해하지 못해.\""], ["Ivan exclaimed reproachfully.", "이반이 책망하듯 소리쳤다."]], [["“You’ll be too much ashamed, if you confess it all.", "\"죄다 자백하시면 나리는 너무도 부끄러우실 겁니다."], ["And, what’s more, it will be no use at all, for I shall say straight out that I never said anything of the sort to you, and that you are either ill (and it looks like it, too), or that you’re so sorry for your brother that you are sacrificing yourself to save him and have invented it all against me, for you’ve always thought no more of me than if I’d been a fly.", "게다가 무엇보다 그래 봐야 아무 소용이 없지요, 전 단번에 잘라 말할 테니까요, 나리께 그런 얘긴 한 적이 없다고요, 그리고 나리는 편찮으시거나(보아하니 정말 그래 보입니다만), 아니면 형님이 하도 가여운 나머지 그분을 구하려고 자신을 희생하시느라 죄다 절 모함해 지어내신 거라고요, 나리는 늘 절 파리 한 마리만도 못하게 여기셨으니까요."], ["And who will believe you, and what single proof have you got?”", "한데 누가 나리 말을 믿겠습니까, 게다가 나리한테 무슨 증거가 단 하나라도 있으십니까?\""]], [["“Listen, you showed me those notes just now to convince me.”", "\"이봐, 자넨 방금 날 납득시키려고 그 지폐를 보여 줬잖나.\""]], [["Smerdyakov lifted the book off the notes and laid it on one side.", "스메르댜코프는 지폐 위에 덮어 둔 책을 들어 한쪽으로 치워 놨다."]], [["“Take that money away with you,”", "\"그 돈은 가지고 가십시오.\""], ["Smerdyakov sighed.", "스메르댜코프가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“Of course, I shall take it.", "\"물론 가져가지."], ["But why do you give it to me, if you committed the murder for the sake of it?”", "한데 자네가 그 돈 때문에 살인을 저질렀다면서, 어째서 그걸 나한테 주는 거지?\""], ["Ivan looked at him with great surprise.", "이반은 몹시 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I don’t want it,”", "\"전 그게 필요 없습니다.\""], ["Smerdyakov articulated in a shaking voice, with a gesture of refusal.", "스메르댜코프는 거절하는 손짓을 하며, 떨리는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다."], ["“I did have an idea of beginning a new life with that money in Moscow or, better still, abroad.", "\"전 그 돈을 가지고 모스크바에서, 아니 그보다는 외국에서 새 삶을 시작해 볼 생각이 있긴 했지요."], ["I did dream of it, chiefly because ‘all things are lawful.’ That was quite right what you taught me, for you talked a lot to me about that.", "정말 그런 꿈을 꿨습니다, 무엇보다 '모든 것이 허용된다'고 했으니까요. 나리께서 제게 가르쳐 주신 게 전적으로 옳았지요, 나리는 제게 그 얘길 숱하게 하셨으니까요."], ["For if there’s no everlasting God, there’s no such thing as virtue, and there’s no need of it.", "만약 영원하신 하느님이 없다면, 미덕이란 것도 없고, 그런 게 필요하지도 않으니 말입니다."], ["You were right there.", "나리 말씀이 옳았어요."], ["So that’s how I looked at it.”", "그래서 전 그렇게 본 거지요.\""]], [["“Did you come to that of yourself?”", "\"그걸 자네 혼자 깨우쳤나?\""], ["asked Ivan, with a wry smile.", "이반이 입을 비뚜름히 일그러뜨리며 물었다."]], [["“With your guidance.”", "\"나리의 가르침을 받아서지요.\""]], [["“And now, I suppose, you believe in God, since you are giving back the money?”", "\"그럼 이제, 돈을 돌려주는 걸 보니, 하느님을 믿는 모양이군?\""]], [["“No, I don’t believe,”", "\"아뇨, 전 안 믿습니다.\""], ["whispered Smerdyakov.", "스메르댜코프가 속삭였다."]], [["“Then why are you giving it back?”", "\"그럼 어째서 그걸 돌려주는 거지?\""]], [["“Leave off ...", "\"그만두세요……"], ["that’s enough!”", "그만하면 됐습니다!\""], ["Smerdyakov waved his hand again.", "스메르댜코프가 다시 손을 내저었다."], ["“You used to say yourself that everything was lawful, so now why are you so upset, too?", "\"모든 것이 허용된다고 나리께서 손수 말씀하시지 않았습니까, 그런데 이제 와서 나리는 왜 그렇게 안절부절못하시는 겁니까?"], ["You even want to go and give evidence against yourself....", "심지어 가서 자신에게 불리한 증언까지 하시겠다니요……"], ["Only there’ll be nothing of the sort!", "하지만 그런 일은 절대 없을 겁니다!"], ["You won’t go to give evidence,”", "나리는 증언하러 가지 않으실 거예요.\""], ["Smerdyakov decided with conviction.", "스메르댜코프가 확신에 차서 단정했다."]], [["“You’ll see,”", "\"두고 보면 알겠지.\""], ["said Ivan.", "이반이 말했다."]], [["“It isn’t possible.", "\"있을 수 없는 일입니다."], ["You are very clever.", "나리는 무척 영리하시지요."], ["You are fond of money, I know that.", "돈을 좋아하시고요, 그건 제가 압니다."], ["You like to be respected, too, for you’re very proud; you are far too fond of female charms, too, and you mind most of all about living in undisturbed comfort, without having to depend on any one—that’s what you care most about.", "또 존경받는 것도 좋아하시지요, 자존심이 무척 세시니까요. 게다가 여자의 매력도 지나치게 밝히시고요, 무엇보다 아무에게도 기대지 않고 마음 편히 안락하게 사는 걸 가장 중히 여기시지요—그게 나리가 가장 소중히 여기시는 겁니다."], ["You won’t want to spoil your life for ever by taking such a disgrace on yourself.", "나리는 그런 치욕을 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 걸로 평생을 영영 망쳐 버리고 싶진 않으실 겁니다."], ["You are like Fyodor Pavlovitch, you are more like him than any of his children; you’ve the same soul as he had.”", "나리는 표도르 파블로비치를 닮으셨어요, 그분의 어느 자식보다도 나리가 그분을 더 닮으셨지요. 나리한테는 그분과 똑같은 영혼이 깃들어 있습니다.\""]], [["“You are not a fool,”", "\"넌 바보가 아니군.\""], ["said Ivan, seeming struck.", "이반은 충격을 받은 듯 말했다."], ["The blood rushed to his face.", "얼굴로 피가 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["“You are serious now!”", "\"이제 진지하게 말하는군!\""], ["he observed, looking suddenly at Smerdyakov with a different expression.", "그는 별안간 달라진 표정으로 스메르댜코프를 바라보며 한마디 했다."]], [["“It was your pride made you think I was a fool.", "\"제가 바보라고 여기신 건 나리의 자존심 때문이었지요."], ["Take the money.”", "돈을 가지고 가십시오.\""]], [["Ivan took the three rolls of notes and put them in his pocket without wrapping them in anything.", "이반은 지폐 세 다발을 집어, 아무것에도 싸지 않은 채 주머니에 쑤셔 넣었다."]], [["“I shall show them at the court to-morrow,”", "\"이걸 내일 법정에서 내보이겠다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Nobody will believe you, as you’ve plenty of money of your own; you may simply have taken it out of your cash-box and brought it to the court.”", "\"아무도 나리 말을 믿지 않을 겁니다, 나리한테는 본디 돈이 넉넉하니까요. 그저 나리가 금고에서 꺼내 법정에 들고 오신 거라고 여기겠지요.\""]], [["Ivan rose from his seat.", "이반은 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“I repeat,”", "\"다시 말하지만,\""], ["he said, “the only reason I haven’t killed you is that I need you for to-morrow, remember that, don’t forget it!”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 널 죽이지 않은 단 하나의 까닭은 내일을 위해 네가 필요해서다, 그걸 기억해 둬, 잊지 말라고!\""]], [["“Well, kill me.", "\"그럼, 절 죽이세요."], ["Kill me now,”", "지금 죽여 보십시오.\""], ["Smerdyakov said, all at once looking strangely at Ivan.", "스메르댜코프가 별안간 묘한 눈빛으로 이반을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“You won’t dare do that even!”", "\"나리는 그것조차 감히 못 하실 겁니다!\""], ["he added, with a bitter smile.", "그가 쓰디쓴 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."], ["“You won’t dare to do anything, you, who used to be so bold!”", "\"나리는 아무것도 감히 못 하실 거예요, 한때 그토록 대담하시던 나리가 말입니다!\""]], [["“Till to-morrow,”", "\"내일 보자.\""], ["cried Ivan, and moved to go out.", "이반이 소리치며 나가려고 몸을 움직였다."]], [["“Stay a moment....", "\"잠깐만요……"], ["Show me those notes again.”", "그 지폐를 다시 한번 보여 주십시오.\""]], [["Ivan took out the notes and showed them to him.", "이반은 지폐를 꺼내 그에게 보여 주었다."], ["Smerdyakov looked at them for ten seconds.", "스메르댜코프는 십 초쯤 그것을 바라보았다."]], [["“Well, you can go,”", "\"그럼, 가셔도 됩니다.\""], ["he said, with a wave of his hand.", "그가 손을 한 번 내저으며 말했다."], ["“Ivan Fyodorovitch!”", "\"이반 표도로비치!\""], ["he called after him again.", "그가 다시 그를 불러 세웠다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 원하나?\""], ["Ivan turned without stopping. “Good-by!”", "이반은 걸음을 멈추지 않은 채 돌아보았다."]], [["“Till to-morrow!”", "\"안녕히 가십시오!\""], ["Ivan cried again, and he walked out of the cottage.", "\"내일 보자!\" 이반이 다시 소리쳤고, 오두막을 걸어 나왔다."]], [["The snowstorm was still raging.", "눈보라는 여전히 사납게 몰아치고 있었다."], ["He walked the first few steps boldly, but suddenly began staggering.", "그는 처음 몇 걸음은 당당하게 내디뎠으나, 별안간 비틀거리기 시작했다."], ["“It’s something physical,”", "\"몸 탓이군.\""], ["he thought with a grin.", "그는 쓴웃음을 지으며 생각했다."], ["Something like joy was springing up in his heart.", "무언가 기쁨 비슷한 것이 그의 가슴속에서 솟아오르고 있었다."], ["He was conscious of unbounded resolution; he would make an end of the wavering that had so tortured him of late.", "그는 한없는 결의를 자각하고 있었으니, 요즘 들어 그토록 그를 괴롭히던 망설임을 끝장내려는 것이었다."], ["His determination was taken, “and now it will not be changed,”", "결심은 섰고, \"이젠 바뀌지 않을 거야.\""], ["he thought with relief.", "그는 안도하며 생각했다."], ["At that moment he stumbled against something and almost fell down.", "바로 그 순간 그는 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리다 하마터면 넘어질 뻔했다."], ["Stopping short, he made out at his feet the peasant he had knocked down, still lying senseless and motionless.", "우뚝 멈춰 선 그는 자기 발치에서, 자기가 쓰러뜨린 그 농부가 여전히 정신을 잃고 꼼짝없이 누워 있는 것을 알아보았다."], ["The snow had almost covered his face.", "눈이 그의 얼굴을 거의 다 덮고 있었다."], ["Ivan seized him and lifted him in his arms.", "이반은 그를 붙들어 두 팔로 안아 일으켰다."], ["Seeing a light in the little house to the right he went up, knocked at the shutters, and asked the man to whom the house belonged to help him carry the peasant to the police-station, promising him three roubles.", "오른편의 작은 집에서 불빛이 새어 나오는 것을 보고, 그는 그리로 다가가 덧창을 두드린 뒤, 집주인에게 삼 루블을 주겠노라 약속하며 그 농부를 경찰서까지 옮기는 걸 거들어 달라고 청했다."], ["The man got ready and came out.", "사내는 채비를 하고 밖으로 나왔다."], ["I won’t describe in detail how Ivan succeeded in his object, bringing the peasant to the police-station and arranging for a doctor to see him at once, providing with a liberal hand for the expenses.", "이반이 어떻게 자기 뜻을 이루어, 그 농부를 경찰서로 데려가 의사가 당장 그를 진찰하도록 조처하고, 후한 손길로 그 비용을 댔는지를 일일이 적지는 않겠다."], ["I will only say that this business took a whole hour, but Ivan was well content with it.", "다만 이 일에 꼬박 한 시간이 걸렸다는 것, 그러나 이반은 그것에 흡족해했다는 것만 말해 두겠다."], ["His mind wandered and worked incessantly.", "그의 마음은 쉴 새 없이 헤매고 또 움직였다."]], [["“If I had not taken my decision so firmly for to-morrow,”", "\"내가 내일 일을 그토록 단단히 결심해 두지 않았더라면,\""], ["he reflected with satisfaction, “I should not have stayed a whole hour to look after the peasant, but should have passed by, without caring about his being frozen.", "그는 만족스레 곱씹었다. \"꼬박 한 시간이나 머물며 저 농부를 돌보지 않고, 저자가 얼어 죽든 말든 아랑곳없이 그냥 지나쳐 버렸겠지."], ["I am quite capable of watching myself, by the way,”", "그건 그렇고 난 내 자신을 충분히 살필 줄 아는군.\""], ["he thought at the same instant, with still greater satisfaction, “although they have decided that I am going out of my mind!”", "그는 바로 그 순간 한층 더 큰 만족감에 젖어 생각했다. \"사람들은 내가 미쳐 가고 있다고 단정 지었지만 말이야!\""]], [["Just as he reached his own house he stopped short, asking himself suddenly hadn’t he better go at once to the prosecutor and tell him everything.", "자기 집에 다다른 바로 그때 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 별안간 지금 당장 검사에게 가서 죄다 털어놓는 편이 낫지 않을까 자문했다."], ["He decided the question by turning back to the house.", "그는 집으로 발길을 되돌리는 것으로 그 물음에 답을 내렸다."], ["“Everything together to-morrow!”", "\"내일 죄다 함께!\""], ["he whispered to himself, and, strange to say, almost all his gladness and self-satisfaction passed in one instant.", "그는 혼자 속삭였는데, 이상하게도 그 기쁨과 자기만족이 거의 죄다 한순간에 사라져 버렸다."]], [["As he entered his own room he felt something like a touch of ice on his heart, like a recollection or, more exactly, a reminder, of something agonizing and revolting that was in that room now, at that moment, and had been there before.", "자기 방으로 들어서면서 그는 가슴에 얼음이 닿는 듯한 느낌을 받았는데, 그것은 지금 이 순간 그 방 안에 있고 또 전에도 있었던, 어떤 괴롭고 역겨운 것에 대한 회상, 더 정확히 말하면 일깨움 같은 것이었다."], ["He sank wearily on his sofa.", "그는 지친 몸을 소파에 털썩 묻었다."], ["The old woman brought him a samovar; he made tea, but did not touch it.", "노파가 그에게 사모바르를 가져왔고, 그는 차를 끓였으나 입에 대지는 않았다."], ["He sat on the sofa and felt giddy.", "그는 소파에 앉아 어질어질한 현기증을 느꼈다."], ["He felt that he was ill and helpless.", "그는 자신이 병들고 무력하다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He was beginning to drop asleep, but got up uneasily and walked across the room to shake off his drowsiness.", "그는 막 잠에 빠져들려다가도 불안하게 일어나, 졸음을 떨쳐 내려고 방을 가로질러 거닐었다."], ["At moments he fancied he was delirious, but it was not illness that he thought of most.", "이따금 그는 자기가 헛것을 보고 있는 게 아닌가 싶었으나, 그가 가장 골똘히 생각한 것은 병이 아니었다."], ["Sitting down again, he began looking round, as though searching for something.", "다시 자리에 앉은 그는 무언가를 찾기라도 하듯 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["This happened several times.", "이런 일이 여러 차례 되풀이되었다."], ["At last his eyes were fastened intently on one point.", "마침내 그의 눈은 한 지점에 골똘히 못 박혔다."], ["Ivan smiled, but an angry flush suffused his face.", "이반은 미소를 지었으나, 그의 얼굴에는 성난 홍조가 번졌다."], ["He sat a long time in his place, his head propped on both arms, though he looked sideways at the same point, at the sofa that stood against the opposite wall.", "그는 머리를 두 팔에 괸 채 오랫동안 제자리에 앉아 있었는데, 그러면서도 눈은 곁눈질로 줄곧 그 한 지점을, 맞은편 벽에 붙어 있는 소파를 바라보고 있었다."], ["There was evidently something, some object, that irritated him there, worried him and tormented him.", "거기에는 분명 무언가가, 어떤 물건이 있어, 그것이 그를 짜증나게 하고, 안달하게 하고, 또 괴롭히고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter IX. The Devil. Ivan’s Nightmare", "# Chapter IX. The Devil. Ivan’s Nightmare"]], [["I am not a doctor, but yet I feel that the moment has come when I must inevitably give the reader some account of the nature of Ivan’s illness.", "나는 의사가 아니지만, 그래도 이쯤에서 독자에게 이반의 병이 어떤 성질의 것이었는지 부득이 어느 정도 설명을 해 두어야 할 순간이 왔다고 느낀다."], ["Anticipating events I can say at least one thing: he was at that moment on the very eve of an attack of brain fever.", "앞으로 벌어질 일을 미리 짚어 두자면 적어도 한 가지는 말할 수 있으니, 그는 바로 그 순간 뇌염이 발작하기 직전의 상태에 놓여 있었던 것이다."], ["Though his health had long been affected, it had offered a stubborn resistance to the fever which in the end gained complete mastery over it.", "그의 건강은 오래전부터 나빠져 있었으나, 끝내 그를 완전히 사로잡고 만 그 열병에 대해 끈질기게 버텨 왔던 것이다."], ["Though I know nothing of medicine, I venture to hazard the suggestion that he really had perhaps, by a terrible effort of will, succeeded in delaying the attack for a time, hoping, of course, to check it completely.", "의학에 관해서는 아는 바가 없으나, 나는 감히 이런 추측을 던져 보려 한다. 그는 어쩌면 무서운 의지의 힘으로 한동안 발작을 늦추는 데 정말로 성공했던 것이며, 물론 그것을 완전히 억눌러 버리기를 바랐던 것이리라."], ["He knew that he was unwell, but he loathed the thought of being ill at that fatal time, at the approaching crisis in his life, when he needed to have all his wits about him, to say what he had to say boldly and resolutely and “to justify himself to himself.”", "그는 자신이 몸이 성치 않다는 것을 알고 있었으나, 하필이면 그 운명적인 시기, 자기 생애의 다가오는 위기 앞에서 병에 걸린다는 생각만은 진저리가 났으니, 그때야말로 모든 정신을 또렷이 차리고서, 자신이 해야 할 말을 대담하고 단호하게 내뱉어 \"스스로에게 자신을 정당화\"해야 할 때였기 때문이다."]], [["He had, however, consulted the new doctor, who had been brought from Moscow by a fantastic notion of Katerina Ivanovna’s to which I have referred already.", "그러나 그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 기상천외한 발상으로 모스크바에서 불려 온, 앞서 내가 언급한 그 새로운 의사에게 진찰을 받기는 했다."], ["After listening to him and examining him the doctor came to the conclusion that he was actually suffering from some disorder of the brain, and was not at all surprised by an admission which Ivan had reluctantly made him.", "의사는 그의 말을 듣고 진찰을 마친 끝에, 그가 실제로 어떤 뇌 질환을 앓고 있다는 결론에 이르렀으며, 이반이 마지못해 털어놓은 고백에도 조금도 놀라지 않았다."], ["“Hallucinations are quite likely in your condition,”", "\"당신 상태에서 환각이 나타나는 것은 충분히 있을 수 있는 일입니다.\""], ["the doctor opined, “though it would be better to verify them ...", "의사는 이렇게 소견을 밝혔다. \"다만 그것을 확인해 보는 편이 좋겠지요……"], ["you must take steps at once, without a moment’s delay, or things will go badly with you.”", "당장, 한순간도 지체하지 말고 조치를 취해야 합니다. 그러지 않으면 일이 좋지 않게 흘러갈 겁니다.\""], ["But Ivan did not follow this judicious advice and did not take to his bed to be nursed.", "그러나 이반은 이 분별 있는 충고를 따르지 않았고, 자리에 누워 간병을 받으려 하지 않았다."], ["“I am walking about, so I am strong enough, if I drop, it’ll be different then, any one may nurse me who likes,”", "\"이렇게 걸어 다니고 있으니 견딜 만큼은 튼튼한 거지. 쓰러지면 그때는 다르겠지, 그땐 누구든 원하는 사람이 간병하면 그만이고.\""], ["he decided, dismissing the subject.", "그는 이렇게 마음을 정하고는 그 문제를 떨쳐 버렸다."]], [["And so he was sitting almost conscious himself of his delirium and, as I have said already, looking persistently at some object on the sofa against the opposite wall.", "그리하여 그는 거의 자신의 헛소리를 스스로 의식하다시피 한 채 앉아 있었고, 앞서 말한 대로 맞은편 벽에 붙은 소파 위의 어떤 물체를 끈질기게 바라보고 있었다."], ["Some one appeared to be sitting there, though goodness knows how he had come in, for he had not been in the room when Ivan came into it, on his return from Smerdyakov.", "누군가가 거기 앉아 있는 듯했는데, 도대체 어떻게 들어왔는지는 알 도리가 없었으니, 이반이 스메르댜코프에게서 돌아와 방으로 들어왔을 때만 해도 거기엔 아무도 없었기 때문이다."], ["This was a person or, more accurately speaking, a Russian gentleman of a particular kind, no longer young, qui faisait la cinquantaine, as the French say, with rather long, still thick, dark hair, slightly streaked with gray and a small pointed beard.", "그것은 한 사람, 더 정확히 말하자면 어떤 특별한 부류에 속하는 러시아 신사로, 이미 젊지는 않은, 프랑스인들이 말하듯 \"쉰 줄에 접어든(qui faisait la cinquantaine)\" 자였는데, 제법 길고 아직 숱이 많은 검은 머리에는 희끗희끗 새치가 섞여 있었고, 끝이 뾰족한 작은 턱수염을 기르고 있었다."], ["He was wearing a brownish reefer jacket, rather shabby, evidently made by a good tailor though, and of a fashion at least three years old, that had been discarded by smart and well-to-do people for the last two years.", "그는 갈색이 도는 리퍼 재킷을 걸치고 있었는데, 제법 낡기는 했으나 솜씨 좋은 재단사가 지은 것이 분명했고, 적어도 삼 년은 묵은 유행을 따른 것으로, 멋쟁이에 부유한 자들이 최근 이 년 동안에는 내다 버린 그런 양식이었다."], ["His linen and his long scarf-like neck-tie were all such as are worn by people who aim at being stylish, but on closer inspection his linen was not over-clean and his wide scarf was very threadbare.", "그의 셔츠와 목도리처럼 긴 넥타이는 모두 멋을 부리려는 자들이 두르는 그런 것이었으나, 가까이서 들여다보니 셔츠는 그리 깨끗하지 못했고 폭 넓은 목도리는 올이 다 드러나 너덜너덜했다."], ["The visitor’s check trousers were of excellent cut, but were too light in color and too tight for the present fashion.", "손님의 체크무늬 바지는 재단이 훌륭했으나, 색깔이 지나치게 밝고 요즘 유행에 비추어 너무 몸에 꼭 끼었다."], ["His soft fluffy white hat was out of keeping with the season.", "부드럽고 보송보송한 흰 모자는 계절과는 영 어울리지 않았다."]], [["In brief there was every appearance of gentility on straitened means.", "요컨대 살림이 쪼들린 신사의 면모가 구석구석 배어 있었다."], ["It looked as though the gentleman belonged to that class of idle landowners who used to flourish in the times of serfdom.", "그 신사는 농노제 시절에 번성하던 그런 한가로운 지주 부류에 속한 인물처럼 보였다."], ["He had unmistakably been, at some time, in good and fashionable society, had once had good connections, had possibly preserved them indeed, but, after a gay youth, becoming gradually impoverished on the abolition of serfdom, he had sunk into the position of a poor relation of the best class, wandering from one good old friend to another and received by them for his companionable and accommodating disposition and as being, after all, a gentleman who could be asked to sit down with any one, though, of course, not in a place of honor.", "그가 한때 좋은 사교계, 그것도 세련된 사교계에 드나들었으며, 한때는 좋은 인맥을 지녔고, 어쩌면 지금도 그것을 그대로 간직하고 있을지도 모른다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었으나, 흥청대는 젊은 시절을 보낸 뒤 농노제 폐지와 함께 차츰 가산이 기울어, 마침내 상류층의 가난한 식객 신세로 전락하여, 옛 친구 이 집 저 집을 떠돌며 그 붙임성 있고 무던한 성품 덕에, 그리고 어쨌든 누구와 함께 자리에 앉혀도 무방한 신사라는 이유로 환대를 받는 처지가 된 것이었다. 물론 상석에 앉히지는 못할 신사였지만 말이다."], ["Such gentlemen of accommodating temper and dependent position, who can tell a story, take a hand at cards, and who have a distinct aversion for any duties that may be forced upon them, are usually solitary creatures, either bachelors or widowers.", "이렇게 무던한 성품에 남에게 얹혀사는 처지의 신사들, 이야기를 늘어놓을 줄 알고, 카드 한 판쯤 거들 줄도 알며, 자기에게 떠넘겨질 만한 의무라면 무엇이든 질색하는 그런 신사들은 대개 외톨이로, 독신이거나 홀아비다."], ["Sometimes they have children, but if so, the children are always being brought up at a distance, at some aunt’s, to whom these gentlemen never allude in good society, seeming ashamed of the relationship.", "더러는 자식이 있기도 하나, 있다 한들 그 아이들은 늘 멀리 떨어진 어느 이모 집 같은 데서 자라며, 그 신사들은 좋은 사교계에서는 그 인연을 입에 올리는 법이 없으니, 그런 관계를 부끄러워하는 듯하다."], ["They gradually lose sight of their children altogether, though at intervals they receive a birthday or Christmas letter from them and sometimes even answer it.", "그들은 차츰 자식들을 아예 시야에서 놓쳐 버리고 마는데, 그래도 이따금 자식들에게서 생일이나 성탄절 편지를 받으며, 때로는 답장을 보내기까지 한다."]], [["The countenance of the unexpected visitor was not so much good-natured, as accommodating and ready to assume any amiable expression as occasion might arise.", "이 뜻밖의 손님의 얼굴은 선량하다기보다는 무던하니, 경우에 따라 어떤 상냥한 표정이든 능히 지어 보일 채비가 되어 있는 그런 얼굴이었다."], ["He had no watch, but he had a tortoise-shell lorgnette on a black ribbon.", "회중시계는 없었으나, 검은 리본에 매단 별갑 오페라글라스를 지니고 있었다."], ["On the middle finger of his right hand was a massive gold ring with a cheap opal stone in it.", "오른손 가운뎃손가락에는 값싼 오팔이 박힌 묵직한 금반지를 끼고 있었다."]], [["Ivan was angrily silent and would not begin the conversation.", "이반은 화가 난 듯 입을 다문 채 먼저 말을 꺼내려 하지 않았다."], ["The visitor waited and sat exactly like a poor relation who had come down from his room to keep his host company at tea, and was discreetly silent, seeing that his host was frowning and preoccupied.", "손님은 마치 차를 마시는 주인의 말동무가 되려고 제 방에서 내려온 가난한 식객처럼 가만히 기다리며 앉아 있었는데, 주인이 이맛살을 찌푸리고 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 조심스레 침묵을 지켰다."], ["But he was ready for any affable conversation as soon as his host should begin it.", "그러면서도 주인이 입을 열기만 하면 언제든 상냥하게 대화를 받아 줄 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["All at once his face expressed a sudden solicitude.", "별안간 그의 얼굴에 갑작스러운 근심의 빛이 떠올랐다."]], [["“I say,”", "\"이봐요.\""], ["he began to Ivan, “excuse me, I only mention it to remind you.", "그가 이반에게 말을 꺼냈다. \"실례하지만, 그저 일깨워 주려고 하는 말입니다만."], ["You went to Smerdyakov’s to find out about Katerina Ivanovna, but you came away without finding out anything about her, you probably forgot—”", "당신은 카테리나 이바노브나에 관해 알아보려고 스메르댜코프에게 갔는데, 그녀에 대해서는 아무것도 알아내지 못한 채 그냥 나와 버렸어요. 아마 잊어버린 모양이지요……\""]], [["“Ah, yes,”", "\"아, 그렇지.\""], ["broke from Ivan and his face grew gloomy with uneasiness.", "이반의 입에서 이 말이 터져 나왔고, 그의 얼굴은 불안으로 어두워졌다."], ["“Yes, I’d forgotten ...", "\"그래, 깜빡 잊고 있었어……"], ["but it doesn’t matter now, never mind, till to-morrow,”", "하지만 이제 와선 상관없어, 아무래도 좋아, 내일까지는.\""], ["he muttered to himself, “and you,”", "그가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"그리고 당신,\""], ["he added, addressing his visitor, “I should have remembered that myself in a minute, for that was just what was tormenting me!", "그가 손님 쪽을 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"그건 나도 금방 혼자 떠올렸을 거야, 바로 그게 나를 괴롭히고 있었으니까!"], ["Why do you interfere, as if I should believe that you prompted me, and that I didn’t remember it of myself?”", "왜 끼어드는 거지, 마치 그걸 당신이 일러 주었고 내가 혼자서는 떠올리지 못했을 거라고 내가 믿기라도 할 것처럼?\""]], [["“Don’t believe it then,”", "\"그럼 믿지 마시오.\""], ["said the gentleman, smiling amicably, “what’s the good of believing against your will?", "신사가 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마음에 없는데 억지로 믿어 봤자 무슨 소용이겠소?"], ["Besides, proofs are no help to believing, especially material proofs.", "게다가 증거란 믿음에 아무 도움도 안 되는 법이지요, 특히나 물질적인 증거란."], ["Thomas believed, not because he saw Christ risen, but because he wanted to believe, before he saw.", "토마스가 믿은 것은 부활한 그리스도를 보았기 때문이 아니라, 보기도 전에 이미 믿고 싶어 했기 때문이었소."], ["Look at the spiritualists, for instance....", "가령 강신술사들을 보시오……"], ["I am very fond of them ...", "나는 그자들을 무척 좋아하는데……"], ["only fancy, they imagine that they are serving the cause of religion, because the devils show them their horns from the other world.", "글쎄, 그자들은 저세상에서 악마들이 자기네한테 뿔을 보여 준다는 이유로 종교의 대의에 봉사하고 있다고 여긴단 말입니다."], ["That, they say, is a material proof, so to speak, of the existence of another world.", "그게 이를테면 또 다른 세상이 존재한다는 물질적 증거라는 거지요."], ["The other world and material proofs, what next!", "또 다른 세상에 물질적 증거라니, 별소릴 다 듣겠소!"], ["And if you come to that, does proving there’s a devil prove that there’s a God?", "게다가 따지고 보면, 악마가 있다는 걸 증명한다고 해서 신이 있다는 게 증명되는 거요?"], ["I want to join an idealist society, I’ll lead the opposition in it, I’ll say I am a realist, but not a materialist, he he!”", "나는 관념론자 협회에 가입하고 싶소, 그 안에서 반대파를 이끌면서 이렇게 말할 거요, 나는 사실주의자지 유물론자는 아니라고, 헤헤!\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 봐.\""], ["Ivan suddenly got up from the table.", "이반이 별안간 식탁에서 일어섰다."], ["“I seem to be delirious....", "\"나는 헛것을 보고 있는 것 같아……"], ["I am delirious, in fact, talk any nonsense you like, I don’t care!", "사실 헛것을 보고 있는 거지, 무슨 헛소리든 마음대로 지껄여, 난 상관없으니까!"], ["You won’t drive me to fury, as you did last time.", "지난번처럼 나를 광분으로 몰아넣지는 못할 거야."], ["But I feel somehow ashamed....", "하지만 어쩐지 부끄럽군……"], ["I want to walk about the room....", "방 안을 좀 걸어 다녀야겠어……"], ["I sometimes don’t see you and don’t even hear your voice as I did last time, but I always guess what you are prating, for it’s I, I myself speaking, not you.", "지난번처럼 가끔은 당신이 보이지도 않고 목소리조차 들리지 않지만, 그래도 당신이 무슨 헛소리를 지껄이는지는 늘 알아챈다고, 왜냐하면 그건 나니까, 당신이 아니라 나, 나 자신이 말하는 거니까."], ["Only I don’t know whether I was dreaming last time or whether I really saw you.", "다만 지난번에 내가 꿈을 꾼 건지 정말로 당신을 본 건지는 모르겠어."], ["I’ll wet a towel and put it on my head and perhaps you’ll vanish into air.”", "수건을 적셔서 머리에 얹어야겠어, 그러면 당신은 허공으로 사라져 버릴지도 모르지.\""]], [["Ivan went into the corner, took a towel, and did as he said, and with a wet towel on his head began walking up and down the room.", "이반은 구석으로 가서 수건을 집어 들고 말한 대로 했고, 젖은 수건을 머리에 얹은 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["“I am so glad you treat me so familiarly,”", "\"당신이 나를 이렇게 허물없이 대해 주니 참 기쁘오.\""], ["the visitor began.", "손님이 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Fool,”", "\"바보 같으니.\""], ["laughed Ivan, “do you suppose I should stand on ceremony with you?", "이반이 웃었다. \"내가 당신한테 격식을 차릴 줄 알았나?"], ["I am in good spirits now, though I’ve a pain in my forehead ...", "지금은 기분이 좋다고, 이마가 좀 쑤시긴 해도……"], ["and in the top of my head ...", "정수리도 그렇고……"], ["only please don’t talk philosophy, as you did last time.", "다만 제발 지난번처럼 철학 같은 건 늘어놓지 마."], ["If you can’t take yourself off, talk of something amusing.", "썩 꺼지지 못하겠거든 뭐든 재미있는 이야기나 해."], ["Talk gossip, you are a poor relation, you ought to talk gossip.", "잡담이나 늘어놓으라고, 당신은 가난한 식객이니 잡담쯤은 해야지."], ["What a nightmare to have!", "이런 악몽이 다 있나!"], ["But I am not afraid of you.", "하지만 난 당신이 두렵지 않아."], ["I’ll get the better of you.", "내가 당신을 이기고 말 거야."], ["I won’t be taken to a mad-house!”", "정신병원에는 끌려가지 않을 거라고!\""]], [["“C’est charmant, poor relation. Yes, I am in my natural shape.", "\"이거 매력적이군(C'est charmant), 가난한 식객이라."], ["For what am I on earth but a poor relation?", "그렇소, 이게 나의 타고난 모습이오."], ["By the way, I am listening to you and am rather surprised to find you are actually beginning to take me for something real, not simply your fancy, as you persisted in declaring last time—”", "내가 이 땅에서 가난한 식객 아니고 뭐겠소? 그나저나 당신 말을 듣고 있자니 제법 놀랍구려, 당신이 정말로 나를 무언가 실재하는 것으로, 지난번에 그토록 우기던 것처럼 그저 당신의 망상이 아니라 실재로 여기기 시작하다니 말이오……\""]], [["“Never for one minute have I taken you for reality,”", "\"단 한순간도 당신을 실재로 여긴 적은 없어.\""], ["Ivan cried with a sort of fury.", "이반이 일종의 광기 어린 어조로 외쳤다."], ["“You are a lie, you are my illness, you are a phantom.", "\"당신은 거짓이고, 당신은 내 병이고, 당신은 허깨비야."], ["It’s only that I don’t know how to destroy you and I see I must suffer for a time.", "다만 내가 당신을 어떻게 없애야 할지 모를 뿐이고, 한동안은 괴로워해야 하리라는 것도 알아."], ["You are my hallucination.", "당신은 내 환각이야."], ["You are the incarnation of myself, but only of one side of me ...", "당신은 나 자신의 화신이지만, 그저 내 한쪽 면일 뿐이지……"], ["of my thoughts and feelings, but only the nastiest and stupidest of them.", "내 생각과 감정 가운데서도 가장 추악하고 어리석은 것들만의 화신 말이야."], ["From that point of view you might be of interest to me, if only I had time to waste on you—”", "그런 관점에서 보면 당신은 나에게 흥미로운 존재일 수도 있겠지, 당신한테 허비할 시간이 있기만 하다면 말이야……\""]], [["“Excuse me, excuse me, I’ll catch you.", "\"미안하오, 미안하오만, 내가 한번 짚어 보겠소."], ["When you flew out at Alyosha under the lamp-post this evening and shouted to him, ‘You learnt it from him!", "오늘 저녁 당신이 가로등 밑에서 알료샤에게 버럭 화를 내며 이렇게 외쳤지요, '너 그걸 그자한테서 배웠구나!"], ["How do you know that he visits me?’ you were thinking of me then.", "그자가 나를 찾아온다는 걸 네가 어떻게 알아?' 그때 당신은 나를 생각하고 있었던 거요."], ["So for one brief moment you did believe that I really exist,”", "그러니 그 짧은 한순간만큼은 당신도 내가 정말로 존재한다고 믿었던 셈이지.\""], ["the gentleman laughed blandly.", "신사가 부드럽게 웃었다."]], [["“Yes, that was a moment of weakness ...", "\"그래, 그건 약해진 순간이었어……"], ["but I couldn’t believe in you.", "하지만 당신을 믿을 수는 없었지."], ["I don’t know whether I was asleep or awake last time.", "지난번에 내가 잠들어 있었는지 깨어 있었는지 모르겠어."], ["Perhaps I was only dreaming then and didn’t see you really at all—”", "어쩌면 그땐 그저 꿈을 꾸고 있었을 뿐, 당신을 정말로 본 건 아니었는지도……\""]], [["“And why were you so surly with Alyosha just now?", "\"그런데 아까는 어째서 알료샤한테 그렇게 퉁명스럽게 굴었소?"], ["He is a dear; I’ve treated him badly over Father Zossima.”", "그 애는 참 사랑스럽잖소. 나는 조시마 신부 일로 그 애한테 못되게 굴었지만.\""]], [["“Don’t talk of Alyosha!", "\"알료샤 얘긴 꺼내지 마!"], ["How dare you, you flunkey!”", "어딜 감히, 이 비천한 종놈 주제에!\""], ["Ivan laughed again.", "이반이 다시 웃었다."]], [["“You scold me, but you laugh—that’s a good sign.", "\"나를 욕하면서도 웃는구려, 좋은 징조요."], ["But you are ever so much more polite than you were last time and I know why: that great resolution of yours—”", "하지만 당신은 지난번보다 훨씬 더 정중해졌소, 그 까닭도 내가 알지. 당신의 그 위대한 결심 때문이……\""]], [["“Don’t speak of my resolution,”", "\"내 결심 얘긴 입에 올리지 마.\""], ["cried Ivan, savagely.", "이반이 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["“I understand, I understand, c’est noble, c’est charmant, you are going to defend your brother and to sacrifice yourself ...", "\"알겠소, 알겠소, 그건 고귀하고 매력적인 일이지요(c'est noble, c'est charmant), 당신은 형을 변호하고 자신을 희생하려는 거요……"], ["C’est chevaleresque.”", "참으로 기사도적이군요(C'est chevaleresque).\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, I’ll kick you!”", "\"입 닥쳐, 안 그러면 걷어차 버릴 테다!\""]], [["“I shan’t be altogether sorry, for then my object will be attained.", "\"그래도 나로서는 그리 섭섭하지 않을 거요, 그러면 내 목적이 이루어지는 셈이니까."], ["If you kick me, you must believe in my reality, for people don’t kick ghosts.", "당신이 나를 걷어찬다면, 당신은 내가 실재한다고 믿어야 하는 거요, 사람들이 유령을 걷어차지는 않으니까 말이오."], ["Joking apart, it doesn’t matter to me, scold if you like, though it’s better to be a trifle more polite even to me.", "농담은 그만두고, 나야 아무래도 좋소, 욕하고 싶으면 욕하시오, 그래도 나한테까지 조금쯤은 더 정중하게 구는 편이 낫겠지만. '바보다, 종놈이다'"], ["‘Fool, flunkey!’ what words!”", "하니, 무슨 말본새가 그렇소!\""]], [["“Scolding you, I scold myself,”", "\"당신을 욕하는 건 나 자신을 욕하는 거야.\""], ["Ivan laughed again, “you are myself, myself, only with a different face.", "이반이 다시 웃었다. \"당신은 나야, 나 자신이라고, 다만 얼굴만 다를 뿐이지."], ["You just say what I am thinking ...", "당신은 그저 내가 생각하는 걸 말할 뿐이고……"], ["and are incapable of saying anything new!”", "뭔가 새로운 건 한마디도 말하지 못해!\""]], [["“If I am like you in my way of thinking, it’s all to my credit,”", "\"내 사고방식이 당신과 닮았다면, 그건 다 내 공로지요.\""], ["the gentleman declared, with delicacy and dignity.", "신사가 우아하고 위엄 있게 단언했다."]], [["“You choose out only my worst thoughts, and what’s more, the stupid ones.", "\"당신은 내 생각 중에서도 가장 나쁜 것들만, 게다가 어리석은 것들만 골라내."], ["You are stupid and vulgar.", "당신은 어리석고 천박해."], ["You are awfully stupid.", "지독하게 어리석다고."], ["No, I can’t put up with you!", "안 되겠어, 더는 당신을 견딜 수가 없어!"], ["What am I to do, what am I to do?”", "어쩌면 좋지, 대체 어쩌면 좋단 말인가?\""], ["Ivan said through his clenched teeth.", "이반이 이를 악문 채 말했다."]], [["“My dear friend, above all things I want to behave like a gentleman and to be recognized as such,”", "\"여보게, 무엇보다도 나는 신사답게 처신하고 또 그렇게 인정받고 싶을 뿐이오.\""], ["the visitor began in an excess of deprecating and simple-hearted pride, typical of a poor relation.", "손님이 가난한 식객 특유의, 겸양과 순박한 자존심이 넘쳐 나는 어조로 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“I am poor, but ...", "\"나는 가난하지만……"], ["I won’t say very honest, but ...", "아주 정직하다고는 못해도……"], ["it’s an axiom generally accepted in society that I am a fallen angel.", "사교계에서 일반적으로 통하는 공리에 따르면 나는 타락한 천사라오."], ["I certainly can’t conceive how I can ever have been an angel.", "내가 어떻게 천사였을 수 있었는지는 도무지 상상이 안 가오."], ["If I ever was, it must have been so long ago that there’s no harm in forgetting it.", "설령 그랬다 한들 너무도 까마득한 옛날 일이라 잊어버린들 탈 될 게 없을 거요."], ["Now I only prize the reputation of being a gentlemanly person and live as I can, trying to make myself agreeable.", "이제 나는 그저 신사다운 인물이라는 평판이나 소중히 여기며, 되도록 남의 비위를 맞추려고 애쓰면서 형편 닿는 대로 살아가오."], ["I love men genuinely, I’ve been greatly calumniated!", "나는 진심으로 사람을 사랑하건만, 무던히도 모함을 받아 왔소!"], ["Here when I stay with you from time to time, my life gains a kind of reality and that’s what I like most of all.", "이렇게 이따금 당신 곁에 머물면, 내 삶이 일종의 실재성을 얻게 되는데, 나는 그게 무엇보다도 좋소."], ["You see, like you, I suffer from the fantastic and so I love the realism of earth.", "알다시피 나도 당신처럼 환상적인 것 때문에 괴로워하기에, 이 땅의 사실주의를 사랑하는 거요."], ["Here, with you, everything is circumscribed, here all is formulated and geometrical, while we have nothing but indeterminate equations!", "여기, 당신들 곁에서는 모든 것이 한계가 분명하고, 여기서는 모든 것이 공식화되고 기하학적인데, 우리한테는 부정방정식밖에 없거든! 나는 여기서 몽상에 잠긴 채 떠돌아다니오."], ["I wander about here dreaming.", "나는 몽상하기를 좋아하지요."], ["I like dreaming. Besides, on earth I become superstitious.", "게다가 이 땅에서는 나도 미신을 믿게 되오."], ["Please don’t laugh, that’s just what I like, to become superstitious.", "제발 웃지 마시오, 그게 바로 내가 좋아하는 거니까, 미신을 믿게 되는 것 말이오."], ["I adopt all your habits here: I’ve grown fond of going to the public baths, would you believe it?", "나는 여기서 당신들의 온갖 습관을 받아들이오. 글쎄, 대중탕에 가는 걸 좋아하게 됐다면 믿겠소?"], ["and I go and steam myself with merchants and priests.", "가서 상인들이며 사제들과 함께 몸을 푹푹 찌곤 하지요."], ["What I dream of is becoming incarnate once for all and irrevocably in the form of some merchant’s wife weighing eighteen stone, and of believing all she believes.", "내가 꿈꾸는 건 단번에, 그것도 돌이킬 수 없이, 백팔십 근쯤 나가는 어느 상인 마누라의 몸으로 화신하여 그 여자가 믿는 걸 죄다 믿어 버리는 것이오."], ["My ideal is to go to church and offer a candle in simple-hearted faith, upon my word it is.", "내 이상은 교회에 가서 순박한 신앙으로 촛불 하나 바치는 것, 정말이지 그렇소."], ["Then there would be an end to my sufferings.", "그러면 내 고통도 끝장이 날 텐데."], ["I like being doctored too; in the spring there was an outbreak of smallpox and I went and was vaccinated in a foundling hospital—if only you knew how I enjoyed myself that day.", "나는 치료받는 것도 좋아하오. 봄에 천연두가 돌았을 때 나는 고아원에 가서 종두를 맞았는데, 그날 내가 얼마나 즐거웠는지 당신이 알기나 하겠소."], ["I subscribed ten roubles in the cause of the Slavs!...", "슬라브인들을 위한다는 명목으로 십 루블을 기부하기도 했지!……"], ["But you are not listening.", "한데 당신은 듣고 있지 않군."], ["Do you know, you are not at all well this evening?", "그거 아오, 당신 오늘 저녁 영 상태가 좋지 않소."], ["I know you went yesterday to that doctor ...", "어제 그 의사한테 다녀온 거 알고 있소……"], ["well, what about your health?", "그래, 건강은 어떻소?"], ["What did the doctor say?”", "의사가 뭐라 합디까?\""]], [["“Fool!”", "\"바보 같으니!\""], ["Ivan snapped out.", "이반이 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“But you are clever, anyway.", "\"하지만 어쨌든 당신은 영리하잖소."], ["You are scolding again?", "또 욕이오?"], ["I didn’t ask out of sympathy.", "동정해서 물어본 게 아니오."], ["You needn’t answer.", "대답 안 해도 좋소."], ["Now rheumatism has come in again—”", "또 류머티즘이 도졌단 말이지……\""]], [["“Fool!”", "\"바보!\""], ["repeated Ivan.", "이반이 되풀이했다."]], [["“You keep saying the same thing; but I had such an attack of rheumatism last year that I remember it to this day.”", "\"같은 말만 자꾸 하는구려. 하지만 작년에 어찌나 류머티즘 발작이 심했던지 지금까지도 또렷이 기억난다오.\""]], [["“The devil have rheumatism!”", "\"악마가 류머티즘에 걸린다고!\""]], [["“Why not, if I sometimes put on fleshly form?", "\"안 될 게 뭐요, 이따금 육신의 형상을 걸칠 때면? 나도 육신의 형상을 걸치면 그 대가를 치르는 거요."], ["I put on fleshly form and I take the consequences. Satan sum et nihil humanum a me alienum puto.”", "나는 사탄이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇도 나와 무관하지 않다고 여기오(Satan sum et nihil humanum a me alienum puto).\""]], [["“What, what, Satan sum et nihil humanum ...", "\"뭐라고, 뭐, 나는 사탄이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇도(Satan sum et nihil humanum)……"], ["that’s not bad for the devil!”", "악마치고는 제법 그럴듯한데!\""]], [["“I am glad I’ve pleased you at last.”", "\"드디어 당신 마음에 들었다니 기쁘오.\""]], [["“But you didn’t get that from me.”", "\"하지만 그건 나한테서 얻은 게 아니야.\""], ["Ivan stopped suddenly, seeming struck.", "이반이 무언가에 한 대 맞은 듯 별안간 멈춰 섰다."], ["“That never entered my head, that’s strange.”", "\"그건 내 머릿속에 떠오른 적이 없어, 이상하군.\""]], [["“C’est du nouveau, n’est-ce pas? This time I’ll act honestly and explain to you.", "\"이건 새로운 거지요, 그렇지 않소(C'est du nouveau, n'est-ce pas)?"], ["Listen, in dreams and especially in nightmares, from indigestion or anything, a man sees sometimes such artistic visions, such complex and real actuality, such events, even a whole world of events, woven into such a plot, with such unexpected details from the most exalted matters to the last button on a cuff, as I swear Leo Tolstoy has never invented.", "이번에는 정직하게 처신하면서 당신에게 설명해 주겠소. 들어 보시오, 꿈속에서, 특히 악몽 속에서, 소화불량 때문이든 뭐 때문이든, 사람은 이따금 그토록 예술적인 환상을, 그토록 복잡하고 생생한 현실을, 그토록 무수한 사건들, 그것도 사건들로 이루어진 온 세계를, 그토록 짜임새 있는 줄거리로 엮어 내어, 더없이 숭고한 일들에서부터 소맷부리의 마지막 단추 하나에 이르기까지 그토록 뜻밖의 세부를 곁들여 보는 법인데, 맹세컨대 레프 톨스토이도 그런 건 결코 지어낸 적이 없소."], ["Yet such dreams are sometimes seen not by writers, but by the most ordinary people, officials, journalists, priests....", "한데 그런 꿈을 꾸는 건 작가들이 아니라, 가장 평범한 사람들, 관리들, 신문 기자들, 사제들이란 말이오……"], ["The subject is a complete enigma.", "이건 완전히 수수께끼지요."], ["A statesman confessed to me, indeed, that all his best ideas came to him when he was asleep.", "실제로 어느 정치가가 내게 고백하기를, 자기의 가장 훌륭한 발상은 죄다 잠들었을 때 떠올랐다고 합디다."], ["Well, that’s how it is now, though I am your hallucination, yet just as in a nightmare, I say original things which had not entered your head before.", "그래, 지금이 바로 그런 경우요, 나는 당신의 환각이지만 그래도 악몽 속에서와 꼭 마찬가지로, 전에 당신 머릿속에 떠오른 적 없는 독창적인 것들을 말한단 말이오."], ["So I don’t repeat your ideas, yet I am only your nightmare, nothing more.”", "그러니 나는 당신의 생각을 되풀이하는 게 아니라, 그러면서도 그저 당신의 악몽일 뿐이오, 그 이상은 아무것도 아니지요.\""]], [["“You are lying, your aim is to convince me you exist apart and are not my nightmare, and now you are asserting you are a dream.”", "\"거짓말 마, 당신의 목적은 당신이 따로 존재하는 거지 내 악몽이 아니라고 나를 설득하는 거잖아, 그런데 지금은 당신이 꿈이라고 우기고 있군.\""]], [["“My dear fellow, I’ve adopted a special method to-day, I’ll explain it to you afterwards.", "\"여보게, 나는 오늘 특별한 방법을 택했으니, 나중에 그걸 당신에게 설명하리다."], ["Stay, where did I break off?", "가만, 내가 어디서 말을 끊었더라?"], ["Oh, yes!", "아, 그렇지!"], ["I caught cold then, only not here but yonder.”", "그때 내가 감기에 걸렸는데, 다만 여기가 아니라 저쪽에서였소.\""]], [["“Where is yonder?", "\"저쪽이 어디야?"], ["Tell me, will you be here long?", "말해 봐, 당신 여기 오래 있을 거야?"], ["Can’t you go away?”", "좀 가 줄 수는 없나?\""], ["Ivan exclaimed almost in despair.", "이반이 거의 절망에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["He ceased walking to and fro, sat down on the sofa, leaned his elbows on the table again and held his head tight in both hands.", "그는 이리저리 거닐기를 멈추고 소파에 앉아, 다시 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고서 두 손으로 머리를 단단히 움켜쥐었다."], ["He pulled the wet towel off and flung it away in vexation.", "그는 젖은 수건을 잡아채어 짜증스레 내던졌다."], ["It was evidently of no use.", "그것은 아무 소용도 없는 게 분명했다."]], [["“Your nerves are out of order,”", "\"당신은 신경이 다 망가졌소.\""], ["observed the gentleman, with a carelessly easy, though perfectly polite, air.", "신사가 무심하리만치 편안하면서도 더없이 정중한 태도로 말했다."], ["“You are angry with me even for being able to catch cold, though it happened in a most natural way.", "\"내가 감기에 걸릴 수 있다는 것마저도 당신은 못마땅한 모양인데, 그건 더없이 자연스러운 경위로 일어난 일이라오."], ["I was hurrying then to a diplomatic soirée at the house of a lady of high rank in Petersburg, who was aiming at influence in the Ministry.", "그때 나는 페테르부르크의, 부처(府處)에 영향력을 노리던 어느 지체 높은 부인의 저택에서 열리는 외교 야회에 가느라 서두르고 있었소."], ["Well, an evening suit, white tie, gloves, though I was God knows where and had to fly through space to reach your earth....", "그래, 야회복에 흰 넥타이, 장갑까지 갖춰 입었는데, 정작 그때 내가 있던 곳은 신만이 아는 어디였고, 당신네 지구에 닿으려면 우주 공간을 가로질러 날아와야 했단 말이오……"], ["Of course, it took only an instant, but you know a ray of light from the sun takes full eight minutes, and fancy in an evening suit and open waistcoat.", "물론 그야 눈 깜짝할 새였지만, 그래도 알다시피 태양에서 오는 빛줄기 하나도 꼬박 팔 분이 걸리는데, 야회복에 조끼 앞섶을 풀어 헤친 꼴을 한번 상상해 보시오."], ["Spirits don’t freeze, but when one’s in fleshly form, well ...", "영혼이야 얼지 않지만, 육신의 형상을 걸쳤을 때는, 글쎄……"], ["in brief, I didn’t think, and set off, and you know in those ethereal spaces, in the water that is above the firmament, there’s such a frost ...", "요컨대 나는 미처 생각도 못 하고 길을 나섰는데, 알다시피 그 천공(天空)의 공간이라는 데가, 창공 위에 있는 그 물속이라는 데가, 어찌나 추운지……"], ["at least one can’t call it frost, you can fancy, 150 degrees below zero!", "하긴 추위라고 부를 수도 없을 정도라, 한번 상상해 보시오, 영하 백오십 도라니까!"], ["You know the game the village girls play—they invite the unwary to lick an ax in thirty degrees of frost, the tongue instantly freezes to it and the dupe tears the skin off, so it bleeds.", "시골 처녀들이 하는 장난 알지요, 영하 삼십 도쯤 될 때 멋모르는 녀석을 꾀어 도끼를 핥게 하면, 혀가 그 자리에서 쩍 들러붙고, 속아 넘어간 녀석이 살갗을 뜯어내는 바람에 피가 나잖소."], ["But that’s only in 30 degrees, in 150 degrees I imagine it would be enough to put your finger on the ax and it would be the end of it ...", "한데 그건 삼십 도쯤일 때 얘기고, 백오십 도라면 도끼에 손가락만 갖다 대도 그걸로 끝장날 것 같소……"], ["if only there could be an ax there.”", "하긴 거기에 도끼라는 게 있을 수만 있다면 말이오.\""]], [["“And can there be an ax there?”", "\"그런데 거기에 도끼라는 게 있을 수 있나?\""], ["Ivan interrupted, carelessly and disdainfully.", "이반이 무심하고 경멸하듯 말허리를 잘랐다."], ["He was exerting himself to the utmost not to believe in the delusion and not to sink into complete insanity.", "그는 그 헛것을 믿지 않으려고, 완전한 광기 속으로 가라앉지 않으려고 안간힘을 다하고 있었다."]], [["“An ax?”", "\"도끼라?\""], ["the guest interrupted in surprise.", "손님이 놀라서 되물었다."]], [["“Yes, what would become of an ax there?”", "\"그래, 도끼가 거기서 어떻게 되겠느냐고?\""], ["Ivan cried suddenly, with a sort of savage and insistent obstinacy.", "이반이 별안간 일종의 사납고도 집요한 고집을 부리며 외쳤다."]], [["“What would become of an ax in space? Quelle idée!", "\"우주 공간에서 도끼가 어떻게 되겠느냐고?"], ["If it were to fall to any distance, it would begin, I think, flying round the earth without knowing why, like a satellite.", "거참 별 생각을 다 하는구려(Quelle idée)! 그게 어느 거리만큼 떨어져 나간다면, 내 생각엔 영문도 모른 채 위성처럼 지구 둘레를 빙빙 돌기 시작할 거요."], ["The astronomers would calculate the rising and the setting of the ax, Gatzuk would put it in his calendar, that’s all.”", "천문학자들은 그 도끼가 뜨고 지는 시각을 계산해 내고, 가추크가 그걸 자기 달력에 적어 넣겠지, 그게 다요.\""]], [["“You are stupid, awfully stupid,”", "\"당신은 어리석어, 지독하게 어리석다고.\""], ["said Ivan peevishly.", "이반이 짜증스레 말했다."], ["“Fib more cleverly or I won’t listen.", "\"허풍을 떨려거든 좀 더 영리하게 떨든가, 아니면 안 들을 테야."], ["You want to get the better of me by realism, to convince me that you exist, but I don’t want to believe you exist!", "당신은 사실주의로 나를 이겨 먹으려고, 당신이 존재한다고 나를 설득하려 들지만, 나는 당신이 존재한다는 걸 믿고 싶지 않아!"], ["I won’t believe it!”", "믿지 않겠다고!\""]], [["“But I am not fibbing, it’s all the truth; the truth is unhappily hardly ever amusing.", "\"하지만 나는 허풍을 떠는 게 아니라, 죄다 진실이오. 진실이란 안타깝게도 좀처럼 재미있는 법이 없지."], ["I see you persist in expecting something big of me, and perhaps something fine.", "보아하니 당신은 나한테서 뭔가 거창한 것을, 어쩌면 뭔가 근사한 것을 기대하기를 고집하는군."], ["That’s a great pity, for I only give what I can—”", "그거 참 딱한 일이오, 나는 내가 줄 수 있는 것만 줄 뿐이니까……\""]], [["“Don’t talk philosophy, you ass!”", "\"철학 얘기 하지 마, 이 멍청아!\""]], [["“Philosophy, indeed, when all my right side is numb and I am moaning and groaning.", "\"철학이라니, 내 오른쪽 옆구리가 죄다 마비되어 신음하고 끙끙대는 판에. 의학자란 의학자는 죄다 찾아가 봤소."], ["I’ve tried all the medical faculty: they can diagnose beautifully, they have the whole of your disease at their finger-tips, but they’ve no idea how to cure you.", "그들은 진단은 기막히게 잘하고, 당신 병을 손금 들여다보듯 훤히 꿰뚫지만, 정작 어떻게 고치는지는 깜깜이오. 여기 열성에 찬 어린 의학도가 하나 있었는데, '당신은 죽을지도 모르지요.'"], ["There was an enthusiastic little student here, ‘You may die,’ said he, ‘but you’ll know perfectly what disease you are dying of!’ And then what a way they have sending people to specialists!", "하더니 '하지만 무슨 병으로 죽는지는 완벽하게 아실 겁니다!' 하지 뭐요."], ["‘We only diagnose,’ they say, ‘but go to such-and-such a specialist, he’ll cure you.’ The old doctor who used to cure all sorts of disease has completely disappeared, I assure you, now there are only specialists and they all advertise in the newspapers.", "게다가 사람을 전문의한테 떠넘기는 그 작태라니! '우리는 진단만 합니다.'"], ["If anything is wrong with your nose, they send you to Paris: there, they say, is a European specialist who cures noses.", "하면서 '아무개 전문의한테 가시오, 그가 고쳐 줄 겁니다.' 한다오."], ["If you go to Paris, he’ll look at your nose; I can only cure your right nostril, he’ll tell you, for I don’t cure the left nostril, that’s not my speciality, but go to Vienna, there there’s a specialist who will cure your left nostril.", "온갖 병을 다 고치던 옛날 의사는 이제 자취를 싹 감추었소, 정말이오, 이제는 전문의들밖에 없고 죄다 신문에 광고를 낸다오. 코에 무슨 탈이 났다 하면 당신을 파리로 보내지요, 거기 코를 고치는 유럽 전문의가 있다나."], ["What are you to do? I fell back on popular remedies, a German doctor advised me to rub myself with honey and salt in the bath-house.", "파리에 가면 그 전문의가 당신 코를 들여다보고는, 나는 당신 오른쪽 콧구멍밖에 못 고치오, 왼쪽 콧구멍은 안 고치오, 그건 내 전문이 아니라서, 빈으로 가시오, 거기 당신 왼쪽 콧구멍을 고쳐 줄 전문의가 있소, 이러는 거요."], ["Solely to get an extra bath I went, smeared myself all over and it did me no good at all.", "어쩌겠소? 나는 민간요법에 기댈 수밖에 없었지, 어느 독일 의사가 목욕탕에서 꿀과 소금을 몸에 문질러 보라고 일러 줍디다."], ["In despair I wrote to Count Mattei in Milan.", "목욕이나 한 번 더 하려고 갔다가 온몸에 떡칠을 했는데, 아무 효험도 없었소."], ["He sent me a book and some drops, bless him, and, only fancy, Hoff’s malt extract cured me!", "절망에 빠진 나는 밀라노의 마테이 백작에게 편지를 썼소."], ["I bought it by accident, drank a bottle and a half of it, and I was ready to dance, it took it away completely.", "그가 책 한 권과 물약 몇 방울을 보내 줬는데, 그 양반 복받을지어다, 한데 글쎄, 정작 나를 고쳐 준 건 호프 맥아 진액이었지 뭐요!"], ["I made up my mind to write to the papers to thank him, I was prompted by a feeling of gratitude, and only fancy, it led to no end of a bother: not a single paper would take my letter.", "우연히 사서 한 병 반쯤 마셨더니, 춤이라도 출 만큼 멀쩡해지고 씻은 듯이 나아 버렸소. 나는 고마운 마음에 떠밀려 신문에 편지를 써서 그에게 감사를 표하기로 마음먹었는데, 글쎄, 그게 끝도 없는 골칫거리가 되고 말았소."], ["‘It would be very reactionary,’ they said, ‘no one will believe it.", "어느 신문 하나 내 편지를 실어 주려 하지 않았다오. '그건 무척 반동적인 일이오.'"], ["Le diable n’existe point.", "하면서 '아무도 믿지 않을 거요."], ["You’d better remain anonymous,’ they advised me.", "악마는 존재하지 않으니까(Le diable n'existe point). 그러니 익명으로 남는 편이 낫겠소.'"], ["What use is a letter of thanks if it’s anonymous?", "이렇게 충고합디다. 익명이라면 감사 편지가 무슨 소용이오?"], ["I laughed with the men at the newspaper office; ‘It’s reactionary to believe in God in our days,’ I said, ‘but I am the devil, so I may be believed in.’ ‘We quite understand that,’ they said.", "나는 신문사 사람들과 함께 웃으며 이렇게 말했소, '요즘 세상에 신을 믿는 건 반동적인 일이지만, 나는 악마니까 나쯤은 믿어 줘도 되지 않겠소.' 그러자 그들이 말합디다, '그야 충분히 이해합니다."], ["‘Who doesn’t believe in the devil?", "악마를 안 믿는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?"], ["Yet it won’t do, it might injure our reputation.", "그래도 안 될 일입니다, 우리 명성에 누가 될 수도 있어서요."], ["As a joke, if you like.’ But I thought as a joke it wouldn’t be very witty.", "농담이라면 또 모를까.' 하지만 농담으로 하기엔 그리 재치 있을 것 같지 않더란 말이오."], ["So it wasn’t printed.", "그래서 결국 실리지 못했소."], ["And do you know, I have felt sore about it to this day.", "그런데 그거 아오, 나는 지금까지도 그 일이 못내 서운하다오."], ["My best feelings, gratitude, for instance, are literally denied me simply from my social position.”", "내 가장 고결한 감정, 가령 감사 같은 것조차도, 그저 내 사회적 처지 때문에 글자 그대로 거부당하는 거란 말이오.\""]], [["“Philosophical reflections again?”", "\"또 철학적 사색인가?\""], ["Ivan snarled malignantly.", "이반이 악의에 차 으르렁댔다."]], [["“God preserve me from it, but one can’t help complaining sometimes.", "\"신께서 나를 그런 일에서 지켜 주시기를, 하지만 사람이 어찌 가끔 하소연 한번 못 하겠소."], ["I am a slandered man.", "나는 모함당한 사람이오."], ["You upbraid me every moment with being stupid.", "당신은 시도 때도 없이 나더러 어리석다고 나무라지요."], ["One can see you are young.", "보아하니 당신은 젊군."], ["My dear fellow, intelligence isn’t the only thing!", "여보게, 지성이 다는 아니라오!"], ["I have naturally a kind and merry heart.", "나는 본디 다정하고 명랑한 마음을 타고났소."], ["‘I also write vaudevilles of all sorts.’ You seem to take me for Hlestakov grown old, but my fate is a far more serious one.", "'나도 온갖 보드빌을 쓰는 사람이오.' 당신은 나를 늙어 버린 흘레스타코프쯤으로 여기는 모양이지만, 내 운명은 그보다 훨씬 더 심각한 거요."], ["Before time was, by some decree which I could never make out, I was pre-destined ‘to deny’ and yet I am genuinely good-hearted and not at all inclined to negation.", "태초가 있기도 전에, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 명령에 따라, 나는 '부정하도록' 예정되었소, 그러면서도 나는 진심으로 마음씨가 곱고 부정과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 자란 말이오."], ["‘No, you must go and deny, without denial there’s no criticism and what would a journal be without a column of criticism?’ Without criticism it would be nothing but one ‘hosannah.’ But nothing but hosannah is not enough for life, the hosannah must be tried in the crucible of doubt and so on, in the same style.", "'아니, 너는 가서 부정해야 한다, 부정이 없으면 비판도 없는 법이거늘, 비판 난(欄)이 없는 잡지가 무슨 잡지란 말이냐?' 비판이 없으면 그저 '호산나'"], ["But I don’t meddle in that, I didn’t create it, I am not answerable for it.", "한마디뿐일 거요. 하지만 삶에는 호산나 하나만으로는 충분치 않으니, 그 호산나는 의심의 도가니에서 단련되어야 하고, 그런 식으로 계속되는 거지요."], ["Well, they’ve chosen their scapegoat, they’ve made me write the column of criticism and so life was made possible.", "하지만 나는 거기에 끼어들지 않소, 내가 만든 것도 아니고, 나한테 책임이 있는 것도 아니니까. 그래, 그들은 속죄양을 골라 나더러 비판 난을 쓰게 만들었고, 그리하여 삶이 가능해진 거요."], ["We understand that comedy; I, for instance, simply ask for annihilation.", "우리는 그 희극을 이해하오. 가령 나는 그저 소멸을 청하지요."], ["No, live, I am told, for there’d be nothing without you.", "그러나 안 된다, 살아라, 너 없이는 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 테니, 이렇게 명령받는단 말이오."], ["If everything in the universe were sensible, nothing would happen.", "만일 우주의 모든 것이 사리에 맞는다면, 아무 일도 일어나지 않을 거요."], ["There would be no events without you, and there must be events.", "당신이 없으면 아무런 사건도 없을 텐데, 사건은 반드시 있어야 하니까."], ["So against the grain I serve to produce events and do what’s irrational because I am commanded to.", "그래서 나는 비위에 거슬리면서도 사건을 일으키는 데 봉사하며, 명령받은 대로 비합리적인 짓을 하는 거요."], ["For all their indisputable intelligence, men take this farce as something serious, and that is their tragedy.", "사람들은 그 의심할 바 없는 지성에도 불구하고 이 광대극을 무언가 심각한 것으로 받아들이는데, 바로 그게 그들의 비극이오."], ["They suffer, of course ...", "물론 그들은 괴로워하지요……"], ["but then they live, they live a real life, not a fantastic one, for suffering is life.", "하지만 그래도 그들은 살아가오, 환상이 아닌 진짜 삶을 살아가지요, 고통이야말로 삶이니까."], ["Without suffering what would be the pleasure of it?", "고통이 없다면 무슨 즐거움이 있겠소?"], ["It would be transformed into an endless church service; it would be holy, but tedious.", "그건 끝도 없는 예배로 변해 버릴 거요, 거룩하긴 하나 따분하기 짝이 없는."], ["But what about me?", "한데 나는 어떻소?"], ["I suffer, but still, I don’t live.", "나는 괴로워하지만, 그래도 살지는 못한단 말이오."], ["I am x in an indeterminate equation.", "나는 부정방정식 속의 x요."], ["I am a sort of phantom in life who has lost all beginning and end, and who has even forgotten his own name.", "나는 모든 시작도 끝도 잃어버린, 심지어 제 이름조차 잊어버린, 삶 속의 일종의 허깨비란 말이오."], ["You are laughing— no, you are not laughing, you are angry again.", "당신은 웃는군. 아니, 웃는 게 아니라 또 화가 났구려."], ["You are for ever angry, all you care about is intelligence, but I repeat again that I would give away all this super-stellar life, all the ranks and honors, simply to be transformed into the soul of a merchant’s wife weighing eighteen stone and set candles at God’s shrine.”", "당신은 늘 화만 내지, 당신이 신경 쓰는 건 오로지 지성뿐이지만, 다시 한번 말하건대, 나는 이 모든 별 너머의 삶을, 온갖 관등과 영예를 죄다 내던지고서라도, 그저 백팔십 근 나가는 어느 상인 마누라의 영혼으로 변신하여 신의 제단에 촛불을 바칠 수만 있다면 좋겠소.\""]], [["“Then even you don’t believe in God?”", "\"그럼 당신조차도 신을 믿지 않는단 말인가?\""], ["said Ivan, with a smile of hatred.", "이반이 증오에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“What can I say?—that is, if you are in earnest—”", "\"내가 뭐라 답할 수 있겠소? 그러니까, 당신이 진지하게 묻는 거라면 말이오……\""]], [["“Is there a God or not?”", "\"신이 있어, 없어?\""], ["Ivan cried with the same savage intensity.", "이반이 여전히 사납고 격렬한 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah, then you are in earnest!", "\"아, 그럼 당신 진지하게 묻는 거구려!"], ["My dear fellow, upon my word I don’t know.", "여보게, 정말이지 나도 모르오."], ["There!", "자!"], ["I’ve said it now!”", "이제 말해 버렸소!\""]], [["“You don’t know, but you see God?", "\"모른다면서 신을 본다고?"], ["No, you are not some one apart, you are myself, you are I and nothing more!", "아니, 당신은 따로 떨어진 존재가 아니야, 당신은 나 자신이고, 당신은 나, 그 이상도 아무것도 아니야!"], ["You are rubbish, you are my fancy!”", "당신은 쓰레기야, 당신은 내 망상이라고!\""]], [["“Well, if you like, I have the same philosophy as you, that would be true.", "\"글쎄, 정 원한다면, 나도 당신과 같은 철학을 지녔다고 해 두지요, 그게 사실일 테니."], ["Je pense, donc je suis, I know that for a fact; all the rest, all these worlds, God and even Satan—all that is not proved, to my mind.", "나는 생각한다, 고로 존재한다(Je pense, donc je suis), 그것만큼은 나도 확실히 아오. 나머지 전부, 이 모든 세계며, 신이며, 심지어 사탄까지도, 내가 보기엔 죄다 증명되지 않은 것들이오."], ["Does all that exist of itself, or is it only an emanation of myself, a logical development of my ego which alone has existed for ever: but I make haste to stop, for I believe you will be jumping up to beat me directly.”", "그 모든 게 스스로 존재하는 것인지, 아니면 그저 나 자신의 발현, 영원토록 홀로 존재해 온 내 자아의 논리적 전개일 뿐인지…… 하지만 여기서 서둘러 그만두겠소, 안 그러면 당신이 당장 벌떡 일어나 나를 두들겨 팰 것 같으니.\""]], [["“You’d better tell me some anecdote!”", "\"차라리 무슨 일화나 들려주는 게 낫겠어!\""], ["said Ivan miserably.", "이반이 비참하게 말했다."]], [["“There is an anecdote precisely on our subject, or rather a legend, not an anecdote.", "\"꼭 우리 화제에 들어맞는 일화가 하나 있긴 하오, 일화라기보다 전설이라고 해야겠지만."], ["You reproach me with unbelief, you see, you say, yet you don’t believe.", "알다시피 당신은 불신을 나무라며, 그러면서도 너는 믿지 않는다고 하잖소."], ["But, my dear fellow, I am not the only one like that.", "하지만 여보게, 그런 건 나 혼자만이 아니라오."], ["We are all in a muddle over there now and all through your science.", "우리는 지금 저쪽에서 다들 뒤죽박죽이 되어 있는데, 죄다 당신네 과학 탓이오."], ["Once there used to be atoms, five senses, four elements, and then everything hung together somehow.", "한때는 원자가 있고, 오감이 있고, 네 가지 원소가 있어서, 만사가 그럭저럭 들어맞았소."], ["There were atoms in the ancient world even, but since we’ve learned that you’ve discovered the chemical molecule and protoplasm and the devil knows what, we had to lower our crest.", "원자야 고대 세계에도 있었지. 한데 당신네가 화학 분자니 원형질이니 악마나 알 법한 것들을 발견했다는 걸 우리가 알게 된 다음부터는, 우리도 콧대를 꺾을 수밖에 없었소."], ["There’s a regular muddle, and, above all, superstition, scandal; there’s as much scandal among us as among you, you know; a little more in fact, and spying, indeed, for we have our secret police department where private information is received.", "영락없는 뒤죽박죽이고, 무엇보다도 미신이며 추문이 판을 치오. 우리한테도 당신네 못지않게 추문이 들끓는다오, 알다시피, 사실 좀 더 많다고 해야겠지, 게다가 염탐질까지 있소, 정말로, 우리한테는 사사로운 정보를 받아 챙기는 비밀경찰 부서까지 있으니까."], ["Well, this wild legend belongs to our middle ages—not yours, but ours—and no one believes it even among us, except the old ladies of eighteen stone, not your old ladies I mean, but ours.", "그래, 이 황당한 전설은 우리 중세에 속한 것인데, 당신네 중세가 아니라 우리 중세 말이오, 우리들 사이에서조차 아무도 믿지 않는 거요, 백팔십 근 나가는 노파들이나 믿을까, 당신네 노파들이 아니라 우리 노파들 말이오."], ["We’ve everything you have, I am revealing one of our secrets out of friendship for you; though it’s forbidden.", "당신네한테 있는 건 우리한테도 죄다 있소, 당신과의 우정을 봐서 우리 비밀 하나를 누설하는 거요, 금지된 일이긴 하지만."], ["This legend is about Paradise.", "이 전설은 천국에 관한 거요."], ["There was, they say, here on earth a thinker and philosopher.", "듣자 하니, 이 땅에 어떤 사상가요 철학자가 하나 있었다 합디다."], ["He rejected everything, ‘laws, conscience, faith,’ and, above all, the future life.", "그자는 모든 것을 부정했소, '법도, 양심도, 신앙도' 죄다, 그중에서도 특히 내세를 부정했지요."], ["He died; he expected to go straight to darkness and death and he found a future life before him.", "그자가 죽었소. 그자는 곧장 어둠과 죽음 속으로 가리라 여겼는데, 정작 눈앞에 내세가 펼쳐진 거요."], ["He was astounded and indignant.", "그자는 아연실색하며 분개했소."], ["‘This is against my principles!’ he said.", "'이건 내 원칙에 어긋난다!' 이렇게 말했지요."], ["And he was punished for that ...", "그래서 그자는 벌을 받았소……"], ["that is, you must excuse me, I am just repeating what I heard myself, it’s only a legend ...", "그러니까, 미안하오만, 나는 그저 내가 들은 걸 그대로 옮기는 것뿐이오, 어디까지나 전설이니까……"], ["he was sentenced to walk a quadrillion kilometers in the dark (we’ve adopted the metric system, you know) and when he has finished that quadrillion, the gates of heaven would be opened to him and he’ll be forgiven—”", "그자는 어둠 속을 백경(百京) 킬로미터 걷도록 선고받았소(알다시피 우리는 미터법을 채택했거든), 그래서 그 백경 킬로미터를 다 걷고 나면 천국의 문이 그자에게 열리고 그자는 용서받게 되리라는 거였소……\""]], [["“And what tortures have you in the other world besides the quadrillion kilometers?”", "\"그럼 그 백경 킬로미터 말고 저세상에 또 무슨 고문이 있지?\""], ["asked Ivan, with a strange eagerness.", "이반이 묘한 열의를 띠고 물었다."]], [["“What tortures?", "\"무슨 고문이냐고?"], ["Ah, don’t ask.", "아, 묻지 마시오."], ["In old days we had all sorts, but now they have taken chiefly to moral punishments—‘the stings of conscience’ and all that nonsense.", "옛날에는 별의별 게 다 있었지만, 이제는 주로 도덕적 형벌, '양심의 가책'이니 뭐니 하는 그런 헛소리로 옮겨 갔소."], ["We got that, too, from you, from the softening of your manners.", "그것도 당신네한테서, 당신네 풍속이 물러진 데서 얻어 온 거요."], ["And who’s the better for it?", "한데 그래서 누가 덕을 본단 말이오?"], ["Only those who have got no conscience, for how can they be tortured by conscience when they have none?", "그저 양심이라곤 없는 자들이나 덕을 보지, 양심이 없는데 어떻게 양심으로 고통받겠소?"], ["But decent people who have conscience and a sense of honor suffer for it.", "하지만 양심과 명예심이 있는 점잖은 사람들은 그것 때문에 괴로워한다오."], ["Reforms, when the ground has not been prepared for them, especially if they are institutions copied from abroad, do nothing but mischief!", "토양이 마련되지도 않았는데 들이미는 개혁이란, 특히 외국에서 베껴 온 제도라는 것은, 해악밖에 끼치지 못하오!"], ["The ancient fire was better.", "옛날의 불(火)이 차라리 나았소."], ["Well, this man, who was condemned to the quadrillion kilometers, stood still, looked round and lay down across the road.", "그래, 백경 킬로미터를 선고받은 이 사내는, 우뚝 멈춰 서서 둘레를 둘러보더니 길 한복판을 가로질러 벌렁 드러눕고 말았소."], ["‘I won’t go, I refuse on principle!’ Take the soul of an enlightened Russian atheist and mix it with the soul of the prophet Jonah, who sulked for three days and nights in the belly of the whale, and you get the character of that thinker who lay across the road.”", "'안 가겠다, 원칙적으로 거부한다!' 어느 계몽된 러시아 무신론자의 영혼을, 고래 뱃속에서 사흘 밤낮을 뾰로통해 있던 예언자 요나의 영혼과 뒤섞어 보시오, 그러면 길을 가로질러 드러누운 그 사상가의 성격이 나오는 거요.\""]], [["“What did he lie on there?”", "\"그자는 거기서 무엇 위에 드러누웠지?\""]], [["“Well, I suppose there was something to lie on.", "\"글쎄, 드러누울 만한 무언가가 있었겠지요."], ["You are not laughing?”", "안 웃는구려?\""]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"브라보!\""], ["cried Ivan, still with the same strange eagerness.", "이반이 여전히 그 묘한 열의를 띤 채 외쳤다."], ["Now he was listening with an unexpected curiosity.", "이제 그는 뜻밖의 호기심을 품고 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["“Well, is he lying there now?”", "\"그래, 그자는 지금도 거기 드러누워 있나?\""]], [["“That’s the point, that he isn’t.", "\"바로 그게 요점이오, 그자는 그러고 있지 않다는 거."], ["He lay there almost a thousand years and then he got up and went on.”", "그자는 거의 천 년을 거기 드러누워 있다가, 결국 일어나 다시 걷기 시작했소.\""]], [["“What an ass!”", "\"이런 멍청이!\""], ["cried Ivan, laughing nervously and still seeming to be pondering something intently.", "이반이 신경질적으로 웃으면서도 여전히 무언가를 골똘히 곱씹는 듯 외쳤다."], ["“Does it make any difference whether he lies there for ever or walks the quadrillion kilometers?", "\"영영 거기 드러누워 있는 거나 백경 킬로미터를 걷는 거나 무슨 차이가 있겠어?"], ["It would take a billion years to walk it?”", "그걸 걸으려면 십억 년은 걸릴 텐데?\""]], [["“Much more than that.", "\"그보다 훨씬 더 걸리지."], ["I haven’t got a pencil and paper or I could work it out.", "연필과 종이가 있으면 계산해 볼 수 있을 텐데."], ["But he got there long ago, and that’s where the story begins.”", "하지만 그자는 벌써 오래전에 도착했고, 바로 거기서 이야기가 시작되는 거요.\""]], [["“What, he got there?", "\"뭐, 그자가 도착했다고?"], ["But how did he get the billion years to do it?”", "한데 어떻게 그걸 다 걸을 십억 년을 얻었지?\""]], [["“Why, you keep thinking of our present earth!", "\"아니, 당신은 자꾸 우리의 지금 이 지구만 생각하는군!"], ["But our present earth may have been repeated a billion times.", "하지만 우리의 지금 이 지구도 어쩌면 십억 번이나 되풀이됐을지 모르오."], ["Why, it’s become extinct, been frozen; cracked, broken to bits, disintegrated into its elements, again ‘the water above the firmament,’ then again a comet, again a sun, again from the sun it becomes earth—and the same sequence may have been repeated endlessly and exactly the same to every detail, most unseemly and insufferably tedious—”", "아니, 이 지구는 다 타 버리고, 얼어붙고, 쩍쩍 갈라지고, 산산조각 나고, 제 원소로 분해되었다가, 다시 '창공 위의 물'이 되고, 그다음엔 또 혜성이 되고, 또 태양이 되고, 또 태양에서 지구가 되고, 그렇게 똑같은 순서가 끝없이, 그것도 세세한 데까지 토씨 하나 다르지 않게, 더없이 꼴사납고 견딜 수 없으리만치 따분하게 되풀이됐을지도 모른단 말이오……\""]], [["“Well, well, what happened when he arrived?”", "\"그래, 그래, 그자가 도착했을 땐 무슨 일이 벌어졌지?\""]], [["“Why, the moment the gates of Paradise were open and he walked in, before he had been there two seconds, by his watch (though to my thinking his watch must have long dissolved into its elements on the way), he cried out that those two seconds were worth walking not a quadrillion kilometers but a quadrillion of quadrillions, raised to the quadrillionth power!", "\"아니, 천국의 문이 열려 그자가 안으로 걸어 들어간 순간, 거기 머문 지 채 이 초도 되기 전에, 그자는 제 회중시계를 보고는(하긴 내 생각엔 그자의 시계는 오는 길에 진작 제 원소로 녹아 버렸겠지만) 그 이 초야말로 백경 킬로미터가 아니라 백경의 백경 곱하기에 다시 백경 제곱을 한 만큼을 걸을 가치가 있다고 외쳤소!"], ["In fact, he sang ‘hosannah’ and overdid it so, that some persons there of lofty ideas wouldn’t shake hands with him at first—he’d become too rapidly reactionary, they said.", "사실 그자는 '호산나'를 불렀는데, 어찌나 도가 지나쳤던지, 거기 있던 고매한 사상을 지닌 몇몇이 처음엔 그자와 악수조차 하려 들지 않을 정도였소. 그자가 너무 빨리 반동으로 돌아섰다나, 이렇게들 말하면서."], ["The Russian temperament.", "러시아 기질이지."], ["I repeat, it’s a legend.", "거듭 말하지만 이건 전설이오."], ["I give it for what it’s worth.", "들은 그대로 전하는 거요."], ["So that’s the sort of ideas we have on such subjects even now.”", "그러니 이런 문제에 관해선 우리도 지금까지 그런 식의 생각을 품고 있는 셈이지요.\""]], [["“I’ve caught you!”", "\"이제 당신 꼬리를 잡았어!\""], ["Ivan cried, with an almost childish delight, as though he had succeeded in remembering something at last.", "이반이 마침내 무언가를 떠올리는 데 성공하기라도 한 듯 거의 어린아이 같은 환희에 차 외쳤다."], ["“That anecdote about the quadrillion years, I made up myself!", "\"그 백경 년 얘긴 내가 직접 지어낸 거라고!"], ["I was seventeen then, I was at the high school.", "그땐 내가 열일곱 살이었고, 고등학교에 다니고 있었지."], ["I made up that anecdote and told it to a schoolfellow called Korovkin, it was at Moscow....", "내가 그 일화를 지어내서 코로프킨이라는 학교 친구한테 들려줬어, 모스크바에서 있었던 일이지……"], ["The anecdote is so characteristic that I couldn’t have taken it from anywhere.", "그 일화는 어찌나 특이한지 내가 어디서 가져왔을 리가 없어."], ["I thought I’d forgotten it ...", "까맣게 잊은 줄 알았는데……"], ["but I’ve unconsciously recalled it—I recalled it myself—it was not you telling it!", "나도 모르게 그게 떠오른 거야, 내가 혼자 떠올린 거라고, 당신이 들려준 게 아니야!"], ["Thousands of things are unconsciously remembered like that even when people are being taken to execution ...", "사람들은 사형장에 끌려갈 때조차 그렇게 무수한 것들을 무의식중에 떠올리곤 하지……"], ["it’s come back to me in a dream.", "그게 꿈속에서 다시 떠오른 거야."], ["You are that dream!", "당신이 바로 그 꿈이야!"], ["You are a dream, not a living creature!”", "당신은 꿈이지, 살아 있는 존재가 아니라고!\""]], [["“From the vehemence with which you deny my existence,”", "\"당신이 내 존재를 부정하는 그 격렬함을 보니,\""], ["laughed the gentleman, “I am convinced that you believe in me.”", "신사가 웃었다. \"당신이 나를 믿고 있다는 확신이 드오.\""]], [["“Not in the slightest!", "\"눈곱만큼도 안 믿어!"], ["I haven’t a hundredth part of a grain of faith in you!”", "당신에 대한 믿음이라곤 알갱이 하나의 백분의 일도 없단 말이야!\""]], [["“But you have the thousandth of a grain.", "\"하지만 천분의 일은 있군."], ["Homeopathic doses perhaps are the strongest.", "동종요법 약은 어쩌면 희석할수록 더 강해지는 법이지요."], ["Confess that you have faith even to the ten-thousandth of a grain.”", "솔직히 인정해 보시오, 알갱이 하나의 만분의 일만큼은 믿음이 있다고.\""]], [["“Not for one minute,”", "\"단 한순간도 없어.\""], ["cried Ivan furiously.", "이반이 광분하여 외쳤다."], ["“But I should like to believe in you,”", "\"하지만 당신을 믿고 싶기는 해.\""], ["he added strangely.", "그가 묘하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["There’s an admission!", "자백이 나왔군!"], ["But I am good-natured.", "하지만 나는 마음씨가 곱지요."], ["I’ll come to your assistance again.", "다시 한번 당신을 도와주겠소."], ["Listen, it was I caught you, not you me.", "들어 보시오, 당신을 붙잡은 건 나지 당신이 나를 붙잡은 게 아니오."], ["I told you your anecdote you’d forgotten, on purpose, so as to destroy your faith in me completely.”", "당신이 까맣게 잊은 그 일화를 내가 일부러 들려준 거요, 나에 대한 당신의 믿음을 완전히 깨부수려고 말이오.\""]], [["“You are lying.", "\"거짓말이야."], ["The object of your visit is to convince me of your existence!”", "당신이 찾아온 목적은 당신이 존재한다고 나를 설득하는 거잖아!\""]], [["“Just so.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["But hesitation, suspense, conflict between belief and disbelief—is sometimes such torture to a conscientious man, such as you are, that it’s better to hang oneself at once.", "하지만 망설임, 미결, 믿음과 불신 사이의 갈등이라는 것은, 당신 같은 양심적인 인간에게는 이따금 차라리 당장 목을 매는 편이 나을 만큼 큰 고문이 되지요."], ["Knowing that you are inclined to believe in me, I administered some disbelief by telling you that anecdote.", "당신이 나를 믿는 쪽으로 기우는 걸 알았기에, 나는 그 일화를 들려주어 불신을 좀 처방한 거요."], ["I lead you to belief and disbelief by turns, and I have my motive in it.", "나는 당신을 믿음과 불신 사이로 번갈아 이끄는데, 거기엔 내 나름의 동기가 있소."], ["It’s the new method.", "이게 바로 그 새로운 방법이오."], ["As soon as you disbelieve in me completely, you’ll begin assuring me to my face that I am not a dream but a reality.", "당신이 나를 완전히 불신하게 되는 즉시, 당신은 내 면전에 대고 내가 꿈이 아니라 실재라고 우기기 시작할 거요."], ["I know you.", "나는 당신을 알지."], ["Then I shall have attained my object, which is an honorable one.", "그러면 나는 내 목적을 이루는 셈인데, 그건 명예로운 목적이오."], ["I shall sow in you only a tiny grain of faith and it will grow into an oak-tree—and such an oak-tree that, sitting on it, you will long to enter the ranks of ‘the hermits in the wilderness and the saintly women,’ for that is what you are secretly longing for.", "나는 당신 안에 그저 자그마한 믿음의 알갱이 하나를 심어 둘 거요, 그러면 그게 떡갈나무로 자라날 거고, 그것도 어찌나 큰 떡갈나무가 될지, 당신은 그 위에 올라앉아 '광야의 은수자들과 거룩한 여인들'의 대열에 끼어들기를 갈망하게 될 거요, 그게 바로 당신이 남몰래 갈망하는 거니까."], ["You’ll dine on locusts, you’ll wander into the wilderness to save your soul!”", "당신은 메뚜기로 끼니를 때우고, 영혼을 구원하겠다고 광야로 떠돌아 나설 거란 말이오!\""]], [["“Then it’s for the salvation of my soul you are working, is it, you scoundrel?”", "\"그럼 당신이 애쓰는 게 내 영혼의 구원을 위해서라는 거냐, 이 악당아?\""]], [["“One must do a good work sometimes.", "\"사람이 가끔은 선한 일도 해야지요."], ["How ill-humored you are!”", "당신은 어찌 그리 심사가 뒤틀렸소!\""]], [["“Fool!", "\"바보 같으니!"], ["did you ever tempt those holy men who ate locusts and prayed seventeen years in the wilderness till they were overgrown with moss?”", "당신이 언제 그 거룩한 사람들을, 메뚜기를 먹으며 온몸이 이끼로 뒤덮이도록 광야에서 십칠 년이나 기도하던 그들을 유혹해 본 적이라도 있어?\""]], [["“My dear fellow, I’ve done nothing else.", "\"여보게, 나는 그 짓밖에 안 했소."], ["One forgets the whole world and all the worlds, and sticks to one such saint, because he is a very precious diamond.", "사람은 온 세상을, 모든 세계를 다 잊어버리고서 그런 성자 하나에게 들러붙는 법이오, 그자가 더없이 귀한 다이아몬드니까."], ["One such soul, you know, is sometimes worth a whole constellation.", "알다시피 그런 영혼 하나가 이따금 별자리 하나 전체만큼의 값어치가 있단 말이오. 우리한테는 우리 나름의 계산법이 있거든."], ["We have our system of reckoning, you know. The conquest is priceless!", "그런 자를 정복하면 그건 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀한 거요!"], ["And some of them, on my word, are not inferior to you in culture, though you won’t believe it.", "게다가 그중 더러는, 정말이지, 당신은 안 믿겠지만, 교양으로 따져도 당신한테 결코 뒤지지 않소."], ["They can contemplate such depths of belief and disbelief at the same moment that sometimes it really seems that they are within a hair’s-breadth of being ‘turned upside down,’ as the actor Gorbunov says.”", "그들은 같은 순간에 그토록 깊은 믿음과 불신을 동시에 응시할 수 있어서, 이따금 배우 고르부노프 말마따나 정말이지 머리카락 한 올 차이로 '뒤집어질' 지경에 이른단 말이오.\""]], [["“Well, did you get your nose pulled?”[8]", "\"그래서, 당신 코는 잡아당겨졌나?\""]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "* 코를 잡아당기다: 속아 넘어가다, 우롱당하다를 뜻하는 러시아어 관용 표현. \"여보게,\""], ["observed the visitor sententiously, “it’s better to get off with your nose pulled than without a nose at all.", "손님이 경구를 읊듯 말했다. \"코가 잡아당겨지는 게 코를 통째로 잃는 것보다야 낫지요."], ["As an afflicted marquis observed not long ago (he must have been treated by a specialist) in confession to his spiritual father—a Jesuit.", "얼마 전에 어느 가엾은 후작이(그자는 분명 전문의한테 치료를 받은 모양이오) 자기 영적 아버지인 예수회 신부에게 고해성사를 하면서 한 말이오. 나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 정말이지 매력적이었소."], ["I was present, it was simply charming.", "'제 코를 돌려주십시오!'"], ["‘Give me back my nose!’ he said, and he beat his breast.", "그자가 이러면서 제 가슴을 쳤소. '내 아들아,'"], ["‘My son,’ said the priest evasively, ‘all things are accomplished in accordance with the inscrutable decrees of Providence, and what seems a misfortune sometimes leads to extraordinary, though unapparent, benefits.", "신부가 얼버무리며 말합디다, '만물은 헤아릴 수 없는 섭리의 명령에 따라 이루어지는 법이며, 불행처럼 보이는 일도 이따금 비범한, 비록 눈에 띄지는 않으나, 은혜로 이어진단다. 모진 운명이 그대에게서 코를 앗아 갔다면, 그건 이제 아무도 그대의 코를 잡아당길 수 없으니 도리어 그대에게 이로운 일이니라.'"], ["If stern destiny has deprived you of your nose, it’s to your advantage that no one can ever pull you by your nose.’ ‘Holy father, that’s no comfort,’ cried the despairing marquis.", "'거룩한 신부님, 그건 위로가 안 됩니다.' 절망에 빠진 후작이 외칩디다."], ["‘I’d be delighted to have my nose pulled every day of my life, if it were only in its proper place.’ ‘My son,’ sighs the priest, ‘you can’t expect every blessing at once.", "'제자리에 붙어만 있다면, 저는 평생 날마다 코를 잡아당겨져도 기꺼이 좋겠습니다.' '내 아들아,'"], ["This is murmuring against Providence, who even in this has not forgotten you, for if you repine as you repined just now, declaring you’d be glad to have your nose pulled for the rest of your life, your desire has already been fulfilled indirectly, for when you lost your nose, you were led by the nose.’”", "신부가 한숨을 쉬며 말합디다, '모든 축복을 한꺼번에 바랄 수는 없는 법이니라. 이는 섭리에 대한 불평이로다, 섭리는 이 일에 있어서조차 그대를 잊지 않으셨거늘, 그대가 방금 그러했듯이 평생 코를 잡아당겨져도 기쁘겠노라 외치며 불평한다면, 그대의 소원은 이미 에둘러 이루어진 셈이니, 그대가 코를 잃었을 때 그대는 이미 코가 꿰여 끌려다닌 것이기 때문이니라.'\""]], [["“Fool, how stupid!”", "\"바보 같으니, 무슨 그런 어처구니없는 소릴!\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."]], [["“My dear friend, I only wanted to amuse you.", "\"여보게, 나는 그저 당신을 즐겁게 해 주고 싶었을 뿐이오."], ["But I swear that’s the genuine Jesuit casuistry and I swear that it all happened word for word as I’ve told you.", "하지만 맹세컨대 그게 바로 진짜 예수회식 궤변이고, 또 맹세컨대 모든 게 내가 들려준 그대로 토씨 하나 다르지 않게 일어난 일이오."], ["It happened lately and gave me a great deal of trouble.", "얼마 전에 벌어진 일인데, 나한테 적잖은 골칫거리를 안겨 주었지."], ["The unhappy young man shot himself that very night when he got home.", "그 가엾은 청년은 집에 돌아온 바로 그날 밤 권총으로 자살했소."], ["I was by his side till the very last moment.", "나는 마지막 순간까지 그자 곁을 지켰지."], ["Those Jesuit confessionals are really my most delightful diversion at melancholy moments.", "그 예수회 고해소야말로 우울할 때 나의 가장 즐거운 소일거리라오."], ["Here’s another incident that happened only the other day.", "여기 바로 며칠 전에 벌어진 또 다른 사건이 있소."], ["A little blonde Norman girl of twenty—a buxom, unsophisticated beauty that would make your mouth water—comes to an old priest.", "스무 살 난 자그마한 금발의 노르망디 처녀가―군침이 절로 돌 만큼 풍만하고 순진한 미인이오―늙은 신부를 찾아왔소. 그녀가 몸을 굽히고 격자창에 대고 자기 죄를 속삭였소."], ["She bends down and whispers her sin into the grating.", "'아니, 내 딸아, 벌써 또 타락했단 말이냐?' 신부가 외칩디다."], ["‘Why, my daughter, have you fallen again already?’ cries the priest.", "'오 거룩하신 마리아여, 이게 무슨 소린가! 이번엔 같은 사내도 아니라니, 대체 이게 언제까지 계속될 셈이냐?"], ["‘O Sancta Maria, what do I hear!", "부끄럽지도 않으냐!'"], ["Not the same man this time, how long is this going on?", "'아, 신부님(Ah, mon père),'"], ["Aren’t you ashamed!’ ‘Ah, mon père,’ answers the sinner with tears of penitence, ‘ça lui fait tant de plaisir, et à moi si peu de peine!’ Fancy, such an answer!", "죄지은 여자가 참회의 눈물을 흘리며 대답합디다, '그분께는 그토록 큰 기쁨이 되고, 저한테는 그토록 작은 괴로움일 뿐인걸요(ça lui fait tant de plaisir, et à moi si peu de peine)!' 상상해 보시오, 그런 대답이라니!"], ["I drew back.", "나는 흠칫 물러섰소."], ["It was the cry of nature, better than innocence itself, if you like.", "그건 자연의 외침이었고, 굳이 말하자면 순결 그 자체보다도 더 나은 것이었소."], ["I absolved her sin on the spot and was turning to go, but I was forced to turn back.", "나는 그 자리에서 그녀의 죄를 사해 주고 돌아서 나오려 했는데, 다시 발길을 돌릴 수밖에 없었소."], ["I heard the priest at the grating making an appointment with her for the evening—though he was an old man hard as flint, he fell in an instant!", "격자창 너머로 그 신부가 그녀와 저녁에 만날 약속을 잡는 소리가 들렸으니 말이오―부싯돌처럼 굳은 늙은이였건만, 순식간에 넘어가 버린 거요!"], ["It was nature, the truth of nature asserted its rights!", "그게 자연이었소, 자연의 진리가 제 권리를 주장한 거지!"], ["What, you are turning up your nose again?", "뭐요, 또 코를 찡긋거리는구려? 또 화가 났소?"], ["Angry again? I don’t know how to please you—”", "도무지 당신 비위를 어떻게 맞춰야 할지 모르겠군……\""]], [["“Leave me alone, you are beating on my brain like a haunting nightmare,”", "\"날 좀 내버려 둬, 당신은 따라다니는 악몽처럼 내 머릿속을 두드려 대고 있어.\""], ["Ivan moaned miserably, helpless before his apparition.", "이반이 제 환영 앞에서 속수무책으로 비참하게 신음했다."], ["“I am bored with you, agonizingly and insufferably.", "\"당신한텐 진절머리가 나, 괴롭고 견딜 수 없을 만큼."], ["I would give anything to be able to shake you off!”", "당신을 떨쳐 낼 수만 있다면 무엇이든 다 내주겠어!\""]], [["“I repeat, moderate your expectations, don’t demand of me ‘everything great and noble’ and you’ll see how well we shall get on,” said the gentleman impressively.", "\"거듭 말하지만, 기대를 좀 누그러뜨리시오, 나한테 '위대하고 고귀한 것'을 다 내놓으라 하지 마시오, 그러면 우리가 얼마나 잘 지낼 수 있는지 알게 될 거요.\""], ["“You are really angry with me for not having appeared to you in a red glow, with thunder and lightning, with scorched wings, but have shown myself in such a modest form.", "신사가 인상 깊게 말했다. \"당신은 내가 천둥 번개를 거느리고 날개를 그을린 채 시뻘건 불빛 속에서 나타나지 않고, 이렇게 수수한 모습으로 나타났다고 정말로 화가 난 거요."], ["You are wounded, in the first place, in your esthetic feelings, and, secondly, in your pride.", "첫째로 당신은 미적 감정에 상처를 입었고, 둘째로 자존심에 상처를 입은 거지요."], ["How could such a vulgar devil visit such a great man as you!", "어떻게 이렇게 천박한 악마가 당신처럼 위대한 인물을 찾아올 수 있느냔 말이오!"], ["Yes, there is that romantic strain in you, that was so derided by Byelinsky.", "그래, 당신한테는 그 낭만적인 기질이 있소, 벨린스키가 그토록 비웃던 그 기질 말이오."], ["I can’t help it, young man, as I got ready to come to you I did think as a joke of appearing in the figure of a retired general who had served in the Caucasus, with a star of the Lion and the Sun on my coat.", "어쩔 수 없지요, 젊은이, 당신을 찾아오려고 채비하면서 나도 농담 삼아 카프카스에서 복무한 퇴역 장군의 모습으로, 외투에 사자와 태양 훈장을 단 채 나타날까 생각해 봤소."], ["But I was positively afraid of doing it, for you’d have thrashed me for daring to pin the Lion and the Sun on my coat, instead of, at least, the Polar Star or the Sirius.", "하지만 그러기엔 정말이지 겁이 났소, 사자와 태양 훈장 따위를, 적어도 북극성 훈장이나 시리우스 훈장도 아니고, 그걸 외투에 감히 달았다고 당신이 나를 흠씬 두들겨 팼을 테니 말이오."], ["And you keep on saying I am stupid, but, mercy on us!", "게다가 당신은 자꾸 나더러 어리석다고 하는데, 원, 천만에!"], ["I make no claim to be equal to you in intelligence.", "나는 지성에서 당신과 맞먹겠다고 주장하지도 않소."], ["Mephistopheles declared to Faust that he desired evil, but did only good.", "메피스토펠레스는 파우스트에게 자기는 악을 바라면서도 선만 행한다고 선언했지요."], ["Well, he can say what he likes, it’s quite the opposite with me.", "글쎄, 그자야 무슨 말이든 하라지, 나는 정반대요."], ["I am perhaps the one man in all creation who loves the truth and genuinely desires good.", "나는 어쩌면 온 천지에서 진리를 사랑하고 진심으로 선을 바라는 유일한 존재일 거요."], ["I was there when the Word, Who died on the Cross, rose up into heaven bearing on His bosom the soul of the penitent thief.", "나는 그 자리에 있었소, 십자가에서 죽으신 말씀이, 회개한 도둑의 영혼을 품에 안고 하늘로 솟아오르실 때 말이오."], ["I heard the glad shrieks of the cherubim singing and shouting hosannah and the thunderous rapture of the seraphim which shook heaven and all creation, and I swear to you by all that’s sacred, I longed to join the choir and shout hosannah with them all.", "나는 케루빔들이 호산나를 노래하고 외치는 환희에 찬 절규를 들었고, 하늘과 온 천지를 뒤흔드는 세라핌들의 우레 같은 환희를 들었소, 그리고 거룩한 모든 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 나도 그 합창에 끼어들어 그들 모두와 함께 호산나를 외치고 싶었소."], ["The word had almost escaped me, had almost broken from my lips ...", "그 말이 하마터면 내게서 새어 나올 뻔했소, 하마터면 내 입술에서 터져 나올 뻔했지……"], ["you know how susceptible and esthetically impressionable I am.", "알다시피 나는 무척 예민하고 미적으로 쉽게 감응하는 자니까."], ["But common sense—oh, a most unhappy trait in my character—kept me in due bounds and I let the moment pass!", "하지만 상식이―오, 내 성격의 가장 불행한 특질인―나를 적당한 선에서 붙들어 두었고, 그래서 나는 그 순간을 그냥 흘려보내고 말았소!"], ["For what would have happened, I reflected, what would have happened after my hosannah?", "무슨 일이 벌어졌겠는가, 나는 곰곰이 생각해 봤소, 내가 호산나를 외쳤다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌겠는가?"], ["Everything on earth would have been extinguished at once and no events could have occurred.", "이 땅의 모든 것이 당장 꺼져 버리고 아무 사건도 일어날 수 없게 됐을 거요."], ["And so, solely from a sense of duty and my social position, I was forced to suppress the good moment and to stick to my nasty task.", "그래서 나는 오로지 의무감과 내 사회적 처지 때문에, 그 선한 순간을 억누르고 내 추악한 소임에 매달릴 수밖에 없었소."], ["Somebody takes all the credit of what’s good for Himself, and nothing but nastiness is left for me.", "누군가가 선한 것에 대한 공은 죄다 제 것으로 가로채고, 나한테는 추악한 것밖에 남겨 주지 않는 거요."], ["But I don’t envy the honor of a life of idle imposture, I am not ambitious.", "하지만 나는 한가로이 사기나 치며 사는 영예 따위는 부럽지 않소, 나는 야심이 없으니까."], ["Why am I, of all creatures in the world, doomed to be cursed by all decent people and even to be kicked, for if I put on mortal form I am bound to take such consequences sometimes?", "어째서 나는, 온 세상 모든 피조물 가운데 하필이면, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 저주하고 심지어 발길질까지 당하도록 운명 지어진 거요, 내가 죽을 육신의 형상을 걸치면 이따금 그런 대가를 치를 수밖에 없으니 말이오?"], ["I know, of course, there’s a secret in it, but they won’t tell me the secret for anything, for then perhaps, seeing the meaning of it, I might bawl hosannah, and the indispensable minus would disappear at once, and good sense would reign supreme throughout the whole world.", "물론 거기에 어떤 비밀이 있다는 건 나도 알지만, 그들은 무슨 일이 있어도 나한테 그 비밀을 일러 주지 않을 거요, 왜냐하면 내가 그 의미를 알게 되면 어쩌면 호산나를 목청껏 외쳐 댈 테고, 그러면 그 없어서는 안 될 마이너스가 당장 사라져 버려, 온 세상에 양식(良識)이 군림하게 될 테니까."], ["And that, of course, would mean the end of everything, even of magazines and newspapers, for who would take them in?", "그러면 물론 만물의 종말을 뜻하게 되겠지, 잡지며 신문까지도 말이오, 그런 걸 누가 구독하려 들겠소?"], ["I know that at the end of all things I shall be reconciled.", "만물의 끝에 가서는 나도 화해하게 되리라는 것을 나는 알고 있소."], ["I, too, shall walk my quadrillion and learn the secret.", "나 역시 내 백경 킬로미터를 걷고 그 비밀을 알게 될 거요."], ["But till that happens I am sulking and fulfill my destiny though it’s against the grain—that is, to ruin thousands for the sake of saving one.", "하지만 그렇게 되기까지 나는 뾰로통한 채로, 비위에 거슬리면서도 내 운명을 다하는 거요―그러니까, 하나를 구원하기 위해 수천을 파멸시키는 일 말이오."], ["How many souls have had to be ruined and how many honorable reputations destroyed for the sake of that one righteous man, Job, over whom they made such a fool of me in old days!", "옛날에 그들이 나를 그토록 우롱했던 그 의로운 사람 욥 하나를 위해, 얼마나 많은 영혼이 파멸되어야 했고 얼마나 많은 명예로운 평판이 무너져야 했던지!"], ["Yes, till the secret is revealed, there are two sorts of truths for me—one, their truth, yonder, which I know nothing about so far, and the other my own.", "그렇소, 그 비밀이 밝혀지기 전까지 나한테는 두 가지 진리가 있소―하나는 저쪽에 있는 그들의 진리로, 나로서는 아직 아무것도 모르는 것이고, 다른 하나는 나 자신의 진리요."], ["And there’s no knowing which will turn out the better....", "그리고 어느 쪽이 더 나은 걸로 드러날지는 알 수 없는 노릇이오……"], ["Are you asleep?”", "당신 잠들었소?\""]], [["“I might well be,”", "\"잠들 만도 하지.\""], ["Ivan groaned angrily.", "이반이 화난 듯 신음했다."], ["“All my stupid ideas—outgrown, thrashed out long ago, and flung aside like a dead carcass—you present to me as something new!”", "\"내 어리석은 생각들―진작에 벗어났고, 오래전에 두들겨 부숴 송장처럼 내팽개쳐 버린 것들을―당신은 무슨 새로운 것인 양 나한테 들이미는군!\""]], [["“There’s no pleasing you!", "\"당신 비위는 도무지 못 맞추겠소!"], ["And I thought I should fascinate you by my literary style.", "나는 내 문체로 당신을 매료시킬 줄 알았는데."], ["That hosannah in the skies really wasn’t bad, was it?", "그 하늘의 호산나 대목은 정말이지 나쁘지 않았소, 안 그렇소?"], ["And then that ironical tone à la Heine, eh?”", "그다음 그 하이네풍의 반어적인 어조는 또 어떻고, 응?\""]], [["“No, I was never such a flunkey!", "\"천만에, 나는 결코 그런 종놈이었던 적이 없어!"], ["How then could my soul beget a flunkey like you?”", "그러니 어떻게 내 영혼이 당신 같은 종놈을 낳을 수 있겠어?\""]], [["“My dear fellow, I know a most charming and attractive young Russian gentleman, a young thinker and a great lover of literature and art, the author of a promising poem entitled The Grand Inquisitor.", "\"여보게, 나는 더없이 매력적이고 호감 가는 어느 젊은 러시아 신사를 하나 아는데, 젊은 사상가요 문학과 예술을 무척 사랑하는 자로, 〈대심문관〉이라는 제목의 장래가 촉망되는 서사시의 작가요."], ["I was only thinking of him!”", "나는 그저 그자를 생각하고 있었을 뿐이오!\""]], [["“I forbid you to speak of The Grand Inquisitor,”", "\"〈대심문관〉 얘긴 입에 올리지 마.\""], ["cried Ivan, crimson with shame.", "이반이 수치심으로 시뻘게져 외쳤다."]], [["“And the Geological Cataclysm.", "\"그럼 〈지질학적 대변동〉은?"], ["Do you remember?", "기억하오?"], ["That was a poem, now!”", "그건 정말 한 편의 시였지!\""]], [["“Hold your tongue, or I’ll kill you!”", "\"입 닥쳐, 안 그러면 죽여 버릴 테다!\""]], [["“You’ll kill me?", "\"나를 죽인다고?"], ["No, excuse me, I will speak.", "천만에, 미안하지만, 나는 말하겠소."], ["I came to treat myself to that pleasure.", "나는 그 즐거움을 만끽하려고 찾아온 거니까."], ["Oh, I love the dreams of my ardent young friends, quivering with eagerness for life!", "오, 나는 삶을 향한 열망으로 부르르 떠는 내 열렬한 젊은 친구들의 꿈을 사랑하오!"], ["‘There are new men,’ you decided last spring, when you were meaning to come here, ‘they propose to destroy everything and begin with cannibalism.", "'새로운 인간들이 있다.' 당신은 작년 봄, 여기 오려고 마음먹었을 때 이렇게 단정했소, '그들은 모든 것을 파괴하고 식인(食人)부터 시작하자고 한다."], ["Stupid fellows!", "어리석은 자들 같으니!"], ["they didn’t ask my advice!", "그들은 내게 조언을 구하지도 않았다!"], ["I maintain that nothing need be destroyed, that we only need to destroy the idea of God in man, that’s how we have to set to work.", "나는 주장한다, 아무것도 파괴할 필요가 없으며, 우리는 다만 인간 안의 신이라는 관념을 파괴하기만 하면 된다고, 그것이 바로 우리가 일에 착수해야 할 방식이라고."], ["It’s that, that we must begin with.", "바로 그것, 그것에서부터 시작해야 한다."], ["Oh, blind race of men who have no understanding!", "오, 아무런 이해도 없는 눈먼 인류여!"], ["As soon as men have all of them denied God—and I believe that period, analogous with geological periods, will come to pass—the old conception of the universe will fall of itself without cannibalism, and, what’s more, the old morality, and everything will begin anew.", "인간들이 모조리 신을 부정하게 되는 즉시―나는 지질학적 시대에 견줄 만한 그런 시대가 도래하리라 믿는다―우주에 대한 낡은 관념은 식인 없이도 저절로 무너질 것이며, 게다가 낡은 도덕까지도 무너질 것이고, 모든 것이 새로 시작될 것이다."], ["Men will unite to take from life all it can give, but only for joy and happiness in the present world.", "인간들은 삶이 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 거기서 취하려고 단결할 것이나, 그것은 오로지 현세에서의 기쁨과 행복만을 위해서다."], ["Man will be lifted up with a spirit of divine Titanic pride and the man-god will appear.", "인간은 신적인, 거인 같은 자긍심으로 드높여질 것이며, 그리하여 인신(人神)이 출현할 것이다."], ["From hour to hour extending his conquest of nature infinitely by his will and his science, man will feel such lofty joy from hour to hour in doing it that it will make up for all his old dreams of the joys of heaven.", "자신의 의지와 과학으로 자연에 대한 정복을 시시각각 무한히 넓혀 가면서, 인간은 그 일을 하며 시시각각 그토록 드높은 기쁨을 느낄 것이고, 그것이 천국의 기쁨에 대한 그의 모든 낡은 꿈을 보상해 줄 것이다."], ["Every one will know that he is mortal and will accept death proudly and serenely like a god.", "누구나 자신이 죽을 존재임을 알 것이며, 신처럼 당당하고 평온하게 죽음을 받아들일 것이다."], ["His pride will teach him that it’s useless for him to repine at life’s being a moment, and he will love his brother without need of reward.", "그의 자긍심은 삶이 한순간임을 한탄해 봐야 부질없음을 그에게 가르칠 것이고, 그는 보답을 바랄 것도 없이 제 형제를 사랑할 것이다."], ["Love will be sufficient only for a moment of life, but the very consciousness of its momentariness will intensify its fire, which now is dissipated in dreams of eternal love beyond the grave’...", "사랑은 삶의 한순간에만 족할 테지만, 바로 그 순간성의 의식이야말로 그 불길을 더욱 거세게 타오르게 할 것이니, 그 불길은 지금이야 무덤 너머 영원한 사랑의 꿈속에서 흩어져 버리고 있지만'……"], ["and so on and so on in the same style.", "운운, 그런 식으로 계속되는 거요."], ["Charming!”", "매력적이지!\""]], [["Ivan sat with his eyes on the floor, and his hands pressed to his ears, but he began trembling all over.", "이반은 두 눈을 마룻바닥에 떨군 채 두 손으로 귀를 틀어막고 앉아 있었으나, 온몸을 부들부들 떨기 시작했다."], ["The voice continued.", "그 목소리는 계속됐다."]], [["“The question now is, my young thinker reflected, is it possible that such a period will ever come?", "\"이제 문제는, 하고 내 젊은 사상가가 곰곰이 생각했소, 과연 그런 시대가 도래할 수 있겠는가 하는 것이다."], ["If it does, everything is determined and humanity is settled for ever.", "만일 그렇게 된다면, 모든 것이 결판나고 인류는 영원히 안정되리라."], ["But as, owing to man’s inveterate stupidity, this cannot come about for at least a thousand years, every one who recognizes the truth even now may legitimately order his life as he pleases, on the new principles.", "하지만 인간의 고질적인 어리석음 탓에 그것이 적어도 천 년 안에는 이루어질 수 없는 노릇이니, 지금이라도 그 진리를 깨달은 자라면 누구든 그 새로운 원칙에 따라 제멋대로 자기 삶을 꾸려도 정당하리라."], ["In that sense, ‘all things are lawful’ for him.", "그런 의미에서 그에게는 '모든 것이 허용된다.'"], ["What’s more, even if this period never comes to pass, since there is anyway no God and no immortality, the new man may well become the man-god, even if he is the only one in the whole world, and promoted to his new position, he may lightheartedly overstep all the barriers of the old morality of the old slave-man, if necessary.", "더 나아가, 설령 그런 시대가 영영 도래하지 않는다 해도, 어차피 신도 없고 불멸도 없는 이상, 새로운 인간은 능히 인신이 될 수 있으니, 설령 그가 온 세상에 단 하나뿐인 인신이라 할지라도, 그 새로운 지위로 격상되어, 필요하다면 낡은 노예 인간의 낡은 도덕의 온갖 장벽을 가뿐히 넘어서도 좋으리라."], ["There is no law for God.", "신에게는 법이 없다."], ["Where God stands, the place is holy.", "신이 서 있는 곳, 그곳이 곧 거룩한 곳이다."], ["Where I stand will be at once the foremost place ...", "내가 서는 곳이 당장 으뜸가는 곳이 되리라……"], ["‘all things are lawful’ and that’s the end of it!", "'모든 것이 허용된다', 그걸로 끝이다!"], ["That’s all very charming; but if you want to swindle why do you want a moral sanction for doing it?", "그거야 다 매력적이오. 하지만 사기를 치고 싶거든 무엇하러 거기에 도덕적 명분까지 필요하단 말이오?"], ["But that’s our modern Russian all over.", "한데 그게 바로 요즘 러시아인의 본색이오."], ["He can’t bring himself to swindle without a moral sanction.", "도덕적 명분 없이는 사기조차 칠 수가 없는 거지요."], ["He is so in love with truth—”", "그자는 진리를 그토록 사랑하니까……\""]], [["The visitor talked, obviously carried away by his own eloquence, speaking louder and louder and looking ironically at his host.", "손님은 보아하니 제 능변에 도취된 듯 점점 더 목청을 높여 가며, 주인을 반어적인 눈길로 바라보면서 떠들어 댔다."], ["But he did not succeed in finishing; Ivan suddenly snatched a glass from the table and flung it at the orator.", "그러나 그는 말을 끝맺지 못했으니, 이반이 별안간 식탁에서 유리잔을 낚아채어 그 웅변가에게 내던졌던 것이다."]], [["“Ah, mais c’est bête enfin,”", "\"아, 이거 영 어처구니없군(Ah, mais c'est bête enfin).\""], ["cried the latter, jumping up from the sofa and shaking the drops of tea off himself.", "후자가 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 제 몸에 묻은 찻방울을 털어 내며 외쳤다."], ["“He remembers Luther’s inkstand!", "\"루터의 잉크병이 생각나는 모양이군!"], ["He takes me for a dream and throws glasses at a dream!", "나를 꿈으로 여기면서 그 꿈한테 유리잔을 던지다니!"], ["It’s like a woman!", "꼭 여자 같소!"], ["I suspected you were only pretending to stop up your ears.”", "당신이 귀를 틀어막는 척만 하고 있다고 짐작은 했지.\""]], [["A loud, persistent knocking was suddenly heard at the window.", "별안간 창문에서 요란하고 집요하게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Ivan jumped up from the sofa.", "이반은 소파에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Do you hear?", "\"들리오?"], ["You’d better open,”", "어서 열어 주는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["cried the visitor; “it’s your brother Alyosha with the most interesting and surprising news, I’ll be bound!”", "손님이 외쳤다. \"당신 동생 알료샤가 더없이 흥미롭고 놀라운 소식을 들고 온 거요, 내 장담하지!\""]], [["“Be silent, deceiver, I knew it was Alyosha, I felt he was coming, and of course he has not come for nothing; of course he brings ‘news,’”", "\"입 닥쳐, 이 사기꾼아, 알료샤라는 건 나도 알았어, 그 애가 오고 있다는 걸 느꼈다고, 물론 그 애가 헛걸음하러 온 건 아니겠지, 물론 '소식'을 들고 온 거겠지.\""], ["Ivan exclaimed frantically.", "이반이 미친 듯이 외쳤다."]], [["“Open, open to him.", "\"열어 주시오, 그 애한테 문을 열어 주시오."], ["There’s a snowstorm and he is your brother.", "눈보라가 치는데 그 애는 당신 동생이잖소. 무슈, 지금 날씨가 어떤지 아시오?"], ["Monsieur sait-il le temps qu’il fait?", "이런 날씬 개도 밖에 내놓지 못할 지경이오(Monsieur sait-il le temps qu'il fait?"], ["C’est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors.”", "C'est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors).\""]], [["The knocking continued.", "두드리는 소리는 계속됐다."], ["Ivan wanted to rush to the window, but something seemed to fetter his arms and legs.", "이반은 창가로 달려가려 했으나, 무언가가 그의 팔다리를 옭아매는 것만 같았다."], ["He strained every effort to break his chains, but in vain.", "그는 그 쇠사슬을 끊으려고 안간힘을 다했으나, 헛수고였다."], ["The knocking at the window grew louder and louder.", "창문을 두드리는 소리는 점점 더 커졌다."], ["At last the chains were broken and Ivan leapt up from the sofa.", "마침내 쇠사슬이 끊어지고 이반은 소파에서 벌떡 뛰어 일어났다."], ["He looked round him wildly.", "그는 미친 듯이 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["Both candles had almost burnt out, the glass he had just thrown at his visitor stood before him on the table, and there was no one on the sofa opposite.", "두 자루의 촛불은 거의 다 타 버렸고, 방금 손님에게 던진 유리잔이 식탁 위 제 앞에 놓여 있었으며, 맞은편 소파에는 아무도 없었다."], ["The knocking on the window frame went on persistently, but it was by no means so loud as it had seemed in his dream; on the contrary, it was quite subdued.", "창틀을 두드리는 소리는 집요하게 계속됐으나, 결코 꿈속에서 들렸던 것만큼 요란하지는 않았다. 오히려 아주 잦아든 소리였다."]], [["“It was not a dream!", "\"꿈이 아니었어!"], ["No, I swear it was not a dream, it all happened just now!”", "아니, 맹세컨대 꿈이 아니었어, 모든 게 방금 정말로 일어난 일이야!\""], ["cried Ivan.", "이반이 외쳤다."], ["He rushed to the window and opened the movable pane.", "그는 창가로 달려가 여닫이 유리창을 열었다."]], [["“Alyosha, I told you not to come,”", "\"알료샤, 오지 말라고 했잖아.\""], ["he cried fiercely to his brother.", "그가 동생에게 사납게 외쳤다."], ["“In two words, what do you want?", "\"두 마디로, 무슨 일이야?"], ["In two words, do you hear?”", "두 마디로, 알겠어?\""]], [["“An hour ago Smerdyakov hanged himself,”", "\"한 시간 전에 스메르댜코프가 목을 맸어요.\""], ["Alyosha answered from the yard.", "알료샤가 마당에서 대답했다."]], [["“Come round to the steps, I’ll open at once,”", "\"현관 계단 쪽으로 돌아와, 곧 열어 줄게.\""], ["said Ivan, going to open the door to Alyosha.", "이반은 이렇게 말하며 알료샤에게 문을 열어 주러 갔다."]], [["# Chapter X. “It Was He Who Said That”", "# Chapter X. “It Was He Who Said That”"]], [["Alyosha coming in told Ivan that a little over an hour ago Marya Kondratyevna had run to his rooms and informed him Smerdyakov had taken his own life.", "알료샤가 들어서며 한 시간 남짓 전에 마리야 콘드라티예브나가 자기 거처로 달려와 스메르댜코프가 스스로 목숨을 끊었다고 알려 왔다고 이반에게 말했다."], ["“I went in to clear away the samovar and he was hanging on a nail in the wall.”", "\"사모바르를 치우러 들어갔더니 그이가 벽에 박힌 못에 매달려 있지 뭐예요.\""], ["On Alyosha’s inquiring whether she had informed the police, she answered that she had told no one, “but I flew straight to you, I’ve run all the way.”", "경찰에는 알렸느냐고 알료샤가 묻자, 그녀는 아무에게도 말하지 않았다고, \"곧장 도련님께로 날아왔어요, 줄곧 뛰어왔어요\"라고 대답했다."], ["She seemed perfectly crazy, Alyosha reported, and was shaking like a leaf.", "그녀는 완전히 제정신이 아닌 것 같았다고 알료샤는 전했고, 나뭇잎처럼 떨고 있었다는 것이다."], ["When Alyosha ran with her to the cottage, he found Smerdyakov still hanging.", "알료샤가 그녀와 함께 오두막으로 달려갔을 때 스메르댜코프는 아직도 매달려 있었다."], ["On the table lay a note: “I destroy my life of my own will and desire, so as to throw no blame on any one.”", "탁자 위에는 쪽지 한 장이 놓여 있었다. \"나는 누구에게도 죄를 씌우지 않으려고 내 의지와 바람에 따라 내 목숨을 끊는다.\""], ["Alyosha left the note on the table and went straight to the police captain and told him all about it.", "알료샤는 쪽지를 탁자 위에 그대로 두고 곧장 경찰서장에게 가서 일의 자초지종을 다 이야기했다."], ["“And from him I’ve come straight to you,”", "\"그러고는 거기서 곧장 형한테로 온 거야.\""], ["said Alyosha, in conclusion, looking intently into Ivan’s face.", "알료샤는 이반의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 이야기를 맺었다."], ["He had not taken his eyes off him while he told his story, as though struck by something in his expression.", "그는 이야기를 하는 내내 형에게서 눈을 떼지 못했으니, 그 표정 속의 무언가에 충격을 받은 듯했다."]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형.\""], ["he cried suddenly, “you must be terribly ill.", "그가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"형 정말 몹시 아픈 거 아냐."], ["You look and don’t seem to understand what I tell you.”", "보고 있으면서도 내 말을 알아듣지 못하는 것 같아.\""]], [["“It’s a good thing you came,”", "\"네가 와 줘서 다행이다.\""], ["said Ivan, as though brooding, and not hearing Alyosha’s exclamation.", "이반은 무언가에 골똘히 잠긴 듯, 알료샤의 외침은 듣지도 못한 채 말했다."], ["“I knew he had hanged himself.”", "\"나는 그자가 목을 맨 걸 알고 있었어.\""]], [["“From whom?”", "\"누구한테서 들었는데?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"몰라."], ["But I knew.", "그래도 알고 있었지."], ["Did I know?", "내가 알았나?"], ["Yes, he told me.", "그래, 그자가 말해 줬어."], ["He told me so just now.”", "방금 그렇게 말해 줬다고.\""]], [["Ivan stood in the middle of the room, and still spoke in the same brooding tone, looking at the ground.", "이반은 방 한가운데 서서 여전히 같은 골똘한 어조로, 바닥을 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["“Who is he?”", "\"그자가 누군데?\""], ["asked Alyosha, involuntarily looking round.", "알료샤가 저도 모르게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["“He’s slipped away.”", "\"빠져나가 버렸어.\""]], [["Ivan raised his head and smiled softly.", "이반이 고개를 들고 부드럽게 미소 지었다."]], [["“He was afraid of you, of a dove like you.", "\"그자는 너를 무서워했어, 너 같은 비둘기를 말이야."], ["You are a ‘pure cherub.’ Dmitri calls you a cherub.", "너는 '순결한 천사'지. 드미트리가 너더러 천사라고 부르잖아."], ["Cherub!...", "천사라!…"], ["the thunderous rapture of the seraphim.", "천사들의 우레 같은 황홀경이라니."], ["What are seraphim?", "천사가 대체 뭐냐?"], ["Perhaps a whole constellation.", "어쩌면 별자리 하나일지도 모르지."], ["But perhaps that constellation is only a chemical molecule.", "그렇지만 어쩌면 그 별자리가 화학 분자 하나에 지나지 않을지도."], ["There’s a constellation of the Lion and the Sun.", "사자자리와 태양자리라는 게 있지."], ["Don’t you know it?”", "너 그거 모르냐?\""]], [["“Brother, sit down,”", "\"형, 좀 앉아.\""], ["said Alyosha in alarm.", "알료샤가 놀라서 말했다."], ["“For goodness’ sake, sit down on the sofa!", "\"제발 좀, 소파에 앉으라니까!"], ["You are delirious; put your head on the pillow, that’s right.", "형 헛소리를 하고 있어. 베개에 머리를 좀 대, 그래, 그렇게."], ["Would you like a wet towel on your head?", "머리에 젖은 수건을 얹어 줄까?"], ["Perhaps it will do you good.”", "그러면 좀 나아질지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Give me the towel: it’s here on the chair.", "\"수건 좀 줘. 의자 위에 있어."], ["I just threw it down there.”", "방금 내가 거기 던져 놨거든.\""]], [["“It’s not here.", "\"여긴 없는데."], ["Don’t worry yourself.", "걱정 마."], ["I know where it is—here,”", "어디 있는지 내가 알아, 여기 있네.\""], ["said Alyosha, finding a clean towel, folded up and unused, by Ivan’s dressing-table in the other corner of the room.", "알료샤가 방 반대편 구석 이반의 화장대 옆에서, 개켜진 채 쓰지 않은 깨끗한 수건을 찾아내며 말했다."], ["Ivan looked strangely at the towel: recollection seemed to come back to him for an instant.", "이반은 그 수건을 기이한 눈빛으로 바라보았으니, 한순간 기억이 되돌아오는 듯했다."]], [["“Stay”—he got up from the sofa—“an hour ago I took that new towel from there and wetted it.", "\"가만\"—그가 소파에서 일어났다—\"한 시간 전에 내가 저기서 저 새 수건을 가져다 적셨어."], ["I wrapped it round my head and threw it down here ...", "그걸 머리에 둘렀다가 여기다 던져 놨다고…"], ["How is it it’s dry?", "그게 어떻게 말라 있지?"], ["There was no other.”", "다른 건 없었는데.\""]], [["“You put that towel on your head?”", "\"형이 그 수건을 머리에 얹었다고?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and walked up and down the room an hour ago ...", "\"그래, 한 시간 전에 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하면서 말이야…"], ["Why have the candles burnt down so?", "양초가 왜 이렇게 다 타 버렸지?"], ["What’s the time?”", "지금 몇 시야?\""]], [["“Nearly twelve.”", "\"거의 열두 시야.\""]], [["“No, no, no!”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야!\""], ["Ivan cried suddenly.", "이반이 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“It was not a dream.", "\"그건 꿈이 아니었어."], ["He was here; he was sitting here, on that sofa.", "그자가 여기 있었어. 바로 여기, 저 소파에 앉아 있었다고."], ["When you knocked at the window, I threw a glass at him ...", "네가 창문을 두드렸을 때 내가 그자한테 유리잔을 던졌어…"], ["this one.", "이거 말이야."], ["Wait a minute.", "잠깐만."], ["I was asleep last time, but this dream was not a dream.", "지난번엔 내가 잠들어 있었는데, 이번 꿈은 꿈이 아니었어."], ["It has happened before.", "전에도 있었던 일이야."], ["I have dreams now, Alyosha ...", "나는 요즘 꿈을 꿔, 알료샤…"], ["yet they are not dreams, but reality.", "그런데 그건 꿈이 아니라 현실이야."], ["I walk about, talk and see ...", "나는 돌아다니고, 말하고, 보고…"], ["though I am asleep.", "그러면서도 잠들어 있어."], ["But he was sitting here, on that sofa there....", "그렇지만 그자는 여기 앉아 있었어, 바로 저기 저 소파에…."], ["He is frightfully stupid, Alyosha, frightfully stupid.”", "그자는 끔찍하게 멍청해, 알료샤, 끔찍하게 멍청하다고.\""], ["Ivan laughed suddenly and began pacing about the room.", "이반이 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨리고는 방 안을 서성이기 시작했다."]], [["“Who is stupid?", "\"누가 멍청한데?"], ["Of whom are you talking, brother?”", "누구 얘기를 하는 거야, 형?\""], ["Alyosha asked anxiously again.", "알료샤가 또다시 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“The devil!", "\"악마 말이야!"], ["He’s taken to visiting me.", "그자가 나를 찾아오기 시작했어."], ["He’s been here twice, almost three times.", "두 번, 거의 세 번이나 다녀갔지."], ["He taunted me with being angry at his being a simple devil and not Satan, with scorched wings, in thunder and lightning.", "그자가 천둥 번개 속에서 날개를 그을린 사탄이 아니라 한낱 평범한 악마라는 데 내가 화를 낸다고 나를 비웃더군."], ["But he is not Satan: that’s a lie.", "하지만 그자는 사탄이 아니야. 그건 거짓말이지."], ["He is an impostor.", "그자는 사기꾼이야."], ["He is simply a devil—a paltry, trivial devil.", "그냥 악마, 보잘것없고 하찮은 악마라고."], ["He goes to the baths.", "그자는 목욕탕에도 가."], ["If you undressed him, you’d be sure to find he had a tail, long and smooth like a Danish dog’s, a yard long, dun color....", "그자 옷을 벗겨 보면 틀림없이 꼬리가 달려 있을 거야, 덴마크 개 꼬리처럼 길고 매끈한, 한 야드는 되는, 우중충한 갈색 꼬리가 말이야…."], ["Alyosha, you are cold.", "알료샤, 너 춥겠구나."], ["You’ve been in the snow.", "눈 속을 헤치고 왔으니."], ["Would you like some tea?", "차라도 좀 마실래? 뭐?"], ["What?", "추워?"], ["Is it cold? Shall I tell her to bring some?", "그 여자한테 좀 가져오라고 할까?"], ["C’est à ne pas mettre un chien dehors....”", "세 아 느 파 메트르 욍 시앵 드오르….\""]], [["Alyosha ran to the washing-stand, wetted the towel, persuaded Ivan to sit down again, and put the wet towel round his head.", "알료샤는 세면대로 달려가 수건을 적시고는 이반을 다시 자리에 앉히도록 설득해, 젖은 수건을 그의 머리에 둘러 주었다."], ["He sat down beside him.", "그러고는 그 옆에 앉았다."]], [["“What were you telling me just now about Lise?”", "\"아까 네가 리즈에 대해 무슨 얘기를 했었지?\""], ["Ivan began again.", "이반이 다시 입을 열었다."], ["(He was becoming very talkative.) “I like Lise.", "(그는 몹시 수다스러워지고 있었다.) \"나는 리즈가 좋아."], ["I said something nasty about her.", "그 애에 대해 못된 소리를 했지."], ["It was a lie.", "그건 거짓말이었어."], ["I like her ...", "나는 그 애가 좋아…"], ["I am afraid for Katya to-morrow.", "나는 내일 카탸가 두려워."], ["I am more afraid of her than of anything.", "그 무엇보다도 그 여자가 더 두렵다고."], ["On account of the future.", "앞일 때문이지."], ["She will cast me off to-morrow and trample me under foot.", "그 여자는 내일 나를 내쳐 버리고 발밑에 짓밟을 거야."], ["She thinks that I am ruining Mitya from jealousy on her account!", "내가 자기 때문에 질투에 사로잡혀 미탸를 파멸시키려 한다고 여기거든!"], ["Yes, she thinks that!", "그래, 그렇게 생각한단 말이야!"], ["But it’s not so.", "하지만 그게 아냐."], ["To-morrow the cross, but not the gallows.", "내일은 십자가일 뿐, 교수대는 아니지."], ["No, I shan’t hang myself.", "아니, 나는 목을 매지 않을 거야."], ["Do you know, I can never commit suicide, Alyosha.", "알겠니, 나는 결코 자살 같은 건 할 수 없어, 알료샤."], ["Is it because I am base?", "그게 내가 비열해서일까?"], ["I am not a coward.", "나는 겁쟁이가 아니야."], ["Is it from love of life?", "삶에 대한 애착 때문일까?"], ["How did I know that Smerdyakov had hanged himself?", "내가 스메르댜코프가 목을 맸다는 걸 어떻게 알았겠어?"], ["Yes, it was he told me so.”", "그래, 바로 그자가 나한테 말해 준 거야.\""]], [["“And you are quite convinced that there has been some one here?”", "\"그러니까 형은 여기 누군가가 있었다고 완전히 확신하는 거야?\""], ["asked Alyosha.", "알료샤가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, on that sofa in the corner.", "\"그래, 저기 구석 소파에 말이야."], ["You would have driven him away.", "너라면 그자를 쫓아냈겠지."], ["You did drive him away: he disappeared when you arrived.", "실제로 네가 쫓아냈잖아. 네가 오니까 그자가 사라져 버렸으니까."], ["I love your face, Alyosha.", "나는 네 얼굴이 좋아, 알료샤."], ["Did you know that I loved your face?", "내가 네 얼굴을 좋아하는 걸 너 알았니?"], ["And he is myself, Alyosha.", "그리고 그자는 바로 나 자신이야, 알료샤."], ["All that’s base in me, all that’s mean and contemptible.", "내 안의 비열한 것, 천박하고 경멸스러운 것 전부 말이야."], ["Yes, I am a romantic.", "그래, 나는 낭만주의자야."], ["He guessed it ...", "그자가 그걸 알아챘지…"], ["though it’s a libel.", "하지만 그건 중상모략이야."], ["He is frightfully stupid; but it’s to his advantage.", "그자는 끔찍하게 멍청해. 하지만 그게 그자한테는 유리하게 작용하지."], ["He has cunning, animal cunning—he knew how to infuriate me.", "그자한테는 교활함이, 짐승 같은 교활함이 있어서, 어떻게 나를 격분하게 만드는지를 알았어."], ["He kept taunting me with believing in him, and that was how he made me listen to him.", "그자는 내가 자기를 믿는다고 계속 비웃었는데, 그렇게 해서 나로 하여금 자기 말에 귀를 기울이게 만든 거야."], ["He fooled me like a boy.", "나를 어린애처럼 놀려 댔지."], ["He told me a great deal that was true about myself, though.", "그래도 나 자신에 관해 진실인 것도 잔뜩 말해 주긴 했어."], ["I should never have owned it to myself.", "나라면 결코 스스로 인정하지 못했을 것들을 말이야."], ["Do you know, Alyosha,”", "알겠니, 알료샤.\""], ["Ivan added in an intensely earnest and confidential tone, “I should be awfully glad to think that it was he and not I.”", "이반이 더없이 진지하고 은밀한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그게 내가 아니라 바로 그자였다고 생각할 수만 있다면 나는 정말 더없이 기쁠 거야.\""]], [["“He has worn you out,”", "\"형은 그자한테 시달려서 기진맥진해진 거야.\""], ["said Alyosha, looking compassionately at his brother.", "알료샤가 형을 가엾게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“He’s been teasing me.", "\"그자가 나를 놀려 댔어."], ["And you know he does it so cleverly, so cleverly. ‘Conscience!", "그런데 말이야, 그자는 어찌나 교묘하게, 어찌나 교묘하게 그러는지."], ["What is conscience?", "'양심! 양심이 뭐냐?"], ["I make it up for myself.", "나 스스로 지어내는 거지."], ["Why am I tormented by it?", "왜 내가 그것 때문에 고통받지?"], ["From habit.", "습관 때문이야."], ["From the universal habit of mankind for the seven thousand years.", "칠천 년 동안 이어진 인류의 보편적인 습관 때문이라고."], ["So let us give it up, and we shall be gods.’ It was he said that, it was he said that!”", "그러니 그런 건 내던져 버리자, 그러면 우리는 신이 될 거야.' 그자가 그렇게 말했어, 바로 그자가 그렇게 말했다고!\""]], [["“And not you, not you?”", "\"형이 아니라, 형이 아니라?\""], ["Alyosha could not help crying, looking frankly at his brother.", "알료샤가 형을 솔직하게 바라보며 외치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“Never mind him, anyway; have done with him and forget him.", "\"어쨌든 그자는 신경 쓰지 마. 그자랑은 끝장을 내고 잊어버려."], ["And let him take with him all that you curse now, and never come back!”", "그리고 형이 지금 저주하는 모든 걸 그자가 다 짊어지고 가게 해, 다시는 돌아오지 못하게!\""]], [["“Yes, but he is spiteful.", "\"그래, 하지만 그자는 악의에 차 있어."], ["He laughed at me.", "나를 비웃었지."], ["He was impudent, Alyosha,”", "뻔뻔스러웠어, 알료샤.\""], ["Ivan said, with a shudder of offense.", "이반이 모욕감에 몸서리치며 말했다."], ["“But he was unfair to me, unfair to me about lots of things.", "\"그런데 그자는 나한테 부당했어, 많은 일에서 나한테 부당했다고."], ["He told lies about me to my face.", "내 면전에 대고 거짓말을 늘어놨어."], ["‘Oh, you are going to perform an act of heroic virtue: to confess you murdered your father, that the valet murdered him at your instigation.’”", "'오, 너는 영웅적인 미덕의 행위를 하려는 거지. 네가 아버지를 죽였다고, 하인이 네 사주를 받아 죽였다고 자백하려는 거잖아.'\""]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형.\""], ["Alyosha interposed, “restrain yourself.", "알료샤가 끼어들었다. \"정신 차려."], ["It was not you murdered him.", "형이 아버지를 죽인 게 아니야."], ["It’s not true!”", "그건 사실이 아니라고!\""]], [["“That’s what he says, he, and he knows it.", "\"그자가 하는 말이 바로 그거야, 그자가, 그러면서도 그자는 그걸 알고 있어. '너는 영웅적인 미덕의 행위를 하려고 하지만 미덕 따위는 믿지도 않아."], ["‘You are going to perform an act of heroic virtue, and you don’t believe in virtue; that’s what tortures you and makes you angry, that’s why you are so vindictive.’ He said that to me about me and he knows what he says.”", "바로 그게 너를 괴롭히고 너를 화나게 하는 거고, 그래서 네가 그토록 앙심을 품는 거지.' 그자가 나에 관해 나한테 그렇게 말했고, 그자는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 알고 있어.\""]], [["“It’s you say that, not he,”", "\"그건 형이 하는 말이지, 그자가 아니야.\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha mournfully, “and you say it because you are ill and delirious, tormenting yourself.”", "알료샤가 비통하게 외쳤다. \"형이 그런 말을 하는 건 형이 아프고 헛소리를 하며 스스로를 괴롭히고 있기 때문이야.\""]], [["“No, he knows what he says.", "\"아니야, 그자는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아."], ["‘You are going from pride,’ he says.", "'너는 자존심 때문에 가는 거야.' 그자가 말하지."], ["‘You’ll stand up and say it was I killed him, and why do you writhe with horror?", "'너는 떨치고 일어나서 내가 그를 죽였다고 말할 텐데, 그러면서 왜 공포에 몸부림치는 거지?"], ["You are lying!", "너는 거짓말을 하고 있어!"], ["I despise your opinion, I despise your horror!’ He said that about me.", "나는 너의 그 잘난 생각을 경멸해, 너의 그 공포를 경멸한다고!' 그자가 나에 관해 그렇게 말했어."], ["‘And do you know you are longing for their praise—“he is a criminal, a murderer, but what a generous soul; he wanted to save his brother and he confessed.”’ That’s a lie, Alyosha!”", "'그리고 너 알아, 너는 그자들이 너를 칭송해 주기를 갈망하고 있어—\"그자는 범죄자다, 살인자다, 하지만 얼마나 너그러운 영혼인가. 동생을 구하려고 자백을 했으니\"라고 말이야.'"], ["Ivan cried suddenly, with flashing eyes.", "그건 거짓말이야, 알료샤!\" 이반이 눈을 번득이며 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“I don’t want the low rabble to praise me, I swear I don’t!", "\"나는 그 천한 무리들이 나를 칭송하는 걸 원하지 않아, 맹세코 원하지 않아!"], ["That’s a lie!", "그건 거짓말이야!"], ["That’s why I threw the glass at him and it broke against his ugly face.”", "그래서 내가 그자한테 유리잔을 던졌고 그게 그자의 추악한 낯짝에 부딪혀 깨진 거라고.\""]], [["“Brother, calm yourself, stop!”", "\"형, 진정해, 그만해!\""], ["Alyosha entreated him.", "알료샤가 그에게 간청했다."]], [["“Yes, he knows how to torment one.", "\"그래, 그자는 사람을 괴롭힐 줄 알아."], ["He’s cruel,”", "잔인하지.\""], ["Ivan went on, unheeding.", "이반이 아랑곳없이 말을 이었다."], ["“I had an inkling from the first what he came for.", "\"나는 처음부터 그자가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 어렴풋이 알아챘어."], ["‘Granting that you go through pride, still you had a hope that Smerdyakov might be convicted and sent to Siberia, and Mitya would be acquitted, while you would only be punished with moral condemnation’ (‘Do you hear?’ he laughed then)—‘and some people will praise you.", "'네가 자존심 때문에 간다고 치자, 그래도 너는 스메르댜코프가 유죄 선고를 받아 시베리아로 보내지고 미탸는 무죄로 풀려나며, 너는 그저 도덕적 비난이라는 벌만 받기를 바라는 희망을 품고 있었지' ('알아듣겠냐?'"], ["But now Smerdyakov’s dead, he has hanged himself, and who’ll believe you alone?", "그자는 그러면서 웃었어)—'그리고 어떤 사람들은 너를 칭송할 거고. 하지만 이제 스메르댜코프는 죽었어, 목을 맸지, 그러니 너 혼자만의 말을 누가 믿겠어?"], ["But yet you are going, you are going, you’ll go all the same, you’ve decided to go.", "그런데도 너는 가고 있어, 가고 있어, 어쨌든 갈 거야, 가기로 마음먹었으니까."], ["What are you going for now?’ That’s awful, Alyosha.", "그런데 이제 와서 뭐 하러 가는 거지?' 그건 끔찍한 일이야, 알료샤."], ["I can’t endure such questions.", "나는 그런 질문은 견딜 수가 없어."], ["Who dare ask me such questions?”", "누가 감히 나한테 그런 질문을 한단 말이야?\""]], [["“Brother,”", "\"형.\""], ["interposed Alyosha—his heart sank with terror, but he still seemed to hope to bring Ivan to reason—“how could he have told you of Smerdyakov’s death before I came, when no one knew of it and there was no time for any one to know of it?”", "알료샤가 끼어들었다—공포로 가슴이 내려앉았지만, 그래도 이반을 이성으로 돌려놓을 수 있으리라는 희망을 여전히 품은 듯했다—\"아무도 모르고, 누구도 알아낼 시간이 없었는데, 내가 오기도 전에 그자가 어떻게 형한테 스메르댜코프의 죽음을 이야기할 수 있었겠어?\""]], [["“He told me,”", "\"그자가 말해 줬다니까.\""], ["said Ivan firmly, refusing to admit a doubt.", "이반이 의심의 여지를 인정하지 않으려 단호하게 말했다."], ["“It was all he did talk about, if you come to that.", "\"따지고 보면 그자가 한 얘기라곤 온통 그것뿐이었어."], ["‘And it would be all right if you believed in virtue,’ he said.", "'네가 미덕을 믿기만 한다면야 다 괜찮을 텐데 말이야.' 그자가 그러더군."], ["‘No matter if they disbelieve you, you are going for the sake of principle.", "'사람들이 너를 믿지 않는대도 상관없어, 너는 신념을 위해 가는 거니까."], ["But you are a little pig like Fyodor Pavlovitch, and what do you want with virtue?", "하지만 너는 표도르 파블로비치 같은 한 마리 돼지 새끼인데, 미덕 따위가 너한테 무슨 소용이람?"], ["Why do you want to go meddling if your sacrifice is of no use to any one?", "네 희생이 누구에게도 쓸모가 없는데 왜 가서 참견하려 드는 거지?"], ["Because you don’t know yourself why you go!", "너 자신조차 네가 왜 가는지 모르니까 그래!"], ["Oh, you’d give a great deal to know yourself why you go!", "오, 네가 왜 가는지를 스스로 알 수만 있다면 너는 무엇이든 다 내줄 텐데!"], ["And can you have made up your mind?", "그런데 너 정말 마음을 정하기는 한 거야?"], ["You’ve not made up your mind.", "너는 마음을 정하지 못했어."], ["You’ll sit all night deliberating whether to go or not.", "밤새도록 앉아서 갈까 말까 궁리만 하겠지. 하지만 너는 갈 거야."], ["But you will go; you know you’ll go.", "네가 가리란 걸 너도 알아."], ["You know that whichever way you decide, the decision does not depend on you.", "어느 쪽으로 결정하든 그 결정이 너한테 달려 있지 않다는 걸 너도 알지."], ["You’ll go because you won’t dare not to go.", "너는 갈 거야, 감히 가지 않을 엄두가 안 나니까."], ["Why won’t you dare?", "왜 엄두가 안 나느냐고?"], ["You must guess that for yourself.", "그건 네가 알아서 짐작해 봐."], ["That’s a riddle for you!’ He got up and went away.", "너한테 던지는 수수께끼다!' 그자가 일어나서 가 버렸어."], ["You came and he went.", "네가 오니까 그자가 가 버린 거야."], ["He called me a coward, Alyosha!", "그자가 나더러 겁쟁이라고 했어, 알료샤!"], ["Le mot de l’énigme is that I am a coward.", "르 모 드 레니그므, 그 수수께끼의 답은 내가 겁쟁이라는 거야."], ["‘It is not for such eagles to soar above the earth.’ It was he added that—he!", "'그런 독수리들이나 땅 위로 솟구쳐 날아오르는 거지.' 그자가 그 말을 덧붙였어—그자가!"], ["And Smerdyakov said the same.", "그리고 스메르댜코프도 똑같은 말을 했어."], ["He must be killed!", "그자는 죽어 마땅해!"], ["Katya despises me.", "카탸는 나를 경멸해."], ["I’ve seen that for a month past.", "나는 한 달 전부터 그걸 알고 있었어."], ["Even Lise will begin to despise me!", "리즈마저 나를 경멸하기 시작할 거야!"], ["‘You are going in order to be praised.’ That’s a brutal lie!", "'너는 칭송받으려고 가는 거다.' 그건 잔인한 거짓말이야!"], ["And you despise me too, Alyosha.", "그리고 너도 나를 경멸하잖아, 알료샤."], ["Now I am going to hate you again!", "이제 나는 너를 또다시 미워하게 될 거야!"], ["And I hate the monster, too!", "그리고 그 괴물도 미워해!"], ["I hate the monster!", "나는 그 괴물이 미워!"], ["I don’t want to save the monster.", "나는 그 괴물을 구하고 싶지 않아."], ["Let him rot in Siberia!", "시베리아에서 썩어 버리라지!"], ["He’s begun singing a hymn!", "그자는 찬송가나 부르기 시작했으니!"], ["Oh, to-morrow I’ll go, stand before them, and spit in their faces!”", "오, 내일 나는 가서, 그자들 앞에 서서, 그자들 낯짝에 침을 뱉어 줄 테다!\""]], [["He jumped up in a frenzy, flung off the towel, and fell to pacing up and down the room again.", "그는 광란에 사로잡혀 벌떡 일어나더니 수건을 내던지고 다시금 방 안을 이리저리 서성이기 시작했다."], ["Alyosha recalled what he had just said.", "알료샤는 형이 방금 한 말을 떠올렸다."], ["“I seem to be sleeping awake....", "\"나는 깨어 있으면서 잠들어 있는 것 같아…."], ["I walk, I speak, I see, but I am asleep.”", "나는 걷고, 말하고, 보지만, 잠들어 있어.\""], ["It seemed to be just like that now.", "지금이 바로 그런 모양이었다."], ["Alyosha did not leave him.", "알료샤는 형의 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["The thought passed through his mind to run for a doctor, but he was afraid to leave his brother alone: there was no one to whom he could leave him.", "의사를 부르러 달려갈까 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤지만, 형을 혼자 두기가 두려웠다. 형을 맡길 만한 사람이 아무도 없었던 것이다."], ["By degrees Ivan lost consciousness completely at last.", "마침내 이반은 차츰차츰 완전히 의식을 잃어 갔다."], ["He still went on talking, talking incessantly, but quite incoherently, and even articulated his words with difficulty.", "그는 여전히 끊임없이 말을 하고 또 했지만, 도무지 두서가 없었고, 발음조차 힘겹게 했다."], ["Suddenly he staggered violently; but Alyosha was in time to support him.", "갑자기 그가 몹시 비틀거렸다. 그러나 알료샤가 때맞춰 그를 부축했다."], ["Ivan let him lead him to his bed.", "이반은 그가 자기를 침대로 이끄는 대로 몸을 맡겼다."], ["Alyosha undressed him somehow and put him to bed.", "알료샤는 그럭저럭 형의 옷을 벗겨 침대에 눕혔다."], ["He sat watching over him for another two hours.", "그러고는 두 시간을 더 형을 지켜보며 앉아 있었다."], ["The sick man slept soundly, without stirring, breathing softly and evenly.", "병자는 미동도 없이, 부드럽고 고르게 숨을 쉬며 깊이 잠들어 있었다."], ["Alyosha took a pillow and lay down on the sofa, without undressing.", "알료샤는 베개 하나를 가져다 옷도 벗지 않은 채 소파에 누웠다."]], [["As he fell asleep he prayed for Mitya and Ivan.", "잠이 들면서 그는 미탸와 이반을 위해 기도했다."], ["He began to understand Ivan’s illness.", "그는 이반의 병을 차츰 이해하기 시작했다."], ["“The anguish of a proud determination.", "\"오만한 결단이 빚어내는 고뇌."], ["An earnest conscience!”", "진실한 양심!\""], ["God, in Whom he disbelieved, and His truth were gaining mastery over his heart, which still refused to submit.", "그가 믿지 않던 하느님과 그분의 진리가, 여전히 굴복하기를 거부하는 그의 마음을 사로잡아 가고 있는 것이었다."], ["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["the thought floated through Alyosha’s head as it lay on the pillow, “yes, if Smerdyakov is dead, no one will believe Ivan’s evidence; but he will go and give it.”", "베개를 벤 알료샤의 머릿속으로 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"그래, 스메르댜코프가 죽었으니 아무도 이반의 증언을 믿지 않겠지."], ["Alyosha smiled softly.", "하지만 형은 가서 증언할 거야.\""], ["“God will conquer!” he thought.", "알료샤는 부드럽게 미소 지었다."], ["“He will either rise up in the light of truth, or ...", "\"하느님이 승리하실 거야!\" 그는 생각했다."], ["he’ll perish in hate, revenging on himself and on every one his having served the cause he does not believe in,”", "\"형은 진리의 빛 속에 떨쳐 일어나거나, 아니면… 자기가 믿지 않는 대의를 위해 봉사했다는 것 때문에 자기 자신과 모든 사람에게 복수하면서 증오 속에서 스러져 가겠지.\""], ["Alyosha added bitterly, and again he prayed for Ivan.", "알료샤는 씁쓸하게 덧붙이고는 다시금 이반을 위해 기도했다."]], [["# Book XII. A Judicial Error Chapter I. The Fatal Day", "# Book XII. A Judicial Error Chapter I. The Fatal Day"]], [["At ten o’clock in the morning of the day following the events I have described, the trial of Dmitri Karamazov began in our district court.", "내가 앞서 서술한 사건들이 있은 이튿날 아침 열 시, 드미트리 카라마조프의 재판이 우리 고을의 지방 법원에서 시작되었다."]], [["I hasten to emphasize the fact that I am far from esteeming myself capable of reporting all that took place at the trial in full detail, or even in the actual order of events.", "서둘러 밝혀 두거니와, 나는 재판에서 벌어진 일을 빠짐없이 세세하게, 아니 실제로 벌어진 순서 그대로 보고할 능력을 지녔다고는 결코 자부하지 않는다."], ["I imagine that to mention everything with full explanation would fill a volume, even a very large one.", "모든 것을 충분한 설명을 곁들여 언급하자면 한 권의 책, 그것도 아주 두꺼운 책으로도 모자라리라 짐작한다."], ["And so I trust I may not be reproached, for confining myself to what struck me.", "그러니 내가 인상 깊었던 대목에만 매달린다 하여 책망받지 않기를 바랄 뿐이다."], ["I may have selected as of most interest what was of secondary importance, and may have omitted the most prominent and essential details.", "어쩌면 나는 부차적인 것을 가장 흥미로운 것으로 골라냈을 수도 있고, 가장 두드러지고 본질적인 세부들을 빠뜨렸을 수도 있다."], ["But I see I shall do better not to apologize.", "그러나 변명을 늘어놓지 않는 편이 나으리라는 것을 안다."], ["I will do my best and the reader will see for himself that I have done all I can.", "나는 최선을 다할 것이고, 독자는 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했음을 스스로 알게 될 것이다."]], [["And, to begin with, before entering the court, I will mention what surprised me most on that day.", "그리고 우선, 법정에 들어서기에 앞서, 그날 나를 가장 놀라게 한 것부터 언급하고자 한다."], ["Indeed, as it appeared later, every one was surprised at it, too.", "사실 나중에 드러난 바로는 누구나 그 점에 놀랐다."], ["We all knew that the affair had aroused great interest, that every one was burning with impatience for the trial to begin, that it had been a subject of talk, conjecture, exclamation and surmise for the last two months in local society.", "우리는 이 사건이 큰 관심을 불러일으켰다는 것을, 누구나 재판이 시작되기를 애타게 고대하고 있다는 것을, 지난 두 달 동안 그것이 우리 고을 사교계에서 화제와 억측과 탄성과 추측의 소재가 되어 왔다는 것을 모두 알고 있었다."], ["Every one knew, too, that the case had become known throughout Russia, but yet we had not imagined that it had aroused such burning, such intense, interest in every one, not only among ourselves, but all over Russia.", "또한 이 사건이 러시아 전역에 알려졌다는 것도 누구나 알았으나, 그것이 그토록 뜨겁고 그토록 격렬한 관심을, 그것도 우리뿐 아니라 러시아 전역에 걸쳐 누구에게나 불러일으켰을 줄은 미처 상상하지 못했다."], ["This became evident at the trial this day.", "그것이 바로 이날 재판에서 분명하게 드러났다."]], [["Visitors had arrived not only from the chief town of our province, but from several other Russian towns, as well as from Moscow and Petersburg.", "방청객들은 우리 도의 도청 소재지뿐 아니라 다른 여러 러시아 도시에서, 게다가 모스크바와 페테르부르크에서도 몰려왔다."], ["Among them were lawyers, ladies, and even several distinguished personages.", "그들 가운데에는 법률가들과 귀부인들, 심지어 몇몇 저명한 인사들도 끼어 있었다."], ["Every ticket of admission had been snatched up.", "입장권은 한 장도 남김없이 채여 나갔다."], ["A special place behind the table at which the three judges sat was set apart for the most distinguished and important of the men visitors; a row of arm-chairs had been placed there—something exceptional, which had never been allowed before.", "세 명의 판사가 앉는 탁자 뒤편에는 가장 저명하고 중요한 남자 방청객들을 위한 특별석이 따로 마련되었으니, 그곳에는 안락의자 한 줄이 놓였는데, 이는 전례 없는 이례적인 조치로서 예전에는 결코 허용된 적이 없는 일이었다."], ["A large proportion—not less than half of the public—were ladies.", "방청객 가운데 상당수가, 적어도 절반은 귀부인이었다."], ["There was such a large number of lawyers from all parts that they did not know where to seat them, for every ticket had long since been eagerly sought for and distributed.", "각지에서 몰려온 법률가들이 어찌나 많았던지 그들을 어디에 앉혀야 할지 몰랐으니, 입장권은 이미 오래전에 다투어 신청되고 배분된 터였다."], ["I saw at the end of the room, behind the platform, a special partition hurriedly put up, behind which all these lawyers were admitted, and they thought themselves lucky to have standing room there, for all chairs had been removed for the sake of space, and the crowd behind the partition stood throughout the case closely packed, shoulder to shoulder.", "나는 법정 끝, 단상 뒤편에 급히 세워진 특별 칸막이를 보았는데, 그 뒤로 이 모든 법률가들이 들어가도록 허락되었고, 그들은 거기 서 있을 자리만이라도 얻은 것을 다행으로 여겼으니, 공간을 마련하느라 의자가 죄다 치워진 까닭에 칸막이 뒤의 무리는 재판 내내 어깨를 맞댄 채 빽빽이 들어차 서 있었다."]], [["Some of the ladies, especially those who came from a distance, made their appearance in the gallery very smartly dressed, but the majority of the ladies were oblivious even of dress.", "귀부인들 가운데 일부는, 특히 먼 곳에서 온 이들은 회랑에 아주 맵시 있게 차려입고 모습을 드러냈으나, 대다수 귀부인은 옷차림 따위는 안중에도 없었다."], ["Their faces betrayed hysterical, intense, almost morbid, curiosity.", "그들의 얼굴에는 발작적이고 격렬하며 거의 병적이기까지 한 호기심이 드러나 있었다."], ["A peculiar fact—established afterwards by many observations—was that almost all the ladies, or, at least the vast majority of them, were on Mitya’s side and in favor of his being acquitted.", "한 가지 기묘한 사실은—나중에 여러 차례 관찰로 확인된 것이지만—거의 모든 귀부인이, 적어도 그 압도적 다수가 미탸 편이었고 그가 무죄로 풀려나기를 바랐다는 점이다."], ["This was perhaps chiefly owing to his reputation as a conqueror of female hearts.", "이는 어쩌면 그가 여인의 마음을 사로잡는 정복자로 이름이 났던 탓이 가장 클 것이다."], ["It was known that two women rivals were to appear in the case.", "두 명의 연적이 이 사건에 등장하리라는 것은 알려진 바였다."], ["One of them—Katerina Ivanovna—was an object of general interest.", "그중 한 사람인 카테리나 이바노브나는 세간의 관심의 표적이었다."], ["All sorts of extraordinary tales were told about her, amazing anecdotes of her passion for Mitya, in spite of his crime. Her pride and “aristocratic connections”", "그녀에 관해서는 온갖 기이한 이야기가, 그가 저지른 범죄에도 불구하고 미탸를 향한 그녀의 열정에 얽힌 놀라운 일화들이 떠돌았다."], ["were particularly insisted upon (she had called upon scarcely any one in the town). People said she intended to petition the Government for leave to accompany the criminal to Siberia and to be married to him somewhere in the mines.", "사람들은 특히 그녀의 자존심과 \"귀족 가문의 연줄\"을 강조했는데(그녀는 고을에서 거의 누구도 찾아가 인사한 적이 없었다), 그녀가 정부에 청원을 넣어 그 죄인을 따라 시베리아로 가서 어느 광산에서든 그와 결혼할 작정이라고들 했다."], ["The appearance of Grushenka in court was awaited with no less impatience.", "그루셴카가 법정에 나타나기를 기다리는 마음도 그에 못지않게 조바심으로 가득했다."], ["The public was looking forward with anxious curiosity to the meeting of the two rivals—the proud aristocratic girl and “the hetaira.”", "방청객들은 두 연적의 대면을, 곧 그 도도한 귀족 처녀와 \"그 화류계 여자\"의 맞닥뜨림을 애타는 호기심으로 고대하고 있었다."], ["But Grushenka was a more familiar figure to the ladies of the district than Katerina Ivanovna.", "그러나 그루셴카는 카테리나 이바노브나보다 이 고을 귀부인들에게 한결 낯익은 인물이었다."], ["They had already seen “the woman who had ruined Fyodor Pavlovitch and his unhappy son,” and all, almost without exception, wondered how father and son could be so in love with “such a very common, ordinary Russian girl, who was not even pretty.”", "그들은 이미 \"표도르 파블로비치와 그 불행한 아들을 파멸시킨 여자\"를 본 적이 있었고, 거의 예외 없이 모두가, 어떻게 아버지와 아들이 \"예쁘지도 않은 그토록 흔하디흔한 평범한 러시아 처녀\"를 그토록 사랑할 수 있었는지 의아해했다."]], [["In brief, there was a great deal of talk.", "요컨대 말이 많았다."], ["I know for a fact that there were several serious family quarrels on Mitya’s account in our town.", "나는 우리 고을에서 미탸 때문에 여러 차례 심각한 집안싸움이 벌어졌다는 것을 사실로 알고 있다."], ["Many ladies quarreled violently with their husbands over differences of opinion about the dreadful case, and it was only natural that the husbands of these ladies, far from being favorably disposed to the prisoner, should enter the court bitterly prejudiced against him.", "적잖은 귀부인이 이 끔찍한 사건을 두고 의견이 엇갈려 남편들과 격렬하게 다투었으니, 이런 부인의 남편들이 죄수에게 호의를 품기는커녕 도리어 그에게 모진 편견을 품고 법정에 들어선 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다."], ["In fact, one may say pretty certainly that the masculine, as distinguished from the feminine, part of the audience were biased against the prisoner.", "사실상 청중 가운데 여성과 구별되는 남성 쪽은 대체로 죄수에게 적대적이었다고 거의 단언할 수 있다."], ["There were numbers of severe, frowning, even vindictive faces.", "엄하고 찌푸린, 심지어 앙심에 찬 얼굴이 수두룩했다."], ["Mitya, indeed, had managed to offend many people during his stay in the town.", "실로 미탸는 고을에 머무는 동안 많은 이들의 비위를 거슬러 놓은 터였다."], ["Some of the visitors were, of course, in excellent spirits and quite unconcerned as to the fate of Mitya personally.", "물론 방청객들 가운데에는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 기분으로 미탸 개인의 운명 따위에는 전혀 무관심한 이들도 있었다."], ["But all were interested in the trial, and the majority of the men were certainly hoping for the conviction of the criminal, except perhaps the lawyers, who were more interested in the legal than in the moral aspect of the case.", "그러나 모두가 재판에 흥미를 느꼈고, 남자들 대다수는 분명 죄인이 유죄 판결을 받기를 바라고 있었으니, 다만 법률가들만은 예외였을 텐데, 이들은 사건의 도덕적 측면보다 법률적 측면에 더 관심이 쏠려 있었다."]], [["Everybody was excited at the presence of the celebrated lawyer, Fetyukovitch.", "모두가 저명한 변호사 페튜코비치의 등장에 술렁였다."], ["His talent was well known, and this was not the first time he had defended notorious criminal cases in the provinces.", "그의 재능은 익히 알려져 있었고, 그가 지방에서 세상을 떠들썩하게 한 형사 사건을 변호하는 것은 이번이 처음도 아니었다."], ["And if he defended them, such cases became celebrated and long remembered all over Russia.", "그리고 그가 변호를 맡으면 그런 사건은 명성을 얻어 러시아 전역에서 오래도록 기억되곤 했다."], ["There were stories, too, about our prosecutor and about the President of the Court.", "우리 고을 검사와 재판장에 관한 이야기도 떠돌았다."], ["It was said that Ippolit Kirillovitch was in a tremor at meeting Fetyukovitch, and that they had been enemies from the beginning of their careers in Petersburg, that though our sensitive prosecutor, who always considered that he had been aggrieved by some one in Petersburg because his talents had not been properly appreciated, was keenly excited over the Karamazov case, and was even dreaming of rebuilding his flagging fortunes by means of it, Fetyukovitch, they said, was his one anxiety.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 페튜코비치와 마주하게 되어 떨고 있다는 말이 있었고, 두 사람은 페테르부르크에서 경력을 쌓기 시작한 이래로 줄곧 원수지간이었다는 것이다. 또한 우리 고을의 예민한 검사는, 자기 재능이 제대로 인정받지 못했다 하여 페테르부르크의 누군가에게 늘 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여겨 왔으나, 이 카라마조프 사건에 자못 흥분해 있었고 심지어 이 사건을 발판 삼아 기울어 가는 자기 운명을 다시 일으켜 세우는 꿈까지 꾸고 있었는데, 페튜코비치야말로 그의 유일한 근심거리라고들 했다."], ["But these rumors were not quite just.", "그러나 이런 소문은 그다지 정확하지 못했다."], ["Our prosecutor was not one of those men who lose heart in face of danger.", "우리 검사는 위험 앞에서 기가 꺾이는 그런 부류의 인간이 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, his self-confidence increased with the increase of danger.", "오히려 위험이 커질수록 그의 자신감도 커졌다."], ["It must be noted that our prosecutor was in general too hasty and morbidly impressionable.", "짚어 두어야 할 것은, 우리 검사가 대체로 지나치게 성급하고 병적으로 예민한 사람이었다는 점이다."], ["He would put his whole soul into some case and work at it as though his whole fate and his whole fortune depended on its result.", "그는 어떤 사건에 자기 온 영혼을 쏟아부어, 마치 자기 운명 전체와 신상 전체가 그 결과에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 매달리곤 했다."], ["This was the subject of some ridicule in the legal world, for just by this characteristic our prosecutor had gained a wider notoriety than could have been expected from his modest position.", "이는 법조계에서 약간의 웃음거리가 되었으니, 바로 이런 기질 덕분에 우리 검사는 그 변변찮은 지위에서 기대할 수 있는 것보다 더 널리 이름을 떨치게 되었던 것이다."], ["People laughed particularly at his passion for psychology.", "사람들은 특히 그의 심리학에 대한 열정을 두고 비웃었다."], ["In my opinion, they were wrong, and our prosecutor was, I believe, a character of greater depth than was generally supposed.", "내 생각에 그들이 틀렸으니, 우리 검사는 세간에서 짐작하는 것보다 한층 깊이 있는 인물이었다고 나는 믿는다."], ["But with his delicate health he had failed to make his mark at the outset of his career and had never made up for it later.", "그러나 그는 허약한 건강 탓에 경력의 출발에서 두각을 나타내지 못했고, 나중에도 끝내 그것을 만회하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["As for the President of our Court, I can only say that he was a humane and cultured man, who had a practical knowledge of his work and progressive views.", "우리 고을 재판장으로 말하자면, 다만 그가 인간적이고 교양 있는 사람으로서 자기 일에 실무적인 지식을 갖추고 진보적인 견해를 지녔다고만 말할 수 있겠다."], ["He was rather ambitious, but did not concern himself greatly about his future career.", "그는 다소 야심이 있었으나 자기 앞날의 출세 따위에는 그다지 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["The great aim of his life was to be a man of advanced ideas.", "그의 인생의 큰 목표는 진보적 사상을 지닌 사람이 되는 것이었다."], ["He was, too, a man of connections and property.", "그는 또한 연줄과 재산을 갖춘 사람이었다."], ["He felt, as we learnt afterwards, rather strongly about the Karamazov case, but from a social, not from a personal standpoint.", "우리가 나중에 알게 된 바로는 그는 카라마조프 사건에 대해 자못 강한 감정을 품고 있었으나, 그것은 개인적 관점에서가 아니라 사회적 관점에서였다."], ["He was interested in it as a social phenomenon, in its classification and its character as a product of our social conditions, as typical of the national character, and so on, and so on.", "그는 이 사건을 하나의 사회 현상으로서, 그 분류와 우리 사회 조건의 산물로서의 성격, 또 민족성의 전형으로서 등등 그런 식으로 흥미롭게 여겼다."], ["His attitude to the personal aspect of the case, to its tragic significance and the persons involved in it, including the prisoner, was rather indifferent and abstract, as was perhaps fitting, indeed.", "사건의 개인적 측면에 대해서는, 그 비극적 의미와 죄수를 포함해 거기 얽힌 인물들에 대해서는 다소 무관심하고 추상적인 태도를 지녔으니, 실로 어쩌면 그것이 마땅한 일이었을 것이다."]], [["The court was packed and overflowing long before the judges made their appearance.", "판사들이 모습을 드러내기 한참 전부터 법정은 빈틈없이 들어차 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["Our court is the best hall in the town—spacious, lofty, and good for sound.", "우리 고을 법정은 고을에서 가장 좋은 홀로, 널찍하고 천장이 높으며 소리가 잘 울렸다."], ["On the right of the judges, who were on a raised platform, a table and two rows of chairs had been put ready for the jury.", "높은 단상에 자리한 판사들의 오른편에는 배심원을 위한 탁자 하나와 의자 두 줄이 마련되어 있었다."], ["On the left was the place for the prisoner and the counsel for the defense.", "왼편은 죄수와 변호인의 자리였다."], ["In the middle of the court, near the judges, was a table with the “material proofs.”", "법정 한가운데 판사들 가까이에는 \"물적 증거\"를 올려놓은 탁자가 있었다."], ["On it lay Fyodor Pavlovitch’s white silk dressing-gown, stained with blood; the fatal brass pestle with which the supposed murder had been committed; Mitya’s shirt, with a blood-stained sleeve; his coat, stained with blood in patches over the pocket in which he had put his handkerchief; the handkerchief itself, stiff with blood and by now quite yellow; the pistol loaded by Mitya at Perhotin’s with a view to suicide, and taken from him on the sly at Mokroe by Trifon Borissovitch; the envelope in which the three thousand roubles had been put ready for Grushenka, the narrow pink ribbon with which it had been tied, and many other articles I don’t remember.", "그 위에는 표도르 파블로비치의 피 묻은 흰 비단 실내복, 추정컨대 살인이 저질러졌다는 그 운명의 놋쇠 공이, 소맷자락에 피가 묻은 미탸의 셔츠, 손수건을 넣어 두었던 호주머니 위로 군데군데 피가 얼룩진 그의 외투, 피로 빳빳하게 굳어 이제는 누렇게 변해 버린 그 손수건 자체, 미탸가 자살할 작정으로 페르호틴의 집에서 장전했다가 모크로예에서 트리폰 보리소비치에게 몰래 빼앗긴 권총, 그루셴카에게 주려고 삼천 루블을 넣어 두었던 봉투와 그것을 묶었던 가느다란 분홍 리본, 그 밖에 내가 기억하지 못하는 여러 물건이 놓여 있었다."], ["In the body of the hall, at some distance, came the seats for the public.", "홀의 본체에는, 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에 방청객 좌석이 있었다."], ["But in front of the balustrade a few chairs had been placed for witnesses who remained in the court after giving their evidence.", "그러나 난간 앞쪽에는 증언을 마친 뒤에도 법정에 남은 증인들을 위해 의자 몇 개가 놓여 있었다."]], [["At ten o’clock the three judges arrived—the President, one honorary justice of the peace, and one other.", "열 시에 세 명의 판사가 도착했으니, 재판장과 명예 치안판사 한 명, 그리고 또 한 명이었다."], ["The prosecutor, of course, entered immediately after.", "물론 검사는 그 직후에 들어왔다."], ["The President was a short, stout, thick-set man of fifty, with a dyspeptic complexion, dark hair turning gray and cut short, and a red ribbon, of what Order I don’t remember.", "재판장은 키가 작고 통통하며 다부진 쉰 살가량의 사내로, 위장이 나쁜 듯한 안색에 희끗희끗해진 검은 머리를 짧게 깎았고, 무슨 훈장인지 기억나지 않는 붉은 리본을 달고 있었다."], ["The prosecutor struck me and the others, too, as looking particularly pale, almost green.", "검사는 나에게도, 다른 이들에게도 유난히 창백해 보였으니, 거의 새파랗다 할 정도였다."], ["His face seemed to have grown suddenly thinner, perhaps in a single night, for I had seen him looking as usual only two days before.", "그의 얼굴은 갑자기, 어쩌면 단 하룻밤 사이에 부쩍 야위어 버린 듯했으니, 이틀 전만 해도 나는 그가 평소와 다름없는 모습인 것을 보았던 것이다."], ["The President began with asking the court whether all the jury were present.", "재판장은 배심원이 모두 출석했는지를 법정에 묻는 것으로 시작했다."]], [["But I see I can’t go on like this, partly because some things I did not hear, others I did not notice, and others I have forgotten, but most of all because, as I have said before, I have literally no time or space to mention everything that was said and done.", "그러나 나는 더 이상 이런 식으로 이어 갈 수 없음을 깨닫는다. 한편으로는 내가 듣지 못한 것도, 알아채지 못한 것도, 잊어버린 것도 있기 때문이고, 무엇보다 앞서 말했듯이 거기서 오간 말과 행동을 죄다 언급할 시간도 지면도 정말이지 없기 때문이다."], ["I only know that neither side objected to very many of the jurymen.", "다만 내가 아는 것은, 어느 쪽도 배심원 다수에 대해서는 별다른 이의를 제기하지 않았다는 사실이다."], ["I remember the twelve jurymen—four were petty officials of the town, two were merchants, and six peasants and artisans of the town.", "나는 열두 명의 배심원을 기억하니, 넷은 고을의 하급 관리였고, 둘은 상인, 여섯은 고을의 농민과 직공이었다."], ["I remember, long before the trial, questions were continually asked with some surprise, especially by ladies: “Can such a delicate, complex and psychological case be submitted for decision to petty officials and even peasants?”", "재판이 시작되기 한참 전부터, 특히 귀부인들 사이에서 약간 놀란 듯한 물음이 끊임없이 던져지던 것을 나는 기억한다. \"이토록 미묘하고 복잡한 심리적 사건을 하급 관리들, 심지어 농민들의 판단에 맡길 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["and “What can an official, still more a peasant, understand in such an affair?”", "그리고 \"관리가, 더구나 농민이 이런 사건에서 무엇을 이해할 수 있겠는가?\""], ["All the four officials in the jury were, in fact, men of no consequence and of low rank.", "하는 것이었다. 사실 배심원에 든 네 명의 관리는 죄다 보잘것없고 직급이 낮은 자들이었다."], ["Except one who was rather younger, they were gray-headed men, little known in society, who had vegetated on a pitiful salary, and who probably had elderly, unpresentable wives and crowds of children, perhaps even without shoes and stockings.", "그중 비교적 젊은 한 명을 빼면 모두 백발이 성성한 사내들로, 사교계에는 거의 알려지지 않았고, 쥐꼬리만 한 봉급으로 근근이 연명해 왔으며, 십중팔구는 나이 들고 사람들 앞에 내놓기 민망한 아내와, 어쩌면 신발도 양말도 없는 한 떼의 아이들을 거느리고 있을 터였다."], ["At most, they spent their leisure over cards and, of course, had never read a single book.", "여가가 나면 기껏해야 카드놀이로 시간을 보낼 뿐, 물론 책이라곤 단 한 권도 읽어 본 적이 없었다."], ["The two merchants looked respectable, but were strangely silent and stolid.", "두 상인은 점잖아 보였으나 묘하게 말이 없고 무뚝뚝했다."], ["One of them was close-shaven, and was dressed in European style; the other had a small, gray beard, and wore a red ribbon with some sort of a medal upon it on his neck.", "그중 한 사람은 수염을 말끔히 깎고 유럽풍으로 차려입었고, 다른 한 사람은 희끗한 잔수염을 길렀으며 목에 무슨 메달인지가 달린 붉은 리본을 두르고 있었다."], ["There is no need to speak of the artisans and the peasants.", "직공과 농민에 대해서는 말할 필요도 없다."], ["The artisans of Skotoprigonyevsk are almost peasants, and even work on the land.", "스코토프리고니옙스크의 직공이란 거의 농민이나 다름없고, 더러는 땅을 부치기까지 한다."], ["Two of them also wore European dress, and, perhaps for that reason, were dirtier and more uninviting-looking than the others.", "그중 둘 또한 유럽풍으로 차려입었는데, 어쩌면 그 때문에 다른 이들보다 더 꾀죄죄하고 더 볼품없어 보였다."], ["So that one might well wonder, as I did as soon as I had looked at them, “what men like that could possibly make of such a case?”", "그러니 내가 그들을 보자마자 그러했듯, \"저런 사내들이 대체 이런 사건을 어찌 이해할 수 있겠는가?\" 하고 의아해할 만도 했다."], ["Yet their faces made a strangely imposing, almost menacing, impression; they were stern and frowning.", "그런데도 그들의 얼굴은 묘하게 위엄 있는, 거의 위협적이기까지 한 인상을 풍겼으니, 그들은 엄숙하고 잔뜩 찌푸린 표정이었다."]], [["At last the President opened the case of the murder of Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov.", "마침내 재판장이 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프 살해 사건의 심리를 개시했다."], ["I don’t quite remember how he described him.", "그가 그를 어떻게 묘사했는지는 잘 기억나지 않는다."], ["The court usher was told to bring in the prisoner, and Mitya made his appearance.", "정리(廷吏)에게 죄수를 데려오라는 지시가 내려졌고, 미탸가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["There was a hush through the court.", "법정에 정적이 흘렀다."], ["One could have heard a fly.", "파리 한 마리가 날아도 들릴 만했다."], ["I don’t know how it was with others, but Mitya made a most unfavorable impression on me.", "다른 이들은 어떠했는지 모르겠으나, 미탸는 나에게 더없이 좋지 못한 인상을 주었다."], ["He looked an awful dandy in a brand-new frock-coat.", "그는 새것이 분명한 프록코트 차림으로 굉장한 멋쟁이처럼 보였다."], ["I heard afterwards that he had ordered it in Moscow expressly for the occasion from his own tailor, who had his measure.", "나중에 들으니 그는 이 자리를 위해 일부러 모스크바에서, 제 치수를 아는 단골 재단사에게 그것을 주문했다고 했다."], ["He wore immaculate black kid gloves and exquisite linen.", "그는 흠 하나 없는 검은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 빼어난 아마포 옷을 받쳐 입고 있었다."], ["He walked in with his yard-long strides, looking stiffly straight in front of him, and sat down in his place with a most unperturbed air.", "그는 한 발에 일 야드는 됨 직한 성큼성큼한 걸음으로 들어와, 정면을 뻣뻣하게 똑바로 응시한 채 더없이 태연한 태도로 제자리에 앉았다."]], [["At the same moment the counsel for defense, the celebrated Fetyukovitch, entered, and a sort of subdued hum passed through the court.", "바로 그 순간 변호인인 저 저명한 페튜코비치가 들어섰고, 일종의 나직한 술렁임이 법정을 훑고 지나갔다."], ["He was a tall, spare man, with long thin legs, with extremely long, thin, pale fingers, clean-shaven face, demurely brushed, rather short hair, and thin lips that were at times curved into something between a sneer and a smile.", "그는 키가 크고 깡마른 사내로, 길고 가는 다리에, 유난히 길고 가늘고 핏기 없는 손가락을 지녔으며, 말끔히 면도한 얼굴에, 얌전히 빗어 넘긴 다소 짧은 머리, 그리고 때때로 비웃음과 미소의 중간쯤 되는 무엇인가로 휘어지는 얇은 입술의 소유자였다."], ["He looked about forty.", "마흔쯤 되어 보였다."], ["His face would have been pleasant, if it had not been for his eyes, which, in themselves small and inexpressive, were set remarkably close together, with only the thin, long nose as a dividing line between them.", "그의 얼굴은 그 눈만 아니었더라면 호감을 주었을 것이니, 그 눈은 그 자체로 작고 무표정한 데다 놀라우리만치 서로 바짝 붙어 있어, 가늘고 긴 코만이 둘 사이의 경계선 노릇을 할 뿐이었다."], ["In fact, there was something strikingly birdlike about his face.", "사실 그의 얼굴에는 어딘가 새를 빼닮은 데가 두드러졌다."], ["He was in evening dress and white tie.", "그는 야회복에 흰 넥타이 차림이었다."]], [["I remember the President’s first questions to Mitya, about his name, his calling, and so on.", "나는 재판장이 미탸에게 던진 첫 질문들을, 그의 이름과 직업 따위에 관한 것이었음을 기억한다."], ["Mitya answered sharply, and his voice was so unexpectedly loud that it made the President start and look at the prisoner with surprise.", "미탸는 또렷하게 대답했는데, 그 목소리가 어찌나 뜻밖에 우렁찼던지 재판장이 흠칫 놀라며 의아한 눈빛으로 죄수를 바라볼 정도였다."], ["Then followed a list of persons who were to take part in the proceedings—that is, of the witnesses and experts.", "그다음으로는 심리에 참여할 사람들의, 곧 증인과 감정인의 명단이 이어졌다."], ["It was a long list.", "긴 명단이었다."], ["Four of the witnesses were not present—Miüsov, who had given evidence at the preliminary inquiry, but was now in Paris; Madame Hohlakov and Maximov, who were absent through illness; and Smerdyakov, through his sudden death, of which an official statement from the police was presented.", "증인 가운데 네 사람은 출석하지 않았으니, 예심에서 진술했으나 지금은 파리에 가 있는 미우소프, 병환으로 불참한 호흘라코바 부인과 막시모프, 그리고 급작스러운 죽음으로 불참한 스메르댜코프였는데, 그에 관해서는 경찰의 공식 진술서가 제출되었다."], ["The news of Smerdyakov’s death produced a sudden stir and whisper in the court.", "스메르댜코프의 죽음 소식은 법정에 돌연 동요와 수군거림을 불러일으켰다."], ["Many of the audience, of course, had not heard of the sudden suicide.", "물론 방청객 가운데 많은 이들이 이 갑작스러운 자살에 대해 듣지 못했던 것이다."], ["What struck people most was Mitya’s sudden outburst.", "사람들을 가장 놀라게 한 것은 미탸의 돌연한 격발이었다."], ["As soon as the statement of Smerdyakov’s death was made, he cried out aloud from his place:", "스메르댜코프의 죽음에 관한 진술서가 낭독되자마자 그는 제자리에서 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“He was a dog and died like a dog!”", "\"개 같은 놈이 개처럼 뒈진 거지!\""]], [["I remember how his counsel rushed to him, and how the President addressed him, threatening to take stern measures, if such an irregularity were repeated.", "나는 그의 변호인이 그에게 달려가던 모습을, 그리고 재판장이 만약 그런 무례한 행동이 되풀이된다면 엄중한 조치를 취하겠다고 위협하며 그에게 이르던 것을 기억한다."], ["Mitya nodded and in a subdued voice repeated several times abruptly to his counsel, with no show of regret:", "미탸는 고개를 끄덕이며 후회하는 기색이라곤 없이 나직한 목소리로 변호인을 향해 여러 번 무뚝뚝하게 되뇌었다."]], [["“I won’t again, I won’t.", "\"다시는 안 그러겠소, 안 그래요."], ["It escaped me.", "그만 입에서 새 나간 거요."], ["I won’t do it again.”", "다시는 안 그러겠소.\""]], [["And, of course, this brief episode did him no good with the jury or the public.", "그리고 물론 이 짧은 소동은 배심원에게나 방청객에게나 그에게 아무런 득이 되지 못했다."], ["His character was displayed, and it spoke for itself.", "그의 성격이 고스란히 드러났고, 그것은 스스로 말해 주고 있었다."], ["It was under the influence of this incident that the opening statement was read.", "이 사건의 영향이 가시지 않은 가운데 공소장이 낭독되었다."], ["It was rather short, but circumstantial.", "그것은 다소 짧았으나 정황을 빠짐없이 담고 있었다."], ["It only stated the chief reasons why he had been arrested, why he must be tried, and so on.", "거기에는 다만 그가 체포된 주된 이유와 그가 재판에 부쳐져야 하는 까닭 따위만이 적혀 있었다."], ["Yet it made a great impression on me.", "그런데도 그것은 나에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["The clerk read it loudly and distinctly.", "서기는 그것을 크고 또렷한 목소리로 낭독했다."], ["The whole tragedy was suddenly unfolded before us, concentrated, in bold relief, in a fatal and pitiless light.", "비극 전체가 응축되어, 또렷한 윤곽을 띤 채, 운명적이고 가차 없는 빛 속에서 돌연 우리 앞에 펼쳐졌다."], ["I remember how, immediately after it had been read, the President asked Mitya in a loud impressive voice:", "나는 그것이 낭독되자마자 재판장이 크고 인상적인 목소리로 미탸에게 묻던 것을 기억한다."]], [["“Prisoner, do you plead guilty?”", "\"피고는 유죄를 인정하는가?\""]], [["Mitya suddenly rose from his seat.", "미탸가 돌연 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“I plead guilty to drunkenness and dissipation,”", "\"나는 술과 방탕에 대해서는 유죄를 인정하오.\""], ["he exclaimed, again in a startling, almost frenzied, voice, “to idleness and debauchery.", "그는 또다시 깜짝 놀랄 만한, 거의 광란에 가까운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"나태와 방종에 대해서도 인정하오."], ["I meant to become an honest man for good, just at the moment when I was struck down by fate.", "나는 운명의 일격을 받아 쓰러진 바로 그 순간, 영영 정직한 사람이 되려고 마음먹은 참이었소."], ["But I am not guilty of the death of that old man, my enemy and my father.", "그러나 그 노인의, 내 원수이자 내 아버지인 그자의 죽음에 대해서는 나는 죄가 없소."], ["No, no, I am not guilty of robbing him!", "아니, 아니오, 나는 그를 턴 죄도 없소!"], ["I could not be.", "나는 그럴 수 없었소."], ["Dmitri Karamazov is a scoundrel, but not a thief.”", "드미트리 카라마조프는 비열한 놈이지만 도둑은 아니오.\""]], [["He sat down again, visibly trembling all over.", "그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 온몸이 눈에 띄게 떨리고 있었다."], ["The President again briefly, but impressively, admonished him to answer only what was asked, and not to go off into irrelevant exclamations.", "재판장은 또다시 짤막하지만 인상 깊게, 묻는 말에만 대답하고 부질없는 외침으로 빠지지 말라고 그를 타일렀다."], ["Then he ordered the case to proceed.", "그러고는 심리를 진행하라고 명했다."], ["All the witnesses were led up to take the oath.", "모든 증인이 선서를 위해 앞으로 인도되었다."], ["Then I saw them all together.", "그제야 나는 그들 모두를 한자리에서 보았다."], ["The brothers of the prisoner were, however, allowed to give evidence without taking the oath.", "다만 죄수의 형제들은 선서 없이 증언하도록 허락받았다."], ["After an exhortation from the priest and the President, the witnesses were led away and were made to sit as far as possible apart from one another.", "사제와 재판장의 훈계가 있은 뒤, 증인들은 따로 인도되어 되도록 서로 멀찍이 떨어져 앉도록 조치되었다."], ["Then they began calling them up one by one.", "그러고 나서 그들을 한 사람씩 불러내기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter II. Dangerous Witnesses", "# Chapter II. Dangerous Witnesses"]], [["I do not know whether the witnesses for the defense and for the prosecution were separated into groups by the President, and whether it was arranged to call them in a certain order.", "피고 측 증인과 검찰 측 증인이 재판장의 손으로 무리 지어 갈라졌는지, 또 일정한 순서를 정해 호명하도록 짜여 있었는지 나로서는 알 수 없다. 그러나 분명 그렇게 되어 있었을 것이다."], ["But no doubt it was so. I only know that the witnesses for the prosecution were called first.", "다만 내가 아는 것은 검찰 측 증인들이 먼저 호명되었다는 사실뿐이다."], ["I repeat I don’t intend to describe all the questions step by step.", "거듭 말하거니와, 나는 모든 신문(訊問) 과정을 하나하나 차례대로 기술할 생각은 없다."], ["Besides, my account would be to some extent superfluous, because in the speeches for the prosecution and for the defense the whole course of the evidence was brought together and set in a strong and significant light, and I took down parts of those two remarkable speeches in full, and will quote them in due course, together with one extraordinary and quite unexpected episode, which occurred before the final speeches, and undoubtedly influenced the sinister and fatal outcome of the trial.", "게다가 내 설명은 어느 정도 군더더기가 될 터이니, 검찰 측과 피고 측의 논고와 변론에서 증거의 전 과정이 한데 모여 강렬하고 의미심장한 빛 속에 놓이게 되었기 때문이며, 나는 그 두 편의 주목할 만한 변설을 부분적으로 빠짐없이 받아 적어 두었기에 적절한 대목에서 인용하려 하는데, 최후 변론 직전에 일어나 이 재판의 음울하고 치명적인 결말에 의심할 바 없이 영향을 끼친 어느 기이하고 전혀 예기치 못한 사건도 함께 옮길 것이다."]], [["I will only observe that from the first moments of the trial one peculiar characteristic of the case was conspicuous and observed by all, that is, the overwhelming strength of the prosecution as compared with the arguments the defense had to rely upon.", "다만 나는 재판이 시작된 첫 순간부터 이 사건의 한 가지 독특한 특징이 두드러져 모두의 눈에 들어왔다는 점만은 짚어 두려 하는데, 그것은 곧 피고 측이 의지할 수 있는 논거에 비해 검찰 측의 위력이 압도적이었다는 사실이다."], ["Every one realized it from the first moment that the facts began to group themselves round a single point, and the whole horrible and bloody crime was gradually revealed.", "사실들이 한 점을 중심으로 모여들기 시작하고 그 끔찍하고 피비린내 나는 범죄 전모가 차츰 드러나는 첫 순간부터 모두가 그것을 깨달았다."], ["Every one, perhaps, felt from the first that the case was beyond dispute, that there was no doubt about it, that there could be really no discussion, and that the defense was only a matter of form, and that the prisoner was guilty, obviously and conclusively guilty.", "어쩌면 모두가 처음부터 이 사건은 논쟁의 여지가 없으며, 의심할 것이 없고, 실로 토론할 거리가 없으며, 변론이란 형식에 지나지 않고, 피고는 유죄, 명백하고도 결정적으로 유죄라고 느꼈을 것이다."], ["I imagine that even the ladies, who were so impatiently longing for the acquittal of the interesting prisoner, were at the same time, without exception, convinced of his guilt.", "짐작건대 그토록 안달하며 이 흥미로운 피고의 무죄 방면을 갈망하던 부인네들조차도, 동시에 예외 없이 그의 죄를 확신하고 있었으리라."], ["What’s more, I believe they would have been mortified if his guilt had not been so firmly established, as that would have lessened the effect of the closing scene of the criminal’s acquittal.", "더욱이 나는 그들이, 만약 그의 죄가 그토록 확고하게 입증되지 않았더라면 도리어 분개했으리라고 믿는데, 그렇게 되면 죄인의 무죄 방면이라는 마지막 장면의 효과가 줄어들었을 것이기 때문이다."], ["That he would be acquitted, all the ladies, strange to say, were firmly persuaded up to the very last moment.", "그가 무죄로 풀려나리라는 것을, 이상한 말이지만, 모든 부인네들은 바로 마지막 순간까지 굳게 믿고 있었다."], ["“He is guilty, but he will be acquitted, from motives of humanity, in accordance with the new ideas, the new sentiments that had come into fashion,”", "\"그는 유죄지만, 인도주의적 동기에서, 유행하게 된 새로운 사상과 새로운 정서에 따라 무죄로 풀려날 거예요.\" 이런 식으로 그들은 끝없이 떠들어 댔다."], ["and so on, and so on. And that was why they had crowded into the court so impatiently.", "그래서 그들은 그토록 안달하며 법정으로 몰려들었던 것이다."], ["The men were more interested in the contest between the prosecutor and the famous Fetyukovitch.", "남자들은 검사와 저명한 페튜코비치의 대결에 더 큰 관심을 기울였다."], ["All were wondering and asking themselves what could even a talent like Fetyukovitch’s make of such a desperate case; and so they followed his achievements, step by step, with concentrated attention.", "모두가 페튜코비치 같은 재능이라 한들 이토록 절망적인 사건을 두고 무엇을 해낼 수 있을지 의아해하며 스스로 묻고 있었으니, 그래서 그들은 정신을 집중하여 그의 수완을 한 걸음 한 걸음 좇았다."]], [["But Fetyukovitch remained an enigma to all up to the very end, up to his speech.", "그러나 페튜코비치는 끝까지, 그의 변론에 이르기까지 모두에게 수수께끼로 남아 있었다."], ["Persons of experience suspected that he had some design, that he was working towards some object, but it was almost impossible to guess what it was.", "경험 많은 이들은 그가 무슨 복안을 품고 있으며, 어떤 목표를 향해 일을 꾸미고 있다고 짐작했으나, 그것이 무엇인지 알아맞히기란 거의 불가능했다."], ["His confidence and self-reliance were unmistakable, however.", "그러나 그의 확신과 자신감만큼은 누가 봐도 분명했다."], ["Every one noticed with pleasure, moreover, that he, after so short a stay, not more than three days, perhaps, among us, had so wonderfully succeeded in mastering the case and “had studied it to a nicety.”", "게다가 모두가 흐뭇하게 알아챈 것은, 그가 우리 고장에 머문 지 기껏해야 사흘 남짓밖에 안 되는 그토록 짧은 기간 만에 놀랍도록 사건을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 \"세세한 부분까지 빈틈없이 연구해 두었다\"는 사실이었다."], ["People described with relish, afterwards, how cleverly he had “taken down”", "사람들은 훗날 그가 검찰 측 증인들을 얼마나 영리하게 \"코를 납작하게\""], ["all the witnesses for the prosecution, and as far as possible perplexed them and, what’s more, had aspersed their reputation and so depreciated the value of their evidence.", "만들고, 가능한 한 그들을 당황시켰으며, 더욱이 그들의 평판에 먹칠을 하여 증언의 가치를 떨어뜨렸는지 신이 나서 묘사하곤 했다."], ["But it was supposed that he did this rather by way of sport, so to speak, for professional glory, to show nothing had been omitted of the accepted methods, for all were convinced that he could do no real good by such disparagement of the witnesses, and probably was more aware of this than any one, having some idea of his own in the background, some concealed weapon of defense, which he would suddenly reveal when the time came.", "그러나 사람들은 그가 이런 짓을 말하자면 일종의 유희 삼아, 직업적 영예를 위해, 통상적인 방법에서 빠뜨린 것이 하나도 없음을 보이려고 한 것이라 짐작했으니, 모두가 그런 증인 깎아내리기로는 실질적인 득을 볼 수 없다고 확신했고, 그 자신도 누구보다 잘 알고 있을 것이며, 배후에 그만의 어떤 복안, 곧 때가 되면 불쑥 드러낼 어떤 감추어진 변호의 무기를 지니고 있으리라 여겼기 때문이다."], ["But meanwhile, conscious of his strength, he seemed to be diverting himself.", "그러나 그동안 그는 자기 힘을 의식한 채 그저 즐기고 있는 듯 보였다."]], [["So, for instance, when Grigory, Fyodor Pavlovitch’s old servant, who had given the most damning piece of evidence about the open door, was examined, the counsel for the defense positively fastened upon him when his turn came to question him.", "이를테면 표도르 파블로비치의 늙은 하인으로서 열린 문에 관해 가장 결정적인 증언을 한 그리고리가 신문을 받을 때, 자기 차례가 되어 질문할 때가 되자 변호인은 그를 그야말로 물고 늘어졌다."], ["It must be noted that Grigory entered the hall with a composed and almost stately air, not the least disconcerted by the majesty of the court or the vast audience listening to him.", "짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그리고리가 침착하고 거의 위엄에 찬 태도로 법정에 들어섰으며, 법정의 위엄이나 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 방대한 청중에 조금도 주눅 들지 않았다는 점이다."], ["He gave evidence with as much confidence as though he had been talking with his Marfa, only perhaps more respectfully.", "그는 마치 자기 아내 마르파와 이야기하듯, 다만 어쩌면 조금 더 공손하게, 자신만만하게 증언했다."], ["It was impossible to make him contradict himself.", "그를 모순에 빠뜨리기란 불가능했다."], ["The prosecutor questioned him first in detail about the family life of the Karamazovs.", "검사는 먼저 카라마조프 집안의 가정생활에 관해 세세하게 그를 신문했다."], ["The family picture stood out in lurid colors.", "그 가정의 모습이 음산한 빛깔로 또렷이 드러났다."], ["It was plain to ear and eye that the witness was guileless and impartial.", "이 증인이 꾸밈없고 공정하다는 것이 귀로 들어도 눈으로 보아도 분명했다."], ["In spite of his profound reverence for the memory of his deceased master, he yet bore witness that he had been unjust to Mitya and “hadn’t brought up his children as he should.", "그는 작고한 주인의 기억에 깊은 경의를 품고 있었음에도, 주인이 미탸에게 부당했으며 \"마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 자식들을 키우지 않았다\"고 증언했다."], ["He’d have been devoured by lice when he was little, if it hadn’t been for me,”", "\"도련님이 어렸을 적, 제가 없었더라면 이가 들끓어 잡아먹혔을 겁니다.\""], ["he added, describing Mitya’s early childhood.", "그는 미탸의 어린 시절을 묘사하며 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["“It wasn’t fair either of the father to wrong his son over his mother’s property, which was by right his.”", "\"그리고 아버지가 권리상 마땅히 아들 것이었던 어미의 재산을 두고 아들에게 부당하게 군 것도 옳지 못한 일이었지요.\""]], [["In reply to the prosecutor’s question what grounds he had for asserting that Fyodor Pavlovitch had wronged his son in their money relations, Grigory, to the surprise of every one, had no proof at all to bring forward, but he still persisted that the arrangement with the son was “unfair,”", "표도르 파블로비치가 금전 관계에서 아들에게 부당하게 굴었다고 단언하는 근거가 무엇이냐는 검사의 물음에, 그리고리는 모두를 놀라게도 내놓을 만한 증거라곤 하나도 없었으나, 그래도 아들과의 재산 처리가 \"공정치 못했고\""], ["and that he ought “to have paid him several thousand roubles more.”", "주인이 \"수천 루블은 더 주었어야 했다\"고 고집스레 주장했다."], ["I must note, by the way, that the prosecutor asked this question whether Fyodor Pavlovitch had really kept back part of Mitya’s inheritance with marked persistence of all the witnesses who could be asked it, not excepting Alyosha and Ivan, but he obtained no exact information from any one; all alleged that it was so, but were unable to bring forward any distinct proof.", "곁들여 짚어 둘 것은, 검사가 표도르 파블로비치가 정말로 미탸의 유산 일부를 떼어먹었는지를 묻는 이 질문을, 알료샤와 이반까지 빼놓지 않고 물어볼 수 있는 모든 증인에게 두드러진 집요함으로 던졌으나, 누구에게서도 정확한 정보를 얻지 못했다는 사실이다. 모두가 그렇다고 주장은 했지만, 누구도 뚜렷한 증거 하나 내놓지 못했다."], ["Grigory’s description of the scene at the dinner-table, when Dmitri had burst in and beaten his father, threatening to come back to kill him, made a sinister impression on the court, especially as the old servant’s composure in telling it, his parsimony of words and peculiar phraseology, were as effective as eloquence.", "그리고리가 묘사한 만찬 식탁에서의 장면, 곧 드미트리가 쳐들어와 아버지를 때리고 다시 와서 죽이겠노라 위협하던 그 장면은 법정에 불길한 인상을 남겼으니, 특히 그것을 이야기하는 늙은 하인의 침착함과 말의 인색함, 독특한 말투가 웅변 못지않게 효과적이었기 때문이다."], ["He observed that he was not angry with Mitya for having knocked him down and struck him on the face; he had forgiven him long ago, he said.", "그는 미탸가 자기를 때려눕히고 얼굴을 친 것을 두고 화나지 않았으며, 이미 오래전에 용서했노라고 말했다."], ["Of the deceased Smerdyakov he observed, crossing himself, that he was a lad of ability, but stupid and afflicted, and, worse still, an infidel, and that it was Fyodor Pavlovitch and his elder son who had taught him to be so.", "작고한 스메르댜코프에 관해서는 성호를 그으며, 능력은 있는 녀석이었으나 어리석고 병약했고, 더 나쁘게는 신을 믿지 않는 자였으며, 그렇게 만든 것은 표도르 파블로비치와 그 맏아들이라고 말했다."], ["But he defended Smerdyakov’s honesty almost with warmth, and related how Smerdyakov had once found the master’s money in the yard, and, instead of concealing it, had taken it to his master, who had rewarded him with a “gold piece” for it, and trusted him implicitly from that time forward.", "그러나 그는 스메르댜코프의 정직함만큼은 거의 열을 내며 변호했으니, 언젠가 스메르댜코프가 마당에서 주인의 돈을 발견했을 때 그것을 감추기는커녕 주인에게 가져갔고, 주인은 그 대가로 \"금화 한 닢\"을 상으로 주고 그때부터 그를 전적으로 신임하게 되었다는 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["He maintained obstinately that the door into the garden had been open.", "그는 정원으로 통하는 문이 열려 있었노라고 고집스레 주장했다."], ["But he was asked so many questions that I can’t recall them all.", "그러나 그에게 던져진 질문이 어찌나 많았던지 나로서는 그것을 다 떠올릴 수가 없다."]], [["At last the counsel for the defense began to cross-examine him, and the first question he asked was about the envelope in which Fyodor Pavlovitch was supposed to have put three thousand roubles for “a certain person.”", "마침내 변호인이 그를 반대신문하기 시작했는데, 그가 처음 던진 질문은 표도르 파블로비치가 \"어떤 사람\"을 위해 삼천 루블을 넣어 두었다는 그 봉투에 관한 것이었다."], ["“Have you ever seen it, you, who were for so many years in close attendance on your master?”", "\"그토록 오랜 세월 주인을 가까이 모셔 온 당신은, 그것을 본 적이 있습니까?\""], ["Grigory answered that he had not seen it and had never heard of the money from any one “till everybody was talking about it.”", "그리고리는 그것을 본 적이 없으며 \"모두가 그 말을 떠들기 전까지는\" 누구에게서도 그 돈에 관해 들어 본 적이 없다고 대답했다."], ["This question about the envelope Fetyukovitch put to every one who could conceivably have known of it, as persistently as the prosecutor asked his question about Dmitri’s inheritance, and got the same answer from all, that no one had seen the envelope, though many had heard of it.", "봉투에 관한 이 질문을, 페튜코비치는 검사가 드미트리의 유산에 관해 캐묻던 것만큼이나 집요하게, 그것을 알 만한 모든 사람에게 던졌고, 모두에게서 똑같은 답을 얻었으니, 곧 많은 이가 그에 관해 듣기는 했으나 봉투를 본 사람은 아무도 없다는 것이었다."], ["From the beginning every one noticed Fetyukovitch’s persistence on this subject.", "처음부터 모두가 이 문제에 대한 페튜코비치의 집요함을 알아챘다."]], [["“Now, with your permission I’ll ask you a question,”", "\"자, 허락하신다면 한 가지 묻겠습니다.\""], ["Fetyukovitch said, suddenly and unexpectedly.", "페튜코비치가 불쑥 뜻밖의 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Of what was that balsam, or, rather, decoction, made, which, as we learn from the preliminary inquiry, you used on that evening to rub your lumbago, in the hope of curing it?”", "\"그날 저녁, 예심 조서에서 알 수 있듯, 당신이 요통을 고쳐 볼까 하여 문지르는 데 썼다는 그 향유, 아니 정확히는 그 달인 물은 무엇으로 만든 것입니까?\""]], [["Grigory looked blankly at the questioner, and after a brief silence muttered, “There was saffron in it.”", "그리고리는 멍하니 질문자를 바라보다가, 잠시 침묵한 끝에 중얼거렸다. \"사프란이 들어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Nothing but saffron?", "\"사프란뿐이오?"], ["Don’t you remember any other ingredient?”", "다른 재료는 기억나지 않소?\""]], [["“There was milfoil in it, too.”", "\"톱풀도 들어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“And pepper perhaps?”", "\"그리고 후추도 들어 있었던 것 아니오?\""], ["Fetyukovitch queried.", "페튜코비치가 캐물었다."]], [["“Yes, there was pepper, too.”", "\"예, 후추도 들어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Etcetera.", "\"기타 등등."], ["And all dissolved in vodka?” “In spirit.”", "그리고 그 모든 것을 보드카에 녹였소?\""]], [["There was a faint sound of laughter in the court.", "\"독한 술에 녹였습니다.\" 법정에 희미한 웃음소리가 일었다."]], [["“You see, in spirit.", "\"그렇군요, 독한 술에 말이지요."], ["After rubbing your back, I believe, you drank what was left in the bottle with a certain pious prayer, only known to your wife?”", "등을 문지른 뒤에는, 내가 알기로, 당신 아내만 아는 어떤 경건한 기도를 외고서 병에 남은 것을 마셨다지요?\""]], [["“I did.”", "\"마셨습니다.\""]], [["“Did you drink much?", "\"많이 마셨소?"], ["Roughly speaking, a wine-glass or two?”", "대충 말해서, 포도주 잔으로 한두 잔쯤?\""]], [["“It might have been a tumbler-full.”", "\"큰 잔으로 가득 한 잔쯤 됐을 겁니다.\""]], [["“A tumbler-full, even.", "\"큰 잔으로 가득 한 잔이라."], ["Perhaps a tumbler and a half?”", "어쩌면 한 잔 반쯤?\""]], [["Grigory did not answer.", "그리고리는 대답하지 않았다."], ["He seemed to see what was meant.", "무슨 뜻인지 알아챈 듯했다."]], [["“A glass and a half of neat spirit—is not at all bad, don’t you think?", "\"독한 술을 그대로 한 잔 반이면—제법 적지 않은 양이지요, 그렇지 않소?"], ["You might see the gates of heaven open, not only the door into the garden?”", "정원으로 통하는 문이 아니라 천국의 문이 열리는 것을 보았을 수도 있지 않겠소?\""]], [["Grigory remained silent.", "그리고리는 잠자코 있었다."], ["There was another laugh in the court.", "법정에 또 한 차례 웃음이 일었다."], ["The President made a movement.", "재판장이 몸을 움직였다."]], [["“Do you know for a fact,”", "\"당신이 열린 문을 보았을 때 깨어 있었는지 아닌지, 확실히 압니까?\""], ["Fetyukovitch persisted, “whether you were awake or not when you saw the open door?”", "페튜코비치가 집요하게 물었다."]], [["“I was on my legs.”", "\"두 발로 서 있었습니다.\""]], [["“That’s not a proof that you were awake.”", "\"그것은 당신이 깨어 있었다는 증거가 못 됩니다.\""], ["(There was again laughter in the court.) “Could you have answered at that moment, if any one had asked you a question—for instance, what year it is?”", "(법정에 다시 웃음이 일었다.) \"그 순간 누가 당신에게 무언가를 물었더라면 대답할 수 있었겠소—이를테면 올해가 몇 년도냐고 물었다면?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“And what year is it, Anno Domini, do you know?”", "\"그럼 지금이 그리스도 기원 몇 년도인지는 아시오?\""]], [["Grigory stood with a perplexed face, looking straight at his tormentor.", "그리고리는 어리둥절한 얼굴로 자기를 괴롭히는 자를 똑바로 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Strange to say, it appeared he really did not know what year it was.", "이상한 일이지만, 그는 정말로 올해가 몇 년도인지 모르는 것 같았다."]], [["“But perhaps you can tell me how many fingers you have on your hands?”", "\"그렇다면 혹시 당신 손에 손가락이 몇 개인지는 말할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“I am a servant,”", "\"저는 하인입니다.\""], ["Grigory said suddenly, in a loud and distinct voice.", "그리고리가 불쑥, 크고 또렷한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“If my betters think fit to make game of me, it is my duty to suffer it.”", "\"윗분들께서 저를 놀림감으로 삼는 게 마땅하다 여기신다면, 저는 그것을 참는 게 도리지요.\""]], [["Fetyukovitch was a little taken aback, and the President intervened, reminding him that he must ask more relevant questions.", "페튜코비치는 다소 멈칫했고, 재판장이 끼어들어 좀 더 사건과 관련된 질문을 해야 한다고 일깨웠다."], ["Fetyukovitch bowed with dignity and said that he had no more questions to ask of the witness.", "페튜코비치는 위엄 있게 고개를 숙이고, 증인에게 더는 물을 것이 없다고 말했다."], ["The public and the jury, of course, were left with a grain of doubt in their minds as to the evidence of a man who might, while undergoing a certain cure, have seen “the gates of heaven,” and who did not even know what year he was living in.", "물론 방청객과 배심원들의 마음속에는 한 가닥 의심이 남았으니, 어떤 치료를 하던 와중에 \"천국의 문\"을 보았을지도 모르고 자기가 사는 해가 몇 년도인지조차 모르는 사내의 증언에 대한 의심이었다."], ["But before Grigory left the box another episode occurred.", "그러나 그리고리가 증언대를 떠나기 전에 또 하나의 사건이 벌어졌다."], ["The President, turning to the prisoner, asked him whether he had any comment to make on the evidence of the last witness.", "재판장이 피고를 향해, 마지막 증인의 증언에 대해 할 말이 있느냐고 물었던 것이다."]], [["“Except about the door, all he has said is true,”", "\"문에 관한 것만 빼면, 저 사람이 한 말은 다 사실입니다.\""], ["cried Mitya, in a loud voice.", "미탸가 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“For combing the lice off me, I thank him; for forgiving my blows, I thank him.", "\"이를 잡아 준 것, 감사합니다. 제 매질을 용서해 준 것, 감사합니다."], ["The old man has been honest all his life and as faithful to my father as seven hundred poodles.”", "저 노인은 평생 정직했고, 푸들 칠백 마리만큼이나 제 아버지에게 충직했습니다.\""]], [["“Prisoner, be careful in your language,”", "\"피고는 말을 삼가시오.\""], ["the President admonished him.", "재판장이 그를 타일렀다."]], [["“I am not a poodle,”", "\"저는 푸들이 아닙니다.\""], ["Grigory muttered.", "그리고리가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“All right, it’s I am a poodle myself,”", "\"좋아요, 푸들은 바로 나요.\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“If it’s an insult, I take it to myself and I beg his pardon.", "\"그게 모욕이라면, 그건 내게 돌리고 그에게 용서를 빌겠소."], ["I was a beast and cruel to him.", "나는 저 사람에게 짐승처럼 잔인하게 굴었소."], ["I was cruel to Æsop too.”", "이솝에게도 잔인했지요.\""]], [["“What Æsop?”", "\"이솝이라니, 무슨 이솝 말이오?\""], ["the President asked sternly again.", "재판장이 다시 엄하게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, Pierrot ...", "\"아, 피에로 말입니다……"], ["my father, Fyodor Pavlovitch.”", "제 아버지, 표도르 파블로비치 말입니다.\""]], [["The President again and again warned Mitya impressively and very sternly to be more careful in his language.", "재판장은 미탸에게 거듭, 인상 깊게 그리고 매우 엄하게 말을 더 삼가라고 경고했다."]], [["“You are injuring yourself in the opinion of your judges.”", "\"당신은 재판관들의 눈에 스스로 해를 끼치고 있소.\""]], [["The counsel for the defense was equally clever in dealing with the evidence of Rakitin.", "변호인은 라키틴의 증언을 다루는 데도 똑같이 영리했다."], ["I may remark that Rakitin was one of the leading witnesses and one to whom the prosecutor attached great significance.", "짚어 둘 것은, 라키틴이 주요 증인 가운데 하나였으며, 검사가 큰 의미를 두던 인물이었다는 사실이다."], ["It appeared that he knew everything; his knowledge was amazing, he had been everywhere, seen everything, talked to everybody, knew every detail of the biography of Fyodor Pavlovitch and all the Karamazovs.", "그는 모든 것을 다 아는 듯했고, 그의 박식함은 놀라웠으니, 어디든 가 보았고 무엇이든 보았으며 누구와도 이야기를 나눴고, 표도르 파블로비치와 카라마조프 집안 사람들 전부의 일대기를 세세한 데까지 꿰고 있었다."], ["Of the envelope, it is true, he had only heard from Mitya himself.", "봉투에 관한 한, 사실 그는 미탸 본인에게서 들었을 뿐이었다."], ["But he described minutely Mitya’s exploits in the “Metropolis,” all his compromising doings and sayings, and told the story of Captain Snegiryov’s “wisp of tow.”", "그러나 그는 미탸가 \"메트로폴리스\"에서 벌인 행적, 평판을 떨어뜨릴 만한 온갖 행동과 말들을 세세하게 묘사했고, 스네기료프 이등 대위의 \"삼 부스러기\""], ["But even Rakitin could say nothing positive about Mitya’s inheritance, and confined himself to contemptuous generalities.", "이야기도 들려주었다. 그러나 라키틴조차 미탸의 유산에 관해서는 확정적인 것을 아무것도 말하지 못한 채, 경멸 어린 일반론에 그쳤다."]], [["“Who could tell which of them was to blame, and which was in debt to the other, with their crazy Karamazov way of muddling things so that no one could make head or tail of it?”", "\"누가 그들 가운데 누가 잘못했고 누가 누구에게 빚졌는지를 알 수 있겠습니까, 아무도 갈피를 잡을 수 없게끔 모든 일을 뒤죽박죽으로 만드는 그 미치광이 같은 카라마조프 식 행태로 말입니다.\""], ["He attributed the tragic crime to the habits that had become ingrained by ages of serfdom and the distressed condition of Russia, due to the lack of appropriate institutions.", "그는 이 비극적 범죄를, 오랜 농노제 세월 동안 뿌리 깊이 박힌 습성과, 적절한 제도의 부재에서 비롯된 러시아의 곤궁한 처지 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["He was, in fact, allowed some latitude of speech.", "사실 그에게는 다소 자유로운 발언이 허용되었다."], ["This was the first occasion on which Rakitin showed what he could do, and attracted notice.", "라키틴이 자기 역량을 펼쳐 보이며 주목을 끈 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["The prosecutor knew that the witness was preparing a magazine article on the case, and afterwards in his speech, as we shall see later, quoted some ideas from the article, showing that he had seen it already.", "검사는 이 증인이 이 사건에 관한 잡지 기고문을 준비하고 있음을 알고 있었고, 나중에 보겠지만 자기 논고에서 그 기고문의 몇몇 견해를 인용하여, 자신이 이미 그것을 읽었음을 드러냈다."], ["The picture drawn by the witness was a gloomy and sinister one, and greatly strengthened the case for the prosecution.", "이 증인이 그린 그림은 음울하고 불길한 것이어서, 검찰 측의 입지를 크게 강화했다."], ["Altogether, Rakitin’s discourse fascinated the public by its independence and the extraordinary nobility of its ideas.", "대체로 라키틴의 변설은 그 독자성과 사상의 비범한 고결함으로 방청객을 사로잡았다."], ["There were even two or three outbreaks of applause when he spoke of serfdom and the distressed condition of Russia.", "그가 농노제와 러시아의 곤궁한 처지를 이야기할 때는 두세 차례 박수가 터지기까지 했다."]], [["But Rakitin, in his youthful ardor, made a slight blunder, of which the counsel for the defense at once adroitly took advantage.", "그러나 라키틴은 젊은 혈기에 사소한 실책을 저질렀고, 변호인은 그것을 즉시 교묘하게 이용했다."], ["Answering certain questions about Grushenka, and carried away by the loftiness of his own sentiments and his success, of which he was, of course, conscious, he went so far as to speak somewhat contemptuously of Agrafena Alexandrovna as “the kept mistress of Samsonov.”", "그루셴카에 관한 몇 가지 질문에 답하면서, 자신의 드높은 정서와 스스로도 물론 의식하고 있던 성공에 도취된 나머지, 그는 아그라페나 알렉산드로브나를 두고 다소 경멸하듯 \"삼소노프의 첩\"이라고까지 말해 버렸다."], ["He would have given a good deal to take back his words afterwards, for Fetyukovitch caught him out over it at once.", "그는 나중에 그 말을 거두어들이는 데 무엇이든 내놓았을 테지만, 페튜코비치가 즉시 그 말꼬리를 잡았기 때문이다."], ["And it was all because Rakitin had not reckoned on the lawyer having been able to become so intimately acquainted with every detail in so short a time.", "그리고 그 모든 것은 라키틴이, 그 변호사가 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 세세한 부분까지 그토록 속속들이 알게 되었으리라고는 미처 헤아리지 못한 탓이었다."]], [["“Allow me to ask,”", "\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\""], ["began the counsel for the defense, with the most affable and even respectful smile, “you are, of course, the same Mr.", "변호인이 더없이 상냥한, 심지어 공손하기까지 한 미소를 띠며 입을 열었다."], ["Rakitin whose pamphlet, The Life of the Deceased Elder, Father Zossima, published by the diocesan authorities, full of profound and religious reflections and preceded by an excellent and devout dedication to the bishop, I have just read with such pleasure?”", "\"당신은 물론, 교구 당국에서 펴낸 저 소책자 『작고한 장로 조시마 신부의 생애』의 그 라키틴 씨가 맞으시지요? 깊고 종교적인 성찰로 가득하고, 주교님께 바치는 훌륭하고 경건한 헌사가 앞에 붙은, 제가 방금 그토록 즐겁게 읽은 그 책 말입니다.\""]], [["“I did not write it for publication ...", "\"저는 출판하려고 쓴 게 아닙니다……"], ["it was published afterwards,”", "나중에 출판된 겁니다.\""], ["muttered Rakitin, for some reason fearfully disconcerted and almost ashamed.", "라키틴은 무슨 까닭인지 무섭게 당황하며, 거의 부끄러워하면서 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, that’s excellent!", "\"오, 그것 참 훌륭합니다!"], ["A thinker like you can, and indeed ought to, take the widest view of every social question.", "당신 같은 사상가라면 모든 사회 문제를 가장 폭넓은 시각으로 바라볼 수 있고, 또 마땅히 그래야지요."], ["Your most instructive pamphlet has been widely circulated through the patronage of the bishop, and has been of appreciable service....", "당신의 더없이 유익한 그 소책자는 주교님의 후원으로 널리 보급되어 적잖은 공헌을 했습니다……"], ["But this is the chief thing I should like to learn from you.", "그런데 제가 당신에게서 알고 싶은 가장 중요한 것은 이겁니다."], ["You stated just now that you were very intimately acquainted with Madame Svyetlov.”", "당신은 방금, 스베틀로바 부인과 아주 친밀한 사이라고 진술했지요.\""], ["(It must be noted that Grushenka’s surname was Svyetlov.", "(짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그루셴카의 성이 스베틀로바였다는 점이다."], ["I heard it for the first time that day, during the case.)", "나는 그날, 이 사건을 다루는 중에 그것을 처음 들었다.)"]], [["“I cannot answer for all my acquaintances....", "\"제 모든 지인에 대해 책임질 수는 없습니다……"], ["I am a young man ...", "저는 젊은 사람이고……"], ["and who can be responsible for every one he meets?”", "누가 자기가 만나는 사람 하나하나에 대해 책임을 질 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["cried Rakitin, flushing all over.", "라키틴이 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."]], [["“I understand, I quite understand,”", "\"이해합니다, 충분히 이해하고말고요.\""], ["cried Fetyukovitch, as though he, too, were embarrassed and in haste to excuse himself.", "페튜코비치가 마치 자기도 당황하여 황급히 변명이라도 하려는 듯 외쳤다."], ["“You, like any other, might well be interested in an acquaintance with a young and beautiful woman who would readily entertain the élite of the youth of the neighborhood, but ...", "\"당신도 누구나 그렇듯, 인근의 젊은 명사들을 기꺼이 대접하던 젊고 아름다운 여인과 친분을 맺는 데 충분히 관심을 가질 만하지요. 하지만……"], ["I only wanted to know ...", "제가 알고 싶은 것은 다만 이겁니다……"], ["It has come to my knowledge that Madame Svyetlov was particularly anxious a couple of months ago to make the acquaintance of the younger Karamazov, Alexey Fyodorovitch, and promised you twenty-five roubles, if you would bring him to her in his monastic dress.", "제가 알게 된 바로는, 스베틀로바 부인은 두어 달 전 카라마조프 집안의 막내, 알렉세이 표도로비치와 알고 지내기를 유독 간절히 바랐고, 당신이 수도복 차림의 그를 자기에게 데려와 준다면 이십오 루블을 주겠노라 약속했다지요."], ["And that actually took place on the evening of the day on which the terrible crime, which is the subject of the present investigation, was committed.", "그리고 그 일은 실제로, 지금 이 심리의 대상이 된 그 끔찍한 범죄가 저질러진 바로 그날 저녁에 일어났습니다."], ["You brought Alexey Karamazov to Madame Svyetlov, and did you receive the twenty-five roubles from Madame Svyetlov as a reward, that’s what I wanted to hear from you?”", "당신은 알렉세이 카라마조프를 스베틀로바 부인에게 데려갔고, 그 대가로 스베틀로바 부인에게서 이십오 루블을 받았습니까, 바로 그것을 당신에게서 듣고 싶었던 겁니다.\""]], [["“It was a joke....", "\"그건 농담이었습니다……"], ["I don’t see of what interest that can be to you....", "그게 당신에게 무슨 흥밋거리가 되는지 모르겠군요……"], ["I took it for a joke ...", "저는 그걸 농담으로 받았고……"], ["meaning to give it back later....”", "나중에 돌려줄 작정이었습니다……\""]], [["“Then you did take— But you have not given it back yet ...", "\"그렇다면 받기는 받았군요. 하지만 아직 돌려주지는 않았고……"], ["or have you?”", "아니면 돌려주었소?\""]], [["“That’s of no consequence,”", "\"그건 중요한 게 아닙니다.\""], ["muttered Rakitin, “I refuse to answer such questions....", "라키틴이 중얼거렸다. \"그런 질문에는 대답하지 않겠습니다……"], ["Of course I shall give it back.”", "물론 돌려줄 겁니다.\""]], [["The President intervened, but Fetyukovitch declared he had no more questions to ask of the witness. Mr.", "재판장이 끼어들었으나, 페튜코비치는 증인에게 더는 물을 것이 없다고 선언했다."], ["Rakitin left the witness-box not absolutely without a stain upon his character.", "라키틴 씨는 명예에 흠집 하나 없이 증언대를 떠나지는 못했다."], ["The effect left by the lofty idealism of his speech was somewhat marred, and Fetyukovitch’s expression, as he watched him walk away, seemed to suggest to the public “this is a specimen of the lofty-minded persons who accuse him.”", "그의 변설이 풍기던 드높은 이상주의의 효과는 다소 흐려졌고, 그가 걸어 나가는 것을 지켜보는 페튜코비치의 표정은 방청객에게 \"이것이 바로 그를 고발하는 그 고결한 인사들의 표본이올시다\" 하고 일러 주는 듯했다."], ["I remember that this incident, too, did not pass off without an outbreak from Mitya.", "기억하건대 이 사건 역시 미탸의 한바탕 폭발 없이는 지나가지 않았다."], ["Enraged by the tone in which Rakitin had referred to Grushenka, he suddenly shouted “Bernard!”", "라키틴이 그루셴카를 가리키던 그 어조에 격분한 그는, 별안간 \"베르나르!\" 하고 소리쳤다."], ["When, after Rakitin’s cross-examination, the President asked the prisoner if he had anything to say, Mitya cried loudly:", "라키틴의 반대신문이 끝난 뒤, 재판장이 피고에게 할 말이 있느냐고 묻자, 미탸가 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Since I’ve been arrested, he has borrowed money from me!", "\"내가 체포된 뒤로, 저자는 내게서 돈을 꿔 갔소!"], ["He is a contemptible Bernard and opportunist, and he doesn’t believe in God; he took the bishop in!”", "저자는 비열한 베르나르요 기회주의자고, 신을 믿지 않습니다. 주교님을 감쪽같이 속여먹었지요!\""]], [["Mitya, of course, was pulled up again for the intemperance of his language, but Rakitin was done for.", "물론 미탸는 그 무절제한 말 때문에 또다시 제지당했으나, 라키틴은 끝장이 난 셈이었다."], ["Captain Snegiryov’s evidence was a failure, too, but from quite a different reason.", "스네기료프 이등 대위의 증언도 실패였지만, 그것은 전혀 다른 이유에서였다."], ["He appeared in ragged and dirty clothes, muddy boots, and in spite of the vigilance and expert observation of the police officers, he turned out to be hopelessly drunk.", "그는 누덕누덕한 더러운 옷에 진흙투성이 장화 차림으로 나타났는데, 경관들의 경계와 능숙한 관찰에도 불구하고 가망 없이 곤드레만드레 취해 있는 것으로 드러났다."], ["On being asked about Mitya’s attack upon him, he refused to answer.", "미탸가 자기에게 가한 폭행에 관한 질문을 받자, 그는 대답하기를 거부했다."]], [["“God bless him.", "\"그분께 신의 가호가 있기를."], ["Ilusha told me not to.", "일류샤가 말하지 말라고 했습니다."], ["God will make it up to me yonder.”", "신께서 저 세상에서 제게 갚아 주실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Who told you not to tell?", "\"누가 말하지 말라고 했소?"], ["Of whom are you talking?”", "누구 이야기를 하는 거요?\""]], [["“Ilusha, my little son.", "\"제 어린 아들 일류샤 말입니다."], ["‘Father, father, how he insulted you!’ He said that at the stone.", "'아버지, 아버지, 저 사람이 아버지를 어찌나 모욕했는지!' 그 애가 바위 옆에서 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["Now he is dying....”", "지금 그 애는 죽어 가고 있어요……\""]], [["The captain suddenly began sobbing, and plumped down on his knees before the President.", "이등 대위는 별안간 흐느끼기 시작하더니, 재판장 앞에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["He was hurriedly led away amidst the laughter of the public.", "그는 방청객의 웃음 속에 황급히 끌려 나갔다."], ["The effect prepared by the prosecutor did not come off at all.", "검사가 준비해 둔 효과는 전혀 거두지 못했다."]], [["Fetyukovitch went on making the most of every opportunity, and amazed people more and more by his minute knowledge of the case.", "페튜코비치는 계속해서 온갖 기회를 최대한 이용했고, 사건에 대한 세세한 지식으로 사람들을 점점 더 놀라게 했다."], ["Thus, for example, Trifon Borissovitch made a great impression, of course, very prejudicial to Mitya.", "이를테면 트리폰 보리소비치는 물론 미탸에게 매우 불리한 큰 인상을 남겼다."], ["He calculated almost on his fingers that on his first visit to Mokroe, Mitya must have spent three thousand roubles, “or very little less.", "그는 거의 손가락을 꼽아 가며, 미탸가 모크로예를 처음 찾았을 때 삼천 루블, \"아니면 그보다 아주 조금 적은\" 돈을 썼음이 틀림없다고 계산했다."], ["Just think what he squandered on those gypsy girls alone!", "\"그 집시 계집들한테만 얼마나 흥청망청 뿌렸는지 한번 생각해 보십시오!"], ["And as for our lousy peasants, it wasn’t a case of flinging half a rouble in the street, he made them presents of twenty-five roubles each, at least, he didn’t give them less.", "그리고 우리 천한 농부들로 말하자면, 길바닥에 반 루블이나 던져 주는 정도가 아니라, 한 사람 앞에 적어도 이십오 루블씩 선물로 안겼지요, 그보다 적게 주진 않았습니다."], ["And what a lot of money was simply stolen from him!", "게다가 얼마나 많은 돈이 그냥 도둑맞았는지!"], ["And if any one did steal, he did not leave a receipt.", "누가 훔쳐 갔든, 영수증을 남길 리가 있나요."], ["How could one catch the thief when he was flinging his money away all the time?", "돈을 노상 그렇게 뿌려 대는데 어떻게 도둑을 잡겠습니까?"], ["Our peasants are robbers, you know; they have no care for their souls.", "아시다시피 우리 농부들은 도둑놈들이라, 제 영혼은 안중에도 없으니까요."], ["And the way he went on with the girls, our village girls!", "게다가 그가 계집애들, 우리 마을 계집애들하고 놀아난 꼴이라니!"], ["They’re completely set up since then, I tell you, they used to be poor.”", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 그것들은 그 뒤로 아주 팔자가 폈습니다. 전에는 가난했었거든요.\""], ["He recalled, in fact, every item of expense and added it all up.", "사실 그는 지출 항목 하나하나를 떠올려 죄다 합산했다."], ["So the theory that only fifteen hundred had been spent and the rest had been put aside in a little bag seemed inconceivable.", "그러니 천오백 루블만 쓰고 나머지는 작은 주머니에 따로 챙겨 두었다는 가설은 도무지 믿기지 않게 여겨졌다."]], [["“I saw three thousand as clear as a penny in his hands, I saw it with my own eyes; I should think I ought to know how to reckon money,”", "\"제 두 눈으로 그의 손에 삼천 루블이 동전 한 닢처럼 또렷이 있는 걸 봤습니다. 돈 셀 줄은 제가 알 텐데요.\""], ["cried Trifon Borissovitch, doing his best to satisfy “his betters.”", "트리폰 보리소비치는 \"윗분들\"의 비위를 맞추려고 안간힘을 쓰며 외쳤다."]], [["When Fetyukovitch had to cross-examine him, he scarcely tried to refute his evidence, but began asking him about an incident at the first carousal at Mokroe, a month before the arrest, when Timofey and another peasant called Akim had picked up on the floor in the passage a hundred roubles dropped by Mitya when he was drunk, and had given them to Trifon Borissovitch and received a rouble each from him for doing so.", "페튜코비치는 그를 반대신문할 차례가 되자, 그의 증언을 반박하려는 시도는 거의 하지 않고, 체포 한 달 전 모크로예에서의 첫 술판 때 일어난 어떤 일에 관해 묻기 시작했다. 곧 티모페이와 아킴이라는 또 다른 농부가 미탸가 취해서 통로 바닥에 떨어뜨린 백 루블을 주워 트리폰 보리소비치에게 건넸고, 그렇게 한 대가로 그에게서 각각 일 루블씩을 받은 일이었다."], ["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["asked the lawyer, “did you give that hundred roubles back to Mr.", "변호사가 물었다. \"당신은 그 백 루블을 카라마조프 씨에게 돌려주었소?\""], ["Karamazov?” Trifon Borissovitch shuffled in vain....", "트리폰 보리소비치는 부질없이 발뺌하려 했다……"], ["He was obliged, after the peasants had been examined, to admit the finding of the hundred roubles, only adding that he had religiously returned it all to Dmitri Fyodorovitch “in perfect honesty, and it’s only because his honor was in liquor at the time, he wouldn’t remember it.”", "농부들의 신문이 끝나자, 그는 백 루블을 주운 사실을 인정할 수밖에 없었으나, 다만 자기는 그것을 \"더없이 정직하게\" 드미트리 표도로비치에게 고스란히 돌려주었으며, \"다만 그때 나리께서 취해 계셨던지라 그걸 기억 못 하실 뿐\"이라고 덧붙였다."], ["But, as he had denied the incident of the hundred roubles till the peasants had been called to prove it, his evidence as to returning the money to Mitya was naturally regarded with great suspicion.", "그러나 그가 농부들이 불려 나와 증명하기 전까지 백 루블 건을 부인했던 터라, 미탸에게 돈을 돌려주었다는 그의 증언은 당연히 큰 의심을 받았다."], ["So one of the most dangerous witnesses brought forward by the prosecution was again discredited.", "그리하여 검찰 측이 내세운 가장 위험한 증인 가운데 하나가 또다시 신빙성을 잃었다."]], [["The same thing happened with the Poles.", "폴란드인들에게도 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["They took up an attitude of pride and independence; they vociferated loudly that they had both been in the service of the Crown, and that “Pan Mitya”", "그들은 거만하고 도도한 태도를 취하며, 둘 다 국가에 봉직한 바 있고 \"판 미탸\"가 그들에게 \"명예를 매수하려고\""], ["had offered them three thousand “to buy their honor,” and that they had seen a large sum of money in his hands.", "삼천 루블을 제의했으며, 그의 손에 큰돈이 있는 것을 보았노라고 큰 소리로 떠벌렸다."], ["Pan Mussyalovitch introduced a terrible number of Polish words into his sentences, and seeing that this only increased his consequence in the eyes of the President and the prosecutor, grew more and more pompous, and ended by talking in Polish altogether.", "판 무샬로비치는 자기 말 속에 폴란드어 단어를 무시무시하게 많이 끼워 넣었는데, 이것이 재판장과 검사의 눈에 자기 위신을 높여 줄 뿐임을 알아채자 점점 더 거드름을 피우다가, 끝내는 아예 폴란드어로만 떠들기에 이르렀다."], ["But Fetyukovitch caught them, too, in his snares.", "그러나 페튜코비치는 그들마저 자기 올가미에 걸려들게 했다."], ["Trifon Borissovitch, recalled, was forced, in spite of his evasions, to admit that Pan Vrublevsky had substituted another pack of cards for the one he had provided, and that Pan Mussyalovitch had cheated during the game.", "다시 불려 나온 트리폰 보리소비치는 둘러대려 했으나, 판 브루블레프스키가 자기가 마련해 준 카드 한 벌을 다른 것으로 바꿔치기했으며, 판 무샬로비치가 노름판에서 속임수를 썼다는 사실을 인정할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Kalganov confirmed this, and both the Poles left the witness-box with damaged reputations, amidst laughter from the public.", "칼가노프가 이를 확인해 주었고, 두 폴란드인은 방청객의 웃음 속에 평판을 망친 채 증언대를 떠났다."]], [["Then exactly the same thing happened with almost all the most dangerous witnesses.", "그러더니 가장 위험한 증인들 거의 모두에게 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["Fetyukovitch succeeded in casting a slur on all of them, and dismissing them with a certain derision.", "페튜코비치는 그들 모두에게 흠집을 내고, 일종의 조롱과 함께 물러가게 하는 데 성공했다."], ["The lawyers and experts were lost in admiration, and were only at a loss to understand what good purpose could be served by it, for all, I repeat, felt that the case for the prosecution could not be refuted, but was growing more and more tragically overwhelming. But from the confidence of the “great magician”", "변호사들과 전문가들은 감탄을 금치 못했으나, 다만 그것이 무슨 유익한 목적에 보탬이 될 수 있는지를 도무지 이해하지 못했으니, 거듭 말하거니와 모두가 검찰 측의 입지는 반박될 수 없으며 오히려 점점 더 비극적일 만큼 압도적으로 커져 간다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["they saw that he was serene, and they waited, feeling that “such a man” had not come from Petersburg for nothing, and that he was not a man to return unsuccessful.", "그러나 그 \"위대한 마술사\"의 자신감에서 그들은 그가 태연자약함을 보았고, \"그런 사람\"이 까닭 없이 페테르부르크에서 온 것이 아니며, 빈손으로 돌아갈 사내가 아님을 느끼며 기다렸다."]], [["# Chapter III. The Medical Experts And A Pound Of Nuts", "# Chapter III. The Medical Experts And A Pound Of Nuts"]], [["The evidence of the medical experts, too, was of little use to the prisoner.", "의료 전문가들의 증언 역시 피고에게는 별 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["And it appeared later that Fetyukovitch had not reckoned much upon it.", "그리고 페튜코비치 또한 그 증언에 큰 기대를 걸지 않았음이 나중에 드러났다."], ["The medical line of defense had only been taken up through the insistence of Katerina Ivanovna, who had sent for a celebrated doctor from Moscow on purpose.", "의학적 변호 노선은 오로지 카테리나 이바노브나의 고집 때문에 채택되었던 것으로, 그녀는 일부러 모스크바에서 이름난 의사 한 사람을 불러왔던 것이다."], ["The case for the defense could, of course, lose nothing by it and might, with luck, gain something from it.", "물론 그 변호 노선이 피고 측에 손해를 끼칠 일은 없었고, 운이 좋으면 무언가 얻을 수도 있었다."], ["There was, however, an element of comedy about it, through the difference of opinion of the doctors.", "그러나 의사들의 의견이 서로 엇갈리는 바람에 거기엔 희극적인 요소가 깃들어 있었다."], ["The medical experts were the famous doctor from Moscow, our doctor, Herzenstube, and the young doctor, Varvinsky.", "의료 전문가는 모스크바에서 온 유명한 의사와 우리 고장의 의사 헤르첸슈투베, 그리고 젊은 의사 바르빈스키였다."], ["The two latter appeared also as witnesses for the prosecution.", "뒤의 두 사람은 검찰 측 증인으로도 나섰다."]], [["The first to be called in the capacity of expert was Doctor Herzenstube.", "전문가 자격으로 가장 먼저 호명된 이는 헤르첸슈투베 박사였다."], ["He was a gray and bald old man of seventy, of middle height and sturdy build.", "그는 일흔 살의 백발에 머리가 벗어진 노인으로, 중키에 다부진 체격이었다."], ["He was much esteemed and respected by every one in the town.", "그는 온 고장 사람들에게서 두루 존경과 신망을 받았다."], ["He was a conscientious doctor and an excellent and pious man, a Hernguter or Moravian brother, I am not quite sure which.", "그는 양심적인 의사였고 더없이 훌륭하고 경건한 사람으로, 헤른후트파인지 모라비아 형제단원인지, 정확히 어느 쪽인지는 나도 잘 모르겠다."], ["He had been living amongst us for many years and behaved with wonderful dignity.", "그는 여러 해 동안 우리 사이에서 살아왔으며 놀라우리만큼 위엄 있게 처신했다."], ["He was a kind-hearted and humane man.", "그는 마음이 따뜻하고 인정 많은 사람이었다."], ["He treated the sick poor and peasants for nothing, visited them in their slums and huts, and left money for medicine, but he was as obstinate as a mule.", "그는 가난한 환자들과 농민들을 무료로 치료해 주었고, 그들의 빈민굴과 오두막까지 찾아가 약값으로 쓰라며 돈을 놓고 오기까지 했으나, 노새처럼 고집이 셌다."], ["If once he had taken an idea into his head, there was no shaking it.", "한번 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박히면 도무지 흔들리는 법이 없었다."], ["Almost every one in the town was aware, by the way, that the famous doctor had, within the first two or three days of his presence among us, uttered some extremely offensive allusions to Doctor Herzenstube’s qualifications.", "그건 그렇고, 고장 사람들 거의 모두가 알고 있던 사실인데, 그 유명한 의사는 우리 사이에 온 지 사나흘도 채 되지 않아 헤르첸슈투베 박사의 자질에 대해 극도로 모욕적인 빈정거림을 늘어놓았던 것이다."], ["Though the Moscow doctor asked twenty-five roubles for a visit, several people in the town were glad to take advantage of his arrival, and rushed to consult him regardless of expense.", "모스크바 의사는 왕진 한 번에 이십오 루블을 요구했음에도, 고장 사람 여럿이 그의 도착을 반겨 비용을 가리지 않고 앞다투어 진찰을 받으러 몰려들었다."], ["All these had, of course, been previously patients of Doctor Herzenstube, and the celebrated doctor had criticized his treatment with extreme harshness.", "그들은 물론 하나같이 이전에 헤르첸슈투베 박사의 환자였던 자들이었고, 그 유명한 의사는 그의 치료를 극도로 가혹하게 비판했다."], ["Finally, he had asked the patients as soon as he saw them, “Well, who has been cramming you with nostrums?", "급기야 그는 환자들을 보자마자 이렇게 묻기까지 했다. \"그래, 누가 당신한테 이런 엉터리 약을 잔뜩 먹였소?"], ["Herzenstube?", "헤르첸슈투베?"], ["He, he!”", "헤, 헤!\""], ["Doctor Herzenstube, of course, heard all this, and now all the three doctors made their appearance, one after another, to be examined.", "헤르첸슈투베 박사도 물론 이 모든 말을 들어 알고 있었는데, 이제 세 의사가 차례로 모습을 드러내 심문을 받게 되었다."]], [["Doctor Herzenstube roundly declared that the abnormality of the prisoner’s mental faculties was self-evident.", "헤르첸슈투베 박사는 피고의 정신 능력에 이상이 있다는 것은 자명하다고 단호히 선언했다."], ["Then giving his grounds for this opinion, which I omit here, he added that the abnormality was not only evident in many of the prisoner’s actions in the past, but was apparent even now at this very moment.", "그러고 나서 이 견해의 근거를 댔는데, 여기서는 그 부분을 생략하기로 한다, 그는 그 이상이 피고의 과거 행동 가운데 여럿에서 드러났을 뿐만 아니라 바로 지금 이 순간에도 분명히 나타나고 있다고 덧붙였다. 지금 이 순간 어떻게 그것이 드러나느냐는 질문을 받자, 노의사는 순박하리만큼 솔직하게, 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 \"이 상황에서는 주목할 만한, 범상치 않은 태도\"를 보였다고 지적했다."], ["When he was asked to explain how it was apparent now at this moment, the old doctor, with simple-hearted directness, pointed out that the prisoner on entering the court had “an extraordinary air, remarkable in the circumstances”; that he had “marched in like a soldier, looking straight before him, though it would have been more natural for him to look to the left where, among the public, the ladies were sitting, seeing that he was a great admirer of the fair sex and must be thinking much of what the ladies are saying of him now,” the old man concluded in his peculiar language.", "즉 그가 \"군인처럼 곧장 앞만 바라보며 행진하듯 들어왔는데, 정작 그로서는 왼쪽을, 방청석에 부인들이 앉아 있는 쪽을 바라보는 것이 더 자연스러웠을 것이니, 그가 여성을 대단히 흠모하는 사람인 데다 지금 부인들이 자기에 대해 무어라 말하고 있을지 무척 신경을 쓰고 있을 것이 분명하기 때문\"이라고, 노인은 자기 특유의 어법으로 결론을 맺었다."]], [["I must add that he spoke Russian readily, but every phrase was formed in German style, which did not, however, trouble him, for it had always been a weakness of his to believe that he spoke Russian perfectly, better indeed than Russians.", "덧붙이자면 그는 러시아어를 거침없이 구사했으나 모든 구절이 독일식으로 짜여 있었는데, 그렇다고 그것이 그에게 걸림돌이 되지는 않았으니, 자기가 러시아어를 완벽하게, 실로 러시아 사람들보다도 더 잘한다고 믿는 것이 늘 그의 약점이었기 때문이다."], ["And he was very fond of using Russian proverbs, always declaring that the Russian proverbs were the best and most expressive sayings in the whole world.", "그는 러시아 속담을 즐겨 썼고, 러시아 속담이야말로 온 세상에서 가장 훌륭하고 가장 표현력이 풍부한 격언이라고 항상 단언했다."], ["I may remark, too, that in conversation, through absent-mindedness he often forgot the most ordinary words, which sometimes went out of his head, though he knew them perfectly.", "또한 덧붙여 말해 두자면, 그는 대화 중에 정신이 딴 데 팔려 더없이 평범한 낱말조차 잊어버리곤 했는데, 그 낱말을 완벽하게 알고 있으면서도 더러 머릿속에서 사라져 버리는 것이었다."], ["The same thing happened, though, when he spoke German, and at such times he always waved his hand before his face as though trying to catch the lost word, and no one could induce him to go on speaking till he had found the missing word.", "그런 일은 독일어로 말할 때도 똑같이 일어났는데, 그럴 때면 그는 늘 잃어버린 낱말을 붙잡으려는 듯 얼굴 앞에서 손을 휘저었고, 그 사라진 낱말을 찾아내기 전에는 누구도 그에게 말을 계속하게 할 수 없었다."], ["His remark that the prisoner ought to have looked at the ladies on entering roused a whisper of amusement in the audience.", "피고가 들어설 때 부인들 쪽을 바라봤어야 했다는 그의 말에 방청석에서는 재미있어하는 수군거림이 일었다."], ["All our ladies were very fond of our old doctor; they knew, too, that having been all his life a bachelor and a religious man of exemplary conduct, he looked upon women as lofty creatures.", "우리 고장 부인들은 모두 노의사를 무척 좋아했다. 또한 그들은 그가 평생 독신으로 모범적인 품행을 지켜 온 신앙심 깊은 사람으로서 여성을 고귀한 존재로 우러러본다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["And so his unexpected observation struck every one as very queer.", "그래서 그의 뜻밖의 소견은 모두에게 무척 기이하게 여겨졌다."]], [["The Moscow doctor, being questioned in his turn, definitely and emphatically repeated that he considered the prisoner’s mental condition abnormal in the highest degree. He talked at length and with erudition of “aberration”", "이번에는 모스크바 의사가 심문을 받았는데, 그는 피고의 정신 상태가 극도로 비정상이라고 본다고 분명하고 단호하게 거듭 말했다."], ["and “mania,” and argued that, from all the facts collected, the prisoner had undoubtedly been in a condition of aberration for several days before his arrest, and, if the crime had been committed by him, it must, even if he were conscious of it, have been almost involuntary, as he had not the power to control the morbid impulse that possessed him.", "그는 \"착란\"과 \"광증\"에 대해 박식하게 길게 늘어놓으며, 수집된 모든 사실로 미루어 피고가 체포되기 며칠 전부터 의심할 바 없이 착란 상태에 있었고, 만일 범행이 그에 의해 저질러졌다면 설령 그가 그것을 의식하고 있었다 할지라도 거의 무의식적으로 행해진 것일 수밖에 없으니, 그것은 그가 자신을 사로잡은 병적 충동을 억제할 힘이 없었기 때문이라고 주장했다."]], [["But apart from temporary aberration, the doctor diagnosed mania, which premised, in his words, to lead to complete insanity in the future.", "그러나 일시적 착란과는 별개로, 의사는 광증을 진단했으며, 그의 말에 따르면 이는 장차 완전한 정신착란으로 이어질 조짐이었다."], ["(It must be noted that I report this in my own words, the doctor made use of very learned and professional language.) “All his actions are in contravention of common sense and logic,” he continued.", "(여기서 밝혀 두어야 할 것은, 이것은 내가 내 나름의 표현으로 옮긴 것이며, 의사는 매우 학술적이고 전문적인 용어를 구사했다는 점이다.) \"그의 모든 행동은 상식과 논리에 어긋납니다.\""], ["“Not to refer to what I have not seen, that is, the crime itself and the whole catastrophe, the day before yesterday, while he was talking to me, he had an unaccountably fixed look in his eye.", "그는 말을 이었다. \"제가 직접 보지 못한 것, 즉 범행 자체와 사건의 전모는 차치하더라도, 그저께 그가 저와 이야기를 나눌 때 그의 눈에는 영문 모를 고정된 눈빛이 어려 있었습니다."], ["He laughed unexpectedly when there was nothing to laugh at.", "그는 웃을 일이 전혀 없는데도 느닷없이 웃었습니다."], ["He showed continual and inexplicable irritability, using strange words, ‘Bernard!’ ‘Ethics!’ and others equally inappropriate.”", "그는 끊임없이, 설명할 길 없는 짜증을 드러내며 '베르나르!' '윤리!'"], ["But the doctor detected mania, above all, in the fact that the prisoner could not even speak of the three thousand roubles, of which he considered himself to have been cheated, without extraordinary irritation, though he could speak comparatively lightly of other misfortunes and grievances.", "같은, 그리고 그에 못지않게 엉뚱한 이상한 말들을 내뱉었습니다.\" 그러나 의사는 무엇보다도, 피고가 자기를 속여 빼앗아 갔다고 여기는 삼천 루블에 관해서는 다른 불운이나 원한에 대해서는 비교적 가볍게 이야기할 수 있으면서도 유독 그 이야기만 나오면 유난한 짜증 없이는 입에 올리지도 못한다는 사실에서 광증을 간파해 냈다."], ["According to all accounts, he had even in the past, whenever the subject of the three thousand roubles was touched on, flown into a perfect frenzy, and yet he was reported to be a disinterested and not grasping man.", "여러 사람의 진술에 따르면, 그는 과거에도 삼천 루블 이야기만 나오면 그때마다 완전히 광란에 빠지곤 했는데, 그러면서도 욕심 없고 물욕에 매이지 않은 사람으로 전해지고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“As to the opinion of my learned colleague,”", "\"제 학식 높은 동료의 견해, 즉 피고가 법정에 들어설 때 자연스럽게 곧장 앞을 바라보지 않고 부인들 쪽을 바라봤을 것이라는 견해에 대해서는,\""], ["the Moscow doctor added ironically in conclusion, “that the prisoner would, on entering the court, have naturally looked at the ladies and not straight before him, I will only say that, apart from the playfulness of this theory, it is radically unsound.", "모스크바 의사가 마무리하며 비꼬듯 덧붙였다. \"이 이론의 재기 발랄함은 차치하더라도, 근본적으로 잘못되었다고만 말해 두겠습니다."], ["For though I fully agree that the prisoner, on entering the court where his fate will be decided, would not naturally look straight before him in that fixed way, and that that may really be a sign of his abnormal mental condition, at the same time I maintain that he would naturally not look to the left at the ladies, but, on the contrary, to the right to find his legal adviser, on whose help all his hopes rest and on whose defense all his future depends.”", "저로서는 자기 운명이 결정될 법정에 들어서는 피고가 그렇게 고정된 눈빛으로 곧장 앞만 바라보는 것이 자연스럽지 않으며, 그것이 실로 그의 비정상적인 정신 상태의 징후일 수 있다는 데에는 전적으로 동의하는 바이나, 동시에 그가 자연스럽게 바라봤을 곳은 왼쪽의 부인들이 아니라 오히려 오른쪽이었으리라고, 즉 자기의 모든 희망이 걸려 있고 자기의 모든 장래가 그 변호에 달려 있는 법률 고문을 찾았으리라고 주장하는 바입니다.\""], ["The doctor expressed his opinion positively and emphatically.", "의사는 자기 견해를 확신에 차서 단호하게 피력했다."]], [["But the unexpected pronouncement of Doctor Varvinsky gave the last touch of comedy to the difference of opinion between the experts.", "그러나 전문가들 사이의 의견 차이에 마지막 희극적 색채를 더한 것은 바르빈스키 박사의 뜻밖의 단언이었다."], ["In his opinion the prisoner was now, and had been all along, in a perfectly normal condition, and, although he certainly must have been in a nervous and exceedingly excited state before his arrest, this might have been due to several perfectly obvious causes, jealousy, anger, continual drunkenness, and so on.", "그의 견해로는 피고가 지금도, 그리고 줄곧 완벽하게 정상적인 상태에 있었으며, 비록 체포되기 전에는 분명 신경이 곤두서고 극도로 흥분한 상태였음이 틀림없으나, 이는 질투, 분노, 끊임없는 음주 따위의 지극히 명백한 여러 원인에서 비롯된 것일 수 있다고 했다."], ["But this nervous condition would not involve the mental aberration of which mention had just been made.", "그러나 그러한 신경 상태가 방금 거론된 정신 착란을 수반하는 것은 아니라고 했다."], ["As to the question whether the prisoner should have looked to the left or to the right on entering the court, “in his modest opinion,”", "피고가 법정에 들어설 때 왼쪽을 바라봤어야 했는가, 오른쪽을 바라봤어야 했는가 하는 문제에 대해서는, \"자기의 변변찮은 소견으로는,\""], ["the prisoner would naturally look straight before him on entering the court, as he had in fact done, as that was where the judges, on whom his fate depended, were sitting.", "피고가 법정에 들어설 때 곧장 앞을 바라보는 것이 자연스러우며 실제로 그렇게 했는데, 바로 그곳에 자기 운명을 좌우할 판사들이 앉아 있었기 때문이라고 했다."], ["So that it was just by looking straight before him that he showed his perfectly normal state of mind at the present.", "그러니 곧장 앞을 바라봤다는 바로 그 사실이야말로 그가 현재 더없이 정상적인 정신 상태에 있음을 보여 주는 것이라고 했다."], ["The young doctor concluded his “modest”", "젊은 의사는 다소 열을 올려 자기의 \"변변찮은\""], ["testimony with some heat.", "증언을 끝맺었다."]], [["“Bravo, doctor!”", "\"브라보, 의사 선생!\""], ["cried Mitya, from his seat, “just so!”", "미탸가 자기 자리에서 외쳤다. \"바로 그거요!\""]], [["Mitya, of course, was checked, but the young doctor’s opinion had a decisive influence on the judges and on the public, and, as appeared afterwards, every one agreed with him.", "미탸는 물론 제지당했으나, 젊은 의사의 견해는 판사들과 방청객들에게 결정적인 영향을 미쳤고, 나중에 드러난 바로는 모두가 그의 의견에 동의했다."], ["But Doctor Herzenstube, when called as a witness, was quite unexpectedly of use to Mitya.", "그런데 헤르첸슈투베 박사는 증인으로 호명되었을 때 전혀 뜻밖에도 미탸에게 도움이 되어 주었다."], ["As an old resident in the town who had known the Karamazov family for years, he furnished some facts of great value for the prosecution, and suddenly, as though recalling something, he added:", "고장의 오랜 주민으로서 카라마조프 집안을 여러 해 알고 지내 온 그는 검찰 측에 매우 중요한 사실 몇 가지를 제공했으나, 갑자기 무언가가 떠오른 듯 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“But the poor young man might have had a very different life, for he had a good heart both in childhood and after childhood, that I know.", "\"하지만 저 가엾은 젊은이는 전혀 다른 삶을 살 수도 있었습니다, 그 애는 어린 시절에도 그 뒤에도 마음씨가 고왔으니까요, 그건 제가 압니다."], ["But the Russian proverb says, ‘If a man has one head, it’s good, but if another clever man comes to visit him, it would be better still, for then there will be two heads and not only one.’”", "그런데 러시아 속담에 이런 말이 있지요. '사람에게 머리가 하나 있으면 좋은 일이지만, 영리한 사람이 또 한 명 찾아와 준다면 더욱 좋을 것이니, 그러면 머리가 하나가 아니라 둘이 되기 때문이다.'\""]], [["“One head is good, but two are better,”", "\"머리 하나보다는 둘이 낫다는 말이군요.\""], ["the prosecutor put in impatiently.", "검사가 조바심을 내며 끼어들었다."], ["He knew the old man’s habit of talking slowly and deliberately, regardless of the impression he was making and of the delay he was causing, and highly prizing his flat, dull and always gleefully complacent German wit.", "그는 노인이 자기가 주는 인상이나 자기가 일으키는 지체에는 아랑곳없이 느릿느릿 신중하게 말하는 버릇이 있으며, 그 밋밋하고 둔하면서도 늘 흐뭇하게 자족하는 독일식 재담을 대단히 자랑스러워한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["The old man was fond of making jokes.", "노인은 농담하기를 좋아했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, that’s what I say,”", "\"오, 그렇소, 제 말이 바로 그겁니다.\""], ["he went on stubbornly.", "그는 고집스레 말을 이었다."], ["“One head is good, but two are much better, but he did not meet another head with wits, and his wits went.", "\"머리 하나도 좋지만 둘이면 훨씬 더 좋지요, 그런데 그 애는 영리한 머리를 또 하나 만나지 못했고, 그래서 그 애의 머리가 그만... 어디로 가버렸느냐 하면, 아, 그 단어를 잊어버렸군요.\""], ["Where did they go? I’ve forgotten the word.”", "그는 손으로 눈앞을 가리며 말을 이었다."], ["He went on, passing his hand before his eyes, “Oh, yes, spazieren.” “Wandering?”", "\"오, 그렇지, 슈파치렌(spazieren).\""]], [["“Oh, yes, wandering, that’s what I say.", "\"방랑한다는 말입니까?\" \"오, 그렇소, 방랑이지요, 바로 그 말입니다."], ["Well, his wits went wandering and fell in such a deep hole that he lost himself.", "그러니까, 그 애의 머리가 방랑을 떠나 그만 너무도 깊은 구멍에 빠져 자기 자신을 잃어버린 거지요."], ["And yet he was a grateful and sensitive boy.", "그렇지만 그 애는 고마움을 아는 다정한 아이였습니다."], ["Oh, I remember him very well, a little chap so high, left neglected by his father in the back yard, when he ran about without boots on his feet, and his little breeches hanging by one button.”", "오, 저는 그 애를 아주 잘 기억합니다, 요만한 꼬마였는데, 아비한테 버림받아 뒤뜰에 방치된 채 발에 장화도 신지 않고 뛰어다녔고, 작은 바지는 단추 하나에 간신히 걸려 있었지요.\""]], [["A note of feeling and tenderness suddenly came into the honest old man’s voice.", "정직한 노인의 목소리에 갑자기 정감 어린 따스함이 깃들었다."], ["Fetyukovitch positively started, as though scenting something, and caught at it instantly.", "페튜코비치는 무언가 냄새를 맡은 듯 흠칫하더니 곧장 그것을 낚아챘다."]], [["“Oh, yes, I was a young man then....", "\"오, 그렇소, 그때 저는 젊은이였지요..."], ["I was ...", "저는..."], ["well, I was forty-five then, and had only just come here.", "글쎄요, 그때 마흔다섯이었고, 막 이곳에 온 참이었습니다."], ["And I was so sorry for the boy then; I asked myself why shouldn’t I buy him a pound of ...", "그때 저는 그 애가 너무도 가엾어서 이렇게 자문했지요, 내가 저 애한테 한 파운드의..."], ["a pound of what?", "한 파운드의 뭐였더라?"], ["I’ve forgotten what it’s called.", "그 이름을 잊어버렸군요."], ["A pound of what children are very fond of, what is it, what is it?”", "아이들이 무척 좋아하는 그것 한 파운드를, 그게 뭐였더라, 뭐였지?\""], ["The doctor began waving his hands again.", "의사는 다시 손을 휘젓기 시작했다."], ["“It grows on a tree and is gathered and given to every one....”", "\"나무에서 자라고, 따서 모두에게 나눠 주는 그것...\""]], [["“Apples?”", "\"사과 말입니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no, no.", "\"오, 아니, 아니오."], ["You have a dozen of apples, not a pound....", "사과라면 한 다스를 헤아리지 한 파운드라고 하지는 않지요..."], ["No, there are a lot of them, and all little.", "아니오, 그건 잔뜩 있고, 죄다 자그마합니다."], ["You put them in the mouth and crack.” “Nuts?”", "입에 넣고 깨물면 똑 하고...\""]], [["“Quite so, nuts, I say so.”", "\"호두 말입니까?\" \"바로 그겁니다, 호두지요, 제 말이 그겁니다.\""], ["The doctor repeated in the calmest way as though he had been at no loss for a word.", "의사는 마치 그 낱말을 까먹은 적도 없었다는 듯 더없이 태연하게 되풀이했다. \"그래서 저는 그 애한테 호두 한 파운드를 사 주었습니다, 그때까지 아무도 그 애한테 호두 한 파운드 사 준 사람이 없었으니까요."], ["“And I bought him a pound of nuts, for no one had ever bought the boy a pound of nuts before.", "그러고는 손가락을 치켜들고 그 애한테 말했지요, '얘야, 고트 데어 파터(Gott der Vater).' 그러자 그 애가 웃으며 말했습니다, '고트 데어 파터.'..."], ["And I lifted my finger and said to him, ‘Boy, Gott der Vater.’ He laughed and said, ‘Gott der Vater.’...", "'고트 데어 존(Gott der Sohn).' 그 애는 또 웃더니 혀 짧은 소리로 말했지요, '고트 데어 존.'"], ["‘Gott der Sohn.’ He laughed again and lisped, ‘Gott der Sohn.’ ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ Then he laughed and said as best he could, ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ I went away, and two days after I happened to be passing, and he shouted to me of himself, ‘Uncle, Gott der Vater, Gott der Sohn,’ and he had only forgotten ‘Gott der heilige Geist.’ But I reminded him of it and I felt very sorry for him again.", "'고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트(Gott der heilige Geist).' 그러자 그 애가 웃으며 제 딴에는 최선을 다해 말했습니다, '고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트.'"], ["But he was taken away, and I did not see him again.", "저는 떠났고, 이틀 뒤에 마침 그 앞을 지나가게 되었는데, 그 애가 먼저 저를 보고 소리쳤습니다, '아저씨, 고트 데어 파터, 고트 데어 존,' 그런데 '고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트'만 잊어버렸더군요."], ["Twenty-three years passed.", "하지만 제가 그걸 일러 주었고, 또다시 그 애가 무척 가여웠습니다. 그런데 그 애는 다른 데로 보내졌고, 저는 다시는 그 애를 보지 못했습니다."], ["I am sitting one morning in my study, a white-haired old man, when there walks into the room a blooming young man, whom I should never have recognized, but he held up his finger and said, laughing, ‘Gott der Vater, Gott der Sohn, and Gott der heilige Geist.", "이십삼 년이 흘렀지요. 어느 날 아침 제가 머리가 하얗게 센 노인이 되어 서재에 앉아 있는데, 한 혈색 좋은 젊은이가 방으로 걸어 들어왔습니다, 저로서는 도무지 알아볼 수 없는 얼굴이었지요, 그런데 그가 손가락을 치켜들고 웃으며 말하더군요, '고트 데어 파터, 고트 데어 존, 그리고 고트 데어 하일리게 가이스트."], ["I have just arrived and have come to thank you for that pound of nuts, for no one else ever bought me a pound of nuts; you are the only one that ever did.’ And then I remembered my happy youth and the poor child in the yard, without boots on his feet, and my heart was touched and I said, ‘You are a grateful young man, for you have remembered all your life the pound of nuts I bought you in your childhood.’ And I embraced him and blessed him.", "막 도착해서 그 호두 한 파운드에 감사드리러 왔습니다, 저한테 호두 한 파운드 사 준 사람은 그 누구도 없었고, 사 주신 분은 선생님이 유일하니까요.' 그러자 저는 제 행복했던 젊은 시절과 발에 장화도 신지 못한 채 뒤뜰에 있던 그 가엾은 아이가 떠올랐고, 가슴이 뭉클해져서 말했습니다, '자네는 고마움을 아는 젊은이로군, 어린 시절에 내가 사 준 호두 한 파운드를 평생 기억하고 있었으니 말일세.'"], ["And I shed tears.", "그러고는 그를 껴안고 축복해 주었습니다."], ["He laughed, but he shed tears, too ...", "그리고 저는 눈물을 흘렸지요. 그도 웃었지만, 역시 눈물을 흘렸습니다..."], ["for the Russian often laughs when he ought to be weeping.", "러시아 사람은 마땅히 울어야 할 때 곧잘 웃는 법이니까요."], ["But he did weep; I saw it.", "하지만 그는 정말로 울었습니다, 제가 보았으니까요."], ["And now, alas!...”", "그런데 지금은, 아아!...\""]], [["“And I am weeping now, German, I am weeping now, too, you saintly man,”", "\"저도 지금 울고 있습니다, 독일 양반, 저도 지금 울고 있어요, 성자 같은 분이여.\""], ["Mitya cried suddenly.", "미탸가 갑자기 외쳤다."]], [["In any case the anecdote made a certain favorable impression on the public.", "어쨌든 이 일화는 방청객들에게 어느 정도 호의적인 인상을 주었다."], ["But the chief sensation in Mitya’s favor was created by the evidence of Katerina Ivanovna, which I will describe directly.", "그러나 미탸에게 유리한 가장 큰 반향을 불러일으킨 것은 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언이었으니, 그것은 곧이어 묘사하기로 하겠다."], ["Indeed, when the witnesses à décharge, that is, called by the defense, began giving evidence, fortune seemed all at once markedly more favorable to Mitya, and what was particularly striking, this was a surprise even to the counsel for the defense.", "실로 변호 측 증인들, 즉 변호인이 부른 증인들이 증언을 시작하자 운세가 갑자기 미탸에게 눈에 띄게 더 유리해진 듯했고, 특히 놀라운 것은 이것이 변호인 자신에게조차 뜻밖이었다는 점이다."], ["But before Katerina Ivanovna was called, Alyosha was examined, and he recalled a fact which seemed to furnish positive evidence against one important point made by the prosecution.", "그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 호명되기 전에 알료샤가 심문을 받았는데, 그는 검찰이 내세운 한 가지 중요한 논점에 대해 확실한 반증이 될 만한 사실 하나를 떠올렸다."]], [["# Chapter IV. Fortune Smiles On Mitya", "# Chapter IV. Fortune Smiles On Mitya"]], [["It came quite as a surprise even to Alyosha himself.", "그것은 알료샤 자신에게조차 자못 뜻밖의 일이었다."], ["He was not required to take the oath, and I remember that both sides addressed him very gently and sympathetically.", "그는 선서를 요구받지 않았으며, 양측 모두 그를 무척 부드럽고 동정 어린 태도로 대했던 것으로 나는 기억한다."], ["It was evident that his reputation for goodness had preceded him.", "그의 선량함에 관한 평판이 그보다 먼저 이곳에 당도해 있었던 것이 분명했다."], ["Alyosha gave his evidence modestly and with restraint, but his warm sympathy for his unhappy brother was unmistakable.", "알료샤는 겸손하게, 그리고 절제된 어조로 증언했으나, 불행한 형을 향한 그의 따뜻한 동정만큼은 누가 보아도 틀림이 없었다."], ["In answer to one question, he sketched his brother’s character as that of a man, violent-tempered perhaps and carried away by his passions, but at the same time honorable, proud and generous, capable of self-sacrifice, if necessary.", "어느 질문에 답하면서 그는 형의 성품을 이렇게 묘사했다. 어쩌면 격정적이고 자신의 정념에 휩쓸리기 쉬운 사람이지만, 동시에 명예를 알고 자존심이 강하며 너그럽고, 필요하다면 자기를 희생할 줄도 아는 사람이라고."], ["He admitted, however, that, through his passion for Grushenka and his rivalry with his father, his brother had been of late in an intolerable position.", "그러나 그는, 그루셴카를 향한 정념과 아버지를 둘러싼 경쟁 때문에 형이 근래에 견딜 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다는 점은 인정했다."], ["But he repelled with indignation the suggestion that his brother might have committed a murder for the sake of gain, though he recognized that the three thousand roubles had become almost an obsession with Mitya; that he looked upon them as part of the inheritance he had been cheated of by his father, and that, indifferent as he was to money as a rule, he could not even speak of that three thousand without fury.", "다만 형이 이득을 노리고 살인을 저질렀을지도 모른다는 암시에 대해서는 분개하며 물리쳤으니, 그러면서도 삼천 루블이 미탸에게 거의 강박이 되어 있었다는 것, 그가 그 돈을 아버지에게서 사취당한 유산의 일부로 여겼다는 것, 그리고 평소에는 돈에 무심한 사람이면서도 그 삼천 루블에 관해서만큼은 분노 없이는 입에 올리지도 못했다는 것은 인정했다."], ["As for the rivalry of the two “ladies,” as the prosecutor expressed it—that is, of Grushenka and Katya—he answered evasively and was even unwilling to answer one or two questions altogether.", "검사의 표현을 빌리면 두 \"숙녀\"의, 즉 그루셴카와 카탸의 경쟁에 관해서는 그는 얼버무리며 답했고, 한두 가지 질문에는 아예 대답하기를 꺼리기까지 했다."]], [["“Did your brother tell you, anyway, that he intended to kill your father?”", "\"어쨌거나 형이 당신에게, 아버지를 죽일 작정이라고 말한 적이 있습니까?\""], ["asked the prosecutor.", "검사가 물었다."], ["“You can refuse to answer if you think necessary,”", "\"필요하다고 여기시면 답을 거부하셔도 됩니다.\""], ["he added.", "그는 덧붙였다."]], [["“He did not tell me so directly,”", "\"제게 그렇게 대놓고 말한 적은 없습니다.\""], ["answered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 대답했다."]], [["“How so?", "\"그렇다면 어떻게요?"], ["Did he indirectly?”", "에둘러서 말했습니까?\""]], [["“He spoke to me once of his hatred for our father and his fear that at an extreme moment ...", "\"형은 언젠가 제게 아버지에 대한 증오를 말했고, 극단적인 순간에…"], ["at a moment of fury, he might perhaps murder him.”", "분노에 사로잡힌 순간에 어쩌면 아버지를 죽일지도 모른다는 두려움을 토로했습니다.\""]], [["“And you believed him?”", "\"그래서 당신은 그 말을 믿었습니까?\""]], [["“I am afraid to say that I did.", "\"믿었다고 말하기가 두렵습니다."], ["But I never doubted that some higher feeling would always save him at the fatal moment, as it has indeed saved him, for it was not he killed my father,”", "하지만 저는 그 운명의 순간에 언제나 어떤 더 높은 감정이 형을 구해 주리라는 것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었고, 실제로 그것이 형을 구했습니다. 제 아버지를 죽인 사람은 형이 아니니까요.\""], ["Alyosha said firmly, in a loud voice that was heard throughout the court.", "알료샤는 법정 전체에 울려 퍼지는 큰 목소리로 단호하게 말했다."]], [["The prosecutor started like a war-horse at the sound of a trumpet.", "검사는 나팔 소리를 들은 군마처럼 움찔하고 일어섰다."]], [["“Let me assure you that I fully believe in the complete sincerity of your conviction and do not explain it by or identify it with your affection for your unhappy brother.", "\"당신의 확신이 전적으로 진실하다는 것을 제가 충분히 믿고 있으며, 그것을 불행한 형에 대한 애정으로 설명하거나 그것과 동일시하지 않는다는 점을 분명히 말씀드립니다."], ["Your peculiar view of the whole tragic episode is known to us already from the preliminary investigation.", "이 비극적인 사건 전체에 대한 당신의 독특한 견해는 이미 예심에서부터 저희가 알고 있습니다."], ["I won’t attempt to conceal from you that it is highly individual and contradicts all the other evidence collected by the prosecution.", "그것이 지극히 개인적이며 검찰이 수집한 다른 모든 증거와 모순된다는 점을 당신에게 굳이 숨기지는 않겠습니다."], ["And so I think it essential to press you to tell me what facts have led you to this conviction of your brother’s innocence and of the guilt of another person against whom you gave evidence at the preliminary inquiry?”", "그러므로 저는, 도대체 어떤 사실들이 당신으로 하여금 형의 무죄와, 예심에서 당신이 증언하여 지목한 또 다른 인물의 유죄를 이토록 확신하게 만들었는지를 캐묻는 것이 반드시 필요하다고 봅니다.\""]], [["“I only answered the questions asked me at the preliminary inquiry,”", "\"저는 예심에서 제게 던져진 질문들에만 답했을 뿐입니다.\""], ["replied Alyosha, slowly and calmly.", "알료샤가 천천히, 그리고 차분하게 대답했다."], ["“I made no accusation against Smerdyakov of myself.”", "\"제 입으로 스메르댜코프를 고발하지는 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Yet you gave evidence against him?”", "\"하지만 당신은 그에게 불리한 증언을 하지 않았습니까?\""]], [["“I was led to do so by my brother Dmitri’s words.", "\"제 형 드미트리의 말에 이끌려 그렇게 한 것입니다."], ["I was told what took place at his arrest and how he had pointed to Smerdyakov before I was examined.", "저는 형이 체포될 때 무슨 일이 있었는지, 형이 제가 심문받기도 전에 어떻게 스메르댜코프를 지목했는지를 전해 들었습니다."], ["I believe absolutely that my brother is innocent, and if he didn’t commit the murder, then—”", "저는 제 형이 무죄라고 절대적으로 믿으며, 만약 형이 살인을 저지르지 않았다면, 그렇다면…\""]], [["“Then Smerdyakov? Why Smerdyakov?", "\"그렇다면 스메르댜코프란 말입니까?"], ["And why are you so completely persuaded of your brother’s innocence?”", "왜 스메르댜코프죠? 그리고 당신은 어째서 형의 무죄를 그토록 완전히 확신합니까?\""]], [["“I cannot help believing my brother.", "\"저는 제 형을 믿지 않을 수가 없습니다."], ["I know he wouldn’t lie to me.", "형이 제게 거짓말을 하지 않으리라는 것을 압니다."], ["I saw from his face he wasn’t lying.”", "형의 얼굴을 보고 그가 거짓말을 하고 있지 않다는 것을 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Only from his face?", "\"오로지 그 얼굴에서 말입니까?"], ["Is that all the proof you have?”", "당신이 가진 증거라곤 그것이 전부입니까?\""]], [["“I have no other proof.”", "\"다른 증거는 없습니다.\""]], [["“And of Smerdyakov’s guilt you have no proof whatever but your brother’s word and the expression of his face?”", "\"그리고 스메르댜코프의 유죄에 대해서도 형의 말과 그 얼굴 표정 외에는 아무런 증거가 없는 거로군요?\""]], [["“No, I have no other proof.”", "\"네, 다른 증거는 없습니다.\""]], [["The prosecutor dropped the examination at this point.", "검사는 이 대목에서 신문을 중단했다."], ["The impression left by Alyosha’s evidence on the public was most disappointing.", "알료샤의 증언이 방청객에게 남긴 인상은 더없이 실망스러운 것이었다."], ["There had been talk about Smerdyakov before the trial; some one had heard something, some one had pointed out something else, it was said that Alyosha had gathered together some extraordinary proofs of his brother’s innocence and Smerdyakov’s guilt, and after all there was nothing, no evidence except certain moral convictions so natural in a brother.", "재판이 열리기 전부터 스메르댜코프에 관한 이야기가 떠돌았으니, 누군가는 무언가를 들었고, 누군가는 또 다른 무언가를 지적했으며, 알료샤가 형의 무죄와 스메르댜코프의 유죄를 입증할 어떤 비범한 증거들을 한데 모아 두었다는 소문도 있었다. 그러나 결국 아무것도 없었고, 형이라면 당연히 품을 법한 어떤 도덕적 확신 외에는 아무 증거도 없었던 것이다."]], [["But Fetyukovitch began his cross-examination.", "그러나 페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다."], ["On his asking Alyosha when it was that the prisoner had told him of his hatred for his father and that he might kill him, and whether he had heard it, for instance, at their last meeting before the catastrophe, Alyosha started as he answered, as though only just recollecting and understanding something.", "그가 알료샤에게, 피고가 아버지에 대한 증오와 그를 죽일지도 모른다는 말을 그에게 한 것이 언제였는지, 가령 그 참사가 있기 전 마지막 만남에서 들은 것인지를 묻자, 알료샤는 마치 이제야 비로소 무언가를 기억해 내고 깨닫는 듯 움찔하며 대답했다."]], [["“I remember one circumstance now which I’d quite forgotten myself.", "\"이제야 한 가지 정황이 떠오르는데, 저 자신도 까맣게 잊고 있던 일입니다."], ["It wasn’t clear to me at the time, but now—”", "그때는 제게 분명하지 않았으나, 지금은…\""]], [["And, obviously only now for the first time struck by an idea, he recounted eagerly how, at his last interview with Mitya that evening under the tree, on the road to the monastery, Mitya had struck himself on the breast, “the upper part of the breast,”", "그리고 분명 이제야 처음으로 어떤 생각에 부딪힌 듯, 그는 열을 올리며 이야기했다. 그날 저녁 수도원으로 가는 길의 나무 아래에서 미탸와 마지막으로 만났을 때, 미탸가 자기 가슴을, \"가슴 위쪽을\""], ["and had repeated several times that he had a means of regaining his honor, that that means was here, here on his breast.", "치면서 자신의 명예를 되찾을 방도가 있다고, 그 방도가 여기, 바로 이 가슴에 있다고 몇 번이나 되풀이해 말했다는 것이었다."], ["“I thought, when he struck himself on the breast, he meant that it was in his heart,”", "\"형이 가슴을 쳤을 때 저는 그것이 마음속에 있다는 뜻인 줄로만 알았습니다.\""], ["Alyosha continued, “that he might find in his heart strength to save himself from some awful disgrace which was awaiting him and which he did not dare confess even to me.", "알료샤가 계속했다. \"형이 자기를 기다리고 있는 어떤 끔찍한 치욕에서, 제게조차 차마 털어놓지 못하는 그 치욕에서 자기를 구해 낼 힘을 마음속에서 찾을 수 있으리라는 뜻으로요."], ["I must confess I did think at the time that he was speaking of our father, and that the disgrace he was shuddering at was the thought of going to our father and doing some violence to him.", "솔직히 고백하건대, 저는 그때 형이 우리 아버지에 관해 말하고 있으며, 형이 몸서리치는 그 치욕이란 아버지에게 찾아가 무슨 폭행을 저지른다는 생각이라고 여겼습니다."], ["Yet it was just then that he pointed to something on his breast, so that I remember the idea struck me at the time that the heart is not on that part of the breast, but below, and that he struck himself much too high, just below the neck, and kept pointing to that place.", "그런데 바로 그때 형이 자기 가슴의 어딘가를 가리켰고, 그래서 저는 그때 이런 생각이 떠올랐던 것을 기억합니다. 심장은 가슴의 그 부위가 아니라 그 아래에 있는데 형은 너무 높은 곳을, 바로 목 아래쪽을 치며 자꾸 그 자리를 가리키는구나, 하고요."], ["My idea seemed silly to me at the time, but he was perhaps pointing then to that little bag in which he had fifteen hundred roubles!”", "그때는 제 생각이 어리석게 느껴졌지만, 형은 어쩌면 그때 천오백 루블이 든 바로 그 작은 주머니를 가리키고 있었던 겁니다!\""]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그거야.\""], ["Mitya cried from his place.", "미탸가 제자리에서 외쳤다."], ["“That’s right, Alyosha, it was the little bag I struck with my fist.”", "\"맞다, 알료샤, 내가 주먹으로 친 건 그 작은 주머니였어.\""]], [["Fetyukovitch flew to him in hot haste entreating him to keep quiet, and at the same instant pounced on Alyosha.", "페튜코비치는 황급히 그에게 날아가듯 다가가 제발 조용히 해 달라고 간청하는 한편, 같은 순간 알료샤에게 달려들었다."], ["Alyosha, carried away himself by his recollection, warmly expressed his theory that this disgrace was probably just that fifteen hundred roubles on him, which he might have returned to Katerina Ivanovna as half of what he owed her, but which he had yet determined not to repay her and to use for another purpose—namely, to enable him to elope with Grushenka, if she consented.", "알료샤 자신도 기억에 휩쓸려, 그 치욕이란 아마도 형의 몸에 지니고 있던 바로 그 천오백 루블이었으리라는 자신의 추론을 열렬히 펼쳤다. 형은 그 돈을 카테리나 이바노브나에게 빚진 금액의 절반으로 돌려줄 수도 있었지만, 그것을 갚지 않고 다른 목적에—다시 말해 그루셴카가 응한다면 그녀와 함께 달아나는 데 쓰기로 작정하고 있었다는 것이었다."]], [["“It is so, it must be so,”", "\"그래요, 분명히 그럴 겁니다.\""], ["exclaimed Alyosha, in sudden excitement.", "알료샤가 갑자기 흥분하여 외쳤다."], ["“My brother cried several times that half of the disgrace, half of it (he said half several times) he could free himself from at once, but that he was so unhappy in his weakness of will that he wouldn’t do it ...", "\"형은 그 치욕의 절반을, 절반을(형은 절반이라고 몇 번이나 말했습니다) 당장에라도 벗어 버릴 수 있는데, 의지가 약해 너무도 불행하게 그것을 하지 못하겠노라고…"], ["that he knew beforehand he was incapable of doing it!”", "그것을 할 수 없는 인간임을 미리부터 알고 있노라고 몇 번이나 외쳤습니다!\""]], [["“And you clearly, confidently remember that he struck himself just on this part of the breast?”", "\"그리고 당신은 형이 가슴의 바로 이 부위를 쳤다는 것을 분명하게, 확신을 가지고 기억하는 겁니까?\""], ["Fetyukovitch asked eagerly.", "페튜코비치가 열을 올려 물었다."]], [["“Clearly and confidently, for I thought at the time, ‘Why does he strike himself up there when the heart is lower down?’ and the thought seemed stupid to me at the time ...", "\"분명하게, 확신을 가지고 기억합니다. 그때 제가 '심장은 더 아래에 있는데 어째서 저렇게 위쪽을 칠까?'"], ["I remember its seeming stupid ... it flashed through my mind.", "하고 생각했고, 그 생각이 그때는 제게 어리석게 느껴졌으니까요…"], ["That’s what brought it back to me just now.", "그게 어리석게 느껴졌던 것을 기억합니다…"], ["How could I have forgotten it till now?", "머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔습니다."], ["It was that little bag he meant when he said he had the means but wouldn’t give back that fifteen hundred.", "바로 그것이 방금 제게 이 일을 되살려 준 겁니다. 어떻게 지금까지 그걸 잊고 있었을까요?"], ["And when he was arrested at Mokroe he cried out—I know, I was told it—that he considered it the most disgraceful act of his life that when he had the means of repaying Katerina Ivanovna half (half, note!) what he owed her, he yet could not bring himself to repay the money and preferred to remain a thief in her eyes rather than part with it.", "형이 방도가 있는데도 그 천오백 루블을 돌려주지 않겠다고 했을 때 가리킨 것이 바로 그 작은 주머니였습니다. 그리고 형은 모크로예에서 체포되었을 때 이렇게 외쳤다고 합니다—저는 압니다, 전해 들었습니다—카테리나 이바노브나에게 빚진 돈의 절반을(절반입니다, 유의하십시오!) 갚을 방도가 있었으면서도 차마 그 돈을 돌려주지 못했고, 그것을 내놓느니 차라리 그녀의 눈에 도둑으로 남기를 택했다는 것이야말로 자기 인생에서 가장 수치스러운 짓이라고요."], ["And what torture, what torture that debt has been to him!”", "그리고 그 빚이 형에게 얼마나 큰 고통, 얼마나 큰 고통이었던지!\""], ["Alyosha exclaimed in conclusion.", "알료샤가 끝맺으며 외쳤다."]], [["The prosecutor, of course, intervened.", "물론 검사가 끼어들었다."], ["He asked Alyosha to describe once more how it had all happened, and several times insisted on the question, “Had the prisoner seemed to point to anything?", "그는 알료샤에게 그 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지 다시 한번 묘사해 달라고 했고, \"피고가 무언가를 가리키는 것처럼 보였습니까?"], ["Perhaps he had simply struck himself with his fist on the breast?”", "어쩌면 그저 주먹으로 가슴을 친 것은 아니었습니까?\" 하는 질문을 몇 번이나 거듭 다그쳤다."]], [["“But it was not with his fist,”", "\"하지만 주먹은 아니었습니다.\""], ["cried Alyosha; “he pointed with his fingers and pointed here, very high up....", "알료샤가 외쳤다. \"형은 손가락으로 가리켰고, 여기, 아주 높은 곳을 가리켰습니다…."], ["How could I have so completely forgotten it till this moment?”", "어떻게 제가 이 순간까지 그것을 그토록 까맣게 잊고 있었을까요?\""]], [["The President asked Mitya what he had to say to the last witness’s evidence.", "재판장이 미탸에게 마지막 증인의 증언에 대해 할 말이 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Mitya confirmed it, saying that he had been pointing to the fifteen hundred roubles which were on his breast, just below the neck, and that that was, of course, the disgrace, “A disgrace I cannot deny, the most shameful act of my whole life,”", "미탸는 그것을 확인해 주며, 자기는 가슴에, 바로 목 아래에 지니고 있던 천오백 루블을 가리키고 있었으며, 그것이 물론 그 치욕이었다고 말했다. \"부정할 수 없는 치욕입니다, 제 평생에 가장 수치스러운 짓이지요.\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“I might have repaid it and didn’t repay it.", "\"갚을 수 있었는데도 갚지 않았습니다."], ["I preferred to remain a thief in her eyes rather than give it back.", "그것을 돌려주느니 차라리 그녀의 눈에 도둑으로 남기를 택했습니다."], ["And the most shameful part of it was that I knew beforehand I shouldn’t give it back!", "그리고 가장 수치스러운 대목은, 내가 그것을 돌려주지 않으리라는 걸 미리부터 알고 있었다는 겁니다!"], ["You are right, Alyosha!", "네 말이 맞다, 알료샤!"], ["Thanks, Alyosha!”", "고맙다, 알료샤!\""]], [["So Alyosha’s cross-examination ended.", "이렇게 하여 알료샤에 대한 반대신문이 끝났다."], ["What was important and striking about it was that one fact at least had been found, and even though this were only one tiny bit of evidence, a mere hint at evidence, it did go some little way towards proving that the bag had existed and had contained fifteen hundred roubles and that the prisoner had not been lying at the preliminary inquiry when he alleged at Mokroe that those fifteen hundred roubles were “his own.”", "그 중요하고 인상적인 점은, 적어도 한 가지 사실이 밝혀졌다는 것이었으니, 그것이 비록 증거의 자그마한 한 조각, 증거의 한낱 실마리에 지나지 않더라도, 그 주머니가 실제로 존재했고 그 안에 천오백 루블이 들어 있었다는 것, 그리고 모크로예에서 그 천오백 루블이 \"자기 것\"이라고 주장했을 때 피고가 예심에서 거짓말을 하고 있던 것이 아니라는 것을 어느 정도 입증하는 데 보탬이 되었기 때문이다."], ["Alyosha was glad.", "알료샤는 기뻤다."], ["With a flushed face he moved away to the seat assigned to him.", "그는 상기된 얼굴로 자기에게 배정된 자리로 물러났다."], ["He kept repeating to himself: “How was it I forgot?", "그는 줄곧 속으로 되뇌었다. \"어쩌다 잊고 있었을까?"], ["How could I have forgotten it?", "어떻게 그걸 잊을 수 있었을까?"], ["And what made it come back to me now?”", "그리고 무엇이 지금 그걸 되살려 냈을까?\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna was called to the witness-box.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 증언대로 불려 나왔다."], ["As she entered something extraordinary happened in the court.", "그녀가 들어서자 법정에서 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["The ladies clutched their lorgnettes and opera-glasses.", "부인들은 손잡이 안경과 오페라글라스를 움켜쥐었다."], ["There was a stir among the men: some stood up to get a better view.", "남자들 사이에서도 술렁임이 일어, 더 잘 보려고 자리에서 일어서는 이들도 있었다."], ["Everybody alleged afterwards that Mitya had turned “white as a sheet”", "사람들은 훗날 입을 모아, 그녀가 들어서는 순간 미탸의 얼굴이 \"백지장처럼 하얗게\""], ["on her entrance.", "질렸다고 말했다."], ["All in black, she advanced modestly, almost timidly.", "온통 검은 옷을 입은 그녀는 겸손하게, 거의 머뭇거리듯 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["It was impossible to tell from her face that she was agitated; but there was a resolute gleam in her dark and gloomy eyes.", "그녀가 동요하고 있는지는 그 얼굴로는 도무지 알 수 없었으나, 어둡고 음울한 두 눈에는 단호한 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["I may remark that many people mentioned that she looked particularly handsome at that moment.", "많은 사람이 그 순간 그녀가 유난히 아름다워 보였다고 언급했다는 것을 나는 짚어 둘 만하다."], ["She spoke softly but clearly, so that she was heard all over the court.", "그녀는 나직하면서도 또렷하게 말했고, 그래서 법정 전체에 그 소리가 들렸다."], ["She expressed herself with composure, or at least tried to appear composed.", "그녀는 침착하게, 적어도 침착해 보이려 애쓰며 자신의 생각을 표현했다."], ["The President began his examination discreetly and very respectfully, as though afraid to touch on “certain chords,” and showing consideration for her great unhappiness.", "재판장은 \"어떤 현(絃)\"을 건드리기를 두려워하듯, 또 그녀의 크나큰 불행을 배려하듯, 신중하고 매우 정중하게 신문을 시작했다."], ["But in answer to one of the first questions Katerina Ivanovna replied firmly that she had been formerly betrothed to the prisoner, “until he left me of his own accord...”", "그러나 처음 던진 질문들 가운데 하나에 답하면서 카테리나 이바노브나는, 자기가 한때 피고와 약혼했었다고 단호하게 대답하고는 \"그가 제 발로 저를 떠날 때까지요…\""], ["she added quietly.", "하고 조용히 덧붙였다."], ["When they asked her about the three thousand she had entrusted to Mitya to post to her relations, she said firmly, “I didn’t give him the money simply to send it off.", "그녀가 친척에게 부쳐 달라고 미탸에게 맡긴 삼천 루블에 관해 물어보자, 그녀는 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 그저 부쳐 달라고 그 돈을 그에게 준 것이 아닙니다."], ["I felt at the time that he was in great need of money....", "저는 그때 그가 돈이 몹시 절실히 필요하다는 것을 느끼고 있었어요…."], ["I gave him the three thousand on the understanding that he should post it within the month if he cared to.", "저는 그가 원한다면 한 달 안에 부치면 된다는 양해 아래 삼천 루블을 주었습니다."], ["There was no need for him to worry himself about that debt afterwards.”", "그가 나중에 그 빚을 두고 스스로를 괴롭힐 필요는 전혀 없었어요.\""]], [["I will not repeat all the questions asked her and all her answers in detail.", "그녀에게 던져진 모든 질문과 그녀의 모든 대답을 일일이 옮기지는 않겠다."], ["I will only give the substance of her evidence.", "그저 그녀 증언의 요지만을 전하겠다."]], [["“I was firmly convinced that he would send off that sum as soon as he got money from his father,”", "\"저는 그가 아버지에게서 돈을 받는 즉시 그 액수를 부치리라고 굳게 확신하고 있었습니다.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“I have never doubted his disinterestedness and his honesty ...", "\"저는 그의 무사무욕함과 정직함을… 돈 문제에 있어서의…"], ["his scrupulous honesty ... in money matters.", "그 빈틈없는 정직함을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없습니다."], ["He felt quite certain that he would receive the money from his father, and spoke to me several times about it.", "그는 아버지에게서 돈을 받으리라고 굳게 믿었고, 그것에 대해 제게 여러 번 이야기했어요."], ["I knew he had a feud with his father and have always believed that he had been unfairly treated by his father.", "저는 그가 아버지와 반목하고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그가 아버지에게 부당한 대우를 받아 왔다고 늘 믿어 왔습니다."], ["I don’t remember any threat uttered by him against his father.", "그가 아버지를 향해 어떤 위협을 입에 올린 것은 기억나지 않아요."], ["He certainly never uttered any such threat before me.", "적어도 제 앞에서는 결코 그런 위협을 한 적이 없습니다."], ["If he had come to me at that time, I should have at once relieved his anxiety about that unlucky three thousand roubles, but he had given up coming to see me ...", "만약 그가 그때 제게 찾아왔더라면, 저는 그 불운한 삼천 루블에 대한 그의 불안을 당장 덜어 주었을 거예요. 하지만 그는 저를 찾아오기를 그만두었고…"], ["and I myself was put in such a position ... that I could not invite him....", "또 저 자신도 그를 부를 수 없는 그런 처지에 놓여 있었습니다…."], ["And I had no right, indeed, to be exacting as to that money,”", "그리고 사실 저는 그 돈에 대해 까다롭게 굴 권리도 없었어요.\""], ["she added suddenly, and there was a ring of resolution in her voice.", "그녀가 갑자기 덧붙였고, 그 목소리에는 결연한 울림이 있었다."], ["“I was once indebted to him for assistance in money for more than three thousand, and I took it, although I could not at that time foresee that I should ever be in a position to repay my debt.”", "\"저는 한때 삼천 루블이 넘는 돈을 그에게서 도움받아 빚진 적이 있고, 그때는 제가 그 빚을 갚을 처지가 되리라고 미처 내다볼 수 없었음에도 그 돈을 받았으니까요.\""]], [["There was a note of defiance in her voice.", "그녀의 목소리에는 도전적인 가락이 묻어났다."], ["It was then Fetyukovitch began his cross-examination.", "바로 그때 페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다."]], [["“Did that take place not here, but at the beginning of your acquaintance?”", "\"그것은 이곳에서가 아니라, 두 분이 알게 된 초기에 있었던 일입니까?\""], ["Fetyukovitch suggested cautiously, feeling his way, instantly scenting something favorable.", "페튜코비치가 무언가 유리한 것의 냄새를 단숨에 맡고는, 조심스럽게 길을 더듬듯 넌지시 물었다."], ["I must mention in parenthesis that, though Fetyukovitch had been brought from Petersburg partly at the instance of Katerina Ivanovna herself, he knew nothing about the episode of the four thousand roubles given her by Mitya, and of her “bowing to the ground to him.”", "여기서 한마디 곁들여 두어야 할 것은, 페튜코비치가 일부는 카테리나 이바노브나 자신의 청에 따라 페테르부르크에서 불려 온 것이었음에도, 미탸가 그녀에게 준 사천 루블의 일화와 그녀가 \"그에게 땅에 닿도록 절을 한\" 일에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고 있었다는 사실이다."], ["She concealed this from him and said nothing about it, and that was strange.", "그녀는 그것을 그에게 숨기고 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 이는 기이한 일이었다."], ["It may be pretty certainly assumed that she herself did not know till the very last minute whether she would speak of that episode in the court, and waited for the inspiration of the moment.", "그녀 자신도 마지막 순간까지 법정에서 그 일화를 말할지 말지 알지 못한 채, 그 순간의 영감을 기다리고 있었던 것이라고 거의 확실하게 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["No, I can never forget those moments.", "아니, 나는 그 순간들을 결코 잊을 수 없다."], ["She began telling her story.", "그녀는 자기 이야기를 시작했다."], ["She told everything, the whole episode that Mitya had told Alyosha, and her bowing to the ground, and her reason.", "미탸가 알료샤에게 들려주었던 그 일화 전부를, 자기가 땅에 닿도록 절한 일과 그 이유까지 죄다 털어놓았다."], ["She told about her father and her going to Mitya, and did not in one word, in a single hint, suggest that Mitya had himself, through her sister, proposed they should “send him Katerina Ivanovna” to fetch the money.", "그녀는 자기 아버지에 관해, 그리고 자기가 미탸에게 찾아간 일에 관해 이야기했으나, 미탸가 자기 누이를 통해 돈을 가지러 \"카테리나 이바노브나를 보내자\"고 직접 제안했다는 것은 단 한마디로도, 단 한 가닥 암시로도 비치지 않았다."], ["She generously concealed that and was not ashamed to make it appear as though she had of her own impulse run to the young officer, relying on something ...", "그녀는 너그럽게 그것을 감추었고, 마치 자기가 무언가를 믿고서 제 충동에 따라 그 젊은 장교에게 달려가… 돈을 구걸한 것처럼 보이게 하면서도 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["to beg him for the money.", "그것은 실로 엄청난 일이었다!"], ["It was something tremendous! I turned cold and trembled as I listened.", "나는 그 이야기를 들으며 온몸이 싸늘해지고 떨려 왔다."], ["The court was hushed, trying to catch each word.", "법정은 한마디 한마디를 놓치지 않으려 숨을 죽였다."], ["It was something unexampled.", "그것은 전례 없는 일이었다."], ["Even from such a self-willed and contemptuously proud girl as she was, such an extremely frank avowal, such sacrifice, such self-immolation, seemed incredible.", "그녀처럼 제멋대로이고 경멸하듯 오만한 처녀에게서조차, 그토록 극도로 솔직한 고백, 그토록 큰 희생, 그토록 큰 자기 헌신은 도무지 믿기지 않는 것이었다."], ["And for what, for whom?", "그리고 무엇을 위해, 누구를 위해?"], ["To save the man who had deceived and insulted her and to help, in however small a degree, in saving him, by creating a strong impression in his favor.", "자기를 속이고 모욕한 사내를 구하기 위해, 그리고 그에게 유리한 강렬한 인상을 자아내어, 단 한 점이나마 그를 구하는 데 보탬이 되기 위해서였다."], ["And, indeed, the figure of the young officer who, with a respectful bow to the innocent girl, handed her his last four thousand roubles—all he had in the world—was thrown into a very sympathetic and attractive light, but ...", "그리고 실로, 순결한 처녀에게 공손히 절을 하며 자기 수중의 마지막 사천 루블을—세상에 가진 전부를—건넨 그 젊은 장교의 모습은 무척 동정 어리고 매혹적인 빛 속에 던져졌으나, 그러나…"], ["I had a painful misgiving at heart!", "내 마음에는 괴로운 불안이 자리했다!"], ["I felt that calumny might come of it later (and it did, in fact, it did).", "나는 훗날 이것에서 중상이 빚어질 수 있으리라는 것을 느꼈다(그리고 실제로 그렇게 되었다, 정말로)."], ["It was repeated all over the town afterwards with spiteful laughter that the story was perhaps not quite complete—that is, in the statement that the officer had let the young lady depart “with nothing but a respectful bow.”", "훗날 온 고을에 심술궂은 웃음과 함께 되풀이된 이야기는, 그 사연이 어쩌면 그리 완전하지 않으리라는 것—즉 장교가 그 젊은 아가씨를 \"공손한 절 하나만으로\" 돌려보냈다는 진술 말이다."], ["It was hinted that something was here omitted.", "여기에는 무언가 빠진 것이 있다는 암시가 떠돌았다."]], [["“And even if nothing had been omitted, if this were the whole story,”", "\"설령 빠진 것이 없다 한들, 이것이 사연의 전부라 한들,\""], ["the most highly respected of our ladies maintained, “even then it’s very doubtful whether it was creditable for a young girl to behave in that way, even for the sake of saving her father.”", "우리 고을에서 가장 존경받는 부인이 주장했다. \"그렇더라도 젊은 처녀가 제 아비를 구하기 위해서라지만 그런 식으로 처신하는 것이 과연 떳떳한 일이었는지는 매우 의심스럽지요.\""]], [["And can Katerina Ivanovna, with her intelligence, her morbid sensitiveness, have failed to understand that people would talk like that?", "그러니 카테리나 이바노브나가, 그 명석함과 그 병적인 예민함을 지닌 그녀가, 사람들이 이렇게 수군대리라는 것을 헤아리지 못했을 리가 있겠는가?"], ["She must have understood it, yet she made up her mind to tell everything.", "그녀는 분명 그것을 헤아렸으면서도 모든 것을 털어놓기로 결심한 것이다."], ["Of course, all these nasty little suspicions as to the truth of her story only arose afterwards and at the first moment all were deeply impressed by it.", "물론 그녀 이야기의 진실성을 둘러싼 이 모든 추잡하고 자질구레한 의혹은 죄다 훗날에야 일어난 것이고, 첫 순간에는 모두가 깊이 감동했다."], ["As for the judges and the lawyers, they listened in reverent, almost shame-faced silence to Katerina Ivanovna.", "판사들과 변호인들로 말하자면, 그들은 경건하게, 거의 면구스러운 침묵 속에서 카테리나 이바노브나의 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["The prosecutor did not venture upon even one question on the subject.", "검사는 그 일에 대해 단 하나의 질문도 감히 꺼내지 못했다."], ["Fetyukovitch made a low bow to her.", "페튜코비치는 그녀에게 깊이 고개를 숙여 절했다."], ["Oh, he was almost triumphant!", "오, 그는 거의 승리감에 차 있었다!"], ["Much ground had been gained.", "많은 발판이 마련된 것이었다."], ["For a man to give his last four thousand on a generous impulse and then for the same man to murder his father for the sake of robbing him of three thousand—the idea seemed too incongruous.", "한 사내가 너그러운 충동에 휩쓸려 자기 수중의 마지막 사천 루블을 내주었던 그 사내가, 삼천 루블을 빼앗으려고 제 아버지를 죽인다는 것은—그 발상부터가 너무도 앞뒤가 맞지 않게 여겨졌다."], ["Fetyukovitch felt that now the charge of theft, at least, was as good as disproved. “The case”", "페튜코비치는 이제 적어도 절도의 혐의만큼은 사실상 반증된 거나 다름없다고 느꼈다."], ["was thrown into quite a different light.", "\"사건\"은 사뭇 다른 빛 속으로 던져졌다."], ["There was a wave of sympathy for Mitya.", "미탸에 대한 동정의 물결이 일었다."], ["As for him....", "그 자신으로 말하자면…."], ["I was told that once or twice, while Katerina Ivanovna was giving her evidence, he jumped up from his seat, sank back again, and hid his face in his hands.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 증언하는 동안 그가 한두 번 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다가 다시 주저앉으며 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다고 나는 전해 들었다."], ["But when she had finished, he suddenly cried in a sobbing voice:", "그러나 그녀가 말을 마치자, 그는 별안간 흐느끼는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Katya, why have you ruined me?”", "\"카탸, 어째서 당신은 나를 파멸시킨 거요?\""], ["and his sobs were audible all over the court.", "그의 흐느낌은 법정 전체에 들렸다."], ["But he instantly restrained himself, and cried again:", "그러나 그는 이내 자제하더니 다시 외쳤다."]], [["“Now I am condemned!”", "\"이제 나는 끝장이오!\""]], [["Then he sat rigid in his place, with his teeth clenched and his arms across his chest.", "그러고는 이를 악물고 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 얹은 채 제자리에 굳은 듯 앉아 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna remained in the court and sat down in her place.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 법정에 남아 자기 자리에 앉았다."], ["She was pale and sat with her eyes cast down.", "그녀는 창백한 낯빛으로 눈을 내리깐 채 앉아 있었다."], ["Those who were sitting near her declared that for a long time she shivered all over as though in a fever.", "그녀 가까이 앉아 있던 사람들은, 그녀가 한참 동안 마치 열병이라도 앓듯 온몸을 떨었다고 전했다."], ["Grushenka was called.", "그루셴카가 불려 나왔다."]], [["I am approaching the sudden catastrophe which was perhaps the final cause of Mitya’s ruin.", "나는 어쩌면 미탸의 파멸을 결정지은 최종 원인이었을지도 모를 그 돌연한 파국에 다가가고 있다."], ["For I am convinced, so is every one—all the lawyers said the same afterwards—that if the episode had not occurred, the prisoner would at least have been recommended to mercy.", "나는—아니 모두가—그 일화가 일어나지 않았더라면 피고는 적어도 선처 권고는 받았으리라고 확신하고 있으며, 훗날 변호사들도 모두 같은 말을 했기 때문이다."], ["But of that later. A few words first about Grushenka.", "그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하고, 먼저 그루셴카에 관해 몇 마디 해 두겠다."]], [["She, too, was dressed entirely in black, with her magnificent black shawl on her shoulders.", "그녀 또한 온통 검은 옷차림에, 어깨에는 그 화려한 검은 숄을 두르고 있었다."], ["She walked to the witness-box with her smooth, noiseless tread, with the slightly swaying gait common in women of full figure.", "그녀는 매끄럽고 소리 없는 걸음걸이로, 풍만한 몸매의 여인들에게 흔한 살짝 흔들리는 듯한 자태로 증언대를 향해 걸어갔다."], ["She looked steadily at the President, turning her eyes neither to the right nor to the left.", "그녀는 시선을 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 돌리지 않고 재판장을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["To my thinking she looked very handsome at that moment, and not at all pale, as the ladies alleged afterwards.", "내가 보기에 그녀는 그 순간 무척 아름다워 보였고, 훗날 부인들이 주장한 것처럼 결코 창백하지 않았다."], ["They declared, too, that she had a concentrated and spiteful expression.", "그들은 또한 그녀의 표정이 굳어 있고 심술궂었다고도 말했다."], ["I believe that she was simply irritated and painfully conscious of the contemptuous and inquisitive eyes of our scandal-loving public.", "나는 그녀가 그저 짜증이 났을 뿐이며, 추문이나 좋아하는 우리 방청객들의 경멸 어리고 캐묻는 듯한 시선을 괴롭게 의식하고 있었던 거라고 믿는다."], ["She was proud and could not stand contempt.", "그녀는 자존심이 강했고 경멸을 견디지 못했다."], ["She was one of those people who flare up, angry and eager to retaliate, at the mere suggestion of contempt.", "그녀는 경멸의 기미만 비쳐도 발끈 성을 내며 앙갚음하려 드는 그런 부류의 사람이었다."], ["There was an element of timidity, too, of course, and inward shame at her own timidity, so it was not strange that her tone kept changing.", "물론 거기에는 소심함의 요소도, 그리고 자기 소심함에 대한 내심의 부끄러움도 있었으니, 그녀의 어조가 자꾸 바뀐 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다."], ["At one moment it was angry, contemptuous and rough, and at another there was a sincere note of self-condemnation.", "어느 순간에는 성나고 경멸에 차고 거칠다가도, 또 어느 순간에는 진심 어린 자책의 가락이 묻어났다."], ["Sometimes she spoke as though she were taking a desperate plunge; as though she felt, “I don’t care what happens, I’ll say it....”", "때로 그녀는 마치 필사적으로 몸을 던지는 것처럼, 마치 \"무슨 일이 벌어지든 상관없어, 말해 버리겠어…\" 하고 느끼기라도 하는 것처럼 말했다."], ["Apropos of her acquaintance with Fyodor Pavlovitch, she remarked curtly, “That’s all nonsense, and was it my fault that he would pester me?”", "표도르 파블로비치와 알게 된 사정에 관해서는 그녀가 퉁명스럽게 한마디 했다. \"그건 다 헛소리예요, 그 사람이 절 귀찮게 따라다닌 게 내 잘못이었나요?\""], ["But a minute later she added, “It was all my fault.", "그러나 잠시 뒤 그녀는 덧붙였다. \"다 제 잘못이었어요."], ["I was laughing at them both—at the old man and at him, too—and I brought both of them to this.", "제가 그 둘 다를—그 영감이며 그 사람이며—다 가지고 놀았고, 그래서 둘 다 이 지경으로 끌고 온 거예요."], ["It was all on account of me it happened.”", "다 저 때문에 일어난 일이에요.\""]], [["Samsonov’s name came up somehow.", "어쩌다 삼소노프의 이름이 거론되었다."], ["“That’s nobody’s business,”", "\"그건 누구도 상관할 일이 아니에요.\""], ["she snapped at once, with a sort of insolent defiance.", "그녀가 일종의 뻔뻔스러운 반항조로 당장 쏘아붙였다."], ["“He was my benefactor; he took me when I hadn’t a shoe to my foot, when my family had turned me out.”", "\"그분은 제 은인이세요. 제 식구들이 절 내쫓아 발에 신을 신발조차 없을 때 절 거두어 준 분이라고요.\""], ["The President reminded her, though very politely, that she must answer the questions directly, without going off into irrelevant details.", "재판장이, 비록 매우 정중하게나마, 그녀에게 곁가지 같은 자질구레한 이야기로 빠지지 말고 질문에 곧이곧대로 답해야 한다고 일러 주었다."], ["Grushenka crimsoned and her eyes flashed.", "그루셴카는 새빨개졌고 두 눈이 번뜩였다."]], [["The envelope with the notes in it she had not seen, but had only heard from “that wicked wretch” that Fyodor Pavlovitch had an envelope with notes for three thousand in it.", "지폐가 든 봉투를 그녀는 본 적이 없으며, 다만 \"그 사악한 놈\"에게서 표도르 파블로비치가 삼천 루블어치 지폐가 든 봉투를 가지고 있다는 말을 들었을 뿐이라고 했다."], ["“But that was all foolishness.", "\"하지만 그건 다 어리석은 짓이었어요."], ["I was only laughing. I wouldn’t have gone to him for anything.”", "저는 그저 비웃고 있었을 뿐, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테는 가지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["“To whom are you referring as ‘that wicked wretch’?”", "\"'그 사악한 놈'이라고 하셨는데 누구를 가리키는 겁니까?\""], ["inquired the prosecutor.", "검사가 물었다."]], [["“The lackey, Smerdyakov, who murdered his master and hanged himself last night.”", "\"하인이던 스메르댜코프죠, 제 주인을 죽이고 어젯밤 목매달아 죽은 그놈요.\""]], [["She was, of course, at once asked what ground she had for such a definite accusation; but it appeared that she, too, had no grounds for it.", "물론 그녀는 곧 그토록 단정적인 고발을 하는 무슨 근거가 있느냐는 질문을 받았으나, 그녀에게도 역시 아무런 근거가 없는 것으로 드러났다."]], [["“Dmitri Fyodorovitch told me so himself; you can believe him.", "\"드미트리 표도로비치가 제게 직접 그렇게 말했어요. 그 사람 말을 믿으셔도 됩니다."], ["The woman who came between us has ruined him; she is the cause of it all, let me tell you,”", "우리 사이에 끼어든 그 여자가 그 사람을 파멸시킨 거예요. 그 여자가 이 모든 일의 원흉이라고요, 똑똑히 말씀드리지요.\""], ["Grushenka added.", "그루셴카가 덧붙였다."], ["She seemed to be quivering with hatred, and there was a vindictive note in her voice.", "그녀는 증오로 부르르 떨고 있는 듯했고, 그 목소리에는 앙심에 찬 가락이 묻어 있었다."]], [["She was again asked to whom she was referring.", "그녀는 누구를 가리키는 것이냐는 질문을 다시 받았다."]], [["“The young lady, Katerina Ivanovna there.", "\"저기 저 아가씨, 카테리나 이바노브나요."], ["She sent for me, offered me chocolate, tried to fascinate me.", "그 여자가 절 불러다 초콜릿을 권하며 절 호리려고 들었어요."], ["There’s not much true shame about her, I can tell you that....”", "그 여자한테 진짜 수치심 같은 건 별로 없다고요, 제가 똑똑히 말씀드리지요….\""]], [["At this point the President checked her sternly, begging her to moderate her language.", "이 대목에서 재판장이 그녀를 준엄하게 제지하며 말을 삼가 달라고 청했다."], ["But the jealous woman’s heart was burning, and she did not care what she did.", "그러나 질투에 사로잡힌 여자의 가슴은 활활 타오르고 있었으니, 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하든 개의치 않았다."]], [["“When the prisoner was arrested at Mokroe,”", "\"피고가 모크로예에서 체포될 때,\" 검사가 물었다."], ["the prosecutor asked, “every one saw and heard you run out of the next room and cry out: ‘It’s all my fault.", "\"모두가 당신이 옆방에서 뛰쳐나와 '이건 다 제 잘못이에요. 우리 함께 시베리아로 가요!'"], ["We’ll go to Siberia together!’ So you already believed him to have murdered his father?”", "하고 외치는 것을 보고 들었습니다. 그렇다면 당신은 이미 그가 제 아버지를 죽였다고 믿고 있었던 겁니까?\""]], [["“I don’t remember what I felt at the time,”", "\"그때 제가 무엇을 느꼈는지는 기억나지 않아요.\""], ["answered Grushenka.", "그루셴카가 대답했다."], ["“Every one was crying out that he had killed his father, and I felt that it was my fault, that it was on my account he had murdered him.", "\"다들 그가 제 아버지를 죽였다고 소리쳐 대고 있었고, 저는 그게 제 잘못이며 저 때문에 그가 그를 죽인 거라고 느꼈어요."], ["But when he said he wasn’t guilty, I believed him at once, and I believe him now and always shall believe him.", "하지만 그가 자기는 무죄라고 말하자, 저는 곧장 그를 믿었고, 지금도 믿으며, 앞으로도 늘 믿을 거예요."], ["He is not the man to tell a lie.”", "그 사람은 거짓말할 사람이 아니에요.\""]], [["Fetyukovitch began his cross-examination.", "페튜코비치가 반대신문을 시작했다."], ["I remember that among other things he asked about Rakitin and the twenty-five roubles “you paid him for bringing Alexey Fyodorovitch Karamazov to see you.”", "그가 여러 가지 중에서도 라키틴에 관해, 그리고 \"당신이 알렉세이 표도로비치 카라마조프를 데려와 만나게 해 준 대가로 그에게 치른\" 이십오 루블에 관해 물었던 것을 나는 기억한다."]], [["“There was nothing strange about his taking the money,”", "\"그 사람이 돈을 받은 게 뭐 이상할 거 있나요.\" 그루셴카가 성난 경멸을 담아 비웃었다."], ["sneered Grushenka, with angry contempt.", "\"그 사람은 늘 제게 돈을 받으러 왔어요."], ["“He was always coming to me for money: he used to get thirty roubles a month at least out of me, chiefly for luxuries: he had enough to keep him without my help.”", "한 달에 적어도 삼십 루블씩은 제게서 뜯어 갔는데, 주로 사치품에 쓰려는 거였죠. 제 도움 없이도 제 앞가림은 할 만한 사람이었어요.\""]], [["“What led you to be so liberal to Mr. Rakitin?”", "\"무엇 때문에 당신은 라키틴 씨에게 그토록 후하게 굴었습니까?\""], ["Fetyukovitch asked, in spite of an uneasy movement on the part of the President.", "페튜코비치가, 재판장이 불안한 듯 몸을 움직이는데도 아랑곳없이 물었다."]], [["“Why, he is my cousin.", "\"아니, 그 사람은 제 사촌인걸요."], ["His mother was my mother’s sister.", "그 사람 어머니가 제 어머니의 동생이거든요."], ["But he’s always besought me not to tell any one here of it, he is so dreadfully ashamed of me.”", "그런데도 그 사람은 자길 너무도 부끄러워하는 통에, 여기 누구한테도 그 얘길 하지 말아 달라고 늘 제게 간청했어요.\""]], [["This fact was a complete surprise to every one; no one in the town nor in the monastery, not even Mitya, knew of it.", "이 사실은 모두에게 완전히 뜻밖이었다. 고을에서도 수도원에서도, 심지어 미탸조차 그것을 아는 사람이 없었다."], ["I was told that Rakitin turned purple with shame where he sat.", "라키틴이 자기 자리에서 부끄러움에 시뻘게졌다고 나는 전해 들었다."], ["Grushenka had somehow heard before she came into the court that he had given evidence against Mitya, and so she was angry.", "그루셴카는 법정에 들어오기 전에 어떻게 들어 알고 있었으니, 그가 미탸에게 불리한 증언을 했다는 것을, 그래서 그녀는 성이 나 있었던 것이다."], ["The whole effect on the public, of Rakitin’s speech, of his noble sentiments, of his attacks upon serfdom and the political disorder of Russia, was this time finally ruined.", "라키틴의 연설이며 그 고결한 정서며 농노제와 러시아 정치의 무질서를 향한 그의 공격이 방청객에게 미친 효과는, 이번에야말로 결정적으로 망가지고 말았다. 페튜코비치는 흡족했다."], ["Fetyukovitch was satisfied: it was another godsend.", "그것은 또 하나의 하늘이 내린 선물이었다."], ["Grushenka’s cross-examination did not last long and, of course, there could be nothing particularly new in her evidence.", "그루셴카에 대한 반대신문은 오래 이어지지 않았고, 물론 그녀의 증언에 특별히 새로울 것은 있을 수 없었다."], ["She left a very disagreeable impression on the public; hundreds of contemptuous eyes were fixed upon her, as she finished giving her evidence and sat down again in the court, at a good distance from Katerina Ivanovna.", "그녀는 방청객에게 매우 불쾌한 인상을 남겼다. 그녀가 증언을 마치고 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 한참 떨어진 곳, 법정의 자기 자리에 다시 앉을 때, 수백의 경멸 어린 눈길이 그녀에게 쏟아졌다."], ["Mitya was silent throughout her evidence.", "미탸는 그녀의 증언 내내 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["He sat as though turned to stone, with his eyes fixed on the ground.", "그는 마치 돌처럼 굳어 두 눈을 땅바닥에 못 박은 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["Ivan was called to give evidence.", "이반이 증언하라고 불려 나왔다."]], [["# Chapter V. A Sudden Catastrophe", "# Chapter V. A Sudden Catastrophe"]], [["I may note that he had been called before Alyosha.", "한 가지 덧붙여 둘 것은, 그가 알료샤보다 먼저 호명되었었다는 점이다."], ["But the usher of the court announced to the President that, owing to an attack of illness or some sort of fit, the witness could not appear at the moment, but was ready to give his evidence as soon as he recovered.", "그러나 법정의 정리(廷吏)가 재판장에게 알리기를, 그 증인은 병이 도졌거나 무슨 발작 같은 것 때문에 당장은 출두할 수 없으나 회복되는 대로 곧 진술할 채비가 되어 있다고 했다."], ["But no one seemed to have heard it and it only came out later.", "그런데 아무도 그 말을 듣지 못한 듯했고, 그것은 한참 뒤에야 비로소 드러났다."]], [["His entrance was for the first moment almost unnoticed.", "그가 들어선 첫 순간에는 거의 아무도 그를 알아차리지 못했다."], ["The principal witnesses, especially the two rival ladies, had already been questioned.", "주요 증인들, 특히 두 연적(戀敵)인 부인들은 이미 신문을 받은 뒤였다."], ["Curiosity was satisfied for the time; the public was feeling almost fatigued.", "호기심은 당분간 충족되었고, 방청객들은 거의 지쳐 있는 기색이었다."], ["Several more witnesses were still to be heard, who probably had little information to give after all that had been given.", "아직 몇몇 증인이 더 진술해야 했으나, 이미 나온 이야기들에 비추어 보면 그들이 새로 보탤 정보는 아마 별로 없을 듯했다."], ["Time was passing.", "시간이 흘러갔다."], ["Ivan walked up with extraordinary slowness, looking at no one, and with his head bowed, as though plunged in gloomy thought.", "이반은 누구도 쳐다보지 않은 채 유난히 느릿느릿 걸어 나왔는데, 고개를 숙이고 있어 마치 음울한 생각에 잠긴 사람 같았다."], ["He was irreproachably dressed, but his face made a painful impression, on me at least: there was an earthy look in it, a look like a dying man’s.", "그는 흠잡을 데 없이 차려입었으나, 그 얼굴은 적어도 내게는 고통스러운 인상을 주었으니, 그 얼굴에는 흙빛이, 죽어 가는 사람 같은 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["His eyes were lusterless; he raised them and looked slowly round the court.", "그의 두 눈은 광채가 없었는데, 그가 그것을 들어 천천히 법정을 둘러보았다."], ["Alyosha jumped up from his seat and moaned “Ah!”", "알료샤가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 \"아!\""], ["I remember that, but it was hardly noticed.", "하고 신음하던 것을 나는 기억하지만, 그것을 알아챈 이는 거의 없었다."]], [["The President began by informing him that he was a witness not on oath, that he might answer or refuse to answer, but that, of course, he must bear witness according to his conscience, and so on, and so on.", "재판장은 먼저 그에게 알리기를, 그는 선서하지 않은 증인이므로 답할 수도 답하기를 거부할 수도 있으나, 물론 자기 양심에 따라 증언해야 한다는 등등을 일러 주었다."], ["Ivan listened and looked at him blankly, but his face gradually relaxed into a smile, and as soon as the President, looking at him in astonishment, finished, he laughed outright.", "이반은 그 말을 들으며 멍하니 그를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 차츰 풀리며 미소가 번졌고, 재판장이 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말을 맺자마자 그는 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Well, and what else?”", "\"자, 그래서, 또 뭡니까?\""], ["he asked in a loud voice.", "그가 큰 소리로 물었다."]], [["There was a hush in the court; there was a feeling of something strange.", "법정 안에 정적이 흘렀고, 무언가 기이한 기운이 감돌았다."], ["The President showed signs of uneasiness.", "재판장이 불안한 기색을 내비쳤다."]], [["“You ...", "\"증인은……"], ["are perhaps still unwell?”", "아직 몸이 편치 않으신 모양이오?\""], ["he began, looking everywhere for the usher.", "그가 정리를 사방으로 찾으며 말문을 열었다."]], [["“Don’t trouble yourself, your excellency, I am well enough and can tell you something interesting,”", "\"염려 마십시오, 재판장님, 저는 충분히 멀쩡하고 흥미로운 얘기 하나를 들려드릴 수 있습니다.\""], ["Ivan answered with sudden calmness and respectfulness.", "이반이 돌연 차분하고 공손하게 대답했다."]], [["“You have some special communication to make?”", "\"무슨 특별히 전할 말씀이라도 있는 것이오?\""], ["the President went on, still mistrustfully.", "재판장이 여전히 미심쩍어하며 말을 이었다."]], [["Ivan looked down, waited a few seconds and, raising his head, answered, almost stammering:", "이반은 눈을 내리뜨고 몇 초간 뜸을 들이더니, 고개를 들어 거의 더듬거리듯 대답했다."]], [["“No ...", "\"아니요……"], ["I haven’t.", "없습니다."], ["I have nothing particular.”", "특별한 건 아무것도 없습니다.\""]], [["They began asking him questions.", "사람들이 그에게 질문을 던지기 시작했다."], ["He answered, as it were, reluctantly, with extreme brevity, with a sort of disgust which grew more and more marked, though he answered rationally.", "그는 마지못한 듯, 극도로 짧게, 점점 더 뚜렷해지는 일종의 혐오를 담아 대답했는데, 그래도 답변만큼은 조리가 있었다."], ["To many questions he answered that he did not know.", "여러 질문에 그는 모른다고 답했다."], ["He knew nothing of his father’s money relations with Dmitri.", "아버지와 드미트리 사이의 금전 관계에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["“I wasn’t interested in the subject,”", "\"저는 그 문제에 관심이 없었습니다.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["Threats to murder his father he had heard from the prisoner.", "아버지를 죽이겠다는 위협이라면 피고에게서 들은 적이 있었다."], ["Of the money in the envelope he had heard from Smerdyakov.", "봉투 속의 돈에 관해서는 스메르댜코프에게서 들었다."]], [["“The same thing over and over again,”", "\"똑같은 얘기를 자꾸 되풀이하는군요.\""], ["he interrupted suddenly, with a look of weariness.", "그가 돌연 지친 기색으로 말을 끊었다."], ["“I have nothing particular to tell the court.”", "\"법정에 특별히 들려드릴 얘기는 없습니다.\""]], [["“I see you are unwell and understand your feelings,”", "\"증인이 편치 않은 줄 알겠고, 그 심정도 이해하오.\""], ["the President began.", "재판장이 입을 열었다."]], [["He turned to the prosecutor and the counsel for the defense to invite them to examine the witness, if necessary, when Ivan suddenly asked in an exhausted voice:", "그는 검사와 변호인 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 필요하다면 증인을 신문하라고 권하려 했는데, 그때 이반이 돌연 기진맥진한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Let me go, your excellency, I feel very ill.”", "\"보내 주십시오, 재판장님, 몸이 몹시 좋지 않습니다.\""]], [["And with these words, without waiting for permission, he turned to walk out of the court.", "그리고 이 말과 함께 그는 허락을 기다리지도 않고 법정을 나가려고 몸을 돌렸다."], ["But after taking four steps he stood still, as though he had reached a decision, smiled slowly, and went back.", "그러나 네 걸음쯤 떼고는 마치 결심이라도 선 듯 멈춰 서더니, 천천히 미소 짓고는 도로 돌아왔다."]], [["“I am like the peasant girl, your excellency ...", "\"저는 그 시골 처녀와 같습니다, 재판장님……"], ["you know.", "아시지요."], ["How does it go?", "그게 어떻게 되더라?"], ["‘I’ll stand up if I like, and I won’t if I don’t.’ They were trying to put on her sarafan to take her to church to be married, and she said, ‘I’ll stand up if I like, and I won’t if I don’t.’...", "'내키면 일어나고, 안 내키면 안 일어나겠어요.' 사람들이 그 처녀에게 사라판을 입혀 교회로 데려가 혼인시키려 하니까 그 애가 말했다지요, '내키면 일어나고, 안 내키면 안 일어나겠어요.'……"], ["It’s in some book about the peasantry.”", "농민들에 관한 무슨 책에 나오는 얘깁니다.\""]], [["“What do you mean by that?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["the President asked severely.", "재판장이 준엄하게 물었다."]], [["“Why, this,”", "\"왜냐면, 이런 겁니다.\""], ["Ivan suddenly pulled out a roll of notes.", "이반이 돌연 지폐 뭉치 하나를 꺼냈다."], ["“Here’s the money ...", "\"여기 돈이 있습니다……"], ["the notes that lay in that envelope”", "그 봉투 안에 들어 있던 지폐들입니다.\""], ["(he nodded towards the table on which lay the material evidence), “for the sake of which our father was murdered.", "(그는 물증이 놓인 탁자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다.) \"우리 아버지가 이것 때문에 살해당한 그 돈입니다."], ["Where shall I put them? Mr.", "어디에 둘까요?"], ["Superintendent, take them.”", "정리 양반, 받으시오.\""]], [["The usher of the court took the whole roll and handed it to the President.", "법정의 정리가 그 뭉치를 통째로 받아 재판장에게 건넸다."]], [["“How could this money have come into your possession if it is the same money?”", "\"만일 이것이 그 돈이라면, 어떻게 이 돈이 증인의 수중에 들어올 수 있었소?\""], ["the President asked wonderingly.", "재판장이 의아해하며 물었다."]], [["“I got them from Smerdyakov, from the murderer, yesterday....", "\"어제 스메르댜코프에게서, 그 살인범에게서 받았습니다……"], ["I was with him just before he hanged himself.", "그가 목을 매기 직전에 그와 함께 있었지요."], ["It was he, not my brother, killed our father.", "우리 아버지를 죽인 건 그놈입니다, 제 형이 아니라."], ["He murdered him and I incited him to do it ...", "그놈이 죽였고, 제가 그러도록 부추겼습니다……"], ["Who doesn’t desire his father’s death?”", "누군들 제 아비가 죽기를 바라지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Are you in your right mind?”", "\"증인은 제정신이오?\""], ["broke involuntarily from the President.", "재판장의 입에서 절로 튀어나왔다."]], [["“I should think I am in my right mind ...", "\"제정신이고말고요……"], ["in the same nasty mind as all of you ...", "여러분 모두와 똑같이 추악한 정신이지요…… 이 모든……"], ["as all these ... ugly faces.”", "추한 얼굴들과 똑같이.\""], ["He turned suddenly to the audience.", "그가 돌연 방청석을 향해 돌아섰다."], ["“My father has been murdered and they pretend they are horrified,”", "\"내 아버지가 살해당했는데, 저들은 끔찍해하는 척이나 하고 있어.\""], ["he snarled, with furious contempt.", "그가 사나운 경멸을 담아 으르렁거렸다."], ["“They keep up the sham with one another. Liars!", "\"저들은 서로서로 거짓 시늉을 계속하고 있지."], ["They all desire the death of their fathers.", "거짓말쟁이들! 저들 모두 제 아비가 죽기를 바라고 있어."], ["One reptile devours another....", "한 마리 파충류가 다른 파충류를 삼키는 거야……"], ["If there hadn’t been a murder, they’d have been angry and gone home ill-humored.", "살인이 없었더라면 저들은 화가 나서 언짢은 기분으로 집에 돌아갔을걸. 저들이 원하는 건 구경거리야!"], ["It’s a spectacle they want! Panem et circenses.", "빵과 서커스(Panem et circenses) 말이지."], ["Though I am one to talk!", "하기야 내가 누굴 탓하겠나마는!"], ["Have you any water?", "물 있습니까?"], ["Give me a drink for Christ’s sake!”", "제발, 그리스도의 이름으로 마실 것 좀 주시오!\""], ["He suddenly clutched his head.", "그가 돌연 제 머리를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["The usher at once approached him.", "정리가 곧장 그에게 다가갔다."], ["Alyosha jumped up and cried, “He is ill.", "알료샤가 벌떡 일어나 소리쳤다. \"저 사람은 병에 걸렸습니다."], ["Don’t believe him: he has brain fever.”", "믿지 마십시오, 뇌염을 앓고 있습니다.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna rose impulsively from her seat and, rigid with horror, gazed at Ivan.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 충동적으로 자리에서 일어나, 공포로 굳어진 채 이반을 응시했다."], ["Mitya stood up and greedily looked at his brother and listened to him with a wild, strange smile.", "미탸가 일어서서 형을 게걸스레 바라보며, 거칠고 기이한 미소를 띤 채 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Don’t disturb yourselves.", "\"동요들 마십시오."], ["I am not mad, I am only a murderer,”", "나는 미친 게 아니라, 그저 살인자일 뿐이오.\""], ["Ivan began again.", "이반이 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“You can’t expect eloquence from a murderer,”", "\"살인자에게 웅변을 기대할 수야 없지요.\""], ["he added suddenly for some reason and laughed a queer laugh.", "그가 무슨 까닭에선지 돌연 덧붙이고는 기묘한 웃음을 웃었다."]], [["The prosecutor bent over to the President in obvious dismay.", "검사가 명백히 당황한 기색으로 재판장에게 몸을 굽혔다."], ["The two other judges communicated in agitated whispers.", "다른 두 판사가 흥분한 속삭임으로 의견을 주고받았다."], ["Fetyukovitch pricked up his ears as he listened: the hall was hushed in expectation.", "페튜코비치는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었으며, 법정은 기대 속에 숨을 죽였다."], ["The President seemed suddenly to recollect himself.", "재판장이 돌연 정신을 차린 듯했다."]], [["“Witness, your words are incomprehensible and impossible here.", "\"증인, 증인의 말은 여기서 이해할 수도 없고 있을 수도 없는 것이오."], ["Calm yourself, if you can, and tell your story ...", "할 수 있다면 진정하고, 정말 할 말이 있거든……"], ["if you really have something to tell.", "증인의 이야기를 해 보시오."], ["How can you confirm your statement ...", "어떻게 증인의 진술을 입증할 수 있소……"], ["if indeed you are not delirious?”", "만일 정말로 헛소리를 하는 게 아니라면 말이오?\""]], [["“That’s just it.", "\"바로 그게 문제입니다."], ["I have no proof.", "저는 증거가 없습니다."], ["That cur Smerdyakov won’t send you proofs from the other world ...", "그 개 같은 스메르댜코프가 저세상에서…… 봉투에 담아 증거를 부쳐 줄 리는 없으니까요."], ["in an envelope. You think of nothing but envelopes—one is enough.", "여러분은 봉투 생각밖에 안 하시는군요—하나로 충분한데."], ["I’ve no witnesses ...", "저는 증인이 없습니다……"], ["except one, perhaps,”", "하나라면 모를까, 아마.\""], ["he smiled thoughtfully.", "그가 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“Who is your witness?”", "\"증인이 누구요?\""]], [["“He has a tail, your excellency, and that would be irregular!", "\"그놈은 꼬리가 달렸습니다, 재판장님, 그러니 격식에 맞질 않겠지요!"], ["Le diable n’existe point!", "악마는 존재하지 않는다(Le diable n'existe point)!"], ["Don’t pay attention: he is a paltry, pitiful devil,”", "신경 쓰지 마십시오, 그놈은 하찮고 가련한 악마입니다.\""], ["he added suddenly.", "그가 돌연 덧붙였다."], ["He ceased laughing and spoke as it were, confidentially.", "그는 웃음을 그치고, 마치 은밀히 털어놓듯 말했다."], ["“He is here somewhere, no doubt—under that table with the material evidence on it, perhaps.", "\"그놈은 분명 여기 어딘가에 있습니다—저 물증이 놓인 탁자 밑쯤일지도 모르지요."], ["Where should he sit if not there?", "거기가 아니면 어디 앉아 있겠습니까?"], ["You see, listen to me.", "자, 들어 보십시오."], ["I told him I don’t want to keep quiet, and he talked about the geological cataclysm ...", "제가 그놈에게 잠자코 있고 싶지 않다고 말했더니, 그놈은 지질학적 격변 얘기를 늘어놓더군요……"], ["idiocy!", "멍청한 소리!"], ["Come, release the monster ...", "자, 저 괴물을 풀어 주십시오……"], ["he’s been singing a hymn.", "그놈이 찬송가를 부르고 있었거든요."], ["That’s because his heart is light!", "제 마음이 가벼워서 그런 겁니다!"], ["It’s like a drunken man in the street bawling how ‘Vanka went to Petersburg,’ and I would give a quadrillion quadrillions for two seconds of joy.", "마치 길거리의 술 취한 사내가 '반카가 페테르부르크로 떠났다네' 하고 떠들어대는 것 같은데, 나라면 단 이 초의 기쁨을 위해 천조 곱하기 천조라도 내놓겠소."], ["You don’t know me!", "여러분은 나를 모릅니다!"], ["Oh, how stupid all this business is!", "아, 이 모든 일이 어찌 이리 멍청한지!"], ["Come, take me instead of him!", "자, 그놈 대신 나를 잡아가시오!"], ["I didn’t come for nothing....", "나는 괜히 온 게 아니란 말이오……"], ["Why, why is everything so stupid?...”", "어째서, 어째서 모든 게 이토록 멍청한 거요?……\""]], [["And he began slowly, and as it were reflectively, looking round him again.", "그러고서 그는 다시 주위를 둘러보며 느릿느릿, 마치 골똘히 생각하듯 말하기 시작했다."], ["But the court was all excitement by now.", "그러나 이제 법정은 온통 술렁이고 있었다."], ["Alyosha rushed towards him, but the court usher had already seized Ivan by the arm.", "알료샤가 그에게 달려갔으나, 법정의 정리가 이미 이반의 팔을 붙든 뒤였다."]], [["“What are you about?”", "\"무슨 짓이오?\""], ["he cried, staring into the man’s face, and suddenly seizing him by the shoulders, he flung him violently to the floor.", "그가 정리의 얼굴을 노려보며 소리치더니, 돌연 그의 어깨를 움켜쥐고는 바닥에 거칠게 내동댕이쳤다."], ["But the police were on the spot and he was seized.", "그러나 경관들이 곧장 달려들어 그를 제압했다."], ["He screamed furiously.", "그는 미친 듯이 비명을 질러댔다."], ["And all the time he was being removed, he yelled and screamed something incoherent.", "그리고 끌려 나가는 내내 그는 무언가 알아들을 수 없는 말을 고래고래 외쳐댔다."]], [["The whole court was thrown into confusion.", "법정 전체가 아수라장이 되었다."], ["I don’t remember everything as it happened.", "나는 그 일이 일어난 그대로 모두를 기억하지는 못한다."], ["I was excited myself and could not follow.", "나 자신도 흥분해 있어 미처 따라가지 못했다."], ["I only know that afterwards, when everything was quiet again and every one understood what had happened, the court usher came in for a reprimand, though he very reasonably explained that the witness had been quite well, that the doctor had seen him an hour ago, when he had a slight attack of giddiness, but that, until he had come into the court, he had talked quite consecutively, so that nothing could have been foreseen—that he had, in fact, insisted on giving evidence.", "다만 내가 아는 것은, 그 뒤 모든 것이 다시 잠잠해지고 무슨 일이 있었는지 다들 알게 되었을 때, 법정의 정리가 견책을 받았다는 사실이다. 비록 그는 매우 조리 있게 해명하기를, 그 증인은 멀쩡했고 한 시간 전에 의사가 그를 진찰했을 때 가벼운 현기증 발작이 있었을 뿐이며, 법정에 들어오기 전까지는 아주 또박또박 말을 했으므로 무슨 일이 벌어질지 예견할 도리가 없었다고, 게다가 그가 한사코 증언하겠노라 고집했다고 했지만 말이다."], ["But before every one had completely regained their composure and recovered from this scene, it was followed by another.", "그러나 모두가 평정을 완전히 되찾고 이 광경에서 벗어나기도 전에, 또 다른 광경이 뒤따랐다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna had an attack of hysterics.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 히스테리 발작을 일으킨 것이다."], ["She sobbed, shrieking loudly, but refused to leave the court, struggled, and besought them not to remove her.", "그녀는 크게 비명을 지르며 흐느꼈으나, 법정을 나가기를 거부한 채 몸부림치며 자기를 끌어내지 말아 달라고 애원했다."], ["Suddenly she cried to the President:", "그러더니 돌연 재판장을 향해 소리쳤다."]], [["“There is more evidence I must give at once ...", "\"제가 당장 더 드릴 증언이 있습니다……"], ["at once!", "당장이요!"], ["Here is a document, a letter ...", "여기 문서가, 편지가 있습니다……"], ["take it, read it quickly, quickly!", "받으세요, 어서, 어서 읽어 보세요!"], ["It’s a letter from that monster ...", "저 괴물이 보낸 편지예요……"], ["that man there, there!”", "저기, 저기 있는 저 남자가!\""], ["she pointed to Mitya.", "그녀가 미탸를 가리켰다."], ["“It was he killed his father, you will see that directly.", "\"저 사람이 제 아비를 죽인 거예요, 곧 아시게 될 거예요."], ["He wrote to me how he would kill his father!", "어떻게 제 아비를 죽일지 저한테 써 보냈다고요!"], ["But the other one is ill, he is ill, he is delirious!”", "하지만 저 다른 사람은 병에 걸렸어요, 병에 걸려서, 헛소리를 하는 거예요!\""], ["she kept crying out, beside herself.", "그녀가 제정신이 아닌 채로 거듭 외쳐댔다."]], [["The court usher took the document she held out to the President, and she, dropping into her chair, hiding her face in her hands, began convulsively and noiselessly sobbing, shaking all over, and stifling every sound for fear she should be ejected from the court.", "법정의 정리가 그녀가 내민 문서를 받아 재판장에게 건넸고, 그녀는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고는, 온몸을 떨며 발작적으로 소리 없이 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 법정에서 쫓겨날까 두려워 모든 소리를 억눌렀다."], ["The document she had handed up was that letter Mitya had written at the “Metropolis”", "그녀가 건넨 문서는 미탸가 '수도(首都)'"], ["tavern, which Ivan had spoken of as a “mathematical proof.”", "선술집에서 쓴, 이반이 \"수학적 증거\"라 일컬었던 바로 그 편지였다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["its mathematical conclusiveness was recognized, and had it not been for that letter, Mitya might have escaped his doom or, at least, that doom would have been less terrible.", "그 수학적 확실성은 인정되고 말았으니, 그 편지만 없었던들 미탸는 어쩌면 파멸을 면했거나, 적어도 그 파멸이 덜 끔찍했을지도 모른다."], ["It was, I repeat, difficult to notice every detail.", "거듭 말하거니와, 모든 세부를 일일이 알아채기란 어려웠다."], ["What followed is still confused to my mind.", "그 뒤에 벌어진 일은 지금도 내 머릿속에서 어수선하기만 하다."], ["The President must, I suppose, have at once passed on the document to the judges, the jury, and the lawyers on both sides.", "짐작건대 재판장은 그 문서를 곧장 판사들과 배심원, 그리고 양쪽 변호인에게 넘겼을 것이다."], ["I only remember how they began examining the witness.", "나는 다만 그들이 증인을 신문하기 시작하던 광경만 기억한다."], ["On being gently asked by the President whether she had recovered sufficiently, Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed impetuously:", "재판장이 충분히 진정되었느냐고 부드럽게 묻자, 카테리나 이바노브나는 격하게 외쳤다."]], [["“I am ready, I am ready!", "\"준비됐어요, 준비됐다고요!"], ["I am quite equal to answering you,”", "충분히 답할 수 있어요.\""], ["she added, evidently still afraid that she would somehow be prevented from giving evidence.", "그녀가 덧붙였는데, 어쩐지 자신이 증언을 못 하도록 방해받을까 봐 여전히 두려워하는 기색이 역력했다."], ["She was asked to explain in detail what this letter was and under what circumstances she received it.", "사람들이 그녀에게 이 편지가 무엇이며 어떤 정황에서 받게 되었는지 자세히 설명하라고 청했다."]], [["“I received it the day before the crime was committed, but he wrote it the day before that, at the tavern—that is, two days before he committed the crime.", "\"범행이 저질러지기 전날 받았지만, 그가 쓴 건 그보다 하루 전, 그 선술집에서였어요—그러니까 범행 이틀 전이지요."], ["Look, it is written on some sort of bill!”", "보세요, 무슨 계산서 같은 종이에 쓰여 있어요!\""], ["she cried breathlessly.", "그녀가 숨가쁘게 외쳤다."], ["“He hated me at that time, because he had behaved contemptibly and was running after that creature ...", "\"그 무렵 그 사람은 저를 미워했어요, 자기가 비열하게 굴면서 그 계집 꽁무니를 쫓아다니고 있었으니까요……"], ["and because he owed me that three thousand....", "게다가 저한테 그 삼천 루블을 빚지고 있었으니까요……"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["he was humiliated by that three thousand on account of his own meanness!", "그 사람은 제 비열함 탓에 그 삼천 루블 때문에 굴욕을 느꼈던 거예요!"], ["This is how it happened about that three thousand.", "그 삼천 루블 일은 이렇게 된 거예요."], ["I beg you, I beseech you, to hear me.", "부탁이에요, 간청하건대, 제 말을 들어 주세요."], ["Three weeks before he murdered his father, he came to me one morning.", "그가 제 아비를 죽이기 삼 주 전 어느 날 아침, 그가 저를 찾아왔어요."], ["I knew he was in want of money, and what he wanted it for.", "저는 그가 돈이 궁한 걸, 또 그 돈을 어디에 쓰려는지를 알고 있었어요."], ["Yes, yes—to win that creature and carry her off.", "그래요, 그래요—그 계집을 차지해 데리고 달아나려는 거였지요."], ["I knew then that he had been false to me and meant to abandon me, and it was I, I, who gave him that money, who offered it to him on the pretext of his sending it to my sister in Moscow.", "저는 그때 이미 그가 저를 배신했고 저를 버릴 작정인 걸 알고 있었는데, 그런데도 저는, 바로 제가 그에게 그 돈을 줬어요, 모스크바에 있는 제 언니에게 부쳐 달라는 핑계로 그 돈을 내밀었던 거예요."], ["And as I gave it him, I looked him in the face and said that he could send it when he liked, ‘in a month’s time would do.’ How, how could he have failed to understand that I was practically telling him to his face, ‘You want money to be false to me with your creature, so here’s the money for you.", "그러고서 돈을 건네면서 저는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 보며 말했어요, 내키는 대로 아무 때나 보내면 된다고, '한 달 뒤라도 괜찮다'고요. 어떻게, 어떻게 그가 모를 수가 있었겠어요, 제가 사실상 그의 면전에 대고 이렇게 말하고 있었다는 걸요, '당신은 그 계집과 함께 나를 배신하는 데 쓸 돈이 필요하지, 그러니 여기 돈이 있어."], ["I give it to you myself.", "내가 직접 주는 거야."], ["Take it, if you have so little honor as to take it!’ I wanted to prove what he was, and what happened?", "받을 만큼 염치가 없거든 받아!' 저는 그가 어떤 사람인지 증명하고 싶었던 거예요, 그런데 어떻게 됐을까요?"], ["He took it, he took it, and squandered it with that creature in one night....", "그는 받았어요, 받아서, 그 계집과 함께 하룻밤 새 탕진해 버렸어요……"], ["But he knew, he knew that I knew all about it.", "하지만 그는 알았어요, 제가 그 모든 걸 안다는 걸 알고 있었어요."], ["I assure you he understood, too, that I gave him that money to test him, to see whether he was so lost to all sense of honor as to take it from me.", "단언컨대 그도 알았어요, 제가 그를 시험하려고, 저한테서 돈을 받을 만큼 염치를 깡그리 잃은 사람인지 보려고 그 돈을 줬다는 걸요."], ["I looked into his eyes and he looked into mine, and he understood it all and he took it—he carried off my money!”", "저는 그의 눈을 들여다봤고 그도 제 눈을 들여다봤는데, 그는 그 모든 걸 알면서도 받아 갔어요—제 돈을 가지고 가 버린 거예요!\""]], [["“That’s true, Katya,”", "\"맞소, 카탸.\""], ["Mitya roared suddenly, “I looked into your eyes and I knew that you were dishonoring me, and yet I took your money.", "미탸가 돌연 우렁차게 외쳤다. \"나는 당신 눈을 들여다봤고, 당신이 나를 모욕하고 있다는 걸 알면서도, 그래도 당신 돈을 받았소."], ["Despise me as a scoundrel, despise me, all of you!", "나를 비열한 놈이라고 경멸하시오, 경멸해, 여러분 모두!"], ["I’ve deserved it!”", "나는 그래 마땅하오!\""]], [["“Prisoner,”", "\"피고,\""], ["cried the President, “another word and I will order you to be removed.”", "재판장이 외쳤다. \"한마디만 더 하면 퇴정시키겠소.\""]], [["“That money was a torment to him,”", "\"그 돈은 그에게 고통이었어요.\""], ["Katya went on with impulsive haste.", "카탸가 충동적으로 다급하게 말을 이었다."], ["“He wanted to repay it me.", "\"그는 저한테 그 돈을 갚고 싶어 했어요."], ["He wanted to, that’s true; but he needed money for that creature, too.", "갚고 싶어 했지요, 그건 사실이에요. 하지만 그 계집한테도 돈이 필요했으니까요."], ["So he murdered his father, but he didn’t repay me, and went off with her to that village where he was arrested.", "그래서 그는 제 아비를 죽였지만, 저한테는 갚지 않고 그 여자와 함께 그가 체포된 그 마을로 떠나 버렸어요."], ["There, again, he squandered the money he had stolen after the murder of his father.", "거기서 또 그는 제 아비를 죽인 뒤 훔친 돈을 탕진했지요."], ["And a day before the murder he wrote me this letter.", "그리고 살인 하루 전에 그는 저한테 이 편지를 썼어요."], ["He was drunk when he wrote it.", "그걸 쓸 때 그는 취해 있었어요."], ["I saw it at once, at the time.", "저는 그때 당장 알아챘지요."], ["He wrote it from spite, and feeling certain, positively certain, that I should never show it to any one, even if he did kill him, or else he wouldn’t have written it.", "그는 앙심에서 그걸 썼고, 제가 그걸 절대로 누구에게도 보이지 않으리라고 확신하면서, 설령 그가 정말 아비를 죽이더라도 그러리라고 단단히 믿으면서 썼던 거예요, 안 그랬으면 쓰지도 않았겠지요."], ["For he knew I shouldn’t want to revenge myself and ruin him!", "제가 복수를 하려 들거나 그를 파멸시키려 들지 않으리란 걸 알았으니까요!"], ["But read it, read it attentively—more attentively, please—and you will see that he had described it all in his letter, all beforehand, how he would kill his father and where his money was kept.", "하지만 읽어 보세요, 주의 깊게 읽어 보세요—더 주의 깊게, 부디—그러면 그가 편지에 모든 걸 미리, 어떻게 제 아비를 죽일지, 또 그 돈이 어디 숨겨져 있는지를 다 써 놓은 걸 아시게 될 거예요."], ["Look, please, don’t overlook that, there’s one phrase there, ‘I shall kill him as soon as Ivan has gone away.’ So he thought it all out beforehand how he would kill him,”", "보세요, 부디 그 대목을 놓치지 마세요, 거기 이런 구절이 있어요, '이반이 떠나는 즉시 나는 그를 죽일 것이다.' 그러니까 그는 어떻게 죽일지를 모두 미리 궁리해 놓았던 거예요.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna pointed out to the court with venomous and malignant triumph.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 독기 어린 악의에 찬 승리감으로 법정을 향해 지적했다. 아아!"], ["Oh! it was clear she had studied every line of that letter and detected every meaning underlining it.", "그녀가 그 편지의 한 줄 한 줄을 샅샅이 연구해 그 행간에 깔린 의미를 죄다 간파했음이 분명했다."], ["“If he hadn’t been drunk, he wouldn’t have written to me; but, look, everything is written there beforehand, just as he committed the murder after.", "\"취하지만 않았던들 그는 저한테 쓰지 않았을 거예요. 하지만 보세요, 모든 게 거기 미리 쓰여 있어요, 나중에 그가 살인을 저지른 그대로 말예요."], ["A complete program of it!”", "완벽한 계획서라고요!\""], ["she exclaimed frantically.", "그녀가 미친 듯이 외쳤다."]], [["She was reckless now of all consequences to herself, though, no doubt, she had foreseen them even a month ago, for even then, perhaps, shaking with anger, she had pondered whether to show it at the trial or not.", "이제 그녀는 자신에게 닥칠 온갖 결과에 아랑곳하지 않았으나, 의심할 바 없이 그녀는 한 달 전에 이미 그 결과를 내다보았을 것이니, 그때에도 어쩌면 분노에 떨면서 이것을 재판에서 내놓을지 말지를 곰곰이 헤아렸을 것이다."], ["Now she had taken the fatal plunge.", "이제 그녀는 그 치명적인 도약을 감행한 것이었다."], ["I remember that the letter was read aloud by the clerk, directly afterwards, I believe.", "내 기억으로 그 편지는 직후에 곧바로 서기에 의해 낭독되었던 듯하다."], ["It made an overwhelming impression.", "그것은 압도적인 인상을 주었다."], ["They asked Mitya whether he admitted having written the letter.", "사람들이 미탸에게 이 편지를 쓴 것을 인정하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“It’s mine, mine!”", "\"제 거예요, 제 거!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“I shouldn’t have written it, if I hadn’t been drunk!...", "\"취하지만 않았던들 쓰지 않았을 텐데!……"], ["We’ve hated each other for many things, Katya, but I swear, I swear I loved you even while I hated you, and you didn’t love me!”", "우리는 여러 가지로 서로를 미워했지, 카탸, 하지만 맹세코, 맹세코 나는 당신을 미워하면서도 사랑했소, 그런데 당신은 나를 사랑하지 않았소!\""]], [["He sank back on his seat, wringing his hands in despair.", "그는 절망에 빠져 두 손을 비틀며 자리에 풀썩 주저앉았다."], ["The prosecutor and counsel for the defense began cross-examining her, chiefly to ascertain what had induced her to conceal such a document and to give her evidence in quite a different tone and spirit just before.", "검사와 변호인이 그녀를 반대신문하기 시작했는데, 주로 무엇이 그녀로 하여금 그런 문서를 숨기고 또 조금 전까지 전혀 다른 어조와 정신으로 증언하게 만들었는지를 알아내려는 것이었다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["I was telling lies just now.", "저는 방금 거짓말을 하고 있었어요."], ["I was lying against my honor and my conscience, but I wanted to save him, for he has hated and despised me so!”", "제 명예와 양심을 저버리고 거짓을 말했어요, 하지만 그를 구하고 싶었어요, 그가 저를 그토록 미워하고 경멸했으니까요!\""], ["Katya cried madly.", "카탸가 미친 듯이 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, he has despised me horribly, he has always despised me, and do you know, he has despised me from the very moment that I bowed down to him for that money.", "\"아, 그는 저를 끔찍하게 경멸했어요, 늘 저를 경멸했지요, 그리고 아세요, 제가 그 돈 때문에 그에게 머리를 조아린 바로 그 순간부터 저를 경멸했어요."], ["I saw that....", "저는 봤어요……"], ["I felt it at once at the time, but for a long time I wouldn’t believe it.", "그때 당장 그걸 느꼈지만, 오랫동안 믿으려 하지 않았어요."], ["How often I have read it in his eyes, ‘You came of yourself, though.’ Oh, he didn’t understand, he had no idea why I ran to him, he can suspect nothing but baseness, he judged me by himself, he thought every one was like himself!”", "제가 그의 눈 속에서 얼마나 자주 그걸 읽었던지요, '그래도 네가 제 발로 왔잖아.' 아, 그는 몰랐어요, 제가 왜 그에게 달려갔는지 전혀 몰랐어요, 그는 비열함밖에는 아무것도 의심할 줄 모르고, 자기 잣대로 저를 판단했어요, 누구나 다 자기 같은 줄로 알았던 거예요!\""], ["Katya hissed furiously, in a perfect frenzy.", "카탸가 완전히 광란에 빠져 사납게 쉭쉭거렸다."], ["“And he only wanted to marry me, because I’d inherited a fortune, because of that, because of that!", "\"그리고 그가 저와 결혼하려 한 건 오로지 제가 재산을 물려받았기 때문이에요, 그것 때문에, 그것 때문이라고요!"], ["I always suspected it was because of that!", "저는 늘 그것 때문이라고 의심했어요!"], ["Oh, he is a brute!", "아, 그는 짐승이에요!"], ["He was always convinced that I should be trembling with shame all my life before him, because I went to him then, and that he had a right to despise me for ever for it, and so to be superior to me—that’s why he wanted to marry me!", "그는 제가 그때 그에게 갔다는 이유로 평생토록 그 앞에서 수치심에 떨리라고, 그리고 그 일로 자기는 저를 영원히 경멸할 권리가 있어 저보다 우월하리라고—언제나 그렇게 확신했어요, 그래서 저와 결혼하려 했던 거예요!"], ["That’s so, that’s all so!", "바로 그거예요, 모두 그래요!"], ["I tried to conquer him by my love—a love that knew no bounds.", "저는 제 사랑으로 그를 정복하려 했어요—한이 없는 사랑으로요."], ["I even tried to forgive his faithlessness; but he understood nothing, nothing!", "심지어 그의 불성실까지 용서하려고 애썼어요. 하지만 그는 아무것도, 아무것도 몰랐어요!"], ["How could he understand indeed?", "하기야 그가 어떻게 알았겠어요?"], ["He is a monster!", "그는 괴물인걸요!"], ["I only received that letter the next evening: it was brought me from the tavern—and only that morning, only that morning I wanted to forgive him everything, everything—even his treachery!”", "저는 그 편지를 바로 그 이튿날 저녁에야 받았어요. 선술집에서 제게 전해진 거예요—그런데 바로 그날 아침, 바로 그날 아침에 저는 그에게 모든 걸, 모든 걸 용서하고 싶었어요—그의 배신마저도요!\""]], [["The President and the prosecutor, of course, tried to calm her.", "물론 재판장과 검사는 그녀를 진정시키려 애썼다."], ["I can’t help thinking that they felt ashamed of taking advantage of her hysteria and of listening to such avowals.", "그들이 그녀의 히스테리를 이용해 그런 고백을 듣고 있는 것에 부끄러움을 느꼈으리라는 생각을 나는 떨칠 수가 없다."], ["I remember hearing them say to her, “We understand how hard it is for you; be sure we are able to feel for you,”", "그들이 그녀에게 \"당신에게 이것이 얼마나 괴로운지 우리도 압니다, 부디 우리도 당신의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있다고 믿으십시오\" 하는 둥, 이런저런 말을 하던 것을 나는 기억한다."], ["and so on, and so on. And yet they dragged the evidence out of the raving, hysterical woman.", "그러면서도 그들은 광란에 빠진 그 히스테리컬한 여자에게서 증언을 끌어냈다."], ["She described at last with extraordinary clearness, which is so often seen, though only for a moment, in such over-wrought states, how Ivan had been nearly driven out of his mind during the last two months trying to save “the monster and murderer,” his brother.", "마침내 그녀는, 이런 극도의 흥분 상태에서 잠시나마 흔히 보이곤 하는 그 비상한 명료함으로, 이반이 지난 두 달 동안 자기 형인 \"그 괴물이자 살인자\"를 구하려다 어떻게 거의 실성할 지경에 이르렀는지를 묘사했다."]], [["“He tortured himself,”", "\"그는 스스로를 괴롭혔어요.\""], ["she exclaimed, “he was always trying to minimize his brother’s guilt and confessing to me that he, too, had never loved his father, and perhaps desired his death himself.", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"그는 늘 형의 죄를 줄여 보려 애쓰면서, 자기 역시 한 번도 아버지를 사랑한 적이 없으며 어쩌면 자기 스스로도 아버지의 죽음을 바랐을지 모른다고 제게 고백했어요."], ["Oh, he has a tender, over-tender conscience!", "아, 그는 여리디여린 양심을 지녔어요!"], ["He tormented himself with his conscience!", "그는 제 양심에 스스로를 짓이겼어요!"], ["He told me everything, everything!", "그는 제게 모든 걸, 모든 걸 털어놨어요!"], ["He came every day and talked to me as his only friend.", "날마다 찾아와 유일한 벗에게 하듯 제게 이야기했지요."], ["I have the honor to be his only friend!”", "저는 그의 유일한 벗이라는 영광을 누리고 있어요!\""], ["she cried suddenly with a sort of defiance, and her eyes flashed.", "그녀가 돌연 일종의 반항기를 띠고 외쳤고, 그녀의 두 눈이 번득였다."], ["“He had been twice to see Smerdyakov.", "\"그는 스메르댜코프를 두 번 찾아갔어요."], ["One day he came to me and said, ‘If it was not my brother, but Smerdyakov committed the murder’ (for the legend was circulating everywhere that Smerdyakov had done it), ‘perhaps I too am guilty, for Smerdyakov knew I didn’t like my father and perhaps believed that I desired my father’s death.’ Then I brought out that letter and showed it him.", "어느 날 그가 제게 와서 말했어요, '만일 내 형이 아니라 스메르댜코프가 그 살인을 저지른 거라면'(스메르댜코프가 했다는 소문이 사방에 떠돌고 있었으니까요), '어쩌면 나도 죄가 있는지 몰라, 스메르댜코프는 내가 아버지를 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 알았고 어쩌면 내가 아버지의 죽음을 바란다고 믿었을지도 모르니까.' 그래서 저는 그 편지를 꺼내 그에게 보여 줬어요."], ["He was entirely convinced that his brother had done it, and he was overwhelmed by it.", "그는 형이 저질렀다고 완전히 확신했고, 그것에 압도당했어요. 자기 친형이 친부 살해범이라는 생각을 견딜 수 없었던 거예요!"], ["He couldn’t endure the thought that his own brother was a parricide!", "불과 일주일 전에 저는 그것이 그를 병들게 하고 있는 걸 봤어요."], ["Only a week ago I saw that it was making him ill.", "지난 며칠 동안 그는 제 앞에서 횡설수설했어요."], ["During the last few days he has talked incoherently in my presence.", "저는 그의 정신이 무너져 가는 걸 봤어요."], ["I saw his mind was giving way. He walked about, raving; he was seen muttering in the streets.", "그는 헛소리를 하며 돌아다녔고, 거리에서 혼자 중얼거리는 게 사람들 눈에 띄기도 했어요."], ["The doctor from Moscow, at my request, examined him the day before yesterday and told me that he was on the eve of brain fever—and all on his account, on account of this monster!", "모스크바에서 온 의사가, 제 청을 받고 그저께 그를 진찰하고는, 그가 뇌염 직전이라고 제게 말했어요—이게 다 그 사람 때문이에요, 이 괴물 때문이라고요!"], ["And last night he learnt that Smerdyakov was dead!", "그리고 어젯밤 그는 스메르댜코프가 죽었다는 걸 알게 됐어요!"], ["It was such a shock that it drove him out of his mind ...", "그 충격이 어찌나 컸던지 그를 실성하게 만들었어요……"], ["and all through this monster, all for the sake of saving the monster!”", "이게 다 이 괴물 때문이에요, 다 이 괴물을 구하겠다고 한 일 때문이라고요!\""]], [["Oh, of course, such an outpouring, such an avowal is only possible once in a lifetime—at the hour of death, for instance, on the way to the scaffold!", "아, 물론 이런 토로, 이런 고백이란 평생에 단 한 번이나 가능한 것이니—이를테면 죽음의 시각, 단두대로 끌려가는 길 위에서 같은 때 말이다!"], ["But it was in Katya’s character, and it was such a moment in her life.", "그러나 그것은 카탸의 기질에 들어 있는 것이었고, 그녀의 생애에서 바로 그런 순간이었다."], ["It was the same impetuous Katya who had thrown herself on the mercy of a young profligate to save her father; the same Katya who had just before, in her pride and chastity, sacrificed herself and her maidenly modesty before all these people, telling of Mitya’s generous conduct, in the hope of softening his fate a little.", "그녀는 아버지를 구하려고 어느 젊은 난봉꾼의 자비에 제 몸을 던졌던 바로 그 격정적인 카탸였고, 조금 전 자존심과 정결함 속에서 미탸의 운명을 조금이나마 누그러뜨려 보려는 바람으로 미탸의 너그러운 처신을 이야기하며 이 모든 사람들 앞에 자신과 처녀로서의 수치심을 희생했던 바로 그 카탸였다."], ["And now, again, she sacrificed herself; but this time it was for another, and perhaps only now—perhaps only at this moment—she felt and knew how dear that other was to her!", "그리고 이제 다시 그녀는 자신을 희생했으나, 이번에는 다른 사람을 위해서였고, 어쩌면 바로 지금에야—어쩌면 바로 이 순간에야—그녀는 그 다른 사람이 자기에게 얼마나 소중한지를 느끼고 깨달은 것이었다!"], ["She had sacrificed herself in terror for him, conceiving all of a sudden that he had ruined himself by his confession that it was he who had committed the murder, not his brother, she had sacrificed herself to save him, to save his good name, his reputation!", "그녀는 그를 위한 공포 속에서 자신을 희생했으니, 살인을 저지른 것이 형이 아니라 자기라는 그 고백으로 그가 스스로를 파멸시켰다고 돌연 단정하고는, 그를 구하려고, 그의 명예와 평판을 구하려고 자신을 희생한 것이었다!"]], [["And yet one terrible doubt occurred to one—was she lying in her description of her former relations with Mitya?—that was the question.", "그런데도 한 가지 끔찍한 의혹이 떠올랐으니—미탸와의 지난 관계를 묘사하면서 그녀가 거짓을 말한 것은 아닐까?—그것이 문제였다."], ["No, she had not intentionally slandered him when she cried that Mitya despised her for her bowing down to him!", "아니, 미탸가 자기의 절을 두고 자기를 경멸했다고 외쳤을 때, 그녀는 고의로 그를 중상한 것이 아니었다!"], ["She believed it herself.", "그녀 자신이 그것을 믿고 있었다."], ["She had been firmly convinced, perhaps ever since that bow, that the simple-hearted Mitya, who even then adored her, was laughing at her and despising her.", "그녀는, 어쩌면 그 절을 한 순간부터 줄곧, 그때조차도 자기를 흠모하던 그 순박한 미탸가 자기를 비웃고 경멸하고 있다고 굳게 믿어 왔던 것이다."], ["She had loved him with an hysterical, “lacerated”", "그녀는 오로지 자존심에서, 상처 입은 자존심에서, 히스테리컬하고 \"갈가리 찢긴\""], ["love only from pride, from wounded pride, and that love was not like love, but more like revenge.", "사랑으로 그를 사랑해 온 것이었으니, 그 사랑은 사랑이라기보다는 복수에 더 가까웠다."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["perhaps that lacerated love would have grown into real love, perhaps Katya longed for nothing more than that, but Mitya’s faithlessness had wounded her to the bottom of her heart, and her heart could not forgive him.", "어쩌면 그 갈가리 찢긴 사랑이 참된 사랑으로 자라났을지도 모르고, 어쩌면 카탸가 다른 무엇보다 바란 것이 바로 그것이었을지도 모르지만, 미탸의 불성실이 그녀의 마음을 밑바닥까지 후벼 놓았고, 그녀의 마음은 그를 용서할 수 없었다."], ["The moment of revenge had come upon her suddenly, and all that had been accumulating so long and so painfully in the offended woman’s breast burst out all at once and unexpectedly.", "복수의 순간이 돌연 그녀에게 들이닥쳤고, 모욕당한 여인의 가슴속에 그토록 오래 그토록 고통스럽게 쌓여 온 모든 것이 별안간 예기치 못하게 한꺼번에 터져 나온 것이었다."], ["She betrayed Mitya, but she betrayed herself, too.", "그녀는 미탸를 배신했으나, 자기 자신마저 배신했다."], ["And no sooner had she given full expression to her feelings than the tension of course was over and she was overwhelmed with shame.", "그리고 자신의 감정을 남김없이 쏟아내자마자, 물론 그 긴장은 끝났고 그녀는 수치심에 압도되었다."], ["Hysterics began again: she fell on the floor, sobbing and screaming.", "히스테리가 다시 시작되었으니, 그녀는 흐느끼며 비명을 지르고 바닥에 쓰러졌다."], ["She was carried out.", "사람들이 그녀를 들어 내갔다."], ["At that moment Grushenka, with a wail, rushed towards Mitya before they had time to prevent her.", "바로 그 순간 그루셴카가 통곡하며, 사람들이 미처 막을 새도 없이 미탸에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Mitya,”", "\"미탸,\""], ["she wailed, “your serpent has destroyed you!", "그녀가 통곡했다. \"당신의 그 독사가 당신을 파멸시켰어요!"], ["There, she has shown you what she is!”", "자, 봤지요, 저 여자가 제 본색을 드러냈잖아요!\""], ["she shouted to the judges, shaking with anger.", "그녀가 분노에 떨며 판사들을 향해 외쳤다."], ["At a signal from the President they seized her and tried to remove her from the court.", "재판장의 신호에 따라 사람들이 그녀를 붙들어 법정에서 끌어내려 했다."], ["She wouldn’t allow it.", "그녀는 응하지 않았다."], ["She fought and struggled to get back to Mitya.", "미탸에게로 되돌아가려고 몸부림치며 발버둥쳤다."], ["Mitya uttered a cry and struggled to get to her.", "미탸가 비명을 지르며 그녀에게 가려고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["He was overpowered.", "그는 제압당했다."]], [["Yes, I think the ladies who came to see the spectacle must have been satisfied—the show had been a varied one.", "그렇다, 구경거리를 보러 온 부인네들은 틀림없이 만족했을 것이니—그 볼거리는 참으로 다채로운 것이었다."], ["Then I remember the Moscow doctor appeared on the scene.", "그러고서 모스크바의 그 의사가 등장하던 장면이 내 기억에 떠오른다."], ["I believe the President had previously sent the court usher to arrange for medical aid for Ivan.", "짐작건대 재판장이 미리 정리를 보내 이반에게 의료 조치를 마련하도록 했던 모양이다."], ["The doctor announced to the court that the sick man was suffering from a dangerous attack of brain fever, and that he must be at once removed.", "의사는 법정에 알리기를, 그 환자는 위험한 뇌염 발작을 앓고 있으니 당장 옮겨야 한다고 했다."], ["In answer to questions from the prosecutor and the counsel for the defense he said that the patient had come to him of his own accord the day before yesterday and that he had warned him that he had such an attack coming on, but he had not consented to be looked after.", "검사와 변호인의 질문에 그는 답하기를, 그저께 그 환자가 제 발로 자기를 찾아왔으며, 그런 발작이 닥쳐오고 있다고 미리 경고해 주었으나 그가 보살핌을 받기를 마다했다고 했다. \"그는 분명 정상적인 정신 상태가 아니었습니다."], ["“He was certainly not in a normal state of mind: he told me himself that he saw visions when he was awake, that he met several persons in the street, who were dead, and that Satan visited him every evening,”", "그는 깨어 있을 때 환영을 본다고, 거리에서 이미 죽은 여러 사람을 만난다고, 그리고 매일 저녁 사탄이 찾아온다고 제게 직접 말했습니다.\""], ["said the doctor, in conclusion.", "의사가 끝으로 말했다."], ["Having given his evidence, the celebrated doctor withdrew.", "증언을 마치자 그 저명한 의사는 물러났다."], ["The letter produced by Katerina Ivanovna was added to the material proofs.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 제출한 편지는 물증에 추가되었다."], ["After some deliberation, the judges decided to proceed with the trial and to enter both the unexpected pieces of evidence (given by Ivan and Katerina Ivanovna) on the protocol.", "얼마간 심의한 끝에 판사들은 재판을 계속 진행하고, 예기치 못한 두 증언(이반과 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언)을 모두 조서에 올리기로 결정했다."]], [["But I will not detail the evidence of the other witnesses, who only repeated and confirmed what had been said before, though all with their characteristic peculiarities.", "그러나 다른 증인들의 증언은 일일이 적지 않겠으니, 그들은 저마다 특유의 면면을 지니기는 했어도 그저 앞서 나온 이야기를 되풀이하고 확인했을 뿐이다."], ["I repeat, all was brought together in the prosecutor’s speech, which I shall quote immediately.", "거듭 말하거니와, 모든 것은 검사의 논고에 한데 모였고, 그것을 나는 곧 인용하려 한다."], ["Every one was excited, every one was electrified by the late catastrophe, and all were awaiting the speeches for the prosecution and the defense with intense impatience.", "다들 흥분해 있었고, 다들 방금의 파국에 감전된 듯했으며, 모두가 검사 측 논고와 변호 측 변론을 격렬한 조바심으로 기다리고 있었다."], ["Fetyukovitch was obviously shaken by Katerina Ivanovna’s evidence.", "페튜코비치는 카테리나 이바노브나의 증언에 명백히 동요한 기색이었다."], ["But the prosecutor was triumphant.", "그러나 검사는 의기양양했다."], ["When all the evidence had been taken, the court was adjourned for almost an hour.", "모든 증언이 끝나자 법정은 거의 한 시간가량 휴정되었다."], ["I believe it was just eight o’clock when the President returned to his seat and our prosecutor, Ippolit Kirillovitch, began his speech.", "내 기억으로는 재판장이 자리로 돌아오고 우리 고장의 검사 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 논고를 시작한 것이 막 여덟 시였을 것이다."]], [["# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor’s Speech. Sketches Of Character", "# Chapter VI. The Prosecutor’s Speech. Sketches Of Character"]], [["Ippolit Kirillovitch began his speech, trembling with nervousness, with cold sweat on his forehead, feeling hot and cold all over by turns.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 신경의 떨림에 사로잡힌 채, 이마에 식은땀을 흘리며, 온몸이 번갈아 달아올랐다 식었다 하는 것을 느끼며 논고를 시작했다."], ["He described this himself afterwards.", "그는 훗날 이것을 스스로 그렇게 묘사했다."], ["He regarded this speech as his chef-d’œuvre, the chef-d’œuvre of his whole life, as his swan-song.", "그는 이 논고를 자신의 걸작, 평생을 통틀어 가장 뛰어난 걸작이자 자신의 백조의 노래로 여겼다."], ["He died, it is true, nine months later of rapid consumption, so that he had the right, as it turned out, to compare himself to a swan singing his last song.", "사실 그는 아홉 달 뒤 급성 폐결핵으로 세상을 떠났으니, 결국에 가서는 자신을 마지막 노래를 부르는 백조에 견줄 권리가 있었던 셈이다."], ["He had put his whole heart and all the brain he had into that speech.", "그는 그 논고에 자신의 온 마음과 가진 두뇌를 죄다 쏟아부었다."], ["And poor Ippolit Kirillovitch unexpectedly revealed that at least some feeling for the public welfare and “the eternal question” lay concealed in him.", "그리고 가엾은 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 적어도 공공의 안녕과 \"영원한 문제\"에 대한 어떤 감정이 자기 안에 감춰져 있었음을 뜻밖에도 드러냈다."], ["Where his speech really excelled was in its sincerity.", "그의 논고가 진정으로 빛을 발한 대목은 그 진실함이었다."], ["He genuinely believed in the prisoner’s guilt; he was accusing him not as an official duty only, and in calling for vengeance he quivered with a genuine passion “for the security of society.”", "그는 피고의 유죄를 진심으로 믿었으니, 단지 공무상의 의무로서 그를 단죄한 것이 아니었고, 복수를 부르짖을 때에도 \"사회의 안전을 위하여\"라는 진정한 열정으로 부르르 떨었다."], ["Even the ladies in the audience, though they remained hostile to Ippolit Kirillovitch, admitted that he made an extraordinary impression on them.", "청중석의 부인들조차, 비록 이폴리트 키릴로비치에게 적대적인 태도를 거두지 않았으나, 그가 자신들에게 비상한 인상을 남겼다는 것을 인정했다."], ["He began in a breaking voice, but it soon gained strength and filled the court to the end of his speech.", "그는 갈라지는 목소리로 시작했으나, 이내 그 목소리는 힘을 얻어 논고가 끝날 때까지 법정을 가득 채웠다."], ["But as soon as he had finished, he almost fainted.", "그러나 말을 마치자마자 그는 거의 실신할 지경이었다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury,”", "\"배심원 여러분,\""], ["began the prosecutor, “this case has made a stir throughout Russia.", "검사가 입을 열었다. \"이 사건은 온 러시아를 떠들썩하게 만들었습니다."], ["But what is there to wonder at, what is there so peculiarly horrifying in it for us?", "그러나 여기에 놀랄 것이 무엇이며, 우리에게 무엇이 그토록 유난히 끔찍하단 말입니까?"], ["We are so accustomed to such crimes!", "우리는 이런 범죄에 이미 너무도 익숙해져 있습니다!"], ["That’s what’s so horrible, that such dark deeds have ceased to horrify us.", "바로 그것이 가장 끔찍한 일이니, 이토록 어두운 행위들이 더 이상 우리를 전율케 하지 못한다는 것입니다."], ["What ought to horrify us is that we are so accustomed to it, and not this or that isolated crime.", "우리를 전율케 해야 할 것은 우리가 그것에 이토록 익숙해져 있다는 사실이지, 이런저런 개별 범죄가 아닙니다."], ["What are the causes of our indifference, our lukewarm attitude to such deeds, to such signs of the times, ominous of an unenviable future?", "이런 행위들, 부러워할 것 없는 미래를 예고하는 이런 시대의 징후들에 대한 우리의 무관심, 우리의 미온적인 태도의 원인은 무엇입니까?"], ["Is it our cynicism, is it the premature exhaustion of intellect and imagination in a society that is sinking into decay, in spite of its youth?", "그것은 우리의 냉소주의입니까, 아니면 젊음에도 불구하고 쇠락의 늪으로 가라앉고 있는 사회에서 지성과 상상력이 때 이르게 고갈된 탓입니까?"], ["Is it that our moral principles are shattered to their foundations, or is it, perhaps, a complete lack of such principles among us?", "우리의 도덕적 원칙이 그 토대까지 산산이 부서진 탓입니까, 아니면 어쩌면 우리에게 그런 원칙이 아예 완전히 결여된 탓입니까? 나는 이런 물음들에 답할 수 없습니다."], ["I cannot answer such questions; nevertheless they are disturbing, and every citizen not only must, but ought to be harassed by them.", "그럼에도 이 물음들은 마음을 어지럽히는 것이며, 모든 시민은 마땅히 이 물음들에 시달려야 할 뿐 아니라 시달리지 않을 수 없는 것입니다."], ["Our newborn and still timid press has done good service to the public already, for without it we should never have heard of the horrors of unbridled violence and moral degradation which are continually made known by the press, not merely to those who attend the new jury courts established in the present reign, but to every one.", "갓 태어나 아직 소심한 우리의 언론은 이미 대중에게 좋은 봉사를 해 왔으니, 그것이 없었던들 우리는 억제되지 않은 폭력과 도덕적 타락의 참상을 결코 알지 못했을 것입니다. 그런 일들은 언론을 통해 끊임없이 알려지는데, 단지 현 치세에 설립된 새로운 배심 재판정에 참석하는 이들에게만이 아니라 모든 사람에게 전해지는 것입니다."], ["And what do we read almost daily?", "그렇다면 우리는 거의 날마다 무엇을 읽고 있습니까?"], ["Of things beside which the present case grows pale, and seems almost commonplace.", "그것에 비하면 지금 이 사건이 빛이 바래고 거의 평범하게까지 보일 만한 일들입니다."], ["But what is most important is that the majority of our national crimes of violence bear witness to a widespread evil, now so general among us that it is difficult to contend against it.", "그러나 가장 중요한 것은, 우리나라에서 벌어지는 폭력 범죄의 대다수가 이제 우리 사이에 너무도 널리 퍼져 맞서 싸우기조차 어려운 어떤 만연한 악을 증언하고 있다는 점입니다."]], [["“One day we see a brilliant young officer of high society, at the very outset of his career, in a cowardly underhand way, without a pang of conscience, murdering an official who had once been his benefactor, and the servant girl, to steal his own I.O.U. and what ready money he could find on him; ‘it will come in handy for my pleasures in the fashionable world and for my career in the future.’ After murdering them, he puts pillows under the head of each of his victims; he goes away. Next, a young hero ‘decorated for bravery’ kills the mother of his chief and benefactor, like a highwayman, and to urge his companions to join him he asserts that ‘she loves him like a son, and so will follow all his directions and take no precautions.’ Granted that he is a monster, yet I dare not say in these days that he is unique. Another man will not commit the murder, but will feel and think like him, and is as dishonorable in soul. In silence, alone with his conscience, he asks himself perhaps, ‘What is honor, and isn’t the condemnation of bloodshed a prejudice?’", "\"어느 날 우리는 상류 사회의 한 빛나는 젊은 장교가, 그 출세 가도의 바로 첫걸음에서, 비겁하고 음험한 방식으로, 양심의 가책 한 점 없이, 한때 자신의 은인이었던 관리와 그 하녀를 살해하는 것을 봅니다. 그것도 자기가 써 준 차용증과 그에게서 찾아낼 수 있는 현금을 훔치기 위해서였으니, '상류 사교계에서의 내 쾌락과 앞으로의 출세에 요긴하게 쓰이겠지' 하는 것입니다. 그들을 죽인 뒤 그는 희생자들 각자의 머리 밑에 베개를 받쳐 놓고는 그곳을 떠납니다. 다음으로, '용맹으로 훈장을 받은' 한 젊은 영웅이 자기 상관이자 은인인 사람의 어머니를 노상강도처럼 죽이고는, 자기 패거리를 끌어들이려고 '그 노파는 자기를 아들처럼 사랑하니 자기 말이라면 무엇이든 따를 것이고 아무런 경계도 하지 않을 것'이라고 장담합니다. 그가 괴물이라는 것은 인정합시다. 그러나 요즘 세상에 나는 감히 그가 유일무이한 존재라고는 말하지 못하겠습니다. 또 다른 누군가는 살인을 저지르지는 않으나 그와 똑같이 느끼고 똑같이 생각하며, 그 영혼이 그만큼이나 명예를 모릅니다. 침묵 속에서, 홀로 제 양심과 마주한 채 그는 어쩌면 스스로에게 묻고 있을 것입니다. '명예란 대체 무엇이며, 피 흘림을 단죄하는 것은 한낱 편견이 아닌가?'"]], [["“Perhaps people will cry out against me that I am morbid, hysterical, that it is a monstrous slander, that I am exaggerating.", "\"어쩌면 사람들은 나를 향해, 내가 병적이고 신경질적이라느니, 이것은 터무니없는 중상이라느니, 내가 과장하고 있다느니 하며 소리칠지도 모릅니다. 그렇게 말하라지요—그리고 맙소사!"], ["Let them say so—and heavens!", "만약 정말 그렇다면 나야말로 가장 먼저 기뻐할 사람입니다!"], ["I should be the first to rejoice if it were so!", "오, 부디 나를 믿지 마시고 나를 병적인 자로 여기십시오."], ["Oh, don’t believe me, think of me as morbid, but remember my words; if only a tenth, if only a twentieth part of what I say is true—even so it’s awful!", "다만 내 말을 기억해 주십시오. 만약 내가 하는 말의 십분의 일, 아니 이십분의 일만이라도 진실이라면—그렇다 해도 끔찍한 일입니다!"], ["Look how our young people commit suicide, without asking themselves Hamlet’s question what there is beyond, without a sign of such a question, as though all that relates to the soul and to what awaits us beyond the grave had long been erased in their minds and buried under the sands.", "보십시오, 우리 젊은이들이 어떻게 자살하는지를. 그 너머에 무엇이 있는지 묻는 햄릿의 물음조차 스스로에게 던지지 않은 채, 그런 물음의 기색조차 없이, 마치 영혼에 관한 모든 것, 무덤 너머에서 우리를 기다리는 모든 것이 그들의 마음속에서 오래전에 지워져 모래 밑에 묻혀 버리기라도 한 듯이 말입니다."], ["Look at our vice, at our profligates.", "우리의 악덕을 보십시오, 우리의 방탕한 자들을 보십시오."], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch, the luckless victim in the present case, was almost an innocent babe compared with many of them.", "이 사건의 불운한 희생자인 표도르 파블로비치는 그들 가운데 많은 자들에 견주면 거의 순진무구한 갓난아기나 다름없었습니다."], ["And yet we all knew him, ‘he lived among us!’...", "그런데도 우리는 모두 그를 알았으니, '그는 우리 사이에서 살았습니다!'……"]], [["“Yes, one day perhaps the leading intellects of Russia and of Europe will study the psychology of Russian crime, for the subject is worth it.", "\"그렇습니다, 어쩌면 언젠가 러시아와 유럽의 으뜸가는 지성들이 러시아 범죄의 심리를 연구할 날이 올 것이니, 그 주제는 그럴 만한 값어치가 있습니다."], ["But this study will come later, at leisure, when all the tragic topsy-turvydom of to-day is farther behind us, so that it’s possible to examine it with more insight and more impartiality than I can do.", "그러나 이 연구는 훗날, 여유가 생겼을 때, 오늘날의 이 모든 비극적 혼돈이 우리에게서 더 멀어져서, 내가 할 수 있는 것보다 더 깊은 통찰과 더 큰 공정함으로 그것을 살펴볼 수 있을 때 이루어질 것입니다."], ["Now we are either horrified or pretend to be horrified, though we really gloat over the spectacle, and love strong and eccentric sensations which tickle our cynical, pampered idleness.", "지금 우리는 전율하거나, 아니면 전율하는 척하지만, 실은 그 광경을 보며 은근히 고소해하고, 우리의 냉소적이고 응석받이로 자란 나태를 간질이는 강렬하고 기괴한 자극을 좋아합니다."], ["Or, like little children, we brush the dreadful ghosts away and hide our heads in the pillow so as to return to our sports and merriment as soon as they have vanished.", "그렇지 않으면 어린아이들처럼 그 무서운 유령들을 쫓아 버리고 베개 속에 머리를 파묻었다가, 유령이 사라지기 무섭게 다시 놀이와 흥겨움으로 돌아갑니다."], ["But we must one day begin life in sober earnest, we must look at ourselves as a society; it’s time we tried to grasp something of our social position, or at least to make a beginning in that direction.", "그러나 우리는 언젠가 진지하고 엄숙하게 삶을 시작해야 하며, 우리 자신을 하나의 사회로서 바라보아야 합니다. 우리의 사회적 처지를 조금이라도 파악하려고, 적어도 그 방향으로 첫발을 떼려고 시도할 때가 된 것입니다."]], [["“A great writer[9] of the last epoch, comparing Russia to a swift troika galloping to an unknown goal, exclaims, ‘Oh, troika, birdlike troika, who invented thee!’ and adds, in proud ecstasy, that all the peoples of the world stand aside respectfully to make way for the recklessly galloping troika to pass.", "\"지난 시대의 한 위대한 작가는, 러시아를 알 수 없는 목적지를 향해 질주하는 빠른 트로이카에 견주며 이렇게 외쳤습니다. '오, 트로이카여, 새와도 같은 트로이카여, 누가 너를 만들었느냐!'"], ["That may be, they may stand aside, respectfully or no, but in my poor opinion the great writer ended his book in this way either in an access of childish and naïve optimism, or simply in fear of the censorship of the day.", "그리고 그는 자랑스러운 환희에 차서, 세상 만민이 경의를 표하며 비켜서서 그 무모하게 질주하는 트로이카가 지나가도록 길을 내준다고 덧붙입니다. 그럴지도 모릅니다, 그들은 경의를 표하든 안 표하든 비켜설지도 모릅니다."], ["For if the troika were drawn by his heroes, Sobakevitch, Nozdryov, Tchitchikov, it could reach no rational goal, whoever might be driving it.", "그러나 내 변변찮은 소견으로는, 그 위대한 작가가 자기 책을 이렇게 끝맺은 것은 유치하고 순진한 낙관주의의 발작 탓이거나, 아니면 그저 당대 검열에 대한 두려움 탓이었습니다. 왜냐하면 만약 그 트로이카를 그의 주인공들, 곧 소바케비치, 노즈드료프, 치치코프가 끌고 있다면, 누가 모는들 그것은 어떤 합리적인 목적지에도 다다를 수 없을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["And those were the heroes of an older generation, ours are worse specimens still....”", "그런데 그들은 앞선 세대의 주인공들이었고, 우리 세대의 견본들은 그보다도 더 못한 것들입니다……\""]], [["At this point Ippolit Kirillovitch’s speech was interrupted by applause.", "이 대목에서 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 논고는 박수갈채에 가로막혔다."], ["The liberal significance of this simile was appreciated.", "이 비유가 담은 자유주의적 의미가 호응을 얻은 것이다."], ["The applause was, it’s true, of brief duration, so that the President did not think it necessary to caution the public, and only looked severely in the direction of the offenders.", "사실 박수는 잠깐 사이에 그쳤으므로, 재판장은 굳이 청중에게 주의를 줄 필요를 느끼지 못하고 그저 박수를 친 자들 쪽을 엄하게 노려보았을 뿐이다."], ["But Ippolit Kirillovitch was encouraged; he had never been applauded before!", "그러나 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 고무되었으니, 그는 전에는 한 번도 박수를 받아 본 적이 없었던 것이다!"], ["He had been all his life unable to get a hearing, and now he suddenly had an opportunity of securing the ear of all Russia.", "그는 평생토록 자기 말에 귀를 기울이게 하지 못했는데, 이제 갑자기 온 러시아의 귀를 사로잡을 기회를 얻은 셈이었다."]], [["“What, after all, is this Karamazov family, which has gained such an unenviable notoriety throughout Russia?”", "\"대체 이 카라마조프 집안이란 무엇이기에, 온 러시아에 걸쳐 이토록 부러울 것 없는 악명을 떨치게 되었을까요?\""], ["he continued.", "그는 말을 이었다."], ["“Perhaps I am exaggerating, but it seems to me that certain fundamental features of the educated class of to-day are reflected in this family picture—only, of course, in miniature, ‘like the sun in a drop of water.’ Think of that unhappy, vicious, unbridled old man, who has met with such a melancholy end, the head of a family!", "\"어쩌면 내가 과장하는 것인지도 모르나, 내가 보기에는 오늘날 교양 계급의 어떤 근본적인 특징들이 이 가족의 초상에 비쳐 있는 듯합니다—물론 축소된 모습으로, '물방울에 비친 태양처럼'"], ["Beginning life of noble birth, but in a poor dependent position, through an unexpected marriage he came into a small fortune.", "말입니다. 그 불행하고 사악하며 절제를 모르던 노인을, 그토록 음울한 최후를 맞은 그 한 가정의 가장을 떠올려 보십시오!"], ["A petty knave, a toady and buffoon, of fairly good, though undeveloped, intelligence, he was, above all, a moneylender, who grew bolder with growing prosperity.", "귀족으로 태어났으나 가난하여 남에게 빌붙는 처지로 삶을 시작한 그는, 뜻밖의 결혼을 통해 약간의 재산을 손에 넣었습니다. 좀스러운 악당이요 아첨꾼이며 어릿광대였던 그는, 제법 괜찮으나 갈고닦이지 않은 지능을 지닌 자였으되, 무엇보다도 고리대금업자였고, 살림이 늘어 갈수록 점점 더 대담해졌습니다."], ["His abject and servile characteristics disappeared, his malicious and sarcastic cynicism was all that remained.", "그의 비굴하고 굽실거리던 특성은 사라지고, 악의에 차고 빈정대는 냉소주의만이 남았습니다."], ["On the spiritual side he was undeveloped, while his vitality was excessive.", "영적인 면에서 그는 미숙했던 반면, 그 생명력은 지나치게 넘쳤습니다."], ["He saw nothing in life but sensual pleasure, and he brought his children up to be the same.", "그는 삶에서 관능적 쾌락 외에는 아무것도 보지 못했고, 제 자식들도 똑같이 그렇게 되도록 길렀습니다."], ["He had no feelings for his duties as a father.", "그는 아버지로서의 의무에 대해 아무런 감정도 없었습니다."], ["He ridiculed those duties.", "그는 그 의무를 비웃었습니다."], ["He left his little children to the servants, and was glad to be rid of them, forgot about them completely.", "그는 어린 자식들을 하인들에게 내맡기고는, 그들을 떼어 낸 것을 다행으로 여기며 그들을 까맣게 잊어버렸습니다."], ["The old man’s maxim was Après moi le déluge.", "그 노인의 좌우명은 '내가 죽은 뒤에는 홍수가 나든 말든'이었습니다."], ["He was an example of everything that is opposed to civic duty, of the most complete and malignant individualism.", "그는 시민적 의무에 대립하는 모든 것의, 가장 철저하고 악의에 찬 개인주의의 본보기였습니다."], ["‘The world may burn for aught I care, so long as I am all right,’ and he was all right; he was content, he was eager to go on living in the same way for another twenty or thirty years.", "'나만 무사하다면 세상이야 불타든 말든 내 알 바 아니다'—그리고 그는 무사했고, 만족했으며, 앞으로도 이삼십 년쯤 똑같은 방식으로 계속 살아가기를 열망했습니다."], ["He swindled his own son and spent his money, his maternal inheritance, on trying to get his mistress from him.", "그는 제 친아들을 등쳐 먹고, 그 어머니가 남긴 유산인 그의 돈을 그에게서 정부를 빼앗으려는 데 써 버렸습니다."], ["No, I don’t intend to leave the prisoner’s defense altogether to my talented colleague from Petersburg.", "아닙니다, 나는 피고의 변론을 페테르부르크에서 오신 재능 있는 동료에게 죄다 떠넘길 생각은 없습니다."], ["I will speak the truth myself, I can well understand what resentment he had heaped up in his son’s heart against him.", "진실은 나 자신이 말하겠습니다. 나는 아버지가 아들의 마음속에 얼마나 큰 원한을 쌓아 올렸는지 충분히 이해할 수 있습니다."]], [["“But enough, enough of that unhappy old man; he has paid the penalty.", "\"그러나 그 불행한 노인에 관해서는 이쯤 해 둡시다, 이쯤이면 충분합니다. 그는 그 대가를 치렀습니다."], ["Let us remember, however, that he was a father, and one of the typical fathers of to-day.", "그러나 한 가지만은 기억해 둡시다. 그는 한 사람의 아버지였으며, 오늘날의 전형적인 아버지들 가운데 하나였다는 것을."], ["Am I unjust, indeed, in saying that he is typical of many modern fathers?", "그가 오늘날 수많은 아버지의 전형이라고 말하는 것이 과연 부당한 일입니까?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["many of them only differ in not openly professing such cynicism, for they are better educated, more cultured, but their philosophy is essentially the same as his.", "그들 가운데 많은 자들은 다만 그런 냉소주의를 드러내 놓고 떠벌리지 않는다는 점에서만 다를 뿐이니, 그들이 더 잘 교육받고 더 교양 있는 까닭입니다. 그러나 그들의 철학은 본질적으로 그의 것과 똑같습니다."], ["Perhaps I am a pessimist, but you have agreed to forgive me.", "어쩌면 나는 비관주의자인지도 모릅니다. 그러나 여러분은 나를 용서하기로 합의해 주셨습니다."], ["Let us agree beforehand, you need not believe me, but let me speak.", "미리 이렇게 약속해 둡시다, 여러분은 나를 믿지 않으셔도 좋으니, 다만 내가 말하도록 내버려 두십시오."], ["Let me say what I have to say, and remember something of my words.", "내가 해야 할 말을 하게 해 주시고, 내 말 가운데 무언가를 기억해 주십시오."]], [["“Now for the children of this father, this head of a family.", "\"이제 이 아버지의, 이 한 가정 가장의 자식들로 넘어갑시다."], ["One of them is the prisoner before us, all the rest of my speech will deal with him.", "그중 하나가 우리 앞에 선 피고이며, 내 논고의 나머지 전부는 그를 다룰 것입니다."], ["Of the other two I will speak only cursorily.", "나머지 두 사람에 관해서는 그저 간략하게만 말하겠습니다."]], [["“The elder is one of those modern young men of brilliant education and vigorous intellect, who has lost all faith in everything.", "\"맏이는 빛나는 교양과 왕성한 지력을 갖춘, 그러면서도 모든 것에 대한 일체의 신앙을 잃어버린 오늘날의 젊은이들 가운데 하나입니다. 그는 제 아버지처럼 이미 많은 것을 부정하고 배척했습니다."], ["He has denied and rejected much already, like his father.", "우리는 모두 그의 말을 들은 적이 있으며, 그는 이 고장 사교계에서 환영받는 손님이었습니다."], ["We have all heard him, he was a welcome guest in local society.", "그는 결코 자기 견해를 감추지 않았으니, 사실은 정반대였습니다."], ["He never concealed his opinions, quite the contrary in fact, which justifies me in speaking rather openly of him now, of course, not as an individual, but as a member of the Karamazov family.", "바로 그 때문에 나는 지금 그에 관해 다소 거리낌 없이 이야기해도 무방하다고 봅니다. 물론 한 개인으로서가 아니라 카라마조프 집안의 한 사람으로서 말입니다."], ["Another personage closely connected with the case died here by his own hand last night.", "이 사건과 밀접하게 얽힌 또 다른 인물이 어젯밤 이곳에서 제 손으로 목숨을 끊었습니다."], ["I mean an afflicted idiot, formerly the servant, and possibly the illegitimate son, of Fyodor Pavlovitch, Smerdyakov.", "나는 표도르 파블로비치의 하인이었으며 어쩌면 그 사생아였을지도 모르는, 병든 백치 스메르댜코프를 가리키는 것입니다."], ["At the preliminary inquiry, he told me with hysterical tears how the young Ivan Karamazov had horrified him by his spiritual audacity.", "예심에서 그는 신경질적인 눈물을 흘리며, 젊은 이반 카라마조프가 자신의 영적 대담함으로 자기를 얼마나 전율케 했는지를 내게 이야기했습니다. '그분에 따르면 세상의 모든 것이 허용되며, 앞으로는 그 무엇도 금지되어서는 안 된다는 겁니다—그분이 늘 저에게 가르치신 게 그것입니다.'"], ["‘Everything in the world is lawful according to him, and nothing must be forbidden in the future—that is what he always taught me.’ I believe that idiot was driven out of his mind by this theory, though, of course, the epileptic attacks from which he suffered, and this terrible catastrophe, have helped to unhinge his faculties.", "나는 그 백치가 이 이론 때문에 정신이 나가 버린 것이라고 믿습니다. 물론 그가 앓던 간질 발작과 이 끔찍한 참사가 그의 정신을 흐트러뜨리는 데 한몫했겠지만 말입니다."], ["But he dropped one very interesting observation, which would have done credit to a more intelligent observer, and that is, indeed, why I’ve mentioned it: ‘If there is one of the sons that is like Fyodor Pavlovitch in character, it is Ivan Fyodorovitch.’", "그러나 그는 한 가지 매우 흥미로운 견해를 흘렸으니, 그것은 더 영민한 관찰자가 했더라면 그에게 명예가 되었을 만한 것이었고, 사실 내가 그것을 언급하는 까닭도 거기에 있습니다. '아들들 가운데 표도르 파블로비치를 성격으로 빼닮은 사람이 있다면, 그건 이반 표도로비치입니다.'"]], [["“With that remark I conclude my sketch of his character, feeling it indelicate to continue further.", "\"이 한마디로 나는 그의 성격에 관한 묘사를 끝맺겠습니다. 더 깊이 들어가는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이라 여기는 까닭입니다."], ["Oh, I don’t want to draw any further conclusions and croak like a raven over the young man’s future.", "오, 나는 더 이상 어떤 결론도 끌어내어 까마귀처럼 그 젊은이의 미래를 두고 불길하게 까악거리고 싶지 않습니다."], ["We’ve seen to-day in this court that there are still good impulses in his young heart, that family feeling has not been destroyed in him by lack of faith and cynicism, which have come to him rather by inheritance than by the exercise of independent thought.", "우리는 오늘 이 법정에서, 그의 젊은 가슴속에 아직 선한 충동이 남아 있다는 것을, 신앙의 결핍과 냉소주의가—그것도 독립적인 사고의 행사를 통해서라기보다는 차라리 유전을 통해 그에게 물려진 것입니다만—그 안의 가족애를 파괴하지는 못했다는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["“Then the third son.", "\"다음으로 셋째 아들입니다."], ["Oh, he is a devout and modest youth, who does not share his elder brother’s gloomy and destructive theory of life.", "오, 그는 경건하고 겸손한 청년으로서, 형의 음울하고 파괴적인 인생관을 공유하지 않습니다."], ["He has sought to cling to the ‘ideas of the people,’ or to what goes by that name in some circles of our intellectual classes.", "그는 '민중의 이상'에, 혹은 우리 지식 계급의 어떤 무리들 사이에서 그렇게 일컫는 것에 매달리고자 애써 왔습니다."], ["He clung to the monastery, and was within an ace of becoming a monk.", "그는 수도원에 매달렸고, 하마터면 수도사가 될 뻔했습니다."], ["He seems to me to have betrayed unconsciously, and so early, that timid despair which leads so many in our unhappy society, who dread cynicism and its corrupting influences, and mistakenly attribute all the mischief to European enlightenment, to return to their ‘native soil,’ as they say, to the bosom, so to speak, of their mother earth, like frightened children, yearning to fall asleep on the withered bosom of their decrepit mother, and to sleep there for ever, only to escape the horrors that terrify them.", "내가 보기에 그는, 그토록 이른 나이에 무의식중에 어떤 소심한 절망을 드러내 보인 듯합니다. 그 절망이란, 냉소주의와 그 타락시키는 영향을 두려워하고 모든 해악을 유럽의 계몽 탓으로 잘못 돌리며, 그들이 말하듯 자신의 '본래의 토양'으로, 말하자면 어머니 대지의 품으로 돌아가도록 우리의 불행한 사회에서 그토록 많은 이들을 이끄는 것이니, 마치 겁에 질린 아이들이 늙고 쇠약한 어머니의 메마른 가슴 위에서 잠들기를, 자신들을 떨게 하는 공포에서 벗어나고자 그곳에서 영원히 잠들기를 갈망하는 것과도 같습니다."]], [["“For my part I wish the excellent and gifted young man every success; I trust that his youthful idealism and impulse towards the ideas of the people may never degenerate, as often happens, on the moral side into gloomy mysticism, and on the political into blind chauvinism—two elements which are even a greater menace to Russia than the premature decay, due to misunderstanding and gratuitous adoption of European ideas, from which his elder brother is suffering.”", "\"나 자신으로 말하자면, 이 훌륭하고 재능 있는 청년이 온갖 성공을 거두기를 바랍니다. 나는 그의 젊은 이상주의와 민중의 이상을 향한 충동이, 흔히 그러하듯, 도덕적인 측면에서는 음울한 신비주의로, 정치적인 측면에서는 맹목적인 배외주의로 변질되지 않기를 바랍니다. 이 두 요소야말로, 그의 형이 앓고 있는—유럽 사상의 몰이해와 무분별한 수용에서 비롯된—때 이른 쇠락보다도 러시아에 한층 더 큰 위협이 되는 것입니다.\""]], [["Two or three people clapped their hands at the mention of chauvinism and mysticism.", "배외주의와 신비주의가 언급되자 두세 사람이 박수를 쳤다."], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch had been, indeed, carried away by his own eloquence.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 사실 자기 자신의 웅변에 도취되어 있었던 것이다."], ["All this had little to do with the case in hand, to say nothing of the fact of its being somewhat vague, but the sickly and consumptive man was overcome by the desire to express himself once in his life.", "이 모든 이야기는 다소 모호하다는 점은 차치하더라도 지금 다루는 사건과는 별 관련이 없었으나, 병들고 폐결핵에 걸린 이 사내는 평생에 단 한 번이라도 자기를 표현하고 싶다는 욕망에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다."], ["People said afterwards that he was actuated by unworthy motives in his criticism of Ivan, because the latter had on one or two occasions got the better of him in argument, and Ippolit Kirillovitch, remembering it, tried now to take his revenge.", "사람들은 훗날 그가 이반을 비판한 데에는 떳떳지 못한 동기가 작용했다고 수군거렸으니, 이반이 한두 차례 논쟁에서 그를 눌렀던 일이 있었던 까닭에, 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 그것을 기억하고 이제 앙갚음을 하려 든 것이라는 말이었다."], ["But I don’t know whether it was true.", "그러나 그것이 사실이었는지는 나도 모르겠다."], ["All this was only introductory, however, and the speech passed to more direct consideration of the case.", "어쨌든 이 모든 것은 서론에 불과했으며, 논고는 이제 사건에 대한 좀 더 직접적인 고찰로 넘어갔다."]], [["“But to return to the eldest son,”", "\"그러나 맏아들 이야기로 돌아갑시다.\""], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch went on.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치가 말을 이었다."], ["“He is the prisoner before us.", "\"그가 바로 우리 앞에 선 피고입니다."], ["We have his life and his actions, too, before us; the fatal day has come and all has been brought to the surface.", "우리 앞에는 그의 삶과 그의 행적 또한 놓여 있습니다. 그 숙명의 날이 닥쳐왔고 모든 것이 수면 위로 떠올랐습니다."], ["While his brothers seem to stand for ‘Europeanism’ and ‘the principles of the people,’ he seems to represent Russia as she is.", "그의 형제들이 '유럽주의'와 '민중의 원리'를 대변하는 듯하다면, 그는 있는 그대로의 러시아를 대표하는 듯합니다. 오, 러시아 전체는 아닙니다, 전체는 아닙니다!"], ["Oh, not all Russia, not all! God preserve us, if it were!", "만약 그렇다면 하느님이 우리를 지켜 주시기를!"], ["Yet, here we have her, our mother Russia, the very scent and sound of her.", "그러나 여기 우리에게는 그녀가, 우리 어머니 러시아가, 그녀의 바로 그 냄새와 그 소리가 있습니다."], ["Oh, he is spontaneous, he is a marvelous mingling of good and evil, he is a lover of culture and Schiller, yet he brawls in taverns and plucks out the beards of his boon companions.", "오, 그는 천성이 거침없고, 선과 악이 놀랍도록 뒤섞인 존재이며, 교양과 실러를 사랑하는 자이면서도 술집에서 난동을 부리고 술친구들의 수염을 쥐어뜯습니다."], ["Oh, he, too, can be good and noble, but only when all goes well with him.", "오, 그 역시 선하고 고결할 수 있으나, 다만 만사가 그에게 순조롭게 풀릴 때에만 그렇습니다."], ["What is more, he can be carried off his feet, positively carried off his feet by noble ideals, but only if they come of themselves, if they fall from heaven for him, if they need not be paid for.", "게다가 그는 고결한 이상에 휩쓸릴 수도, 그야말로 발이 땅에서 떨어질 만큼 휩쓸릴 수도 있으나, 다만 그 이상이 제 발로 찾아올 때에만, 그를 위해 하늘에서 떨어질 때에만, 대가를 치를 필요가 없을 때에만 그렇습니다."], ["He dislikes paying for anything, but is very fond of receiving, and that’s so with him in everything.", "그는 무엇에든 대가를 치르기를 싫어하나 받는 것은 무척 좋아하니, 매사가 그에게는 그러합니다."], ["Oh, give him every possible good in life (he couldn’t be content with less), and put no obstacle in his way, and he will show that he, too, can be noble.", "오, 그에게 삶에서 가능한 온갖 좋은 것을 주고(그는 그보다 못한 것으로는 만족하지 못할 것입니다), 그의 앞길에 아무런 장애도 두지 마십시오. 그러면 그도 고결할 수 있음을 보여 줄 것입니다."], ["He is not greedy, no, but he must have money, a great deal of money, and you will see how generously, with what scorn of filthy lucre, he will fling it all away in the reckless dissipation of one night.", "그는 탐욕스럽지는 않습니다, 그렇습니다. 그러나 그에게는 돈이, 그것도 아주 많은 돈이 있어야만 하며, 그러면 여러분은 그가 얼마나 너그럽게, 더러운 돈을 얼마나 경멸하며, 하룻밤의 무모한 방탕 속에 그 모든 것을 죄다 내던져 버리는지 보게 될 것입니다."], ["But if he has not money, he will show what he is ready to do to get it when he is in great need of it.", "그러나 만약 그에게 돈이 없다면, 그것이 절실히 필요한 처지에 몰렸을 때 그것을 손에 넣으려고 그가 무슨 짓까지 할 각오가 되어 있는지를 보여 줄 것입니다."], ["But all this later, let us take events in their chronological order.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 뒤로 미루고, 사건들을 시간 순서대로 짚어 봅시다."]], [["“First, we have before us a poor abandoned child, running about the back-yard ‘without boots on his feet,’ as our worthy and esteemed fellow citizen, of foreign origin, alas!", "\"먼저, 우리 앞에는 가엾게 버려진 한 아이가, 방금 우리의 존경스럽고 훌륭한—아아, 외국 태생의!—동료 시민이 표현한 대로 '발에 신발도 신지 못한 채' 뒷마당을 뛰어다니던 한 아이가 있습니다."], ["expressed it just now. I repeat it again, I yield to no one the defense of the criminal.", "다시 한 번 말하거니와, 나는 이 범죄자의 변론을 누구에게도 양보하지 않겠습니다."], ["I am here to accuse him, but to defend him also.", "나는 그를 고발하러 이 자리에 섰으나, 또한 그를 변호하러 선 것이기도 합니다."], ["Yes, I, too, am human; I, too, can weigh the influence of home and childhood on the character.", "그렇습니다, 나 역시 인간입니다. 나 역시 가정과 어린 시절이 성격에 미치는 영향을 가늠할 줄 압니다."], ["But the boy grows up and becomes an officer; for a duel and other reckless conduct he is exiled to one of the remote frontier towns of Russia.", "그러나 그 소년은 자라나 장교가 됩니다. 결투와 그 밖의 무모한 행실 때문에 그는 러시아의 외딴 변경 마을 가운데 한 곳으로 유배됩니다."], ["There he led a wild life as an officer.", "그곳에서 그는 장교로서 거친 삶을 살았습니다."], ["And, of course, he needed money, money before all things, and so after prolonged disputes he came to a settlement with his father, and the last six thousand was sent him.", "그리고 물론 그에게는 돈이, 무엇보다도 돈이 필요했으니, 그리하여 오랜 분쟁 끝에 그는 아버지와 합의에 이르렀고, 마지막 육천 루블을 그에게 부쳐 주게 되었습니다."], ["A letter is in existence in which he practically gives up his claim to the rest and settles his conflict with his father over the inheritance on the payment of this six thousand.", "그가 사실상 나머지에 대한 청구권을 포기하고 이 육천 루블의 지급으로 유산을 둘러싼 아버지와의 갈등을 마무리한다는 내용의 편지가 한 통 남아 있습니다."]], [["“Then came his meeting with a young girl of lofty character and brilliant education.", "\"그다음에 그는 고결한 성품과 빛나는 교양을 갖춘 한 젊은 처녀와 만나게 됩니다."], ["Oh, I do not venture to repeat the details; you have only just heard them.", "오, 나는 감히 그 상세한 내막을 되풀이하지 않겠습니다. 여러분은 방금 그것을 들으셨습니다."], ["Honor, self-sacrifice were shown there, and I will be silent.", "거기에는 명예와 자기희생이 드러나 있었으니, 나는 입을 다물겠습니다."], ["The figure of the young officer, frivolous and profligate, doing homage to true nobility and a lofty ideal, was shown in a very sympathetic light before us.", "경박하고 방탕한 젊은 장교가 진정한 고결함과 드높은 이상에 경의를 표하던 그 모습은, 우리 앞에 매우 호감 가는 빛으로 비쳤습니다."], ["But the other side of the medal was unexpectedly turned to us immediately after in this very court.", "그러나 메달의 다른 면이 그 직후 바로 이 법정에서 뜻밖에도 우리에게 돌려졌습니다."], ["Again I will not venture to conjecture why it happened so, but there were causes.", "다시 한 번 나는 왜 일이 그렇게 되었는지를 감히 추측하지 않겠으나, 거기에는 원인이 있었습니다."], ["The same lady, bathed in tears of long-concealed indignation, alleged that he, he of all men, had despised her for her action, which, though incautious, reckless perhaps, was still dictated by lofty and generous motives.", "그 똑같은 여인이, 오래도록 감춰 온 분노의 눈물에 젖은 채, 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 그가, 비록 경솔하고 어쩌면 무모했을지언정 그래도 드높고 너그러운 동기에서 비롯된 자기 행동을 두고 자기를 멸시했다고 주장했습니다."], ["He, he, the girl’s betrothed, looked at her with that smile of mockery, which was more insufferable from him than from any one.", "그가, 바로 그가, 그 처녀의 약혼자였던 그가, 다른 누구의 것보다도 더 견디기 힘든 그 조롱의 미소를 띠고 그녀를 바라보았다는 것입니다."], ["And knowing that he had already deceived her (he had deceived her, believing that she was bound to endure everything from him, even treachery), she intentionally offered him three thousand roubles, and clearly, too clearly, let him understand that she was offering him money to deceive her.", "그리고 그가 이미 자기를 속였다는 것을 알면서도(그는 그녀를 속였으니, 그녀가 자기에게서라면 배신마저도 무엇이든 견뎌 낼 수밖에 없으리라 믿었던 것입니다), 그녀는 일부러 그에게 삼천 루블을 내밀었고, 그것이 자기를 속이라고 주는 돈임을 그가 알아차리도록 분명하게, 너무도 분명하게 내비쳤습니다."], ["‘Well, will you take it or not, are you so lost to shame?’ was the dumb question in her scrutinizing eyes.", "'자, 받겠어요, 안 받겠어요, 그토록 수치를 모르는 사람인가요?'—이것이 그녀의 살피는 눈에 담긴 무언의 물음이었습니다."], ["He looked at her, saw clearly what was in her mind (he’s admitted here before you that he understood it all), appropriated that three thousand unconditionally, and squandered it in two days with the new object of his affections.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 있는지를 분명히 알아차렸으며(그는 여기 여러분 앞에서 그 모든 것을 이해했다고 인정했습니다), 그 삼천 루블을 아무 조건 없이 제 것으로 만들고는, 이틀 만에 새로운 애정의 대상과 함께 그것을 흥청망청 써 버렸습니다."]], [["“What are we to believe then?", "\"그렇다면 우리는 무엇을 믿어야 합니까?"], ["The first legend of the young officer sacrificing his last farthing in a noble impulse of generosity and doing reverence to virtue, or this other revolting picture?", "마지막 한 푼까지 너그러운 고결함의 충동에서 내놓고 미덕에 경의를 표하던 젊은 장교에 관한 첫 번째 전설입니까, 아니면 이 또 다른 역겨운 그림입니까?"], ["As a rule, between two extremes one has to find the mean, but in the present case this is not true.", "흔히 두 극단 사이에서는 그 중간을 찾아야 마땅하나, 지금 이 경우에는 그것이 맞지 않습니다."], ["The probability is that in the first case he was genuinely noble, and in the second as genuinely base.", "십중팔구 첫 번째 경우에 그는 진정으로 고결했고, 두 번째 경우에는 그만큼이나 진정으로 비열했습니다. 그것은 왜입니까?"], ["And why? Because he was of the broad Karamazov character—that’s just what I am leading up to—capable of combining the most incongruous contradictions, and capable of the greatest heights and of the greatest depths.", "그가 폭넓은 카라마조프적 성격을 지녔기 때문이니—바로 이것이 내가 이끌어 가려는 바입니다—가장 어울리지 않는 모순들을 한데 결합할 수 있고, 가장 높은 정점에도 가장 깊은 나락에도 다다를 수 있는 성격이기 때문입니다."], ["Remember the brilliant remark made by a young observer who has seen the Karamazov family at close quarters—Mr.", "카라마조프 집안을 가까이에서 지켜본 한 젊은 관찰자, 라키틴 씨가 했던 빛나는 한마디를 기억하십시오."], ["Rakitin: ‘The sense of their own degradation is as essential to those reckless, unbridled natures as the sense of their lofty generosity.’ And that’s true, they need continually this unnatural mixture.", "'그 무모하고 절제를 모르는 천성에게는 제 자신의 비천함에 대한 자각이, 제 드높은 너그러움에 대한 자각만큼이나 본질적으로 필요한 것이다.' 그리고 그것은 사실이니, 그들에게는 이 부자연스러운 혼합이 끊임없이 필요합니다."], ["Two extremes at the same moment, or they are miserable and dissatisfied and their existence is incomplete.", "두 극단을 같은 순간에 누리지 못하면 그들은 비참해하고 만족하지 못하며 그들의 존재는 미완으로 남습니다."], ["They are wide, wide as mother Russia; they include everything and put up with everything.", "그들은 넓디넓으니, 어머니 러시아만큼이나 넓습니다. 그들은 모든 것을 품고 모든 것을 견뎌 냅니다."]], [["“By the way, gentlemen of the jury, we’ve just touched upon that three thousand roubles, and I will venture to anticipate things a little.", "\"그건 그렇고, 배심원 여러분, 우리는 방금 그 삼천 루블 이야기를 잠깐 건드렸으니, 나는 감히 이야기를 조금 앞당겨 보고자 합니다."], ["Can you conceive that a man like that, on receiving that sum and in such a way, at the price of such shame, such disgrace, such utter degradation, could have been capable that very day of setting apart half that sum, that very day, and sewing it up in a little bag, and would have had the firmness of character to carry it about with him for a whole month afterwards, in spite of every temptation and his extreme need of it!", "여러분은 그런 사내가, 그런 식으로, 그토록 큰 수치와 그토록 큰 치욕과 그토록 철저한 비천함을 대가로 그 돈을 받아 든 자가, 바로 그날 그 절반을 따로 떼어 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣고, 그 뒤로 온갖 유혹과 그것에 대한 극심한 궁핍에도 불구하고 꼬박 한 달 동안 그것을 몸에 지니고 다닐 만한 성격의 굳셈을 지녔으리라고 상상하실 수 있겠습니까!"], ["Neither in drunken debauchery in taverns, nor when he was flying into the country, trying to get from God knows whom, the money so essential to him to remove the object of his affections from being tempted by his father, did he bring himself to touch that little bag!", "술집에서 곤드레만드레 흥청거릴 때에도, 혹은 자기 애정의 대상이 제 아버지의 유혹에 넘어가지 못하도록 그녀를 빼돌리는 데 필요한 그 절실한 돈을 누구에게서인지도 모를 사람에게서 구하려고 시골로 내달릴 때에도, 그는 차마 그 작은 주머니에 손을 대지 못했습니다!"], ["Why, if only to avoid abandoning his mistress to the rival of whom he was so jealous, he would have been certain to have opened that bag and to have stayed at home to keep watch over her, and to await the moment when she would say to him at last ‘I am yours,’ and to fly with her far from their fatal surroundings.", "아니, 그토록 질투하던 연적에게 제 정부를 내주지 않기 위해서라도, 그는 분명히 그 주머니를 열고 집에 머물며 그녀를 지키고, 마침내 그녀가 그에게 '나는 당신의 것이에요'라고 말해 줄 순간을 기다렸다가 그 숙명적인 환경에서 멀리 그녀와 함께 달아났을 것이 틀림없습니다."]], [["“But no, he did not touch his talisman, and what is the reason he gives for it?", "\"그러나 아닙니다, 그는 자기 부적에 손을 대지 않았습니다. 그러면 그가 그 까닭으로 대는 이유는 무엇입니까?"], ["The chief reason, as I have just said, was that when she would say, ‘I am yours, take me where you will,’ he might have the wherewithal to take her.", "방금 말한 대로 가장 큰 이유는, 그녀가 '나는 당신의 것이에요, 어디로든 데려가 줘요'라고 말할 때 그녀를 데려갈 밑천을 갖고 있기 위해서였다는 것입니다. 그러나 피고 자신의 말에 따르면, 그 첫 번째 이유는 두 번째 이유에 비하면 별 무게가 없었습니다."], ["But that first reason, in the prisoner’s own words, was of little weight beside the second.", "이 돈을 몸에 지니고 있는 한 나는 악당이지 도둑은 아니다, 라고 그는 말했습니다. 왜냐하면 나는 언제든 모욕당한 약혼녀에게 찾아가, 내가 부정하게 가로챈 돈의 절반을 내려놓으며 언제든 그녀에게 이렇게 말할 수 있기 때문이라는 것입니다."], ["While I have that money on me, he said, I am a scoundrel, not a thief, for I can always go to my insulted betrothed, and, laying down half the sum I have fraudulently appropriated, I can always say to her, ‘You see, I’ve squandered half your money, and shown I am a weak and immoral man, and, if you like, a scoundrel’ (I use the prisoner’s own expressions), ‘but though I am a scoundrel, I am not a thief, for if I had been a thief, I shouldn’t have brought you back this half of the money, but should have taken it as I did the other half!’ A marvelous explanation!", "'보시오, 나는 당신 돈의 절반을 탕진했고, 내가 나약하고 부도덕한 인간이라는 것을, 원한다면 악당이라는 것을(나는 피고 자신의 표현을 그대로 쓰고 있습니다) 보여 주었소. 그러나 비록 내가 악당일지언정 도둑은 아니오."], ["This frantic, but weak man, who could not resist the temptation of accepting the three thousand roubles at the price of such disgrace, this very man suddenly develops the most stoical firmness, and carries about a thousand roubles without daring to touch it.", "만약 내가 도둑이었다면 당신에게 이 절반의 돈을 도로 가져오지 않고 나머지 절반처럼 가져 버렸을 테니까!' 참으로 놀라운 변명입니다!"], ["Does that fit in at all with the character we have analyzed?", "그런 치욕을 대가로 삼천 루블을 받아 드는 유혹을 이겨 내지 못했던 이 광적이면서도 나약한 사내가, 바로 그 사내가 갑자기 더없이 금욕적인 굳셈을 발휘하여, 감히 손도 대지 못하면서 천 루블을 몸에 지니고 다닌다는 것입니다. 그것이 과연 우리가 분석한 그 성격과 조금이라도 들어맞습니까?"], ["No, and I venture to tell you how the real Dmitri Karamazov would have behaved in such circumstances, if he really had brought himself to put away the money.", "아닙니다, 그래서 나는 감히 여러분께 말씀드리겠습니다. 진짜 드미트리 카라마조프라면, 만약 그가 정말로 그 돈을 따로 떼어 둘 마음을 먹었다 하더라도, 그런 상황에서 어떻게 행동했을지를 말입니다."]], [["“At the first temptation—for instance, to entertain the woman with whom he had already squandered half the money—he would have unpicked his little bag and have taken out some hundred roubles, for why should he have taken back precisely half the money, that is, fifteen hundred roubles?", "\"첫 번째 유혹이 닥치면—이를테면 그가 이미 절반의 돈을 함께 탕진했던 그 여자를 대접하려는 유혹 말입니다—그는 자기 작은 주머니의 실밥을 뜯어 백 루블쯤 꺼냈을 것입니다. 어째서 그가 하필 돈의 정확히 절반을, 곧 천오백 루블을 도로 가져가야 한단 말입니까?"], ["why not fourteen hundred?", "어째서 천사백 루블은 안 됩니까?"], ["He could just as well have said then that he was not a thief, because he brought back fourteen hundred roubles.", "그래도 그는 천사백 루블을 도로 가져왔으니 자기는 도둑이 아니라고 똑같이 말할 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["Then another time he would have unpicked it again and taken out another hundred, and then a third, and then a fourth, and before the end of the month he would have taken the last note but one, feeling that if he took back only a hundred it would answer the purpose, for a thief would have stolen it all.", "그러고는 또 한 번 주머니의 실밥을 뜯어 다시 백 루블을 꺼내고, 그다음에는 또 한 번, 그다음에는 또 한 번, 그리하여 한 달이 채 가기 전에 마지막에서 두 번째 지폐까지 꺼냈을 것이니, 백 루블만 도로 가져가도 목적에는 충분하리라 여겼을 것입니다. 도둑이라면 그 모두를 훔쳤을 테니까요."], ["And then he would have looked at this last note, and have said to himself, ‘It’s really not worth while to give back one hundred; let’s spend that, too!’ That’s how the real Dmitri Karamazov, as we know him, would have behaved.", "그러고 나서 그는 이 마지막 지폐를 바라보며 스스로에게 이렇게 말했을 것입니다. '백 루블 도로 돌려주는 건 정말 그럴 가치도 없지."], ["One cannot imagine anything more incongruous with the actual fact than this legend of the little bag.", "이것마저 써 버리자!' 우리가 아는 진짜 드미트리 카라마조프라면 바로 이렇게 행동했을 것입니다."], ["Nothing could be more inconceivable.", "그 작은 주머니에 관한 이 전설만큼 실제 사실과 어울리지 않는 것은 상상할 수 없습니다. 그보다 더 믿기 어려운 것은 없을 것입니다."], ["But we shall return to that later.”", "그러나 이에 관해서는 나중에 다시 다루도록 하겠습니다.\""]], [["After touching upon what had come out in the proceedings concerning the financial relations of father and son, and arguing again and again that it was utterly impossible, from the facts known, to determine which was in the wrong, Ippolit Kirillovitch passed to the evidence of the medical experts in reference to Mitya’s fixed idea about the three thousand owing him.", "아버지와 아들의 금전 관계에 관해 재판 과정에서 드러난 것들을 짚고, 알려진 사실들로는 어느 쪽이 잘못이었는지 도무지 판가름할 수 없다고 거듭거듭 주장한 끝에, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 미탸가 자신에게 마땅히 돌아와야 할 삼천 루블에 대해 품었던 고정관념과 관련한 의료 전문가들의 증언으로 넘어갔다."]], [["# Chapter VII. An Historical Survey", "# Chapter VII. An Historical Survey"]], [["“The medical experts have striven to convince us that the prisoner is out of his mind and, in fact, a maniac.", "\"의료 전문가들은 피고가 정신이 나갔으며, 사실상 광인이라고 우리를 설득하려 애써 왔습니다."], ["I maintain that he is in his right mind, and that if he had not been, he would have behaved more cleverly.", "저는 그가 제정신이라고 주장하며, 만약 제정신이 아니었다면 오히려 더 영리하게 처신했으리라고 봅니다."], ["As for his being a maniac, that I would agree with, but only in one point, that is, his fixed idea about the three thousand.", "그가 광인이라는 점에 대해서는, 다만 한 가지 점에서만 동의할 수 있으니, 곧 그 삼천 루블에 관한 고정관념이 그것입니다."], ["Yet I think one might find a much simpler cause than his tendency to insanity.", "그러나 저는 그의 정신착란 성향보다 훨씬 더 단순한 원인을 찾을 수 있으리라고 생각합니다."], ["For my part I agree thoroughly with the young doctor who maintained that the prisoner’s mental faculties have always been normal, and that he has only been irritable and exasperated.", "저로서는 피고의 정신 기능이 언제나 정상이었으며, 다만 신경이 곤두서고 격앙되어 있었을 뿐이라고 주장한 젊은 의사의 견해에 전적으로 동의하는 바입니다."], ["The object of the prisoner’s continual and violent anger was not the sum itself; there was a special motive at the bottom of it.", "피고가 끊임없이 격렬한 분노를 쏟아부은 대상은 그 돈 자체가 아니었으니, 그 밑바닥에는 특별한 동기가 깔려 있었습니다."], ["That motive is jealousy!”", "그 동기란 바로 질투입니다!\""]], [["Here Ippolit Kirillovitch described at length the prisoner’s fatal passion for Grushenka.", "여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 그루셴카를 향한 피고의 치명적 정념을 장황하게 묘사했다."], ["He began from the moment when the prisoner went to the “young person’s”", "그는 피고가 그 \"젊은 여인\"의 거처로 \"그녀를 때리러\" 갔던 순간부터 이야기를 시작했다."], ["lodgings “to beat her”—“I use his own expression,”", "\"이것은 피고 자신의 표현을 그대로 쓰는 것입니다.\""], ["the prosecutor explained—“but instead of beating her, he remained there, at her feet.", "하고 검사가 설명했다. \"그러나 그는 그녀를 때리기는커녕 그곳에 머물러, 그녀의 발치에 주저앉고 말았습니다."], ["That was the beginning of the passion.", "이것이 그 정념의 시작이었습니다."], ["At the same time the prisoner’s father was captivated by the same young person—a strange and fatal coincidence, for they both lost their hearts to her simultaneously, though both had known her before.", "같은 시기에 피고의 아버지 또한 같은 젊은 여인에게 사로잡혔으니, 참으로 기이하고도 숙명적인 우연이었습니다. 두 사람은 모두 그녀를 전부터 알고 있었으면서도 동시에 그녀에게 마음을 빼앗기고 말았던 것입니다."], ["And she inspired in both of them the most violent, characteristically Karamazov passion.", "그리고 그녀는 두 사람 모두에게서 가장 격렬한, 카라마조프 집안 특유의 정념을 불러일으켰습니다. 우리는 그녀 자신의 고백을 가지고 있습니다."], ["We have her own confession: ‘I was laughing at both of them.’ Yes, the sudden desire to make a jest of them came over her, and she conquered both of them at once.", "'저는 두 사람을 모두 놀리고 있었어요.' 그렇습니다, 갑자기 두 사람을 가지고 장난을 치고 싶은 욕망이 그녀를 사로잡았고, 그리하여 그녀는 단숨에 두 사람을 모두 정복해 버렸습니다."], ["The old man, who worshiped money, at once set aside three thousand roubles as a reward for one visit from her, but soon after that, he would have been happy to lay his property and his name at her feet, if only she would become his lawful wife.", "돈을 숭배하던 그 늙은이는 즉시 그녀의 한 번 방문에 대한 대가로 삼천 루블을 마련해 두었으나, 그 직후에는 그녀가 자신의 합법적인 아내가 되어 주기만 한다면 자신의 재산과 이름을 기꺼이 그녀의 발치에 바쳤을 것입니다."], ["We have good evidence of this.", "우리는 이에 관한 확실한 증거를 가지고 있습니다."], ["As for the prisoner, the tragedy of his fate is evident; it is before us.", "피고로 말하자면, 그 운명의 비극은 명백하니, 바로 우리 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있습니다."], ["But such was the young person’s ‘game.’ The enchantress gave the unhappy young man no hope until the last moment, when he knelt before her, stretching out hands that were already stained with the blood of his father and rival.", "그러나 그것이야말로 그 젊은 여인의 '유희'였던 것입니다. 그 요부는 불행한 청년에게 마지막 순간까지 아무런 희망도 주지 않았으니, 마침내 그가 이미 아버지이자 연적의 피로 물든 두 손을 내밀며 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇었던 그 순간까지도 그러했습니다."], ["It was in that position that he was arrested.", "바로 그 자세로 그는 체포되었습니다."], ["‘Send me to Siberia with him, I have brought him to this, I am most to blame,’ the woman herself cried, in genuine remorse at the moment of his arrest.", "'저도 그이와 함께 시베리아로 보내 주세요, 제가 그이를 이 지경으로 만들었어요, 가장 큰 죄인은 저예요.' 하고 그 여자 자신이, 그의 체포 순간에 진정한 회한에 잠겨 외쳤던 것입니다."]], [["“The talented young man, to whom I have referred already, Mr.", "\"제가 이미 언급한 바 있는 그 재능 있는 청년, 라키틴 군은 이 여주인공을 간결하고도 인상적인 말로 규정했습니다."], ["Rakitin, characterized this heroine in brief and impressive terms: ‘She was disillusioned early in life, deceived and ruined by a betrothed, who seduced and abandoned her.", "'그녀는 일찍이 인생에서 환멸을 겪었으니, 그녀를 유혹하고 버린 약혼자에게 기만당하고 파멸했습니다."], ["She was left in poverty, cursed by her respectable family, and taken under the protection of a wealthy old man, whom she still, however, considers as her benefactor.", "그녀는 빈궁 속에 남겨졌고, 점잖은 가족에게 저주받았으며, 어느 부유한 노인의 보호 아래 들어갔으되, 그러면서도 그녀는 여전히 그 노인을 자신의 은인으로 여기고 있습니다."], ["There was perhaps much that was good in her young heart, but it was embittered too early.", "그 젊은 마음속에는 어쩌면 선한 것이 많이 있었을지 모르나, 그것은 너무 일찍 쓰디쓰게 변해 버렸습니다."], ["She became prudent and saved money.", "그녀는 타산적으로 변해 돈을 모았습니다."], ["She grew sarcastic and resentful against society.’ After this sketch of her character it may well be understood that she might laugh at both of them simply from mischief, from malice.", "그녀는 빈정거리게 되었고 사회를 향해 원한을 품게 되었습니다.' 이러한 성격 묘사를 듣고 나면, 그녀가 그저 장난삼아, 악의에서 두 사람을 모두 비웃을 수도 있었으리라는 것을 충분히 이해할 수 있습니다."]], [["“After a month of hopeless love and moral degradation, during which he betrayed his betrothed and appropriated money entrusted to his honor, the prisoner was driven almost to frenzy, almost to madness by continual jealousy—and of whom?", "\"한 달에 걸친 희망 없는 사랑과 도덕적 타락 속에서, 그동안 그는 자신의 약혼녀를 배신하고 자신의 명예에 맡겨진 돈을 가로챘으며, 끊임없는 질투로 인해 거의 광란에 가까운, 거의 광기에 가까운 상태로 내몰렸습니다. 그런데 그 질투의 상대가 누구였습니까?"], ["His father!", "그의 아버지였습니다!"], ["And the worst of it was that the crazy old man was alluring and enticing the object of his affection by means of that very three thousand roubles, which the son looked upon as his own property, part of his inheritance from his mother, of which his father was cheating him.", "그리고 그중에서도 가장 끔찍한 것은, 그 미친 늙은이가 다름 아닌 그 삼천 루블을 미끼로 그가 애정을 품은 대상을 유혹하고 꾀어내고 있었다는 사실이니, 그 돈을 아들은 자신의 소유물로, 어머니로부터 받을 유산의 일부로 여겼으며, 아버지가 그것을 가로채 자신을 속이고 있다고 보았던 것입니다."], ["Yes, I admit it was hard to bear!", "그렇습니다, 저도 그것이 견디기 어려웠으리라는 점은 인정합니다!"], ["It might well drive a man to madness.", "그것은 능히 사람을 광기로 몰아갈 만했습니다."], ["It was not the money, but the fact that this money was used with such revolting cynicism to ruin his happiness!”", "문제는 돈이 아니라, 그 돈이 그토록 역겨운 냉소와 함께 그의 행복을 파멸시키는 데 쓰였다는 사실이었습니다!\""]], [["Then the prosecutor went on to describe how the idea of murdering his father had entered the prisoner’s head, and illustrated his theory with facts.", "이어 검사는 아버지를 살해한다는 생각이 어떻게 피고의 머릿속에 들어왔는지 묘사하기 시작했으며, 자신의 이론을 사실로써 뒷받침했다."]], [["“At first he only talked about it in taverns—he was talking about it all that month.", "\"처음에 그는 그저 술집에서 그 일을 입에 올리기만 했으니, 그는 그달 내내 그 이야기를 떠벌렸습니다."], ["Ah, he likes being always surrounded with company, and he likes to tell his companions everything, even his most diabolical and dangerous ideas; he likes to share every thought with others, and expects, for some reason, that those he confides in will meet him with perfect sympathy, enter into all his troubles and anxieties, take his part and not oppose him in anything.", "아, 그는 언제나 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있기를 좋아하며, 동료들에게 무엇이든, 심지어 자신의 가장 악마적이고 위험한 생각까지도 죄다 털어놓기를 좋아합니다. 그는 모든 생각을 남들과 나누기를 좋아하며, 무슨 까닭에서인지 자기가 속내를 털어놓는 사람들이 자신을 완벽한 공감으로 맞아 주고, 자신의 온갖 고민과 불안 속으로 함께 들어와 주며, 자신의 편을 들고 무슨 일에서든 반대하지 않으리라 기대합니다."], ["If not, he flies into a rage and smashes up everything in the tavern.", "만약 그렇지 않으면, 그는 격분하여 술집에 있는 모든 것을 때려 부숩니다."], ["[Then followed the anecdote about Captain Snegiryov.] Those who heard the prisoner began to think at last that he might mean more than threats, and that such a frenzy might turn threats into actions.”", "(그러고는 스네기료프 대위에 관한 일화가 이어졌다.) 피고의 말을 들은 자들은 마침내, 그것이 단순한 위협 이상의 의미를 담고 있을지도 모르며, 그러한 광란이 위협을 행동으로 바꿔 놓을 수도 있으리라고 생각하기 시작했습니다.\""]], [["Here the prosecutor described the meeting of the family at the monastery, the conversations with Alyosha, and the horrible scene of violence when the prisoner had rushed into his father’s house just after dinner.", "여기서 검사는 수도원에서의 가족 모임과 알료샤와 나눈 대화들, 그리고 피고가 식사 직후 아버지의 집으로 뛰어들었을 때 벌어진 끔찍한 폭력의 장면을 묘사했다."]], [["“I cannot positively assert,” the prosecutor continued, “that the prisoner fully intended to murder his father before that incident.", "\"저는 피고가 그 사건 이전에 아버지를 살해할 명백한 의도를 완전히 품고 있었다고 단정할 수는 없습니다.\""], ["Yet the idea had several times presented itself to him, and he had deliberated on it—for that we have facts, witnesses, and his own words.", "하고 검사가 말을 이었다. \"그러나 그 생각은 여러 차례 그에게 떠올랐고, 그는 그것을 곱씹어 보았으니, 이에 관해서는 우리에게 사실과 증인과 그 자신의 말이 있습니다."], ["I confess, gentlemen of the jury,”", "배심원 여러분, 저는 고백하건대,\""], ["he added, “that till to-day I have been uncertain whether to attribute to the prisoner conscious premeditation.", "하고 그가 덧붙였다. \"오늘에 이르기까지 저는 피고에게 의식적인 사전 계획을 인정해야 할지 확신하지 못하고 있었습니다."], ["I was firmly convinced that he had pictured the fatal moment beforehand, but had only pictured it, contemplating it as a possibility.", "저는 그가 그 운명의 순간을 미리 머릿속에 그려 보았으되, 다만 그려 보았을 뿐이며, 그것을 하나의 가능성으로서 떠올렸을 뿐이라고 굳게 믿고 있었습니다."], ["He had not definitely considered when and how he might commit the crime.", "그는 언제 어떻게 그 범죄를 저지를지 명확히 따져 보지는 않았던 것입니다."]], [["“But I was only uncertain till to-day, till that fatal document was presented to the court just now.", "\"그러나 저의 망설임은 오늘까지였을 뿐, 방금 그 운명적인 문서가 법정에 제출되기 전까지였습니다."], ["You yourselves heard that young lady’s exclamation, ‘It is the plan, the program of the murder!’ That is how she defined that miserable, drunken letter of the unhappy prisoner.", "여러분 스스로 저 젊은 여성의 외침을 들으셨습니다. '이것은 살인의 계획, 살인의 청사진이에요!'"], ["And, in fact, from that letter we see that the whole fact of the murder was premeditated.", "그녀는 그 불행한 피고가 취중에 쓴 가련한 편지를 그렇게 규정했습니다."], ["It was written two days before, and so we know now for a fact that, forty-eight hours before the perpetration of his terrible design, the prisoner swore that, if he could not get money next day, he would murder his father in order to take the envelope with the notes from under his pillow, as soon as Ivan had left.", "그리고 실제로 그 편지에서 우리는 살인의 전모가 사전에 계획되었음을 알 수 있습니다. 그것은 이틀 전에 쓰인 것이니, 그리하여 우리는 이제 그 끔찍한 계획을 실행에 옮기기 마흔여덟 시간 전에, 피고가 이튿날 돈을 구하지 못한다면 이반이 떠나는 즉시 아버지를 살해하여 베개 밑에서 지폐가 든 봉투를 빼앗겠노라고 맹세했다는 사실을 확실히 알고 있습니다."], ["‘As soon as Ivan had gone away’—you hear that; so he had thought everything out, weighing every circumstance, and he carried it all out just as he had written it.", "'이반이 떠나가는 즉시'라고 했으니, 여러분도 들으셨다시피, 그는 모든 것을 따져 보았고, 모든 정황을 저울질했으며, 자신이 써 놓은 그대로 그 모든 것을 실행에 옮긴 것입니다."], ["The proof of premeditation is conclusive; the crime must have been committed for the sake of the money, that is stated clearly, that is written and signed.", "사전 계획의 증거는 결정적입니다. 그 범죄는 돈을 위해 저질러진 것임에 틀림없으니, 그것은 명백히 진술되어 있고, 글로 쓰여 서명까지 되어 있습니다."], ["The prisoner does not deny his signature.", "피고는 자신의 서명을 부인하지 않습니다."]], [["“I shall be told he was drunk when he wrote it.", "\"누군가는 그가 그것을 쓸 때 취해 있었다고 말할 것입니다."], ["But that does not diminish the value of the letter, quite the contrary; he wrote when drunk what he had planned when sober.", "그러나 그것이 이 편지의 가치를 떨어뜨리지는 않으며, 오히려 정반대입니다. 그는 술에 취해서, 맨정신일 때 계획했던 것을 쓴 것입니다."], ["Had he not planned it when sober, he would not have written it when drunk.", "만약 맨정신일 때 그것을 계획하지 않았더라면, 취중에 그것을 쓰지도 않았을 것입니다."], ["I shall be asked: Then why did he talk about it in taverns?", "그러면 어찌하여 그가 술집에서 그 이야기를 떠벌렸는가 하고 물으실 것입니다."], ["A man who premeditates such a crime is silent and keeps it to himself.", "그런 범죄를 미리 계획하는 자라면 입을 다물고 혼자만 간직하는 법이라고 말입니다."], ["Yes, but he talked about it before he had formed a plan, when he had only the desire, only the impulse to it.", "그렇습니다, 그러나 그가 그 이야기를 떠벌린 것은 아직 계획을 세우기 전, 그저 그러한 욕망만을, 그저 그러한 충동만을 품고 있었을 때입니다."], ["Afterwards he talked less about it.", "그 후로 그는 그 이야기를 덜 했습니다."], ["On the evening he wrote that letter at the ‘Metropolis’ tavern, contrary to his custom he was silent, though he had been drinking.", "그가 '메트로폴리스' 술집에서 그 편지를 쓰던 날 저녁, 그는 자신의 습관과는 달리 입을 다물고 있었으니, 비록 술을 마시고는 있었지만 말입니다."], ["He did not play billiards, he sat in a corner, talked to no one.", "그는 당구도 치지 않았고, 한구석에 앉아 아무와도 이야기하지 않았습니다."], ["He did indeed turn a shopman out of his seat, but that was done almost unconsciously, because he could never enter a tavern without making a disturbance.", "물론 그는 한 점원을 그 자리에서 쫓아내기는 했으나, 그것은 거의 무의식적으로 한 짓이었으니, 그는 술집에 들어서면 소동을 일으키지 않고는 못 배기는 위인이었기 때문입니다."], ["It is true that after he had taken the final decision, he must have felt apprehensive that he had talked too much about his design beforehand, and that this might lead to his arrest and prosecution afterwards.", "최종 결심을 내린 뒤에 그가 자신의 계획을 미리 너무 많이 떠벌린 탓에 그것이 나중에 자신의 체포와 기소로 이어질지도 모른다는 불안을 느꼈으리라는 것은 사실입니다."], ["But there was nothing for it; he could not take his words back, but his luck had served him before, it would serve him again.", "그러나 어쩔 도리가 없었으니, 이미 내뱉은 말을 도로 거둬들일 수는 없는 노릇이었지만, 운이 전에도 그를 도왔으니 다시 한번 도와주리라 여겼던 것입니다."], ["He believed in his star, you know!", "그는, 아시다시피, 자신의 별을 믿고 있었습니다!"], ["I must confess, too, that he did a great deal to avoid the fatal catastrophe.", "저는 또한 그가 그 운명의 파국을 피하고자 무던히도 애를 썼다는 점을 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["‘To-morrow I shall try and borrow the money from every one,’ as he writes in his peculiar language, ‘and if they won’t give it to me, there will be bloodshed.’”", "'내일 나는 모든 사람에게 돈을 빌려 보려 애쓸 것이다.' 하고 그는 자신의 독특한 말투로 쓰고 있으니, '그래도 그들이 내게 돈을 주지 않는다면, 피를 보게 될 것이다'라고 말입니다.\""]], [["Here Ippolit Kirillovitch passed to a detailed description of all Mitya’s efforts to borrow the money.", "여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 돈을 빌리려던 미탸의 모든 노력에 대한 상세한 묘사로 넘어갔다."], ["He described his visit to Samsonov, his journey to Lyagavy.", "그는 삼소노프를 찾아간 일과 랴가비를 찾아간 여정을 묘사했다."], ["“Harassed, jeered at, hungry, after selling his watch to pay for the journey (though he tells us he had fifteen hundred roubles on him—a likely story), tortured by jealousy at having left the object of his affections in the town, suspecting that she would go to Fyodor Pavlovitch in his absence, he returned at last to the town, to find, to his joy, that she had not been near his father.", "\"시달리고, 조롱당하고, 굶주린 채로, 여비를 마련하느라 자신의 시계를 팔고 나서—비록 그는 자신에게 천오백 루블이 있었다고 우리에게 말하지만, 참으로 그럴듯한 이야기입니다만—애정의 대상을 읍내에 남겨 두고 떠난 데 대한 질투로 괴로워하며, 자신이 없는 동안 그녀가 표도르 파블로비치에게 가지 않을까 의심하면서, 그는 마침내 읍내로 돌아왔고, 그녀가 자신의 아버지 근처에 얼씬도 하지 않았음을 알고는 기뻐했습니다."], ["He accompanied her himself to her protector.", "그는 직접 그녀를 그 보호자에게 바래다주었습니다."], ["(Strange to say, he doesn’t seem to have been jealous of Samsonov, which is psychologically interesting.) Then he hastens back to his ambush in the back gardens, and there learns that Smerdyakov is in a fit, that the other servant is ill—the coast is clear and he knows the ‘signals’—what a temptation!", "(이상하게도 그는 삼소노프에 대해서는 질투하지 않은 듯한데, 이는 심리적으로 흥미로운 대목입니다.) 그러고 나서 그는 뒤뜰에 마련한 자신의 매복 장소로 서둘러 돌아왔고, 거기서 스메르댜코프가 발작을 일으켰으며 다른 하인은 병이 났다는 사실을 알게 됩니다. 길은 훤히 트여 있고, 그는 '신호'를 알고 있으니, 이 얼마나 큰 유혹입니까!"], ["Still he resists it; he goes off to a lady who has for some time been residing in the town, and who is highly esteemed among us, Madame Hohlakov.", "그래도 그는 그 유혹을 뿌리치고, 한동안 읍내에 거주하며 우리 사이에서 높은 존경을 받아 온 한 부인, 호흘라코바 부인을 찾아갑니다."], ["That lady, who had long watched his career with compassion, gave him the most judicious advice, to give up his dissipated life, his unseemly love-affair, the waste of his youth and vigor in pot-house debauchery, and to set off to Siberia to the gold-mines: ‘that would be an outlet for your turbulent energies, your romantic character, your thirst for adventure.’”", "오래도록 연민을 품고 그의 행적을 지켜봐 온 그 부인은 그에게 더없이 분별 있는 충고를 주었으니, 곧 방탕한 생활과 꼴사나운 연애, 술집의 난봉질로 청춘과 정력을 허비하는 짓을 그만두고 시베리아의 금광으로 떠나라는 것이었습니다. '그것이야말로 당신의 들끓는 정력과 낭만적인 성격, 모험을 향한 갈증을 풀어 줄 출구가 될 거예요'라고 말입니다.\""]], [["After describing the result of this conversation and the moment when the prisoner learnt that Grushenka had not remained at Samsonov’s, the sudden frenzy of the luckless man worn out with jealousy and nervous exhaustion, at the thought that she had deceived him and was now with his father, Ippolit Kirillovitch concluded by dwelling upon the fatal influence of chance.", "이 대화의 결말과, 피고가 그루셴카가 삼소노프의 집에 머물러 있지 않았음을 알게 된 순간, 그리고 그녀가 자신을 속이고 이제 자신의 아버지와 함께 있다는 생각에 질투와 신경의 탈진으로 기진맥진한 그 불운한 사내를 사로잡은 돌연한 광란을 묘사하고 난 뒤, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 우연이라는 것의 치명적인 영향력을 곱씹는 것으로 결론을 맺었다."], ["“Had the maid told him that her mistress was at Mokroe with her former lover, nothing would have happened.", "\"만약 그 하녀가 자신의 여주인이 옛 연인과 함께 모크로예에 있다고 그에게 말했더라면, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았을 것입니다."], ["But she lost her head, she could only swear and protest her ignorance, and if the prisoner did not kill her on the spot, it was only because he flew in pursuit of his false mistress.", "그러나 그녀는 정신을 잃고, 그저 맹세하며 자신은 모른다고 항변할 수밖에 없었으니, 피고가 그 자리에서 그녀를 죽이지 않은 것은 오로지 자신을 배신한 정부의 뒤를 쫓아 날아가 버렸기 때문일 뿐입니다."]], [["“But note, frantic as he was, he took with him a brass pestle. Why that?", "\"그러나 주목하십시오, 그토록 광란에 빠져 있으면서도 그는 놋쇠 절굿공이를 챙겨 들고 갔습니다."], ["Why not some other weapon?", "어찌하여 그것을?"], ["But since he had been contemplating his plan and preparing himself for it for a whole month, he would snatch up anything like a weapon that caught his eye.", "어찌하여 다른 무기가 아니라? 그러나 그는 한 달 내내 자신의 계획을 곱씹으며 그것에 대비해 왔으므로, 눈에 띈 무기 비슷한 것이라면 무엇이든 움켜쥐었을 것입니다."], ["He had realized for a month past that any object of the kind would serve as a weapon, so he instantly, without hesitation, recognized that it would serve his purpose.", "그는 지난 한 달 동안 그런 종류의 물건이라면 무엇이든 무기로 쓸 수 있으리라는 것을 깨닫고 있었기에, 즉시, 망설임 없이 그것이 자신의 목적에 쓸모가 있으리라는 것을 알아차렸습니다."], ["So it was by no means unconsciously, by no means involuntarily, that he snatched up that fatal pestle.", "그러니 그가 그 운명의 절굿공이를 움켜쥔 것은 결코 무의식적인 일도, 결코 자기도 모르게 한 일도 아니었습니다."], ["And then we find him in his father’s garden—the coast is clear, there are no witnesses, darkness and jealousy.", "그러고 나서 우리는 그가 아버지의 정원에 있는 것을 발견합니다. 길은 훤히 트여 있고, 증인도 없으며, 어둠과 질투뿐입니다."], ["The suspicion that she was there, with him, with his rival, in his arms, and perhaps laughing at him at that moment—took his breath away.", "그녀가 거기에, 그와 함께, 자신의 연적과 함께, 그 품 안에 안겨, 어쩌면 바로 그 순간 자신을 비웃고 있을지도 모른다는 의심이 그의 숨을 멎게 했습니다."], ["And it was not mere suspicion, the deception was open, obvious.", "그리고 그것은 단순한 의심이 아니었으니, 기만은 노골적이고 명백한 것이었습니다."], ["She must be there, in that lighted room, she must be behind the screen; and the unhappy man would have us believe that he stole up to the window, peeped respectfully in, and discreetly withdrew, for fear something terrible and immoral should happen.", "그녀는 틀림없이 거기에, 저 불 켜진 방에 있을 것이며, 틀림없이 칸막이 뒤에 있을 것입니다. 그런데도 그 불행한 사내는, 자기가 창가로 살그머니 다가가 공손히 안을 들여다본 뒤, 무언가 끔찍하고 부도덕한 일이 벌어질까 두려워 조심스레 물러났다고 우리더러 믿어 달라는 것입니다."], ["And he tries to persuade us of that, us, who understand his character, who know his state of mind at the moment, and that he knew the signals by which he could at once enter the house.”", "더욱이 그는 우리를, 곧 그의 성격을 이해하고 있고, 그 순간 그의 심리 상태를 알고 있으며, 그가 그 집에 즉시 들어갈 수 있는 신호를 알고 있었다는 것을 아는 우리를 그렇게 설득하려 드는 것입니다.\""], ["At this point Ippolit Kirillovitch broke off to discuss exhaustively the suspected connection of Smerdyakov with the murder.", "이 대목에서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 말을 멈추고, 스메르댜코프가 살인에 연루되었다는 의혹을 낱낱이 논하기 시작했다."], ["He did this very circumstantially, and every one realized that, although he professed to despise that suspicion, he thought the subject of great importance.", "그는 이를 매우 세세하게 다루었으니, 비록 그가 그 의혹을 경멸하는 척하기는 했으나 그 문제를 대단히 중요하게 여기고 있다는 것을 모두가 깨달았다."]], [["# Chapter VIII. A Treatise On Smerdyakov", "# Chapter VIII. A Treatise On Smerdyakov"]], [["“To begin with, what was the source of this suspicion?” (Ippolit Kirillovitch began.) “The first person who cried out that Smerdyakov had committed the murder was the prisoner himself at the moment of his arrest, yet from that time to this he had not brought forward a single fact to confirm the charge, nor the faintest suggestion of a fact.", "\"우선, 이 의혹은 대체 어디에서 비롯된 것입니까?\"(이폴리트 키릴로비치가 입을 열었다.) \"스메르댜코프가 살인을 저질렀다고 처음으로 부르짖은 사람은 다름 아닌 피고 자신으로, 그가 체포되던 바로 그 순간이었습니다."], ["The charge is confirmed by three persons only—the two brothers of the prisoner and Madame Svyetlov.", "그런데도 그때부터 지금에 이르기까지 그는 이 혐의를 뒷받침할 단 하나의 사실도, 사실의 희미한 암시조차도 내놓지 못했습니다."], ["The elder of these brothers expressed his suspicions only to-day, when he was undoubtedly suffering from brain fever.", "이 혐의를 뒷받침하는 사람은 단 세 명뿐이니—피고의 두 형제와 스베틀로바 부인입니다. 이들 형제 가운데 형이 자신의 의혹을 입에 담은 것은 다름 아닌 오늘이었는데, 그때 그는 의심할 바 없이 뇌염을 앓고 있었습니다."], ["But we know that for the last two months he has completely shared our conviction of his brother’s guilt and did not attempt to combat that idea. But of that later.", "그러나 우리가 아는 바로는 그가 지난 두 달 동안 동생의 유죄에 관한 우리의 확신을 완전히 함께해 왔고, 그 생각에 맞서려 들지 않았다는 것입니다."], ["The younger brother has admitted that he has not the slightest fact to support his notion of Smerdyakov’s guilt, and has only been led to that conclusion from the prisoner’s own words and the expression of his face.", "그러나 그 이야기는 나중에 하겠습니다. 동생은 스메르댜코프의 유죄에 관한 자기 생각을 뒷받침할 사실이라곤 손톱만큼도 없으며, 다만 피고 자신의 말과 그 얼굴 표정으로부터 그런 결론에 이르렀을 뿐이라고 인정했습니다."], ["Yes, that astounding piece of evidence has been brought forward twice to-day by him.", "그렇습니다, 그 어이없는 증거를 그는 오늘 두 차례나 내놓았습니다. 스베틀로바 부인은 한층 더 어이없었습니다."], ["Madame Svyetlov was even more astounding.", "'피고가 여러분께 하는 말은 믿으셔야 합니다."], ["‘What the prisoner tells you, you must believe; he is not a man to tell a lie.’ That is all the evidence against Smerdyakov produced by these three persons, who are all deeply concerned in the prisoner’s fate.", "그분은 거짓말을 할 사람이 아닙니다.' 스메르댜코프에게 불리한 증거라고는 이 세 사람이 내놓은 것이 전부인데, 이들은 모두 피고의 운명에 깊이 얽혀 있는 사람들입니다."], ["And yet the theory of Smerdyakov’s guilt has been noised about, has been and is still maintained.", "그런데도 스메르댜코프의 유죄라는 가설이 떠들썩하게 퍼져 나갔고, 지금도 여전히 주장되고 있습니다. 이것이 믿을 만한 일입니까?"], ["Is it credible? Is it conceivable?”", "이것이 생각할 수나 있는 일입니까?\""]], [["Here Ippolit Kirillovitch thought it necessary to describe the personality of Smerdyakov, “who had cut short his life in a fit of insanity.”", "여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 \"광기의 발작 속에서 스스로 목숨을 끊은\" 스메르댜코프의 인물됨을 묘사할 필요를 느꼈다."], ["He depicted him as a man of weak intellect, with a smattering of education, who had been thrown off his balance by philosophical ideas above his level and certain modern theories of duty, which he learnt in practice from the reckless life of his master, who was also perhaps his father—Fyodor Pavlovitch; and, theoretically, from various strange philosophical conversations with his master’s elder son, Ivan Fyodorovitch, who readily indulged in this diversion, probably feeling dull or wishing to amuse himself at the valet’s expense.", "그는 스메르댜코프를 지력이 약하고 어설픈 교양을 갖춘 사내로 그렸으니, 자기 수준을 넘어서는 철학적 관념들과 어떤 현대적 의무론에 휘둘려 균형을 잃은 자였는데, 그 의무론을 그는 실천으로는 자기 주인이자 어쩌면 친아버지였을 표도르 파블로비치의 무절제한 삶에서 배웠고, 이론으로는 주인의 큰아들 이반 표도로비치와 나눈 갖가지 기이한 철학적 대화에서 배웠다고 했다. 이반 표도로비치는 아마도 따분함을 느꼈거나 하인을 상대로 자신을 즐겁게 하고자 했던 탓에 이런 소일거리에 기꺼이 빠져들었던 것이다."], ["“He spoke to me himself of his spiritual condition during the last few days at his father’s house,”", "\"그자는 자기 아버지 집에서 보낸 마지막 며칠 동안의 정신 상태에 관해 제게 직접 이야기했습니다.\""], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch explained; “but others too have borne witness to it—the prisoner himself, his brother, and the servant Grigory—that is, all who knew him well.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치가 설명했다. \"그러나 다른 이들 또한 이를 증언하였으니—피고 자신과 그 형제, 그리고 하인 그리고리, 다시 말해 그를 잘 알던 사람들 모두가 그러했습니다."]], [["“Moreover, Smerdyakov, whose health was shaken by his attacks of epilepsy, had not the courage of a chicken.", "\"더욱이 간질 발작으로 건강이 흔들리던 스메르댜코프는 닭만큼의 용기도 없었습니다."], ["‘He fell at my feet and kissed them,’ the prisoner himself has told us, before he realized how damaging such a statement was to himself.", "'그자는 내 발치에 엎드려 발에 입을 맞추었다.' 피고 자신이 우리에게 이렇게 말했는데, 그런 진술이 자신에게 얼마나 불리한지 미처 깨닫기도 전이었습니다."], ["‘He is an epileptic chicken,’ he declared about him in his characteristic language.", "'그자는 간질 걸린 닭이다.' 그는 자기 특유의 말투로 그를 두고 이렇게 단언했습니다."], ["And the prisoner chose him for his confidant (we have his own word for it) and he frightened him into consenting at last to act as a spy for him.", "그리고 피고는 그를 자신의 심복으로 골랐으며(이것은 그 자신의 입에서 나온 말입니다), 겁을 주어 마침내 자기를 위한 첩자 노릇에 동의하도록 만들었습니다."], ["In that capacity he deceived his master, revealing to the prisoner the existence of the envelope with the notes in it and the signals by means of which he could get into the house.", "그 자격으로 그는 주인을 속였고, 지폐가 든 봉투의 존재와 집 안으로 들어올 수 있는 신호를 피고에게 일러바쳤습니다."], ["How could he help telling him, indeed?", "사실 어떻게 그가 말하지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["‘He would have killed me, I could see that he would have killed me,’ he said at the inquiry, trembling and shaking even before us, though his tormentor was by that time arrested and could do him no harm.", "'그자는 나를 죽였을 겁니다, 저는 그자가 나를 죽일 것임을 알 수 있었습니다.' 그는 심문 자리에서 우리 앞에서마저 부들부들 떨고 벌벌 떨며 이렇게 말했으니, 그를 괴롭히던 자가 그 무렵 이미 체포되어 그에게 아무런 해도 끼칠 수 없었는데도 그러했습니다."], ["‘He suspected me at every instant.", "'그자는 시시각각 저를 의심했습니다."], ["In fear and trembling I hastened to tell him every secret to pacify him, that he might see that I had not deceived him and let me off alive.’ Those are his own words.", "저는 두려움에 떨며 서둘러 모든 비밀을 그자에게 털어놓아 그를 달랬으니, 제가 그를 속이지 않았음을 알고 저를 살려 보내 주기를 바랐던 것입니다.' 이것이 그 자신의 말입니다."], ["I wrote them down and I remember them.", "저는 그것을 받아 적었고 또렷이 기억합니다."], ["‘When he began shouting at me, I would fall on my knees.’", "'그자가 제게 소리를 지르기 시작하면 저는 무릎을 꿇곤 했습니다.'"]], [["“He was naturally very honest and enjoyed the complete confidence of his master, ever since he had restored him some money he had lost.", "\"그는 본디 매우 정직했고, 주인이 잃어버린 돈을 되찾아 준 이래 주인의 완전한 신임을 누리고 있었습니다."], ["So it may be supposed that the poor fellow suffered pangs of remorse at having deceived his master, whom he loved as his benefactor.", "그러므로 이 가엾은 사내가 은인으로 여겨 사랑하던 주인을 속인 데 대해 회한의 고통을 느꼈으리라고 짐작할 수 있습니다."], ["Persons severely afflicted with epilepsy are, so the most skillful doctors tell us, always prone to continual and morbid self-reproach.", "가장 노련한 의사들이 우리에게 말해 주는 바에 따르면, 간질을 심하게 앓는 사람은 언제나 끊임없고 병적인 자책에 빠지기 쉽다고 합니다."], ["They worry over their ‘wickedness,’ they are tormented by pangs of conscience, often entirely without cause; they exaggerate and often invent all sorts of faults and crimes.", "그들은 자신의 '사악함'을 두고 괴로워하고, 흔히 아무런 이유도 없이 양심의 가책에 시달리며, 온갖 종류의 허물과 죄악을 부풀리고 흔히 지어내기까지 합니다."], ["And here we have a man of that type who had really been driven to wrong-doing by terror and intimidation.", "그리고 여기, 정말로 공포와 협박에 떠밀려 잘못을 저지른 그런 유형의 사내가 있는 것입니다."]], [["“He had, besides, a strong presentiment that something terrible would be the outcome of the situation that was developing before his eyes.", "\"게다가 그는 자기 눈앞에서 전개되어 가는 상황으로부터 무언가 끔찍한 일이 비롯될 것이라는 강한 예감을 품고 있었습니다."], ["When Ivan Fyodorovitch was leaving for Moscow, just before the catastrophe, Smerdyakov besought him to remain, though he was too timid to tell him plainly what he feared.", "이반 표도로비치가 그 참사 직전에 모스크바로 떠나려 할 때, 스메르댜코프는 자기가 무엇을 두려워하는지 분명히 말할 만큼 대담하지는 못했지만 그에게 남아 달라고 애원했습니다."], ["He confined himself to hints, but his hints were not understood.", "그는 넌지시 암시하는 데 그쳤으나, 그 암시는 이해받지 못했습니다."]], [["“It must be observed that he looked on Ivan Fyodorovitch as a protector, whose presence in the house was a guarantee that no harm would come to pass.", "\"여기서 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 그가 이반 표도로비치를 보호자로 여겼다는 점이니, 그가 집 안에 있다는 것은 아무런 해도 일어나지 않으리라는 보증과도 같았던 것입니다. 드미트리 카라마조프가 술에 취해 쓴 편지의 한 구절을 떠올려 보십시오."], ["Remember the phrase in Dmitri Karamazov’s drunken letter, ‘I shall kill the old man, if only Ivan goes away.’ So Ivan Fyodorovitch’s presence seemed to every one a guarantee of peace and order in the house.", "'이반이 떠나기만 한다면 나는 그 늙은이를 죽이겠다.' 그러니 이반 표도로비치가 있다는 것은 누구에게나 집안의 평화와 질서에 대한 보증으로 보였던 것입니다."]], [["“But he went away, and within an hour of his young master’s departure Smerdyakov was taken with an epileptic fit.", "\"그러나 그는 떠났고, 젊은 주인이 떠난 지 한 시간도 못 되어 스메르댜코프는 간질 발작을 일으켰습니다."], ["But that’s perfectly intelligible.", "그러나 이것은 충분히 이해할 만한 일입니다."], ["Here I must mention that Smerdyakov, oppressed by terror and despair of a sort, had felt during those last few days that one of the fits from which he had suffered before at moments of strain, might be coming upon him again.", "여기서 짚어 두어야 할 것은, 스메르댜코프가 일종의 공포와 절망에 짓눌린 채 그 마지막 며칠 동안, 긴장의 순간에 전에 그를 괴롭히던 발작 가운데 하나가 다시 닥쳐올지도 모른다고 느꼈다는 점입니다."], ["The day and hour of such an attack cannot, of course, be foreseen, but every epileptic can feel beforehand that he is likely to have one.", "물론 그러한 발작의 날과 시각을 미리 알 수는 없으나, 모든 간질 환자는 자신에게 발작이 일어날 듯하다는 것을 미리 느낄 수 있다고 합니다."], ["So the doctors tell us.", "의사들이 우리에게 그렇게 일러 줍니다."], ["And so, as soon as Ivan Fyodorovitch had driven out of the yard, Smerdyakov, depressed by his lonely and unprotected position, went to the cellar.", "그리하여 이반 표도로비치가 마차로 마당을 빠져나가자마자 스메르댜코프는 외롭고 보호받지 못하는 자기 처지에 의기소침해져 지하 저장고로 내려갔습니다."], ["He went down the stairs wondering if he would have a fit or not, and what if it were to come upon him at once.", "그는 발작이 일어날지 어떨지, 또 만일 당장 닥쳐온다면 어쩔까 생각하며 계단을 내려갔습니다."], ["And that very apprehension, that very wonder, brought on the spasm in his throat that always precedes such attacks, and he fell unconscious into the cellar.", "바로 그 불안, 바로 그 의심이 그런 발작에 늘 앞서는 목구멍의 경련을 불러일으켰고, 그는 정신을 잃고 저장고로 굴러떨어졌습니다. 그런데 이 더할 나위 없이 자연스러운 사건에서 사람들은 그가 일부러 발작을 가장했다는 의혹, 그 암시를 찾아내려 합니다."], ["And in this perfectly natural occurrence people try to detect a suspicion, a hint that he was shamming an attack on purpose.", "그러나 만일 일부러였다면 곧바로 이런 의문이 떠오릅니다. 그의 동기가 무엇이었습니까?"], ["But, if it were on purpose, the question arises at once, what was his motive?", "그는 무엇을 노렸습니까? 무엇을 겨냥했습니까?"], ["What was he reckoning on? What was he aiming at?", "의학에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["I say nothing about medicine: science, I am told, may go astray: the doctors were not able to discriminate between the counterfeit and the real.", "듣자 하니 과학도 길을 잃을 수 있다고 합니다. 의사들은 가짜와 진짜를 가려내지 못했습니다."], ["That may be so, but answer me one question: what motive had he for such a counterfeit?", "그럴 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 제게 한 가지 질문에만 답해 주십시오."], ["Could he, had he been plotting the murder, have desired to attract the attention of the household by having a fit just before?", "그런 가장을 할 무슨 동기가 그에게 있었습니까? 만일 그가 살인을 꾸미고 있었다면, 바로 직전에 발작을 일으켜 온 집안의 주의를 끌고 싶어 했겠습니까?"]], [["“You see, gentlemen of the jury, on the night of the murder, there were five persons in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s—Fyodor Pavlovitch himself (but he did not kill himself, that’s evident); then his servant, Grigory, but he was almost killed himself; the third person was Grigory’s wife, Marfa Ignatyevna, but it would be simply shameful to imagine her murdering her master.", "\"보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 살인이 일어난 그날 밤 표도르 파블로비치의 집에는 다섯 사람이 있었습니다—표도르 파블로비치 자신(그러나 그가 자기를 죽이지 않았음은 분명합니다), 그다음으로 그의 하인 그리고리, 그러나 그 자신이 거의 죽임을 당할 뻔했습니다. 세 번째 인물은 그리고리의 아내 마르파 이그나티예브나이나, 그녀가 주인을 살해했다고 상상하는 것은 그저 부끄러운 일일 따름입니다."], ["Two persons are left—the prisoner and Smerdyakov.", "그러면 두 사람이 남습니다—피고와 스메르댜코프입니다."], ["But, if we are to believe the prisoner’s statement that he is not the murderer, then Smerdyakov must have been, for there is no other alternative, no one else can be found.", "그러나 만일 자기가 살인자가 아니라는 피고의 진술을 믿어야 한다면, 스메르댜코프가 살인자였을 수밖에 없으니, 다른 대안이 없고 달리 찾을 사람이 없기 때문입니다."], ["That is what accounts for the artful, astounding accusation against the unhappy idiot who committed suicide yesterday.", "바로 이것이, 어제 스스로 목숨을 끊은 그 불행한 백치에 대한 교묘하고도 어이없는 고발의 까닭입니다."], ["Had a shadow of suspicion rested on any one else, had there been any sixth person, I am persuaded that even the prisoner would have been ashamed to accuse Smerdyakov, and would have accused that sixth person, for to charge Smerdyakov with that murder is perfectly absurd.", "만일 누구든 다른 사람에게 한 가닥 의혹의 그림자라도 드리워졌더라면, 만일 여섯 번째 인물이 있었더라면, 저는 확신컨대 피고조차 스메르댜코프를 고발하기를 부끄러워했을 것이며 그 여섯 번째 인물을 고발했을 것입니다. 스메르댜코프에게 그 살인의 혐의를 씌우는 것은 완전히 터무니없는 일이기 때문입니다."]], [["“Gentlemen, let us lay aside psychology, let us lay aside medicine, let us even lay aside logic, let us turn only to the facts and see what the facts tell us.", "\"여러분, 심리학은 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 의학도 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 논리학마저도 옆으로 치워 둡시다, 오로지 사실로만 눈을 돌려 사실이 우리에게 무엇을 말해 주는지 봅시다."], ["If Smerdyakov killed him, how did he do it?", "만일 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽였다면 어떻게 죽였습니까?"], ["Alone or with the assistance of the prisoner?", "혼자서입니까, 아니면 피고의 도움을 받아서입니까?"], ["Let us consider the first alternative—that he did it alone.", "첫 번째 대안을 따져 봅시다—그가 혼자 했다는 것 말입니다."], ["If he had killed him it must have been with some object, for some advantage to himself.", "만일 그가 죽였다면 거기에는 어떤 목적이, 자기에게 돌아올 어떤 이득이 있었어야 합니다."], ["But not having a shadow of the motive that the prisoner had for the murder—hatred, jealousy, and so on—Smerdyakov could only have murdered him for the sake of gain, in order to appropriate the three thousand roubles he had seen his master put in the envelope.", "그러나 피고가 살인을 저지른 동기—증오, 질투 따위—라고는 그림자만큼도 없었던 스메르댜코프로서는 오로지 이득을 위해, 주인이 봉투에 넣는 것을 자기 눈으로 본 그 삼천 루블을 가로채기 위해서만 그를 죽일 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["And yet he tells another person—and a person most closely interested, that is, the prisoner—everything about the money and the signals, where the envelope lay, what was written on it, what it was tied up with, and, above all, told him of those signals by which he could enter the house.", "그런데도 그는 다른 사람에게—그것도 가장 깊이 이해관계가 얽힌 사람, 곧 피고에게—그 돈과 신호에 관한 모든 것을, 봉투가 어디에 놓여 있는지, 거기에 무엇이 적혀 있는지, 무엇으로 묶여 있는지, 그리고 무엇보다도 집 안으로 들어올 수 있는 그 신호까지 일러 주었습니다."], ["Did he do this simply to betray himself, or to invite to the same enterprise one who would be anxious to get that envelope for himself?", "그가 이렇게 한 것이 그저 자신을 폭로하기 위해서였습니까, 아니면 그 봉투를 손에 넣고 싶어 안달할 사람을 같은 일에 끌어들이기 위해서였습니까?"], ["‘Yes,’ I shall be told, ‘but he betrayed it from fear.’ But how do you explain this?", "'그렇지만,' 사람들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요, '그는 두려워서 그것을 털어놓은 것이다.'"], ["A man who could conceive such an audacious, savage act, and carry it out, tells facts which are known to no one else in the world, and which, if he held his tongue, no one would ever have guessed!", "그러나 이것을 어떻게 설명하시겠습니까? 그토록 대담하고 흉포한 행위를 머릿속에 그리고 또 실행에 옮길 수 있는 사내가, 세상 누구도 모르는, 그리고 그가 입을 다물고만 있었다면 그 누구도 결코 짐작조차 못 했을 사실들을 털어놓는단 말입니까!"]], [["“No, however cowardly he might be, if he had plotted such a crime, nothing would have induced him to tell any one about the envelope and the signals, for that was as good as betraying himself beforehand.", "\"아닙니다, 그가 아무리 겁쟁이였다 한들, 만일 그런 범죄를 꾸미고 있었다면 봉투와 신호에 관해 누구에게도 발설하도록 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었을 터이니, 그것은 미리 자신을 폭로하는 것이나 다름없었기 때문입니다."], ["He would have invented something, he would have told some lie if he had been forced to give information, but he would have been silent about that.", "만일 무엇이라도 알려 주도록 강요당했다면 그는 무언가 지어냈을 것이고 어떤 거짓말이라도 했을 것이나, 그 점에 관해서는 입을 다물었을 것입니다."], ["For, on the other hand, if he had said nothing about the money, but had committed the murder and stolen the money, no one in the world could have charged him with murder for the sake of robbery, since no one but he had seen the money, no one but he knew of its existence in the house.", "왜냐하면 다른 한편으로, 만일 그가 돈에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않고 살인을 저질러 돈을 훔쳤다면, 세상 누구도 그를 강도살인의 혐의로 고발할 수 없었을 것이니, 그 돈을 본 사람도 그 외에는 없었고 그것이 집 안에 있다는 것을 아는 사람도 그 외에는 없었기 때문입니다."], ["Even if he had been accused of the murder, it could only have been thought that he had committed it from some other motive.", "설령 그가 살인 혐의로 고발되었다 하더라도, 다만 다른 어떤 동기에서 저질렀으리라고만 여겨졌을 것입니다."], ["But since no one had observed any such motive in him beforehand, and every one saw, on the contrary, that his master was fond of him and honored him with his confidence, he would, of course, have been the last to be suspected.", "그러나 아무도 미리 그에게서 그런 동기를 보지 못했고, 도리어 모두가 주인이 그를 아끼고 신임으로 그를 존중했음을 보았으니, 그는 물론 가장 마지막으로 의심받았을 사람입니다."], ["People would have suspected first the man who had a motive, a man who had himself declared he had such motives, who had made no secret of it; they would, in fact, have suspected the son of the murdered man, Dmitri Fyodorovitch.", "사람들은 동기를 지닌 사람을, 자기에게 그런 동기가 있다고 스스로 떠벌리고 그것을 조금도 숨기지 않은 사람을 가장 먼저 의심했을 것입니다. 사실상 그들은 살해당한 자의 아들 드미트리 표도로비치를 의심했을 것입니다."], ["Had Smerdyakov killed and robbed him, and the son been accused of it, that would, of course, have suited Smerdyakov.", "만일 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽이고 돈을 훔쳤는데 그 아들이 그 죄로 고발되었더라면, 그것이야말로 물론 스메르댜코프에게 안성맞춤이었을 것입니다."], ["Yet are we to believe that, though plotting the murder, he told that son, Dmitri, about the money, the envelope, and the signals?", "그런데도 우리는, 그가 살인을 꾸미면서도 그 아들 드미트리에게 돈과 봉투와 신호에 관해 일러 주었다고 믿어야 합니까? 그것이 논리에 맞습니까?"], ["Is that logical? Is that clear?", "그것이 분명한 일입니까?"]], [["“When the day of the murder planned by Smerdyakov came, we have him falling downstairs in a feigned fit—with what object?", "\"스메르댜코프가 계획한 살인의 날이 닥쳐오자, 우리는 그가 가장된 발작을 일으켜 계단에서 굴러떨어지는 것을 봅니다—무슨 목적으로 말입니까?"], ["In the first place that Grigory, who had been intending to take his medicine, might put it off and remain on guard, seeing there was no one to look after the house, and, in the second place, I suppose, that his master seeing that there was no one to guard him, and in terror of a visit from his son, might redouble his vigilance and precaution.", "첫째로, 약을 먹으려 작정하고 있던 그리고리가 집을 지킬 사람이 없음을 보고 그것을 미루고 망을 보며 남도록 하기 위해서요, 둘째로, 짐작건대 자기를 지켜 줄 사람이 없음을 본 주인이 아들의 방문을 두려워한 나머지 경계와 조심을 갑절로 더하도록 하기 위해서일 것입니다."], ["And, most of all, I suppose that he, Smerdyakov, disabled by the fit, might be carried from the kitchen, where he always slept, apart from all the rest, and where he could go in and out as he liked, to Grigory’s room at the other end of the lodge, where he was always put, shut off by a screen three paces from their own bed.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 짐작건대 발작으로 몸을 가누지 못하게 된 그 자신, 곧 스메르댜코프가, 그가 늘 다른 모든 이들과 떨어져 자고 마음대로 드나들 수 있던 부엌에서, 별채 반대편 끝에 있는 그리고리의 방으로 옮겨지기 위해서일 것이니, 그곳은 그가 늘 눕혀지던 곳으로, 그들 부부의 침대에서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 칸막이로 가려져 있었습니다."], ["This was the immemorial custom established by his master and the kind-hearted Marfa Ignatyevna, whenever he had a fit.", "이것은 그가 발작을 일으킬 때마다 주인과 인정 많은 마르파 이그나티예브나가 세워 둔 오래된 관습이었습니다."], ["There, lying behind the screen, he would most likely, to keep up the sham, have begun groaning, and so keeping them awake all night (as Grigory and his wife testified).", "거기, 칸막이 뒤에 누워서 그는 십중팔구 시늉을 계속하느라 신음을 내기 시작하여 (그리고리와 그의 아내가 증언했듯이) 밤새도록 그들을 잠 못 들게 했을 것입니다."], ["And all this, we are to believe, that he might more conveniently get up and murder his master!", "그리고 이 모든 것이, 우리더러 믿으라는 바로는, 그가 더 편리하게 일어나 주인을 살해하기 위해서였다는 것입니다!"]], [["“But I shall be told that he shammed illness on purpose that he might not be suspected and that he told the prisoner of the money and the signals to tempt him to commit the murder, and when he had murdered him and had gone away with the money, making a noise, most likely, and waking people, Smerdyakov got up, am I to believe, and went in—what for?", "\"그러나 사람들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요. 그가 의심받지 않기 위해 일부러 병을 가장했고, 살인을 저지르도록 유혹하려고 피고에게 돈과 신호를 일러 주었으며, 피고가 그를 살해하고 돈을 가지고 떠난 뒤—십중팔구 소란을 피워 사람들을 깨우면서—스메르댜코프가 일어나 들어갔다고, 제가 믿어야 한단 말입니까?"], ["To murder his master a second time and carry off the money that had already been stolen?", "무엇 하러 말입니까? 주인을 두 번째로 살해하고 이미 훔쳐 간 돈을 빼내 가려고요?"], ["Gentlemen, are you laughing?", "여러분, 웃으십니까?"], ["I am ashamed to put forward such suggestions, but, incredible as it seems, that’s just what the prisoner alleges.", "저는 이런 추측을 내놓는 것이 부끄럽습니다만, 믿기 어려운 일이긴 해도, 바로 그것이 피고가 주장하는 바입니다."], ["When he had left the house, had knocked Grigory down and raised an alarm, he tells us Smerdyakov got up, went in and murdered his master and stole the money!", "피고가 집을 떠나 그리고리를 쓰러뜨리고 소동을 일으킨 뒤에 스메르댜코프가 일어나 들어가 주인을 살해하고 돈을 훔쳤다는 것입니다!"], ["I won’t press the point that Smerdyakov could hardly have reckoned on this beforehand, and have foreseen that the furious and exasperated son would simply come to peep in respectfully, though he knew the signals, and beat a retreat, leaving Smerdyakov his booty.", "스메르댜코프가 이것을 미리 헤아렸을 리 없다는 점은, 또 격분하여 노발대발한 아들이 신호를 알면서도 그저 공손히 안을 들여다보려고만 와서 스메르댜코프에게 노획물을 남겨 둔 채 물러나리라고 미리 내다보았을 리 없다는 점은 굳이 강조하지 않겠습니다."], ["Gentlemen of the jury, I put this question to you in earnest; when was the moment when Smerdyakov could have committed his crime?", "배심원 여러분, 저는 여러분께 이 질문을 진지하게 던집니다. 스메르댜코프가 자기 범죄를 저지를 수 있었던 순간은 언제였습니까?"], ["Name that moment, or you can’t accuse him.", "그 순간을 말해 보십시오, 그러지 못한다면 여러분은 그를 고발할 수 없습니다."]], [["“But, perhaps, the fit was a real one, the sick man suddenly recovered, heard a shout, and went out.", "\"그러나 어쩌면 발작은 진짜였고, 병든 그자가 갑자기 정신을 차려 외침 소리를 듣고 밖으로 나갔을 수도 있습니다."], ["Well—what then?", "그래서요—그다음은?"], ["He looked about him and said, ‘Why not go and kill the master?’ And how did he know what had happened, since he had been lying unconscious till that moment?", "그는 주위를 둘러보고 이렇게 말했다는 겁니까, '주인을 죽이러 가면 어떨까?' 그런데 그 순간까지 정신을 잃고 누워 있었던 그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 어떻게 알았겠습니까?"], ["But there’s a limit to these flights of fancy.", "그러나 이런 공상의 비약에도 한계가 있는 법입니다."]], [["“‘Quite so,’ some astute people will tell me, ‘but what if they were in agreement?", "\"'바로 그렇소.' 어떤 약삭빠른 이들은 제게 이렇게 말하겠지요, '하지만 만일 그들이 공모했다면 어떻소?"], ["What if they murdered him together and shared the money—what then?’ A weighty question, truly!", "만일 그들이 함께 그를 살해하고 돈을 나눠 가졌다면—그땐 어떻소?' 참으로 묵직한 질문입니다!"], ["And the facts to confirm it are astounding.", "그리고 그것을 뒷받침할 사실들이라는 게 가관입니다."], ["One commits the murder and takes all the trouble while his accomplice lies on one side shamming a fit, apparently to arouse suspicion in every one, alarm in his master and alarm in Grigory.", "한 사람은 살인을 저지르며 온갖 수고를 떠맡는데, 그 공범은 한쪽에 드러누워 발작을 가장하니, 보아하니 모든 사람에게 의혹을 불러일으키고 주인에게 경각심을, 그리고리에게 경각심을 불러일으키기 위해서랍니다."], ["It would be interesting to know what motives could have induced the two accomplices to form such an insane plan.", "두 공범이 그런 정신 나간 계획을 짜도록 만든 동기가 무엇이었을지 알아본다면 흥미로울 것입니다."]], [["“But perhaps it was not a case of active complicity on Smerdyakov’s part, but only of passive acquiescence; perhaps Smerdyakov was intimidated and agreed not to prevent the murder, and foreseeing that he would be blamed for letting his master be murdered, without screaming for help or resisting, he may have obtained permission from Dmitri Karamazov to get out of the way by shamming a fit—‘you may murder him as you like; it’s nothing to me.’ But as this attack of Smerdyakov’s was bound to throw the household into confusion, Dmitri Karamazov could never have agreed to such a plan.", "\"그러나 어쩌면 스메르댜코프 편에서는 능동적인 공모가 아니라 그저 수동적인 묵인의 경우였을지도 모릅니다. 어쩌면 스메르댜코프는 겁을 먹고 살인을 막지 않기로 동의했을 것이며, 비명을 지르거나 저항하지 않고 주인이 살해당하도록 내버려 둔 데 대해 자기가 책망받으리라 내다보고, 발작을 가장하여 자리를 피해도 좋다는 허락을 드미트리 카라마조프에게서 얻었을지도 모릅니다—'마음대로 그를 죽여라, 그건 내 알 바 아니다.'"], ["I will waive that point however.", "그러나 스메르댜코프의 이 발작은 온 집안을 혼란에 빠뜨릴 수밖에 없었으니, 드미트리 카라마조프가 그런 계획에 결코 동의했을 리 없습니다."], ["Supposing that he did agree, it would still follow that Dmitri Karamazov is the murderer and the instigator, and Smerdyakov is only a passive accomplice, and not even an accomplice, but merely acquiesced against his will through terror.", "그렇지만 그 점은 일단 접어 두겠습니다. 설령 그가 동의했다 하더라도, 그래도 결국 드미트리 카라마조프가 살인자이자 교사자이며, 스메르댜코프는 그저 수동적인 공범에 지나지 않으니, 아니, 공범도 아니고 다만 공포에 떠밀려 제 뜻과 달리 묵인했을 뿐이라는 결론이 나옵니다."]], [["“But what do we see?", "\"그러나 우리가 보는 것은 무엇입니까?"], ["As soon as he is arrested the prisoner instantly throws all the blame on Smerdyakov, not accusing him of being his accomplice, but of being himself the murderer.", "체포되자마자 피고는 즉각 모든 죄를 스메르댜코프에게 떠넘기는데, 그를 공범으로 고발하는 것이 아니라 그가 바로 살인자라고 고발합니다. '그자가 혼자 했다.'"], ["‘He did it alone,’ he says.", "그는 말합니다."], ["‘He murdered and robbed him.", "'그자가 그를 죽이고 돈을 훔쳤다."], ["It was the work of his hands.’ Strange sort of accomplices who begin to accuse one another at once!", "그자의 손이 한 짓이다.' 곧바로 서로를 고발하기 시작하는 참으로 별난 공범들입니다!"], ["And think of the risk for Karamazov.", "게다가 카라마조프로서는 그것이 얼마나 위험한지 생각해 보십시오."], ["After committing the murder while his accomplice lay in bed, he throws the blame on the invalid, who might well have resented it and in self-preservation might well have confessed the truth.", "공범이 침대에 누워 있는 동안 살인을 저지른 뒤에, 그는 그 병자에게 죄를 떠넘기는데, 병자라면 충분히 그것에 분개하여 자기를 지키고자 진실을 자백할 만했습니다."], ["For he might well have seen that the court would at once judge how far he was responsible, and so he might well have reckoned that if he were punished, it would be far less severely than the real murderer.", "왜냐하면 법정이 곧 그가 어디까지 책임이 있는지 가려내리라는 것을 그가 충분히 알았을 것이고, 그래서 만일 처벌받는다 해도 진짜 살인자보다는 훨씬 가벼우리라고 충분히 헤아릴 만했기 때문입니다."], ["But in that case he would have been certain to make a confession, yet he has not done so.", "그러나 그렇다면 그는 틀림없이 자백을 했을 터인데도 그렇게 하지 않았습니다."], ["Smerdyakov never hinted at their complicity, though the actual murderer persisted in accusing him and declaring that he had committed the crime alone.", "진짜 살인자가 끈질기게 그를 고발하며 자기 혼자 범죄를 저질렀다고 단언했는데도, 스메르댜코프는 그들의 공모를 결코 입에 올리지 않았습니다."]], [["“What’s more, Smerdyakov at the inquiry volunteered the statement that it was he who had told the prisoner of the envelope of notes and of the signals, and that, but for him, he would have known nothing about them.", "\"더욱이 스메르댜코프는 심문 자리에서, 피고에게 지폐가 든 봉투와 신호에 관해 일러 준 사람이 바로 자기이며, 자기가 없었다면 피고는 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐을 것이라고 자청하여 진술했습니다."], ["If he had really been a guilty accomplice, would he so readily have made this statement at the inquiry?", "만일 그가 정말 죄 있는 공범이었다면, 심문 자리에서 그토록 선뜻 이런 진술을 했겠습니까?"], ["On the contrary, he would have tried to conceal it, to distort the facts or minimize them.", "도리어 그는 그것을 감추려, 사실을 왜곡하거나 축소하려 했을 것입니다."], ["But he was far from distorting or minimizing them.", "그러나 그는 사실을 왜곡하거나 축소하기는커녕 도리어 거리가 멀었습니다."], ["No one but an innocent man, who had no fear of being charged with complicity, could have acted as he did.", "공모 혐의로 고발될까 봐 두려워할 것이 없는 결백한 사내가 아니고서는 누구도 그처럼 행동할 수 없었을 것입니다."], ["And in a fit of melancholy arising from his disease and this catastrophe he hanged himself yesterday.", "그리고 자기 병과 이 참사에서 비롯된 우울의 발작 속에서 그는 어제 목을 매었습니다. 그는 자기 특유의 말투로 쓴 쪽지를 남겼습니다."], ["He left a note written in his peculiar language, ‘I destroy myself of my own will and inclination so as to throw no blame on any one.’ What would it have cost him to add: ‘I am the murderer, not Karamazov’?", "'나는 누구에게도 죄를 씌우지 않으려고 내 뜻과 의향에 따라 스스로 목숨을 끊는다.' '나는 살인자다, 카라마조프가 아니다'라고 덧붙이는 것이 그에게 무슨 대수였겠습니까?"], ["But that he did not add.", "그러나 그는 그것을 덧붙이지 않았습니다."], ["Did his conscience lead him to suicide and not to avowing his guilt?", "양심이 그를 자살로는 이끌면서 죄의 고백으로는 이끌지 못했단 말입니까?"]], [["“And what followed?", "\"그래서 그다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌습니까?"], ["Notes for three thousand roubles were brought into the court just now, and we were told that they were the same that lay in the envelope now on the table before us, and that the witness had received them from Smerdyakov the day before.", "방금 삼천 루블어치의 지폐가 법정으로 들어왔고, 우리는 그것이 지금 우리 앞 탁자 위에 놓인 봉투 속에 들어 있던 바로 그 지폐이며, 증인이 그것을 하루 전에 스메르댜코프에게서 받았다는 말을 들었습니다."], ["But I need not recall the painful scene, though I will make one or two comments, selecting such trivial ones as might not be obvious at first sight to every one, and so may be overlooked.", "그러나 저는 그 괴로운 장면을 다시 떠올릴 필요는 없겠고, 다만 한두 가지 논평을 덧붙이려 하는데, 처음에는 누구에게나 한눈에 드러나지 않아서 간과되기 쉬운 그런 사소한 것들을 골라 보겠습니다."], ["In the first place, Smerdyakov must have given back the money and hanged himself yesterday from remorse.", "첫째로, 스메르댜코프는 회한 때문에 돈을 돌려주고 어제 목을 매었음이 분명합니다."], ["And only yesterday he confessed his guilt to Ivan Karamazov, as the latter informs us.", "그리고 그가 자기 죄를 이반 카라마조프에게 고백한 것도 바로 어제로, 이반 카라마조프가 우리에게 그렇게 알려 주었습니다."], ["If it were not so, indeed, why should Ivan Fyodorovitch have kept silence till now?", "만일 정말 그렇지 않다면, 대체 왜 이반 표도로비치는 지금까지 입을 다물고 있었겠습니까?"], ["And so, if he has confessed, then why, I ask again, did he not avow the whole truth in the last letter he left behind, knowing that the innocent prisoner had to face this terrible ordeal the next day?", "그러니 만일 그가 고백했다면, 죄 없는 피고가 이튿날 이 끔찍한 시련에 맞서야 한다는 것을 알면서도, 어째서, 거듭 묻건대, 그는 뒤에 남긴 마지막 쪽지에서 모든 진실을 털어놓지 않았단 말입니까?"]], [["“The money alone is no proof.", "\"돈만으로는 아무런 증거가 되지 못합니다."], ["A week ago, quite by chance, the fact came to the knowledge of myself and two other persons in this court that Ivan Fyodorovitch had sent two five per cent. coupons of five thousand each—that is, ten thousand in all—to the chief town of the province to be changed.", "일주일 전, 그야말로 우연히도, 이 법정에 있는 저와 다른 두 사람의 귀에 한 가지 사실이 들어왔는데, 이반 표도로비치가 각각 오천 루블짜리 오 퍼센트 이표 두 장—다시 말해 도합 일만 루블을—환전하기 위해 도청 소재지로 보냈다는 것입니다."], ["I only mention this to point out that any one may have money, and that it can’t be proved that these notes are the same as were in Fyodor Pavlovitch’s envelope.", "저는 다만 누구에게나 돈이 있을 수 있다는 것, 그리고 이 지폐가 표도르 파블로비치의 봉투 속에 들어 있던 것과 같은 것임이 증명될 수 없다는 것을 지적하기 위해 이를 언급할 따름입니다."]], [["“Ivan Karamazov, after receiving yesterday a communication of such importance from the real murderer, did not stir.", "\"이반 카라마조프는 어제 진짜 살인자에게서 그토록 중대한 통보를 받고서도 꼼짝하지 않았습니다. 왜 그는 그것을 곧바로 신고하지 않았습니까?"], ["Why didn’t he report it at once?", "왜 그 모든 것을 아침까지 미루었습니까?"], ["Why did he put it all off till morning?", "저는 그 까닭을 추측할 권리가 있다고 봅니다."], ["I think I have a right to conjecture why.", "그의 건강은 지난 일주일 동안 무너져 가고 있었습니다."], ["His health had been giving way for a week past: he had admitted to a doctor and to his most intimate friends that he was suffering from hallucinations and seeing phantoms of the dead: he was on the eve of the attack of brain fever by which he has been stricken down to-day.", "그는 한 의사에게, 그리고 가장 가까운 친구들에게, 자기가 환각에 시달리며 죽은 자들의 환영을 본다고 털어놓았습니다. 그는 오늘 그를 쓰러뜨린 뇌염의 발작 직전에 있었습니다."], ["In this condition he suddenly heard of Smerdyakov’s death, and at once reflected, ‘The man is dead, I can throw the blame on him and save my brother.", "이런 상태에서 그는 갑자기 스메르댜코프의 죽음을 전해 들었고, 곧바로 이렇게 생각한 것입니다. '그자는 죽었으니 그자에게 죄를 떠넘겨 동생을 구할 수 있다."], ["I have money.", "내게는 돈이 있다."], ["I will take a roll of notes and say that Smerdyakov gave them me before his death.’ You will say that was dishonorable: it’s dishonorable to slander even the dead, and even to save a brother.", "지폐 한 뭉치를 가져다가 스메르댜코프가 죽기 전에 내게 주었다고 말하자.' 여러분은 그것이 떳떳지 못한 일이라고 하시겠지요."], ["True, but what if he slandered him unconsciously?", "죽은 자를 헐뜯는 것은, 비록 동생을 구하기 위해서라 해도 떳떳지 못한 일입니다. 옳은 말씀입니다."], ["What if, finally unhinged by the sudden news of the valet’s death, he imagined it really was so?", "그러나 만일 그가 무의식중에 그를 헐뜯은 것이라면 어떻습니까? 만일 하인의 갑작스러운 죽음이라는 소식에 끝내 정신이 무너져, 정말로 그러했다고 그가 상상한 것이라면 어떻습니까?"], ["You saw the recent scene: you have seen the witness’s condition.", "여러분은 조금 전의 장면을 보셨습니다. 여러분은 그 증인의 상태를 보셨습니다."], ["He was standing up and was speaking, but where was his mind?", "그는 일어서서 말을 하고 있었으나, 그의 정신은 어디에 있었습니까?"]], [["“Then followed the document, the prisoner’s letter written two days before the crime, and containing a complete program of the murder.", "\"그다음에는 문서가, 범죄 이틀 전에 쓰인 피고의 편지가 뒤따랐는데, 거기에는 살인의 완전한 청사진이 담겨 있었습니다."], ["Why, then, are we looking for any other program?", "그렇다면 우리가 무엇 하러 다른 청사진을 찾고 있는 것입니까?"], ["The crime was committed precisely according to this program, and by no other than the writer of it.", "범죄는 정확히 이 청사진대로, 다름 아닌 그것을 쓴 자에 의해 저질러졌습니다."], ["Yes, gentlemen of the jury, it went off without a hitch!", "그렇습니다, 배심원 여러분, 일은 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 진행되었습니다!"], ["He did not run respectfully and timidly away from his father’s window, though he was firmly convinced that the object of his affections was with him.", "그는 자기 애정의 대상이 함께 있다고 굳게 믿었으면서도, 아버지의 창문에서 공손하게 머뭇거리며 달아나지 않았습니다. 아닙니다, 그것은 터무니없고 있을 법하지 않은 일입니다!"], ["No, that is absurd and unlikely! He went in and murdered him.", "그는 안으로 들어가 그를 살해했습니다."], ["Most likely he killed him in anger, burning with resentment, as soon as he looked on his hated rival.", "십중팔구 그는 분노에 휩싸여, 증오하던 연적을 보자마자 원한에 불타올라 그를 죽였을 것입니다."], ["But having killed him, probably with one blow of the brass pestle, and having convinced himself, after careful search, that she was not there, he did not, however, forget to put his hand under the pillow and take out the envelope, the torn cover of which lies now on the table before us.", "그러나 그를 죽이고 나서, 아마도 놋쇠 절굿공이로 단 한 차례 내리쳐 죽인 뒤에, 그리고 꼼꼼히 살펴 그녀가 거기에 없음을 확인하고 나서도, 그는 베개 밑으로 손을 넣어 봉투를—지금 우리 앞 탁자 위에 그 찢긴 껍데기가 놓여 있는 그 봉투를—꺼내는 것은 잊지 않았습니다."]], [["“I mention this fact that you may note one, to my thinking, very characteristic circumstance.", "\"제가 이 사실을 언급하는 것은, 제 생각으로는 매우 특징적인 한 가지 정황을 여러분께서 주목하시도록 하기 위해서입니다."], ["Had he been an experienced murderer and had he committed the murder for the sake of gain only, would he have left the torn envelope on the floor as it was found, beside the corpse?", "만일 그가 노련한 살인자였고 오로지 이득을 위해 살인을 저질렀더라면, 발견된 그대로 시신 곁 바닥에 찢긴 봉투를 남겨 두었겠습니까?"], ["Had it been Smerdyakov, for instance, murdering his master to rob him, he would have simply carried away the envelope with him, without troubling himself to open it over his victim’s corpse, for he would have known for certain that the notes were in the envelope—they had been put in and sealed up in his presence—and had he taken the envelope with him, no one would ever have known of the robbery.", "가령 그것이 스메르댜코프였더라면, 주인을 죽여 그를 털려 했더라면, 그는 그저 봉투를 통째로 가지고 떠났을 것이고, 굳이 희생자의 시신 위에서 그것을 뜯어 보는 수고는 하지 않았을 것이니, 지폐가 봉투 속에 들어 있음을—그것은 그가 보는 앞에서 넣어지고 봉인되었으니—틀림없이 알았을 것이고, 봉투를 통째로 가지고 갔더라면 그 누구도 도난 사실을 결코 알지 못했을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["I ask you, gentlemen, would Smerdyakov have behaved in that way?", "여러분께 묻습니다, 스메르댜코프가 그런 식으로 행동했겠습니까?"], ["Would he have left the envelope on the floor?", "그가 봉투를 바닥에 남겨 두었겠습니까?"]], [["“No, this was the action of a frantic murderer, a murderer who was not a thief and had never stolen before that day, who snatched the notes from under the pillow, not like a thief stealing them, but as though seizing his own property from the thief who had stolen it.", "\"아닙니다, 이것은 광란에 빠진 살인자의 행동, 도둑이 아니며 그날 이전에는 한 번도 도둑질해 본 적 없는 살인자의 행동이었으니, 그는 베개 밑에서 지폐를 낚아챘으되, 그것을 훔치는 도둑처럼이 아니라, 마치 그것을 훔쳐 간 도둑에게서 제 것을 빼앗는 것처럼 그리한 것입니다."], ["For that was the idea which had become almost an insane obsession in Dmitri Karamazov in regard to that money.", "왜냐하면 바로 그것이 그 돈에 관하여 드미트리 카라마조프 속에서 거의 미친 집착이 되어 버린 관념이었기 때문입니다."], ["And pouncing upon the envelope, which he had never seen before, he tore it open to make sure whether the money was in it, and ran away with the money in his pocket, even forgetting to consider that he had left an astounding piece of evidence against himself in that torn envelope on the floor.", "그래서 그는 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 봉투에 덤벼들어, 그 속에 돈이 들어 있는지 확인하려고 그것을 뜯어 열고는, 돈을 주머니에 넣은 채 달아났으니, 바닥에 그 찢긴 봉투로 자신에게 불리한 어이없는 증거를 남겨 두었다는 사실을 헤아릴 생각조차 하지 못했습니다."], ["All because it was Karamazov, not Smerdyakov, he didn’t think, he didn’t reflect, and how should he?", "이 모든 것은 그가 스메르댜코프가 아니라 카라마조프였기 때문이니, 그는 생각하지 않았고, 곱씹지 않았으며, 또 어찌 그럴 수 있었겠습니까? 그는 달아났습니다."], ["He ran away; he heard behind him the servant cry out; the old man caught him, stopped him and was felled to the ground by the brass pestle.", "그는 등 뒤에서 하인이 외치는 소리를 들었습니다. 늙은이가 그를 붙들어 멈춰 세웠다가 놋쇠 절굿공이에 맞아 땅바닥에 쓰러졌습니다."]], [["“The prisoner, moved by pity, leapt down to look at him.", "\"피고는 연민에 사로잡혀 그를 보려고 뛰어내렸습니다."], ["Would you believe it, he tells us that he leapt down out of pity, out of compassion, to see whether he could do anything for him.", "믿어지십니까, 그는 자기가 연민에서, 동정심에서, 그를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 있을까 보려고 뛰어내렸다고 우리에게 말합니다."], ["Was that a moment to show compassion?", "그때가 동정심을 드러낼 순간이었습니까?"], ["No; he jumped down simply to make certain whether the only witness of his crime were dead or alive.", "아닙니다. 그는 그저 자기 범죄의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 살았는지 확인하려고 뛰어내린 것입니다."], ["Any other feeling, any other motive would be unnatural.", "그 밖의 다른 감정, 다른 동기라면 무엇이든 부자연스러울 것입니다."], ["Note that he took trouble over Grigory, wiped his head with his handkerchief and, convincing himself he was dead, he ran to the house of his mistress, dazed and covered with blood.", "그가 그리고리에게 수고를 들여 손수건으로 머리를 닦아 주었고, 그가 죽었다고 스스로 확신하고서, 얼이 빠진 채 피투성이가 되어 자기 애인의 집으로 달려갔다는 점을 주목하십시오."], ["How was it he never thought that he was covered with blood and would be at once detected?", "자기가 피투성이여서 곧바로 발각되리라는 것을 그가 어떻게 한 번도 생각하지 못했단 말입니까?"], ["But the prisoner himself assures us that he did not even notice that he was covered with blood.", "그러나 피고 자신은 자기가 피투성이라는 것조차 알아차리지 못했다고 우리에게 단언합니다."], ["That may be believed, that is very possible, that always happens at such moments with criminals.", "그것은 믿을 만한 일이며, 충분히 있을 수 있는 일이고, 그런 순간에 범죄자들에게는 늘 그런 일이 일어납니다."], ["On one point they will show diabolical cunning, while another will escape them altogether.", "한 가지 점에서는 그들이 악마 같은 교활함을 드러내는가 하면, 다른 점은 그들에게서 송두리째 빠져나가 버립니다."], ["But he was thinking at that moment of one thing only—where was she?", "그러나 그는 그 순간 오로지 한 가지만을 생각하고 있었습니다—그녀는 어디에 있는가?"], ["He wanted to find out at once where she was, so he ran to her lodging and learnt an unexpected and astounding piece of news—she had gone off to Mokroe to meet her first lover.”", "그는 즉각 그녀가 어디 있는지 알아내고 싶었기에 그녀의 거처로 달려갔다가, 뜻밖의 어이없는 소식을 알게 되었습니다—그녀가 첫 연인을 만나러 모크로예로 떠나 버렸다는 것이었습니다.\""]], [["# Chapter IX. The Galloping Troika. The End Of The Prosecutor’s Speech.", "# Chapter IX. The Galloping Troika. The End Of The Prosecutor’s Speech."]], [["Ippolit Kirillovitch had chosen the historical method of exposition, beloved by all nervous orators, who find in its limitation a check on their own eager rhetoric.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 역사적 서술법을 택했는데, 이는 신경질적인 웅변가라면 누구나 즐겨 쓰는 방식으로, 그들은 그 방식의 제약 속에서 제 분방한 수사에 대한 고삐를 발견하는 것이다."], ["At this moment in his speech he went off into a dissertation on Grushenka’s “first lover,” and brought forward several interesting thoughts on this theme.", "연설의 이 대목에 이르러 그는 그루셴카의 \"첫사랑 남자\"에 관한 논평으로 흘러들어, 이 주제를 두고 흥미로운 생각 몇 가지를 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["“Karamazov, who had been frantically jealous of every one, collapsed, so to speak, and effaced himself at once before this first lover.", "\"누구에게나 미친 듯이 질투를 느끼던 카라마조프는, 말하자면 이 첫사랑 남자 앞에서 단숨에 무너지고 스스로를 지워 버렸습니다."], ["What makes it all the more strange is that he seems to have hardly thought of this formidable rival.", "더욱 기이한 것은, 그가 이 무서운 연적을 거의 생각해 본 적조차 없었던 듯하다는 점입니다."], ["But he had looked upon him as a remote danger, and Karamazov always lives in the present.", "그는 그자를 먼 곳의 위험쯤으로만 여겼고, 카라마조프란 언제나 현재 속에서 사는 인간입니다."], ["Possibly he regarded him as a fiction.", "어쩌면 그는 그자를 한낱 허구로 보았는지도 모릅니다."], ["But his wounded heart grasped instantly that the woman had been concealing this new rival and deceiving him, because he was anything but a fiction to her, because he was the one hope of her life.", "그러나 상처 입은 그의 마음은 이 새로운 연적을 그 여자가 감추고 자기를 속여 왔다는 사실을 단번에 알아챘으니, 그자는 그 여자에게만은 결코 허구가 아니었고, 도리어 그녀의 삶에서 단 하나뿐인 희망이었기 때문입니다."], ["Grasping this instantly, he resigned himself.", "이를 단번에 깨닫자 그는 체념하고 말았습니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, I cannot help dwelling on this unexpected trait in the prisoner’s character.", "\"배심원 여러분, 저는 피고의 성격에서 드러난 이 뜻밖의 면모를 짚고 넘어가지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["He suddenly evinces an irresistible desire for justice, a respect for woman and a recognition of her right to love.", "그는 별안간 거부할 수 없는 정의에의 갈망과, 여인에 대한 존중과, 그녀가 사랑할 권리를 지녔다는 인정을 드러냅니다. 그것도 바로 그녀를 위해 제 손을 아비의 피로 더럽힌 바로 그 순간에 말입니다!"], ["And all this at the very moment when he had stained his hands with his father’s blood for her sake!", "사실인즉 그가 흘린 피는 이미 복수를 외치고 있었으니, 이 세상에서 제 영혼과 제 삶을 망가뜨려 놓은 그는 바로 그 순간 스스로에게 물을 수밖에 없었던 것입니다."], ["It is true that the blood he had shed was already crying out for vengeance, for, after having ruined his soul and his life in this world, he was forced to ask himself at that same instant what he was and what he could be now to her, to that being, dearer to him than his own soul, in comparison with that former lover who had returned penitent, with new love, to the woman he had once betrayed, with honorable offers, with the promise of a reformed and happy life.", "자기 영혼보다 더 소중한 그 존재, 그녀에게 이제 자기란 무엇이며 무엇이 될 수 있겠는가를 말입니다. 한때 자기가 배신했던 여인에게 새로운 사랑을 품고 참회하여 돌아온, 명예로운 청혼과 함께 개과천선한 행복한 삶을 약속하는 그 옛 연인과 견주어서 말입니다."], ["And he, luckless man, what could he give her now, what could he offer her?", "그런데 그, 이 불운한 사내가 이제 그녀에게 무엇을 줄 수 있으며, 무엇을 바칠 수 있겠습니까?"]], [["“Karamazov felt all this, knew that all ways were barred to him by his crime and that he was a criminal under sentence, and not a man with life before him!", "\"카라마조프는 이 모든 것을 느꼈고, 자신의 범죄로 인해 모든 길이 막혀 버렸으며, 자기가 앞날을 가진 인간이 아니라 선고를 받은 죄인이라는 것을 알았습니다!"], ["This thought crushed him.", "이 생각이 그를 짓눌렀습니다."], ["And so he instantly flew to one frantic plan, which, to a man of Karamazov’s character, must have appeared the one inevitable way out of his terrible position.", "그리하여 그는 즉시 어떤 미친 듯한 계획 하나로 날아들었으니, 카라마조프 같은 성격의 인간에게는 그것이야말로 자신의 끔찍한 처지에서 벗어날 단 하나의 불가피한 출구로 보였을 것이 분명합니다."], ["That way out was suicide.", "그 출구란 곧 자살이었습니다."], ["He ran for the pistols he had left in pledge with his friend Perhotin and on the way, as he ran, he pulled out of his pocket the money, for the sake of which he had stained his hands with his father’s gore.", "그는 친구 페르호틴에게 저당 잡혀 둔 권총을 찾으러 달려갔고, 달려가는 도중에 주머니에서 돈을 꺼냈으니, 바로 그 돈을 위해 그는 제 손을 아비의 핏물로 더럽혔던 것입니다."], ["Oh, now he needed money more than ever.", "오, 이제 그에게는 그 어느 때보다도 돈이 절실했습니다."], ["Karamazov would die, Karamazov would shoot himself and it should be remembered!", "카라마조프는 죽으리라, 카라마조프는 제 손으로 총을 쏘리라, 그리고 그것이 사람들의 기억에 남으리라!"], ["To be sure, he was a poet and had burnt the candle at both ends all his life.", "과연 그는 시인이었으니, 평생토록 촛불을 양 끝에서 동시에 태워 온 인간이었습니다."], ["‘To her, to her!", "'그녀에게로, 그녀에게로!"], ["and there, oh, there I will give a feast to the whole world, such as never was before, that will be remembered and talked of long after!", "그리고 거기서, 오 거기서 나는 온 세상에 잔치를 베풀어 주리라, 일찍이 없던 그런 잔치를, 두고두고 기억되고 회자될 그런 잔치를!"], ["In the midst of shouts of wild merriment, reckless gypsy songs and dances I shall raise the glass and drink to the woman I adore and her new-found happiness!", "거친 환락의 함성 속에서, 무모한 집시의 노래와 춤 속에서 나는 잔을 들어 내가 사모하는 여인과 그녀가 새로이 찾은 행복을 위해 건배하리라!"], ["And then, on the spot, at her feet, I shall dash out my brains before her and punish myself!", "그러고는 그 자리에서, 그녀의 발치에서, 그녀 앞에 내 머리통을 박살 내어 나 자신을 벌하리라!"], ["She will remember Mitya Karamazov sometimes, she will see how Mitya loved her, she will feel for Mitya!’", "그녀는 이따금 미탸 카라마조프를 떠올리리라, 미탸가 그녀를 얼마나 사랑했는지 알리라, 미탸를 가엾이 여기리라!'"]], [["“Here we see in excess a love of effect, a romantic despair and sentimentality, and the wild recklessness of the Karamazovs.", "\"여기서 우리는 효과를 노리는 지나친 욕심과, 낭만적인 절망과 감상, 그리고 카라마조프 일가 특유의 거친 무모함을 봅니다."], ["Yes, but there is something else, gentlemen of the jury, something that cries out in the soul, throbs incessantly in the mind, and poisons the heart unto death—that something is conscience, gentlemen of the jury, its judgment, its terrible torments!", "그렇습니다, 그러나 그 밖에도 무언가가 더 있습니다, 배심원 여러분, 영혼 속에서 부르짖고, 끊임없이 마음속에서 고동치며, 가슴을 죽음에 이르도록 좀먹는 무언가가 말입니다. 그 무언가란 바로 양심입니다, 배심원 여러분, 양심의 심판이요, 그 무서운 고통입니다!"], ["The pistol will settle everything, the pistol is the only way out!", "권총이 모든 것을 매듭지으리라, 권총만이 유일한 출구다!"], ["But beyond—I don’t know whether Karamazov wondered at that moment ‘What lies beyond,’ and whether Karamazov could, like Hamlet, wonder ‘What lies beyond.’ No, gentlemen of the jury, they have their Hamlets, but we still have our Karamazovs!”", "그러나 그 너머는—나는 카라마조프가 그 순간 '그 너머에 무엇이 있는가'를 의아해했는지, 또 카라마조프가 햄릿처럼 '그 너머에 무엇이 있는가'를 의아해할 수 있었는지 모릅니다. 아닙니다, 배심원 여러분, 그들에게는 그들의 햄릿이 있으나, 우리에게는 아직도 우리의 카라마조프가 있을 따름입니다!\""]], [["Here Ippolit Kirillovitch drew a minute picture of Mitya’s preparations, the scene at Perhotin’s, at the shop, with the drivers.", "여기서 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 미탸가 채비를 갖추던 광경을, 페르호틴의 집에서, 가게에서, 마부들과 함께 벌어진 장면을 세밀하게 그려 보였다."], ["He quoted numerous words and actions, confirmed by witnesses, and the picture made a terrible impression on the audience.", "그는 증인들이 확인해 준 수많은 말과 행동을 인용했고, 그 묘사는 청중에게 끔찍한 인상을 남겼다."], ["The guilt of this harassed and desperate man stood out clear and convincing, when the facts were brought together.", "사실들을 한데 모아 놓으니, 이 시달리고 절망에 빠진 사내의 죄상이 또렷하고도 설득력 있게 떠올랐다."]], [["“What need had he of precaution?", "\"그가 무엇 때문에 조심할 필요가 있었겠습니까?"], ["Two or three times he almost confessed, hinted at it, all but spoke out.”", "그는 두세 번이나 거의 자백할 뻔했고, 넌지시 비추었으며, 하마터면 다 털어놓을 뻔했습니다.\" (이어서 증인들의 진술이 따라 나왔다.) \"그는 심지어 자기를 태우고 달리던 농부에게 이렇게 외치기까지 했습니다."], ["(Then followed the evidence given by witnesses.) “He even cried out to the peasant who drove him, ‘Do you know, you are driving a murderer!’ But it was impossible for him to speak out, he had to get to Mokroe and there to finish his romance.", "'이보게, 자네는 지금 살인자를 태우고 가는 줄 아나!' 그러나 그로서는 다 털어놓는 것이 불가능했으니, 그는 모크로예에 당도하여 거기서 자신의 로맨스를 끝맺어야만 했던 것입니다."], ["But what was awaiting the luckless man?", "그렇다면 이 불운한 사내를 기다리고 있던 것은 무엇이었겠습니까?"], ["Almost from the first minute at Mokroe he saw that his invincible rival was perhaps by no means so invincible, that the toast to their new-found happiness was not desired and would not be acceptable.", "모크로예에 당도한 거의 첫 순간부터 그는, 자신의 무적의 연적이 어쩌면 결코 그토록 무적은 아닐지도 모른다는 것을, 그들이 새로이 찾은 행복을 위한 건배 따위는 바라지도 않고 받아들여지지도 않으리라는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["But you know the facts, gentlemen of the jury, from the preliminary inquiry.", "그러나 배심원 여러분, 여러분은 예심을 통해 이미 그 사실들을 알고 계십니다."], ["Karamazov’s triumph over his rival was complete and his soul passed into quite a new phase, perhaps the most terrible phase through which his soul has passed or will pass.", "연적에 대한 카라마조프의 승리는 완벽했고, 그의 영혼은 전혀 새로운 국면으로, 어쩌면 그의 영혼이 지나왔거나 앞으로 지나갈 가운데 가장 끔찍한 국면으로 옮아갔습니다."]], [["“One may say with certainty, gentlemen of the jury,”", "\"배심원 여러분, 단언컨대,\" 검사가 말을 이었다."], ["the prosecutor continued, “that outraged nature and the criminal heart bring their own vengeance more completely than any earthly justice.", "\"능욕당한 본성과 죄지은 가슴은 그 어떤 지상의 정의보다도 더 완벽하게 스스로 복수를 가합니다. 뿐만 아니라 지상의 정의와 형벌은 도리어 본성의 형벌을 덜어 주며, 실로 그런 순간에 죄인의 영혼에게는 절망으로부터의 구원으로서 없어서는 안 될 것입니다."], ["What’s more, justice and punishment on earth positively alleviate the punishment of nature and are, indeed, essential to the soul of the criminal at such moments, as its salvation from despair.", "그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것을, 그를 위해 그녀가 첫사랑 남자를 물리쳤다는 것을, 그녀가 그를, 미탸를 새로운 삶으로 부르고 있다는 것을, 그녀가 그에게 행복을 약속하고 있다는 것을 알았을 때—더구나 언제 말입니까?"], ["For I cannot imagine the horror and moral suffering of Karamazov when he learnt that she loved him, that for his sake she had rejected her first lover, that she was summoning him, Mitya, to a new life, that she was promising him happiness—and when? When everything was over for him and nothing was possible!", "그에게는 모든 것이 끝나 버리고 아무것도 가능하지 않게 된 바로 그때 말입니다!—카라마조프가 느꼈을 공포와 도덕적 고뇌를 나는 차마 상상조차 할 수 없습니다!"]], [["“By the way, I will note in parenthesis a point of importance for the light it throws on the prisoner’s position at the moment.", "\"덧붙여, 그 순간 피고의 처지를 비추어 주는 중요한 한 가지를 괄호 안에 넣어 두는 셈으로 짚어 두겠습니다."], ["This woman, this love of his, had been till the last moment, till the very instant of his arrest, a being unattainable, passionately desired by him but unattainable.", "이 여인, 그의 이 사랑은 마지막 순간까지, 그가 체포되는 바로 그 찰나까지도 그에게는 도저히 닿을 수 없는 존재, 열렬히 갈망하면서도 닿을 수 없는 존재였습니다."], ["Yet why did he not shoot himself then, why did he relinquish his design and even forget where his pistol was?", "그런데 어찌하여 그는 그때 제 손으로 총을 쏘지 않았으며, 어찌하여 자신의 계획을 단념하고 심지어 권총이 어디 있는지조차 잊어버렸겠습니까?"], ["It was just that passionate desire for love and the hope of satisfying it that restrained him.", "그를 붙들어 둔 것은 바로 그 열렬한 사랑에의 갈망과 그것을 채우리라는 희망이었던 것입니다."], ["Throughout their revels he kept close to his adored mistress, who was at the banquet with him and was more charming and fascinating to him than ever—he did not leave her side, abasing himself in his homage before her.", "흥청거리는 내내 그는 자신이 흠모하는 정부 곁에 바짝 붙어 있었으니, 그녀는 그와 함께 그 잔치 자리에 있었고 그에게 그 어느 때보다도 매혹적이고 사로잡는 존재였습니다—그는 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않고, 그녀 앞에서 자신을 낮추며 경배를 바쳤습니다."]], [["“His passion might well, for a moment, stifle not only the fear of arrest, but even the torments of conscience.", "\"그의 정념은 한순간 체포의 두려움뿐 아니라 양심의 고통마저도 충분히 잠재울 만했습니다."], ["For a moment, oh, only for a moment!", "한순간, 오, 그저 한순간만은 말입니다!"], ["I can picture the state of mind of the criminal hopelessly enslaved by these influences—first, the influence of drink, of noise and excitement, of the thud of the dance and the scream of the song, and of her, flushed with wine, singing and dancing and laughing to him!", "나는 이러한 영향에 속절없이 사로잡힌 죄인의 마음 상태를 그려 볼 수 있습니다—첫째로 술과 소음과 흥분의 영향, 춤의 쿵쿵거림과 노래의 비명 같은 외침의 영향, 그리고 술기운에 발그레해진 채 그를 향해 노래하고 춤추고 웃어 보이는 그녀의 영향입니다!"], ["Secondly, the hope in the background that the fatal end might still be far off, that not till next morning, at least, they would come and take him.", "둘째로는, 그 숙명적인 종말이 아직 멀리 있을지도 모른다는, 적어도 이튿날 아침까지는 그들이 와서 자기를 잡아가지는 않으리라는 막연한 희망입니다."], ["So he had a few hours and that’s much, very much!", "그러니 그에게는 몇 시간이 남아 있었고, 그것은 큰 것입니다, 아주 큰 것입니다!"], ["In a few hours one can think of many things.", "몇 시간이면 사람은 많은 것을 생각할 수 있습니다."], ["I imagine that he felt something like what criminals feel when they are being taken to the scaffold.", "나는 그가 사형대로 끌려가는 죄수들이 느끼는 것과 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈으리라 상상합니다."], ["They have another long, long street to pass down and at walking pace, past thousands of people.", "그들 앞에는 또 하나의 길고 긴 거리가 펼쳐져 있고, 수천 명의 사람들 곁을 지나 천천히 걸어가야 합니다."], ["Then there will be a turning into another street and only at the end of that street the dread place of execution!", "그러고는 또 다른 거리로 꺾어 들어, 그 거리의 끝에 이르러서야 비로소 무시무시한 처형 장소가 나타나는 것입니다!"], ["I fancy that at the beginning of the journey the condemned man, sitting on his shameful cart, must feel that he has infinite life still before him.", "나는 그 길의 처음에 그 죄인이 치욕스러운 수레에 앉아 자기 앞에 아직도 무한한 삶이 남아 있다고 느끼리라 짐작합니다."], ["The houses recede, the cart moves on—oh, that’s nothing, it’s still far to the turning into the second street and he still looks boldly to right and to left at those thousands of callously curious people with their eyes fixed on him, and he still fancies that he is just such a man as they.", "집들이 뒤로 물러나고 수레는 나아갑니다—오, 그것은 아무것도 아니다, 두 번째 거리로 꺾어드는 모퉁이까지는 아직 멀다, 그리고 그는 여전히 대담하게 좌우를 둘러보며, 무심하면서도 호기심에 찬 눈길로 그를 응시하는 수천 명의 사람들을 바라보고, 자기도 그들과 똑같은 사람이라고 여전히 여깁니다."], ["But now the turning comes to the next street.", "그러나 이제 다음 거리로 꺾어드는 모퉁이가 다가옵니다."], ["Oh, that’s nothing, nothing, there’s still a whole street before him, and however many houses have been passed, he will still think there are many left.", "오, 그것은 아무것도 아니다, 아무것도 아니다, 아직 거리 하나가 통째로 남아 있다, 그리고 아무리 많은 집을 지나쳤어도 그는 여전히 남은 집이 많다고 생각할 것입니다."], ["And so to the very end, to the very scaffold.", "그렇게 마지막 순간까지, 바로 그 사형대에 이르기까지 말입니다."]], [["“This I imagine is how it was with Karamazov then.", "\"카라마조프도 그때 그러했으리라고 나는 상상합니다. '저들에게는 아직 짬이 없었다,'"], ["‘They’ve not had time yet,’ he must have thought, ‘I may still find some way out, oh, there’s still time to make some plan of defense, and now, now—she is so fascinating!’", "그는 분명 이렇게 생각했을 것입니다. '나는 아직 어떤 출구를 찾을 수 있을지 모른다, 오, 아직 무슨 방어책을 세울 시간이 있다, 그런데 지금, 지금—그녀가 이토록 매혹적이지 않은가!'"]], [["“His soul was full of confusion and dread, but he managed, however, to put aside half his money and hide it somewhere—I cannot otherwise explain the disappearance of quite half of the three thousand he had just taken from his father’s pillow.", "\"그의 영혼은 혼란과 두려움으로 가득했으나, 그래도 그는 가까스로 돈의 절반을 따로 떼어 어딘가에 감추었습니다—그렇지 않고서는 그가 방금 아비의 베개 밑에서 빼낸 삼천 루블 가운데 꼭 절반이 사라진 것을 달리 설명할 길이 없습니다."], ["He had been in Mokroe more than once before, he had caroused there for two days together already, he knew the old big house with all its passages and outbuildings.", "그는 전에도 한두 번 모크로예에 가 본 적이 있었고, 이미 그곳에서 이틀이나 내리 흥청거린 적이 있었으니, 통로며 별채가 딸린 그 오래되고 커다란 집을 잘 알고 있었습니다."], ["I imagine that part of the money was hidden in that house, not long before the arrest, in some crevice, under some floor, in some corner, under the roof.", "나는 그 돈의 일부가 체포되기 얼마 전에 그 집 어딘가에, 어떤 틈새에, 어떤 마룻장 밑에, 어떤 구석에, 지붕 밑에 감추어졌으리라 상상합니다."], ["With what object? I shall be asked.", "무슨 목적으로 그랬느냐고 물으실 것입니다."], ["Why, the catastrophe may take place at once, of course; he hadn’t yet considered how to meet it, he hadn’t the time, his head was throbbing and his heart was with her, but money—money was indispensable in any case!", "아니, 파국은 당장에라도 닥칠 수 있는 것입니다, 물론이지요. 그는 아직 그것에 어떻게 대처할지 궁리하지 못했고, 그럴 시간도 없었으며, 그의 머리는 욱신거렸고 그의 마음은 그녀에게 가 있었으나, 돈은—돈은 어쨌거나 없어서는 안 될 것이었습니다!"], ["With money a man is always a man.", "돈이 있으면 사람은 언제나 사람 구실을 하는 법입니다."], ["Perhaps such foresight at such a moment may strike you as unnatural?", "그런 순간에 그런 선견지명을 부린다는 것이 여러분에게는 부자연스럽게 비칠지도 모르겠습니다."], ["But he assures us himself that a month before, at a critical and exciting moment, he had halved his money and sewn it up in a little bag.", "그러나 그는 한 달 전 어느 위태롭고 흥분된 순간에 자기 돈을 반으로 나누어 작은 주머니에 꿰매어 두었다고 우리에게 직접 장담하고 있습니다."], ["And though that was not true, as we shall prove directly, it shows the idea was a familiar one to Karamazov, he had contemplated it.", "그것이 사실이 아니라는 것은 곧 증명해 보이겠습니다만, 그것은 적어도 그 발상이 카라마조프에게 낯익은 것이었음을, 그가 그것을 마음속에 그려 본 적이 있음을 보여 줍니다."], ["What’s more, when he declared at the inquiry that he had put fifteen hundred roubles in a bag (which never existed) he may have invented that little bag on the inspiration of the moment, because he had two hours before divided his money and hidden half of it at Mokroe till morning, in case of emergency, simply not to have it on himself.", "뿐만 아니라 그가 예심에서 천오백 루블을 (실재하지도 않은) 주머니에 넣어 두었노라고 진술했을 때, 그는 어쩌면 그 자리에서 영감처럼 떠오른 대로 그 작은 주머니를 지어낸 것인지도 모릅니다. 그는 두 시간 전에 자기 돈을 나누어 그 절반을 만일에 대비하여 아침까지 모크로예에 감추어 두었으니, 그저 그것을 몸에 지니고 있지 않으려 했던 것입니다."], ["Two extremes, gentlemen of the jury, remember that Karamazov can contemplate two extremes and both at once.", "두 극단, 배심원 여러분, 카라마조프는 두 극단을, 그것도 동시에 둘 다 마음에 품을 수 있는 인간임을 기억해 두십시오."]], [["“We have looked in the house, but we haven’t found the money.", "\"우리는 그 집을 뒤졌으나 돈을 찾아내지는 못했습니다."], ["It may still be there or it may have disappeared next day and be in the prisoner’s hands now.", "그것은 아직 거기 있을 수도 있고, 이튿날 사라져 지금 피고의 수중에 있을 수도 있습니다."], ["In any case he was at her side, on his knees before her, she was lying on the bed, he had his hands stretched out to her and he had so entirely forgotten everything that he did not even hear the men coming to arrest him.", "어쨌든 그는 그녀 곁에서 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었고, 그녀는 침대에 누워 있었으며, 그는 그녀에게 두 손을 뻗은 채 모든 것을 어찌나 까맣게 잊고 있었던지 자기를 체포하러 오는 사람들의 기척조차 듣지 못했습니다."], ["He hadn’t time to prepare any line of defense in his mind.", "그는 마음속에 그 어떤 방어선도 미처 마련해 두지 못했습니다."], ["He was caught unawares and confronted with his judges, the arbiters of his destiny.", "그는 불시에 붙들려 자신의 심판관들, 곧 자기 운명의 결정자들과 맞닥뜨리게 되었습니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, there are moments in the execution of our duties when it is terrible for us to face a man, terrible on his account, too!", "\"배심원 여러분, 우리가 직무를 수행하는 가운데 한 인간과 마주하는 것이 끔찍스럽게 느껴지는 순간들이 있으니, 그 인간을 생각해 보아도 끔찍한 일입니다!"], ["The moments of contemplating that animal fear, when the criminal sees that all is lost, but still struggles, still means to struggle, the moments when every instinct of self-preservation rises up in him at once and he looks at you with questioning and suffering eyes, studies you, your face, your thoughts, uncertain on which side you will strike, and his distracted mind frames thousands of plans in an instant, but he is still afraid to speak, afraid of giving himself away!", "죄인이 모든 것이 끝장났음을 보면서도 여전히 발버둥 치고, 여전히 발버둥 치려 들 때의 그 짐승 같은 공포를 지켜보는 순간들, 자기 보존의 본능이란 본능이 그의 안에서 한꺼번에 곤두서서, 그가 묻는 듯하고 고통에 찬 눈으로 여러분을 바라보며, 여러분을, 여러분의 얼굴을, 여러분의 생각을 살피고, 여러분이 어느 쪽에서 내리칠지 가늠하지 못한 채, 그 어지러운 머릿속으로 순식간에 수천 가지 계획을 짜내면서도, 여전히 입을 떼기를 두려워하고, 제 속을 드러낼까 두려워하는 그 순간들 말입니다!"], ["This purgatory of the spirit, this animal thirst for self-preservation, these humiliating moments of the human soul, are awful, and sometimes arouse horror and compassion for the criminal even in the lawyer.", "이 영혼의 연옥, 이 짐승 같은 자기 보존의 갈증, 인간 영혼의 이 굴욕적인 순간들은 무시무시한 것이며, 때로는 변호사에게마저 죄인에 대한 공포와 연민을 불러일으킵니다."], ["And this was what we all witnessed then.", "그리고 그것이 바로 그때 우리 모두가 목격한 것이었습니다."]], [["“At first he was thunderstruck and in his terror dropped some very compromising phrases.", "\"처음에 그는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯했고, 두려움에 사로잡혀 몹시 불리한 말들을 몇 마디 흘렸습니다."], ["‘Blood!", "'피다!"], ["I’ve deserved it!’ But he quickly restrained himself.", "나는 그것을 받아 마땅하다!' 그러나 그는 이내 자제했습니다."], ["He had not prepared what he was to say, what answer he was to make, he had nothing but a bare denial ready.", "그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 어떤 대답을 내놓아야 할지 미리 준비해 두지 못했으니, 그에게 준비된 것이라곤 노골적인 부인뿐이었습니다."], ["‘I am not guilty of my father’s death.’ That was his fence for the moment and behind it he hoped to throw up a barricade of some sort.", "'나는 아버지의 죽음에 죄가 없다.' 그것이 당장의 그의 방패막이였고, 그 뒤에서 그는 어떻게든 방벽을 쌓아 올리기를 바랐습니다."], ["His first compromising exclamations he hastened to explain by declaring that he was responsible for the death of the servant Grigory only.", "그는 자신이 처음 흘린 그 불리한 외침들을, 하인 그리고리의 죽음에 대해서만 책임이 있을 뿐이라고 선언함으로써 서둘러 둘러댔습니다."], ["‘Of that bloodshed I am guilty, but who has killed my father, gentlemen, who has killed him?", "'그 피흘림에 대해서는 내가 죄가 있으나, 그렇다면 누가 내 아버지를 죽였단 말입니까, 여러분, 누가 그를 죽였단 말입니까?"], ["Who can have killed him, if not I?’ Do you hear, he asked us that, us, who had come to ask him that question!", "내가 아니라면 대체 누가 그를 죽일 수 있었겠습니까?' 들으셨습니까, 그는 우리에게 그것을 물었습니다, 바로 그것을 그에게 물으러 온 우리에게 말입니다!"], ["Do you hear that phrase uttered with such premature haste—‘if not I’—the animal cunning, the naïveté, the Karamazov impatience of it?", "그토록 성급하게 앞질러 튀어나온 그 말, '내가 아니라면'이라는 그 말, 그 속에 깃든 짐승 같은 교활함, 그 순진함, 그 카라마조프 특유의 조바심을 들으셨습니까? '나는 그를 죽이지 않았소, 그러니 내가 죽였다고 여겨서는 안 되오!"], ["‘I didn’t kill him and you mustn’t think I did!", "나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소, 여러분, 나는 그를 죽이고 싶었소,'"], ["I wanted to kill him, gentlemen, I wanted to kill him,’ he hastens to admit (he was in a hurry, in a terrible hurry), ‘but still I am not guilty, it is not I murdered him.’ He concedes to us that he wanted to murder him, as though to say, you can see for yourselves how truthful I am, so you’ll believe all the sooner that I didn’t murder him.", "그는 서둘러 시인합니다 (그는 서둘렀으니, 끔찍이도 서둘렀습니다), '그러나 그래도 나는 죄가 없소, 그를 죽인 것은 내가 아니오.' 그는 자기가 그를 죽이고 싶었다는 것은 우리에게 인정하니, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯합니다."], ["Oh, in such cases the criminal is often amazingly shallow and credulous.", "내가 얼마나 솔직한지 여러분 스스로 보시오, 그러니 내가 그를 죽이지 않았다는 것도 그만큼 더 빨리 믿어 주시오. 오, 이런 경우에 죄인이란 흔히 놀라우리만치 얕고 잘 속아 넘어가는 법입니다."]], [["“At that point one of the lawyers asked him, as it were incidentally, the most simple question, ‘Wasn’t it Smerdyakov killed him?’ Then, as we expected, he was horribly angry at our having anticipated him and caught him unawares, before he had time to pave the way to choose and snatch the moment when it would be most natural to bring in Smerdyakov’s name.", "\"바로 그 대목에서 우리 측 한 사람이 그에게, 마치 지나가는 말처럼, 더없이 단순한 질문을 던졌습니다. '스메르댜코프가 죽인 것은 아니오?'"], ["He rushed at once to the other extreme, as he always does, and began to assure us that Smerdyakov could not have killed him, was not capable of it.", "그러자 우리가 예상한 대로 그는 우리가 자기를 앞질러 불시에 붙든 데 대해 무섭게 화를 냈으니, 그로서는 미처 발판을 마련해 스메르댜코프의 이름을 끌어들이기에 가장 자연스러운 순간을 골라 낚아챌 겨를이 없었던 까닭입니다. 그는 늘 그렇듯이 곧장 반대편 극단으로 치달아, 스메르댜코프는 그를 죽일 수 없었고, 그럴 만한 위인이 못 된다고 우리에게 장담하기 시작했습니다."], ["But don’t believe him, that was only his cunning; he didn’t really give up the idea of Smerdyakov; on the contrary, he meant to bring him forward again; for, indeed, he had no one else to bring forward, but he would do that later, because for the moment that line was spoiled for him.", "그러나 그를 믿어서는 안 됩니다, 그것은 한낱 그의 교활함이었을 뿐입니다. 그는 실은 스메르댜코프라는 발상을 단념한 것이 아니었으니, 도리어 그를 다시 끌어들일 작정이었던 것입니다."], ["He would bring him forward perhaps next day, or even a few days later, choosing an opportunity to cry out to us, ‘You know I was more skeptical about Smerdyakov than you, you remember that yourselves, but now I am convinced.", "사실 그가 끌어들일 수 있는 사람이라곤 달리 없었으나, 다만 그것을 나중으로 미루었을 뿐이니, 당장은 그 노선이 그에게는 망가져 버린 까닭이었습니다. 그는 어쩌면 이튿날, 또는 며칠 뒤에 기회를 보아 그를 끌어들여 우리에게 이렇게 외쳤을 것입니다."], ["He killed him, he must have done!’ And for the present he falls back upon a gloomy and irritable denial.", "'여러분도 알다시피 나는 스메르댜코프에 대해 여러분보다 더 회의적이었소, 그 점은 여러분 스스로 기억할 것이오, 그러나 이제 나는 확신하오. 그가 죽인 것이오, 그가 죽인 것이 틀림없소!'"], ["Impatience and anger prompted him, however, to the most inept and incredible explanation of how he looked into his father’s window and how he respectfully withdrew.", "그리고 당장은 음울하고 신경질적인 부인으로 물러나 버립니다. 그러나 조바심과 분노에 떠밀려 그는, 자기가 어떻게 아버지의 창문을 들여다보았고 또 어떻게 공손하게 물러났는지에 대해 더없이 서툴고 믿을 수 없는 설명을 늘어놓았습니다."], ["The worst of it was that he was unaware of the position of affairs, of the evidence given by Grigory.", "무엇보다 고약한 것은, 그가 사태의 형편을, 그리고리가 진술한 증거를 까맣게 모르고 있었다는 점입니다."]], [["“We proceeded to search him.", "\"우리는 이어서 그의 몸을 수색했습니다."], ["The search angered, but encouraged him, the whole three thousand had not been found on him, only half of it.", "그 수색은 그를 화나게 했으나 한편으로는 그를 고무했으니, 삼천 루블이 통째로 그의 몸에서 나오지 않고 그 절반만 나왔기 때문입니다."], ["And no doubt only at that moment of angry silence, the fiction of the little bag first occurred to him.", "그리고 분명 화가 나서 입을 다물고 있던 바로 그 순간에 그 작은 주머니라는 허구가 처음으로 그에게 떠올랐던 것입니다."], ["No doubt he was conscious himself of the improbability of the story and strove painfully to make it sound more likely, to weave it into a romance that would sound plausible.", "분명 그 자신도 그 이야기가 그럴듯하지 않다는 것을 의식하고 있었으며, 그것을 좀 더 그럴싸하게 들리도록, 제법 그럴듯한 로맨스로 엮어 내려고 안간힘을 다해 애썼습니다."], ["In such cases the first duty, the chief task of the investigating lawyers, is to prevent the criminal being prepared, to pounce upon him unexpectedly so that he may blurt out his cherished ideas in all their simplicity, improbability and inconsistency.", "이런 경우 수사를 맡은 자들의 첫째 임무, 으뜸가는 과업은 죄인이 미리 채비를 갖추지 못하게 막는 것, 불시에 덮쳐서 그가 제 속에 품은 생각들을 그 온갖 단순함과 허무맹랑함과 모순 그대로 불쑥 내뱉게 하는 것입니다."], ["The criminal can only be made to speak by the sudden and apparently incidental communication of some new fact, of some circumstance of great importance in the case, of which he had no previous idea and could not have foreseen.", "죄인의 입을 열게 하려면, 그가 미처 알지 못했고 예상할 수도 없었던 어떤 새로운 사실, 사건에 매우 중대한 어떤 정황을 별안간, 그러면서도 짐짓 지나가는 말처럼 알려 주는 수밖에 없습니다."], ["We had such a fact in readiness—that was Grigory’s evidence about the open door through which the prisoner had run out.", "우리에게는 그런 사실이 마련되어 있었으니—그것은 피고가 빠져나간 그 열린 문에 관한 그리고리의 증언이었습니다."], ["He had completely forgotten about that door and had not even suspected that Grigory could have seen it.", "그는 그 문에 대해 까맣게 잊고 있었고, 그리고리가 그것을 볼 수 있었으리라고는 의심조차 하지 못했습니다."]], [["“The effect of it was amazing.", "\"그 효과는 놀라웠습니다. 그는 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 우리에게 소리쳤습니다."], ["He leapt up and shouted to us, ‘Then Smerdyakov murdered him, it was Smerdyakov!’ and so betrayed the basis of the defense he was keeping back, and betrayed it in its most improbable shape, for Smerdyakov could only have committed the murder after he had knocked Grigory down and run away.", "'그렇다면 스메르댜코프가 그를 죽인 것이오, 스메르댜코프였소!' 그리하여 그는 감추어 두었던 변호의 토대를 스스로 드러내고 말았으니, 그것도 가장 그럴듯하지 못한 모양새로 드러낸 것입니다."], ["When we told him that Grigory saw the door was open before he fell down, and had heard Smerdyakov behind the screen as he came out of his bedroom—Karamazov was positively crushed.", "스메르댜코프가 살인을 저지를 수 있었다면 그것은 오직 그가 그리고리를 쓰러뜨리고 달아난 뒤라야 했기 때문입니다. 우리가 그에게, 그리고리는 쓰러지기 전에 문이 열려 있는 것을 보았고, 자기 침실에서 나오던 길에 칸막이 뒤에서 스메르댜코프의 기척을 들었다고 말해 주자—카라마조프는 그야말로 무너지고 말았습니다."], ["My esteemed and witty colleague, Nikolay Parfenovitch, told me afterwards that he was almost moved to tears at the sight of him.", "나의 존경하는 재치 있는 동료 니콜라이 파르페노비치는 나중에 내게, 그 모습을 보고 거의 눈물이 날 뻔했노라고 털어놓았습니다."], ["And to improve matters, the prisoner hastened to tell us about the much-talked-of little bag—so be it, you shall hear this romance!", "게다가 사태를 더 그르치려는 듯, 피고는 서둘러 우리에게 그 말 많던 작은 주머니 이야기를 늘어놓았습니다—좋습니다, 그렇다면 여러분도 이 로맨스를 들어 보시지요!"]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, I have told you already why I consider this romance not only an absurdity, but the most improbable invention that could have been brought forward in the circumstances.", "\"배심원 여러분, 나는 이 로맨스를 어째서 한낱 터무니없는 소리일 뿐 아니라 그 정황에서 끌어댈 수 있는 가장 그럴듯하지 못한 지어낸 이야기로 여기는지, 이미 여러분께 말씀드렸습니다. 누군가가 내기 삼아 가장 있을 법하지 않은 이야기를 지어내려 애쓴다 해도, 이보다 더 믿기 어려운 것은 좀처럼 찾아내지 못할 것입니다."], ["If one tried for a bet to invent the most unlikely story, one could hardly find anything more incredible.", "이런 이야기들의 가장 고약한 점은, 의기양양한 그 이야기꾼들이 언제나, 실제 삶이 그토록 풍부하게 갖추고 있으나 이 가련하고 본의 아닌 이야기꾼들이 하찮은 사소한 일이라고 소홀히 여기는 바로 그 세부에 걸려 혼쭐이 나고 무너지고 만다는 데 있습니다."], ["The worst of such stories is that the triumphant romancers can always be put to confusion and crushed by the very details in which real life is so rich and which these unhappy and involuntary story-tellers neglect as insignificant trifles.", "오, 그들에게는 그런 세부에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없으니, 그들의 마음은 자신의 거창한 지어낸 이야기 전체에만 쏠려 있는지라, 어느 누가 감히 사소한 일로 자기들의 말허리를 자른단 말인가 하는 것입니다! 그러나 그들이 덜미를 잡히는 것은 바로 그 때문입니다."], ["Oh, they have no thought to spare for such details, their minds are concentrated on their grand invention as a whole, and fancy any one daring to pull them up for a trifle!", "피고는 이런 질문을 받았습니다. '그 작은 주머니의 천은 어디서 났으며, 누가 만들어 주었소?'"], ["But that’s how they are caught.", "'내가 직접 만들었소.' '그렇다면 그 천은 어디서 났소?'"], ["The prisoner was asked the question, ‘Where did you get the stuff for your little bag and who made it for you?’ ‘I made it myself.’ ‘And where did you get the linen?’ The prisoner was positively offended, he thought it almost insulting to ask him such a trivial question, and would you believe it, his resentment was genuine!", "피고는 대놓고 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 기색이었으니, 그토록 하찮은 질문을 던지는 것을 거의 무례한 짓으로 여겼고, 여러분은 믿으시겠습니까, 그 분개는 진심이었습니다! 그러나 그들은 다들 그렇습니다."], ["But they are all like that.", "'셔츠에서 찢어 냈소.'"], ["‘I tore it off my shirt.’ ‘Then we shall find that shirt among your linen to-morrow, with a piece torn off.’ And only fancy, gentlemen of the jury, if we really had found that torn shirt (and how could we have failed to find it in his chest of drawers or trunk?) that would have been a fact, a material fact in support of his statement!", "'그렇다면 내일 당신의 빨랫감 가운데서 한 조각이 찢겨 나간 그 셔츠를 찾아낼 수 있겠군요.' 그리고 한번 상상해 보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 만일 우리가 정말로 그 찢긴 셔츠를 찾아냈다면 (그리고 그의 서랍장이나 트렁크에서 그것을 어찌 찾아내지 못할 수 있었겠습니까?) 그것은 하나의 사실, 그의 진술을 뒷받침하는 물증이 되었을 것입니다!"], ["But he was incapable of that reflection.", "그러나 그는 거기까지 헤아릴 줄을 몰랐습니다. '기억나지 않소, 셔츠에서 떼어 낸 게 아니었는지도 모르오, 나는 그것을 내 하숙집 안주인의 두건 하나에 꿰매어 넣었소.'"], ["‘I don’t remember, it may not have been off my shirt, I sewed it up in one of my landlady’s caps.’ ‘What sort of a cap?’ ‘It was an old cotton rag of hers lying about.’ ‘And do you remember that clearly?’ ‘No, I don’t.’ And he was angry, very angry, and yet imagine not remembering it!", "'어떤 두건이오?' '그 여자가 아무렇게나 굴러다니게 둔 낡은 무명 헝겊이었소.'"], ["At the most terrible moments of man’s life, for instance when he is being led to execution, he remembers just such trifles.", "'그것을 똑똑히 기억하오?' '아니, 기억나지 않소.'"], ["He will forget anything but some green roof that has flashed past him on the road, or a jackdaw on a cross—that he will remember.", "그러면서도 그는 화를 냈습니다, 몹시 화를 냈습니다, 그런데도 한번 상상해 보십시오, 그것이 기억나지 않는다니요! 인간의 삶에서 가장 끔찍한 순간들에, 이를테면 처형장으로 끌려갈 때조차 사람은 바로 그런 사소한 것들을 기억하는 법입니다."], ["He concealed the making of that little bag from his household, he must have remembered his humiliating fear that some one might come in and find him needle in hand, how at the slightest sound he slipped behind the screen (there is a screen in his lodgings).", "그는 무엇이든 다 잊어버려도, 길가에 휙 스쳐 지나간 어떤 초록 지붕이나, 십자가 위에 앉은 갈까마귀 한 마리만은—그것만은 기억할 것입니다. 그는 그 작은 주머니를 만든 일을 식구들에게 감추었으니, 누군가 들어와 바늘을 손에 쥔 자기를 보지나 않을까 하던 그 굴욕적인 두려움을, 아주 작은 소리에도 칸막이 뒤로 슬쩍 몸을 숨기던 일을(그의 하숙방에는 칸막이가 있습니다) 분명 기억하고 있었을 것입니다."]], [["“But, gentlemen of the jury, why do I tell you all this, all these details, trifles?”", "\"그러나 배심원 여러분, 내가 어찌하여 여러분께 이 모든 것을, 이 모든 세부와 사소한 일들을 늘어놓는 것이겠습니까?\""], ["cried Ippolit Kirillovitch suddenly.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치가 별안간 소리쳤다."], ["“Just because the prisoner still persists in these absurdities to this moment.", "\"그것은 다름 아니라 피고가 이 순간까지도 여전히 이런 터무니없는 소리들을 고집하고 있기 때문입니다."], ["He has not explained anything since that fatal night two months ago, he has not added one actual illuminating fact to his former fantastic statements; all those are trivialities.", "두 달 전 그 숙명적인 밤 이래로 그는 아무것도 설명하지 못했으며, 자신의 예전 환상적인 진술에 단 하나의 실질적이고 환히 밝혀 주는 사실도 보태지 못했습니다. 그 모든 것은 부질없는 군말일 뿐입니다."], ["‘You must believe it on my honor.’ Oh, we are glad to believe it, we are eager to believe it, even if only on his word of honor!", "'내 명예를 걸고 그것을 믿어 주셔야 합니다.' 오, 우리는 기꺼이 믿고 싶습니다, 우리는 그것을 믿고 싶어 안달입니다, 설령 그의 명예를 건 말 하나에 지나지 않더라도 말입니다!"], ["Are we jackals thirsting for human blood?", "우리가 인간의 피에 목말라하는 승냥이라도 된단 말입니까?"], ["Show us a single fact in the prisoner’s favor and we shall rejoice; but let it be a substantial, real fact, and not a conclusion drawn from the prisoner’s expression by his own brother, or that when he beat himself on the breast he must have meant to point to the little bag, in the darkness, too.", "피고에게 유리한 사실 단 하나라도 우리에게 내보여 주십시오, 그러면 우리는 기뻐할 것입니다. 그러나 그것은 실질적이고 진정한 사실이어야 하며, 피고의 표정에서 그의 친동생이 끌어낸 추측이라든가, 그가 제 가슴을 쳤을 때 그것은 분명, 그것도 어둠 속에서, 그 작은 주머니를 가리키려는 뜻이었으리라는 따위여서는 안 됩니다."], ["We shall rejoice at the new fact, we shall be the first to repudiate our charge, we shall hasten to repudiate it.", "우리는 그 새로운 사실에 기뻐할 것이며, 우리야말로 맨 먼저 우리의 기소를 철회할 것이고, 서둘러 그것을 철회할 것입니다."], ["But now justice cries out and we persist, we cannot repudiate anything.”", "그러나 지금은 정의가 부르짖고 있으니, 우리는 고집할 수밖에 없으며, 그 무엇도 철회할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["Ippolit Kirillovitch passed to his final peroration.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 마지막 결론으로 넘어갔다."], ["He looked as though he was in a fever, he spoke of the blood that cried for vengeance, the blood of the father murdered by his son, with the base motive of robbery!", "그는 열병에라도 걸린 듯한 모습이었고, 복수를 부르짖는 피에 대해, 비천한 강도질의 동기로 아들의 손에 살해당한 아비의 피에 대해 이야기했다!"], ["He pointed to the tragic and glaring consistency of the facts.", "그는 사실들이 빚어내는 비극적이고도 명백한 일관성을 가리켜 보였다."]], [["“And whatever you may hear from the talented and celebrated counsel for the defense,”", "\"그리고 여러분이 저 재능 있고 명망 높은 변호인에게서 어떤 말을 듣더라도,\""], ["Ippolit Kirillovitch could not resist adding, “whatever eloquent and touching appeals may be made to your sensibilities, remember that at this moment you are in a temple of justice.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다. \"여러분의 감수성에 호소하는 그 어떤 능변의 절절한 호소가 펼쳐지더라도, 여러분은 지금 이 순간 정의의 전당에 계시다는 것을 기억하십시오."], ["Remember that you are the champions of our justice, the champions of our holy Russia, of her principles, her family, everything that she holds sacred!", "여러분은 우리의 정의를 지키는 투사이며, 우리의 거룩한 러시아를, 그 원칙을, 그 가정을, 러시아가 신성히 여기는 그 모든 것을 지키는 투사임을 기억하십시오!"], ["Yes, you represent Russia here at this moment, and your verdict will be heard not in this hall only but will reëcho throughout the whole of Russia, and all Russia will hear you, as her champions and her judges, and she will be encouraged or disheartened by your verdict.", "그렇습니다, 여러분은 지금 이 순간 이 자리에서 러시아를 대표하는 것이며, 여러분의 평결은 이 법정 안에서만 들리는 것이 아니라 온 러시아에 메아리쳐 울려 퍼질 것이고, 온 러시아가 여러분을, 자신을 지키는 투사이자 심판관인 여러분의 말을 들을 것이며, 여러분의 평결에 따라 러시아는 힘을 얻기도 하고 낙담하기도 할 것입니다."], ["Do not disappoint Russia and her expectations.", "러시아와 그 기대를 저버리지 마십시오."], ["Our fatal troika dashes on in her headlong flight perhaps to destruction and in all Russia for long past men have stretched out imploring hands and called a halt to its furious reckless course.", "우리의 숙명적인 트로이카는 어쩌면 파멸을 향하여 무서운 기세로 내달리고 있으며, 온 러시아에서는 이미 오래전부터 사람들이 애원하듯 손을 내밀어 그 광포하고 무모한 질주를 멈추라고 외쳐 왔습니다."], ["And if other nations stand aside from that troika that may be, not from respect, as the poet would fain believe, but simply from horror.", "그리고 만일 다른 나라들이 그 트로이카에서 비켜선다면, 그것은 어느 시인이 기꺼이 믿고 싶어 하듯 존경에서 비켜서는 것이 아니라, 그저 공포에서 비켜서는 것일지도 모릅니다."], ["From horror, perhaps from disgust.", "공포에서, 어쩌면 혐오에서 말입니다."], ["And well it is that they stand aside, but maybe they will cease one day to do so and will form a firm wall confronting the hurrying apparition and will check the frenzied rush of our lawlessness, for the sake of their own safety, enlightenment and civilization.", "그들이 비켜서 주는 것만으로도 다행이나, 어쩌면 그들도 언젠가는 비켜서기를 그치고, 그 내달리는 환영 앞에 굳건한 벽을 이루어 맞서서, 자신들의 안전과 계몽과 문명을 위하여 우리의 무법한 질주를 가로막아 버릴지도 모릅니다."], ["Already we have heard voices of alarm from Europe, they already begin to sound.", "우리는 이미 유럽에서 경종을 울리는 목소리들을 들었으며, 그것은 이미 울리기 시작했습니다."], ["Do not tempt them!", "그들을 시험하지 마십시오!"], ["Do not heap up their growing hatred by a sentence justifying the murder of a father by his son!”", "아들이 아비를 살해한 일을 정당화하는 판결로써 그들의 자라나는 증오를 더 쌓아 올리지 마십시오!\""]], [["Though Ippolit Kirillovitch was genuinely moved, he wound up his speech with this rhetorical appeal—and the effect produced by him was extraordinary.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치는 진심으로 격앙되어 있었으나, 이런 수사적인 호소로 연설을 끝맺었다—그리고 그가 빚어낸 효과는 자못 비상한 것이었다."], ["When he had finished his speech, he went out hurriedly and, as I have mentioned before, almost fainted in the adjoining room.", "연설을 마치자 그는 황급히 밖으로 나갔고, 앞서 말한 대로 옆방에서 거의 졸도할 뻔했다."], ["There was no applause in the court, but serious persons were pleased.", "법정 안에 박수는 없었으나, 진중한 사람들은 흡족해했다."], ["The ladies were not so well satisfied, though even they were pleased with his eloquence, especially as they had no apprehensions as to the upshot of the trial and had full trust in Fetyukovitch.", "부인들은 그만큼 만족스러워하지는 않았으되, 그래도 그 능변에는 그들조차 기뻐했으니, 더구나 그들은 재판의 결말에 대해 아무런 걱정이 없었고 페튜코비치를 온전히 믿고 있던 까닭이었다."], ["“He will speak at last and of course carry all before him.”", "\"마침내 그가 입을 열 것이고, 당연히 모든 것을 휩쓸어 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["Every one looked at Mitya; he sat silent through the whole of the prosecutor’s speech, clenching his teeth, with his hands clasped, and his head bowed.", "모두가 미탸를 쳐다보았다. 그는 검사의 연설이 이어지는 내내 이를 악물고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 고개를 숙인 채 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["Only from time to time he raised his head and listened, especially when Grushenka was spoken of.", "다만 이따금 고개를 들고 귀를 기울였는데, 특히 그루셴카에 대한 이야기가 나올 때가 그러했다."], ["When the prosecutor mentioned Rakitin’s opinion of her, a smile of contempt and anger passed over his face and he murmured rather audibly, “The Bernards!”", "검사가 그녀에 대한 라키틴의 견해를 입에 올리자, 경멸과 분노가 어린 미소가 그의 얼굴을 스쳤고, 그는 제법 또렷이 들리도록 중얼거렸다. \"베르나르 같은 작자들이로군!\""], ["When Ippolit Kirillovitch described how he had questioned and tortured him at Mokroe, Mitya raised his head and listened with intense curiosity.", "이폴리트 키릴로비치가 자신이 모크로예에서 그를 어떻게 심문하고 다그쳤는지를 묘사할 때는, 미탸가 고개를 들고 강렬한 호기심에 차서 귀를 기울였다."], ["At one point he seemed about to jump up and cry out, but controlled himself and only shrugged his shoulders disdainfully.", "한 대목에서는 그가 벌떡 일어나 소리라도 지를 듯했으나, 스스로를 억누르고 그저 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐이다."], ["People talked afterwards of the end of the speech, of the prosecutor’s feat in examining the prisoner at Mokroe, and jeered at Ippolit Kirillovitch.", "사람들은 나중에 그 연설의 끝부분에 대해, 검사가 모크로예에서 피고를 심문한 그 공적에 대해 이야기하며 이폴리트 키릴로비치를 비웃었다."], ["“The man could not resist boasting of his cleverness,”", "\"그 사람은 제 영리함을 자랑하지 않고는 못 배기는 작자야.\""], ["they said.", "그들은 이렇게 말했다."]], [["The court was adjourned, but only for a short interval, a quarter of an hour or twenty minutes at most.", "법정은 휴정에 들어갔으나, 잠깐 동안일 뿐이었으니, 길어야 십오 분이나 이십 분이었다."], ["There was a hum of conversation and exclamations in the audience.", "방청석에서는 대화와 탄성이 웅성거렸다."], ["I remember some of them.", "그 가운데 몇몇이 기억난다."]], [["“A weighty speech,”", "\"묵직한 연설이었어.\""], ["a gentleman in one group observed gravely.", "한 무리에 끼어 있던 신사가 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“He brought in too much psychology,”", "\"심리 분석을 너무 많이 끌어들였어.\""], ["said another voice.", "또 다른 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“But it was all true, the absolute truth!”", "\"하지만 죄다 사실이었지, 한 치도 틀림없는 사실 말이오!\""]], [["“Yes, he is first rate at it.”", "\"그래, 그 사람은 그런 데는 일류야.\""]], [["“He summed it all up.”", "\"그 사람이 모든 걸 정리해 버렸어.\""]], [["“Yes, he summed us up, too,”", "\"그래, 우리까지 싸잡아서 정리해 버렸지.\""], ["chimed in another voice.", "또 다른 목소리가 끼어들었다."], ["“Do you remember, at the beginning of his speech, making out we were all like Fyodor Pavlovitch?”", "\"연설 첫머리에서 우리가 죄다 표도르 파블로비치 같은 인간이라고 몰아붙인 거 기억나오?\""]], [["“And at the end, too.", "\"끝에서도 그랬지."], ["But that was all rot.”", "하지만 그건 죄다 허튼소리였어.\""]], [["“And obscure too.”", "\"게다가 알아먹기도 힘들었고.\""]], [["“He was a little too much carried away.”", "\"좀 지나치게 흥분했더군.\""]], [["“It’s unjust, it’s unjust.”", "\"부당해, 부당하다고.\""]], [["“No, it was smartly done, anyway.", "\"아니, 어쨌든 솜씨는 영리하게 부렸지."], ["He’s had long to wait, but he’s had his say, ha ha!”", "오래도록 기다려 온 끝에 드디어 제 할 말을 한 거야, 하하!\""]], [["“What will the counsel for the defense say?”", "\"변호인은 뭐라고 할까?\""]], [["In another group I heard:", "또 다른 무리에서는 이런 말이 들렸다."]], [["“He had no business to make a thrust at the Petersburg man like that; ‘appealing to your sensibilities’—do you remember?”", "\"페테르부르크에서 온 그 사람을 그런 식으로 찌를 까닭은 없었는데, '여러분의 감수성에 호소하는'이라니—기억나오?\""]], [["“Yes, that was awkward of him.”", "\"그래, 그건 그 사람답지 않게 어설펐어.\""]], [["“He was in too great a hurry.”", "\"너무 서둘렀던 거야.\""]], [["“He is a nervous man.”", "\"신경질적인 위인이니까.\""]], [["“We laugh, but what must the prisoner be feeling?”", "\"우리야 웃지만, 정작 피고는 어떤 심정일까?\""]], [["“Yes, what must it be for Mitya?”", "\"그래, 미탸로서는 어떤 심정이겠나?\""]], [["In a third group:", "세 번째 무리에서는."]], [["“What lady is that, the fat one, with the lorgnette, sitting at the end?”", "\"저 부인은 누구요, 저 끝에 앉은, 손잡이 안경을 든 뚱뚱한 여자 말이오?\""]], [["“She is a general’s wife, divorced, I know her.”", "\"장군 부인인데, 이혼했지, 내가 아는 여자야.\""]], [["“That’s why she has the lorgnette.”", "\"그래서 손잡이 안경을 들고 있군.\""]], [["“She is not good for much.”", "\"별 볼 일 없는 여자야.\""]], [["“Oh, no, she is a piquante little woman.”", "\"오, 천만에, 톡 쏘는 데가 있는 앙증맞은 여자라네.\""]], [["“Two places beyond her there is a little fair woman, she is prettier.”", "\"그 여자 두 자리 너머에 자그마한 금발 여자가 있는데, 그쪽이 더 예쁘오.\""]], [["“They caught him smartly at Mokroe, didn’t they, eh?”", "\"모크로예에서 그 사람을 솜씨 좋게 옭아맸지, 안 그렇소, 응?\""]], [["“Oh, it was smart enough.", "\"오, 솜씨는 제법이었지."], ["We’ve heard it before, how often he has told the story at people’s houses!”", "하지만 전에도 들은 얘기야, 그 사람이 남의 집을 돌며 그 이야기를 얼마나 자주 늘어놓았는데!\""]], [["“And he couldn’t resist doing it now.", "\"그러니 지금도 그 짓을 하지 않고는 못 배긴 거지."], ["That’s vanity.”", "그게 다 허영이야.\""]], [["“He is a man with a grievance, he he!”", "\"불만이 잔뜩 쌓인 위인이라니까, 헤헤!\""]], [["“Yes, and quick to take offense.", "\"그래, 게다가 걸핏하면 발끈하지."], ["And there was too much rhetoric, such long sentences.”", "그리고 수사가 너무 많았어, 그런 긴긴 문장들 말이야.\""]], [["“Yes, he tries to alarm us, he kept trying to alarm us.", "\"그래, 그 사람은 우리를 겁주려 들었어, 줄곧 우리를 겁주려고 했지."], ["Do you remember about the troika?", "트로이카 얘기 기억나오?"], ["Something about ‘They have Hamlets, but we have, so far, only Karamazovs!’ That was cleverly said!”", "'그들에게는 햄릿이 있으나, 우리에게는 아직 카라마조프밖에 없다'느니 하던 거 말이오! 그건 영리하게 말한 거였어!\""]], [["“That was to propitiate the liberals.", "\"그건 자유주의자들 비위를 맞추려는 거였지."], ["He is afraid of them.”", "그 사람은 그자들을 두려워하니까.\""]], [["“Yes, and he is afraid of the lawyer, too.”", "\"그래, 게다가 변호사도 두려워하고 있어.\""]], [["“Yes, what will Fetyukovitch say?”", "\"그래, 페튜코비치는 뭐라고 할까?\""]], [["“Whatever he says, he won’t get round our peasants.”", "\"무슨 말을 하든, 우리 농부들 마음을 돌려놓지는 못할걸.\""]], [["“Don’t you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하시오?\""]], [["A fourth group:", "네 번째 무리에서는."]], [["“What he said about the troika was good, that piece about the other nations.”", "\"트로이카에 대해 한 말은 좋았어, 다른 나라들에 관한 그 대목 말이야.\""]], [["“And that was true what he said about other nations not standing it.”", "\"다른 나라들이 그걸 가만 두고 보지 않으리라고 한 것도 사실이었지.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소리요?\""]], [["“Why, in the English Parliament a Member got up last week and speaking about the Nihilists asked the Ministry whether it was not high time to intervene, to educate this barbarous people.", "\"아니, 지난주에 영국 의회에서 한 의원이 일어나 허무주의자들에 대해 말하면서, 이 야만스러운 민족을 교화하기 위해 이제 끼어들 때가 되지 않았느냐고 정부에 물었다지 않소."], ["Ippolit was thinking of him, I know he was.", "이폴리트는 바로 그 사람을 염두에 두고 있었던 거야, 분명히 그래."], ["He was talking about that last week.”", "지난주에 그 얘기를 하고 있었거든.\""]], [["“Not an easy job.”", "\"쉬운 일은 아니지.\""]], [["“Not an easy job?", "\"쉬운 일이 아니라니?"], ["Why not?”", "어째서요?\""]], [["“Why, we’d shut up Kronstadt and not let them have any corn.", "\"아니, 우리가 크론슈타트를 봉쇄해서 곡물을 한 톨도 못 넘기게 하면 되지."], ["Where would they get it?”", "저들이 그걸 어디서 구하겠소?\""]], [["“In America.", "\"미국에서지."], ["They get it from America now.”", "요즘은 미국에서 들여오니까.\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리!\""]], [["But the bell rang, all rushed to their places.", "그러나 종이 울리자 모두가 제자리로 우르르 몰려갔다. 페튜코비치가 연단에 올랐다."], ["Fetyukovitch mounted the tribune.", "* 트로이카(тройка): 말 세 필이 끄는 러시아의 전통적인 마차나 썰매. 고골의 「죽은 혼」에서 질주하는 러시아를 상징하는 형상으로 그려진 이래, 러시아 자체를 비유하는 표현으로 쓰였다."]], [["# Chapter X. The Speech For The Defense. An Argument That Cuts Both Ways", "# Chapter X. The Speech For The Defense. An Argument That Cuts Both Ways"]], [["All was hushed as the first words of the famous orator rang out.", "이름난 변호인의 첫마디가 울려 퍼지자 장내는 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해졌다."], ["The eyes of the audience were fastened upon him.", "방청객의 시선이 일제히 그에게 못 박혔다."], ["He began very simply and directly, with an air of conviction, but not the slightest trace of conceit.", "그는 아주 단순하고 직접적으로, 확신에 찬 태도로 입을 열었으나 자만하는 기색이라고는 털끝만큼도 없었다."], ["He made no attempt at eloquence, at pathos, or emotional phrases.", "그는 웅변을 부리려 하지도, 비장한 어조나 감상적인 미사여구를 동원하려 하지도 않았다."], ["He was like a man speaking in a circle of intimate and sympathetic friends.", "그는 마치 친밀하고 마음 맞는 벗들의 무리 안에서 이야기를 나누는 사람 같았다."], ["His voice was a fine one, sonorous and sympathetic, and there was something genuine and simple in the very sound of it.", "그의 음성은 훌륭했으니, 낭랑하면서도 호소력이 있었고, 그 울림 자체에 진실하고 소박한 무언가가 깃들어 있었다."], ["But every one realized at once that the speaker might suddenly rise to genuine pathos and “pierce the heart with untold power.”", "그러나 듣는 이들은 곧 깨달았으니, 이 화자가 어느 순간 문득 진정한 비장함으로 솟구쳐 올라 \"이루 말할 수 없는 힘으로 가슴을 꿰뚫을\""], ["His language was perhaps more irregular than Ippolit Kirillovitch’s, but he spoke without long phrases, and indeed, with more precision.", "수도 있으리라는 것을. 그의 화법은 어쩌면 이폴리트 키릴로비치의 것보다 더 들쭉날쭉했으나, 장황한 문구 없이, 도리어 더 정확하게 말했다."], ["One thing did not please the ladies: he kept bending forward, especially at the beginning of his speech, not exactly bowing, but as though he were about to dart at his listeners, bending his long spine in half, as though there were a spring in the middle that enabled him to bend almost at right angles.", "다만 한 가지가 부인들의 마음에 들지 않았으니, 그는 특히 연설 초입에서 자꾸만 앞으로 몸을 굽혔는데, 정확히 절을 하는 것은 아니었으나 마치 청중을 향해 달려들기라도 할 것처럼, 그 긴 등뼈를 반으로 접듯 굽혔으니, 마치 등 한가운데 용수철이라도 박혀 있어 거의 직각으로 꺾일 수 있게 해 주는 것만 같았다."]], [["At the beginning of his speech he spoke rather disconnectedly, without system, one may say, dealing with facts separately, though, at the end, these facts formed a whole.", "연설을 시작할 무렵 그는 다소 두서없이, 말하자면 체계 없이 이야기하며 사실들을 따로따로 다루었으나, 끝에 이르자 이 사실들이 하나의 전체를 이루었다."], ["His speech might be divided into two parts, the first consisting of criticism in refutation of the charge, sometimes malicious and sarcastic.", "그의 연설은 두 부분으로 나눌 수 있었으니, 첫째 부분은 공소 사실을 반박하는 비판으로 이루어져 있었고, 때로는 악의적이고 빈정거리는 데가 있었다."], ["But in the second half he suddenly changed his tone, and even his manner, and at once rose to pathos.", "그러나 후반부에 이르러 그는 돌연 어조를, 심지어 태도까지 바꾸어 단숨에 비장함으로 솟구쳤다."], ["The audience seemed on the look-out for it, and quivered with enthusiasm.", "청중은 마치 그것을 기다리고 있었던 듯, 열광으로 몸을 떨었다."]], [["He went straight to the point, and began by saying that although he practiced in Petersburg, he had more than once visited provincial towns to defend prisoners, of whose innocence he had a conviction or at least a preconceived idea.", "그는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자신이 비록 페테르부르크에서 변호 활동을 하고 있으나 그동안 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례 지방 도시를 찾아 피고를 변호해 왔으며, 그 피고들에 대해서는 무죄를 확신하거나 적어도 그러리라는 예단을 품고 있었다고 운을 떼었다."], ["“That is what has happened to me in the present case,” he explained.", "\"이번 사건에서 제게 일어난 것이 바로 그런 일입니다.\""], ["“From the very first accounts in the newspapers I was struck by something which strongly prepossessed me in the prisoner’s favor.", "그는 이렇게 설명했다. \"신문에 처음 실린 보도들에서부터 저는 어떤 점에 강하게 마음이 끌려 피고에게 호의를 품게 되었습니다."], ["What interested me most was a fact which often occurs in legal practice, but rarely, I think, in such an extreme and peculiar form as in the present case.", "무엇보다 저의 흥미를 끈 것은 법정 실무에서 자주 일어나기는 하나, 제 생각으로는 이번 사건에서처럼 그토록 극단적이고 기이한 형태로 나타나는 일은 드문 어떤 사실이었습니다."], ["I ought to formulate that peculiarity only at the end of my speech, but I will do so at the very beginning, for it is my weakness to go to work directly, not keeping my effects in reserve and economizing my material.", "본래 그 특이점은 제 연설의 맨 끝에 가서야 정리해야 마땅하겠으나, 저는 그것을 맨 처음에 말씀드리겠습니다. 곧장 일에 착수하고 효과를 비축해 두거나 자료를 아껴 두지 못하는 것이 저의 약점이기 때문입니다."], ["That may be imprudent on my part, but at least it’s sincere.", "이는 제 쪽에서 보면 경솔한 일일지 모르나, 적어도 진실한 태도이기는 합니다."], ["What I have in my mind is this: there is an overwhelming chain of evidence against the prisoner, and at the same time not one fact that will stand criticism, if it is examined separately.", "제가 마음에 두고 있는 바는 이것입니다. 피고에게는 압도적인 증거의 사슬이 얽혀 있으나, 동시에 그 어느 사실도 따로 떼어 살펴보면 비판을 견뎌 낼 만한 것이 하나도 없다는 점입니다."], ["As I followed the case more closely in the papers my idea was more and more confirmed, and I suddenly received from the prisoner’s relatives a request to undertake his defense.", "신문을 통해 사건을 더 가까이 좇아갈수록 저의 생각은 점점 더 굳어졌고, 그러던 차에 문득 피고의 친지들로부터 그를 변호해 달라는 청을 받았습니다."], ["I at once hurried here, and here I became completely convinced.", "저는 곧장 이곳으로 달려왔고, 와서 보니 완전히 확신하게 되었습니다."], ["It was to break down this terrible chain of facts, and to show that each piece of evidence taken separately was unproved and fantastic, that I undertook the case.”", "제가 이 사건을 맡은 것은 바로 이 무서운 사실의 사슬을 끊어 내고, 따로 떼어 놓고 보면 증거의 한 조각 한 조각이 입증되지 않은 것이며 허황된 것임을 보여 드리기 위함입니다.\""]], [["So Fetyukovitch began.", "페튜코비치는 이렇게 입을 떼었다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury,”", "\"배심원 여러분.\""], ["he suddenly protested, “I am new to this district.", "그가 돌연 항변하듯 말했다. \"저는 이 고장에 처음 온 사람입니다."], ["I have no preconceived ideas.", "저에게는 선입견이 없습니다."], ["The prisoner, a man of turbulent and unbridled temper, has not insulted me.", "피고는 거칠고 제멋대로인 성미의 사내이나, 저를 모욕한 적은 없습니다."], ["But he has insulted perhaps hundreds of persons in this town, and so prejudiced many people against him beforehand.", "그러나 그는 어쩌면 이 도시에서 수백 명에 이르는 사람들을 모욕했고, 그리하여 미리부터 많은 이들로 하여금 그에게 편견을 품게 만들었습니다."], ["Of course I recognize that the moral sentiment of local society is justly excited against him.", "물론 저는 이 고장 사회의 도덕적 감정이 그를 향해 정당하게 들끓고 있음을 인정합니다."], ["The prisoner is of turbulent and violent temper.", "피고는 거칠고 난폭한 성미의 사내입니다."], ["Yet he was received in society here; he was even welcome in the family of my talented friend, the prosecutor.”", "그럼에도 그는 이곳 사교계에 받아들여졌고, 심지어 제 재능 있는 벗인 검사의 가정에서도 환영을 받았습니다.\""]], [["(N.B. At these words there were two or three laughs in the audience, quickly suppressed, but noticed by all.", "(주의: 이 말에 방청석에서 두세 번 웃음이 터졌으나 곧 억눌렸지만, 모두가 알아챘다."], ["All of us knew that the prosecutor received Mitya against his will, solely because he had somehow interested his wife—a lady of the highest virtue and moral worth, but fanciful, capricious, and fond of opposing her husband, especially in trifles.", "우리는 모두 알고 있었으니, 검사가 미탸를 마지못해 받아들인 것은 오로지 그가 어찌어찌 검사의 부인의 흥미를 끌었기 때문이었다. 그 부인은 더없이 고결하고 도덕적 품격을 갖춘 여인이었으나, 공상이 심하고 변덕스러우며, 특히 사소한 일에서 남편에게 맞서기를 좋아하는 사람이었다."], ["Mitya’s visits, however, had not been frequent.)", "그러나 미탸의 방문이 잦았던 것은 아니었다.)"]], [["“Nevertheless I venture to suggest,”", "\"그렇기는 하나 저는 감히 이렇게 말씀드리고자 합니다.\""], ["Fetyukovitch continued, “that in spite of his independent mind and just character, my opponent may have formed a mistaken prejudice against my unfortunate client.", "페튜코비치가 말을 이었다. \"독립적인 정신과 공정한 성품을 지닌 분임에도, 제 상대편이신 검사께서는 어쩌면 저의 불행한 의뢰인에게 그릇된 편견을 품게 되셨을지도 모릅니다."], ["Oh, that is so natural; the unfortunate man has only too well deserved such prejudice.", "오, 그것은 너무도 자연스러운 일입니다. 이 불행한 사내는 그런 편견을 받아 마땅할 만큼의 짓을 너무도 충분히 저질렀으니까요."], ["Outraged morality, and still more outraged taste, is often relentless.", "모욕당한 도덕, 그리고 그보다 더욱 모욕당한 취향이란 흔히 가차 없는 법입니다."], ["We have, in the talented prosecutor’s speech, heard a stern analysis of the prisoner’s character and conduct, and his severe critical attitude to the case was evident.", "우리는 재능 있는 검사의 연설에서 피고의 성격과 행실에 대한 준엄한 분석을 들었고, 그가 이 사건을 대하는 엄격하고 비판적인 태도가 역력히 드러났습니다."], ["And, what’s more, he went into psychological subtleties into which he could not have entered, if he had the least conscious and malicious prejudice against the prisoner.", "게다가 그는 심리학적 미묘함에까지 파고들었으니, 만약 그가 피고에게 조금이라도 의식적이고 악의적인 편견을 품고 있었다면 결코 그렇게까지 들어갈 수 없었을 것입니다."], ["But there are things which are even worse, even more fatal in such cases, than the most malicious and consciously unfair attitude.", "그러나 이런 사건들에서는 가장 악의적이고 의식적으로 불공정한 태도보다도 더 나쁘고 더 치명적인 것들이 있습니다."], ["It is worse if we are carried away by the artistic instinct, by the desire to create, so to speak, a romance, especially if God has endowed us with psychological insight.", "우리가 예술가적 본능에, 말하자면 한 편의 소설을 지어내고 싶은 욕망에 휩쓸려 버린다면 그것이 더 나쁜 일이며, 특히 신께서 우리에게 심리적 통찰력을 부여하셨을 때 더욱 그렇습니다."], ["Before I started on my way here, I was warned in Petersburg, and was myself aware, that I should find here a talented opponent whose psychological insight and subtlety had gained him peculiar renown in legal circles of recent years.", "제가 이곳으로 길을 떠나기 전에 저는 페테르부르크에서 경고를 들었고, 또 저 자신도 알고 있었으니, 이곳에 와서 재능 있는 상대를 만나게 되리라는 것이었습니다. 그의 심리적 통찰과 섬세함은 근년에 법조계에서 그에게 남다른 명성을 안겨 주었습니다."], ["But profound as psychology is, it’s a knife that cuts both ways.”", "그러나 심리학이란 아무리 심오하다 한들 양쪽을 다 베는 칼입니다.\""], ["(Laughter among the public.) “You will, of course, forgive me my comparison; I can’t boast of eloquence.", "(방청석에서 웃음.) \"물론 여러분, 제 비유를 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 저는 웅변에 능하다고 뽐낼 처지가 못 됩니다."], ["But I will take as an example any point in the prosecutor’s speech.", "그러나 한 가지 예로 검사 연설 중의 아무 대목이나 들어 보겠습니다."]], [["“The prisoner, running away in the garden in the dark, climbed over the fence, was seized by the servant, and knocked him down with a brass pestle.", "\"피고는 어둠 속 정원을 가로질러 달아나다가 담을 넘었고, 하인에게 붙들리자 놋쇠 절굿공이로 그를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸습니다."], ["Then he jumped back into the garden and spent five minutes over the man, trying to discover whether he had killed him or not.", "그러고는 다시 정원 안으로 뛰어 내려와 그 사내를 죽였는지 아닌지 알아내려고 오 분이나 매달려 있었습니다."], ["And the prosecutor refuses to believe the prisoner’s statement that he ran to old Grigory out of pity.", "그런데 검사께서는 피고가 측은한 마음에 늙은 그리고리에게 달려갔다는 진술을 믿으려 하지 않습니다. 검사께서는 이렇게 말씀하십니다."], ["‘No,’ he says, ‘such sensibility is impossible at such a moment, that’s unnatural; he ran to find out whether the only witness of his crime was dead or alive, and so showed that he had committed the murder, since he would not have run back for any other reason.’", "'아니다, 그런 순간에 그런 감수성이란 있을 수 없으며 부자연스럽다. 그는 자기 범행의 유일한 목격자가 죽었는지 살았는지 알아보러 달려간 것이며, 그리하여 자신이 살인을 저질렀음을 스스로 드러낸 것이니, 다른 어떤 이유로도 되돌아 달려가지는 않았을 것이다.'"]], [["“Here you have psychology; but let us take the same method and apply it to the case the other way round, and our result will be no less probable.", "\"여기 여러분 앞에 심리학이 있습니다. 그러나 같은 방법을 가져다 이 사건에 거꾸로 적용해 보면, 우리가 얻는 결과 또한 그에 못지않게 그럴듯합니다."], ["The murderer, we are told, leapt down to find out, as a precaution, whether the witness was alive or not, yet he had left in his murdered father’s study, as the prosecutor himself argues, an amazing piece of evidence in the shape of a torn envelope, with an inscription that there had been three thousand roubles in it.", "그 살인자가 만일을 위해 목격자가 살았는지 죽었는지 알아보려고 뛰어내렸다고들 합니다. 그런데 검사 자신이 주장하는 바와 같이, 그는 살해당한 아버지의 서재에 삼천 루블이 들어 있었다는 글이 적힌 찢어진 봉투라는 놀라운 증거물을 남겨 두었습니다."], ["‘If he had carried that envelope away with him, no one in the world would have known of that envelope and of the notes in it, and that the money had been stolen by the prisoner.’ Those are the prosecutor’s own words.", "'만약 그가 그 봉투를 가지고 가 버렸다면, 그 봉투와 그 안에 들었던 지폐에 대해, 그리고 그 돈을 피고가 훔쳐 갔다는 사실에 대해 세상 누구도 알지 못했을 것이다.' 이것은 검사 자신의 말입니다."], ["So on one side you see a complete absence of precaution, a man who has lost his head and run away in a fright, leaving that clew on the floor, and two minutes later, when he has killed another man, we are entitled to assume the most heartless and calculating foresight in him.", "그러니 한편으로는 완전히 경계심을 잃은 모습, 곧 정신이 나가 겁에 질려 달아나며 그 단서를 바닥에 남겨 둔 사내가 보이는데, 이 분 뒤에는, 또 다른 사내를 죽였을 때는, 우리가 그에게서 더없이 무정하고 치밀한 계산과 선견지명을 인정해야 한다는 것입니다."], ["But even admitting this was so, it is psychological subtlety, I suppose, that discerns that under certain circumstances I become as bloodthirsty and keen-sighted as a Caucasian eagle, while at the next I am as timid and blind as a mole.", "그러나 설령 그것이 사실이라 하더라도, 어떤 상황에서는 제가 캅카스의 독수리처럼 피에 굶주리고 눈이 날카로워지다가, 그다음 순간에는 두더지처럼 겁 많고 눈먼 자가 된다고 분별해 내는 것이 바로 심리학적 섬세함이라는 것이겠지요."], ["But if I am so bloodthirsty and cruelly calculating that when I kill a man I only run back to find out whether he is alive to witness against me, why should I spend five minutes looking after my victim at the risk of encountering other witnesses?", "그러나 만일 제가 그토록 피에 굶주리고 잔혹하게 계산적이어서, 사람을 죽인 뒤에 오로지 그가 나를 고발할 증인으로 살아 있는지 알아보려고만 되돌아 달려간 자라면, 어찌하여 다른 목격자들과 마주칠 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 오 분이나 제 희생자를 살피며 보냈겠습니까?"], ["Why soak my handkerchief, wiping the blood off his head so that it may be evidence against me later?", "어찌하여 손수건을 적셔 가며 그의 머리에서 피를 닦아 내어 훗날 저에게 불리한 증거가 되게 했겠습니까?"], ["If he were so cold-hearted and calculating, why not hit the servant on the head again and again with the same pestle so as to kill him outright and relieve himself of all anxiety about the witness?", "만일 그가 그토록 냉혹하고 계산적이었다면, 어찌하여 그 절굿공이로 하인의 머리를 거듭거듭 내리쳐 아예 죽여 버리고 목격자에 대한 온갖 근심을 떨쳐 버리지 않았겠습니까?"]], [["“Again, though he ran to see whether the witness was alive, he left another witness on the path, that brass pestle which he had taken from the two women, and which they could always recognize afterwards as theirs, and prove that he had taken it from them.", "\"또한 그는 목격자가 살아 있는지 보려고 달려갔으면서도, 또 다른 증거를 길에 남겨 두었으니, 곧 그가 두 여인에게서 가져온 놋쇠 절굿공이입니다. 그들은 훗날 언제든 그것이 제 것임을 알아보고 그가 그것을 자기들에게서 가져갔음을 입증할 수 있었습니다."], ["And it is not as though he had forgotten it on the path, dropped it through carelessness or haste, no, he had flung away his weapon, for it was found fifteen paces from where Grigory lay.", "게다가 그것은 그가 길에 잊고 떨어뜨린 것이거나 부주의나 황급함 탓에 떨어뜨린 것이 아닙니다. 아닙니다, 그는 자기 무기를 내던진 것이니, 그것이 그리고리가 쓰러져 있던 자리에서 열다섯 걸음이나 떨어진 곳에서 발견되었기 때문입니다."], ["Why did he do so?", "어찌하여 그가 그렇게 했겠습니까?"], ["Just because he was grieved at having killed a man, an old servant; and he flung away the pestle with a curse, as a murderous weapon.", "다름이 아니라 사람을, 그것도 늙은 하인을 죽인 것이 슬펐기 때문이며, 그래서 그는 사람 죽인 흉기인 그 절굿공이를 저주를 내뱉으며 내던진 것입니다."], ["That’s how it must have been, what other reason could he have had for throwing it so far?", "분명 그러했을 것이니, 그렇지 않고서야 무슨 다른 까닭으로 그것을 그토록 멀리 던졌겠습니까?"], ["And if he was capable of feeling grief and pity at having killed a man, it shows that he was innocent of his father’s murder.", "그리고 만일 그가 사람을 죽인 것에 슬픔과 연민을 느낄 수 있는 자였다면, 이는 그가 아버지를 죽인 죄에서 결백하다는 것을 보여 줍니다."], ["Had he murdered him, he would never have run to another victim out of pity; then he would have felt differently; his thoughts would have been centered on self-preservation.", "만일 그가 아버지를 살해했다면, 그는 결코 측은한 마음에 또 다른 희생자에게 달려가지 않았을 것입니다. 그랬다면 그는 다른 감정을 느꼈을 것이니, 그의 생각은 오로지 제 한 몸 지키는 데로 쏠렸을 것입니다."], ["He would have had none to spare for pity, that is beyond doubt.", "연민에 내어 줄 마음의 여유라고는 한 점도 없었을 것이니, 이는 의심할 여지가 없습니다."], ["On the contrary, he would have broken his skull instead of spending five minutes looking after him.", "도리어 그는 오 분 동안 그를 살피며 보내는 대신 그의 머리통을 깨부수었을 것입니다."], ["There was room for pity and good-feeling just because his conscience had been clear till then.", "연민과 선한 감정이 들어설 자리가 있었던 것은 바로 그때까지 그의 양심이 깨끗했기 때문입니다."], ["Here we have a different psychology.", "여기서 우리는 전혀 다른 심리학을 마주하게 됩니다."], ["I have purposely resorted to this method, gentlemen of the jury, to show that you can prove anything by it.", "배심원 여러분, 저는 이 방법으로는 무엇이든 입증해 낼 수 있음을 보여 드리려고 일부러 그것에 의지해 본 것입니다."], ["It all depends on who makes use of it.", "모든 것은 누가 그것을 써먹느냐에 달려 있습니다."], ["Psychology lures even most serious people into romancing, and quite unconsciously.", "심리학은 더없이 진지한 사람들마저 소설을 지어내도록, 그것도 전혀 의식하지 못하는 사이에 꾀어내고 맙니다."], ["I am speaking of the abuse of psychology, gentlemen.”", "여러분, 저는 심리학의 오용에 대해 말씀드리는 것입니다.\""]], [["Sounds of approval and laughter, at the expense of the prosecutor, were again audible in the court.", "검사를 빗댄 동의의 소리와 웃음소리가 다시금 법정 안에 들려왔다."], ["I will not repeat the speech in detail; I will only quote some passages from it, some leading points.", "나는 그 연설을 자세히 되풀이하지는 않겠고, 다만 거기서 몇몇 대목, 몇몇 요긴한 요점만을 인용하기로 하겠다."]], [["# Chapter XI. There Was No Money. There Was No Robbery", "# Chapter XI. There Was No Money. There Was No Robbery"]], [["There was one point that struck every one in Fetyukovitch’s speech.", "돈은 없었다. 강도질도 없었다\n페튜코비치의 변론에는 모든 이의 마음을 강타한 한 가지 논점이 있었다."], ["He flatly denied the existence of the fatal three thousand roubles, and consequently, the possibility of their having been stolen.", "그는 운명을 가른 그 삼천 루블이라는 돈의 존재 자체를, 따라서 그것이 도둑맞았을 가능성마저 단호히 부인하고 나섰다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury,” he began.", "\"배심원 여러분.\""], ["“Every new and unprejudiced observer must be struck by a characteristic peculiarity in the present case, namely, the charge of robbery, and the complete impossibility of proving that there was anything to be stolen.", "그가 입을 열었다. \"새롭고 선입견 없는 관찰자라면 누구나 이번 사건의 한 가지 특징적인 면모에, 곧 강도질이라는 죄목과, 도둑맞을 만한 무언가가 있었음을 증명하기란 완전히 불가능하다는 사실에 충격을 받지 않을 수 없을 것입니다."], ["We are told that money was stolen—three thousand roubles—but whether those roubles ever existed, nobody knows.", "우리는 돈이, 삼천 루블이 도둑맞았다는 말을 듣고 있습니다만, 정작 그 루블이라는 것이 실제로 존재하기는 했는지 아는 사람이 아무도 없습니다. 한번 생각해 보십시오."], ["Consider, how have we heard of that sum, and who has seen the notes?", "우리가 어떻게 그 액수에 관해 듣게 되었으며, 또 그 지폐를 본 사람이 누구입니까?"], ["The only person who saw them, and stated that they had been put in the envelope, was the servant, Smerdyakov.", "그것을 보고, 봉투 속에 넣어 두었노라고 진술한 유일한 사람은 하인 스메르자코프였습니다."], ["He had spoken of it to the prisoner and his brother, Ivan Fyodorovitch, before the catastrophe.", "그는 이 사건이 벌어지기 전에 피고와 그 동생 이반 표도로비치에게 그 이야기를 했습니다."], ["Madame Svyetlov, too, had been told of it.", "스베틀로바 부인 역시 그 이야기를 전해 들었습니다."], ["But not one of these three persons had actually seen the notes, no one but Smerdyakov had seen them.", "그러나 이 세 사람 가운데 그 누구도 실제로 그 지폐를 보지는 못했고, 오직 스메르자코프만이 그것을 보았던 것입니다."]], [["“Here the question arises, if it’s true that they did exist, and that Smerdyakov had seen them, when did he see them for the last time?", "여기서 한 가지 의문이 생깁니다. 만약 그 돈이 정말로 존재했고, 또 스메르자코프가 그것을 보았다는 것이 사실이라면, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 본 것은 언제였습니까?"], ["What if his master had taken the notes from under his bed and put them back in his cash-box without telling him?", "만약 그의 주인이 침대 밑에서 지폐를 꺼내어 그에게는 알리지 않은 채 다시 자기 돈궤에 집어넣었다면 어쩌겠습니까? 유념하십시오."], ["Note, that according to Smerdyakov’s story the notes were kept under the mattress; the prisoner must have pulled them out, and yet the bed was absolutely unrumpled; that is carefully recorded in the protocol.", "스메르자코프의 이야기에 따르면 지폐는 매트리스 밑에 보관되어 있었습니다. 그렇다면 피고는 그것을 끄집어냈을 텐데, 정작 침대는 조금도 흐트러진 데가 없었습니다."], ["How could the prisoner have found the notes without disturbing the bed?", "이 점은 조서에 세심하게 기록되어 있습니다. 피고가 어떻게 침대를 흐트러뜨리지 않고서 지폐를 찾아낼 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["How could he have helped soiling with his blood-stained hands the fine and spotless linen with which the bed had been purposely made?", "그가 어떻게 피로 물든 손으로, 일부러 매끈하게 정돈해 두었던 그 곱고 티 없는 침대보를 더럽히지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?"]], [["“But I shall be asked: What about the envelope on the floor?", "그러나 이런 질문을 받을 것입니다. 바닥에 떨어져 있던 봉투는 어찌 된 것이냐?"], ["Yes, it’s worth saying a word or two about that envelope.", "그렇습니다, 그 봉투에 관해서는 한두 마디 짚고 넘어갈 만합니다."], ["I was somewhat surprised just now to hear the highly talented prosecutor declare of himself—of himself, observe—that but for that envelope, but for its being left on the floor, no one in the world would have known of the existence of that envelope and the notes in it, and therefore of the prisoner’s having stolen it.", "저는 방금 그 대단히 유능하신 검사께서 스스로—자기 입으로 말입니다, 유의하십시오—만약 그 봉투가 없었더라면, 그것이 바닥에 떨어져 있지 않았더라면, 세상 그 누구도 그 봉투와 그 안에 든 지폐의 존재를, 따라서 피고가 그것을 훔쳤다는 사실을 알지 못했을 것이라고 단언하시는 것을 듣고 적잖이 놀랐습니다. 그러니 그 찢어진 종잇조각이야말로 검사 자신의 인정에 따르건대 강도질이라는 죄목이 떠받쳐지는 유일한 증거인 셈입니다."], ["And so that torn scrap of paper is, by the prosecutor’s own admission, the sole proof on which the charge of robbery rests, ‘otherwise no one would have known of the robbery, nor perhaps even of the money.’ But is the mere fact that that scrap of paper was lying on the floor a proof that there was money in it, and that that money had been stolen?", "'그렇지 않았다면 아무도 그 강도질을, 어쩌면 그 돈조차 알지 못했을 것이다'라는 것이지요. 그러나 그 종잇조각이 바닥에 떨어져 있었다는 단순한 사실 하나가, 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었으며 그 돈이 도둑맞았다는 증거가 되겠습니까?"], ["Yet, it will be objected, Smerdyakov had seen the money in the envelope.", "그래도 스메르자코프가 봉투 안의 돈을 보았다고 반박할 것입니다."], ["But when, when had he seen it for the last time, I ask you that?", "그러나 언제, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 본 것이 언제였느냐고 저는 여러분께 묻는 바입니다."], ["I talked to Smerdyakov, and he told me that he had seen the notes two days before the catastrophe.", "저는 스메르자코프와 이야기를 나누었는데, 그는 사건이 벌어지기 이틀 전에 그 지폐를 보았노라고 말했습니다."], ["Then why not imagine that old Fyodor Pavlovitch, locked up alone in impatient and hysterical expectation of the object of his adoration, may have whiled away the time by breaking open the envelope and taking out the notes.", "그렇다면 늙은 표도르 파블로비치가, 자기가 흠모하는 대상을 애가 타고 발작적인 심정으로 기다리며 홀로 틀어박혀 있던 차에, 봉투를 뜯어 지폐를 꺼내며 시간을 때웠으리라고 어찌 상상하지 못하겠습니까. '봉투 따위가 무슨 소용인가?'"], ["‘What’s the use of the envelope?’ he may have asked himself.", "그는 스스로 이렇게 물었을지 모릅니다. '그 여자는 안에 지폐가 있다는 것을 믿지 않겠지."], ["‘She won’t believe the notes are there, but when I show her the thirty rainbow-colored notes in one roll, it will make more impression, you may be sure, it will make her mouth water.’ And so he tears open the envelope, takes out the money, and flings the envelope on the floor, conscious of being the owner and untroubled by any fears of leaving evidence.", "하지만 무지갯빛 지폐 서른 장을 한 다발로 보여 주면 더 깊은 인상을 줄 게 틀림없어, 군침을 흘리고 말 거야.' 그리하여 그는 봉투를 찢어 열고 돈을 꺼낸 뒤, 자신이 그 주인임을 의식하고 증거를 남길 걱정 따위 하지도 않은 채 봉투를 바닥에 내던졌을 것입니다."]], [["“Listen, gentlemen, could anything be more likely than this theory and such an action?", "들어 보십시오, 여러분. 이런 추론과 이런 행동보다 더 그럴듯한 것이 또 있겠습니까?"], ["Why is it out of the question?", "어째서 이것이 논외라는 말입니까?"], ["But if anything of the sort could have taken place, the charge of robbery falls to the ground; if there was no money, there was no theft of it.", "만약 이와 비슷한 일이 일어났을 수 있다면 강도질이라는 죄목은 무너지고 맙니다. 돈이 없었다면 그것을 훔친 일도 없는 것입니다."], ["If the envelope on the floor may be taken as evidence that there had been money in it, why may I not maintain the opposite, that the envelope was on the floor because the money had been taken from it by its owner?", "바닥에 떨어진 봉투를 그 안에 돈이 들어 있었다는 증거로 받아들일 수 있다면, 어찌하여 저는 그 반대를, 곧 그 주인이 봉투에서 돈을 꺼냈기 때문에 봉투가 바닥에 떨어져 있었다고 주장할 수 없다는 말입니까?"]], [["“But I shall be asked what became of the money if Fyodor Pavlovitch took it out of the envelope since it was not found when the police searched the house?", "그러나 이런 질문을 받을 것입니다. 표도르 파블로비치가 봉투에서 돈을 꺼냈다면 그 돈은 어찌 되었느냐, 경찰이 집을 수색했을 때는 발견되지 않았는데?"], ["In the first place, part of the money was found in the cash-box, and secondly, he might have taken it out that morning or the evening before to make some other use of it, to give or send it away; he may have changed his idea, his plan of action completely, without thinking it necessary to announce the fact to Smerdyakov beforehand.", "첫째로, 그 돈의 일부는 돈궤에서 발견되었습니다. 그리고 둘째로, 그는 그날 아침이나 그 전날 저녁에 그것을 다른 데 쓰려고, 누구에게 주거나 보내려고 꺼냈을 수 있습니다."], ["And if there is the barest possibility of such an explanation, how can the prisoner be so positively accused of having committed murder for the sake of robbery, and of having actually carried out that robbery?", "그는 자기 생각을, 행동 계획을 완전히 바꾸었으되 그 사실을 미리 스메르자코프에게 알릴 필요까지는 없다고 여겼을지 모릅니다. 그리고 만약 이런 설명이 조금이라도 가능하다면, 어떻게 피고가 강도질을 위해 살인을 저질렀으며 실제로 그 강도질을 실행에 옮겼다고 그토록 단정적으로 고발될 수 있겠습니까?"], ["This is encroaching on the domain of romance.", "이는 공상 소설의 영역을 침범하는 일입니다."], ["If it is maintained that something has been stolen, the thing must be produced, or at least its existence must be proved beyond doubt.", "무언가가 도둑맞았다고 주장하려면 그 물건을 제시하거나, 적어도 그것이 존재했음을 의심의 여지 없이 증명해야 합니다."], ["Yet no one had ever seen these notes.", "그러나 이 지폐를 본 사람은 아무도 없었던 것입니다."]], [["“Not long ago in Petersburg a young man of eighteen, hardly more than a boy, who carried on a small business as a costermonger, went in broad daylight into a moneychanger’s shop with an ax, and with extraordinary, typical audacity killed the master of the shop and carried off fifteen hundred roubles.", "얼마 전 페테르부르크에서 열여덟 살, 소년이나 다름없는 어느 젊은이가, 행상으로 자그마한 장사를 하던 자가, 백주 대낮에 도끼를 들고 환전상의 가게로 들어가, 유례없으리만치 전형적인 대담함으로 가게 주인을 죽이고 천오백 루블을 가지고 달아났습니다."], ["Five hours later he was arrested, and, except fifteen roubles he had already managed to spend, the whole sum was found on him.", "다섯 시간 뒤 그는 체포되었는데, 이미 써 버린 십오 루블을 제외한 전액이 그의 몸에서 발견되었습니다."], ["Moreover, the shopman, on his return to the shop after the murder, informed the police not only of the exact sum stolen, but even of the notes and gold coins of which that sum was made up, and those very notes and coins were found on the criminal.", "게다가 가게의 점원은 살인이 벌어진 뒤 가게로 돌아와, 도둑맞은 정확한 액수뿐 아니라 그 액수를 이루고 있던 지폐와 금화까지 경찰에 알렸으며, 바로 그 지폐와 금화가 범인에게서 발견되었습니다."], ["This was followed by a full and genuine confession on the part of the murderer.", "그 뒤로 살인자 측의 완전하고 진실된 자백이 이어졌습니다."], ["That’s what I call evidence, gentlemen of the jury!", "배심원 여러분, 저는 이런 것을 증거라고 부르는 바입니다!"], ["In that case I know, I see, I touch the money, and cannot deny its existence.", "그 경우에는 제가 돈을 알고, 보고, 만져 볼 수 있으니 그 존재를 부인할 수가 없습니다."], ["Is it the same in the present case?", "이번 사건도 그러합니까?"], ["And yet it is a question of life and death.", "그러면서도 이것은 한 사람의 생사가 걸린 문제입니다."]], [["“Yes, I shall be told, but he was carousing that night, squandering money; he was shown to have had fifteen hundred roubles—where did he get the money?", "물론 이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그러나 그는 그날 밤 흥청망청 술판을 벌이며 돈을 펑펑 썼고, 천오백 루블을 지니고 있었던 것으로 드러나지 않았는가, 그 돈은 어디서 났단 말이냐?"], ["But the very fact that only fifteen hundred could be found, and the other half of the sum could nowhere be discovered, shows that that money was not the same, and had never been in any envelope.", "그러나 바로 천오백밖에 발견되지 않았고 나머지 절반은 어디서도 찾아내지 못했다는 사실 자체가, 그 돈이 같은 돈이 아니었으며 애초에 어떤 봉투에도 들어 있던 적이 없었음을 보여 줍니다."], ["By strict calculation of time it was proved at the preliminary inquiry that the prisoner ran straight from those women servants to Perhotin’s without going home, and that he had been nowhere.", "예심에서는 시간을 엄밀히 계산하여, 피고가 그 하녀들에게서 곧장 페르호틴의 집으로 달려갔고 집에는 들르지 않았으며 그 어디에도 가지 않았음이 증명되었습니다."], ["So he had been all the time in company and therefore could not have divided the three thousand in half and hidden half in the town.", "그러니 그는 줄곧 사람들과 함께 있었던 셈이고, 따라서 삼천 루블을 절반으로 나누어 절반을 읍내에 숨겨 둘 수가 없었습니다."], ["It’s just this consideration that has led the prosecutor to assume that the money is hidden in some crevice at Mokroe.", "바로 이 점 때문에 검사는 그 돈이 모크로예 어딘가의 틈새에 숨겨져 있다고 가정하기에 이른 것입니다."], ["Why not in the dungeons of the castle of Udolpho, gentlemen?", "차라리 우돌포 성의 지하 감옥에 숨겨 두었다고 하는 게 어떻겠습니까, 여러분?"], ["Isn’t this supposition really too fantastic and too romantic?", "이 추측이야말로 너무도 환상적이고 너무도 소설 같지 않습니까?"], ["And observe, if that supposition breaks down, the whole charge of robbery is scattered to the winds, for in that case what could have become of the other fifteen hundred roubles?", "그리고 유의하십시오. 만약 그 추측이 무너지면 강도질이라는 죄목 전체가 바람에 흩어지고 마는데, 그렇게 되면 나머지 천오백 루블은 대체 어찌 되었겠습니까?"], ["By what miracle could they have disappeared, since it’s proved the prisoner went nowhere else?", "피고가 그 어디에도 가지 않았음이 증명된 마당에, 그것이 무슨 기적으로 사라질 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["And we are ready to ruin a man’s life with such tales!", "그런데도 우리는 이런 이야기를 가지고 한 사람의 인생을 파멸시킬 채비를 하고 있습니다!"]], [["“I shall be told that he could not explain where he got the fifteen hundred that he had, and every one knew that he was without money before that night.", "이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그는 자신이 지니고 있던 천오백 루블을 어디서 얻었는지 설명하지 못했으며, 그날 밤 전까지 그에게 돈이 없었다는 것을 누구나 알고 있었다고 말입니다."], ["Who knew it, pray?", "대체 그것을 누가 알았다는 것입니까?"], ["The prisoner has made a clear and unflinching statement of the source of that money, and if you will have it so, gentlemen of the jury, nothing can be more probable than that statement, and more consistent with the temper and spirit of the prisoner.", "피고는 그 돈의 출처에 관해 명확하고 흔들림 없는 진술을 했으며, 여러분이 굳이 따져 보고자 한다면, 배심원 여러분, 그 진술만큼 개연성이 높고 피고의 기질과 정신에 들어맞는 것은 없습니다."], ["The prosecutor is charmed with his own romance.", "검사는 자신의 소설에 도취되어 있습니다."], ["A man of weak will, who had brought himself to take the three thousand so insultingly offered by his betrothed, could not, we are told, have set aside half and sewn it up, but would, even if he had done so, have unpicked it every two days and taken out a hundred, and so would have spent it all in a month.", "자신의 약혼녀가 그토록 모욕적으로 내놓은 삼천 루블을 차마 받아 든 의지박약한 사내가, 우리가 듣기로는, 그 절반을 따로 떼어 꿰매 넣었을 리 없으며, 설령 그리했다 하더라도 이틀에 한 번씩 그것을 뜯어 백 루블씩 꺼내 한 달 만에 죄다 써 버렸으리라는 것입니다."], ["All this, you will remember, was put forward in a tone that brooked no contradiction.", "여러분도 기억하시겠지만, 이 모든 것이 반박을 허용하지 않는 어조로 제시되었습니다."], ["But what if the thing happened quite differently?", "그러나 만약 그 일이 전혀 다르게 벌어졌다면 어쩌겠습니까?"], ["What if you’ve been weaving a romance, and about quite a different kind of man?", "만약 여러분이 소설을 한 편 짜내고 있었던 것이라면, 그것도 전혀 다른 종류의 인간에 관해서라면?"], ["That’s just it, you have invented quite a different man!", "바로 그렇습니다, 여러분은 전혀 다른 인간을 지어낸 것입니다!"]], [["“I shall be told, perhaps, there are witnesses that he spent on one day all that three thousand given him by his betrothed a month before the catastrophe, so he could not have divided the sum in half.", "어쩌면 이런 말을 들을지도 모릅니다. 그가 사건이 벌어지기 한 달 전 약혼녀가 준 그 삼천 루블 전부를 하루 만에 써 버렸다는 증인들이 있으니, 그가 그 액수를 절반으로 나누었을 리 없다고 말입니다."], ["But who are these witnesses?", "그러나 그 증인들이란 대체 누구입니까?"], ["The value of their evidence has been shown in court already.", "그들 증언의 가치는 이미 법정에서 드러났습니다."], ["Besides, in another man’s hand a crust always seems larger, and no one of these witnesses counted that money; they all judged simply at sight.", "게다가 남의 손에 들린 빵 조각은 늘 더 커 보이는 법이며, 그 증인들 가운데 누구도 그 돈을 세어 본 적이 없으니, 모두가 그저 눈대중으로 짐작했을 뿐입니다."], ["And the witness Maximov has testified that the prisoner had twenty thousand in his hand.", "그리고 증인 막시모프는 피고가 이만 루블을 손에 쥐고 있었다고 증언했습니다."], ["You see, gentlemen of the jury, psychology is a two-edged weapon.", "보십시오, 배심원 여러분, 심리학이란 양날의 검입니다."], ["Let me turn the other edge now and see what comes of it.", "이제 그 다른 쪽 날로 돌려서 무슨 결과가 나오는지 살펴보겠습니다."]], [["“A month before the catastrophe the prisoner was entrusted by Katerina Ivanovna with three thousand roubles to send off by post.", "사건이 벌어지기 한 달 전, 피고는 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 우편으로 부쳐 달라며 삼천 루블을 위탁받았습니다."], ["But the question is: is it true that they were entrusted to him in such an insulting and degrading way as was proclaimed just now?", "그러나 문제는 이것입니다. 그 돈이 방금 선언된 것처럼 그토록 모욕적이고 굴욕적인 방식으로 그에게 맡겨졌다는 것이 사실입니까?"], ["The first statement made by the young lady on the subject was different, perfectly different.", "그 처녀가 이 문제에 관해 처음 한 진술은 달랐습니다, 완전히 달랐습니다."], ["In the second statement we heard only cries of resentment and revenge, cries of long-concealed hatred.", "두 번째 진술에서 우리가 들은 것은 다만 원한과 복수의 외침, 오래 감추어 온 증오의 외침뿐이었습니다."], ["And the very fact that the witness gave her first evidence incorrectly, gives us a right to conclude that her second piece of evidence may have been incorrect also.", "그리고 그 증인이 처음 증언을 부정확하게 했다는 사실 자체가, 그 두 번째 증언 또한 부정확했을 수 있다는 결론을 내릴 권리를 우리에게 줍니다. 검사는 그 이야기를 건드리려 하지 않을 것이고, 감히 건드리지도 못할 것입니다(그 자신의 말입니다)."], ["The prosecutor will not, dare not (his own words) touch on that story. So be it.", "그렇다면 그러라지요."], ["I will not touch on it either, but will only venture to observe that if a lofty and high-principled person, such as that highly respected young lady unquestionably is, if such a person, I say, allows herself suddenly in court to contradict her first statement, with the obvious motive of ruining the prisoner, it is clear that this evidence has been given not impartially, not coolly.", "저 역시 그것을 건드리지 않겠습니다만, 다만 이 한 가지만 감히 지적하고자 합니다. 만약 의심할 바 없이 그토록 존경받는 처녀처럼 고결하고 도덕적인 사람이, 그런 사람이 말입니다, 피고를 파멸시키려는 뻔한 동기를 품고 법정에서 돌연 자신의 첫 진술을 뒤집는 것을 스스로에게 허용한다면, 그 증언이 공정하지도, 냉정하지도 않게 행해졌음이 분명합니다."], ["Have not we the right to assume that a revengeful woman might have exaggerated much?", "복수심에 불타는 여인이라면 많은 것을 과장했으리라고 가정할 권리가 우리에게 없겠습니까?"], ["Yes, she may well have exaggerated, in particular, the insult and humiliation of her offering him the money.", "그렇습니다, 그녀는 능히 과장했을 만하며, 특히 그에게 돈을 내밀 때의 그 모욕과 굴욕을 과장했을 것입니다."], ["No, it was offered in such a way that it was possible to take it, especially for a man so easy-going as the prisoner, above all, as he expected to receive shortly from his father the three thousand roubles that he reckoned was owing to him.", "아닙니다, 그 돈은 받을 수 있을 만한 방식으로 내밀어졌으니, 특히 피고처럼 무던한 사내에게는 그러했고, 무엇보다 그는 머지않아 아버지에게서 자기 몫이라고 여기는 삼천 루블을 받으리라 기대하고 있었습니다."], ["It was unreflecting of him, but it was just his irresponsible want of reflection that made him so confident that his father would give him the money, that he would get it, and so could always dispatch the money entrusted to him and repay the debt.", "그것은 그의 경솔함이었습니다만, 바로 그 무책임한 생각 없음이야말로 그로 하여금 아버지가 돈을 주리라고, 자기가 그것을 받게 되리라고, 그래서 자기에게 맡겨진 돈을 언제든 부쳐 빚을 갚을 수 있으리라고 그토록 확신하게 만든 것입니다."]], [["“But the prosecutor refuses to allow that he could the same day have set aside half the money and sewn it up in a little bag.", "그러나 검사는 그가 같은 날 그 돈의 절반을 따로 떼어 작은 주머니에 꿰매 넣었을 수 있다는 것을 인정하지 않으려 합니다."], ["That’s not his character, he tells us, he couldn’t have had such feelings.", "그건 그의 성격이 아니다, 그는 그런 감정을 품을 수 없었다고 우리에게 말합니다."], ["But yet he talked himself of the broad Karamazov nature; he cried out about the two extremes which a Karamazov can contemplate at once.", "그러면서도 검사 자신이 카라마조프 집안의 광활한 본성에 관해 떠들었으며, 카라마조프라면 두 극단을 한꺼번에 품을 수 있다고 외쳤습니다."], ["Karamazov is just such a two-sided nature, fluctuating between two extremes, that even when moved by the most violent craving for riotous gayety, he can pull himself up, if something strikes him on the other side.", "카라마조프란 바로 그렇게 양면적인 본성을 지녀 두 극단 사이를 오가는 인간이니, 광란의 환락을 향한 가장 격렬한 갈망에 휩싸여 있을 때조차도, 다른 한편에서 무언가가 그의 마음을 치면 스스로를 다잡을 수 있는 것입니다."], ["And on the other side is love—that new love which had flamed up in his heart, and for that love he needed money; oh, far more than for carousing with his mistress.", "그리고 그 다른 한편에는 사랑이—그의 가슴에서 타오른 그 새로운 사랑이 있었으며, 그 사랑을 위해 그는 돈이 필요했습니다. 아, 정부와 흥청대며 노는 것보다 훨씬 더 절실하게 말입니다."], ["If she were to say to him, ‘I am yours, I won’t have Fyodor Pavlovitch,’ then he must have money to take her away.", "만약 그녀가 그에게 '나는 당신 거예요, 표도르 파블로비치는 싫어요'라고 말한다면, 그는 그녀를 데리고 떠날 돈이 있어야 했습니다."], ["That was more important than carousing.", "그것이 흥청거리는 것보다 더 중요했습니다."], ["Could a Karamazov fail to understand it?", "카라마조프가 그것을 이해하지 못했을 리 있겠습니까?"], ["That anxiety was just what he was suffering from—what is there improbable in his laying aside that money and concealing it in case of emergency?", "그가 시달리고 있던 것은 바로 그 불안이었으니, 그가 만일의 사태에 대비해 그 돈을 따로 떼어 숨겨 두었다 한들 무엇이 그리 있을 법하지 않은 일이겠습니까?"]], [["“But time passed, and Fyodor Pavlovitch did not give the prisoner the expected three thousand; on the contrary, the latter heard that he meant to use this sum to seduce the woman he, the prisoner, loved. ‘If Fyodor Pavlovitch doesn’t give the money,’ he thought, ‘I shall be put in the position of a thief before Katerina Ivanovna.’ And then the idea presented itself to him that he would go to Katerina Ivanovna, lay before her the fifteen hundred roubles he still carried round his neck, and say, ‘I am a scoundrel, but not a thief.’ So here we have already a twofold reason why he should guard that sum of money as the apple of his eye, why he shouldn’t unpick the little bag, and spend it a hundred at a time. Why should you deny the prisoner a sense of honor? Yes, he has a sense of honor, granted that it’s misplaced, granted it’s often mistaken, yet it exists and amounts to a passion, and he has proved that.", "그러나 시간이 흘러도 표도르 파블로비치는 피고에게 기대했던 삼천 루블을 주지 않았습니다. 오히려 피고는 아버지가 그 돈을 써서 자기가, 곧 피고가 사랑하는 여인을 유혹할 작정이라는 말을 들었습니다. '표도르 파블로비치가 돈을 주지 않으면,' 그는 생각했습니다. '나는 카테리나 이바노브나 앞에서 도둑 신세가 되고 마는 거야.' 그러자 이런 생각이 떠올랐습니다. 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가서, 여태 목에 걸고 다니던 천오백 루블을 그녀 앞에 내놓고 이렇게 말하겠다고. '나는 비열한 놈이지만, 도둑은 아니오.' 그러니 여기서 우리는 이미 그가 어째서 그 돈을 눈동자처럼 지켜야 했는지, 어째서 그 작은 주머니를 뜯어 백 루블씩 써 버려서는 안 되었는지에 대한 이중의 이유를 갖게 됩니다. 어찌하여 여러분은 피고에게 명예 감각이 있음을 부정하려 하십니까? 그렇습니다, 그에게는 명예 감각이 있으니, 그것이 빗나간 것이라 치고, 흔히 그릇된 것이라 친다 해도, 그래도 그것은 엄연히 존재하며 하나의 정념에까지 이르렀고, 그는 그것을 증명해 보였습니다."]], [["“But now the affair becomes even more complex; his jealous torments reach a climax, and those same two questions torture his fevered brain more and more: ‘If I repay Katerina Ivanovna, where can I find the means to go off with Grushenka?’ If he behaved wildly, drank, and made disturbances in the taverns in the course of that month, it was perhaps because he was wretched and strained beyond his powers of endurance.", "그러나 이제 사태는 한층 더 복잡해집니다. 그의 질투의 고통이 절정에 이르고, 바로 그 두 가지 의문이 그의 열병에 들뜬 머릿속을 점점 더 괴롭힙니다."], ["These two questions became so acute that they drove him at last to despair.", "'카테리나 이바노브나에게 돈을 갚는다면, 그루셴카와 떠날 돈은 어디서 마련한단 말인가?' 그가 그 한 달 동안 거칠게 굴고, 술을 마시고, 선술집에서 소동을 부렸다면, 그것은 어쩌면 그가 비참한 처지에서 견딜 수 있는 한계를 넘도록 시달렸기 때문일 것입니다."], ["He sent his younger brother to beg for the last time for the three thousand roubles, but without waiting for a reply, burst in himself and ended by beating the old man in the presence of witnesses.", "이 두 가지 의문이 너무도 날카로워져 마침내 그를 절망으로 몰아넣었습니다. 그는 동생을 보내 마지막으로 삼천 루블을 청해 보려 했으나, 답을 기다리지도 못한 채 직접 뛰어들어, 결국 증인들이 보는 앞에서 늙은 아버지를 두들겨 패고 말았습니다."], ["After that he had no prospect of getting it from any one; his father would not give it him after that beating.", "그 뒤로 그는 누구에게서도 그 돈을 얻을 가망이 없었습니다. 그렇게 매질을 당한 뒤로 아버지가 그에게 돈을 줄 리 없었던 것입니다."]], [["“The same evening he struck himself on the breast, just on the upper part of the breast where the little bag was, and swore to his brother that he had the means of not being a scoundrel, but that still he would remain a scoundrel, for he foresaw that he would not use that means, that he wouldn’t have the character, that he wouldn’t have the will-power to do it.", "바로 그날 저녁, 그는 자기 가슴을, 그 작은 주머니가 있던 가슴의 윗부분을 치면서 동생에게 맹세하기를, 자기에게는 비열한 놈이 되지 않을 방도가 있으나 그래도 자기는 비열한 놈으로 남으리라고 했습니다. 자기가 그 방도를 쓰지 않으리라는 것을, 그럴 만한 성격도, 그럴 만한 의지력도 없으리라는 것을 스스로 내다보았기 때문입니다."], ["Why, why does the prosecutor refuse to believe the evidence of Alexey Karamazov, given so genuinely and sincerely, so spontaneously and convincingly?", "어찌하여, 어찌하여 검사는 알렉세이 카라마조프의 증언을, 그토록 진실되고 성실하게, 그토록 거짓 없고 설득력 있게 행해진 그 증언을 믿기를 거부하는 것입니까?"], ["And why, on the contrary, does he force me to believe in money hidden in a crevice, in the dungeons of the castle of Udolpho?", "그러면서 어찌하여 도리어 저더러 틈새에 숨겨진 돈을, 우돌포 성의 지하 감옥에 숨겨진 돈을 믿으라고 강요하는 것입니까?"]], [["“The same evening, after his talk with his brother, the prisoner wrote that fatal letter, and that letter is the chief, the most stupendous proof of the prisoner having committed robbery!", "바로 그날 저녁, 동생과 이야기를 나눈 뒤 피고는 그 운명의 편지를 썼으니, 그 편지야말로 피고가 강도질을 저질렀다는 가장 주요하고도 가장 어마어마한 증거라는 것입니다!"], ["‘I shall beg from every one, and if I don’t get it I shall murder my father and shall take the envelope with the pink ribbon on it from under his mattress as soon as Ivan has gone.’ A full program of the murder, we are told, so it must have been he.", "'나는 모든 사람에게 구걸해 보고, 그래도 얻지 못하면 아버지를 죽이고 이반이 떠나는 즉시 매트리스 밑에서 분홍 리본이 달린 봉투를 꺼내겠다.' 우리가 듣기로는 살인의 완벽한 계획서라는 것이니, 따라서 범인은 그일 수밖에 없다는 것입니다."], ["‘It has all been done as he wrote,’ cries the prosecutor.", "'그가 쓴 그대로 모든 것이 실행되었다'고 검사는 외칩니다."]], [["“But in the first place, it’s the letter of a drunken man and written in great irritation; secondly, he writes of the envelope from what he has heard from Smerdyakov again, for he has not seen the envelope himself; and thirdly, he wrote it indeed, but how can you prove that he did it?", "그러나 첫째로, 그것은 술 취한 자의 편지이며 극도의 흥분 상태에서 쓰인 것입니다. 둘째로, 그가 봉투에 관해 쓴 것은 또다시 스메르자코프에게서 들은 것에 따른 것이니, 그는 봉투를 자기 눈으로 본 적이 없기 때문입니다."], ["Did the prisoner take the envelope from under the pillow, did he find the money, did that money exist indeed?", "그리고 셋째로, 그가 그것을 쓴 것은 사실이지만, 그가 그 일을 실제로 행했다는 것을 어떻게 증명할 수 있겠습니까? 피고가 베개 밑에서 봉투를 꺼냈습니까, 그가 돈을 찾아냈습니까, 그 돈이 실제로 존재하기는 했습니까?"], ["And was it to get money that the prisoner ran off, if you remember?", "그리고 기억하신다면, 피고가 달려간 것이 돈을 얻기 위해서였습니까?"], ["He ran off post-haste not to steal, but to find out where she was, the woman who had crushed him.", "그는 훔치려고 그토록 황급히 달려간 것이 아니라, 그녀가, 자신을 짓밟아 놓은 그 여인이 어디 있는지 알아내려고 달려간 것입니다."], ["He was not running to carry out a program, to carry out what he had written, that is, not for an act of premeditated robbery, but he ran suddenly, spontaneously, in a jealous fury.", "그는 어떤 계획을 실행하려고, 자기가 쓴 것을 실행하려고 달려간 것이 아니었습니다. 다시 말해 미리 계획된 강도질의 행위를 위해서가 아니라, 돌연히, 충동적으로, 질투의 광란에 휩싸여 달려간 것입니다."], ["Yes!", "그렇습니다!"], ["I shall be told, but when he got there and murdered him he seized the money, too.", "이런 말을 들을 것입니다. 그러나 그가 그곳에 이르러 아버지를 죽였을 때 돈마저 움켜쥐지 않았느냐고 말입니다."], ["But did he murder him after all?", "그러나 그가 정말로 아버지를 죽이기는 했습니까?"], ["The charge of robbery I repudiate with indignation.", "강도질이라는 죄목을 저는 분개하며 부인하는 바입니다."], ["A man cannot be accused of robbery, if it’s impossible to state accurately what he has stolen; that’s an axiom.", "무엇을 훔쳤는지 정확하게 진술할 수 없다면 그 사람을 강도질로 고발할 수 없으니, 그것은 하나의 공리입니다."], ["But did he murder him without robbery, did he murder him at all?", "그러나 그가 강도질 없이 아버지를 죽였습니까, 아니 도대체 아버지를 죽이기는 했습니까?"], ["Is that proved?", "그것이 증명되었습니까?"], ["Isn’t that, too, a romance?”", "그것 또한 소설이 아니겠습니까?\""]], [["# Chapter XII. And There Was No Murder Either", "# Chapter XII. And There Was No Murder Either"]], [["“Allow me, gentlemen of the jury, to remind you that a man’s life is at stake and that you must be careful.", "배심원 여러분, 여기서 한 사람의 목숨이 걸려 있으며 여러분께서 신중하셔야 한다는 점을 다시금 일깨워 드리는 것을 허락해 주십시오."], ["We have heard the prosecutor himself admit that until to-day he hesitated to accuse the prisoner of a full and conscious premeditation of the crime; he hesitated till he saw that fatal drunken letter which was produced in court to-day.", "우리는 검사 자신이, 오늘에 이르기까지 피고가 이 범죄를 온전히, 의식적으로 미리 계획했다고 단정하기를 망설였노라고 시인하는 것을 들었습니다. 그는 오늘 법정에 제출된 그 운명적인 취중의 편지를 보기 전까지 망설였던 것입니다."], ["‘All was done as written.’ But, I repeat again, he was running to her, to seek her, solely to find out where she was.", "‘쓰인 그대로 모두 행해졌다’는 것이지요. 그러나 거듭 말씀드리거니와, 그는 그녀에게로, 그녀를 찾으러, 오로지 그녀가 어디 있는지 알아내려고 달려갔던 것입니다."], ["That’s a fact that can’t be disputed.", "이것은 누구도 반박할 수 없는 사실입니다."], ["Had she been at home, he would not have run away, but would have remained at her side, and so would not have done what he promised in the letter.", "만일 그녀가 집에 있었다면 그는 달아나지 않고 그녀 곁에 머물렀을 것이며, 따라서 편지에서 약속한 일을 저지르지 않았을 것입니다."], ["He ran unexpectedly and accidentally, and by that time very likely he did not even remember his drunken letter.", "그는 뜻밖에, 우연히 달려간 것이며, 그때쯤이면 십중팔구 자기가 쓴 취중의 편지조차 기억하지 못했을 것입니다."], ["‘He snatched up the pestle,’ they say, and you will remember how a whole edifice of psychology was built on that pestle—why he was bound to look at that pestle as a weapon, to snatch it up, and so on, and so on.", "‘그는 절굿공이를 움켜쥐었다’고들 하며, 여러분께서는 그 절굿공이를 토대로 어떻게 심리학의 전 구조물이 세워졌는지를 기억하실 것입니다. 어찌하여 그가 그 절굿공이를 무기로 바라보고 그것을 움켜쥐어야 했는가, 운운, 운운 말입니다."], ["A very commonplace idea occurs to me at this point: What if that pestle had not been in sight, had not been lying on the shelf from which it was snatched by the prisoner, but had been put away in a cupboard?", "바로 이 대목에서 지극히 평범한 생각 하나가 제 머리에 떠오릅니다. 만일 그 절굿공이가 눈에 띄지 않았더라면, 피고가 그것을 움켜쥐었다는 그 선반 위에 놓여 있지 않고 찬장 속에 치워져 있었더라면 어찌 되었겠습니까?"], ["It would not have caught the prisoner’s eye, and he would have run away without a weapon, with empty hands, and then he would certainly not have killed any one.", "그것은 피고의 눈에 들어오지 않았을 것이고, 그는 무기도 없이 빈손으로 달려갔을 것이며, 그랬다면 그는 분명 아무도 죽이지 않았을 것입니다."], ["How then can I look upon the pestle as a proof of premeditation?", "그렇다면 제가 어찌 그 절굿공이를 사전 계획의 증거로 볼 수 있겠습니까?"]], [["“Yes, but he talked in the taverns of murdering his father, and two days before, on the evening when he wrote his drunken letter, he was quiet and only quarreled with a shopman in the tavern, because a Karamazov could not help quarreling, forsooth!", "그렇습니다, 그러나 그는 술집에서 자기 아버지를 죽이겠다고 떠들었으며, 이틀 전, 곧 취중의 편지를 쓴 그날 저녁에는 조용했고 다만 술집에서 한 점원과 다투었을 뿐인데, 그것도 카라마조프란 어쩔 수 없이 다투게 마련이기 때문이라고들 합니다그려! 그러나 이에 대한 제 대답은 이렇습니다."], ["But my answer to that is, that, if he was planning such a murder in accordance with his letter, he certainly would not have quarreled even with a shopman, and probably would not have gone into the tavern at all, because a person plotting such a crime seeks quiet and retirement, seeks to efface himself, to avoid being seen and heard, and that not from calculation, but from instinct.", "만일 그가 편지에 적힌 대로 그런 살인을 계획하고 있었다면, 그는 점원하고조차 다투지 않았을 것이며, 아마 술집에 발도 들이지 않았을 것입니다. 그런 범죄를 꾸미는 자는 고요함과 은둔을 찾고, 자기를 지우려 하며, 남의 눈과 귀에 띄지 않으려 하는 법이니, 그것도 계산에서가 아니라 본능에서 그러하기 때문입니다."], ["Gentlemen of the jury, the psychological method is a two-edged weapon, and we, too, can use it.", "배심원 여러분, 심리학적 방법이란 양날의 무기이며, 우리 역시 그것을 쓸 수 있습니다."], ["As for all this shouting in taverns throughout the month, don’t we often hear children, or drunkards coming out of taverns shout, ‘I’ll kill you’?", "한 달 내내 술집에서 떠들어댔다는 그 모든 일로 말하자면, 우리는 어린아이들이나 술집에서 나오는 취객들이 ‘너를 죽여 버리겠다!’고 외치는 것을 흔히 듣지 않습니까?"], ["but they don’t murder any one.", "그러나 그들은 아무도 죽이지 않습니다."], ["And that fatal letter—isn’t that simply drunken irritability, too?", "그리고 그 운명적인 편지—그것 또한 그저 취중의 짜증에 지나지 않는 것이 아니겠습니까?"], ["Isn’t that simply the shout of the brawler outside the tavern, ‘I’ll kill you!", "그것이 그저 술집 앞에서 주정뱅이가 내지르는 고함, ‘너를 죽이겠다! 너희를 모조리 죽이겠다!’와 다를 것이 무엇입니까?"], ["I’ll kill the lot of you!’ Why not, why could it not be that?", "어찌 그럴 수 없겠습니까, 어찌 그럴 수 없겠습니까?"], ["What reason have we to call that letter ‘fatal’ rather than absurd?", "우리가 그 편지를 ‘터무니없다’가 아니라 ‘운명적이다’라고 부를 무슨 근거를 가지고 있습니까?"], ["Because his father has been found murdered, because a witness saw the prisoner running out of the garden with a weapon in his hand, and was knocked down by him: therefore, we are told, everything was done as he had planned in writing, and the letter was not ‘absurd,’ but ‘fatal.’", "그의 아버지가 살해된 채 발견되었기 때문이며, 한 증인이 피고가 손에 무기를 들고 정원에서 달려 나오는 것을 보았고 그에게 쓰러뜨려졌기 때문입니다. 그러니 우리는 모든 것이 그가 글로 계획한 그대로 행해졌으며, 그 편지는 ‘터무니없는’ 것이 아니라 ‘운명적인’ 것이라고 듣게 되는 것이지요."]], [["“Now, thank God!", "자, 이제 하느님께 감사하게도!"], ["we’ve come to the real point: ‘since he was in the garden, he must have murdered him.’ In those few words: ‘since he was, then he must’ lies the whole case for the prosecution.", "우리는 진짜 요점에 이르렀습니다. ‘그가 정원에 있었으니, 틀림없이 그가 죽였다.’ 이 몇 마디 말, 곧 ‘그가 있었으니, 그렇다면 그가 틀림없이’라는 말 속에 검찰 측 주장의 전부가 들어 있습니다."], ["He was there, so he must have. And what if there is no must about it, even if he was there?", "그가 거기 있었으니, 틀림없이 그가 했다는 것이지요."], ["Oh, I admit that the chain of evidence—the coincidences—are really suggestive.", "그런데 만일 그가 거기 있었다 한들, 거기에 ‘틀림없이’라는 것이 없다면 어찌 됩니까?"], ["But examine all these facts separately, regardless of their connection.", "오, 저는 증거의 사슬이—그 우연의 일치들이—실로 암시적이라는 점을 인정합니다."], ["Why, for instance, does the prosecution refuse to admit the truth of the prisoner’s statement that he ran away from his father’s window?", "그러나 이 모든 사실을 그 연결고리와 무관하게 하나하나 따로 검토해 보십시오. 가령 어찌하여 검찰 측은 피고가 아버지의 창문에서 달아났다는 진술의 진실성을 인정하기를 거부합니까?"], ["Remember the sarcasms in which the prosecutor indulged at the expense of the respectful and ‘pious’ sentiments which suddenly came over the murderer.", "그 살인자에게 갑자기 찾아들었다는 그 공손하고 ‘경건한’ 감정을 두고 검사가 늘어놓은 빈정거림을 기억하십시오."], ["But what if there were something of the sort, a feeling of religious awe, if not of filial respect?", "그러나 만일 정말로 그런 무언가가, 자식 된 도리에서 우러난 존경까지는 아니더라도 종교적 경외의 감정 같은 것이 있었다면 어찌 됩니까?"], ["‘My mother must have been praying for me at that moment,’ were the prisoner’s words at the preliminary inquiry, and so he ran away as soon as he convinced himself that Madame Svyetlov was not in his father’s house.", "‘바로 그 순간 어머니가 저를 위해 기도하고 계셨던 것이 틀림없습니다’라고 한 것이 예심에서 피고가 한 말이었으며, 그리하여 그는 스베틀로바 부인이 아버지의 집에 없다는 것을 스스로 확인하자마자 달아났던 것입니다."], ["‘But he could not convince himself by looking through the window,’ the prosecutor objects.", "‘그러나 그는 창문을 들여다본다고 그것을 확인할 수 없었을 것이다’라고 검사는 반박합니다."], ["But why couldn’t he? Why?", "그러나 어찌 못 했겠습니까?"], ["The window opened at the signals given by the prisoner.", "어찌하여 말입니까? 그 창문은 피고가 보낸 신호에 따라 열렸습니다."], ["Some word might have been uttered by Fyodor Pavlovitch, some exclamation which showed the prisoner that she was not there.", "표도르 파블로비치가 무슨 말을, 그녀가 거기 없다는 것을 피고에게 알려 주는 어떤 외침을 내뱉었을 수도 있습니다."], ["Why should we assume everything as we imagine it, as we make up our minds to imagine it?", "어찌하여 우리는 모든 것을 우리가 상상하는 대로, 우리가 마음먹고 상상하기로 한 대로 단정해야 합니까?"], ["A thousand things may happen in reality which elude the subtlest imagination.", "가장 정교한 상상력조차 비껴가는 일이 현실에서는 무수히 일어나는 법입니다."]], [["“‘Yes, but Grigory saw the door open and so the prisoner certainly was in the house, therefore he killed him.’ Now about that door, gentlemen of the jury....", "‘그렇다, 그러나 그리고리는 문이 열려 있는 것을 보았으니 피고는 분명 집 안에 있었고, 따라서 그가 죽인 것이다.’ 자, 그 문에 관해서 말입니다, 배심원 여러분……"], ["Observe that we have only the statement of one witness as to that door, and he was at the time in such a condition, that— But supposing the door was open; supposing the prisoner has lied in denying it, from an instinct of self-defense, natural in his position; supposing he did go into the house—well, what then?", "그 문에 관해서는 우리에게 단 한 증인의 진술밖에 없으며, 그 증인은 그 당시 이러이러한 상태에 있었다는 점에 유념하십시오, 그러니까— 그러나 그 문이 열려 있었다고 칩시다. 피고가 자기 처지에서 자연스러운 자기방어의 본능에서 그것을 부인하며 거짓말했다고 칩시다."], ["How does it follow that because he was there he committed the murder?", "그가 집 안에 들어갔다고 칩시다. 자, 그래서 어떻다는 것입니까?"], ["He might have dashed in, run through the rooms; might have pushed his father away; might have struck him; but as soon as he had made sure Madame Svyetlov was not there, he may have run away rejoicing that she was not there and that he had not killed his father.", "그가 거기 있었다고 해서 어찌 그가 살인을 저질렀다는 결론이 나옵니까? 그는 안으로 뛰어들어 방들을 가로질러 달렸을 수도 있고, 아버지를 밀쳤을 수도 있고, 아버지를 쳤을 수도 있습니다."], ["And it was perhaps just because he had escaped from the temptation to kill his father, because he had a clear conscience and was rejoicing at not having killed him, that he was capable of a pure feeling, the feeling of pity and compassion, and leapt off the fence a minute later to the assistance of Grigory after he had, in his excitement, knocked him down.", "그러나 스베틀로바 부인이 거기 없다는 것을 확인하자마자, 그는 그녀가 없다는 것에, 그리고 자기가 아버지를 죽이지 않았다는 것에 기뻐하며 달아났을 수도 있습니다. 그리고 어쩌면 그가 바로 아버지를 죽이려는 유혹에서 벗어났기 때문에, 떳떳한 양심을 가지고 아버지를 죽이지 않은 것을 기뻐하고 있었기 때문에, 그는 순수한 감정, 곧 연민과 동정의 감정을 느낄 수 있었고, 흥분한 나머지 그리고리를 쓰러뜨린 뒤 일 분쯤 지나 담장에서 뛰어내려 그를 도우러 갔던 것입니다."]], [["“With terrible eloquence the prosecutor has described to us the dreadful state of the prisoner’s mind at Mokroe when love again lay before him calling him to new life, while love was impossible for him because he had his father’s bloodstained corpse behind him and beyond that corpse—retribution.", "검사는 무서운 웅변으로, 모크로예에서 사랑이 다시 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 새 삶으로 그를 부르고 있었으나 그에게는 사랑이 불가능했던, 피고의 마음의 끔찍한 상태를 우리에게 묘사해 주었습니다. 그의 등 뒤에는 피투성이가 된 아버지의 시신이 있었고, 그 시신 너머에는—응보가 있었기 때문이라는 것이지요."], ["And yet the prosecutor allowed him love, which he explained, according to his method, talking about his drunken condition, about a criminal being taken to execution, about it being still far off, and so on and so on.", "그러면서도 검사는 그에게 사랑을 허락했는데, 그는 자기 방법에 따라 그것을 설명하면서 그가 취해 있었던 상태에 대해, 처형장으로 끌려가는 죄수에 대해, 그것이 아직 멀었다는 것에 대해, 운운, 운운 늘어놓았습니다."], ["But again I ask, Mr. Prosecutor, have you not invented a new personality?", "그러나 저는 다시 묻습니다, 검사님, 당신은 새로운 인격을 지어내신 것이 아닙니까?"], ["Is the prisoner so coarse and heartless as to be able to think at that moment of love and of dodges to escape punishment, if his hands were really stained with his father’s blood? No, no, no!", "피고가, 만일 그의 손이 정말로 아버지의 피로 물들어 있었다면, 바로 그 순간에 사랑을 생각하고 처벌을 모면할 잔꾀를 생각할 수 있을 만큼 그토록 거칠고 무정한 자입니까?"], ["As soon as it was made plain to him that she loved him and called him to her side, promising him new happiness, oh!", "아니요, 아니요, 아닙니다! 그녀가 그를 사랑하며 그를 자기 곁으로 부르고 새로운 행복을 약속한다는 것이 그에게 분명해지자마자, 오!"], ["then, I protest he must have felt the impulse to suicide doubled, trebled, and must have killed himself, if he had his father’s murder on his conscience.", "단언컨대 그는 자살하고 싶은 충동을 두 배로, 세 배로 느꼈을 것이며, 만일 아버지의 살해를 양심에 짊어지고 있었다면 틀림없이 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 것입니다. 오, 아닙니다!"], ["Oh, no! he would not have forgotten where his pistols lay!", "그는 자기 권총이 어디 놓여 있는지 잊지 않았을 것입니다!"], ["I know the prisoner: the savage, stony heartlessness ascribed to him by the prosecutor is inconsistent with his character.", "저는 피고를 압니다. 검사가 그에게 돌린 야만적이고 돌처럼 차가운 무정함은 그의 성격과 맞지 않습니다."], ["He would have killed himself, that’s certain.", "그는 스스로 목숨을 끊었을 것입니다, 그것은 분명합니다."], ["He did not kill himself just because ‘his mother’s prayers had saved him,’ and he was innocent of his father’s blood.", "그가 자살하지 않은 것은 다만 ‘어머니의 기도가 그를 구했기’ 때문이며, 그가 아버지의 피에 죄가 없었기 때문입니다."], ["He was troubled, he was grieving that night at Mokroe only about old Grigory and praying to God that the old man would recover, that his blow had not been fatal, and that he would not have to suffer for it.", "그날 밤 모크로예에서 그가 괴로워하고 슬퍼한 것은 오로지 늙은 그리고리 때문이었으며, 그 노인이 회복되기를, 자기가 가한 일격이 치명적이지 않기를, 그래서 자기가 그 때문에 고통받지 않아도 되기를 하느님께 기도하고 있었던 것입니다."], ["Why not accept such an interpretation of the facts?", "어찌하여 사실에 대한 이런 해석을 받아들이지 못한단 말입니까?"], ["What trustworthy proof have we that the prisoner is lying?", "피고가 거짓말을 하고 있다는 무슨 믿을 만한 증거를 우리가 가지고 있습니까?"]], [["“But we shall be told at once again, ‘There is his father’s corpse!", "그러나 우리는 또 즉각 이런 말을 듣게 될 것입니다. ‘저기 그의 아버지의 시신이 있지 않은가!"], ["If he ran away without murdering him, who did murder him?’ Here, I repeat, you have the whole logic of the prosecution.", "그가 죽이지 않고 달아났다면, 대체 누가 그를 죽였단 말인가?’ 여기에, 거듭 말씀드리지만, 검찰 측 논리의 전부가 있습니다."], ["Who murdered him, if not he?", "그가 아니라면 누가 그를 죽였느냐는 것이지요."], ["There’s no one to put in his place.", "그의 자리에 세울 사람이 아무도 없다는 것입니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, is that really so?", "배심원 여러분, 정말로 그러합니까?"], ["Is it positively, actually true that there is no one else at all?", "정말로, 실제로, 다른 사람이 아무도 없다는 것이 사실입니까?"], ["We’ve heard the prosecutor count on his fingers all the persons who were in that house that night.", "우리는 검사가 그날 밤 그 집에 있던 모든 사람을 손가락으로 꼽는 것을 들었습니다."], ["They were five in number; three of them, I agree, could not have been responsible—the murdered man himself, old Grigory, and his wife.", "그들은 다섯 명이었으며, 그중 셋은—살해된 본인, 늙은 그리고리, 그리고 그의 아내는—책임이 있을 수 없다는 데 저도 동의합니다."], ["There are left then the prisoner and Smerdyakov, and the prosecutor dramatically exclaims that the prisoner pointed to Smerdyakov because he had no one else to fix on, that had there been a sixth person, even a phantom of a sixth person, he would have abandoned the charge against Smerdyakov at once in shame and have accused that other.", "그러면 피고와 스메르댜코프가 남는데, 검사는 극적으로 외치기를, 피고가 달리 지목할 사람이 없었기에 스메르댜코프를 가리킨 것이며, 만일 여섯 번째 사람이, 여섯 번째 사람의 환영이라도 있었다면 그는 부끄러움에 당장 스메르댜코프에 대한 고발을 거두고 그 다른 사람을 고발했을 것이라고 합니다."], ["But, gentlemen of the jury, why may I not draw the very opposite conclusion?", "그러나 배심원 여러분, 어찌하여 저는 정반대의 결론을 끌어내서는 안 됩니까?"], ["There are two persons—the prisoner and Smerdyakov.", "두 사람이 있습니다—피고와 스메르댜코프입니다."], ["Why can I not say that you accuse my client, simply because you have no one else to accuse?", "어찌하여 저는, 당신들이 달리 고발할 사람이 없기에 다만 제 의뢰인을 고발하는 것이라고 말해서는 안 됩니까?"], ["And you have no one else only because you have determined to exclude Smerdyakov from all suspicion.", "그리고 당신들에게 달리 고발할 사람이 없는 것은 오로지 당신들이 스메르댜코프를 모든 혐의에서 배제하기로 작정했기 때문입니다."]], [["“It’s true, indeed, Smerdyakov is accused only by the prisoner, his two brothers, and Madame Svyetlov.", "스메르댜코프를 고발하는 사람이 피고와 그의 두 형제, 그리고 스베틀로바 부인뿐인 것은 사실입니다."], ["But there are others who accuse him: there are vague rumors of a question, of a suspicion, an obscure report, a feeling of expectation.", "그러나 그를 고발하는 다른 것들이 있습니다. 막연한 의문의 소문, 의혹, 흐릿한 풍문, 무언가 기대하는 느낌이 있습니다."], ["Finally, we have the evidence of a combination of facts very suggestive, though, I admit, inconclusive.", "끝으로, 우리에게는 비록 결정적이지는 않다는 점을 인정하지만 매우 암시적인 정황들의 결합이라는 증거가 있습니다."], ["In the first place we have precisely on the day of the catastrophe that fit, for the genuineness of which the prosecutor, for some reason, has felt obliged to make a careful defense.", "첫째로, 다름 아닌 그 참사의 날에 일어난 그 발작이 있는데, 검사는 무슨 까닭에선지 그 진정성을 위해 애써 변호해야 한다고 느꼈습니다."], ["Then Smerdyakov’s sudden suicide on the eve of the trial.", "다음으로, 재판 전날 스메르댜코프가 별안간 자살한 일이 있습니다."], ["Then the equally startling evidence given in court to-day by the elder of the prisoner’s brothers, who had believed in his guilt, but has to-day produced a bundle of notes and proclaimed Smerdyakov as the murderer.", "다음으로, 그의 유죄를 믿어 왔으나 오늘 한 묶음의 지폐를 내놓으며 스메르댜코프를 살인자로 선언한, 피고의 큰형이 오늘 법정에서 한 못지않게 놀라운 증언이 있습니다."], ["Oh, I fully share the court’s and the prosecutor’s conviction that Ivan Karamazov is suffering from brain fever, that his statement may really be a desperate effort, planned in delirium, to save his brother by throwing the guilt on the dead man.", "오, 저는 이반 카라마조프가 뇌염을 앓고 있으며, 그의 진술이 정말로 섬망 속에서 꾸며진, 죽은 사람에게 죄를 떠넘겨 동생을 구하려는 필사적인 노력일 수 있다는 법정과 검사의 확신에 전적으로 동의합니다. 그러나 또다시 스메르댜코프의 이름이 입에 오르고, 또다시 어떤 수수께끼의 암시가 있습니다."], ["But again Smerdyakov’s name is pronounced, again there is a suggestion of mystery.", "설명되지 않은, 미완의 무언가가 있는 것입니다."], ["There is something unexplained, incomplete.", "그리고 그것은 어쩌면 언젠가 밝혀질지도 모릅니다."], ["And perhaps it may one day be explained.", "그러나 지금은 그 문제로 들어가지 않겠습니다."], ["But we won’t go into that now. Of that later.", "그것은 나중에 이야기하지요."]], [["“The court has resolved to go on with the trial, but, meantime, I might make a few remarks about the character-sketch of Smerdyakov drawn with subtlety and talent by the prosecutor.", "법정은 재판을 계속 진행하기로 결정했으나, 그동안에 저는 검사가 정교함과 재능으로 그려낸 스메르댜코프의 성격 묘사에 대해 몇 마디 말씀드리고자 합니다."], ["But while I admire his talent I cannot agree with him.", "그러나 저는 그의 재능에 감탄하면서도 그에게 동의할 수는 없습니다."], ["I have visited Smerdyakov, I have seen him and talked to him, and he made a very different impression on me.", "저는 스메르댜코프를 찾아가, 그를 보고 그와 이야기를 나누었는데, 그는 제게 사뭇 다른 인상을 주었습니다."], ["He was weak in health, it is true; but in character, in spirit, he was by no means the weak man the prosecutor has made him out to be.", "그가 건강이 허약했던 것은 사실입니다. 그러나 성격에서, 정신에서, 그는 결코 검사가 그려낸 것처럼 나약한 인간이 아니었습니다."], ["I found in him no trace of the timidity on which the prosecutor so insisted.", "저는 그에게서 검사가 그토록 강조한 그 소심함의 흔적을 전혀 찾지 못했습니다."], ["There was no simplicity about him, either.", "그에게는 단순함 또한 없었습니다."], ["I found in him, on the contrary, an extreme mistrustfulness concealed under a mask of naïveté, and an intelligence of considerable range.", "오히려 저는 그에게서, 순박함의 가면 아래 감추어진 극도의 불신과 상당히 넓은 범위에 걸친 지력을 발견했습니다. 검사는 그를 어리석은 자로 여긴 점에서 너무 단순했습니다."], ["The prosecutor was too simple in taking him for weak-minded.", "그는 제게 매우 뚜렷한 인상을 주었습니다."], ["He made a very definite impression on me: I left him with the conviction that he was a distinctly spiteful creature, excessively ambitious, vindictive, and intensely envious.", "저는 그가 분명히 심술궂은 인간이며, 지나치게 야심에 차 있고, 앙심이 깊고, 몹시 시샘이 많은 자라는 확신을 안고 그와 헤어졌습니다. 저는 몇 가지를 알아보았습니다."], ["I made some inquiries: he resented his parentage, was ashamed of it, and would clench his teeth when he remembered that he was the son of ‘stinking Lizaveta.’ He was disrespectful to the servant Grigory and his wife, who had cared for him in his childhood.", "그는 자기 출신을 분하게 여겼고, 그것을 부끄러워했으며, 자기가 ‘냄새나는 리자베타’의 아들이라는 것을 떠올릴 때면 이를 악물곤 했습니다. 그는 어린 시절 자기를 돌봐 준 하인 그리고리와 그의 아내에게 무례하게 굴었습니다."], ["He cursed and jeered at Russia.", "그는 러시아를 저주하고 비웃었습니다."], ["He dreamed of going to France and becoming a Frenchman.", "그는 프랑스로 가서 프랑스인이 되기를 꿈꾸었습니다."], ["He used often to say that he hadn’t the means to do so.", "그는 자기에게 그럴 만한 재력이 없다고 자주 말하곤 했습니다."], ["I fancy he loved no one but himself and had a strangely high opinion of himself.", "짐작건대 그는 자기 자신밖에는 누구도 사랑하지 않았으며, 자기 자신에 대해 이상하리만치 높은 평가를 품고 있었습니다."], ["His conception of culture was limited to good clothes, clean shirt-fronts and polished boots.", "그의 교양에 대한 관념이라야 좋은 옷, 깨끗한 셔츠 앞섶, 잘 닦인 장화에 국한되어 있었습니다."], ["Believing himself to be the illegitimate son of Fyodor Pavlovitch (there is evidence of this), he might well have resented his position, compared with that of his master’s legitimate sons.", "자기가 표도르 파블로비치의 사생아라고 믿고 있었으니(이를 뒷받침하는 증거가 있습니다), 그는 주인의 적자들의 처지와 견주어 자기 처지를 분하게 여겼을 만합니다."], ["They had everything, he nothing.", "그들은 모든 것을 가졌고, 그는 아무것도 갖지 못했습니다."], ["They had all the rights, they had the inheritance, while he was only the cook.", "그들은 모든 권리를, 상속을 가졌으나, 그는 일개 요리사에 지나지 않았습니다."], ["He told me himself that he had helped Fyodor Pavlovitch to put the notes in the envelope.", "그는 자기가 표도르 파블로비치를 도와 봉투에 지폐를 넣었노라고 제게 직접 말했습니다."], ["The destination of that sum—a sum which would have made his career—must have been hateful to him.", "그 돈의 행방은—자기 앞날을 열어 줄 만한 액수의 돈이—그에게는 증오스러웠을 것이 틀림없습니다."], ["Moreover, he saw three thousand roubles in new rainbow-colored notes. (I asked him about that on purpose.) Oh, beware of showing an ambitious and envious man a large sum of money at once!", "게다가 그는 삼천 루블을 새 무지갯빛 지폐로 보았습니다.(저는 일부러 그것에 대해 그에게 물었습니다.) 오, 야심 많고 시샘 많은 사람에게 한꺼번에 큰돈을 보여 주는 것을 경계하십시오!"], ["And it was the first time he had seen so much money in the hands of one man.", "그리고 그가 한 사람의 손에 그토록 많은 돈이 들려 있는 것을 본 것은 그것이 처음이었습니다."], ["The sight of the rainbow-colored notes may have made a morbid impression on his imagination, but with no immediate results.", "그 무지갯빛 지폐의 광경은 그의 상상력에 병적인 인상을 남겼을 수 있으나, 당장의 결과를 낳지는 않았습니다."]], [["“The talented prosecutor, with extraordinary subtlety, sketched for us all the arguments for and against the hypothesis of Smerdyakov’s guilt, and asked us in particular what motive he had in feigning a fit.", "재능 있는 검사는 비상한 정교함으로 스메르댜코프의 유죄라는 가설에 찬성하는 논거와 반대하는 논거를 모두 우리에게 그려 보여 주었으며, 특히 그가 발작을 가장할 무슨 동기가 있었느냐고 우리에게 물었습니다."], ["But he may not have been feigning at all, the fit may have happened quite naturally, but it may have passed off quite naturally, and the sick man may have recovered, not completely perhaps, but still regaining consciousness, as happens with epileptics.", "그러나 그는 발작을 전혀 가장하지 않았을 수도 있으니, 그 발작은 지극히 자연스럽게 일어났을 수도 있으며, 또한 지극히 자연스럽게 지나갔을 수도 있고, 그 병자는 회복되었을 수도, 어쩌면 완전히는 아니지만 그래도 간질 환자에게 흔히 그러하듯 의식을 되찾았을 수도 있는 것입니다."]], [["“The prosecutor asks at what moment could Smerdyakov have committed the murder.", "검사는 어느 순간에 스메르댜코프가 살인을 저지를 수 있었겠느냐고 묻습니다."], ["But it is very easy to point out that moment.", "그러나 그 순간을 지목하기란 매우 쉽습니다."], ["He might have waked up from deep sleep (for he was only asleep—an epileptic fit is always followed by a deep sleep) at that moment when the old Grigory shouted at the top of his voice ‘Parricide!’ That shout in the dark and stillness may have waked Smerdyakov whose sleep may have been less sound at the moment: he might naturally have waked up an hour before.", "그는 늙은 그리고리가 목청껏 ‘아비 죽인 놈!’ 하고 외친 바로 그 순간에 깊은 잠에서 깨어났을 수 있습니다.(그는 그저 잠들어 있었을 뿐이니—간질 발작 뒤에는 언제나 깊은 잠이 따르는 법입니다.) 어둠과 정적 속의 그 외침이 그 순간 잠이 덜 깊었을 수도 있는 스메르댜코프를 깨웠을 수 있습니다. 그는 한 시간쯤 전에 자연스럽게 깨어났을 수도 있는 것입니다."]], [["“Getting out of bed, he goes almost unconsciously and with no definite motive towards the sound to see what’s the matter.", "침대에서 일어난 그는 거의 무의식적으로, 뚜렷한 동기도 없이, 무슨 일인가 보려고 그 소리 쪽으로 갑니다."], ["His head is still clouded with his attack, his faculties are half asleep; but, once in the garden, he walks to the lighted windows and he hears terrible news from his master, who would be, of course, glad to see him.", "그의 머리는 아직 발작으로 흐려 있고, 그의 정신력은 반쯤 잠들어 있습니다. 그러나 일단 정원에 나서자 그는 불 켜진 창문 쪽으로 걸어가, 물론 그를 보면 반가워할 주인에게서 끔찍한 소식을 듣습니다."], ["His mind sets to work at once.", "그의 머리는 즉각 돌아가기 시작합니다."], ["He hears all the details from his frightened master, and gradually in his disordered brain there shapes itself an idea—terrible, but seductive and irresistibly logical.", "그는 겁에 질린 주인에게서 온갖 세세한 사정을 듣고, 그의 어지러운 머릿속에 차츰 한 생각이—끔찍하지만, 유혹적이고 거역할 수 없이 논리적인 한 생각이—모습을 갖춰 갑니다."], ["To kill the old man, take the three thousand, and throw all the blame on to his young master.", "노인을 죽이고, 삼천 루블을 빼앗고, 모든 죄를 자기 젊은 주인에게 뒤집어씌우자는 것이지요."], ["A terrible lust of money, of booty, might seize upon him as he realized his security from detection.", "발각될 염려가 없다는 것을 깨달으면서, 돈과 노획물에 대한 무서운 탐욕이 그를 사로잡았을 수 있습니다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["these sudden and irresistible impulses come so often when there is a favorable opportunity, and especially with murderers who have had no idea of committing a murder beforehand.", "이런 갑작스럽고 거역할 수 없는 충동은 호기가 찾아왔을 때 너무도 자주 일어나는 법이며, 더욱이 미리 살인을 저지를 생각이 전혀 없었던 살인자들에게서 그러합니다."], ["And Smerdyakov may have gone in and carried out his plan.", "그리하여 스메르댜코프는 안으로 들어가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮겼을 수 있습니다."], ["With what weapon?", "무슨 무기로 말입니까?"], ["Why, with any stone picked up in the garden.", "그야, 정원에서 주운 아무 돌멩이로 그랬겠지요."], ["But what for, with what object?", "그러나 무엇 때문에, 무슨 목적으로 말입니까?"], ["Why, the three thousand which means a career for him.", "그야, 자기에게 앞날을 열어 줄 그 삼천 루블 때문이지요."], ["Oh, I am not contradicting myself—the money may have existed.", "오, 저는 제 말을 뒤집고 있는 것이 아닙니다—그 돈은 실재했을 수 있습니다."], ["And perhaps Smerdyakov alone knew where to find it, where his master kept it.", "그리고 어쩌면 스메르댜코프만이 그것을 어디서 찾을지, 주인이 그것을 어디 두는지 알고 있었을 것입니다."], ["And the covering of the money—the torn envelope on the floor?", "그렇다면 그 돈을 싸고 있던 것은—바닥에 떨어진 그 찢긴 봉투는 어찌 된 일입니까?"]], [["“Just now, when the prosecutor was explaining his subtle theory that only an inexperienced thief like Karamazov would have left the envelope on the floor, and not one like Smerdyakov, who would have avoided leaving a piece of evidence against himself, I thought as I listened that I was hearing something very familiar, and, would you believe it, I have heard that very argument, that very conjecture, of how Karamazov would have behaved, precisely two days before, from Smerdyakov himself.", "조금 전, 검사가 카라마조프처럼 미숙한 도둑이라야 봉투를 바닥에 남겨 두었을 것이며, 자기에게 불리한 증거 한 조각도 남기기를 피했을 스메르댜코프 같은 자라면 그러지 않았을 것이라는 자기의 정교한 논리를 설명하고 있을 때, 저는 그것을 들으며 무언가 매우 낯익은 것을 듣고 있다는 생각이 들었습니다. 그리고, 믿으시겠습니까, 저는 카라마조프가 어떻게 행동했을 것인가에 관한 바로 그 논거, 바로 그 추측을, 정확히 이틀 전에 스메르댜코프 자신에게서 들었던 것입니다."], ["What’s more, it struck me at the time.", "게다가 그것은 당시 제 마음에 걸렸습니다."], ["I fancied that there was an artificial simplicity about him; that he was in a hurry to suggest this idea to me that I might fancy it was my own.", "저는 그에게 무언가 꾸며 낸 듯한 단순함이 있다는 느낌을 받았습니다. 그가, 제가 그것을 제 생각이라고 여기도록, 그 생각을 제게 서둘러 암시하고 있다는 느낌을 받았던 것입니다."], ["He insinuated it, as it were.", "그는 말하자면 그것을 슬그머니 심어 놓았던 것입니다."], ["Did he not insinuate the same idea at the inquiry and suggest it to the talented prosecutor?", "그가 예심에서도 같은 생각을 슬그머니 심어 재능 있는 검사에게 암시하지 않았겠습니까?"]], [["“I shall be asked, ‘What about the old woman, Grigory’s wife?", "저는 이런 질문을 받게 될 것입니다. ‘그 노파, 그리고리의 아내는 어찌 된 일이냐?"], ["She heard the sick man moaning close by, all night.’ Yes, she heard it, but that evidence is extremely unreliable.", "그녀는 바로 가까이서 그 병자가 밤새도록 신음하는 소리를 들었다.’ 그렇습니다, 그녀는 그것을 들었습니다, 그러나 그 증언은 지극히 믿을 수 없는 것입니다."], ["I knew a lady who complained bitterly that she had been kept awake all night by a dog in the yard.", "저는 마당의 개 한 마리 때문에 밤새도록 잠을 못 이루었다고 호되게 불평하던 어느 부인을 알고 있었습니다."], ["Yet the poor beast, it appeared, had only yelped once or twice in the night.", "그런데 알고 보니 그 가엾은 짐승은 밤새 겨우 한두 번 짖었을 뿐이었습니다."], ["And that’s natural.", "그리고 그것은 당연한 일입니다."], ["If any one is asleep and hears a groan he wakes up, annoyed at being waked, but instantly falls asleep again.", "누군가 잠들어 있다가 신음을 들으면, 그는 잠을 깬 데 짜증을 내며 깨어났다가도 곧바로 다시 잠들어 버립니다."], ["Two hours later, again a groan, he wakes up and falls asleep again; and the same thing again two hours later—three times altogether in the night.", "두 시간 뒤에 또 한 번 신음이 들리면, 그는 깨어났다가 다시 잠듭니다. 그리고 또 두 시간 뒤에 똑같은 일이—밤새 도합 세 번 일어납니다."], ["Next morning the sleeper wakes up and complains that some one has been groaning all night and keeping him awake.", "다음 날 아침이면 그 잠든 이는 깨어나 누군가 밤새도록 신음하며 자기 잠을 방해했다고 불평합니다."], ["And it is bound to seem so to him: the intervals of two hours of sleep he does not remember, he only remembers the moments of waking, so he feels he has been waked up all night.", "그리고 그에게는 으레 그렇게 여겨질 수밖에 없으니, 두 시간씩의 잠의 간격은 기억하지 못하고 깨어 있던 순간만을 기억하는지라, 그는 밤새도록 잠을 깨우는 소리에 시달렸다고 느끼는 것입니다."]], [["“But why, why, asks the prosecutor, did not Smerdyakov confess in his last letter?", "그러나 어찌하여, 어찌하여, 하고 검사는 묻습니다, 스메르댜코프는 마지막 편지에서 자백하지 않았는가?"], ["Why did his conscience prompt him to one step and not to both?", "어찌하여 그의 양심은 그를 한 걸음으로는 이끌면서 두 걸음 모두로는 이끌지 않았는가?"], ["But, excuse me, conscience implies penitence, and the suicide may not have felt penitence, but only despair.", "그러나 죄송하지만, 양심이란 회개를 전제하는데, 그 자살자는 회개가 아니라 다만 절망을 느꼈을 수도 있습니다."], ["Despair and penitence are two very different things.", "절망과 회개는 매우 다른 두 가지입니다."], ["Despair may be vindictive and irreconcilable, and the suicide, laying his hands on himself, may well have felt redoubled hatred for those whom he had envied all his life.", "절망은 앙심에 차고 화해할 줄 모를 수 있으며, 그 자살자는 제 손으로 제 목숨을 끊으면서, 평생토록 시샘해 온 자들에 대한 증오를 곱절로 느꼈을 만합니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, beware of a miscarriage of justice!", "배심원 여러분, 오심을 경계하십시오!"], ["What is there unlikely in all I have put before you just now?", "제가 방금 여러분 앞에 펼쳐 놓은 이 모든 것에 무엇이 있을 법하지 않은 것이 있습니까?"], ["Find the error in my reasoning; find the impossibility, the absurdity.", "제 추론에서 오류를 찾아내십시오. 불가능함을, 터무니없음을 찾아내십시오."], ["And if there is but a shade of possibility, but a shade of probability in my propositions, do not condemn him.", "그리고 제 명제 속에 한 가닥의 가능성이라도, 한 가닥의 개연성이라도 있다면, 그를 단죄하지 마십시오."], ["And is there only a shade?", "그런데 과연 한 가닥뿐이겠습니까?"], ["I swear by all that is sacred, I fully believe in the explanation of the murder I have just put forward.", "저는 신성한 모든 것에 걸고 맹세하거니와, 제가 방금 제시한 그 살인에 관한 설명을 온전히 믿습니다."], ["What troubles me and makes me indignant is that of all the mass of facts heaped up by the prosecution against the prisoner, there is not a single one certain and irrefutable.", "저를 괴롭히고 분노케 하는 것은, 검찰 측이 피고에게 불리하게 쌓아 올린 그 산더미 같은 사실들 가운데 확실하고 반박할 수 없는 것이 단 하나도 없다는 점입니다."], ["And yet the unhappy man is to be ruined by the accumulation of these facts.", "그런데도 이 불행한 사람은 이 사실들의 누적으로 인해 파멸당하려 하고 있습니다."], ["Yes, the accumulated effect is awful: the blood, the blood dripping from his fingers, the bloodstained shirt, the dark night resounding with the shout ‘Parricide!’ and the old man falling with a broken head.", "그렇습니다, 그 누적된 효과는 무시무시합니다. 피, 그의 손가락에서 뚝뚝 떨어지는 피, 피로 물든 셔츠, ‘아비 죽인 놈!’ 하는 외침으로 울리는 어두운 밤, 그리고 머리가 깨진 채 쓰러지는 노인."], ["And then the mass of phrases, statements, gestures, shouts!", "그리고 그 산더미 같은 말들, 진술들, 몸짓들, 외침들!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["this has so much influence, it can so bias the mind; but, gentlemen of the jury, can it bias your minds?", "이것은 그토록 큰 영향을 미치며, 그토록 사람의 마음을 기울게 할 수 있습니다. 그러나 배심원 여러분, 그것이 여러분의 마음을 기울게 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Remember, you have been given absolute power to bind and to loose, but the greater the power, the more terrible its responsibility.", "기억하십시오, 여러분께는 묶고 푸는 절대적인 권능이 주어졌으나, 그 권능이 클수록 그 책임은 더욱 무서운 법입니다."]], [["“I do not draw back one iota from what I have said just now, but suppose for one moment I agreed with the prosecution that my luckless client had stained his hands with his father’s blood.", "저는 방금 말씀드린 것에서 한 치도 물러서지 않습니다. 그러나 한순간, 제 불운한 의뢰인이 자기 아버지의 피로 손을 더럽혔다는 검찰 측 주장에 제가 동의한다고 가정해 봅시다."], ["This is only hypothesis, I repeat; I never for one instant doubt of his innocence.", "거듭 말씀드리지만 이것은 어디까지나 가설입니다. 저는 단 한순간도 그의 무죄를 의심하지 않습니다."], ["But, so be it, I assume that my client is guilty of parricide.", "그러나 그렇다 치고, 제 의뢰인이 아비 살해의 죄를 지었다고 상정해 봅시다."], ["Even so, hear what I have to say.", "그렇다 하더라도, 제가 드릴 말씀을 들어 주십시오."], ["I have it in my heart to say something more to you, for I feel that there must be a great conflict in your hearts and minds....", "저는 여러분께 무언가 더 말씀드리고 싶은 것이 제 가슴속에 있으니, 여러분의 가슴과 머릿속에 큰 갈등이 있을 것임을 느끼기 때문입니다……"], ["Forgive my referring to your hearts and minds, gentlemen of the jury, but I want to be truthful and sincere to the end.", "여러분의 가슴과 머리를 거론하는 것을 용서해 주십시오, 배심원 여러분, 그러나 저는 끝까지 진실하고 진솔하고자 합니다."], ["Let us all be sincere!”", "우리 모두 진솔해집시다!”"]], [["At this point the speech was interrupted by rather loud applause.", "이 대목에서 연설은 제법 큰 박수 소리에 가로막혔다."], ["The last words, indeed, were pronounced with a note of such sincerity that every one felt that he really might have something to say, and that what he was about to say would be of the greatest consequence.", "실로 마지막 말은 어찌나 진솔한 가락으로 발음되었던지, 저마다 그가 정말로 할 말이 있으며 그가 막 하려는 말이 더없이 중대한 것이리라고 느꼈다."], ["But the President, hearing the applause, in a loud voice threatened to clear the court if such an incident were repeated.", "그러나 재판장은 박수 소리를 듣고 큰 목소리로, 만일 그런 일이 되풀이된다면 법정을 비우게 하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["Every sound was hushed and Fetyukovitch began in a voice full of feeling quite unlike the tone he had used hitherto.", "모든 소리가 잠잠해졌고, 페튜코비치는 여태껏 써 온 어조와는 사뭇 다른, 감정이 가득한 목소리로 입을 열었다."]], [["# Chapter XIII. A Corrupter Of Thought", "# Chapter XIII. A Corrupter Of Thought"]], [["“It’s not only the accumulation of facts that threatens my client with ruin, gentlemen of the jury,”", "\"배심원 여러분, 제 의뢰인을 파멸로 몰아가는 것은 비단 정황 증거들이 쌓였기 때문만이 아닙니다.\""], ["he began, “what is really damning for my client is one fact—the dead body of his father.", "그는 입을 열었다. \"제 의뢰인에게 정말로 치명적인 것은 단 하나의 사실, 곧 그 아버지의 시신입니다."], ["Had it been an ordinary case of murder you would have rejected the charge in view of the triviality, the incompleteness, and the fantastic character of the evidence, if you examine each part of it separately; or, at least, you would have hesitated to ruin a man’s life simply from the prejudice against him which he has, alas!", "만약 이것이 평범한 살인 사건이었다면, 여러분은 그 증거들을 하나하나 따로 떼어 살펴볼 적에 그 하찮음과 불완전함과 환상적인 성격을 헤아려 공소를 기각하셨을 것입니다. 적어도 그자에 대한 편견만으로—아, 그가 너무도 마땅히 받아 마땅한 그 편견만으로—한 인간의 생애를 망가뜨리는 데에는 주저하셨을 것입니다."], ["only too well deserved. But it’s not an ordinary case of murder, it’s a case of parricide.", "그러나 이것은 평범한 살인이 아니라 친부 살해 사건입니다."], ["That impresses men’s minds, and to such a degree that the very triviality and incompleteness of the evidence becomes less trivial and less incomplete even to an unprejudiced mind.", "그것이 사람들의 마음에 강한 인상을 남기되, 어찌나 강하게 남기는지 바로 그 증거의 하찮음과 불완전함조차도 편견 없는 마음에마저 덜 하찮고 덜 불완전한 것으로 비치는 것입니다."], ["How can such a prisoner be acquitted?", "어떻게 이런 피고를 무죄로 풀어 줄 수 있단 말인가?"], ["What if he committed the murder and gets off unpunished?", "만약 그가 살인을 저질렀는데 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나간다면 어쩔 것인가?"], ["That is what every one, almost involuntarily, instinctively, feels at heart.", "이것이 거의 모든 사람이 본의 아니게, 본능적으로 마음속에서 느끼는 바입니다."]], [["“Yes, it’s a fearful thing to shed a father’s blood—the father who has begotten me, loved me, not spared his life for me, grieved over my illnesses from childhood up, troubled all his life for my happiness, and has lived in my joys, in my successes.", "\"그렇습니다, 아버지의 피를 흘리게 한다는 것은 무서운 일입니다. 나를 낳아 주고, 나를 사랑해 주고, 나를 위해 제 목숨을 아끼지 않았으며, 어린 시절부터 내 병을 두고 애태우고, 평생토록 내 행복을 위해 노심초사하고, 내 기쁨과 내 성공 속에서 함께 살아온 아버지를."], ["To murder such a father—that’s inconceivable.", "그런 아버지를 죽인다는 것—그것은 상상조차 할 수 없는 일입니다."], ["Gentlemen of the jury, what is a father—a real father?", "배심원 여러분, 아버지란 무엇입니까—참된 아버지란?"], ["What is the meaning of that great word?", "그 위대한 단어의 뜻은 무엇입니까?"], ["What is the great idea in that name?", "그 이름 안에 담긴 위대한 관념은 무엇입니까?"], ["We have just indicated in part what a true father is and what he ought to be.", "참된 아버지란 무엇이며 마땅히 어떠해야 하는가를 우리는 방금 부분적으로나마 짚어 보았습니다."], ["In the case in which we are now so deeply occupied and over which our hearts are aching—in the present case, the father, Fyodor Pavlovitch Karamazov, did not correspond to that conception of a father to which we have just referred.", "우리가 지금 이토록 깊이 매달려 있고 우리 마음을 아프게 하는 이 사건에서—이 사건에서, 아버지인 표도르 파블로비치 카라마조프는 우리가 방금 언급한 그 아버지의 관념에 부합하지 않았습니다."], ["That’s the misfortune.", "그것이 바로 불행입니다."], ["And indeed some fathers are a misfortune.", "실로 어떤 아버지들은 하나의 불행입니다."], ["Let us examine this misfortune rather more closely: we must shrink from nothing, gentlemen of the jury, considering the importance of the decision you have to make.", "이 불행을 좀 더 가까이 들여다보기로 합시다. 배심원 여러분, 여러분이 내려야 할 결정의 중대함을 헤아린다면 우리는 그 무엇 앞에서도 움츠러들어서는 안 됩니다."], ["It’s our particular duty not to shrink from any idea, like children or frightened women, as the talented prosecutor happily expresses it.", "재능 있는 검사께서 멋지게 표현하셨듯이, 어린아이나 겁먹은 여자들처럼 그 어떤 생각 앞에서도 움츠러들지 않는 것이 우리의 각별한 의무입니다."]], [["“But in the course of his heated speech my esteemed opponent (and he was my opponent before I opened my lips) exclaimed several times, ‘Oh, I will not yield the defense of the prisoner to the lawyer who has come down from Petersburg. I accuse, but I defend also!’ He exclaimed that several times, but forgot to mention that if this terrible prisoner was for twenty-three years so grateful for a mere pound of nuts given him by the only man who had been kind to him, as a child in his father’s house, might not such a man well have remembered for twenty-three years how he ran in his father’s back-yard, ‘without boots on his feet and with his little trousers hanging by one button’—to use the expression of the kind-hearted doctor, Herzenstube?", "\"그러나 저의 존경하는 적수께서는—그분은 제가 입을 열기도 전에 이미 저의 적수였습니다만—그 열변을 토하시는 중에 몇 번이나 이렇게 외치셨습니다. '오, 나는 페테르부르크에서 내려온 변호사에게 피고의 변호를 양보하지 않겠다. 나는 고발하지만 또한 변호도 하노라!' 그분은 그렇게 여러 번 외치셨으나, 한 가지만은 언급하기를 잊으셨습니다. 만약 이 무시무시한 피고가 어린 시절 아버지의 집에서 자기에게 친절을 베푼 유일한 사람에게 받은 한 줌의 호두 때문에 이십삼 년 동안이나 그토록 감사하는 마음을 품었다면, 그런 사람이라면 아버지의 뒷마당을 '발에는 장화도 신지 않고 작은 바지는 단추 하나에 매달린 채로' 뛰어다니던 일을—마음씨 고운 의사 게르첸슈투베의 표현을 빌리자면—이십삼 년 동안 능히 기억하고 있지 않았겠습니까?"]], [["“Oh, gentlemen of the jury, why need we look more closely at this misfortune, why repeat what we all know already?", "\"오, 배심원 여러분, 우리가 어찌하여 이 불행을 더 자세히 들여다봐야 하며, 어찌하여 우리 모두가 이미 알고 있는 것을 되풀이해야겠습니까?"], ["What did my client meet with when he arrived here, at his father’s house, and why depict my client as a heartless egoist and monster?", "제 의뢰인이 이곳, 아버지의 집에 당도했을 때 무엇과 마주쳤습니까? 그런데 어찌하여 제 의뢰인을 무정한 이기주의자요 괴물로 그려 내려 하는 것입니까?"], ["He is uncontrolled, he is wild and unruly—we are trying him now for that—but who is responsible for his life?", "그는 자제할 줄 모르고, 거칠고 다스리기 어려운 사람입니다—우리는 지금 그 점을 두고 그를 재판하고 있습니다—그러나 그의 삶에 책임이 있는 자는 누구입니까?"], ["Who is responsible for his having received such an unseemly bringing up, in spite of his excellent disposition and his grateful and sensitive heart?", "그가 그토록 빼어난 천성과 감사할 줄 아는 예민한 마음을 지녔음에도 그토록 꼴사나운 양육을 받게 된 데에 책임이 있는 자는 누구입니까?"], ["Did any one train him to be reasonable?", "누가 그를 사리에 밝은 사람으로 길렀습니까?"], ["Was he enlightened by study?", "누가 그를 학문으로 깨우쳐 주었습니까?"], ["Did any one love him ever so little in his childhood?", "어린 시절 누가 그를 조금이라도 사랑해 주었습니까?"], ["My client was left to the care of Providence like a beast of the field.", "제 의뢰인은 들판의 짐승처럼 섭리의 손에 내맡겨졌습니다."], ["He thirsted perhaps to see his father after long years of separation.", "그는 어쩌면 오랜 세월의 이별 끝에 아버지를 보고 싶어 목말라했을 것입니다."], ["A thousand times perhaps he may, recalling his childhood, have driven away the loathsome phantoms that haunted his childish dreams and with all his heart he may have longed to embrace and to forgive his father!", "어쩌면 천 번이라도, 자신의 어린 시절을 떠올리며 어린 꿈을 따라다니던 혐오스러운 환영들을 쫓아내고, 온 마음을 다해 아버지를 끌어안고 용서하기를 갈망했을 것입니다! 그런데 그를 기다리고 있던 것은 무엇이었습니까?"], ["And what awaited him? He was met by cynical taunts, suspicions and wrangling about money.", "그를 맞이한 것은 냉소적인 조롱과 의심과 돈을 둘러싼 다툼이었습니다."], ["He heard nothing but revolting talk and vicious precepts uttered daily over the brandy, and at last he saw his father seducing his mistress from him with his own money.", "그는 날마다 브랜디 잔을 기울이며 내뱉어지는 역겨운 말과 사악한 훈계 외에는 아무것도 듣지 못했고, 마침내 자기 아버지가 자기 돈으로 자기 정부를 빼앗아 가는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Oh, gentlemen of the jury, that was cruel and revolting!", "오, 배심원 여러분, 그것은 잔혹하고 역겨운 일이었습니다!"], ["And that old man was always complaining of the disrespect and cruelty of his son.", "그런데도 그 늙은이는 아들의 불효와 잔인함을 늘 한탄했습니다."], ["He slandered him in society, injured him, calumniated him, bought up his unpaid debts to get him thrown into prison.", "사교계에서 그를 헐뜯고, 그에게 해를 끼치고, 그를 모함하고, 그를 감옥에 처넣으려고 그가 갚지 못한 빚을 사들이기까지 했습니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, people like my client, who are fierce, unruly, and uncontrolled on the surface, are sometimes, most frequently indeed, exceedingly tender-hearted, only they don’t express it.", "\"배심원 여러분, 제 의뢰인과 같은 사람들, 겉으로는 사납고 다스리기 어렵고 자제할 줄 모르는 사람들은 때로, 아니 실로 아주 흔히, 더없이 다정한 마음을 지니고 있되 다만 그것을 드러내지 못할 뿐입니다."], ["Don’t laugh, don’t laugh at my idea!", "웃지 마십시오, 제 생각을 비웃지 마십시오!"], ["The talented prosecutor laughed mercilessly just now at my client for loving Schiller—loving the sublime and beautiful!", "재능 있는 검사께서는 방금 제 의뢰인이 실러를 사랑한다고—숭고하고 아름다운 것을 사랑한다고 해서 가차 없이 비웃으셨습니다!"], ["I should not have laughed at that in his place.", "제가 그분의 처지였다면 그것을 비웃지 않았을 것입니다."], ["Yes, such natures—oh, let me speak in defense of such natures, so often and so cruelly misunderstood—these natures often thirst for tenderness, goodness, and justice, as it were, in contrast to themselves, their unruliness, their ferocity—they thirst for it unconsciously.", "그렇습니다, 그런 천성들은—오, 그토록 자주 그토록 가혹하게 오해받는 그런 천성들을 변호하게 해 주십시오—이런 천성들은 흔히 자기 자신과, 자신의 다스릴 수 없음과 자신의 사나움과 대비되는 듯한 다정함과 선함과 정의를 갈망하되, 그것을 무의식적으로 갈망합니다."], ["Passionate and fierce on the surface, they are painfully capable of loving woman, for instance, and with a spiritual and elevated love.", "겉으로는 격정적이고 사납지만, 이를테면 여자를 사랑할 때면 고통스러우리만치 깊이, 그것도 정신적이고 고결한 사랑으로 사랑할 수 있는 것입니다."], ["Again do not laugh at me, this is very often the case in such natures.", "거듭 말하지만 저를 비웃지 마십시오. 이는 그런 천성들에서 아주 흔히 있는 일입니다."], ["But they cannot hide their passions—sometimes very coarse—and that is conspicuous and is noticed, but the inner man is unseen.", "그러나 그들은 자기 격정을—때로는 아주 거친 격정을—숨기지 못하니, 바로 그것이 눈에 띄고 사람들의 주목을 받지만, 그 속사람은 보이지 않는 것입니다. 그들의 격정은 빠르게 소진됩니다."], ["Their passions are quickly exhausted; but, by the side of a noble and lofty creature that seemingly coarse and rough man seeks a new life, seeks to correct himself, to be better, to become noble and honorable, ‘sublime and beautiful,’ however much the expression has been ridiculed.", "그러나 고귀하고 드높은 존재 곁에서 그 겉보기에 거칠고 투박한 사람은 새로운 삶을 찾으며, 자신을 바로잡고 더 나아지고 고귀하고 명예로운 사람이 되어, 그 표현이 아무리 조롱당했을지라도 '숭고하고 아름다운' 사람이 되기를 추구합니다."]], [["“I said just now that I would not venture to touch upon my client’s engagement.", "\"저는 방금 제 의뢰인의 약혼에 관해서는 감히 건드리지 않겠다고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["But I may say half a word.", "그러나 한마디만 하겠습니다."], ["What we heard just now was not evidence, but only the scream of a frenzied and revengeful woman, and it was not for her—oh, not for her!—to reproach him with treachery, for she has betrayed him!", "우리가 방금 들은 것은 증언이 아니라 광란에 빠진 복수심에 찬 한 여인의 비명에 지나지 않았으며, 그를 배신으로 책망할 자격은—오, 그녀에게는 결코 없었습니다!—다름 아닌 그녀였으니, 그녀야말로 그를 배신한 것이기 때문입니다!"], ["If she had had but a little time for reflection she would not have given such evidence.", "그녀에게 조금이라도 곰곰이 생각할 시간이 있었더라면 그런 증언은 하지 않았을 것입니다."], ["Oh, do not believe her!", "오, 그녀의 말을 믿지 마십시오!"], ["No, my client is not a monster, as she called him!", "아닙니다, 제 의뢰인은 그녀가 부른 것처럼 괴물이 아닙니다!"]], [["“The Lover of Mankind on the eve of His Crucifixion said: ‘I am the Good Shepherd.", "\"인류를 사랑하신 분께서 십자가에 못 박히시기 전날 말씀하셨습니다. '나는 선한 목자라."], ["The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep, so that not one of them might be lost.’ Let not a man’s soul be lost through us!", "선한 목자는 양들을 위하여 목숨을 버리나니, 그중 하나도 잃지 않게 하려 함이라.' 우리로 말미암아 한 인간의 영혼이 잃어지지 않게 하소서!"]], [["“I asked just now what does ‘father’ mean, and exclaimed that it was a great word, a precious name.", "\"저는 방금 '아버지'란 무엇을 뜻하는가를 물으며, 그것이 위대한 단어요 소중한 이름이라고 외쳤습니다."], ["But one must use words honestly, gentlemen, and I venture to call things by their right names: such a father as old Karamazov cannot be called a father and does not deserve to be.", "그러나 여러분, 우리는 말을 정직하게 써야 하니, 저는 감히 사물을 그 마땅한 이름으로 부르고자 합니다. 카라마조프 노인과 같은 아버지는 아버지라 부를 수 없으며 그럴 자격도 없습니다."], ["Filial love for an unworthy father is an absurdity, an impossibility.", "무가치한 아버지에 대한 자식의 사랑이란 부조리요, 불가능입니다."], ["Love cannot be created from nothing: only God can create something from nothing.", "사랑은 무에서 만들어질 수 없으니, 오직 하느님만이 무에서 무언가를 창조하실 수 있습니다."]], [["“‘Fathers, provoke not your children to wrath,’ the apostle writes, from a heart glowing with love.", "\"'아비들아, 너희 자녀를 노엽게 하지 말라.' 사도는 사랑으로 타오르는 마음에서 이렇게 적었습니다."], ["It’s not for the sake of my client that I quote these sacred words, I mention them for all fathers.", "제가 이 거룩한 말씀을 인용하는 것은 제 의뢰인을 위해서가 아니라 모든 아버지를 위해서입니다."], ["Who has authorized me to preach to fathers?", "누가 제게 아버지들에게 설교할 권한을 주었습니까? 아무도 없습니다."], ["No one. But as a man and a citizen I make my appeal—vivos voco!", "그러나 한 사람으로서, 한 시민으로서 저는 호소합니다—비보스 보코!"], ["We are not long on earth, we do many evil deeds and say many evil words.", "우리는 이 땅에 오래 머물지 않으며, 수많은 악한 일을 행하고 수많은 악한 말을 합니다."], ["So let us all catch a favorable moment when we are all together to say a good word to each other.", "그러니 우리 모두 함께 모인 이 좋은 기회를 붙잡아 서로에게 좋은 말 한마디를 건넵시다. 저는 지금 그것을 하고 있습니다."], ["That’s what I am doing: while I am in this place I take advantage of my opportunity.", "이 자리에 서 있는 동안 저는 제 기회를 이용하는 것입니다. 이 연단이 최고의 권위로부터 우리에게 주어진 것은 헛된 일이 아니니—온 러시아가 우리의 말을 듣고 있습니다!"], ["Not for nothing is this tribune given us by the highest authority—all Russia hears us!", "저는 이 자리에 계신 아버지들에게만 말하는 것이 아니라, 모든 아버지에게 큰 소리로 외칩니다."], ["I am not speaking only for the fathers here present, I cry aloud to all fathers: ‘Fathers, provoke not your children to wrath.’ Yes, let us first fulfill Christ’s injunction ourselves and only then venture to expect it of our children.", "'아비들아, 너희 자녀를 노엽게 하지 말라.' 그렇습니다, 우리가 먼저 그리스도의 계명을 스스로 이행하고 난 다음에야 비로소 우리 자녀에게 그것을 기대할 수 있습니다."], ["Otherwise we are not fathers, but enemies of our children, and they are not our children, but our enemies, and we have made them our enemies ourselves.", "그러지 않으면 우리는 자녀의 아버지가 아니라 자녀의 원수이며, 그들은 우리의 자녀가 아니라 우리의 원수이니, 우리 스스로 그들을 우리의 원수로 만든 것입니다."], ["‘What measure ye mete it shall be measured unto you again’—it’s not I who say that, it’s the Gospel precept, measure to others according as they measure to you.", "'너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라'—이는 제가 하는 말이 아니라 복음서의 가르침으로, 남이 너희를 헤아리는 만큼 너희도 남을 헤아리라는 것입니다."], ["How can we blame children if they measure us according to our measure?", "자녀들이 우리가 헤아린 만큼 우리를 헤아린다 한들 어찌 우리가 그들을 탓할 수 있겠습니까?"]], [["“Not long ago a servant girl in Finland was suspected of having secretly given birth to a child.", "\"얼마 전 핀란드에서 한 하녀가 몰래 아이를 낳았다는 의심을 받았습니다."], ["She was watched, and a box of which no one knew anything was found in the corner of the loft, behind some bricks.", "사람들이 그녀를 감시했는데, 다락방 한구석 벽돌 더미 뒤에서 아무도 모르던 상자 하나가 발견되었습니다."], ["It was opened and inside was found the body of a new-born child which she had killed.", "그것을 열어 보니 안에는 그녀가 죽인 갓난아이의 시신이 들어 있었습니다."], ["In the same box were found the skeletons of two other babies which, according to her own confession, she had killed at the moment of their birth.", "그 같은 상자 안에서 다른 두 아기의 유골도 발견되었는데, 그녀 자신의 자백에 따르면 그 아기들도 태어나는 순간에 죽였다는 것이었습니다."]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, was she a mother to her children?", "\"배심원 여러분, 그 여자가 제 아이들에게 어머니였습니까?"], ["She gave birth to them, indeed; but was she a mother to them?", "그녀가 아이들을 낳기는 했습니다. 그러나 그녀가 아이들에게 어머니였습니까?"], ["Would any one venture to give her the sacred name of mother?", "누가 감히 그녀에게 어머니라는 거룩한 이름을 붙이겠습니까? 여러분, 우리 대담해집시다, 아니 과감해지기까지 합시다."], ["Let us be bold, gentlemen, let us be audacious even: it’s our duty to be so at this moment and not to be afraid of certain words and ideas like the Moscow women in Ostrovsky’s play, who are scared at the sound of certain words.", "지금 이 순간 그렇게 하는 것이 우리의 의무이며, 오스트롭스키의 희곡에 나오는 모스크바 여인들처럼 어떤 말이나 생각 앞에서 두려워해서는 안 됩니다. 그들은 어떤 말소리에만 들어도 겁을 집어먹지요."], ["No, let us prove that the progress of the last few years has touched even us, and let us say plainly, the father is not merely he who begets the child, but he who begets it and does his duty by it.", "아니, 우리는 지난 몇 해의 진보가 우리에게까지 닿았음을 증명하고, 분명히 말합시다. 아버지란 단지 아이를 낳은 자가 아니라, 아이를 낳고 그 아이에 대한 의무를 다하는 자라고."]], [["“Oh, of course, there is the other meaning, there is the other interpretation of the word ‘father,’ which insists that any father, even though he be a monster, even though he be the enemy of his children, still remains my father simply because he begot me.", "\"오, 물론 다른 의미도, '아버지'라는 단어의 다른 해석도 있습니다. 그것은 어떤 아버지든, 비록 그가 괴물일지라도, 비록 그가 제 자녀의 원수일지라도, 나를 낳았다는 이유만으로 여전히 나의 아버지라고 주장합니다."], ["But this is, so to say, the mystical meaning which I cannot comprehend with my intellect, but can only accept by faith, or, better to say, on faith, like many other things which I do not understand, but which religion bids me believe.", "그러나 이것은, 말하자면, 제 지성으로는 헤아릴 수 없고 오직 믿음으로, 아니 더 정확히 말하자면 믿음에 기대어 받아들일 수밖에 없는 신비한 의미입니다. 제가 이해하지는 못하나 종교가 믿으라 명하는 다른 많은 것들이 그러하듯 말입니다."], ["But in that case let it be kept outside the sphere of actual life.", "그러나 그렇다면 그것은 실제 삶의 영역 바깥에 두기로 합시다."], ["In the sphere of actual life, which has, indeed, its own rights, but also lays upon us great duties and obligations, in that sphere, if we want to be humane—Christian, in fact—we must, or ought to, act only upon convictions justified by reason and experience, which have been passed through the crucible of analysis; in a word, we must act rationally, and not as though in dream and delirium, that we may not do harm, that we may not ill-treat and ruin a man.", "실제 삶의 영역은, 실로 그 나름의 권리를 지니면서도 또한 우리에게 큰 의무와 책무를 지우거니와, 만약 우리가 인간적이고자 한다면—실로 그리스도교적이고자 한다면—그 영역에서 우리는 이성과 경험으로 정당화되고 분석의 도가니를 거친 확신에 따라서만 행동해야 하며, 또 마땅히 그래야 합니다. 한마디로 우리는 합리적으로 행동해야 하며, 꿈속이나 헛소리 속에서 하듯이 해서는 안 됩니다."], ["Then it will be real Christian work, not only mystic, but rational and philanthropic....”", "그래야만 우리가 한 인간에게 해를 끼치지 않고, 학대하지 않고, 망가뜨리지 않을 수 있습니다. 그래야 비로소 그것이 신비적일 뿐만 아니라 합리적이고 박애적인, 참된 그리스도교적 행위가 될 것입니다…….\""]], [["There was violent applause at this passage from many parts of the court, but Fetyukovitch waved his hands as though imploring them to let him finish without interruption.", "이 대목에서 법정 곳곳에서 격렬한 박수가 터져 나왔으나, 페튜코비치는 방해받지 않고 끝까지 말하게 해 달라고 간청하듯 두 손을 내저었다."], ["The court relapsed into silence at once.", "법정은 곧 다시 정적에 잠겼다."], ["The orator went on.", "변사는 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Do you suppose, gentlemen, that our children as they grow up and begin to reason can avoid such questions?", "\"여러분, 우리 자녀들이 자라나 사리를 따지기 시작할 때 이런 질문들을 피할 수 있다고 생각하십니까? 아닙니다, 그들은 피할 수 없으며, 우리도 그들에게 불가능한 제약을 강요하지 않을 것입니다."], ["No, they cannot, and we will not impose on them an impossible restriction.", "무가치한 아버지를 보는 것은 어린 존재에게 본의 아니게 괴로운 질문들을 떠올리게 합니다. 더욱이 자기 친구들의 훌륭한 아버지들과 그를 견주어 볼 때면 그러합니다."], ["The sight of an unworthy father involuntarily suggests tormenting questions to a young creature, especially when he compares him with the excellent fathers of his companions.", "이 질문에 대한 통상적인 대답은 이렇습니다. '그가 너를 낳았으니 너는 그의 살이요 피라, 그러므로 너는 마땅히 그를 사랑해야 한다.'"], ["The conventional answer to this question is: ‘He begot you, and you are his flesh and blood, and therefore you are bound to love him.’ The youth involuntarily reflects: ‘But did he love me when he begot me?’ he asks, wondering more and more.", "그러면 그 젊은이는 본의 아니게 곰곰이 생각합니다. '그러나 그가 나를 낳을 때 나를 사랑했던가?'"], ["‘Was it for my sake he begot me?", "그는 점점 더 의아해하며 묻습니다. '그가 나를 위해 나를 낳았던가?"], ["He did not know me, not even my sex, at that moment, at the moment of passion, perhaps, inflamed by wine, and he has only transmitted to me a propensity to drunkenness—that’s all he’s done for me....", "그 순간, 어쩌면 술에 달아오른 정욕의 순간에 그는 나를 알지 못했으며, 심지어 내 성별조차 알지 못했다. 그리고 그가 내게 물려준 것이라곤 술에 빠지는 기질뿐이니—그것이 나를 위해 그가 한 일의 전부다……."], ["Why am I bound to love him simply for begetting me when he has cared nothing for me all my life after?’", "그 후 평생토록 나를 조금도 돌보지 않은 그를, 단지 나를 낳았다는 이유만으로 내가 어찌 사랑할 의무가 있단 말인가?'"]], [["“Oh, perhaps those questions strike you as coarse and cruel, but do not expect an impossible restraint from a young mind.", "\"오, 어쩌면 이런 질문들이 여러분에게는 거칠고 잔인하게 들릴지 모릅니다. 그러나 어린 마음에서 불가능한 자제를 기대하지 마십시오."], ["‘Drive nature out of the door and it will fly in at the window,’ and, above all, let us not be afraid of words, but decide the question according to the dictates of reason and humanity and not of mystic ideas.", "'천성을 문밖으로 내쫓으면 그것은 창문으로 날아 들어온다'고 합니다. 그러니 무엇보다도 말을 두려워하지 말고, 이 질문을 신비한 관념이 아니라 이성과 인간애의 명령에 따라 판단합시다."], ["How shall it be decided?", "어떻게 판단해야 할까요?"], ["Why, like this.", "바로 이렇습니다."], ["Let the son stand before his father and ask him, ‘Father, tell me, why must I love you?", "아들이 아버지 앞에 서서 이렇게 묻게 하십시오. '아버지, 제가 왜 아버지를 사랑해야 하는지 말씀해 주십시오."], ["Father, show me that I must love you,’ and if that father is able to answer him and show him good reason, we have a real, normal, parental relation, not resting on mystical prejudice, but on a rational, responsible and strictly humanitarian basis.", "아버지, 제가 아버지를 사랑해야 한다는 것을 보여 주십시오.' 만약 그 아버지가 아들에게 대답하여 정당한 이유를 보여 줄 수 있다면, 우리는 신비한 편견에 기댄 것이 아니라 합리적이고 책임 있으며 엄정하게 인도주의적인 토대 위에 선, 참되고 정상적인 부자 관계를 갖게 되는 것입니다."], ["But if he does not, there’s an end to the family tie.", "그러나 그가 보여 주지 못한다면, 가족의 유대는 거기서 끝입니다."], ["He is not a father to him, and the son has a right to look upon him as a stranger, and even an enemy.", "그는 아들에게 아버지가 아니며, 아들은 그를 타인으로, 심지어 원수로 여길 권리가 있습니다."], ["Our tribune, gentlemen of the jury, ought to be a school of true and sound ideas.”", "배심원 여러분, 우리의 이 연단은 참되고 건전한 사상의 학교가 되어야 합니다.\""]], [["(Here the orator was interrupted by irrepressible and almost frantic applause.", "(이 대목에서 변사는 억누를 수 없는, 거의 광적인 박수 때문에 말을 멈춰야 했다."], ["Of course, it was not the whole audience, but a good half of it applauded.", "물론 청중 전체는 아니었으나 그 절반쯤은 박수를 보냈다."], ["The fathers and mothers present applauded.", "자리에 있던 아버지들과 어머니들이 박수를 쳤다."], ["Shrieks and exclamations were heard from the gallery, where the ladies were sitting.", "부인들이 앉아 있던 위층 관람석에서는 비명과 탄성이 터져 나왔다."], ["Handkerchiefs were waved.", "손수건들이 나부꼈다."], ["The President began ringing his bell with all his might.", "재판장은 있는 힘껏 종을 울리기 시작했다."], ["He was obviously irritated by the behavior of the audience, but did not venture to clear the court as he had threatened.", "그는 청중의 행태에 분명 짜증이 나 있었으나, 앞서 위협했던 대로 법정을 비우게 할 엄두는 내지 못했다."], ["Even persons of high position, old men with stars on their breasts, sitting on specially reserved seats behind the judges, applauded the orator and waved their handkerchiefs.", "재판관들 뒤편 특별석에 앉아 있던, 가슴에 훈장을 단 고관대작 노인들마저 변사에게 박수를 보내며 손수건을 흔들었다."], ["So that when the noise died down, the President confined himself to repeating his stern threat to clear the court, and Fetyukovitch, excited and triumphant, continued his speech.)", "그리하여 소란이 잦아들자 재판장은 법정을 비우게 하겠다는 준엄한 위협을 되풀이하는 데 그쳤고, 흥분하여 의기양양해진 페튜코비치는 연설을 이어 갔다.)"]], [["“Gentlemen of the jury, you remember that awful night of which so much has been said to-day, when the son got over the fence and stood face to face with the enemy and persecutor who had begotten him.", "\"배심원 여러분, 오늘 그토록 많이 거론된 그 무시무시한 밤을 여러분은 기억하실 것입니다. 그날 밤 아들은 울타리를 넘어, 자기를 낳은 그 원수요 박해자와 얼굴을 마주하고 섰습니다."], ["I insist most emphatically it was not for money he ran to his father’s house: the charge of robbery is an absurdity, as I proved before.", "저는 가장 단호하게 주장합니다. 그가 아버지의 집으로 달려간 것은 돈 때문이 아니었습니다."], ["And it was not to murder him he broke into the house, oh, no!", "강도질이라는 혐의는, 제가 앞서 증명했듯이 부조리입니다."], ["If he had had that design he would, at least, have taken the precaution of arming himself beforehand.", "그리고 그가 집 안으로 침입한 것도 아버지를 죽이기 위해서가 아니었습니다, 오, 아닙니다!"], ["The brass pestle he caught up instinctively without knowing why he did it.", "만약 그런 의도가 있었다면 그는 적어도 미리 무장하는 신중함이라도 보였을 것입니다."], ["Granted that he deceived his father by tapping at the window, granted that he made his way in—I’ve said already that I do not for a moment believe that legend, but let it be so, let us suppose it for a moment.", "그 놋쇠 절굿공이는 그가 왜 그러는지도 모른 채 본능적으로 집어 든 것입니다. 그가 창문을 두드려 아버지를 속였다고 칩시다, 그가 안으로 들어갔다고 칩시다—저는 그 전설을 한순간도 믿지 않는다고 이미 말씀드렸으나, 그렇다고 칩시다, 잠시 그렇게 가정해 봅시다."], ["Gentlemen, I swear to you by all that’s holy, if it had not been his father, but an ordinary enemy, he would, after running through the rooms and satisfying himself that the woman was not there, have made off, post-haste, without doing any harm to his rival.", "여러분, 저는 거룩한 모든 것을 두고 여러분께 맹세합니다. 만약 그가 아버지가 아니라 평범한 원수였다면, 그는 방들을 두루 살펴 그 여자가 거기 없음을 확인한 뒤, 자기 연적에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않고 부리나케 달아났을 것입니다."], ["He would have struck him, pushed him away perhaps, nothing more, for he had no thought and no time to spare for that.", "그는 그를 한 대 치거나 어쩌면 밀쳐 냈을지언정 그 이상은 하지 않았을 것이니, 그럴 생각도 그럴 겨를도 없었기 때문입니다."], ["What he wanted to know was where she was. But his father, his father!", "그가 알고 싶었던 것은 그녀가 어디 있느냐 하는 것뿐이었습니다."], ["The mere sight of the father who had hated him from his childhood, had been his enemy, his persecutor, and now his unnatural rival, was enough!", "그러나 그의 아버지, 그의 아버지였습니다! 어린 시절부터 그를 미워했고, 그의 원수였고, 그의 박해자였으며, 이제는 그의 패륜적인 연적이 된 그 아버지를 보는 것만으로도 충분했습니다!"], ["A feeling of hatred came over him involuntarily, irresistibly, clouding his reason.", "본의 아니게, 거역할 수 없이 증오의 감정이 그를 덮쳐 그의 이성을 흐려 놓았습니다."], ["It all surged up in one moment!", "모든 것이 한순간에 솟구쳐 올랐던 것입니다!"], ["It was an impulse of madness and insanity, but also an impulse of nature, irresistibly and unconsciously (like everything in nature) avenging the violation of its eternal laws.", "그것은 광기와 정신착란의 충동이었으나, 동시에 자연의 충동이기도 했으니, (자연 속의 모든 것이 그러하듯) 거역할 수 없이 무의식적으로 제 영원한 법칙이 침해된 것에 복수하는 충동이었습니다."]], [["“But the prisoner even then did not murder him—I maintain that, I cry that aloud!—no, he only brandished the pestle in a burst of indignant disgust, not meaning to kill him, not knowing that he would kill him.", "\"그러나 피고는 그때조차 그를 죽이지 않았습니다—저는 그것을 주장합니다, 저는 그것을 큰 소리로 외칩니다!—아닙니다, 그는 다만 분노에 찬 혐오감이 북받쳐 절굿공이를 휘둘렀을 뿐, 그를 죽일 생각도 없었고 자기가 그를 죽이리라는 것도 알지 못했습니다."], ["Had he not had this fatal pestle in his hand, he would have only knocked his father down perhaps, but would not have killed him.", "그의 손에 그 운명적인 절굿공이가 들려 있지 않았더라면 그는 어쩌면 아버지를 때려눕히기만 했을 뿐 죽이지는 않았을 것입니다."], ["As he ran away, he did not know whether he had killed the old man.", "달아나면서도 그는 자기가 노인을 죽였는지 어떤지 알지 못했습니다."], ["Such a murder is not a murder.", "그런 살인은 살인이 아닙니다."], ["Such a murder is not a parricide.", "그런 살인은 친부 살해가 아닙니다."], ["No, the murder of such a father cannot be called parricide.", "아닙니다, 그런 아버지를 죽인 것은 친부 살해라 부를 수 없습니다."], ["Such a murder can only be reckoned parricide by prejudice.", "그런 살인은 오직 편견에 의해서만 친부 살해로 셈해질 수 있을 따름입니다."]], [["“But I appeal to you again and again from the depths of my soul; did this murder actually take place?", "\"그러나 저는 제 영혼 깊은 곳으로부터 거듭거듭 여러분께 호소합니다. 이 살인이 실제로 일어나기는 했습니까?"], ["Gentlemen of the jury, if we convict and punish him, he will say to himself: ‘These people have done nothing for my bringing up, for my education, nothing to improve my lot, nothing to make me better, nothing to make me a man.", "배심원 여러분, 만약 우리가 그를 유죄로 판결하고 처벌한다면, 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 것입니다. '이 사람들은 나를 길러 주기 위해서도, 나를 가르치기 위해서도 아무것도 하지 않았고, 내 처지를 낫게 하려고도, 나를 더 나은 인간으로 만들려고도, 나를 사람답게 만들려고도 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["These people have not given me to eat and to drink, have not visited me in prison and nakedness, and here they have sent me to penal servitude.", "이 사람들은 내게 먹을 것과 마실 것도 주지 않았고, 옥에 갇히고 헐벗었을 때 나를 찾아보지도 않았으면서, 이제 와서는 나를 징역살이로 보내는구나. 이제 나는 셈을 다 치렀으니, 더는 그들에게 빚진 것이 없고, 앞으로 영원히 그 누구에게도 빚진 것이 없다."], ["I am quits, I owe them nothing now, and owe no one anything for ever.", "그들이 악하니 나도 악해지리라. 그들이 잔인하니 나도 잔인해지리라.'"], ["They are wicked and I will be wicked.", "배심원 여러분, 그는 그렇게 말할 것입니다."], ["They are cruel and I will be cruel.’ That is what he will say, gentlemen of the jury.", "그리고 저는 맹세합니다. 그를 유죄로 판결함으로써 여러분은 오직 그를 더 편하게 해 줄 뿐입니다."], ["And I swear, by finding him guilty you will only make it easier for him: you will ease his conscience, he will curse the blood he has shed and will not regret it.", "여러분은 그의 양심을 가볍게 해 줄 것이며, 그는 자기가 흘린 피를 저주하면서도 그것을 뉘우치지는 않을 것입니다."], ["At the same time you will destroy in him the possibility of becoming a new man, for he will remain in his wickedness and blindness all his life.", "동시에 여러분은 그가 새사람이 될 가능성을 그 안에서 파괴할 것이니, 그는 평생토록 자신의 악함과 눈멂 속에 머물러 있을 것이기 때문입니다."]], [["“But do you want to punish him fearfully, terribly, with the most awful punishment that could be imagined, and at the same time to save him and regenerate his soul?", "\"그러나 여러분은 그를 무섭게, 끔찍하게, 상상할 수 있는 가장 무서운 형벌로 처벌하면서도, 동시에 그를 구원하고 그 영혼을 다시 태어나게 하기를 원하십니까?"], ["If so, overwhelm him with your mercy!", "그렇다면 여러분의 자비로 그를 압도하십시오!"], ["You will see, you will hear how he will tremble and be horror-struck.", "여러분은 보실 것이며, 그가 어떻게 떨며 두려움에 사로잡히는지를 들으실 것입니다."], ["‘How can I endure this mercy?", "'내가 어떻게 이 자비를 감당할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["How can I endure so much love?", "내가 어떻게 이토록 큰 사랑을 감당할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Am I worthy of it?’ That’s what he will exclaim.", "내가 그럴 자격이 있는가?' 그는 그렇게 외칠 것입니다."]], [["“Oh, I know, I know that heart, that wild but grateful heart, gentlemen of the jury!", "\"오, 저는 압니다, 저는 그 마음을, 그 거칠지만 감사할 줄 아는 마음을 압니다, 배심원 여러분!"], ["It will bow before your mercy; it thirsts for a great and loving action, it will melt and mount upwards.", "그 마음은 여러분의 자비 앞에 머리를 숙일 것입니다. 그것은 위대하고 사랑에 찬 행위를 갈망하니, 녹아내려 위로 솟아오를 것입니다."], ["There are souls which, in their limitation, blame the whole world.", "어떤 영혼들은 그 편협함 때문에 온 세상을 탓합니다."], ["But subdue such a soul with mercy, show it love, and it will curse its past, for there are many good impulses in it.", "그러나 그런 영혼을 자비로 다스리고 사랑을 보여 주면, 그것은 제 과거를 저주할 것이니, 그 안에는 선한 충동들이 많이 깃들어 있기 때문입니다. 그런 마음은 활짝 열려, 하느님은 자비로우시며 사람들은 선하고 의롭다는 것을 깨달을 것입니다."], ["Such a heart will expand and see that God is merciful and that men are good and just.", "그는 두려움에 사로잡힐 것이며, 회한과 이제부터 자기에게 지워진 그 막대한 책무에 짓눌릴 것입니다."], ["He will be horror-stricken; he will be crushed by remorse and the vast obligation laid upon him henceforth.", "그리고 그때 그는 '나는 셈을 다 치렀다'고 말하지 않고, '나는 모든 사람 앞에서 죄인이며, 그 누구보다도 무가치한 자다'라고 말할 것입니다. 참회의 눈물과 애절하고 다정한 고뇌에 젖어 그는 외칠 것입니다."], ["And he will not say then, ‘I am quits,’ but will say, ‘I am guilty in the sight of all men and am more unworthy than all.’ With tears of penitence and poignant, tender anguish, he will exclaim: ‘Others are better than I, they wanted to save me, not to ruin me!’ Oh, this act of mercy is so easy for you, for in the absence of anything like real evidence it will be too awful for you to pronounce: ‘Yes, he is guilty.’", "'다른 사람들은 나보다 낫구나, 그들은 나를 망치려 한 것이 아니라 나를 구하려 한 것이었구나!' 오, 이 자비의 행위는 여러분에게 참으로 쉬운 일이니, 진정한 증거라 할 만한 것이 아무것도 없는 마당에 '그렇다, 그는 유죄다'라고 선고하기란 여러분에게 너무도 무서운 일일 것이기 때문입니다."]], [["“Better acquit ten guilty men than punish one innocent man!", "\"죄인 열 명을 풀어 주는 것이 무고한 한 사람을 벌하는 것보다 낫습니다!"], ["Do you hear, do you hear that majestic voice from the past century of our glorious history?", "들리십니까, 우리의 영광스러운 역사의 지난 세기로부터 울려오는 저 장엄한 목소리가 들리십니까?"], ["It is not for an insignificant person like me to remind you that the Russian court does not exist for the punishment only, but also for the salvation of the criminal!", "러시아의 법정이 처벌만을 위해서가 아니라 죄인의 구원을 위해서도 존재한다는 것을 여러분께 일깨우는 일이 저 같은 보잘것없는 사람에게 어울리는 바는 아닙니다! 다른 민족들이 응보와 법조문에 매달리게 두십시오."], ["Let other nations think of retribution and the letter of the law, we will cling to the spirit and the meaning—the salvation and the reformation of the lost.", "우리는 그 정신과 그 의미에—잃어버린 자의 구원과 갱생에 매달릴 것입니다. 만약 이것이 참이라면, 만약 러시아와 그 정의가 그러하다면, 러시아는 기쁜 마음으로 앞으로 나아가도 좋습니다!"], ["If this is true, if Russia and her justice are such, she may go forward with good cheer!", "모든 민족이 혐오스럽게 비켜서는 여러분의 광란하는 트로이카로 우리를 겁주려 하지 마십시오."], ["Do not try to scare us with your frenzied troikas from which all the nations stand aside in disgust.", "폭주하는 트로이카가 아니라 위풍당당한 러시아의 마차가 침착하고 장엄하게 그 목표를 향해 나아갈 것입니다."], ["Not a runaway troika, but the stately chariot of Russia will move calmly and majestically to its goal.", "여러분의 손에 제 의뢰인의 운명이, 여러분의 손에 러시아 정의의 운명이 달려 있습니다."], ["In your hands is the fate of my client, in your hands is the fate of Russian justice.", "여러분은 그것을 지켜 낼 것이며, 그것을 구원할 것이며, 그것을 지키고 보살필 사람들이 있다는 것을, 그것이 좋은 손에 맡겨져 있다는 것을 증명할 것입니다!\""], ["You will defend it, you will save it, you will prove that there are men to watch over it, that it is in good hands!”", "* 비보스 보코(vivos voco): \"나는 산 자들을 부른다\"는 뜻의 라틴어로, 실러의 시 「종의 노래」에 인용되어 널리 알려진 교회 종에 새긴 명문에서 따온 것이다."]], [["# Chapter XIV. The Peasants Stand Firm", "# Chapter XIV. The Peasants Stand Firm"]], [["This was how Fetyukovitch concluded his speech, and the enthusiasm of the audience burst like an irresistible storm.", "페튜코비치는 이렇게 연설을 맺었고, 청중의 열광은 걷잡을 수 없는 폭풍처럼 터져 나왔다. 그것을 가라앉힌다는 것은 도무지 불가능한 일이었다."], ["It was out of the question to stop it: the women wept, many of the men wept too, even two important personages shed tears.", "여자들은 울었고, 남자들 가운데서도 적잖은 이들이 울었으며, 심지어 지체 높은 인사 두 사람마저 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["The President submitted, and even postponed ringing his bell.", "재판장도 결국 손을 들고는 종을 울리는 것조차 미루었다."], ["The suppression of such an enthusiasm would be the suppression of something sacred, as the ladies cried afterwards.", "그런 열광을 억누른다는 것은 무언가 신성한 것을 억누르는 일이라고, 부인들은 나중에 외쳐 댔다."], ["The orator himself was genuinely touched.", "연설가 자신도 진심으로 감동한 모습이었다."]], [["And it was at this moment that Ippolit Kirillovitch got up to make certain objections.", "바로 그 순간 이폴리트 키릴로비치가 몇 가지 반론을 펴려고 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["People looked at him with hatred. “What?", "사람들은 증오 어린 눈초리로 그를 바라보았다."], ["What’s the meaning of it?", "\"뭐라고? 저게 무슨 짓이야?"], ["He positively dares to make objections,”", "정말로 반론을 펼 작정인가 봐.\""], ["the ladies babbled.", "부인들이 종알거렸다."], ["But if the whole world of ladies, including his wife, had protested he could not have been stopped at that moment.", "그러나 그 순간만큼은 온 세상 부인들이, 그의 아내까지 합세하여 항의한다 한들 그를 멈출 수는 없었으리라."], ["He was pale, he was shaking with emotion, his first phrases were even unintelligible, he gasped for breath, could hardly speak clearly, lost the thread.", "그는 창백했고, 격정에 떨고 있었으며, 첫마디는 아예 알아들을 수도 없었고, 숨을 헐떡이며 또렷하게 말하지 못한 채 갈피를 잃었다."], ["But he soon recovered himself.", "그러나 그는 곧 평정을 되찾았다."], ["Of this new speech of his I will quote only a few sentences.", "그의 이 새로운 연설에서 나는 다만 몇 문장만을 인용하기로 하겠다."]], [["“... I am reproached with having woven a romance.", "\"…저는 한 편의 소설을 지어냈다고 비난받고 있습니다."], ["But what is this defense if not one romance on the top of another? All that was lacking was poetry.", "그러나 이 변론이야말로 한 편의 소설 위에 또 한 편의 소설을 쌓아 올린 것이 아니고 무엇이겠습니까?"], ["Fyodor Pavlovitch, while waiting for his mistress, tears open the envelope and throws it on the floor.", "거기에 빠진 것이라곤 운문뿐이었습니다. 표도르 파블로비치는 정부를 기다리던 중에 봉투를 찢어 마룻바닥에 던졌습니다."], ["We are even told what he said while engaged in this strange act.", "우리는 그가 이 기이한 짓을 하면서 무슨 말을 했는지까지 듣게 되었습니다."], ["Is not this a flight of fancy?", "이것이야말로 상상의 비약이 아니겠습니까?"], ["And what proof have we that he had taken out the money?", "그가 돈을 꺼냈다는 무슨 증거라도 우리에게 있습니까?"], ["Who heard what he said?", "그가 한 말을 들은 자가 누구입니까?"], ["The weak-minded idiot, Smerdyakov, transformed into a Byronic hero, avenging society for his illegitimate birth—isn’t this a romance in the Byronic style?", "저 정신박약의 백치 스메르자코프가 사생아로 태어난 데 대해 사회에 복수하는 바이런풍 영웅으로 둔갑하다니—이것이야말로 바이런식 소설이 아니겠습니까?"], ["And the son who breaks into his father’s house and murders him without murdering him is not even a romance—this is a sphinx setting us a riddle which he cannot solve himself.", "그리고 아비의 집에 침입하여 죽이되 죽이지 않으면서 살해했다는 그 아들은, 소설조차도 못 됩니다—이것은 제 손으로도 풀지 못할 수수께끼를 우리에게 던지는 스핑크스입니다."], ["If he murdered him, he murdered him, and what’s the meaning of his murdering him without having murdered him—who can make head or tail of this?", "그가 죽였다면 죽인 것이지, 죽이지 않으면서 죽였다는 것이 도대체 무슨 말입니까—이걸 무슨 수로 앞뒤를 가려낼 수 있단 말입니까?"]], [["“Then we are admonished that our tribune is a tribune of true and sound ideas and from this tribune of ‘sound ideas’ is heard a solemn declaration that to call the murder of a father ‘parricide’ is nothing but a prejudice!", "\"그다음 우리는 우리의 법정이 진실하고 건전한 사상의 법정이라는 훈계를 받았으며, 이 '건전한 사상'의 법정에서 아비를 죽이는 일을 '친부 살해'라 부르는 것이 한낱 편견에 지나지 않는다는 엄숙한 선언이 들려왔습니다!"], ["But if parricide is a prejudice, and if every child is to ask his father why he is to love him, what will become of us?", "그러나 친부 살해가 편견이라면, 그리고 모든 자식이 제 아비에게 어찌하여 당신을 사랑해야 하느냐고 따져 묻게 된다면, 우리는 대체 어찌 되겠습니까?"], ["What will become of the foundations of society?", "사회의 기초는 어찌 되겠습니까? 가정은 어찌 되겠습니까?"], ["What will become of the family? Parricide, it appears, is only a bogy of Moscow merchants’ wives.", "친부 살해란, 알고 보니, 모스크바 상인 마누라들이나 무서워하는 도깨비에 불과하다는 것입니다."], ["The most precious, the most sacred guarantees for the destiny and future of Russian justice are presented to us in a perverted and frivolous form, simply to attain an object—to obtain the justification of something which cannot be justified.", "러시아 사법의 운명과 미래를 위한 가장 소중하고 가장 신성한 보증이, 한 가지 목적을 이루기 위해서—곧 도저히 정당화될 수 없는 무언가를 정당화하기 위해서—왜곡되고 경박한 모습으로 우리 앞에 제시되고 있습니다. '오, 자비로써 그를 짓눌러 주소서.'"], ["‘Oh, crush him by mercy,’ cries the counsel for the defense; but that’s all the criminal wants, and to-morrow it will be seen how much he is crushed.", "변호인은 이렇게 외칩니다. 그러나 그것이야말로 바로 그 범죄자가 바라는 바이며, 내일이면 그가 얼마나 짓눌렸는지 드러날 것입니다."], ["And is not the counsel for the defense too modest in asking only for the acquittal of the prisoner?", "게다가 변호인은 한낱 피고인의 무죄 방면만을 청하다니, 너무 겸손한 것이 아니겠습니까?"], ["Why not found a charity in the honor of the parricide to commemorate his exploit among future generations?", "차라리 이 친부 살해자를 기려 그 위업을 후세에 길이 전할 자선 사업이라도 세우는 게 어떻겠습니까?"], ["Religion and the Gospel are corrected—that’s all mysticism, we are told, and ours is the only true Christianity which has been subjected to the analysis of reason and common sense.", "종교와 복음서는 수정되었습니다—그런 것은 죄다 신비주의이며, 이성과 상식의 분석을 거친 우리의 것이야말로 유일하게 참된 기독교라고 우리는 듣습니다."], ["And so they set up before us a false semblance of Christ!", "그리하여 그들은 우리 앞에 그리스도의 거짓 형상을 세워 놓는 것입니다!"], ["‘What measure ye mete so it shall be meted unto you again,’ cried the counsel for the defense, and instantly deduces that Christ teaches us to measure as it is measured to us—and this from the tribune of truth and sound sense!", "'너희가 헤아리는 그 헤아림으로 너희도 헤아림을 받을 것이니라.' 변호인은 이렇게 외치고는, 곧장 그리스도께서 우리가 헤아림을 받는 대로 헤아리라고 가르치셨다는 결론을 끌어냅니다—그것도 진실과 건전한 양식의 법정에서 말입니다!"], ["We peep into the Gospel only on the eve of making speeches, in order to dazzle the audience by our acquaintance with what is, anyway, a rather original composition, which may be of use to produce a certain effect—all to serve the purpose!", "우리가 복음서를 들여다보는 것은 오직 연설을 앞둔 전날 밤뿐이니, 어쨌거나 제법 독창적인 작품에 대한 우리의 식견을 과시하여 청중의 눈을 어지럽히고, 어떤 효과를 거두는 데 써먹어 보려는 것입니다—죄다 그 목적에 봉사하려는 것이지요!"], ["But what Christ commands us is something very different: He bids us beware of doing this, because the wicked world does this, but we ought to forgive and to turn the other cheek, and not to measure to our persecutors as they measure to us.", "그러나 그리스도께서 우리에게 명하신 바는 그것과는 전혀 다른 것입니다. 그분은 우리에게 이렇게 하기를 삼가라 이르셨으니, 사악한 세상이 이렇게 하는 까닭이며, 우리는 마땅히 용서하고 다른 뺨을 돌려 대야지, 우리를 박해하는 자들이 우리를 헤아리는 대로 그들을 헤아려서는 안 된다 하셨습니다."], ["This is what our God has taught us and not that to forbid children to murder their fathers is a prejudice.", "이것이야말로 우리의 하느님께서 우리에게 가르치신 바이며, 자식들이 제 아비를 죽이는 것을 금하는 것이 편견이라는 따위가 아닙니다."], ["And we will not from the tribune of truth and good sense correct the Gospel of our Lord, Whom the counsel for the defense deigns to call only ‘the crucified lover of humanity,’ in opposition to all orthodox Russia, which calls to Him, ‘For Thou art our God!’”", "그리고 우리는 진실과 건전한 양식의 법정에서 우리 주님의 복음서를 수정하지 않을 것이니, 변호인은 정통 러시아 전체에 맞서 그분을 그저 '십자가에 못 박힌 인류의 연인'이라고만 부르기를 황송하게도 좋아하지만, 정통 러시아는 그분을 향해 이렇게 부르짖습니다. '이는 당신이 우리의 하느님이시기 때문입니다!'\""]], [["At this the President intervened and checked the over-zealous speaker, begging him not to exaggerate, not to overstep the bounds, and so on, as presidents always do in such cases.", "이 대목에서 재판장이 끼어들어 지나치게 열을 올리는 연설가를 제지하며, 과장하지 말 것을, 도를 넘지 말 것을 청했고, 그 밖에도 그런 경우에 재판장들이 으레 하는 말들을 늘어놓았다."], ["The audience, too, was uneasy.", "청중 역시 술렁였다."], ["The public was restless: there were even exclamations of indignation.", "방청객들은 안절부절못했고, 분개하는 외침마저 터져 나왔다."], ["Fetyukovitch did not so much as reply; he only mounted the tribune to lay his hand on his heart and, with an offended voice, utter a few words full of dignity.", "페튜코비치는 굳이 답하지도 않았다."], ["He only touched again, lightly and ironically, on “romancing” and “psychology,”", "그는 다만 단상에 올라 가슴에 손을 얹고는, 모욕당한 목소리로 위엄에 찬 몇 마디를 내뱉었다."], ["and in an appropriate place quoted, “Jupiter, you are angry, therefore you are wrong,” which provoked a burst of approving laughter in the audience, for Ippolit Kirillovitch was by no means like Jupiter.", "그는 다만 \"소설 짓기\"와 \"심리\"를 다시금 가볍고 빈정대는 투로 건드렸을 뿐이며, 적절한 대목에서 \"유피테르여, 당신은 화를 내시니 곧 그르신 것입니다\"라는 말을 인용했는데, 이는 청중 가운데서 한바탕 찬동하는 웃음을 자아냈으니, 이폴리트 키릴로비치는 결코 유피테르 같은 인물이 못 되었기 때문이다."], ["Then, à propos of the accusation that he was teaching the young generation to murder their fathers, Fetyukovitch observed, with great dignity, that he would not even answer.", "그러고는 자기가 젊은 세대에게 제 아비를 죽이라고 가르친다는 비난과 관련하여, 페튜코비치는 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 그런 것에는 답조차 하지 않겠노라고 말했다."], ["As for the prosecutor’s charge of uttering unorthodox opinions, Fetyukovitch hinted that it was a personal insinuation and that he had expected in this court to be secure from accusations “damaging to my reputation as a citizen and a loyal subject.”", "검사가 그가 정통에 어긋나는 견해를 입에 담았다고 비난한 것에 대해서는, 페튜코비치는 그것이 개인적인 빈정거림이라는 점을 넌지시 비치며, 자신은 이 법정에서만큼은 \"한 시민이자 충성스러운 신민으로서의 내 명예를 훼손하는\" 비난으로부터 안전하리라 기대했었다고 말했다."], ["But at these words the President pulled him up, too, and Fetyukovitch concluded his speech with a bow, amid a hum of approbation in the court.", "그러나 이 말에 이르러 재판장이 그 역시 제지했고, 페튜코비치는 법정 안에 찬동의 웅성거림이 퍼지는 가운데 머리를 숙이며 연설을 맺었다."], ["And Ippolit Kirillovitch was, in the opinion of our ladies, “crushed for good.”", "그리고 이폴리트 키릴로비치는, 우리 부인들의 견해로는 \"아주 박살이 나고\" 말았다."]], [["Then the prisoner was allowed to speak.", "이어 피고인에게 발언이 허락되었다."], ["Mitya stood up, but said very little.", "미탸는 일어섰으나 거의 말을 하지 않았다."], ["He was fearfully exhausted, physically and mentally.", "그는 육체적으로나 정신적으로나 무섭도록 지쳐 있었다."], ["The look of strength and independence with which he had entered in the morning had almost disappeared.", "아침에 들어설 때 보였던 그 힘차고 당당한 기색은 거의 사라지고 없었다."], ["He seemed as though he had passed through an experience that day, which had taught him for the rest of his life something very important he had not understood till then.", "그는 그날 어떤 경험을 통과하면서, 그때까지 미처 깨닫지 못했던 무언가 지극히 중요한 것을 남은 평생을 두고 배운 사람처럼 보였다."], ["His voice was weak, he did not shout as before.", "그의 목소리는 힘이 없었고, 전처럼 소리치지도 않았다."], ["In his words there was a new note of humility, defeat and submission.", "그의 말에는 겸허와 패배와 굴복의 새로운 가락이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["“What am I to say, gentlemen of the jury?", "\"제가 무슨 말을 하겠습니까, 배심원 여러분?"], ["The hour of judgment has come for me, I feel the hand of God upon me!", "제게 심판의 시간이 닥쳐왔으니, 저는 하느님의 손길이 제게 임하신 것을 느낍니다!"], ["The end has come to an erring man!", "길을 잘못 든 한 인간에게 종말이 온 것입니다!"], ["But, before God, I repeat to you, I am innocent of my father’s blood!", "그러나 하느님 앞에서 다시 한번 여러분께 말씀드립니다, 저는 제 아비의 피에 대해서는 결백합니다!"], ["For the last time I repeat, it wasn’t I killed him!", "마지막으로 다시 말씀드립니다, 그를 죽인 것은 제가 아닙니다!"], ["I was erring, but I loved what is good.", "저는 길을 잘못 들었으나, 선한 것을 사랑했습니다."], ["Every instant I strove to reform, but I lived like a wild beast.", "매 순간 저는 새사람이 되려 애썼으나, 짐승처럼 살았습니다."], ["I thank the prosecutor, he told me many things about myself that I did not know; but it’s not true that I killed my father, the prosecutor is mistaken.", "저는 검사님께 감사드립니다, 그분은 제 자신에 대해 제가 알지 못했던 많은 것을 말씀해 주셨습니다. 그러나 제가 제 아비를 죽였다는 것은 사실이 아니며, 검사님은 잘못 알고 계십니다."], ["I thank my counsel, too.", "저는 제 변호인께도 감사드립니다."], ["I cried listening to him; but it’s not true that I killed my father, and he needn’t have supposed it.", "저는 그분의 말씀을 들으며 울었습니다. 그러나 제가 제 아비를 죽였다는 것은 사실이 아니니, 그분은 그렇게 짐작하시지 않으셨어야 했습니다."], ["And don’t believe the doctors.", "그리고 의사들의 말도 믿지 마십시오."], ["I am perfectly sane, only my heart is heavy.", "저는 더없이 멀쩡한 정신이며, 다만 가슴이 무거울 뿐입니다."], ["If you spare me, if you let me go, I will pray for you.", "저를 살려 주신다면, 저를 풀어 주신다면, 저는 여러분을 위해 기도하겠습니다."], ["I will be a better man.", "저는 더 나은 사람이 되겠습니다."], ["I give you my word before God I will!", "하느님 앞에서 여러분께 맹세코 그리하겠습니다!"], ["And if you will condemn me, I’ll break my sword over my head myself and kiss the pieces.", "그러나 만일 저를 단죄하신다면, 저는 제 손으로 제 머리 위에서 칼을 부러뜨리고 그 조각들에 입 맞추겠습니다."], ["But spare me, do not rob me of my God!", "그러나 저를 살려 주십시오, 제게서 제 하느님을 앗아 가지 마십시오!"], ["I know myself, I shall rebel!", "저는 제 자신을 압니다, 저는 반역할 것입니다!"], ["My heart is heavy, gentlemen ...", "가슴이 무겁습니다, 여러분…"], ["spare me!”", "저를 살려 주십시오!\""]], [["He almost fell back in his place: his voice broke: he could hardly articulate the last phrase.", "그는 거의 제자리에 쓰러지듯 주저앉았고, 목소리가 갈라졌으며, 마지막 말은 거의 또렷하게 발음하지 못했다."], ["Then the judges proceeded to put the questions and began to ask both sides to formulate their conclusions.", "이어 재판관들은 질문을 던지기 시작했고, 양측에 각각의 결론을 정식으로 진술하라고 요구하기 시작했다."]], [["But I will not describe the details.", "그러나 나는 그 시시콜콜한 절차를 묘사하지는 않겠다."], ["At last the jury rose to retire for consultation.", "마침내 배심원들이 협의를 위해 자리에서 일어났다."], ["The President was very tired, and so his last charge to the jury was rather feeble.", "재판장은 무척 지쳐 있었던 터라, 배심원에게 내린 마지막 당부도 다소 맥이 빠진 것이었다."], ["“Be impartial, don’t be influenced by the eloquence of the defense, but yet weigh the arguments.", "\"공정하게 임하고, 변론의 웅변에 휘둘리지 말되, 그래도 논거를 잘 따져 보십시오."], ["Remember that there is a great responsibility laid upon you,”", "여러분에게 막중한 책임이 지워졌음을 명심하십시오.\""], ["and so on and so on.", "운운하는 식이었다."]], [["The jury withdrew and the court adjourned.", "배심원들이 물러갔고 법정은 휴정에 들어갔다."], ["People could get up, move about, exchange their accumulated impressions, refresh themselves at the buffet.", "사람들은 일어서서 돌아다니고, 쌓인 소감을 주고받으며, 매점에서 요기를 할 수 있었다."], ["It was very late, almost one o’clock in the night, but nobody went away: the strain was so great that no one could think of repose.", "시간은 무척 늦어 거의 새벽 한 시에 가까웠으나, 아무도 자리를 뜨지 않았다. 긴장이 어찌나 팽팽했던지 누구도 쉴 생각을 할 수 없었다."], ["All waited with sinking hearts; though that is, perhaps, too much to say, for the ladies were only in a state of hysterical impatience and their hearts were untroubled.", "모두가 가슴을 졸이며 기다렸다. 하기야 그것은 다소 지나친 말일지도 모르니, 부인들로 말하자면 히스테릭한 초조함에 사로잡혀 있었을 뿐 그 가슴은 조금도 불안하지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["An acquittal, they thought, was inevitable.", "무죄 방면은 피할 수 없다고 그녀들은 생각했다."], ["They all prepared themselves for a dramatic moment of general enthusiasm.", "그녀들은 모두 다 함께 열광할 극적인 순간에 대비하고 있었다."], ["I must own there were many among the men, too, who were convinced that an acquittal was inevitable.", "솔직히 인정하건대, 남자들 가운데서도 무죄 방면이 틀림없다고 확신하는 이가 적지 않았다."], ["Some were pleased, others frowned, while some were simply dejected, not wanting him to be acquitted.", "어떤 이는 흐뭇해했고, 어떤 이는 인상을 찌푸렸으며, 또 어떤 이는 그가 방면되기를 바라지 않아 그저 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["Fetyukovitch himself was confident of his success.", "페튜코비치 자신은 자신의 성공을 확신했다."], ["He was surrounded by people congratulating him and fawning upon him.", "그의 주위로 사람들이 몰려들어 축하하고 알랑거렸다."]], [["“There are,”", "\"이런 것이 있습니다.\""], ["he said to one group, as I was told afterwards, “there are invisible threads binding the counsel for the defense with the jury.", "나중에 들은 바로, 그는 한 무리에게 이렇게 말했다고 한다. \"변호인과 배심원을 잇는 보이지 않는 실이 있는 법입니다."], ["One feels during one’s speech if they are being formed.", "연설을 하는 동안 그 실이 짜이고 있는지 어떤지를 느낄 수 있지요. 저는 그것을 느꼈습니다."], ["I was aware of them.", "그것은 분명히 존재합니다."], ["They exist. Our cause is won.", "우리의 사건은 이긴 것입니다."], ["Set your mind at rest.”", "마음 놓으십시오.\""]], [["“What will our peasants say now?”", "\"이제 우리 농민들이 뭐라고 할까요?\""], ["said one stout, cross-looking, pock-marked gentleman, a landowner of the neighborhood, approaching a group of gentlemen engaged in conversation.", "인근의 한 지주인, 살집 좋고 심술궂은 표정에 곰보 자국이 있는 신사가, 한창 이야기에 열을 올리는 신사들 무리에 다가서며 말했다."]], [["“But they are not all peasants.", "\"하지만 그들이 다 농민은 아니지요."], ["There are four government clerks among them.”", "그중에 관청 서기가 넷이나 끼어 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, there are clerks,”", "\"그렇지요, 서기들이 있습니다.\""], ["said a member of the district council, joining the group.", "군 자치회 의원 하나가 무리에 끼어들며 말했다."]], [["“And do you know that Nazaryev, the merchant with the medal, a juryman?”", "\"그리고 그 메달을 단 상인 나자리예프가 배심원인 것을 아십니까?\""]], [["“What of him?”", "\"그가 어떻다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“He is a man with brains.”", "\"머리가 비상한 사람입니다.\""]], [["“But he never speaks.”", "\"하지만 그는 도통 말이 없잖습니까.\""]], [["“He is no great talker, but so much the better.", "\"말수가 적긴 하지요, 하지만 오히려 그 편이 낫습니다."], ["There’s no need for the Petersburg man to teach him: he could teach all Petersburg himself.", "그 페테르부르크 양반이 그를 가르칠 필요가 없어요. 그가 페테르부르크 전체를 가르치고도 남을 위인이지요."], ["He’s the father of twelve children.", "그는 열두 자식의 아비랍니다."], ["Think of that!”", "한번 생각해 보십시오!\""]], [["“Upon my word, you don’t suppose they won’t acquit him?”", "\"맙소사, 설마 그들이 그를 무죄로 방면하지 않으리라고 보시는 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["one of our young officials exclaimed in another group.", "우리 고장의 한 젊은 관리가 다른 무리에서 외쳤다."]], [["“They’ll acquit him for certain,”", "\"틀림없이 무죄로 방면할 겁니다.\""], ["said a resolute voice.", "단호한 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“It would be shameful, disgraceful, not to acquit him!”", "\"그를 방면하지 않는다면 그건 부끄럽고 망신스러운 일이지요!\""], ["cried the official.", "관리가 소리쳤다."], ["“Suppose he did murder him—there are fathers and fathers!", "\"설령 그가 정말로 죽였다 칩시다—그래도 아비에도 여러 아비가 있는 법 아닙니까!"], ["And, besides, he was in such a frenzy....", "게다가 그는 그토록 광란에 사로잡혀 있었으니…"], ["He really may have done nothing but swing the pestle in the air, and so knocked the old man down.", "사실은 그저 절굿공이를 허공에 휘둘렀을 뿐인데 그 바람에 노인을 쓰러뜨렸을지도 모르는 일입니다. 다만 그 하인 놈을 끌어들인 건 안타까운 일이었어요."], ["But it was a pity they dragged the valet in.", "그건 그저 터무니없는 가설이었지요!"], ["That was simply an absurd theory!", "내가 페튜코비치 처지였다면, 나는 그냥 대놓고 이렇게 말했을 겁니다."], ["If I’d been in Fetyukovitch’s place, I should simply have said straight out: ‘He murdered him; but he is not guilty, hang it all!’”", "'그가 그를 죽였소. 하지만 그는 죄가 없소, 빌어먹을!'\""]], [["“That’s what he did, only without saying, ‘Hang it all!’”", "\"그가 한 게 바로 그겁니다, 다만 '빌어먹을!'이라는 말만 안 붙였을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“No, Mihail Semyonovitch, he almost said that, too,”", "\"아니, 미하일 세묜오비치, 그는 그 말까지도 거의 한 거나 다름없었습니다.\""], ["put in a third voice.", "세 번째 목소리가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Why, gentlemen, in Lent an actress was acquitted in our town who had cut the throat of her lover’s lawful wife.”", "\"이보십시오, 여러분, 사순절에 우리 고장에서 어떤 여배우가 무죄로 방면된 적이 있는데, 그 여자가 제 정부의 정식 마누라 목을 그어 놓았었지요.\""]], [["“Oh, but she did not finish cutting it.”", "\"오, 하지만 그 여자는 끝까지 긋지는 못했잖습니까.\""]], [["“That makes no difference.", "\"그게 무슨 차이가 있습니까."], ["She began cutting it.”", "어쨌든 긋기 시작은 한 거지요.\""]], [["“What did you think of what he said about children?", "\"그가 자식들에 대해 한 말은 어떻게 보셨습니까?"], ["Splendid, wasn’t it?”", "일품이었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“Splendid!”", "\"일품이었습니다!\""]], [["“And about mysticism, too!”", "\"신비주의에 대한 것도 그렇고요!\""]], [["“Oh, drop mysticism, do!”", "\"오, 신비주의 얘기는 그만 좀 합시다!\""], ["cried some one else; “think of Ippolit and his fate from this day forth.", "또 다른 누군가가 외쳤다. \"오늘부터 이폴리트와 그의 신세나 한번 생각해 보십시오."], ["His wife will scratch his eyes out to-morrow for Mitya’s sake.” “Is she here?”", "내일이면 그의 아내가 미탸 때문에 그의 두 눈을 후벼 팔 겁니다.\""]], [["“What an idea!", "\"그 부인이 여기 와 있습니까?\""], ["If she’d been here she’d have scratched them out in court.", "\"무슨 그런 생각을! 여기 와 있었더라면 법정에서 진작 후벼 팠을 겁니다."], ["She is at home with toothache.", "그 부인은 치통이 나서 집에 있답니다."], ["He he he!”", "헤헤헤!\""]], [["“He he he!”", "\"헤헤헤!\""]], [["In a third group:", "또 다른 무리에서는 이런 말이 오갔다."]], [["“I dare say they will acquit Mitenka, after all.”", "\"아무래도 미텐카는 결국 방면될 것 같소.\""]], [["“I should not be surprised if he turns the ‘Metropolis’ upside down to-morrow.", "\"내일이면 그가 '메트로폴리스'를 발칵 뒤집어 놓는다 해도 나는 놀라지 않겠소."], ["He will be drinking for ten days!”", "열흘은 줄곧 퍼마실 테니!\""]], [["“Oh, the devil!”", "\"오, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["“The devil’s bound to have a hand in it.", "\"빌어먹을 놈이 끼어들지 않을 리 없지."], ["Where should he be if not here?”", "여기가 아니면 그놈이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["“Well, gentlemen, I admit it was eloquent.", "\"이보시오, 여러분, 나도 그게 웅변이었다는 건 인정하오."], ["But still it’s not the thing to break your father’s head with a pestle!", "하지만 그래도 아비의 머리통을 절굿공이로 깨부순다는 건 될 법한 일이 아니잖소!"], ["Or what are we coming to?”", "안 그러면 우리가 대체 어찌 되겠소?\""]], [["“The chariot!", "\"전차 말이오!"], ["Do you remember the chariot?”", "그 전차 기억하시오?\""]], [["“Yes; he turned a cart into a chariot!”", "\"그렇소. 그가 짐수레를 전차로 둔갑시켰지요!\""]], [["“And to-morrow he will turn a chariot into a cart, just to suit his purpose.”", "\"그러고는 내일이면 제 속셈에 맞춰 전차를 다시 짐수레로 둔갑시킬 거요.\""]], [["“What cunning chaps there are nowadays!", "\"요즘 세상엔 어찌 그리 약삭빠른 작자들이 많은지!"], ["Is there any justice to be had in Russia?”", "러시아에 정의라는 게 남아 있기는 한 거요?\""]], [["But the bell rang.", "그러나 종이 울렸다."], ["The jury deliberated for exactly an hour, neither more nor less.", "배심원들은 더도 덜도 없이 꼭 한 시간 동안 숙의했다."], ["A profound silence reigned in the court as soon as the public had taken their seats.", "방청객들이 자리에 앉자마자 법정에는 깊은 정적이 깔렸다."], ["I remember how the jurymen walked into the court.", "나는 배심원들이 법정으로 걸어 들어오던 모습을 기억한다."], ["At last!", "드디어!"], ["I won’t repeat the questions in order, and, indeed, I have forgotten them.", "나는 질문들을 차례대로 되풀이하지는 않겠으며, 실은 그것들을 잊어버리고 말았다."], ["I remember only the answer to the President’s first and chief question: “Did the prisoner commit the murder for the sake of robbery and with premeditation?”", "다만 재판장의 첫 번째이자 가장 중요한 질문에 대한 답만은 기억한다. \"피고인은 강도질을 목적으로 또 미리 계획하여 살인을 저질렀는가?\"(정확한 문구는 기억나지 않는다.) 완전한 정적이 감돌았다."], ["(I don’t remember the exact words.) There was a complete hush. The foreman of the jury, the youngest of the clerks, pronounced, in a clear, loud voice, amidst the deathlike stillness of the court:", "배심원 대표인, 서기들 가운데 가장 젊은 사내가, 죽음 같은 법정의 적막 속에서 또렷하고 우렁찬 목소리로 선고했다."]], [["“Yes, guilty!”", "\"그렇다, 유죄!\""]], [["And the same answer was repeated to every question: “Yes, guilty!”", "그리고 모든 질문에 똑같은 답이 되풀이되었다. \"그렇다, 유죄!\""], ["and without the slightest extenuating comment.", "그것도 정상참작에 대한 일언반구의 언급도 없이."], ["This no one had expected; almost every one had reckoned upon a recommendation to mercy, at least.", "이것은 아무도 예상하지 못한 일이었으니, 거의 모든 이가 적어도 자비를 권하는 단서 정도는 붙으리라 헤아리고 있었던 것이다."], ["The deathlike silence in the court was not broken—all seemed petrified: those who desired his conviction as well as those who had been eager for his acquittal.", "죽음 같은 법정의 적막은 깨지지 않았다—그를 유죄로 만들고 싶어 했던 자들이나 그의 방면을 간절히 바랐던 자들이나 다 함께 돌처럼 굳어 버린 듯했다."], ["But that was only for the first instant, and it was followed by a fearful hubbub.", "그러나 그것은 다만 첫 한순간뿐이었고, 곧이어 무시무시한 소란이 잇따랐다."], ["Many of the men in the audience were pleased.", "방청객 가운데 남자들 다수는 흐뭇해했다."], ["Some were rubbing their hands with no attempt to conceal their joy.", "어떤 이들은 기쁨을 숨기려는 기색도 없이 두 손을 비벼 댔다."], ["Those who disagreed with the verdict seemed crushed, shrugged their shoulders, whispered, but still seemed unable to realize this.", "평결에 동의하지 않는 자들은 짓눌린 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 수군거렸으나, 그래도 여전히 이 사태를 실감하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["But how shall I describe the state the ladies were in?", "그러나 부인들이 처한 상태를 내가 어찌 묘사할 수 있겠는가?"], ["I thought they would create a riot.", "나는 그녀들이 폭동이라도 일으킬 줄 알았다."], ["At first they could scarcely believe their ears.", "처음에 그녀들은 제 귀를 좀처럼 믿지 못했다."], ["Then suddenly the whole court rang with exclamations: “What’s the meaning of it?", "그러다 별안간 온 법정이 외침으로 울려 퍼졌다. \"이게 무슨 영문이야?"], ["What next?”", "다음엔 또 뭐람?\""], ["They leapt up from their places.", "그녀들은 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어났다."], ["They seemed to fancy that it might be at once reconsidered and reversed.", "그녀들은 이 평결이 당장이라도 재고되어 뒤집힐 수 있다고 공상하는 듯했다."], ["At that instant Mitya suddenly stood up and cried in a heartrending voice, stretching his hands out before him:", "바로 그 순간 미탸가 별안간 일어서더니 두 손을 앞으로 내뻗으며 가슴을 찢는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“I swear by God and the dreadful Day of Judgment I am not guilty of my father’s blood!", "\"하느님과 저 무서운 심판의 날에 두고 맹세하노니, 저는 제 아비의 피에 대해 죄가 없습니다!"], ["Katya, I forgive you!", "카탸, 나는 당신을 용서하오!"], ["Brothers, friends, have pity on the other woman!”", "형제들이여, 벗들이여, 저 다른 여인을 가엾이 여겨 주오!\""]], [["He could not go on, and broke into a terrible sobbing wail that was heard all over the court in a strange, unnatural voice unlike his own.", "그는 더 말을 잇지 못한 채, 제 것이라고는 믿기지 않는 기이하고 부자연스러운 목소리로 온 법정에 울려 퍼지는 끔찍한 통곡을 터뜨렸다."], ["From the farthest corner at the back of the gallery came a piercing shriek—it was Grushenka.", "방청석 맨 뒤 가장 먼 구석에서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나왔다—그루셴카였다."], ["She had succeeded in begging admittance to the court again before the beginning of the lawyers’ speeches.", "그녀는 변호인들의 연설이 시작되기 전에 다시금 간청하여 법정에 들어오는 데 성공했던 것이다. 미탸는 끌려 나갔다."], ["Mitya was taken away.", "선고는 이튿날로 미루어졌다."], ["The passing of the sentence was deferred till next day.", "온 법정이 소란에 휩싸였으나 나는 더 들으려 머물지 않았다."], ["The whole court was in a hubbub but I did not wait to hear.", "다만 밖으로 나가며 계단에서 들은 몇 마디 외침만이 기억에 남아 있다."], ["I only remember a few exclamations I heard on the steps as I went out.", "\"저 친구, 광산에서 이십 년짜리 여행을 하게 생겼군!\""]], [["“He’ll have a twenty years’ trip to the mines!” “Not less.”", "\"그보다 덜하지는 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“Well, our peasants have stood firm.”", "\"흠, 우리 농민들이 단단히 버텼군.\""]], [["“And have done for our Mitya.”", "\"그래서 우리 미탸를 끝장내 버렸지.\""]], [["# Chapter I. Plans For Mitya’s Escape", "# Chapter I. Plans For Mitya’s Escape"]], [["Very early, at nine o’clock in the morning, five days after the trial, Alyosha went to Katerina Ivanovna’s to talk over a matter of great importance to both of them, and to give her a message.", "재판이 끝난 지 닷새째 되던 날 아침 아주 이른 시각, 아홉 시에 알료샤는 두 사람 모두에게 더없이 중대한 어떤 문제를 의논하고 또 전할 말을 건네고자 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 찾았다."], ["She sat and talked to him in the very room in which she had once received Grushenka.", "그녀는 한때 그루셴카를 맞이했던 바로 그 방에 앉아 그와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["In the next room Ivan Fyodorovitch lay unconscious in a high fever.", "옆방에는 이반 표도로비치가 고열에 시달리며 의식을 잃은 채 누워 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna had immediately after the scene at the trial ordered the sick and unconscious man to be carried to her house, disregarding the inevitable gossip and general disapproval of the public.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 법정에서 그 장면이 벌어지자마자, 피할 수 없는 뒷공론과 세간의 노골적인 비난 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고 병들어 의식을 잃은 그를 자기 집으로 옮기게 했다."], ["One of the two relations who lived with her had departed to Moscow immediately after the scene in court, the other remained.", "그녀와 함께 살던 두 친척 가운데 한 사람은 법정에서 그 장면이 벌어진 직후 모스크바로 떠났고, 다른 한 사람은 남았다."], ["But if both had gone away, Katerina Ivanovna would have adhered to her resolution, and would have gone on nursing the sick man and sitting by him day and night.", "그러나 설령 둘 다 떠나 버렸다 해도 카테리나 이바노브나는 자신의 결심을 굽히지 않고 밤낮없이 그의 곁에 앉아 병자를 계속 간호했을 것이었다."], ["Varvinsky and Herzenstube were attending him.", "바르빈스키와 게르첸슈투베가 그를 진찰하고 있었다."], ["The famous doctor had gone back to Moscow, refusing to give an opinion as to the probable end of the illness.", "그 유명한 의사는 병의 결말이 어찌 될지에 대한 견해 밝히기를 거부한 채 모스크바로 돌아가 버렸다."], ["Though the doctors encouraged Katerina Ivanovna and Alyosha, it was evident that they could not yet give them positive hopes of recovery.", "의사들이 카테리나 이바노브나와 알료샤를 안심시키려 했으나, 그들이 아직 회복에 대한 확실한 희망을 줄 수는 없다는 것이 분명했다."]], [["Alyosha came to see his sick brother twice a day.", "알료샤는 하루에 두 번 앓아누운 형을 보러 왔다."], ["But this time he had specially urgent business, and he foresaw how difficult it would be to approach the subject, yet he was in great haste.", "그러나 이번에는 특별히 다급한 용무가 있어서, 이 화제를 꺼내기가 얼마나 어려울지 미리 헤아리면서도 마음만은 몹시 바빴다."], ["He had another engagement that could not be put off for that same morning, and there was need of haste.", "그날 아침에는 미룰 수 없는 또 다른 약속이 있어 서둘러야만 했던 것이다."]], [["They had been talking for a quarter of an hour.", "두 사람은 십오 분째 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna was pale and terribly fatigued, yet at the same time in a state of hysterical excitement.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 창백하고 끔찍하게 지쳐 보였으나, 동시에 히스테릭한 흥분에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["She had a presentiment of the reason why Alyosha had come to her.", "그녀는 알료샤가 무엇 때문에 자기를 찾아왔는지 어렴풋이 짐작하고 있었다."]], [["“Don’t worry about his decision,”", "\"그이의 결심은 걱정하지 마세요.\""], ["she said, with confident emphasis to Alyosha.", "그녀는 확신에 찬 어조로 알료샤에게 못 박듯 말했다."], ["“One way or another he is bound to come to it. He must escape.", "\"이러나저러나 그이는 결국 그 결심에 이를 수밖에 없어요."], ["That unhappy man, that hero of honor and principle—not he, not Dmitri Fyodorovitch, but the man lying the other side of that door, who has sacrificed himself for his brother,”", "그이는 달아나야 해요. 그 불행한 사람, 명예와 신념의 영웅인 그 사람이—드미트리 표도로비치가 아니라, 저 문 너머에 누워 있는 사람, 형을 위해 제 몸을 희생한 그 사람 말이에요—\""], ["Katya added, with flashing eyes—“told me the whole plan of escape long ago.", "카탸는 눈을 번쩍이며 덧붙였다. \"오래전에 제게 탈출 계획을 송두리째 다 들려주었답니다."], ["You know he has already entered into negotiations....", "아시겠지만 그이는 이미 교섭에 들어갔어요…."], ["I’ve told you something already....", "제가 이미 얼마쯤은 말씀드렸지요…."], ["You see, it will probably come off at the third étape from here, when the party of prisoners is being taken to Siberia.", "보세요, 그건 아마 죄수 일행이 시베리아로 이송되는 길에 여기서 세 번째 역참에 이르렀을 때 이뤄질 거예요."], ["Oh, it’s a long way off yet.", "아, 아직은 한참 먼 일이지요."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch has already visited the superintendent of the third étape.", "이반 표도로비치는 벌써 세 번째 역참의 책임자를 찾아갔어요."], ["But we don’t know yet who will be in charge of the party, and it’s impossible to find that out so long beforehand.", "하지만 누가 그 일행을 인솔하게 될지는 아직 모르고, 그렇게 한참 전부터 그걸 알아내기란 불가능해요."], ["To-morrow perhaps I will show you in detail the whole plan which Ivan Fyodorovitch left me on the eve of the trial in case of need....", "내일쯤이면 이반 표도로비치가 재판 전날 만일을 대비해 제게 남겨 둔 계획 전체를 자세히 보여 드릴 수 있을 거예요…."], ["That was when—do you remember?—you found us quarreling.", "그때가 언제냐 하면—기억하세요?—당신이 우리가 다투는 걸 보았던 그때예요."], ["He had just gone down-stairs, but seeing you I made him come back; do you remember?", "그이는 막 아래층으로 내려간 참이었지만, 당신을 보고서 제가 그이를 다시 불러올렸지요."], ["Do you know what we were quarreling about then?”", "기억하세요? 그때 우리가 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 아세요?\""]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "\"아뇨, 모릅니다.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Of course he did not tell you.", "\"물론 그이는 당신께 말하지 않았겠지요."], ["It was about that plan of escape.", "바로 그 탈출 계획 때문이었어요."], ["He had told me the main idea three days before, and we began quarreling about it at once and quarreled for three days.", "그이는 사흘 전에 제게 핵심을 들려주었고, 우리는 곧장 그 일로 다투기 시작해 사흘을 내리 다퉜답니다."], ["We quarreled because, when he told me that if Dmitri Fyodorovitch were convicted he would escape abroad with that creature, I felt furious at once—I can’t tell you why, I don’t know myself why....", "우리가 다툰 까닭은, 그이가 만약 드미트리 표도로비치가 유죄 판결을 받으면 그 계집과 함께 외국으로 달아날 거라고 말하자, 제가 대번에 격분했기 때문이에요—왜 그랬는지 말씀드릴 수도 없고, 저 자신도 왜 그랬는지 몰라요…."], ["Oh, of course, I was furious then about that creature, and that she, too, should go abroad with Dmitri!”", "아, 물론 그때 저는 그 계집 때문에 분노했어요, 그 여자까지 드미트리와 함께 외국으로 가다니!\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna exclaimed suddenly, her lips quivering with anger.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 갑자기 소리쳤고, 입술이 분노로 파르르 떨렸다."], ["“As soon as Ivan Fyodorovitch saw that I was furious about that woman, he instantly imagined I was jealous of Dmitri and that I still loved Dmitri.", "\"이반 표도로비치는 제가 그 여자 때문에 격분하는 걸 보자마자, 곧장 제가 드미트리를 질투하는 거라고, 제가 아직도 드미트리를 사랑하는 거라고 단정해 버렸어요."], ["That is how our first quarrel began.", "우리의 첫 다툼은 그렇게 시작됐답니다."], ["I would not give an explanation, I could not ask forgiveness.", "저는 해명하려 들지 않았고, 용서를 빌 수도 없었어요."], ["I could not bear to think that such a man could suspect me of still loving that ...", "그런 사람이 제가 아직도 그…를 사랑한다고 의심하다니, 차마 견딜 수가 없었지요."], ["and when I myself had told him long before that I did not love Dmitri, that I loved no one but him!", "게다가 저는 이미 오래전에 그이에게, 드미트리는 사랑하지 않으며 오직 그이만을 사랑한다고 말했는데도 말이에요!"], ["It was only resentment against that creature that made me angry with him.", "그 계집을 향한 분함만이 그이에게 화를 내게 한 거였어요."], ["Three days later, on the evening you came, he brought me a sealed envelope, which I was to open at once, if anything happened to him.", "사흘 뒤, 당신이 찾아온 그날 저녁에 그이는 봉인된 봉투 하나를 가져다주며, 자기에게 무슨 일이 생기면 곧장 열어 보라고 했어요."], ["Oh, he foresaw his illness!", "아, 그이는 자기 병을 예감했던 거예요!"], ["He told me that the envelope contained the details of the escape, and that if he died or was taken dangerously ill, I was to save Mitya alone.", "그 봉투 안에는 탈출의 세부 사항이 들어 있으며, 만약 자기가 죽거나 위중한 병에 걸리면 저 혼자서라도 미탸를 구해 내야 한다고 했어요."], ["Then he left me money, nearly ten thousand—those notes to which the prosecutor referred in his speech, having learnt from some one that he had sent them to be changed.", "그러고는 제게 돈을 남겼지요, 거의 만 루블이나 되는—검사가 논고에서 언급한 그 지폐들 말이에요, 누군가에게서 그이가 환전하려고 보냈다는 걸 알아낸 거지요."], ["I was tremendously impressed to find that Ivan Fyodorovitch had not given up his idea of saving his brother, and was confiding this plan of escape to me, though he was still jealous of me and still convinced that I loved Mitya.", "이반 표도로비치가 형을 구하려는 생각을 포기하지 않았으며, 여전히 저를 질투하고 제가 미탸를 사랑한다고 확신하면서도 이 탈출 계획을 제게 맡겼다는 사실에 저는 엄청난 감동을 받았어요."], ["Oh, that was a sacrifice!", "아, 그건 희생이었어요!"], ["No, you cannot understand the greatness of such self-sacrifice, Alexey Fyodorovitch.", "아니에요, 당신은 그런 자기희생의 위대함을 이해하지 못해요, 알렉세이 표도로비치."], ["I wanted to fall at his feet in reverence, but I thought at once that he would take it only for my joy at the thought of Mitya’s being saved (and he certainly would have imagined that!), and I was so exasperated at the mere possibility of such an unjust thought on his part that I lost my temper again, and instead of kissing his feet, flew into a fury again!", "저는 경외심에 차 그이의 발치에 엎드리고 싶었지만, 곧 그이가 그걸 그저 미탸가 구원받는다는 생각에 제가 기뻐하는 것으로만 받아들이리라는 생각이 들었어요(그이는 분명 그렇게 상상했을 거예요!). 그이가 그렇게 부당한 생각을 품을 가능성이 있다는 것만으로도 어찌나 화가 나던지, 저는 또 이성을 잃고, 그이의 발에 입맞추는 대신 다시 격노에 휩싸이고 말았어요!"], ["Oh, I am unhappy!", "아, 저는 불행한 여자예요!"], ["It’s my character, my awful, unhappy character!", "이게 제 성격이에요, 제 끔찍하고 불행한 성격이라고요!"], ["Oh, you will see, I shall end by driving him, too, to abandon me for another with whom he can get on better, like Dmitri.", "아, 두고 보세요, 저는 결국 그이마저 드미트리처럼 저보다 더 잘 맞는 다른 여자에게로 떠나가게 만들 거예요."], ["But ...", "하지만…"], ["no, I could not bear it, I should kill myself.", "아니에요, 저는 그걸 견딜 수 없어요, 차라리 목숨을 끊고 말 거예요."], ["And when you came in then, and when I called to you and told him to come back, I was so enraged by the look of contempt and hatred he turned on me that—do you remember?—I cried out to you that it was he, he alone who had persuaded me that his brother Dmitri was a murderer!", "그때 당신이 들어오고, 제가 당신을 불러 세우며 그이더러 돌아오라고 했을 때, 그이가 제게 던진 그 경멸과 증오의 눈빛에 어찌나 격분했던지—기억하세요?—저는 당신을 향해 소리쳤지요, 그이가, 오직 그이만이 자기 형 드미트리가 살인자라고 저를 설득했다고 말이에요!"], ["I said that malicious thing on purpose to wound him again.", "저는 그이를 다시 한번 상처 입히려고 일부러 그 악의에 찬 말을 했어요."], ["He had never, never persuaded me that his brother was a murderer.", "그이는 결코, 결코 자기 형이 살인자라고 저를 설득한 적이 없어요."], ["On the contrary, it was I who persuaded him!", "오히려 그이를 설득한 건 저였어요!"], ["Oh, my vile temper was the cause of everything!", "아, 제 더러운 성미가 모든 것의 원인이었어요!"], ["I paved the way to that hideous scene at the trial.", "제가 그 재판정의 끔찍한 장면으로 가는 길을 닦아 놓은 거예요."], ["He wanted to show me that he was an honorable man, and that, even if I loved his brother, he would not ruin him for revenge or jealousy.", "그이는 제게 자기가 명예로운 사람이라는 걸, 설령 제가 자기 형을 사랑한다 해도 복수나 질투 때문에 형을 파멸시키지는 않으리라는 걸 보여 주고 싶었던 거예요."], ["So he came to the court ...", "그래서 법정에 나간 거고요…."], ["I am the cause of it all, I alone am to blame!”", "모든 게 제 탓이에요, 오직 저 하나만이 죄인이에요!\""]], [["Katya never had made such confessions to Alyosha before, and he felt that she was now at that stage of unbearable suffering when even the proudest heart painfully crushes its pride and falls vanquished by grief.", "카탸가 알료샤에게 이런 고백을 한 적은 전에 없었으니, 그는 그녀가 이제 가장 오만한 마음조차 제 오만을 고통스레 짓밟으며 슬픔에 굴복해 쓰러지는, 그 견딜 수 없는 괴로움의 단계에 이르렀음을 느꼈다."], ["Oh, Alyosha knew another terrible reason of her present misery, though she had carefully concealed it from him during those days since the trial; but it would have been for some reason too painful to him if she had been brought so low as to speak to him now about that.", "아, 알료샤는 지금 그녀가 겪는 비참함의 또 다른 무서운 까닭을 알고 있었으나, 그녀는 재판이 끝난 뒤로 그 며칠 동안 그것을 그에게서 조심스레 감추어 왔다. 다만 그녀가 그토록 비참한 지경으로 떨어져 지금 그 일을 그에게 입에 올린다면, 무슨 까닭에서인지 그에게도 너무나 고통스러운 일이 되었으리라."], ["She was suffering for her “treachery”", "그녀는 법정에서 저지른 자신의 \"배신\""], ["at the trial, and Alyosha felt that her conscience was impelling her to confess it to him, to him, Alyosha, with tears and cries and hysterical writhings on the floor.", "때문에 괴로워하고 있었고, 알료샤는 그녀의 양심이 그것을 그에게, 바로 자기 알료샤에게 눈물과 절규와 바닥에 몸부림치는 히스테리로 고백하라고 떠밀고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["But he dreaded that moment and longed to spare her.", "그러나 그는 그 순간이 두려웠고 그녀를 그것으로부터 지켜 주고 싶었다."], ["It made the commission on which he had come even more difficult.", "그 때문에 그가 떠맡고 온 임무는 더더욱 어려워졌다."], ["He spoke of Mitya again.", "그는 다시 미탸에 대한 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“It’s all right, it’s all right, don’t be anxious about him!”", "\"괜찮아요, 괜찮아요, 그이 걱정은 마세요!\""], ["she began again, sharply and stubbornly.", "그녀가 날카롭고 고집스럽게 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“All that is only momentary, I know him, I know his heart only too well.", "\"그 모든 건 한순간일 뿐이에요, 저는 그이를 알아요, 그이의 마음을 너무도 잘 안다고요."], ["You may be sure he will consent to escape.", "분명히 말하건대 그이는 탈출에 동의할 거예요."], ["It’s not as though it would be immediately; he will have time to make up his mind to it.", "당장이라는 게 아니에요. 그이에겐 마음을 정할 시간이 있을 테니까요."], ["Ivan Fyodorovitch will be well by that time and will manage it all himself, so that I shall have nothing to do with it.", "그때쯤이면 이반 표도로비치가 건강을 되찾아 모든 걸 직접 처리할 테니, 저는 거기에 관여할 일이 없을 거예요."], ["Don’t be anxious; he will consent to run away.", "걱정 마세요, 그이는 달아나는 데 동의할 거예요."], ["He has agreed already: do you suppose he would give up that creature?", "이미 동의했어요. 그이가 그 계집을 포기하겠어요?"], ["And they won’t let her go to him, so he is bound to escape.", "그리고 그들은 그 여자가 그이에게 가도록 내버려 두지 않을 테니, 그이는 달아날 수밖에 없어요."], ["It’s you he’s most afraid of, he is afraid you won’t approve of his escape on moral grounds.", "그이가 가장 두려워하는 건 바로 당신이에요, 당신이 도덕적인 이유로 자기 탈출을 인정하지 않을까 봐 무서워하는 거지요."], ["But you must generously allow it, if your sanction is so necessary,”", "하지만 당신의 승인이 그렇게나 꼭 필요하다면, 당신은 너그럽게 그걸 허락해 주셔야 해요.\""], ["Katya added viciously.", "카탸가 독기 어린 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["She paused and smiled.", "그녀는 말을 멈추고 미소 지었다."]], [["“He talks about some hymn,”", "\"그이는 무슨 찬가가 어쩌고 떠들어요.\" 그녀가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["she went on again, “some cross he has to bear, some duty; I remember Ivan Fyodorovitch told me a great deal about it, and if you knew how he talked!”", "\"자기가 짊어져야 할 무슨 십자가가 어쩌고, 무슨 의무가 어쩌고 하면서요. 이반 표도로비치가 그 일에 대해 제게 많은 이야기를 들려주었던 게 기억나요, 그이가 어떻게 이야기했는지 당신이 아셨다면!\""], ["Katya cried suddenly, with feeling she could not repress, “if you knew how he loved that wretched man at the moment he told me, and how he hated him, perhaps, at the same moment.", "카탸가 갑자기, 억누를 수 없는 감정에 차 소리쳤다. \"그이가 제게 그 이야기를 들려주던 그 순간 그 가엾은 사람을 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그러면서도 어쩌면 그 똑같은 순간에 그를 얼마나 미워했는지 당신이 아셨다면 말이에요."], ["And I heard his story and his tears with sneering disdain.", "그런데 저는 그이의 이야기와 눈물을 비웃는 듯한 멸시로 들었어요. 짐승 같으니!"], ["Brute! Yes, I am a brute.", "그래요, 저는 짐승이에요."], ["I am responsible for his fever.", "그이의 열병은 제 탓이에요."], ["But that man in prison is incapable of suffering,”", "하지만 감옥에 있는 저 사람은 괴로워할 줄을 몰라요.\""], ["Katya concluded irritably.", "카탸가 짜증스레 결론지었다."], ["“Can such a man suffer?", "\"그런 사람이 괴로워할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Men like him never suffer!”", "그이 같은 사람들은 절대로 괴로워하지 않아요!\""]], [["There was a note of hatred and contemptuous repulsion in her words.", "그녀의 말에는 증오와 경멸 어린 혐오의 가락이 서려 있었다."], ["And yet it was she who had betrayed him.", "그러면서도 정작 그를 배신한 것은 그녀였다."], ["“Perhaps because she feels how she’s wronged him she hates him at moments,”", "'어쩌면 자기가 그이에게 얼마나 잘못했는지 느끼기 때문에 순간순간 그이를 미워하는 거겠지.'"], ["Alyosha thought to himself.", "알료샤는 속으로 생각했다."], ["He hoped that it was only “at moments.”", "그는 그것이 단지 '순간순간'에 지나지 않기를 바랐다."], ["In Katya’s last words he detected a challenging note, but he did not take it up.", "카탸의 마지막 말에서 그는 도전하는 듯한 가락을 알아챘으나, 거기에 응하지는 않았다."]], [["“I sent for you this morning to make you promise to persuade him yourself.", "\"오늘 아침 당신을 부른 건, 당신이 직접 그이를 설득하겠다고 약속하게 하려는 거예요."], ["Or do you, too, consider that to escape would be dishonorable, cowardly, or something ...", "아니면 당신도, 탈출이 명예롭지 못한 일, 비겁한 일, 아니면 무슨… 어쩌면 기독교도답지 못한 일이라고 여기시나요?\""], ["unchristian, perhaps?” Katya added, even more defiantly.", "카탸가 한층 더 도발적으로 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, no.", "\"아, 아니에요."], ["I’ll tell him everything,”", "그분께 다 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."], ["“He asks you to come and see him to-day,”", "\"그분은 당신이 오늘 자기를 보러 와 주기를 청하고 있어요.\""], ["he blurted out suddenly, looking her steadily in the face.", "그는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["She started, and drew back a little from him on the sofa.", "그녀는 흠칫 놀라 소파에서 그에게서 조금 물러났다."]], [["“Me?", "\"저더러요?"], ["Can that be?”", "그럴 수가 있나요?\""], ["she faltered, turning pale.", "그녀가 창백해지며 더듬거렸다."]], [["“It can and ought to be!”", "\"그럴 수 있고, 또 그러셔야 해요!\""], ["Alyosha began emphatically, growing more animated.", "알료샤가 점점 활기를 띠며 힘주어 말하기 시작했다."], ["“He needs you particularly just now.", "\"그분은 지금 특별히 당신을 필요로 하고 있어요."], ["I would not have opened the subject and worried you, if it were not necessary.", "꼭 필요한 일이 아니었다면 저도 이 화제를 꺼내 당신을 괴롭히지 않았을 거예요."], ["He is ill, he is beside himself, he keeps asking for you.", "그분은 병들었고, 제정신이 아니에요, 줄곧 당신을 찾고 있어요."], ["It is not to be reconciled with you that he wants you, but only that you would go and show yourself at his door.", "당신과 화해하려고 당신을 원하는 게 아니라, 다만 당신이 가서 그 문간에 모습을 보여 주기를 바라는 거예요."], ["So much has happened to him since that day.", "그날 이후로 그분에게 많은 일이 일어났어요."], ["He realizes that he has injured you beyond all reckoning.", "그분은 자기가 당신에게 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 깊은 상처를 입혔다는 걸 깨닫고 있어요."], ["He does not ask your forgiveness—‘It’s impossible to forgive me,’ he says himself—but only that you would show yourself in his doorway.”", "그분은 당신의 용서를 구하지 않아요—'나를 용서하기란 불가능하다'고 스스로 말하면서요—다만 당신이 그 문간에 모습을 보여 주기만을 바랄 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“It’s so sudden....”", "\"너무 갑작스러워요….\""], ["faltered Katya.", "카탸가 더듬거렸다."], ["“I’ve had a presentiment all these days that you would come with that message.", "\"그동안 줄곧, 당신이 그 전갈을 가지고 오리라는 예감이 들었어요."], ["I knew he would ask me to come.", "그이가 제게 와 달라고 청하리라는 걸 알았지요."], ["It’s impossible!”", "그건 불가능해요!\""]], [["“Let it be impossible, but do it.", "\"불가능하더라도, 그렇게 해 주세요."], ["Only think, he realizes for the first time how he has wounded you, the first time in his life; he had never grasped it before so fully.", "생각해 보세요, 그분은 평생 처음으로 자기가 당신에게 얼마나 깊은 상처를 입혔는지 깨닫고 있어요, 난생처음으로요. 전에는 그렇게 온전히 헤아린 적이 없었던 거예요."], ["He said, ‘If she refuses to come I shall be unhappy all my life.’ Do you hear?", "그분은 이렇게 말했어요, '만약 그녀가 오기를 거부한다면 나는 평생토록 불행할 것이다'라고요."], ["though he is condemned to penal servitude for twenty years, he is still planning to be happy—is not that piteous?", "들으셨어요? 이십 년의 징역형을 선고받았으면서도 그분은 여전히 행복해지려고 마음먹고 있어요—이거야말로 가엾지 않나요?"], ["Think—you must visit him; though he is ruined, he is innocent,”", "생각해 보세요—당신은 그분을 찾아가야 해요. 그분은 파멸했지만, 죄가 없어요.\""], ["broke like a challenge from Alyosha.", "알료샤의 입에서 도전하듯 말이 터져 나왔다."], ["“His hands are clean, there is no blood on them!", "\"그분의 손은 깨끗해요, 거기엔 피가 묻어 있지 않아요!"], ["For the sake of his infinite sufferings in the future visit him now.", "앞으로 그분이 겪을 그 끝없는 고통을 위해서라도 지금 그분을 찾아가 주세요."], ["Go, greet him on his way into the darkness—stand at his door, that is all....", "가셔서, 어둠 속으로 향하는 그분의 길에 인사를 건네 주세요—그 문간에 서 주세요, 그게 다예요…."], ["You ought to do it, you ought to!”", "당신은 그렇게 하셔야 해요, 그렇게 하셔야 한다고요!\""], ["Alyosha concluded, laying immense stress on the word “ought.”", "알료샤는 \"하셔야\"라는 말에 엄청난 무게를 실으며 끝맺었다."]], [["“I ought to ...", "\"저는 그렇게 해야 하죠…"], ["but I cannot....”", "하지만 할 수가 없어요….\""], ["Katya moaned.", "카탸가 신음했다."], ["“He will look at me....", "\"그이가 저를 바라볼 거예요…."], ["I can’t.”", "저는 그럴 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Your eyes ought to meet.", "\"두 분의 눈이 마주쳐야 해요."], ["How will you live all your life, if you don’t make up your mind to do it now?”", "지금 마음을 정하지 못한다면, 당신은 평생을 어떻게 살아가시겠어요?\""]], [["“Better suffer all my life.”", "\"차라리 평생을 괴로워하는 편이 나아요.\""]], [["“You ought to go, you ought to go,”", "\"가셔야 해요, 가셔야 해요.\""], ["Alyosha repeated with merciless emphasis.", "알료샤가 가차 없이 힘주어 거듭 말했다."]], [["“But why to-day, why at once?...", "\"하지만 왜 하필 오늘이죠, 왜 당장이어야 하죠?…"], ["I can’t leave our patient—”", "저는 우리 환자를 두고 떠날 수 없어요—\""]], [["“You can for a moment.", "\"잠깐이면 됩니다."], ["It will only be a moment.", "그저 한순간일 뿐이에요."], ["If you don’t come, he will be in delirium by to-night.", "당신이 오지 않으면, 그분은 오늘 밤 헛소리에 빠질 거예요."], ["I would not tell you a lie; have pity on him!”", "거짓말을 하는 게 아니에요. 부디 그분을 가엾이 여겨 주세요!\""]], [["“Have pity on me!”", "\"저를 가엾이 여겨 주세요!\""], ["Katya said, with bitter reproach, and she burst into tears.", "카탸가 쓰라린 원망을 담아 말하고는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Then you will come,”", "\"그럼 와 주시겠지요.\""], ["said Alyosha firmly, seeing her tears.", "알료샤가 그녀의 눈물을 보며 단호히 말했다."], ["“I’ll go and tell him you will come directly.”", "\"제가 가서 당신이 곧 오실 거라고 그분께 전하겠어요.\""]], [["“No, don’t tell him so on any account,”", "\"아니에요, 무슨 일이 있어도 그이에게 그렇게 말하진 마세요.\""], ["cried Katya in alarm.", "카탸가 놀라 소리쳤다."], ["“I will come, but don’t tell him beforehand, for perhaps I may go, but not go in....", "\"갈게요, 하지만 미리 그이에게 말하진 마세요. 어쩌면 제가 가더라도, 안으로 들어가지 못할지도 모르니까요…."], ["I don’t know yet—”", "저도 아직 모르겠어요—\""]], [["Her voice failed her.", "그녀의 목소리가 끊겼다."], ["She gasped for breath.", "그녀는 숨을 헐떡였다."], ["Alyosha got up to go.", "알료샤가 가려고 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“And what if I meet any one?”", "\"그런데 만약 누군가와 마주치면 어쩌죠?\""], ["she said suddenly, in a low voice, turning white again.", "그녀가 다시 새파랗게 질리며 갑자기 나직한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“That’s just why you must go now, to avoid meeting any one.", "\"바로 그래서 지금 가셔야 하는 거예요, 누구와도 마주치지 않으려면요."], ["There will be no one there, I can tell you that for certain.", "거기엔 아무도 없을 거예요, 그건 제가 확실히 말씀드릴 수 있어요."], ["We will expect you,”", "저희가 기다리겠습니다.\""], ["he concluded emphatically, and went out of the room.", "그는 힘주어 끝맺고는 방에서 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter II. For A Moment The Lie Becomes Truth", "# Chapter II. For A Moment The Lie Becomes Truth"]], [["He hurried to the hospital where Mitya was lying now.", "그는 미탸가 누워 있는 병원으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["The day after his fate was determined, Mitya had fallen ill with nervous fever, and was sent to the prison division of the town hospital.", "운명이 결정된 이튿날 미탸는 신경성 열병에 걸려 시립병원의 죄수 병동으로 보내졌다."], ["But at the request of several persons (Alyosha, Madame Hohlakov, Lise, etc.), Doctor Varvinsky had put Mitya not with other prisoners, but in a separate little room, the one where Smerdyakov had been.", "그러나 몇몇 사람들(알료샤, 호흘라코바 부인, 리즈 등)의 청에 따라 바르빈스키 의사는 미탸를 다른 죄수들과 함께 두지 않고 따로 떼어 둔 작은 방, 곧 한때 스메르댜코프가 있던 그 방에 들였다."], ["It is true that there was a sentinel at the other end of the corridor, and there was a grating over the window, so that Varvinsky could be at ease about the indulgence he had shown, which was not quite legal, indeed; but he was a kind-hearted and compassionate young man.", "사실 복도 저쪽 끝에는 보초가 서 있었고 창문에는 쇠창살이 쳐져 있었으니, 바르빈스키로서는 자신이 베푼 호의가, 엄밀히 말하면 그다지 합법적이지 않은 그 호의에 대해 마음을 놓을 수 있었던 것이다. 어쨌든 그는 마음씨 곱고 동정심 많은 젊은이였다."], ["He knew how hard it would be for a man like Mitya to pass at once so suddenly into the society of robbers and murderers, and that he must get used to it by degrees.", "그는 미탸 같은 사람이 그토록 느닷없이 한꺼번에 강도와 살인자들 무리 속으로 들어가는 것이 얼마나 견디기 힘든 일인지를 알았고, 그가 차츰차츰 거기에 익숙해져야 한다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["The visits of relations and friends were informally sanctioned by the doctor and overseer, and even by the police captain.", "친척과 친구들의 면회는 의사와 감독관은 물론, 경찰서장에게서까지 비공식적으로 허락을 받았다."], ["But only Alyosha and Grushenka had visited Mitya.", "그러나 미탸를 찾아온 사람은 알료샤와 그루셴카뿐이었다."], ["Rakitin had tried to force his way in twice, but Mitya persistently begged Varvinsky not to admit him.", "라키틴이 두 번이나 억지로 비집고 들어오려 했으나, 미탸는 한사코 바르빈스키에게 그를 들이지 말라고 사정했다."]], [["Alyosha found him sitting on his bed in a hospital dressing-gown, rather feverish, with a towel, soaked in vinegar and water, on his head.", "알료샤가 들어서니 미탸는 병원 가운을 걸친 채 침대에 앉아 있었는데, 다소 열에 들떠 머리에는 식초와 물에 적신 수건을 얹고 있었다."], ["He looked at Alyosha as he came in with an undefined expression, but there was a shade of something like dread discernible in it.", "그는 들어오는 알료샤를 알 수 없는 표정으로 바라보았으나, 그 표정에는 두려움 비슷한 그늘이 어른거렸다."], ["He had become terribly preoccupied since the trial; sometimes he would be silent for half an hour together, and seemed to be pondering something heavily and painfully, oblivious of everything about him.", "그는 재판 이후로 끔찍하게 골똘해져서, 어떤 때는 반 시간이나 입을 다문 채 주위의 모든 것을 잊고 무언가를 무겁고 괴롭게 곱씹는 듯했다."], ["If he roused himself from his brooding and began to talk, he always spoke with a kind of abruptness and never of what he really wanted to say.", "깊은 생각에서 깨어나 말을 시작하면, 그는 언제나 어딘가 퉁명스러운 투로, 정작 하고 싶은 말은 결코 하지 않았다."], ["He looked sometimes with a face of suffering at his brother.", "이따금 그는 고통에 찬 얼굴로 동생을 바라보았다."], ["He seemed to be more at ease with Grushenka than with Alyosha.", "그는 알료샤보다 그루셴카와 함께 있을 때 더 마음이 편해 보였다."], ["It is true, he scarcely spoke to her at all, but as soon as she came in, his whole face lighted up with joy.", "사실 그는 그루셴카와는 거의 말을 나누지 않았지만, 그녀가 들어서기만 하면 그의 온 얼굴이 기쁨으로 환해지는 것이었다."]], [["Alyosha sat down beside him on the bed in silence.", "알료샤는 말없이 그의 곁 침대에 걸터앉았다."], ["This time Mitya was waiting for Alyosha in suspense, but he did not dare ask him a question.", "이번에 미탸는 마음을 졸이며 알료샤를 기다리고 있었으나, 차마 그에게 무엇도 물어보지 못했다."], ["He felt it almost unthinkable that Katya would consent to come, and at the same time he felt that if she did not come, something inconceivable would happen.", "카탸가 와 주기로 마음먹는다는 것은 거의 생각조차 할 수 없는 일로 여겨졌고, 그러면서도 동시에 그녀가 오지 않는다면 무언가 상상도 못 할 일이 벌어지리라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Alyosha understood his feelings.", "알료샤는 그의 심정을 헤아렸다."]], [["“Trifon Borissovitch,”", "\"트리폰 보리소비치가 말이야,\""], ["Mitya began nervously, “has pulled his whole inn to pieces, I am told.", "미탸가 초조하게 입을 열었다, \"제 여인숙을 죄다 부숴 놓았다는 거야, 그렇게 들었어."], ["He’s taken up the flooring, pulled apart the planks, split up all the gallery, I am told.", "바닥을 뜯어내고 널빤지를 떼어 내고 회랑이란 회랑은 다 쪼개 놓았다는 거지."], ["He is seeking treasure all the time—the fifteen hundred roubles which the prosecutor said I’d hidden there.", "보물을 줄곧 찾고 있는 거야—검사가 내가 거기 숨겨 놓았다고 한 천오백 루블 말이야."], ["He began playing these tricks, they say, as soon as he got home.", "집에 돌아오자마자 그런 짓을 시작했다는군."], ["Serve him right, the swindler!", "사기꾼 놈, 쌤통이지!"], ["The guard here told me yesterday; he comes from there.”", "여기 간수가 어제 일러 줬어, 그자가 거기 출신이거든.\""]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 봐,\""], ["began Alyosha.", "알료샤가 입을 열었다."], ["“She will come, but I don’t know when.", "\"그분은 올 거야. 다만 언제일지는 모르겠어."], ["Perhaps to-day, perhaps in a few days, that I can’t tell.", "어쩌면 오늘일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 며칠 후일 수도 있고, 그건 알 수가 없어."], ["But she will come, she will, that’s certain.”", "하지만 올 거야, 틀림없이 와.\""]], [["Mitya started, would have said something, but was silent.", "미탸는 흠칫하더니 무언가 말하려다가 입을 다물었다."], ["The news had a tremendous effect on him.", "그 소식은 그에게 엄청난 충격을 주었다."], ["It was evident that he would have liked terribly to know what had been said, but he was again afraid to ask.", "그녀가 무슨 말을 했는지 끔찍이도 알고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했으나, 그는 또다시 차마 묻지 못했다."], ["Something cruel and contemptuous from Katya would have cut him like a knife at that moment.", "카탸에게서 나온 잔인하고 경멸 어린 말 한마디라도 있었다면 그 순간 그것은 칼처럼 그를 베었을 것이다."]], [["“This was what she said among other things; that I must be sure to set your conscience at rest about escaping.", "\"그분이 다른 여러 가지 가운데서도 이런 말을 했어. 탈주에 관해서 형의 양심을 반드시 편안하게 해 주어야 한다고."], ["If Ivan is not well by then she will see to it all herself.”", "그때까지 이반 형이 낫지 않으면 자기가 모든 걸 손수 처리하겠다고.\""]], [["“You’ve spoken of that already,”", "\"그건 벌써 했던 얘기잖아,\""], ["Mitya observed musingly.", "미탸가 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다."]], [["“And you have repeated it to Grusha,”", "\"그리고 형은 그걸 그루샤한테 되풀이했지,\""], ["observed Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["Mitya admitted.", "미탸가 인정했다."], ["“She won’t come this morning.”", "\"그 사람은 오늘 아침엔 안 와.\""], ["He looked timidly at his brother.", "그는 동생을 조심스레 바라보았다."], ["“She won’t come till the evening.", "\"저녁때나 돼야 올 거야."], ["When I told her yesterday that Katya was taking measures, she was silent, but she set her mouth.", "어제 내가 카탸가 조처를 취하고 있다고 말했더니, 그 사람은 잠자코 있다가 입을 꾹 다물더라고. 그저 '그러라죠!'"], ["She only whispered, ‘Let her!’ She understood that it was important.", "하고 속삭일 뿐이었어. 그게 중요한 일이라는 걸 알아챈 거지."], ["I did not dare to try her further.", "난 더는 그 사람을 떠볼 엄두가 안 났어."], ["She understands now, I think, that Katya no longer cares for me, but loves Ivan.”", "이제 그 사람은 카탸가 더 이상 날 마음에 두지 않고 이반 형을 사랑한다는 걸 알아챈 것 같아.\""]], [["“Does she?”", "\"정말 그래?\""], ["broke from Alyosha.", "알료샤가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps she does not.", "\"아닐지도 모르지."], ["Only she is not coming this morning,”", "다만 그 사람이 오늘 아침엔 안 온다는 거야,\""], ["Mitya hastened to explain again; “I asked her to do something for me.", "미탸가 또다시 서둘러 변명하듯 말했다. \"그 사람한테 내가 부탁을 좀 했거든."], ["You know, Ivan is superior to all of us.", "알다시피 이반 형은 우리 모두보다 뛰어난 사람이야."], ["He ought to live, not us.", "살아야 할 건 우리가 아니라 그 형이지."], ["He will recover.”", "그 형은 회복될 거야.\""]], [["“Would you believe it, though Katya is alarmed about him, she scarcely doubts of his recovery,”", "\"믿어지겠어, 카탸가 그 형 때문에 그렇게 걱정하면서도 회복을 거의 의심하지 않는단 말이지,\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“That means that she is convinced he will die.", "\"그건 그 사람이 그 형이 죽을 거라고 확신하고 있다는 뜻이야."], ["It’s because she is frightened she’s so sure he will get well.”", "두려워서 그렇게 나으리라고 단단히 믿는 거라고.\""]], [["“Ivan has a strong constitution, and I, too, believe there’s every hope that he will get well,”", "\"이반 형은 체질이 튼튼하니까, 나도 회복될 가망이 충분하다고 믿어,\""], ["Alyosha observed anxiously.", "알료샤가 근심스레 말했다."]], [["“Yes, he will get well.", "\"그래, 그 형은 나을 거야."], ["But she is convinced that he will die.", "하지만 그 사람은 그 형이 죽을 거라고 확신해."], ["She has a great deal of sorrow to bear...”", "짊어져야 할 슬픔이 그 사람에겐 많아……\""], ["A silence followed.", "침묵이 흘렀다."], ["A grave anxiety was fretting Mitya.", "무거운 불안이 미탸를 좀먹고 있었다."]], [["“Alyosha, I love Grusha terribly,”", "\"알료샤, 난 그루샤를 끔찍이 사랑해,\""], ["he said suddenly in a shaking voice, full of tears.", "그가 별안간 눈물이 가득한, 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“They won’t let her go out there to you,”", "\"그 사람들은 그 여자가 거기 형한테 따라가는 걸 허락하지 않을 거야,\""], ["Alyosha put in at once.", "알료샤가 곧바로 끼어들었다."]], [["“And there is something else I wanted to tell you,”", "\"그리고 또 한 가지 너한테 하고 싶은 말이 있어,\""], ["Mitya went on, with a sudden ring in his voice.", "미탸가 별안간 목소리에 울림을 띠며 말을 이었다."], ["“If they beat me on the way or out there, I won’t submit to it.", "\"가는 길에서든 거기서든 놈들이 날 때린다면, 난 가만있지 않을 거야."], ["I shall kill some one, and shall be shot for it.", "누군가를 죽일 거고, 그 때문에 총살당하겠지."], ["And this will be going on for twenty years!", "그런데 그게 이십 년 동안이나 이어질 거란 말이지!"], ["They speak to me rudely as it is.", "지금도 놈들은 나한테 함부로 말을 해."], ["I’ve been lying here all night, passing judgment on myself.", "밤새도록 여기 누워서 나 자신에게 판결을 내려 봤어."], ["I am not ready!", "난 준비가 안 됐어!"], ["I am not able to resign myself.", "난 스스로 받아들일 수가 없어."], ["I wanted to sing a ‘hymn’; but if a guard speaks rudely to me, I have not the strength to bear it.", "'찬가'를 부르고 싶었지만, 간수 하나가 나한테 함부로 말을 걸면 난 그걸 견뎌 낼 힘이 없어."], ["For Grusha I would bear anything ...", "그루샤를 위해서라면 무엇이든 견디겠어……"], ["anything except blows....", "매질만 빼고는 무엇이든……"], ["But she won’t be allowed to come there.”", "하지만 그 여잔 거기엔 따라오지 못할 거야.\""]], [["Alyosha smiled gently.", "알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Listen, brother, once for all,”", "\"들어 봐, 형, 단번에 다 말할게,\""], ["he said.", "그가 입을 열었다."], ["“This is what I think about it.", "\"이게 그 일에 대한 내 생각이야."], ["And you know that I would not tell you a lie.", "그리고 형도 알지, 내가 형한테 거짓말은 하지 않으리라는 걸."], ["Listen: you are not ready, and such a cross is not for you.", "들어 봐. 형은 준비가 안 됐고, 그런 십자가는 형을 위한 게 아니야."], ["What’s more, you don’t need such a martyr’s cross when you are not ready for it.", "게다가 형은 준비도 안 된 그런 순교자의 십자가가 필요하지도 않아."], ["If you had murdered our father, it would grieve me that you should reject your punishment.", "만일 형이 우리 아버지를 죽였다면, 형이 자기 형벌을 거부하는 게 나로선 가슴 아팠을 거야."], ["But you are innocent, and such a cross is too much for you.", "하지만 형은 죄가 없고, 그런 십자가는 형에게 너무 과해."], ["You wanted to make yourself another man by suffering.", "형은 고통을 통해 자신을 다른 사람으로 만들려고 했지."], ["I say, only remember that other man always, all your life and wherever you go; and that will be enough for you.", "내 말은, 그저 그 다른 사람을 늘, 평생토록, 어디를 가든 잊지 말라는 거야. 그것만으로도 형에겐 충분할 거야."], ["Your refusal of that great cross will only serve to make you feel all your life an even greater duty, and that constant feeling will do more to make you a new man, perhaps, than if you went there.", "형이 그 위대한 십자가를 거부한다는 것은, 오히려 평생토록 한층 더 큰 의무를 느끼게 하는 구실이 될 뿐이고, 그 끊임없는 느낌이 어쩌면 형이 거기로 가는 것보다 형을 새사람으로 만드는 데 더 큰 몫을 할 거야."], ["For there you would not endure it and would repine, and perhaps at last would say: ‘I am quits.’ The lawyer was right about that.", "거기서라면 형은 견뎌 내지 못하고 원망하다가, 어쩌면 끝내는 '이젠 빚을 다 갚았다'고 말하게 될 테니까. 변호사가 그 점에 대해선 옳았어."], ["Such heavy burdens are not for all men.", "그렇게 무거운 짐은 모든 사람을 위한 게 아니야."], ["For some they are impossible.", "어떤 사람들에겐 그건 불가능한 일이지."], ["These are my thoughts about it, if you want them so much.", "이게 그 일에 대한 내 생각이야, 형이 그렇게나 듣고 싶어 하니까."], ["If other men would have to answer for your escape, officers or soldiers, then I would not have ‘allowed’ you,”", "만일 형의 탈주에 대해 다른 사람들이, 장교나 병사들이 책임져야 한다면, 난 형을 '허락하지'"], ["smiled Alyosha.", "않았을 거야,\""], ["“But they declare—the superintendent of that étape told Ivan himself—that if it’s well managed there will be no great inquiry, and that they can get off easily.", "알료샤가 미소 지었다. \"그런데 그 사람들 말로는—그 호송 중계소 책임자가 이반 형한테 직접 말했다는데—일만 잘 처리하면 별다른 조사도 없을 거고, 쉽게 빠져나갈 수 있다는 거야."], ["Of course, bribing is dishonest even in such a case, but I can’t undertake to judge about it, because if Ivan and Katya commissioned me to act for you, I know I should go and give bribes.", "물론 이런 경우라도 뇌물을 쓰는 건 떳떳지 못한 일이지만, 난 그걸 두고 판단할 처지가 못 돼. 왜냐면 이반 형과 카탸가 나더러 형을 위해 나서 달라고 맡긴다면, 난 분명히 가서 뇌물을 쓸 테니까."], ["I must tell you the truth.", "형한테 진실을 말해야겠어."], ["And so I can’t judge of your own action.", "그러니 나로선 형 자신의 행동을 판단할 수가 없어."], ["But let me assure you that I shall never condemn you.", "다만 내가 형을 결코 비난하지 않으리라는 것만은 장담할게."], ["And it would be a strange thing if I could judge you in this.", "게다가 내가 이 일을 두고 형을 판단할 수 있다면 그건 이상한 노릇이지."], ["Now I think I’ve gone into everything.”", "자, 이제 모든 걸 다 짚어 본 것 같아.\""]], [["“But I do condemn myself!”", "\"하지만 난 나 자신을 비난해!\""], ["cried Mitya.", "미탸가 외쳤다."], ["“I shall escape, that was settled apart from you; could Mitya Karamazov do anything but run away?", "\"난 탈주할 거야, 그건 너와는 별개로 이미 정해진 일이지. 미탸 카라마조프가 달아나는 것 말고 무슨 짓을 할 수 있겠어?"], ["But I shall condemn myself, and I will pray for my sin for ever.", "하지만 난 나 자신을 단죄할 거고, 내 죄를 영원히 기도로 빌 거야."], ["That’s how the Jesuits talk, isn’t it?", "예수회 수사들이 꼭 이렇게 말하지 않나?"], ["Just as we are doing?”", "우리가 지금 하는 것처럼 말이야.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""], ["Alyosha smiled gently.", "알료샤가 부드럽게 미소 지었다."]], [["“I love you for always telling the whole truth and never hiding anything,”", "\"난 네가 늘 온전한 진실만 말하고 아무것도 숨기지 않아서 널 사랑해,\""], ["cried Mitya, with a joyful laugh.", "미탸가 즐거운 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["“So I’ve caught my Alyosha being Jesuitical.", "\"그러니까 내가 우리 알료샤가 예수회 수사처럼 구는 걸 딱 잡은 거지."], ["I must kiss you for that.", "그 일로 너한테 입을 맞춰야겠어."], ["Now listen to the rest; I’ll open the other side of my heart to you.", "자, 이제 나머지도 들어 봐. 내 마음의 다른 한쪽도 너한테 열어 보일게."], ["This is what I planned and decided.", "내가 계획하고 결정한 게 바로 이거야."], ["If I run away, even with money and a passport, and even to America, I should be cheered up by the thought that I am not running away for pleasure, not for happiness, but to another exile as bad, perhaps, as Siberia.", "만일 내가 돈과 여권을 갖고, 더구나 아메리카로까지 달아난다 해도, 난 내가 즐거움을 위해서, 행복을 위해서 달아나는 게 아니라 또 다른 유배지로, 어쩌면 시베리아만큼이나 못한 곳으로 가는 거라는 생각에 위안을 얻을 거야."], ["It is as bad, Alyosha, it is!", "그래, 거기도 똑같이 끔찍해, 알료샤, 정말 그래!"], ["I hate that America, damn it, already.", "난 벌써 그 아메리카가, 빌어먹을, 싫어."], ["Even though Grusha will be with me.", "그루샤가 나와 함께 있다 해도 말이지."], ["Just look at her; is she an American?", "그 여잘 좀 봐. 그 여자가 아메리카 사람이야?"], ["She is Russian, Russian to the marrow of her bones; she will be homesick for the mother country, and I shall see every hour that she is suffering for my sake, that she has taken up that cross for me.", "그 여잔 러시아 사람이야, 뼛속까지 러시아 사람이라고. 모국을 그리워하며 향수에 젖을 거고, 난 그 여자가 날 위해 괴로워한다는 걸, 날 위해 그 십자가를 짊어졌다는 걸 시시각각 보게 되겠지."], ["And what harm has she done?", "게다가 그 여자가 무슨 잘못을 저질렀단 말이야?"], ["And how shall I, too, put up with the rabble out there, though they may be better than I, every one of them?", "그리고 나 또한 거기 천한 무리들을 어떻게 견뎌 내겠어, 비록 그놈들이 하나같이 나보다 나은 인간이라 해도?"], ["I hate that America already!", "난 벌써 그 아메리카가 싫어!"], ["And though they may be wonderful at machinery, every one of them, damn them, they are not of my soul.", "그놈들이 하나같이 기계에는 기막히게 능하다 해도, 빌어먹을, 그놈들은 내 영혼의 사람들이 아니야."], ["I love Russia, Alyosha, I love the Russian God, though I am a scoundrel myself.", "난 러시아를 사랑해, 알료샤, 난 러시아의 신을 사랑해, 비록 나 자신은 비열한 놈이지만."], ["I shall choke there!”", "거기선 난 숨이 막혀 죽을 거야!\""], ["he exclaimed, his eyes suddenly flashing.", "그가 별안간 눈을 번득이며 외쳤다."], ["His voice was trembling with tears.", "그의 목소리는 눈물로 떨리고 있었다."], ["“So this is what I’ve decided, Alyosha, listen,”", "\"그래서 내가 이렇게 결정했어, 알료샤, 들어 봐,\""], ["he began again, mastering his emotion.", "그가 감정을 가다듬으며 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“As soon as I arrive there with Grusha, we will set to work at once on the land, in solitude, somewhere very remote, with wild bears.", "\"그루샤랑 거기 도착하는 즉시 우리는 곧장 땅을 부쳐 일을 시작할 거야, 외따로, 아주 외진 어딘가에서, 사나운 곰들이나 있는 곳에서 말이야."], ["There must be some remote parts even there.", "거기에도 분명 외진 구석이 있겠지."], ["I am told there are still Redskins there, somewhere, on the edge of the horizon.", "듣자니 거기엔 아직도 어딘가, 지평선 끄트머리에 인디언들이 남아 있다는 거야."], ["So to the country of the Last of the Mohicans, and there we’ll tackle the grammar at once, Grusha and I.", "그러니 '모히칸족의 최후'의 고장으로 가서, 거기서 그루샤랑 나랑 곧장 문법을 붙들고 늘어지는 거지."], ["Work and grammar—that’s how we’ll spend three years.", "노동과 문법—그렇게 삼 년을 보내는 거야."], ["And by that time we shall speak English like any Englishman.", "그때쯤이면 우린 영국 사람처럼 영어를 할 줄 알게 될 거야."], ["And as soon as we’ve learnt it—good-by to America!", "그리고 그걸 다 익히는 즉시—아메리카여, 안녕!"], ["We’ll run here to Russia as American citizens.", "우린 아메리카 시민이 되어 여기 러시아로 달려올 거야."], ["Don’t be uneasy—we would not come to this little town.", "걱정 마—이 작은 읍내로는 안 올 테니까."], ["We’d hide somewhere, a long way off, in the north or in the south.", "우린 어딘가, 멀리 떨어진 곳, 북쪽이든 남쪽이든 거기 숨어 살 거야."], ["I shall be changed by that time, and she will, too, in America.", "그때쯤이면 난 변해 있을 거고, 그 여자도 아메리카에서 변할 거야."], ["The doctors shall make me some sort of wart on my face—what’s the use of their being so mechanical!—or else I’ll put out one eye, let my beard grow a yard, and I shall turn gray, fretting for Russia.", "의사들이 내 얼굴에 무슨 사마귀 같은 걸 만들어 주겠지—그놈들이 그렇게 기계에 능하다는 게 다 무슨 소용이겠어!—아니면 내 눈 하나를 멀게 하거나, 수염을 한 자나 기르거나, 러시아를 그리며 애태우다 머리가 세어 버리겠지."], ["I dare say they won’t recognize us.", "아마 놈들은 우릴 못 알아볼 거야."], ["And if they do, let them send us to Siberia.", "설령 알아본다 한들, 시베리아로 보내라지."], ["I don’t care.", "난 상관없어."], ["It will show it’s our fate.", "그게 우리 운명이라는 걸 보여 주는 거니까."], ["We’ll work on the land here, too, somewhere in the wilds, and I’ll make up as an American all my life.", "우린 여기서도 땅을 부치며, 어딘가 황량한 곳에서, 평생토록 아메리카인 행세를 하는 거지."], ["But we shall die on our own soil.", "하지만 우린 우리 땅에서 죽을 거야."], ["That’s my plan, and it shan’t be altered.", "그게 내 계획이고, 이건 바꾸지 않을 거야."], ["Do you approve?”", "찬성하니?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said Alyosha, not wanting to contradict him.", "알료샤는 그의 말을 거스르고 싶지 않아 대답했다."], ["Mitya paused for a minute and said suddenly:", "미탸는 잠시 입을 다물었다가 별안간 말했다."]], [["“And how they worked it up at the trial!", "\"그런데 재판에서 놈들이 그걸 어떻게 꾸며 댔는지!"], ["Didn’t they work it up!”", "정말 어떻게 꾸며 댔는지 봤지!\""]], [["“If they had not, you would have been convicted just the same,”", "\"그렇게 안 했어도 형은 마찬가지로 유죄가 됐을 거야,\""], ["said Alyosha, with a sigh.", "알료샤가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, people are sick of me here!", "\"그래, 여기 사람들은 내가 지긋지긋한 거야!"], ["God bless them, but it’s hard,”", "그들에게 신의 가호가 있기를, 하지만 괴롭구나,\""], ["Mitya moaned miserably.", "미탸가 비참하게 신음했다."], ["Again there was silence for a minute.", "다시 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Alyosha, put me out of my misery at once!”", "\"알료샤, 당장 날 이 괴로움에서 꺼내 줘!\""], ["he exclaimed suddenly.", "그가 별안간 외쳤다."], ["“Tell me, is she coming now, or not?", "\"말해 줘, 그 사람이 지금 오는 거야, 아니야?"], ["Tell me?", "말해 줘."], ["What did she say?", "그 사람이 뭐라고 했어?"], ["How did she say it?”", "어떻게 말했어?\""]], [["“She said she would come, but I don’t know whether she will come to-day.", "\"그분은 오겠다고 했어. 다만 오늘 올지는 모르겠어."], ["It’s hard for her, you know,”", "알다시피 그분으로선 힘든 일이니까,\""], ["Alyosha looked timidly at his brother.", "알료샤가 동생을 조심스레 바라보았다."]], [["“I should think it is hard for her!", "\"그 사람한테 힘든 일이고말고!"], ["Alyosha, it will drive me out of my mind.", "알료샤, 이러다 미쳐 버리겠어."], ["Grusha keeps looking at me.", "그루샤가 자꾸 날 쳐다봐."], ["She understands.", "그 여잔 다 알아채고 있어."], ["My God, calm my heart: what is it I want?", "맙소사, 내 마음을 가라앉혀 줘. 내가 원하는 게 대체 뭐야?"], ["I want Katya!", "난 카탸를 원해!"], ["Do I understand what I want?", "내가 뭘 원하는지 내가 알기나 하는 거야?"], ["It’s the headstrong, evil Karamazov spirit!", "이건 그 고집스럽고 사악한 카라마조프의 기질이야!"], ["No, I am not fit for suffering.", "아니, 난 고통을 감당할 위인이 못 돼."], ["I am a scoundrel, that’s all one can say.”", "난 비열한 놈이야, 그 한마디로 다야.\""]], [["“Here she is!”", "\"저기 오신다!\""], ["cried Alyosha.", "알료샤가 외쳤다."]], [["At that instant Katya appeared in the doorway.", "바로 그 순간 카탸가 문간에 나타났다."], ["For a moment she stood still, gazing at Mitya with a dazed expression.", "그녀는 잠시 멈춰 서서 멍한 표정으로 미탸를 바라보았다."], ["He leapt impulsively to his feet, and a scared look came into his face.", "그는 충동적으로 벌떡 일어났고, 그의 얼굴에는 겁먹은 빛이 떠올랐다."], ["He turned pale, but a timid, pleading smile appeared on his lips at once, and with an irresistible impulse he held out both hands to Katya.", "그는 새파랗게 질렸으나, 이내 그의 입가에는 소심하고 애원하는 듯한 미소가 떠올랐고, 그는 억누를 수 없는 충동에 두 손을 카탸에게 내밀었다."], ["Seeing it, she flew impetuously to him.", "그것을 본 그녀는 격정에 휩싸여 그에게 달려왔다."], ["She seized him by the hands, and almost by force made him sit down on the bed.", "그녀는 그의 두 손을 붙잡고는 거의 억지로 그를 침대에 앉혔다."], ["She sat down beside him, and still keeping his hands pressed them violently.", "그녀는 그의 곁에 앉아, 여전히 그의 손을 잡은 채 세차게 움켜쥐었다."], ["Several times they both strove to speak, but stopped short and again gazed speechless with a strange smile, their eyes fastened on one another.", "두 사람은 여러 번 말을 하려 했으나 도중에 멈추고는, 다시 묘한 미소를 띤 채 말없이 서로에게 눈길을 박고 바라보았다."], ["So passed two minutes.", "그렇게 이 분이 흘렀다."]], [["“Have you forgiven me?”", "\"날 용서했어?\""], ["Mitya faltered at last, and at the same moment turning to Alyosha, his face working with joy, he cried, “Do you hear what I am asking, do you hear?”", "미탸가 마침내 더듬더듬 말했고, 동시에 알료샤를 돌아보며, 기쁨으로 일그러진 얼굴로 외쳤다, \"내가 무얼 묻고 있는지 들리니, 들려?\""]], [["“That’s what I loved you for, that you are generous at heart!”", "\"바로 그 점 때문에 내가 당신을 사랑했어요, 당신이 마음이 너그럽다는 점 때문에!\""], ["broke from Katya.", "카탸의 입에서 말이 터져 나왔다."], ["“My forgiveness is no good to you, nor yours to me; whether you forgive me or not, you will always be a sore place in my heart, and I in yours—so it must be....”", "\"내 용서는 당신에게 아무 소용 없고, 당신 용서도 내게 아무 소용 없어요. 당신이 날 용서하든 안 하든, 당신은 언제까지나 내 마음속의 아픈 자리로 남을 것이고, 나 또한 당신 마음속에 그렇게 남을 거예요—그렇게 될 수밖에 없어요……\""], ["She stopped to take breath.", "그녀는 숨을 고르려 말을 멈췄다."], ["“What have I come for?”", "\"내가 무엇 때문에 왔겠어요?\""], ["she began again with nervous haste: “to embrace your feet, to press your hands like this, till it hurts—you remember how in Moscow I used to squeeze them—to tell you again that you are my god, my joy, to tell you that I love you madly,”", "그녀가 다시 초조하게 서두르며 입을 열었다. \"당신의 발을 끌어안고, 이렇게 당신의 손을 아플 때까지 꼭 쥐려고—모스크바에서 내가 어떻게 당신 손을 꼭 쥐곤 했는지 기억하죠—당신이 내 신이고, 내 기쁨이라고 다시 말하려고, 당신을 미치도록 사랑한다고 말하려고 왔어요,\""], ["she moaned in anguish, and suddenly pressed his hand greedily to her lips.", "그녀는 고뇌에 차서 신음하더니, 별안간 게걸스럽게 그의 손을 제 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["Tears streamed from her eyes.", "눈에서 눈물이 흘러내렸다."], ["Alyosha stood speechless and confounded; he had never expected what he was seeing.", "알료샤는 말문이 막힌 채 어찌할 바를 몰라 서 있었다. 그는 자신이 보고 있는 광경을 결코 예상하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“Love is over, Mitya!”", "\"사랑은 끝났어요, 미탸!\""], ["Katya began again, “but the past is painfully dear to me.", "카탸가 다시 입을 열었다. \"하지만 지난날은 내게 가슴 저미도록 소중해요."], ["Know that you will always be so.", "알아 두세요, 당신은 언제까지나 그렇게 남으리라는 걸."], ["But now let what might have been come true for one minute,”", "하지만 이제, 그럴 수도 있었을 일이 단 일 분 동안만이라도 이루어지게 해 줘요,\""], ["she faltered, with a drawn smile, looking into his face joyfully again.", "그녀는 일그러진 미소를 띤 채 더듬거리며, 다시 기쁜 듯이 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["“You love another woman, and I love another man, and yet I shall love you for ever, and you will love me; do you know that?", "\"당신은 다른 여자를 사랑하고, 나는 다른 남자를 사랑하지만, 그래도 난 당신을 영원히 사랑할 것이고, 당신도 날 사랑할 거예요."], ["Do you hear?", "그걸 알겠어요?"], ["Love me, love me all your life!”", "들려요? 날 사랑해 줘요, 평생토록 날 사랑해 줘요!\""], ["she cried, with a quiver almost of menace in her voice.", "그녀가 목소리에 거의 위협에 가까운 떨림을 띠며 외쳤다."]], [["“I shall love you, and ...", "\"당신을 사랑할게……"], ["do you know, Katya,”", "그런데 말이야, 카탸,\""], ["Mitya began, drawing a deep breath at each word, “do you know, five days ago, that same evening, I loved you....", "미탸가 한마디 한마디마다 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 입을 열었다, \"그런데 말이야, 닷새 전 그날 저녁에 난 당신을 사랑했어……"], ["When you fell down and were carried out ...", "당신이 쓰러져 실려 나갔을 때…… 평생토록!"], ["All my life! So it will be, so it will always be—”", "그렇게 될 거야, 언제까지나 그렇게 될 거야—\""]], [["So they murmured to one another frantic words, almost meaningless, perhaps not even true, but at that moment it was all true, and they both believed what they said implicitly.", "그렇게 두 사람은 광란에 찬, 거의 아무 뜻도 없는, 어쩌면 진실조차 아닌 말들을 서로에게 속삭였으나, 그 순간만큼은 그것이 모두 진실이었고, 두 사람 모두 자기들이 하는 말을 한 점 의심 없이 믿었다."]], [["“Katya,”", "\"카탸,\""], ["cried Mitya suddenly, “do you believe I murdered him?", "미탸가 별안간 외쳤다, \"당신은 내가 그분을 죽였다고 믿어?"], ["I know you don’t believe it now, but then ...", "지금은 안 믿는다는 걸 알아, 하지만 그때는……"], ["when you gave evidence....", "당신이 증언했을 때는……"], ["Surely, surely you did not believe it!”", "정말, 정말 안 믿었던 거야!\""]], [["“I did not believe it even then.", "\"그때조차 난 안 믿었어요."], ["I’ve never believed it.", "단 한 번도 믿은 적 없어요."], ["I hated you, and for a moment I persuaded myself.", "난 당신이 미웠고, 그래서 한순간 나 자신을 그렇게 믿게끔 설득했던 거예요."], ["While I was giving evidence I persuaded myself and believed it, but when I’d finished speaking I left off believing it at once.", "증언하는 동안 난 나 자신을 설득해서 그걸 믿었지만, 말을 끝마치자마자 곧바로 믿기를 그만뒀어요."], ["Don’t doubt that!", "그건 의심하지 마세요!"], ["I have forgotten that I came here to punish myself,”", "난 내가 나 자신을 벌하려고 여기 왔다는 걸 잊었어요,\""], ["she said, with a new expression in her voice, quite unlike the loving tones of a moment before.", "그녀가 조금 전의 사랑스럽던 어조와는 전혀 다른, 새로운 빛깔을 띤 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Woman, yours is a heavy burden,”", "\"여인이여, 당신이 지닌 짐은 무겁구려,\""], ["broke, as it were, involuntarily from Mitya.", "미탸의 입에서 마치 저도 모르게인 듯 말이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Let me go,”", "\"보내 줘요,\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“I’ll come again.", "\"다시 올게요."], ["It’s more than I can bear now.”", "지금은 도저히 견딜 수가 없어요.\""]], [["She was getting up from her place, but suddenly uttered a loud scream and staggered back.", "그녀는 자리에서 일어서려다가, 별안간 날카로운 비명을 지르며 비틀비틀 물러섰다."], ["Grushenka walked suddenly and noiselessly into the room.", "그루셴카가 느닷없이 소리도 없이 방 안으로 걸어 들어온 것이다."], ["No one had expected her.", "아무도 그녀를 예상하지 못했다."], ["Katya moved swiftly to the door, but when she reached Grushenka, she stopped suddenly, turned as white as chalk and moaned softly, almost in a whisper:", "카탸가 잽싸게 문 쪽으로 움직였으나, 그루셴카에게 이르자 별안간 걸음을 멈추더니 백지장처럼 새하얗게 질려 거의 속삭이듯 나직이 신음했다."]], [["“Forgive me!”", "\"용서해 주세요!\""]], [["Grushenka stared at her and, pausing for an instant, in a vindictive, venomous voice, answered:", "그루셴카는 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 앙심에 찬, 독기 어린 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“We are full of hatred, my girl, you and I!", "\"우린 서로 미움으로 가득 차 있어요, 아가씨, 당신이나 나나!"], ["We are both full of hatred!", "우리 둘 다 미움으로 가득 차 있다고요!"], ["As though we could forgive one another!", "우리가 서로를 용서할 수나 있겠어요!"], ["Save him, and I’ll worship you all my life.”", "저이를 구해 주세요, 그러면 난 평생토록 당신을 떠받들겠어요.\""]], [["“You won’t forgive her!”", "\"넌 저 사람을 용서하지 않으려는구나!\""], ["cried Mitya, with frantic reproach.", "미탸가 미친 듯한 질책을 담아 외쳤다."]], [["“Don’t be anxious, I’ll save him for you!”", "\"걱정 마세요, 당신을 위해 저이를 구해 줄게요!\""], ["Katya whispered rapidly, and she ran out of the room.", "카탸가 빠르게 속삭이고는 방을 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“And you could refuse to forgive her when she begged your forgiveness herself?”", "\"저 사람이 제 입으로 용서를 빌었는데도 넌 용서하기를 마다할 수가 있어?\""], ["Mitya exclaimed bitterly again.", "미탸가 다시 쓰라리게 외쳤다."]], [["“Mitya, don’t dare to blame her; you have no right to!”", "\"미탸, 감히 그 사람을 탓하지 마요. 형한텐 그럴 자격이 없어요!\""], ["Alyosha cried hotly.", "알료샤가 격하게 외쳤다."]], [["“Her proud lips spoke, not her heart,”", "\"저 여자의 오만한 입술이 한 말이지, 마음에서 우러난 게 아니에요,\""], ["Grushenka brought out in a tone of disgust.", "그루셴카가 역겨운 듯한 어조로 내뱉었다."], ["“If she saves you I’ll forgive her everything—”", "\"저 여자가 당신을 구해 준다면, 난 저 여자의 모든 걸 용서하겠어요—\""]], [["She stopped speaking, as though suppressing something.", "그녀는 무언가를 억누르는 듯 말을 멈췄다."], ["She could not yet recover herself.", "그녀는 아직 마음을 추스르지 못하고 있었다."], ["She had come in, as appeared afterwards, accidentally, with no suspicion of what she would meet.", "나중에 드러난 바로, 그녀는 우연히 들어온 것이었고 자기가 무엇을 마주치게 될지 전혀 짐작도 못 했던 것이다."]], [["“Alyosha, run after her!”", "\"알료샤, 저 사람을 쫓아가!\""], ["Mitya cried to his brother; “tell her ...", "미탸가 동생에게 외쳤다. \"저 사람한테 전해 줘……"], ["I don’t know ...", "뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어……"], ["don’t let her go away like this!”", "저렇게 가 버리게 하지 마!\""]], [["“I’ll come to you again at nightfall,”", "\"날이 저물 무렵에 형한테 다시 올게,\""], ["said Alyosha, and he ran after Katya.", "알료샤는 이렇게 말하고는 카탸를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."], ["He overtook her outside the hospital grounds.", "그는 병원 구내를 벗어난 곳에서 그녀를 따라잡았다."], ["She was walking fast, but as soon as Alyosha caught her up she said quickly:", "그녀는 빠르게 걷고 있었으나, 알료샤가 따라붙기 무섭게 빠르게 말했다."]], [["“No, before that woman I can’t punish myself!", "\"안 돼요, 저 여자 앞에선 난 나 자신을 벌할 수가 없어요!"], ["I asked her forgiveness because I wanted to punish myself to the bitter end.", "내가 저 여자에게 용서를 빈 건 끝까지, 쓰라리도록 나 자신을 벌하고 싶어서였어요."], ["She would not forgive me....", "저 여잔 날 용서하려 들지 않았어요……"], ["I like her for that!”", "난 그 점이 마음에 들어요!\""], ["she added, in an unnatural voice, and her eyes flashed with fierce resentment.", "그녀가 부자연스러운 목소리로 덧붙였고, 그녀의 눈은 사나운 원한으로 번득였다."]], [["“My brother did not expect this in the least,”", "\"우리 형은 이런 걸 조금도 예상하지 못했어요,\""], ["muttered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 중얼거렸다."], ["“He was sure she would not come—”", "\"형은 그분이 오지 않으리라고 확신했거든요—\""]], [["“No doubt.", "\"그렇겠죠."], ["Let us leave that,”", "그 얘긴 그만둬요,\""], ["she snapped.", "그녀가 쏘아붙였다."], ["“Listen: I can’t go with you to the funeral now.", "\"들어요. 지금은 당신과 함께 장례식에 갈 수가 없어요."], ["I’ve sent them flowers.", "그 집에 꽃은 보냈어요."], ["I think they still have money.", "그 사람들한테 아직 돈이 남아 있을 거예요."], ["If necessary, tell them I’ll never abandon them....", "필요하면, 내가 결코 그들을 저버리지 않겠다고 전해 줘요……"], ["Now leave me, leave me, please.", "이제 날 내버려 둬요, 제발 날 내버려 둬요."], ["You are late as it is—the bells are ringing for the service....", "당신은 이미 늦었어요—예배를 알리는 종이 울리고 있어요……"], ["Leave me, please!”", "날 내버려 둬요, 제발!\""]], [["# Chapter III. Ilusha’s Funeral. The Speech At The Stone", "# Chapter III. Ilusha’s Funeral. The Speech At The Stone"]], [["He really was late.", "그는 정말로 늦었다."], ["They had waited for him and had already decided to bear the pretty flower-decked little coffin to the church without him.", "사람들은 그를 기다렸으나, 결국 그가 없이도 꽃으로 곱게 단장한 자그마한 관을 교회로 옮기기로 이미 결정한 참이었다."], ["It was the coffin of poor little Ilusha.", "그것은 가엾은 어린 일류샤의 관이었다."], ["He had died two days after Mitya was sentenced.", "그는 미탸의 판결이 내려진 지 이틀 뒤에 죽었다."], ["At the gate of the house Alyosha was met by the shouts of the boys, Ilusha’s schoolfellows.", "집 대문에서 알료샤를 맞이한 것은 소년들의, 곧 일류샤의 학교 친구들의 외침이었다."], ["They had all been impatiently expecting him and were glad that he had come at last.", "그들은 모두 그를 애타게 기다리고 있던 터라 그가 마침내 와 준 것을 기뻐했다."], ["There were about twelve of them, they all had their school-bags or satchels on their shoulders.", "모두 열두 명쯤 되었고, 하나같이 책가방이나 배낭을 어깨에 메고 있었다."], ["“Father will cry, be with father,”", "\"아버지가 우실 거야, 아버지 곁에 있어 줘.\""], ["Ilusha had told them as he lay dying, and the boys remembered it.", "일류샤가 죽어 가며 그들에게 그렇게 일렀고, 소년들은 그 말을 기억하고 있었다."], ["Kolya Krassotkin was the foremost of them.", "콜랴 크라소트킨이 그들 중 맨 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["“How glad I am you’ve come, Karamazov!”", "\"네가 와 줘서 정말 기뻐, 카라마조프!\""], ["he cried, holding out his hand to Alyosha.", "그가 알료샤에게 손을 내밀며 소리쳤다."], ["“It’s awful here.", "\"여기 정말 끔찍해."], ["It’s really horrible to see it.", "차마 보기 힘들 만큼 끔찍해."], ["Snegiryov is not drunk, we know for a fact he’s had nothing to drink to-day, but he seems as if he were drunk ...", "스네기료프는 취하지 않았어, 오늘은 한 모금도 마시지 않은 게 분명한데, 그런데도 마치 취한 사람 같아……"], ["I am always manly, but this is awful.", "나는 늘 사내답게 굴려고 하지만, 이건 정말 끔찍해."], ["Karamazov, if I am not keeping you, one question before you go in?”", "카라마조프, 붙드는 게 아니라면, 들어가기 전에 한 가지만 물어봐도 될까?\""]], [["“What is it, Kolya?”", "\"뭔데, 콜랴?\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Is your brother innocent or guilty?", "\"네 형은 무죄야, 유죄야?"], ["Was it he killed your father or was it the valet?", "너희 아버지를 죽인 게 그 사람이야, 아니면 하인이었어?"], ["As you say, so it will be.", "네가 말하는 대로 나는 믿을 거야."], ["I haven’t slept for the last four nights for thinking of it.”", "그 생각 때문에 나는 지난 나흘 밤을 한숨도 못 잤어.\""]], [["“The valet killed him, my brother is innocent,”", "\"하인이 죽인 거야, 우리 형은 무죄야.\""], ["answered Alyosha.", "알료샤가 대답했다."]], [["“That’s what I said,”", "\"내가 그렇게 말했잖아!\""], ["cried Smurov.", "스무로프가 외쳤다."]], [["“So he will perish an innocent victim!”", "\"그럼 형은 무죄한 희생자로 스러지는 거구나!\""], ["exclaimed Kolya; “though he is ruined he is happy!", "콜랴가 외쳤다. \"비록 신세를 망쳤어도 행복한 사람이야!"], ["I could envy him!”", "나는 그가 부럽기까지 해!\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["How can you? Why?”", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있어?"], ["cried Alyosha surprised.", "왜?\" 알료샤가 놀라 소리쳤다."]], [["“Oh, if I, too, could sacrifice myself some day for truth!”", "\"아, 나도 언젠가 진실을 위해 내 한 몸을 바칠 수 있다면!\""], ["said Kolya with enthusiasm.", "콜랴가 열을 올리며 말했다."]], [["“But not in such a cause, not with such disgrace and such horror!”", "\"하지만 그런 명분으로는 안 돼, 그런 치욕과 그런 끔찍함을 안고서는 안 돼!\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“Of course ...", "\"물론이지……"], ["I should like to die for all humanity, and as for disgrace, I don’t care about that—our names may perish.", "나는 온 인류를 위해 죽고 싶어, 그리고 치욕으로 말하자면, 그런 건 상관없어—우리 이름이야 스러져도 좋아."], ["I respect your brother!”", "나는 네 형을 존경해!\""]], [["“And so do I!”", "\"나도 그래!\""], ["the boy, who had once declared that he knew who had founded Troy, cried suddenly and unexpectedly, and he blushed up to his ears like a peony as he had done on that occasion.", "한때 트로이를 누가 세웠는지 자기가 안다고 큰소리쳤던 소년이 갑작스럽고 뜻밖에 외치고는, 그때 그랬듯이 작약처럼 귀밑까지 새빨개졌다."]], [["Alyosha went into the room.", "알료샤는 방 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Ilusha lay with his hands folded and his eyes closed in a blue coffin with a white frill round it.", "일류샤는 흰 주름 장식이 둘린 파란 관 속에 두 손을 모으고 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었다."], ["His thin face was hardly changed at all, and strange to say there was no smell of decay from the corpse.", "그 야윈 얼굴은 거의 변하지 않았고, 기이하게도 시신에서는 썩는 냄새가 나지 않았다."], ["The expression of his face was serious and, as it were, thoughtful.", "그 얼굴의 표정은 엄숙했고, 말하자면 무언가를 골똘히 생각하는 듯했다."], ["His hands, crossed over his breast, looked particularly beautiful, as though chiseled in marble.", "가슴 위로 포개진 두 손은 유난히 아름다워 보였으니, 마치 대리석으로 깎아 놓은 것 같았다."], ["There were flowers in his hands and the coffin, inside and out, was decked with flowers, which had been sent early in the morning by Lise Hohlakov.", "그 손에는 꽃이 들려 있었고, 관 안팎은 꽃으로 단장되어 있었는데, 그것은 아침 일찍 리즈 호흘라코바가 보내온 것이었다."], ["But there were flowers too from Katerina Ivanovna, and when Alyosha opened the door, the captain had a bunch in his trembling hands and was strewing them again over his dear boy.", "그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 보낸 꽃도 있어서, 알료샤가 문을 열었을 때 이등 대위는 떨리는 두 손에 한 다발을 쥐고 사랑하는 아들 위에 다시 흩뿌리고 있었다."], ["He scarcely glanced at Alyosha when he came in, and he would not look at any one, even at his crazy weeping wife, “mamma,” who kept trying to stand on her crippled legs to get a nearer look at her dead boy.", "그는 알료샤가 들어왔을 때 그를 거의 쳐다보지도 않았고, 누구도 보려 하지 않았으니, 죽은 아들을 더 가까이서 보려고 절름거리는 다리로 끊임없이 일어서려 애쓰는 미친 듯 우는 아내, \"엄마\"조차 거들떠보지 않았다."], ["Nina had been pushed in her chair by the boys close up to the coffin.", "니나는 소년들이 의자째 밀어 관 가까이로 옮겨 놓았다."], ["She sat with her head pressed to it and she too was no doubt quietly weeping.", "그녀는 관에 머리를 댄 채 앉아 있었고, 그녀 역시 의심할 바 없이 소리 없이 울고 있었다."], ["Snegiryov’s face looked eager, yet bewildered and exasperated.", "스네기료프의 얼굴은 다급해 보였으나, 동시에 어리둥절하고 격앙되어 있었다."], ["There was something crazy about his gestures and the words that broke from him.", "그의 몸짓에도, 그의 입에서 터져 나오는 말에도 무언가 실성한 듯한 데가 있었다."], ["“Old man, dear old man!”", "\"꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야!\""], ["he exclaimed every minute, gazing at Ilusha.", "그는 일류샤를 바라보며 시시각각 외쳤다."], ["It was his habit to call Ilusha “old man,” as a term of affection when he was alive.", "일류샤가 살아 있을 때 그를 \"꼬마\"라고 부르는 것이 애정의 표현으로서 그의 버릇이었다."]], [["“Father, give me a flower, too; take that white one out of his hand and give it me,”", "\"여보, 나한테도 꽃 한 송이 줘요. 저 애 손에서 흰 꽃을 빼내서 나한테 줘요.\""], ["the crazy mother begged, whimpering.", "미친 어머니가 흐느끼며 졸랐다."], ["Either because the little white rose in Ilusha’s hand had caught her fancy or that she wanted one from his hand to keep in memory of him, she moved restlessly, stretching out her hands for the flower.", "일류샤의 손에 들린 그 작은 흰 장미가 마음에 들었던 것인지, 아니면 그를 기리며 간직하려고 그의 손에서 나온 꽃을 갖고 싶었던 것인지, 그녀는 꽃을 향해 두 손을 뻗으며 안절부절못했다."]], [["“I won’t give it to any one, I won’t give you anything,”", "\"누구한테도 안 줘, 당신한테는 아무것도 안 줘.\""], ["Snegiryov cried callously.", "스네기료프가 매정하게 소리쳤다."], ["“They are his flowers, not yours!", "\"이건 저 애 꽃이야, 당신 것이 아니라고!"], ["Everything is his, nothing is yours!”", "전부 저 애 거야, 당신 건 아무것도 없어!\""]], [["“Father, give mother a flower!”", "\"아빠, 엄마한테 꽃 한 송이 줘요!\""], ["said Nina, lifting her face wet with tears.", "니나가 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 들며 말했다."]], [["“I won’t give away anything and to her less than any one!", "\"나는 아무것도 안 내줘, 더구나 저 여자한테는 누구보다도 안 줘!"], ["She didn’t love Ilusha.", "저 여자는 일류샤를 사랑하지 않았어."], ["She took away his little cannon and he gave it to her,”", "저 여자가 저 애의 작은 대포를 빼앗아 갔는데, 저 애가 그걸 줬던 거지.\""], ["the captain broke into loud sobs at the thought of how Ilusha had given up his cannon to his mother.", "일류샤가 어머니에게 자기 대포를 내주었던 일을 떠올리며 이등 대위는 큰 소리로 흐느끼기 시작했다."], ["The poor, crazy creature was bathed in noiseless tears, hiding her face in her hands.", "가엾고 실성한 여인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 소리 없는 눈물로 흠뻑 젖어 있었다."]], [["The boys, seeing that the father would not leave the coffin and that it was time to carry it out, stood round it in a close circle and began to lift it up.", "소년들은 아버지가 관에서 떨어지려 하지 않는 것을 보고, 또 이제 관을 운구할 때가 되었음을 알고는, 관 둘레에 빽빽이 둘러서서 그것을 들어 올리기 시작했다."]], [["“I don’t want him to be buried in the churchyard,”", "\"저 애를 묘지에 묻고 싶지 않아.\""], ["Snegiryov wailed suddenly; “I’ll bury him by the stone, by our stone!", "스네기료프가 갑자기 통곡했다. \"나는 그 돌 옆에, 우리 돌 옆에 묻을 거야!"], ["Ilusha told me to.", "일류샤가 나한테 그렇게 일렀어."], ["I won’t let him be carried out!”", "저 애를 못 내가게 하겠어!\""]], [["He had been saying for the last three days that he would bury him by the stone, but Alyosha, Krassotkin, the landlady, her sister and all the boys interfered.", "그는 지난 사흘 동안 아들을 그 돌 옆에 묻겠다고 말해 왔으나, 알료샤와 크라소트킨, 집주인 여자와 그 자매, 그리고 모든 소년들이 가로막았다."]], [["“What an idea, bury him by an unholy stone, as though he had hanged himself!”", "\"무슨 그런 생각을, 마치 목이라도 맨 사람처럼 부정한 돌 옆에 묻다니!\""], ["the old landlady said sternly.", "늙은 집주인 여자가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“There in the churchyard the ground has been crossed.", "\"저기 묘지에는 땅이 축성되어 있어요."], ["He’ll be prayed for there.", "거기서라야 그 애를 위해 기도를 드릴 수 있지요."], ["One can hear the singing in church and the deacon reads so plainly and verbally that it will reach him every time just as though it were read over his grave.”", "교회에서 부르는 성가도 들리고, 부제가 어찌나 또렷하고 분명하게 읽는지, 마치 그 애 무덤 위에서 읽어 주는 것처럼 그때마다 그 애한테 가닿을 거예요.\""]], [["At last the captain made a gesture of despair as though to say, “Take him where you will.”", "마침내 이등 대위는 \"마음대로 데려가라\"고 말하듯 절망적인 몸짓을 했다."], ["The boys raised the coffin, but as they passed the mother, they stopped for a moment and lowered it that she might say good-by to Ilusha.", "소년들이 관을 들어 올렸으나, 어머니 곁을 지날 때 잠시 멈춰 서서 관을 내려 그녀가 일류샤에게 작별을 고할 수 있게 했다."], ["But on seeing that precious little face, which for the last three days she had only looked at from a distance, she trembled all over and her gray head began twitching spasmodically over the coffin.", "그러나 지난 사흘 동안 멀찍이서만 바라보았던 그 소중한 작은 얼굴을 보자, 그녀는 온몸을 부들부들 떨었고, 그 희끗희끗한 머리가 관 위에서 경련하듯 씰룩거리기 시작했다."]], [["“Mother, make the sign of the cross over him, give him your blessing, kiss him,”", "\"엄마, 저 애한테 성호를 그어 줘요, 축복해 줘요, 입을 맞춰 줘요.\""], ["Nina cried to her.", "니나가 그녀에게 외쳤다."], ["But her head still twitched like an automaton and with a face contorted with bitter grief she began, without a word, beating her breast with her fist.", "그러나 그녀의 머리는 여전히 자동인형처럼 씰룩거렸고, 비통한 슬픔으로 일그러진 얼굴로 그녀는 아무 말 없이 주먹으로 제 가슴을 두드리기 시작했다."], ["They carried the coffin past her.", "사람들이 관을 들고 그녀 앞을 지나갔다."], ["Nina pressed her lips to her brother’s for the last time as they bore the coffin by her.", "그들이 관을 들고 곁을 지날 때 니나는 마지막으로 오빠에게 입술을 댔다."], ["As Alyosha went out of the house he begged the landlady to look after those who were left behind, but she interrupted him before he had finished.", "알료샤는 집을 나서며 집주인 여자에게 뒤에 남는 이들을 돌보아 달라고 부탁했으나, 그가 말을 미처 끝내기도 전에 그녀가 가로막았다."]], [["“To be sure, I’ll stay with them, we are Christians, too.”", "\"물론이지요, 내가 저들과 함께 있을게요, 우리도 기독교인인걸요.\""], ["The old woman wept as she said it.", "늙은 여인은 그렇게 말하며 울었다."]], [["They had not far to carry the coffin to the church, not more than three hundred paces.", "교회까지 관을 운구하는 길은 멀지 않아서, 삼백 걸음 남짓이었다."], ["It was a still, clear day, with a slight frost.", "고요하고 맑은 날에 가벼운 서리가 내려 있었다."], ["The church bells were still ringing.", "교회 종소리가 여전히 울리고 있었다."], ["Snegiryov ran fussing and distracted after the coffin, in his short old summer overcoat, with his head bare and his soft, old, wide-brimmed hat in his hand.", "스네기료프는 낡고 짧은 여름 외투를 걸치고, 머리에는 아무것도 쓰지 않은 채 부드럽고 낡은 챙 넓은 모자를 손에 든 채로, 어쩔 줄 모르고 안달하며 관 뒤를 따라 달렸다."], ["He seemed in a state of bewildered anxiety.", "그는 어리둥절한 불안에 사로잡힌 듯했다."], ["At one minute he stretched out his hand to support the head of the coffin and only hindered the bearers, at another he ran alongside and tried to find a place for himself there.", "한순간에는 손을 뻗어 관의 머리 쪽을 받치려다 운구하는 이들을 방해할 뿐이었고, 또 한순간에는 관 옆을 나란히 달리며 거기서 제가 설 자리를 찾으려 했다."], ["A flower fell on the snow and he rushed to pick it up as though everything in the world depended on the loss of that flower.", "꽃 한 송이가 눈 위로 떨어지자 그는 마치 그 꽃 한 송이를 잃느냐 마느냐에 온 세상이 달려 있기라도 한 듯 그것을 주우려고 달려들었다."]], [["“And the crust of bread, we’ve forgotten the crust!”", "\"그리고 빵 조각, 우리가 빵 조각을 깜빡했어!\""], ["he cried suddenly in dismay.", "그가 갑자기 당황하여 외쳤다."], ["But the boys reminded him at once that he had taken the crust of bread already and that it was in his pocket.", "그러나 소년들이 곧바로, 그가 빵 조각을 벌써 챙겨 주머니에 넣었다고 일깨워 주었다."], ["He instantly pulled it out and was reassured.", "그는 즉시 그것을 꺼내 보고서 안심했다."]], [["“Ilusha told me to, Ilusha,”", "\"일류샤가 나한테 일렀어, 일류샤가.\""], ["he explained at once to Alyosha.", "그가 곧바로 알료샤에게 설명했다. \"어느 날 밤 내가 저 애 곁에 앉아 있는데 저 애가 갑자기 나한테 말하더군."], ["“I was sitting by him one night and he suddenly told me: ‘Father, when my grave is filled up crumble a piece of bread on it so that the sparrows may fly down, I shall hear and it will cheer me up not to be lying alone.’”", "'아버지, 제 무덤에 흙이 다 덮이거든 그 위에 빵 한 조각을 부숴 뿌려 주세요. 참새들이 날아 내려오게요, 그러면 제가 그 소리를 듣고 혼자 누워 있지 않게 되어 기분이 좋아질 거예요.'\""]], [["“That’s a good thing,”", "\"그것 참 좋은 일이오.\""], ["said Alyosha, “we must often take some.”", "알료샤가 말했다. \"우리가 자주 빵을 좀 가져와야겠소.\""]], [["“Every day, every day!”", "\"날마다, 날마다요!\""], ["said the captain quickly, seeming cheered at the thought.", "이등 대위가 그 생각에 기운이 나는 듯 얼른 말했다."]], [["They reached the church at last and set the coffin in the middle of it.", "마침내 그들은 교회에 이르러 관을 한가운데에 놓았다."], ["The boys surrounded it and remained reverently standing so, all through the service.", "소년들은 관을 둘러싸고 예배가 진행되는 내내 경건하게 서 있었다."], ["It was an old and rather poor church; many of the ikons were without settings; but such churches are the best for praying in.", "그곳은 오래되고 제법 가난한 교회였으니, 많은 성상들이 장식 틀도 없었다. 그러나 그런 교회야말로 기도하기에는 가장 좋은 곳이다."], ["During the mass Snegiryov became somewhat calmer, though at times he had outbursts of the same unconscious and, as it were, incoherent anxiety.", "미사가 진행되는 동안 스네기료프는 다소 진정되었으나, 때때로 똑같이 무의식적이고, 말하자면 종잡을 수 없는 불안에 사로잡혀 발작을 일으켰다."], ["At one moment he went up to the coffin to set straight the cover or the wreath, when a candle fell out of the candlestick he rushed to replace it and was a fearful time fumbling over it, then he subsided and stood quietly by the coffin with a look of blank uneasiness and perplexity.", "어느 순간에는 관으로 다가가 덮개나 화환을 바로잡았고, 촛대에서 초가 떨어지자 그것을 다시 꽂으려고 달려들어 한참을 더듬거리며 애를 먹었으며, 그러다가 가라앉아서는 멍한 불안과 당혹의 빛을 띤 채 관 곁에 가만히 서 있었다."], ["After the Epistle he suddenly whispered to Alyosha, who was standing beside him, that the Epistle had not been read properly but did not explain what he meant.", "사도 서간이 봉독된 뒤에 그는 갑자기 옆에 서 있던 알료샤에게 사도 서간이 제대로 봉독되지 않았다고 속삭였으나, 무슨 뜻인지는 설명하지 않았다."], ["During the prayer, “Like the Cherubim,” he joined in the singing but did not go on to the end.", "\"거룩한 천사들처럼\"이라는 기도가 진행되는 동안 그는 성가에 끼어들었으나 끝까지 부르지는 않았다."], ["Falling on his knees, he pressed his forehead to the stone floor and lay so for a long while.", "그는 무릎을 꿇더니 돌바닥에 이마를 댄 채 오랫동안 그렇게 엎드려 있었다."]], [["At last came the funeral service itself and candles were distributed.", "마침내 장례 예식 자체가 시작되었고 초가 나누어졌다."], ["The distracted father began fussing about again, but the touching and impressive funeral prayers moved and roused his soul.", "정신을 못 차리는 아버지는 다시 안절부절못하기 시작했으나, 가슴을 울리고 감동을 자아내는 장례 기도가 그의 영혼을 흔들어 일깨웠다."], ["He seemed suddenly to shrink together and broke into rapid, short sobs, which he tried at first to smother, but at last he sobbed aloud.", "그는 갑자기 몸이 오그라드는 듯하더니 빠르고 짧은 흐느낌을 터뜨렸고, 처음에는 그것을 억누르려 했으나 끝내 소리 내어 흐느꼈다."], ["When they began taking leave of the dead and closing the coffin, he flung his arms about, as though he would not allow them to cover Ilusha, and began greedily and persistently kissing his dead boy on the lips.", "죽은 이에게 작별을 고하고 관을 닫으려 하자, 그는 마치 일류샤를 덮는 것을 허락하지 않으려는 듯 두 팔을 휘저으며 죽은 아들의 입술에 게걸스럽고 집요하게 입을 맞추기 시작했다."], ["At last they succeeded in persuading him to come away from the step, but suddenly he impulsively stretched out his hand and snatched a few flowers from the coffin.", "마침내 사람들이 그를 설득해 관 옆에서 물러나게 하는 데 성공했으나, 그는 갑자기 충동적으로 손을 뻗어 관에서 꽃 몇 송이를 낚아챘다."], ["He looked at them and a new idea seemed to dawn upon him, so that he apparently forgot his grief for a minute.", "그는 그것을 들여다보았고, 무언가 새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯해서 잠시 동안 자기 슬픔을 잊은 것처럼 보였다."], ["Gradually he seemed to sink into brooding and did not resist when the coffin was lifted up and carried to the grave.", "차츰 그는 깊은 상념에 잠기는 듯하더니 관이 들어 올려져 무덤으로 운구될 때에도 저항하지 않았다."], ["It was an expensive one in the churchyard close to the church, Katerina Ivanovna had paid for it.", "그것은 교회 가까운 묘지의 값비싼 자리였으니, 카테리나 이바노브나가 비용을 치렀다."], ["After the customary rites the grave-diggers lowered the coffin.", "관례적인 의식이 끝난 뒤 인부들이 관을 내렸다."], ["Snegiryov with his flowers in his hands bent down so low over the open grave that the boys caught hold of his coat in alarm and pulled him back.", "스네기료프는 손에 꽃을 쥔 채 파헤쳐진 무덤 위로 어찌나 깊이 몸을 숙였던지, 소년들이 놀라서 그의 외투를 붙잡아 끌어당겼다."], ["He did not seem to understand fully what was happening.", "그는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 온전히 이해하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["When they began filling up the grave, he suddenly pointed anxiously at the falling earth and began trying to say something, but no one could make out what he meant, and he stopped suddenly.", "사람들이 무덤을 메우기 시작하자, 그는 갑자기 떨어지는 흙을 불안하게 가리키며 무언가를 말하려 했으나, 아무도 그가 무슨 뜻인지 알아듣지 못했고, 그는 별안간 멈추었다."], ["Then he was reminded that he must crumble the bread and he was awfully excited, snatched up the bread and began pulling it to pieces and flinging the morsels on the grave.", "그러자 빵을 부숴 뿌려야 한다는 것을 누군가 일깨워 주었고, 그는 몹시 흥분하여 빵을 움켜쥐고는 그것을 잘게 부수어 그 부스러기를 무덤 위로 흩뿌리기 시작했다."]], [["“Come, fly down, birds, fly down, sparrows!”", "\"자, 날아 내려와라, 새들아, 날아 내려와라, 참새들아!\""], ["he muttered anxiously.", "그는 불안하게 중얼거렸다."]], [["One of the boys observed that it was awkward for him to crumble the bread with the flowers in his hands and suggested he should give them to some one to hold for a time.", "소년 중 하나가, 손에 꽃을 쥔 채로 빵을 부수려니 불편해 보인다며, 잠시 누군가에게 꽃을 들고 있게 하라고 권했다."], ["But he would not do this and seemed indeed suddenly alarmed for his flowers, as though they wanted to take them from him altogether.", "그러나 그는 그러려 하지 않았고, 실은 마치 누군가 자기 꽃을 영영 빼앗아 가려는 것처럼 갑자기 꽃을 두고 놀라는 듯했다."], ["And after looking at the grave, and as it were, satisfying himself that everything had been done and the bread had been crumbled, he suddenly, to the surprise of every one, turned, quite composedly even, and made his way homewards.", "그리고 무덤을 들여다보고 나서, 말하자면 모든 일이 끝났고 빵도 다 부수어 뿌렸음을 스스로 확인한 뒤, 그는 모두를 놀라게 하면서 별안간, 게다가 자못 침착하게 돌아서서 집을 향해 걷기 시작했다."], ["But his steps became more and more hurried, he almost ran.", "그러나 그의 발걸음은 점점 더 다급해져, 거의 달리다시피 했다."], ["The boys and Alyosha kept up with him.", "소년들과 알료샤가 그를 따라붙었다."]], [["“The flowers are for mamma, the flowers are for mamma!", "\"이 꽃은 엄마한테 줄 거야, 이 꽃은 엄마 거야!"], ["I was unkind to mamma,”", "내가 엄마한테 매정하게 굴었어.\""], ["he began exclaiming suddenly.", "그가 갑자기 외쳐 대기 시작했다."]], [["Some one called to him to put on his hat as it was cold.", "누군가 그에게 날이 추우니 모자를 쓰라고 했다."], ["But he flung the hat in the snow as though he were angry and kept repeating, “I won’t have the hat, I won’t have the hat.”", "그러나 그는 마치 화가 난 듯 모자를 눈 위로 내던지며 \"모자는 안 써, 모자는 안 써\"라고 거듭 말했다."], ["Smurov picked it up and carried it after him.", "스무로프가 그것을 주워 들고 그를 따라가며 들고 갔다."], ["All the boys were crying, and Kolya and the boy who discovered about Troy most of all.", "소년들은 모두 울고 있었고, 그 가운데서도 콜랴와 트로이에 관해 알아냈다는 그 소년이 가장 심하게 울었다."], ["Though Smurov, with the captain’s hat in his hand, was crying bitterly too, he managed, as he ran, to snatch up a piece of red brick that lay on the snow of the path, to fling it at the flock of sparrows that was flying by.", "이등 대위의 모자를 손에 든 스무로프 역시 비통하게 울면서도, 달리는 와중에 길의 눈 위에 놓인 붉은 벽돌 조각 하나를 집어 들어, 마침 날아가는 참새 떼를 향해 던질 틈을 냈다."], ["He missed them, of course, and went on crying as he ran.", "물론 그는 빗맞혔고, 달리면서 계속 울었다."], ["Half-way, Snegiryov suddenly stopped, stood still for half a minute, as though struck by something, and suddenly turning back to the church, ran towards the deserted grave.", "길을 절반쯤 갔을 때 스네기료프는 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 무언가에 부딪힌 듯 반 분쯤 우뚝 서 있다가, 별안간 교회 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 인적 없는 무덤을 향해 달렸다."], ["But the boys instantly overtook him and caught hold of him on all sides.", "그러나 소년들이 곧바로 그를 따라잡아 사방에서 그를 붙들었다."], ["Then he fell helpless on the snow as though he had been knocked down, and struggling, sobbing, and wailing, he began crying out, “Ilusha, old man, dear old man!”", "그러자 그는 마치 누가 떠밀어 쓰러뜨리기라도 한 듯 힘없이 눈 위로 쓰러졌고, 몸부림치고 흐느끼고 통곡하면서 \"일류샤, 꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야!\""], ["Alyosha and Kolya tried to make him get up, soothing and persuading him.", "하고 외치기 시작했다. 알료샤와 콜랴가 그를 일으키려 하며 어르고 달랬다."]], [["“Captain, give over, a brave man must show fortitude,”", "\"대위님, 그만하세요, 용감한 사내라면 꿋꿋함을 보여야지요.\""], ["muttered Kolya.", "콜랴가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You’ll spoil the flowers,”", "\"꽃이 상하겠어요.\""], ["said Alyosha, “and mamma is expecting them, she is sitting crying because you would not give her any before.", "알료샤가 말했다. \"엄마가 기다리고 계세요, 아까 당신이 꽃을 한 송이도 주지 않아서 앉아 우신다고요."], ["Ilusha’s little bed is still there—”", "일류샤의 작은 침대도 아직 그대로 있고요—\""]], [["“Yes, yes, mamma!”", "\"그래, 그래, 엄마!\""], ["Snegiryov suddenly recollected, “they’ll take away the bed, they’ll take it away,”", "스네기료프가 갑자기 생각이 났는지 외쳤다. \"저들이 침대를 가져갈 거야, 가져갈 거라고.\""], ["he added as though alarmed that they really would.", "그는 정말로 사람들이 그럴까 봐 놀라는 듯 덧붙였다."], ["He jumped up and ran homewards again.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 다시 집을 향해 달렸다."], ["But it was not far off and they all arrived together.", "그러나 거리가 멀지 않아 모두 함께 도착했다."], ["Snegiryov opened the door hurriedly and called to his wife with whom he had so cruelly quarreled just before:", "스네기료프는 황급히 문을 열고, 바로 조금 전에 그토록 모질게 다투었던 아내를 불렀다."]], [["“Mamma, poor crippled darling, Ilusha has sent you these flowers,”", "\"엄마, 가여운 절름발이 여보, 일류샤가 당신한테 이 꽃을 보냈어.\""], ["he cried, holding out to her a little bunch of flowers that had been frozen and broken while he was struggling in the snow.", "그가, 그가 눈 속에서 몸부림치는 동안 얼어붙고 부러진 작은 꽃다발을 그녀에게 내밀며 외쳤다."], ["But at that instant he saw in the corner, by the little bed, Ilusha’s little boots, which the landlady had put tidily side by side.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 작은 침대 옆 구석에 집주인 여자가 가지런히 나란히 놓아 둔 일류샤의 작은 장화를 보았다."], ["Seeing the old, patched, rusty-looking, stiff boots he flung up his hands and rushed to them, fell on his knees, snatched up one boot and, pressing his lips to it, began kissing it greedily, crying, “Ilusha, old man, dear old man, where are your little feet?”", "낡고 기운 자국이 있는, 녹슨 듯한 빛깔의 뻣뻣한 그 장화를 보자 그는 두 손을 치켜들고 그쪽으로 달려가 무릎을 꿇더니, 장화 한 짝을 움켜쥐고 거기에 입술을 댄 채 게걸스럽게 입 맞추기 시작했고, \"일류샤, 꼬마야, 가여운 꼬마야, 네 작은 발은 어디 있느냐?\" 하고 외쳐 댔다."]], [["“Where have you taken him away?", "\"저 애를 어디로 데려간 거야?"], ["Where have you taken him?”", "어디로 데려갔어?\""], ["the lunatic cried in a heartrending voice.", "실성한 여인이 가슴을 찢는 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["Nina, too, broke into sobs.", "니나 역시 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."], ["Kolya ran out of the room, the boys followed him.", "콜랴가 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 소년들이 그를 따라 나갔다."], ["At last Alyosha too went out.", "마침내 알료샤도 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["“Let them weep,”", "\"저들이 울게 두자.\""], ["he said to Kolya, “it’s no use trying to comfort them just now.", "그가 콜랴에게 말했다. \"지금은 위로해 봐야 소용없어."], ["Let us wait a minute and then go back.”", "잠깐 기다렸다가 다시 들어가자.\""]], [["“No, it’s no use, it’s awful,”", "\"그래, 소용없어, 끔찍해.\""], ["Kolya assented.", "콜랴가 동의했다."], ["“Do you know, Karamazov,”", "\"있잖아, 카라마조프.\""], ["he dropped his voice so that no one could hear them, “I feel dreadfully sad, and if it were only possible to bring him back, I’d give anything in the world to do it.”", "그가 아무도 듣지 못하게 목소리를 낮추었다. \"나는 너무나 슬퍼, 그리고 만약 그 애를 다시 살려 낼 수만 있다면, 나는 이 세상의 무엇이든 다 내놓겠어.\""]], [["“Ah, so would I,”", "\"아, 나도 그래.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“What do you think, Karamazov?", "\"어떻게 생각해, 카라마조프?"], ["Had we better come back here to-night?", "우리 오늘 밤에 여기 다시 오는 게 좋을까?"], ["He’ll be drunk, you know.”", "알다시피 그 사람은 취해 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“Perhaps he will.", "\"아마 그럴 거야."], ["Let us come together, you and I, that will be enough, to spend an hour with them, with the mother and Nina.", "너랑 나, 우리 둘이서만 함께 오자, 그거면 충분해, 어머니와 니나하고 한 시간쯤 보내려면."], ["If we all come together we shall remind them of everything again,”", "우리가 다 함께 오면 저들에게 또다시 모든 걸 떠올리게 할 테니까.\""], ["Alyosha suggested.", "알료샤가 제안했다."]], [["“The landlady is laying the table for them now—there’ll be a funeral dinner or something, the priest is coming; shall we go back to it, Karamazov?”", "\"집주인 아주머니가 지금 저들에게 상을 차리고 있어—장례 만찬이라든가 뭐 그런 게 있을 거야, 신부님도 오시고. 우리 거기 다시 갈까, 카라마조프?\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["said Alyosha.", "알료샤가 말했다."]], [["“It’s all so strange, Karamazov, such sorrow and then pancakes after it, it all seems so unnatural in our religion.”", "\"다 너무 이상해, 카라마조프, 그런 슬픔이 있고 나서 곧장 블린이라니, 우리 종교에선 다 그렇게 부자연스러워 보여.\""]], [["“They are going to have salmon, too,”", "\"연어도 나올 거야.\""], ["the boy who had discovered about Troy observed in a loud voice.", "트로이에 관해 알아냈다는 그 소년이 큰 소리로 한마디 했다."]], [["“I beg you most earnestly, Kartashov, not to interrupt again with your idiotic remarks, especially when one is not talking to you and doesn’t care to know whether you exist or not!”", "\"카르타쇼프, 제발 진심으로 부탁하는데, 그 멍청한 소리로 또 끼어들지 좀 마, 더구나 아무도 너한테 말 거는 사람이 없고 네가 존재하든 말든 알 바도 아닐 때는!\""], ["Kolya snapped out irritably.", "콜랴가 짜증스럽게 쏘아붙였다."], ["The boy flushed crimson but did not dare to reply.", "소년은 얼굴이 새빨개졌으나 감히 대꾸하지는 못했다."]], [["Meantime they were strolling slowly along the path and suddenly Smurov exclaimed:", "그러는 사이 그들은 길을 따라 천천히 거닐고 있었는데, 별안간 스무로프가 외쳤다."]], [["“There’s Ilusha’s stone, under which they wanted to bury him.”", "\"저기 일류샤의 돌이야, 저들이 그 밑에 일류샤를 묻으려 했던.\""]], [["They all stood still by the big stone.", "그들은 모두 그 큰 돌 옆에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Alyosha looked and the whole picture of what Snegiryov had described to him that day, how Ilusha, weeping and hugging his father, had cried, “Father, father, how he insulted you,”", "알료샤는 그것을 바라보았고, 그날 스네기료프가 그에게 들려주었던 광경 전체가, 곧 일류샤가 울면서 아버지를 끌어안고 \"아버지, 아버지, 저자가 아버지를 얼마나 모욕했는지!\""], ["rose at once before his imagination.", "하고 외쳤다던 그 광경이 단번에 그의 상상 속에 떠올랐다."]], [["A sudden impulse seemed to come into his soul.", "문득 어떤 충동이 그의 영혼 속에 차오르는 듯했다."], ["With a serious and earnest expression he looked from one to another of the bright, pleasant faces of Ilusha’s schoolfellows, and suddenly said to them:", "그는 진지하고 간곡한 표정으로 일류샤의 학교 친구들의 밝고 정다운 얼굴을 하나하나 둘러보다가, 별안간 그들에게 말했다."]], [["“Boys, I should like to say one word to you, here at this place.”", "\"얘들아, 바로 이 자리에서 너희에게 한마디 하고 싶구나.\""]], [["The boys stood round him and at once bent attentive and expectant eyes upon him.", "소년들은 그를 둘러싸고 곧바로 주의 깊고 기대에 찬 눈길을 그에게 쏟았다."]], [["“Boys, we shall soon part.", "\"얘들아, 우리는 곧 헤어지게 될 거야."], ["I shall be for some time with my two brothers, of whom one is going to Siberia and the other is lying at death’s door.", "나는 한동안 두 형과 함께 지낼 텐데, 한 사람은 시베리아로 가게 됐고 다른 한 사람은 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있단다."], ["But soon I shall leave this town, perhaps for a long time, so we shall part.", "하지만 머지않아 나는 이 도시를 떠날 거야, 어쩌면 오랫동안 말이야, 그러니 우리는 헤어지게 되는 거지."], ["Let us make a compact here, at Ilusha’s stone, that we will never forget Ilusha and one another.", "자, 여기 일류샤의 돌 옆에서 약속을 하자, 우리는 일류샤를, 그리고 서로를 결코 잊지 않겠다고."], ["And whatever happens to us later in life, if we don’t meet for twenty years afterwards, let us always remember how we buried the poor boy at whom we once threw stones, do you remember, by the bridge?", "그리고 앞으로 살면서 우리에게 무슨 일이 일어나든, 설령 그 뒤로 이십 년 동안 만나지 못한다 해도, 우리가 한때 돌을 던졌던 그 가여운 아이를 어떻게 묻었는지 늘 기억하자, 기억나니, 다리 옆에서 말이야?"], ["and afterwards we all grew so fond of him.", "그러고 나서 우리 모두 그 애를 무척 좋아하게 됐지."], ["He was a fine boy, a kind-hearted, brave boy, he felt for his father’s honor and resented the cruel insult to him and stood up for him.", "그 애는 훌륭한 아이였고, 마음씨 곱고 용감한 아이였어, 아버지의 명예를 위해 가슴 아파했고 아버지가 받은 잔인한 모욕에 분개해 아버지 편에 서서 맞섰지."], ["And so in the first place, we will remember him, boys, all our lives.", "그러니 무엇보다 먼저, 우리는 평생토록 그 애를 기억하자, 얘들아."], ["And even if we are occupied with most important things, if we attain to honor or fall into great misfortune—still let us remember how good it was once here, when we were all together, united by a good and kind feeling which made us, for the time we were loving that poor boy, better perhaps than we are.", "그리고 설령 우리가 더없이 중요한 일에 몰두하게 되더라도, 명예를 얻든 큰 불행에 빠지든—그래도 한때 여기서 우리 모두가 함께 모여 어떤 선하고 따뜻한 마음으로 하나가 되어, 그 가여운 아이를 사랑하던 그동안만큼은 어쩌면 지금의 우리보다 더 나은 사람이 되었던 그 시절이 얼마나 좋았는지 기억하자."], ["My little doves—let me call you so, for you are very like them, those pretty blue birds, at this minute as I look at your good dear faces.", "나의 작은 비둘기들아—너희를 그렇게 부르게 해 다오, 지금 너희의 선하고 정다운 얼굴들을 바라보고 있노라니 너희가 그 어여쁜 파랑새들을 꼭 닮았으니 말이다."], ["My dear children, perhaps you won’t understand what I am saying to you, because I often speak very unintelligibly, but you’ll remember it all the same and will agree with my words some time.", "나의 사랑하는 아이들아, 어쩌면 너희는 내가 너희에게 하는 말을 이해하지 못할지도 모르겠다, 나는 흔히 아주 알아듣기 어렵게 말하니까. 하지만 너희는 그래도 이 말을 기억할 테고, 언젠가는 내 말에 동의하게 될 거야."], ["You must know that there is nothing higher and stronger and more wholesome and good for life in the future than some good memory, especially a memory of childhood, of home.", "너희가 알아 두어야 할 것은, 앞으로의 삶을 위해 어떤 좋은 추억보다, 특히 어린 시절의 추억, 집에 대한 추억보다 더 높고 더 강하고 더 건전하고 좋은 것은 없다는 사실이야."], ["People talk to you a great deal about your education, but some good, sacred memory, preserved from childhood, is perhaps the best education.", "사람들은 너희에게 교육에 관해 많은 이야기를 하지만, 어린 시절부터 간직해 온 어떤 선하고 거룩한 추억이야말로 어쩌면 가장 훌륭한 교육이란다."], ["If a man carries many such memories with him into life, he is safe to the end of his days, and if one has only one good memory left in one’s heart, even that may sometime be the means of saving us.", "만약 사람이 그런 추억을 많이 지니고 삶 속으로 들어간다면, 그는 죽는 날까지 안전한 거야. 그리고 마음속에 좋은 추억이 단 하나만 남아 있어도, 그것조차 언젠가 우리를 구원하는 수단이 될 수 있어."], ["Perhaps we may even grow wicked later on, may be unable to refrain from a bad action, may laugh at men’s tears and at those people who say as Kolya did just now, ‘I want to suffer for all men,’ and may even jeer spitefully at such people.", "어쩌면 우리는 훗날 사악해질 수도 있고, 나쁜 짓을 저지르지 않고는 못 배기게 될 수도 있고, 사람들의 눈물을, 그리고 방금 콜랴가 그랬듯이 '나는 온 인류를 위해 고통받고 싶다'고 말하는 그런 사람들을 비웃을 수도 있고, 심지어 그런 사람들을 악의에 차서 조롱할 수도 있어."], ["But however bad we may become—which God forbid—yet, when we recall how we buried Ilusha, how we loved him in his last days, and how we have been talking like friends all together, at this stone, the cruelest and most mocking of us—if we do become so—will not dare to laugh inwardly at having been kind and good at this moment!", "하지만 우리가 아무리 나빠진다 해도—하느님께서 막아 주시기를—그래도 우리가 일류샤를 어떻게 묻었는지, 그 애의 마지막 날들에 우리가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그리고 이 돌 옆에서 우리 모두 친구처럼 어떻게 이야기를 나누었는지 떠올린다면, 우리 가운데 가장 잔인하고 가장 비웃기 좋아하는 자라도—혹시 우리가 그렇게 된다 한들—이 순간 우리가 선하고 따뜻했다는 사실을 속으로 비웃지는 못할 거야!"], ["What’s more, perhaps, that one memory may keep him from great evil and he will reflect and say, ‘Yes, I was good and brave and honest then!’ Let him laugh to himself, that’s no matter, a man often laughs at what’s good and kind.", "그뿐 아니라, 어쩌면 그 하나의 추억이 그를 큰 악에서 지켜 줄 테고, 그는 곰곰이 생각하며 이렇게 말하겠지, '그래, 나는 그때 선하고 용감하고 정직했어!'라고. 그가 혼자 비웃게 두어도 좋아, 그건 상관없어, 사람은 흔히 선하고 따뜻한 것을 비웃으니까."], ["That’s only from thoughtlessness.", "그건 그저 생각이 모자라서 그러는 거야."], ["But I assure you, boys, that as he laughs he will say at once in his heart, ‘No, I do wrong to laugh, for that’s not a thing to laugh at.’”", "하지만 얘들아, 내가 장담하건대, 그는 비웃으면서도 곧 마음속으로 이렇게 말할 거야, '아니야, 내가 비웃는 건 잘못이야, 이건 비웃을 일이 아니니까.'\""]], [["“That will be so, I understand you, Karamazov!”", "\"그렇게 될 거예요, 나는 형님 말을 알겠어요, 카라마조프!\""], ["cried Kolya, with flashing eyes.", "콜랴가 눈을 반짝이며 외쳤다."]], [["The boys were excited and they, too, wanted to say something, but they restrained themselves, looking with intentness and emotion at the speaker.", "소년들은 흥분했고, 그들 역시 무언가 말하고 싶었으나 자제하면서, 골똘하고 감격에 찬 눈길로 화자를 바라보았다."]], [["“I say this in case we become bad,”", "\"내가 이 말을 하는 건 혹시 우리가 나빠질까 봐서야.\""], ["Alyosha went on, “but there’s no reason why we should become bad, is there, boys?", "알료샤가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 우리가 나빠질 까닭이 어디 있겠니, 그렇지, 얘들아?"], ["Let us be, first and above all, kind, then honest and then let us never forget each other!", "무엇보다 우선 우리는 선하게 살자, 그다음에는 정직하게, 그리고 결코 서로를 잊지 말자!"], ["I say that again.", "다시 한번 말하마."], ["I give you my word for my part that I’ll never forget one of you.", "나로서는 너희 중 누구도 결코 잊지 않겠다고 너희에게 약속하마."], ["Every face looking at me now I shall remember even for thirty years.", "지금 나를 바라보는 이 얼굴들을 하나하나, 나는 삼십 년이 지나도 기억할 거야."], ["Just now Kolya said to Kartashov that we did not care to know whether he exists or not.", "방금 콜랴가 카르타쇼프에게, 그가 존재하든 말든 우리는 알 바 아니라고 말했지."], ["But I cannot forget that Kartashov exists and that he is not blushing now as he did when he discovered the founders of Troy, but is looking at me with his jolly, kind, dear little eyes.", "하지만 나는 카르타쇼프가 존재한다는 것을, 그리고 그가 트로이를 세운 사람들에 대해 알아냈을 때처럼 지금 얼굴을 붉히고 있는 게 아니라, 명랑하고 다정한, 사랑스러운 작은 눈으로 나를 바라보고 있다는 것을 잊을 수가 없어."], ["Boys, my dear boys, let us all be generous and brave like Ilusha, clever, brave and generous like Kolya (though he will be ever so much cleverer when he is grown up), and let us all be as modest, as clever and sweet as Kartashov.", "얘들아, 나의 사랑하는 아이들아, 우리 모두 일류샤처럼 너그럽고 용감해지자, 콜랴처럼 영리하고 용감하고 너그러워지자(하긴 콜랴는 다 자라면 훨씬 더 영리해지겠지만), 그리고 우리 모두 카르타쇼프처럼 겸손하고 영리하고 사랑스러워지자."], ["But why am I talking about those two?", "그런데 내가 왜 그 둘만 이야기하고 있지?"], ["You are all dear to me, boys, from this day forth, I have a place in my heart for you all, and I beg you to keep a place in your hearts for me!", "너희 모두가 나에게 소중해, 얘들아, 오늘 이날부터 내 마음속에 너희 모두를 위한 자리가 있단다, 그러니 너희도 너희 마음속에 나를 위한 자리를 간직해 주기를 부탁한다!"], ["Well, and who has united us in this kind, good feeling which we shall remember and intend to remember all our lives?", "자, 그렇다면 우리가 기억할 테고 또 평생 기억하려 마음먹은 이 선하고 따뜻한 마음으로 우리를 하나로 묶어 준 이가 누구냐?"], ["Who, if not Ilusha, the good boy, the dear boy, precious to us for ever!", "일류샤가 아니라면 누구겠니, 그 선한 아이, 그 사랑스러운 아이, 영원히 우리에게 소중할 그 아이가 아니라면!"], ["Let us never forget him.", "우리 결코 그 애를 잊지 말자."], ["May his memory live for ever in our hearts from this time forth!”", "이날 이후로 그 애에 대한 기억이 우리 마음속에 영원히 살아 있기를!\""]], [["“Yes, yes, for ever, for ever!”", "\"그래요, 그래요, 영원히, 영원히요!\""], ["the boys cried in their ringing voices, with softened faces.", "소년들이 부드러워진 얼굴로 낭랑한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Let us remember his face and his clothes and his poor little boots, his coffin and his unhappy, sinful father, and how boldly he stood up for him alone against the whole school.”", "\"그 애의 얼굴과 옷과 가여운 작은 장화를, 그 애의 관과 불행하고 죄 많은 아버지를, 그리고 그 애가 온 학교를 상대로 혼자서 얼마나 용감하게 아버지 편에 서서 맞섰는지를 기억하자.\""]], [["“We will remember, we will remember,”", "\"기억할 거예요, 기억할 거예요.\""], ["cried the boys.", "소년들이 외쳤다."], ["“He was brave, he was good!”", "\"그 애는 용감했어요, 착했어요!\""]], [["“Ah, how I loved him!”", "\"아, 내가 그 애를 얼마나 사랑했는지!\""], ["exclaimed Kolya.", "콜랴가 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah, children, ah, dear friends, don’t be afraid of life!", "\"아, 아이들아, 아, 사랑하는 친구들아, 삶을 두려워하지 말아라!"], ["How good life is when one does something good and just!”", "무언가 선하고 의로운 일을 할 때 삶은 얼마나 아름다운지!\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["the boys repeated enthusiastically.", "소년들이 열렬히 따라 말했다."]], [["“Karamazov, we love you!”", "\"카라마조프, 우리는 형님을 사랑해요!\""], ["a voice, probably Kartashov’s, cried impulsively.", "한 목소리, 아마도 카르타쇼프의 목소리가 충동적으로 외쳤다."]], [["“We love you, we love you!”", "\"우리는 형님을 사랑해요, 사랑해요!\""], ["they all caught it up.", "모두가 그 말을 받아 외쳤다."], ["There were tears in the eyes of many of them.", "그들 가운데 많은 이의 눈에 눈물이 어렸다."]], [["“Hurrah for Karamazov!”", "\"카라마조프 만세!\""], ["Kolya shouted ecstatically.", "콜랴가 황홀하게 외쳤다."]], [["“And may the dead boy’s memory live for ever!”", "\"그리고 죽은 아이에 대한 기억이 영원히 살아 있기를!\""], ["Alyosha added again with feeling.", "알료샤가 다시 한번 감정을 담아 덧붙였다."]], [["“For ever!”", "\"영원히!\""], ["the boys chimed in again.", "소년들이 또다시 화답했다."]], [["“Karamazov,”", "\"카라마조프.\""], ["cried Kolya, “can it be true what’s taught us in religion, that we shall all rise again from the dead and shall live and see each other again, all, Ilusha too?”", "콜랴가 외쳤다. \"종교에서 우리에게 가르치는 게, 곧 우리 모두 죽음에서 다시 살아나 다시 살게 되고 서로를, 모두를, 일류샤까지도 다시 보게 된다는 게 정말일까요?\""]], [["“Certainly we shall all rise again, certainly we shall see each other and shall tell each other with joy and gladness all that has happened!”", "\"분명히 우리는 모두 다시 살아날 거야, 분명히 우리는 서로를 다시 보게 될 테고, 그동안 있었던 모든 일을 기쁘고 즐거운 마음으로 서로에게 이야기하게 될 거야!\""], ["Alyosha answered, half laughing, half enthusiastic.", "알료샤가 반은 웃으며, 반은 열에 차서 대답했다."]], [["“Ah, how splendid it will be!”", "\"아, 얼마나 멋질까요!\""], ["broke from Kolya.", "콜랴의 입에서 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Well, now we will finish talking and go to his funeral dinner.", "\"자, 이제 이야기는 그만하고 그 애의 장례 만찬에 가자."], ["Don’t be put out at our eating pancakes—it’s a very old custom and there’s something nice in that!”", "우리가 블린을 먹는다고 언짢아하지 마—그건 아주 오래된 풍습이고, 거기엔 무언가 좋은 데가 있으니까!\" 알료샤가 웃었다."], ["laughed Alyosha.", "\"자, 가자!"], ["“Well, let us go! And now we go hand in hand.”", "그리고 이제 우리는 손에 손을 잡고 가는 거야.\""]], [["“And always so, all our lives hand in hand!", "\"그리고 언제까지나 그렇게, 평생토록 손에 손을 잡고!"], ["Hurrah for Karamazov!”", "카라마조프 만세!\""], ["Kolya cried once more rapturously, and once more the boys took up his exclamation: “Hurrah for Karamazov!”", "콜랴가 다시 한번 황홀하게 외쳤고, 소년들은 또다시 그의 외침을 받아 외쳤다."], ["[1] In Russian, “silen.”", "\"카라마조프 만세!\""]], [["[5] Probably the public event was the Decabrist plot against the Tsar, of December 1825, in which the most distinguished men in Russia were concerned.—TRANSLATOR’S NOTE. [6] When a monk’s body is carried out from the cell to the church and from the church to the graveyard, the canticle “What earthly joy...” is sung. If the deceased was a priest as well as a monk the canticle “Our Helper and Defender” is sung instead.", "끝"]], [["[8] Literally: “Did you get off with a long nose made at you?”—a proverbial expression in Russia for failure. [9] Gogol is meant.", "블린: 러시아식 팬케이크로, 장례 후 추모 만찬에 내는 전통 음식이다."]]];
